if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-967"]="There once lived, in a sequestered part of the county of Devonshire, one Mr. Godfrey Nickleby: a worthy gentleman, who, taking it into his head rather late in life that he must get married, and not being young enough or rich enough to aspire to the hand of a lady of fortune, had wedded an old flame out of mere attachment, who in her turn had taken him for the same reason. Thus two people who cannot afford to play cards for money, sometimes sit down to a quiet game for love.\n\nSome ill-conditioned persons who sneer at the life-matrimonial, may perhaps suggest, in this place, that the good couple would be better likened to two principals in a sparring match, who, when fortune is low and backers scarce, will chivalrously set to, for the mere pleasure of the buffeting; and in one respect indeed this comparison would hold good; for, as the adventurous pair of the Fives’ Court will afterwards send round a hat, and trust to the bounty of the lookers-on for the means of regaling themselves, so Mr. Godfrey Nickleby and HIS partner, the honeymoon being over, looked out wistfully into the world, relying in no inconsiderable degree upon chance for the improvement of their means. Mr. Nickleby’s income, at the period of his marriage, fluctuated between sixty and eighty pounds PER ANNUM.\n\nThere are people enough in the world, Heaven knows! and even in London (where Mr. Nickleby dwelt in those days) but few complaints prevail, of the population being scanty. It is extraordinary how long a man may look among the crowd without discovering the face of a friend, but it is no less true. Mr. Nickleby looked, and looked, till his eyes became sore as his heart, but no friend appeared; and when, growing tired of the search, he turned his eyes homeward, he saw very little there to relieve his weary vision. A painter who has gazed too long upon some glaring colour, refreshes his dazzled sight by looking upon a darker and more sombre tint; but everything that met Mr. Nickleby’s gaze wore so black and gloomy a hue, that he would have been beyond description refreshed by the very reverse of the contrast.\n\nAt length, after five years, when Mrs. Nickleby had presented her husband with a couple of sons, and that embarrassed gentleman, impressed with the necessity of making some provision for his family, was seriously revolving in his mind a little commercial speculation of insuring his life next quarter-day, and then falling from the top of the Monument by accident, there came, one morning, by the general post, a black-bordered letter to inform him how his uncle, Mr. Ralph Nickleby, was dead, and had left him the bulk of his little property, amounting in all to five thousand pounds sterling.\n\nAs the deceased had taken no further notice of his nephew in his lifetime, than sending to his eldest boy (who had been christened after him, on desperate speculation) a silver spoon in a morocco case, which, as he had not too much to eat with it, seemed a kind of satire upon his having been born without that useful article of plate in his mouth, Mr. Godfrey Nickleby could, at first, scarcely believe the tidings thus conveyed to him. On examination, however, they turned out to be strictly correct. The amiable old gentleman, it seemed, had intended to leave the whole to the Royal Humane Society, and had indeed executed a will to that effect; but the Institution, having been unfortunate enough, a few months before, to save the life of a poor relation to whom he paid a weekly allowance of three shillings and sixpence, he had, in a fit of very natural exasperation, revoked the bequest in a codicil, and left it all to Mr. Godfrey Nickleby; with a special mention of his indignation, not only against the society for saving the poor relation’s life, but against the poor relation also, for allowing himself to be saved.\n\nWith a portion of this property Mr. Godfrey Nickleby purchased a small farm, near Dawlish in Devonshire, whither he retired with his wife and two children, to live upon the best interest he could get for the rest of his money, and the little produce he could raise from his land. The two prospered so well together that, when he died, some fifteen years after this period, and some five after his wife, he was enabled to leave, to his eldest son, Ralph, three thousand pounds in cash, and to his youngest son, Nicholas, one thousand and the farm, which was as small a landed estate as one would desire to see.\n\nThese two brothers had been brought up together in a school at Exeter; and, being accustomed to go home once a week, had often heard, from their mother’s lips, long accounts of their father’s sufferings in his days of poverty, and of their deceased uncle’s importance in his days of affluence: which recitals produced a very different impression on the two: for, while the younger, who was of a timid and retiring disposition, gleaned from thence nothing but forewarnings to shun the great world and attach himself to the quiet routine of a country life, Ralph, the elder, deduced from the often-repeated tale the two great morals that riches are the only true source of happiness and power, and that it is lawful and just to compass their acquisition by all means short of felony. ‘And,’ reasoned Ralph with himself, ‘if no good came of my uncle’s money when he was alive, a great deal of good came of it after he was dead, inasmuch as my father has got it now, and is saving it up for me, which is a highly virtuous purpose; and, going back to the old gentleman, good DID come of it to him too, for he had the pleasure of thinking of it all his life long, and of being envied and courted by all his family besides.’ And Ralph always wound up these mental soliloquies by arriving at the conclusion, that there was nothing like money.\n\nNot confining himself to theory, or permitting his faculties to rust, even at that early age, in mere abstract speculations, this promising lad commenced usurer on a limited scale at school; putting out at good interest a small capital of slate-pencil and marbles, and gradually extending his operations until they aspired to the copper coinage of this realm, in which he speculated to considerable advantage. Nor did he trouble his borrowers with abstract calculations of figures, or references to ready-reckoners; his simple rule of interest being all comprised in the one golden sentence, ‘two-pence for every half-penny,’ which greatly simplified the accounts, and which, as a familiar precept, more easily acquired and retained in the memory than any known rule of arithmetic, cannot be too strongly recommended to the notice of capitalists, both large and small, and more especially of money-brokers and bill-discounters. Indeed, to do these gentlemen justice, many of them are to this day in the frequent habit of adopting it, with eminent success.\n\nIn like manner, did young Ralph Nickleby avoid all those minute and intricate calculations of odd days, which nobody who has worked sums in simple-interest can fail to have found most embarrassing, by establishing the one general rule that all sums of principal and interest should be paid on pocket-money day, that is to say, on Saturday: and that whether a loan were contracted on the Monday, or on the Friday, the amount of interest should be, in both cases, the same. Indeed he argued, and with great show of reason, that it ought to be rather more for one day than for five, inasmuch as the borrower might in the former case be very fairly presumed to be in great extremity, otherwise he would not borrow at all with such odds against him. This fact is interesting, as illustrating the secret connection and sympathy which always exist between great minds. Though Master Ralph Nickleby was not at that time aware of it, the class of gentlemen before alluded to, proceed on just the same principle in all their transactions.\n\nFrom what we have said of this young gentleman, and the natural admiration the reader will immediately conceive of his character, it may perhaps be inferred that he is to be the hero of the work which we shall presently begin. To set this point at rest, for once and for ever, we hasten to undeceive them, and stride to its commencement.\n\nOn the death of his father, Ralph Nickleby, who had been some time before placed in a mercantile house in London, applied himself passionately to his old pursuit of money-getting, in which he speedily became so buried and absorbed, that he quite forgot his brother for many years; and if, at times, a recollection of his old playfellow broke upon him through the haze in which he lived--for gold conjures up a mist about a man, more destructive of all his old senses and lulling to his feelings than the fumes of charcoal--it brought along with it a companion thought, that if they were intimate he would want to borrow money of him. So, Mr. Ralph Nickleby shrugged his shoulders, and said things were better as they were.\n\nAs for Nicholas, he lived a single man on the patrimonial estate until he grew tired of living alone, and then he took to wife the daughter of a neighbouring gentleman with a dower of one thousand pounds. This good lady bore him two children, a son and a daughter, and when the son was about nineteen, and the daughter fourteen, as near as we can guess--impartial records of young ladies’ ages being, before the passing of the new act, nowhere preserved in the registries of this country--Mr Nickleby looked about him for the means of repairing his capital, now sadly reduced by this increase in his family, and the expenses of their education.\n\n‘Speculate with it,’ said Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘Spec--u--late, my dear?’ said Mr. Nickleby, as though in doubt.\n\n‘Why not?’ asked Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘Because, my dear, if we SHOULD lose it,’ rejoined Mr. Nickleby, who was a slow and time-taking speaker, ‘if we SHOULD lose it, we shall no longer be able to live, my dear.’\n\n‘Fiddle,’ said Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘I am not altogether sure of that, my dear,’ said Mr. Nickleby.\n\n‘There’s Nicholas,’ pursued the lady, ‘quite a young man--it’s time he was in the way of doing something for himself; and Kate too, poor girl, without a penny in the world. Think of your brother! Would he be what he is, if he hadn’t speculated?’\n\n‘That’s true,’ replied Mr. Nickleby. ‘Very good, my dear. Yes. I WILL speculate, my dear.’\n\nSpeculation is a round game; the players see little or nothing of their cards at first starting; gains MAY be great--and so may losses. The run of luck went against Mr. Nickleby. A mania prevailed, a bubble burst, four stock-brokers took villa residences at Florence, four hundred nobodies were ruined, and among them Mr. Nickleby.\n\n‘The very house I live in,’ sighed the poor gentleman, ‘may be taken from me tomorrow. Not an article of my old furniture, but will be sold to strangers!’\n\nThe last reflection hurt him so much, that he took at once to his bed; apparently resolved to keep that, at all events.\n\n‘Cheer up, sir!’ said the apothecary.\n\n‘You mustn’t let yourself be cast down, sir,’ said the nurse.\n\n‘Such things happen every day,’ remarked the lawyer.\n\n‘And it is very sinful to rebel against them,’ whispered the clergyman.\n\n‘And what no man with a family ought to do,’ added the neighbours.\n\nMr. Nickleby shook his head, and motioning them all out of the room, embraced his wife and children, and having pressed them by turns to his languidly beating heart, sunk exhausted on his pillow. They were concerned to find that his reason went astray after this; for he babbled, for a long time, about the generosity and goodness of his brother, and the merry old times when they were at school together. This fit of wandering past, he solemnly commended them to One who never deserted the widow or her fatherless children, and, smiling gently on them, turned upon his face, and observed, that he thought he could fall asleep.\n\n# CHAPTER 2\n\nOf Mr. Ralph Nickleby, and his Establishments, and his Undertakings, and of a great Joint Stock Company of vast national Importance\nMr. Ralph Nickleby was not, strictly speaking, what you would call a merchant, neither was he a banker, nor an attorney, nor a special pleader, nor a notary. He was certainly not a tradesman, and still less could he lay any claim to the title of a professional gentleman; for it would have been impossible to mention any recognised profession to which he belonged. Nevertheless, as he lived in a spacious house in Golden Square, which, in addition to a brass plate upon the street-door, had another brass plate two sizes and a half smaller upon the left hand door-post, surrounding a brass model of an infant’s fist grasping a fragment of a skewer, and displaying the word ‘Office,’ it was clear that Mr. Ralph Nickleby did, or pretended to do, business of some kind; and the fact, if it required any further circumstantial evidence, was abundantly demonstrated by the diurnal attendance, between the hours of half-past nine and five, of a sallow-faced man in rusty brown, who sat upon an uncommonly hard stool in a species of butler’s pantry at the end of the passage, and always had a pen behind his ear when he answered the bell.\n\nAlthough a few members of the graver professions live about Golden Square, it is not exactly in anybody’s way to or from anywhere. It is one of the squares that have been; a quarter of the town that has gone down in the world, and taken to letting lodgings. Many of its first and second floors are let, furnished, to single gentlemen; and it takes boarders besides. It is a great resort of foreigners. The dark-complexioned men who wear large rings, and heavy watch-guards, and bushy whiskers, and who congregate under the Opera Colonnade, and about the box-office in the season, between four and five in the afternoon, when they give away the orders,--all live in Golden Square, or within a street of it. Two or three violins and a wind instrument from the Opera band reside within its precincts. Its boarding-houses are musical, and the notes of pianos and harps float in the evening time round the head of the mournful statue, the guardian genius of a little wilderness of shrubs, in the centre of the square. On a summer’s night, windows are thrown open, and groups of swarthy moustached men are seen by the passer-by, lounging at the casements, and smoking fearfully. Sounds of gruff voices practising vocal music invade the evening’s silence; and the fumes of choice tobacco scent the air. There, snuff and cigars, and German pipes and flutes, and violins and violoncellos, divide the supremacy between them. It is the region of song and smoke. Street bands are on their mettle in Golden Square; and itinerant glee-singers quaver involuntarily as they raise their voices within its boundaries.\n\nThis would not seem a spot very well adapted to the transaction of business; but Mr. Ralph Nickleby had lived there, notwithstanding, for many years, and uttered no complaint on that score. He knew nobody round about, and nobody knew him, although he enjoyed the reputation of being immensely rich. The tradesmen held that he was a sort of lawyer, and the other neighbours opined that he was a kind of general agent; both of which guesses were as correct and definite as guesses about other people’s affairs usually are, or need to be.\n\nMr. Ralph Nickleby sat in his private office one morning, ready dressed to walk abroad. He wore a bottle-green spencer over a blue coat; a white waistcoat, grey mixture pantaloons, and Wellington boots drawn over them. The corner of a small-plaited shirt-frill struggled out, as if insisting to show itself, from between his chin and the top button of his spencer; and the latter garment was not made low enough to conceal a long gold watch-chain, composed of a series of plain rings, which had its beginning at the handle of a gold repeater in Mr. Nickleby’s pocket, and its termination in two little keys: one belonging to the watch itself, and the other to some patent padlock. He wore a sprinkling of powder upon his head, as if to make himself look benevolent; but if that were his purpose, he would perhaps have done better to powder his countenance also, for there was something in its very wrinkles, and in his cold restless eye, which seemed to tell of cunning that would announce itself in spite of him. However this might be, there he was; and as he was all alone, neither the powder, nor the wrinkles, nor the eyes, had the smallest effect, good or bad, upon anybody just then, and are consequently no business of ours just now.\n\nMr. Nickleby closed an account-book which lay on his desk, and, throwing himself back in his chair, gazed with an air of abstraction through the dirty window. Some London houses have a melancholy little plot of ground behind them, usually fenced in by four high whitewashed walls, and frowned upon by stacks of chimneys: in which there withers on, from year to year, a crippled tree, that makes a show of putting forth a few leaves late in autumn when other trees shed theirs, and, drooping in the effort, lingers on, all crackled and smoke-dried, till the following season, when it repeats the same process, and perhaps, if the weather be particularly genial, even tempts some rheumatic sparrow to chirrup in its branches. People sometimes call these dark yards ‘gardens’; it is not supposed that they were ever planted, but rather that they are pieces of unreclaimed land, with the withered vegetation of the original brick-field. No man thinks of walking in this desolate place, or of turning it to any account. A few hampers, half-a-dozen broken bottles, and such-like rubbish, may be thrown there, when the tenant first moves in, but nothing more; and there they remain until he goes away again: the damp straw taking just as long to moulder as it thinks proper: and mingling with the scanty box, and stunted everbrowns, and broken flower-pots, that are scattered mournfully about--a prey to ‘blacks’ and dirt.\n\nIt was into a place of this kind that Mr. Ralph Nickleby gazed, as he sat with his hands in his pockets looking out of the window. He had fixed his eyes upon a distorted fir tree, planted by some former tenant in a tub that had once been green, and left there, years before, to rot away piecemeal. There was nothing very inviting in the object, but Mr Nickleby was wrapt in a brown study, and sat contemplating it with far greater attention than, in a more conscious mood, he would have deigned to bestow upon the rarest exotic. At length, his eyes wandered to a little dirty window on the left, through which the face of the clerk was dimly visible; that worthy chancing to look up, he beckoned him to attend.\n\nIn obedience to this summons the clerk got off the high stool (to which he had communicated a high polish by countless gettings off and on), and presented himself in Mr. Nickleby’s room. He was a tall man of middle age, with two goggle eyes whereof one was a fixture, a rubicund nose, a cadaverous face, and a suit of clothes (if the term be allowable when they suited him not at all) much the worse for wear, very much too small, and placed upon such a short allowance of buttons that it was marvellous how he contrived to keep them on.\n\n‘Was that half-past twelve, Noggs?’ said Mr. Nickleby, in a sharp and grating voice.\n\n‘Not more than five-and-twenty minutes by the--’ Noggs was going to add public-house clock, but recollecting himself, substituted ‘regular time.’\n\n‘My watch has stopped,’ said Mr. Nickleby; ‘I don’t know from what cause.’\n\n‘Not wound up,’ said Noggs.\n\n‘Yes it is,’ said Mr. Nickleby.\n\n‘Over-wound then,’ rejoined Noggs.\n\n‘That can’t very well be,’ observed Mr. Nickleby.\n\n‘Must be,’ said Noggs.\n\n‘Well!’ said Mr. Nickleby, putting the repeater back in his pocket; ‘perhaps it is.’\n\nNoggs gave a peculiar grunt, as was his custom at the end of all disputes with his master, to imply that he (Noggs) triumphed; and (as he rarely spoke to anybody unless somebody spoke to him) fell into a grim silence, and rubbed his hands slowly over each other: cracking the joints of his fingers, and squeezing them into all possible distortions. The incessant performance of this routine on every occasion, and the communication of a fixed and rigid look to his unaffected eye, so as to make it uniform with the other, and to render it impossible for anybody to determine where or at what he was looking, were two among the numerous peculiarities of Mr. Noggs, which struck an inexperienced observer at first sight.\n\n‘I am going to the London Tavern this morning,’ said Mr. Nickleby.\n\n‘Public meeting?’ inquired Noggs.\n\nMr. Nickleby nodded. ‘I expect a letter from the solicitor respecting that mortgage of Ruddle’s. If it comes at all, it will be here by the two o’clock delivery. I shall leave the city about that time and walk to Charing Cross on the left-hand side of the way; if there are any letters, come and meet me, and bring them with you.’\n\nNoggs nodded; and as he nodded, there came a ring at the office bell. The master looked up from his papers, and the clerk calmly remained in a stationary position.\n\n‘The bell,’ said Noggs, as though in explanation. ‘At home?’\n\n‘Yes.’\n\n‘To anybody?’\n\n‘Yes.’\n\n‘To the tax-gatherer?’\n\n‘No! Let him call again.’\n\nNoggs gave vent to his usual grunt, as much as to say ‘I thought so!’ and, the ring being repeated, went to the door, whence he presently returned, ushering in, by the name of Mr. Bonney, a pale gentleman in a violent hurry, who, with his hair standing up in great disorder all over his head, and a very narrow white cravat tied loosely round his throat, looked as if he had been knocked up in the night and had not dressed himself since.\n\n‘My dear Nickleby,’ said the gentleman, taking off a white hat which was so full of papers that it would scarcely stick upon his head, ‘there’s not a moment to lose; I have a cab at the door. Sir Matthew Pupker takes the chair, and three members of Parliament are positively coming. I have seen two of them safely out of bed. The third, who was at Crockford’s all night, has just gone home to put a clean shirt on, and take a bottle or two of soda water, and will certainly be with us, in time to address the meeting. He is a little excited by last night, but never mind that; he always speaks the stronger for it.’\n\n‘It seems to promise pretty well,’ said Mr. Ralph Nickleby, whose deliberate manner was strongly opposed to the vivacity of the other man of business.\n\n‘Pretty well!’ echoed Mr. Bonney. ‘It’s the finest idea that was ever started. “United Metropolitan Improved Hot Muffin and Crumpet Baking and Punctual Delivery Company. Capital, five millions, in five hundred thousand shares of ten pounds each.” Why the very name will get the shares up to a premium in ten days.’\n\n‘And when they ARE at a premium,’ said Mr. Ralph Nickleby, smiling.\n\n‘When they are, you know what to do with them as well as any man alive, and how to back quietly out at the right time,’ said Mr. Bonney, slapping the capitalist familiarly on the shoulder. ‘By-the-bye, what a VERY remarkable man that clerk of yours is.’\n\n‘Yes, poor devil!’ replied Ralph, drawing on his gloves. ‘Though Newman Noggs kept his horses and hounds once.’\n\n‘Ay, ay?’ said the other carelessly.\n\n‘Yes,’ continued Ralph, ‘and not many years ago either; but he squandered his money, invested it anyhow, borrowed at interest, and in short made first a thorough fool of himself, and then a beggar. He took to drinking, and had a touch of paralysis, and then came here to borrow a pound, as in his better days I had--’\n\n‘Done business with him,’ said Mr. Bonney with a meaning look.\n\n‘Just so,’ replied Ralph; ‘I couldn’t lend it, you know.’\n\n‘Oh, of course not.’\n\n‘But as I wanted a clerk just then, to open the door and so forth, I took him out of charity, and he has remained with me ever since. He is a little mad, I think,’ said Mr. Nickleby, calling up a charitable look, ‘but he is useful enough, poor creature--useful enough.’\n\nThe kind-hearted gentleman omitted to add that Newman Noggs, being utterly destitute, served him for rather less than the usual wages of a boy of thirteen; and likewise failed to mention in his hasty chronicle, that his eccentric taciturnity rendered him an especially valuable person in a place where much business was done, of which it was desirable no mention should be made out of doors. The other gentleman was plainly impatient to be gone, however, and as they hurried into the hackney cabriolet immediately afterwards, perhaps Mr. Nickleby forgot to mention circumstances so unimportant.\n\nThere was a great bustle in Bishopsgate Street Within, as they drew up, and (it being a windy day) half-a-dozen men were tacking across the road under a press of paper, bearing gigantic announcements that a Public Meeting would be holden at one o’clock precisely, to take into consideration the propriety of petitioning Parliament in favour of the United Metropolitan Improved Hot Muffin and Crumpet Baking and Punctual Delivery Company, capital five millions, in five hundred thousand shares of ten pounds each; which sums were duly set forth in fat black figures of considerable size. Mr. Bonney elbowed his way briskly upstairs, receiving in his progress many low bows from the waiters who stood on the landings to show the way; and, followed by Mr. Nickleby, dived into a suite of apartments behind the great public room: in the second of which was a business-looking table, and several business-looking people.\n\n‘Hear!’ cried a gentleman with a double chin, as Mr. Bonney presented himself. ‘Chair, gentlemen, chair!’\n\nThe new-comers were received with universal approbation, and Mr. Bonney bustled up to the top of the table, took off his hat, ran his fingers through his hair, and knocked a hackney-coachman’s knock on the table with a little hammer: whereat several gentlemen cried ‘Hear!’ and nodded slightly to each other, as much as to say what spirited conduct that was. Just at this moment, a waiter, feverish with agitation, tore into the room, and throwing the door open with a crash, shouted ‘Sir Matthew Pupker!’\n\nThe committee stood up and clapped their hands for joy, and while they were clapping them, in came Sir Matthew Pupker, attended by two live members of Parliament, one Irish and one Scotch, all smiling and bowing, and looking so pleasant that it seemed a perfect marvel how any man could have the heart to vote against them. Sir Matthew Pupker especially, who had a little round head with a flaxen wig on the top of it, fell into such a paroxysm of bows, that the wig threatened to be jerked off, every instant. When these symptoms had in some degree subsided, the gentlemen who were on speaking terms with Sir Matthew Pupker, or the two other members, crowded round them in three little groups, near one or other of which the gentlemen who were NOT on speaking terms with Sir Matthew Pupker or the two other members, stood lingering, and smiling, and rubbing their hands, in the desperate hope of something turning up which might bring them into notice. All this time, Sir Matthew Pupker and the two other members were relating to their separate circles what the intentions of government were, about taking up the bill; with a full account of what the government had said in a whisper the last time they dined with it, and how the government had been observed to wink when it said so; from which premises they were at no loss to draw the conclusion, that if the government had one object more at heart than another, that one object was the welfare and advantage of the United Metropolitan Improved Hot Muffin and Crumpet Baking and Punctual Delivery Company.\n\nMeanwhile, and pending the arrangement of the proceedings, and a fair division of the speechifying, the public in the large room were eyeing, by turns, the empty platform, and the ladies in the Music Gallery. In these amusements the greater portion of them had been occupied for a couple of hours before, and as the most agreeable diversions pall upon the taste on a too protracted enjoyment of them, the sterner spirits now began to hammer the floor with their boot-heels, and to express their dissatisfaction by various hoots and cries. These vocal exertions, emanating from the people who had been there longest, naturally proceeded from those who were nearest to the platform and furthest from the policemen in attendance, who having no great mind to fight their way through the crowd, but entertaining nevertheless a praiseworthy desire to do something to quell the disturbance, immediately began to drag forth, by the coat tails and collars, all the quiet people near the door; at the same time dealing out various smart and tingling blows with their truncheons, after the manner of that ingenious actor, Mr. Punch: whose brilliant example, both in the fashion of his weapons and their use, this branch of the executive occasionally follows.\n\nSeveral very exciting skirmishes were in progress, when a loud shout attracted the attention even of the belligerents, and then there poured on to the platform, from a door at the side, a long line of gentlemen with their hats off, all looking behind them, and uttering vociferous cheers; the cause whereof was sufficiently explained when Sir Matthew Pupker and the two other real members of Parliament came to the front, amidst deafening shouts, and testified to each other in dumb motions that they had never seen such a glorious sight as that, in the whole course of their public career.\n\nAt length, and at last, the assembly left off shouting, but Sir Matthew Pupker being voted into the chair, they underwent a relapse which lasted five minutes. This over, Sir Matthew Pupker went on to say what must be his feelings on that great occasion, and what must be that occasion in the eyes of the world, and what must be the intelligence of his fellow-countrymen before him, and what must be the wealth and respectability of his honourable friends behind him, and lastly, what must be the importance to the wealth, the happiness, the comfort, the liberty, the very existence of a free and great people, of such an Institution as the United Metropolitan Improved Hot Muffin and Crumpet Baking and Punctual Delivery Company!\n\nMr. Bonney then presented himself to move the first resolution; and having run his right hand through his hair, and planted his left, in an easy manner, in his ribs, he consigned his hat to the care of the gentleman with the double chin (who acted as a species of bottle-holder to the orators generally), and said he would read to them the first resolution--‘That this meeting views with alarm and apprehension, the existing state of the Muffin Trade in this Metropolis and its neighbourhood; that it considers the Muffin Boys, as at present constituted, wholly underserving the confidence of the public; and that it deems the whole Muffin system alike prejudicial to the health and morals of the people, and subversive of the best interests of a great commercial and mercantile community.’ The honourable gentleman made a speech which drew tears from the eyes of the ladies, and awakened the liveliest emotions in every individual present. He had visited the houses of the poor in the various districts of London, and had found them destitute of the slightest vestige of a muffin, which there appeared too much reason to believe some of these indigent persons did not taste from year’s end to year’s end. He had found that among muffin-sellers there existed drunkenness, debauchery, and profligacy, which he attributed to the debasing nature of their employment as at present exercised; he had found the same vices among the poorer class of people who ought to be muffin consumers; and this he attributed to the despair engendered by their being placed beyond the reach of that nutritious article, which drove them to seek a false stimulant in intoxicating liquors. He would undertake to prove before a committee of the House of Commons, that there existed a combination to keep up the price of muffins, and to give the bellmen a monopoly; he would prove it by bellmen at the bar of that House; and he would also prove, that these men corresponded with each other by secret words and signs as ‘Snooks,’ ‘Walker,’ ‘Ferguson,’ ‘Is Murphy right?’ and many others. It was this melancholy state of things that the Company proposed to correct; firstly, by prohibiting, under heavy penalties, all private muffin trading of every description; secondly, by themselves supplying the public generally, and the poor at their own homes, with muffins of first quality at reduced prices. It was with this object that a bill had been introduced into Parliament by their patriotic chairman Sir Matthew Pupker; it was this bill that they had met to support; it was the supporters of this bill who would confer undying brightness and splendour upon England, under the name of the United Metropolitan Improved Hot Muffin and Crumpet Baking and Punctual Delivery Company; he would add, with a capital of Five Millions, in five hundred thousand shares of ten pounds each.\n\nMr. Ralph Nickleby seconded the resolution, and another gentleman having moved that it be amended by the insertion of the words ‘and crumpet’ after the word ‘muffin,’ whenever it occurred, it was carried triumphantly. Only one man in the crowd cried ‘No!’ and he was promptly taken into custody, and straightway borne off.\n\nThe second resolution, which recognised the expediency of immediately abolishing ‘all muffin (or crumpet) sellers, all traders in muffins (or crumpets) of whatsoever description, whether male or female, boys or men, ringing hand-bells or otherwise,’ was moved by a grievous gentleman of semi-clerical appearance, who went at once into such deep pathetics, that he knocked the first speaker clean out of the course in no time. You might have heard a pin fall--a pin! a feather--as he described the cruelties inflicted on muffin boys by their masters, which he very wisely urged were in themselves a sufficient reason for the establishment of that inestimable company. It seemed that the unhappy youths were nightly turned out into the wet streets at the most inclement periods of the year, to wander about, in darkness and rain--or it might be hail or snow--for hours together, without shelter, food, or warmth; and let the public never forget upon the latter point, that while the muffins were provided with warm clothing and blankets, the boys were wholly unprovided for, and left to their own miserable resources. (Shame!) The honourable gentleman related one case of a muffin boy, who having been exposed to this inhuman and barbarous system for no less than five years, at length fell a victim to a cold in the head, beneath which he gradually sunk until he fell into a perspiration and recovered; this he could vouch for, on his own authority, but he had heard (and he had no reason to doubt the fact) of a still more heart-rending and appalling circumstance. He had heard of the case of an orphan muffin boy, who, having been run over by a hackney carriage, had been removed to the hospital, had undergone the amputation of his leg below the knee, and was now actually pursuing his occupation on crutches. Fountain of justice, were these things to last!\n\nThis was the department of the subject that took the meeting, and this was the style of speaking to enlist their sympathies. The men shouted; the ladies wept into their pocket-handkerchiefs till they were moist, and waved them till they were dry; the excitement was tremendous; and Mr. Nickleby whispered his friend that the shares were thenceforth at a premium of five-and-twenty per cent.\n\nThe resolution was, of course, carried with loud acclamations, every man holding up both hands in favour of it, as he would in his enthusiasm have held up both legs also, if he could have conveniently accomplished it. This done, the draft of the proposed petition was read at length: and the petition said, as all petitions DO say, that the petitioners were very humble, and the petitioned very honourable, and the object very virtuous; therefore (said the petition) the bill ought to be passed into a law at once, to the everlasting honour and glory of that most honourable and glorious Commons of England in Parliament assembled.\n\nThen, the gentleman who had been at Crockford’s all night, and who looked something the worse about the eyes in consequence, came forward to tell his fellow-countrymen what a speech he meant to make in favour of that petition whenever it should be presented, and how desperately he meant to taunt the parliament if they rejected the bill; and to inform them also, that he regretted his honourable friends had not inserted a clause rendering the purchase of muffins and crumpets compulsory upon all classes of the community, which he--opposing all half-measures, and preferring to go the extreme animal--pledged himself to propose and divide upon, in committee. After announcing this determination, the honourable gentleman grew jocular; and as patent boots, lemon-coloured kid gloves, and a fur coat collar, assist jokes materially, there was immense laughter and much cheering, and moreover such a brilliant display of ladies’ pocket-handkerchiefs, as threw the grievous gentleman quite into the shade.\n\nAnd when the petition had been read and was about to be adopted, there came forward the Irish member (who was a young gentleman of ardent temperament,) with such a speech as only an Irish member can make, breathing the true soul and spirit of poetry, and poured forth with such fervour, that it made one warm to look at him; in the course whereof, he told them how he would demand the extension of that great boon to his native country; how he would claim for her equal rights in the muffin laws as in all other laws; and how he yet hoped to see the day when crumpets should be toasted in her lowly cabins, and muffin bells should ring in her rich green valleys. And, after him, came the Scotch member, with various pleasant allusions to the probable amount of profits, which increased the good humour that the poetry had awakened; and all the speeches put together did exactly what they were intended to do, and established in the hearers’ minds that there was no speculation so promising, or at the same time so praiseworthy, as the United Metropolitan Improved Hot Muffin and Crumpet Baking and Punctual Delivery Company.\n\nSo, the petition in favour of the bill was agreed upon, and the meeting adjourned with acclamations, and Mr. Nickleby and the other directors went to the office to lunch, as they did every day at half-past one o’clock; and to remunerate themselves for which trouble, (as the company was yet in its infancy,) they only charged three guineas each man for every such attendance.\n\n# CHAPTER 3\n\nMr. Ralph Nickleby receives Sad Tidings of his Brother, but bears up nobly against the Intelligence communicated to him. The Reader is informed how he liked Nicholas, who is herein introduced, and how kindly he proposed to make his Fortune at once\nHaving rendered his zealous assistance towards dispatching the lunch, with all that promptitude and energy which are among the most important qualities that men of business can possess, Mr. Ralph Nickleby took a cordial farewell of his fellow-speculators, and bent his steps westward in unwonted good humour. As he passed St Paul’s he stepped aside into a doorway to set his watch, and with his hand on the key and his eye on the cathedral dial, was intent upon so doing, when a man suddenly stopped before him. It was Newman Noggs.\n\n‘Ah! Newman,’ said Mr. Nickleby, looking up as he pursued his occupation. ‘The letter about the mortgage has come, has it? I thought it would.’\n\n‘Wrong,’ replied Newman.\n\n‘What! and nobody called respecting it?’ inquired Mr. Nickleby, pausing. Noggs shook his head.\n\n‘What HAS come, then?’ inquired Mr. Nickleby.\n\n‘I have,’ said Newman.\n\n‘What else?’ demanded the master, sternly.\n\n‘This,’ said Newman, drawing a sealed letter slowly from his pocket. ‘Post-mark, Strand, black wax, black border, woman’s hand, C. N. in the corner.’\n\n‘Black wax?’ said Mr. Nickleby, glancing at the letter. ‘I know something of that hand, too. Newman, I shouldn’t be surprised if my brother were dead.’\n\n‘I don’t think you would,’ said Newman, quietly.\n\n‘Why not, sir?’ demanded Mr. Nickleby.\n\n‘You never are surprised,’ replied Newman, ‘that’s all.’\n\nMr. Nickleby snatched the letter from his assistant, and fixing a cold look upon him, opened, read it, put it in his pocket, and having now hit the time to a second, began winding up his watch.\n\n‘It is as I expected, Newman,’ said Mr. Nickleby, while he was thus engaged. ‘He IS dead. Dear me! Well, that’s sudden thing. I shouldn’t have thought it, really.’ With these touching expressions of sorrow, Mr Nickleby replaced his watch in his fob, and, fitting on his gloves to a nicety, turned upon his way, and walked slowly westward with his hands behind him.\n\n‘Children alive?’ inquired Noggs, stepping up to him.\n\n‘Why, that’s the very thing,’ replied Mr. Nickleby, as though his thoughts were about them at that moment. ‘They are both alive.’\n\n‘Both!’ repeated Newman Noggs, in a low voice.\n\n‘And the widow, too,’ added Mr. Nickleby, ‘and all three in London, confound them; all three here, Newman.’\n\nNewman fell a little behind his master, and his face was curiously twisted as by a spasm; but whether of paralysis, or grief, or inward laughter, nobody but himself could possibly explain. The expression of a man’s face is commonly a help to his thoughts, or glossary on his speech; but the countenance of Newman Noggs, in his ordinary moods, was a problem which no stretch of ingenuity could solve.\n\n‘Go home!’ said Mr. Nickleby, after they had walked a few paces: looking round at the clerk as if he were his dog. The words were scarcely uttered when Newman darted across the road, slunk among the crowd, and disappeared in an instant.\n\n‘Reasonable, certainly!’ muttered Mr. Nickleby to himself, as he walked on, ‘very reasonable! My brother never did anything for me, and I never expected it; the breath is no sooner out of his body than I am to be looked to, as the support of a great hearty woman, and a grown boy and girl. What are they to me! I never saw them.’\n\nFull of these, and many other reflections of a similar kind, Mr. Nickleby made the best of his way to the Strand, and, referring to his letter as if to ascertain the number of the house he wanted, stopped at a private door about half-way down that crowded thoroughfare.\n\nA miniature painter lived there, for there was a large gilt frame screwed upon the street-door, in which were displayed, upon a black velvet ground, two portraits of naval dress coats with faces looking out of them, and telescopes attached; one of a young gentleman in a very vermilion uniform, flourishing a sabre; and one of a literary character with a high forehead, a pen and ink, six books, and a curtain. There was, moreover, a touching representation of a young lady reading a manuscript in an unfathomable forest, and a charming whole length of a large-headed little boy, sitting on a stool with his legs fore-shortened to the size of salt-spoons. Besides these works of art, there were a great many heads of old ladies and gentlemen smirking at each other out of blue and brown skies, and an elegantly written card of terms with an embossed border.\n\nMr. Nickleby glanced at these frivolities with great contempt, and gave a double knock, which, having been thrice repeated, was answered by a servant girl with an uncommonly dirty face.\n\n‘Is Mrs. Nickleby at home, girl?’ demanded Ralph sharply.\n\n‘Her name ain’t Nickleby,’ said the girl, ‘La Creevy, you mean.’\n\nMr. Nickleby looked very indignant at the handmaid on being thus corrected, and demanded with much asperity what she meant; which she was about to state, when a female voice proceeding from a perpendicular staircase at the end of the passage, inquired who was wanted.\n\n‘Mrs. Nickleby,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘It’s the second floor, Hannah,’ said the same voice; ‘what a stupid thing you are! Is the second floor at home?’\n\n‘Somebody went out just now, but I think it was the attic which had been a cleaning of himself,’ replied the girl.\n\n‘You had better see,’ said the invisible female. ‘Show the gentleman where the bell is, and tell him he mustn’t knock double knocks for the second floor; I can’t allow a knock except when the bell’s broke, and then it must be two single ones.’\n\n‘Here,’ said Ralph, walking in without more parley, ‘I beg your pardon; is that Mrs. La what’s-her-name?’\n\n‘Creevy--La Creevy,’ replied the voice, as a yellow headdress bobbed over the banisters.\n\n‘I’ll speak to you a moment, ma’am, with your leave,’ said Ralph.\n\nThe voice replied that the gentleman was to walk up; but he had walked up before it spoke, and stepping into the first floor, was received by the wearer of the yellow head-dress, who had a gown to correspond, and was of much the same colour herself. Miss La Creevy was a mincing young lady of fifty, and Miss La Creevy’s apartment was the gilt frame downstairs on a larger scale and something dirtier.\n\n‘Hem!’ said Miss La Creevy, coughing delicately behind her black silk mitten. ‘A miniature, I presume. A very strongly-marked countenance for the purpose, sir. Have you ever sat before?’\n\n‘You mistake my purpose, I see, ma’am,’ replied Mr. Nickleby, in his usual blunt fashion. ‘I have no money to throw away on miniatures, ma’am, and nobody to give one to (thank God) if I had. Seeing you on the stairs, I wanted to ask a question of you, about some lodgers here.’\n\nMiss La Creevy coughed once more--this cough was to conceal her disappointment--and said, ‘Oh, indeed!’\n\n‘I infer from what you said to your servant, that the floor above belongs to you, ma’am,’ said Mr. Nickleby.\n\nYes it did, Miss La Creevy replied. The upper part of the house belonged to her, and as she had no necessity for the second-floor rooms just then, she was in the habit of letting them. Indeed, there was a lady from the country and her two children in them, at that present speaking.\n\n‘A widow, ma’am?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Yes, she is a widow,’ replied the lady.\n\n‘A POOR widow, ma’am,’ said Ralph, with a powerful emphasis on that little adjective which conveys so much.\n\n‘Well, I’m afraid she IS poor,’ rejoined Miss La Creevy.\n\n‘I happen to know that she is, ma’am,’ said Ralph. ‘Now, what business has a poor widow in such a house as this, ma’am?’\n\n‘Very true,’ replied Miss La Creevy, not at all displeased with this implied compliment to the apartments. ‘Exceedingly true.’\n\n‘I know her circumstances intimately, ma’am,’ said Ralph; ‘in fact, I am a relation of the family; and I should recommend you not to keep them here, ma’am.’\n\n‘I should hope, if there was any incompatibility to meet the pecuniary obligations,’ said Miss La Creevy with another cough, ‘that the lady’s family would--’\n\n‘No they wouldn’t, ma’am,’ interrupted Ralph, hastily. ‘Don’t think it.’\n\n‘If I am to understand that,’ said Miss La Creevy, ‘the case wears a very different appearance.’\n\n‘You may understand it then, ma’am,’ said Ralph, ‘and make your arrangements accordingly. I am the family, ma’am--at least, I believe I am the only relation they have, and I think it right that you should know I can’t support them in their extravagances. How long have they taken these lodgings for?’\n\n‘Only from week to week,’ replied Miss La Creevy. ‘Mrs. Nickleby paid the first week in advance.’\n\n‘Then you had better get them out at the end of it,’ said Ralph. ‘They can’t do better than go back to the country, ma’am; they are in everybody’s way here.’\n\n‘Certainly,’ said Miss La Creevy, rubbing her hands, ‘if Mrs. Nickleby took the apartments without the means of paying for them, it was very unbecoming a lady.’\n\n‘Of course it was, ma’am,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘And naturally,’ continued Miss La Creevy, ‘I who am, AT PRESENT--hem--an unprotected female, cannot afford to lose by the apartments.’\n\n‘Of course you can’t, ma’am,’ replied Ralph.\n\n‘Though at the same time,’ added Miss La Creevy, who was plainly wavering between her good-nature and her interest, ‘I have nothing whatever to say against the lady, who is extremely pleasant and affable, though, poor thing, she seems terribly low in her spirits; nor against the young people either, for nicer, or better-behaved young people cannot be.’\n\n‘Very well, ma’am,’ said Ralph, turning to the door, for these encomiums on poverty irritated him; ‘I have done my duty, and perhaps more than I ought: of course nobody will thank me for saying what I have.’\n\n‘I am sure I am very much obliged to you at least, sir,’ said Miss La Creevy in a gracious manner. ‘Would you do me the favour to look at a few specimens of my portrait painting?’\n\n‘You’re very good, ma’am,’ said Mr. Nickleby, making off with great speed; ‘but as I have a visit to pay upstairs, and my time is precious, I really can’t.’\n\n‘At any other time when you are passing, I shall be most happy,’ said Miss La Creevy. ‘Perhaps you will have the kindness to take a card of terms with you? Thank you--good-morning!’\n\n‘Good-morning, ma’am,’ said Ralph, shutting the door abruptly after him to prevent any further conversation. ‘Now for my sister-in-law. Bah!’\n\nClimbing up another perpendicular flight, composed with great mechanical ingenuity of nothing but corner stairs, Mr. Ralph Nickleby stopped to take breath on the landing, when he was overtaken by the handmaid, whom the politeness of Miss La Creevy had dispatched to announce him, and who had apparently been making a variety of unsuccessful attempts, since their last interview, to wipe her dirty face clean, upon an apron much dirtier.\n\n‘What name?’ said the girl.\n\n‘Nickleby,’ replied Ralph.\n\n‘Oh! Mrs. Nickleby,’ said the girl, throwing open the door, ‘here’s Mr Nickleby.’\n\nA lady in deep mourning rose as Mr. Ralph Nickleby entered, but appeared incapable of advancing to meet him, and leant upon the arm of a slight but very beautiful girl of about seventeen, who had been sitting by her. A youth, who appeared a year or two older, stepped forward and saluted Ralph as his uncle.\n\n‘Oh,’ growled Ralph, with an ill-favoured frown, ‘you are Nicholas, I suppose?’\n\n‘That is my name, sir,’ replied the youth.\n\n‘Put my hat down,’ said Ralph, imperiously. ‘Well, ma’am, how do you do? You must bear up against sorrow, ma’am; I always do.’\n\n‘Mine was no common loss!’ said Mrs. Nickleby, applying her handkerchief to her eyes.\n\n‘It was no UNcommon loss, ma’am,’ returned Ralph, as he coolly unbuttoned his spencer. ‘Husbands die every day, ma’am, and wives too.’\n\n‘And brothers also, sir,’ said Nicholas, with a glance of indignation.\n\n‘Yes, sir, and puppies, and pug-dogs likewise,’ replied his uncle, taking a chair. ‘You didn’t mention in your letter what my brother’s complaint was, ma’am.’\n\n‘The doctors could attribute it to no particular disease,’ said Mrs Nickleby; shedding tears. ‘We have too much reason to fear that he died of a broken heart.’\n\n‘Pooh!’ said Ralph, ‘there’s no such thing. I can understand a man’s dying of a broken neck, or suffering from a broken arm, or a broken head, or a broken leg, or a broken nose; but a broken heart!--nonsense, it’s the cant of the day. If a man can’t pay his debts, he dies of a broken heart, and his widow’s a martyr.’\n\n‘Some people, I believe, have no hearts to break,’ observed Nicholas, quietly.\n\n‘How old is this boy, for God’s sake?’ inquired Ralph, wheeling back his chair, and surveying his nephew from head to foot with intense scorn.\n\n‘Nicholas is very nearly nineteen,’ replied the widow.\n\n‘Nineteen, eh!’ said Ralph; ‘and what do you mean to do for your bread, sir?’\n\n‘Not to live upon my mother,’ replied Nicholas, his heart swelling as he spoke.\n\n‘You’d have little enough to live upon, if you did,’ retorted the uncle, eyeing him contemptuously.\n\n‘Whatever it be,’ said Nicholas, flushed with anger, ‘I shall not look to you to make it more.’\n\n‘Nicholas, my dear, recollect yourself,’ remonstrated Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘Dear Nicholas, pray,’ urged the young lady.\n\n‘Hold your tongue, sir,’ said Ralph. ‘Upon my word! Fine beginnings, Mrs Nickleby--fine beginnings!’\n\nMrs. Nickleby made no other reply than entreating Nicholas by a gesture to keep silent; and the uncle and nephew looked at each other for some seconds without speaking. The face of the old man was stern, hard-featured, and forbidding; that of the young one, open, handsome, and ingenuous. The old man’s eye was keen with the twinklings of avarice and cunning; the young man’s bright with the light of intelligence and spirit. His figure was somewhat slight, but manly and well formed; and, apart from all the grace of youth and comeliness, there was an emanation from the warm young heart in his look and bearing which kept the old man down.\n\nHowever striking such a contrast as this may be to lookers-on, none ever feel it with half the keenness or acuteness of perfection with which it strikes to the very soul of him whose inferiority it marks. It galled Ralph to the heart’s core, and he hated Nicholas from that hour.\n\nThe mutual inspection was at length brought to a close by Ralph withdrawing his eyes, with a great show of disdain, and calling Nicholas ‘a boy.’ This word is much used as a term of reproach by elderly gentlemen towards their juniors: probably with the view of deluding society into the belief that if they could be young again, they wouldn’t on any account.\n\n‘Well, ma’am,’ said Ralph, impatiently, ‘the creditors have administered, you tell me, and there’s nothing left for you?’\n\n‘Nothing,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘And you spent what little money you had, in coming all the way to London, to see what I could do for you?’ pursued Ralph.\n\n‘I hoped,’ faltered Mrs. Nickleby, ‘that you might have an opportunity of doing something for your brother’s children. It was his dying wish that I should appeal to you in their behalf.’\n\n‘I don’t know how it is,’ muttered Ralph, walking up and down the room, ‘but whenever a man dies without any property of his own, he always seems to think he has a right to dispose of other people’s. What is your daughter fit for, ma’am?’\n\n‘Kate has been well educated,’ sobbed Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Tell your uncle, my dear, how far you went in French and extras.’\n\nThe poor girl was about to murmur something, when her uncle stopped her, very unceremoniously.\n\n‘We must try and get you apprenticed at some boarding-school,’ said Ralph. ‘You have not been brought up too delicately for that, I hope?’\n\n‘No, indeed, uncle,’ replied the weeping girl. ‘I will try to do anything that will gain me a home and bread.’\n\n‘Well, well,’ said Ralph, a little softened, either by his niece’s beauty or her distress (stretch a point, and say the latter). ‘You must try it, and if the life is too hard, perhaps dressmaking or tambour-work will come lighter. Have YOU ever done anything, sir?’ (turning to his nephew.)\n\n‘No,’ replied Nicholas, bluntly.\n\n‘No, I thought not!’ said Ralph. ‘This is the way my brother brought up his children, ma’am.’\n\n‘Nicholas has not long completed such education as his poor father could give him,’ rejoined Mrs. Nickleby, ‘and he was thinking of--’\n\n‘Of making something of him someday,’ said Ralph. ‘The old story; always thinking, and never doing. If my brother had been a man of activity and prudence, he might have left you a rich woman, ma’am: and if he had turned his son into the world, as my father turned me, when I wasn’t as old as that boy by a year and a half, he would have been in a situation to help you, instead of being a burden upon you, and increasing your distress. My brother was a thoughtless, inconsiderate man, Mrs. Nickleby, and nobody, I am sure, can have better reason to feel that, than you.’\n\nThis appeal set the widow upon thinking that perhaps she might have made a more successful venture with her one thousand pounds, and then she began to reflect what a comfortable sum it would have been just then; which dismal thoughts made her tears flow faster, and in the excess of these griefs she (being a well-meaning woman enough, but weak withal) fell first to deploring her hard fate, and then to remarking, with many sobs, that to be sure she had been a slave to poor Nicholas, and had often told him she might have married better (as indeed she had, very often), and that she never knew in his lifetime how the money went, but that if he had confided in her they might all have been better off that day; with other bitter recollections common to most married ladies, either during their coverture, or afterwards, or at both periods. Mrs Nickleby concluded by lamenting that the dear departed had never deigned to profit by her advice, save on one occasion; which was a strictly veracious statement, inasmuch as he had only acted upon it once, and had ruined himself in consequence.\n\nMr. Ralph Nickleby heard all this with a half-smile; and when the widow had finished, quietly took up the subject where it had been left before the above outbreak.\n\n‘Are you willing to work, sir?’ he inquired, frowning on his nephew.\n\n‘Of course I am,’ replied Nicholas haughtily.\n\n‘Then see here, sir,’ said his uncle. ‘This caught my eye this morning, and you may thank your stars for it.’\n\nWith this exordium, Mr. Ralph Nickleby took a newspaper from his pocket, and after unfolding it, and looking for a short time among the advertisements, read as follows:\n\n‘“EDUCATION.--At Mr. Wackford Squeers’s Academy, Dotheboys Hall, at the delightful village of Dotheboys, near Greta Bridge in Yorkshire, Youth are boarded, clothed, booked, furnished with pocket-money, provided with all necessaries, instructed in all languages living and dead, mathematics, orthography, geometry, astronomy, trigonometry, the use of the globes, algebra, single stick (if required), writing, arithmetic, fortification, and every other branch of classical literature. Terms, twenty guineas per annum. No extras, no vacations, and diet unparalleled. Mr. Squeers is in town, and attends daily, from one till four, at the Saracen’s Head, Snow Hill. N.B. An able assistant wanted. Annual salary 5 pounds. A Master of Arts would be preferred.”\n\n‘There!’ said Ralph, folding the paper again. ‘Let him get that situation, and his fortune is made.’\n\n‘But he is not a Master of Arts,’ said Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘That,’ replied Ralph, ‘that, I think, can be got over.’\n\n‘But the salary is so small, and it is such a long way off, uncle!’ faltered Kate.\n\n‘Hush, Kate my dear,’ interposed Mrs. Nickleby; ‘your uncle must know best.’\n\n‘I say,’ repeated Ralph, tartly, ‘let him get that situation, and his fortune is made. If he don’t like that, let him get one for himself. Without friends, money, recommendation, or knowledge of business of any kind, let him find honest employment in London, which will keep him in shoe leather, and I’ll give him a thousand pounds. At least,’ said Mr Ralph Nickleby, checking himself, ‘I would if I had it.’\n\n‘Poor fellow!’ said the young lady. ‘Oh! uncle, must we be separated so soon!’\n\n‘Don’t tease your uncle with questions when he is thinking only for our good, my love,’ said Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Nicholas, my dear, I wish you would say something.’\n\n‘Yes, mother, yes,’ said Nicholas, who had hitherto remained silent and absorbed in thought. ‘If I am fortunate enough to be appointed to this post, sir, for which I am so imperfectly qualified, what will become of those I leave behind?’\n\n‘Your mother and sister, sir,’ replied Ralph, ‘will be provided for, in that case (not otherwise), by me, and placed in some sphere of life in which they will be able to be independent. That will be my immediate care; they will not remain as they are, one week after your departure, I will undertake.’\n\n‘Then,’ said Nicholas, starting gaily up, and wringing his uncle’s hand, ‘I am ready to do anything you wish me. Let us try our fortune with Mr Squeers at once; he can but refuse.’\n\n‘He won’t do that,’ said Ralph. ‘He will be glad to have you on my recommendation. Make yourself of use to him, and you’ll rise to be a partner in the establishment in no time. Bless me, only think! if he were to die, why your fortune’s made at once.’\n\n‘To be sure, I see it all,’ said poor Nicholas, delighted with a thousand visionary ideas, that his good spirits and his inexperience were conjuring up before him. ‘Or suppose some young nobleman who is being educated at the Hall, were to take a fancy to me, and get his father to appoint me his travelling tutor when he left, and when we come back from the continent, procured me some handsome appointment. Eh! uncle?’\n\n‘Ah, to be sure!’ sneered Ralph.\n\n‘And who knows, but when he came to see me when I was settled (as he would of course), he might fall in love with Kate, who would be keeping my house, and--and marry her, eh! uncle? Who knows?’\n\n‘Who, indeed!’ snarled Ralph.\n\n‘How happy we should be!’ cried Nicholas with enthusiasm. ‘The pain of parting is nothing to the joy of meeting again. Kate will be a beautiful woman, and I so proud to hear them say so, and mother so happy to be with us once again, and all these sad times forgotten, and--’ The picture was too bright a one to bear, and Nicholas, fairly overpowered by it, smiled faintly, and burst into tears.\n\nThis simple family, born and bred in retirement, and wholly unacquainted with what is called the world--a conventional phrase which, being interpreted, often signifieth all the rascals in it--mingled their tears together at the thought of their first separation; and, this first gush of feeling over, were proceeding to dilate with all the buoyancy of untried hope on the bright prospects before them, when Mr. Ralph Nickleby suggested, that if they lost time, some more fortunate candidate might deprive Nicholas of the stepping-stone to fortune which the advertisement pointed out, and so undermine all their air-built castles. This timely reminder effectually stopped the conversation. Nicholas, having carefully copied the address of Mr. Squeers, the uncle and nephew issued forth together in quest of that accomplished gentleman; Nicholas firmly persuading himself that he had done his relative great injustice in disliking him at first sight; and Mrs. Nickleby being at some pains to inform her daughter that she was sure he was a much more kindly disposed person than he seemed; which, Miss Nickleby dutifully remarked, he might very easily be.\n\nTo tell the truth, the good lady’s opinion had been not a little influenced by her brother-in-law’s appeal to her better understanding, and his implied compliment to her high deserts; and although she had dearly loved her husband, and still doted on her children, he had struck so successfully on one of those little jarring chords in the human heart (Ralph was well acquainted with its worst weaknesses, though he knew nothing of its best), that she had already begun seriously to consider herself the amiable and suffering victim of her late husband’s imprudence.\n\n# CHAPTER 4\n\nNicholas and his Uncle (to secure the Fortune without loss of time) wait upon Mr. Wackford Squeers, the Yorkshire Schoolmaster\nSnow Hill! What kind of place can the quiet townspeople who see the words emblazoned, in all the legibility of gilt letters and dark shading, on the north-country coaches, take Snow Hill to be? All people have some undefined and shadowy notion of a place whose name is frequently before their eyes, or often in their ears. What a vast number of random ideas there must be perpetually floating about, regarding this same Snow Hill. The name is such a good one. Snow Hill--Snow Hill too, coupled with a Saracen’s Head: picturing to us by a double association of ideas, something stern and rugged! A bleak desolate tract of country, open to piercing blasts and fierce wintry storms--a dark, cold, gloomy heath, lonely by day, and scarcely to be thought of by honest folks at night--a place which solitary wayfarers shun, and where desperate robbers congregate;--this, or something like this, should be the prevalent notion of Snow Hill, in those remote and rustic parts, through which the Saracen’s Head, like some grim apparition, rushes each day and night with mysterious and ghost-like punctuality; holding its swift and headlong course in all weathers, and seeming to bid defiance to the very elements themselves.\n\nThe reality is rather different, but by no means to be despised notwithstanding. There, at the very core of London, in the heart of its business and animation, in the midst of a whirl of noise and motion: stemming as it were the giant currents of life that flow ceaselessly on from different quarters, and meet beneath its walls: stands Newgate; and in that crowded street on which it frowns so darkly--within a few feet of the squalid tottering houses--upon the very spot on which the vendors of soup and fish and damaged fruit are now plying their trades--scores of human beings, amidst a roar of sounds to which even the tumult of a great city is as nothing, four, six, or eight strong men at a time, have been hurried violently and swiftly from the world, when the scene has been rendered frightful with excess of human life; when curious eyes have glared from casement and house-top, and wall and pillar; and when, in the mass of white and upturned faces, the dying wretch, in his all-comprehensive look of agony, has met not one--not one--that bore the impress of pity or compassion.\n\nNear to the jail, and by consequence near to Smithfield also, and the Compter, and the bustle and noise of the city; and just on that particular part of Snow Hill where omnibus horses going eastward seriously think of falling down on purpose, and where horses in hackney cabriolets going westward not unfrequently fall by accident, is the coach-yard of the Saracen’s Head Inn; its portal guarded by two Saracens’ heads and shoulders, which it was once the pride and glory of the choice spirits of this metropolis to pull down at night, but which have for some time remained in undisturbed tranquillity; possibly because this species of humour is now confined to St James’s parish, where door knockers are preferred as being more portable, and bell-wires esteemed as convenient toothpicks. Whether this be the reason or not, there they are, frowning upon you from each side of the gateway. The inn itself garnished with another Saracen’s Head, frowns upon you from the top of the yard; while from the door of the hind boot of all the red coaches that are standing therein, there glares a small Saracen’s Head, with a twin expression to the large Saracens’ Heads below, so that the general appearance of the pile is decidedly of the Saracenic order.\n\nWhen you walk up this yard, you will see the booking-office on your left, and the tower of St Sepulchre’s church, darting abruptly up into the sky, on your right, and a gallery of bedrooms on both sides. Just before you, you will observe a long window with the words ‘coffee-room’ legibly painted above it; and looking out of that window, you would have seen in addition, if you had gone at the right time, Mr. Wackford Squeers with his hands in his pockets.\n\nMr. Squeers’s appearance was not prepossessing. He had but one eye, and the popular prejudice runs in favour of two. The eye he had, was unquestionably useful, but decidedly not ornamental: being of a greenish grey, and in shape resembling the fan-light of a street door. The blank side of his face was much wrinkled and puckered up, which gave him a very sinister appearance, especially when he smiled, at which times his expression bordered closely on the villainous. His hair was very flat and shiny, save at the ends, where it was brushed stiffly up from a low protruding forehead, which assorted well with his harsh voice and coarse manner. He was about two or three and fifty, and a trifle below the middle size; he wore a white neckerchief with long ends, and a suit of scholastic black; but his coat sleeves being a great deal too long, and his trousers a great deal too short, he appeared ill at ease in his clothes, and as if he were in a perpetual state of astonishment at finding himself so respectable.\n\nMr. Squeers was standing in a box by one of the coffee-room fire-places, fitted with one such table as is usually seen in coffee-rooms, and two of extraordinary shapes and dimensions made to suit the angles of the partition. In a corner of the seat, was a very small deal trunk, tied round with a scanty piece of cord; and on the trunk was perched--his lace-up half-boots and corduroy trousers dangling in the air--a diminutive boy, with his shoulders drawn up to his ears, and his hands planted on his knees, who glanced timidly at the schoolmaster, from time to time, with evident dread and apprehension.\n\n‘Half-past three,’ muttered Mr. Squeers, turning from the window, and looking sulkily at the coffee-room clock. ‘There will be nobody here today.’\n\nMuch vexed by this reflection, Mr. Squeers looked at the little boy to see whether he was doing anything he could beat him for. As he happened not to be doing anything at all, he merely boxed his ears, and told him not to do it again.\n\n‘At Midsummer,’ muttered Mr. Squeers, resuming his complaint, ‘I took down ten boys; ten twenties is two hundred pound. I go back at eight o’clock tomorrow morning, and have got only three--three oughts is an ought--three twos is six--sixty pound. What’s come of all the boys? what’s parents got in their heads? what does it all mean?’\n\nHere the little boy on the top of the trunk gave a violent sneeze.\n\n‘Halloa, sir!’ growled the schoolmaster, turning round. ‘What’s that, sir?’\n\n‘Nothing, please sir,’ replied the little boy.\n\n‘Nothing, sir!’ exclaimed Mr. Squeers.\n\n‘Please sir, I sneezed,’ rejoined the boy, trembling till the little trunk shook under him.\n\n‘Oh! sneezed, did you?’ retorted Mr. Squeers. ‘Then what did you say “nothing” for, sir?’\n\nIn default of a better answer to this question, the little boy screwed a couple of knuckles into each of his eyes and began to cry, wherefore Mr Squeers knocked him off the trunk with a blow on one side of the face, and knocked him on again with a blow on the other.\n\n‘Wait till I get you down into Yorkshire, my young gentleman,’ said Mr Squeers, ‘and then I’ll give you the rest. Will you hold that noise, sir?’\n\n‘Ye--ye--yes,’ sobbed the little boy, rubbing his face very hard with the Beggar’s Petition in printed calico.\n\n‘Then do so at once, sir,’ said Squeers. ‘Do you hear?’\n\nAs this admonition was accompanied with a threatening gesture, and uttered with a savage aspect, the little boy rubbed his face harder, as if to keep the tears back; and, beyond alternately sniffing and choking, gave no further vent to his emotions.\n\n‘Mr. Squeers,’ said the waiter, looking in at this juncture; ‘here’s a gentleman asking for you at the bar.’\n\n‘Show the gentleman in, Richard,’ replied Mr. Squeers, in a soft voice. ‘Put your handkerchief in your pocket, you little scoundrel, or I’ll murder you when the gentleman goes.’\n\nThe schoolmaster had scarcely uttered these words in a fierce whisper, when the stranger entered. Affecting not to see him, Mr. Squeers feigned to be intent upon mending a pen, and offering benevolent advice to his youthful pupil.\n\n‘My dear child,’ said Mr. Squeers, ‘all people have their trials. This early trial of yours that is fit to make your little heart burst, and your very eyes come out of your head with crying, what is it? Nothing; less than nothing. You are leaving your friends, but you will have a father in me, my dear, and a mother in Mrs. Squeers. At the delightful village of Dotheboys, near Greta Bridge in Yorkshire, where youth are boarded, clothed, booked, washed, furnished with pocket-money, provided with all necessaries--’\n\n‘It IS the gentleman,’ observed the stranger, stopping the schoolmaster in the rehearsal of his advertisement. ‘Mr. Squeers, I believe, sir?’\n\n‘The same, sir,’ said Mr. Squeers, with an assumption of extreme surprise.\n\n‘The gentleman,’ said the stranger, ‘that advertised in the Times newspaper?’\n\n‘--Morning Post, Chronicle, Herald, and Advertiser, regarding the Academy called Dotheboys Hall at the delightful village of Dotheboys, near Greta Bridge in Yorkshire,’ added Mr. Squeers. ‘You come on business, sir. I see by my young friends. How do you do, my little gentleman? and how do you do, sir?’ With this salutation Mr. Squeers patted the heads of two hollow-eyed, small-boned little boys, whom the applicant had brought with him, and waited for further communications.\n\n‘I am in the oil and colour way. My name is Snawley, sir,’ said the stranger.\n\nSqueers inclined his head as much as to say, ‘And a remarkably pretty name, too.’\n\nThe stranger continued. ‘I have been thinking, Mr. Squeers, of placing my two boys at your school.’\n\n‘It is not for me to say so, sir,’ replied Mr. Squeers, ‘but I don’t think you could possibly do a better thing.’\n\n‘Hem!’ said the other. ‘Twenty pounds per annewum, I believe, Mr Squeers?’\n\n‘Guineas,’ rejoined the schoolmaster, with a persuasive smile.\n\n‘Pounds for two, I think, Mr. Squeers,’ said Mr. Snawley, solemnly.\n\n‘I don’t think it could be done, sir,’ replied Squeers, as if he had never considered the proposition before. ‘Let me see; four fives is twenty, double that, and deduct the--well, a pound either way shall not stand betwixt us. You must recommend me to your connection, sir, and make it up that way.’\n\n‘They are not great eaters,’ said Mr. Snawley.\n\n‘Oh! that doesn’t matter at all,’ replied Squeers. ‘We don’t consider the boys’ appetites at our establishment.’ This was strictly true; they did not.\n\n‘Every wholesome luxury, sir, that Yorkshire can afford,’ continued Squeers; ‘every beautiful moral that Mrs. Squeers can instil; every--in short, every comfort of a home that a boy could wish for, will be theirs, Mr. Snawley.’\n\n‘I should wish their morals to be particularly attended to,’ said Mr Snawley.\n\n‘I am glad of that, sir,’ replied the schoolmaster, drawing himself up. ‘They have come to the right shop for morals, sir.’\n\n‘You are a moral man yourself,’ said Mr. Snawley.\n\n‘I rather believe I am, sir,’ replied Squeers.\n\n‘I have the satisfaction to know you are, sir,’ said Mr. Snawley. ‘I asked one of your references, and he said you were pious.’\n\n‘Well, sir, I hope I am a little in that line,’ replied Squeers.\n\n‘I hope I am also,’ rejoined the other. ‘Could I say a few words with you in the next box?’\n\n‘By all means,’ rejoined Squeers with a grin. ‘My dears, will you speak to your new playfellow a minute or two? That is one of my boys, sir. Belling his name is,--a Taunton boy that, sir.’\n\n‘Is he, indeed?’ rejoined Mr. Snawley, looking at the poor little urchin as if he were some extraordinary natural curiosity.\n\n‘He goes down with me tomorrow, sir,’ said Squeers. ‘That’s his luggage that he is a sitting upon now. Each boy is required to bring, sir, two suits of clothes, six shirts, six pair of stockings, two nightcaps, two pocket-handkerchiefs, two pair of shoes, two hats, and a razor.’\n\n‘A razor!’ exclaimed Mr. Snawley, as they walked into the next box. ‘What for?’\n\n‘To shave with,’ replied Squeers, in a slow and measured tone.\n\nThere was not much in these three words, but there must have been something in the manner in which they were said, to attract attention; for the schoolmaster and his companion looked steadily at each other for a few seconds, and then exchanged a very meaning smile. Snawley was a sleek, flat-nosed man, clad in sombre garments, and long black gaiters, and bearing in his countenance an expression of much mortification and sanctity; so, his smiling without any obvious reason was the more remarkable.\n\n‘Up to what age do you keep boys at your school then?’ he asked at length.\n\n‘Just as long as their friends make the quarterly payments to my agent in town, or until such time as they run away,’ replied Squeers. ‘Let us understand each other; I see we may safely do so. What are these boys;--natural children?’\n\n‘No,’ rejoined Snawley, meeting the gaze of the schoolmaster’s one eye. ‘They ain’t.’\n\n‘I thought they might be,’ said Squeers, coolly. ‘We have a good many of them; that boy’s one.’\n\n‘Him in the next box?’ said Snawley.\n\nSqueers nodded in the affirmative; his companion took another peep at the little boy on the trunk, and, turning round again, looked as if he were quite disappointed to see him so much like other boys, and said he should hardly have thought it.\n\n‘He is,’ cried Squeers. ‘But about these boys of yours; you wanted to speak to me?’\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Snawley. ‘The fact is, I am not their father, Mr. Squeers. I’m only their father-in-law.’\n\n‘Oh! Is that it?’ said the schoolmaster. ‘That explains it at once. I was wondering what the devil you were going to send them to Yorkshire for. Ha! ha! Oh, I understand now.’\n\n‘You see I have married the mother,’ pursued Snawley; ‘it’s expensive keeping boys at home, and as she has a little money in her own right, I am afraid (women are so very foolish, Mr. Squeers) that she might be led to squander it on them, which would be their ruin, you know.’\n\n‘I see,’ returned Squeers, throwing himself back in his chair, and waving his hand.\n\n‘And this,’ resumed Snawley, ‘has made me anxious to put them to some school a good distance off, where there are no holidays--none of those ill-judged coming home twice a year that unsettle children’s minds so--and where they may rough it a little--you comprehend?’\n\n‘The payments regular, and no questions asked,’ said Squeers, nodding his head.\n\n‘That’s it, exactly,’ rejoined the other. ‘Morals strictly attended to, though.’\n\n‘Strictly,’ said Squeers.\n\n‘Not too much writing home allowed, I suppose?’ said the father-in-law, hesitating.\n\n‘None, except a circular at Christmas, to say they never were so happy, and hope they may never be sent for,’ rejoined Squeers.\n\n‘Nothing could be better,’ said the father-in-law, rubbing his hands.\n\n‘Then, as we understand each other,’ said Squeers, ‘will you allow me to ask you whether you consider me a highly virtuous, exemplary, and well-conducted man in private life; and whether, as a person whose business it is to take charge of youth, you place the strongest confidence in my unimpeachable integrity, liberality, religious principles, and ability?’\n\n‘Certainly I do,’ replied the father-in-law, reciprocating the schoolmaster’s grin.\n\n‘Perhaps you won’t object to say that, if I make you a reference?’\n\n‘Not the least in the world.’\n\n‘That’s your sort!’ said Squeers, taking up a pen; ‘this is doing business, and that’s what I like.’\n\nHaving entered Mr. Snawley’s address, the schoolmaster had next to perform the still more agreeable office of entering the receipt of the first quarter’s payment in advance, which he had scarcely completed, when another voice was heard inquiring for Mr. Squeers.\n\n‘Here he is,’ replied the schoolmaster; ‘what is it?’\n\n‘Only a matter of business, sir,’ said Ralph Nickleby, presenting himself, closely followed by Nicholas. ‘There was an advertisement of yours in the papers this morning?’\n\n‘There was, sir. This way, if you please,’ said Squeers, who had by this time got back to the box by the fire-place. ‘Won’t you be seated?’\n\n‘Why, I think I will,’ replied Ralph, suiting the action to the word, and placing his hat on the table before him. ‘This is my nephew, sir, Mr Nicholas Nickleby.’\n\n‘How do you do, sir?’ said Squeers.\n\nNicholas bowed, said he was very well, and seemed very much astonished at the outward appearance of the proprietor of Dotheboys Hall: as indeed he was.\n\n‘Perhaps you recollect me?’ said Ralph, looking narrowly at the schoolmaster.\n\n‘You paid me a small account at each of my half-yearly visits to town, for some years, I think, sir,’ replied Squeers.\n\n‘I did,’ rejoined Ralph.\n\n‘For the parents of a boy named Dorker, who unfortunately--’\n\n‘--unfortunately died at Dotheboys Hall,’ said Ralph, finishing the sentence.\n\n‘I remember very well, sir,’ rejoined Squeers. ‘Ah! Mrs. Squeers, sir, was as partial to that lad as if he had been her own; the attention, sir, that was bestowed upon that boy in his illness! Dry toast and warm tea offered him every night and morning when he couldn’t swallow anything--a candle in his bedroom on the very night he died--the best dictionary sent up for him to lay his head upon--I don’t regret it though. It is a pleasant thing to reflect that one did one’s duty by him.’\n\nRalph smiled, as if he meant anything but smiling, and looked round at the strangers present.\n\n‘These are only some pupils of mine,’ said Wackford Squeers, pointing to the little boy on the trunk and the two little boys on the floor, who had been staring at each other without uttering a word, and writhing their bodies into most remarkable contortions, according to the custom of little boys when they first become acquainted. ‘This gentleman, sir, is a parent who is kind enough to compliment me upon the course of education adopted at Dotheboys Hall, which is situated, sir, at the delightful village of Dotheboys, near Greta Bridge in Yorkshire, where youth are boarded, clothed, booked, washed, furnished with pocket-money--’\n\n‘Yes, we know all about that, sir,’ interrupted Ralph, testily. ‘It’s in the advertisement.’\n\n‘You are very right, sir; it IS in the advertisement,’ replied Squeers.\n\n‘And in the matter of fact besides,’ interrupted Mr. Snawley. ‘I feel bound to assure you, sir, and I am proud to have this opportunity OF assuring you, that I consider Mr. Squeers a gentleman highly virtuous, exemplary, well conducted, and--’\n\n‘I make no doubt of it, sir,’ interrupted Ralph, checking the torrent of recommendation; ‘no doubt of it at all. Suppose we come to business?’\n\n‘With all my heart, sir,’ rejoined Squeers. ‘“Never postpone business,” is the very first lesson we instil into our commercial pupils. Master Belling, my dear, always remember that; do you hear?’\n\n‘Yes, sir,’ repeated Master Belling.\n\n‘He recollects what it is, does he?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Tell the gentleman,’ said Squeers.\n\n‘“Never,”’ repeated Master Belling.\n\n‘Very good,’ said Squeers; ‘go on.’\n\n‘Never,’ repeated Master Belling again.\n\n‘Very good indeed,’ said Squeers. ‘Yes.’\n\n‘P,’ suggested Nicholas, good-naturedly.\n\n‘Perform--business!’ said Master Belling. ‘Never--perform--business!’\n\n‘Very well, sir,’ said Squeers, darting a withering look at the culprit. ‘You and I will perform a little business on our private account by-and-by.’\n\n‘And just now,’ said Ralph, ‘we had better transact our own, perhaps.’\n\n‘If you please,’ said Squeers.\n\n‘Well,’ resumed Ralph, ‘it’s brief enough; soon broached; and I hope easily concluded. You have advertised for an able assistant, sir?’\n\n‘Precisely so,’ said Squeers.\n\n‘And you really want one?’\n\n‘Certainly,’ answered Squeers.\n\n‘Here he is!’ said Ralph. ‘My nephew Nicholas, hot from school, with everything he learnt there, fermenting in his head, and nothing fermenting in his pocket, is just the man you want.’\n\n‘I am afraid,’ said Squeers, perplexed with such an application from a youth of Nicholas’s figure, ‘I am afraid the young man won’t suit me.’\n\n‘Yes, he will,’ said Ralph; ‘I know better. Don’t be cast down, sir; you will be teaching all the young noblemen in Dotheboys Hall in less than a week’s time, unless this gentleman is more obstinate than I take him to be.’\n\n‘I fear, sir,’ said Nicholas, addressing Mr. Squeers, ‘that you object to my youth, and to my not being a Master of Arts?’\n\n‘The absence of a college degree IS an objection,’ replied Squeers, looking as grave as he could, and considerably puzzled, no less by the contrast between the simplicity of the nephew and the worldly manner of the uncle, than by the incomprehensible allusion to the young noblemen under his tuition.\n\n‘Look here, sir,’ said Ralph; ‘I’ll put this matter in its true light in two seconds.’\n\n‘If you’ll have the goodness,’ rejoined Squeers.\n\n‘This is a boy, or a youth, or a lad, or a young man, or a hobbledehoy, or whatever you like to call him, of eighteen or nineteen, or thereabouts,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘That I see,’ observed the schoolmaster.\n\n‘So do I,’ said Mr. Snawley, thinking it as well to back his new friend occasionally.\n\n‘His father is dead, he is wholly ignorant of the world, has no resources whatever, and wants something to do,’ said Ralph. ‘I recommend him to this splendid establishment of yours, as an opening which will lead him to fortune if he turns it to proper account. Do you see that?’\n\n‘Everybody must see that,’ replied Squeers, half imitating the sneer with which the old gentleman was regarding his unconscious relative.\n\n‘I do, of course,’ said Nicholas, eagerly.\n\n‘He does, of course, you observe,’ said Ralph, in the same dry, hard manner. ‘If any caprice of temper should induce him to cast aside this golden opportunity before he has brought it to perfection, I consider myself absolved from extending any assistance to his mother and sister. Look at him, and think of the use he may be to you in half-a-dozen ways! Now, the question is, whether, for some time to come at all events, he won’t serve your purpose better than twenty of the kind of people you would get under ordinary circumstances. Isn’t that a question for consideration?’\n\n‘Yes, it is,’ said Squeers, answering a nod of Ralph’s head with a nod of his own.\n\n‘Good,’ rejoined Ralph. ‘Let me have two words with you.’\n\nThe two words were had apart; in a couple of minutes Mr. Wackford Squeers announced that Mr. Nicholas Nickleby was, from that moment, thoroughly nominated to, and installed in, the office of first assistant master at Dotheboys Hall.\n\n‘Your uncle’s recommendation has done it, Mr. Nickleby,’ said Wackford Squeers.\n\nNicholas, overjoyed at his success, shook his uncle’s hand warmly, and could almost have worshipped Squeers upon the spot.\n\n‘He is an odd-looking man,’ thought Nicholas. ‘What of that? Porson was an odd-looking man, and so was Doctor Johnson; all these bookworms are.’\n\n‘At eight o’clock tomorrow morning, Mr. Nickleby,’ said Squeers, ‘the coach starts. You must be here at a quarter before, as we take these boys with us.’\n\n‘Certainly, sir,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘And your fare down, I have paid,’ growled Ralph. ‘So, you’ll have nothing to do but keep yourself warm.’\n\nHere was another instance of his uncle’s generosity! Nicholas felt his unexpected kindness so much, that he could scarcely find words to thank him; indeed, he had not found half enough, when they took leave of the schoolmaster, and emerged from the Saracen’s Head gateway.\n\n‘I shall be here in the morning to see you fairly off,’ said Ralph. ‘No skulking!’\n\n‘Thank you, sir,’ replied Nicholas; ‘I never shall forget this kindness.’\n\n‘Take care you don’t,’ replied his uncle. ‘You had better go home now, and pack up what you have got to pack. Do you think you could find your way to Golden Square first?’\n\n‘Certainly,’ said Nicholas. ‘I can easily inquire.’\n\n‘Leave these papers with my clerk, then,’ said Ralph, producing a small parcel, ‘and tell him to wait till I come home.’\n\nNicholas cheerfully undertook the errand, and bidding his worthy uncle an affectionate farewell, which that warm-hearted old gentleman acknowledged by a growl, hastened away to execute his commission.\n\nHe found Golden Square in due course; Mr. Noggs, who had stepped out for a minute or so to the public-house, was opening the door with a latch-key, as he reached the steps.\n\n‘What’s that?’ inquired Noggs, pointing to the parcel.\n\n‘Papers from my uncle,’ replied Nicholas; ‘and you’re to have the goodness to wait till he comes home, if you please.’\n\n‘Uncle!’ cried Noggs.\n\n‘Mr. Nickleby,’ said Nicholas in explanation.\n\n‘Come in,’ said Newman.\n\nWithout another word he led Nicholas into the passage, and thence into the official pantry at the end of it, where he thrust him into a chair, and mounting upon his high stool, sat, with his arms hanging, straight down by his sides, gazing fixedly upon him, as from a tower of observation.\n\n‘There is no answer,’ said Nicholas, laying the parcel on a table beside him.\n\nNewman said nothing, but folding his arms, and thrusting his head forward so as to obtain a nearer view of Nicholas’s face, scanned his features closely.\n\n‘No answer,’ said Nicholas, speaking very loud, under the impression that Newman Noggs was deaf.\n\nNewman placed his hands upon his knees, and, without uttering a syllable, continued the same close scrutiny of his companion’s face.\n\nThis was such a very singular proceeding on the part of an utter stranger, and his appearance was so extremely peculiar, that Nicholas, who had a sufficiently keen sense of the ridiculous, could not refrain from breaking into a smile as he inquired whether Mr. Noggs had any commands for him.\n\nNoggs shook his head and sighed; upon which Nicholas rose, and remarking that he required no rest, bade him good-morning.\n\nIt was a great exertion for Newman Noggs, and nobody knows to this day how he ever came to make it, the other party being wholly unknown to him, but he drew a long breath and actually said, out loud, without once stopping, that if the young gentleman did not object to tell, he should like to know what his uncle was going to do for him.\n\nNicholas had not the least objection in the world, but on the contrary was rather pleased to have an opportunity of talking on the subject which occupied his thoughts; so, he sat down again, and (his sanguine imagination warming as he spoke) entered into a fervent and glowing description of all the honours and advantages to be derived from his appointment at that seat of learning, Dotheboys Hall.\n\n‘But, what’s the matter--are you ill?’ said Nicholas, suddenly breaking off, as his companion, after throwing himself into a variety of uncouth attitudes, thrust his hands under the stool, and cracked his finger-joints as if he were snapping all the bones in his hands.\n\nNewman Noggs made no reply, but went on shrugging his shoulders and cracking his finger-joints; smiling horribly all the time, and looking steadfastly at nothing, out of the tops of his eyes, in a most ghastly manner.\n\nAt first, Nicholas thought the mysterious man was in a fit, but, on further consideration, decided that he was in liquor, under which circumstances he deemed it prudent to make off at once. He looked back when he had got the street-door open. Newman Noggs was still indulging in the same extraordinary gestures, and the cracking of his fingers sounded louder that ever.\n\n# CHAPTER 5\n\nNicholas starts for Yorkshire. Of his Leave-taking and his Fellow-Travellers, and what befell them on the Road\nIf tears dropped into a trunk were charms to preserve its owner from sorrow and misfortune, Nicholas Nickleby would have commenced his expedition under most happy auspices. There was so much to be done, and so little time to do it in; so many kind words to be spoken, and such bitter pain in the hearts in which they rose to impede their utterance; that the little preparations for his journey were made mournfully indeed. A hundred things which the anxious care of his mother and sister deemed indispensable for his comfort, Nicholas insisted on leaving behind, as they might prove of some after use, or might be convertible into money if occasion required. A hundred affectionate contests on such points as these, took place on the sad night which preceded his departure; and, as the termination of every angerless dispute brought them nearer and nearer to the close of their slight preparations, Kate grew busier and busier, and wept more silently.\n\nThe box was packed at last, and then there came supper, with some little delicacy provided for the occasion, and as a set-off against the expense of which, Kate and her mother had feigned to dine when Nicholas was out. The poor lad nearly choked himself by attempting to partake of it, and almost suffocated himself in affecting a jest or two, and forcing a melancholy laugh. Thus, they lingered on till the hour of separating for the night was long past; and then they found that they might as well have given vent to their real feelings before, for they could not suppress them, do what they would. So, they let them have their way, and even that was a relief.\n\nNicholas slept well till six next morning; dreamed of home, or of what was home once--no matter which, for things that are changed or gone will come back as they used to be, thank God! in sleep--and rose quite brisk and gay. He wrote a few lines in pencil, to say the goodbye which he was afraid to pronounce himself, and laying them, with half his scanty stock of money, at his sister’s door, shouldered his box and crept softly downstairs.\n\n‘Is that you, Hannah?’ cried a voice from Miss La Creevy’s sitting-room, whence shone the light of a feeble candle.\n\n‘It is I, Miss La Creevy,’ said Nicholas, putting down the box and looking in.\n\n‘Bless us!’ exclaimed Miss La Creevy, starting and putting her hand to her curl-papers. ‘You’re up very early, Mr. Nickleby.’\n\n‘So are you,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘It’s the fine arts that bring me out of bed, Mr. Nickleby,’ returned the lady. ‘I’m waiting for the light to carry out an idea.’\n\nMiss La Creevy had got up early to put a fancy nose into a miniature of an ugly little boy, destined for his grandmother in the country, who was expected to bequeath him property if he was like the family.\n\n‘To carry out an idea,’ repeated Miss La Creevy; ‘and that’s the great convenience of living in a thoroughfare like the Strand. When I want a nose or an eye for any particular sitter, I have only to look out of window and wait till I get one.’\n\n‘Does it take long to get a nose, now?’ inquired Nicholas, smiling.\n\n‘Why, that depends in a great measure on the pattern,’ replied Miss La Creevy. ‘Snubs and Romans are plentiful enough, and there are flats of all sorts and sizes when there’s a meeting at Exeter Hall; but perfect aquilines, I am sorry to say, are scarce, and we generally use them for uniforms or public characters.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ said Nicholas. ‘If I should meet with any in my travels, I’ll endeavour to sketch them for you.’\n\n‘You don’t mean to say that you are really going all the way down into Yorkshire this cold winter’s weather, Mr. Nickleby?’ said Miss La Creevy. ‘I heard something of it last night.’\n\n‘I do, indeed,’ replied Nicholas. ‘Needs must, you know, when somebody drives. Necessity is my driver, and that is only another name for the same gentleman.’\n\n‘Well, I am very sorry for it; that’s all I can say,’ said Miss La Creevy; ‘as much on your mother’s and sister’s account as on yours. Your sister is a very pretty young lady, Mr. Nickleby, and that is an additional reason why she should have somebody to protect her. I persuaded her to give me a sitting or two, for the street-door case. ‘Ah! she’ll make a sweet miniature.’ As Miss La Creevy spoke, she held up an ivory countenance intersected with very perceptible sky-blue veins, and regarded it with so much complacency, that Nicholas quite envied her.\n\n‘If you ever have an opportunity of showing Kate some little kindness,’ said Nicholas, presenting his hand, ‘I think you will.’\n\n‘Depend upon that,’ said the good-natured miniature painter; ‘and God bless you, Mr. Nickleby; and I wish you well.’\n\nIt was very little that Nicholas knew of the world, but he guessed enough about its ways to think, that if he gave Miss La Creevy one little kiss, perhaps she might not be the less kindly disposed towards those he was leaving behind. So, he gave her three or four with a kind of jocose gallantry, and Miss La Creevy evinced no greater symptoms of displeasure than declaring, as she adjusted her yellow turban, that she had never heard of such a thing, and couldn’t have believed it possible.\n\nHaving terminated the unexpected interview in this satisfactory manner, Nicholas hastily withdrew himself from the house. By the time he had found a man to carry his box it was only seven o’clock, so he walked slowly on, a little in advance of the porter, and very probably with not half as light a heart in his breast as the man had, although he had no waistcoat to cover it with, and had evidently, from the appearance of his other garments, been spending the night in a stable, and taking his breakfast at a pump.\n\nRegarding, with no small curiosity and interest, all the busy preparations for the coming day which every street and almost every house displayed; and thinking, now and then, that it seemed rather hard that so many people of all ranks and stations could earn a livelihood in London, and that he should be compelled to journey so far in search of one; Nicholas speedily arrived at the Saracen’s Head, Snow Hill. Having dismissed his attendant, and seen the box safely deposited in the coach-office, he looked into the coffee-room in search of Mr. Squeers.\n\nHe found that learned gentleman sitting at breakfast, with the three little boys before noticed, and two others who had turned up by some lucky chance since the interview of the previous day, ranged in a row on the opposite seat. Mr. Squeers had before him a small measure of coffee, a plate of hot toast, and a cold round of beef; but he was at that moment intent on preparing breakfast for the little boys.\n\n‘This is twopenn’orth of milk, is it, waiter?’ said Mr. Squeers, looking down into a large blue mug, and slanting it gently, so as to get an accurate view of the quantity of liquid contained in it.\n\n‘That’s twopenn’orth, sir,’ replied the waiter.\n\n‘What a rare article milk is, to be sure, in London!’ said Mr. Squeers, with a sigh. ‘Just fill that mug up with lukewarm water, William, will you?’\n\n‘To the wery top, sir?’ inquired the waiter. ‘Why, the milk will be drownded.’\n\n‘Never you mind that,’ replied Mr. Squeers. ‘Serve it right for being so dear. You ordered that thick bread and butter for three, did you?’\n\n‘Coming directly, sir.’\n\n‘You needn’t hurry yourself,’ said Squeers; ‘there’s plenty of time. Conquer your passions, boys, and don’t be eager after vittles.’ As he uttered this moral precept, Mr. Squeers took a large bite out of the cold beef, and recognised Nicholas.\n\n‘Sit down, Mr. Nickleby,’ said Squeers. ‘Here we are, a breakfasting you see!’\n\nNicholas did NOT see that anybody was breakfasting, except Mr. Squeers; but he bowed with all becoming reverence, and looked as cheerful as he could.\n\n‘Oh! that’s the milk and water, is it, William?’ said Squeers. ‘Very good; don’t forget the bread and butter presently.’\n\nAt this fresh mention of the bread and butter, the five little boys looked very eager, and followed the waiter out, with their eyes; meanwhile Mr. Squeers tasted the milk and water.\n\n‘Ah!’ said that gentleman, smacking his lips, ‘here’s richness! Think of the many beggars and orphans in the streets that would be glad of this, little boys. A shocking thing hunger, isn’t it, Mr. Nickleby?’\n\n‘Very shocking, sir,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘When I say number one,’ pursued Mr. Squeers, putting the mug before the children, ‘the boy on the left hand nearest the window may take a drink; and when I say number two, the boy next him will go in, and so till we come to number five, which is the last boy. Are you ready?’\n\n‘Yes, sir,’ cried all the little boys with great eagerness.\n\n‘That’s right,’ said Squeers, calmly getting on with his breakfast; ‘keep ready till I tell you to begin. Subdue your appetites, my dears, and you’ve conquered human natur. This is the way we inculcate strength of mind, Mr. Nickleby,’ said the schoolmaster, turning to Nicholas, and speaking with his mouth very full of beef and toast.\n\nNicholas murmured something--he knew not what--in reply; and the little boys, dividing their gaze between the mug, the bread and butter (which had by this time arrived), and every morsel which Mr. Squeers took into his mouth, remained with strained eyes in torments of expectation.\n\n‘Thank God for a good breakfast,’ said Squeers, when he had finished. ‘Number one may take a drink.’\n\nNumber one seized the mug ravenously, and had just drunk enough to make him wish for more, when Mr. Squeers gave the signal for number two, who gave up at the same interesting moment to number three; and the process was repeated until the milk and water terminated with number five.\n\n‘And now,’ said the schoolmaster, dividing the bread and butter for three into as many portions as there were children, ‘you had better look sharp with your breakfast, for the horn will blow in a minute or two, and then every boy leaves off.’\n\nPermission being thus given to fall to, the boys began to eat voraciously, and in desperate haste: while the schoolmaster (who was in high good humour after his meal) picked his teeth with a fork, and looked smilingly on. In a very short time, the horn was heard.\n\n‘I thought it wouldn’t be long,’ said Squeers, jumping up and producing a little basket from under the seat; ‘put what you haven’t had time to eat, in here, boys! You’ll want it on the road!’\n\nNicholas was considerably startled by these very economical arrangements; but he had no time to reflect upon them, for the little boys had to be got up to the top of the coach, and their boxes had to be brought out and put in, and Mr. Squeers’s luggage was to be seen carefully deposited in the boot, and all these offices were in his department. He was in the full heat and bustle of concluding these operations, when his uncle, Mr. Ralph Nickleby, accosted him.\n\n‘Oh! here you are, sir!’ said Ralph. ‘Here are your mother and sister, sir.’\n\n‘Where?’ cried Nicholas, looking hastily round.\n\n‘Here!’ replied his uncle. ‘Having too much money and nothing at all to do with it, they were paying a hackney coach as I came up, sir.’\n\n‘We were afraid of being too late to see him before he went away from us,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, embracing her son, heedless of the unconcerned lookers-on in the coach-yard.\n\n‘Very good, ma’am,’ returned Ralph, ‘you’re the best judge of course. I merely said that you were paying a hackney coach. I never pay a hackney coach, ma’am; I never hire one. I haven’t been in a hackney coach of my own hiring, for thirty years, and I hope I shan’t be for thirty more, if I live as long.’\n\n‘I should never have forgiven myself if I had not seen him,’ said Mrs Nickleby. ‘Poor dear boy--going away without his breakfast too, because he feared to distress us!’\n\n‘Mighty fine certainly,’ said Ralph, with great testiness. ‘When I first went to business, ma’am, I took a penny loaf and a ha’porth of milk for my breakfast as I walked to the city every morning; what do you say to that, ma’am? Breakfast! Bah!’\n\n‘Now, Nickleby,’ said Squeers, coming up at the moment buttoning his greatcoat; ‘I think you’d better get up behind. I’m afraid of one of them boys falling off and then there’s twenty pound a year gone.’\n\n‘Dear Nicholas,’ whispered Kate, touching her brother’s arm, ‘who is that vulgar man?’\n\n‘Eh!’ growled Ralph, whose quick ears had caught the inquiry. ‘Do you wish to be introduced to Mr. Squeers, my dear?’\n\n‘That the schoolmaster! No, uncle. Oh no!’ replied Kate, shrinking back.\n\n‘I’m sure I heard you say as much, my dear,’ retorted Ralph in his cold sarcastic manner. ‘Mr. Squeers, here’s my niece: Nicholas’s sister!’\n\n‘Very glad to make your acquaintance, miss,’ said Squeers, raising his hat an inch or two. ‘I wish Mrs. Squeers took gals, and we had you for a teacher. I don’t know, though, whether she mightn’t grow jealous if we had. Ha! ha! ha!’\n\nIf the proprietor of Dotheboys Hall could have known what was passing in his assistant’s breast at that moment, he would have discovered, with some surprise, that he was as near being soundly pummelled as he had ever been in his life. Kate Nickleby, having a quicker perception of her brother’s emotions, led him gently aside, and thus prevented Mr. Squeers from being impressed with the fact in a peculiarly disagreeable manner.\n\n‘My dear Nicholas,’ said the young lady, ‘who is this man? What kind of place can it be that you are going to?’\n\n‘I hardly know, Kate,’ replied Nicholas, pressing his sister’s hand. ‘I suppose the Yorkshire folks are rather rough and uncultivated; that’s all.’\n\n‘But this person,’ urged Kate.\n\n‘Is my employer, or master, or whatever the proper name may be,’ replied Nicholas quickly; ‘and I was an ass to take his coarseness ill. They are looking this way, and it is time I was in my place. Bless you, love, and goodbye! Mother, look forward to our meeting again someday! Uncle, farewell! Thank you heartily for all you have done and all you mean to do. Quite ready, sir!’\n\nWith these hasty adieux, Nicholas mounted nimbly to his seat, and waved his hand as gallantly as if his heart went with it.\n\nAt this moment, when the coachman and guard were comparing notes for the last time before starting, on the subject of the way-bill; when porters were screwing out the last reluctant sixpences, itinerant newsmen making the last offer of a morning paper, and the horses giving the last impatient rattle to their harness; Nicholas felt somebody pulling softly at his leg. He looked down, and there stood Newman Noggs, who pushed up into his hand a dirty letter.\n\n‘What’s this?’ inquired Nicholas.\n\n‘Hush!’ rejoined Noggs, pointing to Mr. Ralph Nickleby, who was saying a few earnest words to Squeers, a short distance off: ‘Take it. Read it. Nobody knows. That’s all.’\n\n‘Stop!’ cried Nicholas.\n\n‘No,’ replied Noggs.\n\nNicholas cried stop, again, but Newman Noggs was gone.\n\nA minute’s bustle, a banging of the coach doors, a swaying of the vehicle to one side, as the heavy coachman, and still heavier guard, climbed into their seats; a cry of all right, a few notes from the horn, a hasty glance of two sorrowful faces below, and the hard features of Mr Ralph Nickleby--and the coach was gone too, and rattling over the stones of Smithfield.\n\nThe little boys’ legs being too short to admit of their feet resting upon anything as they sat, and the little boys’ bodies being consequently in imminent hazard of being jerked off the coach, Nicholas had enough to do over the stones to hold them on. Between the manual exertion and the mental anxiety attendant upon this task, he was not a little relieved when the coach stopped at the Peacock at Islington. He was still more relieved when a hearty-looking gentleman, with a very good-humoured face, and a very fresh colour, got up behind, and proposed to take the other corner of the seat.\n\n‘If we put some of these youngsters in the middle,’ said the new-comer, ‘they’ll be safer in case of their going to sleep; eh?’\n\n‘If you’ll have the goodness, sir,’ replied Squeers, ‘that’ll be the very thing. Mr. Nickleby, take three of them boys between you and the gentleman. Belling and the youngest Snawley can sit between me and the guard. Three children,’ said Squeers, explaining to the stranger, ‘books as two.’\n\n‘I have not the least objection I am sure,’ said the fresh-coloured gentleman; ‘I have a brother who wouldn’t object to book his six children as two at any butcher’s or baker’s in the kingdom, I dare say. Far from it.’\n\n‘Six children, sir?’ exclaimed Squeers.\n\n‘Yes, and all boys,’ replied the stranger.\n\n‘Mr. Nickleby,’ said Squeers, in great haste, ‘catch hold of that basket. Let me give you a card, sir, of an establishment where those six boys can be brought up in an enlightened, liberal, and moral manner, with no mistake at all about it, for twenty guineas a year each--twenty guineas, sir--or I’d take all the boys together upon a average right through, and say a hundred pound a year for the lot.’\n\n‘Oh!’ said the gentleman, glancing at the card, ‘you are the Mr. Squeers mentioned here, I presume?’\n\n‘Yes, I am, sir,’ replied the worthy pedagogue; ‘Mr. Wackford Squeers is my name, and I’m very far from being ashamed of it. These are some of my boys, sir; that’s one of my assistants, sir--Mr. Nickleby, a gentleman’s son, and a good scholar, mathematical, classical, and commercial. We don’t do things by halves at our shop. All manner of learning my boys take down, sir; the expense is never thought of; and they get paternal treatment and washing in.’\n\n‘Upon my word,’ said the gentleman, glancing at Nicholas with a half-smile, and a more than half expression of surprise, ‘these are advantages indeed.’\n\n‘You may say that, sir,’ rejoined Squeers, thrusting his hands into his great-coat pockets. ‘The most unexceptionable references are given and required. I wouldn’t take a reference with any boy, that wasn’t responsible for the payment of five pound five a quarter, no, not if you went down on your knees, and asked me, with the tears running down your face, to do it.’\n\n‘Highly considerate,’ said the passenger.\n\n‘It’s my great aim and end to be considerate, sir,’ rejoined Squeers. ‘Snawley, junior, if you don’t leave off chattering your teeth, and shaking with the cold, I’ll warm you with a severe thrashing in about half a minute’s time.’\n\n‘Sit fast here, genelmen,’ said the guard as he clambered up.\n\n‘All right behind there, Dick?’ cried the coachman.\n\n‘All right,’ was the reply. ‘Off she goes!’ And off she did go--if coaches be feminine--amidst a loud flourish from the guard’s horn, and the calm approval of all the judges of coaches and coach-horses congregated at the Peacock, but more especially of the helpers, who stood, with the cloths over their arms, watching the coach till it disappeared, and then lounged admiringly stablewards, bestowing various gruff encomiums on the beauty of the turn-out.\n\nWhen the guard (who was a stout old Yorkshireman) had blown himself quite out of breath, he put the horn into a little tunnel of a basket fastened to the coach-side for the purpose, and giving himself a plentiful shower of blows on the chest and shoulders, observed it was uncommon cold; after which, he demanded of every person separately whether he was going right through, and if not, where he WAS going. Satisfactory replies being made to these queries, he surmised that the roads were pretty heavy arter that fall last night, and took the liberty of asking whether any of them gentlemen carried a snuff-box. It happening that nobody did, he remarked with a mysterious air that he had heard a medical gentleman as went down to Grantham last week, say how that snuff-taking was bad for the eyes; but for his part he had never found it so, and what he said was, that everybody should speak as they found. Nobody attempting to controvert this position, he took a small brown-paper parcel out of his hat, and putting on a pair of horn spectacles (the writing being crabbed) read the direction half-a-dozen times over; having done which, he consigned the parcel to its old place, put up his spectacles again, and stared at everybody in turn. After this, he took another blow at the horn by way of refreshment; and, having now exhausted his usual topics of conversation, folded his arms as well as he could in so many coats, and falling into a solemn silence, looked carelessly at the familiar objects which met his eye on every side as the coach rolled on; the only things he seemed to care for, being horses and droves of cattle, which he scrutinised with a critical air as they were passed upon the road.\n\nThe weather was intensely and bitterly cold; a great deal of snow fell from time to time; and the wind was intolerably keen. Mr. Squeers got down at almost every stage--to stretch his legs as he said--and as he always came back from such excursions with a very red nose, and composed himself to sleep directly, there is reason to suppose that he derived great benefit from the process. The little pupils having been stimulated with the remains of their breakfast, and further invigorated by sundry small cups of a curious cordial carried by Mr. Squeers, which tasted very like toast-and-water put into a brandy bottle by mistake, went to sleep, woke, shivered, and cried, as their feelings prompted. Nicholas and the good-tempered man found so many things to talk about, that between conversing together, and cheering up the boys, the time passed with them as rapidly as it could, under such adverse circumstances.\n\nSo the day wore on. At Eton Slocomb there was a good coach dinner, of which the box, the four front outsides, the one inside, Nicholas, the good-tempered man, and Mr. Squeers, partook; while the five little boys were put to thaw by the fire, and regaled with sandwiches. A stage or two further on, the lamps were lighted, and a great to-do occasioned by the taking up, at a roadside inn, of a very fastidious lady with an infinite variety of cloaks and small parcels, who loudly lamented, for the behoof of the outsides, the non-arrival of her own carriage which was to have taken her on, and made the guard solemnly promise to stop every green chariot he saw coming; which, as it was a dark night and he was sitting with his face the other way, that officer undertook, with many fervent asseverations, to do. Lastly, the fastidious lady, finding there was a solitary gentleman inside, had a small lamp lighted which she carried in reticule, and being after much trouble shut in, the horses were put into a brisk canter and the coach was once more in rapid motion.\n\nThe night and the snow came on together, and dismal enough they were. There was no sound to be heard but the howling of the wind; for the noise of the wheels, and the tread of the horses’ feet, were rendered inaudible by the thick coating of snow which covered the ground, and was fast increasing every moment. The streets of Stamford were deserted as they passed through the town; and its old churches rose, frowning and dark, from the whitened ground. Twenty miles further on, two of the front outside passengers, wisely availing themselves of their arrival at one of the best inns in England, turned in, for the night, at the George at Grantham. The remainder wrapped themselves more closely in their coats and cloaks, and leaving the light and warmth of the town behind them, pillowed themselves against the luggage, and prepared, with many half-suppressed moans, again to encounter the piercing blast which swept across the open country.\n\nThey were little more than a stage out of Grantham, or about halfway between it and Newark, when Nicholas, who had been asleep for a short time, was suddenly roused by a violent jerk which nearly threw him from his seat. Grasping the rail, he found that the coach had sunk greatly on one side, though it was still dragged forward by the horses; and while--confused by their plunging and the loud screams of the lady inside--he hesitated, for an instant, whether to jump off or not, the vehicle turned easily over, and relieved him from all further uncertainty by flinging him into the road.\n\n# CHAPTER 6\n\nIn which the Occurrence of the Accident mentioned in the last Chapter, affords an Opportunity to a couple of Gentlemen to tell Stories against each other\n‘Wo ho!’ cried the guard, on his legs in a minute, and running to the leaders’ heads. ‘Is there ony genelmen there as can len’ a hond here? Keep quiet, dang ye! Wo ho!’\n\n‘What’s the matter?’ demanded Nicholas, looking sleepily up.\n\n‘Matther mun, matter eneaf for one neight,’ replied the guard; ‘dang the wall-eyed bay, he’s gane mad wi’ glory I think, carse t’coorch is over. Here, can’t ye len’ a hond? Dom it, I’d ha’ dean it if all my boans were brokken.’\n\n‘Here!’ cried Nicholas, staggering to his feet, ‘I’m ready. I’m only a little abroad, that’s all.’\n\n‘Hoold ‘em toight,’ cried the guard, ‘while ar coot treaces. Hang on tiv’em sumhoo. Well deane, my lod. That’s it. Let’em goa noo. Dang ‘em, they’ll gang whoam fast eneaf!’\n\nIn truth, the animals were no sooner released than they trotted back, with much deliberation, to the stable they had just left, which was distant not a mile behind.\n\n‘Can you blo’ a harn?’ asked the guard, disengaging one of the coach-lamps.\n\n‘I dare say I can,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘Then just blo’ away into that ‘un as lies on the grund, fit to wakken the deead, will’ee,’ said the man, ‘while I stop sum o’ this here squealing inside. Cumin’, cumin’. Dean’t make that noise, wooman.’\n\nAs the man spoke, he proceeded to wrench open the uppermost door of the coach, while Nicholas, seizing the horn, awoke the echoes far and wide with one of the most extraordinary performances on that instrument ever heard by mortal ears. It had its effect, however, not only in rousing such of their fall, but in summoning assistance to their relief; for lights gleamed in the distance, and people were already astir.\n\nIn fact, a man on horseback galloped down, before the passengers were well collected together; and a careful investigation being instituted, it appeared that the lady inside had broken her lamp, and the gentleman his head; that the two front outsides had escaped with black eyes; the box with a bloody nose; the coachman with a contusion on the temple; Mr. Squeers with a portmanteau bruise on his back; and the remaining passengers without any injury at all--thanks to the softness of the snow-drift in which they had been overturned. These facts were no sooner thoroughly ascertained, than the lady gave several indications of fainting, but being forewarned that if she did, she must be carried on some gentleman’s shoulders to the nearest public-house, she prudently thought better of it, and walked back with the rest.\n\nThey found on reaching it, that it was a lonely place with no very great accommodation in the way of apartments--that portion of its resources being all comprised in one public room with a sanded floor, and a chair or two. However, a large faggot and a plentiful supply of coals being heaped upon the fire, the appearance of things was not long in mending; and, by the time they had washed off all effaceable marks of the late accident, the room was warm and light, which was a most agreeable exchange for the cold and darkness out of doors.\n\n‘Well, Mr. Nickleby,’ said Squeers, insinuating himself into the warmest corner, ‘you did very right to catch hold of them horses. I should have done it myself if I had come to in time, but I am very glad you did it. You did it very well; very well.’\n\n‘So well,’ said the merry-faced gentleman, who did not seem to approve very much of the patronising tone adopted by Squeers, ‘that if they had not been firmly checked when they were, you would most probably have had no brains left to teach with.’\n\nThis remark called up a discourse relative to the promptitude Nicholas had displayed, and he was overwhelmed with compliments and commendations.\n\n‘I am very glad to have escaped, of course,’ observed Squeers: ‘every man is glad when he escapes from danger; but if any one of my charges had been hurt--if I had been prevented from restoring any one of these little boys to his parents whole and sound as I received him--what would have been my feelings? Why the wheel a-top of my head would have been far preferable to it.’\n\n‘Are they all brothers, sir?’ inquired the lady who had carried the ‘Davy’ or safety-lamp.\n\n‘In one sense they are, ma’am,’ replied Squeers, diving into his greatcoat pocket for cards. ‘They are all under the same parental and affectionate treatment. Mrs. Squeers and myself are a mother and father to every one of ‘em. Mr. Nickleby, hand the lady them cards, and offer these to the gentleman. Perhaps they might know of some parents that would be glad to avail themselves of the establishment.’\n\nExpressing himself to this effect, Mr. Squeers, who lost no opportunity of advertising gratuitously, placed his hands upon his knees, and looked at the pupils with as much benignity as he could possibly affect, while Nicholas, blushing with shame, handed round the cards as directed.\n\n‘I hope you suffer no inconvenience from the overturn, ma’am?’ said the merry-faced gentleman, addressing the fastidious lady, as though he were charitably desirous to change the subject.\n\n‘No bodily inconvenience,’ replied the lady.\n\n‘No mental inconvenience, I hope?’\n\n‘The subject is a very painful one to my feelings, sir,’ replied the lady with strong emotion; ‘and I beg you as a gentleman, not to refer to it.’\n\n‘Dear me,’ said the merry-faced gentleman, looking merrier still, ‘I merely intended to inquire--’\n\n‘I hope no inquiries will be made,’ said the lady, ‘or I shall be compelled to throw myself on the protection of the other gentlemen. Landlord, pray direct a boy to keep watch outside the door--and if a green chariot passes in the direction of Grantham, to stop it instantly.’\n\nThe people of the house were evidently overcome by this request, and when the lady charged the boy to remember, as a means of identifying the expected green chariot, that it would have a coachman with a gold-laced hat on the box, and a footman, most probably in silk stockings, behind, the attentions of the good woman of the inn were redoubled. Even the box-passenger caught the infection, and growing wonderfully deferential, immediately inquired whether there was not very good society in that neighbourhood, to which the lady replied yes, there was: in a manner which sufficiently implied that she moved at the very tiptop and summit of it all.\n\n‘As the guard has gone on horseback to Grantham to get another coach,’ said the good-tempered gentleman when they had been all sitting round the fire, for some time, in silence, ‘and as he must be gone a couple of hours at the very least, I propose a bowl of hot punch. What say you, sir?’\n\nThis question was addressed to the broken-headed inside, who was a man of very genteel appearance, dressed in mourning. He was not past the middle age, but his hair was grey; it seemed to have been prematurely turned by care or sorrow. He readily acceded to the proposal, and appeared to be prepossessed by the frank good-nature of the individual from whom it emanated.\n\nThis latter personage took upon himself the office of tapster when the punch was ready, and after dispensing it all round, led the conversation to the antiquities of York, with which both he and the grey-haired gentleman appeared to be well acquainted. When this topic flagged, he turned with a smile to the grey-headed gentleman, and asked if he could sing.\n\n‘I cannot indeed,’ replied gentleman, smiling in his turn.\n\n‘That’s a pity,’ said the owner of the good-humoured countenance. ‘Is there nobody here who can sing a song to lighten the time?’\n\nThe passengers, one and all, protested that they could not; that they wished they could; that they couldn’t remember the words of anything without the book; and so forth.\n\n‘Perhaps the lady would not object,’ said the president with great respect, and a merry twinkle in his eye. ‘Some little Italian thing out of the last opera brought out in town, would be most acceptable I am sure.’\n\nAs the lady condescended to make no reply, but tossed her head contemptuously, and murmured some further expression of surprise regarding the absence of the green chariot, one or two voices urged upon the president himself, the propriety of making an attempt for the general benefit.\n\n‘I would if I could,’ said he of the good-tempered face; ‘for I hold that in this, as in all other cases where people who are strangers to each other are thrown unexpectedly together, they should endeavour to render themselves as pleasant, for the joint sake of the little community, as possible.’\n\n‘I wish the maxim were more generally acted on, in all cases,’ said the grey-headed gentleman.\n\n‘I’m glad to hear it,’ returned the other. ‘Perhaps, as you can’t sing, you’ll tell us a story?’\n\n‘Nay. I should ask you.’\n\n‘After you, I will, with pleasure.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ said the grey-haired gentleman, smiling, ‘Well, let it be so. I fear the turn of my thoughts is not calculated to lighten the time you must pass here; but you have brought this upon yourselves, and shall judge. We were speaking of York Minster just now. My story shall have some reference to it. Let us call it\n\n# THE FIVE SISTERS OF YORK\n\nAfter a murmur of approbation from the other passengers, during which the fastidious lady drank a glass of punch unobserved, the grey-headed gentleman thus went on:\n\n‘A great many years ago--for the fifteenth century was scarce two years old at the time, and King Henry the Fourth sat upon the throne of England--there dwelt, in the ancient city of York, five maiden sisters, the subjects of my tale.\n\n‘These five sisters were all of surpassing beauty. The eldest was in her twenty-third year, the second a year younger, the third a year younger than the second, and the fourth a year younger than the third. They were tall stately figures, with dark flashing eyes and hair of jet; dignity and grace were in their every movement; and the fame of their great beauty had spread through all the country round.\n\n‘But, if the four elder sisters were lovely, how beautiful was the youngest, a fair creature of sixteen! The blushing tints in the soft bloom on the fruit, or the delicate painting on the flower, are not more exquisite than was the blending of the rose and lily in her gentle face, or the deep blue of her eye. The vine, in all its elegant luxuriance, is not more graceful than were the clusters of rich brown hair that sported round her brow.\n\n‘If we all had hearts like those which beat so lightly in the bosoms of the young and beautiful, what a heaven this earth would be! If, while our bodies grow old and withered, our hearts could but retain their early youth and freshness, of what avail would be our sorrows and sufferings! But, the faint image of Eden which is stamped upon them in childhood, chafes and rubs in our rough struggles with the world, and soon wears away: too often to leave nothing but a mournful blank remaining.\n\n‘The heart of this fair girl bounded with joy and gladness. Devoted attachment to her sisters, and a fervent love of all beautiful things in nature, were its pure affections. Her gleesome voice and merry laugh were the sweetest music of their home. She was its very light and life. The brightest flowers in the garden were reared by her; the caged birds sang when they heard her voice, and pined when they missed its sweetness. Alice, dear Alice; what living thing within the sphere of her gentle witchery, could fail to love her!\n\n‘You may seek in vain, now, for the spot on which these sisters lived, for their very names have passed away, and dusty antiquaries tell of them as of a fable. But they dwelt in an old wooden house--old even in those days--with overhanging gables and balconies of rudely-carved oak, which stood within a pleasant orchard, and was surrounded by a rough stone wall, whence a stout archer might have winged an arrow to St Mary’s Abbey. The old abbey flourished then; and the five sisters, living on its fair domains, paid yearly dues to the black monks of St Benedict, to which fraternity it belonged.\n\n‘It was a bright and sunny morning in the pleasant time of summer, when one of those black monks emerged from the abbey portal, and bent his steps towards the house of the fair sisters. Heaven above was blue, and earth beneath was green; the river glistened like a path of diamonds in the sun; the birds poured forth their songs from the shady trees; the lark soared high above the waving corn; and the deep buzz of insects filled the air. Everything looked gay and smiling; but the holy man walked gloomily on, with his eyes bent upon the ground. The beauty of the earth is but a breath, and man is but a shadow. What sympathy should a holy preacher have with either?\n\n‘With eyes bent upon the ground, then, or only raised enough to prevent his stumbling over such obstacles as lay in his way, the religious man moved slowly forward until he reached a small postern in the wall of the sisters’ orchard, through which he passed, closing it behind him. The noise of soft voices in conversation, and of merry laughter, fell upon his ears ere he had advanced many paces; and raising his eyes higher than was his humble wont, he descried, at no great distance, the five sisters seated on the grass, with Alice in the centre: all busily plying their customary task of embroidering.\n\n‘“Save you, fair daughters!” said the friar; and fair in truth they were. Even a monk might have loved them as choice masterpieces of his Maker’s hand.\n\n‘The sisters saluted the holy man with becoming reverence, and the eldest motioned him to a mossy seat beside them. But the good friar shook his head, and bumped himself down on a very hard stone,--at which, no doubt, approving angels were gratified.\n\n‘“Ye were merry, daughters,” said the monk.\n\n‘“You know how light of heart sweet Alice is,” replied the eldest sister, passing her fingers through the tresses of the smiling girl.\n\n‘“And what joy and cheerfulness it wakes up within us, to see all nature beaming in brightness and sunshine, father,” added Alice, blushing beneath the stern look of the recluse.\n\n‘The monk answered not, save by a grave inclination of the head, and the sisters pursued their task in silence.\n\n‘“Still wasting the precious hours,” said the monk at length, turning to the eldest sister as he spoke, “still wasting the precious hours on this vain trifling. Alas, alas! that the few bubbles on the surface of eternity--all that Heaven wills we should see of that dark deep stream--should be so lightly scattered!”\n\n‘“Father,” urged the maiden, pausing, as did each of the others, in her busy task, “we have prayed at matins, our daily alms have been distributed at the gate, the sick peasants have been tended,--all our morning tasks have been performed. I hope our occupation is a blameless one?’\n\n‘“See here,” said the friar, taking the frame from her hand, “an intricate winding of gaudy colours, without purpose or object, unless it be that one day it is destined for some vain ornament, to minister to the pride of your frail and giddy sex. Day after day has been employed upon this senseless task, and yet it is not half accomplished. The shade of each departed day falls upon our graves, and the worm exults as he beholds it, to know that we are hastening thither. Daughters, is there no better way to pass the fleeting hours?”\n\n‘The four elder sisters cast down their eyes as if abashed by the holy man’s reproof, but Alice raised hers, and bent them mildly on the friar.\n\n‘“Our dear mother,” said the maiden; “Heaven rest her soul!”\n\n‘“Amen!” cried the friar in a deep voice.\n\n‘“Our dear mother,” faltered the fair Alice, “was living when these long tasks began, and bade us, when she should be no more, ply them in all discretion and cheerfulness, in our leisure hours; she said that if in harmless mirth and maidenly pursuits we passed those hours together, they would prove the happiest and most peaceful of our lives, and that if, in later times, we went forth into the world, and mingled with its cares and trials--if, allured by its temptations and dazzled by its glitter, we ever forgot that love and duty which should bind, in holy ties, the children of one loved parent--a glance at the old work of our common girlhood would awaken good thoughts of bygone days, and soften our hearts to affection and love.”\n\n‘“Alice speaks truly, father,” said the elder sister, somewhat proudly. And so saying she resumed her work, as did the others.\n\n‘It was a kind of sampler of large size, that each sister had before her; the device was of a complex and intricate description, and the pattern and colours of all five were the same. The sisters bent gracefully over their work; the monk, resting his chin upon his hands, looked from one to the other in silence.\n\n‘“How much better,” he said at length, “to shun all such thoughts and chances, and, in the peaceful shelter of the church, devote your lives to Heaven! Infancy, childhood, the prime of life, and old age, wither as rapidly as they crowd upon each other. Think how human dust rolls onward to the tomb, and turning your faces steadily towards that goal, avoid the cloud which takes its rise among the pleasures of the world, and cheats the senses of their votaries. The veil, daughters, the veil!”\n\n‘“Never, sisters,” cried Alice. “Barter not the light and air of heaven, and the freshness of earth and all the beautiful things which breathe upon it, for the cold cloister and the cell. Nature’s own blessings are the proper goods of life, and we may share them sinlessly together. To die is our heavy portion, but, oh, let us die with life about us; when our cold hearts cease to beat, let warm hearts be beating near; let our last look be upon the bounds which God has set to his own bright skies, and not on stone walls and bars of iron! Dear sisters, let us live and die, if you list, in this green garden’s compass; only shun the gloom and sadness of a cloister, and we shall be happy.”\n\n‘The tears fell fast from the maiden’s eyes as she closed her impassioned appeal, and hid her face in the bosom of her sister.\n\n‘“Take comfort, Alice,” said the eldest, kissing her fair forehead. “The veil shall never cast its shadow on thy young brow. How say you, sisters? For yourselves you speak, and not for Alice, or for me.”\n\n‘The sisters, as with one accord, cried that their lot was cast together, and that there were dwellings for peace and virtue beyond the convent’s walls.\n\n‘“Father,” said the eldest lady, rising with dignity, “you hear our final resolve. The same pious care which enriched the abbey of St Mary, and left us, orphans, to its holy guardianship, directed that no constraint should be imposed upon our inclinations, but that we should be free to live according to our choice. Let us hear no more of this, we pray you. Sisters, it is nearly noon. Let us take shelter until evening!” With a reverence to the friar, the lady rose and walked towards the house, hand in hand with Alice; the other sisters followed.\n\n‘The holy man, who had often urged the same point before, but had never met with so direct a repulse, walked some little distance behind, with his eyes bent upon the earth, and his lips moving AS IF in prayer. As the sisters reached the porch, he quickened his pace, and called upon them to stop.\n\n‘“Stay!” said the monk, raising his right hand in the air, and directing an angry glance by turns at Alice and the eldest sister. “Stay, and hear from me what these recollections are, which you would cherish above eternity, and awaken--if in mercy they slumbered--by means of idle toys. The memory of earthly things is charged, in after life, with bitter disappointment, affliction, death; with dreary change and wasting sorrow. The time will one day come, when a glance at those unmeaning baubles will tear open deep wounds in the hearts of some among you, and strike to your inmost souls. When that hour arrives--and, mark me, come it will--turn from the world to which you clung, to the refuge which you spurned. Find me the cell which shall be colder than the fire of mortals grows, when dimmed by calamity and trial, and there weep for the dreams of youth. These things are Heaven’s will, not mine,” said the friar, subduing his voice as he looked round upon the shrinking girls. “The Virgin’s blessing be upon you, daughters!”\n\n‘With these words he disappeared through the postern; and the sisters hastening into the house were seen no more that day.\n\n‘But nature will smile though priests may frown, and next day the sun shone brightly, and on the next, and the next again. And in the morning’s glare, and the evening’s soft repose, the five sisters still walked, or worked, or beguiled the time by cheerful conversation, in their quiet orchard.\n\n‘Time passed away as a tale that is told; faster indeed than many tales that are told, of which number I fear this may be one. The house of the five sisters stood where it did, and the same trees cast their pleasant shade upon the orchard grass. The sisters too were there, and lovely as at first, but a change had come over their dwelling. Sometimes, there was the clash of armour, and the gleaming of the moon on caps of steel; and, at others, jaded coursers were spurred up to the gate, and a female form glided hurriedly forth, as if eager to demand tidings of the weary messenger. A goodly train of knights and ladies lodged one night within the abbey walls, and next day rode away, with two of the fair sisters among them. Then, horsemen began to come less frequently, and seemed to bring bad tidings when they did, and at length they ceased to come at all, and footsore peasants slunk to the gate after sunset, and did their errand there, by stealth. Once, a vassal was dispatched in haste to the abbey at dead of night, and when morning came, there were sounds of woe and wailing in the sisters’ house; and after this, a mournful silence fell upon it, and knight or lady, horse or armour, was seen about it no more.\n\n‘There was a sullen darkness in the sky, and the sun had gone angrily down, tinting the dull clouds with the last traces of his wrath, when the same black monk walked slowly on, with folded arms, within a stone’s-throw of the abbey. A blight had fallen on the trees and shrubs; and the wind, at length beginning to break the unnatural stillness that had prevailed all day, sighed heavily from time to time, as though foretelling in grief the ravages of the coming storm. The bat skimmed in fantastic flights through the heavy air, and the ground was alive with crawling things, whose instinct brought them forth to swell and fatten in the rain.\n\n‘No longer were the friar’s eyes directed to the earth; they were cast abroad, and roamed from point to point, as if the gloom and desolation of the scene found a quick response in his own bosom. Again he paused near the sisters’ house, and again he entered by the postern.\n\n‘But not again did his ear encounter the sound of laughter, or his eyes rest upon the beautiful figures of the five sisters. All was silent and deserted. The boughs of the trees were bent and broken, and the grass had grown long and rank. No light feet had pressed it for many, many a day.\n\n‘With the indifference or abstraction of one well accustomed to the change, the monk glided into the house, and entered a low, dark room. Four sisters sat there. Their black garments made their pale faces whiter still, and time and sorrow had worked deep ravages. They were stately yet; but the flush and pride of beauty were gone.\n\n‘And Alice--where was she? In Heaven.\n\n‘The monk--even the monk--could bear with some grief here; for it was long since these sisters had met, and there were furrows in their blanched faces which years could never plough. He took his seat in silence, and motioned them to continue their speech.\n\n‘“They are here, sisters,” said the elder lady in a trembling voice. “I have never borne to look upon them since, and now I blame myself for my weakness. What is there in her memory that we should dread? To call up our old days shall be a solemn pleasure yet.”\n\n‘She glanced at the monk as she spoke, and, opening a cabinet, brought forth the five frames of work, completed long before. Her step was firm, but her hand trembled as she produced the last one; and, when the feelings of the other sisters gushed forth at sight of it, her pent-up tears made way, and she sobbed “God bless her!”\n\n‘The monk rose and advanced towards them. “It was almost the last thing she touched in health,” he said in a low voice.\n\n‘“It was,” cried the elder lady, weeping bitterly.\n\n‘The monk turned to the second sister.\n\n‘“The gallant youth who looked into thine eyes, and hung upon thy very breath when first he saw thee intent upon this pastime, lies buried on a plain whereof the turf is red with blood. Rusty fragments of armour, once brightly burnished, lie rotting on the ground, and are as little distinguishable for his, as are the bones that crumble in the mould!”\n\n‘The lady groaned, and wrung her hands.\n\n‘“The policy of courts,” he continued, turning to the two other sisters, “drew ye from your peaceful home to scenes of revelry and splendour. The same policy, and the restless ambition of--proud and fiery men, have sent ye back, widowed maidens, and humbled outcasts. Do I speak truly?”\n\n‘The sobs of the two sisters were their only reply.\n\n‘“There is little need,” said the monk, with a meaning look, “to fritter away the time in gewgaws which shall raise up the pale ghosts of hopes of early years. Bury them, heap penance and mortification on their heads, keep them down, and let the convent be their grave!”\n\n‘The sisters asked for three days to deliberate; and felt, that night, as though the veil were indeed the fitting shroud for their dead joys. But, morning came again, and though the boughs of the orchard trees drooped and ran wild upon the ground, it was the same orchard still. The grass was coarse and high, but there was yet the spot on which they had so often sat together, when change and sorrow were but names. There was every walk and nook which Alice had made glad; and in the minster nave was one flat stone beneath which she slept in peace.\n\n‘And could they, remembering how her young heart had sickened at the thought of cloistered walls, look upon her grave, in garbs which would chill the very ashes within it? Could they bow down in prayer, and when all Heaven turned to hear them, bring the dark shade of sadness on one angel’s face? No.\n\n‘They sent abroad, to artists of great celebrity in those times, and having obtained the church’s sanction to their work of piety, caused to be executed, in five large compartments of richly stained glass, a faithful copy of their old embroidery work. These were fitted into a large window until that time bare of ornament; and when the sun shone brightly, as she had so well loved to see it, the familiar patterns were reflected in their original colours, and throwing a stream of brilliant light upon the pavement, fell warmly on the name of Alice.\n\n‘For many hours in every day, the sisters paced slowly up and down the nave, or knelt by the side of the flat broad stone. Only three were seen in the customary place, after many years; then but two, and, for a long time afterwards, but one solitary female bent with age. At length she came no more, and the stone bore five plain Christian names.\n\n‘That stone has worn away and been replaced by others, and many generations have come and gone since then. Time has softened down the colours, but the same stream of light still falls upon the forgotten tomb, of which no trace remains; and, to this day, the stranger is shown in York Cathedral, an old window called the Five Sisters.’\n\n‘That’s a melancholy tale,’ said the merry-faced gentleman, emptying his glass.\n\n‘It is a tale of life, and life is made up of such sorrows,’ returned the other, courteously, but in a grave and sad tone of voice.\n\n‘There are shades in all good pictures, but there are lights too, if we choose to contemplate them,’ said the gentleman with the merry face. ‘The youngest sister in your tale was always light-hearted.’\n\n‘And died early,’ said the other, gently.\n\n‘She would have died earlier, perhaps, had she been less happy,’ said the first speaker, with much feeling. ‘Do you think the sisters who loved her so well, would have grieved the less if her life had been one of gloom and sadness? If anything could soothe the first sharp pain of a heavy loss, it would be--with me--the reflection, that those I mourned, by being innocently happy here, and loving all about them, had prepared themselves for a purer and happier world. The sun does not shine upon this fair earth to meet frowning eyes, depend upon it.’\n\n‘I believe you are right,’ said the gentleman who had told the story.\n\n‘Believe!’ retorted the other, ‘can anybody doubt it? Take any subject of sorrowful regret, and see with how much pleasure it is associated. The recollection of past pleasure may become pain--’\n\n‘It does,’ interposed the other.\n\n‘Well; it does. To remember happiness which cannot be restored, is pain, but of a softened kind. Our recollections are unfortunately mingled with much that we deplore, and with many actions which we bitterly repent; still in the most chequered life I firmly think there are so many little rays of sunshine to look back upon, that I do not believe any mortal (unless he had put himself without the pale of hope) would deliberately drain a goblet of the waters of Lethe, if he had it in his power.’\n\n‘Possibly you are correct in that belief,’ said the grey-haired gentleman after a short reflection. ‘I am inclined to think you are.’\n\n‘Why, then,’ replied the other, ‘the good in this state of existence preponderates over the bad, let miscalled philosophers tell us what they will. If our affections be tried, our affections are our consolation and comfort; and memory, however sad, is the best and purest link between this world and a better. But come! I’ll tell you a story of another kind.’\n\nAfter a very brief silence, the merry-faced gentleman sent round the punch, and glancing slyly at the fastidious lady, who seemed desperately apprehensive that he was going to relate something improper, began\n\n# THE BARON OF GROGZWIG\n\n‘The Baron Von Koeldwethout, of Grogzwig in Germany, was as likely a young baron as you would wish to see. I needn’t say that he lived in a castle, because that’s of course; neither need I say that he lived in an old castle; for what German baron ever lived in a new one? There were many strange circumstances connected with this venerable building, among which, not the least startling and mysterious were, that when the wind blew, it rumbled in the chimneys, or even howled among the trees in the neighbouring forest; and that when the moon shone, she found her way through certain small loopholes in the wall, and actually made some parts of the wide halls and galleries quite light, while she left others in gloomy shadow. I believe that one of the baron’s ancestors, being short of money, had inserted a dagger in a gentleman who called one night to ask his way, and it WAS supposed that these miraculous occurrences took place in consequence. And yet I hardly know how that could have been, either, because the baron’s ancestor, who was an amiable man, felt very sorry afterwards for having been so rash, and laying violent hands upon a quantity of stone and timber which belonged to a weaker baron, built a chapel as an apology, and so took a receipt from Heaven, in full of all demands.\n\n‘Talking of the baron’s ancestor puts me in mind of the baron’s great claims to respect, on the score of his pedigree. I am afraid to say, I am sure, how many ancestors the baron had; but I know that he had a great many more than any other man of his time; and I only wish that he had lived in these latter days, that he might have had more. It is a very hard thing upon the great men of past centuries, that they should have come into the world so soon, because a man who was born three or four hundred years ago, cannot reasonably be expected to have had as many relations before him, as a man who is born now. The last man, whoever he is--and he may be a cobbler or some low vulgar dog for aught we know--will have a longer pedigree than the greatest nobleman now alive; and I contend that this is not fair.\n\n‘Well, but the Baron Von Koeldwethout of Grogzwig! He was a fine swarthy fellow, with dark hair and large moustachios, who rode a-hunting in clothes of Lincoln green, with russet boots on his feet, and a bugle slung over his shoulder like the guard of a long stage. When he blew this bugle, four-and-twenty other gentlemen of inferior rank, in Lincoln green a little coarser, and russet boots with a little thicker soles, turned out directly: and away galloped the whole train, with spears in their hands like lacquered area railings, to hunt down the boars, or perhaps encounter a bear: in which latter case the baron killed him first, and greased his whiskers with him afterwards.\n\n‘This was a merry life for the Baron of Grogzwig, and a merrier still for the baron’s retainers, who drank Rhine wine every night till they fell under the table, and then had the bottles on the floor, and called for pipes. Never were such jolly, roystering, rollicking, merry-making blades, as the jovial crew of Grogzwig.\n\n‘But the pleasures of the table, or the pleasures of under the table, require a little variety; especially when the same five-and-twenty people sit daily down to the same board, to discuss the same subjects, and tell the same stories. The baron grew weary, and wanted excitement. He took to quarrelling with his gentlemen, and tried kicking two or three of them every day after dinner. This was a pleasant change at first; but it became monotonous after a week or so, and the baron felt quite out of sorts, and cast about, in despair, for some new amusement.\n\n‘One night, after a day’s sport in which he had outdone Nimrod or Gillingwater, and slaughtered “another fine bear,” and brought him home in triumph, the Baron Von Koeldwethout sat moodily at the head of his table, eyeing the smoky roof of the hall with a discontented aspect. He swallowed huge bumpers of wine, but the more he swallowed, the more he frowned. The gentlemen who had been honoured with the dangerous distinction of sitting on his right and left, imitated him to a miracle in the drinking, and frowned at each other.\n\n‘“I will!” cried the baron suddenly, smiting the table with his right hand, and twirling his moustache with his left. “Fill to the Lady of Grogzwig!”\n\n‘The four-and-twenty Lincoln greens turned pale, with the exception of their four-and-twenty noses, which were unchangeable.\n\n‘“I said to the Lady of Grogzwig,” repeated the baron, looking round the board.\n\n‘“To the Lady of Grogzwig!” shouted the Lincoln greens; and down their four-and-twenty throats went four-and-twenty imperial pints of such rare old hock, that they smacked their eight-and-forty lips, and winked again.\n\n‘“The fair daughter of the Baron Von Swillenhausen,” said Koeldwethout, condescending to explain. “We will demand her in marriage of her father, ere the sun goes down tomorrow. If he refuse our suit, we will cut off his nose.”\n\n‘A hoarse murmur arose from the company; every man touched, first the hilt of his sword, and then the tip of his nose, with appalling significance.\n\n‘What a pleasant thing filial piety is to contemplate! If the daughter of the Baron Von Swillenhausen had pleaded a preoccupied heart, or fallen at her father’s feet and corned them in salt tears, or only fainted away, and complimented the old gentleman in frantic ejaculations, the odds are a hundred to one but Swillenhausen Castle would have been turned out at window, or rather the baron turned out at window, and the castle demolished. The damsel held her peace, however, when an early messenger bore the request of Von Koeldwethout next morning, and modestly retired to her chamber, from the casement of which she watched the coming of the suitor and his retinue. She was no sooner assured that the horseman with the large moustachios was her proffered husband, than she hastened to her father’s presence, and expressed her readiness to sacrifice herself to secure his peace. The venerable baron caught his child to his arms, and shed a wink of joy.\n\n‘There was great feasting at the castle, that day. The four-and-twenty Lincoln greens of Von Koeldwethout exchanged vows of eternal friendship with twelve Lincoln greens of Von Swillenhausen, and promised the old baron that they would drink his wine “Till all was blue”--meaning probably until their whole countenances had acquired the same tint as their noses. Everybody slapped everybody else’s back, when the time for parting came; and the Baron Von Koeldwethout and his followers rode gaily home.\n\n‘For six mortal weeks, the bears and boars had a holiday. The houses of Koeldwethout and Swillenhausen were united; the spears rusted; and the baron’s bugle grew hoarse for lack of blowing.\n\n‘Those were great times for the four-and-twenty; but, alas! their high and palmy days had taken boots to themselves, and were already walking off.\n\n‘“My dear,” said the baroness.\n\n‘“My love,” said the baron.\n\n‘“Those coarse, noisy men--”\n\n‘“Which, ma’am?” said the baron, starting.\n\n‘The baroness pointed, from the window at which they stood, to the courtyard beneath, where the unconscious Lincoln greens were taking a copious stirrup-cup, preparatory to issuing forth after a boar or two.\n\n‘“My hunting train, ma’am,” said the baron.\n\n‘“Disband them, love,” murmured the baroness.\n\n‘“Disband them!” cried the baron, in amazement.\n\n‘“To please me, love,” replied the baroness.\n\n‘“To please the devil, ma’am,” answered the baron.\n\n‘Whereupon the baroness uttered a great cry, and swooned away at the baron’s feet.\n\n‘What could the baron do? He called for the lady’s maid, and roared for the doctor; and then, rushing into the yard, kicked the two Lincoln greens who were the most used to it, and cursing the others all round, bade them go--but never mind where. I don’t know the German for it, or I would put it delicately that way.\n\n‘It is not for me to say by what means, or by what degrees, some wives manage to keep down some husbands as they do, although I may have my private opinion on the subject, and may think that no Member of Parliament ought to be married, inasmuch as three married members out of every four, must vote according to their wives’ consciences (if there be such things), and not according to their own. All I need say, just now, is, that the Baroness Von Koeldwethout somehow or other acquired great control over the Baron Von Koeldwethout, and that, little by little, and bit by bit, and day by day, and year by year, the baron got the worst of some disputed question, or was slyly unhorsed from some old hobby; and that by the time he was a fat hearty fellow of forty-eight or thereabouts, he had no feasting, no revelry, no hunting train, and no hunting--nothing in short that he liked, or used to have; and that, although he was as fierce as a lion, and as bold as brass, he was decidedly snubbed and put down, by his own lady, in his own castle of Grogzwig.\n\n‘Nor was this the whole extent of the baron’s misfortunes. About a year after his nuptials, there came into the world a lusty young baron, in whose honour a great many fireworks were let off, and a great many dozens of wine drunk; but next year there came a young baroness, and next year another young baron, and so on, every year, either a baron or baroness (and one year both together), until the baron found himself the father of a small family of twelve. Upon every one of these anniversaries, the venerable Baroness Von Swillenhausen was nervously sensitive for the well-being of her child the Baroness Von Koeldwethout; and although it was not found that the good lady ever did anything material towards contributing to her child’s recovery, still she made it a point of duty to be as nervous as possible at the castle of Grogzwig, and to divide her time between moral observations on the baron’s housekeeping, and bewailing the hard lot of her unhappy daughter. And if the Baron of Grogzwig, a little hurt and irritated at this, took heart, and ventured to suggest that his wife was at least no worse off than the wives of other barons, the Baroness Von Swillenhausen begged all persons to take notice, that nobody but she, sympathised with her dear daughter’s sufferings; upon which, her relations and friends remarked, that to be sure she did cry a great deal more than her son-in-law, and that if there were a hard-hearted brute alive, it was that Baron of Grogzwig.\n\n‘The poor baron bore it all as long as he could, and when he could bear it no longer lost his appetite and his spirits, and sat himself gloomily and dejectedly down. But there were worse troubles yet in store for him, and as they came on, his melancholy and sadness increased. Times changed. He got into debt. The Grogzwig coffers ran low, though the Swillenhausen family had looked upon them as inexhaustible; and just when the baroness was on the point of making a thirteenth addition to the family pedigree, Von Koeldwethout discovered that he had no means of replenishing them.\n\n‘“I don’t see what is to be done,” said the baron. “I think I’ll kill myself.”\n\n‘This was a bright idea. The baron took an old hunting-knife from a cupboard hard by, and having sharpened it on his boot, made what boys call “an offer” at his throat.\n\n‘“Hem!” said the baron, stopping short. “Perhaps it’s not sharp enough.”\n\n‘The baron sharpened it again, and made another offer, when his hand was arrested by a loud screaming among the young barons and baronesses, who had a nursery in an upstairs tower with iron bars outside the window, to prevent their tumbling out into the moat.\n\n‘“If I had been a bachelor,” said the baron sighing, “I might have done it fifty times over, without being interrupted. Hallo! Put a flask of wine and the largest pipe in the little vaulted room behind the hall.”\n\n‘One of the domestics, in a very kind manner, executed the baron’s order in the course of half an hour or so, and Von Koeldwethout being apprised thereof, strode to the vaulted room, the walls of which, being of dark shining wood, gleamed in the light of the blazing logs which were piled upon the hearth. The bottle and pipe were ready, and, upon the whole, the place looked very comfortable.\n\n‘“Leave the lamp,” said the baron.\n\n‘“Anything else, my lord?” inquired the domestic.\n\n‘“The room,” replied the baron. The domestic obeyed, and the baron locked the door.\n\n‘“I’ll smoke a last pipe,” said the baron, “and then I’ll be off.” So, putting the knife upon the table till he wanted it, and tossing off a goodly measure of wine, the Lord of Grogzwig threw himself back in his chair, stretched his legs out before the fire, and puffed away.\n\n‘He thought about a great many things--about his present troubles and past days of bachelorship, and about the Lincoln greens, long since dispersed up and down the country, no one knew whither: with the exception of two who had been unfortunately beheaded, and four who had killed themselves with drinking. His mind was running upon bears and boars, when, in the process of draining his glass to the bottom, he raised his eyes, and saw, for the first time and with unbounded astonishment, that he was not alone.\n\n‘No, he was not; for, on the opposite side of the fire, there sat with folded arms a wrinkled hideous figure, with deeply sunk and bloodshot eyes, and an immensely long cadaverous face, shadowed by jagged and matted locks of coarse black hair. He wore a kind of tunic of a dull bluish colour, which, the baron observed, on regarding it attentively, was clasped or ornamented down the front with coffin handles. His legs, too, were encased in coffin plates as though in armour; and over his left shoulder he wore a short dusky cloak, which seemed made of a remnant of some pall. He took no notice of the baron, but was intently eyeing the fire.\n\n‘“Halloa!” said the baron, stamping his foot to attract attention.\n\n‘“Halloa!” replied the stranger, moving his eyes towards the baron, but not his face or himself “What now?”\n\n‘“What now!” replied the baron, nothing daunted by his hollow voice and lustreless eyes. “I should ask that question. How did you get here?”\n\n‘“Through the door,” replied the figure.\n\n‘“What are you?” says the baron.\n\n‘“A man,” replied the figure.\n\n‘“I don’t believe it,” says the baron.\n\n‘“Disbelieve it then,” says the figure.\n\n‘“I will,” rejoined the baron.\n\n‘The figure looked at the bold Baron of Grogzwig for some time, and then said familiarly,\n\n‘“There’s no coming over you, I see. I’m not a man!”\n\n‘“What are you then?” asked the baron.\n\n‘“A genius,” replied the figure.\n\n‘“You don’t look much like one,” returned the baron scornfully.\n\n‘“I am the Genius of Despair and Suicide,” said the apparition. “Now you know me.”\n\n‘With these words the apparition turned towards the baron, as if composing himself for a talk--and, what was very remarkable, was, that he threw his cloak aside, and displaying a stake, which was run through the centre of his body, pulled it out with a jerk, and laid it on the table, as composedly as if it had been a walking-stick.\n\n‘“Now,” said the figure, glancing at the hunting-knife, “are you ready for me?”\n\n‘“Not quite,” rejoined the baron; “I must finish this pipe first.”\n\n‘“Look sharp then,” said the figure.\n\n‘“You seem in a hurry,” said the baron.\n\n‘“Why, yes, I am,” answered the figure; “they’re doing a pretty brisk business in my way, over in England and France just now, and my time is a good deal taken up.”\n\n‘“Do you drink?” said the baron, touching the bottle with the bowl of his pipe.\n\n‘“Nine times out of ten, and then very hard,” rejoined the figure, drily.\n\n‘“Never in moderation?” asked the baron.\n\n‘“Never,” replied the figure, with a shudder, “that breeds cheerfulness.”\n\n‘The baron took another look at his new friend, whom he thought an uncommonly queer customer, and at length inquired whether he took any active part in such little proceedings as that which he had in contemplation.\n\n‘“No,” replied the figure evasively; “but I am always present.”\n\n‘“Just to see fair, I suppose?” said the baron.\n\n‘“Just that,” replied the figure, playing with his stake, and examining the ferule. “Be as quick as you can, will you, for there’s a young gentleman who is afflicted with too much money and leisure wanting me now, I find.”\n\n‘“Going to kill himself because he has too much money!” exclaimed the baron, quite tickled. “Ha! ha! that’s a good one.” (This was the first time the baron had laughed for many a long day.)\n\n‘“I say,” expostulated the figure, looking very much scared; “don’t do that again.”\n\n‘“Why not?” demanded the baron.\n\n‘“Because it gives me pain all over,” replied the figure. “Sigh as much as you please: that does me good.”\n\n‘The baron sighed mechanically at the mention of the word; the figure, brightening up again, handed him the hunting-knife with most winning politeness.\n\n‘“It’s not a bad idea though,” said the baron, feeling the edge of the weapon; “a man killing himself because he has too much money.”\n\n‘“Pooh!” said the apparition, petulantly, “no better than a man’s killing himself because he has none or little.”\n\n‘Whether the genius unintentionally committed himself in saying this, or whether he thought the baron’s mind was so thoroughly made up that it didn’t matter what he said, I have no means of knowing. I only know that the baron stopped his hand, all of a sudden, opened his eyes wide, and looked as if quite a new light had come upon him for the first time.\n\n‘“Why, certainly,” said Von Koeldwethout, “nothing is too bad to be retrieved.”\n\n‘“Except empty coffers,” cried the genius.\n\n‘“Well; but they may be one day filled again,” said the baron.\n\n‘“Scolding wives,” snarled the genius.\n\n‘“Oh! They may be made quiet,” said the baron.\n\n‘“Thirteen children,” shouted the genius.\n\n‘“Can’t all go wrong, surely,” said the baron.\n\n‘The genius was evidently growing very savage with the baron, for holding these opinions all at once; but he tried to laugh it off, and said if he would let him know when he had left off joking he should feel obliged to him.\n\n‘“But I am not joking; I was never farther from it,” remonstrated the baron.\n\n‘“Well, I am glad to hear that,” said the genius, looking very grim, “because a joke, without any figure of speech, IS the death of me. Come! Quit this dreary world at once.”\n\n‘“I don’t know,” said the baron, playing with the knife; “it’s a dreary one certainly, but I don’t think yours is much better, for you have not the appearance of being particularly comfortable. That puts me in mind--what security have I, that I shall be any the better for going out of the world after all!” he cried, starting up; “I never thought of that.”\n\n‘“Dispatch,” cried the figure, gnashing his teeth.\n\n‘“Keep off!” said the baron. ‘I’ll brood over miseries no longer, but put a good face on the matter, and try the fresh air and the bears again; and if that don’t do, I’ll talk to the baroness soundly, and cut the Von Swillenhausens dead.’ With this the baron fell into his chair, and laughed so loud and boisterously, that the room rang with it.\n\n‘The figure fell back a pace or two, regarding the baron meanwhile with a look of intense terror, and when he had ceased, caught up the stake, plunged it violently into its body, uttered a frightful howl, and disappeared.\n\n‘Von Koeldwethout never saw it again. Having once made up his mind to action, he soon brought the baroness and the Von Swillenhausens to reason, and died many years afterwards: not a rich man that I am aware of, but certainly a happy one: leaving behind him a numerous family, who had been carefully educated in bear and boar-hunting under his own personal eye. And my advice to all men is, that if ever they become hipped and melancholy from similar causes (as very many men do), they look at both sides of the question, applying a magnifying-glass to the best one; and if they still feel tempted to retire without leave, that they smoke a large pipe and drink a full bottle first, and profit by the laudable example of the Baron of Grogzwig.’\n\n‘The fresh coach is ready, ladies and gentlemen, if you please,’ said a new driver, looking in.\n\nThis intelligence caused the punch to be finished in a great hurry, and prevented any discussion relative to the last story. Mr. Squeers was observed to draw the grey-headed gentleman on one side, and to ask a question with great apparent interest; it bore reference to the Five Sisters of York, and was, in fact, an inquiry whether he could inform him how much per annum the Yorkshire convents got in those days with their boarders.\n\nThe journey was then resumed. Nicholas fell asleep towards morning, and, when he awoke, found, with great regret, that, during his nap, both the Baron of Grogzwig and the grey-haired gentleman had got down and were gone. The day dragged on uncomfortably enough. At about six o’clock that night, he and Mr. Squeers, and the little boys, and their united luggage, were all put down together at the George and New Inn, Greta Bridge.\n\nMr. and Mrs. Squeers at Home\nMr. Squeers, being safely landed, left Nicholas and the boys standing with the luggage in the road, to amuse themselves by looking at the coach as it changed horses, while he ran into the tavern and went through the leg-stretching process at the bar. After some minutes, he returned, with his legs thoroughly stretched, if the hue of his nose and a short hiccup afforded any criterion; and at the same time there came out of the yard a rusty pony-chaise, and a cart, driven by two labouring men.\n\n‘Put the boys and the boxes into the cart,’ said Squeers, rubbing his hands; ‘and this young man and me will go on in the chaise. Get in, Nickleby.’\n\nNicholas obeyed. Mr. Squeers with some difficulty inducing the pony to obey also, they started off, leaving the cart-load of infant misery to follow at leisure.\n\n‘Are you cold, Nickleby?’ inquired Squeers, after they had travelled some distance in silence.\n\n‘Rather, sir, I must say.’\n\n‘Well, I don’t find fault with that,’ said Squeers; ‘it’s a long journey this weather.’\n\n‘Is it much farther to Dotheboys Hall, sir?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘About three mile from here,’ replied Squeers. ‘But you needn’t call it a Hall down here.’\n\nNicholas coughed, as if he would like to know why.\n\n‘The fact is, it ain’t a Hall,’ observed Squeers drily.\n\n‘Oh, indeed!’ said Nicholas, whom this piece of intelligence much astonished.\n\n‘No,’ replied Squeers. ‘We call it a Hall up in London, because it sounds better, but they don’t know it by that name in these parts. A man may call his house an island if he likes; there’s no act of Parliament against that, I believe?’\n\n‘I believe not, sir,’ rejoined Nicholas.\n\nSqueers eyed his companion slyly, at the conclusion of this little dialogue, and finding that he had grown thoughtful and appeared in nowise disposed to volunteer any observations, contented himself with lashing the pony until they reached their journey’s end.\n\n‘Jump out,’ said Squeers. ‘Hallo there! Come and put this horse up. Be quick, will you!’\n\nWhile the schoolmaster was uttering these and other impatient cries, Nicholas had time to observe that the school was a long, cold-looking house, one storey high, with a few straggling out-buildings behind, and a barn and stable adjoining. After the lapse of a minute or two, the noise of somebody unlocking the yard-gate was heard, and presently a tall lean boy, with a lantern in his hand, issued forth.\n\n‘Is that you, Smike?’ cried Squeers.\n\n‘Yes, sir,’ replied the boy.\n\n‘Then why the devil didn’t you come before?’\n\n‘Please, sir, I fell asleep over the fire,’ answered Smike, with humility.\n\n‘Fire! what fire? Where’s there a fire?’ demanded the schoolmaster, sharply.\n\n‘Only in the kitchen, sir,’ replied the boy. ‘Missus said as I was sitting up, I might go in there for a warm.’\n\n‘Your missus is a fool,’ retorted Squeers. ‘You’d have been a deuced deal more wakeful in the cold, I’ll engage.’\n\nBy this time Mr. Squeers had dismounted; and after ordering the boy to see to the pony, and to take care that he hadn’t any more corn that night, he told Nicholas to wait at the front-door a minute while he went round and let him in.\n\nA host of unpleasant misgivings, which had been crowding upon Nicholas during the whole journey, thronged into his mind with redoubled force when he was left alone. His great distance from home and the impossibility of reaching it, except on foot, should he feel ever so anxious to return, presented itself to him in most alarming colours; and as he looked up at the dreary house and dark windows, and upon the wild country round, covered with snow, he felt a depression of heart and spirit which he had never experienced before.\n\n‘Now then!’ cried Squeers, poking his head out at the front-door. ‘Where are you, Nickleby?’\n\n‘Here, sir,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘Come in, then,’ said Squeers ‘the wind blows in, at this door, fit to knock a man off his legs.’\n\nNicholas sighed, and hurried in. Mr. Squeers, having bolted the door to keep it shut, ushered him into a small parlour scantily furnished with a few chairs, a yellow map hung against the wall, and a couple of tables; one of which bore some preparations for supper; while, on the other, a tutor’s assistant, a Murray’s grammar, half-a-dozen cards of terms, and a worn letter directed to Wackford Squeers, Esquire, were arranged in picturesque confusion.\n\nThey had not been in this apartment a couple of minutes, when a female bounced into the room, and, seizing Mr. Squeers by the throat, gave him two loud kisses: one close after the other, like a postman’s knock. The lady, who was of a large raw-boned figure, was about half a head taller than Mr. Squeers, and was dressed in a dimity night-jacket; with her hair in papers; she had also a dirty nightcap on, relieved by a yellow cotton handkerchief which tied it under the chin.\n\n‘How is my Squeery?’ said this lady in a playful manner, and a very hoarse voice.\n\n‘Quite well, my love,’ replied Squeers. ‘How’s the cows?’\n\n‘All right, every one of’em,’ answered the lady.\n\n‘And the pigs?’ said Squeers.\n\n‘As well as they were when you went away.’\n\n‘Come; that’s a blessing,’ said Squeers, pulling off his great-coat. ‘The boys are all as they were, I suppose?’\n\n‘Oh, yes, they’re well enough,’ replied Mrs. Squeers, snappishly. ‘That young Pitcher’s had a fever.’\n\n‘No!’ exclaimed Squeers. ‘Damn that boy, he’s always at something of that sort.’\n\n‘Never was such a boy, I do believe,’ said Mrs. Squeers; ‘whatever he has is always catching too. I say it’s obstinacy, and nothing shall ever convince me that it isn’t. I’d beat it out of him; and I told you that, six months ago.’\n\n‘So you did, my love,’ rejoined Squeers. ‘We’ll try what can be done.’\n\nPending these little endearments, Nicholas had stood, awkwardly enough, in the middle of the room: not very well knowing whether he was expected to retire into the passage, or to remain where he was. He was now relieved from his perplexity by Mr. Squeers.\n\n‘This is the new young man, my dear,’ said that gentleman.\n\n‘Oh,’ replied Mrs. Squeers, nodding her head at Nicholas, and eyeing him coldly from top to toe.\n\n‘He’ll take a meal with us tonight,’ said Squeers, ‘and go among the boys tomorrow morning. You can give him a shake-down here, tonight, can’t you?’\n\n‘We must manage it somehow,’ replied the lady. ‘You don’t much mind how you sleep, I suppose, sir?’\n\nNo, indeed,’ replied Nicholas, ‘I am not particular.’\n\n‘That’s lucky,’ said Mrs. Squeers. And as the lady’s humour was considered to lie chiefly in retort, Mr. Squeers laughed heartily, and seemed to expect that Nicholas should do the same.\n\nAfter some further conversation between the master and mistress relative to the success of Mr. Squeers’s trip and the people who had paid, and the people who had made default in payment, a young servant girl brought in a Yorkshire pie and some cold beef, which being set upon the table, the boy Smike appeared with a jug of ale.\n\nMr. Squeers was emptying his great-coat pockets of letters to different boys, and other small documents, which he had brought down in them. The boy glanced, with an anxious and timid expression, at the papers, as if with a sickly hope that one among them might relate to him. The look was a very painful one, and went to Nicholas’s heart at once; for it told a long and very sad history.\n\nIt induced him to consider the boy more attentively, and he was surprised to observe the extraordinary mixture of garments which formed his dress. Although he could not have been less than eighteen or nineteen years old, and was tall for that age, he wore a skeleton suit, such as is usually put upon very little boys, and which, though most absurdly short in the arms and legs, was quite wide enough for his attenuated frame. In order that the lower part of his legs might be in perfect keeping with this singular dress, he had a very large pair of boots, originally made for tops, which might have been once worn by some stout farmer, but were now too patched and tattered for a beggar. Heaven knows how long he had been there, but he still wore the same linen which he had first taken down; for, round his neck, was a tattered child’s frill, only half concealed by a coarse, man’s neckerchief. He was lame; and as he feigned to be busy in arranging the table, glanced at the letters with a look so keen, and yet so dispirited and hopeless, that Nicholas could hardly bear to watch him.\n\n‘What are you bothering about there, Smike?’ cried Mrs. Squeers; ‘let the things alone, can’t you?’\n\n‘Eh!’ said Squeers, looking up. ‘Oh! it’s you, is it?’\n\n‘Yes, sir,’ replied the youth, pressing his hands together, as though to control, by force, the nervous wandering of his fingers. ‘Is there--’\n\n‘Well!’ said Squeers.\n\n‘Have you--did anybody--has nothing been heard--about me?’\n\n‘Devil a bit,’ replied Squeers testily.\n\nThe lad withdrew his eyes, and, putting his hand to his face, moved towards the door.\n\n‘Not a word,’ resumed Squeers, ‘and never will be. Now, this is a pretty sort of thing, isn’t it, that you should have been left here, all these years, and no money paid after the first six--nor no notice taken, nor no clue to be got who you belong to? It’s a pretty sort of thing that I should have to feed a great fellow like you, and never hope to get one penny for it, isn’t it?’\n\nThe boy put his hand to his head as if he were making an effort to recollect something, and then, looking vacantly at his questioner, gradually broke into a smile, and limped away.\n\n‘I’ll tell you what, Squeers,’ remarked his wife as the door closed, ‘I think that young chap’s turning silly.’\n\n‘I hope not,’ said the schoolmaster; ‘for he’s a handy fellow out of doors, and worth his meat and drink, anyway. I should think he’d have wit enough for us though, if he was. But come; let’s have supper, for I am hungry and tired, and want to get to bed.’\n\nThis reminder brought in an exclusive steak for Mr. Squeers, who speedily proceeded to do it ample justice. Nicholas drew up his chair, but his appetite was effectually taken away.\n\n‘How’s the steak, Squeers?’ said Mrs. S.\n\n‘Tender as a lamb,’ replied Squeers. ‘Have a bit.’\n\n‘I couldn’t eat a morsel,’ replied his wife. ‘What’ll the young man take, my dear?’\n\n‘Whatever he likes that’s present,’ rejoined Squeers, in a most unusual burst of generosity.\n\n‘What do you say, Mr. Knuckleboy?’ inquired Mrs. Squeers.\n\n‘I’ll take a little of the pie, if you please,’ replied Nicholas. ‘A very little, for I’m not hungry.’\n\nWell, it’s a pity to cut the pie if you’re not hungry, isn’t it?’ said Mrs. Squeers. ‘Will you try a bit of the beef?’\n\n‘Whatever you please,’ replied Nicholas abstractedly; ‘it’s all the same to me.’\n\nMrs. Squeers looked vastly gracious on receiving this reply; and nodding to Squeers, as much as to say that she was glad to find the young man knew his station, assisted Nicholas to a slice of meat with her own fair hands.\n\n‘Ale, Squeery?’ inquired the lady, winking and frowning to give him to understand that the question propounded, was, whether Nicholas should have ale, and not whether he (Squeers) would take any.\n\n‘Certainly,’ said Squeers, re-telegraphing in the same manner. ‘A glassful.’\n\nSo Nicholas had a glassful, and being occupied with his own reflections, drank it, in happy innocence of all the foregone proceedings.\n\n‘Uncommon juicy steak that,’ said Squeers, as he laid down his knife and fork, after plying it, in silence, for some time.\n\n‘It’s prime meat,’ rejoined his lady. ‘I bought a good large piece of it myself on purpose for--’\n\n‘For what!’ exclaimed Squeers hastily. ‘Not for the--’\n\n‘No, no; not for them,’ rejoined Mrs. Squeers; ‘on purpose for you against you came home. Lor! you didn’t think I could have made such a mistake as that.’\n\n‘Upon my word, my dear, I didn’t know what you were going to say,’ said Squeers, who had turned pale.\n\n‘You needn’t make yourself uncomfortable,’ remarked his wife, laughing heartily. ‘To think that I should be such a noddy! Well!’\n\nThis part of the conversation was rather unintelligible; but popular rumour in the neighbourhood asserted that Mr. Squeers, being amiably opposed to cruelty to animals, not unfrequently purchased for boy consumption the bodies of horned cattle who had died a natural death; possibly he was apprehensive of having unintentionally devoured some choice morsel intended for the young gentlemen.\n\nSupper being over, and removed by a small servant girl with a hungry eye, Mrs. Squeers retired to lock it up, and also to take into safe custody the clothes of the five boys who had just arrived, and who were half-way up the troublesome flight of steps which leads to death’s door, in consequence of exposure to the cold. They were then regaled with a light supper of porridge, and stowed away, side by side, in a small bedstead, to warm each other, and dream of a substantial meal with something hot after it, if their fancies set that way: which it is not at all improbable they did.\n\nMr. Squeers treated himself to a stiff tumbler of brandy and water, made on the liberal half-and-half principle, allowing for the dissolution of the sugar; and his amiable helpmate mixed Nicholas the ghost of a small glassful of the same compound. This done, Mr. and Mrs. Squeers drew close up to the fire, and sitting with their feet on the fender, talked confidentially in whispers; while Nicholas, taking up the tutor’s assistant, read the interesting legends in the miscellaneous questions, and all the figures into the bargain, with as much thought or consciousness of what he was doing, as if he had been in a magnetic slumber.\n\nAt length, Mr. Squeers yawned fearfully, and opined that it was high time to go to bed; upon which signal, Mrs. Squeers and the girl dragged in a small straw mattress and a couple of blankets, and arranged them into a couch for Nicholas.\n\n‘We’ll put you into your regular bedroom tomorrow, Nickelby,’ said Squeers. ‘Let me see! Who sleeps in Brooks’s bed, my dear?’\n\n‘In Brooks’s,’ said Mrs. Squeers, pondering. ‘There’s Jennings, little Bolder, Graymarsh, and what’s his name.’\n\n‘So there is,’ rejoined Squeers. ‘Yes! Brooks is full.’\n\n‘Full!’ thought Nicholas. ‘I should think he was.’\n\n‘There’s a place somewhere, I know,’ said Squeers; ‘but I can’t at this moment call to mind where it is. However, we’ll have that all settled tomorrow. Good-night, Nickleby. Seven o’clock in the morning, mind.’\n\n‘I shall be ready, sir,’ replied Nicholas. ‘Good-night.’\n\n‘I’ll come in myself and show you where the well is,’ said Squeers. ‘You’ll always find a little bit of soap in the kitchen window; that belongs to you.’\n\nNicholas opened his eyes, but not his mouth; and Squeers was again going away, when he once more turned back.\n\n‘I don’t know, I am sure,’ he said, ‘whose towel to put you on; but if you’ll make shift with something tomorrow morning, Mrs. Squeers will arrange that, in the course of the day. My dear, don’t forget.’\n\n‘I’ll take care,’ replied Mrs. Squeers; ‘and mind YOU take care, young man, and get first wash. The teacher ought always to have it; but they get the better of him if they can.’\n\nMr. Squeers then nudged Mrs. Squeers to bring away the brandy bottle, lest Nicholas should help himself in the night; and the lady having seized it with great precipitation, they retired together.\n\nNicholas, being left alone, took half-a-dozen turns up and down the room in a condition of much agitation and excitement; but, growing gradually calmer, sat himself down in a chair, and mentally resolved that, come what come might, he would endeavour, for a time, to bear whatever wretchedness might be in store for him, and that remembering the helplessness of his mother and sister, he would give his uncle no plea for deserting them in their need. Good resolutions seldom fail of producing some good effect in the mind from which they spring. He grew less desponding, and--so sanguine and buoyant is youth--even hoped that affairs at Dotheboys Hall might yet prove better than they promised.\n\nHe was preparing for bed, with something like renewed cheerfulness, when a sealed letter fell from his coat pocket. In the hurry of leaving London, it had escaped his attention, and had not occurred to him since, but it at once brought back to him the recollection of the mysterious behaviour of Newman Noggs.\n\n‘Dear me!’ said Nicholas; ‘what an extraordinary hand!’\n\nIt was directed to himself, was written upon very dirty paper, and in such cramped and crippled writing as to be almost illegible. After great difficulty and much puzzling, he contrived to read as follows:--\n\nMy dear young Man.\n\nI know the world. Your father did not, or he would not have done me a kindness when there was no hope of return. You do not, or you would not be bound on such a journey.\n\nIf ever you want a shelter in London (don’t be angry at this, I once thought I never should), they know where I live, at the sign of the Crown, in Silver Street, Golden Square. It is at the corner of Silver Street and James Street, with a bar door both ways. You can come at night. Once, nobody was ashamed--never mind that. It’s all over.\n\nExcuse errors. I should forget how to wear a whole coat now. I have forgotten all my old ways. My spelling may have gone with them.\n\n# NEWMAN NOGGS.\n\nP.S. If you should go near Barnard Castle, there is good ale at the King’s Head. Say you know me, and I am sure they will not charge you for it. You may say Mr. Noggs there, for I was a gentleman then. I was indeed.\n\nIt may be a very undignified circumstances to record, but after he had folded this letter and placed it in his pocket-book, Nicholas Nickleby’s eyes were dimmed with a moisture that might have been taken for tears.\n\nOf the Internal Economy of Dotheboys Hall\nA ride of two hundred and odd miles in severe weather, is one of the best softeners of a hard bed that ingenuity can devise. Perhaps it is even a sweetener of dreams, for those which hovered over the rough couch of Nicholas, and whispered their airy nothings in his ear, were of an agreeable and happy kind. He was making his fortune very fast indeed, when the faint glimmer of an expiring candle shone before his eyes, and a voice he had no difficulty in recognising as part and parcel of Mr Squeers, admonished him that it was time to rise.\n\n‘Past seven, Nickleby,’ said Mr. Squeers.\n\n‘Has morning come already?’ asked Nicholas, sitting up in bed.\n\n‘Ah! that has it,’ replied Squeers, ‘and ready iced too. Now, Nickleby, come; tumble up, will you?’\n\nNicholas needed no further admonition, but ‘tumbled up’ at once, and proceeded to dress himself by the light of the taper, which Mr. Squeers carried in his hand.\n\n‘Here’s a pretty go,’ said that gentleman; ‘the pump’s froze.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ said Nicholas, not much interested in the intelligence.\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Squeers. ‘You can’t wash yourself this morning.’\n\n‘Not wash myself!’ exclaimed Nicholas.\n\n‘No, not a bit of it,’ rejoined Squeers tartly. ‘So you must be content with giving yourself a dry polish till we break the ice in the well, and can get a bucketful out for the boys. Don’t stand staring at me, but do look sharp, will you?’\n\nOffering no further observation, Nicholas huddled on his clothes. Squeers, meanwhile, opened the shutters and blew the candle out; when the voice of his amiable consort was heard in the passage, demanding admittance.\n\n‘Come in, my love,’ said Squeers.\n\nMrs. Squeers came in, still habited in the primitive night-jacket which had displayed the symmetry of her figure on the previous night, and further ornamented with a beaver bonnet of some antiquity, which she wore, with much ease and lightness, on the top of the nightcap before mentioned.\n\n‘Drat the things,’ said the lady, opening the cupboard; ‘I can’t find the school spoon anywhere.’\n\n‘Never mind it, my dear,’ observed Squeers in a soothing manner; ‘it’s of no consequence.’\n\n‘No consequence, why how you talk!’ retorted Mrs. Squeers sharply; ‘isn’t it brimstone morning?’\n\n‘I forgot, my dear,’ rejoined Squeers; ‘yes, it certainly is. We purify the boys’ bloods now and then, Nickleby.’\n\n‘Purify fiddlesticks’ ends,’ said his lady. ‘Don’t think, young man, that we go to the expense of flower of brimstone and molasses, just to purify them; because if you think we carry on the business in that way, you’ll find yourself mistaken, and so I tell you plainly.’\n\n‘My dear,’ said Squeers frowning. ‘Hem!’\n\n‘Oh! nonsense,’ rejoined Mrs. Squeers. ‘If the young man comes to be a teacher here, let him understand, at once, that we don’t want any foolery about the boys. They have the brimstone and treacle, partly because if they hadn’t something or other in the way of medicine they’d be always ailing and giving a world of trouble, and partly because it spoils their appetites and comes cheaper than breakfast and dinner. So, it does them good and us good at the same time, and that’s fair enough I’m sure.’\n\nHaving given this explanation, Mrs. Squeers put her head into the closet and instituted a stricter search after the spoon, in which Mr. Squeers assisted. A few words passed between them while they were thus engaged, but as their voices were partially stifled by the cupboard, all that Nicholas could distinguish was, that Mr. Squeers said what Mrs. Squeers had said, was injudicious, and that Mrs. Squeers said what Mr. Squeers said, was ‘stuff.’\n\nA vast deal of searching and rummaging ensued, and it proving fruitless, Smike was called in, and pushed by Mrs. Squeers, and boxed by Mr. Squeers; which course of treatment brightening his intellects, enabled him to suggest that possibly Mrs. Squeers might have the spoon in her pocket, as indeed turned out to be the case. As Mrs. Squeers had previously protested, however, that she was quite certain she had not got it, Smike received another box on the ear for presuming to contradict his mistress, together with a promise of a sound thrashing if he were not more respectful in future; so that he took nothing very advantageous by his motion.\n\n‘A most invaluable woman, that, Nickleby,’ said Squeers when his consort had hurried away, pushing the drudge before her.\n\n‘Indeed, sir!’ observed Nicholas.\n\n‘I don’t know her equal,’ said Squeers; ‘I do not know her equal. That woman, Nickleby, is always the same--always the same bustling, lively, active, saving creetur that you see her now.’\n\nNicholas sighed involuntarily at the thought of the agreeable domestic prospect thus opened to him; but Squeers was, fortunately, too much occupied with his own reflections to perceive it.\n\n‘It’s my way to say, when I am up in London,’ continued Squeers, ‘that to them boys she is a mother. But she is more than a mother to them; ten times more. She does things for them boys, Nickleby, that I don’t believe half the mothers going, would do for their own sons.’\n\n‘I should think they would not, sir,’ answered Nicholas.\n\nNow, the fact was, that both Mr. and Mrs. Squeers viewed the boys in the light of their proper and natural enemies; or, in other words, they held and considered that their business and profession was to get as much from every boy as could by possibility be screwed out of him. On this point they were both agreed, and behaved in unison accordingly. The only difference between them was, that Mrs. Squeers waged war against the enemy openly and fearlessly, and that Squeers covered his rascality, even at home, with a spice of his habitual deceit; as if he really had a notion of someday or other being able to take himself in, and persuade his own mind that he was a very good fellow.\n\n‘But come,’ said Squeers, interrupting the progress of some thoughts to this effect in the mind of his usher, ‘let’s go to the schoolroom; and lend me a hand with my school-coat, will you?’\n\nNicholas assisted his master to put on an old fustian shooting-jacket, which he took down from a peg in the passage; and Squeers, arming himself with his cane, led the way across a yard, to a door in the rear of the house.\n\n‘There,’ said the schoolmaster as they stepped in together; ‘this is our shop, Nickleby!’\n\nIt was such a crowded scene, and there were so many objects to attract attention, that, at first, Nicholas stared about him, really without seeing anything at all. By degrees, however, the place resolved itself into a bare and dirty room, with a couple of windows, whereof a tenth part might be of glass, the remainder being stopped up with old copy-books and paper. There were a couple of long old rickety desks, cut and notched, and inked, and damaged, in every possible way; two or three forms; a detached desk for Squeers; and another for his assistant. The ceiling was supported, like that of a barn, by cross-beams and rafters; and the walls were so stained and discoloured, that it was impossible to tell whether they had ever been touched with paint or whitewash.\n\nBut the pupils--the young noblemen! How the last faint traces of hope, the remotest glimmering of any good to be derived from his efforts in this den, faded from the mind of Nicholas as he looked in dismay around! Pale and haggard faces, lank and bony figures, children with the countenances of old men, deformities with irons upon their limbs, boys of stunted growth, and others whose long meagre legs would hardly bear their stooping bodies, all crowded on the view together; there were the bleared eye, the hare-lip, the crooked foot, and every ugliness or distortion that told of unnatural aversion conceived by parents for their offspring, or of young lives which, from the earliest dawn of infancy, had been one horrible endurance of cruelty and neglect. There were little faces which should have been handsome, darkened with the scowl of sullen, dogged suffering; there was childhood with the light of its eye quenched, its beauty gone, and its helplessness alone remaining; there were vicious-faced boys, brooding, with leaden eyes, like malefactors in a jail; and there were young creatures on whom the sins of their frail parents had descended, weeping even for the mercenary nurses they had known, and lonesome even in their loneliness. With every kindly sympathy and affection blasted in its birth, with every young and healthy feeling flogged and starved down, with every revengeful passion that can fester in swollen hearts, eating its evil way to their core in silence, what an incipient Hell was breeding here!\n\nAnd yet this scene, painful as it was, had its grotesque features, which, in a less interested observer than Nicholas, might have provoked a smile. Mrs. Squeers stood at one of the desks, presiding over an immense basin of brimstone and treacle, of which delicious compound she administered a large instalment to each boy in succession: using for the purpose a common wooden spoon, which might have been originally manufactured for some gigantic top, and which widened every young gentleman’s mouth considerably: they being all obliged, under heavy corporal penalties, to take in the whole of the bowl at a gasp. In another corner, huddled together for companionship, were the little boys who had arrived on the preceding night, three of them in very large leather breeches, and two in old trousers, a something tighter fit than drawers are usually worn; at no great distance from these was seated the juvenile son and heir of Mr. Squeers--a striking likeness of his father--kicking, with great vigour, under the hands of Smike, who was fitting upon him a pair of new boots that bore a most suspicious resemblance to those which the least of the little boys had worn on the journey down--as the little boy himself seemed to think, for he was regarding the appropriation with a look of most rueful amazement. Besides these, there was a long row of boys waiting, with countenances of no pleasant anticipation, to be treacled; and another file, who had just escaped from the infliction, making a variety of wry mouths indicative of anything but satisfaction. The whole were attired in such motley, ill-assorted, extraordinary garments, as would have been irresistibly ridiculous, but for the foul appearance of dirt, disorder, and disease, with which they were associated.\n\n‘Now,’ said Squeers, giving the desk a great rap with his cane, which made half the little boys nearly jump out of their boots, ‘is that physicking over?’\n\n‘Just over,’ said Mrs. Squeers, choking the last boy in her hurry, and tapping the crown of his head with the wooden spoon to restore him. ‘Here, you Smike; take away now. Look sharp!’\n\nSmike shuffled out with the basin, and Mrs. Squeers having called up a little boy with a curly head, and wiped her hands upon it, hurried out after him into a species of wash-house, where there was a small fire and a large kettle, together with a number of little wooden bowls which were arranged upon a board.\n\nInto these bowls, Mrs. Squeers, assisted by the hungry servant, poured a brown composition, which looked like diluted pincushions without the covers, and was called porridge. A minute wedge of brown bread was inserted in each bowl, and when they had eaten their porridge by means of the bread, the boys ate the bread itself, and had finished their breakfast; whereupon Mr. Squeers said, in a solemn voice, ‘For what we have received, may the Lord make us truly thankful!’--and went away to his own.\n\nNicholas distended his stomach with a bowl of porridge, for much the same reason which induces some savages to swallow earth--lest they should be inconveniently hungry when there is nothing to eat. Having further disposed of a slice of bread and butter, allotted to him in virtue of his office, he sat himself down, to wait for school-time.\n\nHe could not but observe how silent and sad the boys all seemed to be. There was none of the noise and clamour of a schoolroom; none of its boisterous play, or hearty mirth. The children sat crouching and shivering together, and seemed to lack the spirit to move about. The only pupil who evinced the slightest tendency towards locomotion or playfulness was Master Squeers, and as his chief amusement was to tread upon the other boys’ toes in his new boots, his flow of spirits was rather disagreeable than otherwise.\n\nAfter some half-hour’s delay, Mr. Squeers reappeared, and the boys took their places and their books, of which latter commodity the average might be about one to eight learners. A few minutes having elapsed, during which Mr. Squeers looked very profound, as if he had a perfect apprehension of what was inside all the books, and could say every word of their contents by heart if he only chose to take the trouble, that gentleman called up the first class.\n\nObedient to this summons there ranged themselves in front of the schoolmaster’s desk, half-a-dozen scarecrows, out at knees and elbows, one of whom placed a torn and filthy book beneath his learned eye.\n\n‘This is the first class in English spelling and philosophy, Nickleby,’ said Squeers, beckoning Nicholas to stand beside him. ‘We’ll get up a Latin one, and hand that over to you. Now, then, where’s the first boy?’\n\n‘Please, sir, he’s cleaning the back-parlour window,’ said the temporary head of the philosophical class.\n\n‘So he is, to be sure,’ rejoined Squeers. ‘We go upon the practical mode of teaching, Nickleby; the regular education system. C-l-e-a-n, clean, verb active, to make bright, to scour. W-i-n, win, d-e-r, der, winder, a casement. When the boy knows this out of book, he goes and does it. It’s just the same principle as the use of the globes. Where’s the second boy?’\n\n‘Please, sir, he’s weeding the garden,’ replied a small voice.\n\n‘To be sure,’ said Squeers, by no means disconcerted. ‘So he is. B-o-t, bot, t-i-n, tin, bottin, n-e-y, ney, bottinney, noun substantive, a knowledge of plants. When he has learned that bottinney means a knowledge of plants, he goes and knows ‘em. That’s our system, Nickleby: what do you think of it?’\n\n‘It’s very useful one, at any rate,’ answered Nicholas.\n\n‘I believe you,’ rejoined Squeers, not remarking the emphasis of his usher. ‘Third boy, what’s horse?’\n\n‘A beast, sir,’ replied the boy.\n\n‘So it is,’ said Squeers. ‘Ain’t it, Nickleby?’\n\n‘I believe there is no doubt of that, sir,’ answered Nicholas.\n\n‘Of course there isn’t,’ said Squeers. ‘A horse is a quadruped, and quadruped’s Latin for beast, as everybody that’s gone through the grammar knows, or else where’s the use of having grammars at all?’\n\n‘Where, indeed!’ said Nicholas abstractedly.\n\n‘As you’re perfect in that,’ resumed Squeers, turning to the boy, ‘go and look after MY horse, and rub him down well, or I’ll rub you down. The rest of the class go and draw water up, till somebody tells you to leave off, for it’s washing-day tomorrow, and they want the coppers filled.’\n\nSo saying, he dismissed the first class to their experiments in practical philosophy, and eyed Nicholas with a look, half cunning and half doubtful, as if he were not altogether certain what he might think of him by this time.\n\n‘That’s the way we do it, Nickleby,’ he said, after a pause.\n\nNicholas shrugged his shoulders in a manner that was scarcely perceptible, and said he saw it was.\n\n‘And a very good way it is, too,’ said Squeers. ‘Now, just take them fourteen little boys and hear them some reading, because, you know, you must begin to be useful. Idling about here won’t do.’\n\nMr. Squeers said this, as if it had suddenly occurred to him, either that he must not say too much to his assistant, or that his assistant did not say enough to him in praise of the establishment. The children were arranged in a semicircle round the new master, and he was soon listening to their dull, drawling, hesitating recital of those stories of engrossing interest which are to be found in the more antiquated spelling-books.\n\nIn this exciting occupation, the morning lagged heavily on. At one o’clock, the boys, having previously had their appetites thoroughly taken away by stir-about and potatoes, sat down in the kitchen to some hard salt beef, of which Nicholas was graciously permitted to take his portion to his own solitary desk, to eat it there in peace. After this, there was another hour of crouching in the schoolroom and shivering with cold, and then school began again.\n\nIt was Mr. Squeer’s custom to call the boys together, and make a sort of report, after every half-yearly visit to the metropolis, regarding the relations and friends he had seen, the news he had heard, the letters he had brought down, the bills which had been paid, the accounts which had been left unpaid, and so forth. This solemn proceeding always took place in the afternoon of the day succeeding his return; perhaps, because the boys acquired strength of mind from the suspense of the morning, or, possibly, because Mr. Squeers himself acquired greater sternness and inflexibility from certain warm potations in which he was wont to indulge after his early dinner. Be this as it may, the boys were recalled from house-window, garden, stable, and cow-yard, and the school were assembled in full conclave, when Mr. Squeers, with a small bundle of papers in his hand, and Mrs. S. following with a pair of canes, entered the room and proclaimed silence.\n\n‘Let any boy speak a word without leave,’ said Mr. Squeers mildly, ‘and I’ll take the skin off his back.’\n\nThis special proclamation had the desired effect, and a deathlike silence immediately prevailed, in the midst of which Mr. Squeers went on to say:\n\n‘Boys, I’ve been to London, and have returned to my family and you, as strong and well as ever.’\n\nAccording to half-yearly custom, the boys gave three feeble cheers at this refreshing intelligence. Such cheers! Sights of extra strength with the chill on.\n\n‘I have seen the parents of some boys,’ continued Squeers, turning over his papers, ‘and they’re so glad to hear how their sons are getting on, that there’s no prospect at all of their going away, which of course is a very pleasant thing to reflect upon, for all parties.’\n\nTwo or three hands went to two or three eyes when Squeers said this, but the greater part of the young gentlemen having no particular parents to speak of, were wholly uninterested in the thing one way or other.\n\n‘I have had disappointments to contend against,’ said Squeers, looking very grim; ‘Bolder’s father was two pound ten short. Where is Bolder?’\n\n‘Here he is, please sir,’ rejoined twenty officious voices. Boys are very like men to be sure.\n\n‘Come here, Bolder,’ said Squeers.\n\nAn unhealthy-looking boy, with warts all over his hands, stepped from his place to the master’s desk, and raised his eyes imploringly to Squeers’s face; his own, quite white from the rapid beating of his heart.\n\n‘Bolder,’ said Squeers, speaking very slowly, for he was considering, as the saying goes, where to have him. ‘Bolder, if you father thinks that because--why, what’s this, sir?’\n\nAs Squeers spoke, he caught up the boy’s hand by the cuff of his jacket, and surveyed it with an edifying aspect of horror and disgust.\n\n‘What do you call this, sir?’ demanded the schoolmaster, administering a cut with the cane to expedite the reply.\n\n‘I can’t help it, indeed, sir,’ rejoined the boy, crying. ‘They will come; it’s the dirty work I think, sir--at least I don’t know what it is, sir, but it’s not my fault.’\n\n‘Bolder,’ said Squeers, tucking up his wristbands, and moistening the palm of his right hand to get a good grip of the cane, ‘you’re an incorrigible young scoundrel, and as the last thrashing did you no good, we must see what another will do towards beating it out of you.’\n\nWith this, and wholly disregarding a piteous cry for mercy, Mr. Squeers fell upon the boy and caned him soundly: not leaving off, indeed, until his arm was tired out.\n\n‘There,’ said Squeers, when he had quite done; ‘rub away as hard as you like, you won’t rub that off in a hurry. Oh! you won’t hold that noise, won’t you? Put him out, Smike.’\n\nThe drudge knew better from long experience, than to hesitate about obeying, so he bundled the victim out by a side-door, and Mr. Squeers perched himself again on his own stool, supported by Mrs. Squeers, who occupied another at his side.\n\n‘Now let us see,’ said Squeers. ‘A letter for Cobbey. Stand up, Cobbey.’\n\nAnother boy stood up, and eyed the letter very hard while Squeers made a mental abstract of the same.\n\n‘Oh!’ said Squeers: ‘Cobbey’s grandmother is dead, and his uncle John has took to drinking, which is all the news his sister sends, except eighteenpence, which will just pay for that broken square of glass. Mrs Squeers, my dear, will you take the money?’\n\nThe worthy lady pocketed the eighteenpence with a most business-like air, and Squeers passed on to the next boy, as coolly as possible.\n\n‘Graymarsh,’ said Squeers, ‘he’s the next. Stand up, Graymarsh.’\n\nAnother boy stood up, and the schoolmaster looked over the letter as before.\n\n‘Graymarsh’s maternal aunt,’ said Squeers, when he had possessed himself of the contents, ‘is very glad to hear he’s so well and happy, and sends her respectful compliments to Mrs. Squeers, and thinks she must be an angel. She likewise thinks Mr. Squeers is too good for this world; but hopes he may long be spared to carry on the business. Would have sent the two pair of stockings as desired, but is short of money, so forwards a tract instead, and hopes Graymarsh will put his trust in Providence. Hopes, above all, that he will study in everything to please Mr. and Mrs Squeers, and look upon them as his only friends; and that he will love Master Squeers; and not object to sleeping five in a bed, which no Christian should. Ah!’ said Squeers, folding it up, ‘a delightful letter. Very affecting indeed.’\n\nIt was affecting in one sense, for Graymarsh’s maternal aunt was strongly supposed, by her more intimate friends, to be no other than his maternal parent; Squeers, however, without alluding to this part of the story (which would have sounded immoral before boys), proceeded with the business by calling out ‘Mobbs,’ whereupon another boy rose, and Graymarsh resumed his seat.\n\n‘Mobbs’s step-mother,’ said Squeers, ‘took to her bed on hearing that he wouldn’t eat fat, and has been very ill ever since. She wishes to know, by an early post, where he expects to go to, if he quarrels with his vittles; and with what feelings he could turn up his nose at the cow’s-liver broth, after his good master had asked a blessing on it. This was told her in the London newspapers--not by Mr. Squeers, for he is too kind and too good to set anybody against anybody--and it has vexed her so much, Mobbs can’t think. She is sorry to find he is discontented, which is sinful and horrid, and hopes Mr. Squeers will flog him into a happier state of mind; with which view, she has also stopped his halfpenny a week pocket-money, and given a double-bladed knife with a corkscrew in it to the Missionaries, which she had bought on purpose for him.’\n\n‘A sulky state of feeling,’ said Squeers, after a terrible pause, during which he had moistened the palm of his right hand again, ‘won’t do. Cheerfulness and contentment must be kept up. Mobbs, come to me!’\n\nMobbs moved slowly towards the desk, rubbing his eyes in anticipation of good cause for doing so; and he soon afterwards retired by the side-door, with as good cause as a boy need have.\n\nMr. Squeers then proceeded to open a miscellaneous collection of letters; some enclosing money, which Mrs. Squeers ‘took care of;’ and others referring to small articles of apparel, as caps and so forth, all of which the same lady stated to be too large, or too small, and calculated for nobody but young Squeers, who would appear indeed to have had most accommodating limbs, since everything that came into the school fitted him to a nicety. His head, in particular, must have been singularly elastic, for hats and caps of all dimensions were alike to him.\n\nThis business dispatched, a few slovenly lessons were performed, and Squeers retired to his fireside, leaving Nicholas to take care of the boys in the school-room, which was very cold, and where a meal of bread and cheese was served out shortly after dark.\n\nThere was a small stove at that corner of the room which was nearest to the master’s desk, and by it Nicholas sat down, so depressed and self-degraded by the consciousness of his position, that if death could have come upon him at that time, he would have been almost happy to meet it. The cruelty of which he had been an unwilling witness, the coarse and ruffianly behaviour of Squeers even in his best moods, the filthy place, the sights and sounds about him, all contributed to this state of feeling; but when he recollected that, being there as an assistant, he actually seemed--no matter what unhappy train of circumstances had brought him to that pass--to be the aider and abettor of a system which filled him with honest disgust and indignation, he loathed himself, and felt, for the moment, as though the mere consciousness of his present situation must, through all time to come, prevent his raising his head again.\n\nBut, for the present, his resolve was taken, and the resolution he had formed on the preceding night remained undisturbed. He had written to his mother and sister, announcing the safe conclusion of his journey, and saying as little about Dotheboys Hall, and saying that little as cheerfully, as he possibly could. He hoped that by remaining where he was, he might do some good, even there; at all events, others depended too much on his uncle’s favour, to admit of his awakening his wrath just then.\n\nOne reflection disturbed him far more than any selfish considerations arising out of his own position. This was the probable destination of his sister Kate. His uncle had deceived him, and might he not consign her to some miserable place where her youth and beauty would prove a far greater curse than ugliness and decrepitude? To a caged man, bound hand and foot, this was a terrible idea--but no, he thought, his mother was by; there was the portrait-painter, too--simple enough, but still living in the world, and of it. He was willing to believe that Ralph Nickleby had conceived a personal dislike to himself. Having pretty good reason, by this time, to reciprocate it, he had no great difficulty in arriving at this conclusion, and tried to persuade himself that the feeling extended no farther than between them.\n\nAs he was absorbed in these meditations, he all at once encountered the upturned face of Smike, who was on his knees before the stove, picking a few stray cinders from the hearth and planting them on the fire. He had paused to steal a look at Nicholas, and when he saw that he was observed, shrunk back, as if expecting a blow.\n\n‘You need not fear me,’ said Nicholas kindly. ‘Are you cold?’\n‘N-n-o.’\n\n‘You are shivering.’\n\n‘I am not cold,’ replied Smike quickly. ‘I am used to it.’\n\nThere was such an obvious fear of giving offence in his manner, and he was such a timid, broken-spirited creature, that Nicholas could not help exclaiming, ‘Poor fellow!’\n\nIf he had struck the drudge, he would have slunk away without a word. But, now, he burst into tears.\n\n‘Oh dear, oh dear!’ he cried, covering his face with his cracked and horny hands. ‘My heart will break. It will, it will.’\n\n‘Hush!’ said Nicholas, laying his hand upon his shoulder. ‘Be a man; you are nearly one by years, God help you.’\n\n‘By years!’ cried Smike. ‘Oh dear, dear, how many of them! How many of them since I was a little child, younger than any that are here now! Where are they all!’\n\n‘Whom do you speak of?’ inquired Nicholas, wishing to rouse the poor half-witted creature to reason. ‘Tell me.’\n\n‘My friends,’ he replied, ‘myself--my--oh! what sufferings mine have been!’\n\n‘There is always hope,’ said Nicholas; he knew not what to say.\n\n‘No,’ rejoined the other, ‘no; none for me. Do you remember the boy that died here?’\n\n‘I was not here, you know,’ said Nicholas gently; ‘but what of him?’\n\n‘Why,’ replied the youth, drawing closer to his questioner’s side, ‘I was with him at night, and when it was all silent he cried no more for friends he wished to come and sit with him, but began to see faces round his bed that came from home; he said they smiled, and talked to him; and he died at last lifting his head to kiss them. Do you hear?’\n\n‘Yes, yes,’ rejoined Nicholas.\n\n‘What faces will smile on me when I die!’ cried his companion, shivering. ‘Who will talk to me in those long nights! They cannot come from home; they would frighten me, if they did, for I don’t know what it is, and shouldn’t know them. Pain and fear, pain and fear for me, alive or dead. No hope, no hope!’\n\nThe bell rang to bed: and the boy, subsiding at the sound into his usual listless state, crept away as if anxious to avoid notice. It was with a heavy heart that Nicholas soon afterwards--no, not retired; there was no retirement there--followed--to his dirty and crowded dormitory.\n\n# CHAPTER 9\n\nOf Miss Squeers, Mrs. Squeers, Master Squeers, and Mr. Squeers; and of various Matters and Persons connected no less with the Squeerses than Nicholas Nickleby\nWhen Mr. Squeers left the schoolroom for the night, he betook himself, as has been before remarked, to his own fireside, which was situated--not in the room in which Nicholas had supped on the night of his arrival, but in a smaller apartment in the rear of the premises, where his lady wife, his amiable son, and accomplished daughter, were in the full enjoyment of each other’s society; Mrs. Squeers being engaged in the matronly pursuit of stocking-darning; and the young lady and gentleman being occupied in the adjustment of some youthful differences, by means of a pugilistic contest across the table, which, on the approach of their honoured parent, subsided into a noiseless exchange of kicks beneath it.\n\nAnd, in this place, it may be as well to apprise the reader, that Miss Fanny Squeers was in her three-and-twentieth year. If there be any one grace or loveliness inseparable from that particular period of life, Miss Squeers may be presumed to have been possessed of it, as there is no reason to suppose that she was a solitary exception to an universal rule. She was not tall like her mother, but short like her father; from the former she inherited a voice of harsh quality; from the latter a remarkable expression of the right eye, something akin to having none at all.\n\nMiss Squeers had been spending a few days with a neighbouring friend, and had only just returned to the parental roof. To this circumstance may be referred, her having heard nothing of Nicholas, until Mr. Squeers himself now made him the subject of conversation.\n\n‘Well, my dear,’ said Squeers, drawing up his chair, ‘what do you think of him by this time?’\n\n‘Think of who?’ inquired Mrs. Squeers; who (as she often remarked) was no grammarian, thank Heaven.\n\n‘Of the young man--the new teacher--who else could I mean?’\n\n‘Oh! that Knuckleboy,’ said Mrs. Squeers impatiently. ‘I hate him.’\n\n‘What do you hate him for, my dear?’ asked Squeers.\n\n‘What’s that to you?’ retorted Mrs. Squeers. ‘If I hate him, that’s enough, ain’t it?’\n\n‘Quite enough for him, my dear, and a great deal too much I dare say, if he knew it,’ replied Squeers in a pacific tone. ‘I only ask from curiosity, my dear.’\n\n‘Well, then, if you want to know,’ rejoined Mrs. Squeers, ‘I’ll tell you. Because he’s a proud, haughty, consequential, turned-up-nosed peacock.’\n\nMrs. Squeers, when excited, was accustomed to use strong language, and, moreover, to make use of a plurality of epithets, some of which were of a figurative kind, as the word peacock, and furthermore the allusion to Nicholas’s nose, which was not intended to be taken in its literal sense, but rather to bear a latitude of construction according to the fancy of the hearers.\n\nNeither were they meant to bear reference to each other, so much as to the object on whom they were bestowed, as will be seen in the present case: a peacock with a turned-up nose being a novelty in ornithology, and a thing not commonly seen.\n\n‘Hem!’ said Squeers, as if in mild deprecation of this outbreak. ‘He is cheap, my dear; the young man is very cheap.’\n\n‘Not a bit of it,’ retorted Mrs. Squeers.\n\n‘Five pound a year,’ said Squeers.\n\n‘What of that; it’s dear if you don’t want him, isn’t it?’ replied his wife.\n\n‘But we DO want him,’ urged Squeers.\n\n‘I don’t see that you want him any more than the dead,’ said Mrs. Squeers. ‘Don’t tell me. You can put on the cards and in the advertisements, “Education by Mr. Wackford Squeers and able assistants,” without having any assistants, can’t you? Isn’t it done every day by all the masters about? I’ve no patience with you.’\n\n‘Haven’t you!’ said Squeers, sternly. ‘Now I’ll tell you what, Mrs Squeers. In this matter of having a teacher, I’ll take my own way, if you please. A slave driver in the West Indies is allowed a man under him, to see that his blacks don’t run away, or get up a rebellion; and I’ll have a man under me to do the same with OUR blacks, till such time as little Wackford is able to take charge of the school.’\n\n‘Am I to take care of the school when I grow up a man, father?’ said Wackford junior, suspending, in the excess of his delight, a vicious kick which he was administering to his sister.\n\n‘You are, my son,’ replied Mr. Squeers, in a sentimental voice.\n\n‘Oh my eye, won’t I give it to the boys!’ exclaimed the interesting child, grasping his father’s cane. ‘Oh, father, won’t I make ‘em squeak again!’\n\nIt was a proud moment in Mr. Squeers’s life, when he witnessed that burst of enthusiasm in his young child’s mind, and saw in it a foreshadowing of his future eminence. He pressed a penny into his hand, and gave vent to his feelings (as did his exemplary wife also), in a shout of approving laughter. The infantine appeal to their common sympathies, at once restored cheerfulness to the conversation, and harmony to the company.\n\n‘He’s a nasty stuck-up monkey, that’s what I consider him,’ said Mrs Squeers, reverting to Nicholas.\n\n‘Supposing he is,’ said Squeers, ‘he is as well stuck up in our schoolroom as anywhere else, isn’t he?--especially as he don’t like it.’\n\n‘Well,’ observed Mrs. Squeers, ‘there’s something in that. I hope it’ll bring his pride down, and it shall be no fault of mine if it don’t.’\n\nNow, a proud usher in a Yorkshire school was such a very extraordinary and unaccountable thing to hear of,--any usher at all being a novelty; but a proud one, a being of whose existence the wildest imagination could never have dreamed--that Miss Squeers, who seldom troubled herself with scholastic matters, inquired with much curiosity who this Knuckleboy was, that gave himself such airs.\n\n‘Nickleby,’ said Squeers, spelling the name according to some eccentric system which prevailed in his own mind; ‘your mother always calls things and people by their wrong names.’\n\n‘No matter for that,’ said Mrs. Squeers; ‘I see them with right eyes, and that’s quite enough for me. I watched him when you were laying on to little Bolder this afternoon. He looked as black as thunder, all the while, and, one time, started up as if he had more than got it in his mind to make a rush at you. I saw him, though he thought I didn’t.’\n\n‘Never mind that, father,’ said Miss Squeers, as the head of the family was about to reply. ‘Who is the man?’\n\n‘Why, your father has got some nonsense in his head that he’s the son of a poor gentleman that died the other day,’ said Mrs. Squeers.\n\n‘The son of a gentleman!’\n\n‘Yes; but I don’t believe a word of it. If he’s a gentleman’s son at all, he’s a fondling, that’s my opinion.’\n\n‘Mrs. Squeers intended to say ‘foundling,’ but, as she frequently remarked when she made any such mistake, it would be all the same a hundred years hence; with which axiom of philosophy, indeed, she was in the constant habit of consoling the boys when they laboured under more than ordinary ill-usage.\n\n‘He’s nothing of the kind,’ said Squeers, in answer to the above remark, ‘for his father was married to his mother years before he was born, and she is alive now. If he was, it would be no business of ours, for we make a very good friend by having him here; and if he likes to learn the boys anything besides minding them, I have no objection I am sure.’\n\n‘I say again, I hate him worse than poison,’ said Mrs. Squeers vehemently.\n\n‘If you dislike him, my dear,’ returned Squeers, ‘I don’t know anybody who can show dislike better than you, and of course there’s no occasion, with him, to take the trouble to hide it.’\n\n‘I don’t intend to, I assure you,’ interposed Mrs. S.\n\n‘That’s right,’ said Squeers; ‘and if he has a touch of pride about him, as I think he has, I don’t believe there’s woman in all England that can bring anybody’s spirit down, as quick as you can, my love.’\n\nMrs. Squeers chuckled vastly on the receipt of these flattering compliments, and said, she hoped she had tamed a high spirit or two in her day. It is but due to her character to say, that in conjunction with her estimable husband, she had broken many and many a one.\n\nMiss Fanny Squeers carefully treasured up this, and much more conversation on the same subject, until she retired for the night, when she questioned the hungry servant, minutely, regarding the outward appearance and demeanour of Nicholas; to which queries the girl returned such enthusiastic replies, coupled with so many laudatory remarks touching his beautiful dark eyes, and his sweet smile, and his straight legs--upon which last-named articles she laid particular stress; the general run of legs at Dotheboys Hall being crooked--that Miss Squeers was not long in arriving at the conclusion that the new usher must be a very remarkable person, or, as she herself significantly phrased it, ‘something quite out of the common.’ And so Miss Squeers made up her mind that she would take a personal observation of Nicholas the very next day.\n\nIn pursuance of this design, the young lady watched the opportunity of her mother being engaged, and her father absent, and went accidentally into the schoolroom to get a pen mended: where, seeing nobody but Nicholas presiding over the boys, she blushed very deeply, and exhibited great confusion.\n\n‘I beg your pardon,’ faltered Miss Squeers; ‘I thought my father was--or might be--dear me, how very awkward!’\n\n‘Mr. Squeers is out,’ said Nicholas, by no means overcome by the apparition, unexpected though it was.\n\n‘Do you know will he be long, sir?’ asked Miss Squeers, with bashful hesitation.\n\n‘He said about an hour,’ replied Nicholas--politely of course, but without any indication of being stricken to the heart by Miss Squeers’s charms.\n\n‘I never knew anything happen so cross,’ exclaimed the young lady. ‘Thank you! I am very sorry I intruded, I am sure. If I hadn’t thought my father was here, I wouldn’t upon any account have--it is very provoking--must look so very strange,’ murmured Miss Squeers, blushing once more, and glancing, from the pen in her hand, to Nicholas at his desk, and back again.\n\n‘If that is all you want,’ said Nicholas, pointing to the pen, and smiling, in spite of himself, at the affected embarrassment of the schoolmaster’s daughter, ‘perhaps I can supply his place.’\n\nMiss Squeers glanced at the door, as if dubious of the propriety of advancing any nearer to an utter stranger; then round the schoolroom, as though in some measure reassured by the presence of forty boys; and finally sidled up to Nicholas and delivered the pen into his hand, with a most winning mixture of reserve and condescension.\n\n‘Shall it be a hard or a soft nib?’ inquired Nicholas, smiling to prevent himself from laughing outright.\n\n‘He HAS a beautiful smile,’ thought Miss Squeers.\n\n‘Which did you say?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘Dear me, I was thinking of something else for the moment, I declare,’ replied Miss Squeers. ‘Oh! as soft as possible, if you please.’ With which words, Miss Squeers sighed. It might be, to give Nicholas to understand that her heart was soft, and that the pen was wanted to match.\n\nUpon these instructions Nicholas made the pen; when he gave it to Miss Squeers, Miss Squeers dropped it; and when he stooped to pick it up, Miss Squeers stooped also, and they knocked their heads together; whereat five-and-twenty little boys laughed aloud: being positively for the first and only time that half-year.\n\n‘Very awkward of me,’ said Nicholas, opening the door for the young lady’s retreat.\n\n‘Not at all, sir,’ replied Miss Squeers; ‘it was my fault. It was all my foolish--a--a--good-morning!’\n\n‘Goodbye,’ said Nicholas. ‘The next I make for you, I hope will be made less clumsily. Take care! You are biting the nib off now.’\n\n‘Really,’ said Miss Squeers; ‘so embarrassing that I scarcely know what I--very sorry to give you so much trouble.’\n\n‘Not the least trouble in the world,’ replied Nicholas, closing the schoolroom door.\n\n‘I never saw such legs in the whole course of my life!’ said Miss Squeers, as she walked away.\n\nIn fact, Miss Squeers was in love with Nicholas Nickleby.\n\nTo account for the rapidity with which this young lady had conceived a passion for Nicholas, it may be necessary to state, that the friend from whom she had so recently returned, was a miller’s daughter of only eighteen, who had contracted herself unto the son of a small corn-factor, resident in the nearest market town. Miss Squeers and the miller’s daughter, being fast friends, had covenanted together some two years before, according to a custom prevalent among young ladies, that whoever was first engaged to be married, should straightway confide the mighty secret to the bosom of the other, before communicating it to any living soul, and bespeak her as bridesmaid without loss of time; in fulfilment of which pledge the miller’s daughter, when her engagement was formed, came out express, at eleven o’clock at night as the corn-factor’s son made an offer of his hand and heart at twenty-five minutes past ten by the Dutch clock in the kitchen, and rushed into Miss Squeers’s bedroom with the gratifying intelligence. Now, Miss Squeers being five years older, and out of her teens (which is also a great matter), had, since, been more than commonly anxious to return the compliment, and possess her friend with a similar secret; but, either in consequence of finding it hard to please herself, or harder still to please anybody else, had never had an opportunity so to do, inasmuch as she had no such secret to disclose. The little interview with Nicholas had no sooner passed, as above described, however, than Miss Squeers, putting on her bonnet, made her way, with great precipitation, to her friend’s house, and, upon a solemn renewal of divers old vows of secrecy, revealed how that she was--not exactly engaged, but going to be--to a gentleman’s son--(none of your corn-factors, but a gentleman’s son of high descent)--who had come down as teacher to Dotheboys Hall, under most mysterious and remarkable circumstances--indeed, as Miss Squeers more than once hinted she had good reason to believe, induced, by the fame of her many charms, to seek her out, and woo and win her.\n\n‘Isn’t it an extraordinary thing?’ said Miss Squeers, emphasising the adjective strongly.\n\n‘Most extraordinary,’ replied the friend. ‘But what has he said to you?’\n\n‘Don’t ask me what he said, my dear,’ rejoined Miss Squeers. ‘If you had only seen his looks and smiles! I never was so overcome in all my life.’\n\n‘Did he look in this way?’ inquired the miller’s daughter, counterfeiting, as nearly as she could, a favourite leer of the corn-factor.\n\n‘Very like that--only more genteel,’ replied Miss Squeers.\n\n‘Ah!’ said the friend, ‘then he means something, depend on it.’\n\nMiss Squeers, having slight misgivings on the subject, was by no means ill pleased to be confirmed by a competent authority; and discovering, on further conversation and comparison of notes, a great many points of resemblance between the behaviour of Nicholas, and that of the corn-factor, grew so exceedingly confidential, that she intrusted her friend with a vast number of things Nicholas had NOT said, which were all so very complimentary as to be quite conclusive. Then, she dilated on the fearful hardship of having a father and mother strenuously opposed to her intended husband; on which unhappy circumstance she dwelt at great length; for the friend’s father and mother were quite agreeable to her being married, and the whole courtship was in consequence as flat and common-place an affair as it was possible to imagine.\n\n‘How I should like to see him!’ exclaimed the friend.\n\n‘So you shall, ‘Tilda,’ replied Miss Squeers. ‘I should consider myself one of the most ungrateful creatures alive, if I denied you. I think mother’s going away for two days to fetch some boys; and when she does, I’ll ask you and John up to tea, and have him to meet you.’\n\nThis was a charming idea, and having fully discussed it, the friends parted.\n\nIt so fell out, that Mrs. Squeers’s journey, to some distance, to fetch three new boys, and dun the relations of two old ones for the balance of a small account, was fixed that very afternoon, for the next day but one; and on the next day but one, Mrs. Squeers got up outside the coach, as it stopped to change at Greta Bridge, taking with her a small bundle containing something in a bottle, and some sandwiches, and carrying besides a large white top-coat to wear in the night-time; with which baggage she went her way.\n\nWhenever such opportunities as these occurred, it was Squeers’s custom to drive over to the market town, every evening, on pretence of urgent business, and stop till ten or eleven o’clock at a tavern he much affected. As the party was not in his way, therefore, but rather afforded a means of compromise with Miss Squeers, he readily yielded his full assent thereunto, and willingly communicated to Nicholas that he was expected to take his tea in the parlour that evening, at five o’clock.\n\nTo be sure Miss Squeers was in a desperate flutter as the time approached, and to be sure she was dressed out to the best advantage: with her hair--it had more than a tinge of red, and she wore it in a crop--curled in five distinct rows, up to the very top of her head, and arranged dexterously over the doubtful eye; to say nothing of the blue sash which floated down her back, or the worked apron or the long gloves, or the green gauze scarf worn over one shoulder and under the other; or any of the numerous devices which were to be as so many arrows to the heart of Nicholas. She had scarcely completed these arrangements to her entire satisfaction, when the friend arrived with a whity-brown parcel--flat and three-cornered--containing sundry small adornments which were to be put on upstairs, and which the friend put on, talking incessantly. When Miss Squeers had ‘done’ the friend’s hair, the friend ‘did’ Miss Squeers’s hair, throwing in some striking improvements in the way of ringlets down the neck; and then, when they were both touched up to their entire satisfaction, they went downstairs in full state with the long gloves on, all ready for company.\n\n‘Where’s John, ‘Tilda?’ said Miss Squeers.\n\n‘Only gone home to clean himself,’ replied the friend. ‘He will be here by the time the tea’s drawn.’\n\n‘I do so palpitate,’ observed Miss Squeers.\n\n‘Ah! I know what it is,’ replied the friend.\n\n‘I have not been used to it, you know, ‘Tilda,’ said Miss Squeers, applying her hand to the left side of her sash.\n\n‘You’ll soon get the better of it, dear,’ rejoined the friend. While they were talking thus, the hungry servant brought in the tea-things, and, soon afterwards, somebody tapped at the room door.\n\n‘There he is!’ cried Miss Squeers. ‘Oh ‘Tilda!’\n\n‘Hush!’ said ‘Tilda. ‘Hem! Say, come in.’\n\n‘Come in,’ cried Miss Squeers faintly. And in walked Nicholas.\n\n‘Good-evening,’ said that young gentleman, all unconscious of his conquest. ‘I understood from Mr. Squeers that--’\n\n‘Oh yes; it’s all right,’ interposed Miss Squeers. ‘Father don’t tea with us, but you won’t mind that, I dare say.’ (This was said archly.)\n\nNicholas opened his eyes at this, but he turned the matter off very coolly--not caring, particularly, about anything just then--and went through the ceremony of introduction to the miller’s daughter with so much grace, that that young lady was lost in admiration.\n\n‘We are only waiting for one more gentleman,’ said Miss Squeers, taking off the teapot lid, and looking in, to see how the tea was getting on.\n\nIt was matter of equal moment to Nicholas whether they were waiting for one gentleman or twenty, so he received the intelligence with perfect unconcern; and, being out of spirits, and not seeing any especial reason why he should make himself agreeable, looked out of the window and sighed involuntarily.\n\nAs luck would have it, Miss Squeers’s friend was of a playful turn, and hearing Nicholas sigh, she took it into her head to rally the lovers on their lowness of spirits.\n\n‘But if it’s caused by my being here,’ said the young lady, ‘don’t mind me a bit, for I’m quite as bad. You may go on just as you would if you were alone.’\n\n‘’Tilda,’ said Miss Squeers, colouring up to the top row of curls, ‘I am ashamed of you;’ and here the two friends burst into a variety of giggles, and glanced from time to time, over the tops of their pocket-handkerchiefs, at Nicholas, who from a state of unmixed astonishment, gradually fell into one of irrepressible laughter--occasioned, partly by the bare notion of his being in love with Miss Squeers, and partly by the preposterous appearance and behaviour of the two girls. These two causes of merriment, taken together, struck him as being so keenly ridiculous, that, despite his miserable condition, he laughed till he was thoroughly exhausted.\n\n‘Well,’ thought Nicholas, ‘as I am here, and seem expected, for some reason or other, to be amiable, it’s of no use looking like a goose. I may as well accommodate myself to the company.’\n\nWe blush to tell it; but his youthful spirits and vivacity getting, for the time, the better of his sad thoughts, he no sooner formed this resolution than he saluted Miss Squeers and the friend with great gallantry, and drawing a chair to the tea-table, began to make himself more at home than in all probability an usher has ever done in his employer’s house since ushers were first invented.\n\nThe ladies were in the full delight of this altered behaviour on the part of Mr. Nickleby, when the expected swain arrived, with his hair very damp from recent washing, and a clean shirt, whereof the collar might have belonged to some giant ancestor, forming, together with a white waistcoat of similar dimensions, the chief ornament of his person.\n\n‘Well, John,’ said Miss Matilda Price (which, by-the-bye, was the name of the miller’s daughter).\n\n‘Weel,’ said John with a grin that even the collar could not conceal.\n\n‘I beg your pardon,’ interposed Miss Squeers, hastening to do the honours. ‘Mr. Nickleby--Mr. John Browdie.’\n\n‘Servant, sir,’ said John, who was something over six feet high, with a face and body rather above the due proportion than below it.\n\n‘Yours to command, sir,’ replied Nicholas, making fearful ravages on the bread and butter.\n\nMr. Browdie was not a gentleman of great conversational powers, so he grinned twice more, and having now bestowed his customary mark of recognition on every person in company, grinned at nothing in particular, and helped himself to food.\n\n‘Old wooman awa’, bean’t she?’ said Mr. Browdie, with his mouth full.\n\nMiss Squeers nodded assent.\n\nMr. Browdie gave a grin of special width, as if he thought that really was something to laugh at, and went to work at the bread and butter with increased vigour. It was quite a sight to behold how he and Nicholas emptied the plate between them.\n\n‘Ye wean’t get bread and butther ev’ry neight, I expect, mun,’ said Mr Browdie, after he had sat staring at Nicholas a long time over the empty plate.\n\nNicholas bit his lip, and coloured, but affected not to hear the remark.\n\n‘Ecod,’ said Mr. Browdie, laughing boisterously, ‘they dean’t put too much intiv’em. Ye’ll be nowt but skeen and boans if you stop here long eneaf. Ho! ho! ho!’\n\n‘You are facetious, sir,’ said Nicholas, scornfully.\n\n‘Na; I dean’t know,’ replied Mr. Browdie, ‘but t’oother teacher, ‘cod he wur a learn ‘un, he wur.’ The recollection of the last teacher’s leanness seemed to afford Mr. Browdie the most exquisite delight, for he laughed until he found it necessary to apply his coat-cuffs to his eyes.\n\n‘I don’t know whether your perceptions are quite keen enough, Mr Browdie, to enable you to understand that your remarks are offensive,’ said Nicholas in a towering passion, ‘but if they are, have the goodness to--’\n\n‘If you say another word, John,’ shrieked Miss Price, stopping her admirer’s mouth as he was about to interrupt, ‘only half a word, I’ll never forgive you, or speak to you again.’\n\n‘Weel, my lass, I dean’t care aboot ‘un,’ said the corn-factor, bestowing a hearty kiss on Miss Matilda; ‘let ‘un gang on, let ‘un gang on.’\n\nIt now became Miss Squeers’s turn to intercede with Nicholas, which she did with many symptoms of alarm and horror; the effect of the double intercession was, that he and John Browdie shook hands across the table with much gravity; and such was the imposing nature of the ceremonial, that Miss Squeers was overcome and shed tears.\n\n‘What’s the matter, Fanny?’ said Miss Price.\n\n‘Nothing, ‘Tilda,’ replied Miss Squeers, sobbing.\n\n‘There never was any danger,’ said Miss Price, ‘was there, Mr. Nickleby?’\n\n‘None at all,’ replied Nicholas. ‘Absurd.’\n\n‘That’s right,’ whispered Miss Price, ‘say something kind to her, and she’ll soon come round. Here! Shall John and I go into the little kitchen, and come back presently?’\n\n‘Not on any account,’ rejoined Nicholas, quite alarmed at the proposition. ‘What on earth should you do that for?’\n\n‘Well,’ said Miss Price, beckoning him aside, and speaking with some degree of contempt--‘you ARE a one to keep company.’\n\n‘What do you mean?’ said Nicholas; ‘I am not a one to keep company at all--here at all events. I can’t make this out.’\n\n‘No, nor I neither,’ rejoined Miss Price; ‘but men are always fickle, and always were, and always will be; that I can make out, very easily.’\n\n‘Fickle!’ cried Nicholas; ‘what do you suppose? You don’t mean to say that you think--’\n\n‘Oh no, I think nothing at all,’ retorted Miss Price, pettishly. ‘Look at her, dressed so beautiful and looking so well--really ALMOST handsome. I am ashamed at you.’\n\n‘My dear girl, what have I got to do with her dressing beautifully or looking well?’ inquired Nicholas.\n\n‘Come, don’t call me a dear girl,’ said Miss Price--smiling a little though, for she was pretty, and a coquette too in her small way, and Nicholas was good-looking, and she supposed him the property of somebody else, which were all reasons why she should be gratified to think she had made an impression on him,--‘or Fanny will be saying it’s my fault. Come; we’re going to have a game at cards.’ Pronouncing these last words aloud, she tripped away and rejoined the big Yorkshireman.\n\nThis was wholly unintelligible to Nicholas, who had no other distinct impression on his mind at the moment, than that Miss Squeers was an ordinary-looking girl, and her friend Miss Price a pretty one; but he had not time to enlighten himself by reflection, for the hearth being by this time swept up, and the candle snuffed, they sat down to play speculation.\n\n‘There are only four of us, ‘Tilda,’ said Miss Squeers, looking slyly at Nicholas; ‘so we had better go partners, two against two.’\n\n‘What do you say, Mr. Nickleby?’ inquired Miss Price.\n\n‘With all the pleasure in life,’ replied Nicholas. And so saying, quite unconscious of his heinous offence, he amalgamated into one common heap those portions of a Dotheboys Hall card of terms, which represented his own counters, and those allotted to Miss Price, respectively.\n\n‘Mr. Browdie,’ said Miss Squeers hysterically, ‘shall we make a bank against them?’\n\nThe Yorkshireman assented--apparently quite overwhelmed by the new usher’s impudence--and Miss Squeers darted a spiteful look at her friend, and giggled convulsively.\n\nThe deal fell to Nicholas, and the hand prospered.\n\n‘We intend to win everything,’ said he.\n\n‘’Tilda HAS won something she didn’t expect, I think, haven’t you, dear?’ said Miss Squeers, maliciously.\n\n‘Only a dozen and eight, love,’ replied Miss Price, affecting to take the question in a literal sense.\n\n‘How dull you are tonight!’ sneered Miss Squeers.\n\n‘No, indeed,’ replied Miss Price, ‘I am in excellent spirits. I was thinking YOU seemed out of sorts.’\n\n‘Me!’ cried Miss Squeers, biting her lips, and trembling with very jealousy. ‘Oh no!’\n\n‘That’s well,’ remarked Miss Price. ‘Your hair’s coming out of curl, dear.’\n\n‘Never mind me,’ tittered Miss Squeers; ‘you had better attend to your partner.’\n\n‘Thank you for reminding her,’ said Nicholas. ‘So she had.’\n\nThe Yorkshireman flattened his nose, once or twice, with his clenched fist, as if to keep his hand in, till he had an opportunity of exercising it upon the features of some other gentleman; and Miss Squeers tossed her head with such indignation, that the gust of wind raised by the multitudinous curls in motion, nearly blew the candle out.\n\n‘I never had such luck, really,’ exclaimed coquettish Miss Price, after another hand or two. ‘It’s all along of you, Mr. Nickleby, I think. I should like to have you for a partner always.’\n\n‘I wish you had.’\n\n‘You’ll have a bad wife, though, if you always win at cards,’ said Miss Price.\n\n‘Not if your wish is gratified,’ replied Nicholas. ‘I am sure I shall have a good one in that case.’\n\nTo see how Miss Squeers tossed her head, and the corn-factor flattened his nose, while this conversation was carrying on! It would have been worth a small annuity to have beheld that; let alone Miss Price’s evident joy at making them jealous, and Nicholas Nickleby’s happy unconsciousness of making anybody uncomfortable.\n\n‘We have all the talking to ourselves, it seems,’ said Nicholas, looking good-humouredly round the table as he took up the cards for a fresh deal.\n\n‘You do it so well,’ tittered Miss Squeers, ‘that it would be a pity to interrupt, wouldn’t it, Mr. Browdie? He! he! he!’\n\n‘Nay,’ said Nicholas, ‘we do it in default of having anybody else to talk to.’\n\n‘We’ll talk to you, you know, if you’ll say anything,’ said Miss Price.\n\n‘Thank you, ‘Tilda, dear,’ retorted Miss Squeers, majestically.\n\n‘Or you can talk to each other, if you don’t choose to talk to us,’ said Miss Price, rallying her dear friend. ‘John, why don’t you say something?’\n\n‘Say summat?’ repeated the Yorkshireman.\n\n‘Ay, and not sit there so silent and glum.’\n\n‘Weel, then!’ said the Yorkshireman, striking the table heavily with his fist, ‘what I say’s this--Dang my boans and boddy, if I stan’ this ony longer. Do ye gang whoam wi’ me, and do yon loight an’ toight young whipster look sharp out for a brokken head, next time he cums under my hond.’\n\n‘Mercy on us, what’s all this?’ cried Miss Price, in affected astonishment.\n\n‘Cum whoam, tell ‘e, cum whoam,’ replied the Yorkshireman, sternly. And as he delivered the reply, Miss Squeers burst into a shower of tears; arising in part from desperate vexation, and in part from an impotent desire to lacerate somebody’s countenance with her fair finger-nails.\n\nThis state of things had been brought about by divers means and workings. Miss Squeers had brought it about, by aspiring to the high state and condition of being matrimonially engaged, without good grounds for so doing; Miss Price had brought it about, by indulging in three motives of action: first, a desire to punish her friend for laying claim to a rivalship in dignity, having no good title: secondly, the gratification of her own vanity, in receiving the compliments of a smart young man: and thirdly, a wish to convince the corn-factor of the great danger he ran, in deferring the celebration of their expected nuptials; while Nicholas had brought it about, by half an hour’s gaiety and thoughtlessness, and a very sincere desire to avoid the imputation of inclining at all to Miss Squeers. So the means employed, and the end produced, were alike the most natural in the world; for young ladies will look forward to being married, and will jostle each other in the race to the altar, and will avail themselves of all opportunities of displaying their own attractions to the best advantage, down to the very end of time, as they have done from its beginning.\n\n‘Why, and here’s Fanny in tears now!’ exclaimed Miss Price, as if in fresh amazement. ‘What can be the matter?’\n\n‘Oh! you don’t know, miss, of course you don’t know. Pray don’t trouble yourself to inquire,’ said Miss Squeers, producing that change of countenance which children call making a face.\n\n‘Well, I’m sure!’ exclaimed Miss Price.\n\n‘And who cares whether you are sure or not, ma’am?’ retorted Miss Squeers, making another face.\n\n‘You are monstrous polite, ma’am,’ said Miss Price.\n\n‘I shall not come to you to take lessons in the art, ma’am!’ retorted Miss Squeers.\n\n‘You needn’t take the trouble to make yourself plainer than you are, ma’am, however,’ rejoined Miss Price, ‘because that’s quite unnecessary.’\n\nMiss Squeers, in reply, turned very red, and thanked God that she hadn’t got the bold faces of some people. Miss Price, in rejoinder, congratulated herself upon not being possessed of the envious feeling of other people; whereupon Miss Squeers made some general remark touching the danger of associating with low persons; in which Miss Price entirely coincided: observing that it was very true indeed, and she had thought so a long time.\n\n‘’Tilda,’ exclaimed Miss Squeers with dignity, ‘I hate you.’\n\n‘Ah! There’s no love lost between us, I assure you,’ said Miss Price, tying her bonnet strings with a jerk. ‘You’ll cry your eyes out, when I’m gone; you know you will.’\n\n‘I scorn your words, Minx,’ said Miss Squeers.\n\n‘You pay me a great compliment when you say so,’ answered the miller’s daughter, curtseying very low. ‘Wish you a very good-night, ma’am, and pleasant dreams attend your sleep!’\n\nWith this parting benediction, Miss Price swept from the room, followed by the huge Yorkshireman, who exchanged with Nicholas, at parting, that peculiarly expressive scowl with which the cut-and-thrust counts, in melodramatic performances, inform each other they will meet again.\n\nThey were no sooner gone, than Miss Squeers fulfilled the prediction of her quondam friend by giving vent to a most copious burst of tears, and uttering various dismal lamentations and incoherent words. Nicholas stood looking on for a few seconds, rather doubtful what to do, but feeling uncertain whether the fit would end in his being embraced, or scratched, and considering that either infliction would be equally agreeable, he walked off very quietly while Miss Squeers was moaning in her pocket-handkerchief.\n\n‘This is one consequence,’ thought Nicholas, when he had groped his way to the dark sleeping-room, ‘of my cursed readiness to adapt myself to any society in which chance carries me. If I had sat mute and motionless, as I might have done, this would not have happened.’\n\nHe listened for a few minutes, but all was quiet.\n\n‘I was glad,’ he murmured, ‘to grasp at any relief from the sight of this dreadful place, or the presence of its vile master. I have set these people by the ears, and made two new enemies, where, Heaven knows, I needed none. Well, it is a just punishment for having forgotten, even for an hour, what is around me now!’\n\nSo saying, he felt his way among the throng of weary-hearted sleepers, and crept into his poor bed.\n\n# CHAPTER 10\n\nHow Mr. Ralph Nickleby provided for his Niece and Sister-in-Law\nOn the second morning after the departure of Nicholas for Yorkshire, Kate Nickleby sat in a very faded chair raised upon a very dusty throne in Miss La Creevy’s room, giving that lady a sitting for the portrait upon which she was engaged; and towards the full perfection of which, Miss La Creevy had had the street-door case brought upstairs, in order that she might be the better able to infuse into the counterfeit countenance of Miss Nickleby, a bright salmon flesh-tint which she had originally hit upon while executing the miniature of a young officer therein contained, and which bright salmon flesh-tint was considered, by Miss La Creevy’s chief friends and patrons, to be quite a novelty in art: as indeed it was.\n\n‘I think I have caught it now,’ said Miss La Creevy. ‘The very shade! This will be the sweetest portrait I have ever done, certainly.’\n\n‘It will be your genius that makes it so, then, I am sure,’ replied Kate, smiling.\n\n‘No, no, I won’t allow that, my dear,’ rejoined Miss La Creevy. ‘It’s a very nice subject--a very nice subject, indeed--though, of course, something depends upon the mode of treatment.’\n\n‘And not a little,’ observed Kate.\n\n‘Why, my dear, you are right there,’ said Miss La Creevy, ‘in the main you are right there; though I don’t allow that it is of such very great importance in the present case. Ah! The difficulties of Art, my dear, are great.’\n\n‘They must be, I have no doubt,’ said Kate, humouring her good-natured little friend.\n\n‘They are beyond anything you can form the faintest conception of,’ replied Miss La Creevy. ‘What with bringing out eyes with all one’s power, and keeping down noses with all one’s force, and adding to heads, and taking away teeth altogether, you have no idea of the trouble one little miniature is.’\n\n‘The remuneration can scarcely repay you,’ said Kate.\n\n‘Why, it does not, and that’s the truth,’ answered Miss La Creevy; ‘and then people are so dissatisfied and unreasonable, that, nine times out of ten, there’s no pleasure in painting them. Sometimes they say, “Oh, how very serious you have made me look, Miss La Creevy!” and at others, “La, Miss La Creevy, how very smirking!” when the very essence of a good portrait is, that it must be either serious or smirking, or it’s no portrait at all.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ said Kate, laughing.\n\n‘Certainly, my dear; because the sitters are always either the one or the other,’ replied Miss La Creevy. ‘Look at the Royal Academy! All those beautiful shiny portraits of gentlemen in black velvet waistcoats, with their fists doubled up on round tables, or marble slabs, are serious, you know; and all the ladies who are playing with little parasols, or little dogs, or little children--it’s the same rule in art, only varying the objects--are smirking. In fact,’ said Miss La Creevy, sinking her voice to a confidential whisper, ‘there are only two styles of portrait painting; the serious and the smirk; and we always use the serious for professional people (except actors sometimes), and the smirk for private ladies and gentlemen who don’t care so much about looking clever.’\n\nKate seemed highly amused by this information, and Miss La Creevy went on painting and talking, with immovable complacency.\n\n‘What a number of officers you seem to paint!’ said Kate, availing herself of a pause in the discourse, and glancing round the room.\n\n‘Number of what, child?’ inquired Miss La Creevy, looking up from her work. ‘Character portraits, oh yes--they’re not real military men, you know.’\n\n‘No!’\n‘Bless your heart, of course not; only clerks and that, who hire a uniform coat to be painted in, and send it here in a carpet bag. Some artists,’ said Miss La Creevy, ‘keep a red coat, and charge seven-and-sixpence extra for hire and carmine; but I don’t do that myself, for I don’t consider it legitimate.’\n\nDrawing herself up, as though she plumed herself greatly upon not resorting to these lures to catch sitters, Miss La Creevy applied herself, more intently, to her task: only raising her head occasionally, to look with unspeakable satisfaction at some touch she had just put in: and now and then giving Miss Nickleby to understand what particular feature she was at work upon, at the moment; ‘not,’ she expressly observed, ‘that you should make it up for painting, my dear, but because it’s our custom sometimes to tell sitters what part we are upon, in order that if there’s any particular expression they want introduced, they may throw it in, at the time, you know.’\n\n‘And when,’ said Miss La Creevy, after a long silence, to wit, an interval of full a minute and a half, ‘when do you expect to see your uncle again?’\n\n‘I scarcely know; I had expected to have seen him before now,’ replied Kate. ‘Soon I hope, for this state of uncertainty is worse than anything.’\n\n‘I suppose he has money, hasn’t he?’ inquired Miss La Creevy.\n\n‘He is very rich, I have heard,’ rejoined Kate. ‘I don’t know that he is, but I believe so.’\n\n‘Ah, you may depend upon it he is, or he wouldn’t be so surly,’ remarked Miss La Creevy, who was an odd little mixture of shrewdness and simplicity. ‘When a man’s a bear, he is generally pretty independent.’\n\n‘His manner is rough,’ said Kate.\n\n‘Rough!’ cried Miss La Creevy, ‘a porcupine’s a featherbed to him! I never met with such a cross-grained old savage.’\n\n‘It is only his manner, I believe,’ observed Kate, timidly; ‘he was disappointed in early life, I think I have heard, or has had his temper soured by some calamity. I should be sorry to think ill of him until I knew he deserved it.’\n\n‘Well; that’s very right and proper,’ observed the miniature painter, ‘and Heaven forbid that I should be the cause of your doing so! But, now, mightn’t he, without feeling it himself, make you and your mama some nice little allowance that would keep you both comfortable until you were well married, and be a little fortune to her afterwards? What would a hundred a year for instance, be to him?’\n\n‘I don’t know what it would be to him,’ said Kate, with energy, ‘but it would be that to me I would rather die than take.’\n\n‘Heyday!’ cried Miss La Creevy.\n\n‘A dependence upon him,’ said Kate, ‘would embitter my whole life. I should feel begging a far less degradation.’\n\n‘Well!’ exclaimed Miss La Creevy. ‘This of a relation whom you will not hear an indifferent person speak ill of, my dear, sounds oddly enough, I confess.’\n\n‘I dare say it does,’ replied Kate, speaking more gently, ‘indeed I am sure it must. I--I--only mean that with the feelings and recollection of better times upon me, I could not bear to live on anybody’s bounty--not his particularly, but anybody’s.’\n\nMiss La Creevy looked slyly at her companion, as if she doubted whether Ralph himself were not the subject of dislike, but seeing that her young friend was distressed, made no remark.\n\n‘I only ask of him,’ continued Kate, whose tears fell while she spoke, ‘that he will move so little out of his way, in my behalf, as to enable me by his recommendation--only by his recommendation--to earn, literally, my bread and remain with my mother. Whether we shall ever taste happiness again, depends upon the fortunes of my dear brother; but if he will do this, and Nicholas only tells us that he is well and cheerful, I shall be contented.’\n\nAs she ceased to speak, there was a rustling behind the screen which stood between her and the door, and some person knocked at the wainscot.’\n\n‘Come in, whoever it is!’ cried Miss La Creevy.\n\nThe person complied, and, coming forward at once, gave to view the form and features of no less an individual than Mr. Ralph Nickleby himself.\n\n‘Your servant, ladies,’ said Ralph, looking sharply at them by turns. ‘You were talking so loud, that I was unable to make you hear.’\n\nWhen the man of business had a more than commonly vicious snarl lurking at his heart, he had a trick of almost concealing his eyes under their thick and protruding brows, for an instant, and then displaying them in their full keenness. As he did so now, and tried to keep down the smile which parted his thin compressed lips, and puckered up the bad lines about his mouth, they both felt certain that some part, if not the whole, of their recent conversation, had been overheard.\n\n‘I called in, on my way upstairs, more than half expecting to find you here,’ said Ralph, addressing his niece, and looking contemptuously at the portrait. ‘Is that my niece’s portrait, ma’am?’\n\n‘Yes it is, Mr. Nickleby,’ said Miss La Creevy, with a very sprightly air, ‘and between you and me and the post, sir, it will be a very nice portrait too, though I say it who am the painter.’\n\n‘Don’t trouble yourself to show it to me, ma’am,’ cried Ralph, moving away, ‘I have no eye for likenesses. Is it nearly finished?’\n\n‘Why, yes,’ replied Miss La Creevy, considering with the pencil end of her brush in her mouth. ‘Two sittings more will--’\n\n‘Have them at once, ma’am,’ said Ralph. ‘She’ll have no time to idle over fooleries after tomorrow. Work, ma’am, work; we must all work. Have you let your lodgings, ma’am?’\n\n‘I have not put a bill up yet, sir.’\n\n‘Put it up at once, ma’am; they won’t want the rooms after this week, or if they do, can’t pay for them. Now, my dear, if you’re ready, we’ll lose no more time.’\n\nWith an assumption of kindness which sat worse upon him even than his usual manner, Mr. Ralph Nickleby motioned to the young lady to precede him, and bowing gravely to Miss La Creevy, closed the door and followed upstairs, where Mrs. Nickleby received him with many expressions of regard. Stopping them somewhat abruptly, Ralph waved his hand with an impatient gesture, and proceeded to the object of his visit.\n\n‘I have found a situation for your daughter, ma’am,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Well,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Now, I will say that that is only just what I have expected of you. “Depend upon it,” I said to Kate, only yesterday morning at breakfast, “that after your uncle has provided, in that most ready manner, for Nicholas, he will not leave us until he has done at least the same for you.” These were my very words, as near as I remember. Kate, my dear, why don’t you thank your--’\n\n‘Let me proceed, ma’am, pray,’ said Ralph, interrupting his sister-in-law in the full torrent of her discourse.\n\n‘Kate, my love, let your uncle proceed,’ said Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘I am most anxious that he should, mama,’ rejoined Kate.\n\n‘Well, my dear, if you are anxious that he should, you had better allow your uncle to say what he has to say, without interruption,’ observed Mrs. Nickleby, with many small nods and frowns. ‘Your uncle’s time is very valuable, my dear; and however desirous you may be--and naturally desirous, as I am sure any affectionate relations who have seen so little of your uncle as we have, must naturally be to protract the pleasure of having him among us, still, we are bound not to be selfish, but to take into consideration the important nature of his occupations in the city.’\n\n‘I am very much obliged to you, ma’am,’ said Ralph with a scarcely perceptible sneer. ‘An absence of business habits in this family leads, apparently, to a great waste of words before business--when it does come under consideration--is arrived at, at all.’\n\n‘I fear it is so indeed,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby with a sigh. ‘Your poor brother--’\n\n‘My poor brother, ma’am,’ interposed Ralph tartly, ‘had no idea what business was--was unacquainted, I verily believe, with the very meaning of the word.’\n\n‘I fear he was,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, with her handkerchief to her eyes. ‘If it hadn’t been for me, I don’t know what would have become of him.’\n\nWhat strange creatures we are! The slight bait so skilfully thrown out by Ralph, on their first interview, was dangling on the hook yet. At every small deprivation or discomfort which presented itself in the course of the four-and-twenty hours to remind her of her straitened and altered circumstances, peevish visions of her dower of one thousand pounds had arisen before Mrs. Nickleby’s mind, until, at last, she had come to persuade herself that of all her late husband’s creditors she was the worst used and the most to be pitied. And yet, she had loved him dearly for many years, and had no greater share of selfishness than is the usual lot of mortals. Such is the irritability of sudden poverty. A decent annuity would have restored her thoughts to their old train, at once.\n\n‘Repining is of no use, ma’am,’ said Ralph. ‘Of all fruitless errands, sending a tear to look after a day that is gone is the most fruitless.’\n\n‘So it is,’ sobbed Mrs. Nickleby. ‘So it is.’\n\n‘As you feel so keenly, in your own purse and person, the consequences of inattention to business, ma’am,’ said Ralph, ‘I am sure you will impress upon your children the necessity of attaching themselves to it early in life.’\n\n‘Of course I must see that,’ rejoined Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Sad experience, you know, brother-in-law.--Kate, my dear, put that down in the next letter to Nicholas, or remind me to do it if I write.’\n\nRalph paused for a few moments, and seeing that he had now made pretty sure of the mother, in case the daughter objected to his proposition, went on to say:\n\n‘The situation that I have made interest to procure, ma’am, is with--with a milliner and dressmaker, in short.’\n\n‘A milliner!’ cried Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘A milliner and dressmaker, ma’am,’ replied Ralph. ‘Dressmakers in London, as I need not remind you, ma’am, who are so well acquainted with all matters in the ordinary routine of life, make large fortunes, keep equipages, and become persons of great wealth and fortune.’\n\nNow, the first idea called up in Mrs. Nickleby’s mind by the words milliner and dressmaker were connected with certain wicker baskets lined with black oilskin, which she remembered to have seen carried to and fro in the streets; but, as Ralph proceeded, these disappeared, and were replaced by visions of large houses at the West end, neat private carriages, and a banker’s book; all of which images succeeded each other with such rapidity, that he had no sooner finished speaking, than she nodded her head and said ‘Very true,’ with great appearance of satisfaction.\n\n‘What your uncle says is very true, Kate, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nickleby. ‘I recollect when your poor papa and I came to town after we were married, that a young lady brought me home a chip cottage-bonnet, with white and green trimming, and green persian lining, in her own carriage, which drove up to the door full gallop;--at least, I am not quite certain whether it was her own carriage or a hackney chariot, but I remember very well that the horse dropped down dead as he was turning round, and that your poor papa said he hadn’t had any corn for a fortnight.’\n\nThis anecdote, so strikingly illustrative of the opulence of milliners, was not received with any great demonstration of feeling, inasmuch as Kate hung down her head while it was relating, and Ralph manifested very intelligible symptoms of extreme impatience.\n\n‘The lady’s name,’ said Ralph, hastily striking in, ‘is Mantalini--Madame Mantalini. I know her. She lives near Cavendish Square. If your daughter is disposed to try after the situation, I’ll take her there directly.’\n\n‘Have you nothing to say to your uncle, my love?’ inquired Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘A great deal,’ replied Kate; ‘but not now. I would rather speak to him when we are alone;--it will save his time if I thank him and say what I wish to say to him, as we walk along.’\n\nWith these words, Kate hurried away, to hide the traces of emotion that were stealing down her face, and to prepare herself for the walk, while Mrs. Nickleby amused her brother-in-law by giving him, with many tears, a detailed account of the dimensions of a rosewood cabinet piano they had possessed in their days of affluence, together with a minute description of eight drawing-room chairs, with turned legs and green chintz squabs to match the curtains, which had cost two pounds fifteen shillings apiece, and had gone at the sale for a mere nothing.\n\nThese reminiscences were at length cut short by Kate’s return in her walking dress, when Ralph, who had been fretting and fuming during the whole time of her absence, lost no time, and used very little ceremony, in descending into the street.\n\n‘Now,’ he said, taking her arm, ‘walk as fast as you can, and you’ll get into the step that you’ll have to walk to business with, every morning.’ So saying, he led Kate off, at a good round pace, towards Cavendish Square.\n\n‘I am very much obliged to you, uncle,’ said the young lady, after they had hurried on in silence for some time; ‘very.’\n\n‘I’m glad to hear it,’ said Ralph. ‘I hope you’ll do your duty.’\n\n‘I will try to please, uncle,’ replied Kate: ‘indeed I--’\n\n‘Don’t begin to cry,’ growled Ralph; ‘I hate crying.’\n\n‘It’s very foolish, I know, uncle,’ began poor Kate.\n\n‘It is,’ replied Ralph, stopping her short, ‘and very affected besides. Let me see no more of it.’\n\nPerhaps this was not the best way to dry the tears of a young and sensitive female, about to make her first entry on an entirely new scene of life, among cold and uninterested strangers; but it had its effect notwithstanding. Kate coloured deeply, breathed quickly for a few moments, and then walked on with a firmer and more determined step.\n\nIt was a curious contrast to see how the timid country girl shrunk through the crowd that hurried up and down the streets, giving way to the press of people, and clinging closely to Ralph as though she feared to lose him in the throng; and how the stern and hard-featured man of business went doggedly on, elbowing the passengers aside, and now and then exchanging a gruff salutation with some passing acquaintance, who turned to look back upon his pretty charge, with looks expressive of surprise, and seemed to wonder at the ill-assorted companionship. But, it would have been a stranger contrast still, to have read the hearts that were beating side by side; to have laid bare the gentle innocence of the one, and the rugged villainy of the other; to have hung upon the guileless thoughts of the affectionate girl, and been amazed that, among all the wily plots and calculations of the old man, there should not be one word or figure denoting thought of death or of the grave. But so it was; and stranger still--though this is a thing of every day--the warm young heart palpitated with a thousand anxieties and apprehensions, while that of the old worldly man lay rusting in its cell, beating only as a piece of cunning mechanism, and yielding no one throb of hope, or fear, or love, or care, for any living thing.\n\n‘Uncle,’ said Kate, when she judged they must be near their destination, ‘I must ask one question of you. I am to live at home?’\n\n‘At home!’ replied Ralph; ‘where’s that?’\n\n‘I mean with my mother--THE WIDOW,’ said Kate emphatically.\n\n‘You will live, to all intents and purposes, here,’ rejoined Ralph; ‘for here you will take your meals, and here you will be from morning till night--occasionally perhaps till morning again.’\n\n‘But at night, I mean,’ said Kate; ‘I cannot leave her, uncle. I must have some place that I can call a home; it will be wherever she is, you know, and may be a very humble one.’\n\n‘May be!’ said Ralph, walking faster, in the impatience provoked by the remark; ‘must be, you mean. May be a humble one! Is the girl mad?’\n\n‘The word slipped from my lips, I did not mean it indeed,’ urged Kate.\n\n‘I hope not,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘But my question, uncle; you have not answered it.’\n\n‘Why, I anticipated something of the kind,’ said Ralph; ‘and--though I object very strongly, mind--have provided against it. I spoke of you as an out-of-door worker; so you will go to this home that may be humble, every night.’\n\nThere was comfort in this. Kate poured forth many thanks for her uncle’s consideration, which Ralph received as if he had deserved them all, and they arrived without any further conversation at the dressmaker’s door, which displayed a very large plate, with Madame Mantalini’s name and occupation, and was approached by a handsome flight of steps. There was a shop to the house, but it was let off to an importer of otto of roses. Madame Mantalini’s shows-rooms were on the first-floor: a fact which was notified to the nobility and gentry by the casual exhibition, near the handsomely curtained windows, of two or three elegant bonnets of the newest fashion, and some costly garments in the most approved taste.\n\nA liveried footman opened the door, and in reply to Ralph’s inquiry whether Madame Mantalini was at home, ushered them, through a handsome hall and up a spacious staircase, into the show saloon, which comprised two spacious drawing-rooms, and exhibited an immense variety of superb dresses and materials for dresses: some arranged on stands, others laid carelessly on sofas, and others again, scattered over the carpet, hanging on the cheval-glasses, or mingling, in some other way, with the rich furniture of various descriptions, which was profusely displayed.\n\nThey waited here a much longer time than was agreeable to Mr. Ralph Nickleby, who eyed the gaudy frippery about him with very little concern, and was at length about to pull the bell, when a gentleman suddenly popped his head into the room, and, seeing somebody there, as suddenly popped it out again.\n\n‘Here. Hollo!’ cried Ralph. ‘Who’s that?’\n\nAt the sound of Ralph’s voice, the head reappeared, and the mouth, displaying a very long row of very white teeth, uttered in a mincing tone the words, ‘Demmit. What, Nickleby! oh, demmit!’ Having uttered which ejaculations, the gentleman advanced, and shook hands with Ralph, with great warmth. He was dressed in a gorgeous morning gown, with a waistcoat and Turkish trousers of the same pattern, a pink silk neckerchief, and bright green slippers, and had a very copious watch-chain wound round his body. Moreover, he had whiskers and a moustache, both dyed black and gracefully curled.\n\n‘Demmit, you don’t mean to say you want me, do you, demmit?’ said this gentleman, smiting Ralph on the shoulder.\n\n‘Not yet,’ said Ralph, sarcastically.\n\n‘Ha! ha! demmit,’ cried the gentleman; when, wheeling round to laugh with greater elegance, he encountered Kate Nickleby, who was standing near.\n\n‘My niece,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘I remember,’ said the gentleman, striking his nose with the knuckle of his forefinger as a chastening for his forgetfulness. ‘Demmit, I remember what you come for. Step this way, Nickleby; my dear, will you follow me? Ha! ha! They all follow me, Nickleby; always did, demmit, always.’\n\nGiving loose to the playfulness of his imagination, after this fashion, the gentleman led the way to a private sitting-room on the second floor, scarcely less elegantly furnished than the apartment below, where the presence of a silver coffee-pot, an egg-shell, and sloppy china for one, seemed to show that he had just breakfasted.\n\n‘Sit down, my dear,’ said the gentleman: first staring Miss Nickleby out of countenance, and then grinning in delight at the achievement. ‘This cursed high room takes one’s breath away. These infernal sky parlours--I’m afraid I must move, Nickleby.’\n\n‘I would, by all means,’ replied Ralph, looking bitterly round.\n\n‘What a demd rum fellow you are, Nickleby,’ said the gentleman, ‘the demdest, longest-headed, queerest-tempered old coiner of gold and silver ever was--demmit.’\n\nHaving complimented Ralph to this effect, the gentleman rang the bell, and stared at Miss Nickleby until it was answered, when he left off to bid the man desire his mistress to come directly; after which, he began again, and left off no more until Madame Mantalini appeared.\n\nThe dressmaker was a buxom person, handsomely dressed and rather good-looking, but much older than the gentleman in the Turkish trousers, whom she had wedded some six months before. His name was originally Muntle; but it had been converted, by an easy transition, into Mantalini: the lady rightly considering that an English appellation would be of serious injury to the business. He had married on his whiskers; upon which property he had previously subsisted, in a genteel manner, for some years; and which he had recently improved, after patient cultivation by the addition of a moustache, which promised to secure him an easy independence: his share in the labours of the business being at present confined to spending the money, and occasionally, when that ran short, driving to Mr. Ralph Nickleby to procure discount--at a percentage--for the customers’ bills.\n\n‘My life,’ said Mr. Mantalini, ‘what a demd devil of a time you have been!’\n\n‘I didn’t even know Mr. Nickleby was here, my love,’ said Madame Mantalini.\n\n‘Then what a doubly demd infernal rascal that footman must be, my soul,’ remonstrated Mr. Mantalini.\n\n‘My dear,’ said Madame, ‘that is entirely your fault.’\n\n‘My fault, my heart’s joy?’\n\n‘Certainly,’ returned the lady; ‘what can you expect, dearest, if you will not correct the man?’\n\n‘Correct the man, my soul’s delight!’\n\n‘Yes; I am sure he wants speaking to, badly enough,’ said Madame, pouting.\n\n‘Then do not vex itself,’ said Mr. Mantalini; ‘he shall be horse-whipped till he cries out demnebly.’ With this promise Mr. Mantalini kissed Madame Mantalini, and, after that performance, Madame Mantalini pulled Mr. Mantalini playfully by the ear: which done, they descended to business.\n\n‘Now, ma’am,’ said Ralph, who had looked on, at all this, with such scorn as few men can express in looks, ‘this is my niece.’\n\n‘Just so, Mr. Nickleby,’ replied Madame Mantalini, surveying Kate from head to foot, and back again. ‘Can you speak French, child?’\n\n‘Yes, ma’am,’ replied Kate, not daring to look up; for she felt that the eyes of the odious man in the dressing-gown were directed towards her.\n\n‘Like a demd native?’ asked the husband.\n\nMiss Nickleby offered no reply to this inquiry, but turned her back upon the questioner, as if addressing herself to make answer to what his wife might demand.\n\n‘We keep twenty young women constantly employed in the establishment,’ said Madame.\n\n‘Indeed, ma’am!’ replied Kate, timidly.\n\n‘Yes; and some of ‘em demd handsome, too,’ said the master.\n\n‘Mantalini!’ exclaimed his wife, in an awful voice.\n\n‘My senses’ idol!’ said Mantalini.\n\n‘Do you wish to break my heart?’\n\n‘Not for twenty thousand hemispheres populated with--with--with little ballet-dancers,’ replied Mantalini in a poetical strain.\n\n‘Then you will, if you persevere in that mode of speaking,’ said his wife. ‘What can Mr. Nickleby think when he hears you?’\n\n‘Oh! Nothing, ma’am, nothing,’ replied Ralph. ‘I know his amiable nature, and yours,--mere little remarks that give a zest to your daily intercourse--lovers’ quarrels that add sweetness to those domestic joys which promise to last so long--that’s all; that’s all.’\n\nIf an iron door could be supposed to quarrel with its hinges, and to make a firm resolution to open with slow obstinacy, and grind them to powder in the process, it would emit a pleasanter sound in so doing, than did these words in the rough and bitter voice in which they were uttered by Ralph. Even Mr. Mantalini felt their influence, and turning affrighted round, exclaimed: ‘What a demd horrid croaking!’\n\n‘You will pay no attention, if you please, to what Mr. Mantalini says,’ observed his wife, addressing Miss Nickleby.\n\n‘I do not, ma’am,’ said Kate, with quiet contempt.\n\n‘Mr. Mantalini knows nothing whatever about any of the young women,’ continued Madame, looking at her husband, and speaking to Kate. ‘If he has seen any of them, he must have seen them in the street, going to, or returning from, their work, and not here. He was never even in the room. I do not allow it. What hours of work have you been accustomed to?’\n\n‘I have never yet been accustomed to work at all, ma’am,’ replied Kate, in a low voice.\n\n‘For which reason she’ll work all the better now,’ said Ralph, putting in a word, lest this confession should injure the negotiation.\n\n‘I hope so,’ returned Madame Mantalini; ‘our hours are from nine to nine, with extra work when we’re very full of business, for which I allow payment as overtime.’\n\nKate bowed her head, to intimate that she heard, and was satisfied.\n\n‘Your meals,’ continued Madame Mantalini, ‘that is, dinner and tea, you will take here. I should think your wages would average from five to seven shillings a week; but I can’t give you any certain information on that point, until I see what you can do.’\n\nKate bowed her head again.\n\n‘If you’re ready to come,’ said Madame Mantalini, ‘you had better begin on Monday morning at nine exactly, and Miss Knag the forewoman shall then have directions to try you with some easy work at first. Is there anything more, Mr. Nickleby?’\n\n‘Nothing more, ma’am,’ replied Ralph, rising.\n\n‘Then I believe that’s all,’ said the lady. Having arrived at this natural conclusion, she looked at the door, as if she wished to be gone, but hesitated notwithstanding, as though unwilling to leave to Mr Mantalini the sole honour of showing them downstairs. Ralph relieved her from her perplexity by taking his departure without delay: Madame Mantalini making many gracious inquiries why he never came to see them; and Mr. Mantalini anathematising the stairs with great volubility as he followed them down, in the hope of inducing Kate to look round,--a hope, however, which was destined to remain ungratified.\n\n‘There!’ said Ralph when they got into the street; ‘now you’re provided for.’\n\nKate was about to thank him again, but he stopped her.\n\n‘I had some idea,’ he said, ‘of providing for your mother in a pleasant part of the country--(he had a presentation to some almshouses on the borders of Cornwall, which had occurred to him more than once)--but as you want to be together, I must do something else for her. She has a little money?’\n\n‘A very little,’ replied Kate.\n\n‘A little will go a long way if it’s used sparingly,’ said Ralph. ‘She must see how long she can make it last, living rent free. You leave your lodgings on Saturday?’\n\n‘You told us to do so, uncle.’\n\n‘Yes; there is a house empty that belongs to me, which I can put you into till it is let, and then, if nothing else turns up, perhaps I shall have another. You must live there.’\n\n‘Is it far from here, sir?’ inquired Kate.\n\n‘Pretty well,’ said Ralph; ‘in another quarter of the town--at the East end; but I’ll send my clerk down to you, at five o’clock on Saturday, to take you there. Goodbye. You know your way? Straight on.’\n\nColdly shaking his niece’s hand, Ralph left her at the top of Regent Street, and turned down a by-thoroughfare, intent on schemes of money-getting. Kate walked sadly back to their lodgings in the Strand.\n\n# CHAPTER 11\n\nNewman Noggs inducts Mrs. and Miss Nickleby into their New Dwelling in the City\nMiss Nickleby’s reflections, as she wended her way homewards, were of that desponding nature which the occurrences of the morning had been sufficiently calculated to awaken. Her uncle’s was not a manner likely to dispel any doubts or apprehensions she might have formed, in the outset, neither was the glimpse she had had of Madame Mantalini’s establishment by any means encouraging. It was with many gloomy forebodings and misgivings, therefore, that she looked forward, with a heavy heart, to the opening of her new career.\n\nIf her mother’s consolations could have restored her to a pleasanter and more enviable state of mind, there were abundance of them to produce the effect. By the time Kate reached home, the good lady had called to mind two authentic cases of milliners who had been possessed of considerable property, though whether they had acquired it all in business, or had had a capital to start with, or had been lucky and married to advantage, she could not exactly remember. However, as she very logically remarked, there must have been SOME young person in that way of business who had made a fortune without having anything to begin with, and that being taken for granted, why should not Kate do the same? Miss La Creevy, who was a member of the little council, ventured to insinuate some doubts relative to the probability of Miss Nickleby’s arriving at this happy consummation in the compass of an ordinary lifetime; but the good lady set that question entirely at rest, by informing them that she had a presentiment on the subject--a species of second-sight with which she had been in the habit of clenching every argument with the deceased Mr. Nickleby, and, in nine cases and three-quarters out of every ten, determining it the wrong way.\n\n‘I am afraid it is an unhealthy occupation,’ said Miss La Creevy. ‘I recollect getting three young milliners to sit to me, when I first began to paint, and I remember that they were all very pale and sickly.’\n\n‘Oh! that’s not a general rule by any means,’ observed Mrs. Nickleby; ‘for I remember, as well as if it was only yesterday, employing one that I was particularly recommended to, to make me a scarlet cloak at the time when scarlet cloaks were fashionable, and she had a very red face--a very red face, indeed.’\n\n‘Perhaps she drank,’ suggested Miss La Creevy.\n\n‘I don’t know how that may have been,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby: ‘but I know she had a very red face, so your argument goes for nothing.’\n\nIn this manner, and with like powerful reasoning, did the worthy matron meet every little objection that presented itself to the new scheme of the morning. Happy Mrs. Nickleby! A project had but to be new, and it came home to her mind, brightly varnished and gilded as a glittering toy.\n\nThis question disposed of, Kate communicated her uncle’s desire about the empty house, to which Mrs. Nickleby assented with equal readiness, characteristically remarking, that, on the fine evenings, it would be a pleasant amusement for her to walk to the West end to fetch her daughter home; and no less characteristically forgetting, that there were such things as wet nights and bad weather to be encountered in almost every week of the year.\n\n‘I shall be sorry--truly sorry to leave you, my kind friend,’ said Kate, on whom the good feeling of the poor miniature painter had made a deep impression.\n\n‘You shall not shake me off, for all that,’ replied Miss La Creevy, with as much sprightliness as she could assume. ‘I shall see you very often, and come and hear how you get on; and if, in all London, or all the wide world besides, there is no other heart that takes an interest in your welfare, there will be one little lonely woman that prays for it night and day.’\n\nWith this, the poor soul, who had a heart big enough for Gog, the guardian genius of London, and enough to spare for Magog to boot, after making a great many extraordinary faces which would have secured her an ample fortune, could she have transferred them to ivory or canvas, sat down in a corner, and had what she termed ‘a real good cry.’\n\nBut no crying, or talking, or hoping, or fearing, could keep off the dreaded Saturday afternoon, or Newman Noggs either; who, punctual to his time, limped up to the door, and breathed a whiff of cordial gin through the keyhole, exactly as such of the church clocks in the neighbourhood as agreed among themselves about the time, struck five. Newman waited for the last stroke, and then knocked.\n\n‘From Mr. Ralph Nickleby,’ said Newman, announcing his errand, when he got upstairs, with all possible brevity.\n\n‘We shall be ready directly,’ said Kate. ‘We have not much to carry, but I fear we must have a coach.’\n\n‘I’ll get one,’ replied Newman.\n\n‘Indeed you shall not trouble yourself,’ said Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘I will,’ said Newman.\n\n‘I can’t suffer you to think of such a thing,’ said Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘You can’t help it,’ said Newman.\n\n‘Not help it!’\n\n‘No; I thought of it as I came along; but didn’t get one, thinking you mightn’t be ready. I think of a great many things. Nobody can prevent that.’\n\n‘Oh yes, I understand you, Mr. Noggs,’ said Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Our thoughts are free, of course. Everybody’s thoughts are their own, clearly.’\n\n‘They wouldn’t be, if some people had their way,’ muttered Newman.\n\n‘Well, no more they would, Mr. Noggs, and that’s very true,’ rejoined Mrs Nickleby. ‘Some people to be sure are such--how’s your master?’\n\nNewman darted a meaning glance at Kate, and replied with a strong emphasis on the last word of his answer, that Mr. Ralph Nickleby was well, and sent his LOVE.\n\n‘I am sure we are very much obliged to him,’ observed Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘Very,’ said Newman. ‘I’ll tell him so.’\n\nIt was no very easy matter to mistake Newman Noggs, after having once seen him, and as Kate, attracted by the singularity of his manner (in which on this occasion, however, there was something respectful and even delicate, notwithstanding the abruptness of his speech), looked at him more closely, she recollected having caught a passing glimpse of that strange figure before.\n\n‘Excuse my curiosity,’ she said, ‘but did I not see you in the coachyard, on the morning my brother went away to Yorkshire?’\n\nNewman cast a wistful glance on Mrs. Nickleby and said ‘No,’ most unblushingly.\n\n‘No!’ exclaimed Kate, ‘I should have said so anywhere.’\n\n‘You’d have said wrong,’ rejoined Newman. ‘It’s the first time I’ve been out for three weeks. I’ve had the gout.’\n\nNewman was very, very far from having the appearance of a gouty subject, and so Kate could not help thinking; but the conference was cut short by Mrs. Nickleby’s insisting on having the door shut, lest Mr. Noggs should take cold, and further persisting in sending the servant girl for a coach, for fear he should bring on another attack of his disorder. To both conditions, Newman was compelled to yield. Presently, the coach came; and, after many sorrowful farewells, and a great deal of running backwards and forwards across the pavement on the part of Miss La Creevy, in the course of which the yellow turban came into violent contact with sundry foot-passengers, it (that is to say the coach, not the turban) went away again, with the two ladies and their luggage inside; and Newman, despite all Mrs. Nickleby’s assurances that it would be his death--on the box beside the driver.\n\nThey went into the city, turning down by the river side; and, after a long and very slow drive, the streets being crowded at that hour with vehicles of every kind, stopped in front of a large old dingy house in Thames Street: the door and windows of which were so bespattered with mud, that it would have appeared to have been uninhabited for years.\n\nThe door of this deserted mansion Newman opened with a key which he took out of his hat--in which, by-the-bye, in consequence of the dilapidated state of his pockets, he deposited everything, and would most likely have carried his money if he had had any--and the coach being discharged, he led the way into the interior of the mansion.\n\nOld, and gloomy, and black, in truth it was, and sullen and dark were the rooms, once so bustling with life and enterprise. There was a wharf behind, opening on the Thames. An empty dog-kennel, some bones of animals, fragments of iron hoops, and staves of old casks, lay strewn about, but no life was stirring there. It was a picture of cold, silent decay.\n\n‘This house depresses and chills one,’ said Kate, ‘and seems as if some blight had fallen on it. If I were superstitious, I should be almost inclined to believe that some dreadful crime had been perpetrated within these old walls, and that the place had never prospered since. How frowning and how dark it looks!’\n\n‘Lord, my dear,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby, ‘don’t talk in that way, or you’ll frighten me to death.’\n\n‘It is only my foolish fancy, mama,’ said Kate, forcing a smile.\n\n‘Well, then, my love, I wish you would keep your foolish fancy to yourself, and not wake up MY foolish fancy to keep it company,’ retorted Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Why didn’t you think of all this before--you are so careless--we might have asked Miss La Creevy to keep us company or borrowed a dog, or a thousand things--but it always was the way, and was just the same with your poor dear father. Unless I thought of everything--’ This was Mrs. Nickleby’s usual commencement of a general lamentation, running through a dozen or so of complicated sentences addressed to nobody in particular, and into which she now launched until her breath was exhausted.\n\nNewman appeared not to hear these remarks, but preceded them to a couple of rooms on the first floor, which some kind of attempt had been made to render habitable. In one, were a few chairs, a table, an old hearth-rug, and some faded baize; and a fire was ready laid in the grate. In the other stood an old tent bedstead, and a few scanty articles of chamber furniture.\n\n‘Well, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, trying to be pleased, ‘now isn’t this thoughtful and considerate of your uncle? Why, we should not have had anything but the bed we bought yesterday, to lie down upon, if it hadn’t been for his thoughtfulness!’\n\n‘Very kind, indeed,’ replied Kate, looking round.\n\nNewman Noggs did not say that he had hunted up the old furniture they saw, from attic and cellar; or that he had taken in the halfpennyworth of milk for tea that stood upon a shelf, or filled the rusty kettle on the hob, or collected the woodchips from the wharf, or begged the coals. But the notion of Ralph Nickleby having directed it to be done, tickled his fancy so much, that he could not refrain from cracking all his ten fingers in succession: at which performance Mrs. Nickleby was rather startled at first, but supposing it to be in some remote manner connected with the gout, did not remark upon.\n\n‘We need detain you no longer, I think,’ said Kate.\n\n‘Is there nothing I can do?’ asked Newman.\n\n‘Nothing, thank you,’ rejoined Miss Nickleby.\n\n‘Perhaps, my dear, Mr. Noggs would like to drink our healths,’ said Mrs Nickleby, fumbling in her reticule for some small coin.\n\n‘I think, mama,’ said Kate hesitating, and remarking Newman’s averted face, ‘you would hurt his feelings if you offered it.’\n\nNewman Noggs, bowing to the young lady more like a gentleman than the miserable wretch he seemed, placed his hand upon his breast, and, pausing for a moment, with the air of a man who struggles to speak but is uncertain what to say, quitted the room.\n\nAs the jarring echoes of the heavy house-door, closing on its latch, reverberated dismally through the building, Kate felt half tempted to call him back, and beg him to remain a little while; but she was ashamed to own her fears, and Newman Noggs was on his road homewards.\n\n# CHAPTER 12\n\nWhereby the Reader will be enabled to trace the further course of Miss Fanny Squeer’s Love, and to ascertain whether it ran smooth or otherwise.\nIt was a fortunate circumstance for Miss Fanny Squeers, that when her worthy papa returned home on the night of the small tea-party, he was what the initiated term ‘too far gone’ to observe the numerous tokens of extreme vexation of spirit which were plainly visible in her countenance. Being, however, of a rather violent and quarrelsome mood in his cups, it is not impossible that he might have fallen out with her, either on this or some imaginary topic, if the young lady had not, with a foresight and prudence highly commendable, kept a boy up, on purpose, to bear the first brunt of the good gentleman’s anger; which, having vented itself in a variety of kicks and cuffs, subsided sufficiently to admit of his being persuaded to go to bed. Which he did with his boots on, and an umbrella under his arm.\n\nThe hungry servant attended Miss Squeers in her own room according to custom, to curl her hair, perform the other little offices of her toilet, and administer as much flattery as she could get up, for the purpose; for Miss Squeers was quite lazy enough (and sufficiently vain and frivolous withal) to have been a fine lady; and it was only the arbitrary distinctions of rank and station which prevented her from being one.\n\n‘How lovely your hair do curl tonight, miss!’ said the handmaiden. ‘I declare if it isn’t a pity and a shame to brush it out!’\n\n‘Hold your tongue!’ replied Miss Squeers wrathfully.\n\nSome considerable experience prevented the girl from being at all surprised at any outbreak of ill-temper on the part of Miss Squeers. Having a half-perception of what had occurred in the course of the evening, she changed her mode of making herself agreeable, and proceeded on the indirect tack.\n\n‘Well, I couldn’t help saying, miss, if you was to kill me for it,’ said the attendant, ‘that I never see nobody look so vulgar as Miss Price this night.’\n\nMiss Squeers sighed, and composed herself to listen.\n\n‘I know it’s very wrong in me to say so, miss,’ continued the girl, delighted to see the impression she was making, ‘Miss Price being a friend of your’n, and all; but she do dress herself out so, and go on in such a manner to get noticed, that--oh--well, if people only saw themselves!’\n\n‘What do you mean, Phib?’ asked Miss Squeers, looking in her own little glass, where, like most of us, she saw--not herself, but the reflection of some pleasant image in her own brain. ‘How you talk!’\n\n‘Talk, miss! It’s enough to make a Tom cat talk French grammar, only to see how she tosses her head,’ replied the handmaid.\n\n‘She DOES toss her head,’ observed Miss Squeers, with an air of abstraction.\n\n‘So vain, and so very--very plain,’ said the girl.\n\n‘Poor ‘Tilda!’ sighed Miss Squeers, compassionately.\n\n‘And always laying herself out so, to get to be admired,’ pursued the servant. ‘Oh, dear! It’s positive indelicate.’\n\n‘I can’t allow you to talk in that way, Phib,’ said Miss Squeers. ‘’Tilda’s friends are low people, and if she don’t know any better, it’s their fault, and not hers.’\n\n‘Well, but you know, miss,’ said Phoebe, for which name ‘Phib’ was used as a patronising abbreviation, ‘if she was only to take copy by a friend--oh! if she only knew how wrong she was, and would but set herself right by you, what a nice young woman she might be in time!’\n\n‘Phib,’ rejoined Miss Squeers, with a stately air, ‘it’s not proper for me to hear these comparisons drawn; they make ‘Tilda look a coarse improper sort of person, and it seems unfriendly in me to listen to them. I would rather you dropped the subject, Phib; at the same time, I must say, that if ‘Tilda Price would take pattern by somebody--not me particularly--’\n\n‘Oh yes; you, miss,’ interposed Phib.\n\n‘Well, me, Phib, if you will have it so,’ said Miss Squeers. ‘I must say, that if she would, she would be all the better for it.’\n\n‘So somebody else thinks, or I am much mistaken,’ said the girl mysteriously.\n\n‘What do you mean?’ demanded Miss Squeers.\n\n‘Never mind, miss,’ replied the girl; ‘I know what I know; that’s all.’\n\n‘Phib,’ said Miss Squeers dramatically, ‘I insist upon your explaining yourself. What is this dark mystery? Speak.’\n\n‘Why, if you will have it, miss, it’s this,’ said the servant girl. ‘Mr John Browdie thinks as you think; and if he wasn’t too far gone to do it creditable, he’d be very glad to be off with Miss Price, and on with Miss Squeers.’\n\n‘Gracious heavens!’ exclaimed Miss Squeers, clasping her hands with great dignity. ‘What is this?’\n\n‘Truth, ma’am, and nothing but truth,’ replied the artful Phib.\n\n‘What a situation!’ cried Miss Squeers; ‘on the brink of unconsciously destroying the peace and happiness of my own ‘Tilda. What is the reason that men fall in love with me, whether I like it or not, and desert their chosen intendeds for my sake?’\n\n‘Because they can’t help it, miss,’ replied the girl; ‘the reason’s plain.’ (If Miss Squeers were the reason, it was very plain.)\n\n‘Never let me hear of it again,’ retorted Miss Squeers. ‘Never! Do you hear? ‘Tilda Price has faults--many faults--but I wish her well, and above all I wish her married; for I think it highly desirable--most desirable from the very nature of her failings--that she should be married as soon as possible. No, Phib. Let her have Mr. Browdie. I may pity HIM, poor fellow; but I have a great regard for ‘Tilda, and only hope she may make a better wife than I think she will.’\n\nWith this effusion of feeling, Miss Squeers went to bed.\n\nSpite is a little word; but it represents as strange a jumble of feelings, and compound of discords, as any polysyllable in the language. Miss Squeers knew as well in her heart of hearts that what the miserable serving-girl had said was sheer, coarse, lying flattery, as did the girl herself; yet the mere opportunity of venting a little ill-nature against the offending Miss Price, and affecting to compassionate her weaknesses and foibles, though only in the presence of a solitary dependant, was almost as great a relief to her spleen as if the whole had been gospel truth. Nay, more. We have such extraordinary powers of persuasion when they are exerted over ourselves, that Miss Squeers felt quite high-minded and great after her noble renunciation of John Browdie’s hand, and looked down upon her rival with a kind of holy calmness and tranquillity, that had a mighty effect in soothing her ruffled feelings.\n\nThis happy state of mind had some influence in bringing about a reconciliation; for, when a knock came at the front-door next day, and the miller’s daughter was announced, Miss Squeers betook herself to the parlour in a Christian frame of spirit, perfectly beautiful to behold.\n\n‘Well, Fanny,’ said the miller’s daughter, ‘you see I have come to see you, although we HAD some words last night.’\n\n‘I pity your bad passions, ‘Tilda,’ replied Miss Squeers, ‘but I bear no malice. I am above it.’\n\n‘Don’t be cross, Fanny,’ said Miss Price. ‘I have come to tell you something that I know will please you.’\n\n‘What may that be, ‘Tilda?’ demanded Miss Squeers; screwing up her lips, and looking as if nothing in earth, air, fire, or water, could afford her the slightest gleam of satisfaction.\n\n‘This,’ rejoined Miss Price. ‘After we left here last night John and I had a dreadful quarrel.’\n\n‘That doesn’t please me,’ said Miss Squeers--relaxing into a smile though.\n\n‘Lor! I wouldn’t think so bad of you as to suppose it did,’ rejoined her companion. ‘That’s not it.’\n\n‘Oh!’ said Miss Squeers, relapsing into melancholy. ‘Go on.’\n\n‘After a great deal of wrangling, and saying we would never see each other any more,’ continued Miss Price, ‘we made it up, and this morning John went and wrote our names down to be put up, for the first time, next Sunday, so we shall be married in three weeks, and I give you notice to get your frock made.’\n\nThere was mingled gall and honey in this intelligence. The prospect of the friend’s being married so soon was the gall, and the certainty of her not entertaining serious designs upon Nicholas was the honey. Upon the whole, the sweet greatly preponderated over the bitter, so Miss Squeers said she would get the frock made, and that she hoped ‘Tilda might be happy, though at the same time she didn’t know, and would not have her build too much upon it, for men were strange creatures, and a great many married women were very miserable, and wished themselves single again with all their hearts; to which condolences Miss Squeers added others equally calculated to raise her friend’s spirits and promote her cheerfulness of mind.\n\n‘But come now, Fanny,’ said Miss Price, ‘I want to have a word or two with you about young Mr. Nickleby.’\n\n‘He is nothing to me,’ interrupted Miss Squeers, with hysterical symptoms. ‘I despise him too much!’\n\n‘Oh, you don’t mean that, I am sure?’ replied her friend. ‘Confess, Fanny; don’t you like him now?’\n\nWithout returning any direct reply, Miss Squeers, all at once, fell into a paroxysm of spiteful tears, and exclaimed that she was a wretched, neglected, miserable castaway.\n\n‘I hate everybody,’ said Miss Squeers, ‘and I wish that everybody was dead--that I do.’\n\n‘Dear, dear,’ said Miss Price, quite moved by this avowal of misanthropical sentiments. ‘You are not serious, I am sure.’\n\n‘Yes, I am,’ rejoined Miss Squeers, tying tight knots in her pocket-handkerchief and clenching her teeth. ‘And I wish I was dead too. There!’\n\n‘Oh! you’ll think very differently in another five minutes,’ said Matilda. ‘How much better to take him into favour again, than to hurt yourself by going on in that way. Wouldn’t it be much nicer, now, to have him all to yourself on good terms, in a company-keeping, love-making, pleasant sort of manner?’\n\n‘I don’t know but what it would,’ sobbed Miss Squeers. ‘Oh! ‘Tilda, how could you have acted so mean and dishonourable! I wouldn’t have believed it of you, if anybody had told me.’\n\n‘Heyday!’ exclaimed Miss Price, giggling. ‘One would suppose I had been murdering somebody at least.’\n\n‘Very nigh as bad,’ said Miss Squeers passionately.\n\n‘And all this because I happen to have enough of good looks to make people civil to me,’ cried Miss Price. ‘Persons don’t make their own faces, and it’s no more my fault if mine is a good one than it is other people’s fault if theirs is a bad one.’\n\n‘Hold your tongue,’ shrieked Miss Squeers, in her shrillest tone; ‘or you’ll make me slap you, ‘Tilda, and afterwards I should be sorry for it!’\n\nIt is needless to say, that, by this time, the temper of each young lady was in some slight degree affected by the tone of her conversation, and that a dash of personality was infused into the altercation, in consequence. Indeed, the quarrel, from slight beginnings, rose to a considerable height, and was assuming a very violent complexion, when both parties, falling into a great passion of tears, exclaimed simultaneously, that they had never thought of being spoken to in that way: which exclamation, leading to a remonstrance, gradually brought on an explanation: and the upshot was, that they fell into each other’s arms and vowed eternal friendship; the occasion in question making the fifty-second time of repeating the same impressive ceremony within a twelvemonth.\n\nPerfect amicability being thus restored, a dialogue naturally ensued upon the number and nature of the garments which would be indispensable for Miss Price’s entrance into the holy state of matrimony, when Miss Squeers clearly showed that a great many more than the miller could, or would, afford, were absolutely necessary, and could not decently be dispensed with. The young lady then, by an easy digression, led the discourse to her own wardrobe, and after recounting its principal beauties at some length, took her friend upstairs to make inspection thereof. The treasures of two drawers and a closet having been displayed, and all the smaller articles tried on, it was time for Miss Price to return home; and as she had been in raptures with all the frocks, and had been stricken quite dumb with admiration of a new pink scarf, Miss Squeers said in high good humour, that she would walk part of the way with her, for the pleasure of her company; and off they went together: Miss Squeers dilating, as they walked along, upon her father’s accomplishments: and multiplying his income by ten, to give her friend some faint notion of the vast importance and superiority of her family.\n\nIt happened that that particular time, comprising the short daily interval which was suffered to elapse between what was pleasantly called the dinner of Mr. Squeers’s pupils, and their return to the pursuit of useful knowledge, was precisely the hour when Nicholas was accustomed to issue forth for a melancholy walk, and to brood, as he sauntered listlessly through the village, upon his miserable lot. Miss Squeers knew this perfectly well, but had perhaps forgotten it, for when she caught sight of that young gentleman advancing towards them, she evinced many symptoms of surprise and consternation, and assured her friend that she ‘felt fit to drop into the earth.’\n\n‘Shall we turn back, or run into a cottage?’ asked Miss Price. ‘He don’t see us yet.’\n\n‘No, ‘Tilda,’ replied Miss Squeers, ‘it is my duty to go through with it, and I will!’\n\nAs Miss Squeers said this, in the tone of one who has made a high moral resolution, and was, besides, taken with one or two chokes and catchings of breath, indicative of feelings at a high pressure, her friend made no further remark, and they bore straight down upon Nicholas, who, walking with his eyes bent upon the ground, was not aware of their approach until they were close upon him; otherwise, he might, perhaps, have taken shelter himself.\n\n‘Good-morning,’ said Nicholas, bowing and passing by.\n\n‘He is going,’ murmured Miss Squeers. ‘I shall choke, ‘Tilda.’\n\n‘Come back, Mr. Nickleby, do!’ cried Miss Price, affecting alarm at her friend’s threat, but really actuated by a malicious wish to hear what Nicholas would say; ‘come back, Mr. Nickleby!’\n\nMr. Nickleby came back, and looked as confused as might be, as he inquired whether the ladies had any commands for him.\n\n‘Don’t stop to talk,’ urged Miss Price, hastily; ‘but support her on the other side. How do you feel now, dear?’\n\n‘Better,’ sighed Miss Squeers, laying a beaver bonnet of a reddish brown with a green veil attached, on Mr. Nickleby’s shoulder. ‘This foolish faintness!’\n\n‘Don’t call it foolish, dear,’ said Miss Price: her bright eye dancing with merriment as she saw the perplexity of Nicholas; ‘you have no reason to be ashamed of it. It’s those who are too proud to come round again, without all this to-do, that ought to be ashamed.’\n\n‘You are resolved to fix it upon me, I see,’ said Nicholas, smiling, ‘although I told you, last night, it was not my fault.’\n\n‘There; he says it was not his fault, my dear,’ remarked the wicked Miss Price. ‘Perhaps you were too jealous, or too hasty with him? He says it was not his fault. You hear; I think that’s apology enough.’\n\n‘You will not understand me,’ said Nicholas. ‘Pray dispense with this jesting, for I have no time, and really no inclination, to be the subject or promoter of mirth just now.’\n\n‘What do you mean?’ asked Miss Price, affecting amazement.\n\n‘Don’t ask him, ‘Tilda,’ cried Miss Squeers; ‘I forgive him.’\n\n‘Dear me,’ said Nicholas, as the brown bonnet went down on his shoulder again, ‘this is more serious than I supposed. Allow me! Will you have the goodness to hear me speak?’\n\nHere he raised up the brown bonnet, and regarding with most unfeigned astonishment a look of tender reproach from Miss Squeers, shrunk back a few paces to be out of the reach of the fair burden, and went on to say:\n\n‘I am very sorry--truly and sincerely sorry--for having been the cause of any difference among you, last night. I reproach myself, most bitterly, for having been so unfortunate as to cause the dissension that occurred, although I did so, I assure you, most unwittingly and heedlessly.’\n\n‘Well; that’s not all you have got to say surely,’ exclaimed Miss Price as Nicholas paused.\n\n‘I fear there is something more,’ stammered Nicholas with a half-smile, and looking towards Miss Squeers, ‘it is a most awkward thing to say--but--the very mention of such a supposition makes one look like a puppy--still--may I ask if that lady supposes that I entertain any--in short, does she think that I am in love with her?’\n\n‘Delightful embarrassment,’ thought Miss Squeers, ‘I have brought him to it, at last. Answer for me, dear,’ she whispered to her friend.\n\n‘Does she think so?’ rejoined Miss Price; ‘of course she does.’\n\n‘She does!’ exclaimed Nicholas with such energy of utterance as might have been, for the moment, mistaken for rapture.\n\n‘Certainly,’ replied Miss Price\n\n‘If Mr. Nickleby has doubted that, ‘Tilda,’ said the blushing Miss Squeers in soft accents, ‘he may set his mind at rest. His sentiments are recipro--’\n\n‘Stop,’ cried Nicholas hurriedly; ‘pray hear me. This is the grossest and wildest delusion, the completest and most signal mistake, that ever human being laboured under, or committed. I have scarcely seen the young lady half-a-dozen times, but if I had seen her sixty times, or am destined to see her sixty thousand, it would be, and will be, precisely the same. I have not one thought, wish, or hope, connected with her, unless it be--and I say this, not to hurt her feelings, but to impress her with the real state of my own--unless it be the one object, dear to my heart as life itself, of being one day able to turn my back upon this accursed place, never to set foot in it again, or think of it--even think of it--but with loathing and disgust.’\n\nWith this particularly plain and straightforward declaration, which he made with all the vehemence that his indignant and excited feelings could bring to bear upon it, Nicholas waiting to hear no more, retreated.\n\nBut poor Miss Squeers! Her anger, rage, and vexation; the rapid succession of bitter and passionate feelings that whirled through her mind; are not to be described. Refused! refused by a teacher, picked up by advertisement, at an annual salary of five pounds payable at indefinite periods, and ‘found’ in food and lodging like the very boys themselves; and this too in the presence of a little chit of a miller’s daughter of eighteen, who was going to be married, in three weeks’ time, to a man who had gone down on his very knees to ask her. She could have choked in right good earnest, at the thought of being so humbled.\n\nBut, there was one thing clear in the midst of her mortification; and that was, that she hated and detested Nicholas with all the narrowness of mind and littleness of purpose worthy a descendant of the house of Squeers. And there was one comfort too; and that was, that every hour in every day she could wound his pride, and goad him with the infliction of some slight, or insult, or deprivation, which could not but have some effect on the most insensible person, and must be acutely felt by one so sensitive as Nicholas. With these two reflections uppermost in her mind, Miss Squeers made the best of the matter to her friend, by observing that Mr. Nickleby was such an odd creature, and of such a violent temper, that she feared she should be obliged to give him up; and parted from her.\n\nAnd here it may be remarked, that Miss Squeers, having bestowed her affections (or whatever it might be that, in the absence of anything better, represented them) on Nicholas Nickleby, had never once seriously contemplated the possibility of his being of a different opinion from herself in the business. Miss Squeers reasoned that she was prepossessing and beautiful, and that her father was master, and Nicholas man, and that her father had saved money, and Nicholas had none, all of which seemed to her conclusive arguments why the young man should feel only too much honoured by her preference. She had not failed to recollect, either, how much more agreeable she could render his situation if she were his friend, and how much more disagreeable if she were his enemy; and, doubtless, many less scrupulous young gentlemen than Nicholas would have encouraged her extravagance had it been only for this very obvious and intelligible reason. However, he had thought proper to do otherwise, and Miss Squeers was outrageous.\n\n‘Let him see,’ said the irritated young lady, when she had regained her own room, and eased her mind by committing an assault on Phib, ‘if I don’t set mother against him a little more when she comes back!’\n\nIt was scarcely necessary to do this, but Miss Squeers was as good as her word; and poor Nicholas, in addition to bad food, dirty lodging, and the being compelled to witness one dull unvarying round of squalid misery, was treated with every special indignity that malice could suggest, or the most grasping cupidity put upon him.\n\nNor was this all. There was another and deeper system of annoyance which made his heart sink, and nearly drove him wild, by its injustice and cruelty.\n\nThe wretched creature, Smike, since the night Nicholas had spoken kindly to him in the schoolroom, had followed him to and fro, with an ever-restless desire to serve or help him; anticipating such little wants as his humble ability could supply, and content only to be near him. He would sit beside him for hours, looking patiently into his face; and a word would brighten up his care-worn visage, and call into it a passing gleam, even of happiness. He was an altered being; he had an object now; and that object was, to show his attachment to the only person--that person a stranger--who had treated him, not to say with kindness, but like a human creature.\n\nUpon this poor being, all the spleen and ill-humour that could not be vented on Nicholas were unceasingly bestowed. Drudgery would have been nothing--Smike was well used to that. Buffetings inflicted without cause, would have been equally a matter of course; for to them also he had served a long and weary apprenticeship; but it was no sooner observed that he had become attached to Nicholas, than stripes and blows, stripes and blows, morning, noon, and night, were his only portion. Squeers was jealous of the influence which his man had so soon acquired, and his family hated him, and Smike paid for both. Nicholas saw it, and ground his teeth at every repetition of the savage and cowardly attack.\n\nHe had arranged a few regular lessons for the boys; and one night, as he paced up and down the dismal schoolroom, his swollen heart almost bursting to think that his protection and countenance should have increased the misery of the wretched being whose peculiar destitution had awakened his pity, he paused mechanically in a dark corner where sat the object of his thoughts.\n\nThe poor soul was poring hard over a tattered book, with the traces of recent tears still upon his face; vainly endeavouring to master some task which a child of nine years old, possessed of ordinary powers, could have conquered with ease, but which, to the addled brain of the crushed boy of nineteen, was a sealed and hopeless mystery. Yet there he sat, patiently conning the page again and again, stimulated by no boyish ambition, for he was the common jest and scoff even of the uncouth objects that congregated about him, but inspired by the one eager desire to please his solitary friend.\n\nNicholas laid his hand upon his shoulder.\n\n‘I can’t do it,’ said the dejected creature, looking up with bitter disappointment in every feature. ‘No, no.’\n\n‘Do not try,’ replied Nicholas.\n\nThe boy shook his head, and closing the book with a sigh, looked vacantly round, and laid his head upon his arm. He was weeping.\n\n‘Do not for God’s sake,’ said Nicholas, in an agitated voice; ‘I cannot bear to see you.’\n\n‘They are more hard with me than ever,’ sobbed the boy.\n\n‘I know it,’ rejoined Nicholas. ‘They are.’\n\n‘But for you,’ said the outcast, ‘I should die. They would kill me; they would; I know they would.’\n\n‘You will do better, poor fellow,’ replied Nicholas, shaking his head mournfully, ‘when I am gone.’\n\n‘Gone!’ cried the other, looking intently in his face.\n\n‘Softly!’ rejoined Nicholas. ‘Yes.’\n\n‘Are you going?’ demanded the boy, in an earnest whisper.\n\n‘I cannot say,’ replied Nicholas. ‘I was speaking more to my own thoughts, than to you.’\n\n‘Tell me,’ said the boy imploringly, ‘oh do tell me, WILL you go--WILL you?’\n\n‘I shall be driven to that at last!’ said Nicholas. ‘The world is before me, after all.’\n\n‘Tell me,’ urged Smike, ‘is the world as bad and dismal as this place?’\n\n‘Heaven forbid,’ replied Nicholas, pursuing the train of his own thoughts; ‘its hardest, coarsest toil, were happiness to this.’\n\n‘Should I ever meet you there?’ demanded the boy, speaking with unusual wildness and volubility.\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Nicholas, willing to soothe him.\n\n‘No, no!’ said the other, clasping him by the hand. ‘Should I--should I--tell me that again. Say I should be sure to find you.’\n\n‘You would,’ replied Nicholas, with the same humane intention, ‘and I would help and aid you, and not bring fresh sorrow on you as I have done here.’\n\nThe boy caught both the young man’s hands passionately in his, and, hugging them to his breast, uttered a few broken sounds which were unintelligible. Squeers entered at the moment, and he shrunk back into his old corner.\n\n# CHAPTER 13\n\nNicholas varies the Monotony of Dothebys Hall by a most vigorous and remarkable proceeding, which leads to Consequences of some Importance\n\nThe cold, feeble dawn of a January morning was stealing in at the windows of the common sleeping-room, when Nicholas, raising himself on his arm, looked among the prostrate forms which on every side surrounded him, as though in search of some particular object.\nIt needed a quick eye to detect, from among the huddled mass of sleepers, the form of any given individual. As they lay closely packed together, covered, for warmth’s sake, with their patched and ragged clothes, little could be distinguished but the sharp outlines of pale faces, over which the sombre light shed the same dull heavy colour; with, here and there, a gaunt arm thrust forth: its thinness hidden by no covering, but fully exposed to view, in all its shrunken ugliness. There were some who, lying on their backs with upturned faces and clenched hands, just visible in the leaden light, bore more the aspect of dead bodies than of living creatures; and there were others coiled up into strange and fantastic postures, such as might have been taken for the uneasy efforts of pain to gain some temporary relief, rather than the freaks of slumber. A few--and these were among the youngest of the children--slept peacefully on, with smiles upon their faces, dreaming perhaps of home; but ever and again a deep and heavy sigh, breaking the stillness of the room, announced that some new sleeper had awakened to the misery of another day; and, as morning took the place of night, the smiles gradually faded away, with the friendly darkness which had given them birth.\n\nDreams are the bright creatures of poem and legend, who sport on earth in the night season, and melt away in the first beam of the sun, which lights grim care and stern reality on their daily pilgrimage through the world.\n\nNicholas looked upon the sleepers; at first, with the air of one who gazes upon a scene which, though familiar to him, has lost none of its sorrowful effect in consequence; and, afterwards, with a more intense and searching scrutiny, as a man would who missed something his eye was accustomed to meet, and had expected to rest upon. He was still occupied in this search, and had half risen from his bed in the eagerness of his quest, when the voice of Squeers was heard, calling from the bottom of the stairs.\n\n‘Now then,’ cried that gentleman, ‘are you going to sleep all day, up there--’\n\n‘You lazy hounds?’ added Mrs. Squeers, finishing the sentence, and producing, at the same time, a sharp sound, like that which is occasioned by the lacing of stays.\n\n‘We shall be down directly, sir,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘Down directly!’ said Squeers. ‘Ah! you had better be down directly, or I’ll be down upon some of you in less. Where’s that Smike?’\n\nNicholas looked hurriedly round again, but made no answer.\n\n‘Smike!’ shouted Squeers.\n\n‘Do you want your head broke in a fresh place, Smike?’ demanded his amiable lady in the same key.\n\nStill there was no reply, and still Nicholas stared about him, as did the greater part of the boys, who were by this time roused.\n\n‘Confound his impudence!’ muttered Squeers, rapping the stair-rail impatiently with his cane. ‘Nickleby!’\n\n‘Well, sir.’\n\n‘Send that obstinate scoundrel down; don’t you hear me calling?’\n\n‘He is not here, sir,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘Don’t tell me a lie,’ retorted the schoolmaster. ‘He is.’\n\n‘He is not,’ retorted Nicholas angrily, ‘don’t tell me one.’\n\n‘We shall soon see that,’ said Mr. Squeers, rushing upstairs. ‘I’ll find him, I warrant you.’\n\nWith which assurance, Mr. Squeers bounced into the dormitory, and, swinging his cane in the air ready for a blow, darted into the corner where the lean body of the drudge was usually stretched at night. The cane descended harmlessly upon the ground. There was nobody there.\n\n‘What does this mean?’ said Squeers, turning round with a very pale face. ‘Where have you hid him?’\n\n‘I have seen nothing of him since last night,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘Come,’ said Squeers, evidently frightened, though he endeavoured to look otherwise, ‘you won’t save him this way. Where is he?’\n\n‘At the bottom of the nearest pond for aught I know,’ rejoined Nicholas in a low voice, and fixing his eyes full on the master’s face.\n\n‘Damn you, what do you mean by that?’ retorted Squeers in great perturbation. Without waiting for a reply, he inquired of the boys whether any one among them knew anything of their missing schoolmate.\n\nThere was a general hum of anxious denial, in the midst of which, one shrill voice was heard to say (as, indeed, everybody thought):\n\n‘Please, sir, I think Smike’s run away, sir.’\n\n‘Ha!’ cried Squeers, turning sharp round. ‘Who said that?’\n\n‘Tomkins, please sir,’ rejoined a chorus of voices. Mr. Squeers made a plunge into the crowd, and at one dive, caught a very little boy, habited still in his night-gear, and the perplexed expression of whose countenance, as he was brought forward, seemed to intimate that he was as yet uncertain whether he was about to be punished or rewarded for the suggestion. He was not long in doubt.\n\n‘You think he has run away, do you, sir?’ demanded Squeers.\n\n‘Yes, please sir,’ replied the little boy.\n\n‘And what, sir,’ said Squeers, catching the little boy suddenly by the arms and whisking up his drapery in a most dexterous manner, ‘what reason have you to suppose that any boy would want to run away from this establishment? Eh, sir?’\n\nThe child raised a dismal cry, by way of answer, and Mr. Squeers, throwing himself into the most favourable attitude for exercising his strength, beat him until the little urchin in his writhings actually rolled out of his hands, when he mercifully allowed him to roll away, as he best could.\n\n‘There,’ said Squeers. ‘Now if any other boy thinks Smike has run away, I shall be glad to have a talk with him.’\n\nThere was, of course, a profound silence, during which Nicholas showed his disgust as plainly as looks could show it.\n\n‘Well, Nickleby,’ said Squeers, eyeing him maliciously. ‘YOU think he has run away, I suppose?’\n\n‘I think it extremely likely,’ replied Nicholas, in a quiet manner.\n\n‘Oh, you do, do you?’ sneered Squeers. ‘Maybe you know he has?’\n\n‘I know nothing of the kind.’\n\n‘He didn’t tell you he was going, I suppose, did he?’ sneered Squeers.\n\n‘He did not,’ replied Nicholas; ‘I am very glad he did not, for it would then have been my duty to have warned you in time.’\n\n‘Which no doubt you would have been devilish sorry to do,’ said Squeers in a taunting fashion.\n\n‘I should indeed,’ replied Nicholas. ‘You interpret my feelings with great accuracy.’\n\nMrs. Squeers had listened to this conversation, from the bottom of the stairs; but, now losing all patience, she hastily assumed her night-jacket, and made her way to the scene of action.\n\n‘What’s all this here to-do?’ said the lady, as the boys fell off right and left, to save her the trouble of clearing a passage with her brawny arms. ‘What on earth are you a talking to him for, Squeery!’\n\n‘Why, my dear,’ said Squeers, ‘the fact is, that Smike is not to be found.’\n\n‘Well, I know that,’ said the lady, ‘and where’s the wonder? If you get a parcel of proud-stomached teachers that set the young dogs a rebelling, what else can you look for? Now, young man, you just have the kindness to take yourself off to the schoolroom, and take the boys off with you, and don’t you stir out of there till you have leave given you, or you and I may fall out in a way that’ll spoil your beauty, handsome as you think yourself, and so I tell you.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Yes; and indeed and indeed again, Mister Jackanapes,’ said the excited lady; ‘and I wouldn’t keep such as you in the house another hour, if I had my way.’\n\n‘Nor would you if I had mine,’ replied Nicholas. ‘Now, boys!’\n\n‘Ah! Now, boys,’ said Mrs. Squeers, mimicking, as nearly as she could, the voice and manner of the usher. ‘Follow your leader, boys, and take pattern by Smike if you dare. See what he’ll get for himself, when he is brought back; and, mind! I tell you that you shall have as bad, and twice as bad, if you so much as open your mouths about him.’\n\n‘If I catch him,’ said Squeers, ‘I’ll only stop short of flaying him alive. I give you notice, boys.’\n\n‘IF you catch him,’ retorted Mrs. Squeers, contemptuously; ‘you are sure to; you can’t help it, if you go the right way to work. Come! Away with you!’\n\nWith these words, Mrs. Squeers dismissed the boys, and after a little light skirmishing with those in the rear who were pressing forward to get out of the way, but were detained for a few moments by the throng in front, succeeded in clearing the room, when she confronted her spouse alone.\n\n‘He is off,’ said Mrs. Squeers. ‘The cow-house and stable are locked up, so he can’t be there; and he’s not downstairs anywhere, for the girl has looked. He must have gone York way, and by a public road too.’\n\n‘Why must he?’ inquired Squeers.\n\n‘Stupid!’ said Mrs. Squeers angrily. ‘He hadn’t any money, had he?’\n\n‘Never had a penny of his own in his whole life, that I know of,’ replied Squeers.\n\n‘To be sure,’ rejoined Mrs. Squeers, ‘and he didn’t take anything to eat with him; that I’ll answer for. Ha! ha! ha!’\n\n‘Ha! ha! ha!’ laughed Squeers.\n\n‘Then, of course,’ said Mrs. S., ‘he must beg his way, and he could do that, nowhere, but on the public road.’\n\n‘That’s true,’ exclaimed Squeers, clapping his hands.\n\n‘True! Yes; but you would never have thought of it, for all that, if I hadn’t said so,’ replied his wife. ‘Now, if you take the chaise and go one road, and I borrow Swallow’s chaise, and go the other, what with keeping our eyes open, and asking questions, one or other of us is pretty certain to lay hold of him.’\n\nThe worthy lady’s plan was adopted and put in execution without a moment’s delay. After a very hasty breakfast, and the prosecution of some inquiries in the village, the result of which seemed to show that he was on the right track, Squeers started forth in the pony-chaise, intent upon discovery and vengeance. Shortly afterwards, Mrs. Squeers, arrayed in the white top-coat, and tied up in various shawls and handkerchiefs, issued forth in another chaise and another direction, taking with her a good-sized bludgeon, several odd pieces of strong cord, and a stout labouring man: all provided and carried upon the expedition, with the sole object of assisting in the capture, and (once caught) insuring the safe custody of the unfortunate Smike.\n\nNicholas remained behind, in a tumult of feeling, sensible that whatever might be the upshot of the boy’s flight, nothing but painful and deplorable consequences were likely to ensue from it. Death, from want and exposure to the weather, was the best that could be expected from the protracted wandering of so poor and helpless a creature, alone and unfriended, through a country of which he was wholly ignorant. There was little, perhaps, to choose between this fate and a return to the tender mercies of the Yorkshire school; but the unhappy being had established a hold upon his sympathy and compassion, which made his heart ache at the prospect of the suffering he was destined to undergo. He lingered on, in restless anxiety, picturing a thousand possibilities, until the evening of next day, when Squeers returned, alone, and unsuccessful.\n\n‘No news of the scamp!’ said the schoolmaster, who had evidently been stretching his legs, on the old principle, not a few times during the journey. ‘I’ll have consolation for this out of somebody, Nickleby, if Mrs. Squeers don’t hunt him down; so I give you warning.’\n\n‘It is not in my power to console you, sir,’ said Nicholas. ‘It is nothing to me.’\n\n‘Isn’t it?’ said Squeers in a threatening manner. ‘We shall see!’\n\n‘We shall,’ rejoined Nicholas.\n\n‘Here’s the pony run right off his legs, and me obliged to come home with a hack cob, that’ll cost fifteen shillings besides other expenses,’ said Squeers; ‘who’s to pay for that, do you hear?’\n\nNicholas shrugged his shoulders and remained silent.\n\n‘I’ll have it out of somebody, I tell you,’ said Squeers, his usual harsh crafty manner changed to open bullying ‘None of your whining vapourings here, Mr. Puppy, but be off to your kennel, for it’s past your bedtime! Come! Get out!’\n\nNicholas bit his lip and knit his hands involuntarily, for his fingerends tingled to avenge the insult; but remembering that the man was drunk, and that it could come to little but a noisy brawl, he contented himself with darting a contemptuous look at the tyrant, and walked, as majestically as he could, upstairs: not a little nettled, however, to observe that Miss Squeers and Master Squeers, and the servant girl, were enjoying the scene from a snug corner; the two former indulging in many edifying remarks about the presumption of poor upstarts, which occasioned a vast deal of laughter, in which even the most miserable of all miserable servant girls joined: while Nicholas, stung to the quick, drew over his head such bedclothes as he had, and sternly resolved that the outstanding account between himself and Mr. Squeers should be settled rather more speedily than the latter anticipated.\n\nAnother day came, and Nicholas was scarcely awake when he heard the wheels of a chaise approaching the house. It stopped. The voice of Mrs Squeers was heard, and in exultation, ordering a glass of spirits for somebody, which was in itself a sufficient sign that something extraordinary had happened. Nicholas hardly dared to look out of the window; but he did so, and the very first object that met his eyes was the wretched Smike: so bedabbled with mud and rain, so haggard and worn, and wild, that, but for his garments being such as no scarecrow was ever seen to wear, he might have been doubtful, even then, of his identity.\n\n‘Lift him out,’ said Squeers, after he had literally feasted his eyes, in silence, upon the culprit. ‘Bring him in; bring him in!’\n\n‘Take care,’ cried Mrs. Squeers, as her husband proffered his assistance. ‘We tied his legs under the apron and made’em fast to the chaise, to prevent his giving us the slip again.’\n\nWith hands trembling with delight, Squeers unloosened the cord; and Smike, to all appearance more dead than alive, was brought into the house and securely locked up in a cellar, until such time as Mr. Squeers should deem it expedient to operate upon him, in presence of the assembled school.\n\nUpon a hasty consideration of the circumstances, it may be matter of surprise to some persons, that Mr. and Mrs. Squeers should have taken so much trouble to repossess themselves of an incumbrance of which it was their wont to complain so loudly; but their surprise will cease when they are informed that the manifold services of the drudge, if performed by anybody else, would have cost the establishment some ten or twelve shillings per week in the shape of wages; and furthermore, that all runaways were, as a matter of policy, made severe examples of, at Dotheboys Hall, inasmuch as, in consequence of the limited extent of its attractions, there was but little inducement, beyond the powerful impulse of fear, for any pupil, provided with the usual number of legs and the power of using them, to remain.\n\nThe news that Smike had been caught and brought back in triumph, ran like wild-fire through the hungry community, and expectation was on tiptoe all the morning. On tiptoe it was destined to remain, however, until afternoon; when Squeers, having refreshed himself with his dinner, and further strengthened himself by an extra libation or so, made his appearance (accompanied by his amiable partner) with a countenance of portentous import, and a fearful instrument of flagellation, strong, supple, wax-ended, and new,--in short, purchased that morning, expressly for the occasion.\n\n‘Is every boy here?’ asked Squeers, in a tremendous voice.\n\nEvery boy was there, but every boy was afraid to speak, so Squeers glared along the lines to assure himself; and every eye drooped, and every head cowered down, as he did so.\n\n‘Each boy keep his place,’ said Squeers, administering his favourite blow to the desk, and regarding with gloomy satisfaction the universal start which it never failed to occasion. ‘Nickleby! to your desk, sir.’\n\nIt was remarked by more than one small observer, that there was a very curious and unusual expression in the usher’s face; but he took his seat, without opening his lips in reply. Squeers, casting a triumphant glance at his assistant and a look of most comprehensive despotism on the boys, left the room, and shortly afterwards returned, dragging Smike by the collar--or rather by that fragment of his jacket which was nearest the place where his collar would have been, had he boasted such a decoration.\n\nIn any other place, the appearance of the wretched, jaded, spiritless object would have occasioned a murmur of compassion and remonstrance. It had some effect, even there; for the lookers-on moved uneasily in their seats; and a few of the boldest ventured to steal looks at each other, expressive of indignation and pity.\n\nThey were lost on Squeers, however, whose gaze was fastened on the luckless Smike, as he inquired, according to custom in such cases, whether he had anything to say for himself.\n\n‘Nothing, I suppose?’ said Squeers, with a diabolical grin.\n\nSmike glanced round, and his eye rested, for an instant, on Nicholas, as if he had expected him to intercede; but his look was riveted on his desk.\n\n‘Have you anything to say?’ demanded Squeers again: giving his right arm two or three flourishes to try its power and suppleness. ‘Stand a little out of the way, Mrs. Squeers, my dear; I’ve hardly got room enough.’\n\n‘Spare me, sir!’ cried Smike.\n\n‘Oh! that’s all, is it?’ said Squeers. ‘Yes, I’ll flog you within an inch of your life, and spare you that.’\n\n‘Ha, ha, ha,’ laughed Mrs. Squeers, ‘that’s a good ‘un!’\n\n‘I was driven to do it,’ said Smike faintly; and casting another imploring look about him.\n\n‘Driven to do it, were you?’ said Squeers. ‘Oh! it wasn’t your fault; it was mine, I suppose--eh?’\n\n‘A nasty, ungrateful, pig-headed, brutish, obstinate, sneaking dog,’ exclaimed Mrs. Squeers, taking Smike’s head under her arm, and administering a cuff at every epithet; ‘what does he mean by that?’\n\n‘Stand aside, my dear,’ replied Squeers. ‘We’ll try and find out.’\n\nMrs. Squeers, being out of breath with her exertions, complied. Squeers caught the boy firmly in his grip; one desperate cut had fallen on his body--he was wincing from the lash and uttering a scream of pain--it was raised again, and again about to fall--when Nicholas Nickleby, suddenly starting up, cried ‘Stop!’ in a voice that made the rafters ring.\n\n‘Who cried stop?’ said Squeers, turning savagely round.\n\n‘I,’ said Nicholas, stepping forward. ‘This must not go on.’\n\n‘Must not go on!’ cried Squeers, almost in a shriek.\n\n‘No!’ thundered Nicholas.\n\nAghast and stupefied by the boldness of the interference, Squeers released his hold of Smike, and, falling back a pace or two, gazed upon Nicholas with looks that were positively frightful.\n\n‘I say must not,’ repeated Nicholas, nothing daunted; ‘shall not. I will prevent it.’\n\nSqueers continued to gaze upon him, with his eyes starting out of his head; but astonishment had actually, for the moment, bereft him of speech.\n\n‘You have disregarded all my quiet interference in the miserable lad’s behalf,’ said Nicholas; ‘you have returned no answer to the letter in which I begged forgiveness for him, and offered to be responsible that he would remain quietly here. Don’t blame me for this public interference. You have brought it upon yourself; not I.’\n\n‘Sit down, beggar!’ screamed Squeers, almost beside himself with rage, and seizing Smike as he spoke.\n\n‘Wretch,’ rejoined Nicholas, fiercely, ‘touch him at your peril! I will not stand by, and see it done. My blood is up, and I have the strength of ten such men as you. Look to yourself, for by Heaven I will not spare you, if you drive me on!’\n\n‘Stand back,’ cried Squeers, brandishing his weapon.\n\n‘I have a long series of insults to avenge,’ said Nicholas, flushed with passion; ‘and my indignation is aggravated by the dastardly cruelties practised on helpless infancy in this foul den. Have a care; for if you do raise the devil within me, the consequences shall fall heavily upon your own head!’\n\nHe had scarcely spoken, when Squeers, in a violent outbreak of wrath, and with a cry like the howl of a wild beast, spat upon him, and struck him a blow across the face with his instrument of torture, which raised up a bar of livid flesh as it was inflicted. Smarting with the agony of the blow, and concentrating into that one moment all his feelings of rage, scorn, and indignation, Nicholas sprang upon him, wrested the weapon from his hand, and pinning him by the throat, beat the ruffian till he roared for mercy.\n\nThe boys--with the exception of Master Squeers, who, coming to his father’s assistance, harassed the enemy in the rear--moved not, hand or foot; but Mrs. Squeers, with many shrieks for aid, hung on to the tail of her partner’s coat, and endeavoured to drag him from his infuriated adversary; while Miss Squeers, who had been peeping through the keyhole in expectation of a very different scene, darted in at the very beginning of the attack, and after launching a shower of inkstands at the usher’s head, beat Nicholas to her heart’s content; animating herself, at every blow, with the recollection of his having refused her proffered love, and thus imparting additional strength to an arm which (as she took after her mother in this respect) was, at no time, one of the weakest.\n\nNicholas, in the full torrent of his violence, felt the blows no more than if they had been dealt with feathers; but, becoming tired of the noise and uproar, and feeling that his arm grew weak besides, he threw all his remaining strength into half-a-dozen finishing cuts, and flung Squeers from him with all the force he could muster. The violence of his fall precipitated Mrs. Squeers completely over an adjacent form; and Squeers striking his head against it in his descent, lay at his full length on the ground, stunned and motionless.\n\nHaving brought affairs to this happy termination, and ascertained, to his thorough satisfaction, that Squeers was only stunned, and not dead (upon which point he had had some unpleasant doubts at first), Nicholas left his family to restore him, and retired to consider what course he had better adopt. He looked anxiously round for Smike, as he left the room, but he was nowhere to be seen.\n\nAfter a brief consideration, he packed up a few clothes in a small leathern valise, and, finding that nobody offered to oppose his progress, marched boldly out by the front-door, and shortly afterwards, struck into the road which led to Greta Bridge.\n\nWhen he had cooled sufficiently to be enabled to give his present circumstances some little reflection, they did not appear in a very encouraging light; he had only four shillings and a few pence in his pocket, and was something more than two hundred and fifty miles from London, whither he resolved to direct his steps, that he might ascertain, among other things, what account of the morning’s proceedings Mr. Squeers transmitted to his most affectionate uncle.\n\nLifting up his eyes, as he arrived at the conclusion that there was no remedy for this unfortunate state of things, he beheld a horseman coming towards him, whom, on nearer approach, he discovered, to his infinite chagrin, to be no other than Mr. John Browdie, who, clad in cords and leather leggings, was urging his animal forward by means of a thick ash stick, which seemed to have been recently cut from some stout sapling.\n\n‘I am in no mood for more noise and riot,’ thought Nicholas, ‘and yet, do what I will, I shall have an altercation with this honest blockhead, and perhaps a blow or two from yonder staff.’\n\nIn truth, there appeared some reason to expect that such a result would follow from the encounter, for John Browdie no sooner saw Nicholas advancing, than he reined in his horse by the footpath, and waited until such time as he should come up; looking meanwhile, very sternly between the horse’s ears, at Nicholas, as he came on at his leisure.\n\n‘Servant, young genelman,’ said John.\n\n‘Yours,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Weel; we ha’ met at last,’ observed John, making the stirrup ring under a smart touch of the ash stick.\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Nicholas, hesitating. ‘Come!’ he said, frankly, after a moment’s pause, ‘we parted on no very good terms the last time we met; it was my fault, I believe; but I had no intention of offending you, and no idea that I was doing so. I was very sorry for it, afterwards. Will you shake hands?’\n\n‘Shake honds!’ cried the good-humoured Yorkshireman; ‘ah! that I weel;’ at the same time, he bent down from the saddle, and gave Nicholas’s fist a huge wrench: ‘but wa’at be the matther wi’ thy feace, mun? it be all brokken loike.’\n\n‘It is a cut,’ said Nicholas, turning scarlet as he spoke,--‘a blow; but I returned it to the giver, and with good interest too.’\n\n‘Noa, did ‘ee though?’ exclaimed John Browdie. ‘Well deane! I loike ‘un for thot.’\n\n‘The fact is,’ said Nicholas, not very well knowing how to make the avowal, ‘the fact is, that I have been ill-treated.’\n\n‘Noa!’ interposed John Browdie, in a tone of compassion; for he was a giant in strength and stature, and Nicholas, very likely, in his eyes, seemed a mere dwarf; ‘dean’t say thot.’\n\n‘Yes, I have,’ replied Nicholas, ‘by that man Squeers, and I have beaten him soundly, and am leaving this place in consequence.’\n\n‘What!’ cried John Browdie, with such an ecstatic shout, that the horse quite shied at it. ‘Beatten the schoolmeasther! Ho! ho! ho! Beatten the schoolmeasther! who ever heard o’ the loike o’ that noo! Giv’ us thee hond agean, yoongster. Beatten the schoolmeasther! Dang it, I loov’ thee for’t.’\n\nWith these expressions of delight, John Browdie laughed and laughed again--so loud that the echoes, far and wide, sent back nothing but jovial peals of merriment--and shook Nicholas by the hand meanwhile, no less heartily. When his mirth had subsided, he inquired what Nicholas meant to do; on his informing him, to go straight to London, he shook his head doubtfully, and inquired if he knew how much the coaches charged to carry passengers so far.\n\n‘No, I do not,’ said Nicholas; ‘but it is of no great consequence to me, for I intend walking.’\n\n‘Gang awa’ to Lunnun afoot!’ cried John, in amazement.\n\n‘Every step of the way,’ replied Nicholas. ‘I should be many steps further on by this time, and so goodbye!’\n\n‘Nay noo,’ replied the honest countryman, reining in his impatient horse, ‘stan’ still, tellee. Hoo much cash hast thee gotten?’\n\n‘Not much,’ said Nicholas, colouring, ‘but I can make it enough. Where there’s a will, there’s a way, you know.’\n\nJohn Browdie made no verbal answer to this remark, but putting his hand in his pocket, pulled out an old purse of solid leather, and insisted that Nicholas should borrow from him whatever he required for his present necessities.\n\n‘Dean’t be afeard, mun,’ he said; ‘tak’ eneaf to carry thee whoam. Thee’lt pay me yan day, a’ warrant.’\n\nNicholas could by no means be prevailed upon to borrow more than a sovereign, with which loan Mr. Browdie, after many entreaties that he would accept of more (observing, with a touch of Yorkshire caution, that if he didn’t spend it all, he could put the surplus by, till he had an opportunity of remitting it carriage free), was fain to content himself.\n\n‘Tak’ that bit o’ timber to help thee on wi’, mun,’ he added, pressing his stick on Nicholas, and giving his hand another squeeze; ‘keep a good heart, and bless thee. Beatten the schoolmeasther! ‘Cod it’s the best thing a’ve heerd this twonty year!’\n\nSo saying, and indulging, with more delicacy than might have been expected from him, in another series of loud laughs, for the purpose of avoiding the thanks which Nicholas poured forth, John Browdie set spurs to his horse, and went off at a smart canter: looking back, from time to time, as Nicholas stood gazing after him, and waving his hand cheerily, as if to encourage him on his way. Nicholas watched the horse and rider until they disappeared over the brow of a distant hill, and then set forward on his journey.\n\nHe did not travel far that afternoon, for by this time it was nearly dark, and there had been a heavy fall of snow, which not only rendered the way toilsome, but the track uncertain and difficult to find, after daylight, save by experienced wayfarers. He lay, that night, at a cottage, where beds were let at a cheap rate to the more humble class of travellers; and, rising betimes next morning, made his way before night to Boroughbridge. Passing through that town in search of some cheap resting-place, he stumbled upon an empty barn within a couple of hundred yards of the roadside; in a warm corner of which, he stretched his weary limbs, and soon fell asleep.\n\nWhen he awoke next morning, and tried to recollect his dreams, which had been all connected with his recent sojourn at Dotheboys Hall, he sat up, rubbed his eyes and stared--not with the most composed countenance possible--at some motionless object which seemed to be stationed within a few yards in front of him.\n\n‘Strange!’ cried Nicholas; ‘can this be some lingering creation of the visions that have scarcely left me! It cannot be real--and yet I--I am awake! Smike!’\n\nThe form moved, rose, advanced, and dropped upon its knees at his feet. It was Smike indeed.\n\n‘Why do you kneel to me?’ said Nicholas, hastily raising him.\n\n‘To go with you--anywhere--everywhere--to the world’s end--to the churchyard grave,’ replied Smike, clinging to his hand. ‘Let me, oh do let me. You are my home--my kind friend--take me with you, pray.’\n\n‘I am a friend who can do little for you,’ said Nicholas, kindly. ‘How came you here?’\n\nHe had followed him, it seemed; had never lost sight of him all the way; had watched while he slept, and when he halted for refreshment; and had feared to appear before, lest he should be sent back. He had not intended to appear now, but Nicholas had awakened more suddenly than he looked for, and he had had no time to conceal himself.\n\n‘Poor fellow!’ said Nicholas, ‘your hard fate denies you any friend but one, and he is nearly as poor and helpless as yourself.’\n\n‘May I--may I go with you?’ asked Smike, timidly. ‘I will be your faithful hard-working servant, I will, indeed. I want no clothes,’ added the poor creature, drawing his rags together; ‘these will do very well. I only want to be near you.’\n\n‘And you shall,’ cried Nicholas. ‘And the world shall deal by you as it does by me, till one or both of us shall quit it for a better. Come!’\n\nWith these words, he strapped his burden on his shoulders, and, taking his stick in one hand, extended the other to his delighted charge; and so they passed out of the old barn, together.\n\n# CHAPTER 14\n\nHaving the Misfortune to treat of none but Common People, is necessarily of a Mean and Vulgar Character\n\nIn that quarter of London in which Golden Square is situated, there is a bygone, faded, tumble-down street, with two irregular rows of tall meagre houses, which seem to have stared each other out of countenance years ago. The very chimneys appear to have grown dismal and melancholy, from having had nothing better to look at than the chimneys over the way. Their tops are battered, and broken, and blackened with smoke; and, here and there, some taller stack than the rest, inclining heavily to one side, and toppling over the roof, seems to meditate taking revenge for half a century’s neglect, by crushing the inhabitants of the garrets beneath.\n\nThe fowls who peck about the kennels, jerking their bodies hither and thither with a gait which none but town fowls are ever seen to adopt, and which any country cock or hen would be puzzled to understand, are perfectly in keeping with the crazy habitations of their owners. Dingy, ill-plumed, drowsy flutterers, sent, like many of the neighbouring children, to get a livelihood in the streets, they hop, from stone to stone, in forlorn search of some hidden eatable in the mud, and can scarcely raise a crow among them. The only one with anything approaching to a voice, is an aged bantam at the baker’s; and even he is hoarse, in consequence of bad living in his last place.\n\nTo judge from the size of the houses, they have been, at one time, tenanted by persons of better condition than their present occupants; but they are now let off, by the week, in floors or rooms, and every door has almost as many plates or bell-handles as there are apartments within. The windows are, for the same reason, sufficiently diversified in appearance, being ornamented with every variety of common blind and curtain that can easily be imagined; while every doorway is blocked up, and rendered nearly impassable, by a motley collection of children and porter pots of all sizes, from the baby in arms and the half-pint pot, to the full-grown girl and half-gallon can.\n\nIn the parlour of one of these houses, which was perhaps a thought dirtier than any of its neighbours; which exhibited more bell-handles, children, and porter pots, and caught in all its freshness the first gust of the thick black smoke that poured forth, night and day, from a large brewery hard by; hung a bill, announcing that there was yet one room to let within its walls, though on what story the vacant room could be--regard being had to the outward tokens of many lodgers which the whole front displayed, from the mangle in the kitchen window to the flower-pots on the parapet--it would have been beyond the power of a calculating boy to discover.\n\nThe common stairs of this mansion were bare and carpetless; but a curious visitor who had to climb his way to the top, might have observed that there were not wanting indications of the progressive poverty of the inmates, although their rooms were shut. Thus, the first-floor lodgers, being flush of furniture, kept an old mahogany table--real mahogany--on the landing-place outside, which was only taken in, when occasion required. On the second story, the spare furniture dwindled down to a couple of old deal chairs, of which one, belonging to the back-room, was shorn of a leg, and bottomless. The story above, boasted no greater excess than a worm-eaten wash-tub; and the garret landing-place displayed no costlier articles than two crippled pitchers, and some broken blacking-bottles.\n\nIt was on this garret landing-place that a hard-featured square-faced man, elderly and shabby, stopped to unlock the door of the front attic, into which, having surmounted the task of turning the rusty key in its still more rusty wards, he walked with the air of legal owner.\n\nThis person wore a wig of short, coarse, red hair, which he took off with his hat, and hung upon a nail. Having adopted in its place a dirty cotton nightcap, and groped about in the dark till he found a remnant of candle, he knocked at the partition which divided the two garrets, and inquired, in a loud voice, whether Mr. Noggs had a light.\n\nThe sounds that came back were stifled by the lath and plaster, and it seemed moreover as though the speaker had uttered them from the interior of a mug or other drinking vessel; but they were in the voice of Newman, and conveyed a reply in the affirmative.\n\n‘A nasty night, Mr. Noggs!’ said the man in the nightcap, stepping in to light his candle.\n\n‘Does it rain?’ asked Newman.\n\n‘Does it?’ replied the other pettishly. ‘I am wet through.’\n\n‘It doesn’t take much to wet you and me through, Mr. Crowl,’ said Newman, laying his hand upon the lappel of his threadbare coat.\n\n‘Well; and that makes it the more vexatious,’ observed Mr. Crowl, in the same pettish tone.\n\nUttering a low querulous growl, the speaker, whose harsh countenance was the very epitome of selfishness, raked the scanty fire nearly out of the grate, and, emptying the glass which Noggs had pushed towards him, inquired where he kept his coals.\n\nNewman Noggs pointed to the bottom of a cupboard, and Mr. Crowl, seizing the shovel, threw on half the stock: which Noggs very deliberately took off again, without saying a word.\n\n‘You have not turned saving, at this time of day, I hope?’ said Crowl.\n\nNewman pointed to the empty glass, as though it were a sufficient refutation of the charge, and briefly said that he was going downstairs to supper.\n\n‘To the Kenwigses?’ asked Crowl.\n\nNewman nodded assent.\n\n‘Think of that now!’ said Crowl. ‘If I didn’t--thinking that you were certain not to go, because you said you wouldn’t--tell Kenwigs I couldn’t come, and make up my mind to spend the evening with you!’\n\n‘I was obliged to go,’ said Newman. ‘They would have me.’\n\n‘Well; but what’s to become of me?’ urged the selfish man, who never thought of anybody else. ‘It’s all your fault. I’ll tell you what--I’ll sit by your fire till you come back again.’\n\nNewman cast a despairing glance at his small store of fuel, but, not having the courage to say no--a word which in all his life he never had said at the right time, either to himself or anyone else--gave way to the proposed arrangement. Mr. Crowl immediately went about making himself as comfortable, with Newman Nogg’s means, as circumstances would admit of his being made.\n\nThe lodgers to whom Crowl had made allusion under the designation of ‘the Kenwigses,’ were the wife and olive branches of one Mr. Kenwigs, a turner in ivory, who was looked upon as a person of some consideration on the premises, inasmuch as he occupied the whole of the first floor, comprising a suite of two rooms. Mrs. Kenwigs, too, was quite a lady in her manners, and of a very genteel family, having an uncle who collected a water-rate; besides which distinction, the two eldest of her little girls went twice a week to a dancing school in the neighbourhood, and had flaxen hair, tied with blue ribbons, hanging in luxuriant pigtails down their backs; and wore little white trousers with frills round the ankles--for all of which reasons, and many more equally valid but too numerous to mention, Mrs. Kenwigs was considered a very desirable person to know, and was the constant theme of all the gossips in the street, and even three or four doors round the corner at both ends.\n\nIt was the anniversary of that happy day on which the Church of England as by law established, had bestowed Mrs. Kenwigs upon Mr. Kenwigs; and in grateful commemoration of the same, Mrs. Kenwigs had invited a few select friends to cards and a supper in the first floor, and had put on a new gown to receive them in: which gown, being of a flaming colour and made upon a juvenile principle, was so successful that Mr. Kenwigs said the eight years of matrimony and the five children seemed all a dream, and Mrs. Kenwigs younger and more blooming than on the very first Sunday he had kept company with her.\n\nBeautiful as Mrs. Kenwigs looked when she was dressed though, and so stately that you would have supposed she had a cook and housemaid at least, and nothing to do but order them about, she had a world of trouble with the preparations; more, indeed, than she, being of a delicate and genteel constitution, could have sustained, had not the pride of housewifery upheld her. At last, however, all the things that had to be got together were got together, and all the things that had to be got out of the way were got out of the way, and everything was ready, and the collector himself having promised to come, fortune smiled upon the occasion.\n\nThe party was admirably selected. There were, first of all, Mr. Kenwigs and Mrs. Kenwigs, and four olive Kenwigses who sat up to supper; firstly, because it was but right that they should have a treat on such a day; and secondly, because their going to bed, in presence of the company, would have been inconvenient, not to say improper. Then, there was a young lady who had made Mrs. Kenwigs’s dress, and who--it was the most convenient thing in the world--living in the two-pair back, gave up her bed to the baby, and got a little girl to watch it. Then, to match this young lady, was a young man, who had known Mr. Kenwigs when he was a bachelor, and was much esteemed by the ladies, as bearing the reputation of a rake. To these were added a newly-married couple, who had visited Mr. and Mrs. Kenwigs in their courtship; and a sister of Mrs. Kenwigs’s, who was quite a beauty; besides whom, there was another young man, supposed to entertain honourable designs upon the lady last mentioned; and Mr. Noggs, who was a genteel person to ask, because he had been a gentleman once. There were also an elderly lady from the back-parlour, and one more young lady, who, next to the collector, perhaps was the great lion of the party, being the daughter of a theatrical fireman, who ‘went on’ in the pantomime, and had the greatest turn for the stage that was ever known, being able to sing and recite in a manner that brought the tears into Mrs. Kenwigs’s eyes. There was only one drawback upon the pleasure of seeing such friends, and that was, that the lady in the back-parlour, who was very fat, and turned of sixty, came in a low book-muslin dress and short kid gloves, which so exasperated Mrs Kenwigs, that that lady assured her visitors, in private, that if it hadn’t happened that the supper was cooking at the back-parlour grate at that moment, she certainly would have requested its representative to withdraw.\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, ‘wouldn’t it be better to begin a round game?’\n\n‘Kenwigs, my dear,’ returned his wife, ‘I am surprised at you. Would you begin without my uncle?’\n\n‘I forgot the collector,’ said Kenwigs; ‘oh no, that would never do.’\n\n‘He’s so particular,’ said Mrs. Kenwigs, turning to the other married lady, ‘that if we began without him, I should be out of his will for ever.’\n\n‘Dear!’ cried the married lady.\n\n‘You’ve no idea what he is,’ replied Mrs. Kenwigs; ‘and yet as good a creature as ever breathed.’\n\n‘The kindest-hearted man as ever was,’ said Kenwigs.\n\n‘It goes to his heart, I believe, to be forced to cut the water off, when the people don’t pay,’ observed the bachelor friend, intending a joke.\n\n‘George,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, solemnly, ‘none of that, if you please.’\n\n‘It was only my joke,’ said the friend, abashed.\n\n‘George,’ rejoined Mr. Kenwigs, ‘a joke is a wery good thing--a wery good thing--but when that joke is made at the expense of Mrs. Kenwigs’s feelings, I set my face against it. A man in public life expects to be sneered at--it is the fault of his elewated sitiwation, and not of himself. Mrs. Kenwigs’s relation is a public man, and that he knows, George, and that he can bear; but putting Mrs. Kenwigs out of the question (if I COULD put Mrs. Kenwigs out of the question on such an occasion as this), I have the honour to be connected with the collector by marriage; and I cannot allow these remarks in my--’ Mr. Kenwigs was going to say ‘house,’ but he rounded the sentence with ‘apartments’.\n\nAt the conclusion of these observations, which drew forth evidences of acute feeling from Mrs. Kenwigs, and had the intended effect of impressing the company with a deep sense of the collector’s dignity, a ring was heard at the bell.\n\n‘That’s him,’ whispered Mr. Kenwigs, greatly excited. ‘Morleena, my dear, run down and let your uncle in, and kiss him directly you get the door open. Hem! Let’s be talking.’\n\nAdopting Mr. Kenwigs’s suggestion, the company spoke very loudly, to look easy and unembarrassed; and almost as soon as they had begun to do so, a short old gentleman in drabs and gaiters, with a face that might have been carved out of LIGNUM VITAE, for anything that appeared to the contrary, was led playfully in by Miss Morleena Kenwigs, regarding whose uncommon Christian name it may be here remarked that it had been invented and composed by Mrs. Kenwigs previous to her first lying-in, for the special distinction of her eldest child, in case it should prove a daughter.\n\n‘Oh, uncle, I am SO glad to see you,’ said Mrs. Kenwigs, kissing the collector affectionately on both cheeks. ‘So glad!’\n\n‘Many happy returns of the day, my dear,’ replied the collector, returning the compliment.\n\nNow, this was an interesting thing. Here was a collector of water-rates, without his book, without his pen and ink, without his double knock, without his intimidation, kissing--actually kissing--an agreeable female, and leaving taxes, summonses, notices that he had called, or announcements that he would never call again, for two quarters’ due, wholly out of the question. It was pleasant to see how the company looked on, quite absorbed in the sight, and to behold the nods and winks with which they expressed their gratification at finding so much humanity in a tax-gatherer.\n\n‘Where will you sit, uncle?’ said Mrs. Kenwigs, in the full glow of family pride, which the appearance of her distinguished relation occasioned.\n\n‘Anywheres, my dear,’ said the collector, ‘I am not particular.’\n\nNot particular! What a meek collector! If he had been an author, who knew his place, he couldn’t have been more humble.\n\n‘Mr. Lillyvick,’ said Kenwigs, addressing the collector, ‘some friends here, sir, are very anxious for the honour of--thank you--Mr. and Mrs Cutler, Mr. Lillyvick.’\n\n‘Proud to know you, sir,’ said Mr. Cutler; ‘I’ve heerd of you very often.’ These were not mere words of ceremony; for, Mr. Cutler, having kept house in Mr. Lillyvick’s parish, had heard of him very often indeed. His attention in calling had been quite extraordinary.\n\n‘George, you know, I think, Mr. Lillyvick,’ said Kenwigs; ‘lady from downstairs--Mr. Lillyvick. Mr. Snewkes--Mr. Lillyvick. Miss Green--Mr Lillyvick. Mr. Lillyvick--Miss Petowker of the Theatre Royal, Drury Lane. Very glad to make two public characters acquainted! Mrs. Kenwigs, my dear, will you sort the counters?’\n\nMrs. Kenwigs, with the assistance of Newman Noggs, (who, as he performed sundry little acts of kindness for the children, at all times and seasons, was humoured in his request to be taken no notice of, and was merely spoken about, in a whisper, as the decayed gentleman), did as he was desired; and the greater part of the guests sat down to speculation, while Newman himself, Mrs. Kenwigs, and Miss Petowker of the Theatre Royal Drury Lane, looked after the supper-table.\n\nWhile the ladies were thus busying themselves, Mr. Lillyvick was intent upon the game in progress, and as all should be fish that comes to a water-collector’s net, the dear old gentleman was by no means scrupulous in appropriating to himself the property of his neighbours, which, on the contrary, he abstracted whenever an opportunity presented itself, smiling good-humouredly all the while, and making so many condescending speeches to the owners, that they were delighted with his amiability, and thought in their hearts that he deserved to be Chancellor of the Exchequer at least.\n\nAfter a great deal of trouble, and the administration of many slaps on the head to the infant Kenwigses, whereof two of the most rebellious were summarily banished, the cloth was laid with much elegance, and a pair of boiled fowls, a large piece of pork, apple-pie, potatoes and greens, were served; at sight of which, the worthy Mr. Lillyvick vented a great many witticisms, and plucked up amazingly: to the immense delight and satisfaction of the whole body of admirers.\n\nVery well and very fast the supper went off; no more serious difficulties occurring, than those which arose from the incessant demand for clean knives and forks; which made poor Mrs. Kenwigs wish, more than once, that private society adopted the principle of schools, and required that every guest should bring his own knife, fork, and spoon; which doubtless would be a great accommodation in many cases, and to no one more so than to the lady and gentleman of the house, especially if the school principle were carried out to the full extent, and the articles were expected, as a matter of delicacy, not to be taken away again.\n\nEverybody having eaten everything, the table was cleared in a most alarming hurry, and with great noise; and the spirits, whereat the eyes of Newman Noggs glistened, being arranged in order, with water both hot and cold, the party composed themselves for conviviality; Mr. Lillyvick being stationed in a large armchair by the fireside, and the four little Kenwigses disposed on a small form in front of the company with their flaxen tails towards them, and their faces to the fire; an arrangement which was no sooner perfected, than Mrs. Kenwigs was overpowered by the feelings of a mother, and fell upon the left shoulder of Mr. Kenwigs dissolved in tears.\n\n‘They are so beautiful!’ said Mrs. Kenwigs, sobbing.\n\n‘Oh, dear,’ said all the ladies, ‘so they are! it’s very natural you should feel proud of that; but don’t give way, don’t.’\n\n‘I can--not help it, and it don’t signify,’ sobbed Mrs. Kenwigs; ‘oh! they’re too beautiful to live, much too beautiful!’\n\nOn hearing this alarming presentiment of their being doomed to an early death in the flower of their infancy, all four little girls raised a hideous cry, and burying their heads in their mother’s lap simultaneously, screamed until the eight flaxen tails vibrated again; Mrs. Kenwigs meanwhile clasping them alternately to her bosom, with attitudes expressive of distraction, which Miss Petowker herself might have copied.\n\nAt length, the anxious mother permitted herself to be soothed into a more tranquil state, and the little Kenwigses, being also composed, were distributed among the company, to prevent the possibility of Mrs. Kenwigs being again overcome by the blaze of their combined beauty. This done, the ladies and gentlemen united in prophesying that they would live for many, many years, and that there was no occasion at all for Mrs. Kenwigs to distress herself; which, in good truth, there did not appear to be; the loveliness of the children by no means justifying her apprehensions.\n\n‘This day eight year,’ said Mr. Kenwigs after a pause. ‘Dear me--ah!’\n\nThis reflection was echoed by all present, who said ‘Ah!’ first, and ‘dear me,’ afterwards.\n\n‘I was younger then,’ tittered Mrs. Kenwigs.\n\n‘No,’ said the collector.\n\n‘Certainly not,’ added everybody.\n\n‘I remember my niece,’ said Mr. Lillyvick, surveying his audience with a grave air; ‘I remember her, on that very afternoon, when she first acknowledged to her mother a partiality for Kenwigs. “Mother,” she says, “I love him.”’\n\n‘“Adore him,” I said, uncle,’ interposed Mrs. Kenwigs.\n\n‘“Love him,” I think, my dear,’ said the collector, firmly.\n\n‘Perhaps you are right, uncle,’ replied Mrs. Kenwigs, submissively. ‘I thought it was “adore.”’\n\n‘“Love,” my dear,’ retorted Mr. Lillyvick. ‘“Mother,” she says, “I love him!” “What do I hear?” cries her mother; and instantly falls into strong conwulsions.’\n\nA general exclamation of astonishment burst from the company.\n\n‘Into strong conwulsions,’ repeated Mr. Lillyvick, regarding them with a rigid look. ‘Kenwigs will excuse my saying, in the presence of friends, that there was a very great objection to him, on the ground that he was beneath the family, and would disgrace it. You remember, Kenwigs?’\n\n‘Certainly,’ replied that gentleman, in no way displeased at the reminiscence, inasmuch as it proved, beyond all doubt, what a high family Mrs. Kenwigs came of.\n\n‘I shared in that feeling,’ said Mr. Lillyvick: ‘perhaps it was natural; perhaps it wasn’t.’\n\nA gentle murmur seemed to say, that, in one of Mr. Lillyvick’s station, the objection was not only natural, but highly praiseworthy.\n\n‘I came round to him in time,’ said Mr. Lillyvick. ‘After they were married, and there was no help for it, I was one of the first to say that Kenwigs must be taken notice of. The family DID take notice of him, in consequence, and on my representation; and I am bound to say--and proud to say--that I have always found him a very honest, well-behaved, upright, respectable sort of man. Kenwigs, shake hands.’\n\n‘I am proud to do it, sir,’ said Mr. Kenwigs.\n\n‘So am I, Kenwigs,’ rejoined Mr. Lillyvick.\n\n‘A very happy life I have led with your niece, sir,’ said Kenwigs.\n\n‘It would have been your own fault if you had not, sir,’ remarked Mr Lillyvick.\n\n‘Morleena Kenwigs,’ cried her mother, at this crisis, much affected, ‘kiss your dear uncle!’\n\nThe young lady did as she was requested, and the three other little girls were successively hoisted up to the collector’s countenance, and subjected to the same process, which was afterwards repeated on them by the majority of those present.\n\n‘Oh dear, Mrs. Kenwigs,’ said Miss Petowker, ‘while Mr. Noggs is making that punch to drink happy returns in, do let Morleena go through that figure dance before Mr. Lillyvick.’\n\n‘No, no, my dear,’ replied Mrs. Kenwigs, ‘it will only worry my uncle.’\n\n‘It can’t worry him, I am sure,’ said Miss Petowker. ‘You will be very much pleased, won’t you, sir?’\n\n‘That I am sure I shall’ replied the collector, glancing at the punch-mixer.\n\n‘Well then, I’ll tell you what,’ said Mrs. Kenwigs, ‘Morleena shall do the steps, if uncle can persuade Miss Petowker to recite us the Blood-Drinker’s Burial, afterwards.’\n\nThere was a great clapping of hands and stamping of feet, at this proposition; the subject whereof, gently inclined her head several times, in acknowledgment of the reception.\n\n‘You know,’ said Miss Petowker, reproachfully, ‘that I dislike doing anything professional in private parties.’\n\n‘Oh, but not here!’ said Mrs. Kenwigs. ‘We are all so very friendly and pleasant, that you might as well be going through it in your own room; besides, the occasion--’\n\n‘I can’t resist that,’ interrupted Miss Petowker; ‘anything in my humble power I shall be delighted to do.’\n\nMrs. Kenwigs and Miss Petowker had arranged a small PROGRAMME of the entertainments between them, of which this was the prescribed order, but they had settled to have a little pressing on both sides, because it looked more natural. The company being all ready, Miss Petowker hummed a tune, and Morleena danced a dance; having previously had the soles of her shoes chalked, with as much care as if she were going on the tight-rope. It was a very beautiful figure, comprising a great deal of work for the arms, and was received with unbounded applause.\n\n‘If I was blessed with a--a child--’ said Miss Petowker, blushing, ‘of such genius as that, I would have her out at the Opera instantly.’\n\nMrs. Kenwigs sighed, and looked at Mr. Kenwigs, who shook his head, and observed that he was doubtful about it.\n\n‘Kenwigs is afraid,’ said Mrs. K.\n\n‘What of?’ inquired Miss Petowker, ‘not of her failing?’\n\n‘Oh no,’ replied Mrs. Kenwigs, ‘but if she grew up what she is now,--only think of the young dukes and marquises.’\n\n‘Very right,’ said the collector.\n\n‘Still,’ submitted Miss Petowker, ‘if she took a proper pride in herself, you know--’\n\n‘There’s a good deal in that,’ observed Mrs. Kenwigs, looking at her husband.\n\n‘I only know--’ faltered Miss Petowker,--‘it may be no rule to be sure--but I have never found any inconvenience or unpleasantness of that sort.’\n\nMr. Kenwigs, with becoming gallantry, said that settled the question at once, and that he would take the subject into his serious consideration. This being resolved upon, Miss Petowker was entreated to begin the Blood-Drinker’s Burial; to which end, that young lady let down her back hair, and taking up her position at the other end of the room, with the bachelor friend posted in a corner, to rush out at the cue ‘in death expire,’ and catch her in his arms when she died raving mad, went through the performance with extraordinary spirit, and to the great terror of the little Kenwigses, who were all but frightened into fits.\n\nThe ecstasies consequent upon the effort had not yet subsided, and Newman (who had not been thoroughly sober at so late an hour for a long long time,) had not yet been able to put in a word of announcement, that the punch was ready, when a hasty knock was heard at the room-door, which elicited a shriek from Mrs. Kenwigs, who immediately divined that the baby had fallen out of bed.\n\n‘Who is that?’ demanded Mr. Kenwigs, sharply.\n\n‘Don’t be alarmed, it’s only me,’ said Crowl, looking in, in his nightcap. ‘The baby is very comfortable, for I peeped into the room as I came down, and it’s fast asleep, and so is the girl; and I don’t think the candle will set fire to the bed-curtain, unless a draught was to get into the room--it’s Mr. Noggs that’s wanted.’\n\n‘Me!’ cried Newman, much astonished.\n\n‘Why, it IS a queer hour, isn’t it?’ replied Crowl, who was not best pleased at the prospect of losing his fire; ‘and they are queer-looking people, too, all covered with rain and mud. Shall I tell them to go away?’\n\n‘No,’ said Newman, rising. ‘People? How many?’\n\n‘Two,’ rejoined Crowl.\n\n‘Want me? By name?’ asked Newman.\n\n‘By name,’ replied Crowl. ‘Mr. Newman Noggs, as pat as need be.’\n\nNewman reflected for a few seconds, and then hurried away, muttering that he would be back directly. He was as good as his word; for, in an exceedingly short time, he burst into the room, and seizing, without a word of apology or explanation, a lighted candle and tumbler of hot punch from the table, darted away like a madman.\n\n‘What the deuce is the matter with him?’ exclaimed Crowl, throwing the door open. ‘Hark! Is there any noise above?’\n\nThe guests rose in great confusion, and, looking in each other’s faces with much perplexity and some fear, stretched their necks forward, and listened attentively.\n\n# CHAPTER 15\n\nAcquaints the Reader with the Cause and Origin of the Interruption described in the last Chapter, and with some other Matters necessary to be known\n\nNewman Noggs scrambled in violent haste upstairs with the steaming beverage, which he had so unceremoniously snatched from the table of Mr Kenwigs, and indeed from the very grasp of the water-rate collector, who was eyeing the contents of the tumbler, at the moment of its unexpected abstraction, with lively marks of pleasure visible in his countenance. He bore his prize straight to his own back-garret, where, footsore and nearly shoeless, wet, dirty, jaded, and disfigured with every mark of fatiguing travel, sat Nicholas and Smike, at once the cause and partner of his toil; both perfectly worn out by their unwonted and protracted exertion.\n\nNewman’s first act was to compel Nicholas, with gentle force, to swallow half of the punch at a breath, nearly boiling as it was; and his next, to pour the remainder down the throat of Smike, who, never having tasted anything stronger than aperient medicine in his whole life, exhibited various odd manifestations of surprise and delight, during the passage of the liquor down his throat, and turned up his eyes most emphatically when it was all gone.\n\n‘You are wet through,’ said Newman, passing his hand hastily over the coat which Nicholas had thrown off; ‘and I--I--haven’t even a change,’ he added, with a wistful glance at the shabby clothes he wore himself.\n\n‘I have dry clothes, or at least such as will serve my turn well, in my bundle,’ replied Nicholas. ‘If you look so distressed to see me, you will add to the pain I feel already, at being compelled, for one night, to cast myself upon your slender means for aid and shelter.’\n\nNewman did not look the less distressed to hear Nicholas talking in this strain; but, upon his young friend grasping him heartily by the hand, and assuring him that nothing but implicit confidence in the sincerity of his professions, and kindness of feeling towards himself, would have induced him, on any consideration, even to have made him acquainted with his arrival in London, Mr. Noggs brightened up again, and went about making such arrangements as were in his power for the comfort of his visitors, with extreme alacrity.\n\nThese were simple enough; poor Newman’s means halting at a very considerable distance short of his inclinations; but, slight as they were, they were not made without much bustling and running about. As Nicholas had husbanded his scanty stock of money, so well that it was not yet quite expended, a supper of bread and cheese, with some cold beef from the cook’s shop, was soon placed upon the table; and these viands being flanked by a bottle of spirits and a pot of porter, there was no ground for apprehension on the score of hunger or thirst, at all events. Such preparations as Newman had it in his power to make, for the accommodation of his guests during the night, occupied no very great time in completing; and as he had insisted, as an express preliminary, that Nicholas should change his clothes, and that Smike should invest himself in his solitary coat (which no entreaties would dissuade him from stripping off for the purpose), the travellers partook of their frugal fare, with more satisfaction than one of them at least had derived from many a better meal.\n\nThey then drew near the fire, which Newman Noggs had made up as well as he could, after the inroads of Crowl upon the fuel; and Nicholas, who had hitherto been restrained by the extreme anxiety of his friend that he should refresh himself after his journey, now pressed him with earnest questions concerning his mother and sister.\n\n‘Well,’ replied Newman, with his accustomed taciturnity; ‘both well.’\n\n‘They are living in the city still?’ inquired Nicholas.\n\n‘They are,’ said Newman.\n\n‘And my sister,’--added Nicholas. ‘Is she still engaged in the business which she wrote to tell me she thought she should like so much?’\n\nNewman opened his eyes rather wider than usual, but merely replied by a gasp, which, according to the action of the head that accompanied it, was interpreted by his friends as meaning yes or no. In the present instance, the pantomime consisted of a nod, and not a shake; so Nicholas took the answer as a favourable one.\n\n‘Now listen to me,’ said Nicholas, laying his hand on Newman’s shoulder. ‘Before I would make an effort to see them, I deemed it expedient to come to you, lest, by gratifying my own selfish desire, I should inflict an injury upon them which I can never repair. What has my uncle heard from Yorkshire?’\n\nNewman opened and shut his mouth, several times, as though he were trying his utmost to speak, but could make nothing of it, and finally fixed his eyes on Nicholas with a grim and ghastly stare.\n\n‘What has he heard?’ urged Nicholas, colouring. ‘You see that I am prepared to hear the very worst that malice can have suggested. Why should you conceal it from me? I must know it sooner or later; and what purpose can be gained by trifling with the matter for a few minutes, when half the time would put me in possession of all that has occurred? Tell me at once, pray.’\n\n‘Tomorrow morning,’ said Newman; ‘hear it tomorrow.’\n\n‘What purpose would that answer?’ urged Nicholas.\n\n‘You would sleep the better,’ replied Newman.\n\n‘I should sleep the worse,’ answered Nicholas, impatiently. ‘Sleep! Exhausted as I am, and standing in no common need of rest, I cannot hope to close my eyes all night, unless you tell me everything.’\n\n‘And if I should tell you everything,’ said Newman, hesitating.\n\n‘Why, then you may rouse my indignation or wound my pride,’ rejoined Nicholas; ‘but you will not break my rest; for if the scene were acted over again, I could take no other part than I have taken; and whatever consequences may accrue to myself from it, I shall never regret doing as I have done--never, if I starve or beg in consequence. What is a little poverty or suffering, to the disgrace of the basest and most inhuman cowardice! I tell you, if I had stood by, tamely and passively, I should have hated myself, and merited the contempt of every man in existence. The black-hearted scoundrel!’\n\nWith this gentle allusion to the absent Mr. Squeers, Nicholas repressed his rising wrath, and relating to Newman exactly what had passed at Dotheboys Hall, entreated him to speak out without more pressing. Thus adjured, Mr. Noggs took, from an old trunk, a sheet of paper, which appeared to have been scrawled over in great haste; and after sundry extraordinary demonstrations of reluctance, delivered himself in the following terms.\n\n‘My dear young man, you mustn’t give way to--this sort of thing will never do, you know--as to getting on in the world, if you take everybody’s part that’s ill-treated--Damn it, I am proud to hear of it; and would have done it myself!’\n\nNewman accompanied this very unusual outbreak with a violent blow upon the table, as if, in the heat of the moment, he had mistaken it for the chest or ribs of Mr. Wackford Squeers. Having, by this open declaration of his feelings, quite precluded himself from offering Nicholas any cautious worldly advice (which had been his first intention), Mr. Noggs went straight to the point.\n\n‘The day before yesterday,’ said Newman, ‘your uncle received this letter. I took a hasty copy of it, while he was out. Shall I read it?’\n\n‘If you please,’ replied Nicholas. Newman Noggs accordingly read as follows:\n\n‘My pa requests me to write to you, the doctors considering it doubtful whether he will ever recuvver the use of his legs which prevents his holding a pen.\n\n‘We are in a state of mind beyond everything, and my pa is one mask of brooses both blue and green likewise two forms are steepled in his Goar. We were kimpelled to have him carried down into the kitchen where he now lays. You will judge from this that he has been brought very low.\n\n‘When your nevew that you recommended for a teacher had done this to my pa and jumped upon his body with his feet and also langwedge which I will not pollewt my pen with describing, he assaulted my ma with dreadful violence, dashed her to the earth, and drove her back comb several inches into her head. A very little more and it must have entered her skull. We have a medical certifiket that if it had, the tortershell would have affected the brain.\n\n‘Me and my brother were then the victims of his feury since which we have suffered very much which leads us to the arrowing belief that we have received some injury in our insides, especially as no marks of violence are visible externally. I am screaming out loud all the time I write and so is my brother which takes off my attention rather and I hope will excuse mistakes.\n\n‘The monster having sasiated his thirst for blood ran away, taking with him a boy of desperate character that he had excited to rebellyon, and a garnet ring belonging to my ma, and not having been apprehended by the constables is supposed to have been took up by some stage-coach. My pa begs that if he comes to you the ring may be returned, and that you will let the thief and assassin go, as if we prosecuted him he would only be transported, and if he is let go he is sure to be hung before long which will save us trouble and be much more satisfactory. Hoping to hear from you when convenient\n\n‘I remain ‘Yours and cetrer ‘FANNY SQUEERS.\n\n‘P.S. I pity his ignorance and despise him.’\n\nA profound silence succeeded to the reading of this choice epistle, during which Newman Noggs, as he folded it up, gazed with a kind of grotesque pity at the boy of desperate character therein referred to; who, having no more distinct perception of the matter in hand, than that he had been the unfortunate cause of heaping trouble and falsehood upon Nicholas, sat mute and dispirited, with a most woe-begone and heart-stricken look.\n\n‘Mr. Noggs,’ said Nicholas, after a few moments’ reflection, ‘I must go out at once.’\n\n‘Go out!’ cried Newman.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Nicholas, ‘to Golden Square. Nobody who knows me would believe this story of the ring; but it may suit the purpose, or gratify the hatred of Mr. Ralph Nickleby to feign to attach credence to it. It is due--not to him, but to myself--that I should state the truth; and moreover, I have a word or two to exchange with him, which will not keep cool.’\n\n‘They must,’ said Newman.\n\n‘They must not, indeed,’ rejoined Nicholas firmly, as he prepared to leave the house.\n\n‘Hear me speak,’ said Newman, planting himself before his impetuous young friend. ‘He is not there. He is away from town. He will not be back for three days; and I know that letter will not be answered before he returns.’\n\n‘Are you sure of this?’ asked Nicholas, chafing violently, and pacing the narrow room with rapid strides.\n\n‘Quite,’ rejoined Newman. ‘He had hardly read it when he was called away. Its contents are known to nobody but himself and us.’\n\n‘Are you certain?’ demanded Nicholas, precipitately; ‘not even to my mother or sister? If I thought that they--I will go there--I must see them. Which is the way? Where is it?’\n\n‘Now, be advised by me,’ said Newman, speaking for the moment, in his earnestness, like any other man--‘make no effort to see even them, till he comes home. I know the man. Do not seem to have been tampering with anybody. When he returns, go straight to him, and speak as boldly as you like. Guessing at the real truth, he knows it as well as you or I. Trust him for that.’\n\n‘You mean well to me, and should know him better than I can,’ replied Nicholas, after some consideration. ‘Well; let it be so.’\n\nNewman, who had stood during the foregoing conversation with his back planted against the door, ready to oppose any egress from the apartment by force, if necessary, resumed his seat with much satisfaction; and as the water in the kettle was by this time boiling, made a glassful of spirits and water for Nicholas, and a cracked mug-full for the joint accommodation of himself and Smike, of which the two partook in great harmony, while Nicholas, leaning his head upon his hand, remained buried in melancholy meditation.\n\nMeanwhile, the company below stairs, after listening attentively and not hearing any noise which would justify them in interfering for the gratification of their curiosity, returned to the chamber of the Kenwigses, and employed themselves in hazarding a great variety of conjectures relative to the cause of Mr. Noggs’ sudden disappearance and detention.\n\n‘Lor, I’ll tell you what,’ said Mrs. Kenwigs. ‘Suppose it should be an express sent up to say that his property has all come back again!’\n\n‘Dear me,’ said Mr. Kenwigs; ‘it’s not impossible. Perhaps, in that case, we’d better send up and ask if he won’t take a little more punch.’\n\n‘Kenwigs!’ said Mr. Lillyvick, in a loud voice, ‘I’m surprised at you.’\n\n‘What’s the matter, sir?’ asked Mr. Kenwigs, with becoming submission to the collector of water-rates.\n\n‘Making such a remark as that, sir,’ replied Mr. Lillyvick, angrily. ‘He has had punch already, has he not, sir? I consider the way in which that punch was cut off, if I may use the expression, highly disrespectful to this company; scandalous, perfectly scandalous. It may be the custom to allow such things in this house, but it’s not the kind of behaviour that I’ve been used to see displayed, and so I don’t mind telling you, Kenwigs. A gentleman has a glass of punch before him to which he is just about to set his lips, when another gentleman comes and collars that glass of punch, without a “with your leave”, or “by your leave”, and carries that glass of punch away. This may be good manners--I dare say it is--but I don’t understand it, that’s all; and what’s more, I don’t care if I never do. It’s my way to speak my mind, Kenwigs, and that is my mind; and if you don’t like it, it’s past my regular time for going to bed, and I can find my way home without making it later.’\n\nHere was an untoward event! The collector had sat swelling and fuming in offended dignity for some minutes, and had now fairly burst out. The great man--the rich relation--the unmarried uncle--who had it in his power to make Morleena an heiress, and the very baby a legatee--was offended. Gracious Powers, where was this to end!\n\n‘I am very sorry, sir,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, humbly.\n\n‘Don’t tell me you’re sorry,’ retorted Mr. Lillyvick, with much sharpness. ‘You should have prevented it, then.’\n\nThe company were quite paralysed by this domestic crash. The back-parlour sat with her mouth wide open, staring vacantly at the collector, in a stupor of dismay; the other guests were scarcely less overpowered by the great man’s irritation. Mr. Kenwigs, not being skilful in such matters, only fanned the flame in attempting to extinguish it.\n\n‘I didn’t think of it, I am sure, sir,’ said that gentleman. ‘I didn’t suppose that such a little thing as a glass of punch would have put you out of temper.’\n\n‘Out of temper! What the devil do you mean by that piece of impertinence, Mr. Kenwigs?’ said the collector. ‘Morleena, child--give me my hat.’\n\n‘Oh, you’re not going, Mr. Lillyvick, sir,’ interposed Miss Petowker, with her most bewitching smile.\n\nBut still Mr. Lillyvick, regardless of the siren, cried obdurately, ‘Morleena, my hat!’ upon the fourth repetition of which demand, Mrs Kenwigs sunk back in her chair, with a cry that might have softened a water-butt, not to say a water-collector; while the four little girls (privately instructed to that effect) clasped their uncle’s drab shorts in their arms, and prayed him, in imperfect English, to remain.\n\n‘Why should I stop here, my dears?’ said Mr. Lillyvick; ‘I’m not wanted here.’\n\n‘Oh, do not speak so cruelly, uncle,’ sobbed Mrs. Kenwigs, ‘unless you wish to kill me.’\n\n‘I shouldn’t wonder if some people were to say I did,’ replied Mr Lillyvick, glancing angrily at Kenwigs. ‘Out of temper!’\n\n‘Oh! I cannot bear to see him look so, at my husband,’ cried Mrs Kenwigs. ‘It’s so dreadful in families. Oh!’\n\n‘Mr. Lillyvick,’ said Kenwigs, ‘I hope, for the sake of your niece, that you won’t object to be reconciled.’\n\nThe collector’s features relaxed, as the company added their entreaties to those of his nephew-in-law. He gave up his hat, and held out his hand.\n\n‘There, Kenwigs,’ said Mr. Lillyvick; ‘and let me tell you, at the same time, to show you how much out of temper I was, that if I had gone away without another word, it would have made no difference respecting that pound or two which I shall leave among your children when I die.’\n\n‘Morleena Kenwigs,’ cried her mother, in a torrent of affection. ‘Go down upon your knees to your dear uncle, and beg him to love you all his life through, for he’s more a angel than a man, and I’ve always said so.’\n\nMiss Morleena approaching to do homage, in compliance with this injunction, was summarily caught up and kissed by Mr. Lillyvick; and thereupon Mrs. Kenwigs darted forward and kissed the collector, and an irrepressible murmur of applause broke from the company who had witnessed his magnanimity.\n\nThe worthy gentleman then became once more the life and soul of the society; being again reinstated in his old post of lion, from which high station the temporary distraction of their thoughts had for a moment dispossessed him. Quadruped lions are said to be savage, only when they are hungry; biped lions are rarely sulky longer than when their appetite for distinction remains unappeased. Mr. Lillyvick stood higher than ever; for he had shown his power; hinted at his property and testamentary intentions; gained great credit for disinterestedness and virtue; and, in addition to all, was finally accommodated with a much larger tumbler of punch than that which Newman Noggs had so feloniously made off with.\n\n‘I say! I beg everybody’s pardon for intruding again,’ said Crowl, looking in at this happy juncture; ‘but what a queer business this is, isn’t it? Noggs has lived in this house, now going on for five years, and nobody has ever been to see him before, within the memory of the oldest inhabitant.’\n\n‘It’s a strange time of night to be called away, sir, certainly,’ said the collector; ‘and the behaviour of Mr. Noggs himself, is, to say the least of it, mysterious.’\n\n‘Well, so it is,’ rejoined Crowl; ‘and I’ll tell you what’s more--I think these two geniuses, whoever they are, have run away from somewhere.’\n\n‘What makes you think that, sir?’ demanded the collector, who seemed, by a tacit understanding, to have been chosen and elected mouthpiece to the company. ‘You have no reason to suppose that they have run away from anywhere without paying the rates and taxes due, I hope?’\n\nMr. Crowl, with a look of some contempt, was about to enter a general protest against the payment of rates or taxes, under any circumstances, when he was checked by a timely whisper from Kenwigs, and several frowns and winks from Mrs. K., which providentially stopped him.\n\n‘Why the fact is,’ said Crowl, who had been listening at Newman’s door with all his might and main; ‘the fact is, that they have been talking so loud, that they quite disturbed me in my room, and so I couldn’t help catching a word here, and a word there; and all I heard, certainly seemed to refer to their having bolted from some place or other. I don’t wish to alarm Mrs. Kenwigs; but I hope they haven’t come from any jail or hospital, and brought away a fever or some unpleasantness of that sort, which might be catching for the children.’\n\nMrs. Kenwigs was so overpowered by this supposition, that it needed all the tender attentions of Miss Petowker, of the Theatre Royal, Drury Lane, to restore her to anything like a state of calmness; not to mention the assiduity of Mr. Kenwigs, who held a fat smelling-bottle to his lady’s nose, until it became matter of some doubt whether the tears which coursed down her face were the result of feelings or SAL VOLATILE.\n\nThe ladies, having expressed their sympathy, singly and separately, fell, according to custom, into a little chorus of soothing expressions, among which, such condolences as ‘Poor dear!’--‘I should feel just the same, if I was her’--‘To be sure, it’s a very trying thing’--and ‘Nobody but a mother knows what a mother’s feelings is,’ were among the most prominent, and most frequently repeated. In short, the opinion of the company was so clearly manifested, that Mr. Kenwigs was on the point of repairing to Mr. Noggs’s room, to demand an explanation, and had indeed swallowed a preparatory glass of punch, with great inflexibility and steadiness of purpose, when the attention of all present was diverted by a new and terrible surprise.\n\nThis was nothing less than the sudden pouring forth of a rapid succession of the shrillest and most piercing screams, from an upper story; and to all appearance from the very two-pair back, in which the infant Kenwigs was at that moment enshrined. They were no sooner audible, than Mrs. Kenwigs, opining that a strange cat had come in, and sucked the baby’s breath while the girl was asleep, made for the door, wringing her hands, and shrieking dismally; to the great consternation and confusion of the company.\n\n‘Mr. Kenwigs, see what it is; make haste!’ cried the sister, laying violent hands upon Mrs. Kenwigs, and holding her back by force. ‘Oh don’t twist about so, dear, or I can never hold you.’\n\n‘My baby, my blessed, blessed, blessed, blessed baby!’ screamed Mrs Kenwigs, making every blessed louder than the last. ‘My own darling, sweet, innocent Lillyvick--Oh let me go to him. Let me go-o-o-o!’\n\nPending the utterance of these frantic cries, and the wails and lamentations of the four little girls, Mr. Kenwigs rushed upstairs to the room whence the sounds proceeded; at the door of which, he encountered Nicholas, with the child in his arms, who darted out with such violence, that the anxious father was thrown down six stairs, and alighted on the nearest landing-place, before he had found time to open his mouth to ask what was the matter.\n\n‘Don’t be alarmed,’ cried Nicholas, running down; ‘here it is; it’s all out, it’s all over; pray compose yourselves; there’s no harm done;’ and with these, and a thousand other assurances, he delivered the baby (whom, in his hurry, he had carried upside down), to Mrs. Kenwigs, and ran back to assist Mr. Kenwigs, who was rubbing his head very hard, and looking much bewildered by his tumble.\n\nReassured by this cheering intelligence, the company in some degree recovered from their fears, which had been productive of some most singular instances of a total want of presence of mind; thus, the bachelor friend had, for a long time, supported in his arms Mrs Kenwigs’s sister, instead of Mrs. Kenwigs; and the worthy Mr. Lillyvick had been actually seen, in the perturbation of his spirits, to kiss Miss Petowker several times, behind the room-door, as calmly as if nothing distressing were going forward.\n\n‘It is a mere nothing,’ said Nicholas, returning to Mrs. Kenwigs; ‘the little girl, who was watching the child, being tired I suppose, fell asleep, and set her hair on fire.’\n\n‘Oh you malicious little wretch!’ cried Mrs. Kenwigs, impressively shaking her forefinger at the small unfortunate, who might be thirteen years old, and was looking on with a singed head and a frightened face.\n\n‘I heard her cries,’ continued Nicholas, ‘and ran down, in time to prevent her setting fire to anything else. You may depend upon it that the child is not hurt; for I took it off the bed myself, and brought it here to convince you.’\n\nThis brief explanation over, the infant, who, as he was christened after the collector! rejoiced in the names of Lillyvick Kenwigs, was partially suffocated under the caresses of the audience, and squeezed to his mother’s bosom, until he roared again. The attention of the company was then directed, by a natural transition, to the little girl who had had the audacity to burn her hair off, and who, after receiving sundry small slaps and pushes from the more energetic of the ladies, was mercifully sent home: the ninepence, with which she was to have been rewarded, being escheated to the Kenwigs family.\n\n‘And whatever we are to say to you, sir,’ exclaimed Mrs. Kenwigs, addressing young Lillyvick’s deliverer, ‘I am sure I don’t know.’\n\n‘You need say nothing at all,’ replied Nicholas. ‘I have done nothing to found any very strong claim upon your eloquence, I am sure.’\n\n‘He might have been burnt to death, if it hadn’t been for you, sir,’ simpered Miss Petowker.\n\n‘Not very likely, I think,’ replied Nicholas; ‘for there was abundance of assistance here, which must have reached him before he had been in any danger.’\n\n‘You will let us drink your health, anyvays, sir!’ said Mr. Kenwigs motioning towards the table.\n\n‘--In my absence, by all means,’ rejoined Nicholas, with a smile. ‘I have had a very fatiguing journey, and should be most indifferent company--a far greater check upon your merriment, than a promoter of it, even if I kept awake, which I think very doubtful. If you will allow me, I’ll return to my friend, Mr. Noggs, who went upstairs again, when he found nothing serious had occurred. Good-night.’\n\nExcusing himself, in these terms, from joining in the festivities, Nicholas took a most winning farewell of Mrs. Kenwigs and the other ladies, and retired, after making a very extraordinary impression upon the company.\n\n‘What a delightful young man!’ cried Mrs. Kenwigs.\n\n‘Uncommon gentlemanly, really,’ said Mr. Kenwigs. ‘Don’t you think so, Mr Lillyvick?’\n\n‘Yes,’ said the collector, with a dubious shrug of his shoulders, ‘He is gentlemanly, very gentlemanly--in appearance.’\n\n‘I hope you don’t see anything against him, uncle?’ inquired Mrs Kenwigs.\n\n‘No, my dear,’ replied the collector, ‘no. I trust he may not turn out--well--no matter--my love to you, my dear, and long life to the baby!’\n\n‘Your namesake,’ said Mrs. Kenwigs, with a sweet smile.\n\n‘And I hope a worthy namesake,’ observed Mr. Kenwigs, willing to propitiate the collector. ‘I hope a baby as will never disgrace his godfather, and as may be considered, in arter years, of a piece with the Lillyvicks whose name he bears. I do say--and Mrs. Kenwigs is of the same sentiment, and feels it as strong as I do--that I consider his being called Lillyvick one of the greatest blessings and Honours of my existence.’\n\n‘THE greatest blessing, Kenwigs,’ murmured his lady.\n\n‘THE greatest blessing,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, correcting himself. ‘A blessing that I hope, one of these days, I may be able to deserve.’\n\nThis was a politic stroke of the Kenwigses, because it made Mr. Lillyvick the great head and fountain of the baby’s importance. The good gentleman felt the delicacy and dexterity of the touch, and at once proposed the health of the gentleman, name unknown, who had signalised himself, that night, by his coolness and alacrity.\n\n‘Who, I don’t mind saying,’ observed Mr. Lillyvick, as a great concession, ‘is a good-looking young man enough, with manners that I hope his character may be equal to.’\n\n‘He has a very nice face and style, really,’ said Mrs. Kenwigs.\n\n‘He certainly has,’ added Miss Petowker. ‘There’s something in his appearance quite--dear, dear, what’s that word again?’\n\n‘What word?’ inquired Mr. Lillyvick.\n\n‘Why--dear me, how stupid I am,’ replied Miss Petowker, hesitating. ‘What do you call it, when Lords break off door-knockers and beat policemen, and play at coaches with other people’s money, and all that sort of thing?’\n\n‘Aristocratic?’ suggested the collector.\n\n‘Ah! aristocratic,’ replied Miss Petowker; ‘something very aristocratic about him, isn’t there?’\n\nThe gentleman held their peace, and smiled at each other, as who should say, ‘Well! there’s no accounting for tastes;’ but the ladies resolved unanimously that Nicholas had an aristocratic air; and nobody caring to dispute the position, it was established triumphantly.\n\nThe punch being, by this time, drunk out, and the little Kenwigses (who had for some time previously held their little eyes open with their little forefingers) becoming fractious, and requesting rather urgently to be put to bed, the collector made a move by pulling out his watch, and acquainting the company that it was nigh two o’clock; whereat some of the guests were surprised and others shocked, and hats and bonnets being groped for under the tables, and in course of time found, their owners went away, after a vast deal of shaking of hands, and many remarks how they had never spent such a delightful evening, and how they marvelled to find it so late, expecting to have heard that it was half-past ten at the very latest, and how they wished that Mr. and Mrs Kenwigs had a wedding-day once a week, and how they wondered by what hidden agency Mrs. Kenwigs could possibly have managed so well; and a great deal more of the same kind. To all of which flattering expressions, Mr. and Mrs. Kenwigs replied, by thanking every lady and gentleman, SERIATIM, for the favour of their company, and hoping they might have enjoyed themselves only half as well as they said they had.\n\nAs to Nicholas, quite unconscious of the impression he had produced, he had long since fallen asleep, leaving Mr. Newman Noggs and Smike to empty the spirit bottle between them; and this office they performed with such extreme good-will, that Newman was equally at a loss to determine whether he himself was quite sober, and whether he had ever seen any gentleman so heavily, drowsily, and completely intoxicated as his new acquaintance.\n\n# CHAPTER 16\n\nNicholas seeks to employ himself in a New Capacity, and being unsuccessful, accepts an engagement as Tutor in a Private Family\nThe first care of Nicholas, next morning, was, to look after some room in which, until better times dawned upon him, he could contrive to exist, without trenching upon the hospitality of Newman Noggs, who would have slept upon the stairs with pleasure, so that his young friend was accommodated.\n\nThe vacant apartment to which the bill in the parlour window bore reference, appeared, on inquiry, to be a small back-room on the second floor, reclaimed from the leads, and overlooking a soot-bespeckled prospect of tiles and chimney-pots. For the letting of this portion of the house from week to week, on reasonable terms, the parlour lodger was empowered to treat; he being deputed by the landlord to dispose of the rooms as they became vacant, and to keep a sharp look-out that the lodgers didn’t run away. As a means of securing the punctual discharge of which last service he was permitted to live rent-free, lest he should at any time be tempted to run away himself.\n\nOf this chamber, Nicholas became the tenant; and having hired a few common articles of furniture from a neighbouring broker, and paid the first week’s hire in advance, out of a small fund raised by the conversion of some spare clothes into ready money, he sat himself down to ruminate upon his prospects, which, like the prospect outside his window, were sufficiently confined and dingy. As they by no means improved on better acquaintance, and as familiarity breeds contempt, he resolved to banish them from his thoughts by dint of hard walking. So, taking up his hat, and leaving poor Smike to arrange and rearrange the room with as much delight as if it had been the costliest palace, he betook himself to the streets, and mingled with the crowd which thronged them.\n\nAlthough a man may lose a sense of his own importance when he is a mere unit among a busy throng, all utterly regardless of him, it by no means follows that he can dispossess himself, with equal facility, of a very strong sense of the importance and magnitude of his cares. The unhappy state of his own affairs was the one idea which occupied the brain of Nicholas, walk as fast as he would; and when he tried to dislodge it by speculating on the situation and prospects of the people who surrounded him, he caught himself, in a few seconds, contrasting their condition with his own, and gliding almost imperceptibly back into his old train of thought again.\n\nOccupied in these reflections, as he was making his way along one of the great public thoroughfares of London, he chanced to raise his eyes to a blue board, whereon was inscribed, in characters of gold, ‘General Agency Office; for places and situations of all kinds inquire within.’ It was a shop-front, fitted up with a gauze blind and an inner door; and in the window hung a long and tempting array of written placards, announcing vacant places of every grade, from a secretary’s to a foot-boy’s.\n\nNicholas halted, instinctively, before this temple of promise, and ran his eye over the capital-text openings in life which were so profusely displayed. When he had completed his survey he walked on a little way, and then back, and then on again; at length, after pausing irresolutely several times before the door of the General Agency Office, he made up his mind, and stepped in.\n\nHe found himself in a little floor-clothed room, with a high desk railed off in one corner, behind which sat a lean youth with cunning eyes and a protruding chin, whose performances in capital-text darkened the window. He had a thick ledger lying open before him, and with the fingers of his right hand inserted between the leaves, and his eyes fixed on a very fat old lady in a mob-cap--evidently the proprietress of the establishment--who was airing herself at the fire, seemed to be only waiting her directions to refer to some entries contained within its rusty clasps.\n\nAs there was a board outside, which acquainted the public that servants-of-all-work were perpetually in waiting to be hired from ten till four, Nicholas knew at once that some half-dozen strong young women, each with pattens and an umbrella, who were sitting upon a form in one corner, were in attendance for that purpose: especially as the poor things looked anxious and weary. He was not quite so certain of the callings and stations of two smart young ladies who were in conversation with the fat lady before the fire, until--having sat himself down in a corner, and remarked that he would wait until the other customers had been served--the fat lady resumed the dialogue which his entrance had interrupted.\n\n‘Cook, Tom,’ said the fat lady, still airing herself as aforesaid.\n\n‘Cook,’ said Tom, turning over some leaves of the ledger. ‘Well!’\n\n‘Read out an easy place or two,’ said the fat lady.\n\n‘Pick out very light ones, if you please, young man,’ interposed a genteel female, in shepherd’s-plaid boots, who appeared to be the client.\n\n‘“Mrs. Marker,”’ said Tom, reading, ‘“Russell Place, Russell Square; offers eighteen guineas; tea and sugar found. Two in family, and see very little company. Five servants kept. No man. No followers.”’\n\n‘Oh Lor!’ tittered the client. ‘THAT won’t do. Read another, young man, will you?’\n\n‘“Mrs. Wrymug,”’ said Tom, ‘“Pleasant Place, Finsbury. Wages, twelve guineas. No tea, no sugar. Serious family--“’\n\n‘Ah! you needn’t mind reading that,’ interrupted the client.\n\n‘“Three serious footmen,”’ said Tom, impressively.\n\n‘Three? did you say?’ asked the client in an altered tone.\n\n‘Three serious footmen,’ replied Tom. ‘“Cook, housemaid, and nursemaid; each female servant required to join the Little Bethel Congregation three times every Sunday--with a serious footman. If the cook is more serious than the footman, she will be expected to improve the footman; if the footman is more serious than the cook, he will be expected to improve the cook.”’\n\n‘I’ll take the address of that place,’ said the client; ‘I don’t know but what it mightn’t suit me pretty well.’\n\n‘Here’s another,’ remarked Tom, turning over the leaves. ‘“Family of Mr Gallanbile, MP. Fifteen guineas, tea and sugar, and servants allowed to see male cousins, if godly. Note. Cold dinner in the kitchen on the Sabbath, Mr. Gallanbile being devoted to the Observance question. No victuals whatever cooked on the Lord’s Day, with the exception of dinner for Mr. and Mrs. Gallanbile, which, being a work of piety and necessity, is exempted. Mr. Gallanbile dines late on the day of rest, in order to prevent the sinfulness of the cook’s dressing herself.”’\n\n‘I don’t think that’ll answer as well as the other,’ said the client, after a little whispering with her friend. ‘I’ll take the other direction, if you please, young man. I can but come back again, if it don’t do.’\n\nTom made out the address, as requested, and the genteel client, having satisfied the fat lady with a small fee, meanwhile, went away accompanied by her friend.\n\nAs Nicholas opened his mouth, to request the young man to turn to letter S, and let him know what secretaryships remained undisposed of, there came into the office an applicant, in whose favour he immediately retired, and whose appearance both surprised and interested him.\n\nThis was a young lady who could be scarcely eighteen, of very slight and delicate figure, but exquisitely shaped, who, walking timidly up to the desk, made an inquiry, in a very low tone of voice, relative to some situation as governess, or companion to a lady. She raised her veil, for an instant, while she preferred the inquiry, and disclosed a countenance of most uncommon beauty, though shaded by a cloud of sadness, which, in one so young, was doubly remarkable. Having received a card of reference to some person on the books, she made the usual acknowledgment, and glided away.\n\nShe was neatly, but very quietly attired; so much so, indeed, that it seemed as though her dress, if it had been worn by one who imparted fewer graces of her own to it, might have looked poor and shabby. Her attendant--for she had one--was a red-faced, round-eyed, slovenly girl, who, from a certain roughness about the bare arms that peeped from under her draggled shawl, and the half-washed-out traces of smut and blacklead which tattooed her countenance, was clearly of a kin with the servants-of-all-work on the form: between whom and herself there had passed various grins and glances, indicative of the freemasonry of the craft.\n\nThis girl followed her mistress; and, before Nicholas had recovered from the first effects of his surprise and admiration, the young lady was gone. It is not a matter of such complete and utter improbability as some sober people may think, that he would have followed them out, had he not been restrained by what passed between the fat lady and her book-keeper.\n\n‘When is she coming again, Tom?’ asked the fat lady.\n\n‘Tomorrow morning,’ replied Tom, mending his pen.\n\n‘Where have you sent her to?’ asked the fat lady.\n\n‘Mrs. Clark’s,’ replied Tom.\n\n‘She’ll have a nice life of it, if she goes there,’ observed the fat lady, taking a pinch of snuff from a tin box.\n\nTom made no other reply than thrusting his tongue into his cheek, and pointing the feather of his pen towards Nicholas--reminders which elicited from the fat lady an inquiry, of ‘Now, sir, what can we do for YOU?’\n\nNicholas briefly replied, that he wanted to know whether there was any such post to be had, as secretary or amanuensis to a gentleman.\n\n‘Any such!’ rejoined the mistress; ‘a-dozen-such. An’t there, Tom?’\n\n‘I should think so,’ answered that young gentleman; and as he said it, he winked towards Nicholas, with a degree of familiarity which he, no doubt, intended for a rather flattering compliment, but with which Nicholas was most ungratefully disgusted.\n\nUpon reference to the book, it appeared that the dozen secretaryships had dwindled down to one. Mr. Gregsbury, the great member of parliament, of Manchester Buildings, Westminster, wanted a young man, to keep his papers and correspondence in order; and Nicholas was exactly the sort of young man that Mr. Gregsbury wanted.\n\n‘I don’t know what the terms are, as he said he’d settle them himself with the party,’ observed the fat lady; ‘but they must be pretty good ones, because he’s a member of parliament.’\n\nInexperienced as he was, Nicholas did not feel quite assured of the force of this reasoning, or the justice of this conclusion; but without troubling himself to question it, he took down the address, and resolved to wait upon Mr. Gregsbury without delay.\n\n‘I don’t know what the number is,’ said Tom; ‘but Manchester Buildings isn’t a large place; and if the worst comes to the worst it won’t take you very long to knock at all the doors on both sides of the way till you find him out. I say, what a good-looking gal that was, wasn’t she?’\n\n‘What girl?’ demanded Nicholas, sternly.\n\n‘Oh yes. I know--what gal, eh?’ whispered Tom, shutting one eye, and cocking his chin in the air. ‘You didn’t see her, you didn’t--I say, don’t you wish you was me, when she comes tomorrow morning?’\n\nNicholas looked at the ugly clerk, as if he had a mind to reward his admiration of the young lady by beating the ledger about his ears, but he refrained, and strode haughtily out of the office; setting at defiance, in his indignation, those ancient laws of chivalry, which not only made it proper and lawful for all good knights to hear the praise of the ladies to whom they were devoted, but rendered it incumbent upon them to roam about the world, and knock at head all such matter-of-fact and un-poetical characters, as declined to exalt, above all the earth, damsels whom they had never chanced to look upon or hear of--as if that were any excuse!\n\nThinking no longer of his own misfortunes, but wondering what could be those of the beautiful girl he had seen, Nicholas, with many wrong turns, and many inquiries, and almost as many misdirections, bent his steps towards the place whither he had been directed.\n\nWithin the precincts of the ancient city of Westminster, and within half a quarter of a mile of its ancient sanctuary, is a narrow and dirty region, the sanctuary of the smaller members of Parliament in modern days. It is all comprised in one street of gloomy lodging-houses, from whose windows, in vacation-time, there frown long melancholy rows of bills, which say, as plainly as did the countenances of their occupiers, ranged on ministerial and opposition benches in the session which slumbers with its fathers, ‘To Let’, ‘To Let’. In busier periods of the year these bills disappear, and the houses swarm with legislators. There are legislators in the parlours, in the first floor, in the second, in the third, in the garrets; the small apartments reek with the breath of deputations and delegates. In damp weather, the place is rendered close, by the steams of moist acts of parliament and frouzy petitions; general postmen grow faint as they enter its infected limits, and shabby figures in quest of franks, flit restlessly to and fro like the troubled ghosts of Complete Letter-writers departed. This is Manchester Buildings; and here, at all hours of the night, may be heard the rattling of latch-keys in their respective keyholes: with now and then--when a gust of wind sweeping across the water which washes the Buildings’ feet, impels the sound towards its entrance--the weak, shrill voice of some young member practising tomorrow’s speech. All the livelong day, there is a grinding of organs and clashing and clanging of little boxes of music; for Manchester Buildings is an eel-pot, which has no outlet but its awkward mouth--a case-bottle which has no thoroughfare, and a short and narrow neck--and in this respect it may be typical of the fate of some few among its more adventurous residents, who, after wriggling themselves into Parliament by violent efforts and contortions, find that it, too, is no thoroughfare for them; that, like Manchester Buildings, it leads to nothing beyond itself; and that they are fain at last to back out, no wiser, no richer, not one whit more famous, than they went in.\n\nInto Manchester Buildings Nicholas turned, with the address of the great Mr. Gregsbury in his hand. As there was a stream of people pouring into a shabby house not far from the entrance, he waited until they had made their way in, and then making up to the servant, ventured to inquire if he knew where Mr. Gregsbury lived.\n\nThe servant was a very pale, shabby boy, who looked as if he had slept underground from his infancy, as very likely he had. ‘Mr. Gregsbury?’ said he; ‘Mr. Gregsbury lodges here. It’s all right. Come in!’\n\nNicholas thought he might as well get in while he could, so in he walked; and he had no sooner done so, than the boy shut the door, and made off.\n\nThis was odd enough: but what was more embarrassing was, that all along the passage, and all along the narrow stairs, blocking up the window, and making the dark entry darker still, was a confused crowd of persons with great importance depicted in their looks; who were, to all appearance, waiting in silent expectation of some coming event. From time to time, one man would whisper to his neighbour, or a little group would whisper together, and then the whisperers would nod fiercely to each other, or give their heads a relentless shake, as if they were bent upon doing something very desperate, and were determined not to be put off, whatever happened.\n\nAs a few minutes elapsed without anything occurring to explain this phenomenon, and as he felt his own position a peculiarly uncomfortable one, Nicholas was on the point of seeking some information from the man next him, when a sudden move was visible on the stairs, and a voice was heard to cry, ‘Now, gentleman, have the goodness to walk up!’\n\nSo far from walking up, the gentlemen on the stairs began to walk down with great alacrity, and to entreat, with extraordinary politeness, that the gentlemen nearest the street would go first; the gentlemen nearest the street retorted, with equal courtesy, that they couldn’t think of such a thing on any account; but they did it, without thinking of it, inasmuch as the other gentlemen pressing some half-dozen (among whom was Nicholas) forward, and closing up behind, pushed them, not merely up the stairs, but into the very sitting-room of Mr. Gregsbury, which they were thus compelled to enter with most unseemly precipitation, and without the means of retreat; the press behind them, more than filling the apartment.\n\n‘Gentlemen,’ said Mr. Gregsbury, ‘you are welcome. I am rejoiced to see you.’\n\nFor a gentleman who was rejoiced to see a body of visitors, Mr. Gregsbury looked as uncomfortable as might be; but perhaps this was occasioned by senatorial gravity, and a statesmanlike habit of keeping his feelings under control. He was a tough, burly, thick-headed gentleman, with a loud voice, a pompous manner, a tolerable command of sentences with no meaning in them, and, in short, every requisite for a very good member indeed.\n\n‘Now, gentlemen,’ said Mr. Gregsbury, tossing a great bundle of papers into a wicker basket at his feet, and throwing himself back in his chair with his arms over the elbows, ‘you are dissatisfied with my conduct, I see by the newspapers.’\n\n‘Yes, Mr. Gregsbury, we are,’ said a plump old gentleman in a violent heat, bursting out of the throng, and planting himself in the front.\n\n‘Do my eyes deceive me,’ said Mr. Gregsbury, looking towards the speaker, ‘or is that my old friend Pugstyles?’\n\n‘I am that man, and no other, sir,’ replied the plump old gentleman.\n\n‘Give me your hand, my worthy friend,’ said Mr. Gregsbury. ‘Pugstyles, my dear friend, I am very sorry to see you here.’\n\n‘I am very sorry to be here, sir,’ said Mr. Pugstyles; ‘but your conduct, Mr. Gregsbury, has rendered this deputation from your constituents imperatively necessary.’\n\n‘My conduct, Pugstyles,’ said Mr. Gregsbury, looking round upon the deputation with gracious magnanimity--‘my conduct has been, and ever will be, regulated by a sincere regard for the true and real interests of this great and happy country. Whether I look at home, or abroad; whether I behold the peaceful industrious communities of our island home: her rivers covered with steamboats, her roads with locomotives, her streets with cabs, her skies with balloons of a power and magnitude hitherto unknown in the history of aeronautics in this or any other nation--I say, whether I look merely at home, or, stretching my eyes farther, contemplate the boundless prospect of conquest and possession--achieved by British perseverance and British valour--which is outspread before me, I clasp my hands, and turning my eyes to the broad expanse above my head, exclaim, “Thank Heaven, I am a Briton!”’\n\nThe time had been, when this burst of enthusiasm would have been cheered to the very echo; but now, the deputation received it with chilling coldness. The general impression seemed to be, that as an explanation of Mr. Gregsbury’s political conduct, it did not enter quite enough into detail; and one gentleman in the rear did not scruple to remark aloud, that, for his purpose, it savoured rather too much of a ‘gammon’ tendency.\n\n‘The meaning of that term--gammon,’ said Mr. Gregsbury, ‘is unknown to me. If it means that I grow a little too fervid, or perhaps even hyperbolical, in extolling my native land, I admit the full justice of the remark. I AM proud of this free and happy country. My form dilates, my eye glistens, my breast heaves, my heart swells, my bosom burns, when I call to mind her greatness and her glory.’\n\n‘We wish, sir,’ remarked Mr. Pugstyles, calmly, ‘to ask you a few questions.’\n\n‘If you please, gentlemen; my time is yours--and my country’s--and my country’s--’ said Mr. Gregsbury.\n\nThis permission being conceded, Mr. Pugstyles put on his spectacles, and referred to a written paper which he drew from his pocket; whereupon nearly every other member of the deputation pulled a written paper from HIS pocket, to check Mr. Pugstyles off, as he read the questions.\n\nThis done, Mr. Pugstyles proceeded to business.\n\n‘Question number one.--Whether, sir, you did not give a voluntary pledge previous to your election, that in event of your being returned, you would immediately put down the practice of coughing and groaning in the House of Commons. And whether you did not submit to be coughed and groaned down in the very first debate of the session, and have since made no effort to effect a reform in this respect? Whether you did not also pledge yourself to astonish the government, and make them shrink in their shoes? And whether you have astonished them, and made them shrink in their shoes, or not?’\n\n‘Go on to the next one, my dear Pugstyles,’ said Mr. Gregsbury.\n\n‘Have you any explanation to offer with reference to that question, sir?’ asked Mr. Pugstyles.\n\n‘Certainly not,’ said Mr. Gregsbury.\n\nThe members of the deputation looked fiercely at each other, and afterwards at the member. ‘Dear Pugstyles’ having taken a very long stare at Mr. Gregsbury over the tops of his spectacles, resumed his list of inquiries.\n\n‘Question number two.--Whether, sir, you did not likewise give a voluntary pledge that you would support your colleague on every occasion; and whether you did not, the night before last, desert him and vote upon the other side, because the wife of a leader on that other side had invited Mrs. Gregsbury to an evening party?’\n\n‘Go on,’ said Mr. Gregsbury.\n\n‘Nothing to say on that, either, sir?’ asked the spokesman.\n\n‘Nothing whatever,’ replied Mr. Gregsbury. The deputation, who had only seen him at canvassing or election time, were struck dumb by his coolness. He didn’t appear like the same man; then he was all milk and honey; now he was all starch and vinegar. But men ARE so different at different times!\n\n‘Question number three--and last,’ said Mr. Pugstyles, emphatically. ‘Whether, sir, you did not state upon the hustings, that it was your firm and determined intention to oppose everything proposed; to divide the house upon every question, to move for returns on every subject, to place a motion on the books every day, and, in short, in your own memorable words, to play the very devil with everything and everybody?’ With this comprehensive inquiry, Mr. Pugstyles folded up his list of questions, as did all his backers.\n\nMr. Gregsbury reflected, blew his nose, threw himself further back in his chair, came forward again, leaning his elbows on the table, made a triangle with his two thumbs and his two forefingers, and tapping his nose with the apex thereof, replied (smiling as he said it), ‘I deny everything.’\n\nAt this unexpected answer, a hoarse murmur arose from the deputation; and the same gentleman who had expressed an opinion relative to the gammoning nature of the introductory speech, again made a monosyllabic demonstration, by growling out ‘Resign!’ Which growl being taken up by his fellows, swelled into a very earnest and general remonstrance.\n\n‘I am requested, sir, to express a hope,’ said Mr. Pugstyles, with a distant bow, ‘that on receiving a requisition to that effect from a great majority of your constituents, you will not object at once to resign your seat in favour of some candidate whom they think they can better trust.’\n\nTo this, Mr. Gregsbury read the following reply, which, anticipating the request, he had composed in the form of a letter, whereof copies had been made to send round to the newspapers.\n\n‘Next to the welfare of our beloved island--this great and free and happy country, whose powers and resources are, I sincerely believe, illimitable--I value that noble independence which is an Englishman’s proudest boast, and which I fondly hope to bequeath to my children, untarnished and unsullied. Actuated by no personal motives, but moved only by high and great constitutional considerations; which I will not attempt to explain, for they are really beneath the comprehension of those who have not made themselves masters, as I have, of the intricate and arduous study of politics; I would rather keep my seat, and intend doing so.\n\n‘Will you do me the favour to present my compliments to the constituent body, and acquaint them with this circumstance?\n\n‘With great esteem, ‘My dear Mr. Pugstyles, ‘&c.&c.’\n\n‘Then you will not resign, under any circumstances?’ asked the spokesman.\n\nMr. Gregsbury smiled, and shook his head.\n\n‘Then, good-morning, sir,’ said Pugstyles, angrily.\n\n‘Heaven bless you!’ said Mr. Gregsbury. And the deputation, with many growls and scowls, filed off as quickly as the narrowness of the staircase would allow of their getting down.\n\nThe last man being gone, Mr. Gregsbury rubbed his hands and chuckled, as merry fellows will, when they think they have said or done a more than commonly good thing; he was so engrossed in this self-congratulation, that he did not observe that Nicholas had been left behind in the shadow of the window-curtains, until that young gentleman, fearing he might otherwise overhear some soliloquy intended to have no listeners, coughed twice or thrice, to attract the member’s notice.\n\n‘What’s that?’ said Mr. Gregsbury, in sharp accents.\n\nNicholas stepped forward, and bowed.\n\n‘What do you do here, sir?’ asked Mr. Gregsbury; ‘a spy upon my privacy! A concealed voter! You have heard my answer, sir. Pray follow the deputation.’\n\n‘I should have done so, if I had belonged to it, but I do not,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Then how came you here, sir?’ was the natural inquiry of Mr. Gregsbury, MP. ‘And where the devil have you come from, sir?’ was the question which followed it.\n\n‘I brought this card from the General Agency Office, sir,’ said Nicholas, ‘wishing to offer myself as your secretary, and understanding that you stood in need of one.’\n\n‘That’s all you have come for, is it?’ said Mr. Gregsbury, eyeing him in some doubt.\n\nNicholas replied in the affirmative.\n\n‘You have no connection with any of those rascally papers have you?’ said Mr. Gregsbury. ‘You didn’t get into the room, to hear what was going forward, and put it in print, eh?’\n\n‘I have no connection, I am sorry to say, with anything at present,’ rejoined Nicholas,--politely enough, but quite at his ease.\n\n‘Oh!’ said Mr. Gregsbury. ‘How did you find your way up here, then?’\n\nNicholas related how he had been forced up by the deputation.\n\n‘That was the way, was it?’ said Mr. Gregsbury. ‘Sit down.’\n\nNicholas took a chair, and Mr. Gregsbury stared at him for a long time, as if to make certain, before he asked any further questions, that there were no objections to his outward appearance.\n\n‘You want to be my secretary, do you?’ he said at length.\n\n‘I wish to be employed in that capacity, sir,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘Well,’ said Mr. Gregsbury; ‘now what can you do?’\n\n‘I suppose,’ replied Nicholas, smiling, ‘that I can do what usually falls to the lot of other secretaries.’\n\n‘What’s that?’ inquired Mr. Gregsbury.\n\n‘What is it?’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘Ah! What is it?’ retorted the member, looking shrewdly at him, with his head on one side.\n\n‘A secretary’s duties are rather difficult to define, perhaps,’ said Nicholas, considering. ‘They include, I presume, correspondence?’\n\n‘Good,’ interposed Mr. Gregsbury.\n\n‘The arrangement of papers and documents?’\n\n‘Very good.’\n\n‘Occasionally, perhaps, the writing from your dictation; and possibly, sir,’ said Nicholas, with a half-smile, ‘the copying of your speech for some public journal, when you have made one of more than usual importance.’\n\n‘Certainly,’ rejoined Mr. Gregsbury. ‘What else?’\n\n‘Really,’ said Nicholas, after a moment’s reflection, ‘I am not able, at this instant, to recapitulate any other duty of a secretary, beyond the general one of making himself as agreeable and useful to his employer as he can, consistently with his own respectability, and without overstepping that line of duties which he undertakes to perform, and which the designation of his office is usually understood to imply.’\n\nMr. Gregsbury looked fixedly at Nicholas for a short time, and then glancing warily round the room, said in a suppressed voice:\n\n‘This is all very well, Mr--what is your name?’\n\n‘Nickleby.’\n\n‘This is all very well, Mr. Nickleby, and very proper, so far as it goes--so far as it goes, but it doesn’t go far enough. There are other duties, Mr. Nickleby, which a secretary to a parliamentary gentleman must never lose sight of. I should require to be crammed, sir.’\n\n‘I beg your pardon,’ interposed Nicholas, doubtful whether he had heard aright.\n\n‘--To be crammed, sir,’ repeated Mr. Gregsbury.\n\n‘May I beg your pardon again, if I inquire what you mean, sir?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘My meaning, sir, is perfectly plain,’ replied Mr. Gregsbury with a solemn aspect. ‘My secretary would have to make himself master of the foreign policy of the world, as it is mirrored in the newspapers; to run his eye over all accounts of public meetings, all leading articles, and accounts of the proceedings of public bodies; and to make notes of anything which it appeared to him might be made a point of, in any little speech upon the question of some petition lying on the table, or anything of that kind. Do you understand?’\n\n‘I think I do, sir,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘Then,’ said Mr. Gregsbury, ‘it would be necessary for him to make himself acquainted, from day to day, with newspaper paragraphs on passing events; such as “Mysterious disappearance, and supposed suicide of a potboy,” or anything of that sort, upon which I might found a question to the Secretary of State for the Home Department. Then, he would have to copy the question, and as much as I remembered of the answer (including a little compliment about independence and good sense); and to send the manuscript in a frank to the local paper, with perhaps half-a-dozen lines of leader, to the effect, that I was always to be found in my place in parliament, and never shrunk from the responsible and arduous duties, and so forth. You see?’\n\nNicholas bowed.\n\n‘Besides which,’ continued Mr. Gregsbury, ‘I should expect him, now and then, to go through a few figures in the printed tables, and to pick out a few results, so that I might come out pretty well on timber duty questions, and finance questions, and so on; and I should like him to get up a few little arguments about the disastrous effects of a return to cash payments and a metallic currency, with a touch now and then about the exportation of bullion, and the Emperor of Russia, and bank notes, and all that kind of thing, which it’s only necessary to talk fluently about, because nobody understands it. Do you take me?’\n\n‘I think I understand,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘With regard to such questions as are not political,’ continued Mr Gregsbury, warming; ‘and which one can’t be expected to care a curse about, beyond the natural care of not allowing inferior people to be as well off as ourselves--else where are our privileges?--I should wish my secretary to get together a few little flourishing speeches, of a patriotic cast. For instance, if any preposterous bill were brought forward, for giving poor grubbing devils of authors a right to their own property, I should like to say, that I for one would never consent to opposing an insurmountable bar to the diffusion of literature among THE PEOPLE,--you understand?--that the creations of the pocket, being man’s, might belong to one man, or one family; but that the creations of the brain, being God’s, ought as a matter of course to belong to the people at large--and if I was pleasantly disposed, I should like to make a joke about posterity, and say that those who wrote for posterity should be content to be rewarded by the approbation OF posterity; it might take with the house, and could never do me any harm, because posterity can’t be expected to know anything about me or my jokes either--do you see?’\n\n‘I see that, sir,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘You must always bear in mind, in such cases as this, where our interests are not affected,’ said Mr. Gregsbury, ‘to put it very strong about the people, because it comes out very well at election-time; and you could be as funny as you liked about the authors; because I believe the greater part of them live in lodgings, and are not voters. This is a hasty outline of the chief things you’d have to do, except waiting in the lobby every night, in case I forgot anything, and should want fresh cramming; and, now and then, during great debates, sitting in the front row of the gallery, and saying to the people about--‘You see that gentleman, with his hand to his face, and his arm twisted round the pillar--that’s Mr. Gregsbury--the celebrated Mr. Gregsbury,’--with any other little eulogium that might strike you at the moment. And for salary,’ said Mr. Gregsbury, winding up with great rapidity; for he was out of breath--‘and for salary, I don’t mind saying at once in round numbers, to prevent any dissatisfaction--though it’s more than I’ve been accustomed to give--fifteen shillings a week, and find yourself. There!’\n\nWith this handsome offer, Mr. Gregsbury once more threw himself back in his chair, and looked like a man who had been most profligately liberal, but is determined not to repent of it notwithstanding.\n\n‘Fifteen shillings a week is not much,’ said Nicholas, mildly.\n\n‘Not much! Fifteen shillings a week not much, young man?’ cried Mr Gregsbury. ‘Fifteen shillings a--’\n\n‘Pray do not suppose that I quarrel with the sum, sir,’ replied Nicholas; ‘for I am not ashamed to confess, that whatever it may be in itself, to me it is a great deal. But the duties and responsibilities make the recompense small, and they are so very heavy that I fear to undertake them.’\n\n‘Do you decline to undertake them, sir?’ inquired Mr. Gregsbury, with his hand on the bell-rope.\n\n‘I fear they are too great for my powers, however good my will may be, sir,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘That is as much as to say that you had rather not accept the place, and that you consider fifteen shillings a week too little,’ said Mr Gregsbury, ringing. ‘Do you decline it, sir?’\n\n‘I have no alternative but to do so,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘Door, Matthews!’ said Mr. Gregsbury, as the boy appeared.\n\n‘I am sorry I have troubled you unnecessarily, sir,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘I am sorry you have,’ rejoined Mr. Gregsbury, turning his back upon him. ‘Door, Matthews!’\n\n‘Good-morning, sir,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Door, Matthews!’ cried Mr. Gregsbury.\n\nThe boy beckoned Nicholas, and tumbling lazily downstairs before him, opened the door, and ushered him into the street. With a sad and pensive air, he retraced his steps homewards.\n\nSmike had scraped a meal together from the remnant of last night’s supper, and was anxiously awaiting his return. The occurrences of the morning had not improved Nicholas’s appetite, and, by him, the dinner remained untasted. He was sitting in a thoughtful attitude, with the plate which the poor fellow had assiduously filled with the choicest morsels, untouched, by his side, when Newman Noggs looked into the room.\n\n‘Come back?’ asked Newman.\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Nicholas, ‘tired to death: and, what is worse, might have remained at home for all the good I have done.’\n\n‘Couldn’t expect to do much in one morning,’ said Newman.\n\n‘Maybe so, but I am sanguine, and did expect,’ said Nicholas, ‘and am proportionately disappointed.’ Saying which, he gave Newman an account of his proceedings.\n\n‘If I could do anything,’ said Nicholas, ‘anything, however slight, until Ralph Nickleby returns, and I have eased my mind by confronting him, I should feel happier. I should think it no disgrace to work, Heaven knows. Lying indolently here, like a half-tamed sullen beast, distracts me.’\n\n‘I don’t know,’ said Newman; ‘small things offer--they would pay the rent, and more--but you wouldn’t like them; no, you could hardly be expected to undergo it--no, no.’\n\n‘What could I hardly be expected to undergo?’ asked Nicholas, raising his eyes. ‘Show me, in this wide waste of London, any honest means by which I could even defray the weekly hire of this poor room, and see if I shrink from resorting to them! Undergo! I have undergone too much, my friend, to feel pride or squeamishness now. Except--’ added Nicholas hastily, after a short silence, ‘except such squeamishness as is common honesty, and so much pride as constitutes self-respect. I see little to choose, between assistant to a brutal pedagogue, and toad-eater to a mean and ignorant upstart, be he member or no member.’\n\n‘I hardly know whether I should tell you what I heard this morning, or not,’ said Newman.\n\n‘Has it reference to what you said just now?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘It has.’\n\n‘Then in Heaven’s name, my good friend, tell it me,’ said Nicholas. ‘For God’s sake consider my deplorable condition; and, while I promise to take no step without taking counsel with you, give me, at least, a vote in my own behalf.’\n\nMoved by this entreaty, Newman stammered forth a variety of most unaccountable and entangled sentences, the upshot of which was, that Mrs. Kenwigs had examined him, at great length that morning, touching the origin of his acquaintance with, and the whole life, adventures, and pedigree of, Nicholas; that Newman had parried these questions as long as he could, but being, at length, hard pressed and driven into a corner, had gone so far as to admit, that Nicholas was a tutor of great accomplishments, involved in some misfortunes which he was not at liberty to explain, and bearing the name of Johnson. That Mrs. Kenwigs, impelled by gratitude, or ambition, or maternal pride, or maternal love, or all four powerful motives conjointly, had taken secret conference with Mr. Kenwigs, and had finally returned to propose that Mr. Johnson should instruct the four Miss Kenwigses in the French language as spoken by natives, at the weekly stipend of five shillings, current coin of the realm; being at the rate of one shilling per week, per each Miss Kenwigs, and one shilling over, until such time as the baby might be able to take it out in grammar.\n\n‘Which, unless I am very much mistaken,’ observed Mrs. Kenwigs in making the proposition, ‘will not be very long; for such clever children, Mr Noggs, never were born into this world, I do believe.’\n\n‘There,’ said Newman, ‘that’s all. It’s beneath you, I know; but I thought that perhaps you might--’\n\n‘Might!’ cried Nicholas, with great alacrity; ‘of course I shall. I accept the offer at once. Tell the worthy mother so, without delay, my dear fellow; and that I am ready to begin whenever she pleases.’\n\nNewman hastened, with joyful steps, to inform Mrs. Kenwigs of his friend’s acquiescence, and soon returning, brought back word that they would be happy to see him in the first floor as soon as convenient; that Mrs. Kenwigs had, upon the instant, sent out to secure a second-hand French grammar and dialogues, which had long been fluttering in the sixpenny box at the bookstall round the corner; and that the family, highly excited at the prospect of this addition to their gentility, wished the initiatory lesson to come off immediately.\n\nAnd here it may be observed, that Nicholas was not, in the ordinary sense of the word, a young man of high spirit. He would resent an affront to himself, or interpose to redress a wrong offered to another, as boldly and freely as any knight that ever set lance in rest; but he lacked that peculiar excess of coolness and great-minded selfishness, which invariably distinguish gentlemen of high spirit. In truth, for our own part, we are disposed to look upon such gentleman as being rather incumbrances than otherwise in rising families: happening to be acquainted with several whose spirit prevents their settling down to any grovelling occupation, and only displays itself in a tendency to cultivate moustachios, and look fierce; and although moustachios and ferocity are both very pretty things in their way, and very much to be commended, we confess to a desire to see them bred at the owner’s proper cost, rather than at the expense of low-spirited people.\n\nNicholas, therefore, not being a high-spirited young man according to common parlance, and deeming it a greater degradation to borrow, for the supply of his necessities, from Newman Noggs, than to teach French to the little Kenwigses for five shillings a week, accepted the offer with the alacrity already described, and betook himself to the first floor with all convenient speed.\n\nHere, he was received by Mrs. Kenwigs with a genteel air, kindly intended to assure him of her protection and support; and here, too, he found Mr Lillyvick and Miss Petowker; the four Miss Kenwigses on their form of audience; and the baby in a dwarf porter’s chair with a deal tray before it, amusing himself with a toy horse without a head; the said horse being composed of a small wooden cylinder, not unlike an Italian iron, supported on four crooked pegs, and painted in ingenious resemblance of red wafers set in blacking.\n\n‘How do you do, Mr. Johnson?’ said Mrs. Kenwigs. ‘Uncle--Mr. Johnson.’\n\n‘How do you do, sir?’ said Mr. Lillyvick--rather sharply; for he had not known what Nicholas was, on the previous night, and it was rather an aggravating circumstance if a tax collector had been too polite to a teacher.\n\n‘Mr. Johnson is engaged as private master to the children, uncle,’ said Mrs. Kenwigs.\n\n‘So you said just now, my dear,’ replied Mr. Lillyvick.\n\n‘But I hope,’ said Mrs. Kenwigs, drawing herself up, ‘that that will not make them proud; but that they will bless their own good fortune, which has born them superior to common people’s children. Do you hear, Morleena?’\n\n‘Yes, ma,’ replied Miss Kenwigs.\n\n‘And when you go out in the streets, or elsewhere, I desire that you don’t boast of it to the other children,’ said Mrs. Kenwigs; ‘and that if you must say anything about it, you don’t say no more than “We’ve got a private master comes to teach us at home, but we ain’t proud, because ma says it’s sinful.” Do you hear, Morleena?’\n\n‘Yes, ma,’ replied Miss Kenwigs again.\n\n‘Then mind you recollect, and do as I tell you,’ said Mrs. Kenwigs. ‘Shall Mr. Johnson begin, uncle?’\n\n‘I am ready to hear, if Mr. Johnson is ready to commence, my dear,’ said the collector, assuming the air of a profound critic. ‘What sort of language do you consider French, sir?’\n\n‘How do you mean?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘Do you consider it a good language, sir?’ said the collector; ‘a pretty language, a sensible language?’\n\n‘A pretty language, certainly,’ replied Nicholas; ‘and as it has a name for everything, and admits of elegant conversation about everything, I presume it is a sensible one.’\n\n‘I don’t know,’ said Mr. Lillyvick, doubtfully. ‘Do you call it a cheerful language, now?’\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Nicholas, ‘I should say it was, certainly.’\n\n‘It’s very much changed since my time, then,’ said the collector, ‘very much.’\n\n‘Was it a dismal one in your time?’ asked Nicholas, scarcely able to repress a smile.\n\n‘Very,’ replied Mr. Lillyvick, with some vehemence of manner. ‘It’s the war time that I speak of; the last war. It may be a cheerful language. I should be sorry to contradict anybody; but I can only say that I’ve heard the French prisoners, who were natives, and ought to know how to speak it, talking in such a dismal manner, that it made one miserable to hear them. Ay, that I have, fifty times, sir--fifty times!’\n\nMr. Lillyvick was waxing so cross, that Mrs. Kenwigs thought it expedient to motion to Nicholas not to say anything; and it was not until Miss Petowker had practised several blandishments, to soften the excellent old gentleman, that he deigned to break silence by asking,\n\n‘What’s the water in French, sir?’\n\n‘L’EAU,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘Ah!’ said Mr. Lillyvick, shaking his head mournfully, ‘I thought as much. Lo, eh? I don’t think anything of that language--nothing at all.’\n\n‘I suppose the children may begin, uncle?’ said Mrs. Kenwigs.\n\n‘Oh yes; they may begin, my dear,’ replied the collector, discontentedly. ‘I have no wish to prevent them.’\n\nThis permission being conceded, the four Miss Kenwigses sat in a row, with their tails all one way, and Morleena at the top: while Nicholas, taking the book, began his preliminary explanations. Miss Petowker and Mrs. Kenwigs looked on, in silent admiration, broken only by the whispered assurances of the latter, that Morleena would have it all by heart in no time; and Mr. Lillyvick regarded the group with frowning and attentive eyes, lying in wait for something upon which he could open a fresh discussion on the language.\n\nFollows the Fortunes of Miss Nickleby\nIt was with a heavy heart, and many sad forebodings which no effort could banish, that Kate Nickleby, on the morning appointed for the commencement of her engagement with Madame Mantalini, left the city when its clocks yet wanted a quarter of an hour of eight, and threaded her way alone, amid the noise and bustle of the streets, towards the west end of London.\n\nAt this early hour many sickly girls, whose business, like that of the poor worm, is to produce, with patient toil, the finery that bedecks the thoughtless and luxurious, traverse our streets, making towards the scene of their daily labour, and catching, as if by stealth, in their hurried walk, the only gasp of wholesome air and glimpse of sunlight which cheer their monotonous existence during the long train of hours that make a working day. As she drew nigh to the more fashionable quarter of the town, Kate marked many of this class as they passed by, hurrying like herself to their painful occupation, and saw, in their unhealthy looks and feeble gait, but too clear an evidence that her misgivings were not wholly groundless.\n\nShe arrived at Madame Mantalini’s some minutes before the appointed hour, and after walking a few times up and down, in the hope that some other female might arrive and spare her the embarrassment of stating her business to the servant, knocked timidly at the door: which, after some delay, was opened by the footman, who had been putting on his striped jacket as he came upstairs, and was now intent on fastening his apron.\n\n‘Is Madame Mantalini in?’ faltered Kate.\n\n‘Not often out at this time, miss,’ replied the man in a tone which rendered “Miss,” something more offensive than “My dear.”\n\n‘Can I see her?’ asked Kate.\n\n‘Eh?’ replied the man, holding the door in his hand, and honouring the inquirer with a stare and a broad grin, ‘Lord, no.’\n\n‘I came by her own appointment,’ said Kate; ‘I am--I am--to be employed here.’\n\n‘Oh! you should have rung the worker’s bell,’ said the footman, touching the handle of one in the door-post. ‘Let me see, though, I forgot--Miss Nickleby, is it?’\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Kate.\n\n‘You’re to walk upstairs then, please,’ said the man. ‘Madame Mantalini wants to see you--this way--take care of these things on the floor.’\n\nCautioning her, in these terms, not to trip over a heterogeneous litter of pastry-cook’s trays, lamps, waiters full of glasses, and piles of rout seats which were strewn about the hall, plainly bespeaking a late party on the previous night, the man led the way to the second story, and ushered Kate into a back-room, communicating by folding-doors with the apartment in which she had first seen the mistress of the establishment.\n\n‘If you’ll wait here a minute,’ said the man, ‘I’ll tell her presently.’ Having made this promise with much affability, he retired and left Kate alone.\n\nThere was not much to amuse in the room; of which the most attractive feature was, a half-length portrait in oil, of Mr. Mantalini, whom the artist had depicted scratching his head in an easy manner, and thus displaying to advantage a diamond ring, the gift of Madame Mantalini before her marriage. There was, however, the sound of voices in conversation in the next room; and as the conversation was loud and the partition thin, Kate could not help discovering that they belonged to Mr and Mrs. Mantalini.\n\n‘If you will be odiously, demnebly, outrigeously jealous, my soul,’ said Mr. Mantalini, ‘you will be very miserable--horrid miserable--demnition miserable.’ And then, there was a sound as though Mr. Mantalini were sipping his coffee.\n\n‘I AM miserable,’ returned Madame Mantalini, evidently pouting.\n\n‘Then you are an ungrateful, unworthy, demd unthankful little fairy,’ said Mr. Mantalini.\n\n‘I am not,’ returned Madame, with a sob.\n\n‘Do not put itself out of humour,’ said Mr. Mantalini, breaking an egg. ‘It is a pretty, bewitching little demd countenance, and it should not be out of humour, for it spoils its loveliness, and makes it cross and gloomy like a frightful, naughty, demd hobgoblin.’\n\n‘I am not to be brought round in that way, always,’ rejoined Madame, sulkily.\n\n‘It shall be brought round in any way it likes best, and not brought round at all if it likes that better,’ retorted Mr. Mantalini, with his egg-spoon in his mouth.\n\n‘It’s very easy to talk,’ said Mrs. Mantalini.\n\n‘Not so easy when one is eating a demnition egg,’ replied Mr. Mantalini; ‘for the yolk runs down the waistcoat, and yolk of egg does not match any waistcoat but a yellow waistcoat, demmit.’\n\n‘You were flirting with her during the whole night,’ said Madame Mantalini, apparently desirous to lead the conversation back to the point from which it had strayed.\n\n‘No, no, my life.’\n\n‘You were,’ said Madame; ‘I had my eye upon you all the time.’\n\n‘Bless the little winking twinkling eye; was it on me all the time!’ cried Mantalini, in a sort of lazy rapture. ‘Oh, demmit!’\n\n‘And I say once more,’ resumed Madame, ‘that you ought not to waltz with anybody but your own wife; and I will not bear it, Mantalini, if I take poison first.’\n\n‘She will not take poison and have horrid pains, will she?’ said Mantalini; who, by the altered sound of his voice, seemed to have moved his chair, and taken up his position nearer to his wife. ‘She will not take poison, because she had a demd fine husband who might have married two countesses and a dowager--’\n\n‘Two countesses,’ interposed Madame. ‘You told me one before!’\n\n‘Two!’ cried Mantalini. ‘Two demd fine women, real countesses and splendid fortunes, demmit.’\n\n‘And why didn’t you?’ asked Madame, playfully.\n\n‘Why didn’t I!’ replied her husband. ‘Had I not seen, at a morning concert, the demdest little fascinator in all the world, and while that little fascinator is my wife, may not all the countesses and dowagers in England be--’\n\nMr. Mantalini did not finish the sentence, but he gave Madame Mantalini a very loud kiss, which Madame Mantalini returned; after which, there seemed to be some more kissing mixed up with the progress of the breakfast.\n\n‘And what about the cash, my existence’s jewel?’ said Mantalini, when these endearments ceased. ‘How much have we in hand?’\n\n‘Very little indeed,’ replied Madame.\n\n‘We must have some more,’ said Mantalini; ‘we must have some discount out of old Nickleby to carry on the war with, demmit.’\n\n‘You can’t want any more just now,’ said Madame coaxingly.\n\n‘My life and soul,’ returned her husband, ‘there is a horse for sale at Scrubbs’s, which it would be a sin and a crime to lose--going, my senses’ joy, for nothing.’\n\n‘For nothing,’ cried Madame, ‘I am glad of that.’\n\n‘For actually nothing,’ replied Mantalini. ‘A hundred guineas down will buy him; mane, and crest, and legs, and tail, all of the demdest beauty. I will ride him in the park before the very chariots of the rejected countesses. The demd old dowager will faint with grief and rage; the other two will say “He is married, he has made away with himself, it is a demd thing, it is all up!” They will hate each other demnebly, and wish you dead and buried. Ha! ha! Demmit.’\n\nMadame Mantalini’s prudence, if she had any, was not proof against these triumphal pictures; after a little jingling of keys, she observed that she would see what her desk contained, and rising for that purpose, opened the folding-door, and walked into the room where Kate was seated.\n\n‘Dear me, child!’ exclaimed Madame Mantalini, recoiling in surprise. ‘How came you here?’\n\n‘Child!’ cried Mantalini, hurrying in. ‘How came--eh!--oh--demmit, how d’ye do?’\n\n‘I have been waiting, here some time, ma’am,’ said Kate, addressing Madame Mantalini. ‘The servant must have forgotten to let you know that I was here, I think.’\n\n‘You really must see to that man,’ said Madame, turning to her husband. ‘He forgets everything.’\n\n‘I will twist his demd nose off his countenance for leaving such a very pretty creature all alone by herself,’ said her husband.\n\n‘Mantalini,’ cried Madame, ‘you forget yourself.’\n\n‘I don’t forget you, my soul, and never shall, and never can,’ said Mantalini, kissing his wife’s hand, and grimacing aside, to Miss Nickleby, who turned away.\n\nAppeased by this compliment, the lady of the business took some papers from her desk which she handed over to Mr. Mantalini, who received them with great delight. She then requested Kate to follow her, and after several feints on the part of Mr. Mantalini to attract the young lady’s attention, they went away: leaving that gentleman extended at full length on the sofa, with his heels in the air and a newspaper in his hand.\n\nMadame Mantalini led the way down a flight of stairs, and through a passage, to a large room at the back of the premises where were a number of young women employed in sewing, cutting out, making up, altering, and various other processes known only to those who are cunning in the arts of millinery and dressmaking. It was a close room with a skylight, and as dull and quiet as a room need be.\n\nOn Madame Mantalini calling aloud for Miss Knag, a short, bustling, over-dressed female, full of importance, presented herself, and all the young ladies suspending their operations for the moment, whispered to each other sundry criticisms upon the make and texture of Miss Nickleby’s dress, her complexion, cast of features, and personal appearance, with as much good breeding as could have been displayed by the very best society in a crowded ball-room.\n\n‘Oh, Miss Knag,’ said Madame Mantalini, ‘this is the young person I spoke to you about.’\n\nMiss Knag bestowed a reverential smile upon Madame Mantalini, which she dexterously transformed into a gracious one for Kate, and said that certainly, although it was a great deal of trouble to have young people who were wholly unused to the business, still, she was sure the young person would try to do her best--impressed with which conviction she (Miss Knag) felt an interest in her, already.\n\n‘I think that, for the present at all events, it will be better for Miss Nickleby to come into the show-room with you, and try things on for people,’ said Madame Mantalini. ‘She will not be able for the present to be of much use in any other way; and her appearance will--’\n\n‘Suit very well with mine, Madame Mantalini,’ interrupted Miss Knag. ‘So it will; and to be sure I might have known that you would not be long in finding that out; for you have so much taste in all those matters, that really, as I often say to the young ladies, I do not know how, when, or where, you possibly could have acquired all you know--hem--Miss Nickleby and I are quite a pair, Madame Mantalini, only I am a little darker than Miss Nickleby, and--hem--I think my foot may be a little smaller. Miss Nickleby, I am sure, will not be offended at my saying that, when she hears that our family always have been celebrated for small feet ever since--hem--ever since our family had any feet at all, indeed, I think. I had an uncle once, Madame Mantalini, who lived in Cheltenham, and had a most excellent business as a tobacconist--hem--who had such small feet, that they were no bigger than those which are usually joined to wooden legs--the most symmetrical feet, Madame Mantalini, that even you can imagine.’\n\n‘They must have had something of the appearance of club feet, Miss Knag,’ said Madame.\n\n‘Well now, that is so like you,’ returned Miss Knag, ‘Ha! ha! ha! Of club feet! Oh very good! As I often remark to the young ladies, “Well I must say, and I do not care who knows it, of all the ready humour--hem--I ever heard anywhere”--and I have heard a good deal; for when my dear brother was alive (I kept house for him, Miss Nickleby), we had to supper once a week two or three young men, highly celebrated in those days for their humour, Madame Mantalini--“Of all the ready humour,” I say to the young ladies, “I ever heard, Madame Mantalini’s is the most remarkable--hem. It is so gentle, so sarcastic, and yet so good-natured (as I was observing to Miss Simmonds only this morning), that how, or when, or by what means she acquired it, is to me a mystery indeed.”’\n\nHere Miss Knag paused to take breath, and while she pauses it may be observed--not that she was marvellously loquacious and marvellously deferential to Madame Mantalini, since these are facts which require no comment; but that every now and then, she was accustomed, in the torrent of her discourse, to introduce a loud, shrill, clear ‘hem!’ the import and meaning of which, was variously interpreted by her acquaintance; some holding that Miss Knag dealt in exaggeration, and introduced the monosyllable when any fresh invention was in course of coinage in her brain; others, that when she wanted a word, she threw it in to gain time, and prevent anybody else from striking into the conversation. It may be further remarked, that Miss Knag still aimed at youth, although she had shot beyond it, years ago; and that she was weak and vain, and one of those people who are best described by the axiom, that you may trust them as far as you can see them, and no farther.\n\n‘You’ll take care that Miss Nickleby understands her hours, and so forth,’ said Madame Mantalini; ‘and so I’ll leave her with you. You’ll not forget my directions, Miss Knag?’\n\nMiss Knag of course replied, that to forget anything Madame Mantalini had directed, was a moral impossibility; and that lady, dispensing a general good-morning among her assistants, sailed away.\n\n‘Charming creature, isn’t she, Miss Nickleby?’ said Miss Knag, rubbing her hands together.\n\n‘I have seen very little of her,’ said Kate. ‘I hardly know yet.’\n\n‘Have you seen Mr. Mantalini?’ inquired Miss Knag.\n\n‘Yes; I have seen him twice.’\n\n‘Isn’t HE a charming creature?’\n\n‘Indeed he does not strike me as being so, by any means,’ replied Kate.\n\n‘No, my dear!’ cried Miss Knag, elevating her hands. ‘Why, goodness gracious mercy, where’s your taste? Such a fine tall, full-whiskered dashing gentlemanly man, with such teeth and hair, and--hem--well now, you DO astonish me.’\n\n‘I dare say I am very foolish,’ replied Kate, laying aside her bonnet; ‘but as my opinion is of very little importance to him or anyone else, I do not regret having formed it, and shall be slow to change it, I think.’\n\n‘He is a very fine man, don’t you think so?’ asked one of the young ladies.\n\n‘Indeed he may be, for anything I could say to the contrary,’ replied Kate.\n\n‘And drives very beautiful horses, doesn’t he?’ inquired another.\n\n‘I dare say he may, but I never saw them,’ answered Kate.\n\n‘Never saw them!’ interposed Miss Knag. ‘Oh, well! There it is at once you know; how can you possibly pronounce an opinion about a gentleman--hem--if you don’t see him as he turns out altogether?’\n\nThere was so much of the world--even of the little world of the country girl--in this idea of the old milliner, that Kate, who was anxious, for every reason, to change the subject, made no further remark, and left Miss Knag in possession of the field.\n\nAfter a short silence, during which most of the young people made a closer inspection of Kate’s appearance, and compared notes respecting it, one of them offered to help her off with her shawl, and the offer being accepted, inquired whether she did not find black very uncomfortable wear.\n\n‘I do indeed,’ replied Kate, with a bitter sigh.\n\n‘So dusty and hot,’ observed the same speaker, adjusting her dress for her.\n\nKate might have said, that mourning is sometimes the coldest wear which mortals can assume; that it not only chills the breasts of those it clothes, but extending its influence to summer friends, freezes up their sources of good-will and kindness, and withering all the buds of promise they once so liberally put forth, leaves nothing but bared and rotten hearts exposed. There are few who have lost a friend or relative constituting in life their sole dependence, who have not keenly felt this chilling influence of their sable garb. She had felt it acutely, and feeling it at the moment, could not quite restrain her tears.\n\n‘I am very sorry to have wounded you by my thoughtless speech,’ said her companion. ‘I did not think of it. You are in mourning for some near relation?’\n\n‘For my father,’ answered Kate.\n\n‘For what relation, Miss Simmonds?’ asked Miss Knag, in an audible voice.\n\n‘Her father,’ replied the other softly.\n\n‘Her father, eh?’ said Miss Knag, without the slightest depression of her voice. ‘Ah! A long illness, Miss Simmonds?’\n\n‘Hush,’ replied the girl; ‘I don’t know.’\n\n‘Our misfortune was very sudden,’ said Kate, turning away, ‘or I might perhaps, at a time like this, be enabled to support it better.’\n\nThere had existed not a little desire in the room, according to invariable custom, when any new ‘young person’ came, to know who Kate was, and what she was, and all about her; but, although it might have been very naturally increased by her appearance and emotion, the knowledge that it pained her to be questioned, was sufficient to repress even this curiosity; and Miss Knag, finding it hopeless to attempt extracting any further particulars just then, reluctantly commanded silence, and bade the work proceed.\n\nIn silence, then, the tasks were plied until half-past one, when a baked leg of mutton, with potatoes to correspond, were served in the kitchen. The meal over, and the young ladies having enjoyed the additional relaxation of washing their hands, the work began again, and was again performed in silence, until the noise of carriages rattling through the streets, and of loud double knocks at doors, gave token that the day’s work of the more fortunate members of society was proceeding in its turn.\n\nOne of these double knocks at Madame Mantalini’s door, announced the equipage of some great lady--or rather rich one, for there is occasionally a distinction between riches and greatness--who had come with her daughter to approve of some court-dresses which had been a long time preparing, and upon whom Kate was deputed to wait, accompanied by Miss Knag, and officered of course by Madame Mantalini.\n\nKate’s part in the pageant was humble enough, her duties being limited to holding articles of costume until Miss Knag was ready to try them on, and now and then tying a string, or fastening a hook-and-eye. She might, not unreasonably, have supposed herself beneath the reach of any arrogance, or bad humour; but it happened that the lady and daughter were both out of temper that day, and the poor girl came in for her share of their revilings. She was awkward--her hands were cold--dirty--coarse--she could do nothing right; they wondered how Madame Mantalini could have such people about her; requested they might see some other young woman the next time they came; and so forth.\n\nSo common an occurrence would be hardly deserving of mention, but for its effect. Kate shed many bitter tears when these people were gone, and felt, for the first time, humbled by her occupation. She had, it is true, quailed at the prospect of drudgery and hard service; but she had felt no degradation in working for her bread, until she found herself exposed to insolence and pride. Philosophy would have taught her that the degradation was on the side of those who had sunk so low as to display such passions habitually, and without cause: but she was too young for such consolation, and her honest feeling was hurt. May not the complaint, that common people are above their station, often take its rise in the fact of UNcommon people being below theirs?\n\nIn such scenes and occupations the time wore on until nine o’clock, when Kate, jaded and dispirited with the occurrences of the day, hastened from the confinement of the workroom, to join her mother at the street corner, and walk home:--the more sadly, from having to disguise her real feelings, and feign to participate in all the sanguine visions of her companion.\n\n‘Bless my soul, Kate,’ said Mrs. Nickleby; ‘I’ve been thinking all day what a delightful thing it would be for Madame Mantalini to take you into partnership--such a likely thing too, you know! Why, your poor dear papa’s cousin’s sister-in-law--a Miss Browndock--was taken into partnership by a lady that kept a school at Hammersmith, and made her fortune in no time at all. I forget, by-the-bye, whether that Miss Browndock was the same lady that got the ten thousand pounds prize in the lottery, but I think she was; indeed, now I come to think of it, I am sure she was. “Mantalini and Nickleby”, how well it would sound!--and if Nicholas has any good fortune, you might have Doctor Nickleby, the head-master of Westminster School, living in the same street.’\n\n‘Dear Nicholas!’ cried Kate, taking from her reticule her brother’s letter from Dotheboys Hall. ‘In all our misfortunes, how happy it makes me, mama, to hear he is doing well, and to find him writing in such good spirits! It consoles me for all we may undergo, to think that he is comfortable and happy.’\n\nPoor Kate! she little thought how weak her consolation was, and how soon she would be undeceived.\n\n# CHAPTER 18\n\nMiss Knag, after doting on Kate Nickleby for three whole Days, makes up her Mind to hate her for evermore. The Causes which led Miss Knag to form this Resolution\n\nThere are many lives of much pain, hardship, and suffering, which, having no stirring interest for any but those who lead them, are disregarded by persons who do not want thought or feeling, but who pamper their compassion and need high stimulants to rouse it.\n\nThere are not a few among the disciples of charity who require, in their vocation, scarcely less excitement than the votaries of pleasure in theirs; and hence it is that diseased sympathy and compassion are every day expended on out-of-the-way objects, when only too many demands upon the legitimate exercise of the same virtues in a healthy state, are constantly within the sight and hearing of the most unobservant person alive. In short, charity must have its romance, as the novelist or playwright must have his. A thief in fustian is a vulgar character, scarcely to be thought of by persons of refinement; but dress him in green velvet, with a high-crowned hat, and change the scene of his operations, from a thickly-peopled city, to a mountain road, and you shall find in him the very soul of poetry and adventure. So it is with the one great cardinal virtue, which, properly nourished and exercised, leads to, if it does not necessarily include, all the others. It must have its romance; and the less of real, hard, struggling work-a-day life there is in that romance, the better.\n\nThe life to which poor Kate Nickleby was devoted, in consequence of the unforeseen train of circumstances already developed in this narrative, was a hard one; but lest the very dulness, unhealthy confinement, and bodily fatigue, which made up its sum and substance, should deprive it of any interest with the mass of the charitable and sympathetic, I would rather keep Miss Nickleby herself in view just now, than chill them in the outset, by a minute and lengthened description of the establishment presided over by Madame Mantalini.\n\n‘Well, now, indeed, Madame Mantalini,’ said Miss Knag, as Kate was taking her weary way homewards on the first night of her novitiate; ‘that Miss Nickleby is a very creditable young person--a very creditable young person indeed--hem--upon my word, Madame Mantalini, it does very extraordinary credit even to your discrimination that you should have found such a very excellent, very well-behaved, very--hem--very unassuming young woman to assist in the fitting on. I have seen some young women when they had the opportunity of displaying before their betters, behave in such a--oh, dear--well--but you’re always right, Madame Mantalini, always; and as I very often tell the young ladies, how you do contrive to be always right, when so many people are so often wrong, is to me a mystery indeed.’\n\n‘Beyond putting a very excellent client out of humour, Miss Nickleby has not done anything very remarkable today--that I am aware of, at least,’ said Madame Mantalini in reply.\n\n‘Oh, dear!’ said Miss Knag; ‘but you must allow a great deal for inexperience, you know.’\n\n‘And youth?’ inquired Madame.\n\n‘Oh, I say nothing about that, Madame Mantalini,’ replied Miss Knag, reddening; ‘because if youth were any excuse, you wouldn’t have--’\n\n‘Quite so good a forewoman as I have, I suppose,’ suggested Madame.\n\n‘Well, I never did know anybody like you, Madame Mantalini,’ rejoined Miss Knag most complacently, ‘and that’s the fact, for you know what one’s going to say, before it has time to rise to one’s lips. Oh, very good! Ha, ha, ha!’\n\n‘For myself,’ observed Madame Mantalini, glancing with affected carelessness at her assistant, and laughing heartily in her sleeve, ‘I consider Miss Nickleby the most awkward girl I ever saw in my life.’\n\n‘Poor dear thing,’ said Miss Knag, ‘it’s not her fault. If it was, we might hope to cure it; but as it’s her misfortune, Madame Mantalini, why really you know, as the man said about the blind horse, we ought to respect it.’\n\n‘Her uncle told me she had been considered pretty,’ remarked Madame Mantalini. ‘I think her one of the most ordinary girls I ever met with.’\n\n‘Ordinary!’ cried Miss Knag with a countenance beaming delight; ‘and awkward! Well, all I can say is, Madame Mantalini, that I quite love the poor girl; and that if she was twice as indifferent-looking, and twice as awkward as she is, I should be only so much the more her friend, and that’s the truth of it.’\n\nIn fact, Miss Knag had conceived an incipient affection for Kate Nickleby, after witnessing her failure that morning, and this short conversation with her superior increased the favourable prepossession to a most surprising extent; which was the more remarkable, as when she first scanned that young lady’s face and figure, she had entertained certain inward misgivings that they would never agree.\n\n‘But now,’ said Miss Knag, glancing at the reflection of herself in a mirror at no great distance, ‘I love her--I quite love her--I declare I do!’\n\nOf such a highly disinterested quality was this devoted friendship, and so superior was it to the little weaknesses of flattery or ill-nature, that the kind-hearted Miss Knag candidly informed Kate Nickleby, next day, that she saw she would never do for the business, but that she need not give herself the slightest uneasiness on this account, for that she (Miss Knag), by increased exertions on her own part, would keep her as much as possible in the background, and that all she would have to do, would be to remain perfectly quiet before company, and to shrink from attracting notice by every means in her power. This last suggestion was so much in accordance with the timid girl’s own feelings and wishes, that she readily promised implicit reliance on the excellent spinster’s advice: without questioning, or indeed bestowing a moment’s reflection upon, the motives that dictated it.\n\n‘I take quite a lively interest in you, my dear soul, upon my word,’ said Miss Knag; ‘a sister’s interest, actually. It’s the most singular circumstance I ever knew.’\n\nUndoubtedly it was singular, that if Miss Knag did feel a strong interest in Kate Nickleby, it should not rather have been the interest of a maiden aunt or grandmother; that being the conclusion to which the difference in their respective ages would have naturally tended. But Miss Knag wore clothes of a very youthful pattern, and perhaps her feelings took the same shape.\n\n‘Bless you!’ said Miss Knag, bestowing a kiss upon Kate at the conclusion of the second day’s work, ‘how very awkward you have been all day.’\n\n‘I fear your kind and open communication, which has rendered me more painfully conscious of my own defects, has not improved me,’ sighed Kate.\n\n‘No, no, I dare say not,’ rejoined Miss Knag, in a most uncommon flow of good humour. ‘But how much better that you should know it at first, and so be able to go on, straight and comfortable! Which way are you walking, my love?’\n\n‘Towards the city,’ replied Kate.\n\n‘The city!’ cried Miss Knag, regarding herself with great favour in the glass as she tied her bonnet. ‘Goodness gracious me! now do you really live in the city?’\n\n‘Is it so very unusual for anybody to live there?’ asked Kate, half smiling.\n\n‘I couldn’t have believed it possible that any young woman could have lived there, under any circumstances whatever, for three days together,’ replied Miss Knag.\n\n‘Reduced--I should say poor people,’ answered Kate, correcting herself hastily, for she was afraid of appearing proud, ‘must live where they can.’\n\n‘Ah! very true, so they must; very proper indeed!’ rejoined Miss Knag with that sort of half-sigh, which, accompanied by two or three slight nods of the head, is pity’s small change in general society; ‘and that’s what I very often tell my brother, when our servants go away ill, one after another, and he thinks the back-kitchen’s rather too damp for ‘em to sleep in. These sort of people, I tell him, are glad to sleep anywhere! Heaven suits the back to the burden. What a nice thing it is to think that it should be so, isn’t it?’\n\n‘Very,’ replied Kate.\n\n‘I’ll walk with you part of the way, my dear,’ said Miss Knag, ‘for you must go very near our house; and as it’s quite dark, and our last servant went to the hospital a week ago, with St Anthony’s fire in her face, I shall be glad of your company.’\n\nKate would willingly have excused herself from this flattering companionship; but Miss Knag having adjusted her bonnet to her entire satisfaction, took her arm with an air which plainly showed how much she felt the compliment she was conferring, and they were in the street before she could say another word.\n\n‘I fear,’ said Kate, hesitating, ‘that mama--my mother, I mean--is waiting for me.’\n\n‘You needn’t make the least apology, my dear,’ said Miss Knag, smiling sweetly as she spoke; ‘I dare say she is a very respectable old person, and I shall be quite--hem--quite pleased to know her.’\n\nAs poor Mrs. Nickleby was cooling--not her heels alone, but her limbs generally at the street corner, Kate had no alternative but to make her known to Miss Knag, who, doing the last new carriage customer at second-hand, acknowledged the introduction with condescending politeness. The three then walked away, arm in arm: with Miss Knag in the middle, in a special state of amiability.\n\n‘I have taken such a fancy to your daughter, Mrs. Nickleby, you can’t think,’ said Miss Knag, after she had proceeded a little distance in dignified silence.\n\n‘I am delighted to hear it,’ said Mrs. Nickleby; ‘though it is nothing new to me, that even strangers should like Kate.’\n\n‘Hem!’ cried Miss Knag.\n\n‘You will like her better when you know how good she is,’ said Mrs Nickleby. ‘It is a great blessing to me, in my misfortunes, to have a child, who knows neither pride nor vanity, and whose bringing-up might very well have excused a little of both at first. You don’t know what it is to lose a husband, Miss Knag.’\n\nAs Miss Knag had never yet known what it was to gain one, it followed, very nearly as a matter of course, that she didn’t know what it was to lose one; so she said, in some haste, ‘No, indeed I don’t,’ and said it with an air intending to signify that she should like to catch herself marrying anybody--no, no, she knew better than that.\n\n‘Kate has improved even in this little time, I have no doubt,’ said Mrs Nickleby, glancing proudly at her daughter.\n\n‘Oh! of course,’ said Miss Knag.\n\n‘And will improve still more,’ added Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘That she will, I’ll be bound,’ replied Miss Knag, squeezing Kate’s arm in her own, to point the joke.\n\n‘She always was clever,’ said poor Mrs. Nickleby, brightening up, ‘always, from a baby. I recollect when she was only two years and a half old, that a gentleman who used to visit very much at our house--Mr Watkins, you know, Kate, my dear, that your poor papa went bail for, who afterwards ran away to the United States, and sent us a pair of snow shoes, with such an affectionate letter that it made your poor dear father cry for a week. You remember the letter? In which he said that he was very sorry he couldn’t repay the fifty pounds just then, because his capital was all out at interest, and he was very busy making his fortune, but that he didn’t forget you were his god-daughter, and he should take it very unkind if we didn’t buy you a silver coral and put it down to his old account? Dear me, yes, my dear, how stupid you are! and spoke so affectionately of the old port wine that he used to drink a bottle and a half of every time he came. You must remember, Kate?’\n\n‘Yes, yes, mama; what of him?’\n\n‘Why, that Mr. Watkins, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nickleby slowly, as if she were making a tremendous effort to recollect something of paramount importance; ‘that Mr. Watkins--he wasn’t any relation, Miss Knag will understand, to the Watkins who kept the Old Boar in the village; by-the-bye, I don’t remember whether it was the Old Boar or the George the Third, but it was one of the two, I know, and it’s much the same--that Mr. Watkins said, when you were only two years and a half old, that you were one of the most astonishing children he ever saw. He did indeed, Miss Knag, and he wasn’t at all fond of children, and couldn’t have had the slightest motive for doing it. I know it was he who said so, because I recollect, as well as if it was only yesterday, his borrowing twenty pounds of her poor dear papa the very moment afterwards.’\n\nHaving quoted this extraordinary and most disinterested testimony to her daughter’s excellence, Mrs. Nickleby stopped to breathe; and Miss Knag, finding that the discourse was turning upon family greatness, lost no time in striking in, with a small reminiscence on her own account.\n\n‘Don’t talk of lending money, Mrs. Nickleby,’ said Miss Knag, ‘or you’ll drive me crazy, perfectly crazy. My mama--hem--was the most lovely and beautiful creature, with the most striking and exquisite--hem--the most exquisite nose that ever was put upon a human face, I do believe, Mrs Nickleby (here Miss Knag rubbed her own nose sympathetically); the most delightful and accomplished woman, perhaps, that ever was seen; but she had that one failing of lending money, and carried it to such an extent that she lent--hem--oh! thousands of pounds, all our little fortunes, and what’s more, Mrs. Nickleby, I don’t think, if we were to live till--till--hem--till the very end of time, that we should ever get them back again. I don’t indeed.’\n\nAfter concluding this effort of invention without being interrupted, Miss Knag fell into many more recollections, no less interesting than true, the full tide of which, Mrs. Nickleby in vain attempting to stem, at length sailed smoothly down by adding an under-current of her own recollections; and so both ladies went on talking together in perfect contentment; the only difference between them being, that whereas Miss Knag addressed herself to Kate, and talked very loud, Mrs. Nickleby kept on in one unbroken monotonous flow, perfectly satisfied to be talking and caring very little whether anybody listened or not.\n\nIn this manner they walked on, very amicably, until they arrived at Miss Knag’s brother’s, who was an ornamental stationer and small circulating library keeper, in a by-street off Tottenham Court Road; and who let out by the day, week, month, or year, the newest old novels, whereof the titles were displayed in pen-and-ink characters on a sheet of pasteboard, swinging at his door-post. As Miss Knag happened, at the moment, to be in the middle of an account of her twenty-second offer from a gentleman of large property, she insisted upon their all going in to supper together; and in they went.\n\n‘Don’t go away, Mortimer,’ said Miss Knag as they entered the shop. ‘It’s only one of our young ladies and her mother. Mrs. and Miss Nickleby.’\n\n‘Oh, indeed!’ said Mr. Mortimer Knag. ‘Ah!’\n\nHaving given utterance to these ejaculations with a very profound and thoughtful air, Mr. Knag slowly snuffed two kitchen candles on the counter, and two more in the window, and then snuffed himself from a box in his waistcoat pocket.\n\nThere was something very impressive in the ghostly air with which all this was done; and as Mr. Knag was a tall lank gentleman of solemn features, wearing spectacles, and garnished with much less hair than a gentleman bordering on forty, or thereabouts, usually boasts, Mrs Nickleby whispered her daughter that she thought he must be literary.\n\n‘Past ten,’ said Mr. Knag, consulting his watch. ‘Thomas, close the warehouse.’\n\nThomas was a boy nearly half as tall as a shutter, and the warehouse was a shop about the size of three hackney coaches.\n\n‘Ah!’ said Mr. Knag once more, heaving a deep sigh as he restored to its parent shelf the book he had been reading. ‘Well--yes--I believe supper is ready, sister.’\n\nWith another sigh Mr. Knag took up the kitchen candles from the counter, and preceded the ladies with mournful steps to a back-parlour, where a charwoman, employed in the absence of the sick servant, and remunerated with certain eighteenpences to be deducted from her wages due, was putting the supper out.\n\n‘Mrs. Blockson,’ said Miss Knag, reproachfully, ‘how very often I have begged you not to come into the room with your bonnet on!’\n\n‘I can’t help it, Miss Knag,’ said the charwoman, bridling up on the shortest notice. ‘There’s been a deal o’cleaning to do in this house, and if you don’t like it, I must trouble you to look out for somebody else, for it don’t hardly pay me, and that’s the truth, if I was to be hung this minute.’\n\n‘I don’t want any remarks if YOU please,’ said Miss Knag, with a strong emphasis on the personal pronoun. ‘Is there any fire downstairs for some hot water presently?’\n\n‘No there is not, indeed, Miss Knag,’ replied the substitute; ‘and so I won’t tell you no stories about it.’\n\n‘Then why isn’t there?’ said Miss Knag.\n\n‘Because there arn’t no coals left out, and if I could make coals I would, but as I can’t I won’t, and so I make bold to tell you, Mem,’ replied Mrs. Blockson.\n\n‘Will you hold your tongue--female?’ said Mr. Mortimer Knag, plunging violently into this dialogue.\n\n‘By your leave, Mr. Knag,’ retorted the charwoman, turning sharp round. ‘I’m only too glad not to speak in this house, excepting when and where I’m spoke to, sir; and with regard to being a female, sir, I should wish to know what you considered yourself?’\n\n‘A miserable wretch,’ exclaimed Mr. Knag, striking his forehead. ‘A miserable wretch.’\n\n‘I’m very glad to find that you don’t call yourself out of your name, sir,’ said Mrs. Blockson; ‘and as I had two twin children the day before yesterday was only seven weeks, and my little Charley fell down a airy and put his elber out, last Monday, I shall take it as a favour if you’ll send nine shillings, for one week’s work, to my house, afore the clock strikes ten tomorrow.’\n\nWith these parting words, the good woman quitted the room with great ease of manner, leaving the door wide open; Mr. Knag, at the same moment, flung himself into the ‘warehouse,’ and groaned aloud.\n\n‘What is the matter with that gentleman, pray?’ inquired Mrs. Nickleby, greatly disturbed by the sound.\n\n‘Is he ill?’ inquired Kate, really alarmed.\n\n‘Hush!’ replied Miss Knag; ‘a most melancholy history. He was once most devotedly attached to--hem--to Madame Mantalini.’\n\n‘Bless me!’ exclaimed Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘Yes,’ continued Miss Knag, ‘and received great encouragement too, and confidently hoped to marry her. He has a most romantic heart, Mrs. Nickleby, as indeed--hem--as indeed all our family have, and the disappointment was a dreadful blow. He is a wonderfully accomplished man--most extraordinarily accomplished--reads--hem--reads every novel that comes out; I mean every novel that--hem--that has any fashion in it, of course. The fact is, that he did find so much in the books he read, applicable to his own misfortunes, and did find himself in every respect so much like the heroes--because of course he is conscious of his own superiority, as we all are, and very naturally--that he took to scorning everything, and became a genius; and I am quite sure that he is, at this very present moment, writing another book.’\n\n‘Another book!’ repeated Kate, finding that a pause was left for somebody to say something.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Miss Knag, nodding in great triumph; ‘another book, in three volumes post octavo. Of course it’s a great advantage to him, in all his little fashionable descriptions, to have the benefit of my--hem--of my experience, because, of course, few authors who write about such things can have such opportunities of knowing them as I have. He’s so wrapped up in high life, that the least allusion to business or worldly matters--like that woman just now, for instance--quite distracts him; but, as I often say, I think his disappointment a great thing for him, because if he hadn’t been disappointed he couldn’t have written about blighted hopes and all that; and the fact is, if it hadn’t happened as it has, I don’t believe his genius would ever have come out at all.’\n\nHow much more communicative Miss Knag might have become under more favourable circumstances, it is impossible to divine, but as the gloomy one was within ear-shot, and the fire wanted making up, her disclosures stopped here. To judge from all appearances, and the difficulty of making the water warm, the last servant could not have been much accustomed to any other fire than St Anthony’s; but a little brandy and water was made at last, and the guests, having been previously regaled with cold leg of mutton and bread and cheese, soon afterwards took leave; Kate amusing herself, all the way home, with the recollection of her last glimpse of Mr. Mortimer Knag deeply abstracted in the shop; and Mrs. Nickleby by debating within herself whether the dressmaking firm would ultimately become ‘Mantalini, Knag, and Nickleby’, or ‘Mantalini, Nickleby, and Knag’.\n\nAt this high point, Miss Knag’s friendship remained for three whole days, much to the wonderment of Madame Mantalini’s young ladies who had never beheld such constancy in that quarter, before; but on the fourth, it received a check no less violent than sudden, which thus occurred.\n\nIt happened that an old lord of great family, who was going to marry a young lady of no family in particular, came with the young lady, and the young lady’s sister, to witness the ceremony of trying on two nuptial bonnets which had been ordered the day before, and Madame Mantalini announcing the fact, in a shrill treble, through the speaking-pipe, which communicated with the workroom, Miss Knag darted hastily upstairs with a bonnet in each hand, and presented herself in the show-room, in a charming state of palpitation, intended to demonstrate her enthusiasm in the cause. The bonnets were no sooner fairly on, than Miss Knag and Madame Mantalini fell into convulsions of admiration.\n\n‘A most elegant appearance,’ said Madame Mantalini.\n\n‘I never saw anything so exquisite in all my life,’ said Miss Knag.\n\nNow, the old lord, who was a VERY old lord, said nothing, but mumbled and chuckled in a state of great delight, no less with the nuptial bonnets and their wearers, than with his own address in getting such a fine woman for his wife; and the young lady, who was a very lively young lady, seeing the old lord in this rapturous condition, chased the old lord behind a cheval-glass, and then and there kissed him, while Madame Mantalini and the other young lady looked, discreetly, another way.\n\nBut, pending the salutation, Miss Knag, who was tinged with curiosity, stepped accidentally behind the glass, and encountered the lively young lady’s eye just at the very moment when she kissed the old lord; upon which the young lady, in a pouting manner, murmured something about ‘an old thing,’ and ‘great impertinence,’ and finished by darting a look of displeasure at Miss Knag, and smiling contemptuously.\n\n‘Madame Mantalini,’ said the young lady.\n\n‘Ma’am,’ said Madame Mantalini.\n\n‘Pray have up that pretty young creature we saw yesterday.’\n\n‘Oh yes, do,’ said the sister.\n\n‘Of all things in the world, Madame Mantalini,’ said the lord’s intended, throwing herself languidly on a sofa, ‘I hate being waited upon by frights or elderly persons. Let me always see that young creature, I beg, whenever I come.’\n\n‘By all means,’ said the old lord; ‘the lovely young creature, by all means.’\n\n‘Everybody is talking about her,’ said the young lady, in the same careless manner; ‘and my lord, being a great admirer of beauty, must positively see her.’\n\n‘She IS universally admired,’ replied Madame Mantalini. ‘Miss Knag, send up Miss Nickleby. You needn’t return.’\n\n‘I beg your pardon, Madame Mantalini, what did you say last?’ asked Miss Knag, trembling.\n\n‘You needn’t return,’ repeated the superior, sharply. Miss Knag vanished without another word, and in all reasonable time was replaced by Kate, who took off the new bonnets and put on the old ones: blushing very much to find that the old lord and the two young ladies were staring her out of countenance all the time.\n\n‘Why, how you colour, child!’ said the lord’s chosen bride.\n\n‘She is not quite so accustomed to her business, as she will be in a week or two,’ interposed Madame Mantalini with a gracious smile.\n\n‘I am afraid you have been giving her some of your wicked looks, my lord,’ said the intended.\n\n‘No, no, no,’ replied the old lord, ‘no, no, I’m going to be married, and lead a new life. Ha, ha, ha! a new life, a new life! ha, ha, ha!’\n\nIt was a satisfactory thing to hear that the old gentleman was going to lead a new life, for it was pretty evident that his old one would not last him much longer. The mere exertion of protracted chuckling reduced him to a fearful ebb of coughing and gasping; it was some minutes before he could find breath to remark that the girl was too pretty for a milliner.\n\n‘I hope you don’t think good looks a disqualification for the business, my lord,’ said Madame Mantalini, simpering.\n\n‘Not by any means,’ replied the old lord, ‘or you would have left it long ago.’\n\n‘You naughty creature,’ said the lively lady, poking the peer with her parasol; ‘I won’t have you talk so. How dare you?’\n\nThis playful inquiry was accompanied with another poke, and another, and then the old lord caught the parasol, and wouldn’t give it up again, which induced the other lady to come to the rescue, and some very pretty sportiveness ensued.\n\n‘You will see that those little alterations are made, Madame Mantalini,’ said the lady. ‘Nay, you bad man, you positively shall go first; I wouldn’t leave you behind with that pretty girl, not for half a second. I know you too well. Jane, my dear, let him go first, and we shall be quite sure of him.’\n\nThe old lord, evidently much flattered by this suspicion, bestowed a grotesque leer upon Kate as he passed; and, receiving another tap with the parasol for his wickedness, tottered downstairs to the door, where his sprightly body was hoisted into the carriage by two stout footmen.\n\n‘Foh!’ said Madame Mantalini, ‘how he ever gets into a carriage without thinking of a hearse, I can’t think. There, take the things away, my dear, take them away.’\n\nKate, who had remained during the whole scene with her eyes modestly fixed upon the ground, was only too happy to avail herself of the permission to retire, and hasten joyfully downstairs to Miss Knag’s dominion.\n\nThe circumstances of the little kingdom had greatly changed, however, during the short period of her absence. In place of Miss Knag being stationed in her accustomed seat, preserving all the dignity and greatness of Madame Mantalini’s representative, that worthy soul was reposing on a large box, bathed in tears, while three or four of the young ladies in close attendance upon her, together with the presence of hartshorn, vinegar, and other restoratives, would have borne ample testimony, even without the derangement of the head-dress and front row of curls, to her having fainted desperately.\n\n‘Bless me!’ said Kate, stepping hastily forward, ‘what is the matter?’\n\nThis inquiry produced in Miss Knag violent symptoms of a relapse; and several young ladies, darting angry looks at Kate, applied more vinegar and hartshorn, and said it was ‘a shame.’\n\n‘What is a shame?’ demanded Kate. ‘What is the matter? What has happened? tell me.’\n\n‘Matter!’ cried Miss Knag, coming, all at once, bolt upright, to the great consternation of the assembled maidens; ‘matter! Fie upon you, you nasty creature!’\n\n‘Gracious!’ cried Kate, almost paralysed by the violence with which the adjective had been jerked out from between Miss Knag’s closed teeth; ‘have I offended you?’\n\n‘YOU offended me!’ retorted Miss Knag, ‘YOU! a chit, a child, an upstart nobody! Oh, indeed! Ha, ha!’\n\nNow, it was evident, as Miss Knag laughed, that something struck her as being exceedingly funny; and as the young ladies took their tone from Miss Knag--she being the chief--they all got up a laugh without a moment’s delay, and nodded their heads a little, and smiled sarcastically to each other, as much as to say how very good that was!\n\n‘Here she is,’ continued Miss Knag, getting off the box, and introducing Kate with much ceremony and many low curtseys to the delighted throng; ‘here she is--everybody is talking about her--the belle, ladies--the beauty, the--oh, you bold-faced thing!’\n\nAt this crisis, Miss Knag was unable to repress a virtuous shudder, which immediately communicated itself to all the young ladies; after which, Miss Knag laughed, and after that, cried.\n\n‘For fifteen years,’ exclaimed Miss Knag, sobbing in a most affecting manner, ‘for fifteen years have I been the credit and ornament of this room and the one upstairs. Thank God,’ said Miss Knag, stamping first her right foot and then her left with remarkable energy, ‘I have never in all that time, till now, been exposed to the arts, the vile arts, of a creature, who disgraces us with all her proceedings, and makes proper people blush for themselves. But I feel it, I do feel it, although I am disgusted.’\n\nMiss Knag here relapsed into softness, and the young ladies renewing their attentions, murmured that she ought to be superior to such things, and that for their part they despised them, and considered them beneath their notice; in witness whereof, they called out, more emphatically than before, that it was a shame, and that they felt so angry, they did, they hardly knew what to do with themselves.\n\n‘Have I lived to this day to be called a fright!’ cried Miss Knag, suddenly becoming convulsive, and making an effort to tear her front off.\n\n‘Oh no, no,’ replied the chorus, ‘pray don’t say so; don’t now!’\n\n‘Have I deserved to be called an elderly person?’ screamed Miss Knag, wrestling with the supernumeraries.\n\n‘Don’t think of such things, dear,’ answered the chorus.\n\n‘I hate her,’ cried Miss Knag; ‘I detest and hate her. Never let her speak to me again; never let anybody who is a friend of mine speak to her; a slut, a hussy, an impudent artful hussy!’ Having denounced the object of her wrath, in these terms, Miss Knag screamed once, hiccuped thrice, gurgled in her throat several times, slumbered, shivered, woke, came to, composed her head-dress, and declared herself quite well again.\n\nPoor Kate had regarded these proceedings, at first, in perfect bewilderment. She had then turned red and pale by turns, and once or twice essayed to speak; but, as the true motives of this altered behaviour developed themselves, she retired a few paces, and looked calmly on without deigning a reply. Nevertheless, although she walked proudly to her seat, and turned her back upon the group of little satellites who clustered round their ruling planet in the remotest corner of the room, she gave way, in secret, to some such bitter tears as would have gladdened Miss Knag’s inmost soul, if she could have seen them fall.\n\n# CHAPTER 19\n\nDescriptive of a Dinner at Mr. Ralph Nickleby’s, and of the Manner in which the Company entertained themselves, before Dinner, at Dinner, and after Dinner.\n\nThe bile and rancour of the worthy Miss Knag undergoing no diminution during the remainder of the week, but rather augmenting with every successive hour; and the honest ire of all the young ladies rising, or seeming to rise, in exact proportion to the good spinster’s indignation, and both waxing very hot every time Miss Nickleby was called upstairs; it will be readily imagined that that young lady’s daily life was none of the most cheerful or enviable kind. She hailed the arrival of Saturday night, as a prisoner would a few delicious hours’ respite from slow and wearing torture, and felt that the poor pittance for her first week’s labour would have been dearly and hardly earned, had its amount been trebled.\n\nWhen she joined her mother, as usual, at the street corner, she was not a little surprised to find her in conversation with Mr. Ralph Nickleby; but her surprise was soon redoubled, no less by the matter of their conversation, than by the smoothed and altered manner of Mr. Nickleby himself.\n\n‘Ah! my dear!’ said Ralph; ‘we were at that moment talking about you.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ replied Kate, shrinking, though she scarce knew why, from her uncle’s cold glistening eye.\n\n‘That instant,’ said Ralph. ‘I was coming to call for you, making sure to catch you before you left; but your mother and I have been talking over family affairs, and the time has slipped away so rapidly--’\n\n‘Well, now, hasn’t it?’ interposed Mrs. Nickleby, quite insensible to the sarcastic tone of Ralph’s last remark. ‘Upon my word, I couldn’t have believed it possible, that such a--Kate, my dear, you’re to dine with your uncle at half-past six o’clock tomorrow.’\n\nTriumphing in having been the first to communicate this extraordinary intelligence, Mrs. Nickleby nodded and smiled a great many times, to impress its full magnificence on Kate’s wondering mind, and then flew off, at an acute angle, to a committee of ways and means.\n\n‘Let me see,’ said the good lady. ‘Your black silk frock will be quite dress enough, my dear, with that pretty little scarf, and a plain band in your hair, and a pair of black silk stock--Dear, dear,’ cried Mrs Nickleby, flying off at another angle, ‘if I had but those unfortunate amethysts of mine--you recollect them, Kate, my love--how they used to sparkle, you know--but your papa, your poor dear papa--ah! there never was anything so cruelly sacrificed as those jewels were, never!’ Overpowered by this agonising thought, Mrs. Nickleby shook her head, in a melancholy manner, and applied her handkerchief to her eyes.\n\nI don’t want them, mama, indeed,’ said Kate. ‘Forget that you ever had them.’\n\n‘Lord, Kate, my dear,’ rejoined Mrs. Nickleby, pettishly, ‘how like a child you talk! Four-and-twenty silver tea-spoons, brother-in-law, two gravies, four salts, all the amethysts--necklace, brooch, and ear-rings--all made away with, at the same time, and I saying, almost on my bended knees, to that poor good soul, “Why don’t you do something, Nicholas? Why don’t you make some arrangement?” I am sure that anybody who was about us at that time, will do me the justice to own, that if I said that once, I said it fifty times a day. Didn’t I, Kate, my dear? Did I ever lose an opportunity of impressing it on your poor papa?’\n\n‘No, no, mama, never,’ replied Kate. And to do Mrs. Nickleby justice, she never had lost--and to do married ladies as a body justice, they seldom do lose--any occasion of inculcating similar golden percepts, whose only blemish is, the slight degree of vagueness and uncertainty in which they are usually enveloped.\n\n‘Ah!’ said Mrs. Nickleby, with great fervour, ‘if my advice had been taken at the beginning--Well, I have always done MY duty, and that’s some comfort.’\n\nWhen she had arrived at this reflection, Mrs. Nickleby sighed, rubbed her hands, cast up her eyes, and finally assumed a look of meek composure; thus importing that she was a persecuted saint, but that she wouldn’t trouble her hearers by mentioning a circumstance which must be so obvious to everybody.\n\n‘Now,’ said Ralph, with a smile, which, in common with all other tokens of emotion, seemed to skulk under his face, rather than play boldly over it--‘to return to the point from which we have strayed. I have a little party of--of--gentlemen with whom I am connected in business just now, at my house tomorrow; and your mother has promised that you shall keep house for me. I am not much used to parties; but this is one of business, and such fooleries are an important part of it sometimes. You don’t mind obliging me?’\n\n‘Mind!’ cried Mrs. Nickleby. ‘My dear Kate, why--’\n\n‘Pray,’ interrupted Ralph, motioning her to be silent. ‘I spoke to my niece.’\n\n‘I shall be very glad, of course, uncle,’ replied Kate; ‘but I am afraid you will find me awkward and embarrassed.’\n\n‘Oh no,’ said Ralph; ‘come when you like, in a hackney coach--I’ll pay for it. Good-night--a--a--God bless you.’\n\nThe blessing seemed to stick in Mr. Ralph Nickleby’s throat, as if it were not used to the thoroughfare, and didn’t know the way out. But it got out somehow, though awkwardly enough; and having disposed of it, he shook hands with his two relatives, and abruptly left them.\n\n‘What a very strongly marked countenance your uncle has!’ said Mrs Nickleby, quite struck with his parting look. ‘I don’t see the slightest resemblance to his poor brother.’\n\n‘Mama!’ said Kate reprovingly. ‘To think of such a thing!’\n\n‘No,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, musing. ‘There certainly is none. But it’s a very honest face.’\n\nThe worthy matron made this remark with great emphasis and elocution, as if it comprised no small quantity of ingenuity and research; and, in truth, it was not unworthy of being classed among the extraordinary discoveries of the age. Kate looked up hastily, and as hastily looked down again.\n\n‘What has come over you, my dear, in the name of goodness?’ asked Mrs Nickleby, when they had walked on, for some time, in silence.\n\n‘I was only thinking, mama,’ answered Kate.\n\n‘Thinking!’ repeated Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Ay, and indeed plenty to think about, too. Your uncle has taken a strong fancy to you, that’s quite clear; and if some extraordinary good fortune doesn’t come to you, after this, I shall be a little surprised, that’s all.’\n\nWith this she launched out into sundry anecdotes of young ladies, who had had thousand-pound notes given them in reticules, by eccentric uncles; and of young ladies who had accidentally met amiable gentlemen of enormous wealth at their uncles’ houses, and married them, after short but ardent courtships; and Kate, listening first in apathy, and afterwards in amusement, felt, as they walked home, something of her mother’s sanguine complexion gradually awakening in her own bosom, and began to think that her prospects might be brightening, and that better days might be dawning upon them. Such is hope, Heaven’s own gift to struggling mortals; pervading, like some subtle essence from the skies, all things, both good and bad; as universal as death, and more infectious than disease!\n\nThe feeble winter’s sun--and winter’s suns in the city are very feeble indeed--might have brightened up, as he shone through the dim windows of the large old house, on witnessing the unusual sight which one half-furnished room displayed. In a gloomy corner, where, for years, had stood a silent dusty pile of merchandise, sheltering its colony of mice, and frowning, a dull and lifeless mass, upon the panelled room, save when, responding to the roll of heavy waggons in the street without, it quaked with sturdy tremblings and caused the bright eyes of its tiny citizens to grow brighter still with fear, and struck them motionless, with attentive ear and palpitating heart, until the alarm had passed away--in this dark corner, was arranged, with scrupulous care, all Kate’s little finery for the day; each article of dress partaking of that indescribable air of jauntiness and individuality which empty garments--whether by association, or that they become moulded, as it were, to the owner’s form--will take, in eyes accustomed to, or picturing, the wearer’s smartness. In place of a bale of musty goods, there lay the black silk dress: the neatest possible figure in itself. The small shoes, with toes delicately turned out, stood upon the very pressure of some old iron weight; and a pile of harsh discoloured leather had unconsciously given place to the very same little pair of black silk stockings, which had been the objects of Mrs. Nickleby’s peculiar care. Rats and mice, and such small gear, had long ago been starved, or had emigrated to better quarters: and, in their stead, appeared gloves, bands, scarfs, hair-pins, and many other little devices, almost as ingenious in their way as rats and mice themselves, for the tantalisation of mankind. About and among them all, moved Kate herself, not the least beautiful or unwonted relief to the stern, old, gloomy building.\n\nIn good time, or in bad time, as the reader likes to take it--for Mrs Nickleby’s impatience went a great deal faster than the clocks at that end of the town, and Kate was dressed to the very last hair-pin a full hour and a half before it was at all necessary to begin to think about it--in good time, or in bad time, the toilet was completed; and it being at length the hour agreed upon for starting, the milkman fetched a coach from the nearest stand, and Kate, with many adieux to her mother, and many kind messages to Miss La Creevy, who was to come to tea, seated herself in it, and went away in state, if ever anybody went away in state in a hackney coach yet. And the coach, and the coachman, and the horses, rattled, and jangled, and whipped, and cursed, and swore, and tumbled on together, until they came to Golden Square.\n\nThe coachman gave a tremendous double knock at the door, which was opened long before he had done, as quickly as if there had been a man behind it, with his hand tied to the latch. Kate, who had expected no more uncommon appearance than Newman Noggs in a clean shirt, was not a little astonished to see that the opener was a man in handsome livery, and that there were two or three others in the hall. There was no doubt about its being the right house, however, for there was the name upon the door; so she accepted the laced coat-sleeve which was tendered her, and entering the house, was ushered upstairs, into a back drawing-room, where she was left alone.\n\nIf she had been surprised at the apparition of the footman, she was perfectly absorbed in amazement at the richness and splendour of the furniture. The softest and most elegant carpets, the most exquisite pictures, the costliest mirrors; articles of richest ornament, quite dazzling from their beauty and perplexing from the prodigality with which they were scattered around; encountered her on every side. The very staircase nearly down to the hall-door, was crammed with beautiful and luxurious things, as though the house were brimful of riches, which, with a very trifling addition, would fairly run over into the street.\n\nPresently, she heard a series of loud double knocks at the street-door, and after every knock some new voice in the next room; the tones of Mr Ralph Nickleby were easily distinguishable at first, but by degrees they merged into the general buzz of conversation, and all she could ascertain was, that there were several gentlemen with no very musical voices, who talked very loud, laughed very heartily, and swore more than she would have thought quite necessary. But this was a question of taste.\n\nAt length, the door opened, and Ralph himself, divested of his boots, and ceremoniously embellished with black silks and shoes, presented his crafty face.\n\n‘I couldn’t see you before, my dear,’ he said, in a low tone, and pointing, as he spoke, to the next room. ‘I was engaged in receiving them. Now--shall I take you in?’\n\n‘Pray, uncle,’ said Kate, a little flurried, as people much more conversant with society often are, when they are about to enter a room full of strangers, and have had time to think of it previously, ‘are there any ladies here?’\n\n‘No,’ said Ralph, shortly, ‘I don’t know any.’\n\n‘Must I go in immediately?’ asked Kate, drawing back a little.\n\n‘As you please,’ said Ralph, shrugging his shoulders. ‘They are all come, and dinner will be announced directly afterwards--that’s all.’\n\nKate would have entreated a few minutes’ respite, but reflecting that her uncle might consider the payment of the hackney-coach fare a sort of bargain for her punctuality, she suffered him to draw her arm through his, and to lead her away.\n\nSeven or eight gentlemen were standing round the fire when they went in, and, as they were talking very loud, were not aware of their entrance until Mr. Ralph Nickleby, touching one on the coat-sleeve, said in a harsh emphatic voice, as if to attract general attention--\n\n‘Lord Frederick Verisopht, my niece, Miss Nickleby.’\n\nThe group dispersed, as if in great surprise, and the gentleman addressed, turning round, exhibited a suit of clothes of the most superlative cut, a pair of whiskers of similar quality, a moustache, a head of hair, and a young face.\n\n‘Eh!’ said the gentleman. ‘What--the--deyvle!’\n\nWith which broken ejaculations, he fixed his glass in his eye, and stared at Miss Nickleby in great surprise.\n\n‘My niece, my lord,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Then my ears did not deceive me, and it’s not wa-a-x work,’ said his lordship. ‘How de do? I’m very happy.’ And then his lordship turned to another superlative gentleman, something older, something stouter, something redder in the face, and something longer upon town, and said in a loud whisper that the girl was ‘deyvlish pitty.’\n\n‘Introduce me, Nickleby,’ said this second gentleman, who was lounging with his back to the fire, and both elbows on the chimneypiece.\n\n‘Sir Mulberry Hawk,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Otherwise the most knowing card in the pa-ack, Miss Nickleby,’ said Lord Frederick Verisopht.\n\n‘Don’t leave me out, Nickleby,’ cried a sharp-faced gentleman, who was sitting on a low chair with a high back, reading the paper.\n\n‘Mr. Pyke,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Nor me, Nickleby,’ cried a gentleman with a flushed face and a flash air, from the elbow of Sir Mulberry Hawk.\n\n‘Mr. Pluck,’ said Ralph. Then wheeling about again, towards a gentleman with the neck of a stork and the legs of no animal in particular, Ralph introduced him as the Honourable Mr. Snobb; and a white-headed person at the table as Colonel Chowser. The colonel was in conversation with somebody, who appeared to be a make-weight, and was not introduced at all.\n\nThere were two circumstances which, in this early stage of the party, struck home to Kate’s bosom, and brought the blood tingling to her face. One was the flippant contempt with which the guests evidently regarded her uncle, and the other, the easy insolence of their manner towards herself. That the first symptom was very likely to lead to the aggravation of the second, it needed no great penetration to foresee. And here Mr. Ralph Nickleby had reckoned without his host; for however fresh from the country a young lady (by nature) may be, and however unacquainted with conventional behaviour, the chances are, that she will have quite as strong an innate sense of the decencies and proprieties of life as if she had run the gauntlet of a dozen London seasons--possibly a stronger one, for such senses have been known to blunt in this improving process.\n\nWhen Ralph had completed the ceremonial of introduction, he led his blushing niece to a seat. As he did so, he glanced warily round as though to assure himself of the impression which her unlooked-for appearance had created.\n\n‘An unexpected playsure, Nickleby,’ said Lord Frederick Verisopht, taking his glass out of his right eye, where it had, until now, done duty on Kate, and fixing it in his left, to bring it to bear on Ralph.\n\n‘Designed to surprise you, Lord Frederick,’ said Mr. Pluck.\n\n‘Not a bad idea,’ said his lordship, ‘and one that would almost warrant the addition of an extra two and a half per cent.’\n\n‘Nickleby,’ said Sir Mulberry Hawk, in a thick coarse voice, ‘take the hint, and tack it on the other five-and-twenty, or whatever it is, and give me half for the advice.’\n\nSir Mulberry garnished this speech with a hoarse laugh, and terminated it with a pleasant oath regarding Mr. Nickleby’s limbs, whereat Messrs Pyke and Pluck laughed consumedly.\n\nThese gentlemen had not yet quite recovered the jest, when dinner was announced, and then they were thrown into fresh ecstasies by a similar cause; for Sir Mulberry Hawk, in an excess of humour, shot dexterously past Lord Frederick Verisopht who was about to lead Kate downstairs, and drew her arm through his up to the elbow.\n\n‘No, damn it, Verisopht,’ said Sir Mulberry, ‘fair play’s a jewel, and Miss Nickleby and I settled the matter with our eyes ten minutes ago.’\n\n‘Ha, ha, ha!’ laughed the honourable Mr. Snobb, ‘very good, very good.’\n\nRendered additionally witty by this applause, Sir Mulberry Hawk leered upon his friends most facetiously, and led Kate downstairs with an air of familiarity, which roused in her gentle breast such burning indignation, as she felt it almost impossible to repress. Nor was the intensity of these feelings at all diminished, when she found herself placed at the top of the table, with Sir Mulberry Hawk and Lord Frederick Verisopht on either side.\n\n‘Oh, you’ve found your way into our neighbourhood, have you?’ said Sir Mulberry as his lordship sat down.\n\n‘Of course,’ replied Lord Frederick, fixing his eyes on Miss Nickleby, ‘how can you a-ask me?’\n\n‘Well, you attend to your dinner,’ said Sir Mulberry, ‘and don’t mind Miss Nickleby and me, for we shall prove very indifferent company, I dare say.’\n\n‘I wish you’d interfere here, Nickleby,’ said Lord Frederick.\n\n‘What is the matter, my lord?’ demanded Ralph from the bottom of the table, where he was supported by Messrs Pyke and Pluck.\n\n‘This fellow, Hawk, is monopolising your niece,’ said Lord Frederick.\n\n‘He has a tolerable share of everything that you lay claim to, my lord,’ said Ralph with a sneer.\n\n‘’Gad, so he has,’ replied the young man; ‘deyvle take me if I know which is master in my house, he or I.’\n\n‘I know,’ muttered Ralph.\n\n‘I think I shall cut him off with a shilling,’ said the young nobleman, jocosely.\n\n‘No, no, curse it,’ said Sir Mulberry. ‘When you come to the shilling--the last shilling--I’ll cut you fast enough; but till then, I’ll never leave you--you may take your oath of it.’\n\nThis sally (which was strictly founded on fact) was received with a general roar, above which, was plainly distinguishable the laughter of Mr. Pyke and Mr. Pluck, who were, evidently, Sir Mulberry’s toads in ordinary. Indeed, it was not difficult to see, that the majority of the company preyed upon the unfortunate young lord, who, weak and silly as he was, appeared by far the least vicious of the party. Sir Mulberry Hawk was remarkable for his tact in ruining, by himself and his creatures, young gentlemen of fortune--a genteel and elegant profession, of which he had undoubtedly gained the head. With all the boldness of an original genius, he had struck out an entirely new course of treatment quite opposed to the usual method; his custom being, when he had gained the ascendancy over those he took in hand, rather to keep them down than to give them their own way; and to exercise his vivacity upon them openly, and without reserve. Thus, he made them butts, in a double sense, and while he emptied them with great address, caused them to ring with sundry well-administered taps, for the diversion of society.\n\nThe dinner was as remarkable for the splendour and completeness of its appointments as the mansion itself, and the company were remarkable for doing it ample justice, in which respect Messrs Pyke and Pluck particularly signalised themselves; these two gentlemen eating of every dish, and drinking of every bottle, with a capacity and perseverance truly astonishing. They were remarkably fresh, too, notwithstanding their great exertions: for, on the appearance of the dessert, they broke out again, as if nothing serious had taken place since breakfast.\n\n‘Well,’ said Lord Frederick, sipping his first glass of port, ‘if this is a discounting dinner, all I have to say is, deyvle take me, if it wouldn’t be a good pla-an to get discount every day.’\n\n‘You’ll have plenty of it, in your time,’ returned Sir Mulberry Hawk; ‘Nickleby will tell you that.’\n\n‘What do you say, Nickleby?’ inquired the young man; ‘am I to be a good customer?’\n\n‘It depends entirely on circumstances, my lord,’ replied Ralph.\n\n‘On your lordship’s circumstances,’ interposed Colonel Chowser of the Militia--and the race-courses.\n\nThe gallant colonel glanced at Messrs Pyke and Pluck as if he thought they ought to laugh at his joke; but those gentlemen, being only engaged to laugh for Sir Mulberry Hawk, were, to his signal discomfiture, as grave as a pair of undertakers. To add to his defeat, Sir Mulberry, considering any such efforts an invasion of his peculiar privilege, eyed the offender steadily, through his glass, as if astonished at his presumption, and audibly stated his impression that it was an ‘infernal liberty,’ which being a hint to Lord Frederick, he put up HIS glass, and surveyed the object of censure as if he were some extraordinary wild animal then exhibiting for the first time. As a matter of course, Messrs Pyke and Pluck stared at the individual whom Sir Mulberry Hawk stared at; so, the poor colonel, to hide his confusion, was reduced to the necessity of holding his port before his right eye and affecting to scrutinise its colour with the most lively interest.\n\nAll this while, Kate had sat as silently as she could, scarcely daring to raise her eyes, lest they should encounter the admiring gaze of Lord Frederick Verisopht, or, what was still more embarrassing, the bold looks of his friend Sir Mulberry. The latter gentleman was obliging enough to direct general attention towards her.\n\n‘Here is Miss Nickleby,’ observed Sir Mulberry, ‘wondering why the deuce somebody doesn’t make love to her.’\n\n‘No, indeed,’ said Kate, looking hastily up, ‘I--’ and then she stopped, feeling it would have been better to have said nothing at all.\n\n‘I’ll hold any man fifty pounds,’ said Sir Mulberry, ‘that Miss Nickleby can’t look in my face, and tell me she wasn’t thinking so.’\n\n‘Done!’ cried the noble gull. ‘Within ten minutes.’\n\n‘Done!’ responded Sir Mulberry. The money was produced on both sides, and the Honourable Mr. Snobb was elected to the double office of stake-holder and time-keeper.\n\n‘Pray,’ said Kate, in great confusion, while these preliminaries were in course of completion. ‘Pray do not make me the subject of any bets. Uncle, I cannot really--’\n\n‘Why not, my dear?’ replied Ralph, in whose grating voice, however, there was an unusual huskiness, as though he spoke unwillingly, and would rather that the proposition had not been broached. ‘It is done in a moment; there is nothing in it. If the gentlemen insist on it--’\n\n‘I don’t insist on it,’ said Sir Mulberry, with a loud laugh. ‘That is, I by no means insist upon Miss Nickleby’s making the denial, for if she does, I lose; but I shall be glad to see her bright eyes, especially as she favours the mahogany so much.’\n\n‘So she does, and it’s too ba-a-d of you, Miss Nickleby,’ said the noble youth.\n\n‘Quite cruel,’ said Mr. Pyke.\n\n‘Horrid cruel,’ said Mr. Pluck.\n\n‘I don’t care if I do lose,’ said Sir Mulberry; ‘for one tolerable look at Miss Nickleby’s eyes is worth double the money.’\n\n‘More,’ said Mr. Pyke.\n\n‘Far more,’ said Mr. Pluck.\n\n‘How goes the enemy, Snobb?’ asked Sir Mulberry Hawk.\n\n‘Four minutes gone.’\n\n‘Bravo!’\n\n‘Won’t you ma-ake one effort for me, Miss Nickleby?’ asked Lord Frederick, after a short interval.\n\n‘You needn’t trouble yourself to inquire, my buck,’ said Sir Mulberry; ‘Miss Nickleby and I understand each other; she declares on my side, and shows her taste. You haven’t a chance, old fellow. Time, Snobb?’\n\n‘Eight minutes gone.’\n\n‘Get the money ready,’ said Sir Mulberry; ‘you’ll soon hand over.’\n\n‘Ha, ha, ha!’ laughed Mr. Pyke.\n\nMr. Pluck, who always came second, and topped his companion if he could, screamed outright.\n\nThe poor girl, who was so overwhelmed with confusion that she scarcely knew what she did, had determined to remain perfectly quiet; but fearing that by so doing she might seem to countenance Sir Mulberry’s boast, which had been uttered with great coarseness and vulgarity of manner, raised her eyes, and looked him in the face. There was something so odious, so insolent, so repulsive in the look which met her, that, without the power to stammer forth a syllable, she rose and hurried from the room. She restrained her tears by a great effort until she was alone upstairs, and then gave them vent.\n\n‘Capital!’ said Sir Mulberry Hawk, putting the stakes in his pocket.\n\n‘That’s a girl of spirit, and we’ll drink her health.’\n\nIt is needless to say, that Pyke and Co. responded, with great warmth of manner, to this proposal, or that the toast was drunk with many little insinuations from the firm, relative to the completeness of Sir Mulberry’s conquest. Ralph, who, while the attention of the other guests was attracted to the principals in the preceding scene, had eyed them like a wolf, appeared to breathe more freely now his niece was gone; the decanters passing quickly round, he leaned back in his chair, and turned his eyes from speaker to speaker, as they warmed with wine, with looks that seemed to search their hearts, and lay bare, for his distempered sport, every idle thought within them.\n\nMeanwhile Kate, left wholly to herself, had, in some degree, recovered her composure. She had learnt from a female attendant, that her uncle wished to see her before she left, and had also gleaned the satisfactory intelligence, that the gentlemen would take coffee at table. The prospect of seeing them no more, contributed greatly to calm her agitation, and, taking up a book, she composed herself to read.\n\nShe started sometimes, when the sudden opening of the dining-room door let loose a wild shout of noisy revelry, and more than once rose in great alarm, as a fancied footstep on the staircase impressed her with the fear that some stray member of the party was returning alone. Nothing occurring, however, to realise her apprehensions, she endeavoured to fix her attention more closely on her book, in which by degrees she became so much interested, that she had read on through several chapters without heed of time or place, when she was terrified by suddenly hearing her name pronounced by a man’s voice close at her ear.\n\nThe book fell from her hand. Lounging on an ottoman close beside her, was Sir Mulberry Hawk, evidently the worse--if a man be a ruffian at heart, he is never the better--for wine.\n\n‘What a delightful studiousness!’ said this accomplished gentleman. ‘Was it real, now, or only to display the eyelashes?’\n\nKate, looking anxiously towards the door, made no reply.\n\n‘I have looked at ‘em for five minutes,’ said Sir Mulberry. ‘Upon my soul, they’re perfect. Why did I speak, and destroy such a pretty little picture?’\n\n‘Do me the favour to be silent now, sir,’ replied Kate.\n\n‘No, don’t,’ said Sir Mulberry, folding his crushed hat to lay his elbow on, and bringing himself still closer to the young lady; ‘upon my life, you oughtn’t to. Such a devoted slave of yours, Miss Nickleby--it’s an infernal thing to treat him so harshly, upon my soul it is.’\n\n‘I wish you to understand, sir,’ said Kate, trembling in spite of herself, but speaking with great indignation, ‘that your behaviour offends and disgusts me. If you have a spark of gentlemanly feeling remaining, you will leave me.’\n\n‘Now why,’ said Sir Mulberry, ‘why will you keep up this appearance of excessive rigour, my sweet creature? Now, be more natural--my dear Miss Nickleby, be more natural--do.’\n\nKate hastily rose; but as she rose, Sir Mulberry caught her dress, and forcibly detained her.\n\n‘Let me go, sir,’ she cried, her heart swelling with anger. ‘Do you hear? Instantly--this moment.’\n\n‘Sit down, sit down,’ said Sir Mulberry; ‘I want to talk to you.’\n\n‘Unhand me, sir, this instant,’ cried Kate.\n\n‘Not for the world,’ rejoined Sir Mulberry. Thus speaking, he leaned over, as if to replace her in her chair; but the young lady, making a violent effort to disengage herself, he lost his balance, and measured his length upon the ground. As Kate sprung forward to leave the room, Mr Ralph Nickleby appeared in the doorway, and confronted her.\n\n‘What is this?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘It is this, sir,’ replied Kate, violently agitated: ‘that beneath the roof where I, a helpless girl, your dead brother’s child, should most have found protection, I have been exposed to insult which should make you shrink to look upon me. Let me pass you.’\n\nRalph DID shrink, as the indignant girl fixed her kindling eye upon him; but he did not comply with her injunction, nevertheless: for he led her to a distant seat, and returning, and approaching Sir Mulberry Hawk, who had by this time risen, motioned towards the door.\n\n‘Your way lies there, sir,’ said Ralph, in a suppressed voice, that some devil might have owned with pride.\n\n‘What do you mean by that?’ demanded his friend, fiercely.\n\nThe swoln veins stood out like sinews on Ralph’s wrinkled forehead, and the nerves about his mouth worked as though some unendurable emotion wrung them; but he smiled disdainfully, and again pointed to the door.\n\n‘Do you know me, you old madman?’ asked Sir Mulberry.\n\n‘Well,’ said Ralph. The fashionable vagabond for the moment quite quailed under the steady look of the older sinner, and walked towards the door, muttering as he went.\n\n‘You wanted the lord, did you?’ he said, stopping short when he reached the door, as if a new light had broken in upon him, and confronting Ralph again. ‘Damme, I was in the way, was I?’\n\nRalph smiled again, but made no answer.\n\n‘Who brought him to you first?’ pursued Sir Mulberry; ‘and how, without me, could you ever have wound him in your net as you have?’\n\n‘The net is a large one, and rather full,’ said Ralph. ‘Take care that it chokes nobody in the meshes.’\n\n‘You would sell your flesh and blood for money; yourself, if you have not already made a bargain with the devil,’ retorted the other. ‘Do you mean to tell me that your pretty niece was not brought here as a decoy for the drunken boy downstairs?’\n\nAlthough this hurried dialogue was carried on in a suppressed tone on both sides, Ralph looked involuntarily round to ascertain that Kate had not moved her position so as to be within hearing. His adversary saw the advantage he had gained, and followed it up.\n\n‘Do you mean to tell me,’ he asked again, ‘that it is not so? Do you mean to say that if he had found his way up here instead of me, you wouldn’t have been a little more blind, and a little more deaf, and a little less flourishing, than you have been? Come, Nickleby, answer me that.’\n\n‘I tell you this,’ replied Ralph, ‘that if I brought her here, as a matter of business--’\n\n‘Ay, that’s the word,’ interposed Sir Mulberry, with a laugh. ‘You’re coming to yourself again now.’\n\n‘--As a matter of business,’ pursued Ralph, speaking slowly and firmly, as a man who has made up his mind to say no more, ‘because I thought she might make some impression on the silly youth you have taken in hand and are lending good help to ruin, I knew--knowing him--that it would be long before he outraged her girl’s feelings, and that unless he offended by mere puppyism and emptiness, he would, with a little management, respect the sex and conduct even of his usurer’s niece. But if I thought to draw him on more gently by this device, I did not think of subjecting the girl to the licentiousness and brutality of so old a hand as you. And now we understand each other.’\n\n‘Especially as there was nothing to be got by it--eh?’ sneered Sir Mulberry.\n\n‘Exactly so,’ said Ralph. He had turned away, and looked over his shoulder to make this last reply. The eyes of the two worthies met, with an expression as if each rascal felt that there was no disguising himself from the other; and Sir Mulberry Hawk shrugged his shoulders and walked slowly out.\n\nHis friend closed the door, and looked restlessly towards the spot where his niece still remained in the attitude in which he had left her. She had flung herself heavily upon the couch, and with her head drooping over the cushion, and her face hidden in her hands, seemed to be still weeping in an agony of shame and grief.\n\nRalph would have walked into any poverty-stricken debtor’s house, and pointed him out to a bailiff, though in attendance upon a young child’s death-bed, without the smallest concern, because it would have been a matter quite in the ordinary course of business, and the man would have been an offender against his only code of morality. But, here was a young girl, who had done no wrong save that of coming into the world alive; who had patiently yielded to all his wishes; who had tried hard to please him--above all, who didn’t owe him money--and he felt awkward and nervous.\n\nRalph took a chair at some distance; then, another chair a little nearer; then, moved a little nearer still; then, nearer again, and finally sat himself on the same sofa, and laid his hand on Kate’s arm.\n\n‘Hush, my dear!’ he said, as she drew it back, and her sobs burst out afresh. ‘Hush, hush! Don’t mind it, now; don’t think of it.’\n\n‘Oh, for pity’s sake, let me go home,’ cried Kate. ‘Let me leave this house, and go home.’\n\n‘Yes, yes,’ said Ralph. ‘You shall. But you must dry your eyes first, and compose yourself. Let me raise your head. There--there.’\n\n‘Oh, uncle!’ exclaimed Kate, clasping her hands. ‘What have I done--what have I done--that you should subject me to this? If I had wronged you in thought, or word, or deed, it would have been most cruel to me, and the memory of one you must have loved in some old time; but--’\n\n‘Only listen to me for a moment,’ interrupted Ralph, seriously alarmed by the violence of her emotions. ‘I didn’t know it would be so; it was impossible for me to foresee it. I did all I could.--Come, let us walk about. You are faint with the closeness of the room, and the heat of these lamps. You will be better now, if you make the slightest effort.’\n\n‘I will do anything,’ replied Kate, ‘if you will only send me home.’\n\n‘Well, well, I will,’ said Ralph; ‘but you must get back your own looks; for those you have, will frighten them, and nobody must know of this but you and I. Now let us walk the other way. There. You look better even now.’\n\nWith such encouragements as these, Ralph Nickleby walked to and fro, with his niece leaning on his arm; actually trembling beneath her touch.\n\nIn the same manner, when he judged it prudent to allow her to depart, he supported her downstairs, after adjusting her shawl and performing such little offices, most probably for the first time in his life. Across the hall, and down the steps, Ralph led her too; nor did he withdraw his hand until she was seated in the coach.\n\nAs the door of the vehicle was roughly closed, a comb fell from Kate’s hair, close at her uncle’s feet; and as he picked it up, and returned it into her hand, the light from a neighbouring lamp shone upon her face. The lock of hair that had escaped and curled loosely over her brow, the traces of tears yet scarcely dry, the flushed cheek, the look of sorrow, all fired some dormant train of recollection in the old man’s breast; and the face of his dead brother seemed present before him, with the very look it bore on some occasion of boyish grief, of which every minutest circumstance flashed upon his mind, with the distinctness of a scene of yesterday.\n\nRalph Nickleby, who was proof against all appeals of blood and kindred--who was steeled against every tale of sorrow and distress--staggered while he looked, and went back into his house, as a man who had seen a spirit from some world beyond the grave.\n\n# CHAPTER 20\n\nWherein Nicholas at length encounters his Uncle, to whom he expresses his Sentiments with much Candour. His Resolution.\nLittle Miss La Creevy trotted briskly through divers streets at the west end of the town, early on Monday morning--the day after the dinner--charged with the important commission of acquainting Madame Mantalini that Miss Nickleby was too unwell to attend that day, but hoped to be enabled to resume her duties on the morrow. And as Miss La Creevy walked along, revolving in her mind various genteel forms and elegant turns of expression, with a view to the selection of the very best in which to couch her communication, she cogitated a good deal upon the probable causes of her young friend’s indisposition.\n\n‘I don’t know what to make of it,’ said Miss La Creevy. ‘Her eyes were decidedly red last night. She said she had a headache; headaches don’t occasion red eyes. She must have been crying.’\n\nArriving at this conclusion, which, indeed, she had established to her perfect satisfaction on the previous evening, Miss La Creevy went on to consider--as she had done nearly all night--what new cause of unhappiness her young friend could possibly have had.\n\n‘I can’t think of anything,’ said the little portrait painter. ‘Nothing at all, unless it was the behaviour of that old bear. Cross to her, I suppose? Unpleasant brute!’\n\nRelieved by this expression of opinion, albeit it was vented upon empty air, Miss La Creevy trotted on to Madame Mantalini’s; and being informed that the governing power was not yet out of bed, requested an interview with the second in command; whereupon Miss Knag appeared.\n\n‘So far as I am concerned,’ said Miss Knag, when the message had been delivered, with many ornaments of speech; ‘I could spare Miss Nickleby for evermore.’\n\n‘Oh, indeed, ma’am!’ rejoined Miss La Creevy, highly offended. ‘But, you see, you are not mistress of the business, and therefore it’s of no great consequence.’\n\n‘Very good, ma’am,’ said Miss Knag. ‘Have you any further commands for me?’\n\n‘No, I have not, ma’am,’ rejoined Miss La Creevy.\n\n‘Then good-morning, ma’am,’ said Miss Knag.\n\n‘Good-morning to you, ma’am; and many obligations for your extreme politeness and good breeding,’ rejoined Miss La Creevy.\n\nThus terminating the interview, during which both ladies had trembled very much, and been marvellously polite--certain indications that they were within an inch of a very desperate quarrel--Miss La Creevy bounced out of the room, and into the street.\n\n‘I wonder who that is,’ said the queer little soul. ‘A nice person to know, I should think! I wish I had the painting of her: I’D do her justice.’ So, feeling quite satisfied that she had said a very cutting thing at Miss Knag’s expense, Miss La Creevy had a hearty laugh, and went home to breakfast in great good humour.\n\nHere was one of the advantages of having lived alone so long! The little bustling, active, cheerful creature existed entirely within herself, talked to herself, made a confidante of herself, was as sarcastic as she could be, on people who offended her, by herself; pleased herself, and did no harm. If she indulged in scandal, nobody’s reputation suffered; and if she enjoyed a little bit of revenge, no living soul was one atom the worse. One of the many to whom, from straitened circumstances, a consequent inability to form the associations they would wish, and a disinclination to mix with the society they could obtain, London is as complete a solitude as the plains of Syria, the humble artist had pursued her lonely, but contented way for many years; and, until the peculiar misfortunes of the Nickleby family attracted her attention, had made no friends, though brimful of the friendliest feelings to all mankind. There are many warm hearts in the same solitary guise as poor little Miss La Creevy’s.\n\nHowever, that’s neither here nor there, just now. She went home to breakfast, and had scarcely caught the full flavour of her first sip of tea, when the servant announced a gentleman, whereat Miss La Creevy, at once imagining a new sitter transfixed by admiration at the street-door case, was in unspeakable consternation at the presence of the tea-things.\n\n‘Here, take ‘em away; run with ‘em into the bedroom; anywhere,’ said Miss La Creevy. ‘Dear, dear; to think that I should be late on this particular morning, of all others, after being ready for three weeks by half-past eight o’clock, and not a soul coming near the place!’\n\n‘Don’t let me put you out of the way,’ said a voice Miss La Creevy knew. ‘I told the servant not to mention my name, because I wished to surprise you.’\n\n‘Mr. Nicholas!’ cried Miss La Creevy, starting in great astonishment. ‘You have not forgotten me, I see,’ replied Nicholas, extending his hand.\n\n‘Why, I think I should even have known you if I had met you in the street,’ said Miss La Creevy, with a smile. ‘Hannah, another cup and saucer. Now, I’ll tell you what, young man; I’ll trouble you not to repeat the impertinence you were guilty of, on the morning you went away.’\n\n‘You would not be very angry, would you?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘Wouldn’t I!’ said Miss La Creevy. ‘You had better try; that’s all!’\n\nNicholas, with becoming gallantry, immediately took Miss La Creevy at her word, who uttered a faint scream and slapped his face; but it was not a very hard slap, and that’s the truth.\n\n‘I never saw such a rude creature!’ exclaimed Miss La Creevy.\n\n‘You told me to try,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Well; but I was speaking ironically,’ rejoined Miss La Creevy.\n\n‘Oh! that’s another thing,’ said Nicholas; ‘you should have told me that, too.’\n\n‘I dare say you didn’t know, indeed!’ retorted Miss La Creevy. ‘But, now I look at you again, you seem thinner than when I saw you last, and your face is haggard and pale. And how come you to have left Yorkshire?’\n\nShe stopped here; for there was so much heart in her altered tone and manner, that Nicholas was quite moved.\n\n‘I need look somewhat changed,’ he said, after a short silence; ‘for I have undergone some suffering, both of mind and body, since I left London. I have been very poor, too, and have even suffered from want.’\n\n‘Good Heaven, Mr. Nicholas!’ exclaimed Miss La Creevy, ‘what are you telling me?’\n\n‘Nothing which need distress you quite so much,’ answered Nicholas, with a more sprightly air; ‘neither did I come here to bewail my lot, but on matter more to the purpose. I wish to meet my uncle face to face. I should tell you that first.’\n\n‘Then all I have to say about that is,’ interposed Miss La Creevy, ‘that I don’t envy you your taste; and that sitting in the same room with his very boots, would put me out of humour for a fortnight.’\n\n‘In the main,’ said Nicholas, ‘there may be no great difference of opinion between you and me, so far; but you will understand, that I desire to confront him, to justify myself, and to cast his duplicity and malice in his throat.’\n\n‘That’s quite another matter,’ rejoined Miss La Creevy. ‘Heaven forgive me; but I shouldn’t cry my eyes quite out of my head, if they choked him. Well?’\n\n‘To this end, I called upon him this morning,’ said Nicholas. ‘He only returned to town on Saturday, and I knew nothing of his arrival until late last night.’\n\n‘And did you see him?’ asked Miss La Creevy.\n\n‘No,’ replied Nicholas. ‘He had gone out.’\n\n‘Hah!’ said Miss La Creevy; ‘on some kind, charitable business, I dare say.’\n\n‘I have reason to believe,’ pursued Nicholas, ‘from what has been told me, by a friend of mine who is acquainted with his movements, that he intends seeing my mother and sister today, and giving them his version of the occurrences that have befallen me. I will meet him there.’\n\n‘That’s right,’ said Miss La Creevy, rubbing her hands. ‘And yet, I don’t know,’ she added, ‘there is much to be thought of--others to be considered.’\n\n‘I have considered others,’ rejoined Nicholas; ‘but as honesty and honour are both at issue, nothing shall deter me.’\n\n‘You should know best,’ said Miss La Creevy.\n\n‘In this case I hope so,’ answered Nicholas. ‘And all I want you to do for me, is, to prepare them for my coming. They think me a long way off, and if I went wholly unexpected, I should frighten them. If you can spare time to tell them that you have seen me, and that I shall be with them in a quarter of an hour afterwards, you will do me a great service.’\n\n‘I wish I could do you, or any of you, a greater,’ said Miss La Creevy; ‘but the power to serve, is as seldom joined with the will, as the will is with the power, I think.’\n\nTalking on very fast and very much, Miss La Creevy finished her breakfast with great expedition, put away the tea-caddy and hid the key under the fender, resumed her bonnet, and, taking Nicholas’s arm, sallied forth at once to the city. Nicholas left her near the door of his mother’s house, and promised to return within a quarter of an hour.\n\nIt so chanced that Ralph Nickleby, at length seeing fit, for his own purposes, to communicate the atrocities of which Nicholas had been guilty, had (instead of first proceeding to another quarter of the town on business, as Newman Noggs supposed he would) gone straight to his sister-in-law. Hence, when Miss La Creevy, admitted by a girl who was cleaning the house, made her way to the sitting-room, she found Mrs Nickleby and Kate in tears, and Ralph just concluding his statement of his nephew’s misdemeanours. Kate beckoned her not to retire, and Miss La Creevy took a seat in silence.\n\n‘You are here already, are you, my gentleman?’ thought the little woman. ‘Then he shall announce himself, and see what effect that has on you.’\n\n‘This is pretty,’ said Ralph, folding up Miss Squeers’s note; ‘very pretty. I recommend him--against all my previous conviction, for I knew he would never do any good--to a man with whom, behaving himself properly, he might have remained, in comfort, for years. What is the result? Conduct for which he might hold up his hand at the Old Bailey.’\n\n‘I never will believe it,’ said Kate, indignantly; ‘never. It is some base conspiracy, which carries its own falsehood with it.’\n\n‘My dear,’ said Ralph, ‘you wrong the worthy man. These are not inventions. The man is assaulted, your brother is not to be found; this boy, of whom they speak, goes with him--remember, remember.’\n\n‘It is impossible,’ said Kate. ‘Nicholas!--and a thief too! Mama, how can you sit and hear such statements?’\n\nPoor Mrs. Nickleby, who had, at no time, been remarkable for the possession of a very clear understanding, and who had been reduced by the late changes in her affairs to a most complicated state of perplexity, made no other reply to this earnest remonstrance than exclaiming from behind a mass of pocket-handkerchief, that she never could have believed it--thereby most ingeniously leaving her hearers to suppose that she did believe it.\n\n‘It would be my duty, if he came in my way, to deliver him up to justice,’ said Ralph, ‘my bounden duty; I should have no other course, as a man of the world and a man of business, to pursue. And yet,’ said Ralph, speaking in a very marked manner, and looking furtively, but fixedly, at Kate, ‘and yet I would not. I would spare the feelings of his--of his sister. And his mother of course,’ added Ralph, as though by an afterthought, and with far less emphasis.\n\nKate very well understood that this was held out as an additional inducement to her to preserve the strictest silence regarding the events of the preceding night. She looked involuntarily towards Ralph as he ceased to speak, but he had turned his eyes another way, and seemed for the moment quite unconscious of her presence.\n\n‘Everything,’ said Ralph, after a long silence, broken only by Mrs Nickleby’s sobs, ‘everything combines to prove the truth of this letter, if indeed there were any possibility of disputing it. Do innocent men steal away from the sight of honest folks, and skulk in hiding-places, like outlaws? Do innocent men inveigle nameless vagabonds, and prowl with them about the country as idle robbers do? Assault, riot, theft, what do you call these?’\n\n‘A lie!’ cried a voice, as the door was dashed open, and Nicholas came into the room.\n\nIn the first moment of surprise, and possibly of alarm, Ralph rose from his seat, and fell back a few paces, quite taken off his guard by this unexpected apparition. In another moment, he stood, fixed and immovable with folded arms, regarding his nephew with a scowl; while Kate and Miss La Creevy threw themselves between the two, to prevent the personal violence which the fierce excitement of Nicholas appeared to threaten.\n\n‘Dear Nicholas,’ cried his sister, clinging to him. ‘Be calm, consider--’\n\n‘Consider, Kate!’ cried Nicholas, clasping her hand so tight in the tumult of his anger, that she could scarcely bear the pain. ‘When I consider all, and think of what has passed, I need be made of iron to stand before him.’\n\n‘Or bronze,’ said Ralph, quietly; ‘there is not hardihood enough in flesh and blood to face it out.’\n\n‘Oh dear, dear!’ cried Mrs. Nickleby, ‘that things should have come to such a pass as this!’\n\n‘Who speaks in a tone, as if I had done wrong, and brought disgrace on them?’ said Nicholas, looking round.\n\n‘Your mother, sir,’ replied Ralph, motioning towards her.\n\n‘Whose ears have been poisoned by you,’ said Nicholas; ‘by you--who, under pretence of deserving the thanks she poured upon you, heaped every insult, wrong, and indignity upon my head. You, who sent me to a den where sordid cruelty, worthy of yourself, runs wanton, and youthful misery stalks precocious; where the lightness of childhood shrinks into the heaviness of age, and its every promise blights, and withers as it grows. I call Heaven to witness,’ said Nicholas, looking eagerly round, ‘that I have seen all this, and that he knows it.’\n\n‘Refute these calumnies,’ said Kate, ‘and be more patient, so that you may give them no advantage. Tell us what you really did, and show that they are untrue.’\n\n‘Of what do they--or of what does he--accuse me?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘First, of attacking your master, and being within an ace of qualifying yourself to be tried for murder,’ interposed Ralph. ‘I speak plainly, young man, bluster as you will.’\n\n‘I interfered,’ said Nicholas, ‘to save a miserable creature from the vilest cruelty. In so doing, I inflicted such punishment upon a wretch as he will not readily forget, though far less than he deserved from me. If the same scene were renewed before me now, I would take the same part; but I would strike harder and heavier, and brand him with such marks as he should carry to his grave, go to it when he would.’\n\n‘You hear?’ said Ralph, turning to Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Penitence, this!’\n\n‘Oh dear me!’ cried Mrs. Nickleby, ‘I don’t know what to think, I really don’t.’\n\n‘Do not speak just now, mama, I entreat you,’ said Kate. ‘Dear Nicholas, I only tell you, that you may know what wickedness can prompt, but they accuse you of--a ring is missing, and they dare to say that--’\n\n‘The woman,’ said Nicholas, haughtily, ‘the wife of the fellow from whom these charges come, dropped--as I suppose--a worthless ring among some clothes of mine, early in the morning on which I left the house. At least, I know that she was in the bedroom where they lay, struggling with an unhappy child, and that I found it when I opened my bundle on the road. I returned it, at once, by coach, and they have it now.’\n\n‘I knew, I knew,’ said Kate, looking towards her uncle. ‘About this boy, love, in whose company they say you left?’\n\n‘The boy, a silly, helpless creature, from brutality and hard usage, is with me now,’ rejoined Nicholas.\n\n‘You hear?’ said Ralph, appealing to the mother again, ‘everything proved, even upon his own confession. Do you choose to restore that boy, sir?’\n\n‘No, I do not,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘You do not?’ sneered Ralph.\n\n‘No,’ repeated Nicholas, ‘not to the man with whom I found him. I would that I knew on whom he has the claim of birth: I might wring something from his sense of shame, if he were dead to every tie of nature.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ said Ralph. ‘Now, sir, will you hear a word or two from me?’\n\n‘You can speak when and what you please,’ replied Nicholas, embracing his sister. ‘I take little heed of what you say or threaten.’\n\n‘Mighty well, sir,’ retorted Ralph; ‘but perhaps it may concern others, who may think it worth their while to listen, and consider what I tell them. I will address your mother, sir, who knows the world.’\n\n‘Ah! and I only too dearly wish I didn’t,’ sobbed Mrs. Nickleby.\n\nThere really was no necessity for the good lady to be much distressed upon this particular head; the extent of her worldly knowledge being, to say the least, very questionable; and so Ralph seemed to think, for he smiled as she spoke. He then glanced steadily at her and Nicholas by turns, as he delivered himself in these words:\n\n‘Of what I have done, or what I meant to do, for you, ma’am, and my niece, I say not one syllable. I held out no promise, and leave you to judge for yourself. I hold out no threat now, but I say that this boy, headstrong, wilful and disorderly as he is, should not have one penny of my money, or one crust of my bread, or one grasp of my hand, to save him from the loftiest gallows in all Europe. I will not meet him, come where he comes, or hear his name. I will not help him, or those who help him. With a full knowledge of what he brought upon you by so doing, he has come back in his selfish sloth, to be an aggravation of your wants, and a burden upon his sister’s scanty wages. I regret to leave you, and more to leave her, now, but I will not encourage this compound of meanness and cruelty, and, as I will not ask you to renounce him, I see you no more.’\n\nIf Ralph had not known and felt his power in wounding those he hated, his glances at Nicholas would have shown it him, in all its force, as he proceeded in the above address. Innocent as the young man was of all wrong, every artful insinuation stung, every well-considered sarcasm cut him to the quick; and when Ralph noted his pale face and quivering lip, he hugged himself to mark how well he had chosen the taunts best calculated to strike deep into a young and ardent spirit.\n\n‘I can’t help it,’ cried Mrs. Nickleby. ‘I know you have been very good to us, and meant to do a good deal for my dear daughter. I am quite sure of that; I know you did, and it was very kind of you, having her at your house and all--and of course it would have been a great thing for her and for me too. But I can’t, you know, brother-in-law, I can’t renounce my own son, even if he has done all you say he has--it’s not possible; I couldn’t do it; so we must go to rack and ruin, Kate, my dear. I can bear it, I dare say.’ Pouring forth these and a perfectly wonderful train of other disjointed expressions of regret, which no mortal power but Mrs. Nickleby’s could ever have strung together, that lady wrung her hands, and her tears fell faster.\n\n‘Why do you say “IF Nicholas has done what they say he has,” mama?’ asked Kate, with honest anger. ‘You know he has not.’\n\n‘I don’t know what to think, one way or other, my dear,’ said Mrs Nickleby; ‘Nicholas is so violent, and your uncle has so much composure, that I can only hear what he says, and not what Nicholas does. Never mind, don’t let us talk any more about it. We can go to the Workhouse, or the Refuge for the Destitute, or the Magdalen Hospital, I dare say; and the sooner we go the better.’ With this extraordinary jumble of charitable institutions, Mrs. Nickleby again gave way to her tears.\n\n‘Stay,’ said Nicholas, as Ralph turned to go. ‘You need not leave this place, sir, for it will be relieved of my presence in one minute, and it will be long, very long, before I darken these doors again.’\n\n‘Nicholas,’ cried Kate, throwing herself on her brother’s shoulder, ‘do not say so. My dear brother, you will break my heart. Mama, speak to him. Do not mind her, Nicholas; she does not mean it, you should know her better. Uncle, somebody, for Heaven’s sake speak to him.’\n\n‘I never meant, Kate,’ said Nicholas, tenderly, ‘I never meant to stay among you; think better of me than to suppose it possible. I may turn my back on this town a few hours sooner than I intended, but what of that? We shall not forget each other apart, and better days will come when we shall part no more. Be a woman, Kate,’ he whispered, proudly, ‘and do not make me one, while HE looks on.’\n\n‘No, no, I will not,’ said Kate, eagerly, ‘but you will not leave us. Oh! think of all the happy days we have had together, before these terrible misfortunes came upon us; of all the comfort and happiness of home, and the trials we have to bear now; of our having no protector under all the slights and wrongs that poverty so much favours, and you cannot leave us to bear them alone, without one hand to help us.’\n\n‘You will be helped when I am away,’ replied Nicholas hurriedly. ‘I am no help to you, no protector; I should bring you nothing but sorrow, and want, and suffering. My own mother sees it, and her fondness and fears for you, point to the course that I should take. And so all good angels bless you, Kate, till I can carry you to some home of mine, where we may revive the happiness denied to us now, and talk of these trials as of things gone by. Do not keep me here, but let me go at once. There. Dear girl--dear girl.’\n\nThe grasp which had detained him relaxed, and Kate swooned in his arms. Nicholas stooped over her for a few seconds, and placing her gently in a chair, confided her to their honest friend.\n\n‘I need not entreat your sympathy,’ he said, wringing her hand, ‘for I know your nature. You will never forget them.’\n\nHe stepped up to Ralph, who remained in the same attitude which he had preserved throughout the interview, and moved not a finger.\n\n‘Whatever step you take, sir,’ he said, in a voice inaudible beyond themselves, ‘I shall keep a strict account of. I leave them to you, at your desire. There will be a day of reckoning sooner or later, and it will be a heavy one for you if they are wronged.’\n\nRalph did not allow a muscle of his face to indicate that he heard one word of this parting address. He hardly knew that it was concluded, and Mrs. Nickleby had scarcely made up her mind to detain her son by force if necessary, when Nicholas was gone.\n\nAs he hurried through the streets to his obscure lodging, seeking to keep pace, as it were, with the rapidity of the thoughts which crowded upon him, many doubts and hesitations arose in his mind, and almost tempted him to return. But what would they gain by this? Supposing he were to put Ralph Nickleby at defiance, and were even fortunate enough to obtain some small employment, his being with them could only render their present condition worse, and might greatly impair their future prospects; for his mother had spoken of some new kindnesses towards Kate which she had not denied. ‘No,’ thought Nicholas, ‘I have acted for the best.’\n\nBut, before he had gone five hundred yards, some other and different feeling would come upon him, and then he would lag again, and pulling his hat over his eyes, give way to the melancholy reflections which pressed thickly upon him. To have committed no fault, and yet to be so entirely alone in the world; to be separated from the only persons he loved, and to be proscribed like a criminal, when six months ago he had been surrounded by every comfort, and looked up to, as the chief hope of his family--this was hard to bear. He had not deserved it either. Well, there was comfort in that; and poor Nicholas would brighten up again, to be again depressed, as his quickly shifting thoughts presented every variety of light and shade before him.\n\nUndergoing these alternations of hope and misgiving, which no one, placed in a situation of ordinary trial, can fail to have experienced, Nicholas at length reached his poor room, where, no longer borne up by the excitement which had hitherto sustained him, but depressed by the revulsion of feeling it left behind, he threw himself on the bed, and turning his face to the wall, gave free vent to the emotions he had so long stifled.\n\nHe had not heard anybody enter, and was unconscious of the presence of Smike, until, happening to raise his head, he saw him, standing at the upper end of the room, looking wistfully towards him. He withdrew his eyes when he saw that he was observed, and affected to be busied with some scanty preparations for dinner.\n\n‘Well, Smike,’ said Nicholas, as cheerfully as he could speak, ‘let me hear what new acquaintances you have made this morning, or what new wonder you have found out, in the compass of this street and the next one.’\n\n‘No,’ said Smike, shaking his head mournfully; ‘I must talk of something else today.’\n\n‘Of what you like,’ replied Nicholas, good-humouredly.\n\n‘Of this,’ said Smike. ‘I know you are unhappy, and have got into great trouble by bringing me away. I ought to have known that, and stopped behind--I would, indeed, if I had thought it then. You--you--are not rich; you have not enough for yourself, and I should not be here. You grow,’ said the lad, laying his hand timidly on that of Nicholas, ‘you grow thinner every day; your cheek is paler, and your eye more sunk. Indeed I cannot bear to see you so, and think how I am burdening you. I tried to go away today, but the thought of your kind face drew me back. I could not leave you without a word.’ The poor fellow could say no more, for his eyes filled with tears, and his voice was gone.\n\n‘The word which separates us,’ said Nicholas, grasping him heartily by the shoulder, ‘shall never be said by me, for you are my only comfort and stay. I would not lose you now, Smike, for all the world could give. The thought of you has upheld me through all I have endured today, and shall, through fifty times such trouble. Give me your hand. My heart is linked to yours. We will journey from this place together, before the week is out. What, if I am steeped in poverty? You lighten it, and we will be poor together.’\n\n# CHAPTER 21\n\nMadam Mantalini finds herself in a Situation of some Difficulty, and Miss Nickleby finds herself in no Situation at all\nThe agitation she had undergone, rendered Kate Nickleby unable to resume her duties at the dressmaker’s for three days, at the expiration of which interval she betook herself at the accustomed hour, and with languid steps, to the temple of fashion where Madame Mantalini reigned paramount and supreme.\n\nThe ill-will of Miss Knag had lost nothing of its virulence in the interval. The young ladies still scrupulously shrunk from all companionship with their denounced associate; and when that exemplary female arrived a few minutes afterwards, she was at no pains to conceal the displeasure with which she regarded Kate’s return.\n\n‘Upon my word!’ said Miss Knag, as the satellites flocked round, to relieve her of her bonnet and shawl; ‘I should have thought some people would have had spirit enough to stop away altogether, when they know what an incumbrance their presence is to right-minded persons. But it’s a queer world; oh! it’s a queer world!’\n\nMiss Knag, having passed this comment on the world, in the tone in which most people do pass comments on the world when they are out of temper, that is to say, as if they by no means belonged to it, concluded by heaving a sigh, wherewith she seemed meekly to compassionate the wickedness of mankind.\n\nThe attendants were not slow to echo the sigh, and Miss Knag was apparently on the eve of favouring them with some further moral reflections, when the voice of Madame Mantalini, conveyed through the speaking-tube, ordered Miss Nickleby upstairs to assist in the arrangement of the show-room; a distinction which caused Miss Knag to toss her head so much, and bite her lips so hard, that her powers of conversation were, for the time, annihilated.\n\n‘Well, Miss Nickleby, child,’ said Madame Mantalini, when Kate presented herself; ‘are you quite well again?’\n\n‘A great deal better, thank you,’ replied Kate.\n\n‘I wish I could say the same,’ remarked Madame Mantalini, seating herself with an air of weariness.\n\n‘Are you ill?’ asked Kate. ‘I am very sorry for that.’\n\n‘Not exactly ill, but worried, child--worried,’ rejoined Madame.\n\n‘I am still more sorry to hear that,’ said Kate, gently. ‘Bodily illness is more easy to bear than mental.’\n\n‘Ah! and it’s much easier to talk than to bear either,’ said Madame, rubbing her nose with much irritability of manner. ‘There, get to your work, child, and put the things in order, do.’\n\nWhile Kate was wondering within herself what these symptoms of unusual vexation portended, Mr. Mantalini put the tips of his whiskers, and, by degrees, his head, through the half-opened door, and cried in a soft voice--\n\n‘Is my life and soul there?’\n\n‘No,’ replied his wife.\n\n‘How can it say so, when it is blooming in the front room like a little rose in a demnition flower-pot?’ urged Mantalini. ‘May its poppet come in and talk?’\n\n‘Certainly not,’ replied Madame: ‘you know I never allow you here. Go along!’\n\nThe poppet, however, encouraged perhaps by the relenting tone of this reply, ventured to rebel, and, stealing into the room, made towards Madame Mantalini on tiptoe, blowing her a kiss as he came along.\n\n‘Why will it vex itself, and twist its little face into bewitching nutcrackers?’ said Mantalini, putting his left arm round the waist of his life and soul, and drawing her towards him with his right.\n\n‘Oh! I can’t bear you,’ replied his wife.\n\n‘Not--eh, not bear ME!’ exclaimed Mantalini. ‘Fibs, fibs. It couldn’t be. There’s not a woman alive, that could tell me such a thing to my face--to my own face.’ Mr. Mantalini stroked his chin, as he said this, and glanced complacently at an opposite mirror.\n\n‘Such destructive extravagance,’ reasoned his wife, in a low tone.\n\n‘All in its joy at having gained such a lovely creature, such a little Venus, such a demd, enchanting, bewitching, engrossing, captivating little Venus,’ said Mantalini.\n\n‘See what a situation you have placed me in!’ urged Madame.\n\n‘No harm will come, no harm shall come, to its own darling,’ rejoined Mr. Mantalini. ‘It is all over; there will be nothing the matter; money shall be got in; and if it don’t come in fast enough, old Nickleby shall stump up again, or have his jugular separated if he dares to vex and hurt the little--’\n\n‘Hush!’ interposed Madame. ‘Don’t you see?’\n\nMr. Mantalini, who, in his eagerness to make up matters with his wife, had overlooked, or feigned to overlook, Miss Nickleby hitherto, took the hint, and laying his finger on his lip, sunk his voice still lower. There was, then, a great deal of whispering, during which Madame Mantalini appeared to make reference, more than once, to certain debts incurred by Mr. Mantalini previous to her coverture; and also to an unexpected outlay of money in payment of the aforesaid debts; and furthermore, to certain agreeable weaknesses on that gentleman’s part, such as gaming, wasting, idling, and a tendency to horse-flesh; each of which matters of accusation Mr. Mantalini disposed of, by one kiss or more, as its relative importance demanded. The upshot of it all was, that Madame Mantalini was in raptures with him, and that they went upstairs to breakfast.\n\nKate busied herself in what she had to do, and was silently arranging the various articles of decoration in the best taste she could display, when she started to hear a strange man’s voice in the room, and started again, to observe, on looking round, that a white hat, and a red neckerchief, and a broad round face, and a large head, and part of a green coat were in the room too.\n\n‘Don’t alarm yourself, miss,’ said the proprietor of these appearances. ‘I say; this here’s the mantie-making consarn, an’t it?’\n\n‘Yes,’ rejoined Kate, greatly astonished. ‘What did you want?’\n\nThe stranger answered not; but, first looking back, as though to beckon to some unseen person outside, came, very deliberately, into the room, and was closely followed by a little man in brown, very much the worse for wear, who brought with him a mingled fumigation of stale tobacco and fresh onions. The clothes of this gentleman were much bespeckled with flue; and his shoes, stockings, and nether garments, from his heels to the waist buttons of his coat inclusive, were profusely embroidered with splashes of mud, caught a fortnight previously--before the setting-in of the fine weather.\n\nKate’s very natural impression was, that these engaging individuals had called with the view of possessing themselves, unlawfully, of any portable articles that chanced to strike their fancy. She did not attempt to disguise her apprehensions, and made a move towards the door.\n\n‘Wait a minnit,’ said the man in the green coat, closing it softly, and standing with his back against it. ‘This is a unpleasant bisness. Vere’s your govvernor?’\n\n‘My what--did you say?’ asked Kate, trembling; for she thought ‘governor’ might be slang for watch or money.\n\n‘Mister Muntlehiney,’ said the man. ‘Wot’s come on him? Is he at home?’\n\n‘He is above stairs, I believe,’ replied Kate, a little reassured by this inquiry. ‘Do you want him?’\n\n‘No,’ replied the visitor. ‘I don’t ezactly want him, if it’s made a favour on. You can jist give him that ‘ere card, and tell him if he wants to speak to ME, and save trouble, here I am; that’s all.’\n\nWith these words, the stranger put a thick square card into Kate’s hand, and, turning to his friend, remarked, with an easy air, ‘that the rooms was a good high pitch;’ to which the friend assented, adding, by way of illustration, ‘that there was lots of room for a little boy to grow up a man in either on ‘em, vithout much fear of his ever bringing his head into contract vith the ceiling.’\n\nAfter ringing the bell which would summon Madame Mantalini, Kate glanced at the card, and saw that it displayed the name of ‘Scaley,’ together with some other information to which she had not had time to refer, when her attention was attracted by Mr. Scaley himself, who, walking up to one of the cheval-glasses, gave it a hard poke in the centre with his stick, as coolly as if it had been made of cast iron.\n\n‘Good plate this here, Tix,’ said Mr. Scaley to his friend.\n\n‘Ah!’ rejoined Mr. Tix, placing the marks of his four fingers, and a duplicate impression of his thumb, on a piece of sky-blue silk; ‘and this here article warn’t made for nothing, mind you.’\n\nFrom the silk, Mr. Tix transferred his admiration to some elegant articles of wearing apparel, while Mr. Scaley adjusted his neckcloth, at leisure, before the glass, and afterwards, aided by its reflection, proceeded to the minute consideration of a pimple on his chin; in which absorbing occupation he was yet engaged, when Madame Mantalini, entering the room, uttered an exclamation of surprise which roused him.\n\n‘Oh! Is this the missis?’ inquired Scaley.\n\n‘It is Madame Mantalini,’ said Kate.\n\n‘Then,’ said Mr. Scaley, producing a small document from his pocket and unfolding it very slowly, ‘this is a writ of execution, and if it’s not conwenient to settle we’ll go over the house at wunst, please, and take the inwentory.’\n\nPoor Madame Mantalini wrung her hands for grief, and rung the bell for her husband; which done, she fell into a chair and a fainting fit, simultaneously. The professional gentlemen, however, were not at all discomposed by this event, for Mr. Scaley, leaning upon a stand on which a handsome dress was displayed (so that his shoulders appeared above it, in nearly the same manner as the shoulders of the lady for whom it was designed would have done if she had had it on), pushed his hat on one side and scratched his head with perfect unconcern, while his friend Mr. Tix, taking that opportunity for a general survey of the apartment preparatory to entering on business, stood with his inventory-book under his arm and his hat in his hand, mentally occupied in putting a price upon every object within his range of vision.\n\nSuch was the posture of affairs when Mr. Mantalini hurried in; and as that distinguished specimen had had a pretty extensive intercourse with Mr. Scaley’s fraternity in his bachelor days, and was, besides, very far from being taken by surprise on the present agitating occasion, he merely shrugged his shoulders, thrust his hands down to the bottom of his pockets, elevated his eyebrows, whistled a bar or two, swore an oath or two, and, sitting astride upon a chair, put the best face upon the matter with great composure and decency.\n\n‘What’s the demd total?’ was the first question he asked.\n\n‘Fifteen hundred and twenty-seven pound, four and ninepence ha’penny,’ replied Mr. Scaley, without moving a limb.\n\n‘The halfpenny be demd,’ said Mr. Mantalini, impatiently.\n\n‘By all means if you vish it,’ retorted Mr. Scaley; ‘and the ninepence.’\n\n‘It don’t matter to us if the fifteen hundred and twenty-seven pound went along with it, that I know on,’ observed Mr. Tix.\n\n‘Not a button,’ said Scaley.\n\n‘Well,’ said the same gentleman, after a pause, ‘wot’s to be done--anything? Is it only a small crack, or a out-and-out smash? A break-up of the constitootion is it?--werry good. Then Mr. Tom Tix, esk-vire, you must inform your angel wife and lovely family as you won’t sleep at home for three nights to come, along of being in possession here. Wot’s the good of the lady a fretting herself?’ continued Mr Scaley, as Madame Mantalini sobbed. ‘A good half of wot’s here isn’t paid for, I des-say, and wot a consolation oughtn’t that to be to her feelings!’\n\nWith these remarks, combining great pleasantry with sound moral encouragement under difficulties, Mr. Scaley proceeded to take the inventory, in which delicate task he was materially assisted by the uncommon tact and experience of Mr. Tix, the broker.\n\n‘My cup of happiness’s sweetener,’ said Mantalini, approaching his wife with a penitent air; ‘will you listen to me for two minutes?’\n\n‘Oh! don’t speak to me,’ replied his wife, sobbing. ‘You have ruined me, and that’s enough.’\n\nMr. Mantalini, who had doubtless well considered his part, no sooner heard these words pronounced in a tone of grief and severity, than he recoiled several paces, assumed an expression of consuming mental agony, rushed headlong from the room, and was, soon afterwards, heard to slam the door of an upstairs dressing-room with great violence.\n\n‘Miss Nickleby,’ cried Madame Mantalini, when this sound met her ear, ‘make haste, for Heaven’s sake, he will destroy himself! I spoke unkindly to him, and he cannot bear it from me. Alfred, my darling Alfred.’\n\nWith such exclamations, she hurried upstairs, followed by Kate who, although she did not quite participate in the fond wife’s apprehensions, was a little flurried, nevertheless. The dressing-room door being hastily flung open, Mr. Mantalini was disclosed to view, with his shirt-collar symmetrically thrown back: putting a fine edge to a breakfast knife by means of his razor strop.\n\n‘Ah!’ cried Mr. Mantalini, ‘interrupted!’ and whisk went the breakfast knife into Mr. Mantalini’s dressing-gown pocket, while Mr. Mantalini’s eyes rolled wildly, and his hair floating in wild disorder, mingled with his whiskers.\n\n‘Alfred,’ cried his wife, flinging her arms about him, ‘I didn’t mean to say it, I didn’t mean to say it!’\n\n‘Ruined!’ cried Mr. Mantalini. ‘Have I brought ruin upon the best and purest creature that ever blessed a demnition vagabond! Demmit, let me go.’ At this crisis of his ravings Mr. Mantalini made a pluck at the breakfast knife, and being restrained by his wife’s grasp, attempted to dash his head against the wall--taking very good care to be at least six feet from it.\n\n‘Compose yourself, my own angel,’ said Madame. ‘It was nobody’s fault; it was mine as much as yours, we shall do very well yet. Come, Alfred, come.’\n\nMr. Mantalini did not think proper to come to, all at once; but, after calling several times for poison, and requesting some lady or gentleman to blow his brains out, gentler feelings came upon him, and he wept pathetically. In this softened frame of mind he did not oppose the capture of the knife--which, to tell the truth, he was rather glad to be rid of, as an inconvenient and dangerous article for a skirt pocket--and finally he suffered himself to be led away by his affectionate partner.\n\nAfter a delay of two or three hours, the young ladies were informed that their services would be dispensed with until further notice, and at the expiration of two days, the name of Mantalini appeared in the list of bankrupts: Miss Nickleby received an intimation per post, on the same morning, that the business would be, in future, carried on under the name of Miss Knag, and that her assistance would no longer be required--a piece of intelligence with which Mrs. Nickleby was no sooner made acquainted, than that good lady declared she had expected it all along and cited divers unknown occasions on which she had prophesied to that precise effect.\n\n‘And I say again,’ remarked Mrs. Nickleby (who, it is scarcely necessary to observe, had never said so before), ‘I say again, that a milliner’s and dressmaker’s is the very last description of business, Kate, that you should have thought of attaching yourself to. I don’t make it a reproach to you, my love; but still I will say, that if you had consulted your own mother--’\n\n‘Well, well, mama,’ said Kate, mildly: ‘what would you recommend now?’\n\n‘Recommend!’ cried Mrs. Nickleby, ‘isn’t it obvious, my dear, that of all occupations in this world for a young lady situated as you are, that of companion to some amiable lady is the very thing for which your education, and manners, and personal appearance, and everything else, exactly qualify you? Did you never hear your poor dear papa speak of the young lady who was the daughter of the old lady who boarded in the same house that he boarded in once, when he was a bachelor--what was her name again? I know it began with a B, and ended with g, but whether it was Waters or--no, it couldn’t have been that, either; but whatever her name was, don’t you know that that young lady went as companion to a married lady who died soon afterwards, and that she married the husband, and had one of the finest little boys that the medical man had ever seen--all within eighteen months?’\n\nKate knew, perfectly well, that this torrent of favourable recollection was occasioned by some opening, real or imaginary, which her mother had discovered, in the companionship walk of life. She therefore waited, very patiently, until all reminiscences and anecdotes, bearing or not bearing upon the subject, had been exhausted, and at last ventured to inquire what discovery had been made. The truth then came out. Mrs Nickleby had, that morning, had a yesterday’s newspaper of the very first respectability from the public-house where the porter came from; and in this yesterday’s newspaper was an advertisement, couched in the purest and most grammatical English, announcing that a married lady was in want of a genteel young person as companion, and that the married lady’s name and address were to be known, on application at a certain library at the west end of the town, therein mentioned.\n\n‘And I say,’ exclaimed Mrs. Nickleby, laying the paper down in triumph, ‘that if your uncle don’t object, it’s well worth the trial.’\n\nKate was too sick at heart, after the rough jostling she had already had with the world, and really cared too little at the moment what fate was reserved for her, to make any objection. Mr. Ralph Nickleby offered none, but, on the contrary, highly approved of the suggestion; neither did he express any great surprise at Madame Mantalini’s sudden failure, indeed it would have been strange if he had, inasmuch as it had been procured and brought about chiefly by himself. So, the name and address were obtained without loss of time, and Miss Nickleby and her mama went off in quest of Mrs. Wititterly, of Cadogan Place, Sloane Street, that same forenoon.\n\nCadogan Place is the one slight bond that joins two great extremes; it is the connecting link between the aristocratic pavements of Belgrave Square, and the barbarism of Chelsea. It is in Sloane Street, but not of it. The people in Cadogan Place look down upon Sloane Street, and think Brompton low. They affect fashion too, and wonder where the New Road is. Not that they claim to be on precisely the same footing as the high folks of Belgrave Square and Grosvenor Place, but that they stand, with reference to them, rather in the light of those illegitimate children of the great who are content to boast of their connections, although their connections disavow them. Wearing as much as they can of the airs and semblances of loftiest rank, the people of Cadogan Place have the realities of middle station. It is the conductor which communicates to the inhabitants of regions beyond its limit, the shock of pride of birth and rank, which it has not within itself, but derives from a fountain-head beyond; or, like the ligament which unites the Siamese twins, it contains something of the life and essence of two distinct bodies, and yet belongs to neither.\n\nUpon this doubtful ground, lived Mrs. Wititterly, and at Mrs. Wititterly’s door Kate Nickleby knocked with trembling hand. The door was opened by a big footman with his head floured, or chalked, or painted in some way (it didn’t look genuine powder), and the big footman, receiving the card of introduction, gave it to a little page; so little, indeed, that his body would not hold, in ordinary array, the number of small buttons which are indispensable to a page’s costume, and they were consequently obliged to be stuck on four abreast. This young gentleman took the card upstairs on a salver, and pending his return, Kate and her mother were shown into a dining-room of rather dirty and shabby aspect, and so comfortably arranged as to be adapted to almost any purpose rather than eating and drinking.\n\nNow, in the ordinary course of things, and according to all authentic descriptions of high life, as set forth in books, Mrs. Wititterly ought to have been in her BOUDOIR; but whether it was that Mr. Wititterly was at that moment shaving himself in the BOUDOIR or what not, certain it is that Mrs. Wititterly gave audience in the drawing-room, where was everything proper and necessary, including curtains and furniture coverings of a roseate hue, to shed a delicate bloom on Mrs. Wititterly’s complexion, and a little dog to snap at strangers’ legs for Mrs Wititterly’s amusement, and the afore-mentioned page, to hand chocolate for Mrs. Wititterly’s refreshment.\n\nThe lady had an air of sweet insipidity, and a face of engaging paleness; there was a faded look about her, and about the furniture, and about the house. She was reclining on a sofa in such a very unstudied attitude, that she might have been taken for an actress all ready for the first scene in a ballet, and only waiting for the drop curtain to go up.\n\n‘Place chairs.’\n\nThe page placed them.\n\n‘Leave the room, Alphonse.’\n\nThe page left it; but if ever an Alphonse carried plain Bill in his face and figure, that page was the boy.\n\n‘I have ventured to call, ma’am,’ said Kate, after a few seconds of awkward silence, ‘from having seen your advertisement.’\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Mrs. Wititterly, ‘one of my people put it in the paper--Yes.’\n\n‘I thought, perhaps,’ said Kate, modestly, ‘that if you had not already made a final choice, you would forgive my troubling you with an application.’\n\n‘Yes,’ drawled Mrs. Wititterly again.\n\n‘If you have already made a selection--’\n\n‘Oh dear no,’ interrupted the lady, ‘I am not so easily suited. I really don’t know what to say. You have never been a companion before, have you?’\n\nMrs. Nickleby, who had been eagerly watching her opportunity, came dexterously in, before Kate could reply. ‘Not to any stranger, ma’am,’ said the good lady; ‘but she has been a companion to me for some years. I am her mother, ma’am.’\n\n‘Oh!’ said Mrs. Wititterly, ‘I apprehend you.’\n\n‘I assure you, ma’am,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘that I very little thought, at one time, that it would be necessary for my daughter to go out into the world at all, for her poor dear papa was an independent gentleman, and would have been at this moment if he had but listened in time to my constant entreaties and--’\n\n‘Dear mama,’ said Kate, in a low voice.\n\n‘My dear Kate, if you will allow me to speak,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘I shall take the liberty of explaining to this lady--’\n\n‘I think it is almost unnecessary, mama.’\n\nAnd notwithstanding all the frowns and winks with which Mrs. Nickleby intimated that she was going to say something which would clench the business at once, Kate maintained her point by an expressive look, and for once Mrs. Nickleby was stopped upon the very brink of an oration.\n\n‘What are your accomplishments?’ asked Mrs. Wititterly, with her eyes shut.\n\nKate blushed as she mentioned her principal acquirements, and Mrs Nickleby checked them all off, one by one, on her fingers; having calculated the number before she came out. Luckily the two calculations agreed, so Mrs. Nickleby had no excuse for talking.\n\n‘You are a good temper?’ asked Mrs. Wititterly, opening her eyes for an instant, and shutting them again.\n\n‘I hope so,’ rejoined Kate.\n\n‘And have a highly respectable reference for everything, have you?’\n\nKate replied that she had, and laid her uncle’s card upon the table.\n\n‘Have the goodness to draw your chair a little nearer, and let me look at you,’ said Mrs. Wititterly; ‘I am so very nearsighted that I can’t quite discern your features.’\n\nKate complied, though not without some embarrassment, with this request, and Mrs. Wititterly took a languid survey of her countenance, which lasted some two or three minutes.\n\n‘I like your appearance,’ said that lady, ringing a little bell. ‘Alphonse, request your master to come here.’\n\nThe page disappeared on this errand, and after a short interval, during which not a word was spoken on either side, opened the door for an important gentleman of about eight-and-thirty, of rather plebeian countenance, and with a very light head of hair, who leant over Mrs Wititterly for a little time, and conversed with her in whispers.\n\n‘Oh!’ he said, turning round, ‘yes. This is a most important matter. Mrs Wititterly is of a very excitable nature; very delicate, very fragile; a hothouse plant, an exotic.’\n\n‘Oh! Henry, my dear,’ interposed Mrs. Wititterly.\n\n‘You are, my love, you know you are; one breath--’ said Mr. W., blowing an imaginary feather away. ‘Pho! you’re gone!’\n\nThe lady sighed.\n\n‘Your soul is too large for your body,’ said Mr. Wititterly. ‘Your intellect wears you out; all the medical men say so; you know that there is not a physician who is not proud of being called in to you. What is their unanimous declaration? “My dear doctor,” said I to Sir Tumley Snuffim, in this very room, the very last time he came. “My dear doctor, what is my wife’s complaint? Tell me all. I can bear it. Is it nerves?” “My dear fellow,” he said, “be proud of that woman; make much of her; she is an ornament to the fashionable world, and to you. Her complaint is soul. It swells, expands, dilates--the blood fires, the pulse quickens, the excitement increases--Whew!”’ Here Mr. Wititterly, who, in the ardour of his description, had flourished his right hand to within something less than an inch of Mrs. Nickleby’s bonnet, drew it hastily back again, and blew his nose as fiercely as if it had been done by some violent machinery.\n\n‘You make me out worse than I am, Henry,’ said Mrs. Wititterly, with a faint smile.\n\n‘I do not, Julia, I do not,’ said Mr. W. ‘The society in which you move--necessarily move, from your station, connection, and endowments--is one vortex and whirlpool of the most frightful excitement. Bless my heart and body, can I ever forget the night you danced with the baronet’s nephew at the election ball, at Exeter! It was tremendous.’\n\n‘I always suffer for these triumphs afterwards,’ said Mrs. Wititterly.\n\n‘And for that very reason,’ rejoined her husband, ‘you must have a companion, in whom there is great gentleness, great sweetness, excessive sympathy, and perfect repose.’\n\nHere, both Mr. and Mrs. Wititterly, who had talked rather at the Nicklebys than to each other, left off speaking, and looked at their two hearers, with an expression of countenance which seemed to say, ‘What do you think of all this?’\n\n‘Mrs. Wititterly,’ said her husband, addressing himself to Mrs. Nickleby, ‘is sought after and courted by glittering crowds and brilliant circles. She is excited by the opera, the drama, the fine arts, the--the--the--’\n\n‘The nobility, my love,’ interposed Mrs. Wititterly.\n\n‘The nobility, of course,’ said Mr. Wititterly. ‘And the military. She forms and expresses an immense variety of opinions on an immense variety of subjects. If some people in public life were acquainted with Mrs Wititterly’s real opinion of them, they would not hold their heads, perhaps, quite as high as they do.’\n\n‘Hush, Henry,’ said the lady; ‘this is scarcely fair.’\n\n‘I mention no names, Julia,’ replied Mr. Wititterly; ‘and nobody is injured. I merely mention the circumstance to show that you are no ordinary person, that there is a constant friction perpetually going on between your mind and your body; and that you must be soothed and tended. Now let me hear, dispassionately and calmly, what are this young lady’s qualifications for the office.’\n\nIn obedience to this request, the qualifications were all gone through again, with the addition of many interruptions and cross-questionings from Mr. Wititterly. It was finally arranged that inquiries should be made, and a decisive answer addressed to Miss Nickleby under cover of her uncle, within two days. These conditions agreed upon, the page showed them down as far as the staircase window; and the big footman, relieving guard at that point, piloted them in perfect safety to the street-door.\n\n‘They are very distinguished people, evidently,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, as she took her daughter’s arm. ‘What a superior person Mrs. Wititterly is!’\n\n‘Do you think so, mama?’ was all Kate’s reply.\n\n‘Why, who can help thinking so, Kate, my love?’ rejoined her mother. ‘She is pale though, and looks much exhausted. I hope she may not be wearing herself out, but I am very much afraid.’\n\nThese considerations led the deep-sighted lady into a calculation of the probable duration of Mrs. Wititterly’s life, and the chances of the disconsolate widower bestowing his hand on her daughter. Before reaching home, she had freed Mrs. Wititterly’s soul from all bodily restraint; married Kate with great splendour at St George’s, Hanover Square; and only left undecided the minor question, whether a splendid French-polished mahogany bedstead should be erected for herself in the two-pair back of the house in Cadogan Place, or in the three-pair front: between which apartments she could not quite balance the advantages, and therefore adjusted the question at last, by determining to leave it to the decision of her son-in-law.\n\nThe inquiries were made. The answer--not to Kate’s very great joy--was favourable; and at the expiration of a week she betook herself, with all her movables and valuables, to Mrs. Wititterly’s mansion, where for the present we will leave her.\n\n# CHAPTER 22\n\nNicholas, accompanied by Smike, sallies forth to seek his Fortune. He encounters Mr. Vincent Crummles; and who he was, is herein made manifest\nThe whole capital which Nicholas found himself entitled to, either in possession, reversion, remainder, or expectancy, after paying his rent and settling with the broker from whom he had hired his poor furniture, did not exceed, by more than a few halfpence, the sum of twenty shillings. And yet he hailed the morning on which he had resolved to quit London, with a light heart, and sprang from his bed with an elasticity of spirit which is happily the lot of young persons, or the world would never be stocked with old ones.\n\nIt was a cold, dry, foggy morning in early spring. A few meagre shadows flitted to and fro in the misty streets, and occasionally there loomed through the dull vapour, the heavy outline of some hackney coach wending homewards, which, drawing slowly nearer, rolled jangling by, scattering the thin crust of frost from its whitened roof, and soon was lost again in the cloud. At intervals were heard the tread of slipshod feet, and the chilly cry of the poor sweep as he crept, shivering, to his early toil; the heavy footfall of the official watcher of the night, pacing slowly up and down and cursing the tardy hours that still intervened between him and sleep; the rambling of ponderous carts and waggons; the roll of the lighter vehicles which carried buyers and sellers to the different markets; the sound of ineffectual knocking at the doors of heavy sleepers--all these noises fell upon the ear from time to time, but all seemed muffled by the fog, and to be rendered almost as indistinct to the ear as was every object to the sight. The sluggish darkness thickened as the day came on; and those who had the courage to rise and peep at the gloomy street from their curtained windows, crept back to bed again, and coiled themselves up to sleep.\n\nBefore even these indications of approaching morning were rife in busy London, Nicholas had made his way alone to the city, and stood beneath the windows of his mother’s house. It was dull and bare to see, but it had light and life for him; for there was at least one heart within its old walls to which insult or dishonour would bring the same blood rushing, that flowed in his own veins.\n\nHe crossed the road, and raised his eyes to the window of the room where he knew his sister slept. It was closed and dark. ‘Poor girl,’ thought Nicholas, ‘she little thinks who lingers here!’\n\nHe looked again, and felt, for the moment, almost vexed that Kate was not there to exchange one word at parting. ‘Good God!’ he thought, suddenly correcting himself, ‘what a boy I am!’\n\n‘It is better as it is,’ said Nicholas, after he had lounged on, a few paces, and returned to the same spot. ‘When I left them before, and could have said goodbye a thousand times if I had chosen, I spared them the pain of leave-taking, and why not now?’ As he spoke, some fancied motion of the curtain almost persuaded him, for the instant, that Kate was at the window, and by one of those strange contradictions of feeling which are common to us all, he shrunk involuntarily into a doorway, that she might not see him. He smiled at his own weakness; said ‘God bless them!’ and walked away with a lighter step.\n\nSmike was anxiously expecting him when he reached his old lodgings, and so was Newman, who had expended a day’s income in a can of rum and milk to prepare them for the journey. They had tied up the luggage, Smike shouldered it, and away they went, with Newman Noggs in company; for he had insisted on walking as far as he could with them, overnight.\n\n‘Which way?’ asked Newman, wistfully.\n\n‘To Kingston first,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘And where afterwards?’ asked Newman. ‘Why won’t you tell me?’\n\n‘Because I scarcely know myself, good friend,’ rejoined Nicholas, laying his hand upon his shoulder; ‘and if I did, I have neither plan nor prospect yet, and might shift my quarters a hundred times before you could possibly communicate with me.’\n\n‘I am afraid you have some deep scheme in your head,’ said Newman, doubtfully.\n\n‘So deep,’ replied his young friend, ‘that even I can’t fathom it. Whatever I resolve upon, depend upon it I will write you soon.’\n\n‘You won’t forget?’ said Newman.\n\n‘I am not very likely to,’ rejoined Nicholas. ‘I have not so many friends that I shall grow confused among the number, and forget my best one.’\n\nOccupied in such discourse, they walked on for a couple of hours, as they might have done for a couple of days if Nicholas had not sat himself down on a stone by the wayside, and resolutely declared his intention of not moving another step until Newman Noggs turned back. Having pleaded ineffectually first for another half-mile, and afterwards for another quarter, Newman was fain to comply, and to shape his course towards Golden Square, after interchanging many hearty and affectionate farewells, and many times turning back to wave his hat to the two wayfarers when they had become mere specks in the distance.\n\n‘Now listen to me, Smike,’ said Nicholas, as they trudged with stout hearts onwards. ‘We are bound for Portsmouth.’\n\nSmike nodded his head and smiled, but expressed no other emotion; for whether they had been bound for Portsmouth or Port Royal would have been alike to him, so they had been bound together.\n\n‘I don’t know much of these matters,’ resumed Nicholas; ‘but Portsmouth is a seaport town, and if no other employment is to be obtained, I should think we might get on board some ship. I am young and active, and could be useful in many ways. So could you.’\n\n‘I hope so,’ replied Smike. ‘When I was at that--you know where I mean?’\n\n‘Yes, I know,’ said Nicholas. ‘You needn’t name the place.’\n\n‘Well, when I was there,’ resumed Smike; his eyes sparkling at the prospect of displaying his abilities; ‘I could milk a cow, and groom a horse, with anybody.’\n\n‘Ha!’ said Nicholas, gravely. ‘I am afraid they don’t keep many animals of either kind on board ship, Smike, and even when they have horses, that they are not very particular about rubbing them down; still you can learn to do something else, you know. Where there’s a will, there’s a way.’\n\n‘And I am very willing,’ said Smike, brightening up again.\n\n‘God knows you are,’ rejoined Nicholas; ‘and if you fail, it shall go hard but I’ll do enough for us both.’\n\n‘Do we go all the way today?’ asked Smike, after a short silence.\n\n‘That would be too severe a trial, even for your willing legs,’ said Nicholas, with a good-humoured smile. ‘No. Godalming is some thirty and odd miles from London--as I found from a map I borrowed--and I purpose to rest there. We must push on again tomorrow, for we are not rich enough to loiter. Let me relieve you of that bundle! Come!’\n\n‘No, no,’ rejoined Smike, falling back a few steps. ‘Don’t ask me to give it up to you.’\n\n‘Why not?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘Let me do something for you, at least,’ said Smike. ‘You will never let me serve you as I ought. You will never know how I think, day and night, of ways to please you.’\n\n‘You are a foolish fellow to say it, for I know it well, and see it, or I should be a blind and senseless beast,’ rejoined Nicholas. ‘Let me ask you a question while I think of it, and there is no one by,’ he added, looking him steadily in the face. ‘Have you a good memory?’\n\n‘I don’t know,’ said Smike, shaking his head sorrowfully. ‘I think I had once; but it’s all gone now--all gone.’\n\n‘Why do you think you had once?’ asked Nicholas, turning quickly upon him as though the answer in some way helped out the purport of his question.\n\n‘Because I could remember, when I was a child,’ said Smike, ‘but that is very, very long ago, or at least it seems so. I was always confused and giddy at that place you took me from; and could never remember, and sometimes couldn’t even understand, what they said to me. I--let me see--let me see!’\n\n‘You are wandering now,’ said Nicholas, touching him on the arm.\n\n‘No,’ replied his companion, with a vacant look ‘I was only thinking how--’ He shivered involuntarily as he spoke.\n\n‘Think no more of that place, for it is all over,’ retorted Nicholas, fixing his eyes full upon that of his companion, which was fast settling into an unmeaning stupefied gaze, once habitual to him, and common even then. ‘What of the first day you went to Yorkshire?’\n\n‘Eh!’ cried the lad.\n\n‘That was before you began to lose your recollection, you know,’ said Nicholas quietly. ‘Was the weather hot or cold?’\n\n‘Wet,’ replied the boy. ‘Very wet. I have always said, when it has rained hard, that it was like the night I came: and they used to crowd round and laugh to see me cry when the rain fell heavily. It was like a child, they said, and that made me think of it more. I turned cold all over sometimes, for I could see myself as I was then, coming in at the very same door.’\n\n‘As you were then,’ repeated Nicholas, with assumed carelessness; ‘how was that?’\n\n‘Such a little creature,’ said Smike, ‘that they might have had pity and mercy upon me, only to remember it.’\n\n‘You didn’t find your way there, alone!’ remarked Nicholas.\n\n‘No,’ rejoined Smike, ‘oh no.’\n\n‘Who was with you?’\n\n‘A man--a dark, withered man. I have heard them say so, at the school, and I remembered that before. I was glad to leave him, I was afraid of him; but they made me more afraid of them, and used me harder too.’\n\n‘Look at me,’ said Nicholas, wishing to attract his full attention. ‘There; don’t turn away. Do you remember no woman, no kind woman, who hung over you once, and kissed your lips, and called you her child?’\n\n‘No,’ said the poor creature, shaking his head, ‘no, never.’\n\n‘Nor any house but that house in Yorkshire?’\n\n‘No,’ rejoined the youth, with a melancholy look; ‘a room--I remember I slept in a room, a large lonesome room at the top of a house, where there was a trap-door in the ceiling. I have covered my head with the clothes often, not to see it, for it frightened me: a young child with no one near at night: and I used to wonder what was on the other side. There was a clock too, an old clock, in one corner. I remember that. I have never forgotten that room; for when I have terrible dreams, it comes back, just as it was. I see things and people in it that I had never seen then, but there is the room just as it used to be; THAT never changes.’\n\n‘Will you let me take the bundle now?’ asked Nicholas, abruptly changing the theme.\n\n‘No,’ said Smike, ‘no. Come, let us walk on.’\n\nHe quickened his pace as he said this, apparently under the impression that they had been standing still during the whole of the previous dialogue. Nicholas marked him closely, and every word of this conversation remained upon his memory.\n\nIt was, by this time, within an hour of noon, and although a dense vapour still enveloped the city they had left, as if the very breath of its busy people hung over their schemes of gain and profit, and found greater attraction there than in the quiet region above, in the open country it was clear and fair. Occasionally, in some low spots they came upon patches of mist which the sun had not yet driven from their strongholds; but these were soon passed, and as they laboured up the hills beyond, it was pleasant to look down, and see how the sluggish mass rolled heavily off, before the cheering influence of day. A broad, fine, honest sun lighted up the green pastures and dimpled water with the semblance of summer, while it left the travellers all the invigorating freshness of that early time of year. The ground seemed elastic under their feet; the sheep-bells were music to their ears; and exhilarated by exercise, and stimulated by hope, they pushed onward with the strength of lions.\n\nThe day wore on, and all these bright colours subsided, and assumed a quieter tint, like young hopes softened down by time, or youthful features by degrees resolving into the calm and serenity of age. But they were scarcely less beautiful in their slow decline, than they had been in their prime; for nature gives to every time and season some beauties of its own; and from morning to night, as from the cradle to the grave, is but a succession of changes so gentle and easy, that we can scarcely mark their progress.\n\nTo Godalming they came at last, and here they bargained for two humble beds, and slept soundly. In the morning they were astir: though not quite so early as the sun: and again afoot; if not with all the freshness of yesterday, still, with enough of hope and spirit to bear them cheerily on.\n\nIt was a harder day’s journey than yesterday’s, for there were long and weary hills to climb; and in journeys, as in life, it is a great deal easier to go down hill than up. However, they kept on, with unabated perseverance, and the hill has not yet lifted its face to heaven that perseverance will not gain the summit of at last.\n\nThey walked upon the rim of the Devil’s Punch Bowl; and Smike listened with greedy interest as Nicholas read the inscription upon the stone which, reared upon that wild spot, tells of a murder committed there by night. The grass on which they stood, had once been dyed with gore; and the blood of the murdered man had run down, drop by drop, into the hollow which gives the place its name. ‘The Devil’s Bowl,’ thought Nicholas, as he looked into the void, ‘never held fitter liquor than that!’\n\nOnward they kept, with steady purpose, and entered at length upon a wide and spacious tract of downs, with every variety of little hill and plain to change their verdant surface. Here, there shot up, almost perpendicularly, into the sky, a height so steep, as to be hardly accessible to any but the sheep and goats that fed upon its sides, and there, stood a mound of green, sloping and tapering off so delicately, and merging so gently into the level ground, that you could scarce define its limits. Hills swelling above each other; and undulations shapely and uncouth, smooth and rugged, graceful and grotesque, thrown negligently side by side, bounded the view in each direction; while frequently, with unexpected noise, there uprose from the ground a flight of crows, who, cawing and wheeling round the nearest hills, as if uncertain of their course, suddenly poised themselves upon the wing and skimmed down the long vista of some opening valley, with the speed of light itself.\n\nBy degrees, the prospect receded more and more on either hand, and as they had been shut out from rich and extensive scenery, so they emerged once again upon the open country. The knowledge that they were drawing near their place of destination, gave them fresh courage to proceed; but the way had been difficult, and they had loitered on the road, and Smike was tired. Thus, twilight had already closed in, when they turned off the path to the door of a roadside inn, yet twelve miles short of Portsmouth.\n\n‘Twelve miles,’ said Nicholas, leaning with both hands on his stick, and looking doubtfully at Smike.\n\n‘Twelve long miles,’ repeated the landlord.\n\n‘Is it a good road?’ inquired Nicholas.\n\n‘Very bad,’ said the landlord. As of course, being a landlord, he would say.\n\n‘I want to get on,’ observed Nicholas, hesitating. ‘I scarcely know what to do.’\n\n‘Don’t let me influence you,’ rejoined the landlord. ‘I wouldn’t go on if it was me.’\n\n‘Wouldn’t you?’ asked Nicholas, with the same uncertainty.\n\n‘Not if I knew when I was well off,’ said the landlord. And having said it he pulled up his apron, put his hands into his pockets, and, taking a step or two outside the door, looked down the dark road with an assumption of great indifference.\n\nA glance at the toil-worn face of Smike determined Nicholas, so without any further consideration he made up his mind to stay where he was.\n\nThe landlord led them into the kitchen, and as there was a good fire he remarked that it was very cold. If there had happened to be a bad one he would have observed that it was very warm.\n\n‘What can you give us for supper?’ was Nicholas’s natural question.\n\n‘Why--what would you like?’ was the landlord’s no less natural answer.\n\nNicholas suggested cold meat, but there was no cold meat--poached eggs, but there were no eggs--mutton chops, but there wasn’t a mutton chop within three miles, though there had been more last week than they knew what to do with, and would be an extraordinary supply the day after tomorrow.\n\n‘Then,’ said Nicholas, ‘I must leave it entirely to you, as I would have done, at first, if you had allowed me.’\n\n‘Why, then I’ll tell you what,’ rejoined the landlord. ‘There’s a gentleman in the parlour that’s ordered a hot beef-steak pudding and potatoes, at nine. There’s more of it than he can manage, and I have very little doubt that if I ask leave, you can sup with him. I’ll do that, in a minute.’\n\n‘No, no,’ said Nicholas, detaining him. ‘I would rather not. I--at least--pshaw! why cannot I speak out? Here; you see that I am travelling in a very humble manner, and have made my way hither on foot. It is more than probable, I think, that the gentleman may not relish my company; and although I am the dusty figure you see, I am too proud to thrust myself into his.’\n\n‘Lord love you,’ said the landlord, ‘it’s only Mr. Crummles; HE isn’t particular.’\n\n‘Is he not?’ asked Nicholas, on whose mind, to tell the truth, the prospect of the savoury pudding was making some impression.\n\n‘Not he,’ replied the landlord. ‘He’ll like your way of talking, I know. But we’ll soon see all about that. Just wait a minute.’\n\nThe landlord hurried into the parlour, without staying for further permission, nor did Nicholas strive to prevent him: wisely considering that supper, under the circumstances, was too serious a matter to be trifled with. It was not long before the host returned, in a condition of much excitement.\n\n‘All right,’ he said in a low voice. ‘I knew he would. You’ll see something rather worth seeing, in there. Ecod, how they are a-going of it!’\n\nThere was no time to inquire to what this exclamation, which was delivered in a very rapturous tone, referred; for he had already thrown open the door of the room; into which Nicholas, followed by Smike with the bundle on his shoulder (he carried it about with him as vigilantly as if it had been a sack of gold), straightway repaired.\n\nNicholas was prepared for something odd, but not for something quite so odd as the sight he encountered. At the upper end of the room, were a couple of boys, one of them very tall and the other very short, both dressed as sailors--or at least as theatrical sailors, with belts, buckles, pigtails, and pistols complete--fighting what is called in play-bills a terrific combat, with two of those short broad-swords with basket hilts which are commonly used at our minor theatres. The short boy had gained a great advantage over the tall boy, who was reduced to mortal strait, and both were overlooked by a large heavy man, perched against the corner of a table, who emphatically adjured them to strike a little more fire out of the swords, and they couldn’t fail to bring the house down, on the very first night.\n\n‘Mr. Vincent Crummles,’ said the landlord with an air of great deference. ‘This is the young gentleman.’\n\nMr. Vincent Crummles received Nicholas with an inclination of the head, something between the courtesy of a Roman emperor and the nod of a pot companion; and bade the landlord shut the door and begone.\n\n‘There’s a picture,’ said Mr. Crummles, motioning Nicholas not to advance and spoil it. ‘The little ‘un has him; if the big ‘un doesn’t knock under, in three seconds, he’s a dead man. Do that again, boys.’\n\nThe two combatants went to work afresh, and chopped away until the swords emitted a shower of sparks: to the great satisfaction of Mr Crummles, who appeared to consider this a very great point indeed. The engagement commenced with about two hundred chops administered by the short sailor and the tall sailor alternately, without producing any particular result, until the short sailor was chopped down on one knee; but this was nothing to him, for he worked himself about on the one knee with the assistance of his left hand, and fought most desperately until the tall sailor chopped his sword out of his grasp. Now, the inference was, that the short sailor, reduced to this extremity, would give in at once and cry quarter, but, instead of that, he all of a sudden drew a large pistol from his belt and presented it at the face of the tall sailor, who was so overcome at this (not expecting it) that he let the short sailor pick up his sword and begin again. Then, the chopping recommenced, and a variety of fancy chops were administered on both sides; such as chops dealt with the left hand, and under the leg, and over the right shoulder, and over the left; and when the short sailor made a vigorous cut at the tall sailor’s legs, which would have shaved them clean off if it had taken effect, the tall sailor jumped over the short sailor’s sword, wherefore to balance the matter, and make it all fair, the tall sailor administered the same cut, and the short sailor jumped over HIS sword. After this, there was a good deal of dodging about, and hitching up of the inexpressibles in the absence of braces, and then the short sailor (who was the moral character evidently, for he always had the best of it) made a violent demonstration and closed with the tall sailor, who, after a few unavailing struggles, went down, and expired in great torture as the short sailor put his foot upon his breast, and bored a hole in him through and through.\n\n‘That’ll be a double ENCORE if you take care, boys,’ said Mr. Crummles. ‘You had better get your wind now and change your clothes.’\n\nHaving addressed these words to the combatants, he saluted Nicholas, who then observed that the face of Mr. Crummles was quite proportionate in size to his body; that he had a very full under-lip, a hoarse voice, as though he were in the habit of shouting very much, and very short black hair, shaved off nearly to the crown of his head--to admit (as he afterwards learnt) of his more easily wearing character wigs of any shape or pattern.\n\n‘What did you think of that, sir?’ inquired Mr. Crummles.\n\n‘Very good, indeed--capital,’ answered Nicholas.\n\n‘You won’t see such boys as those very often, I think,’ said Mr Crummles.\n\nNicholas assented--observing that if they were a little better match--\n\n‘Match!’ cried Mr. Crummles.\n\n‘I mean if they were a little more of a size,’ said Nicholas, explaining himself.\n\n‘Size!’ repeated Mr. Crummles; ‘why, it’s the essence of the combat that there should be a foot or two between them. How are you to get up the sympathies of the audience in a legitimate manner, if there isn’t a little man contending against a big one?--unless there’s at least five to one, and we haven’t hands enough for that business in our company.’\n\n‘I see,’ replied Nicholas. ‘I beg your pardon. That didn’t occur to me, I confess.’\n\n‘It’s the main point,’ said Mr. Crummles. ‘I open at Portsmouth the day after tomorrow. If you’re going there, look into the theatre, and see how that’ll tell.’\n\nNicholas promised to do so, if he could, and drawing a chair near the fire, fell into conversation with the manager at once. He was very talkative and communicative, stimulated perhaps, not only by his natural disposition, but by the spirits and water he sipped very plentifully, or the snuff he took in large quantities from a piece of whitey-brown paper in his waistcoat pocket. He laid open his affairs without the smallest reserve, and descanted at some length upon the merits of his company, and the acquirements of his family; of both of which, the two broad-sword boys formed an honourable portion. There was to be a gathering, it seemed, of the different ladies and gentlemen at Portsmouth on the morrow, whither the father and sons were proceeding (not for the regular season, but in the course of a wandering speculation), after fulfilling an engagement at Guildford with the greatest applause.\n\n‘You are going that way?’ asked the manager.\n\n‘Ye-yes,’ said Nicholas. ‘Yes, I am.’\n\n‘Do you know the town at all?’ inquired the manager, who seemed to consider himself entitled to the same degree of confidence as he had himself exhibited.\n\n‘No,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘Never there?’\n\n‘Never.’\nMr. Vincent Crummles gave a short dry cough, as much as to say, ‘If you won’t be communicative, you won’t;’ and took so many pinches of snuff from the piece of paper, one after another, that Nicholas quite wondered where it all went to.\n\nWhile he was thus engaged, Mr. Crummles looked, from time to time, with great interest at Smike, with whom he had appeared considerably struck from the first. He had now fallen asleep, and was nodding in his chair.\n\n‘Excuse my saying so,’ said the manager, leaning over to Nicholas, and sinking his voice, ‘but what a capital countenance your friend has got!’\n\n‘Poor fellow!’ said Nicholas, with a half-smile, ‘I wish it were a little more plump, and less haggard.’\n\n‘Plump!’ exclaimed the manager, quite horrified, ‘you’d spoil it for ever.’\n\n‘Do you think so?’\n\n‘Think so, sir! Why, as he is now,’ said the manager, striking his knee emphatically; ‘without a pad upon his body, and hardly a touch of paint upon his face, he’d make such an actor for the starved business as was never seen in this country. Only let him be tolerably well up in the Apothecary in Romeo and Juliet, with the slightest possible dab of red on the tip of his nose, and he’d be certain of three rounds the moment he put his head out of the practicable door in the front grooves O.P.’\n\n‘You view him with a professional eye,’ said Nicholas, laughing.\n\n‘And well I may,’ rejoined the manager. ‘I never saw a young fellow so regularly cut out for that line, since I’ve been in the profession. And I played the heavy children when I was eighteen months old.’\n\nThe appearance of the beef-steak pudding, which came in simultaneously with the junior Vincent Crummleses, turned the conversation to other matters, and indeed, for a time, stopped it altogether. These two young gentlemen wielded their knives and forks with scarcely less address than their broad-swords, and as the whole party were quite as sharp set as either class of weapons, there was no time for talking until the supper had been disposed of.\n\nThe Master Crummleses had no sooner swallowed the last procurable morsel of food, than they evinced, by various half-suppressed yawns and stretchings of their limbs, an obvious inclination to retire for the night, which Smike had betrayed still more strongly: he having, in the course of the meal, fallen asleep several times while in the very act of eating. Nicholas therefore proposed that they should break up at once, but the manager would by no means hear of it; vowing that he had promised himself the pleasure of inviting his new acquaintance to share a bowl of punch, and that if he declined, he should deem it very unhandsome behaviour.\n\n‘Let them go,’ said Mr. Vincent Crummles, ‘and we’ll have it snugly and cosily together by the fire.’\n\nNicholas was not much disposed to sleep--being in truth too anxious--so, after a little demur, he accepted the offer, and having exchanged a shake of the hand with the young Crummleses, and the manager having on his part bestowed a most affectionate benediction on Smike, he sat himself down opposite to that gentleman by the fireside to assist in emptying the bowl, which soon afterwards appeared, steaming in a manner which was quite exhilarating to behold, and sending forth a most grateful and inviting fragrance.\n\nBut, despite the punch and the manager, who told a variety of stories, and smoked tobacco from a pipe, and inhaled it in the shape of snuff, with a most astonishing power, Nicholas was absent and dispirited. His thoughts were in his old home, and when they reverted to his present condition, the uncertainty of the morrow cast a gloom upon him, which his utmost efforts were unable to dispel. His attention wandered; although he heard the manager’s voice, he was deaf to what he said; and when Mr. Vincent Crummles concluded the history of some long adventure with a loud laugh, and an inquiry what Nicholas would have done under the same circumstances, he was obliged to make the best apology in his power, and to confess his entire ignorance of all he had been talking about.\n\n‘Why, so I saw,’ observed Mr. Crummles. ‘You’re uneasy in your mind. What’s the matter?’\n\nNicholas could not refrain from smiling at the abruptness of the question; but, thinking it scarcely worth while to parry it, owned that he was under some apprehensions lest he might not succeed in the object which had brought him to that part of the country.\n\n‘And what’s that?’ asked the manager.\n\n‘Getting something to do which will keep me and my poor fellow-traveller in the common necessaries of life,’ said Nicholas. ‘That’s the truth. You guessed it long ago, I dare say, so I may as well have the credit of telling it you with a good grace.’\n\n‘What’s to be got to do at Portsmouth more than anywhere else?’ asked Mr Vincent Crummles, melting the sealing-wax on the stem of his pipe in the candle, and rolling it out afresh with his little finger.\n\n‘There are many vessels leaving the port, I suppose,’ replied Nicholas. ‘I shall try for a berth in some ship or other. There is meat and drink there at all events.’\n\n‘Salt meat and new rum; pease-pudding and chaff-biscuits,’ said the manager, taking a whiff at his pipe to keep it alight, and returning to his work of embellishment.\n\n‘One may do worse than that,’ said Nicholas. ‘I can rough it, I believe, as well as most young men of my age and previous habits.’\n\n‘You need be able to,’ said the manager, ‘if you go on board ship; but you won’t.’\n\n‘Why not?’\n\n‘Because there’s not a skipper or mate that would think you worth your salt, when he could get a practised hand,’ replied the manager; ‘and they as plentiful there, as the oysters in the streets.’\n\n‘What do you mean?’ asked Nicholas, alarmed by this prediction, and the confident tone in which it had been uttered. ‘Men are not born able seamen. They must be reared, I suppose?’\n\nMr. Vincent Crummles nodded his head. ‘They must; but not at your age, or from young gentlemen like you.’\n\nThere was a pause. The countenance of Nicholas fell, and he gazed ruefully at the fire.\n\n‘Does no other profession occur to you, which a young man of your figure and address could take up easily, and see the world to advantage in?’ asked the manager.\n\n‘No,’ said Nicholas, shaking his head.\n\n‘Why, then, I’ll tell you one,’ said Mr. Crummles, throwing his pipe into the fire, and raising his voice. ‘The stage.’\n\n‘The stage!’ cried Nicholas, in a voice almost as loud.\n\n‘The theatrical profession,’ said Mr. Vincent Crummles. ‘I am in the theatrical profession myself, my wife is in the theatrical profession, my children are in the theatrical profession. I had a dog that lived and died in it from a puppy; and my chaise-pony goes on, in Timour the Tartar. I’ll bring you out, and your friend too. Say the word. I want a novelty.’\n\n‘I don’t know anything about it,’ rejoined Nicholas, whose breath had been almost taken away by this sudden proposal. ‘I never acted a part in my life, except at school.’\n\n‘There’s genteel comedy in your walk and manner, juvenile tragedy in your eye, and touch-and-go farce in your laugh,’ said Mr. Vincent Crummles. ‘You’ll do as well as if you had thought of nothing else but the lamps, from your birth downwards.’\n\nNicholas thought of the small amount of small change that would remain in his pocket after paying the tavern bill; and he hesitated.\n\n‘You can be useful to us in a hundred ways,’ said Mr. Crummles. ‘Think what capital bills a man of your education could write for the shop-windows.’\n\n‘Well, I think I could manage that department,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘To be sure you could,’ replied Mr. Crummles. ‘“For further particulars see small hand-bills”--we might have half a volume in every one of ‘em. Pieces too; why, you could write us a piece to bring out the whole strength of the company, whenever we wanted one.’\n\n‘I am not quite so confident about that,’ replied Nicholas. ‘But I dare say I could scribble something now and then, that would suit you.’\n\n‘We’ll have a new show-piece out directly,’ said the manager. ‘Let me see--peculiar resources of this establishment--new and splendid scenery--you must manage to introduce a real pump and two washing-tubs.’\n\n‘Into the piece?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Yes,’ replied the manager. ‘I bought ‘em cheap, at a sale the other day, and they’ll come in admirably. That’s the London plan. They look up some dresses, and properties, and have a piece written to fit ‘em. Most of the theatres keep an author on purpose.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ cried Nicholas.\n\n‘Oh, yes,’ said the manager; ‘a common thing. It’ll look very well in the bills in separate lines--Real pump!--Splendid tubs!--Great attraction! You don’t happen to be anything of an artist, do you?’\n\n‘That is not one of my accomplishments,’ rejoined Nicholas.\n\n‘Ah! Then it can’t be helped,’ said the manager. ‘If you had been, we might have had a large woodcut of the last scene for the posters, showing the whole depth of the stage, with the pump and tubs in the middle; but, however, if you’re not, it can’t be helped.’\n\n‘What should I get for all this?’ inquired Nicholas, after a few moments’ reflection. ‘Could I live by it?’\n\n‘Live by it!’ said the manager. ‘Like a prince! With your own salary, and your friend’s, and your writings, you’d make--ah! you’d make a pound a week!’\n\n‘You don’t say so!’\n\n‘I do indeed, and if we had a run of good houses, nearly double the money.’\n\nNicholas shrugged his shoulders; but sheer destitution was before him; and if he could summon fortitude to undergo the extremes of want and hardship, for what had he rescued his helpless charge if it were only to bear as hard a fate as that from which he had wrested him? It was easy to think of seventy miles as nothing, when he was in the same town with the man who had treated him so ill and roused his bitterest thoughts; but now, it seemed far enough. What if he went abroad, and his mother or Kate were to die the while?\n\nWithout more deliberation, he hastily declared that it was a bargain, and gave Mr. Vincent Crummles his hand upon it.\n\n# CHAPTER 23\n\nTreats of the Company of Mr. Vincent Crummles, and of his Affairs, Domestic and Theatrical\nAs Mr. Crummles had a strange four-legged animal in the inn stables, which he called a pony, and a vehicle of unknown design, on which he bestowed the appellation of a four-wheeled phaeton, Nicholas proceeded on his journey next morning with greater ease than he had expected: the manager and himself occupying the front seat: and the Master Crummleses and Smike being packed together behind, in company with a wicker basket defended from wet by a stout oilskin, in which were the broad-swords, pistols, pigtails, nautical costumes, and other professional necessaries of the aforesaid young gentlemen.\n\nThe pony took his time upon the road, and--possibly in consequence of his theatrical education--evinced, every now and then, a strong inclination to lie down. However, Mr. Vincent Crummles kept him up pretty well, by jerking the rein, and plying the whip; and when these means failed, and the animal came to a stand, the elder Master Crummles got out and kicked him. By dint of these encouragements, he was persuaded to move from time to time, and they jogged on (as Mr. Crummles truly observed) very comfortably for all parties.\n\n‘He’s a good pony at bottom,’ said Mr. Crummles, turning to Nicholas.\n\nHe might have been at bottom, but he certainly was not at top, seeing that his coat was of the roughest and most ill-favoured kind. So, Nicholas merely observed that he shouldn’t wonder if he was.\n\n‘Many and many is the circuit this pony has gone,’ said Mr. Crummles, flicking him skilfully on the eyelid for old acquaintance’ sake. ‘He is quite one of us. His mother was on the stage.’\n\n‘Was she?’ rejoined Nicholas.\n\n‘She ate apple-pie at a circus for upwards of fourteen years,’ said the manager; ‘fired pistols, and went to bed in a nightcap; and, in short, took the low comedy entirely. His father was a dancer.’\n\n‘Was he at all distinguished?’\n\n‘Not very,’ said the manager. ‘He was rather a low sort of pony. The fact is, he had been originally jobbed out by the day, and he never quite got over his old habits. He was clever in melodrama too, but too broad--too broad. When the mother died, he took the port-wine business.’\n\n‘The port-wine business!’ cried Nicholas.\n\n‘Drinking port-wine with the clown,’ said the manager; ‘but he was greedy, and one night bit off the bowl of the glass, and choked himself, so his vulgarity was the death of him at last.’\n\nThe descendant of this ill-starred animal requiring increased attention from Mr. Crummles as he progressed in his day’s work, that gentleman had very little time for conversation. Nicholas was thus left at leisure to entertain himself with his own thoughts, until they arrived at the drawbridge at Portsmouth, when Mr. Crummles pulled up.\n\n‘We’ll get down here,’ said the manager, ‘and the boys will take him round to the stable, and call at my lodgings with the luggage. You had better let yours be taken there, for the present.’\n\nThanking Mr. Vincent Crummles for his obliging offer, Nicholas jumped out, and, giving Smike his arm, accompanied the manager up High Street on their way to the theatre; feeling nervous and uncomfortable enough at the prospect of an immediate introduction to a scene so new to him.\n\nThey passed a great many bills, pasted against the walls and displayed in windows, wherein the names of Mr. Vincent Crummles, Mrs. Vincent Crummles, Master Crummles, Master P. Crummles, and Miss Crummles, were printed in very large letters, and everything else in very small ones; and, turning at length into an entry, in which was a strong smell of orange-peel and lamp-oil, with an under-current of sawdust, groped their way through a dark passage, and, descending a step or two, threaded a little maze of canvas screens and paint pots, and emerged upon the stage of the Portsmouth Theatre.\n\n‘Here we are,’ said Mr. Crummles.\n\nIt was not very light, but Nicholas found himself close to the first entrance on the prompt side, among bare walls, dusty scenes, mildewed clouds, heavily daubed draperies, and dirty floors. He looked about him; ceiling, pit, boxes, gallery, orchestra, fittings, and decorations of every kind,--all looked coarse, cold, gloomy, and wretched.\n\n‘Is this a theatre?’ whispered Smike, in amazement; ‘I thought it was a blaze of light and finery.’\n\n‘Why, so it is,’ replied Nicholas, hardly less surprised; ‘but not by day, Smike--not by day.’\n\nThe manager’s voice recalled him from a more careful inspection of the building, to the opposite side of the proscenium, where, at a small mahogany table with rickety legs and of an oblong shape, sat a stout, portly female, apparently between forty and fifty, in a tarnished silk cloak, with her bonnet dangling by the strings in her hand, and her hair (of which she had a great quantity) braided in a large festoon over each temple.\n\n‘Mr. Johnson,’ said the manager (for Nicholas had given the name which Newman Noggs had bestowed upon him in his conversation with Mrs Kenwigs), ‘let me introduce Mrs. Vincent Crummles.’\n\n‘I am glad to see you, sir,’ said Mrs. Vincent Crummles, in a sepulchral voice. ‘I am very glad to see you, and still more happy to hail you as a promising member of our corps.’\n\nThe lady shook Nicholas by the hand as she addressed him in these terms; he saw it was a large one, but had not expected quite such an iron grip as that with which she honoured him.\n\n‘And this,’ said the lady, crossing to Smike, as tragic actresses cross when they obey a stage direction, ‘and this is the other. You too, are welcome, sir.’\n\n‘He’ll do, I think, my dear?’ said the manager, taking a pinch of snuff.\n\n‘He is admirable,’ replied the lady. ‘An acquisition indeed.’\n\nAs Mrs. Vincent Crummles recrossed back to the table, there bounded on to the stage from some mysterious inlet, a little girl in a dirty white frock with tucks up to the knees, short trousers, sandaled shoes, white spencer, pink gauze bonnet, green veil and curl papers; who turned a pirouette, cut twice in the air, turned another pirouette, then, looking off at the opposite wing, shrieked, bounded forward to within six inches of the footlights, and fell into a beautiful attitude of terror, as a shabby gentleman in an old pair of buff slippers came in at one powerful slide, and chattering his teeth, fiercely brandished a walking-stick.\n\n‘They are going through the Indian Savage and the Maiden,’ said Mrs Crummles.\n\n‘Oh!’ said the manager, ‘the little ballet interlude. Very good, go on. A little this way, if you please, Mr. Johnson. That’ll do. Now!’\n\nThe manager clapped his hands as a signal to proceed, and the savage, becoming ferocious, made a slide towards the maiden; but the maiden avoided him in six twirls, and came down, at the end of the last one, upon the very points of her toes. This seemed to make some impression upon the savage; for, after a little more ferocity and chasing of the maiden into corners, he began to relent, and stroked his face several times with his right thumb and four fingers, thereby intimating that he was struck with admiration of the maiden’s beauty. Acting upon the impulse of this passion, he (the savage) began to hit himself severe thumps in the chest, and to exhibit other indications of being desperately in love, which being rather a prosy proceeding, was very likely the cause of the maiden’s falling asleep; whether it was or no, asleep she did fall, sound as a church, on a sloping bank, and the savage perceiving it, leant his left ear on his left hand, and nodded sideways, to intimate to all whom it might concern that she WAS asleep, and no shamming. Being left to himself, the savage had a dance, all alone. Just as he left off, the maiden woke up, rubbed her eyes, got off the bank, and had a dance all alone too--such a dance that the savage looked on in ecstasy all the while, and when it was done, plucked from a neighbouring tree some botanical curiosity, resembling a small pickled cabbage, and offered it to the maiden, who at first wouldn’t have it, but on the savage shedding tears relented. Then the savage jumped for joy; then the maiden jumped for rapture at the sweet smell of the pickled cabbage. Then the savage and the maiden danced violently together, and, finally, the savage dropped down on one knee, and the maiden stood on one leg upon his other knee; thus concluding the ballet, and leaving the spectators in a state of pleasing uncertainty, whether she would ultimately marry the savage, or return to her friends.\n\n‘Very well indeed,’ said Mr. Crummles; ‘bravo!’\n\n‘Bravo!’ cried Nicholas, resolved to make the best of everything. ‘Beautiful!’\n\n‘This, sir,’ said Mr. Vincent Crummles, bringing the maiden forward, ‘this is the infant phenomenon--Miss Ninetta Crummles.’\n\n‘Your daughter?’ inquired Nicholas.\n\n‘My daughter--my daughter,’ replied Mr. Vincent Crummles; ‘the idol of every place we go into, sir. We have had complimentary letters about this girl, sir, from the nobility and gentry of almost every town in England.’\n\n‘I am not surprised at that,’ said Nicholas; ‘she must be quite a natural genius.’\n\n‘Quite a--!’ Mr. Crummles stopped: language was not powerful enough to describe the infant phenomenon. ‘I’ll tell you what, sir,’ he said; ‘the talent of this child is not to be imagined. She must be seen, sir--seen--to be ever so faintly appreciated. There; go to your mother, my dear.’\n\n‘May I ask how old she is?’ inquired Nicholas.\n\n‘You may, sir,’ replied Mr. Crummles, looking steadily in his questioner’s face, as some men do when they have doubts about being implicitly believed in what they are going to say. ‘She is ten years of age, sir.’\n\n‘Not more!’\n‘Not a day.’\n\n‘Dear me!’ said Nicholas, ‘it’s extraordinary.’\n\nIt was; for the infant phenomenon, though of short stature, had a comparatively aged countenance, and had moreover been precisely the same age--not perhaps to the full extent of the memory of the oldest inhabitant, but certainly for five good years. But she had been kept up late every night, and put upon an unlimited allowance of gin-and-water from infancy, to prevent her growing tall, and perhaps this system of training had produced in the infant phenomenon these additional phenomena.\n\nWhile this short dialogue was going on, the gentleman who had enacted the savage, came up, with his walking shoes on his feet, and his slippers in his hand, to within a few paces, as if desirous to join in the conversation. Deeming this a good opportunity, he put in his word.\n\n‘Talent there, sir!’ said the savage, nodding towards Miss Crummles.\n\nNicholas assented.\n\n‘Ah!’ said the actor, setting his teeth together, and drawing in his breath with a hissing sound, ‘she oughtn’t to be in the provinces, she oughtn’t.’\n\n‘What do you mean?’ asked the manager.\n\n‘I mean to say,’ replied the other, warmly, ‘that she is too good for country boards, and that she ought to be in one of the large houses in London, or nowhere; and I tell you more, without mincing the matter, that if it wasn’t for envy and jealousy in some quarter that you know of, she would be. Perhaps you’ll introduce me here, Mr. Crummles.’\n\n‘Mr. Folair,’ said the manager, presenting him to Nicholas.\n\n‘Happy to know you, sir.’ Mr. Folair touched the brim of his hat with his forefinger, and then shook hands. ‘A recruit, sir, I understand?’\n\n‘An unworthy one,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘Did you ever see such a set-out as that?’ whispered the actor, drawing him away, as Crummles left them to speak to his wife.\n\n‘As what?’\n\nMr. Folair made a funny face from his pantomime collection, and pointed over his shoulder.\n\n‘You don’t mean the infant phenomenon?’\n\n‘Infant humbug, sir,’ replied Mr. Folair. ‘There isn’t a female child of common sharpness in a charity school, that couldn’t do better than that. She may thank her stars she was born a manager’s daughter.’\n\n‘You seem to take it to heart,’ observed Nicholas, with a smile.\n\n‘Yes, by Jove, and well I may,’ said Mr. Folair, drawing his arm through his, and walking him up and down the stage. ‘Isn’t it enough to make a man crusty to see that little sprawler put up in the best business every night, and actually keeping money out of the house, by being forced down the people’s throats, while other people are passed over? Isn’t it extraordinary to see a man’s confounded family conceit blinding him, even to his own interest? Why I KNOW of fifteen and sixpence that came to Southampton one night last month, to see me dance the Highland Fling; and what’s the consequence? I’ve never been put up in it since--never once--while the “infant phenomenon” has been grinning through artificial flowers at five people and a baby in the pit, and two boys in the gallery, every night.’\n\n‘If I may judge from what I have seen of you,’ said Nicholas, ‘you must be a valuable member of the company.’\n\n‘Oh!’ replied Mr. Folair, beating his slippers together, to knock the dust out; ‘I CAN come it pretty well--nobody better, perhaps, in my own line--but having such business as one gets here, is like putting lead on one’s feet instead of chalk, and dancing in fetters without the credit of it. Holloa, old fellow, how are you?’\n\nThe gentleman addressed in these latter words was a dark-complexioned man, inclining indeed to sallow, with long thick black hair, and very evident inclinations (although he was close shaved) of a stiff beard, and whiskers of the same deep shade. His age did not appear to exceed thirty, though many at first sight would have considered him much older, as his face was long, and very pale, from the constant application of stage paint. He wore a checked shirt, an old green coat with new gilt buttons, a neckerchief of broad red and green stripes, and full blue trousers; he carried, too, a common ash walking-stick, apparently more for show than use, as he flourished it about, with the hooked end downwards, except when he raised it for a few seconds, and throwing himself into a fencing attitude, made a pass or two at the side-scenes, or at any other object, animate or inanimate, that chanced to afford him a pretty good mark at the moment.\n\n‘Well, Tommy,’ said this gentleman, making a thrust at his friend, who parried it dexterously with his slipper, ‘what’s the news?’\n\n‘A new appearance, that’s all,’ replied Mr. Folair, looking at Nicholas.\n\n‘Do the honours, Tommy, do the honours,’ said the other gentleman, tapping him reproachfully on the crown of the hat with his stick.\n\n‘This is Mr. Lenville, who does our first tragedy, Mr. Johnson,’ said the pantomimist.\n\n‘Except when old bricks and mortar takes it into his head to do it himself, you should add, Tommy,’ remarked Mr. Lenville. ‘You know who bricks and mortar is, I suppose, sir?’\n\n‘I do not, indeed,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘We call Crummles that, because his style of acting is rather in the heavy and ponderous way,’ said Mr. Lenville. ‘I mustn’t be cracking jokes though, for I’ve got a part of twelve lengths here, which I must be up in tomorrow night, and I haven’t had time to look at it yet; I’m a confounded quick study, that’s one comfort.’\n\nConsoling himself with this reflection, Mr. Lenville drew from his coat pocket a greasy and crumpled manuscript, and, having made another pass at his friend, proceeded to walk to and fro, conning it to himself and indulging occasionally in such appropriate action as his imagination and the text suggested.\n\nA pretty general muster of the company had by this time taken place; for besides Mr. Lenville and his friend Tommy, there were present, a slim young gentleman with weak eyes, who played the low-spirited lovers and sang tenor songs, and who had come arm-in-arm with the comic countryman--a man with a turned-up nose, large mouth, broad face, and staring eyes. Making himself very amiable to the infant phenomenon, was an inebriated elderly gentleman in the last depths of shabbiness, who played the calm and virtuous old men; and paying especial court to Mrs Crummles was another elderly gentleman, a shade more respectable, who played the irascible old men--those funny fellows who have nephews in the army and perpetually run about with thick sticks to compel them to marry heiresses. Besides these, there was a roving-looking person in a rough great-coat, who strode up and down in front of the lamps, flourishing a dress cane, and rattling away, in an undertone, with great vivacity for the amusement of an ideal audience. He was not quite so young as he had been, and his figure was rather running to seed; but there was an air of exaggerated gentility about him, which bespoke the hero of swaggering comedy. There was, also, a little group of three or four young men with lantern jaws and thick eyebrows, who were conversing in one corner; but they seemed to be of secondary importance, and laughed and talked together without attracting any attention.\n\nThe ladies were gathered in a little knot by themselves round the rickety table before mentioned. There was Miss Snevellicci--who could do anything, from a medley dance to Lady Macbeth, and also always played some part in blue silk knee-smalls at her benefit--glancing, from the depths of her coal-scuttle straw bonnet, at Nicholas, and affecting to be absorbed in the recital of a diverting story to her friend Miss Ledrook, who had brought her work, and was making up a ruff in the most natural manner possible. There was Miss Belvawney--who seldom aspired to speaking parts, and usually went on as a page in white silk hose, to stand with one leg bent, and contemplate the audience, or to go in and out after Mr. Crummles in stately tragedy--twisting up the ringlets of the beautiful Miss Bravassa, who had once had her likeness taken ‘in character’ by an engraver’s apprentice, whereof impressions were hung up for sale in the pastry-cook’s window, and the greengrocer’s, and at the circulating library, and the box-office, whenever the announce bills came out for her annual night. There was Mrs. Lenville, in a very limp bonnet and veil, decidedly in that way in which she would wish to be if she truly loved Mr. Lenville; there was Miss Gazingi, with an imitation ermine boa tied in a loose knot round her neck, flogging Mr. Crummles, junior, with both ends, in fun. Lastly, there was Mrs. Grudden in a brown cloth pelisse and a beaver bonnet, who assisted Mrs. Crummles in her domestic affairs, and took money at the doors, and dressed the ladies, and swept the house, and held the prompt book when everybody else was on for the last scene, and acted any kind of part on any emergency without ever learning it, and was put down in the bills under any name or names whatever, that occurred to Mr. Crummles as looking well in print.\n\nMr. Folair having obligingly confided these particulars to Nicholas, left him to mingle with his fellows; the work of personal introduction was completed by Mr. Vincent Crummles, who publicly heralded the new actor as a prodigy of genius and learning.\n\n‘I beg your pardon,’ said Miss Snevellicci, sidling towards Nicholas, ‘but did you ever play at Canterbury?’\n\n‘I never did,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘I recollect meeting a gentleman at Canterbury,’ said Miss Snevellicci, ‘only for a few moments, for I was leaving the company as he joined it, so like you that I felt almost certain it was the same.’\n\n‘I see you now for the first time,’ rejoined Nicholas with all due gallantry. ‘I am sure I never saw you before; I couldn’t have forgotten it.’\n\n‘Oh, I’m sure--it’s very flattering of you to say so,’ retorted Miss Snevellicci with a graceful bend. ‘Now I look at you again, I see that the gentleman at Canterbury hadn’t the same eyes as you--you’ll think me very foolish for taking notice of such things, won’t you?’\n\n‘Not at all,’ said Nicholas. ‘How can I feel otherwise than flattered by your notice in any way?’\n\n‘Oh! you men are such vain creatures!’ cried Miss Snevellicci. Whereupon, she became charmingly confused, and, pulling out her pocket-handkerchief from a faded pink silk reticule with a gilt clasp, called to Miss Ledrook--\n\n‘Led, my dear,’ said Miss Snevellicci.\n\n‘Well, what is the matter?’ said Miss Ledrook.\n\n‘It’s not the same.’\n\n‘Not the same what?’\n\n‘Canterbury--you know what I mean. Come here! I want to speak to you.’\n\nBut Miss Ledrook wouldn’t come to Miss Snevellicci, so Miss Snevellicci was obliged to go to Miss Ledrook, which she did, in a skipping manner that was quite fascinating; and Miss Ledrook evidently joked Miss Snevellicci about being struck with Nicholas; for, after some playful whispering, Miss Snevellicci hit Miss Ledrook very hard on the backs of her hands, and retired up, in a state of pleasing confusion.\n\n‘Ladies and gentlemen,’ said Mr. Vincent Crummles, who had been writing on a piece of paper, ‘we’ll call the Mortal Struggle tomorrow at ten; everybody for the procession. Intrigue, and Ways and Means, you’re all up in, so we shall only want one rehearsal. Everybody at ten, if you please.’\n\n‘Everybody at ten,’ repeated Mrs. Grudden, looking about her.\n\n‘On Monday morning we shall read a new piece,’ said Mr. Crummles; ‘the name’s not known yet, but everybody will have a good part. Mr. Johnson will take care of that.’\n\n‘Hallo!’ said Nicholas, starting. ‘I--’\n\n‘On Monday morning,’ repeated Mr. Crummles, raising his voice, to drown the unfortunate Mr. Johnson’s remonstrance; ‘that’ll do, ladies and gentlemen.’\n\nThe ladies and gentlemen required no second notice to quit; and, in a few minutes, the theatre was deserted, save by the Crummles family, Nicholas, and Smike.\n\n‘Upon my word,’ said Nicholas, taking the manager aside, ‘I don’t think I can be ready by Monday.’\n\n‘Pooh, pooh,’ replied Mr. Crummles.\n\n‘But really I can’t,’ returned Nicholas; ‘my invention is not accustomed to these demands, or possibly I might produce--’\n\n‘Invention! what the devil’s that got to do with it!’ cried the manager hastily.\n\n‘Everything, my dear sir.’\n\n‘Nothing, my dear sir,’ retorted the manager, with evident impatience. ‘Do you understand French?’\n\n‘Perfectly well.’\n\n‘Very good,’ said the manager, opening the table drawer, and giving a roll of paper from it to Nicholas. ‘There! Just turn that into English, and put your name on the title-page. Damn me,’ said Mr. Crummles, angrily, ‘if I haven’t often said that I wouldn’t have a man or woman in my company that wasn’t master of the language, so that they might learn it from the original, and play it in English, and save all this trouble and expense.’\n\nNicholas smiled and pocketed the play.\n\n‘What are you going to do about your lodgings?’ said Mr. Crummles.\n\nNicholas could not help thinking that, for the first week, it would be an uncommon convenience to have a turn-up bedstead in the pit, but he merely remarked that he had not turned his thoughts that way.\n\n‘Come home with me then,’ said Mr. Crummles, ‘and my boys shall go with you after dinner, and show you the most likely place.’\n\nThe offer was not to be refused; Nicholas and Mr. Crummles gave Mrs Crummles an arm each, and walked up the street in stately array. Smike, the boys, and the phenomenon, went home by a shorter cut, and Mrs Grudden remained behind to take some cold Irish stew and a pint of porter in the box-office.\n\nMrs. Crummles trod the pavement as if she were going to immediate execution with an animating consciousness of innocence, and that heroic fortitude which virtue alone inspires. Mr. Crummles, on the other hand, assumed the look and gait of a hardened despot; but they both attracted some notice from many of the passers-by, and when they heard a whisper of ‘Mr. and Mrs. Crummles!’ or saw a little boy run back to stare them in the face, the severe expression of their countenances relaxed, for they felt it was popularity.\n\nMr. Crummles lived in St Thomas’s Street, at the house of one Bulph, a pilot, who sported a boat-green door, with window-frames of the same colour, and had the little finger of a drowned man on his parlour mantelshelf, with other maritime and natural curiosities. He displayed also a brass knocker, a brass plate, and a brass bell-handle, all very bright and shining; and had a mast, with a vane on the top of it, in his back yard.\n\n‘You are welcome,’ said Mrs. Crummles, turning round to Nicholas when they reached the bow-windowed front room on the first floor.\n\nNicholas bowed his acknowledgments, and was unfeignedly glad to see the cloth laid.\n\n‘We have but a shoulder of mutton with onion sauce,’ said Mrs. Crummles, in the same charnel-house voice; ‘but such as our dinner is, we beg you to partake of it.’\n\n‘You are very good,’ replied Nicholas, ‘I shall do it ample justice.’\n\n‘Vincent,’ said Mrs. Crummles, ‘what is the hour?’\n\n‘Five minutes past dinner-time,’ said Mr. Crummles.\n\nMrs. Crummles rang the bell. ‘Let the mutton and onion sauce appear.’\n\nThe slave who attended upon Mr. Bulph’s lodgers, disappeared, and after a short interval reappeared with the festive banquet. Nicholas and the infant phenomenon opposed each other at the pembroke-table, and Smike and the master Crummleses dined on the sofa bedstead.\n\n‘Are they very theatrical people here?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘No,’ replied Mr. Crummles, shaking his head, ‘far from it--far from it.’\n\n‘I pity them,’ observed Mrs. Crummles.\n\n‘So do I,’ said Nicholas; ‘if they have no relish for theatrical entertainments, properly conducted.’\n\n‘Then they have none, sir,’ rejoined Mr. Crummles. ‘To the infant’s benefit, last year, on which occasion she repeated three of her most popular characters, and also appeared in the Fairy Porcupine, as originally performed by her, there was a house of no more than four pound twelve.’\n\n‘Is it possible?’ cried Nicholas.\n\n‘And two pound of that was trust, pa,’ said the phenomenon.\n\n‘And two pound of that was trust,’ repeated Mr. Crummles. ‘Mrs. Crummles herself has played to mere handfuls.’\n\n‘But they are always a taking audience, Vincent,’ said the manager’s wife.\n\n‘Most audiences are, when they have good acting--real good acting--the regular thing,’ replied Mr. Crummles, forcibly.\n\n‘Do you give lessons, ma’am?’ inquired Nicholas.\n\n‘I do,’ said Mrs. Crummles.\n\n‘There is no teaching here, I suppose?’\n\n‘There has been,’ said Mrs. Crummles. ‘I have received pupils here. I imparted tuition to the daughter of a dealer in ships’ provision; but it afterwards appeared that she was insane when she first came to me. It was very extraordinary that she should come, under such circumstances.’\n\nNot feeling quite so sure of that, Nicholas thought it best to hold his peace.\n\n‘Let me see,’ said the manager cogitating after dinner. ‘Would you like some nice little part with the infant?’\n\n‘You are very good,’ replied Nicholas hastily; ‘but I think perhaps it would be better if I had somebody of my own size at first, in case I should turn out awkward. I should feel more at home, perhaps.’\n\n‘True,’ said the manager. ‘Perhaps you would. And you could play up to the infant, in time, you know.’\n\n‘Certainly,’ replied Nicholas: devoutly hoping that it would be a very long time before he was honoured with this distinction.\n\n‘Then I’ll tell you what we’ll do,’ said Mr. Crummles. ‘You shall study Romeo when you’ve done that piece--don’t forget to throw the pump and tubs in by-the-bye--Juliet Miss Snevellicci, old Grudden the nurse.--Yes, that’ll do very well. Rover too;--you might get up Rover while you were about it, and Cassio, and Jeremy Diddler. You can easily knock them off; one part helps the other so much. Here they are, cues and all.’\n\nWith these hasty general directions Mr. Crummles thrust a number of little books into the faltering hands of Nicholas, and bidding his eldest son go with him and show where lodgings were to be had, shook him by the hand, and wished him good night.\n\nThere is no lack of comfortable furnished apartments in Portsmouth, and no difficulty in finding some that are proportionate to very slender finances; but the former were too good, and the latter too bad, and they went into so many houses, and came out unsuited, that Nicholas seriously began to think he should be obliged to ask permission to spend the night in the theatre, after all.\n\nEventually, however, they stumbled upon two small rooms up three pair of stairs, or rather two pair and a ladder, at a tobacconist’s shop, on the Common Hard: a dirty street leading down to the dockyard. These Nicholas engaged, only too happy to have escaped any request for payment of a week’s rent beforehand.\n\n‘There! Lay down our personal property, Smike,’ he said, after showing young Crummles downstairs. ‘We have fallen upon strange times, and Heaven only knows the end of them; but I am tired with the events of these three days, and will postpone reflection till tomorrow--if I can.’\n\n# CHAPTER 24\n\nOf the Great Bespeak for Miss Snevellicci, and the first Appearance of Nicholas upon any Stage\nNicholas was up betimes in the morning; but he had scarcely begun to dress, notwithstanding, when he heard footsteps ascending the stairs, and was presently saluted by the voices of Mr. Folair the pantomimist, and Mr. Lenville, the tragedian.\n\n‘House, house, house!’ cried Mr. Folair.\n\n‘What, ho! within there,’ said Mr. Lenville, in a deep voice.\n\n‘Confound these fellows!’ thought Nicholas; ‘they have come to breakfast, I suppose. I’ll open the door directly, if you’ll wait an instant.’\n\nThe gentlemen entreated him not to hurry himself; and, to beguile the interval, had a fencing bout with their walking-sticks on the very small landing-place: to the unspeakable discomposure of all the other lodgers downstairs.\n\n‘Here, come in,’ said Nicholas, when he had completed his toilet. ‘In the name of all that’s horrible, don’t make that noise outside.’\n\n‘An uncommon snug little box this,’ said Mr. Lenville, stepping into the front room, and taking his hat off, before he could get in at all. ‘Pernicious snug.’\n\n‘For a man at all particular in such matters, it might be a trifle too snug,’ said Nicholas; ‘for, although it is, undoubtedly, a great convenience to be able to reach anything you want from the ceiling or the floor, or either side of the room, without having to move from your chair, still these advantages can only be had in an apartment of the most limited size.’\n\n‘It isn’t a bit too confined for a single man,’ returned Mr. Lenville. ‘That reminds me,--my wife, Mr. Johnson,--I hope she’ll have some good part in this piece of yours?’\n\n‘I glanced at the French copy last night,’ said Nicholas. ‘It looks very good, I think.’\n\n‘What do you mean to do for me, old fellow?’ asked Mr. Lenville, poking the struggling fire with his walking-stick, and afterwards wiping it on the skirt of his coat. ‘Anything in the gruff and grumble way?’\n\n‘You turn your wife and child out of doors,’ said Nicholas; ‘and, in a fit of rage and jealousy, stab your eldest son in the library.’\n\n‘Do I though!’ exclaimed Mr. Lenville. ‘That’s very good business.’\n\n‘After which,’ said Nicholas, ‘you are troubled with remorse till the last act, and then you make up your mind to destroy yourself. But, just as you are raising the pistol to your head, a clock strikes--ten.’\n\n‘I see,’ cried Mr. Lenville. ‘Very good.’\n\n‘You pause,’ said Nicholas; ‘you recollect to have heard a clock strike ten in your infancy. The pistol falls from your hand--you are overcome--you burst into tears, and become a virtuous and exemplary character for ever afterwards.’\n\n‘Capital!’ said Mr. Lenville: ‘that’s a sure card, a sure card. Get the curtain down with a touch of nature like that, and it’ll be a triumphant success.’\n\n‘Is there anything good for me?’ inquired Mr. Folair, anxiously.\n\n‘Let me see,’ said Nicholas. ‘You play the faithful and attached servant; you are turned out of doors with the wife and child.’\n\n‘Always coupled with that infernal phenomenon,’ sighed Mr. Folair; ‘and we go into poor lodgings, where I won’t take any wages, and talk sentiment, I suppose?’\n\n‘Why--yes,’ replied Nicholas: ‘that is the course of the piece.’\n\n‘I must have a dance of some kind, you know,’ said Mr. Folair. ‘You’ll have to introduce one for the phenomenon, so you’d better make a PAS DE DEUX, and save time.’\n\n‘There’s nothing easier than that,’ said Mr. Lenville, observing the disturbed looks of the young dramatist.\n\n‘Upon my word I don’t see how it’s to be done,’ rejoined Nicholas.\n\n‘Why, isn’t it obvious?’ reasoned Mr. Lenville. ‘Gadzooks, who can help seeing the way to do it?--you astonish me! You get the distressed lady, and the little child, and the attached servant, into the poor lodgings, don’t you?--Well, look here. The distressed lady sinks into a chair, and buries her face in her pocket-handkerchief. “What makes you weep, mama?” says the child. “Don’t weep, mama, or you’ll make me weep too!”--“And me!” says the favourite servant, rubbing his eyes with his arm. “What can we do to raise your spirits, dear mama?” says the little child. “Ay, what CAN we do?” says the faithful servant. “Oh, Pierre!” says the distressed lady; “would that I could shake off these painful thoughts.”--“Try, ma’am, try,” says the faithful servant; “rouse yourself, ma’am; be amused.”--“I will,” says the lady, “I will learn to suffer with fortitude. Do you remember that dance, my honest friend, which, in happier days, you practised with this sweet angel? It never failed to calm my spirits then. Oh! let me see it once again before I die!”--There it is--cue for the band, BEFORE I DIE,--and off they go. That’s the regular thing; isn’t it, Tommy?’\n\n‘That’s it,’ replied Mr. Folair. ‘The distressed lady, overpowered by old recollections, faints at the end of the dance, and you close in with a picture.’\n\nProfiting by these and other lessons, which were the result of the personal experience of the two actors, Nicholas willingly gave them the best breakfast he could, and, when he at length got rid of them, applied himself to his task: by no means displeased to find that it was so much easier than he had at first supposed. He worked very hard all day, and did not leave his room until the evening, when he went down to the theatre, whither Smike had repaired before him to go on with another gentleman as a general rebellion.\n\nHere all the people were so much changed, that he scarcely knew them. False hair, false colour, false calves, false muscles--they had become different beings. Mr. Lenville was a blooming warrior of most exquisite proportions; Mr. Crummles, his large face shaded by a profusion of black hair, a Highland outlaw of most majestic bearing; one of the old gentlemen a jailer, and the other a venerable patriarch; the comic countryman, a fighting-man of great valour, relieved by a touch of humour; each of the Master Crummleses a prince in his own right; and the low-spirited lover, a desponding captive. There was a gorgeous banquet ready spread for the third act, consisting of two pasteboard vases, one plate of biscuits, a black bottle, and a vinegar cruet; and, in short, everything was on a scale of the utmost splendour and preparation.\n\nNicholas was standing with his back to the curtain, now contemplating the first scene, which was a Gothic archway, about two feet shorter than Mr. Crummles, through which that gentleman was to make his first entrance, and now listening to a couple of people who were cracking nuts in the gallery, wondering whether they made the whole audience, when the manager himself walked familiarly up and accosted him.\n\n‘Been in front tonight?’ said Mr. Crummles.\n\n‘No,’ replied Nicholas, ‘not yet. I am going to see the play.’\n\n‘We’ve had a pretty good Let,’ said Mr. Crummles. ‘Four front places in the centre, and the whole of the stage-box.’\n\n‘Oh, indeed!’ said Nicholas; ‘a family, I suppose?’\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Mr. Crummles, ‘yes. It’s an affecting thing. There are six children, and they never come unless the phenomenon plays.’\n\nIt would have been difficult for any party, family, or otherwise, to have visited the theatre on a night when the phenomenon did NOT play, inasmuch as she always sustained one, and not uncommonly two or three, characters, every night; but Nicholas, sympathising with the feelings of a father, refrained from hinting at this trifling circumstance, and Mr Crummles continued to talk, uninterrupted by him.\n\n‘Six,’ said that gentleman; ‘pa and ma eight, aunt nine, governess ten, grandfather and grandmother twelve. Then, there’s the footman, who stands outside, with a bag of oranges and a jug of toast-and-water, and sees the play for nothing through the little pane of glass in the box-door--it’s cheap at a guinea; they gain by taking a box.’\n\n‘I wonder you allow so many,’ observed Nicholas.\n\n‘There’s no help for it,’ replied Mr. Crummles; ‘it’s always expected in the country. If there are six children, six people come to hold them in their laps. A family-box carries double always. Ring in the orchestra, Grudden!’\n\nThat useful lady did as she was requested, and shortly afterwards the tuning of three fiddles was heard. Which process having been protracted as long as it was supposed that the patience of the audience could possibly bear it, was put a stop to by another jerk of the bell, which, being the signal to begin in earnest, set the orchestra playing a variety of popular airs, with involuntary variations.\n\nIf Nicholas had been astonished at the alteration for the better which the gentlemen displayed, the transformation of the ladies was still more extraordinary. When, from a snug corner of the manager’s box, he beheld Miss Snevellicci in all the glories of white muslin with a golden hem, and Mrs. Crummles in all the dignity of the outlaw’s wife, and Miss Bravassa in all the sweetness of Miss Snevellicci’s confidential friend, and Miss Belvawney in the white silks of a page doing duty everywhere and swearing to live and die in the service of everybody, he could scarcely contain his admiration, which testified itself in great applause, and the closest possible attention to the business of the scene. The plot was most interesting. It belonged to no particular age, people, or country, and was perhaps the more delightful on that account, as nobody’s previous information could afford the remotest glimmering of what would ever come of it. An outlaw had been very successful in doing something somewhere, and came home, in triumph, to the sound of shouts and fiddles, to greet his wife--a lady of masculine mind, who talked a good deal about her father’s bones, which it seemed were unburied, though whether from a peculiar taste on the part of the old gentleman himself, or the reprehensible neglect of his relations, did not appear. This outlaw’s wife was, somehow or other, mixed up with a patriarch, living in a castle a long way off, and this patriarch was the father of several of the characters, but he didn’t exactly know which, and was uncertain whether he had brought up the right ones in his castle, or the wrong ones; he rather inclined to the latter opinion, and, being uneasy, relieved his mind with a banquet, during which solemnity somebody in a cloak said ‘Beware!’ which somebody was known by nobody (except the audience) to be the outlaw himself, who had come there, for reasons unexplained, but possibly with an eye to the spoons. There was an agreeable little surprise in the way of certain love passages between the desponding captive and Miss Snevellicci, and the comic fighting-man and Miss Bravassa; besides which, Mr. Lenville had several very tragic scenes in the dark, while on throat-cutting expeditions, which were all baffled by the skill and bravery of the comic fighting-man (who overheard whatever was said all through the piece) and the intrepidity of Miss Snevellicci, who adopted tights, and therein repaired to the prison of her captive lover, with a small basket of refreshments and a dark lantern. At last, it came out that the patriarch was the man who had treated the bones of the outlaw’s father-in-law with so much disrespect, for which cause and reason the outlaw’s wife repaired to his castle to kill him, and so got into a dark room, where, after a good deal of groping in the dark, everybody got hold of everybody else, and took them for somebody besides, which occasioned a vast quantity of confusion, with some pistolling, loss of life, and torchlight; after which, the patriarch came forward, and observing, with a knowing look, that he knew all about his children now, and would tell them when they got inside, said that there could not be a more appropriate occasion for marrying the young people than that; and therefore he joined their hands, with the full consent of the indefatigable page, who (being the only other person surviving) pointed with his cap into the clouds, and his right hand to the ground; thereby invoking a blessing and giving the cue for the curtain to come down, which it did, amidst general applause.\n\n‘What did you think of that?’ asked Mr. Crummles, when Nicholas went round to the stage again. Mr. Crummles was very red and hot, for your outlaws are desperate fellows to shout.\n\n‘I think it was very capital indeed,’ replied Nicholas; ‘Miss Snevellicci in particular was uncommonly good.’\n\n‘She’s a genius,’ said Mr. Crummles; ‘quite a genius, that girl. By-the-bye, I’ve been thinking of bringing out that piece of yours on her bespeak night.’\n\n‘When?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘The night of her bespeak. Her benefit night, when her friends and patrons bespeak the play,’ said Mr. Crummles.\n\n‘Oh! I understand,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘You see,’ said Mr. Crummles, ‘it’s sure to go, on such an occasion, and even if it should not work up quite as well as we expect, why it will be her risk, you know, and not ours.’\n\n‘Yours, you mean,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘I said mine, didn’t I?’ returned Mr. Crummles. ‘Next Monday week. What do you say? You’ll have done it, and are sure to be up in the lover’s part, long before that time.’\n\n‘I don’t know about “long before,”’ replied Nicholas; ‘but BY that time I think I can undertake to be ready.’\n\n‘Very good,’ pursued Mr. Crummles, ‘then we’ll call that settled. Now, I want to ask you something else. There’s a little--what shall I call it?--a little canvassing takes place on these occasions.’\n\n‘Among the patrons, I suppose?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Among the patrons; and the fact is, that Snevellicci has had so many bespeaks in this place, that she wants an attraction. She had a bespeak when her mother-in-law died, and a bespeak when her uncle died; and Mrs. Crummles and myself have had bespeaks on the anniversary of the phenomenon’s birthday, and our wedding-day, and occasions of that description, so that, in fact, there’s some difficulty in getting a good one. Now, won’t you help this poor girl, Mr. Johnson?’ said Crummles, sitting himself down on a drum, and taking a great pinch of snuff, as he looked him steadily in the face.\n\n‘How do you mean?’ rejoined Nicholas.\n\n‘Don’t you think you could spare half an hour tomorrow morning, to call with her at the houses of one or two of the principal people?’ murmured the manager in a persuasive tone.\n\n‘Oh dear me,’ said Nicholas, with an air of very strong objection, ‘I shouldn’t like to do that.’\n\n‘The infant will accompany her,’ said Mr. Crummles. ‘The moment it was suggested to me, I gave permission for the infant to go. There will not be the smallest impropriety--Miss Snevellicci, sir, is the very soul of honour. It would be of material service--the gentleman from London--author of the new piece--actor in the new piece--first appearance on any boards--it would lead to a great bespeak, Mr. Johnson.’\n\n‘I am very sorry to throw a damp upon the prospects of anybody, and more especially a lady,’ replied Nicholas; ‘but really I must decidedly object to making one of the canvassing party.’\n\n‘What does Mr. Johnson say, Vincent?’ inquired a voice close to his ear; and, looking round, he found Mrs. Crummles and Miss Snevellicci herself standing behind him.\n\n‘He has some objection, my dear,’ replied Mr. Crummles, looking at Nicholas.\n\n‘Objection!’ exclaimed Mrs. Crummles. ‘Can it be possible?’\n\n‘Oh, I hope not!’ cried Miss Snevellicci. ‘You surely are not so cruel--oh, dear me!--Well, I--to think of that now, after all one’s looking forward to it!’\n\n‘Mr. Johnson will not persist, my dear,’ said Mrs. Crummles. ‘Think better of him than to suppose it. Gallantry, humanity, all the best feelings of his nature, must be enlisted in this interesting cause.’\n\n‘Which moves even a manager,’ said Mr. Crummles, smiling.\n\n‘And a manager’s wife,’ added Mrs. Crummles, in her accustomed tragedy tones. ‘Come, come, you will relent, I know you will.’\n\n‘It is not in my nature,’ said Nicholas, moved by these appeals, ‘to resist any entreaty, unless it is to do something positively wrong; and, beyond a feeling of pride, I know nothing which should prevent my doing this. I know nobody here, and nobody knows me. So be it then. I yield.’\n\nMiss Snevellicci was at once overwhelmed with blushes and expressions of gratitude, of which latter commodity neither Mr. nor Mrs. Crummles was by any means sparing. It was arranged that Nicholas should call upon her, at her lodgings, at eleven next morning, and soon after they parted: he to return home to his authorship: Miss Snevellicci to dress for the after-piece: and the disinterested manager and his wife to discuss the probable gains of the forthcoming bespeak, of which they were to have two-thirds of the profits by solemn treaty of agreement.\n\nAt the stipulated hour next morning, Nicholas repaired to the lodgings of Miss Snevellicci, which were in a place called Lombard Street, at the house of a tailor. A strong smell of ironing pervaded the little passage; and the tailor’s daughter, who opened the door, appeared in that flutter of spirits which is so often attendant upon the periodical getting up of a family’s linen.\n\n‘Miss Snevellicci lives here, I believe?’ said Nicholas, when the door was opened.\n\nThe tailor’s daughter replied in the affirmative.\n\n‘Will you have the goodness to let her know that Mr. Johnson is here?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Oh, if you please, you’re to come upstairs,’ replied the tailor’s daughter, with a smile.\n\nNicholas followed the young lady, and was shown into a small apartment on the first floor, communicating with a back-room; in which, as he judged from a certain half-subdued clinking sound, as of cups and saucers, Miss Snevellicci was then taking her breakfast in bed.\n\n‘You’re to wait, if you please,’ said the tailor’s daughter, after a short period of absence, during which the clinking in the back-room had ceased, and been succeeded by whispering--‘She won’t be long.’\n\nAs she spoke, she pulled up the window-blind, and having by this means (as she thought) diverted Mr. Johnson’s attention from the room to the street, caught up some articles which were airing on the fender, and had very much the appearance of stockings, and darted off.\n\nAs there were not many objects of interest outside the window, Nicholas looked about the room with more curiosity than he might otherwise have bestowed upon it. On the sofa lay an old guitar, several thumbed pieces of music, and a scattered litter of curl-papers; together with a confused heap of play-bills, and a pair of soiled white satin shoes with large blue rosettes. Hanging over the back of a chair was a half-finished muslin apron with little pockets ornamented with red ribbons, such as waiting-women wear on the stage, and (by consequence) are never seen with anywhere else. In one corner stood the diminutive pair of top-boots in which Miss Snevellicci was accustomed to enact the little jockey, and, folded on a chair hard by, was a small parcel, which bore a very suspicious resemblance to the companion smalls.\n\nBut the most interesting object of all was, perhaps, the open scrapbook, displayed in the midst of some theatrical duodecimos that were strewn upon the table; and pasted into which scrapbook were various critical notices of Miss Snevellicci’s acting, extracted from different provincial journals, together with one poetic address in her honour, commencing--\n\nSing, God of Love, and tell me in what dearth Thrice-gifted SNEVELLICCI came on earth, To thrill us with her smile, her tear, her eye, Sing, God of Love, and tell me quickly why.\n\nBesides this effusion, there were innumerable complimentary allusions, also extracted from newspapers, such as--‘We observe from an advertisement in another part of our paper of today, that the charming and highly-talented Miss Snevellicci takes her benefit on Wednesday, for which occasion she has put forth a bill of fare that might kindle exhilaration in the breast of a misanthrope. In the confidence that our fellow-townsmen have not lost that high appreciation of public utility and private worth, for which they have long been so pre-eminently distinguished, we predict that this charming actress will be greeted with a bumper.’ ‘To Correspondents.--J.S. is misinformed when he supposes that the highly-gifted and beautiful Miss Snevellicci, nightly captivating all hearts at our pretty and commodious little theatre, is NOT the same lady to whom the young gentleman of immense fortune, residing within a hundred miles of the good city of York, lately made honourable proposals. We have reason to know that Miss Snevellicci IS the lady who was implicated in that mysterious and romantic affair, and whose conduct on that occasion did no less honour to her head and heart, than do her histrionic triumphs to her brilliant genius.’ A copious assortment of such paragraphs as these, with long bills of benefits all ending with ‘Come Early’, in large capitals, formed the principal contents of Miss Snevellicci’s scrapbook.\n\nNicholas had read a great many of these scraps, and was absorbed in a circumstantial and melancholy account of the train of events which had led to Miss Snevellicci’s spraining her ankle by slipping on a piece of orange-peel flung by a monster in human form, (so the paper said,) upon the stage at Winchester,--when that young lady herself, attired in the coal-scuttle bonnet and walking-dress complete, tripped into the room, with a thousand apologies for having detained him so long after the appointed time.\n\n‘But really,’ said Miss Snevellicci, ‘my darling Led, who lives with me here, was taken so very ill in the night that I thought she would have expired in my arms.’\n\n‘Such a fate is almost to be envied,’ returned Nicholas, ‘but I am very sorry to hear it nevertheless.’\n\n‘What a creature you are to flatter!’ said Miss Snevellicci, buttoning her glove in much confusion.\n\n‘If it be flattery to admire your charms and accomplishments,’ rejoined Nicholas, laying his hand upon the scrapbook, ‘you have better specimens of it here.’\n\n‘Oh you cruel creature, to read such things as those! I’m almost ashamed to look you in the face afterwards, positively I am,’ said Miss Snevellicci, seizing the book and putting it away in a closet. ‘How careless of Led! How could she be so naughty!’\n\n‘I thought you had kindly left it here, on purpose for me to read,’ said Nicholas. And really it did seem possible.\n\n‘I wouldn’t have had you see it for the world!’ rejoined Miss Snevellicci. ‘I never was so vexed--never! But she is such a careless thing, there’s no trusting her.’\n\nThe conversation was here interrupted by the entrance of the phenomenon, who had discreetly remained in the bedroom up to this moment, and now presented herself, with much grace and lightness, bearing in her hand a very little green parasol with a broad fringe border, and no handle. After a few words of course, they sallied into the street.\n\nThe phenomenon was rather a troublesome companion, for first the right sandal came down, and then the left, and these mischances being repaired, one leg of the little white trousers was discovered to be longer than the other; besides these accidents, the green parasol was dropped down an iron grating, and only fished up again with great difficulty and by dint of much exertion. However, it was impossible to scold her, as she was the manager’s daughter, so Nicholas took it all in perfect good humour, and walked on, with Miss Snevellicci, arm-in-arm on one side, and the offending infant on the other.\n\nThe first house to which they bent their steps, was situated in a terrace of respectable appearance. Miss Snevellicci’s modest double-knock was answered by a foot-boy, who, in reply to her inquiry whether Mrs. Curdle was at home, opened his eyes very wide, grinned very much, and said he didn’t know, but he’d inquire. With this he showed them into a parlour where he kept them waiting, until the two women-servants had repaired thither, under false pretences, to see the play-actors; and having compared notes with them in the passage, and joined in a vast quantity of whispering and giggling, he at length went upstairs with Miss Snevellicci’s name.\n\nNow, Mrs. Curdle was supposed, by those who were best informed on such points, to possess quite the London taste in matters relating to literature and the drama; and as to Mr. Curdle, he had written a pamphlet of sixty-four pages, post octavo, on the character of the Nurse’s deceased husband in Romeo and Juliet, with an inquiry whether he really had been a ‘merry man’ in his lifetime, or whether it was merely his widow’s affectionate partiality that induced her so to report him. He had likewise proved, that by altering the received mode of punctuation, any one of Shakespeare’s plays could be made quite different, and the sense completely changed; it is needless to say, therefore, that he was a great critic, and a very profound and most original thinker.\n\n‘Well, Miss Snevellicci,’ said Mrs. Curdle, entering the parlour, ‘and how do YOU do?’\n\nMiss Snevellicci made a graceful obeisance, and hoped Mrs. Curdle was well, as also Mr. Curdle, who at the same time appeared. Mrs. Curdle was dressed in a morning wrapper, with a little cap stuck upon the top of her head. Mr. Curdle wore a loose robe on his back, and his right forefinger on his forehead after the portraits of Sterne, to whom somebody or other had once said he bore a striking resemblance.\n\n‘I venture to call, for the purpose of asking whether you would put your name to my bespeak, ma’am,’ said Miss Snevellicci, producing documents.\n\n‘Oh! I really don’t know what to say,’ replied Mrs. Curdle. ‘It’s not as if the theatre was in its high and palmy days--you needn’t stand, Miss Snevellicci--the drama is gone, perfectly gone.’\n\n‘As an exquisite embodiment of the poet’s visions, and a realisation of human intellectuality, gilding with refulgent light our dreamy moments, and laying open a new and magic world before the mental eye, the drama is gone, perfectly gone,’ said Mr. Curdle.\n\n‘What man is there, now living, who can present before us all those changing and prismatic colours with which the character of Hamlet is invested?’ exclaimed Mrs. Curdle.\n\n‘What man indeed--upon the stage,’ said Mr. Curdle, with a small reservation in favour of himself. ‘Hamlet! Pooh! ridiculous! Hamlet is gone, perfectly gone.’\n\nQuite overcome by these dismal reflections, Mr. and Mrs. Curdle sighed, and sat for some short time without speaking. At length, the lady, turning to Miss Snevellicci, inquired what play she proposed to have.\n\n‘Quite a new one,’ said Miss Snevellicci, ‘of which this gentleman is the author, and in which he plays; being his first appearance on any stage. Mr. Johnson is the gentleman’s name.’\n\n‘I hope you have preserved the unities, sir?’ said Mr. Curdle.\n\n‘The original piece is a French one,’ said Nicholas. ‘There is abundance of incident, sprightly dialogue, strongly-marked characters--’\n\n‘--All unavailing without a strict observance of the unities, sir,’ returned Mr. Curdle. ‘The unities of the drama, before everything.’\n\n‘Might I ask you,’ said Nicholas, hesitating between the respect he ought to assume, and his love of the whimsical, ‘might I ask you what the unities are?’\n\nMr. Curdle coughed and considered. ‘The unities, sir,’ he said, ‘are a completeness--a kind of universal dovetailedness with regard to place and time--a sort of a general oneness, if I may be allowed to use so strong an expression. I take those to be the dramatic unities, so far as I have been enabled to bestow attention upon them, and I have read much upon the subject, and thought much. I find, running through the performances of this child,’ said Mr. Curdle, turning to the phenomenon, ‘a unity of feeling, a breadth, a light and shade, a warmth of colouring, a tone, a harmony, a glow, an artistical development of original conceptions, which I look for, in vain, among older performers--I don’t know whether I make myself understood?’\n\n‘Perfectly,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘Just so,’ said Mr. Curdle, pulling up his neckcloth. ‘That is my definition of the unities of the drama.’\n\nMrs. Curdle had sat listening to this lucid explanation with great complacency. It being finished, she inquired what Mr. Curdle thought, about putting down their names.\n\n‘I don’t know, my dear; upon my word I don’t know,’ said Mr. Curdle. ‘If we do, it must be distinctly understood that we do not pledge ourselves to the quality of the performances. Let it go forth to the world, that we do not give THEM the sanction of our names, but that we confer the distinction merely upon Miss Snevellicci. That being clearly stated, I take it to be, as it were, a duty, that we should extend our patronage to a degraded stage, even for the sake of the associations with which it is entwined. Have you got two-and-sixpence for half-a-crown, Miss Snevellicci?’ said Mr. Curdle, turning over four of those pieces of money.\n\nMiss Snevellicci felt in all the corners of the pink reticule, but there was nothing in any of them. Nicholas murmured a jest about his being an author, and thought it best not to go through the form of feeling in his own pockets at all.\n\n‘Let me see,’ said Mr. Curdle; ‘twice four’s eight--four shillings a-piece to the boxes, Miss Snevellicci, is exceedingly dear in the present state of the drama--three half-crowns is seven-and-six; we shall not differ about sixpence, I suppose? Sixpence will not part us, Miss Snevellicci?’\n\nPoor Miss Snevellicci took the three half-crowns, with many smiles and bends, and Mrs. Curdle, adding several supplementary directions relative to keeping the places for them, and dusting the seat, and sending two clean bills as soon as they came out, rang the bell, as a signal for breaking up the conference.\n\n‘Odd people those,’ said Nicholas, when they got clear of the house.\n\n‘I assure you,’ said Miss Snevellicci, taking his arm, ‘that I think myself very lucky they did not owe all the money instead of being sixpence short. Now, if you were to succeed, they would give people to understand that they had always patronised you; and if you were to fail, they would have been quite certain of that from the very beginning.’\n\nAt the next house they visited, they were in great glory; for, there, resided the six children who were so enraptured with the public actions of the phenomenon, and who, being called down from the nursery to be treated with a private view of that young lady, proceeded to poke their fingers into her eyes, and tread upon her toes, and show her many other little attentions peculiar to their time of life.\n\n‘I shall certainly persuade Mr. Borum to take a private box,’ said the lady of the house, after a most gracious reception. ‘I shall only take two of the children, and will make up the rest of the party, of gentlemen--your admirers, Miss Snevellicci. Augustus, you naughty boy, leave the little girl alone.’\n\nThis was addressed to a young gentleman who was pinching the phenomenon behind, apparently with a view of ascertaining whether she was real.\n\n‘I am sure you must be very tired,’ said the mama, turning to Miss Snevellicci. ‘I cannot think of allowing you to go, without first taking a glass of wine. Fie, Charlotte, I am ashamed of you! Miss Lane, my dear, pray see to the children.’\n\nMiss Lane was the governess, and this entreaty was rendered necessary by the abrupt behaviour of the youngest Miss Borum, who, having filched the phenomenon’s little green parasol, was now carrying it bodily off, while the distracted infant looked helplessly on.\n\n‘I am sure, where you ever learnt to act as you do,’ said good-natured Mrs. Borum, turning again to Miss Snevellicci, ‘I cannot understand (Emma, don’t stare so); laughing in one piece, and crying in the next, and so natural in all--oh, dear!’\n\n‘I am very happy to hear you express so favourable an opinion,’ said Miss Snevellicci. ‘It’s quite delightful to think you like it.’\n\n‘Like it!’ cried Mrs. Borum. ‘Who can help liking it? I would go to the play, twice a week if I could: I dote upon it--only you’re too affecting sometimes. You do put me in such a state--into such fits of crying! Goodness gracious me, Miss Lane, how can you let them torment that poor child so!’\n\nThe phenomenon was really in a fair way of being torn limb from limb; for two strong little boys, one holding on by each of her hands, were dragging her in different directions as a trial of strength. However, Miss Lane (who had herself been too much occupied in contemplating the grown-up actors, to pay the necessary attention to these proceedings) rescued the unhappy infant at this juncture, who, being recruited with a glass of wine, was shortly afterwards taken away by her friends, after sustaining no more serious damage than a flattening of the pink gauze bonnet, and a rather extensive creasing of the white frock and trousers.\n\nIt was a trying morning; for there were a great many calls to make, and everybody wanted a different thing. Some wanted tragedies, and others comedies; some objected to dancing; some wanted scarcely anything else. Some thought the comic singer decidedly low, and others hoped he would have more to do than he usually had. Some people wouldn’t promise to go, because other people wouldn’t promise to go; and other people wouldn’t go at all, because other people went. At length, and by little and little, omitting something in this place, and adding something in that, Miss Snevellicci pledged herself to a bill of fare which was comprehensive enough, if it had no other merit (it included among other trifles, four pieces, divers songs, a few combats, and several dances); and they returned home, pretty well exhausted with the business of the day.\n\nNicholas worked away at the piece, which was speedily put into rehearsal, and then worked away at his own part, which he studied with great perseverance and acted--as the whole company said--to perfection. And at length the great day arrived. The crier was sent round, in the morning, to proclaim the entertainments with the sound of bell in all the thoroughfares; and extra bills of three feet long by nine inches wide, were dispersed in all directions, flung down all the areas, thrust under all the knockers, and developed in all the shops. They were placarded on all the walls too, though not with complete success, for an illiterate person having undertaken this office during the indisposition of the regular bill-sticker, a part were posted sideways, and the remainder upside down.\n\nAt half-past five, there was a rush of four people to the gallery-door; at a quarter before six, there were at least a dozen; at six o’clock the kicks were terrific; and when the elder Master Crummles opened the door, he was obliged to run behind it for his life. Fifteen shillings were taken by Mrs. Grudden in the first ten minutes.\n\nBehind the scenes, the same unwonted excitement prevailed. Miss Snevellicci was in such a perspiration that the paint would scarcely stay on her face. Mrs. Crummles was so nervous that she could hardly remember her part. Miss Bravassa’s ringlets came out of curl with the heat and anxiety; even Mr. Crummles himself kept peeping through the hole in the curtain, and running back, every now and then, to announce that another man had come into the pit.\n\nAt last, the orchestra left off, and the curtain rose upon the new piece. The first scene, in which there was nobody particular, passed off calmly enough, but when Miss Snevellicci went on in the second, accompanied by the phenomenon as child, what a roar of applause broke out! The people in the Borum box rose as one man, waving their hats and handkerchiefs, and uttering shouts of ‘Bravo!’ Mrs. Borum and the governess cast wreaths upon the stage, of which, some fluttered into the lamps, and one crowned the temples of a fat gentleman in the pit, who, looking eagerly towards the scene, remained unconscious of the honour; the tailor and his family kicked at the panels of the upper boxes till they threatened to come out altogether; the very ginger-beer boy remained transfixed in the centre of the house; a young officer, supposed to entertain a passion for Miss Snevellicci, stuck his glass in his eye as though to hide a tear. Again and again Miss Snevellicci curtseyed lower and lower, and again and again the applause came down, louder and louder. At length, when the phenomenon picked up one of the smoking wreaths and put it on, sideways, over Miss Snevellicci’s eye, it reached its climax, and the play proceeded.\n\nBut when Nicholas came on for his crack scene with Mrs. Crummles, what a clapping of hands there was! When Mrs. Crummles (who was his unworthy mother), sneered, and called him ‘presumptuous boy,’ and he defied her, what a tumult of applause came on! When he quarrelled with the other gentleman about the young lady, and producing a case of pistols, said, that if he WAS a gentleman, he would fight him in that drawing-room, until the furniture was sprinkled with the blood of one, if not of two--how boxes, pit, and gallery, joined in one most vigorous cheer! When he called his mother names, because she wouldn’t give up the young lady’s property, and she relenting, caused him to relent likewise, and fall down on one knee and ask her blessing, how the ladies in the audience sobbed! When he was hid behind the curtain in the dark, and the wicked relation poked a sharp sword in every direction, save where his legs were plainly visible, what a thrill of anxious fear ran through the house! His air, his figure, his walk, his look, everything he said or did, was the subject of commendation. There was a round of applause every time he spoke. And when, at last, in the pump-and-tub scene, Mrs Grudden lighted the blue fire, and all the unemployed members of the company came in, and tumbled down in various directions--not because that had anything to do with the plot, but in order to finish off with a tableau--the audience (who had by this time increased considerably) gave vent to such a shout of enthusiasm as had not been heard in those walls for many and many a day.\n\nIn short, the success both of new piece and new actor was complete, and when Miss Snevellicci was called for at the end of the play, Nicholas led her on, and divided the applause.\n\n# CHAPTER 25\n\nConcerning a young Lady from London, who joins the Company, and an elderly Admirer who follows in her Train; with an affecting Ceremony consequent on their Arrival\n\nThe new piece being a decided hit, was announced for every evening of performance until further notice, and the evenings when the theatre was closed, were reduced from three in the week to two. Nor were these the only tokens of extraordinary success; for, on the succeeding Saturday, Nicholas received, by favour of the indefatigable Mrs. Grudden, no less a sum than thirty shillings; besides which substantial reward, he enjoyed considerable fame and honour: having a presentation copy of Mr. Curdle’s pamphlet forwarded to the theatre, with that gentleman’s own autograph (in itself an inestimable treasure) on the fly-leaf, accompanied with a note, containing many expressions of approval, and an unsolicited assurance that Mr. Curdle would be very happy to read Shakespeare to him for three hours every morning before breakfast during his stay in the town.\n\n‘I’ve got another novelty, Johnson,’ said Mr. Crummles one morning in great glee.\n\n‘What’s that?’ rejoined Nicholas. ‘The pony?’\n\n‘No, no, we never come to the pony till everything else has failed,’ said Mr. Crummles. ‘I don’t think we shall come to the pony at all, this season. No, no, not the pony.’\n\n‘A boy phenomenon, perhaps?’ suggested Nicholas.\n\n‘There is only one phenomenon, sir,’ replied Mr. Crummles impressively, ‘and that’s a girl.’\n\n‘Very true,’ said Nicholas. ‘I beg your pardon. Then I don’t know what it is, I am sure.’\n\n‘What should you say to a young lady from London?’ inquired Mr. Crummles. ‘Miss So-and-so, of the Theatre Royal, Drury Lane?’\n\n‘I should say she would look very well in the bills,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘You’re about right there,’ said Mr. Crummles; ‘and if you had said she would look very well upon the stage too, you wouldn’t have been far out. Look here; what do you think of this?’\n\nWith this inquiry Mr. Crummles unfolded a red poster, and a blue poster, and a yellow poster, at the top of each of which public notification was inscribed in enormous characters--‘First appearance of the unrivalled Miss Petowker of the Theatre Royal, Drury Lane!’\n\n‘Dear me!’ said Nicholas, ‘I know that lady.’\n\n‘Then you are acquainted with as much talent as was ever compressed into one young person’s body,’ retorted Mr. Crummles, rolling up the bills again; ‘that is, talent of a certain sort--of a certain sort. “The Blood Drinker,”’ added Mr. Crummles with a prophetic sigh, ‘“The Blood Drinker” will die with that girl; and she’s the only sylph I ever saw, who could stand upon one leg, and play the tambourine on her other knee, LIKE a sylph.’\n\n‘When does she come down?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘We expect her today,’ replied Mr. Crummles. ‘She is an old friend of Mrs Crummles’s. Mrs. Crummles saw what she could do--always knew it from the first. She taught her, indeed, nearly all she knows. Mrs. Crummles was the original Blood Drinker.’\n\n‘Was she, indeed?’\n\n‘Yes. She was obliged to give it up though.’\n\n‘Did it disagree with her?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘Not so much with her, as with her audiences,’ replied Mr. Crummles. ‘Nobody could stand it. It was too tremendous. You don’t quite know what Mrs. Crummles is yet.’\n\nNicholas ventured to insinuate that he thought he did.\n\n‘No, no, you don’t,’ said Mr. Crummles; ‘you don’t, indeed. I don’t, and that’s a fact. I don’t think her country will, till she is dead. Some new proof of talent bursts from that astonishing woman every year of her life. Look at her--mother of six children--three of ‘em alive, and all upon the stage!’\n\n‘Extraordinary!’ cried Nicholas.\n\n‘Ah! extraordinary indeed,’ rejoined Mr. Crummles, taking a complacent pinch of snuff, and shaking his head gravely. ‘I pledge you my professional word I didn’t even know she could dance, till her last benefit, and then she played Juliet, and Helen Macgregor, and did the skipping-rope hornpipe between the pieces. The very first time I saw that admirable woman, Johnson,’ said Mr. Crummles, drawing a little nearer, and speaking in the tone of confidential friendship, ‘she stood upon her head on the butt-end of a spear, surrounded with blazing fireworks.’\n\n‘You astonish me!’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘SHE astonished ME!’ returned Mr. Crummles, with a very serious countenance. ‘Such grace, coupled with such dignity! I adored her from that moment!’\n\nThe arrival of the gifted subject of these remarks put an abrupt termination to Mr. Crummles’s eulogium. Almost immediately afterwards, Master Percy Crummles entered with a letter, which had arrived by the General Post, and was directed to his gracious mother; at sight of the superscription whereof, Mrs. Crummles exclaimed, ‘From Henrietta Petowker, I do declare!’ and instantly became absorbed in the contents.\n\n‘Is it--?’ inquired Mr. Crummles, hesitating.\n\n‘Oh, yes, it’s all right,’ replied Mrs. Crummles, anticipating the question. ‘What an excellent thing for her, to be sure!’\n\n‘It’s the best thing altogether, that I ever heard of, I think,’ said Mr Crummles; and then Mr. Crummles, Mrs. Crummles, and Master Percy Crummles, all fell to laughing violently. Nicholas left them to enjoy their mirth together, and walked to his lodgings; wondering very much what mystery connected with Miss Petowker could provoke such merriment, and pondering still more on the extreme surprise with which that lady would regard his sudden enlistment in a profession of which she was such a distinguished and brilliant ornament.\n\nBut, in this latter respect he was mistaken; for--whether Mr. Vincent Crummles had paved the way, or Miss Petowker had some special reason for treating him with even more than her usual amiability--their meeting at the theatre next day was more like that of two dear friends who had been inseparable from infancy, than a recognition passing between a lady and gentleman who had only met some half-dozen times, and then by mere chance. Nay, Miss Petowker even whispered that she had wholly dropped the Kenwigses in her conversations with the manager’s family, and had represented herself as having encountered Mr. Johnson in the very first and most fashionable circles; and on Nicholas receiving this intelligence with unfeigned surprise, she added, with a sweet glance, that she had a claim on his good nature now, and might tax it before long.\n\nNicholas had the honour of playing in a slight piece with Miss Petowker that night, and could not but observe that the warmth of her reception was mainly attributable to a most persevering umbrella in the upper boxes; he saw, too, that the enchanting actress cast many sweet looks towards the quarter whence these sounds proceeded; and that every time she did so, the umbrella broke out afresh. Once, he thought that a peculiarly shaped hat in the same corner was not wholly unknown to him; but, being occupied with his share of the stage business, he bestowed no great attention upon this circumstance, and it had quite vanished from his memory by the time he reached home.\n\nHe had just sat down to supper with Smike, when one of the people of the house came outside the door, and announced that a gentleman below stairs wished to speak to Mr. Johnson.\n\n‘Well, if he does, you must tell him to come up; that’s all I know,’ replied Nicholas. ‘One of our hungry brethren, I suppose, Smike.’\n\nHis fellow-lodger looked at the cold meat in silent calculation of the quantity that would be left for dinner next day, and put back a slice he had cut for himself, in order that the visitor’s encroachments might be less formidable in their effects.\n\n‘It is not anybody who has been here before,’ said Nicholas, ‘for he is tumbling up every stair. Come in, come in. In the name of wonder! Mr Lillyvick?’\n\nIt was, indeed, the collector of water-rates who, regarding Nicholas with a fixed look and immovable countenance, shook hands with most portentous solemnity, and sat himself down in a seat by the chimney-corner.\n\n‘Why, when did you come here?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘This morning, sir,’ replied Mr. Lillyvick.\n\n‘Oh! I see; then you were at the theatre tonight, and it was your umb--’\n\n‘This umbrella,’ said Mr. Lillyvick, producing a fat green cotton one with a battered ferrule. ‘What did you think of that performance?’\n\n‘So far as I could judge, being on the stage,’ replied Nicholas, ‘I thought it very agreeable.’\n\n‘Agreeable!’ cried the collector. ‘I mean to say, sir, that it was delicious.’\n\nMr. Lillyvick bent forward to pronounce the last word with greater emphasis; and having done so, drew himself up, and frowned and nodded a great many times.\n\n‘I say, delicious,’ repeated Mr. Lillyvick. ‘Absorbing, fairy-like, toomultuous,’ and again Mr. Lillyvick drew himself up, and again he frowned and nodded.\n\n‘Ah!’ said Nicholas, a little surprised at these symptoms of ecstatic approbation. ‘Yes--she is a clever girl.’\n\n‘She is a divinity,’ returned Mr. Lillyvick, giving a collector’s double knock on the ground with the umbrella before-mentioned. ‘I have known divine actresses before now, sir, I used to collect--at least I used to CALL for--and very often call for--the water-rate at the house of a divine actress, who lived in my beat for upwards of four year but never--no, never, sir of all divine creatures, actresses or no actresses, did I see a diviner one than is Henrietta Petowker.’\n\nNicholas had much ado to prevent himself from laughing; not trusting himself to speak, he merely nodded in accordance with Mr. Lillyvick’s nods, and remained silent.\n\n‘Let me speak a word with you in private,’ said Mr. Lillyvick.\n\nNicholas looked good-humouredly at Smike, who, taking the hint, disappeared.\n\n‘A bachelor is a miserable wretch, sir,’ said Mr. Lillyvick.\n\n‘Is he?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘He is,’ rejoined the collector. ‘I have lived in the world for nigh sixty year, and I ought to know what it is.’\n\n‘You OUGHT to know, certainly,’ thought Nicholas; ‘but whether you do or not, is another question.’\n\n‘If a bachelor happens to have saved a little matter of money,’ said Mr Lillyvick, ‘his sisters and brothers, and nephews and nieces, look TO that money, and not to him; even if, by being a public character, he is the head of the family, or, as it may be, the main from which all the other little branches are turned on, they still wish him dead all the while, and get low-spirited every time they see him looking in good health, because they want to come into his little property. You see that?’\n\n‘Oh yes,’ replied Nicholas: ‘it’s very true, no doubt.’\n\n‘The great reason for not being married,’ resumed Mr. Lillyvick, ‘is the expense; that’s what’s kept me off, or else--Lord!’ said Mr. Lillyvick, snapping his fingers, ‘I might have had fifty women.’\n\n‘Fine women?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘Fine women, sir!’ replied the collector; ‘ay! not so fine as Henrietta Petowker, for she is an uncommon specimen, but such women as don’t fall into every man’s way, I can tell you. Now suppose a man can get a fortune IN a wife instead of with her--eh?’\n\n‘Why, then, he’s a lucky fellow,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘That’s what I say,’ retorted the collector, patting him benignantly on the side of the head with his umbrella; ‘just what I say. Henrietta Petowker, the talented Henrietta Petowker has a fortune in herself, and I am going to--’\n\n‘To make her Mrs. Lillyvick?’ suggested Nicholas.\n\n‘No, sir, not to make her Mrs. Lillyvick,’ replied the collector. ‘Actresses, sir, always keep their maiden names--that’s the regular thing--but I’m going to marry her; and the day after tomorrow, too.’\n\n‘I congratulate you, sir,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Thank you, sir,’ replied the collector, buttoning his waistcoat. ‘I shall draw her salary, of course, and I hope after all that it’s nearly as cheap to keep two as it is to keep one; that’s a consolation.’\n\n‘Surely you don’t want any consolation at such a moment?’ observed Nicholas.\n\n‘No,’ replied Mr. Lillyvick, shaking his head nervously: ‘no--of course not.’\n\n‘But how come you both here, if you’re going to be married, Mr Lillyvick?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘Why, that’s what I came to explain to you,’ replied the collector of water-rate. ‘The fact is, we have thought it best to keep it secret from the family.’\n\n‘Family!’ said Nicholas. ‘What family?’\n\n‘The Kenwigses of course,’ rejoined Mr. Lillyvick. ‘If my niece and the children had known a word about it before I came away, they’d have gone into fits at my feet, and never have come out of ‘em till I took an oath not to marry anybody--or they’d have got out a commission of lunacy, or some dreadful thing,’ said the collector, quite trembling as he spoke.\n\n‘To be sure,’ said Nicholas. ‘Yes; they would have been jealous, no doubt.’\n\n‘To prevent which,’ said Mr. Lillyvick, ‘Henrietta Petowker (it was settled between us) should come down here to her friends, the Crummleses, under pretence of this engagement, and I should go down to Guildford the day before, and join her on the coach there, which I did, and we came down from Guildford yesterday together. Now, for fear you should be writing to Mr. Noggs, and might say anything about us, we have thought it best to let you into the secret. We shall be married from the Crummleses’ lodgings, and shall be delighted to see you--either before church or at breakfast-time, which you like. It won’t be expensive, you know,’ said the collector, highly anxious to prevent any misunderstanding on this point; ‘just muffins and coffee, with perhaps a shrimp or something of that sort for a relish, you know.’\n\n‘Yes, yes, I understand,’ replied Nicholas. ‘Oh, I shall be most happy to come; it will give me the greatest pleasure. Where’s the lady stopping--with Mrs. Crummles?’\n\n‘Why, no,’ said the collector; ‘they couldn’t very well dispose of her at night, and so she is staying with an acquaintance of hers, and another young lady; they both belong to the theatre.’\n\n‘Miss Snevellicci, I suppose?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Yes, that’s the name.’\n\n‘And they’ll be bridesmaids, I presume?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Why,’ said the collector, with a rueful face, ‘they WILL have four bridesmaids; I’m afraid they’ll make it rather theatrical.’\n\n‘Oh no, not at all,’ replied Nicholas, with an awkward attempt to convert a laugh into a cough. ‘Who may the four be? Miss Snevellicci of course--Miss Ledrook--’\n\n‘The--the phenomenon,’ groaned the collector.\n\n‘Ha, ha!’ cried Nicholas. ‘I beg your pardon, I don’t know what I’m laughing at--yes, that’ll be very pretty--the phenomenon--who else?’\n\n‘Some young woman or other,’ replied the collector, rising; ‘some other friend of Henrietta Petowker’s. Well, you’ll be careful not to say anything about it, will you?’\n\n‘You may safely depend upon me,’ replied Nicholas. ‘Won’t you take anything to eat or drink?’\n\n‘No,’ said the collector; ‘I haven’t any appetite. I should think it was a very pleasant life, the married one, eh?’\n\n‘I have not the least doubt of it,’ rejoined Nicholas.\n\n‘Yes,’ said the collector; ‘certainly. Oh yes. No doubt. Good night.’\n\nWith these words, Mr. Lillyvick, whose manner had exhibited through the whole of this interview a most extraordinary compound of precipitation, hesitation, confidence and doubt, fondness, misgiving, meanness, and self-importance, turned his back upon the room, and left Nicholas to enjoy a laugh by himself if he felt so disposed.\n\nWithout stopping to inquire whether the intervening day appeared to Nicholas to consist of the usual number of hours of the ordinary length, it may be remarked that, to the parties more directly interested in the forthcoming ceremony, it passed with great rapidity, insomuch that when Miss Petowker awoke on the succeeding morning in the chamber of Miss Snevellicci, she declared that nothing should ever persuade her that that really was the day which was to behold a change in her condition.\n\n‘I never will believe it,’ said Miss Petowker; ‘I cannot really. It’s of no use talking, I never can make up my mind to go through with such a trial!’\n\nOn hearing this, Miss Snevellicci and Miss Ledrook, who knew perfectly well that their fair friend’s mind had been made up for three or four years, at any period of which time she would have cheerfully undergone the desperate trial now approaching if she could have found any eligible gentleman disposed for the venture, began to preach comfort and firmness, and to say how very proud she ought to feel that it was in her power to confer lasting bliss on a deserving object, and how necessary it was for the happiness of mankind in general that women should possess fortitude and resignation on such occasions; and that although for their parts they held true happiness to consist in a single life, which they would not willingly exchange--no, not for any worldly consideration--still (thank God), if ever the time SHOULD come, they hoped they knew their duty too well to repine, but would the rather submit with meekness and humility of spirit to a fate for which Providence had clearly designed them with a view to the contentment and reward of their fellow-creatures.\n\n‘I might feel it was a great blow,’ said Miss Snevellicci, ‘to break up old associations and what-do-you-callems of that kind, but I would submit, my dear, I would indeed.’\n\n‘So would I,’ said Miss Ledrook; ‘I would rather court the yoke than shun it. I have broken hearts before now, and I’m very sorry for it: for it’s a terrible thing to reflect upon.’\n\n‘It is indeed,’ said Miss Snevellicci. ‘Now Led, my dear, we must positively get her ready, or we shall be too late, we shall indeed.’\n\nThis pious reasoning, and perhaps the fear of being too late, supported the bride through the ceremony of robing, after which, strong tea and brandy were administered in alternate doses as a means of strengthening her feeble limbs and causing her to walk steadier.\n\n‘How do you feel now, my love?’ inquired Miss Snevellicci.\n\n‘Oh Lillyvick!’ cried the bride. ‘If you knew what I am undergoing for you!’\n\n‘Of course he knows it, love, and will never forget it,’ said Miss Ledrook.\n\n‘Do you think he won’t?’ cried Miss Petowker, really showing great capability for the stage. ‘Oh, do you think he won’t? Do you think Lillyvick will always remember it--always, always, always?’\n\nThere is no knowing in what this burst of feeling might have ended, if Miss Snevellicci had not at that moment proclaimed the arrival of the fly, which so astounded the bride that she shook off divers alarming symptoms which were coming on very strong, and running to the glass adjusted her dress, and calmly declared that she was ready for the sacrifice.\n\nShe was accordingly supported into the coach, and there ‘kept up’ (as Miss Snevellicci said) with perpetual sniffs of SAL VOLATILE and sips of brandy and other gentle stimulants, until they reached the manager’s door, which was already opened by the two Master Crummleses, who wore white cockades, and were decorated with the choicest and most resplendent waistcoats in the theatrical wardrobe. By the combined exertions of these young gentlemen and the bridesmaids, assisted by the coachman, Miss Petowker was at length supported in a condition of much exhaustion to the first floor, where she no sooner encountered the youthful bridegroom than she fainted with great decorum.\n\n‘Henrietta Petowker!’ said the collector; ‘cheer up, my lovely one.’\n\nMiss Petowker grasped the collector’s hand, but emotion choked her utterance.\n\n‘Is the sight of me so dreadful, Henrietta Petowker?’ said the collector.\n\n‘Oh no, no, no,’ rejoined the bride; ‘but all the friends--the darling friends--of my youthful days--to leave them all--it is such a shock!’\n\nWith such expressions of sorrow, Miss Petowker went on to enumerate the dear friends of her youthful days one by one, and to call upon such of them as were present to come and embrace her. This done, she remembered that Mrs. Crummles had been more than a mother to her, and after that, that Mr. Crummles had been more than a father to her, and after that, that the Master Crummleses and Miss Ninetta Crummles had been more than brothers and sisters to her. These various remembrances being each accompanied with a series of hugs, occupied a long time, and they were obliged to drive to church very fast, for fear they should be too late.\n\nThe procession consisted of two flys; in the first of which were Miss Bravassa (the fourth bridesmaid), Mrs. Crummles, the collector, and Mr Folair, who had been chosen as his second on the occasion. In the other were the bride, Mr. Crummles, Miss Snevellicci, Miss Ledrook, and the phenomenon. The costumes were beautiful. The bridesmaids were quite covered with artificial flowers, and the phenomenon, in particular, was rendered almost invisible by the portable arbour in which she was enshrined. Miss Ledrook, who was of a romantic turn, wore in her breast the miniature of some field-officer unknown, which she had purchased, a great bargain, not very long before; the other ladies displayed several dazzling articles of imitative jewellery, almost equal to real, and Mrs Crummles came out in a stern and gloomy majesty, which attracted the admiration of all beholders.\n\nBut, perhaps the appearance of Mr. Crummles was more striking and appropriate than that of any member of the party. This gentleman, who personated the bride’s father, had, in pursuance of a happy and original conception, ‘made up’ for the part by arraying himself in a theatrical wig, of a style and pattern commonly known as a brown George, and moreover assuming a snuff-coloured suit, of the previous century, with grey silk stockings, and buckles to his shoes. The better to support his assumed character he had determined to be greatly overcome, and, consequently, when they entered the church, the sobs of the affectionate parent were so heart-rending that the pew-opener suggested the propriety of his retiring to the vestry, and comforting himself with a glass of water before the ceremony began.\n\nThe procession up the aisle was beautiful. The bride, with the four bridesmaids, forming a group previously arranged and rehearsed; the collector, followed by his second, imitating his walk and gestures to the indescribable amusement of some theatrical friends in the gallery; Mr. Crummles, with an infirm and feeble gait; Mrs. Crummles advancing with that stage walk, which consists of a stride and a stop alternately--it was the completest thing ever witnessed. The ceremony was very quickly disposed of, and all parties present having signed the register (for which purpose, when it came to his turn, Mr. Crummles carefully wiped and put on an immense pair of spectacles), they went back to breakfast in high spirits. And here they found Nicholas awaiting their arrival.\n\n‘Now then,’ said Crummles, who had been assisting Mrs. Grudden in the preparations, which were on a more extensive scale than was quite agreeable to the collector. ‘Breakfast, breakfast.’\n\nNo second invitation was required. The company crowded and squeezed themselves at the table as well as they could, and fell to, immediately: Miss Petowker blushing very much when anybody was looking, and eating very much when anybody was NOT looking; and Mr. Lillyvick going to work as though with the cool resolve, that since the good things must be paid for by him, he would leave as little as possible for the Crummleses to eat up afterwards.\n\n‘It’s very soon done, sir, isn’t it?’ inquired Mr. Folair of the collector, leaning over the table to address him.\n\n‘What is soon done, sir?’ returned Mr. Lillyvick.\n\n‘The tying up--the fixing oneself with a wife,’ replied Mr. Folair. ‘It don’t take long, does it?’\n\n‘No, sir,’ replied Mr. Lillyvick, colouring. ‘It does not take long. And what then, sir?’\n\n‘Oh! nothing,’ said the actor. ‘It don’t take a man long to hang himself, either, eh? ha, ha!’\n\nMr. Lillyvick laid down his knife and fork, and looked round the table with indignant astonishment.\n\n‘To hang himself!’ repeated Mr. Lillyvick.\n\nA profound silence came upon all, for Mr. Lillyvick was dignified beyond expression.\n\n‘To hang himself!’ cried Mr. Lillyvick again. ‘Is any parallel attempted to be drawn in this company between matrimony and hanging?’\n\n‘The noose, you know,’ said Mr. Folair, a little crest-fallen.\n\n‘The noose, sir?’ retorted Mr. Lillyvick. ‘Does any man dare to speak to me of a noose, and Henrietta Pe--’\n\n‘Lillyvick,’ suggested Mr. Crummles.\n\n‘--And Henrietta Lillyvick in the same breath?’ said the collector. ‘In this house, in the presence of Mr. and Mrs. Crummles, who have brought up a talented and virtuous family, to be blessings and phenomenons, and what not, are we to hear talk of nooses?’\n\n‘Folair,’ said Mr. Crummles, deeming it a matter of decency to be affected by this allusion to himself and partner, ‘I’m astonished at you.’\n\n‘What are you going on in this way at me for?’ urged the unfortunate actor. ‘What have I done?’\n\n‘Done, sir!’ cried Mr. Lillyvick, ‘aimed a blow at the whole framework of society--’\n\n‘And the best and tenderest feelings,’ added Crummles, relapsing into the old man.\n\n‘And the highest and most estimable of social ties,’ said the collector. ‘Noose! As if one was caught, trapped into the married state, pinned by the leg, instead of going into it of one’s own accord and glorying in the act!’\n\n‘I didn’t mean to make it out, that you were caught and trapped, and pinned by the leg,’ replied the actor. ‘I’m sorry for it; I can’t say any more.’\n\n‘So you ought to be, sir,’ returned Mr. Lillyvick; ‘and I am glad to hear that you have enough of feeling left to be so.’\n\nThe quarrel appearing to terminate with this reply, Mrs. Lillyvick considered that the fittest occasion (the attention of the company being no longer distracted) to burst into tears, and require the assistance of all four bridesmaids, which was immediately rendered, though not without some confusion, for the room being small and the table-cloth long, a whole detachment of plates were swept off the board at the very first move. Regardless of this circumstance, however, Mrs. Lillyvick refused to be comforted until the belligerents had passed their words that the dispute should be carried no further, which, after a sufficient show of reluctance, they did, and from that time Mr. Folair sat in moody silence, contenting himself with pinching Nicholas’s leg when anything was said, and so expressing his contempt both for the speaker and the sentiments to which he gave utterance.\n\nThere were a great number of speeches made; some by Nicholas, and some by Crummles, and some by the collector; two by the Master Crummleses in returning thanks for themselves, and one by the phenomenon on behalf of the bridesmaids, at which Mrs. Crummles shed tears. There was some singing, too, from Miss Ledrook and Miss Bravassa, and very likely there might have been more, if the fly-driver, who stopped to drive the happy pair to the spot where they proposed to take steamboat to Ryde, had not sent in a peremptory message intimating, that if they didn’t come directly he should infallibly demand eighteen-pence over and above his agreement.\n\nThis desperate threat effectually broke up the party. After a most pathetic leave-taking, Mr. Lillyvick and his bride departed for Ryde, where they were to spend the next two days in profound retirement, and whither they were accompanied by the infant, who had been appointed travelling bridesmaid on Mr. Lillyvick’s express stipulation: as the steamboat people, deceived by her size, would (he had previously ascertained) transport her at half-price.\n\nAs there was no performance that night, Mr. Crummles declared his intention of keeping it up till everything to drink was disposed of; but Nicholas having to play Romeo for the first time on the ensuing evening, contrived to slip away in the midst of a temporary confusion, occasioned by the unexpected development of strong symptoms of inebriety in the conduct of Mrs. Grudden.\n\nTo this act of desertion he was led, not only by his own inclinations, but by his anxiety on account of Smike, who, having to sustain the character of the Apothecary, had been as yet wholly unable to get any more of the part into his head than the general idea that he was very hungry, which--perhaps from old recollections--he had acquired with great aptitude.\n\n‘I don’t know what’s to be done, Smike,’ said Nicholas, laying down the book. ‘I am afraid you can’t learn it, my poor fellow.’\n\n‘I am afraid not,’ said Smike, shaking his head. ‘I think if you--but that would give you so much trouble.’\n\n‘What?’ inquired Nicholas. ‘Never mind me.’\n\n‘I think,’ said Smike, ‘if you were to keep saying it to me in little bits, over and over again, I should be able to recollect it from hearing you.’\n\n‘Do you think so?’ exclaimed Nicholas. ‘Well said. Let us see who tires first. Not I, Smike, trust me. Now then. Who calls so loud?’\n\n‘“Who calls so loud?”’ said Smike.\n\n‘“Who calls so loud?”’ repeated Nicholas.\n\n‘“Who calls so loud?”’ cried Smike.\n\nThus they continued to ask each other who called so loud, over and over again; and when Smike had that by heart Nicholas went to another sentence, and then to two at a time, and then to three, and so on, until at midnight poor Smike found to his unspeakable joy that he really began to remember something about the text.\n\nEarly in the morning they went to it again, and Smike, rendered more confident by the progress he had already made, got on faster and with better heart. As soon as he began to acquire the words pretty freely, Nicholas showed him how he must come in with both hands spread out upon his stomach, and how he must occasionally rub it, in compliance with the established form by which people on the stage always denote that they want something to eat. After the morning’s rehearsal they went to work again, nor did they stop, except for a hasty dinner, until it was time to repair to the theatre at night.\n\nNever had master a more anxious, humble, docile pupil. Never had pupil a more patient, unwearying, considerate, kindhearted master.\n\nAs soon as they were dressed, and at every interval when he was not upon the stage, Nicholas renewed his instructions. They prospered well. The Romeo was received with hearty plaudits and unbounded favour, and Smike was pronounced unanimously, alike by audience and actors, the very prince and prodigy of Apothecaries.\n\n# CHAPTER 26\n\nIs fraught with some Danger to Miss Nickleby’s Peace of Mind\n\nThe place was a handsome suite of private apartments in Regent Street; the time was three o’clock in the afternoon to the dull and plodding, and the first hour of morning to the gay and spirited; the persons were Lord Frederick Verisopht, and his friend Sir Mulberry Hawk.\n\nThese distinguished gentlemen were reclining listlessly on a couple of sofas, with a table between them, on which were scattered in rich confusion the materials of an untasted breakfast. Newspapers lay strewn about the room, but these, like the meal, were neglected and unnoticed; not, however, because any flow of conversation prevented the attractions of the journals from being called into request, for not a word was exchanged between the two, nor was any sound uttered, save when one, in tossing about to find an easier resting-place for his aching head, uttered an exclamation of impatience, and seemed for a moment to communicate a new restlessness to his companion.\n\nThese appearances would in themselves have furnished a pretty strong clue to the extent of the debauch of the previous night, even if there had not been other indications of the amusements in which it had been passed. A couple of billiard balls, all mud and dirt, two battered hats, a champagne bottle with a soiled glove twisted round the neck, to allow of its being grasped more surely in its capacity of an offensive weapon; a broken cane; a card-case without the top; an empty purse; a watch-guard snapped asunder; a handful of silver, mingled with fragments of half-smoked cigars, and their stale and crumbled ashes;--these, and many other tokens of riot and disorder, hinted very intelligibly at the nature of last night’s gentlemanly frolics.\n\nLord Frederick Verisopht was the first to speak. Dropping his slippered foot on the ground, and, yawning heavily, he struggled into a sitting posture, and turned his dull languid eyes towards his friend, to whom he called in a drowsy voice.\n\n‘Hallo!’ replied Sir Mulberry, turning round.\n\n‘Are we going to lie here all da-a-y?’ said the lord.\n\n‘I don’t know that we’re fit for anything else,’ replied Sir Mulberry; ‘yet awhile, at least. I haven’t a grain of life in me this morning.’\n\n‘Life!’ cried Lord Verisopht. ‘I feel as if there would be nothing so snug and comfortable as to die at once.’\n\n‘Then why don’t you die?’ said Sir Mulberry.\n\nWith which inquiry he turned his face away, and seemed to occupy himself in an attempt to fall asleep.\n\nHis hopeful friend and pupil drew a chair to the breakfast-table, and essayed to eat; but, finding that impossible, lounged to the window, then loitered up and down the room with his hand to his fevered head, and finally threw himself again on his sofa, and roused his friend once more.\n\n‘What the devil’s the matter?’ groaned Sir Mulberry, sitting upright on the couch.\n\nAlthough Sir Mulberry said this with sufficient ill-humour, he did not seem to feel himself quite at liberty to remain silent; for, after stretching himself very often, and declaring with a shiver that it was ‘infernal cold,’ he made an experiment at the breakfast-table, and proving more successful in it than his less-seasoned friend, remained there.\n\n‘Suppose,’ said Sir Mulberry, pausing with a morsel on the point of his fork, ‘suppose we go back to the subject of little Nickleby, eh?’\n\n‘Which little Nickleby; the money-lender or the ga-a-l?’ asked Lord Verisopht.\n\n‘You take me, I see,’ replied Sir Mulberry. ‘The girl, of course.’\n\n‘You promised me you’d find her out,’ said Lord Verisopht.\n\n‘So I did,’ rejoined his friend; ‘but I have thought further of the matter since then. You distrust me in the business--you shall find her out yourself.’\n\n‘Na-ay,’ remonstrated Lord Verisopht.\n\n‘But I say yes,’ returned his friend. ‘You shall find her out yourself. Don’t think that I mean, when you can--I know as well as you that if I did, you could never get sight of her without me. No. I say you shall find her out--SHALL--and I’ll put you in the way.’\n\n‘Now, curse me, if you ain’t a real, deyvlish, downright, thorough-paced friend,’ said the young lord, on whom this speech had produced a most reviving effect.\n\n‘I’ll tell you how,’ said Sir Mulberry. ‘She was at that dinner as a bait for you.’\n\n‘No!’ cried the young lord. ‘What the dey--’\n\n‘As a bait for you,’ repeated his friend; ‘old Nickleby told me so himself.’\n\n‘What a fine old cock it is!’ exclaimed Lord Verisopht; ‘a noble rascal!’\n\n‘Yes,’ said Sir Mulberry, ‘he knew she was a smart little creature--’\n\n‘Smart!’ interposed the young lord. ‘Upon my soul, Hawk, she’s a perfect beauty--a--a picture, a statue, a--a--upon my soul she is!’\n\n‘Well,’ replied Sir Mulberry, shrugging his shoulders and manifesting an indifference, whether he felt it or not; ‘that’s a matter of taste; if mine doesn’t agree with yours, so much the better.’\n\n‘Confound it!’ reasoned the lord, ‘you were thick enough with her that day, anyhow. I could hardly get in a word.’\n\n‘Well enough for once, well enough for once,’ replied Sir Mulberry; ‘but not worth the trouble of being agreeable to again. If you seriously want to follow up the niece, tell the uncle that you must know where she lives and how she lives, and with whom, or you are no longer a customer of his. He’ll tell you fast enough.’\n\n‘Why didn’t you say this before?’ asked Lord Verisopht, ‘instead of letting me go on burning, consuming, dragging out a miserable existence for an a-age!’\n\n‘I didn’t know it, in the first place,’ answered Sir Mulberry carelessly; ‘and in the second, I didn’t believe you were so very much in earnest.’\n\nNow, the truth was, that in the interval which had elapsed since the dinner at Ralph Nickleby’s, Sir Mulberry Hawk had been furtively trying by every means in his power to discover whence Kate had so suddenly appeared, and whither she had disappeared. Unassisted by Ralph, however, with whom he had held no communication since their angry parting on that occasion, all his efforts were wholly unavailing, and he had therefore arrived at the determination of communicating to the young lord the substance of the admission he had gleaned from that worthy. To this he was impelled by various considerations; among which the certainty of knowing whatever the weak young man knew was decidedly not the least, as the desire of encountering the usurer’s niece again, and using his utmost arts to reduce her pride, and revenge himself for her contempt, was uppermost in his thoughts. It was a politic course of proceeding, and one which could not fail to redound to his advantage in every point of view, since the very circumstance of his having extorted from Ralph Nickleby his real design in introducing his niece to such society, coupled with his extreme disinterestedness in communicating it so freely to his friend, could not but advance his interests in that quarter, and greatly facilitate the passage of coin (pretty frequent and speedy already) from the pockets of Lord Frederick Verisopht to those of Sir Mulberry Hawk.\n\nThus reasoned Sir Mulberry, and in pursuance of this reasoning he and his friend soon afterwards repaired to Ralph Nickleby’s, there to execute a plan of operations concerted by Sir Mulberry himself, avowedly to promote his friend’s object, and really to attain his own.\n\nThey found Ralph at home, and alone. As he led them into the drawing-room, the recollection of the scene which had taken place there seemed to occur to him, for he cast a curious look at Sir Mulberry, who bestowed upon it no other acknowledgment than a careless smile.\n\nThey had a short conference upon some money matters then in progress, which were scarcely disposed of when the lordly dupe (in pursuance of his friend’s instructions) requested with some embarrassment to speak to Ralph alone.\n\n‘Alone, eh?’ cried Sir Mulberry, affecting surprise. ‘Oh, very good. I’ll walk into the next room here. Don’t keep me long, that’s all.’\n\nSo saying, Sir Mulberry took up his hat, and humming a fragment of a song disappeared through the door of communication between the two drawing-rooms, and closed it after him.\n\n‘Now, my lord,’ said Ralph, ‘what is it?’\n\n‘Nickleby,’ said his client, throwing himself along the sofa on which he had been previously seated, so as to bring his lips nearer to the old man’s ear, ‘what a pretty creature your niece is!’\n\n‘Is she, my lord?’ replied Ralph. ‘Maybe--maybe--I don’t trouble my head with such matters.’\n\n‘You know she’s a deyvlish fine girl,’ said the client. ‘You must know that, Nickleby. Come, don’t deny that.’\n\n‘Yes, I believe she is considered so,’ replied Ralph. ‘Indeed, I know she is. If I did not, you are an authority on such points, and your taste, my lord--on all points, indeed--is undeniable.’\n\nNobody but the young man to whom these words were addressed could have been deaf to the sneering tone in which they were spoken, or blind to the look of contempt by which they were accompanied. But Lord Frederick Verisopht was both, and took them to be complimentary.\n\n‘Well,’ he said, ‘p’raps you’re a little right, and p’raps you’re a little wrong--a little of both, Nickleby. I want to know where this beauty lives, that I may have another peep at her, Nickleby.’\n\n‘Really--’ Ralph began in his usual tones.\n\n‘Don’t talk so loud,’ cried the other, achieving the great point of his lesson to a miracle. ‘I don’t want Hawk to hear.’\n\n‘You know he is your rival, do you?’ said Ralph, looking sharply at him.\n\n‘He always is, d-a-amn him,’ replied the client; ‘and I want to steal a march upon him. Ha, ha, ha! He’ll cut up so rough, Nickleby, at our talking together without him. Where does she live, Nickleby, that’s all? Only tell me where she lives, Nickleby.’\n\n‘He bites,’ thought Ralph. ‘He bites.’\n\n‘Eh, Nickleby, eh?’ pursued the client. ‘Where does she live?’\n\n‘Really, my lord,’ said Ralph, rubbing his hands slowly over each other, ‘I must think before I tell you.’\n\n‘No, not a bit of it, Nickleby; you mustn’t think at all,’ replied Verisopht. ‘Where is it?’\n\n‘No good can come of your knowing,’ replied Ralph. ‘She has been virtuously and well brought up; to be sure she is handsome, poor, unprotected! Poor girl, poor girl.’\n\nRalph ran over this brief summary of Kate’s condition as if it were merely passing through his own mind, and he had no intention to speak aloud; but the shrewd sly look which he directed at his companion as he delivered it, gave this poor assumption the lie.\n\n‘I tell you I only want to see her,’ cried his client. ‘A ma-an may look at a pretty woman without harm, mayn’t he? Now, where DOES she live? You know you’re making a fortune out of me, Nickleby, and upon my soul nobody shall ever take me to anybody else, if you only tell me this.’\n\n‘As you promise that, my lord,’ said Ralph, with feigned reluctance, ‘and as I am most anxious to oblige you, and as there’s no harm in it--no harm--I’ll tell you. But you had better keep it to yourself, my lord; strictly to yourself.’ Ralph pointed to the adjoining room as he spoke, and nodded expressively.\n\nThe young lord, feigning to be equally impressed with the necessity of this precaution, Ralph disclosed the present address and occupation of his niece, observing that from what he heard of the family they appeared very ambitious to have distinguished acquaintances, and that a lord could, doubtless, introduce himself with great ease, if he felt disposed.\n\n‘Your object being only to see her again,’ said Ralph, ‘you could effect it at any time you chose by that means.’\n\nLord Verisopht acknowledged the hint with a great many squeezes of Ralph’s hard, horny hand, and whispering that they would now do well to close the conversation, called to Sir Mulberry Hawk that he might come back.\n\n‘I thought you had gone to sleep,’ said Sir Mulberry, reappearing with an ill-tempered air.\n\n‘Sorry to detain you,’ replied the gull; ‘but Nickleby has been so ama-azingly funny that I couldn’t tear myself away.’\n\n‘No, no,’ said Ralph; ‘it was all his lordship. You know what a witty, humorous, elegant, accomplished man Lord Frederick is. Mind the step, my lord--Sir Mulberry, pray give way.’\n\nWith such courtesies as these, and many low bows, and the same cold sneer upon his face all the while, Ralph busied himself in showing his visitors downstairs, and otherwise than by the slightest possible motion about the corners of his mouth, returned no show of answer to the look of admiration with which Sir Mulberry Hawk seemed to compliment him on being such an accomplished and most consummate scoundrel.\n\nThere had been a ring at the bell a few minutes before, which was answered by Newman Noggs just as they reached the hall. In the ordinary course of business Newman would have either admitted the new-comer in silence, or have requested him or her to stand aside while the gentlemen passed out. But he no sooner saw who it was, than as if for some private reason of his own, he boldly departed from the established custom of Ralph’s mansion in business hours, and looking towards the respectable trio who were approaching, cried in a loud and sonorous voice, ‘Mrs Nickleby!’\n\n‘Mrs. Nickleby!’ cried Sir Mulberry Hawk, as his friend looked back, and stared him in the face.\n\nIt was, indeed, that well-intentioned lady, who, having received an offer for the empty house in the city directed to the landlord, had brought it post-haste to Mr. Nickleby without delay.\n\n‘Nobody YOU know,’ said Ralph. ‘Step into the office, my--my--dear. I’ll be with you directly.’\n\n‘Nobody I know!’ cried Sir Mulberry Hawk, advancing to the astonished lady. ‘Is this Mrs. Nickleby--the mother of Miss Nickleby--the delightful creature that I had the happiness of meeting in this house the very last time I dined here? But no;’ said Sir Mulberry, stopping short. ‘No, it can’t be. There is the same cast of features, the same indescribable air of--But no; no. This lady is too young for that.’\n\n‘I think you can tell the gentleman, brother-in-law, if it concerns him to know,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, acknowledging the compliment with a graceful bend, ‘that Kate Nickleby is my daughter.’\n\n‘Her daughter, my lord!’ cried Sir Mulberry, turning to his friend. ‘This lady’s daughter, my lord.’\n\n‘My lord!’ thought Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Well, I never did--’\n\n‘This, then, my lord,’ said Sir Mulberry, ‘is the lady to whose obliging marriage we owe so much happiness. This lady is the mother of sweet Miss Nickleby. Do you observe the extraordinary likeness, my lord? Nickleby--introduce us.’\n\nRalph did so, in a kind of desperation.\n\n‘Upon my soul, it’s a most delightful thing,’ said Lord Frederick, pressing forward. ‘How de do?’\n\nMrs. Nickleby was too much flurried by these uncommonly kind salutations, and her regrets at not having on her other bonnet, to make any immediate reply, so she merely continued to bend and smile, and betray great agitation.\n\n‘A--and how is Miss Nickleby?’ said Lord Frederick. ‘Well, I hope?’\n\n‘She is quite well, I’m obliged to you, my lord,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby, recovering. ‘Quite well. She wasn’t well for some days after that day she dined here, and I can’t help thinking, that she caught cold in that hackney coach coming home. Hackney coaches, my lord, are such nasty things, that it’s almost better to walk at any time, for although I believe a hackney coachman can be transported for life, if he has a broken window, still they are so reckless, that they nearly all have broken windows. I once had a swelled face for six weeks, my lord, from riding in a hackney coach--I think it was a hackney coach,’ said Mrs Nickleby reflecting, ‘though I’m not quite certain whether it wasn’t a chariot; at all events I know it was a dark green, with a very long number, beginning with a nought and ending with a nine--no, beginning with a nine, and ending with a nought, that was it, and of course the stamp-office people would know at once whether it was a coach or a chariot if any inquiries were made there--however that was, there it was with a broken window and there was I for six weeks with a swelled face--I think that was the very same hackney coach, that we found out afterwards, had the top open all the time, and we should never even have known it, if they hadn’t charged us a shilling an hour extra for having it open, which it seems is the law, or was then, and a most shameful law it appears to be--I don’t understand the subject, but I should say the Corn Laws could be nothing to THAT act of Parliament.’\n\nHaving pretty well run herself out by this time, Mrs. Nickleby stopped as suddenly as she had started off; and repeated that Kate was quite well. ‘Indeed,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘I don’t think she ever was better, since she had the hooping-cough, scarlet-fever, and measles, all at the same time, and that’s the fact.’\n\n‘Is that letter for me?’ growled Ralph, pointing to the little packet Mrs. Nickleby held in her hand.\n\n‘For you, brother-in-law,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby, ‘and I walked all the way up here on purpose to give it you.’\n\n‘All the way up here!’ cried Sir Mulberry, seizing upon the chance of discovering where Mrs. Nickleby had come from. ‘What a confounded distance! How far do you call it now?’\n\n‘How far do I call it?’ said Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Let me see. It’s just a mile from our door to the Old Bailey.’\n\n‘No, no. Not so much as that,’ urged Sir Mulberry.\n\n‘Oh! It is indeed,’ said Mrs. Nickleby. ‘I appeal to his lordship.’\n\n‘I should decidedly say it was a mile,’ remarked Lord Frederick, with a solemn aspect.\n\n‘It must be; it can’t be a yard less,’ said Mrs. Nickleby. ‘All down Newgate Street, all down Cheapside, all up Lombard Street, down Gracechurch Street, and along Thames Street, as far as Spigwiffin’s Wharf. Oh! It’s a mile.’\n\n‘Yes, on second thoughts I should say it was,’ replied Sir Mulberry. ‘But you don’t surely mean to walk all the way back?’\n\n‘Oh, no,’ rejoined Mrs. Nickleby. ‘I shall go back in an omnibus. I didn’t travel about in omnibuses, when my poor dear Nicholas was alive, brother-in-law. But as it is, you know--’\n\n‘Yes, yes,’ replied Ralph impatiently, ‘and you had better get back before dark.’\n\n‘Thank you, brother-in-law, so I had,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby. ‘I think I had better say goodbye, at once.’\n\n‘Not stop and--rest?’ said Ralph, who seldom offered refreshments unless something was to be got by it.\n\n‘Oh dear me no,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby, glancing at the dial.\n\n‘Lord Frederick,’ said Sir Mulberry, ‘we are going Mrs. Nickleby’s way. We’ll see her safe to the omnibus?’\n\n‘By all means. Ye-es.’\n\n‘Oh! I really couldn’t think of it!’ said Mrs. Nickleby.\n\nBut Sir Mulberry Hawk and Lord Verisopht were peremptory in their politeness, and leaving Ralph, who seemed to think, not unwisely, that he looked less ridiculous as a mere spectator, than he would have done if he had taken any part in these proceedings, they quitted the house with Mrs. Nickleby between them; that good lady in a perfect ecstasy of satisfaction, no less with the attentions shown her by two titled gentlemen, than with the conviction that Kate might now pick and choose, at least between two large fortunes, and most unexceptionable husbands.\n\nAs she was carried away for the moment by an irresistible train of thought, all connected with her daughter’s future greatness, Sir Mulberry Hawk and his friend exchanged glances over the top of the bonnet which the poor lady so much regretted not having left at home, and proceeded to dilate with great rapture, but much respect on the manifold perfections of Miss Nickleby.\n\n‘What a delight, what a comfort, what a happiness, this amiable creature must be to you,’ said Sir Mulberry, throwing into his voice an indication of the warmest feeling.\n\n‘She is indeed, sir,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby; ‘she is the sweetest-tempered, kindest-hearted creature--and so clever!’\n\n‘She looks clayver,’ said Lord Verisopht, with the air of a judge of cleverness.\n\n‘I assure you she is, my lord,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby. ‘When she was at school in Devonshire, she was universally allowed to be beyond all exception the very cleverest girl there, and there were a great many very clever ones too, and that’s the truth--twenty-five young ladies, fifty guineas a year without the et-ceteras, both the Miss Dowdles the most accomplished, elegant, fascinating creatures--Oh dear me!’ said Mrs Nickleby, ‘I never shall forget what pleasure she used to give me and her poor dear papa, when she was at that school, never--such a delightful letter every half-year, telling us that she was the first pupil in the whole establishment, and had made more progress than anybody else! I can scarcely bear to think of it even now. The girls wrote all the letters themselves,’ added Mrs. Nickleby, ‘and the writing-master touched them up afterwards with a magnifying glass and a silver pen; at least I think they wrote them, though Kate was never quite certain about that, because she didn’t know the handwriting of hers again; but anyway, I know it was a circular which they all copied, and of course it was a very gratifying thing--very gratifying.’\n\nWith similar recollections Mrs. Nickleby beguiled the tediousness of the way, until they reached the omnibus, which the extreme politeness of her new friends would not allow them to leave until it actually started, when they took their hats, as Mrs. Nickleby solemnly assured her hearers on many subsequent occasions, ‘completely off,’ and kissed their straw-coloured kid gloves till they were no longer visible.\n\nMrs. Nickleby leant back in the furthest corner of the conveyance, and, closing her eyes, resigned herself to a host of most pleasing meditations. Kate had never said a word about having met either of these gentlemen; ‘that,’ she thought, ‘argues that she is strongly prepossessed in favour of one of them.’ Then the question arose, which one could it be. The lord was the youngest, and his title was certainly the grandest; still Kate was not the girl to be swayed by such considerations as these. ‘I will never put any constraint upon her inclinations,’ said Mrs. Nickleby to herself; ‘but upon my word I think there’s no comparison between his lordship and Sir Mulberry--Sir Mulberry is such an attentive gentlemanly creature, so much manner, such a fine man, and has so much to say for himself. I hope it’s Sir Mulberry--I think it must be Sir Mulberry!’ And then her thoughts flew back to her old predictions, and the number of times she had said, that Kate with no fortune would marry better than other people’s daughters with thousands; and, as she pictured with the brightness of a mother’s fancy all the beauty and grace of the poor girl who had struggled so cheerfully with her new life of hardship and trial, her heart grew too full, and the tears trickled down her face.\n\nMeanwhile, Ralph walked to and fro in his little back-office, troubled in mind by what had just occurred. To say that Ralph loved or cared for--in the most ordinary acceptation of those terms--any one of God’s creatures, would be the wildest fiction. Still, there had somehow stolen upon him from time to time a thought of his niece which was tinged with compassion and pity; breaking through the dull cloud of dislike or indifference which darkened men and women in his eyes, there was, in her case, the faintest gleam of light--a most feeble and sickly ray at the best of times--but there it was, and it showed the poor girl in a better and purer aspect than any in which he had looked on human nature yet.\n\n‘I wish,’ thought Ralph, ‘I had never done this. And yet it will keep this boy to me, while there is money to be made. Selling a girl--throwing her in the way of temptation, and insult, and coarse speech. Nearly two thousand pounds profit from him already though. Pshaw! match-making mothers do the same thing every day.’\n\nHe sat down, and told the chances, for and against, on his fingers.\n\n‘If I had not put them in the right track today,’ thought Ralph, ‘this foolish woman would have done so. Well. If her daughter is as true to herself as she should be from what I have seen, what harm ensues? A little teasing, a little humbling, a few tears. Yes,’ said Ralph, aloud, as he locked his iron safe. ‘She must take her chance. She must take her chance.’\n\n# CHAPTER 27\n\nMrs. Nickleby becomes acquainted with Messrs Pyke and Pluck, whose Affection and Interest are beyond all Bounds\nMrs. Nickleby had not felt so proud and important for many a day, as when, on reaching home, she gave herself wholly up to the pleasant visions which had accompanied her on her way thither. Lady Mulberry Hawk--that was the prevalent idea. Lady Mulberry Hawk!--On Tuesday last, at St George’s, Hanover Square, by the Right Reverend the Bishop of Llandaff, Sir Mulberry Hawk, of Mulberry Castle, North Wales, to Catherine, only daughter of the late Nicholas Nickleby, Esquire, of Devonshire. ‘Upon my word!’ cried Mrs. Nicholas Nickleby, ‘it sounds very well.’\n\nHaving dispatched the ceremony, with its attendant festivities, to the perfect satisfaction of her own mind, the sanguine mother pictured to her imagination a long train of honours and distinctions which could not fail to accompany Kate in her new and brilliant sphere. She would be presented at court, of course. On the anniversary of her birthday, which was upon the nineteenth of July [‘at ten minutes past three o’clock in the morning,’ thought Mrs. Nickleby in a parenthesis, ‘for I recollect asking what o’clock it was’), Sir Mulberry would give a great feast to all his tenants, and would return them three and a half per cent on the amount of their last half-year’s rent, as would be fully described and recorded in the fashionable intelligence, to the immeasurable delight and admiration of all the readers thereof. Kate’s picture, too, would be in at least half-a-dozen of the annuals, and on the opposite page would appear, in delicate type, ‘Lines on contemplating the Portrait of Lady Mulberry Hawk. By Sir Dingleby Dabber.’ Perhaps some one annual, of more comprehensive design than its fellows, might even contain a portrait of the mother of Lady Mulberry Hawk, with lines by the father of Sir Dingleby Dabber. More unlikely things had come to pass. Less interesting portraits had appeared. As this thought occurred to the good lady, her countenance unconsciously assumed that compound expression of simpering and sleepiness which, being common to all such portraits, is perhaps one reason why they are always so charming and agreeable.\n\nWith such triumphs of aerial architecture did Mrs. Nickleby occupy the whole evening after her accidental introduction to Ralph’s titled friends; and dreams, no less prophetic and equally promising, haunted her sleep that night. She was preparing for her frugal dinner next day, still occupied with the same ideas--a little softened down perhaps by sleep and daylight--when the girl who attended her, partly for company, and partly to assist in the household affairs, rushed into the room in unwonted agitation, and announced that two gentlemen were waiting in the passage for permission to walk upstairs.\n\n‘Bless my heart!’ cried Mrs. Nickleby, hastily arranging her cap and front, ‘if it should be--dear me, standing in the passage all this time--why don’t you go and ask them to walk up, you stupid thing?’\n\nWhile the girl was gone on this errand, Mrs. Nickleby hastily swept into a cupboard all vestiges of eating and drinking; which she had scarcely done, and seated herself with looks as collected as she could assume, when two gentlemen, both perfect strangers, presented themselves.\n\n‘How do you DO?’ said one gentleman, laying great stress on the last word of the inquiry.\n\n‘HOW do you do?’ said the other gentleman, altering the emphasis, as if to give variety to the salutation.\n\nMrs. Nickleby curtseyed and smiled, and curtseyed again, and remarked, rubbing her hands as she did so, that she hadn’t the--really--the honour to--\n\n‘To know us,’ said the first gentleman. ‘The loss has been ours, Mrs Nickleby. Has the loss been ours, Pyke?’\n\n‘It has, Pluck,’ answered the other gentleman.\n\n‘We have regretted it very often, I believe, Pyke?’ said the first gentleman.\n\n‘Very often, Pluck,’ answered the second.\n\n‘But now,’ said the first gentleman, ‘now we have the happiness we have pined and languished for. Have we pined and languished for this happiness, Pyke, or have we not?’\n\n‘You know we have, Pluck,’ said Pyke, reproachfully.\n\n‘You hear him, ma’am?’ said Mr. Pluck, looking round; ‘you hear the unimpeachable testimony of my friend Pyke--that reminds me,--formalities, formalities, must not be neglected in civilised society. Pyke--Mrs. Nickleby.’\n\nMr. Pyke laid his hand upon his heart, and bowed low.\n\n‘Whether I shall introduce myself with the same formality,’ said Mr Pluck--‘whether I shall say myself that my name is Pluck, or whether I shall ask my friend Pyke (who being now regularly introduced, is competent to the office) to state for me, Mrs. Nickleby, that my name is Pluck; whether I shall claim your acquaintance on the plain ground of the strong interest I take in your welfare, or whether I shall make myself known to you as the friend of Sir Mulberry Hawk--these, Mrs Nickleby, are considerations which I leave to you to determine.’\n\n‘Any friend of Sir Mulberry Hawk’s requires no better introduction to me,’ observed Mrs. Nickleby, graciously.\n\n‘It is delightful to hear you say so,’ said Mr. Pluck, drawing a chair close to Mrs. Nickleby, and sitting himself down. ‘It is refreshing to know that you hold my excellent friend, Sir Mulberry, in such high esteem. A word in your ear, Mrs. Nickleby. When Sir Mulberry knows it, he will be a happy man--I say, Mrs. Nickleby, a happy man. Pyke, be seated.’\n\n‘MY good opinion,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, and the poor lady exulted in the idea that she was marvellously sly,--‘my good opinion can be of very little consequence to a gentleman like Sir Mulberry.’\n\n‘Of little consequence!’ exclaimed Mr. Pluck. ‘Pyke, of what consequence to our friend, Sir Mulberry, is the good opinion of Mrs. Nickleby?’\n\n‘Of what consequence?’ echoed Pyke.\n\n‘Ay,’ repeated Pluck; ‘is it of the greatest consequence?’\n\n‘Of the very greatest consequence,’ replied Pyke.\n\n‘Mrs. Nickleby cannot be ignorant,’ said Mr. Pluck, ‘of the immense impression which that sweet girl has--’\n\n‘Pluck!’ said his friend, ‘beware!’\n\n‘Pyke is right,’ muttered Mr. Pluck, after a short pause; ‘I was not to mention it. Pyke is very right. Thank you, Pyke.’\n\n‘Well now, really,’ thought Mrs. Nickleby within herself. ‘Such delicacy as that, I never saw!’\n\nMr. Pluck, after feigning to be in a condition of great embarrassment for some minutes, resumed the conversation by entreating Mrs. Nickleby to take no heed of what he had inadvertently said--to consider him imprudent, rash, injudicious. The only stipulation he would make in his own favour was, that she should give him credit for the best intentions.\n\n‘But when,’ said Mr. Pluck, ‘when I see so much sweetness and beauty on the one hand, and so much ardour and devotion on the other, I--pardon me, Pyke, I didn’t intend to resume that theme. Change the subject, Pyke.’\n\n‘We promised Sir Mulberry and Lord Frederick,’ said Pyke, ‘that we’d call this morning and inquire whether you took any cold last night.’\n\n‘Not the least in the world last night, sir,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby, ‘with many thanks to his lordship and Sir Mulberry for doing me the honour to inquire; not the least--which is the more singular, as I really am very subject to colds, indeed--very subject. I had a cold once,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘I think it was in the year eighteen hundred and seventeen; let me see, four and five are nine, and--yes, eighteen hundred and seventeen, that I thought I never should get rid of; actually and seriously, that I thought I never should get rid of. I was only cured at last by a remedy that I don’t know whether you ever happened to hear of, Mr. Pluck. You have a gallon of water as hot as you can possibly bear it, with a pound of salt, and sixpen’orth of the finest bran, and sit with your head in it for twenty minutes every night just before going to bed; at least, I don’t mean your head--your feet. It’s a most extraordinary cure--a most extraordinary cure. I used it for the first time, I recollect, the day after Christmas Day, and by the middle of April following the cold was gone. It seems quite a miracle when you come to think of it, for I had it ever since the beginning of September.’\n\n‘What an afflicting calamity!’ said Mr. Pyke.\n\n‘Perfectly horrid!’ exclaimed Mr. Pluck.\n\n‘But it’s worth the pain of hearing, only to know that Mrs. Nickleby recovered it, isn’t it, Pluck?’ cried Mr. Pyke.\n\n‘That is the circumstance which gives it such a thrilling interest,’ replied Mr. Pluck.\n\n‘But come,’ said Pyke, as if suddenly recollecting himself; ‘we must not forget our mission in the pleasure of this interview. We come on a mission, Mrs. Nickleby.’\n\n‘On a mission,’ exclaimed that good lady, to whose mind a definite proposal of marriage for Kate at once presented itself in lively colours.\n\n‘From Sir Mulberry,’ replied Pyke. ‘You must be very dull here.’\n\n‘Rather dull, I confess,’ said Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘We bring the compliments of Sir Mulberry Hawk, and a thousand entreaties that you’ll take a seat in a private box at the play tonight,’ said Mr. Pluck.\n\n‘Oh dear!’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘I never go out at all, never.’\n\n‘And that is the very reason, my dear Mrs. Nickleby, why you should go out tonight,’ retorted Mr. Pluck. ‘Pyke, entreat Mrs. Nickleby.’\n\n‘Oh, pray do,’ said Pyke.\n\n‘You positively must,’ urged Pluck.\n\n‘You are very kind,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, hesitating; ‘but--’\n\n‘There’s not a but in the case, my dear Mrs. Nickleby,’ remonstrated Mr Pluck; ‘not such a word in the vocabulary. Your brother-in-law joins us, Lord Frederick joins us, Sir Mulberry joins us, Pyke joins us--a refusal is out of the question. Sir Mulberry sends a carriage for you--twenty minutes before seven to the moment--you’ll not be so cruel as to disappoint the whole party, Mrs. Nickleby?’\n\n‘You are so very pressing, that I scarcely know what to say,’ replied the worthy lady.\n\n‘Say nothing; not a word, not a word, my dearest madam,’ urged Mr. Pluck. ‘Mrs. Nickleby,’ said that excellent gentleman, lowering his voice, ‘there is the most trifling, the most excusable breach of confidence in what I am about to say; and yet if my friend Pyke there overheard it--such is that man’s delicate sense of honour, Mrs. Nickleby--he’d have me out before dinner-time.’\n\nMrs. Nickleby cast an apprehensive glance at the warlike Pyke, who had walked to the window; and Mr. Pluck, squeezing her hand, went on:\n\n‘Your daughter has made a conquest--a conquest on which I may congratulate you. Sir Mulberry, my dear ma’am, Sir Mulberry is her devoted slave. Hem!’\n\n‘Hah!’ cried Mr. Pyke at this juncture, snatching something from the chimney-piece with a theatrical air. ‘What is this! what do I behold!’\n\n‘What DO you behold, my dear fellow?’ asked Mr. Pluck.\n\n‘It is the face, the countenance, the expression,’ cried Mr. Pyke, falling into his chair with a miniature in his hand; ‘feebly portrayed, imperfectly caught, but still THE face, THE countenance, THE expression.’\n\n‘I recognise it at this distance!’ exclaimed Mr. Pluck in a fit of enthusiasm. ‘Is it not, my dear madam, the faint similitude of--’\n\n‘It is my daughter’s portrait,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, with great pride. And so it was. And little Miss La Creevy had brought it home for inspection only two nights before.\n\nMr. Pyke no sooner ascertained that he was quite right in his conjecture, than he launched into the most extravagant encomiums of the divine original; and in the warmth of his enthusiasm kissed the picture a thousand times, while Mr. Pluck pressed Mrs. Nickleby’s hand to his heart, and congratulated her on the possession of such a daughter, with so much earnestness and affection, that the tears stood, or seemed to stand, in his eyes. Poor Mrs. Nickleby, who had listened in a state of enviable complacency at first, became at length quite overpowered by these tokens of regard for, and attachment to, the family; and even the servant girl, who had peeped in at the door, remained rooted to the spot in astonishment at the ecstasies of the two friendly visitors.\n\nBy degrees these raptures subsided, and Mrs. Nickleby went on to entertain her guests with a lament over her fallen fortunes, and a picturesque account of her old house in the country: comprising a full description of the different apartments, not forgetting the little store-room, and a lively recollection of how many steps you went down to get into the garden, and which way you turned when you came out at the parlour door, and what capital fixtures there were in the kitchen. This last reflection naturally conducted her into the wash-house, where she stumbled upon the brewing utensils, among which she might have wandered for an hour, if the mere mention of those implements had not, by an association of ideas, instantly reminded Mr. Pyke that he was ‘amazing thirsty.’\n\n‘And I’ll tell you what,’ said Mr. Pyke; ‘if you’ll send round to the public-house for a pot of milk half-and-half, positively and actually I’ll drink it.’\n\nAnd positively and actually Mr. Pyke DID drink it, and Mr. Pluck helped him, while Mrs. Nickleby looked on in divided admiration of the condescension of the two, and the aptitude with which they accommodated themselves to the pewter-pot; in explanation of which seeming marvel it may be here observed, that gentlemen who, like Messrs Pyke and Pluck, live upon their wits (or not so much, perhaps, upon the presence of their own wits as upon the absence of wits in other people) are occasionally reduced to very narrow shifts and straits, and are at such periods accustomed to regale themselves in a very simple and primitive manner.\n\n‘At twenty minutes before seven, then,’ said Mr. Pyke, rising, ‘the coach will be here. One more look--one little look--at that sweet face. Ah! here it is. Unmoved, unchanged!’ This, by the way, was a very remarkable circumstance, miniatures being liable to so many changes of expression--‘Oh, Pluck! Pluck!’\n\nMr. Pluck made no other reply than kissing Mrs. Nickleby’s hand with a great show of feeling and attachment; Mr. Pyke having done the same, both gentlemen hastily withdrew.\n\nMrs. Nickleby was commonly in the habit of giving herself credit for a pretty tolerable share of penetration and acuteness, but she had never felt so satisfied with her own sharp-sightedness as she did that day. She had found it all out the night before. She had never seen Sir Mulberry and Kate together--never even heard Sir Mulberry’s name--and yet hadn’t she said to herself from the very first, that she saw how the case stood? and what a triumph it was, for there was now no doubt about it. If these flattering attentions to herself were not sufficient proofs, Sir Mulberry’s confidential friend had suffered the secret to escape him in so many words. ‘I am quite in love with that dear Mr Pluck, I declare I am,’ said Mrs. Nickleby.\n\nThere was one great source of uneasiness in the midst of this good fortune, and that was the having nobody by, to whom she could confide it. Once or twice she almost resolved to walk straight to Miss La Creevy’s and tell it all to her. ‘But I don’t know,’ thought Mrs Nickleby; ‘she is a very worthy person, but I am afraid too much beneath Sir Mulberry’s station for us to make a companion of. Poor thing!’ Acting upon this grave consideration she rejected the idea of taking the little portrait painter into her confidence, and contented herself with holding out sundry vague and mysterious hopes of preferment to the servant girl, who received these obscure hints of dawning greatness with much veneration and respect.\n\nPunctual to its time came the promised vehicle, which was no hackney coach, but a private chariot, having behind it a footman, whose legs, although somewhat large for his body, might, as mere abstract legs, have set themselves up for models at the Royal Academy. It was quite exhilarating to hear the clash and bustle with which he banged the door and jumped up behind after Mrs. Nickleby was in; and as that good lady was perfectly unconscious that he applied the gold-headed end of his long stick to his nose, and so telegraphed most disrespectfully to the coachman over her very head, she sat in a state of much stiffness and dignity, not a little proud of her position.\n\nAt the theatre entrance there was more banging and more bustle, and there were also Messrs Pyke and Pluck waiting to escort her to her box; and so polite were they, that Mr. Pyke threatened with many oaths to ‘smifligate’ a very old man with a lantern who accidentally stumbled in her way--to the great terror of Mrs. Nickleby, who, conjecturing more from Mr. Pyke’s excitement than any previous acquaintance with the etymology of the word that smifligation and bloodshed must be in the main one and the same thing, was alarmed beyond expression, lest something should occur. Fortunately, however, Mr. Pyke confined himself to mere verbal smifligation, and they reached their box with no more serious interruption by the way, than a desire on the part of the same pugnacious gentleman to ‘smash’ the assistant box-keeper for happening to mistake the number.\n\nMrs. Nickleby had scarcely been put away behind the curtain of the box in an armchair, when Sir Mulberry and Lord Verisopht arrived, arrayed from the crowns of their heads to the tips of their gloves, and from the tips of their gloves to the toes of their boots, in the most elegant and costly manner. Sir Mulberry was a little hoarser than on the previous day, and Lord Verisopht looked rather sleepy and queer; from which tokens, as well as from the circumstance of their both being to a trifling extent unsteady upon their legs, Mrs. Nickleby justly concluded that they had taken dinner.\n\n‘We have been--we have been--toasting your lovely daughter, Mrs Nickleby,’ whispered Sir Mulberry, sitting down behind her.\n\n‘Oh, ho!’ thought that knowing lady; ‘wine in, truth out.--You are very kind, Sir Mulberry.’\n\n‘No, no upon my soul!’ replied Sir Mulberry Hawk. ‘It’s you that’s kind, upon my soul it is. It was so kind of you to come tonight.’\n\n‘So very kind of you to invite me, you mean, Sir Mulberry,’ replied Mrs Nickleby, tossing her head, and looking prodigiously sly.\n\n‘I am so anxious to know you, so anxious to cultivate your good opinion, so desirous that there should be a delicious kind of harmonious family understanding between us,’ said Sir Mulberry, ‘that you mustn’t think I’m disinterested in what I do. I’m infernal selfish; I am--upon my soul I am.’\n\n‘I am sure you can’t be selfish, Sir Mulberry!’ replied Mrs. Nickleby. ‘You have much too open and generous a countenance for that.’\n\n‘What an extraordinary observer you are!’ said Sir Mulberry Hawk.\n\n‘Oh no, indeed, I don’t see very far into things, Sir Mulberry,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby, in a tone of voice which left the baronet to infer that she saw very far indeed.\n\n‘I am quite afraid of you,’ said the baronet. ‘Upon my soul,’ repeated Sir Mulberry, looking round to his companions; ‘I am afraid of Mrs Nickleby. She is so immensely sharp.’\n\nMessrs Pyke and Pluck shook their heads mysteriously, and observed together that they had found that out long ago; upon which Mrs. Nickleby tittered, and Sir Mulberry laughed, and Pyke and Pluck roared.\n\n‘But where’s my brother-in-law, Sir Mulberry?’ inquired Mrs. Nickleby. ‘I shouldn’t be here without him. I hope he’s coming.’\n\n‘Pyke,’ said Sir Mulberry, taking out his toothpick and lolling back in his chair, as if he were too lazy to invent a reply to this question. ‘Where’s Ralph Nickleby?’\n\n‘Pluck,’ said Pyke, imitating the baronet’s action, and turning the lie over to his friend, ‘where’s Ralph Nickleby?’\n\nMr. Pluck was about to return some evasive reply, when the hustle caused by a party entering the next box seemed to attract the attention of all four gentlemen, who exchanged glances of much meaning. The new party beginning to converse together, Sir Mulberry suddenly assumed the character of a most attentive listener, and implored his friends not to breathe--not to breathe.\n\n‘Why not?’ said Mrs. Nickleby. ‘What is the matter?’\n\n‘Hush!’ replied Sir Mulberry, laying his hand on her arm. ‘Lord Frederick, do you recognise the tones of that voice?’\n\n‘Deyvle take me if I didn’t think it was the voice of Miss Nickleby.’\n\n‘Lor, my lord!’ cried Miss Nickleby’s mama, thrusting her head round the curtain. ‘Why actually--Kate, my dear, Kate.’\n\n‘YOU here, mama! Is it possible!’\n\n‘Possible, my dear? Yes.’\n\n‘Why who--who on earth is that you have with you, mama?’ said Kate, shrinking back as she caught sight of a man smiling and kissing his hand.\n\n‘Who do you suppose, my dear?’ replied Mrs. Nickleby, bending towards Mrs Wititterly, and speaking a little louder for that lady’s edification. ‘There’s Mr. Pyke, Mr. Pluck, Sir Mulberry Hawk, and Lord Frederick Verisopht.’\n\n‘Gracious Heaven!’ thought Kate hurriedly. ‘How comes she in such society?’\n\nNow, Kate thought thus SO hurriedly, and the surprise was so great, and moreover brought back so forcibly the recollection of what had passed at Ralph’s delectable dinner, that she turned extremely pale and appeared greatly agitated, which symptoms being observed by Mrs. Nickleby, were at once set down by that acute lady as being caused and occasioned by violent love. But, although she was in no small degree delighted by this discovery, which reflected so much credit on her own quickness of perception, it did not lessen her motherly anxiety in Kate’s behalf; and accordingly, with a vast quantity of trepidation, she quitted her own box to hasten into that of Mrs. Wititterly. Mrs. Wititterly, keenly alive to the glory of having a lord and a baronet among her visiting acquaintance, lost no time in signing to Mr. Wititterly to open the door, and thus it was that in less than thirty seconds Mrs. Nickleby’s party had made an irruption into Mrs. Wititterly’s box, which it filled to the very door, there being in fact only room for Messrs Pyke and Pluck to get in their heads and waistcoats.\n\n‘My dear Kate,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, kissing her daughter affectionately. ‘How ill you looked a moment ago! You quite frightened me, I declare!’\n\n‘It was mere fancy, mama,--the--the--reflection of the lights perhaps,’ replied Kate, glancing nervously round, and finding it impossible to whisper any caution or explanation.\n\n‘Don’t you see Sir Mulberry Hawk, my dear?’\n\nKate bowed slightly, and biting her lip turned her head towards the stage.\n\nBut Sir Mulberry Hawk was not to be so easily repulsed, for he advanced with extended hand; and Mrs. Nickleby officiously informing Kate of this circumstance, she was obliged to extend her own. Sir Mulberry detained it while he murmured a profusion of compliments, which Kate, remembering what had passed between them, rightly considered as so many aggravations of the insult he had already put upon her. Then followed the recognition of Lord Verisopht, and then the greeting of Mr. Pyke, and then that of Mr Pluck, and finally, to complete the young lady’s mortification, she was compelled at Mrs. Wititterly’s request to perform the ceremony of introducing the odious persons, whom she regarded with the utmost indignation and abhorrence.\n\n‘Mrs. Wititterly is delighted,’ said Mr. Wititterly, rubbing his hands; ‘delighted, my lord, I am sure, with this opportunity of contracting an acquaintance which, I trust, my lord, we shall improve. Julia, my dear, you must not allow yourself to be too much excited, you must not. Indeed you must not. Mrs. Wititterly is of a most excitable nature, Sir Mulberry. The snuff of a candle, the wick of a lamp, the bloom on a peach, the down on a butterfly. You might blow her away, my lord; you might blow her away.’\n\nSir Mulberry seemed to think that it would be a great convenience if the lady could be blown away. He said, however, that the delight was mutual, and Lord Verisopht added that it was mutual, whereupon Messrs Pyke and Pluck were heard to murmur from the distance that it was very mutual indeed.\n\n‘I take an interest, my lord,’ said Mrs. Wititterly, with a faint smile, ‘such an interest in the drama.’\n\n‘Ye--es. It’s very interesting,’ replied Lord Verisopht.\n\n‘I’m always ill after Shakespeare,’ said Mrs. Wititterly. ‘I scarcely exist the next day; I find the reaction so very great after a tragedy, my lord, and Shakespeare is such a delicious creature.’\n\n‘Ye--es!’ replied Lord Verisopht. ‘He was a clayver man.’\n\n‘Do you know, my lord,’ said Mrs. Wititterly, after a long silence, ‘I find I take so much more interest in his plays, after having been to that dear little dull house he was born in! Were you ever there, my lord?’\n\n‘No, nayver,’ replied Verisopht.\n\n‘Then really you ought to go, my lord,’ returned Mrs. Wititterly, in very languid and drawling accents. ‘I don’t know how it is, but after you’ve seen the place and written your name in the little book, somehow or other you seem to be inspired; it kindles up quite a fire within one.’\n\n‘Ye--es!’ replied Lord Verisopht, ‘I shall certainly go there.’\n\n‘Julia, my life,’ interposed Mr. Wititterly, ‘you are deceiving his lordship--unintentionally, my lord, she is deceiving you. It is your poetical temperament, my dear--your ethereal soul--your fervid imagination, which throws you into a glow of genius and excitement. There is nothing in the place, my dear--nothing, nothing.’\n\n‘I think there must be something in the place,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, who had been listening in silence; ‘for, soon after I was married, I went to Stratford with my poor dear Mr. Nickleby, in a post-chaise from Birmingham--was it a post-chaise though?’ said Mrs. Nickleby, considering; ‘yes, it must have been a post-chaise, because I recollect remarking at the time that the driver had a green shade over his left eye;--in a post-chaise from Birmingham, and after we had seen Shakespeare’s tomb and birthplace, we went back to the inn there, where we slept that night, and I recollect that all night long I dreamt of nothing but a black gentleman, at full length, in plaster-of-Paris, with a lay-down collar tied with two tassels, leaning against a post and thinking; and when I woke in the morning and described him to Mr Nickleby, he said it was Shakespeare just as he had been when he was alive, which was very curious indeed. Stratford--Stratford,’ continued Mrs. Nickleby, considering. ‘Yes, I am positive about that, because I recollect I was in the family way with my son Nicholas at the time, and I had been very much frightened by an Italian image boy that very morning. In fact, it was quite a mercy, ma’am,’ added Mrs. Nickleby, in a whisper to Mrs. Wititterly, ‘that my son didn’t turn out to be a Shakespeare, and what a dreadful thing that would have been!’\n\nWhen Mrs. Nickleby had brought this interesting anecdote to a close, Pyke and Pluck, ever zealous in their patron’s cause, proposed the adjournment of a detachment of the party into the next box; and with so much skill were the preliminaries adjusted, that Kate, despite all she could say or do to the contrary, had no alternative but to suffer herself to be led away by Sir Mulberry Hawk. Her mother and Mr. Pluck accompanied them, but the worthy lady, pluming herself upon her discretion, took particular care not so much as to look at her daughter during the whole evening, and to seem wholly absorbed in the jokes and conversation of Mr. Pluck, who, having been appointed sentry over Mrs Nickleby for that especial purpose, neglected, on his side, no possible opportunity of engrossing her attention.\n\nLord Frederick Verisopht remained in the next box to be talked to by Mrs Wititterly, and Mr. Pyke was in attendance to throw in a word or two when necessary. As to Mr. Wititterly, he was sufficiently busy in the body of the house, informing such of his friends and acquaintance as happened to be there, that those two gentlemen upstairs, whom they had seen in conversation with Mrs. W., were the distinguished Lord Frederick Verisopht and his most intimate friend, the gay Sir Mulberry Hawk--a communication which inflamed several respectable house-keepers with the utmost jealousy and rage, and reduced sixteen unmarried daughters to the very brink of despair.\n\nThe evening came to an end at last, but Kate had yet to be handed downstairs by the detested Sir Mulberry; and so skilfully were the manoeuvres of Messrs Pyke and Pluck conducted, that she and the baronet were the last of the party, and were even--without an appearance of effort or design--left at some little distance behind.\n\n‘Don’t hurry, don’t hurry,’ said Sir Mulberry, as Kate hastened on, and attempted to release her arm.\n\nShe made no reply, but still pressed forward.\n\n‘Nay, then--’ coolly observed Sir Mulberry, stopping her outright.\n\n‘You had best not seek to detain me, sir!’ said Kate, angrily.\n\n‘And why not?’ retorted Sir Mulberry. ‘My dear creature, now why do you keep up this show of displeasure?’\n\n‘SHOW!’ repeated Kate, indignantly. ‘How dare you presume to speak to me, sir--to address me--to come into my presence?’\n\n‘You look prettier in a passion, Miss Nickleby,’ said Sir Mulberry Hawk, stooping down, the better to see her face.\n\n‘I hold you in the bitterest detestation and contempt, sir,’ said Kate. ‘If you find any attraction in looks of disgust and aversion, you--let me rejoin my friends, sir, instantly. Whatever considerations may have withheld me thus far, I will disregard them all, and take a course that even YOU might feel, if you do not immediately suffer me to proceed.’\n\nSir Mulberry smiled, and still looking in her face and retaining her arm, walked towards the door.\n\n‘If no regard for my sex or helpless situation will induce you to desist from this coarse and unmanly persecution,’ said Kate, scarcely knowing, in the tumult of her passions, what she said,--‘I have a brother who will resent it dearly, one day.’\n\n‘Upon my soul!’ exclaimed Sir Mulberry, as though quietly communing with himself; passing his arm round her waist as he spoke, ‘she looks more beautiful, and I like her better in this mood, than when her eyes are cast down, and she is in perfect repose!’\n\nHow Kate reached the lobby where her friends were waiting she never knew, but she hurried across it without at all regarding them, and disengaged herself suddenly from her companion, sprang into the coach, and throwing herself into its darkest corner burst into tears.\n\nMessrs Pyke and Pluck, knowing their cue, at once threw the party into great commotion by shouting for the carriages, and getting up a violent quarrel with sundry inoffensive bystanders; in the midst of which tumult they put the affrighted Mrs. Nickleby in her chariot, and having got her safely off, turned their thoughts to Mrs. Wititterly, whose attention also they had now effectually distracted from the young lady, by throwing her into a state of the utmost bewilderment and consternation. At length, the conveyance in which she had come rolled off too with its load, and the four worthies, being left alone under the portico, enjoyed a hearty laugh together.\n\n‘There,’ said Sir Mulberry, turning to his noble friend. ‘Didn’t I tell you last night that if we could find where they were going by bribing a servant through my fellow, and then established ourselves close by with the mother, these people’s honour would be our own? Why here it is, done in four-and-twenty hours.’\n\n‘Ye--es,’ replied the dupe. ‘But I have been tied to the old woman all ni-ight.’\n\n‘Hear him,’ said Sir Mulberry, turning to his two friends. ‘Hear this discontented grumbler. Isn’t it enough to make a man swear never to help him in his plots and schemes again? Isn’t it an infernal shame?’\n\nPyke asked Pluck whether it was not an infernal shame, and Pluck asked Pyke; but neither answered.\n\n‘Isn’t it the truth?’ demanded Verisopht. ‘Wasn’t it so?’\n\n‘Wasn’t it so!’ repeated Sir Mulberry. ‘How would you have had it? How could we have got a general invitation at first sight--come when you like, go when you like, stop as long as you like, do what you like--if you, the lord, had not made yourself agreeable to the foolish mistress of the house? Do I care for this girl, except as your friend? Haven’t I been sounding your praises in her ears, and bearing her pretty sulks and peevishness all night for you? What sort of stuff do you think I’m made of? Would I do this for every man? Don’t I deserve even gratitude in return?’\n\n‘You’re a deyvlish good fellow,’ said the poor young lord, taking his friend’s arm. ‘Upon my life you’re a deyvlish good fellow, Hawk.’\n\n‘And I have done right, have I?’ demanded Sir Mulberry.\n\n‘Quite ri-ght.’\n\n‘And like a poor, silly, good-natured, friendly dog as I am, eh?’\n\n‘Ye--es, ye--es; like a friend,’ replied the other.\n\n‘Well then,’ replied Sir Mulberry, ‘I’m satisfied. And now let’s go and have our revenge on the German baron and the Frenchman, who cleaned you out so handsomely last night.’\n\nWith these words the friendly creature took his companion’s arm and led him away, turning half round as he did so, and bestowing a wink and a contemptuous smile on Messrs Pyke and Pluck, who, cramming their handkerchiefs into their mouths to denote their silent enjoyment of the whole proceedings, followed their patron and his victim at a little distance.\n\n# CHAPTER 28\n\nMiss Nickleby, rendered desperate by the Persecution of Sir Mulberry Hawk, and the Complicated Difficulties and Distresses which surround her, appeals, as a last resource, to her Uncle for Protection\n\nThe ensuing morning brought reflection with it, as morning usually does; but widely different was the train of thought it awakened in the different persons who had been so unexpectedly brought together on the preceding evening, by the active agency of Messrs Pyke and Pluck.\nThe reflections of Sir Mulberry Hawk--if such a term can be applied to the thoughts of the systematic and calculating man of dissipation, whose joys, regrets, pains, and pleasures, are all of self, and who would seem to retain nothing of the intellectual faculty but the power to debase himself, and to degrade the very nature whose outward semblance he wears--the reflections of Sir Mulberry Hawk turned upon Kate Nickleby, and were, in brief, that she was undoubtedly handsome; that her coyness MUST be easily conquerable by a man of his address and experience, and that the pursuit was one which could not fail to redound to his credit, and greatly to enhance his reputation with the world. And lest this last consideration--no mean or secondary one with Sir Mulberry--should sound strangely in the ears of some, let it be remembered that most men live in a world of their own, and that in that limited circle alone are they ambitious for distinction and applause. Sir Mulberry’s world was peopled with profligates, and he acted accordingly.\n\nThus, cases of injustice, and oppression, and tyranny, and the most extravagant bigotry, are in constant occurrence among us every day. It is the custom to trumpet forth much wonder and astonishment at the chief actors therein setting at defiance so completely the opinion of the world; but there is no greater fallacy; it is precisely because they do consult the opinion of their own little world that such things take place at all, and strike the great world dumb with amazement.\n\nThe reflections of Mrs. Nickleby were of the proudest and most complacent kind; and under the influence of her very agreeable delusion she straightway sat down and indited a long letter to Kate, in which she expressed her entire approval of the admirable choice she had made, and extolled Sir Mulberry to the skies; asserting, for the more complete satisfaction of her daughter’s feelings, that he was precisely the individual whom she (Mrs. Nickleby) would have chosen for her son-in-law, if she had had the picking and choosing from all mankind. The good lady then, with the preliminary observation that she might be fairly supposed not to have lived in the world so long without knowing its ways, communicated a great many subtle precepts applicable to the state of courtship, and confirmed in their wisdom by her own personal experience. Above all things she commended a strict maidenly reserve, as being not only a very laudable thing in itself, but as tending materially to strengthen and increase a lover’s ardour. ‘And I never,’ added Mrs Nickleby, ‘was more delighted in my life than to observe last night, my dear, that your good sense had already told you this.’ With which sentiment, and various hints of the pleasure she derived from the knowledge that her daughter inherited so large an instalment of her own excellent sense and discretion (to nearly the full measure of which she might hope, with care, to succeed in time), Mrs. Nickleby concluded a very long and rather illegible letter.\n\nPoor Kate was well-nigh distracted on the receipt of four closely-written and closely-crossed sides of congratulation on the very subject which had prevented her closing her eyes all night, and kept her weeping and watching in her chamber; still worse and more trying was the necessity of rendering herself agreeable to Mrs. Wititterly, who, being in low spirits after the fatigue of the preceding night, of course expected her companion (else wherefore had she board and salary?) to be in the best spirits possible. As to Mr. Wititterly, he went about all day in a tremor of delight at having shaken hands with a lord, and having actually asked him to come and see him in his own house. The lord himself, not being troubled to any inconvenient extent with the power of thinking, regaled himself with the conversation of Messrs Pyke and Pluck, who sharpened their wit by a plentiful indulgence in various costly stimulants at his expense.\n\nIt was four in the afternoon--that is, the vulgar afternoon of the sun and the clock--and Mrs. Wititterly reclined, according to custom, on the drawing-room sofa, while Kate read aloud a new novel in three volumes, entitled ‘The Lady Flabella,’ which Alphonse the doubtful had procured from the library that very morning. And it was a production admirably suited to a lady labouring under Mrs. Wititterly’s complaint, seeing that there was not a line in it, from beginning to end, which could, by the most remote contingency, awaken the smallest excitement in any person breathing.\nKate read on.\n\n‘“Cherizette,” said the Lady Flabella, inserting her mouse-like feet in the blue satin slippers, which had unwittingly occasioned the half-playful half-angry altercation between herself and the youthful Colonel Befillaire, in the Duke of Mincefenille’s SALON DE DANSE on the previous night. “CHERIZETTE, MA CHERE, DONNEZ-MOI DE L’EAU-DE-COLOGNE, S’IL VOUS PLAIT, MON ENFANT.”\n\n‘“MERCIE--thank you,” said the Lady Flabella, as the lively but devoted Cherizette plentifully besprinkled with the fragrant compound the Lady Flabella’s MOUCHOIR of finest cambric, edged with richest lace, and emblazoned at the four corners with the Flabella crest, and gorgeous heraldic bearings of that noble family. “MERCIE--that will do.”\n\n‘At this instant, while the Lady Flabella yet inhaled that delicious fragrance by holding the MOUCHOIR to her exquisite, but thoughtfully-chiselled nose, the door of the BOUDOIR (artfully concealed by rich hangings of silken damask, the hue of Italy’s firmament) was thrown open, and with noiseless tread two VALETS-DE-CHAMBRE, clad in sumptuous liveries of peach-blossom and gold, advanced into the room followed by a page in BAS DE SOIE--silk stockings--who, while they remained at some distance making the most graceful obeisances, advanced to the feet of his lovely mistress, and dropping on one knee presented, on a golden salver gorgeously chased, a scented BILLET.\n\n‘The Lady Flabella, with an agitation she could not repress, hastily tore off the ENVELOPE and broke the scented seal. It WAS from Befillaire--the young, the slim, the low-voiced--HER OWN Befillaire.’\n\n‘Oh, charming!’ interrupted Kate’s patroness, who was sometimes taken literary. ‘Poetic, really. Read that description again, Miss Nickleby.’\n\nKate complied.\n\n‘Sweet, indeed!’ said Mrs. Wititterly, with a sigh. ‘So voluptuous, is it not--so soft?’\n\n‘Yes, I think it is,’ replied Kate, gently; ‘very soft.’\n\n‘Close the book, Miss Nickleby,’ said Mrs. Wititterly. ‘I can hear nothing more today; I should be sorry to disturb the impression of that sweet description. Close the book.’\n\nKate complied, not unwillingly; and, as she did so, Mrs. Wititterly raising her glass with a languid hand, remarked, that she looked pale.\n\n‘It was the fright of that--that noise and confusion last night,’ said Kate.\n\n‘How very odd!’ exclaimed Mrs. Wititterly, with a look of surprise. And certainly, when one comes to think of it, it WAS very odd that anything should have disturbed a companion. A steam-engine, or other ingenious piece of mechanism out of order, would have been nothing to it.\n\n‘How did you come to know Lord Frederick, and those other delightful creatures, child?’ asked Mrs. Wititterly, still eyeing Kate through her glass.\n\n‘I met them at my uncle’s,’ said Kate, vexed to feel that she was colouring deeply, but unable to keep down the blood which rushed to her face whenever she thought of that man.\n\n‘Have you known them long?’\n\n‘No,’ rejoined Kate. ‘Not long.’\n\n‘I was very glad of the opportunity which that respectable person, your mother, gave us of being known to them,’ said Mrs. Wititterly, in a lofty manner. ‘Some friends of ours were on the very point of introducing us, which makes it quite remarkable.’\n\nThis was said lest Miss Nickleby should grow conceited on the honour and dignity of having known four great people (for Pyke and Pluck were included among the delightful creatures), whom Mrs. Wititterly did not know. But as the circumstance had made no impression one way or other upon Kate’s mind, the force of the observation was quite lost upon her.\n\n‘They asked permission to call,’ said Mrs. Wititterly. ‘I gave it them of course.’\n\n‘Do you expect them today?’ Kate ventured to inquire.\n\nMrs. Wititterly’s answer was lost in the noise of a tremendous rapping at the street-door, and before it had ceased to vibrate, there drove up a handsome cabriolet, out of which leaped Sir Mulberry Hawk and his friend Lord Verisopht.\n\n‘They are here now,’ said Kate, rising and hurrying away.\n\n‘Miss Nickleby!’ cried Mrs. Wititterly, perfectly aghast at a companion’s attempting to quit the room, without her permission first had and obtained. ‘Pray don’t think of going.’\n\n‘You are very good!’ replied Kate. ‘But--’\n\n‘For goodness’ sake, don’t agitate me by making me speak so much,’ said Mrs. Wititterly, with great sharpness. ‘Dear me, Miss Nickleby, I beg--’\n\nIt was in vain for Kate to protest that she was unwell, for the footsteps of the knockers, whoever they were, were already on the stairs. She resumed her seat, and had scarcely done so, when the doubtful page darted into the room and announced, Mr. Pyke, and Mr. Pluck, and Lord Verisopht, and Sir Mulberry Hawk, all at one burst.\n\n‘The most extraordinary thing in the world,’ said Mr. Pluck, saluting both ladies with the utmost cordiality; ‘the most extraordinary thing. As Lord Frederick and Sir Mulberry drove up to the door, Pyke and I had that instant knocked.’\n\n‘That instant knocked,’ said Pyke.\n\n‘No matter how you came, so that you are here,’ said Mrs. Wititterly, who, by dint of lying on the same sofa for three years and a half, had got up quite a little pantomime of graceful attitudes, and now threw herself into the most striking of the whole series, to astonish the visitors. ‘I am delighted, I am sure.’\n\n‘And how is Miss Nickleby?’ said Sir Mulberry Hawk, accosting Kate, in a low voice--not so low, however, but that it reached the ears of Mrs Wititterly.\n\n‘Why, she complains of suffering from the fright of last night,’ said the lady. ‘I am sure I don’t wonder at it, for my nerves are quite torn to pieces.’\n\n‘And yet you look,’ observed Sir Mulberry, turning round; ‘and yet you look--’\n\n‘Beyond everything,’ said Mr. Pyke, coming to his patron’s assistance. Of course Mr. Pluck said the same.\n\n‘I am afraid Sir Mulberry is a flatterer, my lord,’ said Mrs. Wititterly, turning to that young gentleman, who had been sucking the head of his cane in silence, and staring at Kate.\n\n‘Oh, deyvlish!’ replied Verisopht. Having given utterance to which remarkable sentiment, he occupied himself as before.\n\n‘Neither does Miss Nickleby look the worse,’ said Sir Mulberry, bending his bold gaze upon her. ‘She was always handsome, but upon my soul, ma’am, you seem to have imparted some of your own good looks to her besides.’\n\nTo judge from the glow which suffused the poor girl’s countenance after this speech, Mrs. Wititterly might, with some show of reason, have been supposed to have imparted to it some of that artificial bloom which decorated her own. Mrs. Wititterly admitted, though not with the best grace in the world, that Kate DID look pretty. She began to think, too, that Sir Mulberry was not quite so agreeable a creature as she had at first supposed him; for, although a skilful flatterer is a most delightful companion if you can keep him all to yourself, his taste becomes very doubtful when he takes to complimenting other people.\n\n‘Pyke,’ said the watchful Mr. Pluck, observing the effect which the praise of Miss Nickleby had produced.\n\n‘Well, Pluck,’ said Pyke.\n\n‘Is there anybody,’ demanded Mr. Pluck, mysteriously, ‘anybody you know, that Mrs. Wititterly’s profile reminds you of?’\n\n‘Reminds me of!’ answered Pyke. ‘Of course there is.’\n\n‘Who do you mean?’ said Pluck, in the same mysterious manner. ‘The D. of B.?’\n\n‘The C. of B.,’ replied Pyke, with the faintest trace of a grin lingering in his countenance. ‘The beautiful sister is the countess; not the duchess.’\n\n‘True,’ said Pluck, ‘the C. of B. The resemblance is wonderful!’\n\n‘Perfectly startling,’ said Mr. Pyke.\n\nHere was a state of things! Mrs. Wititterly was declared, upon the testimony of two veracious and competent witnesses, to be the very picture of a countess! This was one of the consequences of getting into good society. Why, she might have moved among grovelling people for twenty years, and never heard of it. How could she, indeed? what did THEY know about countesses?\n\nThe two gentlemen having, by the greediness with which this little bait was swallowed, tested the extent of Mrs. Wititterly’s appetite for adulation, proceeded to administer that commodity in very large doses, thus affording to Sir Mulberry Hawk an opportunity of pestering Miss Nickleby with questions and remarks, to which she was absolutely obliged to make some reply. Meanwhile, Lord Verisopht enjoyed unmolested the full flavour of the gold knob at the top of his cane, as he would have done to the end of the interview if Mr. Wititterly had not come home, and caused the conversation to turn to his favourite topic.\n\n‘My lord,’ said Mr. Wititterly, ‘I am delighted--honoured--proud. Be seated again, my lord, pray. I am proud, indeed--most proud.’\n\nIt was to the secret annoyance of his wife that Mr. Wititterly said all this, for, although she was bursting with pride and arrogance, she would have had the illustrious guests believe that their visit was quite a common occurrence, and that they had lords and baronets to see them every day in the week. But Mr. Wititterly’s feelings were beyond the power of suppression.\n\n‘It is an honour, indeed!’ said Mr. Wititterly. ‘Julia, my soul, you will suffer for this tomorrow.’\n\n‘Suffer!’ cried Lord Verisopht.\n\n‘The reaction, my lord, the reaction,’ said Mr. Wititterly. ‘This violent strain upon the nervous system over, my lord, what ensues? A sinking, a depression, a lowness, a lassitude, a debility. My lord, if Sir Tumley Snuffim was to see that delicate creature at this moment, he would not give a--a--THIS for her life.’ In illustration of which remark, Mr Wititterly took a pinch of snuff from his box, and jerked it lightly into the air as an emblem of instability.\n\n‘Not THAT,’ said Mr. Wititterly, looking about him with a serious countenance. ‘Sir Tumley Snuffim would not give that for Mrs Wititterly’s existence.’\n\nMr. Wititterly told this with a kind of sober exultation, as if it were no trifling distinction for a man to have a wife in such a desperate state, and Mrs. Wititterly sighed and looked on, as if she felt the honour, but had determined to bear it as meekly as might be.\n\n‘Mrs. Wititterly,’ said her husband, ‘is Sir Tumley Snuffim’s favourite patient. I believe I may venture to say, that Mrs. Wititterly is the first person who took the new medicine which is supposed to have destroyed a family at Kensington Gravel Pits. I believe she was. If I am wrong, Julia, my dear, you will correct me.’\n\n‘I believe I was,’ said Mrs. Wititterly, in a faint voice.\n\nAs there appeared to be some doubt in the mind of his patron how he could best join in this conversation, the indefatigable Mr. Pyke threw himself into the breach, and, by way of saying something to the point, inquired--with reference to the aforesaid medicine--whether it was nice.\n\n‘No, sir, it was not. It had not even that recommendation,’ said Mr. W.\n\n‘Mrs. Wititterly is quite a martyr,’ observed Pyke, with a complimentary bow.\n\n‘I THINK I am,’ said Mrs. Wititterly, smiling.\n\n‘I think you are, my dear Julia,’ replied her husband, in a tone which seemed to say that he was not vain, but still must insist upon their privileges. ‘If anybody, my lord,’ added Mr. Wititterly, wheeling round to the nobleman, ‘will produce to me a greater martyr than Mrs Wititterly, all I can say is, that I shall be glad to see that martyr, whether male or female--that’s all, my lord.’\n\nPyke and Pluck promptly remarked that certainly nothing could be fairer than that; and the call having been by this time protracted to a very great length, they obeyed Sir Mulberry’s look, and rose to go. This brought Sir Mulberry himself and Lord Verisopht on their legs also. Many protestations of friendship, and expressions anticipative of the pleasure which must inevitably flow from so happy an acquaintance, were exchanged, and the visitors departed, with renewed assurances that at all times and seasons the mansion of the Wititterlys would be honoured by receiving them beneath its roof.\n\nThat they came at all times and seasons--that they dined there one day, supped the next, dined again on the next, and were constantly to and fro on all--that they made parties to visit public places, and met by accident at lounges--that upon all these occasions Miss Nickleby was exposed to the constant and unremitting persecution of Sir Mulberry Hawk, who now began to feel his character, even in the estimation of his two dependants, involved in the successful reduction of her pride--that she had no intervals of peace or rest, except at those hours when she could sit in her solitary room, and weep over the trials of the day--all these were consequences naturally flowing from the well-laid plans of Sir Mulberry, and their able execution by the auxiliaries, Pyke and Pluck.\n\nAnd thus for a fortnight matters went on. That any but the weakest and silliest of people could have seen in one interview that Lord Verisopht, though he was a lord, and Sir Mulberry Hawk, though he was a baronet, were not persons accustomed to be the best possible companions, and were certainly not calculated by habits, manners, tastes, or conversation, to shine with any very great lustre in the society of ladies, need scarcely be remarked. But with Mrs. Wititterly the two titles were all sufficient; coarseness became humour, vulgarity softened itself down into the most charming eccentricity; insolence took the guise of an easy absence of reserve, attainable only by those who had had the good fortune to mix with high folks.\n\nIf the mistress put such a construction upon the behaviour of her new friends, what could the companion urge against them? If they accustomed themselves to very little restraint before the lady of the house, with how much more freedom could they address her paid dependent! Nor was even this the worst. As the odious Sir Mulberry Hawk attached himself to Kate with less and less of disguise, Mrs. Wititterly began to grow jealous of the superior attractions of Miss Nickleby. If this feeling had led to her banishment from the drawing-room when such company was there, Kate would have been only too happy and willing that it should have existed, but unfortunately for her she possessed that native grace and true gentility of manner, and those thousand nameless accomplishments which give to female society its greatest charm; if these be valuable anywhere, they were especially so where the lady of the house was a mere animated doll. The consequence was, that Kate had the double mortification of being an indispensable part of the circle when Sir Mulberry and his friends were there, and of being exposed, on that very account, to all Mrs. Wititterly’s ill-humours and caprices when they were gone. She became utterly and completely miserable.\n\nMrs. Wititterly had never thrown off the mask with regard to Sir Mulberry, but when she was more than usually out of temper, attributed the circumstance, as ladies sometimes do, to nervous indisposition. However, as the dreadful idea that Lord Verisopht also was somewhat taken with Kate, and that she, Mrs. Wititterly, was quite a secondary person, dawned upon that lady’s mind and gradually developed itself, she became possessed with a large quantity of highly proper and most virtuous indignation, and felt it her duty, as a married lady and a moral member of society, to mention the circumstance to ‘the young person’ without delay.\n\nAccordingly Mrs. Wititterly broke ground next morning, during a pause in the novel-reading.\n\n‘Miss Nickleby,’ said Mrs. Wititterly, ‘I wish to speak to you very gravely. I am sorry to have to do it, upon my word I am very sorry, but you leave me no alternative, Miss Nickleby.’ Here Mrs. Wititterly tossed her head--not passionately, only virtuously--and remarked, with some appearance of excitement, that she feared that palpitation of the heart was coming on again.\n\n‘Your behaviour, Miss Nickleby,’ resumed the lady, ‘is very far from pleasing me--very far. I am very anxious indeed that you should do well, but you may depend upon it, Miss Nickleby, you will not, if you go on as you do.’\n\n‘Ma’am!’ exclaimed Kate, proudly.\n\n‘Don’t agitate me by speaking in that way, Miss Nickleby, don’t,’ said Mrs. Wititterly, with some violence, ‘or you’ll compel me to ring the bell.’\n\nKate looked at her, but said nothing.\n\n‘You needn’t suppose,’ resumed Mrs. Wititterly, ‘that your looking at me in that way, Miss Nickleby, will prevent my saying what I am going to say, which I feel to be a religious duty. You needn’t direct your glances towards me,’ said Mrs. Wititterly, with a sudden burst of spite; ‘I am not Sir Mulberry, no, nor Lord Frederick Verisopht, Miss Nickleby, nor am I Mr. Pyke, nor Mr. Pluck either.’\n\nKate looked at her again, but less steadily than before; and resting her elbow on the table, covered her eyes with her hand.\n\n‘If such things had been done when I was a young girl,’ said Mrs Wititterly (this, by the way, must have been some little time before), ‘I don’t suppose anybody would have believed it.’\n\n‘I don’t think they would,’ murmured Kate. ‘I do not think anybody would believe, without actually knowing it, what I seem doomed to undergo!’\n\n‘Don’t talk to me of being doomed to undergo, Miss Nickleby, if you please,’ said Mrs. Wititterly, with a shrillness of tone quite surprising in so great an invalid. ‘I will not be answered, Miss Nickleby. I am not accustomed to be answered, nor will I permit it for an instant. Do you hear?’ she added, waiting with some apparent inconsistency FOR an answer.\n\n‘I do hear you, ma’am,’ replied Kate, ‘with surprise--with greater surprise than I can express.’\n\n‘I have always considered you a particularly well-behaved young person for your station in life,’ said Mrs. Wititterly; ‘and as you are a person of healthy appearance, and neat in your dress and so forth, I have taken an interest in you, as I do still, considering that I owe a sort of duty to that respectable old female, your mother. For these reasons, Miss Nickleby, I must tell you once for all, and begging you to mind what I say, that I must insist upon your immediately altering your very forward behaviour to the gentlemen who visit at this house. It really is not becoming,’ said Mrs. Wititterly, closing her chaste eyes as she spoke; ‘it is improper--quite improper.’\n\n‘Oh!’ cried Kate, looking upwards and clasping her hands; ‘is not this, is not this, too cruel, too hard to bear! Is it not enough that I should have suffered as I have, night and day; that I should almost have sunk in my own estimation from very shame of having been brought into contact with such people; but must I also be exposed to this unjust and most unfounded charge!’\n\n‘You will have the goodness to recollect, Miss Nickleby,’ said Mrs Wititterly, ‘that when you use such terms as “unjust”, and “unfounded”, you charge me, in effect, with stating that which is untrue.’\n\n‘I do,’ said Kate with honest indignation. ‘Whether you make this accusation of yourself, or at the prompting of others, is alike to me. I say it IS vilely, grossly, wilfully untrue. Is it possible!’ cried Kate, ‘that anyone of my own sex can have sat by, and not have seen the misery these men have caused me? Is it possible that you, ma’am, can have been present, and failed to mark the insulting freedom that their every look bespoke? Is it possible that you can have avoided seeing, that these libertines, in their utter disrespect for you, and utter disregard of all gentlemanly behaviour, and almost of decency, have had but one object in introducing themselves here, and that the furtherance of their designs upon a friendless, helpless girl, who, without this humiliating confession, might have hoped to receive from one so much her senior something like womanly aid and sympathy? I do not--I cannot believe it!’\n\nIf poor Kate had possessed the slightest knowledge of the world, she certainly would not have ventured, even in the excitement into which she had been lashed, upon such an injudicious speech as this. Its effect was precisely what a more experienced observer would have foreseen. Mrs. Wititterly received the attack upon her veracity with exemplary calmness, and listened with the most heroic fortitude to Kate’s account of her own sufferings. But allusion being made to her being held in disregard by the gentlemen, she evinced violent emotion, and this blow was no sooner followed up by the remark concerning her seniority, than she fell back upon the sofa, uttering dismal screams.\n\n‘What is the matter?’ cried Mr. Wititterly, bouncing into the room. ‘Heavens, what do I see? Julia! Julia! look up, my life, look up!’\n\nBut Julia looked down most perseveringly, and screamed still louder; so Mr. Wititterly rang the bell, and danced in a frenzied manner round the sofa on which Mrs. Wititterly lay; uttering perpetual cries for Sir Tumley Snuffim, and never once leaving off to ask for any explanation of the scene before him.\n\n‘Run for Sir Tumley,’ cried Mr. Wititterly, menacing the page with both fists. ‘I knew it, Miss Nickleby,’ he said, looking round with an air of melancholy triumph, ‘that society has been too much for her. This is all soul, you know, every bit of it.’ With this assurance Mr. Wititterly took up the prostrate form of Mrs. Wititterly, and carried her bodily off to bed.\n\nKate waited until Sir Tumley Snuffim had paid his visit and looked in with a report, that, through the special interposition of a merciful Providence (thus spake Sir Tumley), Mrs. Wititterly had gone to sleep. She then hastily attired herself for walking, and leaving word that she should return within a couple of hours, hurried away towards her uncle’s house.\n\nIt had been a good day with Ralph Nickleby--quite a lucky day; and as he walked to and fro in his little back-room with his hands clasped behind him, adding up in his own mind all the sums that had been, or would be, netted from the business done since morning, his mouth was drawn into a hard stern smile; while the firmness of the lines and curves that made it up, as well as the cunning glance of his cold, bright eye, seemed to tell, that if any resolution or cunning would increase the profits, they would not fail to be excited for the purpose.\n\n‘Very good!’ said Ralph, in allusion, no doubt, to some proceeding of the day. ‘He defies the usurer, does he? Well, we shall see. “Honesty is the best policy,” is it? We’ll try that too.’\n\nHe stopped, and then walked on again.\n\n‘He is content,’ said Ralph, relaxing into a smile, ‘to set his known character and conduct against the power of money--dross, as he calls it. Why, what a dull blockhead this fellow must be! Dross to, dross! Who’s that?’\n\n‘Me,’ said Newman Noggs, looking in. ‘Your niece.’\n\n‘What of her?’ asked Ralph sharply.\n\n‘She’s here.’\n‘Here!’\n\nNewman jerked his head towards his little room, to signify that she was waiting there.\n\n‘What does she want?’ asked Ralph.\n\n‘I don’t know,’ rejoined Newman. ‘Shall I ask?’ he added quickly.\n\n‘No,’ replied Ralph. ‘Show her in! Stay.’ He hastily put away a padlocked cash-box that was on the table, and substituted in its stead an empty purse. ‘There,’ said Ralph. ‘NOW she may come in.’\n\nNewman, with a grim smile at this manoeuvre, beckoned the young lady to advance, and having placed a chair for her, retired; looking stealthily over his shoulder at Ralph as he limped slowly out.\n\n‘Well,’ said Ralph, roughly enough; but still with something more of kindness in his manner than he would have exhibited towards anybody else. ‘Well, my--dear. What now?’\n\nKate raised her eyes, which were filled with tears; and with an effort to master her emotion strove to speak, but in vain. So drooping her head again, she remained silent. Her face was hidden from his view, but Ralph could see that she was weeping.\n\n‘I can guess the cause of this!’ thought Ralph, after looking at her for some time in silence. ‘I can--I can--guess the cause. Well! Well!’ thought Ralph--for the moment quite disconcerted, as he watched the anguish of his beautiful niece. ‘Where is the harm? only a few tears; and it’s an excellent lesson for her, an excellent lesson.’\n\n‘What is the matter?’ asked Ralph, drawing a chair opposite, and sitting down.\n\nHe was rather taken aback by the sudden firmness with which Kate looked up and answered him.\n\n‘The matter which brings me to you, sir,’ she said, ‘is one which should call the blood up into your cheeks, and make you burn to hear, as it does me to tell. I have been wronged; my feelings have been outraged, insulted, wounded past all healing, and by your friends.’\n\n‘Friends!’ cried Ralph, sternly. ‘I have no friends, girl.’\n\n‘By the men I saw here, then,’ returned Kate, quickly. ‘If they were no friends of yours, and you knew what they were,--oh, the more shame on you, uncle, for bringing me among them. To have subjected me to what I was exposed to here, through any misplaced confidence or imperfect knowledge of your guests, would have required some strong excuse; but if you did it--as I now believe you did--knowing them well, it was most dastardly and cruel.’\n\nRalph drew back in utter amazement at this plain speaking, and regarded Kate with the sternest look. But she met his gaze proudly and firmly, and although her face was very pale, it looked more noble and handsome, lighted up as it was, than it had ever appeared before.\n\n‘There is some of that boy’s blood in you, I see,’ said Ralph, speaking in his harshest tones, as something in the flashing eye reminded him of Nicholas at their last meeting.\n\n‘I hope there is!’ replied Kate. ‘I should be proud to know it. I am young, uncle, and all the difficulties and miseries of my situation have kept it down, but I have been roused today beyond all endurance, and come what may, I WILL NOT, as I am your brother’s child, bear these insults longer.’\n\n‘What insults, girl?’ demanded Ralph, sharply.\n\n‘Remember what took place here, and ask yourself,’ replied Kate, colouring deeply. ‘Uncle, you must--I am sure you will--release me from such vile and degrading companionship as I am exposed to now. I do not mean,’ said Kate, hurrying to the old man, and laying her arm upon his shoulder; ‘I do not mean to be angry and violent--I beg your pardon if I have seemed so, dear uncle,--but you do not know what I have suffered, you do not indeed. You cannot tell what the heart of a young girl is--I have no right to expect you should; but when I tell you that I am wretched, and that my heart is breaking, I am sure you will help me. I am sure, I am sure you will!’\n\nRalph looked at her for an instant; then turned away his head, and beat his foot nervously upon the ground.\n\n‘I have gone on day after day,’ said Kate, bending over him, and timidly placing her little hand in his, ‘in the hope that this persecution would cease; I have gone on day after day, compelled to assume the appearance of cheerfulness, when I was most unhappy. I have had no counsellor, no adviser, no one to protect me. Mama supposes that these are honourable men, rich and distinguished, and how CAN I--how can I undeceive her--when she is so happy in these little delusions, which are the only happiness she has? The lady with whom you placed me, is not the person to whom I could confide matters of so much delicacy, and I have come at last to you, the only friend I have at hand--almost the only friend I have at all--to entreat and implore you to assist me.’\n\n‘How can I assist you, child?’ said Ralph, rising from his chair, and pacing up and down the room in his old attitude.\n\n‘You have influence with one of these men, I KNOW,’ rejoined Kate, emphatically. ‘Would not a word from you induce them to desist from this unmanly course?’\n\n‘No,’ said Ralph, suddenly turning; ‘at least--that--I can’t say it, if it would.’\n\n‘Can’t say it!’\n\n‘No,’ said Ralph, coming to a dead stop, and clasping his hands more tightly behind him. ‘I can’t say it.’\n\nKate fell back a step or two, and looked at him, as if in doubt whether she had heard aright.\n\n‘We are connected in business,’ said Ralph, poising himself alternately on his toes and heels, and looking coolly in his niece’s face, ‘in business, and I can’t afford to offend them. What is it after all? We have all our trials, and this is one of yours. Some girls would be proud to have such gallants at their feet.’\n\n‘Proud!’ cried Kate.\n\n‘I don’t say,’ rejoined Ralph, raising his forefinger, ‘but that you do right to despise them; no, you show your good sense in that, as indeed I knew from the first you would. Well. In all other respects you are comfortably bestowed. It’s not much to bear. If this young lord does dog your footsteps, and whisper his drivelling inanities in your ears, what of it? It’s a dishonourable passion. So be it; it won’t last long. Some other novelty will spring up one day, and you will be released. In the mean time--’\n\n‘In the mean time,’ interrupted Kate, with becoming pride and indignation, ‘I am to be the scorn of my own sex, and the toy of the other; justly condemned by all women of right feeling, and despised by all honest and honourable men; sunken in my own esteem, and degraded in every eye that looks upon me. No, not if I work my fingers to the bone, not if I am driven to the roughest and hardest labour. Do not mistake me. I will not disgrace your recommendation. I will remain in the house in which it placed me, until I am entitled to leave it by the terms of my engagement; though, mind, I see these men no more. When I quit it, I will hide myself from them and you, and, striving to support my mother by hard service, I will live, at least, in peace, and trust in God to help me.’\n\nWith these words, she waved her hand, and quitted the room, leaving Ralph Nickleby motionless as a statue.\n\nThe surprise with which Kate, as she closed the room-door, beheld, close beside it, Newman Noggs standing bolt upright in a little niche in the wall like some scarecrow or Guy Faux laid up in winter quarters, almost occasioned her to call aloud. But, Newman laying his finger upon his lips, she had the presence of mind to refrain.\n\n‘Don’t,’ said Newman, gliding out of his recess, and accompanying her across the hall. ‘Don’t cry, don’t cry.’ Two very large tears, by-the-bye, were running down Newman’s face as he spoke.\n\n‘I see how it is,’ said poor Noggs, drawing from his pocket what seemed to be a very old duster, and wiping Kate’s eyes with it, as gently as if she were an infant. ‘You’re giving way now. Yes, yes, very good; that’s right, I like that. It was right not to give way before him. Yes, yes! Ha, ha, ha! Oh, yes. Poor thing!’\n\nWith these disjointed exclamations, Newman wiped his own eyes with the afore-mentioned duster, and, limping to the street-door, opened it to let her out.\n\n‘Don’t cry any more,’ whispered Newman. ‘I shall see you soon. Ha! ha! ha! And so shall somebody else too. Yes, yes. Ho! ho!’\n\n‘God bless you,’ answered Kate, hurrying out, ‘God bless you.’\n\n‘Same to you,’ rejoined Newman, opening the door again a little way to say so. ‘Ha, ha, ha! Ho! ho! ho!’\n\nAnd Newman Noggs opened the door once again to nod cheerfully, and laugh--and shut it, to shake his head mournfully, and cry.\n\nRalph remained in the same attitude till he heard the noise of the closing door, when he shrugged his shoulders, and after a few turns about the room--hasty at first, but gradually becoming slower, as he relapsed into himself--sat down before his desk.\n\nIt is one of those problems of human nature, which may be noted down, but not solved;--although Ralph felt no remorse at that moment for his conduct towards the innocent, true-hearted girl; although his libertine clients had done precisely what he had expected, precisely what he most wished, and precisely what would tend most to his advantage, still he hated them for doing it, from the very bottom of his soul.\n\n‘Ugh!’ said Ralph, scowling round, and shaking his clenched hand as the faces of the two profligates rose up before his mind; ‘you shall pay for this. Oh! you shall pay for this!’\n\nAs the usurer turned for consolation to his books and papers, a performance was going on outside his office door, which would have occasioned him no small surprise, if he could by any means have become acquainted with it.\n\nNewman Noggs was the sole actor. He stood at a little distance from the door, with his face towards it; and with the sleeves of his coat turned back at the wrists, was occupied in bestowing the most vigorous, scientific, and straightforward blows upon the empty air.\n\nAt first sight, this would have appeared merely a wise precaution in a man of sedentary habits, with the view of opening the chest and strengthening the muscles of the arms. But the intense eagerness and joy depicted in the face of Newman Noggs, which was suffused with perspiration; the surprising energy with which he directed a constant succession of blows towards a particular panel about five feet eight from the ground, and still worked away in the most untiring and persevering manner, would have sufficiently explained to the attentive observer, that his imagination was thrashing, to within an inch of his life, his body’s most active employer, Mr. Ralph Nickleby.\n\n# CHAPTER 29\n\nOf the Proceedings of Nicholas, and certain Internal Divisions in the Company of Mr. Vincent Crummles\nThe unexpected success and favour with which his experiment at Portsmouth had been received, induced Mr. Crummles to prolong his stay in that town for a fortnight beyond the period he had originally assigned for the duration of his visit, during which time Nicholas personated a vast variety of characters with undiminished success, and attracted so many people to the theatre who had never been seen there before, that a benefit was considered by the manager a very promising speculation. Nicholas assenting to the terms proposed, the benefit was had, and by it he realised no less a sum than twenty pounds.\n\nPossessed of this unexpected wealth, his first act was to enclose to honest John Browdie the amount of his friendly loan, which he accompanied with many expressions of gratitude and esteem, and many cordial wishes for his matrimonial happiness. To Newman Noggs he forwarded one half of the sum he had realised, entreating him to take an opportunity of handing it to Kate in secret, and conveying to her the warmest assurances of his love and affection. He made no mention of the way in which he had employed himself; merely informing Newman that a letter addressed to him under his assumed name at the Post Office, Portsmouth, would readily find him, and entreating that worthy friend to write full particulars of the situation of his mother and sister, and an account of all the grand things that Ralph Nickleby had done for them since his departure from London.\n\n‘You are out of spirits,’ said Smike, on the night after the letter had been dispatched.\n\n‘Not I!’ rejoined Nicholas, with assumed gaiety, for the confession would have made the boy miserable all night; ‘I was thinking about my sister, Smike.’\n\n‘Sister!’\n\n‘Ay.’\n\n‘Is she like you?’ inquired Smike.\n\n‘Why, so they say,’ replied Nicholas, laughing, ‘only a great deal handsomer.’\n\n‘She must be VERY beautiful,’ said Smike, after thinking a little while with his hands folded together, and his eyes bent upon his friend.\n\n‘Anybody who didn’t know you as well as I do, my dear fellow, would say you were an accomplished courtier,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘I don’t even know what that is,’ replied Smike, shaking his head. ‘Shall I ever see your sister?’\n\n‘To be sure,’ cried Nicholas; ‘we shall all be together one of these days--when we are rich, Smike.’\n\n‘How is it that you, who are so kind and good to me, have nobody to be kind to you?’ asked Smike. ‘I cannot make that out.’\n\n‘Why, it is a long story,’ replied Nicholas, ‘and one you would have some difficulty in comprehending, I fear. I have an enemy--you understand what that is?’\n\n‘Oh, yes, I understand that,’ said Smike.\n\n‘Well, it is owing to him,’ returned Nicholas. ‘He is rich, and not so easily punished as YOUR old enemy, Mr. Squeers. He is my uncle, but he is a villain, and has done me wrong.’\n\n‘Has he though?’ asked Smike, bending eagerly forward. ‘What is his name? Tell me his name.’\n\n‘Ralph--Ralph Nickleby.’\n\n‘Ralph Nickleby,’ repeated Smike. ‘Ralph. I’ll get that name by heart.’\n\nHe had muttered it over to himself some twenty times, when a loud knock at the door disturbed him from his occupation. Before he could open it, Mr. Folair, the pantomimist, thrust in his head.\n\nMr. Folair’s head was usually decorated with a very round hat, unusually high in the crown, and curled up quite tight in the brims. On the present occasion he wore it very much on one side, with the back part forward in consequence of its being the least rusty; round his neck he wore a flaming red worsted comforter, whereof the straggling ends peeped out beneath his threadbare Newmarket coat, which was very tight and buttoned all the way up. He carried in his hand one very dirty glove, and a cheap dress cane with a glass handle; in short, his whole appearance was unusually dashing, and demonstrated a far more scrupulous attention to his toilet than he was in the habit of bestowing upon it.\n\n‘Good-evening, sir,’ said Mr. Folair, taking off the tall hat, and running his fingers through his hair. ‘I bring a communication. Hem!’\n\n‘From whom and what about?’ inquired Nicholas. ‘You are unusually mysterious tonight.’\n\n‘Cold, perhaps,’ returned Mr. Folair; ‘cold, perhaps. That is the fault of my position--not of myself, Mr. Johnson. My position as a mutual friend requires it, sir.’ Mr. Folair paused with a most impressive look, and diving into the hat before noticed, drew from thence a small piece of whity-brown paper curiously folded, whence he brought forth a note which it had served to keep clean, and handing it over to Nicholas, said--\n\n‘Have the goodness to read that, sir.’\n\nNicholas, in a state of much amazement, took the note and broke the seal, glancing at Mr. Folair as he did so, who, knitting his brow and pursing up his mouth with great dignity, was sitting with his eyes steadily fixed upon the ceiling.\n\nIt was directed to blank Johnson, Esq., by favour of Augustus Folair, Esq.; and the astonishment of Nicholas was in no degree lessened, when he found it to be couched in the following laconic terms:--\n\n“Mr. Lenville presents his kind regards to Mr. Johnson, and will feel obliged if he will inform him at what hour tomorrow morning it will be most convenient to him to meet Mr. L. at the Theatre, for the purpose of having his nose pulled in the presence of the company.\n\n“Mr. Lenville requests Mr. Johnson not to neglect making an appointment, as he has invited two or three professional friends to witness the ceremony, and cannot disappoint them upon any account whatever.\n\nIndignant as he was at this impertinence, there was something so exquisitely absurd in such a cartel of defiance, that Nicholas was obliged to bite his lip and read the note over two or three times before he could muster sufficient gravity and sternness to address the hostile messenger, who had not taken his eyes from the ceiling, nor altered the expression of his face in the slightest degree.\n\n‘Do you know the contents of this note, sir?’ he asked, at length.\n\n‘Yes,’ rejoined Mr. Folair, looking round for an instant, and immediately carrying his eyes back again to the ceiling.\n\n‘And how dare you bring it here, sir?’ asked Nicholas, tearing it into very little pieces, and jerking it in a shower towards the messenger. ‘Had you no fear of being kicked downstairs, sir?’\n\nMr. Folair turned his head--now ornamented with several fragments of the note--towards Nicholas, and with the same imperturbable dignity, briefly replied ‘No.’\n\n‘Then,’ said Nicholas, taking up the tall hat and tossing it towards the door, ‘you had better follow that article of your dress, sir, or you may find yourself very disagreeably deceived, and that within a dozen seconds.’\n\n‘I say, Johnson,’ remonstrated Mr. Folair, suddenly losing all his dignity, ‘none of that, you know. No tricks with a gentleman’s wardrobe.’\n\n‘Leave the room,’ returned Nicholas. ‘How could you presume to come here on such an errand, you scoundrel?’\n\n‘Pooh! pooh!’ said Mr. Folair, unwinding his comforter, and gradually getting himself out of it. ‘There--that’s enough.’\n\n‘Enough!’ cried Nicholas, advancing towards him. ‘Take yourself off, sir.’\n\n‘Pooh! pooh! I tell you,’ returned Mr. Folair, waving his hand in deprecation of any further wrath; ‘I wasn’t in earnest. I only brought it in joke.’\n\n‘You had better be careful how you indulge in such jokes again,’ said Nicholas, ‘or you may find an allusion to pulling noses rather a dangerous reminder for the subject of your facetiousness. Was it written in joke, too, pray?’\n\n‘No, no, that’s the best of it,’ returned the actor; ‘right down earnest--honour bright.’\n\nNicholas could not repress a smile at the odd figure before him, which, at all times more calculated to provoke mirth than anger, was especially so at that moment, when with one knee upon the ground, Mr. Folair twirled his old hat round upon his hand, and affected the extremest agony lest any of the nap should have been knocked off--an ornament which it is almost superfluous to say, it had not boasted for many months.\n\n‘Come, sir,’ said Nicholas, laughing in spite of himself. ‘Have the goodness to explain.’\n\n‘Why, I’ll tell you how it is,’ said Mr. Folair, sitting himself down in a chair with great coolness. ‘Since you came here Lenville has done nothing but second business, and, instead of having a reception every night as he used to have, they have let him come on as if he was nobody.’\n\n‘What do you mean by a reception?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘Jupiter!’ exclaimed Mr. Folair, ‘what an unsophisticated shepherd you are, Johnson! Why, applause from the house when you first come on. So he has gone on night after night, never getting a hand, and you getting a couple of rounds at least, and sometimes three, till at length he got quite desperate, and had half a mind last night to play Tybalt with a real sword, and pink you--not dangerously, but just enough to lay you up for a month or two.’\n\n‘Very considerate,’ remarked Nicholas.\n\n‘Yes, I think it was under the circumstances; his professional reputation being at stake,’ said Mr. Folair, quite seriously. ‘But his heart failed him, and he cast about for some other way of annoying you, and making himself popular at the same time--for that’s the point. Notoriety, notoriety, is the thing. Bless you, if he had pinked you,’ said Mr. Folair, stopping to make a calculation in his mind, ‘it would have been worth--ah, it would have been worth eight or ten shillings a week to him. All the town would have come to see the actor who nearly killed a man by mistake; I shouldn’t wonder if it had got him an engagement in London. However, he was obliged to try some other mode of getting popular, and this one occurred to him. It’s a clever idea, really. If you had shown the white feather, and let him pull your nose, he’d have got it into the paper; if you had sworn the peace against him, it would have been in the paper too, and he’d have been just as much talked about as you--don’t you see?’\n\n‘Oh, certainly,’ rejoined Nicholas; ‘but suppose I were to turn the tables, and pull HIS nose, what then? Would that make his fortune?’\n\n‘Why, I don’t think it would,’ replied Mr. Folair, scratching his head, ‘because there wouldn’t be any romance about it, and he wouldn’t be favourably known. To tell you the truth though, he didn’t calculate much upon that, for you’re always so mild-spoken, and are so popular among the women, that we didn’t suspect you of showing fight. If you did, however, he has a way of getting out of it easily, depend upon that.’\n\n‘Has he?’ rejoined Nicholas. ‘We will try, tomorrow morning. In the meantime, you can give whatever account of our interview you like best. Good-night.’\n\nAs Mr. Folair was pretty well known among his fellow-actors for a man who delighted in mischief, and was by no means scrupulous, Nicholas had not much doubt but that he had secretly prompted the tragedian in the course he had taken, and, moreover, that he would have carried his mission with a very high hand if he had not been disconcerted by the very unexpected demonstrations with which it had been received. It was not worth his while to be serious with him, however, so he dismissed the pantomimist, with a gentle hint that if he offended again it would be under the penalty of a broken head; and Mr. Folair, taking the caution in exceedingly good part, walked away to confer with his principal, and give such an account of his proceedings as he might think best calculated to carry on the joke.\n\nHe had no doubt reported that Nicholas was in a state of extreme bodily fear; for when that young gentleman walked with much deliberation down to the theatre next morning at the usual hour, he found all the company assembled in evident expectation, and Mr. Lenville, with his severest stage face, sitting majestically on a table, whistling defiance.\n\nNow the ladies were on the side of Nicholas, and the gentlemen (being jealous) were on the side of the disappointed tragedian; so that the latter formed a little group about the redoubtable Mr. Lenville, and the former looked on at a little distance in some trepidation and anxiety. On Nicholas stopping to salute them, Mr. Lenville laughed a scornful laugh, and made some general remark touching the natural history of puppies.\n\n‘Oh!’ said Nicholas, looking quietly round, ‘are you there?’\n\n‘Slave!’ returned Mr. Lenville, flourishing his right arm, and approaching Nicholas with a theatrical stride. But somehow he appeared just at that moment a little startled, as if Nicholas did not look quite so frightened as he had expected, and came all at once to an awkward halt, at which the assembled ladies burst into a shrill laugh.\n\n‘Object of my scorn and hatred!’ said Mr. Lenville, ‘I hold ye in contempt.’\n\nNicholas laughed in very unexpected enjoyment of this performance; and the ladies, by way of encouragement, laughed louder than before; whereat Mr. Lenville assumed his bitterest smile, and expressed his opinion that they were ‘minions’.\n\n‘But they shall not protect ye!’ said the tragedian, taking an upward look at Nicholas, beginning at his boots and ending at the crown of his head, and then a downward one, beginning at the crown of his head, and ending at his boots--which two looks, as everybody knows, express defiance on the stage. ‘They shall not protect ye--boy!’\n\nThus speaking, Mr. Lenville folded his arms, and treated Nicholas to that expression of face with which, in melodramatic performances, he was in the habit of regarding the tyrannical kings when they said, ‘Away with him to the deepest dungeon beneath the castle moat;’ and which, accompanied with a little jingling of fetters, had been known to produce great effects in its time.\n\nWhether it was the absence of the fetters or not, it made no very deep impression on Mr. Lenville’s adversary, however, but rather seemed to increase the good-humour expressed in his countenance; in which stage of the contest, one or two gentlemen, who had come out expressly to witness the pulling of Nicholas’s nose, grew impatient, murmuring that if it were to be done at all it had better be done at once, and that if Mr Lenville didn’t mean to do it he had better say so, and not keep them waiting there. Thus urged, the tragedian adjusted the cuff of his right coat sleeve for the performance of the operation, and walked in a very stately manner up to Nicholas, who suffered him to approach to within the requisite distance, and then, without the smallest discomposure, knocked him down.\n\nBefore the discomfited tragedian could raise his head from the boards, Mrs. Lenville (who, as has been before hinted, was in an interesting state) rushed from the rear rank of ladies, and uttering a piercing scream threw herself upon the body.\n\n‘Do you see this, monster? Do you see THIS?’ cried Mr. Lenville, sitting up, and pointing to his prostrate lady, who was holding him very tight round the waist.\n\n‘Come,’ said Nicholas, nodding his head, ‘apologise for the insolent note you wrote to me last night, and waste no more time in talking.’\n\n‘Never!’ cried Mr. Lenville.\n\n‘Yes--yes--yes!’ screamed his wife. ‘For my sake--for mine, Lenville--forego all idle forms, unless you would see me a blighted corse at your feet.’\n\n‘This is affecting!’ said Mr. Lenville, looking round him, and drawing the back of his hand across his eyes. ‘The ties of nature are strong. The weak husband and the father--the father that is yet to be--relents. I apologise.’\n\n‘Humbly and submissively?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Humbly and submissively,’ returned the tragedian, scowling upwards. ‘But only to save her,--for a time will come--’\n\n‘Very good,’ said Nicholas; ‘I hope Mrs. Lenville may have a good one; and when it does come, and you are a father, you shall retract it if you have the courage. There. Be careful, sir, to what lengths your jealousy carries you another time; and be careful, also, before you venture too far, to ascertain your rival’s temper.’ With this parting advice Nicholas picked up Mr. Lenville’s ash stick which had flown out of his hand, and breaking it in half, threw him the pieces and withdrew, bowing slightly to the spectators as he walked out.\n\nThe profoundest deference was paid to Nicholas that night, and the people who had been most anxious to have his nose pulled in the morning, embraced occasions of taking him aside, and telling him with great feeling, how very friendly they took it that he should have treated that Lenville so properly, who was a most unbearable fellow, and on whom they had all, by a remarkable coincidence, at one time or other contemplated the infliction of condign punishment, which they had only been restrained from administering by considerations of mercy; indeed, to judge from the invariable termination of all these stories, there never was such a charitable and kind-hearted set of people as the male members of Mr. Crummles’s company.\n\nNicholas bore his triumph, as he had his success in the little world of the theatre, with the utmost moderation and good humour. The crestfallen Mr. Lenville made an expiring effort to obtain revenge by sending a boy into the gallery to hiss, but he fell a sacrifice to popular indignation, and was promptly turned out without having his money back.\n\n‘Well, Smike,’ said Nicholas when the first piece was over, and he had almost finished dressing to go home, ‘is there any letter yet?’\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Smike, ‘I got this one from the post-office.’\n\n‘From Newman Noggs,’ said Nicholas, casting his eye upon the cramped direction; ‘it’s no easy matter to make his writing out. Let me see--let me see.’\n\nBy dint of poring over the letter for half an hour, he contrived to make himself master of the contents, which were certainly not of a nature to set his mind at ease. Newman took upon himself to send back the ten pounds, observing that he had ascertained that neither Mrs. Nickleby nor Kate was in actual want of money at the moment, and that a time might shortly come when Nicholas might want it more. He entreated him not to be alarmed at what he was about to say;--there was no bad news--they were in good health--but he thought circumstances might occur, or were occurring, which would render it absolutely necessary that Kate should have her brother’s protection, and if so, Newman said, he would write to him to that effect, either by the next post or the next but one.\n\nNicholas read this passage very often, and the more he thought of it the more he began to fear some treachery upon the part of Ralph. Once or twice he felt tempted to repair to London at all hazards without an hour’s delay, but a little reflection assured him that if such a step were necessary, Newman would have spoken out and told him so at once.\n\n‘At all events I should prepare them here for the possibility of my going away suddenly,’ said Nicholas; ‘I should lose no time in doing that.’ As the thought occurred to him, he took up his hat and hurried to the green-room.\n\n‘Well, Mr. Johnson,’ said Mrs. Crummles, who was seated there in full regal costume, with the phenomenon as the Maiden in her maternal arms, ‘next week for Ryde, then for Winchester, then for--’\n\n‘I have some reason to fear,’ interrupted Nicholas, ‘that before you leave here my career with you will have closed.’\n\n‘Closed!’ cried Mrs. Crummles, raising her hands in astonishment.\n\n‘Closed!’ cried Miss Snevellicci, trembling so much in her tights that she actually laid her hand upon the shoulder of the manageress for support.\n\n‘Why he don’t mean to say he’s going!’ exclaimed Mrs. Grudden, making her way towards Mrs. Crummles. ‘Hoity toity! Nonsense.’\n\nThe phenomenon, being of an affectionate nature and moreover excitable, raised a loud cry, and Miss Belvawney and Miss Bravassa actually shed tears. Even the male performers stopped in their conversation, and echoed the word ‘Going!’ although some among them (and they had been the loudest in their congratulations that day) winked at each other as though they would not be sorry to lose such a favoured rival; an opinion, indeed, which the honest Mr. Folair, who was ready dressed for the savage, openly stated in so many words to a demon with whom he was sharing a pot of porter.\n\nNicholas briefly said that he feared it would be so, although he could not yet speak with any degree of certainty; and getting away as soon as he could, went home to con Newman’s letter once more, and speculate upon it afresh.\n\nHow trifling all that had been occupying his time and thoughts for many weeks seemed to him during that sleepless night, and how constantly and incessantly present to his imagination was the one idea that Kate in the midst of some great trouble and distress might even then be looking--and vainly too--for him!\n\n# CHAPTER 30\n\nFestivities are held in honour of Nicholas, who suddenly withdraws himself from the Society of Mr. Vincent Crummles and his Theatrical Companions\nMr. Vincent Crummles was no sooner acquainted with the public announcement which Nicholas had made relative to the probability of his shortly ceasing to be a member of the company, than he evinced many tokens of grief and consternation; and, in the extremity of his despair, even held out certain vague promises of a speedy improvement not only in the amount of his regular salary, but also in the contingent emoluments appertaining to his authorship. Finding Nicholas bent upon quitting the society--for he had now determined that, even if no further tidings came from Newman, he would, at all hazards, ease his mind by repairing to London and ascertaining the exact position of his sister--Mr. Crummles was fain to content himself by calculating the chances of his coming back again, and taking prompt and energetic measures to make the most of him before he went away.\n\n‘Let me see,’ said Mr. Crummles, taking off his outlaw’s wig, the better to arrive at a cool-headed view of the whole case. ‘Let me see. This is Wednesday night. We’ll have posters out the first thing in the morning, announcing positively your last appearance for tomorrow.’\n\n‘But perhaps it may not be my last appearance, you know,’ said Nicholas. ‘Unless I am summoned away, I should be sorry to inconvenience you by leaving before the end of the week.’\n\n‘So much the better,’ returned Mr. Crummles. ‘We can have positively your last appearance, on Thursday--re-engagement for one night more, on Friday--and, yielding to the wishes of numerous influential patrons, who were disappointed in obtaining seats, on Saturday. That ought to bring three very decent houses.’\n\n‘Then I am to make three last appearances, am I?’ inquired Nicholas, smiling.\n\n‘Yes,’ rejoined the manager, scratching his head with an air of some vexation; ‘three is not enough, and it’s very bungling and irregular not to have more, but if we can’t help it we can’t, so there’s no use in talking. A novelty would be very desirable. You couldn’t sing a comic song on the pony’s back, could you?’\n\n‘No,’ replied Nicholas, ‘I couldn’t indeed.’\n\n‘It has drawn money before now,’ said Mr. Crummles, with a look of disappointment. ‘What do you think of a brilliant display of fireworks?’\n\n‘That it would be rather expensive,’ replied Nicholas, drily.\n\n‘Eighteen-pence would do it,’ said Mr. Crummles. ‘You on the top of a pair of steps with the phenomenon in an attitude; “Farewell!” on a transparency behind; and nine people at the wings with a squib in each hand--all the dozen and a half going off at once--it would be very grand--awful from the front, quite awful.’\n\nAs Nicholas appeared by no means impressed with the solemnity of the proposed effect, but, on the contrary, received the proposition in a most irreverent manner, and laughed at it very heartily, Mr. Crummles abandoned the project in its birth, and gloomily observed that they must make up the best bill they could with combats and hornpipes, and so stick to the legitimate drama.\n\nFor the purpose of carrying this object into instant execution, the manager at once repaired to a small dressing-room, adjacent, where Mrs. Crummles was then occupied in exchanging the habiliments of a melodramatic empress for the ordinary attire of matrons in the nineteenth century. And with the assistance of this lady, and the accomplished Mrs. Grudden (who had quite a genius for making out bills, being a great hand at throwing in the notes of admiration, and knowing from long experience exactly where the largest capitals ought to go), he seriously applied himself to the composition of the poster.\n\n‘Heigho!’ sighed Nicholas, as he threw himself back in the prompter’s chair, after telegraphing the needful directions to Smike, who had been playing a meagre tailor in the interlude, with one skirt to his coat, and a little pocket-handkerchief with a large hole in it, and a woollen nightcap, and a red nose, and other distinctive marks peculiar to tailors on the stage. ‘Heigho! I wish all this were over.’\n\n‘Over, Mr. Johnson!’ repeated a female voice behind him, in a kind of plaintive surprise.\n\n‘It was an ungallant speech, certainly,’ said Nicholas, looking up to see who the speaker was, and recognising Miss Snevellicci. ‘I would not have made it if I had known you had been within hearing.’\n\n‘What a dear that Mr. Digby is!’ said Miss Snevellicci, as the tailor went off on the opposite side, at the end of the piece, with great applause. (Smike’s theatrical name was Digby.)\n\n‘I’ll tell him presently, for his gratification, that you said so,’ returned Nicholas.\n\n‘Oh you naughty thing!’ rejoined Miss Snevellicci. ‘I don’t know though, that I should much mind HIS knowing my opinion of him; with some other people, indeed, it might be--’ Here Miss Snevellicci stopped, as though waiting to be questioned, but no questioning came, for Nicholas was thinking about more serious matters.\n\n‘How kind it is of you,’ resumed Miss Snevellicci, after a short silence, ‘to sit waiting here for him night after night, night after night, no matter how tired you are; and taking so much pains with him, and doing it all with as much delight and readiness as if you were coining gold by it!’\n\n‘He well deserves all the kindness I can show him, and a great deal more,’ said Nicholas. ‘He is the most grateful, single-hearted, affectionate creature that ever breathed.’\n\n‘So odd, too,’ remarked Miss Snevellicci, ‘isn’t he?’\n\n‘God help him, and those who have made him so; he is indeed,’ rejoined Nicholas, shaking his head.\n\n‘He is such a devilish close chap,’ said Mr. Folair, who had come up a little before, and now joined in the conversation. ‘Nobody can ever get anything out of him.’\n\n‘What SHOULD they get out of him?’ asked Nicholas, turning round with some abruptness.\n\n‘Zooks! what a fire-eater you are, Johnson!’ returned Mr. Folair, pulling up the heel of his dancing shoe. ‘I’m only talking of the natural curiosity of the people here, to know what he has been about all his life.’\n\n‘Poor fellow! it is pretty plain, I should think, that he has not the intellect to have been about anything of much importance to them or anybody else,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Ay,’ rejoined the actor, contemplating the effect of his face in a lamp reflector, ‘but that involves the whole question, you know.’\n\n‘What question?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘Why, the who he is and what he is, and how you two, who are so different, came to be such close companions,’ replied Mr. Folair, delighted with the opportunity of saying something disagreeable. ‘That’s in everybody’s mouth.’\n\n‘The “everybody” of the theatre, I suppose?’ said Nicholas, contemptuously.\n\n‘In it and out of it too,’ replied the actor. ‘Why, you know, Lenville says--’\n\n‘I thought I had silenced him effectually,’ interrupted Nicholas, reddening.\n\n‘Perhaps you have,’ rejoined the immovable Mr. Folair; ‘if you have, he said this before he was silenced: Lenville says that you’re a regular stick of an actor, and that it’s only the mystery about you that has caused you to go down with the people here, and that Crummles keeps it up for his own sake; though Lenville says he don’t believe there’s anything at all in it, except your having got into a scrape and run away from somewhere, for doing something or other.’\n\n‘Oh!’ said Nicholas, forcing a smile.\n\n‘That’s a part of what he says,’ added Mr. Folair. ‘I mention it as the friend of both parties, and in strict confidence. I don’t agree with him, you know. He says he takes Digby to be more knave than fool; and old Fluggers, who does the heavy business you know, HE says that when he delivered messages at Covent Garden the season before last, there used to be a pickpocket hovering about the coach-stand who had exactly the face of Digby; though, as he very properly says, Digby may not be the same, but only his brother, or some near relation.’\n\n‘Oh!’ cried Nicholas again.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Mr. Folair, with undisturbed calmness, ‘that’s what they say. I thought I’d tell you, because really you ought to know. Oh! here’s this blessed phenomenon at last. Ugh, you little imposition, I should like to--quite ready, my darling,--humbug--Ring up, Mrs. G., and let the favourite wake ‘em.’\n\nUttering in a loud voice such of the latter allusions as were complimentary to the unconscious phenomenon, and giving the rest in a confidential ‘aside’ to Nicholas, Mr. Folair followed the ascent of the curtain with his eyes, regarded with a sneer the reception of Miss Crummles as the Maiden, and, falling back a step or two to advance with the better effect, uttered a preliminary howl, and ‘went on’ chattering his teeth and brandishing his tin tomahawk as the Indian Savage.\n\n‘So these are some of the stories they invent about us, and bandy from mouth to mouth!’ thought Nicholas. ‘If a man would commit an inexpiable offence against any society, large or small, let him be successful. They will forgive him any crime but that.’\n\n‘You surely don’t mind what that malicious creature says, Mr. Johnson?’ observed Miss Snevellicci in her most winning tones.\n\n‘Not I,’ replied Nicholas. ‘If I were going to remain here, I might think it worth my while to embroil myself. As it is, let them talk till they are hoarse. But here,’ added Nicholas, as Smike approached, ‘here comes the subject of a portion of their good-nature, so let he and I say good night together.’\n\n‘No, I will not let either of you say anything of the kind,’ returned Miss Snevellicci. ‘You must come home and see mama, who only came to Portsmouth today, and is dying to behold you. Led, my dear, persuade Mr Johnson.’\n\n‘Oh, I’m sure,’ returned Miss Ledrook, with considerable vivacity, ‘if YOU can’t persuade him--’ Miss Ledrook said no more, but intimated, by a dexterous playfulness, that if Miss Snevellicci couldn’t persuade him, nobody could.\n\n‘Mr. and Mrs. Lillyvick have taken lodgings in our house, and share our sitting-room for the present,’ said Miss Snevellicci. ‘Won’t that induce you?’\n\n‘Surely,’ returned Nicholas, ‘I can require no possible inducement beyond your invitation.’\n\n‘Oh no! I dare say,’ rejoined Miss Snevellicci. And Miss Ledrook said, ‘Upon my word!’ Upon which Miss Snevellicci said that Miss Ledrook was a giddy thing; and Miss Ledrook said that Miss Snevellicci needn’t colour up quite so much; and Miss Snevellicci beat Miss Ledrook, and Miss Ledrook beat Miss Snevellicci.\n\n‘Come,’ said Miss Ledrook, ‘it’s high time we were there, or we shall have poor Mrs. Snevellicci thinking that you have run away with her daughter, Mr. Johnson; and then we should have a pretty to-do.’\n\n‘My dear Led,’ remonstrated Miss Snevellicci, ‘how you do talk!’\n\nMiss Ledrook made no answer, but taking Smike’s arm in hers, left her friend and Nicholas to follow at their pleasure; which it pleased them, or rather pleased Nicholas, who had no great fancy for a TETE-A-TETE under the circumstances, to do at once.\n\nThere were not wanting matters of conversation when they reached the street, for it turned out that Miss Snevellicci had a small basket to carry home, and Miss Ledrook a small bandbox, both containing such minor articles of theatrical costume as the lady performers usually carried to and fro every evening. Nicholas would insist upon carrying the basket, and Miss Snevellicci would insist upon carrying it herself, which gave rise to a struggle, in which Nicholas captured the basket and the bandbox likewise. Then Nicholas said, that he wondered what could possibly be inside the basket, and attempted to peep in, whereat Miss Snevellicci screamed, and declared that if she thought he had seen, she was sure she should faint away. This declaration was followed by a similar attempt on the bandbox, and similar demonstrations on the part of Miss Ledrook, and then both ladies vowed that they wouldn’t move a step further until Nicholas had promised that he wouldn’t offer to peep again. At last Nicholas pledged himself to betray no further curiosity, and they walked on: both ladies giggling very much, and declaring that they never had seen such a wicked creature in all their born days--never.\n\nLightening the way with such pleasantry as this, they arrived at the tailor’s house in no time; and here they made quite a little party, there being present besides Mr. Lillyvick and Mrs. Lillyvick, not only Miss Snevellicci’s mama, but her papa also. And an uncommonly fine man Miss Snevellicci’s papa was, with a hook nose, and a white forehead, and curly black hair, and high cheek bones, and altogether quite a handsome face, only a little pimply as though with drinking. He had a very broad chest had Miss Snevellicci’s papa, and he wore a threadbare blue dress-coat buttoned with gilt buttons tight across it; and he no sooner saw Nicholas come into the room, than he whipped the two forefingers of his right hand in between the two centre buttons, and sticking his other arm gracefully a-kimbo seemed to say, ‘Now, here I am, my buck, and what have you got to say to me?’\n\nSuch was, and in such an attitude sat Miss Snevellicci’s papa, who had been in the profession ever since he had first played the ten-year-old imps in the Christmas pantomimes; who could sing a little, dance a little, fence a little, act a little, and do everything a little, but not much; who had been sometimes in the ballet, and sometimes in the chorus, at every theatre in London; who was always selected in virtue of his figure to play the military visitors and the speechless noblemen; who always wore a smart dress, and came on arm-in-arm with a smart lady in short petticoats,--and always did it too with such an air that people in the pit had been several times known to cry out ‘Bravo!’ under the impression that he was somebody. Such was Miss Snevellicci’s papa, upon whom some envious persons cast the imputation that he occasionally beat Miss Snevellicci’s mama, who was still a dancer, with a neat little figure and some remains of good looks; and who now sat, as she danced,--being rather too old for the full glare of the foot-lights,--in the background.\n\nTo these good people Nicholas was presented with much formality. The introduction being completed, Miss Snevellicci’s papa (who was scented with rum-and-water) said that he was delighted to make the acquaintance of a gentleman so highly talented; and furthermore remarked, that there hadn’t been such a hit made--no, not since the first appearance of his friend Mr. Glavormelly, at the Coburg.\n\n‘You have seen him, sir?’ said Miss Snevellicci’s papa.\n\n‘No, really I never did,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘You never saw my friend Glavormelly, sir!’ said Miss Snevellicci’s papa. ‘Then you have never seen acting yet. If he had lived--’\n\n‘Oh, he is dead, is he?’ interrupted Nicholas.\n\n‘He is,’ said Mr. Snevellicci, ‘but he isn’t in Westminster Abbey, more’s the shame. He was a--. Well, no matter. He is gone to that bourne from whence no traveller returns. I hope he is appreciated THERE.’\n\nSo saying Miss Snevellicci’s papa rubbed the tip of his nose with a very yellow silk handkerchief, and gave the company to understand that these recollections overcame him.\n\n‘Well, Mr. Lillyvick,’ said Nicholas, ‘and how are you?’\n\n‘Quite well, sir,’ replied the collector. ‘There is nothing like the married state, sir, depend upon it.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ said Nicholas, laughing.\n\n‘Ah! nothing like it, sir,’ replied Mr. Lillyvick solemnly. ‘How do you think,’ whispered the collector, drawing him aside, ‘how do you think she looks tonight?’\n\n‘As handsome as ever,’ replied Nicholas, glancing at the late Miss Petowker.\n\n‘Why, there’s air about her, sir,’ whispered the collector, ‘that I never saw in anybody. Look at her now she moves to put the kettle on. There! Isn’t it fascination, sir?’\n\n‘You’re a lucky man,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Ha, ha, ha!’ rejoined the collector. ‘No. Do you think I am though, eh? Perhaps I may be, perhaps I may be. I say, I couldn’t have done much better if I had been a young man, could I? You couldn’t have done much better yourself, could you--eh--could you?’ With such inquires, and many more such, Mr. Lillyvick jerked his elbow into Nicholas’s side, and chuckled till his face became quite purple in the attempt to keep down his satisfaction.\n\nBy this time the cloth had been laid under the joint superintendence of all the ladies, upon two tables put together, one being high and narrow, and the other low and broad. There were oysters at the top, sausages at the bottom, a pair of snuffers in the centre, and baked potatoes wherever it was most convenient to put them. Two additional chairs were brought in from the bedroom: Miss Snevellicci sat at the head of the table, and Mr. Lillyvick at the foot; and Nicholas had not only the honour of sitting next Miss Snevellicci, but of having Miss Snevellicci’s mama on his right hand, and Miss Snevellicci’s papa over the way. In short, he was the hero of the feast; and when the table was cleared and something warm introduced, Miss Snevellicci’s papa got up and proposed his health in a speech containing such affecting allusions to his coming departure, that Miss Snevellicci wept, and was compelled to retire into the bedroom.\n\n‘Hush! Don’t take any notice of it,’ said Miss Ledrook, peeping in from the bedroom. ‘Say, when she comes back, that she exerts herself too much.’\n\nMiss Ledrook eked out this speech with so many mysterious nods and frowns before she shut the door again, that a profound silence came upon all the company, during which Miss Snevellicci’s papa looked very big indeed--several sizes larger than life--at everybody in turn, but particularly at Nicholas, and kept on perpetually emptying his tumbler and filling it again, until the ladies returned in a cluster, with Miss Snevellicci among them.\n\n‘You needn’t alarm yourself a bit, Mr. Snevellicci,’ said Mrs. Lillyvick. ‘She is only a little weak and nervous; she has been so ever since the morning.’\n\n‘Oh,’ said Mr. Snevellicci, ‘that’s all, is it?’\n\n‘Oh yes, that’s all. Don’t make a fuss about it,’ cried all the ladies together.\n\nNow this was not exactly the kind of reply suited to Mr. Snevellicci’s importance as a man and a father, so he picked out the unfortunate Mrs Snevellicci, and asked her what the devil she meant by talking to him in that way.\n\n‘Dear me, my dear!’ said Mrs. Snevellicci.\n\n‘Don’t call me your dear, ma’am,’ said Mr. Snevellicci, ‘if you please.’\n\n‘Pray, pa, don’t,’ interposed Miss Snevellicci.\n\n‘Don’t what, my child?’\n\n‘Talk in that way.’\n\n‘Why not?’ said Mr. Snevellicci. ‘I hope you don’t suppose there’s anybody here who is to prevent my talking as I like?’\n\n‘Nobody wants to, pa,’ rejoined his daughter.\n\n‘Nobody would if they did want to,’ said Mr. Snevellicci. ‘I am not ashamed of myself, Snevellicci is my name; I’m to be found in Broad Court, Bow Street, when I’m in town. If I’m not at home, let any man ask for me at the stage-door. Damme, they know me at the stage-door I suppose. Most men have seen my portrait at the cigar shop round the corner. I’ve been mentioned in the newspapers before now, haven’t I? Talk! I’ll tell you what; if I found out that any man had been tampering with the affections of my daughter, I wouldn’t talk. I’d astonish him without talking; that’s my way.’\n\nSo saying, Mr. Snevellicci struck the palm of his left hand three smart blows with his clenched fist; pulled a phantom nose with his right thumb and forefinger, and swallowed another glassful at a draught. ‘That’s my way,’ repeated Mr. Snevellicci.\n\nMost public characters have their failings; and the truth is that Mr Snevellicci was a little addicted to drinking; or, if the whole truth must be told, that he was scarcely ever sober. He knew in his cups three distinct stages of intoxication,--the dignified--the quarrelsome--the amorous. When professionally engaged he never got beyond the dignified; in private circles he went through all three, passing from one to another with a rapidity of transition often rather perplexing to those who had not the honour of his acquaintance.\n\nThus Mr. Snevellicci had no sooner swallowed another glassful than he smiled upon all present in happy forgetfulness of having exhibited symptoms of pugnacity, and proposed ‘The ladies! Bless their hearts!’ in a most vivacious manner.\n\n‘I love ‘em,’ said Mr. Snevellicci, looking round the table, ‘I love ‘em, every one.’\n\n‘Not every one,’ reasoned Mr. Lillyvick, mildly.\n\n‘Yes, every one,’ repeated Mr. Snevellicci.\n\n‘That would include the married ladies, you know,’ said Mr. Lillyvick.\n\n‘I love them too, sir,’ said Mr. Snevellicci.\n\nThe collector looked into the surrounding faces with an aspect of grave astonishment, seeming to say, ‘This is a nice man!’ and appeared a little surprised that Mrs. Lillyvick’s manner yielded no evidences of horror and indignation.\n\n‘One good turn deserves another,’ said Mr. Snevellicci. ‘I love them and they love me.’ And as if this avowal were not made in sufficient disregard and defiance of all moral obligations, what did Mr. Snevellicci do? He winked--winked openly and undisguisedly; winked with his right eye--upon Henrietta Lillyvick!\n\nThe collector fell back in his chair in the intensity of his astonishment. If anybody had winked at her as Henrietta Petowker, it would have been indecorous in the last degree; but as Mrs. Lillyvick! While he thought of it in a cold perspiration, and wondered whether it was possible that he could be dreaming, Mr. Snevellicci repeated the wink, and drinking to Mrs. Lillyvick in dumb show, actually blew her a kiss! Mr. Lillyvick left his chair, walked straight up to the other end of the table, and fell upon him--literally fell upon him--instantaneously. Mr. Lillyvick was no light weight, and consequently when he fell upon Mr. Snevellicci, Mr. Snevellicci fell under the table. Mr. Lillyvick followed him, and the ladies screamed.\n\n‘What is the matter with the men! Are they mad?’ cried Nicholas, diving under the table, dragging up the collector by main force, and thrusting him, all doubled up, into a chair, as if he had been a stuffed figure. ‘What do you mean to do? What do you want to do? What is the matter with you?’\n\nWhile Nicholas raised up the collector, Smike had performed the same office for Mr. Snevellicci, who now regarded his late adversary in tipsy amazement.\n\n‘Look here, sir,’ replied Mr. Lillyvick, pointing to his astonished wife, ‘here is purity and elegance combined, whose feelings have been outraged--violated, sir!’\n\n‘Lor, what nonsense he talks!’ exclaimed Mrs. Lillyvick in answer to the inquiring look of Nicholas. ‘Nobody has said anything to me.’\n\n‘Said, Henrietta!’ cried the collector. ‘Didn’t I see him--’ Mr Lillyvick couldn’t bring himself to utter the word, but he counterfeited the motion of the eye.\n\n‘Well!’ cried Mrs. Lillyvick. ‘Do you suppose nobody is ever to look at me? A pretty thing to be married indeed, if that was law!’\n\n‘You didn’t mind it?’ cried the collector.\n\n‘Mind it!’ repeated Mrs. Lillyvick contemptuously. ‘You ought to go down on your knees and beg everybody’s pardon, that you ought.’\n\n‘Pardon, my dear?’ said the dismayed collector.\n\n‘Yes, and mine first,’ replied Mrs. Lillyvick. ‘Do you suppose I ain’t the best judge of what’s proper and what’s improper?’\n\n‘To be sure,’ cried all the ladies. ‘Do you suppose WE shouldn’t be the first to speak, if there was anything that ought to be taken notice of?’\n\n‘Do you suppose THEY don’t know, sir?’ said Miss Snevellicci’s papa, pulling up his collar, and muttering something about a punching of heads, and being only withheld by considerations of age. With which Miss Snevellicci’s papa looked steadily and sternly at Mr. Lillyvick for some seconds, and then rising deliberately from his chair, kissed the ladies all round, beginning with Mrs. Lillyvick.\n\nThe unhappy collector looked piteously at his wife, as if to see whether there was any one trait of Miss Petowker left in Mrs. Lillyvick, and finding too surely that there was not, begged pardon of all the company with great humility, and sat down such a crest-fallen, dispirited, disenchanted man, that despite all his selfishness and dotage, he was quite an object of compassion.\n\nMiss Snevellicci’s papa being greatly exalted by this triumph, and incontestable proof of his popularity with the fair sex, quickly grew convivial, not to say uproarious; volunteering more than one song of no inconsiderable length, and regaling the social circle between-whiles with recollections of divers splendid women who had been supposed to entertain a passion for himself, several of whom he toasted by name, taking occasion to remark at the same time that if he had been a little more alive to his own interest, he might have been rolling at that moment in his chariot-and-four. These reminiscences appeared to awaken no very torturing pangs in the breast of Mrs. Snevellicci, who was sufficiently occupied in descanting to Nicholas upon the manifold accomplishments and merits of her daughter. Nor was the young lady herself at all behind-hand in displaying her choicest allurements; but these, heightened as they were by the artifices of Miss Ledrook, had no effect whatever in increasing the attentions of Nicholas, who, with the precedent of Miss Squeers still fresh in his memory, steadily resisted every fascination, and placed so strict a guard upon his behaviour that when he had taken his leave the ladies were unanimous in pronouncing him quite a monster of insensibility.\n\nNext day the posters appeared in due course, and the public were informed, in all the colours of the rainbow, and in letters afflicted with every possible variation of spinal deformity, how that Mr. Johnson would have the honour of making his last appearance that evening, and how that an early application for places was requested, in consequence of the extraordinary overflow attendant on his performances,--it being a remarkable fact in theatrical history, but one long since established beyond dispute, that it is a hopeless endeavour to attract people to a theatre unless they can be first brought to believe that they will never get into it.\n\nNicholas was somewhat at a loss, on entering the theatre at night, to account for the unusual perturbation and excitement visible in the countenances of all the company, but he was not long in doubt as to the cause, for before he could make any inquiry respecting it Mr. Crummles approached, and in an agitated tone of voice, informed him that there was a London manager in the boxes.\n\n‘It’s the phenomenon, depend upon it, sir,’ said Crummles, dragging Nicholas to the little hole in the curtain that he might look through at the London manager. ‘I have not the smallest doubt it’s the fame of the phenomenon--that’s the man; him in the great-coat and no shirt-collar. She shall have ten pound a week, Johnson; she shall not appear on the London boards for a farthing less. They shan’t engage her either, unless they engage Mrs. Crummles too--twenty pound a week for the pair; or I’ll tell you what, I’ll throw in myself and the two boys, and they shall have the family for thirty. I can’t say fairer than that. They must take us all, if none of us will go without the others. That’s the way some of the London people do, and it always answers. Thirty pound a week--it’s too cheap, Johnson. It’s dirt cheap.’\n\nNicholas replied, that it certainly was; and Mr. Vincent Crummles taking several huge pinches of snuff to compose his feelings, hurried away to tell Mrs. Crummles that he had quite settled the only terms that could be accepted, and had resolved not to abate one single farthing.\n\nWhen everybody was dressed and the curtain went up, the excitement occasioned by the presence of the London manager increased a thousand-fold. Everybody happened to know that the London manager had come down specially to witness his or her own performance, and all were in a flutter of anxiety and expectation. Some of those who were not on in the first scene, hurried to the wings, and there stretched their necks to have a peep at him; others stole up into the two little private boxes over the stage-doors, and from that position reconnoitred the London manager. Once the London manager was seen to smile--he smiled at the comic countryman’s pretending to catch a blue-bottle, while Mrs Crummles was making her greatest effect. ‘Very good, my fine fellow,’ said Mr. Crummles, shaking his fist at the comic countryman when he came off, ‘you leave this company next Saturday night.’\n\nIn the same way, everybody who was on the stage beheld no audience but one individual; everybody played to the London manager. When Mr. Lenville in a sudden burst of passion called the emperor a miscreant, and then biting his glove, said, ‘But I must dissemble,’ instead of looking gloomily at the boards and so waiting for his cue, as is proper in such cases, he kept his eye fixed upon the London manager. When Miss Bravassa sang her song at her lover, who according to custom stood ready to shake hands with her between the verses, they looked, not at each other, but at the London manager. Mr. Crummles died point blank at him; and when the two guards came in to take the body off after a very hard death, it was seen to open its eyes and glance at the London manager. At length the London manager was discovered to be asleep, and shortly after that he woke up and went away, whereupon all the company fell foul of the unhappy comic countryman, declaring that his buffoonery was the sole cause; and Mr. Crummles said, that he had put up with it a long time, but that he really couldn’t stand it any longer, and therefore would feel obliged by his looking out for another engagement.\n\nAll this was the occasion of much amusement to Nicholas, whose only feeling upon the subject was one of sincere satisfaction that the great man went away before he appeared. He went through his part in the two last pieces as briskly as he could, and having been received with unbounded favour and unprecedented applause--so said the bills for next day, which had been printed an hour or two before--he took Smike’s arm and walked home to bed.\n\nWith the post next morning came a letter from Newman Noggs, very inky, very short, very dirty, very small, and very mysterious, urging Nicholas to return to London instantly; not to lose an instant; to be there that night if possible.\n\n‘I will,’ said Nicholas. ‘Heaven knows I have remained here for the best, and sorely against my own will; but even now I may have dallied too long. What can have happened? Smike, my good fellow, here--take my purse. Put our things together, and pay what little debts we owe--quick, and we shall be in time for the morning coach. I will only tell them that we are going, and will return to you immediately.’\n\nSo saying, he took his hat, and hurrying away to the lodgings of Mr Crummles, applied his hand to the knocker with such hearty good-will, that he awakened that gentleman, who was still in bed, and caused Mr Bulph the pilot to take his morning’s pipe very nearly out of his mouth in the extremity of his surprise.\n\nThe door being opened, Nicholas ran upstairs without any ceremony, and bursting into the darkened sitting-room on the one-pair front, found that the two Master Crummleses had sprung out of the sofa-bedstead and were putting on their clothes with great rapidity, under the impression that it was the middle of the night, and the next house was on fire.\n\nBefore he could undeceive them, Mr. Crummles came down in a flannel gown and nightcap; and to him Nicholas briefly explained that circumstances had occurred which rendered it necessary for him to repair to London immediately.\n\n‘So goodbye,’ said Nicholas; ‘goodbye, goodbye.’\n\nHe was half-way downstairs before Mr. Crummles had sufficiently recovered his surprise to gasp out something about the posters.\n\n‘I can’t help it,’ replied Nicholas. ‘Set whatever I may have earned this week against them, or if that will not repay you, say at once what will. Quick, quick.’\n\n‘We’ll cry quits about that,’ returned Crummles. ‘But can’t we have one last night more?’\n\n‘Not an hour--not a minute,’ replied Nicholas, impatiently.\n\n‘Won’t you stop to say something to Mrs. Crummles?’ asked the manager, following him down to the door.\n\n‘I couldn’t stop if it were to prolong my life a score of years,’ rejoined Nicholas. ‘Here, take my hand, and with it my hearty thanks.--Oh! that I should have been fooling here!’\n\nAccompanying these words with an impatient stamp upon the ground, he tore himself from the manager’s detaining grasp, and darting rapidly down the street was out of sight in an instant.\n\n‘Dear me, dear me,’ said Mr. Crummles, looking wistfully towards the point at which he had just disappeared; ‘if he only acted like that, what a deal of money he’d draw! He should have kept upon this circuit; he’d have been very useful to me. But he don’t know what’s good for him. He is an impetuous youth. Young men are rash, very rash.’\n\nMr. Crummles being in a moralising mood, might possibly have moralised for some minutes longer if he had not mechanically put his hand towards his waistcoat pocket, where he was accustomed to keep his snuff. The absence of any pocket at all in the usual direction, suddenly recalled to his recollection the fact that he had no waistcoat on; and this leading him to a contemplation of the extreme scantiness of his attire, he shut the door abruptly, and retired upstairs with great precipitation.\n\nSmike had made good speed while Nicholas was absent, and with his help everything was soon ready for their departure. They scarcely stopped to take a morsel of breakfast, and in less than half an hour arrived at the coach-office: quite out of breath with the haste they had made to reach it in time. There were yet a few minutes to spare, so, having secured the places, Nicholas hurried into a slopseller’s hard by, and bought Smike a great-coat. It would have been rather large for a substantial yeoman, but the shopman averring (and with considerable truth) that it was a most uncommon fit, Nicholas would have purchased it in his impatience if it had been twice the size.\n\nAs they hurried up to the coach, which was now in the open street and all ready for starting, Nicholas was not a little astonished to find himself suddenly clutched in a close and violent embrace, which nearly took him off his legs; nor was his amazement at all lessened by hearing the voice of Mr. Crummles exclaim, ‘It is he--my friend, my friend!’\n\n‘Bless my heart,’ cried Nicholas, struggling in the manager’s arms, ‘what are you about?’\n\nThe manager made no reply, but strained him to his breast again, exclaiming as he did so, ‘Farewell, my noble, my lion-hearted boy!’\n\nIn fact, Mr. Crummles, who could never lose any opportunity for professional display, had turned out for the express purpose of taking a public farewell of Nicholas; and to render it the more imposing, he was now, to that young gentleman’s most profound annoyance, inflicting upon him a rapid succession of stage embraces, which, as everybody knows, are performed by the embracer’s laying his or her chin on the shoulder of the object of affection, and looking over it. This Mr. Crummles did in the highest style of melodrama, pouring forth at the same time all the most dismal forms of farewell he could think of, out of the stock pieces. Nor was this all, for the elder Master Crummles was going through a similar ceremony with Smike; while Master Percy Crummles, with a very little second-hand camlet cloak, worn theatrically over his left shoulder, stood by, in the attitude of an attendant officer, waiting to convey the two victims to the scaffold.\n\nThe lookers-on laughed very heartily, and as it was as well to put a good face upon the matter, Nicholas laughed too when he had succeeded in disengaging himself; and rescuing the astonished Smike, climbed up to the coach roof after him, and kissed his hand in honour of the absent Mrs. Crummles as they rolled away.\n\n# CHAPTER 31\n\nOf Ralph Nickleby and Newman Noggs, and some wise Precautions, the success or failure of which will appear in the Sequel\nIn blissful unconsciousness that his nephew was hastening at the utmost speed of four good horses towards his sphere of action, and that every passing minute diminished the distance between them, Ralph Nickleby sat that morning occupied in his customary avocations, and yet unable to prevent his thoughts wandering from time to time back to the interview which had taken place between himself and his niece on the previous day. At such intervals, after a few moments of abstraction, Ralph would mutter some peevish interjection, and apply himself with renewed steadiness of purpose to the ledger before him, but again and again the same train of thought came back despite all his efforts to prevent it, confusing him in his calculations, and utterly distracting his attention from the figures over which he bent. At length Ralph laid down his pen, and threw himself back in his chair as though he had made up his mind to allow the obtrusive current of reflection to take its own course, and, by giving it full scope, to rid himself of it effectually.\n\n‘I am not a man to be moved by a pretty face,’ muttered Ralph sternly. ‘There is a grinning skull beneath it, and men like me who look and work below the surface see that, and not its delicate covering. And yet I almost like the girl, or should if she had been less proudly and squeamishly brought up. If the boy were drowned or hanged, and the mother dead, this house should be her home. I wish they were, with all my soul.’\n\nNotwithstanding the deadly hatred which Ralph felt towards Nicholas, and the bitter contempt with which he sneered at poor Mrs Nickleby--notwithstanding the baseness with which he had behaved, and was then behaving, and would behave again if his interest prompted him, towards Kate herself--still there was, strange though it may seem, something humanising and even gentle in his thoughts at that moment. He thought of what his home might be if Kate were there; he placed her in the empty chair, looked upon her, heard her speak; he felt again upon his arm the gentle pressure of the trembling hand; he strewed his costly rooms with the hundred silent tokens of feminine presence and occupation; he came back again to the cold fireside and the silent dreary splendour; and in that one glimpse of a better nature, born as it was in selfish thoughts, the rich man felt himself friendless, childless, and alone. Gold, for the instant, lost its lustre in his eyes, for there were countless treasures of the heart which it could never purchase.\n\nA very slight circumstance was sufficient to banish such reflections from the mind of such a man. As Ralph looked vacantly out across the yard towards the window of the other office, he became suddenly aware of the earnest observation of Newman Noggs, who, with his red nose almost touching the glass, feigned to be mending a pen with a rusty fragment of a knife, but was in reality staring at his employer with a countenance of the closest and most eager scrutiny.\n\nRalph exchanged his dreamy posture for his accustomed business attitude: the face of Newman disappeared, and the train of thought took to flight, all simultaneously, and in an instant.\n\nAfter a few minutes, Ralph rang his bell. Newman answered the summons, and Ralph raised his eyes stealthily to his face, as if he almost feared to read there, a knowledge of his recent thoughts.\n\nThere was not the smallest speculation, however, in the countenance of Newman Noggs. If it be possible to imagine a man, with two eyes in his head, and both wide open, looking in no direction whatever, and seeing nothing, Newman appeared to be that man while Ralph Nickleby regarded him.\n\n‘How now?’ growled Ralph.\n\n‘Oh!’ said Newman, throwing some intelligence into his eyes all at once, and dropping them on his master, ‘I thought you rang.’ With which laconic remark Newman turned round and hobbled away.\n\n‘Stop!’ said Ralph.\n\nNewman stopped; not at all disconcerted.\n\n‘I did ring.’\n\n‘I knew you did.’\n\n‘Then why do you offer to go if you know that?’\n\n‘I thought you rang to say you didn’t ring,’ replied Newman. ‘You often do.’\n\n‘How dare you pry, and peer, and stare at me, sirrah?’ demanded Ralph.\n\n‘Stare!’ cried Newman, ‘at YOU! Ha, ha!’ which was all the explanation Newman deigned to offer.\n\n‘Be careful, sir,’ said Ralph, looking steadily at him. ‘Let me have no drunken fooling here. Do you see this parcel?’\n\n‘It’s big enough,’ rejoined Newman.\n\n‘Carry it into the city; to Cross, in Broad Street, and leave it there--quick. Do you hear?’\n\nNewman gave a dogged kind of nod to express an affirmative reply, and, leaving the room for a few seconds, returned with his hat. Having made various ineffective attempts to fit the parcel (which was some two feet square) into the crown thereof, Newman took it under his arm, and after putting on his fingerless gloves with great precision and nicety, keeping his eyes fixed upon Mr. Ralph Nickleby all the time, he adjusted his hat upon his head with as much care, real or pretended, as if it were a bran-new one of the most expensive quality, and at last departed on his errand.\n\nHe executed his commission with great promptitude and dispatch, only calling at one public-house for half a minute, and even that might be said to be in his way, for he went in at one door and came out at the other; but as he returned and had got so far homewards as the Strand, Newman began to loiter with the uncertain air of a man who has not quite made up his mind whether to halt or go straight forwards. After a very short consideration, the former inclination prevailed, and making towards the point he had had in his mind, Newman knocked a modest double knock, or rather a nervous single one, at Miss La Creevy’s door.\n\nIt was opened by a strange servant, on whom the odd figure of the visitor did not appear to make the most favourable impression possible, inasmuch as she no sooner saw him than she very nearly closed it, and placing herself in the narrow gap, inquired what he wanted. But Newman merely uttering the monosyllable ‘Noggs,’ as if it were some cabalistic word, at sound of which bolts would fly back and doors open, pushed briskly past and gained the door of Miss La Creevy’s sitting-room, before the astonished servant could offer any opposition.\n\n‘Walk in if you please,’ said Miss La Creevy in reply to the sound of Newman’s knuckles; and in he walked accordingly.\n\n‘Bless us!’ cried Miss La Creevy, starting as Newman bolted in; ‘what did you want, sir?’\n\n‘You have forgotten me,’ said Newman, with an inclination of the head. ‘I wonder at that. That nobody should remember me who knew me in other days, is natural enough; but there are few people who, seeing me once, forget me NOW.’ He glanced, as he spoke, at his shabby clothes and paralytic limb, and slightly shook his head.\n\n‘I did forget you, I declare,’ said Miss La Creevy, rising to receive Newman, who met her half-way, ‘and I am ashamed of myself for doing so; for you are a kind, good creature, Mr. Noggs. Sit down and tell me all about Miss Nickleby. Poor dear thing! I haven’t seen her for this many a week.’\n\n‘How’s that?’ asked Newman.\n\n‘Why, the truth is, Mr. Noggs,’ said Miss La Creevy, ‘that I have been out on a visit--the first visit I have made for fifteen years.’\n\n‘That is a long time,’ said Newman, sadly.\n\n‘So it is a very long time to look back upon in years, though, somehow or other, thank Heaven, the solitary days roll away peacefully and happily enough,’ replied the miniature painter. ‘I have a brother, Mr Noggs--the only relation I have--and all that time I never saw him once. Not that we ever quarrelled, but he was apprenticed down in the country, and he got married there; and new ties and affections springing up about him, he forgot a poor little woman like me, as it was very reasonable he should, you know. Don’t suppose that I complain about that, because I always said to myself, “It is very natural; poor dear John is making his way in the world, and has a wife to tell his cares and troubles to, and children now to play about him, so God bless him and them, and send we may all meet together one day where we shall part no more.” But what do you think, Mr. Noggs,’ said the miniature painter, brightening up and clapping her hands, ‘of that very same brother coming up to London at last, and never resting till he found me out; what do you think of his coming here and sitting down in that very chair, and crying like a child because he was so glad to see me--what do you think of his insisting on taking me down all the way into the country to his own house (quite a sumptuous place, Mr. Noggs, with a large garden and I don’t know how many fields, and a man in livery waiting at table, and cows and horses and pigs and I don’t know what besides), and making me stay a whole month, and pressing me to stop there all my life--yes, all my life--and so did his wife, and so did the children--and there were four of them, and one, the eldest girl of all, they--they had named her after me eight good years before, they had indeed. I never was so happy; in all my life I never was!’ The worthy soul hid her face in her handkerchief, and sobbed aloud; for it was the first opportunity she had had of unburdening her heart, and it would have its way.\n\n‘But bless my life,’ said Miss La Creevy, wiping her eyes after a short pause, and cramming her handkerchief into her pocket with great bustle and dispatch; ‘what a foolish creature I must seem to you, Mr. Noggs! I shouldn’t have said anything about it, only I wanted to explain to you how it was I hadn’t seen Miss Nickleby.’\n\n‘Have you seen the old lady?’ asked Newman.\n\n‘You mean Mrs. Nickleby?’ said Miss La Creevy. ‘Then I tell you what, Mr Noggs, if you want to keep in the good books in that quarter, you had better not call her the old lady any more, for I suspect she wouldn’t be best pleased to hear you. Yes, I went there the night before last, but she was quite on the high ropes about something, and was so grand and mysterious, that I couldn’t make anything of her: so, to tell you the truth, I took it into my head to be grand too, and came away in state. I thought she would have come round again before this, but she hasn’t been here.’\n\n‘About Miss Nickleby--’ said Newman.\n\n‘Why, she was here twice while I was away,’ returned Miss La Creevy. ‘I was afraid she mightn’t like to have me calling on her among those great folks in what’s-its-name Place, so I thought I’d wait a day or two, and if I didn’t see her, write.’\n\n‘Ah!’ exclaimed Newman, cracking his fingers.\n\n‘However, I want to hear all the news about them from you,’ said Miss La Creevy. ‘How is the old rough and tough monster of Golden Square? Well, of course; such people always are. I don’t mean how is he in health, but how is he going on: how is he behaving himself?’\n\n‘Damn him!’ cried Newman, dashing his cherished hat on the floor; ‘like a false hound.’\n\n‘Gracious, Mr. Noggs, you quite terrify me!’ exclaimed Miss La Creevy, turning pale.\n\n‘I should have spoilt his features yesterday afternoon if I could have afforded it,’ said Newman, moving restlessly about, and shaking his fist at a portrait of Mr. Canning over the mantelpiece. ‘I was very near it. I was obliged to put my hands in my pockets, and keep ‘em there very tight. I shall do it some day in that little back-parlour, I know I shall. I should have done it before now, if I hadn’t been afraid of making bad worse. I shall double-lock myself in with him and have it out before I die, I’m quite certain of it.’\n\n‘I shall scream if you don’t compose yourself, Mr. Noggs,’ said Miss La Creevy; ‘I’m sure I shan’t be able to help it.’\n\n‘Never mind,’ rejoined Newman, darting violently to and fro. ‘He’s coming up tonight: I wrote to tell him. He little thinks I know; he little thinks I care. Cunning scoundrel! he don’t think that. Not he, not he. Never mind, I’ll thwart him--I, Newman Noggs. Ho, ho, the rascal!’\n\nLashing himself up to an extravagant pitch of fury, Newman Noggs jerked himself about the room with the most eccentric motion ever beheld in a human being: now sparring at the little miniatures on the wall, and now giving himself violent thumps on the head, as if to heighten the delusion, until he sank down in his former seat quite breathless and exhausted.\n\n‘There,’ said Newman, picking up his hat; ‘that’s done me good. Now I’m better, and I’ll tell you all about it.’\n\nIt took some little time to reassure Miss La Creevy, who had been almost frightened out of her senses by this remarkable demonstration; but that done, Newman faithfully related all that had passed in the interview between Kate and her uncle, prefacing his narrative with a statement of his previous suspicions on the subject, and his reasons for forming them; and concluding with a communication of the step he had taken in secretly writing to Nicholas.\n\nThough little Miss La Creevy’s indignation was not so singularly displayed as Newman’s, it was scarcely inferior in violence and intensity. Indeed, if Ralph Nickleby had happened to make his appearance in the room at that moment, there is some doubt whether he would not have found Miss La Creevy a more dangerous opponent than even Newman Noggs himself.\n\n‘God forgive me for saying so,’ said Miss La Creevy, as a wind-up to all her expressions of anger, ‘but I really feel as if I could stick this into him with pleasure.’\n\nIt was not a very awful weapon that Miss La Creevy held, it being in fact nothing more nor less than a black-lead pencil; but discovering her mistake, the little portrait painter exchanged it for a mother-of-pearl fruit knife, wherewith, in proof of her desperate thoughts, she made a lunge as she spoke, which would have scarcely disturbed the crumb of a half-quartern loaf.\n\n‘She won’t stop where she is after tonight,’ said Newman. ‘That’s a comfort.’\n\n‘Stop!’ cried Miss La Creevy, ‘she should have left there, weeks ago.’\n\n‘--If we had known of this,’ rejoined Newman. ‘But we didn’t. Nobody could properly interfere but her mother or brother. The mother’s weak--poor thing--weak. The dear young man will be here tonight.’\n\n‘Heart alive!’ cried Miss La Creevy. ‘He will do something desperate, Mr Noggs, if you tell him all at once.’\n\nNewman left off rubbing his hands, and assumed a thoughtful look.\n\n‘Depend upon it,’ said Miss La Creevy, earnestly, ‘if you are not very careful in breaking out the truth to him, he will do some violence upon his uncle or one of these men that will bring some terrible calamity upon his own head, and grief and sorrow to us all.’\n\n‘I never thought of that,’ rejoined Newman, his countenance falling more and more. ‘I came to ask you to receive his sister in case he brought her here, but--’\n\n‘But this is a matter of much greater importance,’ interrupted Miss La Creevy; ‘that you might have been sure of before you came, but the end of this, nobody can foresee, unless you are very guarded and careful.’\n\n‘What CAN I do?’ cried Newman, scratching his head with an air of great vexation and perplexity. ‘If he was to talk of pistoling ‘em all, I should be obliged to say, “Certainly--serve ‘em right.”’\n\nMiss La Creevy could not suppress a small shriek on hearing this, and instantly set about extorting a solemn pledge from Newman that he would use his utmost endeavours to pacify the wrath of Nicholas; which, after some demur, was conceded. They then consulted together on the safest and surest mode of communicating to him the circumstances which had rendered his presence necessary.\n\n‘He must have time to cool before he can possibly do anything,’ said Miss La Creevy. ‘That is of the greatest consequence. He must not be told until late at night.’\n\n‘But he’ll be in town between six and seven this evening,’ replied Newman. ‘I can’t keep it from him when he asks me.’\n\n‘Then you must go out, Mr. Noggs,’ said Miss La Creevy. ‘You can easily have been kept away by business, and must not return till nearly midnight.’\n\n‘Then he will come straight here,’ retorted Newman.\n\n‘So I suppose,’ observed Miss La Creevy; ‘but he won’t find me at home, for I’ll go straight to the city the instant you leave me, make up matters with Mrs. Nickleby, and take her away to the theatre, so that he may not even know where his sister lives.’\n\nUpon further discussion, this appeared the safest and most feasible mode of proceeding that could possibly be adopted. Therefore it was finally determined that matters should be so arranged, and Newman, after listening to many supplementary cautions and entreaties, took his leave of Miss La Creevy and trudged back to Golden Square; ruminating as he went upon a vast number of possibilities and impossibilities which crowded upon his brain, and arose out of the conversation that had just terminated.\n\n# CHAPTER 32\n\nRelating chiefly to some remarkable Conversation, and some remarkable Proceedings to which it gives rise\n‘London at last!’ cried Nicholas, throwing back his greatcoat and rousing Smike from a long nap. ‘It seemed to me as though we should never reach it.’\n\n‘And yet you came along at a tidy pace too,’ observed the coachman, looking over his shoulder at Nicholas with no very pleasant expression of countenance.\n\n‘Ay, I know that,’ was the reply; ‘but I have been very anxious to be at my journey’s end, and that makes the way seem long.’\n\n‘Well,’ remarked the coachman, ‘if the way seemed long with such cattle as you’ve sat behind, you MUST have been most uncommon anxious;’ and so saying, he let out his whip-lash and touched up a little boy on the calves of his legs by way of emphasis.\n\nThey rattled on through the noisy, bustling, crowded street of London, now displaying long double rows of brightly-burning lamps, dotted here and there with the chemists’ glaring lights, and illuminated besides with the brilliant flood that streamed from the windows of the shops, where sparkling jewellery, silks and velvets of the richest colours, the most inviting delicacies, and most sumptuous articles of luxurious ornament, succeeded each other in rich and glittering profusion. Streams of people apparently without end poured on and on, jostling each other in the crowd and hurrying forward, scarcely seeming to notice the riches that surrounded them on every side; while vehicles of all shapes and makes, mingled up together in one moving mass, like running water, lent their ceaseless roar to swell the noise and tumult.\n\nAs they dashed by the quickly-changing and ever-varying objects, it was curious to observe in what a strange procession they passed before the eye. Emporiums of splendid dresses, the materials brought from every quarter of the world; tempting stores of everything to stimulate and pamper the sated appetite and give new relish to the oft-repeated feast; vessels of burnished gold and silver, wrought into every exquisite form of vase, and dish, and goblet; guns, swords, pistols, and patent engines of destruction; screws and irons for the crooked, clothes for the newly-born, drugs for the sick, coffins for the dead, and churchyards for the buried--all these jumbled each with the other and flocking side by side, seemed to flit by in motley dance like the fantastic groups of the old Dutch painter, and with the same stern moral for the unheeding restless crowd.\n\nNor were there wanting objects in the crowd itself to give new point and purpose to the shifting scene. The rags of the squalid ballad-singer fluttered in the rich light that showed the goldsmith’s treasures, pale and pinched-up faces hovered about the windows where was tempting food, hungry eyes wandered over the profusion guarded by one thin sheet of brittle glass--an iron wall to them; half-naked shivering figures stopped to gaze at Chinese shawls and golden stuffs of India. There was a christening party at the largest coffin-maker’s and a funeral hatchment had stopped some great improvements in the bravest mansion. Life and death went hand in hand; wealth and poverty stood side by side; repletion and starvation laid them down together.\n\nBut it was London; and the old country lady inside, who had put her head out of the coach-window a mile or two this side Kingston, and cried out to the driver that she was sure he must have passed it and forgotten to set her down, was satisfied at last.\n\nNicholas engaged beds for himself and Smike at the inn where the coach stopped, and repaired, without the delay of another moment, to the lodgings of Newman Noggs; for his anxiety and impatience had increased with every succeeding minute, and were almost beyond control.\n\nThere was a fire in Newman’s garret; and a candle had been left burning; the floor was cleanly swept, the room was as comfortably arranged as such a room could be, and meat and drink were placed in order upon the table. Everything bespoke the affectionate care and attention of Newman Noggs, but Newman himself was not there.\n\n‘Do you know what time he will be home?’ inquired Nicholas, tapping at the door of Newman’s front neighbour.\n\n‘Ah, Mr. Johnson!’ said Crowl, presenting himself. ‘Welcome, sir. How well you’re looking! I never could have believed--’\n\n‘Pardon me,’ interposed Nicholas. ‘My question--I am extremely anxious to know.’\n\n‘Why, he has a troublesome affair of business,’ replied Crowl, ‘and will not be home before twelve o’clock. He was very unwilling to go, I can tell you, but there was no help for it. However, he left word that you were to make yourself comfortable till he came back, and that I was to entertain you, which I shall be very glad to do.’\n\nIn proof of his extreme readiness to exert himself for the general entertainment, Mr. Crowl drew a chair to the table as he spoke, and helping himself plentifully to the cold meat, invited Nicholas and Smike to follow his example.\n\nDisappointed and uneasy, Nicholas could touch no food, so, after he had seen Smike comfortably established at the table, he walked out (despite a great many dissuasions uttered by Mr. Crowl with his mouth full), and left Smike to detain Newman in case he returned first.\n\nAs Miss La Creevy had anticipated, Nicholas betook himself straight to her house. Finding her from home, he debated within himself for some time whether he should go to his mother’s residence, and so compromise her with Ralph Nickleby. Fully persuaded, however, that Newman would not have solicited him to return unless there was some strong reason which required his presence at home, he resolved to go there, and hastened eastwards with all speed.\n\nMrs. Nickleby would not be at home, the girl said, until past twelve, or later. She believed Miss Nickleby was well, but she didn’t live at home now, nor did she come home except very seldom. She couldn’t say where she was stopping, but it was not at Madame Mantalini’s. She was sure of that.\n\nWith his heart beating violently, and apprehending he knew not what disaster, Nicholas returned to where he had left Smike. Newman had not been home. He wouldn’t be, till twelve o’clock; there was no chance of it. Was there no possibility of sending to fetch him if it were only for an instant, or forwarding to him one line of writing to which he might return a verbal reply? That was quite impracticable. He was not at Golden Square, and probably had been sent to execute some commission at a distance.\n\nNicholas tried to remain quietly where he was, but he felt so nervous and excited that he could not sit still. He seemed to be losing time unless he was moving. It was an absurd fancy, he knew, but he was wholly unable to resist it. So, he took up his hat and rambled out again.\n\nHe strolled westward this time, pacing the long streets with hurried footsteps, and agitated by a thousand misgivings and apprehensions which he could not overcome. He passed into Hyde Park, now silent and deserted, and increased his rate of walking as if in the hope of leaving his thoughts behind. They crowded upon him more thickly, however, now there were no passing objects to attract his attention; and the one idea was always uppermost, that some stroke of ill-fortune must have occurred so calamitous in its nature that all were fearful of disclosing it to him. The old question arose again and again--What could it be? Nicholas walked till he was weary, but was not one bit the wiser; and indeed he came out of the Park at last a great deal more confused and perplexed than when he went in.\n\nHe had taken scarcely anything to eat or drink since early in the morning, and felt quite worn out and exhausted. As he returned languidly towards the point from which he had started, along one of the thoroughfares which lie between Park Lane and Bond Street, he passed a handsome hotel, before which he stopped mechanically.\n\n‘An expensive place, I dare say,’ thought Nicholas; ‘but a pint of wine and a biscuit are no great debauch wherever they are had. And yet I don’t know.’\n\nHe walked on a few steps, but looking wistfully down the long vista of gas-lamps before him, and thinking how long it would take to reach the end of it and being besides in that kind of mood in which a man is most disposed to yield to his first impulse--and being, besides, strongly attracted to the hotel, in part by curiosity, and in part by some odd mixture of feelings which he would have been troubled to define--Nicholas turned back again, and walked into the coffee-room.\n\nIt was very handsomely furnished. The walls were ornamented with the choicest specimens of French paper, enriched with a gilded cornice of elegant design. The floor was covered with a rich carpet; and two superb mirrors, one above the chimneypiece and one at the opposite end of the room reaching from floor to ceiling, multiplied the other beauties and added new ones of their own to enhance the general effect. There was a rather noisy party of four gentlemen in a box by the fire-place, and only two other persons present--both elderly gentlemen, and both alone.\n\nObserving all this in the first comprehensive glance with which a stranger surveys a place that is new to him, Nicholas sat himself down in the box next to the noisy party, with his back towards them, and postponing his order for a pint of claret until such time as the waiter and one of the elderly gentlemen should have settled a disputed question relative to the price of an item in the bill of fare, took up a newspaper and began to read.\n\nHe had not read twenty lines, and was in truth himself dozing, when he was startled by the mention of his sister’s name. ‘Little Kate Nickleby’ were the words that caught his ear. He raised his head in amazement, and as he did so, saw by the reflection in the opposite glass, that two of the party behind him had risen and were standing before the fire. ‘It must have come from one of them,’ thought Nicholas. He waited to hear more with a countenance of some indignation, for the tone of speech had been anything but respectful, and the appearance of the individual whom he presumed to have been the speaker was coarse and swaggering.\n\nThis person--so Nicholas observed in the same glance at the mirror which had enabled him to see his face--was standing with his back to the fire conversing with a younger man, who stood with his back to the company, wore his hat, and was adjusting his shirt-collar by the aid of the glass. They spoke in whispers, now and then bursting into a loud laugh, but Nicholas could catch no repetition of the words, nor anything sounding at all like the words, which had attracted his attention.\n\nAt length the two resumed their seats, and more wine being ordered, the party grew louder in their mirth. Still there was no reference made to anybody with whom he was acquainted, and Nicholas became persuaded that his excited fancy had either imagined the sounds altogether, or converted some other words into the name which had been so much in his thoughts.\n\n‘It is remarkable too,’ thought Nicholas: ‘if it had been “Kate” or “Kate Nickleby,” I should not have been so much surprised: but “little Kate Nickleby!”’\n\nThe wine coming at the moment prevented his finishing the sentence. He swallowed a glassful and took up the paper again. At that instant--\n\n‘Little Kate Nickleby!’ cried the voice behind him.\n\n‘I was right,’ muttered Nicholas as the paper fell from his hand. ‘And it was the man I supposed.’\n\n‘As there was a proper objection to drinking her in heel-taps,’ said the voice, ‘we’ll give her the first glass in the new magnum. Little Kate Nickleby!’\n\n‘Little Kate Nickleby,’ cried the other three. And the glasses were set down empty.\n\nKeenly alive to the tone and manner of this slight and careless mention of his sister’s name in a public place, Nicholas fired at once; but he kept himself quiet by a great effort, and did not even turn his head.\n\n‘The jade!’ said the same voice which had spoken before. ‘She’s a true Nickleby--a worthy imitator of her old uncle Ralph--she hangs back to be more sought after--so does he; nothing to be got out of Ralph unless you follow him up, and then the money comes doubly welcome, and the bargain doubly hard, for you’re impatient and he isn’t. Oh! infernal cunning.’\n\n‘Infernal cunning,’ echoed two voices.\n\nNicholas was in a perfect agony as the two elderly gentlemen opposite, rose one after the other and went away, lest they should be the means of his losing one word of what was said. But the conversation was suspended as they withdrew, and resumed with even greater freedom when they had left the room.\n\n‘I am afraid,’ said the younger gentleman, ‘that the old woman has grown jea-a-lous, and locked her up. Upon my soul it looks like it.’\n\n‘If they quarrel and little Nickleby goes home to her mother, so much the better,’ said the first. ‘I can do anything with the old lady. She’ll believe anything I tell her.’\n\n‘Egad that’s true,’ returned the other voice. ‘Ha, ha, ha! Poor deyvle!’\n\nThe laugh was taken up by the two voices which always came in together, and became general at Mrs. Nickleby’s expense. Nicholas turned burning hot with rage, but he commanded himself for the moment, and waited to hear more.\n\nWhat he heard need not be repeated here. Suffice it that as the wine went round he heard enough to acquaint him with the characters and designs of those whose conversation he overhead; to possess him with the full extent of Ralph’s villainy, and the real reason of his own presence being required in London. He heard all this and more. He heard his sister’s sufferings derided, and her virtuous conduct jeered at and brutally misconstrued; he heard her name bandied from mouth to mouth, and herself made the subject of coarse and insolent wagers, free speech, and licentious jesting.\n\nThe man who had spoken first, led the conversation, and indeed almost engrossed it, being only stimulated from time to time by some slight observation from one or other of his companions. To him then Nicholas addressed himself when he was sufficiently composed to stand before the party, and force the words from his parched and scorching throat.\n\n‘Let me have a word with you, sir,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘With me, sir?’ retorted Sir Mulberry Hawk, eyeing him in disdainful surprise.\n\n‘I said with you,’ replied Nicholas, speaking with great difficulty, for his passion choked him.\n\n‘A mysterious stranger, upon my soul!’ exclaimed Sir Mulberry, raising his wine-glass to his lips, and looking round upon his friends.\n\n‘Will you step apart with me for a few minutes, or do you refuse?’ said Nicholas sternly.\n\nSir Mulberry merely paused in the act of drinking, and bade him either name his business or leave the table.\n\nNicholas drew a card from his pocket, and threw it before him.\n\n‘There, sir,’ said Nicholas; ‘my business you will guess.’\n\nA momentary expression of astonishment, not unmixed with some confusion, appeared in the face of Sir Mulberry as he read the name; but he subdued it in an instant, and tossing the card to Lord Verisopht, who sat opposite, drew a toothpick from a glass before him, and very leisurely applied it to his mouth.\n\n‘Your name and address?’ said Nicholas, turning paler as his passion kindled.\n\n‘I shall give you neither,’ replied Sir Mulberry.\n\n‘If there is a gentleman in this party,’ said Nicholas, looking round and scarcely able to make his white lips form the words, ‘he will acquaint me with the name and residence of this man.’\n\nThere was a dead silence.\n\n‘I am the brother of the young lady who has been the subject of conversation here,’ said Nicholas. ‘I denounce this person as a liar, and impeach him as a coward. If he has a friend here, he will save him the disgrace of the paltry attempt to conceal his name--and utterly useless one--for I will find it out, nor leave him until I have.’\n\nSir Mulberry looked at him contemptuously, and, addressing his companions, said--\n\n‘Let the fellow talk, I have nothing serious to say to boys of his station; and his pretty sister shall save him a broken head, if he talks till midnight.’\n\n‘You are a base and spiritless scoundrel!’ said Nicholas, ‘and shall be proclaimed so to the world. I WILL know you; I will follow you home if you walk the streets till morning.’\n\nSir Mulberry’s hand involuntarily closed upon the decanter, and he seemed for an instant about to launch it at the head of his challenger. But he only filled his glass, and laughed in derision.\n\nNicholas sat himself down, directly opposite to the party, and, summoning the waiter, paid his bill.\n\n‘Do you know that person’s name?’ he inquired of the man in an audible voice; pointing out Sir Mulberry as he put the question.\n\nSir Mulberry laughed again, and the two voices which had always spoken together, echoed the laugh; but rather feebly.\n\n‘That gentleman, sir?’ replied the waiter, who, no doubt, knew his cue, and answered with just as little respect, and just as much impertinence as he could safely show: ‘no, sir, I do not, sir.’\n\n‘Here, you sir,’ cried Sir Mulberry, as the man was retiring; ‘do you know THAT person’s name?’\n\n‘Name, sir? No, sir.’\n\n‘Then you’ll find it there,’ said Sir Mulberry, throwing Nicholas’s card towards him; ‘and when you have made yourself master of it, put that piece of pasteboard in the fire--do you hear me?’\n\nThe man grinned, and, looking doubtfully at Nicholas, compromised the matter by sticking the card in the chimney-glass. Having done this, he retired.\n\nNicholas folded his arms, and biting his lip, sat perfectly quiet; sufficiently expressing by his manner, however, a firm determination to carry his threat of following Sir Mulberry home, into steady execution.\n\nIt was evident from the tone in which the younger member of the party appeared to remonstrate with his friend, that he objected to this course of proceeding, and urged him to comply with the request which Nicholas had made. Sir Mulberry, however, who was not quite sober, and who was in a sullen and dogged state of obstinacy, soon silenced the representations of his weak young friend, and further seemed--as if to save himself from a repetition of them--to insist on being left alone. However this might have been, the young gentleman and the two who had always spoken together, actually rose to go after a short interval, and presently retired, leaving their friend alone with Nicholas.\n\nIt will be very readily supposed that to one in the condition of Nicholas, the minutes appeared to move with leaden wings indeed, and that their progress did not seem the more rapid from the monotonous ticking of a French clock, or the shrill sound of its little bell which told the quarters. But there he sat; and in his old seat on the opposite side of the room reclined Sir Mulberry Hawk, with his legs upon the cushion, and his handkerchief thrown negligently over his knees: finishing his magnum of claret with the utmost coolness and indifference.\n\nThus they remained in perfect silence for upwards of an hour--Nicholas would have thought for three hours at least, but that the little bell had only gone four times. Twice or thrice he looked angrily and impatiently round; but there was Sir Mulberry in the same attitude, putting his glass to his lips from time to time, and looking vacantly at the wall, as if he were wholly ignorant of the presence of any living person.\n\nAt length he yawned, stretched himself, and rose; walked coolly to the glass, and having surveyed himself therein, turned round and honoured Nicholas with a long and contemptuous stare. Nicholas stared again with right good-will; Sir Mulberry shrugged his shoulders, smiled slightly, rang the bell, and ordered the waiter to help him on with his greatcoat.\n\nThe man did so, and held the door open.\n\n‘Don’t wait,’ said Sir Mulberry; and they were alone again.\n\nSir Mulberry took several turns up and down the room, whistling carelessly all the time; stopped to finish the last glass of claret which he had poured out a few minutes before, walked again, put on his hat, adjusted it by the glass, drew on his gloves, and, at last, walked slowly out. Nicholas, who had been fuming and chafing until he was nearly wild, darted from his seat, and followed him: so closely, that before the door had swung upon its hinges after Sir Mulberry’s passing out, they stood side by side in the street together.\n\nThere was a private cabriolet in waiting; the groom opened the apron, and jumped out to the horse’s head.\n\n‘Will you make yourself known to me?’ asked Nicholas in a suppressed voice.\n\n‘No,’ replied the other fiercely, and confirming the refusal with an oath. ‘No.’\n\n‘If you trust to your horse’s speed, you will find yourself mistaken,’ said Nicholas. ‘I will accompany you. By Heaven I will, if I hang on to the foot-board.’\n\n‘You shall be horsewhipped if you do,’ returned Sir Mulberry.\n\n‘You are a villain,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘You are an errand-boy for aught I know,’ said Sir Mulberry Hawk.\n\n‘I am the son of a country gentleman,’ returned Nicholas, ‘your equal in birth and education, and your superior I trust in everything besides. I tell you again, Miss Nickleby is my sister. Will you or will you not answer for your unmanly and brutal conduct?’\n\n‘To a proper champion--yes. To you--no,’ returned Sir Mulberry, taking the reins in his hand. ‘Stand out of the way, dog. William, let go her head.’\n\n‘You had better not,’ cried Nicholas, springing on the step as Sir Mulberry jumped in, and catching at the reins. ‘He has no command over the horse, mind. You shall not go--you shall not, I swear--till you have told me who you are.’\n\nThe groom hesitated, for the mare, who was a high-spirited animal and thorough-bred, plunged so violently that he could scarcely hold her.\n\n‘Leave go, I tell you!’ thundered his master.\n\nThe man obeyed. The animal reared and plunged as though it would dash the carriage into a thousand pieces, but Nicholas, blind to all sense of danger, and conscious of nothing but his fury, still maintained his place and his hold upon the reins.\n\n‘Will you unclasp your hand?’\n\n‘Will you tell me who you are?’\n\n‘No!’\n‘No!’\n\nIn less time than the quickest tongue could tell it, these words were exchanged, and Sir Mulberry shortening his whip, applied it furiously to the head and shoulders of Nicholas. It was broken in the struggle; Nicholas gained the heavy handle, and with it laid open one side of his antagonist’s face from the eye to the lip. He saw the gash; knew that the mare had darted off at a wild mad gallop; a hundred lights danced in his eyes, and he felt himself flung violently upon the ground.\n\nHe was giddy and sick, but staggered to his feet directly, roused by the loud shouts of the men who were tearing up the street, and screaming to those ahead to clear the way. He was conscious of a torrent of people rushing quickly by--looking up, could discern the cabriolet whirled along the foot-pavement with frightful rapidity--then heard a loud cry, the smashing of some heavy body, and the breaking of glass--and then the crowd closed in in the distance, and he could see or hear no more.\n\nThe general attention had been entirely directed from himself to the person in the carriage, and he was quite alone. Rightly judging that under such circumstances it would be madness to follow, he turned down a bye-street in search of the nearest coach-stand, finding after a minute or two that he was reeling like a drunken man, and aware for the first time of a stream of blood that was trickling down his face and breast.\n\n# CHAPTER 33\n\nIn which Mr. Ralph Nickleby is relieved, by a very expeditious Process, from all Commerce with his Relations\nSmike and Newman Noggs, who in his impatience had returned home long before the time agreed upon, sat before the fire, listening anxiously to every footstep on the stairs, and the slightest sound that stirred within the house, for the approach of Nicholas. Time had worn on, and it was growing late. He had promised to be back in an hour; and his prolonged absence began to excite considerable alarm in the minds of both, as was abundantly testified by the blank looks they cast upon each other at every new disappointment.\n\nAt length a coach was heard to stop, and Newman ran out to light Nicholas up the stairs. Beholding him in the trim described at the conclusion of the last chapter, he stood aghast in wonder and consternation.\n\n‘Don’t be alarmed,’ said Nicholas, hurrying him back into the room. ‘There is no harm done, beyond what a basin of water can repair.’\n\n‘No harm!’ cried Newman, passing his hands hastily over the back and arms of Nicholas, as if to assure himself that he had broken no bones. ‘What have you been doing?’\n\n‘I know all,’ interrupted Nicholas; ‘I have heard a part, and guessed the rest. But before I remove one jot of these stains, I must hear the whole from you. You see I am collected. My resolution is taken. Now, my good friend, speak out; for the time for any palliation or concealment is past, and nothing will avail Ralph Nickleby now.’\n\n‘Your dress is torn in several places; you walk lame, and I am sure you are suffering pain,’ said Newman. ‘Let me see to your hurts first.’\n\n‘I have no hurts to see to, beyond a little soreness and stiffness that will soon pass off,’ said Nicholas, seating himself with some difficulty. ‘But if I had fractured every limb, and still preserved my senses, you should not bandage one till you had told me what I have the right to know. Come,’ said Nicholas, giving his hand to Noggs. ‘You had a sister of your own, you told me once, who died before you fell into misfortune. Now think of her, and tell me, Newman.’\n\n‘Yes, I will, I will,’ said Noggs. ‘I’ll tell you the whole truth.’\n\nNewman did so. Nicholas nodded his head from time to time, as it corroborated the particulars he had already gleaned; but he fixed his eyes upon the fire, and did not look round once.\n\nHis recital ended, Newman insisted upon his young friend’s stripping off his coat and allowing whatever injuries he had received to be properly tended. Nicholas, after some opposition, at length consented, and, while some pretty severe bruises on his arms and shoulders were being rubbed with oil and vinegar, and various other efficacious remedies which Newman borrowed from the different lodgers, related in what manner they had been received. The recital made a strong impression on the warm imagination of Newman; for when Nicholas came to the violent part of the quarrel, he rubbed so hard, as to occasion him the most exquisite pain, which he would not have exhibited, however, for the world, it being perfectly clear that, for the moment, Newman was operating on Sir Mulberry Hawk, and had quite lost sight of his real patient.\n\nThis martyrdom over, Nicholas arranged with Newman that while he was otherwise occupied next morning, arrangements should be made for his mother’s immediately quitting her present residence, and also for dispatching Miss La Creevy to break the intelligence to her. He then wrapped himself in Smike’s greatcoat, and repaired to the inn where they were to pass the night, and where (after writing a few lines to Ralph, the delivery of which was to be intrusted to Newman next day), he endeavoured to obtain the repose of which he stood so much in need.\n\nDrunken men, they say, may roll down precipices, and be quite unconscious of any serious personal inconvenience when their reason returns. The remark may possibly apply to injuries received in other kinds of violent excitement: certain it is, that although Nicholas experienced some pain on first awakening next morning, he sprung out of bed as the clock struck seven, with very little difficulty, and was soon as much on the alert as if nothing had occurred.\n\nMerely looking into Smike’s room, and telling him that Newman Noggs would call for him very shortly, Nicholas descended into the street, and calling a hackney coach, bade the man drive to Mrs. Wititterly’s, according to the direction which Newman had given him on the previous night.\n\nIt wanted a quarter to eight when they reached Cadogan Place. Nicholas began to fear that no one might be stirring at that early hour, when he was relieved by the sight of a female servant, employed in cleaning the door-steps. By this functionary he was referred to the doubtful page, who appeared with dishevelled hair and a very warm and glossy face, as of a page who had just got out of bed.\n\nBy this young gentleman he was informed that Miss Nickleby was then taking her morning’s walk in the gardens before the house. On the question being propounded whether he could go and find her, the page desponded and thought not; but being stimulated with a shilling, the page grew sanguine and thought he could.\n\n‘Say to Miss Nickleby that her brother is here, and in great haste to see her,’ said Nicholas.\n\nThe plated buttons disappeared with an alacrity most unusual to them, and Nicholas paced the room in a state of feverish agitation which made the delay even of a minute insupportable. He soon heard a light footstep which he well knew, and before he could advance to meet her, Kate had fallen on his neck and burst into tears.\n\n‘My darling girl,’ said Nicholas as he embraced her. ‘How pale you are!’\n\n‘I have been so unhappy here, dear brother,’ sobbed poor Kate; ‘so very, very miserable. Do not leave me here, dear Nicholas, or I shall die of a broken heart.’\n\n‘I will leave you nowhere,’ answered Nicholas--‘never again, Kate,’ he cried, moved in spite of himself as he folded her to his heart. ‘Tell me that I acted for the best. Tell me that we parted because I feared to bring misfortune on your head; that it was a trial to me no less than to yourself, and that if I did wrong it was in ignorance of the world and unknowingly.’\n\n‘Why should I tell you what we know so well?’ returned Kate soothingly. ‘Nicholas--dear Nicholas--how can you give way thus?’\n\n‘It is such bitter reproach to me to know what you have undergone,’ returned her brother; ‘to see you so much altered, and yet so kind and patient--God!’ cried Nicholas, clenching his fist and suddenly changing his tone and manner, ‘it sets my whole blood on fire again. You must leave here with me directly; you should not have slept here last night, but that I knew all this too late. To whom can I speak, before we drive away?’\n\nThis question was most opportunely put, for at that instant Mr Wititterly walked in, and to him Kate introduced her brother, who at once announced his purpose, and the impossibility of deferring it.\n\n‘The quarter’s notice,’ said Mr. Wititterly, with the gravity of a man on the right side, ‘is not yet half expired. Therefore--’\n\n‘Therefore,’ interposed Nicholas, ‘the quarter’s salary must be lost, sir. You will excuse this extreme haste, but circumstances require that I should immediately remove my sister, and I have not a moment’s time to lose. Whatever she brought here I will send for, if you will allow me, in the course of the day.’\n\nMr. Wititterly bowed, but offered no opposition to Kate’s immediate departure; with which, indeed, he was rather gratified than otherwise, Sir Tumley Snuffim having given it as his opinion, that she rather disagreed with Mrs. Wititterly’s constitution.\n\n‘With regard to the trifle of salary that is due,’ said Mr. Wititterly, ‘I will’--here he was interrupted by a violent fit of coughing--‘I will--owe it to Miss Nickleby.’\n\nMr. Wititterly, it should be observed, was accustomed to owe small accounts, and to leave them owing. All men have some little pleasant way of their own; and this was Mr. Wititterly’s.\n\n‘If you please,’ said Nicholas. And once more offering a hurried apology for so sudden a departure, he hurried Kate into the vehicle, and bade the man drive with all speed into the city.\n\nTo the city they went accordingly, with all the speed the hackney coach could make; and as the horses happened to live at Whitechapel and to be in the habit of taking their breakfast there, when they breakfasted at all, they performed the journey with greater expedition than could reasonably have been expected.\n\nNicholas sent Kate upstairs a few minutes before him, that his unlooked-for appearance might not alarm his mother, and when the way had been paved, presented himself with much duty and affection. Newman had not been idle, for there was a little cart at the door, and the effects were hurrying out already.\n\nNow, Mrs. Nickleby was not the sort of person to be told anything in a hurry, or rather to comprehend anything of peculiar delicacy or importance on a short notice. Wherefore, although the good lady had been subjected to a full hour’s preparation by little Miss La Creevy, and was now addressed in most lucid terms both by Nicholas and his sister, she was in a state of singular bewilderment and confusion, and could by no means be made to comprehend the necessity of such hurried proceedings.\n\n‘Why don’t you ask your uncle, my dear Nicholas, what he can possibly mean by it?’ said Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘My dear mother,’ returned Nicholas, ‘the time for talking has gone by. There is but one step to take, and that is to cast him off with the scorn and indignation he deserves. Your own honour and good name demand that, after the discovery of his vile proceedings, you should not be beholden to him one hour, even for the shelter of these bare walls.’\n\n‘To be sure,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, crying bitterly, ‘he is a brute, a monster; and the walls are very bare, and want painting too, and I have had this ceiling whitewashed at the expense of eighteen-pence, which is a very distressing thing, considering that it is so much gone into your uncle’s pocket. I never could have believed it--never.’\n\n‘Nor I, nor anybody else,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Lord bless my life!’ exclaimed Mrs. Nickleby. ‘To think that that Sir Mulberry Hawk should be such an abandoned wretch as Miss La Creevy says he is, Nicholas, my dear; when I was congratulating myself every day on his being an admirer of our dear Kate’s, and thinking what a thing it would be for the family if he was to become connected with us, and use his interest to get you some profitable government place. There are very good places to be got about the court, I know; for a friend of ours (Miss Cropley, at Exeter, my dear Kate, you recollect), he had one, and I know that it was the chief part of his duty to wear silk stockings, and a bag wig like a black watch-pocket; and to think that it should come to this after all--oh, dear, dear, it’s enough to kill one, that it is!’ With which expressions of sorrow, Mrs. Nickleby gave fresh vent to her grief, and wept piteously.\n\nAs Nicholas and his sister were by this time compelled to superintend the removal of the few articles of furniture, Miss La Creevy devoted herself to the consolation of the matron, and observed with great kindness of manner that she must really make an effort, and cheer up.\n\n‘Oh I dare say, Miss La Creevy,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby, with a petulance not unnatural in her unhappy circumstances, ‘it’s very easy to say cheer up, but if you had as many occasions to cheer up as I have had--and there,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, stopping short. ‘Think of Mr. Pyke and Mr Pluck, two of the most perfect gentlemen that ever lived, what am I too say to them--what can I say to them? Why, if I was to say to them, “I’m told your friend Sir Mulberry is a base wretch,” they’d laugh at me.’\n\n‘They will laugh no more at us, I take it,’ said Nicholas, advancing. ‘Come, mother, there is a coach at the door, and until Monday, at all events, we will return to our old quarters.’\n\n‘--Where everything is ready, and a hearty welcome into the bargain,’ added Miss La Creevy. ‘Now, let me go with you downstairs.’\n\nBut Mrs. Nickleby was not to be so easily moved, for first she insisted on going upstairs to see that nothing had been left, and then on going downstairs to see that everything had been taken away; and when she was getting into the coach she had a vision of a forgotten coffee-pot on the back-kitchen hob, and after she was shut in, a dismal recollection of a green umbrella behind some unknown door. At last Nicholas, in a condition of absolute despair, ordered the coachman to drive away, and in the unexpected jerk of a sudden starting, Mrs. Nickleby lost a shilling among the straw, which fortunately confined her attention to the coach until it was too late to remember anything else.\n\nHaving seen everything safely out, discharged the servant, and locked the door, Nicholas jumped into a cabriolet and drove to a bye place near Golden Square where he had appointed to meet Noggs; and so quickly had everything been done, that it was barely half-past nine when he reached the place of meeting.\n\n‘Here is the letter for Ralph,’ said Nicholas, ‘and here the key. When you come to me this evening, not a word of last night. Ill news travels fast, and they will know it soon enough. Have you heard if he was much hurt?’\n\nNewman shook his head.\n\n‘I will ascertain that myself without loss of time,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘You had better take some rest,’ returned Newman. ‘You are fevered and ill.’\n\nNicholas waved his hand carelessly, and concealing the indisposition he really felt, now that the excitement which had sustained him was over, took a hurried farewell of Newman Noggs, and left him.\n\nNewman was not three minutes’ walk from Golden Square, but in the course of that three minutes he took the letter out of his hat and put it in again twenty times at least. First the front, then the back, then the sides, then the superscription, then the seal, were objects of Newman’s admiration. Then he held it at arm’s length as if to take in the whole at one delicious survey, and then he rubbed his hands in a perfect ecstasy with his commission.\n\nHe reached the office, hung his hat on its accustomed peg, laid the letter and key upon the desk, and waited impatiently until Ralph Nickleby should appear. After a few minutes, the well-known creaking of his boots was heard on the stairs, and then the bell rung.\n\n‘Has the post come in?’\n\n‘No.’\n‘Any other letters?’\n\n‘One.’ Newman eyed him closely, and laid it on the desk.\n\n‘What’s this?’ asked Ralph, taking up the key.\n\n‘Left with the letter;--a boy brought them--quarter of an hour ago, or less.’\n\nRalph glanced at the direction, opened the letter, and read as follows:--\n\n‘You are known to me now. There are no reproaches I could heap upon your head which would carry with them one thousandth part of the grovelling shame that this assurance will awaken even in your breast.\n\n‘Your brother’s widow and her orphan child spurn the shelter of your roof, and shun you with disgust and loathing. Your kindred renounce you, for they know no shame but the ties of blood which bind them in name with you.\n\n‘You are an old man, and I leave you to the grave. May every recollection of your life cling to your false heart, and cast their darkness on your death-bed.’\n\nRalph Nickleby read this letter twice, and frowning heavily, fell into a fit of musing; the paper fluttered from his hand and dropped upon the floor, but he clasped his fingers, as if he held it still.\n\nSuddenly, he started from his seat, and thrusting it all crumpled into his pocket, turned furiously to Newman Noggs, as though to ask him why he lingered. But Newman stood unmoved, with his back towards him, following up, with the worn and blackened stump of an old pen, some figures in an Interest-table which was pasted against the wall, and apparently quite abstracted from every other object.\n\n# CHAPTER 34\n\nWherein Mr. Ralph Nickleby is visited by Persons with whom the Reader has been already made acquainted\n‘What a demnition long time you have kept me ringing at this confounded old cracked tea-kettle of a bell, every tinkle of which is enough to throw a strong man into blue convulsions, upon my life and soul, oh demmit,’--said Mr. Mantalini to Newman Noggs, scraping his boots, as he spoke, on Ralph Nickleby’s scraper.\n\n‘I didn’t hear the bell more than once,’ replied Newman.\n\n‘Then you are most immensely and outr-i-geously deaf,’ said Mr Mantalini, ‘as deaf as a demnition post.’\n\nMr. Mantalini had got by this time into the passage, and was making his way to the door of Ralph’s office with very little ceremony, when Newman interposed his body; and hinting that Mr. Nickleby was unwilling to be disturbed, inquired whether the client’s business was of a pressing nature.\n\n‘It is most demnebly particular,’ said Mr. Mantalini. ‘It is to melt some scraps of dirty paper into bright, shining, chinking, tinkling, demd mint sauce.’\n\nNewman uttered a significant grunt, and taking Mr. Mantalini’s proffered card, limped with it into his master’s office. As he thrust his head in at the door, he saw that Ralph had resumed the thoughtful posture into which he had fallen after perusing his nephew’s letter, and that he seemed to have been reading it again, as he once more held it open in his hand. The glance was but momentary, for Ralph, being disturbed, turned to demand the cause of the interruption.\n\nAs Newman stated it, the cause himself swaggered into the room, and grasping Ralph’s horny hand with uncommon affection, vowed that he had never seen him looking so well in all his life.\n\n‘There is quite a bloom upon your demd countenance,’ said Mr. Mantalini, seating himself unbidden, and arranging his hair and whiskers. ‘You look quite juvenile and jolly, demmit!’\n\n‘We are alone,’ returned Ralph, tartly. ‘What do you want with me?’\n\n‘Good!’ cried Mr. Mantalini, displaying his teeth. ‘What did I want! Yes. Ha, ha! Very good. WHAT did I want. Ha, ha. Oh dem!’\n\n‘What DO you want, man?’ demanded Ralph, sternly.\n\n‘Demnition discount,’ returned Mr. Mantalini, with a grin, and shaking his head waggishly.\n\n‘Money is scarce,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Demd scarce, or I shouldn’t want it,’ interrupted Mr. Mantalini.\n\n‘The times are bad, and one scarcely knows whom to trust,’ continued Ralph. ‘I don’t want to do business just now, in fact I would rather not; but as you are a friend--how many bills have you there?’\n\n‘Two,’ returned Mr. Mantalini.\n\n‘What is the gross amount?’\n\n‘Demd trifling--five-and-seventy.’\n\n‘And the dates?’\n\n‘Two months, and four.’\n\n‘I’ll do them for you--mind, for YOU; I wouldn’t for many people--for five-and-twenty pounds,’ said Ralph, deliberately.\n\n‘Oh demmit!’ cried Mr. Mantalini, whose face lengthened considerably at this handsome proposal.\n\n‘Why, that leaves you fifty,’ retorted Ralph. ‘What would you have? Let me see the names.’\n\n‘You are so demd hard, Nickleby,’ remonstrated Mr. Mantalini.\n\n‘Let me see the names,’ replied Ralph, impatiently extending his hand for the bills. ‘Well! They are not sure, but they are safe enough. Do you consent to the terms, and will you take the money? I don’t want you to do so. I would rather you didn’t.’\n\n‘Demmit, Nickleby, can’t you--’ began Mr. Mantalini.\n\n‘No,’ replied Ralph, interrupting him. ‘I can’t. Will you take the money--down, mind; no delay, no going into the city and pretending to negotiate with some other party who has no existence, and never had. Is it a bargain, or is it not?’\n\nRalph pushed some papers from him as he spoke, and carelessly rattled his cash-box, as though by mere accident. The sound was too much for Mr Mantalini. He closed the bargain directly it reached his ears, and Ralph told the money out upon the table.\n\nHe had scarcely done so, and Mr. Mantalini had not yet gathered it all up, when a ring was heard at the bell, and immediately afterwards Newman ushered in no less a person than Madame Mantalini, at sight of whom Mr Mantalini evinced considerable discomposure, and swept the cash into his pocket with remarkable alacrity.\n\n‘Oh, you ARE here,’ said Madame Mantalini, tossing her head.\n\n‘Yes, my life and soul, I am,’ replied her husband, dropping on his knees, and pouncing with kitten-like playfulness upon a stray sovereign. ‘I am here, my soul’s delight, upon Tom Tiddler’s ground, picking up the demnition gold and silver.’\n\n‘I am ashamed of you,’ said Madame Mantalini, with much indignation.\n\n‘Ashamed--of ME, my joy? It knows it is talking demd charming sweetness, but naughty fibs,’ returned Mr. Mantalini. ‘It knows it is not ashamed of its own popolorum tibby.’\n\nWhatever were the circumstances which had led to such a result, it certainly appeared as though the popolorum tibby had rather miscalculated, for the nonce, the extent of his lady’s affection. Madame Mantalini only looked scornful in reply; and, turning to Ralph, begged him to excuse her intrusion.\n\n‘Which is entirely attributable,’ said Madame, ‘to the gross misconduct and most improper behaviour of Mr. Mantalini.’\n\n‘Of me, my essential juice of pineapple!’\n\n‘Of you,’ returned his wife. ‘But I will not allow it. I will not submit to be ruined by the extravagance and profligacy of any man. I call Mr Nickleby to witness the course I intend to pursue with you.’\n\n‘Pray don’t call me to witness anything, ma’am,’ said Ralph. ‘Settle it between yourselves, settle it between yourselves.’\n\n‘No, but I must beg you as a favour,’ said Madame Mantalini, ‘to hear me give him notice of what it is my fixed intention to do--my fixed intention, sir,’ repeated Madame Mantalini, darting an angry look at her husband.\n\n‘Will she call me “Sir”?’ cried Mantalini. ‘Me who dote upon her with the demdest ardour! She, who coils her fascinations round me like a pure angelic rattlesnake! It will be all up with my feelings; she will throw me into a demd state.’\n\n‘Don’t talk of feelings, sir,’ rejoined Madame Mantalini, seating herself, and turning her back upon him. ‘You don’t consider mine.’\n\n‘I do not consider yours, my soul!’ exclaimed Mr. Mantalini.\n\n‘No,’ replied his wife.\n\nAnd notwithstanding various blandishments on the part of Mr. Mantalini, Madame Mantalini still said no, and said it too with such determined and resolute ill-temper, that Mr. Mantalini was clearly taken aback.\n\n‘His extravagance, Mr. Nickleby,’ said Madame Mantalini, addressing herself to Ralph, who leant against his easy-chair with his hands behind him, and regarded the amiable couple with a smile of the supremest and most unmitigated contempt,--‘his extravagance is beyond all bounds.’\n\n‘I should scarcely have supposed it,’ answered Ralph, sarcastically.\n\n‘I assure you, Mr. Nickleby, however, that it is,’ returned Madame Mantalini. ‘It makes me miserable! I am under constant apprehensions, and in constant difficulty. And even this,’ said Madame Mantalini, wiping her eyes, ‘is not the worst. He took some papers of value out of my desk this morning without asking my permission.’\n\nMr. Mantalini groaned slightly, and buttoned his trousers pocket.\n\n‘I am obliged,’ continued Madame Mantalini, ‘since our late misfortunes, to pay Miss Knag a great deal of money for having her name in the business, and I really cannot afford to encourage him in all his wastefulness. As I have no doubt that he came straight here, Mr Nickleby, to convert the papers I have spoken of, into money, and as you have assisted us very often before, and are very much connected with us in this kind of matters, I wish you to know the determination at which his conduct has compelled me to arrive.’\n\nMr. Mantalini groaned once more from behind his wife’s bonnet, and fitting a sovereign into one of his eyes, winked with the other at Ralph. Having achieved this performance with great dexterity, he whipped the coin into his pocket, and groaned again with increased penitence.\n\n‘I have made up my mind,’ said Madame Mantalini, as tokens of impatience manifested themselves in Ralph’s countenance, ‘to allowance him.’\n\n‘To do that, my joy?’ inquired Mr. Mantalini, who did not seem to have caught the words.\n\n‘To put him,’ said Madame Mantalini, looking at Ralph, and prudently abstaining from the slightest glance at her husband, lest his many graces should induce her to falter in her resolution, ‘to put him upon a fixed allowance; and I say that if he has a hundred and twenty pounds a year for his clothes and pocket-money, he may consider himself a very fortunate man.’\n\nMr. Mantalini waited, with much decorum, to hear the amount of the proposed stipend, but when it reached his ears, he cast his hat and cane upon the floor, and drawing out his pocket-handkerchief, gave vent to his feelings in a dismal moan.\n\n‘Demnition!’ cried Mr. Mantalini, suddenly skipping out of his chair, and as suddenly skipping into it again, to the great discomposure of his lady’s nerves. ‘But no. It is a demd horrid dream. It is not reality. No!’\n\nComforting himself with this assurance, Mr. Mantalini closed his eyes and waited patiently till such time as he should wake up.\n\n‘A very judicious arrangement,’ observed Ralph with a sneer, ‘if your husband will keep within it, ma’am--as no doubt he will.’\n\n‘Demmit!’ exclaimed Mr. Mantalini, opening his eyes at the sound of Ralph’s voice, ‘it is a horrid reality. She is sitting there before me. There is the graceful outline of her form; it cannot be mistaken--there is nothing like it. The two countesses had no outlines at all, and the dowager’s was a demd outline. Why is she so excruciatingly beautiful that I cannot be angry with her, even now?’\n\n‘You have brought it upon yourself, Alfred,’ returned Madame Mantalini--still reproachfully, but in a softened tone.\n\n‘I am a demd villain!’ cried Mr. Mantalini, smiting himself on the head. ‘I will fill my pockets with change for a sovereign in halfpence and drown myself in the Thames; but I will not be angry with her, even then, for I will put a note in the twopenny-post as I go along, to tell her where the body is. She will be a lovely widow. I shall be a body. Some handsome women will cry; she will laugh demnebly.’\n\n‘Alfred, you cruel, cruel creature,’ said Madame Mantalini, sobbing at the dreadful picture.\n\n‘She calls me cruel--me--me--who for her sake will become a demd, damp, moist, unpleasant body!’ exclaimed Mr. Mantalini.\n\n‘You know it almost breaks my heart, even to hear you talk of such a thing,’ replied Madame Mantalini.\n\n‘Can I live to be mistrusted?’ cried her husband. ‘Have I cut my heart into a demd extraordinary number of little pieces, and given them all away, one after another, to the same little engrossing demnition captivater, and can I live to be suspected by her? Demmit, no I can’t.’\n\n‘Ask Mr. Nickleby whether the sum I have mentioned is not a proper one,’ reasoned Madame Mantalini.\n\n‘I don’t want any sum,’ replied her disconsolate husband; ‘I shall require no demd allowance. I will be a body.’\n\nOn this repetition of Mr. Mantalini’s fatal threat, Madame Mantalini wrung her hands, and implored the interference of Ralph Nickleby; and after a great quantity of tears and talking, and several attempts on the part of Mr. Mantalini to reach the door, preparatory to straightway committing violence upon himself, that gentleman was prevailed upon, with difficulty, to promise that he wouldn’t be a body. This great point attained, Madame Mantalini argued the question of the allowance, and Mr Mantalini did the same, taking occasion to show that he could live with uncommon satisfaction upon bread and water, and go clad in rags, but that he could not support existence with the additional burden of being mistrusted by the object of his most devoted and disinterested affection. This brought fresh tears into Madame Mantalini’s eyes, which having just begun to open to some few of the demerits of Mr. Mantalini, were only open a very little way, and could be easily closed again. The result was, that without quite giving up the allowance question, Madame Mantalini, postponed its further consideration; and Ralph saw, clearly enough, that Mr. Mantalini had gained a fresh lease of his easy life, and that, for some time longer at all events, his degradation and downfall were postponed.\n\n‘But it will come soon enough,’ thought Ralph; ‘all love--bah! that I should use the cant of boys and girls--is fleeting enough; though that which has its sole root in the admiration of a whiskered face like that of yonder baboon, perhaps lasts the longest, as it originates in the greater blindness and is fed by vanity. Meantime the fools bring grist to my mill, so let them live out their day, and the longer it is, the better.’\n\nThese agreeable reflections occurred to Ralph Nickleby, as sundry small caresses and endearments, supposed to be unseen, were exchanged between the objects of his thoughts.\n\n‘If you have nothing more to say, my dear, to Mr. Nickleby,’ said Madame Mantalini, ‘we will take our leaves. I am sure we have detained him much too long already.’\n\nMr. Mantalini answered, in the first instance, by tapping Madame Mantalini several times on the nose, and then, by remarking in words that he had nothing more to say.\n\n‘Demmit! I have, though,’ he added almost immediately, drawing Ralph into a corner. ‘Here’s an affair about your friend Sir Mulberry. Such a demd extraordinary out-of-the-way kind of thing as never was--eh?’\n\n‘What do you mean?’ asked Ralph.\n\n‘Don’t you know, demmit?’ asked Mr. Mantalini.\n\n‘I see by the paper that he was thrown from his cabriolet last night, and severely injured, and that his life is in some danger,’ answered Ralph with great composure; ‘but I see nothing extraordinary in that--accidents are not miraculous events, when men live hard, and drive after dinner.’\n\n‘Whew!’ cried Mr. Mantalini in a long shrill whistle. ‘Then don’t you know how it was?’\n\n‘Not unless it was as I have just supposed,’ replied Ralph, shrugging his shoulders carelessly, as if to give his questioner to understand that he had no curiosity upon the subject.\n\n‘Demmit, you amaze me,’ cried Mantalini.\n\nRalph shrugged his shoulders again, as if it were no great feat to amaze Mr. Mantalini, and cast a wistful glance at the face of Newman Noggs, which had several times appeared behind a couple of panes of glass in the room door; it being a part of Newman’s duty, when unimportant people called, to make various feints of supposing that the bell had rung for him to show them out: by way of a gentle hint to such visitors that it was time to go.\n\n‘Don’t you know,’ said Mr. Mantalini, taking Ralph by the button, ‘that it wasn’t an accident at all, but a demd, furious, manslaughtering attack made upon him by your nephew?’\n\n‘What!’ snarled Ralph, clenching his fists and turning a livid white.\n\n‘Demmit, Nickleby, you’re as great a tiger as he is,’ said Mantalini, alarmed at these demonstrations.\n\n‘Go on,’ cried Ralph. ‘Tell me what you mean. What is this story? Who told you? Speak,’ growled Ralph. ‘Do you hear me?’\n\n‘’Gad, Nickleby,’ said Mr. Mantalini, retreating towards his wife, ‘what a demneble fierce old evil genius you are! You’re enough to frighten the life and soul out of her little delicious wits--flying all at once into such a blazing, ravaging, raging passion as never was, demmit!’\n\n‘Pshaw,’ rejoined Ralph, forcing a smile. ‘It is but manner.’\n\n‘It is a demd uncomfortable, private-madhouse-sort of a manner,’ said Mr Mantalini, picking up his cane.\n\nRalph affected to smile, and once more inquired from whom Mr. Mantalini had derived his information.\n\n‘From Pyke; and a demd, fine, pleasant, gentlemanly dog it is,’ replied Mantalini. ‘Demnition pleasant, and a tip-top sawyer.’\n\n‘And what said he?’ asked Ralph, knitting his brows.\n\n‘That it happened this way--that your nephew met him at a coffeehouse, fell upon him with the most demneble ferocity, followed him to his cab, swore he would ride home with him, if he rode upon the horse’s back or hooked himself on to the horse’s tail; smashed his countenance, which is a demd fine countenance in its natural state; frightened the horse, pitched out Sir Mulberry and himself, and--’\n\n‘And was killed?’ interposed Ralph with gleaming eyes. ‘Was he? Is he dead?’\n\nMantalini shook his head.\n\n‘Ugh,’ said Ralph, turning away. ‘Then he has done nothing. Stay,’ he added, looking round again. ‘He broke a leg or an arm, or put his shoulder out, or fractured his collar-bone, or ground a rib or two? His neck was saved for the halter, but he got some painful and slow-healing injury for his trouble? Did he? You must have heard that, at least.’\n\n‘No,’ rejoined Mantalini, shaking his head again. ‘Unless he was dashed into such little pieces that they blew away, he wasn’t hurt, for he went off as quiet and comfortable as--as--as demnition,’ said Mr. Mantalini, rather at a loss for a simile.\n\n‘And what,’ said Ralph, hesitating a little, ‘what was the cause of quarrel?’\n\n‘You are the demdest, knowing hand,’ replied Mr. Mantalini, in an admiring tone, ‘the cunningest, rummest, superlativest old fox--oh dem!--to pretend now not to know that it was the little bright-eyed niece--the softest, sweetest, prettiest--’\n\n‘Alfred!’ interposed Madame Mantalini.\n\n‘She is always right,’ rejoined Mr. Mantalini soothingly, ‘and when she says it is time to go, it is time, and go she shall; and when she walks along the streets with her own tulip, the women shall say, with envy, she has got a demd fine husband; and the men shall say with rapture, he has got a demd fine wife; and they shall both be right and neither wrong, upon my life and soul--oh demmit!’\n\nWith which remarks, and many more, no less intellectual and to the purpose, Mr. Mantalini kissed the fingers of his gloves to Ralph Nickleby, and drawing his lady’s arm through his, led her mincingly away.\n\n‘So, so,’ muttered Ralph, dropping into his chair; ‘this devil is loose again, and thwarting me, as he was born to do, at every turn. He told me once there should be a day of reckoning between us, sooner or later. I’ll make him a true prophet, for it shall surely come.’\n\n‘Are you at home?’ asked Newman, suddenly popping in his head.\n\n‘No,’ replied Ralph, with equal abruptness.\n\nNewman withdrew his head, but thrust it in again.\n\n‘You’re quite sure you’re not at home, are you?’ said Newman.\n\n‘What does the idiot mean?’ cried Ralph, testily.\n\n‘He has been waiting nearly ever since they first came in, and may have heard your voice--that’s all,’ said Newman, rubbing his hands.\n\n‘Who has?’ demanded Ralph, wrought by the intelligence he had just heard, and his clerk’s provoking coolness, to an intense pitch of irritation.\n\nThe necessity of a reply was superseded by the unlooked-for entrance of a third party--the individual in question--who, bringing his one eye (for he had but one) to bear on Ralph Nickleby, made a great many shambling bows, and sat himself down in an armchair, with his hands on his knees, and his short black trousers drawn up so high in the legs by the exertion of seating himself, that they scarcely reached below the tops of his Wellington boots.\n\n‘Why, this IS a surprise!’ said Ralph, bending his gaze upon the visitor, and half smiling as he scrutinised him attentively; ‘I should know your face, Mr. Squeers.’\n\n‘Ah!’ replied that worthy, ‘and you’d have know’d it better, sir, if it hadn’t been for all that I’ve been a-going through. Just lift that little boy off the tall stool in the back-office, and tell him to come in here, will you, my man?’ said Squeers, addressing himself to Newman. ‘Oh, he’s lifted his-self off. My son, sir, little Wackford. What do you think of him, sir, for a specimen of the Dotheboys Hall feeding? Ain’t he fit to bust out of his clothes, and start the seams, and make the very buttons fly off with his fatness? Here’s flesh!’ cried Squeers, turning the boy about, and indenting the plumpest parts of his figure with divers pokes and punches, to the great discomposure of his son and heir. ‘Here’s firmness, here’s solidness! Why you can hardly get up enough of him between your finger and thumb to pinch him anywheres.’\n\nIn however good condition Master Squeers might have been, he certainly did not present this remarkable compactness of person, for on his father’s closing his finger and thumb in illustration of his remark, he uttered a sharp cry, and rubbed the place in the most natural manner possible.\n\n‘Well,’ remarked Squeers, a little disconcerted, ‘I had him there; but that’s because we breakfasted early this morning, and he hasn’t had his lunch yet. Why you couldn’t shut a bit of him in a door, when he’s had his dinner. Look at them tears, sir,’ said Squeers, with a triumphant air, as Master Wackford wiped his eyes with the cuff of his jacket, ‘there’s oiliness!’\n\n‘He looks well, indeed,’ returned Ralph, who, for some purposes of his own, seemed desirous to conciliate the schoolmaster. ‘But how is Mrs Squeers, and how are you?’\n\n‘Mrs. Squeers, sir,’ replied the proprietor of Dotheboys, ‘is as she always is--a mother to them lads, and a blessing, and a comfort, and a joy to all them as knows her. One of our boys--gorging his-self with vittles, and then turning in; that’s their way--got a abscess on him last week. To see how she operated upon him with a pen-knife! Oh Lor!’ said Squeers, heaving a sigh, and nodding his head a great many times, ‘what a member of society that woman is!’\n\nMr. Squeers indulged in a retrospective look, for some quarter of a minute, as if this allusion to his lady’s excellences had naturally led his mind to the peaceful village of Dotheboys near Greta Bridge in Yorkshire; and then looked at Ralph, as if waiting for him to say something.\n\n‘Have you quite recovered that scoundrel’s attack?’ asked Ralph.\n\n‘I’ve only just done it, if I’ve done it now,’ replied Squeers. ‘I was one blessed bruise, sir,’ said Squeers, touching first the roots of his hair, and then the toes of his boots, ‘from HERE to THERE. Vinegar and brown paper, vinegar and brown paper, from morning to night. I suppose there was a matter of half a ream of brown paper stuck upon me, from first to last. As I laid all of a heap in our kitchen, plastered all over, you might have thought I was a large brown-paper parcel, chock full of nothing but groans. Did I groan loud, Wackford, or did I groan soft?’ asked Mr. Squeers, appealing to his son.\n\n‘Loud,’ replied Wackford.\n\n‘Was the boys sorry to see me in such a dreadful condition, Wackford, or was they glad?’ asked Mr. Squeers, in a sentimental manner.\n\n‘Gl--’\n\n‘Eh?’ cried Squeers, turning sharp round.\n\n‘Sorry,’ rejoined his son.\n\n‘Oh!’ said Squeers, catching him a smart box on the ear. ‘Then take your hands out of your pockets, and don’t stammer when you’re asked a question. Hold your noise, sir, in a gentleman’s office, or I’ll run away from my family and never come back any more; and then what would become of all them precious and forlorn lads as would be let loose on the world, without their best friend at their elbers?’\n\n‘Were you obliged to have medical attendance?’ inquired Ralph.\n\n‘Ay, was I,’ rejoined Squeers, ‘and a precious bill the medical attendant brought in too; but I paid it though.’\n\nRalph elevated his eyebrows in a manner which might be expressive of either sympathy or astonishment--just as the beholder was pleased to take it.\n\n‘Yes, I paid it, every farthing,’ replied Squeers, who seemed to know the man he had to deal with, too well to suppose that any blinking of the question would induce him to subscribe towards the expenses; ‘I wasn’t out of pocket by it after all, either.’\n\n‘No!’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Not a halfpenny,’ replied Squeers. ‘The fact is, we have only one extra with our boys, and that is for doctors when required--and not then, unless we’re sure of our customers. Do you see?’\n\n‘I understand,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Very good,’ rejoined Squeers. ‘Then, after my bill was run up, we picked out five little boys (sons of small tradesmen, as was sure pay) that had never had the scarlet fever, and we sent one to a cottage where they’d got it, and he took it, and then we put the four others to sleep with him, and THEY took it, and then the doctor came and attended ‘em once all round, and we divided my total among ‘em, and added it on to their little bills, and the parents paid it. Ha! ha! ha!’\n\n‘And a good plan too,’ said Ralph, eyeing the schoolmaster stealthily.\n\n‘I believe you,’ rejoined Squeers. ‘We always do it. Why, when Mrs Squeers was brought to bed with little Wackford here, we ran the hooping-cough through half-a-dozen boys, and charged her expenses among ‘em, monthly nurse included. Ha! ha! ha!’\n\nRalph never laughed, but on this occasion he produced the nearest approach to it that he could, and waiting until Mr. Squeers had enjoyed the professional joke to his heart’s content, inquired what had brought him to town.\n\n‘Some bothering law business,’ replied Squeers, scratching his head, ‘connected with an action, for what they call neglect of a boy. I don’t know what they would have. He had as good grazing, that boy had, as there is about us.’\n\nRalph looked as if he did not quite understand the observation.\n\n‘Grazing,’ said Squeers, raising his voice, under the impression that as Ralph failed to comprehend him, he must be deaf. ‘When a boy gets weak and ill and don’t relish his meals, we give him a change of diet--turn him out, for an hour or so every day, into a neighbour’s turnip field, or sometimes, if it’s a delicate case, a turnip field and a piece of carrots alternately, and let him eat as many as he likes. There an’t better land in the country than this perwerse lad grazed on, and yet he goes and catches cold and indigestion and what not, and then his friends brings a lawsuit against ME! Now, you’d hardly suppose,’ added Squeers, moving in his chair with the impatience of an ill-used man, ‘that people’s ingratitude would carry them quite as far as that; would you?’\n\n‘A hard case, indeed,’ observed Ralph.\n\n‘You don’t say more than the truth when you say that,’ replied Squeers. ‘I don’t suppose there’s a man going, as possesses the fondness for youth that I do. There’s youth to the amount of eight hundred pound a year at Dotheboys Hall at this present time. I’d take sixteen hundred pound worth if I could get ‘em, and be as fond of every individual twenty pound among ‘em as nothing should equal it!’\n\n‘Are you stopping at your old quarters?’ asked Ralph.\n\n‘Yes, we are at the Saracen,’ replied Squeers, ‘and as it don’t want very long to the end of the half-year, we shall continney to stop there till I’ve collected the money, and some new boys too, I hope. I’ve brought little Wackford up, on purpose to show to parents and guardians. I shall put him in the advertisement, this time. Look at that boy--himself a pupil. Why he’s a miracle of high feeding, that boy is!’\n\n‘I should like to have a word with you,’ said Ralph, who had both spoken and listened mechanically for some time, and seemed to have been thinking.\n\n‘As many words as you like, sir,’ rejoined Squeers. ‘Wackford, you go and play in the back office, and don’t move about too much or you’ll get thin, and that won’t do. You haven’t got such a thing as twopence, Mr Nickleby, have you?’ said Squeers, rattling a bunch of keys in his coat pocket, and muttering something about its being all silver.\n\n‘I--think I have,’ said Ralph, very slowly, and producing, after much rummaging in an old drawer, a penny, a halfpenny, and two farthings.\n\n‘Thankee,’ said Squeers, bestowing it upon his son. ‘Here! You go and buy a tart--Mr. Nickleby’s man will show you where--and mind you buy a rich one. Pastry,’ added Squeers, closing the door on Master Wackford, ‘makes his flesh shine a good deal, and parents thinks that a healthy sign.’\n\nWith this explanation, and a peculiarly knowing look to eke it out, Mr. Squeers moved his chair so as to bring himself opposite to Ralph Nickleby at no great distance off; and having planted it to his entire satisfaction, sat down.\n\n‘Attend to me,’ said Ralph, bending forward a little.\n\nSqueers nodded.\n\n‘I am not to suppose,’ said Ralph, ‘that you are dolt enough to forgive or forget, very readily, the violence that was committed upon you, or the exposure which accompanied it?’\n\n‘Devil a bit,’ replied Squeers, tartly.\n\n‘Or to lose an opportunity of repaying it with interest, if you could get one?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Show me one, and try,’ rejoined Squeers.\n\n‘Some such object it was, that induced you to call on me?’ said Ralph, raising his eyes to the schoolmaster’s face.\n\n‘N-n-no, I don’t know that,’ replied Squeers. ‘I thought that if it was in your power to make me, besides the trifle of money you sent, any compensation--’\n\n‘Ah!’ cried Ralph, interrupting him. ‘You needn’t go on.’\n\nAfter a long pause, during which Ralph appeared absorbed in contemplation, he again broke silence by asking:\n\n‘Who is this boy that he took with him?’\n\nSqueers stated his name.\n\n‘Was he young or old, healthy or sickly, tractable or rebellious? Speak out, man,’ retorted Ralph.\n\n‘Why, he wasn’t young,’ answered Squeers; ‘that is, not young for a boy, you know.’\n\n‘That is, he was not a boy at all, I suppose?’ interrupted Ralph.\n\n‘Well,’ returned Squeers, briskly, as if he felt relieved by the suggestion, ‘he might have been nigh twenty. He wouldn’t seem so old, though, to them as didn’t know him, for he was a little wanting here,’ touching his forehead; ‘nobody at home, you know, if you knocked ever so often.’\n\n‘And you DID knock pretty often, I dare say?’ muttered Ralph.\n\n‘Pretty well,’ returned Squeers with a grin.\n\n‘When you wrote to acknowledge the receipt of this trifle of money as you call it,’ said Ralph, ‘you told me his friends had deserted him long ago, and that you had not the faintest clue or trace to tell you who he was. Is that the truth?’\n\n‘It is, worse luck!’ replied Squeers, becoming more and more easy and familiar in his manner, as Ralph pursued his inquiries with the less reserve. ‘It’s fourteen years ago, by the entry in my book, since a strange man brought him to my place, one autumn night, and left him there; paying five pound five, for his first quarter in advance. He might have been five or six year old at that time--not more.’\n\n‘What more do you know about him?’ demanded Ralph.\n\n‘Devilish little, I’m sorry to say,’ replied Squeers. ‘The money was paid for some six or eight year, and then it stopped. He had given an address in London, had this chap; but when it came to the point, of course nobody knowed anything about him. So I kept the lad out of--out of--’\n\n‘Charity?’ suggested Ralph drily.\n\n‘Charity, to be sure,’ returned Squeers, rubbing his knees, ‘and when he begins to be useful in a certain sort of way, this young scoundrel of a Nickleby comes and carries him off. But the most vexatious and aggeravating part of the whole affair is,’ said Squeers, dropping his voice, and drawing his chair still closer to Ralph, ‘that some questions have been asked about him at last--not of me, but, in a roundabout kind of way, of people in our village. So, that just when I might have had all arrears paid up, perhaps, and perhaps--who knows? such things have happened in our business before--a present besides for putting him out to a farmer, or sending him to sea, so that he might never turn up to disgrace his parents, supposing him to be a natural boy, as many of our boys are--damme, if that villain of a Nickleby don’t collar him in open day, and commit as good as highway robbery upon my pocket.’\n\n‘We will both cry quits with him before long,’ said Ralph, laying his hand on the arm of the Yorkshire schoolmaster.\n\n‘Quits!’ echoed Squeers. ‘Ah! and I should like to leave a small balance in his favour, to be settled when he can. I only wish Mrs. Squeers could catch hold of him. Bless her heart! She’d murder him, Mr. Nickleby--she would, as soon as eat her dinner.’\n\n‘We will talk of this again,’ said Ralph. ‘I must have time to think of it. To wound him through his own affections and fancies--. If I could strike him through this boy--’\n\n‘Strike him how you like, sir,’ interrupted Squeers, ‘only hit him hard enough, that’s all--and with that, I’ll say good-morning. Here!--just chuck that little boy’s hat off that corner peg, and lift him off the stool will you?’\n\nBawling these requests to Newman Noggs, Mr. Squeers betook himself to the little back-office, and fitted on his child’s hat with parental anxiety, while Newman, with his pen behind his ear, sat, stiff and immovable, on his stool, regarding the father and son by turns with a broad stare.\n\n‘He’s a fine boy, an’t he?’ said Squeers, throwing his head a little on one side, and falling back to the desk, the better to estimate the proportions of little Wackford.\n\n‘Very,’ said Newman.\n\n‘Pretty well swelled out, an’t he?’ pursued Squeers. ‘He has the fatness of twenty boys, he has.’\n\n‘Ah!’ replied Newman, suddenly thrusting his face into that of Squeers, ‘he has;--the fatness of twenty!--more! He’s got it all. God help that others. Ha! ha! Oh Lord!’\n\nHaving uttered these fragmentary observations, Newman dropped upon his desk and began to write with most marvellous rapidity.\n\n‘Why, what does the man mean?’ cried Squeers, colouring. ‘Is he drunk?’\n\nNewman made no reply.\n\n‘Is he mad?’ said Squeers.\n\nBut, still Newman betrayed no consciousness of any presence save his own; so, Mr. Squeers comforted himself by saying that he was both drunk AND mad; and, with this parting observation, he led his hopeful son away.\n\nIn exact proportion as Ralph Nickleby became conscious of a struggling and lingering regard for Kate, had his detestation of Nicholas augmented. It might be, that to atone for the weakness of inclining to any one person, he held it necessary to hate some other more intensely than before; but such had been the course of his feelings. And now, to be defied and spurned, to be held up to her in the worst and most repulsive colours, to know that she was taught to hate and despise him: to feel that there was infection in his touch, and taint in his companionship--to know all this, and to know that the mover of it all was that same boyish poor relation who had twitted him in their very first interview, and openly bearded and braved him since, wrought his quiet and stealthy malignity to such a pitch, that there was scarcely anything he would not have hazarded to gratify it, if he could have seen his way to some immediate retaliation.\n\nBut, fortunately for Nicholas, Ralph Nickleby did not; and although he cast about all that day, and kept a corner of his brain working on the one anxious subject through all the round of schemes and business that came with it, night found him at last, still harping on the same theme, and still pursuing the same unprofitable reflections.\n\n‘When my brother was such as he,’ said Ralph, ‘the first comparisons were drawn between us--always in my disfavour. HE was open, liberal, gallant, gay; I a crafty hunks of cold and stagnant blood, with no passion but love of saving, and no spirit beyond a thirst for gain. I recollected it well when I first saw this whipster; but I remember it better now.’\n\nHe had been occupied in tearing Nicholas’s letter into atoms; and as he spoke, he scattered it in a tiny shower about him.\n\n‘Recollections like these,’ pursued Ralph, with a bitter smile, ‘flock upon me--when I resign myself to them--in crowds, and from countless quarters. As a portion of the world affect to despise the power of money, I must try and show them what it is.’\n\nAnd being, by this time, in a pleasant frame of mind for slumber, Ralph Nickleby went to bed.\n\n# CHAPTER 35\n\nSmike becomes known to Mrs. Nickleby and Kate. Nicholas also meets with new Acquaintances. Brighter Days seem to dawn upon the Family\nHaving established his mother and sister in the apartments of the kind-hearted miniature painter, and ascertained that Sir Mulberry Hawk was in no danger of losing his life, Nicholas turned his thoughts to poor Smike, who, after breakfasting with Newman Noggs, had remained, in a disconsolate state, at that worthy creature’s lodgings, waiting, with much anxiety, for further intelligence of his protector.\n\n‘As he will be one of our own little household, wherever we live, or whatever fortune is in reserve for us,’ thought Nicholas, ‘I must present the poor fellow in due form. They will be kind to him for his own sake, and if not (on that account solely) to the full extent I could wish, they will stretch a point, I am sure, for mine.’\n\nNicholas said ‘they’, but his misgivings were confined to one person. He was sure of Kate, but he knew his mother’s peculiarities, and was not quite so certain that Smike would find favour in the eyes of Mrs Nickleby.\n\n‘However,’ thought Nicholas as he departed on his benevolent errand; ‘she cannot fail to become attached to him, when she knows what a devoted creature he is, and as she must quickly make the discovery, his probation will be a short one.’\n\n‘I was afraid,’ said Smike, overjoyed to see his friend again, ‘that you had fallen into some fresh trouble; the time seemed so long, at last, that I almost feared you were lost.’\n\n‘Lost!’ replied Nicholas gaily. ‘You will not be rid of me so easily, I promise you. I shall rise to the surface many thousand times yet, and the harder the thrust that pushes me down, the more quickly I shall rebound, Smike. But come; my errand here is to take you home.’\n\n‘Home!’ faltered Smike, drawing timidly back.\n\n‘Ay,’ rejoined Nicholas, taking his arm. ‘Why not?’\n\n‘I had such hopes once,’ said Smike; ‘day and night, day and night, for many years. I longed for home till I was weary, and pined away with grief, but now--’\n\n‘And what now?’ asked Nicholas, looking kindly in his face. ‘What now, old friend?’\n\n‘I could not part from you to go to any home on earth,’ replied Smike, pressing his hand; ‘except one, except one. I shall never be an old man; and if your hand placed me in the grave, and I could think, before I died, that you would come and look upon it sometimes with one of your kind smiles, and in the summer weather, when everything was alive--not dead like me--I could go to that home almost without a tear.’\n\n‘Why do you talk thus, poor boy, if your life is a happy one with me?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Because I should change; not those about me. And if they forgot me, I should never know it,’ replied Smike. ‘In the churchyard we are all alike, but here there are none like me. I am a poor creature, but I know that.’\n\n‘You are a foolish, silly creature,’ said Nicholas cheerfully. ‘If that is what you mean, I grant you that. Why, here’s a dismal face for ladies’ company!--my pretty sister too, whom you have so often asked me about. Is this your Yorkshire gallantry? For shame! for shame!’\n\nSmike brightened up and smiled.\n\n‘When I talk of home,’ pursued Nicholas, ‘I talk of mine--which is yours of course. If it were defined by any particular four walls and a roof, God knows I should be sufficiently puzzled to say whereabouts it lay; but that is not what I mean. When I speak of home, I speak of the place where--in default of a better--those I love are gathered together; and if that place were a gypsy’s tent, or a barn, I should call it by the same good name notwithstanding. And now, for what is my present home, which, however alarming your expectations may be, will neither terrify you by its extent nor its magnificence!’\n\nSo saying, Nicholas took his companion by the arm, and saying a great deal more to the same purpose, and pointing out various things to amuse and interest him as they went along, led the way to Miss La Creevy’s house.\n\n‘And this, Kate,’ said Nicholas, entering the room where his sister sat alone, ‘is the faithful friend and affectionate fellow-traveller whom I prepared you to receive.’\n\nPoor Smike was bashful, and awkward, and frightened enough, at first, but Kate advanced towards him so kindly, and said, in such a sweet voice, how anxious she had been to see him after all her brother had told her, and how much she had to thank him for having comforted Nicholas so greatly in their very trying reverses, that he began to be very doubtful whether he should shed tears or not, and became still more flurried. However, he managed to say, in a broken voice, that Nicholas was his only friend, and that he would lay down his life to help him; and Kate, although she was so kind and considerate, seemed to be so wholly unconscious of his distress and embarrassment, that he recovered almost immediately and felt quite at home.\n\nThen, Miss La Creevy came in; and to her Smike had to be presented also. And Miss La Creevy was very kind too, and wonderfully talkative: not to Smike, for that would have made him uneasy at first, but to Nicholas and his sister. Then, after a time, she would speak to Smike himself now and then, asking him whether he was a judge of likenesses, and whether he thought that picture in the corner was like herself, and whether he didn’t think it would have looked better if she had made herself ten years younger, and whether he didn’t think, as a matter of general observation, that young ladies looked better not only in pictures, but out of them too, than old ones; with many more small jokes and facetious remarks, which were delivered with such good-humour and merriment, that Smike thought, within himself, she was the nicest lady he had ever seen; even nicer than Mrs. Grudden, of Mr. Vincent Crummles’s theatre; and she was a nice lady too, and talked, perhaps more, but certainly louder, than Miss La Creevy.\n\nAt length the door opened again, and a lady in mourning came in; and Nicholas kissing the lady in mourning affectionately, and calling her his mother, led her towards the chair from which Smike had risen when she entered the room.\n\n‘You are always kind-hearted, and anxious to help the oppressed, my dear mother,’ said Nicholas, ‘so you will be favourably disposed towards him, I know.’\n\n‘I am sure, my dear Nicholas,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby, looking very hard at her new friend, and bending to him with something more of majesty than the occasion seemed to require: ‘I am sure any friend of yours has, as indeed he naturally ought to have, and must have, of course, you know, a great claim upon me, and of course, it is a very great pleasure to me to be introduced to anybody you take an interest in. There can be no doubt about that; none at all; not the least in the world,’ said Mrs Nickleby. ‘At the same time I must say, Nicholas, my dear, as I used to say to your poor dear papa, when he WOULD bring gentlemen home to dinner, and there was nothing in the house, that if he had come the day before yesterday--no, I don’t mean the day before yesterday now; I should have said, perhaps, the year before last--we should have been better able to entertain him.’\n\nWith which remarks, Mrs. Nickleby turned to her daughter, and inquired, in an audible whisper, whether the gentleman was going to stop all night.\n\n‘Because, if he is, Kate, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘I don’t see that it’s possible for him to sleep anywhere, and that’s the truth.’\n\nKate stepped gracefully forward, and without any show of annoyance or irritation, breathed a few words into her mother’s ear.\n\n‘La, Kate, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, shrinking back, ‘how you do tickle one! Of course, I understand THAT, my love, without your telling me; and I said the same to Nicholas, and I AM very much pleased. You didn’t tell me, Nicholas, my dear,’ added Mrs. Nickleby, turning round with an air of less reserve than she had before assumed, ‘what your friend’s name is.’\n\n‘His name, mother,’ replied Nicholas, ‘is Smike.’\n\nThe effect of this communication was by no means anticipated; but the name was no sooner pronounced, than Mrs. Nickleby dropped upon a chair, and burst into a fit of crying.\n\n‘What is the matter?’ exclaimed Nicholas, running to support her.\n\n‘It’s so like Pyke,’ cried Mrs. Nickleby; ‘so exactly like Pyke. Oh! don’t speak to me--I shall be better presently.’\n\nAnd after exhibiting every symptom of slow suffocation in all its stages, and drinking about a tea-spoonful of water from a full tumbler, and spilling the remainder, Mrs. Nickleby WAS better, and remarked, with a feeble smile, that she was very foolish, she knew.\n\n‘It’s a weakness in our family,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘so, of course, I can’t be blamed for it. Your grandmama, Kate, was exactly the same--precisely. The least excitement, the slightest surprise--she fainted away directly. I have heard her say, often and often, that when she was a young lady, and before she was married, she was turning a corner into Oxford Street one day, when she ran against her own hairdresser, who, it seems, was escaping from a bear;--the mere suddenness of the encounter made her faint away directly. Wait, though,’ added Mrs. Nickleby, pausing to consider. ‘Let me be sure I’m right. Was it her hairdresser who had escaped from a bear, or was it a bear who had escaped from her hairdresser’s? I declare I can’t remember just now, but the hairdresser was a very handsome man, I know, and quite a gentleman in his manners; so that it has nothing to do with the point of the story.’\n\nMrs. Nickleby having fallen imperceptibly into one of her retrospective moods, improved in temper from that moment, and glided, by an easy change of the conversation occasionally, into various other anecdotes, no less remarkable for their strict application to the subject in hand.\n\n‘Mr. Smike is from Yorkshire, Nicholas, my dear?’ said Mrs. Nickleby, after dinner, and when she had been silent for some time.\n\n‘Certainly, mother,’ replied Nicholas. ‘I see you have not forgotten his melancholy history.’\n\n‘O dear no,’ cried Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Ah! melancholy, indeed. You don’t happen, Mr. Smike, ever to have dined with the Grimbles of Grimble Hall, somewhere in the North Riding, do you?’ said the good lady, addressing herself to him. ‘A very proud man, Sir Thomas Grimble, with six grown-up and most lovely daughters, and the finest park in the county.’\n\n‘My dear mother,’ reasoned Nicholas, ‘do you suppose that the unfortunate outcast of a Yorkshire school was likely to receive many cards of invitation from the nobility and gentry in the neighbourhood?’\n\n‘Really, my dear, I don’t know why it should be so very extraordinary,’ said Mrs. Nickleby. ‘I know that when I was at school, I always went at least twice every half-year to the Hawkinses at Taunton Vale, and they are much richer than the Grimbles, and connected with them in marriage; so you see it’s not so very unlikely, after all.’\n\nHaving put down Nicholas in this triumphant manner, Mrs. Nickleby was suddenly seized with a forgetfulness of Smike’s real name, and an irresistible tendency to call him Mr. Slammons; which circumstance she attributed to the remarkable similarity of the two names in point of sound both beginning with an S, and moreover being spelt with an M. But whatever doubt there might be on this point, there was none as to his being a most excellent listener; which circumstance had considerable influence in placing them on the very best terms, and inducing Mrs Nickleby to express the highest opinion of his general deportment and disposition.\n\nThus, the little circle remained, on the most amicable and agreeable footing, until the Monday morning, when Nicholas withdrew himself from it for a short time, seriously to reflect upon the state of his affairs, and to determine, if he could, upon some course of life, which would enable him to support those who were so entirely dependent upon his exertions.\n\nMr. Crummles occurred to him more than once; but although Kate was acquainted with the whole history of his connection with that gentleman, his mother was not; and he foresaw a thousand fretful objections, on her part, to his seeking a livelihood upon the stage. There were graver reasons, too, against his returning to that mode of life. Independently of those arising out of its spare and precarious earnings, and his own internal conviction that he could never hope to aspire to any great distinction, even as a provincial actor, how could he carry his sister from town to town, and place to place, and debar her from any other associates than those with whom he would be compelled, almost without distinction, to mingle? ‘It won’t do,’ said Nicholas, shaking his head; ‘I must try something else.’\n\nIt was much easier to make this resolution than to carry it into effect. With no greater experience of the world than he had acquired for himself in his short trials; with a sufficient share of headlong rashness and precipitation (qualities not altogether unnatural at his time of life); with a very slender stock of money, and a still more scanty stock of friends; what could he do? ‘Egad!’ said Nicholas, ‘I’ll try that Register Office again.’\n\nHe smiled at himself as he walked away with a quick step; for, an instant before, he had been internally blaming his own precipitation. He did not laugh himself out of the intention, however, for on he went: picturing to himself, as he approached the place, all kinds of splendid possibilities, and impossibilities too, for that matter, and thinking himself, perhaps with good reason, very fortunate to be endowed with so buoyant and sanguine a temperament.\n\nThe office looked just the same as when he had left it last, and, indeed, with one or two exceptions, there seemed to be the very same placards in the window that he had seen before. There were the same unimpeachable masters and mistresses in want of virtuous servants, and the same virtuous servants in want of unimpeachable masters and mistresses, and the same magnificent estates for the investment of capital, and the same enormous quantities of capital to be invested in estates, and, in short, the same opportunities of all sorts for people who wanted to make their fortunes. And a most extraordinary proof it was of the national prosperity, that people had not been found to avail themselves of such advantages long ago.\n\nAs Nicholas stopped to look in at the window, an old gentleman happened to stop too; and Nicholas, carrying his eye along the window-panes from left to right in search of some capital-text placard which should be applicable to his own case, caught sight of this old gentleman’s figure, and instinctively withdrew his eyes from the window, to observe the same more closely.\n\nHe was a sturdy old fellow in a broad-skirted blue coat, made pretty large, to fit easily, and with no particular waist; his bulky legs clothed in drab breeches and high gaiters, and his head protected by a low-crowned broad-brimmed white hat, such as a wealthy grazier might wear. He wore his coat buttoned; and his dimpled double chin rested in the folds of a white neckerchief--not one of your stiff-starched apoplectic cravats, but a good, easy, old-fashioned white neckcloth that a man might go to bed in and be none the worse for. But what principally attracted the attention of Nicholas was the old gentleman’s eye,--never was such a clear, twinkling, honest, merry, happy eye, as that. And there he stood, looking a little upward, with one hand thrust into the breast of his coat, and the other playing with his old-fashioned gold watch-chain: his head thrown a little on one side, and his hat a little more on one side than his head, (but that was evidently accident; not his ordinary way of wearing it,) with such a pleasant smile playing about his mouth, and such a comical expression of mingled slyness, simplicity, kind-heartedness, and good-humour, lighting up his jolly old face, that Nicholas would have been content to have stood there and looked at him until evening, and to have forgotten, meanwhile, that there was such a thing as a soured mind or a crabbed countenance to be met with in the whole wide world.\n\nBut, even a very remote approach to this gratification was not to be made, for although he seemed quite unconscious of having been the subject of observation, he looked casually at Nicholas; and the latter, fearful of giving offence, resumed his scrutiny of the window instantly.\n\nStill, the old gentleman stood there, glancing from placard to placard, and Nicholas could not forbear raising his eyes to his face again. Grafted upon the quaintness and oddity of his appearance, was something so indescribably engaging, and bespeaking so much worth, and there were so many little lights hovering about the corners of his mouth and eyes, that it was not a mere amusement, but a positive pleasure and delight to look at him.\n\nThis being the case, it is no wonder that the old man caught Nicholas in the fact, more than once. At such times, Nicholas coloured and looked embarrassed: for the truth is, that he had begun to wonder whether the stranger could, by any possibility, be looking for a clerk or secretary; and thinking this, he felt as if the old gentleman must know it.\n\nLong as all this takes to tell, it was not more than a couple of minutes in passing. As the stranger was moving away, Nicholas caught his eye again, and, in the awkwardness of the moment, stammered out an apology.\n\n‘No offence. Oh no offence!’ said the old man.\n\nThis was said in such a hearty tone, and the voice was so exactly what it should have been from such a speaker, and there was such a cordiality in the manner, that Nicholas was emboldened to speak again.\n\n‘A great many opportunities here, sir,’ he said, half smiling as he motioned towards the window.\n\n‘A great many people willing and anxious to be employed have seriously thought so very often, I dare say,’ replied the old man. ‘Poor fellows, poor fellows!’\n\nHe moved away as he said this; but seeing that Nicholas was about to speak, good-naturedly slackened his pace, as if he were unwilling to cut him short. After a little of that hesitation which may be sometimes observed between two people in the street who have exchanged a nod, and are both uncertain whether they shall turn back and speak, or not, Nicholas found himself at the old man’s side.\n\n‘You were about to speak, young gentleman; what were you going to say?’\n\n‘Merely that I almost hoped--I mean to say, thought--you had some object in consulting those advertisements,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Ay, ay? what object now--what object?’ returned the old man, looking slyly at Nicholas. ‘Did you think I wanted a situation now--eh? Did you think I did?’\n\nNicholas shook his head.\n\n‘Ha! ha!’ laughed the old gentleman, rubbing his hands and wrists as if he were washing them. ‘A very natural thought, at all events, after seeing me gazing at those bills. I thought the same of you, at first; upon my word I did.’\n\n‘If you had thought so at last, too, sir, you would not have been far from the truth,’ rejoined Nicholas.\n\n‘Eh?’ cried the old man, surveying him from head to foot. ‘What! Dear me! No, no. Well-behaved young gentleman reduced to such a necessity! No no, no no.’\n\nNicholas bowed, and bidding him good-morning, turned upon his heel.\n\n‘Stay,’ said the old man, beckoning him into a bye street, where they could converse with less interruption. ‘What d’ye mean, eh?’\n\n‘Merely that your kind face and manner--both so unlike any I have ever seen--tempted me into an avowal, which, to any other stranger in this wilderness of London, I should not have dreamt of making,’ returned Nicholas.\n\n‘Wilderness! Yes, it is, it is. Good! It IS a wilderness,’ said the old man with much animation. ‘It was a wilderness to me once. I came here barefoot. I have never forgotten it. Thank God!’ and he raised his hat from his head, and looked very grave.\n\n‘What’s the matter? What is it? How did it all come about?’ said the old man, laying his hand on the shoulder of Nicholas, and walking him up the street. ‘You’re--Eh?’ laying his finger on the sleeve of his black coat. ‘Who’s it for, eh?’\n\n‘My father,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘Ah!’ said the old gentleman quickly. ‘Bad thing for a young man to lose his father. Widowed mother, perhaps?’\n\nNicholas sighed.\n\n‘Brothers and sisters too? Eh?’\n\n‘One sister,’ rejoined Nicholas.\n\n‘Poor thing, poor thing! You are a scholar too, I dare say?’ said the old man, looking wistfully into the face of the young one.\n\n‘I have been tolerably well educated,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Fine thing,’ said the old gentleman, ‘education a great thing: a very great thing! I never had any. I admire it the more in others. A very fine thing. Yes, yes. Tell me more of your history. Let me hear it all. No impertinent curiosity--no, no, no.’\n\nThere was something so earnest and guileless in the way in which all this was said, and such a complete disregard of all conventional restraints and coldnesses, that Nicholas could not resist it. Among men who have any sound and sterling qualities, there is nothing so contagious as pure openness of heart. Nicholas took the infection instantly, and ran over the main points of his little history without reserve: merely suppressing names, and touching as lightly as possible upon his uncle’s treatment of Kate. The old man listened with great attention, and when he had concluded, drew his arm eagerly through his own.\n\n‘Don’t say another word. Not another word’ said he. ‘Come along with me. We mustn’t lose a minute.’\n\nSo saying, the old gentleman dragged him back into Oxford Street, and hailing an omnibus on its way to the city, pushed Nicholas in before him, and followed himself.\n\nAs he appeared in a most extraordinary condition of restless excitement, and whenever Nicholas offered to speak, immediately interposed with: ‘Don’t say another word, my dear sir, on any account--not another word,’ the young man thought it better to attempt no further interruption. Into the city they journeyed accordingly, without interchanging any conversation; and the farther they went, the more Nicholas wondered what the end of the adventure could possibly be.\n\nThe old gentleman got out, with great alacrity, when they reached the Bank, and once more taking Nicholas by the arm, hurried him along Threadneedle Street, and through some lanes and passages on the right, until they, at length, emerged in a quiet shady little square. Into the oldest and cleanest-looking house of business in the square, he led the way. The only inscription on the door-post was ‘Cheeryble, Brothers;’ but from a hasty glance at the directions of some packages which were lying about, Nicholas supposed that the brothers Cheeryble were German merchants.\n\nPassing through a warehouse which presented every indication of a thriving business, Mr. Cheeryble (for such Nicholas supposed him to be, from the respect which had been shown him by the warehousemen and porters whom they passed) led him into a little partitioned-off counting-house like a large glass case, in which counting-house there sat--as free from dust and blemish as if he had been fixed into the glass case before the top was put on, and had never come out since--a fat, elderly, large-faced clerk, with silver spectacles and a powdered head.\n\n‘Is my brother in his room, Tim?’ said Mr. Cheeryble, with no less kindness of manner than he had shown to Nicholas.\n\n‘Yes, he is, sir,’ replied the fat clerk, turning his spectacle-glasses towards his principal, and his eyes towards Nicholas, ‘but Mr. Trimmers is with him.’\n\n‘Ay! And what has he come about, Tim?’ said Mr. Cheeryble.\n\n‘He is getting up a subscription for the widow and family of a man who was killed in the East India Docks this morning, sir,’ rejoined Tim. ‘Smashed, sir, by a cask of sugar.’\n\n‘He is a good creature,’ said Mr. Cheeryble, with great earnestness. ‘He is a kind soul. I am very much obliged to Trimmers. Trimmers is one of the best friends we have. He makes a thousand cases known to us that we should never discover of ourselves. I am VERY much obliged to Trimmers.’ Saying which, Mr. Cheeryble rubbed his hands with infinite delight, and Mr. Trimmers happening to pass the door that instant, on his way out, shot out after him and caught him by the hand.\n\n‘I owe you a thousand thanks, Trimmers, ten thousand thanks. I take it very friendly of you, very friendly indeed,’ said Mr. Cheeryble, dragging him into a corner to get out of hearing. ‘How many children are there, and what has my brother Ned given, Trimmers?’\n\n‘There are six children,’ replied the gentleman, ‘and your brother has given us twenty pounds.’\n\n‘My brother Ned is a good fellow, and you’re a good fellow too, Trimmers,’ said the old man, shaking him by both hands with trembling eagerness. ‘Put me down for another twenty--or--stop a minute, stop a minute. We mustn’t look ostentatious; put me down ten pound, and Tim Linkinwater ten pound. A cheque for twenty pound for Mr. Trimmers, Tim. God bless you, Trimmers--and come and dine with us some day this week; you’ll always find a knife and fork, and we shall be delighted. Now, my dear sir--cheque from Mr. Linkinwater, Tim. Smashed by a cask of sugar, and six poor children--oh dear, dear, dear!’\n\nTalking on in this strain, as fast as he could, to prevent any friendly remonstrances from the collector of the subscription on the large amount of his donation, Mr. Cheeryble led Nicholas, equally astonished and affected by what he had seen and heard in this short space, to the half-opened door of another room.\n\n‘Brother Ned,’ said Mr. Cheeryble, tapping with his knuckles, and stooping to listen, ‘are you busy, my dear brother, or can you spare time for a word or two with me?’\n\n‘Brother Charles, my dear fellow,’ replied a voice from the inside, so like in its tones to that which had just spoken, that Nicholas started, and almost thought it was the same, ‘don’t ask me such a question, but come in directly.’\n\nThey went in, without further parley. What was the amazement of Nicholas when his conductor advanced, and exchanged a warm greeting with another old gentleman, the very type and model of himself--the same face, the same figure, the same coat, waistcoat, and neckcloth, the same breeches and gaiters--nay, there was the very same white hat hanging against the wall!\n\nAs they shook each other by the hand: the face of each lighted up by beaming looks of affection, which would have been most delightful to behold in infants, and which, in men so old, was inexpressibly touching: Nicholas could observe that the last old gentleman was something stouter than his brother; this, and a slight additional shade of clumsiness in his gait and stature, formed the only perceptible difference between them. Nobody could have doubted their being twin brothers.\n\n‘Brother Ned,’ said Nicholas’s friend, closing the room-door, ‘here is a young friend of mine whom we must assist. We must make proper inquiries into his statements, in justice to him as well as to ourselves, and if they are confirmed--as I feel assured they will be--we must assist him, we must assist him, brother Ned.’\n\n‘It is enough, my dear brother, that you say we should,’ returned the other. ‘When you say that, no further inquiries are needed. He SHALL be assisted. What are his necessities, and what does he require? Where is Tim Linkinwater? Let us have him here.’\n\nBoth the brothers, it may be here remarked, had a very emphatic and earnest delivery; both had lost nearly the same teeth, which imparted the same peculiarity to their speech; and both spoke as if, besides possessing the utmost serenity of mind that the kindliest and most unsuspecting nature could bestow, they had, in collecting the plums from Fortune’s choicest pudding, retained a few for present use, and kept them in their mouths.\n\n‘Where is Tim Linkinwater?’ said brother Ned.\n\n‘Stop, stop, stop!’ said brother Charles, taking the other aside. ‘I’ve a plan, my dear brother, I’ve a plan. Tim is getting old, and Tim has been a faithful servant, brother Ned; and I don’t think pensioning Tim’s mother and sister, and buying a little tomb for the family when his poor brother died, was a sufficient recompense for his faithful services.’\n\n‘No, no, no,’ replied the other. ‘Certainly not. Not half enough, not half.’\n\n‘If we could lighten Tim’s duties,’ said the old gentleman, ‘and prevail upon him to go into the country, now and then, and sleep in the fresh air, besides, two or three times a week (which he could, if he began business an hour later in the morning), old Tim Linkinwater would grow young again in time; and he’s three good years our senior now. Old Tim Linkinwater young again! Eh, brother Ned, eh? Why, I recollect old Tim Linkinwater quite a little boy, don’t you? Ha, ha, ha! Poor Tim, poor Tim!’\n\nAnd the fine old fellows laughed pleasantly together: each with a tear of regard for old Tim Linkinwater standing in his eye.\n\n‘But hear this first--hear this first, brother Ned,’ said the old man, hastily, placing two chairs, one on each side of Nicholas: ‘I’ll tell it you myself, brother Ned, because the young gentleman is modest, and is a scholar, Ned, and I shouldn’t feel it right that he should tell us his story over and over again as if he was a beggar, or as if we doubted him. No, no no.’\n\n‘No, no, no,’ returned the other, nodding his head gravely. ‘Very right, my dear brother, very right.’\n\n‘He will tell me I’m wrong, if I make a mistake,’ said Nicholas’s friend. ‘But whether I do or not, you’ll be very much affected, brother Ned, remembering the time when we were two friendless lads, and earned our first shilling in this great city.’\n\nThe twins pressed each other’s hands in silence; and in his own homely manner, brother Charles related the particulars he had heard from Nicholas. The conversation which ensued was a long one, and when it was over, a secret conference of almost equal duration took place between brother Ned and Tim Linkinwater in another room. It is no disparagement to Nicholas to say, that before he had been closeted with the two brothers ten minutes, he could only wave his hand at every fresh expression of kindness and sympathy, and sob like a little child.\n\nAt length brother Ned and Tim Linkinwater came back together, when Tim instantly walked up to Nicholas and whispered in his ear in a very brief sentence (for Tim was ordinarily a man of few words), that he had taken down the address in the Strand, and would call upon him that evening, at eight. Having done which, Tim wiped his spectacles and put them on, preparatory to hearing what more the brothers Cheeryble had got to say.\n\n‘Tim,’ said brother Charles, ‘you understand that we have an intention of taking this young gentleman into the counting-house?’\n\nBrother Ned remarked that Tim was aware of that intention, and quite approved of it; and Tim having nodded, and said he did, drew himself up and looked particularly fat, and very important. After which, there was a profound silence.\n\n‘I’m not coming an hour later in the morning, you know,’ said Tim, breaking out all at once, and looking very resolute. ‘I’m not going to sleep in the fresh air; no, nor I’m not going into the country either. A pretty thing at this time of day, certainly. Pho!’\n\n‘Damn your obstinacy, Tim Linkinwater,’ said brother Charles, looking at him without the faintest spark of anger, and with a countenance radiant with attachment to the old clerk. ‘Damn your obstinacy, Tim Linkinwater, what do you mean, sir?’\n\n‘It’s forty-four year,’ said Tim, making a calculation in the air with his pen, and drawing an imaginary line before he cast it up, ‘forty-four year, next May, since I first kept the books of Cheeryble, Brothers. I’ve opened the safe every morning all that time (Sundays excepted) as the clock struck nine, and gone over the house every night at half-past ten (except on Foreign Post nights, and then twenty minutes before twelve) to see the doors fastened, and the fires out. I’ve never slept out of the back-attic one single night. There’s the same mignonette box in the middle of the window, and the same four flower-pots, two on each side, that I brought with me when I first came. There an’t--I’ve said it again and again, and I’ll maintain it--there an’t such a square as this in the world. I KNOW there an’t,’ said Tim, with sudden energy, and looking sternly about him. ‘Not one. For business or pleasure, in summer-time or winter--I don’t care which--there’s nothing like it. There’s not such a spring in England as the pump under the archway. There’s not such a view in England as the view out of my window; I’ve seen it every morning before I shaved, and I ought to know something about it. I have slept in that room,’ added Tim, sinking his voice a little, ‘for four-and-forty year; and if it wasn’t inconvenient, and didn’t interfere with business, I should request leave to die there.’\n\n‘Damn you, Tim Linkinwater, how dare you talk about dying?’ roared the twins by one impulse, and blowing their old noses violently.\n\n‘That’s what I’ve got to say, Mr. Edwin and Mr. Charles,’ said Tim, squaring his shoulders again. ‘This isn’t the first time you’ve talked about superannuating me; but, if you please, we’ll make it the last, and drop the subject for evermore.’\n\nWith these words, Tim Linkinwater stalked out, and shut himself up in his glass case, with the air of a man who had had his say, and was thoroughly resolved not to be put down.\n\nThe brothers interchanged looks, and coughed some half-dozen times without speaking.\n\n‘He must be done something with, brother Ned,’ said the other, warmly; ‘we must disregard his old scruples; they can’t be tolerated, or borne. He must be made a partner, brother Ned; and if he won’t submit to it peaceably, we must have recourse to violence.’\n\n‘Quite right,’ replied brother Ned, nodding his head as a man thoroughly determined; ‘quite right, my dear brother. If he won’t listen to reason, we must do it against his will, and show him that we are determined to exert our authority. We must quarrel with him, brother Charles.’\n\n‘We must. We certainly must have a quarrel with Tim Linkinwater,’ said the other. ‘But in the meantime, my dear brother, we are keeping our young friend; and the poor lady and her daughter will be anxious for his return. So let us say goodbye for the present, and--there, there--take care of that box, my dear sir--and--no, no, not a word now; but be careful of the crossings and--’\n\nAnd with any disjointed and unconnected words which would prevent Nicholas from pouring forth his thanks, the brothers hurried him out: shaking hands with him all the way, and affecting very unsuccessfully--they were poor hands at deception!--to be wholly unconscious of the feelings that completely mastered him.\n\nNicholas’s heart was too full to allow of his turning into the street until he had recovered some composure. When he at last glided out of the dark doorway corner in which he had been compelled to halt, he caught a glimpse of the twins stealthily peeping in at one corner of the glass case, evidently undecided whether they should follow up their late attack without delay, or for the present postpone laying further siege to the inflexible Tim Linkinwater.\n\nTo recount all the delight and wonder which the circumstances just detailed awakened at Miss La Creevy’s, and all the things that were done, said, thought, expected, hoped, and prophesied in consequence, is beside the present course and purpose of these adventures. It is sufficient to state, in brief, that Mr. Timothy Linkinwater arrived, punctual to his appointment; that, oddity as he was, and jealous, as he was bound to be, of the proper exercise of his employers’ most comprehensive liberality, he reported strongly and warmly in favour of Nicholas; and that, next day, he was appointed to the vacant stool in the counting-house of Cheeryble, Brothers, with a present salary of one hundred and twenty pounds a year.\n\n‘And I think, my dear brother,’ said Nicholas’s first friend, ‘that if we were to let them that little cottage at Bow which is empty, at something under the usual rent, now? Eh, brother Ned?’\n\n‘For nothing at all,’ said brother Ned. ‘We are rich, and should be ashamed to touch the rent under such circumstances as these. Where is Tim Linkinwater?--for nothing at all, my dear brother, for nothing at all.’\n\n‘Perhaps it would be better to say something, brother Ned,’ suggested the other, mildly; ‘it would help to preserve habits of frugality, you know, and remove any painful sense of overwhelming obligations. We might say fifteen pound, or twenty pound, and if it was punctually paid, make it up to them in some other way. And I might secretly advance a small loan towards a little furniture, and you might secretly advance another small loan, brother Ned; and if we find them doing well--as we shall; there’s no fear, no fear--we can change the loans into gifts. Carefully, brother Ned, and by degrees, and without pressing upon them too much; what do you say now, brother?’\n\nBrother Ned gave his hand upon it, and not only said it should be done, but had it done too; and, in one short week, Nicholas took possession of the stool, and Mrs. Nickleby and Kate took possession of the house, and all was hope, bustle, and light-heartedness.\n\nThere surely never was such a week of discoveries and surprises as the first week of that cottage. Every night when Nicholas came home, something new had been found out. One day it was a grapevine, and another day it was a boiler, and another day it was the key of the front-parlour closet at the bottom of the water-butt, and so on through a hundred items. Then, this room was embellished with a muslin curtain, and that room was rendered quite elegant by a window-blind, and such improvements were made, as no one would have supposed possible. Then there was Miss La Creevy, who had come out in the omnibus to stop a day or two and help, and who was perpetually losing a very small brown-paper parcel of tin tacks and a very large hammer, and running about with her sleeves tucked up at the wrists, and falling off pairs of steps and hurting herself very much--and Mrs. Nickleby, who talked incessantly, and did something now and then, but not often--and Kate, who busied herself noiselessly everywhere, and was pleased with everything--and Smike, who made the garden a perfect wonder to look upon--and Nicholas, who helped and encouraged them every one--all the peace and cheerfulness of home restored, with such new zest imparted to every frugal pleasure, and such delight to every hour of meeting, as misfortune and separation alone could give!\n\nIn short, the poor Nicklebys were social and happy; while the rich Nickleby was alone and miserable.\n\n# CHAPTER 36\n\nPrivate and confidential; relating to Family Matters. Showing how Mr Kenwigs underwent violent Agitation, and how Mrs. Kenwigs was as well as could be expected\nIt might have been seven o’clock in the evening, and it was growing dark in the narrow streets near Golden Square, when Mr. Kenwigs sent out for a pair of the cheapest white kid gloves--those at fourteen-pence--and selecting the strongest, which happened to be the right-hand one, walked downstairs with an air of pomp and much excitement, and proceeded to muffle the knob of the street-door knocker therein. Having executed this task with great nicety, Mr. Kenwigs pulled the door to, after him, and just stepped across the road to try the effect from the opposite side of the street. Satisfied that nothing could possibly look better in its way, Mr. Kenwigs then stepped back again, and calling through the keyhole to Morleena to open the door, vanished into the house, and was seen no longer.\n\nNow, considered as an abstract circumstance, there was no more obvious cause or reason why Mr. Kenwigs should take the trouble of muffling this particular knocker, than there would have been for his muffling the knocker of any nobleman or gentleman resident ten miles off; because, for the greater convenience of the numerous lodgers, the street-door always stood wide open, and the knocker was never used at all. The first floor, the second floor, and the third floor, had each a bell of its own. As to the attics, no one ever called on them; if anybody wanted the parlours, they were close at hand, and all he had to do was to walk straight into them; while the kitchen had a separate entrance down the area steps. As a question of mere necessity and usefulness, therefore, this muffling of the knocker was thoroughly incomprehensible.\n\nBut knockers may be muffled for other purposes than those of mere utilitarianism, as, in the present instance, was clearly shown. There are certain polite forms and ceremonies which must be observed in civilised life, or mankind relapse into their original barbarism. No genteel lady was ever yet confined--indeed, no genteel confinement can possibly take place--without the accompanying symbol of a muffled knocker. Mrs. Kenwigs was a lady of some pretensions to gentility; Mrs Kenwigs was confined. And, therefore, Mr. Kenwigs tied up the silent knocker on the premises in a white kid glove.\n\n‘I’m not quite certain neither,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, arranging his shirt-collar, and walking slowly upstairs, ‘whether, as it’s a boy, I won’t have it in the papers.’\n\nPondering upon the advisability of this step, and the sensation it was likely to create in the neighbourhood, Mr. Kenwigs betook himself to the sitting-room, where various extremely diminutive articles of clothing were airing on a horse before the fire, and Mr. Lumbey, the doctor, was dandling the baby--that is, the old baby--not the new one.\n\n‘It’s a fine boy, Mr. Kenwigs,’ said Mr. Lumbey, the doctor.\n\n‘You consider him a fine boy, do you, sir?’ returned Mr. Kenwigs.\n\n‘It’s the finest boy I ever saw in all my life,’ said the doctor. ‘I never saw such a baby.’\n\nIt is a pleasant thing to reflect upon, and furnishes a complete answer to those who contend for the gradual degeneration of the human species, that every baby born into the world is a finer one than the last.\n\n‘I ne--ver saw such a baby,’ said Mr. Lumbey, the doctor.\n\n‘Morleena was a fine baby,’ remarked Mr. Kenwigs; as if this were rather an attack, by implication, upon the family.\n\n‘They were all fine babies,’ said Mr. Lumbey. And Mr. Lumbey went on nursing the baby with a thoughtful look. Whether he was considering under what head he could best charge the nursing in the bill, was best known to himself.\n\nDuring this short conversation, Miss Morleena, as the eldest of the family, and natural representative of her mother during her indisposition, had been hustling and slapping the three younger Miss Kenwigses, without intermission; which considerate and affectionate conduct brought tears into the eyes of Mr. Kenwigs, and caused him to declare that, in understanding and behaviour, that child was a woman.\n\n‘She will be a treasure to the man she marries, sir,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, half aside; ‘I think she’ll marry above her station, Mr. Lumbey.’\n\n‘I shouldn’t wonder at all,’ replied the doctor.\n\n‘You never see her dance, sir, did you?’ asked Mr. Kenwigs.\n\nThe doctor shook his head.\n\n‘Ay!’ said Mr. Kenwigs, as though he pitied him from his heart, ‘then you don’t know what she’s capable of.’\n\nAll this time there had been a great whisking in and out of the other room; the door had been opened and shut very softly about twenty times a minute (for it was necessary to keep Mrs. Kenwigs quiet); and the baby had been exhibited to a score or two of deputations from a select body of female friends, who had assembled in the passage, and about the street-door, to discuss the event in all its bearings. Indeed, the excitement extended itself over the whole street, and groups of ladies might be seen standing at the doors, (some in the interesting condition in which Mrs. Kenwigs had last appeared in public,) relating their experiences of similar occurrences. Some few acquired great credit from having prophesied, the day before yesterday, exactly when it would come to pass; others, again, related, how that they guessed what it was, directly they saw Mr. Kenwigs turn pale and run up the street as hard as ever he could go. Some said one thing, and some another; but all talked together, and all agreed upon two points: first, that it was very meritorious and highly praiseworthy in Mrs. Kenwigs to do as she had done: and secondly, that there never was such a skilful and scientific doctor as that Dr Lumbey.\n\nIn the midst of this general hubbub, Dr Lumbey sat in the first-floor front, as before related, nursing the deposed baby, and talking to Mr Kenwigs. He was a stout bluff-looking gentleman, with no shirt-collar to speak of, and a beard that had been growing since yesterday morning; for Dr Lumbey was popular, and the neighbourhood was prolific; and there had been no less than three other knockers muffled, one after the other within the last forty-eight hours.\n\n‘Well, Mr. Kenwigs,’ said Dr Lumbey, ‘this makes six. You’ll have a fine family in time, sir.’\n\n‘I think six is almost enough, sir,’ returned Mr. Kenwigs.\n\n‘Pooh! pooh!’ said the doctor. ‘Nonsense! not half enough.’\n\nWith this, the doctor laughed; but he didn’t laugh half as much as a married friend of Mrs. Kenwigs’s, who had just come in from the sick chamber to report progress, and take a small sip of brandy-and-water: and who seemed to consider it one of the best jokes ever launched upon society.\n\n‘They’re not altogether dependent upon good fortune, neither,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, taking his second daughter on his knee; ‘they have expectations.’\n\n‘Oh, indeed!’ said Mr. Lumbey, the doctor.\n\n‘And very good ones too, I believe, haven’t they?’ asked the married lady.\n\n‘Why, ma’am,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, ‘it’s not exactly for me to say what they may be, or what they may not be. It’s not for me to boast of any family with which I have the honour to be connected; at the same time, Mrs Kenwigs’s is--I should say,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, abruptly, and raising his voice as he spoke, ‘that my children might come into a matter of a hundred pound apiece, perhaps. Perhaps more, but certainly that.’\n\n‘And a very pretty little fortune,’ said the married lady.\n\n‘There are some relations of Mrs. Kenwigs’s,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, taking a pinch of snuff from the doctor’s box, and then sneezing very hard, for he wasn’t used to it, ‘that might leave their hundred pound apiece to ten people, and yet not go begging when they had done it.’\n\n‘Ah! I know who you mean,’ observed the married lady, nodding her head.\n\n‘I made mention of no names, and I wish to make mention of no names,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, with a portentous look. ‘Many of my friends have met a relation of Mrs. Kenwigs’s in this very room, as would do honour to any company; that’s all.’\n\n‘I’ve met him,’ said the married lady, with a glance towards Dr Lumbey.\n\n‘It’s naterally very gratifying to my feelings as a father, to see such a man as that, a kissing and taking notice of my children,’ pursued Mr Kenwigs. ‘It’s naterally very gratifying to my feelings as a man, to know that man. It will be naterally very gratifying to my feelings as a husband, to make that man acquainted with this ewent.’\n\nHaving delivered his sentiments in this form of words, Mr. Kenwigs arranged his second daughter’s flaxen tail, and bade her be a good girl and mind what her sister, Morleena, said.\n\n‘That girl grows more like her mother every day,’ said Mr. Lumbey, suddenly stricken with an enthusiastic admiration of Morleena.\n\n‘There!’ rejoined the married lady. ‘What I always say; what I always did say! She’s the very picter of her.’ Having thus directed the general attention to the young lady in question, the married lady embraced the opportunity of taking another sip of the brandy-and-water--and a pretty long sip too.\n\n‘Yes! there is a likeness,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, after some reflection. ‘But such a woman as Mrs. Kenwigs was, afore she was married! Good gracious, such a woman!’\n\nMr. Lumbey shook his head with great solemnity, as though to imply that he supposed she must have been rather a dazzler.\n\n‘Talk of fairies!’ cried Mr. Kenwigs ‘I never see anybody so light to be alive, never. Such manners too; so playful, and yet so sewerely proper! As for her figure! It isn’t generally known,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, dropping his voice; ‘but her figure was such, at that time, that the sign of the Britannia, over in the Holloway Road, was painted from it!’\n\n‘But only see what it is now,’ urged the married lady. ‘Does SHE look like the mother of six?’\n\n‘Quite ridiculous,’ cried the doctor.\n\n‘She looks a deal more like her own daughter,’ said the married lady.\n\n‘So she does,’ assented Mr. Lumbey. ‘A great deal more.’\n\nMr. Kenwigs was about to make some further observations, most probably in confirmation of this opinion, when another married lady, who had looked in to keep up Mrs. Kenwigs’s spirits, and help to clear off anything in the eating and drinking way that might be going about, put in her head to announce that she had just been down to answer the bell, and that there was a gentleman at the door who wanted to see Mr. Kenwigs ‘most particular.’\n\nShadowy visions of his distinguished relation flitted through the brain of Mr. Kenwigs, as this message was delivered; and under their influence, he dispatched Morleena to show the gentleman up straightway.\n\n‘Why, I do declare,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, standing opposite the door so as to get the earliest glimpse of the visitor, as he came upstairs, ‘it’s Mr. Johnson! How do you find yourself, sir?’\n\nNicholas shook hands, kissed his old pupils all round, intrusted a large parcel of toys to the guardianship of Morleena, bowed to the doctor and the married ladies, and inquired after Mrs. Kenwigs in a tone of interest, which went to the very heart and soul of the nurse, who had come in to warm some mysterious compound, in a little saucepan over the fire.\n\n‘I ought to make a hundred apologies to you for calling at such a season,’ said Nicholas, ‘but I was not aware of it until I had rung the bell, and my time is so fully occupied now, that I feared it might be some days before I could possibly come again.’\n\n‘No time like the present, sir,’ said Mr. Kenwigs. ‘The sitiwation of Mrs Kenwigs, sir, is no obstacle to a little conversation between you and me, I hope?’\n\n‘You are very good,’ said Nicholas.\n\nAt this juncture, proclamation was made by another married lady, that the baby had begun to eat like anything; whereupon the two married ladies, already mentioned, rushed tumultuously into the bedroom to behold him in the act.\n\n‘The fact is,’ resumed Nicholas, ‘that before I left the country, where I have been for some time past, I undertook to deliver a message to you.’\n\n‘Ay, ay?’ said Mr. Kenwigs.\n\n‘And I have been,’ added Nicholas, ‘already in town for some days, without having had an opportunity of doing so.’\n\n‘It’s no matter, sir,’ said Mr. Kenwigs. ‘I dare say it’s none the worse for keeping cold. Message from the country!’ said Mr. Kenwigs, ruminating; ‘that’s curious. I don’t know anybody in the country.’\n\n‘Miss Petowker,’ suggested Nicholas.\n\n‘Oh! from her, is it?’ said Mr. Kenwigs. ‘Oh dear, yes. Ah! Mrs. Kenwigs will be glad to hear from her. Henrietta Petowker, eh? How odd things come about, now! That you should have met her in the country! Well!’\n\nHearing this mention of their old friend’s name, the four Miss Kenwigses gathered round Nicholas, open eyed and mouthed, to hear more. Mr. Kenwigs looked a little curious too, but quite comfortable and unsuspecting.\n\n‘The message relates to family matters,’ said Nicholas, hesitating.\n\n‘Oh, never mind,’ said Kenwigs, glancing at Mr. Lumbey, who, having rashly taken charge of little Lillyvick, found nobody disposed to relieve him of his precious burden. ‘All friends here.’\n\nNicholas hemmed once or twice, and seemed to have some difficulty in proceeding.\n\n‘At Portsmouth, Henrietta Petowker is,’ observed Mr. Kenwigs.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Nicholas, ‘Mr. Lillyvick is there.’\n\nMr. Kenwigs turned pale, but he recovered, and said, THAT was an odd coincidence also.\n\n‘The message is from him,’ said Nicholas.\n\nMr. Kenwigs appeared to revive. He knew that his niece was in a delicate state, and had, no doubt, sent word that they were to forward full particulars. Yes. That was very kind of him; so like him too!\n\n‘He desired me to give his kindest love,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Very much obliged to him, I’m sure. Your great-uncle, Lillyvick, my dears!’ interposed Mr. Kenwigs, condescendingly explaining it to the children.\n\n‘His kindest love,’ resumed Nicholas; ‘and to say that he had no time to write, but that he was married to Miss Petowker.’\n\nMr. Kenwigs started from his seat with a petrified stare, caught his second daughter by her flaxen tail, and covered his face with his pocket-handkerchief. Morleena fell, all stiff and rigid, into the baby’s chair, as she had seen her mother fall when she fainted away, and the two remaining little Kenwigses shrieked in affright.\n\n‘My children, my defrauded, swindled infants!’ cried Mr. Kenwigs, pulling so hard, in his vehemence, at the flaxen tail of his second daughter, that he lifted her up on tiptoe, and kept her, for some seconds, in that attitude. ‘Villain, ass, traitor!’\n\n‘Drat the man!’ cried the nurse, looking angrily around. ‘What does he mean by making that noise here?’\n\n‘Silence, woman!’ said Mr. Kenwigs, fiercely.\n\n‘I won’t be silent,’ returned the nurse. ‘Be silent yourself, you wretch. Have you no regard for your baby?’\n\n‘No!’ returned Mr. Kenwigs.\n\n‘More shame for you,’ retorted the nurse. ‘Ugh! you unnatural monster.’\n\n‘Let him die,’ cried Mr. Kenwigs, in the torrent of his wrath. ‘Let him die! He has no expectations, no property to come into. We want no babies here,’ said Mr. Kenwigs recklessly. ‘Take ‘em away, take ‘em away to the Fondling!’\n\nWith these awful remarks, Mr. Kenwigs sat himself down in a chair, and defied the nurse, who made the best of her way into the adjoining room, and returned with a stream of matrons: declaring that Mr. Kenwigs had spoken blasphemy against his family, and must be raving mad.\n\nAppearances were certainly not in Mr. Kenwigs’s favour, for the exertion of speaking with so much vehemence, and yet in such a tone as should prevent his lamentations reaching the ears of Mrs. Kenwigs, had made him very black in the face; besides which, the excitement of the occasion, and an unwonted indulgence in various strong cordials to celebrate it, had swollen and dilated his features to a most unusual extent. But, Nicholas and the doctor--who had been passive at first, doubting very much whether Mr. Kenwigs could be in earnest--interfering to explain the immediate cause of his condition, the indignation of the matrons was changed to pity, and they implored him, with much feeling, to go quietly to bed.\n\n‘The attention,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, looking around with a plaintive air, ‘the attention that I’ve shown to that man! The hyseters he has eat, and the pints of ale he has drank, in this house--!’\n\n‘It’s very trying, and very hard to bear, we know,’ said one of the married ladies; ‘but think of your dear darling wife.’\n\n‘Oh yes, and what she’s been a undergoing of, only this day,’ cried a great many voices. ‘There’s a good man, do.’\n\n‘The presents that have been made to him,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, reverting to his calamity, ‘the pipes, the snuff-boxes--a pair of india-rubber goloshes, that cost six-and-six--’\n\n‘Ah! it won’t bear thinking of, indeed,’ cried the matrons generally; ‘but it’ll all come home to him, never fear.’\n\nMr. Kenwigs looked darkly upon the ladies, as if he would prefer its all coming home to HIM, as there was nothing to be got by it; but he said nothing, and resting his head upon his hand, subsided into a kind of doze.\n\nThen, the matrons again expatiated on the expediency of taking the good gentleman to bed; observing that he would be better tomorrow, and that they knew what was the wear and tear of some men’s minds when their wives were taken as Mrs. Kenwigs had been that day, and that it did him great credit, and there was nothing to be ashamed of in it; far from it; they liked to see it, they did, for it showed a good heart. And one lady observed, as a case bearing upon the present, that her husband was often quite light-headed from anxiety on similar occasions, and that once, when her little Johnny was born, it was nearly a week before he came to himself again, during the whole of which time he did nothing but cry ‘Is it a boy, is it a boy?’ in a manner which went to the hearts of all his hearers.\n\nAt length, Morleena (who quite forgot she had fainted, when she found she was not noticed) announced that a chamber was ready for her afflicted parent; and Mr. Kenwigs, having partially smothered his four daughters in the closeness of his embrace, accepted the doctor’s arm on one side, and the support of Nicholas on the other, and was conducted upstairs to a bedroom which been secured for the occasion.\n\nHaving seen him sound asleep, and heard him snore most satisfactorily, and having further presided over the distribution of the toys, to the perfect contentment of all the little Kenwigses, Nicholas took his leave. The matrons dropped off one by one, with the exception of six or eight particular friends, who had determined to stop all night; the lights in the houses gradually disappeared; the last bulletin was issued that Mrs. Kenwigs was as well as could be expected; and the whole family were left to their repose.\n\n# CHAPTER 37\n\nNicholas finds further Favour in the Eyes of the brothers Cheeryble and Mr. Timothy Linkinwater. The brothers give a Banquet on a great Annual Occasion. Nicholas, on returning Home from it, receives a mysterious and important Disclosure from the Lips of Mrs. Nickleby\nThe square in which the counting-house of the brothers Cheeryble was situated, although it might not wholly realise the very sanguine expectations which a stranger would be disposed to form on hearing the fervent encomiums bestowed upon it by Tim Linkinwater, was, nevertheless, a sufficiently desirable nook in the heart of a busy town like London, and one which occupied a high place in the affectionate remembrances of several grave persons domiciled in the neighbourhood, whose recollections, however, dated from a much more recent period, and whose attachment to the spot was far less absorbing, than were the recollections and attachment of the enthusiastic Tim.\n\nAnd let not those whose eyes have been accustomed to the aristocratic gravity of Grosvenor Square and Hanover Square, the dowager barrenness and frigidity of Fitzroy Square, or the gravel walks and garden seats of the Squares of Russell and Euston, suppose that the affections of Tim Linkinwater, or the inferior lovers of this particular locality, had been awakened and kept alive by any refreshing associations with leaves, however dingy, or grass, however bare and thin. The city square has no enclosure, save the lamp-post in the middle: and no grass, but the weeds which spring up round its base. It is a quiet, little-frequented, retired spot, favourable to melancholy and contemplation, and appointments of long-waiting; and up and down its every side the Appointed saunters idly by the hour together wakening the echoes with the monotonous sound of his footsteps on the smooth worn stones, and counting, first the windows, and then the very bricks of the tall silent houses that hem him round about. In winter-time, the snow will linger there, long after it has melted from the busy streets and highways. The summer’s sun holds it in some respect, and while he darts his cheerful rays sparingly into the square, keeps his fiery heat and glare for noisier and less-imposing precincts. It is so quiet, that you can almost hear the ticking of your own watch when you stop to cool in its refreshing atmosphere. There is a distant hum--of coaches, not of insects--but no other sound disturbs the stillness of the square. The ticket porter leans idly against the post at the corner: comfortably warm, but not hot, although the day is broiling. His white apron flaps languidly in the air, his head gradually droops upon his breast, he takes very long winks with both eyes at once; even he is unable to withstand the soporific influence of the place, and is gradually falling asleep. But now, he starts into full wakefulness, recoils a step or two, and gazes out before him with eager wildness in his eye. Is it a job, or a boy at marbles? Does he see a ghost, or hear an organ? No; sight more unwonted still--there is a butterfly in the square--a real, live butterfly! astray from flowers and sweets, and fluttering among the iron heads of the dusty area railings.\n\nBut if there were not many matters immediately without the doors of Cheeryble Brothers, to engage the attention or distract the thoughts of the young clerk, there were not a few within, to interest and amuse him. There was scarcely an object in the place, animate or inanimate, which did not partake in some degree of the scrupulous method and punctuality of Mr. Timothy Linkinwater. Punctual as the counting-house dial, which he maintained to be the best time-keeper in London next after the clock of some old, hidden, unknown church hard by, (for Tim held the fabled goodness of that at the Horse Guards to be a pleasant fiction, invented by jealous West-enders,) the old clerk performed the minutest actions of the day, and arranged the minutest articles in the little room, in a precise and regular order, which could not have been exceeded if it had actually been a real glass case, fitted with the choicest curiosities. Paper, pens, ink, ruler, sealing-wax, wafers, pounce-box, string-box, fire-box, Tim’s hat, Tim’s scrupulously-folded gloves, Tim’s other coat--looking precisely like a back view of himself as it hung against the wall--all had their accustomed inches of space. Except the clock, there was not such an accurate and unimpeachable instrument in existence as the little thermometer which hung behind the door. There was not a bird of such methodical and business-like habits in all the world, as the blind blackbird, who dreamed and dozed away his days in a large snug cage, and had lost his voice, from old age, years before Tim first bought him. There was not such an eventful story in the whole range of anecdote, as Tim could tell concerning the acquisition of that very bird; how, compassionating his starved and suffering condition, he had purchased him, with the view of humanely terminating his wretched life; how he determined to wait three days and see whether the bird revived; how, before half the time was out, the bird did revive; and how he went on reviving and picking up his appetite and good looks until he gradually became what--‘what you see him now, sir,’--Tim would say, glancing proudly at the cage. And with that, Tim would utter a melodious chirrup, and cry ‘Dick;’ and Dick, who, for any sign of life he had previously given, might have been a wooden or stuffed representation of a blackbird indifferently executed, would come to the side of the cage in three small jumps, and, thrusting his bill between the bars, turn his sightless head towards his old master--and at that moment it would be very difficult to determine which of the two was the happier, the bird or Tim Linkinwater.\n\nNor was this all. Everything gave back, besides, some reflection of the kindly spirit of the brothers. The warehousemen and porters were such sturdy, jolly fellows, that it was a treat to see them. Among the shipping announcements and steam-packet lists which decorated the counting-house wall, were designs for almshouses, statements of charities, and plans for new hospitals. A blunderbuss and two swords hung above the chimney-piece, for the terror of evil-doers, but the blunderbuss was rusty and shattered, and the swords were broken and edgeless. Elsewhere, their open display in such a condition would have realised a smile; but, there, it seemed as though even violent and offensive weapons partook of the reigning influence, and became emblems of mercy and forbearance.\n\nSuch thoughts as these occurred to Nicholas very strongly, on the morning when he first took possession of the vacant stool, and looked about him, more freely and at ease, than he had before enjoyed an opportunity of doing. Perhaps they encouraged and stimulated him to exertion, for, during the next two weeks, all his spare hours, late at night and early in the morning, were incessantly devoted to acquiring the mysteries of book-keeping and some other forms of mercantile account. To these, he applied himself with such steadiness and perseverance that, although he brought no greater amount of previous knowledge to the subject than certain dim recollections of two or three very long sums entered into a ciphering-book at school, and relieved for parental inspection by the effigy of a fat swan tastefully flourished by the writing-master’s own hand, he found himself, at the end of a fortnight, in a condition to report his proficiency to Mr. Linkinwater, and to claim his promise that he, Nicholas Nickleby, should now be allowed to assist him in his graver labours.\n\nIt was a sight to behold Tim Linkinwater slowly bring out a massive ledger and day-book, and, after turning them over and over, and affectionately dusting their backs and sides, open the leaves here and there, and cast his eyes, half mournfully, half proudly, upon the fair and unblotted entries.\n\n‘Four-and-forty year, next May!’ said Tim. ‘Many new ledgers since then. Four-and-forty year!’\n\nTim closed the book again.\n\n‘Come, come,’ said Nicholas, ‘I am all impatience to begin.’\n\nTim Linkinwater shook his head with an air of mild reproof. Mr. Nickleby was not sufficiently impressed with the deep and awful nature of his undertaking. Suppose there should be any mistake--any scratching out!\n\nYoung men are adventurous. It is extraordinary what they will rush upon, sometimes. Without even taking the precaution of sitting himself down upon his stool, but standing leisurely at the desk, and with a smile upon his face--actually a smile--there was no mistake about it; Mr Linkinwater often mentioned it afterwards--Nicholas dipped his pen into the inkstand before him, and plunged into the books of Cheeryble Brothers!\n\nTim Linkinwater turned pale, and tilting up his stool on the two legs nearest Nicholas, looked over his shoulder in breathless anxiety. Brother Charles and brother Ned entered the counting-house together; but Tim Linkinwater, without looking round, impatiently waved his hand as a caution that profound silence must be observed, and followed the nib of the inexperienced pen with strained and eager eyes.\n\nThe brothers looked on with smiling faces, but Tim Linkinwater smiled not, nor moved for some minutes. At length, he drew a long slow breath, and still maintaining his position on the tilted stool, glanced at brother Charles, secretly pointed with the feather of his pen towards Nicholas, and nodded his head in a grave and resolute manner, plainly signifying ‘He’ll do.’\n\nBrother Charles nodded again, and exchanged a laughing look with brother Ned; but, just then, Nicholas stopped to refer to some other page, and Tim Linkinwater, unable to contain his satisfaction any longer, descended from his stool, and caught him rapturously by the hand.\n\n‘He has done it!’ said Tim, looking round at his employers and shaking his head triumphantly. ‘His capital B’s and D’s are exactly like mine; he dots all his small i’s and crosses every t as he writes it. There an’t such a young man as this in all London,’ said Tim, clapping Nicholas on the back; ‘not one. Don’t tell me! The city can’t produce his equal. I challenge the city to do it!’\n\nWith this casting down of his gauntlet, Tim Linkinwater struck the desk such a blow with his clenched fist, that the old blackbird tumbled off his perch with the start it gave him, and actually uttered a feeble croak, in the extremity of his astonishment.\n\n‘Well said, Tim--well said, Tim Linkinwater!’ cried brother Charles, scarcely less pleased than Tim himself, and clapping his hands gently as he spoke. ‘I knew our young friend would take great pains, and I was quite certain he would succeed, in no time. Didn’t I say so, brother Ned?’\n\n‘You did, my dear brother; certainly, my dear brother, you said so, and you were quite right,’ replied Ned. ‘Quite right. Tim Linkinwater is excited, but he is justly excited, properly excited. Tim is a fine fellow. Tim Linkinwater, sir--you’re a fine fellow.’\n\n‘Here’s a pleasant thing to think of!’ said Tim, wholly regardless of this address to himself, and raising his spectacles from the ledger to the brothers. ‘Here’s a pleasant thing. Do you suppose I haven’t often thought of what would become of these books when I was gone? Do you suppose I haven’t often thought that things might go on irregular and untidy here, after I was taken away? But now,’ said Tim, extending his forefinger towards Nicholas, ‘now, when I’ve shown him a little more, I’m satisfied. The business will go on, when I’m dead, as well as it did when I was alive--just the same--and I shall have the satisfaction of knowing that there never were such books--never were such books! No, nor never will be such books--as the books of Cheeryble Brothers.’\n\nHaving thus expressed his sentiments, Mr. Linkinwater gave vent to a short laugh, indicative of defiance to the cities of London and Westminster, and, turning again to his desk, quietly carried seventy-six from the last column he had added up, and went on with his work.\n\n‘Tim Linkinwater, sir,’ said brother Charles; ‘give me your hand, sir. This is your birthday. How dare you talk about anything else till you have been wished many happy returns of the day, Tim Linkinwater? God bless you, Tim! God bless you!’\n\n‘My dear brother,’ said the other, seizing Tim’s disengaged fist, ‘Tim Linkinwater looks ten years younger than he did on his last birthday.’\n\n‘Brother Ned, my dear boy,’ returned the other old fellow, ‘I believe that Tim Linkinwater was born a hundred and fifty years old, and is gradually coming down to five-and-twenty; for he’s younger every birthday than he was the year before.’\n\n‘So he is, brother Charles, so he is,’ replied brother Ned. ‘There’s not a doubt about it.’\n\n‘Remember, Tim,’ said brother Charles, ‘that we dine at half-past five today instead of two o’clock; we always depart from our usual custom on this anniversary, as you very well know, Tim Linkinwater. Mr. Nickleby, my dear sir, you will make one. Tim Linkinwater, give me your snuff-box as a remembrance to brother Charles and myself of an attached and faithful rascal, and take that, in exchange, as a feeble mark of our respect and esteem, and don’t open it until you go to bed, and never say another word upon the subject, or I’ll kill the blackbird. A dog! He should have had a golden cage half-a-dozen years ago, if it would have made him or his master a bit the happier. Now, brother Ned, my dear fellow, I’m ready. At half-past five, remember, Mr. Nickleby! Tim Linkinwater, sir, take care of Mr. Nickleby at half-past five. Now, brother Ned.’\n\nChattering away thus, according to custom, to prevent the possibility of any thanks or acknowledgment being expressed on the other side, the twins trotted off, arm-in-arm; having endowed Tim Linkinwater with a costly gold snuff-box, enclosing a bank note worth more than its value ten times told.\n\nAt a quarter past five o’clock, punctual to the minute, arrived, according to annual usage, Tim Linkinwater’s sister; and a great to-do there was, between Tim Linkinwater’s sister and the old housekeeper, respecting Tim Linkinwater’s sister’s cap, which had been dispatched, per boy, from the house of the family where Tim Linkinwater’s sister boarded, and had not yet come to hand: notwithstanding that it had been packed up in a bandbox, and the bandbox in a handkerchief, and the handkerchief tied on to the boy’s arm; and notwithstanding, too, that the place of its consignment had been duly set forth, at full length, on the back of an old letter, and the boy enjoined, under pain of divers horrible penalties, the full extent of which the eye of man could not foresee, to deliver the same with all possible speed, and not to loiter by the way. Tim Linkinwater’s sister lamented; the housekeeper condoled; and both kept thrusting their heads out of the second-floor window to see if the boy was ‘coming’--which would have been highly satisfactory, and, upon the whole, tantamount to his being come, as the distance to the corner was not quite five yards--when, all of a sudden, and when he was least expected, the messenger, carrying the bandbox with elaborate caution, appeared in an exactly opposite direction, puffing and panting for breath, and flushed with recent exercise; as well he might be; for he had taken the air, in the first instance, behind a hackney coach that went to Camberwell, and had followed two Punches afterwards and had seen the Stilts home to their own door. The cap was all safe, however--that was one comfort--and it was no use scolding him--that was another; so the boy went upon his way rejoicing, and Tim Linkinwater’s sister presented herself to the company below-stairs, just five minutes after the half-hour had struck by Tim Linkinwater’s own infallible clock.\n\nThe company consisted of the brothers Cheeryble, Tim Linkinwater, a ruddy-faced white-headed friend of Tim’s (who was a superannuated bank clerk), and Nicholas, who was presented to Tim Linkinwater’s sister with much gravity and solemnity. The party being now completed, brother Ned rang for dinner, and, dinner being shortly afterwards announced, led Tim Linkinwater’s sister into the next room, where it was set forth with great preparation. Then, brother Ned took the head of the table, and brother Charles the foot; and Tim Linkinwater’s sister sat on the left hand of brother Ned, and Tim Linkinwater himself on his right: and an ancient butler of apoplectic appearance, and with very short legs, took up his position at the back of brother Ned’s armchair, and, waving his right arm preparatory to taking off the covers with a flourish, stood bolt upright and motionless.\n\n‘For these and all other blessings, brother Charles,’ said Ned.\n\n‘Lord, make us truly thankful, brother Ned,’ said Charles.\n\nWhereupon the apoplectic butler whisked off the top of the soup tureen, and shot, all at once, into a state of violent activity.\n\nThere was abundance of conversation, and little fear of its ever flagging, for the good-humour of the glorious old twins drew everybody out, and Tim Linkinwater’s sister went off into a long and circumstantial account of Tim Linkinwater’s infancy, immediately after the very first glass of champagne--taking care to premise that she was very much Tim’s junior, and had only become acquainted with the facts from their being preserved and handed down in the family. This history concluded, brother Ned related how that, exactly thirty-five years ago, Tim Linkinwater was suspected to have received a love-letter, and how that vague information had been brought to the counting-house of his having been seen walking down Cheapside with an uncommonly handsome spinster; at which there was a roar of laughter, and Tim Linkinwater being charged with blushing, and called upon to explain, denied that the accusation was true; and further, that there would have been any harm in it if it had been; which last position occasioned the superannuated bank clerk to laugh tremendously, and to declare that it was the very best thing he had ever heard in his life, and that Tim Linkinwater might say a great many things before he said anything which would beat THAT.\n\nThere was one little ceremony peculiar to the day, both the matter and manner of which made a very strong impression upon Nicholas. The cloth having been removed and the decanters sent round for the first time, a profound silence succeeded, and in the cheerful faces of the brothers there appeared an expression, not of absolute melancholy, but of quiet thoughtfulness very unusual at a festive table. As Nicholas, struck by this sudden alteration, was wondering what it could portend, the brothers rose together, and the one at the top of the table leaning forward towards the other, and speaking in a low voice as if he were addressing him individually, said:\n\n‘Brother Charles, my dear fellow, there is another association connected with this day which must never be forgotten, and never can be forgotten, by you and me. This day, which brought into the world a most faithful and excellent and exemplary fellow, took from it the kindest and very best of parents, the very best of parents to us both. I wish that she could have seen us in our prosperity, and shared it, and had the happiness of knowing how dearly we loved her in it, as we did when we were two poor boys; but that was not to be. My dear brother--The Memory of our Mother.’\n\n‘Good Lord!’ thought Nicholas, ‘and there are scores of people of their own station, knowing all this, and twenty thousand times more, who wouldn’t ask these men to dinner because they eat with their knives and never went to school!’\n\nBut there was no time to moralise, for the joviality again became very brisk, and the decanter of port being nearly out, brother Ned pulled the bell, which was instantly answered by the apoplectic butler.\n\n‘David,’ said brother Ned.\n\n‘Sir,’ replied the butler.\n\n‘A magnum of the double-diamond, David, to drink the health of Mr Linkinwater.’\n\nInstantly, by a feat of dexterity, which was the admiration of all the company, and had been, annually, for some years past, the apoplectic butler, bringing his left hand from behind the small of his back, produced the bottle with the corkscrew already inserted; uncorked it at a jerk; and placed the magnum and the cork before his master with the dignity of conscious cleverness.\n\n‘Ha!’ said brother Ned, first examining the cork and afterwards filling his glass, while the old butler looked complacently and amiably on, as if it were all his own property, but the company were quite welcome to make free with it, ‘this looks well, David.’\n\n‘It ought to, sir,’ replied David. ‘You’d be troubled to find such a glass of wine as is our double-diamond, and that Mr. Linkinwater knows very well. That was laid down when Mr. Linkinwater first come: that wine was, gentlemen.’\n\n‘Nay, David, nay,’ interposed brother Charles.\n\n‘I wrote the entry in the cellar-book myself, sir, if you please,’ said David, in the tone of a man, quite confident in the strength of his facts. ‘Mr. Linkinwater had only been here twenty year, sir, when that pipe of double-diamond was laid down.’\n\n‘David is quite right, quite right, brother Charles,’ said Ned: ‘are the people here, David?’\n\n‘Outside the door, sir,’ replied the butler.\n\n‘Show ‘em in, David, show ‘em in.’\n\nAt this bidding, the older butler placed before his master a small tray of clean glasses, and opening the door admitted the jolly porters and warehousemen whom Nicholas had seen below. They were four in all, and as they came in, bowing, and grinning, and blushing, the housekeeper, and cook, and housemaid, brought up the rear.\n\n‘Seven,’ said brother Ned, filling a corresponding number of glasses with the double-diamond, ‘and David, eight. There! Now, you’re all of you to drink the health of your best friend Mr. Timothy Linkinwater, and wish him health and long life and many happy returns of this day, both for his own sake and that of your old masters, who consider him an inestimable treasure. Tim Linkinwater, sir, your health. Devil take you, Tim Linkinwater, sir, God bless you.’\n\nWith this singular contradiction of terms, brother Ned gave Tim Linkinwater a slap on the back, which made him look, for the moment, almost as apoplectic as the butler: and tossed off the contents of his glass in a twinkling.\n\nThe toast was scarcely drunk with all honour to Tim Linkinwater, when the sturdiest and jolliest subordinate elbowed himself a little in advance of his fellows, and exhibiting a very hot and flushed countenance, pulled a single lock of grey hair in the middle of his forehead as a respectful salute to the company, and delivered himself as follows--rubbing the palms of his hands very hard on a blue cotton handkerchief as he did so:\n\n‘We’re allowed to take a liberty once a year, gen’lemen, and if you please we’ll take it now; there being no time like the present, and no two birds in the hand worth one in the bush, as is well known--leastways in a contrairy sense, which the meaning is the same. (A pause--the butler unconvinced.) What we mean to say is, that there never was (looking at the butler)--such--(looking at the cook) noble--excellent--(looking everywhere and seeing nobody) free, generous-spirited masters as them as has treated us so handsome this day. And here’s thanking of ‘em for all their goodness as is so constancy a diffusing of itself over everywhere, and wishing they may live long and die happy!’\n\nWhen the foregoing speech was over--and it might have been much more elegant and much less to the purpose--the whole body of subordinates under command of the apoplectic butler gave three soft cheers; which, to that gentleman’s great indignation, were not very regular, inasmuch as the women persisted in giving an immense number of little shrill hurrahs among themselves, in utter disregard of the time. This done, they withdrew; shortly afterwards, Tim Linkinwater’s sister withdrew; in reasonable time after that, the sitting was broken up for tea and coffee, and a round game of cards.\n\nAt half-past ten--late hours for the square--there appeared a little tray of sandwiches and a bowl of bishop, which bishop coming on the top of the double-diamond, and other excitements, had such an effect upon Tim Linkinwater, that he drew Nicholas aside, and gave him to understand, confidentially, that it was quite true about the uncommonly handsome spinster, and that she was to the full as good-looking as she had been described--more so, indeed--but that she was in too much of a hurry to change her condition, and consequently, while Tim was courting her and thinking of changing his, got married to somebody else. ‘After all, I dare say it was my fault,’ said Tim. ‘I’ll show you a print I have got upstairs, one of these days. It cost me five-and-twenty shillings. I bought it soon after we were cool to each other. Don’t mention it, but it’s the most extraordinary accidental likeness you ever saw--her very portrait, sir!’\n\nBy this time it was past eleven o’clock; and Tim Linkinwater’s sister declaring that she ought to have been at home a full hour ago, a coach was procured, into which she was handed with great ceremony by brother Ned, while brother Charles imparted the fullest directions to the coachman, and besides paying the man a shilling over and above his fare, in order that he might take the utmost care of the lady, all but choked him with a glass of spirits of uncommon strength, and then nearly knocked all the breath out of his body in his energetic endeavours to knock it in again.\n\nAt length the coach rumbled off, and Tim Linkinwater’s sister being now fairly on her way home, Nicholas and Tim Linkinwater’s friend took their leaves together, and left old Tim and the worthy brothers to their repose.\n\nAs Nicholas had some distance to walk, it was considerably past midnight by the time he reached home, where he found his mother and Smike sitting up to receive him. It was long after their usual hour of retiring, and they had expected him, at the very latest, two hours ago; but the time had not hung heavily on their hands, for Mrs. Nickleby had entertained Smike with a genealogical account of her family by the mother’s side, comprising biographical sketches of the principal members, and Smike had sat wondering what it was all about, and whether it was learnt from a book, or said out of Mrs. Nickleby’s own head; so that they got on together very pleasantly.\n\nNicholas could not go to bed without expatiating on the excellences and munificence of the brothers Cheeryble, and relating the great success which had attended his efforts that day. But before he had said a dozen words, Mrs. Nickleby, with many sly winks and nods, observed, that she was sure Mr. Smike must be quite tired out, and that she positively must insist on his not sitting up a minute longer.\n\n‘A most biddable creature he is, to be sure,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, when Smike had wished them good-night and left the room. ‘I know you’ll excuse me, Nicholas, my dear, but I don’t like to do this before a third person; indeed, before a young man it would not be quite proper, though really, after all, I don’t know what harm there is in it, except that to be sure it’s not a very becoming thing, though some people say it is very much so, and really I don’t know why it should not be, if it’s well got up, and the borders are small-plaited; of course, a good deal depends upon that.’\n\nWith which preface, Mrs. Nickleby took her nightcap from between the leaves of a very large prayer-book where it had been folded up small, and proceeded to tie it on: talking away in her usual discursive manner, all the time.\n\n‘People may say what they like,’ observed Mrs. Nickleby, ‘but there’s a great deal of comfort in a nightcap, as I’m sure you would confess, Nicholas my dear, if you would only have strings to yours, and wear it like a Christian, instead of sticking it upon the very top of your head like a blue-coat boy. You needn’t think it an unmanly or quizzical thing to be particular about your nightcap, for I have often heard your poor dear papa, and the Reverend Mr. What’s-his-name, who used to read prayers in that old church with the curious little steeple that the weathercock was blown off the night week before you were born,--I have often heard them say, that the young men at college are uncommonly particular about their nightcaps, and that the Oxford nightcaps are quite celebrated for their strength and goodness; so much so, indeed, that the young men never dream of going to bed without ‘em, and I believe it’s admitted on all hands that THEY know what’s good, and don’t coddle themselves.’\n\nNicholas laughed, and entering no further into the subject of this lengthened harangue, reverted to the pleasant tone of the little birthday party. And as Mrs. Nickleby instantly became very curious respecting it, and made a great number of inquiries touching what they had had for dinner, and how it was put on table, and whether it was overdone or underdone, and who was there, and what ‘the Mr. Cherrybles’ said, and what Nicholas said, and what the Mr. Cherrybles said when he said that; Nicholas described the festivities at full length, and also the occurrences of the morning.\n\n‘Late as it is,’ said Nicholas, ‘I am almost selfish enough to wish that Kate had been up to hear all this. I was all impatience, as I came along, to tell her.’\n\n‘Why, Kate,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, putting her feet upon the fender, and drawing her chair close to it, as if settling herself for a long talk. ‘Kate has been in bed--oh! a couple of hours--and I’m very glad, Nicholas my dear, that I prevailed upon her not to sit up, for I wished very much to have an opportunity of saying a few words to you. I am naturally anxious about it, and of course it’s a very delightful and consoling thing to have a grown-up son that one can put confidence in, and advise with; indeed I don’t know any use there would be in having sons at all, unless people could put confidence in them.’\n\nNicholas stopped in the middle of a sleepy yawn, as his mother began to speak: and looked at her with fixed attention.\n\n‘There was a lady in our neighbourhood,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘speaking of sons puts me in mind of it--a lady in our neighbourhood when we lived near Dawlish, I think her name was Rogers; indeed I am sure it was if it wasn’t Murphy, which is the only doubt I have--’\n\n‘Is it about her, mother, that you wished to speak to me?’ said Nicholas quietly.\n\n‘About HER!’ cried Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Good gracious, Nicholas, my dear, how CAN you be so ridiculous! But that was always the way with your poor dear papa,--just his way--always wandering, never able to fix his thoughts on any one subject for two minutes together. I think I see him now!’ said Mrs. Nickleby, wiping her eyes, ‘looking at me while I was talking to him about his affairs, just as if his ideas were in a state of perfect conglomeration! Anybody who had come in upon us suddenly, would have supposed I was confusing and distracting him instead of making things plainer; upon my word they would.’\n\n‘I am very sorry, mother, that I should inherit this unfortunate slowness of apprehension,’ said Nicholas, kindly; ‘but I’ll do my best to understand you, if you’ll only go straight on: indeed I will.’\n\n‘Your poor pa!’ said Mrs. Nickleby, pondering. ‘He never knew, till it was too late, what I would have had him do!’\n\nThis was undoubtedly the case, inasmuch as the deceased Mr. Nickleby had not arrived at the knowledge when he died. Neither had Mrs. Nickleby herself; which is, in some sort, an explanation of the circumstance.\n\n‘However,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, drying her tears, ‘this has nothing to do--certainly nothing whatever to do--with the gentleman in the next house.’\n\n‘I should suppose that the gentleman in the next house has as little to do with us,’ returned Nicholas.\n\n‘There can be no doubt,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘that he IS a gentleman, and has the manners of a gentleman, and the appearance of a gentleman, although he does wear smalls and grey worsted stockings. That may be eccentricity, or he may be proud of his legs. I don’t see why he shouldn’t be. The Prince Regent was proud of his legs, and so was Daniel Lambert, who was also a fat man; HE was proud of his legs. So was Miss Biffin: she was--no,’ added Mrs. Nickleby, correcting, herself, ‘I think she had only toes, but the principle is the same.’\n\nNicholas looked on, quite amazed at the introduction of this new theme. Which seemed just what Mrs. Nickleby had expected him to be.\n\n‘You may well be surprised, Nicholas, my dear,’ she said, ‘I am sure I was. It came upon me like a flash of fire, and almost froze my blood. The bottom of his garden joins the bottom of ours, and of course I had several times seen him sitting among the scarlet-beans in his little arbour, or working at his little hot-beds. I used to think he stared rather, but I didn’t take any particular notice of that, as we were newcomers, and he might be curious to see what we were like. But when he began to throw his cucumbers over our wall--’\n\n‘To throw his cucumbers over our wall!’ repeated Nicholas, in great astonishment.\n\n‘Yes, Nicholas, my dear,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby in a very serious tone; ‘his cucumbers over our wall. And vegetable marrows likewise.’\n\n‘Confound his impudence!’ said Nicholas, firing immediately. ‘What does he mean by that?’\n\n‘I don’t think he means it impertinently at all,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘What!’ said Nicholas, ‘cucumbers and vegetable marrows flying at the heads of the family as they walk in their own garden, and not meant impertinently! Why, mother--’\n\nNicholas stopped short; for there was an indescribable expression of placid triumph, mingled with a modest confusion, lingering between the borders of Mrs. Nickleby’s nightcap, which arrested his attention suddenly.\n\n‘He must be a very weak, and foolish, and inconsiderate man,’ said Mrs. Nickleby; ‘blamable indeed--at least I suppose other people would consider him so; of course I can’t be expected to express any opinion on that point, especially after always defending your poor dear papa when other people blamed him for making proposals to me; and to be sure there can be no doubt that he has taken a very singular way of showing it. Still at the same time, his attentions are--that is, as far as it goes, and to a certain extent of course--a flattering sort of thing; and although I should never dream of marrying again with a dear girl like Kate still unsettled in life--’\n\n‘Surely, mother, such an idea never entered your brain for an instant?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Bless my heart, Nicholas my dear,’ returned his mother in a peevish tone, ‘isn’t that precisely what I am saying, if you would only let me speak? Of course, I never gave it a second thought, and I am surprised and astonished that you should suppose me capable of such a thing. All I say is, what step is the best to take, so as to reject these advances civilly and delicately, and without hurting his feelings too much, and driving him to despair, or anything of that kind? My goodness me!’ exclaimed Mrs. Nickleby, with a half-simper, ‘suppose he was to go doing anything rash to himself. Could I ever be happy again, Nicholas?’\n\nDespite his vexation and concern, Nicholas could scarcely help smiling, as he rejoined, ‘Now, do you think, mother, that such a result would be likely to ensue from the most cruel repulse?’\n\n‘Upon my word, my dear, I don’t know,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby; ‘really, I don’t know. I am sure there was a case in the day before yesterday’s paper, extracted from one of the French newspapers, about a journeyman shoemaker who was jealous of a young girl in an adjoining village, because she wouldn’t shut herself up in an air-tight three-pair-of-stairs, and charcoal herself to death with him; and who went and hid himself in a wood with a sharp-pointed knife, and rushed out, as she was passing by with a few friends, and killed himself first, and then all the friends, and then her--no, killed all the friends first, and then herself, and then HIMself--which it is quite frightful to think of. Somehow or other,’ added Mrs. Nickleby, after a momentary pause, ‘they always ARE journeyman shoemakers who do these things in France, according to the papers. I don’t know how it is--something in the leather, I suppose.’\n\n‘But this man, who is not a shoemaker--what has he done, mother, what has he said?’ inquired Nicholas, fretted almost beyond endurance, but looking nearly as resigned and patient as Mrs. Nickleby herself. ‘You know, there is no language of vegetables, which converts a cucumber into a formal declaration of attachment.’\n\n‘My dear,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby, tossing her head and looking at the ashes in the grate, ‘he has done and said all sorts of things.’\n\n‘Is there no mistake on your part?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘Mistake!’ cried Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Lord, Nicholas my dear, do you suppose I don’t know when a man’s in earnest?’\n\n‘Well, well!’ muttered Nicholas.\n\n‘Every time I go to the window,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘he kisses one hand, and lays the other upon his heart--of course it’s very foolish of him to do so, and I dare say you’ll say it’s very wrong, but he does it very respectfully--very respectfully indeed--and very tenderly, extremely tenderly. So far, he deserves the greatest credit; there can be no doubt about that. Then, there are the presents which come pouring over the wall every day, and very fine they certainly are, very fine; we had one of the cucumbers at dinner yesterday, and think of pickling the rest for next winter. And last evening,’ added Mrs. Nickleby, with increased confusion, ‘he called gently over the wall, as I was walking in the garden, and proposed marriage, and an elopement. His voice is as clear as a bell or a musical glass--very like a musical glass indeed--but of course I didn’t listen to it. Then, the question is, Nicholas my dear, what am I to do?’\n\n‘Does Kate know of this?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘I have not said a word about it yet,’ answered his mother.\n\n‘Then, for Heaven’s sake,’ rejoined Nicholas, rising, ‘do not, for it would make her very unhappy. And with regard to what you should do, my dear mother, do what your good sense and feeling, and respect for my father’s memory, would prompt. There are a thousand ways in which you can show your dislike of these preposterous and doting attentions. If you act as decidedly as you ought and they are still continued, and to your annoyance, I can speedily put a stop to them. But I should not interfere in a matter so ridiculous, and attach importance to it, until you have vindicated yourself. Most women can do that, but especially one of your age and condition, in circumstances like these, which are unworthy of a serious thought. I would not shame you by seeming to take them to heart, or treat them earnestly for an instant. Absurd old idiot!’\n\nSo saying, Nicholas kissed his mother, and bade her good-night, and they retired to their respective chambers.\n\nTo do Mrs. Nickleby justice, her attachment to her children would have prevented her seriously contemplating a second marriage, even if she could have so far conquered her recollections of her late husband as to have any strong inclinations that way. But, although there was no evil and little real selfishness in Mrs. Nickleby’s heart, she had a weak head and a vain one; and there was something so flattering in being sought (and vainly sought) in marriage at this time of day, that she could not dismiss the passion of the unknown gentleman quite so summarily or lightly as Nicholas appeared to deem becoming.\n\n‘As to its being preposterous, and doting, and ridiculous,’ thought Mrs Nickleby, communing with herself in her own room, ‘I don’t see that, at all. It’s hopeless on his part, certainly; but why he should be an absurd old idiot, I confess I don’t see. He is not to be supposed to know it’s hopeless. Poor fellow! He is to be pitied, I think!’\n\nHaving made these reflections, Mrs. Nickleby looked in her little dressing-glass, and walking backward a few steps from it, tried to remember who it was who used to say that when Nicholas was one-and-twenty he would have more the appearance of her brother than her son. Not being able to call the authority to mind, she extinguished her candle, and drew up the window-blind to admit the light of morning, which had, by this time, begun to dawn.\n\n‘It’s a bad light to distinguish objects in,’ murmured Mrs. Nickleby, peering into the garden, ‘and my eyes are not very good--I was short-sighted from a child--but, upon my word, I think there’s another large vegetable marrow sticking, at this moment, on the broken glass bottles at the top of the wall!’\n\n# CHAPTER 38\n\nComprises certain Particulars arising out of a Visit of Condolence, which may prove important hereafter. Smike unexpectedly encounters a very old Friend, who invites him to his House, and will take no Denial\n\nQuite unconscious of the demonstrations of their amorous neighbour, or their effects upon the susceptible bosom of her mama, Kate Nickleby had, by this time, begun to enjoy a settled feeling of tranquillity and happiness, to which, even in occasional and transitory glimpses, she had long been a stranger. Living under the same roof with the beloved brother from whom she had been so suddenly and hardly separated: with a mind at ease, and free from any persecutions which could call a blush into her cheek, or a pang into her heart, she seemed to have passed into a new state of being. Her former cheerfulness was restored, her step regained its elasticity and lightness, the colour which had forsaken her cheek visited it once again, and Kate Nickleby looked more beautiful than ever.\n\nSuch was the result to which Miss La Creevy’s ruminations and observations led her, when the cottage had been, as she emphatically said, ‘thoroughly got to rights, from the chimney-pots to the street-door scraper,’ and the busy little woman had at length a moment’s time to think about its inmates.\n\n‘Which I declare I haven’t had since I first came down here,’ said Miss La Creevy; ‘for I have thought of nothing but hammers, nails, screwdrivers, and gimlets, morning, noon, and night.’\n\n‘You never bestowed one thought upon yourself, I believe,’ returned Kate, smiling.\n\n‘Upon my word, my dear, when there are so many pleasanter things to think of, I should be a goose if I did,’ said Miss La Creevy. ‘By-the-bye, I HAVE thought of somebody too. Do you know, that I observe a great change in one of this family--a very extraordinary change?’\n\n‘In whom?’ asked Kate, anxiously. ‘Not in--’\n\n‘Not in your brother, my dear,’ returned Miss La Creevy, anticipating the close of the sentence, ‘for he is always the same affectionate good-natured clever creature, with a spice of the--I won’t say who--in him when there’s any occasion, that he was when I first knew you. No. Smike, as he WILL be called, poor fellow! for he won’t hear of a MR before his name, is greatly altered, even in this short time.’\n\n‘How?’ asked Kate. ‘Not in health?’\n\n‘N--n--o; perhaps not in health exactly,’ said Miss La Creevy, pausing to consider, ‘although he is a worn and feeble creature, and has that in his face which it would wring my heart to see in yours. No; not in health.’\n\n‘How then?’\n\n‘I scarcely know,’ said the miniature painter. ‘But I have watched him, and he has brought the tears into my eyes many times. It is not a very difficult matter to do that, certainly, for I am easily melted; still I think these came with good cause and reason. I am sure that since he has been here, he has grown, from some strong cause, more conscious of his weak intellect. He feels it more. It gives him greater pain to know that he wanders sometimes, and cannot understand very simple things. I have watched him when you have not been by, my dear, sit brooding by himself, with such a look of pain as I could scarcely bear to see, and then get up and leave the room: so sorrowfully, and in such dejection, that I cannot tell you how it has hurt me. Not three weeks ago, he was a light-hearted busy creature, overjoyed to be in a bustle, and as happy as the day was long. Now, he is another being--the same willing, harmless, faithful, loving creature--but the same in nothing else.’\n\n‘Surely this will all pass off,’ said Kate. ‘Poor fellow!’\n\n‘I hope,’ returned her little friend, with a gravity very unusual in her, ‘it may. I hope, for the sake of that poor lad, it may. However,’ said Miss La Creevy, relapsing into the cheerful, chattering tone, which was habitual to her, ‘I have said my say, and a very long say it is, and a very wrong say too, I shouldn’t wonder at all. I shall cheer him up tonight, at all events, for if he is to be my squire all the way to the Strand, I shall talk on, and on, and on, and never leave off, till I have roused him into a laugh at something. So the sooner he goes, the better for him, and the sooner I go, the better for me, I am sure, or else I shall have my maid gallivanting with somebody who may rob the house--though what there is to take away, besides tables and chairs, I don’t know, except the miniatures: and he is a clever thief who can dispose of them to any great advantage, for I can’t, I know, and that’s the honest truth.’\n\nSo saying, little Miss La Creevy hid her face in a very flat bonnet, and herself in a very big shawl; and fixing herself tightly into the latter, by means of a large pin, declared that the omnibus might come as soon as it pleased, for she was quite ready.\n\nBut there was still Mrs. Nickleby to take leave of; and long before that good lady had concluded some reminiscences bearing upon, and appropriate to, the occasion, the omnibus arrived. This put Miss La Creevy in a great bustle, in consequence whereof, as she secretly rewarded the servant girl with eighteen-pence behind the street-door, she pulled out of her reticule ten-pennyworth of halfpence, which rolled into all possible corners of the passage, and occupied some considerable time in the picking up. This ceremony had, of course, to be succeeded by a second kissing of Kate and Mrs. Nickleby, and a gathering together of the little basket and the brown-paper parcel, during which proceedings, ‘the omnibus,’ as Miss La Creevy protested, ‘swore so dreadfully, that it was quite awful to hear it.’ At length and at last, it made a feint of going away, and then Miss La Creevy darted out, and darted in, apologising with great volubility to all the passengers, and declaring that she wouldn’t purposely have kept them waiting on any account whatever. While she was looking about for a convenient seat, the conductor pushed Smike in, and cried that it was all right--though it wasn’t--and away went the huge vehicle, with the noise of half-a-dozen brewers’ drays at least.\n\nLeaving it to pursue its journey at the pleasure of the conductor aforementioned, who lounged gracefully on his little shelf behind, smoking an odoriferous cigar; and leaving it to stop, or go on, or gallop, or crawl, as that gentleman deemed expedient and advisable; this narrative may embrace the opportunity of ascertaining the condition of Sir Mulberry Hawk, and to what extent he had, by this time, recovered from the injuries consequent on being flung violently from his cabriolet, under the circumstances already detailed.\n\nWith a shattered limb, a body severely bruised, a face disfigured by half-healed scars, and pallid from the exhaustion of recent pain and fever, Sir Mulberry Hawk lay stretched upon his back, on the couch to which he was doomed to be a prisoner for some weeks yet to come. Mr. Pyke and Mr. Pluck sat drinking hard in the next room, now and then varying the monotonous murmurs of their conversation with a half-smothered laugh, while the young lord--the only member of the party who was not thoroughly irredeemable, and who really had a kind heart--sat beside his Mentor, with a cigar in his mouth, and read to him, by the light of a lamp, such scraps of intelligence from a paper of the day, as were most likely to yield him interest or amusement.\n\n‘Curse those hounds!’ said the invalid, turning his head impatiently towards the adjoining room; ‘will nothing stop their infernal throats?’\n\nMessrs Pyke and Pluck heard the exclamation, and stopped immediately: winking to each other as they did so, and filling their glasses to the brim, as some recompense for the deprivation of speech.\n\n‘Damn!’ muttered the sick man between his teeth, and writhing impatiently in his bed. ‘Isn’t this mattress hard enough, and the room dull enough, and pain bad enough, but THEY must torture me? What’s the time?’\n\n‘Half-past eight,’ replied his friend.\n\n‘Here, draw the table nearer, and let us have the cards again,’ said Sir Mulberry. ‘More piquet. Come.’\n\nIt was curious to see how eagerly the sick man, debarred from any change of position save the mere turning of his head from side to side, watched every motion of his friend in the progress of the game; and with what eagerness and interest he played, and yet how warily and coolly. His address and skill were more than twenty times a match for his adversary, who could make little head against them, even when fortune favoured him with good cards, which was not often the case. Sir Mulberry won every game; and when his companion threw down the cards, and refused to play any longer, thrust forth his wasted arm and caught up the stakes with a boastful oath, and the same hoarse laugh, though considerably lowered in tone, that had resounded in Ralph Nickleby’s dining-room, months before.\n\nWhile he was thus occupied, his man appeared, to announce that Mr. Ralph Nickleby was below, and wished to know how he was, tonight.\n\n‘Better,’ said Sir Mulberry, impatiently.\n\n‘Mr. Nickleby wishes to know, sir--’\n\n‘I tell you, better,’ replied Sir Mulberry, striking his hand upon the table.\n\nThe man hesitated for a moment or two, and then said that Mr. Nickleby had requested permission to see Sir Mulberry Hawk, if it was not inconvenient.\n\n‘It IS inconvenient. I can’t see him. I can’t see anybody,’ said his master, more violently than before. ‘You know that, you blockhead.’\n\n‘I am very sorry, sir,’ returned the man. ‘But Mr. Nickleby pressed so much, sir--’\n\nThe fact was, that Ralph Nickleby had bribed the man, who, being anxious to earn his money with a view to future favours, held the door in his hand, and ventured to linger still.\n\n‘Did he say whether he had any business to speak about?’ inquired Sir Mulberry, after a little impatient consideration.\n\n‘No, sir. He said he wished to see you, sir. Particularly, Mr. Nickleby said, sir.’\n\n‘Tell him to come up. Here,’ cried Sir Mulberry, calling the man back, as he passed his hand over his disfigured face, ‘move that lamp, and put it on the stand behind me. Wheel that table away, and place a chair there--further off. Leave it so.’\n\nThe man obeyed these directions as if he quite comprehended the motive with which they were dictated, and left the room. Lord Frederick Verisopht, remarking that he would look in presently, strolled into the adjoining apartment, and closed the folding door behind him.\n\nThen was heard a subdued footstep on the stairs; and Ralph Nickleby, hat in hand, crept softly into the room, with his body bent forward as if in profound respect, and his eyes fixed upon the face of his worthy client.\n\n‘Well, Nickleby,’ said Sir Mulberry, motioning him to the chair by the couch side, and waving his hand in assumed carelessness, ‘I have had a bad accident, you see.’\n\n‘I see,’ rejoined Ralph, with the same steady gaze. ‘Bad, indeed! I should not have known you, Sir Mulberry. Dear, dear! This IS bad.’\n\nRalph’s manner was one of profound humility and respect; and the low tone of voice was that, which the gentlest consideration for a sick man would have taught a visitor to assume. But the expression of his face, Sir Mulberry’s being averted, was in extraordinary contrast; and as he stood, in his usual attitude, calmly looking on the prostrate form before him, all that part of his features which was not cast into shadow by his protruding and contracted brows, bore the impress of a sarcastic smile.\n\n‘Sit down,’ said Sir Mulberry, turning towards him, as though by a violent effort. ‘Am I a sight, that you stand gazing there?’\n\nAs he turned his face, Ralph recoiled a step or two, and making as though he were irresistibly impelled to express astonishment, but was determined not to do so, sat down with well-acted confusion.\n\n‘I have inquired at the door, Sir Mulberry, every day,’ said Ralph, ‘twice a day, indeed, at first--and tonight, presuming upon old acquaintance, and past transactions by which we have mutually benefited in some degree, I could not resist soliciting admission to your chamber. Have you--have you suffered much?’ said Ralph, bending forward, and allowing the same harsh smile to gather upon his face, as the other closed his eyes.\n\n‘More than enough to please me, and less than enough to please some broken-down hacks that you and I know of, and who lay their ruin between us, I dare say,’ returned Sir Mulberry, tossing his arm restlessly upon the coverlet.\n\nRalph shrugged his shoulders in deprecation of the intense irritation with which this had been said; for there was an aggravating, cold distinctness in his speech and manner which so grated on the sick man that he could scarcely endure it.\n\n‘And what is it in these “past transactions,” that brought you here tonight?’ asked Sir Mulberry.\n\n‘Nothing,’ replied Ralph. ‘There are some bills of my lord’s which need renewal; but let them be till you are well. I--I--came,’ said Ralph, speaking more slowly, and with harsher emphasis, ‘I came to say how grieved I am that any relative of mine, although disowned by me, should have inflicted such punishment on you as--’\n\n‘Punishment!’ interposed Sir Mulberry.\n\n‘I know it has been a severe one,’ said Ralph, wilfully mistaking the meaning of the interruption, ‘and that has made me the more anxious to tell you that I disown this vagabond--that I acknowledge him as no kin of mine--and that I leave him to take his deserts from you, and every man besides. You may wring his neck if you please. I shall not interfere.’\n\n‘This story that they tell me here, has got abroad then, has it?’ asked Sir Mulberry, clenching his hands and teeth.\n\n‘Noised in all directions,’ replied Ralph. ‘Every club and gaming-room has rung with it. There has been a good song made about it, as I am told,’ said Ralph, looking eagerly at his questioner. ‘I have not heard it myself, not being in the way of such things, but I have been told it’s even printed--for private circulation--but that’s all over town, of course.’\n\n‘It’s a lie!’ said Sir Mulberry; ‘I tell you it’s all a lie. The mare took fright.’\n\n‘They SAY he frightened her,’ observed Ralph, in the same unmoved and quiet manner. ‘Some say he frightened you, but THAT’S a lie, I know. I have said that boldly--oh, a score of times! I am a peaceable man, but I can’t hear folks tell that of you. No, no.’\n\nWhen Sir Mulberry found coherent words to utter, Ralph bent forward with his hand to his ear, and a face as calm as if its every line of sternness had been cast in iron.\n\n‘When I am off this cursed bed,’ said the invalid, actually striking at his broken leg in the ecstasy of his passion, ‘I’ll have such revenge as never man had yet. By God, I will. Accident favouring him, he has marked me for a week or two, but I’ll put a mark on him that he shall carry to his grave. I’ll slit his nose and ears, flog him, maim him for life. I’ll do more than that; I’ll drag that pattern of chastity, that pink of prudery, the delicate sister, through--’\n\nIt might have been that even Ralph’s cold blood tingled in his cheeks at that moment. It might have been that Sir Mulberry remembered, that, knave and usurer as he was, he must, in some early time of infancy, have twined his arm about her father’s neck. He stopped, and menacing with his hand, confirmed the unuttered threat with a tremendous oath.\n\n‘It is a galling thing,’ said Ralph, after a short term of silence, during which he had eyed the sufferer keenly, ‘to think that the man about town, the rake, the ROUE, the rook of twenty seasons should be brought to this pass by a mere boy!’\n\nSir Mulberry darted a wrathful look at him, but Ralph’s eyes were bent upon the ground, and his face wore no other expression than one of thoughtfulness.\n\n‘A raw, slight stripling,’ continued Ralph, ‘against a man whose very weight might crush him; to say nothing of his skill in--I am right, I think,’ said Ralph, raising his eyes, ‘you WERE a patron of the ring once, were you not?’\n\nThe sick man made an impatient gesture, which Ralph chose to consider as one of acquiescence.\n\n‘Ha!’ he said, ‘I thought so. That was before I knew you, but I was pretty sure I couldn’t be mistaken. He is light and active, I suppose. But those were slight advantages compared with yours. Luck, luck! These hang-dog outcasts have it.’\n\n‘He’ll need the most he has, when I am well again,’ said Sir Mulberry Hawk, ‘let him fly where he will.’\n\n‘Oh!’ returned Ralph quickly, ‘he doesn’t dream of that. He is here, good sir, waiting your pleasure, here in London, walking the streets at noonday; carrying it off jauntily; looking for you, I swear,’ said Ralph, his face darkening, and his own hatred getting the upper hand of him, for the first time, as this gay picture of Nicholas presented itself; ‘if we were only citizens of a country where it could be safely done, I’d give good money to have him stabbed to the heart and rolled into the kennel for the dogs to tear.’\n\nAs Ralph, somewhat to the surprise of his old client, vented this little piece of sound family feeling, and took up his hat preparatory to departing, Lord Frederick Verisopht looked in.\n\n‘Why what in the deyvle’s name, Hawk, have you and Nickleby been talking about?’ said the young man. ‘I neyver heard such an insufferable riot. Croak, croak, croak. Bow, wow, wow. What has it all been about?’\n\n‘Sir Mulberry has been angry, my Lord,’ said Ralph, looking towards the couch.\n\n‘Not about money, I hope? Nothing has gone wrong in business, has it, Nickleby?’\n\n‘No, my Lord, no,’ returned Ralph. ‘On that point we always agree. Sir Mulberry has been calling to mind the cause of--’\n\nThere was neither necessity nor opportunity for Ralph to proceed; for Sir Mulberry took up the theme, and vented his threats and oaths against Nicholas, almost as ferociously as before.\n\nRalph, who was no common observer, was surprised to see that as this tirade proceeded, the manner of Lord Frederick Verisopht, who at the commencement had been twirling his whiskers with a most dandified and listless air, underwent a complete alteration. He was still more surprised when, Sir Mulberry ceasing to speak, the young lord angrily, and almost unaffectedly, requested never to have the subject renewed in his presence.\n\n‘Mind that, Hawk!’ he added, with unusual energy. ‘I never will be a party to, or permit, if I can help it, a cowardly attack upon this young fellow.’\n\n‘Cowardly!’ interrupted his friend.\n\n‘Ye-es,’ said the other, turning full upon him. ‘If you had told him who you were; if you had given him your card, and found out, afterwards, that his station or character prevented your fighting him, it would have been bad enough then; upon my soul it would have been bad enough then. As it is, you did wrong. I did wrong too, not to interfere, and I am sorry for it. What happened to you afterwards, was as much the consequence of accident as design, and more your fault than his; and it shall not, with my knowledge, be cruelly visited upon him, it shall not indeed.’\n\nWith this emphatic repetition of his concluding words, the young lord turned upon his heel; but before he had reached the adjoining room he turned back again, and said, with even greater vehemence than he had displayed before,\n\n‘I do believe, now; upon my honour I do believe, that the sister is as virtuous and modest a young lady as she is a handsome one; and of the brother, I say this, that he acted as her brother should, and in a manly and spirited manner. And I only wish, with all my heart and soul, that any one of us came out of this matter half as well as he does.’\n\nSo saying, Lord Frederick Verisopht walked out of the room, leaving Ralph Nickleby and Sir Mulberry in most unpleasant astonishment.\n\n‘Is this your pupil?’ asked Ralph, softly, ‘or has he come fresh from some country parson?’\n\n‘Green fools take these fits sometimes,’ replied Sir Mulberry Hawk, biting his lip, and pointing to the door. ‘Leave him to me.’\n\nRalph exchanged a familiar look with his old acquaintance; for they had suddenly grown confidential again in this alarming surprise; and took his way home, thoughtfully and slowly.\n\nWhile these things were being said and done, and long before they were concluded, the omnibus had disgorged Miss La Creevy and her escort, and they had arrived at her own door. Now, the good-nature of the little miniature painter would by no means allow of Smike’s walking back again, until he had been previously refreshed with just a sip of something comfortable and a mixed biscuit or so; and Smike, entertaining no objection either to the sip of something comfortable, or the mixed biscuit, but, considering on the contrary that they would be a very pleasant preparation for a walk to Bow, it fell out that he delayed much longer than he originally intended, and that it was some half-hour after dusk when he set forth on his journey home.\n\nThere was no likelihood of his losing his way, for it lay quite straight before him, and he had walked into town with Nicholas, and back alone, almost every day. So, Miss La Creevy and he shook hands with mutual confidence, and, being charged with more kind remembrances to Mrs. and Miss Nickleby, Smike started off.\n\nAt the foot of Ludgate Hill, he turned a little out of the road to satisfy his curiosity by having a look at Newgate. After staring up at the sombre walls, from the opposite side of the way, with great care and dread for some minutes, he turned back again into the old track, and walked briskly through the city; stopping now and then to gaze in at the window of some particularly attractive shop, then running for a little way, then stopping again, and so on, as any other country lad might do.\n\nHe had been gazing for a long time through a jeweller’s window, wishing he could take some of the beautiful trinkets home as a present, and imagining what delight they would afford if he could, when the clocks struck three-quarters past eight; roused by the sound, he hurried on at a very quick pace, and was crossing the corner of a by-street when he felt himself violently brought to, with a jerk so sudden that he was obliged to cling to a lamp-post to save himself from falling. At the same moment, a small boy clung tight round his leg, and a shrill cry of ‘Here he is, father! Hooray!’ vibrated in his ears.\n\nSmike knew that voice too well. He cast his despairing eyes downward towards the form from which it had proceeded, and, shuddering from head to foot, looked round. Mr. Squeers had hooked him in the coat collar with the handle of his umbrella, and was hanging on at the other end with all his might and main. The cry of triumph proceeded from Master Wackford, who, regardless of all his kicks and struggles, clung to him with the tenacity of a bull-dog!\n\nOne glance showed him this; and in that one glance the terrified creature became utterly powerless and unable to utter a sound.\n\n‘Here’s a go!’ cried Mr. Squeers, gradually coming hand-over-hand down the umbrella, and only unhooking it when he had got tight hold of the victim’s collar. ‘Here’s a delicious go! Wackford, my boy, call up one of them coaches.’\n\n‘A coach, father!’ cried little Wackford.\n\n‘Yes, a coach, sir,’ replied Squeers, feasting his eyes upon the countenance of Smike. ‘Damn the expense. Let’s have him in a coach.’\n\n‘What’s he been a doing of?’ asked a labourer with a hod of bricks, against whom and a fellow-labourer Mr. Squeers had backed, on the first jerk of the umbrella.\n\n‘Everything!’ replied Mr. Squeers, looking fixedly at his old pupil in a sort of rapturous trance. ‘Everything--running away, sir--joining in bloodthirsty attacks upon his master--there’s nothing that’s bad that he hasn’t done. Oh, what a delicious go is this here, good Lord!’\n\nThe man looked from Squeers to Smike; but such mental faculties as the poor fellow possessed, had utterly deserted him. The coach came up; Master Wackford entered; Squeers pushed in his prize, and following close at his heels, pulled up the glasses. The coachman mounted his box and drove slowly off, leaving the two bricklayers, and an old apple-woman, and a town-made little boy returning from an evening school, who had been the only witnesses of the scene, to meditate upon it at their leisure.\n\nMr. Squeers sat himself down on the opposite seat to the unfortunate Smike, and, planting his hands firmly on his knees, looked at him for some five minutes, when, seeming to recover from his trance, he uttered a loud laugh, and slapped his old pupil’s face several times--taking the right and left sides alternately.\n\n‘It isn’t a dream!’ said Squeers. ‘That’s real flesh and blood! I know the feel of it!’ and being quite assured of his good fortune by these experiments, Mr. Squeers administered a few boxes on the ear, lest the entertainments should seem to partake of sameness, and laughed louder and longer at every one.\n\n‘Your mother will be fit to jump out of her skin, my boy, when she hears of this,’ said Squeers to his son.\n\n‘Oh, won’t she though, father?’ replied Master Wackford.\n\n‘To think,’ said Squeers, ‘that you and me should be turning out of a street, and come upon him at the very nick; and that I should have him tight, at only one cast of the umbrella, as if I had hooked him with a grappling-iron! Ha, ha!’\n\n‘Didn’t I catch hold of his leg, neither, father?’ said little Wackford.\n\n‘You did; like a good ‘un, my boy,’ said Mr. Squeers, patting his son’s head, ‘and you shall have the best button-over jacket and waistcoat that the next new boy brings down, as a reward of merit. Mind that. You always keep on in the same path, and do them things that you see your father do, and when you die you’ll go right slap to Heaven and no questions asked.’\n\nImproving the occasion in these words, Mr. Squeers patted his son’s head again, and then patted Smike’s--but harder; and inquired in a bantering tone how he found himself by this time.\n\n‘I must go home,’ replied Smike, looking wildly round.\n\n‘To be sure you must. You’re about right there,’ replied Mr. Squeers. ‘You’ll go home very soon, you will. You’ll find yourself at the peaceful village of Dotheboys, in Yorkshire, in something under a week’s time, my young friend; and the next time you get away from there, I give you leave to keep away. Where’s the clothes you run off in, you ungrateful robber?’ said Mr. Squeers, in a severe voice.\n\nSmike glanced at the neat attire which the care of Nicholas had provided for him; and wrung his hands.\n\n‘Do you know that I could hang you up, outside of the Old Bailey, for making away with them articles of property?’ said Squeers. ‘Do you know that it’s a hanging matter--and I an’t quite certain whether it an’t an anatomy one besides--to walk off with up’ards of the valley of five pound from a dwelling-house? Eh? Do you know that? What do you suppose was the worth of them clothes you had? Do you know that that Wellington boot you wore, cost eight-and-twenty shillings when it was a pair, and the shoe seven-and-six? But you came to the right shop for mercy when you came to me, and thank your stars that it IS me as has got to serve you with the article.’\n\nAnybody not in Mr. Squeers’s confidence would have supposed that he was quite out of the article in question, instead of having a large stock on hand ready for all comers; nor would the opinion of sceptical persons have undergone much alteration when he followed up the remark by poking Smike in the chest with the ferrule of his umbrella, and dealing a smart shower of blows, with the ribs of the same instrument, upon his head and shoulders.\n\n‘I never threshed a boy in a hackney coach before,’ said Mr. Squeers, when he stopped to rest. ‘There’s inconveniency in it, but the novelty gives it a sort of relish, too!’\n\nPoor Smike! He warded off the blows, as well as he could, and now shrunk into a corner of the coach, with his head resting on his hands, and his elbows on his knees; he was stunned and stupefied, and had no more idea that any act of his, would enable him to escape from the all-powerful Squeers, now that he had no friend to speak to or to advise with, than he had had in all the weary years of his Yorkshire life which preceded the arrival of Nicholas.\n\nThe journey seemed endless; street after street was entered and left behind; and still they went jolting on. At last Mr. Squeers began to thrust his head out of the widow every half-minute, and to bawl a variety of directions to the coachman; and after passing, with some difficulty, through several mean streets which the appearance of the houses and the bad state of the road denoted to have been recently built, Mr. Squeers suddenly tugged at the check string with all his might, and cried, ‘Stop!’\n\n‘What are you pulling a man’s arm off for?’ said the coachman looking angrily down.\n\n‘That’s the house,’ replied Squeers. ‘The second of them four little houses, one story high, with the green shutters. There’s brass plate on the door, with the name of Snawley.’\n\n‘Couldn’t you say that without wrenching a man’s limbs off his body?’ inquired the coachman.\n\n‘No!’ bawled Mr. Squeers. ‘Say another word, and I’ll summons you for having a broken winder. Stop!’\n\nObedient to this direction, the coach stopped at Mr. Snawley’s door. Mr. Snawley may be remembered as the sleek and sanctified gentleman who confided two sons (in law) to the parental care of Mr. Squeers, as narrated in the fourth chapter of this history. Mr. Snawley’s house was on the extreme borders of some new settlements adjoining Somers Town, and Mr. Squeers had taken lodgings therein for a short time, as his stay was longer than usual, and the Saracen, having experience of Master Wackford’s appetite, had declined to receive him on any other terms than as a full-grown customer.\n\n‘Here we are!’ said Squeers, hurrying Smike into the little parlour, where Mr. Snawley and his wife were taking a lobster supper. ‘Here’s the vagrant--the felon--the rebel--the monster of unthankfulness.’\n\n‘What! The boy that run away!’ cried Snawley, resting his knife and fork upright on the table, and opening his eyes to their full width.\n\n‘The very boy’, said Squeers, putting his fist close to Smike’s nose, and drawing it away again, and repeating the process several times, with a vicious aspect. ‘If there wasn’t a lady present, I’d fetch him such a--: never mind, I’ll owe it him.’\n\nAnd here Mr. Squeers related how, and in what manner, and when and where, he had picked up the runaway.\n\n‘It’s clear that there has been a Providence in it, sir,’ said Mr Snawley, casting down his eyes with an air of humility, and elevating his fork, with a bit of lobster on the top of it, towards the ceiling.\n\n‘Providence is against him, no doubt,’ replied Mr. Squeers, scratching his nose. ‘Of course; that was to be expected. Anybody might have known that.’\n\n‘Hard-heartedness and evil-doing will never prosper, sir,’ said Mr Snawley.\n\n‘Never was such a thing known,’ rejoined Squeers, taking a little roll of notes from his pocket-book, to see that they were all safe.\n\n‘I have been, Mr. Snawley,’ said Mr. Squeers, when he had satisfied himself upon this point, ‘I have been that chap’s benefactor, feeder, teacher, and clother. I have been that chap’s classical, commercial, mathematical, philosophical, and trigonomical friend. My son--my only son, Wackford--has been his brother; Mrs. Squeers has been his mother, grandmother, aunt,--ah! and I may say uncle too, all in one. She never cottoned to anybody, except them two engaging and delightful boys of yours, as she cottoned to this chap. What’s my return? What’s come of my milk of human kindness? It turns into curds and whey when I look at him.’\n\n‘Well it may, sir,’ said Mrs. Snawley. ‘Oh! Well it may, sir.’\n\n‘Where has he been all this time?’ inquired Snawley. ‘Has he been living with--?’\n\n‘Ah, sir!’ interposed Squeers, confronting him again. ‘Have you been a living with that there devilish Nickleby, sir?’\n\nBut no threats or cuffs could elicit from Smike one word of reply to this question; for he had internally resolved that he would rather perish in the wretched prison to which he was again about to be consigned, than utter one syllable which could involve his first and true friend. He had already called to mind the strict injunctions of secrecy as to his past life, which Nicholas had laid upon him when they travelled from Yorkshire; and a confused and perplexed idea that his benefactor might have committed some terrible crime in bringing him away, which would render him liable to heavy punishment if detected, had contributed, in some degree, to reduce him to his present state of apathy and terror.\n\nSuch were the thoughts--if to visions so imperfect and undefined as those which wandered through his enfeebled brain, the term can be applied--which were present to the mind of Smike, and rendered him deaf alike to intimidation and persuasion. Finding every effort useless, Mr Squeers conducted him to a little back room up-stairs, where he was to pass the night; and, taking the precaution of removing his shoes, and coat and waistcoat, and also of locking the door on the outside, lest he should muster up sufficient energy to make an attempt at escape, that worthy gentleman left him to his meditations.\n\nWhat those meditations were, and how the poor creature’s heart sunk within him when he thought--when did he, for a moment, cease to think?--of his late home, and the dear friends and familiar faces with which it was associated, cannot be told. To prepare the mind for such a heavy sleep, its growth must be stopped by rigour and cruelty in childhood; there must be years of misery and suffering, lightened by no ray of hope; the chords of the heart, which beat a quick response to the voice of gentleness and affection, must have rusted and broken in their secret places, and bear the lingering echo of no old word of love or kindness. Gloomy, indeed, must have been the short day, and dull the long, long twilight, preceding such a night of intellect as his.\n\nThere were voices which would have roused him, even then; but their welcome tones could not penetrate there; and he crept to bed the same listless, hopeless, blighted creature, that Nicholas had first found him at the Yorkshire school.\n\n# CHAPTER 39\n\nIn which another old Friend encounters Smike, very opportunely and to some Purpose\nThe night, fraught with so much bitterness to one poor soul, had given place to a bright and cloudless summer morning, when a north-country mail-coach traversed, with cheerful noise, the yet silent streets of Islington, and, giving brisk note of its approach with the lively winding of the guard’s horn, clattered onward to its halting-place hard by the Post Office.\n\nThe only outside passenger was a burly, honest-looking countryman on the box, who, with his eyes fixed upon the dome of St Paul’s Cathedral, appeared so wrapt in admiring wonder, as to be quite insensible to all the bustle of getting out the bags and parcels, until one of the coach windows being let sharply down, he looked round, and encountered a pretty female face which was just then thrust out.\n\n‘See there, lass!’ bawled the countryman, pointing towards the object of his admiration. ‘There be Paul’s Church. ‘Ecod, he be a soizable ‘un, he be.’\n\n‘Goodness, John! I shouldn’t have thought it could have been half the size. What a monster!’\n\n‘Monsther!--Ye’re aboot right theer, I reckon, Mrs. Browdie,’ said the countryman good-humouredly, as he came slowly down in his huge top-coat; ‘and wa’at dost thee tak yon place to be noo--thot’un owor the wa’? Ye’d never coom near it ‘gin you thried for twolve moonths. It’s na’ but a Poast Office! Ho! ho! They need to charge for dooble-latthers. A Poast Office! Wa’at dost thee think o’ thot? ‘Ecod, if thot’s on’y a Poast Office, I’d loike to see where the Lord Mayor o’ Lunnun lives.’\n\nSo saying, John Browdie--for he it was--opened the coach-door, and tapping Mrs. Browdie, late Miss Price, on the cheek as he looked in, burst into a boisterous fit of laughter.\n\n‘Weel!’ said John. ‘Dang my bootuns if she bean’t asleep agean!’\n\n‘She’s been asleep all night, and was, all yesterday, except for a minute or two now and then,’ replied John Browdie’s choice, ‘and I was very sorry when she woke, for she has been SO cross!’\n\nThe subject of these remarks was a slumbering figure, so muffled in shawl and cloak, that it would have been matter of impossibility to guess at its sex but for a brown beaver bonnet and green veil which ornamented the head, and which, having been crushed and flattened, for two hundred and fifty miles, in that particular angle of the vehicle from which the lady’s snores now proceeded, presented an appearance sufficiently ludicrous to have moved less risible muscles than those of John Browdie’s ruddy face.\n\n‘Hollo!’ cried John, twitching one end of the dragged veil. ‘Coom, wakken oop, will ‘ee?’\n\nAfter several burrowings into the old corner, and many exclamations of impatience and fatigue, the figure struggled into a sitting posture; and there, under a mass of crumpled beaver, and surrounded by a semicircle of blue curl-papers, were the delicate features of Miss Fanny Squeers.\n\n‘Oh, ‘Tilda!’ cried Miss Squeers, ‘how you have been kicking of me through this blessed night!’\n\n‘Well, I do like that,’ replied her friend, laughing, ‘when you have had nearly the whole coach to yourself.’\n\n‘Don’t deny it, ‘Tilda,’ said Miss Squeers, impressively, ‘because you have, and it’s no use to go attempting to say you haven’t. You mightn’t have known it in your sleep, ‘Tilda, but I haven’t closed my eyes for a single wink, and so I THINK I am to be believed.’\n\nWith which reply, Miss Squeers adjusted the bonnet and veil, which nothing but supernatural interference and an utter suspension of nature’s laws could have reduced to any shape or form; and evidently flattering herself that it looked uncommonly neat, brushed off the sandwich-crumbs and bits of biscuit which had accumulated in her lap, and availing herself of John Browdie’s proffered arm, descended from the coach.\n\n‘Noo,’ said John, when a hackney coach had been called, and the ladies and the luggage hurried in, ‘gang to the Sarah’s Head, mun.’\n\n‘To the VERE?’ cried the coachman.\n\n‘Lawk, Mr. Browdie!’ interrupted Miss Squeers. ‘The idea! Saracen’s Head.’\n\n‘Sure-ly,’ said John, ‘I know’d it was something aboot Sarah’s Son’s Head. Dost thou know thot?’\n\n‘Oh, ah! I know that,’ replied the coachman gruffly, as he banged the door.\n\n‘’Tilda, dear, really,’ remonstrated Miss Squeers, ‘we shall be taken for I don’t know what.’\n\n‘Let them tak’ us as they foind us,’ said John Browdie; ‘we dean’t come to Lunnun to do nought but ‘joy oursel, do we?’\n\n‘I hope not, Mr. Browdie,’ replied Miss Squeers, looking singularly dismal.\n\n‘Well, then,’ said John, ‘it’s no matther. I’ve only been a married man fower days, ‘account of poor old feyther deein, and puttin’ it off. Here be a weddin’ party--broide and broide’s-maid, and the groom--if a mun dean’t ‘joy himsel noo, when ought he, hey? Drat it all, thot’s what I want to know.’\n\nSo, in order that he might begin to enjoy himself at once, and lose no time, Mr. Browdie gave his wife a hearty kiss, and succeeded in wresting another from Miss Squeers, after a maidenly resistance of scratching and struggling on the part of that young lady, which was not quite over when they reached the Saracen’s Head.\n\nHere, the party straightway retired to rest; the refreshment of sleep being necessary after so long a journey; and here they met again about noon, to a substantial breakfast, spread by direction of Mr. John Browdie, in a small private room upstairs commanding an uninterrupted view of the stables.\n\nTo have seen Miss Squeers now, divested of the brown beaver, the green veil, and the blue curl-papers, and arrayed in all the virgin splendour of a white frock and spencer, with a white muslin bonnet, and an imitative damask rose in full bloom on the inside thereof--her luxuriant crop of hair arranged in curls so tight that it was impossible they could come out by any accident, and her bonnet-cap trimmed with little damask roses, which might be supposed to be so many promising scions of the big rose--to have seen all this, and to have seen the broad damask belt, matching both the family rose and the little roses, which encircled her slender waist, and by a happy ingenuity took off from the shortness of the spencer behind,--to have beheld all this, and to have taken further into account the coral bracelets (rather short of beads, and with a very visible black string) which clasped her wrists, and the coral necklace which rested on her neck, supporting, outside her frock, a lonely cornelian heart, typical of her own disengaged affections--to have contemplated all these mute but expressive appeals to the purest feelings of our nature, might have thawed the frost of age, and added new and inextinguishable fuel to the fire of youth.\n\nThe waiter was touched. Waiter as he was, he had human passions and feelings, and he looked very hard at Miss Squeers as he handed the muffins.\n\n‘Is my pa in, do you know?’ asked Miss Squeers with dignity.\n\n‘Beg your pardon, miss?’\n\n‘My pa,’ repeated Miss Squeers; ‘is he in?’\n\n‘In where, miss?’\n\n‘In here--in the house!’ replied Miss Squeers. ‘My pa--Mr. Wackford Squeers--he’s stopping here. Is he at home?’\n\n‘I didn’t know there was any gen’l’man of that name in the house, miss’ replied the waiter. ‘There may be, in the coffee-room.’\n\nMAY BE. Very pretty this, indeed! Here was Miss Squeers, who had been depending, all the way to London, upon showing her friends how much at home she would be, and how much respectful notice her name and connections would excite, told that her father MIGHT be there! ‘As if he was a feller!’ observed Miss Squeers, with emphatic indignation.\n\n‘Ye’d betther inquire, mun,’ said John Browdie. ‘An’ hond up another pigeon-pie, will ‘ee? Dang the chap,’ muttered John, looking into the empty dish as the waiter retired; ‘does he ca’ this a pie--three yoong pigeons and a troifling matther o’ steak, and a crust so loight that you doant know when it’s in your mooth and when it’s gane? I wonder hoo many pies goes to a breakfast!’\n\nAfter a short interval, which John Browdie employed upon the ham and a cold round of beef, the waiter returned with another pie, and the information that Mr. Squeers was not stopping in the house, but that he came there every day and that directly he arrived, he should be shown upstairs. With this, he retired; and he had not retired two minutes, when he returned with Mr. Squeers and his hopeful son.\n\n‘Why, who’d have thought of this?’ said Mr. Squeers, when he had saluted the party and received some private family intelligence from his daughter.\n\n‘Who, indeed, pa!’ replied that young lady, spitefully. ‘But you see ‘Tilda IS married at last.’\n\n‘And I stond threat for a soight o’ Lunnun, schoolmeasther,’ said John, vigorously attacking the pie.\n\n‘One of them things that young men do when they get married,’ returned Squeers; ‘and as runs through with their money like nothing at all! How much better wouldn’t it be now, to save it up for the eddication of any little boys, for instance! They come on you,’ said Mr. Squeers in a moralising way, ‘before you’re aware of it; mine did upon me.’\n\n‘Will ‘ee pick a bit?’ said John.\n\n‘I won’t myself,’ returned Squeers; ‘but if you’ll just let little Wackford tuck into something fat, I’ll be obliged to you. Give it him in his fingers, else the waiter charges it on, and there’s lot of profit on this sort of vittles without that. If you hear the waiter coming, sir, shove it in your pocket and look out of the window, d’ye hear?’\n\n‘I’m awake, father,’ replied the dutiful Wackford.\n\n‘Well,’ said Squeers, turning to his daughter, ‘it’s your turn to be married next. You must make haste.’\n\n‘Oh, I’m in no hurry,’ said Miss Squeers, very sharply.\n\n‘No, Fanny?’ cried her old friend with some archness.\n\n‘No, ‘Tilda,’ replied Miss Squeers, shaking her head vehemently. ‘I can wait.’\n\n‘So can the young men, it seems, Fanny,’ observed Mrs. Browdie.\n\n‘They an’t draw’d into it by ME, ‘Tilda,’ retorted Miss Squeers.\n\n‘No,’ returned her friend; ‘that’s exceedingly true.’\n\nThe sarcastic tone of this reply might have provoked a rather acrimonious retort from Miss Squeers, who, besides being of a constitutionally vicious temper--aggravated, just now, by travel and recent jolting--was somewhat irritated by old recollections and the failure of her own designs upon Mr. Browdie; and the acrimonious retort might have led to a great many other retorts, which might have led to Heaven knows what, if the subject of conversation had not been, at that precise moment, accidentally changed by Mr. Squeers himself\n\n‘What do you think?’ said that gentleman; ‘who do you suppose we have laid hands on, Wackford and me?’\n\n‘Pa! not Mr--?’ Miss Squeers was unable to finish the sentence, but Mrs Browdie did it for her, and added, ‘Nickleby?’\n\n‘No,’ said Squeers. ‘But next door to him though.’\n\n‘You can’t mean Smike?’ cried Miss Squeers, clapping her hands.\n\n‘Yes, I can though,’ rejoined her father. ‘I’ve got him, hard and fast.’\n\n‘Wa’at!’ exclaimed John Browdie, pushing away his plate. ‘Got that poor--dom’d scoondrel? Where?’\n\n‘Why, in the top back room, at my lodging,’ replied Squeers, ‘with him on one side, and the key on the other.’\n\n‘At thy loodgin’! Thee’st gotten him at thy loodgin’? Ho! ho! The schoolmeasther agin all England. Give us thee hond, mun; I’m darned but I must shak thee by the hond for thot.--Gotten him at thy loodgin’?’\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Squeers, staggering in his chair under the congratulatory blow on the chest which the stout Yorkshireman dealt him; ‘thankee. Don’t do it again. You mean it kindly, I know, but it hurts rather. Yes, there he is. That’s not so bad, is it?’\n\n‘Ba’ad!’ repeated John Browdie. ‘It’s eneaf to scare a mun to hear tell on.’\n\n‘I thought it would surprise you a bit,’ said Squeers, rubbing his hands. ‘It was pretty neatly done, and pretty quick too.’\n\n‘Hoo wor it?’ inquired John, sitting down close to him. ‘Tell us all aboot it, mun; coom, quick!’\n\nAlthough he could not keep pace with John Browdie’s impatience, Mr Squeers related the lucky chance by which Smike had fallen into his hands, as quickly as he could, and, except when he was interrupted by the admiring remarks of his auditors, paused not in the recital until he had brought it to an end.\n\n‘For fear he should give me the slip, by any chance,’ observed Squeers, when he had finished, looking very cunning, ‘I’ve taken three outsides for tomorrow morning--for Wackford and him and me--and have arranged to leave the accounts and the new boys to the agent, don’t you see? So it’s very lucky you come today, or you’d have missed us; and as it is, unless you could come and tea with me tonight, we shan’t see anything more of you before we go away.’\n\n‘Dean’t say anoother wurd,’ returned the Yorkshireman, shaking him by the hand. ‘We’d coom, if it was twonty mile.’\n\n‘No, would you though?’ returned Mr. Squeers, who had not expected quite such a ready acceptance of his invitation, or he would have considered twice before he gave it.\n\nJohn Browdie’s only reply was another squeeze of the hand, and an assurance that they would not begin to see London till tomorrow, so that they might be at Mr. Snawley’s at six o’clock without fail; and after some further conversation, Mr. Squeers and his son departed.\n\nDuring the remainder of the day, Mr. Browdie was in a very odd and excitable state; bursting occasionally into an explosion of laughter, and then taking up his hat and running into the coach-yard to have it out by himself. He was very restless too, constantly walking in and out, and snapping his fingers, and dancing scraps of uncouth country dances, and, in short, conducting himself in such a very extraordinary manner, that Miss Squeers opined he was going mad, and, begging her dear ‘Tilda not to distress herself, communicated her suspicions in so many words. Mrs. Browdie, however, without discovering any great alarm, observed that she had seen him so once before, and that although he was almost sure to be ill after it, it would not be anything very serious, and therefore he was better left alone.\n\nThe result proved her to be perfectly correct for, while they were all sitting in Mr. Snawley’s parlour that night, and just as it was beginning to get dusk, John Browdie was taken so ill, and seized with such an alarming dizziness in the head, that the whole company were thrown into the utmost consternation. His good lady, indeed, was the only person present, who retained presence of mind enough to observe that if he were allowed to lie down on Mr. Squeers’s bed for an hour or so, and left entirely to himself, he would be sure to recover again almost as quickly as he had been taken ill. Nobody could refuse to try the effect of so reasonable a proposal, before sending for a surgeon. Accordingly, John was supported upstairs, with great difficulty; being a monstrous weight, and regularly tumbling down two steps every time they hoisted him up three; and, being laid on the bed, was left in charge of his wife, who, after a short interval, reappeared in the parlour, with the gratifying intelligence that he had fallen fast asleep.\n\nNow, the fact was, that at that particular moment, John Browdie was sitting on the bed with the reddest face ever seen, cramming the corner of the pillow into his mouth, to prevent his roaring out loud with laughter. He had no sooner succeeded in suppressing this emotion, than he slipped off his shoes, and creeping to the adjoining room where the prisoner was confined, turned the key, which was on the outside, and darting in, covered Smike’s mouth with his huge hand before he could utter a sound.\n\n‘Ods-bobs, dost thee not know me, mun?’ whispered the Yorkshireman to the bewildered lad. ‘Browdie. Chap as met thee efther schoolmeasther was banged?’\n\n‘Yes, yes,’ cried Smike. ‘Oh! help me.’\n\n‘Help thee!’ replied John, stopping his mouth again, the instant he had said this much. ‘Thee didn’t need help, if thee warn’t as silly yoongster as ever draw’d breath. Wa’at did ‘ee come here for, then?’\n\n‘He brought me; oh! he brought me,’ cried Smike.\n\n‘Brout thee!’ replied John. ‘Why didn’t ‘ee punch his head, or lay theeself doon and kick, and squeal out for the pollis? I’d ha’ licked a doozen such as him when I was yoong as thee. But thee be’est a poor broken-doon chap,’ said John, sadly, ‘and God forgi’ me for bragging ower yan o’ his weakest creeturs!’\n\nSmike opened his mouth to speak, but John Browdie stopped him.\n\n‘Stan’ still,’ said the Yorkshireman, ‘and doant’ee speak a morsel o’ talk till I tell’ee.’\n\nWith this caution, John Browdie shook his head significantly, and drawing a screwdriver from his pocket, took off the box of the lock in a very deliberate and workmanlike manner, and laid it, together with the implement, on the floor.\n\n‘See thot?’ said John ‘Thot be thy doin’. Noo, coot awa’!’\n\nSmike looked vacantly at him, as if unable to comprehend his meaning.\n\n‘I say, coot awa’,’ repeated John, hastily. ‘Dost thee know where thee livest? Thee dost? Weel. Are yon thy clothes, or schoolmeasther’s?’\n\n‘Mine,’ replied Smike, as the Yorkshireman hurried him to the adjoining room, and pointed out a pair of shoes and a coat which were lying on a chair.\n\n‘On wi’ ‘em,’ said John, forcing the wrong arm into the wrong sleeve, and winding the tails of the coat round the fugitive’s neck. ‘Noo, foller me, and when thee get’st ootside door, turn to the right, and they wean’t see thee pass.’\n\n‘But--but--he’ll hear me shut the door,’ replied Smike, trembling from head to foot.\n\n‘Then dean’t shut it at all,’ retorted John Browdie. ‘Dang it, thee bean’t afeard o’ schoolmeasther’s takkin cold, I hope?’\n\n‘N-no,’ said Smike, his teeth chattering in his head. ‘But he brought me back before, and will again. He will, he will indeed.’\n\n‘He wull, he wull!’ replied John impatiently. ‘He wean’t, he wean’t. Look’ee! I wont to do this neighbourly loike, and let them think thee’s gotten awa’ o’ theeself, but if he cooms oot o’ thot parlour awhiles theer’t clearing off, he mun’ have mercy on his oun boans, for I wean’t. If he foinds it oot, soon efther, I’ll put ‘un on a wrong scent, I warrant ‘ee. But if thee keep’st a good hart, thee’lt be at whoam afore they know thee’st gotten off. Coom!’\n\nSmike, who comprehended just enough of this to know it was intended as encouragement, prepared to follow with tottering steps, when John whispered in his ear.\n\n‘Thee’lt just tell yoong Measther that I’m sploiced to ‘Tilly Price, and to be heerd on at the Saracen by latther, and that I bean’t jealous of ‘un--dang it, I’m loike to boost when I think o’ that neight! ‘Cod, I think I see ‘un now, a powderin’ awa’ at the thin bread an’ butther!’\n\nIt was rather a ticklish recollection for John just then, for he was within an ace of breaking out into a loud guffaw. Restraining himself, however, just in time, by a great effort, he glided downstairs, hauling Smike behind him; and placing himself close to the parlour door, to confront the first person that might come out, signed to him to make off.\n\nHaving got so far, Smike needed no second bidding. Opening the house-door gently, and casting a look of mingled gratitude and terror at his deliverer, he took the direction which had been indicated to him, and sped away like the wind.\n\nThe Yorkshireman remained on his post for a few minutes, but, finding that there was no pause in the conversation inside, crept back again unheard, and stood, listening over the stair-rail, for a full hour. Everything remaining perfectly quiet, he got into Mr. Squeers’s bed, once more, and drawing the clothes over his head, laughed till he was nearly smothered.\n\nIf there could only have been somebody by, to see how the bedclothes shook, and to see the Yorkshireman’s great red face and round head appear above the sheets, every now and then, like some jovial monster coming to the surface to breathe, and once more dive down convulsed with the laughter which came bursting forth afresh--that somebody would have been scarcely less amused than John Browdie himself.\n\n# CHAPTER 40\n\nIn which Nicholas falls in Love. He employs a Mediator, whose Proceedings are crowned with unexpected Success, excepting in one solitary Particular\n\nOnce more out of the clutches of his old persecutor, it needed no fresh stimulation to call forth the utmost energy and exertion that Smike was capable of summoning to his aid. Without pausing for a moment to reflect upon the course he was taking, or the probability of its leading him homewards or the reverse, he fled away with surprising swiftness and constancy of purpose, borne upon such wings as only Fear can wear, and impelled by imaginary shouts in the well remembered voice of Squeers, who, with a host of pursuers, seemed to the poor fellow’s disordered senses to press hard upon his track; now left at a greater distance in the rear, and now gaining faster and faster upon him, as the alternations of hope and terror agitated him by turns. Long after he had become assured that these sounds were but the creation of his excited brain, he still held on, at a pace which even weakness and exhaustion could scarcely retard. It was not until the darkness and quiet of a country road, recalled him to a sense of external objects, and the starry sky, above, warned him of the rapid flight of time, that, covered with dust and panting for breath, he stopped to listen and look about him.\n\nAll was still and silent. A glare of light in the distance, casting a warm glow upon the sky, marked where the huge city lay. Solitary fields, divided by hedges and ditches, through many of which he had crashed and scrambled in his flight, skirted the road, both by the way he had come and upon the opposite side. It was late now. They could scarcely trace him by such paths as he had taken, and if he could hope to regain his own dwelling, it must surely be at such a time as that, and under cover of the darkness. This, by degrees, became pretty plain, even to the mind of Smike. He had, at first, entertained some vague and childish idea of travelling into the country for ten or a dozen miles, and then returning homewards by a wide circuit, which should keep him clear of London--so great was his apprehension of traversing the streets alone, lest he should again encounter his dreaded enemy--but, yielding to the conviction which these thoughts inspired, he turned back, and taking the open road, though not without many fears and misgivings, made for London again, with scarcely less speed of foot than that with which he had left the temporary abode of Mr. Squeers.\n\nBy the time he re-entered it, at the western extremity, the greater part of the shops were closed. Of the throngs of people who had been tempted abroad after the heat of the day, but few remained in the streets, and they were lounging home. But of these he asked his way from time to time, and by dint of repeated inquiries, he at length reached the dwelling of Newman Noggs.\n\nAll that evening, Newman had been hunting and searching in byways and corners for the very person who now knocked at his door, while Nicholas had been pursuing the same inquiry in other directions. He was sitting, with a melancholy air, at his poor supper, when Smike’s timorous and uncertain knock reached his ears. Alive to every sound, in his anxious and expectant state, Newman hurried downstairs, and, uttering a cry of joyful surprise, dragged the welcome visitor into the passage and up the stairs, and said not a word until he had him safe in his own garret and the door was shut behind them, when he mixed a great mug-full of gin-and-water, and holding it to Smike’s mouth, as one might hold a bowl of medicine to the lips of a refractory child, commanded him to drain it to the last drop.\n\nNewman looked uncommonly blank when he found that Smike did little more than put his lips to the precious mixture; he was in the act of raising the mug to his own mouth with a deep sigh of compassion for his poor friend’s weakness, when Smike, beginning to relate the adventures which had befallen him, arrested him half-way, and he stood listening, with the mug in his hand.\n\nIt was odd enough to see the change that came over Newman as Smike proceeded. At first he stood, rubbing his lips with the back of his hand, as a preparatory ceremony towards composing himself for a draught; then, at the mention of Squeers, he took the mug under his arm, and opening his eyes very wide, looked on, in the utmost astonishment. When Smike came to the assault upon himself in the hackney coach, he hastily deposited the mug upon the table, and limped up and down the room in a state of the greatest excitement, stopping himself with a jerk, every now and then, as if to listen more attentively. When John Browdie came to be spoken of, he dropped, by slow and gradual degrees, into a chair, and rubbing his hands upon his knees--quicker and quicker as the story reached its climax--burst, at last, into a laugh composed of one loud sonorous ‘Ha! ha!’ having given vent to which, his countenance immediately fell again as he inquired, with the utmost anxiety, whether it was probable that John Browdie and Squeers had come to blows.\n\n‘No! I think not,’ replied Smike. ‘I don’t think he could have missed me till I had got quite away.’\n\nNewman scratched his head with a shout of great disappointment, and once more lifting up the mug, applied himself to the contents; smiling meanwhile, over the rim, with a grim and ghastly smile at Smike.\n\n‘You shall stay here,’ said Newman; ‘you’re tired--fagged. I’ll tell them you’re come back. They have been half mad about you. Mr. Nicholas--’\n\n‘God bless him!’ cried Smike.\n\n‘Amen!’ returned Newman. ‘He hasn’t had a minute’s rest or peace; no more has the old lady, nor Miss Nickleby.’\n\n‘No, no. Has SHE thought about me?’ said Smike. ‘Has she though? oh, has she, has she? Don’t tell me so if she has not.’\n\n‘She has,’ cried Newman. ‘She is as noble-hearted as she is beautiful.’\n\n‘Yes, yes!’ cried Smike. ‘Well said!’\n\n‘So mild and gentle,’ said Newman.\n\n‘Yes, yes!’ cried Smike, with increasing eagerness.\n\n‘And yet with such a true and gallant spirit,’ pursued Newman.\n\nHe was going on, in his enthusiasm, when, chancing to look at his companion, he saw that he had covered his face with his hands, and that tears were stealing out between his fingers.\n\nA moment before, the boy’s eyes were sparkling with unwonted fire, and every feature had been lighted up with an excitement which made him appear, for the moment, quite a different being.\n\n‘Well, well,’ muttered Newman, as if he were a little puzzled. ‘It has touched ME, more than once, to think such a nature should have been exposed to such trials; this poor fellow--yes, yes,--he feels that too--it softens him--makes him think of his former misery. Hah! That’s it? Yes, that’s--hum!’\n\nIt was by no means clear, from the tone of these broken reflections, that Newman Noggs considered them as explaining, at all satisfactorily, the emotion which had suggested them. He sat, in a musing attitude, for some time, regarding Smike occasionally with an anxious and doubtful glance, which sufficiently showed that he was not very remotely connected with his thoughts.\n\nAt length he repeated his proposition that Smike should remain where he was for that night, and that he (Noggs) should straightway repair to the cottage to relieve the suspense of the family. But, as Smike would not hear of this--pleading his anxiety to see his friends again--they eventually sallied forth together; and the night being, by this time, far advanced, and Smike being, besides, so footsore that he could hardly crawl along, it was within an hour of sunrise when they reached their destination.\n\nAt the first sound of their voices outside the house, Nicholas, who had passed a sleepless night, devising schemes for the recovery of his lost charge, started from his bed, and joyfully admitted them. There was so much noisy conversation, and congratulation, and indignation, that the remainder of the family were soon awakened, and Smike received a warm and cordial welcome, not only from Kate, but from Mrs. Nickleby also, who assured him of her future favour and regard, and was so obliging as to relate, for his entertainment and that of the assembled circle, a most remarkable account extracted from some work the name of which she had never known, of a miraculous escape from some prison, but what one she couldn’t remember, effected by an officer whose name she had forgotten, confined for some crime which she didn’t clearly recollect.\n\nAt first Nicholas was disposed to give his uncle credit for some portion of this bold attempt (which had so nearly proved successful) to carry off Smike; but on more mature consideration, he was inclined to think that the full merit of it rested with Mr. Squeers. Determined to ascertain, if he could, through John Browdie, how the case really stood, he betook himself to his daily occupation: meditating, as he went, on a great variety of schemes for the punishment of the Yorkshire schoolmaster, all of which had their foundation in the strictest principles of retributive justice, and had but the one drawback of being wholly impracticable.\n\n‘A fine morning, Mr. Linkinwater!’ said Nicholas, entering the office.\n\n‘Ah!’ replied Tim, ‘talk of the country, indeed! What do you think of this, now, for a day--a London day--eh?’\n\n‘It’s a little clearer out of town,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Clearer!’ echoed Tim Linkinwater. ‘You should see it from my bedroom window.’\n\n‘You should see it from MINE,’ replied Nicholas, with a smile.\n\n‘Pooh! pooh!’ said Tim Linkinwater, ‘don’t tell me. Country!’ (Bow was quite a rustic place to Tim.) ‘Nonsense! What can you get in the country but new-laid eggs and flowers? I can buy new-laid eggs in Leadenhall Market, any morning before breakfast; and as to flowers, it’s worth a run upstairs to smell my mignonette, or to see the double wallflower in the back-attic window, at No. 6, in the court.’\n\n‘There is a double wallflower at No. 6, in the court, is there?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Yes, is there!’ replied Tim, ‘and planted in a cracked jug, without a spout. There were hyacinths there, this last spring, blossoming, in--but you’ll laugh at that, of course.’\n\n‘At what?’\n\n‘At their blossoming in old blacking-bottles,’ said Tim.\n\n‘Not I, indeed,’ returned Nicholas.\n\nTim looked wistfully at him, for a moment, as if he were encouraged by the tone of this reply to be more communicative on the subject; and sticking behind his ear, a pen that he had been making, and shutting up his knife with a smart click, said,\n\n‘They belong to a sickly bedridden hump-backed boy, and seem to be the only pleasure, Mr. Nickleby, of his sad existence. How many years is it,’ said Tim, pondering, ‘since I first noticed him, quite a little child, dragging himself about on a pair of tiny crutches? Well! Well! Not many; but though they would appear nothing, if I thought of other things, they seem a long, long time, when I think of him. It is a sad thing,’ said Tim, breaking off, ‘to see a little deformed child sitting apart from other children, who are active and merry, watching the games he is denied the power to share in. He made my heart ache very often.’\n\n‘It is a good heart,’ said Nicholas, ‘that disentangles itself from the close avocations of every day, to heed such things. You were saying--’\n\n‘That the flowers belonged to this poor boy,’ said Tim; ‘that’s all. When it is fine weather, and he can crawl out of bed, he draws a chair close to the window, and sits there, looking at them and arranging them, all day long. He used to nod, at first, and then we came to speak. Formerly, when I called to him of a morning, and asked him how he was, he would smile, and say, “Better!” but now he shakes his head, and only bends more closely over his old plants. It must be dull to watch the dark housetops and the flying clouds, for so many months; but he is very patient.’\n\n‘Is there nobody in the house to cheer or help him?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘His father lives there, I believe,’ replied Tim, ‘and other people too; but no one seems to care much for the poor sickly cripple. I have asked him, very often, if I can do nothing for him; his answer is always the same. “Nothing.” His voice is growing weak of late, but I can SEE that he makes the old reply. He can’t leave his bed now, so they have moved it close beside the window, and there he lies, all day: now looking at the sky, and now at his flowers, which he still makes shift to trim and water, with his own thin hands. At night, when he sees my candle, he draws back his curtain, and leaves it so, till I am in bed. It seems such company to him to know that I am there, that I often sit at my window for an hour or more, that he may see I am still awake; and sometimes I get up in the night to look at the dull melancholy light in his little room, and wonder whether he is awake or sleeping.\n\n‘The night will not be long coming,’ said Tim, ‘when he will sleep, and never wake again on earth. We have never so much as shaken hands in all our lives; and yet I shall miss him like an old friend. Are there any country flowers that could interest me like these, do you think? Or do you suppose that the withering of a hundred kinds of the choicest flowers that blow, called by the hardest Latin names that were ever invented, would give me one fraction of the pain that I shall feel when these old jugs and bottles are swept away as lumber? Country!’ cried Tim, with a contemptuous emphasis; ‘don’t you know that I couldn’t have such a court under my bedroom window, anywhere, but in London?’\n\nWith which inquiry, Tim turned his back, and pretending to be absorbed in his accounts, took an opportunity of hastily wiping his eyes when he supposed Nicholas was looking another way.\n\nWhether it was that Tim’s accounts were more than usually intricate that morning, or whether it was that his habitual serenity had been a little disturbed by these recollections, it so happened that when Nicholas returned from executing some commission, and inquired whether Mr. Charles Cheeryble was alone in his room, Tim promptly, and without the smallest hesitation, replied in the affirmative, although somebody had passed into the room not ten minutes before, and Tim took especial and particular pride in preventing any intrusion on either of the brothers when they were engaged with any visitor whatever.\n\n‘I’ll take this letter to him at once,’ said Nicholas, ‘if that’s the case.’ And with that, he walked to the room and knocked at the door.\n\nNo answer.\n\nAnother knock, and still no answer.\n\n‘He can’t be here,’ thought Nicholas. ‘I’ll lay it on his table.’\n\nSo, Nicholas opened the door and walked in; and very quickly he turned to walk out again, when he saw, to his great astonishment and discomfiture, a young lady upon her knees at Mr. Cheeryble’s feet, and Mr Cheeryble beseeching her to rise, and entreating a third person, who had the appearance of the young lady’s female attendant, to add her persuasions to his to induce her to do so.\n\nNicholas stammered out an awkward apology, and was precipitately retiring, when the young lady, turning her head a little, presented to his view the features of the lovely girl whom he had seen at the register-office on his first visit long before. Glancing from her to the attendant, he recognised the same clumsy servant who had accompanied her then; and between his admiration of the young lady’s beauty, and the confusion and surprise of this unexpected recognition, he stood stock-still, in such a bewildered state of surprise and embarrassment that, for the moment, he was quite bereft of the power either to speak or move.\n\n‘My dear ma’am--my dear young lady,’ cried brother Charles in violent agitation, ‘pray don’t--not another word, I beseech and entreat you! I implore you--I beg of you--to rise. We--we--are not alone.’\n\nAs he spoke, he raised the young lady, who staggered to a chair and swooned away.\n\n‘She has fainted, sir,’ said Nicholas, darting eagerly forward.\n\n‘Poor dear, poor dear!’ cried brother Charles ‘Where is my brother Ned? Ned, my dear brother, come here pray.’\n\n‘Brother Charles, my dear fellow,’ replied his brother, hurrying into the room, ‘what is the--ah! what--’\n\n‘Hush! hush!--not a word for your life, brother Ned,’ returned the other. ‘Ring for the housekeeper, my dear brother--call Tim Linkinwater! Here, Tim Linkinwater, sir--Mr. Nickleby, my dear sir, leave the room, I beg and beseech of you.’\n\n‘I think she is better now,’ said Nicholas, who had been watching the patient so eagerly, that he had not heard the request.\n\n‘Poor bird!’ cried brother Charles, gently taking her hand in his, and laying her head upon his arm. ‘Brother Ned, my dear fellow, you will be surprised, I know, to witness this, in business hours; but--’ here he was again reminded of the presence of Nicholas, and shaking him by the hand, earnestly requested him to leave the room, and to send Tim Linkinwater without an instant’s delay.\n\nNicholas immediately withdrew and, on his way to the counting-house, met both the old housekeeper and Tim Linkinwater, jostling each other in the passage, and hurrying to the scene of action with extraordinary speed. Without waiting to hear his message, Tim Linkinwater darted into the room, and presently afterwards Nicholas heard the door shut and locked on the inside.\n\nHe had abundance of time to ruminate on this discovery, for Tim Linkinwater was absent during the greater part of an hour, during the whole of which time Nicholas thought of nothing but the young lady, and her exceeding beauty, and what could possibly have brought her there, and why they made such a mystery of it. The more he thought of all this, the more it perplexed him, and the more anxious he became to know who and what she was. ‘I should have known her among ten thousand,’ thought Nicholas. And with that he walked up and down the room, and recalling her face and figure (of which he had a peculiarly vivid remembrance), discarded all other subjects of reflection and dwelt upon that alone.\n\nAt length Tim Linkinwater came back--provokingly cool, and with papers in his hand, and a pen in his mouth, as if nothing had happened.\n\n‘Is she quite recovered?’ said Nicholas, impetuously.\n\n‘Who?’ returned Tim Linkinwater.\n\n‘Who!’ repeated Nicholas. ‘The young lady.’\n\n‘What do you make, Mr. Nickleby,’ said Tim, taking his pen out of his mouth, ‘what do you make of four hundred and twenty-seven times three thousand two hundred and thirty-eight?’\n\n‘Nay,’ returned Nicholas, ‘what do you make of my question first? I asked you--’\n\n‘About the young lady,’ said Tim Linkinwater, putting on his spectacles. ‘To be sure. Yes. Oh! she’s very well.’\n\n‘Very well, is she?’ returned Nicholas.\n\n‘Very well,’ replied Mr. Linkinwater, gravely.\n\n‘Will she be able to go home today?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘She’s gone,’ said Tim.\n\n‘Gone!’\n‘Yes.’\n\n‘I hope she has not far to go?’ said Nicholas, looking earnestly at the other.\n\n‘Ay,’ replied the immovable Tim, ‘I hope she hasn’t.’\n\nNicholas hazarded one or two further remarks, but it was evident that Tim Linkinwater had his own reasons for evading the subject, and that he was determined to afford no further information respecting the fair unknown, who had awakened so much curiosity in the breast of his young friend. Nothing daunted by this repulse, Nicholas returned to the charge next day, emboldened by the circumstance of Mr. Linkinwater being in a very talkative and communicative mood; but, directly he resumed the theme, Tim relapsed into a state of most provoking taciturnity, and from answering in monosyllables, came to returning no answers at all, save such as were to be inferred from several grave nods and shrugs, which only served to whet that appetite for intelligence in Nicholas, which had already attained a most unreasonable height.\n\nFoiled in these attempts, he was fain to content himself with watching for the young lady’s next visit, but here again he was disappointed. Day after day passed, and she did not return. He looked eagerly at the superscription of all the notes and letters, but there was not one among them which he could fancy to be in her handwriting. On two or three occasions he was employed on business which took him to a distance, and had formerly been transacted by Tim Linkinwater. Nicholas could not help suspecting that, for some reason or other, he was sent out of the way on purpose, and that the young lady was there in his absence. Nothing transpired, however, to confirm this suspicion, and Tim could not be entrapped into any confession or admission tending to support it in the smallest degree.\n\nMystery and disappointment are not absolutely indispensable to the growth of love, but they are, very often, its powerful auxiliaries. ‘Out of sight, out of mind,’ is well enough as a proverb applicable to cases of friendship, though absence is not always necessary to hollowness of heart, even between friends, and truth and honesty, like precious stones, are perhaps most easily imitated at a distance, when the counterfeits often pass for real. Love, however, is very materially assisted by a warm and active imagination: which has a long memory, and will thrive, for a considerable time, on very slight and sparing food. Thus it is, that it often attains its most luxuriant growth in separation and under circumstances of the utmost difficulty; and thus it was, that Nicholas, thinking of nothing but the unknown young lady, from day to day and from hour to hour, began, at last, to think that he was very desperately in love with her, and that never was such an ill-used and persecuted lover as he.\n\nStill, though he loved and languished after the most orthodox models, and was only deterred from making a confidante of Kate by the slight considerations of having never, in all his life, spoken to the object of his passion, and having never set eyes upon her, except on two occasions, on both of which she had come and gone like a flash of lightning--or, as Nicholas himself said, in the numerous conversations he held with himself, like a vision of youth and beauty much too bright to last--his ardour and devotion remained without its reward. The young lady appeared no more; so there was a great deal of love wasted (enough indeed to have set up half-a-dozen young gentlemen, as times go, with the utmost decency), and nobody was a bit the wiser for it; not even Nicholas himself, who, on the contrary, became more dull, sentimental, and lackadaisical, every day.\n\nWhile matters were in this state, the failure of a correspondent of the brothers Cheeryble, in Germany, imposed upon Tim Linkinwater and Nicholas the necessity of going through some very long and complicated accounts, extending over a considerable space of time. To get through them with the greater dispatch, Tim Linkinwater proposed that they should remain at the counting-house, for a week or so, until ten o’clock at night; to this, as nothing damped the zeal of Nicholas in the service of his kind patrons--not even romance, which has seldom business habits--he cheerfully assented. On the very first night of these later hours, at nine exactly, there came: not the young lady herself, but her servant, who, being closeted with brother Charles for some time, went away, and returned next night at the same hour, and on the next, and on the next again.\n\nThese repeated visits inflamed the curiosity of Nicholas to the very highest pitch. Tantalised and excited, beyond all bearing, and unable to fathom the mystery without neglecting his duty, he confided the whole secret to Newman Noggs, imploring him to be on the watch next night; to follow the girl home; to set on foot such inquiries relative to the name, condition, and history of her mistress, as he could, without exciting suspicion; and to report the result to him with the least possible delay.\n\nBeyond all measure proud of this commission, Newman Noggs took up his post, in the square, on the following evening, a full hour before the needful time, and planting himself behind the pump and pulling his hat over his eyes, began his watch with an elaborate appearance of mystery, admirably calculated to excite the suspicion of all beholders. Indeed, divers servant girls who came to draw water, and sundry little boys who stopped to drink at the ladle, were almost scared out of their senses, by the apparition of Newman Noggs looking stealthily round the pump, with nothing of him visible but his face, and that wearing the expression of a meditative Ogre.\n\nPunctual to her time, the messenger came again, and, after an interview of rather longer duration than usual, departed. Newman had made two appointments with Nicholas: one for the next evening, conditional on his success: and one the next night following, which was to be kept under all circumstances. The first night he was not at the place of meeting (a certain tavern about half-way between the city and Golden Square), but on the second night he was there before Nicholas, and received him with open arms.\n\n‘It’s all right,’ whispered Newman. ‘Sit down. Sit down, there’s a dear young man, and let me tell you all about it.’\n\nNicholas needed no second invitation, and eagerly inquired what was the news.\n\n‘There’s a great deal of news,’ said Newman, in a flutter of exultation. ‘It’s all right. Don’t be anxious. I don’t know where to begin. Never mind that. Keep up your spirits. It’s all right.’\n\n‘Well?’ said Nicholas eagerly. ‘Yes?’\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Newman. ‘That’s it.’\n\n‘What’s it?’ said Nicholas. ‘The name--the name, my dear fellow!’\n\n‘The name’s Bobster,’ replied Newman.\n\n‘Bobster!’ repeated Nicholas, indignantly.\n\n‘That’s the name,’ said Newman. ‘I remember it by lobster.’\n\n‘Bobster!’ repeated Nicholas, more emphatically than before. ‘That must be the servant’s name.’\n\n‘No, it an’t,’ said Newman, shaking his head with great positiveness. ‘Miss Cecilia Bobster.’\n\n‘Cecilia, eh?’ returned Nicholas, muttering the two names together over and over again in every variety of tone, to try the effect. ‘Well, Cecilia is a pretty name.’\n\n‘Very. And a pretty creature too,’ said Newman.\n\n‘Who?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Miss Bobster.’\n\n‘Why, where have you seen her?’ demanded Nicholas.\n\n‘Never mind, my dear boy,’ retorted Noggs, clapping him on the shoulder. ‘I HAVE seen her. You shall see her. I’ve managed it all.’\n\n‘My dear Newman,’ cried Nicholas, grasping his hand, ‘are you serious?’\n\n‘I am,’ replied Newman. ‘I mean it all. Every word. You shall see her tomorrow night. She consents to hear you speak for yourself. I persuaded her. She is all affability, goodness, sweetness, and beauty.’\n\n‘I know she is; I know she must be, Newman!’ said Nicholas, wringing his hand.\n\n‘You are right,’ returned Newman.\n\n‘Where does she live?’ cried Nicholas. ‘What have you learnt of her history? Has she a father--mother--any brothers--sisters? What did she say? How came you to see her? Was she not very much surprised? Did you say how passionately I have longed to speak to her? Did you tell her where I had seen her? Did you tell her how, and when, and where, and how long, and how often, I have thought of that sweet face which came upon me in my bitterest distress like a glimpse of some better world--did you, Newman--did you?’\n\nPoor Noggs literally gasped for breath as this flood of questions rushed upon him, and moved spasmodically in his chair at every fresh inquiry, staring at Nicholas meanwhile with a most ludicrous expression of perplexity.\n\n‘No,’ said Newman, ‘I didn’t tell her that.’\n\n‘Didn’t tell her which?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘About the glimpse of the better world,’ said Newman. ‘I didn’t tell her who you were, either, or where you’d seen her. I said you loved her to distraction.’\n\n‘That’s true, Newman,’ replied Nicholas, with his characteristic vehemence. ‘Heaven knows I do!’\n\n‘I said too, that you had admired her for a long time in secret,’ said Newman.\n\n‘Yes, yes. What did she say to that?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘Blushed,’ said Newman.\n\n‘To be sure. Of course she would,’ said Nicholas approvingly. Newman then went on to say, that the young lady was an only child, that her mother was dead, that she resided with her father, and that she had been induced to allow her lover a secret interview, at the intercession of her servant, who had great influence with her. He further related how it required much moving and great eloquence to bring the young lady to this pass; how it was expressly understood that she merely afforded Nicholas an opportunity of declaring his passion; and how she by no means pledged herself to be favourably impressed with his attentions. The mystery of her visits to the brothers Cheeryble remained wholly unexplained, for Newman had not alluded to them, either in his preliminary conversations with the servant or his subsequent interview with the mistress, merely remarking that he had been instructed to watch the girl home and plead his young friend’s cause, and not saying how far he had followed her, or from what point. But Newman hinted that from what had fallen from the confidante, he had been led to suspect that the young lady led a very miserable and unhappy life, under the strict control of her only parent, who was of a violent and brutal temper; a circumstance which he thought might in some degree account, both for her having sought the protection and friendship of the brothers, and her suffering herself to be prevailed upon to grant the promised interview. The last he held to be a very logical deduction from the premises, inasmuch as it was but natural to suppose that a young lady, whose present condition was so unenviable, would be more than commonly desirous to change it.\n\nIt appeared, on further questioning--for it was only by a very long and arduous process that all this could be got out of Newman Noggs--that Newman, in explanation of his shabby appearance, had represented himself as being, for certain wise and indispensable purposes connected with that intrigue, in disguise; and, being questioned how he had come to exceed his commission so far as to procure an interview, he responded, that the lady appearing willing to grant it, he considered himself bound, both in duty and gallantry, to avail himself of such a golden means of enabling Nicholas to prosecute his addresses. After these and all possible questions had been asked and answered twenty times over, they parted, undertaking to meet on the following night at half-past ten, for the purpose of fulfilling the appointment; which was for eleven o’clock.\n\n‘Things come about very strangely!’ thought Nicholas, as he walked home. ‘I never contemplated anything of this kind; never dreamt of the possibility of it. To know something of the life of one in whom I felt such interest; to see her in the street, to pass the house in which she lived, to meet her sometimes in her walks, to hope that a day might come when I might be in a condition to tell her of my love, this was the utmost extent of my thoughts. Now, however--but I should be a fool, indeed, to repine at my own good fortune!’\n\nStill, Nicholas was dissatisfied; and there was more in the dissatisfaction than mere revulsion of feeling. He was angry with the young lady for being so easily won, ‘because,’ reasoned Nicholas, ‘it is not as if she knew it was I, but it might have been anybody,’--which was certainly not pleasant. The next moment, he was angry with himself for entertaining such thoughts, arguing that nothing but goodness could dwell in such a temple, and that the behaviour of the brothers sufficiently showed the estimation in which they held her. ‘The fact is, she’s a mystery altogether,’ said Nicholas. This was not more satisfactory than his previous course of reflection, and only drove him out upon a new sea of speculation and conjecture, where he tossed and tumbled, in great discomfort of mind, until the clock struck ten, and the hour of meeting drew nigh.\n\nNicholas had dressed himself with great care, and even Newman Noggs had trimmed himself up a little; his coat presenting the phenomenon of two consecutive buttons, and the supplementary pins being inserted at tolerably regular intervals. He wore his hat, too, in the newest taste, with a pocket-handkerchief in the crown, and a twisted end of it straggling out behind after the fashion of a pigtail, though he could scarcely lay claim to the ingenuity of inventing this latter decoration, inasmuch as he was utterly unconscious of it: being in a nervous and excited condition which rendered him quite insensible to everything but the great object of the expedition.\n\nThey traversed the streets in profound silence; and after walking at a round pace for some distance, arrived in one, of a gloomy appearance and very little frequented, near the Edgeware Road.\n\n‘Number twelve,’ said Newman.\n\n‘Oh!’ replied Nicholas, looking about him.\n\n‘Good street?’ said Newman.\n\n‘Yes,’ returned Nicholas. ‘Rather dull.’\n\nNewman made no answer to this remark, but, halting abruptly, planted Nicholas with his back to some area railings, and gave him to understand that he was to wait there, without moving hand or foot, until it was satisfactorily ascertained that the coast was clear. This done, Noggs limped away with great alacrity; looking over his shoulder every instant, to make quite certain that Nicholas was obeying his directions; and, ascending the steps of a house some half-dozen doors off, was lost to view.\n\nAfter a short delay, he reappeared, and limping back again, halted midway, and beckoned Nicholas to follow him.\n\n‘Well?’ said Nicholas, advancing towards him on tiptoe.\n\n‘All right,’ replied Newman, in high glee. ‘All ready; nobody at home. Couldn’t be better. Ha! ha!’\n\nWith this fortifying assurance, he stole past a street-door, on which Nicholas caught a glimpse of a brass plate, with ‘BOBSTER,’ in very large letters; and, stopping at the area-gate, which was open, signed to his young friend to descend.\n\n‘What the devil!’ cried Nicholas, drawing back. ‘Are we to sneak into the kitchen, as if we came after the forks?’\n\n‘Hush!’ replied Newman. ‘Old Bobster--ferocious Turk. He’d kill ‘em all--box the young lady’s ears--he does--often.’\n\n‘What!’ cried Nicholas, in high wrath, ‘do you mean to tell me that any man would dare to box the ears of such a--’\n\nHe had no time to sing the praises of his mistress, just then, for Newman gave him a gentle push which had nearly precipitated him to the bottom of the area steps. Thinking it best to take the hint in good part, Nicholas descended, without further remonstrance, but with a countenance bespeaking anything rather than the hope and rapture of a passionate lover. Newman followed--he would have followed head first, but for the timely assistance of Nicholas--and, taking his hand, led him through a stone passage, profoundly dark, into a back-kitchen or cellar, of the blackest and most pitchy obscurity, where they stopped.\n\n‘Well!’ said Nicholas, in a discontented whisper, ‘this is not all, I suppose, is it?’\n\n‘No, no,’ rejoined Noggs; ‘they’ll be here directly. It’s all right.’\n\n‘I am glad to hear it,’ said Nicholas. ‘I shouldn’t have thought it, I confess.’\n\nThey exchanged no further words, and there Nicholas stood, listening to the loud breathing of Newman Noggs, and imagining that his nose seemed to glow like a red-hot coal, even in the midst of the darkness which enshrouded them. Suddenly the sound of cautious footsteps attracted his ear, and directly afterwards a female voice inquired if the gentleman was there.\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Nicholas, turning towards the corner from which the voice proceeded. ‘Who is that?’\n\n‘Only me, sir,’ replied the voice. ‘Now if you please, ma’am.’\n\nA gleam of light shone into the place, and presently the servant girl appeared, bearing a light, and followed by her young mistress, who seemed to be overwhelmed by modesty and confusion.\n\nAt sight of the young lady, Nicholas started and changed colour; his heart beat violently, and he stood rooted to the spot. At that instant, and almost simultaneously with her arrival and that of the candle, there was heard a loud and furious knocking at the street-door, which caused Newman Noggs to jump up, with great agility, from a beer-barrel on which he had been seated astride, and to exclaim abruptly, and with a face of ashy paleness, ‘Bobster, by the Lord!’\n\nThe young lady shrieked, the attendant wrung her hands, Nicholas gazed from one to the other in apparent stupefaction, and Newman hurried to and fro, thrusting his hands into all his pockets successively, and drawing out the linings of every one in the excess of his irresolution. It was but a moment, but the confusion crowded into that one moment no imagination can exaggerate.\n\n‘Leave the house, for Heaven’s sake! We have done wrong, we deserve it all,’ cried the young lady. ‘Leave the house, or I am ruined and undone for ever.’\n\n‘Will you hear me say but one word?’ cried Nicholas. ‘Only one. I will not detain you. Will you hear me say one word, in explanation of this mischance?’\n\nBut Nicholas might as well have spoken to the wind, for the young lady, with distracted looks, hurried up the stairs. He would have followed her, but Newman, twisting his hand in his coat collar, dragged him towards the passage by which they had entered.\n\n‘Let me go, Newman, in the Devil’s name!’ cried Nicholas. ‘I must speak to her. I will! I will not leave this house without.’\n\n‘Reputation--character--violence--consider,’ said Newman, clinging round him with both arms, and hurrying him away. ‘Let them open the door. We’ll go, as we came, directly it’s shut. Come. This way. Here.’\n\nOverpowered by the remonstrances of Newman, and the tears and prayers of the girl, and the tremendous knocking above, which had never ceased, Nicholas allowed himself to be hurried off; and, precisely as Mr. Bobster made his entrance by the street-door, he and Noggs made their exit by the area-gate.\n\nThey hurried away, through several streets, without stopping or speaking. At last, they halted and confronted each other with blank and rueful faces.\n\n‘Never mind,’ said Newman, gasping for breath. ‘Don’t be cast down. It’s all right. More fortunate next time. It couldn’t be helped. I did MY part.’\n\n‘Excellently,’ replied Nicholas, taking his hand. ‘Excellently, and like the true and zealous friend you are. Only--mind, I am not disappointed, Newman, and feel just as much indebted to you--only IT WAS THE WRONG LADY.’\n\n‘Eh?’ cried Newman Noggs. ‘Taken in by the servant?’\n\n‘Newman, Newman,’ said Nicholas, laying his hand upon his shoulder: ‘it was the wrong servant too.’\n\nNewman’s under-jaw dropped, and he gazed at Nicholas, with his sound eye fixed fast and motionless in his head.\n\n‘Don’t take it to heart,’ said Nicholas; ‘it’s of no consequence; you see I don’t care about it; you followed the wrong person, that’s all.’\n\nThat WAS all. Whether Newman Noggs had looked round the pump, in a slanting direction, so long, that his sight became impaired; or whether, finding that there was time to spare, he had recruited himself with a few drops of something stronger than the pump could yield--by whatsoever means it had come to pass, this was his mistake. And Nicholas went home to brood upon it, and to meditate upon the charms of the unknown young lady, now as far beyond his reach as ever.\n\n# CHAPTER 41\n\nContaining some Romantic Passages between Mrs. Nickleby and the Gentleman in the Small-clothes next Door\nEver since her last momentous conversation with her son, Mrs. Nickleby had begun to display unusual care in the adornment of her person, gradually superadding to those staid and matronly habiliments, which had, up to that time, formed her ordinary attire, a variety of embellishments and decorations, slight perhaps in themselves, but, taken together, and considered with reference to the subject of her disclosure, of no mean importance. Even her black dress assumed something of a deadly-lively air from the jaunty style in which it was worn; and, eked out as its lingering attractions were; by a prudent disposal, here and there, of certain juvenile ornaments of little or no value, which had, for that reason alone, escaped the general wreck and been permitted to slumber peacefully in odd corners of old drawers and boxes where daylight seldom shone, her mourning garments assumed quite a new character. From being the outward tokens of respect and sorrow for the dead, they became converted into signals of very slaughterous and killing designs upon the living.\n\nMrs. Nickleby might have been stimulated to this proceeding by a lofty sense of duty, and impulses of unquestionable excellence. She might, by this time, have become impressed with the sinfulness of long indulgence in unavailing woe, or the necessity of setting a proper example of neatness and decorum to her blooming daughter. Considerations of duty and responsibility apart, the change might have taken its rise in feelings of the purest and most disinterested charity. The gentleman next door had been vilified by Nicholas; rudely stigmatised as a dotard and an idiot; and for these attacks upon his understanding, Mrs. Nickleby was, in some sort, accountable. She might have felt that it was the act of a good Christian to show by all means in her power, that the abused gentleman was neither the one nor the other. And what better means could she adopt, towards so virtuous and laudable an end, than proving to all men, in her own person, that his passion was the most rational and reasonable in the world, and just the very result, of all others, which discreet and thinking persons might have foreseen, from her incautiously displaying her matured charms, without reserve, under the very eye, as it were, of an ardent and too-susceptible man?\n\n‘Ah!’ said Mrs. Nickleby, gravely shaking her head; ‘if Nicholas knew what his poor dear papa suffered before we were engaged, when I used to hate him, he would have a little more feeling. Shall I ever forget the morning I looked scornfully at him when he offered to carry my parasol? Or that night, when I frowned at him? It was a mercy he didn’t emigrate. It very nearly drove him to it.’\n\nWhether the deceased might not have been better off if he had emigrated in his bachelor days, was a question which his relict did not stop to consider; for Kate entered the room, with her workbox, in this stage of her reflections; and a much slighter interruption, or no interruption at all, would have diverted Mrs. Nickleby’s thoughts into a new channel at any time.\n\n‘Kate, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nickleby; ‘I don’t know how it is, but a fine warm summer day like this, with the birds singing in every direction, always puts me in mind of roast pig, with sage and onion sauce, and made gravy.’\n\n‘That’s a curious association of ideas, is it not, mama?’\n\n‘Upon my word, my dear, I don’t know,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Roast pig; let me see. On the day five weeks after you were christened, we had a roast--no, that couldn’t have been a pig, either, because I recollect there were a pair of them to carve, and your poor papa and I could never have thought of sitting down to two pigs--they must have been partridges. Roast pig! I hardly think we ever could have had one, now I come to remember, for your papa could never bear the sight of them in the shops, and used to say that they always put him in mind of very little babies, only the pigs had much fairer complexions; and he had a horror of little babies, too, because he couldn’t very well afford any increase to his family, and had a natural dislike to the subject. It’s very odd now, what can have put that in my head! I recollect dining once at Mrs. Bevan’s, in that broad street round the corner by the coachmaker’s, where the tipsy man fell through the cellar-flap of an empty house nearly a week before the quarter-day, and wasn’t found till the new tenant went in--and we had roast pig there. It must be that, I think, that reminds me of it, especially as there was a little bird in the room that would keep on singing all the time of dinner--at least, not a little bird, for it was a parrot, and he didn’t sing exactly, for he talked and swore dreadfully: but I think it must be that. Indeed I am sure it must. Shouldn’t you say so, my dear?’\n\n‘I should say there was not a doubt about it, mama,’ returned Kate, with a cheerful smile.\n\n‘No; but DO you think so, Kate?’ said Mrs. Nickleby, with as much gravity as if it were a question of the most imminent and thrilling interest. ‘If you don’t, say so at once, you know; because it’s just as well to be correct, particularly on a point of this kind, which is very curious and worth settling while one thinks about it.’\n\nKate laughingly replied that she was quite convinced; and as her mama still appeared undetermined whether it was not absolutely essential that the subject should be renewed, proposed that they should take their work into the summer-house, and enjoy the beauty of the afternoon. Mrs. Nickleby readily assented, and to the summer-house they repaired, without further discussion.\n\n‘Well, I will say,’ observed Mrs. Nickleby, as she took her seat, ‘that there never was such a good creature as Smike. Upon my word, the pains he has taken in putting this little arbour to rights, and training the sweetest flowers about it, are beyond anything I could have--I wish he wouldn’t put ALL the gravel on your side, Kate, my dear, though, and leave nothing but mould for me.’\n\n‘Dear mama,’ returned Kate, hastily, ‘take this seat--do--to oblige me, mama.’\n\n‘No, indeed, my dear. I shall keep my own side,’ said Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Well! I declare!’\n\nKate looked up inquiringly.\n\n‘If he hasn’t been,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘and got, from somewhere or other, a couple of roots of those flowers that I said I was so fond of, the other night, and asked you if you were not--no, that YOU said YOU were so fond of, the other night, and asked me if I wasn’t--it’s the same thing. Now, upon my word, I take that as very kind and attentive indeed! I don’t see,’ added Mrs. Nickleby, looking narrowly about her, ‘any of them on my side, but I suppose they grow best near the gravel. You may depend upon it they do, Kate, and that’s the reason they are all near you, and he has put the gravel there, because it’s the sunny side. Upon my word, that’s very clever now! I shouldn’t have had half as much thought myself!’\n\n‘Mama,’ said Kate, bending over her work so that her face was almost hidden, ‘before you were married--’\n\n‘Dear me, Kate,’ interrupted Mrs. Nickleby, ‘what in the name of goodness graciousness makes you fly off to the time before I was married, when I’m talking to you about his thoughtfulness and attention to me? You don’t seem to take the smallest interest in the garden.’\n\n‘Oh! mama,’ said Kate, raising her face again, ‘you know I do.’\n\n‘Well then, my dear, why don’t you praise the neatness and prettiness with which it’s kept?’ said Mrs. Nickleby. ‘How very odd you are, Kate!’\n\n‘I do praise it, mama,’ answered Kate, gently. ‘Poor fellow!’\n\n‘I scarcely ever hear you, my dear,’ retorted Mrs. Nickleby; ‘that’s all I’ve got to say.’ By this time the good lady had been a long while upon one topic, so she fell at once into her daughter’s little trap, if trap it were, and inquired what she had been going to say.\n\n‘About what, mama?’ said Kate, who had apparently quite forgotten her diversion.\n\n‘Lor, Kate, my dear,’ returned her mother, ‘why, you’re asleep or stupid! About the time before I was married.’\n\n‘Oh yes!’ said Kate, ‘I remember. I was going to ask, mama, before you were married, had you many suitors?’\n\n‘Suitors, my dear!’ cried Mrs. Nickleby, with a smile of wonderful complacency. ‘First and last, Kate, I must have had a dozen at least.’\n\n‘Mama!’ returned Kate, in a tone of remonstrance.\n\n‘I had indeed, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nickleby; ‘not including your poor papa, or a young gentleman who used to go, at that time, to the same dancing school, and who WOULD send gold watches and bracelets to our house in gilt-edged paper, (which were always returned,) and who afterwards unfortunately went out to Botany Bay in a cadet ship--a convict ship I mean--and escaped into a bush and killed sheep, (I don’t know how they got there,) and was going to be hung, only he accidentally choked himself, and the government pardoned him. Then there was young Lukin,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, beginning with her left thumb and checking off the names on her fingers--‘Mogley--Tipslark--Cabbery--Smifser--’\n\nHaving now reached her little finger, Mrs. Nickleby was carrying the account over to the other hand, when a loud ‘Hem!’ which appeared to come from the very foundation of the garden-wall, gave both herself and her daughter a violent start.\n\n‘Mama! what was that?’ said Kate, in a low tone of voice.\n\n‘Upon my word, my dear,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby, considerably startled, ‘unless it was the gentleman belonging to the next house, I don’t know what it could possibly--’\n\n‘A--hem!’ cried the same voice; and that, not in the tone of an ordinary clearing of the throat, but in a kind of bellow, which woke up all the echoes in the neighbourhood, and was prolonged to an extent which must have made the unseen bellower quite black in the face.\n\n‘I understand it now, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, laying her hand on Kate’s; ‘don’t be alarmed, my love, it’s not directed to you, and is not intended to frighten anybody. Let us give everybody their due, Kate; I am bound to say that.’\n\nSo saying, Mrs. Nickleby nodded her head, and patted the back of her daughter’s hand, a great many times, and looked as if she could tell something vastly important if she chose, but had self-denial, thank Heaven; and wouldn’t do it.\n\n‘What do you mean, mama?’ demanded Kate, in evident surprise.\n\n‘Don’t be flurried, my dear,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby, looking towards the garden-wall, ‘for you see I’m not, and if it would be excusable in anybody to be flurried, it certainly would--under all the circumstances--be excusable in me, but I am not, Kate--not at all.’\n\n‘It seems designed to attract our attention, mama,’ said Kate.\n\n‘It is designed to attract our attention, my dear; at least,’ rejoined Mrs. Nickleby, drawing herself up, and patting her daughter’s hand more blandly than before, ‘to attract the attention of one of us. Hem! you needn’t be at all uneasy, my dear.’\n\nKate looked very much perplexed, and was apparently about to ask for further explanation, when a shouting and scuffling noise, as of an elderly gentleman whooping, and kicking up his legs on loose gravel, with great violence, was heard to proceed from the same direction as the former sounds; and before they had subsided, a large cucumber was seen to shoot up in the air with the velocity of a sky-rocket, whence it descended, tumbling over and over, until it fell at Mrs. Nickleby’s feet.\n\nThis remarkable appearance was succeeded by another of a precisely similar description; then a fine vegetable marrow, of unusually large dimensions, was seen to whirl aloft, and come toppling down; then, several cucumbers shot up together; and, finally, the air was darkened by a shower of onions, turnip-radishes, and other small vegetables, which fell rolling and scattering, and bumping about, in all directions.\n\nAs Kate rose from her seat, in some alarm, and caught her mother’s hand to run with her into the house, she felt herself rather retarded than assisted in her intention; and following the direction of Mrs. Nickleby’s eyes, was quite terrified by the apparition of an old black velvet cap, which, by slow degrees, as if its wearer were ascending a ladder or pair of steps, rose above the wall dividing their garden from that of the next cottage, (which, like their own, was a detached building,) and was gradually followed by a very large head, and an old face, in which were a pair of most extraordinary grey eyes: very wild, very wide open, and rolling in their sockets, with a dull, languishing, leering look, most ugly to behold.\n\n‘Mama!’ cried Kate, really terrified for the moment, ‘why do you stop, why do you lose an instant? Mama, pray come in!’\n\n‘Kate, my dear,’ returned her mother, still holding back, ‘how can you be so foolish? I’m ashamed of you. How do you suppose you are ever to get through life, if you’re such a coward as this? What do you want, sir?’ said Mrs. Nickleby, addressing the intruder with a sort of simpering displeasure. ‘How dare you look into this garden?’\n\n‘Queen of my soul,’ replied the stranger, folding his hands together, ‘this goblet sip!’\n\n‘Nonsense, sir,’ said Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Kate, my love, pray be quiet.’\n\n‘Won’t you sip the goblet?’ urged the stranger, with his head imploringly on one side, and his right hand on his breast. ‘Oh, do sip the goblet!’\n\n‘I shall not consent to do anything of the kind, sir,’ said Mrs Nickleby. ‘Pray, begone.’\n\n‘Why is it,’ said the old gentleman, coming up a step higher, and leaning his elbows on the wall, with as much complacency as if he were looking out of window, ‘why is it that beauty is always obdurate, even when admiration is as honourable and respectful as mine?’ Here he smiled, kissed his hand, and made several low bows. ‘Is it owing to the bees, who, when the honey season is over, and they are supposed to have been killed with brimstone, in reality fly to Barbary and lull the captive Moors to sleep with their drowsy songs? Or is it,’ he added, dropping his voice almost to a whisper, ‘in consequence of the statue at Charing Cross having been lately seen, on the Stock Exchange at midnight, walking arm-in-arm with the Pump from Aldgate, in a riding-habit?’\n\n‘Mama,’ murmured Kate, ‘do you hear him?’\n\n‘Hush, my dear!’ replied Mrs. Nickleby, in the same tone of voice, ‘he is very polite, and I think that was a quotation from the poets. Pray, don’t worry me so--you’ll pinch my arm black and blue. Go away, sir!’\n\n‘Quite away?’ said the gentleman, with a languishing look. ‘Oh! quite away?’\n\n‘Yes,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby, ‘certainly. You have no business here. This is private property, sir; you ought to know that.’\n\n‘I do know,’ said the old gentleman, laying his finger on his nose, with an air of familiarity, most reprehensible, ‘that this is a sacred and enchanted spot, where the most divine charms’--here he kissed his hand and bowed again--‘waft mellifluousness over the neighbours’ gardens, and force the fruit and vegetables into premature existence. That fact I am acquainted with. But will you permit me, fairest creature, to ask you one question, in the absence of the planet Venus, who has gone on business to the Horse Guards, and would otherwise--jealous of your superior charms--interpose between us?’\n\n‘Kate,’ observed Mrs. Nickleby, turning to her daughter, ‘it’s very awkward, positively. I really don’t know what to say to this gentleman. One ought to be civil, you know.’\n\n‘Dear mama,’ rejoined Kate, ‘don’t say a word to him, but let us run away as fast as we can, and shut ourselves up till Nicholas comes home.’\n\nMrs. Nickleby looked very grand, not to say contemptuous, at this humiliating proposal; and, turning to the old gentleman, who had watched them during these whispers with absorbing eagerness, said:\n\n‘If you will conduct yourself, sir, like the gentleman I should imagine you to be, from your language and--and--appearance, (quite the counterpart of your grandpapa, Kate, my dear, in his best days,) and will put your question to me in plain words, I will answer it.’\n\nIf Mrs. Nickleby’s excellent papa had borne, in his best days, a resemblance to the neighbour now looking over the wall, he must have been, to say the least, a very queer-looking old gentleman in his prime. Perhaps Kate thought so, for she ventured to glance at his living portrait with some attention, as he took off his black velvet cap, and, exhibiting a perfectly bald head, made a long series of bows, each accompanied with a fresh kiss of the hand. After exhausting himself, to all appearance, with this fatiguing performance, he covered his head once more, pulled the cap very carefully over the tips of his ears, and resuming his former attitude, said,\n\n‘The question is--’\n\nHere he broke off to look round in every direction, and satisfy himself beyond all doubt that there were no listeners near. Assured that there were not, he tapped his nose several times, accompanying the action with a cunning look, as though congratulating himself on his caution; and stretching out his neck, said in a loud whisper,\n\n‘Are you a princess?’\n\n‘You are mocking me, sir,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby, making a feint of retreating towards the house.\n\n‘No, but are you?’ said the old gentleman.\n\n‘You know I am not, sir,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘Then are you any relation to the Archbishop of Canterbury?’ inquired the old gentleman with great anxiety, ‘or to the Pope of Rome? Or the Speaker of the House of Commons? Forgive me, if I am wrong, but I was told you were niece to the Commissioners of Paving, and daughter-in-law to the Lord Mayor and Court of Common Council, which would account for your relationship to all three.’\n\n‘Whoever has spread such reports, sir,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby, with some warmth, ‘has taken great liberties with my name, and one which I am sure my son Nicholas, if he was aware of it, would not allow for an instant. The idea!’ said Mrs. Nickleby, drawing herself up, ‘niece to the Commissioners of Paving!’\n\n‘Pray, mama, come away!’ whispered Kate.\n\n‘“Pray mama!” Nonsense, Kate,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, angrily, ‘but that’s just the way. If they had said I was niece to a piping bullfinch, what would you care? But I have no sympathy,’ whimpered Mrs. Nickleby. ‘I don’t expect it, that’s one thing.’\n\n‘Tears!’ cried the old gentleman, with such an energetic jump, that he fell down two or three steps and grated his chin against the wall. ‘Catch the crystal globules--catch ‘em--bottle ‘em up--cork ‘em tight--put sealing wax on the top--seal ‘em with a cupid--label ‘em “Best quality”--and stow ‘em away in the fourteen binn, with a bar of iron on the top to keep the thunder off!’\n\nIssuing these commands, as if there were a dozen attendants all actively engaged in their execution, he turned his velvet cap inside out, put it on with great dignity so as to obscure his right eye and three-fourths of his nose, and sticking his arms a-kimbo, looked very fiercely at a sparrow hard by, till the bird flew away, when he put his cap in his pocket with an air of great satisfaction, and addressed himself with respectful demeanour to Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘Beautiful madam,’ such were his words, ‘if I have made any mistake with regard to your family or connections, I humbly beseech you to pardon me. If I supposed you to be related to Foreign Powers or Native Boards, it is because you have a manner, a carriage, a dignity, which you will excuse my saying that none but yourself (with the single exception perhaps of the tragic muse, when playing extemporaneously on the barrel organ before the East India Company) can parallel. I am not a youth, ma’am, as you see; and although beings like you can never grow old, I venture to presume that we are fitted for each other.’\n\n‘Really, Kate, my love!’ said Mrs. Nickleby faintly, and looking another way.\n\n‘I have estates, ma’am,’ said the old gentleman, flourishing his right hand negligently, as if he made very light of such matters, and speaking very fast; ‘jewels, lighthouses, fish-ponds, a whalery of my own in the North Sea, and several oyster-beds of great profit in the Pacific Ocean. If you will have the kindness to step down to the Royal Exchange and to take the cocked-hat off the stoutest beadle’s head, you will find my card in the lining of the crown, wrapped up in a piece of blue paper. My walking-stick is also to be seen on application to the chaplain of the House of Commons, who is strictly forbidden to take any money for showing it. I have enemies about me, ma’am,’ he looked towards his house and spoke very low, ‘who attack me on all occasions, and wish to secure my property. If you bless me with your hand and heart, you can apply to the Lord Chancellor or call out the military if necessary--sending my toothpick to the commander-in-chief will be sufficient--and so clear the house of them before the ceremony is performed. After that, love, bliss and rapture; rapture, love and bliss. Be mine, be mine!’\n\nRepeating these last words with great rapture and enthusiasm, the old gentleman put on his black velvet cap again, and looking up into the sky in a hasty manner, said something that was not quite intelligible concerning a balloon he expected, and which was rather after its time.\n\n‘Be mine, be mine!’ repeated the old gentleman.\n\n‘Kate, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘I have hardly the power to speak; but it is necessary for the happiness of all parties that this matter should be set at rest for ever.’\n\n‘Surely there is no necessity for you to say one word, mama?’ reasoned Kate.\n\n‘You will allow me, my dear, if you please, to judge for myself,’ said Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘Be mine, be mine!’ cried the old gentleman.\n\n‘It can scarcely be expected, sir,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, fixing her eyes modestly on the ground, ‘that I should tell a stranger whether I feel flattered and obliged by such proposals, or not. They certainly are made under very singular circumstances; still at the same time, as far as it goes, and to a certain extent of course’ (Mrs. Nickleby’s customary qualification), ‘they must be gratifying and agreeable to one’s feelings.’\n\n‘Be mine, be mine,’ cried the old gentleman. ‘Gog and Magog, Gog and Magog. Be mine, be mine!’\n\n‘It will be sufficient for me to say, sir,’ resumed Mrs. Nickleby, with perfect seriousness--‘and I’m sure you’ll see the propriety of taking an answer and going away--that I have made up my mind to remain a widow, and to devote myself to my children. You may not suppose I am the mother of two children--indeed many people have doubted it, and said that nothing on earth could ever make ‘em believe it possible--but it is the case, and they are both grown up. We shall be very glad to have you for a neighbour--very glad; delighted, I’m sure--but in any other character it’s quite impossible, quite. As to my being young enough to marry again, that perhaps may be so, or it may not be; but I couldn’t think of it for an instant, not on any account whatever. I said I never would, and I never will. It’s a very painful thing to have to reject proposals, and I would much rather that none were made; at the same time this is the answer that I determined long ago to make, and this is the answer I shall always give.’\n\nThese observations were partly addressed to the old gentleman, partly to Kate, and partly delivered in soliloquy. Towards their conclusion, the suitor evinced a very irreverent degree of inattention, and Mrs. Nickleby had scarcely finished speaking, when, to the great terror both of that lady and her daughter, he suddenly flung off his coat, and springing on the top of the wall, threw himself into an attitude which displayed his small-clothes and grey worsteds to the fullest advantage, and concluded by standing on one leg, and repeating his favourite bellow with increased vehemence.\n\nWhile he was still dwelling on the last note, and embellishing it with a prolonged flourish, a dirty hand was observed to glide stealthily and swiftly along the top of the wall, as if in pursuit of a fly, and then to clasp with the utmost dexterity one of the old gentleman’s ankles. This done, the companion hand appeared, and clasped the other ankle.\n\nThus encumbered the old gentleman lifted his legs awkwardly once or twice, as if they were very clumsy and imperfect pieces of machinery, and then looking down on his own side of the wall, burst into a loud laugh.\n\n‘It’s you, is it?’ said the old gentleman.\n\n‘Yes, it’s me,’ replied a gruff voice.\n\n‘How’s the Emperor of Tartary?’ said the old gentleman.\n\n‘Oh! he’s much the same as usual,’ was the reply. ‘No better and no worse.’\n\n‘The young Prince of China,’ said the old gentleman, with much interest. ‘Is he reconciled to his father-in-law, the great potato salesman?’\n\n‘No,’ answered the gruff voice; ‘and he says he never will be, that’s more.’\n\n‘If that’s the case,’ observed the old gentleman, ‘perhaps I’d better come down.’\n\n‘Well,’ said the man on the other side, ‘I think you had, perhaps.’\n\nOne of the hands being then cautiously unclasped, the old gentleman dropped into a sitting posture, and was looking round to smile and bow to Mrs. Nickleby, when he disappeared with some precipitation, as if his legs had been pulled from below.\n\nVery much relieved by his disappearance, Kate was turning to speak to her mama, when the dirty hands again became visible, and were immediately followed by the figure of a coarse squat man, who ascended by the steps which had been recently occupied by their singular neighbour.\n\n‘Beg your pardon, ladies,’ said this new comer, grinning and touching his hat. ‘Has he been making love to either of you?’\n\n‘Yes,’ said Kate.\n\n‘Ah!’ rejoined the man, taking his handkerchief out of his hat and wiping his face, ‘he always will, you know. Nothing will prevent his making love.’\n\n‘I need not ask you if he is out of his mind, poor creature,’ said Kate.\n\n‘Why no,’ replied the man, looking into his hat, throwing his handkerchief in at one dab, and putting it on again. ‘That’s pretty plain, that is.’\n\n‘Has he been long so?’ asked Kate.\n\n‘A long while.’\n\n‘And is there no hope for him?’ said Kate, compassionately\n\n‘Not a bit, and don’t deserve to be,’ replied the keeper. ‘He’s a deal pleasanter without his senses than with ‘em. He was the cruellest, wickedest, out-and-outerest old flint that ever drawed breath.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ said Kate.\n\n‘By George!’ replied the keeper, shaking his head so emphatically that he was obliged to frown to keep his hat on. ‘I never come across such a vagabond, and my mate says the same. Broke his poor wife’s heart, turned his daughters out of doors, drove his sons into the streets; it was a blessing he went mad at last, through evil tempers, and covetousness, and selfishness, and guzzling, and drinking, or he’d have drove many others so. Hope for HIM, an old rip! There isn’t too much hope going, but I’ll bet a crown that what there is, is saved for more deserving chaps than him, anyhow.’\n\nWith which confession of his faith, the keeper shook his head again, as much as to say that nothing short of this would do, if things were to go on at all; and touching his hat sulkily--not that he was in an ill humour, but that his subject ruffled him--descended the ladder, and took it away.\n\nDuring this conversation, Mrs. Nickleby had regarded the man with a severe and steadfast look. She now heaved a profound sigh, and pursing up her lips, shook her head in a slow and doubtful manner.\n\n‘Poor creature!’ said Kate.\n\n‘Ah! poor indeed!’ rejoined Mrs. Nickleby. ‘It’s shameful that such things should be allowed. Shameful!’\n\n‘How can they be helped, mama?’ said Kate, mournfully. ‘The infirmities of nature--’\n\n‘Nature!’ said Mrs. Nickleby. ‘What! Do YOU suppose this poor gentleman is out of his mind?’\n\n‘Can anybody who sees him entertain any other opinion, mama?’\n\n‘Why then, I just tell you this, Kate,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby, ‘that, he is nothing of the kind, and I am surprised you can be so imposed upon. It’s some plot of these people to possess themselves of his property--didn’t he say so himself? He may be a little odd and flighty, perhaps, many of us are that; but downright mad! and express himself as he does, respectfully, and in quite poetical language, and making offers with so much thought, and care, and prudence--not as if he ran into the streets, and went down upon his knees to the first chit of a girl he met, as a madman would! No, no, Kate, there’s a great deal too much method in HIS madness; depend upon that, my dear.’\n\n# CHAPTER 42\n\nIllustrative of the convivial Sentiment, that the best of Friends must sometimes part\nThe pavement of Snow Hill had been baking and frying all day in the heat, and the twain Saracens’ heads guarding the entrance to the hostelry of whose name and sign they are the duplicate presentments, looked--or seemed, in the eyes of jaded and footsore passers-by, to look--more vicious than usual, after blistering and scorching in the sun, when, in one of the inn’s smallest sitting-rooms, through whose open window there rose, in a palpable steam, wholesome exhalations from reeking coach-horses, the usual furniture of a tea-table was displayed in neat and inviting order, flanked by large joints of roast and boiled, a tongue, a pigeon pie, a cold fowl, a tankard of ale, and other little matters of the like kind, which, in degenerate towns and cities, are generally understood to belong more particularly to solid lunches, stage-coach dinners, or unusually substantial breakfasts.\n\nMr. John Browdie, with his hands in his pockets, hovered restlessly about these delicacies, stopping occasionally to whisk the flies out of the sugar-basin with his wife’s pocket-handkerchief, or to dip a teaspoon in the milk-pot and carry it to his mouth, or to cut off a little knob of crust, and a little corner of meat, and swallow them at two gulps like a couple of pills. After every one of these flirtations with the eatables, he pulled out his watch, and declared with an earnestness quite pathetic that he couldn’t undertake to hold out two minutes longer.\n\n‘Tilly!’ said John to his lady, who was reclining half awake and half asleep upon a sofa.\n\n‘Well, John!’\n\n‘Well, John!’ retorted her husband, impatiently. ‘Dost thou feel hoongry, lass?’\n\n‘Not very,’ said Mrs. Browdie.\n\n‘Not vary!’ repeated John, raising his eyes to the ceiling. ‘Hear her say not vary, and us dining at three, and loonching off pasthry thot aggravates a mon ‘stead of pacifying him! Not vary!’\n\n‘Here’s a gen’l’man for you, sir,’ said the waiter, looking in.\n\n‘A wa’at for me?’ cried John, as though he thought it must be a letter, or a parcel.\n\n‘A gen’l’man, sir.’\n\n‘Stars and garthers, chap!’ said John, ‘wa’at dost thou coom and say thot for? In wi’ ‘un.’\n\n‘Are you at home, sir?’\n\n‘At whoam!’ cried John, ‘I wish I wur; I’d ha tea’d two hour ago. Why, I told t’oother chap to look sharp ootside door, and tell ‘un d’rectly he coom, thot we war faint wi’ hoonger. In wi’ ‘un. Aha! Thee hond, Misther Nickleby. This is nigh to be the proodest day o’ my life, sir. Hoo be all wi’ ye? Ding! But, I’m glod o’ this!’\n\nQuite forgetting even his hunger in the heartiness of his salutation, John Browdie shook Nicholas by the hand again and again, slapping his palm with great violence between each shake, to add warmth to the reception.\n\n‘Ah! there she be,’ said John, observing the look which Nicholas directed towards his wife. ‘There she be--we shan’t quarrel about her noo--eh? Ecod, when I think o’ thot--but thou want’st soom’at to eat. Fall to, mun, fall to, and for wa’at we’re aboot to receive--’\n\nNo doubt the grace was properly finished, but nothing more was heard, for John had already begun to play such a knife and fork, that his speech was, for the time, gone.\n\n‘I shall take the usual licence, Mr. Browdie,’ said Nicholas, as he placed a chair for the bride.\n\n‘Tak’ whatever thou like’st,’ said John, ‘and when a’s gane, ca’ for more.’\n\nWithout stopping to explain, Nicholas kissed the blushing Mrs. Browdie, and handed her to her seat.\n\n‘I say,’ said John, rather astounded for the moment, ‘mak’ theeself quite at whoam, will ‘ee?’\n\n‘You may depend upon that,’ replied Nicholas; ‘on one condition.’\n\n‘And wa’at may thot be?’ asked John.\n\n‘That you make me a godfather the very first time you have occasion for one.’\n\n‘Eh! d’ye hear thot?’ cried John, laying down his knife and fork. ‘A godfeyther! Ha! ha! ha! Tilly--hear till ‘un--a godfeyther! Divn’t say a word more, ye’ll never beat thot. Occasion for ‘un--a godfeyther! Ha! ha! ha!’\n\nNever was man so tickled with a respectable old joke, as John Browdie was with this. He chuckled, roared, half suffocated himself by laughing large pieces of beef into his windpipe, roared again, persisted in eating at the same time, got red in the face and black in the forehead, coughed, cried, got better, went off again laughing inwardly, got worse, choked, had his back thumped, stamped about, frightened his wife, and at last recovered in a state of the last exhaustion and with the water streaming from his eyes, but still faintly ejaculating, ‘A godfeyther--a godfeyther, Tilly!’ in a tone bespeaking an exquisite relish of the sally, which no suffering could diminish.\n\n‘You remember the night of our first tea-drinking?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Shall I e’er forget it, mun?’ replied John Browdie.\n\n‘He was a desperate fellow that night though, was he not, Mrs. Browdie?’ said Nicholas. ‘Quite a monster!’\n\n‘If you had only heard him as we were going home, Mr. Nickleby, you’d have said so indeed,’ returned the bride. ‘I never was so frightened in all my life.’\n\n‘Coom, coom,’ said John, with a broad grin; ‘thou know’st betther than thot, Tilly.’\n\n‘So I was,’ replied Mrs. Browdie. ‘I almost made up my mind never to speak to you again.’\n\n‘A’most!’ said John, with a broader grin than the last. ‘A’most made up her mind! And she wur coaxin’, and coaxin’, and wheedlin’, and wheedlin’ a’ the blessed wa’. “Wa’at didst thou let yon chap mak’ oop tiv’ee for?” says I. “I deedn’t, John,” says she, a squeedgin my arm. “You deedn’t?” says I. “Noa,” says she, a squeedgin of me agean.’\n\n‘Lor, John!’ interposed his pretty wife, colouring very much. ‘How can you talk such nonsense? As if I should have dreamt of such a thing!’\n\n‘I dinnot know whether thou’d ever dreamt of it, though I think that’s loike eneaf, mind,’ retorted John; ‘but thou didst it. “Ye’re a feeckle, changeable weathercock, lass,” says I. “Not feeckle, John,” says she. “Yes,” says I, “feeckle, dom’d feeckle. Dinnot tell me thou bean’t, efther yon chap at schoolmeasther’s,” says I. “Him!” says she, quite screeching. “Ah! him!” says I. “Why, John,” says she--and she coom a deal closer and squeedged a deal harder than she’d deane afore--“dost thou think it’s nat’ral noo, that having such a proper mun as thou to keep company wi’, I’d ever tak’ opp wi’ such a leetle scanty whipper-snapper as yon?” she says. Ha! ha! ha! She said whipper-snapper! “Ecod!” I says, “efther thot, neame the day, and let’s have it ower!” Ha! ha! ha!’\n\nNicholas laughed very heartily at this story, both on account of its telling against himself, and his being desirous to spare the blushes of Mrs. Browdie, whose protestations were drowned in peals of laughter from her husband. His good-nature soon put her at her ease; and although she still denied the charge, she laughed so heartily at it, that Nicholas had the satisfaction of feeling assured that in all essential respects it was strictly true.\n\n‘This is the second time,’ said Nicholas, ‘that we have ever taken a meal together, and only third I have ever seen you; and yet it really seems to me as if I were among old friends.’\n\n‘Weel!’ observed the Yorkshireman, ‘so I say.’\n\n‘And I am sure I do,’ added his young wife.\n\n‘I have the best reason to be impressed with the feeling, mind,’ said Nicholas; ‘for if it had not been for your kindness of heart, my good friend, when I had no right or reason to expect it, I know not what might have become of me or what plight I should have been in by this time.’\n\n‘Talk aboot soom’at else,’ replied John, gruffly, ‘and dinnot bother.’\n\n‘It must be a new song to the same tune then,’ said Nicholas, smiling. ‘I told you in my letter that I deeply felt and admired your sympathy with that poor lad, whom you released at the risk of involving yourself in trouble and difficulty; but I can never tell you how grateful he and I, and others whom you don’t know, are to you for taking pity on him.’\n\n‘Ecod!’ rejoined John Browdie, drawing up his chair; ‘and I can never tell YOU hoo gratful soom folks that we do know would be loikewise, if THEY know’d I had takken pity on him.’\n\n‘Ah!’ exclaimed Mrs. Browdie, ‘what a state I was in that night!’\n\n‘Were they at all disposed to give you credit for assisting in the escape?’ inquired Nicholas of John Browdie.\n\n‘Not a bit,’ replied the Yorkshireman, extending his mouth from ear to ear. ‘There I lay, snoog in schoolmeasther’s bed long efther it was dark, and nobody coom nigh the pleace. “Weel!” thinks I, “he’s got a pretty good start, and if he bean’t whoam by noo, he never will be; so you may coom as quick as you loike, and foind us reddy”--that is, you know, schoolmeasther might coom.’\n\n‘I understand,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Presently,’ resumed John, ‘he DID coom. I heerd door shut doonstairs, and him a warking, oop in the daark. “Slow and steddy,” I says to myself, “tak’ your time, sir--no hurry.” He cooms to the door, turns the key--turns the key when there warn’t nothing to hoold the lock--and ca’s oot “Hallo, there!”--“Yes,” thinks I, “you may do thot agean, and not wakken anybody, sir.” “Hallo, there,” he says, and then he stops. “Thou’d betther not aggravate me,” says schoolmeasther, efther a little time. “I’ll brak’ every boan in your boddy, Smike,” he says, efther another little time. Then all of a soodden, he sings oot for a loight, and when it cooms--ecod, such a hoorly-boorly! “Wa’at’s the matter?” says I. “He’s gane,” says he,--stark mad wi’ vengeance. “Have you heerd nought?” “Ees,” says I, “I heerd street-door shut, no time at a’ ago. I heerd a person run doon there” (pointing t’other wa’--eh?) “Help!” he cries. “I’ll help you,” says I; and off we set--the wrong wa’! Ho! ho! ho!’\n\n‘Did you go far?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘Far!’ replied John; ‘I run him clean off his legs in quarther of an hoor. To see old schoolmeasther wi’out his hat, skimming along oop to his knees in mud and wather, tumbling over fences, and rowling into ditches, and bawling oot like mad, wi’ his one eye looking sharp out for the lad, and his coat-tails flying out behind, and him spattered wi’ mud all ower, face and all! I tho’t I should ha’ dropped doon, and killed myself wi’ laughing.’\n\nJohn laughed so heartily at the mere recollection, that he communicated the contagion to both his hearers, and all three burst into peals of laughter, which were renewed again and again, until they could laugh no longer.\n\n‘He’s a bad ‘un,’ said John, wiping his eyes; ‘a very bad ‘un, is schoolmeasther.’\n\n‘I can’t bear the sight of him, John,’ said his wife.\n\n‘Coom,’ retorted John, ‘thot’s tidy in you, thot is. If it wa’nt along o’ you, we shouldn’t know nought aboot ‘un. Thou know’d ‘un first, Tilly, didn’t thou?’\n\n‘I couldn’t help knowing Fanny Squeers, John,’ returned his wife; ‘she was an old playmate of mine, you know.’\n\n‘Weel,’ replied John, ‘dean’t I say so, lass? It’s best to be neighbourly, and keep up old acquaintance loike; and what I say is, dean’t quarrel if ‘ee can help it. Dinnot think so, Mr. Nickleby?’\n\n‘Certainly,’ returned Nicholas; ‘and you acted upon that principle when I meet you on horseback on the road, after our memorable evening.’\n\n‘Sure-ly,’ said John. ‘Wa’at I say, I stick by.’\n\n‘And that’s a fine thing to do, and manly too,’ said Nicholas, ‘though it’s not exactly what we understand by “coming Yorkshire over us” in London. Miss Squeers is stopping with you, you said in your note.’\n\n‘Yes,’ replied John, ‘Tilly’s bridesmaid; and a queer bridesmaid she be, too. She wean’t be a bride in a hurry, I reckon.’\n\n‘For shame, John,’ said Mrs. Browdie; with an acute perception of the joke though, being a bride herself.\n\n‘The groom will be a blessed mun,’ said John, his eyes twinkling at the idea. ‘He’ll be in luck, he will.’\n\n‘You see, Mr. Nickleby,’ said his wife, ‘that it was in consequence of her being here, that John wrote to you and fixed tonight, because we thought that it wouldn’t be pleasant for you to meet, after what has passed.’\n\n‘Unquestionably. You were quite right in that,’ said Nicholas, interrupting.\n\n‘Especially,’ observed Mrs. Browdie, looking very sly, ‘after what we know about past and gone love matters.’\n\n‘We know, indeed!’ said Nicholas, shaking his head. ‘You behaved rather wickedly there, I suspect.’\n\n‘O’ course she did,’ said John Browdie, passing his huge forefinger through one of his wife’s pretty ringlets, and looking very proud of her. ‘She wur always as skittish and full o’ tricks as a--’\n\n‘Well, as a what?’ said his wife.\n\n‘As a woman,’ returned John. ‘Ding! But I dinnot know ought else that cooms near it.’\n\n‘You were speaking about Miss Squeers,’ said Nicholas, with the view of stopping some slight connubialities which had begun to pass between Mr and Mrs. Browdie, and which rendered the position of a third party in some degree embarrassing, as occasioning him to feel rather in the way than otherwise.\n\n‘Oh yes,’ rejoined Mrs. Browdie. ‘John ha’ done. John fixed tonight, because she had settled that she would go and drink tea with her father. And to make quite sure of there being nothing amiss, and of your being quite alone with us, he settled to go out there and fetch her home.’\n\n‘That was a very good arrangement,’ said Nicholas, ‘though I am sorry to be the occasion of so much trouble.’\n\n‘Not the least in the world,’ returned Mrs. Browdie; ‘for we have looked forward to see you--John and I have--with the greatest possible pleasure. Do you know, Mr. Nickleby,’ said Mrs. Browdie, with her archest smile, ‘that I really think Fanny Squeers was very fond of you?’\n\n‘I am very much obliged to her,’ said Nicholas; ‘but upon my word, I never aspired to making any impression upon her virgin heart.’\n\n‘How you talk!’ tittered Mrs. Browdie. ‘No, but do you know that really--seriously now and without any joking--I was given to understand by Fanny herself, that you had made an offer to her, and that you two were going to be engaged quite solemn and regular.’\n\n‘Was you, ma’am--was you?’ cried a shrill female voice, ‘was you given to understand that I--I--was going to be engaged to an assassinating thief that shed the gore of my pa? Do you--do you think, ma’am--that I was very fond of such dirt beneath my feet, as I couldn’t condescend to touch with kitchen tongs, without blacking and crocking myself by the contract? Do you, ma’am--do you? Oh! base and degrading ‘Tilda!’\n\nWith these reproaches Miss Squeers flung the door wide open, and disclosed to the eyes of the astonished Browdies and Nicholas, not only her own symmetrical form, arrayed in the chaste white garments before described (a little dirtier), but the form of her brother and father, the pair of Wackfords.\n\n‘This is the hend, is it?’ continued Miss Squeers, who, being excited, aspirated her h’s strongly; ‘this is the hend, is it, of all my forbearance and friendship for that double-faced thing--that viper, that--that--mermaid?’ (Miss Squeers hesitated a long time for this last epithet, and brought it out triumphantly at last, as if it quite clinched the business.) ‘This is the hend, is it, of all my bearing with her deceitfulness, her lowness, her falseness, her laying herself out to catch the admiration of vulgar minds, in a way which made me blush for my--for my--’\n\n‘Gender,’ suggested Mr. Squeers, regarding the spectators with a malevolent eye--literally A malevolent eye.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Miss Squeers; ‘but I thank my stars that my ma is of the same--’\n\n‘Hear, hear!’ remarked Mr. Squeers; ‘and I wish she was here to have a scratch at this company.’\n\n‘This is the hend, is it,’ said Miss Squeers, tossing her head, and looking contemptuously at the floor, ‘of my taking notice of that rubbishing creature, and demeaning myself to patronise her?’\n\n‘Oh, come,’ rejoined Mrs. Browdie, disregarding all the endeavours of her spouse to restrain her, and forcing herself into a front row, ‘don’t talk such nonsense as that.’\n\n‘Have I not patronised you, ma’am?’ demanded Miss Squeers.\n\n‘No,’ returned Mrs. Browdie.\n\n‘I will not look for blushes in such a quarter,’ said Miss Squeers, haughtily, ‘for that countenance is a stranger to everything but hignominiousness and red-faced boldness.’\n\n‘I say,’ interposed John Browdie, nettled by these accumulated attacks on his wife, ‘dra’ it mild, dra’ it mild.’\n\n‘You, Mr. Browdie,’ said Miss Squeers, taking him up very quickly, ‘I pity. I have no feeling for you, sir, but one of unliquidated pity.’\n\n‘Oh!’ said John.\n\n‘No,’ said Miss Squeers, looking sideways at her parent, ‘although I AM a queer bridesmaid, and SHAN’T be a bride in a hurry, and although my husband WILL be in luck, I entertain no sentiments towards you, sir, but sentiments of pity.’\n\nHere Miss Squeers looked sideways at her father again, who looked sideways at her, as much as to say, ‘There you had him.’\n\n‘I know what you’ve got to go through,’ said Miss Squeers, shaking her curls violently. ‘I know what life is before you, and if you was my bitterest and deadliest enemy, I could wish you nothing worse.’\n\n‘Couldn’t you wish to be married to him yourself, if that was the case?’ inquired Mrs. Browdie, with great suavity of manner.\n\n‘Oh, ma’am, how witty you are,’ retorted Miss Squeers with a low curtsy, ‘almost as witty, ma’am, as you are clever. How very clever it was in you, ma’am, to choose a time when I had gone to tea with my pa, and was sure not to come back without being fetched! What a pity you never thought that other people might be as clever as yourself and spoil your plans!’\n\n‘You won’t vex me, child, with such airs as these,’ said the late Miss Price, assuming the matron.\n\n‘Don’t MISSIS me, ma’am, if you please,’ returned Miss Squeers, sharply. ‘I’ll not bear it. Is THIS the hend--’\n\n‘Dang it a’,’ cried John Browdie, impatiently. ‘Say thee say out, Fanny, and mak’ sure it’s the end, and dinnot ask nobody whether it is or not.’\n\n‘Thanking you for your advice which was not required, Mr. Browdie,’ returned Miss Squeers, with laborious politeness, ‘have the goodness not to presume to meddle with my Christian name. Even my pity shall never make me forget what’s due to myself, Mr. Browdie. ‘Tilda,’ said Miss Squeers, with such a sudden accession of violence that John started in his boots, ‘I throw you off for ever, miss. I abandon you. I renounce you. I wouldn’t,’ cried Miss Squeers in a solemn voice, ‘have a child named ‘Tilda, not to save it from its grave.’\n\n‘As for the matther o’ that,’ observed John, ‘it’ll be time eneaf to think aboot neaming of it when it cooms.’\n\n‘John!’ interposed his wife, ‘don’t tease her.’\n\n‘Oh! Tease, indeed!’ cried Miss Squeers, bridling up. ‘Tease, indeed! He, he! Tease, too! No, don’t tease her. Consider her feelings, pray!’\n\n‘If it’s fated that listeners are never to hear any good of themselves,’ said Mrs. Browdie, ‘I can’t help it, and I am very sorry for it. But I will say, Fanny, that times out of number I have spoken so kindly of you behind your back, that even you could have found no fault with what I said.’\n\n‘Oh, I dare say not, ma’am!’ cried Miss Squeers, with another curtsy. ‘Best thanks to you for your goodness, and begging and praying you not to be hard upon me another time!’\n\n‘I don’t know,’ resumed Mrs. Browdie, ‘that I have said anything very bad of you, even now. At all events, what I did say was quite true; but if I have, I am very sorry for it, and I beg your pardon. You have said much worse of me, scores of times, Fanny; but I have never borne any malice to you, and I hope you’ll not bear any to me.’\n\nMiss Squeers made no more direct reply than surveying her former friend from top to toe, and elevating her nose in the air with ineffable disdain. But some indistinct allusions to a ‘puss,’ and a ‘minx,’ and a ‘contemptible creature,’ escaped her; and this, together with a severe biting of the lips, great difficulty in swallowing, and very frequent comings and goings of breath, seemed to imply that feelings were swelling in Miss Squeers’s bosom too great for utterance.\n\nWhile the foregoing conversation was proceeding, Master Wackford, finding himself unnoticed, and feeling his preponderating inclinations strong upon him, had by little and little sidled up to the table and attacked the food with such slight skirmishing as drawing his fingers round and round the inside of the plates, and afterwards sucking them with infinite relish; picking the bread, and dragging the pieces over the surface of the butter; pocketing lumps of sugar, pretending all the time to be absorbed in thought; and so forth. Finding that no interference was attempted with these small liberties, he gradually mounted to greater, and, after helping himself to a moderately good cold collation, was, by this time, deep in the pie.\n\nNothing of this had been unobserved by Mr. Squeers, who, so long as the attention of the company was fixed upon other objects, hugged himself to think that his son and heir should be fattening at the enemy’s expense. But there being now an appearance of a temporary calm, in which the proceedings of little Wackford could scarcely fail to be observed, he feigned to be aware of the circumstance for the first time, and inflicted upon the face of that young gentleman a slap that made the very tea-cups ring.\n\n‘Eating!’ cried Mr. Squeers, ‘of what his father’s enemies has left! It’s fit to go and poison you, you unnat’ral boy.’\n\n‘It wean’t hurt him,’ said John, apparently very much relieved by the prospect of having a man in the quarrel; ‘let’ un eat. I wish the whole school was here. I’d give’em soom’at to stay their unfort’nate stomachs wi’, if I spent the last penny I had!’\n\nSqueers scowled at him with the worst and most malicious expression of which his face was capable--it was a face of remarkable capability, too, in that way--and shook his fist stealthily.\n\n‘Coom, coom, schoolmeasther,’ said John, ‘dinnot make a fool o’ thyself; for if I was to sheake mine--only once--thou’d fa’ doon wi’ the wind o’ it.’\n\n‘It was you, was it,’ returned Squeers, ‘that helped off my runaway boy? It was you, was it?’\n\n‘Me!’ returned John, in a loud tone. ‘Yes, it wa’ me, coom; wa’at o’ that? It wa’ me. Noo then!’\n\n‘You hear him say he did it, my child!’ said Squeers, appealing to his daughter. ‘You hear him say he did it!’\n\n‘Did it!’ cried John. ‘I’ll tell ‘ee more; hear this, too. If thou’d got another roonaway boy, I’d do it agean. If thou’d got twonty roonaway boys, I’d do it twonty times ower, and twonty more to thot; and I tell thee more,’ said John, ‘noo my blood is oop, that thou’rt an old ra’ascal; and that it’s weel for thou, thou be’est an old ‘un, or I’d ha’ poonded thee to flour when thou told an honest mun hoo thou’d licked that poor chap in t’ coorch.’\n\n‘An honest man!’ cried Squeers, with a sneer.\n\n‘Ah! an honest man,’ replied John; ‘honest in ought but ever putting legs under seame table wi’ such as thou.’\n\n‘Scandal!’ said Squeers, exultingly. ‘Two witnesses to it; Wackford knows the nature of an oath, he does; we shall have you there, sir. Rascal, eh?’ Mr. Squeers took out his pocketbook and made a note of it. ‘Very good. I should say that was worth full twenty pound at the next assizes, without the honesty, sir.’\n\n‘’Soizes,’ cried John, ‘thou’d betther not talk to me o’ ‘Soizes. Yorkshire schools have been shown up at ‘Soizes afore noo, mun, and it’s a ticklish soobjact to revive, I can tell ye.’\n\nMr. Squeers shook his head in a threatening manner, looking very white with passion; and taking his daughter’s arm, and dragging little Wackford by the hand, retreated towards the door.\n\n‘As for you,’ said Squeers, turning round and addressing Nicholas, who, as he had caused him to smart pretty soundly on a former occasion, purposely abstained from taking any part in the discussion, ‘see if I ain’t down upon you before long. You’ll go a kidnapping of boys, will you? Take care their fathers don’t turn up--mark that--take care their fathers don’t turn up, and send ‘em back to me to do as I like with, in spite of you.’\n\n‘I am not afraid of that,’ replied Nicholas, shrugging his shoulders contemptuously, and turning away.\n\n‘Ain’t you!’ retorted Squeers, with a diabolical look. ‘Now then, come along.’\n\n‘I leave such society, with my pa, for Hever,’ said Miss Squeers, looking contemptuously and loftily round. ‘I am defiled by breathing the air with such creatures. Poor Mr. Browdie! He! he! he! I do pity him, that I do; he’s so deluded. He! he! he!--Artful and designing ‘Tilda!’\n\nWith this sudden relapse into the sternest and most majestic wrath, Miss Squeers swept from the room; and having sustained her dignity until the last possible moment, was heard to sob and scream and struggle in the passage.\n\nJohn Browdie remained standing behind the table, looking from his wife to Nicholas, and back again, with his mouth wide open, until his hand accidentally fell upon the tankard of ale, when he took it up, and having obscured his features therewith for some time, drew a long breath, handed it over to Nicholas, and rang the bell.\n\n‘Here, waither,’ said John, briskly. ‘Look alive here. Tak’ these things awa’, and let’s have soomat broiled for sooper--vary comfortable and plenty o’ it--at ten o’clock. Bring soom brandy and soom wather, and a pair o’ slippers--the largest pair in the house--and be quick aboot it. Dash ma wig!’ said John, rubbing his hands, ‘there’s no ganging oot to neeght, noo, to fetch anybody whoam, and ecod, we’ll begin to spend the evening in airnest.’\n\n# CHAPTER 43\n\nOfficiates as a kind of Gentleman Usher, in bringing various People together\nThe storm had long given place to a calm the most profound, and the evening was pretty far advanced--indeed supper was over, and the process of digestion proceeding as favourably as, under the influence of complete tranquillity, cheerful conversation, and a moderate allowance of brandy-and-water, most wise men conversant with the anatomy and functions of the human frame will consider that it ought to have proceeded, when the three friends, or as one might say, both in a civil and religious sense, and with proper deference and regard to the holy state of matrimony, the two friends, (Mr. and Mrs. Browdie counting as no more than one,) were startled by the noise of loud and angry threatenings below stairs, which presently attained so high a pitch, and were conveyed besides in language so towering, sanguinary, and ferocious, that it could hardly have been surpassed, if there had actually been a Saracen’s head then present in the establishment, supported on the shoulders and surmounting the trunk of a real, live, furious, and most unappeasable Saracen.\n\nThis turmoil, instead of quickly subsiding after the first outburst, (as turmoils not unfrequently do, whether in taverns, legislative assemblies, or elsewhere,) into a mere grumbling and growling squabble, increased every moment; and although the whole din appeared to be raised by but one pair of lungs, yet that one pair was of so powerful a quality, and repeated such words as ‘scoundrel,’ ‘rascal,’ ‘insolent puppy,’ and a variety of expletives no less flattering to the party addressed, with such great relish and strength of tone, that a dozen voices raised in concert under any ordinary circumstances would have made far less uproar and created much smaller consternation.\n\n‘Why, what’s the matter?’ said Nicholas, moving hastily towards the door.\n\nJohn Browdie was striding in the same direction when Mrs. Browdie turned pale, and, leaning back in her chair, requested him with a faint voice to take notice, that if he ran into any danger it was her intention to fall into hysterics immediately, and that the consequences might be more serious than he thought for. John looked rather disconcerted by this intelligence, though there was a lurking grin on his face at the same time; but, being quite unable to keep out of the fray, he compromised the matter by tucking his wife’s arm under his own, and, thus accompanied, following Nicholas downstairs with all speed.\n\nThe passage outside the coffee-room door was the scene of disturbance, and here were congregated the coffee-room customers and waiters, together with two or three coachmen and helpers from the yard. These had hastily assembled round a young man who from his appearance might have been a year or two older than Nicholas, and who, besides having given utterance to the defiances just now described, seemed to have proceeded to even greater lengths in his indignation, inasmuch as his feet had no other covering than a pair of stockings, while a couple of slippers lay at no great distance from the head of a prostrate figure in an opposite corner, who bore the appearance of having been shot into his present retreat by means of a kick, and complimented by having the slippers flung about his ears afterwards.\n\nThe coffee-room customers, and the waiters, and the coachmen, and the helpers--not to mention a barmaid who was looking on from behind an open sash window--seemed at that moment, if a spectator might judge from their winks, nods, and muttered exclamations, strongly disposed to take part against the young gentleman in the stockings. Observing this, and that the young gentleman was nearly of his own age and had in nothing the appearance of an habitual brawler, Nicholas, impelled by such feelings as will influence young men sometimes, felt a very strong disposition to side with the weaker party, and so thrust himself at once into the centre of the group, and in a more emphatic tone, perhaps, than circumstances might seem to warrant, demanded what all that noise was about.\n\n‘Hallo!’ said one of the men from the yard, ‘this is somebody in disguise, this is.’\n\n‘Room for the eldest son of the Emperor of Roosher, gen’l’men!’ cried another fellow.\n\nDisregarding these sallies, which were uncommonly well received, as sallies at the expense of the best-dressed persons in a crowd usually are, Nicholas glanced carelessly round, and addressing the young gentleman, who had by this time picked up his slippers and thrust his feet into them, repeated his inquiries with a courteous air.\n\n‘A mere nothing!’ he replied.\n\nAt this a murmur was raised by the lookers-on, and some of the boldest cried, ‘Oh, indeed!--Wasn’t it though?--Nothing, eh?--He called that nothing, did he? Lucky for him if he found it nothing.’ These and many other expressions of ironical disapprobation having been exhausted, two or three of the out-of-door fellows began to hustle Nicholas and the young gentleman who had made the noise: stumbling against them by accident, and treading on their toes, and so forth. But this being a round game, and one not necessarily limited to three or four players, was open to John Browdie too, who, bursting into the little crowd--to the great terror of his wife--and falling about in all directions, now to the right, now to the left, now forwards, now backwards, and accidentally driving his elbow through the hat of the tallest helper, who had been particularly active, speedily caused the odds to wear a very different appearance; while more than one stout fellow limped away to a respectful distance, anathematising with tears in his eyes the heavy tread and ponderous feet of the burly Yorkshireman.\n\n‘Let me see him do it again,’ said he who had been kicked into the corner, rising as he spoke, apparently more from the fear of John Browdie’s inadvertently treading upon him, than from any desire to place himself on equal terms with his late adversary. ‘Let me see him do it again. That’s all.’\n\n‘Let me hear you make those remarks again,’ said the young man, ‘and I’ll knock that head of yours in among the wine-glasses behind you there.’\n\nHere a waiter who had been rubbing his hands in excessive enjoyment of the scene, so long as only the breaking of heads was in question, adjured the spectators with great earnestness to fetch the police, declaring that otherwise murder would be surely done, and that he was responsible for all the glass and china on the premises.\n\n‘No one need trouble himself to stir,’ said the young gentleman, ‘I am going to remain in the house all night, and shall be found here in the morning if there is any assault to answer for.’\n\n‘What did you strike him for?’ asked one of the bystanders.\n\n‘Ah! what did you strike him for?’ demanded the others.\n\nThe unpopular gentleman looked coolly round, and addressing himself to Nicholas, said:\n\n‘You inquired just now what was the matter here. The matter is simply this. Yonder person, who was drinking with a friend in the coffee-room when I took my seat there for half an hour before going to bed, (for I have just come off a journey, and preferred stopping here tonight, to going home at this hour, where I was not expected until tomorrow,) chose to express himself in very disrespectful, and insolently familiar terms, of a young lady, whom I recognised from his description and other circumstances, and whom I have the honour to know. As he spoke loud enough to be overheard by the other guests who were present, I informed him most civilly that he was mistaken in his conjectures, which were of an offensive nature, and requested him to forbear. He did so for a little time, but as he chose to renew his conversation when leaving the room, in a more offensive strain than before, I could not refrain from making after him, and facilitating his departure by a kick, which reduced him to the posture in which you saw him just now. I am the best judge of my own affairs, I take it,’ said the young man, who had certainly not quite recovered from his recent heat; ‘if anybody here thinks proper to make this quarrel his own, I have not the smallest earthly objection, I do assure him.’\n\nOf all possible courses of proceeding under the circumstances detailed, there was certainly not one which, in his then state of mind, could have appeared more laudable to Nicholas than this. There were not many subjects of dispute which at that moment could have come home to his own breast more powerfully, for having the unknown uppermost in his thoughts, it naturally occurred to him that he would have done just the same if any audacious gossiper durst have presumed in his hearing to speak lightly of her. Influenced by these considerations, he espoused the young gentleman’s quarrel with great warmth, protesting that he had done quite right, and that he respected him for it; which John Browdie (albeit not quite clear as to the merits) immediately protested too, with not inferior vehemence.\n\n‘Let him take care, that’s all,’ said the defeated party, who was being rubbed down by a waiter, after his recent fall on the dusty boards. ‘He don’t knock me about for nothing, I can tell him that. A pretty state of things, if a man isn’t to admire a handsome girl without being beat to pieces for it!’\n\nThis reflection appeared to have great weight with the young lady in the bar, who (adjusting her cap as she spoke, and glancing at a mirror) declared that it would be a very pretty state of things indeed; and that if people were to be punished for actions so innocent and natural as that, there would be more people to be knocked down than there would be people to knock them down, and that she wondered what the gentleman meant by it, that she did.\n\n‘My dear girl,’ said the young gentleman in a low voice, advancing towards the sash window.\n\n‘Nonsense, sir!’ replied the young lady sharply, smiling though as she turned aside, and biting her lip, (whereat Mrs. Browdie, who was still standing on the stairs, glanced at her with disdain, and called to her husband to come away).\n\n‘No, but listen to me,’ said the young man. ‘If admiration of a pretty face were criminal, I should be the most hopeless person alive, for I cannot resist one. It has the most extraordinary effect upon me, checks and controls me in the most furious and obstinate mood. You see what an effect yours has had upon me already.’\n\n‘Oh, that’s very pretty,’ replied the young lady, tossing her head, ‘but--’\n\n‘Yes, I know it’s very pretty,’ said the young man, looking with an air of admiration in the barmaid’s face; ‘I said so, you know, just this moment. But beauty should be spoken of respectfully--respectfully, and in proper terms, and with a becoming sense of its worth and excellence, whereas this fellow has no more notion--’\n\nThe young lady interrupted the conversation at this point, by thrusting her head out of the bar-window, and inquiring of the waiter in a shrill voice whether that young man who had been knocked down was going to stand in the passage all night, or whether the entrance was to be left clear for other people. The waiters taking the hint, and communicating it to the hostlers, were not slow to change their tone too, and the result was, that the unfortunate victim was bundled out in a twinkling.\n\n‘I am sure I have seen that fellow before,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Indeed!’ replied his new acquaintance.\n\n‘I am certain of it,’ said Nicholas, pausing to reflect. ‘Where can I have--stop!--yes, to be sure--he belongs to a register-office up at the west end of the town. I knew I recollected the face.’\n\nIt was, indeed, Tom, the ugly clerk.\n\n‘That’s odd enough!’ said Nicholas, ruminating upon the strange manner in which the register-office seemed to start up and stare him in the face every now and then, and when he least expected it.\n\n‘I am much obliged to you for your kind advocacy of my cause when it most needed an advocate,’ said the young man, laughing, and drawing a card from his pocket. ‘Perhaps you’ll do me the favour to let me know where I can thank you.’\n\nNicholas took the card, and glancing at it involuntarily as he returned the compliment, evinced very great surprise.\n\n‘Mr. Frank Cheeryble!’ said Nicholas. ‘Surely not the nephew of Cheeryble Brothers, who is expected tomorrow!’\n\n‘I don’t usually call myself the nephew of the firm,’ returned Mr. Frank, good-humouredly; ‘but of the two excellent individuals who compose it, I am proud to say I AM the nephew. And you, I see, are Mr. Nickleby, of whom I have heard so much! This is a most unexpected meeting, but not the less welcome, I assure you.’\n\nNicholas responded to these compliments with others of the same kind, and they shook hands warmly. Then he introduced John Browdie, who had remained in a state of great admiration ever since the young lady in the bar had been so skilfully won over to the right side. Then Mrs. John Browdie was introduced, and finally they all went upstairs together and spent the next half-hour with great satisfaction and mutual entertainment; Mrs. John Browdie beginning the conversation by declaring that of all the made-up things she ever saw, that young woman below-stairs was the vainest and the plainest.\n\nThis Mr. Frank Cheeryble, although, to judge from what had recently taken place, a hot-headed young man (which is not an absolute miracle and phenomenon in nature), was a sprightly, good-humoured, pleasant fellow, with much both in his countenance and disposition that reminded Nicholas very strongly of the kind-hearted brothers. His manner was as unaffected as theirs, and his demeanour full of that heartiness which, to most people who have anything generous in their composition, is peculiarly prepossessing. Add to this, that he was good-looking and intelligent, had a plentiful share of vivacity, was extremely cheerful, and accommodated himself in five minutes’ time to all John Browdie’s oddities with as much ease as if he had known him from a boy; and it will be a source of no great wonder that, when they parted for the night, he had produced a most favourable impression, not only upon the worthy Yorkshireman and his wife, but upon Nicholas also, who, revolving all these things in his mind as he made the best of his way home, arrived at the conclusion that he had laid the foundation of a most agreeable and desirable acquaintance.\n\n‘But it’s a most extraordinary thing about that register-office fellow!’ thought Nicholas. ‘Is it likely that this nephew can know anything about that beautiful girl? When Tim Linkinwater gave me to understand the other day that he was coming to take a share in the business here, he said he had been superintending it in Germany for four years, and that during the last six months he had been engaged in establishing an agency in the north of England. That’s four years and a half--four years and a half. She can’t be more than seventeen--say eighteen at the outside. She was quite a child when he went away, then. I should say he knew nothing about her and had never seen her, so HE can give me no information. At all events,’ thought Nicholas, coming to the real point in his mind, ‘there can be no danger of any prior occupation of her affections in that quarter; that’s quite clear.’\n\nIs selfishness a necessary ingredient in the composition of that passion called love, or does it deserve all the fine things which poets, in the exercise of their undoubted vocation, have said of it? There are, no doubt, authenticated instances of gentlemen having given up ladies and ladies having given up gentlemen to meritorious rivals, under circumstances of great high-mindedness; but is it quite established that the majority of such ladies and gentlemen have not made a virtue of necessity, and nobly resigned what was beyond their reach; as a private soldier might register a vow never to accept the order of the Garter, or a poor curate of great piety and learning, but of no family--save a very large family of children--might renounce a bishopric?\n\nHere was Nicholas Nickleby, who would have scorned the thought of counting how the chances stood of his rising in favour or fortune with the brothers Cheeryble, now that their nephew had returned, already deep in calculations whether that same nephew was likely to rival him in the affections of the fair unknown--discussing the matter with himself too, as gravely as if, with that one exception, it were all settled; and recurring to the subject again and again, and feeling quite indignant and ill-used at the notion of anybody else making love to one with whom he had never exchanged a word in all his life. To be sure, he exaggerated rather than depreciated the merits of his new acquaintance; but still he took it as a kind of personal offence that he should have any merits at all--in the eyes of this particular young lady, that is; for elsewhere he was quite welcome to have as many as he pleased. There was undoubted selfishness in all this, and yet Nicholas was of a most free and generous nature, with as few mean or sordid thoughts, perhaps, as ever fell to the lot of any man; and there is no reason to suppose that, being in love, he felt and thought differently from other people in the like sublime condition.\n\nHe did not stop to set on foot an inquiry into his train of thought or state of feeling, however; but went thinking on all the way home, and continued to dream on in the same strain all night. For, having satisfied himself that Frank Cheeryble could have no knowledge of, or acquaintance with, the mysterious young lady, it began to occur to him that even he himself might never see her again; upon which hypothesis he built up a very ingenious succession of tormenting ideas which answered his purpose even better than the vision of Mr. Frank Cheeryble, and tantalised and worried him, waking and sleeping.\n\nNotwithstanding all that has been said and sung to the contrary, there is no well-established case of morning having either deferred or hastened its approach by the term of an hour or so for the mere gratification of a splenetic feeling against some unoffending lover: the sun having, in the discharge of his public duty, as the books of precedent report, invariably risen according to the almanacs, and without suffering himself to be swayed by any private considerations. So, morning came as usual, and with it business-hours, and with them Mr Frank Cheeryble, and with him a long train of smiles and welcomes from the worthy brothers, and a more grave and clerk-like, but scarcely less hearty reception from Mr. Timothy Linkinwater.\n\n‘That Mr. Frank and Mr. Nickleby should have met last night,’ said Tim Linkinwater, getting slowly off his stool, and looking round the counting-house with his back planted against the desk, as was his custom when he had anything very particular to say: ‘that those two young men should have met last night in that manner is, I say, a coincidence, a remarkable coincidence. Why, I don’t believe now,’ added Tim, taking off his spectacles, and smiling as with gentle pride, ‘that there’s such a place in all the world for coincidences as London is!’\n\n‘I don’t know about that,’ said Mr. Frank; ‘but--’\n\n‘Don’t know about it, Mr. Francis!’ interrupted Tim, with an obstinate air. ‘Well, but let us know. If there is any better place for such things, where is it? Is it in Europe? No, that it isn’t. Is it in Asia? Why, of course it’s not. Is it in Africa? Not a bit of it. Is it in America? YOU know better than that, at all events. Well, then,’ said Tim, folding his arms resolutely, ‘where is it?’\n\n‘I was not about to dispute the point, Tim,’ said young Cheeryble, laughing. ‘I am not such a heretic as that. All I was going to say was, that I hold myself under an obligation to the coincidence, that’s all.’\n\n‘Oh! if you don’t dispute it,’ said Tim, quite satisfied, ‘that’s another thing. I’ll tell you what though. I wish you had. I wish you or anybody would. I would so put that man down,’ said Tim, tapping the forefinger of his left hand emphatically with his spectacles, ‘so put that man down by argument--’\n\nIt was quite impossible to find language to express the degree of mental prostration to which such an adventurous wight would be reduced in the keen encounter with Tim Linkinwater, so Tim gave up the rest of his declaration in pure lack of words, and mounted his stool again.\n\n‘We may consider ourselves, brother Ned,’ said Charles, after he had patted Tim Linkinwater approvingly on the back, ‘very fortunate in having two such young men about us as our nephew Frank and Mr. Nickleby. It should be a source of great satisfaction and pleasure to us.’\n\n‘Certainly, Charles, certainly,’ returned the other.\n\n‘Of Tim,’ added brother Ned, ‘I say nothing whatever, because Tim is a mere child--an infant--a nobody that we never think of or take into account at all. Tim, you villain, what do you say to that, sir?’\n\n‘I am jealous of both of ‘em,’ said Tim, ‘and mean to look out for another situation; so provide yourselves, gentlemen, if you please.’\n\nTim thought this such an exquisite, unparalleled, and most extraordinary joke, that he laid his pen upon the inkstand, and rather tumbling off his stool than getting down with his usual deliberation, laughed till he was quite faint, shaking his head all the time so that little particles of powder flew palpably about the office. Nor were the brothers at all behind-hand, for they laughed almost as heartily at the ludicrous idea of any voluntary separation between themselves and old Tim. Nicholas and Mr. Frank laughed quite boisterously, perhaps to conceal some other emotion awakened by this little incident, (and so, indeed, did the three old fellows after the first burst,) so perhaps there was as much keen enjoyment and relish in that laugh, altogether, as the politest assembly ever derived from the most poignant witticism uttered at any one person’s expense.\n\n‘Mr. Nickleby,’ said brother Charles, calling him aside, and taking him kindly by the hand, ‘I--I--am anxious, my dear sir, to see that you are properly and comfortably settled in the cottage. We cannot allow those who serve us well to labour under any privation or discomfort that it is in our power to remove. I wish, too, to see your mother and sister: to know them, Mr. Nickleby, and have an opportunity of relieving their minds by assuring them that any trifling service we have been able to do them is a great deal more than repaid by the zeal and ardour you display.--Not a word, my dear sir, I beg. Tomorrow is Sunday. I shall make bold to come out at teatime, and take the chance of finding you at home; if you are not, you know, or the ladies should feel a delicacy in being intruded on, and would rather not be known to me just now, why I can come again another time, any other time would do for me. Let it remain upon that understanding. Brother Ned, my dear fellow, let me have a word with you this way.’\n\nThe twins went out of the office arm-in-arm, and Nicholas, who saw in this act of kindness, and many others of which he had been the subject that morning, only so many delicate renewals on the arrival of their nephew of the kind assurance which the brothers had given him in his absence, could scarcely feel sufficient admiration and gratitude for such extraordinary consideration.\n\nThe intelligence that they were to have a visitor--and such a visitor--next day, awakened in the breast of Mrs. Nickleby mingled feelings of exultation and regret; for whereas on the one hand she hailed it as an omen of her speedy restoration to good society and the almost-forgotten pleasures of morning calls and evening tea-drinkings, she could not, on the other, but reflect with bitterness of spirit on the absence of a silver teapot with an ivory knob on the lid, and a milk-jug to match, which had been the pride of her heart in days of yore, and had been kept from year’s end to year’s end wrapped up in wash-leather on a certain top shelf which now presented itself in lively colours to her sorrowing imagination.\n\n‘I wonder who’s got that spice-box,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, shaking her head. ‘It used to stand in the left-hand corner, next but two to the pickled onions. You remember that spice-box, Kate?’\n\n‘Perfectly well, mama.’\n\n‘I shouldn’t think you did, Kate,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby, in a severe manner, ‘talking about it in that cold and unfeeling way! If there is any one thing that vexes me in these losses more than the losses themselves, I do protest and declare,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, rubbing her nose with an impassioned air, ‘that it is to have people about me who take things with such provoking calmness.’\n\n‘My dear mama,’ said Kate, stealing her arm round her mother’s neck, ‘why do you say what I know you cannot seriously mean or think, or why be angry with me for being happy and content? You and Nicholas are left to me, we are together once again, and what regard can I have for a few trifling things of which we never feel the want? When I have seen all the misery and desolation that death can bring, and known the lonesome feeling of being solitary and alone in crowds, and all the agony of separation in grief and poverty when we most needed comfort and support from each other, can you wonder that I look upon this as a place of such delicious quiet and rest, that with you beside me I have nothing to wish for or regret? There was a time, and not long since, when all the comforts of our old home did come back upon me, I own, very often--oftener than you would think perhaps--but I affected to care nothing for them, in the hope that you would so be brought to regret them the less. I was not insensible, indeed. I might have felt happier if I had been. Dear mama,’ said Kate, in great agitation, ‘I know no difference between this home and that in which we were all so happy for so many years, except that the kindest and gentlest heart that ever ached on earth has passed in peace to heaven.’\n\n‘Kate my dear, Kate,’ cried Mrs. Nickleby, folding her in her arms.\n\n‘I have so often thought,’ sobbed Kate, ‘of all his kind words--of the last time he looked into my little room, as he passed upstairs to bed, and said “God bless you, darling.” There was a paleness in his face, mama--the broken heart--I know it was--I little thought so--then--’\n\nA gush of tears came to her relief, and Kate laid her head upon her mother’s breast, and wept like a little child.\n\nIt is an exquisite and beautiful thing in our nature, that when the heart is touched and softened by some tranquil happiness or affectionate feeling, the memory of the dead comes over it most powerfully and irresistibly. It would almost seem as though our better thoughts and sympathies were charms, in virtue of which the soul is enabled to hold some vague and mysterious intercourse with the spirits of those whom we dearly loved in life. Alas! how often and how long may those patient angels hover above us, watching for the spell which is so seldom uttered, and so soon forgotten!\n\nPoor Mrs. Nickleby, accustomed to give ready utterance to whatever came uppermost in her mind, had never conceived the possibility of her daughter’s dwelling upon these thoughts in secret, the more especially as no hard trial or querulous reproach had ever drawn them from her. But now, when the happiness of all that Nicholas had just told them, and of their new and peaceful life, brought these recollections so strongly upon Kate that she could not suppress them, Mrs. Nickleby began to have a glimmering that she had been rather thoughtless now and then, and was conscious of something like self-reproach as she embraced her daughter, and yielded to the emotions which such a conversation naturally awakened.\n\nThere was a mighty bustle that night, and a vast quantity of preparation for the expected visitor, and a very large nosegay was brought from a gardener’s hard by, and cut up into a number of very small ones, with which Mrs. Nickleby would have garnished the little sitting-room, in a style that certainly could not have failed to attract anybody’s attention, if Kate had not offered to spare her the trouble, and arranged them in the prettiest and neatest manner possible. If the cottage ever looked pretty, it must have been on such a bright and sunshiny day as the next day was. But Smike’s pride in the garden, or Mrs. Nickleby’s in the condition of the furniture, or Kate’s in everything, was nothing to the pride with which Nicholas looked at Kate herself; and surely the costliest mansion in all England might have found in her beautiful face and graceful form its most exquisite and peerless ornament.\n\nAbout six o’clock in the afternoon Mrs. Nickleby was thrown into a great flutter of spirits by the long-expected knock at the door, nor was this flutter at all composed by the audible tread of two pair of boots in the passage, which Mrs. Nickleby augured, in a breathless state, must be ‘the two Mr. Cheerybles;’ as it certainly was, though not the two Mrs. Nickleby expected, because it was Mr. Charles Cheeryble, and his nephew, Mr. Frank, who made a thousand apologies for his intrusion, which Mrs. Nickleby (having tea-spoons enough and to spare for all) most graciously received. Nor did the appearance of this unexpected visitor occasion the least embarrassment, (save in Kate, and that only to the extent of a blush or two at first,) for the old gentleman was so kind and cordial, and the young gentleman imitated him in this respect so well, that the usual stiffness and formality of a first meeting showed no signs of appearing, and Kate really more than once detected herself in the very act of wondering when it was going to begin.\n\nAt the tea-table there was plenty of conversation on a great variety of subjects, nor were there wanting jocose matters of discussion, such as they were; for young Mr. Cheeryble’s recent stay in Germany happening to be alluded to, old Mr. Cheeryble informed the company that the aforesaid young Mr. Cheeryble was suspected to have fallen deeply in love with the daughter of a certain German burgomaster. This accusation young Mr. Cheeryble most indignantly repelled, upon which Mrs. Nickleby slyly remarked, that she suspected, from the very warmth of the denial, there must be something in it. Young Mr. Cheeryble then earnestly entreated old Mr. Cheeryble to confess that it was all a jest, which old Mr. Cheeryble at last did, young Mr. Cheeryble being so much in earnest about it, that--as Mrs. Nickleby said many thousand times afterwards in recalling the scene--he ‘quite coloured,’ which she rightly considered a memorable circumstance, and one worthy of remark, young men not being as a class remarkable for modesty or self-denial, especially when there is a lady in the case, when, if they colour at all, it is rather their practice to colour the story, and not themselves.\n\nAfter tea there was a walk in the garden, and the evening being very fine they strolled out at the garden-gate into some lanes and bye-roads, and sauntered up and down until it grew quite dark. The time seemed to pass very quickly with all the party. Kate went first, leaning upon her brother’s arm, and talking with him and Mr. Frank Cheeryble; and Mrs. Nickleby and the elder gentleman followed at a short distance, the kindness of the good merchant, his interest in the welfare of Nicholas, and his admiration of Kate, so operating upon the good lady’s feelings, that the usual current of her speech was confined within very narrow and circumscribed limits. Smike (who, if he had ever been an object of interest in his life, had been one that day) accompanied them, joining sometimes one group and sometimes the other, as brother Charles, laying his hand upon his shoulder, bade him walk with him, or Nicholas, looking smilingly round, beckoned him to come and talk with the old friend who understood him best, and who could win a smile into his careworn face when none else could.\n\nPride is one of the seven deadly sins; but it cannot be the pride of a mother in her children, for that is a compound of two cardinal virtues--faith and hope. This was the pride which swelled Mrs. Nickleby’s heart that night, and this it was which left upon her face, glistening in the light when they returned home, traces of the most grateful tears she had ever shed.\n\nThere was a quiet mirth about the little supper, which harmonised exactly with this tone of feeling, and at length the two gentlemen took their leave. There was one circumstance in the leave-taking which occasioned a vast deal of smiling and pleasantry, and that was, that Mr Frank Cheeryble offered his hand to Kate twice over, quite forgetting that he had bade her adieu already. This was held by the elder Mr Cheeryble to be a convincing proof that he was thinking of his German flame, and the jest occasioned immense laughter. So easy is it to move light hearts.\n\nIn short, it was a day of serene and tranquil happiness; and as we all have some bright day--many of us, let us hope, among a crowd of others--to which we revert with particular delight, so this one was often looked back to afterwards, as holding a conspicuous place in the calendar of those who shared it.\n\nWas there one exception, and that one he who needed to have been most happy?\n\nWho was that who, in the silence of his own chamber, sunk upon his knees to pray as his first friend had taught him, and folding his hands and stretching them wildly in the air, fell upon his face in a passion of bitter grief?\n\n# CHAPTER 44\n\nMr. Ralph Nickleby cuts an old Acquaintance. It would also appear from the Contents hereof, that a Joke, even between Husband and Wife, may be sometimes carried too far\nThere are some men who, living with the one object of enriching themselves, no matter by what means, and being perfectly conscious of the baseness and rascality of the means which they will use every day towards this end, affect nevertheless--even to themselves--a high tone of moral rectitude, and shake their heads and sigh over the depravity of the world. Some of the craftiest scoundrels that ever walked this earth, or rather--for walking implies, at least, an erect position and the bearing of a man--that ever crawled and crept through life by its dirtiest and narrowest ways, will gravely jot down in diaries the events of every day, and keep a regular debtor and creditor account with Heaven, which shall always show a floating balance in their own favour. Whether this is a gratuitous (the only gratuitous) part of the falsehood and trickery of such men’s lives, or whether they really hope to cheat Heaven itself, and lay up treasure in the next world by the same process which has enabled them to lay up treasure in this--not to question how it is, so it is. And, doubtless, such book-keeping (like certain autobiographies which have enlightened the world) cannot fail to prove serviceable, in the one respect of sparing the recording Angel some time and labour.\n\nRalph Nickleby was not a man of this stamp. Stern, unyielding, dogged, and impenetrable, Ralph cared for nothing in life, or beyond it, save the gratification of two passions, avarice, the first and predominant appetite of his nature, and hatred, the second. Affecting to consider himself but a type of all humanity, he was at little pains to conceal his true character from the world in general, and in his own heart he exulted over and cherished every bad design as it had birth. The only scriptural admonition that Ralph Nickleby heeded, in the letter, was ‘know thyself.’ He knew himself well, and choosing to imagine that all mankind were cast in the same mould, hated them; for, though no man hates himself, the coldest among us having too much self-love for that, yet most men unconsciously judge the world from themselves, and it will be very generally found that those who sneer habitually at human nature, and affect to despise it, are among its worst and least pleasant samples.\n\nBut the present business of these adventures is with Ralph himself, who stood regarding Newman Noggs with a heavy frown, while that worthy took off his fingerless gloves, and spreading them carefully on the palm of his left hand, and flattening them with his right to take the creases out, proceeded to roll them up with an absent air as if he were utterly regardless of all things else, in the deep interest of the ceremonial.\n\n‘Gone out of town!’ said Ralph, slowly. ‘A mistake of yours. Go back again.’\n\n‘No mistake,’ returned Newman. ‘Not even going; gone.’\n\n‘Has he turned girl or baby?’ muttered Ralph, with a fretful gesture.\n\n‘I don’t know,’ said Newman, ‘but he’s gone.’\n\nThe repetition of the word ‘gone’ seemed to afford Newman Noggs inexpressible delight, in proportion as it annoyed Ralph Nickleby. He uttered the word with a full round emphasis, dwelling upon it as long as he decently could, and when he could hold out no longer without attracting observation, stood gasping it to himself as if even that were a satisfaction.\n\n‘And WHERE has he gone?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘France,’ replied Newman. ‘Danger of another attack of erysipelas--a worse attack--in the head. So the doctors ordered him off. And he’s gone.’\n\n‘And Lord Frederick--?’ began Ralph.\n\n‘He’s gone too,’ replied Newman.\n\n‘And he carries his drubbing with him, does he?’ said Ralph, turning away; ‘pockets his bruises, and sneaks off without the retaliation of a word, or seeking the smallest reparation!’\n\n‘He’s too ill,’ said Newman.\n\n‘Too ill!’ repeated Ralph. ‘Why I would have it if I were dying; in that case I should only be the more determined to have it, and that without delay--I mean if I were he. But he’s too ill! Poor Sir Mulberry! Too ill!’\n\nUttering these words with supreme contempt and great irritation of manner, Ralph signed hastily to Newman to leave the room; and throwing himself into his chair, beat his foot impatiently upon the ground.\n\n‘There is some spell about that boy,’ said Ralph, grinding his teeth. ‘Circumstances conspire to help him. Talk of fortune’s favours! What is even money to such Devil’s luck as this?’\n\nHe thrust his hands impatiently into his pockets, but notwithstanding his previous reflection there was some consolation there, for his face relaxed a little; and although there was still a deep frown upon the contracted brow, it was one of calculation, and not of disappointment.\n\n‘This Hawk will come back, however,’ muttered Ralph; ‘and if I know the man (and I should by this time) his wrath will have lost nothing of its violence in the meanwhile. Obliged to live in retirement--the monotony of a sick-room to a man of his habits--no life--no drink--no play--nothing that he likes and lives by. He is not likely to forget his obligations to the cause of all this. Few men would; but he of all others? No, no!’\n\nHe smiled and shook his head, and resting his chin upon his hand, fell a musing, and smiled again. After a time he rose and rang the bell.\n\n‘That Mr. Squeers; has he been here?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘He was here last night. I left him here when I went home,’ returned Newman.\n\n‘I know that, fool, do I not?’ said Ralph, irascibly. ‘Has he been here since? Was he here this morning?’\n\n‘No,’ bawled Newman, in a very loud key.\n\n‘If he comes while I am out--he is pretty sure to be here by nine tonight--let him wait. And if there’s another man with him, as there will be--perhaps,’ said Ralph, checking himself, ‘let him wait too.’\n\n‘Let ‘em both wait?’ said Newman.\n\n‘Ay,’ replied Ralph, turning upon him with an angry look. ‘Help me on with this spencer, and don’t repeat after me, like a croaking parrot.’\n\n‘I wish I was a parrot,’ Newman, sulkily.\n\n‘I wish you were,’ rejoined Ralph, drawing his spencer on; ‘I’d have wrung your neck long ago.’\n\nNewman returned no answer to this compliment, but looked over Ralph’s shoulder for an instant, (he was adjusting the collar of the spencer behind, just then,) as if he were strongly disposed to tweak him by the nose. Meeting Ralph’s eye, however, he suddenly recalled his wandering fingers, and rubbed his own red nose with a vehemence quite astonishing.\n\nBestowing no further notice upon his eccentric follower than a threatening look, and an admonition to be careful and make no mistake, Ralph took his hat and gloves, and walked out.\n\nHe appeared to have a very extraordinary and miscellaneous connection, and very odd calls he made, some at great rich houses, and some at small poor ones, but all upon one subject: money. His face was a talisman to the porters and servants of his more dashing clients, and procured him ready admission, though he trudged on foot, and others, who were denied, rattled to the door in carriages. Here he was all softness and cringing civility; his step so light, that it scarcely produced a sound upon the thick carpets; his voice so soft that it was not audible beyond the person to whom it was addressed. But in the poorer habitations Ralph was another man; his boots creaked upon the passage floor as he walked boldly in; his voice was harsh and loud as he demanded the money that was overdue; his threats were coarse and angry. With another class of customers, Ralph was again another man. These were attorneys of more than doubtful reputation, who helped him to new business, or raised fresh profits upon old. With them Ralph was familiar and jocose, humorous upon the topics of the day, and especially pleasant upon bankruptcies and pecuniary difficulties that made good for trade. In short, it would have been difficult to have recognised the same man under these various aspects, but for the bulky leather case full of bills and notes which he drew from his pocket at every house, and the constant repetition of the same complaint, (varied only in tone and style of delivery,) that the world thought him rich, and that perhaps he might be if he had his own; but there was no getting money in when it was once out, either principal or interest, and it was a hard matter to live; even to live from day to day.\n\nIt was evening before a long round of such visits (interrupted only by a scanty dinner at an eating-house) terminated at Pimlico, and Ralph walked along St James’s Park, on his way home.\n\nThere were some deep schemes in his head, as the puckered brow and firmly-set mouth would have abundantly testified, even if they had been unaccompanied by a complete indifference to, or unconsciousness of, the objects about him. So complete was his abstraction, however, that Ralph, usually as quick-sighted as any man, did not observe that he was followed by a shambling figure, which at one time stole behind him with noiseless footsteps, at another crept a few paces before him, and at another glided along by his side; at all times regarding him with an eye so keen, and a look so eager and attentive, that it was more like the expression of an intrusive face in some powerful picture or strongly marked dream, than the scrutiny even of a most interested and anxious observer.\n\nThe sky had been lowering and dark for some time, and the commencement of a violent storm of rain drove Ralph for shelter to a tree. He was leaning against it with folded arms, still buried in thought, when, happening to raise his eyes, he suddenly met those of a man who, creeping round the trunk, peered into his face with a searching look. There was something in the usurer’s expression at the moment, which the man appeared to remember well, for it decided him; and stepping close up to Ralph, he pronounced his name.\n\nAstonished for the moment, Ralph fell back a couple of paces and surveyed him from head to foot. A spare, dark, withered man, of about his own age, with a stooping body, and a very sinister face rendered more ill-favoured by hollow and hungry cheeks, deeply sunburnt, and thick black eyebrows, blacker in contrast with the perfect whiteness of his hair; roughly clothed in shabby garments, of a strange and uncouth make; and having about him an indefinable manner of depression and degradation--this, for a moment, was all he saw. But he looked again, and the face and person seemed gradually to grow less strange; to change as he looked, to subside and soften into lineaments that were familiar, until at last they resolved themselves, as if by some strange optical illusion, into those of one whom he had known for many years, and forgotten and lost sight of for nearly as many more.\n\nThe man saw that the recognition was mutual, and beckoning to Ralph to take his former place under the tree, and not to stand in the falling rain, of which, in his first surprise, he had been quite regardless, addressed him in a hoarse, faint tone.\n\n‘You would hardly have known me from my voice, I suppose, Mr. Nickleby?’ he said.\n\n‘No,’ returned Ralph, bending a severe look upon him. ‘Though there is something in that, that I remember now.’\n\n‘There is little in me that you can call to mind as having been there eight years ago, I dare say?’ observed the other.\n\n‘Quite enough,’ said Ralph, carelessly, and averting his face. ‘More than enough.’\n\n‘If I had remained in doubt about YOU, Mr. Nickleby,’ said the other, ‘this reception, and YOUR manner, would have decided me very soon.’\n\n‘Did you expect any other?’ asked Ralph, sharply.\n\n‘No!’ said the man.\n\n‘You were right,’ retorted Ralph; ‘and as you feel no surprise, need express none.’\n\n‘Mr. Nickleby,’ said the man, bluntly, after a brief pause, during which he had seemed to struggle with an inclination to answer him by some reproach, ‘will you hear a few words that I have to say?’\n\n‘I am obliged to wait here till the rain holds a little,’ said Ralph, looking abroad. ‘If you talk, sir, I shall not put my fingers in my ears, though your talking may have as much effect as if I did.’\n\n‘I was once in your confidence--’ thus his companion began. Ralph looked round, and smiled involuntarily.\n\n‘Well,’ said the other, ‘as much in your confidence as you ever chose to let anybody be.’\n\n‘Ah!’ rejoined Ralph, folding his arms; ‘that’s another thing, quite another thing.’\n\n‘Don’t let us play upon words, Mr. Nickleby, in the name of humanity.’\n\n‘Of what?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Of humanity,’ replied the other, sternly. ‘I am hungry and in want. If the change that you must see in me after so long an absence--must see, for I, upon whom it has come by slow and hard degrees, see it and know it well--will not move you to pity, let the knowledge that bread; not the daily bread of the Lord’s Prayer, which, as it is offered up in cities like this, is understood to include half the luxuries of the world for the rich, and just as much coarse food as will support life for the poor--not that, but bread, a crust of dry hard bread, is beyond my reach today--let that have some weight with you, if nothing else has.’\n\n‘If this is the usual form in which you beg, sir,’ said Ralph, ‘you have studied your part well; but if you will take advice from one who knows something of the world and its ways, I should recommend a lower tone; a little lower tone, or you stand a fair chance of being starved in good earnest.’\n\nAs he said this, Ralph clenched his left wrist tightly with his right hand, and inclining his head a little on one side and dropping his chin upon his breast, looked at him whom he addressed with a frowning, sullen face. The very picture of a man whom nothing could move or soften.\n\n‘Yesterday was my first day in London,’ said the old man, glancing at his travel-stained dress and worn shoes.\n\n‘It would have been better for you, I think, if it had been your last also,’ replied Ralph.\n\n‘I have been seeking you these two days, where I thought you were most likely to be found,’ resumed the other more humbly, ‘and I met you here at last, when I had almost given up the hope of encountering you, Mr Nickleby.’\n\nHe seemed to wait for some reply, but Ralph giving him none, he continued:\n\n‘I am a most miserable and wretched outcast, nearly sixty years old, and as destitute and helpless as a child of six.’\n\n‘I am sixty years old, too,’ replied Ralph, ‘and am neither destitute nor helpless. Work. Don’t make fine play-acting speeches about bread, but earn it.’\n\n‘How?’ cried the other. ‘Where? Show me the means. Will you give them to me--will you?’\n\n‘I did once,’ replied Ralph, composedly; ‘you scarcely need ask me whether I will again.’\n\n‘It’s twenty years ago, or more,’ said the man, in a suppressed voice, ‘since you and I fell out. You remember that? I claimed a share in the profits of some business I brought to you, and, as I persisted, you arrested me for an old advance of ten pounds, odd shillings, including interest at fifty per cent, or so.’\n\n‘I remember something of it,’ replied Ralph, carelessly. ‘What then?’\n\n‘That didn’t part us,’ said the man. ‘I made submission, being on the wrong side of the bolts and bars; and as you were not the made man then that you are now, you were glad enough to take back a clerk who wasn’t over nice, and who knew something of the trade you drove.’\n\n‘You begged and prayed, and I consented,’ returned Ralph. ‘That was kind of me. Perhaps I did want you. I forget. I should think I did, or you would have begged in vain. You were useful; not too honest, not too delicate, not too nice of hand or heart; but useful.’\n\n‘Useful, indeed!’ said the man. ‘Come. You had pinched and ground me down for some years before that, but I had served you faithfully up to that time, in spite of all your dog’s usage. Had I?’\n\nRalph made no reply.\n\n‘Had I?’ said the man again.\n\n‘You had had your wages,’ rejoined Ralph, ‘and had done your work. We stood on equal ground so far, and could both cry quits.’\n\n‘Then, but not afterwards,’ said the other.\n\n‘Not afterwards, certainly, nor even then, for (as you have just said) you owed me money, and do still,’ replied Ralph.\n\n‘That’s not all,’ said the man, eagerly. ‘That’s not all. Mark that. I didn’t forget that old sore, trust me. Partly in remembrance of that, and partly in the hope of making money someday by the scheme, I took advantage of my position about you, and possessed myself of a hold upon you, which you would give half of all you have to know, and never can know but through me. I left you--long after that time, remember--and, for some poor trickery that came within the law, but was nothing to what you money-makers daily practise just outside its bounds, was sent away a convict for seven years. I have returned what you see me. Now, Mr Nickleby,’ said the man, with a strange mixture of humility and sense of power, ‘what help and assistance will you give me; what bribe, to speak out plainly? My expectations are not monstrous, but I must live, and to live I must eat and drink. Money is on your side, and hunger and thirst on mine. You may drive an easy bargain.’\n\n‘Is that all?’ said Ralph, still eyeing his companion with the same steady look, and moving nothing but his lips.\n\n‘It depends on you, Mr. Nickleby, whether that’s all or not,’ was the rejoinder.\n\n‘Why then, harkye, Mr--, I don’t know by what name I am to call you,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘By my old one, if you like.’\n\n‘Why then, harkye, Mr. Brooker,’ said Ralph, in his harshest accents, ‘and don’t expect to draw another speech from me. Harkye, sir. I know you of old for a ready scoundrel, but you never had a stout heart; and hard work, with (maybe) chains upon those legs of yours, and shorter food than when I “pinched” and “ground” you, has blunted your wits, or you would not come with such a tale as this to me. You a hold upon me! Keep it, or publish it to the world, if you like.’\n\n‘I can’t do that,’ interposed Brooker. ‘That wouldn’t serve me.’\n\n‘Wouldn’t it?’ said Ralph. ‘It will serve you as much as bringing it to me, I promise you. To be plain with you, I am a careful man, and know my affairs thoroughly. I know the world, and the world knows me. Whatever you gleaned, or heard, or saw, when you served me, the world knows and magnifies already. You could tell it nothing that would surprise it, unless, indeed, it redounded to my credit or honour, and then it would scout you for a liar. And yet I don’t find business slack, or clients scrupulous. Quite the contrary. I am reviled or threatened every day by one man or another,’ said Ralph; ‘but things roll on just the same, and I don’t grow poorer either.’\n\n‘I neither revile nor threaten,’ rejoined the man. ‘I can tell you of what you have lost by my act, what I only can restore, and what, if I die without restoring, dies with me, and never can be regained.’\n\n‘I tell my money pretty accurately, and generally keep it in my own custody,’ said Ralph. ‘I look sharply after most men that I deal with, and most of all I looked sharply after you. You are welcome to all you have kept from me.’\n\n‘Are those of your own name dear to you?’ said the man emphatically. ‘If they are--’\n\n‘They are not,’ returned Ralph, exasperated at this perseverance, and the thought of Nicholas, which the last question awakened. ‘They are not. If you had come as a common beggar, I might have thrown a sixpence to you in remembrance of the clever knave you used to be; but since you try to palm these stale tricks upon one you might have known better, I’ll not part with a halfpenny--nor would I to save you from rotting. And remember this, ‘scape-gallows,’ said Ralph, menacing him with his hand, ‘that if we meet again, and you so much as notice me by one begging gesture, you shall see the inside of a jail once more, and tighten this hold upon me in intervals of the hard labour that vagabonds are put to. There’s my answer to your trash. Take it.’\n\nWith a disdainful scowl at the object of his anger, who met his eye but uttered not a word, Ralph walked away at his usual pace, without manifesting the slightest curiosity to see what became of his late companion, or indeed once looking behind him. The man remained on the same spot with his eyes fixed upon his retreating figure until it was lost to view, and then drawing his arm about his chest, as if the damp and lack of food struck coldly to him, lingered with slouching steps by the wayside, and begged of those who passed along.\n\nRalph, in no-wise moved by what had lately passed, further than as he had already expressed himself, walked deliberately on, and turning out of the Park and leaving Golden Square on his right, took his way through some streets at the west end of the town until he arrived in that particular one in which stood the residence of Madame Mantalini. The name of that lady no longer appeared on the flaming door-plate, that of Miss Knag being substituted in its stead; but the bonnets and dresses were still dimly visible in the first-floor windows by the decaying light of a summer’s evening, and excepting this ostensible alteration in the proprietorship, the establishment wore its old appearance.\n\n‘Humph!’ muttered Ralph, drawing his hand across his mouth with a connoisseur-like air, and surveying the house from top to bottom; ‘these people look pretty well. They can’t last long; but if I know of their going in good time, I am safe, and a fair profit too. I must keep them closely in view; that’s all.’\n\nSo, nodding his head very complacently, Ralph was leaving the spot, when his quick ear caught the sound of a confused noise and hubbub of voices, mingled with a great running up and down stairs, in the very house which had been the subject of his scrutiny; and while he was hesitating whether to knock at the door or listen at the keyhole a little longer, a female servant of Madame Mantalini’s (whom he had often seen) opened it abruptly and bounced out, with her blue cap-ribbons streaming in the air.\n\n‘Hallo here. Stop!’ cried Ralph. ‘What’s the matter? Here am I. Didn’t you hear me knock?’\n\n‘Oh! Mr. Nickleby, sir,’ said the girl. ‘Go up, for the love of Gracious. Master’s been and done it again.’\n\n‘Done what?’ said Ralph, tartly; ‘what d’ye mean?’\n\n‘I knew he would if he was drove to it,’ cried the girl. ‘I said so all along.’\n\n‘Come here, you silly wench,’ said Ralph, catching her by the wrist; ‘and don’t carry family matters to the neighbours, destroying the credit of the establishment. Come here; do you hear me, girl?’\n\nWithout any further expostulation, he led or rather pulled the frightened handmaid into the house, and shut the door; then bidding her walk upstairs before him, followed without more ceremony.\n\nGuided by the noise of a great many voices all talking together, and passing the girl in his impatience, before they had ascended many steps, Ralph quickly reached the private sitting-room, when he was rather amazed by the confused and inexplicable scene in which he suddenly found himself.\n\nThere were all the young-lady workers, some with bonnets and some without, in various attitudes expressive of alarm and consternation; some gathered round Madame Mantalini, who was in tears upon one chair; and others round Miss Knag, who was in opposition tears upon another; and others round Mr. Mantalini, who was perhaps the most striking figure in the whole group, for Mr. Mantalini’s legs were extended at full length upon the floor, and his head and shoulders were supported by a very tall footman, who didn’t seem to know what to do with them, and Mr Mantalini’s eyes were closed, and his face was pale and his hair was comparatively straight, and his whiskers and moustache were limp, and his teeth were clenched, and he had a little bottle in his right hand, and a little tea-spoon in his left; and his hands, arms, legs, and shoulders, were all stiff and powerless. And yet Madame Mantalini was not weeping upon the body, but was scolding violently upon her chair; and all this amidst a clamour of tongues perfectly deafening, and which really appeared to have driven the unfortunate footman to the utmost verge of distraction.\n\n‘What is the matter here?’ said Ralph, pressing forward.\n\nAt this inquiry, the clamour was increased twenty-fold, and an astounding string of such shrill contradictions as ‘He’s poisoned himself’--‘He hasn’t’--‘Send for a doctor’--‘Don’t’--‘He’s dying’--‘He isn’t, he’s only pretending’--with various other cries, poured forth with bewildering volubility, until Madame Mantalini was seen to address herself to Ralph, when female curiosity to know what she would say, prevailed, and, as if by general consent, a dead silence, unbroken by a single whisper, instantaneously succeeded.\n\n‘Mr. Nickleby,’ said Madame Mantalini; ‘by what chance you came here, I don’t know.’\n\nHere a gurgling voice was heard to ejaculate, as part of the wanderings of a sick man, the words ‘Demnition sweetness!’ but nobody heeded them except the footman, who, being startled to hear such awful tones proceeding, as it were, from between his very fingers, dropped his master’s head upon the floor with a pretty loud crash, and then, without an effort to lift it up, gazed upon the bystanders, as if he had done something rather clever than otherwise.\n\n‘I will, however,’ continued Madame Mantalini, drying her eyes, and speaking with great indignation, ‘say before you, and before everybody here, for the first time, and once for all, that I never will supply that man’s extravagances and viciousness again. I have been a dupe and a fool to him long enough. In future, he shall support himself if he can, and then he may spend what money he pleases, upon whom and how he pleases; but it shall not be mine, and therefore you had better pause before you trust him further.’\n\nThereupon Madame Mantalini, quite unmoved by some most pathetic lamentations on the part of her husband, that the apothecary had not mixed the prussic acid strong enough, and that he must take another bottle or two to finish the work he had in hand, entered into a catalogue of that amiable gentleman’s gallantries, deceptions, extravagances, and infidelities (especially the last), winding up with a protest against being supposed to entertain the smallest remnant of regard for him; and adducing, in proof of the altered state of her affections, the circumstance of his having poisoned himself in private no less than six times within the last fortnight, and her not having once interfered by word or deed to save his life.\n\n‘And I insist on being separated and left to myself,’ said Madame Mantalini, sobbing. ‘If he dares to refuse me a separation, I’ll have one in law--I can--and I hope this will be a warning to all girls who have seen this disgraceful exhibition.’\n\nMiss Knag, who was unquestionably the oldest girl in company, said with great solemnity, that it would be a warning to HER, and so did the young ladies generally, with the exception of one or two who appeared to entertain some doubts whether such whispers could do wrong.\n\n‘Why do you say all this before so many listeners?’ said Ralph, in a low voice. ‘You know you are not in earnest.’\n\n‘I AM in earnest,’ replied Madame Mantalini, aloud, and retreating towards Miss Knag.\n\n‘Well, but consider,’ reasoned Ralph, who had a great interest in the matter. ‘It would be well to reflect. A married woman has no property.’\n\n‘Not a solitary single individual dem, my soul,’ and Mr. Mantalini, raising himself upon his elbow.\n\n‘I am quite aware of that,’ retorted Madame Mantalini, tossing her head; ‘and I have none. The business, the stock, this house, and everything in it, all belong to Miss Knag.’\n\n‘That’s quite true, Madame Mantalini,’ said Miss Knag, with whom her late employer had secretly come to an amicable understanding on this point. ‘Very true, indeed, Madame Mantalini--hem--very true. And I never was more glad in all my life, that I had strength of mind to resist matrimonial offers, no matter how advantageous, than I am when I think of my present position as compared with your most unfortunate and most undeserved one, Madame Mantalini.’\n\n‘Demmit!’ cried Mr. Mantalini, turning his head towards his wife. ‘Will it not slap and pinch the envious dowager, that dares to reflect upon its own delicious?’\n\nBut the day of Mr. Mantalini’s blandishments had departed. ‘Miss Knag, sir,’ said his wife, ‘is my particular friend;’ and although Mr Mantalini leered till his eyes seemed in danger of never coming back to their right places again, Madame Mantalini showed no signs of softening.\n\nTo do the excellent Miss Knag justice, she had been mainly instrumental in bringing about this altered state of things, for, finding by daily experience, that there was no chance of the business thriving, or even continuing to exist, while Mr. Mantalini had any hand in the expenditure, and having now a considerable interest in its well-doing, she had sedulously applied herself to the investigation of some little matters connected with that gentleman’s private character, which she had so well elucidated, and artfully imparted to Madame Mantalini, as to open her eyes more effectually than the closest and most philosophical reasoning could have done in a series of years. To which end, the accidental discovery by Miss Knag of some tender correspondence, in which Madame Mantalini was described as ‘old’ and ‘ordinary,’ had most providentially contributed.\n\nHowever, notwithstanding her firmness, Madame Mantalini wept very piteously; and as she leant upon Miss Knag, and signed towards the door, that young lady and all the other young ladies with sympathising faces, proceeded to bear her out.\n\n‘Nickleby,’ said Mr. Mantalini in tears, ‘you have been made a witness to this demnition cruelty, on the part of the demdest enslaver and captivator that never was, oh dem! I forgive that woman.’\n\n‘Forgive!’ repeated Madame Mantalini, angrily.\n\n‘I do forgive her, Nickleby,’ said Mr. Mantalini. ‘You will blame me, the world will blame me, the women will blame me; everybody will laugh, and scoff, and smile, and grin most demnebly. They will say, “She had a blessing. She did not know it. He was too weak; he was too good; he was a dem’d fine fellow, but he loved too strong; he could not bear her to be cross, and call him wicked names. It was a dem’d case, there never was a demder.” But I forgive her.’\n\nWith this affecting speech Mr. Mantalini fell down again very flat, and lay to all appearance without sense or motion, until all the females had left the room, when he came cautiously into a sitting posture, and confronted Ralph with a very blank face, and the little bottle still in one hand and the tea-spoon in the other.\n\n‘You may put away those fooleries now, and live by your wits again,’ said Ralph, coolly putting on his hat.\n\n‘Demmit, Nickleby, you’re not serious?’\n\n‘I seldom joke,’ said Ralph. ‘Good-night.’\n\n‘No, but Nickleby--’ said Mantalini.\n\n‘I am wrong, perhaps,’ rejoined Ralph. ‘I hope so. You should know best. Good-night.’\n\nAffecting not to hear his entreaties that he would stay and advise with him, Ralph left the crest-fallen Mr. Mantalini to his meditations, and left the house quietly.\n\n‘Oho!’ he said, ‘sets the wind that way so soon? Half knave and half fool, and detected in both characters? I think your day is over, sir.’\n\nAs he said this, he made some memorandum in his pocket-book in which Mr Mantalini’s name figured conspicuously, and finding by his watch that it was between nine and ten o’clock, made all speed home.\n\n‘Are they here?’ was the first question he asked of Newman.\n\nNewman nodded. ‘Been here half an hour.’\n\n‘Two of them? One a fat sleek man?’\n\n‘Ay,’ said Newman. ‘In your room now.’\n\n‘Good,’ rejoined Ralph. ‘Get me a coach.’\n\n‘A coach! What, you--going to--eh?’ stammered Newman.\n\nRalph angrily repeated his orders, and Noggs, who might well have been excused for wondering at such an unusual and extraordinary circumstance (for he had never seen Ralph in a coach in his life) departed on his errand, and presently returned with the conveyance.\n\nInto it went Mr. Squeers, and Ralph, and the third man, whom Newman Noggs had never seen. Newman stood upon the door-step to see them off, not troubling himself to wonder where or upon what business they were going, until he chanced by mere accident to hear Ralph name the address whither the coachman was to drive.\n\nQuick as lightning and in a state of the most extreme wonder, Newman darted into his little office for his hat, and limped after the coach as if with the intention of getting up behind; but in this design he was balked, for it had too much the start of him and was soon hopelessly ahead, leaving him gaping in the empty street.\n\n‘I don’t know though,’ said Noggs, stopping for breath, ‘any good that I could have done by going too. He would have seen me if I had. Drive THERE! What can come of this? If I had only known it yesterday I could have told--drive there! There’s mischief in it. There must be.’\n\nHis reflections were interrupted by a grey-haired man of a very remarkable, though far from prepossessing appearance, who, coming stealthily towards him, solicited relief.\n\nNewman, still cogitating deeply, turned away; but the man followed him, and pressed him with such a tale of misery that Newman (who might have been considered a hopeless person to beg from, and who had little enough to give) looked into his hat for some halfpence which he usually kept screwed up, when he had any, in a corner of his pocket-handkerchief.\n\nWhile he was busily untwisting the knot with his teeth, the man said something which attracted his attention; whatever that something was, it led to something else, and in the end he and Newman walked away side by side--the strange man talking earnestly, and Newman listening.\n\nContaining Matter of a surprising Kind\n‘As we gang awa’ fra’ Lunnun tomorrow neeght, and as I dinnot know that I was e’er so happy in a’ my days, Misther Nickleby, Ding! but I WILL tak’ anoother glass to our next merry meeting!’\n\nSo said John Browdie, rubbing his hands with great joyousness, and looking round him with a ruddy shining face, quite in keeping with the declaration.\n\nThe time at which John found himself in this enviable condition was the same evening to which the last chapter bore reference; the place was the cottage; and the assembled company were Nicholas, Mrs. Nickleby, Mrs Browdie, Kate Nickleby, and Smike.\n\nA very merry party they had been. Mrs. Nickleby, knowing of her son’s obligations to the honest Yorkshireman, had, after some demur, yielded her consent to Mr. and Mrs. Browdie being invited out to tea; in the way of which arrangement, there were at first sundry difficulties and obstacles, arising out of her not having had an opportunity of ‘calling’ upon Mrs. Browdie first; for although Mrs. Nickleby very often observed with much complacency (as most punctilious people do), that she had not an atom of pride or formality about her, still she was a great stickler for dignity and ceremonies; and as it was manifest that, until a call had been made, she could not be (politely speaking, and according to the laws of society) even cognisant of the fact of Mrs. Browdie’s existence, she felt her situation to be one of peculiar delicacy and difficulty.\n\n‘The call MUST originate with me, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘that’s indispensable. The fact is, my dear, that it’s necessary there should be a sort of condescension on my part, and that I should show this young person that I am willing to take notice of her. There’s a very respectable-looking young man,’ added Mrs. Nickleby, after a short consideration, ‘who is conductor to one of the omnibuses that go by here, and who wears a glazed hat--your sister and I have noticed him very often--he has a wart upon his nose, Kate, you know, exactly like a gentleman’s servant.’\n\n‘Have all gentlemen’s servants warts upon their noses, mother?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘Nicholas, my dear, how very absurd you are,’ returned his mother; ‘of course I mean that his glazed hat looks like a gentleman’s servant, and not the wart upon his nose; though even that is not so ridiculous as it may seem to you, for we had a footboy once, who had not only a wart, but a wen also, and a very large wen too, and he demanded to have his wages raised in consequence, because he found it came very expensive. Let me see, what was I--oh yes, I know. The best way that I can think of would be to send a card, and my compliments, (I’ve no doubt he’d take ‘em for a pot of porter,) by this young man, to the Saracen with Two Necks. If the waiter took him for a gentleman’s servant, so much the better. Then all Mrs. Browdie would have to do would be to send her card back by the carrier (he could easily come with a double knock), and there’s an end of it.’\n\n‘My dear mother,’ said Nicholas, ‘I don’t suppose such unsophisticated people as these ever had a card of their own, or ever will have.’\n\n‘Oh that, indeed, Nicholas, my dear,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby, ‘that’s another thing. If you put it upon that ground, why, of course, I have no more to say, than that I have no doubt they are very good sort of persons, and that I have no kind of objection to their coming here to tea if they like, and shall make a point of being very civil to them if they do.’\n\nThe point being thus effectually set at rest, and Mrs. Nickleby duly placed in the patronising and mildly-condescending position which became her rank and matrimonial years, Mr. and Mrs. Browdie were invited and came; and as they were very deferential to Mrs. Nickleby, and seemed to have a becoming appreciation of her greatness, and were very much pleased with everything, the good lady had more than once given Kate to understand, in a whisper, that she thought they were the very best-meaning people she had ever seen, and perfectly well behaved.\n\nAnd thus it came to pass, that John Browdie declared, in the parlour after supper, to wit, and twenty minutes before eleven o’clock p.m., that he had never been so happy in all his days.\n\nNor was Mrs. Browdie much behind her husband in this respect, for that young matron, whose rustic beauty contrasted very prettily with the more delicate loveliness of Kate, and without suffering by the contrast either, for each served as it were to set off and decorate the other, could not sufficiently admire the gentle and winning manners of the young lady, or the engaging affability of the elder one. Then Kate had the art of turning the conversation to subjects upon which the country girl, bashful at first in strange company, could feel herself at home; and if Mrs. Nickleby was not quite so felicitous at times in the selection of topics of discourse, or if she did seem, as Mrs. Browdie expressed it, ‘rather high in her notions,’ still nothing could be kinder, and that she took considerable interest in the young couple was manifest from the very long lectures on housewifery with which she was so obliging as to entertain Mrs. Browdie’s private ear, which were illustrated by various references to the domestic economy of the cottage, in which (those duties falling exclusively upon Kate) the good lady had about as much share, either in theory or practice, as any one of the statues of the Twelve Apostles which embellish the exterior of St Paul’s Cathedral.\n\n‘Mr. Browdie,’ said Kate, addressing his young wife, ‘is the best-humoured, the kindest and heartiest creature I ever saw. If I were oppressed with I don’t know how many cares, it would make me happy only to look at him.’\n\n‘He does seem indeed, upon my word, a most excellent creature, Kate,’ said Mrs. Nickleby; ‘most excellent. And I am sure that at all times it will give me pleasure--really pleasure now--to have you, Mrs. Browdie, to see me in this plain and homely manner. We make no display,’ said Mrs Nickleby, with an air which seemed to insinuate that they could make a vast deal if they were so disposed; ‘no fuss, no preparation; I wouldn’t allow it. I said, “Kate, my dear, you will only make Mrs. Browdie feel uncomfortable, and how very foolish and inconsiderate that would be!”’\n\n‘I am very much obliged to you, I am sure, ma’am,’ returned Mrs. Browdie, gratefully. ‘It’s nearly eleven o’clock, John. I am afraid we are keeping you up very late, ma’am.’\n\n‘Late!’ cried Mrs. Nickleby, with a sharp thin laugh, and one little cough at the end, like a note of admiration expressed. ‘This is quite early for us. We used to keep such hours! Twelve, one, two, three o’clock was nothing to us. Balls, dinners, card-parties! Never were such rakes as the people about where we used to live. I often think now, I am sure, that how we ever could go through with it is quite astonishing, and that is just the evil of having a large connection and being a great deal sought after, which I would recommend all young married people steadily to resist; though of course, and it’s perfectly clear, and a very happy thing too, I think, that very few young married people can be exposed to such temptations. There was one family in particular, that used to live about a mile from us--not straight down the road, but turning sharp off to the left by the turnpike where the Plymouth mail ran over the donkey--that were quite extraordinary people for giving the most extravagant parties, with artificial flowers and champagne, and variegated lamps, and, in short, every delicacy of eating and drinking that the most singular epicure could possibly require. I don’t think that there ever were such people as those Peltiroguses. You remember the Peltiroguses, Kate?’\n\nKate saw that for the ease and comfort of the visitors it was high time to stay this flood of recollection, so answered that she entertained of the Peltiroguses a most vivid and distinct remembrance; and then said that Mr. Browdie had half promised, early in the evening, that he would sing a Yorkshire song, and that she was most impatient that he should redeem his promise, because she was sure it would afford her mama more amusement and pleasure than it was possible to express.\n\nMrs. Nickleby confirming her daughter with the best possible grace--for there was patronage in that too, and a kind of implication that she had a discerning taste in such matters, and was something of a critic--John Browdie proceeded to consider the words of some north-country ditty, and to take his wife’s recollection respecting the same. This done, he made divers ungainly movements in his chair, and singling out one particular fly on the ceiling from the other flies there asleep, fixed his eyes upon him, and began to roar a meek sentiment (supposed to be uttered by a gentle swain fast pining away with love and despair) in a voice of thunder.\n\nAt the end of the first verse, as though some person without had waited until then to make himself audible, was heard a loud and violent knocking at the street-door; so loud and so violent, indeed, that the ladies started as by one accord, and John Browdie stopped.\n\n‘It must be some mistake,’ said Nicholas, carelessly. ‘We know nobody who would come here at this hour.’\n\nMrs. Nickleby surmised, however, that perhaps the counting-house was burnt down, or perhaps ‘the Mr. Cheerybles’ had sent to take Nicholas into partnership (which certainly appeared highly probable at that time of night), or perhaps Mr. Linkinwater had run away with the property, or perhaps Miss La Creevy was taken in, or perhaps--\n\nBut a hasty exclamation from Kate stopped her abruptly in her conjectures, and Ralph Nickleby walked into the room.\n\n‘Stay,’ said Ralph, as Nicholas rose, and Kate, making her way towards him, threw herself upon his arm. ‘Before that boy says a word, hear me.’\n\nNicholas bit his lip and shook his head in a threatening manner, but appeared for the moment unable to articulate a syllable. Kate clung closer to his arm, Smike retreated behind them, and John Browdie, who had heard of Ralph, and appeared to have no great difficulty in recognising him, stepped between the old man and his young friend, as if with the intention of preventing either of them from advancing a step further.\n\n‘Hear me, I say,’ said Ralph, ‘and not him.’\n\n‘Say what thou’st gotten to say then, sir,’ retorted John; ‘and tak’ care thou dinnot put up angry bluid which thou’dst betther try to quiet.’\n\n‘I should know YOU,’ said Ralph, ‘by your tongue; and HIM’ (pointing to Smike) ‘by his looks.’\n\n‘Don’t speak to him,’ said Nicholas, recovering his voice. ‘I will not have it. I will not hear him. I do not know that man. I cannot breathe the air that he corrupts. His presence is an insult to my sister. It is shame to see him. I will not bear it.’\n\n‘Stand!’ cried John, laying his heavy hand upon his chest.\n\n‘Then let him instantly retire,’ said Nicholas, struggling. ‘I am not going to lay hands upon him, but he shall withdraw. I will not have him here. John, John Browdie, is this my house, am I a child? If he stands there,’ cried Nicholas, burning with fury, ‘looking so calmly upon those who know his black and dastardly heart, he’ll drive me mad.’\n\nTo all these exclamations John Browdie answered not a word, but he retained his hold upon Nicholas; and when he was silent again, spoke.\n\n‘There’s more to say and hear than thou think’st for,’ said John. ‘I tell’ee I ha’ gotten scent o’ thot already. Wa’at be that shadow ootside door there? Noo, schoolmeasther, show thyself, mun; dinnot be sheame-feaced. Noo, auld gen’l’man, let’s have schoolmeasther, coom.’\n\nHearing this adjuration, Mr. Squeers, who had been lingering in the passage until such time as it should be expedient for him to enter and he could appear with effect, was fain to present himself in a somewhat undignified and sneaking way; at which John Browdie laughed with such keen and heartfelt delight, that even Kate, in all the pain, anxiety, and surprise of the scene, and though the tears were in her eyes, felt a disposition to join him.\n\n‘Have you done enjoying yourself, sir?’ said Ralph, at length.\n\n‘Pratty nigh for the prasant time, sir,’ replied John.\n\n‘I can wait,’ said Ralph. ‘Take your own time, pray.’\n\nRalph waited until there was a perfect silence, and then turning to Mrs Nickleby, but directing an eager glance at Kate, as if more anxious to watch his effect upon her, said:\n\n‘Now, ma’am, listen to me. I don’t imagine that you were a party to a very fine tirade of words sent me by that boy of yours, because I don’t believe that under his control, you have the slightest will of your own, or that your advice, your opinion, your wants, your wishes, anything which in nature and reason (or of what use is your great experience?) ought to weigh with him, has the slightest influence or weight whatever, or is taken for a moment into account.’\n\nMrs. Nickleby shook her head and sighed, as if there were a good deal in that, certainly.\n\n‘For this reason,’ resumed Ralph, ‘I address myself to you, ma’am. For this reason, partly, and partly because I do not wish to be disgraced by the acts of a vicious stripling whom I was obliged to disown, and who, afterwards, in his boyish majesty, feigns to--ha! ha!--to disown ME, I present myself here tonight. I have another motive in coming: a motive of humanity. I come here,’ said Ralph, looking round with a biting and triumphant smile, and gloating and dwelling upon the words as if he were loath to lose the pleasure of saying them, ‘to restore a parent his child. Ay, sir,’ he continued, bending eagerly forward, and addressing Nicholas, as he marked the change of his countenance, ‘to restore a parent his child; his son, sir; trepanned, waylaid, and guarded at every turn by you, with the base design of robbing him some day of any little wretched pittance of which he might become possessed.’\n\n‘In that, you know you lie,’ said Nicholas, proudly.\n\n‘In this, I know I speak the truth. I have his father here,’ retorted Ralph.\n\n‘Here!’ sneered Squeers, stepping forward. ‘Do you hear that? Here! Didn’t I tell you to be careful that his father didn’t turn up and send him back to me? Why, his father’s my friend; he’s to come back to me directly, he is. Now, what do you say--eh!--now--come--what do you say to that--an’t you sorry you took so much trouble for nothing? an’t you? an’t you?’\n\n‘You bear upon your body certain marks I gave you,’ said Nicholas, looking quietly away, ‘and may talk in acknowledgment of them as much as you please. You’ll talk a long time before you rub them out, Mr Squeers.’\n\nThe estimable gentleman last named cast a hasty look at the table, as if he were prompted by this retort to throw a jug or bottle at the head of Nicholas, but he was interrupted in this design (if such design he had) by Ralph, who, touching him on the elbow, bade him tell the father that he might now appear and claim his son.\n\nThis being purely a labour of love, Mr. Squeers readily complied, and leaving the room for the purpose, almost immediately returned, supporting a sleek personage with an oily face, who, bursting from him, and giving to view the form and face of Mr. Snawley, made straight up to Smike, and tucking that poor fellow’s head under his arm in a most uncouth and awkward embrace, elevated his broad-brimmed hat at arm’s length in the air as a token of devout thanksgiving, exclaiming, meanwhile, ‘How little did I think of this here joyful meeting, when I saw him last! Oh, how little did I think it!’\n\n‘Be composed, sir,’ said Ralph, with a gruff expression of sympathy, ‘you have got him now.’\n\n‘Got him! Oh, haven’t I got him! Have I got him, though?’ cried Mr Snawley, scarcely able to believe it. ‘Yes, here he is, flesh and blood, flesh and blood.’\n\n‘Vary little flesh,’ said John Browdie.\n\nMr. Snawley was too much occupied by his parental feelings to notice this remark; and, to assure himself more completely of the restoration of his child, tucked his head under his arm again, and kept it there.\n\n‘What was it,’ said Snawley, ‘that made me take such a strong interest in him, when that worthy instructor of youth brought him to my house? What was it that made me burn all over with a wish to chastise him severely for cutting away from his best friends, his pastors and masters?’\n\n‘It was parental instinct, sir,’ observed Squeers.\n\n‘That’s what it was, sir,’ rejoined Snawley; ‘the elevated feeling, the feeling of the ancient Romans and Grecians, and of the beasts of the field and birds of the air, with the exception of rabbits and tom-cats, which sometimes devour their offspring. My heart yearned towards him. I could have--I don’t know what I couldn’t have done to him in the anger of a father.’\n\n‘It only shows what Natur is, sir,’ said Mr. Squeers. ‘She’s rum ‘un, is Natur.’\n\n‘She is a holy thing, sir,’ remarked Snawley.\n\n‘I believe you,’ added Mr. Squeers, with a moral sigh. ‘I should like to know how we should ever get on without her. Natur,’ said Mr. Squeers, solemnly, ‘is more easier conceived than described. Oh what a blessed thing, sir, to be in a state of natur!’\n\nPending this philosophical discourse, the bystanders had been quite stupefied with amazement, while Nicholas had looked keenly from Snawley to Squeers, and from Squeers to Ralph, divided between his feelings of disgust, doubt, and surprise. At this juncture, Smike escaping from his father fled to Nicholas, and implored him, in most moving terms, never to give him up, but to let him live and die beside him.\n\n‘If you are this boy’s father,’ said Nicholas, ‘look at the wreck he is, and tell me that you purpose to send him back to that loathsome den from which I brought him.’\n\n‘Scandal again!’ cried Squeers. ‘Recollect, you an’t worth powder and shot, but I’ll be even with you one way or another.’\n\n‘Stop,’ interposed Ralph, as Snawley was about to speak. ‘Let us cut this matter short, and not bandy words here with hare-brained profligates. This is your son, as you can prove. And you, Mr. Squeers, you know this boy to be the same that was with you for so many years under the name of Smike. Do you?’\n\n‘Do I!’ returned Squeers. ‘Don’t I?’\n\n‘Good,’ said Ralph; ‘a very few words will be sufficient here. You had a son by your first wife, Mr. Snawley?’\n\n‘I had,’ replied that person, ‘and there he stands.’\n\n‘We’ll show that presently,’ said Ralph. ‘You and your wife were separated, and she had the boy to live with her, when he was a year old. You received a communication from her, when you had lived apart a year or two, that the boy was dead; and you believed it?’\n\n‘Of course I did!’ returned Snawley. ‘Oh the joy of--’\n\n‘Be rational, sir, pray,’ said Ralph. ‘This is business, and transports interfere with it. This wife died a year and a half ago, or thereabouts--not more--in some obscure place, where she was housekeeper in a family. Is that the case?’\n\n‘That’s the case,’ replied Snawley.\n\n‘Having written on her death-bed a letter or confession to you, about this very boy, which, as it was not directed otherwise than in your name, only reached you, and that by a circuitous course, a few days since?’\n\n‘Just so,’ said Snawley. ‘Correct in every particular, sir.’\n\n‘And this confession,’ resumed Ralph, ‘is to the effect that his death was an invention of hers to wound you--was a part of a system of annoyance, in short, which you seem to have adopted towards each other--that the boy lived, but was of weak and imperfect intellect--that she sent him by a trusty hand to a cheap school in Yorkshire--that she had paid for his education for some years, and then, being poor, and going a long way off, gradually deserted him, for which she prayed forgiveness?’\n\nSnawley nodded his head, and wiped his eyes; the first slightly, the last violently.\n\n‘The school was Mr. Squeers’s,’ continued Ralph; ‘the boy was left there in the name of Smike; every description was fully given, dates tally exactly with Mr. Squeers’s books, Mr. Squeers is lodging with you at this time; you have two other boys at his school: you communicated the whole discovery to him, he brought you to me as the person who had recommended to him the kidnapper of his child; and I brought you here. Is that so?’\n\n‘You talk like a good book, sir, that’s got nothing in its inside but what’s the truth,’ replied Snawley.\n\n‘This is your pocket-book,’ said Ralph, producing one from his coat; ‘the certificates of your first marriage and of the boy’s birth, and your wife’s two letters, and every other paper that can support these statements directly or by implication, are here, are they?’\n\n‘Every one of ‘em, sir.’\n\n‘And you don’t object to their being looked at here, so that these people may be convinced of your power to substantiate your claim at once in law and reason, and you may resume your control over your own son without more delay. Do I understand you?’\n\n‘I couldn’t have understood myself better, sir.’\n\n‘There, then,’ said Ralph, tossing the pocket-book upon the table. ‘Let them see them if they like; and as those are the original papers, I should recommend you to stand near while they are being examined, or you may chance to lose some.’\n\nWith these words Ralph sat down unbidden, and compressing his lips, which were for the moment slightly parted by a smile, folded his arms, and looked for the first time at his nephew.\n\nNicholas, stung by the concluding taunt, darted an indignant glance at him; but commanding himself as well as he could, entered upon a close examination of the documents, at which John Browdie assisted. There was nothing about them which could be called in question. The certificates were regularly signed as extracts from the parish books, the first letter had a genuine appearance of having been written and preserved for some years, the handwriting of the second tallied with it exactly, (making proper allowance for its having been written by a person in extremity,) and there were several other corroboratory scraps of entries and memoranda which it was equally difficult to question.\n\n‘Dear Nicholas,’ whispered Kate, who had been looking anxiously over his shoulder, ‘can this be really the case? Is this statement true?’\n\n‘I fear it is,’ answered Nicholas. ‘What say you, John?’\n\nJohn scratched his head and shook it, but said nothing at all.\n\n‘You will observe, ma’am,’ said Ralph, addressing himself to Mrs Nickleby, ‘that this boy being a minor and not of strong mind, we might have come here tonight, armed with the powers of the law, and backed by a troop of its myrmidons. I should have done so, ma’am, unquestionably, but for my regard for the feelings of yourself, and your daughter.’\n\n‘You have shown your regard for HER feelings well,’ said Nicholas, drawing his sister towards him.\n\n‘Thank you,’ replied Ralph. ‘Your praise, sir, is commendation, indeed.’\n\n‘Well,’ said Squeers, ‘what’s to be done? Them hackney-coach horses will catch cold if we don’t think of moving; there’s one of ‘em a sneezing now, so that he blows the street door right open. What’s the order of the day? Is Master Snawley to come along with us?’\n\n‘No, no, no,’ replied Smike, drawing back, and clinging to Nicholas.\n‘No. Pray, no. I will not go from you with him. No, no.’\n\n‘This is a cruel thing,’ said Snawley, looking to his friends for support. ‘Do parents bring children into the world for this?’\n\n‘Do parents bring children into the world for THOT?’ said John Browdie bluntly, pointing, as he spoke, to Squeers.\n\n‘Never you mind,’ retorted that gentleman, tapping his nose derisively.\n\n‘Never I mind!’ said John, ‘no, nor never nobody mind, say’st thou, schoolmeasther. It’s nobody’s minding that keeps sike men as thou afloat. Noo then, where be’est thou coomin’ to? Dang it, dinnot coom treadin’ ower me, mun.’\n\nSuiting the action to the word, John Browdie just jerked his elbow into the chest of Mr. Squeers who was advancing upon Smike; with so much dexterity that the schoolmaster reeled and staggered back upon Ralph Nickleby, and being unable to recover his balance, knocked that gentleman off his chair, and stumbled heavily upon him.\n\nThis accidental circumstance was the signal for some very decisive proceedings. In the midst of a great noise, occasioned by the prayers and entreaties of Smike, the cries and exclamations of the women, and the vehemence of the men, demonstrations were made of carrying off the lost son by violence. Squeers had actually begun to haul him out, when Nicholas (who, until then, had been evidently undecided how to act) took him by the collar, and shaking him so that such teeth as he had, chattered in his head, politely escorted him to the room-door, and thrusting him into the passage, shut it upon him.\n\n‘Now,’ said Nicholas to the other two, ‘have the goodness to follow your friend.’\n\n‘I want my son,’ said Snawley.\n\n‘Your son,’ replied Nicholas, ‘chooses for himself. He chooses to remain here, and he shall.’\n\n‘You won’t give him up?’ said Snawley.\n\n‘I would not give him up against his will, to be the victim of such brutality as that to which you would consign him,’ replied Nicholas, ‘if he were a dog or a rat.’\n\n‘Knock that Nickleby down with a candlestick,’ cried Mr. Squeers, through the keyhole, ‘and bring out my hat, somebody, will you, unless he wants to steal it.’\n\n‘I am very sorry, indeed,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, who, with Mrs. Browdie, had stood crying and biting her fingers in a corner, while Kate (very pale, but perfectly quiet) had kept as near her brother as she could. ‘I am very sorry, indeed, for all this. I really don’t know what would be best to do, and that’s the truth. Nicholas ought to be the best judge, and I hope he is. Of course, it’s a hard thing to have to keep other people’s children, though young Mr. Snawley is certainly as useful and willing as it’s possible for anybody to be; but, if it could be settled in any friendly manner--if old Mr. Snawley, for instance, would settle to pay something certain for his board and lodging, and some fair arrangement was come to, so that we undertook to have fish twice a week, and a pudding twice, or a dumpling, or something of that sort--I do think that it might be very satisfactory and pleasant for all parties.’\n\nThis compromise, which was proposed with abundance of tears and sighs, not exactly meeting the point at issue, nobody took any notice of it; and poor Mrs. Nickleby accordingly proceeded to enlighten Mrs. Browdie upon the advantages of such a scheme, and the unhappy results flowing, on all occasions, from her not being attended to when she proffered her advice.\n\n‘You, sir,’ said Snawley, addressing the terrified Smike, ‘are an unnatural, ungrateful, unlovable boy. You won’t let me love you when I want to. Won’t you come home, won’t you?’\n\n‘No, no, no,’ cried Smike, shrinking back.\n\n‘He never loved nobody,’ bawled Squeers, through the keyhole. ‘He never loved me; he never loved Wackford, who is next door but one to a cherubim. How can you expect that he’ll love his father? He’ll never love his father, he won’t. He don’t know what it is to have a father. He don’t understand it. It an’t in him.’\n\nMr. Snawley looked steadfastly at his son for a full minute, and then covering his eyes with his hand, and once more raising his hat in the air, appeared deeply occupied in deploring his black ingratitude. Then drawing his arm across his eyes, he picked up Mr. Squeers’s hat, and taking it under one arm, and his own under the other, walked slowly and sadly out.\n\n‘Your romance, sir,’ said Ralph, lingering for a moment, ‘is destroyed, I take it. No unknown; no persecuted descendant of a man of high degree; but the weak, imbecile son of a poor, petty tradesman. We shall see how your sympathy melts before plain matter of fact.’\n\n‘You shall,’ said Nicholas, motioning towards the door.\n\n‘And trust me, sir,’ added Ralph, ‘that I never supposed you would give him up tonight. Pride, obstinacy, reputation for fine feeling, were all against it. These must be brought down, sir, lowered, crushed, as they shall be soon. The protracted and wearing anxiety and expense of the law in its most oppressive form, its torture from hour to hour, its weary days and sleepless nights, with these I’ll prove you, and break your haughty spirit, strong as you deem it now. And when you make this house a hell, and visit these trials upon yonder wretched object (as you will; I know you), and those who think you now a young-fledged hero, we’ll go into old accounts between us two, and see who stands the debtor, and comes out best at last, even before the world.’\n\nRalph Nickleby withdrew. But Mr. Squeers, who had heard a portion of this closing address, and was by this time wound up to a pitch of impotent malignity almost unprecedented, could not refrain from returning to the parlour door, and actually cutting some dozen capers with various wry faces and hideous grimaces, expressive of his triumphant confidence in the downfall and defeat of Nicholas.\n\nHaving concluded this war-dance, in which his short trousers and large boots had borne a very conspicuous figure, Mr. Squeers followed his friends, and the family were left to meditate upon recent occurrences.\n\n# CHAPTER 46\n\nThrows some Light upon Nicholas’s Love; but whether for Good or Evil the Reader must determine\nAfter an anxious consideration of the painful and embarrassing position in which he was placed, Nicholas decided that he ought to lose no time in frankly stating it to the kind brothers. Availing himself of the first opportunity of being alone with Mr. Charles Cheeryble at the close of next day, he accordingly related Smike’s little history, and modestly but firmly expressed his hope that the good old gentleman would, under such circumstances as he described, hold him justified in adopting the extreme course of interfering between parent and child, and upholding the latter in his disobedience; even though his horror and dread of his father might seem, and would doubtless be represented as, a thing so repulsive and unnatural, as to render those who countenanced him in it, fit objects of general detestation and abhorrence.\n\n‘So deeply rooted does this horror of the man appear to be,’ said Nicholas, ‘that I can hardly believe he really is his son. Nature does not seem to have implanted in his breast one lingering feeling of affection for him, and surely she can never err.’\n\n‘My dear sir,’ replied brother Charles, ‘you fall into the very common mistake of charging upon Nature, matters with which she has not the smallest connection, and for which she is in no way responsible. Men talk of Nature as an abstract thing, and lose sight of what is natural while they do so. Here is a poor lad who has never felt a parent’s care, who has scarcely known anything all his life but suffering and sorrow, presented to a man who he is told is his father, and whose first act is to signify his intention of putting an end to his short term of happiness, of consigning him to his old fate, and taking him from the only friend he has ever had--which is yourself. If Nature, in such a case, put into that lad’s breast but one secret prompting which urged him towards his father and away from you, she would be a liar and an idiot.’\n\nNicholas was delighted to find that the old gentleman spoke so warmly, and in the hope that he might say something more to the same purpose, made no reply.\n\n‘The same mistake presents itself to me, in one shape or other, at every turn,’ said brother Charles. ‘Parents who never showed their love, complain of want of natural affection in their children; children who never showed their duty, complain of want of natural feeling in their parents; law-makers who find both so miserable that their affections have never had enough of life’s sun to develop them, are loud in their moralisings over parents and children too, and cry that the very ties of nature are disregarded. Natural affections and instincts, my dear sir, are the most beautiful of the Almighty’s works, but like other beautiful works of His, they must be reared and fostered, or it is as natural that they should be wholly obscured, and that new feelings should usurp their place, as it is that the sweetest productions of the earth, left untended, should be choked with weeds and briers. I wish we could be brought to consider this, and remembering natural obligations a little more at the right time, talk about them a little less at the wrong one.’\n\nAfter this, brother Charles, who had talked himself into a great heat, stopped to cool a little, and then continued:\n\n‘I dare say you are surprised, my dear sir, that I have listened to your recital with so little astonishment. That is easily explained. Your uncle has been here this morning.’\n\nNicholas coloured, and drew back a step or two.\n\n‘Yes,’ said the old gentleman, tapping his desk emphatically, ‘here, in this room. He would listen neither to reason, feeling, nor justice. But brother Ned was hard upon him; brother Ned, sir, might have melted a paving-stone.’\n\n‘He came to--’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘To complain of you,’ returned brother Charles, ‘to poison our ears with calumnies and falsehoods; but he came on a fruitless errand, and went away with some wholesome truths in his ear besides. Brother Ned, my dear Mr. Nickleby--brother Ned, sir, is a perfect lion. So is Tim Linkinwater; Tim is quite a lion. We had Tim in to face him at first, and Tim was at him, sir, before you could say “Jack Robinson.”’\n\n‘How can I ever thank you for all the deep obligations you impose upon me every day?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘By keeping silence upon the subject, my dear sir,’ returned brother Charles. ‘You shall be righted. At least you shall not be wronged. Nobody belonging to you shall be wronged. They shall not hurt a hair of your head, or the boy’s head, or your mother’s head, or your sister’s head. I have said it, brother Ned has said it, Tim Linkinwater has said it. We have all said it, and we’ll all do it. I have seen the father--if he is the father--and I suppose he must be. He is a barbarian and a hypocrite, Mr. Nickleby. I told him, “You are a barbarian, sir.” I did. I said, “You’re a barbarian, sir.” And I’m glad of it, I am VERY glad I told him he was a barbarian, very glad indeed!’\n\nBy this time brother Charles was in such a very warm state of indignation, that Nicholas thought he might venture to put in a word, but the moment he essayed to do so, Mr. Cheeryble laid his hand softly upon his arm, and pointed to a chair.\n\n‘The subject is at an end for the present,’ said the old gentleman, wiping his face. ‘Don’t revive it by a single word. I am going to speak upon another subject, a confidential subject, Mr. Nickleby. We must be cool again, we must be cool.’\n\nAfter two or three turns across the room he resumed his seat, and drawing his chair nearer to that on which Nicholas was seated, said:\n\n‘I am about to employ you, my dear sir, on a confidential and delicate mission.’\n\n‘You might employ many a more able messenger, sir,’ said Nicholas, ‘but a more trustworthy or zealous one, I may be bold to say, you could not find.’\n\n‘Of that I am well assured,’ returned brother Charles, ‘well assured. You will give me credit for thinking so, when I tell you that the object of this mission is a young lady.’\n\n‘A young lady, sir!’ cried Nicholas, quite trembling for the moment with his eagerness to hear more.\n\n‘A very beautiful young lady,’ said Mr. Cheeryble, gravely.\n\n‘Pray go on, sir,’ returned Nicholas.\n\n‘I am thinking how to do so,’ said brother Charles; sadly, as it seemed to his young friend, and with an expression allied to pain. ‘You accidentally saw a young lady in this room one morning, my dear sir, in a fainting fit. Do you remember? Perhaps you have forgotten.’\n\n‘Oh no,’ replied Nicholas, hurriedly. ‘I--I--remember it very well indeed.’\n\n‘SHE is the lady I speak of,’ said brother Charles. Like the famous parrot, Nicholas thought a great deal, but was unable to utter a word.\n\n‘She is the daughter,’ said Mr. Cheeryble, ‘of a lady who, when she was a beautiful girl herself, and I was very many years younger, I--it seems a strange word for me to utter now--I loved very dearly. You will smile, perhaps, to hear a grey-headed man talk about such things. You will not offend me, for when I was as young as you, I dare say I should have done the same.’\n\n‘I have no such inclination, indeed,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘My dear brother Ned,’ continued Mr. Cheeryble, ‘was to have married her sister, but she died. She is dead too now, and has been for many years. She married her choice; and I wish I could add that her after-life was as happy as God knows I ever prayed it might be!’\n\nA short silence intervened, which Nicholas made no effort to break.\n\n‘If trial and calamity had fallen as lightly on his head, as in the deepest truth of my own heart I ever hoped (for her sake) it would, his life would have been one of peace and happiness,’ said the old gentleman calmly. ‘It will be enough to say that this was not the case; that she was not happy; that they fell into complicated distresses and difficulties; that she came, twelve months before her death, to appeal to my old friendship; sadly changed, sadly altered, broken-spirited from suffering and ill-usage, and almost broken-hearted. He readily availed himself of the money which, to give her but one hour’s peace of mind, I would have poured out as freely as water--nay, he often sent her back for more--and yet even while he squandered it, he made the very success of these, her applications to me, the groundwork of cruel taunts and jeers, protesting that he knew she thought with bitter remorse of the choice she had made, that she had married him from motives of interest and vanity (he was a gay young man with great friends about him when she chose him for her husband), and venting in short upon her, by every unjust and unkind means, the bitterness of that ruin and disappointment which had been brought about by his profligacy alone. In those times this young lady was a mere child. I never saw her again until that morning when you saw her also, but my nephew, Frank--’\n\nNicholas started, and indistinctly apologising for the interruption, begged his patron to proceed.\n\n‘--My nephew, Frank, I say,’ resumed Mr. Cheeryble, ‘encountered her by accident, and lost sight of her almost in a minute afterwards, within two days after he returned to England. Her father lay in some secret place to avoid his creditors, reduced, between sickness and poverty, to the verge of death, and she, a child,--we might almost think, if we did not know the wisdom of all Heaven’s decrees--who should have blessed a better man, was steadily braving privation, degradation, and everything most terrible to such a young and delicate creature’s heart, for the purpose of supporting him. She was attended, sir,’ said brother Charles, ‘in these reverses, by one faithful creature, who had been, in old times, a poor kitchen wench in the family, who was then their solitary servant, but who might have been, for the truth and fidelity of her heart--who might have been--ah! the wife of Tim Linkinwater himself, sir!’\n\nPursuing this encomium upon the poor follower with such energy and relish as no words can describe, brother Charles leant back in his chair, and delivered the remainder of his relation with greater composure.\n\nIt was in substance this: That proudly resisting all offers of permanent aid and support from her late mother’s friends, because they were made conditional upon her quitting the wretched man, her father, who had no friends left, and shrinking with instinctive delicacy from appealing in their behalf to that true and noble heart which he hated, and had, through its greatest and purest goodness, deeply wronged by misconstruction and ill report, this young girl had struggled alone and unassisted to maintain him by the labour of her hands. That through the utmost depths of poverty and affliction she had toiled, never turning aside for an instant from her task, never wearied by the petulant gloom of a sick man sustained by no consoling recollections of the past or hopes of the future; never repining for the comforts she had rejected, or bewailing the hard lot she had voluntarily incurred. That every little accomplishment she had acquired in happier days had been put into requisition for this purpose, and directed to this one end. That for two long years, toiling by day and often too by night, working at the needle, the pencil, and the pen, and submitting, as a daily governess, to such caprices and indignities as women (with daughters too) too often love to inflict upon their own sex when they serve in such capacities, as though in jealousy of the superior intelligence which they are necessitated to employ,--indignities, in ninety-nine cases out of every hundred, heaped upon persons immeasurably and incalculably their betters, but outweighing in comparison any that the most heartless blackleg would put upon his groom--that for two long years, by dint of labouring in all these capacities and wearying in none, she had not succeeded in the sole aim and object of her life, but that, overwhelmed by accumulated difficulties and disappointments, she had been compelled to seek out her mother’s old friend, and, with a bursting heart, to confide in him at last.\n\n‘If I had been poor,’ said brother Charles, with sparkling eyes; ‘if I had been poor, Mr. Nickleby, my dear sir, which thank God I am not, I would have denied myself (of course anybody would under such circumstances) the commonest necessaries of life, to help her. As it is, the task is a difficult one. If her father were dead, nothing could be easier, for then she should share and cheer the happiest home that brother Ned and I could have, as if she were our child or sister. But he is still alive. Nobody can help him; that has been tried a thousand times; he was not abandoned by all without good cause, I know.’\n\n‘Cannot she be persuaded to--’ Nicholas hesitated when he had got thus far.\n\n‘To leave him?’ said brother Charles. ‘Who could entreat a child to desert her parent? Such entreaties, limited to her seeing him occasionally, have been urged upon her--not by me--but always with the same result.’\n\n‘Is he kind to her?’ said Nicholas. ‘Does he requite her affection?’\n\n‘True kindness, considerate self-denying kindness, is not in his nature,’ returned Mr. Cheeryble. ‘Such kindness as he knows, he regards her with, I believe. The mother was a gentle, loving, confiding creature, and although he wounded her from their marriage till her death as cruelly and wantonly as ever man did, she never ceased to love him. She commended him on her death-bed to her child’s care. Her child has never forgotten it, and never will.’\n\n‘Have you no influence over him?’ asked Nicholas.\n\n‘I, my dear sir! The last man in the world. Such are his jealousy and hatred of me, that if he knew his daughter had opened her heart to me, he would render her life miserable with his reproaches; although--this is the inconsistency and selfishness of his character--although if he knew that every penny she had came from me, he would not relinquish one personal desire that the most reckless expenditure of her scanty stock could gratify.’\n\n‘An unnatural scoundrel!’ said Nicholas, indignantly.\n\n‘We will use no harsh terms,’ said brother Charles, in a gentle voice; ‘but accommodate ourselves to the circumstances in which this young lady is placed. Such assistance as I have prevailed upon her to accept, I have been obliged, at her own earnest request, to dole out in the smallest portions, lest he, finding how easily money was procured, should squander it even more lightly than he is accustomed to do. She has come to and fro, to and fro, secretly and by night, to take even this; and I cannot bear that things should go on in this way, Mr Nickleby, I really cannot bear it.’\n\nThen it came out by little and little, how that the twins had been revolving in their good old heads manifold plans and schemes for helping this young lady in the most delicate and considerate way, and so that her father should not suspect the source whence the aid was derived; and how they had at last come to the conclusion, that the best course would be to make a feint of purchasing her little drawings and ornamental work at a high price, and keeping up a constant demand for the same. For the furtherance of which end and object it was necessary that somebody should represent the dealer in such commodities, and after great deliberation they had pitched upon Nicholas to support this character.\n\n‘He knows me,’ said brother Charles, ‘and he knows my brother Ned. Neither of us would do. Frank is a very good fellow--a very fine fellow--but we are afraid that he might be a little flighty and thoughtless in such a delicate matter, and that he might, perhaps--that he might, in short, be too susceptible (for she is a beautiful creature, sir; just what her poor mother was), and falling in love with her before he knew well his own mind, carry pain and sorrow into that innocent breast, which we would be the humble instruments of gradually making happy. He took an extraordinary interest in her fortunes when he first happened to encounter her; and we gather from the inquiries we have made of him, that it was she in whose behalf he made that turmoil which led to your first acquaintance.’\n\nNicholas stammered out that he had before suspected the possibility of such a thing; and in explanation of its having occurred to him, described when and where he had seen the young lady himself.\n\n‘Well; then you see,’ continued brother Charles, ‘that HE wouldn’t do. Tim Linkinwater is out of the question; for Tim, sir, is such a tremendous fellow, that he could never contain himself, but would go to loggerheads with the father before he had been in the place five minutes. You don’t know what Tim is, sir, when he is aroused by anything that appeals to his feelings very strongly; then he is terrific, sir, is Tim Linkinwater, absolutely terrific. Now, in you we can repose the strictest confidence; in you we have seen--or at least I have seen, and that’s the same thing, for there’s no difference between me and my brother Ned, except that he is the finest creature that ever lived, and that there is not, and never will be, anybody like him in all the world--in you we have seen domestic virtues and affections, and delicacy of feeling, which exactly qualify you for such an office. And you are the man, sir.’\n\n‘The young lady, sir,’ said Nicholas, who felt so embarrassed that he had no small difficulty in saying anything at all--‘Does--is--is she a party to this innocent deceit?’\n\n‘Yes, yes,’ returned Mr. Cheeryble; ‘at least she knows you come from us; she does NOT know, however, but that we shall dispose of these little productions that you’ll purchase from time to time; and, perhaps, if you did it very well (that is, VERY well indeed), perhaps she might be brought to believe that we--that we made a profit of them. Eh? Eh?’\n\nIn this guileless and most kind simplicity, brother Charles was so happy, and in this possibility of the young lady being led to think that she was under no obligation to him, he evidently felt so sanguine and had so much delight, that Nicholas would not breathe a doubt upon the subject.\n\nAll this time, however, there hovered upon the tip of his tongue a confession that the very same objections which Mr. Cheeryble had stated to the employment of his nephew in this commission applied with at least equal force and validity to himself, and a hundred times had he been upon the point of avowing the real state of his feelings, and entreating to be released from it. But as often, treading upon the heels of this impulse, came another which urged him to refrain, and to keep his secret to his own breast. ‘Why should I,’ thought Nicholas, ‘why should I throw difficulties in the way of this benevolent and high-minded design? What if I do love and reverence this good and lovely creature. Should I not appear a most arrogant and shallow coxcomb if I gravely represented that there was any danger of her falling in love with me? Besides, have I no confidence in myself? Am I not now bound in honour to repress these thoughts? Has not this excellent man a right to my best and heartiest services, and should any considerations of self deter me from rendering them?’\n\nAsking himself such questions as these, Nicholas mentally answered with great emphasis ‘No!’ and persuading himself that he was a most conscientious and glorious martyr, nobly resolved to do what, if he had examined his own heart a little more carefully, he would have found he could not resist. Such is the sleight of hand by which we juggle with ourselves, and change our very weaknesses into stanch and most magnanimous virtues!\n\nMr. Cheeryble, being of course wholly unsuspicious that such reflections were presenting themselves to his young friend, proceeded to give him the needful credentials and directions for his first visit, which was to be made next morning; and all preliminaries being arranged, and the strictest secrecy enjoined, Nicholas walked home for the night very thoughtfully indeed.\n\nThe place to which Mr. Cheeryble had directed him was a row of mean and not over-cleanly houses, situated within ‘the Rules’ of the King’s Bench Prison, and not many hundred paces distant from the obelisk in St George’s Fields. The Rules are a certain liberty adjoining the prison, and comprising some dozen streets in which debtors who can raise money to pay large fees, from which their creditors do NOT derive any benefit, are permitted to reside by the wise provisions of the same enlightened laws which leave the debtor who can raise no money to starve in jail, without the food, clothing, lodging, or warmth, which are provided for felons convicted of the most atrocious crimes that can disgrace humanity. There are many pleasant fictions of the law in constant operation, but there is not one so pleasant or practically humorous as that which supposes every man to be of equal value in its impartial eye, and the benefits of all laws to be equally attainable by all men, without the smallest reference to the furniture of their pockets.\n\nTo the row of houses indicated to him by Mr. Charles Cheeryble, Nicholas directed his steps, without much troubling his head with such matters as these; and at this row of houses--after traversing a very dirty and dusty suburb, of which minor theatricals, shell-fish, ginger-beer, spring vans, greengrocery, and brokers’ shops, appeared to compose the main and most prominent features--he at length arrived with a palpitating heart. There were small gardens in front which, being wholly neglected in all other respects, served as little pens for the dust to collect in, until the wind came round the corner and blew it down the road. Opening the rickety gate which, dangling on its broken hinges before one of these, half admitted and half repulsed the visitor, Nicholas knocked at the street door with a faltering hand.\n\nIt was in truth a shabby house outside, with very dim parlour windows and very small show of blinds, and very dirty muslin curtains dangling across the lower panes on very loose and limp strings. Neither, when the door was opened, did the inside appear to belie the outward promise, as there was faded carpeting on the stairs and faded oil-cloth in the passage; in addition to which discomforts a gentleman Ruler was smoking hard in the front parlour (though it was not yet noon), while the lady of the house was busily engaged in turpentining the disjointed fragments of a tent-bedstead at the door of the back parlour, as if in preparation for the reception of some new lodger who had been fortunate enough to engage it.\n\nNicholas had ample time to make these observations while the little boy, who went on errands for the lodgers, clattered down the kitchen stairs and was heard to scream, as in some remote cellar, for Miss Bray’s servant, who, presently appearing and requesting him to follow her, caused him to evince greater symptoms of nervousness and disorder than so natural a consequence of his having inquired for that young lady would seem calculated to occasion.\n\nUpstairs he went, however, and into a front room he was shown, and there, seated at a little table by the window, on which were drawing materials with which she was occupied, sat the beautiful girl who had so engrossed his thoughts, and who, surrounded by all the new and strong interest which Nicholas attached to her story, seemed now, in his eyes, a thousand times more beautiful than he had ever yet supposed her.\n\nBut how the graces and elegancies which she had dispersed about the poorly-furnished room went to the heart of Nicholas! Flowers, plants, birds, the harp, the old piano whose notes had sounded so much sweeter in bygone times; how many struggles had it cost her to keep these two last links of that broken chain which bound her yet to home! With every slender ornament, the occupation of her leisure hours, replete with that graceful charm which lingers in every little tasteful work of woman’s hands, how much patient endurance and how many gentle affections were entwined! He felt as though the smile of Heaven were on the little chamber; as though the beautiful devotion of so young and weak a creature had shed a ray of its own on the inanimate things around, and made them beautiful as itself; as though the halo with which old painters surround the bright angels of a sinless world played about a being akin in spirit to them, and its light were visibly before him.\n\nAnd yet Nicholas was in the Rules of the King’s Bench Prison! If he had been in Italy indeed, and the time had been sunset, and the scene a stately terrace! But, there is one broad sky over all the world, and whether it be blue or cloudy, the same heaven beyond it; so, perhaps, he had no need of compunction for thinking as he did.\n\nIt is not to be supposed that he took in everything at one glance, for he had as yet been unconscious of the presence of a sick man propped up with pillows in an easy-chair, who, moving restlessly and impatiently in his seat, attracted his attention.\n\nHe was scarce fifty, perhaps, but so emaciated as to appear much older. His features presented the remains of a handsome countenance, but one in which the embers of strong and impetuous passions were easier to be traced than any expression which would have rendered a far plainer face much more prepossessing. His looks were very haggard, and his limbs and body literally worn to the bone, but there was something of the old fire in the large sunken eye notwithstanding, and it seemed to kindle afresh as he struck a thick stick, with which he seemed to have supported himself in his seat, impatiently on the floor twice or thrice, and called his daughter by her name.\n\n‘Madeline, who is this? What does anybody want here? Who told a stranger we could be seen? What is it?’\n\n‘I believe--’ the young lady began, as she inclined her head with an air of some confusion, in reply to the salutation of Nicholas.\n\n‘You always believe,’ returned her father, petulantly. ‘What is it?’\n\nBy this time Nicholas had recovered sufficient presence of mind to speak for himself, so he said (as it had been agreed he should say) that he had called about a pair of hand-screens, and some painted velvet for an ottoman, both of which were required to be of the most elegant design possible, neither time nor expense being of the smallest consideration. He had also to pay for the two drawings, with many thanks, and, advancing to the little table, he laid upon it a bank note, folded in an envelope and sealed.\n\n‘See that the money is right, Madeline,’ said the father. ‘Open the paper, my dear.’\n\n‘It’s quite right, papa, I’m sure.’\n\n‘Here!’ said Mr. Bray, putting out his hand, and opening and shutting his bony fingers with irritable impatience. ‘Let me see. What are you talking about, Madeline? You’re sure? How can you be sure of any such thing? Five pounds--well, is THAT right?’\n\n‘Quite,’ said Madeline, bending over him. She was so busily employed in arranging the pillows that Nicholas could not see her face, but as she stooped he thought he saw a tear fall.\n\n‘Ring the bell, ring the bell,’ said the sick man, with the same nervous eagerness, and motioning towards it with such a quivering hand that the bank note rustled in the air. ‘Tell her to get it changed, to get me a newspaper, to buy me some grapes, another bottle of the wine that I had last week--and--and--I forget half I want just now, but she can go out again. Let her get those first, those first. Now, Madeline, my love, quick, quick! Good God, how slow you are!’\n\n‘He remembers nothing that SHE wants!’ thought Nicholas. Perhaps something of what he thought was expressed in his countenance, for the sick man, turning towards him with great asperity, demanded to know if he waited for a receipt.\n\n‘It is no matter at all,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘No matter! what do you mean, sir?’ was the tart rejoinder. ‘No matter! Do you think you bring your paltry money here as a favour or a gift; or as a matter of business, and in return for value received? D--n you, sir, because you can’t appreciate the time and taste which are bestowed upon the goods you deal in, do you think you give your money away? Do you know that you are talking to a gentleman, sir, who at one time could have bought up fifty such men as you and all you have? What do you mean?’\n\n‘I merely mean that as I shall have many dealings with this lady, if she will kindly allow me, I will not trouble her with such forms,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Then I mean, if you please, that we’ll have as many forms as we can, returned the father. ‘My daughter, sir, requires no kindness from you or anybody else. Have the goodness to confine your dealings strictly to trade and business, and not to travel beyond it. Every petty tradesman is to begin to pity her now, is he? Upon my soul! Very pretty. Madeline, my dear, give him a receipt; and mind you always do so.’\n\nWhile she was feigning to write it, and Nicholas was ruminating upon the extraordinary but by no means uncommon character thus presented to his observation, the invalid, who appeared at times to suffer great bodily pain, sank back in his chair and moaned out a feeble complaint that the girl had been gone an hour, and that everybody conspired to goad him.\n\n‘When,’ said Nicholas, as he took the piece of paper, ‘when shall I call again?’\n\nThis was addressed to the daughter, but the father answered immediately.\n\n‘When you’re requested to call, sir, and not before. Don’t worry and persecute. Madeline, my dear, when is this person to call again?’\n\n‘Oh, not for a long time, not for three or four weeks; it is not necessary, indeed; I can do without,’ said the young lady, with great eagerness.\n\n‘Why, how are we to do without?’ urged her father, not speaking above his breath. ‘Three or four weeks, Madeline! Three or four weeks!’\n\n‘Then sooner, sooner, if you please,’ said the young lady, turning to Nicholas.\n\n‘Three or four weeks!’ muttered the father. ‘Madeline, what on earth--do nothing for three or four weeks!’\n\n‘It is a long time, ma’am,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘YOU think so, do you?’ retorted the father, angrily. ‘If I chose to beg, sir, and stoop to ask assistance from people I despise, three or four months would not be a long time; three or four years would not be a long time. Understand, sir, that is if I chose to be dependent; but as I don’t, you may call in a week.’\n\nNicholas bowed low to the young lady and retired, pondering upon Mr Bray’s ideas of independence, and devoutly hoping that there might be few such independent spirits as he mingling with the baser clay of humanity.\n\nHe heard a light footstep above him as he descended the stairs, and looking round saw that the young lady was standing there, and glancing timidly towards him, seemed to hesitate whether she should call him back or no. The best way of settling the question was to turn back at once, which Nicholas did.\n\n‘I don’t know whether I do right in asking you, sir,’ said Madeline, hurriedly, ‘but pray, pray, do not mention to my poor mother’s dear friends what has passed here today. He has suffered much, and is worse this morning. I beg you, sir, as a boon, a favour to myself.’\n\n‘You have but to hint a wish,’ returned Nicholas fervently, ‘and I would hazard my life to gratify it.’\n\n‘You speak hastily, sir.’\n\n‘Truly and sincerely,’ rejoined Nicholas, his lips trembling as he formed the words, ‘if ever man spoke truly yet. I am not skilled in disguising my feelings, and if I were, I could not hide my heart from you. Dear madam, as I know your history, and feel as men and angels must who hear and see such things, I do entreat you to believe that I would die to serve you.’\n\nThe young lady turned away her head, and was plainly weeping.\n\n‘Forgive me,’ said Nicholas, with respectful earnestness, ‘if I seem to say too much, or to presume upon the confidence which has been intrusted to me. But I could not leave you as if my interest and sympathy expired with the commission of the day. I am your faithful servant, humbly devoted to you from this hour, devoted in strict truth and honour to him who sent me here, and in pure integrity of heart, and distant respect for you. If I meant more or less than this, I should be unworthy his regard, and false to the very nature that prompts the honest words I utter.’\n\nShe waved her hand, entreating him to be gone, but answered not a word. Nicholas could say no more, and silently withdrew. And thus ended his first interview with Madeline Bray.\n\n# CHAPTER 47\n\nMr. Ralph Nickleby has some confidential Intercourse with another old Friend. They concert between them a Project, which promises well for both\n‘There go the three-quarters past!’ muttered Newman Noggs, listening to the chimes of some neighbouring church ‘and my dinner time’s two. He does it on purpose. He makes a point of it. It’s just like him.’\n\nIt was in his own little den of an office and on the top of his official stool that Newman thus soliloquised; and the soliloquy referred, as Newman’s grumbling soliloquies usually did, to Ralph Nickleby.\n\n‘I don’t believe he ever had an appetite,’ said Newman, ‘except for pounds, shillings, and pence, and with them he’s as greedy as a wolf. I should like to have him compelled to swallow one of every English coin. The penny would be an awkward morsel--but the crown--ha! ha!’\n\nHis good-humour being in some degree restored by the vision of Ralph Nickleby swallowing, perforce, a five-shilling piece, Newman slowly brought forth from his desk one of those portable bottles, currently known as pocket-pistols, and shaking the same close to his ear so as to produce a rippling sound very cool and pleasant to listen to, suffered his features to relax, and took a gurgling drink, which relaxed them still more. Replacing the cork, he smacked his lips twice or thrice with an air of great relish, and, the taste of the liquor having by this time evaporated, recurred to his grievance again.\n\n‘Five minutes to three,’ growled Newman; ‘it can’t want more by this time; and I had my breakfast at eight o’clock, and SUCH a breakfast! and my right dinner-time two! And I might have a nice little bit of hot roast meat spoiling at home all this time--how does HE know I haven’t? “Don’t go till I come back,” “Don’t go till I come back,” day after day. What do you always go out at my dinner-time for then--eh? Don’t you know it’s nothing but aggravation--eh?’\n\nThese words, though uttered in a very loud key, were addressed to nothing but empty air. The recital of his wrongs, however, seemed to have the effect of making Newman Noggs desperate; for he flattened his old hat upon his head, and drawing on the everlasting gloves, declared with great vehemence, that come what might, he would go to dinner that very minute.\n\nCarrying this resolution into instant effect, he had advanced as far as the passage, when the sound of the latch-key in the street door caused him to make a precipitate retreat into his own office again.\n\n‘Here he is,’ growled Newman, ‘and somebody with him. Now it’ll be “Stop till this gentleman’s gone.” But I won’t. That’s flat.’\n\nSo saying, Newman slipped into a tall empty closet which opened with two half doors, and shut himself up; intending to slip out directly Ralph was safe inside his own room.\n\n‘Noggs!’ cried Ralph, ‘where is that fellow, Noggs?’\n\nBut not a word said Newman.\n\n‘The dog has gone to his dinner, though I told him not,’ muttered Ralph, looking into the office, and pulling out his watch. ‘Humph!’ You had better come in here, Gride. My man’s out, and the sun is hot upon my room. This is cool and in the shade, if you don’t mind roughing it.’\n\n‘Not at all, Mr. Nickleby, oh not at all! All places are alike to me, sir. Ah! very nice indeed. Oh! very nice!’\n\nThe parson who made this reply was a little old man, of about seventy or seventy-five years of age, of a very lean figure, much bent and slightly twisted. He wore a grey coat with a very narrow collar, an old-fashioned waistcoat of ribbed black silk, and such scanty trousers as displayed his shrunken spindle-shanks in their full ugliness. The only articles of display or ornament in his dress were a steel watch-chain to which were attached some large gold seals; and a black ribbon into which, in compliance with an old fashion scarcely ever observed in these days, his grey hair was gathered behind. His nose and chin were sharp and prominent, his jaws had fallen inwards from loss of teeth, his face was shrivelled and yellow, save where the cheeks were streaked with the colour of a dry winter apple; and where his beard had been, there lingered yet a few grey tufts which seemed, like the ragged eyebrows, to denote the badness of the soil from which they sprung. The whole air and attitude of the form was one of stealthy cat-like obsequiousness; the whole expression of the face was concentrated in a wrinkled leer, compounded of cunning, lecherousness, slyness, and avarice.\n\nSuch was old Arthur Gride, in whose face there was not a wrinkle, in whose dress there was not one spare fold or plait, but expressed the most covetous and griping penury, and sufficiently indicated his belonging to that class of which Ralph Nickleby was a member. Such was old Arthur Gride, as he sat in a low chair looking up into the face of Ralph Nickleby, who, lounging upon the tall office stool, with his arms upon his knees, looked down into his; a match for him on whatever errand he had come.\n\n‘And how have you been?’ said Gride, feigning great interest in Ralph’s state of health. ‘I haven’t seen you for--oh! not for--’\n\n‘Not for a long time,’ said Ralph, with a peculiar smile, importing that he very well knew it was not on a mere visit of compliment that his friend had come. ‘It was a narrow chance that you saw me now, for I had only just come up to the door as you turned the corner.’\n\n‘I am very lucky,’ observed Gride.\n\n‘So men say,’ replied Ralph, drily.\n\nThe older money-lender wagged his chin and smiled, but he originated no new remark, and they sat for some little time without speaking. Each was looking out to take the other at a disadvantage.\n\n‘Come, Gride,’ said Ralph, at length; ‘what’s in the wind today?’\n\n‘Aha! you’re a bold man, Mr. Nickleby,’ cried the other, apparently very much relieved by Ralph’s leading the way to business. ‘Oh dear, dear, what a bold man you are!’\n\n‘Why, you have a sleek and slinking way with you that makes me seem so by contrast,’ returned Ralph. ‘I don’t know but that yours may answer better, but I want the patience for it.’\n\n‘You were born a genius, Mr. Nickleby,’ said old Arthur. ‘Deep, deep, deep. Ah!’\n\n‘Deep enough,’ retorted Ralph, ‘to know that I shall need all the depth I have, when men like you begin to compliment. You know I have stood by when you fawned and flattered other people, and I remember pretty well what THAT always led to.’\n\n‘Ha, ha, ha!’ rejoined Arthur, rubbing his hands. ‘So you do, so you do, no doubt. Not a man knows it better. Well, it’s a pleasant thing now to think that you remember old times. Oh dear!’\n\n‘Now then,’ said Ralph, composedly; ‘what’s in the wind, I ask again? What is it?’\n\n‘See that now!’ cried the other. ‘He can’t even keep from business while we’re chatting over bygones. Oh dear, dear, what a man it is!’\n\n‘WHICH of the bygones do you want to revive?’ said Ralph. ‘One of them, I know, or you wouldn’t talk about them.’\n\n‘He suspects even me!’ cried old Arthur, holding up his hands. ‘Even me! Oh dear, even me. What a man it is! Ha, ha, ha! What a man it is! Mr Nickleby against all the world. There’s nobody like him. A giant among pigmies, a giant, a giant!’\n\nRalph looked at the old dog with a quiet smile as he chuckled on in this strain, and Newman Noggs in the closet felt his heart sink within him as the prospect of dinner grew fainter and fainter.\n\n‘I must humour him though,’ cried old Arthur; ‘he must have his way--a wilful man, as the Scotch say--well, well, they’re a wise people, the Scotch. He will talk about business, and won’t give away his time for nothing. He’s very right. Time is money, time is money.’\n\n‘He was one of us who made that saying, I should think,’ said Ralph. ‘Time is money, and very good money too, to those who reckon interest by it. Time IS money! Yes, and time costs money; it’s rather an expensive article to some people we could name, or I forget my trade.’\n\nIn rejoinder to this sally, old Arthur again raised his hands, again chuckled, and again ejaculated ‘What a man it is!’ which done, he dragged the low chair a little nearer to Ralph’s high stool, and looking upwards into his immovable face, said,\n\n‘What would you say to me, if I was to tell you that I was--that I was--going to be married?’\n\n‘I should tell you,’ replied Ralph, looking coldly down upon him, ‘that for some purpose of your own you told a lie, and that it wasn’t the first time and wouldn’t be the last; that I wasn’t surprised and wasn’t to be taken in.’\n\n‘Then I tell you seriously that I am,’ said old Arthur.\n\n‘And I tell you seriously,’ rejoined Ralph, ‘what I told you this minute. Stay. Let me look at you. There’s a liquorish devilry in your face. What is this?’\n\n‘I wouldn’t deceive YOU, you know,’ whined Arthur Gride; ‘I couldn’t do it, I should be mad to try. I, I, to deceive Mr. Nickleby! The pigmy to impose upon the giant. I ask again--he, he, he!--what should you say to me if I was to tell you that I was going to be married?’\n\n‘To some old hag?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘No, No,’ cried Arthur, interrupting him, and rubbing his hands in an ecstasy. ‘Wrong, wrong again. Mr. Nickleby for once at fault; out, quite out! To a young and beautiful girl; fresh, lovely, bewitching, and not nineteen. Dark eyes, long eyelashes, ripe and ruddy lips that to look at is to long to kiss, beautiful clustering hair that one’s fingers itch to play with, such a waist as might make a man clasp the air involuntarily, thinking of twining his arm about it, little feet that tread so lightly they hardly seem to walk upon the ground--to marry all this, sir, this--hey, hey!’\n\n‘This is something more than common drivelling,’ said Ralph, after listening with a curled lip to the old sinner’s raptures. ‘The girl’s name?’\n\n‘Oh deep, deep! See now how deep that is!’ exclaimed old Arthur. ‘He knows I want his help, he knows he can give it me, he knows it must all turn to his advantage, he sees the thing already. Her name--is there nobody within hearing?’\n\n‘Why, who the devil should there be?’ retorted Ralph, testily.\n\n‘I didn’t know but that perhaps somebody might be passing up or down the stairs,’ said Arthur Gride, after looking out at the door and carefully reclosing it; ‘or but that your man might have come back and might have been listening outside. Clerks and servants have a trick of listening, and I should have been very uncomfortable if Mr. Noggs--’\n\n‘Curse Mr. Noggs,’ said Ralph, sharply, ‘and go on with what you have to say.’\n\n‘Curse Mr. Noggs, by all means,’ rejoined old Arthur; ‘I am sure I have not the least objection to that. Her name is--’\n\n‘Well,’ said Ralph, rendered very irritable by old Arthur’s pausing again ‘what is it?’\n\n‘Madeline Bray.’\n\nWhatever reasons there might have been--and Arthur Gride appeared to have anticipated some--for the mention of this name producing an effect upon Ralph, or whatever effect it really did produce upon him, he permitted none to manifest itself, but calmly repeated the name several times, as if reflecting when and where he had heard it before.\n\n‘Bray,’ said Ralph. ‘Bray--there was young Bray of--no, he never had a daughter.’\n\n‘You remember Bray?’ rejoined Arthur Gride.\n\n‘No,’ said Ralph, looking vacantly at him.\n\n‘Not Walter Bray! The dashing man, who used his handsome wife so ill?’\n\n‘If you seek to recall any particular dashing man to my recollection by such a trait as that,’ said Ralph, shrugging his shoulders, ‘I shall confound him with nine-tenths of the dashing men I have ever known.’\n\n‘Tut, tut. That Bray who is now in the Rules of the Bench,’ said old Arthur. ‘You can’t have forgotten Bray. Both of us did business with him. Why, he owes you money!’\n\n‘Oh HIM!’ rejoined Ralph. ‘Ay, ay. Now you speak. Oh! It’s HIS daughter, is it?’\n\nNaturally as this was said, it was not said so naturally but that a kindred spirit like old Arthur Gride might have discerned a design upon the part of Ralph to lead him on to much more explicit statements and explanations than he would have volunteered, or that Ralph could in all likelihood have obtained by any other means. Old Arthur, however, was so intent upon his own designs, that he suffered himself to be overreached, and had no suspicion but that his good friend was in earnest.\n\n‘I knew you couldn’t forget him, when you came to think for a moment,’ he said.\n\n‘You were right,’ answered Ralph. ‘But old Arthur Gride and matrimony is a most anomalous conjunction of words; old Arthur Gride and dark eyes and eyelashes, and lips that to look at is to long to kiss, and clustering hair that he wants to play with, and waists that he wants to span, and little feet that don’t tread upon anything--old Arthur Gride and such things as these is more monstrous still; but old Arthur Gride marrying the daughter of a ruined “dashing man” in the Rules of the Bench, is the most monstrous and incredible of all. Plainly, friend Arthur Gride, if you want any help from me in this business (which of course you do, or you would not be here), speak out, and to the purpose. And, above all, don’t talk to me of its turning to my advantage, for I know it must turn to yours also, and to a good round tune too, or you would have no finger in such a pie as this.’\n\nThere was enough acerbity and sarcasm not only in the matter of Ralph’s speech, but in the tone of voice in which he uttered it, and the looks with which he eked it out, to have fired even the ancient usurer’s cold blood and flushed even his withered cheek. But he gave vent to no demonstration of anger, contenting himself with exclaiming as before, ‘What a man it is!’ and rolling his head from side to side, as if in unrestrained enjoyment of his freedom and drollery. Clearly observing, however, from the expression in Ralph’s features, that he had best come to the point as speedily as might be, he composed himself for more serious business, and entered upon the pith and marrow of his negotiation.\n\nFirst, he dwelt upon the fact that Madeline Bray was devoted to the support and maintenance, and was a slave to every wish, of her only parent, who had no other friend on earth; to which Ralph rejoined that he had heard something of the kind before, and that if she had known a little more of the world, she wouldn’t have been such a fool.\n\nSecondly, he enlarged upon the character of her father, arguing, that even taking it for granted that he loved her in return with the utmost affection of which he was capable, yet he loved himself a great deal better; which Ralph said it was quite unnecessary to say anything more about, as that was very natural, and probable enough.\n\nAnd, thirdly, old Arthur premised that the girl was a delicate and beautiful creature, and that he had really a hankering to have her for his wife. To this Ralph deigned no other rejoinder than a harsh smile, and a glance at the shrivelled old creature before him, which were, however, sufficiently expressive.\n\n‘Now,’ said Gride, ‘for the little plan I have in my mind to bring this about; because, I haven’t offered myself even to the father yet, I should have told you. But that you have gathered already? Ah! oh dear, oh dear, what an edged tool you are!’\n\n‘Don’t play with me then,’ said Ralph impatiently. ‘You know the proverb.’\n\n‘A reply always on the tip of his tongue!’ cried old Arthur, raising his hands and eyes in admiration. ‘He is always prepared! Oh dear, what a blessing to have such a ready wit, and so much ready money to back it!’ Then, suddenly changing his tone, he went on: ‘I have been backwards and forwards to Bray’s lodgings several times within the last six months. It is just half a year since I first saw this delicate morsel, and, oh dear, what a delicate morsel it is! But that is neither here nor there. I am his detaining creditor for seventeen hundred pounds!’\n\n‘You talk as if you were the only detaining creditor,’ said Ralph, pulling out his pocket-book. ‘I am another for nine hundred and seventy-five pounds four and threepence.’\n\n‘The only other, Mr. Nickleby,’ said old Arthur, eagerly. ‘The only other. Nobody else went to the expense of lodging a detainer, trusting to our holding him fast enough, I warrant you. We both fell into the same snare; oh dear, what a pitfall it was; it almost ruined me! And lent him our money upon bills, with only one name besides his own, which to be sure everybody supposed to be a good one, and was as negotiable as money, but which turned out you know how. Just as we should have come upon him, he died insolvent. Ah! it went very nigh to ruin me, that loss did!’\n\n‘Go on with your scheme,’ said Ralph. ‘It’s of no use raising the cry of our trade just now; there’s nobody to hear us!’\n\n‘It’s always as well to talk that way,’ returned old Arthur, with a chuckle, ‘whether there’s anybody to hear us or not. Practice makes perfect, you know. Now, if I offer myself to Bray as his son-in-law, upon one simple condition that the moment I am fast married he shall be quietly released, and have an allowance to live just t’other side the water like a gentleman (he can’t live long, for I have asked his doctor, and he declares that his complaint is one of the Heart and it is impossible), and if all the advantages of this condition are properly stated and dwelt upon to him, do you think he could resist me? And if he could not resist ME, do you think his daughter could resist HIM? Shouldn’t I have her Mrs. Arthur Gride--pretty Mrs. Arthur Gride--a tit-bit--a dainty chick--shouldn’t I have her Mrs. Arthur Gride in a week, a month, a day--any time I chose to name?’\n\n‘Go on,’ said Ralph, nodding his head deliberately, and speaking in a tone whose studied coldness presented a strange contrast to the rapturous squeak to which his friend had gradually mounted. ‘Go on. You didn’t come here to ask me that.’\n\n‘Oh dear, how you talk!’ cried old Arthur, edging himself closer still to Ralph. ‘Of course I didn’t, I don’t pretend I did! I came to ask what you would take from me, if I prospered with the father, for this debt of yours. Five shillings in the pound, six and-eightpence, ten shillings? I WOULD go as far as ten for such a friend as you, we have always been on such good terms, but you won’t be so hard upon me as that, I know. Now, will you?’\n\n‘There’s something more to be told,’ said Ralph, as stony and immovable as ever.\n\n‘Yes, yes, there is, but you won’t give me time,’ returned Arthur Gride. ‘I want a backer in this matter; one who can talk, and urge, and press a point, which you can do as no man can. I can’t do that, for I am a poor, timid, nervous creature. Now, if you get a good composition for this debt, which you long ago gave up for lost, you’ll stand my friend, and help me. Won’t you?’\n\n‘There’s something more,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘No, no, indeed,’ cried Arthur Gride.\n\n‘Yes, yes, indeed. I tell you yes,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Oh!’ returned old Arthur feigning to be suddenly enlightened. ‘You mean something more, as concerns myself and my intention. Ay, surely, surely. Shall I mention that?’\n\n‘I think you had better,’ rejoined Ralph, drily.\n\n‘I didn’t like to trouble you with that, because I supposed your interest would cease with your own concern in the affair,’ said Arthur Gride. ‘That’s kind of you to ask. Oh dear, how very kind of you! Why, supposing I had a knowledge of some property--some little property--very little--to which this pretty chick was entitled; which nobody does or can know of at this time, but which her husband could sweep into his pouch, if he knew as much as I do, would that account for--’\n\n‘For the whole proceeding,’ rejoined Ralph, abruptly. ‘Now, let me turn this matter over, and consider what I ought to have if I should help you to success.’\n\n‘But don’t be hard,’ cried old Arthur, raising his hands with an imploring gesture, and speaking, in a tremulous voice. ‘Don’t be too hard upon me. It’s a very small property, it is indeed. Say the ten shillings, and we’ll close the bargain. It’s more than I ought to give, but you’re so kind--shall we say the ten? Do now, do.’\n\nRalph took no notice of these supplications, but sat for three or four minutes in a brown study, looking thoughtfully at the person from whom they proceeded. After sufficient cogitation he broke silence, and it certainly could not be objected that he used any needless circumlocution, or failed to speak directly to the purpose.\n\n‘If you married this girl without me,’ said Ralph, ‘you must pay my debt in full, because you couldn’t set her father free otherwise. It’s plain, then, that I must have the whole amount, clear of all deduction or incumbrance, or I should lose from being honoured with your confidence, instead of gaining by it. That’s the first article of the treaty. For the second, I shall stipulate that for my trouble in negotiation and persuasion, and helping you to this fortune, I have five hundred pounds. That’s very little, because you have the ripe lips, and the clustering hair, and what not, all to yourself. For the third and last article, I require that you execute a bond to me, this day, binding yourself in the payment of these two sums, before noon of the day of your marriage with Madeline Bray. You have told me I can urge and press a point. I press this one, and will take nothing less than these terms. Accept them if you like. If not, marry her without me if you can. I shall still get my debt.’\n\nTo all entreaties, protestations, and offers of compromise between his own proposals and those which Arthur Gride had first suggested, Ralph was deaf as an adder. He would enter into no further discussion of the subject, and while old Arthur dilated upon the enormity of his demands and proposed modifications of them, approaching by degrees nearer and nearer to the terms he resisted, sat perfectly mute, looking with an air of quiet abstraction over the entries and papers in his pocket-book. Finding that it was impossible to make any impression upon his staunch friend, Arthur Gride, who had prepared himself for some such result before he came, consented with a heavy heart to the proposed treaty, and upon the spot filled up the bond required (Ralph kept such instruments handy), after exacting the condition that Mr. Nickleby should accompany him to Bray’s lodgings that very hour, and open the negotiation at once, should circumstances appear auspicious and favourable to their designs.\n\nIn pursuance of this last understanding the worthy gentlemen went out together shortly afterwards, and Newman Noggs emerged, bottle in hand, from the cupboard, out of the upper door of which, at the imminent risk of detection, he had more than once thrust his red nose when such parts of the subject were under discussion as interested him most.\n\n‘I have no appetite now,’ said Newman, putting the flask in his pocket. ‘I’ve had MY dinner.’\n\nHaving delivered this observation in a very grievous and doleful tone, Newman reached the door in one long limp, and came back again in another.\n\n‘I don’t know who she may be, or what she may be,’ he said: ‘but I pity her with all my heart and soul; and I can’t help her, nor can I any of the people against whom a hundred tricks, but none so vile as this, are plotted every day! Well, that adds to my pain, but not to theirs. The thing is no worse because I know it, and it tortures me as well as them. Gride and Nickleby! Good pair for a curricle. Oh roguery! roguery! roguery!’\n\nWith these reflections, and a very hard knock on the crown of his unfortunate hat at each repetition of the last word, Newman Noggs, whose brain was a little muddled by so much of the contents of the pocket-pistol as had found their way there during his recent concealment, went forth to seek such consolation as might be derivable from the beef and greens of some cheap eating-house.\n\nMeanwhile the two plotters had betaken themselves to the same house whither Nicholas had repaired for the first time but a few mornings before, and having obtained access to Mr. Bray, and found his daughter from home, had by a train of the most masterly approaches that Ralph’s utmost skill could frame, at length laid open the real object of their visit.\n\n‘There he sits, Mr. Bray,’ said Ralph, as the invalid, not yet recovered from his surprise, reclined in his chair, looking alternately at him and Arthur Gride. ‘What if he has had the ill-fortune to be one cause of your detention in this place? I have been another; men must live; you are too much a man of the world not to see that in its true light. We offer the best reparation in our power. Reparation! Here is an offer of marriage, that many a titled father would leap at, for his child. Mr Arthur Gride, with the fortune of a prince. Think what a haul it is!’\n\n‘My daughter, sir,’ returned Bray, haughtily, ‘as I have brought her up, would be a rich recompense for the largest fortune that a man could bestow in exchange for her hand.’\n\n‘Precisely what I told you,’ said the artful Ralph, turning to his friend, old Arthur. ‘Precisely what made me consider the thing so fair and easy. There is no obligation on either side. You have money, and Miss Madeline has beauty and worth. She has youth, you have money. She has not money, you have not youth. Tit for tat, quits, a match of Heaven’s own making!’\n\n‘Matches are made in Heaven, they say,’ added Arthur Gride, leering hideously at the father-in-law he wanted. ‘If we are married, it will be destiny, according to that.’\n\n‘Then think, Mr. Bray,’ said Ralph, hastily substituting for this argument considerations more nearly allied to earth, ‘think what a stake is involved in the acceptance or rejection of these proposals of my friend.’\n\n‘How can I accept or reject,’ interrupted Mr. Bray, with an irritable consciousness that it really rested with him to decide. ‘It is for my daughter to accept or reject; it is for my daughter. You know that.’\n\n‘True,’ said Ralph, emphatically; ‘but you have still the power to advise; to state the reasons for and against; to hint a wish.’\n\n‘To hint a wish, sir!’ returned the debtor, proud and mean by turns, and selfish at all times. ‘I am her father, am I not? Why should I hint, and beat about the bush? Do you suppose, like her mother’s friends and my enemies--a curse upon them all!--that there is anything in what she has done for me but duty, sir, but duty? Or do you think that my having been unfortunate is a sufficient reason why our relative positions should be changed, and that she should command and I should obey? Hint a wish, too! Perhaps you think, because you see me in this place and scarcely able to leave this chair without assistance, that I am some broken-spirited dependent creature, without the courage or power to do what I may think best for my own child. Still the power to hint a wish! I hope so!’\n\n‘Pardon me,’ returned Ralph, who thoroughly knew his man, and had taken his ground accordingly; ‘you do not hear me out. I was about to say that your hinting a wish, even hinting a wish, would surely be equivalent to commanding.’\n\n‘Why, of course it would,’ retorted Mr. Bray, in an exasperated tone. ‘If you don’t happen to have heard of the time, sir, I tell you that there was a time, when I carried every point in triumph against her mother’s whole family, although they had power and wealth on their side, by my will alone.’\n\n‘Still,’ rejoined Ralph, as mildly as his nature would allow him, ‘you have not heard me out. You are a man yet qualified to shine in society, with many years of life before you; that is, if you lived in freer air, and under brighter skies, and chose your own companions. Gaiety is your element, you have shone in it before. Fashion and freedom for you. France, and an annuity that would support you there in luxury, would give you a new lease of life, would transfer you to a new existence. The town rang with your expensive pleasures once, and you could blaze up on a new scene again, profiting by experience, and living a little at others’ cost, instead of letting others live at yours. What is there on the reverse side of the picture? What is there? I don’t know which is the nearest churchyard, but a gravestone there, wherever it is, and a date, perhaps two years hence, perhaps twenty. That’s all.’\n\nMr. Bray rested his elbow on the arm of his chair, and shaded his face with his hand.\n\n‘I speak plainly,’ said Ralph, sitting down beside him, ‘because I feel strongly. It’s my interest that you should marry your daughter to my friend Gride, because then he sees me paid--in part, that is. I don’t disguise it. I acknowledge it openly. But what interest have you in recommending her to such a step? Keep that in view. She might object, remonstrate, shed tears, talk of his being too old, and plead that her life would be rendered miserable. But what is it now?’\n\nSeveral slight gestures on the part of the invalid showed that these arguments were no more lost upon him, than the smallest iota of his demeanour was upon Ralph.\n\n‘What is it now, I say,’ pursued the wily usurer, ‘or what has it a chance of being? If you died, indeed, the people you hate would make her happy. But can you bear the thought of that?’\n\n‘No!’ returned Bray, urged by a vindictive impulse he could not repress.\n\n‘I should imagine not, indeed!’ said Ralph, quietly. ‘If she profits by anybody’s death,’ this was said in a lower tone, ‘let it be by her husband’s. Don’t let her have to look back to yours, as the event from which to date a happier life. Where is the objection? Let me hear it stated. What is it? That her suitor is an old man? Why, how often do men of family and fortune, who haven’t your excuse, but have all the means and superfluities of life within their reach, how often do they marry their daughters to old men, or (worse still) to young men without heads or hearts, to tickle some idle vanity, strengthen some family interest, or secure some seat in Parliament! Judge for her, sir, judge for her. You must know best, and she will live to thank you.’\n\n‘Hush! hush!’ cried Mr. Bray, suddenly starting up, and covering Ralph’s mouth with his trembling hand. ‘I hear her at the door!’\n\nThere was a gleam of conscience in the shame and terror of this hasty action, which, in one short moment, tore the thin covering of sophistry from the cruel design, and laid it bare in all its meanness and heartless deformity. The father fell into his chair pale and trembling; Arthur Gride plucked and fumbled at his hat, and durst not raise his eyes from the floor; even Ralph crouched for the moment like a beaten hound, cowed by the presence of one young innocent girl!\n\nThe effect was almost as brief as sudden. Ralph was the first to recover himself, and observing Madeline’s looks of alarm, entreated the poor girl to be composed, assuring her that there was no cause for fear.\n\n‘A sudden spasm,’ said Ralph, glancing at Mr. Bray. ‘He is quite well now.’\n\nIt might have moved a very hard and worldly heart to see the young and beautiful creature, whose certain misery they had been contriving but a minute before, throw her arms about her father’s neck, and pour forth words of tender sympathy and love, the sweetest a father’s ear can know, or child’s lips form. But Ralph looked coldly on; and Arthur Gride, whose bleared eyes gloated only over the outward beauties, and were blind to the spirit which reigned within, evinced--a fantastic kind of warmth certainly, but not exactly that kind of warmth of feeling which the contemplation of virtue usually inspires.\n\n‘Madeline,’ said her father, gently disengaging himself, ‘it was nothing.’\n\n‘But you had that spasm yesterday, and it is terrible to see you in such pain. Can I do nothing for you?’\n\n‘Nothing just now. Here are two gentlemen, Madeline, one of whom you have seen before. She used to say,’ added Mr. Bray, addressing Arthur Gride, ‘that the sight of you always made me worse. That was natural, knowing what she did, and only what she did, of our connection and its results. Well, well. Perhaps she may change her mind on that point; girls have leave to change their minds, you know. You are very tired, my dear.’\n\n‘I am not, indeed.’\n\n‘Indeed you are. You do too much.’\n\n‘I wish I could do more.’\n\n‘I know you do, but you overtask your strength. This wretched life, my love, of daily labour and fatigue, is more than you can bear, I am sure it is. Poor Madeline!’\n\nWith these and many more kind words, Mr. Bray drew his daughter to him and kissed her cheek affectionately. Ralph, watching him sharply and closely in the meantime, made his way towards the door, and signed to Gride to follow him.\n\n‘You will communicate with us again?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Yes, yes,’ returned Mr. Bray, hastily thrusting his daughter aside. ‘In a week. Give me a week.’\n\n‘One week,’ said Ralph, turning to his companion, ‘from today. Good-morning. Miss Madeline, I kiss your hand.’\n\n‘We will shake hands, Gride,’ said Mr. Bray, extending his, as old Arthur bowed. ‘You mean well, no doubt. I am bound to say so now. If I owed you money, that was not your fault. Madeline, my love, your hand here.’\n\n‘Oh dear! If the young lady would condescent! Only the tips of her fingers,’ said Arthur, hesitating and half retreating.\n\nMadeline shrunk involuntarily from the goblin figure, but she placed the tips of her fingers in his hand and instantly withdrew them. After an ineffectual clutch, intended to detain and carry them to his lips, old Arthur gave his own fingers a mumbling kiss, and with many amorous distortions of visage went in pursuit of his friend, who was by this time in the street.\n\n‘What does he say, what does he say? What does the giant say to the pigmy?’ inquired Arthur Gride, hobbling up to Ralph.\n\n‘What does the pigmy say to the giant?’ rejoined Ralph, elevating his eyebrows and looking down upon his questioner.\n\n‘He doesn’t know what to say,’ replied Arthur Gride. ‘He hopes and fears. But is she not a dainty morsel?’\n\n‘I have no great taste for beauty,’ growled Ralph.\n\n‘But I have,’ rejoined Arthur, rubbing his hands. ‘Oh dear! How handsome her eyes looked when she was stooping over him! Such long lashes, such delicate fringe! She--she--looked at me so soft.’\n\n‘Not over-lovingly, I think,’ said Ralph. ‘Did she?’\n\n‘No, you think not?’ replied old Arthur. ‘But don’t you think it can be brought about? Don’t you think it can?’\n\nRalph looked at him with a contemptuous frown, and replied with a sneer, and between his teeth:\n\n‘Did you mark his telling her she was tired and did too much, and overtasked her strength?’\n\n‘Ay, ay. What of it?’\n\n‘When do you think he ever told her that before? The life is more than she can bear. Yes, yes. He’ll change it for her.’\n\n‘D’ye think it’s done?’ inquired old Arthur, peering into his companion’s face with half-closed eyes.\n\n‘I am sure it’s done,’ said Ralph. ‘He is trying to deceive himself, even before our eyes, already. He is making believe that he thinks of her good and not his own. He is acting a virtuous part, and so considerate and affectionate, sir, that the daughter scarcely knew him. I saw a tear of surprise in her eye. There’ll be a few more tears of surprise there before long, though of a different kind. Oh! we may wait with confidence for this day week.’\n\n# CHAPTER 48\n\nBeing for the Benefit of Mr. Vincent Crummles, and positively his last Appearance on this Stage\nIt was with a very sad and heavy heart, oppressed by many painful ideas, that Nicholas retraced his steps eastward and betook himself to the counting-house of Cheeryble Brothers. Whatever the idle hopes he had suffered himself to entertain, whatever the pleasant visions which had sprung up in his mind and grouped themselves round the fair image of Madeline Bray, they were now dispelled, and not a vestige of their gaiety and brightness remained.\n\nIt would be a poor compliment to Nicholas’s better nature, and one which he was very far from deserving, to insinuate that the solution, and such a solution, of the mystery which had seemed to surround Madeline Bray, when he was ignorant even of her name, had damped his ardour or cooled the fervour of his admiration. If he had regarded her before, with such a passion as young men attracted by mere beauty and elegance may entertain, he was now conscious of much deeper and stronger feelings. But, reverence for the truth and purity of her heart, respect for the helplessness and loneliness of her situation, sympathy with the trials of one so young and fair and admiration of her great and noble spirit, all seemed to raise her far above his reach, and, while they imparted new depth and dignity to his love, to whisper that it was hopeless.\n\n‘I will keep my word, as I have pledged it to her,’ said Nicholas, manfully. ‘This is no common trust that I have to discharge, and I will perform the double duty that is imposed upon me most scrupulously and strictly. My secret feelings deserve no consideration in such a case as this, and they shall have none.’\n\nStill, there were the secret feelings in existence just the same, and in secret Nicholas rather encouraged them than otherwise; reasoning (if he reasoned at all) that there they could do no harm to anybody but himself, and that if he kept them to himself from a sense of duty, he had an additional right to entertain himself with them as a reward for his heroism.\n\nAll these thoughts, coupled with what he had seen that morning and the anticipation of his next visit, rendered him a very dull and abstracted companion; so much so, indeed, that Tim Linkinwater suspected he must have made the mistake of a figure somewhere, which was preying upon his mind, and seriously conjured him, if such were the case, to make a clean breast and scratch it out, rather than have his whole life embittered by the tortures of remorse.\n\nBut in reply to these considerate representations, and many others both from Tim and Mr. Frank, Nicholas could only be brought to state that he was never merrier in his life; and so went on all day, and so went towards home at night, still turning over and over again the same subjects, thinking over and over again the same things, and arriving over and over again at the same conclusions.\n\nIn this pensive, wayward, and uncertain state, people are apt to lounge and loiter without knowing why, to read placards on the walls with great attention and without the smallest idea of one word of their contents, and to stare most earnestly through shop-windows at things which they don’t see. It was thus that Nicholas found himself poring with the utmost interest over a large play-bill hanging outside a Minor Theatre which he had to pass on his way home, and reading a list of the actors and actresses who had promised to do honour to some approaching benefit, with as much gravity as if it had been a catalogue of the names of those ladies and gentlemen who stood highest upon the Book of Fate, and he had been looking anxiously for his own. He glanced at the top of the bill, with a smile at his own dulness, as he prepared to resume his walk, and there saw announced, in large letters with a large space between each of them, ‘Positively the last appearance of Mr. Vincent Crummles of Provincial Celebrity!!!’\n\n‘Nonsense!’ said Nicholas, turning back again. ‘It can’t be.’\n\nBut there it was. In one line by itself was an announcement of the first night of a new melodrama; in another line by itself was an announcement of the last six nights of an old one; a third line was devoted to the re-engagement of the unrivalled African Knife-swallower, who had kindly suffered himself to be prevailed upon to forego his country engagements for one week longer; a fourth line announced that Mr. Snittle Timberry, having recovered from his late severe indisposition, would have the honour of appearing that evening; a fifth line said that there were ‘Cheers, Tears, and Laughter!’ every night; a sixth, that that was positively the last appearance of Mr. Vincent Crummles of Provincial Celebrity.\n\n‘Surely it must be the same man,’ thought Nicholas. ‘There can’t be two Vincent Crummleses.’\n\nThe better to settle this question he referred to the bill again, and finding that there was a Baron in the first piece, and that Roberto (his son) was enacted by one Master Crummles, and Spaletro (his nephew) by one Master Percy Crummles--THEIR last appearances--and that, incidental to the piece, was a characteristic dance by the characters, and a castanet pas seul by the Infant Phenomenon--HER last appearance--he no longer entertained any doubt; and presenting himself at the stage-door, and sending in a scrap of paper with ‘Mr. Johnson’ written thereon in pencil, was presently conducted by a Robber, with a very large belt and buckle round his waist, and very large leather gauntlets on his hands, into the presence of his former manager.\n\nMr. Crummles was unfeignedly glad to see him, and starting up from before a small dressing-glass, with one very bushy eyebrow stuck on crooked over his left eye, and the fellow eyebrow and the calf of one of his legs in his hand, embraced him cordially; at the same time observing, that it would do Mrs. Crummles’s heart good to bid him goodbye before they went.\n\n‘You were always a favourite of hers, Johnson,’ said Crummles, ‘always were from the first. I was quite easy in my mind about you from that first day you dined with us. One that Mrs. Crummles took a fancy to, was sure to turn out right. Ah! Johnson, what a woman that is!’\n\n‘I am sincerely obliged to her for her kindness in this and all other respects,’ said Nicholas. ‘But where are you going, that you talk about bidding goodbye?’\n\n‘Haven’t you seen it in the papers?’ said Crummles, with some dignity.\n\n‘No,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘I wonder at that,’ said the manager. ‘It was among the varieties. I had the paragraph here somewhere--but I don’t know--oh, yes, here it is.’\n\nSo saying, Mr. Crummles, after pretending that he thought he must have lost it, produced a square inch of newspaper from the pocket of the pantaloons he wore in private life (which, together with the plain clothes of several other gentlemen, lay scattered about on a kind of dresser in the room), and gave it to Nicholas to read:\n\n‘The talented Vincent Crummles, long favourably known to fame as a country manager and actor of no ordinary pretensions, is about to cross the Atlantic on a histrionic expedition. Crummles is to be accompanied, we hear, by his lady and gifted family. We know no man superior to Crummles in his particular line of character, or one who, whether as a public or private individual, could carry with him the best wishes of a larger circle of friends. Crummles is certain to succeed.’\n\n‘Here’s another bit,’ said Mr. Crummles, handing over a still smaller scrap. ‘This is from the notices to correspondents, this one.’\n\nNicholas read it aloud. ‘“Philo-Dramaticus. Crummles, the country manager and actor, cannot be more than forty-three, or forty-four years of age. Crummles is NOT a Prussian, having been born at Chelsea.” Humph!’ said Nicholas, ‘that’s an odd paragraph.’\n\n‘Very,’ returned Crummles, scratching the side of his nose, and looking at Nicholas with an assumption of great unconcern. ‘I can’t think who puts these things in. I didn’t.’\n\nStill keeping his eye on Nicholas, Mr. Crummles shook his head twice or thrice with profound gravity, and remarking, that he could not for the life of him imagine how the newspapers found out the things they did, folded up the extracts and put them in his pocket again.\n\n‘I am astonished to hear this news,’ said Nicholas. ‘Going to America! You had no such thing in contemplation when I was with you.’\n\n‘No,’ replied Crummles, ‘I hadn’t then. The fact is that Mrs Crummles--most extraordinary woman, Johnson.’ Here he broke off and whispered something in his ear.\n\n‘Oh!’ said Nicholas, smiling. ‘The prospect of an addition to your family?’\n\n‘The seventh addition, Johnson,’ returned Mr. Crummles, solemnly. ‘I thought such a child as the Phenomenon must have been a closer; but it seems we are to have another. She is a very remarkable woman.’\n\n‘I congratulate you,’ said Nicholas, ‘and I hope this may prove a phenomenon too.’\n\n‘Why, it’s pretty sure to be something uncommon, I suppose,’ rejoined Mr. Crummles. ‘The talent of the other three is principally in combat and serious pantomime. I should like this one to have a turn for juvenile tragedy; I understand they want something of that sort in America very much. However, we must take it as it comes. Perhaps it may have a genius for the tight-rope. It may have any sort of genius, in short, if it takes after its mother, Johnson, for she is an universal genius; but, whatever its genius is, that genius shall be developed.’\n\nExpressing himself after these terms, Mr. Crummles put on his other eyebrow, and the calves of his legs, and then put on his legs, which were of a yellowish flesh-colour, and rather soiled about the knees, from frequent going down upon those joints, in curses, prayers, last struggles, and other strong passages.\n\nWhile the ex-manager completed his toilet, he informed Nicholas that as he should have a fair start in America from the proceeds of a tolerably good engagement which he had been fortunate enough to obtain, and as he and Mrs. Crummles could scarcely hope to act for ever (not being immortal, except in the breath of Fame and in a figurative sense) he had made up his mind to settle there permanently, in the hope of acquiring some land of his own which would support them in their old age, and which they could afterwards bequeath to their children. Nicholas, having highly commended the resolution, Mr. Crummles went on to impart such further intelligence relative to their mutual friends as he thought might prove interesting; informing Nicholas, among other things, that Miss Snevellicci was happily married to an affluent young wax-chandler who had supplied the theatre with candles, and that Mr. Lillyvick didn’t dare to say his soul was his own, such was the tyrannical sway of Mrs Lillyvick, who reigned paramount and supreme.\n\nNicholas responded to this confidence on the part of Mr. Crummles, by confiding to him his own name, situation, and prospects, and informing him, in as few general words as he could, of the circumstances which had led to their first acquaintance. After congratulating him with great heartiness on the improved state of his fortunes, Mr. Crummles gave him to understand that next morning he and his were to start for Liverpool, where the vessel lay which was to carry them from the shores of England, and that if Nicholas wished to take a last adieu of Mrs. Crummles, he must repair with him that night to a farewell supper, given in honour of the family at a neighbouring tavern; at which Mr. Snittle Timberry would preside, while the honours of the vice-chair would be sustained by the African Swallower.\n\nThe room being by this time very warm and somewhat crowded, in consequence of the influx of four gentlemen, who had just killed each other in the piece under representation, Nicholas accepted the invitation, and promised to return at the conclusion of the performances; preferring the cool air and twilight out of doors to the mingled perfume of gas, orange-peel, and gunpowder, which pervaded the hot and glaring theatre.\n\nHe availed himself of this interval to buy a silver snuff-box--the best his funds would afford--as a token of remembrance for Mr. Crummles, and having purchased besides a pair of ear-rings for Mrs. Crummles, a necklace for the Phenomenon, and a flaming shirt-pin for each of the young gentlemen, he refreshed himself with a walk, and returning a little after the appointed time, found the lights out, the theatre empty, the curtain raised for the night, and Mr. Crummles walking up and down the stage expecting his arrival.\n\n‘Timberry won’t be long,’ said Mr. Crummles. ‘He played the audience out tonight. He does a faithful black in the last piece, and it takes him a little longer to wash himself.’\n\n‘A very unpleasant line of character, I should think?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘No, I don’t know,’ replied Mr. Crummles; ‘it comes off easily enough, and there’s only the face and neck. We had a first-tragedy man in our company once, who, when he played Othello, used to black himself all over. But that’s feeling a part and going into it as if you meant it; it isn’t usual; more’s the pity.’\n\nMr. Snittle Timberry now appeared, arm-in-arm with the African Swallower, and, being introduced to Nicholas, raised his hat half a foot, and said he was proud to know him. The Swallower said the same, and looked and spoke remarkably like an Irishman.\n\n‘I see by the bills that you have been ill, sir,’ said Nicholas to Mr Timberry. ‘I hope you are none the worse for your exertions tonight?’\n\nMr. Timberry, in reply, shook his head with a gloomy air, tapped his chest several times with great significancy, and drawing his cloak more closely about him, said, ‘But no matter, no matter. Come!’\n\nIt is observable that when people upon the stage are in any strait involving the very last extremity of weakness and exhaustion, they invariably perform feats of strength requiring great ingenuity and muscular power. Thus, a wounded prince or bandit chief, who is bleeding to death and too faint to move, except to the softest music (and then only upon his hands and knees), shall be seen to approach a cottage door for aid in such a series of writhings and twistings, and with such curlings up of the legs, and such rollings over and over, and such gettings up and tumblings down again, as could never be achieved save by a very strong man skilled in posture-making. And so natural did this sort of performance come to Mr. Snittle Timberry, that on their way out of the theatre and towards the tavern where the supper was to be holden, he testified the severity of his recent indisposition and its wasting effects upon the nervous system, by a series of gymnastic performances which were the admiration of all witnesses.\n\n‘Why this is indeed a joy I had not looked for!’ said Mrs. Crummles, when Nicholas was presented.\n\n‘Nor I,’ replied Nicholas. ‘It is by a mere chance that I have this opportunity of seeing you, although I would have made a great exertion to have availed myself of it.’\n\n‘Here is one whom you know,’ said Mrs. Crummles, thrusting forward the Phenomenon in a blue gauze frock, extensively flounced, and trousers of the same; ‘and here another--and another,’ presenting the Master Crummleses. ‘And how is your friend, the faithful Digby?’\n\n‘Digby!’ said Nicholas, forgetting at the instant that this had been Smike’s theatrical name. ‘Oh yes. He’s quite--what am I saying?--he is very far from well.’\n\n‘How!’ exclaimed Mrs. Crummles, with a tragic recoil.\n\n‘I fear,’ said Nicholas, shaking his head, and making an attempt to smile, ‘that your better-half would be more struck with him now than ever.’\n\n‘What mean you?’ rejoined Mrs. Crummles, in her most popular manner. ‘Whence comes this altered tone?’\n\n‘I mean that a dastardly enemy of mine has struck at me through him, and that while he thinks to torture me, he inflicts on him such agonies of terror and suspense as--You will excuse me, I am sure,’ said Nicholas, checking himself. ‘I should never speak of this, and never do, except to those who know the facts, but for a moment I forgot myself.’\n\nWith this hasty apology Nicholas stooped down to salute the Phenomenon, and changed the subject; inwardly cursing his precipitation, and very much wondering what Mrs. Crummles must think of so sudden an explosion.\n\nThat lady seemed to think very little about it, for the supper being by this time on table, she gave her hand to Nicholas and repaired with a stately step to the left hand of Mr. Snittle Timberry. Nicholas had the honour to support her, and Mr. Crummles was placed upon the chairman’s right; the Phenomenon and the Master Crummleses sustained the vice.\n\nThe company amounted in number to some twenty-five or thirty, being composed of such members of the theatrical profession, then engaged or disengaged in London, as were numbered among the most intimate friends of Mr. and Mrs. Crummles. The ladies and gentlemen were pretty equally balanced; the expenses of the entertainment being defrayed by the latter, each of whom had the privilege of inviting one of the former as his guest.\n\nIt was upon the whole a very distinguished party, for independently of the lesser theatrical lights who clustered on this occasion round Mr. Snittle Timberry, there was a literary gentleman present who had dramatised in his time two hundred and forty-seven novels as fast as they had come out--some of them faster than they had come out--and who WAS a literary gentleman in consequence.\n\nThis gentleman sat on the left hand of Nicholas, to whom he was introduced by his friend the African Swallower, from the bottom of the table, with a high eulogium upon his fame and reputation.\n\n‘I am happy to know a gentleman of such great distinction,’ said Nicholas, politely.\n\n‘Sir,’ replied the wit, ‘you’re very welcome, I’m sure. The honour is reciprocal, sir, as I usually say when I dramatise a book. Did you ever hear a definition of fame, sir?’\n\n‘I have heard several,’ replied Nicholas, with a smile. ‘What is yours?’\n\n‘When I dramatise a book, sir,’ said the literary gentleman, ‘THAT’S fame. For its author.’\n\n‘Oh, indeed!’ rejoined Nicholas.\n\n‘That’s fame, sir,’ said the literary gentleman.\n\n‘So Richard Turpin, Tom King, and Jerry Abershaw have handed down to fame the names of those on whom they committed their most impudent robberies?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘I don’t know anything about that, sir,’ answered the literary gentleman.\n\n‘Shakespeare dramatised stories which had previously appeared in print, it is true,’ observed Nicholas.\n\n‘Meaning Bill, sir?’ said the literary gentleman. ‘So he did. Bill was an adapter, certainly, so he was--and very well he adapted too--considering.’\n\n‘I was about to say,’ rejoined Nicholas, ‘that Shakespeare derived some of his plots from old tales and legends in general circulation; but it seems to me, that some of the gentlemen of your craft, at the present day, have shot very far beyond him--’\n\n‘You’re quite right, sir,’ interrupted the literary gentleman, leaning back in his chair and exercising his toothpick. ‘Human intellect, sir, has progressed since his time, is progressing, will progress.’\n\n‘Shot beyond him, I mean,’ resumed Nicholas, ‘in quite another respect, for, whereas he brought within the magic circle of his genius, traditions peculiarly adapted for his purpose, and turned familiar things into constellations which should enlighten the world for ages, you drag within the magic circle of your dulness, subjects not at all adapted to the purposes of the stage, and debase as he exalted. For instance, you take the uncompleted books of living authors, fresh from their hands, wet from the press, cut, hack, and carve them to the powers and capacities of your actors, and the capability of your theatres, finish unfinished works, hastily and crudely vamp up ideas not yet worked out by their original projector, but which have doubtless cost him many thoughtful days and sleepless nights; by a comparison of incidents and dialogue, down to the very last word he may have written a fortnight before, do your utmost to anticipate his plot--all this without his permission, and against his will; and then, to crown the whole proceeding, publish in some mean pamphlet, an unmeaning farrago of garbled extracts from his work, to which your name as author, with the honourable distinction annexed, of having perpetrated a hundred other outrages of the same description. Now, show me the distinction between such pilfering as this, and picking a man’s pocket in the street: unless, indeed, it be, that the legislature has a regard for pocket-handkerchiefs, and leaves men’s brains, except when they are knocked out by violence, to take care of themselves.’\n\n‘Men must live, sir,’ said the literary gentleman, shrugging his shoulders.\n\n‘That would be an equally fair plea in both cases,’ replied Nicholas; ‘but if you put it upon that ground, I have nothing more to say, than, that if I were a writer of books, and you a thirsty dramatist, I would rather pay your tavern score for six months, large as it might be, than have a niche in the Temple of Fame with you for the humblest corner of my pedestal, through six hundred generations.’\n\nThe conversation threatened to take a somewhat angry tone when it had arrived thus far, but Mrs. Crummles opportunely interposed to prevent its leading to any violent outbreak, by making some inquiries of the literary gentleman relative to the plots of the six new pieces which he had written by contract to introduce the African Knife-swallower in his various unrivalled performances. This speedily engaged him in an animated conversation with that lady, in the interest of which, all recollection of his recent discussion with Nicholas very quickly evaporated.\n\nThe board being now clear of the more substantial articles of food, and punch, wine, and spirits being placed upon it and handed about, the guests, who had been previously conversing in little groups of three or four, gradually fell off into a dead silence, while the majority of those present glanced from time to time at Mr. Snittle Timberry, and the bolder spirits did not even hesitate to strike the table with their knuckles, and plainly intimate their expectations, by uttering such encouragements as ‘Now, Tim,’ ‘Wake up, Mr. Chairman,’ ‘All charged, sir, and waiting for a toast,’ and so forth.\n\nTo these remonstrances Mr. Timberry deigned no other rejoinder than striking his chest and gasping for breath, and giving many other indications of being still the victim of indisposition--for a man must not make himself too cheap either on the stage or off--while Mr. Crummles, who knew full well that he would be the subject of the forthcoming toast, sat gracefully in his chair with his arm thrown carelessly over the back, and now and then lifted his glass to his mouth and drank a little punch, with the same air with which he was accustomed to take long draughts of nothing, out of the pasteboard goblets in banquet scenes.\n\nAt length Mr. Snittle Timberry rose in the most approved attitude, with one hand in the breast of his waistcoat and the other on the nearest snuff-box, and having been received with great enthusiasm, proposed, with abundance of quotations, his friend Mr. Vincent Crummles: ending a pretty long speech by extending his right hand on one side and his left on the other, and severally calling upon Mr. and Mrs. Crummles to grasp the same. This done, Mr. Vincent Crummles returned thanks, and that done, the African Swallower proposed Mrs. Vincent Crummles, in affecting terms. Then were heard loud moans and sobs from Mrs. Crummles and the ladies, despite of which that heroic woman insisted upon returning thanks herself, which she did, in a manner and in a speech which has never been surpassed and seldom equalled. It then became the duty of Mr. Snittle Timberry to give the young Crummleses, which he did; after which Mr. Vincent Crummles, as their father, addressed the company in a supplementary speech, enlarging on their virtues, amiabilities, and excellences, and wishing that they were the sons and daughter of every lady and gentleman present. These solemnities having been succeeded by a decent interval, enlivened by musical and other entertainments, Mr. Crummles proposed that ornament of the profession, the African Swallower, his very dear friend, if he would allow him to call him so; which liberty (there being no particular reason why he should not allow it) the African Swallower graciously permitted. The literary gentleman was then about to be drunk, but it being discovered that he had been drunk for some time in another acceptation of the term, and was then asleep on the stairs, the intention was abandoned, and the honour transferred to the ladies. Finally, after a very long sitting, Mr Snittle Timberry vacated the chair, and the company with many adieux and embraces dispersed.\n\nNicholas waited to the last to give his little presents. When he had said goodbye all round and came to Mr. Crummles, he could not but mark the difference between their present separation and their parting at Portsmouth. Not a jot of his theatrical manner remained; he put out his hand with an air which, if he could have summoned it at will, would have made him the best actor of his day in homely parts, and when Nicholas shook it with the warmth he honestly felt, appeared thoroughly melted.\n\n‘We were a very happy little company, Johnson,’ said poor Crummles. ‘You and I never had a word. I shall be very glad tomorrow morning to think that I saw you again, but now I almost wish you hadn’t come.’\n\nNicholas was about to return a cheerful reply, when he was greatly disconcerted by the sudden apparition of Mrs. Grudden, who it seemed had declined to attend the supper in order that she might rise earlier in the morning, and who now burst out of an adjoining bedroom, habited in very extraordinary white robes; and throwing her arms about his neck, hugged him with great affection.\n\n‘What! Are you going too?’ said Nicholas, submitting with as good a grace as if she had been the finest young creature in the world.\n\n‘Going?’ returned Mrs. Grudden. ‘Lord ha’ mercy, what do you think they’d do without me?’\n\nNicholas submitted to another hug with even a better grace than before, if that were possible, and waving his hat as cheerfully as he could, took farewell of the Vincent Crummleses.\n\n# CHAPTER 49\n\nChronicles the further Proceedings of the Nickleby Family, and the Sequel of the Adventure of the Gentleman in the Small-clothes\nWhile Nicholas, absorbed in the one engrossing subject of interest which had recently opened upon him, occupied his leisure hours with thoughts of Madeline Bray, and in execution of the commissions which the anxiety of brother Charles in her behalf imposed upon him, saw her again and again, and each time with greater danger to his peace of mind and a more weakening effect upon the lofty resolutions he had formed, Mrs. Nickleby and Kate continued to live in peace and quiet, agitated by no other cares than those which were connected with certain harassing proceedings taken by Mr. Snawley for the recovery of his son, and their anxiety for Smike himself, whose health, long upon the wane, began to be so much affected by apprehension and uncertainty as sometimes to occasion both them and Nicholas considerable uneasiness, and even alarm.\n\nIt was no complaint or murmur on the part of the poor fellow himself that thus disturbed them. Ever eager to be employed in such slight services as he could render, and always anxious to repay his benefactors with cheerful and happy looks, less friendly eyes might have seen in him no cause for any misgiving. But there were times, and often too, when the sunken eye was too bright, the hollow cheek too flushed, the breath too thick and heavy in its course, the frame too feeble and exhausted, to escape their regard and notice.\n\nThere is a dread disease which so prepares its victim, as it were, for death; which so refines it of its grosser aspect, and throws around familiar looks unearthly indications of the coming change; a dread disease, in which the struggle between soul and body is so gradual, quiet, and solemn, and the result so sure, that day by day, and grain by grain, the mortal part wastes and withers away, so that the spirit grows light and sanguine with its lightening load, and, feeling immortality at hand, deems it but a new term of mortal life; a disease in which death and life are so strangely blended, that death takes the glow and hue of life, and life the gaunt and grisly form of death; a disease which medicine never cured, wealth never warded off, or poverty could boast exemption from; which sometimes moves in giant strides, and sometimes at a tardy sluggish pace, but, slow or quick, is ever sure and certain.\n\nIt was with some faint reference in his own mind to this disorder, though he would by no means admit it, even to himself, that Nicholas had already carried his faithful companion to a physician of great repute. There was no cause for immediate alarm, he said. There were no present symptoms which could be deemed conclusive. The constitution had been greatly tried and injured in childhood, but still it MIGHT not be--and that was all.\n\nBut he seemed to grow no worse, and, as it was not difficult to find a reason for these symptoms of illness in the shock and agitation he had recently undergone, Nicholas comforted himself with the hope that his poor friend would soon recover. This hope his mother and sister shared with him; and as the object of their joint solicitude seemed to have no uneasiness or despondency for himself, but each day answered with a quiet smile that he felt better than he had upon the day before, their fears abated, and the general happiness was by degrees restored.\n\nMany and many a time in after years did Nicholas look back to this period of his life, and tread again the humble quiet homely scenes that rose up as of old before him. Many and many a time, in the twilight of a summer evening, or beside the flickering winter’s fire--but not so often or so sadly then--would his thoughts wander back to these old days, and dwell with a pleasant sorrow upon every slight remembrance which they brought crowding home. The little room in which they had so often sat long after it was dark, figuring such happy futures; Kate’s cheerful voice and merry laugh; how, if she were from home, they used to sit and watch for her return scarcely breaking silence but to say how dull it seemed without her; the glee with which poor Smike would start from the darkened corner where he used to sit, and hurry to admit her, and the tears they often saw upon his face, half wondering to see them too, and he so pleased and happy; every little incident, and even slight words and looks of those old days little heeded then, but well remembered when busy cares and trials were quite forgotten, came fresh and thick before him many and many a time, and, rustling above the dusty growth of years, came back green boughs of yesterday.\n\nBut there were other persons associated with these recollections, and many changes came about before they had being. A necessary reflection for the purposes of these adventures, which at once subside into their accustomed train, and shunning all flighty anticipations or wayward wanderings, pursue their steady and decorous course.\n\nIf the brothers Cheeryble, as they found Nicholas worthy of trust and confidence, bestowed upon him every day some new and substantial mark of kindness, they were not less mindful of those who depended on him. Various little presents to Mrs. Nickleby, always of the very things they most required, tended in no slight degree to the improvement and embellishment of the cottage. Kate’s little store of trinkets became quite dazzling; and for company! If brother Charles and brother Ned failed to look in for at least a few minutes every Sunday, or one evening in the week, there was Mr. Tim Linkinwater (who had never made half-a-dozen other acquaintances in all his life, and who took such delight in his new friends as no words can express) constantly coming and going in his evening walks, and stopping to rest; while Mr. Frank Cheeryble happened, by some strange conjunction of circumstances, to be passing the door on some business or other at least three nights in the week.\n\n‘He is the most attentive young man I ever saw, Kate,’ said Mrs. Nickleby to her daughter one evening, when this last-named gentleman had been the subject of the worthy lady’s eulogium for some time, and Kate had sat perfectly silent.\n\n‘Attentive, mama!’ rejoined Kate.\n\n‘Bless my heart, Kate!’ cried Mrs. Nickleby, with her wonted suddenness, ‘what a colour you have got; why, you’re quite flushed!’\n\n‘Oh, mama! what strange things you fancy!’\n\n‘It wasn’t fancy, Kate, my dear, I’m certain of that,’ returned her mother. ‘However, it’s gone now at any rate, so it don’t much matter whether it was or not. What was it we were talking about? Oh! Mr. Frank. I never saw such attention in MY life, never.’\n\n‘Surely you are not serious,’ returned Kate, colouring again; and this time beyond all dispute.\n\n‘Not serious!’ returned Mrs. Nickleby; ‘why shouldn’t I be serious? I’m sure I never was more serious. I will say that his politeness and attention to me is one of the most becoming, gratifying, pleasant things I have seen for a very long time. You don’t often meet with such behaviour in young men, and it strikes one more when one does meet with it.’\n\n‘Oh! attention to YOU, mama,’ rejoined Kate quickly--‘oh yes.’\n\n‘Dear me, Kate,’ retorted Mrs. Nickleby, ‘what an extraordinary girl you are! Was it likely I should be talking of his attention to anybody else? I declare I’m quite sorry to think he should be in love with a German lady, that I am.’\n\n‘He said very positively that it was no such thing, mama,’ returned Kate. ‘Don’t you remember his saying so that very first night he came here? Besides,’ she added, in a more gentle tone, ‘why should WE be sorry if it is the case? What is it to us, mama?’\n\n‘Nothing to US, Kate, perhaps,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, emphatically; ‘but something to ME, I confess. I like English people to be thorough English people, and not half English and half I don’t know what. I shall tell him point-blank next time he comes, that I wish he would marry one of his own country-women; and see what he says to that.’\n\n‘Pray don’t think of such a thing, mama,’ returned Kate, hastily; ‘not for the world. Consider. How very--’\n\n‘Well, my dear, how very what?’ said Mrs. Nickleby, opening her eyes in great astonishment.\n\nBefore Kate had returned any reply, a queer little double knock announced that Miss La Creevy had called to see them; and when Miss La Creevy presented herself, Mrs. Nickleby, though strongly disposed to be argumentative on the previous question, forgot all about it in a gush of supposes about the coach she had come by; supposing that the man who drove must have been either the man in the shirt-sleeves or the man with the black eye; that whoever he was, he hadn’t found that parasol she left inside last week; that no doubt they had stopped a long while at the Halfway House, coming down; or that perhaps being full, they had come straight on; and, lastly, that they, surely, must have passed Nicholas on the road.\n\n‘I saw nothing of him,’ answered Miss La Creevy; ‘but I saw that dear old soul Mr. Linkinwater.’\n\n‘Taking his evening walk, and coming on to rest here, before he turns back to the city, I’ll be bound!’ said Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘I should think he was,’ returned Miss La Creevy; ‘especially as young Mr. Cheeryble was with him.’\n\n‘Surely that is no reason why Mr. Linkinwater should be coming here,’ said Kate.\n\n‘Why I think it is, my dear,’ said Miss La Creevy. ‘For a young man, Mr Frank is not a very great walker; and I observe that he generally falls tired, and requires a good long rest, when he has come as far as this. But where is my friend?’ said the little woman, looking about, after having glanced slyly at Kate. ‘He has not been run away with again, has he?’\n\n‘Ah! where is Mr. Smike?’ said Mrs. Nickleby; ‘he was here this instant.’\n\nUpon further inquiry, it turned out, to the good lady’s unbounded astonishment, that Smike had, that moment, gone upstairs to bed.\n\n‘Well now,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘he is the strangest creature! Last Tuesday--was it Tuesday? Yes, to be sure it was; you recollect, Kate, my dear, the very last time young Mr. Cheeryble was here--last Tuesday night he went off in just the same strange way, at the very moment the knock came to the door. It cannot be that he don’t like company, because he is always fond of people who are fond of Nicholas, and I am sure young Mr Cheeryble is. And the strangest thing is, that he does not go to bed; therefore it cannot be because he is tired. I know he doesn’t go to bed, because my room is the next one, and when I went upstairs last Tuesday, hours after him, I found that he had not even taken his shoes off; and he had no candle, so he must have sat moping in the dark all the time. Now, upon my word,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘when I come to think of it, that’s very extraordinary!’\n\nAs the hearers did not echo this sentiment, but remained profoundly silent, either as not knowing what to say, or as being unwilling to interrupt, Mrs. Nickleby pursued the thread of her discourse after her own fashion.\n\n‘I hope,’ said that lady, ‘that this unaccountable conduct may not be the beginning of his taking to his bed and living there all his life, like the Thirsty Woman of Tutbury, or the Cock-lane Ghost, or some of those extraordinary creatures. One of them had some connection with our family. I forget, without looking back to some old letters I have upstairs, whether it was my great-grandfather who went to school with the Cock-lane Ghost, or the Thirsty Woman of Tutbury who went to school with my grandmother. Miss La Creevy, you know, of course. Which was it that didn’t mind what the clergyman said? The Cock-lane Ghost or the Thirsty Woman of Tutbury?’\n\n‘The Cock-lane Ghost, I believe.’\n\n‘Then I have no doubt,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘that it was with him my great-grandfather went to school; for I know the master of his school was a dissenter, and that would, in a great measure, account for the Cock-lane Ghost’s behaving in such an improper manner to the clergyman when he grew up. Ah! Train up a Ghost--child, I mean--’\n\nAny further reflections on this fruitful theme were abruptly cut short by the arrival of Tim Linkinwater and Mr. Frank Cheeryble; in the hurry of receiving whom, Mrs. Nickleby speedily lost sight of everything else.\n\n‘I am so sorry Nicholas is not at home,’ said Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Kate, my dear, you must be both Nicholas and yourself.’\n\n‘Miss Nickleby need be but herself,’ said Frank. ‘I--if I may venture to say so--oppose all change in her.’\n\n‘Then at all events she shall press you to stay,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Mr. Linkinwater says ten minutes, but I cannot let you go so soon; Nicholas would be very much vexed, I am sure. Kate, my dear!’\n\nIn obedience to a great number of nods, and winks, and frowns of extra significance, Kate added her entreaties that the visitors would remain; but it was observable that she addressed them exclusively to Tim Linkinwater; and there was, besides, a certain embarrassment in her manner, which, although it was as far from impairing its graceful character as the tinge it communicated to her cheek was from diminishing her beauty, was obvious at a glance even to Mrs. Nickleby. Not being of a very speculative character, however, save under circumstances when her speculations could be put into words and uttered aloud, that discreet matron attributed the emotion to the circumstance of her daughter’s not happening to have her best frock on: ‘though I never saw her look better, certainly,’ she reflected at the same time. Having settled the question in this way, and being most complacently satisfied that in this, and in all other instances, her conjecture could not fail to be the right one, Mrs. Nickleby dismissed it from her thoughts, and inwardly congratulated herself on being so shrewd and knowing.\n\nNicholas did not come home nor did Smike reappear; but neither circumstance, to say the truth, had any great effect upon the little party, who were all in the best humour possible. Indeed, there sprung up quite a flirtation between Miss La Creevy and Tim Linkinwater, who said a thousand jocose and facetious things, and became, by degrees, quite gallant, not to say tender. Little Miss La Creevy, on her part, was in high spirits, and rallied Tim on having remained a bachelor all his life with so much success, that Tim was actually induced to declare, that if he could get anybody to have him, he didn’t know but what he might change his condition even yet. Miss La Creevy earnestly recommended a lady she knew, who would exactly suit Mr. Linkinwater, and had a very comfortable property of her own; but this latter qualification had very little effect upon Tim, who manfully protested that fortune would be no object with him, but that true worth and cheerfulness of disposition were what a man should look for in a wife, and that if he had these, he could find money enough for the moderate wants of both. This avowal was considered so honourable to Tim, that neither Mrs. Nickleby nor Miss La Creevy could sufficiently extol it; and stimulated by their praises, Tim launched out into several other declarations also manifesting the disinterestedness of his heart, and a great devotion to the fair sex: which were received with no less approbation. This was done and said with a comical mixture of jest and earnest, and, leading to a great amount of laughter, made them very merry indeed.\n\nKate was commonly the life and soul of the conversation at home; but she was more silent than usual upon this occasion (perhaps because Tim and Miss La Creevy engrossed so much of it), and, keeping aloof from the talkers, sat at the window watching the shadows as the evening closed in, and enjoying the quiet beauty of the night, which seemed to have scarcely less attractions to Frank, who first lingered near, and then sat down beside, her. No doubt, there are a great many things to be said appropriate to a summer evening, and no doubt they are best said in a low voice, as being most suitable to the peace and serenity of the hour; long pauses, too, at times, and then an earnest word or so, and then another interval of silence which, somehow, does not seem like silence either, and perhaps now and then a hasty turning away of the head, or drooping of the eyes towards the ground, all these minor circumstances, with a disinclination to have candles introduced and a tendency to confuse hours with minutes, are doubtless mere influences of the time, as many lovely lips can clearly testify. Neither is there the slightest reason why Mrs. Nickleby should have expressed surprise when, candles being at length brought in, Kate’s bright eyes were unable to bear the light which obliged her to avert her face, and even to leave the room for some short time; because, when one has sat in the dark so long, candles ARE dazzling, and nothing can be more strictly natural than that such results should be produced, as all well-informed young people know. For that matter, old people know it too, or did know it once, but they forget these things sometimes, and more’s the pity.\n\nThe good lady’s surprise, however, did not end here. It was greatly increased when it was discovered that Kate had not the least appetite for supper: a discovery so alarming that there is no knowing in what unaccountable efforts of oratory Mrs. Nickleby’s apprehensions might have been vented, if the general attention had not been attracted, at the moment, by a very strange and uncommon noise, proceeding, as the pale and trembling servant girl affirmed, and as everybody’s sense of hearing seemed to affirm also, ‘right down’ the chimney of the adjoining room.\n\nIt being quite plain to the comprehension of all present that, however extraordinary and improbable it might appear, the noise did nevertheless proceed from the chimney in question; and the noise (which was a strange compound of various shuffling, sliding, rumbling, and struggling sounds, all muffled by the chimney) still continuing, Frank Cheeryble caught up a candle, and Tim Linkinwater the tongs, and they would have very quickly ascertained the cause of this disturbance if Mrs. Nickleby had not been taken very faint, and declined being left behind, on any account. This produced a short remonstrance, which terminated in their all proceeding to the troubled chamber in a body, excepting only Miss La Creevy, who, as the servant girl volunteered a confession of having been subject to fits in her infancy, remained with her to give the alarm and apply restoratives, in case of extremity.\n\nAdvancing to the door of the mysterious apartment, they were not a little surprised to hear a human voice, chanting with a highly elaborated expression of melancholy, and in tones of suffocation which a human voice might have produced from under five or six feather-beds of the best quality, the once popular air of ‘Has she then failed in her truth, the beautiful maid I adore?’ Nor, on bursting into the room without demanding a parley, was their astonishment lessened by the discovery that these romantic sounds certainly proceeded from the throat of some man up the chimney, of whom nothing was visible but a pair of legs, which were dangling above the grate; apparently feeling, with extreme anxiety, for the top bar whereon to effect a landing.\n\nA sight so unusual and unbusiness-like as this, completely paralysed Tim Linkinwater, who, after one or two gentle pinches at the stranger’s ankles, which were productive of no effect, stood clapping the tongs together, as if he were sharpening them for another assault, and did nothing else.\n\n‘This must be some drunken fellow,’ said Frank. ‘No thief would announce his presence thus.’\n\nAs he said this, with great indignation, he raised the candle to obtain a better view of the legs, and was darting forward to pull them down with very little ceremony, when Mrs. Nickleby, clasping her hands, uttered a sharp sound, something between a scream and an exclamation, and demanded to know whether the mysterious limbs were not clad in small-clothes and grey worsted stockings, or whether her eyes had deceived her.\n\n‘Yes,’ cried Frank, looking a little closer. ‘Small-clothes certainly, and--and--rough grey stockings, too. Do you know him, ma’am?’\n\n‘Kate, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, deliberately sitting herself down in a chair with that sort of desperate resignation which seemed to imply that now matters had come to a crisis, and all disguise was useless, ‘you will have the goodness, my love, to explain precisely how this matter stands. I have given him no encouragement--none whatever--not the least in the world. You know that, my dear, perfectly well. He was very respectful, exceedingly respectful, when he declared, as you were a witness to; still at the same time, if I am to be persecuted in this way, if vegetable what’s-his-names and all kinds of garden-stuff are to strew my path out of doors, and gentlemen are to come choking up our chimneys at home, I really don’t know--upon my word I do NOT know--what is to become of me. It’s a very hard case--harder than anything I was ever exposed to, before I married your poor dear papa, though I suffered a good deal of annoyance then--but that, of course, I expected, and made up my mind for. When I was not nearly so old as you, my dear, there was a young gentleman who sat next us at church, who used, almost every Sunday, to cut my name in large letters in the front of his pew while the sermon was going on. It was gratifying, of course, naturally so, but still it was an annoyance, because the pew was in a very conspicuous place, and he was several times publicly taken out by the beadle for doing it. But that was nothing to this. This is a great deal worse, and a great deal more embarrassing. I would rather, Kate, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, with great solemnity, and an effusion of tears: ‘I would rather, I declare, have been a pig-faced lady, than be exposed to such a life as this!’\n\nFrank Cheeryble and Tim Linkinwater looked, in irrepressible astonishment, first at each other and then at Kate, who felt that some explanation was necessary, but who, between her terror at the apparition of the legs, her fear lest their owner should be smothered, and her anxiety to give the least ridiculous solution of the mystery that it was capable of bearing, was quite unable to utter a single word.\n\n‘He gives me great pain,’ continued Mrs. Nickleby, drying her eyes, ‘great pain; but don’t hurt a hair of his head, I beg. On no account hurt a hair of his head.’\n\nIt would not, under existing circumstances, have been quite so easy to hurt a hair of the gentleman’s head as Mrs. Nickleby seemed to imagine, inasmuch as that part of his person was some feet up the chimney, which was by no means a wide one. But, as all this time he had never left off singing about the bankruptcy of the beautiful maid in respect of truth, and now began not only to croak very feebly, but to kick with great violence as if respiration became a task of difficulty, Frank Cheeryble, without further hesitation, pulled at the shorts and worsteds with such heartiness as to bring him floundering into the room with greater precipitation than he had quite calculated upon.\n\n‘Oh! yes, yes,’ said Kate, directly the whole figure of this singular visitor appeared in this abrupt manner. ‘I know who it is. Pray don’t be rough with him. Is he hurt? I hope not. Oh, pray see if he is hurt.’\n\n‘He is not, I assure you,’ replied Frank, handling the object of his surprise, after this appeal, with sudden tenderness and respect. ‘He is not hurt in the least.’\n\n‘Don’t let him come any nearer,’ said Kate, retiring as far as she could.\n\n‘Oh, no, he shall not,’ rejoined Frank. ‘You see I have him secure here. But may I ask you what this means, and whether you expected this old gentleman?’\n\n‘Oh, no,’ said Kate, ‘of course not; but he--mama does not think so, I believe--but he is a mad gentleman who has escaped from the next house, and must have found an opportunity of secreting himself here.’\n\n‘Kate,’ interposed Mrs. Nickleby with severe dignity, ‘I am surprised at you.’\n\n‘Dear mama,’ Kate gently remonstrated.\n\n‘I am surprised at you,’ repeated Mrs. Nickleby; ‘upon my word, Kate, I am quite astonished that you should join the persecutors of this unfortunate gentleman, when you know very well that they have the basest designs upon his property, and that that is the whole secret of it. It would be much kinder of you, Kate, to ask Mr. Linkinwater or Mr. Cheeryble to interfere in his behalf, and see him righted. You ought not to allow your feelings to influence you; it’s not right, very far from it. What should my feelings be, do you suppose? If anybody ought to be indignant, who is it? I, of course, and very properly so. Still, at the same time, I wouldn’t commit such an injustice for the world. No,’ continued Mrs Nickleby, drawing herself up, and looking another way with a kind of bashful stateliness; ‘this gentleman will understand me when I tell him that I repeat the answer I gave him the other day; that I always will repeat it, though I do believe him to be sincere when I find him placing himself in such dreadful situations on my account; and that I request him to have the goodness to go away directly, or it will be impossible to keep his behaviour a secret from my son Nicholas. I am obliged to him, very much obliged to him, but I cannot listen to his addresses for a moment. It’s quite impossible.’\n\nWhile this address was in course of delivery, the old gentleman, with his nose and cheeks embellished with large patches of soot, sat upon the ground with his arms folded, eyeing the spectators in profound silence, and with a very majestic demeanour. He did not appear to take the smallest notice of what Mrs. Nickleby said, but when she ceased to speak he honoured her with a long stare, and inquired if she had quite finished.\n\n‘I have nothing more to say,’ replied that lady modestly. ‘I really cannot say anything more.’\n\n‘Very good,’ said the old gentleman, raising his voice, ‘then bring in the bottled lightning, a clean tumbler, and a corkscrew.’\n\nNobody executing this order, the old gentleman, after a short pause, raised his voice again and demanded a thunder sandwich. This article not being forthcoming either, he requested to be served with a fricassee of boot-tops and goldfish sauce, and then laughing heartily, gratified his hearers with a very long, very loud, and most melodious bellow.\n\nBut still Mrs. Nickleby, in reply to the significant looks of all about her, shook her head as though to assure them that she saw nothing whatever in all this, unless, indeed, it were a slight degree of eccentricity. She might have remained impressed with these opinions down to the latest moment of her life, but for a slight train of circumstances, which, trivial as they were, altered the whole complexion of the case.\n\nIt happened that Miss La Creevy, finding her patient in no very threatening condition, and being strongly impelled by curiosity to see what was going forward, bustled into the room while the old gentleman was in the very act of bellowing. It happened, too, that the instant the old gentleman saw her, he stopped short, skipped suddenly on his feet, and fell to kissing his hand violently: a change of demeanour which almost terrified the little portrait painter out of her senses, and caused her to retreat behind Tim Linkinwater with the utmost expedition.\n\n‘Aha!’ cried the old gentleman, folding his hands, and squeezing them with great force against each other. ‘I see her now; I see her now! My love, my life, my bride, my peerless beauty. She is come at last--at last--and all is gas and gaiters!’\n\nMrs. Nickleby looked rather disconcerted for a moment, but immediately recovering, nodded to Miss La Creevy and the other spectators several times, and frowned, and smiled gravely, giving them to understand that she saw where the mistake was, and would set it all to rights in a minute or two.\n\n‘She is come!’ said the old gentleman, laying his hand upon his heart. ‘Cormoran and Blunderbore! She is come! All the wealth I have is hers if she will take me for her slave. Where are grace, beauty, and blandishments, like those? In the Empress of Madagascar? No. In the Queen of Diamonds? No. In Mrs. Rowland, who every morning bathes in Kalydor for nothing? No. Melt all these down into one, with the three Graces, the nine Muses, and fourteen biscuit-bakers’ daughters from Oxford Street, and make a woman half as lovely. Pho! I defy you.’\n\nAfter uttering this rhapsody, the old gentleman snapped his fingers twenty or thirty times, and then subsided into an ecstatic contemplation of Miss La Creevy’s charms. This affording Mrs. Nickleby a favourable opportunity of explanation, she went about it straight.\n\n‘I am sure,’ said the worthy lady, with a prefatory cough, ‘that it’s a great relief, under such trying circumstances as these, to have anybody else mistaken for me--a very great relief; and it’s a circumstance that never occurred before, although I have several times been mistaken for my daughter Kate. I have no doubt the people were very foolish, and perhaps ought to have known better, but still they did take me for her, and of course that was no fault of mine, and it would be very hard indeed if I was to be made responsible for it. However, in this instance, of course, I must feel that I should do exceedingly wrong if I suffered anybody--especially anybody that I am under great obligations to--to be made uncomfortable on my account. And therefore I think it my duty to tell that gentleman that he is mistaken, that I am the lady who he was told by some impertinent person was niece to the Council of Paving-stones, and that I do beg and entreat of him to go quietly away, if it’s only for,’ here Mrs. Nickleby simpered and hesitated, ‘for MY sake.’\n\nIt might have been expected that the old gentleman would have been penetrated to the heart by the delicacy and condescension of this appeal, and that he would at least have returned a courteous and suitable reply. What, then, was the shock which Mrs. Nickleby received, when, accosting HER in the most unmistakable manner, he replied in a loud and sonourous voice: ‘Avaunt! Cat!’\n\n‘Sir!’ cried Mrs. Nickleby, in a faint tone.\n\n‘Cat!’ repeated the old gentleman. ‘Puss, Kit, Tit, Grimalkin, Tabby, Brindle! Whoosh!’ with which last sound, uttered in a hissing manner between his teeth, the old gentleman swung his arms violently round and round, and at the same time alternately advanced on Mrs. Nickleby, and retreated from her, in that species of savage dance with which boys on market-days may be seen to frighten pigs, sheep, and other animals, when they give out obstinate indications of turning down a wrong street.\n\nMrs. Nickleby wasted no words, but uttered an exclamation of horror and surprise, and immediately fainted away.\n\n‘I’ll attend to mama,’ said Kate, hastily; ‘I am not at all frightened. But pray take him away: pray take him away!’\n\nFrank was not at all confident of his power of complying with this request, until he bethought himself of the stratagem of sending Miss La Creevy on a few paces in advance, and urging the old gentleman to follow her. It succeeded to a miracle; and he went away in a rapture of admiration, strongly guarded by Tim Linkinwater on one side, and Frank himself on the other.\n\n‘Kate,’ murmured Mrs. Nickleby, reviving when the coast was clear, ‘is he gone?’\n\nShe was assured that he was.\n\n‘I shall never forgive myself, Kate,’ said Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Never! That gentleman has lost his senses, and I am the unhappy cause.’\n\n‘YOU the cause!’ said Kate, greatly astonished.\n\n‘I, my love,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby, with a desperate calmness. ‘You saw what he was the other day; you see what he is now. I told your brother, weeks and weeks ago, Kate, that I hoped a disappointment might not be too much for him. You see what a wreck he is. Making allowance for his being a little flighty, you know how rationally, and sensibly, and honourably he talked, when we saw him in the garden. You have heard the dreadful nonsense he has been guilty of this night, and the manner in which he has gone on with that poor unfortunate little old maid. Can anybody doubt how all this has been brought about?’\n\n‘I should scarcely think they could,’ said Kate mildly.\n\n‘I should scarcely think so, either,’ rejoined her mother. ‘Well! if I am the unfortunate cause of this, I have the satisfaction of knowing that I am not to blame. I told Nicholas, I said to him, “Nicholas, my dear, we should be very careful how we proceed.” He would scarcely hear me. If the matter had only been properly taken up at first, as I wished it to be! But you are both of you so like your poor papa. However, I have MY consolation, and that should be enough for me!’\n\nWashing her hands, thus, of all responsibility under this head, past, present, or to come, Mrs. Nickleby kindly added that she hoped her children might never have greater cause to reproach themselves than she had, and prepared herself to receive the escort, who soon returned with the intelligence that the old gentleman was safely housed, and that they found his custodians, who had been making merry with some friends, wholly ignorant of his absence.\n\nQuiet being again restored, a delicious half-hour--so Frank called it, in the course of subsequent conversation with Tim Linkinwater as they were walking home--was spent in conversation, and Tim’s watch at length apprising him that it was high time to depart, the ladies were left alone, though not without many offers on the part of Frank to remain until Nicholas arrived, no matter what hour of the night it might be, if, after the late neighbourly irruption, they entertained the least fear of being left to themselves. As their freedom from all further apprehension, however, left no pretext for his insisting on mounting guard, he was obliged to abandon the citadel, and to retire with the trusty Tim.\n\nNearly three hours of silence passed away. Kate blushed to find, when Nicholas returned, how long she had been sitting alone, occupied with her own thoughts.\n\n‘I really thought it had not been half an hour,’ she said.\n\n‘They must have been pleasant thoughts, Kate,’ rejoined Nicholas gaily, ‘to make time pass away like that. What were they now?’\n\nKate was confused; she toyed with some trifle on the table, looked up and smiled, looked down and dropped a tear.\n\n‘Why, Kate,’ said Nicholas, drawing his sister towards him and kissing her, ‘let me see your face. No? Ah! that was but a glimpse; that’s scarcely fair. A longer look than that, Kate. Come--and I’ll read your thoughts for you.’\n\nThere was something in this proposition, albeit it was said without the slightest consciousness or application, which so alarmed his sister, that Nicholas laughingly changed the subject to domestic matters, and thus gathered, by degrees, as they left the room and went upstairs together, how lonely Smike had been all night--and by very slow degrees, too; for on this subject also, Kate seemed to speak with some reluctance.\n\n‘Poor fellow,’ said Nicholas, tapping gently at his door, ‘what can be the cause of all this?’\n\nKate was hanging on her brother’s arm. The door being quickly opened, she had not time to disengage herself, before Smike, very pale and haggard, and completely dressed, confronted them.\n\n‘And have you not been to bed?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘N--n--no,’ was the reply.\n\nNicholas gently detained his sister, who made an effort to retire; and asked, ‘Why not?’\n\n‘I could not sleep,’ said Smike, grasping the hand which his friend extended to him.\n\n‘You are not well?’ rejoined Nicholas.\n\n‘I am better, indeed. A great deal better,’ said Smike quickly.\n\n‘Then why do you give way to these fits of melancholy?’ inquired Nicholas, in his kindest manner; ‘or why not tell us the cause? You grow a different creature, Smike.’\n\n‘I do; I know I do,’ he replied. ‘I will tell you the reason one day, but not now. I hate myself for this; you are all so good and kind. But I cannot help it. My heart is very full; you do not know how full it is.’\n\nHe wrung Nicholas’s hand before he released it; and glancing, for a moment, at the brother and sister as they stood together, as if there were something in their strong affection which touched him very deeply, withdrew into his chamber, and was soon the only watcher under that quiet roof.\n\nInvolves a serious Catastrophe\nThe little race-course at Hampton was in the full tide and height of its gaiety; the day as dazzling as day could be; the sun high in the cloudless sky, and shining in its fullest splendour. Every gaudy colour that fluttered in the air from carriage seat and garish tent top, shone out in its gaudiest hues. Old dingy flags grew new again, faded gilding was re-burnished, stained rotten canvas looked a snowy white, the very beggars’ rags were freshened up, and sentiment quite forgot its charity in its fervent admiration of poverty so picturesque.\n\nIt was one of those scenes of life and animation, caught in its very brightest and freshest moments, which can scarcely fail to please; for if the eye be tired of show and glare, or the ear be weary with a ceaseless round of noise, the one may repose, turn almost where it will, on eager, happy, and expectant faces, and the other deaden all consciousness of more annoying sounds in those of mirth and exhilaration. Even the sunburnt faces of gypsy children, half naked though they be, suggest a drop of comfort. It is a pleasant thing to see that the sun has been there; to know that the air and light are on them every day; to feel that they ARE children, and lead children’s lives; that if their pillows be damp, it is with the dews of Heaven, and not with tears; that the limbs of their girls are free, and that they are not crippled by distortions, imposing an unnatural and horrible penance upon their sex; that their lives are spent, from day to day, at least among the waving trees, and not in the midst of dreadful engines which make young children old before they know what childhood is, and give them the exhaustion and infirmity of age, without, like age, the privilege to die. God send that old nursery tales were true, and that gypsies stole such children by the score!\n\nThe great race of the day had just been run; and the close lines of people, on either side of the course, suddenly breaking up and pouring into it, imparted a new liveliness to the scene, which was again all busy movement. Some hurried eagerly to catch a glimpse of the winning horse; others darted to and fro, searching, no less eagerly, for the carriages they had left in quest of better stations. Here, a little knot gathered round a pea and thimble table to watch the plucking of some unhappy greenhorn; and there, another proprietor with his confederates in various disguises--one man in spectacles; another, with an eyeglass and a stylish hat; a third, dressed as a farmer well to do in the world, with his top-coat over his arm and his flash notes in a large leathern pocket-book; and all with heavy-handled whips to represent most innocent country fellows who had trotted there on horseback--sought, by loud and noisy talk and pretended play, to entrap some unwary customer, while the gentlemen confederates (of more villainous aspect still, in clean linen and good clothes), betrayed their close interest in the concern by the anxious furtive glance they cast on all new comers. These would be hanging on the outskirts of a wide circle of people assembled round some itinerant juggler, opposed, in his turn, by a noisy band of music, or the classic game of ‘Ring the Bull,’ while ventriloquists holding dialogues with wooden dolls, and fortune-telling women smothering the cries of real babies, divided with them, and many more, the general attention of the company. Drinking-tents were full, glasses began to clink in carriages, hampers to be unpacked, tempting provisions to be set forth, knives and forks to rattle, champagne corks to fly, eyes to brighten that were not dull before, and pickpockets to count their gains during the last heat. The attention so recently strained on one object of interest, was now divided among a hundred; and look where you would, there was a motley assemblage of feasting, laughing, talking, begging, gambling, and mummery.\n\nOf the gambling-booths there was a plentiful show, flourishing in all the splendour of carpeted ground, striped hangings, crimson cloth, pinnacled roofs, geranium pots, and livery servants. There were the Stranger’s club-house, the Athenaeum club-house, the Hampton club-house, the St James’s club-house, and half a mile of club-houses to play IN; and there were ROUGE-ET-NOIR, French hazard, and other games to play AT. It is into one of these booths that our story takes its way.\n\nFitted up with three tables for the purposes of play, and crowded with players and lookers on, it was, although the largest place of the kind upon the course, intensely hot, notwithstanding that a portion of the canvas roof was rolled back to admit more air, and there were two doors for a free passage in and out. Excepting one or two men who, each with a long roll of half-crowns, chequered with a few stray sovereigns, in his left hand, staked their money at every roll of the ball with a business-like sedateness which showed that they were used to it, and had been playing all day, and most probably all the day before, there was no very distinctive character about the players, who were chiefly young men, apparently attracted by curiosity, or staking small sums as part of the amusement of the day, with no very great interest in winning or losing. There were two persons present, however, who, as peculiarly good specimens of a class, deserve a passing notice.\n\nOf these, one was a man of six or eight and fifty, who sat on a chair near one of the entrances of the booth, with his hands folded on the top of his stick, and his chin appearing above them. He was a tall, fat, long-bodied man, buttoned up to the throat in a light green coat, which made his body look still longer than it was. He wore, besides, drab breeches and gaiters, a white neckerchief, and a broad-brimmed white hat. Amid all the buzzing noise of the games, and the perpetual passing in and out of the people, he seemed perfectly calm and abstracted, without the smallest particle of excitement in his composition. He exhibited no indication of weariness, nor, to a casual observer, of interest either. There he sat, quite still and collected. Sometimes, but very rarely, he nodded to some passing face, or beckoned to a waiter to obey a call from one of the tables. The next instant he subsided into his old state. He might have been some profoundly deaf old gentleman, who had come in to take a rest, or he might have been patiently waiting for a friend, without the least consciousness of anybody’s presence, or fixed in a trance, or under the influence of opium. People turned round and looked at him; he made no gesture, caught nobody’s eye, let them pass away, and others come on and be succeeded by others, and took no notice. When he did move, it seemed wonderful how he could have seen anything to occasion it. And so, in truth, it was. But there was not a face that passed in or out, which this man failed to see; not a gesture at any one of the three tables that was lost upon him; not a word, spoken by the bankers, but reached his ear; not a winner or loser he could not have marked. And he was the proprietor of the place.\n\nThe other presided over the ROUGE-ET-NOIR table. He was probably some ten years younger, and was a plump, paunchy, sturdy-looking fellow, with his under-lip a little pursed, from a habit of counting money inwardly as he paid it, but with no decidedly bad expression in his face, which was rather an honest and jolly one than otherwise. He wore no coat, the weather being hot, and stood behind the table with a huge mound of crowns and half-crowns before him, and a cash-box for notes. This game was constantly playing. Perhaps twenty people would be staking at the same time. This man had to roll the ball, to watch the stakes as they were laid down, to gather them off the colour which lost, to pay those who won, to do it all with the utmost dispatch, to roll the ball again, and to keep this game perpetually alive. He did it all with a rapidity absolutely marvellous; never hesitating, never making a mistake, never stopping, and never ceasing to repeat such unconnected phrases as the following, which, partly from habit, and partly to have something appropriate and business-like to say, he constantly poured out with the same monotonous emphasis, and in nearly the same order, all day long:\n\n‘Rooge-a-nore from Paris! Gentlemen, make your game and back your own opinions--any time while the ball rolls--rooge-a-nore from Paris, gentlemen, it’s a French game, gentlemen, I brought it over myself, I did indeed!--Rooge-a-nore from Paris--black wins--black--stop a minute, sir, and I’ll pay you, directly--two there, half a pound there, three there--and one there--gentlemen, the ball’s a rolling--any time, sir, while the ball rolls!--The beauty of this game is, that you can double your stakes or put down your money, gentlemen, any time while the ball rolls--black again--black wins--I never saw such a thing--I never did, in all my life, upon my word I never did; if any gentleman had been backing the black in the last five minutes he must have won five-and-forty pound in four rolls of the ball, he must indeed. Gentlemen, we’ve port, sherry, cigars, and most excellent champagne. Here, wai-ter, bring a bottle of champagne, and let’s have a dozen or fifteen cigars here--and let’s be comfortable, gentlemen--and bring some clean glasses--any time while the ball rolls!--I lost one hundred and thirty-seven pound yesterday, gentlemen, at one roll of the ball, I did indeed!--how do you do, sir?’ (recognising some knowing gentleman without any halt or change of voice, and giving a wink so slight that it seems an accident), ‘will you take a glass of sherry, sir?--here, wai-ter! bring a clean glass, and hand the sherry to this gentleman--and hand it round, will you, waiter?--this is the rooge-a-nore from Paris, gentlemen--any time while the ball rolls!--gentlemen, make your game, and back your own opinions--it’s the rooge-a-nore from Paris--quite a new game, I brought it over myself, I did indeed--gentlemen, the ball’s a-rolling!’\n\nThis officer was busily plying his vocation when half-a-dozen persons sauntered through the booth, to whom, but without stopping either in his speech or work, he bowed respectfully; at the same time directing, by a look, the attention of a man beside him to the tallest figure in the group, in recognition of whom the proprietor pulled off his hat. This was Sir Mulberry Hawk, with whom were his friend and pupil, and a small train of gentlemanly-dressed men, of characters more doubtful than obscure.\n\nThe proprietor, in a low voice, bade Sir Mulberry good-day. Sir Mulberry, in the same tone, bade the proprietor go to the devil, and turned to speak with his friends.\n\nThere was evidently an irritable consciousness about him that he was an object of curiosity, on this first occasion of showing himself in public after the accident that had befallen him; and it was easy to perceive that he appeared on the race-course, that day, more in the hope of meeting with a great many people who knew him, and so getting over as much as possible of the annoyance at once, than with any purpose of enjoying the sport. There yet remained a slight scar upon his face, and whenever he was recognised, as he was almost every minute by people sauntering in and out, he made a restless effort to conceal it with his glove; showing how keenly he felt the disgrace he had undergone.\n\n‘Ah! Hawk,’ said one very sprucely-dressed personage in a Newmarket coat, a choice neckerchief, and all other accessories of the most unexceptionable kind. ‘How d’ye do, old fellow?’\n\nThis was a rival trainer of young noblemen and gentlemen, and the person of all others whom Sir Mulberry most hated and dreaded to meet. They shook hands with excessive cordiality.\n\n‘And how are you now, old fellow, hey?’\n\n‘Quite well, quite well,’ said Sir Mulberry.\n\n‘That’s right,’ said the other. ‘How d’ye do, Verisopht? He’s a little pulled down, our friend here. Rather out of condition still, hey?’\n\nIt should be observed that the gentleman had very white teeth, and that when there was no excuse for laughing, he generally finished with the same monosyllable, which he uttered so as to display them.\n\n‘He’s in very good condition; there’s nothing the matter with him,’ said the young man carelessly.\n\n‘Upon my soul I’m glad to hear it,’ rejoined the other. ‘Have you just returned from Brussels?’\n\n‘We only reached town late last night,’ said Lord Frederick. Sir Mulberry turned away to speak to one of his own party, and feigned not to hear.\n\n‘Now, upon my life,’ said the friend, affecting to speak in a whisper, ‘it’s an uncommonly bold and game thing in Hawk to show himself so soon. I say it advisedly; there’s a vast deal of courage in it. You see he has just rusticated long enough to excite curiosity, and not long enough for men to have forgotten that deuced unpleasant--by-the-bye--you know the rights of the affair, of course? Why did you never give those confounded papers the lie? I seldom read the papers, but I looked in the papers for that, and may I be--’\n\n‘Look in the papers,’ interrupted Sir Mulberry, turning suddenly round, ‘tomorrow--no, next day, will you?’\n\n‘Upon my life, my dear fellow, I seldom or never read the papers,’ said the other, shrugging his shoulders, ‘but I will, at your recommendation. What shall I look for?’\n\n‘Good day,’ said Sir Mulberry, turning abruptly on his heel, and drawing his pupil with him. Falling, again, into the loitering, careless pace at which they had entered, they lounged out, arm in arm.\n\n‘I won’t give him a case of murder to read,’ muttered Sir Mulberry with an oath; ‘but it shall be something very near it if whipcord cuts and bludgeons bruise.’\n\nHis companion said nothing, but there was something in his manner which galled Sir Mulberry to add, with nearly as much ferocity as if his friend had been Nicholas himself:\n\n‘I sent Jenkins to old Nickleby before eight o’clock this morning. He’s a staunch one; he was back with me before the messenger. I had it all from him in the first five minutes. I know where this hound is to be met with; time and place both. But there’s no need to talk; tomorrow will soon be here.’\n\n‘And wha-at’s to be done tomorrow?’ inquired Lord Frederick.\n\nSir Mulberry Hawk honoured him with an angry glance, but condescended to return no verbal answer to this inquiry. Both walked sullenly on, as though their thoughts were busily occupied, until they were quite clear of the crowd, and almost alone, when Sir Mulberry wheeled round to return.\n\n‘Stop,’ said his companion, ‘I want to speak to you in earnest. Don’t turn back. Let us walk here, a few minutes.’\n\n‘What have you to say to me, that you could not say yonder as well as here?’ returned his Mentor, disengaging his arm.\n\n‘Hawk,’ rejoined the other, ‘tell me; I must know.’\n\n‘MUST know,’ interrupted the other disdainfully. ‘Whew! Go on. If you must know, of course there’s no escape for me. Must know!’\n\n‘Must ask then,’ returned Lord Frederick, ‘and must press you for a plain and straightforward answer. Is what you have just said only a mere whim of the moment, occasioned by your being out of humour and irritated, or is it your serious intention, and one that you have actually contemplated?’\n\n‘Why, don’t you remember what passed on the subject one night, when I was laid up with a broken limb?’ said Sir Mulberry, with a sneer.\n\n‘Perfectly well.’\n\n‘Then take that for an answer, in the devil’s name,’ replied Sir Mulberry, ‘and ask me for no other.’\n\nSuch was the ascendancy he had acquired over his dupe, and such the latter’s general habit of submission, that, for the moment, the young man seemed half afraid to pursue the subject. He soon overcame this feeling, however, if it had restrained him at all, and retorted angrily:\n\n‘If I remember what passed at the time you speak of, I expressed a strong opinion on this subject, and said that, with my knowledge or consent, you never should do what you threaten now.’\n\n‘Will you prevent me?’ asked Sir Mulberry, with a laugh.\n\n‘Ye-es, if I can,’ returned the other, promptly.\n\n‘A very proper saving clause, that last,’ said Sir Mulberry; ‘and one you stand in need of. Oh! look to your own business, and leave me to look to mine.’\n\n‘This IS mine,’ retorted Lord Frederick. ‘I make it mine; I will make it mine. It’s mine already. I am more compromised than I should be, as it is.’\n\n‘Do as you please, and what you please, for yourself,’ said Sir Mulberry, affecting an easy good-humour. ‘Surely that must content you! Do nothing for me; that’s all. I advise no man to interfere in proceedings that I choose to take. I am sure you know me better than to do so. The fact is, I see, you mean to offer me advice. It is well meant, I have no doubt, but I reject it. Now, if you please, we will return to the carriage. I find no entertainment here, but quite the reverse. If we prolong this conversation, we might quarrel, which would be no proof of wisdom in either you or me.’\n\nWith this rejoinder, and waiting for no further discussion, Sir Mulberry Hawk yawned, and very leisurely turned back.\n\nThere was not a little tact and knowledge of the young lord’s disposition in this mode of treating him. Sir Mulberry clearly saw that if his dominion were to last, it must be established now. He knew that the moment he became violent, the young man would become violent too. He had, many times, been enabled to strengthen his influence, when any circumstance had occurred to weaken it, by adopting this cool and laconic style; and he trusted to it now, with very little doubt of its entire success.\n\nBut while he did this, and wore the most careless and indifferent deportment that his practised arts enabled him to assume, he inwardly resolved, not only to visit all the mortification of being compelled to suppress his feelings, with additional severity upon Nicholas, but also to make the young lord pay dearly for it, one day, in some shape or other. So long as he had been a passive instrument in his hands, Sir Mulberry had regarded him with no other feeling than contempt; but, now that he presumed to avow opinions in opposition to his, and even to turn upon him with a lofty tone and an air of superiority, he began to hate him. Conscious that, in the vilest and most worthless sense of the term, he was dependent upon the weak young lord, Sir Mulberry could the less brook humiliation at his hands; and when he began to dislike him he measured his dislike--as men often do--by the extent of the injuries he had inflicted upon its object. When it is remembered that Sir Mulberry Hawk had plundered, duped, deceived, and fooled his pupil in every possible way, it will not be wondered at, that, beginning to hate him, he began to hate him cordially.\n\nOn the other hand, the young lord having thought--which he very seldom did about anything--and seriously too, upon the affair with Nicholas, and the circumstances which led to it, had arrived at a manly and honest conclusion. Sir Mulberry’s coarse and insulting behaviour on the occasion in question had produced a deep impression on his mind; a strong suspicion of his having led him on to pursue Miss Nickleby for purposes of his own, had been lurking there for some time; he was really ashamed of his share in the transaction, and deeply mortified by the misgiving that he had been gulled. He had had sufficient leisure to reflect upon these things, during their late retirement; and, at times, when his careless and indolent nature would permit, had availed himself of the opportunity. Slight circumstances, too, had occurred to increase his suspicion. It wanted but a very slight circumstance to kindle his wrath against Sir Mulberry. This his disdainful and insolent tone in their recent conversation (the only one they had held upon the subject since the period to which Sir Mulberry referred), effected.\n\nThus they rejoined their friends: each with causes of dislike against the other rankling in his breast: and the young man haunted, besides, with thoughts of the vindictive retaliation which was threatened against Nicholas, and the determination to prevent it by some strong step, if possible. But this was not all. Sir Mulberry, conceiving that he had silenced him effectually, could not suppress his triumph, or forbear from following up what he conceived to be his advantage. Mr. Pyke was there, and Mr. Pluck was there, and Colonel Chowser, and other gentlemen of the same caste, and it was a great point for Sir Mulberry to show them that he had not lost his influence. At first, the young lord contented himself with a silent determination to take measures for withdrawing himself from the connection immediately. By degrees, he grew more angry, and was exasperated by jests and familiarities which, a few hours before, would have been a source of amusement to him. This did not serve him; for, at such bantering or retort as suited the company, he was no match for Sir Mulberry. Still, no violent rupture took place. They returned to town; Messrs Pyke and Pluck and other gentlemen frequently protesting, on the way thither, that Sir Mulberry had never been in such tiptop spirits in all his life.\n\nThey dined together, sumptuously. The wine flowed freely, as indeed it had done all day. Sir Mulberry drank to recompense himself for his recent abstinence; the young lord, to drown his indignation; and the remainder of the party, because the wine was of the best and they had nothing to pay. It was nearly midnight when they rushed out, wild, burning with wine, their blood boiling, and their brains on fire, to the gaming-table.\n\nHere, they encountered another party, mad like themselves. The excitement of play, hot rooms, and glaring lights was not calculated to allay the fever of the time. In that giddy whirl of noise and confusion, the men were delirious. Who thought of money, ruin, or the morrow, in the savage intoxication of the moment? More wine was called for, glass after glass was drained, their parched and scalding mouths were cracked with thirst. Down poured the wine like oil on blazing fire. And still the riot went on. The debauchery gained its height; glasses were dashed upon the floor by hands that could not carry them to lips; oaths were shouted out by lips which could scarcely form the words to vent them in; drunken losers cursed and roared; some mounted on the tables, waving bottles above their heads and bidding defiance to the rest; some danced, some sang, some tore the cards and raved. Tumult and frenzy reigned supreme; when a noise arose that drowned all others, and two men, seizing each other by the throat, struggled into the middle of the room.\n\nA dozen voices, until now unheard, called aloud to part them. Those who had kept themselves cool, to win, and who earned their living in such scenes, threw themselves upon the combatants, and, forcing them asunder, dragged them some space apart.\n\n‘Let me go!’ cried Sir Mulberry, in a thick hoarse voice; ‘he struck me! Do you hear? I say, he struck me. Have I a friend here? Who is this? Westwood. Do you hear me say he struck me?’\n\n‘I hear, I hear,’ replied one of those who held him. ‘Come away for tonight!’\n\n‘I will not, by G--,’ he replied. ‘A dozen men about us saw the blow.’\n\n‘Tomorrow will be ample time,’ said the friend.\n\n‘It will not be ample time!’ cried Sir Mulberry. ‘Tonight, at once, here!’ His passion was so great, that he could not articulate, but stood clenching his fist, tearing his hair, and stamping upon the ground.\n\n‘What is this, my lord?’ said one of those who surrounded him. ‘Have blows passed?’\n\n‘ONE blow has,’ was the panting reply. ‘I struck him. I proclaim it to all here! I struck him, and he knows why. I say, with him, let this quarrel be adjusted now. Captain Adams,’ said the young lord, looking hurriedly about him, and addressing one of those who had interposed, ‘let me speak with you, I beg.’\n\nThe person addressed stepped forward, and taking the young man’s arm, they retired together, followed shortly afterwards by Sir Mulberry and his friend.\n\nIt was a profligate haunt of the worst repute, and not a place in which such an affair was likely to awaken any sympathy for either party, or to call forth any further remonstrance or interposition. Elsewhere, its further progress would have been instantly prevented, and time allowed for sober and cool reflection; but not there. Disturbed in their orgies, the party broke up; some reeled away with looks of tipsy gravity; others withdrew noisily discussing what had just occurred; the gentlemen of honour who lived upon their winnings remarked to each other, as they went out, that Hawk was a good shot; and those who had been most noisy, fell fast asleep upon the sofas, and thought no more about it.\n\nMeanwhile, the two seconds, as they may be called now, after a long conference, each with his principal, met together in another room. Both utterly heartless, both men upon town, both thoroughly initiated in its worst vices, both deeply in debt, both fallen from some higher estate, both addicted to every depravity for which society can find some genteel name and plead its most depraving conventionalities as an excuse, they were naturally gentlemen of most unblemished honour themselves, and of great nicety concerning the honour of other people.\n\nThese two gentlemen were unusually cheerful just now; for the affair was pretty certain to make some noise, and could scarcely fail to enhance their reputations.\n\n‘This is an awkward affair, Adams,’ said Mr. Westwood, drawing himself up.\n\n‘Very,’ returned the captain; ‘a blow has been struck, and there is but one course, OF course.’\n\n‘No apology, I suppose?’ said Mr. Westwood.\n\n‘Not a syllable, sir, from my man, if we talk till doomsday,’ returned the captain. ‘The original cause of dispute, I understand, was some girl or other, to whom your principal applied certain terms, which Lord Frederick, defending the girl, repelled. But this led to a long recrimination upon a great many sore subjects, charges, and counter-charges. Sir Mulberry was sarcastic; Lord Frederick was excited, and struck him in the heat of provocation, and under circumstances of great aggravation. That blow, unless there is a full retraction on the part of Sir Mulberry, Lord Frederick is ready to justify.’\n\n‘There is no more to be said,’ returned the other, ‘but to settle the hour and the place of meeting. It’s a responsibility; but there is a strong feeling to have it over. Do you object to say at sunrise?’\n\n‘Sharp work,’ replied the captain, referring to his watch; ‘however, as this seems to have been a long time breeding, and negotiation is only a waste of words, no.’\n\n‘Something may possibly be said, out of doors, after what passed in the other room, which renders it desirable that we should be off without delay, and quite clear of town,’ said Mr. Westwood. ‘What do you say to one of the meadows opposite Twickenham, by the river-side?’\n\nThe captain saw no objection.\n\n‘Shall we join company in the avenue of trees which leads from Petersham to Ham House, and settle the exact spot when we arrive there?’ said Mr Westwood.\n\nTo this the captain also assented. After a few other preliminaries, equally brief, and having settled the road each party should take to avoid suspicion, they separated.\n\n‘We shall just have comfortable time, my lord,’ said the captain, when he had communicated the arrangements, ‘to call at my rooms for a case of pistols, and then jog coolly down. If you will allow me to dismiss your servant, we’ll take my cab; for yours, perhaps, might be recognised.’\n\nWhat a contrast, when they reached the street, to the scene they had just left! It was already daybreak. For the flaring yellow light within, was substituted the clear, bright, glorious morning; for a hot, close atmosphere, tainted with the smell of expiring lamps, and reeking with the steams of riot and dissipation, the free, fresh, wholesome air. But to the fevered head on which that cool air blew, it seemed to come laden with remorse for time misspent and countless opportunities neglected. With throbbing veins and burning skin, eyes wild and heavy, thoughts hurried and disordered, he felt as though the light were a reproach, and shrunk involuntarily from the day as if he were some foul and hideous thing.\n\n‘Shivering?’ said the captain. ‘You are cold.’\n\n‘Rather.’\n\n‘It does strike cool, coming out of those hot rooms. Wrap that cloak about you. So, so; now we’re off.’\n\nThey rattled through the quiet streets, made their call at the captain’s lodgings, cleared the town, and emerged upon the open road, without hindrance or molestation.\n\nFields, trees, gardens, hedges, everything looked very beautiful; the young man scarcely seemed to have noticed them before, though he had passed the same objects a thousand times. There was a peace and serenity upon them all, strangely at variance with the bewilderment and confusion of his own half-sobered thoughts, and yet impressive and welcome. He had no fear upon his mind; but, as he looked about him, he had less anger; and though all old delusions, relative to his worthless late companion, were now cleared away, he rather wished he had never known him than thought of its having come to this.\n\nThe past night, the day before, and many other days and nights beside, all mingled themselves up in one unintelligible and senseless whirl; he could not separate the transactions of one time from those of another. Now, the noise of the wheels resolved itself into some wild tune in which he could recognise scraps of airs he knew; now, there was nothing in his ears but a stunning and bewildering sound, like rushing water. But his companion rallied him on being so silent, and they talked and laughed boisterously. When they stopped, he was a little surprised to find himself in the act of smoking; but, on reflection, he remembered when and where he had taken the cigar.\n\nThey stopped at the avenue gate and alighted, leaving the carriage to the care of the servant, who was a smart fellow, and nearly as well accustomed to such proceedings as his master. Sir Mulberry and his friend were already there. All four walked in profound silence up the aisle of stately elm trees, which, meeting far above their heads, formed a long green perspective of Gothic arches, terminating, like some old ruin, in the open sky.\n\nAfter a pause, and a brief conference between the seconds, they, at length, turned to the right, and taking a track across a little meadow, passed Ham House and came into some fields beyond. In one of these, they stopped. The ground was measured, some usual forms gone through, the two principals were placed front to front at the distance agreed upon, and Sir Mulberry turned his face towards his young adversary for the first time. He was very pale, his eyes were bloodshot, his dress disordered, and his hair dishevelled. For the face, it expressed nothing but violent and evil passions. He shaded his eyes with his hand; grazed at his opponent, steadfastly, for a few moments; and, then taking the weapon which was tendered to him, bent his eyes upon that, and looked up no more until the word was given, when he instantly fired.\n\nThe two shots were fired, as nearly as possible, at the same instant. In that instant, the young lord turned his head sharply round, fixed upon his adversary a ghastly stare, and without a groan or stagger, fell down dead.\n\n‘He’s gone!’ cried Westwood, who, with the other second, had run up to the body, and fallen on one knee beside it.\n\n‘His blood on his own head,’ said Sir Mulberry. ‘He brought this upon himself, and forced it upon me.’\n\n‘Captain Adams,’ cried Westwood, hastily, ‘I call you to witness that this was fairly done. Hawk, we have not a moment to lose. We must leave this place immediately, push for Brighton, and cross to France with all speed. This has been a bad business, and may be worse, if we delay a moment. Adams, consult your own safety, and don’t remain here; the living before the dead; goodbye!’\n\nWith these words, he seized Sir Mulberry by the arm, and hurried him away. Captain Adams--only pausing to convince himself, beyond all question, of the fatal result--sped off in the same direction, to concert measures with his servant for removing the body, and securing his own safety likewise.\n\nSo died Lord Frederick Verisopht, by the hand which he had loaded with gifts, and clasped a thousand times; by the act of him, but for whom, and others like him, he might have lived a happy man, and died with children’s faces round his bed.\n\nThe sun came proudly up in all his majesty, the noble river ran its winding course, the leaves quivered and rustled in the air, the birds poured their cheerful songs from every tree, the short-lived butterfly fluttered its little wings; all the light and life of day came on; and, amidst it all, and pressing down the grass whose every blade bore twenty tiny lives, lay the dead man, with his stark and rigid face turned upwards to the sky.\n\n# CHAPTER 51\n\nThe Project of Mr. Ralph Nickleby and his Friend approaching a successful Issue, becomes unexpectedly known to another Party, not admitted into their Confidence\nIn an old house, dismal dark and dusty, which seemed to have withered, like himself, and to have grown yellow and shrivelled in hoarding him from the light of day, as he had in hoarding his money, lived Arthur Gride. Meagre old chairs and tables, of spare and bony make, and hard and cold as misers’ hearts, were ranged, in grim array, against the gloomy walls; attenuated presses, grown lank and lantern-jawed in guarding the treasures they enclosed, and tottering, as though from constant fear and dread of thieves, shrunk up in dark corners, whence they cast no shadows on the ground, and seemed to hide and cower from observation. A tall grim clock upon the stairs, with long lean hands and famished face, ticked in cautious whispers; and when it struck the time, in thin and piping sounds, like an old man’s voice, rattled, as if it were pinched with hunger.\n\nNo fireside couch was there, to invite repose and comfort. Elbow-chairs there were, but they looked uneasy in their minds, cocked their arms suspiciously and timidly, and kept upon their guard. Others, were fantastically grim and gaunt, as having drawn themselves up to their utmost height, and put on their fiercest looks to stare all comers out of countenance. Others, again, knocked up against their neighbours, or leant for support against the wall--somewhat ostentatiously, as if to call all men to witness that they were not worth the taking. The dark square lumbering bedsteads seemed built for restless dreams; the musty hangings seemed to creep in scanty folds together, whispering among themselves, when rustled by the wind, their trembling knowledge of the tempting wares that lurked within the dark and tight-locked closets.\n\nFrom out the most spare and hungry room in all this spare and hungry house there came, one morning, the tremulous tones of old Gride’s voice, as it feebly chirruped forth the fag end of some forgotten song, of which the burden ran:\n\nTa--ran--tan--too, Throw the old shoe, And may the wedding be lucky!\n\nwhich he repeated, in the same shrill quavering notes, again and again, until a violent fit of coughing obliged him to desist, and to pursue in silence, the occupation upon which he was engaged.\n\nThis occupation was, to take down from the shelves of a worm-eaten wardrobe a quantity of frouzy garments, one by one; to subject each to a careful and minute inspection by holding it up against the light, and after folding it with great exactness, to lay it on one or other of two little heaps beside him. He never took two articles of clothing out together, but always brought them forth, singly, and never failed to shut the wardrobe door, and turn the key, between each visit to its shelves.\n\n‘The snuff-coloured suit,’ said Arthur Gride, surveying a threadbare coat. ‘Did I look well in snuff-colour? Let me think.’\n\nThe result of his cogitations appeared to be unfavourable, for he folded the garment once more, laid it aside, and mounted on a chair to get down another, chirping while he did so:\n\nYoung, loving, and fair, Oh what happiness there! The wedding is sure to be lucky!\n\n‘They always put in “young,”’ said old Arthur, ‘but songs are only written for the sake of rhyme, and this is a silly one that the poor country-people sang, when I was a little boy. Though stop--young is quite right too--it means the bride--yes. He, he, he! It means the bride. Oh dear, that’s good. That’s very good. And true besides, quite true!’\n\nIn the satisfaction of this discovery, he went over the verse again, with increased expression, and a shake or two here and there. He then resumed his employment.\n\n‘The bottle-green,’ said old Arthur; ‘the bottle-green was a famous suit to wear, and I bought it very cheap at a pawnbroker’s, and there was--he, he, he!--a tarnished shilling in the waistcoat pocket. To think that the pawnbroker shouldn’t have known there was a shilling in it! I knew it! I felt it when I was examining the quality. Oh, what a dull dog of a pawnbroker! It was a lucky suit too, this bottle-green. The very day I put it on first, old Lord Mallowford was burnt to death in his bed, and all the post-obits fell in. I’ll be married in the bottle-green. Peg. Peg Sliderskew--I’ll wear the bottle-green!’\n\nThis call, loudly repeated twice or thrice at the room-door, brought into the apartment a short, thin, weasen, blear-eyed old woman, palsy-stricken and hideously ugly, who, wiping her shrivelled face upon her dirty apron, inquired, in that subdued tone in which deaf people commonly speak:\n\n‘Was that you a calling, or only the clock a striking? My hearing gets so bad, I never know which is which; but when I hear a noise, I know it must be one of you, because nothing else never stirs in the house.’\n\n‘Me, Peg, me,’ said Arthur Gride, tapping himself on the breast to render the reply more intelligible.\n\n‘You, eh?’ returned Peg. ‘And what do YOU want?’\n\n‘I’ll be married in the bottle-green,’ cried Arthur Gride.\n\n‘It’s a deal too good to be married in, master,’ rejoined Peg, after a short inspection of the suit. ‘Haven’t you got anything worse than this?’\n\n‘Nothing that’ll do,’ replied old Arthur.\n\n‘Why not do?’ retorted Peg. ‘Why don’t you wear your every-day clothes, like a man--eh?’\n\n‘They an’t becoming enough, Peg,’ returned her master.\n\n‘Not what enough?’ said Peg.\n\n‘Becoming.’\n\n‘Becoming what?’ said Peg, sharply. ‘Not becoming too old to wear?’\n\nArthur Gride muttered an imprecation on his housekeeper’s deafness, as he roared in her ear:\n\n‘Not smart enough! I want to look as well as I can.’\n\n‘Look?’ cried Peg. ‘If she’s as handsome as you say she is, she won’t look much at you, master, take your oath of that; and as to how you look yourself--pepper-and-salt, bottle-green, sky-blue, or tartan-plaid will make no difference in you.’\n\nWith which consolatory assurance, Peg Sliderskew gathered up the chosen suit, and folding her skinny arms upon the bundle, stood, mouthing, and grinning, and blinking her watery eyes, like an uncouth figure in some monstrous piece of carving.\n\n‘You’re in a funny humour, an’t you, Peg?’ said Arthur, with not the best possible grace.\n\n‘Why, isn’t it enough to make me?’ rejoined the old woman. ‘I shall, soon enough, be put out, though, if anybody tries to domineer it over me: and so I give you notice, master. Nobody shall be put over Peg Sliderskew’s head, after so many years; you know that, and so I needn’t tell you! That won’t do for me--no, no, nor for you. Try that once, and come to ruin--ruin--ruin!’\n\n‘Oh dear, dear, I shall never try it,’ said Arthur Gride, appalled by the mention of the word, ‘not for the world. It would be very easy to ruin me; we must be very careful; more saving than ever, with another mouth to feed. Only we--we mustn’t let her lose her good looks, Peg, because I like to see ‘em.’\n\n‘Take care you don’t find good looks come expensive,’ returned Peg, shaking her forefinger.\n\n‘But she can earn money herself, Peg,’ said Arthur Gride, eagerly watching what effect his communication produced upon the old woman’s countenance: ‘she can draw, paint, work all manner of pretty things for ornamenting stools and chairs: slippers, Peg, watch-guards, hair-chains, and a thousand little dainty trifles that I couldn’t give you half the names of. Then she can play the piano, (and, what’s more, she’s got one), and sing like a little bird. She’ll be very cheap to dress and keep, Peg; don’t you think she will?’\n\n‘If you don’t let her make a fool of you, she may,’ returned Peg.\n\n‘A fool of ME!’ exclaimed Arthur. ‘Trust your old master not to be fooled by pretty faces, Peg; no, no, no--nor by ugly ones neither, Mrs Sliderskew,’ he softly added by way of soliloquy.\n\n‘You’re a saying something you don’t want me to hear,’ said Peg; ‘I know you are.’\n\n‘Oh dear! the devil’s in this woman,’ muttered Arthur; adding with an ugly leer, ‘I said I trusted everything to you, Peg. That was all.’\n\n‘You do that, master, and all your cares are over,’ said Peg approvingly.\n\n‘WHEN I do that, Peg Sliderskew,’ thought Arthur Gride, ‘they will be.’\n\nAlthough he thought this very distinctly, he durst not move his lips lest the old woman should detect him. He even seemed half afraid that she might have read his thoughts; for he leered coaxingly upon her, as he said aloud:\n\n‘Take up all loose stitches in the bottle-green with the best black silk. Have a skein of the best, and some new buttons for the coat, and--this is a good idea, Peg, and one you’ll like, I know--as I have never given her anything yet, and girls like such attentions, you shall polish up a sparking necklace that I have got upstairs, and I’ll give it her upon the wedding morning--clasp it round her charming little neck myself--and take it away again next day. He, he, he! I’ll lock it up for her, Peg, and lose it. Who’ll be made the fool of there, I wonder, to begin with--eh, Peg?’\n\nMrs. Sliderskew appeared to approve highly of this ingenious scheme, and expressed her satisfaction by various rackings and twitchings of her head and body, which by no means enhanced her charms. These she prolonged until she had hobbled to the door, when she exchanged them for a sour malignant look, and twisting her under-jaw from side to side, muttered hearty curses upon the future Mrs. Gride, as she crept slowly down the stairs, and paused for breath at nearly every one.\n\n‘She’s half a witch, I think,’ said Arthur Gride, when he found himself again alone. ‘But she’s very frugal, and she’s very deaf. Her living costs me next to nothing; and it’s no use her listening at keyholes; for she can’t hear. She’s a charming woman--for the purpose; a most discreet old housekeeper, and worth her weight in--copper.’\n\nHaving extolled the merits of his domestic in these high terms, old Arthur went back to the burden of his song. The suit destined to grace his approaching nuptials being now selected, he replaced the others with no less care than he had displayed in drawing them from the musty nooks where they had silently reposed for many years.\n\nStartled by a ring at the door, he hastily concluded this operation, and locked the press; but there was no need for any particular hurry, as the discreet Peg seldom knew the bell was rung unless she happened to cast her dim eyes upwards, and to see it shaking against the kitchen ceiling. After a short delay, however, Peg tottered in, followed by Newman Noggs.\n\n‘Ah! Mr. Noggs!’ cried Arthur Gride, rubbing his hands. ‘My good friend, Mr. Noggs, what news do you bring for me?’\n\nNewman, with a steadfast and immovable aspect, and his fixed eye very fixed indeed, replied, suiting the action to the word, ‘A letter. From Mr. Nickleby. Bearer waits.’\n\n‘Won’t you take a--a--’\n\nNewman looked up, and smacked his lips.\n\n‘--A chair?’ said Arthur Gride.\n\n‘No,’ replied Newman. ‘Thankee.’\n\nArthur opened the letter with trembling hands, and devoured its contents with the utmost greediness; chuckling rapturously over it, and reading it several times, before he could take it from before his eyes. So many times did he peruse and re-peruse it, that Newman considered it expedient to remind him of his presence.\n\n‘Answer,’ said Newman. ‘Bearer waits.’\n\n‘True,’ replied old Arthur. ‘Yes--yes; I almost forgot, I do declare.’\n\n‘I thought you were forgetting,’ said Newman.\n\n‘Quite right to remind me, Mr. Noggs. Oh, very right indeed,’ said Arthur. ‘Yes. I’ll write a line. I’m--I’m--rather flurried, Mr. Noggs. The news is--’\n\n‘Bad?’ interrupted Newman.\n\n‘No, Mr. Noggs, thank you; good, good. The very best of news. Sit down. I’ll get the pen and ink, and write a line in answer. I’ll not detain you long. I know you’re a treasure to your master, Mr. Noggs. He speaks of you in such terms, sometimes, that, oh dear! you’d be astonished. I may say that I do too, and always did. I always say the same of you.’\n\n‘That’s “Curse Mr. Noggs with all my heart!” then, if you do,’ thought Newman, as Gride hurried out.\n\nThe letter had fallen on the ground. Looking carefully about him for an instant, Newman, impelled by curiosity to know the result of the design he had overheard from his office closet, caught it up and rapidly read as follows:\n\n‘I saw Bray again this morning, and proposed the day after tomorrow (as you suggested) for the marriage. There is no objection on his part, and all days are alike to his daughter. We will go together, and you must be with me by seven in the morning. I need not tell you to be punctual.\n\n‘Make no further visits to the girl in the meantime. You have been there, of late, much oftener than you should. She does not languish for you, and it might have been dangerous. Restrain your youthful ardour for eight-and-forty hours, and leave her to the father. You only undo what he does, and does well.\n\n‘Yours,\n\nA footstep was heard without. Newman dropped the letter on the same spot again, pressed it with his foot to prevent its fluttering away, regained his seat in a single stride, and looked as vacant and unconscious as ever mortal looked. Arthur Gride, after peering nervously about him, spied it on the ground, picked it up, and sitting down to write, glanced at Newman Noggs, who was staring at the wall with an intensity so remarkable, that Arthur was quite alarmed.\n\n‘Do you see anything particular, Mr. Noggs?’ said Arthur, trying to follow the direction of Newman’s eyes--which was an impossibility, and a thing no man had ever done.\n\n‘Only a cobweb,’ replied Newman.\n\n‘Oh! is that all?’\n\n‘No,’ said Newman. ‘There’s a fly in it.’\n\n‘There are a good many cobwebs here,’ observed Arthur Gride.\n\n‘So there are in our place,’ returned Newman; ‘and flies too.’\n\nNewman appeared to derive great entertainment from this repartee, and to the great discomposure of Arthur Gride’s nerves, produced a series of sharp cracks from his finger-joints, resembling the noise of a distant discharge of small artillery. Arthur succeeded in finishing his reply to Ralph’s note, nevertheless, and at length handed it over to the eccentric messenger for delivery.\n\n‘That’s it, Mr. Noggs,’ said Gride.\n\nNewman gave a nod, put it in his hat, and was shuffling away, when Gride, whose doting delight knew no bounds, beckoned him back again, and said, in a shrill whisper, and with a grin which puckered up his whole face, and almost obscured his eyes:\n\n‘Will you--will you take a little drop of something--just a taste?’\n\nIn good fellowship (if Arthur Gride had been capable of it) Newman would not have drunk with him one bubble of the richest wine that was ever made; but to see what he would be at, and to punish him as much as he could, he accepted the offer immediately.\n\nArthur Gride, therefore, again applied himself to the press, and from a shelf laden with tall Flemish drinking-glasses, and quaint bottles: some with necks like so many storks, and others with square Dutch-built bodies and short fat apoplectic throats: took down one dusty bottle of promising appearance, and two glasses of curiously small size.\n\n‘You never tasted this,’ said Arthur. ‘It’s EAU-D’OR--golden water. I like it on account of its name. It’s a delicious name. Water of gold, golden water! O dear me, it seems quite a sin to drink it!’\n\nAs his courage appeared to be fast failing him, and he trifled with the stopper in a manner which threatened the dismissal of the bottle to its old place, Newman took up one of the little glasses, and clinked it, twice or thrice, against the bottle, as a gentle reminder that he had not been helped yet. With a deep sigh, Arthur Gride slowly filled it--though not to the brim--and then filled his own.\n\n‘Stop, stop; don’t drink it yet,’ he said, laying his hand on Newman’s; ‘it was given to me, twenty years ago, and when I take a little taste, which is ve--ry seldom, I like to think of it beforehand, and tease myself. We’ll drink a toast. Shall we drink a toast, Mr. Noggs?’\n\n‘Ah!’ said Newman, eyeing his little glass impatiently. ‘Look sharp. Bearer waits.’\n\n‘Why, then, I’ll tell you what,’ tittered Arthur, ‘we’ll drink--he, he, he!--we’ll drink a lady.’\n\n‘THE ladies?’ said Newman.\n\n‘No, no, Mr. Noggs,’ replied Gride, arresting his hand, ‘A lady. You wonder to hear me say A lady. I know you do, I know you do. Here’s little Madeline. That’s the toast. Mr. Noggs. Little Madeline!’\n\n‘Madeline!’ said Newman; inwardly adding, ‘and God help her!’\n\nThe rapidity and unconcern with which Newman dismissed his portion of the golden water, had a great effect upon the old man, who sat upright in his chair, and gazed at him, open-mouthed, as if the sight had taken away his breath. Quite unmoved, however, Newman left him to sip his own at leisure, or to pour it back again into the bottle, if he chose, and departed; after greatly outraging the dignity of Peg Sliderskew by brushing past her, in the passage, without a word of apology or recognition.\n\nMr. Gride and his housekeeper, immediately on being left alone, resolved themselves into a committee of ways and means, and discussed the arrangements which should be made for the reception of the young bride. As they were, like some other committees, extremely dull and prolix in debate, this history may pursue the footsteps of Newman Noggs; thereby combining advantage with necessity; for it would have been necessary to do so under any circumstances, and necessity has no law, as all the world knows.\n\n‘You’ve been a long time,’ said Ralph, when Newman returned.\n\n‘HE was a long time,’ replied Newman.\n\n‘Bah!’ cried Ralph impatiently. ‘Give me his note, if he gave you one: his message, if he didn’t. And don’t go away. I want a word with you, sir.’\n\nNewman handed in the note, and looked very virtuous and innocent while his employer broke the seal, and glanced his eye over it.\n\n‘He’ll be sure to come,’ muttered Ralph, as he tore it to pieces; ‘why of course, I know he’ll be sure to come. What need to say that? Noggs! Pray, sir, what man was that, with whom I saw you in the street last night?’\n\n‘I don’t know,’ replied Newman.\n\n‘You had better refresh your memory, sir,’ said Ralph, with a threatening look.\n\n‘I tell you,’ returned Newman boldly, ‘that I don’t know. He came here twice, and asked for you. You were out. He came again. You packed him off, yourself. He gave the name of Brooker.’\n\n‘I know he did,’ said Ralph; ‘what then?’\n\n‘What then? Why, then he lurked about and dogged me in the street. He follows me, night after night, and urges me to bring him face to face with you; as he says he has been once, and not long ago either. He wants to see you face to face, he says, and you’ll soon hear him out, he warrants.’\n\n‘And what say you to that?’ inquired Ralph, looking keenly at his drudge.\n\n‘That it’s no business of mine, and I won’t. I told him he might catch you in the street, if that was all he wanted, but no! that wouldn’t do. You wouldn’t hear a word there, he said. He must have you alone in a room with the door locked, where he could speak without fear, and you’d soon change your tone, and hear him patiently.’\n\n‘An audacious dog!’ Ralph muttered.\n\n‘That’s all I know,’ said Newman. ‘I say again, I don’t know what man he is. I don’t believe he knows himself. You have seen him; perhaps YOU do.’\n\n‘I think I do,’ replied Ralph.\n\n‘Well,’ retored Newman, sulkily, ‘don’t expect me to know him too; that’s all. You’ll ask me, next, why I never told you this before. What would you say, if I was to tell you all that people say of you? What do you call me when I sometimes do? “Brute, ass!” and snap at me like a dragon.’\n\nThis was true enough; though the question which Newman anticipated, was, in fact, upon Ralph’s lips at the moment.\n\n‘He is an idle ruffian,’ said Ralph; ‘a vagabond from beyond the sea where he travelled for his crimes; a felon let loose to run his neck into the halter; a swindler, who has the audacity to try his schemes on me who know him well. The next time he tampers with you, hand him over to the police, for attempting to extort money by lies and threats,--d’ye hear?--and leave the rest to me. He shall cool his heels in jail a little time, and I’ll be bound he looks for other folks to fleece, when he comes out. You mind what I say, do you?’\n\n‘I hear,’ said Newman.\n\n‘Do it then,’ returned Ralph, ‘and I’ll reward you. Now, you may go.’\n\nNewman readily availed himself of the permission, and, shutting himself up in his little office, remained there, in very serious cogitation, all day. When he was released at night, he proceeded, with all the expedition he could use, to the city, and took up his old position behind the pump, to watch for Nicholas. For Newman Noggs was proud in his way, and could not bear to appear as his friend, before the brothers Cheeryble, in the shabby and degraded state to which he was reduced.\n\nHe had not occupied this position many minutes, when he was rejoiced to see Nicholas approaching, and darted out from his ambuscade to meet him. Nicholas, on his part, was no less pleased to encounter his friend, whom he had not seen for some time; so, their greeting was a warm one.\n\n‘I was thinking of you, at that moment,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘That’s right,’ rejoined Newman, ‘and I of you. I couldn’t help coming up, tonight. I say, I think I am going to find out something.’\n\n‘And what may that be?’ returned Nicholas, smiling at this odd communication.\n\n‘I don’t know what it may be, I don’t know what it may not be,’ said Newman; ‘it’s some secret in which your uncle is concerned, but what, I’ve not yet been able to discover, although I have my strong suspicions. I’ll not hint ‘em now, in case you should be disappointed.’\n\n‘I disappointed!’ cried Nicholas; ‘am I interested?’\n\n‘I think you are,’ replied Newman. ‘I have a crotchet in my head that it must be so. I have found out a man, who plainly knows more than he cares to tell at once. And he has already dropped such hints to me as puzzle me--I say, as puzzle me,’ said Newman, scratching his red nose into a state of violent inflammation, and staring at Nicholas with all his might and main meanwhile.\n\nAdmiring what could have wound his friend up to such a pitch of mystery, Nicholas endeavoured, by a series of questions, to elucidate the cause; but in vain. Newman could not be drawn into any more explicit statement than a repetition of the perplexities he had already thrown out, and a confused oration, showing, How it was necessary to use the utmost caution; how the lynx-eyed Ralph had already seen him in company with his unknown correspondent; and how he had baffled the said Ralph by extreme guardedness of manner and ingenuity of speech; having prepared himself for such a contingency from the first.\n\nRemembering his companion’s propensity,--of which his nose, indeed, perpetually warned all beholders like a beacon,--Nicholas had drawn him into a sequestered tavern. Here, they fell to reviewing the origin and progress of their acquaintance, as men sometimes do, and tracing out the little events by which it was most strongly marked, came at last to Miss Cecilia Bobster.\n\n‘And that reminds me,’ said Newman, ‘that you never told me the young lady’s real name.’\n\n‘Madeline!’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Madeline!’ cried Newman. ‘What Madeline? Her other name. Say her other name.’\n\n‘Bray,’ said Nicholas, in great astonishment.\n\n‘It’s the same!’ cried Newman. ‘Sad story! Can you stand idly by, and let that unnatural marriage take place without one attempt to save her?’\n\n‘What do you mean?’ exclaimed Nicholas, starting up; ‘marriage! are you mad?’\n\n‘Are you? Is she? Are you blind, deaf, senseless, dead?’ said Newman. ‘Do you know that within one day, by means of your uncle Ralph, she will be married to a man as bad as he, and worse, if worse there is? Do you know that, within one day, she will be sacrificed, as sure as you stand there alive, to a hoary wretch--a devil born and bred, and grey in devils’ ways?’\n\n‘Be careful what you say,’ replied Nicholas. ‘For Heaven’s sake be careful! I am left here alone, and those who could stretch out a hand to rescue her are far away. What is it that you mean?’\n\n‘I never heard her name,’ said Newman, choking with his energy. ‘Why didn’t you tell me? How was I to know? We might, at least, have had some time to think!’\n\n‘What is it that you mean?’ cried Nicholas.\n\nIt was not an easy task to arrive at this information; but, after a great quantity of extraordinary pantomime, which in no way assisted it, Nicholas, who was almost as wild as Newman Noggs himself, forced the latter down upon his seat and held him down until he began his tale.\n\nRage, astonishment, indignation, and a storm of passions, rushed through the listener’s heart, as the plot was laid bare. He no sooner understood it all, than with a face of ashy paleness, and trembling in every limb, he darted from the house.\n\n‘Stop him!’ cried Newman, bolting out in pursuit. ‘He’ll be doing something desperate; he’ll murder somebody. Hallo! there, stop him. Stop thief! stop thief!’\n\n# CHAPTER 52\n\nNicholas despairs of rescuing Madeline Bray, but plucks up his Spirits again, and determines to attempt it. Domestic Intelligence of the Kenwigses and Lillyvicks\n\nFinding that Newman was determined to arrest his progress at any hazard, and apprehensive that some well-intentioned passenger, attracted by the cry of ‘Stop thief,’ might lay violent hands upon his person, and place him in a disagreeable predicament from which he might have some difficulty in extricating himself, Nicholas soon slackened his pace, and suffered Newman Noggs to come up with him: which he did, in so breathless a condition, that it seemed impossible he could have held out for a minute longer.\n\n‘I will go straight to Bray’s,’ said Nicholas. ‘I will see this man. If there is a feeling of humanity lingering in his breast, a spark of consideration for his own child, motherless and friendless as she is, I will awaken it.’\n\n‘You will not,’ replied Newman. ‘You will not, indeed.’\n\n‘Then,’ said Nicholas, pressing onward, ‘I will act upon my first impulse, and go straight to Ralph Nickleby.’\n\n‘By the time you reach his house he will be in bed,’ said Newman.\n\n‘I’ll drag him from it,’ cried Nicholas.\n\n‘Tut, tut,’ said Noggs. ‘Be yourself.’\n\n‘You are the best of friends to me, Newman,’ rejoined Nicholas after a pause, and taking his hand as he spoke. ‘I have made head against many trials; but the misery of another, and such misery, is involved in this one, that I declare to you I am rendered desperate, and know not how to act.’\n\nIn truth, it did seem a hopeless case. It was impossible to make any use of such intelligence as Newman Noggs had gleaned, when he lay concealed in the closet. The mere circumstance of the compact between Ralph Nickleby and Gride would not invalidate the marriage, or render Bray averse to it, who, if he did not actually know of the existence of some such understanding, doubtless suspected it. What had been hinted with reference to some fraud on Madeline, had been put, with sufficient obscurity by Arthur Gride, but coming from Newman Noggs, and obscured still further by the smoke of his pocket-pistol, it became wholly unintelligible, and involved in utter darkness.\n\n‘There seems no ray of hope,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘The greater necessity for coolness, for reason, for consideration, for thought,’ said Newman, pausing at every alternate word, to look anxiously in his friend’s face. ‘Where are the brothers?’\n\n‘Both absent on urgent business, as they will be for a week to come.’\n\n‘Is there no way of communicating with them? No way of getting one of them here by tomorrow night?’\n\n‘Impossible!’ said Nicholas, ‘the sea is between us and them. With the fairest winds that ever blew, to go and return would take three days and nights.’\n\n‘Their nephew,’ said Newman, ‘their old clerk.’\n\n‘What could either do, that I cannot?’ rejoined Nicholas. ‘With reference to them, especially, I am enjoined to the strictest silence on this subject. What right have I to betray the confidence reposed in me, when nothing but a miracle can prevent this sacrifice?’\n\n‘Think,’ urged Newman. ‘Is there no way?’\n\n‘There is none,’ said Nicholas, in utter dejection. ‘Not one. The father urges, the daughter consents. These demons have her in their toils; legal right, might, power, money, and every influence are on their side. How can I hope to save her?’\n\n‘Hope to the last!’ said Newman, clapping him on the back. ‘Always hope; that’s a dear boy. Never leave off hoping; it don’t answer. Do you mind me, Nick? It don’t answer. Don’t leave a stone unturned. It’s always something, to know you’ve done the most you could. But, don’t leave off hoping, or it’s of no use doing anything. Hope, hope, to the last!’\n\nNicholas needed encouragement. The suddenness with which intelligence of the two usurers’ plans had come upon him, the little time which remained for exertion, the probability, almost amounting to certainty itself, that a few hours would place Madeline Bray for ever beyond his reach, consign her to unspeakable misery, and perhaps to an untimely death; all this quite stunned and overwhelmed him. Every hope connected with her that he had suffered himself to form, or had entertained unconsciously, seemed to fall at his feet, withered and dead. Every charm with which his memory or imagination had surrounded her, presented itself before him, only to heighten his anguish and add new bitterness to his despair. Every feeling of sympathy for her forlorn condition, and of admiration for her heroism and fortitude, aggravated the indignation which shook him in every limb, and swelled his heart almost to bursting.\n\nBut, if Nicholas’s own heart embarrassed him, Newman’s came to his relief. There was so much earnestness in his remonstrance, and such sincerity and fervour in his manner, odd and ludicrous as it always was, that it imparted to Nicholas new firmness, and enabled him to say, after he had walked on for some little way in silence:\n\n‘You read me a good lesson, Newman, and I will profit by it. One step, at least, I may take--am bound to take indeed--and to that I will apply myself tomorrow.’\n\n‘What is that?’ asked Noggs wistfully. ‘Not to threaten Ralph? Not to see the father?’\n\n‘To see the daughter, Newman,’ replied Nicholas. ‘To do what, after all, is the utmost that the brothers could do, if they were here, as Heaven send they were! To reason with her upon this hideous union, to point out to her all the horrors to which she is hastening; rashly, it may be, and without due reflection. To entreat her, at least, to pause. She can have had no counsellor for her good. Perhaps even I may move her so far yet, though it is the eleventh hour, and she upon the very brink of ruin.’\n\n‘Bravely spoken!’ said Newman. ‘Well done, well done! Yes. Very good.’\n\n‘And I do declare,’ cried Nicholas, with honest enthusiasm, ‘that in this effort I am influenced by no selfish or personal considerations, but by pity for her, and detestation and abhorrence of this scheme; and that I would do the same, were there twenty rivals in the field, and I the last and least favoured of them all.’\n\n‘You would, I believe,’ said Newman. ‘But where are you hurrying now?’\n\n‘Homewards,’ answered Nicholas. ‘Do you come with me, or I shall say good-night?’\n\n‘I’ll come a little way, if you will but walk: not run,’ said Noggs.\n\n‘I cannot walk tonight, Newman,’ returned Nicholas, hurriedly. ‘I must move rapidly, or I could not draw my breath. I’ll tell you what I’ve said and done tomorrow.’\n\nWithout waiting for a reply, he darted off at a rapid pace, and, plunging into the crowds which thronged the street, was quickly lost to view.\n\n‘He’s a violent youth at times,’ said Newman, looking after him; ‘and yet I like him for it. There’s cause enough now, or the deuce is in it. Hope! I SAID hope, I think! Ralph Nickleby and Gride with their heads together! And hope for the opposite party! Ho! ho!’\n\nIt was with a very melancholy laugh that Newman Noggs concluded this soliloquy; and it was with a very melancholy shake of the head, and a very rueful countenance, that he turned about, and went plodding on his way.\n\nThis, under ordinary circumstances, would have been to some small tavern or dram-shop; that being his way, in more senses than one. But, Newman was too much interested, and too anxious, to betake himself even to this resource, and so, with many desponding and dismal reflections, went straight home.\n\nIt had come to pass, that afternoon, that Miss Morleena Kenwigs had received an invitation to repair next day, per steamer from Westminster Bridge, unto the Eel-pie Island at Twickenham: there to make merry upon a cold collation, bottled beer, shrub, and shrimps, and to dance in the open air to the music of a locomotive band, conveyed thither for the purpose: the steamer being specially engaged by a dancing-master of extensive connection for the accommodation of his numerous pupils, and the pupils displaying their appreciation of the dancing-master’s services, by purchasing themselves, and inducing their friends to do the like, divers light-blue tickets, entitling them to join the expedition. Of these light-blue tickets, one had been presented by an ambitious neighbour to Miss Morleena Kenwigs, with an invitation to join her daughters; and Mrs. Kenwigs, rightly deeming that the honour of the family was involved in Miss Morleena’s making the most splendid appearance possible on so short a notice, and testifying to the dancing-master that there were other dancing-masters besides him, and to all fathers and mothers present that other people’s children could learn to be genteel besides theirs, had fainted away twice under the magnitude of her preparations, but, upheld by a determination to sustain the family name or perish in the attempt, was still hard at work when Newman Noggs came home.\n\nNow, between the italian-ironing of frills, the flouncing of trousers, the trimming of frocks, the faintings and the comings-to again, incidental to the occasion, Mrs. Kenwigs had been so entirely occupied, that she had not observed, until within half an hour before, that the flaxen tails of Miss Morleena’s hair were, in a manner, run to seed; and that, unless she were put under the hands of a skilful hairdresser, she never could achieve that signal triumph over the daughters of all other people, anything less than which would be tantamount to defeat. This discovery drove Mrs. Kenwigs to despair; for the hairdresser lived three streets and eight dangerous crossings off; Morleena could not be trusted to go there alone, even if such a proceeding were strictly proper: of which Mrs. Kenwigs had her doubts; Mr. Kenwigs had not returned from business; and there was nobody to take her. So, Mrs. Kenwigs first slapped Miss Kenwigs for being the cause of her vexation, and then shed tears.\n\n‘You ungrateful child!’ said Mrs. Kenwigs, ‘after I have gone through what I have, this night, for your good.’\n\n‘I can’t help it, ma,’ replied Morleena, also in tears; ‘my hair WILL grow.’\n\n‘Don’t talk to me, you naughty thing!’ said Mrs. Kenwigs, ‘don’t! Even if I was to trust you by yourself and you were to escape being run over, I know you’d run in to Laura Chopkins,’ who was the daughter of the ambitious neighbour, ‘and tell her what you’re going to wear tomorrow, I know you would. You’ve no proper pride in yourself, and are not to be trusted out of sight for an instant.’\n\nDeploring the evil-mindedness of her eldest daughter in these terms, Mrs Kenwigs distilled fresh drops of vexation from her eyes, and declared that she did believe there never was anybody so tried as she was. Thereupon, Morleena Kenwigs wept afresh, and they bemoaned themselves together.\n\nMatters were at this point, as Newman Noggs was heard to limp past the door on his way upstairs; when Mrs. Kenwigs, gaining new hope from the sound of his footsteps, hastily removed from her countenance as many traces of her late emotion as were effaceable on so short a notice: and presenting herself before him, and representing their dilemma, entreated that he would escort Morleena to the hairdresser’s shop.\n\n‘I wouldn’t ask you, Mr. Noggs,’ said Mrs. Kenwigs, ‘if I didn’t know what a good, kind-hearted creature you are; no, not for worlds. I am a weak constitution, Mr. Noggs, but my spirit would no more let me ask a favour where I thought there was a chance of its being refused, than it would let me submit to see my children trampled down and trod upon, by envy and lowness!’\n\nNewman was too good-natured not to have consented, even without this avowal of confidence on the part of Mrs. Kenwigs. Accordingly, a very few minutes had elapsed, when he and Miss Morleena were on their way to the hairdresser’s.\n\nIt was not exactly a hairdresser’s; that is to say, people of a coarse and vulgar turn of mind might have called it a barber’s; for they not only cut and curled ladies elegantly, and children carefully, but shaved gentlemen easily. Still, it was a highly genteel establishment--quite first-rate in fact--and there were displayed in the window, besides other elegancies, waxen busts of a light lady and a dark gentleman which were the admiration of the whole neighbourhood. Indeed, some ladies had gone so far as to assert, that the dark gentleman was actually a portrait of the spirted young proprietor; and the great similarity between their head-dresses--both wore very glossy hair, with a narrow walk straight down the middle, and a profusion of flat circular curls on both sides--encouraged the idea. The better informed among the sex, however, made light of this assertion, for however willing they were (and they were very willing) to do full justice to the handsome face and figure of the proprietor, they held the countenance of the dark gentleman in the window to be an exquisite and abstract idea of masculine beauty, realised sometimes, perhaps, among angels and military men, but very rarely embodied to gladden the eyes of mortals.\n\nIt was to this establishment that Newman Noggs led Miss Kenwigs in safety. The proprietor, knowing that Miss Kenwigs had three sisters, each with two flaxen tails, and all good for sixpence apiece, once a month at least, promptly deserted an old gentleman whom he had just lathered for shaving, and handing him over to the journeyman, (who was not very popular among the ladies, by reason of his obesity and middle age,) waited on the young lady himself.\n\nJust as this change had been effected, there presented himself for shaving, a big, burly, good-humoured coal-heaver with a pipe in his mouth, who, drawing his hand across his chin, requested to know when a shaver would be disengaged.\n\nThe journeyman, to whom this question was put, looked doubtfully at the young proprietor, and the young proprietor looked scornfully at the coal-heaver: observing at the same time:\n\n‘You won’t get shaved here, my man.’\n\n‘Why not?’ said the coal-heaver.\n\n‘We don’t shave gentlemen in your line,’ remarked the young proprietor.\n\n‘Why, I see you a shaving of a baker, when I was a looking through the winder, last week,’ said the coal-heaver.\n\n‘It’s necessary to draw the line somewheres, my fine feller,’ replied the principal. ‘We draw the line there. We can’t go beyond bakers. If we was to get any lower than bakers, our customers would desert us, and we might shut up shop. You must try some other establishment, sir. We couldn’t do it here.’\n\nThe applicant stared; grinned at Newman Noggs, who appeared highly entertained; looked slightly round the shop, as if in depreciation of the pomatum pots and other articles of stock; took his pipe out of his mouth and gave a very loud whistle; and then put it in again, and walked out.\n\nThe old gentleman who had just been lathered, and who was sitting in a melancholy manner with his face turned towards the wall, appeared quite unconscious of this incident, and to be insensible to everything around him in the depth of a reverie--a very mournful one, to judge from the sighs he occasionally vented--in which he was absorbed. Affected by this example, the proprietor began to clip Miss Kenwigs, the journeyman to scrape the old gentleman, and Newman Noggs to read last Sunday’s paper, all three in silence: when Miss Kenwigs uttered a shrill little scream, and Newman, raising his eyes, saw that it had been elicited by the circumstance of the old gentleman turning his head, and disclosing the features of Mr. Lillyvick the collector.\n\nThe features of Mr. Lillyvick they were, but strangely altered. If ever an old gentleman had made a point of appearing in public, shaved close and clean, that old gentleman was Mr. Lillyvick. If ever a collector had borne himself like a collector, and assumed, before all men, a solemn and portentous dignity as if he had the world on his books and it was all two quarters in arrear, that collector was Mr. Lillyvick. And now, there he sat, with the remains of a beard at least a week old encumbering his chin; a soiled and crumpled shirt-frill crouching, as it were, upon his breast, instead of standing boldly out; a demeanour so abashed and drooping, so despondent, and expressive of such humiliation, grief, and shame; that if the souls of forty unsubstantial housekeepers, all of whom had had their water cut off for non-payment of the rate, could have been concentrated in one body, that one body could hardly have expressed such mortification and defeat as were now expressed in the person of Mr. Lillyvick the collector.\n\nNewman Noggs uttered his name, and Mr. Lillyvick groaned: then coughed to hide it. But the groan was a full-sized groan, and the cough was but a wheeze.\n\n‘Is anything the matter?’ said Newman Noggs.\n\n‘Matter, sir!’ cried Mr. Lillyvick. ‘The plug of life is dry, sir, and but the mud is left.’\n\nThis speech--the style of which Newman attributed to Mr. Lillyvick’s recent association with theatrical characters--not being quite explanatory, Newman looked as if he were about to ask another question, when Mr. Lillyvick prevented him by shaking his hand mournfully, and then waving his own.\n\n‘Let me be shaved!’ said Mr. Lillyvick. ‘It shall be done before Morleena; it IS Morleena, isn’t it?’\n\n‘Yes,’ said Newman.\n\n‘Kenwigses have got a boy, haven’t they?’ inquired the collector.\n\nAgain Newman said ‘Yes.’\n\n‘Is it a nice boy?’ demanded the collector.\n\n‘It ain’t a very nasty one,’ returned Newman, rather embarrassed by the question.\n\n‘Susan Kenwigs used to say,’ observed the collector, ‘that if ever she had another boy, she hoped it might be like me. Is this one like me, Mr Noggs?’\n\nThis was a puzzling inquiry; but Newman evaded it, by replying to Mr Lillyvick, that he thought the baby might possibly come like him in time.\n\n‘I should be glad to have somebody like me, somehow,’ said Mr. Lillyvick, ‘before I die.’\n\n‘You don’t mean to do that, yet awhile?’ said Newman.\n\nUnto which Mr. Lillyvick replied in a solemn voice, ‘Let me be shaved!’ and again consigning himself to the hands of the journeyman, said no more.\n\nThis was remarkable behaviour. So remarkable did it seem to Miss Morleena, that that young lady, at the imminent hazard of having her ear sliced off, had not been able to forbear looking round, some score of times, during the foregoing colloquy. Of her, however, Mr. Lillyvick took no notice: rather striving (so, at least, it seemed to Newman Noggs) to evade her observation, and to shrink into himself whenever he attracted her regards. Newman wondered very much what could have occasioned this altered behaviour on the part of the collector; but, philosophically reflecting that he would most likely know, sooner or later, and that he could perfectly afford to wait, he was very little disturbed by the singularity of the old gentleman’s deportment.\n\nThe cutting and curling being at last concluded, the old gentleman, who had been some time waiting, rose to go, and, walking out with Newman and his charge, took Newman’s arm, and proceeded for some time without making any observation. Newman, who in power of taciturnity was excelled by few people, made no attempt to break silence; and so they went on, until they had very nearly reached Miss Morleena’s home, when Mr Lillyvick said:\n\n‘Were the Kenwigses very much overpowered, Mr. Noggs, by that news?’\n\n‘What news?’ returned Newman.\n\n‘That about--my--being--’\n\n‘Married?’ suggested Newman.\n\n‘Ah!’ replied Mr. Lillyvick, with another groan; this time not even disguised by a wheeze.\n\n‘It made ma cry when she knew it,’ interposed Miss Morleena, ‘but we kept it from her for a long time; and pa was very low in his spirits, but he is better now; and I was very ill, but I am better too.’\n\n‘Would you give your great-uncle Lillyvick a kiss if he was to ask you, Morleena?’ said the collector, with some hesitation.\n\n‘Yes; uncle Lillyvick, I would,’ returned Miss Morleena, with the energy of both her parents combined; ‘but not aunt Lillyvick. She’s not an aunt of mine, and I’ll never call her one.’\n\nImmediately upon the utterance of these words, Mr. Lillyvick caught Miss Morleena up in his arms, and kissed her; and, being by this time at the door of the house where Mr. Kenwigs lodged (which, as has been before mentioned, usually stood wide open), he walked straight up into Mr Kenwigs’s sitting-room, and put Miss Morleena down in the midst. Mr. and Mrs. Kenwigs were at supper. At sight of their perjured relative, Mrs Kenwigs turned faint and pale, and Mr. Kenwigs rose majestically.\n\n‘Kenwigs,’ said the collector, ‘shake hands.’\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, ‘the time has been, when I was proud to shake hands with such a man as that man as now surweys me. The time has been, sir,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, ‘when a wisit from that man has excited in me and my family’s boozums sensations both nateral and awakening. But, now, I look upon that man with emotions totally surpassing everythink, and I ask myself where is his Honour, where is his straight-for’ardness, and where is his human natur?’\n\n‘Susan Kenwigs,’ said Mr. Lillyvick, turning humbly to his niece, ‘don’t you say anything to me?’\n\n‘She is not equal to it, sir,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, striking the table emphatically. ‘What with the nursing of a healthy babby, and the reflections upon your cruel conduct, four pints of malt liquor a day is hardly able to sustain her.’\n\n‘I am glad,’ said the poor collector meekly, ‘that the baby is a healthy one. I am very glad of that.’\n\nThis was touching the Kenwigses on their tenderest point. Mrs. Kenwigs instantly burst into tears, and Mr. Kenwigs evinced great emotion.\n\n‘My pleasantest feeling, all the time that child was expected,’ said Mr Kenwigs, mournfully, ‘was a thinking, “If it’s a boy, as I hope it may be; for I have heard its uncle Lillyvick say again and again he would prefer our having a boy next, if it’s a boy, what will his uncle Lillyvick say? What will he like him to be called? Will he be Peter, or Alexander, or Pompey, or Diorgeenes, or what will he be?” And now when I look at him; a precious, unconscious, helpless infant, with no use in his little arms but to tear his little cap, and no use in his little legs but to kick his little self--when I see him a lying on his mother’s lap, cooing and cooing, and, in his innocent state, almost a choking hisself with his little fist--when I see him such a infant as he is, and think that that uncle Lillyvick, as was once a-going to be so fond of him, has withdrawed himself away, such a feeling of wengeance comes over me as no language can depicter, and I feel as if even that holy babe was a telling me to hate him.’\n\nThis affecting picture moved Mrs. Kenwigs deeply. After several imperfect words, which vainly attempted to struggle to the surface, but were drowned and washed away by the strong tide of her tears, she spake.\n\n‘Uncle,’ said Mrs. Kenwigs, ‘to think that you should have turned your back upon me and my dear children, and upon Kenwigs which is the author of their being--you who was once so kind and affectionate, and who, if anybody had told us such a thing of, we should have withered with scorn like lightning--you that little Lillyvick, our first and earliest boy, was named after at the very altar! Oh gracious!’\n\n‘Was it money that we cared for?’ said Mr. Kenwigs. ‘Was it property that we ever thought of?’\n\n‘No,’ cried Mrs. Kenwigs, ‘I scorn it.’\n\n‘So do I,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, ‘and always did.’\n\n‘My feelings have been lancerated,’ said Mrs. Kenwigs, ‘my heart has been torn asunder with anguish, I have been thrown back in my confinement, my unoffending infant has been rendered uncomfortable and fractious, Morleena has pined herself away to nothing; all this I forget and forgive, and with you, uncle, I never can quarrel. But never ask me to receive HER, never do it, uncle. For I will not, I will not, I won’t, I won’t, I won’t!’\n\n‘Susan, my dear,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, ‘consider your child.’\n\n‘Yes,’ shrieked Mrs. Kenwigs, ‘I will consider my child! I will consider my child! My own child, that no uncles can deprive me of; my own hated, despised, deserted, cut-off little child.’ And, here, the emotions of Mrs. Kenwigs became so violent, that Mr. Kenwigs was fain to administer hartshorn internally, and vinegar externally, and to destroy a staylace, four petticoat strings, and several small buttons.\n\nNewman had been a silent spectator of this scene; for Mr. Lillyvick had signed to him not to withdraw, and Mr. Kenwigs had further solicited his presence by a nod of invitation. When Mrs. Kenwigs had been, in some degree, restored, and Newman, as a person possessed of some influence with her, had remonstrated and begged her to compose herself, Mr Lillyvick said in a faltering voice:\n\n‘I never shall ask anybody here to receive my--I needn’t mention the word; you know what I mean. Kenwigs and Susan, yesterday was a week she eloped with a half-pay captain!’\n\nMr. and Mrs. Kenwigs started together.\n\n‘Eloped with a half-pay captain,’ repeated Mr. Lillyvick, ‘basely and falsely eloped with a half-pay captain. With a bottle-nosed captain that any man might have considered himself safe from. It was in this room,’ said Mr. Lillyvick, looking sternly round, ‘that I first see Henrietta Petowker. It is in this room that I turn her off, for ever.’\n\nThis declaration completely changed the whole posture of affairs. Mrs. Kenwigs threw herself upon the old gentleman’s neck, bitterly reproaching herself for her late harshness, and exclaiming, if she had suffered, what must his sufferings have been! Mr. Kenwigs grasped his hand, and vowed eternal friendship and remorse. Mrs. Kenwigs was horror-stricken to think that she should ever have nourished in her bosom such a snake, adder, viper, serpent, and base crocodile as Henrietta Petowker. Mr. Kenwigs argued that she must have been bad indeed not to have improved by so long a contemplation of Mrs. Kenwigs’s virtue. Mrs. Kenwigs remembered that Mr. Kenwigs had often said that he was not quite satisfied of the propriety of Miss Petowker’s conduct, and wondered how it was that she could have been blinded by such a wretch. Mr. Kenwigs remembered that he had had his suspicions, but did not wonder why Mrs. Kenwigs had not had hers, as she was all chastity, purity, and truth, and Henrietta all baseness, falsehood, and deceit. And Mr. and Mrs. Kenwigs both said, with strong feelings and tears of sympathy, that everything happened for the best; and conjured the good collector not to give way to unavailing grief, but to seek consolation in the society of those affectionate relations whose arms and hearts were ever open to him.\n\n‘Out of affection and regard for you, Susan and Kenwigs,’ said Mr Lillyvick, ‘and not out of revenge and spite against her, for she is below it, I shall, tomorrow morning, settle upon your children, and make payable to the survivors of them when they come of age of marry, that money that I once meant to leave ‘em in my will. The deed shall be executed tomorrow, and Mr. Noggs shall be one of the witnesses. He hears me promise this, and he shall see it done.’\n\nOverpowered by this noble and generous offer, Mr. Kenwigs, Mrs. Kenwigs, and Miss Morleena Kenwigs, all began to sob together; and the noise of their sobbing, communicating itself to the next room, where the children lay a-bed, and causing them to cry too, Mr. Kenwigs rushed wildly in, and bringing them out in his arms, by two and two, tumbled them down in their nightcaps and gowns at the feet of Mr. Lillyvick, and called upon them to thank and bless him.\n\n‘And now,’ said Mr. Lillyvick, when a heart-rending scene had ensued and the children were cleared away again, ‘give me some supper. This took place twenty mile from town. I came up this morning, and have being lingering about all day, without being able to make up my mind to come and see you. I humoured her in everything, she had her own way, she did just as she pleased, and now she has done this. There was twelve teaspoons and twenty-four pound in sovereigns--I missed them first--it’s a trial--I feel I shall never be able to knock a double knock again, when I go my rounds--don’t say anything more about it, please--the spoons were worth--never mind--never mind!’\n\nWith such muttered outpourings as these, the old gentleman shed a few tears; but, they got him into the elbow-chair, and prevailed upon him, without much pressing, to make a hearty supper, and by the time he had finished his first pipe, and disposed of half-a-dozen glasses out of a crown bowl of punch, ordered by Mr. Kenwigs, in celebration of his return to the bosom of his family, he seemed, though still very humble, quite resigned to his fate, and rather relieved than otherwise by the flight of his wife.\n\n‘When I see that man,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, with one hand round Mrs Kenwigs’s waist: his other hand supporting his pipe (which made him wink and cough very much, for he was no smoker): and his eyes on Morleena, who sat upon her uncle’s knee, ‘when I see that man as mingling, once again, in the spear which he adorns, and see his affections deweloping themselves in legitimate sitiwations, I feel that his nature is as elewated and expanded, as his standing afore society as a public character is unimpeached, and the woices of my infant children purvided for in life, seem to whisper to me softly, “This is an ewent at which Evins itself looks down!”’\n\n# CHAPTER 53\n\nContaining the further Progress of the Plot contrived by Mr. Ralph Nickleby and Mr. Arthur Gride\n\nWith that settled resolution, and steadiness of purpose to which extreme circumstances so often give birth, acting upon far less excitable and more sluggish temperaments than that which was the lot of Madeline Bray’s admirer, Nicholas started, at dawn of day, from the restless couch which no sleep had visited on the previous night, and prepared to make that last appeal, by whose slight and fragile thread her only remaining hope of escape depended.\n\nAlthough, to restless and ardent minds, morning may be the fitting season for exertion and activity, it is not always at that time that hope is strongest or the spirit most sanguine and buoyant. In trying and doubtful positions, youth, custom, a steady contemplation of the difficulties which surround us, and a familiarity with them, imperceptibly diminish our apprehensions and beget comparative indifference, if not a vague and reckless confidence in some relief, the means or nature of which we care not to foresee. But when we come, fresh, upon such things in the morning, with that dark and silent gap between us and yesterday; with every link in the brittle chain of hope, to rivet afresh; our hot enthusiasm subdued, and cool calm reason substituted in its stead; doubt and misgiving revive. As the traveller sees farthest by day, and becomes aware of rugged mountains and trackless plains which the friendly darkness had shrouded from his sight and mind together, so, the wayfarer in the toilsome path of human life sees, with each returning sun, some new obstacle to surmount, some new height to be attained. Distances stretch out before him which, last night, were scarcely taken into account, and the light which gilds all nature with its cheerful beams, seems but to shine upon the weary obstacles that yet lie strewn between him and the grave.\n\nSo thought Nicholas, when, with the impatience natural to a situation like his, he softly left the house, and, feeling as though to remain in bed were to lose most precious time, and to be up and stirring were in some way to promote the end he had in view, wandered into London; perfectly well knowing that for hours to come he could not obtain speech with Madeline, and could do nothing but wish the intervening time away.\n\nAnd, even now, as he paced the streets, and listlessly looked round on the gradually increasing bustle and preparation for the day, everything appeared to yield him some new occasion for despondency. Last night, the sacrifice of a young, affectionate, and beautiful creature, to such a wretch, and in such a cause, had seemed a thing too monstrous to succeed; and the warmer he grew, the more confident he felt that some interposition must save her from his clutches. But now, when he thought how regularly things went on, from day to day, in the same unvarying round; how youth and beauty died, and ugly griping age lived tottering on; how crafty avarice grew rich, and manly honest hearts were poor and sad; how few they were who tenanted the stately houses, and how many of those who lay in noisome pens, or rose each day and laid them down each night, and lived and died, father and son, mother and child, race upon race, and generation upon generation, without a home to shelter them or the energies of one single man directed to their aid; how, in seeking, not a luxurious and splendid life, but the bare means of a most wretched and inadequate subsistence, there were women and children in that one town, divided into classes, numbered and estimated as regularly as the noble families and folks of great degree, and reared from infancy to drive most criminal and dreadful trades; how ignorance was punished and never taught; how jail-doors gaped, and gallows loomed, for thousands urged towards them by circumstances darkly curtaining their very cradles’ heads, and but for which they might have earned their honest bread and lived in peace; how many died in soul, and had no chance of life; how many who could scarcely go astray, be they vicious as they would, turned haughtily from the crushed and stricken wretch who could scarce do otherwise, and who would have been a greater wonder had he or she done well, than even they had they done ill; how much injustice, misery, and wrong, there was, and yet how the world rolled on, from year to year, alike careless and indifferent, and no man seeking to remedy or redress it; when he thought of all this, and selected from the mass the one slight case on which his thoughts were bent, he felt, indeed, that there was little ground for hope, and little reason why it should not form an atom in the huge aggregate of distress and sorrow, and add one small and unimportant unit to swell the great amount.\n\nBut youth is not prone to contemplate the darkest side of a picture it can shift at will. By dint of reflecting on what he had to do, and reviving the train of thought which night had interrupted, Nicholas gradually summoned up his utmost energy, and when the morning was sufficiently advanced for his purpose, had no thought but that of using it to the best advantage. A hasty breakfast taken, and such affairs of business as required prompt attention disposed of, he directed his steps to the residence of Madeline Bray: whither he lost no time in arriving.\n\nIt had occurred to him that, very possibly, the young lady might be denied, although to him she never had been; and he was still pondering upon the surest method of obtaining access to her in that case, when, coming to the door of the house, he found it had been left ajar--probably by the last person who had gone out. The occasion was not one upon which to observe the nicest ceremony; therefore, availing himself of this advantage, Nicholas walked gently upstairs and knocked at the door of the room into which he had been accustomed to be shown. Receiving permission to enter, from some person on the other side, he opened the door and walked in.\n\nBray and his daughter were sitting there alone. It was nearly three weeks since he had seen her last, but there was a change in the lovely girl before him which told Nicholas, in startling terms, how much mental suffering had been compressed into that short time. There are no words which can express, nothing with which can be compared, the perfect pallor, the clear transparent whiteness, of the beautiful face which turned towards him when he entered. Her hair was a rich deep brown, but shading that face, and straying upon a neck that rivalled it in whiteness, it seemed by the strong contrast raven black. Something of wildness and restlessness there was in the dark eye, but there was the same patient look, the same expression of gentle mournfulness which he well remembered, and no trace of a single tear. Most beautiful--more beautiful, perhaps, than ever--there was something in her face which quite unmanned him, and appeared far more touching than the wildest agony of grief. It was not merely calm and composed, but fixed and rigid, as though the violent effort which had summoned that composure beneath her father’s eye, while it mastered all other thoughts, had prevented even the momentary expression they had communicated to the features from subsiding, and had fastened it there, as an evidence of its triumph.\n\nThe father sat opposite to her; not looking directly in her face, but glancing at her, as he talked with a gay air which ill disguised the anxiety of his thoughts. The drawing materials were not on their accustomed table, nor were any of the other tokens of her usual occupations to be seen. The little vases which Nicholas had always seen filled with fresh flowers were empty, or supplied only with a few withered stalks and leaves. The bird was silent. The cloth that covered his cage at night was not removed. His mistress had forgotten him.\n\nThere are times when, the mind being painfully alive to receive impressions, a great deal may be noted at a glance. This was one, for Nicholas had but glanced round him when he was recognised by Mr. Bray, who said impatiently:\n\n‘Now, sir, what do you want? Name your errand here, quickly, if you please, for my daughter and I are busily engaged with other and more important matters than those you come about. Come, sir, address yourself to your business at once.’\n\nNicholas could very well discern that the irritability and impatience of this speech were assumed, and that Bray, in his heart, was rejoiced at any interruption which promised to engage the attention of his daughter. He bent his eyes involuntarily upon the father as he spoke, and marked his uneasiness; for he coloured and turned his head away.\n\nThe device, however, so far as it was a device for causing Madeline to interfere, was successful. She rose, and advancing towards Nicholas paused half-way, and stretched out her hand as expecting a letter.\n\n‘Madeline,’ said her father impatiently, ‘my love, what are you doing?’\n\n‘Miss Bray expects an inclosure perhaps,’ said Nicholas, speaking very distinctly, and with an emphasis she could scarcely misunderstand. ‘My employer is absent from England, or I should have brought a letter with me. I hope she will give me time--a little time. I ask a very little time.’\n\n‘If that is all you come about, sir,’ said Mr. Bray, ‘you may make yourself easy on that head. Madeline, my dear, I didn’t know this person was in your debt?’\n\n‘A--a trifle, I believe,’ returned Madeline, faintly.\n\n‘I suppose you think now,’ said Bray, wheeling his chair round and confronting Nicholas, ‘that, but for such pitiful sums as you bring here, because my daughter has chosen to employ her time as she has, we should starve?’\n\n‘I have not thought about it,’ returned Nicholas.\n\n‘You have not thought about it!’ sneered the invalid. ‘You know you HAVE thought about it, and have thought that, and think so every time you come here. Do you suppose, young man, that I don’t know what little purse-proud tradesmen are, when, through some fortunate circumstances, they get the upper hand for a brief day--or think they get the upper hand--of a gentleman?’\n\n‘My business,’ said Nicholas respectfully, ‘is with a lady.’\n\n‘With a gentleman’s daughter, sir,’ returned the sick man, ‘and the pettifogging spirit is the same. But perhaps you bring ORDERS, eh? Have you any fresh ORDERS for my daughter, sir?’\n\nNicholas understood the tone of triumph in which this interrogatory was put; but remembering the necessity of supporting his assumed character, produced a scrap of paper purporting to contain a list of some subjects for drawings which his employer desired to have executed; and with which he had prepared himself in case of any such contingency.\n\n‘Oh!’ said Mr. Bray. ‘These are the orders, are they?’\n\n‘Since you insist upon the term, sir, yes,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘Then you may tell your master,’ said Bray, tossing the paper back again, with an exulting smile, ‘that my daughter, Miss Madeline Bray, condescends to employ herself no longer in such labours as these; that she is not at his beck and call, as he supposes her to be; that we don’t live upon his money, as he flatters himself we do; that he may give whatever he owes us, to the first beggar that passes his shop, or add it to his own profits next time he calculates them; and that he may go to the devil for me. That’s my acknowledgment of his orders, sir!’\n\n‘And this is the independence of a man who sells his daughter as he has sold that weeping girl!’ thought Nicholas.\n\nThe father was too much absorbed with his own exultation to mark the look of scorn which, for an instant, Nicholas could not have suppressed had he been upon the rack. ‘There,’ he continued, after a short silence, ‘you have your message and can retire--unless you have any further--ha!--any further orders.’\n\n‘I have none,’ said Nicholas; ‘nor, in the consideration of the station you once held, have I used that or any other word which, however harmless in itself, could be supposed to imply authority on my part or dependence on yours. I have no orders, but I have fears--fears that I will express, chafe as you may--fears that you may be consigning that young lady to something worse than supporting you by the labour of her hands, had she worked herself dead. These are my fears, and these fears I found upon your own demeanour. Your conscience will tell you, sir, whether I construe it well or not.’\n\n‘For Heaven’s sake!’ cried Madeline, interposing in alarm between them. ‘Remember, sir, he is ill.’\n\n‘Ill!’ cried the invalid, gasping and catching for breath. ‘Ill! Ill! I am bearded and bullied by a shop-boy, and she beseeches him to pity me and remember I am ill!’\n\nHe fell into a paroxysm of his disorder, so violent that for a few moments Nicholas was alarmed for his life; but finding that he began to recover, he withdrew, after signifying by a gesture to the young lady that he had something important to communicate, and would wait for her outside the room. He could hear that the sick man came gradually, but slowly, to himself, and that without any reference to what had just occurred, as though he had no distinct recollection of it as yet, he requested to be left alone.\n\n‘Oh!’ thought Nicholas, ‘that this slender chance might not be lost, and that I might prevail, if it were but for one week’s time and reconsideration!’\n\n‘You are charged with some commission to me, sir,’ said Madeline, presenting herself in great agitation. ‘Do not press it now, I beg and pray you. The day after tomorrow; come here then.’\n\n‘It will be too late--too late for what I have to say,’ rejoined Nicholas, ‘and you will not be here. Oh, madam, if you have but one thought of him who sent me here, but one last lingering care for your own peace of mind and heart, I do for God’s sake urge you to give me a hearing.’\n\nShe attempted to pass him, but Nicholas gently detained her.\n\n‘A hearing,’ said Nicholas. ‘I ask you but to hear me: not me alone, but him for whom I speak, who is far away and does not know your danger. In the name of Heaven hear me!’\n\nThe poor attendant, with her eyes swollen and red with weeping, stood by; and to her Nicholas appealed in such passionate terms that she opened a side-door, and, supporting her mistress into an adjoining room, beckoned Nicholas to follow them.\n\n‘Leave me, sir, pray,’ said the young lady.\n\n‘I cannot, will not leave you thus,’ returned Nicholas. ‘I have a duty to discharge; and, either here, or in the room from which we have just now come, at whatever risk or hazard to Mr. Bray, I must beseech you to contemplate again the fearful course to which you have been impelled.’\n\n‘What course is this you speak of, and impelled by whom, sir?’ demanded the young lady, with an effort to speak proudly.\n\n‘I speak of this marriage,’ returned Nicholas, ‘of this marriage, fixed for tomorrow, by one who never faltered in a bad purpose, or lent his aid to any good design; of this marriage, the history of which is known to me, better, far better, than it is to you. I know what web is wound about you. I know what men they are from whom these schemes have come. You are betrayed and sold for money; for gold, whose every coin is rusted with tears, if not red with the blood of ruined men, who have fallen desperately by their own mad hands.’\n\n‘You say you have a duty to discharge,’ said Madeline, ‘and so have I. And with the help of Heaven I will perform it.’\n\n‘Say rather with the help of devils,’ replied Nicholas, ‘with the help of men, one of them your destined husband, who are--’\n\n‘I must not hear this,’ cried the young lady, striving to repress a shudder, occasioned, as it seemed, even by this slight allusion to Arthur Gride. ‘This evil, if evil it be, has been of my own seeking. I am impelled to this course by no one, but follow it of my own free will. You see I am not constrained or forced. Report this,’ said Madeline, ‘to my dear friend and benefactor, and, taking with you my prayers and thanks for him and for yourself, leave me for ever!’\n\n‘Not until I have besought you, with all the earnestness and fervour by which I am animated,’ cried Nicholas, ‘to postpone this marriage for one short week. Not until I have besought you to think more deeply than you can have done, influenced as you are, upon the step you are about to take. Although you cannot be fully conscious of the villainy of this man to whom you are about to give your hand, some of his deeds you know. You have heard him speak, and have looked upon his face. Reflect, reflect, before it is too late, on the mockery of plighting to him at the altar, faith in which your heart can have no share--of uttering solemn words, against which nature and reason must rebel--of the degradation of yourself in your own esteem, which must ensue, and must be aggravated every day, as his detested character opens upon you more and more. Shrink from the loathsome companionship of this wretch as you would from corruption and disease. Suffer toil and labour if you will, but shun him, shun him, and be happy. For, believe me, I speak the truth; the most abject poverty, the most wretched condition of human life, with a pure and upright mind, would be happiness to that which you must undergo as the wife of such a man as this!’\n\nLong before Nicholas ceased to speak, the young lady buried her face in her hands, and gave her tears free way. In a voice at first inarticulate with emotion, but gradually recovering strength as she proceeded, she answered him:\n\n‘I will not disguise from you, sir--though perhaps I ought--that I have undergone great pain of mind, and have been nearly broken-hearted since I saw you last. I do NOT love this gentleman. The difference between our ages, tastes, and habits, forbids it. This he knows, and knowing, still offers me his hand. By accepting it, and by that step alone, I can release my father who is dying in this place; prolong his life, perhaps, for many years; restore him to comfort--I may almost call it affluence; and relieve a generous man from the burden of assisting one, by whom, I grieve to say, his noble heart is little understood. Do not think so poorly of me as to believe that I feign a love I do not feel. Do not report so ill of me, for THAT I could not bear. If I cannot, in reason or in nature, love the man who pays this price for my poor hand, I can discharge the duties of a wife: I can be all he seeks in me, and will. He is content to take me as I am. I have passed my word, and should rejoice, not weep, that it is so. I do. The interest you take in one so friendless and forlorn as I, the delicacy with which you have discharged your trust, the faith you have kept with me, have my warmest thanks: and, while I make this last feeble acknowledgment, move me to tears, as you see. But I do not repent, nor am I unhappy. I am happy in the prospect of all I can achieve so easily. I shall be more so when I look back upon it, and all is done, I know.’\n\n‘Your tears fall faster as you talk of happiness,’ said Nicholas, ‘and you shun the contemplation of that dark future which must be laden with so much misery to you. Defer this marriage for a week. For but one week!’\n\n‘He was talking, when you came upon us just now, with such smiles as I remember to have seen of old, and have not seen for many and many a day, of the freedom that was to come tomorrow,’ said Madeline, with momentary firmness, ‘of the welcome change, the fresh air: all the new scenes and objects that would bring fresh life to his exhausted frame. His eye grew bright, and his face lightened at the thought. I will not defer it for an hour.’\n\n‘These are but tricks and wiles to urge you on,’ cried Nicholas.\n\n‘I’ll hear no more,’ said Madeline, hurriedly; ‘I have heard too much--more than I should--already. What I have said to you, sir, I have said as to that dear friend to whom I trust in you honourably to repeat it. Some time hence, when I am more composed and reconciled to my new mode of life, if I should live so long, I will write to him. Meantime, all holy angels shower blessings on his head, and prosper and preserve him.’\n\nShe was hurrying past Nicholas, when he threw himself before her, and implored her to think, but once again, upon the fate to which she was precipitately hastening.\n\n‘There is no retreat,’ said Nicholas, in an agony of supplication; ‘no withdrawing! All regret will be unavailing, and deep and bitter it must be. What can I say, that will induce you to pause at this last moment? What can I do to save you?’\n\n‘Nothing,’ she incoherently replied. ‘This is the hardest trial I have had. Have mercy on me, sir, I beseech, and do not pierce my heart with such appeals as these. I--I hear him calling. I--I--must not, will not, remain here for another instant.’\n\n‘If this were a plot,’ said Nicholas, with the same violent rapidity with which she spoke, ‘a plot, not yet laid bare by me, but which, with time, I might unravel; if you were (not knowing it) entitled to fortune of your own, which, being recovered, would do all that this marriage can accomplish, would you not retract?’\n\n‘No, no, no! It is impossible; it is a child’s tale. Time would bring his death. He is calling again!’\n\n‘It may be the last time we shall ever meet on earth,’ said Nicholas, ‘it may be better for me that we should never meet more.’\n\n‘For both, for both,’ replied Madeline, not heeding what she said. ‘The time will come when to recall the memory of this one interview might drive me mad. Be sure to tell them, that you left me calm and happy. And God be with you, sir, and my grateful heart and blessing!’\n\nShe was gone. Nicholas, staggering from the house, thought of the hurried scene which had just closed upon him, as if it were the phantom of some wild, unquiet dream. The day wore on; at night, having been enabled in some measure to collect his thoughts, he issued forth again.\n\nThat night, being the last of Arthur Gride’s bachelorship, found him in tiptop spirits and great glee. The bottle-green suit had been brushed, ready for the morrow. Peg Sliderskew had rendered the accounts of her past housekeeping; the eighteen-pence had been rigidly accounted for (she was never trusted with a larger sum at once, and the accounts were not usually balanced more than twice a day); every preparation had been made for the coming festival; and Arthur might have sat down and contemplated his approaching happiness, but that he preferred sitting down and contemplating the entries in a dirty old vellum-book with rusty clasps.\n\n‘Well-a-day!’ he chuckled, as sinking on his knees before a strong chest screwed down to the floor, he thrust in his arm nearly up to the shoulder, and slowly drew forth this greasy volume. ‘Well-a-day now, this is all my library, but it’s one of the most entertaining books that were ever written! It’s a delightful book, and all true and real--that’s the best of it--true as the Bank of England, and real as its gold and silver. Written by Arthur Gride. He, he, he! None of your storybook writers will ever make as good a book as this, I warrant me. It’s composed for private circulation, for my own particular reading, and nobody else’s. He, he, he!’\n\nMuttering this soliloquy, Arthur carried his precious volume to the table, and, adjusting it upon a dusty desk, put on his spectacles, and began to pore among the leaves.\n\n‘It’s a large sum to Mr. Nickleby,’ he said, in a dolorous voice. ‘Debt to be paid in full, nine hundred and seventy-five, four, three. Additional sum as per bond, five hundred pound. One thousand, four hundred and seventy-five pounds, four shillings, and threepence, tomorrow at twelve o’clock. On the other side, though, there’s the PER CONTRA, by means of this pretty chick. But, again, there’s the question whether I mightn’t have brought all this about, myself. “Faint heart never won fair lady.” Why was my heart so faint? Why didn’t I boldly open it to Bray myself, and save one thousand four hundred and seventy-five, four, three?’\n\nThese reflections depressed the old usurer so much, as to wring a feeble groan or two from his breast, and cause him to declare, with uplifted hands, that he would die in a workhouse. Remembering on further cogitation, however, that under any circumstances he must have paid, or handsomely compounded for, Ralph’s debt, and being by no means confident that he would have succeeded had he undertaken his enterprise alone, he regained his equanimity, and chattered and mowed over more satisfactory items, until the entrance of Peg Sliderskew interrupted him.\n\n‘Aha, Peg!’ said Arthur, ‘what is it? What is it now, Peg?’\n\n‘It’s the fowl,’ replied Peg, holding up a plate containing a little, a very little one. Quite a phenomenon of a fowl. So very small and skinny.\n\n‘A beautiful bird!’ said Arthur, after inquiring the price, and finding it proportionate to the size. ‘With a rasher of ham, and an egg made into sauce, and potatoes, and greens, and an apple pudding, Peg, and a little bit of cheese, we shall have a dinner for an emperor. There’ll only be she and me--and you, Peg, when we’ve done.’\n\n‘Don’t you complain of the expense afterwards,’ said Mrs. Sliderskew, sulkily.\n\n‘I am afraid we must live expensively for the first week,’ returned Arthur, with a groan, ‘and then we must make up for it. I won’t eat more than I can help, and I know you love your old master too much to eat more than YOU can help, don’t you, Peg?’\n\n‘Don’t I what?’ said Peg.\n\n‘Love your old master too much--’\n\n‘No, not a bit too much,’ said Peg.\n\n‘Oh, dear, I wish the devil had this woman!’ cried Arthur: ‘love him too much to eat more than you can help at his expense.’\n\n‘At his what?’ said Peg.\n\n‘Oh dear! she can never hear the most important word, and hears all the others!’ whined Gride. ‘At his expense--you catamaran!’\n\nThe last-mentioned tribute to the charms of Mrs. Sliderskew being uttered in a whisper, that lady assented to the general proposition by a harsh growl, which was accompanied by a ring at the street-door.\n\n‘There’s the bell,’ said Arthur.\n\n‘Ay, ay; I know that,’ rejoined Peg.\n\n‘Then why don’t you go?’ bawled Arthur.\n\n‘Go where?’ retorted Peg. ‘I ain’t doing any harm here, am I?’\n\nArthur Gride in reply repeated the word ‘bell’ as loud as he could roar; and, his meaning being rendered further intelligible to Mrs. Sliderskew’s dull sense of hearing by pantomime expressive of ringing at a street-door, Peg hobbled out, after sharply demanding why he hadn’t said there was a ring before, instead of talking about all manner of things that had nothing to do with it, and keeping her half-pint of beer waiting on the steps.\n\n‘There’s a change come over you, Mrs. Peg,’ said Arthur, following her out with his eyes. ‘What it means I don’t quite know; but, if it lasts, we shan’t agree together long I see. You are turning crazy, I think. If you are, you must take yourself off, Mrs. Peg--or be taken off. All’s one to me.’ Turning over the leaves of his book as he muttered this, he soon lighted upon something which attracted his attention, and forgot Peg Sliderskew and everything else in the engrossing interest of its pages.\n\nThe room had no other light than that which it derived from a dim and dirt-clogged lamp, whose lazy wick, being still further obscured by a dark shade, cast its feeble rays over a very little space, and left all beyond in heavy shadow. This lamp the money-lender had drawn so close to him, that there was only room between it and himself for the book over which he bent; and as he sat, with his elbows on the desk, and his sharp cheek-bones resting on his hands, it only served to bring out his ugly features in strong relief, together with the little table at which he sat, and to shroud all the rest of the chamber in a deep sullen gloom. Raising his eyes, and looking vacantly into this gloom as he made some mental calculation, Arthur Gride suddenly met the fixed gaze of a man.\n\n‘Thieves! thieves!’ shrieked the usurer, starting up and folding his book to his breast. ‘Robbers! Murder!’\n\n‘What is the matter?’ said the form, advancing.\n\n‘Keep off!’ cried the trembling wretch. ‘Is it a man or a--a--’\n\n‘For what do you take me, if not for a man?’ was the inquiry.\n\n‘Yes, yes,’ cried Arthur Gride, shading his eyes with his hand, ‘it is a man, and not a spirit. It is a man. Robbers! robbers!’\n\n‘For what are these cries raised? Unless indeed you know me, and have some purpose in your brain?’ said the stranger, coming close up to him. ‘I am no thief.’\n\n‘What then, and how come you here?’ cried Gride, somewhat reassured, but still retreating from his visitor: ‘what is your name, and what do you want?’\n\n‘My name you need not know,’ was the reply. ‘I came here, because I was shown the way by your servant. I have addressed you twice or thrice, but you were too profoundly engaged with your book to hear me, and I have been silently waiting until you should be less abstracted. What I want I will tell you, when you can summon up courage enough to hear and understand me.’\n\nArthur Gride, venturing to regard his visitor more attentively, and perceiving that he was a young man of good mien and bearing, returned to his seat, and muttering that there were bad characters about, and that this, with former attempts upon his house, had made him nervous, requested his visitor to sit down. This, however, he declined.\n\n‘Good God! I don’t stand up to have you at an advantage,’ said Nicholas (for Nicholas it was), as he observed a gesture of alarm on the part of Gride. ‘Listen to me. You are to be married tomorrow morning.’\n\n‘N--n--no,’ rejoined Gride. ‘Who said I was? How do you know that?’\n\n‘No matter how,’ replied Nicholas, ‘I know it. The young lady who is to give you her hand hates and despises you. Her blood runs cold at the mention of your name; the vulture and the lamb, the rat and the dove, could not be worse matched than you and she. You see I know her.’\n\nGride looked at him as if he were petrified with astonishment, but did not speak; perhaps lacking the power.\n\n‘You and another man, Ralph Nickleby by name, have hatched this plot between you,’ pursued Nicholas. ‘You pay him for his share in bringing about this sale of Madeline Bray. You do. A lie is trembling on your lips, I see.’\n\nHe paused; but, Arthur making no reply, resumed again.\n\n‘You pay yourself by defrauding her. How or by what means--for I scorn to sully her cause by falsehood or deceit--I do not know; at present I do not know, but I am not alone or single-handed in this business. If the energy of man can compass the discovery of your fraud and treachery before your death; if wealth, revenge, and just hatred, can hunt and track you through your windings; you will yet be called to a dear account for this. We are on the scent already; judge you, who know what we do not, when we shall have you down!’\n\nHe paused again, and still Arthur Gride glared upon him in silence.\n\n‘If you were a man to whom I could appeal with any hope of touching his compassion or humanity,’ said Nicholas, ‘I would urge upon you to remember the helplessness, the innocence, the youth, of this lady; her worth and beauty, her filial excellence, and last, and more than all, as concerning you more nearly, the appeal she has made to your mercy and your manly feeling. But, I take the only ground that can be taken with men like you, and ask what money will buy you off. Remember the danger to which you are exposed. You see I know enough to know much more with very little help. Bate some expected gain for the risk you save, and say what is your price.’\n\nOld Arthur Gride moved his lips, but they only formed an ugly smile and were motionless again.\n\n‘You think,’ said Nicholas, ‘that the price would not be paid. Miss Bray has wealthy friends who would coin their very hearts to save her in such a strait as this. Name your price, defer these nuptials for but a few days, and see whether those I speak of, shrink from the payment. Do you hear me?’\n\nWhen Nicholas began, Arthur Gride’s impression was, that Ralph Nickleby had betrayed him; but, as he proceeded, he felt convinced that however he had come by the knowledge he possessed, the part he acted was a genuine one, and that with Ralph he had no concern. All he seemed to know, for certain, was, that he, Gride, paid Ralph’s debt; but that, to anybody who knew the circumstances of Bray’s detention--even to Bray himself, on Ralph’s own statement--must be perfectly notorious. As to the fraud on Madeline herself, his visitor knew so little about its nature or extent, that it might be a lucky guess, or a hap-hazard accusation. Whether or no, he had clearly no key to the mystery, and could not hurt him who kept it close within his own breast. The allusion to friends, and the offer of money, Gride held to be mere empty vapouring, for purposes of delay. ‘And even if money were to be had,’ thought Arthur Gride, as he glanced at Nicholas, and trembled with passion at his boldness and audacity, ‘I’d have that dainty chick for my wife, and cheat YOU of her, young smooth-face!’\n\nLong habit of weighing and noting well what clients said, and nicely balancing chances in his mind and calculating odds to their faces, without the least appearance of being so engaged, had rendered Gride quick in forming conclusions, and arriving, from puzzling, intricate, and often contradictory premises, at very cunning deductions. Hence it was that, as Nicholas went on, he followed him closely with his own constructions, and, when he ceased to speak, was as well prepared as if he had deliberated for a fortnight.\n\n‘I hear you,’ he cried, starting from his seat, casting back the fastenings of the window-shutters, and throwing up the sash. ‘Help here! Help! Help!’\n\n‘What are you doing?’ said Nicholas, seizing him by the arm.\n\n‘I’ll cry robbers, thieves, murder, alarm the neighbourhood, struggle with you, let loose some blood, and swear you came to rob me, if you don’t quit my house,’ replied Gride, drawing in his head with a frightful grin, ‘I will!’\n\n‘Wretch!’ cried Nicholas.\n\n‘YOU’LL bring your threats here, will you?’ said Gride, whom jealousy of Nicholas and a sense of his own triumph had converted into a perfect fiend. ‘You, the disappointed lover? Oh dear! He! he! he! But you shan’t have her, nor she you. She’s my wife, my doting little wife. Do you think she’ll miss you? Do you think she’ll weep? I shall like to see her weep, I shan’t mind it. She looks prettier in tears.’\n\n‘Villain!’ said Nicholas, choking with his rage.\n\n‘One minute more,’ cried Arthur Gride, ‘and I’ll rouse the street with such screams, as, if they were raised by anybody else, should wake me even in the arms of pretty Madeline.’\n\n‘You hound!’ said Nicholas. ‘If you were but a younger man--’\n\n‘Oh yes!’ sneered Arthur Gride, ‘If I was but a younger man it wouldn’t be so bad; but for me, so old and ugly! To be jilted by little Madeline for me!’\n\n‘Hear me,’ said Nicholas, ‘and be thankful I have enough command over myself not to fling you into the street, which no aid could prevent my doing if I once grappled with you. I have been no lover of this lady’s. No contract or engagement, no word of love, has ever passed between us. She does not even know my name.’\n\n‘I’ll ask it for all that. I’ll beg it of her with kisses,’ said Arthur Gride. ‘Yes, and she’ll tell me, and pay them back, and we’ll laugh together, and hug ourselves, and be very merry, when we think of the poor youth that wanted to have her, but couldn’t because she was bespoke by me!’\n\nThis taunt brought such an expression into the face of Nicholas, that Arthur Gride plainly apprehended it to be the forerunner of his putting his threat of throwing him into the street in immediate execution; for he thrust his head out of the window, and holding tight on with both hands, raised a pretty brisk alarm. Not thinking it necessary to abide the issue of the noise, Nicholas gave vent to an indignant defiance, and stalked from the room and from the house. Arthur Gride watched him across the street, and then, drawing in his head, fastened the window as before, and sat down to take breath.\n\n‘If she ever turns pettish or ill-humoured, I’ll taunt her with that spark,’ he said, when he had recovered. ‘She’ll little think I know about him; and, if I manage it well, I can break her spirit by this means and have her under my thumb. I’m glad nobody came. I didn’t call too loud. The audacity to enter my house, and open upon me! But I shall have a very good triumph tomorrow, and he’ll be gnawing his fingers off: perhaps drown himself or cut his throat! I shouldn’t wonder! That would make it quite complete, that would: quite.’\n\nWhen he had become restored to his usual condition by these and other comments on his approaching triumph, Arthur Gride put away his book, and, having locked the chest with great caution, descended into the kitchen to warn Peg Sliderskew to bed, and scold her for having afforded such ready admission to a stranger.\n\nThe unconscious Peg, however, not being able to comprehend the offence of which she had been guilty, he summoned her to hold the light, while he made a tour of the fastenings, and secured the street-door with his own hands.\n\n‘Top bolt,’ muttered Arthur, fastening as he spoke, ‘bottom bolt, chain, bar, double lock, and key out to put under my pillow! So, if any more rejected admirers come, they may come through the keyhole. And now I’ll go to sleep till half-past five, when I must get up to be married, Peg!’\n\nWith that, he jocularly tapped Mrs. Sliderskew under the chin, and appeared, for the moment, inclined to celebrate the close of his bachelor days by imprinting a kiss on her shrivelled lips. Thinking better of it, however, he gave her chin another tap, in lieu of that warmer familiarity, and stole away to bed.\n\nThe Crisis of the Project and its Result\nThere are not many men who lie abed too late, or oversleep themselves, on their wedding morning. A legend there is of somebody remarkable for absence of mind, who opened his eyes upon the day which was to give him a young wife, and forgetting all about the matter, rated his servants for providing him with such fine clothes as had been prepared for the festival. There is also a legend of a young gentleman, who, not having before his eyes the fear of the canons of the church for such cases made and provided, conceived a passion for his grandmother. Both cases are of a singular and special kind and it is very doubtful whether either can be considered as a precedent likely to be extensively followed by succeeding generations.\n\nArthur Gride had enrobed himself in his marriage garments of bottle-green, a full hour before Mrs. Sliderskew, shaking off her more heavy slumbers, knocked at his chamber door; and he had hobbled downstairs in full array and smacked his lips over a scanty taste of his favourite cordial, ere that delicate piece of antiquity enlightened the kitchen with her presence.\n\n‘Faugh!’ said Peg, grubbing, in the discharge of her domestic functions, among a scanty heap of ashes in the rusty grate. ‘Wedding indeed! A precious wedding! He wants somebody better than his old Peg to take care of him, does he? And what has he said to me, many and many a time, to keep me content with short food, small wages, and little fire? “My will, Peg! my will!” says he: “I’m a bachelor--no friends--no relations, Peg.” Lies! And now he’s to bring home a new mistress, a baby-faced chit of a girl! If he wanted a wife, the fool, why couldn’t he have one suitable to his age, and that knew his ways? She won’t come in MY way, he says. No, that she won’t, but you little think why, Arthur boy!’\n\nWhile Mrs. Sliderskew, influenced possibly by some lingering feelings of disappointment and personal slight, occasioned by her old master’s preference for another, was giving loose to these grumblings below stairs, Arthur Gride was cogitating in the parlour upon what had taken place last night.\n\n‘I can’t think how he can have picked up what he knows,’ said Arthur, ‘unless I have committed myself--let something drop at Bray’s, for instance--which has been overheard. Perhaps I may. I shouldn’t be surprised if that was it. Mr. Nickleby was often angry at my talking to him before we got outside the door. I mustn’t tell him that part of the business, or he’ll put me out of sorts, and make me nervous for the day.’\n\nRalph was universally looked up to, and recognised among his fellows as a superior genius, but upon Arthur Gride his stern unyielding character and consummate art had made so deep an impression, that he was actually afraid of him. Cringing and cowardly to the core by nature, Arthur Gride humbled himself in the dust before Ralph Nickleby, and, even when they had not this stake in common, would have licked his shoes and crawled upon the ground before him rather than venture to return him word for word, or retort upon him in any other spirit than one of the most slavish and abject sycophancy.\n\nTo Ralph Nickleby’s, Arthur Gride now betook himself according to appointment; and to Ralph Nickleby he related how, last night, some young blustering blade, whom he had never seen, forced his way into his house, and tried to frighten him from the proposed nuptials. Told, in short, what Nicholas had said and done, with the slight reservation upon which he had determined.\n\n‘Well, and what then?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Oh! nothing more,’ rejoined Gride.\n\n‘He tried to frighten you,’ said Ralph, ‘and you WERE frightened I suppose; is that it?’\n\n‘I frightened HIM by crying thieves and murder,’ replied Gride. ‘Once I was in earnest, I tell you that, for I had more than half a mind to swear he uttered threats, and demanded my life or my money.’\n\n‘Oho!’ said Ralph, eyeing him askew. ‘Jealous too!’\n\n‘Dear now, see that!’ cried Arthur, rubbing his hands and affecting to laugh.\n\n‘Why do you make those grimaces, man?’ said Ralph; ‘you ARE jealous--and with good cause I think.’\n\n‘No, no, no; not with good cause, hey? You don’t think with good cause, do you?’ cried Arthur, faltering. ‘Do you though, hey?’\n\n‘Why, how stands the fact?’ returned Ralph. ‘Here is an old man about to be forced in marriage upon a girl; and to this old man there comes a handsome young fellow--you said he was handsome, didn’t you?’\n\n‘No!’ snarled Arthur Gride.\n\n‘Oh!’ rejoined Ralph, ‘I thought you did. Well! Handsome or not handsome, to this old man there comes a young fellow who casts all manner of fierce defiances in his teeth--gums I should rather say--and tells him in plain terms that his mistress hates him. What does he do that for? Philanthropy’s sake?’\n\n‘Not for love of the lady,’ replied Gride, ‘for he said that no word of love--his very words--had ever passed between ‘em.’\n\n‘He said!’ repeated Ralph, contemptuously. ‘But I like him for one thing, and that is, his giving you this fair warning to keep your--what is it?--Tit-tit or dainty chick--which?--under lock and key. Be careful, Gride, be careful. It’s a triumph, too, to tear her away from a gallant young rival: a great triumph for an old man! It only remains to keep her safe when you have her--that’s all.’\n\n‘What a man it is!’ cried Arthur Gride, affecting, in the extremity of his torture, to be highly amused. And then he added, anxiously, ‘Yes; to keep her safe, that’s all. And that isn’t much, is it?’\n\n‘Much!’ said Ralph, with a sneer. ‘Why, everybody knows what easy things to understand and to control, women are. But come, it’s very nearly time for you to be made happy. You’ll pay the bond now, I suppose, to save us trouble afterwards.’\n\n‘Oh what a man you are!’ croaked Arthur.\n\n‘Why not?’ said Ralph. ‘Nobody will pay you interest for the money, I suppose, between this and twelve o’clock; will they?’\n\n‘But nobody would pay you interest for it either, you know,’ returned Arthur, leering at Ralph with all the cunning and slyness he could throw into his face.\n\n‘Besides which,’ said Ralph, suffering his lip to curl into a smile, ‘you haven’t the money about you, and you weren’t prepared for this, or you’d have brought it with you; and there’s nobody you’d so much like to accommodate as me. I see. We trust each other in about an equal degree. Are you ready?’\n\nGride, who had done nothing but grin, and nod, and chatter, during this last speech of Ralph’s, answered in the affirmative; and, producing from his hat a couple of large white favours, pinned one on his breast, and with considerable difficulty induced his friend to do the like. Thus accoutred, they got into a hired coach which Ralph had in waiting, and drove to the residence of the fair and most wretched bride.\n\nGride, whose spirits and courage had gradually failed him more and more as they approached nearer and nearer to the house, was utterly dismayed and cowed by the mournful silence which pervaded it. The face of the poor servant girl, the only person they saw, was disfigured with tears and want of sleep. There was nobody to receive or welcome them; and they stole upstairs into the usual sitting-room, more like two burglars than the bridegroom and his friend.\n\n‘One would think,’ said Ralph, speaking, in spite of himself, in a low and subdued voice, ‘that there was a funeral going on here, and not a wedding.’\n\n‘He, he!’ tittered his friend, ‘you are so--so very funny!’\n\n‘I need be,’ remarked Ralph, drily, ‘for this is rather dull and chilling. Look a little brisker, man, and not so hangdog like!’\n\n‘Yes, yes, I will,’ said Gride. ‘But--but--you don’t think she’s coming just yet, do you?’\n\n‘Why, I suppose she’ll not come till she is obliged,’ returned Ralph, looking at his watch, ‘and she has a good half-hour to spare yet. Curb your impatience.’\n\n‘I--I--am not impatient,’ stammered Arthur. ‘I wouldn’t be hard with her for the world. Oh dear, dear, not on any account. Let her take her time--her own time. Her time shall be ours by all means.’\n\nWhile Ralph bent upon his trembling friend a keen look, which showed that he perfectly understood the reason of this great consideration and regard, a footstep was heard upon the stairs, and Bray himself came into the room on tiptoe, and holding up his hand with a cautious gesture, as if there were some sick person near, who must not be disturbed.\n\n‘Hush!’ he said, in a low voice. ‘She was very ill last night. I thought she would have broken her heart. She is dressed, and crying bitterly in her own room; but she’s better, and quite quiet. That’s everything!’\n\n‘She is ready, is she?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Quite ready,’ returned the father.\n\n‘And not likely to delay us by any young-lady weaknesses--fainting, or so forth?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘She may be safely trusted now,’ returned Bray. ‘I have been talking to her this morning. Here! Come a little this way.’\n\nHe drew Ralph Nickleby to the further end of the room, and pointed towards Gride, who sat huddled together in a corner, fumbling nervously with the buttons of his coat, and exhibiting a face, of which every skulking and base expression was sharpened and aggravated to the utmost by his anxiety and trepidation.\n\n‘Look at that man,’ whispered Bray, emphatically. ‘This seems a cruel thing, after all.’\n\n‘What seems a cruel thing?’ inquired Ralph, with as much stolidity of face, as if he really were in utter ignorance of the other’s meaning.\n\n‘This marriage,’ answered Bray. ‘Don’t ask me what. You know as well as I do.’\n\nRalph shrugged his shoulders, in silent deprecation of Bray’s impatience, and elevated his eyebrows, and pursed his lips, as men do when they are prepared with a sufficient answer to some remark, but wait for a more favourable opportunity of advancing it, or think it scarcely worth while to answer their adversary at all.\n\n‘Look at him. Does it not seem cruel?’ said Bray.\n\n‘No!’ replied Ralph, boldly.\n\n‘I say it does,’ retorted Bray, with a show of much irritation. ‘It is a cruel thing, by all that’s bad and treacherous!’\n\nWhen men are about to commit, or to sanction the commission of some injustice, it is not uncommon for them to express pity for the object either of that or some parallel proceeding, and to feel themselves, at the time, quite virtuous and moral, and immensely superior to those who express no pity at all. This is a kind of upholding of faith above works, and is very comfortable. To do Ralph Nickleby justice, he seldom practised this sort of dissimulation; but he understood those who did, and therefore suffered Bray to say, again and again, with great vehemence, that they were jointly doing a very cruel thing, before he again offered to interpose a word.\n\n‘You see what a dry, shrivelled, withered old chip it is,’ returned Ralph, when the other was at length silent. ‘If he were younger, it might be cruel, but as it is--harkee, Mr. Bray, he’ll die soon, and leave her a rich young widow! Miss Madeline consults your tastes this time; let her consult her own next.’\n\n‘True, true,’ said Bray, biting his nails, and plainly very ill at ease. ‘I couldn’t do anything better for her than advise her to accept these proposals, could I? Now, I ask you, Nickleby, as a man of the world; could I?’\n\n‘Surely not,’ answered Ralph. ‘I tell you what, sir; there are a hundred fathers, within a circuit of five miles from this place; well off; good, rich, substantial men; who would gladly give their daughters, and their own ears with them, to that very man yonder, ape and mummy as he looks.’\n\n‘So there are!’ exclaimed Bray, eagerly catching at anything which seemed a justification of himself. ‘And so I told her, both last night and today.’\n\n‘You told her truth,’ said Ralph, ‘and did well to do so; though I must say, at the same time, that if I had a daughter, and my freedom, pleasure, nay, my very health and life, depended on her taking a husband whom I pointed out, I should hope it would not be necessary to advance any other arguments to induce her to consent to my wishes.’\n\nBray looked at Ralph as if to see whether he spoke in earnest, and having nodded twice or thrice in unqualified assent to what had fallen from him, said:\n\n‘I must go upstairs for a few minutes, to finish dressing. When I come down, I’ll bring Madeline with me. Do you know, I had a very strange dream last night, which I have not remembered till this instant. I dreamt that it was this morning, and you and I had been talking as we have been this minute; that I went upstairs, for the very purpose for which I am going now; and that as I stretched out my hand to take Madeline’s, and lead her down, the floor sunk with me, and after falling from such an indescribable and tremendous height as the imagination scarcely conceives, except in dreams, I alighted in a grave.’\n\n‘And you awoke, and found you were lying on your back, or with your head hanging over the bedside, or suffering some pain from indigestion?’ said Ralph. ‘Pshaw, Mr. Bray! Do as I do (you will have the opportunity, now that a constant round of pleasure and enjoyment opens upon you), and, occupying yourself a little more by day, have no time to think of what you dream by night.’\n\nRalph followed him, with a steady look, to the door; and, turning to the bridegroom, when they were again alone, said,\n\n‘Mark my words, Gride, you won’t have to pay HIS annuity very long. You have the devil’s luck in bargains, always. If he is not booked to make the long voyage before many months are past and gone, I wear an orange for a head!’\n\nTo this prophecy, so agreeable to his ears, Arthur returned no answer than a cackle of great delight. Ralph, throwing himself into a chair, they both sat waiting in profound silence. Ralph was thinking, with a sneer upon his lips, on the altered manner of Bray that day, and how soon their fellowship in a bad design had lowered his pride and established a familiarity between them, when his attentive ear caught the rustling of a female dress upon the stairs, and the footstep of a man.\n\n‘Wake up,’ he said, stamping his foot impatiently upon the ground, ‘and be something like life, man, will you? They are here. Urge those dry old bones of yours this way. Quick, man, quick!’\n\nGride shambled forward, and stood, leering and bowing, close by Ralph’s side, when the door opened and there entered in haste--not Bray and his daughter, but Nicholas and his sister Kate.\n\nIf some tremendous apparition from the world of shadows had suddenly presented itself before him, Ralph Nickleby could not have been more thunder-stricken than he was by this surprise. His hands fell powerless by his side, he reeled back; and with open mouth, and a face of ashy paleness, stood gazing at them in speechless rage: his eyes so prominent, and his face so convulsed and changed by the passions which raged within him, that it would have been difficult to recognise in him the same stern, composed, hard-featured man he had been not a minute ago.\n\n‘The man that came to me last night,’ whispered Gride, plucking at his elbow. ‘The man that came to me last night!’\n\n‘I see,’ muttered Ralph, ‘I know! I might have guessed as much before. Across my every path, at every turn, go where I will, do what I may, he comes!’\n\nThe absence of all colour from the face; the dilated nostril; the quivering of the lips which, though set firmly against each other, would not be still; showed what emotions were struggling for the mastery with Nicholas. But he kept them down, and gently pressing Kate’s arm to reassure her, stood erect and undaunted, front to front with his unworthy relative.\n\nAs the brother and sister stood side by side, with a gallant bearing which became them well, a close likeness between them was apparent, which many, had they only seen them apart, might have failed to remark. The air, carriage, and very look and expression of the brother were all reflected in the sister, but softened and refined to the nicest limit of feminine delicacy and attraction. More striking still was some indefinable resemblance, in the face of Ralph, to both. While they had never looked more handsome, nor he more ugly; while they had never held themselves more proudly, nor he shrunk half so low; there never had been a time when this resemblance was so perceptible, or when all the worst characteristics of a face rendered coarse and harsh by evil thoughts were half so manifest as now.\n\n‘Away!’ was the first word he could utter as he literally gnashed his teeth. ‘Away! What brings you here? Liar, scoundrel, dastard, thief!’\n\n‘I come here,’ said Nicholas in a low deep voice, ‘to save your victim if I can. Liar and scoundrel you are, in every action of your life; theft is your trade; and double dastard you must be, or you were not here today. Hard words will not move me, nor would hard blows. Here I stand, and will, till I have done my errand.’\n\n‘Girl!’ said Ralph, ‘retire! We can use force to him, but I would not hurt you if I could help it. Retire, you weak and silly wench, and leave this dog to be dealt with as he deserves.’\n\n‘I will not retire,’ cried Kate, with flashing eyes and the red blood mantling in her cheeks. ‘You will do him no hurt that he will not repay. You may use force with me; I think you will, for I AM a girl, and that would well become you. But if I have a girl’s weakness, I have a woman’s heart, and it is not you who in a cause like this can turn that from its purpose.’\n\n‘And what may your purpose be, most lofty lady?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘To offer to the unhappy subject of your treachery, at this last moment,’ replied Nicholas, ‘a refuge and a home. If the near prospect of such a husband as you have provided will not prevail upon her, I hope she may be moved by the prayers and entreaties of one of her own sex. At all events they shall be tried. I myself, avowing to her father from whom I come and by whom I am commissioned, will render it an act of greater baseness, meanness, and cruelty in him if he still dares to force this marriage on. Here I wait to see him and his daughter. For this I came and brought my sister even into your presence. Our purpose is not to see or speak with you; therefore to you we stoop to say no more.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ said Ralph. ‘You persist in remaining here, ma’am, do you?’\n\nHis niece’s bosom heaved with the indignant excitement into which he had lashed her, but she gave him no reply.\n\n‘Now, Gride, see here,’ said Ralph. ‘This fellow--I grieve to say my brother’s son: a reprobate and profligate, stained with every mean and selfish crime--this fellow, coming here today to disturb a solemn ceremony, and knowing that the consequence of his presenting himself in another man’s house at such a time, and persisting in remaining there, must be his being kicked into the streets and dragged through them like the vagabond he is--this fellow, mark you, brings with him his sister as a protection, thinking we would not expose a silly girl to the degradation and indignity which is no novelty to him; and, even after I have warned her of what must ensue, he still keeps her by him, as you see, and clings to her apron-strings like a cowardly boy to his mother’s. Is not this a pretty fellow to talk as big as you have heard him now?’\n\n‘And as I heard him last night,’ said Arthur Gride; ‘as I heard him last night when he sneaked into my house, and--he! he! he!--very soon sneaked out again, when I nearly frightened him to death. And HE wanting to marry Miss Madeline too! Oh dear! Is there anything else he’d like? Anything else we can do for him, besides giving her up? Would he like his debts paid and his house furnished, and a few bank notes for shaving paper if he shaves at all? He! he! he!’\n\n‘You will remain, girl, will you?’ said Ralph, turning upon Kate again, ‘to be hauled downstairs like a drunken drab, as I swear you shall if you stop here? No answer! Thank your brother for what follows. Gride, call down Bray--and not his daughter. Let them keep her above.’\n\n‘If you value your head,’ said Nicholas, taking up a position before the door, and speaking in the same low voice in which he had spoken before, and with no more outward passion than he had before displayed; ‘stay where you are!’\n\n‘Mind me, and not him, and call down Bray,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Mind yourself rather than either of us, and stay where you are!’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Will you call down Bray?’ cried Ralph.\n\n‘Remember that you come near me at your peril,’ said Nicholas.\n\nGride hesitated. Ralph being, by this time, as furious as a baffled tiger, made for the door, and, attempting to pass Kate, clasped her arm roughly with his hand. Nicholas, with his eyes darting fire, seized him by the collar. At that moment, a heavy body fell with great violence on the floor above, and, in an instant afterwards, was heard a most appalling and terrific scream.\n\nThey all stood still, and gazed upon each other. Scream succeeded scream; a heavy pattering of feet succeeded; and many shrill voices clamouring together were heard to cry, ‘He is dead!’\n\n‘Stand off!’ cried Nicholas, letting loose all the passion he had restrained till now; ‘if this is what I scarcely dare to hope it is, you are caught, villains, in your own toils.’\n\nHe burst from the room, and, darting upstairs to the quarter from whence the noise proceeded, forced his way through a crowd of persons who quite filled a small bed-chamber, and found Bray lying on the floor quite dead; his daughter clinging to the body.\n\n‘How did this happen?’ he cried, looking wildly about him.\n\nSeveral voices answered together, that he had been observed, through the half-opened door, reclining in a strange and uneasy position upon a chair; that he had been spoken to several times, and not answering, was supposed to be asleep, until some person going in and shaking him by the arm, he fell heavily to the ground and was discovered to be dead.\n\n‘Who is the owner of this house?’ said Nicholas, hastily.\n\nAn elderly woman was pointed out to him; and to her he said, as he knelt down and gently unwound Madeline’s arms from the lifeless mass round which they were entwined: ‘I represent this lady’s nearest friends, as her servant here knows, and must remove her from this dreadful scene. This is my sister to whose charge you confide her. My name and address are upon that card, and you shall receive from me all necessary directions for the arrangements that must be made. Stand aside, every one of you, and give me room and air for God’s sake!’\n\nThe people fell back, scarce wondering more at what had just occurred, than at the excitement and impetuosity of him who spoke. Nicholas, taking the insensible girl in his arms, bore her from the chamber and downstairs into the room he had just quitted, followed by his sister and the faithful servant, whom he charged to procure a coach directly, while he and Kate bent over their beautiful charge and endeavoured, but in vain, to restore her to animation. The girl performed her office with such expedition, that in a very few minutes the coach was ready.\n\nRalph Nickleby and Gride, stunned and paralysed by the awful event which had so suddenly overthrown their schemes (it would not otherwise, perhaps, have made much impression on them), and carried away by the extraordinary energy and precipitation of Nicholas, which bore down all before him, looked on at these proceedings like men in a dream or trance. It was not until every preparation was made for Madeline’s immediate removal that Ralph broke silence by declaring she should not be taken away.\n\n‘Who says so?’ cried Nicholas, rising from his knee and confronting them, but still retaining Madeline’s lifeless hand in his.\n\n‘I!’ answered Ralph, hoarsely.\n\n‘Hush, hush!’ cried the terrified Gride, catching him by the arm again. ‘Hear what he says.’\n\n‘Ay!’ said Nicholas, extending his disengaged hand in the air, ‘hear what he says. That both your debts are paid in the one great debt of nature. That the bond, due today at twelve, is now waste paper. That your contemplated fraud shall be discovered yet. That your schemes are known to man, and overthrown by Heaven. Wretches, that he defies you both to do your worst.’\n\n‘This man,’ said Ralph, in a voice scarcely intelligible, ‘this man claims his wife, and he shall have her.’\n\n‘That man claims what is not his, and he should not have her if he were fifty men, with fifty more to back him,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘Who shall prevent him?’\n\n‘I will.’\n\n‘By what right I should like to know,’ said Ralph. ‘By what right I ask?’\n\n‘By this right. That, knowing what I do, you dare not tempt me further,’ said Nicholas, ‘and by this better right; that those I serve, and with whom you would have done me base wrong and injury, are her nearest and her dearest friends. In their name I bear her hence. Give way!’\n\n‘One word!’ cried Ralph, foaming at the mouth.\n\n‘Not one,’ replied Nicholas, ‘I will not hear of one--save this. Look to yourself, and heed this warning that I give you! Your day is past, and night is comin’ on.’\n\n‘My curse, my bitter, deadly curse, upon you, boy!’\n\n‘Whence will curses come at your command? Or what avails a curse or blessing from a man like you? I tell you, that misfortune and discovery are thickening about your head; that the structures you have raised, through all your ill-spent life, are crumbling into dust; that your path is beset with spies; that this very day, ten thousand pounds of your hoarded wealth have gone in one great crash!’\n\n‘’Tis false!’ cried Ralph, shrinking back.\n\n‘’Tis true, and you shall find it so. I have no more words to waste. Stand from the door. Kate, do you go first. Lay not a hand on her, or on that woman, or on me, or so much a brush their garments as they pass you by!--You let them pass, and he blocks the door again!’\n\nArthur Gride happened to be in the doorway, but whether intentionally or from confusion was not quite apparent. Nicholas swung him away, with such violence as to cause him to spin round the room until he was caught by a sharp angle of the wall, and there knocked down; and then taking his beautiful burden in his arms rushed out. No one cared to stop him, if any were so disposed. Making his way through a mob of people, whom a report of the circumstances had attracted round the house, and carrying Madeline, in his excitement, as easily as if she were an infant, he reached the coach in which Kate and the girl were already waiting, and, confiding his charge to them, jumped up beside the coachman and bade him drive away.\n\n# CHAPTER 55\n\nOf Family Matters, Cares, Hopes, Disappointments, and Sorrows\nAlthough Mrs. Nickleby had been made acquainted by her son and daughter with every circumstance of Madeline Bray’s history which was known to them; although the responsible situation in which Nicholas stood had been carefully explained to her, and she had been prepared, even for the possible contingency of having to receive the young lady in her own house, improbable as such a result had appeared only a few minutes before it came about, still, Mrs. Nickleby, from the moment when this confidence was first reposed in her, late on the previous evening, had remained in an unsatisfactory and profoundly mystified state, from which no explanations or arguments could relieve her, and which every fresh soliloquy and reflection only aggravated more and more.\n\n‘Bless my heart, Kate!’ so the good lady argued; ‘if the Mr. Cheerybles don’t want this young lady to be married, why don’t they file a bill against the Lord Chancellor, make her a Chancery ward, and shut her up in the Fleet prison for safety?--I have read of such things in the newspapers a hundred times. Or, if they are so very fond of her as Nicholas says they are, why don’t they marry her themselves--one of them I mean? And even supposing they don’t want her to be married, and don’t want to marry her themselves, why in the name of wonder should Nicholas go about the world, forbidding people’s banns?’\n\n‘I don’t think you quite understand,’ said Kate, gently.\n\n‘Well I am sure, Kate, my dear, you’re very polite!’ replied Mrs Nickleby. ‘I have been married myself I hope, and I have seen other people married. Not understand, indeed!’\n\n‘I know you have had great experience, dear mama,’ said Kate; ‘I mean that perhaps you don’t quite understand all the circumstances in this instance. We have stated them awkwardly, I dare say.’\n\n‘That I dare say you have,’ retorted her mother, briskly. ‘That’s very likely. I am not to be held accountable for that; though, at the same time, as the circumstances speak for themselves, I shall take the liberty, my love, of saying that I do understand them, and perfectly well too; whatever you and Nicholas may choose to think to the contrary. Why is such a great fuss made because this Miss Magdalen is going to marry somebody who is older than herself? Your poor papa was older than I was, four years and a half older. Jane Dibabs--the Dibabses lived in the beautiful little thatched white house one story high, covered all over with ivy and creeping plants, with an exquisite little porch with twining honysuckles and all sorts of things: where the earwigs used to fall into one’s tea on a summer evening, and always fell upon their backs and kicked dreadfully, and where the frogs used to get into the rushlight shades when one stopped all night, and sit up and look through the little holes like Christians--Jane Dibabs, SHE married a man who was a great deal older than herself, and WOULD marry him, notwithstanding all that could be said to the contrary, and she was so fond of him that nothing was ever equal to it. There was no fuss made about Jane Dibabs, and her husband was a most honourable and excellent man, and everybody spoke well of him. Then why should there by any fuss about this Magdalen?’\n\n‘Her husband is much older; he is not her own choice; his character is the very reverse of that which you have just described. Don’t you see a broad destinction between the two cases?’ said Kate.\n\nTo this, Mrs. Nickleby only replied that she durst say she was very stupid, indeed she had no doubt she was, for her own children almost as much as told her so, every day of her life; to be sure she was a little older than they, and perhaps some foolish people might think she ought reasonably to know best. However, no doubt she was wrong; of course she was; she always was, she couldn’t be right, she couldn’t be expected to be; so she had better not expose herself any more; and to all Kate’s conciliations and concessions for an hour ensuing, the good lady gave no other replies than Oh, certainly, why did they ask HER?, HER opinion was of no consequence, it didn’t matter what SHE said, with many other rejoinders of the same class.\n\nIn this frame of mind (expressed, when she had become too resigned for speech, by nods of the head, upliftings of the eyes, and little beginnings of groans, converted, as they attracted attention, into short coughs), Mrs. Nickleby remained until Nicholas and Kate returned with the object of their solicitude; when, having by this time asserted her own importance, and becoming besides interested in the trials of one so young and beautiful, she not only displayed the utmost zeal and solicitude, but took great credit to herself for recommending the course of procedure which her son had adopted: frequently declaring, with an expressive look, that it was very fortunate things were AS they were: and hinting, that but for great encouragement and wisdom on her own part, they never could have been brought to that pass.\n\nNot to strain the question whether Mrs. Nickleby had or had not any great hand in bringing matters about, it is unquestionable that she had strong ground for exultation. The brothers, on their return, bestowed such commendations on Nicholas for the part he had taken, and evinced so much joy at the altered state of events and the recovery of their young friend from trials so great and dangers so threatening, that, as she more than once informed her daughter, she now considered the fortunes of the family ‘as good as’ made. Mr. Charles Cheeryble, indeed, Mrs. Nickleby positively asserted, had, in the first transports of his surprise and delight, ‘as good as’ said so. Without precisely explaining what this qualification meant, she subsided, whenever she mentioned the subject, into such a mysterious and important state, and had such visions of wealth and dignity in perspective, that (vague and clouded though they were) she was, at such times, almost as happy as if she had really been permanently provided for, on a scale of great splendour.\n\nThe sudden and terrible shock she had received, combined with the great affliction and anxiety of mind which she had, for a long time, endured, proved too much for Madeline’s strength. Recovering from the state of stupefaction into which the sudden death of her father happily plunged her, she only exchanged that condition for one of dangerous and active illness. When the delicate physical powers which have been sustained by an unnatural strain upon the mental energies and a resolute determination not to yield, at last give way, their degree of prostration is usually proportionate to the strength of the effort which has previously upheld them. Thus it was that the illness which fell on Madeline was of no slight or temporary nature, but one which, for a time, threatened her reason, and--scarcely worse--her life itself.\n\nWho, slowly recovering from a disorder so severe and dangerous, could be insensible to the unremitting attentions of such a nurse as gentle, tender, earnest Kate? On whom could the sweet soft voice, the light step, the delicate hand, the quiet, cheerful, noiseless discharge of those thousand little offices of kindness and relief which we feel so deeply when we are ill, and forget so lightly when we are well--on whom could they make so deep an impression as on a young heart stored with every pure and true affection that women cherish; almost a stranger to the endearments and devotion of its own sex, save as it learnt them from itself; and rendered, by calamity and suffering, keenly susceptible of the sympathy so long unknown and so long sought in vain? What wonder that days became as years in knitting them together! What wonder, if with every hour of returning health, there came some stronger and sweeter recognition of the praises which Kate, when they recalled old scenes--they seemed old now, and to have been acted years ago--would lavish on her brother! Where would have been the wonder, even, if those praises had found a quick response in the breast of Madeline, and if, with the image of Nicholas so constantly recurring in the features of his sister that she could scarcely separate the two, she had sometimes found it equally difficult to assign to each the feelings they had first inspired, and had imperceptibly mingled with her gratitude to Nicholas, some of that warmer feeling which she had assigned to Kate?\n\n‘My dear,’ Mrs. Nickleby would say, coming into the room with an elaborate caution, calculated to discompose the nerves of an invalid rather more than the entry of a horse-soldier at full gallop; ‘how do you find yourself tonight? I hope you are better.’\n\n‘Almost well, mama,’ Kate would reply, laying down her work, and taking Madeline’s hand in hers.\n\n‘Kate!’ Mrs. Nickleby would say, reprovingly, ‘don’t talk so loud’ (the worthy lady herself talking in a whisper that would have made the blood of the stoutest man run cold in his veins).\n\nKate would take this reproof very quietly, and Mrs. Nickleby, making every board creak and every thread rustle as she moved stealthily about, would add:\n\n‘My son Nicholas has just come home, and I have come, according to custom, my dear, to know, from your own lips, exactly how you are; for he won’t take my account, and never will.’\n\n‘He is later than usual to-night,’ perhaps Madeline would reply. ‘Nearly half an hour.’\n\n‘Well, I never saw such people in all my life as you are, for time, up here!’ Mrs. Nickleby would exclaim in great astonishment; ‘I declare I never did! I had not the least idea that Nicholas was after his time, not the smallest. Mr. Nickleby used to say--your poor papa, I am speaking of, Kate my dear--used to say, that appetite was the best clock in the world, but you have no appetite, my dear Miss Bray, I wish you had, and upon my word I really think you ought to take something that would give you one. I am sure I don’t know, but I have heard that two or three dozen native lobsters give an appetite, though that comes to the same thing after all, for I suppose you must have an appetite before you can take ‘em. If I said lobsters, I meant oysters, but of course it’s all the same, though really how you came to know about Nicholas--’\n\n‘We happened to be just talking about him, mama; that was it.’\n\n‘You never seem to me to be talking about anything else, Kate, and upon my word I am quite surprised at your being so very thoughtless. You can find subjects enough to talk about sometimes, and when you know how important it is to keep up Miss Bray’s spirits, and interest her, and all that, it really is quite extraordinary to me what can induce you to keep on prose, prose, prose, din, din, din, everlastingly, upon the same theme. You are a very kind nurse, Kate, and a very good one, and I know you mean very well; but I will say this--that if it wasn’t for me, I really don’t know what would become of Miss Bray’s spirits, and so I tell the doctor every day. He says he wonders how I sustain my own, and I am sure I very often wonder myself how I can contrive to keep up as I do. Of course it’s an exertion, but still, when I know how much depends upon me in this house, I am obliged to make it. There’s nothing praiseworthy in that, but it’s necessary, and I do it.’\n\nWith that, Mrs. Nickleby would draw up a chair, and for some three-quarters of an hour run through a great variety of distracting topics in the most distracting manner possible; tearing herself away, at length, on the plea that she must now go and amuse Nicholas while he took his supper. After a preliminary raising of his spirits with the information that she considered the patient decidedly worse, she would further cheer him up by relating how dull, listless, and low-spirited Miss Bray was, because Kate foolishly talked about nothing else but him and family matters. When she had made Nicholas thoroughly comfortable with these and other inspiriting remarks, she would discourse at length on the arduous duties she had performed that day; and, sometimes, be moved to tears in wondering how, if anything were to happen to herself, the family would ever get on without her.\n\nAt other times, when Nicholas came home at night, he would be accompanied by Mr. Frank Cheeryble, who was commissioned by the brothers to inquire how Madeline was that evening. On such occasions (and they were of very frequent occurrence), Mrs. Nickleby deemed it of particular importance that she should have her wits about her; for, from certain signs and tokens which had attracted her attention, she shrewdly suspected that Mr. Frank, interested as his uncles were in Madeline, came quite as much to see Kate as to inquire after her; the more especially as the brothers were in constant communication with the medical man, came backwards and forwards very frequently themselves, and received a full report from Nicholas every morning. These were proud times for Mrs Nickleby; never was anybody half so discreet and sage as she, or half so mysterious withal; and never were there such cunning generalship, and such unfathomable designs, as she brought to bear upon Mr. Frank, with the view of ascertaining whether her suspicions were well founded: and if so, of tantalising him into taking her into his confidence and throwing himself upon her merciful consideration. Extensive was the artillery, heavy and light, which Mrs. Nickleby brought into play for the furtherance of these great schemes; various and opposite the means which she employed to bring about the end she had in view. At one time, she was all cordiality and ease; at another, all stiffness and frigidity. Now, she would seem to open her whole heart to her unhappy victim; the next time they met, she would receive him with the most distant and studious reserve, as if a new light had broken in upon her, and, guessing his intentions, she had resolved to check them in the bud; as if she felt it her bounden duty to act with Spartan firmness, and at once and for ever to discourage hopes which never could be realised. At other times, when Nicholas was not there to overhear, and Kate was upstairs busily tending her sick friend, the worthy lady would throw out dark hints of an intention to send her daughter to France for three or four years, or to Scotland for the improvement of her health impaired by her late fatigues, or to America on a visit, or anywhere that threatened a long and tedious separation. Nay, she even went so far as to hint, obscurely, at an attachment entertained for her daughter by the son of an old neighbour of theirs, one Horatio Peltirogus (a young gentleman who might have been, at that time, four years old, or thereabouts), and to represent it, indeed, as almost a settled thing between the families--only waiting for her daughter’s final decision, to come off with the sanction of the church, and to the unspeakable happiness and content of all parties.\n\nIt was in the full pride and glory of having sprung this last mine one night with extraordinary success, that Mrs. Nickleby took the opportunity of being left alone with her son before retiring to rest, to sound him on the subject which so occupied her thoughts: not doubting that they could have but one opinion respecting it. To this end, she approached the question with divers laudatory and appropriate remarks touching the general amiability of Mr. Frank Cheeryble.\n\n‘You are quite right, mother,’ said Nicholas, ‘quite right. He is a fine fellow.’\n\n‘Good-looking, too,’ said Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘Decidedly good-looking,’ answered Nicholas.\n\n‘What may you call his nose, now, my dear?’ pursued Mrs. Nickleby, wishing to interest Nicholas in the subject to the utmost.\n\n‘Call it?’ repeated Nicholas.\n\n‘Ah!’ returned his mother, ‘what style of nose? What order of architecture, if one may say so. I am not very learned in noses. Do you call it a Roman or a Grecian?’\n\n‘Upon my word, mother,’ said Nicholas, laughing, ‘as well as I remember, I should call it a kind of Composite, or mixed nose. But I have no very strong recollection on the subject. If it will afford you any gratification, I’ll observe it more closely, and let you know.’\n\n‘I wish you would, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, with an earnest look.\n\n‘Very well,’ returned Nicholas. ‘I will.’\n\nNicholas returned to the perusal of the book he had been reading, when the dialogue had gone thus far. Mrs. Nickleby, after stopping a little for consideration, resumed.\n\n‘He is very much attached to you, Nicholas, my dear.’\n\nNicholas laughingly said, as he closed his book, that he was glad to hear it, and observed that his mother seemed deep in their new friend’s confidence already.\n\n‘Hem!’ said Mrs. Nickleby. ‘I don’t know about that, my dear, but I think it is very necessary that somebody should be in his confidence; highly necessary.’\n\nElated by a look of curiosity from her son, and the consciousness of possessing a great secret, all to herself, Mrs. Nickleby went on with great animation:\n\n‘I am sure, my dear Nicholas, how you can have failed to notice it, is, to me, quite extraordinary; though I don’t know why I should say that, either, because, of course, as far as it goes, and to a certain extent, there is a great deal in this sort of thing, especially in this early stage, which, however clear it may be to females, can scarcely be expected to be so evident to men. I don’t say that I have any particular penetration in such matters. I may have; those about me should know best about that, and perhaps do know. Upon that point I shall express no opinion, it wouldn’t become me to do so, it’s quite out of the question, quite.’\n\nNicholas snuffed the candles, put his hands in his pockets, and, leaning back in his chair, assumed a look of patient suffering and melancholy resignation.\n\n‘I think it my duty, Nicholas, my dear,’ resumed his mother, ‘to tell you what I know: not only because you have a right to know it too, and to know everything that happens in this family, but because you have it in your power to promote and assist the thing very much; and there is no doubt that the sooner one can come to a clear understanding on such subjects, it is always better, every way. There are a great many things you might do; such as taking a walk in the garden sometimes, or sitting upstairs in your own room for a little while, or making believe to fall asleep occasionally, or pretending that you recollected some business, and going out for an hour or so, and taking Mr. Smike with you. These seem very slight things, and I dare say you will be amused at my making them of so much importance; at the same time, my dear, I can assure you (and you’ll find this out, Nicholas, for yourself one of these days, if you ever fall in love with anybody; as I trust and hope you will, provided she is respectable and well conducted, and of course you’d never dream of falling in love with anybody who was not), I say, I can assure you that a great deal more depends upon these little things than you would suppose possible. If your poor papa was alive, he would tell you how much depended on the parties being left alone. Of course, you are not to go out of the room as if you meant it and did it on purpose, but as if it was quite an accident, and to come back again in the same way. If you cough in the passage before you open the door, or whistle carelessly, or hum a tune, or something of that sort, to let them know you’re coming, it’s always better; because, of course, though it’s not only natural but perfectly correct and proper under the circumstances, still it is very confusing if you interrupt young people when they are--when they are sitting on the sofa, and--and all that sort of thing: which is very nonsensical, perhaps, but still they will do it.’\n\nThe profound astonishment with which her son regarded her during this long address, gradually increasing as it approached its climax in no way discomposed Mrs. Nickleby, but rather exalted her opinion of her own cleverness; therefore, merely stopping to remark, with much complacency, that she had fully expected him to be surprised, she entered on a vast quantity of circumstantial evidence of a particularly incoherent and perplexing kind; the upshot of which was, to establish, beyond the possibility of doubt, that Mr. Frank Cheeryble had fallen desperately in love with Kate.\n\n‘With whom?’ cried Nicholas.\n\nMrs. Nickleby repeated, with Kate.\n\n‘What! OUR Kate! My sister!’\n\n‘Lord, Nicholas!’ returned Mrs. Nickleby, ‘whose Kate should it be, if not ours; or what should I care about it, or take any interest in it for, if it was anybody but your sister?’\n\n‘Dear mother,’ said Nicholas, ‘surely it can’t be!’\n\n‘Very good, my dear,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby, with great confidence. ‘Wait and see.’\n\nNicholas had never, until that moment, bestowed a thought upon the remote possibility of such an occurrence as that which was now communicated to him; for, besides that he had been much from home of late and closely occupied with other matters, his own jealous fears had prompted the suspicion that some secret interest in Madeline, akin to that which he felt himself, occasioned those visits of Frank Cheeryble which had recently become so frequent. Even now, although he knew that the observation of an anxious mother was much more likely to be correct in such a case than his own, and although she reminded him of many little circumstances which, taken together, were certainly susceptible of the construction she triumphantly put upon them, he was not quite convinced but that they arose from mere good-natured thoughtless gallantry, which would have dictated the same conduct towards any other girl who was young and pleasing. At all events, he hoped so, and therefore tried to believe it.\n\n‘I am very much disturbed by what you tell me,’ said Nicholas, after a little reflection, ‘though I yet hope you may be mistaken.’\n\n‘I don’t understand why you should hope so,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘I confess; but you may depend upon it I am not.’\n\n‘What of Kate?’ inquired Nicholas.\n\n‘Why that, my dear,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby, ‘is just the point upon which I am not yet satisfied. During this sickness, she has been constantly at Madeline’s bedside--never were two people so fond of each other as they have grown--and to tell you the truth, Nicholas, I have rather kept her away now and then, because I think it’s a good plan, and urges a young man on. He doesn’t get too sure, you know.’\n\nShe said this with such a mingling of high delight and self-congratulation, that it was inexpressibly painful to Nicholas to dash her hopes; but he felt that there was only one honourable course before him, and that he was bound to take it.\n\n‘Dear mother,’ he said kindly, ‘don’t you see that if there were really any serious inclination on the part of Mr. Frank towards Kate, and we suffered ourselves for a moment to encourage it, we should be acting a most dishonourable and ungrateful part? I ask you if you don’t see it, but I need not say that I know you don’t, or you would have been more strictly on your guard. Let me explain my meaning to you. Remember how poor we are.’\n\nMrs. Nickleby shook her head, and said, through her tears, that poverty was not a crime.\n\n‘No,’ said Nicholas, ‘and for that reason poverty should engender an honest pride, that it may not lead and tempt us to unworthy actions, and that we may preserve the self-respect which a hewer of wood and drawer of water may maintain, and does better in maintaining than a monarch in preserving his. Think what we owe to these two brothers: remember what they have done, and what they do every day for us with a generosity and delicacy for which the devotion of our whole lives would be a most imperfect and inadequate return. What kind of return would that be which would be comprised in our permitting their nephew, their only relative, whom they regard as a son, and for whom it would be mere childishness to suppose they have not formed plans suitably adapted to the education he has had, and the fortune he will inherit--in our permitting him to marry a portionless girl: so closely connected with us, that the irresistible inference must be, that he was entrapped by a plot; that it was a deliberate scheme, and a speculation amongst us three? Bring the matter clearly before yourself, mother. Now, how would you feel, if they were married, and the brothers, coming here on one of those kind errands which bring them here so often, you had to break out to them the truth? Would you be at ease, and feel that you had played an open part?’\n\nPoor Mrs. Nickleby, crying more and more, murmured that of course Mr Frank would ask the consent of his uncles first.\n\n‘Why, to be sure, that would place HIM in a better situation with them,’ said Nicholas, ‘but we should still be open to the same suspicions; the distance between us would still be as great; the advantages to be gained would still be as manifest as now. We may be reckoning without our host in all this,’ he added more cheerfully, ‘and I trust, and almost believe we are. If it be otherwise, I have that confidence in Kate that I know she will feel as I do--and in you, dear mother, to be assured that after a little consideration you will do the same.’\n\nAfter many more representations and entreaties, Nicholas obtained a promise from Mrs. Nickleby that she would try all she could to think as he did; and that if Mr. Frank persevered in his attentions she would endeavour to discourage them, or, at the least, would render him no countenance or assistance. He determined to forbear mentioning the subject to Kate until he was quite convinced that there existed a real necessity for his doing so; and resolved to assure himself, as well as he could by close personal observation, of the exact position of affairs. This was a very wise resolution, but he was prevented from putting it in practice by a new source of anxiety and uneasiness.\n\nSmike became alarmingly ill; so reduced and exhausted that he could scarcely move from room to room without assistance; and so worn and emaciated, that it was painful to look upon him. Nicholas was warned, by the same medical authority to whom he had at first appealed, that the last chance and hope of his life depended on his being instantly removed from London. That part of Devonshire in which Nicholas had been himself bred was named as the most favourable spot; but this advice was cautiously coupled with the information, that whoever accompanied him thither must be prepared for the worst; for every token of rapid consumption had appeared, and he might never return alive.\n\nThe kind brothers, who were acquainted with the poor creature’s sad history, dispatched old Tim to be present at this consultation. That same morning, Nicholas was summoned by brother Charles into his private room, and thus addressed:\n\n‘My dear sir, no time must be lost. This lad shall not die, if such human means as we can use can save his life; neither shall he die alone, and in a strange place. Remove him tomorrow morning, see that he has every comfort that his situation requires, and don’t leave him; don’t leave him, my dear sir, until you know that there is no longer any immediate danger. It would be hard, indeed, to part you now. No, no, no! Tim shall wait upon you tonight, sir; Tim shall wait upon you tonight with a parting word or two. Brother Ned, my dear fellow, Mr. Nickleby waits to shake hands and say goodbye; Mr. Nickleby won’t be long gone; this poor chap will soon get better, very soon get better; and then he’ll find out some nice homely country-people to leave him with, and will go backwards and forwards sometimes--backwards and forwards you know, Ned. And there’s no cause to be downhearted, for he’ll very soon get better, very soon. Won’t he, won’t he, Ned?’\n\nWhat Tim Linkinwater said, or what he brought with him that night, needs not to be told. Next morning Nicholas and his feeble companion began their journey.\n\nAnd who but one--and that one he who, but for those who crowded round him then, had never met a look of kindness, or known a word of pity--could tell what agony of mind, what blighted thoughts, what unavailing sorrow, were involved in that sad parting?\n\n‘See,’ cried Nicholas eagerly, as he looked from the coach window, ‘they are at the corner of the lane still! And now there’s Kate, poor Kate, whom you said you couldn’t bear to say goodbye to, waving her handkerchief. Don’t go without one gesture of farewell to Kate!’\n\n‘I cannot make it!’ cried his trembling companion, falling back in his seat and covering his eyes. ‘Do you see her now? Is she there still?’\n\n‘Yes, yes!’ said Nicholas earnestly. ‘There! She waves her hand again! I have answered it for you--and now they are out of sight. Do not give way so bitterly, dear friend, don’t. You will meet them all again.’\n\nHe whom he thus encouraged, raised his withered hands and clasped them fervently together.\n\n‘In heaven. I humbly pray to God in heaven.’\n\nIt sounded like the prayer of a broken heart.\n\n# CHAPTER 56\n\nRalph Nickleby, baffled by his Nephew in his late Design, hatches a Scheme of Retaliation which Accident suggests to him, and takes into his Counsels a tried Auxiliary\n\nThe course which these adventures shape out for themselves, and imperatively call upon the historian to observe, now demands that they should revert to the point they attained previously to the commencement of the last chapter, when Ralph Nickleby and Arthur Gride were left together in the house where death had so suddenly reared his dark and heavy banner.\n\nWith clenched hands, and teeth ground together so firm and tight that no locking of the jaws could have fixed and riveted them more securely, Ralph stood, for some minutes, in the attitude in which he had last addressed his nephew: breathing heavily, but as rigid and motionless in other respects as if he had been a brazen statue. After a time, he began, by slow degrees, as a man rousing himself from heavy slumber, to relax. For a moment he shook his clasped fist towards the door by which Nicholas had disappeared; and then thrusting it into his breast, as if to repress by force even this show of passion, turned round and confronted the less hardy usurer, who had not yet risen from the ground.\n\nThe cowering wretch, who still shook in every limb, and whose few grey hairs trembled and quivered on his head with abject dismay, tottered to his feet as he met Ralph’s eye, and, shielding his face with both hands, protested, while he crept towards the door, that it was no fault of his.\n\n‘Who said it was, man?’ returned Ralph, in a suppressed voice. ‘Who said it was?’\n\n‘You looked as if you thought I was to blame,’ said Gride, timidly.\n\n‘Pshaw!’ Ralph muttered, forcing a laugh. ‘I blame him for not living an hour longer. One hour longer would have been long enough. I blame no one else.’\n\n‘N--n--no one else?’ said Gride.\n\n‘Not for this mischance,’ replied Ralph. ‘I have an old score to clear with that young fellow who has carried off your mistress; but that has nothing to do with his blustering just now, for we should soon have been quit of him, but for this cursed accident.’\n\nThere was something so unnatural in the calmness with which Ralph Nickleby spoke, when coupled with his face, the expression of the features, to which every nerve and muscle, as it twitched and throbbed with a spasm whose workings no effort could conceal, gave, every instant, some new and frightful aspect--there was something so unnatural and ghastly in the contrast between his harsh, slow, steady voice (only altered by a certain halting of the breath which made him pause between almost every word like a drunken man bent upon speaking plainly), and these evidences of the most intense and violent passion, and the struggle he made to keep them under; that if the dead body which lay above had stood, instead of him, before the cowering Gride, it could scarcely have presented a spectacle which would have terrified him more.\n\n‘The coach,’ said Ralph after a time, during which he had struggled like some strong man against a fit. ‘We came in a coach. Is it waiting?’\n\nGride gladly availed himself of the pretext for going to the window to see. Ralph, keeping his face steadily the other way, tore at his shirt with the hand which he had thrust into his breast, and muttered in a hoarse whisper:\n\n‘Ten thousand pounds! He said ten thousand! The precise sum paid in but yesterday for the two mortgages, and which would have gone out again, at heavy interest, tomorrow. If that house has failed, and he the first to bring the news!--Is the coach there?’\n\n‘Yes, yes,’ said Gride, startled by the fierce tone of the inquiry. ‘It’s here. Dear, dear, what a fiery man you are!’\n\n‘Come here,’ said Ralph, beckoning to him. ‘We mustn’t make a show of being disturbed. We’ll go down arm in arm.’\n\n‘But you pinch me black and blue,’ urged Gride.\n\nRalph let him go impatiently, and descending the stairs with his usual firm and heavy tread, got into the coach. Arthur Gride followed. After looking doubtfully at Ralph when the man asked where he was to drive, and finding that he remained silent, and expressed no wish upon the subject, Arthur mentioned his own house, and thither they proceeded.\n\nOn their way, Ralph sat in the furthest corner with folded arms, and uttered not a word. With his chin sunk upon his breast, and his downcast eyes quite hidden by the contraction of his knotted brows, he might have been asleep for any sign of consciousness he gave until the coach stopped, when he raised his head, and glancing through the window, inquired what place that was.\n\n‘My house,’ answered the disconsolate Gride, affected perhaps by its loneliness. ‘Oh dear! my house.’\n\n‘True,’ said Ralph ‘I have not observed the way we came. I should like a glass of water. You have that in the house, I suppose?’\n\n‘You shall have a glass of--of anything you like,’ answered Gride, with a groan. ‘It’s no use knocking, coachman. Ring the bell!’\n\nThe man rang, and rang, and rang again; then, knocked until the street re-echoed with the sounds; then, listened at the keyhole of the door. Nobody came. The house was silent as the grave.\n\n‘How’s this?’ said Ralph impatiently.\n\n‘Peg is so very deaf,’ answered Gride with a look of anxiety and alarm. ‘Oh dear! Ring again, coachman. She SEES the bell.’\n\nAgain the man rang and knocked, and knocked and rang again. Some of the neighbours threw up their windows, and called across the street to each other that old Gride’s housekeeper must have dropped down dead. Others collected round the coach, and gave vent to various surmises; some held that she had fallen asleep; some, that she had burnt herself to death; some, that she had got drunk; and one very fat man that she had seen something to eat which had frightened her so much (not being used to it) that she had fallen into a fit. This last suggestion particularly delighted the bystanders, who cheered it rather uproariously, and were, with some difficulty, deterred from dropping down the area and breaking open the kitchen door to ascertain the fact. Nor was this all. Rumours having gone abroad that Arthur was to be married that morning, very particular inquiries were made after the bride, who was held by the majority to be disguised in the person of Mr. Ralph Nickleby, which gave rise to much jocose indignation at the public appearance of a bride in boots and pantaloons, and called forth a great many hoots and groans. At length, the two money-lenders obtained shelter in a house next door, and, being accommodated with a ladder, clambered over the wall of the back-yard--which was not a high one--and descended in safety on the other side.\n\n‘I am almost afraid to go in, I declare,’ said Arthur, turning to Ralph when they were alone. ‘Suppose she should be murdered. Lying with her brains knocked out by a poker, eh?’\n\n‘Suppose she were,’ said Ralph. ‘I tell you, I wish such things were more common than they are, and more easily done. You may stare and shiver. I do!’\n\nHe applied himself to a pump in the yard; and, having taken a deep draught of water and flung a quantity on his head and face, regained his accustomed manner and led the way into the house: Gride following close at his heels.\n\nIt was the same dark place as ever: every room dismal and silent as it was wont to be, and every ghostly article of furniture in its customary place. The iron heart of the grim old clock, undisturbed by all the noise without, still beat heavily within its dusty case; the tottering presses slunk from the sight, as usual, in their melancholy corners; the echoes of footsteps returned the same dreary sound; the long-legged spider paused in his nimble run, and, scared by the sight of men in that his dull domain, hung motionless on the wall, counterfeiting death until they should have passed him by.\n\nFrom cellar to garret went the two usurers, opening every creaking door and looking into every deserted room. But no Peg was there. At last, they sat them down in the apartment which Arthur Gride usually inhabited, to rest after their search.\n\n‘The hag is out, on some preparation for your wedding festivities, I suppose,’ said Ralph, preparing to depart. ‘See here! I destroy the bond; we shall never need it now.’\n\nGride, who had been peering narrowly about the room, fell, at that moment, upon his knees before a large chest, and uttered a terrible yell.\n\n‘How now?’ said Ralph, looking sternly round.\n\n‘Robbed! robbed!’ screamed Arthur Gride.\n\n‘Robbed! of money?’\n\n‘No, no, no. Worse! far worse!’\n\n‘Of what then?’ demanded Ralph.\n\n‘Worse than money, worse than money!’ cried the old man, casting the papers out of the chest, like some beast tearing up the earth. ‘She had better have stolen money--all my money--I haven’t much! She had better have made me a beggar than have done this!’\n\n‘Done what?’ said Ralph. ‘Done what, you devil’s dotard?’\n\nStill Gride made no answer, but tore and scratched among the papers, and yelled and screeched like a fiend in torment.\n\n‘There is something missing, you say,’ said Ralph, shaking him furiously by the collar. ‘What is it?’\n\n‘Papers, deeds. I am a ruined man. Lost, lost! I am robbed, I am ruined! She saw me reading it--reading it of late--I did very often--She watched me, saw me put it in the box that fitted into this, the box is gone, she has stolen it. Damnation seize her, she has robbed me!’\n\n‘Of WHAT?’ cried Ralph, on whom a sudden light appeared to break, for his eyes flashed and his frame trembled with agitation as he clutched Gride by his bony arm. ‘Of what?’\n\n‘She don’t know what it is; she can’t read!’ shrieked Gride, not heeding the inquiry. ‘There’s only one way in which money can be made of it, and that is by taking it to her. Somebody will read it for her, and tell her what to do. She and her accomplice will get money for it and be let off besides; they’ll make a merit of it--say they found it--knew it--and be evidence against me. The only person it will fall upon is me, me, me!’\n\n‘Patience!’ said Ralph, clutching him still tighter and eyeing him with a sidelong look, so fixed and eager as sufficiently to denote that he had some hidden purpose in what he was about to say. ‘Hear reason. She can’t have been gone long. I’ll call the police. Do you but give information of what she has stolen, and they’ll lay hands upon her, trust me. Here! Help!’\n\n‘No, no, no!’ screamed the old man, putting his hand on Ralph’s mouth. ‘I can’t, I daren’t.’\n\n‘Help! help!’ cried Ralph.\n\n‘No, no, no!’ shrieked the other, stamping on the ground with the energy of a madman. ‘I tell you no. I daren’t, I daren’t!’\n\n‘Daren’t make this robbery public?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘No!’ rejoined Gride, wringing his hands. ‘Hush! Hush! Not a word of this; not a word must be said. I am undone. Whichever way I turn, I am undone. I am betrayed. I shall be given up. I shall die in Newgate!’\n\nWith frantic exclamations such as these, and with many others in which fear, grief, and rage, were strangely blended, the panic-stricken wretch gradually subdued his first loud outcry, until it had softened down into a low despairing moan, chequered now and then by a howl, as, going over such papers as were left in the chest, he discovered some new loss. With very little excuse for departing so abruptly, Ralph left him, and, greatly disappointing the loiterers outside the house by telling them there was nothing the matter, got into the coach, and was driven to his own home.\n\nA letter lay on his table. He let it lie there for some time, as if he had not the courage to open it, but at length did so and turned deadly pale.\n\n‘The worst has happened,’ he said; ‘the house has failed. I see. The rumour was abroad in the city last night, and reached the ears of those merchants. Well, well!’\n\nHe strode violently up and down the room and stopped again.\n\n‘Ten thousand pounds! And only lying there for a day--for one day! How many anxious years, how many pinching days and sleepless nights, before I scraped together that ten thousand pounds!--Ten thousand pounds! How many proud painted dames would have fawned and smiled, and how many spendthrift blockheads done me lip-service to my face and cursed me in their hearts, while I turned that ten thousand pounds into twenty! While I ground, and pinched, and used these needy borrowers for my pleasure and profit, what smooth-tongued speeches, and courteous looks, and civil letters, they would have given me! The cant of the lying world is, that men like me compass our riches by dissimulation and treachery: by fawning, cringing, and stooping. Why, how many lies, what mean and abject evasions, what humbled behaviour from upstarts who, but for my money, would spurn me aside as they do their betters every day, would that ten thousand pounds have brought me in! Grant that I had doubled it--made cent. per cent.--for every sovereign told another--there would not be one piece of money in all the heap which wouldn’t represent ten thousand mean and paltry lies, told, not by the money-lender, oh no! but by the money-borrowers, your liberal, thoughtless, generous, dashing folks, who wouldn’t be so mean as save a sixpence for the world!’\n\nStriving, as it would seem, to lose part of the bitterness of his regrets in the bitterness of these other thoughts, Ralph continued to pace the room. There was less and less of resolution in his manner as his mind gradually reverted to his loss; at length, dropping into his elbow-chair and grasping its sides so firmly that they creaked again, he said:\n\n‘The time has been when nothing could have moved me like the loss of this great sum. Nothing. For births, deaths, marriages, and all the events which are of interest to most men, have (unless they are connected with gain or loss of money) no interest for me. But now, I swear, I mix up with the loss, his triumph in telling it. If he had brought it about,--I almost feel as if he had,--I couldn’t hate him more. Let me but retaliate upon him, by degrees, however slow--let me but begin to get the better of him, let me but turn the scale--and I can bear it.’\n\nHis meditations were long and deep. They terminated in his dispatching a letter by Newman, addressed to Mr. Squeers at the Saracen’s Head, with instructions to inquire whether he had arrived in town, and, if so, to wait an answer. Newman brought back the information that Mr. Squeers had come by mail that morning, and had received the letter in bed; but that he sent his duty, and word that he would get up and wait upon Mr Nickleby directly.\n\nThe interval between the delivery of this message, and the arrival of Mr Squeers, was very short; but, before he came, Ralph had suppressed every sign of emotion, and once more regained the hard, immovable, inflexible manner which was habitual to him, and to which, perhaps, was ascribable no small part of the influence which, over many men of no very strong prejudices on the score of morality, he could exert, almost at will.\n\n‘Well, Mr. Squeers,’ he said, welcoming that worthy with his accustomed smile, of which a sharp look and a thoughtful frown were part and parcel: ‘how do YOU do?’\n\n‘Why, sir,’ said Mr. Squeers, ‘I’m pretty well. So’s the family, and so’s the boys, except for a sort of rash as is a running through the school, and rather puts ‘em off their feed. But it’s a ill wind as blows no good to nobody; that’s what I always say when them lads has a wisitation. A wisitation, sir, is the lot of mortality. Mortality itself, sir, is a wisitation. The world is chock full of wisitations; and if a boy repines at a wisitation and makes you uncomfortable with his noise, he must have his head punched. That’s going according to the Scripter, that is.’\n\n‘Mr. Squeers,’ said Ralph, drily.\n\n‘Sir.’\n\n‘We’ll avoid these precious morsels of morality if you please, and talk of business.’\n\n‘With all my heart, sir,’ rejoined Squeers, ‘and first let me say--’\n\n‘First let ME say, if you please.--Noggs!’\n\nNewman presented himself when the summons had been twice or thrice repeated, and asked if his master called.\n\n‘I did. Go to your dinner. And go at once. Do you hear?’\n\n‘It an’t time,’ said Newman, doggedly.\n‘My time is yours, and I say it is,’ returned Ralph.\n\n‘You alter it every day,’ said Newman. ‘It isn’t fair.’\n\n‘You don’t keep many cooks, and can easily apologise to them for the trouble,’ retorted Ralph. ‘Begone, sir!’\n\nRalph not only issued this order in his most peremptory manner, but, under pretence of fetching some papers from the little office, saw it obeyed, and, when Newman had left the house, chained the door, to prevent the possibility of his returning secretly, by means of his latch-key.\n\n‘I have reason to suspect that fellow,’ said Ralph, when he returned to his own office. ‘Therefore, until I have thought of the shortest and least troublesome way of ruining him, I hold it best to keep him at a distance.’\n\n‘It wouldn’t take much to ruin him, I should think,’ said Squeers, with a grin.\n\n‘Perhaps not,’ answered Ralph. ‘Nor to ruin a great many people whom I know. You were going to say--?’\n\nRalph’s summary and matter-of-course way of holding up this example, and throwing out the hint that followed it, had evidently an effect (as doubtless it was designed to have) upon Mr. Squeers, who said, after a little hesitation and in a much more subdued tone:\n\n‘Why, what I was a-going to say, sir, is, that this here business regarding of that ungrateful and hard-hearted chap, Snawley senior, puts me out of my way, and occasions a inconveniency quite unparalleled, besides, as I may say, making, for whole weeks together, Mrs. Squeers a perfect widder. It’s a pleasure to me to act with you, of course.’\n\n‘Of course,’ said Ralph, drily.\n\n‘Yes, I say of course,’ resumed Mr. Squeers, rubbing his knees, ‘but at the same time, when one comes, as I do now, better than two hundred and fifty mile to take a afferdavid, it does put a man out a good deal, letting alone the risk.’\n\n‘And where may the risk be, Mr. Squeers?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘I said, letting alone the risk,’ replied Squeers, evasively.\n\n‘And I said, where was the risk?’\n\n‘I wasn’t complaining, you know, Mr. Nickleby,’ pleaded Squeers. ‘Upon my word I never see such a--’\n\n‘I ask you where is the risk?’ repeated Ralph, emphatically.\n\n‘Where the risk?’ returned Squeers, rubbing his knees still harder. ‘Why, it an’t necessary to mention. Certain subjects is best awoided. Oh, you know what risk I mean.’\n\n‘How often have I told you,’ said Ralph, ‘and how often am I to tell you, that you run no risk? What have you sworn, or what are you asked to swear, but that at such and such a time a boy was left with you in the name of Smike; that he was at your school for a given number of years, was lost under such and such circumstances, is now found, and has been identified by you in such and such keeping? This is all true; is it not?’\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Squeers, ‘that’s all true.’\n\n‘Well, then,’ said Ralph, ‘what risk do you run? Who swears to a lie but Snawley; a man whom I have paid much less than I have you?’\n\n‘He certainly did it cheap, did Snawley,’ observed Squeers.\n\n‘He did it cheap!’ retorted Ralph, testily; ‘yes, and he did it well, and carries it off with a hypocritical face and a sanctified air, but you! Risk! What do you mean by risk? The certificates are all genuine, Snawley HAD another son, he HAS been married twice, his first wife IS dead, none but her ghost could tell that she didn’t write that letter, none but Snawley himself can tell that this is not his son, and that his son is food for worms! The only perjury is Snawley’s, and I fancy he is pretty well used to it. Where’s your risk?’\n\n‘Why, you know,’ said Squeers, fidgeting in his chair, ‘if you come to that, I might say where’s yours?’\n\n‘You might say where’s mine!’ returned Ralph; ‘you may say where’s mine. I don’t appear in the business, neither do you. All Snawley’s interest is to stick well to the story he has told; and all his risk is, to depart from it in the least. Talk of YOUR risk in the conspiracy!’\n\n‘I say,’ remonstrated Squeers, looking uneasily round: ‘don’t call it that! Just as a favour, don’t.’\n\n‘Call it what you like,’ said Ralph, irritably, ‘but attend to me. This tale was originally fabricated as a means of annoyance against one who hurt your trade and half cudgelled you to death, and to enable you to obtain repossession of a half-dead drudge, whom you wished to regain, because, while you wreaked your vengeance on him for his share in the business, you knew that the knowledge that he was again in your power would be the best punishment you could inflict upon your enemy. Is that so, Mr. Squeers?’\n\n‘Why, sir,’ returned Squeers, almost overpowered by the determination which Ralph displayed to make everything tell against him, and by his stern unyielding manner, ‘in a measure it was.’\n\n‘What does that mean?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Why, in a measure means,’ returned Squeers, ‘as it may be, that it wasn’t all on my account, because you had some old grudge to satisfy, too.’\n\n‘If I had not had,’ said Ralph, in no way abashed by the reminder, ‘do you think I should have helped you?’\n\n‘Why no, I don’t suppose you would,’ Squeers replied. ‘I only wanted that point to be all square and straight between us.’\n\n‘How can it ever be otherwise?’ retorted Ralph. ‘Except that the account is against me, for I spend money to gratify my hatred, and you pocket it, and gratify yours at the same time. You are, at least, as avaricious as you are revengeful. So am I. Which is best off? You, who win money and revenge, at the same time and by the same process, and who are, at all events, sure of money, if not of revenge; or I, who am only sure of spending money in any case, and can but win bare revenge at last?’\n\nAs Mr. Squeers could only answer this proposition by shrugs and smiles, Ralph bade him be silent, and thankful that he was so well off; and then, fixing his eyes steadily upon him, proceeded to say:\n\nFirst, that Nicholas had thwarted him in a plan he had formed for the disposal in marriage of a certain young lady, and had, in the confusion attendant on her father’s sudden death, secured that lady himself, and borne her off in triumph.\n\nSecondly, that by some will or settlement--certainly by some instrument in writing, which must contain the young lady’s name, and could be, therefore, easily selected from others, if access to the place where it was deposited were once secured--she was entitled to property which, if the existence of this deed ever became known to her, would make her husband (and Ralph represented that Nicholas was certain to marry her) a rich and prosperous man, and most formidable enemy.\n\nThirdly, that this deed had been, with others, stolen from one who had himself obtained or concealed it fraudulently, and who feared to take any steps for its recovery; and that he (Ralph) knew the thief.\n\nTo all this Mr. Squeers listened, with greedy ears that devoured every syllable, and with his one eye and his mouth wide open: marvelling for what special reason he was honoured with so much of Ralph’s confidence, and to what it all tended.\n\n‘Now,’ said Ralph, leaning forward, and placing his hand on Squeers’s arm, ‘hear the design which I have conceived, and which I must--I say, must, if I can ripen it--have carried into execution. No advantage can be reaped from this deed, whatever it is, save by the girl herself, or her husband; and the possession of this deed by one or other of them is indispensable to any advantage being gained. THAT I have discovered beyond the possibility of doubt. I want that deed brought here, that I may give the man who brings it fifty pounds in gold, and burn it to ashes before his face.’\n\nMr. Squeers, after following with his eye the action of Ralph’s hand towards the fire-place as if he were at that moment consuming the paper, drew a long breath, and said:\n\n‘Yes; but who’s to bring it?’\n\n‘Nobody, perhaps, for much is to be done before it can be got at,’ said Ralph. ‘But if anybody--you!’\n\nMr. Squeers’s first tokens of consternation, and his flat relinquishment of the task, would have staggered most men, if they had not immediately occasioned an utter abandonment of the proposition. On Ralph they produced not the slightest effect. Resuming, when the schoolmaster had quite talked himself out of breath, as coolly as if he had never been interrupted, Ralph proceeded to expatiate on such features of the case as he deemed it most advisable to lay the greatest stress on.\n\nThese were, the age, decrepitude, and weakness of Mrs. Sliderskew; the great improbability of her having any accomplice or even acquaintance: taking into account her secluded habits, and her long residence in such a house as Gride’s; the strong reason there was to suppose that the robbery was not the result of a concerted plan: otherwise she would have watched an opportunity of carrying off a sum of money; the difficulty she would be placed in when she began to think on what she had done, and found herself encumbered with documents of whose nature she was utterly ignorant; and the comparative ease with which somebody, with a full knowledge of her position, obtaining access to her, and working on her fears, if necessary, might worm himself into her confidence and obtain, under one pretence or another, free possession of the deed. To these were added such considerations as the constant residence of Mr. Squeers at a long distance from London, which rendered his association with Mrs Sliderskew a mere masquerading frolic, in which nobody was likely to recognise him, either at the time or afterwards; the impossibility of Ralph’s undertaking the task himself, he being already known to her by sight; and various comments on the uncommon tact and experience of Mr Squeers: which would make his overreaching one old woman a mere matter of child’s play and amusement. In addition to these influences and persuasions, Ralph drew, with his utmost skill and power, a vivid picture of the defeat which Nicholas would sustain, should they succeed, in linking himself to a beggar, where he expected to wed an heiress--glanced at the immeasurable importance it must be to a man situated as Squeers, to preserve such a friend as himself--dwelt on a long train of benefits, conferred since their first acquaintance, when he had reported favourably of his treatment of a sickly boy who had died under his hands (and whose death was very convenient to Ralph and his clients, but this he did NOT say), and finally hinted that the fifty pounds might be increased to seventy-five, or, in the event of very great success, even to a hundred.\n\nThese arguments at length concluded, Mr. Squeers crossed his legs, uncrossed them, scratched his head, rubbed his eye, examined the palms of his hands, and bit his nails, and after exhibiting many other signs of restlessness and indecision, asked ‘whether one hundred pound was the highest that Mr. Nickleby could go.’ Being answered in the affirmative, he became restless again, and, after some thought, and an unsuccessful inquiry ‘whether he couldn’t go another fifty,’ said he supposed he must try and do the most he could for a friend: which was always his maxim, and therefore he undertook the job.\n\n‘But how are you to get at the woman?’ he said; ‘that’s what it is as puzzles me.’\n\n‘I may not get at her at all,’ replied Ralph, ‘but I’ll try. I have hunted people in this city, before now, who have been better hid than she; and I know quarters in which a guinea or two, carefully spent, will often solve darker riddles than this. Ay, and keep them close too, if need be! I hear my man ringing at the door. We may as well part. You had better not come to and fro, but wait till you hear from me.’\n\n‘Good!’ returned Squeers. ‘I say! If you shouldn’t find her out, you’ll pay expenses at the Saracen, and something for loss of time?’\n\n‘Well,’ said Ralph, testily; ‘yes! You have nothing more to say?’\n\nSqueers shaking his head, Ralph accompanied him to the streetdoor, and audibly wondering, for the edification of Newman, why it was fastened as if it were night, let him in and Squeers out, and returned to his own room.\n\n‘Now!’ he muttered, ‘come what come may, for the present I am firm and unshaken. Let me but retrieve this one small portion of my loss and disgrace; let me but defeat him in this one hope, dear to his heart as I know it must be; let me but do this; and it shall be the first link in such a chain which I will wind about him, as never man forged yet.’\n\n# CHAPTER 57\n\nHow Ralph Nickleby’s Auxiliary went about his Work, and how he prospered with it\nIt was a dark, wet, gloomy night in autumn, when in an upper room of a mean house situated in an obscure street, or rather court, near Lambeth, there sat, all alone, a one-eyed man grotesquely habited, either for lack of better garments or for purposes of disguise, in a loose greatcoat, with arms half as long again as his own, and a capacity of breadth and length which would have admitted of his winding himself in it, head and all, with the utmost ease, and without any risk of straining the old and greasy material of which it was composed.\n\nSo attired, and in a place so far removed from his usual haunts and occupations, and so very poor and wretched in its character, perhaps Mrs Squeers herself would have had some difficulty in recognising her lord: quickened though her natural sagacity doubtless would have been by the affectionate yearnings and impulses of a tender wife. But Mrs. Squeers’s lord it was; and in a tolerably disconsolate mood Mrs. Squeers’s lord appeared to be, as, helping himself from a black bottle which stood on the table beside him, he cast round the chamber a look, in which very slight regard for the objects within view was plainly mingled with some regretful and impatient recollection of distant scenes and persons.\n\nThere were, certainly, no particular attractions, either in the room over which the glance of Mr. Squeers so discontentedly wandered, or in the narrow street into which it might have penetrated, if he had thought fit to approach the window. The attic chamber in which he sat was bare and mean; the bedstead, and such few other articles of necessary furniture as it contained, were of the commonest description, in a most crazy state, and of a most uninviting appearance. The street was muddy, dirty, and deserted. Having but one outlet, it was traversed by few but the inhabitants at any time; and the night being one of those on which most people are glad to be within doors, it now presented no other signs of life than the dull glimmering of poor candles from the dirty windows, and few sounds but the pattering of the rain, and occasionally the heavy closing of some creaking door.\n\nMr. Squeers continued to look disconsolately about him, and to listen to these noises in profound silence, broken only by the rustling of his large coat, as he now and then moved his arm to raise his glass to his lips. Mr. Squeers continued to do this for some time, until the increasing gloom warned him to snuff the candle. Seeming to be slightly roused by this exertion, he raised his eye to the ceiling, and fixing it upon some uncouth and fantastic figures, traced upon it by the wet and damp which had penetrated through the roof, broke into the following soliloquy:\n\n‘Well, this is a pretty go, is this here! An uncommon pretty go! Here have I been, a matter of how many weeks--hard upon six--a follering up this here blessed old dowager petty larcenerer,’--Mr. Squeers delivered himself of this epithet with great difficulty and effort,--‘and Dotheboys Hall a-running itself regularly to seed the while! That’s the worst of ever being in with a owdacious chap like that old Nickleby. You never know when he’s done with you, and if you’re in for a penny, you’re in for a pound.’\n\nThis remark, perhaps, reminded Mr. Squeers that he was in for a hundred pound at any rate. His countenance relaxed, and he raised his glass to his mouth with an air of greater enjoyment of its contents than he had before evinced.\n\n‘I never see,’ soliloquised Mr. Squeers in continuation, ‘I never see nor come across such a file as that old Nickleby. Never! He’s out of everybody’s depth, he is. He’s what you may call a rasper, is Nickleby. To see how sly and cunning he grubbed on, day after day, a-worming and plodding and tracing and turning and twining of hisself about, till he found out where this precious Mrs. Peg was hid, and cleared the ground for me to work upon. Creeping and crawling and gliding, like a ugly, old, bright-eyed, stagnation-blooded adder! Ah! He’d have made a good ‘un in our line, but it would have been too limited for him; his genius would have busted all bonds, and coming over every obstacle, broke down all before it, till it erected itself into a monneyment of--Well, I’ll think of the rest, and say it when conwenient.’\n\nMaking a halt in his reflections at this place, Mr. Squeers again put his glass to his lips, and drawing a dirty letter from his pocket, proceeded to con over its contents with the air of a man who had read it very often, and now refreshed his memory rather in the absence of better amusement than for any specific information.\n\n‘The pigs is well,’ said Mr. Squeers, ‘the cows is well, and the boys is bobbish. Young Sprouter has been a-winking, has he? I’ll wink him when I get back. “Cobbey would persist in sniffing while he was a-eating his dinner, and said that the beef was so strong it made him.”--Very good, Cobbey, we’ll see if we can’t make you sniff a little without beef. “Pitcher was took with another fever,”--of course he was--“and being fetched by his friends, died the day after he got home,”--of course he did, and out of aggravation; it’s part of a deep-laid system. There an’t another chap in the school but that boy as would have died exactly at the end of the quarter: taking it out of me to the very last, and then carrying his spite to the utmost extremity. “The juniorest Palmer said he wished he was in Heaven.” I really don’t know, I do NOT know what’s to be done with that young fellow; he’s always a-wishing something horrid. He said once, he wished he was a donkey, because then he wouldn’t have a father as didn’t love him! Pretty wicious that for a child of six!’\n\nMr. Squeers was so much moved by the contemplation of this hardened nature in one so young, that he angrily put up the letter, and sought, in a new train of ideas, a subject of consolation.\n\n‘It’s a long time to have been a-lingering in London,’ he said; ‘and this is a precious hole to come and live in, even if it has been only for a week or so. Still, one hundred pound is five boys, and five boys takes a whole year to pay one hundred pounds, and there’s their keep to be substracted, besides. There’s nothing lost, neither, by one’s being here; because the boys’ money comes in just the same as if I was at home, and Mrs. Squeers she keeps them in order. There’ll be some lost time to make up, of course. There’ll be an arrear of flogging as’ll have to be gone through: still, a couple of days makes that all right, and one don’t mind a little extra work for one hundred pound. It’s pretty nigh the time to wait upon the old woman. From what she said last night, I suspect that if I’m to succeed at all, I shall succeed tonight; so I’ll have half a glass more, to wish myself success, and put myself in spirits. Mrs. Squeers, my dear, your health!’\n\nLeering with his one eye as if the lady to whom he drank had been actually present, Mr. Squeers--in his enthusiasm, no doubt--poured out a full glass, and emptied it; and as the liquor was raw spirits, and he had applied himself to the same bottle more than once already, it is not surprising that he found himself, by this time, in an extremely cheerful state, and quite enough excited for his purpose.\n\nWhat this purpose was soon appeared; for, after a few turns about the room to steady himself, he took the bottle under his arm and the glass in his hand, and blowing out the candle as if he purposed being gone some time, stole out upon the staircase, and creeping softly to a door opposite his own, tapped gently at it.\n\n‘But what’s the use of tapping?’ he said, ‘She’ll never hear. I suppose she isn’t doing anything very particular; and if she is, it don’t much matter, that I see.’\n\nWith this brief preface, Mr. Squeers applied his hand to the latch of the door, and thrusting his head into a garret far more deplorable than that he had just left, and seeing that there was nobody there but an old woman, who was bending over a wretched fire (for although the weather was still warm, the evening was chilly), walked in, and tapped her on the shoulder.\n\n‘Well, my Slider,’ said Mr. Squeers, jocularly.\n\n‘Is that you?’ inquired Peg.\n\n‘Ah! it’s me, and me’s the first person singular, nominative case, agreeing with the verb “it’s”, and governed by Squeers understood, as a acorn, a hour; but when the h is sounded, the a only is to be used, as a and, a art, a ighway,’ replied Mr. Squeers, quoting at random from the grammar. ‘At least, if it isn’t, you don’t know any better, and if it is, I’ve done it accidentally.’\n\nDelivering this reply in his accustomed tone of voice, in which of course it was inaudible to Peg, Mr. Squeers drew a stool to the fire, and placing himself over against her, and the bottle and glass on the floor between them, roared out again, very loud,\n\n‘Well, my Slider!’\n\n‘I hear you,’ said Peg, receiving him very graciously.\n\n‘I’ve come according to promise,’ roared Squeers.\n\n‘So they used to say in that part of the country I come from,’ observed Peg, complacently, ‘but I think oil’s better.’\n\n‘Better than what?’ roared Squeers, adding some rather strong language in an undertone.\n\n‘No,’ said Peg, ‘of course not.’\n\n‘I never saw such a monster as you are!’ muttered Squeers, looking as amiable as he possibly could the while; for Peg’s eye was upon him, and she was chuckling fearfully, as though in delight at having made a choice repartee, ‘Do you see this? This is a bottle.’\n\n‘I see it,’ answered Peg.\n\n‘Well, and do you see THIS?’ bawled Squeers. ‘This is a glass.’ Peg saw that too.\n\n‘See here, then,’ said Squeers, accompanying his remarks with appropriate action, ‘I fill the glass from the bottle, and I say “Your health, Slider,” and empty it; then I rinse it genteelly with a little drop, which I’m forced to throw into the fire--hallo! we shall have the chimbley alight next--fill it again, and hand it over to you.’\n\n‘YOUR health,’ said Peg.\n\n‘She understands that, anyways,’ muttered Squeers, watching Mrs Sliderskew as she dispatched her portion, and choked and gasped in a most awful manner after so doing. ‘Now then, let’s have a talk. How’s the rheumatics?’\n\nMrs. Sliderskew, with much blinking and chuckling, and with looks expressive of her strong admiration of Mr. Squeers, his person, manners, and conversation, replied that the rheumatics were better.\n\n‘What’s the reason,’ said Mr. Squeers, deriving fresh facetiousness from the bottle; ‘what’s the reason of rheumatics? What do they mean? What do people have’em for--eh?’\n\nMrs. Sliderskew didn’t know, but suggested that it was possibly because they couldn’t help it.\n\n‘Measles, rheumatics, hooping-cough, fevers, agers, and lumbagers,’ said Mr. Squeers, ‘is all philosophy together; that’s what it is. The heavenly bodies is philosophy, and the earthly bodies is philosophy. If there’s a screw loose in a heavenly body, that’s philosophy; and if there’s screw loose in a earthly body, that’s philosophy too; or it may be that sometimes there’s a little metaphysics in it, but that’s not often. Philosophy’s the chap for me. If a parent asks a question in the classical, commercial, or mathematical line, says I, gravely, “Why, sir, in the first place, are you a philosopher?”--“No, Mr. Squeers,” he says, “I an’t.” “Then, sir,” says I, “I am sorry for you, for I shan’t be able to explain it.” Naturally, the parent goes away and wishes he was a philosopher, and, equally naturally, thinks I’m one.’\n\nSaying this, and a great deal more, with tipsy profundity and a serio-comic air, and keeping his eye all the time on Mrs. Sliderskew, who was unable to hear one word, Mr. Squeers concluded by helping himself and passing the bottle: to which Peg did becoming reverence.\n\n‘That’s the time of day!’ said Mr. Squeers. ‘You look twenty pound ten better than you did.’\n\nAgain Mrs. Sliderskew chuckled, but modesty forbade her assenting verbally to the compliment.\n\n‘Twenty pound ten better,’ repeated Mr. Squeers, ‘than you did that day when I first introduced myself. Don’t you know?’\n\n‘Ah!’ said Peg, shaking her head, ‘but you frightened me that day.’\n\n‘Did I?’ said Squeers; ‘well, it was rather a startling thing for a stranger to come and recommend himself by saying that he knew all about you, and what your name was, and why you were living so quiet here, and what you had boned, and who you boned it from, wasn’t it?’\n\nPeg nodded her head in strong assent.\n\n‘But I know everything that happens in that way, you see,’ continued Squeers. ‘Nothing takes place, of that kind, that I an’t up to entirely. I’m a sort of a lawyer, Slider, of first-rate standing, and understanding too; I’m the intimate friend and confidential adwiser of pretty nigh every man, woman, and child that gets themselves into difficulties by being too nimble with their fingers, I’m--’\n\nMr. Squeers’s catalogue of his own merits and accomplishments, which was partly the result of a concerted plan between himself and Ralph Nickleby, and flowed, in part, from the black bottle, was here interrupted by Mrs. Sliderskew.\n\n‘Ha, ha, ha!’ she cried, folding her arms and wagging her head; ‘and so he wasn’t married after all, wasn’t he. Not married after all?’\n\n‘No,’ replied Squeers, ‘that he wasn’t!’\n\n‘And a young lover come and carried off the bride, eh?’ said Peg.\n\n‘From under his very nose,’ replied Squeers; ‘and I’m told the young chap cut up rough besides, and broke the winders, and forced him to swaller his wedding favour which nearly choked him.’\n\n‘Tell me all about it again,’ cried Peg, with a malicious relish of her old master’s defeat, which made her natural hideousness something quite fearful; ‘let’s hear it all again, beginning at the beginning now, as if you’d never told me. Let’s have it every word--now--now--beginning at the very first, you know, when he went to the house that morning!’\n\nMr. Squeers, plying Mrs. Sliderskew freely with the liquor, and sustaining himself under the exertion of speaking so loud by frequent applications to it himself, complied with this request by describing the discomfiture of Arthur Gride, with such improvements on the truth as happened to occur to him, and the ingenious invention and application of which had been very instrumental in recommending him to her notice in the beginning of their acquaintance. Mrs. Sliderskew was in an ecstasy of delight, rolling her head about, drawing up her skinny shoulders, and wrinkling her cadaverous face into so many and such complicated forms of ugliness, as awakened the unbounded astonishment and disgust even of Mr Squeers.\n\n‘He’s a treacherous old goat,’ said Peg, ‘and cozened me with cunning tricks and lying promises, but never mind. I’m even with him. I’m even with him.’\n\n‘More than even, Slider,’ returned Squeers; ‘you’d have been even with him if he’d got married; but with the disappointment besides, you’re a long way ahead. Out of sight, Slider, quite out of sight. And that reminds me,’ he added, handing her the glass, ‘if you want me to give you my opinion of them deeds, and tell you what you’d better keep and what you’d better burn, why, now’s your time, Slider.’\n\n‘There an’t no hurry for that,’ said Peg, with several knowing looks and winks.\n\n‘Oh! very well!’ observed Squeers, ‘it don’t matter to me; you asked me, you know. I shouldn’t charge you nothing, being a friend. You’re the best judge of course. But you’re a bold woman, Slider.’\n\n‘How do you mean, bold?’ said Peg.\n\n‘Why, I only mean that if it was me, I wouldn’t keep papers as might hang me, littering about when they might be turned into money--them as wasn’t useful made away with, and them as was, laid by somewheres, safe; that’s all,’ returned Squeers; ‘but everybody’s the best judge of their own affairs. All I say is, Slider, I wouldn’t do it.’\n\n‘Come,’ said Peg, ‘then you shall see ‘em.’\n\n‘I don’t want to see ‘em,’ replied Squeers, affecting to be out of humour; ‘don’t talk as if it was a treat. Show ‘em to somebody else, and take their advice.’\n\nMr. Squeers would, very likely, have carried on the farce of being offended a little longer, if Mrs. Sliderskew, in her anxiety to restore herself to her former high position in his good graces, had not become so extremely affectionate that he stood at some risk of being smothered by her caresses. Repressing, with as good a grace as possible, these little familiarities--for which, there is reason to believe, the black bottle was at least as much to blame as any constitutional infirmity on the part of Mrs. Sliderskew--he protested that he had only been joking: and, in proof of his unimpaired good-humour, that he was ready to examine the deeds at once, if, by so doing, he could afford any satisfaction or relief of mind to his fair friend.\n\n‘And now you’re up, my Slider,’ bawled Squeers, as she rose to fetch them, ‘bolt the door.’\n\nPeg trotted to the door, and after fumbling at the bolt, crept to the other end of the room, and from beneath the coals which filled the bottom of the cupboard, drew forth a small deal box. Having placed this on the floor at Squeers’s feet, she brought, from under the pillow of her bed, a small key, with which she signed to that gentleman to open it. Mr. Squeers, who had eagerly followed her every motion, lost no time in obeying this hint: and, throwing back the lid, gazed with rapture on the documents which lay within.\n\n‘Now you see,’ said Peg, kneeling down on the floor beside him, and staying his impatient hand; ‘what’s of no use we’ll burn; what we can get any money by, we’ll keep; and if there’s any we could get him into trouble by, and fret and waste away his heart to shreds, those we’ll take particular care of; for that’s what I want to do, and what I hoped to do when I left him.’\n\n‘I thought,’ said Squeers, ‘that you didn’t bear him any particular good-will. But, I say, why didn’t you take some money besides?’\n\n‘Some what?’ asked Peg.\n\n‘Some money,’ roared Squeers. ‘I do believe the woman hears me, and wants to make me break a wessel, so that she may have the pleasure of nursing me. Some money, Slider, money!’\n\n‘Why, what a man you are to ask!’ cried Peg, with some contempt. ‘If I had taken money from Arthur Gride, he’d have scoured the whole earth to find me--aye, and he’d have smelt it out, and raked it up, somehow, if I had buried it at the bottom of the deepest well in England. No, no! I knew better than that. I took what I thought his secrets were hid in: and them he couldn’t afford to make public, let’em be worth ever so much money. He’s an old dog; a sly, old, cunning, thankless dog! He first starved, and then tricked me; and if I could I’d kill him.’\n\n‘All right, and very laudable,’ said Squeers. ‘But, first and foremost, Slider, burn the box. You should never keep things as may lead to discovery. Always mind that. So while you pull it to pieces (which you can easily do, for it’s very old and rickety) and burn it in little bits, I’ll look over the papers and tell you what they are.’\n\nPeg, expressing her acquiescence in this arrangement, Mr. Squeers turned the box bottom upwards, and tumbling the contents upon the floor, handed it to her; the destruction of the box being an extemporary device for engaging her attention, in case it should prove desirable to distract it from his own proceedings.\n\n‘There!’ said Squeers; ‘you poke the pieces between the bars, and make up a good fire, and I’ll read the while. Let me see, let me see.’ And taking the candle down beside him, Mr. Squeers, with great eagerness and a cunning grin overspreading his face, entered upon his task of examination.\n\nIf the old woman had not been very deaf, she must have heard, when she last went to the door, the breathing of two persons close behind it: and if those two persons had been unacquainted with her infirmity, they must probably have chosen that moment either for presenting themselves or taking to flight. But, knowing with whom they had to deal, they remained quite still, and now, not only appeared unobserved at the door--which was not bolted, for the bolt had no hasp--but warily, and with noiseless footsteps, advanced into the room.\n\nAs they stole farther and farther in by slight and scarcely perceptible degrees, and with such caution that they scarcely seemed to breathe, the old hag and Squeers little dreaming of any such invasion, and utterly unconscious of there being any soul near but themselves, were busily occupied with their tasks. The old woman, with her wrinkled face close to the bars of the stove, puffing at the dull embers which had not yet caught the wood; Squeers stooping down to the candle, which brought out the full ugliness of his face, as the light of the fire did that of his companion; both intently engaged, and wearing faces of exultation which contrasted strongly with the anxious looks of those behind, who took advantage of the slightest sound to cover their advance, and, almost before they had moved an inch, and all was silent, stopped again. This, with the large bare room, damp walls, and flickering doubtful light, combined to form a scene which the most careless and indifferent spectator (could any have been present) could scarcely have failed to derive some interest from, and would not readily have forgotten.\n\nOf the stealthy comers, Frank Cheeryble was one, and Newman Noggs the other. Newman had caught up, by the rusty nozzle, an old pair of bellows, which were just undergoing a flourish in the air preparatory to a descent upon the head of Mr. Squeers, when Frank, with an earnest gesture, stayed his arm, and, taking another step in advance, came so close behind the schoolmaster that, by leaning slightly forward, he could plainly distinguish the writing which he held up to his eye.\n\nMr. Squeers, not being remarkably erudite, appeared to be considerably puzzled by this first prize, which was in an engrossing hand, and not very legible except to a practised eye. Having tried it by reading from left to right, and from right to left, and finding it equally clear both ways, he turned it upside down with no better success.\n\n‘Ha, ha, ha!’ chuckled Peg, who, on her knees before the fire, was feeding it with fragments of the box, and grinning in most devilish exultation. ‘What’s that writing about, eh?’\n\n‘Nothing particular,’ replied Squeers, tossing it towards her. ‘It’s only an old lease, as well as I can make out. Throw it in the fire.’\n\nMrs. Sliderskew complied, and inquired what the next one was.\n\n‘This,’ said Squeers, ‘is a bundle of overdue acceptances and renewed bills of six or eight young gentlemen, but they’re all MPs, so it’s of no use to anybody. Throw it in the fire!’ Peg did as she was bidden, and waited for the next.\n\n‘This,’ said Squeers, ‘seems to be some deed of sale of the right of presentation to the rectory of Purechurch, in the valley of Cashup. Take care of that, Slider, literally for God’s sake. It’ll fetch its price at the Auction Mart.’\n\n‘What’s the next?’ inquired Peg.\n\n‘Why, this,’ said Squeers, ‘seems, from the two letters that’s with it, to be a bond from a curate down in the country, to pay half a year’s wages of forty pound for borrowing twenty. Take care of that, for if he don’t pay it, his bishop will very soon be down upon him. We know what the camel and the needle’s eye means; no man as can’t live upon his income, whatever it is, must expect to go to heaven at any price. It’s very odd; I don’t see anything like it yet.’\n\n‘What’s the matter?’ said Peg.\n\n‘Nothing,’ replied Squeers, ‘only I’m looking for--’\n\nNewman raised the bellows again. Once more, Frank, by a rapid motion of his arm, unaccompanied by any noise, checked him in his purpose.\n\n‘Here you are,’ said Squeers, ‘bonds--take care of them. Warrant of attorney--take care of that. Two cognovits--take care of them. Lease and release--burn that. Ah! “Madeline Bray--come of age or marry--the said Madeline”--here, burn THAT!’\n\nEagerly throwing towards the old woman a parchment that he caught up for the purpose, Squeers, as she turned her head, thrust into the breast of his large coat, the deed in which these words had caught his eye, and burst into a shout of triumph.\n\n‘I’ve got it!’ said Squeers. ‘I’ve got it! Hurrah! The plan was a good one, though the chance was desperate, and the day’s our own at last!’\n\nPeg demanded what he laughed at, but no answer was returned. Newman’s arm could no longer be restrained; the bellows, descending heavily and with unerring aim on the very centre of Mr. Squeers’s head, felled him to the floor, and stretched him on it flat and senseless.\n\nIn which one Scene of this History is closed\nDividing the distance into two days’ journey, in order that his charge might sustain the less exhaustion and fatigue from travelling so far, Nicholas, at the end of the second day from their leaving home, found himself within a very few miles of the spot where the happiest years of his life had been passed, and which, while it filled his mind with pleasant and peaceful thoughts, brought back many painful and vivid recollections of the circumstances in which he and his had wandered forth from their old home, cast upon the rough world and the mercy of strangers.\n\nIt needed no such reflections as those which the memory of old days, and wanderings among scenes where our childhood has been passed, usually awaken in the most insensible minds, to soften the heart of Nicholas, and render him more than usually mindful of his drooping friend. By night and day, at all times and seasons: always watchful, attentive, and solicitous, and never varying in the discharge of his self-imposed duty to one so friendless and helpless as he whose sands of life were now fast running out and dwindling rapidly away: he was ever at his side. He never left him. To encourage and animate him, administer to his wants, support and cheer him to the utmost of his power, was now his constant and unceasing occupation.\n\nThey procured a humble lodging in a small farmhouse, surrounded by meadows where Nicholas had often revelled when a child with a troop of merry schoolfellows; and here they took up their rest.\n\nAt first, Smike was strong enough to walk about, for short distances at a time, with no other support or aid than that which Nicholas could afford him. At this time, nothing appeared to interest him so much as visiting those places which had been most familiar to his friend in bygone days. Yielding to this fancy, and pleased to find that its indulgence beguiled the sick boy of many tedious hours, and never failed to afford him matter for thought and conversation afterwards, Nicholas made such spots the scenes of their daily rambles: driving him from place to place in a little pony-chair, and supporting him on his arm while they walked slowly among these old haunts, or lingered in the sunlight to take long parting looks of those which were most quiet and beautiful.\n\nIt was on such occasions as these, that Nicholas, yielding almost unconsciously to the interest of old associations, would point out some tree that he had climbed, a hundred times, to peep at the young birds in their nest; and the branch from which he used to shout to little Kate, who stood below terrified at the height he had gained, and yet urging him higher still by the intensity of her admiration. There was the old house too, which they would pass every day, looking up at the tiny window through which the sun used to stream in and wake him on the summer mornings--they were all summer mornings then--and climbing up the garden-wall and looking over, Nicholas could see the very rose-bush which had come, a present to Kate, from some little lover, and she had planted with her own hands. There were the hedgerows where the brother and sister had so often gathered wild flowers together, and the green fields and shady paths where they had so often strayed. There was not a lane, or brook, or copse, or cottage near, with which some childish event was not entwined, and back it came upon the mind--as events of childhood do--nothing in itself: perhaps a word, a laugh, a look, some slight distress, a passing thought or fear: and yet more strongly and distinctly marked, and better remembered, than the hardest trials or severest sorrows of a year ago.\n\nOne of these expeditions led them through the churchyard where was his father’s grave. ‘Even here,’ said Nicholas softly, ‘we used to loiter before we knew what death was, and when we little thought whose ashes would rest beneath; and, wondering at the silence, sit down to rest and speak below our breath. Once, Kate was lost, and after an hour of fruitless search, they found her, fast asleep, under that tree which shades my father’s grave. He was very fond of her, and said when he took her up in his arms, still sleeping, that whenever he died he would wish to be buried where his dear little child had laid her head. You see his wish was not forgotten.’\n\nNothing more passed at the time, but that night, as Nicholas sat beside his bed, Smike started from what had seemed to be a slumber, and laying his hand in his, prayed, as the tears coursed down his face, that he would make him one solemn promise.\n\n‘What is that?’ said Nicholas, kindly. ‘If I can redeem it, or hope to do so, you know I will.’\n\n‘I am sure you will,’ was the reply. ‘Promise me that when I die, I shall be buried near--as near as they can make my grave--to the tree we saw today.’\n\nNicholas gave the promise; he had few words to give it in, but they were solemn and earnest. His poor friend kept his hand in his, and turned as if to sleep. But there were stifled sobs; and the hand was pressed more than once, or twice, or thrice, before he sank to rest, and slowly loosed his hold.\n\nIn a fortnight’s time, he became too ill to move about. Once or twice, Nicholas drove him out, propped up with pillows; but the motion of the chaise was painful to him, and brought on fits of fainting, which, in his weakened state, were dangerous. There was an old couch in the house, which was his favourite resting-place by day; and when the sun shone, and the weather was warm, Nicholas had this wheeled into a little orchard which was close at hand, and his charge being well wrapped up and carried out to it, they used to sit there sometimes for hours together.\n\nIt was on one of these occasions that a circumstance took place, which Nicholas, at the time, thoroughly believed to be the mere delusion of an imagination affected by disease; but which he had, afterwards, too good reason to know was of real and actual occurrence.\n\nHe had brought Smike out in his arms--poor fellow! a child might have carried him then--to see the sunset, and, having arranged his couch, had taken his seat beside it. He had been watching the whole of the night before, and being greatly fatigued both in mind and body, gradually fell asleep.\n\nHe could not have closed his eyes five minutes, when he was awakened by a scream, and starting up in that kind of terror which affects a person suddenly roused, saw, to his great astonishment, that his charge had struggled into a sitting posture, and with eyes almost starting from their sockets, cold dew standing on his forehead, and in a fit of trembling which quite convulsed his frame, was calling to him for help.\n\n‘Good Heaven, what is this?’ said Nicholas, bending over him. ‘Be calm; you have been dreaming.’\n\n‘No, no, no!’ cried Smike, clinging to him. ‘Hold me tight. Don’t let me go. There, there. Behind the tree!’\n\nNicholas followed his eyes, which were directed to some distance behind the chair from which he himself had just risen. But, there was nothing there.\n\n‘This is nothing but your fancy,’ he said, as he strove to compose him; ‘nothing else, indeed.’\n\n‘I know better. I saw as plain as I see now,’ was the answer. ‘Oh! say you’ll keep me with you. Swear you won’t leave me for an instant!’\n\n‘Do I ever leave you?’ returned Nicholas. ‘Lie down again--there! You see I’m here. Now, tell me; what was it?’\n\n‘Do you remember,’ said Smike, in a low voice, and glancing fearfully round, ‘do you remember my telling you of the man who first took me to the school?’\n\n‘Yes, surely.’\n\n‘I raised my eyes, just now, towards that tree--that one with the thick trunk--and there, with his eyes fixed on me, he stood!’\n\n‘Only reflect for one moment,’ said Nicholas; ‘granting, for an instant, that it’s likely he is alive and wandering about a lonely place like this, so far removed from the public road, do you think that at this distance of time you could possibly know that man again?’\n\n‘Anywhere--in any dress,’ returned Smike; ‘but, just now, he stood leaning upon his stick and looking at me, exactly as I told you I remembered him. He was dusty with walking, and poorly dressed--I think his clothes were ragged--but directly I saw him, the wet night, his face when he left me, the parlour I was left in, and the people that were there, all seemed to come back together. When he knew I saw him, he looked frightened; for he started, and shrunk away. I have thought of him by day, and dreamt of him by night. He looked in my sleep, when I was quite a little child, and has looked in my sleep ever since, as he did just now.’\n\nNicholas endeavoured, by every persuasion and argument he could think of, to convince the terrified creature that his imagination had deceived him, and that this close resemblance between the creation of his dreams and the man he supposed he had seen was but a proof of it; but all in vain. When he could persuade him to remain, for a few moments, in the care of the people to whom the house belonged, he instituted a strict inquiry whether any stranger had been seen, and searched himself behind the tree, and through the orchard, and upon the land immediately adjoining, and in every place near, where it was possible for a man to lie concealed; but all in vain. Satisfied that he was right in his original conjecture, he applied himself to calming the fears of Smike, which, after some time, he partially succeeded in doing, though not in removing the impression upon his mind; for he still declared, again and again, in the most solemn and fervid manner, that he had positively seen what he had described, and that nothing could ever remove his conviction of its reality.\n\nAnd now, Nicholas began to see that hope was gone, and that, upon the partner of his poverty, and the sharer of his better fortune, the world was closing fast. There was little pain, little uneasiness, but there was no rallying, no effort, no struggle for life. He was worn and wasted to the last degree; his voice had sunk so low, that he could scarce be heard to speak. Nature was thoroughly exhausted, and he had lain him down to die.\n\nOn a fine, mild autumn day, when all was tranquil and at peace: when the soft sweet air crept in at the open window of the quiet room, and not a sound was heard but the gentle rustling of the leaves: Nicholas sat in his old place by the bedside, and knew that the time was nearly come. So very still it was, that, every now and then, he bent down his ear to listen for the breathing of him who lay asleep, as if to assure himself that life was still there, and that he had not fallen into that deep slumber from which on earth there is no waking.\n\nWhile he was thus employed, the closed eyes opened, and on the pale face there came a placid smile.\n\n‘That’s well!’ said Nicholas. ‘The sleep has done you good.’\n\n‘I have had such pleasant dreams,’ was the answer. ‘Such pleasant, happy dreams!’\n\n‘Of what?’ said Nicholas.\n\nThe dying boy turned towards him, and, putting his arm about his neck, made answer, ‘I shall soon be there!’\n\nAfter a short silence, he spoke again.\n\n‘I am not afraid to die,’ he said. ‘I am quite contented. I almost think that if I could rise from this bed quite well I would not wish to do so, now. You have so often told me we shall meet again--so very often lately, and now I feel the truth of that so strongly--that I can even bear to part from you.’\n\nThe trembling voice and tearful eye, and the closer grasp of the arm which accompanied these latter words, showed how they filled the speaker’s heart; nor were there wanting indications of how deeply they had touched the heart of him to whom they were addressed.\n\n‘You say well,’ returned Nicholas at length, ‘and comfort me very much, dear fellow. Let me hear you say you are happy, if you can.’\n\n‘I must tell you something, first. I should not have a secret from you. You would not blame me, at a time like this, I know.’\n\n‘I blame you!’ exclaimed Nicholas.\n\n‘I am sure you would not. You asked me why I was so changed, and--and sat so much alone. Shall I tell you why?’\n\n‘Not if it pains you,’ said Nicholas. ‘I only asked that I might make you happier, if I could.’\n\n‘I know. I felt that, at the time.’ He drew his friend closer to him. ‘You will forgive me; I could not help it, but though I would have died to make her happy, it broke my heart to see--I know he loves her dearly--Oh! who could find that out so soon as I?’\n\nThe words which followed were feebly and faintly uttered, and broken by long pauses; but, from them, Nicholas learnt, for the first time, that the dying boy, with all the ardour of a nature concentrated on one absorbing, hopeless, secret passion, loved his sister Kate.\n\nHe had procured a lock of her hair, which hung at his breast, folded in one or two slight ribbons she had worn. He prayed that, when he was dead, Nicholas would take it off, so that no eyes but his might see it, and that when he was laid in his coffin and about to be placed in the earth, he would hang it round his neck again, that it might rest with him in the grave.\n\nUpon his knees Nicholas gave him this pledge, and promised again that he should rest in the spot he had pointed out. They embraced, and kissed each other on the cheek.\n\n‘Now,’ he murmured, ‘I am happy.’\n\nHe fell into a light slumber, and waking smiled as before; then, spoke of beautiful gardens, which he said stretched out before him, and were filled with figures of men, women, and many children, all with light upon their faces; then, whispered that it was Eden--and so died.\n\n# CHAPTER 59\n\nThe Plots begin to fail, and Doubts and Dangers to disturb the Plotter\nRalph sat alone, in the solitary room where he was accustomed to take his meals, and to sit of nights when no profitable occupation called him abroad. Before him was an untasted breakfast, and near to where his fingers beat restlessly upon the table, lay his watch. It was long past the time at which, for many years, he had put it in his pocket and gone with measured steps downstairs to the business of the day, but he took as little heed of its monotonous warning, as of the meat and drink before him, and remained with his head resting on one hand, and his eyes fixed moodily on the ground.\n\nThis departure from his regular and constant habit, in one so regular and unvarying in all that appertained to the daily pursuit of riches, would almost of itself have told that the usurer was not well. That he laboured under some mental or bodily indisposition, and that it was one of no slight kind so to affect a man like him, was sufficiently shown by his haggard face, jaded air, and hollow languid eyes: which he raised at last with a start and a hasty glance around him, as one who suddenly awakes from sleep, and cannot immediately recognise the place in which he finds himself.\n\n‘What is this,’ he said, ‘that hangs over me, and I cannot shake off? I have never pampered myself, and should not be ill. I have never moped, and pined, and yielded to fancies; but what CAN a man do without rest?’\n\nHe pressed his hand upon his forehead.\n\n‘Night after night comes and goes, and I have no rest. If I sleep, what rest is that which is disturbed by constant dreams of the same detested faces crowding round me--of the same detested people, in every variety of action, mingling with all I say and do, and always to my defeat? Waking, what rest have I, constantly haunted by this heavy shadow of--I know not what--which is its worst character? I must have rest. One night’s unbroken rest, and I should be a man again.’\n\nPushing the table from him while he spoke, as though he loathed the sight of food, he encountered the watch: the hands of which were almost upon noon.\n\n‘This is strange!’ he said; ‘noon, and Noggs not here! What drunken brawl keeps him away? I would give something now--something in money even after that dreadful loss--if he had stabbed a man in a tavern scuffle, or broken into a house, or picked a pocket, or done anything that would send him abroad with an iron ring upon his leg, and rid me of him. Better still, if I could throw temptation in his way, and lure him on to rob me. He should be welcome to what he took, so I brought the law upon him; for he is a traitor, I swear! How, or when, or where, I don’t know, though I suspect.’\n\nAfter waiting for another half-hour, he dispatched the woman who kept his house to Newman’s lodging, to inquire if he were ill, and why he had not come or sent. She brought back answer that he had not been home all night, and that no one could tell her anything about him.\n\n‘But there is a gentleman, sir,’ she said, ‘below, who was standing at the door when I came in, and he says--’\n\n‘What says he?’ demanded Ralph, turning angrily upon her. ‘I told you I would see nobody.’\n\n‘He says,’ replied the woman, abashed by his harshness, ‘that he comes on very particular business which admits of no excuse; and I thought perhaps it might be about--’\n\n‘About what, in the devil’s name?’ said Ralph. ‘You spy and speculate on people’s business with me, do you?’\n\n‘Dear, no, sir! I saw you were anxious, and thought it might be about Mr Noggs; that’s all.’\n\n‘Saw I was anxious!’ muttered Ralph; ‘they all watch me, now. Where is this person? You did not say I was not down yet, I hope?’\n\nThe woman replied that he was in the little office, and that she had said her master was engaged, but she would take the message.\n\n‘Well,’ said Ralph, ‘I’ll see him. Go you to your kitchen, and keep there. Do you mind me?’\n\nGlad to be released, the woman quickly disappeared. Collecting himself, and assuming as much of his accustomed manner as his utmost resolution could summon, Ralph descended the stairs. After pausing for a few moments, with his hand upon the lock, he entered Newman’s room, and confronted Mr. Charles Cheeryble.\n\nOf all men alive, this was one of the last he would have wished to meet at any time; but, now that he recognised in him only the patron and protector of Nicholas, he would rather have seen a spectre. One beneficial effect, however, the encounter had upon him. It instantly roused all his dormant energies; rekindled in his breast the passions that, for many years, had found an improving home there; called up all his wrath, hatred, and malice; restored the sneer to his lip, and the scowl to his brow; and made him again, in all outward appearance, the same Ralph Nickleby whom so many had bitter cause to remember.\n\n‘Humph!’ said Ralph, pausing at the door. ‘This is an unexpected favour, sir.’\n\n‘And an unwelcome one,’ said brother Charles; ‘an unwelcome one, I know.’\n\n‘Men say you are truth itself, sir,’ replied Ralph. ‘You speak truth now, at all events, and I’ll not contradict you. The favour is, at least, as unwelcome as it is unexpected. I can scarcely say more.’\n\n‘Plainly, sir--’ began brother Charles.\n\n‘Plainly, sir,’ interrupted Ralph, ‘I wish this conference to be a short one, and to end where it begins. I guess the subject upon which you are about to speak, and I’ll not hear you. You like plainness, I believe; there it is. Here is the door as you see. Our way lies in very different directions. Take yours, I beg of you, and leave me to pursue mine in quiet.’\n\n‘In quiet!’ repeated brother Charles mildly, and looking at him with more of pity than reproach. ‘To pursue HIS way in quiet!’\n\n‘You will scarcely remain in my house, I presume, sir, against my will,’ said Ralph; ‘or you can scarcely hope to make an impression upon a man who closes his ears to all that you can say, and is firmly and resolutely determined not to hear you.’\n\n‘Mr. Nickleby, sir,’ returned brother Charles: no less mildly than before, but firmly too: ‘I come here against my will, sorely and grievously against my will. I have never been in this house before; and, to speak my mind, sir, I don’t feel at home or easy in it, and have no wish ever to be here again. You do not guess the subject on which I come to speak to you; you do not indeed. I am sure of that, or your manner would be a very different one.’\n\nRalph glanced keenly at him, but the clear eye and open countenance of the honest old merchant underwent no change of expression, and met his look without reserve.\n\n‘Shall I go on?’ said Mr. Cheeryble.\n\n‘Oh, by all means, if you please,’ returned Ralph drily. ‘Here are walls to speak to, sir, a desk, and two stools: most attentive auditors, and certain not to interrupt you. Go on, I beg; make my house yours, and perhaps by the time I return from my walk, you will have finished what you have to say, and will yield me up possession again.’\n\nSo saying, he buttoned his coat, and turning into the passage, took down his hat. The old gentleman followed, and was about to speak, when Ralph waved him off impatiently, and said:\n\n‘Not a word. I tell you, sir, not a word. Virtuous as you are, you are not an angel yet, to appear in men’s houses whether they will or no, and pour your speech into unwilling ears. Preach to the walls I tell you; not to me!’\n\n‘I am no angel, Heaven knows,’ returned brother Charles, shaking his head, ‘but an erring and imperfect man; nevertheless, there is one quality which all men have, in common with the angels, blessed opportunities of exercising, if they will; mercy. It is an errand of mercy that brings me here. Pray let me discharge it.’\n\n‘I show no mercy,’ retorted Ralph with a triumphant smile, ‘and I ask none. Seek no mercy from me, sir, in behalf of the fellow who has imposed upon your childish credulity, but let him expect the worst that I can do.’\n\n‘HE ask mercy at your hands!’ exclaimed the old merchant warmly; ‘ask it at his, sir; ask it at his. If you will not hear me now, when you may, hear me when you must, or anticipate what I would say, and take measures to prevent our ever meeting again. Your nephew is a noble lad, sir, an honest, noble lad. What you are, Mr. Nickleby, I will not say; but what you have done, I know. Now, sir, when you go about the business in which you have been recently engaged, and find it difficult of pursuing, come to me and my brother Ned, and Tim Linkinwater, sir, and we’ll explain it for you--and come soon, or it may be too late, and you may have it explained with a little more roughness, and a little less delicacy--and never forget, sir, that I came here this morning, in mercy to you, and am still ready to talk to you in the same spirit.’\n\nWith these words, uttered with great emphasis and emotion, brother Charles put on his broad-brimmed hat, and, passing Ralph Nickleby without any other remark, trotted nimbly into the street. Ralph looked after him, but neither moved nor spoke for some time: when he broke what almost seemed the silence of stupefaction, by a scornful laugh.\n\n‘This,’ he said, ‘from its wildness, should be another of those dreams that have so broken my rest of late. In mercy to me! Pho! The old simpleton has gone mad.’\n\nAlthough he expressed himself in this derisive and contemptuous manner, it was plain that, the more Ralph pondered, the more ill at ease he became, and the more he laboured under some vague anxiety and alarm, which increased as the time passed on and no tidings of Newman Noggs arrived. After waiting until late in the afternoon, tortured by various apprehensions and misgivings, and the recollection of the warning which his nephew had given him when they last met: the further confirmation of which now presented itself in one shape of probability, now in another, and haunted him perpetually: he left home, and, scarcely knowing why, save that he was in a suspicious and agitated mood, betook himself to Snawley’s house. His wife presented herself; and, of her, Ralph inquired whether her husband was at home.\n\n‘No,’ she said sharply, ‘he is not indeed, and I don’t think he will be at home for a very long time; that’s more.’\n\n‘Do you know who I am?’ asked Ralph.\n\n‘Oh yes, I know you very well; too well, perhaps, and perhaps he does too, and sorry am I that I should have to say it.’\n\n‘Tell him that I saw him through the window-blind above, as I crossed the road just now, and that I would speak to him on business,’ said Ralph. ‘Do you hear?’\n\n‘I hear,’ rejoined Mrs. Snawley, taking no further notice of the request.\n\n‘I knew this woman was a hypocrite, in the way of psalms and Scripture phrases,’ said Ralph, passing quietly by, ‘but I never knew she drank before.’\n\n‘Stop! You don’t come in here,’ said Mr. Snawley’s better-half, interposing her person, which was a robust one, in the doorway. ‘You have said more than enough to him on business, before now. I always told him what dealing with you and working out your schemes would come to. It was either you or the schoolmaster--one of you, or the two between you--that got the forged letter done; remember that! That wasn’t his doing, so don’t lay it at his door.’\n\n‘Hold your tongue, you Jezebel,’ said Ralph, looking fearfully round.\n\n‘Ah, I know when to hold my tongue, and when to speak, Mr. Nickleby,’ retorted the dame. ‘Take care that other people know when to hold theirs.’\n\n‘You jade,’ said Ralph, ‘if your husband has been idiot enough to trust you with his secrets, keep them; keep them, she-devil that you are!’\n\n‘Not so much his secrets as other people’s secrets, perhaps,’ retorted the woman; ‘not so much his secrets as yours. None of your black looks at me! You’ll want ‘em all, perhaps, for another time. You had better keep ‘em.’\n\n‘Will you,’ said Ralph, suppressing his passion as well as he could, and clutching her tightly by the wrist; ‘will you go to your husband and tell him that I know he is at home, and that I must see him? And will you tell me what it is that you and he mean by this new style of behaviour?’\n\n‘No,’ replied the woman, violently disengaging herself, ‘I’ll do neither.’\n\n‘You set me at defiance, do you?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Yes,’ was the answer. I do.’\n\nFor an instant Ralph had his hand raised, as though he were about to strike her; but, checking himself, and nodding his head and muttering as though to assure her he would not forget this, walked away.\n\nThence, he went straight to the inn which Mr. Squeers frequented, and inquired when he had been there last; in the vague hope that, successful or unsuccessful, he might, by this time, have returned from his mission and be able to assure him that all was safe. But Mr. Squeers had not been there for ten days, and all that the people could tell about him was, that he had left his luggage and his bill.\n\nDisturbed by a thousand fears and surmises, and bent upon ascertaining whether Squeers had any suspicion of Snawley, or was, in any way, a party to this altered behaviour, Ralph determined to hazard the extreme step of inquiring for him at the Lambeth lodging, and having an interview with him even there. Bent upon this purpose, and in that mood in which delay is insupportable, he repaired at once to the place; and being, by description, perfectly acquainted with the situation of his room, crept upstairs and knocked gently at the door.\n\nNot one, nor two, nor three, nor yet a dozen knocks, served to convince Ralph, against his wish, that there was nobody inside. He reasoned that he might be asleep; and, listening, almost persuaded himself that he could hear him breathe. Even when he was satisfied that he could not be there, he sat patiently on a broken stair and waited; arguing, that he had gone out upon some slight errand, and must soon return.\n\nMany feet came up the creaking stairs; and the step of some seemed to his listening ear so like that of the man for whom he waited, that Ralph often stood up to be ready to address him when he reached the top; but, one by one, each person turned off into some room short of the place where he was stationed: and at every such disappointment he felt quite chilled and lonely.\n\nAt length he felt it was hopeless to remain, and going downstairs again, inquired of one of the lodgers if he knew anything of Mr. Squeers’s movements--mentioning that worthy by an assumed name which had been agreed upon between them. By this lodger he was referred to another, and by him to someone else, from whom he learnt, that, late on the previous night, he had gone out hastily with two men, who had shortly afterwards returned for the old woman who lived on the same floor; and that, although the circumstance had attracted the attention of the informant, he had not spoken to them at the time, nor made any inquiry afterwards.\n\nThis possessed him with the idea that, perhaps, Peg Sliderskew had been apprehended for the robbery, and that Mr. Squeers, being with her at the time, had been apprehended also, on suspicion of being a confederate. If this were so, the fact must be known to Gride; and to Gride’s house he directed his steps; now thoroughly alarmed, and fearful that there were indeed plots afoot, tending to his discomfiture and ruin.\n\nArrived at the usurer’s house, he found the windows close shut, the dingy blinds drawn down; all was silent, melancholy, and deserted. But this was its usual aspect. He knocked--gently at first--then loud and vigorously. Nobody came. He wrote a few words in pencil on a card, and having thrust it under the door was going away, when a noise above, as though a window-sash were stealthily raised, caught his ear, and looking up he could just discern the face of Gride himself, cautiously peering over the house parapet from the window of the garret. Seeing who was below, he drew it in again; not so quickly, however, but that Ralph let him know he was observed, and called to him to come down.\n\nThe call being repeated, Gride looked out again, so cautiously that no part of the old man’s body was visible. The sharp features and white hair appearing alone, above the parapet, looked like a severed head garnishing the wall.\n\n‘Hush!’ he cried. ‘Go away, go away!’\n\n‘Come down,’ said Ralph, beckoning him.\n\n‘Go a--way!’ squeaked Gride, shaking his head in a sort of ecstasy of impatience. ‘Don’t speak to me, don’t knock, don’t call attention to the house, but go away.’\n\n‘I’ll knock, I swear, till I have your neighbours up in arms,’ said Ralph, ‘if you don’t tell me what you mean by lurking there, you whining cur.’\n\n‘I can’t hear what you say--don’t talk to me--it isn’t safe--go away--go away!’ returned Gride.\n\n‘Come down, I say. Will you come down?’ said Ralph fiercely.\n\n‘No--o--o--oo,’ snarled Gride. He drew in his head; and Ralph, left standing in the street, could hear the sash closed, as gently and carefully as it had been opened.\n\n‘How is this,’ said he, ‘that they all fall from me, and shun me like the plague, these men who have licked the dust from my feet? IS my day past, and is this indeed the coming on of night? I’ll know what it means! I will, at any cost. I am firmer and more myself, just now, than I have been these many days.’\n\nTurning from the door, which, in the first transport of his rage, he had meditated battering upon until Gride’s very fears should impel him to open it, he turned his face towards the city, and working his way steadily through the crowd which was pouring from it (it was by this time between five and six o’clock in the afternoon) went straight to the house of business of the brothers Cheeryble, and putting his head into the glass case, found Tim Linkinwater alone.\n\n‘My name’s Nickleby,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘I know it,’ replied Tim, surveying him through his spectacles.\n\n‘Which of your firm was it who called on me this morning?’ demanded Ralph.\n\n‘Mr. Charles.’\n\n‘Then, tell Mr. Charles I want to see him.’\n\n‘You shall see,’ said Tim, getting off his stool with great agility, ‘you shall see, not only Mr. Charles, but Mr. Ned likewise.’\n\nTim stopped, looked steadily and severely at Ralph, nodded his head once, in a curt manner which seemed to say there was a little more behind, and vanished. After a short interval, he returned, and, ushering Ralph into the presence of the two brothers, remained in the room himself.\n\n‘I want to speak to you, who spoke to me this morning,’ said Ralph, pointing out with his finger the man whom he addressed.\n\n‘I have no secrets from my brother Ned, or from Tim Linkinwater,’ observed brother Charles quietly.\n\n‘I have,’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Mr. Nickleby, sir,’ said brother Ned, ‘the matter upon which my brother Charles called upon you this morning is one which is already perfectly well known to us three, and to others besides, and must unhappily soon become known to a great many more. He waited upon you, sir, this morning, alone, as a matter of delicacy and consideration. We feel, now, that further delicacy and consideration would be misplaced; and, if we confer together, it must be as we are or not at all.’\n\n‘Well, gentlemen,’ said Ralph with a curl of the lip, ‘talking in riddles would seem to be the peculiar forte of you two, and I suppose your clerk, like a prudent man, has studied the art also with a view to your good graces. Talk in company, gentlemen, in God’s name. I’ll humour you.’\n\n‘Humour!’ cried Tim Linkinwater, suddenly growing very red in the face. ‘He’ll humour us! He’ll humour Cheeryble Brothers! Do you hear that? Do you hear him? DO you hear him say he’ll humour Cheeryble Brothers?’\n\n‘Tim,’ said Charles and Ned together, ‘pray, Tim, pray now, don’t.’\n\nTim, taking the hint, stifled his indignation as well as he could, and suffered it to escape through his spectacles, with the additional safety-valve of a short hysterical laugh now and then, which seemed to relieve him mightily.\n\n‘As nobody bids me to a seat,’ said Ralph, looking round, ‘I’ll take one, for I am fatigued with walking. And now, if you please, gentlemen, I wish to know--I demand to know; I have the right--what you have to say to me, which justifies such a tone as you have assumed, and that underhand interference in my affairs which, I have reason to suppose, you have been practising. I tell you plainly, gentlemen, that little as I care for the opinion of the world (as the slang goes), I don’t choose to submit quietly to slander and malice. Whether you suffer yourselves to be imposed upon too easily, or wilfully make yourselves parties to it, the result to me is the same. In either case, you can’t expect from a plain man like myself much consideration or forbearance.’\n\nSo coolly and deliberately was this said, that nine men out of ten, ignorant of the circumstances, would have supposed Ralph to be really an injured man. There he sat, with folded arms; paler than usual, certainly, and sufficiently ill-favoured, but quite collected--far more so than the brothers or the exasperated Tim--and ready to face out the worst.\n\n‘Very well, sir,’ said brother Charles. ‘Very well. Brother Ned, will you ring the bell?’\n\n‘Charles, my dear fellow! stop one instant,’ returned the other. ‘It will be better for Mr. Nickleby and for our object that he should remain silent, if he can, till we have said what we have to say. I wish him to understand that.’\n\n‘Quite right, quite right,’ said brother Charles.\n\nRalph smiled, but made no reply. The bell was rung; the room-door opened; a man came in, with a halting walk; and, looking round, Ralph’s eyes met those of Newman Noggs. From that moment, his heart began to fail him.\n\n‘This is a good beginning,’ he said bitterly. ‘Oh! this is a good beginning. You are candid, honest, open-hearted, fair-dealing men! I always knew the real worth of such characters as yours! To tamper with a fellow like this, who would sell his soul (if he had one) for drink, and whose every word is a lie. What men are safe if this is done? Oh, it’s a good beginning!’\n\n‘I WILL speak,’ cried Newman, standing on tiptoe to look over Tim’s head, who had interposed to prevent him. ‘Hallo, you sir--old Nickleby!--what do you mean when you talk of “a fellow like this”? Who made me “a fellow like this”? If I would sell my soul for drink, why wasn’t I a thief, swindler, housebreaker, area sneak, robber of pence out of the trays of blind men’s dogs, rather than your drudge and packhorse? If my every word was a lie, why wasn’t I a pet and favourite of yours? Lie! When did I ever cringe and fawn to you. Tell me that! I served you faithfully. I did more work, because I was poor, and took more hard words from you because I despised you and them, than any man you could have got from the parish workhouse. I did. I served you because I was proud; because I was a lonely man with you, and there were no other drudges to see my degradation; and because nobody knew, better than you, that I was a ruined man: that I hadn’t always been what I am: and that I might have been better off, if I hadn’t been a fool and fallen into the hands of you and others who were knaves. Do you deny that?’\n\n‘Gently,’ reasoned Tim; ‘you said you wouldn’t.’\n\n‘I said I wouldn’t!’ cried Newman, thrusting him aside, and moving his hand as Tim moved, so as to keep him at arm’s length; ‘don’t tell me! Here, you Nickleby! Don’t pretend not to mind me; it won’t do; I know better. You were talking of tampering, just now. Who tampered with Yorkshire schoolmasters, and, while they sent the drudge out, that he shouldn’t overhear, forgot that such great caution might render him suspicious, and that he might watch his master out at nights, and might set other eyes to watch the schoolmaster? Who tampered with a selfish father, urging him to sell his daughter to old Arthur Gride, and tampered with Gride too, and did so in the little office, WITH A CLOSET IN THE ROOM?’\n\nRalph had put a great command upon himself; but he could not have suppressed a slight start, if he had been certain to be beheaded for it next moment.\n\n‘Aha!’ cried Newman, ‘you mind me now, do you? What first set this fag to be jealous of his master’s actions, and to feel that, if he hadn’t crossed him when he might, he would have been as bad as he, or worse? That master’s cruel treatment of his own flesh and blood, and vile designs upon a young girl who interested even his broken-down, drunken, miserable hack, and made him linger in his service, in the hope of doing her some good (as, thank God, he had done others once or twice before), when he would, otherwise, have relieved his feelings by pummelling his master soundly, and then going to the Devil. He would--mark that; and mark this--that I’m here now, because these gentlemen thought it best. When I sought them out (as I did; there was no tampering with me), I told them I wanted help to find you out, to trace you down, to go through with what I had begun, to help the right; and that when I had done it, I’d burst into your room and tell you all, face to face, man to man, and like a man. Now I’ve said my say, and let anybody else say theirs, and fire away!’\n\nWith this concluding sentiment, Newman Noggs, who had been perpetually sitting down and getting up again all through his speech, which he had delivered in a series of jerks; and who was, from the violent exercise and the excitement combined, in a state of most intense and fiery heat; became, without passing through any intermediate stage, stiff, upright, and motionless, and so remained, staring at Ralph Nickleby with all his might and main.\n\nRalph looked at him for an instant, and for an instant only; then, waved his hand, and beating the ground with his foot, said in a choking voice:\n\n‘Go on, gentlemen, go on! I’m patient, you see. There’s law to be had, there’s law. I shall call you to an account for this. Take care what you say; I shall make you prove it.’\n\n‘The proof is ready,’ returned brother Charles, ‘quite ready to our hands. The man Snawley, last night, made a confession.’\n\n‘Who may “the man Snawley” be,’ returned Ralph, ‘and what may his “confession” have to do with my affairs?’\n\nTo this inquiry, put with a dogged inflexibility of manner, the old gentleman returned no answer, but went on to say, that to show him how much they were in earnest, it would be necessary to tell him, not only what accusations were made against him, but what proof of them they had, and how that proof had been acquired. This laying open of the whole question brought up brother Ned, Tim Linkinwater, and Newman Noggs, all three at once; who, after a vast deal of talking together, and a scene of great confusion, laid before Ralph, in distinct terms, the following statement.\n\nThat, Newman, having been solemnly assured by one not then producible that Smike was not the son of Snawley, and this person having offered to make oath to that effect, if necessary, they had by this communication been first led to doubt the claim set up, which they would otherwise have seen no reason to dispute, supported as it was by evidence which they had no power of disproving. That, once suspecting the existence of a conspiracy, they had no difficulty in tracing back its origin to the malice of Ralph, and the vindictiveness and avarice of Squeers. That, suspicion and proof being two very different things, they had been advised by a lawyer, eminent for his sagacity and acuteness in such practice, to resist the proceedings taken on the other side for the recovery of the youth as slowly and artfully as possible, and meanwhile to beset Snawley (with whom it was clear the main falsehood must rest); to lead him, if possible, into contradictory and conflicting statements; to harass him by all available means; and so to practise on his fears, and regard for his own safety, as to induce him to divulge the whole scheme, and to give up his employer and whomsoever else he could implicate. That, all this had been skilfully done; but that Snawley, who was well practised in the arts of low cunning and intrigue, had successfully baffled all their attempts, until an unexpected circumstance had brought him, last night, upon his knees.\n\nIt thus arose. When Newman Noggs reported that Squeers was again in town, and that an interview of such secrecy had taken place between him and Ralph that he had been sent out of the house, plainly lest he should overhear a word, a watch was set upon the schoolmaster, in the hope that something might be discovered which would throw some light upon the suspected plot. It being found, however, that he held no further communication with Ralph, nor any with Snawley, and lived quite alone, they were completely at fault; the watch was withdrawn, and they would have observed his motions no longer, if it had not happened that, one night, Newman stumbled unobserved on him and Ralph in the street together. Following them, he discovered, to his surprise, that they repaired to various low lodging-houses, and taverns kept by broken gamblers, to more than one of whom Ralph was known, and that they were in pursuit--so he found by inquiries when they had left--of an old woman, whose description exactly tallied with that of deaf Mrs Sliderskew. Affairs now appearing to assume a more serious complexion, the watch was renewed with increased vigilance; an officer was procured, who took up his abode in the same tavern with Squeers: and by him and Frank Cheeryble the footsteps of the unconscious schoolmaster were dogged, until he was safely housed in the lodging at Lambeth. Mr. Squeers having shifted his lodging, the officer shifted his, and lying concealed in the same street, and, indeed, in the opposite house, soon found that Mr. Squeers and Mrs. Sliderskew were in constant communication.\n\nIn this state of things, Arthur Gride was appealed to. The robbery, partly owing to the inquisitiveness of the neighbours, and partly to his own grief and rage, had, long ago, become known; but he positively refused to give his sanction or yield any assistance to the old woman’s capture, and was seized with such a panic at the idea of being called upon to give evidence against her, that he shut himself up close in his house, and refused to hold communication with anybody. Upon this, the pursuers took counsel together, and, coming so near the truth as to arrive at the conclusion that Gride and Ralph, with Squeers for their instrument, were negotiating for the recovery of some of the stolen papers which would not bear the light, and might possibly explain the hints relative to Madeline which Newman had overheard, resolved that Mrs Sliderskew should be taken into custody before she had parted with them: and Squeers too, if anything suspicious could be attached to him. Accordingly, a search-warrant being procured, and all prepared, Mr Squeers’s window was watched, until his light was put out, and the time arrived when, as had been previously ascertained, he usually visited Mrs. Sliderskew. This done, Frank Cheeryble and Newman stole upstairs to listen to their discourse, and to give the signal to the officer at the most favourable time. At what an opportune moment they arrived, how they listened, and what they heard, is already known to the reader. Mr Squeers, still half stunned, was hurried off with a stolen deed in his possession, and Mrs. Sliderskew was apprehended likewise. The information being promptly carried to Snawley that Squeers was in custody--he was not told for what--that worthy, first extorting a promise that he should be kept harmless, declared the whole tale concerning Smike to be a fiction and forgery, and implicated Ralph Nickleby to the fullest extent. As to Mr. Squeers, he had, that morning, undergone a private examination before a magistrate; and, being unable to account satisfactorily for his possession of the deed or his companionship with Mrs. Sliderskew, had been, with her, remanded for a week.\n\nAll these discoveries were now related to Ralph, circumstantially, and in detail. Whatever impression they secretly produced, he suffered no sign of emotion to escape him, but sat perfectly still, not raising his frowning eyes from the ground, and covering his mouth with his hand. When the narrative was concluded; he raised his head hastily, as if about to speak, but on brother Charles resuming, fell into his old attitude again.\n\n‘I told you this morning,’ said the old gentleman, laying his hand upon his brother’s shoulder, ‘that I came to you in mercy. How far you may be implicated in this last transaction, or how far the person who is now in custody may criminate you, you best know. But, justice must take its course against the parties implicated in the plot against this poor, unoffending, injured lad. It is not in my power, or in the power of my brother Ned, to save you from the consequences. The utmost we can do is, to warn you in time, and to give you an opportunity of escaping them. We would not have an old man like you disgraced and punished by your near relation; nor would we have him forget, like you, all ties of blood and nature. We entreat you--brother Ned, you join me, I know, in this entreaty, and so, Tim Linkinwater, do you, although you pretend to be an obstinate dog, sir, and sit there frowning as if you didn’t--we entreat you to retire from London, to take shelter in some place where you will be safe from the consequences of these wicked designs, and where you may have time, sir, to atone for them, and to become a better man.’\n\n‘And do you think,’ returned Ralph, rising, ‘and do you think, you will so easily crush ME? Do you think that a hundred well-arranged plans, or a hundred suborned witnesses, or a hundred false curs at my heels, or a hundred canting speeches full of oily words, will move me? I thank you for disclosing your schemes, which I am now prepared for. You have not the man to deal with that you think; try me! and remember that I spit upon your fair words and false dealings, and dare you--provoke you--taunt you--to do to me the very worst you can!’\n\nThus they parted, for that time; but the worst had not come yet.\n\nThe Dangers thicken, and the Worst is told\nInstead of going home, Ralph threw himself into the first street cabriolet he could find, and, directing the driver towards the police-office of the district in which Mr. Squeers’s misfortunes had occurred, alighted at a short distance from it, and, discharging the man, went the rest of his way thither on foot. Inquiring for the object of his solicitude, he learnt that he had timed his visit well; for Mr Squeers was, in fact, at that moment waiting for a hackney coach he had ordered, and in which he purposed proceeding to his week’s retirement, like a gentleman.\n\nDemanding speech with the prisoner, he was ushered into a kind of waiting-room in which, by reason of his scholastic profession and superior respectability, Mr. Squeers had been permitted to pass the day. Here, by the light of a guttering and blackened candle, he could barely discern the schoolmaster, fast asleep on a bench in a remote corner. An empty glass stood on a table before him, which, with his somnolent condition and a very strong smell of brandy and water, forewarned the visitor that Mr. Squeers had been seeking, in creature comforts, a temporary forgetfulness of his unpleasant situation.\n\nIt was not a very easy matter to rouse him: so lethargic and heavy were his slumbers. Regaining his faculties by slow and faint glimmerings, he at length sat upright; and, displaying a very yellow face, a very red nose, and a very bristly beard: the joint effect of which was considerably heightened by a dirty white handkerchief, spotted with blood, drawn over the crown of his head and tied under his chin: stared ruefully at Ralph in silence, until his feelings found a vent in this pithy sentence:\n\n‘I say, young fellow, you’ve been and done it now; you have!’\n\n‘What’s the matter with your head?’ asked Ralph.\n\n‘Why, your man, your informing kidnapping man, has been and broke it,’ rejoined Squeers sulkily; ‘that’s what’s the matter with it. You’ve come at last, have you?’\n\n‘Why have you not sent to me?’ said Ralph. ‘How could I come till I knew what had befallen you?’\n\n‘My family!’ hiccuped Mr. Squeers, raising his eye to the ceiling: ‘my daughter, as is at that age when all the sensibilities is a-coming out strong in blow--my son as is the young Norval of private life, and the pride and ornament of a doting willage--here’s a shock for my family! The coat-of-arms of the Squeerses is tore, and their sun is gone down into the ocean wave!’\n\n‘You have been drinking,’ said Ralph, ‘and have not yet slept yourself sober.’\n\n‘I haven’t been drinking YOUR health, my codger,’ replied Mr. Squeers; ‘so you have nothing to do with that.’\n\nRalph suppressed the indignation which the schoolmaster’s altered and insolent manner awakened, and asked again why he had not sent to him.\n\n‘What should I get by sending to you?’ returned Squeers. ‘To be known to be in with you wouldn’t do me a deal of good, and they won’t take bail till they know something more of the case, so here am I hard and fast: and there are you, loose and comfortable.’\n\n‘And so must you be in a few days,’ retorted Ralph, with affected good-humour. ‘They can’t hurt you, man.’\n\n‘Why, I suppose they can’t do much to me, if I explain how it was that I got into the good company of that there ca-daverous old Slider,’ replied Squeers viciously, ‘who I wish was dead and buried, and resurrected and dissected, and hung upon wires in a anatomical museum, before ever I’d had anything to do with her. This is what him with the powdered head says this morning, in so many words: “Prisoner! As you have been found in company with this woman; as you were detected in possession of this document; as you were engaged with her in fraudulently destroying others, and can give no satisfactory account of yourself; I shall remand you for a week, in order that inquiries may be made, and evidence got. And meanwhile I can’t take any bail for your appearance.” Well then, what I say now is, that I CAN give a satisfactory account of myself; I can hand in the card of my establishment and say, “I am the Wackford Squeers as is therein named, sir. I am the man as is guaranteed, by unimpeachable references, to be a out-and-outer in morals and uprightness of principle. Whatever is wrong in this business is no fault of mine. I had no evil design in it, sir. I was not aware that anything was wrong. I was merely employed by a friend, my friend Mr. Ralph Nickleby, of Golden Square. Send for him, sir, and ask him what he has to say; he’s the man; not me!”’\n\n‘What document was it that you had?’ asked Ralph, evading, for the moment, the point just raised.\n\n‘What document? Why, THE document,’ replied Squeers. ‘The Madeline What’s-her-name one. It was a will; that’s what it was.’\n\n‘Of what nature, whose will, when dated, how benefiting her, to what extent?’ asked Ralph hurriedly.\n\n‘A will in her favour; that’s all I know,’ rejoined Squeers, ‘and that’s more than you’d have known, if you’d had them bellows on your head. It’s all owing to your precious caution that they got hold of it. If you had let me burn it, and taken my word that it was gone, it would have been a heap of ashes behind the fire, instead of being whole and sound, inside of my great-coat.’\n\n‘Beaten at every point!’ muttered Ralph.\n\n‘Ah!’ sighed Squeers, who, between the brandy and water and his broken head, wandered strangely, ‘at the delightful village of Dotheboys near Greta Bridge in Yorkshire, youth are boarded, clothed, booked, washed, furnished with pocket-money, provided with all necessaries, instructed in all languages living and dead, mathematics, orthography, geometry, astronomy, trigonometry--this is a altered state of trigonomics, this is! A double 1--all, everything--a cobbler’s weapon. U-p-up, adjective, not down. S-q-u-double e-r-s-Squeers, noun substantive, a educator of youth. Total, all up with Squeers!’\n\nHis running on, in this way, had afforded Ralph an opportunity of recovering his presence of mind, which at once suggested to him the necessity of removing, as far as possible, the schoolmaster’s misgivings, and leading him to believe that his safety and best policy lay in the preservation of a rigid silence.\n\n‘I tell you, once again,’ he said, ‘they can’t hurt you. You shall have an action for false imprisonment, and make a profit of this, yet. We will devise a story for you that should carry you through twenty times such a trivial scrape as this; and if they want security in a thousand pounds for your reappearance in case you should be called upon, you shall have it. All you have to do is, to keep back the truth. You’re a little fuddled tonight, and may not be able to see this as clearly as you would at another time; but this is what you must do, and you’ll need all your senses about you; for a slip might be awkward.’\n\n‘Oh!’ said Squeers, who had looked cunningly at him, with his head stuck on one side, like an old raven. ‘That’s what I’m to do, is it? Now then, just you hear a word or two from me. I an’t a-going to have any stories made for me, and I an’t a-going to stick to any. If I find matters going again me, I shall expect you to take your share, and I’ll take care you do. You never said anything about danger. I never bargained for being brought into such a plight as this, and I don’t mean to take it as quiet as you think. I let you lead me on, from one thing to another, because we had been mixed up together in a certain sort of a way, and if you had liked to be ill-natured you might perhaps have hurt the business, and if you liked to be good-natured you might throw a good deal in my way. Well; if all goes right now, that’s quite correct, and I don’t mind it; but if anything goes wrong, then times are altered, and I shall just say and do whatever I think may serve me most, and take advice from nobody. My moral influence with them lads,’ added Mr. Squeers, with deeper gravity, ‘is a tottering to its basis. The images of Mrs. Squeers, my daughter, and my son Wackford, all short of vittles, is perpetually before me; every other consideration melts away and vanishes, in front of these; the only number in all arithmetic that I know of, as a husband and a father, is number one, under this here most fatal go!’\n\nHow long Mr. Squeers might have declaimed, or how stormy a discussion his declamation might have led to, nobody knows. Being interrupted, at this point, by the arrival of the coach and an attendant who was to bear him company, he perched his hat with great dignity on the top of the handkerchief that bound his head; and, thrusting one hand in his pocket, and taking the attendant’s arm with the other, suffered himself to be led forth.\n\n‘As I supposed from his not sending!’ thought Ralph. ‘This fellow, I plainly see through all his tipsy fooling, has made up his mind to turn upon me. I am so beset and hemmed in, that they are not only all struck with fear, but, like the beasts in the fable, have their fling at me now, though time was, and no longer ago than yesterday too, when they were all civility and compliance. But they shall not move me. I’ll not give way. I will not budge one inch!’\n\nHe went home, and was glad to find his housekeeper complaining of illness, that he might have an excuse for being alone and sending her away to where she lived: which was hard by. Then, he sat down by the light of a single candle, and began to think, for the first time, on all that had taken place that day.\n\nHe had neither eaten nor drunk since last night, and, in addition to the anxiety of mind he had undergone, had been travelling about, from place to place almost incessantly, for many hours. He felt sick and exhausted, but could taste nothing save a glass of water, and continued to sit with his head upon his hand; not resting nor thinking, but laboriously trying to do both, and feeling that every sense but one of weariness and desolation, was for the time benumbed.\n\nIt was nearly ten o’clock when he heard a knocking at the door, and still sat quiet as before, as if he could not even bring his thoughts to bear upon that. It had been often repeated, and he had, several times, heard a voice outside, saying there was a light in the window (meaning, as he knew, his own candle), before he could rouse himself and go downstairs.\n\n‘Mr. Nickleby, there is terrible news for you, and I am sent to beg you will come with me directly,’ said a voice he seemed to recognise. He held his hand above his eyes, and, looking out, saw Tim Linkinwater on the steps.\n\n‘Come where?’ demanded Ralph.\n\n‘To our house, where you came this morning. I have a coach here.’\n\n‘Why should I go there?’ said Ralph.\n\n‘Don’t ask me why, but pray come with me.’\n\n‘Another edition of today!’ returned Ralph, making as though he would shut the door.\n\n‘No, no!’ cried Tim, catching him by the arm and speaking most earnestly; ‘it is only that you may hear something that has occurred: something very dreadful, Mr. Nickleby, which concerns you nearly. Do you think I would tell you so or come to you like this, if it were not the case?’\n\nRalph looked at him more closely. Seeing that he was indeed greatly excited, he faltered, and could not tell what to say or think.\n\n‘You had better hear this now, than at any other time,’ said Tim; ‘it may have some influence with you. For Heaven’s sake come!’\n\nPerhaps, at, another time, Ralph’s obstinacy and dislike would have been proof against any appeal from such a quarter, however emphatically urged; but now, after a moment’s hesitation, he went into the hall for his hat, and returning, got into the coach without speaking a word.\n\nTim well remembered afterwards, and often said, that as Ralph Nickleby went into the house for this purpose, he saw him, by the light of the candle which he had set down upon a chair, reel and stagger like a drunken man. He well remembered, too, that when he had placed his foot upon the coach-steps, he turned round and looked upon him with a face so ashy pale and so very wild and vacant that it made him shudder, and for the moment almost afraid to follow. People were fond of saying that he had some dark presentiment upon him then, but his emotion might, perhaps, with greater show of reason, be referred to what he had undergone that day.\n\nA profound silence was observed during the ride. Arrived at their place of destination, Ralph followed his conductor into the house, and into a room where the two brothers were. He was so astounded, not to say awed, by something of a mute compassion for himself which was visible in their manner and in that of the old clerk, that he could scarcely speak.\n\nHaving taken a seat, however, he contrived to say, though in broken words, ‘What--what have you to say to me--more than has been said already?’\n\nThe room was old and large, very imperfectly lighted, and terminated in a bay window, about which hung some heavy drapery. Casting his eyes in this direction as he spoke, he thought he made out the dusky figure of a man. He was confirmed in this impression by seeing that the object moved, as if uneasy under his scrutiny.\n\n‘Who’s that yonder?’ he said.\n\n‘One who has conveyed to us, within these two hours, the intelligence which caused our sending to you,’ replied brother Charles. ‘Let him be, sir, let him be for the present.’\n\n‘More riddles!’ said Ralph, faintly. ‘Well, sir?’\n\nIn turning his face towards the brothers he was obliged to avert it from the window; but, before either of them could speak, he had looked round again. It was evident that he was rendered restless and uncomfortable by the presence of the unseen person; for he repeated this action several times, and at length, as if in a nervous state which rendered him positively unable to turn away from the place, sat so as to have it opposite him, muttering as an excuse that he could not bear the light.\n\nThe brothers conferred apart for a short time: their manner showing that they were agitated. Ralph glanced at them twice or thrice, and ultimately said, with a great effort to recover his self-possession, ‘Now, what is this? If I am brought from home at this time of night, let it be for something. What have you got to tell me?’ After a short pause, he added, ‘Is my niece dead?’\n\nHe had struck upon a key which rendered the task of commencement an easier one. Brother Charles turned, and said that it was a death of which they had to tell him, but that his niece was well.\n\n‘You don’t mean to tell me,’ said Ralph, as his eyes brightened, ‘that her brother’s dead? No, that’s too good. I’d not believe it, if you told me so. It would be too welcome news to be true.’\n\n‘Shame on you, you hardened and unnatural man,’ cried the other brother, warmly. ‘Prepare yourself for intelligence which, if you have any human feeling in your breast, will make even you shrink and tremble. What if we tell you that a poor unfortunate boy: a child in everything but never having known one of those tender endearments, or one of those lightsome hours which make our childhood a time to be remembered like a happy dream through all our after life: a warm-hearted, harmless, affectionate creature, who never offended you, or did you wrong, but on whom you have vented the malice and hatred you have conceived for your nephew, and whom you have made an instrument for wreaking your bad passions upon him: what if we tell you that, sinking under your persecution, sir, and the misery and ill-usage of a life short in years but long in suffering, this poor creature has gone to tell his sad tale where, for your part in it, you must surely answer?’\n\n‘If you tell me,’ said Ralph; ‘if you tell me that he is dead, I forgive you all else. If you tell me that he is dead, I am in your debt and bound to you for life. He is! I see it in your faces. Who triumphs now? Is this your dreadful news; this your terrible intelligence? You see how it moves me. You did well to send. I would have travelled a hundred miles afoot, through mud, mire, and darkness, to hear this news just at this time.’\n\nEven then, moved as he was by this savage joy, Ralph could see in the faces of the two brothers, mingling with their look of disgust and horror, something of that indefinable compassion for himself which he had noticed before.\n\n‘And HE brought you the intelligence, did he?’ said Ralph, pointing with his finger towards the recess already mentioned; ‘and sat there, no doubt, to see me prostrated and overwhelmed by it! Ha, ha, ha! But I tell him that I’ll be a sharp thorn in his side for many a long day to come; and I tell you two, again, that you don’t know him yet; and that you’ll rue the day you took compassion on the vagabond.’\n\n‘You take me for your nephew,’ said a hollow voice; ‘it would be better for you, and for me too, if I were he indeed.’\n\nThe figure that he had seen so dimly, rose, and came slowly down. He started back, for he found that he confronted--not Nicholas, as he had supposed, but Brooker.\n\nRalph had no reason, that he knew, to fear this man; he had never feared him before; but the pallor which had been observed in his face when he issued forth that night, came upon him again. He was seen to tremble, and his voice changed as he said, keeping his eyes upon him,\n\n‘What does this fellow here? Do you know he is a convict, a felon, a common thief?’\n\n‘Hear what he has to tell you. Oh, Mr. Nickleby, hear what he has to tell you, be he what he may!’ cried the brothers, with such emphatic earnestness, that Ralph turned to them in wonder. They pointed to Brooker. Ralph again gazed at him: as it seemed mechanically.\n\n‘That boy,’ said the man, ‘that these gentlemen have been talking of--’\n\n‘That boy,’ repeated Ralph, looking vacantly at him.\n\n‘Whom I saw, stretched dead and cold upon his bed, and who is now in his grave--’\n\n‘Who is now in his grave,’ echoed Ralph, like one who talks in his sleep.\n\nThe man raised his eyes, and clasped his hands solemnly together:\n\n‘--Was your only son, so help me God in heaven!’\n\nIn the midst of a dead silence, Ralph sat down, pressing his two hands upon his temples. He removed them, after a minute, and never was there seen, part of a living man undisfigured by any wound, such a ghastly face as he then disclosed. He looked at Brooker, who was by this time standing at a short distance from him; but did not say one word, or make the slightest sound or gesture.\n\n‘Gentlemen,’ said the man, ‘I offer no excuses for myself. I am long past that. If, in telling you how this has happened, I tell you that I was harshly used, and perhaps driven out of my real nature, I do it only as a necessary part of my story, and not to shield myself. I am a guilty man.’\n\nHe stopped, as if to recollect, and looking away from Ralph, and addressing himself to the brothers, proceeded in a subdued and humble tone:\n\n‘Among those who once had dealings with this man, gentlemen--that’s from twenty to five-and-twenty years ago--there was one: a rough fox-hunting, hard-drinking gentleman, who had run through his own fortune, and wanted to squander away that of his sister: they were both orphans, and she lived with him and managed his house. I don’t know whether it was, originally, to back his influence and try to over-persuade the young woman or not, but he,’ pointing, to Ralph, ‘used to go down to the house in Leicestershire pretty often, and stop there many days at a time. They had had a great many dealings together, and he may have gone on some of those, or to patch up his client’s affairs, which were in a ruinous state; of course he went for profit. The gentlewoman was not a girl, but she was, I have heard say, handsome, and entitled to a pretty large property. In course of time, he married her. The same love of gain which led him to contract this marriage, led to its being kept strictly private; for a clause in her father’s will declared that if she married without her brother’s consent, the property, in which she had only some life interest while she remained single, should pass away altogether to another branch of the family. The brother would give no consent that the sister didn’t buy, and pay for handsomely; Mr. Nickleby would consent to no such sacrifice; and so they went on, keeping their marriage secret, and waiting for him to break his neck or die of a fever. He did neither, and meanwhile the result of this private marriage was a son. The child was put out to nurse, a long way off; his mother never saw him but once or twice, and then by stealth; and his father--so eagerly did he thirst after the money which seemed to come almost within his grasp now, for his brother-in-law was very ill, and breaking more and more every day--never went near him, to avoid raising any suspicion. The brother lingered on; Mr. Nickleby’s wife constantly urged him to avow their marriage; he peremptorily refused. She remained alone in a dull country house: seeing little or no company but riotous, drunken sportsmen. He lived in London and clung to his business. Angry quarrels and recriminations took place, and when they had been married nearly seven years, and were within a few weeks of the time when the brother’s death would have adjusted all, she eloped with a younger man, and left him.’\n\nHere he paused, but Ralph did not stir, and the brothers signed to him to proceed.\n\n‘It was then that I became acquainted with these circumstances from his own lips. They were no secrets then; for the brother, and others, knew them; but they were communicated to me, not on this account, but because I was wanted. He followed the fugitives. Some said to make money of his wife’s shame, but, I believe, to take some violent revenge, for that was as much his character as the other; perhaps more. He didn’t find them, and she died not long after. I don’t know whether he began to think he might like the child, or whether he wished to make sure that it should never fall into its mother’s hands; but, before he went, he intrusted me with the charge of bringing it home. And I did so.’\n\nHe went on, from this point, in a still more humble tone, and spoke in a very low voice; pointing to Ralph as he resumed.\n\n‘He had used me ill--cruelly--I reminded him in what, not long ago when I met him in the street--and I hated him. I brought the child home to his own house, and lodged him in the front garret. Neglect had made him very sickly, and I was obliged to call in a doctor, who said he must be removed for change of air, or he would die. I think that first put it in my head. I did it then. He was gone six weeks, and when he came back, I told him--with every circumstance well planned and proved; nobody could have suspected me--that the child was dead and buried. He might have been disappointed in some intention he had formed, or he might have had some natural affection, but he WAS grieved at THAT, and I was confirmed in my design of opening up the secret one day, and making it a means of getting money from him. I had heard, like most other men, of Yorkshire schools. I took the child to one kept by a man named Squeers, and left it there. I gave him the name of Smike. Year by year, I paid twenty pounds a-year for him for six years; never breathing the secret all the time; for I had left his father’s service after more hard usage, and quarrelled with him again. I was sent away from this country. I have been away nearly eight years. Directly I came home again, I travelled down into Yorkshire, and, skulking in the village of an evening-time, made inquiries about the boys at the school, and found that this one, whom I had placed there, had run away with a young man bearing the name of his own father. I sought his father out in London, and hinting at what I could tell him, tried for a little money to support life; but he repulsed me with threats. I then found out his clerk, and, going on from little to little, and showing him that there were good reasons for communicating with me, learnt what was going on; and it was I who told him that the boy was no son of the man who claimed to be his father. All this time I had never seen the boy. At length, I heard from this same source that he was very ill, and where he was. I travelled down there, that I might recall myself, if possible, to his recollection and confirm my story. I came upon him unexpectedly; but before I could speak he knew me--he had good cause to remember me, poor lad!--and I would have sworn to him if I had met him in the Indies. I knew the piteous face I had seen in the little child. After a few days’ indecision, I applied to the young gentleman in whose care he was, and I found that he was dead. He knows how quickly he recognised me again, how often he had described me and my leaving him at the school, and how he told him of a garret he recollected: which is the one I have spoken of, and in his father’s house to this day. This is my story. I demand to be brought face to face with the schoolmaster, and put to any possible proof of any part of it, and I will show that it’s too true, and that I have this guilt upon my soul.’\n\n‘Unhappy man!’ said the brothers. ‘What reparation can you make for this?’\n\n‘None, gentlemen, none! I have none to make, and nothing to hope now. I am old in years, and older still in misery and care. This confession can bring nothing upon me but new suffering and punishment; but I make it, and will abide by it whatever comes. I have been made the instrument of working out this dreadful retribution upon the head of a man who, in the hot pursuit of his bad ends, has persecuted and hunted down his own child to death. It must descend upon me too. I know it must fall. My reparation comes too late; and, neither in this world nor in the next, can I have hope again!’\n\nHe had hardly spoken, when the lamp, which stood upon the table close to where Ralph was seated, and which was the only one in the room, was thrown to the ground, and left them in darkness. There was some trifling confusion in obtaining another light; the interval was a mere nothing; but when the light appeared, Ralph Nickleby was gone.\n\nThe good brothers and Tim Linkinwater occupied some time in discussing the probability of his return; and, when it became apparent that he would not come back, they hesitated whether or no to send after him. At length, remembering how strangely and silently he had sat in one immovable position during the interview, and thinking he might possibly be ill, they determined, although it was now very late, to send to his house on some pretence. Finding an excuse in the presence of Brooker, whom they knew not how to dispose of without consulting his wishes, they concluded to act upon this resolution before going to bed.\n\n# CHAPTER 61\n\nWherein Nicholas and his Sister forfeit the good Opinion of all worldly and prudent People\nOn the next morning after Brooker’s disclosure had been made, Nicholas returned home. The meeting between him and those whom he had left there was not without strong emotion on both sides; for they had been informed by his letters of what had occurred: and, besides that his griefs were theirs, they mourned with him the death of one whose forlorn and helpless state had first established a claim upon their compassion, and whose truth of heart and grateful earnest nature had, every day, endeared him to them more and more.\n\n‘I am sure,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, wiping her eyes, and sobbing bitterly, ‘I have lost the best, the most zealous, and most attentive creature that has ever been a companion to me in my life--putting you, my dear Nicholas, and Kate, and your poor papa, and that well-behaved nurse who ran away with the linen and the twelve small forks, out of the question, of course. Of all the tractable, equal-tempered, attached, and faithful beings that ever lived, I believe he was the most so. To look round upon the garden, now, that he took so much pride in, or to go into his room and see it filled with so many of those little contrivances for our comfort that he was so fond of making, and made so well, and so little thought he would leave unfinished--I can’t bear it, I cannot really. Ah! This is a great trial to me, a great trial. It will be comfort to you, my dear Nicholas, to the end of your life, to recollect how kind and good you always were to him--so it will be to me, to think what excellent terms we were always upon, and how fond he always was of me, poor fellow! It was very natural you should have been attached to him, my dear--very--and of course you were, and are very much cut up by this. I am sure it’s only necessary to look at you and see how changed you are, to see that; but nobody knows what my feelings are--nobody can--it’s quite impossible!’\n\nWhile Mrs. Nickleby, with the utmost sincerity, gave vent to her sorrows after her own peculiar fashion of considering herself foremost, she was not the only one who indulged such feelings. Kate, although well accustomed to forget herself when others were to be considered, could not repress her grief; Madeline was scarcely less moved than she; and poor, hearty, honest little Miss La Creevy, who had come upon one of her visits while Nicholas was away, and had done nothing, since the sad news arrived, but console and cheer them all, no sooner beheld him coming in at the door, than she sat herself down upon the stairs, and bursting into a flood of tears, refused for a long time to be comforted.\n\n‘It hurts me so,’ cried the poor body, ‘to see him come back alone. I can’t help thinking what he must have suffered himself. I wouldn’t mind so much if he gave way a little more; but he bears it so manfully.’\n\n‘Why, so I should,’ said Nicholas, ‘should I not?’\n\n‘Yes, yes,’ replied the little woman, ‘and bless you for a good creature! but this does seem at first to a simple soul like me--I know it’s wrong to say so, and I shall be sorry for it presently--this does seem such a poor reward for all you have done.’\n\n‘Nay,’ said Nicholas gently, ‘what better reward could I have, than the knowledge that his last days were peaceful and happy, and the recollection that I was his constant companion, and was not prevented, as I might have been by a hundred circumstances, from being beside him?’\n\n‘To be sure,’ sobbed Miss La Creevy; ‘it’s very true, and I’m an ungrateful, impious, wicked little fool, I know.’\n\nWith that, the good soul fell to crying afresh, and, endeavouring to recover herself, tried to laugh. The laugh and the cry, meeting each other thus abruptly, had a struggle for the mastery; the result was, that it was a drawn battle, and Miss La Creevy went into hysterics.\n\nWaiting until they were all tolerably quiet and composed again, Nicholas, who stood in need of some rest after his long journey, retired to his own room, and throwing himself, dressed as he was, upon the bed, fell into a sound sleep. When he awoke, he found Kate sitting by his bedside, who, seeing that he had opened his eyes, stooped down to kiss him.\n\n‘I came to tell you how glad I am to see you home again.’\n\n‘But I can’t tell you how glad I am to see you, Kate.’\n\n‘We have been wearying so for your return,’ said Kate, ‘mama and I, and--and Madeline.’\n\n‘You said in your last letter that she was quite well,’ said Nicholas, rather hastily, and colouring as he spoke. ‘Has nothing been said, since I have been away, about any future arrangements that the brothers have in contemplation for her?’\n\n‘Oh, not a word,’ replied Kate. ‘I can’t think of parting from her without sorrow; and surely, Nicholas, YOU don’t wish it!’\n\nNicholas coloured again, and, sitting down beside his sister on a little couch near the window, said:\n\n‘No, Kate, no, I do not. I might strive to disguise my real feelings from anybody but you; but I will tell you that--briefly and plainly, Kate--that I love her.’\n\nKate’s eyes brightened, and she was going to make some reply, when Nicholas laid his hand upon her arm, and went on:\n\n‘Nobody must know this but you. She, last of all.’\n‘Dear Nicholas!’\n\n‘Last of all; never, though never is a long day. Sometimes, I try to think that the time may come when I may honestly tell her this; but it is so far off; in such distant perspective, so many years must elapse before it comes, and when it does come (if ever) I shall be so unlike what I am now, and shall have so outlived my days of youth and romance--though not, I am sure, of love for her--that even I feel how visionary all such hopes must be, and try to crush them rudely myself, and have the pain over, rather than suffer time to wither them, and keep the disappointment in store. No, Kate! Since I have been absent, I have had, in that poor fellow who is gone, perpetually before my eyes, another instance of the munificent liberality of these noble brothers. As far as in me lies, I will deserve it, and if I have wavered in my bounden duty to them before, I am now determined to discharge it rigidly, and to put further delays and temptations beyond my reach.’\n\n‘Before you say another word, dear Nicholas,’ said Kate, turning pale, ‘you must hear what I have to tell you. I came on purpose, but I had not the courage. What you say now, gives me new heart.’ She faltered, and burst into tears.\n\nThere was that in her manner which prepared Nicholas for what was coming. Kate tried to speak, but her tears prevented her.\n\n‘Come, you foolish girl,’ said Nicholas; ‘why, Kate, Kate, be a woman! I think I know what you would tell me. It concerns Mr. Frank, does it not?’\n\nKate sunk her head upon his shoulder, and sobbed out ‘Yes.’\n\n‘And he has offered you his hand, perhaps, since I have been away,’ said Nicholas; ‘is that it? Yes. Well, well; it is not so difficult, you see, to tell me, after all. He offered you his hand?’\n\n‘Which I refused,’ said Kate.\n\n‘Yes; and why?’\n\n‘I told him,’ she said, in a trembling voice, ‘all that I have since found you told mama; and while I could not conceal from him, and cannot from you, that--that it was a pang and a great trial, I did so firmly, and begged him not to see me any more.’\n\n‘That’s my own brave Kate!’ said Nicholas, pressing her to his breast. ‘I knew you would.’\n\n‘He tried to alter my resolution,’ said Kate, ‘and declared that, be my decision what it might, he would not only inform his uncles of the step he had taken, but would communicate it to you also, directly you returned. I am afraid,’ she added, her momentary composure forsaking her, ‘I am afraid I may not have said, strongly enough, how deeply I felt such disinterested love, and how earnestly I prayed for his future happiness. If you do talk together, I should--I should like him to know that.’\n\n‘And did you suppose, Kate, when you had made this sacrifice to what you knew was right and honourable, that I should shrink from mine?’ said Nicholas tenderly.\n\n‘Oh no! not if your position had been the same, but--’\n\n‘But it is the same,’ interrupted Nicholas. ‘Madeline is not the near relation of our benefactors, but she is closely bound to them by ties as dear; and I was first intrusted with her history, specially because they reposed unbounded confidence in me, and believed that I was as true as steel. How base would it be of me to take advantage of the circumstances which placed her here, or of the slight service I was happily able to render her, and to seek to engage her affections when the result must be, if I succeeded, that the brothers would be disappointed in their darling wish of establishing her as their own child, and that I must seem to hope to build my fortunes on their compassion for the young creature whom I had so meanly and unworthily entrapped: turning her very gratitude and warmth of heart to my own purpose and account, and trading in her misfortunes! I, too, whose duty, and pride, and pleasure, Kate, it is to have other claims upon me which I will never forget; and who have the means of a comfortable and happy life already, and have no right to look beyond it! I have determined to remove this weight from my mind. I doubt whether I have not done wrong, even now; and today I will, without reserve or equivocation, disclose my real reasons to Mr Cherryble, and implore him to take immediate measures for removing this young lady to the shelter of some other roof.’\n\n‘Today? so very soon?’\n\n‘I have thought of this for weeks, and why should I postpone it? If the scene through which I have just passed has taught me to reflect, and has awakened me to a more anxious and careful sense of duty, why should I wait until the impression has cooled? You would not dissuade me, Kate; now would you?’\n\n‘You may grow rich, you know,’ said Kate.\n\n‘I may grow rich!’ repeated Nicholas, with a mournful smile, ‘ay, and I may grow old! But rich or poor, or old or young, we shall ever be the same to each other, and in that our comfort lies. What if we have but one home? It can never be a solitary one to you and me. What if we were to remain so true to these first impressions as to form no others? It is but one more link to the strong chain that binds us together. It seems but yesterday that we were playfellows, Kate, and it will seem but tomorrow when we are staid old people, looking back to these cares as we look back, now, to those of our childish days: and recollecting with a melancholy pleasure that the time was, when they could move us. Perhaps then, when we are quaint old folks and talk of the times when our step was lighter and our hair not grey, we may be even thankful for the trials that so endeared us to each other, and turned our lives into that current, down which we shall have glided so peacefully and calmly. And having caught some inkling of our story, the young people about us--as young as you and I are now, Kate--may come to us for sympathy, and pour distresses which hope and inexperience could scarcely feel enough for, into the compassionate ears of the old bachelor brother and his maiden sister.’\n\nKate smiled through her tears as Nicholas drew this picture; but they were not tears of sorrow, although they continued to fall when he had ceased to speak.\n\n‘Am I not right, Kate?’ he said, after a short silence.\n\n‘Quite, quite, dear brother; and I cannot tell you how happy I am that I have acted as you would have had me.’\n\n‘You don’t regret?’\n\n‘N--n--no,’ said Kate timidly, tracing some pattern upon the ground with her little foot. ‘I don’t regret having done what was honourable and right, of course; but I do regret that this should have ever happened--at least sometimes I regret it, and sometimes I--I don’t know what I say; I am but a weak girl, Nicholas, and it has agitated me very much.’\n\nIt is no vaunt to affirm that if Nicholas had had ten thousand pounds at the minute, he would, in his generous affection for the owner of the blushing cheek and downcast eye, have bestowed its utmost farthing, in perfect forgetfulness of himself, to secure her happiness. But all he could do was to comfort and console her by kind words; and words they were of such love and kindness, and cheerful encouragement, that poor Kate threw her arms about his neck, and declared she would weep no more.\n\n‘What man,’ thought Nicholas proudly, while on his way, soon afterwards, to the brothers’ house, ‘would not be sufficiently rewarded for any sacrifice of fortune by the possession of such a heart as Kate’s, which, but that hearts weigh light, and gold and silver heavy, is beyond all praise? Frank has money, and wants no more. Where would it buy him such a treasure as Kate? And yet, in unequal marriages, the rich party is always supposed to make a great sacrifice, and the other to get a good bargain! But I am thinking like a lover, or like an ass: which I suppose is pretty nearly the same.’\n\nChecking thoughts so little adapted to the business on which he was bound, by such self-reproofs as this and many others no less sturdy, he proceeded on his way and presented himself before Tim Linkinwater.\n\n‘Ah! Mr. Nickleby!’ cried Tim, ‘God bless you! how d’ye do? Well? Say you’re quite well and never better. Do now.’\n\n‘Quite,’ said Nicholas, shaking him by both hands.\n\n‘Ah!’ said Tim, ‘you look tired though, now I come to look at you. Hark! there he is, d’ye hear him? That was Dick, the blackbird. He hasn’t been himself since you’ve been gone. He’d never get on without you, now; he takes as naturally to you as he does to me.’\n\n‘Dick is a far less sagacious fellow than I supposed him, if he thinks I am half so well worthy of his notice as you,’ replied Nicholas.\n\n‘Why, I’ll tell you what, sir,’ said Tim, standing in his favourite attitude and pointing to the cage with the feather of his pen, ‘it’s a very extraordinary thing about that bird, that the only people he ever takes the smallest notice of, are Mr. Charles, and Mr. Ned, and you, and me.’\n\nHere, Tim stopped and glanced anxiously at Nicholas; then unexpectedly catching his eye repeated, ‘And you and me, sir, and you and me.’ And then he glanced at Nicholas again, and, squeezing his hand, said, ‘I am a bad one at putting off anything I am interested in. I didn’t mean to ask you, but I should like to hear a few particulars about that poor boy. Did he mention Cheeryble Brothers at all?’\n\n‘Yes,’ said Nicholas, ‘many and many a time.’\n\n‘That was right of him,’ returned Tim, wiping his eyes; ‘that was very right of him.’\n\n‘And he mentioned your name a score of times,’ said Nicholas, ‘and often bade me carry back his love to Mr. Linkinwater.’\n\n‘No, no, did he though?’ rejoined Tim, sobbing outright. ‘Poor fellow! I wish we could have had him buried in town. There isn’t such a burying-ground in all London as that little one on the other side of the square--there are counting-houses all round it, and if you go in there, on a fine day, you can see the books and safes through the open windows. And he sent his love to me, did he? I didn’t expect he would have thought of me. Poor fellow, poor fellow! His love too!’\n\nTim was so completely overcome by this little mark of recollection, that he was quite unequal to any more conversation at the moment. Nicholas therefore slipped quietly out, and went to brother Charles’s room.\n\nIf he had previously sustained his firmness and fortitude, it had been by an effort which had cost him no little pain; but the warm welcome, the hearty manner, the homely unaffected commiseration, of the good old man, went to his heart, and no inward struggle could prevent his showing it.\n\n‘Come, come, my dear sir,’ said the benevolent merchant; ‘we must not be cast down; no, no. We must learn to bear misfortune, and we must remember that there are many sources of consolation even in death. Every day that this poor lad had lived, he must have been less and less qualified for the world, and more and more unhappy in is own deficiencies. It is better as it is, my dear sir. Yes, yes, yes, it’s better as it is.’\n\n‘I have thought of all that, sir,’ replied Nicholas, clearing his throat. ‘I feel it, I assure you.’\n\n‘Yes, that’s well,’ replied Mr. Cheeryble, who, in the midst of all his comforting, was quite as much taken aback as honest old Tim; ‘that’s well. Where is my brother Ned? Tim Linkinwater, sir, where is my brother Ned?’\n\n‘Gone out with Mr. Trimmers, about getting that unfortunate man into the hospital, and sending a nurse to his children,’ said Tim.\n\n‘My brother Ned is a fine fellow, a great fellow!’ exclaimed brother Charles as he shut the door and returned to Nicholas. ‘He will be overjoyed to see you, my dear sir. We have been speaking of you every day.’\n\n‘To tell you the truth, sir, I am glad to find you alone,’ said Nicholas, with some natural hesitation; ‘for I am anxious to say something to you. Can you spare me a very few minutes?’\n\n‘Surely, surely,’ returned brother Charles, looking at him with an anxious countenance. ‘Say on, my dear sir, say on.’\n\n‘I scarcely know how, or where, to begin,’ said Nicholas. ‘If ever one mortal had reason to be penetrated with love and reverence for another: with such attachment as would make the hardest service in his behalf a pleasure and delight: with such grateful recollections as must rouse the utmost zeal and fidelity of his nature: those are the feelings which I should entertain for you, and do, from my heart and soul, believe me!’\n\n‘I do believe you,’ replied the old gentleman, ‘and I am happy in the belief. I have never doubted it; I never shall. I am sure I never shall.’\n\n‘Your telling me that so kindly,’ said Nicholas, ‘emboldens me to proceed. When you first took me into your confidence, and dispatched me on those missions to Miss Bray, I should have told you that I had seen her long before; that her beauty had made an impression upon me which I could not efface; and that I had fruitlessly endeavoured to trace her, and become acquainted with her history. I did not tell you so, because I vainly thought I could conquer my weaker feelings, and render every consideration subservient to my duty to you.’\n\n‘Mr. Nickleby,’ said brother Charles, ‘you did not violate the confidence I placed in you, or take an unworthy advantage of it. I am sure you did not.’\n\n‘I did not,’ said Nicholas, firmly. ‘Although I found that the necessity for self-command and restraint became every day more imperious, and the difficulty greater, I never, for one instant, spoke or looked but as I would have done had you been by. I never, for one moment, deserted my trust, nor have I to this instant. But I find that constant association and companionship with this sweet girl is fatal to my peace of mind, and may prove destructive to the resolutions I made in the beginning, and up to this time have faithfully kept. In short, sir, I cannot trust myself, and I implore and beseech you to remove this young lady from under the charge of my mother and sister without delay. I know that to anyone but myself--to you, who consider the immeasurable distance between me and this young lady, who is now your ward, and the object of your peculiar care--my loving her, even in thought, must appear the height of rashness and presumption. I know it is so. But who can see her as I have seen, who can know what her life has been, and not love her? I have no excuse but that; and as I cannot fly from this temptation, and cannot repress this passion, with its object constantly before me, what can I do but pray and beseech you to remove it, and to leave me to forget her?’\n\n‘Mr. Nickleby,’ said the old man, after a short silence, ‘you can do no more. I was wrong to expose a young man like you to this trial. I might have foreseen what would happen. Thank you, sir, thank you. Madeline shall be removed.’\n\n‘If you would grant me one favour, dear sir, and suffer her to remember me with esteem, by never revealing to her this confession--’\n\n‘I will take care,’ said Mr. Cheeryble. ‘And now, is this all you have to tell me?’\n\n‘No!’ returned Nicholas, meeting his eye, ‘it is not.’\n\n‘I know the rest,’ said Mr. Cheeryble, apparently very much relieved by this prompt reply. ‘When did it come to your knowledge?’\n\n‘When I reached home this morning.’\n\n‘You felt it your duty immediately to come to me, and tell me what your sister no doubt acquainted you with?’\n\n‘I did,’ said Nicholas, ‘though I could have wished to have spoken to Mr Frank first.’\n\n‘Frank was with me last night,’ replied the old gentleman. ‘You have done well, Mr. Nickleby--very well, sir--and I thank you again.’\n\nUpon this head, Nicholas requested permission to add a few words. He ventured to hope that nothing he had said would lead to the estrangement of Kate and Madeline, who had formed an attachment for each other, any interruption of which would, he knew, be attended with great pain to them, and, most of all, with remorse and pain to him, as its unhappy cause. When these things were all forgotten, he hoped that Frank and he might still be warm friends, and that no word or thought of his humble home, or of her who was well contented to remain there and share his quiet fortunes, would ever again disturb the harmony between them. He recounted, as nearly as he could, what had passed between himself and Kate that morning: speaking of her with such warmth of pride and affection, and dwelling so cheerfully upon the confidence they had of overcoming any selfish regrets and living contented and happy in each other’s love, that few could have heard him unmoved. More moved himself than he had been yet, he expressed in a few hurried words--as expressive, perhaps, as the most eloquent phrases--his devotion to the brothers, and his hope that he might live and die in their service.\n\nTo all this, brother Charles listened in profound silence, and with his chair so turned from Nicholas that his face could not be seen. He had not spoken either, in his accustomed manner, but with a certain stiffness and embarrassment very foreign to it. Nicholas feared he had offended him. He said, ‘No, no, he had done quite right,’ but that was all.\n\n‘Frank is a heedless, foolish fellow,’ he said, after Nicholas had paused for some time; ‘a very heedless, foolish fellow. I will take care that this is brought to a close without delay. Let us say no more upon the subject; it’s a very painful one to me. Come to me in half an hour; I have strange things to tell you, my dear sir, and your uncle has appointed this afternoon for your waiting upon him with me.’\n\n‘Waiting upon him! With you, sir!’ cried Nicholas.\n\n‘Ay, with me,’ replied the old gentleman. ‘Return to me in half an hour, and I’ll tell you more.’\n\nNicholas waited upon him at the time mentioned, and then learnt all that had taken place on the previous day, and all that was known of the appointment Ralph had made with the brothers; which was for that night; and for the better understanding of which it will be requisite to return and follow his own footsteps from the house of the twin brothers. Therefore, we leave Nicholas somewhat reassured by the restored kindness of their manner towards him, and yet sensible that it was different from what it had been (though he scarcely knew in what respect): so he was full of uneasiness, uncertainty, and disquiet.\n\nRalph makes one last Appointment--and keeps it\n\nCreeping from the house, and slinking off like a thief; groping with his hands, when first he got into the street, as if he were a blind man; and looking often over his shoulder while he hurried away, as though he were followed in imagination or reality by someone anxious to question or detain him; Ralph Nickleby left the city behind him, and took the road to his own home.\n\nThe night was dark, and a cold wind blew, driving the clouds, furiously and fast, before it. There was one black, gloomy mass that seemed to follow him: not hurrying in the wild chase with the others, but lingering sullenly behind, and gliding darkly and stealthily on. He often looked back at this, and, more than once, stopped to let it pass over; but, somehow, when he went forward again, it was still behind him, coming mournfully and slowly up, like a shadowy funeral train.\n\nHe had to pass a poor, mean burial-ground--a dismal place, raised a few feet above the level of the street, and parted from it by a low parapet-wall and an iron railing; a rank, unwholesome, rotten spot, where the very grass and weeds seemed, in their frouzy growth, to tell that they had sprung from paupers’ bodies, and had struck their roots in the graves of men, sodden, while alive, in steaming courts and drunken hungry dens. And here, in truth, they lay, parted from the living by a little earth and a board or two--lay thick and close--corrupting in body as they had in mind--a dense and squalid crowd. Here they lay, cheek by jowl with life: no deeper down than the feet of the throng that passed there every day, and piled high as their throats. Here they lay, a grisly family, all these dear departed brothers and sisters of the ruddy clergyman who did his task so speedily when they were hidden in the ground!\n\nAs he passed here, Ralph called to mind that he had been one of a jury, long before, on the body of a man who had cut his throat; and that he was buried in this place. He could not tell how he came to recollect it now, when he had so often passed and never thought about him, or how it was that he felt an interest in the circumstance; but he did both; and stopping, and clasping the iron railings with his hands, looked eagerly in, wondering which might be his grave.\n\nWhile he was thus engaged, there came towards him, with noise of shouts and singing, some fellows full of drink, followed by others, who were remonstrating with them and urging them to go home in quiet. They were in high good-humour; and one of them, a little, weazen, hump-backed man, began to dance. He was a grotesque, fantastic figure, and the few bystanders laughed. Ralph himself was moved to mirth, and echoed the laugh of one who stood near and who looked round in his face. When they had passed on, and he was left alone again, he resumed his speculation with a new kind of interest; for he recollected that the last person who had seen the suicide alive, had left him very merry, and he remembered how strange he and the other jurors had thought that at the time.\n\nHe could not fix upon the spot among such a heap of graves, but he conjured up a strong and vivid idea of the man himself, and how he looked, and what had led him to do it; all of which he recalled with ease. By dint of dwelling upon this theme, he carried the impression with him when he went away; as he remembered, when a child, to have had frequently before him the figure of some goblin he had once seen chalked upon a door. But as he drew nearer and nearer home he forgot it again, and began to think how very dull and solitary the house would be inside.\n\nThis feeling became so strong at last, that when he reached his own door, he could hardly make up his mind to turn the key and open it. When he had done that, and gone into the passage, he felt as though to shut it again would be to shut out the world. But he let it go, and it closed with a loud noise. There was no light. How very dreary, cold, and still it was!\n\nShivering from head to foot, he made his way upstairs into the room where he had been last disturbed. He had made a kind of compact with himself that he would not think of what had happened until he got home. He was at home now, and suffered himself to consider it.\n\nHis own child, his own child! He never doubted the tale; he felt it was true; knew it as well, now, as if he had been privy to it all along. His own child! And dead too. Dying beside Nicholas, loving him, and looking upon him as something like an angel. That was the worst!\n\nThey had all turned from him and deserted him in his very first need. Even money could not buy them now; everything must come out, and everybody must know all. Here was the young lord dead, his companion abroad and beyond his reach, ten thousand pounds gone at one blow, his plot with Gride overset at the very moment of triumph, his after-schemes discovered, himself in danger, the object of his persecution and Nicholas’s love, his own wretched boy; everything crumbled and fallen upon him, and he beaten down beneath the ruins and grovelling in the dust.\n\nIf he had known his child to be alive; if no deceit had been ever practised, and he had grown up beneath his eye; he might have been a careless, indifferent, rough, harsh father--like enough--he felt that; but the thought would come that he might have been otherwise, and that his son might have been a comfort to him, and they two happy together. He began to think now, that his supposed death and his wife’s flight had had some share in making him the morose, hard man he was. He seemed to remember a time when he was not quite so rough and obdurate; and almost thought that he had first hated Nicholas because he was young and gallant, and perhaps like the stripling who had brought dishonour and loss of fortune on his head.\n\nBut one tender thought, or one of natural regret, in his whirlwind of passion and remorse, was as a drop of calm water in a stormy maddened sea. His hatred of Nicholas had been fed upon his own defeat, nourished on his interference with his schemes, fattened upon his old defiance and success. There were reasons for its increase; it had grown and strengthened gradually. Now it attained a height which was sheer wild lunacy. That his, of all others, should have been the hands to rescue his miserable child; that he should have been his protector and faithful friend; that he should have shown him that love and tenderness which, from the wretched moment of his birth, he had never known; that he should have taught him to hate his own parent and execrate his very name; that he should now know and feel all this, and triumph in the recollection; was gall and madness to the usurer’s heart. The dead boy’s love for Nicholas, and the attachment of Nicholas to him, was insupportable agony. The picture of his deathbed, with Nicholas at his side, tending and supporting him, and he breathing out his thanks, and expiring in his arms, when he would have had them mortal enemies and hating each other to the last, drove him frantic. He gnashed his teeth and smote the air, and looking wildly round, with eyes which gleamed through the darkness, cried aloud:\n\n‘I am trampled down and ruined. The wretch told me true. The night has come! Is there no way to rob them of further triumph, and spurn their mercy and compassion? Is there no devil to help me?’\n\nSwiftly, there glided again into his brain the figure he had raised that night. It seemed to lie before him. The head was covered now. So it was when he first saw it. The rigid, upturned, marble feet too, he remembered well. Then came before him the pale and trembling relatives who had told their tale upon the inquest--the shrieks of women--the silent dread of men--the consternation and disquiet--the victory achieved by that heap of clay, which, with one motion of its hand, had let out the life and made this stir among them--\n\nHe spoke no more; but, after a pause, softly groped his way out of the room, and up the echoing stairs--up to the top--to the front garret--where he closed the door behind him, and remained.\n\nIt was a mere lumber-room now, but it yet contained an old dismantled bedstead; the one on which his son had slept; for no other had ever been there. He avoided it hastily, and sat down as far from it as he could.\n\nThe weakened glare of the lights in the street below, shining through the window which had no blind or curtain to intercept it, was enough to show the character of the room, though not sufficient fully to reveal the various articles of lumber, old corded trunks and broken furniture, which were scattered about. It had a shelving roof; high in one part, and at another descending almost to the floor. It was towards the highest part that Ralph directed his eyes; and upon it he kept them fixed steadily for some minutes, when he rose, and dragging thither an old chest upon which he had been seated, mounted on it, and felt along the wall above his head with both hands. At length, they touched a large iron hook, firmly driven into one of the beams.\n\nAt that moment, he was interrupted by a loud knocking at the door below. After a little hesitation he opened the window, and demanded who it was.\n\n‘I want Mr. Nickleby,’ replied a voice.\n\n‘What with him?’\n\n‘That’s not Mr. Nickleby’s voice, surely?’ was the rejoinder.\n\nIt was not like it; but it was Ralph who spoke, and so he said.\n\nThe voice made answer that the twin brothers wished to know whether the man whom he had seen that night was to be detained; and that although it was now midnight they had sent, in their anxiety to do right.\n\n‘Yes,’ cried Ralph, ‘detain him till tomorrow; then let them bring him here--him and my nephew--and come themselves, and be sure that I will be ready to receive them.’\n\n‘At what hour?’ asked the voice.\n\n‘At any hour,’ replied Ralph fiercely. ‘In the afternoon, tell them. At any hour, at any minute. All times will be alike to me.’\n\nHe listened to the man’s retreating footsteps until the sound had passed, and then, gazing up into the sky, saw, or thought he saw, the same black cloud that had seemed to follow him home, and which now appeared to hover directly above the house.\n\n‘I know its meaning now,’ he muttered, ‘and the restless nights, the dreams, and why I have quailed of late. All pointed to this. Oh! if men by selling their own souls could ride rampant for a term, for how short a term would I barter mine tonight!’\n\nThe sound of a deep bell came along the wind. One.\n\n‘Lie on!’ cried the usurer, ‘with your iron tongue! Ring merrily for births that make expectants writhe, and marriages that are made in hell, and toll ruefully for the dead whose shoes are worn already! Call men to prayers who are godly because not found out, and ring chimes for the coming in of every year that brings this cursed world nearer to its end. No bell or book for me! Throw me on a dunghill, and let me rot there, to infect the air!’\n\nWith a wild look around, in which frenzy, hatred, and despair were horribly mingled, he shook his clenched hand at the sky above him, which was still dark and threatening, and closed the window.\n\nThe rain and hail pattered against the glass; the chimneys quaked and rocked; the crazy casement rattled with the wind, as though an impatient hand inside were striving to burst it open. But no hand was there, and it opened no more.\n\n‘How’s this?’ cried one. ‘The gentleman say they can’t make anybody hear, and have been trying these two hours.’\n\n‘And yet he came home last night,’ said another; ‘for he spoke to somebody out of that window upstairs.’\n\nThey were a little knot of men, and, the window being mentioned, went out into the road to look up at it. This occasioned their observing that the house was still close shut, as the housekeeper had said she had left it on the previous night, and led to a great many suggestions: which terminated in two or three of the boldest getting round to the back, and so entering by a window, while the others remained outside, in impatient expectation.\n\nThey looked into all the rooms below: opening the shutters as they went, to admit the fading light: and still finding nobody, and everything quiet and in its place, doubted whether they should go farther. One man, however, remarking that they had not yet been into the garret, and that it was there he had been last seen, they agreed to look there too, and went up softly; for the mystery and silence made them timid.\n\nAfter they had stood for an instant, on the landing, eyeing each other, he who had proposed their carrying the search so far, turned the handle of the door, and, pushing it open, looked through the chink, and fell back directly.\n\n‘It’s very odd,’ he whispered, ‘he’s hiding behind the door! Look!’\n\nThey pressed forward to see; but one among them thrusting the others aside with a loud exclamation, drew a clasp-knife from his pocket, and dashing into the room, cut down the body.\n\nHe had torn a rope from one of the old trunks, and hung himself on an iron hook immediately below the trap-door in the ceiling--in the very place to which the eyes of his son, a lonely, desolate, little creature, had so often been directed in childish terror, fourteen years before.\n\n# CHAPTER 63\n\nThe Brothers Cheeryble make various Declarations for themselves and others. Tim Linkinwater makes a Declaration for himself\n\nSome weeks had passed, and the first shock of these events had subsided. Madeline had been removed; Frank had been absent; and Nicholas and Kate had begun to try in good earnest to stifle their own regrets, and to live for each other and for their mother--who, poor lady, could in nowise be reconciled to this dull and altered state of affairs--when there came one evening, per favour of Mr. Linkinwater, an invitation from the brothers to dinner on the next day but one: comprehending, not only Mrs. Nickleby, Kate, and Nicholas, but little Miss La Creevy, who was most particularly mentioned.\n\n‘Now, my dears,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, when they had rendered becoming honour to the bidding, and Tim had taken his departure, ‘what does THIS mean?’\n\n‘What do YOU mean, mother?’ asked Nicholas, smiling.\n\n‘I say, my dear,’ rejoined that lady, with a face of unfathomable mystery, ‘what does this invitation to dinner mean? What is its intention and object?’\n\n‘I conclude it means, that on such a day we are to eat and drink in their house, and that its intent and object is to confer pleasure upon us,’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘And that’s all you conclude it is, my dear?’\n\n‘I have not yet arrived at anything deeper, mother.’\n\n‘Then I’ll just tell you one thing,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, you’ll find yourself a little surprised; that’s all. You may depend upon it that this means something besides dinner.’\n\n‘Tea and supper, perhaps,’ suggested Nicholas.\n\n‘I wouldn’t be absurd, my dear, if I were you,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby, in a lofty manner, ‘because it’s not by any means becoming, and doesn’t suit you at all. What I mean to say is, that the Mr. Cheerybles don’t ask us to dinner with all this ceremony for nothing. Never mind; wait and see. You won’t believe anything I say, of course. It’s much better to wait; a great deal better; it’s satisfactory to all parties, and there can be no disputing. All I say is, remember what I say now, and when I say I said so, don’t say I didn’t.’\n\nWith this stipulation, Mrs. Nickleby, who was troubled, night and day, with a vision of a hot messenger tearing up to the door to announce that Nicholas had been taken into partnership, quitted that branch of the subject, and entered upon a new one.\n\n‘It’s a very extraordinary thing,’ she said, ‘a most extraordinary thing, that they should have invited Miss La Creevy. It quite astonishes me, upon my word it does. Of course it’s very pleasant that she should be invited, very pleasant, and I have no doubt that she’ll conduct herself extremely well; she always does. It’s very gratifying to think that we should have been the means of introducing her into such society, and I’m quite glad of it--quite rejoiced--for she certainly is an exceedingly well-behaved and good-natured little person. I could wish that some friend would mention to her how very badly she has her cap trimmed, and what very preposterous bows those are, but of course that’s impossible, and if she likes to make a fright of herself, no doubt she has a perfect right to do so. We never see ourselves--never do, and never did--and I suppose we never shall.’\n\nThis moral reflection reminding her of the necessity of being peculiarly smart on the occasion, so as to counterbalance Miss La Creevy, and be herself an effectual set-off and atonement, led Mrs. Nickleby into a consultation with her daughter relative to certain ribbons, gloves, and trimmings: which, being a complicated question, and one of paramount importance, soon routed the previous one, and put it to flight.\n\nThe great day arriving, the good lady put herself under Kate’s hands an hour or so after breakfast, and, dressing by easy stages, completed her toilette in sufficient time to allow of her daughter’s making hers, which was very simple, and not very long, though so satisfactory that she had never appeared more charming or looked more lovely. Miss La Creevy, too, arrived with two bandboxes (whereof the bottoms fell out as they were handed from the coach) and something in a newspaper, which a gentleman had sat upon, coming down, and which was obliged to be ironed again, before it was fit for service. At last, everybody was dressed, including Nicholas, who had come home to fetch them, and they went away in a coach sent by the brothers for the purpose: Mrs. Nickleby wondering very much what they would have for dinner, and cross-examining Nicholas as to the extent of his discoveries in the morning; whether he had smelt anything cooking at all like turtle, and if not, what he had smelt; and diversifying the conversation with reminiscences of dinners to which she had gone some twenty years ago, concerning which she particularised not only the dishes but the guests, in whom her hearers did not feel a very absorbing interest, as not one of them had ever chanced to hear their names before.\n\nThe old butler received them with profound respect and many smiles, and ushered them into the drawing-room, where they were received by the brothers with so much cordiality and kindness that Mrs. Nickleby was quite in a flutter, and had scarcely presence of mind enough, even to patronise Miss La Creevy. Kate was still more affected by the reception: for, knowing that the brothers were acquainted with all that had passed between her and Frank, she felt her position a most delicate and trying one, and was trembling on the arm of Nicholas, when Mr. Charles took her in his, and led her to another part of the room.\n\n‘Have you seen Madeline, my dear,’ he said, ‘since she left your house?’\n\n‘No, sir!’ replied Kate. ‘Not once.’\n\n‘And not heard from her, eh? Not heard from her?’\n\n‘I have only had one letter,’ rejoined Kate, gently. ‘I thought she would not have forgotten me quite so soon.’\n\n‘Ah,’ said the old man, patting her on the head, and speaking as affectionately as if she had been his favourite child. ‘Poor dear! what do you think of this, brother Ned? Madeline has only written to her once, only once, Ned, and she didn’t think she would have forgotten her quite so soon, Ned.’\n\n‘Oh! sad, sad; very sad!’ said Ned.\n\nThe brothers interchanged a glance, and looking at Kate for a little time without speaking, shook hands, and nodded as if they were congratulating each other on something very delightful.\n\n‘Well, well,’ said brother Charles, ‘go into that room, my dear--that door yonder--and see if there’s not a letter for you from her. I think there’s one upon the table. You needn’t hurry back, my love, if there is, for we don’t dine just yet, and there’s plenty of time. Plenty of time.’\n\nKate retired as she was directed. Brother Charles, having followed her graceful figure with his eyes, turned to Mrs. Nickleby, and said:\n\n‘We took the liberty of naming one hour before the real dinner-time, ma’am, because we had a little business to speak about, which would occupy the interval. Ned, my dear fellow, will you mention what we agreed upon? Mr. Nickleby, sir, have the goodness to follow me.’\n\nWithout any further explanation, Mrs. Nickleby, Miss La Creevy, and brother Ned, were left alone together, and Nicholas followed brother Charles into his private room; where, to his great astonishment, he encountered Frank, whom he supposed to be abroad.\n\n‘Young men,’ said Mr. Cheeryble, ‘shake hands!’\n\n‘I need no bidding to do that,’ said Nicholas, extending his.\n\n‘Nor I,’ rejoined Frank, as he clasped it heartily.\n\nThe old gentleman thought that two handsomer or finer young fellows could scarcely stand side by side than those on whom he looked with so much pleasure. Suffering his eyes to rest upon them, for a short time in silence, he said, while he seated himself at his desk:\n\n‘I wish to see you friends--close and firm friends--and if I thought you otherwise, I should hesitate in what I am about to say. Frank, look here! Mr. Nickleby, will you come on the other side?’\n\nThe young men stepped up on either hand of brother Charles, who produced a paper from his desk, and unfolded it.\n\n‘This,’ he said, ‘is a copy of the will of Madeline’s maternal grandfather, bequeathing her the sum of twelve thousand pounds, payable either upon her coming of age or marrying. It would appear that this gentleman, angry with her (his only relation) because she would not put herself under his protection, and detach herself from the society of her father, in compliance with his repeated overtures, made a will leaving this property (which was all he possessed) to a charitable institution. He would seem to have repented this determination, however, for three weeks afterwards, and in the same month, he executed this. By some fraud, it was abstracted immediately after his decease, and the other--the only will found--was proved and administered. Friendly negotiations, which have only just now terminated, have been proceeding since this instrument came into our hands, and, as there is no doubt of its authenticity, and the witnesses have been discovered (after some trouble), the money has been refunded. Madeline has therefore obtained her right, and is, or will be, when either of the contingencies which I have mentioned has arisen, mistress of this fortune. You understand me?’\n\nFrank replied in the affirmative. Nicholas, who could not trust himself to speak lest his voice should be heard to falter, bowed his head.\n\n‘Now, Frank,’ said the old gentleman, ‘you were the immediate means of recovering this deed. The fortune is but a small one; but we love Madeline; and such as it is, we would rather see you allied to her with that, than to any other girl we know who has three times the money. Will you become a suitor to her for her hand?’\n\n‘No, sir. I interested myself in the recovery of that instrument, believing that her hand was already pledged to one who has a thousand times the claims upon her gratitude, and, if I mistake not, upon her heart, that I or any other man can ever urge. In this it seems I judged hastily.’\n\n‘As you always do, sir,’ cried brother Charles, utterly forgetting his assumed dignity, ‘as you always do. How dare you think, Frank, that we would have you marry for money, when youth, beauty, and every amiable virtue and excellence were to be had for love? How dared you, Frank, go and make love to Mr. Nickleby’s sister without telling us first what you meant to do, and letting us speak for you?’\n\n‘I hardly dared to hope--’\n\n‘You hardly dared to hope! Then, so much the greater reason for having our assistance! Mr. Nickleby, sir, Frank, although he judged hastily, judged, for once, correctly. Madeline’s heart IS occupied. Give me your hand, sir; it is occupied by you, and worthily and naturally. This fortune is destined to be yours, but you have a greater fortune in her, sir, than you would have in money were it forty times told. She chooses you, Mr. Nickleby. She chooses as we, her dearest friends, would have her choose. Frank chooses as we would have HIM choose. He should have your sister’s little hand, sir, if she had refused it a score of times; ay, he should, and he shall! You acted nobly, not knowing our sentiments, but now you know them, sir, you must do as you are bid. What! You are the children of a worthy gentleman! The time was, sir, when my dear brother Ned and I were two poor simple-hearted boys, wandering, almost barefoot, to seek our fortunes: are we changed in anything but years and worldly circumstances since that time? No, God forbid! Oh, Ned, Ned, Ned, what a happy day this is for you and me! If our poor mother had only lived to see us now, Ned, how proud it would have made her dear heart at last!’\n\nThus apostrophised, brother Ned, who had entered with Mrs. Nickleby, and who had been before unobserved by the young men, darted forward, and fairly hugged brother Charles in his arms.\n\n‘Bring in my little Kate,’ said the latter, after a short silence. ‘Bring her in, Ned. Let me see Kate, let me kiss her. I have a right to do so now; I was very near it when she first came; I have often been very near it. Ah! Did you find the letter, my bird? Did you find Madeline herself, waiting for you and expecting you? Did you find that she had not quite forgotten her friend and nurse and sweet companion? Why, this is almost the best of all!’\n\n‘Come, come,’ said Ned, ‘Frank will be jealous, and we shall have some cutting of throats before dinner.’\n\n‘Then let him take her away, Ned, let him take her away. Madeline’s in the next room. Let all the lovers get out of the way, and talk among themselves, if they’ve anything to say. Turn ‘em out, Ned, every one!’\n\nBrother Charles began the clearance by leading the blushing girl to the door, and dismissing her with a kiss. Frank was not very slow to follow, and Nicholas had disappeared first of all. So there only remained Mrs Nickleby and Miss La Creevy, who were both sobbing heartily; the two brothers; and Tim Linkinwater, who now came in to shake hands with everybody: his round face all radiant and beaming with smiles.\n\n‘Well, Tim Linkinwater, sir,’ said brother Charles, who was always spokesman, ‘now the young folks are happy, sir.’\n\n‘You didn’t keep ‘em in suspense as long as you said you would, though,’ returned Tim, archly. ‘Why, Mr. Nickleby and Mr. Frank were to have been in your room for I don’t know how long; and I don’t know what you weren’t to have told them before you came out with the truth.’\n\n‘Now, did you ever know such a villain as this, Ned?’ said the old gentleman; ‘did you ever know such a villain as Tim Linkinwater? He accusing me of being impatient, and he the very man who has been wearying us morning, noon, and night, and torturing us for leave to go and tell ‘em what was in store, before our plans were half complete, or we had arranged a single thing. A treacherous dog!’\n\n‘So he is, brother Charles,’ returned Ned; ‘Tim is a treacherous dog. Tim is not to be trusted. Tim is a wild young fellow. He wants gravity and steadiness; he must sow his wild oats, and then perhaps he’ll become in time a respectable member of society.’\n\nThis being one of the standing jokes between the old fellows and Tim, they all three laughed very heartily, and might have laughed much longer, but that the brothers, seeing that Mrs. Nickleby was labouring to express her feelings, and was really overwhelmed by the happiness of the time, took her between them, and led her from the room under pretence of having to consult her on some most important arrangements.\n\nNow, Tim and Miss La Creevy had met very often, and had always been very chatty and pleasant together--had always been great friends--and consequently it was the most natural thing in the world that Tim, finding that she still sobbed, should endeavour to console her. As Miss La Creevy sat on a large old-fashioned window-seat, where there was ample room for two, it was also natural that Tim should sit down beside her; and as to Tim’s being unusually spruce and particular in his attire that day, why it was a high festival and a great occasion, and that was the most natural thing of all.\n\nTim sat down beside Miss La Creevy, and, crossing one leg over the other so that his foot--he had very comely feet and happened to be wearing the neatest shoes and black silk stockings possible--should come easily within the range of her eye, said in a soothing way:\n\n‘Don’t cry!’\n\n‘I must,’ rejoined Miss La Creevy.\n\n‘No, don’t,’ said Tim. ‘Please don’t; pray don’t.’\n\n‘I am so happy!’ sobbed the little woman.\n\n‘Then laugh,’ said Tim. ‘Do laugh.’\n\nWhat in the world Tim was doing with his arm, it is impossible to conjecture, but he knocked his elbow against that part of the window which was quite on the other side of Miss La Creevy; and it is clear that it could have no business there.\n\n‘Do laugh,’ said Tim, ‘or I’ll cry.’\n\n‘Why should you cry?’ asked Miss La Creevy, smiling.\n\n‘Because I’m happy too,’ said Tim. ‘We are both happy, and I should like to do as you do.’\n\nSurely, there never was a man who fidgeted as Tim must have done then; for he knocked the window again--almost in the same place--and Miss La Creevy said she was sure he’d break it.\n\n‘I knew,’ said Tim, ‘that you would be pleased with this scene.’\n\n‘It was very thoughtful and kind to remember me,’ returned Miss La Creevy. ‘Nothing could have delighted me half so much.’\n\nWhy on earth should Miss La Creevy and Tim Linkinwater have said all this in a whisper? It was no secret. And why should Tim Linkinwater have looked so hard at Miss La Creevy, and why should Miss La Creevy have looked so hard at the ground?\n\n‘It’s a pleasant thing,’ said Tim, ‘to people like us, who have passed all our lives in the world alone, to see young folks that we are fond of, brought together with so many years of happiness before them.’\n\n‘Ah!’ cried the little woman with all her heart, ‘that it is!’\n\n‘Although,’ pursued Tim ‘although it makes one feel quite solitary and cast away. Now don’t it?’\n\nMiss La Creevy said she didn’t know. And why should she say she didn’t know? Because she must have known whether it did or not.\n\n‘It’s almost enough to make us get married after all, isn’t it?’ said Tim.\n\n‘Oh, nonsense!’ replied Miss La Creevy, laughing. ‘We are too old.’\n\n‘Not a bit,’ said Tim; ‘we are too old to be single. Why shouldn’t we both be married, instead of sitting through the long winter evenings by our solitary firesides? Why shouldn’t we make one fireside of it, and marry each other?’\n\n‘Oh, Mr. Linkinwater, you’re joking!’\n\n‘No, no, I’m not. I’m not indeed,’ said Tim. ‘I will, if you will. Do, my dear!’\n\n‘It would make people laugh so.’\n\n‘Let ‘em laugh,’ cried Tim stoutly; ‘we have good tempers I know, and we’ll laugh too. Why, what hearty laughs we have had since we’ve known each other!’\n\n‘So we have,’ cried Miss La Creevy--giving way a little, as Tim thought.\n\n‘It has been the happiest time in all my life; at least, away from the counting-house and Cheeryble Brothers,’ said Tim. ‘Do, my dear! Now say you will.’\n\n‘No, no, we mustn’t think of it,’ returned Miss La Creevy. ‘What would the brothers say?’\n\n‘Why, God bless your soul!’ cried Tim, innocently, ‘you don’t suppose I should think of such a thing without their knowing it! Why they left us here on purpose.’\n\n‘I can never look ‘em in the face again!’ exclaimed Miss La Creevy, faintly.\n\n‘Come,’ said Tim, ‘let’s be a comfortable couple. We shall live in the old house here, where I have been for four-and-forty year; we shall go to the old church, where I’ve been, every Sunday morning, all through that time; we shall have all my old friends about us--Dick, the archway, the pump, the flower-pots, and Mr. Frank’s children, and Mr. Nickleby’s children, that we shall seem like grandfather and grandmother to. Let’s be a comfortable couple, and take care of each other! And if we should get deaf, or lame, or blind, or bed-ridden, how glad we shall be that we have somebody we are fond of, always to talk to and sit with! Let’s be a comfortable couple. Now, do, my dear!’\n\nFive minutes after this honest and straightforward speech, little Miss La Creevy and Tim were talking as pleasantly as if they had been married for a score of years, and had never once quarrelled all the time; and five minutes after that, when Miss La Creevy had bustled out to see if her eyes were red and put her hair to rights, Tim moved with a stately step towards the drawing-room, exclaiming as he went, ‘There an’t such another woman in all London! I KNOW there an’t!’\n\nBy this time, the apoplectic butler was nearly in fits, in consequence of the unheard-of postponement of dinner. Nicholas, who had been engaged in a manner in which every reader may imagine for himself or herself, was hurrying downstairs in obedience to his angry summons, when he encountered a new surprise.\n\nOn his way down, he overtook, in one of the passages, a stranger genteelly dressed in black, who was also moving towards the dining-room. As he was rather lame, and walked slowly, Nicholas lingered behind, and was following him step by step, wondering who he was, when he suddenly turned round and caught him by both hands.\n\n‘Newman Noggs!’ cried Nicholas joyfully\n\n‘Ah! Newman, your own Newman, your own old faithful Newman! My dear boy, my dear Nick, I give you joy--health, happiness, every blessing! I can’t bear it--it’s too much, my dear boy--it makes a child of me!’\n\n‘Where have you been?’ said Nicholas. ‘What have you been doing? How often have I inquired for you, and been told that I should hear before long!’\n\n‘I know, I know!’ returned Newman. ‘They wanted all the happiness to come together. I’ve been helping ‘em. I--I--look at me, Nick, look at me!’\n\n‘You would never let ME do that,’ said Nicholas in a tone of gentle reproach.\n\n‘I didn’t mind what I was, then. I shouldn’t have had the heart to put on gentleman’s clothes. They would have reminded me of old times and made me miserable. I am another man now, Nick. My dear boy, I can’t speak. Don’t say anything to me. Don’t think the worse of me for these tears. You don’t know what I feel today; you can’t, and never will!’\n\nThey walked in to dinner arm-in-arm, and sat down side by side.\n\nNever was such a dinner as that, since the world began. There was the superannuated bank clerk, Tim Linkinwater’s friend; and there was the chubby old lady, Tim Linkinwater’s sister; and there was so much attention from Tim Linkinwater’s sister to Miss La Creevy, and there were so many jokes from the superannuated bank clerk, and Tim Linkinwater himself was in such tiptop spirits, and little Miss La Creevy was in such a comical state, that of themselves they would have composed the pleasantest party conceivable. Then, there was Mrs Nickleby, so grand and complacent; Madeline and Kate, so blushing and beautiful; Nicholas and Frank, so devoted and proud; and all four so silently and tremblingly happy; there was Newman so subdued yet so overjoyed, and there were the twin brothers so delighted and interchanging such looks, that the old servant stood transfixed behind his master’s chair, and felt his eyes grow dim as they wandered round the table.\n\nWhen the first novelty of the meeting had worn off, and they began truly to feel how happy they were, the conversation became more general, and the harmony and pleasure if possible increased. The brothers were in a perfect ecstasy; and their insisting on saluting the ladies all round, before they would permit them to retire, gave occasion to the superannuated bank clerk to say so many good things, that he quite outshone himself, and was looked upon as a prodigy of humour.\n\n‘Kate, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, taking her daughter aside, as soon as they got upstairs, ‘you don’t really mean to tell me that this is actually true about Miss La Creevy and Mr. Linkinwater?’\n\n‘Indeed it is, mama.’\n\n‘Why, I never heard such a thing in my life!’ exclaimed Mrs. Nickleby.\n\n‘Mr. Linkinwater is a most excellent creature,’ reasoned Kate, ‘and, for his age, quite young still.’\n\n‘For HIS age, my dear!’ returned Mrs. Nickleby, ‘yes; nobody says anything against him, except that I think he is the weakest and most foolish man I ever knew. It’s HER age I speak of. That he should have gone and offered himself to a woman who must be--ah, half as old again as I am--and that she should have dared to accept him! It don’t signify, Kate; I’m disgusted with her!’\n\nShaking her head very emphatically indeed, Mrs. Nickleby swept away; and all the evening, in the midst of the merriment and enjoyment that ensued, and in which with that exception she freely participated, conducted herself towards Miss La Creevy in a stately and distant manner, designed to mark her sense of the impropriety of her conduct, and to signify her extreme and cutting disapprobation of the misdemeanour she had so flagrantly committed.\n\n# CHAPTER 64\n\nAn old Acquaintance is recognised under melancholy Circumstances, and Dotheboys Hall breaks up for ever\nNicholas was one of those whose joy is incomplete unless it is shared by the friends of adverse and less fortunate days. Surrounded by every fascination of love and hope, his warm heart yearned towards plain John Browdie. He remembered their first meeting with a smile, and their second with a tear; saw poor Smike once again with the bundle on his shoulder trudging patiently by his side; and heard the honest Yorkshireman’s rough words of encouragement as he left them on their road to London.\n\nMadeline and he sat down, very many times, jointly to produce a letter which should acquaint John at full length with his altered fortunes, and assure him of his friendship and gratitude. It so happened, however, that the letter could never be written. Although they applied themselves to it with the best intentions in the world, it chanced that they always fell to talking about something else, and when Nicholas tried it by himself, he found it impossible to write one-half of what he wished to say, or to pen anything, indeed, which on reperusal did not appear cold and unsatisfactory compared with what he had in his mind. At last, after going on thus from day to day, and reproaching himself more and more, he resolved (the more readily as Madeline strongly urged him) to make a hasty trip into Yorkshire, and present himself before Mr. and Mrs. Browdie without a word of notice.\n\nThus it was that between seven and eight o’clock one evening, he and Kate found themselves in the Saracen’s Head booking-office, securing a place to Greta Bridge by the next morning’s coach. They had to go westward, to procure some little necessaries for his journey, and, as it was a fine night, they agreed to walk there, and ride home.\n\nThe place they had just been in called up so many recollections, and Kate had so many anecdotes of Madeline, and Nicholas so many anecdotes of Frank, and each was so interested in what the other said, and both were so happy and confiding, and had so much to talk about, that it was not until they had plunged for a full half-hour into that labyrinth of streets which lies between Seven Dials and Soho, without emerging into any large thoroughfare, that Nicholas began to think it just possible they might have lost their way.\n\nThe possibility was soon converted into a certainty; for, on looking about, and walking first to one end of the street and then to the other, he could find no landmark he could recognise, and was fain to turn back again in quest of some place at which he could seek a direction.\n\nIt was a by-street, and there was nobody about, or in the few wretched shops they passed. Making towards a faint gleam of light which streamed across the pavement from a cellar, Nicholas was about to descend two or three steps so as to render himself visible to those below and make his inquiry, when he was arrested by a loud noise of scolding in a woman’s voice.\n\n‘Oh come away!’ said Kate, ‘they are quarrelling. You’ll be hurt.’\n\n‘Wait one instant, Kate. Let us hear if there’s anything the matter,’ returned her brother. ‘Hush!’\n\n‘You nasty, idle, vicious, good-for-nothing brute,’ cried the woman, stamping on the ground, ‘why don’t you turn the mangle?’\n\n‘So I am, my life and soul!’ replied the man’s voice. ‘I am always turning. I am perpetually turning, like a demd old horse in a demnition mill. My life is one demd horrid grind!’\n\n‘Then why don’t you go and list for a soldier?’ retorted the woman; ‘you’re welcome to.’\n\n‘For a soldier!’ cried the man. ‘For a soldier! Would his joy and gladness see him in a coarse red coat with a little tail? Would she hear of his being slapped and beat by drummers demnebly? Would she have him fire off real guns, and have his hair cut, and his whiskers shaved, and his eyes turned right and left, and his trousers pipeclayed?’\n\n‘Dear Nicholas,’ whispered Kate, ‘you don’t know who that is. It’s Mr Mantalini I am confident.’\n\n‘Do make sure! Peep at him while I ask the way,’ said Nicholas. ‘Come down a step or two. Come!’\n\nDrawing her after him, Nicholas crept down the steps and looked into a small boarded cellar. There, amidst clothes-baskets and clothes, stripped up to his shirt-sleeves, but wearing still an old patched pair of pantaloons of superlative make, a once brilliant waistcoat, and moustache and whiskers as of yore, but lacking their lustrous dye--there, endeavouring to mollify the wrath of a buxom female--not the lawful Madame Mantalini, but the proprietress of the concern--and grinding meanwhile as if for very life at the mangle, whose creaking noise, mingled with her shrill tones, appeared almost to deafen him--there was the graceful, elegant, fascinating, and once dashing Mantalini.\n\n‘Oh you false traitor!’ cried the lady, threatening personal violence on Mr. Mantalini’s face.\n\n‘False! Oh dem! Now my soul, my gentle, captivating, bewitching, and most demnebly enslaving chick-a-biddy, be calm,’ said Mr. Mantalini, humbly.\n\n‘I won’t!’ screamed the woman. ‘I’ll tear your eyes out!’\n\n‘Oh! What a demd savage lamb!’ cried Mr. Mantalini.\n\n‘You’re never to be trusted,’ screamed the woman; ‘you were out all day yesterday, and gallivanting somewhere I know. You know you were! Isn’t it enough that I paid two pound fourteen for you, and took you out of prison and let you live here like a gentleman, but must you go on like this: breaking my heart besides?’\n\n‘I will never break its heart, I will be a good boy, and never do so any more; I will never be naughty again; I beg its little pardon,’ said Mr. Mantalini, dropping the handle of the mangle, and folding his palms together; ‘it is all up with its handsome friend! He has gone to the demnition bow-wows. It will have pity? It will not scratch and claw, but pet and comfort? Oh, demmit!’\n\nVery little affected, to judge from her action, by this tender appeal, the lady was on the point of returning some angry reply, when Nicholas, raising his voice, asked his way to Piccadilly.\n\nMr. Mantalini turned round, caught sight of Kate, and, without another word, leapt at one bound into a bed which stood behind the door, and drew the counterpane over his face: kicking meanwhile convulsively.\n\n‘Demmit,’ he cried, in a suffocating voice, ‘it’s little Nickleby! Shut the door, put out the candle, turn me up in the bedstead! Oh, dem, dem, dem!’\n\nThe woman looked, first at Nicholas, and then at Mr. Mantalini, as if uncertain on whom to visit this extraordinary behaviour; but Mr Mantalini happening by ill-luck to thrust his nose from under the bedclothes, in his anxiety to ascertain whether the visitors were gone, she suddenly, and with a dexterity which could only have been acquired by long practice, flung a pretty heavy clothes-basket at him, with so good an aim that he kicked more violently than before, though without venturing to make any effort to disengage his head, which was quite extinguished. Thinking this a favourable opportunity for departing before any of the torrent of her wrath discharged itself upon him, Nicholas hurried Kate off, and left the unfortunate subject of this unexpected recognition to explain his conduct as he best could.\n\nThe next morning he began his journey. It was now cold, winter weather: forcibly recalling to his mind under what circumstances he had first travelled that road, and how many vicissitudes and changes he had since undergone. He was alone inside the greater part of the way, and sometimes, when he had fallen into a doze, and, rousing himself, looked out of the window, and recognised some place which he well remembered as having passed, either on his journey down, or in the long walk back with poor Smike, he could hardly believe but that all which had since happened had been a dream, and that they were still plodding wearily on towards London, with the world before them.\n\nTo render these recollections the more vivid, it came on to snow as night set in; and, passing through Stamford and Grantham, and by the little alehouse where he had heard the story of the bold Baron of Grogzwig, everything looked as if he had seen it but yesterday, and not even a flake of the white crust on the roofs had melted away. Encouraging the train of ideas which flocked upon him, he could almost persuade himself that he sat again outside the coach, with Squeers and the boys; that he heard their voices in the air; and that he felt again, but with a mingled sensation of pain and pleasure now, that old sinking of the heart, and longing after home. While he was yet yielding himself up to these fancies he fell asleep, and, dreaming of Madeline, forgot them.\n\nHe slept at the inn at Greta Bridge on the night of his arrival, and, rising at a very early hour next morning, walked to the market town, and inquired for John Browdie’s house. John lived in the outskirts, now he was a family man; and as everbody knew him, Nicholas had no difficulty in finding a boy who undertook to guide him to his residence.\n\nDismissing his guide at the gate, and in his impatience not even stopping to admire the thriving look of cottage or garden either, Nicholas made his way to the kitchen door, and knocked lustily with his stick.\n\n‘Halloa!’ cried a voice inside. ‘Wa’et be the matther noo? Be the toon a-fire? Ding, but thou mak’st noise eneaf!’\n\nWith these words, John Browdie opened the door himself, and opening his eyes too to their utmost width, cried, as he clapped his hands together, and burst into a hearty roar:\n\n‘Ecod, it be the godfeyther, it be the godfeyther! Tilly, here be Misther Nickleby. Gi’ us thee hond, mun. Coom awa’, coom awa’. In wi ‘un, doon beside the fire; tak’ a soop o’ thot. Dinnot say a word till thou’st droonk it a’! Oop wi’ it, mun. Ding! but I’m reeght glod to see thee.’\n\nAdapting his action to his text, John dragged Nicholas into the kitchen, forced him down upon a huge settle beside a blazing fire, poured out from an enormous bottle about a quarter of a pint of spirits, thrust it into his hand, opened his mouth and threw back his head as a sign to him to drink it instantly, and stood with a broad grin of welcome overspreading his great red face like a jolly giant.\n\n‘I might ha’ knowa’d,’ said John, ‘that nobody but thou would ha’ coom wi’ sike a knock as you. Thot was the wa’ thou knocked at schoolmeasther’s door, eh? Ha, ha, ha! But I say; wa’at be a’ this aboot schoolmeasther?’\n\n‘You know it then?’ said Nicholas.\n\n‘They were talking aboot it, doon toon, last neeght,’ replied John, ‘but neane on ‘em seemed quite to un’erstan’ it, loike.’\n\n‘After various shiftings and delays,’ said Nicholas, ‘he has been sentenced to be transported for seven years, for being in the unlawful possession of a stolen will; and, after that, he has to suffer the consequence of a conspiracy.’\n\n‘Whew!’ cried John, ‘a conspiracy! Soom’at in the pooder-plot wa’? Eh? Soom’at in the Guy Faux line?’\n\n‘No, no, no, a conspiracy connected with his school; I’ll explain it presently.’\n\n‘Thot’s reeght!’ said John, ‘explain it arter breakfast, not noo, for thou be’est hoongry, and so am I; and Tilly she mun’ be at the bottom o’ a’ explanations, for she says thot’s the mutual confidence. Ha, ha, ha! Ecod, it’s a room start, is the mutual confidence!’\n\nThe entrance of Mrs. Browdie, with a smart cap on, and very many apologies for their having been detected in the act of breakfasting in the kitchen, stopped John in his discussion of this grave subject, and hastened the breakfast: which, being composed of vast mounds of toast, new-laid eggs, boiled ham, Yorkshire pie, and other cold substantials (of which heavy relays were constantly appearing from another kitchen under the direction of a very plump servant), was admirably adapted to the cold bleak morning, and received the utmost justice from all parties. At last, it came to a close; and the fire which had been lighted in the best parlour having by this time burnt up, they adjourned thither, to hear what Nicholas had to tell.\n\nNicholas told them all, and never was there a story which awakened so many emotions in the breasts of two eager listeners. At one time, honest John groaned in sympathy, and at another roared with joy; at one time he vowed to go up to London on purpose to get a sight of the brothers Cheeryble; and, at another, swore that Tim Linkinwater should receive such a ham by coach, and carriage free, as mortal knife had never carved. When Nicholas began to describe Madeline, he sat with his mouth wide open, nudging Mrs. Browdie from time to time, and exclaiming under his breath that she must be ‘raa’ther a tidy sart,’ and when he heard at last that his young friend had come down purposely to communicate his good fortune, and to convey to him all those assurances of friendship which he could not state with sufficient warmth in writing--that the only object of his journey was to share his happiness with them, and to tell them that when he was married they must come up to see him, and that Madeline insisted on it as well as he--John could hold out no longer, but after looking indignantly at his wife, and demanding to know what she was whimpering for, drew his coat sleeve over his eyes and blubbered outright.\n\n‘Tell’ee wa’at though,’ said John seriously, when a great deal had been said on both sides, ‘to return to schoolmeasther. If this news aboot ‘un has reached school today, the old ‘ooman wean’t have a whole boan in her boddy, nor Fanny neither.’\n\n‘Oh, John!’ cried Mrs. Browdie.\n\n‘Ah! and Oh, John agean,’ replied the Yorkshireman. ‘I dinnot know what they lads mightn’t do. When it first got aboot that schoolmeasther was in trouble, some feythers and moothers sent and took their young chaps awa’. If them as is left, should know waat’s coom tiv’un, there’ll be sike a revolution and rebel!--Ding! But I think they’ll a’ gang daft, and spill bluid like wather!’\n\nIn fact, John Browdie’s apprehensions were so strong that he determined to ride over to the school without delay, and invited Nicholas to accompany him, which, however, he declined, pleading that his presence might perhaps aggravate the bitterness of their adversity.\n\n‘Thot’s true!’ said John; ‘I should ne’er ha’ thought o’ thot.’\n\n‘I must return tomorrow,’ said Nicholas, ‘but I mean to dine with you today, and if Mrs. Browdie can give me a bed--’\n\n‘Bed!’ cried John, ‘I wish thou couldst sleep in fower beds at once. Ecod, thou shouldst have ‘em a’. Bide till I coom back; on’y bide till I coom back, and ecod we’ll make a day of it.’\n\nGiving his wife a hearty kiss, and Nicholas a no less hearty shake of the hand, John mounted his horse and rode off: leaving Mrs. Browdie to apply herself to hospitable preparations, and his young friend to stroll about the neighbourhood, and revisit spots which were rendered familiar to him by many a miserable association.\n\nJohn cantered away, and arriving at Dotheboys Hall, tied his horse to a gate and made his way to the schoolroom door, which he found locked on the inside. A tremendous noise and riot arose from within, and, applying his eye to a convenient crevice in the wall, he did not remain long in ignorance of its meaning.\n\nThe news of Mr. Squeers’s downfall had reached Dotheboys; that was quite clear. To all appearance, it had very recently become known to the young gentlemen; for the rebellion had just broken out.\n\nIt was one of the brimstone-and-treacle mornings, and Mrs. Squeers had entered school according to custom with the large bowl and spoon, followed by Miss Squeers and the amiable Wackford: who, during his father’s absence, had taken upon him such minor branches of the executive as kicking the pupils with his nailed boots, pulling the hair of some of the smaller boys, pinching the others in aggravating places, and rendering himself, in various similar ways, a great comfort and happiness to his mother. Their entrance, whether by premeditation or a simultaneous impulse, was the signal of revolt. While one detachment rushed to the door and locked it, and another mounted on the desks and forms, the stoutest (and consequently the newest) boy seized the cane, and confronting Mrs. Squeers with a stern countenance, snatched off her cap and beaver bonnet, put them on his own head, armed himself with the wooden spoon, and bade her, on pain of death, go down upon her knees and take a dose directly. Before that estimable lady could recover herself, or offer the slightest retaliation, she was forced into a kneeling posture by a crowd of shouting tormentors, and compelled to swallow a spoonful of the odious mixture, rendered more than usually savoury by the immersion in the bowl of Master Wackford’s head, whose ducking was intrusted to another rebel. The success of this first achievement prompted the malicious crowd, whose faces were clustered together in every variety of lank and half-starved ugliness, to further acts of outrage. The leader was insisting upon Mrs. Squeers repeating her dose, Master Squeers was undergoing another dip in the treacle, and a violent assault had been commenced on Miss Squeers, when John Browdie, bursting open the door with a vigorous kick, rushed to the rescue. The shouts, screams, groans, hoots, and clapping of hands, suddenly ceased, and a dead silence ensued.\n\n‘Ye be noice chaps,’ said John, looking steadily round. ‘What’s to do here, thou yoong dogs?’\n\n‘Squeers is in prison, and we are going to run away!’ cried a score of shrill voices. ‘We won’t stop, we won’t stop!’\n\n‘Weel then, dinnot stop,’ replied John; ‘who waants thee to stop? Roon awa’ loike men, but dinnot hurt the women.’\n\n‘Hurrah!’ cried the shrill voices, more shrilly still.\n\n‘Hurrah?’ repeated John. ‘Weel, hurrah loike men too. Noo then, look out. Hip--hip,--hip--hurrah!’\n\n‘Hurrah!’ cried the voices.\n\n‘Hurrah! Agean;’ said John. ‘Looder still.’\n\nThe boys obeyed.\n\n‘Anoother!’ said John. ‘Dinnot be afeared on it. Let’s have a good ‘un!’\n\n‘Hurrah!’\n\n‘Noo then,’ said John, ‘let’s have yan more to end wi’, and then coot off as quick as you loike. Tak’a good breath noo--Squeers be in jail--the school’s brokken oop--it’s a’ ower--past and gane--think o’ thot, and let it be a hearty ‘un! Hurrah!’\n\nSuch a cheer arose as the walls of Dotheboys Hall had never echoed before, and were destined never to respond to again. When the sound had died away, the school was empty; and of the busy noisy crowd which had peopled it but five minutes before, not one remained.\n\n‘Very well, Mr. Browdie!’ said Miss Squeers, hot and flushed from the recent encounter, but vixenish to the last; ‘you’ve been and excited our boys to run away. Now see if we don’t pay you out for that, sir! If my pa IS unfortunate and trod down by henemies, we’re not going to be basely crowed and conquered over by you and ‘Tilda.’\n\n‘Noa!’ replied John bluntly, ‘thou bean’t. Tak’ thy oath o’ thot. Think betther o’ us, Fanny. I tell ‘ee both, that I’m glod the auld man has been caught out at last--dom’d glod--but ye’ll sooffer eneaf wi’out any crowin’ fra’ me, and I be not the mun to crow, nor be Tilly the lass, so I tell ‘ee flat. More than thot, I tell ‘ee noo, that if thou need’st friends to help thee awa’ from this place--dinnot turn up thy nose, Fanny, thou may’st--thou’lt foind Tilly and I wi’ a thout o’ old times aboot us, ready to lend thee a hond. And when I say thot, dinnot think I be asheamed of waa’t I’ve deane, for I say again, Hurrah! and dom the schoolmeasther. There!’\n\nHis parting words concluded, John Browdie strode heavily out, remounted his nag, put him once more into a smart canter, and, carolling lustily forth some fragments of an old song, to which the horse’s hoofs rang a merry accompaniment, sped back to his pretty wife and to Nicholas.\n\nFor some days afterwards, the neighbouring country was overrun with boys, who, the report went, had been secretly furnished by Mr. and Mrs Browdie, not only with a hearty meal of bread and meat, but with sundry shillings and sixpences to help them on their way. To this rumour John always returned a stout denial, which he accompanied, however, with a lurking grin, that rendered the suspicious doubtful, and fully confirmed all previous believers.\n\nThere were a few timid young children, who, miserable as they had been, and many as were the tears they had shed in the wretched school, still knew no other home, and had formed for it a sort of attachment, which made them weep when the bolder spirits fled, and cling to it as a refuge. Of these, some were found crying under hedges and in such places, frightened at the solitude. One had a dead bird in a little cage; he had wandered nearly twenty miles, and when his poor favourite died, lost courage, and lay down beside him. Another was discovered in a yard hard by the school, sleeping with a dog, who bit at those who came to remove him, and licked the sleeping child’s pale face.\n\nThey were taken back, and some other stragglers were recovered, but by degrees they were claimed, or lost again; and, in course of time, Dotheboys Hall and its last breaking-up began to be forgotten by the neighbours, or to be only spoken of as among the things that had been.\n\nConclusion\nWhen her term of mourning had expired, Madeline gave her hand and fortune to Nicholas; and, on the same day and at the same time, Kate became Mrs. Frank Cheeryble. It was expected that Tim Linkinwater and Miss La Creevy would have made a third couple on the occasion, but they declined, and two or three weeks afterwards went out together one morning before breakfast, and, coming back with merry faces, were found to have been quietly married that day.\n\nThe money which Nicholas acquired in right of his wife he invested in the firm of Cheeryble Brothers, in which Frank had become a partner. Before many years elapsed, the business began to be carried on in the names of ‘Cheeryble and Nickleby,’ so that Mrs. Nickleby’s prophetic anticipations were realised at last.\n\nThe twin brothers retired. Who needs to be told that THEY were happy? They were surrounded by happiness of their own creation, and lived but to increase it.\n\nTim Linkinwater condescended, after much entreaty and brow-beating, to accept a share in the house; but he could never be prevailed upon to suffer the publication of his name as a partner, and always persisted in the punctual and regular discharge of his clerkly duties.\n\nHe and his wife lived in the old house, and occupied the very bedchamber in which he had slept for four-and-forty years. As his wife grew older, she became even a more cheerful and light-hearted little creature; and it was a common saying among their friends, that it was impossible to say which looked the happier, Tim as he sat calmly smiling in his elbow-chair on one side of the fire, or his brisk little wife chatting and laughing, and constantly bustling in and out of hers, on the other.\n\nDick, the blackbird, was removed from the counting-house and promoted to a warm corner in the common sitting-room. Beneath his cage hung two miniatures, of Mrs. Linkinwater’s execution; one representing herself, and the other Tim; and both smiling very hard at all beholders. Tim’s head being powdered like a twelfth cake, and his spectacles copied with great nicety, strangers detected a close resemblance to him at the first glance, and this leading them to suspect that the other must be his wife, and emboldening them to say so without scruple, Mrs. Linkinwater grew very proud of these achievements in time, and considered them among the most successful likenesses she had ever painted. Tim had the profoundest faith in them, likewise; for on this, as on all other subjects, they held but one opinion; and if ever there were a ‘comfortable couple’ in the world, it was Mr. and Mrs. Linkinwater.\n\nRalph, having died intestate, and having no relations but those with whom he had lived in such enmity, they would have become in legal course his heirs. But they could not bear the thought of growing rich on money so acquired, and felt as though they could never hope to prosper with it. They made no claim to his wealth; and the riches for which he had toiled all his days, and burdened his soul with so many evil deeds, were swept at last into the coffers of the state, and no man was the better or the happier for them.\n\nArthur Gride was tried for the unlawful possession of the will, which he had either procured to be stolen, or had dishonestly acquired and retained by other means as bad. By dint of an ingenious counsel, and a legal flaw, he escaped; but only to undergo a worse punishment; for, some years afterwards, his house was broken open in the night by robbers, tempted by the rumours of his great wealth, and he was found murdered in his bed.\n\nMrs. Sliderskew went beyond the seas at nearly the same time as Mr Squeers, and in the course of nature never returned. Brooker died penitent. Sir Mulberry Hawk lived abroad for some years, courted and caressed, and in high repute as a fine dashing fellow. Ultimately, returning to this country, he was thrown into jail for debt, and there perished miserably, as such high spirits generally do.\n\nThe first act of Nicholas, when he became a rich and prosperous merchant, was to buy his father’s old house. As time crept on, and there came gradually about him a group of lovely children, it was altered and enlarged; but none of the old rooms were ever pulled down, no old tree was ever rooted up, nothing with which there was any association of bygone times was ever removed or changed.\n\nWithin a stone’s throw was another retreat, enlivened by children’s pleasant voices too; and here was Kate, with many new cares and occupations, and many new faces courting her sweet smile (and one so like her own, that to her mother she seemed a child again), the same true gentle creature, the same fond sister, the same in the love of all about her, as in her girlish days.\n\nMrs. Nickleby lived, sometimes with her daughter, and sometimes with her son, accompanying one or other of them to London at those periods when the cares of business obliged both families to reside there, and always preserving a great appearance of dignity, and relating her experiences (especially on points connected with the management and bringing-up of children) with much solemnity and importance. It was a very long time before she could be induced to receive Mrs. Linkinwater into favour, and it is even doubtful whether she ever thoroughly forgave her.\n\nThere was one grey-haired, quiet, harmless gentleman, who, winter and summer, lived in a little cottage hard by Nicholas’s house, and, when he was not there, assumed the superintendence of affairs. His chief pleasure and delight was in the children, with whom he was a child himself, and master of the revels. The little people could do nothing without dear Newman Noggs.\n\nThe grass was green above the dead boy’s grave, and trodden by feet so small and light, that not a daisy drooped its head beneath their pressure. Through all the spring and summertime, garlands of fresh flowers, wreathed by infant hands, rested on the stone; and, when the children came to change them lest they should wither and be pleasant to him no longer, their eyes filled with tears, and they spoke low and softly of their poor dead cousin.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-967"]="옛날 데번셔 지방의 외딴 마을에 고드프리 니클비라는 사람이 살았어. 점잖은 신사였지. 그런데 나이가 꽤 들어서야 결혼을 해야겠다는 생각이 머릿속에 떠올랐어. 젊지도 않고 부자도 아니어서 돈 많은 집안의 아가씨한테 청혼할 처지가 못 되었지. 그래서 그는 옛날에 좋아하던 여자와 그저 마음 하나로 결혼했어. 그 여자도 같은 이유로 그를 받아들였지. 이렇게 돈을 걸고 카드놀이를 할 형편이 안 되는 두 사람이, 가끔은 그냥 재미로 조용히 한 판을 벌이는 거야.\n\n결혼 생활을 비웃는 심술궂은 사람들은 아마 여기서 이렇게 말할지도 몰라. 이 착한 부부는 권투 시합의 두 선수에 비유하는 게 더 어울린다고 말이야. 돈도 떨어지고 후원자도 없을 때, 그저 치고받는 재미만으로 의리 있게 한판 붙는 그런 선수들 말이지. 사실 한 가지 점에서는 그 비유가 들어맞기도 해. 권투장의 그 용감한 두 사람은 시합이 끝나면 모자를 돌려 구경꾼들의 인심에 기대 한턱 낼 밑천을 마련하잖아. 그것처럼 고드프리 니클비와 그의 아내도 신혼이 끝나자 세상을 그리운 눈으로 둘러보았어. 형편이 나아지기를 적잖이 운에 맡기면서 말이야. 니클비 씨가 결혼할 무렵 그의 수입은 한 해에 육십에서 팔십 파운드 사이를 오갔어.\n\n세상에 사람은 넘쳐 나잖아, 하늘도 알다시피! 니클비 씨가 그 시절 살던 런던도 마찬가지였어. 사람이 부족하다는 불평은 거의 없었지. 그 많은 사람들 틈에서 친구 얼굴 하나 찾지 못하고 한참을 두리번거리는 일이 얼마나 흔한지, 참 놀라운 노릇이지만 그게 사실이야. 니클비 씨는 보고 또 봤어. 눈이 마음만큼이나 아파 올 때까지 말이야. 그래도 친구는 나타나지 않았어. 찾기에 지쳐서 눈길을 집 쪽으로 돌렸지만, 거기서도 지친 눈을 달래 줄 만한 건 거의 보이지 않았어. 화가는 너무 오래 쳐다본 화려한 색에 눈이 부시면 더 어둡고 차분한 색을 보며 눈을 쉬게 하잖아. 그런데 니클비 씨의 눈에 들어오는 건 죄다 새까맣고 우울한 빛깔뿐이었어. 그러니 차라리 그 반대되는 걸 봤더라면 말할 수 없이 눈이 시원해졌을 거야.\n\n그러다 오 년이 지났어. 니클비 부인은 남편에게 아들 둘을 안겨 주었지. 곤궁한 처지의 이 신사는 가족을 위해 뭔가 대비를 해 둬야겠다는 생각에 사로잡혔어. 그래서 다음 분기 결산일에 생명 보험을 든 다음 모뉴먼트 꼭대기에서 사고로 떨어지는, 그런 자그마한 돈벌이 궁리를 진지하게 머릿속에서 굴리고 있었지. 그러던 어느 날 아침, 일반 우편으로 검은 테두리를 두른 편지 한 통이 왔어. 그의 삼촌인 랄프 니클비 씨가 죽었다는 소식이었지. 그리고 삼촌이 자기 재산의 대부분을 그에게 남겼다는 거야. 다 합쳐 오천 파운드나 되는 돈이었어.\n\n세상을 떠난 그 삼촌은 살아 있는 동안 조카에게 더 이상 신경을 쓰지 않았어. 그저 큰조카한테 — 혹시나 하는 마음으로 삼촌 이름을 따서 세례명을 지은 아이였지 — 모로코가죽 상자에 든 은수저 하나를 보내 줬을 뿐이야. 그런데 그 수저로 떠먹을 음식이 넉넉지 못했으니, 그건 마치 입에 그 쓸 만한 은수저를 물고 태어나지 못한 걸 비꼬는 것 같았어. 그러니 고드프리 니클비 씨는 처음에는 이 소식을 도무지 믿을 수가 없었지. 그런데 알아보니 한 치도 틀림없는 사실이었어. 사연인즉 이래. 그 인심 좋은 노신사는 원래 전 재산을 왕립 인명 구조회에 남길 작정이었고, 실제로 그렇게 유언장까지 써 두었어. 그런데 그 구조회가 몇 달 전에 그만 운 나쁘게도, 노신사가 매주 삼 실링 육 펜스씩 용돈을 주던 가난한 친척의 목숨을 구해 준 거야. 그러자 노신사는 당연하다는 듯 발끈 화가 치밀어, 유언 보충서를 써서 그 유산을 도로 거둬들였어. 그러고는 전부 고드프리 니클비 씨에게 남겼지. 거기다 자기가 얼마나 분개했는지도 특별히 적어 두었어. 가난한 친척의 목숨을 구한 구조회에 대해서뿐 아니라, 제 목숨을 구하게 내버려 둔 그 가난한 친척한테도 말이야.\n\n고드프리 니클비 씨는 이 재산의 일부로 데번셔의 돌리시 근처에 작은 농장을 샀어. 그리고 아내와 두 아이를 데리고 그곳으로 내려가 살았지. 남은 돈으로 받을 수 있는 최대한의 이자와, 그 땅에서 거둘 수 있는 얼마 안 되는 소출에 기대어 사는 거야. 부부는 함께 사이좋게 잘 살았어. 그래서 그가 죽을 때 — 이 무렵에서 한 십오 년 뒤였고, 아내가 죽고 오 년쯤 지난 때였지 — 큰아들 랄프에게는 현금 삼천 파운드를, 작은아들 니콜라스에게는 천 파운드와 농장을 남길 수 있었어. 그 농장은 더없이 자그마한 땅뙈기였지만 말이야.\n\n이 두 형제는 엑서터의 한 학교에서 함께 자랐어. 일주일에 한 번씩 집에 가는 게 버릇이 되어 있었고, 그때마다 어머니 입에서 긴 이야기를 자주 들었지. 가난하던 시절 아버지가 겪은 고생담과, 부유하던 시절 돌아가신 삼촌이 얼마나 대단했는지에 관한 이야기였어. 그런데 이 이야기들은 두 형제에게 아주 다른 인상을 남겼어. 동생은 겁이 많고 내성적인 성격이라, 거기서 큰 세상을 피하고 조용한 시골 생활에 정 붙이라는 경고만 새겨들었거든. 하지만 형인 랄프는 그 되풀이되는 이야기에서 두 가지 큰 교훈을 끌어냈어. 하나는 돈이야말로 행복과 권력의 유일한 참된 근원이라는 것. 또 하나는 중범죄만 아니라면 무슨 수를 써서든 돈을 손에 넣는 게 옳고 정당하다는 것이었지. \"게다가,\" 랄프는 혼자 이렇게 따져 봤어. \"삼촌이 살아 있을 땐 그 돈으로 좋은 일이 하나도 없었지만, 죽고 나니 좋은 일이 잔뜩 생겼잖아. 지금은 아버지가 그 돈을 가지고 있고, 나를 위해 모아 두고 계시니까. 이건 아주 갸륵한 일이지. 그리고 그 노신사한테도 좋은 일이긴 했어. 평생 그 돈 생각하는 재미를 누렸고, 게다가 온 식구의 부러움과 비위 맞추기를 받았으니까.\" 그리고 랄프는 늘 이런 속말의 끝을 한 가지 결론으로 마무리했어. 돈만 한 게 없다는 것이었지.\n\n이 장래가 촉망되는 소년은 이론에만 머물거나, 그 어린 나이에 머리를 그저 추상적인 궁리로 녹슬게 두지 않았어. 학교에서부터 작은 규모로 고리대금업을 시작했지. 석필이며 구슬이며 약간의 밑천을 두둑한 이자를 받고 빌려주었어. 그러다 점점 사업을 키워서 마침내 이 나라의 동전까지 다루게 되었고, 거기서 꽤 짭짤한 이득을 봤지. 돈을 빌리는 아이들한테 복잡한 숫자 계산이나 계산표 같은 걸로 골치 아프게 하지도 않았어. 그의 이자 규칙은 아주 간단해서, 황금 같은 한마디에 다 담겨 있었거든. \"반 펜스마다 이 펜스.\" 이러니 계산이 엄청 단순해졌지. 게다가 익숙한 격언처럼 외기 쉽고 기억에도 잘 남으니, 알려진 그 어떤 산수 규칙보다 낫단 말이야. 그러니 크든 작든 모든 자본가들, 특히 돈을 빌려주는 중개인과 어음 할인업자들이 이 규칙을 꼭 눈여겨봐야 해. 사실 이런 사람들 명예를 위해 말하자면, 오늘날에도 그 가운데 많은 이들이 이 규칙을 즐겨 써먹으며 크게 재미를 보고 있거든.\n\n마찬가지로 어린 랄프 니클비는 자질구레한 날수 계산도 다 피해 버렸어. 단리로 계산을 해 본 사람이라면 누구나 이게 가장 골치 아프다는 걸 알 거야. 랄프는 일반 규칙을 하나 정해 버렸지. 원금이든 이자든 모든 돈은 용돈 받는 날, 그러니까 토요일에 갚아야 한다는 거였어. 그리고 돈을 월요일에 빌렸든 금요일에 빌렸든, 이자는 두 경우 다 똑같이 매겼지. 사실 랄프는 그럴듯한 근거를 대며 따졌어. 하루 빌린 사람이 닷새 빌린 사람보다 오히려 이자를 더 내야 한다고 말이야. 하루 만에 빌린 사람은 그만큼 다급한 처지라고 봐도 무방하다는 거였지. 안 그러면 그렇게 불리한 조건으로 굳이 빌리지 않았을 테니까. 이 점은 흥미로워. 위대한 머리들 사이에는 늘 어떤 은밀한 연결과 공감이 있다는 걸 보여 주거든. 그때 랄프 니클비 도련님은 미처 몰랐지만, 앞에서 말한 그런 부류의 신사들도 거래할 때마다 꼭 똑같은 원칙을 따른단 말이지.\n\n이 젊은 신사에 대해 우리가 늘어놓은 이야기와, 독자가 당장 그의 인품에 품게 될 자연스러운 감탄을 보면, 아마 그가 곧 시작될 이 이야기의 주인공이라고 짐작할지도 몰라. 이 점만큼은 단번에, 또 영원히 짚고 넘어가야겠어. 그러니 서둘러 오해를 풀어 주고, 곧장 이야기의 시작으로 성큼 들어가야지.\n\n랄프 니클비는 아버지가 돌아가시기 얼마 전에 이미 런던의 한 상점에 들어가 있었어. 아버지가 죽자 그는 돈 모으는 옛 일에 미친 듯이 매달렸지. 어찌나 거기에 파묻혀 빠져들었던지, 여러 해 동안 동생을 까맣게 잊고 지냈어. 그리고 가끔 그가 사는 안개 속을 뚫고 옛 놀이 친구 생각이 떠오를 때도 — 황금이란 사람 둘레에 안개를 피워 올리거든. 숯 연기보다 더 사람의 옛 감각을 망가뜨리고 마음을 마비시키는 안개를 말이야 — 그럴 때면 늘 한 가지 생각이 따라붙었어. 동생과 친하게 지내면 동생이 돈을 빌려 달라고 할 거라는 생각이었지. 그래서 랄프 니클비 씨는 어깨를 으쓱하고는, 지금 이대로가 낫다고 했어.\n\n니콜라스는 어땠냐면, 물려받은 땅에서 혼자 지내다가 혼자 사는 게 지겨워질 무렵 결혼을 했어. 이웃 신사의 딸을 아내로 맞았지. 천 파운드의 지참금을 가진 여자였어. 이 착한 부인은 그에게 아들 하나 딸 하나, 두 아이를 낳아 주었어. 그리고 아들이 열아홉 살쯤, 딸이 열네 살쯤 됐을 때 — 우리가 짐작할 수 있는 건 대략 이 정도야. 새 법이 통과되기 전에는 이 나라의 어떤 호적부에도 아가씨들 나이가 공평하게 기록되어 있지 않았거든 — 니클비 씨는 밑천을 다시 채울 방법이 없을까 하고 주위를 둘러보았어. 식구가 늘고 아이들 교육비가 들면서 밑천이 안타깝게도 많이 줄어든 참이었지.\n\n\"그걸로 투자를 해 봐요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"투, 자, 라고, 여보?\" 니클비 씨가 미심쩍은 듯 되물었어.\n\n\"왜 안 되는데요?\" 니클비 부인이 물었어.\n\n\"여보, 만약에 그걸 잃기라도 하면,\" 말이 느리고 뜸을 들이는 편인 니클비 씨가 대꾸했어. \"만약에 그걸 잃으면, 우리는 더 이상 살아갈 수가 없게 되잖소, 여보.\"\n\n\"쓸데없는 소리.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"글쎄, 나는 그게 영 확신이 안 서는데, 여보.\" 니클비 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"니콜라스를 봐요.\" 부인이 말을 이었어. \"벌써 청년이잖아요. 이제 자기 앞가림을 할 때도 됐고요. 케이트도 그래요, 가엾은 것, 세상에 한 푼도 없잖아요. 당신 형님을 생각해 봐요! 투자를 안 했다면 지금의 그 사람이 됐겠어요?\"\n\n\"그건 맞는 말이오.\" 니클비 씨가 대답했어. \"아주 좋아, 여보. 그래. 투자를 하겠소, 여보.\"\n\n투자란 일종의 둥근 놀이판 같은 거야. 처음 시작할 땐 자기 패가 어떤지 거의, 아니 전혀 안 보이거든. 이익이 클 수도 있어 — 그리고 손해도 그만큼 클 수 있지. 운은 니클비 씨를 비껴갔어. 광풍이 불었고, 거품이 터졌지. 주식 중개인 넷이 피렌체에 별장을 마련했고, 보잘것없는 사람 사백 명이 망했어. 그 가운데 니클비 씨도 끼어 있었지.\n\n\"내가 사는 이 집조차,\" 가엾은 신사는 한숨을 쉬었어. \"당장 내일이라도 빼앗길지 모르지. 내 낡은 가구 한 점까지 죄다 남의 손에 팔려 나갈 거야!\"\n\n마지막 생각이 어찌나 그를 아프게 했던지, 그는 곧장 자리에 드러누워 버렸어. 적어도 침대만큼은 끝까지 지키기로 마음먹은 모양이었지.\n\n\"기운 내세요, 선생님!\" 약제사가 말했어.\n\n\"의기소침해지시면 안 돼요, 선생님.\" 간호사가 말했어.\n\n\"이런 일은 날마다 일어난답니다.\" 변호사가 한마디 했어.\n\n\"그리고 이런 일에 거역하는 건 아주 죄스러운 짓이지요.\" 목사가 속삭였어.\n\n\"가족 있는 사람이라면 절대 해선 안 될 일이고요.\" 이웃들이 거들었어.\n\n니클비 씨는 고개를 저었어. 그러고는 모두에게 방에서 나가 달라고 손짓하고는, 아내와 아이들을 끌어안았지. 힘없이 뛰는 가슴에 하나씩 차례로 그들을 꼭 품었다가, 기진맥진해서 베개에 푹 가라앉았어. 식구들은 그 뒤로 그의 정신이 오락가락하는 걸 보고 가슴이 아팠어. 그가 한참 동안 형의 너그러움과 착한 마음씨에 대해, 또 학교에서 함께 지내던 즐거운 옛 시절에 대해 횡설수설했거든. 이 헛소리가 잦아들자, 그는 과부와 아버지 없는 아이들을 한 번도 저버린 적 없는 분께 식구들을 엄숙히 맡겼어. 그러고는 식구들을 향해 부드럽게 미소 짓더니 얼굴을 옆으로 돌리며, 이제 잠이 들 것 같다고 말했어.\n\n# CHAPTER 2\n\n랄프 니클비 씨는 엄밀히 말하면 상인이라고 부를 만한 사람이 아니었어. 은행가도 아니고, 변호사도 아니고, 법정 변론인도 아니고, 공증인도 아니었지. 장사꾼은 확실히 아니었고, 전문직 신사라는 이름을 붙일 처지는 더더욱 아니었어. 그가 속한 번듯한 직업을 하나라도 대는 건 도무지 불가능했거든. 그래도 그는 골든 스퀘어의 널찍한 집에 살았어. 거리로 난 현관문에 놋쇠 명패가 붙어 있었고, 거기에 더해 왼쪽 문기둥에도 그보다 두 치수 반쯤 작은 놋쇠 명패가 또 하나 붙어 있었지. 그 명패에는 꼬챙이 조각을 움켜쥔 아기 주먹 모양의 놋쇠 모형이 둘레를 두르고 있었고, '사무소'라는 글자가 적혀 있었어. 그러니 랄프 니클비 씨가 무슨 사업인가를 하고 있거나, 적어도 하는 척하고 있다는 건 분명했지. 이 사실은 만약 더 자세한 정황 증거가 필요하다면, 한 사내의 모습으로 충분히 입증되었어. 누렇게 뜬 얼굴에 빛바랜 갈색 옷을 입은 사내였는데, 매일 아홉 시 반부터 다섯 시까지 복도 끝의 집사용 식기 골방 같은 데서 유난히 딱딱한 걸상에 앉아 있었지. 그리고 종이 울려 응대하러 나갈 때면 늘 펜 한 자루를 귀 뒤에 꽂고 있었어.\n\n좀 더 점잖은 직업을 가진 사람 몇몇이 골든 스퀘어 근처에 살긴 하지만, 그곳은 누가 어딜 오가는 길목에 딱 들어맞는 동네는 아니야. 한때 잘나가던 광장 중 하나였지. 세상에서 한물간 동네라, 이제는 셋방이나 놓고 있었어. 일이 층 방 가운데 상당수가 가구까지 딸려 독신 신사들에게 세를 놓였지. 게다가 하숙도 쳤어. 외국인들이 많이 모이는 곳이기도 했지. 큼직한 반지를 끼고, 묵직한 시곗줄을 늘어뜨리고, 텁수룩한 구레나룻을 기른 가무잡잡한 사내들 말이야. 공연철이면 오후 네 시에서 다섯 시 사이, 입장권을 나눠 줄 때 오페라 회랑 아래며 매표소 근처에 모여드는 그런 사내들. 그들 모두가 골든 스퀘어에 살거나, 거기서 한 골목 안쪽에 살았어. 오페라 악단의 바이올린 두세 대와 관악기 하나도 그 구역 안에 자리 잡고 있었지. 하숙집들은 음악으로 가득했어. 저녁이면 피아노와 하프 소리가 둥둥 떠다녔지. 광장 한가운데 작은 관목 숲의 수호신처럼 서 있는, 그 구슬픈 석상의 머리 둘레를 맴돌면서 말이야. 여름밤이면 창문들이 활짝 열렸어. 그러면 가무잡잡하고 콧수염을 기른 사내들 무리가 보였지. 창틀에 기대어 무시무시하게 담배를 피워 대는 모습을 지나가는 사람들이 봤어. 성악 연습을 하는 굵직한 목소리들이 저녁의 고요를 비집고 들어왔어. 그리고 고급 담배 연기가 공기에 향을 입혔지. 거기서는 코담배와 시가, 독일제 파이프와 플루트, 바이올린과 첼로가 저마다 위세를 나눠 가지고 있었어. 노래와 연기의 고장이었지. 거리 악단들도 골든 스퀘어에서는 신명을 냈어. 떠돌이 합창단도 그 경계 안에서 목청을 높일 때면 저도 모르게 떨림 가락을 넣었지.\n\n이런 곳은 사업을 하기에 그다지 잘 맞는 자리로 보이진 않았어. 그런데도 랄프 니클비 씨는 여기서 오랜 세월을 살았고, 그 점에 대해 불평 한마디 하지 않았지. 그는 근처의 아무도 몰랐고, 아무도 그를 몰랐어. 엄청난 부자라는 평판은 누리고 있었지만 말이야. 장사꾼들은 그를 일종의 변호사쯤으로 여겼고, 다른 이웃들은 그가 무슨 만물 중개인 같은 사람이라고 생각했지. 두 짐작 다 옳고 분명하기로는, 남의 일에 대한 짐작이 으레 그렇거나 그래야 하는 딱 그 정도였어.\n\n어느 날 아침, 랄프 니클비 씨는 외출 채비를 다 마친 차림으로 자기 사무실에 앉아 있었어. 파란 코트 위에 병처럼 짙은 초록색 스펜서를 걸쳤지. 하얀 조끼에 회색 섞인 판탈롱 바지를 입고, 그 위로 웰링턴 부츠를 신었어. 잘게 주름 잡힌 셔츠 장식의 한쪽 끄트머리가, 턱과 스펜서 맨 윗단추 사이로 비집고 나와 있었지. 마치 제 모습을 꼭 보여 주겠다고 우기는 것 같았어. 게다가 그 스펜서는 길고 금빛 나는 시곗줄을 가릴 만큼 충분히 내려오질 않았어. 밋밋한 고리들을 죽 이어 만든 줄이었는데, 니클비 씨 주머니 속 금빛 시계의 손잡이에서 시작되어 작은 열쇠 두 개에서 끝났지. 하나는 시계 자체의 열쇠였고, 다른 하나는 무슨 특허받은 자물쇠 열쇠였어. 그는 머리에 분을 살짝 뿌리고 있었어. 자기 모습을 인자하게 보이게 하려는 것 같았지. 그런데 만약 그게 목적이었다면, 아마 얼굴에도 분을 발랐어야 했을 거야. 그 주름살에도, 그 차갑고 쉴 새 없이 움직이는 눈에도 뭔가가 있었거든. 본인이 아무리 감추려 해도 저절로 드러나고야 마는 교활함을 일러 주는 그런 것 말이야. 어쨌거나 그는 거기 있었지. 그런데 혼자뿐이었으니, 그 분도, 그 주름도, 그 눈도 그 순간 누구한테든 좋게든 나쁘게든 털끝만큼의 영향도 주지 않았어. 그러니 지금 우리가 신경 쓸 일도 아니지.\n\n니클비 씨는 책상에 놓인 장부 하나를 덮었어. 그러고는 의자에 등을 기대고 뒤로 젖히며, 멍한 표정으로 더러운 창문 너머를 바라봤지. 런던 집들 가운데 어떤 건 뒤편에 우울하고 자그마한 땅뙈기가 딸려 있어. 대개 회칠한 높은 담 넷으로 둘러싸여 있고, 굴뚝 더미가 그 위로 찌푸린 듯 내려다보고 있지. 거기서 해마다 절름발이 나무 한 그루가 시들어 가. 다른 나무들이 잎을 떨구는 늦가을이 되어서야 잎 몇 장 내미는 시늉을 하다가, 그 안간힘에 축 늘어진 채 버티는 거야. 온통 쩍쩍 갈라지고 연기에 그을린 채로, 다음 철까지 근근이 견디지. 그러다 또 똑같은 짓을 되풀이하고, 어쩌면 날씨가 유난히 포근하면 류머티즘 걸린 참새 한 마리를 꾀어 그 가지에서 짹짹거리게 만들기도 해. 사람들은 가끔 이 어두침침한 뒤뜰을 '정원'이라고 불러. 하지만 거기에 뭔가 심어 본 적이 있을 거라고 여기진 않아. 오히려 개간되지 않은 땅뙈기, 원래 벽돌 굽던 터의 시든 풀이 남은 자리쯤으로 보는 거지. 누구도 이 황량한 자리를 거닐 생각을 하지 않아. 무슨 쓸모를 찾아볼 생각도 안 하지. 세입자가 처음 이사 올 때 바구니 몇 개, 깨진 병 대여섯 개, 그 비슷한 잡동사니나 거기 던져 둘 뿐 그 이상은 없어. 그리고 그가 다시 떠날 때까지 그것들은 그대로 남아 있지. 축축한 짚은 제멋대로 천천히 썩어 가고, 듬성듬성한 회양목과 비실비실한 상록수, 깨진 화분 들과 뒤섞여 가는 거야. 그 화분들은 구슬프게 여기저기 흩어진 채, 검댕과 먼지의 밥이 되어 있지.\n\n랄프 니클비 씨가 바라본 게 바로 이런 곳이었어. 그는 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 창밖을 내다보고 앉아 있었지. 그의 눈길은 비틀어진 전나무 한 그루에 꽂혀 있었어. 어떤 옛 세입자가 한때 초록색이었던 통에 심어 놓고는, 여러 해 전에 거기 내버려 두어 야금야금 썩어 가게 한 나무였지. 그 물체에 딱히 마음 끌릴 만한 건 없었어. 그런데 니클비 씨는 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있어서, 정신이 좀 더 또렷할 때라면 세상 가장 진귀한 외래종에도 베풀지 않았을 만한 정성으로 그걸 가만히 바라보고 앉아 있었지. 마침내 그의 눈길은 왼쪽의 작고 더러운 창문으로 옮겨 갔어. 그 너머로 서기의 얼굴이 흐릿하게 보였지. 그 갸륵한 사내가 마침 고개를 들었기에, 니클비 씨는 그에게 들어오라고 손짓했어.\n\n이 부름에 따라 서기는 높은 걸상에서 내려왔어. 수도 없이 오르내린 통에 반들반들 윤이 나게 된 걸상이었지. 그러고는 니클비 씨 방에 들어와 섰어. 그는 중년의 키 큰 사내였는데, 부리부리한 두 눈 가운데 하나는 아예 한곳에 박혀 움직이질 않았어. 코는 불그스름했고, 얼굴은 송장처럼 핏기가 없었지. 그리고 옷 한 벌을 걸치고 있었는데 — 도무지 몸에 맞질 않으니 '옷 한 벌'이라는 말이 어울리는지 모르겠지만 — 한참을 입어 후줄근했고, 너무 작았어. 게다가 단추가 어찌나 인색하게 달려 있던지, 그걸 어떻게 여미고 다니는지 신기할 지경이었지.\n\n\"지금이 열두 시 반이었나, 노그스?\" 니클비 씨가 날카롭고 거슬리는 목소리로 물었어.\n\n\"기껏해야 이십오 분 전인데요, 저—\" 노그스는 '술집 시계로'라고 덧붙이려다가, 정신을 차리고는 '정확한 시각으로'라고 바꿔 말했어.\n\n\"내 시계가 멈췄어.\" 니클비 씨가 말했어. \"왜 그런지 모르겠군.\"\n\n\"태엽을 안 감으셨겠죠.\" 노그스가 말했어.\n\n\"감았어.\" 니클비 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"그럼 너무 많이 감으셨네요.\" 노그스가 받아쳤어.\n\n\"그럴 리가 없는데.\" 니클비 씨가 한마디 했어.\n\n\"틀림없어요.\" 노그스가 말했어.\n\n\"그래!\" 니클비 씨는 시계를 도로 주머니에 넣으며 말했어. \"그럴 수도 있겠지.\"\n\n노그스는 묘한 소리로 끙 하고 내뱉었어. 주인과의 말다툼이 끝날 때마다 늘 하는 버릇이었지. 자기(노그스)가 이겼다는 뜻이었어. 그러고는 (누가 먼저 말을 걸지 않으면 좀처럼 입을 열지 않는 사람이라) 험상궂은 침묵에 빠져들었고, 두 손을 천천히 맞비볐지. 손가락 마디를 우두둑 꺾고, 온갖 모양으로 일그러뜨려 가면서 말이야. 무슨 일이 있을 때마다 이 짓을 끊임없이 되풀이하는 것, 그리고 멀쩡한 한쪽 눈에도 고정되고 뻣뻣한 눈빛을 입혀서 다른 쪽 눈과 똑같이 만들어 버리는 것 — 그래서 그가 대체 어디를, 무엇을 보고 있는지 아무도 알 수 없게 하는 것 — 이 두 가지가 노그스 씨의 수많은 별난 점 가운데서도, 처음 보는 사람의 눈에 대뜸 띄는 것들이었지.\n\n\"오늘 아침엔 런던 태번에 갈 거야.\" 니클비 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"공개 집회요?\" 노그스가 물었어.\n\n니클비 씨는 고개를 끄덕였어. \"러들의 그 저당 건으로 변호사한테서 편지가 오기로 돼 있어. 혹시라도 온다면, 두 시 배달로 여기 도착할 거야. 나는 그쯤 시내를 떠나서 길 왼편으로 채링 크로스까지 걸어갈 테니까. 편지가 오면 마중을 나와서 가지고 와.\"\n\n노그스가 고개를 끄덕였어. 그가 끄덕이는데, 사무실 종이 울렸지. 주인은 서류에서 눈을 들었고, 서기는 태연히 제자리에 그대로 서 있었어.\n\n\"종이에요.\" 노그스가 설명이라도 하듯 말했어. \"댁에 계신 걸로요?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"누구한테든요?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"세금 걷는 사람한테도요?\"\n\n\"아니! 그자는 다시 오라고 해.\"\n\n노그스는 늘 하던 대로 끙 소리를 냈어. '그럴 줄 알았다니까!' 하는 듯이 말이야. 그리고 종이 또 울리자 문으로 갔지. 거기서 곧 돌아오면서, '보니 씨'라는 이름으로 한 사람을 안내해 들였어. 몹시 서두르는 창백한 신사였지. 머리카락이 온통 헝클어진 채 사방으로 곤두서 있었고, 아주 가느다란 흰 넥타이를 목에 헐겁게 묶고 있었어. 밤중에 누가 깨운 뒤로 아직 옷도 제대로 못 챙겨 입은 사람 같아 보였지.\n\n\"이보게 니클비,\" 신사는 흰 모자를 벗으며 말했어. 모자 안에 서류가 어찌나 가득 들었던지 머리에 잘 얹히지도 않을 정도였지. \"한순간도 지체할 틈이 없네. 문 앞에 마차를 대 놨어. 매슈 퍼퍼 경이 의장을 맡고, 국회의원 셋이 틀림없이 오기로 했네. 둘은 내가 직접 침대에서 끌어내 챙겼어. 셋째는 밤새 크록퍼드에 있었는데, 방금 집에 가서 깨끗한 셔츠로 갈아입고 소다수를 한두 병 들이켜겠다고 했네. 집회에서 연설할 시간엔 틀림없이 와 있을 거야. 어젯밤 일로 좀 들떠 있긴 하지만, 그건 괜찮아. 그럴 때면 오히려 연설을 더 힘차게 하거든.\"\n\n\"제법 잘될 것 같군.\" 랄프 니클비 씨가 말했어. 그의 느긋한 태도는 또 다른 이 사업가의 활기참과는 영 딴판이었지.\n\n\"제법이라니!\" 보니 씨가 되받았어. \"이건 여태껏 나온 것 중에 가장 기막힌 발상이야. '연합 수도권 개량 따뜻한 머핀·크럼핏 제빵 및 정시 배달 회사. 자본금 오백만 파운드, 십 파운드짜리 주식 오십만 주.' 아, 이 이름만으로도 열흘이면 주식이 웃돈 붙어 나갈걸.\"\n\n\"그리고 웃돈이 붙으면,\" 랄프 니클비 씨가 웃으며 말했어.\n\n\"붙으면 말이지, 자네야 세상 누구 못지않게 그걸 어떻게 처리할지, 적당한 때 어떻게 조용히 발을 뺄지 잘 알지 않나.\" 보니 씨는 그 자본가의 어깨를 친근하게 툭 치며 말했어. \"그건 그렇고, 자네 저 서기는 참 별난 사람이군그래.\"\n\n\"그래, 가엾은 친구지!\" 랄프는 장갑을 끼며 대답했어. \"그래도 뉴먼 노그스가 한때는 말과 사냥개를 거느렸던 사람이라네.\"\n\n\"오, 그래?\" 상대는 무심하게 말했어.\n\n\"그렇다네.\" 랄프가 말을 이었어. \"그것도 그리 오래된 일도 아니야. 그런데 돈을 흥청망청 써 버리고, 아무 데나 마구 투자하고, 이자 물어 가며 돈을 빌리고, 한마디로 처음엔 제대로 바보짓을 하더니 결국엔 거지가 됐지. 술독에 빠졌다가 중풍기까지 왔고, 그러고는 여기로 일 파운드를 꾸러 왔어. 형편이 나았던 시절에 내가 그 사람하고—\"\n\n\"거래를 해 둔 사이였다는 거지.\" 보니 씨가 뜻있는 눈길로 말했어.\n\n\"바로 그렇지.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"그러니 내가 빌려줄 수가 없었어, 알겠나.\"\n\n\"오, 그야 당연하지.\"\n\n\"그런데 마침 그때 서기가 하나 필요했거든. 문도 열어 주고 그런 일을 할 사람 말이야. 그래서 자비를 베푸는 셈 치고 데려다 썼고, 그 뒤로 줄곧 나하고 있는 거지. 저 친구 머리가 좀 어떻게 된 것 같긴 해.\" 니클비 씨가 자비로운 표정을 지어 보이며 말했어. \"그래도 쓸모는 충분하다네, 가엾은 사람—쓸모는 충분해.\"\n\n이 마음씨 고운 신사는 한 가지를 덧붙이지 않았어. 뉴먼 노그스가 완전히 빈털터리라서, 열세 살짜리 소년한테 주는 보통 품삯보다도 적은 돈을 받고 그를 위해 일한다는 사실 말이야. 또 그 황급한 이야기 속에서 이 점도 빼먹었지. 노그스의 별난 과묵함 덕분에, 바깥에 새어 나가면 안 될 일이 많이 오가는 그런 곳에서 그가 특히 값진 사람이 되었다는 것 말이야. 어쨌든 상대 신사는 어서 가고 싶어 안달이 난 게 뻔했어. 그래서 둘이 곧장 삯마차에 황급히 올라탔으니, 어쩌면 니클비 씨가 그렇게 사소한 사정들을 깜빡 잊고 말 안 한 건지도 모르지.\n\n마차가 멈춰 설 무렵 비숍스게이트 거리 안쪽은 큰 소동이 벌어져 있었어. (바람 부는 날이었는데) 사내 대여섯이 종이를 잔뜩 짊어진 채 길을 가로질러 비척거리며 다니고 있었지. 거기엔 어마어마한 공고문이 적혀 있었어. 정확히 한 시에 공개 집회가 열린다는 거야. '연합 수도권 개량 따뜻한 머핀·크럼핏 제빵 및 정시 배달 회사', 자본금 오백만 파운드, 십 파운드짜리 주식 오십만 주를 위해 국회에 청원하는 게 옳은지를 의논하기 위해서 말이지. 그 금액들은 꽤 큼직하고 통통한 검은 숫자로 또박또박 적혀 있었어. 보니 씨는 팔꿈치로 헤치며 잰걸음으로 계단을 올라갔어. 올라가는 동안 층계참에 서서 길을 안내하던 종업원들한테서 굽실거리는 인사를 숱하게 받았지. 그리고 니클비 씨를 뒤에 거느린 채, 큰 공개 회의실 뒤편의 방들로 쑥 들어갔어. 그중 둘째 방에는 사무용으로 보이는 탁자가 하나, 그리고 사무용으로 보이는 사람 여럿이 있었지.\n\n\"옳소!\" 턱이 두 겹인 신사가 보니 씨가 모습을 드러내자 외쳤어. \"의장석으로, 여러분, 의장석으로!\"\n\n새로 온 두 사람은 모두의 갈채를 받으며 맞아들여졌어. 보니 씨는 부산스레 탁자 상석으로 올라가 모자를 벗고는, 손가락으로 머리를 쓸어 넘겼어. 그러고는 작은 망치로 탁자를 탕탕 두드렸지. 삯마차 마부가 두드리듯이 말이야. 그러자 여러 신사가 \"옳소!\" 하고 외치며 서로에게 가볍게 고개를 끄덕였어. 참으로 기개 있는 행동이라는 듯이 말이지. 바로 그때 종업원 하나가 흥분으로 안달이 나서는 방 안으로 뛰어들었어. 그리고 쾅 소리 나게 문을 열어젖히며 외쳤지. \"매슈 퍼퍼 경 드십니다!\"\n\n위원들은 자리에서 일어나 기뻐서 손뼉을 쳤어. 그들이 손뼉을 치는 사이, 매슈 퍼퍼 경이 들어왔지. 살아 있는 국회의원 둘을 거느리고 말이야. 하나는 아일랜드 사람, 하나는 스코틀랜드 사람이었어. 다들 싱글벙글 웃으며 고개를 숙였고, 어찌나 유쾌해 보이던지 누가 저들에게 반대표를 던질 모진 마음을 먹을 수 있을까 싶을 만큼 신기할 정도였지. 특히 매슈 퍼퍼 경은 작고 둥근 머리에 아마빛 가발을 얹고 있었는데, 어찌나 정신없이 고개를 숙여 대는지 매 순간 가발이 홱 벗겨질 것만 같았어. 이런 기색이 어느 정도 가라앉자, 매슈 퍼퍼 경이나 다른 두 의원과 말을 트고 지내는 신사들이 세 무리로 갈라져 그들을 둘러쌌어. 그리고 그 어느 무리 곁에든, 매슈 퍼퍼 경이나 다른 두 의원과 말을 트지 못한 신사들이 서성거리며 머물러 있었지. 웃음 띤 얼굴로 두 손을 비비면서, 뭔가 일이 생겨 자기들도 눈에 띄게 되기를 간절히 바라면서 말이야. 그러는 내내 매슈 퍼퍼 경과 다른 두 의원은 저마다 자기 무리에게 정부의 속셈을 들려주고 있었어. 정부가 이 법안을 다룰 의향이 어떻다느니 하면서 말이지. 지난번 정부와 함께 식사할 때 정부가 귓속말로 뭐라고 했는지, 그 말을 하면서 정부가 어떻게 눈을 찡긋하는 게 보였는지까지 소상히 늘어놓았어. 그런 전제에서 그들은 어렵잖게 결론을 끌어냈지. 정부가 다른 무엇보다 마음 깊이 품은 목표가 하나 있다면, 그건 바로 '연합 수도권 개량 따뜻한 머핀·크럼핏 제빵 및 정시 배달 회사'의 번영과 이익이라고 말이야.\n\n그러는 동안, 진행 순서를 정하고 연설을 공평하게 나누는 일이 마무리되기를 기다리며, 큰 회의실의 사람들은 번갈아 가며 텅 빈 단상과 음악 발코니의 부인들을 쳐다보고 있었어. 그들 대부분이 이미 두 시간 전부터 이런 구경거리로 시간을 보내고 있었지. 그런데 아무리 흥겨운 소일거리도 너무 오래 끌면 입맛이 떨어지는 법이라, 성미 급한 축들이 이제 구두 뒤축으로 바닥을 쿵쿵 치기 시작했어. 그리고 온갖 야유와 고함으로 못마땅함을 드러냈지. 가장 오래 있던 사람들 입에서 터져 나온 이 목청 소리는, 당연히 단상에 가장 가깝고 경비를 서는 경관들로부터는 가장 먼 자리에서 나왔어. 그 경관들은 군중을 헤치고 들어갈 마음은 별로 없으면서도, 그래도 이 소란을 가라앉히려고 뭐라도 해 보겠다는 갸륵한 마음만은 있었지. 그래서 곧장 문가에 있던 얌전한 사람들을 죄다 옷자락이며 멱살을 잡아 끌어냈어. 동시에 곤봉으로 따끔하고 얼얼한 매를 여기저기 갈겼지. 저 재주꾼 배우 펀치 씨의 방식대로 말이야. 무기의 생김새며 그 쓰는 법에 있어서, 이 행정 분야는 이따금 펀치 씨의 빛나는 본보기를 따라 하거든.\n\n아주 흥미진진한 몸싸움 몇 판이 벌어지고 있을 때였어. 우렁찬 함성이 울려 싸우던 사람들조차 정신이 그쪽으로 쏠렸지. 그러더니 옆문에서 신사들이 한 줄로 길게 단상으로 쏟아져 들어왔어. 다들 모자를 벗어 들고, 죄다 뒤를 돌아보며 목청껏 환호를 지르고 있었지. 그 까닭은 곧 충분히 밝혀졌어. 매슈 퍼퍼 경과 다른 진짜 국회의원 둘이 귀가 멍멍한 함성 속에서 앞으로 나왔거든. 그리고 자기들 공직 생활을 통틀어 이토록 영광스러운 광경은 본 적이 없다고, 서로에게 말없는 몸짓으로 증언했지.\n\n마침내, 정말 마침내 사람들이 함성을 그쳤어. 그런데 매슈 퍼퍼 경이 의장으로 추대되자, 다시 도지기를 했고 그게 오 분이나 이어졌지. 이게 끝나자 매슈 퍼퍼 경이 말을 이어 갔어. 이 위대한 자리에서 자기 심정이 어떠할지, 이 자리가 세상 사람들 눈에 어떠할지, 자기 앞에 모인 동포들의 식견이 어떠할지, 자기 뒤에 선 존경하는 벗들의 재산과 명망이 어떠할지, 그리고 마지막으로, '연합 수도권 개량 따뜻한 머핀·크럼핏 제빵 및 정시 배달 회사' 같은 기관이 자유롭고 위대한 국민의 부와 행복과 안락과 자유와, 그 존립 자체에 얼마나 중요할지를 말이야!\n\n그러고 나서 보니 씨가 첫 번째 결의안을 발의하러 나섰어. 오른손으로 머리를 한 번 쓸어 넘기고, 왼손은 느긋하게 옆구리에 척 갖다 댄 다음, 자기 모자는 턱이 두 겹인 신사한테 맡겼지. (그 신사는 대체로 연사들의 시중꾼 노릇을 하고 있었어.) 그러고는 첫 번째 결의안을 읽어 주겠다고 했어. \"본 집회는 이 수도와 그 인근의 머핀 업계의 현 상태를 경악과 우려로써 바라보는 바이며, 현재의 머핀 소년들이 공중의 신뢰를 받을 자격이 전혀 없다고 여기는 바이며, 머핀 제도 전반이 국민의 건강과 도덕에 똑같이 해로우며, 위대한 상업·무역 공동체의 최선의 이익을 뒤엎는 것이라고 단정하는 바이다.\" 이 존경하는 신사는 연설을 했는데, 부인들 눈에서 눈물을 자아냈고 그 자리에 있는 한 사람 한 사람의 가슴에 더없이 생생한 감정을 불러일으켰지. 그는 런던 곳곳 동네의 가난한 이들의 집을 찾아가 봤다고 했어. 그런데 머핀의 흔적이라곤 티끌만큼도 없더라는 거야. 거기엔 이런 가난한 이들 중 더러는 한 해가 다 가도록 머핀 맛을 못 본다고 믿을 만한 까닭이 너무도 많아 보였지. 그는 머핀 장수들 사이에 술주정과 방탕과 난봉이 판친다는 걸 알아냈어. 그리고 그걸 지금처럼 영위되는 그들 일의 천한 성격 탓으로 돌렸지. 그는 머핀을 소비해야 마땅할 가난한 계층 사람들 사이에서도 똑같은 악덕을 발견했어. 그리고 이건 그들이 그 영양가 있는 식품을 손에 넣을 수 없는 처지에 놓여 생긴 절망 탓으로 돌렸지. 그 절망이 그들을 몰아붙여, 독한 술에서 거짓 자극을 찾게 만든다는 거야. 그는 하원 위원회 앞에서 증명해 보이겠다고 했어. 머핀 값을 높게 유지하고 종 치는 장수들에게 독점권을 주려는 담합이 있다는 걸 말이야. 종 치는 장수들을 하원의 증인석에 세워 그걸 증명하겠다고 했지. 또 이런 자들이 '스눅스', '워커', '퍼거슨', '머피는 제대로 하고 있나?' 같은 은밀한 암호와 신호로 서로 연락을 주고받는다는 것도 증명하겠다고 했어. 그밖에도 많다고 했지. 회사가 바로잡으려는 게 바로 이 서글픈 작태라는 거야. 첫째로, 온갖 종류의 모든 사적인 머핀 거래를 무거운 벌로써 금지함으로써, 둘째로, 회사가 직접 일반 대중에게, 또 가난한 이들에게는 그 집까지 찾아가, 최상급 머핀을 싼값에 공급함으로써 말이지. 바로 이 목적으로, 애국적인 의장 매슈 퍼퍼 경이 국회에 법안을 하나 제출해 두었다고 했어. 그들이 지지하려고 모인 게 바로 이 법안이라는 거야. 이 법안을 지지하는 이들이야말로 영국에 길이 빛날 광채와 영광을 안겨 줄 사람들이며, 그 이름은 '연합 수도권 개량 따뜻한 머핀·크럼핏 제빵 및 정시 배달 회사'라고 했지. 거기에 그는 덧붙였어. 자본금 오백만 파운드, 십 파운드짜리 주식 오십만 주라고 말이야.\n\n랄프 니클비 씨가 그 결의안을 재청했어. 그리고 다른 신사 하나가, '머핀'이라는 말이 나올 때마다 그 뒤에 '및 크럼핏'이라는 말을 끼워 넣어 수정하자고 발의하자, 결의안은 의기양양하게 통과되었지. 군중 가운데 단 한 사람만이 \"반대!\"라고 외쳤는데, 그자는 즉각 붙들려 곧장 끌려 나갔어.\n\n두 번째 결의안은, '모든 머핀(또는 크럼핏) 장수, 그 종류를 막론한 모든 머핀(또는 크럼핏) 거래상, 남자든 여자든 소년이든 어른이든, 손 종을 울리든 안 울리든'을 즉각 폐지하는 게 마땅하다고 인정하는 내용이었어. 이걸 발의한 건 반쯤 성직자 같은 행색의, 비통해 보이는 신사였지. 그는 대뜸 어찌나 깊은 비애로 들어갔던지, 첫 번째 연사를 순식간에 완전히 무색하게 만들어 버렸어. 핀 하나 떨어지는 소리도 들렸을 거야 — 핀이라니! 깃털 하나 떨어지는 소리도 말이지 — 그가 머핀 소년들이 주인한테 당하는 학대를 묘사하는 동안 말이야. 그는 아주 현명하게도, 그 학대 자체만으로도 그 더없이 값진 회사를 세울 충분한 이유가 된다고 역설했지. 그 불쌍한 소년들은 한 해 중 가장 모진 철에도 밤마다 축축한 거리로 내쫓긴다는 거야. 어둠과 빗속을 — 어쩌면 우박이나 눈 속을 — 몇 시간씩 헤매고 다닌다는 거지. 비바람을 막을 곳도, 먹을 것도, 따뜻함도 없이 말이야. 그리고 마지막 점에 대해서는 대중이 절대 잊어선 안 된다고 했어. 머핀에는 따뜻한 보온재와 담요가 마련되어 있는데, 정작 소년들에게는 아무것도 마련되어 있지 않아, 제 한 몸 가엾은 처지에 내맡겨진다는 거야. (수치스럽다!) 이 존경하는 신사는 한 머핀 소년의 사례를 들려주었어. 무려 오 년 동안이나 이 비인간적이고 야만적인 제도에 시달린 끝에, 마침내 머리 감기에 걸려 쓰러진 소년이었지. 그 병 아래 점점 가라앉다가, 그만 땀을 흘리고는 회복되었다는 거야. 이건 자기가 직접 보증할 수 있다고 했어. 하지만 더 가슴 찢어지고 소름 끼치는 일도 들었다고 했지. (그리고 그 사실을 의심할 까닭이 없다고 했어.) 그는 한 고아 머핀 소년의 사례를 들었다고 했어. 삯마차에 치여 병원으로 실려 갔고, 무릎 아래로 다리를 잘라 내는 수술을 받았으며, 지금은 실제로 목발을 짚고 그 일을 계속하고 있다는 거야. 정의의 샘이여, 이런 일들이 계속되어야 한단 말인가!\n\n집회의 마음을 사로잡은 게 바로 이 대목이었어. 그리고 사람들의 동정심을 끌어내는 게 바로 이런 연설 투였지. 사내들은 고함을 질렀어. 부인들은 손수건이 축축해질 때까지 거기에 눈물을 쏟더니, 마를 때까지 그걸 흔들어 댔지. 흥분이 어마어마했어. 그리고 니클비 씨는 친구에게 귓속말로, 이제부터 주식이 이십오 퍼센트 웃돈이 붙었다고 말했지.\n\n결의안은 물론 우렁찬 갈채 속에 통과되었어. 모두가 두 손을 번쩍 들어 찬성했지. 어찌나 열성이던지, 편하게 할 수만 있었다면 두 다리까지 들어 올렸을 거야. 이게 끝나자, 제출하기로 한 청원서 초안을 처음부터 끝까지 읽어 내려갔어. 그 청원서는, 모든 청원서가 으레 그러듯, 청원하는 자들은 더없이 비천하고, 청원받는 분들은 더없이 존귀하며, 그 목적은 더없이 갸륵하다고 했지. 그러므로 (청원서가 이르기를) 이 법안은 즉각 법으로 통과되어야 마땅하며, 그래야 국회에 모인 더없이 존귀하고 영광스러운 영국 하원의 영예와 영광이 영원하리라는 거야.\n\n그러고 나서, 밤새 크록퍼드에 있었던 그 신사가 — 그 탓에 눈언저리가 좀 안 좋아 보였지 — 앞으로 나와 동포들에게 말했어. 이 청원서가 제출되기만 하면 자기가 그걸 지지하는 연설을 어떻게 할 작정인지, 만약 국회가 이 법안을 부결한다면 자기가 얼마나 무섭게 국회를 비꼴 작정인지를 말이지. 그리고 또 알려 주었어. 자기 존경하는 벗들이, 모든 계층의 국민에게 머핀과 크럼핏 구매를 의무로 정하는 조항을 끼워 넣지 않은 게 못내 아쉽다고 말이야. 그는 — 어떤 어중간한 방책도 반대하고, 끝까지 밀어붙이는 걸 좋아하는 사람이라 — 위원회에서 그 조항을 발의해 표결에 부치겠다고 다짐했지. 이 결심을 밝히고 나서, 이 존경하는 신사는 익살스러워졌어. 그리고 특허받은 부츠와 레몬빛 새끼염소 가죽 장갑과 모피 외투 깃은 농담에 큰 보탬이 되는 법이라, 엄청난 웃음과 많은 환호가 터졌지. 게다가 부인들의 손수건이 어찌나 눈부시게 펄럭였던지, 아까 그 비통한 신사가 완전히 빛을 잃을 정도였어.\n\n그리고 청원서를 다 읽고 막 채택하려 할 때, 아일랜드 의원이 앞으로 나왔어. (열정적인 기질의 젊은 신사였지.) 오직 아일랜드 의원만이 할 수 있는 그런 연설을 가지고서 말이야. 시의 참된 혼과 정신이 숨 쉬는 연설이었고, 어찌나 열정적으로 쏟아 내던지 그를 보고만 있어도 몸이 뜨거워질 정도였지. 그 연설 가운데 그는 사람들에게 말했어. 자기는 이 위대한 은혜를 자기 고국에까지 넓혀 달라고 요구하겠노라고. 다른 모든 법에서와 마찬가지로 머핀 법에서도 고국을 위해 동등한 권리를 주장하겠노라고. 그리고 자기는 그날이 오기를 여전히 바란다고 했지. 고국의 누추한 오두막에서도 크럼핏이 구워지고, 풍요로운 푸른 골짜기마다 머핀 종이 울리는 그날을 말이야. 그 뒤를 이어 스코틀랜드 의원이 나왔어. 예상되는 수익 규모에 관해 이런저런 유쾌한 언급을 곁들여서, 시가 불러일으킨 좋은 기분을 한층 돋우었지. 그리고 모든 연설이 한데 어우러져, 의도했던 바를 정확히 이루어 냈어. 듣는 이들의 마음에, '연합 수도권 개량 따뜻한 머핀·크럼핏 제빵 및 정시 배달 회사'만큼 전망 밝고 동시에 칭송할 만한 투자처는 없다는 확신을 심어 준 거야.\n\n그렇게 해서 법안을 지지하는 청원이 합의되었고, 집회는 갈채 속에 끝이 났어. 그리고 니클비 씨와 다른 이사들은 점심을 먹으러 사무소로 갔지. 매일 한 시 반이면 으레 그러듯이 말이야. 그리고 이 수고에 대한 보수로 (회사가 아직 걸음마 단계라) 그들은 그런 출석 한 번에 한 사람당 고작 삼 기니씩만 청구했단다.\n\n# CHAPTER 3\n\n랄프 니클비 씨는 점심을 해치우는 데 열성껏 손을 보탰어. 사업하는 사람들이 갖춰야 할 가장 중요한 자질 가운데 하나인 그 재빠르고 기운찬 솜씨로 말이야. 그러고는 함께 투기하던 동료들과 정답게 작별 인사를 나누고, 평소답지 않게 기분 좋은 얼굴로 서쪽을 향해 발걸음을 옮겼어. 세인트폴 성당을 지날 때 그는 문간 옆으로 비켜서서 시계를 맞추려 했어. 손은 태엽에 얹고 눈은 성당 시계판에 둔 채로 그 일에 열중하고 있는데, 갑자기 한 남자가 그의 앞을 가로막았지. 뉴먼 노그스였어.\n\n\"아! 뉴먼.\" 니클비 씨가 하던 일을 계속하며 고개를 들고 말했어. \"저당 잡힌 일로 편지가 온 게로군, 그렇지? 그럴 줄 알았어.\"\n\n\"틀렸어요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어.\n\n\"뭐라고! 그 일로 찾아온 사람도 없고?\" 니클비 씨가 손을 멈추고 물었어. 노그스가 고개를 저었어.\n\n\"그럼 대체 뭐가 왔다는 거야?\" 니클비 씨가 물었어.\n\n\"제가 왔지요.\" 뉴먼이 말했어.\n\n\"그밖에는?\" 주인이 험악하게 다그쳤어.\n\n\"이거요.\" 뉴먼이 봉인된 편지 한 통을 주머니에서 천천히 꺼내며 말했어. \"소인은 스트랜드, 검은 봉랍, 검은 테두리, 여자 글씨, 한쪽 구석에 C. N.\"\n\n\"검은 봉랍이라고?\" 니클비 씨가 편지를 흘끗 보며 말했어. \"이 글씨도 어쩐지 낯이 익은데. 뉴먼, 내 동생이 죽었다 해도 나는 별로 놀라지 않을 것 같군.\"\n\n\"그러실 것 같지 않네요.\" 뉴먼이 조용히 말했어.\n\n\"왜 안 그렇다는 거야?\" 니클비 씨가 따져 물었어.\n\n\"주인님은 결코 놀라는 법이 없으니까요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어. \"그것뿐입니다.\"\n\n니클비 씨는 조수에게서 편지를 낚아챘어. 그러고는 그에게 차가운 눈길을 한 번 박아 넣고는, 봉투를 뜯어 읽고 주머니에 집어넣었지. 이제 시간을 일 초까지 정확히 맞춘 다음, 시계태엽을 감기 시작했어.\n\n\"내가 예상한 대로야, 뉴먼.\" 니클비 씨가 그 일을 하면서 말했어. \"동생이 죽었어. 저런! 그것참, 갑작스러운 일이군. 정말이지, 생각도 못 했는데.\" 이렇게 가슴 아픈 슬픔의 말을 늘어놓고는, 니클비 씨는 시계를 다시 회중 주머니에 넣었어. 그리고 장갑을 한 치 어긋남 없이 끼고는 가던 길로 몸을 돌려, 두 손을 등 뒤로 한 채 천천히 서쪽으로 걸어갔지.\n\n\"애들은 살아 있고요?\" 노그스가 그에게 다가서며 물었어.\n\n\"이런, 바로 그게 문제야.\" 마치 그 순간 그 애들 생각을 하고 있었다는 듯이 니클비 씨가 대답했어. \"둘 다 살아 있어.\"\n\n\"둘 다요!\" 뉴먼 노그스가 낮은 목소리로 되뇌었어.\n\n\"게다가 과부까지.\" 니클비 씨가 덧붙였어. \"셋 다 런던에 있어, 빌어먹을. 셋 다 여기 있다고, 뉴먼.\"\n\n뉴먼은 주인보다 조금 뒤로 처졌어. 그의 얼굴이 경련이라도 인 것처럼 묘하게 일그러졌지. 하지만 그게 마비 때문인지, 슬픔 때문인지, 아니면 속으로 터지는 웃음 때문인지는 그 자신 말고는 아무도 알 수 없었어. 사람의 얼굴 표정은 보통 그 속마음을 짐작하게 해 주거나 말뜻을 풀어 주는 실마리가 되잖아. 그런데 뉴먼 노그스의 평소 얼굴은 아무리 머리를 굴려도 풀 수 없는 수수께끼였어.\n\n\"집에 가!\" 몇 걸음 걷고 나서 니클비 씨가 말했어. 마치 제 개를 보듯 조수를 돌아보면서 말이야. 이 말이 떨어지기가 무섭게 뉴먼은 길을 가로질러 쏜살같이 달려가, 인파 속으로 슬그머니 끼어들더니 순식간에 사라져 버렸어.\n\n\"하긴, 당연한 일이지!\" 니클비 씨가 걸어가며 혼자 중얼거렸어. \"지극히 당연한 일이고말고! 동생은 나한테 해 준 게 하나도 없었고, 나도 바라지 않았어. 그런데 숨이 끊어지기가 무섭게, 떡 벌어진 건강한 여편네 하나에 다 큰 아들놈에 딸년까지 떠받쳐 줄 사람으로 나를 쳐다본다 이거지. 그것들이 나하고 무슨 상관이람! 얼굴 한번 본 적도 없는데.\"\n\n이런 생각과 그밖에 비슷한 여러 생각을 가득 품은 채, 니클비 씨는 부지런히 스트랜드로 향했어. 그리고 찾는 집 번지수를 확인하려는 듯 편지를 들여다보며, 그 붐비는 큰길을 절반쯤 내려간 곳에 있는 어느 집 개인 출입문 앞에 멈춰 섰지.\n\n거기엔 세밀화 화가가 살았어. 길거리 쪽 문에 커다란 금박 액자가 나사로 박혀 있었거든. 그 안에는 검은 벨벳 바탕 위에 해군 정복 두 벌이 초상화로 걸려 있었어. 정복 위로 얼굴들이 빼꼼 내다보고 있었고, 망원경도 딸려 있었지. 하나는 새빨간 군복을 입은 젊은 신사가 군도를 휘두르는 그림이고, 또 하나는 이마가 훤한 문인 하나가 펜과 잉크, 책 여섯 권, 그리고 커튼과 함께 그려진 그림이었어. 그밖에도 가슴 뭉클한 그림이 하나 있었어. 끝 모를 깊은 숲속에서 원고를 읽고 있는 젊은 아가씨 그림이었지. 또 머리가 큰 작은 사내아이가 의자에 앉아 있는 매력적인 전신상도 있었어. 두 다리는 단축법으로 그려져 소금 숟가락만 한 크기로 줄어들어 있었지. 이런 예술 작품들 말고도, 파란 하늘과 갈색 하늘을 배경으로 서로를 향해 히죽거리는 노부인과 노신사의 얼굴 그림이 잔뜩 있었어. 그리고 양각 테두리를 두르고 우아한 글씨로 적은 요금표도 한 장 걸려 있었지.\n\n니클비 씨는 이 시시한 것들을 잔뜩 깔보는 눈으로 흘끗 보고는, 노크를 두 번 했어. 그걸 세 번이나 되풀이하고 나서야, 유난히 더러운 얼굴을 한 하녀가 문을 열어 주었지.\n\n\"니클비 부인 집에 계시냐, 얘?\" 랄프가 날카롭게 물었어.\n\n\"여긴 니클비란 이름 안 살아요.\" 하녀가 말했어. \"라 크리비 말씀이시겠죠.\"\n\n니클비 씨는 이렇게 정정을 당하자 하녀에게 몹시 못마땅한 눈길을 보냈어. 그러고는 그게 무슨 소리냐고 잔뜩 가시 돋친 말투로 따졌지. 하녀가 막 설명을 하려는데, 복도 끝의 가파른 계단 쪽에서 여자 목소리가 들려와 누구를 찾느냐고 물었어.\n\n\"니클비 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"그 사람은 삼층이야, 해나.\" 같은 목소리가 말했어. \"넌 어쩜 그렇게 멍청하니! 삼층에 사람 있니?\"\n\n\"방금 누가 나가긴 했는데, 그건 위층 다락방 사람이 세수를 하고 나간 것 같은데요.\" 하녀가 대답했어.\n\n\"가서 살펴보는 게 좋겠다.\" 모습이 보이지 않는 여자가 말했어. \"이 신사분께 종이 어디 있는지 알려 드리고, 삼층 손님은 두 번 노크하면 안 된다고 말씀드려라. 나는 종이 고장 났을 때 말고는 노크를 허락하지 않아. 그때도 한 번씩 두 번만 쳐야 하고.\"\n\n\"여보세요.\" 랄프가 더 이상 잔말 없이 안으로 걸어 들어가며 말했어. \"실례합니다만, 라 뭐라고 하셨던 그분이십니까?\"\n\n\"크리비, 라 크리비예요.\" 노란 머리 장식이 난간 위로 까딱 모습을 드러내며 목소리가 대답했어.\n\n\"괜찮으시다면 잠깐 말씀 좀 드리고 싶은데요, 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n목소리는 신사분더러 위로 올라오시라고 대답했어. 하지만 그가 말이 끝나기도 전에 이미 올라가 있었지. 그가 이 층으로 들어서자, 노란 머리 장식을 한 여자가 그를 맞았어. 머리 장식에 어울리는 가운을 입었고, 그 여자 자신도 거의 같은 색이었지. 라 크리비 양은 쉰 살 먹은, 점잔을 빼는 아가씨였어. 그리고 라 크리비 양의 방은 아래층 그 금박 액자를 더 큼직하게, 그리고 좀 더 지저분하게 키워 놓은 것 같았지.\n\n\"에헴!\" 라 크리비 양이 검은 비단 손싸개 뒤로 우아하게 기침을 하며 말했어. \"세밀화를 그리러 오셨나 보군요. 그 용도로는 아주 인상이 또렷한 얼굴이세요, 선생님. 전에 앉아 보신 적 있나요?\"\n\n\"제 용건을 잘못 짚으신 모양이군요, 부인.\" 니클비 씨가 평소의 무뚝뚝한 투로 대답했어. \"저는 세밀화 따위에 내다 버릴 돈도 없고, 설령 있다 한들 (고맙게도) 그걸 줄 사람도 없습니다. 계단에서 부인을 뵙고, 여기 사는 어떤 세입자에 관해 한 가지 여쭤보고 싶었던 겁니다.\"\n\n라 크리비 양은 다시 한 번 기침을 했어. 이 기침은 실망감을 감추려는 것이었지. 그러고는 \"아, 그러세요!\" 하고 말했어.\n\n\"하녀에게 하시는 말씀을 들으니, 위층은 부인 거더군요.\" 니클비 씨가 말했어.\n\n그래요, 맞아요, 라고 라 크리비 양이 대답했어. 이 집 위층은 다 자기 거고, 마침 지금은 삼층 방이 필요 없어서 세를 놓는 습관이 있다고. 사실 지금 거기엔 시골에서 올라온 부인 하나와 두 아이가 살고 있다고 했어.\n\n\"과부입니까, 부인?\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"네, 과부예요.\" 그 여자가 대답했어.\n\n\"가난한 과부 말이지요, 부인?\" 랄프가 그 짧은 형용사에 힘껏 무게를 실어 말했어. 그 한마디에 어찌나 많은 뜻이 담기던지.\n\n\"글쎄요, 가난하긴 한 것 같아요.\" 라 크리비 양이 받아 말했어.\n\n\"제가 가난하다는 걸 잘 알고 있습니다, 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그런데, 가난한 과부가 이런 집에 무슨 볼일이 있겠습니까, 부인?\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀이에요.\" 자기 방을 은근히 칭찬하는 말로 들렸는지, 라 크리비 양이 조금도 기분 나쁘지 않은 듯 대답했어. \"지당하신 말씀이고말고요.\"\n\n\"저는 그 댁 형편을 속속들이 알고 있습니다, 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"실은 제가 그 집안의 친척이거든요. 그러니 부인, 그들을 여기 두지 않으시는 게 좋을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"혹시라도 돈 문제를 감당하지 못할 사정이 생긴다면,\" 라 크리비 양이 또 한 번 기침을 하며 말했어. \"그 부인 친척 되시는 분들이—\"\n\n\"아니요, 안 그럴 겁니다, 부인.\" 랄프가 황급히 끼어들었어. \"그런 기대는 마세요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"사정이 아주 달라지네요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 알아 두시면 됩니다, 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그리고 거기에 맞춰 준비하시는 거지요. 제가 그 집안입니다, 부인. 적어도 제가 그들의 유일한 친척인 것 같으니까요. 그러니 분명히 말씀드려야겠는데, 저는 그들의 사치를 떠받쳐 줄 생각이 없습니다. 그들이 이 방을 얼마 동안 빌리기로 했습니까?\"\n\n\"그저 일주일 단위로요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대답했어. \"니클비 부인이 첫 주 방세는 미리 냈고요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 주가 끝나면 내보내시는 게 좋겠습니다.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그들은 시골로 돌아가는 게 상책이에요, 부인. 여기서는 누구한테나 거치적거리는 짐일 뿐이니까요.\"\n\n\"물론이지요.\" 라 크리비 양이 두 손을 비비며 말했어. \"니클비 부인이 방세 낼 형편도 못 되면서 방을 얻었다면, 그건 숙녀로서 아주 꼴사나운 짓이고말고요.\"\n\n\"당연히 그렇지요, 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"그리고 당연한 일이지만,\" 라 크리비 양이 말을 이었어. \"지금, 에헴, 의지할 데 없는 여자인 제가 이 방으로 손해를 볼 수는 없는 노릇이니까요.\"\n\n\"물론 그러실 수 없지요, 부인.\" 랄프가 대답했어.\n\n\"그렇다고 해도,\" 자기 착한 심성과 잇속 사이에서 갈팡질팡하는 게 빤히 보이는 라 크리비 양이 덧붙였어. \"그 부인을 두고 나쁘게 할 말은 하나도 없어요. 무척 상냥하고 싹싹한 분인데, 가엾게도 기운이 영 없어 보이긴 하더라고요. 그 댁 젊은이들도 마찬가지예요. 그보다 더 착하고 예의 바른 젊은이들은 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"잘 알겠습니다, 부인.\" 가난을 이렇게 추켜세우는 소리가 신경에 거슬려, 랄프가 문 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했어. \"저는 제 도리를 다했고, 어쩌면 도리 이상을 한 셈이지요. 물론 제가 이런 말을 했다고 고마워할 사람은 아무도 없겠지만요.\"\n\n\"적어도 저는 정말 감사하게 생각해요, 선생님.\" 라 크리비 양이 상냥하게 말했어. \"제 초상화 작품 몇 점을 봐 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"고맙습니다만, 부인.\" 니클비 씨가 부리나케 자리를 뜨며 말했어. \"위층에 들를 데가 있고, 제 시간이 귀해서요. 정말이지 안 되겠습니다.\"\n\n\"다음에 지나시는 길에 언제든, 저는 더없이 기쁠 거예요.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"요금표 한 장 가져가시는 친절을 베풀어 주시겠어요? 고맙습니다. 안녕히 가세요!\"\n\n\"안녕히 계세요, 부인.\" 랄프가 더 이상의 대화를 막으려고 등 뒤로 문을 휙 닫으며 말했어. \"이제 제수씨 차례로군. 흥!\"\n\n또 다른 가파른 계단을 올라갔어. 기계처럼 교묘하게 짜 맞춰져 죄다 모퉁이 계단뿐인 그런 층계였지. 랄프 니클비 씨는 층계참에서 숨을 돌리려고 멈춰 섰어. 그때 하녀가 그를 따라잡았지. 라 크리비 양이 친절을 베풀어 그가 왔다고 알리라며 보낸 거였어. 하녀는 아까 만난 뒤로 더러운 얼굴을 닦으려고 여러 번 애쓴 모양이었지만, 다 헛수고였지. 얼굴보다 훨씬 더 더러운 앞치마에 닦았으니 말이야.\n\n\"성함이 어떻게 되세요?\" 하녀가 물었어.\n\n\"니클비.\" 랄프가 대답했어.\n\n\"오! 니클비 부인.\" 하녀가 문을 활짝 열어젖히며 말했어. \"여기 니클비 씨 오셨어요.\"\n\n랄프 니클비 씨가 들어서자, 짙은 상복을 입은 부인이 일어섰어. 하지만 그를 맞으러 나설 기력은 없는 것 같았어. 그래서 곁에 앉아 있던 가냘프지만 무척 아름다운, 열일곱 살쯤 된 아가씨의 팔에 기댔지. 한두 살쯤 위로 보이는 청년 하나가 앞으로 나서더니, 랄프에게 삼촌이라며 인사를 했어.\n\n\"오,\" 랄프가 험상궂게 인상을 쓰며 으르렁거렸어. \"네가 니콜라스인 게로구나?\"\n\n\"제 이름이 맞습니다.\" 청년이 대답했어.\n\n\"내 모자 좀 받아 둬라.\" 랄프가 거만하게 말했어. \"자, 제수씨, 어떻게 지내시오? 슬픔은 꿋꿋이 견뎌야 합니다, 제수씨. 나는 늘 그렇게 하지요.\"\n\n\"제 슬픔은 흔한 슬픔이 아니에요!\" 니클비 부인이 손수건을 눈에 갖다 대며 말했어.\n\n\"흔치 않은 슬픔도 아니지요, 제수씨.\" 랄프가 스펜서 외투의 단추를 차갑게 풀며 대꾸했어. \"남편은 날마다 죽고, 아내도 날마다 죽으니까요, 제수씨.\"\n\n\"형제도 마찬가지지요.\" 니콜라스가 분개한 눈길을 던지며 말했어.\n\n\"그렇지, 강아지나 발바리도 날마다 죽고말고.\" 삼촌이 의자에 앉으며 대꾸했어. \"제수씨 편지엔 내 동생이 무슨 병으로 갔는지 적혀 있지 않더군요.\"\n\n\"의사들도 딱히 무슨 병이라고 짚어 내질 못했어요.\" 니클비 부인이 눈물을 흘리며 말했어. \"상심해서 가슴이 무너져 돌아가신 거라고 볼 수밖에 없을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"쳇!\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그런 게 어디 있소. 목이 부러져 죽는다거나, 팔이 부러진다거나, 머리가 깨진다거나, 다리가 부러진다거나, 코가 부러진다거나 하는 건 이해가 가지요. 그런데 가슴이 무너져 죽는다니! 말도 안 되는 소리, 그건 요즘 유행하는 헛소리에 지나지 않아요. 빚을 못 갚으면 가슴이 무너져 죽었다고 하고, 그 과부는 순교자가 되는 거지요.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔, 무너질 가슴이 없는 사람도 있는 것 같은데요.\" 니콜라스가 조용히 한마디 했어.\n\n\"이 녀석은 도대체 몇 살이오?\" 랄프가 의자를 뒤로 홱 돌리고는, 조카를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 잔뜩 깔보는 눈으로 훑어보며 물었어.\n\n\"니콜라스는 곧 열아홉이 돼요.\" 과부가 대답했어.\n\n\"열아홉이라, 흠!\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그래, 넌 밥벌이로 뭘 할 작정이냐?\"\n\n\"어머니께 얹혀살지는 않을 겁니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. 말하는 동안 가슴이 부풀어 올랐지.\n\n\"네 어미한테 얹혀산댔자, 빌어먹을 게 별로 없을 텐데.\" 삼촌이 그를 깔보는 눈으로 보며 쏘아붙였어.\n\n\"그게 뭐든,\" 니콜라스가 화가 나서 얼굴이 벌게져서 말했어. \"그걸 더 보태 달라고 삼촌을 쳐다보지는 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"니콜라스, 얘야, 정신 차리렴.\" 니클비 부인이 타일렀어.\n\n\"니콜라스 오빠, 제발.\" 아가씨도 다급히 말렸어.\n\n\"입 다물어, 이 녀석아.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"원, 별일이군! 시작이 아주 그럴듯하구려, 제수씨, 시작이 아주 그럴듯해!\"\n\n니클비 부인은 다른 대꾸 없이, 그저 니콜라스에게 조용히 하라고 손짓으로 애원할 뿐이었어. 그리고 삼촌과 조카는 몇 초 동안 말없이 서로를 마주 보았지. 노인의 얼굴은 험악하고 모질고 사나웠어. 청년의 얼굴은 환하고 잘생기고 꾸밈없었지. 노인의 눈은 탐욕과 교활함으로 번득이며 날카로웠고, 청년의 눈은 지성과 패기의 빛으로 빛났어. 그의 몸집은 다소 가냘팠지만 사내답고 균형이 잡혀 있었어. 그리고 젊음과 준수함이 주는 온갖 멋스러움은 차치하더라도, 그의 눈빛과 몸가짐에는 따뜻한 젊은 가슴에서 우러나는 무언가가 있어서, 그게 노인을 주눅 들게 했지.\n\n이런 대비가 구경하는 사람들 눈엔 아무리 두드러지게 보인다 해도, 그걸 가장 날카롭고 뼈저리게 느끼는 건 바로 그 대비로 자기 못남이 드러나는 사람 자신이야. 그 누구도 그만큼 절절하게 느끼지는 못하지. 그건 랄프의 가슴속 깊은 곳을 쓰라리게 후벼 팠어. 그리고 그는 그 순간부터 니콜라스를 미워했지.\n\n이 서로의 살핌은 마침내 끝이 났어. 랄프가 잔뜩 경멸하는 티를 내며 눈길을 거두고는, 니콜라스를 \"애송이\"라고 불렀거든. 이 말은 나이 든 신사들이 손아랫사람을 나무랄 때 곧잘 쓰는 말이지. 아마도 자기들이 다시 젊어질 수 있다 해도 무슨 일이 있어도 그러지 않겠다고 세상 사람들을 속여 믿게 하려는 속셈이겠지.\n\n\"자, 제수씨.\" 랄프가 조바심을 내며 말했어. \"채권자들이 재산을 다 처분해서, 제수씨한테 남은 게 하나도 없다는 말이지요?\"\n\n\"하나도 없어요.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어.\n\n\"그런데 그 얼마 안 되는 돈을 다 써 가며, 내가 무엇을 해 줄 수 있나 보려고 그 먼 런던까지 올라왔다는 거고요?\" 랄프가 캐물었어.\n\n\"바라기로는,\" 니클비 부인이 머뭇거리며 말했어. \"도련님이 형의 아이들을 위해 뭔가 해 주실 기회가 있지 않을까 했어요. 도련님께 아이들을 위해 호소해 달라는 게 그이의 마지막 소원이었거든요.\"\n\n\"어찌 된 노릇인지 모르겠군.\" 랄프가 방 안을 왔다 갔다 하며 중얼거렸어. \"제 재산이라곤 한 푼도 없이 죽는 사람은, 어째 꼭 남의 재산을 마음대로 처분할 권리가 있다고 여긴단 말이야. 제수씨, 따님은 무슨 일을 할 수 있소?\"\n\n\"케이트는 교육을 잘 받았어요.\" 니클비 부인이 흐느꼈어. \"삼촌께 말씀드리렴, 얘야, 프랑스어랑 다른 과목들을 어디까지 배웠는지.\"\n\n가엾은 아가씨가 막 무어라 우물거리려는데, 삼촌이 아주 무례하게 말을 끊었어.\n\n\"어디 기숙학교에 견습으로 들여보내 봐야겠군.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"설마 그것도 못 할 만큼 곱게만 자라진 않았겠지?\"\n\n\"네, 정말 안 그래요, 삼촌.\" 우는 아가씨가 대답했어. \"집과 밥을 얻을 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 해 보겠어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 랄프가 조금 누그러져서 말했어. 조카딸의 아름다움 때문인지, 그 애처로운 모습 때문인지는 모르지만 (좋게 봐서, 후자라고 해 두자). \"한번 해 보거라. 그 일이 너무 고되면, 어쩌면 바느질이나 자수 일이 좀 더 수월할지도 모르지. 너는 무슨 일이든 해 본 적 있느냐?\" (조카를 돌아보며 물었어.)\n\n\"없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 퉁명스럽게 대답했어.\n\n\"그래, 없을 줄 알았다!\" 랄프가 말했어. \"이게 내 동생이 제 자식들을 길러 온 꼴이지요, 제수씨.\"\n\n\"니콜라스는 가엾은 제 아버지가 시켜 줄 수 있는 만큼 공부를 마친 지 얼마 안 됐어요.\" 니클비 부인이 받아 말했어. \"그리고 그이는 생각하기를—\"\n\n\"언젠가 이 녀석을 뭔가로 만들 생각이었겠지요.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"늘 똑같은 얘기지. 늘 생각만 하고, 한 번도 하질 않아. 내 동생이 부지런하고 신중한 사람이었다면, 제수씨를 부자로 만들어 놓고 떠났을 거요. 그리고 내 아버지가 나를 세상에 내보냈듯이 — 내가 저 녀석보다 한 살 반이나 어렸을 때였지요 — 제 아들을 세상에 내보냈다면, 그 녀석은 제수씨한테 짐이 되어 곤경을 더하기는커녕, 오히려 제수씨를 도와줄 처지가 되어 있었을 거요. 내 동생은 생각 없고 분별없는 사람이었소, 제수씨. 그리고 그걸 제수씨보다 더 절절히 느낄 사람은 없을 거라고, 나는 확신하오.\"\n\n이 말은 과부의 마음에 이런 생각을 불러일으켰어. 어쩌면 자기가 그 천 파운드로 좀 더 성공적인 투자를 할 수도 있었을지 모른다는. 그러자 부인은 그 돈이 지금 같은 때에 얼마나 든든한 액수였을까 곱씹기 시작했지. 이런 서글픈 생각에 눈물이 더 빨리 흘러내렸어. 그리고 이 슬픔이 북받친 나머지, 부인은 (마음씨는 충분히 착했지만 한편으론 마음이 약한 여자라) 처음엔 자기 기구한 팔자를 한탄하더니, 이어서 흐느껴 가며 이런 말을 늘어놓았어. 정말이지 자기는 가엾은 니콜라스의 노예나 다름없었고, 자기는 더 좋은 데로 시집갈 수도 있었다고 남편한테 곧잘 말하곤 했다고 (사실 정말로 자주 그랬지). 그리고 그이가 살아 있는 동안엔 돈이 어디로 새는지 도무지 알 수가 없었지만, 그이가 자기한테 터놓고 의논했더라면 오늘날 다들 형편이 더 나았을 거라고. 그밖에도 대다수 결혼한 부인들이 결혼 생활 중에든, 그 후에든, 아니면 양쪽 다든 흔히 떠올리는 쓰라린 기억들을 줄줄이 늘어놓았지. 니클비 부인은 결국, 그리운 고인이 한 번을 빼고는 자기 충고를 들어 덕을 보려 한 적이 없었다고 한탄하며 말을 맺었어. 이건 한 치도 틀림없는 사실이었지. 그이는 딱 한 번 그 충고대로 했고, 그 바람에 알거지가 되고 말았으니까.\n\n랄프 니클비 씨는 이 모든 이야기를 엷은 미소를 띤 채 들었어. 그리고 과부가 말을 마치자, 아까 그 푸념이 터지기 전에 멈췄던 화제를 조용히 다시 끄집어냈지.\n\n\"일을 할 마음은 있느냐?\" 그가 조카를 향해 인상을 쓰며 물었어.\n\n\"당연히 있습니다.\" 니콜라스가 도도하게 대답했어.\n\n\"그럼 이걸 봐라.\" 삼촌이 말했어. \"오늘 아침 이게 내 눈에 띄었지. 네 운수에 감사해야 할 거다.\"\n\n이렇게 운을 떼고 나서, 랄프 니클비 씨는 주머니에서 신문을 꺼냈어. 그러고는 신문을 펼쳐 잠깐 광고란을 훑어보더니, 다음과 같이 읽었지.\n\n\"'교육. 워크퍼드 스퀴어스 씨의 학원, 두더보이즈 홀. 요크셔 그레타 브리지 인근의 아름다운 마을 두더보이즈에 있습니다. 학생들에게 숙식과 의복, 교재, 용돈을 제공하고, 필요한 모든 물품을 갖춰 줍니다. 살아 있는 언어와 죽은 언어를 모두 가르치며, 수학, 정서법, 기하학, 천문학, 삼각법, 지구의 사용법, 대수학, (원한다면) 봉술, 글쓰기, 산수, 축성술, 그밖에 고전 문학의 모든 분야를 가르칩니다. 수업료는 연 이십 기니. 추가 비용 없음, 방학 없음, 식사는 비할 데 없이 훌륭합니다. 스퀴어스 씨는 지금 시내에 머물며, 매일 한 시부터 네 시까지 스노 힐의 새러슨스 헤드에서 면담을 받습니다. 추신. 유능한 조교 한 사람 구함. 연봉 오 파운드. 문학 석사 우대.'\"\n\n\"자!\" 랄프가 신문을 다시 접으며 말했어. \"이 자리만 얻으면, 저 녀석 출셋길은 열린 거요.\"\n\n\"하지만 얘는 문학 석사가 아니잖아요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"그거야,\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"그건, 내 생각엔, 어떻게든 넘어갈 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"하지만 연봉이 너무 적고, 게다가 너무 멀리 떨어진 곳이에요, 삼촌!\" 케이트가 머뭇거리며 말했어.\n\n\"쉿, 케이트, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 끼어들었어. \"삼촌이 제일 잘 아실 거야.\"\n\n\"내 말은,\" 랄프가 쌀쌀맞게 되풀이했어. \"이 자리만 얻으면 저 녀석 출셋길은 열린다는 거요. 그게 싫다면, 제 손으로 자리를 구해 보라지. 친구도, 돈도, 추천장도, 어떤 종류의 사업 지식도 없이, 런던에서 제 구두 가죽이라도 댈 만큼의 정직한 일자리를 찾아낸다면, 내가 천 파운드를 주겠소. 적어도,\" 랄프 니클비 씨가 스스로 말을 가다듬으며 덧붙였어. \"그만한 돈이 있다면 그러겠다는 거요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 오빠!\" 아가씨가 말했어. \"오! 삼촌, 우리가 이렇게 빨리 헤어져야 하나요!\"\n\n\"삼촌이 오로지 우리 잘되라고 생각해 주시는데, 이것저것 물어서 귀찮게 해 드리지 마라, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"니콜라스, 얘야, 너도 뭐라고 말 좀 했으면 좋겠구나.\"\n\n\"네, 어머니, 네.\" 그때까지 말없이 생각에 잠겨 있던 니콜라스가 입을 열었어. \"삼촌, 제가 운이 좋아서 이 자리에 — 자격도 이렇게 모자란 자리지만요 — 임명된다면, 제가 두고 가는 사람들은 어찌 됩니까?\"\n\n\"네 어미와 누이 말이냐.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"그 경우엔 (그 경우에 한해서) 내가 보살펴서, 제힘으로 살아갈 수 있는 어떤 처지에 자리 잡게 해 주마. 그게 내가 당장 챙길 일이다. 네가 떠나고 일주일 안에, 그들이 지금 이대로 있게 두지는 않으마, 내가 책임지지.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 니콜라스가 기쁘게 벌떡 일어나 삼촌의 손을 꼭 잡으며 말했어. \"삼촌이 바라시는 일은 무엇이든 할 준비가 됐습니다. 당장 스퀴어스 씨한테 운을 한번 걸어 봐요. 거절당해 봤자 본전이니까요.\"\n\n\"그자는 거절하지 않을 거다.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"내 추천이라면 너를 기꺼이 데려갈 거야. 그자에게 쓸모 있는 사람이 되어 봐라. 그러면 머지않아 그 학교의 동업자로 올라설 거다. 원, 세상에, 생각만 해 보렴! 만약 그자가 죽기라도 하면, 네 출셋길은 단번에 열리는 셈이지.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 다 그려져요.\" 가엾은 니콜라스가 말했어. 좋은 기분과 세상 물정 모르는 마음이 눈앞에 불러일으킨 천 가지 헛된 공상에 들떠서 말이야. \"아니면 그 홀에서 교육받는 어떤 젊은 귀족이 저를 마음에 들어 해서, 학교를 떠날 때 자기 아버지를 졸라 저를 데리고 다닐 가정교사로 삼아 주는 거예요. 그러고는 대륙 여행에서 돌아오면, 저한테 그럴듯한 자리를 마련해 주고요. 어때요, 삼촌?\"\n\n\"아무렴, 그렇고말고!\" 랄프가 비웃었어.\n\n\"그리고 누가 알아요, 제가 자리를 잡고 나서 그분이 저를 보러 오면 (당연히 그러겠지요), 제 집안 살림을 맡아 줄 케이트한테 반해서, 또—또 케이트와 결혼할지도요, 어때요, 삼촌? 누가 알겠어요?\"\n\n\"그래, 누가 알겠냐!\" 랄프가 으르렁거렸어.\n\n\"우리가 얼마나 행복할까요!\" 니콜라스가 들떠서 외쳤어. \"헤어지는 아픔쯤이야, 다시 만나는 기쁨에 비하면 아무것도 아니지요. 케이트는 아름다운 여인이 되어 있을 거고, 사람들이 그렇게 말하는 걸 들으면 저는 어찌나 뿌듯할까요. 그리고 어머니는 우리와 다시 함께 지내게 되어 얼마나 행복하실까요. 이 슬픈 시절은 다 잊고요, 그리고—\" 그림이 너무 눈부셔서 도저히 견딜 수가 없었어. 그래서 니콜라스는 그 벅참에 그만 압도당해, 희미하게 미소 짓다가 그대로 울음을 터뜨렸지.\n\n이 단순한 가족은 세상과 동떨어진 곳에서 태어나 자랐고, 이른바 세상이라는 것을 도무지 몰랐어. 그 세상이라는 흔한 말은, 풀어서 해석하면 흔히 그 안의 온갖 악당들을 가리키는 말이기도 하지. 어쨌든 그들은 첫 이별을 생각하며 함께 눈물을 흘렸어. 그리고 이 첫 감정의 북받침이 가라앉자, 한 번도 시험해 본 적 없는 희망의 들뜬 마음으로 눈앞에 펼쳐진 밝은 앞날을 한껏 부풀려 그려 보기 시작했지. 바로 그때 랄프 니클비 씨가 한마디 거들었어. 꾸물거리다가는 운 좋은 다른 지원자가 광고가 가리키는 그 출세의 디딤돌을 니콜라스한테서 가로채, 그들이 공중에 쌓아 올린 성을 죄다 허물어 버릴지도 모른다고. 이 시의적절한 일깨움에 대화는 딱 멎었어. 니콜라스가 스퀴어스 씨의 주소를 꼼꼼히 옮겨 적은 뒤, 삼촌과 조카는 함께 그 훌륭한 신사를 찾아 길을 나섰지. 니콜라스는 첫눈에 삼촌을 싫어한 것이 그 친척한테 큰 잘못을 저지른 거라고 단단히 믿게 되었어. 그리고 니클비 부인은 삼촌이 겉보기보다 훨씬 친절한 사람인 게 틀림없다고 딸한테 애써 일러 주었지. 그러자 니클비 양은 얌전히, 그야 충분히 그럴 수도 있겠다고 대꾸했어.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 이 착한 부인의 생각은 시동생의 말에 적잖이 흔들린 거였어. 자기 판단력을 추켜세우고, 자기가 대단히 훌륭한 사람이라고 은근히 치켜세운 그 말에 말이야. 그리고 부인은 남편을 더없이 사랑했고 자식들을 여전히 끔찍이 아꼈지만, 랄프가 인간 마음속 그 어긋난 가락 가운데 하나를 어찌나 솜씨 좋게 건드렸던지 (랄프는 인간 마음의 가장 못된 약점은 훤히 꿰고 있었지만, 가장 좋은 점은 도무지 알지 못했어), 부인은 어느새 자기를 죽은 남편의 경솔함이 빚어낸 사랑스럽고 가여운 희생자라고 진지하게 여기기 시작했지.\n\n# CHAPTER 4\n\n스노 힐! 그 이름이 북부행 마차에 금박 글씨와 짙은 그림자로 또렷하게 새겨진 걸 보는 조용한 동네 사람들은, 스노 힐을 대체 어떤 곳이라고 생각할까? 누구나 자주 눈에 띄거나 귀에 들리는 이름이 있으면, 그곳에 대해 어렴풋하고 흐릿한 생각을 품기 마련이지. 그러니 바로 이 스노 힐을 두고 떠도는 제멋대로의 생각들이 또 얼마나 많겠어. 이름이 어찌나 그럴듯한지. 스노 힐 — 게다가 그 스노 힐이 사라센스 헤드라는 마차와 짝을 이루니, 두 가지 생각이 겹쳐서 뭔가 험하고 거친 그림이 떠오르거든! 매서운 바람과 사나운 겨울 폭풍이 몰아치는 황량하고 쓸쓸한 벌판 — 낮에도 적막하고 밤에는 점잖은 사람이라면 떠올리기조차 꺼리는 어둡고 차갑고 음울한 황무지 — 외로운 나그네는 피해 다니고 흉악한 강도들이 모여드는 그런 곳. 이런 것, 아니면 이와 비슷한 것이 그 멀고 외진 시골 사람들이 스노 힐에 대해 흔히 품는 생각일 거야. 그곳에는 사라센스 헤드가 마치 무시무시한 유령처럼 매일 밤낮으로 신비롭고 귀신같은 정확함으로 내달리거든. 어떤 날씨에도 빠르고 거침없이 길을 달리며, 마치 자연의 온갖 기운에 도전이라도 하는 듯이 말이야.\n\n실제 모습은 그것과는 좀 다르지만, 그렇다고 결코 우습게 볼 곳은 아니야. 런던의 한복판, 그 활기와 사업의 심장부, 시끄러운 소음과 움직임이 소용돌이치는 그 가운데에 — 사방에서 끊임없이 흘러나와 그 성벽 아래에서 만나는 거대한 생명의 물결을 거스르듯이 — 뉴게이트가 서 있어. 그리고 그 감옥이 어둡게 내려다보는 그 북적이는 거리에서 — 다 쓰러져 가는 누추한 집들에서 불과 몇 걸음 떨어진 곳, 지금은 수프와 생선과 흠집 난 과일을 파는 장사꾼들이 장사를 벌이고 있는 바로 그 자리에서 — 큰 도시의 소란조차 아무것도 아닌 듯한 굉음 속에서, 수십 명의 사람들이, 한 번에 네 명, 여섯 명, 여덟 명의 건장한 사내들이, 거칠고 빠르게 세상에서 떠밀려 나갔지. 사람들이 너무 많이 몰려들어 그 광경이 끔찍해질 때, 호기심 어린 눈들이 창문에서, 지붕에서, 담벼락과 기둥에서 번뜩일 때, 그리고 위로 쳐들린 하얀 얼굴들 무더기 속에서, 죽어 가는 가엾은 자가 그 모든 고통을 담은 눈길을 두루 던졌지만 단 하나도 — 단 하나도 — 연민이나 동정의 빛을 띤 얼굴을 만나지 못했지.\n\n감옥 가까이에, 따라서 스미스필드와 콤프터, 그리고 도시의 북적임과 소음에서도 가까운 곳, 동쪽으로 가는 합승마차 말들이 진지하게 일부러 쓰러질까 궁리하고, 서쪽으로 가는 삯마차 말들이 심심찮게 정말로 넘어지곤 하는 바로 그 스노 힐의 한 자락에, 사라센스 헤드 여관의 마차 마당이 있어. 그 정문은 사라센 사람의 머리와 어깨 둘이 지키고 있는데, 한때는 이 도시의 별난 멋쟁이들이 밤에 그걸 뜯어내리는 걸 자랑이자 영광으로 여겼지만, 얼마 전부터는 아무도 건드리지 않아 잠잠하게 남아 있지. 아마 그런 부류의 장난이 이제는 세인트 제임스 교구에만 머물러서일 거야. 거기서는 문에 달린 노커가 들고 다니기 편하다고 더 인기고, 초인종 줄은 편리한 이쑤시개로 쳐주거든. 그게 이유든 아니든, 그것들은 거기에 그렇게 정문 양옆에서 너를 노려보고 있어. 여관 자체도 또 다른 사라센 머리로 장식되어 마당 꼭대기에서 너를 노려보고, 거기 서 있는 붉은 마차마다 뒤쪽 짐칸 문에서는 작은 사라센 머리가 아래쪽 큰 사라센 머리들과 똑 닮은 표정으로 노려보고 있지. 그래서 이 건물 전체의 분위기는 누가 봐도 사라센풍이라 할 만해.\n\n이 마당을 걸어 올라가면 왼쪽에 예약 사무소가 보이고, 오른쪽에는 세인트 세펄커 교회의 탑이 하늘로 불쑥 솟아 있는 게 보여. 그리고 양옆으로는 침실들이 늘어선 회랑이 있지. 바로 앞에는 위에 '커피점'이라는 글자가 또렷하게 적힌 긴 창문 하나가 보일 거야. 그리고 그 창문 안을 들여다보면, 마침 알맞은 때에 갔다면, 주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣은 와크포드 스퀴어스 씨도 보였을 거야.\n\n스퀴어스 씨의 생김새는 호감을 주는 편이 아니었어. 눈이 하나밖에 없었는데, 세상 사람들은 보통 둘이 있는 쪽을 더 좋아하잖아. 그가 가진 한쪽 눈은 분명 쓸모는 있었지만 보기에는 영 아니었어. 푸르스름한 잿빛에다, 모양은 현관문 위에 달린 부채꼴 채광창을 닮았거든. 얼굴의 빈 쪽은 잔뜩 주름지고 오그라들어 있어서, 아주 음흉한 인상을 풍겼지. 특히 웃을 때가 그랬는데, 그럴 때 그의 표정은 거의 악당에 가까웠어. 머리카락은 끝부분만 빼고는 아주 납작하고 번들거렸어. 끝부분은 낮고 튀어나온 이마에서부터 빳빳하게 위로 빗어 올려져 있었는데, 그게 그의 거친 목소리며 상스러운 태도와 잘 어울렸지. 나이는 쉰둘이나 셋쯤 됐고, 키는 중간보다 조금 작았어. 끝이 긴 흰 목수건을 두르고, 학자풍의 검은 정장을 입고 있었지. 그런데 윗도리 소매는 너무 길고 바지는 너무 짧아서, 옷이 영 불편해 보였어. 마치 자기가 이렇게 점잖은 모습이라는 걸 늘 깜짝 놀라며 어색해하는 사람처럼 말이야.\n\n스퀴어스 씨는 커피점 벽난로 가운데 하나 옆 칸막이 자리에 서 있었어. 거기에는 커피점에서 흔히 보는 그런 탁자 하나와, 칸막이 모서리에 맞추어 만든 별나게 생기고 별난 크기의 탁자 둘이 놓여 있었지. 그 자리 한구석에는 아주 작은 전나무 궤짝 하나가 변변찮은 노끈으로 묶여 있었어. 그리고 그 궤짝 위에는 — 끈으로 매는 반장화와 코르덴 바지를 허공에 대롱대롱 늘어뜨린 채 — 자그마한 사내아이 하나가 올라앉아 있었어. 어깨를 귀까지 잔뜩 움츠리고 두 손을 무릎에 얹은 채, 그 아이는 이따금 겁에 질린 눈으로 선생을 흘끔거렸지. 누가 봐도 두려움과 불안에 떨고 있었어.\n\n\"세 시 반이군.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 창가에서 돌아서며 커피점 시계를 못마땅하게 바라보더니 중얼거렸어. \"오늘은 아무도 안 오겠어.\"\n\n이런 생각에 잔뜩 짜증이 난 스퀴어스 씨는, 그 사내아이가 때려 줄 만한 짓이라도 하고 있는지 보려고 아이를 쳐다봤어. 그런데 아이는 마침 아무 짓도 하고 있지 않았지. 그래서 그냥 귀싸대기만 한 대 갈기고는, 두 번 다시 그러지 말라고 했어.\n\n\"한여름에는,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 다시 푸념을 늘어놓으며 중얼거렸어. \"열 명을 데려갔단 말이지. 열에 스무 곱이면 이백 파운드야. 그런데 내일 아침 여덟 시에 돌아가는데, 겨우 셋밖에 못 구했어. 셋에 영을 곱하면 영, 셋에 둘을 곱하면 여섯, 육십 파운드라. 애들이 다 어디로 간 거야? 부모들 머릿속에 대체 뭐가 든 거지? 이게 다 무슨 영문이람?\"\n\n이때 궤짝 위에 앉은 사내아이가 요란하게 재채기를 했어.\n\n\"이봐, 너!\" 선생이 홱 돌아서며 으르렁거렸어. \"그게 무슨 짓이야?\"\n\n\"아무것도 아니에요, 선생님.\" 사내아이가 대답했어.\n\n\"아무것도 아니라고!\" 스퀴어스 씨가 버럭 소리쳤어.\n\n\"저, 재채기를 했어요.\" 아이가 다시 말했어. 어찌나 떨었는지 작은 궤짝까지 들썩였지.\n\n\"오! 재채기를 했다고?\" 스퀴어스 씨가 되받아쳤어. \"그럼 왜 '아무것도 아니'라고 했어, 응?\"\n\n이 물음에 마땅한 대답이 떠오르지 않자, 사내아이는 양쪽 눈에 주먹을 한 쌍씩 비벼 넣더니 울기 시작했어. 그러자 스퀴어스 씨는 한쪽 뺨을 후려쳐 아이를 궤짝에서 떨어뜨리고, 다른 쪽 뺨을 후려쳐 다시 올려놓았지.\n\n\"요크셔에 데려갈 때까지 기다려, 이 도련님아.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어. \"그때 나머지를 마저 주마. 그 시끄러운 소리 그칠 거야, 안 그칠 거야?\"\n\n\"네, 네, 네.\" 사내아이가 흐느끼며, 무명천에 인쇄된 거지의 탄원서로 얼굴을 박박 문질렀어.\n\n\"그럼 당장 그쳐.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"들었냐?\"\n\n이 호통에는 위협하는 몸짓이 따랐고, 그것도 사나운 표정으로 내뱉었으니, 사내아이는 눈물을 막으려는 듯 얼굴을 더 세게 문질렀어. 그러고는 번갈아 훌쩍이고 목메는 것 말고는, 더 이상 감정을 드러내지 않았지.\n\n\"스퀴어스 씨.\" 마침 이때 종업원이 안을 들여다보며 말했어. \"어떤 신사분이 술집 쪽에서 찾으시는데요.\"\n\n\"그 신사분을 들여보내게, 리처드.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 부드러운 목소리로 대답했어. \"손수건 주머니에 넣어, 이 못된 녀석아. 안 그러면 손님 가고 나서 죽여 버릴 테니.\"\n\n선생이 이 말을 사납게 속삭이듯 내뱉기가 무섭게, 낯선 손님이 들어왔어. 스퀴어스 씨는 그를 못 본 척하며, 펜을 고치는 데 골몰한 척하면서 어린 제자에게 자애로운 충고를 건네는 시늉을 했어.\n\n\"얘야,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어. \"사람은 누구나 시련을 겪는 법이란다. 어린 네 가슴이 터질 것 같고, 울다가 눈알이 다 빠질 것 같은 이 어린 시절의 시련, 그게 대체 뭐겠니? 아무것도 아니란다. 아무것도 아닌 것보다 못한 거지. 너는 지금 네 친구들과 헤어지고 있지만, 내가 네 아버지가 되어 주마, 얘야. 그리고 스퀴어스 부인이 네 어머니가 되어 주실 거야. 요크셔의 그레타 다리 근처, 그 아름다운 두더보이즈 마을에서는 아이들에게 숙식과 옷, 책, 빨래, 용돈, 그 밖에 필요한 모든 것을 다 마련해 주는데——\"\n\n\"바로 그 신사분이시군요.\" 낯선 손님이 선생이 광고문을 읊는 걸 막으며 말했어. \"스퀴어스 씨 맞으시죠?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 몹시 놀란 척하며 말했어.\n\n\"타임스 신문에 광고를 내신 그 신사분 맞으시죠?\" 낯선 손님이 물었어.\n\n\"——모닝 포스트, 크로니클, 헤럴드, 그리고 애드버타이저에도요. 요크셔의 그레타 다리 근처, 그 아름다운 두더보이즈 마을에 있는 두더보이즈 홀이라는 학교에 관한 광고죠.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 덧붙였어. \"일이 있어서 오셨군요, 선생님. 제 어린 친구들을 보니 알겠습니다. 잘 있었니, 꼬마 신사? 너는 또 어떠니?\" 이렇게 인사하며 스퀴어스 씨는 그 손님이 데려온, 눈이 퀭하고 뼈가 앙상한 두 사내아이의 머리를 쓰다듬었어. 그러고는 다음 이야기를 기다렸지.\n\n\"저는 기름과 물감을 파는 사람입니다. 이름은 스놀리라고 합니다.\" 낯선 손님이 말했어.\n\n스퀴어스는 마치 \"참으로 멋진 이름이군요\" 하고 말하듯 고개를 끄덕였어.\n\n낯선 손님이 말을 이었어. \"스퀴어스 씨, 제 두 아이를 선생님 학교에 보낼까 생각하고 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"제 입으로 이런 말씀 드리긴 뭣하지만요, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 대답했어. \"그보다 더 나은 결정은 없으실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"흠!\" 상대가 말했어. \"한 해에 이십 파운드라고 했지요, 스퀴어스 씨?\"\n\n\"기니입니다.\" 선생이 구슬리는 듯한 미소를 띠며 대꾸했어.\n\n\"둘이서 파운드로 알고 있는데요, 스퀴어스 씨.\" 스놀리 씨가 진지하게 말했어.\n\n\"그건 어려울 것 같습니다만, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 마치 그런 제안은 처음 생각해 본다는 듯 대답했어. \"어디 보자. 다섯에 넷을 곱하면 스물, 그 두 배에서 빼면——뭐, 파운드 하나쯤이 우리 사이를 가로막을 거야 있겠습니까. 대신 선생님께서 아는 분들께 저를 좀 추천해 주셔서 그렇게 메워 주십시오.\"\n\n\"애들이 많이 먹지는 않습니다.\" 스놀리 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"오! 그건 전혀 상관없습니다.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"저희 학교에서는 아이들 식성 따위는 고려하지 않으니까요.\" 이건 한 치도 틀림없는 사실이었어. 정말로 고려하지 않았거든.\n\n\"요크셔에서 누릴 수 있는 건강에 좋은 온갖 호사가요, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 말을 이었어. \"스퀴어스 부인이 가르칠 수 있는 온갖 아름다운 도덕이며, 한마디로, 아이가 바랄 수 있는 가정의 온갖 안락함이 다 아이들 것이 될 겁니다, 스놀리 씨.\"\n\n\"저는 특히 아이들 도덕 교육에 신경을 써 주셨으면 합니다.\" 스놀리 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"그렇다니 다행입니다, 선생님.\" 선생이 몸을 쭉 펴며 대답했어. \"도덕이라면 제대로 찾아오셨습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"선생님 본인이 도덕적인 분이시군요.\" 스놀리 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"저도 그런 편이라고 봅니다, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어.\n\n\"그러시다는 걸 알게 되어 흐뭇합니다, 선생님.\" 스놀리 씨가 말했어. \"선생님이 대 주신 보증인 한 분께 여쭤봤더니, 선생님이 독실하시다더군요.\"\n\n\"뭐, 선생님, 그 방면으로 조금은 그렇다고 봅니다.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어.\n\n\"저도 그러기를 바라는 사람입니다.\" 상대가 다시 말했어. \"옆 칸에서 몇 마디 나눌 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"물론입니다.\" 스퀴어스가 씩 웃으며 대답했어. \"얘들아, 새 놀이 친구하고 잠깐 얘기 좀 나누고 있을래? 저 애가 제 학생 중 하나입니다, 선생님. 이름은 벨링이고요. 톤턴에서 온 아이지요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇습니까?\" 스놀리 씨가 그 가엾은 꼬마를 무슨 진귀한 자연의 신기한 물건이라도 되는 양 바라보며 대꾸했어.\n\n\"내일 저와 함께 내려갈 아이입니다, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"지금 깔고 앉아 있는 게 저 애 짐입니다. 학생마다 가져와야 하는 게요, 선생님, 옷 두 벌, 셔츠 여섯 장, 양말 여섯 켤레, 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자 둘, 손수건 둘, 신발 두 켤레, 모자 둘, 그리고 면도칼 하나입니다.\"\n\n\"면도칼이라고요!\" 스놀리 씨가 옆 칸으로 걸어 들어가며 소리쳤어. \"그건 어디에 쓰려고요?\"\n\n\"면도를 하려는 거죠.\" 스퀴어스가 느리고 또박또박한 말투로 대답했어.\n\n이 짧은 한마디에 별다른 게 담겨 있지는 않았지만, 그 말투에는 분명 사람의 주의를 끄는 뭔가가 있었나 봐. 선생과 그 일행이 잠깐 동안 서로를 빤히 바라보더니, 아주 의미심장한 미소를 주고받았거든. 스놀리는 매끈하고 코가 납작한 사내였어. 칙칙한 옷에 긴 검정 각반을 차고, 얼굴에는 잔뜩 풀이 죽고 거룩한 듯한 표정을 띠고 있었지. 그러니 뚜렷한 이유도 없이 그가 미소를 짓는 건 더더욱 눈에 띄는 일이었어.\n\n\"그러면 학교에서는 아이들을 몇 살까지 데리고 계십니까?\" 그가 마침내 물었어.\n\n\"애들 보호자가 시내에 있는 제 대리인에게 분기마다 돈을 내는 한 계속이지요. 아니면 애들이 달아날 때까지고요.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"우리 서로 터놓고 얘기합시다. 그래도 될 것 같으니까요. 이 아이들은 어떤 애들입니까? 사생아인가요?\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 스놀리가 선생의 외눈과 눈을 맞추며 대꾸했어. \"그건 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있겠다 싶었거든요.\" 스퀴어스가 태연하게 말했어. \"그런 애들이 꽤 많습니다. 저 애도 그중 하나죠.\"\n\n\"옆 칸에 있는 애 말입니까?\" 스놀리가 물었어.\n\n스퀴어스가 그렇다고 고개를 끄덕였어. 그러자 그 일행은 궤짝 위의 사내아이를 다시 한번 흘끔 보더니, 도로 돌아앉으면서 그 애가 여느 아이들과 별로 다를 게 없어 영 실망스럽다는 표정을 짓고는, 그럴 줄은 몰랐다고 말했어.\n\n\"그렇다니까요.\" 스퀴어스가 소리쳤어. \"그건 그렇고, 선생님 그 아이들 말입니다. 저한테 하실 말씀이 있다고요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 스놀리가 대답했어. \"실은요, 스퀴어스 씨, 제가 저 애들 친아버지가 아닙니다. 그저 의붓아버지일 뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"오! 그렇습니까?\" 선생이 말했어. \"그러면 단번에 설명이 되는군요. 대체 왜 애들을 요크셔까지 보내려는 건가 했거든요. 하하! 오, 이제 알겠습니다.\"\n\n\"보시다시피 제가 애들 어미와 결혼했지요.\" 스놀리가 말을 이었어. \"애들을 집에 데리고 있자니 돈이 많이 들고요, 게다가 그 여자가 제 명의로 가진 돈이 좀 있거든요. 그런데 (여자들이란 게 어찌나 어리석은지요, 스퀴어스 씨) 그 돈을 애들한테 쏟아부을까 봐 걱정입니다. 그랬다간 애들 신세를 망치는 거 아니겠습니까.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다.\" 스퀴어스가 의자에 등을 기대며 손을 내저었어.\n\n\"그래서요.\" 스놀리가 다시 말을 이었어. \"애들을 멀찌감치 떨어진 어디 학교에 보내고 싶어서 안달이 났습니다. 방학도 없고요——일 년에 두 번씩 집에 와서 애들 마음을 들쑤셔 놓는 그런 분별없는 짓도 없는 데요. 그리고 좀 거칠게 굴러도 되는 데 말입니다——무슨 말인지 아시겠지요?\"\n\n\"돈은 꼬박꼬박, 그리고 이러쿵저러쿵 묻는 일은 없게요.\" 스퀴어스가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했어.\n\n\"바로 그겁니다.\" 상대가 대꾸했어. \"그래도 도덕만큼은 철저히 신경 써 주시고요.\"\n\n\"철저히 하겠습니다.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어.\n\n\"집에 편지 쓰는 건 너무 많이는 허락하지 않으시겠지요?\" 의붓아버지가 머뭇거리며 말했어.\n\n\"전혀요. 크리스마스에 회람 한 장 보내는 것 말고는요. 거기에 이렇게 어디 한가지로 행복한 적이 없으며, 부디 데려가지 마시기를 바란다고 적게 하지요.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"그보다 좋을 순 없겠군요.\" 의붓아버지가 두 손을 비비며 말했어.\n\n\"그럼 우리가 서로 통했으니 말입니다.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"제가 한 가지 여쭤봐도 될까요? 선생님께서는 저를 사생활에서도 대단히 덕망 있고, 모범적이며, 행실 바른 사람이라고 여기시는지요? 그리고 아이들을 맡아 돌보는 일을 하는 사람으로서, 제 흠잡을 데 없는 성실함과 너그러움, 신앙심, 그리고 능력에 가장 큰 신뢰를 두고 계시는지요?\"\n\n\"물론입니다.\" 의붓아버지가 선생의 씩 웃는 미소를 똑같이 되돌려 주며 대답했어.\n\n\"제가 선생님을 보증인으로 댄다면, 그렇게 말씀해 주시는 데 거리낌은 없으시겠지요?\"\n\n\"조금도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그거죠!\" 스퀴어스가 펜을 집어 들며 말했어. \"이게 바로 일하는 거고, 제가 좋아하는 게 이런 겁니다.\"\n\n스놀리 씨의 주소를 적은 다음, 선생은 그보다 더 흐뭇한 일을 처리해야 했어. 첫 분기 수업료를 미리 받았다는 영수증을 적는 일이었지. 그걸 채 다 적기도 전에, 또 다른 목소리가 스퀴어스 씨를 찾는 게 들려왔어.\n\n\"여기 있습니다.\" 선생이 대답했어. \"무슨 일이시죠?\"\n\n\"그저 일이 좀 있어서요, 선생님.\" 랄프 니클비가 모습을 드러내며 말했어. 그 바로 뒤에는 니콜라스가 따라왔지. \"오늘 아침 신문에 선생님 광고가 났던데요?\"\n\n\"났습니다, 선생님. 이쪽으로 오시지요.\" 그새 벽난로 옆 칸 자리로 돌아와 있던 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"앉으시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"음, 그러는 게 좋겠군요.\" 랄프가 말에 맞춰 행동하며, 모자를 앞 탁자에 올려놓고 대답했어. \"이쪽은 제 조카입니다, 선생님. 니콜라스 니클비입니다.\"\n\n\"안녕하십니까?\" 스퀴어스가 말했어.\n\n니콜라스는 고개를 숙여 인사하며 잘 지낸다고 했어. 그러면서 두더보이즈 홀 교장의 겉모습에 무척 놀란 것 같았어. 실제로도 그랬고 말이야.\n\n\"혹시 저를 기억하십니까?\" 랄프가 선생을 빤히 들여다보며 말했어.\n\n\"몇 년 동안, 반년에 한 번씩 시내에 오실 때마다 제게 적은 액수를 치르신 분 같은데요, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어.\n\n\"그랬지요.\" 랄프가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"도커라는 아이의 부모님을 위해서였죠. 그 애가 불행히도——\"\n\n\"——불행히도 두더보이즈 홀에서 죽었지요.\" 랄프가 문장을 마저 끝냈어.\n\n\"아주 잘 기억합니다, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어. \"아! 스퀴어스 부인이요, 선생님, 그 애를 꼭 제 자식처럼 아꼈답니다. 그 애가 앓을 때 쏟은 정성이라니, 선생님! 그 애가 아무것도 못 삼킬 때 밤이고 아침이고 마른 토스트와 따뜻한 차를 권했고요——죽던 바로 그날 밤에는 그 애 침실에 촛불까지 켜 줬지요——머리를 받치라고 제일 좋은 사전까지 올려보냈고요——그래도 저는 후회하지 않습니다. 그 애한테 도리를 다했다고 돌아보는 건 흐뭇한 일이니까요.\"\n\n랄프는 미소를 지었어. 그런데 미소 말고는 그 무엇을 뜻하는 듯한 미소였지. 그러고는 거기 있던 낯선 사람들을 둘러봤어.\n\n\"이 애들은 그저 제 학생들 몇입니다.\" 와크포드 스퀴어스가 궤짝 위의 사내아이와 바닥에 있는 두 사내아이를 가리키며 말했어. 그 둘은 말 한마디 없이 서로를 빤히 쳐다보면서, 처음 알게 된 사내아이들이 으레 그러듯 별별 희한한 모양으로 몸을 비비 꼬고 있었지. \"이 신사분은요, 선생님, 두더보이즈 홀에서 채택한 교육 방식을 친히 칭찬해 주시는 학부형이십니다. 두더보이즈 홀은요, 선생님, 요크셔의 그레타 다리 근처, 그 아름다운 두더보이즈 마을에 있는데요, 거기서는 아이들에게 숙식과 옷, 책, 빨래, 용돈을——\"\n\n\"네, 그건 다 압니다, 선생님.\" 랄프가 짜증스럽게 끼어들었어. \"광고에 나와 있던데요.\"\n\n\"맞는 말씀입니다, 선생님. 광고에 나와 있지요.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어.\n\n\"게다가 사실이 그렇기도 하고요.\" 스놀리 씨가 끼어들었어. \"선생님께 꼭 확실히 말씀드려야겠습니다. 그리고 이렇게 말씀드릴 기회가 있어서 자랑스럽습니다만, 저는 스퀴어스 씨를 대단히 덕망 있고, 모범적이며, 행실 바른 신사로——\"\n\n\"의심하지 않습니다, 선생님.\" 랄프가 추천의 봇물을 막으며 끼어들었어. \"조금도 의심하지 않아요. 그럼 본론으로 들어갈까요?\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어. \"'일을 미루지 말라.' 이게 바로 저희가 상업을 배우는 학생들에게 제일 먼저 새겨 주는 가르침이지요. 벨링 군, 얘야, 그걸 늘 기억해라. 알겠니?\"\n\n\"네, 선생님.\" 벨링 군이 따라 말했어.\n\n\"저 애가 그게 뭔지 기억하나요?\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"신사분께 말씀드려라.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어.\n\n\"'말라.'\" 벨링 군이 따라 말했어.\n\n\"아주 좋아.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"계속해.\"\n\n\"말라.\" 벨링 군이 또 한 번 되풀이했어.\n\n\"정말 잘했다.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"그래.\"\n\n\"미.\" 니콜라스가 사람 좋게 거들었어.\n\n\"미루지——일을!\" 벨링 군이 말했어. \"말라——미루지——일을!\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 스퀴어스가 그 죄인에게 따끔한 눈길을 쏘아붙이며 말했어. \"너랑 나는 이따가 우리끼리 따로 일을 좀 처리하자꾸나.\"\n\n\"그리고 지금은,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"우리 일부터 처리하는 게 낫겠군요.\"\n\n\"그러시지요.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어.\n\n\"자.\" 랄프가 다시 말을 이었어. \"아주 간단합니다. 금방 꺼낼 수 있고, 쉽게 매듭지을 수 있기를 바랍니다. 선생님께서 유능한 조교를 구한다는 광고를 내셨지요?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어.\n\n\"그리고 정말로 한 사람 필요하시고요?\"\n\n\"물론이지요.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어.\n\n\"여기 있습니다!\" 랄프가 말했어. \"제 조카 니콜라스입니다. 학교를 갓 나와서, 거기서 배운 게 죄다 머릿속에서 부글거리고 있지요. 그런데 주머니에서는 아무것도 부글거리지 않거든요. 선생님께 딱 맞는 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"글쎄요.\" 니콜라스 같은 풍채의 젊은이가 이런 자리에 지원한다는 게 어리둥절했던 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"이 젊은이는 저한테 안 맞을 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 맞습니다.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"제가 더 잘 압니다. 풀 죽지 마십시오, 선생님. 일주일도 안 되어 두더보이즈 홀의 어린 귀족 자제들을 모조리 가르치게 될 겁니다. 이 신사분이 제 짐작보다 더 고집불통이 아니라면 말이지요.\"\n\n\"제가 걱정되는 건요, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 스퀴어스 씨에게 말했어. \"제 나이가 어리고, 제가 석사 학위가 없다는 점이 마음에 걸리시는 거지요?\"\n\n\"대학 학위가 없다는 게 걸리긴 합니다.\" 스퀴어스가 최대한 진지한 표정을 지으며 대답했어. 그는 적잖이 어리둥절했지. 조카의 순박함과 삼촌의 약삭빠른 태도가 어찌나 대조적인지, 그리고 자기가 가르치는 어린 귀족 자제들이라는 알 수 없는 소리가 또 어찌나 영문 모를 일인지 말이야.\n\n\"이보세요, 선생님.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"이 일을 제가 단 이 초 만에 제대로 밝혀 드리지요.\"\n\n\"그래 주신다면야.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"이 애는 소년이라 하든, 청년이라 하든, 풋내기라 하든, 젊은이라 하든, 머슴애라 하든, 뭐라고 부르든 좋습니다만, 열여덟이나 열아홉쯤 됐습니다.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"그건 저도 보면 압니다.\" 선생이 말했어.\n\n\"저도 그렇고요.\" 스놀리 씨가 가끔은 새로 사귄 친구를 거들어 주는 게 좋겠다 싶어 말했어.\n\n\"아비는 죽었고, 세상 물정은 전혀 모르고, 가진 밑천이라곤 하나도 없고, 뭐든 할 일이 필요한 애지요.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"제대로만 활용하면 출세길로 이어질 자리라 여겨서, 선생님의 이 훌륭한 학교를 추천하는 겁니다. 이해되시지요?\"\n\n\"누가 봐도 그렇지요.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. 영문 모를 조카를 바라보는 노신사의 그 비웃음을 반쯤 따라 지으면서 말이야.\n\n\"물론 저도 그렇게 생각합니다.\" 니콜라스가 들떠서 말했어.\n\n\"보다시피 저 애도 물론 그렇게 생각하고요.\" 랄프가 여전히 메마르고 딱딱한 투로 말했어. \"혹시라도 변덕이 도져서 이 황금 같은 기회를 제대로 익히기도 전에 내팽개친다면, 저는 저 애 어미와 누이에게 도움을 줄 의무에서 완전히 벗어나는 거라고 봅니다. 저 애를 보세요. 그리고 저 애를 여러모로 어떻게 부려먹을 수 있을지 생각해 보십시오! 자, 문제는 이겁니다. 적어도 앞으로 한동안은, 저 애가 보통 때 같으면 구할 만한 그런 사람 스무 명보다 선생님 목적에 더 잘 맞지 않겠느냐는 거지요. 한번 생각해 볼 만한 문제 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"그렇군요, 그렇습니다.\" 스퀴어스가 랄프의 고갯짓에 자기도 고개를 끄덕여 답하며 말했어.\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 랄프가 대꾸했어. \"잠깐 따로 두어 마디 나누지요.\"\n\n두 사람은 떨어져서 두어 마디를 나눴어. 그리고 몇 분 뒤, 와크포드 스퀴어스 씨는 이 순간부터 니콜라스 니클비 씨가 두더보이즈 홀의 수석 조교 자리에 정식으로 임명되고 그 직에 앉게 되었다고 선언했지.\n\n\"삼촌분의 추천 덕분입니다, 니클비 씨.\" 와크포드 스퀴어스가 말했어.\n\n니콜라스는 일이 잘 풀린 게 너무 기뻐서 삼촌의 손을 따뜻하게 잡고 흔들었어. 그리고 그 자리에서 스퀴어스를 떠받들 뻔했지.\n\n'생김새가 좀 별나기는 해.' 니콜라스는 생각했어. '하지만 그게 무슨 대수람? 포슨도 생김새가 별났고, 존슨 박사도 그랬잖아. 책벌레들이란 다 그런 거야.'\n\n\"내일 아침 여덟 시에요, 니클비 씨.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"마차가 출발합니다. 이 애들을 데리고 가야 하니, 십오 분 전까지는 여기 와 계셔야 합니다.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"그리고 내려가는 마찻삯은 내가 다 치렀다.\" 랄프가 으르렁거렸어. \"그러니 너는 몸이나 따뜻하게 하고 있으면 돼.\"\n\n이거야말로 삼촌의 또 한 번의 너그러움이 아니고 무엇이겠어! 니콜라스는 뜻밖의 그 친절이 어찌나 가슴에 사무쳤던지, 고맙다는 말을 어찌 꺼내야 할지 모를 지경이었어. 정말이지 절반의 절반도 채 못 한 채, 두 사람은 선생과 작별하고 사라센스 헤드의 정문을 나섰지.\n\n\"아침에 네가 무사히 떠나는 걸 보러 여기 오마.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"꽁무니 빼지 말고!\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 삼촌.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"이 친절은 절대 잊지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"잊지 않도록 해라.\" 삼촌이 대답했어. \"이제 집에 가서 챙겨 갈 것들을 꾸리는 게 좋겠다. 그 전에 골든 스퀘어까지 찾아갈 수 있겠니?\"\n\n\"그럼요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"물어물어 가면 되지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 이 서류들을 내 서기에게 맡기고,\" 랄프가 작은 꾸러미 하나를 꺼내며 말했어. \"내가 집에 올 때까지 기다리라고 일러라.\"\n\n니콜라스는 기꺼이 그 심부름을 맡았어. 그리고 마음씨 좋은 삼촌에게 다정하게 작별 인사를 했지. 그 따뜻한 노신사는 으르렁거리는 소리로 답하고 말이야. 니콜라스는 분부를 받들러 서둘러 떠났어.\n\n이윽고 니콜라스는 골든 스퀘어를 찾아냈어. 잠깐 술집에 다녀온 노그스 씨가, 마침 그가 계단에 다다랐을 때 걸쇠 열쇠로 문을 열고 있었지.\n\n\"그게 뭔가?\" 노그스가 꾸러미를 가리키며 물었어.\n\n\"삼촌이 주신 서류예요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그리고 삼촌이 집에 올 때까지 기다려 주시면 고맙겠다고 하셨어요.\"\n\n\"삼촌!\" 노그스가 소리쳤어.\n\n\"니클비 씨요.\" 니콜라스가 설명했어.\n\n\"들어오게.\" 뉴먼이 말했어.\n\n그는 더 말없이 니콜라스를 복도로 데려갔어. 그리고 복도 끝에 있는 사무실 식료품 방으로 안내했지. 거기서 니콜라스를 의자에 떠밀어 앉히고는, 자기는 높은 걸상에 올라앉아 두 팔을 옆구리에 똑바로 늘어뜨린 채 앉았어. 그러고는 마치 망루에서 내려다보듯 니콜라스를 빤히 바라봤지.\n\n\"답장은 없어요.\" 니콜라스가 꾸러미를 옆 탁자에 내려놓으며 말했어.\n\n뉴먼은 아무 말도 하지 않았어. 그저 두 팔을 포개고 고개를 앞으로 쭉 내밀어 니콜라스의 얼굴을 더 가까이 보더니, 그 생김새를 찬찬히 뜯어봤지.\n\n\"답장 없어요.\" 니콜라스가 뉴먼 노그스가 귀가 먹은 줄 알고 아주 크게 말했어.\n\n뉴먼은 두 손을 무릎에 얹은 채, 한마디도 내뱉지 않고 상대의 얼굴을 똑같이 빤히 뜯어보길 계속했어.\n\n생판 모르는 사람이 이렇게 군다는 게 어찌나 별난 노릇인지, 게다가 그 생김새도 어찌나 별스러운지, 우스운 걸 제법 잘 알아채는 니콜라스는 그만 빙긋 웃음을 터뜨리고 말았어. 그러면서 노그스 씨가 자기한테 무슨 시킬 일이라도 있느냐고 물었지.\n\n노그스는 고개를 젓고 한숨을 쉬었어. 그러자 니콜라스가 일어서서, 자기는 쉬지 않아도 된다고 말하고는 잘 있으라고 인사했어.\n\n뉴먼 노그스로서는 엄청난 용기를 낸 거였어. 상대는 자기가 전혀 모르는 사람이었으니, 그가 대체 어쩌다 그런 말을 하게 됐는지는 오늘날까지도 아무도 몰라. 아무튼 뉴먼은 길게 숨을 들이쉬더니, 정말로 소리 내어, 한 번도 멈추지 않고 이렇게 말했어. 젊은이가 괜찮다면, 삼촌이 그를 위해 무엇을 해 줄 셈인지 알고 싶다고 말이야.\n\n니콜라스로서는 그걸 마다할 까닭이 조금도 없었어. 오히려 머릿속을 가득 채운 그 일을 이야기할 기회가 생긴 게 반가웠지. 그래서 다시 자리에 앉아, (말을 하면서 그의 낙천적인 상상이 점점 달아올라서) 두더보이즈 홀이라는 그 배움의 터에 임명된 데서 얻게 될 온갖 명예와 이점을 열렬하고 신나게 늘어놓기 시작했어.\n\n\"그런데, 무슨 일이세요——어디 편찮으세요?\" 니콜라스가 갑자기 말을 뚝 끊으며 물었어. 상대가 별별 괴상한 자세를 취하더니 두 손을 걸상 밑으로 쑤셔 넣고는, 손뼈를 죄다 부러뜨리기라도 하듯 손가락 마디를 우두둑거렸거든.\n\n뉴먼 노그스는 대꾸도 하지 않고, 계속 어깨를 으쓱거리며 손가락 마디를 우두둑거렸어. 그러면서 내내 끔찍하게 웃었고, 눈을 치켜뜬 채 더없이 섬뜩한 표정으로 허공의 아무것도 아닌 데를 뚫어지게 바라봤지.\n\n처음에 니콜라스는 그 알 수 없는 사내가 발작을 일으킨 줄 알았어. 그런데 다시 생각해 보니 술에 취한 거라는 결론을 내렸지. 그렇다면 당장 자리를 뜨는 게 현명하겠다 싶었어. 그는 현관문을 열고서 뒤를 돌아봤어. 뉴먼 노그스는 여전히 그 별난 몸짓을 이어가고 있었고, 손가락 우두둑거리는 소리는 그 어느 때보다도 크게 울렸지.\n\n# CHAPTER 5\n\n눈물방울이 트렁크에 떨어져서 그 주인을 슬픔과 불행으로부터 지켜 주는 부적이 된다면, 니콜라스 니클비는 더없이 행복한 조짐 속에서 여행을 시작하는 셈이었을 거야. 할 일은 너무 많은데 그 일을 할 시간은 너무 짧았어. 다정한 말은 그렇게 많이 떠올랐지만, 그 말이 솟아오르는 가슴마다 쓰라린 아픔이 가득해서 입 밖으로 나오는 걸 막았지. 그래서 여행을 위한 자그마한 준비조차 정말이지 슬픈 분위기 속에서 이루어졌어. 어머니와 누이가 애써 챙기며 그의 편안함에 꼭 필요하다고 여긴 물건이 백 가지나 됐지만, 니콜라스는 그걸 두고 가겠다고 우겼어. 나중에 쓸모가 있을지도 모르고, 필요할 땐 돈으로 바꿀 수도 있다는 거였지. 이런 일들을 두고 다정한 실랑이가 백 번도 더 벌어졌어. 떠나기 전날, 그 슬픈 밤에 말이야. 그리고 화 한 점 없는 그 다툼이 한 번씩 끝날 때마다 보잘것없는 준비도 점점 마무리에 가까워졌어. 케이트는 점점 더 바삐 손을 놀렸고, 점점 더 소리 없이 울었지.\n\n마침내 상자가 다 꾸려졌어. 그러고는 저녁 식사가 나왔어. 이날을 위해 마련한 자그마한 별미도 곁들였지. 그 비용을 메우려고 케이트와 어머니는 니콜라스가 밖에 나가 있을 때 밥을 먹은 척했어. 가엾은 소년은 그걸 좀 먹어 보려다 하마터면 목이 멜 뻔했어. 그리고 농담을 한두 마디 던지고 억지로 쓸쓸한 웃음을 지어 보이다가 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이 됐지. 그렇게 세 사람은 밤에 헤어질 시간이 한참 지나도록 자리에 머물러 있었어. 그러다 깨달았지. 진작 속마음을 다 털어놓는 편이 나았을 거라고 말이야. 아무리 애를 써도 도무지 감정을 억누를 수가 없었거든. 그래서 그들은 감정이 흐르는 대로 내버려 두었어. 그것만으로도 마음이 한결 가벼워졌지.\n\n니콜라스는 이튿날 아침 여섯 시까지 푹 잤어. 집 꿈을 꾸었지. 아니, 한때 집이었던 곳 꿈을. 어느 쪽이든 상관없었어. 변하거나 사라진 것도 꿈속에서는 옛 모습 그대로 돌아오니까. 정말 고마운 일이지! 그는 아주 기운차고 명랑한 기분으로 일어났어. 그리고 연필로 몇 줄을 적었어. 직접 입으로 꺼내기가 두려웠던 작별 인사를 쓴 거야. 그 쪽지를 얼마 안 되는 돈의 절반과 함께 누이의 방문 앞에 놓아두고는, 상자를 어깨에 둘러메고 살금살금 아래층으로 내려갔어.\n\n\"너니, 해나?\" 라 크리비 양의 거실에서 목소리가 흘러나왔어. 거기서 가냘픈 촛불 빛이 새어 나오고 있었지.\n\n\"저예요, 라 크리비 양.\" 니콜라스가 상자를 내려놓고 안을 들여다보며 말했어.\n\n\"아이고머니!\" 라 크리비 양이 흠칫 놀라더니 머리에 만 종이를 손으로 가렸어. \"정말 일찍 일어나셨네요, 니클비 씨.\"\n\n\"라 크리비 양도 그러신데요.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"저는 예술 때문에 침대에서 일어난 거랍니다, 니클비 씨.\" 부인이 말했어. \"어떤 아이디어를 살리려고 빛이 들기를 기다리는 중이거든요.\"\n\n라 크리비 양은 일찍 일어나서 못생긴 사내아이의 작은 초상화에 그럴듯한 코를 그려 넣으려던 참이었어. 그 그림은 시골에 사는 아이의 할머니에게 보낼 거였지. 아이가 집안 식구를 닮았으면 재산을 물려주기로 되어 있는 할머니 말이야.\n\n\"아이디어를 살린다는 거예요.\" 라 크리비 양이 다시 말했어. \"그게 바로 스트랜드 같은 큰길가에 사는 큰 이점이지요. 어떤 손님한테 어울리는 코나 눈이 필요할 때면, 창밖을 내다보면서 마음에 드는 게 나타날 때까지 기다리기만 하면 되거든요.\"\n\n\"그럼 코 하나 구하는 데 오래 걸리나요?\" 니콜라스가 미소 지으며 물었어.\n\n\"글쎄요, 그건 모양에 따라 많이 다르답니다.\" 라 크리비 양이 대답했어. \"들창코나 매부리코는 아주 흔해요. 그리고 엑서터 홀에 무슨 모임이 있을 때면 온갖 크기의 납작코도 널렸지요. 하지만 완벽한 매부리코는, 안타깝게도 아주 드물어요. 그래서 그런 코는 보통 군복 입은 사람이나 유명 인사를 그릴 때나 쓴답니다.\"\n\n\"그렇군요!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"제가 여행하다가 그런 코를 만나면, 당신을 위해 스케치해 보도록 애써 볼게요.\"\n\n\"이 추운 겨울 날씨에 정말로 요크셔까지 그 먼 길을 내려가신다는 거예요, 니클비 씨?\" 라 크리비 양이 물었어. \"어젯밤에 얼핏 들었거든요.\"\n\n\"정말 그래요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"누군가 등을 떠밀면 어쩔 수 없잖아요. 저를 떠미는 건 다름 아닌 형편이지요. 그게 바로 그 신사의 또 다른 이름이고요.\"\n\n\"그것참, 안됐네요. 제가 할 수 있는 말은 그뿐이에요.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"당신만큼이나 어머니와 누이를 생각하면 더 그렇고요. 당신 누이는 아주 예쁜 아가씨예요, 니클비 씨. 그러니 더더욱 곁에서 지켜 줄 사람이 있어야 하지요. 제가 그 아가씨를 설득해서 한두 번 그림 모델을 서 달라고 했어요. 현관문에 걸어 둘 작품으로요. 아! 정말 사랑스러운 초상화가 나올 거예요.\" 라 크리비 양은 이렇게 말하면서 상아빛 얼굴 하나를 들어 올렸어. 그 얼굴에는 하늘색 핏줄이 또렷이 가로질러 있었지. 부인이 어찌나 흐뭇하게 그걸 바라보던지, 니콜라스는 그만 부러운 마음이 들 정도였어.\n\n\"언젠가 케이트한테 작은 친절이라도 베풀 기회가 생기거든,\" 니콜라스가 손을 내밀며 말했어. \"꼭 그래 주시리라 믿어요.\"\n\n\"그건 믿으셔도 돼요.\" 마음씨 고운 초상화가가 말했어. \"그리고 신의 가호가 있기를 빌어요, 니클비 씨. 잘되길 바랄게요.\"\n\n니콜라스는 세상 물정을 거의 몰랐지만, 그래도 이 정도는 짐작할 만큼은 알았어. 라 크리비 양에게 작은 입맞춤 하나를 건네면, 그가 두고 떠나는 식구들에게 부인이 한결 더 살갑게 대해 줄지도 모른다는 거였지. 그래서 그는 짐짓 장난스럽게 정중한 시늉을 하며 서너 번 입을 맞췄어. 라 크리비 양은 노란 터번을 매만지면서, 이런 일은 처음 들어 본다느니, 도무지 믿을 수 없는 일이라느니 하는 것 말고는 별다른 못마땅한 기색을 보이지 않았지.\n\n이렇게 뜻밖의 만남을 흐뭇하게 마무리한 니콜라스는 서둘러 집을 나섰어. 상자를 들어 줄 사람을 구하고 나니 겨우 일곱 시였지. 그래서 그는 짐꾼보다 조금 앞서서 천천히 걸어갔어. 아마 가슴속 마음만 따지면 짐꾼의 절반만큼도 가볍지 않았을 거야. 그 짐꾼은 가슴을 덮을 조끼 한 벌도 없는 데다, 다른 옷차림을 봐서는 분명 마구간에서 밤을 지새우고 펌프 앞에서 아침을 때운 듯한 행색이었는데도 말이지.\n\n거리마다, 또 거의 모든 집마다 다가오는 하루를 맞으려고 분주히 채비하는 모습을 니콜라스는 적잖은 호기심과 흥미로 바라봤어. 그리고 이따금 이런 생각도 들었지. 신분도 처지도 제각각인 그 많은 사람들이 런던에서 먹고살 길을 찾는데, 정작 자기는 그 길을 찾으러 이렇게 먼 곳까지 떠나야 한다니 좀 야박하다 싶었어. 그렇게 니콜라스는 어느새 스노 힐의 사라센스 헤드에 다다랐어. 짐꾼을 돌려보내고 상자가 마차 사무소에 무사히 맡겨진 걸 확인한 다음, 그는 스퀴어스 씨를 찾으러 커피실 안을 들여다봤지.\n\n그 학식 깊은 신사는 아침을 먹으며 앉아 있었어. 앞에는 전에 말했던 사내아이 셋과, 어제 그 만남 이후 무슨 운 좋은 우연으로 나타난 두 아이가 더 있어서, 맞은편 자리에 한 줄로 죽 앉아 있었지. 스퀴어스 씨 앞에는 자그마한 잔에 담긴 커피와 뜨거운 토스트 한 접시, 그리고 차게 식은 소고기 한 덩이가 놓여 있었어. 하지만 그는 그 순간 아이들 아침을 차려 주는 데 온 정신이 팔려 있었지.\n\n\"이게 이 펜스어치 우유 맞나, 웨이터?\" 스퀴어스 씨가 커다란 파란 머그잔을 내려다보며 물었어. 그러고는 잔을 살살 기울여서 안에 든 우유 양을 정확히 가늠해 봤지.\n\n\"그게 이 펜스어치예요, 손님.\" 웨이터가 대답했어.\n\n\"런던에서 우유라는 게 참 귀하기도 하지!\" 스퀴어스 씨가 한숨을 쉬며 말했어. \"윌리엄, 그 잔에 미지근한 물 좀 채워 주겠나?\"\n\n\"잔 꼭대기까지요, 손님?\" 웨이터가 물었어. \"그럼 우유가 다 묽어져 버릴 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그런 건 신경 쓸 거 없어.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 대꾸했어. \"이렇게 비싸게 받으니 그래도 싸지. 두툼한 버터 빵은 셋 몫으로 시킨 거 맞나?\"\n\n\"곧 나옵니다, 손님.\"\n\n\"서두를 거 없어.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어. \"시간은 넉넉하니까. 욕심을 누르거라, 얘들아. 먹을 거 앞에서 안달하지 말고.\" 이렇게 교훈을 한마디 늘어놓으면서, 스퀴어스 씨는 차게 식은 소고기를 큼지막하게 한 입 베어 물었어. 그러다 니콜라스를 알아봤지.\n\n\"앉으시오, 니클비 씨.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어. \"보다시피 우리 아침 먹는 중이오!\"\n\n니콜라스 눈에는 스퀴어스 씨 말고는 아무도 아침을 먹는 것 같지 않았어. 하지만 그는 더없이 정중하게 고개를 숙이고는, 되도록 밝은 표정을 지어 보였지.\n\n\"오! 그게 우유 탄 물인가, 윌리엄?\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어. \"아주 좋아. 이따 버터 빵 잊지 말게.\"\n\n버터 빵 얘기가 또 나오자, 다섯 아이는 눈을 잔뜩 빛내며 웨이터가 나가는 뒷모습을 눈으로 좇았어. 그사이 스퀴어스 씨는 우유 탄 물을 맛봤지.\n\n\"아!\" 그 신사가 입맛을 다시며 말했어. \"이거 정말 진하구나! 거리에 있는 그 많은 거지와 고아들을 생각해 보거라, 얘들아. 이런 거라도 얻으면 얼마나 좋아하겠니. 굶주림이란 참 끔찍한 거지요, 안 그렇소, 니클비 씨?\"\n\n\"정말 끔찍하지요, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"내가 일 번이라고 하면,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 머그잔을 아이들 앞에 놓으며 말을 이었어. \"창문 쪽에서 제일 가까운 왼쪽 끝 아이가 한 모금 마시는 거다. 그리고 내가 이 번이라고 하면 그 옆 아이가 마시고, 그렇게 오 번까지 가는 거야. 오 번이 마지막 아이고. 준비됐니?\"\n\n\"네, 선생님!\" 아이들이 모두 잔뜩 들떠서 외쳤어.\n\n\"좋아.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 태연히 자기 아침을 계속 먹으며 말했어. \"시작하라고 할 때까지 준비하고 있거라. 식욕을 다스리거라, 얘들아. 그러면 인간의 본성을 정복한 거다. 이게 바로 우리가 정신력을 길러 주는 방식이지요, 니클비 씨.\" 교장은 니콜라스 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며, 소고기와 토스트를 입에 잔뜩 문 채로 말했어.\n\n니콜라스는 뭐라고 중얼거렸어. 자기도 무슨 말을 했는지 몰랐지. 그리고 아이들은 머그잔과 버터 빵을(이때쯤엔 빵이 나와 있었어), 그리고 스퀴어스 씨가 입에 넣는 한 조각 한 조각을 번갈아 바라보면서, 기대로 애가 타서 눈을 잔뜩 부릅뜬 채 앉아 있었어.\n\n\"좋은 아침밥을 주셔서 감사합니다.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 식사를 마치고 말했어. \"일 번이 한 모금 마셔도 좋다.\"\n\n일 번은 게걸스럽게 머그잔을 움켜쥐었어. 그리고 더 마시고 싶어질 만큼만 막 마셨을 때, 스퀴어스 씨가 이 번에게 신호를 보냈지. 그러자 이 번은 바로 그 아쉬운 순간에 잔을 삼 번에게 넘겼어. 이렇게 차례차례 되풀이되다가, 우유 탄 물은 오 번에서 끝나 버렸어.\n\n\"자, 이제.\" 교장은 셋 몫인 버터 빵을 아이들 수만큼 나누면서 말했어. \"어서 아침을 먹는 게 좋을 거다. 나팔이 일이 분 안에 울릴 테고, 그러면 모두 그만 먹어야 하니까.\"\n\n이렇게 먹어도 좋다는 허락이 떨어지자, 아이들은 게걸스럽게, 그리고 죽을 둥 살 둥 허겁지겁 먹기 시작했어. 그사이 교장은(식사를 마치고 기분이 한껏 좋아져서) 포크로 이를 쑤시며 빙그레 웃으며 지켜봤지. 얼마 지나지 않아 나팔 소리가 들려왔어.\n\n\"오래 안 걸릴 줄 알았지.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 벌떡 일어나 좌석 밑에서 작은 바구니를 꺼내며 말했어. \"미처 못 먹은 건 여기 담거라, 얘들아! 길에서 먹어야 할 테니까!\"\n\n니콜라스는 이렇게 알뜰한 처사에 적잖이 놀랐어. 하지만 그걸 곱씹어 볼 틈도 없었지. 아이들을 마차 위로 올려 보내야 했고, 아이들 상자도 꺼내서 실어야 했고, 스퀴어스 씨 짐도 짐칸에 잘 넣어 두는 걸 챙겨야 했거든. 그리고 이 모든 일이 다 그의 몫이었어. 그가 이 일들을 마무리하느라 한창 열을 내며 부산을 떨고 있을 때, 삼촌인 랄프 니클비 씨가 그에게 말을 걸어왔어.\n\n\"오! 여기 있었구나.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"네 어머니랑 누이가 여기 와 있다.\"\n\n\"어디요?\" 니콜라스가 황급히 둘레를 둘러보며 외쳤어.\n\n\"여기!\" 삼촌이 대답했어. \"돈이 너무 많아서 쓸 데가 없는지, 내가 올 때 보니 삯마차 값을 치르고 있더구나.\"\n\n\"이 애가 우리 곁을 떠나기 전에 못 보고 보낼까 봐 겁이 났어요.\" 니클비 부인이 아들을 끌어안으며 말했어. 마차 마당에서 무심히 쳐다보는 사람들 따위는 아랑곳하지 않았지.\n\n\"좋습니다, 형수님.\" 랄프가 대꾸했어. \"물론 형수님이 알아서 잘 판단하셨겠지요. 저는 그저 형수님이 삯마차 값을 치르고 있더라고 말했을 뿐입니다. 저는 삯마차 값을 치르는 법이 없어요, 형수님. 빌려 타지를 않으니까요. 제 돈 내고 삯마차를 타 본 게 삼십 년 동안 한 번도 없고, 앞으로 삼십 년을 더 산다 해도 그럴 일은 없길 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"이 애를 못 보고 보냈으면 나는 평생 나 자신을 용서 못 했을 거예요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"가엾은 것, 아침도 못 먹고 떠나는구나. 우리 마음 아플까 봐 그랬다니!\"\n\n\"그것참 갸륵하기도 하지요.\" 랄프가 잔뜩 짜증을 내며 말했어. \"제가 처음 일을 시작했을 때는요, 형수님, 매일 아침 시내로 걸어가면서 일 페니짜리 빵 한 덩이랑 반 페니어치 우유로 아침을 때웠습니다. 그건 어떻게 생각하십니까, 형수님? 아침밥이라니! 흥!\"\n\n\"자, 니클비.\" 그 순간 외투 단추를 채우며 다가온 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어. \"자네는 뒤쪽에 타는 게 좋겠어. 저 녀석들 중 하나라도 떨어지면, 그게 일 년에 이십 파운드가 날아가는 거니까 말이야.\"\n\n\"오빠.\" 케이트가 오빠의 팔을 건드리며 속삭였어. \"저 천박한 사람은 누구야?\"\n\n\"응!\" 랄프가 으르렁거렸어. 그의 빠른 귀가 그 물음을 놓치지 않았던 거야. \"스퀴어스 씨를 소개받고 싶으냐, 얘야?\"\n\n\"저 사람이 교장이라고요! 아뇨, 삼촌. 아, 아니에요!\" 케이트가 움찔 뒤로 물러서며 대답했어.\n\n\"내 분명히 네가 그렇게 말하는 걸 들었는데, 얘야.\" 랄프가 그 차갑고 비꼬는 투로 되받았어. \"스퀴어스 씨, 여기 제 조카입니다. 니콜라스의 누이지요!\"\n\n\"만나서 정말 반갑습니다, 아가씨.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 모자를 한두 치쯤 들어 올리며 말했어. \"우리 스퀴어스 부인이 여자애들도 받았더라면, 아가씨를 선생으로 모셨으면 좋았을 텐데요. 하기야, 그랬다간 부인이 질투를 부릴지도 모르겠군요. 하하하!\"\n\n두더보이즈 홀의 주인이 그 순간 조수의 가슴속에 무슨 일이 일어나고 있었는지 알았더라면, 적잖이 놀라며 깨달았을 거야. 자기가 평생 처음으로 흠씬 두들겨 맞기 직전이라는 걸 말이지. 케이트 니클비는 오빠의 감정을 누구보다 빠르게 알아채고는, 그를 슬며시 옆으로 데려갔어. 그래서 스퀴어스 씨는 그 사실을 아주 불쾌한 방식으로 실감하는 걸 면할 수 있었지.\n\n\"오빠.\" 아가씨가 말했어. \"저 사람은 도대체 누구야? 오빠가 가는 곳은 대체 어떤 데일까?\"\n\n\"나도 잘 모르겠어, 케이트.\" 니콜라스가 누이의 손을 꼭 잡으며 대답했어. \"요크셔 사람들이 좀 거칠고 안 다듬어진 모양이지. 그뿐이야.\"\n\n\"하지만 저 사람은.\" 케이트가 따지듯 말했어.\n\n\"내 고용주야. 아니면 주인이라고 해야 하나, 뭐 적당한 이름이 뭐든 간에.\" 니콜라스가 얼른 대답했어. \"저 사람 거친 행동을 언짢게 받아들인 내가 바보였어. 저쪽에서 이리 보고 있네. 나도 이제 자리에 가 있어야겠다. 잘 있어, 사랑하는 누이야. 안녕! 어머니, 언젠가 우리 다시 만날 날을 기다려 주세요! 삼촌, 안녕히 계세요! 해 주신 모든 일과 앞으로 해 주실 모든 일에 진심으로 감사드려요. 다 됐습니다, 선생님!\"\n\n이렇게 황급히 작별 인사를 하고는, 니콜라스는 날렵하게 자기 자리로 올라가 손을 흔들었어. 마치 가슴까지 함께 따라가는 것처럼 씩씩하게 말이야.\n\n이 순간, 마부와 차장이 출발하기 전 마지막으로 운임 명세서를 두고 머리를 맞대고 있었어. 짐꾼들은 마지막 남은 육 펜스를 어떻게든 짜내려 하고, 떠돌이 신문팔이들은 마지막으로 조간신문을 사라고 들이밀고, 말들은 마지막으로 안달하듯 마구를 절그럭거렸지. 그때 니콜라스는 누군가 자기 다리를 슬며시 잡아당기는 걸 느꼈어. 내려다보니 거기 뉴먼 노그스가 서 있었지. 그는 니콜라스 손에 꾀죄죄한 편지 한 통을 쥐여 주었어.\n\n\"이게 뭐예요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"쉿!\" 노그스가 대꾸하며, 조금 떨어진 곳에서 스퀴어스 씨에게 뭔가 진지하게 몇 마디를 하고 있는 랄프 니클비 씨를 가리켰어. \"받아요. 읽어요. 아무도 몰라요. 그게 다예요.\"\n\n\"잠깐만요!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어.\n\n\"안 돼요.\" 노그스가 대답했어.\n\n니콜라스가 다시 잠깐만요, 하고 외쳤지만, 뉴먼 노그스는 이미 사라지고 없었어.\n\n잠깐의 부산함, 쾅 닫히는 마차 문, 한쪽으로 기우뚱하는 마차. 덩치 큰 마부와 그보다 더 덩치 큰 차장이 자리로 기어 올라가면서 그렇게 흔들렸지. 다 됐다는 외침, 나팔의 몇 가락, 아래쪽에서 슬픔에 잠긴 두 얼굴과 랄프 니클비 씨의 굳은 얼굴을 황급히 한 번 본 것. 그러고는 마차도 떠나 버렸어. 스미스필드의 돌바닥 위를 덜컹덜컹 굴러가면서 말이야.\n\n아이들 다리가 너무 짧아서 앉았을 때 발이 어디에도 닿지 않았어. 그래서 아이들 몸이 마차 밖으로 튕겨 나갈 위험이 시시각각 컸지. 그러니 니콜라스는 돌바닥 위를 지나는 내내 아이들을 붙잡느라 정신이 없었어. 손으로 끙끙 애쓰는 데다 이 일에 따른 마음 졸임까지 겹쳐서, 마차가 이즐링턴의 피콕에 멈춰 섰을 때 그는 적잖이 안도했어. 그리고 더더욱 마음이 놓인 건, 아주 기분 좋아 보이는 얼굴에 혈색이 좋은 다부진 신사가 뒤쪽으로 올라타서 좌석의 나머지 한 귀퉁이를 차지하겠다고 했을 때였지.\n\n\"이 꼬마들 몇 명을 가운데로 넣으면,\" 새로 온 사람이 말했어. \"혹시 잠들어도 더 안전하지 않겠소? 응?\"\n\n\"그렇게 해 주시면, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 대답했어. \"딱 좋겠군요. 니클비 씨, 저 녀석들 셋을 자네랑 이 신사분 사이에 앉히게. 벨링이랑 막내 스놀리는 나랑 차장 사이에 앉으면 되고. 아이 셋은요,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 낯선 신사에게 설명하듯 말했어. \"둘 몫으로 셉니다.\"\n\n\"전혀 이의 없습니다, 정말로요.\" 혈색 좋은 신사가 말했어. \"제 형제 하나는 자기 아이 여섯을 이 나라 어느 푸줏간이든 빵집이든 둘 몫으로 적어 넣어도 마다하지 않을 사람이거든요. 정말이지요. 마다하기는커녕요.\"\n\n\"아이가 여섯이라고요, 선생님?\" 스퀴어스 씨가 외쳤어.\n\n\"네, 그것도 다 사내아이지요.\" 낯선 신사가 대답했어.\n\n\"니클비 씨.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 잔뜩 서둘러 말했어. \"그 바구니 좀 잡아 주게. 선생님, 명함 한 장 드리지요. 그 여섯 아이를 깨인 정신과 너그럽고 도덕적인 방식으로 길러 줄 곳입니다. 그 점은 한 치도 틀림없고요. 한 명당 일 년에 이십 기니, 이십 기니입니다, 선생님. 아니면 여섯을 다 한꺼번에 평균을 내서, 통틀어 일 년에 백 파운드로 해 드리지요.\"\n\n\"오!\" 신사가 명함을 흘긋 보며 말했어. \"여기 적힌 그 스퀴어스 씨가 바로 당신인가 봅니다?\"\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다, 선생님.\" 그 훌륭한 교육자가 대답했어. \"왁퍼드 스퀴어스가 제 이름이고, 그 이름을 부끄러워하기는커녕입니다. 이 아이들이 제 학생 중 일부고요, 선생님. 저쪽은 제 조수 중 한 사람입니다, 선생님. 니클비 씨인데, 신사의 아들이고 훌륭한 학자지요. 수학, 고전, 상업 다 통달했고요. 우리 학교에선 일을 어중간하게 하는 법이 없습니다. 우리 아이들은 온갖 학문을 다 배워 갑니다, 선생님. 비용은 따지지도 않고요. 게다가 친아버지 같은 보살핌에다 빨래까지 다 포함입니다.\"\n\n\"이거 정말이지,\" 신사가 반쯤 웃으며, 그리고 절반 넘게 놀란 표정으로 니콜라스를 흘긋 보며 말했어. \"굉장한 이점들이군요, 그래요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 말씀하셔도 됩니다, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 외투 주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣으며 대꾸했어. \"더없이 나무랄 데 없는 신원 보증을 받고, 또 요구합니다. 저는 분기마다 오 파운드 오 실링을 책임지고 낼 사람이 보증을 서지 않으면, 어떤 아이도 받지 않습니다. 선생님이 무릎을 꿇고, 얼굴에 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 받아 달라고 빌어도, 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"무척 사려 깊으시군요.\" 승객이 말했어.\n\n\"사려 깊은 게 제 가장 큰 목표이자 목적이지요, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 대꾸했어. \"스놀리, 너 이 작은 녀석아, 이를 딱딱 부딪치고 추워서 덜덜 떠는 거 안 그만두면, 한 삼십 초 안에 호된 매질로 몸을 따뜻하게 해 주마.\"\n\n\"단단히 붙잡으시오, 손님들.\" 차장이 기어 올라오며 말했어.\n\n\"뒤쪽 다 됐나, 딕?\" 마부가 외쳤어.\n\n\"다 됐어.\" 대답이 돌아왔지. \"자, 떠난다!\" 그리고 마차는 정말로 떠났어. 차장의 나팔이 요란하게 한바탕 울리는 가운데, 피콕에 모여 있던 마차와 마차 끄는 말의 감정가들이 모두 차분히 고개를 끄덕이는 가운데 말이야. 특히 마구간 일꾼들이 그랬지. 그들은 팔에 천을 걸친 채 서서 마차가 사라질 때까지 지켜봤어. 그러고는 흐뭇한 얼굴로 어슬렁어슬렁 마구간 쪽으로 걸어가며, 멋진 행차 솜씨를 두고 투박한 칭찬을 이러쿵저러쿵 늘어놓았지.\n\n차장은(요크셔 출신의 다부진 노인이었어) 숨이 다 가쁘도록 나팔을 불고 나서, 그걸 마차 옆에 일부러 매달아 둔 작은 터널 같은 바구니에 도로 넣었어. 그러고는 가슴팍과 어깨를 마구 두들겨 대더니, 날이 어지간히 춥다고 한마디 했지. 그런 다음 한 사람 한 사람에게 따로 물었어. 끝까지 가는 거냐고, 안 그러면 어디까지 가느냐고 말이야. 그 물음에 다들 시원하게 대답하자, 그는 어젯밤 그 눈 때문에 길이 꽤 험할 거라고 짐작했어. 그러고는 손님들 중에 코담뱃갑을 가진 분이 있는지 외람되게 물어봤지. 마침 아무도 없자, 그는 알 듯 모를 듯한 표정으로 한마디 했어. 지난주에 그랜섬으로 내려가던 의사 양반이 코담배는 눈에 안 좋다고 하더라는 거야. 하지만 자기는 한 번도 그런 적이 없었다며, 사람은 누구나 제가 겪은 대로 말해야 한다고 했지. 아무도 이 말에 토를 달지 않자, 그는 모자에서 작은 갈색 종이 꾸러미를 하나 꺼내더니, 뿔테 안경을 끼고는(글씨가 비뚤배뚤했거든) 받는 사람 주소를 예닐곱 번이나 읽었어. 그러고 나서 꾸러미를 도로 제자리에 넣고, 안경도 다시 집어넣고는, 한 사람씩 차례로 빤히 쳐다봤지. 그런 다음 기운을 차리려는 듯 나팔을 또 한 번 불었어. 그리고 이제 평소 늘어놓던 이야깃거리가 다 떨어지자, 그 많은 외투를 껴입은 채로나마 팔짱을 끼고는 엄숙한 침묵에 빠져들었어. 마차가 굴러가는 동안 사방에서 눈에 들어오는 낯익은 풍경들을 무심히 바라보면서 말이야. 그가 유일하게 관심을 두는 건 말과 떼 지어 가는 소들이었지. 길에서 그것들을 지나칠 때마다 그는 평가하는 듯한 눈빛으로 꼼꼼히 뜯어봤어.\n\n날씨는 지독하게, 살을 에듯이 추웠어. 이따금 눈도 펑펑 내렸고, 바람은 견딜 수 없을 만큼 매서웠지. 스퀴어스 씨는 정거장마다 거의 빠짐없이 내렸어. 다리 좀 펴겠다는 거였지. 그리고 그렇게 다녀올 때마다 늘 코가 새빨개져서 돌아와서는 곧장 잠을 청했으니, 그가 그 과정에서 큰 덕을 봤다고 짐작할 만했어. 어린 학생들은 남은 아침밥으로 기운을 차렸고, 거기다 스퀴어스 씨가 가지고 다니는 묘한 강장주를 작은 잔으로 몇 번 더 마시고는 기운이 좀 났지. 그 강장주는 꼭 토스트 우린 물을 실수로 브랜디 병에 넣은 것 같은 맛이었어. 아이들은 잠들었다가 깨고, 덜덜 떨고, 또 기분 내키는 대로 울었어. 니콜라스와 그 마음씨 좋은 신사는 나눌 이야깃거리가 어찌나 많던지, 서로 이야기를 주고받고 아이들을 달래느라 이런 고약한 상황에서도 시간이 그나마 가장 빨리 흘러갔어.\n\n그렇게 하루가 저물어 갔어. 이튼 슬로컴에서는 괜찮은 마차 손님용 저녁이 나왔어. 마부석에 앉은 사람, 앞쪽 바깥 자리 네 사람, 안쪽 한 사람, 니콜라스, 그 마음씨 좋은 신사, 그리고 스퀴어스 씨가 그 저녁을 함께 먹었지. 그사이 다섯 아이는 불가에서 몸을 녹이도록 두고, 샌드위치로 요기를 시켰어. 한두 정거장 더 가자 등불이 켜졌어. 그리고 길가 여관에서 무척 까다로운 부인 하나를 태우느라 한바탕 소동이 벌어졌지. 부인은 온갖 종류의 망토며 자그마한 꾸러미를 끝도 없이 들고 있었어. 그러고는 바깥 자리 사람들 다 들으라는 듯이 큰 소리로 한탄했지. 자기를 데려가기로 했던 자기 마차가 안 왔다고 말이야. 그러고는 차장한테, 오는 길에 초록색 사륜마차가 보이거든 빠짐없이 다 세워 달라고 엄숙히 약속하게 했어. 그런데 캄캄한 밤인 데다 차장은 얼굴을 반대쪽으로 향하고 앉아 있었으니, 그 양반은 갖은 열렬한 맹세를 곁들여 그러겠노라 떠맡았지. 마지막으로 그 까다로운 부인은 안쪽에 신사가 혼자 타고 있는 걸 알고는, 손가방에 넣고 다니던 작은 등불을 켰어. 그리고 한참 애를 쓴 끝에 부인을 안에 들여보내자, 말들은 경쾌한 구보로 내달리기 시작했고, 마차는 다시 한번 빠르게 움직였지.\n\n밤과 눈이 함께 몰려왔어. 둘 다 어지간히 음산했지. 바람이 윙윙거리는 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 들리지 않았어. 바퀴 소리도, 말발굽 디디는 소리도, 땅을 두껍게 덮은 눈에 다 묻혀 들리지 않았거든. 그 눈은 시시각각 빠르게 쌓여 가고 있었지. 마차가 지나갈 때 스탬퍼드의 거리는 텅 비어 있었어. 그리고 그 도시의 오래된 교회들이 하얗게 덮인 땅 위로 어둡게 잔뜩 찌푸린 채 솟아 있었지. 이십 마일을 더 가서, 앞쪽 바깥 자리 승객 둘이 영국에서 제일가는 여관 중 한 곳에 다다른 걸 슬기롭게 이용해, 그랜섬의 조지에서 밤을 묵으려고 들어갔어. 남은 사람들은 외투며 망토를 더 단단히 여미고는, 도시의 불빛과 온기를 뒤로한 채, 짐에 기대어 몸을 누이고, 반쯤 억눌린 신음을 연신 내뱉으며, 탁 트인 들판을 가로질러 휘몰아치는 살을 에는 바람을 다시 맞을 채비를 했어.\n\n그랜섬을 벗어나 한 정거장쯤, 그러니까 그랜섬과 뉴어크의 중간쯤 왔을 때였어. 잠깐 잠들었던 니콜라스가 갑자기 격하게 흔들리는 바람에 화들짝 깼어. 하마터면 자리에서 튕겨 나갈 뻔했지. 난간을 움켜쥐고 보니, 마차가 한쪽으로 크게 기울어 있었어. 그래도 말들은 여전히 마차를 앞으로 끌고 가는 중이었지. 그리고 말들이 마구 날뛰는 데다 안에 탄 부인이 째지게 비명을 질러 대니 정신이 하나도 없었어. 그래서 뛰어내려야 할지 말지 잠깐 머뭇거리는 사이, 마차가 스르르 옆으로 넘어가 버렸어. 그러고는 니콜라스를 길바닥으로 내동댕이쳐서, 더 망설일 거리도 없게 만들어 버렸지.\n\n# CHAPTER 6\n\n\"워, 워!\" 차장이 순식간에 벌떡 일어나더니 앞장선 말들 머리 쪽으로 달려가며 소리쳤다. \"거기 손 좀 빌려줄 신사분 안 계시오? 가만있어, 이놈! 워, 워!\"\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 니콜라스가 잠에 취한 눈을 들며 물었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이라니, 하룻밤치고는 일이 차고 넘치지요.\" 차장이 대꾸했다. \"저 눈깔 흰 밤색 말 좀 보소, 마차가 엎어진 게 무슨 자랑이라도 되는 양 미쳐 날뛰지 뭐요. 자, 손 좀 안 빌려주겠소? 빌어먹을, 내 뼈가 다 부러졌어도 내가 했을 거요.\"\n\n\"여기 있소!\" 니콜라스가 비틀거리며 일어섰다. \"준비됐소. 잠깐 정신이 좀 나갔을 뿐이오.\"\n\n\"꽉 붙들어요.\" 차장이 외쳤다. \"내가 끈을 자를 동안. 어떻게든 매달리시오. 잘했소, 양반. 그렇지. 자, 이제 놔요. 빌어먹을, 저놈들 알아서 집으로 잘도 돌아갈 거요!\"\n\n과연 말들은 풀려나기가 무섭게 어슬렁어슬렁 방금 떠나온 마구간 쪽으로 되돌아 달려갔다. 그곳은 채 일 마일도 안 되는 뒤편에 있었다.\n\n\"뿔나팔 불 줄 아시오?\" 차장이 마차 등 하나를 떼어 내며 물었다.\n\n\"불 줄 알 것 같소.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 저기 땅바닥에 떨어진 등에다 대고 한번 힘껏 불어 보시오. 죽은 사람도 깨울 만큼 말이오.\" 차장이 말했다. \"그동안 나는 안에서 꽥꽥대는 소리 좀 잠재울 테니. 가요, 가. 그렇게 소리 좀 지르지 마쇼, 부인.\"\n\n차장은 그렇게 말하며 마차의 맨 위쪽 문을 비틀어 열기 시작했다. 그사이 니콜라스는 뿔나팔을 쥐고는, 인간의 귀가 일찍이 들어 본 적 없는 가장 별난 연주로 사방의 메아리를 깨웠다. 그래도 효과는 있었다. 사고로 쓰러진 사람들을 깨웠을 뿐 아니라 그들을 도와줄 손길까지 불러 모았으니까. 멀리서 불빛이 반짝였고, 사람들이 벌써 움직이고 있었다.\n\n사실 승객들이 채 다 모이기도 전에 한 남자가 말을 타고 달려왔다. 그러고는 꼼꼼히 살펴본 끝에 이런 사정이 드러났다. 마차 안에 있던 부인은 등을 깨뜨렸고, 신사는 머리를 깨뜨렸다. 앞쪽 바깥자리에 앉았던 두 사람은 눈에 멍이 든 정도로 끝났고, 마부석 옆자리 사람은 코피가 났으며, 마부는 관자놀이에 타박상을 입었다. 스퀴어스 씨는 등에 여행 가방만 한 멍이 들었고, 나머지 승객들은 아무 데도 다치지 않았다. 그들이 처박힌 눈더미가 푹신했던 덕분이었다. 이 사실들이 다 밝혀지자마자 부인은 금방이라도 기절할 듯한 기색을 여러 번 내비쳤다. 그러나 만약 기절하면 어느 신사분 어깨에 업혀 가장 가까운 주막까지 가야 한다는 말을 미리 듣고는, 신중하게 마음을 고쳐먹고 다른 사람들과 함께 걸어서 돌아갔다.\n\n주막에 닿아 보니 외딴곳이었고, 방이라고 할 만한 게 변변치 않았다. 그곳의 살림이라곤 모래를 깔아 둔 바닥에 의자 한두 개가 놓인 공동 거실 하나가 전부였다. 그래도 큼직한 장작 한 다발과 석탄을 넉넉히 불에 쌓아 올리니 사정은 금세 나아졌다. 그리고 그들이 사고 자국 가운데 지울 수 있는 건 다 씻어 냈을 무렵, 방은 따뜻하고 환해졌다. 바깥의 춥고 캄캄한 것과 견주면 더없이 반가운 변화였다.\n\n\"자, 니클비 씨.\" 스퀴어스가 가장 따뜻한 구석으로 슬그머니 파고들며 말했다. \"그 말들 붙잡은 건 아주 잘한 일이오. 내가 제때 정신을 차렸으면 나도 그렇게 했겠지만, 어쨌든 자네가 해 줘서 참 다행이오. 아주 잘했소. 아주 잘했어.\"\n\n\"어찌나 잘했는지,\" 스퀴어스가 내세우는 거들먹거리는 말투가 영 마뜩잖아 보이는, 쾌활한 얼굴의 신사가 말했다. \"그때 말들이 단단히 붙들리지 않았더라면, 당신은 아이들 가르칠 머리도 남아나지 못했을 거요.\"\n\n이 말에 니콜라스가 보여 준 재빠른 솜씨를 두고 이야기가 오갔고, 그는 칭찬과 찬사 세례에 파묻혔다.\n\n\"물론 무사히 빠져나와서 정말 기쁘오.\" 스퀴어스가 한마디 했다. \"위험에서 벗어나면 누구든 기쁜 법이지. 하지만 만약 내가 맡은 아이들 가운데 하나라도 다쳤더라면, 그러니까 이 어린 녀석들 중 누구 하나라도 받았을 때처럼 멀쩡하게 부모 품에 돌려보내지 못하게 됐더라면, 내 심정이 어땠겠소? 차라리 그 바퀴가 내 머리 위로 떨어지는 게 훨씬 나았을 거요.\"\n\n\"이 애들이 다 형제인가요, 선생님?\" '데이비' 등, 그러니까 안전등을 들고 왔던 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"어떤 의미에서는 그렇지요, 부인.\" 스퀴어스가 명함을 찾으려고 외투 주머니를 뒤지며 대답했다. \"이 애들은 다 똑같이 부모처럼 아끼는 손길 아래 있으니까요. 스퀴어스 부인과 나는 이 애들 하나하나에게 어머니이자 아버지랍니다. 니클비 씨, 부인께 이 명함을 건네드리고, 이건 신사분께 드리게나. 어쩌면 학교에 보내고 싶어 할 부모를 알고 계실지도 모르니까.\"\n\n이런 말을 늘어놓으며, 거저 광고할 기회는 절대 놓치지 않는 스퀴어스 씨는 무릎에 손을 얹고는, 지을 수 있는 한 가장 자애로운 표정으로 학생들을 바라보았다. 그사이 니콜라스는 부끄러움에 얼굴을 붉히며 시키는 대로 명함을 돌렸다.\n\n\"마차가 엎어진 일로 불편한 데는 없으신지요, 부인?\" 쾌활한 얼굴의 신사가, 자비롭게도 화제를 바꿔 주려는 듯 까다로운 부인에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"몸이 불편한 데는 없어요.\" 부인이 대답했다.\n\n\"마음이 불편하지도 않으시길 바랍니다만.\"\n\n\"그 이야기는 제 마음을 몹시 아프게 하는군요, 선생님.\" 부인이 격한 감정을 담아 대꾸했다. \"신사로서 부탁드리니, 그 일은 다시 입에 올리지 말아 주세요.\"\n\n\"이런.\" 쾌활한 얼굴의 신사가 더욱 쾌활해진 얼굴로 말했다. \"저는 그저 여쭤보려던 것뿐인데요.\"\n\n\"아무것도 묻지 않으셨으면 해요.\" 부인이 말했다. \"안 그러면 저는 다른 신사분들의 보호에 기댈 수밖에 없겠어요. 주인장, 부디 아이 하나를 시켜서 문밖을 지키게 해 줘요. 그리고 초록색 마차가 그랜섬 쪽으로 지나가거든 당장 세우라고요.\"\n\n주막 사람들은 이 부탁에 단단히 압도된 게 분명했다. 그리고 부인이 그 기다리는 초록색 마차를 알아볼 표시라며, 마부석에는 금실 두른 모자를 쓴 마부가 앉아 있고 뒤쪽에는 십중팔구 비단 양말을 신은 하인이 서 있을 거라고 아이에게 일러두자, 마음씨 좋은 주막 안주인의 정성은 곱절로 늘어났다. 마부석 옆자리에 앉았던 승객까지 그 분위기에 물들었다. 그러더니 놀랄 만큼 공손해져서는, 이 동네에 상류 사회가 꽤 있지 않느냐고 대뜸 물었다. 부인은 그렇다고, 있다고 대답했는데, 그 말투가 자기야말로 그 사회의 맨 꼭대기, 정점에서 노니는 사람이라는 뜻을 충분히 풍겼다.\n\n\"차장이 마차를 새로 구하러 말을 타고 그랜섬까지 갔으니,\" 모두가 한동안 말없이 불가에 둘러앉아 있다가 그 사람 좋은 신사가 입을 열었다. \"아무리 빨라도 두 시간은 걸릴 테니, 따뜻한 펀치 한 사발 어떻소? 어떻게 생각하시오, 선생?\"\n\n이 물음은 머리를 다친 마차 안 승객을 향한 것이었다. 그는 아주 점잖은 차림에 상복을 입은 남자였다. 중년을 넘기지는 않았지만 머리는 희끗했다. 근심이나 슬픔 때문에 일찍 센 모양이었다. 그는 흔쾌히 그 제안을 받아들였고, 제안을 꺼낸 사람의 꾸밈없는 선량함에 마음이 끌린 듯했다.\n\n펀치가 다 되자 이 사람 좋은 신사는 자청해서 술 따르는 일을 맡았다. 그러고는 모두에게 한 잔씩 돌린 뒤, 요크의 옛 유적 이야기로 화제를 이끌었다. 그와 희끗한 머리의 신사 둘 다 그 방면에 밝아 보였다. 이 화제가 시들해지자, 그는 빙긋 웃으며 희끗한 머리의 신사에게 노래를 부를 줄 아느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"부를 줄 모릅니다, 정말로.\" 희끗한 머리의 신사도 마주 웃으며 대답했다.\n\n\"그것참 아쉽군요.\" 쾌활한 얼굴의 임자가 말했다. \"여기 누구 시간 좀 가볍게 보내게 노래 한 곡 불러 줄 분 안 계시오?\"\n\n승객들은 너 나 할 것 없이 한사코 못 부른다고, 부를 수 있으면 좋겠다고, 책 없이는 무슨 노래든 가사가 도무지 생각나지 않는다고, 그런 식으로 손사래를 쳤다.\n\n\"어쩌면 부인께서는 마다하지 않으실지도 모르지요.\" 좌장은 더없이 정중하게, 눈에 짓궂은 빛을 반짝이며 말했다. \"런던에서 갓 올린 오페라에 나오는 이탈리아 노래 한 소절쯤이면 다들 더없이 반길 텐데요.\"\n\n부인이 황송하게도 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고 그저 경멸하듯 고개를 홱 젖히며, 초록색 마차가 안 온다는 둥 또 무어라 놀라움을 중얼거리자, 한두 사람이 좌장 본인에게 모두를 위해 한번 나서 보는 게 어떻겠느냐고 권했다.\n\n\"할 수 있으면 하지요.\" 사람 좋은 얼굴의 신사가 말했다. \"이런 경우든 다른 경우든, 서로 낯선 사람들이 뜻하지 않게 한자리에 모이게 되면, 이 작은 모임 모두를 위해서 되도록 서로 즐겁게 해 주려고 애써야 한다는 게 내 생각이오.\"\n\n\"그 말씀이 어떤 경우에든 좀 더 널리 지켜졌으면 좋겠군요.\" 희끗한 머리의 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"그 말 들으니 기쁘오.\" 상대가 받았다. \"그럼 노래는 못 하신다니, 이야기라도 하나 들려주시겠소?\"\n\n\"아니, 그건 제가 청해야 할 말인데요.\"\n\n\"당신 다음에 나도 기꺼이 하리다.\"\n\n\"정말이오?\" 희끗한 머리의 신사가 빙긋 웃으며 말했다. \"좋소, 그럽시다. 다만 내 생각의 흐름이 여기서 보내실 시간을 가볍게 해 주지는 못할 것 같아 걱정이오. 하지만 자초한 일이니, 판단은 여러분 몫이오. 우리가 방금 요크 대성당 이야기를 했지요. 내 이야기도 거기에 얼마쯤 얽힌 거라오. 제목을 이렇게 붙입시다.\n\n# THE FIVE SISTERS OF YORK\n\n요크의 다섯 자매\n다른 승객들이 좋다는 듯 웅성거리는 사이, 까다로운 부인은 아무도 모르게 펀치를 한 잔 들이켰다. 그러고 나서 희끗한 머리의 신사는 이렇게 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"아주 오래전 일이오. 그때만 해도 십오 세기가 채 두 해도 안 됐고, 영국의 왕좌에는 헨리 사세가 앉아 있었지요. 그 무렵 유서 깊은 도시 요크에, 내 이야기의 주인공인 다섯 처녀 자매가 살고 있었소.\n\n이 다섯 자매는 하나같이 빼어나게 아름다웠소. 맏이는 스물세 살이었고, 둘째는 그보다 한 살 아래, 셋째는 둘째보다 한 살 아래, 넷째는 셋째보다 한 살 아래였소. 키가 크고 기품 있는 자태에, 검은 눈은 반짝였고 머리칼은 칠흑 같았지요. 몸짓 하나하나에 위엄과 우아함이 깃들어 있었고, 그 대단한 미모의 명성은 온 고장에 퍼졌소.\n\n하지만 위의 네 자매가 사랑스러웠다 한들, 막내는 또 얼마나 아름다웠겠소. 열여섯의 고운 처녀였지요. 과일에 부드럽게 피어오른 발그레한 빛깔도, 꽃에 섬세하게 입혀진 색채도, 그 다정한 얼굴에 어우러진 장미와 백합의 빛깔보다, 또 그 깊고 푸른 눈동자보다 더 곱지는 못했소. 우아하게 흐드러진 포도 덩굴도, 그 이마를 감싸고 노니는 짙은 갈색 머리채만큼 고아하지는 않았지요.\n\n우리 모두가 젊고 아름다운 이들의 가슴속에서 그토록 가볍게 뛰는 그런 마음을 지녔다면, 이 땅이 얼마나 천국 같을까요! 우리 몸은 늙고 시들어 가도 마음만은 처음의 젊음과 싱그러움을 간직할 수 있다면, 우리의 슬픔과 고통이 다 무슨 소용일까요! 하지만 어린 시절 마음에 새겨진 그 희미한 에덴의 모습은, 세상과 거칠게 부대끼는 가운데 닳고 쓸려 이내 사라지고 마오. 너무도 흔히, 슬픈 빈자리 말고는 아무것도 남기지 않은 채 말이오.\n\n이 고운 처녀의 마음은 기쁨과 즐거움으로 뛰놀았소. 언니들을 향한 지극한 정과, 자연의 온갖 아름다운 것을 향한 뜨거운 사랑이 그 순수한 애정의 전부였지요. 그 흥겨운 목소리와 명랑한 웃음소리는 집 안에서 가장 달콤한 음악이었소. 그 처녀야말로 그 집의 빛이자 생명이었지요. 정원에서 가장 환한 꽃들은 그 손길에 길러졌고, 새장 속 새들은 그 목소리를 들으면 노래했다가 그 달콤함이 사라지면 시름시름 앓았소. 앨리스, 사랑스러운 앨리스. 그 부드러운 마법이 미치는 곳에서, 살아 있는 그 무엇이 그를 사랑하지 않을 수 있었겠소!\n\n이제 이 자매들이 살던 곳을 아무리 찾아본들 헛수고일 거요. 그들의 이름마저 잊혔고, 먼지투성이 골동품 학자들이나 옛 우화처럼 그들을 이야기할 뿐이니까. 하지만 그들은 낡은 나무집에서 살았소. 그 시절 기준으로도 낡은 집이었지요. 처마가 길게 드리워지고, 투박하게 새긴 떡갈나무 발코니가 달린 집이었소. 그 집은 아늑한 과수원 안에 서 있었고, 거친 돌담으로 둘러싸여 있었지요. 그 담에서라면 솜씨 좋은 궁수가 성 메리 수도원까지 화살을 날릴 만했소. 그 옛 수도원은 그때 한창 번성했고, 다섯 자매는 그 너른 영지 안에 살며 해마다 그곳에 딸린 성 베네딕트 수도회의 검은 옷 수도사들에게 세를 바쳤소.\n\n화창하고 햇살 좋은 어느 여름날 아침이었소. 그 검은 옷 수도사 가운데 하나가 수도원 정문을 나서더니 고운 자매들의 집 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼지요. 위로는 하늘이 푸르렀고, 아래로는 땅이 푸르렀소. 강물은 햇빛 속에서 다이아몬드를 깔아 놓은 길처럼 반짝였지요. 새들은 그늘진 나무에서 노래를 쏟아 냈고, 종달새는 일렁이는 밀밭 위로 높이 솟아올랐으며, 벌레들의 깊은 윙윙거림이 공기를 가득 채웠소. 모든 것이 즐겁고 환하게 웃고 있었지요. 하지만 그 거룩한 수도사는 눈을 땅에 박은 채 침울하게 걸어갔소. 땅의 아름다움이란 한낱 숨결에 지나지 않고, 사람이란 한낱 그림자에 불과하지요. 거룩한 설교자가 그 둘 가운데 어느 쪽엔들 마음을 둘 까닭이 있겠소?\n\n그렇게 눈을 땅에 박은 채, 아니면 앞을 가로막는 것에 걸려 넘어지지 않을 만큼만 겨우 눈을 들고서, 그 수도사는 자매들 과수원 담에 난 작은 쪽문에 이를 때까지 천천히 나아갔소. 그러고는 그리로 들어가 등 뒤로 문을 닫았지요. 몇 걸음 채 옮기기도 전에, 도란도란 정담을 나누는 부드러운 목소리와 명랑한 웃음소리가 그의 귀에 와닿았소. 평소의 겸손한 버릇보다 좀 더 높이 눈을 들자, 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 다섯 자매가 풀밭에 앉아 있는 게 보였소. 앨리스가 한가운데 앉아, 다들 늘 하던 자수 일에 부지런히 손을 놀리고 있었지요.\n\n'평안하시오, 고운 따님들이여!' 수도사가 말했소. 정말이지 그들은 고왔지요. 수도사조차 창조주의 손이 빚어낸 빼어난 걸작이라 여겨 사랑할 만했소.\n\n자매들은 알맞은 공경을 담아 그 거룩한 사람에게 인사했고, 맏이는 그에게 옆에 놓인 이끼 낀 자리를 권했소. 하지만 마음씨 좋은 수도사는 고개를 가로젓더니, 아주 딱딱한 돌 위에 털썩 주저앉았지요. 틀림없이 흐뭇해하는 천사들이 그 모습을 보고 기뻐했을 거요.\n\n'즐거워들 보이는구려, 따님들.' 수도사가 말했소.\n\n'사랑스러운 앨리스가 얼마나 마음이 가벼운 아이인지 아시잖아요.' 맏이가 웃고 있는 처녀의 머리채를 손으로 쓸어내리며 대답했소.\n\n'그리고 온 자연이 환한 빛과 햇살 속에서 빛나는 걸 보면 저희 마음속에 얼마나 큰 기쁨과 즐거움이 일어나는데요, 신부님.' 앨리스가 그 은둔자의 엄한 눈길 아래 얼굴을 붉히며 덧붙였소.\n\n수도사는 그저 무겁게 고개를 한 번 숙일 뿐 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았고, 자매들은 말없이 일을 이어 갔소.\n\n'여전히 귀한 시간을 허비하는구려.' 마침내 수도사가 맏이를 돌아보며 말했소. '여전히 이 헛된 장난질에 귀한 시간을 허비하다니. 아아, 슬프구나! 영원의 표면에 떠 있는 그 몇 안 되는 거품들, 그 어둡고 깊은 강줄기 가운데 하늘이 우리에게 보도록 허락하신 그 전부를, 이토록 가벼이 흩뿌리다니!'\n\n'신부님.' 처녀가 다른 자매들처럼 바쁜 손길을 멈추며 말했소. '저희는 아침 기도를 올렸고, 날마다 베푸는 자선을 문간에서 나누었으며, 병든 농부들을 돌보았어요. 오전에 할 일은 다 마쳤지요. 저희가 하는 일이 흠잡을 데 없는 일이기를 바라요.'\n\n'이걸 보시오.' 수도사가 그의 손에서 수틀을 받아 들며 말했소. '아무 뜻도, 목적도 없이 화려한 색실만 복잡하게 얽어 놓았구려. 언젠가 어느 헛된 장식이 되어, 가냘프고 들뜬 그대들 여인네의 허영을 거들 운명이 아니라면 말이오. 날이면 날마다 이 부질없는 일에 매달리고도 아직 절반도 못 끝냈구려. 떠나간 하루하루의 그림자가 우리 무덤 위로 드리우고, 우리가 그리로 서둘러 가는 걸 지켜보며 벌레가 기뻐 날뛰지요. 따님들, 덧없이 흐르는 시간을 보낼 더 나은 길은 없겠소?'\n\n위의 네 자매는 거룩한 사람의 꾸짖음에 부끄러운 듯 눈을 내리깔았소. 하지만 앨리스는 눈을 들어 수도사를 부드럽게 바라보았지요.\n\n'사랑하는 저희 어머니께서는.' 처녀가 말했소. '하늘이 그 영혼을 편히 쉬게 하시기를!'\n\n'아멘!' 수도사가 깊은 목소리로 외쳤소.\n\n'사랑하는 저희 어머니께서는,' 고운 앨리스가 떨리는 목소리로 이었소. '이 긴 일을 처음 시작했을 무렵 살아 계셨어요. 그러고는 당신이 세상에 없게 되거든, 한가한 시간에 늘 분별 있고 즐겁게 이 일을 해 나가라고 이르셨지요. 어머니께서는 이렇게 말씀하셨어요. 만약 저희가 죄 없는 즐거움과 처녀다운 일거리 속에서 그 시간을 함께 보낸다면, 그 시간이야말로 저희 삶에서 가장 행복하고 평화로운 때가 될 거라고요. 또 훗날 저희가 세상에 나가 그 근심과 시련에 섞이게 되면, 그러니까 세상의 유혹에 끌리고 그 화려함에 눈멀어, 한 사랑하는 부모의 자식들을 거룩한 끈으로 묶어야 할 그 사랑과 도리를 언젠가 잊게 된다면, 함께한 처녀 시절의 이 옛 작품을 한 번 들여다보는 것만으로도 지난날의 좋은 생각들이 되살아나, 마음이 누그러져 다시 다정함과 사랑으로 돌아오게 될 거라고요.'\n\n'앨리스 말이 옳아요, 신부님.' 맏이가 다소 자랑스러운 듯 말했소. 그러고는 다시 일을 시작했고, 다른 자매들도 그러했지요.\n\n자매마다 앞에 큼직한 자수 견본 같은 것을 놓고 있었소. 무늬는 복잡하고 정교했으며, 다섯 자매 모두 본과 색이 똑같았지요. 자매들은 우아하게 일감 위로 몸을 숙였고, 수도사는 두 손에 턱을 괴고 말없이 이 사람 저 사람을 번갈아 바라보았소.\n\n'얼마나 더 나은 일이겠소.' 마침내 그가 말했소. '그런 온갖 생각과 우연을 피해, 교회의 평화로운 품 안에서 그대들의 삶을 하늘에 바친다면 말이오. 갓난 시절, 어린 시절, 한창때, 그리고 늙음이 서로 앞다투어 몰려오는 만큼이나 빠르게 시들어 가지요. 인간의 티끌이 어떻게 무덤을 향해 굴러가는지 생각해 보시오. 그리고 그 끝을 향해 한결같이 얼굴을 돌린 채, 세상의 쾌락에서 피어올라 그 신도들의 감각을 속이는 그 구름을 피하시오. 면사포요, 따님들, 면사포!'\n\n'절대 안 돼요, 언니들.' 앨리스가 외쳤소. '하늘의 빛과 공기를, 땅의 싱그러움을, 그리고 그 위에 숨 쉬는 온갖 아름다운 것을, 차디찬 수도원과 골방과 맞바꾸지 마요. 자연이 베푼 축복이야말로 삶의 마땅한 보물이고, 우리는 죄 없이 그것을 함께 누릴 수 있어요. 죽음은 우리에게 주어진 무거운 몫이지만, 아, 우리 둘레에 생명을 두고 죽어요. 우리 차가운 심장이 멎을 때, 곁에서 따뜻한 심장이 뛰게 해요. 우리의 마지막 눈길이 하느님께서 당신의 환한 하늘에 두신 그 경계에 닿게 해요. 돌담과 쇠창살이 아니라요! 사랑하는 언니들, 언니들이 원한다면 이 푸른 정원 안에서 살다 죽어요. 다만 수도원의 음울함과 슬픔만은 피해요. 그러면 우리는 행복할 거예요.'\n\n처녀는 그 간절한 호소를 마치며 눈물을 비 오듯 흘렸고, 언니의 품에 얼굴을 묻었소.\n\n'마음 놓아라, 앨리스.' 맏이가 그 고운 이마에 입 맞추며 말했소. '면사포 따위가 네 젊은 이마에 그늘을 드리우는 일은 결코 없을 거야. 너희는 어떠니, 동생들? 너희 자신을 두고 하는 말이야. 앨리스나 나를 두고 하는 말이 아니라.'\n\n자매들은 한목소리로 외쳤소. 우리 운명은 함께 묶여 있다고, 수도원 담장 너머에도 평화와 미덕이 머물 곳은 있다고 말이오.\n\n'신부님.' 맏이가 위엄 있게 일어서며 말했소. '저희의 마지막 결심을 들으셨지요. 성 메리 수도원을 부유하게 하고, 고아가 된 저희를 그 거룩한 보살핌에 맡기신 그 경건한 배려는, 동시에 저희 뜻에 그 어떤 강요도 하지 말 것을, 저희가 택한 대로 자유로이 살게 할 것을 정해 두었어요. 부디 이 이야기는 더 이상 듣지 않게 해 주세요. 동생들아, 곧 정오야. 저녁까지 안에 들어가 쉬자!' 맏이는 수도사에게 정중히 절하고는 일어나, 앨리스와 손을 맞잡고 집 쪽으로 걸어갔소. 다른 자매들도 뒤를 따랐지요.\n\n전에도 여러 번 같은 말을 했지만 이토록 단호하게 거절당한 적은 없던 그 거룩한 사람은, 눈을 땅에 박은 채 입술을 기도하듯 달싹이며 조금 뒤처져 걸었소. 자매들이 현관에 이르자, 그는 걸음을 재촉하며 멈추라고 소리쳤지요.\n\n'멈추시오!' 수도사가 오른손을 허공에 치켜들고, 앨리스와 맏이를 번갈아 노려보며 말했소. '멈추고, 내 말을 들으시오. 그대들이 영원보다 더 소중히 여기겠다는, 그래서 자비롭게 잠들어 있는데도 한낱 부질없는 장난감으로 깨우려는 그 기억들이 무엇인지 말이오. 세속의 것에 대한 기억은 훗날 쓰라린 환멸과 고통과 죽음으로, 황량한 변화와 사람을 갉아먹는 슬픔으로 무겁게 채워지지요. 언젠가 그날이 올 거요. 그 뜻 없는 노리개를 한 번 들여다보는 것만으로 그대들 가운데 누군가의 가슴에 깊은 상처가 헤집어지고, 그 영혼 깊은 곳까지 사무치는 날이 말이오. 그때가 오거든, 그리고 명심하시오, 반드시 올 테니, 그토록 매달리던 세상에서 등을 돌려, 그대들이 비웃었던 이 안식처로 오시오. 재앙과 시련에 그 빛이 바랠 때 인간의 불보다 차디찬 골방을 내게 찾아오시오. 그리고 거기서 젊은 날의 꿈을 두고 우시오. 이것은 하늘의 뜻이지 내 뜻이 아니오.' 수도사가 움츠러든 처녀들을 둘러보며 목소리를 누그러뜨리고 말했소. '성모의 축복이 그대들에게 있기를, 따님들이여!'\n\n이 말과 함께 그는 쪽문으로 사라졌소. 그리고 자매들은 서둘러 집 안으로 들어갔고, 그날은 더 이상 모습을 보이지 않았지요.\n\n하지만 사제가 눈살을 찌푸린들 자연은 웃는 법이오. 이튿날 해가 환하게 비쳤고, 그다음 날도, 또 그다음 날도 그러했지요. 그리고 아침의 눈부신 빛 속에서, 또 저녁의 부드러운 쉼 속에서, 다섯 자매는 여전히 그들의 조용한 과수원을 거닐고, 일하고, 즐거운 이야기로 시간을 보냈소.\n\n세월은 한 편의 이야기처럼 흘러갔소. 사실 들려주는 여느 이야기보다도 빠르게 흘렀지요. 어쩌면 이 이야기도 그런 부류일까 걱정이오만. 다섯 자매의 집은 그대로 그 자리에 서 있었고, 같은 나무들이 과수원 풀밭에 정겨운 그늘을 드리웠소. 자매들도 그곳에 있었고 처음처럼 사랑스러웠지만, 그들의 보금자리에는 변화가 찾아왔소. 때로는 갑옷 부딪치는 소리가, 강철 투구 위에 어른거리는 달빛이 보였소. 또 때로는 지친 군마가 박차에 채여 대문까지 내달려 오면, 한 여인의 모습이 그 고단한 전령에게 소식을 다그쳐 묻고 싶은 양 서둘러 미끄러져 나왔지요. 어느 날 밤에는 기사와 귀부인의 멋진 행렬이 수도원 담장 안에 묵었다가, 이튿날 고운 자매 가운데 둘을 데리고 떠났소. 그 뒤로 말 탄 이들의 발길이 뜸해지기 시작했고, 와도 나쁜 소식을 가져오는 듯했으며, 마침내 아예 끊겨 버렸소. 그러더니 발이 부르튼 농부들이 해 진 뒤 대문으로 슬그머니 와서 몰래 볼일을 보고 갔지요. 한번은 한밤중에 하인 하나가 황급히 수도원으로 보내졌고, 날이 밝자 자매들의 집에서 비탄과 통곡 소리가 들려왔소. 그 뒤로는 슬픈 침묵이 그 집에 내려앉았고, 기사도 귀부인도, 말도 갑옷도 다시는 그 둘레에 보이지 않았지요.\n\n하늘에 음울한 어둠이 깔리고, 해가 성난 듯 저물며 흐린 구름을 그 마지막 노여움의 빛으로 물들이고 있을 때, 그 똑같은 검은 옷 수도사가 팔짱을 낀 채 수도원에서 돌 던지면 닿을 거리를 천천히 거닐고 있었소. 나무와 덤불에는 시들음이 덮쳤소. 그리고 바람이 마침내 온종일 감돌던 부자연스러운 고요를 깨기 시작하더니, 이따금 무겁게 한숨지었지요. 다가올 폭풍의 황폐를 슬프게 예고하는 듯이 말이오. 박쥐가 그 무거운 공기를 가르며 괴이하게 날았고, 땅바닥은 기어 다니는 것들로 우글거렸소. 빗속에서 불어나고 살찌라는 본능이 그것들을 밖으로 불러낸 거였지요.\n\n이제 수도사의 눈은 땅을 향해 있지 않았소. 그 눈은 멀리 던져져, 이곳저곳을 헤맸지요. 그 장면의 음울함과 황량함이 제 가슴속에서 곧장 메아리를 찾기라도 한 듯이 말이오. 그는 다시 자매들의 집 가까이서 걸음을 멈췄고, 다시 쪽문으로 들어섰소.\n\n하지만 다시는 그의 귀에 웃음소리가 닿지 않았고, 그의 눈이 다섯 자매의 아름다운 모습에 머무는 일도 없었소. 모든 것이 고요하고 버려져 있었지요. 나뭇가지들은 휘고 부러졌으며, 풀은 길고 무성하게 자라 있었소. 오랫동안, 아주 오랫동안 가벼운 발길이 그것을 밟은 적이 없었던 거요.\n\n그 변화에 어지간히 익숙해진 사람다운 무심함, 아니 골똘함으로, 수도사는 집 안으로 미끄러져 들어가 낮고 어두운 방으로 들어섰소. 거기에 네 자매가 앉아 있었지요. 검은 옷이 그들의 창백한 얼굴을 더욱 희게 만들었고, 세월과 슬픔이 깊은 자국을 새겨 놓았소. 그들은 여전히 기품 있었지만, 미모의 발그레함과 자랑스러움은 사라지고 없었지요.\n\n그럼 앨리스는, 어디 있었느냐고? 하늘나라에.\n\n수도사는, 그 수도사조차, 이곳의 슬픔만큼은 차마 견딜 수 없었소. 이 자매들이 마지막으로 모인 지 오래되었고, 그 핏기 없는 얼굴에는 세월로도 결코 갈아 낼 수 없는 골이 패어 있었으니까. 그는 말없이 자리에 앉아, 그들에게 하던 이야기를 계속하라고 손짓했소.\n\n'여기 있어요, 동생들.' 맏이가 떨리는 목소리로 말했소. '그 뒤로 차마 이걸 들여다본 적이 없었는데, 이제 내 나약함이 부끄럽구나. 그 애를 떠올리는 게 뭐가 그리 두렵겠니? 우리 옛 시절을 다시 불러내는 건 그래도 엄숙한 기쁨이 될 거야.'\n\n그는 그렇게 말하며 수도사를 흘끗 보고는, 장을 열어 오래전에 완성된 다섯 개의 자수틀을 꺼냈소. 걸음은 굳건했지만, 마지막 것을 꺼낼 때 그의 손은 떨렸지요. 그리고 그것을 본 다른 자매들의 감정이 북받쳐 터져 나오자, 그는 눌러 두었던 눈물이 길을 트며 흐느꼈소. '하느님, 그 애를 축복하소서!'\n\n수도사가 일어나 그들 쪽으로 다가갔소. '이건 그 애가 건강했을 때 거의 마지막으로 손댄 것이지요.' 그가 나직한 목소리로 말했소.\n\n'그래요.' 맏이가 비통하게 울며 외쳤소.\n\n수도사는 둘째 자매를 돌아보았소.\n\n'그대가 이 일거리에 골몰한 모습을 처음 보고는, 그대 눈을 들여다보며 그대 숨결 하나에 매달리던 그 늠름한 젊은이는, 잔디가 피로 붉게 물든 벌판에 묻혀 있소. 한때 환히 빛나도록 닦였던 갑옷 조각들은 녹슨 채 땅바닥에 썩어 가고, 흙 속에서 부스러지는 뼈만큼이나 그것이 그의 것인지조차 알아볼 수 없소!'\n\n둘째 자매는 신음하며 두 손을 마주 비틀었소.\n\n'궁정의 술책이,' 그가 나머지 두 자매를 돌아보며 이었소. '그대들을 평화로운 보금자리에서 끌어내 흥청거림과 화려함의 무대로 데려갔지요. 그 똑같은 술책과, 거만하고 불같은 사내들의 그칠 줄 모르는 야망이, 과부가 된 처녀로, 또 천대받는 쫓겨난 자로 그대들을 돌려보냈소. 내 말이 맞소?'\n\n두 자매의 흐느낌만이 그 대답이었소.\n\n'그 부질없는 짓에 시간을 허비할 까닭이 거의 없소.' 수도사가 뜻 있는 눈빛으로 말했소. '젊은 날 희망의 창백한 망령들이나 불러일으킬 그런 짓 말이오. 그것들을 묻으시오. 그 머리 위에 참회와 고행을 쌓아 누르고, 수도원을 그것들의 무덤으로 삼으시오!'\n\n자매들은 사흘만 생각할 말미를 달라고 했소. 그리고 그날 밤, 면사포야말로 죽은 기쁨을 덮을 알맞은 수의처럼 느껴졌지요. 하지만 아침이 다시 왔소. 그리고 과수원 나뭇가지들이 축 늘어져 땅 위로 제멋대로 뻗어 있었어도, 그곳은 여전히 그 과수원이었소. 풀은 거칠고 높이 자랐지만, 변화도 슬픔도 한낱 이름에 지나지 않던 시절 그토록 자주 함께 앉았던 그 자리가 아직 거기 있었지요. 앨리스가 즐겁게 거닐던 모든 산책로와 후미진 구석이 거기 있었고, 대성당 본당 바닥에는 그가 평화로이 잠든 납작한 돌 하나가 있었소.\n\n그 어린 마음이 수도원 담장이라는 생각만으로 얼마나 진저리쳤는지 기억하면서, 그 무덤 속 재마저 얼어붙게 할 옷차림으로 그 무덤을 내려다볼 수 있었겠소? 무릎 꿇어 기도하며, 온 하늘이 귀 기울여 들으려 할 때, 한 천사의 얼굴에 어두운 슬픔의 그늘을 드리울 수 있었겠소? 아니지요.\n\n그들은 멀리, 그 시절 이름난 장인들에게 사람을 보냈소. 그리고 이 경건한 일에 교회의 허락을 얻은 뒤, 화려하게 물들인 스테인드글라스 다섯 칸에 그들의 옛 자수 작품을 충실하게 옮겨 새기게 했지요. 이것들은 그때까지 아무 장식도 없던 큼직한 창에 끼워 넣어졌소. 그리고 앨리스가 그토록 보기 좋아하던 그 햇살이 환히 비치면, 낯익은 무늬들이 본래의 색깔로 비쳐 들었고, 눈부신 빛줄기를 바닥에 던지며 앨리스라는 이름 위로 따스하게 내려앉았소.\n\n날마다 오랜 시간 동안 자매들은 본당을 천천히 오르내리거나, 그 납작하고 너른 돌 곁에 무릎 꿇었소. 여러 해가 지나자 그 늘 앉던 자리에 셋만 보였고, 그다음엔 둘뿐이었으며, 그 뒤로 오랫동안은 나이에 등 굽은 여인 단 하나뿐이었지요. 마침내 그마저 더는 오지 않았고, 돌에는 다섯 개의 소박한 세례명이 새겨졌소.\n\n그 돌은 닳아 없어져 다른 돌들로 바뀌었고, 그 뒤로 여러 세대가 오고 갔소. 세월이 색을 부드럽게 바래게 했지만, 똑같은 빛줄기가 지금도 그 잊힌 무덤 위로 떨어지지요. 이제 그 무덤의 흔적은 남아 있지 않지만 말이오. 그리고 오늘날까지도 요크 대성당에 오면, 사람들은 다섯 자매라 불리는 옛 창문 하나를 낯선 이에게 보여 준다오.\"\n\n\"그것참 우울한 이야기로군요.\" 쾌활한 얼굴의 신사가 잔을 비우며 말했다.\n\n\"삶에 관한 이야기지요. 그리고 삶이란 그런 슬픔으로 이루어진 것이고요.\" 상대가 정중하게, 그러나 무겁고 슬픈 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n\"좋은 그림에는 다 그늘이 있지요. 하지만 우리가 마음먹고 들여다보면 빛도 있어요.\" 쾌활한 얼굴의 신사가 말했다. \"당신 이야기 속 막내는 늘 마음이 가벼웠잖소.\"\n\n\"그리고 일찍 죽었지요.\" 상대가 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"덜 행복했더라면 어쩌면 더 일찍 죽었을지도 모르오.\" 처음 말한 사람이 한껏 감정을 실어 말했다. \"그를 그토록 사랑했던 자매들이, 만약 그의 삶이 음울하고 슬픈 것이었다면 덜 슬퍼했을 것 같소? 무거운 상실의 그 첫 날카로운 아픔을 달래 줄 게 있다면, 나로서는 이런 생각일 거요. 내가 애도하는 이들이 이 세상에서 죄 없이 행복하게 지내고 둘레의 모든 것을 사랑함으로써, 더 순결하고 더 행복한 세상을 맞을 채비를 해 두었다는 생각 말이오. 햇살이 이 아름다운 땅을 비추는 건 찌푸린 얼굴을 만나려는 게 아니오. 그건 틀림없소.\"\n\n\"당신 말이 옳은 것 같군요.\" 이야기를 들려준 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"옳은 것 같다니!\" 상대가 받아쳤다. \"이걸 누가 의심하겠소? 슬픈 회한의 소재를 아무거나 하나 떠올려 보시오. 거기에 얼마나 많은 즐거움이 함께 얽혀 있는지 보일 거요. 지난 기쁨의 추억이 아픔이 될 수도 있지요.\"\n\n\"그렇지요.\" 상대가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그래요, 그렇소. 되돌릴 수 없는 행복을 떠올리는 건 아픈 일이오. 하지만 한결 누그러진 아픔이지요. 우리의 추억은 안타깝게도 한탄스러운 것들과, 또 쓰라리게 뉘우치는 수많은 행동과 뒤섞여 있소. 그래도 아무리 얼룩진 삶이라도, 돌이켜볼 만한 자그마한 햇살 줄기가 그토록 많이 있다고 나는 굳게 믿소. 그러니 어떤 인간도, 제 스스로 희망의 울타리 밖으로 나선 자가 아니라면, 설령 그럴 힘이 있다 한들 망각의 강물을 일부러 들이켜지는 않을 거라 믿소.\"\n\n\"그 믿음이 어쩌면 옳을지도 모르겠군요.\" 희끗한 머리의 신사가 잠시 생각하더니 말했다. \"그런 것 같다는 쪽으로 마음이 기우는군요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 상대가 받았다. \"이른바 철학자라는 자들이 뭐라 떠들든, 이 세상살이에서는 좋은 것이 나쁜 것을 앞지르는 법이오. 우리의 정이 시련을 겪더라도, 그 정이야말로 우리의 위안이요 안식이지요. 그리고 기억은 아무리 슬프다 한들 이 세상과 더 나은 세상을 잇는 가장 좋고 순수한 끈이오. 자, 그럼! 내가 또 다른 종류의 이야기를 하나 들려드리리다.\"\n\n아주 잠깐 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 쾌활한 얼굴의 신사는 펀치를 한 차례 돌리고는, 무언가 점잖지 못한 이야기를 꺼낼까 봐 잔뜩 겁먹은 듯한 까다로운 부인을 곁눈질로 슬쩍 보며 이야기를 시작했다.\n그로그즈위그의 남작\n\n# THE BARON OF GROGZWIG\n\n\"독일 그로그즈위그의 폰 쾰드웨트하우트 남작은 누구나 보고 싶어 할 만한 멋진 젊은 남작이었소. 그가 성에 살았다는 건 말할 것도 없지요. 당연한 일이니까. 게다가 낡은 성에 살았다는 것도 굳이 말할 필요가 없소. 새 성에 사는 독일 남작이 어디 있겠소? 이 유서 깊은 건물에는 별난 사정이 한둘이 아니었는데, 그중에서도 가장 놀랍고 알 수 없는 것은 이런 거였소. 바람이 불면 그 소리가 굴뚝에서 우르릉거리거나, 심지어 이웃한 숲의 나무들 사이에서 울부짖었지요. 또 달이 비치면 그 빛이 담벼락의 작은 틈새들을 뚫고 들어와, 너른 회랑과 복도의 어떤 부분은 환하게 밝히면서 다른 부분은 음침한 그림자 속에 남겨 두었소. 내가 알기로는, 남작의 어느 조상이 돈에 쪼들렸던 차에, 어느 날 밤 길을 묻겠다고 찾아온 신사에게 단검을 찔러 넣었다는데, 그 때문에 이런 기이한 일들이 벌어진 거라고들 했소. 하지만 그게 정말 그랬을까 싶기도 한 게, 그 남작의 조상은 마음씨 고운 사람이라 나중에는 그렇게 경솔하게 군 걸 몹시 후회했거든. 그래서 자기보다 약한 남작 소유의 돌과 목재를 잔뜩 강제로 빼앗아 사죄의 뜻으로 예배당을 지었고, 그렇게 하늘로부터 모든 빚을 청산했다는 영수증을 받아 낸 거지요.\n\n남작의 조상 이야기를 하다 보니, 남작이 가문 덕에 마땅히 누리던 그 대단한 존경거리가 떠오르는구려. 남작에게 조상이 정확히 몇 명이나 있었는지는 차마 말 못 하겠소. 하지만 그가 당대의 누구보다도 훨씬 많은 조상을 두었다는 것만은 알지요. 다만 그가 요즘 같은 시대에 살았더라면 조상을 더 많이 둘 수 있었을 텐데 싶어 아쉬울 따름이오. 지난 세기의 위인들에게는 참 딱한 노릇이오. 너무 일찍 세상에 나온 셈이니까. 삼사백 년 전에 태어난 사람이, 지금 태어난 사람만큼 많은 선대 친족을 거느렸으리라 기대하는 건 무리잖소. 누구든 가장 마지막에 태어난 사람은, 그가 구두장이든 무슨 천한 개잡놈이든 간에, 지금 살아 있는 그 어떤 위대한 귀족보다도 긴 족보를 가지게 될 거요. 나는 이게 공평하지 않다고 보오.\n\n자, 어쨌든 그로그즈위그의 폰 쾰드웨트하우트 남작 말이오! 그는 거무스름하고 잘생긴 사내로, 검은 머리에 커다란 콧수염을 길렀고, 사냥할 때면 링컨 그린 빛깔 옷을 입고 적갈색 장화를 신은 채, 장거리 마차 차장처럼 어깨에 뿔나팔을 둘러메고 말을 달렸소. 그가 이 뿔나팔을 불면, 좀 더 거친 링컨 그린 옷에 밑창이 좀 더 두꺼운 적갈색 장화를 신은, 신분이 낮은 신사 스물넷이 대뜸 뛰쳐나왔지요. 그러면 일행 전체가, 옻칠한 마당 난간 같은 창을 손에 들고 멧돼지를 잡으러, 어쩌면 곰과 맞붙으러 말을 달려 나갔소. 곰을 만났을 경우엔, 남작이 먼저 그놈을 죽인 다음 그 기름으로 제 콧수염을 발랐지요.\n\n그로그즈위그 남작에게는 즐거운 삶이었고, 남작의 부하들에게는 한층 더 즐거운 삶이었소. 그들은 밤마다 라인 포도주를 마시다가 식탁 밑으로 굴러떨어졌고, 그러면 바닥에 술병을 갖다 놓고 담배를 청했지요. 그로그즈위그의 그 유쾌한 패거리만큼 흥겹고 떠들썩하고 신명 나게 즐기는 한량들은 어디에도 없었소.\n\n하지만 식탁 위의 쾌락이든 식탁 밑의 쾌락이든, 좀 변화가 있어야 하는 법이오. 특히 똑같은 스물다섯 사람이 날마다 똑같은 식탁에 둘러앉아, 똑같은 화제를 떠들고 똑같은 이야기를 늘어놓을 때는 말이오. 남작은 점점 따분해졌고, 자극이 필요했소. 그는 부하 신사들과 시비를 거는 데에 맛을 들였고, 저녁을 먹고 나면 날마다 두세 명씩 걷어차 보려 했지요. 처음에는 이게 반가운 변화였소. 하지만 한 주쯤 지나니 지겨워졌고, 남작은 영 기분이 언짢아져서 새로운 놀거리를 찾아 절망 속에 두리번거렸소.\n\n어느 날 밤, 종일 사냥으로 님로드나 길링워터보다도 한 수 위인 솜씨를 뽐내며 '또 한 마리 멋진 곰'을 때려잡아 의기양양하게 집으로 끌고 온 폰 쾰드웨트하우트 남작은, 식탁 상석에 침울하게 앉아 못마땅한 얼굴로 회랑의 그을린 천장을 노려보고 있었소. 그는 큼직한 잔으로 포도주를 들이켰지만, 마시면 마실수록 더 인상을 찌푸렸지요. 그의 오른쪽과 왼쪽에 앉는 그 위태로운 영예를 누리던 신사들은 술 마시는 데서는 신기할 만큼 그를 똑 닮게 따라 하며, 서로를 향해 인상을 찌푸렸소.\n\n'내 그러리라!' 남작이 갑자기 오른손으로 식탁을 내리치고 왼손으로 콧수염을 비틀며 외쳤소. '그로그즈위그의 안주인을 위해 잔을 채워라!'\n\n스물넷의 링컨 그린들은 얼굴이 새파래졌소. 다만 스물넷의 코만은 예외였는데, 그 코들은 절대 변하는 법이 없었지요.\n\n'내 그로그즈위그의 안주인을 위해서라고 했다.' 남작이 식탁을 빙 둘러보며 거듭 말했소.\n\n'그로그즈위그의 안주인을 위하여!' 링컨 그린들이 외쳤소. 그러고는 스물넷의 목구멍으로 진귀한 묵은 백포도주 스물넷 임페리얼 파인트가 넘어갔고, 그들은 마흔여덟 개의 입술을 쩝쩝거리며 다시 눈을 끔벅였소.\n\n'폰 스윌렌하우젠 남작의 고운 따님 말이다.' 쾰드웨트하우트가 황송하게도 설명을 곁들였소. '내일 해가 지기 전에 그 아비에게 청혼하리라. 만약 우리 청혼을 거절하거든, 그 코를 베어 버리겠다.'\n\n좌중에서 쉰 웅성거림이 일었소. 사내들은 너 나 할 것 없이 먼저 칼자루에, 다음엔 제 코끝에 손을 대며 섬뜩한 뜻을 내비쳤지요.\n\n자식의 효심이란 참 보기 좋은 것이오! 만약 폰 스윌렌하우젠 남작의 따님이 이미 마음 둔 데가 있다고 하소연했거나, 아버지 발치에 엎드려 짠 눈물로 그 발을 적셨거나, 아니면 그저 기절해 자빠지며 미친 듯한 비명으로 노신사를 떠받들었더라면, 백에 아흔아홉은 스윌렌하우젠 성이 창밖으로 내던져졌을 거요. 아니, 차라리 남작이 창밖으로 내던져지고 성이 헐려 버렸겠지요. 하지만 그 처녀는, 이튿날 아침 발 빠른 전령이 폰 쾰드웨트하우트의 청혼을 전했을 때 잠자코 있었소. 그러고는 얌전히 제 방으로 물러나, 그 창문으로 구혼자와 그 일행이 다가오는 걸 지켜보았지요. 그 커다란 콧수염을 기른 말 탄 사내가 자기에게 청혼한 남편감이라는 걸 확인하자마자, 처녀는 아버지 앞으로 한달음에 달려가, 아버지의 평안을 지키기 위해 자기를 기꺼이 희생하겠다고 했소. 그러자 그 점잖은 남작은 딸을 와락 품에 안고, 기쁨의 눈물을 한 방울 찔끔 흘렸지요.\n\n그날 성에서는 큰 잔치가 벌어졌소. 폰 쾰드웨트하우트의 스물넷 링컨 그린은 폰 스윌렌하우젠의 열둘 링컨 그린과 영원한 우정을 맹세하며 잔을 나누었고, 노남작에게 '온통 새파래질 때까지' 그의 포도주를 마시겠노라 약속했소. 아마 얼굴 전체가 코와 똑같은 빛깔이 될 때까지라는 뜻이었겠지요. 헤어질 때가 되자 모두가 서로 등을 두드렸소. 그리고 폰 쾰드웨트하우트 남작과 그 부하들은 흥겹게 말을 달려 집으로 돌아갔지요.\n\n꼬박 여섯 주 동안 곰과 멧돼지들은 휴일을 누렸소. 쾰드웨트하우트 가문과 스윌렌하우젠 가문이 하나로 맺어졌지요. 창은 녹슬었고, 남작의 뿔나팔은 불지 않은 탓에 목이 쉬었소.\n\n스물넷에게는 대단한 시절이었소. 하지만 아아! 그들의 드높고 화려한 나날도 제풀에 장화를 챙겨 신고는, 벌써 걸어 나가는 중이었지요.\n\n'여보.' 남작 부인이 말했소.\n\n'여보.' 남작이 받았소.\n\n'저 거칠고 시끄러운 사내들 말이에요.'\n\n'어떤 자들 말이오?' 남작이 흠칫 놀라며 물었소.\n\n남작 부인은 두 사람이 서 있던 창문에서 아래 안마당을 가리켰소. 거기서는 아무것도 모르는 링컨 그린들이, 멧돼지 한두 마리쯤 잡으러 나서기 전에 이별주를 거나하게 들이켜고 있었지요.\n\n'내 사냥 일행이오, 부인.' 남작이 말했소.\n\n'해산시켜요, 여보.' 남작 부인이 속삭였소.\n\n'해산시키라고!' 남작이 기겁하며 외쳤소.\n\n'날 기쁘게 해 주려거든요, 여보.' 남작 부인이 대답했소.\n\n'악마를 기쁘게 하지, 부인.' 남작이 받아쳤소.\n\n그러자 남작 부인은 큰 소리로 외마디 비명을 지르더니 남작의 발치에 풀썩 기절해 쓰러졌소.\n\n남작이 어쩌겠소? 그는 부인의 몸종을 부르고 의사를 부르라 고래고래 소리쳤소. 그러고는 마당으로 뛰쳐나가, 가장 자주 걷어차여 본 링컨 그린 둘을 걷어차고, 나머지에게도 죄다 욕을 퍼부으며 꺼지라고 했소. 어디로 꺼지라고 했는지는 신경 쓰지 마시오. 그 독일말을 모르니, 안다면 점잖게 옮겨 드렸으련만.\n\n어떤 수로, 또 어느 정도까지, 어떤 아내들이 어떤 남편들을 그토록 꽉 잡고 사는지는 내가 말할 자리가 아니오. 하긴 이 문제에 대해 나름의 사견이 있고, 국회의원이란 모름지기 결혼해서는 안 된다고 생각하기는 하오. 결혼한 의원 넷 중 셋은 제 양심이 아니라 아내의 양심에 따라 표를 던질 수밖에 없으니까. 아내에게 그런 게 있다면 말이지만. 지금 내가 할 말은 이것뿐이오. 폰 쾰드웨트하우트 남작 부인은 어찌어찌하여 폰 쾰드웨트하우트 남작을 단단히 휘어잡게 되었다는 것. 그리고 조금씩, 차츰차츰, 날이 가고 해가 갈수록, 남작은 어떤 다툼에서든 늘 지는 쪽이 되거나, 오랜 취미라는 말에서 슬그머니 떨어져 내렸다는 것이오. 그래서 마흔여덟 살 안팎의 살집 좋고 혈색 좋은 사내가 되었을 무렵, 그에게는 잔치도, 흥청거림도, 사냥 일행도, 사냥도 없었소. 한마디로 그가 좋아하던 것, 늘 누리던 것이 하나도 남지 않았지요. 그리고 비록 사자처럼 사납고 황동처럼 뻔뻔했어도, 그는 자기 소유의 그로그즈위그 성에서 자기 아내에게 보기 좋게 면박당하고 짓눌려 살았소.\n\n남작의 불행은 이게 다가 아니었소. 결혼한 지 한 해쯤 지나 우람한 어린 남작이 세상에 나왔고, 그를 기리며 불꽃놀이가 한가득 펼쳐지고 포도주 수십 병이 비워졌지요. 하지만 이듬해엔 어린 여남작이 태어났고, 그다음 해엔 또 어린 남작이, 그렇게 해마다 남작이든 여남작이든 태어났소. 어느 해엔 둘이 한꺼번에 태어나기도 했지요. 그러다 남작은 어느새 열둘이나 되는 자식을 둔 자그마한 한 집안의 가장이 되어 있었소. 그 돌맞이 때마다 점잖은 폰 스윌렌하우젠 노남작 부인은 제 딸 폰 쾰드웨트하우트 남작 부인의 안위를 두고 안절부절못했소. 그 마님이 딸의 회복을 위해 실제로 무슨 보탬이 될 만한 일을 했다는 얘기는 도통 들리지 않았지만, 그래도 그분은 그로그즈위그 성에 와서 되도록 안절부절못하는 걸 자기 본분으로 삼았지요. 그러고는 남작의 살림살이에 대해 도덕적 훈수를 두는 일과, 불행한 딸의 모진 팔자를 한탄하는 일로 시간을 둘로 쪼개 썼소. 그러다 그로그즈위그 남작이 이에 좀 상하고 짜증이 나서 용기를 내어, 적어도 내 아내가 다른 남작들 아내보다 못살지는 않잖느냐고 슬쩍 비치기라도 하면, 폰 스윌렌하우젠 남작 부인은 모두에게 똑똑히 봐 두라고 했소. 사랑하는 제 딸의 고통을 가엾이 여기는 사람은 자기밖에 없다고 말이오. 그러면 그분의 친척과 친구들은, 과연 그분이 사위보다 훨씬 더 많이 우는 게 분명하다고, 세상에 모진 짐승 같은 인간이 있다면 그건 바로 저 그로그즈위그 남작이라고 한마디씩 거들었지요.\n\n가엾은 남작은 견딜 수 있는 데까지 이 모든 걸 견뎠소. 그러다 더는 견딜 수 없게 되자 입맛도 기운도 잃고, 침울하고 풀이 죽은 채 주저앉아 버렸지요. 하지만 그를 기다리는 더 큰 시련이 아직 남아 있었고, 그것들이 닥쳐오면서 그의 우울과 슬픔은 깊어만 갔소. 형편이 바뀌었소. 그는 빚더미에 올라앉았지요. 스윌렌하우젠 집안은 그로그즈위그의 금고가 마르지 않으리라 여겼지만, 그 금고가 바닥을 드러냈소. 그리고 마침 남작 부인이 가문의 족보에 열셋째를 보태려는 참에, 폰 쾰드웨트하우트는 그것을 다시 채울 길이 없다는 걸 알아차렸소.\n\n'어찌해야 할지 모르겠군.' 남작이 말했소. '아무래도 죽어 버려야겠다.'\n\n기막힌 묘안이었소. 남작은 가까운 찬장에서 낡은 사냥칼을 꺼내, 장화에다 쓱쓱 갈더니, 아이들 말로 제 목을 향해 '한번 들이대' 보았지요.\n\n'흠!' 남작이 멈칫하며 말했소. '날이 영 덜 선 모양인데.'\n\n남작은 칼을 다시 갈아 또 한 번 들이댔소. 그런데 어린 남작들과 여남작들의 요란한 비명에 그의 손이 멈췄지요. 그 아이들은 위층 탑에 마련된 방을 썼는데, 해자로 떨어지지 않도록 창문 바깥에 쇠창살이 둘러쳐 있었소.\n\n'내가 독신이었더라면.' 남작이 한숨지으며 말했소. '아무 방해도 안 받고 쉰 번은 해치웠을 텐데. 여봐라! 회랑 뒤편 작은 둥근 천장 방에 포도주 한 병하고 제일 큰 담뱃대를 갖다 놓아라.'\n\n하인 하나가 아주 친절한 태도로 반 시간쯤 만에 남작의 분부를 받들었고, 그 소식을 전해 들은 폰 쾰드웨트하우트는 둥근 천장 방으로 성큼성큼 걸어 들어갔소. 그 방의 벽은 거뭇하니 반들거리는 나무로 되어 있어, 난로에 쌓아 활활 타오르는 장작불 빛에 어른어른 빛났지요. 술병과 담뱃대가 준비되어 있었고, 전체적으로 그 방은 무척 아늑해 보였소.\n\n'램프는 두고 가라.' 남작이 말했소.\n\n'더 필요하신 건 없으신지요, 나리?' 하인이 물었소.\n\n'방.' 남작이 대답했소. 하인은 분부대로 나갔고, 남작은 문을 잠갔소.\n\n'마지막으로 담배 한 대 피우고,' 남작이 말했소. '그러고 나서 떠나야지.' 그래서 그는 쓸 때까지 칼을 식탁에 올려 둔 채, 포도주를 거나하게 한 잔 들이켜고는, 의자에 등을 기대 불 앞으로 다리를 쭉 뻗고 뻐끔뻐끔 담배를 피웠소.\n\n그는 참 많은 것을 생각했소. 지금의 시련과 지난날의 독신 시절을, 그리고 이제는 어디로 갔는지 아무도 모르게 온 나라에 뿔뿔이 흩어진 링컨 그린들을 떠올렸지요. 다만 둘은 불행히도 목이 잘렸고, 넷은 술로 제 명을 끊었소. 그의 마음이 곰과 멧돼지를 좇고 있을 때, 잔을 바닥까지 비우려고 눈을 들었다가, 그는 난생처음으로 한없는 놀라움 속에 자기가 혼자가 아니라는 걸 알아챘소.\n\n그렇소, 혼자가 아니었지요. 난로 맞은편에, 팔짱을 낀 채 주름지고 흉측한 형상 하나가 앉아 있었으니까. 움푹 꺼져 핏발 선 눈에, 시체처럼 길쭉한 얼굴은 거칠고 검은 머리칼이 들쭉날쭉 헝클어져 그늘져 있었소. 그것은 흐릿한 푸르스름한 빛깔의 긴 윗옷 같은 걸 걸쳤는데, 남작이 유심히 들여다보니 그 앞섶을 따라 관 손잡이들이 단추처럼, 혹은 장식처럼 달려 있었소. 다리에도 갑옷처럼 관 장식판이 둘러져 있었고, 왼쪽 어깨에는 어느 관 덮개의 자투리로 지은 듯한 짧고 거뭇한 망토를 걸치고 있었지요. 그것은 남작에게는 눈길도 주지 않고, 그저 뚫어지게 불을 바라보고 있었소.\n\n'이봐!' 남작이 주의를 끌려고 발을 쿵 굴렀소.\n\n'이봐!' 그 형상이 얼굴이나 몸은 그대로 둔 채 눈만 남작 쪽으로 돌리며 대꾸했소. '왜 그러나?'\n\n'왜 그러냐니!' 남작은 그 공허한 목소리와 빛 없는 눈에도 조금도 주눅 들지 않고 받아쳤소. '그건 내가 물을 말이지. 어떻게 여기 들어왔나?'\n\n'문으로.' 그 형상이 대답했소.\n\n'정체가 뭐냐?' 남작이 물었소.\n\n'사람이오.' 그 형상이 대답했소.\n\n'못 믿겠는데.' 남작이 말했소.\n\n'그럼 안 믿든가.' 그 형상이 말했소.\n\n'안 믿지.' 남작이 받았소.\n\n그 형상은 한참 동안 용감한 그로그즈위그 남작을 바라보더니, 허물없이 말했소.\n\n'당신은 도무지 속아 넘어가질 않는군. 나는 사람이 아니오!'\n\n'그럼 정체가 뭐냐?' 남작이 물었소.\n\n'정령이오.' 그 형상이 대답했소.\n\n'별로 그렇게 안 보이는데.' 남작이 비웃듯 받았소.\n\n'나는 절망과 자살의 정령이오.' 그 유령이 말했소. '이제 나를 알겠지.'\n\n이 말과 함께 유령은 한바탕 이야기라도 나눌 양 자세를 가다듬으며 남작 쪽으로 돌아앉았소. 그런데 참으로 놀라운 것은, 그가 망토를 옆으로 젖히더니 제 몸 한가운데를 꿰뚫고 있는 말뚝을 드러내고는, 그걸 휙 잡아 뽑아 마치 지팡이라도 되는 양 태연히 식탁 위에 올려놓는 거였소.\n\n'자.' 그 형상이 사냥칼을 흘끗 보며 말했소. '나를 맞을 준비가 됐나?'\n\n'아직.' 남작이 받았소. '먼저 이 담배부터 다 피워야겠어.'\n\n'그럼 서두르시지.' 그 형상이 말했소.\n\n'바쁜 모양이군.' 남작이 말했소.\n\n'그래, 바쁘지.' 그 형상이 대답했소. '요즘 영국하고 프랑스 쪽에서 내 일감이 꽤 잘나가거든. 시간이 영 빠듯해.'\n\n'한잔하겠나?' 남작이 담뱃대 통으로 술병을 톡 건드리며 말했소.\n\n'열에 아홉은 마시지, 그것도 아주 진탕.' 그 형상이 무뚝뚝하게 받았소.\n\n'적당히는 안 마시고?' 남작이 물었소.\n\n'절대로.' 그 형상이 몸서리치며 대답했소. '적당히 마시면 기분이 좋아지거든.'\n\n남작은 새 친구를 다시 한번 훑어보며, 어지간히 별난 손님이라 여겼소. 그러고는 마침내, 지금 자기가 마음먹고 있는 그런 자그마한 일에 그가 직접 한몫 거드는지 물었소.\n\n'아니.' 그 형상이 얼버무리며 대답했소. '하지만 나는 늘 곁에 있지.'\n\n'그저 공정하게 진행되나 보려고, 그런 거겠지?' 남작이 말했소.\n\n'바로 그거지.' 그 형상이 제 말뚝을 만지작거리며 그 쇠 물미를 살펴보며 대답했소. '되도록 빨리 좀 해 주겠나? 보아하니 지금 돈도 너무 많고 한가하기까지 한 어떤 젊은 양반이 날 기다리고 있거든.'\n\n'돈이 너무 많아서 죽겠다고!' 남작이 어지간히 재미있어하며 소리쳤소. '하하! 그거 한번 웃기는군.' (남작이 이렇게 웃은 건 참으로 오랜만이었소.)\n\n'이봐.' 그 형상이 몹시 겁먹은 얼굴로 항의했소. '그러지 좀 마.'\n\n'왜 안 되는데?' 남작이 따져 물었소.\n\n'온몸이 다 아프니까.' 그 형상이 대답했소. '한숨이라면 얼마든지 쉬어도 좋아. 그건 나한테 보약이거든.'\n\n남작은 그 말이 나오기가 무섭게 기계적으로 한숨을 쉬었소. 그러자 그 형상은 다시 밝아져서, 더없이 살가운 정중함으로 그에게 사냥칼을 건넸지요.\n\n'그래도 꽤 괜찮은 발상이긴 하군.' 남작이 칼날을 만져 보며 말했소. '돈이 너무 많아서 죽는다라.'\n\n'쳇!' 유령이 짜증스레 말했소. '돈이 없어서, 아니면 적어서 죽는 거나 나을 게 하나도 없지.'\n\n그 정령이 이 말을 얼떨결에 내뱉어 제 무덤을 판 건지, 아니면 남작의 마음이 워낙 확고하게 굳었으니 무슨 말을 하든 상관없으리라 여긴 건지, 나로서는 알 길이 없소. 다만 내가 아는 건, 남작이 별안간 손을 멈추고 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨더니, 난생처음 무슨 새로운 빛이라도 비친 듯한 표정을 지었다는 것뿐이오.\n\n'아니, 그렇고말고.' 폰 쾰드웨트하우트가 말했소. '되돌릴 수 없을 만큼 나쁜 건 없지.'\n\n'텅 빈 금고만 빼고.' 정령이 외쳤소.\n\n'글쎄, 하지만 그것도 언젠가 다시 채워질 수 있잖나.' 남작이 말했소.\n\n'바가지 긁는 아내.' 정령이 으르렁댔소.\n\n'아! 조용하게 만들 수도 있고.' 남작이 말했소.\n\n'열셋이나 되는 자식.' 정령이 고함쳤소.\n\n'설마 다 잘못되기야 하겠나.' 남작이 말했소.\n\n정령은 남작이 이런 생각들을 한꺼번에 쏟아 내자 어지간히 사나워진 게 분명했소. 하지만 그는 웃어넘기려 애쓰며, 농담을 다 끝내거든 좀 알려 주면 고맙겠다고 했지요.\n\n'하지만 나는 농담하는 게 아닐세. 농담과는 거리가 멀어.' 남작이 따졌소.\n\n'그래, 그 말 들으니 다행이군.' 정령이 아주 험상궂은 얼굴로 말했소. '비유고 뭐고 없이, 농담은 정말이지 나한테는 죽음이거든. 자! 어서 이 음울한 세상을 떠나시지.'\n\n'글쎄.' 남작이 칼을 만지작거리며 말했소. '음울한 세상이긴 하지. 하지만 자네 쪽도 그리 나아 보이진 않는걸. 자네야말로 딱히 편안해 보이지가 않으니 말이야. 그러고 보니 말인데, 내가 이 세상을 떠난들 정말로 조금이라도 나아지리란 보장이 어디 있나!' 그가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤소. '그 생각은 미처 못 했군.'\n\n'어서!' 그 형상이 이를 갈며 외쳤소.\n\n'물러서!' 남작이 말했소. '더는 비참한 일들을 곱씹지 않겠다. 좋은 낯으로 이 일을 헤쳐 나가, 다시 한번 신선한 바람과 곰들을 맛보겠어. 그래도 안 되면, 마누라한테 따끔하게 한마디 하고, 폰 스윌렌하우젠 집안과는 아예 발을 끊어 버리지.' 이 말과 함께 남작은 의자에 털썩 주저앉아, 어찌나 크고 우렁차게 웃어 댔던지 방 안이 그 소리로 쩌렁쩌렁 울렸소.\n\n그 형상은 한두 걸음 뒷걸음질 치며, 그동안 극심한 공포의 눈빛으로 남작을 노려보았소. 그러고는 남작이 웃음을 그치자, 말뚝을 움켜쥐고 제 몸에 사납게 푹 찔러 넣더니, 소름 끼치는 비명을 지르며 사라져 버렸소.\n\n폰 쾰드웨트하우트는 다시는 그것을 보지 못했소. 한번 행동하기로 마음먹은 그는, 이내 남작 부인과 폰 스윌렌하우젠 집안을 정신 차리게 만들었고, 여러 해 뒤에 세상을 떠났소. 내가 알기로 부자는 아니었지만, 분명 행복한 사람이었지요. 그리고 곰과 멧돼지 사냥을 손수 곁에서 지켜보며 정성껏 가르친 많은 자식들을 남겼소. 그러니 모든 사람에게 주는 내 충고는 이거요. 혹시라도 비슷한 까닭으로 우울과 시름에 빠지거든 (참 많은 사람이 그러지요), 문제의 양면을 다 들여다보되 좋은 쪽에는 돋보기를 갖다 대시오. 그래도 여전히 허락 없이 이 세상을 뜨고 싶은 유혹이 든다면, 먼저 큰 담뱃대로 담배 한 대 피우고 술 한 병을 죄다 비운 다음, 그로그즈위그 남작의 갸륵한 본보기를 따르시오.\"\n\n\"새 마차가 준비됐습니다, 신사 숙녀 여러분, 채비하시지요.\" 새 마부가 안을 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n이 소식에 펀치는 부랴부랴 비워졌고, 마지막 이야기를 두고 더 왈가왈부할 틈도 없었다. 스퀴어스 씨가 희끗한 머리의 신사를 한쪽으로 끌어당겨, 자못 흥미진진한 얼굴로 뭔가 묻는 게 보였다. 그건 요크의 다섯 자매에 관한 것이었는데, 사실은 그 시절 요크셔의 수도원들이 기숙생을 두고 한 해에 얼마씩 받았는지 알려 줄 수 있겠느냐는 물음이었다.\n\n이윽고 여정이 다시 시작되었다. 니콜라스는 새벽녘에 잠이 들었고, 깨어나 보니 자기가 깜박 조는 사이에 그로그즈위그의 남작도 희끗한 머리의 신사도 둘 다 마차에서 내려 가 버린 걸 알고는 몹시 아쉬워했다. 그날 하루는 꽤나 불편하게 더디 흘러갔다. 그날 밤 여섯 시쯤, 그와 스퀴어스 씨와 어린아이들, 그리고 그들의 짐 전부가 그레타 브리지의 조지 앤드 뉴 여관 앞에 한꺼번에 내려졌다.\n\n스퀴어스 씨는 무사히 마차에서 내리자, 니콜라스와 아이들을 짐과 함께 길가에 세워 두었어. 마차가 말을 바꾸는 동안 그거나 구경하며 시간을 보내라는 거였지. 그러고는 자기는 술집으로 뛰어 들어가 술집 카운터에서 다리를 쭉 펴는 일을 했어. 몇 분 뒤에 그가 돌아왔는데, 빨개진 코와 짧은 딸꾹질을 보면 다리를 아주 톡톡히 펴고 온 게 틀림없었지. 그와 동시에 마당에서 낡은 조랑말 마차 한 대와 짐수레 한 대가 나왔어. 짐수레는 일꾼 둘이 몰고 있었지.\n\n\"애들이랑 상자는 짐수레에 실어.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 손을 비비며 말했어. \"이 젊은 양반하고 나는 마차로 갈 테니까. 타게, 니클비.\"\n\n니콜라스는 시키는 대로 했어. 스퀴어스 씨가 조랑말도 시키는 대로 따르게 하느라 좀 애를 먹긴 했지만, 두 사람은 출발했지. 어린 비참함을 한가득 실은 짐수레는 천천히 뒤따라오게 내버려 두고 말이야.\n\n\"춥나, 니클비?\" 한참을 말없이 달린 뒤에 스퀴어스가 물었어.\n\n\"네, 좀 그러네요, 솔직히 말하면요.\"\n\n\"뭐, 그건 탓할 일이 아니지.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"이런 날씨에 긴 여행이니까.\"\n\n\"두더보이즈 홀까지 아직 많이 남았나요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"여기서 한 삼 마일쯤.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"그런데 여기 내려와서는 그걸 홀이라고 부르면 안 돼.\"\n\n니콜라스는 왜 그런지 알고 싶은 듯 헛기침을 했어.\n\n\"사실 홀이 아니거든.\" 스퀴어스가 무뚝뚝하게 말했어.\n\n\"아, 그래요!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. 이 소식에 적잖이 놀란 거야.\n\n\"그래.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"런던에서는 그걸 홀이라고 부르지. 그게 듣기에 더 그럴듯하니까. 그런데 이 동네에서는 그런 이름으로 안 통해. 사람이 자기 집을 섬이라고 부르고 싶으면 그래도 돼. 그걸 막는 법은 없으니까, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"없겠지요.\" 니콜라스가 맞장구쳤어.\n\n이 짧은 대화가 끝나자 스퀴어스는 슬쩍 곁눈질로 동행을 살폈어. 그런데 니콜라스가 생각에 잠긴 듯 더는 무슨 말을 꺼낼 기색이 없자, 그저 목적지에 다다를 때까지 조랑말한테 채찍질이나 하며 만족했지.\n\n\"내려.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"이봐, 거기! 와서 이 말 좀 들여놔. 빨리빨리, 알겠어!\"\n\n선생이 이런저런 성급한 고함을 지르는 동안, 니콜라스는 학교를 둘러볼 틈이 있었어. 길쭉하고 추워 보이는 단층집이었지. 뒤에는 별채 몇 채가 띄엄띄엄 서 있었고, 헛간과 마구간이 붙어 있었어. 한두 분쯤 지나자 누가 마당 문 자물쇠를 따는 소리가 들렸어. 곧 키가 크고 비쩍 마른 소년이 손에 등불을 들고 나왔지.\n\n\"스마이크 너냐?\" 스퀴어스가 소리쳤어.\n\n\"네.\" 소년이 대답했어.\n\n\"그럼 왜 진작 안 나왔어, 이놈아?\"\n\n\"죄송해요, 불 앞에서 잠이 들었어요.\" 스마이크가 주눅 든 목소리로 대답했어.\n\n\"불! 무슨 불? 불이 어디 있어?\" 선생이 날카롭게 따져 물었어.\n\n\"부엌에 있는 거요.\" 소년이 대답했어. \"사모님이, 제가 안 자고 기다리니까 거기 가서 좀 녹여도 된다고 하셨어요.\"\n\n\"네 사모님은 바보야.\" 스퀴어스가 쏘아붙였어. \"추운 데 있었으면 훨씬 더 말똥말똥했을 거다, 내가 장담해.\"\n\n이때쯤 스퀴어스 씨는 마차에서 내려 있었어. 소년한테 조랑말을 돌보라고, 그리고 오늘 밤엔 더 이상 여물을 주지 말라고 단단히 일러두고는, 니콜라스한테 잠깐 현관에서 기다리라고 했어. 자기가 돌아가서 문을 열어 주겠다는 거였지.\n\n여행 내내 니콜라스를 짓누르던 갖가지 불길한 예감이, 혼자 남겨지자 곱절로 거세게 머릿속으로 밀려들었어. 집에서 너무나 멀리 떨어져 있다는 것, 그리고 돌아가고 싶은 마음이 아무리 간절해도 걸어가는 것 말고는 닿을 방법이 없다는 것이, 더없이 섬뜩한 빛깔로 그에게 다가왔어. 그 음산한 집과 캄캄한 창문들, 그리고 눈으로 뒤덮인 사방의 거친 들판을 올려다보자니, 그는 일찍이 한 번도 느껴 본 적 없는 마음과 정신의 무거움을 느꼈어.\n\n\"자 어서!\" 스퀴어스가 현관문으로 고개를 쑥 내밀고 소리쳤어. \"어디 있나, 니클비?\"\n\n\"여기요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"그럼 들어와.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"이 문으로 바람이 들이치는데, 사람을 자빠뜨릴 지경이야.\"\n\n니콜라스는 한숨을 쉬고 서둘러 들어갔어. 스퀴어스 씨는 문이 닫혀 있게 빗장을 지른 다음, 그를 작은 거실로 안내했지. 가구라곤 의자 몇 개와 벽에 걸린 누런 지도 한 장, 그리고 탁자 두 개가 전부인 휑한 방이었어. 한쪽 탁자에는 저녁 준비가 좀 차려져 있었고, 다른 탁자에는 가정교사용 참고서 한 권, 머리의 문법책 한 권, 학비 안내 카드 대여섯 장, 그리고 '왁퍼드 스퀴어스 귀하'라고 적힌 닳아 빠진 편지 한 통이 그림처럼 어수선하게 놓여 있었지.\n\n두 사람이 이 방에 들어선 지 채 이 분도 안 되어, 한 여자가 방으로 불쑥 뛰어들었어. 그러고는 스퀴어스 씨의 목덜미를 와락 끌어안고 쪽쪽 소리 나게 입을 두 번 맞췄지. 우체부가 문 두드리듯 연달아서 말이야. 이 부인은 뼈대가 굵고 우람한 체구였어. 스퀴어스 씨보다 머리 반쯤은 더 컸고, 무명 잠옷을 걸치고 있었지. 머리는 종이로 말아 놓았고, 더러운 잠자리용 모자를 쓴 데다, 그걸 턱밑에서 묶은 노란 무명 손수건이 그나마 봐 줄 만하게 해 주고 있었어.\n\n\"우리 스퀴어 자기, 잘 다녀왔어요?\" 부인이 장난스러운 투로, 아주 쉰 목소리로 말했어.\n\n\"잘 다녀왔지, 여보.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"소들은 어때?\"\n\n\"다들 괜찮아요, 한 마리도 빠짐없이.\" 부인이 대답했어.\n\n\"돼지들은?\" 스퀴어스가 물었어.\n\n\"당신 떠날 때랑 똑같아요.\"\n\n\"거참, 다행이군.\" 스퀴어스가 외투를 벗으며 말했어. \"애들도 다 그대로겠지?\"\n\n\"아, 그럼요, 다 멀쩡해요.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 쏘아붙이듯 대답했어. \"그 어린 피처란 놈이 열이 났어요.\"\n\n\"저런!\" 스퀴어스가 소리쳤어. \"빌어먹을 놈, 늘 무슨 일을 저질러요.\"\n\n\"그런 애는 처음 봐요, 정말이지.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말했어. \"무슨 병에 걸리든 꼭 옮기기까지 한다니까요. 내 말은 저게 고집이라는 거예요. 누가 뭐래도 아니라곤 안 믿어요. 나라면 매로 다스려서 빼 버렸을 거예요. 반년 전에도 그렇게 말했잖아요.\"\n\n\"그랬지, 여보.\" 스퀴어스가 맞장구쳤어. \"어떻게 할 수 있을지 한번 해 보자고.\"\n\n이 자그마한 애정 표현이 오가는 동안, 니콜라스는 어색하기 짝이 없게 방 한가운데 서 있었어. 복도로 물러가 있어야 하는 건지, 아니면 그냥 그 자리에 있어야 하는 건지 도무지 알 수가 없었거든. 그런데 이제 스퀴어스 씨가 그 난처함을 풀어 줬지.\n\n\"이 사람이 새로 온 젊은이요, 여보.\" 그가 말했어.\n\n\"아.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 니콜라스를 향해 고개를 까딱하더니, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 차갑게 훑어보며 대답했어.\n\n\"오늘 밤은 우리랑 한 끼 먹을 거고,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"내일 아침부터 애들 사이로 들어갈 거요. 오늘 밤은 여기서 대충 잠자리를 봐 줄 수 있겠지?\"\n\n\"어떻게든 해 봐야죠.\" 부인이 대답했어. \"잠자리가 어떻든 별로 안 따지시죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"네, 그럼요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"저는 까다롭지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그거 잘됐네요.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말했어. 이 부인의 재치는 주로 쏘아붙이는 데 있다고들 여겨졌기에, 스퀴어스 씨는 껄껄 웃었어. 그리고 니콜라스도 똑같이 웃기를 바라는 눈치였지.\n\n주인 내외 사이에 이런저런 대화가 더 오갔어. 스퀴어스 씨의 이번 여행이 얼마나 잘됐는지, 누가 학비를 냈고 누가 안 냈는지에 관한 얘기였지. 그러는 사이에 어린 하녀가 요크셔 파이와 식은 쇠고기를 좀 들고 들어왔어. 그것들이 탁자에 차려지자, 소년 스마이크가 맥주 한 주전자를 들고 나타났지.\n\n스퀴어스 씨는 외투 주머니에서 여러 아이들 앞으로 온 편지며, 자질구레한 서류들을 꺼내고 있었어. 가져온 것들이었지. 소년은 그 종이들을 불안하고 겁먹은 표정으로 흘끔거렸어. 그중 하나가 혹시 자기와 관련된 것일지도 모른다는 가냘픈 희망을 품은 것처럼 말이야. 그 눈빛은 무척 아렸어. 니콜라스의 가슴에 단번에 와닿았지. 길고도 아주 슬픈 사연을 들려주는 눈빛이었거든.\n\n그 눈빛 때문에 니콜라스는 소년을 좀 더 찬찬히 살펴보게 되었어. 그러고는 소년의 옷차림을 이루는 옷가지들이 어찌나 별나게 뒤섞여 있는지 놀라고 말았지. 소년은 적어도 열여덟이나 열아홉은 됐을 게 분명했고, 그 나이치고도 키가 컸어. 그런데도 그는 아주 어린 사내애한테나 입히는 몸에 붙는 옷을 걸치고 있었어. 팔다리가 우스꽝스러울 만큼 짧았지만, 비쩍 마른 그의 몸에는 그래도 충분히 헐렁한 옷이었지. 종아리 아래쪽까지 이 별난 옷차림과 완벽하게 어울리게 하려는 듯, 그는 아주 커다란 장화 한 켤레를 신고 있었어. 원래는 위쪽을 접어 신는 식으로 만든 거였지. 한때 어느 건장한 농부가 신었을 법한 그 장화는, 이제는 너무 기워지고 너덜너덜해서 거지한테도 못 줄 지경이었어. 그가 거기 온 지 얼마나 됐는지는 하늘이나 알겠지만, 그는 여전히 처음 내려올 때 입었던 그 속옷을 그대로 걸치고 있었어. 목둘레에는 너덜너덜한 어린애용 주름 깃이 둘려 있었는데, 거친 어른용 목도리로 절반쯤만 가려져 있었거든. 그는 다리를 절었어. 그리고 탁자를 차리느라 바쁜 척하면서, 그 편지들을 흘끔거렸지. 어찌나 간절하면서도, 또 어찌나 풀 죽고 희망 없는 눈빛이던지, 니콜라스는 차마 그를 지켜볼 수가 없을 지경이었어.\n\n\"거기서 뭘 꾸물거려, 스마이크?\" 스퀴어스 부인이 소리쳤어. \"그것들 좀 그냥 놔두지 못해?\"\n\n\"응?\" 스퀴어스가 고개를 들며 말했어. \"오! 너냐?\"\n\n\"네.\" 소년이 두 손을 맞잡으며 대답했어. 떨리는 손가락이 어쩔 줄 모르고 움직이는 걸 억지로 누르려는 듯이 말이야. \"혹시…\"\n\n\"뭐!\" 스퀴어스가 말했어.\n\n\"혹시… 누가… 아무 소식도 없었나요… 제 얘기요?\"\n\n\"코빼기도 없다.\" 스퀴어스가 짜증스럽게 대답했어.\n\n소년은 눈길을 거두고, 한 손을 얼굴에 갖다 댄 채 문 쪽으로 움직였어.\n\n\"한마디도 없어.\" 스퀴어스가 말을 이었어. \"앞으로도 없을 거고. 자, 이게 참 꼴좋은 일 아니냐? 그 오랜 세월 너를 여기 처박아 뒀는데, 처음 반년 치 빼곤 돈 한 푼 안 들어오고, 아무도 신경 안 쓰고, 네가 누구네 자식인지 알아낼 실마리 하나 없으니 말이야. 너 같은 큰 놈을 먹여 살려야 하는데, 그러고도 한 푼도 못 받을 게 뻔하니, 이거 참 꼴좋지 않으냐?\"\n\n소년은 무언가 떠올리려고 애쓰는 듯 손을 머리에 갖다 댔어. 그러고는 멍하니 묻는 이를 바라보다가, 차츰 미소를 짓더니 절뚝거리며 가 버렸지.\n\n\"내가 하는 말인데, 스퀴어스.\" 문이 닫히자 부인이 말했어. \"저 녀석 점점 머리가 이상해지는 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"아니길 바라야지.\" 선생이 말했어. \"바깥일에 손이 야무지고, 어쨌든 밥값은 하는 놈이니까. 머리가 돌았어도 우리한테 써먹을 만큼은 영리할 거야. 자, 어서 저녁이나 먹자고. 배도 고프고 피곤해서 빨리 자야겠어.\"\n\n이 말에 스퀴어스 씨만을 위한 특별한 스테이크가 들어왔어. 그는 곧장 그걸 아주 알뜰하게 해치우기 시작했지. 니콜라스도 의자를 끌어당겼지만, 입맛은 싹 가셔 버린 뒤였어.\n\n\"스테이크 어때요, 스퀴어스?\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"양고기처럼 연하군.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"당신도 좀 들어.\"\n\n\"난 한 입도 못 먹겠어요.\" 부인이 대답했어. \"이 젊은이는 뭘 먹겠대요, 여보?\"\n\n\"여기 있는 거 중에 뭐든 좋아하는 걸로.\" 스퀴어스가 아주 드물게 인심을 팍 쓰며 대답했어.\n\n\"뭘 드시겠어요, 너클보이 씨?\" 스퀴어스 부인이 물었어.\n\n\"괜찮으시면 파이를 좀 먹겠습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"아주 조금만요. 배가 별로 안 고파서요.\"\n\n\"뭐, 배도 안 고픈데 파이를 자르기는 아깝잖아요, 안 그래요?\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말했어. \"쇠고기를 좀 들어 볼래요?\"\n\n\"주시는 대로 먹겠습니다.\" 니콜라스가 멍하니 대답했어. \"저는 아무거나 다 괜찮습니다.\"\n\n이 대답을 듣자 스퀴어스 부인은 더없이 너그러운 표정이 되었어. 그리고 스퀴어스를 향해 고개를 끄덕였지. 이 젊은이가 제 분수를 아는 걸 알게 되어 흐뭇하다는 듯이 말이야. 그러고는 제 고운 손수 니콜라스에게 고기 한 점을 덜어 주었어.\n\n\"맥주 줄까요, 스퀴어 자기?\" 부인이 물었어. 눈을 찡긋하고 인상을 찌푸려서, 그 질문이 니콜라스에게 맥주를 줄지 말지를 묻는 거지, 그(스퀴어스)가 맥주를 마실지 묻는 게 아니라는 걸 알리면서 말이야.\n\n\"그럼.\" 스퀴어스가 똑같이 신호를 되보내며 말했어. \"한 잔 가득.\"\n\n그래서 니콜라스는 한 잔 가득 받았어. 그러고는 제 생각에 빠진 채로, 앞서 오간 이 모든 수작을 까맣게 모른 채 천진하게 그걸 마셨지.\n\n\"이 스테이크 보기 드물게 육즙이 좋군.\" 한동안 말없이 칼질을 부지런히 하던 스퀴어스가 칼과 포크를 내려놓으며 말했어.\n\n\"최상급 고기죠.\" 부인이 맞장구쳤어. \"내가 일부러 큼지막한 걸로 한 덩어리 샀거든요, 누구 주려고…\"\n\n\"누구 주려고!\" 스퀴어스가 황급히 외쳤어. \"설마 그…\"\n\n\"아니, 아니에요. 그 애들 주려는 게 아니라.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 받아쳤어. \"당신 집에 돌아올 때 먹이려고 일부러 산 거예요. 어머! 내가 그런 실수를 했을 거라고 생각한 거예요?\"\n\n\"이런, 여보, 난 당신이 무슨 말을 하려는지 몰랐지.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. 그새 얼굴이 새파래져 있었지.\n\n\"마음 졸일 거 없어요.\" 부인이 껄껄 웃으며 말했어. \"내가 그런 멍청이일 거라니! 참!\"\n\n이 대목의 대화는 좀 알아듣기 어려웠어. 그런데 동네에 떠도는 소문에 따르면 이런 거야. 스퀴어스 씨는 동물 학대에 곱게 반대하는 마음씨 좋은 사람이라, 제 명에 죽은 뿔 달린 짐승의 고기를 사다가 아이들한테 먹이는 일이 적잖이 있었다는 거지. 아마 그는 어린 신사분들 몫으로 마련해 둔 어느 맛난 고깃점을 자기도 모르게 먹어 치웠을까 봐 겁이 났던 모양이야.\n\n저녁 식사가 끝나고, 배고픈 눈을 한 작은 하녀가 그릇을 치웠어. 스퀴어스 부인은 그걸 자물쇠 채워 두러, 그리고 방금 도착한 다섯 아이들의 옷도 안전하게 거둬 두러 자리를 떴지. 그 아이들은 추위에 시달린 탓에, 죽음의 문턱으로 이어지는 그 험한 계단을 절반쯤 올라가 있는 상태였어. 그러고는 멀건 죽으로 가벼운 저녁을 대접받고, 작은 침대 하나에 나란히 뉘어졌지. 서로 몸을 녹이며, 마음 내키면 든든한 한 끼와 그 뒤에 나오는 따뜻한 무언가를 꿈꾸라는 거였어. 그리고 정말로 그런 꿈을 꿨을 가능성도 아주 없진 않았지.\n\n스퀴어스 씨는 자기를 위해 브랜디에 물 탄 독한 한 잔을 만들었어. 설탕 녹는 양까지 감안해서 반반씩 후하게 탄 거였지. 그리고 그의 다정한 반려는 니콜라스한테 같은 걸로 자그마한 한 잔의 그림자쯤 되게 타 줬어. 이렇게 하고 나서, 스퀴어스 부부는 불가로 바싹 다가앉았어. 발을 난로 망에 올린 채로, 머리를 맞대고 소곤소곤 은밀히 이야기를 나눴지. 그러는 동안 니콜라스는 가정교사용 참고서를 집어 들고, 잡다한 문제들 속의 흥미로운 이야기며 거기에 딸린 숫자들까지 죄다 읽었어. 그런데 자기가 뭘 하고 있는지 거의 생각도 의식도 없이, 마치 최면에 걸려 잠든 사람처럼 읽고 있었지.\n\n마침내 스퀴어스 씨는 무시무시하게 하품을 하더니, 이제 잘 시간이 됐다고 했어. 이 신호에 스퀴어스 부인과 하녀가 작은 짚 매트리스 하나와 담요 두 장을 끌고 들어와, 니콜라스를 위한 잠자리로 펴 주었지.\n\n\"내일은 자네 정식 침실에 넣어 주겠네, 니클비.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"어디 보자! 브룩스 침대에는 누가 자지, 여보?\"\n\n\"브룩스 침대요?\" 스퀴어스 부인이 곰곰 생각하며 말했어. \"제닝스, 어린 볼더, 그레이마시, 그리고 그 이름이 뭐더라.\"\n\n\"맞아, 그렇지.\" 스퀴어스가 맞장구쳤어. \"그래! 브룩스 침대는 꽉 찼군.\"\n\n'꽉 찼다고!' 니콜라스는 속으로 생각했어. '그렇겠지.'\n\n\"어딘가 자리가 하나 있긴 한데,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"지금 당장은 그게 어딘지 생각이 안 나는군. 어쨌든 그건 내일 다 정리하면 돼. 잘 자게, 니클비. 아침 일곱 시야, 명심하고.\"\n\n\"준비하고 있겠습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"안녕히 주무세요.\"\n\n\"내가 직접 와서 우물이 어딘지 알려 줌세.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"부엌 창턱에 비누가 늘 조그맣게 있을 거야. 그게 자네 거야.\"\n\n니콜라스는 눈은 휘둥그레졌지만 입은 열지 않았어. 스퀴어스가 다시 나가려다가, 또 한 번 뒤를 돌아봤지.\n\n\"자네한테 무슨 수건을 줄지는 나도 잘 모르겠네.\" 그가 말했어. \"그래도 내일 아침엔 아무거나로 대충 해결하면, 스퀴어스 부인이 낮 동안에 그건 마련해 줄 거야. 여보, 잊지 말아요.\"\n\n\"내가 챙길게요.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 대답했어. \"그리고 젊은이, 당신도 잘 챙겨서 제일 먼저 씻으라고. 선생은 늘 먼저 씻어야 하는데, 그것들이 틈만 나면 선수를 친다니까.\"\n\n그러고 나서 스퀴어스 씨는 브랜디 병을 치우라고 스퀴어스 부인을 슬쩍 찔렀어. 니콜라스가 밤중에 멋대로 따라 마실까 봐서였지. 부인이 부랴부랴 그걸 낚아챘고, 둘은 함께 방을 나갔어.\n\n혼자 남겨진 니콜라스는 몹시 동요하고 흥분한 상태로 방 안을 대여섯 번 왔다 갔다 했어. 그러다 차츰 마음이 가라앉자, 의자에 앉아 마음속으로 다짐했지. 무슨 일이 닥치든, 한동안은 자기 앞에 놓인 어떤 비참함이라도 견뎌 보겠다고 말이야. 그리고 어머니와 누이의 처지가 얼마나 막막한지를 떠올리며, 삼촌에게 그들을 곤경 속에 내버려 둘 빌미를 주지 않겠다고 다짐했어. 좋은 결심은 그것이 우러난 마음에 얼마간 좋은 효과를 내기 마련이지. 그는 덜 우울해졌어. 그리고 젊음이란 워낙 희망차고 활기찬 것이라, 두더보이즈 홀의 사정이 보이는 것보다는 그래도 나을지 모른다고 기대하기까지 했지.\n\n그가 새삼 다시 기운을 차린 듯한 기분으로 잘 채비를 하고 있는데, 봉인된 편지 한 통이 외투 주머니에서 툭 떨어졌어. 런던을 떠나느라 정신없는 통에 미처 챙기지 못했고, 그 뒤로도 생각이 안 났던 거지. 그런데 그게 단번에 그에게 뉴먼 노그스의 수상쩍던 행동을 떠올리게 했어.\n\n\"이런!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"글씨가 어쩜 이렇게 별나담!\"\n\n편지는 그 자신 앞으로 온 것이었고, 아주 더러운 종이에 쓰여 있었어. 게다가 글씨가 어찌나 비비 꼬이고 비틀어졌는지 거의 알아볼 수가 없을 지경이었지. 한참을 끙끙대고 갖은 애를 쓴 끝에, 그는 가까스로 이렇게 읽어 냈어.\n\n친애하는 젊은이에게.\n\n나는 세상을 압니다. 자네 아버지는 몰랐지. 그랬더라면 보답받을 가망이 전혀 없는데도 나한테 친절을 베풀지 않았을 테니까. 자네도 세상을 모르네. 그랬더라면 이런 여행길에 오르지 않았을 테니까.\n\n혹시 런던에서 몸 둘 곳이 필요하면 (이런 말에 화내지 말게, 나도 한때는 그런 날이 절대 안 올 줄 알았으니까), 내가 사는 데를 그들이 알 거야. 골든 스퀘어의 실버 스트리트에 있는 크라운 간판을 단 집일세. 실버 스트리트와 제임스 스트리트가 만나는 모퉁이고, 양쪽으로 다 술집 문이 나 있어. 밤에 와도 돼. 한때는 아무도 부끄러워하지 않았지. 그건 됐고. 다 지난 일이니까.\n\n틀린 데가 있어도 봐주게. 나는 이제 멀쩡한 외투를 어떻게 입는지조차 잊어버렸을 거야. 옛날 방식은 죄다 잊어버렸거든. 철자법도 그것들이랑 같이 잊어버렸을지 모르겠네.\n\n# NEWMAN NOGGS.\n\n뉴먼 노그스.\n추신. 혹시 바너드 캐슬 근처에 가게 되면, 킹스 헤드에 좋은 맥주가 있어. 나를 안다고 말하게. 그럼 분명히 자네한테 돈을 안 받을 거야. 거기선 노그스 씨라고 해도 돼. 그땐 내가 신사였으니까. 정말 그랬다네.\n\n기록으로 남기기엔 영 체면이 안 서는 일일지도 모르지만, 이 편지를 접어 수첩에 넣고 나서, 니콜라스 니클비의 두 눈은 눈물이라 해도 좋을 만한 물기로 흐려져 있었어.\n\n혹독한 날씨에 이백 몇 마일을 달려온다는 건, 딱딱한 침대를 부드럽게 만드는 데 사람이 궁리해 낼 수 있는 가장 좋은 방법 가운데 하나야. 어쩌면 꿈까지 달콤하게 해 주는지도 몰라. 니콜라스의 거친 잠자리 위를 맴돌며 귀에 대고 속삭이던 그 가벼운 꿈들은 즐겁고 행복한 것들이었거든. 그가 정말로 빠르게 한밑천 모으고 있는데, 꺼져 가는 촛불의 희미한 불빛이 눈앞에 어른거렸어. 그리고 스퀴어스 씨의 일부라는 걸 어렵지 않게 알아챌 수 있는 목소리가, 이제 일어날 시간이라고 일러 주었지.\n\n\"일곱 시가 넘었어, 니클비.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"벌써 아침이 됐나요?\" 니콜라스가 침대에서 일어나 앉으며 물었어.\n\n\"아! 됐고말고.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"게다가 얼음처럼 차게 준비됐지. 자, 니클비, 어서, 벌떡 일어나라고, 알겠어?\"\n\n니콜라스는 더 이상 재촉이 필요 없었어. 곧장 \"벌떡 일어나서\" 스퀴어스 씨가 손에 든 가느다란 촛불 빛에 옷을 챙겨 입기 시작했지.\n\n\"이거 참 잘됐군.\" 그 신사가 말했어. \"펌프가 얼어붙었어.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 니콜라스가 별로 관심도 없이 말했어.\n\n\"그래.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"오늘 아침엔 씻을 수가 없겠어.\"\n\n\"못 씻는다고요!\" 니콜라스가 소리쳤어.\n\n\"그래, 한 방울도 못 써.\" 스퀴어스가 쌀쌀맞게 받아쳤어. \"그러니 우물의 얼음을 깨서 애들 쓸 물을 한 통 길어 올릴 때까지는, 마른 채로 대충 문지르는 걸로 만족해야 해. 멀거니 날 쳐다보지 말고, 좀 서두르라고, 알겠어?\"\n\n니콜라스는 더 이상 토를 달지 않고 옷을 주섬주섬 걸쳤어. 그러는 동안 스퀴어스는 덧창을 열고 촛불을 불어 껐지. 그때 그의 다정한 아내 목소리가 복도에서 들렸어. 문을 열어 달라는 거였지.\n\n\"들어와요, 여보.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어.\n\n스퀴어스 부인이 들어왔어. 전날 밤 그 몸매의 균형을 자랑했던 그 투박한 잠옷 윗도리를 여전히 걸친 채였고, 거기다 꽤 오래돼 보이는 비버 가죽 보닛까지 얹고 있었지. 앞에서 말한 그 잠자리용 모자 위에다 아주 가뿐하고 편안하게 쓰고 있었어.\n\n\"빌어먹을 물건들.\" 부인이 찬장을 열며 말했어. \"학교 숟가락을 도무지 못 찾겠네.\"\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마, 여보.\" 스퀴어스가 달래듯 말했어. \"별일 아니야.\"\n\n\"별일 아니라니, 무슨 그런 말을!\" 스퀴어스 부인이 날카롭게 받아쳤어. \"오늘이 유황 먹이는 날이잖아요?\"\n\n\"깜빡했군, 여보.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어. \"그래, 맞아, 그렇지. 우리는 가끔 애들 피를 깨끗하게 해 준단다, 니클비.\"\n\n\"피는 무슨 얼어 죽을 피야.\" 그의 아내가 말했어. \"젊은 양반, 우리가 그저 애들 피 깨끗하게 해 주자고 유황 가루랑 당밀에 돈을 쓰는 줄은 알지 마요. 우리가 그런 식으로 장사하는 줄 안다면 큰 오산이니까, 똑똑히 일러두는 거예요.\"\n\n\"여보.\" 스퀴어스가 얼굴을 찌푸리며 말했어. \"흠!\"\n\n\"오! 쓸데없는 소리.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 받아쳤어. \"이 젊은 양반이 여기서 선생 노릇을 할 거라면, 단번에 알아 둬야지. 우리는 애들한테 어리석은 짓 할 생각이 없다는 걸 말이에요. 애들한테 유황이랑 당밀을 먹이는 건, 첫째로 약 같은 걸 뭐라도 안 먹이면 늘 골골거리며 한없이 속을 썩일 테니 그렇고, 둘째로 그걸 먹으면 입맛이 떨어지는데다 아침이랑 저녁밥보다 싸게 먹히니까 그래요. 그러니까 애들한테도 좋고 우리한테도 좋고, 동시에 그런 거지. 이만하면 공평하잖아요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n이렇게 설명을 늘어놓고 스퀴어스 부인은 벽장에 머리를 들이밀고 숟가락을 더 샅샅이 뒤지기 시작했어. 스퀴어스 씨도 거들었지. 그러는 동안 둘 사이에 몇 마디가 오갔는데, 목소리가 찬장에 막혀 어렴풋하게만 들렸어. 그래서 니콜라스가 알아들을 수 있는 거라곤, 스퀴어스 씨는 부인이 한 말이 분별없다고 했고, 스퀴어스 부인은 남편이 한 말이 \"헛소리\"라고 했다는 정도였지.\n\n엄청난 뒤짐과 헤집기가 이어졌지만 헛수고로 끝나자, 스마이크가 불려 왔어. 스퀴어스 부인한테 떠밀리고 스퀴어스 씨한테 한 대 쥐어박혔지. 그런 대접을 받자 머리가 번쩍 든 모양인지, 어쩌면 숟가락이 스퀴어스 부인 주머니에 있을지도 모른다는 의견을 내놓았어. 그리고 정말로 그랬지. 그런데 스퀴어스 부인은 아까 그게 자기한테 없다고 분명히 우겼던 터라, 스마이크는 안주인 말에 토를 단 죄로 또 한 대 귀싸대기를 얻어맞았어. 거기다 앞으로 좀 더 공손하게 굴지 않으면 흠씬 두들겨 패겠다는 으름장도 따라왔지. 그러니 자기 의견을 내놓고도 별로 득 볼 게 없었던 셈이야.\n\n\"정말 더없이 소중한 여자야, 저 사람은, 니클비.\" 아내가 그 머슴 같은 아이를 앞세워 떠밀고 서둘러 나가자 스퀴어스가 말했어.\n\n\"그러시겠지요, 선생님!\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"저런 여자는 또 없어.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"저런 사람은 또 없지. 저 여자는 말이야, 니클비, 언제나 똑같아. 늘 지금처럼 부지런하고, 활기차고, 야무지고, 알뜰살뜰한 사람이지.\"\n\n니콜라스는 그렇게 펼쳐진 즐거운 가정의 앞날을 떠올리며 저도 모르게 한숨을 내쉬었어. 그런데 다행히 스퀴어스는 제 생각에 너무 빠져 있어서 그걸 알아채지 못했지.\n\n\"내가 런던에 올라가면 늘 이렇게 말한단다.\" 스퀴어스가 말을 이었어. \"저 사람은 애들한테 어머니 같은 존재라고 말이야. 아니, 어머니 이상이지. 열 배는 더 어머니 같아. 저 여자는 애들한테 이런 일들을 해 준단다, 니클비. 세상 어머니 절반은 제 친아들한테도 안 해 줄 그런 일들을 말이야.\"\n\n\"그렇겠지요, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n그런데 사실은 이래. 스퀴어스 씨와 부인은 둘 다 아이들을 자기네의 당연하고도 천연의 적으로 여겼어. 다시 말해, 두 사람은 자기네 일이자 직업이란 모든 아이한테서 짜낼 수 있는 만큼 최대한 짜내는 거라고 믿고 또 그렇게 여겼지. 이 점에서 둘은 의견이 딱 맞았고, 거기에 맞춰 한마음으로 움직였어. 두 사람의 유일한 차이라면, 스퀴어스 부인은 그 적을 향해 드러내 놓고 거리낌 없이 싸움을 벌였고, 스퀴어스는 집 안에서도 제 못된 짓을 늘 하던 버릇대로 약간의 거짓으로 덮었다는 거지. 마치 언젠가는 제 스스로를 속여서, 자기는 참 좋은 사람이라고 제 마음을 구슬릴 수 있을 거라는 생각이라도 정말 품은 것처럼 말이야.\n\n\"자, 됐고.\" 스퀴어스는 조교의 머릿속에서 그런 방향으로 흘러가던 생각의 갈래를 끊으며 말했어. \"교실로 가자고. 그리고 내 학교용 외투 입는 것 좀 거들어 줘, 응?\"\n\n니콜라스는 주인이 외투 입는 걸 도왔어. 복도 못에 걸려 있던 걸 스퀴어스가 내린, 낡은 퍼스티언 사냥 재킷이었지. 그리고 스퀴어스는 회초리로 무장하더니, 앞장서서 마당을 가로질러 집 뒤쪽 문으로 갔어.\n\n\"자.\" 둘이 함께 안으로 들어서자 교장이 말했어. \"이게 우리 가게야, 니클비!\"\n\n어찌나 북적이는 광경인지, 또 눈길을 끄는 게 어찌나 많은지, 니콜라스는 처음엔 그저 멀거니 둘러보기만 했어. 사실 아무것도 제대로 보지 못한 채로 말이야. 그러다 차츰 그곳의 모습이 또렷해졌지. 휑하고 지저분한 방이었고, 창문이 두어 개 있었는데, 그중 십분의 일쯤이나 유리일까, 나머지는 죄다 헌 공책이며 종이로 틀어막혀 있었어. 길고 낡은 책상이 두어 개 있었는데, 베이고 파이고 잉크가 묻고 온갖 식으로 망가져 있었지. 긴 의자도 두세 개 있었고, 스퀴어스용 책상이 따로 하나, 조교용 책상이 또 하나 있었어. 천장은 헛간처럼 들보와 서까래로 떠받쳐져 있었고, 벽은 어찌나 얼룩지고 색이 바랬는지, 페인트나 회칠을 한 번이라도 한 적이 있는지조차 알 수 없을 지경이었어.\n\n그런데 그 학생들이라니, 그 어린 귀공자들이라니! 이 소굴에서 자기가 애쓴들 무슨 좋은 결과가 나올까 하던 마지막 희미한 희망의 자취, 가장 아득한 한 줄기 기대마저 어떻게 사라져 버렸는지 몰라. 니콜라스가 망연자실해서 둘러보는 순간 말이야. 창백하고 수척한 얼굴들, 비쩍 마르고 뼈만 앙상한 몸들, 늙은이 같은 표정을 한 아이들, 팔다리에 쇠붙이를 댄 불구의 아이들, 자라다 만 아이들, 길고 깡마른 다리가 구부정한 몸뚱이를 간신히 지탱하는 아이들이 한꺼번에 눈앞에 몰려들었어. 짓무른 눈, 언청이, 굽은 발, 그리고 부모가 제 자식한테 천륜을 거스르는 혐오를 품었음을 말해 주거나, 어린 목숨이 갓난아이 때부터 잔인함과 방치를 끔찍하게 견뎌 온 것임을 말해 주는 온갖 추함과 뒤틀림이 거기 있었지. 본래는 잘생겼을 작은 얼굴들이 부루퉁하고 고집스러운 고통의 찡그림으로 어두워져 있었어. 눈빛은 꺼지고 고움은 사라지고 무력함만 남은 어린 시절이 거기 있었지. 감옥의 죄수처럼 납빛 눈으로 음울하게 곱씹는, 험상궂은 얼굴의 아이들도 있었어. 그리고 못난 부모의 죄를 물려받은 어린것들도 있었는데, 돈 받고 길러 준 유모를 그리워하며 울고, 외로움 속에서조차 외로워했지. 모든 다정한 정과 사랑이 싹트는 순간 짓밟히고, 모든 어리고 건강한 감정이 매질당하고 굶주려 꺾이고, 부어오른 가슴 속에서 곪을 수 있는 온갖 복수심이 말없이 그 마음 한가운데로 악한 길을 파먹어 들어가니, 여기서 얼마나 끔찍한 지옥의 씨앗이 자라나고 있었던지!\n\n그래도 이 광경은, 괴롭긴 했지만, 어딘가 우스꽝스러운 구석도 있었어. 니콜라스만큼 마음이 쓰이지 않는 구경꾼이었다면 픽 웃음이 나올 만한 그런 면 말이야. 스퀴어스 부인이 책상 하나 앞에 서서, 어마어마하게 큰 대야에 담긴 유황과 당밀을 떡 맡고 있었어. 그 맛깔난 혼합물을 아이들한테 한 명씩 차례로 큼지막하게 한 술씩 떠 먹였지. 그러느라 쓰는 게 흔해 빠진 나무 숟가락이었는데, 원래는 무슨 거대한 팽이라도 만들려고 깎은 게 아닐까 싶을 만큼 컸어. 그래서 어린 신사들마다 입이 한참씩 벌어졌지. 다들 무거운 매질을 면하려면 한 술 가득을 단숨에 삼켜야 했거든. 또 다른 구석에는, 함께 모여 서로 의지하려고 옹기종기 붙어 앉은 어린아이들이 있었어. 전날 밤 도착한 아이들이었지. 그중 셋은 아주 큼지막한 가죽 반바지를 입었고, 둘은 헌 바지를 입었는데, 보통 속바지보다도 좀 더 꽉 끼는 거였어. 거기서 멀지 않은 데에는 스퀴어스 씨의 어린 아들이자 상속자가 앉아 있었어. 제 아비를 쏙 빼닮은 아이였지. 그 애가 스마이크의 손길 아래서 힘껏 발길질을 해 대고 있었는데, 스마이크가 그 애한테 새 장화를 신겨 주고 있었거든. 그런데 그 장화가, 내려오는 길에 어린아이들 중 제일 작은 애가 신었던 것이랑 어쩐지 영 수상쩍게 닮아 있었어. 그 어린아이 본인도 그렇게 생각하는 듯했지. 그게 가로채여 가는 걸 더없이 처량하고 어이없다는 눈빛으로 바라보고 있었거든. 이들 말고도, 길게 줄지어 차례를 기다리는 아이들이 있었어. 당밀을 받아먹을 차례인데, 도무지 달갑지 않은 표정들이었지. 또 한 줄은 방금 그 고역에서 벗어난 아이들이었는데, 만족과는 거리가 먼, 온갖 일그러진 입 모양을 짓고 있었어. 죄다 어찌나 잡다하고 안 어울리고 별스러운 옷을 걸쳤는지, 도저히 웃음을 참을 수 없을 지경이었어. 거기 따라붙은 더러움과 어수선함과 병색의 추한 몰골만 아니었더라면 말이지.\n\n\"자.\" 스퀴어스가 회초리로 책상을 쾅 내리치자, 어린아이들 절반이 화들짝 놀라 장화에서 튀어나올 뻔했어. \"약 먹이는 건 다 끝났나?\"\n\n\"방금 끝났어요.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 서두르다가 마지막 아이를 사레들리게 하고는, 나무 숟가락으로 그 애 정수리를 톡톡 두드려 정신 차리게 하며 말했어. \"자, 스마이크, 이제 치워. 서둘러!\"\n\n스마이크가 대야를 들고 질질 끌듯이 나갔어. 스퀴어스 부인은 곱슬머리 어린아이 하나를 불러다 거기에 손을 닦고는, 서둘러 그 애를 따라 일종의 세탁장 같은 데로 나갔어. 거기엔 작은 불이 피워져 있고 커다란 솥이 하나 있었고, 자그마한 나무 그릇들이 여러 개 판자 위에 죽 놓여 있었지.\n\n스퀴어스 부인은 굶주린 하인의 도움을 받아 이 그릇들에다 갈색 죽 같은 걸 부었어. 겉을 벗겨 낸 바늘꽂이를 물에 탄 것처럼 생긴 거였는데, 죽이라고 불렀지. 그릇마다 갈색 빵을 작은 쐐기처럼 한 조각씩 꽂아 넣었어. 아이들은 그 빵으로 죽을 떠먹고, 다 떠먹은 다음엔 빵 자체를 먹어 치우는 걸로 아침 식사를 끝냈어. 그러자 스퀴어스 씨가 엄숙한 목소리로 말했지. \"우리가 받은 양식에 대해, 주께서 우리로 진실로 감사하게 하소서!\" 그러고는 자기 식사를 하러 가 버렸어.\n\n니콜라스는 죽 한 그릇으로 배를 채웠어. 어떤 미개인들이 흙을 삼키는 것과 비슷한 이유에서였지. 먹을 게 아무것도 없을 때 거북하게 배고프지 않으려고 말이야. 거기다 제 자리 덕에 받은 버터 바른 빵 한 조각까지 마저 해치우고는, 자리에 앉아 수업 시간을 기다렸어.\n\n아이들이 모두 어찌나 조용하고 슬퍼 보이는지 눈에 안 띌 수가 없었어. 교실의 떠들썩한 소음도, 와글거리는 소리도 없었지. 시끌벅적한 장난도, 신나는 웃음소리도 없었어. 아이들은 옹기종기 웅크리고 함께 떨면서, 돌아다닐 기운조차 없어 보였어. 조금이라도 움직이거나 장난칠 기색을 보이는 학생이라곤 스퀴어스 도련님 하나뿐이었지. 그런데 그 애의 주된 재미라는 게 새 장화로 다른 아이들 발가락을 밟는 거였으니, 그 넘쳐 나는 기운은 유쾌하기보다 오히려 거슬렸어.\n\n삼십 분쯤 늦어진 뒤에 스퀴어스 씨가 다시 나타났고, 아이들은 제자리에 앉아 책을 펼쳤어. 그런데 그 책이란 게 평균적으로 학생 여덟 명에 한 권쯤 돌아갈까 말까였지. 몇 분이 흘렀어. 그동안 스퀴어스 씨는 아주 심오한 표정을 지었지. 마치 그 모든 책 속에 든 내용을 완벽하게 꿰뚫고 있고, 마음만 먹으면 거기 적힌 한 글자 한 글자를 죄다 외울 수 있다는 듯이 말이야. 그러더니 그 신사는 첫 번째 반을 불러냈어.\n\n이 호출에 따라 교장 책상 앞에 허수아비 같은 아이 여섯이 죽 늘어섰어. 무릎이며 팔꿈치가 다 해진 차림이었지. 그중 하나가 찢어지고 더러운 책 한 권을 그 학식 깊은 눈앞에 갖다 놨어.\n\n\"이게 영어 철자법과 철학 첫 번째 반이야, 니클비.\" 스퀴어스가 니콜라스더러 옆에 서라고 손짓하며 말했어. \"라틴어 반도 하나 꾸려서 자네한테 맡길 거야. 자, 그럼, 첫 번째 아이는 어디 있지?\"\n\n\"저, 선생님, 그 애는 뒤쪽 응접실 창문을 닦고 있어요.\" 철학 반의 임시 우두머리가 말했어.\n\n\"그렇지, 맞아.\" 스퀴어스가 받아쳤어. \"우리는 실전 방식으로 가르친단다, 니클비. 정통 교육 체계지. 닦, 다 — 닦다, 동사, 환하게 만들다, 문지르다. 창, 문 — 창문, 여닫이창. 아이가 이걸 책에서 익히면, 가서 직접 그걸 하는 거야. 지구본을 쓰는 거랑 똑같은 원리지. 두 번째 아이는 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"저, 선생님, 그 애는 정원 잡초를 뽑고 있어요.\" 작은 목소리가 대답했어.\n\n\"그렇지.\" 스퀴어스가 조금도 당황하지 않고 말했어. \"맞아. 식, 물, 학 — 식물학, 명사, 식물에 관한 지식. 식물학이 식물에 관한 지식이란 걸 익히고 나면, 가서 식물들을 직접 아는 거지. 그게 우리 방식이야, 니클비. 어떻게 생각해?\"\n\n\"하여간 아주 쓸모 있는 방식이네요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"그렇고말고.\" 스퀴어스가 조교의 말투에 실린 강조를 알아채지 못한 채 받았어. \"세 번째 아이, 말이 뭐지?\"\n\n\"짐승이요, 선생님.\" 그 아이가 대답했어.\n\n\"맞아.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"안 그런가, 니클비?\"\n\n\"그 점은 의심할 여지가 없겠지요, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"당연히 없지.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"말은 네발짐승이고, 네발짐승은 짐승을 뜻하는 라틴어지. 문법을 뗀 사람이라면 누구나 아는 거야. 안 그러면 문법책이 다 무슨 소용이겠어?\"\n\n\"정말, 무슨 소용이겠어요!\" 니콜라스가 멍하니 말했어.\n\n\"넌 그걸 완벽히 익혔으니까.\" 스퀴어스가 그 아이를 돌아보며 말을 이었어. \"가서 내 말이나 돌보고, 잘 문질러 닦아. 안 그러면 내가 널 문질러 줄 테니까. 나머지 반은 가서 물을 길어 올려. 그만하라고 할 때까지 말이야. 내일이 빨래하는 날이라서 가마솥을 가득 채워야 하거든.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 그는 첫 번째 반을 실전 철학 실습장으로 내보냈어. 그러고는 니콜라스를 반쯤 능청맞고 반쯤 미심쩍은 눈으로 살폈지. 마치 이쯤 됐으면 이 친구를 어떻게 봐야 할지 영 확신이 안 선다는 듯이 말이야.\n\n\"우리는 이렇게 하는 거야, 니클비.\" 그가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말했어.\n\n니콜라스는 알아챌까 말까 할 만큼만 어깨를 으쓱하고는, 그렇게 하는 걸 봤다고 말했어.\n\n\"게다가 아주 좋은 방식이지.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"자, 그럼 저기 어린아이 열넷을 데리고 책 좀 읽혀 봐. 알다시피 자네도 슬슬 쓸모 있는 일을 시작해야 하니까. 여기서 빈둥대는 건 안 되거든.\"\n\n스퀴어스 씨는 이 말을 마치 문득 떠오른 것처럼 했어. 조교한테 말을 너무 많이 해선 안 되겠다는 생각이 들었거나, 아니면 조교가 이 시설에 대한 칭찬을 충분히 늘어놓지 않는다는 생각이 들었거나, 둘 중 하나였지. 아이들은 새 선생을 둘러싸고 반원으로 늘어섰어. 그리고 니콜라스는 곧 아이들이 더듬더듬 느릿느릿 머뭇거리며 읽어 내려가는 소리를 듣게 됐지. 좀 더 케케묵은 철자 교본에서나 찾아볼 수 있는, 그 흥미진진한 이야기들 말이야.\n\n이 신나는 일을 하는 동안 아침나절은 무겁게 흘러갔어. 한 시가 되자 아이들은 — 이미 휘저은 죽이랑 감자로 입맛이 싹 가셔 버린 채 — 부엌에 앉아 딱딱한 소금에 절인 소고기를 먹었어. 니콜라스는 황송하게도 제 몫을 외딴 제 책상으로 가져가 거기서 편히 먹어도 좋다는 허락을 받았지. 이게 끝난 뒤엔 또 한 시간 동안 교실에 웅크려 추위에 떨었고, 그러고 나서 수업이 다시 시작됐어.\n\n스퀴어스 씨한테는 버릇이 하나 있었어. 반년에 한 번씩 런던에 다녀온 다음이면 아이들을 모아 놓고 일종의 보고를 하는 거였지. 자기가 만난 친척이며 친구들 얘기, 들은 소식, 가져온 편지들, 치러진 돈, 안 치러지고 밀린 외상값, 뭐 그런 것들에 관해서 말이야. 이 엄숙한 행사는 늘 그가 돌아온 다음 날 오후에 벌어졌어. 어쩌면 아이들이 오전 내내 마음 졸이며 단단히 각오를 다지라는 거였는지도 몰라. 아니면 스퀴어스 씨 자신이 이른 저녁을 먹은 뒤 으레 즐기던 따끈한 술 몇 잔 덕에 더 매섭고 단호해졌기 때문인지도 모르지. 어쨌거나 아이들은 집 창문에서, 정원에서, 마구간에서, 외양간에서 불려 왔고, 학교 전체가 빠짐없이 모였어. 그때 스퀴어스 씨가 손에 종이 한 묶음을 들고, 뒤따라 스퀴어스 부인이 회초리 한 쌍을 들고 방에 들어와 조용히 하라고 선언했지.\n\n\"누구든 허락 없이 한마디라도 지껄이면,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 부드럽게 말했어. \"등짝 가죽을 벗겨 버릴 테다.\"\n\n이 특별 선언은 바라던 효과를 냈어. 곧장 죽은 듯한 침묵이 깔렸지. 그 한가운데서 스퀴어스 씨가 말을 이었어.\n\n\"얘들아, 내가 런던에 다녀왔다. 그리고 변함없이 튼튼하고 멀쩡한 몸으로 내 가족과 너희한테 돌아왔지.\"\n\n반년에 한 번 하는 관례대로, 아이들은 이 반가운 소식에 힘없는 만세를 세 번 외쳤어. 만세라니, 그런 만세를! 으슬으슬 추위에 떨면서 짜낸 안간힘 같은 거였지.\n\n\"몇몇 아이들 부모를 만나고 왔다.\" 스퀴어스가 종이를 넘기며 말을 이었어. \"자기 아들이 어떻게 지내는지 듣고 어찌나 기뻐하던지, 데려갈 기색이 전혀 없더구나. 물론 이건 모두에게 아주 흐뭇한 일이지.\"\n\n스퀴어스가 이렇게 말하자 두세 개의 손이 두세 개의 눈으로 갔어. 하지만 어린 신사들 대부분은 딱히 입에 올릴 만한 부모도 없는 터라, 그 일에 이러나저러나 도통 관심이 없었지.\n\n\"애를 먹은 일도 있었다.\" 스퀴어스가 아주 험상궂은 얼굴로 말했어. \"볼더 아버지가 이 파운드 십 실링을 덜 냈어. 볼더 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"여기 있어요, 선생님.\" 스무 개쯤 되는 호들갑스러운 목소리가 받았어. 아이들이란 정말 어른이랑 꼭 닮았다니까.\n\n\"이리 와, 볼더.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어.\n\n손마다 사마귀가 잔뜩 난, 병약해 보이는 아이가 제자리에서 나와 주인의 책상 쪽으로 갔어. 그러고는 애원하는 눈빛으로 스퀴어스의 얼굴을 올려다봤지. 그 애 자신의 얼굴은 심장이 쿵쾅쿵쾅 빨리 뛰는 통에 백지장처럼 하얬어.\n\n\"볼더.\" 스퀴어스가 아주 느릿느릿 말했어. 말마따나 이 녀석을 어디부터 잡을까 궁리하는 중이었거든. \"볼더, 네 아비가 만약에 — 어라, 이게 뭐냐, 이 녀석아?\"\n\n스퀴어스는 말하면서 그 애 재킷 소맷부리를 잡아채 손을 끌어올리더니, 보란 듯이 끔찍하고 역겹다는 표정으로 그걸 들여다봤어.\n\n\"이게 뭐냐, 이놈아?\" 교장이 다그치며, 대답을 재촉하려고 회초리로 한 대 후려쳤어.\n\n\"저도 어쩔 수가 없어요, 정말이에요, 선생님.\" 그 아이가 울면서 대꾸했어. \"자꾸 나는걸요. 더러운 일을 해서 그런 것 같아요, 선생님 — 아니, 저도 이게 뭔지 모르겠지만, 제 잘못은 아니에요.\"\n\n\"볼더.\" 스퀴어스가 소맷동을 걷어 올리고, 회초리를 단단히 쥐려고 오른손 바닥에 침을 발랐어. \"넌 구제불능 망나니 녀석이야. 지난번 매질이 약발이 안 들었으니, 이번엔 또 한 번 해서 그 버릇을 두들겨 빼낼 수 있을지 봐야겠다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고는, 살려 달라는 애처로운 울부짖음 따위는 깡그리 무시한 채, 스퀴어스 씨가 그 아이한테 달려들어 흠씬 매질을 했어. 사실 자기 팔이 지칠 때까지 멈추지 않았지.\n\n\"자.\" 완전히 끝내고 나서 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"맘껏 문질러 봐라, 그게 그리 쉽게 지워지진 않을 테니. 오! 그 소리 안 그칠 거냐, 응? 끌어내, 스마이크.\"\n\n그 머슴 같은 아이는 오랜 경험으로 잘 알고 있었어. 시키는 걸 망설였다간 큰일 난다는 걸 말이야. 그래서 그 가엾은 아이를 옆문으로 둘러메다시피 끌어냈지. 스퀴어스 씨는 다시 제 의자에 걸터앉았고, 옆에 또 다른 의자를 차지한 스퀴어스 부인이 곁을 지켰어.\n\n\"자, 어디 보자.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"코비 앞으로 온 편지로군. 일어서, 코비.\"\n\n또 다른 아이가 일어섰어. 그리고 스퀴어스가 그 편지 내용을 머릿속으로 간추리는 동안, 편지를 뚫어져라 쳐다봤지.\n\n\"오!\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"코비 할머니가 돌아가셨고, 존 삼촌은 술독에 빠졌단다. 누나가 전한 소식은 이게 다인데, 거기다 일 실링 육 펜스를 보냈군. 깨진 유리창 한 장 값으로 딱 맞겠어. 여보, 스퀴어스 부인, 이 돈 좀 받아 둘래요?\"\n\n이 갸륵한 부인은 더없이 사무적인 태도로 그 일 실링 육 펜스를 주머니에 챙겼어. 스퀴어스는 더없이 태연하게 다음 아이로 넘어갔지.\n\n\"그레이마시.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"다음은 이 녀석이군. 일어서, 그레이마시.\"\n\n또 다른 아이가 일어섰어. 교장은 아까처럼 편지를 훑었지.\n\n\"그레이마시 외숙모는.\" 스퀴어스가 내용을 다 파악하고 나서 말했어. \"이 녀석이 아주 잘 있고 행복하다는 소식에 무척 기뻐하고, 스퀴어스 부인께 정중한 안부를 전하며, 부인이 분명 천사일 거라고 여긴단다. 또 스퀴어스 씨는 이 세상에 두기엔 아까운 분이라고 생각하지만, 부디 오래오래 살아남아 이 사업을 계속해 주기를 바란다는구나. 부탁한 양말 두 켤레를 보내려 했지만 돈이 부족해서, 대신 전도 책자를 한 권 보내고, 그레이마시가 하느님의 섭리를 믿기를 바란단다. 무엇보다도, 그레이마시가 매사에 스퀴어스 씨 내외를 기쁘게 해 드리려고 애쓰고, 두 분을 자기 유일한 친구로 여기기를 바란다는구나. 또 스퀴어스 도련님을 사랑하고, 한 침대에서 다섯이 자는 걸 마다하지 않기를 바란다고. 기독교인이라면 그래선 안 되니까 말이지. 아!\" 스퀴어스가 편지를 접으며 말했어. \"참 흐뭇한 편지로구나. 정말이지 가슴 뭉클해.\"\n\n한편으로는 가슴 뭉클한 게 맞긴 했어. 사실 그레이마시의 외숙모는, 좀 더 가까운 친구들 사이에서는 다름 아닌 그 애의 친어머니라고 짐작되고 있었거든. 그렇지만 스퀴어스는 그 대목은 입에 올리지 않은 채 — 아이들 앞에서 했다간 부도덕하게 들렸을 테니까 — \"몹스\"를 불러 일을 계속 이어 갔어. 그러자 또 다른 아이가 일어섰고, 그레이마시는 다시 자리에 앉았지.\n\n\"몹스 새어머니는.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"이 녀석이 비계를 안 먹는다는 소릴 듣고 자리에 드러누웠고, 그 뒤로 줄곧 몸져누워 있단다. 빠른 우편으로 알고 싶다는구나. 먹을 걸 가지고 투정을 부리면 도대체 어디로 가게 될 것 같은지 말이야. 그리고 착하신 선생님이 축복까지 빌어 주신 소 간 죽을, 무슨 심보로 코를 쳐들고 거들떠보지도 않았는지도. 이 일은 런던 신문을 통해 알게 된 거란다 — 스퀴어스 씨가 일러바친 건 아니야. 그분은 너무 친절하고 너무 착해서 누구를 누구한테 등 돌리게 할 사람이 아니거든 — 어쨌든 이 일로 어머니가 어찌나 속이 상했는지, 몹스는 상상도 못 할 거야. 어머니는 이 녀석이 불만을 품은 걸 알고 마음 아파한단다. 그건 죄스럽고 끔찍한 일이니까. 그래서 스퀴어스 씨가 회초리로 두들겨서 더 행복한 마음가짐으로 만들어 주기를 바란다는구나. 그런 뜻에서, 일주일 용돈 반 페니도 끊어 버렸고, 일부러 이 녀석한테 주려고 샀던 코르크 따개 달린 양날 칼은 선교사들한테 줘 버렸단다.\"\n\n\"부루퉁한 마음가짐이란.\" 스퀴어스가 무시무시한 침묵 끝에 말했어. 그동안 그는 오른손 바닥에 다시 침을 발랐지. \"안 될 일이지. 명랑함과 만족스러움을 잃지 말아야 해. 몹스, 이리 와!\"\n\n몹스는 책상 쪽으로 천천히 다가갔어. 곧 그럴 만한 충분한 이유가 생길 걸 예상하며 미리 눈을 비비면서 말이야. 그리고 얼마 안 가, 한 아이가 가질 법한 충분한 이유를 안고 옆문으로 물러났지.\n\n그러고 나서 스퀴어스 씨는 잡다한 편지 꾸러미를 열기 시작했어. 어떤 편지엔 돈이 들어 있어서 스퀴어스 부인이 \"챙겨 뒀고\", 또 어떤 편지엔 모자 같은 자잘한 옷가지 얘기가 있었지. 그런데 그것들은 죄다 부인 입으로 너무 크거나 너무 작다고, 어린 스퀴어스밖에 맞을 사람이 없다고 했어. 사실 그 애는 더없이 잘 맞는 팔다리를 가진 모양이었지. 학교에 들어오는 건 뭐든 그 애한테 딱 맞아떨어졌으니까. 특히 그 애 머리는 유난히 잘 늘어났던 게 틀림없어. 온갖 크기의 모자란 모자가 죄다 그 애한테 똑같이 맞았거든.\n\n이 일이 끝나자 대충대충 몇 가지 수업이 진행됐고, 스퀴어스는 제 난롯가로 물러갔어. 교실에 남은 아이들 돌보는 일은 니콜라스한테 맡기고 말이야. 교실은 몹시 추웠고, 어두워진 뒤 얼마 안 돼 빵과 치즈로 끼니가 나왔어.\n\n주인 책상에서 가장 가까운 방 구석에 작은 난로가 하나 있었어. 니콜라스는 그 곁에 앉았지. 제 처지를 자각하며 어찌나 의기소침하고 비참했던지, 만약 그때 죽음이 찾아온다면 차라리 기꺼이 맞이했을 거야. 본의 아니게 지켜봐야 했던 그 잔인함, 기분 좋을 때조차 거칠고 막돼먹은 스퀴어스의 행동, 더러운 그곳, 둘레의 광경과 소리, 이 모든 게 그런 심정을 만들었지. 그런데 자기가 거기 조교로 와 있는 이상, 어쩌다 그렇게 불운한 사정들에 떠밀려 그 지경에 이르렀든 간에, 사실상 자기가 — 정직한 혐오와 분노로 속이 뒤집히게 하는 그 체계의 — 조력자이자 방조자인 셈이라는 데 생각이 미치자, 그는 스스로가 역겨웠어. 그리고 그 순간, 마치 지금 제 처지를 자각하는 것만으로도 앞으로 영영 다시는 고개를 들 수 없을 것만 같았지.\n\n하지만 지금으로선 마음을 굳혔어. 전날 밤에 세운 결심은 흔들리지 않았지. 그는 어머니와 누이한테 편지를 써서, 여행이 무사히 끝났다는 걸 알렸어. 두더보이즈 홀에 대해서는 되도록 말을 아꼈고, 그 적은 말마저 되도록 밝게 적었지. 여기 머무르면, 이런 데서나마 뭔가 좋은 일을 할 수 있을지 모른다고 바랐거든. 어쨌거나 다른 식구들이 삼촌의 호의에 너무 많이 기대고 있어서, 지금 당장 그의 노여움을 살 수는 없었지.\n\n그런데 자기 처지에서 비롯된 어떤 이기적인 걱정보다 그를 훨씬 더 괴롭히는 생각이 하나 있었어. 바로 누이 케이트가 어디로 가게 될까 하는 거였지. 삼촌은 자기를 속였잖아. 그러니 누이도 어디 비참한 데로 보내 버리지 않을까? 거기선 누이의 젊음과 아름다움이 추함과 노쇠함보다 훨씬 더 큰 저주가 될지도 모르는데. 손발이 묶인 채 갇힌 사람한테 이건 끔찍한 생각이었어 — 하지만 아니야, 그는 생각했어. 어머니가 곁에 계시잖아. 게다가 그 초상화가도 있고 — 좀 어수룩하긴 해도, 그래도 세상 속에 살면서 세상 돌아가는 걸 아는 사람이지. 그는 랄프 니클비가 자기 개인한테 반감을 품었다고 믿고 싶었어. 이쯤 되니 그 반감을 똑같이 되돌려 줄 만한 이유가 꽤 충분했던 터라, 그런 결론에 이르는 건 그리 어렵지 않았지. 그리고 그 감정이 자기랑 삼촌 둘 사이에서만 머문다고 스스로를 설득하려 애썼어.\n\n이런 생각에 빠져 있다가, 그는 문득 위를 올려다보는 스마이크의 얼굴과 마주쳤어. 스마이크는 난로 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 앉아, 화덕에서 흩어진 잿불 몇 개를 집어 불에 얹고 있었지. 그러다 잠시 멈추고 니콜라스를 몰래 훔쳐보던 참이었는데, 자기가 들킨 걸 알자 마치 매라도 날아올까 봐 움찔하며 움츠러들었어.\n\n\"날 무서워할 것 없어.\" 니콜라스가 다정하게 말했어. \"춥니?\"\n\n\"아, 아, 아뇨.\"\n\n\"떨고 있잖아.\"\n\"안 추워요.\" 스마이크가 얼른 대답했어. \"익숙해서요.\"\n\n그 태도에는 행여 비위를 거스를까 두려워하는 기색이 너무나 또렷했어. 게다가 어찌나 겁 많고 기죽은 아이인지, 니콜라스는 저도 모르게 외치고 말았지. \"가엾은 녀석!\"\n\n만약 그 머슴 같은 아이를 때렸다면, 그 애는 한마디도 없이 슬그머니 사라졌을 거야. 그런데 지금은 그만 울음을 터뜨리고 말았어.\n\n\"아, 어떡해, 아, 어떡해!\" 그 애가 갈라지고 거칠어진 손으로 얼굴을 가리며 흐느꼈어. \"가슴이 터질 것 같아요. 정말, 정말로요.\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 니콜라스가 그 애 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했어. \"사내답게 굴어. 나이로 치면 거의 어른이잖아, 가엾기도 하지.\"\n\n\"나이로 치면!\" 스마이크가 외쳤어. \"아, 어떡해, 어떡해, 그게 얼마나 많은데요! 제가 어린아이였을 때부터, 지금 여기 있는 어느 애들보다도 어렸을 때부터, 그게 얼마나 많은지요! 다들 어디로 가 버린 거예요!\"\n\n\"누구 얘기를 하는 거야?\" 니콜라스가 물었어. 이 가엾고 반쯤 정신 나간 아이를 정신 차리게 해 주고 싶었거든. \"말해 봐.\"\n\n\"제 친구들요.\" 그 애가 대답했어. \"저 자신도요 — 제 — 아! 제가 겪은 고통이 얼마나 컸는지!\"\n\n\"언제나 희망은 있어.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰랐거든.\n\n\"아뇨.\" 그 애가 받았어. \"아뇨, 저한텐 없어요. 여기서 죽은 그 애 기억하세요?\"\n\n\"난 그때 여기 없었잖아.\" 니콜라스가 부드럽게 말했어. \"그런데 그 애가 어쨌는데?\"\n\n\"그게요.\" 그 애가 묻는 사람 곁으로 더 바싹 다가서며 대답했어. \"밤에 제가 그 애랑 같이 있었거든요. 그러다 사방이 다 조용해지자, 그 애는 더 이상 옆에 와서 앉아 주길 바라던 친구들을 부르며 울지 않고, 침대 둘레로 집에서 온 얼굴들이 보이기 시작했어요. 그 애가 그러는데, 그 얼굴들이 자기한테 웃어 주고 말도 걸어 줬대요. 그리고 결국 그 애는 고개를 들어 그 얼굴들한테 입 맞추면서 죽었어요. 들었어요?\"\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 니콜라스가 받았어.\n\n\"제가 죽을 땐 어떤 얼굴들이 저한테 웃어 줄까요!\" 그 애가 떨면서 외쳤어. \"그 긴긴 밤에 누가 저한테 말을 걸어 줄까요! 그 얼굴들은 집에서 올 수가 없어요. 온다 해도 절 무섭게 만들 거예요. 전 그게 뭔지도 모르고, 그 얼굴들도 못 알아볼 테니까요. 고통과 두려움, 저한텐 살아서도 죽어서도 고통과 두려움뿐이에요. 희망은 없어요, 희망은 없다고요!\"\n\n잠자리에 들라는 종이 울렸어. 그러자 그 아이는 그 소리에 다시 늘 그렇듯 멍하니 풀 죽은 상태로 가라앉아, 남의 눈에 띄지 않으려는 듯 슬그머니 사라졌어. 무거운 마음으로 니콜라스도 얼마 안 가 — 아니, 잠자리에 들었다고는 못 하겠어. 거기엔 편히 쉴 데란 없었으니까 — 더럽고 북적이는 침실로 따라 들어갔지.\n\n# CHAPTER 9\n\n스퀴어스 선생이 밤이 되어 교실을 나선 뒤에는, 앞에서 말했듯이 자기 집 난롯가로 갔다. 그런데 그 난롯가는 니콜라스가 도착한 첫날 밤에 저녁을 먹었던 그 방이 아니라, 건물 뒤쪽의 더 작은 방이었어. 거기서 그의 부인과 사랑스러운 아들, 그리고 재주 많은 딸이 서로의 정겨운 시간을 한껏 누리고 있었지. 스퀴어스 부인은 양말 깁기라는 안주인다운 일에 매달려 있었고, 어린 아가씨와 도련님은 식탁 너머로 주먹다짐을 벌이며 어린애다운 다툼을 정리하는 중이었어. 그런데 존경하는 아버지가 다가오자, 그 다툼은 식탁 밑에서 소리 없이 발길질을 주고받는 걸로 잦아들었지.\n\n그리고 여기서 독자한테 미리 알려 두는 게 좋겠어. 패니 스퀴어스 양은 스물세 살이었지. 인생의 그 시기에 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 어떤 우아함이나 사랑스러움이 있다면, 스퀴어스 양도 그걸 갖추고 있었다고 봐도 될 거야. 그 여자가 만국 공통의 법칙에서 혼자만 예외였을 거라고 볼 이유는 없으니까. 그 여자는 어머니처럼 키가 크지 않고 아버지처럼 작았어. 어머니한테서는 거친 목소리를 물려받았고, 아버지한테서는 오른쪽 눈의 독특한 인상을 물려받았지. 그 눈은 아예 없는 것이나 다름없어 보였어.\n\n스퀴어스 양은 이웃 친구네 집에서 며칠 지내다가 이제 막 친정 지붕 아래로 돌아온 참이었어. 그런 사정 때문에, 스퀴어스 선생이 직접 니콜라스를 화제에 올리기 전까지 그 여자는 니콜라스에 대해 아무것도 듣지 못했던 거야.\n\n\"자, 얘야,\" 스퀴어스가 의자를 끌어당기며 말했어. \"그 사람을 이제 어떻게 생각하느냐?\"\n\n\"누구를 어떻게 생각하느냐고요?\" 스퀴어스 부인이 물었어. 부인은 (스스로 자주 말하듯이) 하늘에 감사하게도 문법 따위는 모르는 사람이었거든.\n\n\"그 젊은이 말이오, 새로 온 선생, 내가 누굴 말하겠소?\"\n\n\"아! 그 너클보이.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 짜증스럽게 말했어. \"난 그 작자가 싫어요.\"\n\n\"왜 싫은데, 여보?\" 스퀴어스가 물었어.\n\n\"그게 당신이랑 무슨 상관이에요?\" 스퀴어스 부인이 쏘아붙였어. \"내가 싫으면 그걸로 됐지, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"그 사람한테야 그걸로 충분하지, 여보, 그가 그걸 안다면 차고 넘칠 만도 하고 말이야.\" 스퀴어스가 누그러진 어조로 대답했어. \"난 그냥 궁금해서 물어보는 거요, 여보.\"\n\n\"그럼 정 알고 싶으면,\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말을 받았어. \"내 말해 주죠. 그 작자가 잘난 척하고, 거만하고, 거들먹거리고, 코를 치켜든 공작새 같은 놈이라서 그래요.\"\n\n스퀴어스 부인은 흥분하면 험한 말을 쓰는 버릇이 있었어. 게다가 여러 가지 별명을 한꺼번에 갖다 붙이곤 했는데, 그중에는 공작새 같은 비유적인 표현도 있었지. 그리고 니콜라스의 코에 대한 말도, 말 그대로 받아들이라는 게 아니라 듣는 사람 마음대로 폭넓게 해석하라는 뜻이었어.\n\n또 그 표현들은 서로 들어맞으라고 한 게 아니라, 그저 그 말을 받는 사람을 가리키려는 거였지. 이번 경우만 봐도 알 수 있어. 코를 치켜든 공작새라니, 조류학에서는 듣도 보도 못한 신기한 거였으니까.\n\n\"흠!\" 스퀴어스가 이 격한 말을 가볍게 나무라듯 말했어. \"그 사람은 싸잖소, 여보. 그 젊은이는 아주 싸단 말이오.\"\n\n\"하나도 안 싸요.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 받아쳤어.\n\n\"한 해에 오 파운드인데.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어.\n\n\"그게 뭐 어때서요? 필요 없는 사람이면 그것도 비싼 거잖아요, 안 그래요?\" 부인이 대꾸했어.\n\n\"하지만 우리한텐 필요하잖소.\" 스퀴어스가 우겼어.\n\n\"죽은 사람이나 마찬가지로 필요 없어 보이는데요.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말했어. \"내 말 좀 들어 봐요. 광고지에다 '왝퍼드 스퀴어스 선생과 유능한 조교들이 가르칩니다' 하고 적어 놓으면 되잖아요, 조교가 한 명도 없어도 말이에요, 안 그래요? 다른 교장들도 다 날마다 그렇게 하잖아요? 정말 당신한테는 두 손 두 발 다 들었다니까요.\"\n\n\"두 손 두 발 다 들었다고!\" 스퀴어스가 매섭게 말했어. \"자, 그럼 내 말 좀 들어 봐요, 스퀴어스 부인. 선생을 두는 이 일만큼은, 미안하지만 내 방식대로 하겠소. 서인도제도의 노예 감독관도 자기 밑에 사람을 하나 둘 수 있단 말이오. 흑인들이 도망치거나 반란을 일으키지 못하게 지키라고 말이지. 나도 내 밑에 사람을 하나 둬서 우리 흑인들한테 똑같이 시킬 거요. 어린 왝퍼드가 학교를 맡을 수 있을 때까지 말이오.\"\n\n\"제가 어른이 되면 학교를 맡는 거예요, 아버지?\" 어린 왝퍼드가 너무 기쁜 나머지, 누이한테 막 날리려던 짓궂은 발길질을 멈추고 물었어.\n\n\"그렇단다, 아들아.\" 스퀴어스 선생이 감격에 찬 목소리로 대답했어.\n\n\"우와, 그럼 제가 애들을 흠씬 패 줄 거예요!\" 그 사랑스러운 아이가 아버지의 회초리를 움켜쥐며 외쳤어. \"오, 아버지, 제가 그놈들을 또 찍소리 못 하게 만들 거라고요!\"\n\n스퀴어스 선생의 인생에서 그건 자랑스러운 순간이었어. 어린 자식의 마음속에서 그렇게 열정이 터져 나오는 걸 보고, 거기서 아이의 장래가 훤히 빛날 조짐을 읽었거든. 그는 아이 손에 일 페니를 꼭 쥐여 주었어. 그러고는 (본받을 만한 부인도 그랬듯이) 흐뭇하게 껄껄 웃으며 감정을 쏟아 냈지. 부모 둘의 공통된 마음을 건드린 그 어린애다운 호소 덕분에, 대화에는 다시 화기가, 자리에는 다시 화목이 돌아왔어.\n\n\"그 작자는 거들먹거리는 더러운 원숭이예요, 내 보기엔 그래요.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 다시 니콜라스 이야기로 돌아가며 말했어.\n\n\"설령 그렇다 쳐도,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"우리 교실에 처박혀 거들먹거리는 게 다른 데서 그러는 것보다야 낫지 않소? 더구나 본인이 그걸 싫어하니까 말이오.\"\n\n\"하긴,\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말했어. \"그건 일리가 있네요. 그 작자 콧대가 꺾였으면 좋겠어요. 안 꺾이면 그건 내 탓이 아닐 거예요.\"\n\n자, 그런데 요크셔의 학교에 콧대 높은 조교가 있다는 건 정말이지 듣도 보도 못한, 설명할 길 없는 일이었어. 조교라는 것 자체가 신기한 일인데, 콧대 높은 조교라니, 제아무리 엉뚱한 상상으로도 그런 존재는 꿈조차 못 꿀 정도였지. 그래서 평소에 학교 일에는 좀처럼 신경 쓰지 않던 스퀴어스 양도, 그렇게 거드름을 피운다는 그 너클보이가 대체 누구냐고 호기심에 차서 물었어.\n\n\"니클비란다.\" 스퀴어스가 자기 머릿속에만 있는 어떤 별난 방식으로 이름을 또박또박 말했어. \"네 어미는 늘 물건이고 사람이고 이름을 엉터리로 부른단 말이야.\"\n\n\"그건 아무래도 좋아요.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말했어. \"난 멀쩡한 눈으로 그것들을 보니까, 그걸로 충분하다고요. 오늘 오후에 당신이 어린 볼더를 두들겨 팰 때 그 작자를 지켜봤어요. 그동안 내내 천둥처럼 시커먼 얼굴을 하고 있더라고요. 한번은 벌떡 일어서기까지 했어요, 당신한테 달려들 작정이라도 한 것처럼 말이에요. 그 작자는 내가 못 본 줄 알았겠지만, 난 다 봤다고요.\"\n\n\"그건 됐고요, 아버지.\" 집안의 가장이 막 대꾸하려는데 스퀴어스 양이 끼어들었어. \"그 사람이 누구예요?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 네 아비가 무슨 말도 안 되는 생각을 머릿속에 담아 두고 있는데, 그 작자가 며칠 전에 죽은 어떤 가난한 신사의 아들이라는 거야.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"신사의 아들이라고요!\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇지만 난 그 말을 한마디도 안 믿어. 그 작자가 정말로 신사의 아들이라면, 그건 사생애지, 그게 내 생각이야.\"\n\n스퀴어스 부인은 '사생아'라고 말하려던 거였어. 하지만 그런 실수를 할 때마다 자주 말했듯이, 백 년 뒤에는 어차피 다 똑같다는 거였지. 사실 부인은 이 철학적 격언을 가지고, 아이들이 유난히 심한 학대에 시달릴 때면 늘 위로해 주는 버릇이 있었어.\n\n\"그런 게 전혀 아니오.\" 스퀴어스가 위의 말에 대답했어. \"왜냐하면 그 애 아비는 그 애가 태어나기 한참 전에 그 애 어미랑 결혼했고, 그 어미는 지금도 살아 있으니까. 설령 사생아라 해도 우리가 알 바 아니지. 우린 그 사람을 여기 둠으로써 아주 좋은 친구를 얻는 셈이니까 말이야. 그리고 애들을 돌보는 것 말고도 뭔가 더 가르치고 싶다면, 나야 정말이지 반대할 게 없지.\"\n\n\"다시 말하지만, 난 그 작자가 독약보다 더 싫어요.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 사납게 말했어.\n\n\"당신이 그 사람을 싫어한다면, 여보,\" 스퀴어스가 받았어. \"싫은 티를 당신만큼 잘 내는 사람을 난 본 적이 없소. 그러니 그 사람한테야 굳이 애써 숨길 필요도 없겠지.\"\n\n\"숨길 생각 없어요, 정말이지.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 끼어들었어.\n\n\"그게 옳지.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"그리고 내 생각엔 그 사람한테 콧대 같은 게 좀 있는 것 같은데, 영국 천지에 당신만큼 누군가의 기를 빨리 꺾을 수 있는 여자는 없을 거요, 여보.\"\n\n스퀴어스 부인은 이 듣기 좋은 칭찬을 받고 한껏 낄낄 웃으며, 자기도 살면서 드센 기 한둘쯤은 길들여 봤기를 바란다고 말했어. 부인의 성격을 두고 공평하게 말하자면, 존경스러운 남편과 손발을 맞춰서 정말이지 수도 없이 많은 아이의 기를 꺾어 놓았지.\n\n패니 스퀴어스 양은 이 이야기를, 또 같은 화제로 오간 더 많은 이야기를 잘 새겨 두었어. 그리고 밤이 되어 잠자리에 들 때, 굶주린 하녀를 붙잡고 니콜라스의 겉모습과 태도에 대해 시시콜콜 캐물었지. 하녀는 그 물음에 열을 올리며 대답했어. 그의 아름다운 검은 눈, 다정한 미소, 그리고 곧게 뻗은 다리에 대해 칭찬을 한가득 늘어놓았지. 특히 그 다리 이야기에 유난히 힘을 줬어. 두더보이즈 홀의 다리들은 대체로 휘어 있었으니까. 그러니 스퀴어스 양은 오래지 않아 결론에 다다랐지. 새로 온 조교는 아주 비범한 사람임이 틀림없다고, 아니 그 여자가 의미심장하게 표현한 대로 '아주 흔치 않은 무언가'임이 틀림없다고 말이야. 그래서 스퀴어스 양은 바로 다음 날 니콜라스를 직접 살펴보기로 마음먹었어.\n\n이 계획을 실행에 옮기려고, 이 아가씨는 어머니가 일에 매여 있고 아버지가 자리를 비운 틈을 노렸어. 그러고는 펜을 고친다는 핑계로 우연인 척 교실에 들어갔지. 그런데 거기서 아이들을 맡고 있는 게 니콜라스밖에 없는 걸 보고는, 얼굴이 빨갛게 달아올라 어쩔 줄 몰라 했어.\n\n\"실례했어요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 더듬거렸어. \"저는 아버지가 계신 줄, 아니면 계실지도 모른다고 생각해서, 어머나, 정말 난처하네!\"\n\n\"스퀴어스 선생님은 나가셨어요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. 예기치 못한 등장이었지만, 그는 그 출현에 조금도 마음이 흔들리지 않았어.\n\n\"오래 걸리실까요, 선생님?\" 스퀴어스 양이 수줍게 머뭇거리며 물었어.\n\n\"한 시간쯤이라고 하셨어요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. 물론 예의 바르게였지만, 스퀴어스 양의 매력에 가슴이 찔린 기색은 전혀 없었지.\n\n\"이렇게 어긋나는 일은 처음이네요.\" 아가씨가 외쳤어. \"고마워요! 방해해서 정말 죄송해요. 아버지가 여기 계신 줄 알았으니 망정이지, 안 그랬으면 무슨 일이 있어도, 정말 약 오르네, 너무 이상해 보이겠어요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 다시 얼굴을 붉히며 중얼거렸어. 그러고는 손에 든 펜에서 책상에 앉은 니콜라스로, 또 다시 펜으로 눈길을 옮겼어.\n\n\"필요하신 게 그것뿐이라면,\" 니콜라스가 펜을 가리키며 말했어. 그리고 교장 딸의 꾸민 듯한 어색함에 저도 모르게 미소를 지었지. \"제가 선생님 대신 해 드릴 수 있을 것 같은데요.\"\n\n스퀴어스 양은 문 쪽을 흘끗 봤어. 생판 모르는 남자한테 더 가까이 다가가도 괜찮을지 망설이는 것 같았지. 그러다 교실을 한 바퀴 둘러봤어. 마흔 명이나 되는 아이들이 있다는 데 어느 정도 안심이 된 모양이었어. 그러고는 마침내 니콜라스 옆으로 슬그머니 다가가, 도도함과 너그러움이 더없이 매력적으로 뒤섞인 태도로 펜을 그의 손에 건넸지.\n\n\"펜촉을 단단하게 할까요, 부드럽게 할까요?\" 니콜라스가 그만 웃음을 터뜨리지 않으려고 미소를 지으며 물었어.\n\n'정말 아름다운 미소를 가졌어.' 스퀴어스 양은 생각했어.\n\n\"뭐라고 하셨죠?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"어머나, 잠깐 딴생각을 했나 봐요, 정말.\" 스퀴어스 양이 대답했어. \"아! 되도록 부드럽게 해 주세요, 부탁이에요.\" 이렇게 말하고 스퀴어스 양은 한숨을 쉬었어. 어쩌면 자기 마음이 부드러우니 펜도 거기 어울려야 한다는 걸 니콜라스한테 알려 주려는 거였는지도 몰라.\n\n니콜라스는 그 주문대로 펜을 만들었어. 그가 펜을 건네자 스퀴어스 양이 그걸 떨어뜨렸지. 그가 주우려고 몸을 숙이자 스퀴어스 양도 같이 숙였고, 둘은 머리를 부딪쳤어. 그 바람에 어린 사내아이 스물다섯 명이 깔깔 웃음을 터뜨렸지. 그게 그 학기 통틀어 처음이자 단 한 번 있는 일이었어.\n\n\"제가 정말 서툴렀네요.\" 니콜라스가 아가씨가 나갈 수 있게 문을 열어 주며 말했어.\n\n\"천만에요, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스 양이 대답했어. \"제 잘못이에요. 다 제가 어리석어서, 어, 어, 안녕히 계세요!\"\n\n\"안녕히 가세요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"다음에 만들어 드릴 땐 좀 덜 서툴렀으면 좋겠네요. 조심하세요! 지금 펜촉을 깨물고 계세요.\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어. \"너무 당황스러워서 제가 무슨, 이렇게 폐를 끼쳐서 정말 죄송해요.\"\n\n\"조금도 폐될 거 없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 교실 문을 닫으며 대답했어.\n\n\"내 평생 그런 다리는 처음 봤어!\" 스퀴어스 양이 걸어가며 말했어.\n\n사실 스퀴어스 양은 니콜라스 니클비에게 반해 버린 거였어.\n\n이 아가씨가 어쩌면 그렇게 빨리 니콜라스한테 마음을 품게 됐는지 설명하려면, 이 말부터 해 둬야겠어. 그 여자가 얼마 전에 다녀온 친구는 방앗간집 딸인데, 겨우 열여덟 살이었어. 그 애는 가장 가까운 장터 마을에 사는 작은 곡물 상인의 아들과 약혼한 사이였지. 스퀴어스 양과 방앗간집 딸은 단짝이었는데, 한 이 년쯤 전에 둘이서 약속을 했어. 젊은 아가씨들 사이에 흔한 풍습대로, 둘 중 누구든 먼저 약혼하게 되면 다른 누구한테 알리기 전에 곧장 그 엄청난 비밀을 상대에게 털어놓고, 지체 없이 신부 들러리로 청하기로 한 거였지. 그 약속대로 방앗간집 딸은 약혼이 성사되자마자 부랴부랴 달려왔어. 부엌의 네덜란드 시계로 열 시 이십오 분에 곡물 상인의 아들이 손과 마음을 바치겠다고 청혼했고, 밤 열한 시에 그 딸이 스퀴어스 양의 침실로 뛰어들어 그 기쁜 소식을 전한 거였지. 그런데 스퀴어스 양은 다섯 살이 더 많은 데다 이미 십 대를 벗어난 처지였어 (이것도 큰 문제였지). 그래서 그 뒤로 그 답례를 하고 싶어, 그러니까 비슷한 비밀로 친구를 놀라게 해 주고 싶어 보통 이상으로 안달이 났지. 하지만 자기 마음에 드는 상대를 찾기가 어려웠는지, 아니면 남의 마음에 들기가 더 어려웠는지, 그럴 기회를 도무지 잡지 못했어. 털어놓을 비밀이 아예 없었으니까. 그런데 앞에서 말한 대로 니콜라스와 그 짧은 만남을 가지자마자, 스퀴어스 양은 모자를 쓰고 부랴부랴 친구네 집으로 달려갔어. 그리고 비밀을 지키겠다는 묵은 맹세를 여러 번 엄숙히 새로 다진 뒤, 털어놓았지. 자기가, 정확히 약혼한 건 아니지만 곧 약혼하게 될 거라고, 그것도 신사의 아들과 (곡물 상인 따위가 아니라 가문 높은 신사의 아들이지) 말이야. 그 사람은 아주 신비롭고 놀라운 사정으로 두더보이즈 홀에 선생으로 내려왔는데, 사실 스퀴어스 양이 한두 번 넌지시 비친 바로는, 그 사람이 자기의 수많은 매력에 대한 소문에 이끌려 자기를 찾아와 구애하고 사로잡으려 온 거라고 믿을 만한 충분한 근거가 있다는 거였어.\n\n\"정말 놀라운 일 아니니?\" 스퀴어스 양이 그 형용사에 잔뜩 힘을 주며 말했어.\n\n\"정말 놀랍다.\" 친구가 대답했어. \"그런데 그 사람이 너한테 뭐라고 했는데?\"\n\n\"무슨 말을 했는지는 묻지 마, 얘.\" 스퀴어스 양이 말을 받았어. \"그 눈빛이랑 미소를 너도 봤어야 하는데! 내 평생 그렇게 마음이 흔들린 적은 처음이야.\"\n\n\"이런 식으로 쳐다봤니?\" 방앗간집 딸이 곡물 상인이 즐겨 짓던 곁눈질을 할 수 있는 한 흉내 내며 물었어.\n\n\"그거랑 아주 비슷한데, 좀 더 점잖았어.\" 스퀴어스 양이 대답했어.\n\n\"아!\" 친구가 말했어. \"그럼 뭔가 마음이 있는 거야, 틀림없어.\"\n\n스퀴어스 양은 이 일을 두고 살짝 미심쩍은 마음이 있던 터라, 믿을 만한 사람한테 확인을 받으니 영 싫지 않았어. 그리고 이야기를 더 나누고 서로 견주어 보면서, 니콜라스의 행동과 곡물 상인의 행동 사이에 닮은 점이 한둘이 아니라는 걸 알게 됐지. 그러자 어찌나 속을 다 터놓게 됐던지, 니콜라스가 하지도 않은 말을 잔뜩 친구한테 들려줬어. 그게 죄다 어찌나 듣기 좋은 말들이던지, 결정적인 증거나 다름없었지. 그러고는 자기의 장래 남편을 부모가 한사코 반대한다는, 그 무시무시한 고초에 대해 늘어놓았어. 이 가엾은 처지를 한참이나 곱씹었지. 친구의 부모는 딸이 결혼하는 데 아주 흔쾌히 찬성하는 터라, 그 연애 전체가 더없이 밋밋하고 평범한 일이었거든.\n\n\"그 사람을 정말 보고 싶다!\" 친구가 외쳤어.\n\n\"보게 될 거야, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 대답했어. \"내가 널 안 보여 주면 세상에서 제일 배은망덕한 사람일 거야. 어머니가 이틀쯤 아이들 데리러 떠나실 것 같아. 그때 너랑 존을 차 마시러 부를게. 그 사람도 와서 너희를 만나게 할 거야.\"\n\n이건 근사한 생각이었어. 그래서 둘이서 그 일을 실컷 의논한 뒤 헤어졌지.\n\n마침 일이 그렇게 됐어. 스퀴어스 부인이 새 아이 셋을 데려오고, 옛날 아이 둘의 친척한테 자잘한 외상값 잔금을 받아 내러 꽤 먼 길을 떠나는 게 바로 그날 오후에 정해졌거든. 그것도 모레 날짜로 말이야. 그리고 모레가 되자, 스퀴어스 부인은 그레타 다리에서 마차가 말을 바꾸려고 멈춘 사이에 마차 바깥자리에 올라탔어. 병에 든 무언가와 샌드위치가 든 작은 보따리를 챙기고, 거기다 밤에 입을 커다란 흰색 외투까지 들고서, 그 짐들을 갖고 길을 떠났지.\n\n이런 기회가 생길 때마다 스퀴어스는 매일 저녁 급한 일이 있다는 핑계로 장터 마을까지 마차를 몰고 가, 단골 술집에서 열 시나 열한 시까지 죽치는 버릇이 있었어. 그러니 이번 모임은 그에게 거슬리기는커녕 오히려 딸과 타협하는 구실이 됐지. 그래서 그는 선뜻 흔쾌히 동의해 주고, 니콜라스한테도 기꺼이 알렸어. 그날 저녁 다섯 시에 응접실에서 차를 마시러 와야 한다고 말이야.\n\n물론 시간이 다가오자 스퀴어스 양은 안절부절못하며 잔뜩 들떴어. 그리고 물론 한껏 멋을 부려 차려입었지. 머리는, 불그스름한 기가 꽤 도는 짧은 머리였는데, 다섯 줄로 또렷이 곱슬곱슬 말아 정수리 꼭대기까지 올렸어. 그리고 그 미심쩍은 눈을 솜씨 좋게 가리도록 매만졌지. 등 뒤로 늘어뜨린 파란 띠며, 수놓은 앞치마며, 긴 장갑이며, 한쪽 어깨 위와 다른 쪽 어깨 아래로 두른 초록 망사 스카프는 말할 것도 없었어. 그밖에도 니콜라스의 심장을 향해 쏠 화살이 되어 줄 온갖 장치들이 있었지. 그 여자가 이 차림을 완전히 흡족할 만큼 막 끝냈을 때, 친구가 도착했어. 누르스름한 갈색의, 납작하고 세모난 꾸러미를 들고서 말이야. 그 안에는 위층에서 달 자잘한 장식들이 들어 있었지. 친구는 쉴 새 없이 떠들며 그것들을 달았어. 스퀴어스 양이 친구 머리를 '해 주자', 친구도 스퀴어스 양 머리를 '해 줬어'. 목덜미로 곱슬머리를 늘어뜨리는 식으로 눈에 띄게 멋도 좀 더했지. 그러고는 둘 다 완전히 만족스럽게 단장을 마치자, 긴 장갑을 끼고 한껏 차려입은 채 손님 맞을 채비를 다 갖추고 아래층으로 내려갔어.\n\n\"존은 어딨니, 틸다?\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어.\n\n\"잠깐 씻으러 집에 갔어.\" 친구가 대답했어. \"차가 우려질 때쯤이면 올 거야.\"\n\n\"가슴이 너무 두근거려.\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어.\n\n\"아! 그게 어떤 건지 나도 알지.\" 친구가 대답했어.\n\n\"난 이런 거에 익숙하질 않잖아, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 띠의 왼쪽에 손을 갖다 대며 말했어.\n\n\"곧 괜찮아질 거야, 얘.\" 친구가 받았어. 둘이 이렇게 이야기하는 동안, 굶주린 하녀가 찻그릇을 들여왔어. 그리고 곧 누군가 방문을 똑똑 두드렸지.\n\n\"그 사람이야!\" 스퀴어스 양이 외쳤어. \"오, 틸다!\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 틸다가 말했어. \"에헴! 어서, 들어오시라고 해.\"\n\n\"들어오세요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 가냘프게 외쳤어. 그러자 니콜라스가 걸어 들어왔지.\n\n\"안녕하세요.\" 그 젊은 신사가 자기가 마음을 사로잡았다는 걸 까맣게 모른 채 말했어. \"스퀴어스 선생님한테 듣기로는······.\"\n\n\"아 네, 다 됐어요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 끼어들었어. \"아버지는 저희랑 차를 안 드시는데, 괜찮으시겠죠.\" (이건 짐짓 새침하게 한 말이었어.)\n\n니콜라스는 이 말에 눈을 동그랗게 떴지만, 아주 무심하게 그냥 넘겼어. 그땐 딱히 무슨 일에도 마음 쓸 여유가 없었거든. 그러고는 방앗간집 딸한테 소개받는 의례를 어찌나 우아하게 치렀던지, 그 아가씨가 그만 넋을 잃고 감탄했지.\n\n\"신사 한 분만 더 기다리면 돼요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 찻주전자 뚜껑을 열고 차가 어떻게 우러나는지 들여다보며 말했어.\n\n니콜라스한테는 한 사람을 기다리든 스무 명을 기다리든 매한가지였어. 그래서 그 소식을 아무 관심 없이 받아들였지. 게다가 기분도 가라앉아 있었고, 굳이 상냥하게 굴 까닭도 딱히 없어서, 창밖을 내다보며 저도 모르게 한숨을 쉬었어.\n\n운이 그렇게 따랐는지, 스퀴어스 양의 친구는 장난기가 많은 성격이었어. 니콜라스가 한숨 쉬는 걸 듣고는, 두 연인이 기운 없어 보인다고 놀려 줄 생각이 머릿속에 떠올랐지.\n\n\"근데 제가 여기 있어서 그런 거면,\" 아가씨가 말했어. \"저는 조금도 신경 쓰지 마세요. 저도 똑같은 처지니까요. 두 분만 있을 때처럼 그냥 하셔도 돼요.\"\n\n\"틸다,\" 스퀴어스 양이 곱슬머리 맨 윗줄까지 빨개지며 말했어. \"너 때문에 창피해 죽겠어.\" 그러고는 두 친구가 갖가지로 키득키득 웃음을 터뜨렸어. 그리고 이따금 손수건 너머로 니콜라스를 흘끔흘끔 봤지. 니콜라스는 그저 어리둥절하던 상태에서 차츰 도무지 참을 수 없는 웃음으로 빠져들었어. 그건 한편으로는 자기가 스퀴어스 양과 사랑에 빠졌다는 그 황당한 생각 때문이었고, 또 한편으로는 두 아가씨의 어처구니없는 차림새와 행동 때문이었지. 이 두 가지 우스운 까닭이 한데 겹치니, 어찌나 지독히 우스꽝스럽게 느껴지던지, 그는 그 비참한 처지에도 완전히 지칠 때까지 웃어 댔어.\n\n'그래,' 니콜라스는 생각했어. '내가 여기 와 있고, 어쩐 일인지 상냥하게 굴기를 기대받는 모양이니, 멍청이처럼 굴어 봤자 소용없지. 차라리 이 자리에 어울려 주는 게 낫겠어.'\n\n이런 말을 하기 부끄럽지만, 그의 젊은 혈기와 발랄함이 잠시나마 슬픈 생각을 이겨 버렸어. 그래서 그는 이 결심을 하자마자 스퀴어스 양과 친구에게 한껏 멋들어지게 인사를 건넸지. 그리고 찻상으로 의자를 끌어당겨, 조교라는 게 처음 생겨난 이래로 고용주 집에서 그 어떤 조교도 못 해 봤을 만큼 제집처럼 편하게 굴기 시작했어.\n\n니클비 씨의 이 달라진 행동에 두 아가씨가 한껏 즐거워하고 있을 때, 기다리던 그 청년이 도착했어. 머리는 방금 씻어서 흠뻑 젖어 있었고, 깨끗한 셔츠를 입었는데 그 옷깃은 어느 거인 조상한테나 어울렸을 법했지. 그것이 비슷한 크기의 흰 조끼와 함께 그의 몸을 장식하는 가장 큰 치장이었어.\n\n\"왔구나, 존.\" 마틸다 프라이스 양이 말했어. (참고로 이게 방앗간집 딸의 이름이었어.)\n\n\"왔다.\" 존이 그 옷깃으로도 가릴 수 없는 활짝 웃음을 지으며 말했어.\n\n\"실례했어요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 얼른 손님 예우를 갖추려고 끼어들었어. \"니클비 씨, 이쪽은 존 브라우디 씨예요.\"\n\n\"처음 뵙겠습니다, 선생님.\" 존이 말했어. 그는 키가 육 피트를 좀 넘었고, 얼굴이며 몸이며 적당한 균형보다는 큰 편이었지.\n\n\"잘 부탁드립니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답하며, 버터 바른 빵을 무섭게 해치웠어.\n\n브라우디 씨는 말솜씨가 뛰어난 신사가 아니었어. 그래서 두 번 더 활짝 웃었지. 그리고 이제 자리에 있는 사람들 모두에게 으레 하는 인사를 마치자, 딱히 뭘 보고 웃는 것도 아니게 그냥 씩 웃고는 음식을 가져다 먹었어.\n\n\"마누라가 집을 비웠지, 안 그러우?\" 브라우디 씨가 입에 음식을 가득 문 채 말했어.\n\n스퀴어스 양이 고개를 끄덕여 그렇다고 했어.\n\n브라우디 씨는 그게 정말 웃을 만한 일이라도 되는 양 유난히 입을 활짝 벌려 씩 웃었어. 그러고는 한층 더 힘차게 버터 바른 빵을 공략했지. 그와 니콜라스가 둘이서 접시를 비워 가는 꼴은 그야말로 구경거리였어.\n\n\"매일 밤 버터 바른 빵을 얻어먹지는 못할 거유, 양반.\" 브라우디 씨가 빈 접시 너머로 한참이나 니콜라스를 빤히 쳐다보고 나서 말했어.\n\n니콜라스는 입술을 깨물고 얼굴을 붉혔지만, 그 말을 못 들은 척했어.\n\n\"허 참,\" 브라우디 씨가 요란하게 웃으며 말했어. \"여긴 음식을 넉넉히 안 주거든. 여기 오래 붙어 있다간 가죽하고 뼈만 남을걸. 허! 허! 허!\"\n\n\"농담도 잘하시는군요, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 경멸하듯 말했어.\n\n\"아니, 난 잘 모르겠소만,\" 브라우디 씨가 대답했어. \"전에 있던 그 선생, 참 그 사람 비쩍 골았었지, 정말로.\" 전임 선생이 비쩍 골았던 기억이 브라우디 씨한테는 더없이 절묘한 기쁨을 주는 모양이었어. 그가 어찌나 웃어 대던지, 마침내 외투 소매로 눈가를 닦아야 할 지경이었지.\n\n\"브라우디 씨, 당신 지각이 충분히 예민해서 자기 말이 무례하다는 걸 알아챌 수 있을지 모르겠지만,\" 니콜라스가 잔뜩 화가 치밀어 말했어. \"만약 그렇다면, 부디·····.\"\n\n\"한마디라도 더 하면, 존,\" 프라이스 양이 자기 흠모자가 막 끼어들려는 입을 막으며 새된 소리를 질렀어. \"딱 반 마디라도 더 하면, 다시는 안 봐 주고 말도 안 할 거야.\"\n\n\"알았어, 내 색시, 난 저 사람 신경 안 써.\" 곡물 상인이 마틸다 양한테 진하게 입을 맞추며 말했어. \"떠들게 둬, 떠들게 두라고.\"\n\n이제는 스퀴어스 양이 니콜라스를 말릴 차례였어. 그 여자는 잔뜩 놀라고 끔찍해하는 기색을 보이며 그렇게 했지. 양쪽에서 말린 결과, 그와 존 브라우디는 식탁 너머로 아주 엄숙하게 악수를 나눴어. 그런데 그 의식이 어찌나 거창했던지, 스퀴어스 양은 그만 감격해서 눈물을 흘리고 말았지.\n\n\"왜 그래, 패니?\" 프라이스 양이 말했어.\n\n\"아무것도 아니야, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 흐느끼며 대답했어.\n\n\"애초에 위험할 거 하나도 없었어.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어. \"그렇죠, 니클비 씨?\"\n\n\"전혀 없었죠.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"터무니없는 일이에요.\"\n\n\"그래야죠.\" 프라이스 양이 속삭였어. \"쟤한테 다정하게 한마디 해 줘요, 그럼 금방 풀릴 거예요. 자! 존이랑 제가 저쪽 작은 부엌에 가 있다가 이따 돌아올까요?\"\n\n\"무슨 일이 있어도 안 됩니다.\" 니콜라스가 그 제안에 잔뜩 놀라 받아쳤어. \"도대체 왜 그런 짓을 한다는 거예요?\"\n\n\"원 참,\" 프라이스 양이 그를 옆으로 부르며 좀 깔보는 투로 말했어. \"교제하는 데 참 별나신 분이네요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"전 교제 같은 거 전혀 안 하는데요, 적어도 여기서는요. 도무지 무슨 영문인지 모르겠네요.\"\n\n\"네, 저도 모르겠어요.\" 프라이스 양이 받았어. \"하지만 남자들은 늘 변덕스럽고, 늘 그래 왔고, 앞으로도 늘 그럴 거예요. 그건 제가 아주 쉽게 알겠네요.\"\n\n\"변덕스럽다고요!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어. \"도대체 무슨 생각을 하시는 거예요? 설마 제가······.\"\n\n\"아, 아뇨, 전 아무 생각도 안 해요.\" 프라이스 양이 토라진 듯 받아쳤어. \"쟤 좀 봐요, 저렇게 예쁘게 차려입고 저렇게 고와 보이는데, 정말 거의 미인이잖아요. 당신 때문에 제가 다 창피하네요.\"\n\n\"아니 아가씨, 저 사람이 예쁘게 차려입든 고와 보이든 그게 저랑 무슨 상관이에요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"이봐요, 아가씨라고 부르지 마세요.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어. 그래도 살짝 미소를 지었지. 그 여자는 예뻤고, 나름의 방식으로 살짝 요염한 데도 있었거든. 게다가 니콜라스는 잘생겼고, 다른 사람의 임자라고 여겼으니, 그한테 자기가 인상을 남겼다고 생각하면 흐뭇할 만한 이유가 다 갖춰진 셈이었지. \"안 그러면 패니가 다 내 탓이라고 할 거예요. 자, 우리 카드놀이나 해요.\" 마지막 말을 큰 소리로 하며, 그 여자는 사뿐사뿐 걸어가 그 덩치 큰 요크셔 남자한테 돌아갔어.\n\n니콜라스한테는 이게 도무지 알 수 없는 일이었어. 그 순간 그의 머릿속에 또렷이 남은 인상이라고는, 스퀴어스 양은 평범하게 생긴 여자고 그 친구 프라이스 양은 예쁜 여자라는 것뿐이었지. 하지만 곰곰이 생각해 깨우칠 틈도 없었어. 그새 난롯가도 다 쓸리고 촛불 심지도 잘려서, 다들 자리에 앉아 스페큘레이션 카드놀이를 시작했거든.\n\n\"우리 넷밖에 없네, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 니콜라스를 슬쩍 훔쳐보며 말했어. \"그러니 둘씩 짝을 지어서 둘 대 둘로 하는 게 낫겠다.\"\n\n\"어떠세요, 니클비 씨?\" 프라이스 양이 물었어.\n\n\"더없이 좋습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. 그러고는 자기가 저지른 끔찍한 잘못을 까맣게 모른 채, 두더보이즈 홀의 요금표 한 장을 잘라 만든 패 가운데 자기 몫과 프라이스 양 몫을 한 무더기로 합쳐 버렸어.\n\n\"브라우디 씨,\" 스퀴어스 양이 신경질적으로 말했어. \"우리가 저쪽에 맞서 한편 먹을까요?\"\n\n요크셔 남자는 그러자고 했어. 새 조교의 뻔뻔함에 아주 어안이 벙벙해진 모양이었지. 스퀴어스 양은 친구한테 심술궂은 눈길을 휙 던지고는, 발작하듯 키득거렸어.\n\n패를 돌릴 차례가 니콜라스한테 돌아왔는데, 패가 잘 풀렸어.\n\n\"우리가 죄다 따 버릴 작정입니다.\" 그가 말했어.\n\n\"틸다는 기대도 안 한 걸 좀 땄지, 안 그러니, 얘?\" 스퀴어스 양이 짓궂게 말했어.\n\n\"고작 한 다스에 여덟이야, 얘.\" 프라이스 양이 그 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들이는 척하며 대답했어.\n\n\"너 오늘 밤 왜 이렇게 굼떠!\" 스퀴어스 양이 비아냥거렸어.\n\n\"아니거든.\" 프라이스 양이 대답했어. \"난 기분 아주 좋은데. 오히려 네가 영 시무룩해 보여서 그랬지.\"\n\n\"내가!\" 스퀴어스 양이 입술을 깨물고 질투에 부들부들 떨며 외쳤어. \"오, 아니야!\"\n\n\"그럼 다행이고.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어. \"얘, 네 머리 곱슬이 풀리고 있어.\"\n\n\"내 걱정은 말고,\" 스퀴어스 양이 깔깔거렸어. \"네 짝꿍이나 신경 쓰지 그래.\"\n\n\"일러 줘서 고마워요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"정말 그래야겠네요.\"\n\n요크셔 남자는 다른 신사의 얼굴짝에 한 방 먹일 기회가 올 때까지 손이 굳지 않게 하려는 듯, 꽉 쥔 주먹으로 자기 코를 한두 번 납작하게 눌렀어. 그리고 스퀴어스 양이 어찌나 분에 차서 고개를 홱홱 젖혔던지, 그 수많은 곱슬머리가 일으킨 바람결에 촛불이 꺼질 뻔했지.\n\n\"정말이지 이런 행운은 처음이에요.\" 요염한 프라이스 양이 한두 판 더 치르고 나서 외쳤어. \"다 당신 덕분인가 봐요, 니클비 씨. 늘 당신이 제 짝꿍이면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"저도 그랬으면 좋겠네요.\"\n\n\"하지만 카드를 늘 따면 아내 복은 없을 거예요.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어.\n\n\"당신 소원이 이루어진다면 그렇지 않겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그렇게 되면 분명 좋은 아내를 얻을 테니까요.\"\n\n이 대화가 오가는 동안 스퀴어스 양이 고개를 어떻게 홱홱 젖혔는지, 그리고 곡물 상인이 코를 어떻게 납작하게 눌렀는지! 그 꼴을 봤더라면 자그마한 연금만큼의 값어치는 했을 거야. 게다가 프라이스 양이 두 사람을 질투 나게 만들며 대놓고 즐거워하던 것도, 또 자기가 누군가를 불편하게 만들고 있다는 걸 까맣게 모르고 행복해하던 니콜라스 니클비도 다 볼만했지.\n\n\"보아하니 우리끼리만 죄다 떠드는 것 같네요.\" 니콜라스가 새 판을 돌리려고 카드를 집으며 식탁을 빙 둘러보고 너스레를 떨었어.\n\n\"워낙 잘하시니까,\" 스퀴어스 양이 깔깔거렸어. \"끼어드는 게 아깝죠, 안 그래요, 브라우디 씨? 히! 히! 히!\"\n\n\"천만에요,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"달리 이야기 나눌 사람이 없어서 우리끼리 하는 거죠.\"\n\n\"하고 싶은 말 있으면 우리가 들어 드릴게요.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어.\n\n\"고맙다, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 도도하게 받아쳤어.\n\n\"우리랑 이야기 나누기 싫으면 둘이서 이야기하든가.\" 프라이스 양이 그 소중한 친구를 놀려 댔어. \"존, 너는 왜 아무 말도 안 해?\"\n\n\"뭔 말을 하라고?\" 요크셔 남자가 되물었어.\n\n\"그래, 그렇게 말없이 시무룩하게 앉아만 있지 말고.\"\n\n\"좋아, 그럼!\" 요크셔 남자가 주먹으로 식탁을 쾅 내리치며 말했어. \"내 할 말은 이거야. 빌어먹을, 내 뼈고 몸뚱이고 다 부서져도 더는 이 꼴 못 참겠다. 자네, 나랑 같이 집에 가지. 그리고 저 까불대고 으스대는 애송이 녀석, 다음에 내 손에 걸리면 대가리 깨질 줄 알고 단단히 조심하라고 해.\"\n\n\"세상에, 이게 다 무슨 소리예요?\" 프라이스 양이 짐짓 놀란 척 외쳤어.\n\n\"가자고, 집에, 집에 가자니까.\" 요크셔 남자가 매섭게 대꾸했어. 그가 이렇게 대꾸하자, 스퀴어스 양은 와락 눈물을 쏟았어. 한편으로는 지독한 분풀이에서 나온 것이었고, 또 한편으로는 고운 손톱으로 누군가의 얼굴을 할퀴고 싶은데 그러지 못해 솟구친 눈물이었지.\n\n이 사달은 여러 가지 까닭과 작용이 얽혀 일어난 거였어. 스퀴어스 양은 마땅한 근거도 없이 약혼한 몸이라는 높은 지위에 오르려고 욕심을 부려 이 일을 빚었어. 프라이스 양은 세 가지 동기에 빠져 이 일을 빚었지. 첫째는 친구가 자격도 없이 자기랑 품위를 견주려 든 걸 혼내 주고 싶은 마음, 둘째는 똑똑한 젊은이의 찬사를 받으며 자기 허영을 채우는 즐거움, 셋째는 곡물 상인한테 그가 자기들 혼례를 미루다가는 얼마나 큰 위험을 무릅쓰는 셈인지 일깨워 주고 싶은 마음이었어. 한편 니콜라스는 삼십 분쯤의 들뜬 기분과 경솔함, 그리고 자기가 스퀴어스 양한테 조금이라도 마음이 있다는 소리를 듣고 싶지 않은 아주 진심 어린 바람 때문에 이 일을 빚었지. 그러니 쓰인 수단도, 빚어진 결과도 다 세상에서 가장 자연스러운 거였어. 젊은 아가씨들은 결혼을 손꼽아 기다리고, 결혼식장으로 가는 경주에서 서로 밀치락거리고, 자기 매력을 가장 돋보이게 뽐낼 기회라면 무엇이든 잡으려 들 테니까. 세상이 시작될 때부터 그래 왔듯이, 세상이 끝나는 그날까지 말이야.\n\n\"어머, 패니가 이제 울고 있네!\" 프라이스 양이 새삼 놀란 척 외쳤어. \"무슨 일이람?\"\n\n\"오! 너야 모르지, 당연히 모르겠지. 굳이 수고스럽게 물어볼 거 없어.\" 스퀴어스 양이, 아이들이 찡그린다고 하는 그 표정을 지으며 말했어.\n\n\"어머, 별꼴이야 정말!\" 프라이스 양이 외쳤어.\n\n\"네가 별꼴이라고 하든 말든 누가 신경이나 쓴대요, 아가씨?\" 스퀴어스 양이 또 한 번 얼굴을 찡그리며 받아쳤어.\n\n\"참 엄청나게 공손하시네요, 아가씨.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어.\n\n\"그 기술을 배우러 당신한테 갈 일은 없을 거예요, 아가씨!\" 스퀴어스 양이 받아쳤어.\n\n\"하지만 굳이 애써 지금보다 더 못나 보이려고 할 것 없어요, 아가씨.\" 프라이스 양이 받았어. \"그건 영 쓸데없는 짓이니까요.\"\n\n이에 스퀴어스 양은 얼굴이 새빨개져서는, 자기가 어떤 사람들처럼 뻔뻔한 낯짝을 가지지 않은 걸 하느님께 감사했어. 프라이스 양은 그 대꾸로, 자기가 다른 사람들 같은 시샘하는 마음을 가지지 않은 걸 다행으로 여겼지. 그러자 스퀴어스 양은 천박한 사람들과 어울리는 게 얼마나 위험한지에 대해 일반론을 한마디 했어. 프라이스 양은 거기에 전적으로 동의했지. 정말이지 옳은 말이라고, 자기도 오래전부터 그렇게 생각해 왔다고 하면서 말이야.\n\n\"틸다,\" 스퀴어스 양이 위엄을 부리며 외쳤어. \"난 네가 싫어.\"\n\n\"아! 우리 사이에 정 같은 건 진작 없었지, 정말이야.\" 프라이스 양이 모자 끈을 홱 묶으며 말했어. \"내가 가고 나면 넌 눈이 빠지게 울 거야. 너도 알잖아.\"\n\n\"네 말 따위 우습기만 해, 이 여우야.\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어.\n\n\"그렇게 말해 주니 정말 큰 칭찬인걸.\" 방앗간집 딸이 아주 깊숙이 절을 하며 대답했어. \"안녕히 주무세요, 아가씨, 단꿈 꾸시길 바라요!\"\n\n이 작별 인사를 끝으로 프라이스 양은 휙 방을 나갔어. 그 덩치 큰 요크셔 남자가 뒤따라 나갔지. 그는 나가면서 니콜라스와, 그 유난히 의미심장한 노려보는 눈빛을 주고받았어. 멜로드라마에서 칼싸움하는 자들이 또 만나자고 서로에게 알리는 그런 눈빛 말이야.\n\n그들이 나가자마자, 스퀴어스 양은 한때 친구였던 그 여자의 예언대로 펑펑 눈물을 쏟아 냈어. 그러고는 갖가지 처량한 한탄과 두서없는 말을 늘어놓았지. 니콜라스는 몇 초간 가만히 지켜보며 어찌해야 할지 좀 망설였어. 하지만 이 발작이 끝나면 자기가 끌어안기게 될지 할퀴게 될지 알 수 없었고, 그 둘 중 어느 쪽이든 똑같이 달갑지 않다고 여겨져서, 스퀴어스 양이 손수건에 대고 흐느끼는 동안 아주 조용히 자리를 떴어.\n\n'이게 바로 그 결과로구나,' 니콜라스는 더듬더듬 어두운 침실로 길을 찾아가며 생각했어. '우연히 어떤 무리에 끼게 되든 거기에 척척 맞춰 버리는 이 빌어먹을 넉살의 결과야. 내가 입 꾹 다물고 가만히만 있었더라면, 그럴 수도 있었는데, 이런 일은 없었을 텐데.'\n\n그는 몇 분간 귀를 기울였지만, 사방이 고요했어.\n\n'그래,' 그가 중얼거렸어. '이 끔찍한 곳을 보는 것도, 이곳의 비열한 주인을 마주하는 것도 잠깐이나마 벗어날 수 있어서 좋았지. 그런데 난 이 사람들을 서로 으르렁대게 만들었고, 굳이 그럴 필요도 없는데, 하늘도 알다시피, 새 원수를 둘이나 만들었어. 그래, 단 한 시간이라도 지금 내 주위가 어떤 곳인지 잊어버린 데 대한 마땅한 벌이야!'\n\n이렇게 말하며, 그는 지쳐 잠든 아이들 무리를 손으로 더듬어 가며 자기의 초라한 침대로 기어 들어갔어.\n\n# CHAPTER 10\n\n니콜라스가 요크셔로 떠난 지 이틀째 되는 날 아침이었어. 케이트 니클비는 라 크리비 양의 방에서, 먼지가 잔뜩 쌓인 받침대 위에 올려진 무척 낡은 의자에 앉아 있었지. 라 크리비 양이 그리고 있는 초상화를 위해 모델을 서 주고 있었던 거야. 그 그림을 더 완벽하게 만들려고 라 크리비 양은 길가 문에 걸어 두는 진열 상자까지 위층으로 가져왔어. 그래야 케이트 니클비 양의 그려진 얼굴에 밝은 연어빛 살색을 더 잘 입힐 수 있다고 생각했거든. 그 빛깔은 원래 그 상자 안에 든 어느 젊은 장교의 세밀화를 그릴 때 처음 떠올린 색이었어. 그리고 라 크리비 양의 주요 친구들과 후원자들은 그 밝은 연어빛 살색을 미술에서 아주 새로운 시도라고 여겼지. 사실 정말 그렇기도 했고.\n\n\"이제 제대로 잡은 것 같아요.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"딱 그 색조예요! 이건 분명히 내가 그린 것 중에 가장 사랑스러운 초상화가 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그건 분명 선생님의 재능 덕분이겠죠.\" 케이트가 웃으며 대꾸했어.\n\n\"아니, 아니, 그렇게는 못 받아들여요, 아가씨.\" 라 크리비 양이 받아쳤어. \"아주 좋은 모델이라서 그래요. 정말 아주 좋은 모델이죠. 물론 다루는 방식에 따라 좀 달라지긴 하지만요.\"\n\n\"적잖이 달라지죠.\" 케이트가 한마디 했어.\n\n\"아, 그래요, 그 점은 아가씨 말이 맞아요.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"대체로 맞는 말이에요. 하지만 이번 경우에 그게 그렇게 대단히 중요하다고는 못 하겠네요. 아! 미술의 어려움이란, 아가씨, 정말 크답니다.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 그렇겠죠.\" 케이트는 마음씨 고운 작은 친구의 기분을 맞춰 주며 말했어.\n\n\"아가씨가 아무리 어렴풋이 짐작해 봐도 그 어려움은 그 이상이에요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대답했어. \"온 힘을 다해 눈을 살려내고, 또 온 힘을 다해 코를 눌러 주고, 머리는 보태고, 이는 아예 죄다 빼 버리고 말이죠. 작은 세밀화 한 점에 얼마나 손이 많이 가는지 아가씨는 짐작도 못 할 거예요.\"\n\n\"보수로는 그 수고를 도저히 못 갚겠네요.\" 케이트가 말했어.\n\n\"그래요, 못 갚죠, 그게 사실이에요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대답했어. \"게다가 사람들이 어찌나 불만이 많고 막무가내인지, 열에 아홉은 그리는 재미가 하나도 없어요. 어떨 땐 '아이고, 라 크리비 양, 어쩜 이렇게 심각하게 그려 놓으셨어요!' 그러고, 또 어떨 땐 '어머, 라 크리비 양, 어쩜 이렇게 실실 웃는 얼굴이에요!' 그러죠. 좋은 초상화의 핵심이란 게 바로 심각하거나 아니면 실실 웃거나 둘 중 하나여야 한다는 건데 말이에요. 안 그러면 초상화라고 할 수도 없거든요.\"\n\n\"그래요!\" 케이트가 웃으며 말했어.\n\n\"그럼요, 아가씨. 모델들이 늘 그 둘 중 하나거든요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대답했어. \"왕립 미술원을 봐요! 검은 벨벳 조끼를 입고 둥근 탁자나 대리석 판 위에 주먹을 꽉 쥐고 있는 신사들의 그 아름답고 번들번들한 초상화들, 그게 다 심각한 쪽이에요. 그리고 작은 양산이나 작은 강아지, 작은 아이들을 데리고 노는 부인들은 죄다 실실 웃는 쪽이고요. 미술에선 다 같은 규칙이고, 그저 대상만 바뀔 뿐이에요. 사실은 말이죠,\" 라 크리비 양은 은밀한 속삭임으로 목소리를 낮췄어. \"초상화에는 딱 두 가지 양식밖에 없어요. 심각한 쪽하고 실실 웃는 쪽. 그래서 우리는 늘 전문직 사람들한테는 심각한 쪽을 쓰고 (가끔 배우들은 빼고요), 그리고 그렇게 똑똑해 보이는 데 별로 신경 안 쓰는 일반 신사 숙녀들한테는 실실 웃는 쪽을 쓰지요.\"\n\n케이트는 이 이야기가 무척 재미있는 모양이었어. 그리고 라 크리비 양은 흔들림 없는 만족감에 젖어 그림을 그리며 이야기를 계속했지.\n\n\"장교를 정말 많이도 그리시나 봐요!\" 케이트는 이야기가 잠시 멈춘 틈을 타서, 방 안을 둘러보며 말했어.\n\n\"많이 뭐라고요, 아가씨?\" 라 크리비 양이 일하던 손을 멈추고 고개를 들며 물었어. \"성격 초상화 말이군요, 아 그래요. 저 사람들은 진짜 군인이 아니에요.\"\n\"아니라고요!\"\n\n\"아이고, 당연히 아니죠. 그냥 사무원이나 뭐 그런 사람들이에요. 그림 그릴 때 입으려고 군복 외투를 빌려서, 가방에 넣어 여기로 보내는 거죠. 어떤 화가들은,\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"빨간 외투를 갖춰 놓고, 대여비랑 카민 물감 값으로 칠 실링 육 펜스를 더 받기도 해요. 하지만 나는 그러지 않아요. 그건 정당하지 않다고 보거든요.\"\n\n라 크리비 양은 그런 미끼로 모델을 낚지 않는다는 게 무척 자랑스럽다는 듯 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우고는, 일에 더 골똘히 매달렸어. 가끔 고개를 들어 방금 칠한 어느 부분을 말할 수 없이 흐뭇하게 바라볼 뿐이었지. 그리고 이따금 지금 어느 부분을 그리는 중인지 케이트한테 일러 주기도 했어. \"그렇다고,\" 라 크리비 양은 굳이 덧붙였어. \"아가씨가 그림 그리기 좋게 표정을 꾸미라는 건 아니에요. 다만 우리 관습이 그래요. 모델한테 지금 어느 부분을 그리는 중인지 말해 주거든요. 혹시 꼭 넣고 싶은 표정이 있으면 그때 지어 보일 수 있게 말이죠.\"\n\n\"그런데,\" 라 크리비 양이 한참, 그러니까 일 분 반쯤이나 잠잠히 있다가 입을 열었어. \"삼촌은 언제 다시 만나기로 했어요?\"\n\n\"잘 모르겠어요. 진작 만났을 줄 알았는데요.\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"곧 만났으면 좋겠어요. 이렇게 막막하게 기다리는 게 무엇보다 힘들거든요.\"\n\n\"돈은 좀 있는 분이겠죠?\" 라 크리비 양이 물었어.\n\n\"아주 부자라고 들었어요.\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"정말 그런지는 모르겠지만, 그런 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"아, 틀림없이 부자일 거예요. 안 그러면 그렇게 무뚝뚝하지도 않겠죠.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. 영리함과 순박함이 묘하게 뒤섞인 작은 여자였거든. \"사내가 곰처럼 굴 땐, 대개 살림이 꽤 넉넉하다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"태도가 거칠긴 해요.\" 케이트가 말했어.\n\n\"거칠다고요!\" 라 크리비 양이 소리쳤어. \"그 사람한테 대면 호저도 깃털 침대 같겠어요! 그렇게 비뚤어진 늙은 야만인은 처음 봤다니까요.\"\n\n\"그저 태도가 그럴 뿐일 거예요.\" 케이트가 조심스레 말했어. \"젊었을 때 무슨 실망스러운 일을 겪었다거나, 아니면 어떤 불행 때문에 성미가 비뚤어졌다고 들은 것 같아요. 그럴 만한 사람이라고 확실히 알기 전엔, 함부로 나쁘게 생각하고 싶지 않아요.\"\n\n\"음, 그건 아주 옳고 마땅한 말이에요.\" 세밀화 화가가 말했어. \"그리고 내가 아가씨를 그렇게 만드는 사람이 되지 않게 하늘이 막아 주시길! 하지만요, 삼촌이 자기는 별로 아쉬울 것도 없으면서, 아가씨랑 어머니한테 자그마한 용돈이라도 주실 순 없을까요? 아가씨가 좋은 데 시집갈 때까지 두 분이 편하게 지낼 만큼, 그리고 나중엔 어머니한테 작은 밑천이 될 만큼요. 예를 들어 한 해 백 파운드쯤이 그 사람한테 대수겠어요?\"\n\n\"그 사람한테 그게 어떤 의미인지는 모르겠어요.\" 케이트가 힘주어 말했어. \"하지만 저한테 그건, 받느니 차라리 죽는 게 낫겠다 싶은 거예요.\"\n\n\"이런 세상에!\" 라 크리비 양이 소리쳤어.\n\n\"그 사람한테 기대고 사는 건,\" 케이트가 말했어. \"제 삶을 통째로 쓰라리게 만들 거예요. 구걸하는 게 차라리 덜 굴욕적일 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"이런!\" 라 크리비 양이 외쳤어. \"남이 험담하는 것도 못 견디겠다던 그 친척에 대해, 정작 아가씨 입에서 이런 말이 나오니, 솔직히 좀 묘하게 들리네요.\"\n\n\"그러게요.\" 케이트가 한결 부드럽게 말했어. \"정말 그렇겠죠. 저는, 저는, 그저 이런 뜻이에요. 더 나았던 시절의 기억과 마음을 품고 있으니, 누구의 적선에도 기대어 살 수가 없다는 거예요. 그 사람뿐 아니라, 그 누구한테도요.\"\n\n라 크리비 양은 친구를 슬쩍 곁눈질했어. 정말 랄프가 싫어서 그러는 건 아닌가 싶었던 거지. 하지만 어린 친구가 괴로워하는 걸 보고는, 아무 말도 하지 않았어.\n\n\"제가 그 사람한테 바라는 건 딱 하나예요.\" 케이트는 말하면서 눈물을 흘렸어. \"저를 위해 아주 조금만 수고해 주시는 거요. 그 사람의 추천으로, 오직 추천만으로요, 말 그대로 제 밥벌이를 하면서 어머니 곁에 남아 있게 해 주는 거예요. 우리가 다시 행복을 맛볼 수 있을지는 사랑하는 오빠가 잘되느냐에 달렸어요. 하지만 그 사람이 이것만 해 준다면, 그리고 니콜라스가 잘 지내고 즐겁게 산다는 소식만 전해 준다면, 저는 만족할 거예요.\"\n\n케이트가 말을 마쳤을 때, 그녀와 문 사이를 가린 가림막 뒤에서 바스락거리는 소리가 났어. 그리고 누군가 벽판을 똑똑 두드렸지.\n\n\"누구든 들어오세요!\" 라 크리비 양이 소리쳤어.\n\n그 사람은 시키는 대로 했어. 그러고는 곧장 앞으로 나오는데, 그 모습과 얼굴을 드러낸 사람은 다름 아닌 랄프 니클비 본인이었지.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 부인들.\" 랄프는 두 사람을 번갈아 날카롭게 쳐다보며 말했어. \"어찌나 큰 소리로 얘기들을 하시는지, 들리게 할 수가 없더군요.\"\n\n이 사업가의 마음속에 평소보다 더 고약한 으르렁거림이 도사리고 있을 때면, 그는 한 가지 버릇이 있었어. 두툼하게 튀어나온 눈썹 밑으로 잠깐 눈을 거의 감추었다가, 그다음에 그 눈을 온전히 날카롭게 드러내는 거였지. 지금도 랄프는 그렇게 했어. 그리고 얇고 꾹 다문 입술을 가르고 입가의 험상궂은 주름을 잡히게 하는 미소를 애써 누르려 했지. 그 모습에 두 사람은, 방금 나눈 이야기의 일부가, 아니 어쩌면 전부가 그의 귀에 들어갔다는 걸 확신했어.\n\n\"위층으로 올라가는 길에 들렀소. 여기 있을 것 같다는 생각이 반은 넘게 들어서 말이오.\" 랄프는 조카에게 말하며, 초상화를 경멸하듯 바라봤어. \"저게 내 조카 초상화요, 부인?\"\n\n\"네, 그래요, 니클비 씨.\" 라 크리비 양이 무척 발랄한 태도로 말했어. \"그리고 우리끼리 얘기지만, 선생님, 이게 아주 멋진 초상화가 될 거예요. 그린 사람이 직접 하는 말이지만요.\"\n\n\"굳이 나한테 보여 줄 것 없소, 부인.\" 랄프가 자리를 옮기며 말했어. \"나는 닮았는지 어쩐지 보는 눈이 없어서. 거의 다 됐소?\"\n\n\"네, 그럼요.\" 라 크리비 양이 붓 자루 끝을 입에 문 채 생각하며 대답했어. \"두 번만 더 앉아 주면—\"\n\n\"당장 다 끝내시오, 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"이 애는 내일부터는 시시한 짓에 빈둥거릴 시간이 없을 거요. 일이오, 부인, 일. 우리는 다 일을 해야 하니까. 세놓을 방은 다 나갔소, 부인?\"\n\n\"아직 광고는 안 붙였어요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"당장 붙이시오, 부인. 이번 주 지나면 그 사람들도 방이 필요 없을 거요. 아니면 필요해도 셋돈을 못 낼 테고. 자, 얘야, 준비됐으면 더 이상 시간 끌지 말자.\"\n\n랄프 니클비는 친절한 척을 했는데, 그게 평소 태도보다 더 어색해 보였어. 그는 조카에게 앞서 가라고 손짓하고는, 라 크리비 양에게 점잖게 고개를 숙여 인사했지. 그러고는 문을 닫고 위층으로 따라 올라갔어. 거기서 니클비 부인이 반가운 인사를 잔뜩 늘어놓으며 그를 맞았어. 랄프는 다소 퉁명스럽게 그 인사를 끊더니, 못마땅한 듯 손을 내저으며 곧장 찾아온 용건으로 들어갔어.\n\n\"따님의 일자리를 하나 구했소, 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"어머나.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어. \"이제야 말하지만, 그게 바로 내가 당신한테 기대했던 거예요. '두고 봐라.' 어제 아침 식사 때 케이트한테 그랬다니까요. '삼촌이 그렇게 척척 니콜라스를 챙겨 줬으니, 너한테도 적어도 그만큼은 해 주기 전엔 우리를 그냥 두지 않으실 거다.' 내가 한 말 그대로예요, 기억나는 한은요. 케이트야, 얘, 어서 삼촌한테 감사 인사를—\"\n\n\"제 말 좀 하게 해 주시오, 부인, 제발.\" 랄프가 제수의 말이 거침없이 쏟아지는 걸 끊으며 말했어.\n\n\"케이트야, 얘, 삼촌이 말씀하시게 두렴.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"저도 삼촌이 말씀하시길 무척 바라고 있어요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 받아 말했어.\n\n\"그래, 얘, 삼촌이 말씀하시길 바란다면, 끼어들지 말고 삼촌이 하실 말씀을 다 하시게 두는 게 좋겠다.\" 니클비 부인이 자잘하게 고개를 끄덕이고 눈살을 찌푸리며 말했어. \"삼촌은 시간이 무척 귀한 분이야, 얘. 그리고 네가 아무리 바란다 해도, 사실 우리처럼 삼촌을 거의 못 뵙고 산 다정한 친척이라면 당연히 삼촌이 우리 곁에 더 오래 머무는 기쁨을 누리고 싶겠지만, 그래도 우리는 이기적으로 굴면 안 돼. 시내에서 하시는 그 중요한 일을 생각해야 한단다.\"\n\n\"무척 고맙소, 부인.\" 랄프가 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 비웃음을 띠며 말했어. \"이 집안에는 사업하는 습관이 없다 보니, 정작 사업 얘기에 들어가기도 전에 말을 어마어마하게 낭비하게 되는 모양이군요. 그나마 사업 얘기가 나오기라도 한다면 말이오.\"\n\n\"정말 그런가 봐요.\" 니클비 부인이 한숨을 쉬며 대답했어. \"가엾은 당신 동생도—\"\n\n\"내 가엾은 동생은, 부인,\" 랄프가 매몰차게 끼어들었어. \"사업이 뭔지 도통 몰랐소. 정말이지 그 말뜻조차 모르고 살았을 거요.\"\n\n\"그랬나 봐요.\" 니클비 부인이 손수건을 눈에 갖다 대며 말했어. \"내가 없었으면 그이가 어떻게 됐을지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n사람이란 참 이상한 존재야! 랄프가 처음 만났을 때 솜씨 좋게 던져 놓은 그 작은 미끼가, 아직도 낚싯바늘에 대롱대롱 매달려 있었거든. 하루 사이에도 자기 처지가 옹색하게 바뀐 걸 일깨우는 사소한 결핍이나 불편이 닥칠 때마다, 니클비 부인의 머릿속엔 천 파운드 지참금에 대한 짜증 섞인 환상이 떠올랐어. 그러다 마침내 그녀는, 죽은 남편의 모든 채권자 가운데 자기가 제일 푸대접을 받았고 제일 가엾은 신세라고 스스로를 설득하기에 이르렀지. 그러면서도 그녀는 오랜 세월 남편을 끔찍이 사랑했고, 보통 사람들보다 더 이기적인 것도 아니었어. 갑작스러운 가난이란 게 사람을 이렇게 못 견디게 만드는 법이지. 변변한 연금 하나만 있었어도 그녀의 생각은 단번에 옛날 자리로 돌아갔을 거야.\n\n\"한탄해 봐야 소용없소, 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"온갖 헛수고 중에서도, 지나간 날을 좇아 눈물 한 방울 흘려보내는 것만큼 헛된 일은 없소.\"\n\n\"그래요.\" 니클비 부인이 흐느꼈어. \"정말 그래요.\"\n\n\"부인이 직접 지갑과 몸으로 사업에 소홀했던 결과를 이토록 뼈저리게 느끼고 있으니,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"분명 자식들한테도 어려서부터 사업에 매달려야 한다는 걸 단단히 일러 주시겠지요.\"\n\n\"물론 그래야죠.\" 니클비 부인이 받아 말했어. \"쓰라린 경험이란 게 그렇잖아요, 아주버님. 케이트야, 얘, 그걸 다음에 니콜라스한테 보내는 편지에 적어 두렴. 아니면 내가 편지 쓸 때 잊지 않게 일러 주든지.\"\n\n랄프는 잠시 말을 멈췄어. 그리고 이제 딸이 자기 제안에 반대하더라도 어미만큼은 확실히 자기편으로 만들어 놨다는 걸 알고는, 말을 이어 갔어.\n\n\"내가 힘을 써서 구한 그 자리는, 부인, 다름 아니라, 짧게 말해 어느 양품점 겸 양장점이오.\"\n\n\"양품점이라고요!\" 니클비 부인이 소리쳤어.\n\n\"양품점 겸 양장점이오, 부인.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"런던의 양장점 주인들은, 일상사에 그토록 밝은 부인한테 굳이 일러 줄 것도 없지만, 큰돈을 벌고 마차를 굴리며 대단한 재산가가 된다오.\"\n\n자, 양품점이니 양장점이니 하는 말에 니클비 부인의 머릿속에 처음 떠오른 건, 검은 방수포를 덧댄 버들 바구니들이었어. 거리에서 이리저리 실려 다니는 걸 본 기억이 있었거든. 하지만 랄프가 말을 이어 가자, 그 바구니들은 사라지고, 그 자리에 웨스트엔드의 큰 저택과 말끔한 자가용 마차, 그리고 은행 통장의 환상이 들어찼어. 이 모든 그림들이 어찌나 빠르게 줄지어 떠오르던지, 그가 말을 채 마치기도 전에 부인은 고개를 끄덕이며 무척 만족스러운 얼굴로 \"정말 그래요.\" 하고 말했어.\n\n\"삼촌 말씀이 정말 옳다, 케이트야, 얘.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"결혼하고 나서 가엾은 네 아빠랑 내가 시내에 처음 왔을 때가 생각나는구나. 어느 젊은 아가씨가 우리 집에 모자를 하나 갖다 줬는데, 하얗고 초록빛 장식에 초록빛 페르시아 안감을 댄 얇은 챙 모자였어. 자기 마차를 타고 와서는 전속력으로 문 앞까지 달려왔지. 적어도 자기 마차였는지 빌린 마차였는지는 확실치 않지만, 마차를 돌리다가 말이 그만 쓰러져 죽어 버린 건 아주 똑똑히 기억나. 그리고 네 가엾은 아빠가 그러더구나, 그 말이 보름 동안 여물을 한 톨도 못 먹었던 거라고.\"\n\n양품점 주인들이 얼마나 부유한지를 이토록 인상적으로 보여 주는 이 일화는, 그다지 큰 호응을 얻지 못했어. 케이트는 이야기가 펼쳐지는 내내 고개를 푹 숙이고 있었고, 랄프는 누가 봐도 알 만큼 극도로 조바심을 내는 기색이었거든.\n\n\"그 부인 이름은,\" 랄프가 황급히 끼어들며 말했어. \"만탈리니요. 만탈리니 부인. 내가 아는 사람이오. 캐번디시 광장 근처에 살지요. 따님이 그 자리를 한번 노려 볼 마음이 있다면, 당장 거기로 데려가겠소.\"\n\n\"삼촌한테 할 말 없니, 얘?\" 니클비 부인이 물었어.\n\n\"많아요.\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"하지만 지금은 아니에요. 단둘이 있을 때 말씀드리고 싶어요. 가는 길에 감사 인사도 드리고 하고 싶은 말도 하면, 삼촌 시간도 아낄 수 있으니까요.\"\n\n그 말을 남기고 케이트는 서둘러 자리를 떴어. 얼굴을 타고 슬며시 흐르는 감정의 흔적을 감추고, 외출 채비를 하려는 거였지. 그동안 니클비 부인은 아주버님을 붙들고, 눈물을 잔뜩 흘리며 옛 부유하던 시절에 갖고 있던 자단목 캐비닛 피아노의 치수를 자세히 늘어놓아 그를 즐겁게 해 줬어. 거기다 다리를 깎아 만들고 커튼에 맞춰 초록빛 친츠 방석을 댄 응접실 의자 여덟 개도 시시콜콜 묘사했지. 하나에 이 파운드 십오 실링이나 주고 산 건데, 경매에서는 거저나 다름없는 값에 넘어갔다는 거였어.\n\n이 추억담은 마침내 외출복 차림으로 돌아온 케이트 덕분에 뚝 끊겼어. 케이트가 없는 동안 내내 안달하며 씩씩대던 랄프는 지체 없이, 그리고 격식이라곤 거의 차리지 않고 거리로 내려갔어.\n\n\"자.\" 그가 케이트의 팔을 잡으며 말했어. \"최대한 빨리 걸어라. 그래야 매일 아침 일터로 걸어갈 때 맞춰야 할 보폭에 익숙해질 테니.\" 그렇게 말하며 랄프는 케이트를 데리고 꽤 빠른 걸음으로 캐번디시 광장 쪽으로 향했어.\n\n\"정말 고마워요, 삼촌.\" 한동안 말없이 서둘러 걷고 나서 케이트가 말했어. \"정말로요.\"\n\n\"그렇다니 다행이구나.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"네 본분을 다하길 바란다.\"\n\n\"기쁘게 해 드리도록 애쓸게요, 삼촌.\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"정말이지 저는—\"\n\n\"울지 마라.\" 랄프가 으르렁댔어. \"나는 우는 걸 질색해.\"\n\n\"바보 같은 짓인 거 저도 알아요, 삼촌.\" 가엾은 케이트가 입을 열었어.\n\n\"그래, 바보 같지.\" 랄프가 말을 뚝 끊으며 대답했어. \"게다가 아주 꾸민 티가 나. 다신 그런 꼴 안 봤으면 좋겠다.\"\n\n차갑고 무심한 낯선 사람들 틈에서 완전히 새로운 인생의 무대에 처음 발을 들이려는 어리고 여린 처녀의 눈물을 말리는 데, 이게 최선의 방법은 아니었을 거야. 그래도 어쨌든 효과는 있었지. 케이트는 얼굴이 새빨개졌고, 잠깐 숨을 가쁘게 몰아쉬더니, 한결 단단하고 결연한 걸음으로 걸어갔어.\n\n참 묘한 대조였어. 겁 많은 시골 처녀는 거리를 분주히 오가는 인파 속에서 잔뜩 움츠러들어, 밀려드는 사람들에게 길을 내주고, 군중 속에서 그를 놓칠세라 랄프에게 바짝 매달렸지. 한편 험상궂고 모진 얼굴의 사업가는 행인들을 팔꿈치로 밀쳐 가며 고집스레 나아갔어. 이따금 지나가는 아는 사람과 무뚝뚝하게 인사를 주고받기도 했는데, 그 사람들은 그가 데리고 가는 예쁜 처녀를 놀란 얼굴로 돌아보며, 어울리지 않는 이 동행을 의아해하는 듯했지. 하지만 나란히 뛰는 두 사람의 가슴속을 읽을 수 있었다면, 그건 한층 더 묘한 대조였을 거야. 한 사람의 부드러운 순수함과 다른 한 사람의 거친 악의를 낱낱이 드러내 본다면 말이야. 다정한 처녀의 티 없는 생각들을 들여다보다가, 늙은 사내의 그 온갖 교활한 음모와 계산 가운데, 죽음이나 무덤을 떠올리는 말 한마디 숫자 하나 없다는 데에 놀라게 됐을 거야. 하지만 사실이 그랬어. 그리고 더 묘한 건, 이건 날마다 일어나는 일이지만, 따뜻하고 젊은 가슴은 천 가지 근심과 걱정으로 두근거리는데, 늙고 세속에 찌든 사내의 가슴은 제 우리 속에 녹슨 채 누워, 그저 교활한 기계 부속처럼만 뛸 뿐, 살아 있는 그 무엇에게도 희망이나 두려움, 사랑이나 염려의 고동을 단 한 번도 내주지 않았다는 거야.\n\n\"삼촌.\" 이제 목적지에 거의 다 왔겠다 싶었을 때 케이트가 말했어. \"한 가지만 여쭤볼게요. 저는 집에서 살게 되는 건가요?\"\n\n\"집에서?\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"그게 어딘데?\"\n\n\"어머니랑 같이요. 그 과부 말이에요.\" 케이트가 힘주어 말했어.\n\n\"실상 너는 여기서 살게 될 거다.\" 랄프가 받아 말했어. \"여기서 밥을 먹고, 아침부터 밤까지 여기 있을 테니까. 가끔은 어쩌면 다시 아침이 될 때까지도 있을지 모르고.\"\n\n\"하지만 밤에요, 제 말은.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"저는 어머니를 떠날 수 없어요, 삼촌. 집이라고 부를 수 있는 곳이 있어야 해요. 그게 어머니가 계신 곳이라면 어디든 좋아요. 아주 누추한 곳이라도요.\"\n\n\"누추한 곳이라도라니!\" 그 말에 발끈해 발걸음을 더 빨리하며 랄프가 말했어. \"누추한 곳일 수밖에 없다는 말이겠지. 누추한 곳이라도 좋다니! 이 애가 정신이 나갔나?\"\n\n\"말이 그냥 나와 버렸어요. 정말 그런 뜻은 아니었어요.\" 케이트가 다급히 말했어.\n\n\"아니길 바란다.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"그런데 제 질문에요, 삼촌. 아직 대답을 안 해 주셨어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그런 비슷한 걸 물을 줄 알았다.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그리고, 나는 아주 강하게 반대지만, 명심해라, 그래도 대비는 해 뒀다. 너를 통근하는 일꾼으로 얘기해 뒀으니까. 그러니 너는 매일 밤 그 누추할지도 모를 집으로 가게 될 거다.\"\n\n여기엔 위안이 있었어. 케이트는 삼촌의 배려에 감사 인사를 쏟아냈고, 랄프는 마치 그 인사를 다 받을 자격이 있다는 듯 받아들였지. 그러고 둘은 더 이상 별다른 말 없이 양장점 문 앞에 다다랐어. 문에는 만탈리니 부인의 이름과 직업을 새긴 아주 큼직한 명패가 걸려 있었고, 멋진 계단을 올라가게 되어 있었어. 건물엔 가게도 하나 딸려 있었지만, 그건 장미 향유 수입상한테 세를 준 상태였지. 만탈리니 부인의 진열실은 이 층에 있었어. 멋들어진 커튼을 친 창가에 최신 유행하는 우아한 모자 두세 개와 더없이 세련된 값비싼 옷가지 몇 벌을 슬쩍 내걸어 둔 것으로, 귀족과 상류층에게 그 사실을 알려 주고 있었지.\n\n제복을 입은 하인이 문을 열었어. 만탈리니 부인이 집에 있느냐는 랄프의 물음에, 하인은 두 사람을 멋진 현관을 지나 널찍한 계단 위로 안내해서 진열실로 데려갔어. 그곳은 널따란 응접실 두 개로 이루어져 있었고, 엄청나게 다양한 화려한 드레스와 옷감들이 진열되어 있었지. 어떤 건 받침대 위에 정돈해 두고, 어떤 건 소파에 아무렇게나 던져 놓고, 또 어떤 건 양탄자 위에 흩어 놓거나 체경에 걸어 두거나, 아니면 여기저기 잔뜩 늘어놓은 갖가지 값진 가구들 사이에 뒤섞여 있었어.\n\n두 사람은 랄프 니클비가 견디기 어려울 만큼 오래 거기서 기다렸어. 랄프는 주위의 번지르르한 싸구려 장식들을 무심히 둘러보다가, 마침내 종을 당기려던 참이었어. 그때 어떤 신사가 불쑥 방 안으로 머리를 들이밀더니, 누가 있는 걸 보고는 또 그만큼 불쑥 머리를 도로 빼 버렸지.\n\n\"이봐요. 여보시오!\" 랄프가 소리쳤어. \"거기 누구요?\"\n\n랄프의 목소리에 그 머리가 다시 나타났어. 그리고 그 입은 아주 길게 늘어선 새하얀 이를 드러내며, 간드러진 말투로 이렇게 내뱉었어. \"젠장. 아니, 니클비잖소! 아, 젠장!\" 이렇게 감탄사를 내뱉고 나서 그 신사는 다가와, 랄프와 아주 다정하게 악수를 했어. 그는 화려한 실내복을 입고 있었는데, 조끼와 터키풍 바지도 같은 무늬였지. 분홍빛 비단 목수건을 두르고 밝은 초록빛 슬리퍼를 신었고, 아주 풍성한 시곗줄을 몸에 칭칭 두르고 있었어. 게다가 구레나룻과 콧수염을 길렀는데, 둘 다 까맣게 물들이고 멋들어지게 말아 올렸지.\n\n\"젠장, 설마 내가 필요하다는 건 아니겠지요, 응, 젠장?\" 이 신사가 랄프의 어깨를 탁 치며 말했어.\n\n\"아직은 아니오.\" 랄프가 비꼬듯 말했어.\n\n\"하! 하! 젠장.\" 그 신사가 외쳤어. 그러고는 좀 더 우아하게 웃으려고 휙 몸을 돌리다가, 근처에 서 있던 케이트 니클비와 마주쳤어.\n\n\"내 조카요.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"기억나는군요.\" 그 신사가 깜빡한 걸 벌하듯 검지 마디로 제 코를 톡 치며 말했어. \"젠장, 무슨 일로 왔는지 기억나요. 이쪽으로 오시오, 니클비. 아가씨, 나를 따라오겠소? 하! 하! 다들 나를 따라오지요, 니클비. 늘 그래요, 젠장, 늘 그렇다오.\"\n\n그 신사는 이런 식으로 제 익살을 마음껏 풀어 놓으며, 삼 층의 어느 개인 응접실로 앞장서 갔어. 아래층 방에 못지않게 우아하게 꾸며진 곳이었지. 거기엔 은제 커피포트와 달걀 껍데기, 그리고 일 인분짜리 어수선한 도자기 그릇이 놓여 있어서, 그가 방금 아침을 먹은 모양이라는 걸 알 수 있었어.\n\n\"앉으시오, 아가씨.\" 그 신사가 말했어. 그러고는 케이트가 무안해질 만큼 빤히 쳐다보더니, 그 짓에 성공했다는 듯 흐뭇하게 씩 웃었어. \"이 빌어먹게 높은 방은 사람 숨이 막힌다니까. 이 망할 놈의 꼭대기 방 말이오. 아무래도 이사를 가야겠소, 니클비.\"\n\n\"꼭 그러시오.\" 랄프가 씁쓸하게 주위를 둘러보며 대답했어.\n\n\"당신은 정말 빌어먹게 묘한 작자요, 니클비.\" 그 신사가 말했어. \"이제껏 본 중에 제일 빌어먹게 약아빠지고, 제일 잔머리 잘 굴리고, 제일 별난 성미를 지닌 늙은 금화 은화 주조꾼이라니까. 젠장.\"\n\n랄프를 이런 식으로 칭찬하고 나서, 그 신사는 종을 울리고는 사람이 올 때까지 케이트를 빤히 쳐다봤어. 사람이 오자 그는 시선을 거두고, 마님더러 당장 오시라고 전하라고 일렀지. 그러고는 다시 케이트를 빤히 쳐다보기 시작해서, 만탈리니 부인이 나타날 때까지 멈추지 않았어.\n\n이 양장점 주인은 풍만한 몸매에, 옷을 멋지게 차려입은 제법 잘생긴 여자였어. 하지만 한 여섯 달 전에 결혼한, 그 터키풍 바지를 입은 신사보다는 훨씬 나이가 많았지. 그의 원래 이름은 먼틀이었어. 그런데 그게 쉬운 변형을 거쳐 만탈리니로 바뀐 거야. 영국식 이름이 사업에 심각한 해가 될 거라고 부인이 옳게 판단했거든. 그는 제 구레나룻 덕에 결혼한 셈이었어. 그 구레나룻을 밑천 삼아 그는 여러 해 동안 점잖게 먹고살아 왔고, 최근엔 끈기 있게 가꾼 끝에 콧수염까지 보태 그 밑천을 개선해 놨지. 그 콧수염이 그에게 편안한 자립을 보장해 줄 것 같았어. 현재 그가 사업에서 맡은 몫이라곤, 돈을 쓰는 일과, 그 돈이 떨어졌을 때 가끔 랄프 니클비한테 마차를 몰고 가서 고객들의 어음을 일정 수수료를 떼고 할인받아 오는 일뿐이었거든.\n\n\"내 생명이여.\" 만탈리니 씨가 말했어. \"어쩌면 그렇게 빌어먹게 지독히도 오래 걸렸소!\"\n\n\"니클비 씨가 와 계신 줄도 몰랐어요, 여보.\" 만탈리니 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"그렇다면 그 하인 녀석이 갑절로 빌어먹을 형편없는 악당이로군, 내 영혼이여.\" 만탈리니 씨가 항의했어.\n\n\"여보.\" 만탈리니 부인이 말했어. \"그건 전부 당신 잘못이에요.\"\n\n\"내 잘못이라고, 내 마음의 기쁨이여?\"\n\n\"그럼요.\" 부인이 대꾸했어. \"당신이 그 사람을 야단치지 않으니, 뭘 기대해요, 여보?\"\n\n\"그를 야단치라고, 내 영혼의 즐거움이여!\"\n\n\"네. 그 사람한테 따끔하게 말 좀 해 줘야겠어요, 정말로요.\" 부인이 입을 삐죽이며 말했어.\n\n\"그럼 화내지 마시오.\" 만탈리니 씨가 말했어. \"그놈을 빌어먹게 비명을 지를 때까지 채찍질해 주리다.\" 이렇게 약속하고 만탈리니 씨는 만탈리니 부인에게 입을 맞췄어. 그러자 만탈리니 부인은 만탈리니 씨의 귀를 장난스레 잡아당겼지. 그러고 나서 둘은 본론으로 들어갔어.\n\n\"자, 부인.\" 이 모든 광경을, 어지간한 사내로선 표정에 담기 힘들 만큼의 경멸을 띠고 지켜보던 랄프가 말했어. \"이 애가 내 조카요.\"\n\n\"그렇군요, 니클비 씨.\" 만탈리니 부인이 케이트를 머리끝부터 발끝까지, 다시 발끝부터 머리끝까지 훑어보며 대답했어. \"프랑스어 할 줄 아니, 얘?\"\n\n\"네, 부인.\" 케이트가 차마 고개를 들지 못한 채 대답했어. 실내복을 입은 그 역겨운 사내의 눈길이 자기를 향하고 있는 게 느껴졌거든.\n\n\"빌어먹게 원어민처럼?\" 남편이 물었어.\n\n케이트는 이 물음엔 아무 대답도 하지 않고, 묻는 사람한테서 등을 돌려 버렸어. 마치 부인이 물을 만한 것에 답하려고 마음먹은 듯이 말이야.\n\n\"우리 가게에선 스무 명의 젊은 여공을 늘 부리고 있단다.\" 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"그렇군요, 부인!\" 케이트가 조심스레 대답했어.\n\n\"그래, 그중엔 빌어먹게 예쁜 애들도 좀 있고.\" 주인이 말했어.\n\n\"만탈리니!\" 그의 아내가 무시무시한 목소리로 외쳤어.\n\n\"내 감각의 우상이여!\" 만탈리니가 말했어.\n\n\"내 가슴을 찢어 놓을 작정이에요?\"\n\n\"꼬마 발레 무희들로 우글거리는 반구가 이만 개 있다 해도 그럴 수야 없지.\" 만탈리니가 시적인 어조로 대답했어.\n\n\"계속 그런 식으로 말하면 정말 그렇게 될 거예요.\" 그의 아내가 말했어. \"니클비 씨가 들으면 어떻게 생각하시겠어요?\"\n\n\"오! 아무 생각도 안 하오, 부인, 아무것도.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"나는 저 사람의 다정한 성품도 알고, 부인의 성품도 아오. 그저 매일의 정담에 흥취를 더해 주는 자그마한 말장난이지요. 그토록 오래 갈 것 같은 가정의 기쁨에 달콤함을 보태는 연인들의 다툼이오. 그게 다요. 그게 다지요.\"\n\n쇠문이 제 경첩과 다투기로 작정하고, 느리고 고집스럽게 열리면서 그 경첩을 갈아 가루로 만들어 버린다고 쳐 봐. 그렇다 해도 그 소리가, 랄프가 거칠고 쓰디쓴 목소리로 내뱉은 이 말보다는 듣기 좋았을 거야. 만탈리니 씨조차 그 영향을 느끼고는, 깜짝 놀라 몸을 돌리며 외쳤어. \"이 무슨 빌어먹게 끔찍한 까마귀 울음소리람!\"\n\n\"제발 만탈리니 씨가 하는 말엔 신경 쓰지 마라.\" 그의 아내가 케이트에게 말했어.\n\n\"네, 안 써요, 부인.\" 케이트가 조용한 경멸을 담아 말했어.\n\n\"만탈리니 씨는 그 어떤 여공에 대해서도 아무것도 몰라.\" 부인이 남편을 쳐다보면서 케이트에게 말을 이었어. \"혹시 봤다 해도, 거리에서 일하러 오가는 걸 봤을 뿐이지 여기서 본 게 아니란다. 이 방에 들어와 본 적도 없어. 내가 허락하질 않으니까. 그래, 너는 몇 시간이나 일하는 데 익숙하니?\"\n\n\"아직 일이라곤 전혀 해 본 적이 없어요, 부인.\" 케이트가 낮은 목소리로 대답했어.\n\n\"그러니 이제 더 잘 배울 거요.\" 이 고백이 협상에 해가 될까 봐 랄프가 한마디 끼워 넣었어.\n\n\"그러길 바라요.\" 만탈리니 부인이 받아 말했어. \"우리 일과는 아홉 시부터 아홉 시까지란다. 일이 아주 밀릴 땐 추가로 일을 하는데, 그건 초과 근무로 따로 삯을 쳐 주지.\"\n\n케이트는 알아들었고 만족한다는 뜻으로 고개를 숙였어.\n\n\"끼니는,\" 만탈리니 부인이 말을 이었어. \"그러니까 점심과 차는 여기서 먹게 될 거야. 삯은 일주일에 다섯에서 일곱 실링쯤 될 것 같구나. 하지만 네 솜씨를 봐야 그 점은 확실히 말해 줄 수 있겠지.\"\n\n케이트는 다시 고개를 숙였어.\n\n\"올 마음이 됐다면,\" 만탈리니 부인이 말했어. \"월요일 아침 아홉 시 정각에 시작하는 게 좋겠다. 그러면 작업반장인 내그 양한테 일러서 처음엔 쉬운 일부터 시켜 보게 하마. 더 하실 말씀 있나요, 니클비 씨?\"\n\n\"더는 없소, 부인.\" 랄프가 일어서며 대답했어.\n\n\"그럼 이게 다인 것 같네요.\" 부인이 말했어. 이렇게 자연스레 결말에 이르자, 그녀는 어서 가고 싶다는 듯 문을 바라봤어. 그래도 만탈리니 씨한테 두 사람을 아래층까지 배웅하는 영광을 혼자 차지하게 두긴 싫은 듯, 그래도 머뭇거렸지. 랄프가 지체 없이 떠나 줘서 그녀의 난처함을 덜어 줬어. 만탈리니 부인은 왜 한 번도 보러 안 오시느냐고 상냥하게 자꾸 물었지. 그리고 만탈리니 씨는 두 사람을 따라 내려가면서, 케이트가 돌아봐 주길 바라는 마음에 계단에 대고 청산유수로 욕을 퍼부었어. 하지만 그 바람은 끝내 이루어질 운명이 아니었지.\n\n\"자!\" 거리로 나오자 랄프가 말했어. \"이제 네 앞가림은 마련됐다.\"\n\n케이트가 다시 감사 인사를 하려 했지만, 그가 막았어.\n\n\"내 생각엔 말이다.\" 그가 말했어. \"네 어머니를 시골 어디 좋은 데 모실까 했었다.\" (그는 콘월 경계에 있는 어느 자선원에 한 사람을 들여보낼 권리가 있었는데, 그게 여러 번 머릿속에 떠올랐던 거야.) \"하지만 너희가 같이 있고 싶다니, 어머니한테는 다른 걸 해 줘야겠지. 네 어머니한테 돈이 좀 있느냐?\"\n\n\"아주 조금요.\" 케이트가 대답했어.\n\n\"조금이라도 아껴 쓰면 오래 가는 법이지.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"집세 안 내고 살면서 그 돈을 얼마나 오래 버틸 수 있는지 봐야지. 토요일에 그 셋방을 비우는 거지?\"\n\n\"삼촌이 그렇게 하라고 하셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"그래. 내 소유인데 비어 있는 집이 하나 있다. 세가 나갈 때까지 거기 너희를 들여보내 주마. 그리고 그때 가서 달리 뾰족한 수가 없으면, 어쩌면 또 다른 집이 생기겠지. 너희는 거기서 살아야 한다.\"\n\n\"여기서 먼가요, 삼촌?\" 케이트가 물었어.\n\n\"꽤 멀지.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"이 도시의 다른 구역, 이스트엔드에 있어. 하지만 토요일 다섯 시에 내 서기를 너희한테 보내 거기로 데려가게 하마. 잘 가라. 길은 알지? 곧장 가면 된다.\"\n\n랄프는 조카의 손을 차갑게 한번 잡아 주고는, 리젠트 거리 꼭대기에서 케이트와 헤어졌어. 그러고는 돈벌이 궁리에 골몰하며 샛길로 접어들어 갔지. 케이트는 슬픈 마음으로 스트랜드에 있는 셋방을 향해 터덜터덜 돌아갔어.\n\n# CHAPTER 11\n\n뉴먼 노그스가 니클비 부인과 케이트를 도시에 있는 새 거처로 안내하다 케이트의 머릿속을 채운 생각들은, 집으로 돌아가는 길에서 우울하기 짝이 없었다. 그날 아침에 겪은 일들을 떠올리면 그럴 만도 했지. 삼촌의 태도는 처음부터 그녀가 품게 된 의심이나 불안을 걷어 줄 만한 게 아니었고, 마담 만탈리니의 가게를 흘끗 본 것도 조금도 마음을 북돋아 주지 못했어. 그래서 케이트는 무거운 마음으로, 곧 시작될 새 일자리를 온갖 어두운 예감과 걱정 속에서 떠올렸던 거야.\n\n어머니의 위로가 케이트를 한결 즐겁고 부러운 마음 상태로 되돌려 줄 수 있었다면, 그런 효과를 낼 위로는 차고 넘쳤어. 케이트가 집에 닿을 무렵, 이 마음씨 좋은 부인은 상당한 재산을 모은 옷가게 주인 두 명의 실제 사례를 떠올려 냈거든. 그 재산을 전부 장사로 벌었는지, 아니면 처음부터 밑천이 있었는지, 그것도 아니면 운이 좋아서 좋은 데로 시집을 갔는지는 정확히 기억나지 않았지만 말이야. 그래도 부인은 아주 논리적으로 이렇게 말했어. 그 장사 길에서 아무것도 없이 시작해 한밑천 잡은 젊은 사람이 분명히 있었을 테고, 그게 사실이라면 케이트라고 못할 게 뭐냐는 거였지. 이 작은 회의의 일원이던 미스 라 크리비가 슬며시 의심을 끼워 넣어 봤어. 보통 사람의 한평생 안에 미스 니클비가 그렇게 행복한 결말에 다다를 가능성이 있겠느냐고 말이야. 하지만 부인은 그 문제를 단번에 잠재워 버렸어. 자기는 이 일에 예감이 든다는 거였지. 일종의 예지력 같은 건데, 부인은 돌아가신 니클비 씨와 다툴 때마다 이걸로 마무리를 짓곤 했어. 그리고 열 번 가운데 아홉 번하고도 사 분의 삼은 틀린 쪽으로 결판이 났지.\n\n\"옷 만드는 일이 건강에 안 좋을까 봐 걱정이에요.\" 미스 라 크리비가 말했어. \"제가 처음 그림을 시작했을 때 젊은 옷가게 아가씨 셋을 모델로 앉혀 본 적이 있는데, 다들 얼굴이 아주 창백하고 병약했던 게 기억나거든요.\"\n\n\"어머! 그게 꼭 그런 건 아니에요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"내가 어제 일처럼 또렷이 기억하는데, 빨간 망토가 유행이던 때에 특별히 추천받은 사람한테 빨간 망토를 하나 맡긴 적이 있거든요. 그런데 그 사람 얼굴이 아주 빨갰어요. 정말이지 아주 빨갰다니까요.\"\n\n\"술을 마셔서 그랬나 보죠.\" 미스 라 크리비가 넌지시 말했어.\n\n\"그건 어땠는지 모르겠지만,\" 니클비 부인이 대꾸했어. \"얼굴이 아주 빨갰던 건 분명해요. 그러니 댁 말은 아무 소용없는 거죠.\"\n\n이런 식으로, 또 이만큼 강력한 논리로, 이 훌륭한 부인은 아침에 세운 새 계획에 들이대는 자잘한 반론을 하나하나 받아쳤어. 행복한 니클비 부인 같으니! 어떤 계획이든 새롭기만 하면, 부인의 머릿속에는 번쩍번쩍 광을 내고 금칠을 한 반짝이는 장난감처럼 다가왔거든.\n\n이 문제가 정리되자, 케이트는 빈집에 관한 삼촌의 뜻을 전했어. 니클비 부인도 똑같이 선뜻 동의했지. 그러면서 자기다운 말을 했어. 날이 좋은 저녁이면 서쪽 끝까지 걸어가 딸을 집으로 데려오는 게 즐거운 소일거리가 되겠다는 거였지. 그리고 역시나 자기답게, 일 년 중 거의 매주 비 오는 밤이나 궂은 날씨를 만나야 한다는 사실은 까맣게 잊고 있었어.\n\n\"정말, 정말로 헤어지기가 아쉬워요, 친절하신 분.\" 케이트가 말했어. 이 가난한 세밀화가의 따뜻한 마음씨가 케이트에게 깊은 인상을 남겼던 거야.\n\n\"그래도 그렇게 쉽게 날 떼어 내진 못할걸요.\" 미스 라 크리비가 짐짓 기운을 내며 대꾸했어. \"아주 자주 만날 거고, 찾아가서 어떻게 지내는지 들을 거예요. 그리고 온 런던, 아니 이 넓은 세상을 통틀어 아가씨가 잘되기를 바라는 마음이 하나도 없다 해도, 밤낮으로 그걸 빌어 주는 외로운 여자가 한 명은 있을 거예요.\"\n\n이 말과 함께, 런던의 수호신 고그를 다 품고도 매고그한테까지 나눠 줄 만큼 큰 마음을 가진 이 가엾은 사람은, 갖가지 유난스러운 표정을 잔뜩 지었어. 그 표정들을 상아나 캔버스에 옮길 수만 있었다면 두둑한 재산을 마련할 수 있었을 텐데 말이야. 그러고는 한쪽 구석에 앉아, 자기 표현으로 \"한바탕 시원하게 우는\" 시간을 가졌지.\n\n하지만 아무리 울고, 떠들고, 바라고, 두려워해 봐도, 그 두려운 토요일 오후를 막을 수는 없었어. 뉴먼 노그스도 마찬가지였지. 그는 시간을 딱 맞춰 절뚝거리며 문 앞까지 와서는 열쇠 구멍으로 향긋한 진 냄새를 훅 풍겼어. 마침 그때, 동네 교회 시계들 가운데 서로 시간이 맞는 것들이 다섯 시를 쳤지. 뉴먼은 마지막 종소리를 기다렸다가 문을 두드렸어.\n\n\"랄프 니클비 씨가 보내서 왔습니다.\" 위층에 올라온 뉴먼이 가능한 한 짧게 용건을 알렸어.\n\n\"금방 준비할게요.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"옮길 짐은 많지 않은데, 그래도 마차가 있어야 할 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"제가 한 대 잡아 오죠.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어.\n\n\"정말 그렇게 수고하실 것 없어요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"잡아 오겠습니다.\" 뉴먼이 말했어.\n\n\"그런 건 생각도 못 하게 하겠어요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"어쩔 수 없으실걸요.\" 뉴먼이 말했어.\n\n\"어쩔 수 없다니요!\"\n\n\"네, 오는 길에 생각은 했는데, 준비가 안 되셨을까 봐 안 잡아 왔거든요. 저는 여러 가지를 생각합니다. 그건 누구도 못 막죠.\"\n\n\"아, 알겠어요, 노그스 씨.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"생각이야 물론 자유죠. 누구든 자기 생각은 자기 거니까요.\"\n\n\"어떤 사람들 뜻대로 됐다면 그렇지도 않았을 겁니다.\" 뉴먼이 중얼거렸어.\n\n\"맞아요, 정말 그렇지 않겠죠, 노그스 씨. 참 맞는 말이에요.\" 니클비 부인이 맞장구쳤어. \"어떤 사람들은 정말이지 그렇게나, 그런데 주인 어른은 잘 계세요?\"\n\n뉴먼은 케이트에게 의미심장한 눈길을 던지고는, 대답 마지막 말에 잔뜩 힘을 주어 이렇게 말했어. 랄프 니클비 씨는 잘 계시고, \"사랑\"을 전한다고 하셨다고.\n\n\"정말 그분께 너무나 감사하네요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"네, 그러시겠죠.\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"그렇게 전하겠습니다.\"\n\n뉴먼 노그스라는 사람은 한 번 보고 나면 잘못 알아볼 일이 없었어. 그래서 케이트는 그의 독특한 태도에 끌려 — 그런데 이번에는 말투가 무뚝뚝한데도 어딘가 정중하고 심지어 섬세한 데가 있었지 — 그를 좀 더 자세히 들여다봤어. 그러자 전에 그 이상한 모습을 흘끗 본 적이 있다는 게 떠올랐어.\n\n\"제 궁금증을 너그러이 봐주세요.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"그런데 제 오빠가 요크셔로 떠나던 날 아침, 마차 마당에서 당신을 보지 않았나요?\"\n\n뉴먼은 니클비 부인을 슬쩍 곁눈질하더니, 낯빛 하나 안 변하고 \"아니요\"라고 했어.\n\n\"아니라고요!\" 케이트가 외쳤어. \"어디서든 분명 당신이라고 했을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"잘못 보신 겁니다.\" 뉴먼이 대꾸했어. \"저는 삼 주 만에 처음 밖에 나왔어요. 통풍을 앓았거든요.\"\n\n뉴먼은 통풍 환자로는 도무지 보이지 않았어. 케이트도 그렇게 생각하지 않을 수 없었지. 하지만 대화는 곧 끊겼어. 니클비 부인이 노그스 씨가 감기 들까 봐 문을 닫아야 한다고 우긴 데다, 그가 또 발작을 일으킬까 봐 하녀를 시켜 마차를 불러오게 하겠다고 고집을 부렸거든. 두 가지 다 뉴먼은 따를 수밖에 없었어. 이윽고 마차가 왔고, 슬픈 작별 인사를 여러 번 나눈 뒤에, 미스 라 크리비가 보도를 이리저리 부산히 오가는 통에 노란 터번이 지나가던 행인 몇몇과 거세게 부딪치는 소동까지 겪은 끝에, 그것이 — 그러니까 터번이 아니라 마차가 — 다시 움직였어. 두 여자와 짐을 안에 싣고서 말이야. 그리고 뉴먼은, 그러다 죽는다는 니클비 부인의 거듭된 장담에도 아랑곳없이 마부 옆 좌석에 올라탔지.\n\n그들은 강가를 따라 내려가 시티 안으로 들어갔어. 그 시간에는 온갖 종류의 탈것이 거리에 가득해서, 길고 아주 느린 마차 여행 끝에 템스 거리의 크고 오래되고 거무튀튀한 집 앞에 멈춰 섰지. 그 집은 문이며 창이며 진흙이 잔뜩 튀어 있어서, 몇 년이나 사람이 안 산 것처럼 보였어.\n\n뉴먼은 모자에서 꺼낸 열쇠로 이 버려진 저택의 문을 열었어. 참고로 그는 주머니가 너덜너덜해진 탓에 모든 걸 모자 안에 넣고 다녔거든. 돈이 있었다면 십중팔구 돈도 거기 넣어 다녔을 거야. 마차를 돌려보낸 뒤, 그는 앞장서서 저택 안으로 들어갔어.\n\n정말로 낡고 음침하고 시커먼 집이었어. 한때 그렇게 활기와 사업으로 북적이던 방들이 이제는 침침하고 어두웠지. 집 뒤로는 템스강과 맞닿은 부두가 있었어. 텅 빈 개집 하나, 짐승 뼈 몇 개, 부서진 쇠테 조각들, 낡은 통 널빤지가 여기저기 흩어져 있었지만, 살아 움직이는 건 아무것도 없었어. 그건 차갑고 조용한 쇠락의 풍경이었지.\n\n\"이 집은 사람을 짓누르고 으스스하게 만들어요.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"마치 무슨 저주라도 내린 것 같아요. 제가 미신을 믿는 사람이었다면, 이 낡은 벽 안에서 무슨 끔찍한 죄가 저질러졌고, 그 뒤로 이곳이 한 번도 흥하지 못한 거라고 거의 믿어 버렸을 거예요. 얼마나 험상궂고 컴컴해 보이는지!\"\n\n\"얘야, 세상에.\" 니클비 부인이 대꾸했어. \"그런 식으로 말하지 마라. 안 그러면 무서워서 죽겠다.\"\n\n\"그냥 제 어리석은 상상이에요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 억지로 미소를 지으며 말했어.\n\n\"그래, 그럼 얘야, 그 어리석은 상상은 너 혼자 간직하고, 거기 짝지어 줄 내 어리석은 상상까지 깨우지는 말아 다오.\" 니클비 부인이 받아쳤어. \"왜 이런 걸 미리 생각 안 했니, 넌 어쩜 그렇게 조심성이 없는지. 미스 라 크리비한테 같이 있어 달라고 부탁하든가, 개라도 한 마리 빌리든가, 방법이야 수천 가지였잖니. 그런데 늘 이런 식이었어, 네 가엾은 아버지 때도 똑같았지. 내가 모든 걸 다 생각하지 않으면—\" 이게 니클비 부인이 으레 늘어놓는 한탄의 시작이었어. 딱히 누구를 향한 것도 아닌 복잡한 문장이 열 개 남짓 줄줄이 이어지는 그런 한탄 말이야. 부인은 이번에도 숨이 다 찰 때까지 그걸 쏟아 냈지.\n\n뉴먼은 이런 말을 못 들은 척하고, 두 사람을 데리고 이층의 방 두 개로 앞장서 갔어. 그래도 사람이 지낼 만하게 어느 정도 손을 써 둔 방이었지. 한 방에는 의자 몇 개와 탁자, 낡은 벽난로 깔개, 색 바랜 모직 천이 있었고, 벽난로에는 불을 피울 준비가 다 되어 있었어. 다른 방에는 오래된 천막식 침대 하나와 침실 가구 몇 점이 단출하게 놓여 있었지.\n\n\"얘야, 자,\" 니클비 부인이 기뻐하려 애쓰며 말했어. \"네 삼촌이 이렇게 사려 깊고 자상하지 않니? 그분이 신경을 안 써 주셨더라면, 우리는 어제 산 침대 말고는 누울 데도 없었을 거야!\"\n\n\"정말 친절하시네요.\" 케이트가 방 안을 둘러보며 대답했어.\n\n뉴먼 노그스는 두 사람이 보고 있는 그 낡은 가구를 자기가 다락과 지하실에서 일일이 뒤져 찾아냈다는 말은 하지 않았어. 선반에 놓인 반 페니어치 차 마실 우유를 자기가 받아 두었다는 것도, 화로 위 녹슨 주전자에 물을 채워 둔 것도, 부두에서 나뭇조각을 주워 모은 것도, 석탄을 얻어 온 것도 말하지 않았지. 하지만 랄프 니클비가 이렇게 하라고 시켰다는 듯한 생각이 어찌나 우습던지, 그는 열 손가락을 차례로 우두둑 꺾지 않을 수 없었어. 그 모습에 니클비 부인은 처음엔 흠칫 놀랐지만, 어쩐지 통풍과 관련이 있겠거니 짐작하고는 아무 말도 하지 않았어.\n\n\"이제 더 붙잡을 일은 없을 것 같네요.\" 케이트가 말했어.\n\n\"제가 해 드릴 일이 더 없을까요?\" 뉴먼이 물었어.\n\n\"없어요, 고맙습니다.\" 케이트가 대답했어.\n\n\"얘야, 노그스 씨가 우리 건강을 위해 한잔하고 싶어 하실지도 모르잖니.\" 니클비 부인이 손지갑을 뒤적여 잔돈을 찾으며 말했어.\n\n\"엄마, 제 생각엔,\" 케이트가 머뭇거리며, 뉴먼이 고개를 돌린 걸 보고 말했어. \"그걸 건네면 저분 마음이 상할 것 같아요.\"\n\n뉴먼 노그스는 보기엔 초라한 신세였지만, 그런 사람보다는 훨씬 신사답게 케이트에게 고개를 숙였어. 그러고는 가슴에 손을 얹고, 무슨 말을 하려고 애쓰지만 뭐라 해야 할지 몰라 망설이는 사람처럼 잠시 멈췄다가, 방을 나갔어.\n\n육중한 현관문이 걸쇠에 닫히면서 그 거슬리는 메아리가 집 안에 음산하게 울려 퍼지자, 케이트는 그를 다시 불러 잠깐만 더 있어 달라고 부탁하고 싶은 마음이 반쯤 들었어. 하지만 자기가 무서워한다는 걸 털어놓기가 부끄러웠지. 그리고 뉴먼 노그스는 이미 집으로 가는 길에 올라 있었어.\n\n# CHAPTER 12\n\n이건 패니 스퀴어스 양에게는 아주 다행스러운 일이었어. 작은 다과회가 있던 그날 밤, 훌륭하신 아버지가 집에 돌아왔을 때 그는 이른바 술꾼들 말로 '갈 데까지 간' 상태였거든. 그래서 딸 얼굴에 또렷이 드러나 있던, 속이 잔뜩 뒤틀린 온갖 기색을 알아챌 처지가 못 되었지. 그런데 그는 술이 들어가면 좀 거칠고 시비를 거는 성미였어. 그러니 이 일로든 아니면 무슨 엉뚱한 트집으로든 딸과 한바탕 싸웠을지도 몰라. 다만 이 아가씨가 칭찬할 만큼 앞을 내다보고 영리하게 굴어서, 일부러 아이 하나를 안 재우고 깨워 두었지. 착하신 아버지의 분노가 처음 터질 때 그 화살을 대신 맞으라고 말이야. 그래서 그 화는 발길질이며 주먹질로 실컷 풀리고 나서야 가라앉았어. 겨우 잠자리로 가도록 달랠 수 있을 만큼 말이야. 그는 장화도 안 벗고, 우산도 옆구리에 낀 채로 자러 갔지.\n\n굶주린 하녀는 늘 하던 대로 스퀴어스 양 방으로 따라가서 머리를 곱슬곱슬 말아 주고, 단장에 필요한 자질구레한 시중을 들고, 그러면서 할 수 있는 만큼 아첨을 늘어놓았어. 다 그러려고 따라간 거였거든. 스퀴어스 양은 게으르기로 치면 (게다가 허영심 많고 경박하기로도) 귀부인이 되고도 남을 정도였어. 단지 신분과 지위라는 멋대로 정해진 차별 때문에 귀부인이 못 됐을 뿐이었지.\n\n\"아가씨, 오늘 밤은 머리가 어쩜 이렇게 예쁘게 말리는지 몰라요!\" 하녀가 말했어. \"이걸 다시 빗어 푸는 게 정말 아깝고 죄스러울 지경이라니까요!\"\n\n\"입 다물어!\" 스퀴어스 양이 성을 내며 대꾸했어.\n\n하녀는 그동안 겪을 만큼 겪어서, 스퀴어스 양이 짜증을 부려도 조금도 놀라지 않았어. 그날 저녁에 무슨 일이 있었는지 어렴풋이 눈치챈 그녀는, 비위 맞추는 방식을 바꿔서 에둘러 가는 쪽으로 작전을 폈지.\n\n\"근데요, 아가씨, 절 죽이신대도 이 말은 안 할 수가 없네요.\" 하녀가 말했어. \"오늘 밤 프라이스 양처럼 천박해 보이는 사람은 난생처음 봤어요.\"\n\n스퀴어스 양은 한숨을 쉬더니, 마음을 가다듬고 귀를 기울였어.\n\n\"제가 이런 말 하는 게 아주 잘못인 줄은 알아요, 아가씨.\" 하녀가 말을 이었어. 자기 말이 먹혀드는 게 보여서 신이 난 거야. \"프라이스 양이 아가씨 친구고 뭐고 다 그런데도요. 그래도 그 사람은 어찌나 차려입고, 어떻게든 눈에 띄려고 그렇게 굴던지요. 그러니까, 아휴, 글쎄, 사람들이 제 꼴을 좀 봐야 한다니까요!\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말이야, 핍?\" 스퀴어스 양이 제 작은 거울을 들여다보며 물었어. 우리 대부분이 그렇듯, 그녀도 거울 속에서 자기 자신이 아니라 제 머릿속에 그려진 어떤 흐뭇한 모습을 비춰 보고 있었지. \"무슨 그런 말을 다 해!\"\n\n\"무슨 말이라뇨, 아가씨! 그 사람이 머리를 쳐드는 꼴을 보면 수고양이도 프랑스어 문법을 떠들 지경인걸요.\" 하녀가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"하긴 머리를 쳐들긴 하더라.\" 스퀴어스 양이 딴 데 정신이 팔린 듯한 투로 말했어.\n\n\"어찌나 잘난 척에다, 게다가 또 어찌나, 정말로, 정말로 못생겼는지요.\" 하녀가 말했어.\n\n\"가엾은 틸다!\" 스퀴어스 양이 측은하다는 듯 한숨을 쉬었어.\n\n\"그러면서 늘 칭찬받으려고 그렇게 애를 쓴다니까요.\" 하녀가 이어 갔어. \"어휴, 세상에! 정말이지 점잖지 못해요.\"\n\n\"네가 그런 식으로 말하는 건 두고 볼 수 없어, 핍.\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어. \"틸다 친구들이 천한 사람들이잖아. 그러니 그 애가 더 나은 걸 모른다면, 그건 그 사람들 탓이지 그 애 탓이 아니야.\"\n\n\"그야 그렇지만요, 아가씨.\" 피비가 말했어. '핍'이라는 이름은 그 애를 한 단계 낮춰 부르는 줄임말이었지. \"그 사람도 친구 하나만 본보기로 삼으면, 아휴! 자기가 얼마나 그른지만 알고, 아가씨를 보고 똑바로 고치기만 하면, 머지않아 얼마나 괜찮은 아가씨가 되겠어요!\"\n\n\"핍,\" 스퀴어스 양이 거드름을 피우며 받았어. \"이런 비교를 듣는 건 나한테 어울리지 않아. 그러면 틸다가 거칠고 점잖지 못한 사람처럼 보이잖니. 게다가 그런 말을 듣고 있는 건 친구로서 의리 없는 짓 같아. 그 얘기는 그만뒀으면 좋겠다, 핍. 그래도 이 말만은 해야겠어. 틸다 프라이스가 누군가를 본보기로 삼는다면 말이야, 꼭 나라는 건 아니지만 —\"\n\n\"아휴, 아니에요. 아가씨죠.\" 핍이 끼어들었어.\n\n\"그래, 나라고 치자, 핍, 네가 굳이 그렇다면.\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어. \"이 말만은 해야겠어. 그 애가 그런다면, 훨씬 더 나아질 텐데 말이야.\"\n\n\"다른 누구도 그렇게 생각한다니까요. 제가 한참 잘못 본 게 아니라면요.\" 하녀가 알쏭달쏭하게 말했어.\n\n\"그게 무슨 말이야?\" 스퀴어스 양이 따져 물었어.\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마세요, 아가씨.\" 하녀가 대꾸했어. \"전 제가 아는 걸 알 뿐이에요. 그게 다예요.\"\n\n\"핍,\" 스퀴어스 양이 자못 비장하게 말했어. \"똑똑히 설명을 해 봐. 이 어두운 비밀이 대체 뭐야? 말해.\"\n\n\"그럼 정 그러시다면, 아가씨, 이런 거예요.\" 하녀가 말했어. \"존 브라우디 씨도 아가씨랑 똑같이 생각해요. 그리고 그분이 갈 데까지 가서 제대로 못 할 처지만 아니었으면요, 프라이스 양은 떼어 버리고 스퀴어스 양이랑 어떻게 해 보고 싶어 안달했을걸요.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 스퀴어스 양이 잔뜩 위엄을 부리며 두 손을 맞잡고 외쳤어. \"이게 무슨 소리야?\"\n\n\"진실이에요, 아가씨, 진실 말고는 아무것도 아니랍니다.\" 약삭빠른 핍이 대꾸했어.\n\n\"이 무슨 처지람!\" 스퀴어스 양이 부르짖었어. \"나도 모르는 새에 우리 틸다의 평화와 행복을 망쳐 버릴 지경이라니. 대체 무슨 까닭으로 남자들이, 내가 좋아하든 말든 나한테 반해서, 정해 놓은 약혼녀까지 버리고 나한테로 오는 걸까?\"\n\n\"어쩔 수 없으니까 그렇죠, 아가씨.\" 하녀가 대꾸했어. \"이유는 뻔하잖아요.\" (스퀴어스 양이 그 이유라면야, 정말 뻔하긴 했지.)\n\n\"다시는 그런 말 내 귀에 들리게 하지 마.\" 스퀴어스 양이 쏘아붙였어. \"절대로! 알겠어? 틸다 프라이스도 흠은 있어. 흠이 많지. 그래도 난 그 애가 잘되길 바라. 무엇보다 그 애가 시집가길 바란다고. 그 애 결점들의 성질로 봐서, 되도록 빨리 시집가는 게 더없이 바람직하다고 생각하거든. 아니야, 핍. 그 애가 브라우디 씨를 갖게 둬. 난 그 사람이 가엾을지도 모르지, 딱한 양반. 하지만 난 틸다를 무척 아끼니까, 그저 그 애가 내 생각보다는 더 나은 아내가 되기만 바랄 뿐이야.\"\n\n이렇게 한바탕 감정을 쏟아 낸 뒤, 스퀴어스 양은 잠자리에 들었어.\n\n심술이란 건 짧은 단어야. 하지만 이 말은 이 언어의 그 어떤 긴 단어 못지않게, 이상하게 뒤엉킨 감정과 어긋난 마음들의 뒤범벅을 담고 있지. 스퀴어스 양은 가슴 깊은 곳에서 잘 알고 있었어. 그 가련한 하녀가 한 말이 죄다 새빨갛고 천박한 거짓 아첨이라는 걸 말이야. 그 하녀 자신이 아는 만큼이나 똑똑히 알았지. 그래도 미운 프라이스 양에게 심술을 좀 풀어 보고, 그 약점이며 흠을 가엾어하는 척해 보는 것만으로도, 비록 의지가지없는 하녀 하나 앞에서뿐이었지만, 그건 그 모든 게 복음서의 진리였던 것만큼이나 화를 푸는 데 큰 위안이 됐어. 아니, 그 이상이었지. 우리는 그 설득의 힘을 우리 자신에게 발휘할 때 어찌나 놀라운지, 스퀴어스 양은 존 브라우디의 손을 그토록 고결하게 마다하고 나니 제 마음이 아주 드높고 위대해진 것 같았어. 그래서 어떤 거룩한 평온과 고요함을 띠고 연적을 내려다보았지. 그게 헝클어진 마음을 달래는 데 엄청난 효험이 있었어.\n\n이렇게 마음이 흐뭇해진 덕에 화해도 어느 정도 이루어졌어. 다음 날 현관문에서 노크 소리가 나고 방앗간 집 딸이 왔다고 알려 왔을 때, 스퀴어스 양은 보기에 더없이 아름다운, 그야말로 자비로운 마음가짐으로 응접실로 갔거든.\n\n\"있잖아, 패니.\" 방앗간 집 딸이 말했어. \"어젯밤에 우리가 좀 말다툼을 하긴 했지만, 그래도 이렇게 너 보러 왔어.\"\n\n\"난 네 못된 성미가 가엾을 뿐이야, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 대꾸했어. \"그래도 난 앙심 같은 건 없어. 난 그런 거 넘어선 사람이거든.\"\n\n\"성내지 마, 패니.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어. \"네가 들으면 분명 좋아할 얘기를 해 주러 왔단 말이야.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔데, 틸다?\" 스퀴어스 양이 입술을 앙다물고 따져 물었어. 땅이든 하늘이든 불이든 물이든, 세상 그 무엇도 그녀에게 한 줄기 만족조차 줄 수 없다는 듯한 표정으로 말이야.\n\n\"이거야.\" 프라이스 양이 받았어. \"어젯밤 우리가 여기서 나간 뒤에, 존이랑 내가 무지하게 싸웠거든.\"\n\n\"그건 안 반가운데.\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어. 그래도 빙긋이 미소가 풀려 나오긴 했지.\n\n\"어머! 네가 그걸 반가워할 만큼 나쁜 애라고는 생각 안 해.\" 친구가 대꾸했어. \"그게 아니야.\"\n\n\"아!\" 스퀴어스 양이 다시 우울해지며 말했어. \"계속해 봐.\"\n\n\"한참을 옥신각신하면서 다시는 서로 안 볼 거라고 하다가,\" 프라이스 양이 말을 이었어. \"결국 화해했어. 그래서 오늘 아침에 존이 가서 우리 이름을 적어 냈어. 이번 주 일요일에 처음으로 결혼 예고가 붙는 거야. 그러니까 우린 삼 주 뒤에 결혼해. 그러니 너도 드레스 맞추라고 미리 일러두는 거야.\"\n\n이 소식에는 쓴 쓸개즙과 단 꿀이 뒤섞여 있었어. 친구가 그렇게 곧 결혼한다는 게 쓸개즙이었고, 그 애가 니콜라스한테 진지한 속셈이 없다는 게 분명해진 건 꿀이었지. 따지고 보면 단맛이 쓴맛을 훨씬 앞섰어. 그래서 스퀴어스 양은 드레스를 맞추겠다고 했어. 그리고 틸다가 행복하길 바란다고 했지. 하지만 동시에, 그건 모를 일이고 너무 큰 기대는 걸지 말라고 했어. 남자란 묘한 생물이고, 결혼한 여자들 가운데 아주 많은 이들이 무척 비참해져서 다시 처녀로 돌아가고 싶어 한다고 진심으로 말이야. 이 위로에 스퀴어스 양은 다른 위로들도 보탰어. 친구의 기운을 북돋고 마음을 즐겁게 해 주려고 한, 그것들도 똑같이 계산된 말들이었지.\n\n\"근데 있잖아, 패니.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어. \"젊은 니클비 씨 얘기를 너랑 한두 마디 나누고 싶은데.\"\n\n\"그 사람은 나랑 아무 사이도 아니야.\" 스퀴어스 양이 신경질적인 기색을 보이며 말을 끊었어. \"난 그 사람 너무 경멸한다고!\"\n\n\"어머, 너 진심 아니지? 그럴 리 없어.\" 친구가 대꾸했어. \"솔직히 말해 봐, 패니. 지금도 그 사람 좋아하잖아?\"\n\n스퀴어스 양은 곧이곧대로 대답은 않고, 별안간 분에 받친 눈물을 쏟아 내며 발작하듯 외쳐 댔어. 자기는 가엾고, 버림받고, 비참한 신세라고 말이야.\n\n\"난 모두가 다 미워.\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어. \"다들 죽어 버렸으면 좋겠어. 정말이야.\"\n\n\"저런, 저런.\" 프라이스 양이 이렇게 사람 미워하는 마음을 털어놓는 데 적잖이 마음이 움직여서 말했어. \"너 진심 아니지? 분명 그럴 거야.\"\n\n\"아니, 진심이야.\" 스퀴어스 양이 손수건에 매듭을 꽉꽉 지으며, 이를 악물고 대꾸했어. \"그리고 나도 죽었으면 좋겠어. 자!\"\n\n\"어머! 오 분만 지나면 생각이 완전히 달라질걸.\" 마틸다가 말했어. \"그렇게 굴면서 너 자신을 괴롭히느니, 그 사람을 다시 받아 주는 게 훨씬 낫잖아. 자, 이제 그 사람을 너 혼자 차지해서 사이좋게, 같이 어울리고, 사랑도 나누고, 즐겁게 지내는 게 훨씬 좋지 않겠어?\"\n\n\"그게 나을지도 모르지.\" 스퀴어스 양이 흐느꼈어. \"아휴! 틸다, 어쩜 그렇게 비열하고 떳떳지 못하게 굴 수가 있었니! 누가 나한테 말해 줬어도 난 네가 그랬을 거라곤 안 믿었을 거야.\"\n\n\"어머나!\" 프라이스 양이 까르르 웃으며 외쳤어. \"꼭 내가 누구라도 죽인 줄 알겠네.\"\n\n\"그거나 거의 마찬가지지.\" 스퀴어스 양이 격하게 말했어.\n\n\"이게 다, 내가 사람들이 친절하게 대해 줄 만큼 인물이 반반하다는 이유 때문이잖아.\" 프라이스 양이 외쳤어. \"얼굴이야 자기가 만드는 게 아니지. 내 얼굴이 잘난 게 내 탓이 아니듯, 남들 얼굴이 못난 것도 그 사람들 탓이 아니야.\"\n\n\"입 닥쳐.\" 스퀴어스 양이 더없이 날카로운 목소리로 빽 소리쳤어. \"안 그러면 너 한 대 갈겨 버릴 거야, 틸다. 그러고 나면 내가 미안해지겠지만!\"\n\n이쯤 되니 두 아가씨의 성미가 저마다 대화의 어조에 어느 정도 물든 건 새삼 말할 것도 없지. 그 바람에 말다툼에 인신공격이 약간 끼어들었어. 사실 그 싸움은 사소하게 시작했다가 꽤 높이 치달았고, 아주 험악한 빛깔을 띠어 갔지. 그러다가 두 사람 다 펑펑 눈물을 쏟으며 동시에 외쳐 댔어. 자기가 그런 식으로 말을 들을 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐다고 말이야. 이 외침이 항변으로 이어졌고, 항변은 차츰 해명으로 번졌어. 그래서 결국 둘은 서로의 품에 안겨 영원한 우정을 맹세했지. 이번 일로 그 똑같이 감동적인 의식을 일 년 안에 쉰두 번째 되풀이하게 된 셈이었어.\n\n이렇게 더없는 화목이 되살아나자, 자연스레 이야기가 이어졌어. 프라이스 양이 거룩한 혼인의 길로 들어서는 데 빼놓을 수 없는 옷가지가 몇 벌이며 어떤 것들이냐 하는 거였지. 스퀴어스 양은, 방앗간 주인이 마련해 줄 수 있거나 마련해 주려는 것보다 훨씬 많은 옷이 반드시 필요하며, 체면상 그걸 빼놓을 수도 없다는 걸 또렷이 보여 줬어. 그러더니 슬쩍 화제를 돌려 자기 옷장 얘기로 끌고 갔어. 그러고는 그 주된 멋들을 한참 늘어놓더니, 친구를 위층으로 데려가서 직접 구경시켜 줬지. 서랍 두 개와 벽장 속 보물들을 죄다 펼쳐 보이고, 자잘한 물건들은 다 입어 보고 나니, 이제 프라이스 양이 집으로 돌아갈 때가 됐어. 프라이스 양이 드레스마다 황홀해하고, 새로 산 분홍색 스카프에는 감탄해서 말문이 막혀 버렸으니, 스퀴어스 양은 아주 기분이 좋아져서 같이 가 주는 게 즐거우니 가는 길을 얼마쯤 함께 걷겠다고 했어. 그래서 둘은 같이 나섰지. 걷는 동안 스퀴어스 양은 아버지의 잘난 점들을 한껏 늘어놓았어. 그리고 아버지 수입을 열 배로 부풀려서, 자기 집안이 얼마나 대단하고 우월한지를 친구가 어렴풋이나마 짐작하게 해 줬지.\n\n그런데 마침 그때가, 듣기 좋게 스퀴어스 씨 학생들의 식사 시간이라고 부르는 그 시간이 끝난 뒤, 다시 유익한 공부로 돌아가기까지 날마다 허락되던 그 짧은 틈이었어. 바로 그 시간이, 니콜라스가 늘 우울한 산책을 나가던 때였지. 그는 마을을 멍하니 어슬렁대며 자기 비참한 처지를 곱씹곤 했거든. 스퀴어스 양도 이걸 빤히 알고 있었어. 그런데 깜빡했던 모양이야. 그 젊은 신사가 자기들 쪽으로 걸어오는 걸 보자, 그녀는 놀라고 당황한 기색을 잔뜩 드러내며 친구한테 '땅속으로 꺼져 버릴 것 같다'고 했거든.\n\n\"우리 돌아갈까, 아니면 어느 집으로 뛰어들까?\" 프라이스 양이 물었어. \"아직 우릴 못 봤어.\"\n\n\"아니야, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 대꾸했어. \"이건 내가 끝까지 견뎌 내야 할 일이야. 그래, 그럴 거야!\"\n\n스퀴어스 양이 이 말을, 무슨 드높은 도덕적 결심이라도 한 사람 같은 어조로 한 데다, 게다가 마음이 잔뜩 부풀어 오른 걸 드러내듯 한두 번 목이 메고 숨이 막히는 시늉까지 하니, 친구는 더 이상 아무 말도 하지 않았어. 그래서 둘은 곧장 니콜라스 쪽으로 밀고 갔지. 니콜라스는 눈을 땅에 떨군 채 걷고 있어서, 둘이 코앞에 닥쳐서야 다가오는 걸 알아챘어. 안 그랬으면 자기도 어디로든 피했을지 몰라.\n\n\"안녕하세요.\" 니콜라스가 고개를 숙이고 지나가며 말했어.\n\n\"가 버리잖아.\" 스퀴어스 양이 중얼거렸어. \"나 목이 막힐 것 같아, 틸다.\"\n\n\"돌아오세요, 니클비 씨, 어서요!\" 프라이스 양이 외쳤어. 친구의 위협에 놀란 척했지만, 사실은 니콜라스가 뭐라고 할지 듣고 싶은 짓궂은 마음에서였지. \"돌아오세요, 니클비 씨!\"\n\n니클비 씨가 돌아와서는, 더없이 어리둥절한 얼굴로 두 분이 무슨 시키실 일이라도 있느냐고 물었어.\n\n\"이야기하느라 멈추지 마시고요.\" 프라이스 양이 다급하게 재촉했어. \"반대쪽에서 이 애를 좀 부축해 주세요. 이제 좀 어떠니, 얘?\"\n\n\"좀 나아.\" 스퀴어스 양이 한숨을 쉬며, 초록색 베일이 달린 불그스름한 갈색 비버 가죽 보닛을 니클비 씨 어깨에 기대 놓았어. \"이 바보 같은 어지럼증이라니!\"\n\n\"바보 같다고 하지 마, 얘.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어. 니콜라스가 쩔쩔매는 꼴을 보며 그 반짝이는 눈이 재미로 춤을 추고 있었지. \"그게 부끄러워할 일은 아니야. 부끄러워해야 할 사람은, 너무 자존심이 세서 이런 소동 없이는 도로 마음을 못 돌리는 그런 사람이라고.\"\n\n\"날 기어이 거기다 엮으려고 작정하셨군요.\" 니콜라스가 미소 지으며 말했어. \"어젯밤에 제 잘못이 아니라고 말씀드렸는데도요.\"\n\n\"거 봐, 자기 잘못이 아니래, 얘.\" 짓궂은 프라이스 양이 말했어. \"어쩌면 네가 너무 질투했거나, 너무 성급하게 대했던 거 아냐? 자기 잘못이 아니라잖아. 너도 들었지. 이만하면 충분한 사과 같은데.\"\n\n\"제 말을 못 알아들으시네요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"제발 이런 농담은 그만두시죠. 저는 지금 그럴 시간도 없고, 솔직히 누구를 웃기는 거리가 되거나 누구를 웃기고 싶은 마음도 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말씀이세요?\" 프라이스 양이 깜짝 놀란 척하며 물었어.\n\n\"저 사람한테 묻지 마, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 외쳤어. \"난 저 사람 용서할게.\"\n\n\"이런.\" 갈색 보닛이 다시 자기 어깨에 내려앉자 니콜라스가 말했어. \"이거 제 생각보다 일이 심각하군요. 잠깐만요! 제 말 좀 들어 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 그는 갈색 보닛을 들어 올렸어. 그러고는 스퀴어스 양이 보내는, 다정하면서도 나무라는 듯한 눈길을 더없이 진심 어린 놀라움으로 마주하더니, 그 아리따운 짐이 닿지 않게 몇 걸음 뒤로 물러서서 말을 이었어.\n\n\"정말 미안합니다. 진심으로, 진정으로 미안해요. 어젯밤 두 분 사이에 어떤 다툼이 생긴 게 저 때문이라면요. 제가 운 나쁘게도 그 불화의 빌미가 된 걸 더없이 쓰라리게 자책하고 있어요. 비록 정말이지 전혀 뜻하지도, 생각지도 못한 채 그런 거지만요.\"\n\n\"근데 그게 하고 싶은 말 전부는 아니겠죠?\" 니콜라스가 말을 멈추자 프라이스 양이 외쳤어.\n\n\"뭔가 더 있는 것 같긴 합니다.\" 니콜라스가 반쯤 웃으며 더듬더듬 말하고는, 스퀴어스 양 쪽을 바라봤어. \"이거 참 말하기 거북하네요. 하지만 — 이런 짐작을 입에 올리는 것만으로도 사람이 건방진 풋내기처럼 보이긴 하지만 — 그래도요 — 혹시 저 아가씨께서, 제가 무슨 마음을 품고 있다고 — 그러니까, 제가 자기를 사랑한다고 생각하시나요?\"\n\n'기분 좋게 쩔쩔매네.' 스퀴어스 양이 생각했어. '드디어 저 사람을 여기까지 몰고 왔구나. 나 대신 대답해 줘, 얘.' 그녀가 친구에게 속삭였어.\n\n\"저 애가 그렇게 생각하느냐고요?\" 프라이스 양이 받았어. \"당연히 그렇게 생각하죠.\"\n\n\"그렇게요!\" 니콜라스가 어찌나 힘주어 내뱉었던지, 그 순간 황홀해하는 걸로 오해받을 만했어.\n\n\"그럼요.\" 프라이스 양이 대꾸했어.\n\n\"니클비 씨가 그걸 의심하셨다면, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 얼굴을 붉히며 부드러운 어조로 말했어. \"마음 푹 놓으셔도 돼. 그분 마음도 똑같—\"\n\n\"멈추세요.\" 니콜라스가 황급히 외쳤어. \"제발 제 말을 들어 주세요. 이건 인간이 빠질 수 있는, 또 저지를 수 있는 가장 어처구니없고 터무니없는 착각이에요. 가장 완벽하고 명백한 오해라고요. 저는 저 아가씨를 여섯 번도 채 못 봤어요. 하지만 예순 번을 봤다 해도, 아니 앞으로 육만 번을 볼 운명이라 해도, 똑같았을 거고 똑같을 겁니다. 저는 저 아가씨와 얽힌 생각도, 바람도, 희망도 하나 없어요. 단 하나만 빼고요 — 이건 저 아가씨 마음을 아프게 하려는 게 아니라, 제 진짜 속마음을 똑똑히 알려 드리려고 하는 말입니다 — 제 목숨만큼이나 마음에 소중한 그 단 하나의 바람만 빼고요. 언젠가 이 빌어먹을 곳에 등을 돌리고 다시는 발도 들이지 않는 것, 다시는 이곳을 떠올리지조차 — 떠올리더라도 — 진저리와 혐오 없이는 떠올리지 않는 것 말입니다.\"\n\n이 유난히 분명하고 거침없는 선언을, 그는 분하고 격앙된 마음에서 끌어올 수 있는 온 격렬함을 실어서 했어. 그러고는 더 들을 것도 없이 니콜라스는 물러갔지.\n\n하지만 가엾은 스퀴어스 양! 그 분노며, 격분이며, 속이 뒤틀리는 심정이며, 그녀의 머릿속을 휘몰아친 쓰라리고 격한 감정들의 빠른 물결은 이루 말로 다 못 해. 거절당하다니! 광고를 보고 주워 온, 일 년에 오 파운드를, 그것도 정해진 때도 없이 받는, 게다가 그 학생들이나 다름없이 먹고 자는 것까지 '얹혀' 사는 교사 따위한테 거절당하다니. 그것도 열여덟 살짜리 방앗간 집 딸 같은 풋내기 계집애 앞에서 말이야. 그 애는 삼 주 뒤면, 무릎까지 꿇고 청혼한 남자와 결혼할 참이었지. 이렇게 망신을 당했다고 생각하니, 그녀는 정말이지 진심으로 숨이 막힐 지경이었어.\n\n그래도 이 굴욕 한가운데서 한 가지만은 분명했어. 그건 그녀가, 스퀴어스 가문의 후손답게 좁아터진 마음과 옹졸한 속셈을 다 끌어모아 니콜라스를 미워하고 진저리 친다는 거였지. 그리고 위안거리도 하나 있었어. 그건 날마다 매시간, 그의 자존심을 후벼 파고, 무슨 무시든 모욕이든 빼앗음이든 안겨서 그를 들들 볶을 수 있다는 거였어. 그런 건 아무리 무딘 사람한테도 효과가 없을 수 없고, 니콜라스처럼 예민한 사람이라면 뼈저리게 느낄 게 분명했지. 이 두 가지 생각을 머릿속 맨 위에 올려놓고, 스퀴어스 양은 친구한테는 이 일을 그럴듯하게 둘러댔어. 니클비 씨가 워낙 괴상한 사람인 데다 성미가 어찌나 사나운지, 아무래도 단념해야 할 것 같아서 걱정이라고 말이야. 그러고는 친구와 헤어졌지.\n\n여기서 한마디 해 둘 게 있어. 스퀴어스 양은 니콜라스 니클비에게 애정을 (혹은 더 나은 게 없는 마당에 애정이랍시고 내세운 그 무엇이든) 쏟으면서도, 이 일에서 그가 자기와 다른 생각일 수도 있다는 가능성은 단 한 번도 진지하게 헤아려 본 적이 없었어. 스퀴어스 양의 계산은 이랬지. 자기는 매력 있고 예쁘다, 게다가 아버지는 주인이고 니콜라스는 일꾼이다, 아버지는 돈을 모았지만 니콜라스는 한 푼도 없다. 이 모든 게 그녀에게는, 저 젊은이가 자기 마음을 받았으니 오히려 분에 넘치게 영광스러워해야 할 결정적인 근거로 보였어. 게다가 그녀는 이것도 잊지 않고 떠올렸지. 자기가 그의 친구가 되어 주면 그의 처지를 얼마나 더 편하게 해 줄 수 있는지, 또 적이 되면 얼마나 더 고약하게 만들 수 있는지를 말이야. 그러니 분명, 니콜라스보다 덜 곧은 젊은이들이라면 많은 경우, 바로 이 뻔하고 알기 쉬운 이유 하나만으로도 그녀의 헛된 기대를 부추겨 줬을 거야. 하지만 그는 달리 처신하는 게 옳다고 여겼고, 그래서 스퀴어스 양은 길길이 날뛰었지.\n\n\"두고 보라지.\" 화가 머리끝까지 난 이 아가씨가, 제 방으로 돌아와 핍한테 손찌검을 해서 분풀이를 하고 나서 말했어. \"엄마 돌아오시면 그 사람한테 더 단단히 등 돌리게 만들어 놓을 거야!\"\n\n이렇게까지 할 필요도 거의 없었지만, 스퀴어스 양은 말한 대로 했어. 그래서 가엾은 니콜라스는, 형편없는 음식에다 더러운 잠자리, 거기에 칙칙하고 변함없이 되풀이되는 비참함의 행렬을 억지로 지켜봐야 하는 것도 모자라, 악의가 짜낼 수 있는, 또 가장 지독한 탐욕이 안길 수 있는 온갖 별난 모욕까지 당하게 됐지.\n\n이게 다가 아니었어. 또 다른, 더 뿌리 깊은 괴롭힘의 수법이 있었어. 그 부당함과 잔인함이 그의 가슴을 무너지게 했고, 거의 미쳐 버릴 지경으로 몰아갔지.\n\n그 가련한 아이, 스마이크는, 니콜라스가 교실에서 다정하게 말을 걸어 준 그날 밤 이후로, 그를 졸졸 쫓아다녔어. 어떻게든 그를 섬기고 돕고 싶다는, 잠시도 가시지 않는 마음으로 말이야. 제 보잘것없는 능력으로 채워 줄 수 있는 자잘한 필요들을 미리 챙겼고, 그저 곁에 있는 것만으로 만족했지. 몇 시간이고 그 옆에 앉아 참을성 있게 그의 얼굴을 들여다봤어. 그러다 말 한마디면 그 근심 어린 얼굴이 환해지면서, 스쳐 가는 빛이, 행복이라 할 만한 빛까지 떠오르곤 했지. 그는 달라진 사람이었어. 이제 그에게는 목표가 생겼거든. 그 목표란, 자기를 — 친절하게는 그만두고라도 — 사람답게 대해 준 단 한 사람, 그것도 낯선 사람인 그에게 자기가 얼마나 마음을 붙였는지 보여 주는 거였지.\n\n니콜라스에게 풀 수 없는 온갖 심술과 짜증은, 죄다 이 가엾은 아이에게 끊임없이 쏟아졌어. 고된 막일이야 아무것도 아니었어. 스마이크는 그런 데는 이골이 나 있었거든. 까닭 없이 얻어맞는 것도 마찬가지로 으레 있는 일이었지. 그것도 길고 고달픈 세월을 거치며 단단히 익혀 둔 거였으니까. 그런데 그가 니콜라스를 따른다는 게 눈에 띄자마자, 회초리질과 주먹질, 또 회초리질과 주먹질이 아침에도 낮에도 밤에도 그의 유일한 몫이 됐어. 스퀴어스는 제 일꾼이 그토록 빨리 얻은 영향력을 시샘했고, 그의 식구들은 그를 미워했지. 그래서 스마이크가 그 두 몫을 다 치른 거야. 니콜라스는 그걸 지켜봤어. 그리고 그 야만스럽고 비겁한 매질이 되풀이될 때마다 이를 갈았지.\n\n그는 아이들을 위해 정규 수업을 몇 가지 짜 놓았어. 그러던 어느 날 밤, 그 음침한 교실을 이리저리 거닐다가, 그의 부푼 가슴이 터질 것만 같았어. 자기가 보살피고 편들어 준 것이 도리어 그 가련한 아이의 비참함을 키운 셈이라는 생각에 말이야. 유난히 딱한 그 처지가 그의 연민을 불러일으킨 아이였는데. 그는 자기도 모르게 어두운 구석에서 발을 멈췄어. 바로 그 생각의 주인공이 앉아 있는 곳이었지.\n\n그 가엾은 영혼은 누덕누덕한 책을 뚫어져라 들여다보고 있었어. 얼굴에는 방금 흘린 눈물 자국이 아직 남아 있었지. 평범한 머리를 가진 아홉 살짜리 아이라면 거뜬히 정복했을 어떤 과제를, 그는 헛되이 붙들고 씨름하는 중이었어. 하지만 짓눌릴 대로 짓눌린 이 열아홉 살 소년의 흐리멍덩한 머리에는, 그게 풀리지 않는, 가망 없는 수수께끼였지. 그래도 그는 거기 앉아, 참을성 있게 그 페이지를 읽고 또 읽었어. 무슨 소년다운 야심에 떠밀린 게 아니었어. 그는 제 둘레에 모여드는 그 거친 무리한테까지도 흔한 웃음거리이자 놀림감이었으니까. 그저 자기 유일한 친구를 기쁘게 해 주고 싶다는 그 간절한 바람 하나에서 비롯된 거였지.\n\n니콜라스가 그의 어깨에 손을 얹었어.\n\n\"못 하겠어요.\" 풀이 죽은 아이가 얼굴마다 쓰라린 실망을 가득 담고 고개를 들며 말했어. \"안 돼요, 안 돼.\"\n\n\"애쓰지 마.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어.\n\n아이는 고개를 저었어. 그러고는 한숨을 쉬며 책을 덮고는, 멍하니 주위를 둘러보다 팔에 머리를 묻었어. 울고 있었지.\n\n\"제발, 부디 그러지 마.\" 니콜라스가 떨리는 목소리로 말했어. \"네가 그러는 걸 차마 못 보겠어.\"\n\n\"이젠 그 어느 때보다 저한테 모질게 굴어요.\" 아이가 흐느꼈어.\n\n\"나도 알아.\" 니콜라스가 받았어. \"그래, 그래.\"\n\n\"형이 아니었으면,\" 그 버림받은 아이가 말했어. \"전 죽었을 거예요. 그 사람들이 절 죽였을걸요. 정말로요. 분명 그랬을 거예요.\"\n\n\"넌 잘 지내게 될 거야, 가엾은 녀석.\" 니콜라스가 구슬프게 고개를 저으며 대꾸했어. \"내가 떠나고 나면 말이야.\"\n\n\"떠난다고요!\" 그 아이가 그의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보며 외쳤어.\n\n\"쉿!\" 니콜라스가 받았어. \"그래.\"\n\n\"가시는 거예요?\" 아이가 간절하게 속삭이며 따져 물었어.\n\n\"그건 모르겠어.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어. \"너한테 한 말이라기보단, 내 생각을 그냥 입에 올린 거야.\"\n\n\"말해 줘요.\" 아이가 애원하듯 말했어. \"아, 제발 말해 줘요. 가실 거예요? 갈 거예요?\"\n\n\"결국 그러도록 내몰리겠지!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"어쨌든 세상은 내 앞에 펼쳐져 있으니까.\"\n\n\"말해 줘요.\" 스마이크가 재촉했어. \"바깥세상도 이곳만큼 나쁘고 음침한가요?\"\n\n\"그럴 리가.\" 니콜라스가 제 생각의 갈피를 좇으며 대답했어. \"거기서 제일 고되고 험한 노동이라도, 여기에 비하면 행복일 거야.\"\n\n\"제가 거기서 형을 다시 만날 수 있을까요?\" 아이가 평소와 달리 거칠고 빠르게 쏟아 내며 물었어.\n\n\"그럼.\" 니콜라스가 그를 달래려는 마음에 대꾸했어.\n\n\"아니, 아니에요!\" 아이가 그의 손을 꼭 붙잡으며 말했어. \"제가, 제가요, 그 말 다시 해 줘요. 형을 꼭 찾을 수 있다고 말해 줘요.\"\n\n\"찾을 수 있어.\" 니콜라스가 똑같이 따뜻한 마음으로 대꾸했어. \"그러면 내가 너를 돕고 거들어 줄게. 여기서처럼 너한테 새 슬픔을 안기는 게 아니라 말이야.\"\n\n아이는 그 젊은이의 두 손을 격하게 제 손에 그러쥐었어. 그러고는 그 손을 가슴에 꼭 끌어안고, 알아들을 수 없는 끊어진 소리를 몇 마디 냈지. 바로 그때 스퀴어스가 들어왔고, 아이는 제 늘 있던 구석으로 움츠러들었어.\n\n# CHAPTER 13\n\n니콜라스가 매우 과감하고 놀라운 행동으로 도더보이즈 홀의 단조로움을 깨뜨리니, 이는 적잖이 중요한 결과로 이어진다\n\n1월 아침의 차갑고 희미한 새벽빛이 공동 침실 창문으로 스며들 무렵, 니콜라스는 팔로 몸을 일으켜 사방으로 자신을 둘러싸고 엎드린 채 잠든 형체들 사이를 둘러보았다. 마치 어떤 특정한 사람을 찾는 듯한 모습이었다. 빽빽이 뒤엉킨 잠든 무리 속에서 누군가 한 사람의 모습을 가려내려면 날카로운 눈이 필요했다. 아이들은 추위를 견디려고 누덕누덕 기운 넝마 옷으로 몸을 덮고 다닥다닥 붙어 누워 있었다. 그래서 보이는 거라곤 창백한 얼굴들의 날카로운 윤곽뿐이었어. 그 위로 음울한 빛이 한결같이 흐릿하고 무거운 색을 드리웠지. 군데군데 앙상한 팔이 삐죽 나와 있었어. 그 마른 팔은 아무것도 덮지 못한 채, 쪼그라들 대로 쪼그라든 흉한 모습을 고스란히 드러내고 있었지. 어떤 아이들은 등을 대고 누워 얼굴을 위로 향한 채 두 손을 꽉 움켜쥐고 있었어. 납빛 어스름 속에 겨우 보이는 그 모습은 살아 있는 사람이라기보다 시신에 가까웠지. 또 어떤 아이들은 이상하고 기괴한 자세로 몸을 웅크리고 있었어. 잠결의 뒤척임이라기보다, 고통이 잠시라도 덜해지길 바라며 안간힘을 쓰는 몸짓 같았지. 몇몇은 — 그 가운데 가장 어린 축에 드는 아이들이었어 — 얼굴에 미소를 띤 채 평화롭게 잠들어 있었어. 아마 집 꿈을 꾸고 있었겠지. 그런데 이따금 깊고 무거운 한숨이 방 안의 고요를 깨뜨렸어. 또 한 아이가 잠에서 깨어, 새로운 하루의 괴로움과 마주하게 됐다는 소리였지. 그리고 밤이 아침으로 바뀌자, 그 미소도 차츰 사라져 갔어. 미소를 빚어낸 그 다정한 어둠과 함께 말이야.\n\n꿈은 시와 전설 속에 사는 환한 존재야. 밤이면 땅 위에서 노닐다가, 첫 햇살이 비치면 스르르 녹아 사라지지. 그 햇살은 무서운 근심과 모진 현실에 빛을 비추며, 세상을 헤쳐 나가는 날마다의 고된 여정을 밝히는 거야.\n\n니콜라스는 잠든 아이들을 바라보았어. 처음엔 익숙한 광경을 보는 사람의 눈빛이었지. 익숙해도 그 슬픈 기운만큼은 조금도 바래지 않은 그런 광경 말이야. 그러다 점점 더 골똘하고 살피는 눈으로 바뀌었어. 늘 눈에 들어오던 무언가가, 으레 거기 있으리라 여겼던 무언가가 보이지 않을 때 사람이 짓는 그런 눈빛이었지. 그는 여전히 누군가를 찾고 있었어. 어찌나 간절했던지 침대에서 반쯤 몸을 일으킨 참이었지. 그때 계단 아래에서 부르는 스퀴어스의 목소리가 들려왔어.\n\n\"자, 이놈들아.\" 그 양반이 소리쳤어. \"거기 위에서 하루 종일 잠이나 잘 셈이냐——\"\n\n\"이 게으른 강아지들 같으니!\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말끝을 마저 채워 넣었어. 그러면서 코르셋 끈을 조일 때 나는 듯한 날카로운 소리를 함께 냈지.\n\n\"곧 내려가겠습니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"곧 내려와!\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"흥! 당장 내려오는 게 좋을 거다. 안 그러면 내가 더 빨리 올라가서 몇 놈 손봐 줄 테니까. 그 스마이크 녀석은 어디 있어?\"\n\n니콜라스는 다시 황급히 둘러보았지만, 아무 대답도 하지 않았어.\n\n\"스마이크!\" 스퀴어스가 고함쳤어.\n\n\"머리통이 또 어디 새로 깨지고 싶은 게냐, 스마이크?\" 그 인자한 부인도 같은 가락으로 다그쳤어.\n\n여전히 아무 대답도 없었어. 니콜라스는 계속 두리번거렸지. 이쯤 잠에서 깬 아이들 대부분도 마찬가지였어.\n\n\"건방진 놈 같으니!\" 스퀴어스가 지팡이로 계단 난간을 신경질적으로 탁탁 치며 중얼거렸어. \"니클비!\"\n\n\"네, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그 고집 센 망나니 놈 좀 내려보내. 내가 부르는 소리 안 들려?\"\n\n\"여기 없습니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"거짓말 마라.\" 교장이 쏘아붙였어. \"있잖아.\"\n\n\"없습니다.\" 니콜라스도 화가 나서 받아쳤어. \"저한테 거짓말하지 마세요.\"\n\n\"곧 알게 되겠지.\" 스퀴어스가 계단을 우당탕 올라오며 말했어. \"내가 꼭 찾아내고 말 테다, 두고 봐라.\"\n\n이렇게 큰소리치고는 스퀴어스가 침실로 쿵 뛰어들었어. 한 대 칠 기세로 지팡이를 허공에 휘두르며, 그 비쩍 마른 머슴애가 밤마다 몸을 뉘던 구석으로 쏜살같이 달려갔지. 지팡이는 애꿎은 바닥만 후려쳤어. 거기엔 아무도 없었거든.\n\n\"이게 무슨 일이야?\" 스퀴어스가 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 홱 돌아서며 말했어. \"그놈을 어디 숨겼어?\"\n\n\"어젯밤 이후로 한 번도 못 봤습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"이봐.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. 아닌 척하려고 애썼지만 겁먹은 게 뻔히 보였지. \"이런다고 그놈을 못 구해. 어디 있어?\"\n\n\"제가 알기로는 가장 가까운 연못 밑바닥에 있을지도 모르죠.\" 니콜라스가 낮은 목소리로 받아쳤어. 그러면서 교장의 얼굴을 똑바로 노려보았지.\n\n\"빌어먹을, 그게 무슨 소리야?\" 스퀴어스가 크게 당황해서 쏘아붙였어. 그러고는 대답도 기다리지 않고, 아이들에게 사라진 친구에 대해 아는 게 있느냐고 물었어.\n\n여기저기서 불안하게 모른다고 웅성거리는 소리가 났어. 그 와중에 한 째지는 목소리가 (사실 다들 그렇게 생각하던 말이었지) 들렸어.\n\n\"저, 선생님, 스마이크가 도망친 것 같아요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"하!\" 스퀴어스가 홱 돌아서며 소리쳤어. \"누가 그런 말을 했어?\"\n\n\"톰킨스예요, 선생님.\" 여러 목소리가 한꺼번에 대답했어. 스퀴어스는 무리 속으로 풍덩 뛰어들었어. 그러고는 한 번에 잠옷 차림의 아주 조그만 아이를 낚아챘지. 그 아이는 앞으로 끌려 나오며 얼떨떨한 표정을 짓고 있었어. 자기가 그 말을 한 대가로 벌을 받을지 상을 받을지 아직 모르겠다는 얼굴이었지. 의문은 오래가지 않았어.\n\n\"그래, 그놈이 도망쳤다고 생각하는 거지, 응?\" 스퀴어스가 다그쳤어.\n\n\"네, 선생님.\" 그 조그만 아이가 대답했어.\n\n\"그래서, 이놈아.\" 스퀴어스가 별안간 아이의 팔을 붙잡고 아주 능숙한 솜씨로 옷자락을 걷어 올리며 말했어. \"이 학교에서 도망치고 싶어 할 놈이 있다고 생각할 무슨 근거가 있냐? 응, 이놈아?\"\n\n아이는 대답 대신 처량한 비명을 질렀어. 스퀴어스는 힘을 쓰기에 가장 좋은 자세를 잡고는, 그 꼬마 녀석이 몸부림치다 못해 손아귀에서 데굴데굴 빠져나갈 때까지 두들겨 팼어. 그러고는 인심 좋게도, 아이가 알아서 굴러가도록 내버려 두었지.\n\n\"자.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"이제 또 누가 스마이크가 도망쳤다고 생각하면, 기꺼이 나랑 한번 얘기해 보자.\"\n\n물론 깊은 침묵만 흘렀어. 그동안 니콜라스는 눈빛으로 드러낼 수 있는 한 분명하게 역겨움을 드러냈지.\n\n\"그래, 니클비.\" 스퀴어스가 그를 심술궂게 흘겨보며 말했어. \"너는 그놈이 도망쳤다고 생각하겠지, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"그럴 가능성이 아주 높다고 봅니다.\" 니콜라스가 차분하게 대답했어.\n\n\"오, 그래? 그렇게 생각해?\" 스퀴어스가 비웃었어. \"어쩌면 그놈이 도망친 걸 알고 있는지도 모르겠군?\"\n\n\"그런 건 전혀 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"그놈이 간다는 말을 너한테 하진 않았겠지, 응?\" 스퀴어스가 비아냥거렸어.\n\n\"안 했습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"하지 않아서 정말 다행이라고 생각합니다. 했다면 미리 선생님께 알리는 게 제 도리였을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그런 도리라면 정말이지 끔찍하게 하기 싫었겠지.\" 스퀴어스가 빈정대듯 말했어.\n\n\"정말 그랬을 겁니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"제 마음을 아주 정확히 짚으셨네요.\"\n\n스퀴어스 부인은 계단 아래에서 이 대화를 듣고 있었어. 그런데 이제 더는 못 참겠던지, 부랴부랴 잠옷 윗도리를 걸치고는 사건 현장으로 올라왔지.\n\n\"여기서 무슨 소란이야?\" 부인이 말했어. 아이들이 좌우로 흩어지며 길을 터 주었어. 그 우람한 팔로 직접 길을 헤칠 수고를 덜어 준 거지. \"도대체 뭐 하러 저런 놈하고 입씨름을 하고 있어요, 스퀴어리!\"\n\n\"그게 말이야, 여보.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"실은 스마이크가 보이질 않아.\"\n\n\"그건 나도 알아요.\" 부인이 말했어. \"그게 뭐 놀랄 일이에요? 자존심만 잔뜩 부풀어서 어린 강아지들을 부추겨 반항하게 만드는 선생을 두면, 그것 말고 뭘 바라겠어요? 자, 젊은 양반. 친절을 베풀어 교실로 썩 꺼져 주시지. 그리고 애들도 데리고 가요. 허락받기 전에는 거기서 한 발짝도 나오지 말고. 안 그러면 당신하고 나하고 한판 붙을 텐데, 잘난 척하는 그 얼굴이 아주 망가질걸. 내 분명히 말해 두는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"그래, 그렇고말고, 거듭 그렇다니까, 이 건방진 애송이 양반아.\" 흥분한 부인이 말했어. \"내 마음대로 할 수 있다면 너 같은 놈은 이 집에 단 한 시간도 더 안 둘 거다.\"\n\n\"제 마음대로 할 수 있다면 저도 마찬가지일 겁니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"자, 얘들아!\"\n\n\"흥! '자, 얘들아.'\" 스퀴어스 부인이 그 보조 교사의 목소리와 말투를 최대한 흉내 내며 말했어. \"우두머리를 따라가렴, 얘들아. 그리고 배짱 있으면 스마이크를 본받아 봐. 그놈이 잡혀 오면 어떤 꼴을 당하는지 두고 봐. 그리고 명심해라! 그놈 얘기를 입에 올리기만 해도 너희도 똑같이, 아니 두 배로 호되게 당할 줄 알아.\"\n\n\"내가 그놈을 잡으면,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"산 채로 가죽을 벗기기 직전까지만 봐줄 거다. 분명히 일러두는 거다, 얘들아.\"\n\n\"잡으면이라고요?\" 스퀴어스 부인이 경멸하듯 받아쳤어. \"틀림없이 잡아요. 제대로만 나서면 안 잡힐 수가 없죠. 자! 다들 꺼져!\"\n\n이 말과 함께 스퀴어스 부인은 아이들을 내보냈어. 뒤쪽에서 빠져나가려고 앞으로 밀려드는데도 앞쪽 무리에 막혀 잠시 멈춰 있던 녀석들과 가볍게 한바탕 실랑이를 벌인 끝에, 부인은 마침내 방을 다 비워 냈지. 그러고는 남편과 단둘이 마주 섰어.\n\n\"그놈은 멀리 갔어요.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말했어. \"외양간이랑 마구간은 잠겨 있으니 거기 있을 리 없고, 아래층 어디에도 없어요. 하녀가 다 살펴봤거든. 틀림없이 요크 쪽으로, 그것도 큰길로 갔을 거예요.\"\n\n\"왜 그렇다는 거야?\" 스퀴어스가 물었어.\n\n\"멍청하긴!\" 스퀴어스 부인이 화를 내며 말했어. \"그놈한테 돈이 한 푼이라도 있었어요?\"\n\n\"내가 알기로는 평생 제 돈이라곤 한 푼도 가져 본 적이 없지.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어.\n\n\"그렇고말고요.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 받았어. \"게다가 먹을 것도 안 챙겨 갔어요. 그건 내가 장담해요. 하! 하! 하!\"\n\n\"하! 하! 하!\" 스퀴어스도 웃었어.\n\n\"그러니 당연히,\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말했어. \"구걸하며 가야 하는데, 그건 큰길 말고는 어디서도 못 하죠.\"\n\n\"그건 맞아!\" 스퀴어스가 손뼉을 치며 외쳤어.\n\n\"맞고말고요! 그래도 내가 말 안 했으면 당신은 절대 거기까지 생각 못 했을걸요.\" 아내가 대꾸했어. \"자, 당신이 마차를 타고 한쪽 길로 가요. 나는 스왈로네 마차를 빌려 다른 길로 갈 테니. 둘 다 눈 똑바로 뜨고, 이것저것 물어 가며 다니면, 우리 둘 중 하나는 틀림없이 그놈을 붙잡을 거예요.\"\n\n이 훌륭한 부인의 계획은 곧장 받아들여졌고, 한순간도 지체 없이 실행에 옮겨졌어. 아주 서둘러 아침을 먹고, 마을에서 이것저것 수소문을 한 결과, 제대로 추적하고 있는 모양이라는 결론이 났지. 스퀴어스는 발견과 복수를 벼르며 조랑말 마차를 타고 나섰어. 잠시 뒤, 스퀴어스 부인도 흰 외투를 걸치고 갖가지 숄과 손수건을 둘둘 두른 채 다른 마차를 타고 다른 방향으로 출발했어. 그러면서 큼지막한 몽둥이 하나, 튼튼한 밧줄 토막 몇 개, 그리고 건장한 일꾼 하나를 데려갔지. 이 모든 건 단 하나의 목적을 위해 마련하고 실어 간 거였어. 가엾은 스마이크를 붙잡는 걸 돕고, 일단 잡으면 안전하게 가둬 두려는 것 말이야.\n\n니콜라스는 뒤에 남아 마음이 어지러웠어. 그 아이의 도주가 어떻게 끝나든, 거기서 나올 거라곤 가슴 아프고 통탄스러운 결말뿐이라는 걸 알았거든. 그렇게 가난하고 의지할 데 없는 아이가, 홀로 친구 하나 없이, 전혀 모르는 고장을 오래도록 헤매다가 기껏 맞을 수 있는 최선이라곤, 굶주림과 추위에 시달리다 죽는 것이었지. 어쩌면 그 운명과 요크셔 학교의 그 다정한 자비 속으로 돌아오는 것 사이에 별 차이가 없었을지도 몰라. 하지만 그 가엾은 아이는 니콜라스의 연민과 동정을 깊이 사로잡고 있었어. 그래서 그 아이가 겪게 될 고통을 떠올리니 가슴이 미어졌지. 니콜라스는 안절부절못한 채 머물러 있었어. 온갖 가능성을 천 가지나 그려 보면서 말이야. 그러다 이튿날 저녁, 스퀴어스가 혼자, 빈손으로 돌아왔어.\n\n\"그 망나니 녀석 소식이 없어!\" 교장이 말했어. 그는 가는 동안 옛 방식대로 다리를 적잖이 풀고 온 게 분명했지. \"스퀴어스 부인이 그놈을 못 잡아 오면, 누구한테서든 이 분풀이를 받아 내고 말 거다, 니클비. 그러니 미리 일러두는 거다.\"\n\n\"제가 위로해 드릴 수 있는 일이 아닙니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"저랑은 아무 상관 없는 일이니까요.\"\n\n\"그래?\" 스퀴어스가 위협하듯 말했어. \"두고 보자!\"\n\n\"두고 보죠.\" 니콜라스가 받아쳤어.\n\n\"조랑말은 다리가 다 풀려 못 쓰게 됐고, 나는 삯말을 빌려 집에 와야 했어. 그게 다른 비용 빼고도 십오 실링이나 든단 말이야.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"그건 누가 물어 줄 거냐, 응?\"\n\n니콜라스는 어깨를 으쓱하고는 잠자코 있었어.\n\n\"누구한테서든 받아 내고 말 거다, 분명히 말하지만.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. 늘 매섭고 교활하던 태도가 대놓고 으름장을 놓는 투로 바뀌어 있었지. \"여기서 징징대며 허세 부릴 생각 마라, 강아지 양반. 네 개집으로 썩 꺼져. 잘 시간 지났으니까! 자! 나가!\"\n\n니콜라스는 입술을 깨물고 저도 모르게 두 손을 움켜쥐었어. 손끝이 그 모욕을 갚아 주고 싶어 근질거렸거든. 하지만 그 사람이 취해 있고, 결국 시끄러운 몸싸움밖에 안 될 거라는 걸 떠올리고는, 그 폭군에게 경멸의 눈길 한 번 던지는 걸로 만족했지. 그러고는 최대한 의젓하게 계단을 올라갔어. 그래도 적잖이 부아가 치밀었지. 스퀴어스 양과 스퀴어스 도련님, 그리고 하녀가 아늑한 구석에서 그 광경을 구경하며 즐기고 있었거든. 앞의 두 사람은 가난뱅이 벼락출세꾼의 주제넘음에 대해 점잖은 척 한마디씩 늘어놓았어. 그 말에 한바탕 웃음이 터졌고, 세상에서 가장 비참한 하녀까지도 거기 끼어 웃었지. 한편 니콜라스는 속이 잔뜩 상한 채, 가진 이불을 머리 위까지 끌어 덮었어. 그러고는 자기와 스퀴어스 사이의 묵은 셈을, 그자가 예상하는 것보다 훨씬 빨리 치러야겠다고 단단히 마음먹었어.\n\n또 하루가 밝았어. 니콜라스가 채 잠에서 깨기도 전에, 마차 바퀴 소리가 집 쪽으로 다가오는 게 들렸지. 마차가 멈췄어. 스퀴어스 부인의 목소리가 들렸어. 의기양양하게 누군가에게 줄 독한 술 한 잔을 시키는 소리였지. 그것만으로도 뭔가 굉장한 일이 벌어졌다는 충분한 신호였어. 니콜라스는 차마 창밖을 내다볼 엄두가 안 났어. 그래도 내다봤지. 그러자 맨 먼저 눈에 들어온 게 그 가엾은 스마이크였어. 진흙과 빗물에 흠뻑 젖고, 어찌나 초췌하고 지치고 사나운 몰골이었던지, 어떤 허수아비도 입어 본 적 없을 그런 옷차림만 아니었다면, 그 순간조차 정말 그 아이가 맞는지 긴가민가했을 거야.\n\n\"끌어 내려.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. 그는 잠자코 그 죄인을 실컷 눈요기한 뒤였지. \"안으로 데려와, 안으로 데려와!\"\n\n\"조심해요.\" 남편이 거들려고 하자 스퀴어스 부인이 외쳤어. \"또 미끄러져 도망 못 가게, 다리를 앞치마 밑에 묶고 마차에 단단히 매어 놨으니까.\"\n\n스퀴어스는 기쁨에 부들부들 떨리는 손으로 밧줄을 풀었어. 그리고 산 사람이라기보다 죽은 사람에 가까운 스마이크는 집 안으로 끌려 들어와, 지하실에 단단히 갇혔지. 스퀴어스가 학교 전체를 모아 놓고 그를 손봐 줄 적당한 때라고 여길 때까지 말이야.\n\n상황을 얼른 따져 보면, 어떤 사람들은 의아할지도 몰라. 스퀴어스 부부가 그렇게 큰 소리로 불평하던 골칫덩이를 도로 붙잡으려고 어쩌면 그토록 애를 썼을까 하고 말이야. 하지만 이 말을 들으면 그 의아함은 사라질 거야. 그 머슴애가 해 온 온갖 일을 만약 다른 사람이 했다면, 학교는 품삯으로 일주일에 십 실링이나 십이 실링쯤 들었을 테니까. 게다가 두더보이즈 홀에서는, 도망친 녀석은 누구든 본보기로 호되게 다루는 게 방침이었어. 학교의 매력이라는 게 워낙 보잘것없다 보니, 다리가 멀쩡히 달려 있고 그걸 쓸 줄 아는 학생이라면 거기 남아 있을 이유라곤, 두려움이라는 강력한 충동 말고는 별로 없었거든.\n\n스마이크가 붙잡혀 의기양양하게 끌려왔다는 소식은 굶주린 무리 사이에 들불처럼 번졌어. 그래서 오전 내내 모두가 잔뜩 기대하며 마음을 졸였지. 하지만 그 기대는 오후까지 졸인 채로 있어야 했어. 스퀴어스가 점심으로 기운을 채우고, 술까지 한두 잔 더 걸쳐 한층 든든해진 뒤에야, 불길한 기색이 가득한 얼굴로 (그 인자한 짝과 함께) 나타났거든. 그러면서 무시무시한 매질 도구도 하나 들고 있었어. 튼튼하고 낭창낭창하고 끝에 밀랍을 먹인 새것이었지. 한마디로 그날 아침, 바로 이 일을 위해 사 온 거였어.\n\n\"빠진 놈 없이 다 모였나?\" 스퀴어스가 우렁찬 목소리로 물었어.\n\n다들 모여 있었지만 누구도 입을 떼지 못했어. 그래서 스퀴어스가 확인하려고 줄지어 선 아이들을 노려보았지. 그가 그러자 모든 눈이 내리깔리고, 모든 고개가 움츠러들었어.\n\n\"각자 제자리에 있어.\" 스퀴어스가 그 좋아하는 솜씨로 책상을 한 대 후려치며 말했어. 그러고는 그 소리에 어김없이 모두가 움찔하는 모습을 음울한 만족감으로 바라보았지. \"니클비! 자네 책상으로 가게.\"\n\n그 보조 교사의 얼굴에 아주 묘하고 심상치 않은 표정이 떠올라 있다는 걸, 어린 구경꾼 여럿이 알아챘어. 하지만 니콜라스는 입 한 번 열어 대꾸하지 않고 자리에 앉았지. 스퀴어스는 조수에게 의기양양한 눈길을, 아이들에게는 더없이 위세 넘치는 눈길을 던지고는 방을 나갔어. 그러고는 곧 스마이크를 멱살을 잡아 끌고 돌아왔지. 아니, 정확히는 멱살이 있을 자리에 가장 가까운 웃옷 조각을 붙잡고 끌고 온 거였어. 그런 장식이 그에게 달려 있기나 했다면 말이지.\n\n다른 곳이었다면, 그 가엾고 지치고 풀 죽은 모습은 동정과 항의의 웅성거림을 불러일으켰을 거야. 거기서도 얼마쯤은 그랬지. 구경하던 아이들이 불편한 듯 자리에서 움찔거렸거든. 그리고 가장 대담한 몇몇은 슬쩍슬쩍 서로 눈길을 주고받으며 분노와 연민을 드러냈어.\n\n하지만 그건 스퀴어스에게는 통하지 않았어. 그의 시선은 운 나쁜 스마이크에게 못 박혀 있었거든. 그는 이런 경우의 관례대로, 할 말이 있느냐고 물었어.\n\n\"아무 할 말 없겠지, 응?\" 스퀴어스가 악마 같은 미소를 지으며 말했어.\n\n스마이크는 주위를 둘러보았어. 그리고 그 눈길이 잠시 니콜라스에게 멈췄지. 끼어들어 막아 주길 바라는 듯 말이야. 하지만 니콜라스의 눈은 책상에 붙박여 있었어.\n\n\"할 말 있어?\" 스퀴어스가 다시 다그쳤어. 그러면서 매질하는 오른팔의 힘과 유연함을 시험하듯 두세 번 휘둘러 보았지. \"조금만 비켜 줘, 여보. 팔 휘두를 자리가 영 좁아서 말이야.\"\n\n\"살려 주세요, 선생님!\" 스마이크가 외쳤어.\n\n\"오! 그게 다야?\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"그래, 목숨이 간당간당할 때까지 두들겨 패고, 딱 그 정도만 살려 주마.\"\n\n\"하, 하, 하.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 웃었어. \"거 한마디 잘했네!\"\n\n\"어쩔 수 없어서 그랬어요.\" 스마이크가 힘없이 말했어. 그러고는 또 한 번 애원하는 눈길로 주위를 둘러보았지.\n\n\"어쩔 수 없어서 그랬다고?\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"오! 네 잘못이 아니라, 내 잘못이란 말이지——응?\"\n\n\"고약하고 배은망덕하고 고집불통에다 짐승 같고 음흉한 개 같은 놈!\" 스퀴어스 부인이 스마이크의 머리를 겨드랑이에 끼고 욕설을 한마디 할 때마다 한 대씩 후려치며 외쳤어. \"이놈이 그게 무슨 소리야?\"\n\n\"비켜, 여보.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"어디 한번 알아내 보자고.\"\n\n스퀴어스 부인은 힘을 쓰느라 숨이 차서 물러났어. 스퀴어스는 그 아이를 단단히 움켜쥐었어. 절박한 매질 한 대가 그 몸에 떨어졌고——스마이크는 채찍에 움찔하며 고통의 비명을 질렀지——채찍이 다시 들렸어. 그리고 다시 막 내리치려는 순간——니콜라스 니클비가 벌떡 일어나 \"멈춰!\" 하고 외쳤어. 서까래가 다 울릴 만큼 쩌렁쩌렁한 목소리였지.\n\n\"멈추라고 한 게 누구야?\" 스퀴어스가 사납게 홱 돌아서며 말했어.\n\n\"접니다.\" 니콜라스가 앞으로 나서며 말했어. \"이건 계속해선 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"계속해선 안 된다고!\" 스퀴어스가 거의 비명을 지르듯 외쳤어.\n\n\"안 됩니다!\" 니콜라스가 우레같이 소리쳤어.\n\n이 대담한 참견에 기겁하고 얼이 빠진 스퀴어스는 스마이크를 붙잡은 손을 놓았어. 그러고는 한두 걸음 뒷걸음치며, 정말이지 무시무시한 눈빛으로 니콜라스를 노려보았지.\n\n\"안 된다고 했습니다.\" 니콜라스가 조금도 주눅 들지 않고 거듭 말했어. \"안 됩니다. 제가 막겠습니다.\"\n\n스퀴어스는 눈알이 튀어나올 듯한 눈으로 계속 그를 노려보았어. 하지만 너무 놀란 나머지, 그 순간만큼은 말문이 막혀 버렸지.\n\n\"선생님은 이 가엾은 아이를 위한 제 조용한 부탁들을 죄다 무시하셨습니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그 아이를 용서해 달라고 빌면서, 여기 얌전히 있도록 제가 책임지겠다고 한 편지에도 아무 답을 안 주셨고요. 이렇게 사람들 앞에서 끼어든다고 저를 탓하지 마세요. 이건 선생님이 자초한 일이지, 제가 그런 게 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"앉아, 이 거지 같은 놈아!\" 스퀴어스가 분노로 거의 제정신이 아닌 채 비명을 질렀어. 그러면서 말이 끝나기 무섭게 스마이크를 다시 붙잡았지.\n\n\"이 인간 말종.\" 니콜라스가 사납게 받아쳤어. \"그 아이를 건드리면 큰일 날 줄 알아! 가만히 서서 그 짓을 보고만 있진 않을 테니까. 나도 피가 끓어오른 데다, 너 같은 인간 열 명을 당할 힘이 있어. 몸조심해. 하늘에 맹세코, 네가 나를 끝까지 몰아붙이면 절대 봐주지 않을 테니!\"\n\n\"물러서!\" 스퀴어스가 매를 휘두르며 외쳤어.\n\n\"갚아 줘야 할 모욕이 줄줄이 쌓였어.\" 니콜라스가 격분으로 얼굴이 벌게져서 말했어. \"게다가 이 더러운 소굴에서 의지할 데 없는 어린것들한테 저질러진 비겁한 만행 때문에 분노가 더 치미는 거야. 조심해. 정말로 내 속의 악마를 깨운다면, 그 대가가 네 머리 위로 호되게 떨어질 테니까!\"\n\n니콜라스가 채 말을 마치기도 전에, 스퀴어스가 노여움을 사납게 터뜨리며 들짐승 울부짖는 듯한 소리를 내질렀어. 그러고는 니콜라스에게 침을 뱉고, 그 고문 도구로 얼굴을 후려쳤지. 매가 닿은 자리에 시퍼런 살자국이 부풀어 올랐어. 그 매질의 아픔에 얼얼해진 니콜라스는, 분노와 경멸과 격분의 온갖 감정을 그 한순간에 몰아넣어 스퀴어스에게 덤벼들었어. 그자의 손에서 매를 빼앗고, 목을 틀어쥐고는, 그 악당이 살려 달라고 울부짖을 때까지 두들겨 팼지.\n\n아이들은——아버지를 도우러 와서 뒤에서 적을 괴롭히던 스퀴어스 도련님만 빼고——손끝 하나, 발끝 하나 움직이지 않았어. 하지만 스퀴어스 부인은 도와 달라고 연신 비명을 지르며 제 짝의 웃옷 자락에 매달려, 격분한 적수에게서 남편을 떼어 내려고 안간힘을 썼지. 한편 스퀴어스 양은 열쇠 구멍으로 아주 다른 장면을 기대하며 엿보고 있었는데, 공격이 시작되자마자 안으로 뛰어들었어. 그러고는 그 보조 교사의 머리에 잉크병을 빗발치듯 던지고는, 실컷 니콜라스를 두들겨 팼지. 한 대씩 칠 때마다, 자기가 내민 사랑을 그가 거절했다는 기억을 떠올리며 기운을 냈어. 그래서 그러잖아도 (이 점에서는 어머니를 닮아) 어느 때고 약한 편이 아니던 그 팔에, 한층 더 힘이 실렸지.\n\n니콜라스는 격렬한 분노가 거세게 솟구치던 터라, 그 매질을 깃털로 맞는 정도로밖에 안 느꼈어. 하지만 그 소란과 아우성에 진력이 났고, 게다가 팔에 힘이 빠지는 게 느껴지자, 남은 힘을 모조리 끌어모아 마무리로 대여섯 대를 갈겼어. 그러고는 있는 힘껏 스퀴어스를 내동댕이쳤지. 그가 어찌나 세게 나가떨어졌던지, 스퀴어스 부인까지 옆에 있던 의자 위로 완전히 나동그라졌어. 그리고 스퀴어스는 떨어지면서 그 의자에 머리를 부딪쳤고, 정신을 잃은 채 꼼짝 못 하고 바닥에 길게 뻗어 버렸어.\n\n이렇게 일을 흐뭇하게 마무리한 니콜라스는, 스퀴어스가 죽은 게 아니라 그저 기절했을 뿐이라는 걸 확인하고는 더없이 만족했어 (처음엔 이 점이 영 께름칙해 마음에 걸렸거든). 그는 식구들이 스퀴어스를 깨우게 내버려 두고, 이제 어떻게 하는 게 좋을지 곰곰 생각하러 물러났지. 방을 나서며 그는 스마이크를 애타게 둘러보았어. 하지만 어디에도 보이지 않았어.\n\n잠시 생각한 끝에, 그는 작은 가죽 가방에 옷가지 몇 벌을 챙겨 넣었어. 그리고 아무도 그의 발걸음을 막으려 들지 않자, 대담하게 정문으로 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔지. 그러고는 얼마 안 가, 그레타 다리로 이어지는 길로 접어들었어.\n\n마음이 어지간히 가라앉아 지금 처지를 조금 곱씹어 볼 만해지자, 형편이 그리 희망적으로 보이지는 않았어. 주머니엔 사 실링에 잔돈 몇 푼이 다였고, 런던까지는 이백오십 마일이 넘었거든. 그래도 런던으로 발길을 돌리기로 마음먹었어. 무엇보다 스퀴어스가 그 다정하기 그지없는 삼촌에게 오늘 아침 일을 어떻게 전했는지 알아봐야겠다 싶었던 거야.\n\n이 딱한 처지를 어쩔 도리가 없다는 결론에 이르러 고개를 들었을 때, 한 말 탄 사람이 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 게 보였어. 가까이 와서 보니, 분하게도 다름 아닌 존 브라우디 씨였지. 코르덴 옷에 가죽 각반을 두른 그는, 굵은 물푸레나무 막대기로 말을 몰아 가고 있었어. 막 어느 튼튼한 어린나무에서 베어 낸 듯한 막대기였지.\n\n'더 시끄럽게 싸우고 떠들고 싶은 기분이 아닌데.' 니콜라스는 생각했어. '그래도 어쩌겠어. 이 정직한 멍청이하고 한바탕 입씨름을 하게 생겼고, 어쩌면 저 막대기로 한두 대 맞을지도 모르겠군.'\n\n사실 이 만남에서 그런 결과가 따를 거라고 예상할 만한 까닭이 좀 있긴 했어. 존 브라우디는 니콜라스가 다가오는 걸 보자마자, 길가에 말고삐를 당겨 세우고는 그가 올 때까지 기다렸거든. 그러면서 말 두 귀 사이로, 느긋이 걸어오는 니콜라스를 아주 험상궂게 노려보고 있었지.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 젊은 양반.\" 존이 말했어.\n\n\"안녕하세요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"음, 드디어 만났구먼.\" 존이 물푸레나무 막대기로 등자를 탁 쳐서 쩌렁 울리게 하며 말했어.\n\n\"그러게요.\" 니콜라스가 머뭇거리며 대답했어. \"저기요!\" 그는 잠깐 뜸을 들이더니 솔직하게 말했어. \"지난번에 만났을 땐 그리 좋게 헤어지지 못했죠. 제 잘못이었을 겁니다. 하지만 당신 기분을 상하게 할 마음은 없었고, 그러고 있는 줄도 몰랐어요. 나중에 정말 미안했습니다. 악수해 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"악수라고!\" 마음씨 좋은 그 요크셔 사내가 외쳤어. \"아! 그러고말고요.\" 그러면서 안장에서 몸을 숙여, 니콜라스의 손을 와락 비틀어 쥐었어. \"근데 얼굴이 왜 그래요, 젊은 양반? 죄다 터진 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"베인 거예요.\" 니콜라스가 말하면서 새빨개졌어. \"한 대 맞았죠. 하지만 때린 놈한테 도로 갚아 줬어요. 그것도 두둑한 이자까지 쳐서요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그랬단 말이오?\" 존 브라우디가 외쳤어. \"잘했네! 그래서 더 마음에 들어요.\"\n\n\"실은 말이죠.\" 니콜라스가 어떻게 털어놔야 할지 잘 몰라 하며 말했어. \"실은 제가 못된 꼴을 당했어요.\"\n\n\"저런!\" 존 브라우디가 안쓰러운 투로 끼어들었어. 그는 힘이며 덩치며 거인 같은 사내였고, 그 눈에는 아마 니콜라스가 한낱 난쟁이처럼 보였을 거야. \"그런 말 마요.\"\n\n\"정말이에요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그 스퀴어스라는 작자한테요. 그래서 제가 그놈을 흠씬 두들겨 패고, 그 길로 거기를 떠나는 길입니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 존 브라우디가 어찌나 신나게 고함을 질렀던지, 말이 깜짝 놀라 펄쩍 뛸 정도였어. \"교장 놈을 패줬다고! 호! 호! 호! 교장 놈을 패줬다니! 세상에 그런 일이 다 있나! 다시 한번 손 좀 주시오, 젊은 양반. 교장 놈을 패주다니! 젠장, 그래서 당신이 더 좋소.\"\n\n이렇게 기뻐하며 존 브라우디는 웃고 또 웃었어——어찌나 크게 웃었던지, 멀고 가까운 메아리가 죄다 유쾌한 웃음소리만 되돌려 보낼 정도였지——그러면서 그 못지않게 진심으로 니콜라스의 손을 흔들어 댔어. 웃음이 잦아들자, 그는 니콜라스가 앞으로 어쩔 셈이냐고 물었어. 곧장 런던으로 갈 거라는 대답에, 그는 못 미덥다는 듯 고개를 절레절레 흔들었지. 그러고는 그렇게 먼 데까지 손님을 태우는 데 마차가 얼마나 받는지 아느냐고 물었어.\n\n\"아뇨, 모릅니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"하지만 저한테는 별로 상관없는 일이에요. 걸어갈 작정이거든요.\"\n\n\"런던까지 걸어간다고!\" 존이 깜짝 놀라 외쳤어.\n\n\"한 걸음도 빼놓지 않고요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"이쯤이면 벌써 한참 더 갔어야 하는데. 그럼 안녕히 가세요!\"\n\n\"아니, 잠깐만.\" 그 정직한 시골 사내가 안달하는 말을 멈춰 세우며 말했어. \"가만 좀 서 봐요. 돈은 얼마나 있소?\"\n\n\"많진 않아요.\" 니콜라스가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했어. \"그래도 어떻게든 꾸려 갈 수 있어요. 뜻이 있는 곳에 길이 있다잖아요.\"\n\n존 브라우디는 이 말에 입으로는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았어. 대신 주머니에 손을 넣어, 단단한 가죽으로 된 낡은 지갑을 꺼냈지. 그러고는 당장 필요한 만큼은 자기한테 꼭 빌려 가라고 우겼어.\n\n\"겁먹지 말아요, 젊은 양반.\" 그가 말했어. \"집까지 갈 만큼은 챙겨요. 언젠가 갚으면 되지, 내 장담하리다.\"\n\n니콜라스는 도무지 일 소버린 이상은 빌리려 들지 않았어. 브라우디 씨는 더 받으라고 한참을 사정했지만 (요크셔 사람 특유의 신중함을 곁들여, 다 안 쓰면 남는 건 따로 두었다가 운임 없이 부칠 기회가 생길 때 돌려주면 된다고도 했지) 결국 그 정도로 만족할 수밖에 없었어.\n\n\"이 막대기도 가져가요, 가는 데 도움 될 거요.\" 그가 막대기를 니콜라스에게 건네며 덧붙였어. 그러고는 또 한 번 손을 꽉 쥐었지. \"기운 내고, 잘 가시오. 교장 놈을 패주다니! 이거 참, 이십 년 만에 들은 가장 통쾌한 소식이오!\"\n\n이렇게 말하고는, 그한테서 기대하기 어려울 만큼 섬세하게도, 존 브라우디는 또 한바탕 큰 소리로 웃어 댔어. 니콜라스가 쏟아 내는 고맙다는 말을 피하려는 거였지. 그러고는 말에 박차를 가해, 경쾌한 구보로 떠나갔어. 니콜라스가 서서 뒷모습을 바라보는 동안, 그는 이따금 뒤를 돌아보며 가는 길에 힘내라는 듯 명랑하게 손을 흔들어 주었지. 니콜라스는 말과 그 사람이 저 멀리 언덕마루 너머로 사라질 때까지 지켜보았어. 그러고 나서 길을 나섰지.\n\n그날 오후엔 그리 멀리 가지 못했어. 이미 날이 거의 저문 데다 눈까지 많이 내렸거든. 그 눈 때문에 길이 고될 뿐 아니라, 날이 밝아도 길눈 밝은 나그네가 아니고서는 자취를 찾기 어렵고 헷갈렸지. 그날 밤 그는 한 오두막에서 묵었어. 가난한 축의 나그네들에게 싼값에 잠자리를 빌려주는 곳이었지. 이튿날 아침 일찍 일어나, 날이 저물기 전에 버러브리지에 다다랐어. 값싼 쉴 곳을 찾아 그 마을을 지나다가, 길가에서 이백 야드쯤 떨어진 빈 헛간을 우연히 발견했어. 그 따뜻한 한구석에 지친 몸을 뉘었고, 이내 잠이 들었지.\n\n이튿날 아침 잠에서 깬 그는, 꾼 꿈을 떠올려 보려 했어. 죄다 얼마 전 두더보이즈 홀에서 지낸 일과 얽힌 꿈이었지. 그는 자리에 일어나 앉아 눈을 비비며, 앞쪽 몇 야드 거리에 멈춰 선 듯한 어떤 꼼짝 않는 형체를 빤히 바라보았어——그리 차분한 얼굴은 아니었지.\n\n\"이상하군!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어. \"이게 아직 채 가시지도 않은 꿈이 남긴 환영인가! 진짜일 리 없는데——그런데 나는——나는 깨어 있잖아! 스마이크!\"\n\n그 형체가 움직였어. 일어서서 다가오더니, 그의 발치에 무릎을 꿇었지. 정말 스마이크였어.\n\n\"왜 나한테 무릎을 꿇는 거야?\" 니콜라스가 얼른 그를 일으키며 말했어.\n\n\"형이랑 같이 가려고요——어디든——어디든지——세상 끝까지라도——교회 묘지의 무덤까지라도요.\" 스마이크가 그의 손을 붙잡으며 대답했어. \"그러게 해 줘요, 오, 제발 그러게 해 줘요. 형이 제 집이에요——제 다정한 친구예요——저를 데려가 줘요, 제발요.\"\n\n\"나는 너를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 게 별로 없는 친구야.\" 니콜라스가 다정하게 말했어. \"어떻게 여기까지 왔어?\"\n\n알고 보니 그는 니콜라스를 따라온 거였어. 오는 내내 한순간도 그를 놓치지 않았다는 거지. 그가 잠잘 때도, 요기하러 멈췄을 때도 지켜보고 있었대. 그러면서도 진작 나서기가 무서웠다는 거야. 다시 돌려보내질까 봐서 말이지. 지금도 나설 생각은 아니었는데, 니콜라스가 생각보다 갑자기 깨는 바람에 몸을 숨길 틈이 없었던 거야.\n\n\"가엾은 녀석.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"모진 팔자 탓에 너한테는 친구라곤 단 하나뿐인데, 그마저도 너만큼이나 가난하고 의지할 데 없는 신세구나.\"\n\n\"저——저 형이랑 같이 가도 돼요?\" 스마이크가 머뭇머뭇 물었어. \"충실하게, 부지런히 일하는 하인이 될게요, 정말이에요. 옷도 필요 없어요.\" 그 가엾은 녀석이 제 넝마를 끌어모으며 덧붙였어. \"이거면 충분해요. 그저 형 곁에 있고 싶을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇게 해.\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어. \"그리고 세상은 나를 대하듯 너도 대할 거야. 우리 둘 중 하나가, 아니면 둘 다 더 나은 곳으로 떠나는 그날까지 말이야. 가자!\"\n\n이 말과 함께, 그는 짐을 둘러메고는 한 손에 막대기를 들고, 다른 손을 기뻐하는 그 아이에게 내밀었어. 그렇게 두 사람은 함께 그 낡은 헛간을 나섰어.\n\n# CHAPTER 14\n\n평민들만 다루는 불행을 안고 있어, 어쩔 수 없이 천하고 비속한 성격을 띤 장\n\n골든 스퀘어가 자리 잡은 런던의 그 동네에는, 한물간 데다 빛바래고 다 쓰러져 가는 거리가 하나 있어. 길 양쪽으로 키만 멀쑥하고 비쩍 마른 집들이 들쭉날쭉 두 줄로 늘어서 있는데, 마치 오래전에 서로 노려보다가 기가 죽어 버린 것처럼 보였지. 굴뚝들마저 음울하고 우울해진 것 같았어. 건너편 굴뚝 말고는 달리 쳐다볼 만한 게 없었으니까. 굴뚝 꼭대기는 죄다 찌그러지고 부서지고 연기에 시커멓게 그을려 있었어. 그리고 여기저기 다른 것보다 유난히 높은 굴뚝 하나가 한쪽으로 심하게 기울어져 지붕 위로 넘어질 듯 휘청거렸지. 마치 반세기 동안 받아 온 무관심에 복수라도 하려는 듯이 말이야. 그 밑 다락방에 사는 사람들을 짓눌러 버리겠다는 듯이.\n\n하수구 둘레를 쪼아 대는 닭들은 몸을 이리저리 홱홱 트는 걸음걸이로 돌아다녔어. 그런 걸음은 도시 닭한테서만 볼 수 있지, 시골 수탉이나 암탉이라면 도무지 이해하지 못할 그런 걸음이었어. 그리고 그 닭들은 제 주인들의 다 허물어져 가는 집과 어찌나 잘 어울리는지. 거무튀튀하고 깃털도 볼품없고 졸린 듯 푸드덕거리는 이 새들은, 동네 아이들 여럿이 그렇듯 거리에 나가 먹고살라고 내몰린 신세였어. 돌멩이에서 돌멩이로 폴짝폴짝 뛰어다니며 진흙 속에 숨은 먹을거리를 가엾게 찾아 헤맸지만, 그 사이에서 제대로 우는 놈 하나 찾기 힘들었지. 목소리 비슷한 거라도 내는 건 빵집의 늙은 밴텀 닭 한 마리뿐이었어. 그런데 그놈마저도, 전에 살던 데서 못 먹고 지낸 탓에 목이 쉬어 있었지.\n\n집들 크기로 짐작하건대, 한때는 지금 사는 사람들보다 형편이 나은 이들이 세 들어 살았던 것 같아. 하지만 지금은 층별로, 또는 방별로 주 단위로 세를 놓고 있었어. 그래서 문마다 안에 든 방 수만큼이나 많은 문패와 초인종 손잡이가 거의 다닥다닥 붙어 있었지. 창문도 같은 이유로 생김새가 가지각색이었어. 쉽게 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 흔한 블라인드와 커튼으로 장식되어 있었거든. 그리고 문간마다 알록달록한 아이들과 온갖 크기의 맥주 단지가 잔뜩 막아서서, 도무지 지나다닐 수가 없을 지경이었어. 품에 안긴 갓난아기와 반 파인트들이 단지부터, 다 큰 처녀와 반 갤런들이 통까지 죄다 있었지.\n\n이런 집들 가운데 한 채의 일층 거실에 광고지 한 장이 붙어 있었어. 그 집은 어쩌면 이웃집들보다 조금 더 지저분했을지도 몰라. 초인종 손잡이도, 아이들도, 맥주 단지도 더 많았고, 바로 옆 큰 양조장에서 밤낮없이 쏟아져 나오는 짙고 검은 연기의 첫 줄기를 고스란히 들이마시는 자리였지. 그 광고지에는 이 집 안에 아직 세놓을 방이 하나 남았다고 적혀 있었어. 그런데 그 빈방이 몇 층에 있는지는, 아무리 셈 잘하는 아이라도 도무지 알아낼 도리가 없었을 거야. 부엌 창문의 빨래 짜는 기계부터 난간의 화분까지, 집 앞면 전체가 세입자가 많다는 증거를 죄다 내보이고 있었으니까.\n\n이 저택의 공용 계단은 휑하니 카펫 한 장 깔려 있지 않았어. 하지만 꼭대기까지 기어 올라가야 하는 호기심 많은 손님이라면, 방문은 다 닫혀 있어도 사는 사람들이 위로 갈수록 점점 더 가난해진다는 표시를 알아챘을 거야. 그러니까 일층 세입자들은 가구가 넉넉해서, 진짜 마호가니로 만든 낡은 마호가니 탁자를 바깥 층계참에 내놓고 있었어. 그 탁자는 필요할 때만 안으로 들여놨지. 이층에 가면 남아도는 가구가 낡은 전나무 의자 두 개로 확 줄어들었어. 그 가운데 뒷방 의자는 다리 하나가 떨어져 나가고 앉는 자리도 뻥 뚫려 있었지. 그 위층은 좀먹은 빨래통 하나밖에 더 자랑할 게 없었어. 그리고 다락방 층계참에는 깨진 물병 두 개와 부서진 구두약병 몇 개보다 값나가는 물건은 하나도 없었지.\n\n바로 이 다락방 층계참에서, 모나게 생긴 데다 얼굴이 네모진 한 남자가 걸음을 멈췄어. 나이가 지긋하고 행색이 초라했지. 그는 앞쪽 다락방 문을 따려고 멈춰 선 거였어. 안 그래도 녹슨 자물쇠 구멍에 더 녹슨 열쇠를 끼워 돌리는 일을 겨우 해치우고는, 법적인 주인이라도 되는 양 거드름을 피우며 안으로 들어갔어.\n\n이 사람은 짧고 뻣뻣한 빨간 머리 가발을 쓰고 있었는데, 모자랑 같이 벗어서 못에 걸어 두었어. 그러고는 그 자리에 더러운 무명 잠자리 모자를 눌러쓰고, 어두운 데서 손으로 더듬더듬 양초 토막 하나를 찾아냈지. 그런 다음 두 다락방을 가르는 칸막이벽을 두드리며, 노그스 씨한테 불 좀 있느냐고 큰 소리로 물었어.\n\n돌아온 소리는 외벽 욋가지와 회반죽에 막혀 잔뜩 먹먹했어. 게다가 말하는 사람이 머그잔이나 다른 무슨 술잔 속에서 소리를 내뱉은 것처럼 들리기도 했지. 하지만 그건 뉴먼의 목소리였고, 그렇다는 대답을 전해 왔어.\n\n\"고약한 밤이네요, 노그스 씨!\" 잠자리 모자를 쓴 남자가 양초에 불을 붙이려고 안으로 들어서며 말했어.\n\n\"비 와요?\" 뉴먼이 물었어.\n\n\"비 오느냐고요?\" 상대가 짜증스럽게 대꾸했어. \"흠뻑 젖었구먼.\"\n\n\"당신이나 나나 젖는 데 비가 많이 와야 하는 것도 아니잖소, 크롤 씨.\" 뉴먼이 자기 닳고 닳은 외투 깃에 손을 얹으며 말했어.\n\n\"그러니까 그게 더 약 오르는 거 아니오.\" 크롤 씨가 여전히 짜증스러운 말투로 말했어.\n\n낮게 투덜대며 불평을 내뱉는 이 남자는, 그 사납게 생긴 얼굴이 이기심을 그대로 빼다 박은 것 같았어. 그는 얼마 안 되는 불씨를 화로에서 거의 다 긁어내고는, 노그스가 밀어 준 잔을 쭉 비우더니 석탄을 어디다 두느냐고 물었어.\n\n뉴먼 노그스가 찬장 밑을 가리키자, 크롤 씨가 부삽을 움켜쥐고 쟁여 둔 석탄의 절반을 불에 끼얹었어. 그러자 노그스는 아무 말 없이 아주 천천히 그걸 도로 걷어 냈지.\n\n\"설마 이 나이에 와서 갑자기 짠돌이가 된 건 아니겠죠?\" 크롤이 말했어.\n\n뉴먼은 그런 혐의를 반박하기에 충분하다는 듯 빈 잔을 가리켰어. 그러고는 아래층에 저녁 먹으러 내려갈 참이라고 짤막하게 말했지.\n\n\"켄위그스네로요?\" 크롤이 물었어.\n\n뉴먼이 그렇다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였어.\n\n\"이거 참, 생각 좀 해 보쇼!\" 크롤이 말했어. \"내가 글쎄, 당신이 안 간다고 했으니 분명 안 갈 줄 알고 말이지, 켄위그스한테는 못 간다고 해 놓고, 저녁을 당신이랑 같이 보내기로 마음먹었단 말이오!\"\n\n\"나는 갈 수밖에 없었소.\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"그쪽에서 한사코 오라고 했거든.\"\n\n\"그래, 그럼 나는 어쩌라고요?\" 남 생각이라고는 도무지 할 줄 모르는 이 이기적인 남자가 졸라 댔어. \"다 당신 잘못이오. 내가 어떻게 할 거냐면 말이지, 당신이 돌아올 때까지 당신 불가에 앉아 있겠소.\"\n\n뉴먼은 얼마 안 되는 자기 땔감을 절망스럽게 바라봤어. 하지만 안 된다고 말할 용기가 없었지. 평생 단 한 번도, 자기한테든 남한테든, 그 말을 제때 해 본 적이 없는 사람이었거든. 그래서 제안받은 대로 양보하고 말았어. 크롤 씨는 곧장 뉴먼 노그스의 살림을 가지고, 형편이 허락하는 한 최대한 편안하게 자리를 잡았지.\n\n크롤이 \"켄위그스네\"라고 부른 그 세입자들은, 켄위그스 씨라는 사람의 아내와 자식들이었어. 켄위그스 씨는 상아를 깎는 선반공이었는데, 이 집에서 꽤나 한자리하는 사람으로 여겨졌지. 방 두 개가 죽 이어진 일층 전체를 다 차지하고 살았으니까. 켄위그스 부인도 몸가짐이 영락없는 귀부인이었고 집안도 아주 점잖았어. 수도세를 걷는 삼촌이 있었거든. 게다가 이런 영예 말고도, 어린 딸들 가운데 큰 두 아이가 일주일에 두 번씩 동네 무용 학원에 다녔지. 아이들은 아마빛 머리를 파란 리본으로 묶어 등 뒤로 탐스러운 댕기를 늘어뜨렸고, 발목에 주름 장식이 달린 자그마한 흰 바지를 입고 다녔어. 이런 이유들 때문에, 그리고 여기 다 적기엔 너무 많지만 똑같이 그럴듯한 다른 이유들 때문에, 켄위그스 부인은 알고 지낼 만한 아주 좋은 사람으로 여겨졌어. 그래서 거리의 온갖 수다쟁이들 입에, 심지어 양쪽 모퉁이를 돌아 서너 집 떨어진 데서까지 늘 오르내리는 화젯거리였지.\n\n마침 그날은 법으로 정해진 영국 국교회가 켄위그스 부인을 켄위그스 씨에게 점지해 준, 그 행복한 날의 기념일이었어. 그래서 켄위그스 부인은 그날을 고맙게 기리려고, 가려 뽑은 친구 몇몇을 일층으로 불러 카드놀이와 저녁 식사 자리를 마련했어. 손님을 맞으려고 새 드레스도 차려입었지. 그 드레스는 색이 화려하고 젊은 취향으로 만든 거였는데, 어찌나 잘 어울렸던지 켄위그스 씨는 결혼 팔 년에 아이 다섯이 다 한바탕 꿈만 같다고, 켄위그스 부인이 함께 사귀던 첫 일요일보다도 더 젊고 화사해 보인다고 했어.\n\n켄위그스 부인이 차려입은 모습은 정말 아름다웠어. 게다가 어찌나 위엄이 넘치던지, 적어도 요리사 하나에 가정부 하나쯤은 두고 그저 부리기만 하면 되는 사람처럼 보일 정도였지. 그래도 음식 준비하느라 어찌나 고생을 했던지. 정말이지, 곱고 점잖은 체질인 그가 살림하는 자부심에 떠받쳐지지 않았더라면 도저히 버텨 내지 못했을 만큼 말이야. 그래도 마침내 모아 와야 할 것들은 다 모아 왔고, 치워야 할 것들은 다 치웠고, 모든 게 준비됐어. 게다가 수도세 걷는 삼촌까지 직접 오겠다고 약속했으니, 이번 일에는 행운이 미소를 지어 준 셈이었지.\n\n손님은 더없이 잘 골라 놨어. 우선 켄위그스 씨와 켄위그스 부인, 그리고 저녁 식탁에 늦게까지 앉아 있는 켄위그스네 어린 네 자식이 있었지. 첫째로는, 이런 날이니 아이들도 한턱 누리는 게 마땅했기 때문이고, 둘째로는 손님들 앞에서 아이들을 재우러 보내는 게 불편한 데다, 점잖지 못하다고까지 할 수 있었기 때문이야. 그다음에는 켄위그스 부인의 드레스를 만들어 준 젊은 아가씨가 있었어. 이 아가씨는 세상에 더없이 편리하게도 뒤쪽 삼층 방에 살아서, 자기 침대를 아기한테 내주고 작은 여자아이를 하나 구해다 아기를 지켜보게 했지. 그리고 이 아가씨와 짝이 맞게, 켄위그스 씨가 총각이던 시절부터 알고 지낸 젊은 남자도 있었어. 이 남자는 바람둥이라는 평판이 있어서 부인들 사이에서 꽤 인기가 많았지. 여기에 갓 결혼한 부부도 더해졌는데, 켄위그스 부부가 연애하던 시절에 둘을 찾아온 적이 있는 사람들이었어. 또 켄위그스 부인의 여동생도 있었는데, 정말이지 미인이었지. 그 밖에도 또 다른 젊은 남자가 하나 있었는데, 방금 말한 그 아가씨한테 진지한 마음을 품고 있다는 사람이었어. 그리고 노그스 씨도 있었는데, 한때 신사였던 사람이라 초대할 만한 점잖은 인물이었지. 또 뒷방에 사는 나이 지긋한 부인도 한 사람 있었고, 젊은 아가씨가 또 하나 있었어. 이 아가씨는 어쩌면 수도세 걷는 삼촌 다음으로 이 자리의 큰 구경거리였는데, 무언극에 \"출연\"하는 극장 소방수의 딸이었거든. 게다가 전에 없던 무대 재능을 타고나서, 노래며 낭송이며 켄위그스 부인의 눈에 눈물이 핑 돌게 할 만큼 잘했지. 이렇게 좋은 친구들을 보는 즐거움에 딱 한 가지 흠이 있었어. 뒷방에 사는 그 부인이, 살이 아주 많이 찌고 예순이 넘은 양반이 가슴이 깊이 팬 얇은 모슬린 드레스에 짧은 새끼 염소 가죽 장갑을 끼고 온 거였어. 켄위그스 부인은 어찌나 약이 올랐던지, 손님들한테 살그머니 장담하기를, 마침 그때 저녁 음식이 뒷방 화로에서 익고 있는 중만 아니었다면 분명 그 양반한테 물러가 달라고 했을 거라고 했지.\n\n\"여보,\" 켄위그스 씨가 말했어. \"이제 다 같이 하는 놀이를 시작하는 게 낫지 않겠소?\"\n\n\"켄위그스, 여보,\" 아내가 대꾸했어. \"당신한테 놀랐어요. 삼촌도 없이 시작하려고요?\"\n\n\"수도세 삼촌을 깜빡했군.\" 켄위그스가 말했어. \"아, 안 되지, 그건 절대 안 될 일이오.\"\n\n\"삼촌이 어찌나 까다로운지,\" 켄위그스 부인이 갓 결혼한 다른 부인 쪽으로 돌아보며 말했어. \"삼촌 없이 시작했다간 유언장에서 영영 빼 버릴 거예요.\"\n\n\"어머나!\" 갓 결혼한 부인이 소리쳤어.\n\n\"삼촌이 어떤 사람인지 모르실 거예요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 대답했어. \"그래도 세상에 둘도 없이 착한 분이지요.\"\n\n\"세상에 그렇게 마음씨 고운 사람도 없지요.\" 켄위그스가 말했어.\n\n\"돈을 안 내는 사람들 수돗물을 끊어야 할 때면, 그분 가슴이 아플 거라니까요.\" 총각 친구가 농담 삼아 한마디 했어.\n\n\"조지,\" 켄위그스 씨가 엄숙하게 말했어. \"제발 그런 소린 좀 그만하게.\"\n\n\"그냥 농담이었네.\" 친구가 머쓱해져서 말했어.\n\n\"조지,\" 켄위그스 씨가 받아쳤어. \"농담이야 아주 좋은 거지. 아주 좋은 거야. 하지만 그 농담이 켄위그스 부인의 기분을 상하게 하는 거라면, 나는 단호히 반대하네. 공직에 몸담은 사람이라면 비웃음을 사는 거야 으레 있는 일이지. 그건 그 사람의 높으신 지위 탓이지, 그 사람 잘못이 아니야. 켄위그스 부인의 친척은 공직에 계신 분이고, 그건 본인도 아는 일이지, 조지. 그리고 그건 견딜 수 있는 일이고. 하지만 켄위그스 부인은 차치하고라도—이런 자리에서 내가 켄위그스 부인을 차치할 수 있을지 모르겠다만—나는 영광스럽게도 그 수도세 어른과 결혼으로 맺어진 사이란 말일세. 그러니 나는 내…에서 그런 말을 그냥 둘 수가 없네.\" 켄위그스 씨는 \"집\"이라고 말하려다가, \"거처\"라는 말로 문장을 마무리했어.\n\n이 말이 끝나자 켄위그스 부인은 북받치는 감정을 드러냈고, 그 자리의 사람들한테는 수도세 어른의 위엄을 깊이 새기게 하려던 의도가 그대로 먹혀들었어. 그때 초인종 울리는 소리가 들렸지.\n\n\"그분이야.\" 켄위그스 씨가 잔뜩 들떠서 속삭였어. \"모를리나, 얘야, 어서 내려가서 삼촌을 들여라. 문 열자마자 바로 입맞춰 드리고. 흠! 자, 이야기들 좀 합시다.\"\n\n켄위그스 씨가 시킨 대로, 손님들은 편안하고 거리낌 없어 보이려고 아주 큰 소리로 떠들었어. 그런데 그렇게 떠들기 시작하자마자, 칙칙한 색 옷에 각반을 두른 키 작은 노신사가 모를리나 켄위그스 양의 손에 이끌려 장난스럽게 들어왔어. 그 노신사는 얼굴이 어찌나 무뚝뚝한지, 달리 볼 게 없으면 그냥 유창목을 깎아 만든 거라고 해도 믿을 정도였지. 그건 그렇고 모를리나라는 이 희한한 세례명에 대해 한마디 해 두자면, 켄위그스 부인이 첫 출산을 앞두고 직접 지어 만든 이름이었어. 혹시 첫째가 딸이면 그 아이만의 특별한 이름으로 쓰려고 말이야.\n\n\"어머, 삼촌, 정말 너무 반가워요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 수도세 어른의 양쪽 뺨에 다정하게 입을 맞추며 말했어. \"정말이지 반가워요!\"\n\n\"오늘 같은 좋은 날이 해마다 돌아오길 빈다, 얘야.\" 수도세 어른이 인사에 답하며 말했어.\n\n자, 이건 흥미로운 일이었어. 여기 수도세 걷는 사람이, 장부도 없이, 펜과 잉크도 없이, 두 번 쾅쾅 두드리는 노크도 없이, 으름장도 없이, 상냥한 여인에게 입을 맞추고 있었으니까. 정말로 입을 맞추고 있었지. 세금이며, 소환장이며, 들렀다 갔다는 통지서며, 두 분기치 밀린 돈 때문에 다시는 안 들르겠다는 고지서 따위는 죄다 까맣게 잊고서 말이야. 손님들이 그 광경에 완전히 빠져들어 구경하는 모습이며, 세금 걷는 사람한테 이렇게 인간미가 넘치는 걸 보고 흡족해하며 주고받는 끄덕임과 눈짓을 보는 건 흐뭇한 일이었어.\n\n\"어디 앉으시겠어요, 삼촌?\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했어. 이 대단한 친척이 나타난 덕에 집안에 대한 자부심으로 한껏 달아올라 있었지.\n\n\"아무 데나 좋다, 얘야.\" 수도세 어른이 말했어. \"나는 가리는 게 없어.\"\n\n가리는 게 없다니! 어쩌면 이렇게 겸손한 수도세 어른인지! 제 분수를 아는 작가였다 해도 이보다 더 겸손하진 못했을 거야.\n\n\"릴리빅 씨,\" 켄위그스가 수도세 어른을 부르며 말했어. \"여기 친구들 몇이 영광스럽게도 인사를 무척—고맙습니다—이쪽은 커틀러 부부입니다, 릴리빅 씨.\"\n\n\"뵙게 되어 영광입니다, 선생님.\" 커틀러 씨가 말했어. \"말씀 자주 들었습니다.\" 이건 그냥 예의상 하는 말이 아니었어. 커틀러 씨가 릴리빅 씨가 맡은 교구에서 살림을 차린 적이 있어서, 정말이지 그분 이야기를 아주 자주 들었거든. 들렀다 가시는 그 정성이 어찌나 대단했던지.\n\n\"조지는 아실 거예요, 릴리빅 씨.\" 켄위그스가 말했어. \"아래층 부인이세요—릴리빅 씨. 스뉴크스 씨—릴리빅 씨. 그린 양—릴리빅 씨. 릴리빅 씨—드루리 레인 왕립 극장의 페토커 양입니다. 공직에 계신 두 분이 인사를 나누시게 되어 정말 기쁩니다! 켄위그스 부인, 여보, 패를 좀 나눠 줄 텐가?\"\n\n켄위그스 부인은 뉴먼 노그스의 도움을 받아 시키는 대로 했어. 뉴먼은 늘 때를 가리지 않고 아이들한테 자질구레한 친절을 베풀어 온 터라, 자기를 신경 쓰지 말아 달라는 부탁이 받아들여졌지. 그래서 그저 \"한물간 신사\"라고 귓속말로나 입에 오를 뿐이었어. 손님들 대부분은 자리에 앉아 카드놀이를 했고, 뉴먼 본인과 켄위그스 부인, 그리고 드루리 레인 왕립 극장의 페토커 양은 저녁 식탁을 챙겼어.\n\n부인들이 이렇게 부산을 떠는 동안, 릴리빅 씨는 한창 벌어지는 놀이에 푹 빠져 있었어. 수도세 걷는 사람의 그물에 걸리는 건 죄다 제 고기인 법이니, 이 사랑스러운 노신사는 남의 패를 제 것으로 챙기는 데 조금도 거리낌이 없었지. 오히려 기회만 났다 하면 슬쩍슬쩍 가로채면서, 그러는 내내 사람 좋게 싱글벙글 웃었어. 게다가 그 임자들한테 너그러운 말을 어찌나 많이 건네던지, 사람들은 그 다정함에 흠뻑 빠져서 속으로 이 양반은 적어도 재무부 장관쯤은 될 만하다고 여겼지.\n\n한참 애를 쓰고, 켄위그스네 어린것들 머리통을 여러 번 찰싹찰싹 때린 끝에—그 가운데 제일 말 안 듣는 둘은 즉결로 쫓겨났지—식탁보가 아주 우아하게 깔렸어. 그리고 삶은 닭 두 마리에 큼지막한 돼지고기 한 덩이, 사과 파이, 감자, 푸성귀가 차려졌지. 그 광경을 보고 훌륭하신 릴리빅 씨는 재담을 잔뜩 쏟아 내며 놀랄 만큼 기운을 차렸어. 따르는 무리 전체의 한없는 기쁨과 만족을 자아내면서 말이야.\n\n저녁 식사는 아주 순조롭고 빠르게 흘러갔어. 깨끗한 나이프와 포크를 끊임없이 달라는 통에 생기는 일 말고는 더 심각한 어려움은 없었지. 그 바람에 가엾은 켄위그스 부인은 한 번 이상 이런 생각을 했어. 사적인 모임도 학교 방식을 따라서, 손님마다 자기 나이프와 포크, 숟가락을 챙겨 오게 했으면 좋겠다고 말이야. 그렇게 하면 분명 여러 경우에 큰 도움이 될 텐데, 누구보다도 집주인 부부한테 그럴 거야. 특히 그 학교 방식을 끝까지 밀어붙여서, 예의상 그 물건들을 도로 가져가지 않는 게 당연하다고 친다면 더더욱 그렇겠지.\n\n다들 차려진 음식을 죄다 먹어 치우자, 식탁은 무시무시할 만큼 허둥지둥, 또 요란한 소리를 내며 치워졌어. 그리고 뉴먼 노그스의 눈이 반짝거리게 만든 술이, 뜨거운 물과 찬물과 함께 가지런히 차려졌지. 손님들은 흥겹게 놀 채비를 했어. 릴리빅 씨는 난롯가의 커다란 안락의자에 자리를 잡았고, 켄위그스네 어린 넷은 손님들 앞 작은 긴 의자에 아마빛 댕기를 사람들 쪽으로 늘어뜨리고 얼굴은 불을 향한 채 앉았어. 이렇게 자리가 다 잡히자마자, 켄위그스 부인은 어머니로서의 감정에 북받쳐 켄위그스 씨의 왼쪽 어깨에 쓰러지며 눈물을 펑펑 쏟았지.\n\n\"애들이 너무 예뻐요!\" 켄위그스 부인이 흐느끼며 말했어.\n\n\"어머나,\" 부인들이 다 같이 말했어. \"정말 그래요! 자랑스러우신 게 당연하지요. 하지만 너무 마음 쓰지 마세요, 진정하세요.\"\n\n\"어쩔 수가 없어요, 그래도 아무 소용없지만.\" 켄위그스 부인이 흐느꼈어. \"아! 살기엔 너무 예뻐요, 너무 예쁘다고요!\"\n\n이 아이들이 어린 꽃 같은 시절에 일찍 죽을 운명이라는 무시무시한 예감을 듣자, 어린 딸 넷이 일제히 끔찍한 울음을 터뜨렸어. 그러고는 동시에 어머니 무릎에 머리를 파묻고, 아마빛 댕기 여덟 가닥이 다시 부르르 떨릴 때까지 빽빽 울어 댔지. 그동안 켄위그스 부인은 아이들을 번갈아 가슴에 끌어안았어. 페토커 양마저도 그대로 본떴을 법한, 넋이 나간 듯한 몸짓으로 말이야.\n\n마침내 애가 탄 어머니는 달램을 받아 좀 더 차분한 상태가 됐어. 켄위그스네 어린것들도 진정되자, 켄위그스 부인이 다시 그 한데 모인 미모의 광채에 압도되는 일이 없도록, 아이들을 손님들 사이로 나눠 앉혔지. 그러고 나자 신사 숙녀들이 입을 모아 예언했어. 이 아이들은 오래오래, 아주 오래 살 거라고, 켄위그스 부인이 마음 졸일 까닭이 조금도 없다고 말이야. 사실 정말로 그럴 까닭은 없어 보였어. 아이들이 예쁘다고 해서 그 걱정이 들어맞을 리는 도무지 없었으니까.\n\n\"이날이 팔 년 전이로군.\" 켄위그스 씨가 잠시 말이 없다가 입을 열었어. \"이런—아!\"\n\n이 회상에 그 자리 사람들이 모두 맞장구를 쳤어. 먼저 \"아!\" 하더니, 뒤이어 \"이런\" 하고들 말했지.\n\n\"그때 난 더 젊었지요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 까르르 웃으며 말했어.\n\n\"아닐세.\" 수도세 어른이 말했어.\n\n\"그럼요, 절대 아니지요.\" 모두가 거들었어.\n\n\"내 조카가 기억나는군.\" 릴리빅 씨가 청중을 진중한 표정으로 둘러보며 말했어. \"기억나고말고, 바로 그날 오후에 그 애가 처음으로 제 어미한테 켄위그스가 좋다고 털어놓던 게 말이야. '어머니,' 그 애가 그러더군. '저 그이를 사랑해요.'\"\n\n\"'그이를 흠모해요'라고 했어요, 삼촌.\" 켄위그스 부인이 끼어들었어.\n\n\"'사랑해요'였던 것 같은데, 얘야.\" 수도세 어른이 단호하게 말했어.\n\n\"삼촌 말씀이 맞을지도 모르겠네요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 고분고분 대답했어. \"전 '흠모해요'였던 것 같았는데.\"\n\n\"'사랑해요'였다, 얘야.\" 릴리빅 씨가 받아쳤어. \"'어머니,' 그 애가 그러더군, '저 그이를 사랑해요!' '내가 지금 무슨 소리를 듣는 거냐?' 제 어미가 소리치더니, 그 자리에서 심한 경련을 일으키고 말았지.\"\n\n그 자리 사람들이 일제히 놀라움의 탄성을 터뜨렸어.\n\n\"심한 경련을 말일세.\" 릴리빅 씨가 사람들을 굳은 눈길로 바라보며 되풀이했어. \"친구들 앞에서 이런 말을 해도 켄위그스가 이해해 주겠지만, 그땐 그 사람을 두고 아주 큰 반대가 있었네. 우리 집안에 비해 격이 떨어진다, 집안 망신이다, 그런 이유로 말이야. 기억하나, 켄위그스?\"\n\n\"물론이지요.\" 켄위그스 씨가 대답했어. 이 회상이 켄위그스 부인이 얼마나 대단한 집안 출신인지를 더없이 분명하게 증명해 주는 거라, 조금도 언짢아하지 않으면서 말이야.\n\n\"나도 그 마음에 같이했지.\" 릴리빅 씨가 말했어. \"그게 당연했을 수도 있고, 아닐 수도 있고.\"\n\n부드러운 웅성거림이, 릴리빅 씨 정도 되는 지위에서는 그런 반대가 당연할 뿐 아니라 크게 칭찬받을 만한 일이었다고 말하는 듯했어.\n\n\"나도 때가 되니 그 사람을 받아들였네.\" 릴리빅 씨가 말했어. \"둘이 결혼하고 나서, 이제 어쩔 도리가 없게 되니, 켄위그스를 챙겨 줘야 한다고 제일 먼저 나선 사람 가운데 하나가 나였지. 그래서 집안에서도 정말로 그 사람을 챙겨 주었네. 내가 그렇게 말했으니까 말이야. 그리고 나는 꼭 말해야겠고—말하게 되어 자랑스럽고—그동안 나는 늘 그 사람이 아주 정직하고, 행실 바르고, 올곧고, 점잖은 사람이라는 걸 알게 되었네. 켄위그스, 악수하세.\"\n\n\"영광입니다, 선생님.\" 켄위그스 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"나도 그렇네, 켄위그스.\" 릴리빅 씨가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"댁의 조카와 아주 행복하게 살아왔습니다, 선생님.\" 켄위그스가 말했어.\n\n\"안 그랬다면 그게 다 자네 잘못이었겠지, 선생.\" 릴리빅 씨가 한마디 했어.\n\n\"모를리나 켄위그스,\" 그 어미가 이 대목에서 한껏 감격해 소리쳤어. \"사랑하는 삼촌께 입맞춰 드려라!\"\n\n이 아가씨는 시키는 대로 했고, 다른 어린 딸 셋도 차례차례 수도세 어른의 얼굴까지 들어 올려져 똑같은 절차를 치렀어. 그러고 나서는 그 자리에 있던 사람들 대부분이 또 그 아이들한테 그걸 되풀이했지.\n\n\"어머나, 켄위그스 부인,\" 페토커 양이 말했어. \"노그스 씨가 그 행복한 날을 기리며 마실 펀치를 만드는 동안, 모를리나한테 릴리빅 씨 앞에서 그 무용을 한번 추게 해 보세요.\"\n\n\"아녜요, 안 돼요, 얘.\" 켄위그스 부인이 대답했어. \"삼촌만 성가시게 할 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"성가실 리가 없잖아요, 분명히.\" 페토커 양이 말했어. \"아주 흐뭇해하실 거예요, 안 그러세요, 선생님?\"\n\n\"암, 그러고말고.\" 수도세 어른이 펀치 만드는 사람 쪽을 흘끗 보며 대답했어.\n\n\"그럼 이렇게 하지요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했어. \"모를리나가 춤을 출 테니, 대신 삼촌이 페토커 양을 설득해서 그다음에 '피를 마시는 자의 장례'를 낭송하게 해 주세요.\"\n\n이 제안에 손뼉을 치고 발을 구르는 소리가 요란하게 일었어. 그 주인공인 페토커 양은 환대에 답하느라 고개를 여러 번 살포시 숙였지.\n\n\"아시잖아요,\" 페토커 양이 나무라듯 말했어. \"저는 사적인 모임에서 무대 일 비슷한 걸 하는 걸 싫어한다는 거.\"\n\n\"어머, 여기선 괜찮아요!\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했어. \"우리 다들 어찌나 정답고 즐거운지, 그냥 댁의 방에서 하는 거나 마찬가지일 거예요. 게다가 이런 날이니—\"\n\n\"그럼 거절할 수가 없네요.\" 페토커 양이 끼어들었어. \"제 보잘것없는 재주로 할 수 있는 거라면 뭐든 기꺼이 하지요.\"\n\n켄위그스 부인과 페토커 양은 둘이서 작은 공연 순서를 미리 짜 두었는데, 이게 바로 정해진 차례였어. 그래도 양쪽에서 조금씩 권하는 척하기로 미리 해 둔 거였지. 그래야 더 자연스러워 보이거든. 손님들이 다 채비를 마치자, 페토커 양이 가락을 흥얼거렸고 모를리나가 춤을 추었어. 미리 신발 바닥에 분필 가루를 묻혀 두었는데, 마치 줄타기라도 하러 나가는 양 꼼꼼하게 발랐지. 아주 아름다운 춤이었고, 팔 동작이 상당히 많았어. 그리고 끝없는 박수갈채를 받았지.\n\n\"제게 만약 저런 재능을 타고난—아—아이가 있었다면,\" 페토커 양이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했어. \"당장 오페라 무대에 올렸을 거예요.\"\n\n켄위그스 부인이 한숨을 쉬며 켄위그스 씨를 바라봤어. 그러자 켄위그스 씨는 고개를 저으며, 그건 좀 어떨지 모르겠다고 했지.\n\n\"켄위그스가 걱정하는 거예요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"뭘 걱정하는데요?\" 페토커 양이 물었어. \"설마 실패할까 봐서는 아니죠?\"\n\n\"아, 그건 아녜요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 대답했어. \"하지만 이 애가 지금 모습 그대로 자라난다면—젊은 공작이며 후작 나리들을 한번 생각해 봐요.\"\n\n\"맞는 말이오.\" 수도세 어른이 말했어.\n\n\"그래도,\" 페토커 양이 조심스레 말했어. \"이 애가 스스로를 제대로 소중히 여길 줄만 안다면, 그러니까—\"\n\n\"그 말에도 일리가 많네요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 남편을 보며 말했어.\n\n\"저는 다만—\" 페토커 양이 머뭇거렸어. \"물론 다 그런 건 아닐 수도 있지만—저는 그런 종류의 불편한 일이나 거북한 일은 한 번도 겪어 본 적이 없거든요.\"\n\n켄위그스 씨는 신사다운 정중함으로, 그 말로 이 문제는 단번에 정리됐다고, 이 일을 진지하게 고려해 보겠다고 했어. 이렇게 결정이 나자, 사람들은 페토커 양한테 '피를 마시는 자의 장례'를 시작해 달라고 청했지. 그러자 이 아가씨는 뒷머리를 풀어 내리고, 방 저쪽 끝에 자리를 잡았어. 총각 친구는 한쪽 구석에 서 있다가 \"죽음 속에 숨을 거두니\"라는 신호에 뛰쳐나가, 페토커 양이 미쳐 날뛰며 죽을 때 두 팔로 받아 안기로 했지. 페토커 양은 놀라운 기백으로 그 공연을 해냈어. 켄위그스네 어린것들이 거의 기겁할 만큼 무서워서 발작을 일으킬 뻔할 정도로 말이야.\n\n이 공연이 자아낸 황홀경은 아직 가라앉지 않았어. 그리고 뉴먼은—이렇게 늦은 시각에 이만큼 멀쩡하게 깬 적이 정말이지 오래오래간만이었는데—펀치가 다 됐다는 말을 미처 한마디도 끼워 넣지 못한 참이었지. 그때 방문을 다급하게 두드리는 소리가 들렸고, 켄위그스 부인이 비명을 질렀어. 아기가 침대에서 떨어졌다고 대번에 직감했거든.\n\n\"누구요?\" 켄위그스 씨가 날카롭게 물었어.\n\n\"놀라지 마쇼, 나요.\" 크롤이 잠자리 모자를 쓴 채 안을 들여다보며 말했어. \"아기는 아주 잘 있어요. 내려오다가 방을 살짝 들여다봤는데, 곤히 자고 있고 그 여자아이도 자고 있더군. 그리고 방에 바람만 안 들어오면 촛불이 침대 커튼에 옮겨붙을 일도 없을 거요—노그스 씨를 찾는 거요.\"\n\n\"나를요!\" 뉴먼이 깜짝 놀라 소리쳤어.\n\n\"이거, 좀 이상한 시각이지요, 안 그래요?\" 자기 불을 잃을 처지가 영 마뜩잖은 크롤이 대꾸했어. \"게다가 사람들 행색도 이상하더군요, 죄다 비랑 진흙투성이라. 가라고 할까요?\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 뉴먼이 일어서며 말했어. \"사람들이라고요? 몇이나?\"\n\n\"둘이요.\" 크롤이 대답했어.\n\n\"나를 찾는다고요? 이름까지 대고?\" 뉴먼이 물었어.\n\n\"이름까지 대고요.\" 크롤이 대답했어. \"뉴먼 노그스 씨라고, 아주 또박또박.\"\n\n뉴먼은 몇 초쯤 생각에 잠겼다가, 곧 돌아오겠다고 중얼거리며 서둘러 나갔어. 그러고는 정말 말한 대로였지. 눈 깜짝할 새에 다시 방으로 뛰어들더니, 미안하다거나 무슨 일이라거나 한마디 말도 없이 식탁에서 불 켜진 양초 하나와 뜨거운 펀치 한 잔을 낚아채고는, 미친 사람처럼 쏜살같이 사라져 버렸어.\n\n\"대체 저 사람한테 무슨 일이 생긴 거야?\" 크롤이 문을 활짝 열어젖히며 소리쳤어. \"쉿! 위에서 무슨 소리 안 나요?\"\n\n손님들은 잔뜩 어수선해져 일어났어. 그러고는 어쩔 줄 모르겠다는, 또 조금은 겁에 질린 얼굴로 서로를 마주 보다가, 목을 앞으로 쭉 빼고 귀를 기울였지.\n\n# CHAPTER 15\n\n지난 장에서 그려진 그 소동이 어떻게, 왜 일어났는지 독자에게 알려 주고, 더불어 꼭 알아야 할 몇 가지 다른 일들도 들려준다\n\n뉴먼 노그스는 김이 모락모락 나는 그 음료를 들고 무섭게 서둘러 위층으로 허둥지둥 올라갔다. 그건 케이트… 아니, 케닉스 씨네 식탁에서, 그것도 수도세 징수원의 바로 손아귀에서 인사도 없이 낚아챈 것이었다. 음료를 빼앗긴 그 순간, 징수원은 잔 속의 내용물을 흐뭇한 표정 가득한 얼굴로 들여다보고 있던 참이었다. 뉴먼은 그 전리품을 곧장 자기 뒤편 다락방으로 가져갔다. 거기에는 니콜라스와 스마이크가 앉아 있었다. 그의 수고의 원인이자 동행자였던 둘은 발이 부르트고 신발도 다 떨어져 가는 데다, 비에 젖고 더럽고 지친 모습이었다. 고된 여행의 온갖 흔적을 다 뒤집어쓰고 있었지. 익숙지 않은 데다 오래 이어진 강행군에 두 사람 다 녹초가 되어 있었다.\n\n뉴먼이 맨 먼저 한 일은, 거의 끓을 듯이 뜨거운 펀치의 절반을 니콜라스에게 부드럽게 떠밀어 단숨에 삼키게 한 것이었다. 그다음에는 나머지를 스마이크의 목구멍에 들이부었다. 스마이크는 평생 변비약보다 독한 건 입에 대 본 적이 없는 터라, 술이 목을 타고 넘어가는 동안 놀라움과 즐거움이 뒤섞인 갖가지 묘한 반응을 보였다. 그리고 다 넘어가고 나자 두 눈을 한껏 위로 치켜떴다.\n\n\"흠뻑 젖었구나.\" 뉴먼이 니콜라스가 벗어 던진 외투에 황급히 손을 대 보며 말했다. \"그런데 나는… 나는… 갈아입을 옷도 없어.\" 그가 자기가 입고 있는 초라한 옷을 안타까운 눈길로 내려다보며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"마른 옷이 있어요. 적어도 당장 쓸 만한 건 제 보따리에 있거든요.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했다. \"저를 보고 그렇게 안타까워하시면, 가뜩이나 마음 아픈 제가 더 괴로워져요. 단 하룻밤이라도 아저씨의 빠듯한 형편에 도움과 잠자리를 기댈 수밖에 없는 게 말이에요.\"\n\n니콜라스가 이런 식으로 말하니 뉴먼은 조금도 덜 안타까워 보이지 않았다. 하지만 젊은 친구가 그의 손을 진심으로 꼭 잡고는, 그가 진심으로 자기를 위하는 마음과 그 따뜻한 정을 굳게 믿었기에, 무슨 일이 있어도 자기가 런던에 왔다는 걸 알린 거라고 단단히 못을 박자, 노그스 씨는 다시 얼굴이 환해졌다. 그러고는 손님들이 편히 지낼 수 있도록 자기 힘 닿는 데까지 부지런히 채비를 갖추기 시작했다.\n\n채비라 해 봐야 아주 단출했다. 가엾은 뉴먼의 형편은 그 마음에 한참 못 미쳤으니까. 하지만 보잘것없는 채비였어도 부산하게 이리저리 뛰어다니지 않고는 마련할 수 없었다. 니콜라스가 얼마 안 되는 돈을 워낙 알뜰하게 아껴 둔 덕에 아직 다 떨어지지는 않았다. 그래서 빵과 치즈에다 음식점에서 사 온 찬 쇠고기까지 곁들인 저녁상이 금세 차려졌다. 거기에 독한 술 한 병과 흑맥주 한 단지까지 더해지니, 적어도 배고픔이나 목마름을 걱정할 일은 없었다. 손님들이 밤을 지낼 수 있도록 뉴먼이 마련할 수 있는 채비를 끝내는 데도 그리 오랜 시간이 걸리지는 않았다. 그리고 그는 먼저 한 가지를 못 박아 두었는데, 니콜라스는 옷을 갈아입고 스마이크는 자기 외투 하나를 걸치라는 것이었다(아무리 말려도 그는 그 외투를 벗어 주려고 기어이 벗어 버렸다). 그렇게 해서 나그네들은 그 소박한 음식을 나누어 먹었다. 적어도 둘 중 하나는 그동안 먹은 어떤 진수성찬보다도 더 만족스러워했다.\n\n이윽고 그들은 난롯가로 다가앉았다. 크롤이 땔감을 축내고 간 뒤에 뉴먼 노그스가 그나마 할 수 있는 만큼 불을 지펴 놓은 참이었다. 니콜라스는 그때까지 여행에 지친 몸을 먼저 추스르라는 친구의 극진한 걱정에 가로막혀 있었지만, 이제는 어머니와 누이동생에 대해 간절히 물어 대기 시작했다.\n\n\"음.\" 뉴먼이 늘 그러듯 말수 적게 대꾸했다. \"둘 다 잘 있어.\"\n\n\"아직 시내에 살고 계시죠?\" 니콜라스가 물었다.\n\n\"그래.\" 뉴먼이 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 제 누이동생은요.\" 니콜라스가 덧붙였다. \"편지로, 정말 마음에 들 것 같다던 그 일을 아직 하고 있나요?\"\n\n뉴먼은 여느 때보다 눈을 좀 더 크게 떴지만, 그저 숨을 한 번 헉 들이쉬는 것으로 대답을 대신했다. 그 숨소리는 함께 따라오는 고갯짓에 따라, 친구들이 예 또는 아니요로 알아듣곤 했다. 이번에는 그 무언극이 고개를 끄덕이는 쪽이었지 가로젓는 쪽은 아니었다. 그래서 니콜라스는 그 대답을 좋은 뜻으로 받아들였다.\n\n\"이제 제 말 좀 들어 보세요.\" 니콜라스가 뉴먼의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"두 사람을 만나려고 애쓰기 전에, 먼저 아저씨한테 오는 게 낫겠다고 생각했어요. 제 이기적인 바람을 채우려다 두 사람한테 영영 돌이킬 수 없는 해를 끼칠까 봐서요. 제 삼촌이 요크셔에서 무슨 소식을 들었나요?\"\n\n뉴먼은 입을 몇 번이고 벌렸다 닫았다 했다. 안간힘을 다해 말을 꺼내려는 것 같았지만 도무지 나오지 않았다. 그러더니 마침내 험상궂고 섬뜩한 눈길로 니콜라스를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"무슨 소식을 들었냐고요?\" 니콜라스가 얼굴을 붉히며 다그쳤다. \"보시다시피 저는 그 악의가 지어낼 수 있는 가장 나쁜 얘기까지도 다 들을 각오가 돼 있어요. 왜 저한테 숨기시는 거예요? 어차피 조만간 알게 될 텐데요. 절반의 시간이면 무슨 일이 있었는지 다 알 수 있는데, 몇 분이나 이렇게 질질 끌어서 무슨 소용이 있겠어요? 제발 당장 말해 주세요.\"\n\n\"내일 아침에.\" 뉴먼이 말했다. \"내일 듣게.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소용이 있다고요?\" 니콜라스가 다그쳤다.\n\n\"잠을 더 잘 잘 수 있을 테니까.\" 뉴먼이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"오히려 더 못 잘걸요.\" 니콜라스가 안달하며 대답했다. \"잠이라뇨! 이렇게 녹초가 되어 누구보다 쉬어야 할 처지인데도, 아저씨가 다 말해 주지 않으면 밤새 눈 한번 못 붙일 거예요.\"\n\n\"내가 다 말해 준다 해도 말이지.\" 뉴먼이 망설이며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼, 제 분노를 들끓게 하거나 제 자존심에 상처를 줄 수는 있겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 받아쳤다. \"하지만 제 잠을 깨뜨리지는 못해요. 그 일이 다시 한번 똑같이 벌어진다 해도, 저는 그때 했던 것과 똑같이 했을 테니까요. 그 일로 저한테 어떤 결과가 닥치든, 제가 한 일을 절대 후회하지 않을 거예요. 그 때문에 굶주리거나 빌어먹게 되더라도 절대로요. 가장 비열하고 가장 잔인한 비겁함이 주는 치욕에 비하면, 약간의 가난이나 고생쯤이야 뭐 대수겠어요! 단언하건대, 만약 제가 가만히 손 놓고 모른 척 서 있었다면, 저는 저 자신을 미워했을 거고, 세상 모든 사람한테 멸시받아 마땅했을 거예요. 그 시커먼 속을 가진 악당 같으니!\"\n\n자리에 없는 스퀴어스 씨를 이렇게 점잖게 빗대고 나서, 니콜라스는 끓어오르는 분노를 가라앉혔다. 그러고는 두더보이즈 홀에서 있었던 일을 뉴먼에게 그대로 들려준 다음, 더 다그치지 않을 테니 어서 털어놓으라고 간청했다. 이렇게 다짐을 받자 노그스 씨는 낡은 트렁크에서 종이 한 장을 꺼냈다. 몹시 서둘러 갈겨쓴 듯한 종이였다. 그리고 한참을 별나게 꺼리는 시늉을 하고 나서, 다음과 같이 입을 열었다.\n\n\"이보게 젊은 친구, 이런 일에 너무 휘둘리면 안 돼. 이런 식으론 절대 안 된다는 거, 자네도 알잖나. 세상에서 출세하는 거 말일세. 학대당하는 사람마다 편을 들고 다니면… 에라, 빌어먹을, 나는 그 얘기를 들으니 오히려 자랑스럽네. 나라도 그렇게 했을 거야!\"\n\n뉴먼은 이 아주 별난 격정을 식탁을 쾅 내리치는 것으로 마무리했다. 흥분한 나머지 그 식탁을 와크포드 스퀴어스 씨의 가슴팍이나 갈비뼈로 착각하기라도 한 듯했다. 이렇게 속마음을 대놓고 드러낸 통에, 노그스 씨는 니콜라스에게 신중한 세상살이 충고를 건넬 길을 스스로 막아 버린 셈이 되었지만(처음엔 그게 그의 속셈이었다), 그는 곧장 본론으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"그저께 말이야.\" 뉴먼이 말했다. \"자네 삼촌이 이 편지를 받았어. 삼촌이 나간 사이에 내가 후딱 베껴 뒀지. 읽어 줄까?\"\n\n\"네, 그래 주세요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다. 그러자 뉴먼 노그스는 이렇게 읽어 내려갔다.\n\"두더보이즈 홀에서, 목요일 아침.\n\n귀하께,\n아빠가 저더러 편지를 쓰라고 하셨어요. 의사 선생님들이 아빠가 다리를 다시 쓰실 수 있을지 어떨지 모르겠다고 해서요. 그래서 펜을 못 잡으세요.\n\n저희는 이루 말할 수 없는 심정이에요. 아빠는 온통 멍투성이에 파랗고 푸르딩딩하고, 의자도 두 개나 아빠의 피웅덩이에 처박혔어요. 저희는 어쩔 수 없이 아빠를 부엌으로 옮겨 모셨고, 지금 거기 누워 계세요. 이걸 보시면 아빠가 얼마나 형편없이 망가지셨는지 짐작하실 거예요.\n\n선생님이 교사라고 추천해 주신 그 조카가 아빠한테 이런 짓을 하고, 발로 아빠 몸 위를 짓밟고, 차마 제 펜으로 옮기기도 더러운 욕설까지 퍼붓고 나서는, 엄마한테 끔찍한 폭력을 휘둘렀어요. 엄마를 땅바닥에 내동댕이치고, 엄마 머리에 꽂혀 있던 빗을 몇 인치나 박아 넣었어요. 조금만 더했어도 머리뼈를 뚫고 들어갈 뻔했어요. 만약 그랬다면 그 거북등껍질 빗이 뇌까지 망가뜨렸을 거라는 의사 진단서도 있어요.\n\n그러고 나서 저랑 남동생이 그 광기의 희생양이 되었고, 그 뒤로 저희는 무척 고통스러웠어요. 그래서 저희 몸속 어딘가가 다쳤다는 가슴 아픈 결론에 이르렀어요. 특히 겉으로는 폭력의 흔적이 하나도 안 보이니 더 그래요. 저는 이 편지를 쓰는 내내 큰 소리로 울부짖고 있고, 남동생도 그러고 있어서 정신이 좀 산만해요. 그래서 틀린 데가 있어도 너그러이 봐 주셨으면 해요.\n\n그 괴물은 피에 대한 갈증을 다 채우고 나서 달아나 버렸어요. 자기가 부추겨 반항하게 만든 막돼먹은 성격의 남자애 하나랑, 엄마의 석류석 반지까지 가지고요. 그리고 순경들한테 붙잡히지 않은 걸 보니 어떤 역마차를 얻어 탄 게 분명해요. 아빠는, 만약 그자가 선생님께 찾아오거든 반지는 돌려받으시고, 그 도둑이자 살인자는 그냥 놓아 주십사 부탁하셨어요. 저희가 고소하면 그자는 기껏해야 유배나 갈 텐데, 그냥 놔두면 머지않아 틀림없이 교수형에 처해질 테니까요. 그게 저희 수고도 덜고 훨씬 더 후련할 거예요. 편하실 때 답장 주시길 바라며,\n\n이만 줄입니다. 패니 스퀴어스 올림.\n\n추신. 저는 그자의 무식함이 가엾고, 그자를 경멸해요.\"\n\n이 절묘한 편지를 다 읽고 나자 깊은 침묵이 뒤따랐다. 그동안 뉴먼 노그스는 편지를 접으면서, 거기 언급된 그 막돼먹은 성격의 남자애를 묘하게 가엾어하는 눈길로 바라보았다. 그 애는 지금 벌어지는 일에 대해, 자기가 불행히도 니콜라스에게 골칫거리와 거짓말을 잔뜩 안긴 빌미가 되었다는 것 말고는 뚜렷이 알지 못한 채, 더없이 처량하고 가슴 미어지는 표정으로 말없이 풀이 죽어 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"노그스 아저씨.\" 니콜라스가 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 말했다. \"저 당장 나가 봐야겠어요.\"\n\n\"나간다고!\" 뉴먼이 소리쳤다.\n\n\"네.\" 니콜라스가 말했다. \"골든 스퀘어로요. 저를 아는 사람이라면 누구도 이 반지 얘기를 믿지 않을 거예요. 하지만 랄프 니클비 씨한테는 이걸 믿는 척하는 게 그 속셈에 맞거나, 그 미움을 채워 줄지도 모르죠. 진실을 밝히는 건 마땅한 일이에요. 그 사람을 위해서가 아니라 저 자신을 위해서요. 게다가 그 사람한테 할 말이 한두 마디 있는데, 그건 식을 때까지 묵혀 둘 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"묵혀 둬야 해.\" 뉴먼이 말했다.\n\n\"정말이지 그럴 순 없어요.\" 니콜라스가 집을 나설 채비를 하며 단호하게 받아쳤다.\n\n\"내 말 좀 들어 봐.\" 뉴먼이 성급한 젊은 친구 앞을 가로막고 서며 말했다. \"그 사람은 거기 없어. 시내를 떠났거든. 사흘은 지나야 돌아올 거야. 그리고 그 편지에 대한 답장도 그자가 돌아오기 전엔 없을 게 분명해.\"\n\n\"확실해요?\" 니콜라스가 몹시 안달하며, 좁은 방을 성큼성큼 빠르게 오가며 물었다.\n\n\"아주 확실하지.\" 뉴먼이 받아쳤다. \"그자는 그 편지를 채 다 읽기도 전에 불려 나갔어. 그 내용을 아는 사람은 그자랑 우리밖에 없어.\"\n\n\"틀림없어요?\" 니콜라스가 다급하게 캐물었다. \"제 어머니나 누이동생도 모르고요? 만약 두 사람이… 그리로 가야겠어요. 만나 봐야겠어요. 어느 쪽이에요? 어디예요?\"\n\n\"자, 내 말대로 해.\" 뉴먼이 그 순간만큼은 간절한 마음에 여느 사람처럼 또박또박 말했다. \"그자가 돌아올 때까지는 두 사람조차 만나려고 하지 마. 나는 그 사람을 잘 알아. 자네가 누군가하고 미리 입을 맞춘 것처럼 보이게 하면 안 돼. 그자가 돌아오거든 곧장 찾아가서, 하고 싶은 만큼 당당하게 말하라고. 그자도 진실을 짐작하고 있고, 자네나 나만큼이나 잘 알고 있어. 그 점은 믿어도 돼.\"\n\n\"아저씨는 저를 위해 주시는 거고, 그 사람도 저보다 더 잘 아시겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 한참 생각하다 대답했다. \"좋아요. 그렇게 하죠.\"\n\n뉴먼은 앞선 대화 내내 등을 문에 딱 붙이고 서서, 필요하면 힘으로라도 방에서 나가지 못하게 막을 채비를 하고 있었는데, 이제 흡족한 마음으로 다시 자리에 앉았다. 그새 주전자의 물이 끓었기에, 그는 니콜라스에게는 잔에 술과 물을 타서 한 잔 만들어 주고, 자기와 스마이크가 함께 마실 몫으로는 금이 간 머그잔에 가득 타 주었다. 둘은 사이좋게 그것을 나누어 마셨고, 그동안 니콜라스는 한 손에 머리를 괴고 우울한 생각에 잠긴 채 앉아 있었다.\n\n한편 아래층의 손님들은, 귀를 쫑긋 세우고 들어 봐도 호기심을 채우려고 끼어들 만한 소리가 들리지 않자, 케닉스 씨네 방으로 돌아갔다. 그러고는 노그스 씨가 갑자기 사라져 붙들려 있는 까닭을 두고 온갖 추측을 늘어놓기 시작했다.\n\n\"어머, 내 말 좀 들어 봐요.\" 케닉스 부인이 말했다. \"혹시 그 사람 재산이 몽땅 도로 돌아왔다는 급한 전갈이 온 거면 어떡해요!\"\n\n\"이런.\" 케닉스 씨가 말했다. \"그럴 수도 있겠는걸. 그렇다면 사람을 올려 보내서 펀치를 좀 더 들지 않겠냐고 물어보는 게 낫겠어.\"\n\n\"케닉스!\" 릴리빅 씨가 큰 소리로 말했다. \"자네한테 놀랐네.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이세요, 어르신?\" 케닉스 씨가 수도세 징수원에게 걸맞은 공손함으로 물었다.\n\n\"그런 소릴 하다니, 이 사람아.\" 릴리빅 씨가 성을 내며 대꾸했다. \"그자는 이미 펀치를 마시지 않았나, 응? 내가 보기엔, 그 펀치를 그렇게 가로채 간 꼬락서니가, 이런 표현을 써도 된다면 말이지, 이 자리에 있는 모두를 아주 무시하는 짓이야. 괘씸하지, 아주 괘씸해. 이 집에선 그런 일을 그냥 넘기는 게 관습인지 몰라도, 내가 여태 보고 살아온 그런 행실은 아니야. 그러니 케닉스, 자네한테 한마디 해 두는 거네. 어떤 신사가 펀치 한 잔을 앞에 두고 막 입술을 갖다 대려는 참인데, 다른 사람이 와서 '실례합니다'라든가 '잠깐 좀 보겠습니다' 한마디 없이 그 잔을 덥석 움켜쥐고는 들고 가 버린단 말이야. 이게 좋은 예절일지도 모르지. 그럴 수도 있겠지. 하지만 난 도무지 이해를 못 하겠어, 그뿐이야. 게다가 앞으로도 영영 이해 못 한대도 상관없어. 나는 할 말은 하고 사는 사람이야, 케닉스. 이게 내 생각이야. 자네가 마음에 안 든다면, 평소 자던 시간도 지났으니, 더 늦기 전에 알아서 집에 갈 수 있네.\"\n\n이거 참 난감한 일이 벌어진 것이다! 징수원은 몇 분 동안 상한 위신을 안고 부글부글 끓어오르며 앉아 있다가, 이제 기어이 터뜨려 버린 것이었다. 그 대단한 인물, 그 돈 많은 친척, 그 결혼 안 한 외삼촌, 마음만 먹으면 모를리나를 상속녀로 만들 수도, 갓난아기까지 유산 상속인으로 삼을 수도 있는 그 사람이 단단히 삐친 것이다. 아이고 맙소사, 이 일을 대체 어떻게 마무리한단 말인가!\n\n\"정말 죄송합니다, 어르신.\" 케닉스 씨가 굽실거리며 말했다.\n\n\"죄송하단 소린 말게.\" 릴리빅 씨가 아주 날카롭게 받아쳤다. \"그럼 애초에 못 하게 막았어야지.\"\n\n손님들은 이 집안의 충돌에 그만 얼어붙고 말았다. 뒷방에서 온 부인은 입을 떡 벌린 채 넋이 나가, 어쩔 줄 모르고 멍하니 징수원을 쳐다보았다. 다른 손님들도 그 대단한 인물의 노여움에 못지않게 짓눌려 있었다. 케닉스 씨는 이런 일에 영 서툴렀던지라, 불을 끄려다 오히려 부채질만 하고 말았다.\n\n\"정말이지 거기까진 생각 못 했습니다, 어르신.\" 케닉스 씨가 말했다. \"펀치 한 잔 같은 그 사소한 일로 어르신께서 화가 나실 줄은 몰랐어요.\"\n\n\"화가 나? 케닉스, 그 무례한 소린 대체 무슨 뜻인가?\" 징수원이 말했다. \"모를리나, 얘야, 내 모자 가져오너라.\"\n\n\"어머, 가시면 안 돼요, 릴리빅 어르신.\" 페토커 양이 가장 매혹적인 미소를 지으며 끼어들었다.\n\n하지만 릴리빅 씨는 그 요부에게도 아랑곳없이 고집스럽게 외쳤다. \"모를리나, 내 모자!\" 그가 네 번째로 그렇게 다그치자 케닉스 부인은 의자에 풀썩 주저앉으며 울음을 터뜨렸는데, 그 소리는 수도세 징수원은 말할 것도 없고 빗물통이라도 누그러뜨릴 만했다. 그러는 사이 네 어린 딸들은(미리 그렇게 하라고 단단히 일러둔 터였다) 외삼촌의 황갈색 반바지를 팔로 부둥켜안고는, 서툰 말로 제발 가지 말아 달라고 애원했다.\n\n\"내가 왜 여기 남아 있어야겠니, 얘들아?\" 릴리빅 씨가 말했다. \"여긴 날 반기지도 않는데.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇게 매정하게 말씀하지 마세요, 외삼촌.\" 케닉스 부인이 흐느꼈다. \"저를 죽이실 작정이 아니라면요.\"\n\n\"내가 그러고 싶어 한다고 말할 사람도 있을 게다.\" 릴리빅 씨가 케닉스를 성난 눈으로 흘기며 대꾸했다. \"화가 났다니!\"\n\n\"아! 저이가 제 남편을 저렇게 노려보는 꼴은 차마 못 보겠어요.\" 케닉스 부인이 울부짖었다. \"한집안끼리 이러는 게 어찌나 끔찍한지. 아!\"\n\n\"릴리빅 어르신.\" 케닉스가 말했다. \"조카딸을 봐서라도, 부디 화해를 마다하지 말아 주십시오.\"\n\n조카사위의 부탁에 손님들까지 거들고 나서자 징수원의 표정이 누그러졌다. 그는 모자를 도로 내려놓고 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"자, 케닉스.\" 릴리빅 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 동시에 한마디 해 두지. 내가 얼마나 화가 났었는지 보여 주려고 하는 말인데, 내가 더 말없이 그냥 가 버렸다 해도, 내가 죽을 때 자네 아이들한테 남겨 줄 그 1, 2파운드에는 아무 변함이 없었을 거야.\"\n\n\"모를리나 케닉스.\" 어미가 애정을 주체 못 하고 소리쳤다. \"어서 다정한 외삼촌 앞에 무릎 꿇고, 평생 너를 사랑해 달라고 빌어라. 외삼촌은 사람이라기보다 천사 같은 분이란다. 내가 늘 그렇게 말해 왔잖니.\"\n\n이 분부에 따라 모를리나 양이 예를 갖추려고 다가가자, 릴리빅 씨는 대뜸 그 애를 번쩍 안아 올려 입을 맞추었다. 그러자 케닉스 부인이 앞으로 달려 나가 징수원에게 입을 맞추었고, 그의 너그러움을 지켜본 손님들 사이에서 참을 수 없는 갈채의 웅성거림이 터져 나왔다.\n\n이 훌륭한 신사는 이내 다시 모임의 활기이자 중심이 되었다. 잠깐 사람들의 정신이 딴 데로 쏠리는 바람에 잠시 밀려났던, 좌중의 주인공이라는 그 높은 자리에 다시 떡하니 올라앉은 것이다. 네발 달린 사자는 배고플 때만 사나워진다고들 한다. 두 발 달린 사자가 뾰로통해 있는 건, 자기를 떠받들어 달라는 욕심이 채워지지 않을 때뿐이고, 그것도 오래가는 법이 거의 없다. 릴리빅 씨는 그 어느 때보다 위세가 높아졌다. 자기 힘을 보여 주었고, 자기 재산과 유언에 담을 뜻을 넌지시 비쳤으며, 사심 없고 덕 있는 사람이라는 큰 평판까지 얻었으니까. 게다가 그 모든 것에 더해, 마침내 뉴먼 노그스가 그토록 흉악하게 들고 가 버린 것보다 훨씬 큰 잔에 펀치를 받아 들기까지 했다.\n\n\"저기요! 또 끼어들어서 모두께 정말 죄송합니다.\" 크롤이 이 흐뭇한 순간에 안을 들여다보며 말했다. \"그런데 이거 참 묘한 일 아닙니까? 노그스가 이 집에 산 지 이제 오 년이 다 되어 가는데, 이 집에서 제일 오래 산 사람이 기억하기로도 여태 그를 찾아온 사람이 하나도 없었거든요.\"\n\n\"한밤중에 불려 나가다니, 참 이상한 시간이긴 하지요.\" 징수원이 말했다. \"게다가 노그스 씨 본인의 행동도, 좋게 말해 봐야 수상쩍단 말이오.\"\n\n\"맞아요, 그러게 말입니다.\" 크롤이 받아쳤다. \"한술 더 떠서 말씀드리자면, 제 생각엔 그 두 친구가, 그게 누구든 간에, 어디선가 도망쳐 온 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"왜 그렇게 생각하시오?\" 징수원이 캐물었다. 어느새 모두의 암묵적인 합의로 좌중의 대변인 자리에 뽑혀 앉은 듯한 그였다. \"혹시 그자들이 어딘가에서 마땅히 내야 할 세금이며 부과금을 안 내고 도망쳐 왔다고 보는 건 아니겠지요?\"\n\n크롤 씨는 다소 경멸 어린 표정으로, 어떤 경우에든 세금이나 부과금을 내는 것에 대해 한바탕 반대를 늘어놓으려던 참이었다. 그런데 케닉스가 때맞춰 속삭여 말리고, 케닉스 부인이 몇 번이고 눈살을 찌푸리며 눈짓을 보내는 통에, 다행히도 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"실은 말이죠.\" 있는 힘껏 뉴먼의 방문에 귀를 대고 엿듣던 크롤이 말했다. \"실은 두 사람이 어찌나 큰 소리로 얘기를 하던지, 제 방까지 다 들려서 영 거슬리더라고요. 그러다 보니 여기서 한마디, 저기서 한마디 안 들으려야 안 들을 수가 없었지요. 그런데 제가 들은 건 죄다 어디선가 줄행랑을 친 얘기 같았어요. 케닉스 부인을 놀라게 하고 싶진 않지만, 부디 그자들이 무슨 감옥이나 병원에서 온 게 아니었으면 좋겠네요. 열병이라든가 그 비슷한 고약한 걸 옮겨 와서, 애들한테 옮기기라도 하면 큰일이니까요.\"\n\n케닉스 부인은 이 추측에 어찌나 압도됐는지, 드루어리 레인 왕립 극장의 페토커 양이 온갖 정성을 다해 보살핀 뒤에야 겨우 평정을 되찾을 수 있었다. 거기에 더해 케닉스 씨도 부지런히 거들었는데, 그는 통통한 각성제 병을 부인의 코에 갖다 대고 있었고, 급기야 그녀의 얼굴을 타고 흐르는 눈물이 정말 북받친 감정 탓인지 아니면 그 휘발성 각성제 탓인지 헷갈릴 지경이 되었다.\n\n부인들은 저마다 따로따로 안타까운 마음을 내비치더니, 으레 그러듯 위로의 말을 작은 합창처럼 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그중에서도 \"가엾어라!\"라든가, \"내가 저 사람이라도 꼭 같은 심정일 거예요\"라든가, \"그럼요, 참 견디기 힘든 일이지요\"라든가, \"어미 마음은 어미만이 아는 법이에요\" 같은 말들이 가장 두드러졌고, 또 가장 자주 되풀이되었다. 한마디로 좌중의 뜻이 어찌나 또렷이 드러났던지, 케닉스 씨는 노그스 씨의 방으로 가서 해명을 요구하려는 참이었다. 그래서 미리 펀치 한 잔까지 단단하고도 흔들림 없는 결의로 들이켠 터였는데, 바로 그때 새롭고도 끔찍한 사태가 벌어져 모두의 정신이 그리로 쏠렸다.\n\n다름이 아니라, 위층에서 더없이 날카롭고 째지는 듯한 비명이 잇따라 터져 나온 것이었다. 그것도 아무리 봐도 갓난 케닉스가 그 순간 고이 모셔져 있는 바로 그 뒷방에서 나는 소리 같았다. 그 소리가 들리기 무섭게 케닉스 부인은, 어떤 낯선 고양이가 들어와 보던 애가 잠든 사이 아기의 숨을 빨아들이고 있다고 단정하고는, 두 손을 부르쥐고 처절하게 비명을 지르며 문으로 달려갔다. 손님들은 그만 기겁하며 우왕좌왕했다.\n\n\"케닉스 씨, 무슨 일인지 보고 와요, 어서요!\" 부인의 동생이 케닉스 부인을 와락 붙들고 힘으로 끌어당기며 외쳤다. \"아, 그렇게 몸부림치지 마, 언니. 안 그러면 도저히 못 붙들겠어.\"\n\n\"내 아기, 내 귀하고 귀하고 귀하고 귀한 아기!\" 케닉스 부인이 '귀하고'를 갈수록 더 크게 외치며 비명을 질렀다. \"내 사랑하는, 다정하고 순결한 릴리빅… 아, 그 애한테 가게 해 줘. 가게 해 줘어어어!\"\n\n이렇게 미친 듯한 절규가 터지고 네 어린 딸들이 통곡하며 울부짖는 사이, 케닉스 씨는 소리가 나는 방을 향해 부리나케 위층으로 뛰어올라 갔다. 그런데 그 방문 앞에서 아기를 품에 안은 니콜라스와 맞닥뜨렸다. 니콜라스가 어찌나 거세게 뛰쳐나왔던지, 애가 탄 아비는 계단 여섯 칸을 굴러떨어져, 무슨 일이냐고 입을 떼 볼 새도 없이 가장 가까운 층계참에 나동그라지고 말았다.\n\n\"놀라지 마세요.\" 니콜라스가 뛰어 내려오며 외쳤다. \"여기 있어요. 다 끝났어요, 다 지나갔다고요. 부디 진정하세요. 아무 일도 없어요.\" 그는 이런 말과 함께 수천 마디 안심시키는 말을 쏟아 내며, (서두르는 통에 거꾸로 안고 있던) 아기를 케닉스 부인에게 건넸다. 그러고는 다시 뛰어 올라가, 머리를 박박 문지르며 자기가 굴러떨어진 데에 어리둥절해하던 케닉스 씨를 부축했다.\n\n이 반가운 소식에 마음을 놓은 손님들은 어느 정도 두려움에서 벗어났다. 그 두려움 탓에 정신을 완전히 놓아 버린 아주 별난 일들이 몇 가지 벌어졌었는데, 가령 그 독신 친구는 한참 동안 케닉스 부인이 아니라 그 동생을 품에 안고 있었고, 점잖은 릴리빅 씨로 말하자면 마음이 어수선한 나머지 방문 뒤에서 페토커 양에게 몇 번이나 입을 맞추는 모습이 실제로 목격되었는데, 그것도 무슨 큰일이 벌어진 게 전혀 아니라는 듯 태연하기 짝이 없었다.\n\n\"별일 아니에요.\" 니콜라스가 케닉스 부인에게 돌아와 말했다. \"아기를 보던 여자애가, 아마 졸렸나 봐요, 잠이 들었다가 제 머리카락에 불이 붙었지 뭐예요.\"\n\n\"오, 이 악독한 못된 것 같으니!\" 케닉스 부인이 그 가엾은 어린 것에게 사뭇 험악하게 집게손가락을 흔들어 대며 소리쳤다. 열세 살쯤 되어 보이는 그 애는 머리가 그을린 채 겁에 질린 얼굴로 멀거니 보고 있었다.\n\n\"그 애 비명을 듣고요.\" 니콜라스가 말을 이었다. \"뛰어 내려가서, 다른 데까지 불이 옮겨붙기 전에 막았어요. 아기는 정말 아무 데도 안 다쳤으니 믿으셔도 돼요. 제가 직접 침대에서 안아 내려서, 보여 드리려고 여기 데려온 거예요.\"\n\n이 짤막한 해명이 끝나자, 징수원의 이름을 따서 세례를 받아 릴리빅 케닉스라는 이름을 자랑스레 지니게 된 그 아기는, 사람들의 입맞춤 세례에 절반쯤 숨이 막히고 제 어미 가슴팍에 어찌나 꽉 눌렸던지 다시 울음을 터뜨렸다. 그러고 나자 좌중의 관심은 자연스레, 감히 제 머리카락을 다 태워 먹은 그 어린 계집애에게로 옮겨 갔다. 그 애는 더 극성스러운 부인들에게 자잘하게 몇 대 얻어맞고 떠밀린 끝에, 자비롭게도 집으로 돌려보내졌다. 다만 그 애한테 주려던 9펜스는 케닉스 집안 몫으로 몰수되었다.\n\n\"그리고 우리가 선생님께 무슨 말씀을 드려야 할지,\" 케닉스 부인이 어린 릴리빅을 구해 준 사람을 향해 외쳤다. \"정말이지 모르겠네요.\"\n\n\"아무 말씀도 하실 것 없어요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다. \"부인의 그 고마운 말씀을 받을 만한 일을 한 것도 없는걸요, 정말로요.\"\n\n\"선생님이 아니었으면 그 애는 불에 타 죽었을지도 몰라요.\" 페토커 양이 새치름하게 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게까지 되진 않았을 거예요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다. \"여기 도와줄 분들이 잔뜩 계셨으니, 그 애가 위험해지기 전에 분명 누군가 달려갔을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 보답으로 선생님 건강을 위해 한잔 들기라도 하게 해 주세요!\" 케닉스 씨가 식탁 쪽으로 손짓하며 말했다.\n\n\"제가 없는 자리에서요, 그러시면 얼마든지요.\" 니콜라스가 빙긋 웃으며 받았다. \"제가 여행에 몹시 지쳐서, 같이 있어 봐야 도무지 흥이 안 나는 사람일 거예요. 흥을 돋우기는커녕 깨기나 할 텐데, 깨어 있는다 해도 그렇고, 그나마 깨어 있을 수 있을지도 영 모르겠어요. 괜찮으시면 저는 친구 노그스 씨한테 돌아가 보겠습니다. 그분은 별일 아닌 걸 아시고 다시 위층으로 올라가셨거든요. 안녕히들 계세요.\"\n\n이런 말로 잔치 자리에 끼는 걸 사양한 니콜라스는, 케닉스 부인과 다른 부인들에게 더없이 정겹게 작별을 고하고 물러갔다. 좌중에 아주 비상한 인상을 남기고서.\n\n\"어쩜 저렇게 멋진 청년이람!\" 케닉스 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"보기 드물게 신사답더군, 정말로.\" 케닉스 씨가 말했다. \"안 그렇습니까, 릴리빅 어르신?\"\n\n\"그렇지.\" 징수원이 미심쩍은 듯 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"신사답긴 하지, 아주 신사다워. 겉보기엔 말이야.\"\n\n\"혹시 저 청년한테 거슬리는 데라도 보이세요, 외삼촌?\" 케닉스 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"아니다, 얘야.\" 징수원이 대답했다. \"아니야. 다만 저 청년이… 글쎄… 아무튼 됐다. 너한테 정을 보내마, 얘야. 그리고 아기는 오래오래 살거라!\"\n\n\"외삼촌 이름을 물려받은 아이잖아요.\" 케닉스 부인이 상냥하게 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"부디 그 이름값을 하는 아이가 되었으면 합니다.\" 징수원의 비위를 맞추려는 마음에 케닉스 씨가 거들었다. \"제 대부의 얼굴에 절대 먹칠하지 않는 아이, 그리고 훗날 자라서 제 이름의 주인인 릴리빅 가문 사람들과 어깨를 나란히 할 만한 아이로 여겨지길 바랍니다. 정말이지 저는, 그리고 케닉스 부인도 같은 마음이고 저만큼이나 절실히 느끼고 있습니다만, 그 애가 릴리빅이라 불리는 것이야말로 제 평생 가장 큰 축복이자 영광 가운데 하나라고 생각합니다.\"\n\n\"가장 큰 축복이지요, 케닉스.\" 그의 아내가 나직이 거들었다.\n\n\"가장 큰 축복이지요.\" 케닉스 씨가 자기 말을 고쳐 말했다. \"언젠가 제가 그 축복을 누릴 자격을 갖출 수 있기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n이건 케닉스 부부의 영리한 한 수였다. 덕분에 릴리빅 씨가 그 아기의 귀함을 떠받치는 으뜸가는 머리이자 샘이 되었으니까. 마음씨 좋은 그 신사는 이 손길의 세심함과 능란함을 알아채고는, 그 자리에서 이름은 모르지만 그날 밤 침착함과 민첩함으로 두드러진 활약을 보인 그 신사의 건강을 위해 건배를 청했다.\n\n\"한마디 덧붙이자면,\" 릴리빅 씨가 큰 인심이라도 쓰듯 말했다. \"꽤 잘생긴 청년이고, 그 됨됨이도 부디 그 예의범절에 못지않기를 바라네.\"\n\n\"정말이지 얼굴도 아주 곱고 풍채도 좋더라고요.\" 케닉스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"정말 그래요.\" 페토커 양이 덧붙였다. \"그 사람 어딘가에 아주… 어머나, 그 말이 뭐였더라?\"\n\n\"무슨 말이오?\" 릴리빅 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그게… 어머, 내 정신 좀 봐.\" 페토커 양이 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"그, 귀족들이 문고리를 부수고 경관들을 두들겨 패고, 남의 돈으로 마차놀이를 하고, 그런 부류의 짓을 다 할 때 뭐라고 부르죠?\"\n\n\"귀족적이라고?\" 징수원이 거들었다.\n\n\"아! 귀족적이요.\" 페토커 양이 대답했다. \"그 사람한테 뭔가 아주 귀족적인 데가 있죠, 안 그래요?\"\n\n남자들은 입을 다문 채 서로를 보며 빙긋 웃었다. 마치 \"흠! 취향이야 뭐라 할 수 없는 거지\" 하고 말하려는 듯이. 하지만 부인들은 한목소리로 니콜라스에게 귀족적인 분위기가 있다고 결론지었다. 그리고 그 주장에 굳이 토를 다는 사람이 없으니, 그것은 보란 듯이 확정되었다.\n\n이쯤 되니 펀치도 다 마셔 버렸고, 어린 케닉스네 아이들도(아까부터 한참을 제 작은 집게손가락으로 작은 눈을 억지로 떠 받치고 있던 터였다) 보채기 시작하며 어서 재워 달라고 꽤 다급하게 졸라 댔다. 그러자 징수원이 회중시계를 꺼내 들고는, 곧 두 시가 다 되어 간다고 좌중에 알리며 자리를 뜰 채비를 했다. 그 말에 손님들 중 더러는 놀라고 더러는 기겁했다. 그리고 모자며 보닛을 식탁 밑이며 여기저기 더듬어 찾아 한참 만에야 찾아낸 뒤, 임자들은 길을 나섰다. 수없이 손을 맞잡고, 이렇게 즐거운 저녁은 처음이라느니, 시간이 이토록 늦은 줄 몰랐다느니, 아무리 늦어도 열 시 반쯤일 줄 알았다느니, 케닉스 부부에게 일주일에 한 번씩 결혼기념일이 있으면 좋겠다느니, 케닉스 부인이 대체 무슨 숨은 재주로 이리도 잘 차려 냈는지 모르겠다느니, 그런 말들을 잔뜩 늘어놓으면서. 이 모든 듣기 좋은 말에 케닉스 부부는, 신사 숙녀 한 사람 한 사람에게 차례차례 와 주셔서 고맙다고 인사하고, 부디 말씀하신 만큼의 절반만이라도 즐거우셨기를 바란다고 답했다.\n\n니콜라스로 말하자면, 자기가 남긴 인상은 전혀 모른 채 진작에 잠들어 버렸고, 뉴먼 노그스 씨와 스마이크에게 둘이서 술병을 비우도록 내맡겨 둔 터였다. 그리고 둘은 어찌나 흔쾌히 그 일을 해치웠던지, 뉴먼은 제정신이 제대로 박혀 있는지조차 알 수 없을 지경이었고, 또 그 새 친구만큼 깊이, 나른하게, 흠뻑 취한 신사를 여태 본 적이 있던가 도무지 가늠이 안 될 정도였다.\n\n# CHAPTER 16\n\n이튿날 아침, 니콜라스가 가장 먼저 한 일은 방을 하나 알아보는 거였어. 형편이 나아질 때까지 그럭저럭 지낼 만한 방 말이야. 뉴먼 노그스의 호의에 더 기대고 싶지 않았거든. 사실 뉴먼은 어린 친구가 편히 묵을 수만 있다면 기꺼이 계단에서라도 잘 사람이었지.\n\n응접실 창문에 붙은 광고가 가리키는 빈방은, 알아보니 삼층 뒤쪽의 작은 방이었어. 지붕 밑 공간을 막아 만든 방이라, 그을음 낀 기와지붕과 굴뚝들이 내다보이는 게 풍경의 전부였지. 이 방을 일주일 단위로 적당한 값에 세놓는 건 응접실에 사는 하숙인이 맡고 있었어. 집주인이 그에게 빈방이 생길 때마다 처리하라고 시킨 거야. 그리고 하숙인들이 도망가지 않게 잘 지켜보라고도 했지. 이 마지막 임무를 빈틈없이 해내라는 뜻에서, 그는 방세를 안 내고 살 수 있었어. 혹시라도 그 자신이 도망갈 마음이 들지 않게 하려는 거였지.\n\n니콜라스는 이 방의 세입자가 되었어. 그러고는 근처 중고품 가게에서 흔한 가구 몇 개를 빌리고, 첫 주 방세를 미리 냈지. 여분의 옷가지를 돈으로 바꿔 마련한 얼마 안 되는 돈에서 낸 거야. 그러고는 자리에 앉아 앞날을 곰곰이 생각해 봤어. 그런데 그 앞날이라는 게, 창밖 풍경처럼 답답하고 칙칙하기 짝이 없었지. 들여다볼수록 나아지기는커녕, 자꾸 보면 정이 떨어진다는 말처럼 더 싫어지기만 했어. 그래서 그는 부지런히 걷기라도 해서 그 생각을 머릿속에서 몰아내기로 했지. 모자를 챙겨 들고, 가엾은 스마이크는 방에 남겨 둔 채 말이야. 스마이크는 그 방이 세상에서 제일 값진 궁전이라도 되는 양 신이 나서 가구를 이리저리 놓아 보고 있었거든. 니콜라스는 거리로 나서서 북적이는 사람들 틈에 섞여 들었어.\n\n바쁘게 오가는 사람들 틈에 끼어 아무도 거들떠보지 않는 한 점 같은 존재가 되면, 자기가 대단한 사람이라는 느낌은 사라질지도 몰라. 하지만 그렇다고 자기 걱정거리가 얼마나 무겁고 큰지에 대한 절절한 느낌까지 그렇게 쉽게 떨쳐 낼 수 있는 건 아니야. 아무리 빨리 걸어도, 니콜라스의 머릿속을 차지한 건 오직 자기 처지가 얼마나 딱한가 하는 생각 하나뿐이었지. 그래서 주위 사람들의 처지와 앞날을 헤아려 보며 그 생각을 밀어내 보려고 했어. 그런데 몇 초도 안 돼서, 어느새 그들의 형편을 자기 것과 견주고 있는 자신을 발견하는 거야. 그러고는 자기도 모르게 슬그머니 다시 원래의 생각으로 미끄러져 들어갔지.\n\n이런 생각에 잠긴 채 런던의 큰 거리 하나를 걸어가던 그가, 우연히 파란 간판으로 눈길을 들었어. 거기엔 금빛 글씨로 이렇게 적혀 있었지. \"종합 직업소개소. 온갖 종류의 일자리와 자리, 안에서 문의 바람.\" 망사 차양과 안쪽 문이 달린 가게였어. 그리고 창문에는 손으로 쓴 광고지가 길게 줄지어 솔깃하게 걸려 있었지. 비서 자리부터 사환 자리까지, 온갖 등급의 빈자리를 알리는 광고였어.\n\n니콜라스는 약속이 가득한 이 신전 앞에 저도 모르게 멈춰 섰어. 그러고는 큼직한 글씨로 잔뜩 내걸린 인생의 기회들을 쭉 훑어봤지. 다 보고 나서 그는 조금 걸어가다가, 다시 돌아왔다가, 또 걸어갔어. 그러다 마침내 소개소 문 앞에서 몇 번이나 망설이며 서성이다가, 마음을 굳히고 안으로 들어섰지.\n\n그가 들어선 곳은 바닥에 천을 깐 작은 방이었어. 한쪽 구석엔 난간을 두른 높직한 책상이 있었고, 그 뒤에 마른 청년 하나가 앉아 있었지. 교활해 보이는 눈에 턱이 툭 튀어나온 그 청년이, 창문을 가린 그 큼직한 글씨들을 쓴 장본인이었어. 그 앞에는 두툼한 장부가 펼쳐져 있었어. 오른손 손가락을 책장 사이에 끼우고, 눈은 챙 달린 모자를 쓴 아주 뚱뚱한 노부인에게 박혀 있었지. 누가 봐도 이 가게의 주인이었어. 부인은 난롯가에서 몸을 쬐고 있었지. 청년은 부인의 지시만 떨어지면 녹슨 잠금쇠 속에 든 기록을 들춰 볼 태세였어.\n\n밖에는 안내판이 하나 걸려 있었어. 열 시부터 네 시까지 온갖 허드렛일을 할 하녀들이 언제든 고용을 기다리고 있다고 알리는 거였지. 그래서 니콜라스는 한쪽 구석 긴 의자에 앉아 있는 건장한 젊은 여자 여섯쯤이, 바로 그 목적으로 와 있다는 걸 대번에 알았어. 저마다 나막신과 우산을 챙긴 데다, 그 가엾은 여자들이 다들 불안하고 지친 기색이었거든. 하지만 난롯가에서 뚱뚱한 부인과 이야기를 나누는 말쑥한 젊은 아가씨 둘은 무슨 일을 하는 어떤 사람들인지 잘 알 수가 없었어. 그러다 니콜라스가 구석에 앉아, 다른 손님들이 다 끝날 때까지 기다리겠다고 말하자, 뚱뚱한 부인이 그가 들어오는 바람에 끊겼던 대화를 다시 이어 갔지.\n\n\"요리사라고, 톰.\" 뚱뚱한 부인이 아까처럼 여전히 몸을 쬐며 말했어.\n\n\"요리사요.\" 톰이 장부 몇 장을 넘기며 말했어. \"어디 보자!\"\n\n\"쉬운 자리로 한두 개 읽어 봐.\" 뚱뚱한 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"아주 일 적은 데로 골라 주세요, 젊은 양반.\" 양치기 격자무늬 부츠를 신은 점잖은 여자가 끼어들었어. 손님인 모양이었지.\n\n\"마커 부인.\" 톰이 읽었어. \"러셀 스퀘어의 러셀 플레이스. 십팔 기니 제시. 차와 설탕 제공. 식구는 둘, 손님 거의 없음. 하인 다섯. 남자 하인 없음. 따라다니는 남자 없을 것.\"\n\n\"어머나!\" 손님이 키득거렸어. \"그건 안 되겠네. 다른 거 읽어 줘 봐요, 젊은 양반.\"\n\n\"라이먹 부인.\" 톰이 말했어. \"핀스버리의 플레전트 플레이스. 급료 십이 기니. 차 없음, 설탕 없음. 독실한 집안—\"\n\n\"아! 그건 안 읽어도 돼요.\" 손님이 말을 끊었어.\n\n\"독실한 하인 셋.\" 톰이 무게를 잡고 말했어.\n\n\"셋이라고? 셋이라 했어요?\" 손님이 어조를 바꿔 물었어.\n\n\"독실한 하인 셋입니다.\" 톰이 대답했어. \"요리사, 하녀, 보모. 여자 하인은 저마다 일요일마다 세 번씩 리틀 베델 교회에 나가야 함. 독실한 하인과 함께. 만약 요리사가 하인보다 더 독실하면 하인을 일깨워 줄 것. 만약 하인이 요리사보다 더 독실하면 요리사를 일깨워 줄 것.\"\n\n\"그 집 주소를 받아 가야겠네.\" 손님이 말했어. \"나한테 꽤 잘 맞을지도 모르겠으니까.\"\n\n\"여기 또 있어요.\" 톰이 책장을 넘기며 말했어. \"하원 의원 갤런바일 씨 댁. 십오 기니에 차와 설탕 제공. 하인이 신앙심 깊은 사촌이라면 남자 사촌 면회 허용. 참고. 안식일에는 부엌에서 찬 음식만. 갤런바일 씨가 안식일 준수 문제에 헌신하기 때문임. 주님의 날에는 어떤 음식도 조리 금지. 단, 갤런바일 부부의 식사는 경건하고 꼭 필요한 일이므로 예외로 함. 갤런바일 씨는 안식일에 늦게 식사함. 요리사가 몸치장하는 죄를 막기 위해서임.\"\n\n\"그건 아까 그 집만큼 좋진 않을 것 같네.\" 손님이 친구와 잠깐 속닥거리고 나서 말했어. \"아까 그 주소를 받아 갈게요, 젊은 양반. 안 맞으면 다시 오면 되니까.\"\n\n톰은 부탁받은 대로 주소를 적어 줬어. 그리고 점잖은 손님은 그새 뚱뚱한 부인에게 적은 수수료를 건네 만족시키고는, 친구와 함께 나갔지.\n\n니콜라스가 입을 열어, 청년에게 에스 항목을 펼쳐서 아직 남아 있는 비서 자리가 있는지 알려 달라고 부탁하려는 참이었어. 그때 한 손님이 소개소로 들어왔지. 그는 곧바로 양보하고 물러섰어. 그런데 그 사람의 모습이 그를 놀라게 하는 동시에 흥미를 끌었지.\n\n이제 막 열여덟쯤 됐을까 싶은 젊은 아가씨였어. 몸매가 아주 가냘프고 여렸지만, 더없이 곱게 빠진 모습이었지. 아가씨는 머뭇거리며 책상으로 다가가더니, 아주 나직한 목소리로 물었어. 가정교사나 귀부인의 말동무로 갈 만한 자리가 있는지 묻는 거였지. 아가씨는 묻는 동안 잠깐 베일을 들어 올렸어. 그러자 보기 드물게 아름다운 얼굴이 드러났지. 다만 그 얼굴엔 슬픔의 그림자가 어려 있었어. 그렇게 어린 사람한테 그런 그늘이 있다는 게 더욱 눈에 띄었지. 아가씨는 장부에 적힌 어떤 사람에게 보내는 소개장을 받아 들고는, 으레 하는 인사를 하고 미끄러지듯 나갔어.\n\n아가씨는 단정했지만 아주 수수하게 차려입고 있었어. 어찌나 수수한지, 자기만의 우아함을 덜 지닌 사람이 그 옷을 입었더라면 초라하고 후줄근해 보였을 것 같을 정도였지. 아가씨에겐 시중드는 아이가 하나 있었어. 얼굴 붉고 눈이 동그란, 칠칠치 못한 여자아이였지. 끌려 내려온 숄 밑으로 드러난 맨팔이 거칠어 보였고, 얼굴엔 검댕과 흑연 자국이 채 안 씻겨 문신처럼 남아 있었어. 누가 봐도 긴 의자에 앉은 허드렛일 하녀들과 한패였지. 그 아이와 하녀들 사이에 오가는 갖가지 히죽거림과 눈짓이, 같은 일을 하는 사람들끼리의 은밀한 동지애를 드러내고 있었거든.\n\n이 아이가 제 주인을 따라 나갔어. 그리고 니콜라스가 놀라움과 감탄에서 미처 헤어나기도 전에, 젊은 아가씨는 가 버렸지. 만약 뚱뚱한 부인과 장부 담당 청년 사이에 오간 말이 그를 붙잡아 두지 않았더라면, 그가 그들을 따라 나갔을지도 몰라. 이건 점잖은 사람들이 생각하는 것만큼 그렇게 말도 안 되는 일은 아니야.\n\n\"저 아가씨 언제 다시 온대, 톰?\" 뚱뚱한 부인이 물었어.\n\n\"내일 아침요.\" 톰이 펜을 다듬으며 대답했어.\n\n\"어디로 보냈는데?\" 뚱뚱한 부인이 물었어.\n\n\"클라크 부인 댁요.\" 톰이 대답했어.\n\n\"거기 가면 팔자 한번 편하겠구먼.\" 뚱뚱한 부인이 양철통에서 코담배를 한 줌 집으며 말했어.\n\n톰은 별다른 대꾸 없이 혀를 볼 안쪽으로 밀어 넣고는, 펜 깃털로 니콜라스 쪽을 가리켰어. 그 신호에 뚱뚱한 부인이 물었지. \"자, 손님, 무슨 일로 오셨수?\"\n\n니콜라스는 짤막하게 대답했어. 신사의 비서나 필경사 같은 자리가 있는지 알고 싶다고 말이야.\n\n\"그런 자리요!\" 주인이 받았어. \"그런 거라면 열 개도 넘지. 안 그래, 톰?\"\n\n\"그럴걸요.\" 그 젊은 청년이 대답했어. 그러면서 그는 니콜라스를 향해 윙크를 했지. 친근함을 한껏 담은 윙크였는데, 본인은 분명 꽤나 듣기 좋은 인사라고 여겼겠지만, 니콜라스는 고맙기는커녕 진저리가 났어.\n\n장부를 들춰 보니, 열 개도 넘던 비서 자리가 결국 하나로 쪼그라들어 있었지. 웨스트민스터 맨체스터 빌딩에 사는 거물 하원 의원 그렉스버리 씨가, 서류와 서신을 정리해 줄 젊은이를 구하고 있다는 거였어. 그리고 니콜라스야말로 그렉스버리 씨가 찾는 바로 그런 젊은이였지.\n\n\"조건이 어떤지는 몰라요. 본인이 직접 당사자랑 정하겠다고 했으니까.\" 뚱뚱한 부인이 말했어. \"하지만 꽤 괜찮을 거유. 하원 의원이니까.\"\n\n니콜라스는 세상 경험이 없긴 했지만, 이 논리가 그렇게 그럴듯한지, 이 결론이 옳은지 영 확신이 안 섰어. 하지만 굳이 따지고 들 것 없이, 주소를 받아 적고는 곧장 그렉스버리 씨를 찾아가기로 마음먹었지.\n\n\"번지수는 모르겠어요.\" 톰이 말했어. \"근데 맨체스터 빌딩이 큰 데가 아니거든요. 정 안 되면 양쪽 집 문을 죄다 두드려서 찾아도 그리 오래 안 걸릴 거예요. 그나저나, 아까 그 아가씨 참 예쁘지 않았어요?\"\n\n\"무슨 아가씨?\" 니콜라스가 딱딱하게 물었어.\n\n\"아, 그래요. 알지—무슨 아가씨, 응?\" 톰이 한쪽 눈을 감고 턱을 치켜들며 속삭였어. \"못 봤죠, 못 봤어—참, 내일 아침 그 아가씨 오면, 나처럼 돼 봤으면 싶지 않아요?\"\n\n니콜라스는 그 못생긴 점원을 노려봤어. 마치 아가씨를 향한 그의 감탄에 대한 보답으로 장부를 그의 귀싸대기에 패대기쳐 주고 싶은 표정이었지. 하지만 그는 참았어. 그러고는 거만하게 소개소를 성큼성큼 빠져나왔지. 분노에 찬 나머지, 옛 기사도의 법도 따위는 깡그리 무시한 채로 말이야. 그 법도에 따르면, 모든 훌륭한 기사는 자기가 흠모하는 귀부인을 칭송하는 말을 마땅히 들어야 옳고, 그건 적법한 일이었지. 게다가 세상을 떠돌며, 한 번 본 적도 들은 적도 없는 처녀를 세상 그 무엇보다 떠받들기를 거부하는, 그런 메마르고 시적이지 못한 자들을 죄다 때려눕히는 것이 기사의 의무이기도 했어. 마치 한 번도 본 적 없다는 게 무슨 핑계라도 된다는 듯이!\n\n니콜라스는 더 이상 자기 불행은 생각하지 않았어. 그저 자기가 본 그 아름다운 아가씨의 불행은 무엇일까 궁금해할 뿐이었지. 그러면서 그는 여러 번 길을 잘못 들고, 여러 번 길을 묻고, 거의 그만큼 잘못된 길 안내를 받아 가며, 일러 준 곳으로 발걸음을 옮겼어.\n\n웨스트민스터라는 오래된 도시 구역 안, 그 옛 성역에서 십 분의 일 마일도 안 되는 곳에, 좁고 지저분한 동네가 하나 있어. 요즘 들어서는 군소 하원 의원들의 도피처가 된 곳이지. 음침한 하숙집들이 늘어선 거리 하나가 전부야. 의회가 쉬는 동안엔 그 집 창문마다 침울한 광고지가 길게 줄지어 인상을 찌푸리고 있어. 그 광고지들은, 마치 이제는 조상 곁에 잠든 회기에 여당석과 야당석에 늘어앉았던 의원들 얼굴이 그러했던 것처럼, 또렷하게 이렇게 말하지. \"세놓음\", \"세놓음.\" 한 해 중 좀 더 바쁜 철이 되면 이 광고지들은 사라지고, 집집마다 입법자들이 우글거려. 응접실에도, 이층에도, 삼층에도, 사층에도, 다락방에도 입법자들이 있지. 그 좁은 방들이 진정서를 들고 온 대표단과 사절들의 입김으로 후끈해. 축축한 날씨엔 이곳이 답답해지지. 눅눅한 법안과 퀴퀴한 청원서에서 김이 풀풀 올라오거든. 우체부들은 이 병든 구역에 발을 들이면 정신이 아득해지고, 무료 우편 허가를 얻으려는 추레한 사람들이 안절부절못하며 이리저리 떠돌아. 마치 죽어 버린 서간문집의 떠도는 유령들처럼 말이야. 이곳이 바로 맨체스터 빌딩이야. 그리고 여기서는 밤 어느 때고, 제각각의 열쇠 구멍에서 빗장 열쇠가 달그락거리는 소리가 들리지. 가끔은—빌딩 발치를 적시는 물 위로 바람이 휙 불어와 그 소리를 입구 쪽으로 떠밀 때면—내일 할 연설을 연습하는 어느 젊은 의원의 가냘프고 새된 목소리가 섞여 들기도 해. 온종일 손풍금 소리가 갈리듯 울리고, 자그마한 음악 상자들이 쨍그랑 댕그랑 부딪혀. 맨체스터 빌딩이란 어설픈 아가리 말고는 출구가 없는 장어 통발 같은 곳이거든. 통로 없이 짧고 좁은 목만 달린 사각 병 같은 곳이지. 이 점에서 이곳은, 좀 더 모험심 강한 거주자 몇몇의 운명을 상징하는지도 몰라. 그들은 격렬하게 몸부림치고 비틀어 가며 의회에 비집고 들어가지만, 곧 깨닫게 되거든. 의회 역시 자기들한테는 출구 없는 막다른 곳이라는 걸. 맨체스터 빌딩처럼, 의회도 그 자체 너머로는 어디로도 이어지지 않는다는 걸 말이야. 그래서 그들은 결국 뒷걸음질로 빠져나올 수밖에 없어. 들어갈 때보다 조금도 더 똑똑해지지도, 부유해지지도, 한 톨만큼도 더 유명해지지도 못한 채로.\n\n니콜라스는 거물 그렉스버리 씨의 주소를 손에 들고 맨체스터 빌딩으로 들어섰어. 입구에서 멀지 않은 어느 허름한 집으로 사람들이 줄줄이 밀려 들어가고 있었지. 그래서 그는 그들이 다 들어갈 때까지 기다렸다가, 하인에게 다가가 그렉스버리 씨가 어디 사는지 아느냐고 조심스레 물었어.\n\n하인은 아주 창백하고 추레한 소년이었어. 갓난아기 때부터 땅속에서 잠을 잔 듯한 몰골이었지. 실제로 그랬을 가능성이 컸어. \"그렉스버리 씨요?\" 소년이 말했어. \"그렉스버리 씨는 여기 사세요. 맞아요. 들어오세요!\"\n\n니콜라스는 들어갈 수 있을 때 들어가는 게 낫겠다 싶어, 안으로 걸어 들어갔어. 그런데 그가 들어서기 무섭게, 소년이 문을 닫고 사라져 버렸지.\n\n이것만 해도 꽤 이상한 노릇이었어. 그런데 더 난처한 건, 복도를 따라, 또 좁은 계단을 따라, 창문을 막아서서 그렇잖아도 어두운 입구를 한층 더 어둡게 만들며, 사람들이 한데 뒤엉켜 북적이고 있다는 거였지. 다들 얼굴에 잔뜩 무게를 잡고 있었어. 보아하니 곧 닥칠 무슨 일을 말없이 기다리는 모양이었지. 이따금 한 사람이 옆 사람에게 귓속말을 하거나, 몇몇이 한데 모여 수군거렸어. 그러고는 수군대던 자들이 서로를 향해 사납게 고개를 끄덕이거나, 매정하게 고개를 절레절레 흔드는 거야. 마치 무슨 비장한 일을 작정하고서, 무슨 일이 있어도 물러서지 않겠다고 다짐한 사람들 같았지.\n\n몇 분이 흘러도 이 광경을 설명해 줄 일이 아무것도 일어나지 않았어. 게다가 니콜라스는 자기 처지가 유난히 불편하게 느껴졌지. 그래서 그가 옆 사람에게 뭐라도 물어보려는 참이었어. 그때 계단 쪽에서 갑자기 움직임이 일더니, 누군가 외치는 소리가 들렸지. \"자, 여러분, 어서 올라오시지요!\"\n\n그런데 계단 위 사람들은 올라가기는커녕 잽싸게 내려오기 시작했어. 그러고는 더없이 정중하게, 거리에 가장 가까이 선 사람들더러 먼저 들어가시라고 청하는 거야. 거리에 가장 가까이 선 사람들도 똑같이 공손하게, 그런 일은 결코 있을 수 없다고 받아쳤지. 하지만 결국 본의 아니게 그렇게 되고 말았어. 다른 사람들이 여섯 명쯤(그중에 니콜라스도 있었지)을 앞으로 떠밀고 뒤에서 바짝 죄어 들었거든. 그래서 그들은 계단 위로 떠밀린 정도가 아니라, 그렉스버리 씨의 거실 안으로까지 떠밀려 들어갔어. 더없이 꼴사납게 허둥지둥, 빠져나갈 길도 없이 말이야. 뒤에서 밀려드는 사람들이 방을 넘치도록 가득 채웠지.\n\n\"여러분.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어. \"잘 오셨습니다. 뵙게 되어 기쁩니다.\"\n\n손님들을 보고 기뻐하는 신사치고는, 그렉스버리 씨는 더없이 불편한 표정이었어. 하지만 어쩌면 그건 의원다운 근엄함과, 감정을 다스리는 정치가다운 습관 탓이었는지도 몰라. 그는 다부지고 우람하며 머리가 둔한 신사였어. 목소리는 우렁차고, 태도는 거들먹거렸지. 알맹이 없는 말을 그럴싸하게 늘어놓는 솜씨도 제법이었어. 한마디로, 아주 훌륭한 의원이 되는 데 필요한 자질을 죄다 갖춘 셈이었지.\n\n\"자, 여러분.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 발치의 등나무 바구니에 두툼한 서류 뭉치를 던져 넣으며 말했어. 그러고는 팔걸이에 양팔을 걸친 채 의자에 등을 기댔지. \"신문을 보니, 여러분이 내 처신에 불만이 있으신 모양이군요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 그렉스버리 씨, 불만입니다.\" 통통한 노신사 하나가 잔뜩 열을 올리며 군중 사이에서 튀어나와 맨 앞에 떡 버티고 섰어.\n\n\"내 눈이 잘못된 건가?\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말한 사람을 바라보며 말했어. \"아니면 저게 내 오랜 친구 퍼그스타일스인가?\"\n\n\"바로 그 사람이올시다, 다른 누구도 아니고요.\" 통통한 노신사가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"악수합시다, 친애하는 벗이여.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어. \"퍼그스타일스, 친애하는 친구, 자네를 여기서 보다니 참으로 유감일세.\"\n\n\"저야말로 여기 오게 되어 유감입니다.\" 퍼그스타일스 씨가 말했어. \"하지만 그렉스버리 씨, 당신의 처신 때문에 유권자들이 보낸 이 대표단이 어쩔 수 없이 필요하게 된 거지요.\"\n\n\"내 처신이라.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 너그러운 도량을 풍기며 대표단을 둘러보며 말했어. \"내 처신은 지금껏 그래 왔고 앞으로도 언제나, 이 위대하고 행복한 나라의 진정하고 참된 이익을 향한 진심 어린 마음으로 이끌어져 왔소. 안으로 눈을 돌리든, 밖으로 눈을 돌리든 말이오. 우리 섬나라의 평화롭고 부지런한 마을들을 바라보든. 증기선으로 뒤덮인 강, 기관차로 뒤덮인 도로, 마차로 뒤덮인 거리, 그리고 이 나라든 다른 어느 나라든 항공의 역사에서 일찍이 없던 위력과 규모의 기구로 뒤덮인 하늘을 바라보든. 내가 말하건대, 그저 안으로 눈을 돌리든, 아니면 더 멀리 눈을 뻗어, 영국인의 끈기와 영국인의 용맹으로 이룩한, 내 앞에 펼쳐진 저 끝없는 정복과 영토의 전망을 바라보든. 나는 두 손을 맞잡고, 머리 위로 드넓게 펼쳐진 창공으로 눈을 들어 이렇게 외친다오. '하늘이여 감사합니다, 나는 영국인입니다!'\"\n\n예전 같았으면 이런 열변은 메아리가 울리도록 환호를 받았겠지. 하지만 지금 대표단은 그 말을 싸늘하게 받아들였어. 다들 받은 인상은 이런 것 같았지. 그렉스버리 씨의 정치적 처신에 대한 해명치고는 너무 두루뭉술하다는 거였어. 그리고 뒤쪽에 있던 한 신사는 큰 소리로 한마디 내뱉기를 서슴지 않았지. 자기가 보기엔 그 말이 너무 '허튼소리' 냄새가 난다고 말이야.\n\n\"그 말—허튼소리라는 말의 뜻은,\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어. \"나로선 알 수가 없군요. 만약 그게 내가 조국을 칭송하느라 좀 지나치게 열을 올렸다거나, 어쩌면 과장이 좀 심했다는 뜻이라면, 나는 그 지적이 전적으로 옳다고 인정하겠소. 나는 이 자유롭고 행복한 나라가 자랑스럽소이다. 이 나라의 위대함과 영광을 떠올리면, 내 몸은 부풀어 오르고, 내 눈은 빛나고, 내 가슴은 들썩이고, 내 심장은 부풀고, 내 가슴속은 불타오른단 말이오.\"\n\n\"저희는, 선생님,\" 퍼그스타일스 씨가 차분히 말했어. \"몇 가지 질문을 드리고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"좋으실 대로 하시오, 여러분. 내 시간은 여러분의 것이오. 그리고 내 조국의 것—내 조국의—\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어.\n\n이 허락이 떨어지자, 퍼그스타일스 씨는 안경을 쓰고 주머니에서 꺼낸 종이를 펼쳤어. 그러자 대표단의 거의 모든 사람이 저마다 주머니에서 종이를 꺼냈지. 퍼그스타일스 씨가 질문을 읽어 나가는 대로 하나씩 짚어 가며 확인하려는 거였어.\n\n이렇게 준비를 마치고, 퍼그스타일스 씨가 본론으로 들어갔어.\n\n\"질문 제일 번. 당신은 선거 전에 자진해서 이렇게 약속하지 않았습니까? 만약 당선되면 곧바로 하원에서 기침하고 신음하는 짓거리를 뿌리 뽑겠다고요. 그런데 회기 첫 토론에서부터 기침과 신음에 묻혀 입도 못 떼고 물러서지 않았습니까? 그리고 그 뒤로 이걸 고치려고 아무 노력도 안 하지 않았습니까? 또 정부를 깜짝 놀라게 해서 벌벌 떨게 만들겠다고도 약속하지 않았습니까? 그래서 정부를 놀라게 해 벌벌 떨게 만들었습니까, 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"다음 질문으로 넘어가게, 친애하는 퍼그스타일스.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"그 질문에 대해 하실 해명이 있으십니까, 선생님?\" 퍼그스타일스 씨가 물었어.\n\n\"전혀 없소.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어.\n\n대표단 사람들이 서로를 사납게 노려보더니, 이어서 의원을 노려봤어. '친애하는 퍼그스타일스'는 안경 너머로 그렉스버리 씨를 한참 동안 빤히 쏘아보고는, 다시 질문 목록을 이어 갔지.\n\n\"질문 제이 번. 당신은 마찬가지로, 무슨 일이 있어도 당신 동료를 지지하겠다고 자진해서 약속하지 않았습니까? 그런데 그저께 밤에 그를 저버리고 반대편에 표를 던지지 않았습니까? 반대편 지도자의 부인이 그렉스버리 부인을 야회에 초대했다는 이유로 말입니다.\"\n\n\"계속하시오.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"그것에 대해서도 하실 말씀이 없으십니까, 선생님?\" 대변인이 물었어.\n\n\"전혀 없소.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 대답했어. 대표단은 선거 운동 때나 선거철에만 그를 봤던 터라, 그의 태연함에 말문이 막혔지. 그는 딴사람 같았어. 그때는 온통 우유와 꿀처럼 다정하더니, 지금은 온통 풀 먹인 옷과 식초처럼 빳빳하고 시큰둥했거든. 하지만 사람이란 때에 따라 이렇게 다른 법이지!\n\n\"질문 제삼 번, 마지막 질문이오.\" 퍼그스타일스 씨가 힘주어 말했어. \"당신은 선거 연단에서, 제안되는 건 무엇이든 반대하겠다고 단호하고 굳게 마음먹었다고 말하지 않았습니까? 모든 안건마다 표결로 의회를 갈라놓고, 사사건건 보고서를 요구하고, 날마다 동의안을 의사록에 올리고, 한마디로 당신 자신의 그 잊지 못할 표현대로, 모든 일과 모든 사람을 상대로 한바탕 난장판을 벌이겠다고 말입니다.\" 이 포괄적인 질문을 끝으로, 퍼그스타일스 씨는 질문 목록을 접었어. 그를 지지하는 사람들도 다 같이 접었지.\n\n그렉스버리 씨는 곰곰이 생각하더니 코를 풀고, 의자에 더 깊숙이 등을 기댔어. 그러다 다시 앞으로 몸을 내밀어 두 팔꿈치를 탁자에 괴고는, 양 엄지와 양 검지로 삼각형을 만들었지. 그러고는 그 꼭짓점으로 코를 톡톡 두드리며 대답했어(말하면서 빙긋 웃었지). \"전부 부인하오.\"\n\n이 뜻밖의 대답에, 대표단 사이에서 쉰 웅성거림이 일었어. 그리고 아까 첫 연설이 허튼소리 같다고 의견을 냈던 바로 그 신사가, 다시 한 단어로 시위를 했지. \"사퇴하시오!\"라고 으르렁댄 거야. 이 으르렁거림을 동료들이 받아 이어가더니, 아주 진지하고 한목소리 같은 항의로 부풀어 올랐어.\n\n\"저는, 선생님, 한 가지 바람을 전해 달라는 부탁을 받았습니다.\" 퍼그스타일스 씨가 서먹하게 인사하며 말했어. \"유권자의 절대다수가 그런 요구서를 보낸다면, 당신이 즉각 의석을 내놓는 데 반대하지 않으셨으면 한다는 겁니다. 그들이 더 믿을 만하다고 여기는 다른 후보를 위해서 말입니다.\"\n\n이에 그렉스버리 씨는 다음과 같은 답을 읽어 줬어. 이런 요구를 미리 내다보고 편지 형식으로 미리 써 둔 거였지. 게다가 신문사마다 돌리려고 사본까지 만들어 둔 거였어.\n\"친애하는 퍼그스타일스 씨께.\n\n우리의 사랑하는 섬나라—이 위대하고 자유롭고 행복한 나라, 그 힘과 자원이 내가 진심으로 믿건대 무한한 이 나라의 안녕 다음으로, 나는 영국인의 가장 자랑스러운 긍지인 그 고귀한 독립을 소중히 여깁니다. 그리고 그것을 한 점 흠도 없이, 한 점 더럼도 없이 내 자식들에게 물려주기를 간절히 바랍니다. 사사로운 동기에서가 아니라, 오직 높고 위대한 헌정적 고려에 따라 움직이는 것입니다. 그 고려가 무엇인지는 굳이 설명하지 않겠습니다. 나처럼 복잡하고 고된 정치 공부를 통달하지 못한 사람들로서는 도저히 이해할 수 없는 것이니까요. 나는 차라리 내 의석을 지키고자 하며, 또 그럴 작정입니다.\n\n부디 유권자 여러분께 내 안부를 전하고, 이 사정을 알려 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주시겠습니까?\n\n깊은 존경을 담아, 친애하는 퍼그스타일스 씨께, 등등 등등.\"\n\n\"그러니까 어떤 경우에도 사퇴하지 않으시겠다는 거지요?\" 대변인이 물었어.\n\n그렉스버리 씨는 빙긋 웃으며 고개를 저었어.\n\n\"그럼, 안녕히 계십시오, 선생님.\" 퍼그스타일스가 화가 나서 말했어.\n\n\"하늘의 축복이 함께하기를!\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어. 그러자 대표단은 갖은 으르렁거림과 험상궂은 얼굴로, 좁은 계단이 허락하는 한 최대한 빠르게 줄지어 내려갔지.\n\n마지막 사람까지 가 버리자, 그렉스버리 씨는 두 손을 비비며 낄낄댔어. 보통 이상으로 잘한 말이나 일을 했다고 여길 때 유쾌한 사람들이 그러듯이 말이야. 그는 이 자화자찬에 어찌나 푹 빠져 있었던지, 니콜라스가 창문 커튼 그늘에 남아 있는 걸 알아채지 못했지. 그러다 그 젊은이가, 그러지 않으면 들으라고 한 게 아닌 혼잣말을 엿듣게 될까 봐, 의원의 주의를 끌려고 두세 번 헛기침을 했어.\n\n\"그게 뭐요?\" 그렉스버리 씨가 날카로운 목소리로 말했어.\n\n니콜라스가 앞으로 나서서 고개를 숙였어.\n\n\"여기서 뭐 하는 거요, 선생?\" 그렉스버리 씨가 물었어. \"내 사생활을 캐는 첩자로군! 숨어든 유권자야! 내 대답은 들었을 테니, 부디 대표단을 따라가시오.\"\n\n\"제가 대표단 소속이었다면 그랬겠지요. 하지만 저는 아닙니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"그럼 어떻게 여기 들어왔단 말이오, 선생?\" 하원 의원 그렉스버리 씨가 당연히 물을 만한 질문이었지. \"그리고 대체 어디서 굴러들어 온 거요, 선생?\" 이어진 질문이었어.\n\n\"종합 직업소개소에서 이 소개장을 받아 왔습니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"선생님의 비서로 저를 추천하려고요. 선생님께서 비서가 필요하시다기에요.\"\n\n\"고작 그것 때문에 왔단 말이오?\" 그렉스버리 씨가 미심쩍은 눈으로 그를 훑어보며 말했어.\n\n니콜라스는 그렇다고 대답했어.\n\n\"혹시 그 못된 신문사 어디랑 엮인 건 아니겠지?\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어. \"여기서 무슨 일이 벌어지나 들으려고 방에 들어와서, 그걸 활자로 찍어 낼 속셈은 아니고, 응?\"\n\n\"안타깝게도 저는 지금 그 무엇과도 엮인 데가 없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 받아쳤어. 충분히 정중하면서도, 아주 느긋하게 말이야.\n\n\"오!\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어. \"그럼 여긴 어떻게 올라온 거요?\"\n\n니콜라스는 대표단에 떠밀려 올라오게 된 사정을 이야기했어.\n\n\"그렇게 된 거였군, 그래?\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어. \"앉으시오.\"\n\n니콜라스가 의자에 앉았어. 그러자 그렉스버리 씨는 한참을 그를 빤히 쳐다봤지. 더 묻기 전에, 그의 겉모습에 흠잡을 데가 없는지 확인하려는 듯이 말이야.\n\n\"내 비서가 되고 싶다, 그 말이오?\" 마침내 그가 말했어.\n\n\"그 자리에 채용되고 싶습니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"좋소.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어. \"그럼, 뭘 할 줄 아시오?\"\n\n\"제 생각엔,\" 니콜라스가 빙긋 웃으며 대답했어. \"보통 다른 비서들이 맡는 일은 할 수 있을 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔데?\" 그렉스버리 씨가 물었어.\n\n\"그게 뭐냐고요?\" 니콜라스가 되물었어.\n\n\"그래! 그게 뭐냐고.\" 의원이 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃하며 빈틈없는 눈으로 그를 바라보며 받아쳤어.\n\n\"비서의 일이란 게 딱 잘라 말하기는 좀 어렵습니다.\" 니콜라스가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했어. \"제가 보기엔, 우선 서신 처리가 들어가겠지요?\"\n\n\"좋소.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 끼어들었어.\n\n\"서류와 문서 정리도 있고요?\"\n\n\"아주 좋소.\"\n\n\"그리고 가끔은 받아쓰기도 하겠지요. 또 어쩌면, 선생님,\" 니콜라스가 슬쩍 미소를 띠며 말했어. \"선생님께서 평소보다 더 중요한 연설을 하셨을 때, 어느 신문에 실으려고 그 연설을 베껴 두는 일도 있겠고요.\"\n\n\"물론이오.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 받았어. \"또 뭐가 있소?\"\n\n\"솔직히,\" 니콜라스가 잠시 생각하고 나서 말했어. \"지금 당장은 비서의 다른 임무를 더 꼽지는 못하겠습니다. 다만 한 가지 두루 통하는 일이라면, 자기 품위를 잃지 않는 선에서, 또 자기가 맡기로 한 일—그러니까 그 직책의 이름이 으레 뜻하는 그 일의 범위를 넘지 않는 선에서, 고용주에게 되도록 마음에 들고 쓸모 있게 처신하는 것이겠지요.\"\n\n그렉스버리 씨는 잠시 니콜라스를 빤히 쳐다보더니, 방 안을 조심스레 둘러보고는 목소리를 낮춰 말했어.\n\n\"다 좋은 말이오, 미스터—이름이 뭐라고 했소?\"\n\n\"니클비입니다.\"\n\n\"다 좋은 말이고 아주 그럴듯하오, 니클비 씨. 거기까지는—거기까지는 좋은데, 충분히 멀리 가질 못하는군. 의회에 몸담은 신사의 비서라면 결코 놓쳐선 안 될 다른 임무가 있단 말이오, 니클비 씨. 나는 머릿속을 채워 줄 사람이 필요하오, 선생.\"\n\n\"죄송하지만요.\" 니콜라스가 끼어들었어. 제대로 들은 건지 긴가민가했거든.\n\n\"—머릿속을 채워 줄 사람 말이오, 선생.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 거듭 말했어.\n\n\"한 번 더 죄송합니다만, 무슨 뜻인지 여쭤봐도 될까요, 선생님?\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"내 말뜻은 더없이 분명하오, 선생.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 자못 엄숙한 얼굴로 대답했어. \"내 비서라면, 신문에 비치는 대로 세계 각국의 외교 정세를 훤히 꿰고 있어야 하오. 모든 공개 집회 기사, 모든 사설, 그리고 공공 기관의 활동 보고를 죄다 훑어봐야 하고. 그리고 탁자에 놓인 어떤 청원에 관한 자그마한 연설이라든가, 뭐 그런 데서 한번 짚고 넘어갈 만하다 싶은 게 있으면 뭐든 메모해 둬야 하지. 알아듣겠소?\"\n\n\"알 것 같습니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"그러니,\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어. \"내 비서는 날마다 일어나는 사건에 관한 신문 기사를 꿰고 있어야 할 거요. 이를테면 '술집 사환의 수상한 실종, 자살 추정' 같은 거 말이오. 뭐 그런 종류의 기사를 가지고, 내가 내무부 장관에게 던질 질문의 근거로 삼는 거지. 그러면 비서는 그 질문을 베끼고, 답변도 내가 기억하는 만큼 베껴야 하오(독립심과 양식에 관한 자그마한 찬사도 곁들여서). 그러고는 그 원고를 무료 우편으로 그 지역 신문에 보내는 거요. 어쩌면 사설 대여섯 줄도 함께 말이오. 내용인즉, 나는 의회의 내 자리를 늘 지키고 있으며, 책임지고 고된 임무를 결코 마다하지 않는다, 뭐 그런 식이지. 알겠소?\"\n\n니콜라스는 고개를 숙였어.\n\n\"그뿐 아니라,\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말을 이었어. \"이따금 인쇄된 통계표의 숫자 몇 개를 짚어 보고, 결과 몇 가지를 뽑아 줬으면 하오. 그래야 내가 목재 관세 문제, 재정 문제 같은 걸로 그럴듯하게 한몫 잡을 수 있거든. 그리고 현금 지불과 금속 화폐로 되돌아가는 게 얼마나 끔찍한 결과를 부르는지에 관한 자그마한 논거 몇 개도 마련해 줬으면 하오. 가끔은 정금 수출이라든가 러시아 황제라든가 은행권 같은 걸 슬쩍 곁들이고 말이오. 그런 건 그저 술술 떠들어 대기만 하면 되는 거요. 아무도 못 알아들으니까. 내 말 알아듣겠소?\"\n\n\"알아들은 것 같습니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"정치와 상관없는 문제들로 말하자면,\" 그렉스버리 씨가 열을 올리며 말을 이었어. \"그런 거야 우리가 눈곱만큼도 신경 쓸 까닭이 없지. 우리보다 못한 사람들이 우리만큼 잘사는 꼴은 두고 보지 않겠다는, 그 당연한 마음 말고는 말이오—안 그러면 우리 특권이 무슨 소용이겠소?—그런 문제라면, 내 비서가 화려한 자그마한 연설 몇 개를, 애국심 넘치는 투로 엮어 줬으면 하오. 이를테면, 글이나 끄적이는 가난뱅이 작가 놈들한테 제 작품에 대한 권리를 준다는 터무니없는 법안이라도 나오면, 나는 이렇게 말하고 싶거든. 적어도 나는 백성들 사이에 문학이 퍼지는 데 결코 넘을 수 없는 벽을 세우는 일에는 절대로 찬성하지 않겠노라고—알아듣겠소?—주머니에서 나온 것은 사람의 것이니 한 사람, 한 집안의 것이 될 수 있다. 하지만 머리에서 나온 것은 하느님의 것이니, 당연히 만백성의 것이 되어야 마땅하다고 말이오. 그리고 기분이 좋으면, 후세에 관해 농담 한마디 던지고 싶거든. 후세를 위해 글을 쓰는 자들은 후세의 칭찬으로 보답받는 데 만족해야 한다고 말이오. 의회에서 먹힐 만한 말이지. 게다가 나한테 해될 것도 전혀 없어. 후세가 나에 대해서든 내 농담에 대해서든 뭘 알 리가 없으니까. 무슨 말인지 알겠소?\"\n\n\"그건 알겠습니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"이런 경우, 그러니까 우리 이익이 걸려 있지 않은 경우엔 늘 명심해야 하오.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어. \"백성에 대해서는 아주 세게 말하는 거요. 선거철에 아주 잘 먹히거든. 그리고 작가들에 대해서는 마음껏 우스갯거리로 삼아도 좋소. 내가 알기로 그치들 태반은 셋방살이를 하니, 유권자도 아니거든. 이게 자네가 해야 할 주된 일들의 대략적인 윤곽이오. 그 밖에 매일 밤 로비에서 대기하는 것도 있고—내가 뭘 빠뜨려서 머릿속을 새로 채워야 할 때가 있으니 말이오. 그리고 가끔, 큰 토론이 있을 때는 방청석 맨 앞줄에 앉아서, 주위 사람들한테 이렇게 말하는 것도 있지. '저 신사분 보이시오? 손을 얼굴에 대고, 팔을 기둥에 휘감은 저분—저분이 그렉스버리 씨요—그 유명한 그렉스버리 씨.' 그때그때 떠오르는 자그마한 찬사도 곁들이고 말이오. 그리고 급료로 말하자면,\" 그렉스버리 씨가 숨이 차서 헐떡이며 무척 빠르게 마무리했어. \"급료로 말하자면, 괜한 불만이 생기지 않게 단도직입으로 어림수로 말해 두겠소—내가 여태 줘 본 것보다 많은 액수이긴 하지만—주급 십오 실링이고, 끼니는 알아서 해결하시오. 자, 어떻소!\"\n\n이 후한 제안을 던지고, 그렉스버리 씨는 다시 의자에 등을 기댔어. 더없이 헤프게 인심을 쓴 사람이지만, 그래도 결코 후회하지는 않겠다고 작정한 사람 같은 표정이었지.\n\n\"주급 십오 실링은 많지 않은데요.\" 니콜라스가 부드럽게 말했어.\n\n\"많지 않다고! 주급 십오 실링이 많지 않다니, 젊은이?\" 그렉스버리 씨가 외쳤어. \"주급 십오 실—\"\n\n\"제가 그 액수를 두고 시비를 거는 거라고 생각하진 말아 주십시오, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"솔직히 털어놓건대, 그 자체가 어떻든 저에게는 큰돈입니다. 부끄러울 것 없어요. 하지만 그 임무와 책임에 비하면 보수가 적습니다. 게다가 그 임무가 어찌나 무거운지, 제가 감당할 수 있을지 두렵습니다.\"\n\n\"맡지 않겠다는 거요, 선생?\" 그렉스버리 씨가 종 끈에 손을 얹으며 물었어.\n\n\"제 의지가 아무리 좋다 해도, 제 힘에 부치는 일인 것 같아 두렵습니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"그건 곧 이 자리를 받지 않겠다는 거고, 주급 십오 실링이 너무 적다고 여긴다는 말이군.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 종을 울리며 말했어. \"거절하는 거요, 선생?\"\n\n\"그럴 수밖에 없을 것 같습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"문, 매슈스!\" 그렉스버리 씨가 소년이 나타나자 말했어.\n\n\"공연히 폐를 끼쳐 죄송합니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"그러게 말이오.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 그에게서 등을 돌리며 받아쳤어. \"문, 매슈스!\"\n\n\"안녕히 계십시오, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"문, 매슈스!\" 그렉스버리 씨가 외쳤어.\n\n소년이 니콜라스에게 손짓을 하고는, 앞장서서 게으름 부리듯 비척비척 계단을 내려가 문을 열고, 그를 거리로 내보냈어. 니콜라스는 슬프고 시름에 잠긴 얼굴로 집을 향해 발걸음을 되짚었지.\n\n스마이크는 간밤에 먹다 남은 것을 그러모아 끼니를 차려 놓고, 그가 돌아오기를 애타게 기다리고 있었어. 아침에 겪은 일들 때문에 니콜라스는 입맛이 돌지 않았지. 그래서 그 음식은 손도 대지 않은 채 그대로 남아 있었어. 그가 생각에 잠긴 자세로 앉아 있었지. 가엾은 친구가 가장 좋은 부분만 골라 정성껏 수북이 담아 둔 접시도, 손도 안 댄 채 곁에 놓여 있었어. 그때 뉴먼 노그스가 방 안을 들여다봤지.\n\n\"돌아왔나?\" 뉴먼이 물었어.\n\n\"네.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"녹초가 됐어요. 게다가 더 딱한 건, 한 일도 없으니 차라리 집에 있을 걸 그랬다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"한나절 만에 큰 걸 바랄 순 없지.\" 뉴먼이 말했어.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르죠. 하지만 저는 희망에 부풀어서 정말 기대를 했거든요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그러니 그만큼 실망도 큰 거죠.\" 이렇게 말하면서, 그는 뉴먼에게 그동안 있었던 일을 들려줬어.\n\n\"무슨 일이든 할 수 있다면,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"아무리 하찮은 일이라도요. 랄프 니클비가 돌아와서, 제가 그자와 맞서 속이라도 풀 때까지 말이에요. 그때까지라도 뭐든 할 수 있다면 마음이 더 편할 것 같아요. 하늘이 아시지만, 저는 일하는 걸 부끄럽게 여기지 않아요. 길든 만 사나운 짐승처럼 여기 빈둥대며 누워 있는 게 저를 미치게 합니다.\"\n\n\"글쎄,\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"자그마한 일거리가 하나 있긴 한데—방세를 내고도 남을 정도지만—자네 마음에는 안 들 거야. 그래, 자네한테 그걸 견디라고 할 순 없지—아니, 아니야.\"\n\n\"제가 뭘 견디기 어렵다는 거죠?\" 니콜라스가 눈을 들며 물었어. \"이 넓디넓은 런던 황무지에서, 이 초라한 방의 주급 방세나마 댈 수 있는 정직한 방도를 보여 주세요. 그래도 제가 거기에 손대길 꺼리는지 한번 보시라고요! 견디라고요! 저는 너무 많은 걸 견뎌 봤어요, 친구. 이제 와 자존심을 내세우거나 까다롭게 굴 처지가 아니에요. 다만—\" 니콜라스가 잠깐 입을 다물었다가 서둘러 덧붙였어. \"다만 그저 정직함에서 나오는 까다로움과, 자존심을 지키는 만큼의 자부심은 빼고요. 잔인한 교사의 조수 노릇이나, 비열하고 무식한 벼락출세자의 아첨꾼 노릇이나, 의원이든 아니든, 별로 다를 게 없다고 봅니다.\"\n\n\"오늘 아침에 들은 얘기를 자네한테 해야 할지 말아야 할지 모르겠군.\" 뉴먼이 말했어.\n\n\"방금 하신 말씀과 관계가 있는 건가요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"그렇다네.\"\n\n\"그럼 제발, 친애하는 친구, 말씀해 주세요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"제발 제 딱한 처지를 헤아려 주세요. 그리고 약속할게요. 당신과 의논하지 않고는 한 발짝도 움직이지 않겠다고요. 그러니 적어도 제 일에 저도 한 표는 행사하게 해 주세요.\"\n\n이 간청에 마음이 움직인 뉴먼은, 도무지 종잡을 수 없고 뒤엉킨 문장들을 더듬더듬 늘어놓았어. 그 요지인즉 이랬지. 그날 아침 켄위그스 부인이 그를 한참 동안 캐물었다는 거였어. 니콜라스와 어떻게 알게 됐는지, 또 니콜라스의 일생과 모험담, 그리고 가문 내력까지 죄다 캐물었다는 거지. 뉴먼은 이 질문들을 할 수 있는 데까지 막아 냈지만, 결국 거세게 몰아붙임을 당해 구석에 몰린 끝에, 이렇게까지 털어놓고 말았다는 거야. 니콜라스는 학식이 대단한 가정교사이며, 자기가 함부로 밝힐 수 없는 어떤 불행에 휘말려 있고, 존슨이라는 이름을 쓴다고 말이지. 그러자 켄위그스 부인은 고마움 때문인지, 야심 때문인지, 어미로서의 자부심 때문인지, 어미로서의 사랑 때문인지, 아니면 이 네 가지 강력한 동기가 한꺼번에 작용한 탓인지, 켄위그스 씨와 은밀히 의논을 했다는 거야. 그러고는 마침내 이런 제안을 들고 돌아왔다는 거지. 존슨 씨가 네 명의 켄위그스 아가씨들에게 프랑스 사람이 쓰는 그대로의 프랑스어를 가르쳐 줬으면 한다고 말이야. 주급으로는 이 나라 통화로 오 실링을 주겠다는 거였어. 켄위그스 아가씨 한 명당 주급 일 실링씩에, 일 실링이 남는 셈이었지. 그 남는 일 실링은, 갓난아기가 문법 공부로 그 값을 받아 갈 만큼 자랄 때까지를 위한 거였어.\n\n\"내가 크게 잘못 본 게 아니라면 말이에요,\" 켄위그스 부인이 이 제안을 하며 말했어. \"그렇게 오래 걸리지도 않을 거예요. 노그스 씨, 이렇게 영리한 아이들은 정말이지 세상에 태어난 적이 없을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"자, 그게 다일세.\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"자네한테 격에 안 맞는 일인 줄은 알아. 하지만 혹시나 자네가—\"\n\n\"혹시나라뇨!\" 니콜라스가 한껏 들떠 외쳤어. \"당연히 하죠. 당장 그 제안을 받겠습니다. 그 갸륵한 어머님께 지체 없이 그렇게 전해 주세요, 친구. 그리고 부인이 원하실 때 언제든 시작할 준비가 되어 있다고요.\"\n\n뉴먼은 기쁜 발걸음으로 서둘러 켄위그스 부인에게 친구가 응했다는 소식을 전하러 갔어. 그러고는 이내 돌아와 이렇게 전했지. 부인은 편하실 때 이층에서 그를 뵙게 되면 기쁘겠다고요. 또 부인이 그 즉시 사람을 보내, 중고 프랑스어 문법책과 회화책을 구해 오게 했다는 거야. 길모퉁이 책방의 육 펜스짜리 헌책 상자 속에서 오래도록 나풀거리던 책이었지. 그리고 가족 모두가 이렇게 품위를 더하게 됐다는 기대에 잔뜩 들떠서, 첫 수업을 당장 시작하고 싶어 한다는 거였어.\n\n여기서 한 가지 짚고 넘어가자면, 니콜라스는 흔히 말하는 그런 의미의 혈기 넘치는 젊은이는 아니었어. 자기가 모욕을 당하면 발끈하고, 남이 부당한 일을 당하면 끼어들어 바로잡아 주기는 했지. 일찍이 창을 겨눠 본 그 어떤 기사 못지않게 대담하고 거리낌 없이 말이야. 하지만 혈기 넘치는 신사들에게서 어김없이 보이는, 그 유별난 냉정함과 거창한 이기심은 없었어. 사실 우리로서는, 그런 신사들이 일어서려는 집안에 도움이 되기는커녕 짐이나 된다고 보는 쪽이야. 우리가 아는 사람 중에 그런 이가 여럿 있는데, 그들은 그 혈기 탓에 비천한 일에는 도무지 안착하질 못하지. 그 혈기라는 게 고작 콧수염이나 기르고 사나운 표정이나 짓는 데서나 드러날 뿐이야. 물론 콧수염과 사나움도 그 나름으로는 꽤 보기 좋고 칭찬할 만한 것들이지. 하지만 우리는 솔직히 바라는 바가 있어. 그런 것들을 기를 거면 혈기 없는 사람들의 돈이 아니라, 제 주머니를 털어 제대로 기르라는 거지.\n\n그러니 니콜라스는 세간에서 말하는 그런 혈기 넘치는 젊은이가 아니었어. 그리고 그는 제 필요를 메우려고 뉴먼 노그스에게 돈을 꾸는 것이, 주급 오 실링에 어린 켄위그스 아이들에게 프랑스어를 가르치는 것보다 더 떳떳지 못한 일이라고 여겼지. 그래서 앞서 말한 대로 선뜻 그 제안을 받아들이고는, 되도록 서둘러 이층으로 올라갔어.\n\n거기서 그는 켄위그스 부인의 점잖은 영접을 받았어. 자기가 그를 보호하고 후원해 주겠다는 뜻을 친절히 담은 태도였지. 그리고 거기엔 릴리빅 씨와 페토커 양도 있었어. 네 명의 켄위그스 아가씨들은 청강석 같은 긴 의자에 앉아 있었고, 갓난아기는 앞에 전나무 쟁반이 달린 자그마한 짐꾼용 의자에 앉아, 머리 없는 장난감 말을 가지고 혼자 놀고 있었지. 그 말이라는 게, 이탈리아식 다리미를 닮은 작은 나무 원통에 구부러진 못 네 개를 받쳐 세운 거였어. 게다가 까만 구두약 위에 빨간 봉랍을 박아 놓은 듯 영리하게 칠해져 있었지.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 존슨 씨?\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했어. \"외삼촌—존슨 씨예요.\"\n\n\"안녕하시오, 선생?\" 릴리빅 씨가 다소 퉁명스럽게 말했어. 전날 밤에는 니콜라스가 어떤 사람인지 몰랐던 데다, 세금 징수원이 한낱 교사에게 너무 정중하게 굴었다면 그건 좀 분통 터지는 일이었거든.\n\n\"존슨 씨가 아이들의 개인 교사로 채용됐어요, 외삼촌.\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"방금 그렇게 말하지 않았느냐, 얘야.\" 릴리빅 씨가 대답했어.\n\n\"하지만 저는 바라요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 자세를 꼿꼿이 세우며 말했어. \"이 일로 아이들이 우쭐대지 않기를요. 그저 자기네 행운에 감사하기를 바랄 뿐이에요. 보통 사람들 자식보다 나은 신분으로 태어났다는 그 행운에요. 듣고 있니, 몰리나?\"\n\n\"네, 엄마.\" 켄위그스 양이 대답했어.\n\n\"그리고 길에 나가거나 어디 가서든, 다른 아이들한테 이걸 자랑하지 마라.\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했어. \"혹시 꼭 무슨 말을 해야 한다면, 그냥 이렇게만 말하렴. '우리는 집으로 와서 가르쳐 주는 개인 교사가 있어. 하지만 우린 우쭐대지 않아. 엄마가 그건 죄받을 일이라고 했거든.' 알아들었니, 몰리나?\"\n\n\"네, 엄마.\" 켄위그스 양이 또 대답했어.\n\n\"그럼 꼭 기억하고, 엄마가 시킨 대로 하렴.\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했어. \"존슨 씨더러 시작하라고 할까요, 외삼촌?\"\n\n\"존슨 씨가 시작할 준비가 됐다면, 나야 들을 준비가 됐다, 얘야.\" 징수원이 자못 깊이 있는 비평가 같은 태도를 지으며 말했어. \"선생은 프랑스어를 어떤 언어라고 보시오?\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"좋은 언어라고 보냐는 말이오, 선생?\" 징수원이 말했어. \"예쁜 언어, 똑똑한 언어 말이오.\"\n\n\"예쁜 언어인 건 분명합니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그리고 무엇이든 부르는 이름이 있고, 무엇에 대해서든 우아하게 대화할 수 있으니, 똑똑한 언어라고 봐도 될 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"글쎄요.\" 릴리빅 씨가 미심쩍은 듯 말했어. \"그럼, 그게 명랑한 언어라고 보시오?\"\n\n\"네.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그건 분명히 그렇다고 말할 수 있겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 내 시절하고는 아주 많이 달라졌구먼.\" 징수원이 말했어. \"아주 많이 말이오.\"\n\n\"선생님 시절엔 침울한 언어였나요?\" 니콜라스가 웃음을 간신히 참으며 물었어.\n\n\"아주 그랬지.\" 릴리빅 씨가 자못 격하게 대답했어. \"내가 말하는 건 전쟁 때요. 지난번 전쟁 말이오. 명랑한 언어일지도 모르지. 누구 말을 반박하고 싶진 않소. 하지만 이것만은 말할 수 있소. 나는 프랑스 포로들이 하는 말을 들어 봤거든. 그치들은 토박이니 프랑스어를 제대로 할 줄 알 텐데, 어찌나 침울하게 말하는지, 듣고 있으면 사람이 다 비참해질 지경이었소. 그렇다니까, 그런 적이 쉰 번도 넘소, 선생—쉰 번도 넘게!\"\n\n릴리빅 씨가 어찌나 심사가 뒤틀려 가는지, 켄위그스 부인은 니콜라스에게 아무 말도 하지 말라고 눈짓하는 게 상책이라고 여겼지. 그리고 페토커 양이 이 훌륭한 노신사를 누그러뜨리려고 갖은 알랑거림을 부린 뒤에야, 그가 비로소 침묵을 깨고 입을 열어 물었어.\n\n\"프랑스어로 물이 뭐요, 선생?\"\n\n\"로(L'EAU)입니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"아!\" 릴리빅 씨가 구슬프게 고개를 저으며 말했어. \"그럴 줄 알았소. 로, 응? 그딴 언어는 아무것도 아니라고 보오—전혀 아무것도 아니지.\"\n\n\"그럼 아이들 시작해도 되겠지요, 외삼촌?\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"오, 그래. 시작해도 된다, 얘야.\" 징수원이 마뜩잖게 대답했어. \"내가 못 하게 막을 생각은 없으니까.\"\n\n이 허락이 떨어지자, 네 명의 켄위그스 아가씨들이 한 줄로 앉았어. 댕기를 모두 한쪽으로 늘어뜨리고, 맨 위에 몰리나가 앉았지. 그동안 니콜라스는 책을 집어 들고 첫 설명을 시작했어. 페토커 양과 켄위그스 부인은 말없이 감탄하며 지켜봤지. 그 침묵을 깨는 건 오직, 몰리나가 금세 죄다 외워 버릴 거라는 부인의 속삭이는 장담뿐이었어. 그리고 릴리빅 씨는 인상을 찌푸린 채 주의 깊은 눈으로 그 무리를 바라봤지. 이 언어를 두고 새로 논쟁을 벌일 만한 꼬투리가 없나 호시탐탐 노리면서 말이야.\n\n케이트 니클비가 마담 맨탈리니와 일을 시작하기로 정해진 날 아침, 그 무거운 마음을 안고 길을 나섰어. 아무리 애를 써도 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 슬픈 예감이 잔뜩 들어찬 채였지. 시내 시계들이 여덟 시까지 아직 십오 분이 남았을 무렵, 케이트는 도시를 빠져나왔어. 그리고 시끌벅적하고 북적이는 거리를 혼자서 헤치고, 런던 서쪽 끝을 향해 걸어갔지.\n\n이 이른 시간에 병약한 처녀들이 많이도 거리를 누벼. 그들이 하는 일이란, 가엾은 누에처럼 끈기 있게 애써서 생각 없고 사치스러운 사람들을 꾸며 줄 고운 옷가지를 만들어 내는 거야. 그들은 하루 일터를 향해 걸음을 재촉하면서, 그 바쁜 걸음 가운데 마치 몰래 훔치듯이 맑은 공기 한 모금과 햇빛 한 줄기를 챙기지. 일하는 하루를 이루는 그 긴 시간들 동안, 그것만이 단조로운 삶을 기쁘게 해 주는 유일한 거니까. 케이트는 좀 더 멋진 동네로 다가가면서, 자기처럼 고된 일터로 서둘러 가는 이런 부류의 처녀들을 많이도 눈여겨봤어. 그리고 그들의 병색 짙은 얼굴과 비틀거리는 걸음걸이에서, 자기 불길한 예감이 아주 근거 없는 건 아니라는 너무도 또렷한 증거를 보았지.\n\n케이트는 약속한 시간보다 몇 분 일찍 마담 맨탈리니의 집에 닿았어. 혹시 다른 여자가 와서, 하인에게 용건을 말해야 하는 어색함을 덜어 줄까 싶어 몇 번 왔다 갔다 한 다음, 조심스럽게 문을 두드렸지. 한참 만에 하인이 문을 열었어. 그는 계단을 올라오면서 줄무늬 윗도리를 걸치던 참이었고, 이제는 앞치마를 매느라 정신이 없었어.\n\n\"마담 맨탈리니 계신가요?\" 케이트가 더듬더듬 물었어.\n\n\"이 시간에 나가 계시는 일은 잘 없죠, 아가씨.\" 하인이 대답했는데, 그 말투 때문에 \"아가씨\"라는 말이 \"이봐요\"보다도 더 거슬리게 들렸지.\n\n\"뵐 수 있을까요?\" 케이트가 물었어.\n\n\"네?\" 하인이 문손잡이를 잡은 채 대꾸했어. 그러고는 묻는 사람을 빤히 쳐다보더니 입을 헤벌쭉 벌리며 빈정대듯 말했지. \"원, 안 되죠.\"\n\n\"마님이 직접 약속하셔서 왔어요.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"저는요, 저는, 여기서 일하게 될 사람이거든요.\"\n\n\"아! 그럼 일꾼용 초인종을 눌렀어야죠.\" 하인이 문기둥에 달린 초인종 하나의 손잡이를 만지며 말했어. \"그런데 가만, 내가 깜빡했네. 니클비 아가씨, 맞죠?\"\n\n\"네.\" 케이트가 대답했어.\n\n\"그럼 위층으로 올라가셔야 해요.\" 하인이 말했어. \"마담 맨탈리니가 보자고 하셨거든요. 이쪽이에요. 바닥에 있는 것들 조심하시고요.\"\n\n이런 말로 케이트한테 조심하라고 일러 준 거야. 현관 바닥에는 제과점 쟁반이며 등잔이며 잔이 잔뜩 실린 받침이며 연회용 의자 더미가 여기저기 어수선하게 널려 있었거든. 누가 봐도 전날 밤 늦게까지 파티가 있었다는 걸 알 수 있었지. 하인은 앞장서서 이층으로 케이트를 데려갔어. 그리고 안쪽 방으로 안내했는데, 그 방은 접이문으로 다른 방과 통했어. 케이트가 처음에 그 가게 안주인을 만났던 바로 그 방이었지.\n\n\"여기서 잠깐 기다리세요.\" 하인이 말했어. \"곧 마님께 알릴 테니까요.\" 이렇게 아주 상냥하게 약속하고는 물러갔고, 케이트만 혼자 남았어.\n\n방 안에는 별로 눈요깃거리가 없었어. 그나마 제일 눈길을 끄는 건, 맨탈리니 씨를 그린 반신 유화 한 점이었지. 화가는 그를 한가롭게 머리를 긁적이는 모습으로 그려 놨는데, 덕분에 다이아몬드 반지가 보기 좋게 드러났어. 마담 맨탈리니가 결혼 전에 선물한 반지였지. 그런데 옆방에서 두런두런 이야기 나누는 소리가 들렸어. 게다가 대화 소리는 컸고 칸막이는 얇았으니, 케이트는 그게 맨탈리니 씨 부부의 목소리라는 걸 알아챌 수밖에 없었지.\n\n\"내 영혼이여, 당신이 그렇게 밉살스럽고 빌어먹게 어처구니없이 질투를 부린다면,\" 맨탈리니 씨가 말했어. \"당신은 아주 비참해질 거야. 끔찍하게 비참하지. 빌어먹게 비참할 거라고.\" 그러더니 맨탈리니 씨가 커피를 홀짝이는 듯한 소리가 났어.\n\n\"나는 정말 비참하다고요.\" 마담 맨탈리니가 입을 삐죽 내밀고 대꾸했어.\n\n\"그럼 당신은 은혜도 모르고, 자격도 없고, 빌어먹게 고마운 줄도 모르는 작은 요정이야.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"아니거든요.\" 마담이 흐느끼며 대꾸했어.\n\n\"기분 상해하지 말라고.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 달걀을 깨며 말했어. \"그건 예쁘고 사람 홀리는 빌어먹게 깜찍한 얼굴이야. 그러니 기분 상해하면 안 돼. 그러면 그 사랑스러움이 다 망가지거든. 그리고 무섭고 못된 빌어먹을 도깨비처럼 심술궂고 침울해 보인다고.\"\n\n\"나는 늘 그런 식으로 넘어가지는 않아요.\" 마담이 토라진 채 받아쳤어.\n\n\"제일 좋아하는 방식으로 풀어 주지. 풀어 주지 않는 게 더 좋다면 아예 안 풀어 줘도 되고.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 달걀 숟가락을 입에 문 채 받아쳤어.\n\n\"말이야 쉽죠.\" 마담 맨탈리니가 말했어.\n\n\"빌어먹을 달걀을 먹고 있을 땐 그리 쉽지가 않다고.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 대답했어. \"노른자가 조끼에 줄줄 흐르거든. 그런데 달걀노른자는 노란 조끼 말고는 어떤 조끼하고도 안 어울려, 젠장.\"\n\n\"당신은 밤새도록 그 여자랑 시시덕거렸어요.\" 마담 맨탈리니가 말했어. 빗나갔던 대화를 다시 원래 자리로 돌려놓고 싶은 모양이었지.\n\n\"아니야, 아니야, 내 생명이여.\"\n\n\"맞아요.\" 마담이 말했어. \"내가 내내 당신을 지켜봤다고요.\"\n\n\"깜빡깜빡 반짝이는 그 작은 눈에 복이 있기를. 그게 내내 나를 보고 있었다고!\" 맨탈리니가 어쩐지 나른한 황홀경에 빠져 외쳤어. \"오, 젠장!\"\n\n\"그리고 다시 한번 말하는데,\" 마담이 말을 이었어. \"당신은 당신 아내 말고는 누구하고도 왈츠를 추면 안 돼요. 안 그러면 가만있지 않을 거예요, 맨탈리니. 내가 먼저 독약을 마셔 버릴 테니까.\"\n\n\"독약을 마시고 끔찍한 고통을 겪지는 않을 거지, 그렇지?\" 맨탈리니가 말했어. 목소리가 달라진 걸 보니, 의자를 옮겨서 아내 가까이 자리를 잡은 모양이었지. \"독약은 안 마실 거야. 왜냐하면 당신한테는 빌어먹게 멋진 남편이 있으니까. 백작 부인 둘하고 미망인 하나하고도 결혼할 수 있었던 남편이 말이야.\"\n\n\"백작 부인 둘이라고요.\" 마담이 끼어들었어. \"전에는 하나라고 했잖아요!\"\n\n\"둘이야!\" 맨탈리니가 외쳤어. \"빌어먹게 멋진 여자 둘. 진짜 백작 부인에다 어마어마한 재산까지, 젠장.\"\n\n\"그런데 왜 안 했어요?\" 마담이 장난스럽게 물었어.\n\n\"왜 안 했냐고!\" 남편이 대답했어. \"내가 어느 날 오전 음악회에서, 온 세상에서 제일 빌어먹게 사람 홀리는 작은 여자를 보지 않았겠어. 그리고 그 작은 마법사가 내 아내인 한, 잉글랜드의 백작 부인이고 미망인이고 다 같이 ─\"\n\n맨탈리니 씨는 그 문장을 끝맺지 않았어. 대신 마담 맨탈리니한테 아주 요란한 입맞춤을 했지. 마담 맨탈리니도 마주 입맞춤을 했어. 그러고 나서는 아침 식사가 이어지는 와중에 입맞춤이 좀 더 섞여 드는 것 같았지.\n\n\"그런데 돈은 어떻게 됐어, 내 인생의 보석?\" 이 살가운 짓들이 잦아들자 맨탈리니가 말했어. \"수중에 얼마나 있어?\"\n\n\"정말 얼마 없어요.\" 마담이 대답했어.\n\n\"좀 더 있어야 하는데.\" 맨탈리니가 말했어. \"늙은 니클비한테서 할인을 좀 받아서 이 싸움을 계속해 나가야지, 젠장.\"\n\n\"지금 당장 더 필요할 리는 없잖아요.\" 마담이 어르듯 말했어.\n\n\"내 생명이자 영혼이여,\" 남편이 대답했어. \"스크럽스네 가게에 팔려고 내놓은 말이 한 마리 있는데, 그걸 놓치는 건 죄악이자 범죄야. 내 감각의 기쁨이여, 거저나 다름없이 넘어가는 거라고.\"\n\n\"거저나 다름없다고요.\" 마담이 외쳤어. \"그것참 다행이네요.\"\n\n\"정말로 거저나 다름없어.\" 맨탈리니가 대답했어. \"현금 백 기니면 그 말을 살 수 있지. 갈기며 목덜미며 다리며 꼬리며, 전부 빌어먹게 아름다워. 차여 버린 백작 부인들 마차 바로 앞에서 그 말을 타고 공원을 달릴 거야. 그 빌어먹을 늙은 미망인은 슬픔과 분노로 기절할 거고. 나머지 둘은 이렇게 말하겠지. '저 남자는 결혼했어, 제 발로 사라져 버렸어, 빌어먹을 일이야, 다 끝났어!' 그 둘은 서로를 빌어먹게 미워하면서, 당신이 죽어 묻혀 버리기를 바랄걸. 하하! 젠장.\"\n\n마담 맨탈리니가 신중함이라는 걸 갖고 있었다 해도, 그 신중함은 이런 의기양양한 그림 앞에서는 버티지 못했어. 그녀는 열쇠를 짤랑짤랑 흔들더니, 책상 안에 뭐가 들었는지 봐야겠다고 했지. 그러고는 그러려고 일어나, 접이문을 열고 케이트가 앉아 있는 방으로 걸어 들어왔어.\n\n\"어머나, 얘야!\" 마담 맨탈리니가 깜짝 놀라 뒤로 물러서며 소리쳤어. \"너 여기 어떻게 왔니?\"\n\n\"얘야!\" 맨탈리니가 서둘러 들어오며 외쳤어. \"어떻게 왔, 엥! 오, 젠장, 안녕하시오?\"\n\n\"여기서 한참을 기다렸어요, 마님.\" 케이트가 마담 맨탈리니를 향해 말했어. \"하인이 제가 와 있는 걸 알려 드리는 걸 깜빡한 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그 사람을 정말 단단히 일러둬야겠어요.\" 마담이 남편을 돌아보며 말했어. \"그자는 뭐든지 다 깜빡한다니까요.\"\n\n\"이렇게 예쁜 아가씨를 혼자 내버려 둔 죄로, 그자의 빌어먹을 코를 얼굴에서 비틀어 떼어 내야겠군.\" 남편이 말했어.\n\n\"맨탈리니,\" 마담이 외쳤어. \"당신 정신 좀 차려요.\"\n\n\"당신을 잊은 적은 없어, 내 영혼이여. 앞으로도 결코 없을 거고, 그럴 수도 없지.\" 맨탈리니가 아내의 손에 입을 맞추며 말했어. 그러고는 옆으로 고개를 돌려 니클비 아가씨한테 우스꽝스러운 표정을 지어 보였지만, 케이트는 외면해 버렸어.\n\n이 칭찬에 누그러진 가게 안주인은 책상에서 서류 몇 장을 꺼내 맨탈리니 씨한테 건넸어. 맨탈리니 씨는 그걸 아주 기쁘게 받았지. 그러고 나서 마담은 케이트더러 따라오라고 했어. 그리고 맨탈리니 씨가 그 젊은 아가씨의 관심을 끌어 보려고 몇 번 수작을 부린 다음, 두 사람은 자리를 떴어. 그 신사는 소파 위에 발뒤꿈치를 허공에 올린 채 길게 드러누워, 손에 신문 한 장을 들고 있었지.\n\n마담 맨탈리니가 앞장서서 계단을 하나 내려가더니, 복도를 지나 건물 뒤쪽에 있는 큰 방으로 갔어. 거기에는 젊은 여자들이 여럿 있었는데, 바느질하고, 마름질하고, 옷을 짓고, 고치고, 그 밖에 모자나 옷 만드는 재주에 익숙한 사람들이나 알 법한 갖가지 일을 하고 있었지. 천장에 채광창이 하나 달린 답답한 방이었어. 그리고 방이라고 하면 으레 그러려니 싶을 만큼 칙칙하고 조용했지.\n\n마담 맨탈리니가 큰 소리로 미스 내그를 부르자, 키 작고 부산스럽고 옷을 잔뜩 차려입은 여자가 나타났어. 자기가 대단한 사람이라는 티가 잔뜩 났지. 그러자 젊은 아가씨들이 다들 하던 일을 잠깐 멈추고, 니클비 아가씨의 옷 모양새며 옷감이며 얼굴빛이며 이목구비며 차림새에 대해 이러쿵저러쿵 서로 소곤거렸어. 사람으로 붐비는 무도회장에서 최고 상류 사회 사람들이 보여 줄 법한 만큼의 점잖음을 갖추고서 말이야.\n\n\"오, 미스 내그,\" 마담 맨탈리니가 말했어. \"이 아가씨가 내가 당신한테 말했던 그 사람이에요.\"\n\n미스 내그는 마담 맨탈리니한테 공손한 미소를 지어 보이더니, 그 미소를 능숙하게 바꿔서 케이트한테 친절한 미소로 보냈어. 그러고는 이렇게 말했지. 일에 전혀 익숙지 않은 젊은 사람을 데리고 있는 게 무척 성가신 일이긴 하지만, 그래도 이 아가씨가 최선을 다하려고 애쓸 거라고 믿는다고. 그런 확신이 들고 보니, 자기는(미스 내그 말이야) 벌써 이 아가씨한테 관심이 간다고 말이야.\n\n\"내 생각엔 적어도 당분간은, 니클비 아가씨를 당신과 함께 진열실에 두고 손님들한테 옷을 입혀 보는 일을 맡기는 게 낫겠어요.\" 마담 맨탈리니가 말했어. \"당분간은 다른 일로는 별로 쓸모가 없을 테니까요. 그리고 저 아가씨 생김새가 ─\"\n\n\"제 생김새하고 아주 잘 어울리죠, 마담 맨탈리니.\" 미스 내그가 끼어들었어. \"그럼요, 그렇고말고요. 사실 마님이 그걸 금방 알아채실 거라는 걸 제가 진작 알았어야 했는데 말이죠. 마님은 그런 일에 안목이 어찌나 뛰어나신지, 정말이지 제가 젊은 아가씨들한테 자주 하는 말이지만, 마님이 알고 계신 그 모든 걸 도대체 어떻게, 언제, 어디서 익히셨는지 저는 도무지 모르겠다니까요. 흠. 니클비 아가씨하고 저는 그야말로 한 쌍이에요, 마담 맨탈리니. 다만 제가 니클비 아가씨보다 살빛이 좀 더 짙고, 흠, 제 생각엔 발도 제 발이 좀 더 작을 것 같네요. 우리 집안은 옛날부터, 흠, 사실 우리 집안에 발이라는 게 달리기 시작한 그때부터 늘 작은 발로 유명했다는 걸 니클비 아가씨가 들으면, 제가 이런 말을 해도 기분 나빠하지 않을 거예요. 저한테 옛날에 삼촌이 한 분 계셨는데요, 마담 맨탈리니, 첼튼엄에 사셨고 담배 가게로 아주 잘되는 장사를 하셨죠. 흠. 그 삼촌 발이 어찌나 작았던지, 흔히 의족에 붙어 있는 발보다도 크지 않았다니까요. 마님조차 상상하실 수 있는 한 제일 균형 잡힌 발이었죠, 마담 맨탈리니.\"\n\n\"그럼 그건 안짱발 비슷하게 생겼겠네요, 미스 내그.\" 마담이 말했어.\n\n\"어머나, 그게 바로 마님다운 말씀이에요.\" 미스 내그가 받아쳤어. \"하! 하! 하! 안짱발이라니! 오, 정말 멋져요! 제가 젊은 아가씨들한테 자주 하는 말이지만, '글쎄, 누가 알든 상관없이 말하겠는데, 내가 어디서든 들어 본 그 모든 즉석 재치 중에서, 흠' 사실 저는 어지간히 많이 들어 봤거든요. 우리 사랑하는 오빠가 살아 있을 때(제가 오빠 집 살림을 맡았었죠, 니클비 아가씨), 일주일에 한 번씩 그 시절에 재치로 아주 유명했던 젊은 남자 두세 명을 저녁 식사에 초대했으니까요, 마담 맨탈리니. '내가 들어 본 그 모든 즉석 재치 중에서', 제가 젊은 아가씨들한테 이렇게 말하죠. '마담 맨탈리니의 재치가 가장 놀라워, 흠. 어찌나 부드럽고, 어찌나 풍자적이면서도, 또 어찌나 너그러운지(오늘 아침에도 미스 시먼즈한테 한 말이지만), 그걸 어떻게, 언제, 무슨 수로 익히셨는지 나로서는 정말 수수께끼야'라고요.\"\n\n여기서 미스 내그는 숨을 돌리려고 말을 멈췄어. 그녀가 잠깐 멈춘 사이에 한 가지 짚어 둘 게 있어. 그녀가 놀랄 만큼 수다스럽고 마담 맨탈리니한테 놀랄 만큼 굽실거린다는 건 굳이 말할 것도 없는 사실이니까 빼고 말이야. 그녀는 말을 폭포처럼 쏟아내는 와중에 이따금 크고 새되고 또렷한 \"흠!\" 소리를 끼워 넣는 버릇이 있었거든. 그 소리가 무슨 뜻인지는 그녀를 아는 사람마다 제각각 해석했어. 어떤 사람들은 미스 내그가 과장을 일삼는데, 머릿속에서 뭔가 새로운 거짓말을 지어내는 중일 때 그 외마디를 끼워 넣는다고 했지. 또 어떤 사람들은, 그녀가 단어가 생각 안 날 때 시간을 벌고 다른 사람이 끼어들지 못하게 하려고 그 소리를 던지는 거라고 했어. 한 가지 더 짚어 두자면, 미스 내그는 젊음에서 이미 몇 해 전에 한참 벗어났는데도 여전히 젊은 척했다는 거야. 그리고 그녀는 마음이 약하고 허영심이 많아서, 이런 격언으로 가장 잘 설명되는 부류였지. 눈에 보이는 데까지만 믿을 수 있고, 그 이상은 못 믿을 사람이라는 격언 말이야.\n\n\"니클비 아가씨가 근무 시간이며 그런 것들을 잘 알아듣도록 챙겨 주세요.\" 마담 맨탈리니가 말했어. \"그럼 이 아가씨를 당신한테 맡길게요. 내 지시 잊지 않으실 거죠, 미스 내그?\"\n\n미스 내그는 물론 이렇게 대답했지. 마담 맨탈리니가 지시한 걸 잊어버리는 건 도덕적으로 불가능한 일이라고. 그러자 마담은 직원들한테 두루 아침 인사를 건네고는 미끄러지듯 떠나갔어.\n\n\"매력적인 분이지, 안 그래, 니클비 아가씨?\" 미스 내그가 두 손을 비비며 말했어.\n\n\"제가 그분을 거의 못 봬서요.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"아직 잘 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"맨탈리니 씨는 봤어?\" 미스 내그가 물었어.\n\n\"네, 두 번 뵀어요.\"\n\n\"그분도 매력적인 분 아니야?\"\n\n\"제가 보기엔 아무래도 그렇지가 않은데요.\" 케이트가 대답했어.\n\n\"어머나, 얘야!\" 미스 내그가 두 손을 치켜들며 외쳤어. \"아니, 세상에 맙소사, 네 안목은 어디다 둔 거니? 그렇게 늘씬하고 키 크고 구레나룻이 멋진 데다 세련된 신사양반을, 그런 치아에 그런 머릿결을, 흠, 글쎄, 너 정말 날 놀라게 하는구나.\"\n\n\"제가 어리석은가 봐요.\" 케이트가 보닛을 내려놓으며 대답했어. \"하지만 제 의견이라는 게 그분한테든 누구한테든 별로 중요하지도 않으니, 그렇게 생각한 걸 후회하지는 않아요. 그리고 그 생각을 쉽게 바꾸지도 않을 것 같고요.\"\n\n\"그분 정말 멋진 분 아니에요?\" 젊은 아가씨 하나가 물었어.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르죠, 아니라고 할 만한 게 저한테는 없으니까요.\" 케이트가 대답했어.\n\n\"그리고 아주 아름다운 말들도 모시잖아요, 안 그래요?\" 또 다른 아가씨가 물었어.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르죠, 하지만 저는 그 말들을 본 적이 없어요.\" 케이트가 대답했어.\n\n\"본 적이 없다고!\" 미스 내그가 끼어들었어. \"오, 저런! 자, 그러니까 당장 이게 문제라는 거야. 신사를 그분이 차려입고 나선 모습 전부로 보지 않으면, 흠, 그분에 대해 무슨 의견을 내놓을 수가 있겠니?\"\n\n이 늙은 모자 장수의 생각에는 세상 물정이라는 게 잔뜩 들어 있었어. 시골 처녀의 작은 세상에 비춰 봐도 그랬지. 무슨 까닭에서든 화제를 돌리고 싶었던 케이트는 더 이상 말을 잇지 않고, 미스 내그한테 마당을 통째로 내줬어.\n\n잠깐 침묵이 흘렀어. 그동안 젊은 사람들 대부분이 케이트의 차림새를 좀 더 자세히 뜯어보고 그것에 대해 서로 의견을 견줬지. 그러다 그중 하나가 케이트의 숄 벗는 걸 도와주겠다고 했어. 케이트가 그 호의를 받아들이자, 그 아가씨는 검은 옷이 입기에 아주 불편하지 않냐고 물었어.\n\n\"정말 그래요.\" 케이트가 쓰디쓴 한숨을 쉬며 대답했어.\n\n\"먼지도 많이 타고 덥기도 하고요.\" 같은 사람이 케이트의 옷매무새를 바로잡아 주며 말했어.\n\n케이트는 이렇게 말할 수도 있었을 거야. 상복이란 때로 사람이 걸칠 수 있는 가장 차가운 옷이라고. 그건 그 옷을 입은 사람의 가슴을 얼릴 뿐 아니라, 그 영향이 좋은 시절의 친구들한테까지 뻗쳐서 그들의 호의와 친절의 샘까지 얼려 버리지. 그리고 그들이 전에 그토록 아낌없이 피워 냈던 약속의 꽃봉오리를 죄다 시들게 하고는, 헐벗고 썩은 마음만 드러낸 채 남겨 두는 거야. 살면서 자기가 유일하게 기댔던 친구나 친척을 잃은 사람치고, 검은 옷의 이 차가운 영향을 사무치게 느껴 보지 않은 사람은 드물어. 케이트는 그걸 뼈저리게 느꼈고, 그 순간에도 느끼면서, 눈물을 차마 다 참지 못했지.\n\n\"생각 없이 한 말로 아가씨 마음을 아프게 해서 정말 미안해요.\" 그 동료가 말했어. \"거기까지는 생각을 못 했어요. 가까운 친척 상중인가요?\"\n\n\"아버지요.\" 케이트가 대답했어.\n\n\"무슨 친척이래, 미스 시먼즈?\" 미스 내그가 들으라는 듯한 목소리로 물었어.\n\n\"아버지래요.\" 다른 아가씨가 나직이 대답했어.\n\n\"아버지라고, 응?\" 미스 내그가 목소리를 조금도 낮추지 않은 채 말했어. \"아! 오래 앓으셨대, 미스 시먼즈?\"\n\n\"쉿,\" 그 아가씨가 대답했어. \"저도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"갑작스러운 불행이었어요.\" 케이트가 고개를 돌리며 말했어. \"안 그랬다면 이런 때에 제가 좀 더 잘 견뎌 낼 수 있었을지도 모르는데.\"\n\n그 방에서는 으레 그렇듯이, 새 '아가씨'가 오면 케이트가 누구인지, 무엇을 하던 사람인지, 그녀에 관한 모든 걸 알고 싶어 하는 마음이 적잖이 있었어. 그리고 그 호기심은 케이트의 생김새와 감정 때문에 아주 자연스럽게 더 커졌을 법도 했지. 하지만 케이트가 질문받는 걸 아파한다는 걸 알게 되자, 그것만으로도 그 호기심을 누르기에 충분했어. 미스 내그는 당장 더 캐물어 봤자 별 소득이 없겠다 싶었던지, 마지못해 조용히 하라고 명하고는 일을 계속하라고 했어.\n\n그렇게 한 시 반까지 다들 말없이 일에 매달렸어. 한 시 반이 되자 구운 양 다릿살에다 거기 맞춘 감자가 부엌에 차려졌지. 식사가 끝나고 젊은 아가씨들이 손 씻는 여유까지 누리고 나자, 일은 다시 시작됐어. 그리고 또 말없이 진행됐지. 마차들이 거리를 덜컹덜컹 달리는 소리와 문마다 요란하게 두 번씩 두드리는 소리가 들리기 전까지 말이야. 그 소리는 세상에서 좀 더 운 좋은 사람들의 하루 일과가 이제 막 시작됐다는 신호였어.\n\n마담 맨탈리니네 문을 그렇게 두 번 두드린 소리 가운데 하나는, 어느 대단한 귀부인의, 아니 차라리 돈 많은 부인의 마차가 왔다는 걸 알리는 거였어. 부유함과 대단함은 가끔 별개니까 말이야. 그 부인은 딸을 데리고 왔는데, 오랫동안 준비해 온 궁정 예복 몇 벌을 살펴보러 온 거였지. 케이트는 그 부인의 시중을 들도록 차출됐어. 미스 내그가 함께했고, 물론 마담 맨탈리니가 지휘를 맡았지.\n\n이 볼거리에서 케이트가 맡은 역할은 보잘것없었어. 그녀가 하는 일이란 미스 내그가 옷을 입혀 볼 준비가 될 때까지 옷가지를 들고 있는 것, 그리고 가끔 끈을 매거나 걸쇠를 채우는 것뿐이었으니까. 케이트는 자기가 어떤 거만함이나 심술도 닿지 못할 자리에 있다고 생각했을 만도 해. 그게 무리한 생각도 아니었고. 그런데 마침 그 부인과 딸이 그날 둘 다 기분이 언짢았던 거야. 그래서 가엾은 케이트도 그들의 욕설에서 제 몫을 받게 됐지. 케이트는 굼뜨다는 둥, 손이 차다는 둥, 더럽다는 둥, 거칠다는 둥, 제대로 하는 게 하나도 없다는 둥. 그들은 마담 맨탈리니가 어떻게 이런 사람들을 곁에 둘 수 있냐며 의아해했어. 다음에 올 때는 다른 아가씨를 보게 해 달라고 했고, 그런 식이었지.\n\n이렇게 흔한 일은 그 결과만 아니라면 굳이 입에 올릴 만한 것도 못 돼. 케이트는 그 사람들이 가고 나자 쓰디쓴 눈물을 많이도 흘렸어. 그리고 자기 일 때문에 처음으로 모욕감을 느꼈지. 케이트가 힘든 잡일과 고된 노동을 앞두고 주춤했던 건 사실이야. 하지만 빵을 벌려고 일하는 것에서는 아무런 비참함도 느끼지 않았었지. 무례함과 오만함에 노출되기 전까지는 말이야. 철학이라면 케이트한테 이렇게 가르쳐 줬을 거야. 비참함은 오히려 까닭도 없이 그런 감정을 버릇처럼 드러낼 만큼 천하게 굴러떨어진 사람들 쪽에 있는 거라고. 하지만 케이트는 그런 위안을 받기에는 너무 어렸고, 그녀의 진솔한 감정은 상처를 입었어. 평범한 사람들이 제 분수를 넘어선다는 불평은, 사실 평범하지 않은 사람들이 제 분수에 못 미치는 데서 비롯되는 경우가 많지 않을까?\n\n이런 장면과 일 속에서 시간은 아홉 시까지 흘러갔어. 그제야 케이트는 그날 있었던 일로 지치고 풀이 죽은 채, 답답한 작업실에서 서둘러 빠져나왔지. 길모퉁이에서 어머니와 만나 집까지 걸어가려고 말이야. 그런데 그 걸음이 더 서글펐던 건, 진짜 속마음을 감추고 어머니의 온갖 장밋빛 환상에 함께 들떠 있는 척해야 했기 때문이야.\n\n\"어머나 세상에, 케이트,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"내가 오늘 하루 종일 무슨 생각을 했냐면, 마담 맨탈리니가 너를 동업자로 들이면 얼마나 좋을까 하는 거였단다. 그것도 충분히 있을 법한 일이고 말이야, 알지! 아니, 너희 가엾은 아빠의 사촌의 시누이가 말이다, 미스 브라운독이라는 분인데, 해머스미스에서 학교를 하던 어떤 부인 밑으로 동업자가 들어가서 눈 깜짝할 새에 떼돈을 벌었거든. 그건 그렇고 그 미스 브라운독이 복권에서 만 파운드 상금을 탄 그 부인하고 같은 사람인지 아닌지는 깜빡했지만, 같은 사람인 것 같아. 사실 지금 생각해 보니 틀림없이 그래. '맨탈리니 앤드 니클비', 얼마나 듣기 좋니! 그리고 니콜라스한테 무슨 좋은 운이라도 따른다면, 웨스트민스터 학교 교장 선생님 니클비 박사님이 같은 거리에 사시게 될 수도 있고 말이야.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 니콜라스!\" 케이트가 손가방에서 두더보이즈 홀에서 온 오빠의 편지를 꺼내며 외쳤어. \"우리가 이 모든 불행을 겪는 와중에도, 엄마, 오빠가 잘 지낸다는 소식을 들으니, 그리고 그렇게 기운찬 글을 쓴 걸 보니 얼마나 마음이 놓이는지 몰라요! 오빠가 편안하고 행복하다고 생각하면, 우리가 앞으로 무슨 일을 겪든 다 위로가 돼요.\"\n\n가엾은 케이트! 그녀는 그 위안이 얼마나 허약한지, 그리고 얼마나 빨리 그 착각에서 깨어나게 될지 까맣게 몰랐어.\n\n# CHAPTER 18\n\n케이트 니클비를 사흘 내내 끔찍이 아끼던 내그 양이, 이제부터 영원히 그녀를 미워하기로 마음을 정한다. 내그 양이 이런 결심을 하게 된 까닭들\n\n세상에는 고통과 고난과 괴로움으로 가득한 삶이 참 많아. 그런데 그런 삶을 직접 사는 사람 말고는 누구한테도 별 흥미를 끌지 못해서, 다들 그냥 지나쳐 버리지. 생각이나 감정이 메마른 사람들이 아닌데도 말이야. 그저 자기 동정심을 곱게 받들어 모시느라, 그 동정심을 일깨우려면 아주 자극이 센 게 필요한 사람들이지.\n\n자선을 베푸는 이들 가운데에는, 자기 일을 하면서 쾌락을 좇는 사람들 못지않게 강한 흥분을 바라는 이들이 적지 않아. 그래서 병든 공감과 동정심이 날마다 엉뚱한 데로 쏟아지지. 정작 건강한 상태에서 그 미덕을 제대로 베풀어야 할 곳이 넘쳐 나는데도 말이야. 그런 곳은 세상에서 제일 무심한 사람의 눈과 귀에도 늘 닿을 만큼 가까이 있는데도. 한마디로, 자선에도 낭만이 있어야 하는 거야. 소설가나 극작가한테 낭만이 필요한 것처럼 말이지. 거친 무명천 옷을 입은 도둑은 천박한 인물이라, 점잖은 사람들은 거들떠보지도 않아. 그런데 그 도둑한테 초록색 벨벳 옷을 입히고 챙이 높은 모자를 씌운 다음, 무대를 사람 북적이는 도시에서 산속 길로 옮겨 봐. 그러면 그 안에서 시와 모험의 정수를 발견하게 될걸. 그 위대한 으뜸 미덕도 마찬가지야. 잘 길러서 제대로 베풀면, 다른 모든 미덕으로 이어지거나, 적어도 그 미덕들을 다 품게 되거든. 거기에도 낭만이 있어야 해. 그리고 그 낭만 속에 실제의, 고되고, 발버둥치는 하루하루의 삶이 적게 담길수록 더 좋은 거지.\n\n가엾은 케이트 니클비가 떠밀려 들어간 삶은, 이 이야기에서 이미 펼쳐 보인 뜻밖의 사정들 때문에, 참 고된 삶이었어. 그런데 그 삶을 이루는 알맹이라곤 따분함과, 답답하고 몸에 해로운 갇힘과, 몸이 녹초가 되는 피로뿐이었지. 자칫하면 그 때문에 자선심 많고 공감 잘하는 사람들이 이 이야기에 흥미를 잃을지도 몰라. 그러니 나는 차라리 지금은 니클비 양 그 자신한테 눈길을 두려고 해. 마담 만탈리니가 다스리는 그 가게를 시시콜콜 길게 늘어놓아서 처음부터 독자들 마음을 식혀 버리는 것보다는 말이야.\n\n\"자, 정말이지, 마담 만탈리니,\" 케이트가 견습 첫날 밤 지친 걸음으로 집을 향해 가고 있을 때 내그 양이 말했어. \"저 니클비 양은 아주 믿음직한 아가씨예요. 정말이지 아주 믿음직해요. 흠. 정말이지, 마담 만탈리니, 저렇게 훌륭하고, 행실 바르고, 아주, 흠, 아주 겸손한 아가씨를 옷 맞추는 일에 보탬으로 뽑으셨다니, 마담의 안목에 정말 대단히 감탄할 일이에요. 저는 어떤 아가씨들은 윗사람들 앞에서 자기를 뽐낼 기회만 생기면 어찌나 굴던지, 아이고 참, 글쎄. 하지만 마담 만탈리니는 늘 옳으시죠, 늘요. 제가 아가씨들한테 자주 하는 말인데, 그 많은 사람들이 그렇게 자주 틀리는데 마담께서는 어떻게 늘 옳으실 수가 있는지, 저한테는 정말 수수께끼라니까요.\"\n\n\"아주 훌륭한 손님 한 분 비위를 거스른 것 말고는, 니클비 양이 오늘 그렇게 대단한 일을 한 건 없는데. 적어도 내가 아는 한은 말이야.\" 마담 만탈리니가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"아이고 참!\" 내그 양이 말했어. \"하지만 경험이 없으니 많이 봐주셔야죠, 아시잖아요.\"\n\n\"그럼 어리니까도?\" 마담이 물었어.\n\n\"아, 그 점에 대해선 아무 말 안 할게요, 마담 만탈리니.\" 내그 양이 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했어. \"왜냐하면 어린 게 변명이 된다면, 마담께서도——\"\n\n\"나도 지금 같은 훌륭한 작업반장은 못 두었겠지, 그 말이겠지.\" 마담이 거들었어.\n\n\"글쎄, 마담 만탈리니 같은 분은 제가 처음 봐요.\" 내그 양이 더없이 흐뭇한 얼굴로 받았어. \"정말이라니까요. 무슨 말을 하려고 하면 그 말이 입에 채 오르기도 전에 다 아시잖아요. 아, 정말 대단해요! 하하하!\"\n\n\"내 생각엔 말이야,\" 마담 만탈리니가 짐짓 무심한 척 자기 조수를 흘끗 보며, 속으로는 실컷 웃으면서 말했어. \"니클비 양은 내 평생 본 아가씨 중에 제일 굼떠.\"\n\n\"가엾은 것,\" 내그 양이 말했어. \"그게 그 애 잘못은 아니죠. 잘못이라면 고쳐 볼 희망이라도 있겠지만, 그건 그 애 불운이니까요. 그러니, 마담 만탈리니, 정말이지 아시잖아요. 눈먼 말을 두고 누가 그랬듯이, 우리가 그걸 존중해 줘야죠.\"\n\n\"그 애 삼촌은 그 애가 예쁘다는 소릴 들었다고 하던데.\" 마담 만탈리니가 한마디 했어. \"내 보기엔 내가 만난 아가씨 중에 제일 평범한 축이야.\"\n\n\"평범하다고요!\" 내그 양이 기쁨에 환해진 얼굴로 외쳤어. \"게다가 굼뜨고요! 글쎄, 제가 할 수 있는 말은 이것뿐이에요, 마담 만탈리니. 저는 그 가엾은 애가 정말 좋아요. 그리고 그 애가 지금보다 두 배로 못나 보이고 두 배로 굼떠도, 저는 그만큼 더 그 애 편이 돼 줄 거예요. 그게 진심이에요.\"\n\n사실 내그 양은 그날 아침 케이트가 실수하는 걸 본 뒤로 슬그머니 정이 싹트기 시작했어. 그리고 윗사람과 나눈 이 짧은 대화가 그 호감을 놀랄 만큼 키워 놓았지. 더 놀라운 건, 처음 그 아가씨의 얼굴과 몸매를 훑어봤을 때만 해도, 내그 양은 둘이 절대 잘 지내지 못할 거라는 속마음을 품고 있었다는 점이야.\n\n\"그래도 이제는,\" 내그 양이 멀지 않은 거울에 비친 자기 모습을 흘끗 보며 말했어. \"그 애가 좋아. 정말 좋다니까. 정말이야!\"\n\n이 한결같은 우정은 어찌나 이해타산이라곤 없는지, 아첨이나 심술 같은 자잘한 약점보다 어찌나 한 수 위인지, 마음씨 고운 내그 양은 다음 날 케이트 니클비한테 솔직하게 일러 줬어. 보아하니 이 일에는 영 안 맞는 것 같다고 말이야. 하지만 그것 때문에 조금도 마음 쓸 것 없다고 했어. 자기(내그 양)가 더 애를 써서 그 애를 되도록 뒤에 숨겨 줄 테니까. 그러니 그 애가 할 일은 그저, 손님들 앞에서 얌전히 가만있고, 온갖 수를 다 써서 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 움츠리고 있는 것뿐이라고 했지. 이 마지막 충고는 겁 많은 케이트의 마음과 바람에 어찌나 딱 들어맞던지, 케이트는 그 훌륭한 노처녀의 충고를 무조건 따르겠다고 선뜻 약속했어. 그 충고가 어떤 속셈에서 나온 건지 따져 보지도, 한순간 곱씹어 보지도 않고 말이야.\n\n\"내가 정말이지 너한테 활짝 정이 들었단다, 얘야.\" 내그 양이 말했어. \"정말 친언니 같은 마음이야. 내 평생 이렇게 별난 일은 처음이라니까.\"\n\n확실히 별난 일이긴 했어. 내그 양이 정말로 케이트 니클비한테 강한 정을 느꼈다면, 그건 차라리 노처녀 이모나 할머니의 마음이어야 어울렸을 테니까. 두 사람의 나이 차이로 보면 자연히 그런 결론이 나올 법했거든. 하지만 내그 양은 아주 젊어 보이는 옷을 입고 다녔어. 그러니 어쩌면 그 마음도 같은 모양을 띠었는지도 모르지.\n\n\"아이고!\" 내그 양이 이튿날 일이 끝날 무렵 케이트한테 입을 맞추며 말했어. \"넌 오늘 하루 종일 어쩜 그렇게 굼떴니.\"\n\n\"제 단점을 더 뼈아프게 깨닫게 해 주신 그 친절하고 솔직한 말씀이, 저를 나아지게 하지는 못한 것 같아 걱정이에요.\" 케이트가 한숨을 쉬었어.\n\n\"아니, 아니, 그렇진 않을 거야.\" 내그 양이 보기 드물게 기분이 한껏 좋아져서 받았어. \"하지만 처음부터 알아 두는 게 얼마나 나으냐. 그래야 마음 편히 똑바로 나아갈 수 있잖니! 넌 어느 쪽으로 가니, 얘야?\"\n\n\"시내 쪽이에요.\" 케이트가 대답했어.\n\n\"시내!\" 내그 양이 모자 끈을 매며 거울 속 자기 모습을 흐뭇하게 바라보면서 외쳤어. \"어머나 세상에! 너 정말로 시내에 사니?\"\n\n\"누가 거기 산다는 게 그렇게 이상한 일인가요?\" 케이트가 반쯤 미소 지으며 물었어.\n\n\"어떤 처지에서든, 젊은 아가씨가 거기서 사흘을 내리 살 수 있다는 게 나는 믿기지가 않는걸.\" 내그 양이 대답했어.\n\n\"형편이 어려운——그러니까 가난한 사람들은,\" 케이트가 얼른 말을 고쳤어. 잘난 척하는 것처럼 보일까 봐 겁이 났거든. \"살 수 있는 데서 살아야죠.\"\n\n\"아! 맞는 말이야, 그래야지. 정말 옳은 말이고말고!\" 내그 양이 그 반쯤 한숨 같은 소리를 내며 받았어. 고개를 두어 번 까딱이는 그 한숨이란 게, 점잖은 자리에서 동정심이 내미는 잔돈 같은 거였지. \"그게 바로 내가 우리 오빠한테 자주 하는 말이야. 우리 집 하인들이 줄줄이 병들어 나갈 때 말이지. 오빠는 뒤채 부엌이 걔들 자기엔 너무 축축하지 않나 하거든. 그럼 내가 그러지. 이런 부류 사람들은 어디서든 자게만 해 줘도 고마워한다고! 하늘이 짐에 맞게 등을 만들어 주시는 거지. 그게 그렇다는 게 참 좋은 일 아니니, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"정말 그래요.\" 케이트가 대답했어.\n\n\"가는 길에 좀 같이 걸어 줄게, 얘야.\" 내그 양이 말했어. \"넌 우리 집 아주 가까이로 지나가야 하니까. 게다가 깜깜하기도 하고, 우리 집 마지막 하인이 일주일 전에 얼굴에 단독이 나서 병원에 가 버렸거든. 네가 같이 가 주면 나도 좋지.\"\n\n케이트는 이 영광스러운 동행을 기꺼이 사양하고 싶었어. 하지만 내그 양은 모자를 흡족하게 매만진 다음, 자기가 베푸는 호의를 얼마나 대단히 여기는지 빤히 드러나는 태도로 케이트의 팔짱을 꼈지. 그리고 케이트가 한마디 더 꺼내기도 전에 둘은 이미 거리로 나와 있었어.\n\n\"걱정이에요.\" 케이트가 망설이며 말했어. \"엄마가——제 어머니 말이에요——저를 기다리고 계실까 봐요.\"\n\n\"조금도 미안해할 거 없단다, 얘야.\" 내그 양이 다정하게 미소 지으며 말했어. \"분명 아주 점잖은 노부인이시겠지. 나도 그분을 알게 되면 정말, 흠, 정말 기쁠 거야.\"\n\n가엾은 니클비 부인이 길모퉁이에서 발만이 아니라 온몸을 식히고 있었던 터라, 케이트로서는 어머니를 내그 양한테 소개하는 수밖에 없었어. 내그 양은 방금 본 새 마차 손님 흉내를 어설프게 내며, 거들먹거리는 정중함으로 인사를 받았지. 그러고 나서 셋은 팔짱을 끼고 걸어갔어. 내그 양이 가운데서, 유난히 상냥한 기분에 젖어서 말이야.\n\n\"제가 댁의 따님을 어찌나 마음에 들어 하는지, 니클비 부인, 상상도 못 하실 거예요.\" 위엄 있는 침묵 속에 한참 걸어가다가 내그 양이 입을 열었어.\n\n\"그 말씀 들으니 기쁘네요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"하지만 낯선 분들까지 케이트를 좋아하는 게 저한테 새삼스러운 일은 아니랍니다.\"\n\n\"흠!\" 내그 양이 소리를 냈어.\n\n\"그 애가 얼마나 착한지 아시면 더 좋아하실 거예요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"이런 불운 속에서, 교만도 허영도 모르는 아이를 둔 건 저한테 큰 복이지요. 그 애 자라온 형편으로 보면 처음엔 교만이나 허영이 조금 묻어도 흉이 아니었을 텐데 말이에요. 남편을 잃는다는 게 어떤 건지 내그 양은 모르실 거예요.\"\n\n내그 양은 아직 남편을 얻는다는 게 어떤 건지도 모르는 처지였으니, 남편을 잃는다는 게 어떤 건지 모르는 것도 거의 당연한 노릇이었지. 그래서 그녀는 다소 황급히, \"그럼요, 정말 모르죠.\" 하고 대답했어. 그러면서 자기가 누구하고든 결혼하는 꼴을 누가 한번 보기나 하라지——아니, 아니, 나는 그렇게 어리석진 않아, 하는 뜻을 풍기는 태도를 지었어.\n\n\"이 짧은 동안에도 케이트는 분명 많이 늘었을 거예요.\" 니클비 부인이 딸을 자랑스럽게 흘끗 보며 말했어.\n\n\"아! 그럼요.\" 내그 양이 말했어.\n\n\"그리고 앞으로 더 늘 거고요.\" 니클비 부인이 덧붙였어.\n\n\"정말 그럴 거예요, 장담해요.\" 내그 양이 농담조로 케이트의 팔을 제 팔로 꼭 끼며 대답했어.\n\n\"그 애는 늘 영리했어요.\" 가엾은 니클비 부인이 환해져서 말했어. \"늘 그랬죠, 갓난아기 때부터요. 기억나는데, 그 애가 겨우 두 살 반이었을 때, 우리 집에 자주 드나들던 신사분이 있었어요——왓킨스 씨 말이야, 케이트, 얘야, 네 가엾은 아빠가 보증을 서 줬던 그분. 나중에 미국으로 도망가서 우리한테 눈신발 한 켤레를 보내 줬잖니. 어찌나 다정한 편지를 같이 보냈던지 네 가엾은 아빠가 일주일을 울었지. 그 편지 기억나니? 그때 당장 오십 파운드를 못 갚아서 정말 미안하다, 자기 돈을 죄다 이자 받게 굴려 놓은 데다 재산 불리느라 너무 바빠서 그렇다, 하지만 네가 자기 대녀라는 걸 잊지 않고 있으니, 우리가 너한테 은제 산호 노리개를 사 주고 그걸 자기 외상 장부에 달아 놓지 않으면 아주 섭섭하겠다, 그렇게 썼던 편지 말이야. 어머나, 얘야, 너 어쩜 그렇게 둔하니! 그리고 자기가 올 때마다 한 병 반씩 마시던 그 묵은 포트 와인 얘기를 어찌나 다정하게 했던지. 기억나야지, 케이트?\"\n\n\"네, 네, 엄마. 그분이 어쨌다는 거예요?\"\n\n\"아, 그러니까 그 왓킨스 씨가 말이다, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이, 무슨 엄청나게 중요한 걸 떠올리려고 안간힘을 쓰듯 느릿느릿 말했어. \"그 왓킨스 씨가——내그 양도 아시겠지만, 그분은 마을에서 올드 보어를 하던 그 왓킨스하고는 아무 친척도 아니에요. 그런데 가만, 그게 올드 보어였는지 조지 더 서드였는지 기억이 안 나네. 하지만 둘 중 하나였던 건 분명해요, 어차피 거기서 거기지만——그 왓킨스 씨가, 네가 겨우 두 살 반일 때, 너더러 자기가 본 아이들 중에 제일 놀라운 아이라고 했단다. 정말 그랬어요, 내그 양. 게다가 그분은 아이를 도통 좋아하지도 않았으니, 그런 말을 할 까닭이 전혀 없었거든요. 그 말을 한 게 그분인 건 분명해요. 바로 그다음 순간에 그분이 그 애 가엾은 아빠한테 이십 파운드를 빌려 간 게 어제 일처럼 또렷이 기억나니까요.\"\n\n자기 딸의 훌륭함을 두고 이런 놀랍고도 더없이 사심 없는 증언을 끌어다 댄 다음, 니클비 부인은 숨을 돌리려고 말을 멈췄어. 그러자 내그 양은 이야기가 집안의 대단함 쪽으로 흘러가는 걸 보고, 지체 없이 끼어들었지. 자기 나름의 자그마한 추억거리를 가지고 말이야.\n\n\"돈 빌려준 얘기는 꺼내지도 마세요, 니클비 부인.\" 내그 양이 말했어. \"안 그러면 제가 미쳐 버릴 거예요, 완전히 미쳐 버린다니까요. 우리 엄마가——흠——세상에 다시없을 사랑스럽고 아름다운 분이었거든요. 그렇게 또렷하고 빼어난——흠——사람 얼굴에 붙은 것 중에 그렇게 빼어난 코는 다시없었을 거예요, 정말이에요, 니클비 부인 (여기서 내그 양은 동질감에 젖어 자기 코를 문질렀어). 아마 세상에 다시없을 더없이 사랑스럽고 교양 있는 분이었죠. 그런데 그 돈 빌려주는 버릇 하나가 흠이었어요. 그걸 어찌나 심하게 했던지——흠——아이고! 수천 파운드를 빌려줬다니까요, 우리 자그마한 재산을 죄다요. 게다가 말이에요, 니클비 부인, 우리가 살고 또 살아서——흠——이 세상 끝까지 산다 해도 그 돈을 다시 받아 낼 것 같지가 않아요. 정말이지 그래요.\"\n\n이렇게 지어낸 이야기를 한 번도 끊기지 않고 끝마친 다음, 내그 양은 더 많은 추억 속으로 빠져들었어. 하나같이 못지않게 흥미롭고, 또 못지않게 사실인 추억들이었지. 그 도도하게 밀려드는 물길을 니클비 부인은 막아 보려 했지만 헛수고였어. 결국 부인은 자기 나름의 추억이라는 밑물을 흘려보내며 매끄럽게 그 흐름을 따라 떠갔지. 그렇게 두 부인은 더없이 흡족하게 함께 이야기를 이어 갔어. 다만 한 가지 차이가 있다면, 내그 양은 케이트를 향해 아주 큰 소리로 떠들어 댔고, 니클비 부인은 한결같이 단조롭게 줄줄 이어 나갔다는 거야. 그저 떠드는 것만으로 흡족해서, 누가 듣든 말든 별로 개의치 않으면서 말이지.\n\n이런 식으로 두 사람은 아주 화기애애하게 걸어가서, 마침내 내그 양 오빠네 집에 다다랐어. 그는 토튼햄 코트 로드에서 갈라진 골목길에서 고급 문구점 겸 자그마한 대여 도서관을 하고 있었지. 가장 새로 나온 헌 소설들을 하루, 일주일, 한 달, 또는 일 년 단위로 빌려주었는데, 그 제목들은 펜과 잉크로 적혀 마분지 한 장에 나붙어 문기둥에 대롱대롱 매달려 있었어. 마침 그때 내그 양은 큰 재산가인 어떤 신사한테서 받은 스물두 번째 청혼 이야기를 한창 늘어놓던 참이라, 모두 함께 들어가서 저녁을 먹자고 우겼지. 그래서 다들 안으로 들어갔어.\n\n\"가지 마, 모티머.\" 그들이 가게로 들어서자 내그 양이 말했어. \"우리 가게 아가씨 하나하고 그 어머니야. 니클비 부인하고 니클비 양.\"\n\n\"오, 그래!\" 모티머 내그 씨가 말했어. \"아!\"\n\n이 감탄사들을 더없이 깊고 사색에 잠긴 태도로 내뱉은 뒤, 내그 씨는 계산대 위 부엌 양초 두 개의 심지를 천천히 잘랐어. 그리고 창가의 양초 두 개도 더 잘랐지. 그러고는 조끼 주머니에서 코담배 갑을 꺼내 자기 코에다 한 줌 들이켰어.\n\n이 모든 걸 해내는 그 유령 같은 분위기에는 사람을 압도하는 무언가가 있었어. 게다가 내그 씨는 엄숙한 이목구비에 안경을 쓴, 키 크고 깡마른 신사였지. 마흔 줄에 접어든, 아니 그 언저리의 신사가 보통 자랑할 만한 것보다 머리숱이 훨씬 적은 사람이었어. 그래서 니클비 부인은 딸한테, 저분은 분명 문학을 하는 사람일 거라고 속삭였지.\n\n\"열 시가 넘었군.\" 내그 씨가 회중시계를 들여다보며 말했어. \"토머스, 창고 문 닫아라.\"\n\n토머스는 키가 덧문 절반쯤 되는 소년이었고, 창고라는 건 합승 마차 세 대만 한 크기의 가게였어.\n\n\"아!\" 내그 씨가 읽던 책을 제자리 선반에 도로 꽂으며 다시 한 번 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었어. \"그럼——그래——저녁이 준비됐을 거다, 누이.\"\n\n또 한 번 한숨을 쉬며 내그 씨는 계산대에서 부엌 양초들을 집어 들었어. 그리고 구슬픈 발걸음으로 부인들을 안내해 뒤채 거실로 들어갔지. 거기에는 병든 하인 대신 고용된 잡일꾼 아주머니가, 밀린 임금에서 일정한 십팔 펜스씩을 떼는 조건으로 삯을 받으며, 저녁상을 차리고 있었어.\n\n\"블록슨 부인,\" 내그 양이 나무라듯 말했어. \"내가 그 모자를 쓴 채로 방에 들어오지 말아 달라고 얼마나 자주 부탁했어요!\"\n\n\"어쩔 수 없잖아요, 내그 양.\" 잡일꾼 아주머니가 대뜸 발끈해서 말했어. \"이 집엔 청소할 게 산더미라고요. 그게 마음에 안 드시면 다른 사람을 알아보시는 수밖에 없어요. 이래선 품삯도 안 나온다고요, 그게 사실이에요, 지금 당장 목이 매달린다 해도요.\"\n\n\"이러쿵저러쿵 말은 듣고 싶지 않아요, 부인은.\" 내그 양이 인칭대명사에 잔뜩 힘을 주며 말했어. \"이따 더운물 쓰게 아래층에 불은 좀 있어요?\"\n\n\"없어요, 정말로요, 내그 양.\" 대신 온 아주머니가 대답했어. \"그러니 거짓말은 안 할래요.\"\n\n\"그럼 왜 없는 거예요?\" 내그 양이 말했어.\n\n\"내놓은 석탄이 없으니까요. 제가 석탄을 만들 수 있으면 만들겠지만, 못 만드니까 안 만드는 거예요. 그래서 이렇게 대놓고 말씀드리는 거고요, 마님.\" 블록슨 부인이 대답했어.\n\n\"입 좀 다물지 못하겠소——이 아낙네야?\" 모티머 내그 씨가 거칠게 이 대화에 끼어들었어.\n\n\"실례지만, 내그 씨.\" 잡일꾼 아주머니가 홱 돌아서며 쏘아붙였어. \"저야 이 집에서 말 안 하는 게 더없이 좋다고요. 누가 묻는 데서 묻는 때 말고는요. 그리고 아낙네라고 하셨는데, 그럼 선생님은 자기를 뭐라고 여기시는지 알고 싶네요.\"\n\n\"비참한 인간이지.\" 내그 씨가 자기 이마를 치며 외쳤어. \"비참한 인간.\"\n\n\"자기를 본명이 아닌 딴 이름으로 부르지 않으시니 다행이네요, 선생님.\" 블록슨 부인이 말했어. \"그리고 제가 그저께가 일곱 주째였던 날에 쌍둥이를 낳았고, 우리 막내 찰리가 지난 월요일에 환기 구덩이에 떨어져서 팔꿈치가 빠졌으니, 한 주 일한 삯 구 실링을 내일 시계가 열 시를 치기 전에 우리 집으로 보내 주시면 고맙겠습니다.\"\n\n이런 작별의 말을 남기고, 이 착한 부인은 아주 태연한 태도로 방을 나갔어. 문은 활짝 열어 둔 채로 말이지. 바로 그 순간 내그 씨는 '창고'로 몸을 내던지더니 큰 소리로 끙끙 앓았어.\n\n\"저 신사분한테 무슨 일이 있는 거예요, 네?\" 니클비 부인이 그 소리에 몹시 마음이 어지러워져 물었어.\n\n\"아프신 거예요?\" 케이트가 정말로 놀라서 물었어.\n\n\"쉿!\" 내그 양이 대답했어. \"참 서글픈 사연이지. 저 사람이 한때 어떤 분한테 끔찍이 빠져 있었거든——흠——마담 만탈리니한테.\"\n\n\"어머나!\" 니클비 부인이 외쳤어.\n\n\"맞아요,\" 내그 양이 말을 이었어. \"게다가 상당한 부추김도 받아서, 그분과 결혼할 거라고 철석같이 바랐죠. 저 사람은 더없이 낭만적인 마음을 지녔거든요, 니클비 부인. 사실——흠——사실 우리 집안사람들이 다 그래요. 그러니 그 실망이 끔찍한 타격이었죠. 저 사람은 굉장히 교양 있는 사람이에요——유달리 교양이 깊어서——읽거든요——흠——나오는 소설이란 소설은 다 읽어요. 그러니까 무슨——흠——물론 좀 유행을 타는 소설은 다 말이에요. 사실은 이래요, 저 사람이 읽는 책 속에서 제 불운에 딱 들어맞는 대목을 어찌나 많이 찾았던지, 그리고 모든 면에서 자기가 그 주인공들하고 어찌나 닮았다고 느꼈던지——물론 자기가 남보다 잘났다는 걸 스스로 알고 있죠, 우리 다 그렇듯이, 그건 아주 당연한 일이고요——그러다 보니 만사를 다 깔보게 됐고, 천재가 돼 버린 거예요. 그리고 저는 저 사람이 바로 이 순간에도 또 다른 책을 쓰고 있다고 확신해요.\"\n\n\"또 다른 책을요!\" 케이트가, 누군가 한마디 하라고 잠깐 틈이 비었다는 걸 알아채고 따라 말했어.\n\n\"맞아요,\" 내그 양이 의기양양하게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했어. \"또 다른 책, 팔절판 세 권짜리예요. 물론 저 사람이 그 자잘한 유행 묘사를 다 할 때 제——흠——제 경험의 덕을 보니 큰 보탬이 되죠. 왜냐하면 물론, 그런 걸 다루는 작가 가운데 그런 걸 저만큼 알 기회를 가진 사람은 거의 없으니까요. 저 사람은 상류 생활에 푹 빠져 있어서, 장사나 세속적인 일을 조금만 들먹여도——이를테면 방금 그 여자처럼요——아주 정신이 산란해져요. 하지만 제가 자주 하는 말인데, 저는 그 실망이 저 사람한테 큰 약이 됐다고 봐요. 실망을 안 했더라면 시든 희망이니 뭐니 하는 걸 쓰지 못했을 테니까요. 사실은요, 일이 이렇게 되지 않았더라면, 저 사람 천재성은 영영 빛을 못 봤을 거예요.\"\n\n좀 더 좋은 형편이었다면 내그 양이 얼마나 더 입을 놀렸을지는 헤아릴 길이 없어. 하지만 그 우울한 양반이 말소리가 들리는 거리에 있었고, 불도 지펴야 했으니, 그녀의 폭로는 거기서 멈췄지. 모든 정황으로 보아, 또 물을 데우기가 그렇게 어려웠던 걸로 보아, 그 마지막 하인은 단독 말고 다른 불이라곤 별로 다뤄 본 적이 없는 것 같았어. 그래도 마침내 브랜디에 물을 탄 게 조금 마련됐어. 손님들은 그에 앞서 차가운 양 다리 고기와 빵과 치즈를 대접받은 터라, 곧이어 작별 인사를 했지. 케이트는 집에 가는 내내, 가게에서 깊은 생각에 빠져 있던 모티머 내그 씨의 마지막 모습을 떠올리며 즐거워했어. 그리고 니클비 부인은, 이 양장점 회사가 결국 '만탈리니, 내그, 그리고 니클비'가 될지, 아니면 '만탈리니, 니클비, 그리고 내그'가 될지 속으로 따져 보며 즐거워했지.\n\n내그 양의 우정은 이렇게 한껏 무르익은 상태로 사흘 내내 이어졌어. 마담 만탈리니네 아가씨들은 그쪽에서 그런 한결같음을 일찍이 본 적이 없던 터라 무척 신기해했지. 그런데 나흘째 되는 날, 그 우정은 못지않게 격렬하고 못지않게 갑작스러운 제동이 걸렸어. 일은 이렇게 벌어졌지.\n\n대단한 가문의 늙은 귀족이 별로 내세울 가문도 없는 젊은 아가씨와 결혼하려는 참이었는데, 그가 그 젊은 아가씨와 그 아가씨의 언니를 데리고 찾아왔어. 전날 주문한 결혼식용 모자 두 개를 써 보는 광경을 보러 온 거지. 마담 만탈리니가 작업실로 통하는 전성관에 대고 날카로운 고음으로 그 사실을 알리자, 내그 양은 양손에 모자를 하나씩 들고 부랴부랴 위층으로 달려 올라갔어. 그러고는 그 일에 얼마나 열성인지를 보여 주려고 매혹적으로 가슴을 두근거리는 모습으로 진열실에 나타났지. 모자를 제대로 씌우자마자, 내그 양과 마담 만탈리니는 감탄의 발작에 빠졌어.\n\n\"더없이 우아한 모습이에요.\" 마담 만탈리니가 말했어.\n\n\"제 평생 이렇게 빼어난 건 처음 봐요.\" 내그 양이 말했어.\n\n자, 그 늙은 귀족은 아주아주 늙은 귀족이라, 아무 말도 없이 그저 우물우물 입을 놀리며 크게 흐뭇해서 낄낄거렸어. 그 결혼식용 모자와 그걸 쓴 사람들 못지않게, 이렇게 어여쁜 여자를 아내로 얻어 낸 자기 수완에도 흡족해하면서 말이지. 그리고 아주 발랄한 그 젊은 아가씨는, 늙은 귀족이 이렇게 황홀해하는 꼴을 보고는, 그를 큰 거울 뒤로 쫓아가더니 거기서 다짜고짜 입을 맞췄어. 그동안 마담 만탈리니와 다른 젊은 아가씨는 점잖게 딴 데로 눈길을 돌렸지.\n\n그런데 그 입맞춤이 진행되는 동안, 호기심에 살짝 물든 내그 양이 어쩌다 거울 뒤로 발을 들였다가, 그 발랄한 젊은 아가씨가 늙은 귀족한테 입 맞추는 바로 그 순간 그 아가씨의 눈과 딱 마주쳤어. 그러자 그 젊은 아가씨는 입을 삐죽이며 '늙은 것'이니 '대단한 무례'니 하고 뭐라고 중얼거렸어. 그러고는 마지막으로 내그 양한테 못마땅한 눈길을 쏘아붙이고는 경멸하듯 빙긋 웃었지.\n\n\"마담 만탈리니.\" 그 젊은 아가씨가 말했어.\n\n\"네, 손님.\" 마담 만탈리니가 말했어.\n\n\"어제 본 그 예쁜 아가씨 좀 올라오라고 해 주세요.\"\n\n\"아, 그래요, 그렇게 해 줘요.\" 자매가 말했어.\n\n\"세상 무엇보다도, 마담 만탈리니,\" 귀족의 신붓감이 소파에 나른하게 몸을 던지며 말했어. \"저는 흉한 사람이나 나이 든 사람한테 시중받는 게 질색이에요. 제가 올 때마다 늘 그 젊은 아가씨를 보게 해 주세요, 부탁이에요.\"\n\n\"물론이지,\" 늙은 귀족이 말했어. \"그 사랑스러운 젊은 아가씨를, 물론이고말고.\"\n\n\"다들 그 애 얘기를 하더라고요.\" 젊은 아가씨가 여전히 무심한 태도로 말했어. \"그리고 우리 귀족님은 미인을 대단히 좋아하시니, 꼭 그 애를 봐야 하시죠.\"\n\n\"그 애는 누구한테나 칭송을 받지요.\" 마담 만탈리니가 대답했어. \"내그 양, 니클비 양을 올려보내요. 당신은 다시 안 와도 돼요.\"\n\n\"실례지만, 마담 만탈리니, 마지막에 뭐라고 하셨죠?\" 내그 양이 부들부들 떨며 물었어.\n\n\"다시 안 와도 된다고요.\" 윗사람이 날카롭게 되풀이했어. 내그 양은 더 말 한마디 없이 사라졌고, 알맞은 시간이 지나 그 자리에 케이트가 대신 들어섰어. 케이트는 새 모자들을 벗기고 헌 모자들을 도로 씌웠어. 늙은 귀족과 두 젊은 아가씨가 그동안 내내 자기를 빤히 쳐다보며 무안하게 만드는 걸 알고 얼굴이 새빨개졌지.\n\n\"어머, 얘 좀 봐, 얼굴이 어쩜 그렇게 붉어지니!\" 귀족이 점찍은 신부가 말했어.\n\n\"이 애가 아직 일에 그리 익숙지 않아서요, 한두 주만 지나면 익숙해질 거예요.\" 마담 만탈리니가 우아하게 웃으며 끼어들었어.\n\n\"우리 귀족님이 그 짓궂은 눈길을 좀 보내셨나 봐요.\" 신붓감이 말했어.\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니야.\" 늙은 귀족이 대답했어. \"아니, 아니, 나는 결혼해서 새 삶을 살 거야. 하하하! 새 삶이라네, 새 삶! 하하하!\"\n\n이 늙은 신사가 새 삶을 살 거라는 말은 듣기에 흐뭇한 일이었어. 왜냐하면 그의 옛 삶이 그리 오래 가지 못할 게 빤히 보였으니까. 그저 한참 낄낄거리는 데에 힘을 쓴 것만으로도 그는 무섭게 기침하고 헐떡이는 지경으로 떨어졌어. 그 아가씨가 양장점 점원치고는 너무 예쁘다고 한마디 할 숨을 고르기까지 몇 분이나 걸렸지.\n\n\"설마 예쁜 얼굴이 이 일에 흠이 된다고 여기시는 건 아니죠, 우리 귀족님?\" 마담 만탈리니가 간드러지게 웃으며 말했어.\n\n\"천만에.\" 늙은 귀족이 대답했어. \"그랬다면 부인은 진작에 이 일을 그만뒀겠지.\"\n\n\"이 짓궂은 양반 같으니.\" 발랄한 아가씨가 파라솔로 그 귀족을 콕 찌르며 말했어. \"그렇게 말하시면 안 돼요. 어쩜 그러세요?\"\n\n이 장난스러운 물음에는 또 한 번, 또 한 번 콕콕 찌르는 짓이 따라붙었어. 그러다 늙은 귀족이 파라솔을 덥석 붙들고 도로 내주려 하지 않자, 다른 아가씨가 구하러 나섰지. 그래서 아주 귀여운 장난이 한바탕 벌어졌어.\n\n\"그 자잘한 손질들 꼭 해 주셔야 해요, 마담 만탈리니.\" 아가씨가 말했어. \"아니에요, 이 못된 양반, 당신이 꼭 먼저 가셔야 해요. 저 예쁜 아가씨하고 단 반 초도 당신을 두고 가지 않을 거예요. 제가 당신을 너무 잘 알거든요. 제인, 얘야, 저분을 먼저 보내자. 그래야 우리가 저분을 확실히 잡아 둘 수 있어.\"\n\n이 의심에 분명 무척 우쭐해진 늙은 귀족은, 지나가면서 케이트한테 괴상한 곁눈질을 던졌어. 그리고 그 짓궂음 때문에 파라솔로 또 한 대 톡 얻어맞고는, 비틀비틀 아래층 문간까지 내려갔지. 거기서 그 기운찬 몸뚱이는 건장한 하인 둘에게 들려 마차에 실렸어.\n\n\"흥!\" 마담 만탈리니가 말했어. \"저 양반이 마차에 탈 때마다 어떻게 영구차 생각을 안 하는지 모르겠다니까. 자, 그것들 치워라, 얘야, 치워.\"\n\n이 모든 장면이 펼쳐지는 동안 내내 다소곳이 바닥에 눈길을 둔 채 서 있던 케이트는, 물러가도 좋다는 허락을 너무나 반갑게 받아들였어. 그리고 기뻐하며 부랴부랴 아래층 내그 양의 영토로 내려갔지.\n\n그런데 그녀가 잠깐 자리를 비운 짧은 사이에, 그 작은 왕국의 사정은 크게 바뀌어 있었어. 내그 양은 늘 앉던 자리에서 마담 만탈리니 대리인으로서의 온갖 위엄과 권위를 지키고 있는 게 아니었어. 그 훌륭한 양반은 큰 상자 위에 늘어져서 눈물범벅이 되어 있었지. 그동안 아가씨 서너 명이 그녀 곁에 바짝 붙어 시중을 들고 있었고, 거기에 사슴뿔 정기며 식초며 다른 정신 차리게 하는 약들까지 곁들여진 걸 보면——머리 장식이 흐트러지고 앞줄 곱슬머리가 헝클어진 것까지 보지 않더라도——그녀가 정신을 잃고 까무러쳤다는 게 넉넉히 짐작이 갔어.\n\n\"어머나!\" 케이트가 황급히 앞으로 나서며 말했어. \"무슨 일이에요?\"\n\n이 물음은 내그 양한테 도로 까무러칠 듯한 격렬한 기미를 일으켰어. 그리고 아가씨 몇몇은 케이트한테 성난 눈길을 쏘아붙이며 식초와 사슴뿔 정기를 더 발라 주고는, '망신스러운 일'이라고 말했지.\n\n\"뭐가 망신스러운 일이라는 거예요?\" 케이트가 따져 물었어. \"무슨 일이에요? 무슨 일이 있었던 거예요? 말해 주세요.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이냐고!\" 내그 양이 모여 있던 아가씨들이 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 별안간 벌떡 꼿꼿이 일어나며 외쳤어. \"무슨 일이냐고! 어디 감히, 이 고약한 것아!\"\n\n\"어머나!\" 케이트가, 내그 양이 앙다문 이 사이로 그 형용사를 홱 내뱉는 그 사나움에 거의 얼어붙어 외쳤어. \"제가 뭘 기분 상하게 해 드렸나요?\"\n\n\"네가 나를 기분 상하게 했냐고!\" 내그 양이 되받아쳤어. \"네가! 풋내기, 어린것, 굴러들어 온 보잘것없는 것이! 아, 그래! 하, 하!\"\n\n자, 내그 양이 웃는 걸 보니, 뭔가가 그녀한테 몹시 우습게 여겨지는 게 분명했어. 그리고 아가씨들은 내그 양한테 장단을 맞추던 터라——그녀가 우두머리였으니까——단 한순간도 지체 없이 다 같이 웃음을 터뜨렸지. 그리고 고개를 살짝 끄덕이며 서로 비웃듯 미소 지었어. 그거 참 멋지다는 듯이 말이야!\n\n\"여기 이 애예요.\" 내그 양이 상자에서 내려와, 들떠 모여든 무리한테 케이트를 거창하게, 또 몇 번이고 깊이 절을 해 가며 소개했어. \"여기 이 애가——다들 이 애 얘기를 하더라죠——미녀랍니다, 여러분——미인이요, 이——아이고, 이 뻔뻔한 것아!\"\n\n이 고비에 내그 양은 의로운 몸서리를 도저히 억누르지 못했어. 그 몸서리는 곧장 아가씨들 모두한테로 옮겨 갔지. 그러고 나서 내그 양은 웃었고, 그다음엔 울었어.\n\n\"십오 년 동안,\" 내그 양이 더없이 가슴 저미게 흐느끼며 외쳤어. \"십오 년 동안 나는 이 방과 위층 방의 자랑이요 꽃이었어. 하느님 감사하게도,\" 내그 양이 오른발을 굴렀다가 다음엔 왼발을 놀라운 기세로 구르며 말했어. \"그 긴 세월 내내 이제껏 단 한 번도, 하는 짓마다 우리를 망신시키고 점잖은 사람들이 부끄러워 얼굴 붉히게 만드는 그런 것의 수작에, 그 비열한 수작에 당한 적이 없었지. 그런데 나는 느껴, 정말로 느껴진다고, 이렇게 진저리가 나는데도 말이야.\"\n\n여기서 내그 양은 다시 누그러져 부드러워졌어. 그러자 아가씨들이 다시 시중을 들며, 그런 것쯤은 초연하셔야 한다고, 자기들 같으면 그런 걸 경멸하고 거들떠보지도 않을 거라고 소곤거렸지. 그 증거로, 아가씨들은 아까보다 더 힘주어 망신스러운 일이라고 외쳤어. 그리고 어찌나 화가 나는지 자기들도 어쩔 줄을 모르겠다고 했지.\n\n\"내가 흉한 것이라는 소리를 들으려고 이 나이까지 살았다니!\" 내그 양이 별안간 발작을 일으키며, 자기 앞머리를 쥐어뜯으려 애쓰며 외쳤어.\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요.\" 합창이 받았어. \"제발 그런 말씀 마세요, 어서요!\"\n\n\"내가 나이 든 사람이라는 소리를 들어 마땅하다는 거니?\" 내그 양이 곁꾼들과 몸싸움을 벌이며 비명을 질렀어.\n\n\"그런 건 생각도 마세요, 언니.\" 합창이 대답했어.\n\n\"나는 그 애가 미워.\" 내그 양이 외쳤어. \"그 애가 진절머리 나게 밉다고. 다시는 그 애가 나한테 말 못 걸게 해. 내 친구라면 누구도 그 애한테 말 걸지 못하게 해. 칠칠치 못한 것, 화냥년, 뻔뻔하고 교활한 화냥년!\" 분노의 대상을 이런 말로 매도하고 나서, 내그 양은 한 번 비명을 지르고, 세 번 딸꾹질을 하고, 목구멍에서 여러 번 그르렁 소리를 내고, 잠깐 늘어졌다가, 부르르 떨고, 깨어나, 정신을 차리고, 머리 장식을 매만지고는, 이제 아주 멀쩡해졌다고 선언했어.\n\n가엾은 케이트는 처음엔 이 소동을 어리둥절한 마음으로 그저 바라보기만 했어. 그러다 얼굴이 붉어졌다 창백해졌다 하기를 거듭했고, 한두 번 말을 꺼내 보려 했지. 하지만 이 달라진 태도의 진짜 속내가 드러나자, 그녀는 몇 걸음 물러서서, 대꾸도 하지 않은 채 차분히 바라보았어. 그래도 그녀는 당당하게 자기 자리로 걸어가, 방의 가장 외진 구석에서 자기들 주관 행성 둘레에 몰려든 그 작은 위성 무리한테 등을 돌렸지만, 남몰래 쓰디쓴 눈물을 흘리고 말았어. 만약 내그 양이 그 눈물이 흐르는 걸 볼 수 있었다면, 그 마음속 깊은 곳까지 기뻐했을 그런 눈물을 말이야.\n\n# CHAPTER 19\n\n랄프 니클비 씨 댁의 만찬, 그리고 손님들이 만찬 전과 만찬 중, 만찬 후에 스스로를 즐겁게 만든 방식에 대하여.\n\n훌륭한 미스 내그의 심술과 원한은 그 주가 다 가도록 조금도 수그러들지 않았어. 오히려 시간이 갈수록 점점 더 부풀어 올랐지. 그리고 모든 젊은 아가씨들의 정직한 분노도 미스 내그의 화에 정확히 비례해서 치솟거나, 치솟는 척했어. 미스 니클비가 위층으로 불려 올라갈 때마다 양쪽 다 부글부글 끓어올랐지. 그러니 케이트의 하루하루가 그다지 즐겁거나 부러울 만한 게 아니었으리라는 건 쉽게 짐작할 수 있을 거야. 케이트는 토요일 밤이 오는 걸 반겼어. 마치 죄수가 더디고 진을 빼는 고문에서 잠깐 풀려나 달콤한 몇 시간을 맞듯이 말이야. 그리고 첫 주 노동의 대가로 받은 그 쥐꼬리만 한 보수가, 설령 세 배였다 해도 참 비싸고 힘들게 번 돈이라는 생각이 들었지.\n\n평소처럼 길모퉁이에서 어머니를 만났을 때, 케이트는 어머니가 랄프 니클비 씨와 이야기를 나누고 있는 걸 보고 적잖이 놀랐어. 그런데 그 놀라움은 곧 두 배로 커졌지. 두 사람이 나누는 이야기 내용도 그렇고, 니클비 씨 자신의 부드러워지고 달라진 태도 때문이기도 했어.\n\n\"아! 얘야!\" 랄프가 말했어. \"지금 막 네 얘기를 하던 참이었단다.\"\n\n\"그러셨어요!\" 케이트가 대답했어. 왜인지도 모른 채 삼촌의 차갑게 번득이는 눈빛 앞에서 움츠러들면서.\n\n\"바로 지금 말이다.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"너를 데리러 가던 길이었지. 네가 떠나기 전에 꼭 붙잡으려고 말이야. 그런데 네 어머니랑 집안일 이야기를 하다 보니 시간이 어찌나 빨리 흘렀는지—\"\n\n\"어머, 정말 그렇지 않니?\" 니클비 부인이 끼어들었어. 랄프의 마지막 말에 담긴 빈정거리는 투는 전혀 알아채지 못한 채로 말이야. \"정말이지, 이렇게나—케이트, 얘야, 너 내일 여섯 시 반에 삼촌 댁에서 저녁을 먹게 됐단다.\"\n\n이 놀라운 소식을 제일 먼저 전했다는 데 우쭐해진 니클비 부인은 고개를 끄덕이며 몇 번이고 빙긋빙긋 웃었어. 그 굉장함을 어리둥절해하는 케이트의 마음에 한껏 새겨 주려는 거였지. 그러더니 곧장 예각으로 방향을 꺾어 살림살이 궁리로 넘어갔어.\n\n\"가만 보자.\" 착한 부인이 말했어. \"네 검정 비단 드레스면 차림으로 충분할 거야, 얘야. 그 예쁘장한 작은 스카프를 두르고, 머리는 수수한 띠로 묶고, 검정 비단 양말 한 켤레—아이고, 아이고,\" 니클비 부인이 또 다른 방향으로 꺾으며 외쳤어. \"그 안타까운 자수정만 있었더라면—기억나지, 케이트, 얘야—그게 얼마나 반짝였는지, 알잖니—그런데 네 아빠가, 가엾은 네 아빠가—아! 그 보석들만큼 그렇게 모질게 희생당한 건 세상에 없었어, 정말!\" 이 가슴 아픈 생각에 짓눌려 니클비 부인은 서글프게 고개를 가로젓고는 손수건을 눈가에 가져다 댔어.\n\n\"전 그거 필요 없어요, 엄마, 정말로요.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"그런 게 있었다는 것도 잊으세요.\"\n\n\"세상에, 케이트, 얘야,\" 니클비 부인이 토라진 듯 받아쳤어. \"어쩜 그렇게 애처럼 말하니! 은 찻숟가락 스물네 개에, 시동생, 그레이비 그릇 둘, 소금 그릇 넷, 자수정은 다—목걸이며 브로치며 귀걸이까지—한꺼번에 다 날아가 버렸지. 그러는 내내 나는 거의 무릎까지 꿇다시피 하면서 그 가엾고 착한 양반한테 말했어. '여보, 뭐라도 좀 해 봐요, 니콜라스? 무슨 방도를 좀 마련해 봐요?' 그때 우리 곁에 있던 사람이라면 누구든 인정해 줄 거야. 내가 그 말을 한 번만 한 게 아니라 하루에 쉰 번은 했다는 걸. 안 그러니, 케이트, 얘야? 내가 네 가엾은 아빠한테 그걸 일깨워 줄 기회를 한 번이라도 놓친 적이 있더냐?\"\n\n\"아니요, 아니요, 엄마, 한 번도요.\" 케이트가 대답했어. 그리고 니클비 부인을 공평하게 말하자면, 부인은 그런 황금 같은 교훈을 새겨 줄 기회를 한 번도 놓친 적이 없었어—그리고 결혼한 부인네들 전체를 공평하게 말하자면, 그들은 그런 기회를 좀처럼 놓치는 법이 없지. 그 교훈의 유일한 흠이라면, 으레 거기에 살짝 모호하고 불확실한 기운이 감돈다는 것뿐이었어.\n\n\"아!\" 니클비 부인이 무척 열을 올리며 말했어. \"처음에 내 충고만 들었더라면—그래도, 나는 늘 내 도리는 다했으니까, 그게 그나마 위안이지.\"\n\n이 생각에 다다르자 니클비 부인은 한숨을 쉬고 두 손을 비비고 눈을 치켜뜨더니, 마침내 온순하고 차분한 표정을 지었어. 그러니까 자기가 박해받는 성인이긴 하지만, 누구나 빤히 아는 그런 사정을 굳이 입에 올려 듣는 사람을 성가시게 하진 않겠다, 뭐 그런 뜻이었지.\n\n\"자,\" 랄프가 미소를 지으며 말했어. 그런데 그 미소는, 다른 모든 감정의 기색이 그렇듯, 얼굴 위에서 대담하게 노니는 게 아니라 오히려 얼굴 밑으로 슬그머니 숨어드는 것 같았어. \"우리가 벗어났던 본론으로 돌아가자꾸나. 내일 우리 집에서 작은 모임이 있단다—요즘 사업으로 엮인—이런저런—신사분들 모임이지. 그래서 네 어머니가 네가 내 집의 안주인 노릇을 해 주기로 약속했단다. 나는 모임 같은 데는 별로 익숙지 않아. 하지만 이건 사업상의 모임이고, 가끔은 그런 바보 같은 짓도 사업의 중요한 일부거든. 나 좀 도와주는 거, 싫지 않지?\"\n\n\"싫다니요!\" 니클비 부인이 외쳤어. \"얘야 케이트, 어째서—\"\n\n\"제발,\" 랄프가 조용히 하라는 손짓을 하며 가로막았어. \"조카한테 한 말입니다.\"\n\n\"물론 기꺼이 하겠어요, 삼촌.\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"하지만 제가 어설프고 쩔쩔매서 삼촌을 곤란하게 할까 봐 걱정이에요.\"\n\n\"아, 그럴 일 없다.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"편한 시간에 오너라, 삯마차를 타고 말이다—삯은 내가 내마. 잘 자거라—그—그—하느님의 가호가 있기를.\"\n\n그 축복의 말은 랄프 니클비 씨의 목구멍에 턱 걸린 것 같았어. 마치 그 길을 다녀 본 적이 없어서 빠져나갈 길을 모르는 것처럼 말이야. 그래도 어쨌든, 어설프기 짝이 없긴 했지만, 어떻게든 밖으로 나오긴 했지. 그러고는 그 말을 처리하고 나서, 그는 두 친척과 악수를 나누고 휭하니 떠나 버렸어.\n\n\"네 삼촌은 얼굴 윤곽이 어찌나 또렷한지!\" 니클비 부인이 그의 작별 표정에 자못 강한 인상을 받은 듯 말했어. \"가엾은 네 아빠랑은 닮은 구석이 조금도 안 보이는구나.\"\n\n\"엄마!\" 케이트가 나무라듯 말했어. \"그런 생각을 하시다니요!\"\n\n\"그래,\" 니클비 부인이 곰곰 생각하며 말했어. \"확실히 하나도 안 닮았어. 그래도 아주 정직한 얼굴이야.\"\n\n이 훌륭한 부인은 이 말을 무척 힘주어, 또박또박 했어. 마치 거기에 적잖은 기지와 탐구가 담겨 있기라도 한 듯이 말이지. 그리고 사실, 그 말은 이 시대의 놀라운 발견 축에 끼어도 손색이 없었어. 케이트는 황급히 고개를 들었다가, 또 그만큼 황급히 다시 고개를 떨궜어.\n\n\"대체 무슨 일이니, 얘야, 도무지 모르겠구나?\" 니클비 부인이 물었어. 둘이 한동안 말없이 걸은 뒤였지.\n\n\"그냥 생각 좀 하고 있었어요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 대답했어.\n\n\"생각이라고!\" 니클비 부인이 되뇌었어. \"그래, 그것도 생각할 게 참 많지. 네 삼촌이 너를 무척 마음에 들어 하시는 게 분명해. 그러니 이 일이 있고 나서 너한테 무슨 굉장한 행운이 찾아오지 않는다면, 나는 좀 놀랄 거야, 그게 다야.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 부인은 갖가지 일화를 줄줄이 늘어놓기 시작했어. 괴짜 삼촌들한테서 손가방에 천 파운드짜리 지폐를 받은 아가씨들 이야기며, 삼촌 집에서 우연히 어마어마한 부자인 다정한 신사를 만나 짧지만 뜨거운 구애 끝에 결혼한 아가씨들 이야기 말이야. 케이트는 처음엔 시큰둥하게, 나중엔 재미있어하며 들었어. 그리고 집으로 걸어가는 동안 어머니의 그 낙천적인 기질 같은 게 자기 가슴속에서도 차츰 깨어나는 걸 느꼈지. 어쩌면 자기 앞날이 환해질지도, 더 나은 날들이 밝아 올지도 모른다는 생각이 들기 시작한 거야. 희망이란 게 그런 거지, 발버둥 치는 인간에게 하늘이 내린 선물 말이야. 하늘에서 내려온 어떤 미묘한 정기처럼 좋은 것이든 나쁜 것이든 모든 것에 스며들고, 죽음만큼이나 보편적이며, 병보다 더 잘 옮는 그런 것 말이지!\n\n힘없는 겨울 햇살이—그리고 도시의 겨울 햇살이란 정말이지 무척 힘이 없거든—그 크고 낡은 집의 흐릿한 창문을 뚫고 들어와, 절반쯤만 꾸며진 어느 방이 펼쳐 보이는 흔치 않은 광경을 목격하고는 한결 밝아졌는지도 몰라. 음침한 한구석에는, 여러 해 동안 먼지투성이 상품 더미가 말없이 쌓여 있었어. 그 안에 생쥐 무리를 품고서, 칸막이를 댄 방을 향해 둔하고 생기 없는 덩어리로 잔뜩 찌푸리고 있었지. 다만 바깥 거리에서 무거운 짐마차가 굴러갈 때면 거기에 응답하듯 묵직하게 부르르 떨었고, 그러면 그 안의 자그마한 주민들의 반짝이는 눈은 겁에 질려 한층 더 반짝였어. 그러고는 꼼짝도 못한 채 귀를 쫑긋 세우고 가슴을 졸이며, 그 소란이 다 지나갈 때까지 그대로 굳어 있었지—바로 이 어두운 구석에, 그날 케이트가 걸칠 자그마한 차림새가 하나하나 정성껏 차려져 있었어. 옷가지마다 그 뭐라 말로 다 못할 산뜻하고 개성 있는 분위기가 배어 있었지. 빈 옷이란 게—입던 사람과 함께 떠올라서든, 아니면 주인의 몸에 맞게 본을 떴기 때문이든—그걸 입은 사람의 맵시에 익숙하거나 그 맵시를 그려 보는 눈에는 으레 그런 분위기를 띠게 마련이거든. 곰팡내 나는 물건 더미가 있던 자리에는 검정 비단 드레스가 놓여 있었어. 그 자체로 더없이 단정한 자태였지. 발끝이 곱게 바깥으로 휜 작은 신발은, 옛날에 쇳덩이 추가 짓누르던 바로 그 자리에 놓여 있었어. 그리고 거칠고 빛바랜 가죽 더미가 있던 자리는, 어느새 니클비 부인이 유난히 애지중지하던 바로 그 검정 비단 양말 한 켤레에 자리를 내주고 말았지. 쥐며 생쥐며 그런 자잘한 무리들은 진작에 굶어 죽었거나 더 나은 거처로 옮겨 가 버린 뒤였어. 그리고 그 자리에는 장갑이며 머리띠며 스카프며 머리핀이며, 인간을 애태우는 데는 그 나름대로 쥐나 생쥐 못지않게 교묘한 자잘한 물건들이 잔뜩 나타났지. 그 모든 것 사이로, 또 그 한복판으로 케이트 자신이 거닐고 있었어. 그 엄숙하고 낡고 음침한 건물에는, 결코 덜 아름답거나 덜 진귀하지 않은 빛이었지.\n\n좋은 시간에든 나쁜 시간에든, 그건 독자가 어떻게 받아들이든 좋아—니클비 부인의 조바심은 그쪽 동네 시계들보다 훨씬 빨리 앞서 나갔거든. 그래서 케이트는 그런 채비를 생각하기 시작해도 될 시각보다 꼬박 한 시간 반이나 일찍 머리핀 하나까지 빈틈없이 차려입었지—좋은 시간에든 나쁜 시간에든, 어쨌든 단장은 다 끝났어. 그리고 마침내 출발하기로 약속한 시각이 되자, 우유 배달부가 제일 가까운 정류장에서 마차를 한 대 불러왔지. 케이트는 어머니에게 몇 번이고 작별 인사를 하고, 차 마시러 오기로 한 미스 라크리비에게도 다정한 안부를 잔뜩 남긴 뒤, 그 마차에 올라타 위풍당당하게 떠나갔어. 삯마차를 타고 위풍당당하게 떠나간 사람이 여태 있었다면 말이지만 말이야. 그리고 마차와 마부와 말들은 덜컹거리고 삐걱대고 채찍질하고 욕을 퍼붓고 악담을 하며 함께 굴러갔어. 골든 스퀘어에 다다를 때까지 말이지.\n\n마부가 현관문을 어찌나 요란하게 두 번 두들겼는지, 그가 다 두들기기도 한참 전에 문이 열렸어. 마치 문 뒤에 어떤 남자가 손을 빗장에 묶어 두고 서 있기라도 한 것처럼 잽싸게 말이야. 케이트는 깨끗한 셔츠를 입은 뉴먼 노그스보다 더 별난 모습은 없으리라 여겼던 터라, 문을 연 사람이 멋진 제복을 입은 남자이고 현관에 그런 사람이 두셋이나 더 있는 걸 보고는 적잖이 놀랐어. 그래도 제대로 찾아온 집이라는 데는 의심의 여지가 없었지. 문에 이름이 적혀 있었으니까. 그래서 케이트는 자기에게 내밀어진 레이스 달린 코트 소매를 잡고 집 안으로 들어섰어. 그러고는 위층으로 안내되어 뒤쪽 응접실로 들어갔고, 거기 혼자 남겨졌지.\n\n하인이 나타난 데 놀랐다면, 케이트는 가구의 호화롭고 으리으리한 모습에는 그야말로 넋을 잃었어. 더없이 부드럽고 우아한 양탄자, 더없이 정교한 그림들, 더없이 값비싼 거울들. 그 아름다움에 눈이 부시고, 사방에 함부로 뿌려 놓은 듯한 그 사치스러움에 어리둥절할 만큼 화려한 장식품들이 사방에서 그녀를 맞이했어. 현관문 바로 아래까지 이어진 계단조차 아름답고 호사스러운 물건들로 가득 차 있었지. 마치 집 안이 온갖 부로 넘쳐 나서, 조금만 더 보태면 그게 거리로 흘러넘칠 것만 같았어.\n\n이내 케이트는 현관문에서 요란하게 두 번씩 두드리는 소리가 잇따라 들려오는 걸 들었어. 그리고 두드리는 소리가 날 때마다 옆방에서 새로운 목소리가 하나씩 들려왔지. 처음엔 랄프 니클비 씨의 목소리가 쉽게 구별됐지만, 차츰 두런두런하는 이야기 소리에 묻혀 버렸어. 그녀가 알아챌 수 있는 거라곤, 그다지 곱지 않은 목소리를 지닌 신사가 여럿 있다는 것뿐이었지. 그들은 아주 크게 떠들고, 아주 호탕하게 웃고, 그녀가 보기엔 굳이 그럴 필요까진 없을 만큼 욕을 해 댔어. 하지만 그거야 취향 문제니까.\n\n마침내 문이 열리고, 랄프가 직접 모습을 드러냈어. 부츠는 벗고, 검정 비단과 구두로 격식 있게 차려입은 채로, 교활한 얼굴을 들이밀었지.\n\n\"아까는 너를 보러 올 수가 없었구나, 얘야.\" 그가 나직한 목소리로 말하며 옆방을 가리켰어. \"손님들 맞느라 바빴거든. 자, 이제—너를 들여보내 줄까?\"\n\n\"저, 삼촌,\" 케이트가 조금 안절부절못하며 말했어. 사교계에 훨씬 익숙한 사람들도 낯선 이들로 가득한 방에 들어서기 직전, 미리 그걸 생각할 틈이 있었을 때면 흔히 그러듯이 말이야. \"거기 여자 분들도 계신가요?\"\n\n\"아니,\" 랄프가 짤막하게 말했어. \"내가 아는 여자는 없구나.\"\n\n\"바로 들어가야 하나요?\" 케이트가 조금 뒷걸음치며 물었어.\n\n\"네 마음대로 하렴.\" 랄프가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했어. \"다들 와 있고, 곧 저녁이 차려질 거라는 것만 알아 두렴—그게 다야.\"\n\n케이트는 몇 분만 말미를 달라고 사정하고 싶었어. 하지만 삼촌이 삯마차 삯을 낸 걸 자기가 시간을 잘 지키기로 한 일종의 거래쯤으로 여길지도 모른다는 생각이 들었지. 그래서 삼촌이 자기 팔을 그의 팔에 끼우고 데려가게 내버려 두었어.\n\n두 사람이 들어섰을 때 신사 일고여덟 명이 난롯가에 둘러서 있었어. 그리고 그들은 아주 크게 떠들고 있던 터라, 랄프 니클비 씨가 그중 한 사람의 코트 소매를 건드리며 모두의 주의를 끌려는 듯 거칠고 힘준 목소리로 말할 때까지 두 사람이 들어온 줄도 몰랐지—\n\n\"프레더릭 베리소프트 경, 제 조카 미스 니클비입니다.\"\n\n무리가 깜짝 놀라기라도 한 듯 흩어졌어. 그리고 호명을 받은 신사가 돌아서자, 더없이 빼어나게 재단된 옷 한 벌과 그에 못지않은 구레나룻, 콧수염, 한 머리 가득한 머리카락, 그리고 젊은 얼굴이 드러났지.\n\n\"어!\" 그 신사가 말했어. \"이런—세상에—맙소사!\"\n\n이 토막 난 탄성과 함께, 그는 외알 안경을 눈에 끼우고 깜짝 놀란 얼굴로 미스 니클비를 빤히 쳐다봤어.\n\n\"제 조카입니다, 경.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"그렇다면 내 귀가 날 속인 게 아니로군. 밀랍 인형도 아니고 말이지.\" 경이 말했어. \"안녕하시오? 참으로 반갑소이다.\" 그러고는 경은 또 다른 빼어난 차림의 신사 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸어. 좀 더 나이가 들고, 좀 더 살집이 있고, 얼굴이 좀 더 불그스레하고, 도시 물을 좀 더 오래 먹은 신사였지. 그러고는 그 처녀가 \"지독하게 예쁘군\"이라고 큰 소리로 속닥였어.\n\n\"나도 소개해 주게, 니클비.\" 이 두 번째 신사가 말했어. 그는 난롯불에 등을 대고 양 팔꿈치를 벽난로 선반에 올린 채 어슬렁거리고 있었지.\n\n\"멀베리 호크 경입니다.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"달리 말하면 한 무리 가운데 가장 닳고 닳은 패라는 분이죠, 미스 니클비.\" 프레더릭 베리소프트 경이 말했어.\n\n\"나도 빼놓지 말게, 니클비.\" 등받이가 높은 낮은 의자에 앉아 신문을 읽고 있던, 얼굴이 날카로운 신사가 외쳤어.\n\n\"파이크 씨입니다.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"나도, 니클비.\" 멀베리 호크 경의 팔꿈치께에서, 얼굴이 벌겋고 야단스러운 분위기를 풍기는 신사가 외쳤어.\n\n\"플럭 씨입니다.\" 랄프가 말했어. 그러고는 다시 휙 돌아서서, 황새 같은 목에 어느 짐승의 것도 아닌 다리를 지닌 신사를 향해, 그를 스놉 씨라고 소개했어. 그리고 식탁에 앉은 백발의 사람은 차우저 대령이라고 했지. 대령은 어떤 사람과 이야기를 나누고 있었는데, 그 사람은 그저 머릿수나 채우는 인물처럼 보였고 아예 소개도 받지 못했어.\n\n이 모임 초반에 케이트의 가슴을 콕콕 찌르며 피를 얼굴로 화끈 솟구치게 한 일이 두 가지 있었어. 하나는 손님들이 자기 삼촌을 대놓고 깔보는 그 경박한 태도였고, 다른 하나는 자기를 향한 그 거리낌 없는 무례함이었지. 첫 번째 낌새가 두 번째를 한층 더 심하게 만들기 쉬우리라는 건, 굳이 대단한 통찰력이 없어도 내다볼 수 있었어. 그리고 여기서 랄프 니클비 씨는 잘못 계산한 셈이었지. 아무리 시골에서 갓 올라온 (천성이 그런) 아가씨라 해도, 또 사교 예법에 아무리 서툴다 해도, 십중팔구 그 아가씨는 세상살이의 예의범절에 대한 타고난 감각을, 런던에서 열 번이나 사교철을 거친 사람 못지않게—어쩌면 그보다 더 강하게—지니고 있게 마련이거든. 그런 감각이란 그렇게 닳고 닳는 과정에서 무뎌지는 법이라고들 하니까.\n\n랄프가 소개 절차를 다 마치고 나서, 그는 얼굴이 발그레해진 조카를 자리로 안내했어. 그러면서 그는 경계하듯 주위를 슬쩍 둘러봤지. 뜻밖에 나타난 그녀가 어떤 인상을 불러일으켰는지 가늠해 보려는 것처럼 말이야.\n\n\"뜻밖의 즐거움이군, 니클비.\" 프레더릭 베리소프트 경이 말했어. 그러면서 지금껏 케이트를 살피느라 끼고 있던 오른쪽 눈의 외알 안경을 빼서 왼쪽 눈에 끼웠지. 랄프를 겨눠 보려고 말이야.\n\n\"경을 놀래 드리려고 마련한 거랍니다, 프레더릭 경.\" 플럭 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"나쁘지 않은 발상이군.\" 경이 말했어. \"이건 거의 이 점 오 퍼센트쯤 더 붙여도 될 만한 일이야.\"\n\n\"니클비,\" 멀베리 호크 경이 굵고 거친 목소리로 말했어. \"그 귀띔을 받아들여서 나머지 스물다섯이든 얼마든 거기에 덧붙이게. 그리고 그 조언값으로 나한테 절반을 주고 말이야.\"\n\n멀베리 경은 이 말에 쉰 웃음을 곁들이고는, 니클비 씨의 사지에 대고 유쾌한 욕설 한마디로 마무리했어. 그러자 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨가 죽어라 웃어 댔지.\n\n이 신사들이 그 농담에서 채 헤어나기도 전에 저녁 식사가 알려졌어. 그러더니 그들은 비슷한 일로 또 한바탕 황홀경에 빠졌지. 멀베리 호크 경이 흥에 겨운 나머지, 케이트를 아래층으로 데려가려던 프레더릭 베리소프트 경의 곁을 잽싸게 스쳐 지나가더니, 케이트의 팔을 팔꿈치까지 자기 팔에 끼워 버렸거든.\n\n\"안 되지, 빌어먹을, 베리소프트,\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"정정당당함이야말로 보배 아닌가. 게다가 미스 니클비랑 나는 십 분 전에 이미 눈으로 이 문제를 매듭지었다네.\"\n\n\"하, 하, 하!\" 스놉 씨가 웃었어. \"아주 좋군, 아주 좋아.\"\n\n이 갈채에 한층 더 재치가 솟은 멀베리 호크 경은 친구들을 향해 더없이 익살맞게 곁눈질하고는, 허물없는 태도로 케이트를 아래층으로 데려갔어. 그 태도에 케이트의 여린 가슴속에서는 도무지 억누르기 힘들 만큼 활활 타오르는 분노가 치밀었지. 그리고 그 격한 감정은 조금도 누그러들지 않았어. 자기가 식탁 상석에 앉혀지고, 양옆에 멀베리 호크 경과 프레더릭 베리소프트 경이 앉은 걸 알았을 때 말이야.\n\n\"오, 우리 동네까지 길을 찾아오셨군그래?\" 경이 자리에 앉자 멀베리 경이 말했어.\n\n\"당연하지,\" 프레더릭 경이 미스 니클비에게 눈길을 고정한 채 대답했어. \"그걸 어찌 물어보나?\"\n\n\"자, 자넨 저녁이나 들게.\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"그리고 미스 니클비랑 나는 신경 쓰지 말게. 우리야 아주 시시한 말동무가 될 테니까 말이야, 아마도.\"\n\n\"여기 좀 끼어들어 주게, 니클비.\" 프레더릭 경이 말했어.\n\n\"무슨 일입니까, 경?\" 식탁 끝에서 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨의 호위를 받고 있던 랄프가 물었어.\n\n\"이 친구, 호크가 자네 조카를 독차지하고 있단 말이야.\" 프레더릭 경이 말했어.\n\n\"경께서 차지하려는 것이라면 무엇이든 저 친구가 웬만큼은 한몫 챙기지요, 경.\" 랄프가 비웃듯 말했어.\n\n\"맞아, 정말 그렇지.\" 젊은이가 대답했어. \"맙소사, 우리 집에서 누가 주인인지 모르겠다니까, 저 친구인지 나인지 말이야.\"\n\n\"저는 압니다.\" 랄프가 중얼거렸어.\n\n\"저 친구를 한 푼만 남기고 잘라 버릴까 봐.\" 젊은 귀족이 농담조로 말했어.\n\n\"아니, 아니, 천만에,\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"자네가 그 한 푼—마지막 한 푼에 다다르면, 내가 잽싸게 자네를 잘라 버릴 걸세. 하지만 그때까진 절대 자네 곁을 안 떠날 거야—그건 맹세코 장담하지.\"\n\n이 농담은 (엄연히 사실에 바탕을 둔 거였지만) 한바탕 웃음으로 받아들여졌어. 그 위로 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨의 웃음소리가 또렷이 구별됐지. 누가 봐도 이 둘은 멀베리 경에게 늘 따라붙는 아첨꾼이었거든. 사실 그 자리의 손님 대다수가 그 가엾은 젊은 경을 등쳐 먹고 있다는 건 어렵잖게 알 수 있었어. 그 젊은 경은 나약하고 어리석긴 해도, 무리 중에서는 단연 가장 덜 못된 축에 들어 보였으니까. 멀베리 호크 경은 자기와 자기 졸개들을 부려서 재산깨나 있는 젊은 신사들을 거덜 내는 데 능란하기로 유명했어—점잖고 우아한 직업이었고, 그가 그 분야에서 으뜸가는 자리에 올랐다는 데는 의심의 여지가 없었지. 그는 독창적인 천재다운 온갖 대담함으로, 흔한 방식과는 완전히 동떨어진 아주 새로운 수법을 고안해 냈어. 그가 손아귀에 넣은 자들 위에 일단 군림하게 되면, 그들을 제멋대로 하게 두기보다는 오히려 짓눌러 두는 게 그의 버릇이었지. 그리고 그들에게 대놓고, 거리낌 없이 자기 재기를 휘둘렀어. 그렇게 그는 두 가지 뜻에서 그들을 표적으로 삼은 거야. 능란한 솜씨로 그들을 텅 비게 만드는가 하면, 사람들 좋으라고 적당히 두드려서 그들이 텅텅 울리게 만들었으니까.\n\n만찬은 그 으리으리하고 빈틈없는 차림새가 저택 자체만큼이나 대단했어. 그리고 손님들은 그 만찬을 한껏 정당하게 대접하는 데 대단했지. 그 점에선 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨가 특히 두드러졌어. 이 두 신사는 요리란 요리는 죄다 먹고 술병이란 술병은 죄다 비웠는데, 그 양과 끈기가 정말이지 놀라울 지경이었지. 게다가 그렇게 무진 애를 썼는데도 두 사람은 놀랄 만큼 멀쩡했어. 후식이 나오자, 마치 아침 식사 이후로 별일 없었다는 듯 다시 또 시작했으니까.\n\n\"자,\" 프레더릭 경이 첫 포트와인 잔을 홀짝이며 말했어. \"이게 어음 할인 만찬이라면, 내가 할 말은 이것뿐이야. 맙소사, 날마다 할인을 받는 것도 괜찮은 수단이겠는걸.\"\n\n\"그럴 기회야 살면서 실컷 누리실 겁니다, 경.\" 멀베리 호크 경이 받아쳤어. \"니클비가 그렇다고 말해 줄 거고요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 말하겠나, 니클비?\" 젊은이가 물었어. \"내가 좋은 고객이 될까?\"\n\n\"그건 전적으로 사정에 달렸지요, 경.\" 랄프가 대답했어.\n\n\"경의 사정에 달렸지요.\" 의용군—그리고 경마장—소속의 차우저 대령이 끼어들었어.\n\n이 늠름한 대령은 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨가 자기 농담에 웃어 줘야 한다고 여기는 듯 그쪽을 흘끔 봤어. 하지만 그 신사들은 오로지 멀베리 호크 경을 위해서만 웃기로 되어 있던 터라, 대령으로선 톡톡히 망신스럽게도, 장의사 둘처럼 근엄하기만 했지. 게다가 대령의 패배를 더하기라도 하듯, 멀베리 경은 그런 시도를 죄다 자기만의 고유한 특권을 침범하는 짓으로 여겨, 외알 안경 너머로 그 무엄한 자를 빤히 노려봤어. 마치 그 주제넘음에 어이가 없다는 듯이 말이지. 그러고는 이건 \"괘씸한 무례\"라는 자기 생각을 다 들리게 떠벌렸어. 그게 프레더릭 경에겐 일종의 신호가 됐고, 경도 자기 외알 안경을 들어 올려, 그 비난의 대상을 마치 처음 전시되는 무슨 진귀한 야생 짐승이라도 되는 양 살펴봤지. 당연한 일이지만, 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨도 멀베리 호크 경이 노려보는 그자를 빤히 쳐다봤어. 그래서 가엾은 대령은 자기 당혹감을 감추려고, 어쩔 수 없이 포트와인 잔을 오른쪽 눈앞에 들고는 더없이 흥미진진하다는 듯 그 빛깔을 들여다보는 척하는 처지가 되고 말았지.\n\n이러는 내내 케이트는 되도록 조용히 앉아 있었어. 좀처럼 눈을 들 엄두도 못 냈지. 프레더릭 베리소프트 경의 흠모하는 눈길이나, 그보다 더 거북한, 그의 친구 멀베리 경의 뻔뻔한 시선과 마주칠까 봐서 말이야. 그런데 이 후자의 신사가 친절하게도 모두의 시선을 그녀 쪽으로 돌려 줬지.\n\n\"여기 미스 니클비가,\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"어째서 아무도 자기한테 사랑 고백을 안 하나 궁금해하고 있군그래.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 정말로요.\" 케이트가 황급히 고개를 들며 말했어. \"전—\" 그러고는 입을 다물었어. 아예 아무 말도 하지 않는 편이 나았겠다 싶어서였지.\n\n\"내 누구한테든 오십 파운드를 걸겠네,\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"미스 니클비가 내 얼굴을 똑바로 보면서, 자기가 그런 생각을 안 하고 있었다고 말하진 못할 거라고 말이야.\"\n\n\"좋아!\" 그 고귀한 봉이 외쳤어. \"십 분 안에.\"\n\n\"좋네!\" 멀베리 경이 응했어. 양쪽에서 돈이 나왔고, 스놉 씨가 판돈 관리인 겸 시간 기록원이라는 두 직책에 뽑혔어.\n\n\"제발,\" 케이트가 몹시 당황한 채로 말했어. 이 준비가 한창 진행되는 동안이었지. \"제발 저를 무슨 내기의 대상으로 삼지 말아 주세요. 삼촌, 정말 저는—\"\n\n\"왜 안 되니, 얘야?\" 랄프가 대답했어. 그런데 그 거슬리는 목소리에는 웬일로 낯선 쉰 기운이 섞여 있었어. 마지못해 말하는 듯, 차라리 그런 제안이 나오지 않았기를 바라는 듯 말이야. \"금방 끝나는 일이야. 별것 아니란다. 신사분들이 굳이 하시겠다면—\"\n\n\"내가 굳이 하자는 건 아닐세.\" 멀베리 경이 큰 소리로 웃으며 말했어. \"그러니까, 미스 니클비더러 꼭 그 부정을 하라고 우기는 건 결코 아니야. 그렇게 하면 내가 지는 거니까. 다만 나는 저 빛나는 눈을 보면 기쁘겠다는 거지. 특히나 저렇게 마호가니 식탁만 어여삐 봐 주고 계시니 말이야.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇소, 그건 너무하시오, 미스 니클비.\" 그 고귀한 젊은이가 말했어.\n\n\"너무 잔인하시군요.\" 파이크 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"끔찍하게 잔인해요.\" 플럭 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"내기에 진다 해도 상관없네.\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"미스 니클비의 눈을 그럴듯하게 한 번 보는 것만 해도 그 돈의 곱절은 되니까.\"\n\n\"그 이상이죠.\" 파이크 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"훨씬 그 이상이죠.\" 플럭 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"적군은 어찌 돼 가나, 스놉?\" 멀베리 호크 경이 물었어.\n\n\"사 분 지났습니다.\"\n\n\"브라보!\"\n\n\"날 위해 한 번 애써 봐 주지 않겠소, 미스 니클비?\" 잠깐 사이를 두고 프레더릭 경이 물었어.\n\n\"굳이 물어볼 것도 없네, 이 친구야.\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"미스 니클비랑 나는 서로 통하거든. 저분은 내 편을 들고, 그렇게 자기 안목을 보여 주고 있다네. 자넨 가망이 없어, 친구. 시간, 스놉?\"\n\n\"팔 분 지났습니다.\"\n\n\"돈이나 준비해 두게.\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"곧 건네주게 될 테니.\"\n\n\"하, 하, 하!\" 파이크 씨가 웃었어.\n\n언제나 둘째로 나서서, 할 수만 있으면 자기 동료를 능가하려 드는 플럭 씨는 아예 째지게 비명을 질렀지.\n\n그 가엾은 처녀는 당혹감에 어찌나 휩싸였던지 자기가 무얼 하는지도 모를 지경이었어. 그래서 그냥 가만히 있기로 마음먹었지. 하지만 그렇게 하면, 그렇게 거칠고 천박한 태도로 내뱉어진 멀베리 경의 그 호언장담을 자기가 인정하는 꼴로 비칠까 봐 두려웠어. 그래서 눈을 들어 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 봤지. 그런데 그녀와 마주친 그 눈빛에는 어찌나 역겹고, 어찌나 무례하고, 어찌나 진저리 나는 무언가가 서려 있었던지, 그녀는 한 마디도 더듬더듬 내뱉을 힘조차 없이 벌떡 일어나 방에서 뛰쳐나가고 말았어. 그녀는 위층에서 혼자가 될 때까지 안간힘을 다해 눈물을 참았어. 그러고는 비로소 터뜨렸지.\n\n\"훌륭해!\" 멀베리 호크 경이 판돈을 주머니에 챙기며 말했어.\n\n\"기개 있는 처녀로군, 저 아가씨의 건강을 위해 한잔하지.\"\n\n파이크 일당이 이 제안에 무척 후끈한 태도로 호응했다는 건, 또 그 건배가 멀베리 경의 정복이 얼마나 완벽한지에 대한 그 패거리의 자잘한 빈정거림과 함께 이루어졌다는 건 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠지. 랄프는 다른 손님들의 주의가 앞 장면의 주역들에게 쏠려 있는 동안, 그들을 늑대처럼 노려보고 있었어. 그런데 조카가 가 버리자 이제야 한결 편하게 숨 쉬는 것 같았지. 술병들이 빠르게 돌고 도는 가운데, 그는 의자에 등을 기댄 채, 술기운이 오르는 사람들을 하나하나 차례로 눈길로 좇았어. 마치 그들의 속내를 헤집어, 그 비뚤어진 재미를 위해 그 안에 든 부질없는 생각이란 생각을 다 까발리려는 듯한 눈빛으로 말이야.\n\n그러는 동안 케이트는 완전히 혼자 남겨진 채 어느 정도 평정을 되찾았어. 하녀에게서, 삼촌이 자기가 떠나기 전에 보고 싶어 한다는 말을 들었지. 또 신사들이 식탁에서 커피를 마실 거라는 반가운 소식도 알아냈어. 그들을 더는 안 봐도 된다는 생각에 그녀의 동요는 한결 가라앉았지. 그래서 책을 한 권 집어 들고, 마음을 가다듬고 읽기 시작했어.\n\n식당 문이 갑자기 열려 떠들썩한 흥청거림의 사나운 함성이 쏟아져 나올 때면, 그녀는 이따금 흠칫했어. 그리고 한 번 이상 크게 놀라 일어나기도 했지. 계단에서 발소리가 들린 것 같아서, 그 패거리 중 누군가가 길을 잘못 들어 혼자 돌아오는 게 아닐까 겁이 났거든. 하지만 그녀의 우려를 현실로 만들 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았어. 그래서 그녀는 책에 좀 더 바짝 정신을 쏟으려고 애썼지. 그리고 차츰 거기에 어찌나 빠져들었던지, 시간도 장소도 잊은 채 몇 장이나 죽 읽어 내려갔어. 그러다 별안간 어떤 남자의 목소리가 귓가 바로 옆에서 자기 이름을 부르는 걸 듣고 소스라치게 놀랐지.\n\n책이 손에서 떨어졌어. 바로 곁의 긴 의자에 비스듬히 기대어, 멀베리 호크 경이 있었어. 누가 봐도 술에 더 추해진 모습이었지—본디 마음속이 불한당인 사람은, 술이 들어가도 결코 나아지는 법이 없으니까.\n\n\"이 얼마나 사랑스러운 학구열인지!\" 이 세련된 신사가 말했어. \"그런데 그게 진짜였소, 아니면 그저 속눈썹이나 뽐내려던 거였소?\"\n\n케이트는 불안한 눈길로 문 쪽을 바라볼 뿐, 아무 대답도 하지 않았어.\n\n\"내 그 속눈썹을 오 분이나 들여다봤다오.\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"맹세코, 완벽하더군. 내 어쩌자고 입을 열어 그런 어여쁜 그림을 망쳐 버렸을까?\"\n\n\"부디 이제 좀 조용히 해 주세요.\" 케이트가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"아니, 그러지 마시오.\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. 그러고는 자기 찌그러진 모자를 접어 팔꿈치를 괴고, 처녀에게 더더욱 바짝 다가들었지. \"맹세코, 그러시면 안 되지. 당신께 이렇게 충실한 종을 그토록 모질게 대하다니—맙소사, 그건 정말 못된 짓이오, 맹세코 그렇다오.\"\n\n\"이걸 분명히 알아 두셨으면 좋겠어요.\" 케이트가 자기도 모르게 떨면서도 무척 분개한 어조로 말했어. \"당신의 행동이 저를 불쾌하게 하고 진저리 나게 한다는 걸요. 신사다운 마음이 조금이라도 남아 있다면, 저를 내버려 두실 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니 어째서,\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"어째서 자꾸 그렇게 지나치게 도도한 척하는 거요, 나의 어여쁜 그대여? 자, 좀 더 자연스럽게 굴어 보시오—나의 사랑스러운 미스 니클비, 좀 더 자연스럽게—제발 좀.\"\n\n케이트는 황급히 일어섰어. 그런데 그녀가 일어서는 순간, 멀베리 경이 그녀의 드레스를 붙잡고 억지로 붙들어 놓았지.\n\n\"놓으세요.\" 그녀가 분노에 가슴이 부풀어 오른 채 소리쳤어. \"들리세요? 당장—이 순간.\"\n\n\"앉으시오, 앉아요.\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"당신과 얘기를 좀 나누고 싶소.\"\n\n\"이 손 놓으세요, 당장요.\" 케이트가 외쳤어.\n\n\"무슨 일이 있어도 안 되지.\" 멀베리 경이 받아쳤어. 이렇게 말하면서 그는, 그녀를 다시 의자에 앉히려는 듯 몸을 기울였어. 그런데 처녀가 거기서 벗어나려고 격렬하게 몸부림치는 바람에, 그는 균형을 잃고 바닥에 벌렁 나자빠지고 말았지. 케이트가 방을 나가려고 앞으로 뛰쳐나가는 순간, 랄프 니클비 씨가 문간에 나타나 그녀와 마주 섰어.\n\n\"이게 무슨 일이냐?\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"이런 일이에요, 삼촌.\" 케이트가 몹시 격앙되어 대답했어. \"저는 삼촌의 돌아가신 동생의 자식이고, 의지할 데 없는 처녀인데, 그 어떤 지붕 아래보다도 마땅히 보호를 받아야 할 이 지붕 밑에서, 삼촌이 저를 차마 똑바로 보지도 못할 만큼 모욕을 당했다는 거예요. 비켜 주세요.\"\n\n랄프는 정말로 움찔했어. 분노한 처녀가 불타는 눈으로 그를 쏘아봤을 때 말이야. 하지만 그래도 그는 그녀의 명령을 따르진 않았어. 그녀를 멀찍한 자리로 데려가서 앉히고는, 다시 돌아와 그새 일어선 멀베리 호크 경에게 다가가더니, 문 쪽을 가리켰거든.\n\n\"당신이 갈 길은 저쪽이오.\" 랄프가 억누른 목소리로 말했어. 어떤 악마라도 자랑스러워했을 법한 목소리로 말이야.\n\n\"그게 무슨 소린가?\" 그의 친구가 사납게 따져 물었어.\n\n랄프의 주름진 이마에는 부푼 핏줄이 힘줄처럼 도드라졌고, 입가의 신경은 무슨 견딜 수 없는 감정에 쥐어짜이기라도 하는 듯 꿈틀거렸어. 하지만 그는 경멸하듯 미소 짓고는, 다시 문을 가리켰지.\n\n\"내가 누군지 알기나 하나, 이 늙은 미치광이야?\" 멀베리 경이 물었어.\n\n\"잘 알지.\" 랄프가 말했어. 이 한량은 한순간 그 더 늙은 죄인의 흔들림 없는 눈빛에 완전히 기가 꺾여, 가는 길에 뭐라 중얼거리며 문 쪽으로 걸어갔어.\n\n\"자넨 그 경을 노린 거였군, 안 그래?\" 그가 문에 다다르자 별안간 우뚝 멈춰 서며 말했어. 마치 무슨 새로운 깨달음이 머릿속에 번뜩 비친 듯, 다시 랄프와 마주 섰지. \"빌어먹을, 내가 거치적거렸던 거로군, 그렇지?\"\n\n랄프는 다시 미소 지었지만, 아무 대답도 하지 않았어.\n\n\"그 경을 자네한테 맨 처음 데려온 게 누군가?\" 멀베리 경이 다그쳤어. \"그리고 내가 없었다면, 자네가 어떻게 지금처럼 그를 자네 그물에 옭아 넣을 수 있었겠나?\"\n\n\"그물은 크고, 꽤 가득 찼지.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그 그물코에 누가 목 졸리는 일이나 없게 조심하게.\"\n\n\"자넨 돈을 위해서라면 자기 피붙이도 팔아넘길 위인이지. 이미 악마와 거래를 맺지 않았다면야 제 몸뚱이라도 팔아넘길 거고.\" 상대가 받아쳤어. \"자네 그 어여쁜 조카가 아래층의 그 취한 애송이를 꾀는 미끼로 이리 끌려온 게 아니라고 우길 셈인가?\"\n\n이 다급한 대화는 양쪽 다 억누른 목소리로 이어지고 있었지만, 랄프는 저도 모르게 주위를 둘러봤어. 케이트가 자리를 옮겨 들리는 데까지 와 있지는 않은지 확인하려고 말이야. 그의 적수는 자기가 잡은 유리한 고지를 알아채고, 거기에 한층 밀어붙였지.\n\n\"이게 그렇지 않다고 우길 셈이냐고 묻는 거야.\" 그가 다시 물었어. \"만약 그 경이 나 대신 여기 위로 길을 찾아 올라왔더라면, 자네가 지금보다 좀 더 눈감아 주고, 좀 더 못 들은 척하고, 좀 덜 기세등등했을 거라는 걸 부인하겠다는 거냐고. 자, 니클비, 그것 좀 대답해 보게.\"\n\n\"내 자네한테 이건 말해 두지.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"내가 사업상의 일로 저 애를 이리 데려왔다면—\"\n\n\"그래, 바로 그 말이야.\" 멀베리 경이 웃으며 끼어들었어. \"이제야 자네 본모습으로 돌아오는군.\"\n\n\"—사업상의 일로 말이야.\" 랄프가 더는 입을 열지 않기로 마음먹은 사람처럼, 느리고 단호하게 말을 이었어. \"자네가 손아귀에 넣고 거덜 내는 데 톡톡히 손을 보태고 있는 그 멍청한 애송이한테 저 애가 무슨 인상이라도 남길까 싶어서였지. 나는 알았어—그 애송이를 아니까—그가 저 애의 처녀다운 마음을 짓밟기까지는 한참 걸릴 거라는 걸. 그리고 그가 그저 풋내기다운 어리석음과 알맹이 없는 짓으로 저 애를 거스르지만 않는다면, 조금만 다루기 나름으로, 제 고리대금업자의 조카라 해도 그 여인의 정숙함과 행실만큼은 존중하리라는 걸 말이야. 그런데 내가 이 수법으로 그를 좀 더 슬슬 끌어들이려 했을지언정, 저 애를 자네 같은 닳고 닳은 인간의 음탕함과 야만에 내맡길 생각은 없었어. 자, 이제 우리 서로 알 만큼 알았군.\"\n\n\"특히나 그래 봤자 얻을 게 아무것도 없었으니까—그렇지?\" 멀베리 경이 비웃었어.\n\n\"바로 그렇지.\" 랄프가 말했어. 그는 몸을 돌린 채, 어깨너머로 이 마지막 대꾸를 던졌어. 두 잘난 자의 눈이 마주쳤지. 마치 두 악당이 저마다, 상대 앞에서는 제 본색을 감출 길이 없다는 걸 느끼는 듯한 표정으로 말이야. 그러고는 멀베리 호크 경이 어깨를 으쓱하고는 느릿느릿 걸어 나갔어.\n\n그의 친구는 문을 닫고, 조카가 아직도 자기가 두고 간 그 자세 그대로 있는 자리 쪽을 불안하게 바라봤어. 그녀는 긴 의자에 무겁게 몸을 내던진 채, 머리를 쿠션 위로 떨구고 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻고 있었지. 여전히 수치심과 비탄의 고통 속에서 울고 있는 것 같았어.\n\n랄프라면, 어떤 가난에 찌든 채무자의 집에 걸어 들어가, 그 사람이 어린 자식의 임종을 지키고 있다 한들, 그를 집달리에게 가리켜 보이고도 눈곱만큼도 개의치 않았을 거야. 그건 그저 지극히 흔한 사업상의 일이고, 그 사람은 그가 가진 유일한 도덕률을 어긴 죄인일 테니까. 하지만 여기 한 어린 처녀가 있었어. 살아서 세상에 태어난 것 말고는 아무 잘못도 저지르지 않은, 그의 온갖 바람에 묵묵히 따라 준, 그를 기쁘게 하려고 무던히도 애쓴—무엇보다도, 그에게 빚진 돈이 없는 처녀가 말이야—그래서 그는 어색하고 불안했어.\n\n랄프는 좀 떨어진 곳에 의자를 하나 가져다 놓았어. 그러더니 또 다른 의자를 조금 더 가까이 가져다 놓았지. 그러고는 조금 더 가까이 다가갔고, 그다음엔 또 조금 더 다가갔어. 그러더니 마침내 같은 소파에 앉아, 케이트의 팔에 손을 얹었지.\n\n\"쉿, 얘야!\" 그녀가 팔을 거두며 다시 흐느낌을 터뜨리자 그가 말했어. \"쉿, 쉿! 이젠 신경 쓰지 말거라. 그 일은 생각도 하지 마.\"\n\n\"오, 제발 부탁이에요, 집에 가게 해 주세요.\" 케이트가 외쳤어. \"이 집을 떠나서, 집에 가게 해 주세요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그러렴. 하지만 먼저 눈물부터 닦고 마음을 좀 가라앉혀야지. 머리 좀 들어 보렴. 자—옳지.\"\n\n\"오, 삼촌!\" 케이트가 두 손을 맞잡으며 부르짖었어. \"제가 무슨 짓을 했기에—제가 무슨 짓을 했기에—저를 이런 일에 내모신 거예요? 제가 생각으로든, 말로든, 행동으로든 삼촌께 잘못을 저질렀다면, 그래도 이건 저한테 너무나 모진 일이었을 거예요. 또 삼촌이 옛 시절 어느 땐가 사랑하셨을 그 사람의 기억에도요. 하지만—\"\n\n\"잠깐만 내 말을 들어 보렴.\" 랄프가 끼어들었어. 그녀의 격한 감정에 정말로 겁이 났던 거지. \"나는 이렇게 될 줄 몰랐다. 내가 이걸 미리 내다보기란 불가능했어. 나는 할 수 있는 건 다 했단다.—자, 우리 좀 걸어 보자꾸나. 방이 답답하고 이 등불들이 뜨거워서 네가 어지러운 거야. 아주 조금만 기운을 내면 한결 나아질 게다.\"\n\n\"무엇이든 하겠어요.\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"저를 집에만 보내 주신다면요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 보내 주마.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"하지만 네 본래 얼굴빛을 되찾아야 한다. 지금 그 얼굴로는 그 사람들이 놀랄 테고, 이 일은 너와 나 말고는 아무도 몰라야 하니까. 자, 이번엔 반대쪽으로 걸어 보자. 옳지. 지금도 한결 나아 보이는구나.\"\n\n이런 식으로 다독이며, 랄프 니클비는 조카를 자기 팔에 기대게 한 채 이리저리 거닐었어. 그녀의 손길 아래서 정말로 떨면서 말이야.\n\n마찬가지로, 이제 그녀를 보내도 되겠다 싶었을 때, 그는 그녀의 숄을 매만져 주고 그런 자잘한 시중을 들어 준 뒤—아마 평생 처음이었을 거야—그녀를 부축해 아래층으로 내려갔어. 현관을 가로질러, 계단을 내려가는 동안에도 랄프는 그녀를 이끌었지. 그리고 그녀가 마차에 앉을 때까지 손을 거두지 않았어.\n\n마차 문이 거칠게 닫힐 때, 케이트의 머리에서 빗 하나가 떨어졌어. 그녀의 삼촌 발치 바로 옆에 말이야. 그리고 그가 그걸 주워 그녀의 손에 돌려주는데, 옆에 있던 가로등 불빛이 그녀의 얼굴을 비췄지. 흘러내려 이마 위로 느슨하게 곱슬진 머리채, 아직 채 마르지도 않은 눈물 자국, 발그레한 뺨, 슬픔에 잠긴 표정—이 모든 게 그 노인의 가슴속에 잠들어 있던 어떤 기억의 실타래에 불을 댕겼어. 그리고 죽은 동생의 얼굴이 그의 눈앞에 떠오르는 것 같았지. 어릴 적 무언가 슬픈 일이 있었을 때 짓던 바로 그 표정 그대로 말이야. 그때의 자질구레한 정황 하나하나가, 마치 어제 일처럼 또렷하게 그의 머릿속에 번뜩 떠올랐어.\n\n피붙이와 혈육의 그 어떤 호소에도 끄떡없던 랄프 니클비, 슬픔과 고난의 그 어떤 사연에도 강철처럼 단단하던 그가, 그 얼굴을 바라보며 휘청거렸어. 그러고는 무덤 너머 어느 세상에서 온 망령이라도 본 사람처럼, 자기 집 안으로 되돌아 들어갔지.\n\n# CHAPTER 20\n\n라 크리비 양은 월요일 이른 아침, 그러니까 만찬이 있었던 다음 날, 마을 서쪽 끝의 여러 거리를 종종걸음으로 바삐 가로질러 갔어. 아주 중요한 심부름을 맡았거든. 니클비 양이 오늘은 몸이 너무 안 좋아서 일을 나갈 수 없지만 내일은 다시 일을 시작할 수 있길 바란다는 말을, 마담 만탈리니한테 전하는 일이었지. 라 크리비 양은 걸어가면서 머릿속으로 점잖고 우아한 표현들을 이리저리 굴려 봤어. 그 가운데 가장 멋진 말로 소식을 전하려고 말이야. 그러면서 어린 친구가 왜 몸져누웠을까, 그 까닭에 대해서도 한참을 곰곰이 생각했어.\n\n\"도무지 모르겠단 말이야.\" 라 크리비 양이 중얼거렸어. \"어젯밤에 분명히 눈이 빨갰거든. 두통이 있다고 했지만, 두통 때문에 눈이 빨개지진 않잖아. 분명히 울었던 거야.\"\n\n이 결론에 다다른 라 크리비 양은 — 사실 어젯밤에 이미 스스로 흡족할 만큼 확신해 둔 결론이었지 — 계속 생각을 이어 갔어. 거의 밤새 그랬던 것처럼 말이야. 어린 친구한테 또 무슨 새로운 슬픔거리가 생겼을까 하고 말이지.\n\n\"아무리 생각해도 모르겠어.\" 자그마한 초상화가가 말했어. \"전혀 모르겠다고. 그 늙은 곰탱이가 못되게 굴었다면 모를까. 케이트한테 심술을 부렸나 봐. 못된 짐승 같으니!\"\n\n이렇게 자기 생각을 입 밖에 내고 나니 한결 마음이 가벼워졌어. 비록 허공에 대고 한 말이긴 했지만 말이야. 라 크리비 양은 종종걸음으로 마담 만탈리니의 가게에 닿았어. 그런데 우두머리는 아직 침대에서 일어나지 않았다는 거야. 그래서 이인자를 만나게 해 달라고 했지. 그랬더니 내그 양이 나타났어.\n\n\"제 입장에서 말씀드리자면,\" 라 크리비 양이 잔뜩 꾸민 말투로 용건을 전하자 내그 양이 말했어. \"니클비 양 없이도 저는 얼마든지 잘 지낼 수 있답니다.\"\n\n\"오, 그러시군요, 부인!\" 라 크리비 양이 잔뜩 기분이 상해 받아쳤어. \"하지만 보시다시피, 부인이 이 가게 주인은 아니잖아요. 그러니 별로 중요한 말은 아니네요.\"\n\n\"아주 좋습니다, 부인.\" 내그 양이 말했어. \"제게 더 하실 분부라도 있으신가요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 없어요, 부인.\" 라 크리비 양이 받아쳤어.\n\n\"그럼 안녕히 가세요, 부인.\" 내그 양이 말했어.\n\n\"안녕히 계세요, 부인. 그리고 그 지극한 예의와 훌륭한 교양에 깊이 감사드려요.\" 라 크리비 양이 되받아쳤어.\n\n이렇게 해서 만남이 끝났어. 그동안 두 부인은 부들부들 떨면서도 놀랄 만큼 예의를 차렸지. 이건 두 사람이 아주 끔찍한 싸움 직전에 와 있다는 분명한 신호였어. 라 크리비 양은 방을 박차고 나와 거리로 튀어나갔지.\n\n\"저 여자는 대체 누굴까.\" 별난 자그마한 사람이 중얼거렸어. \"알고 지내면 참 좋은 사람이겠네! 내가 저 여자 초상화를 그리게 됐으면 좋겠어. 아주 똑같이 그려 줄 텐데.\" 그러고는 내그 양을 두고 아주 톡 쏘는 말을 했다는 생각에 마냥 흐뭇해진 라 크리비 양은, 한바탕 신나게 웃고는 무척 기분 좋게 아침을 먹으러 집으로 돌아갔어.\n\n이런 게 바로 오래 혼자 살아서 좋은 점 가운데 하나였지! 부산스럽고 활기차고 명랑한 이 자그마한 사람은 온전히 자기 안에서 살았어. 혼자 말하고, 혼자한테 속을 털어놓고, 자기 기분을 상하게 한 사람들한테 혼자서 할 수 있는 만큼 빈정대고, 혼자 흡족해하면서도 아무한테도 해를 끼치지 않았지. 흉을 봐도 누구의 평판이 깎이는 일이 없었고, 작은 복수를 즐겨도 세상 그 누구도 털끝만큼 손해 볼 일이 없었어. 형편이 빠듯한 탓에 바라던 사람들과 어울리지 못하고, 어울릴 수 있는 사람들과는 섞이고 싶지 않은 — 그런 많은 사람들 가운데 하나였던 거지. 그런 사람들한테 런던은 시리아 들판만큼이나 완전히 외로운 곳이야. 이 소박한 화가는 외롭지만 만족스러운 길을 여러 해 동안 걸어왔어. 그리고 니클비 가족의 별난 불운이 눈길을 끌기 전까지는, 온 인류를 향한 다정한 마음으로 가슴이 가득 차 있었으면서도 친구 하나 사귀지 못했지. 가엾고 자그마한 라 크리비 양처럼 외로운 모습으로 사는 따뜻한 가슴이 세상엔 참 많아.\n\n하지만 그건 지금 여기서 할 얘기는 아니야. 라 크리비 양은 아침을 먹으러 집에 돌아갔어. 그런데 첫 모금 차의 향을 채 음미하기도 전에, 하녀가 한 신사가 왔다고 알렸어. 라 크리비 양은 곧장 새 손님이 길가에 내놓은 진열장에 감탄해서 넋을 잃고 서 있는 거라고 짐작했지. 그래서 찻잔들이 놓여 있는 걸 보고는 말할 수 없이 당황했어.\n\n\"자, 이것들 좀 치워 줘. 침실로 얼른 가져가. 어디로든.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"어머, 어머나. 하필이면 오늘 아침에 늦다니, 다른 날도 많은데. 삼 주 동안이나 여덟 시 반이면 채비를 다 갖추고 있었는데도 코빼기 하나 안 비치더니!\"\n\n\"저 때문에 번거롭게 마세요.\" 라 크리비 양이 아는 목소리가 말했어. \"하녀한테 제 이름은 말하지 말라고 했어요. 깜짝 놀라게 해 드리고 싶어서요.\"\n\n\"니콜라스 도련님!\" 라 크리비 양이 깜짝 놀라며 소리쳤어. \"절 잊지 않으셨군요.\" 니콜라스가 손을 내밀며 말했어.\n\n\"어머, 길에서 마주쳤어도 알아봤을 것 같은데요.\" 라 크리비 양이 미소 지으며 말했어. \"한나, 잔하고 받침 하나 더 가져와. 그런데 말이에요, 젊은 양반. 떠나던 날 아침에 저지른 그 무례한 짓, 다시는 하지 말아 줬으면 좋겠네요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 많이 화내진 않으실 거죠?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"안 낼 것 같아요!\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"한번 해 보든가. 그게 다예요!\"\n\n니콜라스는 신사다운 멋을 부리며 곧장 라 크리비 양의 말대로 했어. 라 크리비 양은 가느다란 비명을 지르고는 그의 뺨을 찰싹 때렸지. 하지만 그렇게 세게 때린 건 아니었어. 그게 사실이야.\n\n\"이렇게 버릇없는 사람은 처음 봐!\" 라 크리비 양이 외쳤어.\n\n\"한번 해 보라고 하셨잖아요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"그야, 그건 비꼬는 말이었지요.\" 라 크리비 양이 받아쳤어.\n\n\"오! 그건 또 다른 얘기네요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그것도 미리 말씀해 주셨어야죠.\"\n\n\"정말이지 몰랐다는 거예요!\" 라 크리비 양이 쏘아붙였어. \"그건 그렇고, 다시 보니 지난번 봤을 때보다 야위었네요. 얼굴도 핼쑥하고 창백하고요. 그런데 요크셔는 어쩌다 떠나게 됐어요?\"\n\n라 크리비 양은 여기서 말을 멈췄어. 달라진 그 말투와 태도에 어찌나 진심이 담겨 있었던지, 니콜라스가 그만 마음이 뭉클해졌거든.\n\n\"많이 변해 보일 만도 하죠.\" 니콜라스가 잠시 말이 없다가 입을 열었어. \"런던을 떠난 뒤로 마음으로나 몸으로나 고생을 좀 했거든요. 무척 가난하기도 했고, 굶주려 본 적도 있어요.\"\n\n\"세상에, 니콜라스 도련님!\" 라 크리비 양이 외쳤어. \"지금 무슨 말을 하는 거예요?\"\n\n\"그렇게까지 마음 아파하실 일은 아니에요.\" 니콜라스가 한결 밝은 표정으로 대답했어. \"제 신세를 한탄하러 온 것도 아니고요. 더 중요한 일로 왔어요. 삼촌과 얼굴을 마주하고 만나고 싶어요. 그 말씀부터 드려야겠네요.\"\n\n\"그 일에 대해 제가 할 말은 이거 하나예요.\" 라 크리비 양이 끼어들었어. \"그런 취향은 부럽지가 않네요. 그 사람 장화하고 같은 방에 앉아 있는 것만으로도 저는 보름 동안 기분이 잡칠 거예요.\"\n\n\"대체로는,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"거기까진 도련님과 제 생각이 크게 다르지 않을 거예요. 하지만 이해해 주세요. 저는 삼촌과 맞서서 제 결백을 밝히고, 그 두 얼굴과 악의를 그 목구멍에 도로 처넣어 주고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"그건 완전히 다른 얘기네요.\" 라 크리비 양이 받아쳤어. \"하늘이 절 용서하시길. 하지만 누가 그 사람 목을 졸라 버린다 해도, 저는 눈물 한 방울 흘리지 않을 거예요. 그래서요?\"\n\n\"그래서 오늘 아침에 삼촌을 찾아갔어요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"삼촌은 토요일에야 시내로 돌아왔는데, 저는 어젯밤 늦게야 그 소식을 알았거든요.\"\n\n\"그래서 만났어요?\" 라 크리비 양이 물었어.\n\n\"아뇨.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"외출하고 없었어요.\"\n\n\"하!\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"무슨 친절한 자선 사업이라도 하러 갔겠죠, 아마.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔,\" 니콜라스가 말을 이었어. \"삼촌의 움직임을 잘 아는 제 친구한테 들은 바로는, 오늘 어머니와 누이를 만나서 제게 일어난 일들을 자기 식대로 들려줄 작정인 것 같아요. 거기서 삼촌을 만나려고요.\"\n\n\"잘 생각했어요.\" 라 크리비 양이 두 손을 비비며 말했어. \"그렇긴 한데, 글쎄요.\" 그러고는 덧붙였어. \"생각할 게 많아요. 다른 사람들도 고려해야 하고요.\"\n\n\"다른 사람들도 생각해 봤어요.\" 니콜라스가 받아쳤어. \"하지만 정직과 명예가 둘 다 걸린 문제니까, 그 무엇도 절 막을 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"그건 도련님이 제일 잘 알겠죠.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어.\n\n\"이 일만큼은 그러길 바라요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"제가 부탁드리고 싶은 건 딱 하나예요. 제가 간다는 걸 미리 식구들한테 알려 주세요. 식구들은 제가 멀리 있는 줄 알거든요. 아무 예고도 없이 들이닥치면 깜짝 놀랄 거예요. 시간을 좀 내서, 저를 만났고 십오 분쯤 뒤에 제가 갈 거라고 전해 주시면, 큰 도움이 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"도련님한테든 식구들 누구한테든 더 큰 도움을 드릴 수 있으면 좋으련만.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"하지만 돕고 싶은 마음에 도울 힘이 따라 주는 일은, 도울 힘에 마음이 따라 주는 것만큼이나 드문 것 같네요.\"\n\n라 크리비 양은 아주 빠르고 많이 떠들어 대면서 후딱 아침을 먹어 치웠어. 그러고는 차 통을 치우고 열쇠를 난로 망 밑에 감춘 다음, 다시 보닛을 쓰고 니콜라스의 팔을 잡고는 곧장 시내로 나섰지. 니콜라스는 어머니 집 문 가까이에서 그녀와 헤어지면서, 십오 분 안에 돌아오겠다고 약속했어.\n\n그런데 마침 랄프 니클비는, 제 속셈에 따라 니콜라스가 저질렀다는 못된 짓들을 알려야겠다고 마음먹고는, (뉴먼 노그스가 짐작한 대로 일 때문에 시내 다른 곳에 먼저 들르는 대신) 곧장 형수한테 가 있었던 거야. 그래서 집 청소를 하던 여자아이의 안내를 받아 라 크리비 양이 거실로 들어섰을 때, 니클비 부인과 케이트는 눈물을 흘리고 있었고, 랄프는 막 조카가 저지른 비행에 대한 이야기를 끝맺는 참이었어. 케이트가 나가지 말라고 손짓을 했고, 라 크리비 양은 말없이 자리에 앉았지.\n\n'벌써 와 있었군, 이 양반아.' 자그마한 부인이 속으로 생각했어. '그럼 도련님이 직접 등장해서, 그게 당신한테 어떤 효과를 내는지 보게 해 주지.'\n\n\"이것 참 그럴듯하군요.\" 랄프가 스퀴어스 양의 쪽지를 접으며 말했어. \"아주 그럴듯해요. 내가 그 녀석을 추천해 줬는데 말이죠. 제 모든 예전 확신을 거스르면서요. 그 녀석이 결코 잘되지 못할 걸 알면서도 말입니다. 처신만 똑바로 했다면 몇 년이고 편안하게 지낼 수 있는 사람한테 보냈는데, 결과가 뭡니까? 올드 베일리 법정에서 손을 들고 서 있어야 할 짓을 저질렀지요.\"\n\n\"전 절대 안 믿을 거예요.\" 케이트가 분개해서 말했어. \"절대요. 이건 무슨 비열한 음모예요. 거짓이 빤히 들여다보이는 음모라고요.\"\n\n\"얘야,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"넌 그 훌륭한 분을 욕하는 거다. 이건 지어낸 얘기가 아니야. 그 사람은 폭행을 당했고, 네 오빠는 행방이 묘연해. 그들이 말하는 그 애도 네 오빠랑 같이 갔어. 명심해, 명심하라고.\"\n\n\"그럴 리 없어요.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"니콜라스가 — 게다가 도둑이라니요! 엄마, 어떻게 가만히 앉아서 그런 말을 듣고 있어요?\"\n\n가엾은 니클비 부인은 언제 봐도 머리가 그리 또렷하다고 할 만한 사람이 아니었어. 게다가 요즘 들어 형편이 바뀌면서 더없이 복잡한 혼란 속에 빠져 있었지. 그래서 이 간절한 항변에 대해, 손수건 더미 뒤에서 자기는 도무지 믿을 수가 없다고 외칠 뿐 다른 대답을 하지 못했어. 그렇게 해서 아주 교묘하게도, 듣는 사람들이 도리어 그녀가 믿고 있다고 짐작하게 만든 거야.\n\n\"만약 그 애가 내 앞에 나타난다면, 그 애를 법의 심판에 넘기는 게 내 도리겠지요.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"내 마땅한 의무니까요. 세상 물정 아는 사람이자 사업가로서, 그 길밖에는 따를 도리가 없어요. 그렇지만,\" 랄프는 아주 의미심장한 투로 말하면서 케이트를 슬쩍, 그러나 뚫어지게 바라보았어. \"그렇지만 난 그러지 않을 겁니다. 그 애의 — 그 애 누이의 마음을 헤아려 줄 테니까요. 물론 어머니 마음도요.\" 랄프는 마치 뒤늦게 생각났다는 듯, 훨씬 힘을 빼고 덧붙였어.\n\n케이트는 이게 어젯밤 일에 대해 철저히 입을 다물라고 자기한테 던지는 또 하나의 미끼라는 걸 잘 알았어. 랄프가 말을 멈추자 케이트는 저도 모르게 그를 쳐다봤지만, 그는 눈길을 다른 데로 돌린 채 잠시 케이트의 존재를 까맣게 잊은 듯했어.\n\n\"모든 게,\" 니클비 부인의 흐느낌 말고는 아무 소리 없는 긴 침묵 끝에 랄프가 입을 열었어. \"모든 게 이 편지가 사실임을 증명하고 있어요. 설령 이걸 반박할 여지가 조금이라도 있다 쳐도 말이죠. 죄 없는 사람이 정직한 이들의 눈을 피해 슬그머니 빠져나가서, 무법자처럼 은신처에 숨어 다니던가요? 죄 없는 사람이 이름도 모를 부랑자를 꾀어, 게으른 도둑들처럼 그자와 함께 시골을 어슬렁거리던가요? 폭행, 소동, 절도, 이런 걸 뭐라고 부릅니까?\"\n\n\"거짓말이야!\" 한 목소리가 외쳤어. 문이 벌컥 열리더니 니콜라스가 방 안으로 들어왔지.\n\n깜짝 놀란 첫 순간, 어쩌면 겁이 났던지도 모르지, 랄프는 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 몇 걸음 뒤로 물러섰어. 이 뜻밖의 출현에 완전히 허를 찔린 거야. 다음 순간, 그는 팔짱을 낀 채 꼼짝 않고 굳어 서서, 조카를 노려보았어. 한편 케이트와 라 크리비 양은 두 사람 사이로 몸을 던졌어. 니콜라스의 격렬한 흥분이 당장이라도 폭력으로 터질 것 같았거든.\n\n\"오빠.\" 누이가 그를 붙들고 매달리며 외쳤어. \"진정해, 잘 생각해 봐 —\"\n\n\"잘 생각하라고, 케이트!\" 니콜라스가 분노에 차서 누이의 손을 어찌나 꽉 움켜쥐었던지, 케이트는 아파서 견디기 힘들 정도였어. \"이 모든 걸 생각하고, 그동안 있었던 일을 떠올리면, 저 사람 앞에 서 있으려면 나는 쇠로 만들어진 사람이라도 돼야 할 판이야.\"\n\n\"청동으로 만든 사람이거나.\" 랄프가 조용히 말했어. \"살과 피로 된 인간한테는 이 일을 끝까지 버텨 낼 만한 뻔뻔함이 모자라거든.\"\n\n\"어머, 어머나!\" 니클비 부인이 외쳤어. \"일이 이 지경까지 오다니!\"\n\n\"누가 내가 잘못이라도 한 것처럼, 식구들한테 망신이라도 안긴 것처럼 말하는 겁니까?\" 니콜라스가 둘러보며 말했어.\n\n\"네 어머니다.\" 랄프가 그녀 쪽을 가리키며 대답했어.\n\n\"당신 때문에 귀가 더럽혀진 거예요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"당신 때문에요. 당신은 어머니가 쏟아 낸 감사를 받을 자격이 있는 척하면서, 온갖 모욕과 부당함과 치욕을 내 머리 위에 들이부었지요. 당신은 나를 소굴로 보냈어요. 당신한테 딱 어울리는 추악한 잔인함이 제멋대로 날뛰고, 어린 비참함이 조숙하게 활보하는 그런 곳으로요. 거기선 아이다운 발랄함이 늙은이의 무거움 속으로 쪼그라들고, 자라면서 모든 희망이 시들고 말라 버려요. 하늘에 대고 증언하겠어요.\" 니콜라스가 간절히 둘러보며 말했어. \"내가 이 모든 걸 봤다는 걸, 그리고 저 사람도 그걸 다 안다는 걸요.\"\n\n\"그 비방을 반박해 봐.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"그리고 좀 더 참아. 저들한테 빌미를 주면 안 돼. 오빠가 실제로 무슨 짓을 했는지 말해 줘. 그리고 저게 다 거짓이라는 걸 보여 줘.\"\n\n\"저들이 — 아니, 저 사람이 — 나를 뭐라고 고발하는 건데?\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"우선, 네 고용주를 공격해서 살인죄로 재판받기 직전까지 갔다는 거다.\" 랄프가 끼어들었어. \"분명히 말해 두는데, 젊은이, 아무리 큰소리쳐 봐야 소용없어.\"\n\n\"저는 끼어들었을 뿐이에요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"가엾은 한 생명을 더없이 비열한 잔인함에서 구하려고요. 그러면서 그 못된 인간한테 쉽게 잊지 못할 벌을 줬지요. 사실 그자가 내게 받아 마땅한 것에 비하면 훨씬 가벼웠지만요. 지금 내 앞에서 똑같은 장면이 다시 벌어진다면, 나는 똑같이 할 거예요. 다만 더 세게, 더 모질게 때려서, 그자가 무덤까지, 언제 가든 그날까지 지니고 갈 만한 자국을 남겨 줄 거예요.\"\n\n\"들리세요?\" 랄프가 니클비 부인 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했어. \"이게 뉘우치는 겁니까!\"\n\n\"어머, 어쩜!\" 니클비 부인이 외쳤어. \"어떻게 생각해야 할지 정말 모르겠어, 정말로.\"\n\n\"제발 지금은 아무 말도 하지 마세요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"오빠, 한 가지만 말할게. 악의가 사람을 어디까지 몰고 가는지 오빠가 알아야 하니까. 저들이 오빠를 고발하는 건 — 반지가 하나 없어졌다면서, 감히 이렇게 말하는 거야 —\"\n\n\"그 여자가,\" 니콜라스가 거만하게 말했어. \"이 혐의들을 제기한 그 작자의 아내가, 내가 그 집을 떠나던 날 이른 아침에 — 내 짐작으로는 — 내 옷가지 틈에 값어치도 없는 반지를 하나 떨어뜨린 거예요. 적어도 나는 그 여자가 옷이 놓인 침실에 있었다는 걸 알아요. 가엾은 아이랑 실랑이를 벌이면서 말이죠. 그리고 길에서 짐 보따리를 풀었을 때 그 반지를 발견했고요. 나는 곧장 마차 편으로 그걸 돌려보냈어요. 지금 그들 손에 있을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그럴 줄 알았어, 그럴 줄 알았다고.\" 케이트가 삼촌 쪽을 바라보며 말했어. \"오빠, 저들이 오빠랑 같이 떠났다는 그 애에 대해서는?\"\n\n\"그 애는 어수룩하고 의지할 데 없는 아이예요. 잔인함과 모진 학대 때문에 그렇게 된 거죠. 지금 나랑 함께 있어요.\" 니콜라스가 받아쳤어.\n\n\"들리세요?\" 랄프가 다시 니클비 부인에게 호소했어. \"모든 게 증명됐어요. 본인 입으로 자백까지 했고요. 자네, 그 아이를 돌려보낼 건가?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 안 돌려보냅니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"안 돌려보내겠다고?\" 랄프가 비웃었어.\n\n\"네.\" 니콜라스가 되풀이했어. \"내가 그 애를 데려온 그 작자한테는요. 그 애가 누구한테 핏줄로 기댈 수 있는지 알았으면 좋겠어요. 만약 그자가 인간으로서 모든 정을 다 잃은 사람이라 해도, 그 수치심에서 뭐라도 짜낼 수 있을지 모르니까요.\"\n\n\"그것참!\" 랄프가 말했어. \"자, 젊은이, 이제 내가 한두 마디 하는 걸 들어 보겠나?\"\n\n\"하고 싶을 때 하고 싶은 말은 뭐든 하세요.\" 니콜라스가 누이를 끌어안으며 대답했어. \"당신이 무슨 말을 하든, 무슨 위협을 하든 저는 별로 개의치 않아요.\"\n\n\"아주 좋아, 젊은이.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어. \"하지만 어쩌면 다른 사람들한테는 관계되는 일일지도 모르지. 귀 기울여 듣고, 내가 하는 말을 곱씹어 볼 만하다고 여기는 사람들 말이야. 자네 어머니한테 말씀드려야겠군. 세상 물정을 아는 분이니.\"\n\n\"아! 차라리 몰랐으면 얼마나 좋을까.\" 니클비 부인이 흐느꼈어.\n\n이 점에 대해 이 착한 부인이 그렇게까지 괴로워할 필요는 사실 전혀 없었어. 그녀가 아는 세상 물정이란 게, 적게 잡아도 무척 미심쩍은 수준이었거든. 랄프도 그렇게 생각하는 모양이었어. 부인이 말하는 동안 그가 빙긋 웃었으니까. 그러고는 부인과 니콜라스를 번갈아 가며 가만히 쳐다보면서, 이렇게 말을 늘어놓았지.\n\n\"제가 부인과 제 조카딸을 위해 무엇을 해 줬는지, 또는 무엇을 해 주려 했는지, 그에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않겠습니다. 저는 어떤 약속도 하지 않았으니, 부인 스스로 판단하시도록 맡기지요. 지금 어떤 협박을 하려는 것도 아닙니다. 다만 이 말은 하겠어요. 이 녀석은, 이렇게 고집 세고 제멋대로에다 무질서한 이 녀석은, 온 유럽에서 가장 높은 교수대에서 목숨을 건진다 해도, 내 돈 한 푼도, 내 빵 한 조각도, 내 손길 한 번도 받지 못할 겁니다. 이 녀석이 어디로 오든 만나지 않을 거고, 그 이름도 듣지 않을 겁니다. 이 녀석도, 이 녀석을 돕는 자들도 돕지 않겠어요. 자기가 그렇게 함으로써 부인한테 어떤 일이 닥칠지 빤히 알면서도, 이 녀석은 이기적인 게으름에 빠져 돌아왔지요. 부인의 궁핍을 더 무겁게 하고, 제 누이의 쥐꼬리만 한 임금에 짐이 되러 말입니다. 부인을 떠나는 게, 더구나 지금 조카딸을 두고 떠나는 게 안타깝습니다만, 저는 이 비열함과 잔인함 덩어리를 부추기지 않을 겁니다. 그리고 부인한테 이 녀석을 내치라고 요구하지는 않을 테니, 저는 다시는 부인을 뵙지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n랄프가 미워하는 사람들에게 상처를 주는 자기 힘을 모르거나 못 느꼈다 해도, 위의 말을 늘어놓는 동안 니콜라스를 흘끔거리는 눈길만 봤어도 그 힘이 얼마나 대단한지 똑똑히 알았을 거야. 이 청년은 아무런 잘못도 없었지만, 교묘한 빗댐 하나하나가 그를 콕콕 찔렀고, 잘 벼린 빈정거림 하나하나가 그의 마음을 후벼 팠어. 그리고 랄프는 니콜라스의 창백한 얼굴과 떨리는 입술을 알아채고는, 젊고 뜨거운 영혼을 가장 깊이 찌르도록 계산된 조롱을 자기가 얼마나 잘 골랐는지 흐뭇해하며 속으로 쾌재를 불렀어.\n\n\"나도 어쩔 수가 없네요.\" 니클비 부인이 외쳤어. \"당신이 우리한테 무척 잘해 줬다는 거, 내 사랑하는 딸을 위해 많은 걸 해 주려 했다는 거 알아요. 그건 정말 확실해요. 알고 있다고요. 그 애를 당신 집에 데려가고 한 것도 무척 친절한 일이었어요. 물론 그 애한테도, 나한테도 큰 도움이 됐을 거고요. 하지만 도련님, 나는 그럴 수가 없어요. 내 친아들을 내칠 수는 없어요. 설령 그 애가 당신 말대로 그 모든 짓을 다 했다 쳐도요. 그건 안 될 일이에요. 난 못 해요. 그러니 우린 망해서 거덜이 나는 수밖에요, 케이트, 얘야. 그래도 난 견딜 수 있을 거야, 아마.\" 이런 말들과, 그리고 니클비 부인 말고는 그 누구의 힘으로도 결코 엮어 낼 수 없는, 정말 놀라우리만치 두서없는 한탄들을 줄줄이 쏟아 내면서, 부인은 두 손을 비틀어 댔고 눈물은 더 빨리 흘러내렸어.\n\n\"엄마, 왜 '만약에 니콜라스가 저들 말대로 했다면'이라고 말하는 거예요?\" 케이트가 솔직하게 화를 내며 물었어. \"오빠가 안 했다는 거 엄마도 알잖아요.\"\n\n\"이러나저러나 어떻게 생각해야 할지 모르겠어, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"니콜라스는 저렇게 격하고, 네 삼촌은 저렇게 침착하니, 나는 그저 삼촌이 하는 말만 들리고 니콜라스가 하는 말은 안 들리는구나. 됐다, 그 얘기는 이제 그만하자. 우린 구빈원에든, 빈민 보호소에든, 막달레나 수용소에든 가면 되겠지, 아마. 그리고 빨리 갈수록 좋고.\" 자선 기관들을 이렇게 엉뚱하게 뒤섞어 늘어놓고는, 니클비 부인은 다시 눈물을 쏟았어.\n\n\"잠깐만요.\" 랄프가 가려고 돌아서자 니콜라스가 말했어. \"굳이 여기서 나가실 필요 없어요. 일 분 안에 제가 사라져서 이 자리가 홀가분해질 테니까요. 그리고 제가 다시 이 문턱을 넘기까지는 오래, 아주 오랜 시간이 걸릴 거예요.\"\n\n\"오빠.\" 케이트가 오빠의 어깨에 몸을 던지며 외쳤어. \"그런 말 하지 마. 사랑하는 오빠, 내 가슴이 찢어질 것 같아. 엄마, 오빠한테 말 좀 해 봐요. 엄마 말은 신경 쓰지 마, 오빠. 엄마도 진심이 아니야. 오빠가 엄마를 더 잘 알아야지. 삼촌이든, 누구든, 제발 오빠한테 말 좀 해 줘요.\"\n\n\"케이트, 난 처음부터 너희들 곁에 머물 생각이 없었어.\" 니콜라스가 다정하게 말했어. \"내가 그럴 마음이라곤 꿈에도 생각하지 마. 나를 좀 더 좋게 봐 줘. 내가 작정했던 것보다 몇 시간 일찍 이 마을에 등을 돌리는 것뿐이야. 그게 뭐 대수겠어? 떨어져 있어도 우린 서로를 잊지 않을 거고, 다시는 헤어지지 않을 좋은 날들이 올 거야. 의젓해져, 케이트.\" 그가 자랑스럽다는 듯 속삭였어. \"그리고 저 사람이 보고 있는데, 날 울보로 만들지 마.\"\n\n\"응, 안 그럴게, 안 그럴 거야.\" 케이트가 간절하게 말했어. \"하지만 우릴 두고 떠나진 마. 오! 이 끔찍한 불운이 닥치기 전에, 우리가 함께 보낸 그 행복한 날들을 생각해 봐. 집에서 누리던 온갖 편안함과 행복을, 그리고 지금 우리가 견뎌야 하는 이 시련들을 말이야. 가난이 그토록 부추기는 온갖 멸시와 부당함 속에서 우릴 지켜 줄 사람 하나 없는데, 오빠가 우릴 두고 갈 순 없어. 도와줄 손길 하나 없이, 우리 둘만 이 일을 견디게 내버려 둘 순 없잖아.\"\n\n\"내가 떠나면 도움을 받게 될 거야.\" 니콜라스가 다급하게 대답했어. \"난 너희들한테 아무 도움도 못 되고, 지켜 주지도 못해. 내가 곁에 있어 봐야 슬픔과 궁핍과 고통밖에 가져다줄 게 없어. 우리 어머니도 그걸 보고 있고, 어머니가 너를 아끼고 너 때문에 걱정하는 마음이, 내가 가야 할 길을 가리키고 있어. 그러니 케이트, 모든 착한 천사들이 너를 지켜 주길 빌어. 내가 너를 어딘가 내 보금자리로 데려갈 수 있을 때까지 말이야. 거기서 우린 지금 누리지 못하는 행복을 되살리고, 이 시련들을 다 지나간 일처럼 이야기할 수 있을 거야. 날 여기 붙들지 말고, 지금 당장 가게 해 줘. 자. 사랑하는 동생아, 사랑하는 동생아.\"\n\n그를 붙들고 있던 손이 스르르 풀렸고, 케이트는 그의 품에서 정신을 잃었어. 니콜라스는 잠시 누이 위로 몸을 숙였다가, 케이트를 의자에 가만히 앉히고는 정직한 친구한테 맡겼어.\n\n\"굳이 동정을 부탁드리지 않을게요.\" 그가 라 크리비 양의 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했어. \"당신 성품을 아니까요. 당신은 결코 이 사람들을 잊지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n그는 랄프에게 다가갔어. 랄프는 만남 내내 지켜 온 그 자세 그대로 손가락 하나 까딱하지 않고 서 있었지.\n\n\"당신이 무슨 짓을 하든,\" 니콜라스가 두 사람한테만 들릴 만큼 낮은 목소리로 말했어. \"제가 빠짐없이 똑똑히 적어 두겠어요. 당신이 원하니 이 사람들을 당신한테 맡깁니다. 머지않아 셈을 치를 날이 올 거예요. 만약 이 사람들이 부당한 일을 당한다면, 그날은 당신한테 아주 무거운 날이 될 거고요.\"\n\n랄프는 이 작별 인사를 한마디라도 들었다는 기색을 얼굴 근육 하나에도 내비치지 않았어. 그 인사가 끝났는지조차 거의 알아채지 못한 듯했지. 그리고 니클비 부인이 필요하면 억지로라도 아들을 붙잡아야겠다고 채 마음먹기도 전에, 니콜라스는 이미 가 버렸어.\n\n니콜라스는 자기 초라한 거처를 향해 거리를 서둘러 지나갔어. 머릿속으로 몰려드는 생각들의 속도에 보조를 맞추려는 듯 말이야. 그러는 동안 마음속에 온갖 의심과 망설임이 일어, 그를 돌아가고 싶게 만들 뻔했지. 하지만 그렇게 한들 무얼 얻겠어? 설령 랄프 니클비한테 맞서고, 운 좋게 작은 일자리라도 하나 구한다 쳐도, 그가 식구들과 함께 있으면 지금 형편을 더 나쁘게 만들 뿐이고, 앞날의 가망마저 크게 망쳐 놓을지 몰라. 어머니가 케이트한테 베풀 새로운 친절에 대해 말했는데, 케이트가 그걸 거절하지 않았으니 말이야. '아니야.' 니콜라스는 생각했어. '난 가장 잘한 거야.'\n\n하지만 오백 야드도 채 가기 전에, 또 다른 마음이 그를 덮쳐 왔어. 그러면 그는 다시 걸음을 늦추고, 모자를 눈까지 푹 눌러쓴 채, 빽빽이 밀려드는 우울한 생각에 잠겼지. 아무 잘못도 저지르지 않았는데도 세상에 이토록 철저히 혼자라니. 사랑하는 단 하나의 사람들과 떨어져, 죄인처럼 쫓겨나는 신세라니. 여섯 달 전만 해도 온갖 편안함에 둘러싸여, 집안의 가장 큰 희망으로 우러름받던 그가 말이야. 이건 견디기 힘든 일이었어. 게다가 그는 이런 일을 당할 만한 짓을 한 적도 없었어. 음, 거기에 위안이 있었지. 그래서 가엾은 니콜라스는 다시 마음이 밝아졌다가, 또 가라앉았어. 빠르게 바뀌는 생각들이 그의 앞에 온갖 빛과 그늘을 차례차례 펼쳐 놓았거든.\n\n평범한 시련에 처한 사람이라면 누구나 겪어 봤을 이런 희망과 불안의 엇갈림을 거치면서, 니콜라스는 마침내 그 초라한 방에 닿았어. 거기서 그는, 여태 자기를 지탱해 주던 흥분이 더 이상 떠받쳐 주지 않게 되자, 도리어 그 흥분이 남기고 간 감정의 반동에 짓눌려, 침대에 몸을 던졌지. 그러고는 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌리고, 그토록 오래 억눌러 온 감정을 마음껏 쏟아 냈어.\n\n그는 누가 들어오는 소리도 못 들었고, 스마이크가 와 있는 것도 알아채지 못했어. 그러다 우연히 고개를 들었을 때, 방 저쪽 끝에 서서 안타까운 눈으로 자기를 바라보고 있는 스마이크를 봤지. 스마이크는 자기가 들킨 걸 알자 눈길을 거두고는, 변변찮은 저녁 준비를 하느라 바쁜 척했어.\n\n\"음, 스마이크.\" 니콜라스가 애써 밝은 목소리로 말했어. \"오늘 아침엔 어떤 새 친구를 사귀었는지, 아니면 이 거리하고 옆 거리를 다니면서 어떤 새 신기한 걸 알아냈는지, 어디 한번 들어 보자.\"\n\n\"아뇨.\" 스마이크가 슬프게 고개를 저으며 말했어. \"오늘은 다른 얘길 해야겠어요.\"\n\n\"하고 싶은 얘기 해.\" 니콜라스가 다정하게 대답했어.\n\n\"이거예요.\" 스마이크가 말했어. \"형이 불행하다는 거, 저를 데리고 나온 탓에 큰 곤경에 빠졌다는 거 알아요. 진작 그걸 알고 뒤에 남았어야 했는데. 정말이지, 그때 그 생각을 했더라면 그랬을 거예요. 형은 — 형은 — 부자가 아니잖아요. 형 한 몸 건사할 것도 모자란데, 제가 여기 있으면 안 되는 거였어요. 형은,\" 스마이크가 머뭇거리며 니콜라스의 손에 자기 손을 얹었어. \"형은 날마다 야위어 가요. 뺨은 더 창백해지고, 눈은 더 푹 꺼지고요. 정말이지 형이 그러는 걸 보면서, 내가 형한테 얼마나 짐이 되는지 생각하면 견딜 수가 없어요. 오늘 떠나려고 했는데, 형의 다정한 얼굴이 떠올라서 발길이 돌아왔어요. 한마디 말도 없이 형을 두고 떠날 순 없었어요.\" 가엾은 녀석은 더 말을 잇지 못했어. 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁 차오르고, 목소리가 사라져 버렸거든.\n\n\"우릴 갈라놓는 그런 말은,\" 니콜라스가 스마이크의 어깨를 진심으로 꽉 붙들며 말했어. \"내 입에서는 결코 나오지 않을 거야. 넌 내 유일한 위안이고 의지니까. 이제 와서 온 세상을 다 준다 해도 난 널 잃고 싶지 않아, 스마이크. 오늘 내가 견뎌 낸 그 모든 걸 버티게 해 준 건 바로 너를 생각하는 마음이었어. 그리고 그 쉰 곱절의 고난을 겪는다 해도 그럴 거야. 손 이리 줘. 내 마음은 네 마음과 이어져 있어. 우린 이번 주가 가기 전에 함께 이곳을 떠날 거야. 내가 가난에 찌들었다 한들 어때? 네가 그 가난을 덜어 주잖아. 우린 함께 가난하게 지내면 돼.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 21\n\n케이트 니클비는 그동안 겪은 마음고생 때문에 사흘 동안은 양장점 일을 다시 할 수가 없었어. 그러다 사흘이 지나서야 늘 가던 시간에, 무거운 발걸음으로, 마담 만탈리니가 절대 권력을 휘두르는 그 패션의 전당으로 향했지.\n\n미스 내그의 심술은 그동안에도 독기가 조금도 빠지지 않았어. 젊은 아가씨들은 여전히 손가락질받는 동료와는 어울리길 꺼리며 슬슬 피했지. 그리고 그 본보기 같은 여자가 몇 분 뒤에 도착했을 때, 그녀는 케이트가 돌아온 걸 보고 느낀 못마땅함을 굳이 숨기려 들지도 않았어.\n\n\"세상에 원!\" 미스 내그는 부하들이 우르르 몰려와 모자며 숄을 벗겨 주는 동안 이렇게 말했어. \"어떤 사람들은 자기가 제대로 된 사람들한테 얼마나 짐이 되는지 알면, 아예 안 나올 만한 배짱쯤은 있을 줄 알았는데. 하지만 참 묘한 세상이야. 오! 묘한 세상이지!\"\n\n미스 내그는 화가 났을 때 사람들이 흔히 그러는 투로, 그러니까 자기는 그 세상에 절대 속하지 않는다는 듯한 투로, 세상에 대해 이런 한마디를 던졌어. 그러고는 한숨으로 말을 맺었지. 마치 인류의 사악함을 너그러이 가엾어하는 것 같은 한숨이었어.\n\n부하들도 곧장 그 한숨을 따라 쉬었어. 미스 내그가 막 그들에게 도덕적인 훈수를 좀 더 늘어놓으려던 참에, 전성관을 타고 마담 만탈리니의 목소리가 흘러나왔어. 미스 니클비더러 위층으로 올라와 진열실 정리를 도우라는 거였지. 이런 특별 대우에 미스 내그는 어찌나 머리를 휙휙 젖히고 입술을 꽉 깨물던지, 한동안 말할 기운이 싹 사라져 버렸어.\n\n\"자, 니클비 양, 얘야,\" 케이트가 올라오자 마담 만탈리니가 말했어. \"이제 몸은 다 나았니?\"\n\n\"많이 좋아졌어요, 감사합니다.\" 케이트가 대답했어.\n\n\"나도 그렇다고 말할 수 있으면 좋으련만.\" 마담 만탈리니가 지친 기색으로 자리에 앉으며 말했어.\n\n\"어디 편찮으세요?\" 케이트가 물었어. \"그렇다면 정말 안됐네요.\"\n\n\"꼭 아픈 건 아니고, 그냥 골치가 아파서 그래, 얘야. 골치가 아파.\" 마담이 대꾸했어.\n\n\"그 말씀을 들으니 더 안타깝네요.\" 케이트가 조용히 말했어. \"몸이 아픈 건 마음이 아픈 것보다 견디기 쉬운데요.\"\n\n\"아! 게다가 둘 다 직접 견디는 것보다 말로 하는 게 훨씬 쉽지.\" 마담이 신경질적으로 코를 문지르며 말했어. \"자, 어서 일이나 해라, 얘야. 물건들을 좀 정리해 둬, 어서.\"\n\n케이트가 이런 평소답지 않은 신경질이 무슨 뜻일까 속으로 궁금해하고 있을 때였어. 만탈리니 씨가 반쯤 열린 문틈으로 수염 끝을, 그리고 점점 머리까지 들이밀더니 부드러운 목소리로 외쳤어.\n\n\"내 목숨이자 영혼이 여기 있나?\"\n\n\"없어요.\" 그의 아내가 대답했어.\n\n\"앞방에서 그 끝내주는 화분에 핀 작은 장미처럼 활짝 피어 있으면서 어떻게 없다고 하지?\" 만탈리니가 졸랐어. \"그 사람의 귀여운 인형이 들어가서 얘기 좀 해도 될까?\"\n\n\"절대 안 돼요.\" 마담이 대답했어. \"여기 들어오는 거 내가 절대 허락 안 하는 거 알잖아요. 어서 가요!\"\n\n하지만 그 귀여운 인형은 누그러진 대답의 말투에 용기를 얻었는지 반항을 감행했어. 슬그머니 방으로 기어들어 와 발끝으로 살금살금 마담 만탈리니에게 다가가면서, 오는 내내 입맞춤을 날려 보냈지.\n\n\"어쩌자고 속을 끓이면서 그 작은 얼굴을 그렇게 매혹적인 호두까기 모양으로 찌푸리고 그러나?\" 만탈리니가 자기 목숨이자 영혼의 허리를 왼팔로 감고는, 오른팔로 끌어당기며 말했어.\n\n\"오! 당신은 정말 못 봐 주겠어요.\" 그의 아내가 대답했어.\n\n\"못, 어, 나를 못 봐 주겠다고!\" 만탈리니가 소리쳤어. \"거짓말, 거짓말. 그럴 리가 없지. 그런 소릴 내 면전에서, 바로 내 얼굴에 대고 할 수 있는 여자는 세상에 한 명도 없어.\" 만탈리니 씨는 이렇게 말하며 턱을 쓰다듬더니, 맞은편 거울을 흐뭇하게 바라봤어.\n\n\"그렇게 사람 망치는 낭비를 해 놓고.\" 그의 아내가 낮은 목소리로 따졌어.\n\n\"그건 다 이렇게 사랑스러운 사람을, 이렇게 작은 비너스를, 이렇게 끝내주게 황홀하고 매혹적이고 사람 넋을 빼놓는 작은 비너스를 손에 넣은 기쁨에 겨워서 그런 거지.\" 만탈리니가 말했어.\n\n\"내가 지금 어떤 꼴이 됐는지 좀 봐요!\" 마담이 다그쳤어.\n\n\"내 사랑스러운 사람한테는 아무 해도 안 와, 아무 해도 안 올 거야.\" 만탈리니 씨가 대꾸했어. \"다 끝난 일이야. 아무 일도 없을 거라고. 돈은 들어올 테니까. 그리고 그게 빨리 안 들어오면, 늙은 니클비가 또 돈을 내놓겠지. 아니면 감히 이 작은 사람을 괴롭히고 아프게 했다간 목줄기를 따 버릴 거야—\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 마담이 끼어들었어. \"안 보여요?\"\n\n만탈리니 씨는 아내와 화해하려는 데 정신이 팔려 지금까지 미스 니클비를 못 본 척, 혹은 정말로 못 봤는데, 이 눈치를 알아채고는 손가락을 입술에 갖다 대고 목소리를 더 낮췄어. 그러고는 한참 동안 속닥속닥 소곤거렸지. 그러는 동안 마담 만탈리니는 자기와 결혼하기 전에 만탈리니 씨가 진 빚 얘기를 한 번이 아니라 여러 번 들먹이는 것 같았어. 또 그 빚을 갚느라 예상치 못하게 돈이 나간 얘기도 했지. 게다가 그 신사의 사랑스러운 약점들, 그러니까 도박이며, 낭비며, 빈둥거림이며, 경마에 빠지는 버릇까지 들먹였어. 그런데 만탈리니 씨는 이런 비난들을 하나하나, 그 경중에 따라 입맞춤 한 번이나 그 이상으로 다 처리해 버렸어. 결국 이 모든 일의 결말은, 마담 만탈리니가 그에게 홀딱 빠져 버렸다는 것, 그리고 둘이 위층으로 아침을 먹으러 올라갔다는 거였어.\n\n케이트는 자기가 할 일에 몰두하며, 여러 장식품들을 자기가 낼 수 있는 최고의 안목으로 조용히 정리하고 있었어. 그런데 방 안에서 낯선 남자 목소리가 들려와 깜짝 놀랐지. 그리고 돌아보니, 흰 모자에 빨간 목수건에 넓적하고 둥근 얼굴에 커다란 머리에다, 초록색 코트 자락 일부가 방 안에 들어와 있는 걸 보고 또 한 번 놀랐어.\n\n\"놀라지 마세요, 아가씨.\" 이런 모습을 한 그 임자가 말했어. \"저기, 여기가 부인복 짓는 가게 맞죠?\"\n\n\"네.\" 케이트가 몹시 놀라며 대꾸했어. \"무슨 일이세요?\"\n\n낯선 남자는 대답하지 않았어. 그저 먼저 뒤를 돌아보더니, 밖에 있는 보이지 않는 누군가에게 손짓하는 듯하다가, 아주 느긋하게 방으로 들어왔어. 그 뒤를 갈색 옷을 입은 자그마한 남자가 바짝 따라 들어왔는데, 어찌나 후줄근한지 형편없었지. 이 남자는 묵은 담배 냄새와 갓 까낸 양파 냄새가 뒤섞인 악취를 풍기며 들어왔어. 이 신사의 옷에는 보푸라기가 잔뜩 묻어 있었지. 그리고 구두며 양말이며 아래쪽 옷가지들은, 발뒤꿈치부터 코트 허리 단추까지 죄다 진흙 얼룩이 잔뜩 수놓여 있었어. 보름 전에, 그러니까 날씨가 좋아지기 전에 튄 진흙이었지.\n\n케이트는 아주 자연스럽게 이런 인상을 받았어. 이 매력적인 인간들이 눈에 드는 들고 갈 만한 물건이라면 뭐든지 슬쩍 가져갈 작정으로 찾아온 거라고 말이야. 그녀는 두려운 기색을 숨기려 들지도 않고, 문 쪽으로 발을 옮겼어.\n\n\"잠깐만요.\" 초록색 코트를 입은 남자가 문을 살며시 닫고 그 앞에 등을 기대고 서며 말했어. \"이거 좀 거북한 일인데요. 댁의 영감님은 어디 계시오?\"\n\n\"제 뭐가—뭐라고 하셨어요?\" 케이트가 떨면서 물었어. '영감님'이라는 게 시계나 돈을 가리키는 은어일지도 모른다고 생각했거든.\n\n\"먼틀하이니 씨 말이오.\" 남자가 말했어. \"그 양반 어찌 됐소? 집에 있소?\"\n\n\"위층에 계실 거예요.\" 케이트가 이 질문에 조금 안심하며 대답했어. \"그분을 찾으세요?\"\n\n\"아뇨.\" 방문객이 대답했어. \"꼭 그 양반을 찾는 건 아니오, 굳이 따지자면. 그냥 그 양반한테 이 명함이나 건네주쇼. 그리고 만약에 나하고 얘기하고 싶고 일을 줄이고 싶거든, 내가 여기 있다고 전해 주쇼. 그게 다요.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 낯선 남자는 두툼한 네모 명함 한 장을 케이트의 손에 쥐여 줬어. 그러고는 친구 쪽으로 돌아서서 태평한 태도로 한마디 했지. \"방 천장이 꽤 높네.\" 그러자 친구도 맞장구를 치며, 예를 들어 이렇게 덧붙였어. \"어느 방이든 어린애 하나가 어른이 될 때까지 자라도 천장에 머리 부딪칠 걱정은 없겠어. 그만큼 공간이 넉넉하다 이거지.\"\n\n케이트는 마담 만탈리니를 부를 종을 울린 다음 명함을 흘끗 봤어. 거기에는 '스케일리'라는 이름과 함께 다른 정보들도 적혀 있었지. 그런데 그걸 미처 다 읽기도 전에, 스케일리 씨한테 그녀의 시선이 쏠렸어. 그가 전신 거울 하나로 걸어가더니 한가운데를 지팡이로 푹 찔러 본 거야. 마치 그게 무쇠로 만들어지기라도 한 듯이 아주 태연하게 말이야.\n\n\"여기 이 유리 좋은데, 틱스.\" 스케일리 씨가 친구에게 말했어.\n\n\"아!\" 틱스 씨가 하늘색 비단에 손가락 네 개 자국과 엄지 자국 하나를 또렷이 찍으며 대꾸했어. \"그리고 이 물건도 거저 만든 게 아니란 말이지, 알아둬.\"\n\n비단에서 틱스 씨의 감탄은 멋진 옷가지 몇 점으로 옮겨 갔어. 한편 스케일리 씨는 거울 앞에서 느긋하게 목수건을 매만지더니, 그다음엔 거울에 비친 모습을 보며 턱에 난 여드름을 꼼꼼히 들여다보기 시작했어. 그렇게 거기에 푹 빠져 있는 와중에, 마담 만탈리니가 방으로 들어오면서 깜짝 놀라 소리를 질렀고, 그제야 그는 정신이 들었어.\n\n\"오! 이분이 마님이오?\" 스케일리가 물었어.\n\n\"마담 만탈리니세요.\" 케이트가 말했어.\n\n\"그럼,\" 스케일리 씨가 주머니에서 작은 서류를 꺼내 아주 천천히 펼치며 말했어. \"이건 집행 영장이오. 그리고 변제하기가 편치 않으시면, 우리가 당장 집을 둘러보고 재산 목록을 작성하겠소이다.\"\n\n가엾은 마담 만탈리니는 슬픔에 두 손을 비틀었어. 그러고는 남편을 부르려고 종을 울렸지. 그러고 나서 의자에 쓰러지는 동시에 기절해 버렸어. 하지만 이 일에도 그 직업적인 신사들은 조금도 동요하지 않았어. 스케일리 씨는 멋진 드레스가 걸려 있는 받침대에 기댔는데(그러니까 그 드레스를 입을 부인이 입었더라면 어깨가 솟아 보일 그 자리에, 딱 그 모양새로 그의 어깨가 솟아 있었지), 모자를 한쪽으로 밀어 올리고 아주 태연하게 머리를 긁적였어. 한편 친구 틱스 씨는 그 틈을 타 일에 들어가기 전에 방 안을 한 바퀴 죽 둘러봤지. 재산 목록 장부를 겨드랑이에 끼고 모자를 손에 든 채로, 눈에 들어오는 물건마다 머릿속으로 값을 매기느라 바빴어.\n\n만탈리니 씨가 허둥지둥 들어왔을 때 일이 이렇게 돌아가고 있었어. 이 잘난 표본은 총각 시절에 스케일리 씨 같은 부류와 꽤 폭넓게 어울려 봤거든. 게다가 이 골치 아픈 상황에서도 전혀 놀라지 않았지. 그저 어깨를 으쓱하고, 두 손을 주머니 깊숙이 찔러 넣고, 눈썹을 치켜올리고, 한두 소절 휘파람을 불고, 욕을 한두 마디 내뱉더니, 의자에 다리를 벌리고 걸터앉아 아주 침착하고 점잖게 사태에 최선의 얼굴을 들이밀었어.\n\n\"빌어먹을 총액이 얼마요?\" 그가 던진 첫 질문이었어.\n\n\"천오백이십칠 파운드 사 실링 구 펜스 반이올시다.\" 스케일리 씨가 손가락 하나 까딱하지 않고 대답했어.\n\n\"반 펜스는 빌어먹게 떼 버리쇼.\" 만탈리니 씨가 짜증스레 말했어.\n\n\"정 원하신다면 그러죠.\" 스케일리 씨가 받아쳤어. \"구 펜스도요.\"\n\n\"천오백이십칠 파운드까지 같이 떼 줘도 우리한텐 상관없는 일이지요, 내가 아는 한은.\" 틱스 씨가 한마디 했어.\n\n\"손톱만큼도 상관없지.\" 스케일리가 말했어.\n\n\"자,\" 그 신사가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말했어. \"그럼 어찌할 거요—뭐 방법이 있소? 살짝 금만 간 거요, 아니면 아주 완전히 거덜 난 거요? 살림이 완전히 끝장난 거요?—아주 좋소. 그럼 토머스 틱스 나리, 당신은 천사 같은 마누라와 사랑스러운 식구들한테 알려야겠소. 앞으로 사흘 밤은 집에서 못 잔다고 말이오. 여기 들어앉아 지켜야 하니까. 마님이 저렇게 속을 끓여 봐야 무슨 소용이오?\" 스케일리 씨는 마담 만탈리니가 흐느끼자 말을 이었어. \"여기 있는 물건 절반은 아마 값도 안 치렀을 거요, 내 장담하리다. 그러니 그게 얼마나 위안이 되겠소!\"\n\n이렇게 큰 익살과 함께 어려운 처지에 건전한 도덕적 격려까지 곁들인 말을 남기고서, 스케일리 씨는 재산 목록 작성에 들어갔어. 이 섬세한 작업에는 중개인 틱스 씨의 보기 드문 요령과 경험이 큰 도움이 됐지.\n\n\"내 행복의 잔을 달콤하게 해 주는 사람아.\" 만탈리니가 뉘우치는 기색으로 아내에게 다가가며 말했어. \"내 말 좀 이 분만 들어 주겠어?\"\n\n\"오! 내게 말 걸지 마요.\" 그의 아내가 흐느끼며 대답했어. \"당신이 날 망쳐 놨어요. 그걸로 충분해요.\"\n\n만탈리니 씨는 분명 자기 배역을 미리 잘 짜 뒀던 모양이야. 슬픔과 매서움이 담긴 이 말이 떨어지기가 무섭게, 그는 몇 걸음 뒤로 물러서더니, 속이 타들어 가는 듯한 표정을 짓고는, 방에서 쏜살같이 뛰쳐나갔어. 그러고는 곧이어 위층 화장실 문을 쾅 하고 거세게 닫는 소리가 들려왔지.\n\n\"니클비 양.\" 이 소리가 귀에 들려오자 마담 만탈리니가 외쳤어. \"제발 빨리, 어서요. 저 사람이 자살할 거예요! 내가 모질게 말했더니, 저 사람은 나한테서 그런 말을 못 견뎌요. 알프레드, 내 사랑하는 알프레드.\"\n\n이렇게 외치며 그녀는 위층으로 허둥지둥 올라갔어. 케이트도 그 뒤를 따랐는데, 이 다정한 아내만큼 걱정하는 건 아니었지만 그래도 조금은 마음이 다급했지. 화장실 문이 급히 벌컥 열리자 만탈리니 씨의 모습이 드러났어. 셔츠 깃을 좌우 대칭으로 가지런히 젖힌 채, 면도 가죽띠에다 아침 식사용 칼을 슥슥 갈아 날을 세우고 있었지.\n\n\"아!\" 만탈리니 씨가 외쳤어. \"방해받았군!\" 그러고는 그 아침 식사용 칼이 휙 하고 만탈리니 씨의 실내복 주머니 속으로 들어갔어. 만탈리니 씨의 두 눈은 마구 굴렀고, 머리카락은 제멋대로 흐트러져 수염과 뒤엉켜 있었지.\n\n\"알프레드.\" 그의 아내가 두 팔로 그를 끌어안으며 외쳤어. \"그런 말 할 생각은 아니었어요, 그런 말 할 생각은 정말 아니었다고요!\"\n\n\"망했어!\" 만탈리니 씨가 외쳤어. \"이 빌어먹을 떠돌이한테 축복을 내려 준, 세상에서 가장 착하고 순수한 사람을 내가 망쳐 놨다니! 빌어먹을, 날 놔둬.\" 이렇게 미친 듯이 떠들어 대던 절정의 순간에, 만탈리니 씨는 아침 식사용 칼을 홱 잡아 빼려고 했어. 그러다 아내의 손에 붙들리자, 벽에다 머리를 들이받으려 했지—다만 적어도 벽에서 육 피트는 떨어져 있도록 아주 조심하면서 말이야.\n\n\"진정해요, 내 천사.\" 마담이 말했어. \"누구 잘못도 아니에요. 당신 잘못만큼이나 내 잘못이기도 해요. 우린 그래도 아주 잘 살 수 있어요. 자, 알프레드, 자.\"\n\n만탈리니 씨는 단번에 정신을 차리는 게 좋겠다고는 생각하지 않았어. 몇 번이나 독약을 달라고 외치고, 어느 신사분이든 숙녀분이든 자기 머리를 쏴 달라고 부탁한 다음에야, 좀 부드러운 감정이 솟아나 가엾게 흐느껴 울었어. 이렇게 마음이 누그러진 상태에서 그는 칼을 빼앗기는 데도 저항하지 않았지—사실 솔직히 말하면, 치마 주머니에 넣고 다니기엔 거추장스럽고 위험한 물건이라 차라리 치워 버려 다행이라 여겼거든—그리고 마침내 다정한 아내에게 이끌려 자리를 떴어.\n\n두세 시간을 끈 끝에, 젊은 아가씨들은 다음 통보가 있을 때까지 일을 그만둬도 된다는 말을 들었어. 그리고 이틀이 지나자 만탈리니라는 이름이 파산자 명단에 올랐지. 미스 니클비는 같은 날 아침 우편으로 통지 한 장을 받았어. 앞으로 이 가게는 미스 내그라는 이름으로 운영될 거고, 그녀의 도움은 더 이상 필요 없다는 내용이었지. 니클비 부인은 이 소식을 듣자마자, 자기는 진작부터 이렇게 될 줄 알았다고 큰소리쳤어. 그러고는 자기가 바로 이런 일을 예언했던 알 수 없는 여러 경우를 줄줄이 들먹였지.\n\n\"그러니까 내가 다시 말하지만,\" 니클비 부인이 한마디 했어(굳이 짚을 것도 없이, 부인은 전에 이런 말을 한 적이 한 번도 없었어). \"내가 다시 말하지만, 부인복점이나 양장점이라는 건, 케이트, 네가 몸담을 일로 가장 마지막에나 생각했어야 할 거야. 너를 나무라는 건 아니다, 얘야. 그래도 이 말은 해야겠어. 네가 네 어미하고 상의만 했더라면—\"\n\n\"알았어요, 알았어요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 부드럽게 말했어. \"그럼 이제 뭘 추천하시겠어요?\"\n\n\"추천이라니!\" 니클비 부인이 외쳤어. \"뻔하잖니, 얘야. 너 같은 처지의 젊은 아가씨한테 이 세상 모든 일 중에서, 점잖은 부인의 말동무가 되는 일이야말로 네 교육이며, 몸가짐이며, 외모며, 그 밖에 모든 걸로 봐서 딱 맞는 일이지 않니? 너희 가엾은 아버지가 그 젊은 아가씨 얘기 하는 걸 못 들어 봤니? 네 아버지가 총각 시절에 하숙하던 그 집에서 같이 하숙하던 노부인의 딸 말이야—이름이 뭐였더라? B로 시작해서 g로 끝났던 건 아는데, 그게 워터스였나—아니, 그건 또 아니었을 거야. 아무튼 이름이야 뭐였든, 그 젊은 아가씨가 어느 유부녀의 말동무로 갔는데, 그 부인이 얼마 안 가 죽었고, 그 남편하고 결혼해서, 의사가 평생 본 것 중 가장 멋진 사내아이를 낳았다는 거 모르니—그게 다 일 년 반 만에 일어난 일이라잖아?\"\n\n케이트는 이 호의적인 추억담의 봇물이 어디서 터졌는지 잘 알았어. 엄마가 말동무라는 직업 쪽에서 실제든 상상이든 무슨 기회를 찾아낸 거였지. 그래서 케이트는 아주 참을성 있게 기다렸어. 주제에 들어맞든 안 맞든 온갖 추억담과 일화가 다 바닥날 때까지 말이야. 그러고는 마침내 무슨 발견을 했는지 조심스레 물어봤지. 그제야 진실이 드러났어. 니클비 부인은 그날 아침, 포터 맥주를 받아 오는 술집에서 어제 자 신문 한 부를 얻어 왔는데, 더없이 점잖은 신문이었지. 그리고 이 어제 자 신문에 광고 하나가 실려 있었어. 더없이 순수하고 문법에 딱 맞는 영어로 쓰인 광고였지. 어느 유부녀가 말동무로 점잖은 젊은 여성을 구한다는 내용이었어. 그리고 그 유부녀의 이름과 주소는 광고에 적힌, 시내 서쪽 끝의 어느 도서관에 문의하면 알 수 있다는 거였지.\n\n\"그러니까 내가 하는 말이,\" 니클비 부인이 의기양양하게 신문을 내려놓으며 외쳤어. \"네 삼촌만 반대하지 않으면, 한번 해 볼 만한 일이라는 거다.\"\n\n케이트는 이미 세상과 거칠게 부대낀 뒤라 마음이 너무 지쳐 있었어. 게다가 그 순간엔 자기한테 어떤 운명이 닥칠지 정말로 별로 신경 쓰이지도 않아서, 반대할 마음이 없었지. 랄프 니클비 씨도 반대하지 않았어. 오히려 그 제안을 크게 찬성했지. 마담 만탈리니가 갑자기 망한 일에도 별로 놀라는 기색이 없었어. 사실 놀랐다면 그게 이상한 일이었지. 그 일을 꾸미고 부추긴 게 대부분 그 자신이었으니까. 그래서 이름과 주소는 지체 없이 알아냈고, 미스 니클비와 그녀의 엄마는 그날 오전 곧장 슬론 가 캐도건 플레이스에 사는 위티털리 부인을 찾아 나섰어.\n\n캐도건 플레이스는 두 극단을 잇는 단 하나의 가느다란 끈이야. 벨그레이브 광장의 귀족적인 보도와 첼시의 야만 사이를 이어 주는 연결 고리지. 슬론 가에 있긴 하지만, 슬론 가에 속하지는 않아. 캐도건 플레이스 사람들은 슬론 가를 깔보고, 브롬프턴은 천하다고 여기지. 그들도 유행을 흉내 내면서, 뉴 로드는 대체 어디 있느냐는 듯 굴어. 그렇다고 벨그레이브 광장이며 그로스브너 플레이스의 상류층과 똑같은 처지라고 주장하는 건 아니야. 다만 그들에 견주면, 명문가의 사생아 같은 위치에 서 있달까. 그러니까 자기 혈연을 자랑하는 데 만족하는 사생아들 말이야. 정작 그 혈연 쪽에서는 자기들을 모른 척하는데도 말이지. 캐도건 플레이스 사람들은 최고위 신분의 분위기와 겉모습을 할 수 있는 한 다 걸치고 다니지만, 실제로는 중간 계급의 처지야. 캐도건 플레이스는 자기 안에는 없는 출신과 신분의 자랑이라는 충격을, 자기보다 더 높은 곳의 원천에서 끌어다가, 그 경계 너머에 사는 사람들에게 전해 주는 전도체 같은 거야. 아니면 샴쌍둥이를 잇는 인대처럼, 두 개의 별개 몸뚱이의 생명과 본질을 조금씩 품고 있으면서도, 정작 어느 쪽에도 속하지 않는 거지.\n\n이렇게 어정쩡한 땅에 위티털리 부인이 살았어. 그리고 위티털리 부인의 집 문을, 케이트 니클비는 떨리는 손으로 두드렸지. 문을 연 건 머리에 밀가루를 뿌렸는지, 분필 칠을 했는지, 아무튼 무슨 식으로든 칠을 한(진짜 분 같지는 않았어) 덩치 큰 하인이었어. 그 덩치 큰 하인은 소개 명함을 받더니, 그걸 작은 사환에게 건넸지. 그 사환이 어찌나 작은지, 보통 차림으로는 사환 복장에 꼭 필요한 그 작은 단추들을 그 몸뚱이에 다 달 수가 없을 정도였어. 그래서 단추들을 어쩔 수 없이 네 개씩 나란히 붙여 놓아야 했지. 이 젊은 신사는 쟁반에 명함을 받쳐 들고 위층으로 올라갔어. 그가 돌아오기를 기다리는 동안, 케이트와 엄마는 좀 지저분하고 허름해 보이는 식당으로 안내됐지. 그 방은 어찌나 안락하게 꾸며져 있던지, 먹고 마시는 일만 빼고는 거의 다른 무슨 용도에든 딱 맞아 보였어.\n\n자, 일이 평범하게 돌아갔다면, 그리고 책에 적힌 상류층 생활에 관한 온갖 믿을 만한 묘사대로라면, 위티털리 부인은 자기 규방에 있어야 마땅했어. 그런데 마침 위티털리 씨가 그 규방에서 면도를 하고 있었는지 어쨌는지, 아무튼 분명한 건 위티털리 부인은 응접실에서 손님을 맞이했다는 거야. 거기엔 적절하고 필요한 게 다 갖춰져 있었지. 위티털리 부인의 얼굴빛에 은은한 화색을 드리워 줄 장밋빛 커튼과 가구 덮개도 있었고, 위티털리 부인의 심심풀이로 낯선 이의 다리를 물어뜯을 작은 개도 있었고, 위티털리 부인의 기운을 돋워 줄 초콜릿을 받쳐 올릴 앞서 말한 그 사환도 있었어.\n\n부인은 달콤하면서도 밋밋한 분위기에, 사람을 끄는 창백한 얼굴을 하고 있었어. 부인한테도, 가구한테도, 집한테도 어딘지 빛바랜 기색이 감돌았지. 부인은 아주 꾸밈없는 자세로 소파에 비스듬히 기대 있었는데, 어찌나 그런지 발레의 첫 장면을 위해 만반의 준비를 마치고, 그저 막이 오르기만 기다리는 여배우로 봐도 될 정도였어.\n\n\"의자를 내드려라.\"\n\n사환이 의자를 내놨어.\n\n\"방에서 나가거라, 알퐁스.\"\n\n사환이 나갔어. 그런데 평범한 빌이라는 이름을 얼굴과 몸뚱이에 그대로 써 붙인 알퐁스가 세상에 있다면, 그 사환 소년이 바로 그랬지.\n\n\"실례를 무릅쓰고 찾아왔어요, 부인.\" 케이트가 어색한 침묵이 몇 초 흐른 뒤에 말했어. \"광고를 보고요.\"\n\n\"네.\" 위티털리 부인이 대답했어. \"우리 집 사람 중 하나가 신문에 냈죠—네.\"\n\n\"혹시,\" 케이트가 조심스레 말했어. \"아직 최종 결정을 안 하셨다면, 제가 지원하느라 폐를 끼쳐도 너그러이 봐주시지 않을까 했어요.\"\n\n\"네.\" 위티털리 부인이 또 느릿하게 말했어.\n\n\"이미 사람을 고르셨다면—\"\n\n\"오 아니에요.\" 부인이 말을 끊었어. \"난 그렇게 쉽게 맞는 사람을 못 찾아요. 정말 뭐라 해야 할지 모르겠네. 말동무 일은 전에 해 본 적이 없죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n니클비 부인은 기회를 호시탐탐 노리고 있다가, 케이트가 대답하기도 전에 능숙하게 끼어들었어. \"낯선 분 곁에서는 없어요, 부인.\" 이 착한 부인이 말했어. \"하지만 몇 년 동안 제 말동무 노릇은 해 왔답니다. 제가 이 애 어미예요, 부인.\"\n\n\"오!\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어. \"이해했어요.\"\n\n\"정말이지요, 부인.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"한때는 제 딸이 세상에 나가 일을 해야 할 거라곤 꿈에도 생각 못 했답니다. 이 애의 가엾은 아버지가 자기 재산이 있는 신사분이었거든요. 그리고 제가 늘 간곡히 부탁할 때 제때 귀를 기울이기만 했어도 지금까지 그랬을 텐데—\"\n\n\"엄마.\" 케이트가 낮은 목소리로 말했어.\n\n\"케이트야, 내가 말 좀 하게 해 주련.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"내가 실례를 무릅쓰고 이분께 설명을 좀—\"\n\n\"그건 거의 필요 없을 것 같아요, 엄마.\"\n\n니클비 부인은 이 일을 단번에 매듭지을 무슨 말을 하려 한다는 듯 온갖 눈살을 찌푸리고 눈짓을 보냈어. 그래도 케이트는 의미심장한 눈빛으로 자기 뜻을 굽히지 않았지. 그래서 니클비 부인은 한 번쯤은 일장 연설을 막 시작하려던 바로 그 문턱에서 멈추고 말았어.\n\n\"무슨 재주가 있나요?\" 위티털리 부인이 눈을 감은 채 물었어.\n\n케이트는 얼굴을 붉히며 자기가 익힌 주된 재주들을 말했어. 그리고 니클비 부인은 그것들을 하나하나 손가락으로 꼽으며 짚어 갔지. 나오기 전에 그 개수를 미리 계산해 뒀거든. 다행히 두 계산이 맞아떨어져서, 니클비 부인은 또 입을 놀릴 핑계가 없었어.\n\n\"성격은 좋은가요?\" 위티털리 부인이 잠깐 눈을 떴다가 다시 감으며 물었어.\n\n\"그러길 바라요.\" 케이트가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"그리고 모든 면에서 아주 점잖은 신원 보증인이 있나요?\"\n\n케이트는 있다고 대답하며, 삼촌의 명함을 탁자 위에 놓았어.\n\n\"의자를 좀 더 가까이 당겨서, 내가 좀 볼 수 있게 해 줘요.\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어. \"내가 근시가 아주 심해서, 얼굴이 잘 안 보이거든요.\"\n\n케이트는 좀 쑥스럽긴 했지만 이 부탁대로 의자를 당겼어. 그러자 위티털리 부인은 나른하게 그녀의 얼굴을 살폈는데, 이게 한 이삼 분쯤 이어졌지.\n\n\"외모가 마음에 드는군요.\" 그 부인이 작은 종을 울리며 말했어. \"알퐁스, 주인 어른더러 이리 오시라고 해라.\"\n\n사환이 이 심부름을 하러 사라졌어. 그리고 잠깐 동안 양쪽 다 한마디도 없던 사이가 지난 뒤, 사환이 문을 열어 한 거물 신사를 들였지. 서른여덟쯤 된 신사였는데, 얼굴은 좀 평민 같았고, 머리숱은 아주 옅었어. 그는 잠시 위티털리 부인 위로 몸을 숙이고는, 속삭이며 그녀와 이야기를 나눴어.\n\n\"오!\" 그가 돌아서며 말했어. \"그래. 이건 아주 중요한 문제요. 위티털리 부인은 아주 예민한 성품이라서. 아주 섬세하고, 아주 연약하지. 온실 속 화초요, 외래종이라고.\"\n\n\"오! 헨리, 여보.\" 위티털리 부인이 끼어들었어.\n\n\"당신은 그렇잖소, 여보, 당신도 알면서. 숨 한 번이면—\" 위티털리 씨가 보이지 않는 깃털 하나를 후 불어 날리며 말했어. \"휴! 당신은 가 버린다고!\"\n\n부인이 한숨을 쉬었어.\n\n\"당신의 영혼은 몸에 비해 너무 크오.\" 위티털리 씨가 말했어. \"당신의 지성이 당신을 닳게 하지. 의사들도 다 그렇게 말하잖소. 당신한테 왕진 와 달라는 부름을 자랑스러워하지 않는 의사가 없다는 걸 당신도 알 거요. 그들이 입을 모아 하는 말이 뭐겠소? '선생,' 내가 바로 이 방에서, 텀리 스너핌 경이 마지막으로 왔을 때 그에게 말했지. '선생, 내 아내의 병이 대체 뭐요? 다 말해 주시오. 나는 견딜 수 있소. 신경이오?' '여보게,' 그가 말했지. '그 부인을 자랑스러워하게. 그분을 소중히 여기게. 그분은 사교계의 보배이자, 자네의 보배일세. 그분의 병은 영혼이라네. 그게 부풀고, 팽창하고, 늘어나면서—피가 끓고, 맥박이 빨라지고, 흥분이 치솟고—휴!'\" 여기서 위티털리 씨는, 묘사에 열을 올린 나머지 오른손을 휘두르다 니클비 부인의 모자에서 채 한 치도 안 되는 데까지 갖다 댔다가, 황급히 다시 거둬들이고는, 무슨 사나운 기계로 푼 것처럼 거세게 코를 풀었어.\n\n\"당신은 날 실제보다 더 심하게 만드네요, 헨리.\" 위티털리 부인이 희미하게 미소 지으며 말했어.\n\n\"아니오, 줄리아, 아니오.\" 위티털리 씨가 말했어. \"당신이 드나드는—당신의 신분이며, 인맥이며, 타고난 재능 때문에 어쩔 수 없이 드나드는—사교계는 가장 무시무시한 흥분의 소용돌이이자 회오리란 말이오. 맙소사, 엑서터의 선거 무도회에서 당신이 준남작의 조카와 춤춘 그날 밤을 내가 어찌 잊겠소! 정말 대단했지.\"\n\n\"난 그런 영광스러운 일을 치르고 나면 늘 뒤에 고생을 해요.\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"바로 그 이유 때문에,\" 그녀의 남편이 대꾸했어. \"당신한테는 더없이 온화하고, 더없이 다정하고, 한없이 공감해 주고, 완벽하게 차분한 말동무가 있어야 하는 거요.\"\n\n여기서 위티털리 씨 부부는—두 사람은 서로한테라기보다는 니클비 모녀를 향해 이야기하고 있었지—말을 멈추고는, 두 청중을 바라봤어. '이 모든 게 어떠세요?' 하고 말하는 듯한 표정으로 말이야.\n\n\"위티털리 부인은,\" 그녀의 남편이 니클비 부인을 향해 말했어. \"번쩍이는 무리와 화려한 모임들이 모셔 가려 하고 떠받드는 사람이오. 부인은 오페라며, 연극이며, 미술이며, 그—그—그—\"\n\n\"귀족들이죠, 여보.\" 위티털리 부인이 끼어들었어.\n\n\"물론, 귀족들이지.\" 위티털리 씨가 말했어. \"그리고 군인들도. 부인은 어마어마하게 다양한 주제에 대해 어마어마하게 다양한 견해를 짓고 또 펼치지. 공직에 있는 어떤 사람들이 위티털리 부인이 자기들을 정말 어떻게 보는지 안다면, 아마 지금처럼 그렇게 고개를 빳빳이 들고 다니진 못할 거요.\"\n\n\"쉿, 헨리.\" 부인이 말했어. \"이건 좀 온당치 못해요.\"\n\n\"이름은 대지 않겠소, 줄리아.\" 위티털리 씨가 대답했어. \"그러니 다칠 사람은 아무도 없지. 난 그저 당신이 평범한 사람이 아니라는 걸, 당신의 마음과 몸 사이에 끊임없이 마찰이 일어나고 있다는 걸, 그러니 당신은 어루만져 주고 돌봐 줄 사람이 필요하다는 걸 보여 주려고 이 일을 언급한 것뿐이오. 자, 이제 차분하고 냉정하게 들려주시오. 이 젊은 아가씨가 그 일에 어떤 자질을 갖췄는지 말이오.\"\n\n이 요청에 따라, 자질들을 처음부터 다시 죽 늘어놓았어. 거기다 위티털리 씨의 숱한 끼어듦과 꼬치꼬치 캐묻는 질문까지 덧붙여서 말이지. 결국, 신원 조회를 해 본 다음, 이틀 안에 미스 니클비에게 삼촌 앞으로 결정적인 답을 보내기로 합의됐어. 이런 조건들에 합의가 되자, 사환이 계단 창문 있는 데까지 그들을 안내했어. 그리고 그 지점에서 보초를 교대한 덩치 큰 하인이, 현관문까지 그들을 무사히 인도했지.\n\n\"보아하니 아주 지체 높은 사람들이구나.\" 니클비 부인이 딸의 팔짱을 끼며 말했어. \"위티털리 부인은 정말 우월한 분이지 뭐냐!\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하세요, 엄마?\" 케이트의 대답은 이게 다였어.\n\n\"아니, 어떻게 그렇게 생각 안 할 수가 있겠니, 케이트야?\" 엄마가 대꾸했어. \"물론 얼굴은 창백하고, 무척 지쳐 보이긴 하더라. 부디 몸이 닳도록 무리하는 게 아니어야 할 텐데, 아무래도 걱정이구나.\"\n\n이런 생각이 이 통찰력 깊은 부인을 한 가지 계산으로 이끌었어. 위티털리 부인이 앞으로 얼마나 더 살까, 그리고 그 슬픔에 잠긴 홀아비가 자기 딸에게 청혼할 가능성은 얼마나 될까 하는 계산이었지. 집에 닿기도 전에, 부인은 위티털리 부인의 영혼을 온갖 육체의 속박에서 풀어 주었고, 케이트를 하노버 광장의 세인트 조지 성당에서 아주 성대하게 결혼시켰어. 그리고 딱 하나 사소한 문제만 결정을 못 내렸지. 자기를 위한 프랑스식으로 광을 낸 멋진 마호가니 침대를 캐도건 플레이스 집 뒤편 삼 층에 놓을까, 아니면 앞쪽 사 층에 놓을까 하는 문제였어. 이 두 방의 장점을 도무지 저울질하지 못해서, 결국 이 문제는 사위의 결정에 맡기기로 하고 매듭지었지.\n\n신원 조회가 이루어졌어. 그 답은—케이트한테는 그리 큰 기쁨이 아니었지만—호의적이었지. 그리고 일주일이 지나자 케이트는 자기 짐이며 귀중품을 다 챙겨 위티털리 부인의 저택으로 갔어. 우리는 일단 그녀를 거기 남겨 두기로 하자.\n\n# CHAPTER 22\n\n니콜라스가 집세를 치르고, 그 초라한 가구를 빌려 준 중개인과 셈을 마치고 나니, 손에 쥔 돈이든 앞으로 들어올 돈이든, 그가 가질 권리가 있는 전 재산은 다 합쳐 봐야 이십 실링에서 반 페니 몇 푼 더 되는 게 고작이었어. 그런데도 그는 런던을 떠나기로 마음먹은 그날 아침을 가벼운 마음으로 맞았어. 그리고 톡톡 튀는 기운으로 침대에서 벌떡 일어났지. 이런 활기야말로 젊은 사람들이 누리는 다행스러운 복이야. 안 그랬으면 이 세상에 늙은이가 채워질 일도 없었을 테니까.\n\n초봄의 춥고 메마른, 안개 낀 아침이었어. 안개 자욱한 거리에는 야윈 그림자 몇이 이리저리 어른거렸어. 그리고 가끔씩 그 칙칙한 안개 속으로 집으로 돌아가는 삯마차의 묵직한 윤곽이 어렴풋이 떠올랐지. 마차는 천천히 가까이 다가오더니, 덜커덩거리며 굴러 지나갔어. 하얗게 서리 앉은 지붕에서 얇은 서리 껍질을 흩뿌리면서 말이야. 그러고는 이내 다시 안개 속으로 사라져 버렸지. 이따금 질질 끄는 발소리가 들렸어. 그리고 가난한 굴뚝 청소부가 떨면서, 이른 일을 하러 기어 나오며 내지르는 추운 외침 소리도 들렸지. 밤을 지키는 야경꾼의 묵직한 발소리도 났어. 그는 천천히 오르락내리락 걸으며, 잠들기 전까지 아직 남은 더딘 시간을 욕했어. 육중한 짐수레와 마차들이 덜컹거리는 소리, 시장마다 사고파는 사람들을 실어 나르는 가벼운 수레들이 굴러가는 소리도 났어. 곤히 잠든 이들의 문을 두드려 봐야 헛수고인 소리도 났지. 이런 온갖 소리가 때때로 귓가에 닿았어. 하지만 죄다 안개에 파묻힌 듯했지. 그래서 눈에 보이는 모든 것이 흐릿하듯, 귀에 들리는 모든 것도 거의 그만큼 분간하기 어려웠어. 날이 밝아 오면서 굼뜬 어둠은 오히려 짙어졌어. 그리고 용기를 내어 일어나 커튼 친 창문 너머로 음울한 거리를 들여다본 사람들은, 다시 슬그머니 침대로 기어들어 몸을 웅크리고 잠을 청했어.\n\n바쁜 런던에 이런 아침의 기척이 채 퍼지기도 전에, 니콜라스는 혼자서 시내로 들어와 어머니가 사는 집 창문 아래 섰어. 칙칙하고 휑한 모습이었지. 하지만 그에게는 빛과 생기가 있는 곳이었어. 그 낡은 벽 안에는 적어도 하나, 모욕이나 치욕을 당하면 그의 핏줄에 흐르는 것과 똑같은 피가 솟구쳐 오를 심장이 있었으니까.\n\n그는 길을 건너 여동생이 자는 줄로 아는 방 창문으로 눈을 들었어. 창은 닫혀 있고 어두웠지. '가엾은 것.' 니콜라스는 생각했어. '누가 여기 서성이고 있는지도 모르겠지!'\n\n그는 다시 한 번 올려다봤어. 그리고 잠깐이지만, 케이트가 거기 없어서 작별 인사 한마디 나눌 수 없다는 게 거의 야속하게 느껴졌어. '맙소사!' 그는 퍼뜩 스스로를 다잡으며 생각했지. '나도 참 어린애구나!'\n\n\"이게 나아.\" 니콜라스는 몇 걸음 어슬렁어슬렁 걷다가 같은 자리로 되돌아와 말했어. \"전에 식구들을 떠날 때도, 마음만 먹었으면 작별 인사를 천 번이라도 할 수 있었지만, 헤어지는 아픔을 덜어 주었잖아. 그러니 지금이라고 안 될 게 뭐람?\" 그가 이렇게 말하는데, 커튼이 움직이는 듯한 착각이 잠깐 그를 사로잡았어. 케이트가 창가에 있는 것만 같았지. 그러자 우리 모두에게 흔한, 그 묘하게 어긋나는 감정 가운데 하나로, 그는 저도 모르게 출입구 안으로 몸을 숨겼어. 케이트가 자기를 보지 못하도록 말이야. 그는 자신의 약한 마음에 피식 웃었어. 그러고는 \"신의 가호가 있기를!\" 하고 말한 뒤, 한결 가벼운 걸음으로 걸어갔지.\n\n니콜라스가 옛 하숙집에 닿았을 때 스마이크가 애타게 그를 기다리고 있었어. 뉴먼도 마찬가지였지. 뉴먼은 그들이 길을 떠날 채비를 하라고, 하루치 벌이를 럼주와 우유 한 통에 다 써 버렸어. 그들은 짐을 꾸려 묶었어. 스마이크가 그걸 둘러멨고, 셋은 길을 나섰지. 뉴먼 노그스도 함께였어. 전날 밤, 그가 갈 수 있는 데까지 같이 걷겠다고 우겼거든.\n\n\"어느 쪽으로 가나?\" 뉴먼이 아쉬운 듯 물었어.\n\n\"우선 킹스턴으로.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"그다음엔 어디로?\" 뉴먼이 물었어. \"왜 말을 안 해 주는 거야?\"\n\n\"나도 잘 모르거든, 친구.\" 니콜라스가 그의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 대꾸했어. \"안다 해도, 아직 계획도 전망도 없어. 자네가 연락할 새도 없이 거처를 백 번은 옮길지도 모르고.\"\n\n\"자네 머릿속에 무슨 깊은 꿍꿍이가 있는 것 같은데.\" 뉴먼이 미심쩍은 듯 말했어.\n\n\"어찌나 깊은지,\" 젊은 친구가 대답했어. \"나조차도 그 속을 못 헤아릴 정도야. 무얼 결정하든, 곧 편지하겠으니 믿어 줘.\"\n\n\"잊으면 안 돼.\" 뉴먼이 말했어.\n\n\"잊을 리가 없지.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어. \"친구가 그렇게 많아서 그 수에 헷갈려 가장 좋은 친구를 잊을 처지도 못 되거든.\"\n\n이런 이야기를 나누며 그들은 두어 시간을 걸었어. 니콜라스가 길가 돌 위에 주저앉지만 않았더라면, 이틀이라도 그렇게 걸었을지 몰라. 그는 뉴먼 노그스가 돌아가기 전에는 한 발짝도 더 움직이지 않겠다고 단호히 못 박았지. 뉴먼은 처음엔 반 마일만 더, 그다음엔 사분의 일 마일만 더 가자고 졸랐지만 소용없었어. 그래서 하는 수 없이 따랐지. 마음에서 우러난 다정한 작별 인사를 몇 번이고 주고받은 뒤, 그는 골든 스퀘어 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸어. 두 나그네가 멀리 점처럼 작아질 때까지 몇 번이고 돌아서서 모자를 흔들면서 말이야.\n\n\"자, 내 말 좀 들어 봐, 스마이크.\" 둘이 씩씩한 마음으로 터벅터벅 앞으로 나아가며 니콜라스가 말했어. \"우리는 포츠머스로 가는 거야.\"\n\n스마이크는 고개를 끄덕이며 웃었어. 하지만 그 밖에 다른 감정은 드러내지 않았지. 포츠머스로 가든 포트 로열로 가든, 그에게는 다 똑같았으니까. 둘이 함께 가기만 한다면 말이야.\n\n\"나도 이런 일을 잘 아는 건 아니야.\" 니콜라스가 말을 이었어. \"하지만 포츠머스는 항구 도시야. 다른 일자리를 못 구하면 어느 배에든 탈 수 있지 않을까 싶어. 나는 젊고 몸도 잰 편이니까, 여러모로 쓸모가 있을 거야. 너도 그렇고.\"\n\n\"그랬으면 좋겠어.\" 스마이크가 대답했어. \"내가 그… 어딘지 알지?\"\n\n\"그래, 알아.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그곳 이름은 말할 것 없어.\"\n\n\"그러니까, 내가 거기 있을 때,\" 스마이크가 말을 이었어. 제 솜씨를 뽐낼 생각에 눈을 반짝이면서 말이야. \"나는 누구 못지않게 소젖도 짜고, 말도 손질할 수 있었거든.\"\n\n\"허!\" 니콜라스가 점잖게 말했어. \"배 위에선 그런 짐승을 많이 안 키울걸, 스마이크. 말이 있다 해도 빗질에 그리 까다롭지도 않을 거고. 그래도 다른 일을 배우면 되잖아. 뜻이 있는 곳에 길이 있는 법이니까.\"\n\n\"나는 정말 마음은 굴뚝같아.\" 스마이크가 다시 환하게 밝아지며 말했어.\n\n\"네 마음이야 하늘이 알지.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어. \"혹시 네가 안 되더라도, 어지간하면 내가 우리 둘 몫을 다 해낼 테니까.\"\n\n\"오늘 다 가는 거야?\" 잠시 잠잠하다가 스마이크가 물었어.\n\n\"그건 네 부지런한 다리에도 너무 가혹한 시험이지.\" 니콜라스가 사람 좋게 웃으며 말했어. \"아니야. 고댈밍이 런던에서 삼십 몇 마일쯤 돼. 빌린 지도를 보고 알았지. 거기서 쉴 작정이야. 내일 다시 부지런히 가야 해. 우리는 어슬렁댈 만큼 넉넉하지가 않으니까. 그 짐은 내가 좀 들게! 이리 줘!\"\n\n\"아니, 아니.\" 스마이크가 몇 걸음 물러서며 대꾸했어. \"그걸 달라고 하지 마.\"\n\n\"왜 안 돼?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"적어도 너한테 뭐라도 하게 해 줘.\" 스마이크가 말했어. \"넌 내가 마땅히 해야 할 시중조차 들게 해 주질 않잖아. 내가 밤낮으로 어떻게 하면 너를 기쁘게 할까 궁리하는지 넌 영영 모를 거야.\"\n\n\"그런 말을 하다니 바보 같긴. 나도 잘 알고 다 보고 있는걸. 안 그러면 내가 눈멀고 분별없는 짐승이게?\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어. \"생각난 김에, 그리고 옆에 아무도 없으니 한 가지 물어볼게.\" 그는 스마이크의 얼굴을 가만히 들여다보며 덧붙였어. \"너 기억력이 좋니?\"\n\n\"모르겠어.\" 스마이크가 슬픈 듯 고개를 저으며 말했어. \"옛날엔 좋았던 것 같아. 그런데 지금은 다 사라졌어. 죄다 사라졌어.\"\n\n\"옛날엔 좋았던 것 같다는 건 왜 그렇게 생각해?\" 니콜라스가 그 대답이 어쩐지 자기 질문의 속뜻을 풀어 주기라도 하는 듯, 휙 그를 돌아보며 물었어.\n\n\"어릴 적엔 기억할 수 있었거든.\" 스마이크가 말했어. \"그게 아주, 아주 오래전이긴 해. 적어도 그렇게 느껴져. 네가 날 데려온 그곳에선 늘 머리가 어지럽고 멍했어. 사람들이 나한테 한 말을 도무지 기억할 수가 없었고, 가끔은 무슨 말인지 알아듣지도 못했어. 나… 가만, 가만있어 봐!\"\n\n\"지금 또 정신이 딴 데 가 있네.\" 니콜라스가 그의 팔을 건드리며 말했어.\n\n\"아니야.\" 그의 길동무가 멍한 눈빛으로 대답했어. \"그냥 어떻게…\" 그는 말하면서 저도 모르게 몸을 부르르 떨었어.\n\n\"그곳 생각은 이제 그만해. 다 끝난 일이니까.\" 니콜라스가 길동무의 눈을 똑바로 들여다보며 대꾸했어. 그 눈은 어느새 아무 뜻도 없는 멍한 응시로 가라앉고 있었지. 예전에 늘 그랬고, 그때도 흔히 짓던 표정이었어. \"요크셔에 처음 갔던 날은 어땠어?\"\n\n\"응?\" 소년이 외쳤어.\n\n\"그건 네 기억을 잃기 전이잖아.\" 니콜라스가 조용히 말했어. \"날씨가 더웠어, 추웠어?\"\n\n\"비가 왔어.\" 소년이 대답했어. \"엄청 많이 왔지. 비가 세차게 올 때면 나는 늘 말하곤 했어. 내가 처음 오던 그날 밤 같다고. 그러면 사람들이 빙 둘러서서 비 쏟아질 때 내가 우는 걸 보고 깔깔거렸어. 꼭 어린애 같다고들 했지. 그 말에 그날이 더 생각났어. 가끔은 온몸이 싸늘하게 식었어. 그때의 내 모습이 보였거든. 바로 그 똑같은 문으로 들어오는 모습 말이야.\"\n\n\"그때의 네 모습이라.\" 니콜라스가 짐짓 무심한 척 되뇌었어. \"어땠는데?\"\n\n\"아주 조그만 아이였어.\" 스마이크가 말했어. \"그 모습만 기억했어도, 사람들이 나한테 좀 가엾게 여기고 마음을 베풀어 줬을 텐데.\"\n\n\"거기까지 혼자 찾아간 건 아니지!\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"아니야.\" 스마이크가 대꾸했어. \"응, 아니야.\"\n\n\"누구랑 같이 갔어?\"\n\n\"한 남자랑. 거무튀튀하고 비쩍 마른 남자였어. 학교에서 사람들이 그렇게 말하는 걸 들었고, 나도 그건 전부터 기억하고 있었어. 그 남자랑 헤어져서 기뻤어, 그 사람이 무서웠거든. 그런데 사람들은 나를 그 사람보다 더 무섭게 했고, 더 모질게 부려 먹었어.\"\n\n\"날 봐.\" 니콜라스가 스마이크의 온 정신을 끌어모으려고 말했어. \"그래, 고개 돌리지 말고. 너 한 번이라도 너를 굽어보며 네 입술에 입 맞추고 너를 제 자식이라 부른 여자, 다정한 여자를 기억 못 해?\"\n\n\"못 해.\" 가엾은 아이가 고개를 저으며 말했어. \"응, 한 번도.\"\n\n\"요크셔의 그 집 말고 다른 집은?\"\n\n\"없어.\" 소년이 우울한 얼굴로 대꾸했어. \"방 하나… 방 하나에서 잤던 건 기억나. 집 꼭대기의 크고 외로운 방이었어. 천장에 들창이 하나 나 있었지. 그게 무서워서, 안 보려고 자주 이불로 머리를 덮곤 했어. 밤이면 곁에 아무도 없는 어린아이였으니까. 그리고 그 들창 너머엔 뭐가 있을까 늘 궁금했어. 시계도 하나 있었어, 한구석에 낡은 시계가. 그건 기억나. 그 방은 한 번도 잊은 적이 없어. 무서운 꿈을 꿀 때면 그 방이 그때 모습 그대로 돌아오거든. 그 안에서 그때는 본 적도 없는 것들과 사람들이 보이지만, 방만큼은 늘 있던 그대로야. 그건 절대 안 변해.\"\n\n\"이제 그 짐 내가 들면 안 될까?\" 니콜라스가 화제를 불쑥 바꾸며 물었어.\n\n\"안 돼.\" 스마이크가 말했어. \"안 돼. 자, 계속 걷자.\"\n\n그는 이렇게 말하며 걸음을 재촉했어. 앞선 이야기를 나누는 내내 둘이 가만히 서 있기라도 했던 것처럼 말이야. 니콜라스는 그를 유심히 지켜봤어. 그리고 이 대화의 한마디 한마디가 그의 기억에 고스란히 남았지.\n\n이때쯤엔 정오가 한 시간도 채 안 남았어. 그들이 떠나온 도시 위로는 여전히 짙은 안개가 자욱이 덮여 있었지. 마치 바삐 사는 사람들의 숨결 자체가 이익과 이윤을 노리는 그들의 궁리 위에 걸려, 저 위 고요한 하늘보다 거기에 더 끌리기라도 하는 것처럼 말이야. 하지만 탁 트인 시골은 맑고 화창했어. 가끔 움푹한 곳에서는 햇살이 아직 몰아내지 못한 안개 조각과 마주치기도 했지. 하지만 그것도 금세 지나쳤어. 그 너머 언덕을 힘겹게 오르다 아래를 내려다보면, 그 굼뜬 안개 덩어리가 낮의 환한 기운 앞에서 묵직하게 굴러 물러나는 모습이 보였어. 보기에 참 좋았지. 넓고 멋지고 정직한 햇살이 푸른 목초지와 잔물결 이는 물을 마치 여름처럼 환히 비추었어. 그러면서도 길손들에게는 이 이른 계절 특유의 생기 돋는 상쾌함을 그대로 남겨 주었지. 발밑의 땅은 탄력 있게 느껴졌어. 양들의 방울 소리는 그들의 귀에 음악 같았지. 그리고 걷느라 활기가 솟고 희망에 들떠서, 그들은 사자 같은 힘으로 앞으로 밀고 나아갔어.\n\n날이 저물자, 이 모든 밝은 빛깔이 가라앉으며 한결 차분한 색조로 바뀌었어. 마치 세월에 누그러진 젊은 날의 희망처럼, 또 점점 노년의 평온과 고요로 가라앉는 젊은 얼굴처럼 말이야. 그래도 그 더딘 저묾은 한창때 못지않게 아름다웠어. 자연은 어느 때, 어느 철에든 저마다의 아름다움을 베푸니까. 아침부터 밤까지가, 요람에서 무덤까지가 그러하듯, 이어지는 변화가 어찌나 부드럽고 수월한지 우리는 그 흐름을 좀처럼 알아채지도 못하지.\n\n마침내 그들은 고댈밍에 닿았어. 거기서 소박한 침대 두 개를 흥정해 빌리고, 곤히 잠들었지. 아침에 그들은 일어나 움직였어. 해만큼 이르지는 않았지만 말이야. 그리고 다시 길을 나섰지. 어제만큼 생생하지는 않았어도, 그들을 즐겁게 받쳐 줄 만큼의 희망과 기운은 여전히 남아 있었어.\n\n어제보다 더 힘든 하루 길이었어. 길고 고된 언덕을 올라야 했거든. 여행도 인생과 마찬가지로, 언덕은 오르는 것보다 내려가는 게 훨씬 쉬운 법이지. 그래도 그들은 꺾이지 않는 끈기로 계속 나아갔어. 끈기로 끝내 그 꼭대기에 오르지 못할 언덕은, 아직 이 세상에 솟아오른 적이 없거든.\n\n그들은 데블스 펀치 볼의 가장자리를 걸었어. 그 거친 곳에 세워진 돌에는, 한밤중에 거기서 벌어진 살인 사건을 알리는 글귀가 새겨져 있었지. 니콜라스가 그걸 읽어 주자, 스마이크는 빨려들 듯한 호기심으로 귀를 기울였어. 그들이 밟고 선 풀밭은 한때 피로 물들었던 곳이야. 살해당한 사람의 피가 한 방울씩 흘러내려, 이 자리에 이름을 붙여 준 그 움푹한 곳으로 떨어졌지. '데블스 볼이라.' 니콜라스는 그 텅 빈 곳을 들여다보며 생각했어. '이보다 더 어울리는 술을 담아 본 적은 없겠군!'\n\n그들은 한결같은 마음으로 계속 나아갔어. 그리고 마침내 넓고 광활한 구릉지에 들어섰지. 자그마한 언덕과 평지가 갖가지로 어우러져, 그 푸른 표면을 바꿔 가는 곳이었어. 어떤 곳에는 거의 수직으로 하늘을 향해 솟구친 봉우리가 있었어. 어찌나 가파른지 비탈에서 풀 뜯는 양과 염소가 아니고는 좀처럼 오를 수도 없었지. 또 어떤 곳에는 초록빛 둔덕이 서 있었어. 어찌나 곱게 비탈지고 가늘어지며, 어찌나 부드럽게 평지로 녹아드는지 그 경계를 가늠하기조차 어려웠지. 언덕들이 서로 겹쳐 부풀어 올랐어. 맵시 있는 것과 투박한 것, 매끈한 것과 거친 것, 우아한 것과 기괴한 것이 한데 무심히 나란히 놓여, 사방으로 시야를 둘러쌌어. 그러다 가끔 뜻밖의 소리와 함께 땅에서 까마귀 떼가 솟구쳐 올랐지. 까악까악 울며 가장 가까운 언덕 둘레를 빙빙 돌다가, 마치 갈 길을 못 정한 듯하더니, 별안간 날갯짓을 가다듬고 길게 열린 골짜기를 따라 빛처럼 빠르게 미끄러져 내려갔어.\n\n차츰차츰 양옆의 경치가 점점 멀어져 갔어. 그렇게 풍요롭고 드넓은 풍광에서 벗어나, 그들은 다시 한 번 탁 트인 시골로 나왔지. 목적지가 가까워졌다는 생각에 다시 나아갈 새 힘이 솟았어. 하지만 길이 험했던 데다 도중에 어정거리기도 해서, 스마이크는 지쳐 있었어. 그래서 그들이 길에서 벗어나 어느 길가 여관 문 앞으로 들어설 무렵엔, 이미 땅거미가 내려앉아 있었어. 포츠머스까지는 아직 십이 마일이나 남은 곳이었지.\n\n\"십이 마일이라.\" 니콜라스가 두 손을 지팡이에 짚고 기대어, 스마이크를 미심쩍게 바라보며 말했어.\n\n\"멀고 먼 십이 마일이지요.\" 여관 주인이 되풀이했어.\n\n\"길은 좋은가요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"아주 나빠요.\" 주인이 말했어. 여관 주인이니, 당연히 그렇게 말하겠지.\n\n\"계속 가긴 가야 하는데.\" 니콜라스가 망설이며 중얼거렸어. \"어찌해야 할지 모르겠네요.\"\n\n\"제 말에 휘둘리진 마세요.\" 주인이 대꾸했어. \"저 같으면 안 갑니다.\"\n\n\"안 가신다고요?\" 니콜라스가 여전히 갈팡질팡하며 물었어.\n\n\"제 처지가 나은 줄 안다면야 안 가지요.\" 주인이 말했어. 그러고는 앞치마를 추켜올리고 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣더니, 문밖으로 두어 걸음 나가 짐짓 무관심한 척 어두운 길을 내려다봤어.\n\n니콜라스는 고생에 찌든 스마이크의 얼굴을 한 번 보고는 마음을 정했어. 그래서 더는 따지지 않고, 여기 머물기로 결심했지.\n\n주인은 그들을 부엌으로 안내했어. 그리고 불이 잘 타고 있는데도 날이 무척 춥다고 한마디 했지. 만일 불이 변변치 못했더라면, 날이 무척 따뜻하다고 했을 거야.\n\n\"저녁으로 뭘 주실 수 있나요?\" 니콜라스가 당연히 그렇게 물었어.\n\n\"글쎄요, 뭘 드시고 싶으신데요?\" 주인의 대답도 그에 못지않게 당연했지.\n\n니콜라스는 차가운 고기를 청해 봤어. 그런데 차가운 고기는 없었지. 수란을 청했지만 달걀도 없었어. 양고기 토막을 청했지만, 삼 마일 안엔 양고기 토막 하나 없었어. 지난주엔 주체할 수 없을 만큼 많았고, 모레는 또 엄청나게 들어올 거라는데도 말이야.\n\n\"그럼,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"당신한테 다 맡길게요. 처음부터 그러라고 하셨으면 그랬을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그럼 이러면 어떨까요.\" 주인이 대꾸했어. \"안쪽 방에 한 신사분이 계신데, 아홉 시에 뜨거운 비프스테이크 푸딩이랑 감자를 시켜 놓으셨거든요. 혼자 다 드시기엔 양이 많아요. 제가 여쭤보면, 손님이 같이 드셔도 된다고 하실 게 거의 틀림없습니다. 얼른 가서 여쭤보고 오지요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니요.\" 니콜라스가 그를 붙잡으며 말했어. \"그건 좀 사양하고 싶군요. 나… 그러니까… 에잇! 왜 시원하게 말을 못 할까? 자, 보시다시피 나는 아주 초라하게 여행하는 데다, 여기까지 걸어서 왔어요. 그 신사분이 내가 같이 앉는 걸 안 좋아하실 게 거의 분명해요. 이렇게 먼지투성이 꼴이긴 하지만, 그래도 그분한테 비집고 들어갈 만큼 염치없진 못하답니다.\"\n\n\"세상에, 손님도 참.\" 주인이 말했어. \"그저 크럼블스 씨인걸요. 그분은 까다로운 분이 아니에요.\"\n\n\"그렇지 않다고요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어. 사실대로 말하자면, 구수한 푸딩 생각이 그의 마음에 슬슬 와닿고 있었거든.\n\n\"천만에요.\" 주인이 대답했어. \"그분은 손님 말솜씨를 분명히 좋아하실 겁니다. 어쨌든 곧 알게 되겠지요. 잠깐만 기다리세요.\"\n\n주인은 더 허락을 구할 것도 없이 서둘러 안쪽 방으로 들어갔어. 니콜라스도 굳이 말리려 들지 않았지. 이런 처지에 저녁 한 끼는 가벼이 여길 수 없는 자못 진지한 문제라고, 현명하게 판단한 거야. 얼마 지나지 않아 주인이 잔뜩 들뜬 채 돌아왔어.\n\n\"됐습니다.\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 말했어. \"그러실 줄 알았어요. 저 안에서 볼 만한 구경거리도 좀 보실 겁니다. 어이쿠, 어찌나 신명 나게들 하던지!\"\n\n이 외침이, 그것도 잔뜩 황홀한 말투로 내뱉은 외침이 무엇을 두고 한 말인지 물어볼 새도 없었어. 주인이 벌써 방문을 활짝 열어젖혔거든. 니콜라스는 곧장 그리로 들어갔지. 어깨에 짐을 멘 스마이크가 그 뒤를 따랐고. 스마이크는 그 짐을 마치 금자루라도 되는 양 한시도 눈을 떼지 않고 지니고 다녔어.\n\n니콜라스는 뭔가 별난 광경을 각오하고는 있었어. 하지만 눈앞에 펼쳐진 만큼 별난 건 미처 생각지 못했지. 방 안쪽 끝에는 사내아이 둘이 있었어. 하나는 아주 키가 컸고 다른 하나는 아주 작았는데, 둘 다 선원 차림이었지. 적어도 연극에 나오는 선원 차림이었어. 허리띠며 버클이며 땋은 머리며 권총까지 빠짐없이 갖췄지. 둘은 연극 광고지에 흔히 적히는 그 '치열한 결투'를 벌이고 있었어. 우리네 작은 극장에서 흔히 쓰는, 바구니 모양 손잡이가 달린 짤막하고 넓적한 칼 두 자루를 들고 말이야. 작은 아이가 큰 아이를 크게 몰아붙여, 큰 아이는 죽기 직전의 막다른 처지에 몰려 있었지. 그리고 둘을 굽어보는 덩치 크고 묵직한 사내가 탁자 모서리에 걸터앉아 있었어. 그는 칼에서 불꽃을 좀 더 튀기라고 힘주어 다그쳤지. 그렇게만 하면 첫날밤부터 객석을 들썩이게 할 게 틀림없다면서 말이야.\n\n\"빈센트 크럼블스 씨입니다.\" 여관 주인이 자못 공손한 태도로 말했어. \"이쪽이 그 젊은 신사분이고요.\"\n\n빈센트 크럼블스 씨는 고개를 한 번 까딱하며 니콜라스를 맞았어. 로마 황제의 예법과 술친구의 끄덕임 사이쯤 되는 인사였지. 그러고는 주인더러 문을 닫고 나가라고 했어.\n\n\"멋진 한 장면이지요.\" 크럼블스 씨가 니콜라스더러 앞으로 나와 장면을 망치지 말라고 손짓하며 말했어. \"작은 녀석이 큰 녀석을 잡았어요. 큰 녀석이 삼 초 안에 항복하지 않으면 죽은 목숨이지요. 얘들아, 한 번 더 해 봐라.\"\n\n두 결투자는 다시 한판 붙었어. 그리고 칼에서 불꽃이 소나기처럼 쏟아질 때까지 마구 후려쳤지. 크럼블스 씨는 무척 흡족해했어. 그는 이걸 정말 중요한 대목이라 여기는 모양이었지. 결투는 작은 선원과 큰 선원이 번갈아 한 이백 번쯤 후려치는 걸로 시작됐어. 별다른 결판이 안 나다가, 작은 선원이 한쪽 무릎을 꿇으며 베여 쓰러졌지. 하지만 그게 그한테는 아무것도 아니었어. 왼손을 짚어 한쪽 무릎으로 빙빙 몸을 틀면서 죽을힘을 다해 싸웠거든. 그러다 큰 선원이 그의 손에서 칼을 쳐서 떨어뜨렸어. 자, 이쯤 되면 막다른 처지에 몰린 작은 선원이 곧장 항복하고 살려 달라 외칠 거라 짐작하겠지. 그런데 그러기는커녕, 그는 별안간 허리띠에서 커다란 권총을 뽑아 큰 선원의 얼굴에 들이댔어. 큰 선원은 (미처 예상치 못해서) 이에 어찌나 당황했던지, 작은 선원이 칼을 다시 주워 들고 또 시작하게 내버려 뒀지. 그러고는 다시 후려치기가 시작됐어. 양쪽에서 갖가지 멋들어진 칼질이 오갔지. 왼손으로 휘두르는 칼질, 다리 밑으로 넣는 칼질, 오른쪽 어깨 너머로, 왼쪽 어깨 너머로 넘기는 칼질 따위 말이야. 작은 선원이 큰 선원의 다리를 향해 힘껏 베었는데, 제대로 맞았더라면 다리가 싹둑 잘려 나갔을 그 칼질을, 큰 선원이 펄쩍 뛰어넘었어. 그래서 균형을 맞춰 공평하게 하느라, 큰 선원도 똑같이 베었고 작은 선원이 그 칼을 펄쩍 뛰어넘었지. 그러고 나서는 한참 동안 이리저리 피하고, 멜빵이 없어서 흘러내리는 바지를 추켜올리고 했어. 그러다 작은 선원이 (보아하니 분명 의로운 인물이었지. 늘 우위를 차지했으니까) 격렬한 공세를 펴며 큰 선원에게 달려들었어. 큰 선원은 몇 번 헛되이 발버둥 치다 쓰러졌지. 그러고는 작은 선원이 그의 가슴을 발로 밟고 몸을 푹 꿰뚫어 구멍을 내자, 끔찍한 고통 속에 숨을 거두었어.\n\n\"잘만 하면 두 번 앙코르를 받겠다, 얘들아.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"이제 숨 좀 돌리고 옷을 갈아입어라.\"\n\n이 말을 결투자들에게 건넨 뒤, 그는 니콜라스에게 인사했어. 그제야 니콜라스는 크럼블스 씨의 얼굴이 몸집에 딱 어울리게 큼직하다는 걸 보았지. 그는 아랫입술이 무척 두툼했어. 목소리는 쉬어 있었는데, 평소 어지간히 소리를 질러 대는 버릇이 있는 것 같았어. 그리고 새까만 머리는 아주 짧았어. 정수리 가까이까지 거의 밀어 버린 상태였지. 나중에 알게 된 거지만, 어떤 모양이나 무늬의 분장용 가발이든 더 수월하게 쓰려고 그런 거였어.\n\n\"어떻게 보셨습니까, 선생?\" 크럼블스 씨가 물었어.\n\n\"정말 훌륭했어요. 일품이던데요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"저런 아이들은 그리 흔히 못 보실 겁니다.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어.\n\n니콜라스도 맞다고 했어. 그러면서 두 아이가 조금만 더 어울리는 짝이었으면 하고 한마디 보탰지.\n\n\"짝이라니!\" 크럼블스 씨가 외쳤어.\n\n\"제 말은, 둘이 덩치가 좀 더 비슷했더라면 하는 거였어요.\" 니콜라스가 제 뜻을 풀어 설명했어.\n\n\"덩치라니!\" 크럼블스 씨가 되받았어. \"아니, 둘 사이에 한두 자쯤 차이가 나야 그게 결투의 묘미지요. 작은 사람이 큰 사람을 상대로 맞서는 게 아니면, 어떻게 관객의 마음을 제대로 끌어올리겠어요? 적어도 다섯 대 하나는 돼야 하는데, 우리 극단엔 그럴 만큼 일손이 없거든요.\"\n\n\"그렇군요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"죄송합니다. 솔직히 거기까진 미처 생각을 못 했네요.\"\n\n\"그게 핵심입니다.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"나는 모레 포츠머스에서 막을 올려요. 그쪽으로 가신다면 극장에 한번 들러서, 그게 얼마나 먹히는지 보세요.\"\n\n니콜라스는 가능하면 그러겠다고 약속했어. 그러고는 불 가까이 의자를 끌어다 앉아, 곧장 단장과 이야기를 나누기 시작했지. 단장은 무척 수다스럽고 거리낌 없이 말을 늘어놨어. 어쩌면 타고난 성품 때문만이 아니라, 그가 넉넉히 홀짝이는 술과 물, 또 조끼 주머니 속 누르스름한 종이에서 한 움큼씩 집어 들이켜는 코담배 덕분이었는지도 몰라. 그는 조금도 숨김없이 자기 사정을 털어놨어. 그러고는 자기 극단의 장점이며 식구들의 재주를 한참 늘어놓았지. 그 둘 모두에서 넓적칼을 휘두르던 두 아이가 자랑스러운 한몫을 차지했어. 듣자 하니 이튿날 포츠머스에서 여러 단원들이 모이기로 돼 있었어. 아버지와 두 아들은 그리로 가는 길이었지. (정규 공연철 때문이 아니라, 떠돌이 흥행을 다니던 중이었어.) 길퍼드에서 한 차례 공연을 큰 갈채 속에 마치고 오는 길이었지.\n\n\"그쪽으로 가신다고요?\" 단장이 물었어.\n\n\"네… 네.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"네, 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"그 동네는 좀 아세요?\" 단장이 물었어. 자기가 그만큼 속을 터놓았으니, 그만한 신뢰를 받을 자격이 있다고 여기는 모양이었지.\n\"아니요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"한 번도 안 가 보셨어요?\"\n\n\"한 번도요.\"\n\n빈센트 크럼블스 씨는 짤막하게 마른기침을 했어. '터놓고 말 안 할 거면 말고요' 하는 듯한 기침이었지. 그러고는 그 종이에서 코담배를 한 움큼씩 연거푸 집어 들이켰어. 어찌나 많이 집던지, 니콜라스는 그게 다 어디로 들어가는지 어리둥절할 지경이었어.\n\n이러는 동안 크럼블스 씨는 이따금 스마이크를 무척 흥미로운 눈길로 바라봤어. 처음부터 그를 꽤 눈여겨본 듯했지. 스마이크는 어느새 잠이 들어 의자에 앉은 채 꾸벅거리고 있었어.\n\n\"이런 말 해서 미안합니다만,\" 단장이 니콜라스 쪽으로 몸을 기울이며 목소리를 낮춰 말했어. \"선생 친구 얼굴이 정말 일품이로군요!\"\n\n\"가엾은 친구지요!\" 니콜라스가 씁쓸하게 웃으며 말했어. \"좀 더 살이 오르고, 덜 핼쑥했으면 싶은데요.\"\n\n\"살이라니요!\" 단장이 화들짝 놀라 외쳤어. \"그랬다간 영영 망쳐 버려요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하세요?\"\n\n\"생각하다마다요, 선생! 지금 저 모습 그대로면,\" 단장이 제 무릎을 탁 치며 말했어. \"몸에 패드 하나 안 대고, 얼굴에 분칠 한 번 안 해도, 굶주린 역할로는 이 나라에서 둘도 없는 배우가 될 겁니다. 그저 〈로미오와 줄리엣〉의 약제사 역만 그럭저럭 익히게 해서, 코끝에 빨간색을 아주 살짝만 찍어 주면, 무대 앞 오른쪽 진짜 문으로 머리만 내밀어도 단번에 박수 세 차례는 따 놓은 당상이지요.\"\n\n\"전문가의 눈으로 보시는군요.\" 니콜라스가 웃으며 말했어.\n\n\"그럴 만도 하지요.\" 단장이 대꾸했어. \"이 바닥에 발 들인 이래로 그 역할에 그렇게 딱 맞아떨어지는 젊은이는 처음 봅니다. 나로 말할 것 같으면 생후 열여덟 달 때부터 비중 있는 아역을 맡았던 사람이거든요.\"\n\n비프스테이크 푸딩이 나오는 바람에 — 크럼블스 집안의 두 아들도 때맞춰 들어왔지 — 이야기는 다른 데로 흘렀어. 사실 한동안은 아예 말이 끊겼지. 이 두 젊은 신사는 넓적칼 못지않게 능숙하게 나이프와 포크를 놀렸어. 그리고 일행 모두가 어느 쪽 무기 못지않게 입맛이 칼칼하게 서 있던 터라, 저녁상을 다 비울 때까지는 이야기할 새가 없었지.\n\n크럼블스 형제는 마지막 한 입까지 삼키자마자, 어떻게든 참으려는 하품을 거듭 흘리고 팔다리를 늘이며, 잠자리에 들고 싶은 기색을 역력히 드러냈어. 그건 스마이크가 한층 더 뚜렷하게 보여 준 기색이었지. 그는 식사하는 내내 음식을 먹는 그 와중에도 몇 번이나 잠들곤 했거든. 그래서 니콜라스가 이제 그만 자리를 파하자고 제안했어. 하지만 단장은 도무지 들으려 하지 않았지. 새로 사귄 벗에게 펀치 한 사발을 함께하자고 청하는 즐거움을 스스로에게 약속했는데, 만일 거절한다면 아주 인정머리 없는 처사로 여기겠다며 맹세하듯 우겼어.\n\n\"애들은 자게 두지요.\" 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"우리끼리 난롯가에서 오붓하고 아늑하게 한잔합시다.\"\n\n니콜라스는 그다지 잠이 오지 않았어. 사실 너무 마음이 졸여서였지. 그래서 잠깐 망설인 끝에 그 청을 받아들였어. 그리고 어린 크럼블스 형제와 악수를 나눴지. 단장은 단장대로 스마이크에게 더없이 다정한 축복을 내려 주었어. 니콜라스는 난롯가에 그 신사와 마주 앉아, 사발을 비우는 걸 거들기로 했지. 그 사발은 곧 들어왔는데, 보기만 해도 신명 나게 김을 모락모락 피워 올리며, 더없이 구수하고 군침 도는 향을 풍기고 있었어.\n\n하지만 펀치도, 갖가지 이야기를 들려주고 파이프로 담배를 피우다가 코담배로도 들이마시던 그 놀라운 능력의 단장도 소용없었어. 니콜라스는 넋이 나간 채 의기소침해 있었지. 그의 마음은 옛집에 가 있었어. 그러다 지금 처지로 생각이 돌아오면, 앞날의 막막함이 그를 어둡게 짓눌렀어. 아무리 안간힘을 써도 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었지. 정신은 자꾸 딴 데로 흩어졌어. 단장의 목소리는 들렸지만, 무슨 말을 하는지는 귀에 들어오지 않았어. 그러다 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 어느 길고 긴 모험담을 큰 웃음으로 마무리하며, 같은 처지였다면 니콜라스는 어떻게 했겠느냐고 물었어. 니콜라스는 할 수 있는 한 그럴듯하게 사과하는 수밖에 없었지. 그가 늘어놓은 이야기를 자기가 죄다 못 알아들었다고 털어놓으면서 말이야.\n\n\"아, 그래 보이더군요.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"마음이 편치 않으신 거죠. 무슨 일입니까?\"\n\n니콜라스는 그 불쑥 던진 물음에 웃음을 참을 수가 없었어. 하지만 굳이 피해 갈 것도 없다 싶어, 자기를 이 고장까지 데려온 그 목적을 못 이룰까 봐 좀 걱정하던 참이라고 털어놨어.\n\n\"그게 뭔데요?\" 단장이 물었어.\n\n\"나와 이 가엾은 길동무가 먹고살 만한 기본 생계라도 꾸려 갈 일거리를 구하는 거지요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그게 사실입니다. 진작 알아채셨겠지만요. 그러니 차라리 내 입으로 깔끔하게 말씀드리는 게 낫겠다 싶네요.\"\n\n\"포츠머스에 다른 데보다 더 할 일이 있겠어요?\" 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 물었어. 촛불에 파이프 대롱의 봉랍을 녹여서, 새끼손가락으로 다시 굴려 매만지면서 말이야.\n\n\"항구를 떠나는 배가 많을 것 같아서요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"어느 배든 자리 하나를 구해 볼 작정입니다. 거기라면 그래도 먹을 것 마실 것은 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"소금에 절인 고기에 갓 빚은 럼주, 완두콩 푸딩에 왕겨 비스킷이지요.\" 단장이 파이프 불이 꺼지지 않게 한 모금 빨면서, 다시 봉랍 매만지는 일로 돌아가며 말했어.\n\n\"그것보다 못한 일도 많지요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"내 또래에 이런 처지로 자란 젊은이들 못지않게, 나도 고생쯤은 견딜 수 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래야 할 거예요.\" 단장이 말했어. \"배를 탄다면 말이지요. 하지만 못 타실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"왜요?\"\n\n\"숙련된 일꾼을 구할 수 있는데, 어느 선장이나 항해사가 선생을 밥값 한다고 보겠어요?\" 단장이 대답했어. \"거기엔 그런 일꾼이 길거리 굴 껍데기만큼이나 흔하거든요.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말씀이세요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어. 이 예언과, 그걸 내뱉는 자신만만한 말투에 덜컥 겁이 났거든. \"사람이 처음부터 능숙한 뱃사람으로 태어나는 건 아니잖아요. 길러져야 하는 거 아닌가요?\"\n\n빈센트 크럼블스 씨는 고개를 끄덕였어. \"길러져야지요. 하지만 선생 나이엔 아니고, 선생 같은 젊은 신사한테서 길러지는 것도 아니에요.\"\n\n잠시 말이 끊겼어. 니콜라스의 낯빛이 어두워졌지. 그는 처량하게 불을 들여다봤어.\n\n\"선생 같은 풍채와 말솜씨를 지닌 젊은이가 쉽게 시작할 만하고, 거기서 덕을 보며 세상 구경도 할 만한 다른 직업이 떠오르진 않으세요?\" 단장이 물었어.\n\n\"없네요.\" 니콜라스가 고개를 저으며 말했어.\n\n\"그럼 내가 하나 일러 드리지요.\" 크럼블스 씨가 파이프를 불 속에 던지고 목소리를 높이며 말했어. \"무대입니다.\"\n\n\"무대라고요!\" 니콜라스가 거의 그만큼 큰 목소리로 외쳤어.\n\n\"연극이라는 직업 말이에요.\" 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"나도 연극을 업으로 삼고 있고, 내 아내도 연극이 업이고, 내 아이들도 다 연극이 업입니다. 강아지 때부터 평생 연극을 하다 죽은 개도 있었어요. 내 마차 끄는 조랑말도 〈타타르의 티무르〉에 나간답니다. 선생도 무대에 세워 드리지요. 친구분도요. 한마디만 하시면 됩니다. 나는 새 얼굴이 필요하거든요.\"\n\n\"저는 그런 거 아무것도 모르는데요.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어. 이 느닷없는 제안에 그는 숨이 턱 막힐 지경이었거든. \"학교 때 말고는 평생 배역 한 번 맡아 본 적이 없어요.\"\n\n\"선생 걸음걸이와 몸가짐엔 점잖은 희극이 있고, 눈빛엔 풋풋한 비극이 있고, 웃음엔 가볍게 톡톡 튀는 소극이 있어요.\" 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"태어나면서부터 줄곧 무대 조명 말고는 아무것도 생각해 본 적 없는 사람만큼이나 잘 해내실 겁니다.\"\n\n니콜라스는 여관 셈을 치르고 나면 주머니에 남을 자잘한 동전이 얼마 안 된다는 걸 떠올렸어. 그래서 망설였지.\n\n\"선생은 여러모로 우리한테 쓸모가 있어요.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"선생 같은 학식 있는 사람이 상점 진열창에 붙일 광고문을 써 준다고 생각해 보세요. 얼마나 근사하겠습니까.\"\n\n\"음, 그 일이라면 제가 맡을 수 있을 것 같네요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"맡으시고말고요.\" 크럼블스 씨가 대답했어. \"'자세한 내용은 작은 전단지 참조'라고 써 놓고, 그 전단지 하나하나에 책 반 권 분량을 담을 수도 있지요. 작품도 마찬가지예요. 필요할 때마다 우리 극단의 온 역량을 끌어낼 작품을 써 주실 수 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"그건 그렇게 자신은 없는데요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"하지만 가끔 당신네한테 어울릴 만한 걸 끄적여 볼 수는 있을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"새 흥행작을 당장 하나 내봅시다.\" 단장이 말했어. \"어디 보자… 우리 극단만의 특별한 자산… 새롭고 화려한 무대 장치라… 진짜 펌프 하나랑 빨래통 두 개를 어떻게든 집어넣어 주셔야 해요.\"\n\n\"작품 속에다 말인가요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"그렇지요.\" 단장이 대답했어. \"며칠 전 경매에서 싸게 사 뒀는데, 아주 요긴하게 쓰일 겁니다. 그게 런던식이에요. 의상이랑 소품을 먼저 마련해 놓고, 거기 맞춰 작품을 쓰게 하는 거지요. 극장 대부분이 일부러 작가를 따로 둔답니다.\"\n\n\"정말요!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어.\n\n\"아, 그럼요.\" 단장이 말했어. \"흔한 일이에요. 광고지에 한 줄씩 따로 박아 놓으면 아주 보기 좋지요. 진짜 펌프! 화려한 빨래통! 굉장한 볼거리! 혹시 그림 솜씨가 좀 있진 않으세요?\"\n\n\"그건 제 재주가 아니라서요.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"아! 그럼 어쩔 수 없지요.\" 단장이 말했어. \"그림을 그릴 줄 아셨으면, 포스터에 마지막 장면을 큼직한 목판화로 넣을 수 있었거든요. 무대 안쪽 깊이를 죄다 보여 주면서, 한가운데 펌프랑 빨래통을 박아 넣고요. 하지만 못 그리신다니, 어쩔 수 없네요.\"\n\n\"이 일을 다 하면 제가 얼마를 받게 되나요?\" 니콜라스가 잠깐 생각하다 물었어. \"이걸로 먹고살 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"먹고사느냐고요!\" 단장이 말했어. \"왕족처럼 살지요! 선생 봉급에다 친구분 봉급, 거기다 글값까지 합치면… 아, 일주일에 일 파운드는 버실 겁니다!\"\n\n\"설마요!\"\n\n\"정말이에요. 게다가 손님이 줄줄이 들기라도 하면, 그 갑절에 가까운 돈을 버시고요.\"\n\n니콜라스는 어깨를 으쓱했어. 하지만 눈앞엔 영락없는 빈털터리 신세가 기다리고 있었지. 게다가 설령 그가 굶주림과 고생의 끝을 견뎌 낼 굳은 마음을 끌어모은다 한들, 이 의지가지없는 아이를 무엇 하러 구해 냈겠어? 결국 자기가 끌어내 준 그 처지만큼이나 모진 운명을 또 짊어지게 할 거라면 말이야. 자기를 그렇게 못되게 굴어 가장 쓰라린 마음을 들쑤셔 놓은 그 사람과 같은 도시에 있을 때는, 칠십 마일쯤이야 아무것도 아니라고 여기기 쉬웠어. 하지만 지금은 그게 까마득히 멀게만 느껴졌지. 만일 그가 멀리 떠나 있는 동안 어머니나 케이트가 죽기라도 하면 어쩐담?\n\n그는 더 따져 볼 것도 없이, 서둘러 그렇게 하기로 했다고 말했어. 그러고는 빈센트 크럼블스 씨에게 그 약조의 표시로 손을 내밀었지.\n\n# CHAPTER 23\n\n크럼블스 씨는 여관 마구간에 다리 넷 달린 이상한 동물 한 마리를 두고 있었어. 그는 그걸 조랑말이라고 불렀지. 또 정체를 알 수 없는 탈것도 한 대 있었는데, 그건 네 바퀴 사두마차라고 부르더라고. 덕분에 니콜라스는 이튿날 아침, 생각보다 훨씬 편하게 길을 떠날 수 있었어. 단장과 니콜라스가 앞자리를 차지하고, 크럼블스 도련님들과 스마이크는 뒤에 한데 끼어 앉았지. 그리고 그 옆에는 두툼한 방수포로 비를 막아 둔 버드나무 바구니가 있었어. 그 안에는 앞서 말한 어린 신사들의 양날 검이며 권총, 변발 가발, 선원 의상, 그 밖의 직업상 필요한 물건들이 들어 있었지.\n\n조랑말은 길에서 제멋대로 늑장을 부렸어. 그리고 — 아마 연극판에서 받은 교육 탓이었는지 — 이따금 드러눕고 싶어 안달하는 기색을 강하게 보였지. 그래도 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 고삐를 홱홱 당기고 채찍을 휘둘러서 그럭저럭 일으켜 세웠어. 그리고 이 방법이 통하지 않아 동물이 아예 멈춰 서 버리면, 큰아들 크럼블스가 내려서 발길질을 했지. 이런 격려 덕분에 조랑말은 가끔씩 다시 움직이도록 설득당했어. 그래서 (크럼블스 씨가 딱 맞게 말한 대로) 다들 아주 편안하게 덜컹덜컹 나아갔지.\n\n\"이놈이 속은 착한 조랑말이라오.\" 크럼블스 씨가 니콜라스 쪽으로 돌아보며 말했어.\n\n속은 그럴지도 몰라. 하지만 겉은 분명히 아니었지. 털가죽이 거칠대로 거칠고 볼품이라고는 없었거든. 그래서 니콜라스는 그냥, 그럴 만도 하겠다고만 했어.\n\n\"이 조랑말이 돌아다닌 순회 길이 얼마나 많은지 모른다오.\" 크럼블스 씨가 옛정을 생각하며 능숙하게 눈꺼풀을 툭 쳐 주며 말했어. \"이놈도 우리 식구나 다름없지. 어미가 무대에 섰거든.\"\n\n\"그랬어요?\" 니콜라스가 받아 물었어.\n\n\"서커스에서 십사 년 넘게 사과 파이를 먹었지.\" 단장이 말했어. \"권총도 쏘고, 잠잘 땐 나이트캡까지 쓰고 잤다오. 한마디로 저급 희극을 도맡았던 거지. 아비는 무용수였고.\"\n\n\"아비는 좀 이름이 났었나요?\"\n\n\"별로.\" 단장이 말했어. \"오히려 좀 천한 축에 드는 조랑말이었지. 사실 처음에 날품으로 빌려 쓰이던 놈이라, 그 옛 버릇을 끝내 못 고쳤어. 멜로드라마에도 솜씨가 있긴 했는데, 너무 과해서 — 너무 과했지. 어미가 죽고 나선 포트와인 일을 맡았다오.\"\n\n\"포트와인 일이라뇨!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어.\n\n\"광대랑 같이 포트와인을 마시는 거지.\" 단장이 말했어. \"그런데 이놈이 욕심이 많아서, 어느 날 밤 잔의 둥근 부분을 물어뜯어 버렸다가 목이 막혀 버렸어. 그러니 결국 그 천박함이 제 명을 재촉한 셈이지.\"\n\n이 기구한 동물의 후손은 그날의 일과가 진행될수록 점점 더 손이 많이 갔어. 그래서 크럼블스 씨는 이야기를 나눌 틈이 거의 없었지. 덕분에 니콜라스는 한가로이 제 생각에 잠겨 시간을 보낼 수 있었어. 그러다 포츠머스의 도개교에 다다르자 크럼블스 씨가 말을 세웠어.\n\n\"여기서 내립시다.\" 단장이 말했어. \"애들이 이놈을 마구간으로 데려갔다가, 짐을 내 숙소에 갖다 놓을 거요. 당신 짐도 우선은 거기로 가져다 두는 게 좋겠소.\"\n\n빈센트 크럼블스 씨의 친절한 제안에 고맙다고 한 뒤, 니콜라스는 마차에서 폴짝 뛰어내렸어. 그러고는 스마이크에게 팔을 내주고, 단장을 따라 하이 스트리트를 올라 극장으로 향했지. 그토록 낯선 곳에 곧장 들어선다고 생각하니 어지간히 떨리고 불편한 기분이었어.\n\n두 사람은 벽에 붙고 창문에 내걸린 수많은 벽보 곁을 지났어. 거기에는 빈센트 크럼블스 씨, 빈센트 크럼블스 부인, 크럼블스 도련님, P. 크럼블스 도련님, 크럼블스 양의 이름이 아주 큼직한 글씨로 박혀 있었지. 나머지는 죄다 아주 작은 글씨였고. 그러다 마침내 어느 입구로 꺾어 들었는데, 거기서는 오렌지 껍질과 등유 냄새가 진하게 풍겼고, 그 밑으로 톱밥 냄새가 깔려 있었어. 두 사람은 어두운 통로를 더듬어 나아가, 한두 계단을 내려간 뒤, 천 가림막과 물감통이 빚어 놓은 작은 미로를 헤치고 빠져나왔지. 그러자 포츠머스 극장 무대 위로 나왔어.\n\n\"자, 다 왔소.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어.\n\n그리 밝지는 않았어. 니콜라스는 어느새 무대 우측 첫 번째 출입구 가까이에 서 있었는데, 둘레는 텅 빈 벽이며 먼지 낀 배경판, 곰팡이 핀 구름 그림, 두껍게 칠해 댄 휘장, 더러운 바닥뿐이었지. 그는 둘레를 둘러봤어. 천장, 일층석, 박스석, 위층석, 오케스트라석, 온갖 비품과 장식들 — 죄다 거칠고, 썰렁하고, 음침하고, 초라해 보였어.\n\n\"이게 극장이라고요?\" 스마이크가 어리둥절해서 속삭였어. \"저는 환한 불빛에 화려한 곳인 줄 알았는데요.\"\n\n\"맞아, 그렇지.\" 니콜라스도 못지않게 놀라며 대꾸했어. \"하지만 낮엔 아니야, 스마이크 — 낮엔 아니라고.\"\n\n단장의 목소리에 니콜라스는 건물을 더 꼼꼼히 살펴보다 말고 정신을 차렸어. 그쪽을 보니 무대 앞 반대편에, 다리가 흔들거리는 길쭉한 마호가니 작은 탁자가 있었지. 그 앞에는 살집 좋고 풍채 넉넉한 여인이 앉아 있었어. 보아하니 마흔에서 쉰 사이쯤이었지. 빛바랜 비단 망토를 두르고, 보닛은 끈을 손에 쥔 채 늘어뜨리고 있었어. 그리고 머리카락은 — 숱이 어마어마했는데 — 양쪽 관자놀이 위로 큼직하게 화환처럼 땋아 늘어뜨렸더라고.\n\n\"존슨 씨.\" 단장이 말했어 (니콜라스는 뉴먼 노그스가 케닉스 부인과 이야기할 때 지어 준 그 이름을 댔거든). \"빈센트 크럼블스 부인을 소개하리다.\"\n\n\"만나서 반가워요.\" 빈센트 크럼블스 부인이 무덤 속에서 울려 나오는 듯한 목소리로 말했어. \"정말 반가워요. 게다가 우리 극단의 촉망받는 일원으로 맞이하게 되어 더없이 기쁘답니다.\"\n\n부인은 이렇게 말하면서 니콜라스의 손을 잡고 흔들었어. 손이 큼직한 건 보였지만, 이렇게 쇠집게 같은 악력으로 영광을 베풀 줄은 미처 예상하지 못했지.\n\n\"그리고 이쪽은.\" 부인이 스마이크 쪽으로 걸어갔어. 비극 여배우가 무대 지시에 따라 걸음을 옮길 때처럼 말이야. \"이쪽은 또 한 사람이군요. 당신도 환영해요.\"\n\n\"이 친구도 쓸 만하겠지, 여보?\" 단장이 코담배를 한 줌 집으며 말했어.\n\n\"훌륭해요.\" 부인이 대답했어. \"정말 좋은 인재예요.\"\n\n빈센트 크럼블스 부인이 다시 탁자 쪽으로 걸음을 돌렸을 때였어. 어딘가 알 수 없는 입구에서 어린 여자아이 하나가 무대 위로 폴짝 뛰어나왔지. 무릎까지 주름을 잡은 더러운 흰 원피스에, 짧은 바지, 끈 달린 샌들, 흰 스펜서 재킷, 분홍 망사 보닛, 초록 면사포, 그리고 종이로 만 곱슬머리를 하고 있었어. 그 아이는 한 바퀴 피루엣을 돌더니, 공중에서 두 번 발을 부딪치고, 또 한 번 피루엣을 돌았지. 그러고는 반대편 무대 옆을 바라보며 비명을 지르고, 앞으로 뛰어나와 각광 등에서 십오 센티미터쯤 되는 곳에서 멈춰 서더니, 아름다운 공포의 자세로 쓰러졌어. 그때 낡은 황갈색 슬리퍼를 신은 허름한 신사가 한 번에 힘차게 미끄러져 들어왔지. 그러고는 이를 딱딱 부딪치며 지팡이를 사납게 휘둘렀어.\n\n\"지금 '인디언 야만인과 처녀'를 연습하는 중이에요.\" 크럼블스 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"오!\" 단장이 말했어. \"그 짧은 발레 막간극이로군. 아주 좋아, 계속해. 이쪽으로 조금만 와요, 존슨 씨. 그래, 됐어. 자!\"\n\n단장이 시작하라는 신호로 손뼉을 쳤어. 그러자 야만인이 사나워지며 처녀를 향해 미끄러져 갔지. 하지만 처녀는 여섯 번 빙글빙글 돌며 그를 피했고, 마지막 회전 끝에 발끝으로 사뿐히 내려섰어. 이게 야만인에게 좀 인상을 남긴 모양이었어. 야만인은 조금 더 사납게 굴며 처녀를 구석으로 몰아붙이다가, 마음이 누그러지기 시작했거든. 그러고는 오른손 엄지와 네 손가락으로 제 얼굴을 몇 번 쓰다듬었어. 처녀의 아름다움에 감탄했다는 뜻이었지. 이 격정에 사로잡힌 야만인은 제 가슴을 세게 쾅쾅 두드리기 시작했고, 또 죽도록 사랑에 빠졌다는 온갖 표시를 내보였어. 그런데 이게 좀 따분하게 늘어진 탓이었는지, 아마 그래서 처녀가 잠들어 버린 것 같았어. 그게 이유였든 아니든, 처녀는 잠이 들었어. 비탈진 둔덕 위에서 교회처럼 새근새근 깊이 잠들었지. 야만인은 그걸 알아채고는 왼손에 왼쪽 귀를 기대고 고개를 옆으로 끄덕였어. 관계된 모든 이에게 처녀가 정말로 잠들었지, 자는 척하는 게 아니라는 걸 알리려는 거였지. 혼자 남은 야만인은 홀로 춤을 췄어. 그가 막 춤을 멈추자, 처녀가 깨어나 눈을 비비고 둔덕에서 내려와 또 혼자 춤을 췄지. 어찌나 멋진 춤이었던지 야만인은 내내 황홀하게 바라봤어. 춤이 끝나자 그는 옆의 나무에서 무슨 식물 같은 신기한 걸 뜯어 왔는데, 작은 절인 양배추처럼 생긴 거였어. 그걸 처녀에게 내밀었지. 처녀는 처음엔 받지 않으려 했지만, 야만인이 눈물을 흘리자 마음을 누그러뜨렸어. 그러자 야만인은 기뻐서 펄쩍 뛰었지. 이어서 처녀도 그 절인 양배추의 달콤한 냄새에 황홀해져 펄쩍 뛰었어. 그러고는 야만인과 처녀가 격렬하게 함께 춤을 췄어. 마침내 야만인이 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고, 처녀가 그의 다른 쪽 무릎 위에 한 발로 섰지. 이렇게 발레가 끝났어. 그리고 관객들은 즐거운 궁금증에 잠긴 채 남겨졌지. 처녀가 끝내 야만인과 결혼할지, 아니면 제 친구들에게 돌아갈지 말이야.\n\n\"정말 훌륭해.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"브라보!\"\n\n\"브라보!\" 니콜라스도 외쳤어. 뭐든 좋게 보려고 마음먹은 거지. \"아름답습니다!\"\n\n\"이 아이가 말이오.\" 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 처녀를 앞으로 데려오며 말했어. \"이 아이가 바로 그 어린 신동 — 니네타 크럼블스 양이라오.\"\n\n\"따님인가요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"내 딸이지, 내 딸.\" 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 대답했어. \"우리가 가는 곳마다 우상이라오. 이 아이에 대해 영국 거의 모든 마을의 귀족과 신사들한테서 칭찬 편지를 받았다니까.\"\n\n\"그럴 만도 하네요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"타고난 천재가 틀림없어요.\"\n\n\"그야말로 —!\" 크럼블스 씨가 말을 멈췄어. 이 어린 신동을 표현할 만큼 강한 말이 없었던 거지. \"내 말해 두지만 말이오.\" 그가 말했어. \"이 아이의 재능은 도무지 상상도 못 할 정도라오. 직접 봐야만 — 봐야만 — 그나마 어렴풋이라도 알아볼 수 있다오. 자, 어머니한테 가 보거라, 얘야.\"\n\n\"실례지만 몇 살인지 물어봐도 될까요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"물어봐도 되고말고.\" 크럼블스 씨가 대답했어. 자기 말이 곧이곧대로 믿어질지 의심스러울 때 어떤 사람들이 그러듯, 질문한 사람의 얼굴을 똑바로 응시하면서 말이야. \"열 살이라오.\"\n\"그것밖에 안 됐다고요!\"\n\n\"하루도 더 안 됐지.\"\n\n\"아이고!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"정말 놀랍군요.\"\n\n놀랍긴 했어. 이 어린 신동은 키는 작았지만 얼굴은 비교적 나이 들어 보였거든. 게다가 정확히 이 나이로 머물러 있은 지가 — 가장 나이 많은 마을 사람의 기억을 다 더듬을 정도까지는 아니어도 — 확실히 오 년은 족히 됐단 말이지. 하지만 이 아이는 키가 자라지 못하도록 밤마다 늦게까지 깨어 있었고, 갓난아기 때부터 진과 물을 무제한으로 마셔 댔어. 아마 이런 훈련 방식이 어린 신동에게 그 추가적인 신기한 현상들을 만들어 낸 모양이야.\n\n이 짧은 대화가 오가는 동안, 야만인 역을 맡았던 신사가 다가왔어. 발에는 산책용 구두를 신고, 손에는 슬리퍼를 든 채로 말이야. 대화에 끼고 싶은 듯 몇 걸음 앞까지 와 멈췄지. 그러고는 이때다 싶었는지 한마디 끼워 넣었어.\n\n\"저 아이, 재능이 대단하지요!\" 야만인이 크럼블스 양 쪽으로 고갯짓하며 말했어.\n\n니콜라스도 맞장구쳤어.\n\n\"아!\" 배우가 이를 악물고 쉬익 소리를 내며 숨을 들이켰어. \"저 아이는 지방에 있을 애가 아닌데, 아니라고요.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소린가?\" 단장이 물었어.\n\n\"내 말은 말입니다.\" 상대방이 열을 올리며 대답했어. \"저 아이는 시골 무대엔 너무 아까운 인재라, 런던의 큰 극장 가운데 하나에 서든가, 아니면 아예 어디에도 안 서는 게 맞다는 거지요. 게다가 한마디 더 보태자면, 에두르지 않고 말해서, 당신도 아는 어떤 쪽의 시기와 질투만 아니었으면 진작 그렇게 됐을 거란 말입니다. 그건 그렇고, 저를 좀 소개해 주시지요, 크럼블스 씨.\"\n\n\"폴레어 씨라오.\" 단장이 그를 니콜라스에게 소개했어.\n\n\"만나서 반갑소이다.\" 폴레어 씨가 집게손가락으로 모자챙을 건드리고 나서 악수를 청했어. \"새로 들어온 분이라고요?\"\n\n\"보잘것없는 신참이지요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"저런 꼴 본 적 있소?\" 배우가 니콜라스를 한쪽으로 끌고 가며 속삭였어. 마침 크럼블스가 아내에게 말을 걸려고 두 사람 곁을 떠났거든.\n\n\"어떤 꼴 말입니까?\"\n\n폴레어 씨는 자기 무언극 밑천에서 우스꽝스러운 표정 하나를 골라 짓더니, 어깨너머를 가리켰어.\n\n\"설마 그 어린 신동을 말하는 건 아니지요?\"\n\n\"신동이 아니라 사기예요.\" 폴레어 씨가 대답했어. \"자선 학교에 있는, 그저 그만큼 영악한 여자애라도 저것보단 잘하겠소. 저 애는 단장 딸로 태어난 제 팔자에 감사나 해야지.\"\n\n\"단단히 마음에 두고 계신 모양이군요.\" 니콜라스가 웃으며 말했어.\n\n\"그렇소, 정말이지 그럴 만하잖소.\" 폴레어 씨가 니콜라스의 팔에 제 팔을 끼고 무대 위를 왔다 갔다 거닐며 말했어. \"저 조그만 게 밤마다 제일 좋은 자리에 떡하니 올라가서는, 사람들 목구멍에 억지로 밀어 넣어지는 통에 오히려 손님을 쫓아낸단 말이오. 그 사이에 다른 사람들은 그냥 지나쳐지고. 이러니 사람 심사가 안 뒤틀리겠소? 게다가 한 인간이 제 집안 잘난 척에 눈이 멀어서, 제 잇속까지 못 보는 걸 지켜보는 게 어디 보통 일이오? 글쎄, 내 알기로 지난달 어느 날 밤, 내가 하이랜드 플링 추는 걸 보겠다고 사우샘프턴에서 십오 실링 육 펜스가 들어왔단 말이오. 그런데 그 결과가 뭐였소? 그 뒤로 난 그 춤판에 한 번도 못 올라갔소 — 단 한 번도 — 그동안 저 '어린 신동'은 매일 밤 조화 사이로 이를 드러내고 웃어 댔지. 일층석에 손님 다섯에 갓난애 하나, 위층석에 사내애 둘 앞에서 말이오.\"\n\n\"제가 본 바로 미루어 짐작컨대.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"선생은 이 극단에서 아주 값진 단원이 틀림없겠군요.\"\n\n\"오!\" 폴레어 씨가 슬리퍼를 맞부딪쳐 먼지를 털며 대답했어. \"내 솜씨야 꽤 괜찮소 — 적어도 내 분야에선 나만 한 사람도 없을 거요 — 하지만 여기서 맡는 역이라는 게, 발에 분필 대신 납덩이를 매단 꼴이오. 인정도 못 받으면서 족쇄를 차고 춤추는 거나 마찬가지지. 어이, 친구, 잘 지냈나?\"\n\n이 마지막 말이 향한 신사는 가무잡잡한 사내였어. 사실 누르스름한 쪽에 가까웠지. 길고 숱 많은 검은 머리에, 말끔하게 면도를 했는데도 뻣뻣한 수염 자국이 또렷이 드러나 있었고, 구레나룻도 같은 짙은 빛이었어. 나이는 서른을 넘지 않아 보였지만, 처음 보면 훨씬 더 들어 보인다고 여길 사람도 많았을 거야. 무대 분장을 끊임없이 한 탓에 얼굴이 길쭉하고 아주 창백했거든. 그는 체크무늬 셔츠에, 새 금빛 단추를 단 낡은 초록 코트를 입고, 붉고 푸른 굵은 줄무늬 목도리를 두르고, 통 넓은 파란 바지를 입고 있었어. 또 평범한 물푸레나무 지팡이도 들고 있었는데, 쓰임새보다는 폼을 위한 거였지. 갈고리 진 끝을 아래로 향한 채 휘두르다가, 가끔 몇 초쯤 들어 올려서는 펜싱 자세를 잡고 무대 옆 배경이나, 그 순간 꽤 그럴듯한 표적이 되어 준 다른 무언가를 향해 — 살아 있든 죽어 있든 — 찌르는 시늉을 한두 번 하곤 했어.\n\n\"어, 토미.\" 이 신사가 친구를 향해 한 번 찔러 보며 말했어. 친구는 슬리퍼로 능숙하게 막아 냈지. \"뭐 새로운 소식 없나?\"\n\n\"새 얼굴 하나 들어왔지, 그게 다야.\" 폴레어 씨가 니콜라스를 보며 대답했어.\n\n\"예의를 차려야지, 토미, 예의를 차려.\" 다른 신사가 지팡이로 폴레어의 모자 꼭대기를 나무라듯 톡톡 치며 말했어.\n\n\"이쪽은 우리 일급 비극을 맡은 렌빌 씨예요, 존슨 씨.\" 무언극 배우가 말했어.\n\n\"가끔 벽돌이랑 회반죽 영감이 손수 하고 싶어 할 때만 빼고지, 그렇게 덧붙여야겠네, 토미.\" 렌빌 씨가 한마디 했어. \"벽돌이랑 회반죽이 누군지는 아시겠지요?\"\n\n\"실은 모릅니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"우리가 크럼블스를 그렇게 부른다오. 연기 스타일이 좀 무겁고 둔중한 편이라서.\" 렌빌 씨가 말했어. \"하지만 농담이나 하고 있을 처지가 아니지. 여기 열두 장짜리 대사를 받았는데, 내일 밤까지 다 외워야 하거든. 아직 들여다볼 틈도 없었소. 그래도 내가 외우는 건 지독히 빨라서, 그게 그나마 위안이오.\"\n\n이런 생각으로 스스로를 달래며, 렌빌 씨는 코트 주머니에서 기름때 묻고 구깃구깃한 원고를 꺼냈어. 그러고는 친구에게 한 번 더 찌르는 시늉을 하더니, 이리저리 거닐기 시작했지. 혼잣말로 대사를 외면서, 가끔 자기 상상과 본문이 일러 주는 대로 어울리는 동작을 곁들였어.\n\n이때쯤이면 극단원들이 꽤 두루 모여 있었어. 렌빌 씨와 그 친구 토미 말고도, 눈이 약한 호리호리한 젊은 신사가 와 있었지. 그는 풀죽은 연인 역을 맡고 테너 노래를 불렀는데, 코미디 시골뜨기 역과 팔짱을 끼고 들어왔어. 그 시골뜨기 역은 들창코에 입이 크고 얼굴이 넓적하고 눈을 부릅뜬 사내였지. 어린 신동에게 살갑게 구는 건, 누추함의 극치에 이른 술 취한 늙은 신사였어. 그는 차분하고 덕 있는 노인 역을 맡았지. 그리고 크럼블스 부인에게 유난히 비위를 맞추는 건 또 다른 늙은 신사였는데, 그쪽보다는 한결 점잖아 보였어. 그는 성마른 노인 역을 맡았지 — 군대에 조카를 둬서 그 조카더러 상속녀와 결혼하라고 굵은 지팡이를 들고 줄곧 쫓아다니는, 그런 우스운 친구들 말이야. 이들 말고도 거친 외투를 걸친, 떠돌이 같은 인상의 사내가 하나 있었어. 그는 각광 등 앞을 성큼성큼 오가며 정장용 지팡이를 휘두르고, 가상의 관객을 즐겁게 하려는 듯 낮은 소리로 활기차게 떠들어 댔지. 한창때만큼 젊지는 않았고, 몸도 좀 망가져 가는 참이었지만, 어딘가 과장된 점잔이 풍겨서 거들먹거리는 희극의 주인공임을 알 수 있었어. 또 한구석에는 턱이 길쭉하고 눈썹이 짙은 젊은이 서넛이 작게 무리 지어 이야기를 나누고 있었지. 하지만 이들은 그리 중요한 축은 아닌 듯, 아무 눈길도 끌지 못한 채 자기들끼리 웃고 떠들었어.\n\n여자들은 앞서 말한 그 흔들거리는 탁자 둘레에 따로 작은 무리를 이루어 모여 있었어. 거기엔 스네벨리치 양이 있었지 — 그녀는 메들리 댄스부터 맥베스 부인 역까지 못 하는 게 없었고, 게다가 자기 후원 공연 때면 늘 파란 비단 반바지 차림으로 어떤 역이든 하나는 맡았어 — 석탄통처럼 생긴 밀짚 보닛 속 깊은 데서 니콜라스를 힐끔거리면서, 친구 레드룩 양에게 무슨 재미난 이야기를 들려주는 데 푹 빠진 척하고 있었지. 레드룩 양은 바느질거리를 가져와서, 더없이 자연스러운 솜씨로 주름 깃을 만들고 있었어. 또 벨바우니 양도 있었어 — 그녀는 대사 있는 역엔 좀처럼 욕심내지 않고, 보통은 흰 비단 양말을 신은 시동 역으로 나와서 한쪽 다리를 굽히고 서서 관객을 바라보거나, 위풍당당한 비극에서 크럼블스 씨를 따라 들락날락했지 — 그 벨바우니 양이 아름다운 브라바사 양의 곱슬머리를 말아 올리고 있었어. 브라바사 양은 언젠가 판화 견습공한테 '배역 차림으로' 초상을 찍은 적이 있었는데, 그 인쇄본이 그녀의 연례 공연 벽보가 나붙을 때마다 제과점 진열창이며 채소 가게, 대출 도서관, 매표소에 내걸려 팔리곤 했지. 또 렌빌 부인도 있었어. 아주 축 늘어진 보닛에 면사포를 쓰고서, 만약 그녀가 정말로 렌빌 씨를 사랑한다면 바라 마지않을 바로 그런 상태로 말이야. 또 가징기 양도 있었는데, 모조 담비 목도리를 헐겁게 매듭지어 목에 두르고는, 그 양쪽 끝으로 어린 크럼블스를 장난삼아 후려치고 있었어. 마지막으로 그러든 부인이 있었어. 갈색 모직 외투에 비버 가죽 보닛 차림이었지. 그녀는 크럼블스 부인의 집안일을 거들고, 문간에서 입장료를 받고, 여배우들 옷을 입혀 주고, 극장을 쓸고, 마지막 장면에 다들 무대에 나가 있을 땐 프롬프트 대본을 들었어. 또 어떤 비상시에든 외운 적도 없는 아무 역이나 척척 해냈고, 벽보엔 크럼블스 씨가 인쇄로 보기 좋다 싶은 아무 이름이나, 이름이 몇 개든 갖다 붙여 올렸지.\n\n폴레어 씨가 친절하게 이런 자세한 사정을 니콜라스에게 일러 준 뒤, 동료들과 어울리러 가 버렸어. 그리고 사람들에게 일일이 소개해 주는 일은 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 마무리했지. 그는 이 새 배우를 천재와 학식의 신동이라고 공공연히 알렸어.\n\n\"실례지만요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 니콜라스 쪽으로 슬며시 다가오며 말했어. \"혹시 캔터베리에서 무대에 선 적 있으세요?\"\n\n\"없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"제가 캔터베리에서 어떤 신사분을 만난 기억이 나서요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했어. \"그저 잠깐이었지요. 그분이 극단에 들어올 때 저는 떠나는 참이었거든요. 당신하고 어찌나 닮았던지, 같은 사람이 거의 틀림없다 싶었어요.\"\n\n\"저는 지금 처음 뵙는걸요.\" 니콜라스가 합당한 정중함을 다해 대꾸했어. \"분명히 전엔 뵌 적이 없습니다. 봤다면 잊었을 리가 없지요.\"\n\n\"어머, 정말이지 — 그렇게 말씀해 주시니 무척 으쓱하네요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 우아하게 허리를 굽히며 받아쳤어. \"이제 다시 보니, 캔터베리의 그 신사분은 당신하고 눈이 다르네요 — 이런 걸 일일이 눈여겨본다고 저를 무척 어리석다 여기시겠죠, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"천만에요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"어떤 식으로든 당신이 저를 눈여겨봐 주시는데 으쓱하지 않을 리가 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"어머! 남자분들은 어쩜 그리 다들 허영이 넘치는지!\" 스네벨리치 양이 외쳤어. 그러고는 사랑스럽게 당황하더니, 금빛 잠금쇠가 달린 빛바랜 분홍 비단 손가방에서 손수건을 꺼내며 레드룩 양을 불렀어.\n\n\"레드, 얘.\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했어.\n\n\"왜, 무슨 일인데?\" 레드룩 양이 말했어.\n\n\"그 사람 아니야.\"\n\n\"뭐가 아니라는 거야?\"\n\n\"캔터베리 — 무슨 말인지 알잖아. 이리 와 봐! 너한테 할 말이 있어.\"\n\n하지만 레드룩 양은 스네벨리치 양에게 오려 하지 않았어. 그래서 스네벨리치 양이 레드룩 양에게 갈 수밖에 없었지. 그녀는 깡충깡충 뛰는 듯한 걸음으로 갔는데, 그게 어찌나 매력적이던지. 그리고 레드룩 양은 분명 스네벨리치 양이 니콜라스에게 반했다고 놀려 댔어. 장난스레 한참 속닥인 끝에, 스네벨리치 양이 레드룩 양의 손등을 아주 세게 한 대 때리고는, 즐겁게 당황한 채 뒤로 물러났거든.\n\n\"신사 숙녀 여러분.\" 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 종이쪽에 뭔가 적고 나서 말했어. \"내일 열 시에 '필사의 격투'를 연습합시다. 다들 행진 장면에 나오고. '음모'랑 '수단과 방법'은 다들 외워 두었으니, 한 번만 리허설하면 되겠소. 모두 열 시까지 와 주시오.\"\n\n\"모두 열 시.\" 그러든 부인이 둘레를 둘러보며 따라 말했어.\n\n\"월요일 아침엔 새 작품을 읽겠소.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"제목은 아직 안 정해졌지만, 다들 좋은 역을 맡을 거요. 그건 존슨 씨가 챙길 테니.\"\n\n\"엥!\" 니콜라스가 흠칫 놀라며 말했어. \"제가 —\"\n\n\"월요일 아침이오.\" 크럼블스 씨가 가엾은 존슨 씨의 항의를 묻어 버리려고 목소리를 높여 되풀이했어. \"그럼 됐소, 신사 숙녀 여러분.\"\n\n신사 숙녀들은 두 번 말할 것도 없이 자리를 떴어. 그리고 몇 분 만에 극장에는 크럼블스 가족과 니콜라스, 스마이크만 남았지.\n\n\"솔직히 말씀드리자면.\" 니콜라스가 단장을 한쪽으로 데려가며 말했어. \"월요일까지는 준비가 못 될 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"쯧쯧, 무슨 소리.\" 크럼블스 씨가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"하지만 정말 못 합니다.\" 니콜라스가 받았어. \"제 창작력이 이런 요구에 익숙지 않아서요. 아니면 어쩌면 만들어 낼 수도 —\"\n\n\"창작력! 그게 도대체 이거랑 무슨 상관이오!\" 단장이 다급히 외쳤어.\n\n\"전부 다 상관있지요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"아무 상관도 없소, 선생.\" 단장이 대놓고 못 참겠다는 듯 받아쳤어. \"프랑스어 할 줄 아시오?\"\n\n\"아주 잘합니다.\"\n\n\"아주 좋아.\" 단장이 탁자 서랍을 열어 그 안에서 둘둘 만 종이 한 뭉치를 꺼내 니콜라스에게 주며 말했어. \"자! 이걸 영어로 옮기고, 표지에 당신 이름만 적어 넣으면 되오. 빌어먹을.\" 크럼블스 씨가 화난 듯 말했어. \"내가 노상 말해 왔잖소. 우리 극단엔 그 언어를 통달한 남자나 여자가 아니면 두지 않겠다고. 그래야 원본에서 직접 배워서, 영어로 공연하고, 이 온갖 수고와 비용을 다 덜 수 있을 거 아니오.\"\n\n니콜라스는 웃으며 그 희곡을 주머니에 넣었어.\n\n\"숙소는 어떻게 할 거요?\" 크럼블스 씨가 물었어.\n\n니콜라스는 한 주쯤은 일층석에 접이식 침대를 놓는 게 더없이 편하겠다는 생각을 떨칠 수 없었지만, 그저 거기까진 미처 생각해 보지 못했다고만 했어.\n\n\"그럼 나랑 같이 갑시다.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"저녁 먹고 나서 우리 애들이 따라가서, 가장 괜찮을 만한 데를 알려 줄 테니.\"\n\n거절할 수 없는 제안이었어. 니콜라스와 크럼블스 씨가 크럼블스 부인에게 한쪽씩 팔을 내주고, 위풍당당한 행렬을 이루어 거리를 올라갔지. 스마이크와 아이들과 신동은 지름길로 집에 갔고, 그러든 부인은 매표소에 남아 차게 식은 아일랜드식 스튜와 포터 맥주 한 파인트를 들기로 했어.\n\n크럼블스 부인은 마치 곧장 처형장으로 끌려가는 사람처럼 보도를 밟았어. 결백하다는 가슴 벅찬 자각과, 오직 미덕만이 불어넣는 그 영웅적 의연함을 품고서 말이야. 반대로 크럼블스 씨는 잔인무도한 폭군의 표정과 걸음걸이를 취했지. 하지만 두 사람 다 지나가는 사람들 사이에서 적잖은 눈길을 끌었어. 그리고 \"크럼블스 부부다!\" 하는 속삭임이 들리거나, 어린 사내애가 얼굴을 빤히 보려고 되돌아 달려오면, 그들의 근엄한 표정이 스르르 풀렸어. 그게 바로 인기라는 걸 느꼈거든.\n\n크럼블스 씨는 세인트 토머스 거리, 벌프라는 도선사의 집에 살았어. 벌프는 배처럼 초록색 문을 자랑스레 달아 두었는데, 창틀도 똑같은 색이었지. 또 응접실 벽난로 선반에는 물에 빠져 죽은 사람의 새끼손가락을 다른 바다 관련 물건이며 기이한 자연물들과 함께 올려 두었어. 게다가 놋쇠 문고리, 놋쇠 문패, 놋쇠 초인종 손잡이도 내걸었는데, 죄다 아주 반짝반짝 윤이 났지. 뒷마당에는 꼭대기에 풍향계를 단 돛대까지 세워 두었어.\n\n\"어서 오세요.\" 크럼블스 부인이 이 층의 활처럼 둥근 창이 난 앞방에 다다르자, 니콜라스 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했어.\n\n니콜라스는 고개 숙여 감사를 표했어. 그리고 식탁보가 깔려 있는 걸 보고 정말이지 반가웠지.\n\n\"양 어깨살에 양파 소스밖에 없어요.\" 크럼블스 부인이 그 납골당 같은 목소리로 말했어. \"하지만 변변찮은 저녁이나마 함께 드시기를 청합니다.\"\n\n\"정말 친절하시군요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"남김없이 잘 먹겠습니다.\"\n\n\"빈센트.\" 크럼블스 부인이 말했어. \"지금 몇 시지요?\"\n\n\"저녁 시간 오 분 지났소.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어.\n\n크럼블스 부인이 종을 울렸어. \"양고기와 양파 소스를 등장시키세요.\"\n\n벌프 씨네 하숙인들 시중을 드는 하녀가 사라졌다가, 잠깐 사이에 그 잔칫상을 들고 다시 나타났어. 니콜라스와 어린 신동이 펨브로크 식탁을 사이에 두고 마주 앉았고, 스마이크와 크럼블스 도련님들은 소파 겸 침대에서 저녁을 먹었지.\n\n\"여기 사람들은 연극을 무척 좋아하나요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"아니오.\" 크럼블스 씨가 고개를 저으며 대답했어. \"택도 없지 — 어림도 없어.\"\n\n\"가엾기도 하지.\" 크럼블스 부인이 한마디 했어.\n\n\"저도 그래요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"제대로 된 연극 공연에 흥미가 없다니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"흥미라곤 도무지 없다오, 선생.\" 크럼블스 씨가 받았어. \"작년에 신동의 후원 공연이 있었지요. 그날 우리 애가 자기 인기 배역을 셋이나 다시 선보였고, 또 자기가 처음 맡았던 그대로 '요정 호저' 역에도 나왔는데, 그날 극장 수입이 사 파운드 십이 실링밖에 안 됐다오.\"\n\n\"그게 가능한 일인가요?\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어.\n\n\"그중 이 파운드는 외상이었고요, 아빠.\" 신동이 말했어.\n\n\"그중 이 파운드는 외상이었지.\" 크럼블스 씨가 따라 말했어. \"크럼블스 부인 본인도 손가락으로 셀 만큼 적은 관객 앞에서 연기한 적이 있다오.\"\n\n\"그래도 관객들이 늘 잘 받아 주잖아요, 빈센트.\" 단장의 아내가 말했어.\n\n\"좋은 연기, 정말 좋은 연기, 제대로 된 진짜를 보여 주면 관객은 대개 그렇기 마련이지.\" 크럼블스 씨가 힘주어 대답했어.\n\n\"부인은 레슨도 하시나요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"하지요.\" 크럼블스 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"여기선 가르칠 사람이 없겠군요?\"\n\n\"있긴 했어요.\" 크럼블스 부인이 말했어. \"여기서 제자를 받은 적이 있지요. 선박 식료품 상인의 딸을 가르쳤거든요. 그런데 알고 보니 그 아이가 저한테 처음 왔을 때 이미 정신이 온전치 않았더라고요. 그런 상태로 찾아오다니 정말 별난 일이지요.\"\n\n거기엔 그리 확신이 서지 않았던 니콜라스는, 잠자코 있는 게 상책이다 싶었어.\n\n\"가만있자.\" 저녁을 먹고 나서 단장이 골똘히 궁리하며 말했어. \"신동이랑 같이 하는 자그마하고 괜찮은 역을 해 보겠소?\"\n\n\"정말 친절하시네요.\" 니콜라스가 다급히 대답했어. \"하지만 처음엔 제 또래 사람이랑 하는 게 나을 것 같습니다. 혹시 제가 서툴게 굴까 봐서요. 그래야 좀 더 편할 듯해서요.\"\n\n\"그도 그렇군.\" 단장이 말했어. \"그럴 수도 있겠소. 그러다 차차 신동을 받쳐 주면 되지, 안 그렇소.\"\n\n\"물론입니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. 속으로는 그 영광을 누리게 되는 날이 아주아주 멀기를 간절히 바라면서 말이야.\n\n\"그럼 이렇게 합시다.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"그 작품을 끝내고 나면 로미오를 연구하시오 — 그건 그렇고, 펌프랑 물통 던지는 장면도 잊지 말고 넣고 — 줄리엣은 스네벨리치 양, 늙은 그러든이 유모 역. 그래, 그게 아주 좋겠군. 로버 역도 — 하는 김에 로버 역도 익혀 두면 되겠고, 또 카시오, 제레미 디들러도. 금방 해치울 수 있다오. 역들이 서로 워낙 도움이 되니까. 자, 여기 있소, 큐 사인까지 다 적혀 있는 걸로.\"\n\n크럼블스 씨는 이렇게 서둘러 두루뭉술한 지시를 내리며, 작은 책 여러 권을 니콜라스의 머뭇거리는 손에 떠안겼어. 그러고는 맏아들더러 같이 가서 숙소 구할 데를 알려 주라 이르고는, 니콜라스의 손을 흔들며 잘 자라고 인사했지.\n\n포츠머스에는 가구 딸린 안락한 셋방이 모자라지 않았어. 아주 빠듯한 주머니 사정에 맞는 곳을 찾기도 어렵지 않았지. 그런데 앞쪽은 너무 좋았고, 뒤쪽은 너무 형편없었어. 두 사람은 숱한 집에 들어갔다가 마음에 안 들어 도로 나오기를 거듭했지. 그러다 니콜라스는 결국 극장에서 하룻밤 지내게 해 달라고 부탁해야 하는 거 아닌가 진지하게 생각하기 시작했어.\n\n하지만 마침내 두 사람은 작은 방 두 칸을 우연히 찾아냈어. 세 층 계단을 올라간 곳, 아니 정확히는 두 층 계단에 사다리 하나를 더 올라간 곳이었지. 담배 가게 위층이었고, 조선소로 내려가는 더러운 거리, 코먼 하드에 있었어. 니콜라스는 이 방을 빌렸어. 한 주치 방세를 미리 내라는 소리를 듣지 않은 게 그저 더없이 다행스러웠지.\n\n\"자! 우리 짐을 내려놓자, 스마이크.\" 어린 크럼블스를 아래층까지 바래다준 뒤 니콜라스가 말했어. \"참 묘한 시절에 들어섰구나. 끝이 어떻게 될지는 하느님만 아실 테고. 하지만 이 사흘 동안 벌어진 일들로 지쳤으니, 생각은 내일로 미뤄야겠다 — 미룰 수 있다면 말이지.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 24\n\n니콜라스는 아침 일찍 일어났어. 그런데 옷을 채 입기도 전에 계단을 올라오는 발소리가 들렸지. 곧이어 무언극 배우 폴레어 씨와 비극 배우 렌빌 씨의 목소리가 들려왔어.\n\n\"집주인! 집주인! 집주인 어디 있소!\" 폴레어 씨가 외쳤어.\n\n\"여어, 이봐! 안에 있나!\" 렌빌 씨가 굵은 목소리로 말했어.\n\n'이 사람들 정말!' 니콜라스는 속으로 생각했어. '아침을 같이 먹으러 온 모양이군. 잠깐만 기다려요. 곧 문을 열어 줄 테니.'\n\n두 사람은 서두르지 말라고 했어. 그러고는 기다리는 동안 심심풀이로, 그 좁디좁은 층계참에서 지팡이를 휘두르며 펜싱 시합을 벌였지. 아래층에 사는 다른 하숙인들이 이루 말할 수 없이 불편해할 정도였어.\n\n\"자, 들어와요.\" 단장을 마친 니콜라스가 말했어. \"제발, 끔찍한 그 소동 좀 밖에서 벌이지 말고요.\"\n\n\"여기 참 아늑한 작은 방이로군.\" 렌빌 씨가 앞방으로 들어서며 말했어. 그런데 다 들어서기도 전에 모자부터 벗어야 했지. \"기막히게 아늑해.\"\n\n\"그런 걸 따지는 사람한테는 좀 지나치게 아늑할지도 모르죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"물론, 의자에서 꼼짝 안 하고도 천장이든 바닥이든 방 어느 쪽이든 손만 뻗으면 다 닿으니 엄청 편하긴 하죠. 하지만 그런 편리함은 더없이 비좁은 방에서나 누릴 수 있는 거잖아요.\"\n\n\"혼자 사는 남자한테는 조금도 답답하지 않아.\" 렌빌 씨가 받았어. \"그러고 보니 생각났는데, 존슨 씨, 내 아내 말이오. 이번 자네 작품에서 좋은 역 하나 맡았으면 좋겠는데.\"\n\n\"어젯밤에 프랑스어 원본을 훑어봤어요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"꽤 좋아 보이던데요.\"\n\n\"나한테는 뭘 해 줄 셈인가, 친구?\" 렌빌 씨가 지팡이로 시들시들 타는 불을 쑤시더니, 그 끝을 코트 자락에 닦으며 물었어. \"거칠게 으르렁대는 그런 역할이라도 있나?\"\n\n\"아내와 아이를 집에서 내쫓는 거예요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그리고 분노와 질투에 휩싸여, 서재에서 큰아들을 칼로 찌르고요.\"\n\n\"내가 그런다고!\" 렌빌 씨가 소리쳤어. \"그거 아주 괜찮은 역이군.\"\n\n\"그 뒤에,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"마지막 막까지 죄책감에 시달리다가, 결국 스스로 목숨을 끊기로 마음먹어요. 그런데 막 권총을 머리에 갖다 대는 순간, 시계가 울려요. 열 시를 알리는 종소리가요.\"\n\n\"알겠어.\" 렌빌 씨가 외쳤어. \"아주 좋아.\"\n\n\"멈칫하는 거예요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"어릴 적에 들었던 열 시 종소리가 떠오르는 거죠. 권총이 손에서 떨어지고, 당신은 무너져 내리며 울음을 터뜨려요. 그러고는 그 뒤로 영원히 착하고 모범적인 사람이 되는 거죠.\"\n\n\"최고로군!\" 렌빌 씨가 말했어. \"그건 확실한 패야, 확실한 패. 그런 자연스러운 감동으로 막을 내리면, 대성공이 따 놓은 당상이지.\"\n\n\"나한테 좋은 역은 없나?\" 폴레어 씨가 걱정스레 물었어.\n\n\"어디 보자.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"당신은 충직하고 헌신적인 하인을 맡아요. 아내와 아이와 함께 집에서 쫓겨나죠.\"\n\n\"늘 그 지긋지긋한 천재 신동이랑 짝지어진다니까.\" 폴레어 씨가 한숨을 쉬었어. \"그래서 우리는 허름한 하숙집으로 가고, 거기서 나는 품삯도 안 받겠다며 감상적인 소리나 늘어놓겠지?\"\n\n\"뭐, 그렇죠.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그게 작품 줄거리니까요.\"\n\n\"알다시피 나는 무슨 춤이든 한 번은 춰야 해.\" 폴레어 씨가 말했어. \"신동을 위해서도 춤을 하나 넣어야 할 테니, 차라리 둘이 추는 춤으로 만드는 게 좋을 거야. 그래야 시간도 아끼고.\"\n\n\"그것보다 쉬운 게 어디 있나.\" 렌빌 씨가 젊은 극작가의 난처한 표정을 보고 말했어.\n\n\"솔직히 그걸 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠는데요.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"아니, 뻔하지 않나?\" 렌빌 씨가 따지듯 말했어. \"이런, 그 방법을 모를 사람이 어디 있겠어? 자네 정말 놀라운 사람이군! 곤경에 빠진 부인이랑 어린아이랑 헌신적인 하인을 그 허름한 하숙집에 들여보내잖아? 그렇지? 자, 봐. 곤경에 빠진 부인이 의자에 푹 주저앉아서 손수건에 얼굴을 파묻어. '엄마, 왜 우세요?' 아이가 말하지. '엄마, 울지 마세요. 안 그러면 저도 울어요!' '저도요!' 충직한 하인이 팔로 눈을 비비며 말해. '사랑하는 엄마, 어떻게 하면 기운을 차리시겠어요?' 어린아이가 말하지. '그래, 우리가 뭘 할 수 있을까?' 충직한 하인이 말해. '오, 피에르!' 곤경에 빠진 부인이 말하지. '이 괴로운 생각들을 떨쳐 버릴 수만 있다면.' '해 보세요, 부인. 해 보세요.' 충직한 하인이 말해. '기운을 내세요, 부인. 즐거운 일을 해 보세요.' '그래야지.' 부인이 말해. '꿋꿋하게 견디는 법을 배워야지. 충직한 친구여, 그 춤을 기억하나? 행복했던 시절에 자네가 이 사랑스러운 천사와 함께 추던 그 춤 말일세. 그땐 그 춤을 보면 늘 마음이 가라앉았지. 오! 죽기 전에 한 번만 더 보고 싶구나!' 자, 바로 그거야. 악단한테 신호를 주는 거지, '죽기 전에'에서 말이야. 그럼 둘이 춤을 추는 거야. 그게 정석이지. 안 그래, 토미?\"\n\n\"바로 그거야.\" 폴레어 씨가 대답했어. \"곤경에 빠진 부인이 옛 추억에 북받쳐서 춤이 끝날 때 기절하고, 자네는 한 폭의 그림 같은 장면으로 마무리하는 거지.\"\n\n이런저런 가르침을 얻었는데, 다 두 배우가 몸소 겪어서 터득한 것들이었지. 니콜라스는 기꺼이 자기가 차릴 수 있는 가장 좋은 아침을 대접했어. 그리고 마침내 두 사람을 보내고 나서야 자기 일에 매달렸지. 그 일이 처음 생각했던 것보다 훨씬 쉽다는 걸 알고는 영 싫지 않았어. 그날 하루 종일 열심히 일했고, 저녁이 되어서야 방을 나서 극장으로 내려갔어. 스마이크는 다른 배우와 함께 반란군 무리 역을 맡아, 니콜라스보다 먼저 극장에 가 있었지.\n\n극장에 와 보니 사람들이 어찌나 달라졌는지, 니콜라스는 누가 누군지 거의 알아보지 못했어. 가짜 머리, 가짜 분칠, 가짜 종아리, 가짜 근육까지, 다들 완전히 딴사람이 되어 있었거든. 렌빌 씨는 더없이 균형 잡힌 몸매를 뽐내는 혈기왕성한 전사가 되어 있었어. 크럼블스 씨는 그 큼지막한 얼굴을 새까만 머리카락으로 잔뜩 가리고, 더없이 위풍당당한 하일랜드 무법자로 변해 있었지. 늙은 신사 한 명은 간수가, 다른 한 명은 위엄 있는 노족장이 되었어. 익살스러운 시골뜨기는 용맹무쌍한 데다 유머까지 한 줌 곁들인 싸움꾼이 되었고, 어린 크럼블스 형제는 저마다 어엿한 왕자가 되었어. 그리고 풀죽은 연인 역의 배우는 절망에 빠진 포로가 되었지. 3막을 위해 화려한 연회상도 차려져 있었어. 마분지 꽃병 두 개, 비스킷 한 접시, 검은 병 하나, 식초 병 하나로 이루어진 상이었지. 요컨대, 모든 것이 더없이 호화롭고 빈틈없이 준비되어 있었어.\n\n니콜라스는 막을 등진 채 서 있었어. 한편으로는 첫 장면을 바라보고 있었지. 고딕풍 아치문이었는데, 크럼블스 씨보다 한 육십 센티미터쯤 낮았어. 크럼블스 씨가 그 문으로 처음 등장할 참이었지. 또 한편으로는 위층 관람석에서 견과를 까먹고 있는 두 사람의 소리에 귀를 기울이며, 저 둘이 관객의 전부인가 하고 궁금해하고 있었어. 그때 단장이 직접 친근하게 다가와 말을 걸었지.\n\n\"오늘 밤 객석은 둘러봤나?\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"아니요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"아직요. 이제 연극을 보러 가려고요.\"\n\n\"표가 꽤 잘 팔렸어.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"가운데 앞자리 네 개에다, 무대 옆 특별석을 통째로 팔았지.\"\n\n\"오, 정말요!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"어느 가족인가 보죠?\"\n\n\"그래.\" 크럼블스 씨가 대답했어. \"그래. 그게 참 가슴 뭉클한 일이야. 그 집 아이가 여섯인데, 신동이 출연하지 않으면 절대 안 온단 말이지.\"\n\n사실 어떤 일행이든, 가족이든 누구든, 신동이 출연하지 않는 밤에 극장을 찾기란 어려운 일이었어. 그 아이는 매일 밤 한 역을, 흔하게는 두세 역을 맡았으니까. 하지만 니콜라스는 아버지의 마음에 공감해서, 이 사소한 사정은 입에 올리지 않았어. 그래서 크럼블스 씨는 방해받지 않고 계속 떠들 수 있었지.\n\n\"여섯이지.\" 그가 말했어. \"아빠 엄마까지 여덟, 이모까지 아홉, 가정교사까지 열, 할아버지 할머니까지 열둘이야. 거기다 하인도 하나 있는데, 그놈은 밖에 서서 오렌지 한 자루랑 구운 빵 띄운 물 한 주전자를 들고, 특별석 문에 난 작은 유리창으로 공짜로 연극을 본단 말이지. 일 기니로 그 정도면 싼 거야. 특별석을 잡으니 그만큼 이득이거든.\"\n\n\"그렇게 많이 들여보내시는 게 의외네요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"어쩔 도리가 없어.\" 크럼블스 씨가 대답했어. \"시골에서는 늘 그러려니 하거든. 아이가 여섯이면, 무릎에 앉히려고 어른 여섯이 따라오는 거지. 가족 특별석은 늘 두 배로 잡힌단 말이야. 그루든, 악단 들여보내!\"\n\n그 쓸모 있는 여자가 시키는 대로 했어. 그러자 곧 바이올린 세 대를 조율하는 소리가 들려왔지. 관객의 인내심이 도저히 더는 못 견디겠다 싶을 때까지 이 작업을 질질 끌다가, 종을 한 번 더 흔들어 멈췄어. 그게 본격적으로 시작하라는 신호였지. 그러자 악단이 인기 있는 곡들을 이것저것 연주하기 시작했어. 본의 아닌 변주를 곁들여서 말이야.\n\n남자 배우들이 보여 준 멋진 변신에 니콜라스가 놀랐다면, 여자 배우들의 변신은 더더욱 놀라웠어. 단장의 특별석 한쪽 아늑한 구석에서, 니콜라스는 금실로 단을 두른 새하얀 모슬린을 차려입고 한껏 빛나는 스네벨리치 양을 바라봤어. 또 무법자 아내의 위엄을 한껏 갖춘 크럼블스 부인과, 스네벨리치 양의 절친한 친구로서 더없는 상냥함을 풍기는 브라바사 양도 봤지. 그리고 시동 역의 새하얀 비단옷을 입고 사방을 누비며, 모두를 위해 살고 죽겠다고 맹세하는 벨바우니 양도 봤어. 니콜라스는 감탄을 참지 못해 큰 박수를 보냈고, 장면 하나하나에 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울였어. 줄거리는 정말 흥미진진했지. 어느 시대, 어느 민족, 어느 나라의 이야기도 아니었는데, 그래서 오히려 더 즐거웠는지도 몰라. 아무도 미리 알던 정보로는 이 이야기가 어떻게 흘러갈지 손톱만큼도 짐작할 수 없었으니까. 어떤 무법자가 어딘가에서 무언가를 멋지게 해냈어. 그러고는 함성과 바이올린 소리에 맞춰 의기양양하게 집으로 돌아와 아내를 맞이하지. 그 아내는 사내대장부 같은 성격이었는데, 자기 아버지의 뼈 이야기를 줄곧 늘어놓았어. 그 뼈가 아직 묻히지 못한 모양이었지. 그게 노신사 본인의 별난 취향 때문인지, 아니면 친척들의 괘씸한 무관심 탓인지는 분명치 않았어. 이 무법자의 아내는 어찌어찌해서, 저 멀리 성에 사는 한 족장과 얽히게 돼. 그 족장은 등장인물 여럿의 아버지였는데, 정작 누가 자기 자식인지는 정확히 몰랐어. 자기 성에서 키운 아이들이 진짜 자식인지 가짜 자식인지조차 확신하지 못했지. 그는 오히려 가짜 자식이라는 쪽으로 마음이 기울어, 불안한 나머지 연회를 열어 마음을 달랬어. 그 엄숙한 자리에서 망토를 두른 누군가가 \"조심하라!\" 하고 외쳤지. 그게 누군지는 아무도 몰랐어. 관객만 빼고 말이야. 사실 그건 바로 그 무법자였어. 까닭은 밝혀지지 않았지만 거기 와 있었지. 어쩌면 은수저를 노리고 왔는지도 몰라. 그리고 절망에 빠진 포로와 스네벨리치 양 사이, 또 익살스러운 싸움꾼과 브라바사 양 사이에 사랑 이야기가 펼쳐지는 즐겁고 작은 깜짝 장면도 있었어. 그뿐만 아니라 렌빌 씨는 어둠 속에서 목을 베러 다니는 아주 비극적인 장면을 여러 번 연기했어. 그런데 그 시도는 익살스러운 싸움꾼의 솜씨와 용기에 번번이 좌절됐지. 이 싸움꾼은 극 내내 무슨 말이든 다 엿들었거든. 거기에 스네벨리치 양의 대담함도 한몫했어. 그녀는 몸에 딱 붙는 옷을 입고, 다과 바구니 하나와 어두운 등불 하나를 들고 포로가 된 연인의 감옥을 찾아갔지. 마침내 밝혀진 사실은, 그 족장이 바로 무법자 장인의 뼈를 그토록 함부로 다룬 장본인이라는 거였어. 그 까닭에 무법자의 아내는 그를 죽이려고 그의 성을 찾아갔어. 그러다 어두운 방에 들어가게 됐지. 거기서 한참을 어둠 속에서 더듬거리다가, 모두가 서로를 붙잡고는 엉뚱한 다른 사람으로 착각하는 거야. 그 바람에 엄청난 혼란이 벌어졌어. 권총질도 좀 있었고, 목숨을 잃기도 했고, 횃불도 등장했지. 그 뒤에 족장이 앞으로 나와, 알겠다는 표정으로 이제 자기 자식들에 관해 다 알았다고, 안에 들어가면 다 말해 주겠다고 말했어. 그러고는 이만한 자리에서 젊은이들을 결혼시키는 것보다 더 알맞은 때가 없다고 했지. 그래서 그는 두 사람의 손을 맞잡아 주었어. 지칠 줄 모르는 시동도 흔쾌히 동의했지. 그 시동은 살아남은 유일한 다른 사람이었는데, 모자로 하늘을 가리키고 오른손으로 땅을 가리켰어. 그렇게 축복을 빌며 막을 내리라는 신호를 줬지. 그러자 막이 내렸어. 우레와 같은 박수 속에서 말이야.\n\n\"어땠나?\" 니콜라스가 다시 무대 뒤로 돌아가자 크럼블스 씨가 물었어. 크럼블스 씨는 얼굴이 시뻘겋게 달아오르고 땀범벅이었어. 무법자 역이란 게 워낙 목청껏 소리를 질러 대야 하는 거였거든.\n\n\"정말 훌륭했어요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"특히 스네벨리치 양이 유난히 좋았어요.\"\n\n\"그 애는 천재야.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"정말 천재라니까, 저 아이는. 그건 그렇고, 자네 그 작품을 그 애의 자선 공연 밤에 올릴까 생각 중이었어.\"\n\n\"언제요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"그 애가 예약 공연을 하는 밤 말이야. 자선 공연 밤인데, 그 애 친구들이랑 후원자들이 연극을 예약하는 거지.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"아! 알겠어요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"있잖아,\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"그런 자리에서는 분명 성공할 거야. 설령 우리 기대만큼 잘 안 풀린다 해도, 그건 그 애가 떠안을 위험이지 우리 위험이 아니거든.\"\n\n\"단장님 위험이겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"내 위험이라고 했잖나, 안 그래?\" 크럼블스 씨가 대꾸했어. \"다음다음 주 월요일이야. 어떤가? 그때까지 작품도 다 끝내고, 연인 역도 틀림없이 다 외울 수 있을 거야. 그것도 한참 전에 말이지.\"\n\n\"한참 전이라곤 못 하겠는데요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"하지만 그때까지는 준비를 마치겠다고 약속할 수 있을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"아주 좋아.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말을 이었어. \"그럼 그렇게 정한 걸로 하지. 자, 한 가지 더 부탁할 게 있는데. 이런 자리에는 조그만, 뭐랄까, 조그만 표 모으는 일이 따라온단 말이지.\"\n\n\"후원자들 사이에서요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"후원자들 사이에서지. 사실은 말이야, 스네벨리치가 이 동네에서 예약 공연을 워낙 많이 해서, 이제 끌어들일 거리가 필요해. 시어머니가 죽었을 때도 예약 공연을 했고, 삼촌이 죽었을 때도 했지. 게다가 나랑 크럼블스 부인도 신동의 생일 기념일이며 우리 결혼기념일이며, 그런 갖가지 날에 예약 공연을 했단 말이야. 그러니 사실 이제 괜찮은 명분 하나 잡기가 영 어렵단 말이지. 자, 이 가엾은 아가씨를 좀 도와주지 않겠나, 존슨 씨?\" 크럼블스 씨가 북 위에 앉아 코담배를 한 줌 크게 집으며 말했어. 그러고는 니콜라스의 얼굴을 가만히 들여다봤어.\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요?\" 니콜라스가 되물었어.\n\n\"내일 아침에 반 시간만 내서, 그 애랑 같이 주요 인사 한두 명의 집에 들러 줄 수 없겠나?\" 단장이 설득하는 어조로 나직이 말했어.\n\n\"이런, 어쩌죠.\" 니콜라스가 영 내키지 않는 기색으로 말했어. \"그건 정말 하고 싶지 않은데요.\"\n\n\"아이도 같이 갈 거야.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"그 얘기가 나오자마자, 내가 아이도 가도 좋다고 허락했지. 조금도 점잖지 못한 일은 없을 거야. 스네벨리치 양은 그야말로 명예의 화신이거든. 그게 큰 도움이 될 거야. 런던에서 온 신사, 새 작품의 작가, 새 작품의 배우, 무대에 처음 서는 사람이라니, 그러면 큰 예약 공연으로 이어질 거야, 존슨 씨.\"\n\n\"누구의 앞날에든 찬물을 끼얹게 되어 정말 미안해요. 더구나 숙녀분의 일이라면 더더욱요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"하지만 정말이지 그 표 모으는 무리에 끼는 건 단호히 거절해야겠어요.\"\n\n\"존슨 씨가 뭐라고 하나요, 빈센트?\" 귓가에서 목소리가 들려왔어. 니콜라스가 돌아보니, 크럼블스 부인과 스네벨리치 양 본인이 그의 뒤에 서 있었지.\n\n\"뭔가 못마땅한 게 있다는구려, 여보.\" 크럼블스 씨가 니콜라스를 보며 대답했어.\n\n\"못마땅하다고요!\" 크럼블스 부인이 외쳤어. \"그럴 리가 있나요?\"\n\n\"오, 제발 아니라고 해 주세요!\" 스네벨리치 양이 외쳤어. \"설마 그렇게 모질지는 않으시겠죠. 오, 이를 어째! 그래, 내가, 이런 일을 다 겪다니, 그렇게나 기대했는데!\"\n\n\"존슨 씨는 고집부리지 않을 거예요, 여보.\" 크럼블스 부인이 말했어. \"그런 분이라고 의심하지 말아요. 신사다움이며 인정이며, 저분 천성의 가장 고운 마음씨가 모두 이 갸륵한 일에 동원될 거예요.\"\n\n\"단장의 마음마저 움직이는 일이지.\" 크럼블스 씨가 미소 지으며 말했어.\n\n\"단장 아내의 마음까지요.\" 크럼블스 부인이 늘 그렇듯 비극 배우 같은 목소리로 덧붙였어. \"자, 자, 마음을 누그러뜨리실 거예요. 그러실 줄 알아요.\"\n\n\"제 천성이,\" 니콜라스가 이 호소들에 마음이 움직여 말했어. \"어떤 간청도 뿌리치지 못하는 편이라서요. 그게 명백히 그릇된 일만 아니라면요. 그리고 자존심 하나 빼고는, 이 일을 마다할 까닭이 없네요. 여기서는 제가 아는 사람도 없고, 저를 아는 사람도 없으니까요. 그럼 그렇게 하죠. 제가 졌어요.\"\n\n스네벨리치 양은 단번에 얼굴이 빨개지며 고맙다는 말을 쏟아 냈어. 그 고맙다는 말이라면 크럼블스 씨도 부인도 결코 아끼지 않았지. 니콜라스가 이튿날 아침 열한 시에 그녀의 하숙집으로 찾아가기로 정해졌어. 그러고는 곧 헤어졌지. 니콜라스는 집필을 하러 집으로 돌아갔고, 스네벨리치 양은 막간극 의상을 갈아입으러 갔어. 그리고 사심 없는 단장과 그 아내는 다가올 예약 공연으로 얼마나 벌이가 될지를 의논했어. 엄숙한 합의에 따라 그 수익의 삼분의 이를 두 사람이 가져가기로 되어 있었거든.\n\n이튿날 아침 약속한 시각에, 니콜라스는 스네벨리치 양의 하숙집으로 찾아갔어. 롬바드 거리라는 곳에 있는, 어느 재단사의 집이었지. 좁은 복도에는 다림질 냄새가 진동했어. 문을 열어 준 재단사의 딸은, 온 식구의 빨랫감을 정기적으로 손질할 때면 으레 따라오는 그 들뜬 기색이었지.\n\n\"스네벨리치 양이 여기 사는 게 맞죠?\" 문이 열리자 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n재단사의 딸은 그렇다고 대답했어.\n\n\"존슨이 왔다고 좀 전해 주시겠어요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"오, 괜찮으시면 위층으로 올라오세요.\" 재단사의 딸이 웃으며 대답했어.\n\n니콜라스는 그 아가씨를 따라갔어. 그리고 이 층의 작은 방으로 안내받았지. 그 방은 안쪽 방과 통해 있었어. 어렴풋이 짤그락거리는 소리, 찻잔과 받침이 부딪치는 듯한 소리로 미루어, 스네벨리치 양은 그때 침대에서 아침을 먹고 있는 모양이었지.\n\n\"괜찮으시면 좀 기다리세요.\" 잠깐 자리를 비웠다 돌아온 재단사의 딸이 말했어. 그새 안쪽 방의 짤그락거리는 소리는 멎고, 속삭이는 소리로 바뀌어 있었지. \"곧 나오실 거예요.\"\n\n말을 하면서 그녀는 창문 가리개를 끌어 올렸어. 그렇게 해서 (스스로 생각하기에) 존슨 씨의 눈길을 방에서 거리로 돌려놓은 다음, 난로망에 널어 말리던 물건 몇 개를 잽싸게 집어 들었어. 영락없이 스타킹처럼 보이는 것들이었지. 그러고는 휙 사라졌어.\n\n창밖에 눈길 끌 만한 게 별로 없어서, 니콜라스는 방 안을 둘러봤어. 평소 같으면 그러지 않았을 텐데, 호기심이 더 일었지. 소파에는 낡은 기타 하나와, 손때 묻은 악보 몇 장과, 여기저기 흩어진 종이 머리 마는 도구가 놓여 있었어. 거기다 어수선하게 쌓인 공연 전단지 더미와, 커다란 파란 장미 매듭이 달린 더러운 흰 새틴 신발 한 켤레도 있었지. 의자 등받이에는 반쯤 만들다 만 모슬린 앞치마가 걸려 있었어. 빨간 리본으로 장식한 작은 주머니가 달린 거였지. 무대에서 시녀들이 두르는, 그래서 (당연히) 다른 데서는 절대 볼 수 없는 그런 앞치마였어. 한쪽 구석에는 자그마한 장화 한 켤레가 놓여 있었어. 스네벨리치 양이 어린 기수 역을 할 때 신던 거였지. 그 옆 의자에는 작은 꾸러미 하나가 개켜져 있었는데, 짝을 이루는 짧은 바지를 영락없이 닮았더라고.\n\n하지만 무엇보다 흥미로운 건, 아마 펼쳐져 있던 스크랩북이었을 거야. 탁자 위에 흩어진 작은 연극 책들 한가운데에 놓여 있었지. 그 스크랩북에는 스네벨리치 양의 연기에 관한 갖가지 비평이 붙어 있었어. 여러 지방 신문에서 오려 낸 것들이었지. 거기에 그녀를 기리는 시 한 편도 같이 붙어 있었는데, 이렇게 시작했어.\n\n노래하라, 사랑의 신이여, 그리고 말해 다오, 어떤 메마른 시절에 세 겹의 재능을 타고난 스네벨리치가 이 땅에 왔는지를. 그 미소로, 그 눈물로, 그 눈빛으로 우리를 떨리게 하려고, 노래하라, 사랑의 신이여, 그리고 어서 그 까닭을 말해 다오.\n\n이 격정 어린 시 말고도, 신문에서 오려 낸 칭찬의 글이 수도 없이 많았어. 이런 식이었지. \"오늘 신문 다른 면의 광고에서, 매력적이고 재능 넘치는 스네벨리치 양이 수요일에 자선 공연을 연다는 소식을 보았다. 이날을 위해 그녀가 내놓은 공연 목록은 인간 혐오자의 가슴에도 흥을 지필 만하다. 우리 이웃 시민들이 공익에 대한 높은 안목과 개인의 진가를 알아보는 식견을 여전히 잃지 않았으리라 믿는다. 그들은 오랫동안 그 점에서 더없이 두드러져 왔으니. 우리는 이 매력적인 여배우가 만원사례의 환대를 받으리라 예언하는 바이다.\" \"독자께 알림. J. S. 씨께서는 잘못 알고 계신다. 우리의 아담하고 편리한 작은 극장에서 매일 밤 모든 이의 마음을 사로잡는, 재능 넘치고 아름다운 스네벨리치 양이, 요크 시 백 마일 이내에 사는 막대한 재산의 젊은 신사가 최근 정중히 청혼한 그 숙녀가 아니라고 말이다. 우리는 알고 있다. 스네벨리치 양이야말로 그 신비롭고 낭만적인 사건에 얽혀 있던 그 숙녀라는 것을. 그리고 그때 그녀가 보인 처신은, 그녀의 빛나는 천재성을 증명하는 연기 위의 승리만큼이나, 그녀의 머리와 마음에 더없는 영예를 안겨 주었다.\" 이런 글들이 잔뜩 모여 있었고, 거기에 큼지막한 대문자로 죄다 \"일찍 오세요\"로 끝나는 긴 자선 공연 전단지들까지. 그게 스네벨리치 양 스크랩북의 주된 내용이었어.\n\n니콜라스는 이런 오려 낸 글들을 꽤 많이 읽었어. 그러다 어떤 자세하고 구슬픈 사연에 푹 빠졌지. 스네벨리치 양이 발목을 삐게 된 일련의 사건에 관한 이야기였어. 윈체스터의 무대에서 사람 탈을 쓴 괴물이 (신문에는 그렇게 적혀 있었어) 던진 오렌지 껍질을 밟고 미끄러진 거였지. 그때 바로 그 아가씨가, 석탄 통처럼 챙이 깊은 보닛에 외출복까지 완벽하게 차려입고 사뿐사뿐 방으로 들어왔어. 약속한 시간보다 한참 늦게 그를 붙잡아 둔 데 대해 천 번이나 사과하면서 말이야.\n\n\"그게요, 정말이지,\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했어. \"여기서 저랑 같이 사는 사랑하는 레드가 밤새 어찌나 심하게 앓던지, 제 품에서 숨이 넘어가는 줄 알았어요.\"\n\n\"그런 운명이라면 차라리 부럽기까지 한데요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그래도 그 얘기를 들으니 정말 안타깝네요.\"\n\n\"어쩜 그렇게 아부를 잘하세요!\" 스네벨리치 양이 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 장갑 단추를 채우면서 말했어.\n\n\"당신의 매력과 재능에 감탄하는 게 아부라면,\" 니콜라스가 스크랩북에 손을 올리며 대꾸했어. \"여기 그보다 더 나은 아부의 본보기들이 잔뜩 있는걸요.\"\n\n\"오, 짓궂으셔라. 그런 걸 다 읽으시다니! 이제 얼굴을 마주 볼 낯이 없어요, 정말이에요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 책을 낚아채 벽장 안에 치우며 말했어. \"레드도 참 칠칠치 못하지! 어쩜 그렇게 못된 짓을!\"\n\n\"제가 읽으라고 일부러 친절히 여기 놔두신 줄 알았는데요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. 사실 그럴 법도 했지.\n\n\"무슨 일이 있어도 그건 보여 드리고 싶지 않았어요!\" 스네벨리치 양이 대꾸했어. \"이렇게 속상한 적은 없었어요, 한 번도요! 그 애가 워낙 칠칠치 못해서 도무지 믿을 수가 없다니까요.\"\n\n여기서 대화가 끊겼어. 신동이 들어왔거든. 이 순간까지 얌전히 침실에 머물러 있던 신동은, 이제 더없이 우아하고 사뿐하게 모습을 드러냈어. 손에는 아주 자그마한 초록색 양산을 들고 있었지. 넓은 술 장식이 둘러진, 손잡이 없는 양산이었어. 으레 하는 인사 몇 마디를 나눈 뒤, 그들은 거리로 나섰어.\n\n신동은 꽤나 성가신 동행이었어. 먼저 오른쪽 샌들이 벗겨지고, 다음엔 왼쪽이 벗겨졌거든. 그걸 고쳐 신기고 나니, 이번엔 작은 흰 바지의 한쪽 가랑이가 다른 쪽보다 길다는 게 드러났지. 게다가 초록색 양산이 쇠창살 틈으로 떨어져서, 한참 애를 쓰고 갖은 수를 써서야 겨우 건져 올렸어. 그래도 야단을 칠 수는 없었지. 단장의 딸이었으니까. 그래서 니콜라스는 이 모든 걸 너그러이 받아들이고 계속 걸었어. 한쪽 팔에는 스네벨리치 양이 팔짱을 끼고, 다른 쪽에는 말썽쟁이 아이를 데리고서 말이야.\n\n그들이 가장 먼저 발길을 향한 집은, 점잖아 보이는 연립 주택가에 있었어. 스네벨리치 양이 점잖게 문을 두 번 두드리자, 사환 소년이 나왔지. 커들 부인이 집에 계시냐는 물음에, 소년은 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 히죽히죽 웃으며, 잘 모르겠지만 여쭤보겠다고 했어. 그러고는 그들을 응접실로 안내해 기다리게 했지. 그새 하녀 두 명이 핑계를 대고 그리로 와서는, 연극배우들을 구경했어. 그러고는 복도에서 소년이랑 서로 정보를 나누고, 잔뜩 속닥거리고 킥킥대더니, 마침내 소년이 스네벨리치 양의 이름을 들고 위층으로 올라갔어.\n\n자, 이런 일에 밝은 사람들 사이에서 커들 부인은 문학이며 연극에 관한 한 그야말로 런던 취향을 지녔다고 여겨졌어. 그리고 커들 씨로 말하자면, 그는 팔 절판 육십사 쪽짜리 소책자를 한 권 쓴 적이 있었지. 「로미오와 줄리엣」에 나오는 유모의 죽은 남편의 성격에 관한 거였어. 그가 생전에 정말로 \"유쾌한 사람\"이었는지, 아니면 그저 미망인이 애틋한 정에 끌려 그렇게 전한 것뿐인지 따져 보는 내용이었지. 그는 또 통용되는 구두점 찍는 방식을 바꾸면, 셰익스피어의 어떤 희곡이든 완전히 다른 작품으로 만들어 뜻을 송두리째 바꿀 수 있다는 것도 입증했어. 그러니 두말할 것도 없이, 그는 대단한 비평가요, 더없이 심오하고 독창적인 사상가였지.\n\n\"어머, 스네벨리치 양.\" 커들 부인이 응접실로 들어서며 말했어. \"그래, 잘 지냈어요?\"\n\n스네벨리치 양은 우아하게 인사하고는, 커들 부인이 안녕하시기를, 또 마침 같이 나타난 커들 씨도 안녕하시기를 바랐어. 커들 부인은 실내복 차림에, 머리 꼭대기에 작은 모자를 얹고 있었어. 커들 씨는 등에 헐렁한 가운을 걸치고, 오른손 검지를 이마에 댄 자세였지. 스턴의 초상화를 본뜬 거였어. 누군가가 그에게 스턴과 놀랄 만큼 닮았다고 한 적이 있었거든.\n\n\"제 예약 공연에 이름을 올려 주실 수 있을까 여쭈러 외람되이 찾아왔어요, 부인.\" 스네벨리치 양이 서류를 꺼내며 말했어.\n\n\"오! 정말 뭐라고 해야 할지 모르겠네요.\" 커들 부인이 대답했어. \"연극이 한창 흥하고 번창하던 시절도 아니고. 서 있지 말아요, 스네벨리치 양. 연극은 갔어요, 완전히 갔어요.\"\n\n\"시인의 환상을 더없이 정교하게 구현한 것이자, 인간 지성을 실현한 것으로서, 우리의 몽롱한 순간을 찬란한 빛으로 물들이고, 마음의 눈앞에 새롭고 마법 같은 세계를 펼쳐 보이던 연극은, 갔어요, 완전히 갔어요.\" 커들 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"지금 살아 있는 사람 가운데, 햄릿이라는 인물에 깃든 그 변화무쌍하고 영롱한 빛깔들을 우리 앞에 펼쳐 보일 수 있는 자가 어디 있겠어요?\" 커들 부인이 외쳤어.\n\n\"정말이지 누가 있겠소, 무대 위에 말이오.\" 커들 씨가 자기만은 살짝 예외로 두며 말했어. \"햄릿이라! 흥! 우스운 일이지! 햄릿은 갔어요, 완전히 갔어요.\"\n\n이 음울한 상념에 그만 압도되어, 커들 씨 부부는 한숨을 쉬고는 한동안 말없이 앉아 있었어. 마침내 부인이 스네벨리치 양을 돌아보며, 어떤 연극을 올릴 생각이냐고 물었어.\n\n\"완전히 새 작품이에요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했어. \"여기 이 신사분이 작가시고, 이분이 직접 출연도 하세요. 무대에 처음 서시는 거고요. 존슨 씨라는 분이에요.\"\n\n\"삼일치는 지키셨겠지요, 선생?\" 커들 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"원작은 프랑스 작품이에요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"사건도 풍성하고, 대사도 발랄하고, 인물도 뚜렷하게 그려져 있죠.\"\n\n\"삼일치를 엄격히 지키지 않으면 다 소용없어요, 선생.\" 커들 씨가 받았어. \"연극의 삼일치, 그게 무엇보다 먼저요.\"\n\n\"여쭤봐도 될까요.\" 니콜라스가 마땅히 차려야 할 예의와 짓궂은 걸 좋아하는 마음 사이에서 망설이며 말했어. \"그 삼일치라는 게 뭔지 여쭤봐도 될까요?\"\n\n커들 씨가 헛기침을 하고는 곰곰이 생각했어. \"삼일치란 말이오, 선생.\" 그가 말했어. \"하나의 완결성이지요. 장소와 시간에 관한 일종의 보편적인 짜맞춤이라고 할까. 이렇게 강한 표현을 써도 된다면, 일종의 전반적인 하나됨이오. 내가 거기에 주의를 기울일 수 있었던 한에서는, 그것이 바로 연극의 삼일치라고 보오. 나는 이 주제에 관해 많이 읽었고, 많이 생각했소. 이 아이의 연기를 보면,\" 커들 씨가 신동을 돌아보며 말했어. \"거기엔 감정의 통일성과, 넓이와, 명암과, 따뜻한 색조와, 음색과, 조화와, 빛남과, 독창적 착상의 예술적 전개가 흐르고 있소. 나이 든 배우들에게서는 아무리 찾으려 해도 찾을 수 없는 것들이지. 내 말을 알아듣겠소?\"\n\n\"완벽히요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"바로 그렇소.\" 커들 씨가 목도리를 끌어 올리며 말했어. \"그게 내가 내리는 연극 삼일치의 정의요.\"\n\n커들 부인은 이 명쾌한 설명에 흡족해하며 귀를 기울이고 앉아 있었어. 설명이 끝나자, 부인은 이름을 적는 일에 대해 커들 씨가 어떻게 생각하는지 물었어.\n\n\"모르겠소, 여보. 정말이지 모르겠소.\" 커들 씨가 말했어. \"만약 적는다면, 우리가 공연의 질을 보증하는 게 아니라는 걸 분명히 못 박아 둬야 하오. 세상에 알려야 한단 말이오. 우리가 그들에게 우리 이름의 인정을 내주는 게 아니라, 그저 스네벨리치 양에게만 그 영예를 베푸는 거라고. 그 점을 분명히 한다면, 비록 그 연극에 얽힌 추억을 봐서라도, 타락한 무대에 우리의 후원을 베푸는 게, 말하자면 하나의 도리라고 보오. 반 크라운 거슬러 줄 이 실링 육 펜스 있소, 스네벨리치 양?\" 커들 씨가 그 동전 네 닢을 뒤적이며 말했어.\n\n스네벨리치 양은 분홍색 손가방 구석구석을 더듬어 봤지만, 어느 구석에도 아무것도 없었어. 니콜라스는 자기는 작가일 뿐이라며 농담을 중얼거렸고, 자기 주머니를 뒤지는 시늉조차 아예 안 하는 게 낫겠다고 생각했지.\n\n\"어디 보자.\" 커들 씨가 말했어. \"이 곱하기 사는 팔이고, 한 자리에 사 실링짜리 특별석이라니, 지금처럼 연극이 형편없는 때에는 너무 비싸군, 스네벨리치 양. 반 크라운 세 닢이면 칠 실링 육 펜스이고. 육 펜스 가지고는 다투지 않겠지, 그렇지? 육 펜스 때문에 갈라서지는 않겠지, 스네벨리치 양?\"\n\n가엾은 스네벨리치 양은 연신 웃고 고개를 숙이며 반 크라운 세 닢을 받았어. 그리고 커들 부인은 자리를 잡아 둘 것이며, 좌석을 닦아 둘 것이며, 새 전단지가 나오는 대로 깨끗한 걸로 두 장 보내 줄 것이며, 이런저런 당부를 몇 마디 덧붙이고는, 종을 울렸어. 면담을 끝내자는 신호였지.\n\n\"별난 사람들이네요.\" 그 집에서 벗어나자 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 스네벨리치 양이 그의 팔을 잡으며 말했어. \"그분들이 육 펜스 모자란 게 아니라 돈을 다 빚지지 않은 게 다행이다 싶어요. 자, 만약 당신이 성공하면, 그분들은 자기들이 늘 당신을 후원했다고 떠벌리고 다닐 거예요. 그러다 당신이 실패하면, 처음부터 그럴 줄 알았다고 했겠죠.\"\n\n다음으로 찾아간 집에서, 그들은 큰 영광을 누렸어. 거기엔 신동의 공연에 그토록 푹 빠진 그 여섯 아이가 살고 있었거든. 아이들은 그 아가씨를 따로 구경하라고 육아실에서 불려 내려왔어. 그러고는 신동의 눈을 손가락으로 쿡쿡 찌르고, 발가락을 밟아 대고, 그 나이대 특유의 자잘한 친절을 잔뜩 베풀었지.\n\n\"보럼 씨를 설득해서 꼭 개인 특별석을 잡게 할게요.\" 더없이 정중히 맞이한 뒤에 그 집 안주인이 말했어. \"아이는 둘만 데려가고, 나머지 일행은 신사분들로 채울 거예요. 스네벨리치 양, 당신을 흠모하는 분들로요. 오거스터스, 이 못된 녀석, 어린 아가씨 좀 가만 놔둬라.\"\n\n이 말은 한 젊은 신사를 향한 거였어. 그 아이는 신동의 등 뒤를 꼬집고 있었지. 그게 진짜 사람인지 확인해 보려는 모양이었어.\n\n\"분명 무척 피곤하실 거예요.\" 엄마가 스네벨리치 양을 돌아보며 말했어. \"포도주 한 잔도 안 드시고 가시게 둘 수는 없죠. 어머나, 샬럿, 너 부끄러운 줄 알아라! 레인 양, 미안하지만 아이들 좀 봐 줘요.\"\n\n레인 양은 가정교사였어. 이런 부탁이 필요했던 건, 막내 보럼 아가씨의 난데없는 행동 때문이었지. 그 아이가 신동의 작은 초록색 양산을 슬쩍해서는, 그대로 들고 도망가고 있었거든. 정신이 쏙 빠진 신동은 어쩔 줄 몰라 멀거니 바라보기만 했어.\n\n\"도대체 어디서 그렇게 연기하는 법을 배웠는지,\" 마음씨 좋은 보럼 부인이 다시 스네벨리치 양을 돌아보며 말했어. \"도무지 알 수가 없어요. (엠마, 그렇게 빤히 쳐다보지 마라.) 이 장면에서 웃다가, 저 장면에서 울다가, 다 어쩜 그렇게 자연스러운지. 오, 세상에!\"\n\n\"그렇게 좋게 봐 주시니 정말 기뻐요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했어. \"마음에 드신다니 더없이 즐겁네요.\"\n\n\"마음에 들다뿐이겠어요!\" 보럼 부인이 외쳤어. \"어떻게 마음에 안 들 수가 있어요? 할 수만 있다면 일주일에 두 번이라도 연극을 보러 가고 싶어요. 정말 푹 빠졌다니까요. 다만 가끔 너무 가슴을 울려서 탈이에요. 어찌나 사람을 그런 지경으로 만드는지, 펑펑 울게 만든다니까요! 어머나, 세상에, 레인 양, 어떻게 저 가엾은 아이를 저렇게 들볶게 두는 거예요!\"\n\n신동은 정말이지 사지가 찢겨 나갈 판이었어. 기운 센 사내아이 둘이 신동의 양손을 하나씩 붙잡고, 누가 더 센지 겨루느라 서로 반대 방향으로 잡아당기고 있었거든. 하지만 마침 레인 양이 그 가엾은 아이를 구해 냈어. 사실 레인 양은 다 큰 배우들을 구경하느라 너무 정신이 팔려서, 이 일에 마땅한 주의를 기울이지 못했던 거였지. 신동은 포도주 한 잔으로 기운을 차린 뒤, 곧 친구들 손에 이끌려 돌아갔어. 다행히 분홍 망사 보닛이 납작해지고, 흰 원피스와 바지가 꽤 심하게 구겨진 것 말고는 더 큰 손상은 입지 않았지.\n\n힘든 아침이었어. 들러야 할 집이 워낙 많았는데, 사람마다 원하는 게 제각각이었거든. 어떤 이는 비극을, 어떤 이는 희극을 원했어. 어떤 이는 춤은 빼라고 했고, 어떤 이는 춤 말고는 별로 보고 싶은 게 없다고 했지. 어떤 이는 희극 가수가 딱 봐도 격이 떨어진다고 했고, 어떤 이는 그 가수가 평소보다 출연을 더 많이 했으면 좋겠다고 했어. 어떤 사람들은 가겠다는 약속을 안 했어. 다른 사람들이 가겠다는 약속을 안 했다는 이유로 말이야. 또 어떤 사람들은 아예 안 가겠다고 했지. 다른 사람들이 간다는 이유로 말이야. 마침내, 조금씩 조금씩, 여기서는 뭘 빼고 저기서는 뭘 더하면서, 스네벨리치 양은 한 공연 목록을 약속하기에 이르렀어. 다른 장점은 없을지언정 내용 하나는 풍성한 목록이었지. (다른 자질구레한 것들 가운데 연극 네 편, 노래 여러 곡, 결투 몇 차례, 춤 여러 번이 들어 있었어.) 그러고는 두 사람은 집으로 돌아왔어. 그날 일로 꽤 녹초가 되어서 말이야.\n\n니콜라스는 작품을 써 나갔어. 작품은 곧바로 연습에 들어갔지. 그러고 나서 그는 자기 배역을 파고들었어. 엄청난 끈기로 익혀서, 온 극단이 입을 모아 말하기를, 완벽하게 연기해 냈어. 그리고 마침내 그 큰 날이 왔지. 아침에 거리마다 포고꾼을 보내, 종소리와 함께 공연을 알렸어. 그리고 길이 삼 피트에 너비 구 인치짜리 추가 전단지를 사방에 뿌렸지. 모든 지하 입구에 던져 넣고, 모든 문고리 밑에 끼워 넣고, 모든 상점에 펼쳐 놨어. 벽이란 벽에도 다 붙였지. 그런데 완전히 성공적이지는 못했어. 본래 전단 붙이는 사람이 아파서 글 모르는 사람이 그 일을 맡았거든. 그래서 일부는 옆으로 돌아가게 붙었고, 나머지는 거꾸로 붙었지.\n\n다섯 시 반에 위층 관람석 문으로 네 사람이 몰려왔어. 여섯 시 십오 분 전에는 적어도 열두 명은 됐지. 여섯 시가 되자 문을 차 대는 소리가 무시무시했어. 그래서 큰 크럼블스 도련님이 문을 열었을 때는, 목숨을 부지하려고 문 뒤로 뛰어들어야 했지. 첫 십 분 만에 그루든 부인이 십오 실링을 거뒀어.\n\n무대 뒤에서도 똑같이 색다른 흥분이 감돌았어. 스네벨리치 양은 어찌나 땀을 흘리던지, 얼굴에 분이 도무지 붙어 있질 않았어. 크럼블스 부인은 어찌나 긴장했던지, 자기 대사를 거의 기억하지 못했지. 브라바사 양의 곱슬머리는 열기와 불안에 그만 다 풀려 버렸어. 심지어 크럼블스 씨 본인까지 막에 난 구멍으로 자꾸 엿보고는, 이따금 뛰어 들어와서 일 층 좌석에 한 사람이 더 들어왔다고 알렸지.\n\n마침내 악단이 연주를 멈추고, 새 작품의 막이 올랐어. 별다른 사람이 안 나오는 첫 장면은 그런대로 조용히 지나갔지. 그런데 둘째 장면에서 스네벨리치 양이, 아이 역의 신동을 데리고 등장하자, 그야말로 박수갈채가 터져 나왔어! 보럼 가족석의 사람들이 일제히 일어서서, 모자와 손수건을 흔들며 \"브라보!\"를 외쳤어. 보럼 부인과 가정교사는 무대 위로 화환을 던졌어. 그 가운데 몇 개는 조명등 속으로 펄럭이며 떨어졌고, 하나는 일 층 좌석에 앉은 뚱뚱한 신사의 머리에 씌워졌지. 그 신사는 무대 쪽을 뚫어져라 바라보느라, 그 영예를 까맣게 모르고 있었어. 재단사와 그 식구는 위층 특별석 칸막이를 어찌나 차 대던지, 통째로 떨어져 나올 지경이었지. 진저비어 파는 소년조차 극장 한복판에 못 박힌 듯 서 있었어. 스네벨리치 양에게 연정을 품은 것으로 여겨지는 한 젊은 장교는, 눈물을 가리려는 듯 안경을 눈에 갖다 댔지. 스네벨리치 양은 거듭거듭 점점 더 깊이 절을 했고, 박수는 거듭거듭 점점 더 크게 쏟아졌어. 마침내 신동이 김이 모락모락 나는 화환 하나를 집어서, 스네벨리치 양의 눈 위로 비스듬히 씌우자, 갈채가 절정에 이르렀지. 그러고서 연극은 계속됐어.\n\n그런데 니콜라스가 크럼블스 부인과의 명장면을 위해 등장하자, 그 박수 소리란! 크럼블스 부인이 (그의 못난 어머니 역이었지) 코웃음 치며 그를 \"건방진 녀석\"이라 부르고, 그가 맞서자, 박수가 그야말로 떠나갈 듯 터졌어! 그가 그 아가씨를 두고 다른 신사와 다투며 권총 한 상자를 꺼내, 네가 정녕 신사라면 이 응접실에서 한 사람의, 아니 어쩌면 두 사람의 피가 가구에 흩뿌려질 때까지 결투를 벌이겠다고 하자, 특별석이고 일 층이고 위층 관람석이고 다 같이 더없이 우렁찬 환호를 보냈어! 그가 어머니가 그 아가씨의 재산을 내놓지 않는다며 욕을 퍼붓다가, 어머니가 누그러지자 자기도 누그러져서, 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 축복을 빌자, 객석의 여인들이 어찌나 흐느꼈는지! 그가 어둠 속에서 막 뒤에 숨고, 못된 친척이 날카로운 칼을 사방으로 쑤셔 대는데, 정작 그의 다리가 빤히 보이는 데만 빼고 찌르자, 극장 안에 불안한 두려움의 전율이 좍 퍼졌어! 그의 분위기며, 자태며, 걸음걸이며, 표정이며, 그가 말하고 행하는 모든 게 다 찬사의 대상이었지. 그가 입을 열 때마다 박수가 한바탕 쏟아졌어. 그리고 마침내, 펌프와 물통 장면에서, 그루든 부인이 파란 불을 켜고, 출연 없는 단원들이 모두 들어와 사방으로 나뒹굴자, 그건 줄거리와는 아무 상관도 없고 그저 한 폭의 그림 같은 장면으로 마무리하려는 거였지만, 그새 부쩍 늘어난 관객은 그야말로 열광에 찬 함성을 내질렀어. 그 벽 안에서 그토록 오랫동안 들어 본 적 없는 함성이었지.\n\n요컨대, 새 작품도 새 배우도 완벽한 성공이었어. 그리고 연극이 끝나고 스네벨리치 양이 무대로 불려 나오자, 니콜라스가 그녀를 이끌고 나와, 박수갈채를 함께 나눴지.\n\n# CHAPTER 25\n\n런던에서 온 젊은 아가씨가 극단에 합류하는 일과, 그녀를 따라 함께 온 나이 든 흠모자에 관한 이야기. 그리고 두 사람이 도착한 뒤 벌어진 감동적인 예식에 관하여\n\n새 작품이 확실한 흥행작으로 떠오르면서, 추후 공지가 있을 때까지 공연하는 저녁마다 무대에 올린다고 알려졌어. 그리고 극장 문을 닫는 저녁도 일주일에 사흘에서 이틀로 줄었지. 엄청난 성공의 표시는 이것만이 아니었어. 다음 토요일에 니콜라스는 지칠 줄 모르는 그러든 부인의 호의로 무려 삼십 실링이나 되는 돈을 받았거든. 이 두둑한 보상 말고도 그는 적잖은 명성과 영예까지 누렸어. 컬들 씨의 소책자 증정본이 극장으로 보내져 왔는데, 속표지에 그 신사가 직접 쓴 사인이 적혀 있었지. 그 자체로 값을 매길 수 없는 보물이었어. 거기에는 칭찬의 말을 잔뜩 담은 쪽지도 함께 들어 있었어. 게다가 청하지도 않았는데, 니콜라스가 이 도시에 머무는 동안 매일 아침 식사 전 세 시간씩 셰익스피어를 읽어 주면 정말 기쁘겠다는 다짐까지 적혀 있었지.\n\n\"또 새로운 게 생겼어, 존슨.\" 어느 날 아침 크럼믈스 씨가 잔뜩 신이 나서 말했어.\n\n\"그게 뭔데요?\" 니콜라스가 되물었어. \"조랑말인가요?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니. 조랑말은 다른 게 다 안 통할 때나 꺼내 드는 거야.\" 크럼믈스 씨가 말했어. \"이번 시즌엔 아예 조랑말까지 갈 일이 없을 것 같아. 아니, 아니, 조랑말은 아니야.\"\n\n\"그럼 신동 소년인가요?\" 니콜라스가 짐작해 봤어.\n\n\"신동은 하나뿐이야, 자네.\" 크럼믈스 씨가 묵직하게 대답했어. \"그리고 그건 여자아이지.\"\n\n\"맞아요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"죄송합니다. 그럼 정말 뭔지 모르겠는데요.\"\n\n\"런던에서 온 젊은 아가씨라면 어떤가?\" 크럼믈스 씨가 물었어. \"드루어리 레인 왕립 극장의 아무개 양 말이야.\"\n\n\"전단지에 아주 보기 좋겠는데요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"제법 맞췄어.\" 크럼믈스 씨가 말했어. \"거기다 무대에서도 아주 보기 좋을 거라고 했으면, 그것도 틀린 말은 아니었을 거야. 자, 여기. 이걸 어떻게 생각해?\"\n\n이렇게 물으면서 크럼믈스 씨는 빨간 포스터, 파란 포스터, 노란 포스터를 차례로 펼쳐 보였어. 저마다 맨 위에는 커다란 글자로 이렇게 알리는 문구가 박혀 있었지. \"드루어리 레인 왕립 극장의 견줄 데 없는 페토커 양, 첫 출연!\"\n\n\"이런!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"저 아가씨를 제가 알아요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 자네는 한 젊은 사람 몸뚱이에 꽉꽉 눌러 담긴, 둘도 없는 재능을 알고 있는 셈이지.\" 크럼믈스 씨가 포스터를 다시 둘둘 말며 대꾸했어. \"그러니까 어떤 종류의 재능 말이야. 어떤 종류의 재능. '피를 마시는 자.'\" 크럼믈스 씨가 예언이라도 하듯 한숨을 쉬며 덧붙였어. \"'피를 마시는 자'는 저 아가씨와 함께 죽고 말 거야. 그리고 저 애만큼 한쪽 다리로 서서 다른 쪽 무릎에 탬버린을 치며, 그것도 요정처럼 해내는 요정은 내 평생 본 적이 없어.\"\n\n\"언제 내려온대요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"오늘쯤 올 거야.\" 크럼믈스 씨가 대답했어. \"크럼믈스 부인의 오랜 친구거든. 크럼믈스 부인이 저 애 솜씨를 알아봤지. 처음부터 죽 알고 있었어. 사실 저 애가 아는 건 거의 다 부인이 가르쳐 준 거야. 크럼믈스 부인이 바로 원조 '피를 마시는 자'였거든.\"\n\n\"그래요?\"\n\n\"그래. 하지만 그만둘 수밖에 없었지.\"\n\n\"부인한테 안 맞았나요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"부인보다는 관객들한테 안 맞았어.\" 크럼믈스 씨가 대답했어. \"아무도 견뎌 내질 못했거든. 너무 어마어마했지. 자네는 아직 크럼믈스 부인이 어떤 사람인지 잘 몰라.\"\n\n니콜라스는 자기는 안다고 생각하는데, 하고 슬쩍 끼워 넣어 봤어.\n\n\"아니, 아니, 자네는 몰라.\" 크럼믈스 씨가 말했어. \"정말 몰라. 나도 몰라, 그게 사실이야. 부인이 죽기 전까지는 이 나라도 모를걸. 저 놀라운 여자한테선 살아온 해마다 무슨 새로운 재능이 터져 나온다니까. 저 사람을 봐. 여섯 아이의 어머니야. 그 가운데 셋이 살아 있고, 다 무대에 서고 있지!\"\n\n\"대단하네요!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어.\n\n\"아! 정말 대단하지.\" 크럼믈스 씨가 흐뭇하게 코담배를 한 줌 집으며, 진지하게 고개를 끄덕이며 대꾸했어. \"직업인의 명예를 걸고 말하는데, 부인이 마지막 후원 공연을 하기 전까진 춤도 출 줄 아는지 몰랐어. 그때 부인이 줄리엣도 하고 헬렌 맥그리거도 하더니, 막간에는 줄넘기 혼파이프까지 추더라고. 내가 그 훌륭한 여자를 처음 봤을 때는 말이야, 존슨.\" 크럼믈스 씨가 조금 더 가까이 다가와, 은밀한 우정을 나누는 말투로 말했어. \"활활 타오르는 불꽃놀이에 둘러싸여서, 창 자루 끝에 물구나무를 서 있었지.\"\n\n\"놀라운데요!\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"내가 놀랐다니까!\" 크럼믈스 씨가 아주 진지한 얼굴로 대꾸했어. \"그렇게 우아하면서도 그렇게 위엄이 있다니! 나는 그 순간부터 부인을 흠모했어!\"\n\n이런 말들의 재능 넘치는 주인공이 도착하는 바람에 크럼믈스 씨의 찬사는 뚝 끊기고 말았어. 거의 곧바로 퍼시 크럼믈스 도련님이 편지 한 통을 들고 들어왔거든. 일반 우편으로 온 거였고, 자애로운 그의 어머니 앞으로 온 편지였지. 겉봉의 글씨를 보자마자 크럼믈스 부인이 외쳤어. \"헨리에타 페토커한테서 왔잖아, 분명히!\" 그러고는 곧장 편지 내용에 푹 빠져들었어.\n\n\"그게 정말―?\" 크럼믈스 씨가 머뭇거리며 물었어.\n\n\"아, 그럼요, 다 됐어요.\" 크럼믈스 부인이 질문을 앞질러 대답했어. \"저 애한테는 참말로 잘된 일이지요!\"\n\n\"내가 듣기로는 이게 통틀어 제일 잘된 일인 것 같소.\" 크럼믈스 씨가 말했어. 그러고는 크럼믈스 씨도, 크럼믈스 부인도, 퍼시 크럼믈스 도련님도 다 같이 요란하게 웃기 시작했어. 니콜라스는 그들끼리 즐겁게 웃도록 내버려 두고 하숙집으로 걸어갔어. 페토커 양과 관련된 무슨 비밀이 저렇게 웃음을 자아내는 걸까 몹시 궁금했지. 그리고 그 아가씨가 이렇게나 빛나고 뛰어난 장식 같은 존재로 있는 직업에, 자기가 느닷없이 발을 들였다는 걸 알면 얼마나 놀랄까 하는 생각에 더 골똘히 잠겼어.\n\n하지만 이 뒤쪽 일에 대해서는 그가 잘못 짚었어. 빈센트 크럼믈스 씨가 미리 길을 닦아 둔 건지, 아니면 페토커 양이 평소보다도 더 다정하게 그를 대할 무슨 특별한 이유가 있었던 건지, 어쨌든 이튿날 극장에서 두 사람이 만난 모습은 그저 여섯 번쯤, 그것도 우연히 마주쳤을 뿐인 신사 숙녀의 인사라기보다는, 갓난아기 때부터 떨어진 적 없는 절친한 두 친구의 만남 같았거든. 아니, 페토커 양은 단장 식구들과 이야기할 때 켄위그스네 식구 얘기는 통째로 빼 버렸다고, 그리고 자기가 존슨 씨를 처음부터 가장 세련된 사교계에서 만난 양 둘러댔다고 귓속말까지 했어. 니콜라스가 진짜로 놀라서 이 말을 듣자, 그녀는 달콤한 눈길을 보내며 이제 자기가 그의 너그러움에 기댈 일이 있다고, 머지않아 그 신세를 좀 질지도 모른다고 덧붙였어.\n\n그날 밤 니콜라스는 페토커 양과 함께 가벼운 작품에 출연하는 영광을 누렸어. 그런데 그녀가 그렇게 뜨거운 환영을 받은 게 주로 위쪽 박스석에서 끈질기게 두드려 대는 우산 하나 덕분이라는 걸 알아챌 수밖에 없었지. 게다가 그 매혹적인 여배우가 그 소리가 나는 쪽으로 달콤한 눈길을 잔뜩 보낸다는 것도 봤어. 그리고 그녀가 그럴 때마다 그 우산이 다시 요란하게 두드려 댄다는 것도 봤지. 한번은 그 구석에 있는 유난히 묘하게 생긴 모자가 어쩐지 낯설지 않다는 생각이 들었어. 하지만 자기 무대 일에 정신이 팔려 있던 터라 그 일에 크게 마음을 두지 않았고, 집에 닿을 무렵엔 기억에서 완전히 사라지고 말았지.\n\n그가 스마이크와 막 저녁을 먹으려고 앉았을 때, 하숙집 사람 하나가 문밖에 와서 아래층에 어떤 신사가 존슨 씨와 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한다고 알렸어.\n\n\"그래요, 그렇다면 올라오시라고 하세요. 내가 아는 건 그게 다예요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"배곯는 우리 동료 중 하나겠지, 스마이크.\"\n\n함께 사는 그 친구는 차가운 고기를 물끄러미 바라보며, 내일 저녁거리로 얼마나 남겨질지 속으로 가늠해 봤어. 그러고는 손님이 헤집어 놓을 피해가 좀 덜하도록, 제 몫으로 베어 두었던 고기 한 조각을 도로 내려놓았지.\n\n\"전에 여기 왔던 사람은 아니야.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"계단마다 비틀거리며 올라오는 걸 보니 말이야. 들어오세요, 들어오세요. 세상에, 이게 누구야! 릴리빅 씨?\"\n\n정말로 수도세 징수원이었어. 그는 니콜라스를 뚫어지게 바라보며 꼼짝 않는 얼굴로, 무슨 큰일이라도 난 듯 엄숙하게 악수를 하고는 굴뚝 옆 자리에 앉았어.\n\n\"아니, 언제 여기 오셨어요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"오늘 아침에 왔지, 자네.\" 릴리빅 씨가 대답했어.\n\n\"아! 알겠어요. 그럼 오늘 밤 극장에 계셨던 거고, 그 우산이―\"\n\n\"이 우산이지.\" 릴리빅 씨가 끝이 찌그러진, 두툼한 초록색 무명 우산을 꺼내 보이며 말했어. \"그 공연을 어떻게 봤나?\"\n\n\"무대에 서 있어서 제대로 판단하긴 어려웠지만.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"아주 보기 좋았어요.\"\n\n\"보기 좋았다고!\" 징수원이 외쳤어. \"내 말은, 자네, 그건 황홀했다는 거야.\"\n\n릴리빅 씨는 마지막 단어를 더 힘주어 말하려고 몸을 앞으로 숙였어. 그러고는 몸을 다시 곧추세우더니 인상을 찌푸리고 여러 번 고개를 끄덕였지.\n\n\"황홀했어, 내 말은.\" 릴리빅 씨가 되풀이했어. \"넋을 빼놓고, 요정 같고, 가슴 벅찼지.\" 그러고는 릴리빅 씨가 다시 몸을 곧추세우더니, 또 인상을 찌푸리고 고개를 끄덕였어.\n\n\"아!\" 니콜라스는 이 황홀한 칭찬의 기미에 조금 놀라며 말했어. \"네, 영리한 아가씨예요.\"\n\n\"여신이지.\" 릴리빅 씨가 앞서 말한 그 우산으로 바닥을 두 번 똑똑 두드리며 대꾸했어. 징수원다운 두드림이었지. \"나도 전에 여신 같은 여배우들을 알았어, 자네. 옛날에 수도세를 거두러―적어도 받으러 다녔거든. 아주 자주 받으러 갔지. 한 여신 같은 여배우 집에 말이야. 사 년 넘게 내 구역에 살았던 여자였어. 하지만 내 평생, 아니, 단 한 번도, 여신 같은 존재 가운데, 여배우든 아니든 간에, 헨리에타 페토커보다 더 여신 같은 사람은 본 적이 없어.\"\n\n니콜라스는 웃음이 터져 나오는 걸 참느라 무진 애를 썼어. 입을 열 자신이 없어서, 그저 릴리빅 씨의 끄덕임에 맞춰 같이 고개만 끄덕이며 입을 다물고 있었지.\n\n\"자네하고 단둘이 할 얘기가 있어.\" 릴리빅 씨가 말했어.\n\n니콜라스가 스마이크를 향해 싱긋 웃자, 스마이크는 눈치를 채고 사라졌어.\n\n\"독신 남자란 비참한 신세야, 자네.\" 릴리빅 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"그래요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"그렇지.\" 징수원이 대꾸했어. \"내가 이 세상을 육십 년 가까이 살아 봤으니, 그게 어떤 건지 알 만하지 않겠나.\"\n\n'아는 게 당연하긴 하죠.' 니콜라스는 속으로 생각했어. '하지만 진짜 아시는지 어떤지는 또 다른 문제고요.'\n\n\"독신 남자가 어쩌다 돈푼이라도 좀 모아 두면 말이야.\" 릴리빅 씨가 말했어. \"그 형제자매며 조카들이 죄다 그 남자가 아니라 그 돈만 쳐다본다니까. 공인이라서 집안의 가장이라거나, 말하자면 다른 자잘한 가지들이 죄다 거기서 뻗어 나오는 원줄기라 해도, 그래도 그자들은 늘 그 남자가 죽기를 바란단 말이야. 그 남자가 정정해 보일 때마다 풀이 죽고. 그 쥐꼬리만 한 재산을 차지하고 싶어서지. 알겠나?\"\n\n\"아, 네.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"정말 그렇겠네요, 틀림없이.\"\n\n\"결혼을 안 하는 가장 큰 이유는 말이야.\" 릴리빅 씨가 말을 이었어. \"돈이 드는 거야. 그게 날 막아 온 거지. 안 그랬으면―어휴!\" 릴리빅 씨가 손가락을 딱 튕기며 말했어. \"여자 쉰 명은 거뜬했을걸.\"\n\n\"멋진 여자들로요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"멋진 여자들이지, 자네!\" 징수원이 대답했어. \"암! 헨리에타 페토커만큼 멋지진 않지. 그 여자는 흔치 않은 보배니까. 하지만 아무 남자한테나 굴러오는 그런 여자들은 아니었다고, 내 말해 두지. 자, 한 남자가 여자한테 딸려 오는 재산이 아니라, 여자 자체가 재산인 여자를 얻을 수 있다고 쳐 봐. 응?\"\n\n\"그야, 그럼 그 남자는 운 좋은 사람이죠.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"내 말이 그거야.\" 징수원이 우산으로 그의 머리통 옆을 다정하게 톡톡 두드리며 대꾸했어. \"딱 내 말이 그거라고. 헨리에타 페토커, 그 재능 넘치는 헨리에타 페토커는 그 자체가 재산이야. 그래서 내가 그 여자를―\"\n\n\"릴리빅 부인으로 만드시려고요?\" 니콜라스가 거들었어.\n\n\"아니, 자네, 릴리빅 부인으로 만드는 건 아니야.\" 징수원이 대답했어. \"여배우들은 말이야, 자네, 늘 처녀 적 이름을 그대로 쓰거든. 그게 관례지. 하지만 어쨌든 난 그 여자와 결혼할 거야. 그것도 모레.\"\n\n\"축하드립니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"고맙네, 자네.\" 징수원이 조끼 단추를 채우며 대답했어. \"물론 그 여자 급료는 내가 받아 챙길 거야. 그리고 어쨌든 둘을 건사하는 게 하나를 건사하는 거랑 얼추 비슷하게 싸게 먹히면 좋겠어. 그게 위안거리지.\"\n\n\"설마 이런 순간에 무슨 위안 같은 게 필요하신 건 아니죠?\" 니콜라스가 한마디 했어.\n\n\"아니.\" 릴리빅 씨가 초조하게 고개를 저으며 대답했어. \"아니―당연히 아니지.\"\n\n\"그런데 결혼을 하실 거라면, 어떻게 두 분이 다 여기 와 계신 거예요, 릴리빅 씨?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"아, 그걸 자네한테 설명하러 온 거야.\" 수도세 징수원이 대답했어. \"사실은 말이야, 우리가 식구들한테는 비밀로 하는 게 제일 낫겠다고 생각했거든.\"\n\n\"식구요!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"무슨 식구요?\"\n\n\"당연히 켄위그스네지.\" 릴리빅 씨가 대꾸했어. \"내가 떠나오기 전에 조카딸이랑 애들이 이 일을 한마디라도 알았더라면, 내 발치에 쓰러져 발작을 일으켰을 거고, 아무하고도 결혼 안 하겠다고 내가 맹세할 때까지 발작에서 안 깨어났을 거야. 아니면 정신 이상자라고 위원회라도 꾸려서 무슨 끔찍한 짓을 벌였겠지.\" 징수원이 말하면서 부들부들 떨었어.\n\n\"그렇겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"네, 분명 샘을 냈을 거예요, 틀림없이.\"\n\n\"그걸 막으려고 말이야.\" 릴리빅 씨가 말했어. \"헨리에타 페토커가―우리끼리 그렇게 정한 거지―이 출연을 핑계로 자기 친구들인 크럼믈스네한테 먼저 내려오고, 나는 그 전날 길퍼드로 내려가서 거기서 마차에 같이 타기로 했어. 그렇게 했지. 그래서 우리는 어제 길퍼드에서 함께 내려온 거야. 자, 혹시 자네가 노그스 씨한테 편지를 써서 우리 얘기를 할까 봐, 자네한테 이 비밀을 알려 두는 게 제일 낫겠다 싶었어. 우린 크럼믈스네 하숙집에서 식을 올릴 거야. 자네가 와 주면 정말 기쁘겠어. 식 전이든 아침 식사 때든, 자네 좋을 대로. 돈이 많이 드는 건 아니야, 알지.\" 징수원이 이 점에 대해 행여 오해가 있을까 봐 몹시 안달하며 말했어. \"그냥 머핀에 커피, 그리고 입맛 돋우게 새우 한 마리쯤 곁들이는 정도지, 알겠나.\"\n\n\"네, 네, 알겠어요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"아, 기꺼이 가야죠. 더없이 기쁜 일인걸요. 그 아가씨는 어디 묵고 있어요? 크럼믈스 부인하고요?\"\n\n\"아니, 그건 아니야.\" 징수원이 말했어. \"밤에 그 여자를 재울 데가 마땅찮아서, 자기 아는 사람하고 다른 젊은 아가씨하고 같이 지내고 있어. 둘 다 극단 사람이지.\"\n\n\"스네벨리치 양 말이군요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"그래, 그 이름이야.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 사람들이 신부 들러리를 서겠네요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"그게 말이야.\" 징수원이 울상을 지으며 말했어. \"들러리를 넷이나 세우겠다는 거야. 아무래도 일이 좀 연극처럼 되어 버릴까 봐 걱정이야.\"\n\n\"아, 아니에요, 전혀요.\" 니콜라스가 웃음을 헛기침으로 바꾸려 어설프게 애쓰며 대답했어. \"그 넷이 누군데요? 당연히 스네벨리치 양이고―레드룩 양이고―\"\n\n\"그―그 신동이지.\" 징수원이 끙 앓는 소리를 냈어.\n\n\"하하!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어. \"죄송해요, 제가 왜 웃는지도 모르겠네요―네, 그거 아주 예쁘겠어요―그 신동이라―또 누구죠?\"\n\n\"무슨 젊은 여자 하나 더 있어.\" 징수원이 일어서며 대답했어. \"헨리에타 페토커의 또 다른 친구라더군. 자, 이 일에 대해 아무 말도 안 하게 조심해 주겠지, 응?\"\n\n\"믿으셔도 돼요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"뭐 좀 드시거나 마시지 않으실래요?\"\n\n\"아니.\" 징수원이 말했어. \"입맛이 없어. 결혼 생활이란 게 아주 즐거운 거겠지, 응?\"\n\n\"조금도 의심할 여지가 없죠.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"그래.\" 징수원이 말했어. \"그렇고말고. 아, 그럼. 틀림없지. 잘 자게.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 릴리빅 씨는―이 면담 내내 서두름과 망설임, 자신감과 의심, 애정과 불안, 인색함과 거드름이 뒤섞인 더없이 별난 모습을 보여 주었지―방에 등을 돌리고 나가, 니콜라스가 내키면 혼자 실컷 웃도록 내버려 두었어.\n\n그 사이에 낀 하루가 니콜라스에게 여느 때처럼 평범한 길이의 시간들로 채워진 것처럼 느껴졌는지 어떤지는 굳이 따져 묻지 않더라도, 곧 다가올 예식에 더 직접 얽혀 있는 사람들에게는 그날이 무척이나 빠르게 흘러갔다는 것만은 짚어 둘 만해. 그래서 이튿날 아침 페토커 양이 스네벨리치 양의 방에서 눈을 떴을 때, 그녀는 오늘이 정말로 자기 처지가 바뀌는 그날이라는 걸 무슨 수로도 믿을 수 없다고 선언했지.\n\n\"난 절대로 못 믿겠어.\" 페토커 양이 말했어. \"정말로 못 믿겠다고. 말해 봐야 소용없어. 이런 시련을 끝까지 겪어 낼 마음이 도무지 안 잡혀!\"\n\n이 말을 듣고 스네벨리치 양과 레드룩 양은―두 사람은 자기네 어여쁜 친구의 마음이 이미 삼사 년 전부터 굳어져 있었고, 그동안 언제든 모험에 나설 만한 어엿한 신사를 찾을 수만 있었다면 지금 다가오는 이 절박한 시련을 기꺼이 겪어 냈으리란 걸 너무도 잘 알고 있었지―위로와 굳센 마음을 설교하기 시작했어. 그리고 자격 있는 사람에게 영원한 행복을 안겨 줄 힘이 자기한테 있다는 게 얼마나 자랑스러운 일이냐고, 이런 때에 여자가 꿋꿋함과 체념의 미덕을 갖추는 게 인류 전체의 행복을 위해 얼마나 꼭 필요한 일이냐고 했지. 그리고 자기네로 말하자면 참된 행복은 독신 생활에 있다고 여기며, 그 무엇과도 기꺼이 바꾸지 않을 거라고―그래, 세상 그 어떤 것을 준대도―그래도 (하느님 감사하게도) 행여 그런 때가 온다면, 자기네는 투덜대기엔 도리를 너무 잘 알기에, 그보다는 동료 인간들의 만족과 보답을 위해 하늘이 분명히 점지해 준 운명에 온순하고 겸손한 마음으로 따르기를 바란다고 했어.\n\n\"오랜 인연이며 그런 뭐랄까 하는 것들을 끊어 내야 한다는 게 나한테는 큰 타격일지도 몰라.\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했어. \"하지만 난 따를 거야, 얘. 정말로 따를 거라고.\"\n\n\"나도 그래.\" 레드룩 양이 말했어. \"난 그 멍에를 피하느니 차라리 자청하겠어. 전에도 남자 마음을 여럿 아프게 한 적이 있는데, 그게 참 미안해. 돌이켜 생각하면 끔찍한 일이거든.\"\n\n\"정말 그래.\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했어. \"자, 레드, 얘, 우리가 무슨 일이 있어도 얘 채비를 시켜야지. 안 그러면 너무 늦어. 정말 늦는다니까.\"\n\n이 경건한 설득과, 어쩌면 너무 늦을지 모른다는 두려움이 신부를 떠받쳐 옷 입는 예식을 견디게 해 줬어. 그러고 나서는 진한 차와 브랜디를 번갈아 가며 한 모금씩 먹였지. 후들거리는 다리에 힘을 넣어 좀 더 똑바로 걷게 하려는 거였어.\n\n\"이제 좀 어때, 얘?\" 스네벨리치 양이 물었어.\n\n\"오, 릴리빅!\" 신부가 외쳤어. \"내가 당신을 위해 무슨 일을 겪고 있는지 안다면!\"\n\n\"그야 당연히 그분이 알지, 얘. 절대 잊지 않으실 거야.\" 레드룩 양이 말했어.\n\n\"안 잊을 것 같아?\" 페토커 양이, 정말이지 무대에 어울리는 대단한 솜씨를 뽐내며 외쳤어. \"오, 정말 안 잊을 것 같아? 릴리빅이 그걸 늘 기억할 것 같아? 늘, 늘, 늘 말이야?\"\n\n이 북받치는 감정이 어디까지 치달았을지는 알 수 없는 노릇이었어. 마침 그 순간 스네벨리치 양이 마차가 왔다고 알리지 않았더라면 말이지. 그 말에 신부는 어찌나 화들짝 놀랐던지, 잔뜩 몰려오던 갖가지 심상찮은 증세를 떨쳐 버리고는, 거울로 달려가 옷매무새를 가다듬더니, 차분하게 이 희생을 치를 채비가 됐다고 선언했어.\n\n그래서 사람들은 그녀를 부축해 마차에 태웠어. 그 안에서도 (스네벨리치 양 말마따나) 각성 소금을 연신 들이켜고 브랜디며 다른 순한 강장제를 홀짝이게 하며 그녀를 '추스르게' 했지. 그렇게 단장의 집 문 앞에 닿았는데, 문은 벌써 두 크럼믈스 도련님이 열어 두고 있었어. 둘은 하얀 장식 매듭을 달고, 극단 의상실에서 제일 좋고 제일 화려한 조끼로 차려입었더라고. 이 두 젊은 신사와 들러리들이, 마부의 거듦까지 받아 가며 한데 힘을 모은 끝에, 페토커 양은 기진맥진한 채로 가까스로 이층까지 부축을 받아 올라갔어. 그리고 거기서 젊은 신랑과 마주치자마자, 아주 예의 바르게 까무러쳤지.\n\n\"헨리에타 페토커!\" 징수원이 말했어. \"기운 내요, 내 사랑스러운 사람.\"\n\n페토커 양이 징수원의 손을 꼭 잡았지만, 벅찬 감정에 말문이 막혔어.\n\n\"내 모습이 그렇게 끔찍하오, 헨리에타 페토커?\" 징수원이 말했어.\n\n\"오, 아니, 아니에요.\" 신부가 대꾸했어. \"하지만 그 모든 친구들―내 젊은 날의 사랑스러운 친구들―그들을 다 두고 떠난다는 게―이렇게 충격이라니!\"\n\n이런 슬픔의 말들과 함께, 페토커 양은 젊은 날의 다정한 친구들을 하나하나 꼽기 시작했고, 그 자리에 있는 사람들을 향해 다가와 안아 달라고 청했어. 이걸 마치고 나서는, 크럼믈스 부인이 자기에게 어머니나 다름없었다는 게 떠올랐고, 그다음엔 크럼믈스 씨가 아버지나 다름없었다는 게, 또 그다음엔 크럼믈스 도련님들과 니네타 크럼믈스 양이 형제자매나 다름없었다는 게 떠올랐지. 이 갖가지 추억마다 줄줄이 포옹이 따라붙는 통에 한참이 걸렸고, 결국 너무 늦을까 봐 교회까지 마차를 아주 빨리 몰아야 했어.\n\n행렬은 마차 두 대로 이루어졌어. 첫 번째 마차에는 브라바사 양(네 번째 들러리)과 크럼믈스 부인, 징수원, 그리고 이 자리에서 신랑의 들러리로 뽑힌 폴레어 씨가 탔지. 다른 마차에는 신부와 크럼믈스 씨, 스네벨리치 양, 레드룩 양, 그리고 그 신동이 탔어. 의상은 아름다웠어. 들러리들은 조화로 온통 뒤덮여 있었고, 특히 그 신동은 자기를 모셔 놓은 들고 다니는 꽃 정자에 파묻혀 거의 안 보일 지경이었지. 낭만적인 기질의 레드룩 양은 어느 알 수 없는 영관급 장교의 초상화를 가슴에 달았는데, 그리 오래되지 않은 때에 엄청 싼값에 사들인 거였어. 다른 숙녀들은 진짜에 거의 맞먹는 휘황찬란한 모조 장신구를 여럿 뽐냈고, 크럼믈스 부인은 엄숙하고 음울한 위엄을 풍기며 등장해 보는 이마다 감탄을 자아냈지.\n\n하지만 일행 가운데 누구보다도 더 인상적이고 어울리는 모습을 보인 건 어쩌면 크럼믈스 씨였을 거야. 신부의 아버지 역을 맡은 이 신사는, 기발하고 독창적인 착상에 따라 그 역에 맞게 '분장'을 했어. 흔히 브라운 조지라고 불리는 모양새의 연극용 가발을 쓰고, 거기다 지난 세기풍의 담배색 양복을 차려입었지. 회색 비단 양말에 신발에는 버클까지 달았더라고. 자기가 맡은 인물을 한층 더 그럴듯하게 받쳐 주려고, 그는 감정에 크게 휩싸이기로 작정했어. 그래서 일행이 교회에 들어섰을 때, 이 정 많은 아버지의 흐느낌이 어찌나 가슴을 미어지게 하던지, 좌석 안내인이 식이 시작되기 전에 성구실로 물러나 물 한 잔 마시며 마음을 좀 추스르는 게 좋겠다고 권할 정도였지.\n\n통로를 따라 올라가는 행렬은 아름다웠어. 신부는 네 명의 들러리와 함께, 미리 짜고 연습해 둔 무리를 이루었지. 징수원은 그 뒤를 들러리가 따랐는데, 위층 관람석에 있던 극단 친구 몇몇이 형언 못 할 만큼 재미있어할 정도로 징수원의 걸음걸이와 몸짓을 흉내 냈어. 크럼믈스 씨는 약하고 비틀거리는 걸음으로, 크럼믈스 부인은 성큼 한 발 내딛고 멈추기를 번갈아 하는 그 무대용 걸음으로 나아갔지. 여태껏 본 적 없는 더없이 완벽한 장면이었어. 식은 아주 후딱 끝났고, 참석한 사람들이 모두 혼인부에 서명을 마치자(이를 위해 자기 차례가 오자 크럼믈스 씨는 어마어마하게 큰 안경을 정성껏 닦아서 꼈지), 다들 기분이 한껏 들떠 아침 식사를 하러 돌아왔어. 그리고 여기서 그들은 자기네가 오기를 기다리고 있던 니콜라스를 만났지.\n\n\"자, 그럼.\" 크럼믈스가 말했어. 그는 그러든 부인을 도와 준비를 거들고 있었는데, 그 준비가 징수원이 흡족해할 만한 수준보다 좀 더 푸짐한 규모였지. \"아침 식사, 아침 식사.\"\n\n두 번 청할 것도 없었어. 사람들은 식탁에 되도록 빽빽이 끼어 비집고 앉아서는 곧장 음식에 달려들었지. 페토커 양은 누가 쳐다보면 얼굴을 잔뜩 붉혔고, 아무도 안 볼 때는 잔뜩 먹어 댔어. 그리고 릴리빅 씨는, 어차피 이 맛있는 음식 값을 자기가 치러야 하니 크럼믈스네가 나중에 먹어 치울 게 되도록 적게 남게 하겠다고 작정한 듯, 냉정하게 음식에 손을 댔지.\n\n\"이거 금방 끝나는 거지요, 선생님, 안 그래요?\" 폴레어 씨가 식탁 너머로 몸을 기울여 징수원에게 물었어.\n\n\"뭐가 금방 끝난다는 건가, 자네?\" 릴리빅 씨가 되물었어.\n\n\"매듭짓는 거―아내한테 자기를 묶는 거 말이에요.\" 폴레어 씨가 대답했어. \"오래 안 걸리잖아요, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"안 걸리지, 자네.\" 릴리빅 씨가 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했어. \"오래 안 걸려. 그래서 뭐 어쨌다는 건가, 자네?\"\n\n\"아! 아무것도요.\" 배우가 말했어. \"사람이 목매다는 것도 오래 안 걸리잖아요, 안 그래요? 하하!\"\n\n릴리빅 씨는 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고 분개한 놀라움에 차서 식탁을 둘러보았어.\n\n\"목을 매단다고!\" 릴리빅 씨가 되받았어.\n\n깊은 침묵이 모두에게 내려앉았어. 릴리빅 씨가 말로 다 못 할 만큼 위엄에 차 있었거든.\n\n\"목을 매단다고!\" 릴리빅 씨가 다시 외쳤어. \"이 자리에서 결혼과 목매다는 걸 견주려는 셈인가?\"\n\n\"올가미 말이에요, 그러니까.\" 폴레어 씨가 조금 풀이 죽어서 말했어.\n\n\"올가미라고, 자네?\" 릴리빅 씨가 되받았어. \"감히 누가 나한테, 그리고 헨리에타 페―와 한 입으로 올가미 얘기를 한단 말인가―\"\n\n\"릴리빅.\" 크럼믈스 씨가 거들었어.\n\n\"―그리고 헨리에타 릴리빅을 한 입으로?\" 징수원이 말했어. \"이 집에서, 재능 있고 덕망 있는 식구를 길러 내 축복이며 신동이며 뭐며 다 이뤄 낸 크럼믈스 씨 부부 앞에서, 우리가 올가미 얘기를 들어야 한단 말인가?\"\n\n\"폴레어.\" 크럼믈스 씨가 자기와 아내를 빗댄 이 말에 감동받는 게 도리라 여기고 말했어. \"자네한테 놀랐네.\"\n\n\"왜들 이렇게 나한테 몰아붙이는 거예요?\" 가엾은 배우가 따졌어. \"내가 뭘 어쨌다고?\"\n\n\"어쨌느냐고, 자네!\" 릴리빅 씨가 외쳤어. \"사회의 골조 전체에 한 방 먹였잖나―\"\n\n\"그리고 가장 곱고 가장 여린 감정에도.\" 크럼믈스가 다시 그 노인 역으로 빠져들며 덧붙였어.\n\n\"그리고 가장 드높고 가장 값진 사회적 인연에도 말이야.\" 징수원이 말했어. \"올가미라니! 제 발로 들어가서 그 일을 영광스러워하는 게 아니라, 마치 누가 덫에 걸려 결혼이라는 처지에 빠지고, 다리를 핀으로 꽂힌 것처럼 말이지!\"\n\n\"덫에 걸리고 잡혀서 다리가 핀에 꽂혔다는 뜻으로 한 말은 아니에요.\" 배우가 대답했어. \"죄송합니다. 더는 할 말이 없네요.\"\n\n\"그래야 마땅하지, 자네.\" 릴리빅 씨가 대꾸했어. \"그리고 자네한테 그럴 만한 감정이 아직 남아 있다니 다행이군.\"\n\n이 대답으로 다툼이 끝나는 듯하자, 릴리빅 부인은 (이제 사람들의 주의가 더는 흐트러지지 않았으니) 지금이 울음을 터뜨려 네 들러리의 부축을 모두 청하기에 가장 알맞은 때라고 여겼어. 그 부축은 곧장 베풀어졌지만, 약간의 소동이 없진 않았지. 방은 좁고 식탁보는 긴 탓에, 첫 움직임에 접시 한 무더기가 식탁에서 쓸려 떨어졌거든. 하지만 그런 건 아랑곳없이, 릴리빅 부인은 다툰 두 사람이 더는 시비를 키우지 않겠다고 약속할 때까지 위로받기를 거부했어. 두 사람은 한참 마지못한 기색을 보인 뒤 그렇게 약속했지. 그때부터 폴레어 씨는 뚱하니 입을 다물고 앉아서, 무슨 말이 나올 때마다 니콜라스의 다리를 꼬집는 걸로 만족했어. 그렇게 말하는 사람도, 그 사람이 내뱉는 소리도 둘 다 경멸한다는 뜻을 나타낸 거지.\n\n연설이 아주 많이 나왔어. 더러는 니콜라스가, 더러는 크럼믈스가, 더러는 징수원이 했지. 크럼믈스 도련님들이 자기네 몫으로 두 번 답례 연설을 했고, 신동이 들러리들을 대표해 한 번 했는데, 거기서 크럼믈스 부인은 눈물을 흘렸어. 노래도 좀 있었지. 레드룩 양과 브라바사 양이 불렀어. 노래가 더 이어졌을지도 모를 일이었지만, 행복한 한 쌍을 라이드행 증기선을 탈 곳까지 태워다 주려고 기다리던 마차꾼이 단호한 전갈을 보내왔거든. 당장 안 나오면 약속한 삯에 일 실링 육 펜스를 더 받아 내고 말겠다는 거였지.\n\n이 절박한 으름장에 모임은 결국 흩어지고 말았어. 더없이 애절한 작별 인사를 마친 뒤, 릴리빅 씨와 그의 신부는 라이드로 떠났어. 거기서 이틀 동안 깊은 칩거에 들어갈 작정이었지. 그리고 그 길에는 갓난아기도 함께 갔는데, 릴리빅 씨가 못 박아 둔 조건에 따라 여행용 들러리로 임명된 거였지. 증기선 사람들이 그 애 덩치에 속아서 반값에 태워 주리란 걸 (그가 미리 알아 두었거든) 노린 거였어.\n\n그날 밤은 공연이 없어서, 크럼믈스 씨는 마실 게 다 동날 때까지 흥을 이어 가겠다고 선언했어. 하지만 니콜라스는 이튿날 저녁 처음으로 로미오를 연기해야 했기에, 그러든 부인의 행동에서 만취의 뚜렷한 징후가 느닷없이 드러나는 통에 잠깐 소란이 인 틈을 타 슬그머니 빠져나왔지.\n\n이렇게 자리를 뜬 데에는 자기 마음 때문만이 아니라 스마이크에 대한 걱정도 한몫했어. 스마이크는 약제사 역을 맡아야 했는데, 아직껏 대사라곤 자기가 무척 배가 고프다는 큰 줄거리 말고는 도무지 머릿속에 넣질 못했거든. 그건 ― 어쩌면 옛 기억 덕분인지 ― 아주 척척 익혔지만 말이야.\n\n\"어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠다, 스마이크.\" 니콜라스가 책을 내려놓으며 말했어. \"이걸 못 외울까 봐 걱정이야, 가엾은 친구.\"\n\n\"나도 못 외울까 봐 걱정이에요.\" 스마이크가 고개를 저으며 말했어. \"형이 만약에―그런데 그러면 형이 너무 수고스러울 텐데.\"\n\n\"뭐?\" 니콜라스가 물었어. \"내 걱정은 말고.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔요.\" 스마이크가 말했어. \"형이 저한테 짧게 토막토막 끊어서 자꾸자꾸 되풀이해 말해 주면, 듣고서 그걸 외울 수 있을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그럴 것 같아?\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어. \"좋아. 누가 먼저 지치나 보자. 난 안 지쳐, 스마이크, 날 믿어. 자, 그럼. 누가 이리 큰 소리로 부르느냐?\"\n\n\"'누가 이리 큰 소리로 부르느냐?'\" 스마이크가 말했어.\n\n\"'누가 이리 큰 소리로 부르느냐?'\" 니콜라스가 되풀이했어.\n\n\"'누가 이리 큰 소리로 부르느냐?'\" 스마이크가 외쳤어.\n\n이렇게 둘은 누가 이리 큰 소리로 부르느냐고 서로에게 묻기를 몇 번이고 되풀이했어. 그러다 스마이크가 그걸 다 외우자 니콜라스는 다음 문장으로 넘어갔고, 그다음엔 한 번에 두 문장, 그다음엔 세 문장, 이런 식으로 계속해 나갔지. 그러다 자정 무렵, 가엾은 스마이크는 자기가 정말로 대사를 좀 기억하기 시작했다는 걸 알고 이루 말할 수 없이 기뻐했어.\n\n이른 아침에 둘은 다시 그 일에 매달렸어. 그리고 이미 이뤄 낸 진척에 한층 자신감을 얻은 스마이크는 더 빠르게, 더 힘차게 나아갔지. 그가 대사를 제법 술술 익히기 시작하자, 니콜라스는 그에게 양손을 배 위에 쫙 펴고 들어와야 한다는 것과, 가끔 배를 문질러야 한다는 것을 알려 줬어. 무대 위 사람들이 뭔가 먹고 싶다는 걸 늘 그렇게 나타내는 정해진 방식에 맞춘 거였지. 아침 연습이 끝나고 둘은 또다시 일에 매달렸고, 부랴부랴 점심을 먹을 때 말고는 쉬지 않다가, 밤에 극장으로 갈 시간이 되어서야 그쳤어.\n\n이보다 더 마음 졸이고, 더 겸손하고, 더 고분고분한 제자를 둔 스승은 없었어. 그리고 이보다 더 참을성 있고, 더 지칠 줄 모르고, 더 사려 깊고, 더 마음 따뜻한 스승을 둔 제자도 없었지.\n\n옷을 다 차려입자마자, 그리고 무대에 서지 않는 막간마다, 니콜라스는 거듭 일러 주었어. 일은 잘 풀렸지. 로미오는 진심 어린 갈채와 끝없는 호평을 받았어. 그리고 스마이크는 관객과 배우 모두에게 한목소리로, 약제사 중의 더없는 왕이자 신동이라는 평을 받았지.\n\n# CHAPTER 26\n\n니클비 양의 마음의 평온에 적잖은 위험이 닥치다\n\n장소는 리젠트 거리에 있는 멋진 개인 거처였어. 시간은 게으르고 굼뜬 사람들에게는 오후 세 시였지만, 흥청대고 활기찬 사람들에게는 아침 첫 시간이었지. 그곳에 있는 사람은 프레더릭 베리소프트 경과 그의 친구 멀버리 호크 경이었어.\n\n이 점잖은 두 신사는 소파 두 개에 나른하게 기대 누워 있었어. 그 사이에는 탁자가 하나 놓여 있었는데, 손도 대지 않은 아침 식사 재료가 어수선하게 잔뜩 흩어져 있었지. 방 안에는 신문이 여기저기 널려 있었지만, 식사처럼 신문도 거들떠보지도 않고 내버려져 있었어. 그렇다고 둘 사이에 대화가 오가서 신문에 눈길 줄 틈이 없었던 것도 아니야. 두 사람 사이엔 단 한마디도 오가지 않았거든. 아무 소리도 나지 않았지. 다만 둘 중 하나가 지끈거리는 머리를 좀 더 편히 누일 자리를 찾느라 몸을 뒤척이며 짜증스러운 소리를 내뱉을 때만 빼고 말이야. 그러면 잠깐 동안 그 새로운 안절부절못함이 곁의 친구한테로 옮아가는 것 같았어.\n\n이런 모습만 봐도 지난밤 흥청망청 놀아 댄 정도를 꽤 또렷하게 짐작할 수 있었어. 어떻게 밤을 보냈는지 보여 주는 다른 흔적이 없었다 해도 말이야. 진흙과 먼지가 잔뜩 묻은 당구공 두 개, 찌그러진 모자 두 개, 목에 더러운 장갑이 칭칭 감긴 샴페인 병 하나 — 공격 무기로 쓸 때 더 단단히 움켜쥐려고 그랬던 거지 — 부러진 지팡이, 뚜껑 없는 명함첩, 빈 지갑, 두 동강 난 시계줄, 반쯤 피우다 만 시가 토막과 그 케케묵은 부스러진 재가 한데 섞인 은화 한 줌. 이런 것들에다 난동과 무질서를 보여 주는 다른 여러 흔적들까지, 지난밤 신사들의 놀이가 어떤 것이었는지 아주 알기 쉽게 일러 주고 있었지.\n\n프레더릭 베리소프트 경이 먼저 입을 열었어. 슬리퍼 신은 발을 바닥에 떨어뜨리고 크게 하품을 하더니, 끙끙대며 겨우 몸을 일으켜 앉았어. 그러고는 흐리멍덩하고 나른한 눈을 친구 쪽으로 돌리고는, 졸린 목소리로 불렀지.\n\n\"여어!\" 멀버리 경이 돌아누우며 대답했어.\n\n\"우리 하루 종일 여기 이러고 누워 있을 거야아?\" 경이 말했어.\n\n\"우리가 달리 뭘 할 수 있을 것 같지도 않은데.\" 멀버리 경이 대꾸했어. \"적어도 지금은 말이야. 오늘 아침엔 내 몸에 기운이라곤 한 톨도 없어.\"\n\n\"기운!\" 베리소프트 경이 소리쳤어. \"지금 당장 죽어 버리는 것보다 더 아늑하고 편안한 게 없을 것 같은 기분이야.\"\n\n\"그럼 왜 안 죽어?\" 멀버리 경이 말했어.\n\n이렇게 묻고는 얼굴을 돌려 버렸어. 그러고는 잠을 청해 보려는 데만 골몰하는 것 같았지.\n\n그의 장래가 촉망되는 친구이자 제자는 의자를 아침 식탁 쪽으로 끌어다 놓고 뭐라도 먹어 보려 했어. 하지만 도저히 안 되겠던지, 어슬렁어슬렁 창가로 갔지. 그러고는 열에 들뜬 머리에 손을 얹은 채 방 안을 어정버정 오갔어. 결국 다시 소파에 몸을 던지고는, 한 번 더 친구를 깨웠지.\n\n\"도대체 뭐가 문제냐고?\" 멀버리 경이 끙 소리를 내며 소파에 똑바로 일어나 앉았어.\n\n멀버리 경은 이 말을 잔뜩 기분 나쁜 투로 내뱉었지만, 그렇다고 가만히 입 다물고 있을 마음은 들지 않는 모양이었어. 몇 번이고 기지개를 켜고는, 부르르 떨면서 \"끔찍하게 춥군.\" 하고 투덜대더니, 아침 식탁에서 한번 먹어 보려고 했지. 그러고는 덜 단련된 친구보다 더 성공을 거두어서, 그 자리에 그대로 눌러앉았어.\n\n\"이봐,\" 멀버리 경이 포크 끝에 음식 한 점을 꽂은 채 멈추고는 말했어. \"우리 니클비네 딸 얘기로 다시 돌아가 볼까, 응?\"\n\n\"어느 니클비? 그 돈놀이꾼 말이야, 아니면 그 처어녀 말이야?\" 베리소프트 경이 물었어.\n\n\"무슨 말인지 알아듣는구먼.\" 멀버리 경이 대꾸했어. \"당연히 그 처녀지.\"\n\n\"네가 그 여자를 찾아 주겠다고 약속했잖아.\" 베리소프트 경이 말했어.\n\n\"그랬지.\" 친구가 받아쳤어. \"그런데 그 뒤로 이 일을 좀 더 생각해 봤거든. 너는 이 일에서 나를 못 믿잖아 — 그러니 네가 직접 그 여자를 찾아내.\"\n\n\"아아니.\" 베리소프트 경이 항의했어.\n\n\"아니, 그래야 해.\" 친구가 대꾸했어. \"네가 직접 그 여자를 찾아내라고. 네가 찾을 수 있을 때 찾으라는 뜻이 아니야 — 나 없이는 네가 그 여자 얼굴 한번 못 본다는 걸 너만큼이나 나도 잘 알아. 아니지. 내 말은 네가 직접 찾아내야 한다는 거야 — 반드시 — 그리고 그 길을 내가 일러 주지.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 너야말로 진짜, 더럽게, 틀림없는, 끝내주는 친구로구먼.\" 젊은 경이 말했어. 이 말에 그는 완전히 기운이 되살아난 듯했지.\n\n\"어떻게 하는지 알려 주지.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어. \"그 처녀가 그날 저녁 식사 자리에 있었던 건 너를 낚으려는 미끼였어.\"\n\n\"설마!\" 젊은 경이 소리쳤어. \"도대체 무슨—\"\n\n\"너를 낚으려는 미끼였다고.\" 친구가 되풀이했어. \"늙은 니클비가 직접 나한테 그렇게 말했어.\"\n\n\"정말 대단한 늙은이로군!\" 베리소프트 경이 외쳤어. \"기막힌 악당이야!\"\n\n\"그래.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어. \"그자는 그 처녀가 영리하고 깜찍한 것임을 알고 있었지—\"\n\n\"영리하다고!\" 젊은 경이 끼어들었어. \"맹세컨대, 호크, 그 여잔 완벽한 미인이야 — 한 폭의 그림, 조각상, 그야말로—맹세컨대 그렇다고!\"\n\n\"글쎄.\" 멀버리 경이 어깨를 으쓱하며, 정말 그렇든 아니든 무심한 척하며 대꾸했어. \"그건 취향 문제지. 내 취향이 네 취향이랑 안 맞는다면, 그거야 잘된 일이고.\"\n\n\"제기랄!\" 경이 따졌어. \"어쨌든 그날 너는 그 여자랑 어지간히 죽이 잘 맞던데. 나는 끼어들 틈도 거의 없었다고.\"\n\n\"한 번쯤은 충분히 그럴 만했지, 한 번쯤은.\" 멀버리 경이 대꾸했어. \"하지만 다시 비위 맞춰 줄 만큼 수고할 가치는 없어. 네가 정말로 그 조카딸을 쫓아가 볼 생각이라면, 그 삼촌한테 말해. 그 여자가 어디 사는지, 어떻게 사는지, 누구랑 사는지 꼭 알아야겠다고. 안 그러면 더 이상 네 거래는 없다고 말이야. 그러면 그자가 냉큼 일러 줄 거야.\"\n\n\"왜 이걸 진작 말 안 했어?\" 베리소프트 경이 물었어. \"나를 이렇게 한 세에월이나 애태우고, 속 끓이고, 비참한 꼴로 질질 끌게 내버려 두고 말이야!\"\n\n\"우선 첫째로, 나도 몰랐거든.\" 멀버리 경이 무심하게 대답했어. \"그리고 둘째로, 네가 그렇게까지 진심인 줄 몰랐고.\"\n\n사실인즉 이래. 랄프 니클비의 저녁 식사 자리 이후로 흐른 동안, 멀버리 호크 경은 케이트가 어디서 그렇게 갑자기 나타났다가 또 어디로 사라졌는지 알아내려고 온갖 수단을 동원해 은밀히 애써 왔어. 하지만 랄프의 도움 없이는 — 그날 화를 내며 헤어진 뒤로 그와는 한 번도 연락을 주고받지 않았거든 — 그 모든 노력이 죄다 헛수고였지. 그래서 멀버리 경은 그 훌륭한 양반한테서 캐낸 자백의 알맹이를 젊은 경에게 알려 주기로 마음먹은 거야. 여기엔 여러 가지 속셈이 작용했는데, 그중에서도 이 미욱한 젊은이가 아는 건 뭐든 자기도 확실히 알아 둔다는 점이 결코 가볍지 않았지. 무엇보다 그 돈놀이꾼의 조카딸을 다시 만나서, 온갖 수완을 다 부려 그 여자의 자존심을 꺾고, 자기를 깔본 데 앙갚음하겠다는 마음이 머릿속에 제일 크게 자리 잡고 있었거든. 이건 영악한 처사였어. 어느 모로 보나 자기한테 이로울 수밖에 없는 수였지. 랄프 니클비가 조카딸을 그런 사람들 틈에 끌어들인 진짜 속셈을 자기가 캐냈다는 사실에다, 그걸 친구한테 이렇게 선뜻 일러 주는 더없는 무사심까지 더하면, 그쪽으로 자기 잇속이 늘 수밖에 없었거든. 그리고 프레더릭 베리소프트 경의 주머니에서 멀버리 호크 경의 주머니로 돈이 (이미 꽤 자주, 또 빠르게 넘어오고 있었지만) 더 수월하게 옮겨 오게 만들 테니까.\n\n멀버리 경은 이렇게 따져 보았어. 그리고 이 계산에 따라, 그와 친구는 곧 랄프 니클비네로 향했지. 거기서 멀버리 경이 직접 짜 놓은 작전을 실행하려는 거였어. 겉으로는 친구의 목적을 이루어 준다는 명분이었지만, 실은 자기 잇속을 채우려는 거였지.\n\n두 사람은 랄프가 집에 혼자 있는 걸 보았어. 랄프가 그들을 응접실로 안내할 때, 그곳에서 벌어졌던 그 장면이 떠오른 모양이었어. 호기심 어린 눈길로 멀버리 경을 흘끔 보았거든. 그런데 멀버리 경은 그저 무심한 미소로만 그 눈길을 받아넘겼지.\n\n세 사람은 마침 진행 중인 돈 문제를 놓고 잠깐 이야기를 나누었어. 그 얘기가 채 끝나기도 전에, 그 귀족 호구가 (친구의 지시에 따라) 좀 멋쩍어하며 랄프와 단둘이 얘기하고 싶다고 했지.\n\n\"단둘이서, 응?\" 멀버리 경이 놀란 척하며 외쳤어. \"오, 아주 좋아. 나는 여기 옆방으로 가 있지. 너무 오래 붙들어 두지나 마, 그것뿐이야.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 멀버리 경은 모자를 집어 들었어. 그러고는 노래 한 자락을 흥얼거리며, 두 응접실 사이를 잇는 문으로 사라지더니 등 뒤로 문을 닫았지.\n\n\"자, 경.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"무슨 일이십니까?\"\n\n\"니클비.\" 그의 고객이, 아까 앉았던 소파에 길게 몸을 누이며 늙은이의 귀에 입을 더 가까이 가져갔어. \"댁의 조카딸이 어찌나 예쁜 처녀던지!\"\n\n\"그렇습니까, 경?\" 랄프가 대꾸했어. \"글쎄요 — 그럴지도요 — 저는 그런 일에 머리를 쓰지 않습니다만.\"\n\n\"그 여자가 끝내주게 멋진 처녀라는 거 너도 알잖아.\" 고객이 말했어. \"그건 너도 알 거야, 니클비. 자, 아니라고는 못 할걸.\"\n\n\"예, 그렇게들 여기는 줄로 압니다.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"사실 그렇다는 걸 알고 있지요. 설령 제가 몰랐다 해도, 경께서는 그런 점에 권위자이시니까요. 그리고 경의 안목은 — 사실 모든 점에서 — 두말할 나위가 없으니까요.\"\n\n이 말이 어떤 비웃는 투로 던져졌는지 알아채지 못할 사람, 또 그 말에 따라붙은 경멸의 눈빛을 못 알아볼 사람은, 그 말을 들은 그 젊은이 말고는 아무도 없었을 거야. 그런데 프레더릭 베리소프트 경은 그 둘 다였어. 그래서 그 말을 칭찬으로 받아들였지.\n\n\"글쎄.\" 그가 말했어. \"네 말이 좀 맞기도 하고, 좀 틀리기도 해 — 둘 다 조금씩이지, 니클비. 나는 이 미인이 어디 사는지 알고 싶어. 한 번 더 그 여자를 몰래 들여다보게 말이야, 니클비.\"\n\n\"실은요—\" 랄프가 평소 말투로 입을 열었어.\n\n\"그렇게 큰 소리로 말하지 마.\" 그가 외쳤어. 배운 대로 그 중요한 대목을 기막히게 해낸 거지. \"호크한테 들리는 거 싫으니까.\"\n\n\"그자가 경의 연적이라는 거, 아십니까?\" 랄프가 그를 날카롭게 쳐다보며 말했어.\n\n\"그자는 늘 그러지, 빌어머어글.\" 고객이 대꾸했어. \"그래서 나는 그자보다 한발 앞서고 싶은 거야. 하하하! 그자는 우리 둘이 자기 빼고 얘기했다는 걸 알면 엄청 까칠하게 굴 거야, 니클비. 그 여자가 어디 사느냐고, 그것뿐이야. 어디 사는지만 말해 줘, 니클비.\"\n\n'걸려들었군.' 랄프가 생각했어. '걸려들었어.'\n\n\"응, 니클비, 응?\" 고객이 다그쳤어. \"그 여자가 어디 살아?\"\n\n\"실은요, 경.\" 랄프가 두 손을 천천히 비비며 말했어. \"말씀드리기 전에 좀 생각을 해 봐야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"아냐, 절대 안 돼, 니클비. 생각 따윈 하지 마.\" 베리소프트 경이 대꾸했어. \"어디냐고?\"\n\n\"아셔 봐야 좋을 게 없습니다.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"그 아이는 정숙하고 반듯하게 자랐지요. 아닌 게 아니라 예쁘긴 하지만, 가난하고, 지켜 줄 사람도 없으니까요! 가엾은 것, 가엾은 것.\"\n\n랄프는 케이트의 처지를 이렇게 짤막하게 늘어놓았는데, 마치 자기 머릿속을 그냥 스쳐 가는 생각일 뿐 입 밖에 낼 마음은 없었던 것처럼 굴었어. 그런데 그 말을 하면서 곁의 사람을 향해 던진 그 약삭빠르고 음흉한 눈길이, 이 어설픈 시늉을 거짓으로 들통내고 말았지.\n\n\"내 말은, 나는 그저 그 여자를 보고 싶을 뿐이라고.\" 고객이 외쳤어. \"남자가 예쁜 여자 좀 본다고 해될 게 뭐 있어, 안 그래? 자, 그 여자가 도대체 어디 살아? 너도 나한테서 한밑천 잡고 있는 거 알잖아, 니클비. 맹세컨대, 이것만 말해 주면 나는 절대 누가 다른 사람한테로 데려간다 해도 절대 안 갈 거야.\"\n\n\"그렇게 약속하시니까, 경.\" 랄프가 마지못한 척하며 말했어. \"그리고 저도 경의 뜻을 들어드리고 싶어 안달이 나 있고, 또 해될 것도 없으니까요 — 해될 게 없지요 — 말씀드리겠습니다. 하지만 이건 경 혼자만 알고 계시는 게 좋습니다. 철저히 혼자만요.\" 랄프는 이렇게 말하며 옆방을 가리키고는, 뜻이 통하게끔 고개를 끄덕였어.\n\n젊은 경도 이 조심이 꼭 필요하다는 데 똑같이 마음을 쓰는 척하자, 랄프는 조카딸이 지금 어디 사는지, 무슨 일을 하는지 알려 주었어. 그러고는 자기가 들은 바로는 그 식구가 이름난 사람들과 사귀고 싶어 안달이 난 것 같다고, 그러니 귀족쯤 되시면 마음만 먹으면 틀림없이 아주 쉽게 자기소개를 할 수 있을 거라고 덧붙였지.\n\n\"경의 목적이 그저 그 아이를 다시 보는 것뿐이라면,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그 방법으로 원하실 때 언제든 이룰 수 있으실 겁니다.\"\n\n베리소프트 경은 랄프의 딱딱하고 거친 손을 여러 번 꼭 쥐며 그 귀띔에 고마움을 표했어. 그러고는 이제 이쯤에서 이야기를 끝내는 게 좋겠다고 속삭이고는, 멀버리 호크 경더러 돌아오라고 불렀지.\n\n\"잠든 줄 알았네.\" 멀버리 경이 못마땅한 기색으로 다시 나타나며 말했어.\n\n\"붙들어 둬서 미안하네.\" 호구가 대꾸했어. \"근데 니클비가 어찌나 우우스운지 도무지 자리를 뜰 수가 있어야지.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 아닙니다.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"다 경께서 그러신 거지요. 프레더릭 경께서 얼마나 재치 있고, 익살스럽고, 품위 있고, 교양 넘치는 분이신지 아시잖습니까. 발밑 조심하십시오, 경 — 멀버리 경, 부디 먼저 가시지요.\"\n\n이런 정중한 인사와 수없이 굽실대는 절을 곁들이며, 그러면서도 줄곧 그 차가운 비웃음을 얼굴에 띤 채, 랄프는 손님들을 아래층으로 배웅하느라 분주했어. 멀버리 호크 경은 이렇게 교양 있고 더없이 완벽한 악당이 되었다며 감탄 어린 눈길로 그를 추켜세우는 듯했는데, 랄프는 입꼬리를 아주 살짝 움직이는 것 말고는 거기에 아무런 대꾸도 내보이지 않았지.\n\n몇 분 전에 초인종이 울렸는데, 그들이 막 현관에 다다랐을 때 뉴먼 노그스가 거기에 응했어. 평소 같으면 뉴먼은 새로 온 사람을 말없이 들이거나, 신사들이 나가는 동안 옆으로 비켜서 있어 달라고 청했을 거야. 그런데 누가 왔는지 보자마자, 뭔가 자기 나름의 속셈이라도 있는 듯, 영업 시간 중 랄프네 저택의 정해진 관례를 대담하게 깨 버렸지. 다가오는 그 점잖은 세 사람 쪽을 바라보며, 크고 우렁찬 목소리로 외친 거야. \"니클비 부인!\"\n\n\"니클비 부인!\" 멀버리 호크 경이 외쳤어. 그러자 친구가 뒤돌아 그의 얼굴을 빤히 쳐다봤지.\n\n정말로 그 마음씨 착한 부인이었어. 도시에 있는 빈집을 사겠다는 제안이 집주인 앞으로 왔는데, 그걸 지체 없이 니클비 씨한테 부랴부랴 들고 온 거였지.\n\n\"자네가 아는 사람이 아니네.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"사무실로 들어가 계시구려, 형—형수님. 곧 가겠습니다.\"\n\n\"제가 아는 사람이 아니라고요!\" 멀버리 호크 경이 어리둥절해하는 부인 쪽으로 다가서며 외쳤어. \"이분이 니클비 부인 — 니클비 양의 어머니 — 제가 이 댁에서 마지막으로 식사하던 바로 그날, 만나 뵙는 행복을 누렸던 그 사랑스러운 분이신가요? 아니, 아니지.\" 멀버리 경이 우뚝 멈추며 말했어. \"아니, 그럴 리가 없어. 똑같은 이목구비에, 똑같이 뭐라 말로 다 할 수 없는 분위기까지 — 하지만 아니야, 아니지. 이분은 그러기엔 너무 젊으셔.\"\n\n\"제부, 그 신사분이 알아 둘 만한 일이라면 직접 말씀드려도 좋겠네요.\" 니클비 부인이 그 칭찬을 우아하게 고개 숙여 받으며 말했어. \"케이트 니클비가 제 딸이라고요.\"\n\n\"부인의 따님이라고요, 경!\" 멀버리 경이 친구 쪽을 돌아보며 외쳤어. \"이분의 따님이라네요, 경.\"\n\n'경이라고?' 니클비 부인이 생각했어. '어머나, 세상에—'\n\n\"그렇다면, 경.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어. \"이분이 바로 그 고마운 결혼을 해 주신 덕에 우리가 이토록 큰 행복을 입게 된 그 부인이로군요. 이분이 그 사랑스러운 니클비 양의 어머니라니. 이 놀라운 닮은꼴이 보이십니까, 경? 니클비 — 우리를 좀 소개해 주시오.\"\n\n랄프는 자포자기한 심정으로 그렇게 했어.\n\n\"맹세컨대, 정말 더없이 기쁜 일입니다.\" 프레더릭 경이 앞으로 나서며 말했어. \"안녕하십니까?\"\n\n니클비 부인은 이 유난히 다정한 인사에 너무 당황한 데다, 다른 보닛을 쓰고 오지 못한 게 못내 아쉬워서, 당장 무슨 대답을 할 수가 없었어. 그저 연신 고개를 숙이고 미소만 지으며, 무척 들뜬 기색을 내비칠 뿐이었지.\n\n\"그—그래, 니클비 양은 어떻게 지내시나요?\" 프레더릭 경이 말했어. \"잘 지내시지요?\"\n\n\"잘 지내요, 고마워요, 경.\" 니클비 부인이 정신을 차리며 대답했어. \"아주 잘 지낸답니다. 그 애가 여기서 식사하던 그날 이후로 며칠 몸이 안 좋았는데, 아무래도 집에 오는 길에 그 삯마차에서 감기에 걸린 것 같다는 생각이 자꾸 들어요. 삯마차란 게, 경, 어찌나 더러운 물건인지, 언제든 차라리 걷는 게 나을 정도예요. 삯마차 마부는 창문이 깨져 있으면 종신 유배형까지 받을 수 있다고 알고 있는데도, 어찌나들 막무가내인지, 거의 다 창문이 깨져 있거든요. 저도 한번은 삯마차를 탔다가 얼굴이 여섯 주나 부어 있었답니다, 경 — 그게 삯마차였던 것 같아요.\" 니클비 부인이 곰곰이 생각하며 말했어. \"그게 사륜마차가 아니었는지는 확실치 않지만요. 어쨌든 짙은 초록색이었고, 번호가 아주 길었는데, 영으로 시작해서 구로 끝났어요 — 아니, 구로 시작해서 영으로 끝났던가, 그게 맞아요. 그러니까 누가 마차 등록소에 물어보면 그게 삯마차였는지 사륜마차였는지 단번에 알 텐데요 — 아무튼 그게, 창문이 깨진 채로 거기 있었고, 저는 얼굴이 부은 채로 여섯 주를 보냈답니다 — 그게 바로 그 삯마차였던 것 같아요. 나중에 알고 보니 그동안 내내 지붕이 열려 있었던 건데, 지붕 열어 둔 값이라며 한 시간에 일 실링씩 더 물리지만 않았어도 우리는 그런 줄도 몰랐을 거예요. 그게 법이라나요, 적어도 그때는요. 참 부끄러운 법인 것 같아요 — 제가 그 일은 잘 모르지만, 그 의회 법에 비하면 곡물법쯤은 아무것도 아닐 거예요.\"\n\n이쯤에서 니클비 부인은 어지간히 할 말이 다 떨어졌는지, 시작할 때처럼 갑자기 입을 다물었어. 그러고는 케이트가 아주 잘 지낸다는 말을 다시 한번 했지. \"정말이지,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"백일해랑 성홍열이랑 홍역을 한꺼번에 앓고 난 뒤로, 지금처럼 건강한 적이 없었던 것 같아요. 정말이에요.\"\n\n\"그 편지, 나한테 온 거요?\" 랄프가 니클비 부인이 손에 든 작은 꾸러미를 가리키며 으르렁대듯 말했어.\n\n\"제부한테 온 거예요.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어. \"이걸 드리려고 일부러 여기까지 그 먼 길을 걸어왔답니다.\"\n\n\"여기까지 그 먼 길을요!\" 멀버리 경이, 니클비 부인이 어디서 왔는지 알아낼 기회를 잡으며 외쳤어. \"지긋지긋하게 먼 거리지요! 자, 그게 얼마나 멀다고 보십니까?\"\n\n\"얼마나 멀다고 보냐고요?\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"어디 보자. 우리 집 문에서 올드 베일리까지 딱 일 마일이에요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니요. 그 정도까진 안 됩니다.\" 멀버리 경이 우겼어.\n\n\"어머! 정말 그렇다니까요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"여기 경께 여쭤볼게요.\"\n\n\"제가 보기엔 분명 일 마일은 됩니다.\" 프레더릭 경이 엄숙한 얼굴로 한마디 했어.\n\n\"틀림없어요. 일 야드도 모자라지 않을걸요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"뉴게이트 거리를 쭉 내려가서, 칩사이드를 쭉 내려가서, 롬바드 거리를 쭉 올라가서, 그레이스처치 거리를 내려가서, 템스 거리를 따라 스피그위핀스 부두까지요. 어머! 일 마일은 된다니까요.\"\n\n\"예, 다시 생각해 보니 그렇겠군요.\" 멀버리 경이 대꾸했어. \"그런데 설마 그 먼 길을 다시 걸어서 돌아가실 생각은 아니시겠지요?\"\n\n\"어머, 아니에요.\" 니클비 부인이 받아쳤어. \"옴니버스를 타고 돌아갈 거예요. 제 가엾은 남편 니콜라스가 살아 있을 땐 옴니버스 같은 거 타고 돌아다니지 않았답니다, 제부. 하지만 사정이 이러니까요, 아시잖아요—\"\n\n\"예, 예.\" 랄프가 못 참겠다는 듯 대꾸했어. \"그러니 날 저물기 전에 돌아가시는 게 좋겠소.\"\n\n\"고마워요, 제부, 그래야겠네요.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어. \"지금 당장 작별 인사를 하는 게 낫겠어요.\"\n\n\"좀 머물다 — 쉬어 가지 않고요?\" 랄프가 말했어. 그는 뭔가 얻을 게 있지 않고서야 좀처럼 음식 대접 같은 건 권하지 않았지.\n\n\"어머, 무슨, 됐어요.\" 니클비 부인이 시계판을 흘끔 보며 대답했어.\n\n\"프레더릭 경.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어. \"우리도 니클비 부인 가시는 길로 가는 참이잖아. 옴니버스까지 무사히 모셔다 드릴까?\"\n\n\"물론이지. 그—러—세.\"\n\n\"어머! 정말이지 그러실 것까진 없는데요!\" 니클비 부인이 말했어.\n\n하지만 멀버리 호크 경과 베리소프트 경은 막무가내로 정중하게 굴었어. 그래서 랄프는 — 이런 일에 끼어드느니 그냥 구경꾼으로 있는 편이 덜 우스워 보인다고 여긴 모양인데, 그게 그리 어리석은 생각은 아니었지 — 남겨 둔 채, 둘은 니클비 부인을 가운데 끼고 집을 나섰어. 그 착한 부인은 더없는 만족감에 푹 빠져 있었지. 작위 가진 두 신사가 자기한테 이렇게 떠받들어 주는 게 흐뭇하기도 했지만, 이제 케이트가 적어도 두 큰 재산과, 흠잡을 데 없는 두 신랑감 사이에서 골라잡을 수 있겠구나 하는 확신이 못지않게 기뻤거든.\n\n니클비 부인이 잠시 딸의 앞날에 펼쳐질 영화와 얽힌 거역할 수 없는 생각의 흐름에 빠져 넋이 나가 있는 사이, 멀버리 호크 경과 그의 친구는 그 가엾은 부인이 집에 두고 오지 않은 걸 그토록 아쉬워하던 보닛 너머로 눈짓을 주고받았어. 그러고는 잔뜩 황홀해하면서도, 한껏 정중한 태도로 니클비 양의 갖가지 빼어난 점에 대해 늘어놓기 시작했지.\n\n\"이렇게 사랑스러운 아가씨가 부인께는 얼마나 큰 기쁨이고, 위안이고, 행복이겠습니까.\" 멀버리 경이 더없이 따뜻한 감정이 묻어나게 목소리를 꾸미며 말했어.\n\n\"정말 그렇답니다, 선생님.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어. \"그 애는 더없이 상냥하고, 마음씨 곱고 — 게다가 어찌나 똑똑한지요!\"\n\n\"똑똑해 보이긴 하더군요.\" 베리소프트 경이 똑똑함을 가리는 감정가라도 되는 듯한 투로 말했어.\n\n\"틀림없다니까요, 경.\" 니클비 부인이 받아쳤어. \"그 애가 데번셔에서 학교에 다닐 때, 거기서 두말할 것 없이 가장 똑똑한 아이라고 다들 인정했답니다. 게다가 똑똑한 아이들도 아주 많았는데 말이에요. 정말이라니까요 — 아가씨가 스물다섯 명, 한 해 학비가 잡비 빼고 오십 기니에, 다우들 집안 자매도 둘이나 있었는데 더없이 교양 있고, 우아하고, 매력적인 아이들이었지요 — 어머나!\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"그 애가 그 학교에 다닐 때 저랑 가엾은 그 애 아빠한테 안겨 준 기쁨을 저는 절대 못 잊을 거예요, 절대로 — 반년마다 그렇게 기쁜 편지가 왔답니다. 자기가 학교 전체에서 일등이고, 누구보다 더 많이 발전했다고요! 지금 생각해도 가슴이 벅차서 견디기가 힘들 정도예요. 편지는 아이들이 다 직접 썼어요.\" 니클비 부인이 덧붙였어. \"글씨 선생님이 나중에 돋보기랑 은펜으로 손을 봐 주긴 했지만요. 적어도 아이들이 썼다고는 생각하는데, 케이트는 그것조차 영 확실치가 않대요. 자기 글씨를 다시 봐도 못 알아보겠다나요. 어쨌든 그게 다들 베껴 쓴 똑같은 회람용 편지였던 건 알아요. 그래도 물론 아주 기쁜 일이었지요 — 정말 기쁜 일이었어요.\"\n\n니클비 부인은 이런 추억들로 지루한 길을 달래 가며, 마침내 옴니버스에 다다랐어. 새 친구들은 어찌나 극진히 정중한지, 마차가 실제로 출발할 때까지 자리를 뜨려 하지 않았지. 그러고는 출발하는 순간, 모자를 — 니클비 부인이 그 뒤로 여러 차례 듣는 이들에게 엄숙히 장담했듯이 — \"완전히 벗어 들고,\" 그 짚빛 새끼 염소가죽 장갑이 더 이상 보이지 않을 때까지 입을 맞췄어.\n\n니클비 부인은 마차의 가장 안쪽 구석에 등을 기댔어. 그러고는 눈을 감고, 더없이 흐뭇한 온갖 상념에 몸을 내맡겼지. 케이트는 이 신사들 중 누구를 만났다는 말을 한마디도 한 적이 없었어. '저건,' 부인은 생각했어. '저 애가 둘 중 한 사람한테 단단히 마음이 기울었다는 뜻이지.' 그러자 그게 둘 중 누구일까 하는 물음이 떠올랐어. 경이 더 젊었고, 작위도 분명 더 대단했지. 그래도 케이트는 그런 걸 따져서 마음이 흔들릴 애가 아니었어. '나는 그 애 마음에 절대 억지를 부리지 않을 거야.' 니클비 부인은 혼자 생각했어. '하지만 정말이지, 그 경하고 멀버리 경은 비교가 안 되는 것 같단 말이야 — 멀버리 경은 어찌나 자상하고 신사다운 분인지, 풍채도 좋고, 인물도 훤하고, 또 말솜씨도 좋고. 멀버리 경이었으면 좋겠어 — 멀버리 경이 틀림없어!' 그러자 부인의 생각은 오래전 자기가 했던 예언으로 날아갔어. 케이트가 한 푼 없이도, 수천 파운드를 가진 남의 집 딸들보다 더 좋은 데로 시집갈 거라고 몇 번이나 말했던가 하는 거였지. 그리고 가난한 새 삶의 고난과 시련을 그토록 씩씩하게 헤쳐 온 그 가엾은 딸의 온갖 아름다움과 우아함을 어머니의 환한 상상으로 그려 보자니, 가슴이 너무 벅차올라 눈물이 주르륵 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸어.\n\n한편 랄프는 작은 뒤쪽 사무실을 이리저리 거닐고 있었어. 방금 일어난 일로 마음이 어지러웠거든. 랄프가 — 그 말의 가장 평범한 뜻으로 — 신이 지으신 어느 한 사람이라도 사랑했다거나 아꼈다고 하면, 그건 더없이 터무니없는 꾸며 낸 이야기일 거야. 그래도 어쩐 일인지, 이따금 조카딸 생각이 동정과 연민이 어린 채로 그의 마음에 슬며시 스며들곤 했어. 그의 눈에 비치는 남자며 여자를 죄다 어둡게 덮어 버리는 그 칙칙한 미움과 무관심의 구름을 뚫고, 그 아이의 경우엔 아주 희미한 한 줄기 빛이 새어 들었던 거야 — 아무리 좋을 때라도 더없이 가냘프고 병약한 빛줄기였지만 — 어쨌든 그건 거기 있었어. 그리고 그 빛은 그 가엾은 아이를, 그가 여태 인간 본성을 바라보며 보아 온 그 어떤 모습보다도 더 곱고 더 맑은 모습으로 비춰 주었지.\n\n'내가,' 랄프는 생각했어. '이 짓을 하지 말걸 그랬어. 그래도 돈벌이가 되는 한, 이걸로 그 애송이를 내 곁에 붙들어 둘 수 있겠지. 계집애를 팔아넘기는 거지 — 유혹과 모욕과 상스러운 말 앞에 그 애를 내던지는 거야. 하지만 벌써 그 녀석한테서 이천 파운드 가까이 벌었잖아. 흥! 딸 시집보내려는 어미들이 날마다 하는 짓이 바로 이거야.'\n\n그는 자리에 앉아, 손가락을 꼽아 가며 이러쿵저러쿵 가능성을 따져 보았어.\n\n'오늘 내가 그자들을 옳은 길로 들여놓지 않았어도,' 랄프는 생각했어. '저 어리석은 여편네가 그렇게 했을 거야. 그래. 그 딸이 내가 본 대로 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 제 몸을 지킬 줄 안다면, 무슨 해가 생기겠어? 약간의 놀림, 약간의 망신, 눈물 몇 방울이지. 그래.' 랄프가 쇠금고를 잠그며 소리 내어 말했어. '그 애도 운에 맡겨야지. 운에 맡겨야 하고말고.'\n\n# CHAPTER 27\n\n니클비 부인은 집에 돌아온 뒤로 며칠 동안 느껴 보지 못한 자부심과 우쭐함에 흠뻑 젖었어. 집으로 오는 내내 함께했던 그 즐거운 상상에 푹 빠져 있었거든. 멀버리 호크 부인 — 머릿속을 떠나지 않는 생각이 바로 그거였지. 멀버리 호크 부인! 지난 화요일, 하노버 광장의 세인트 조지 교회에서 랜다프 주교의 주례로, 북웨일스 멀버리 성의 멀버리 호크 경이, 데번셔의 고 니콜라스 니클비 씨의 외동딸 캐서린과 혼인하였습니다. \"어머나!\" 니콜라스 니클비 부인이 외쳤어. \"참 그럴듯하게 들리네.\"\n\n식과 그에 딸린 잔치까지 흡족하게 마음속에서 다 치르고 나자, 희망에 부푼 어머니는 케이트가 새로 발 디딜 그 눈부신 세상에서 누리게 될 온갖 영예와 영광을 길게 그려 보았어. 물론 케이트는 궁중에 소개되겠지. 케이트의 생일은 칠월 십구일인데 (니클비 부인은 괄호 치듯 속으로 덧붙였어. '새벽 세 시 십 분이었지. 몇 시냐고 물어봤던 게 기억나니까'), 그날이 돌아오면 멀버리 경은 소작인들 모두에게 성대한 잔치를 베풀 거야. 그리고 지난 반년 치 소작료의 삼 점 오 퍼센트를 돌려주겠지. 그 모든 게 사교계 소식란에 빠짐없이 실리고 기록되어, 읽는 이들마다 한없는 기쁨과 감탄에 잠길 테고. 케이트의 초상화도 적어도 대여섯 권의 화보집에 실릴 거야. 그리고 그 맞은편 면에는 고운 활자로 이렇게 적혀 있겠지. '멀버리 호크 부인의 초상을 바라보며 짓다. 딩글비 대버 경.' 어쩌면 그 가운데 한 권쯤은, 다른 것들보다 더 폭넓은 구성을 자랑하느라, 멀버리 호크 부인의 어머니 초상화까지 실어 줄지도 몰라. 거기엔 딩글비 대버 경의 아버지가 지은 시가 붙어 있겠지. 이보다 더 있을 법하지 않은 일도 일어나곤 했잖아. 이보다 덜 흥미로운 초상화도 실린 적이 있었고. 이런 생각이 떠오르자 이 착한 부인의 얼굴에는 저도 모르게 그런 묘한 표정이 떠올랐어. 새침하게 웃는 듯하면서도 졸린 듯한 표정 말이야. 그런 초상화에는 죄다 그런 표정이 담기는데, 어쩌면 그래서 그런 초상화들이 늘 그렇게 매력적이고 보기 좋은지도 몰라.\n\n니클비 부인은 랄프의 지체 높은 친구들에게 우연히 소개받은 그날 저녁 내내 이런 허공에 짓는 누각의 영광에 푹 잠겨 있었어. 그리고 그날 밤 잠자리에서도 그 못지않게 예언 같고 희망찬 꿈이 그녀를 떠나지 않았지. 다음 날 부인은 여전히 같은 생각에 잠긴 채 — 잠과 햇빛에 어쩌면 조금쯤 누그러졌겠지만 — 소박한 저녁상을 차리고 있었어. 그때 부인의 시중을 드는 처녀 아이가 — 말동무도 되고 집안일도 거들려고 두었지 — 평소답지 않게 잔뜩 들떠서 방으로 뛰어들어 왔어. 그러고는 신사 두 분이 복도에서 위층으로 올라가도 되는지 허락을 기다리고 있다고 알렸지.\n\n\"어머나 세상에!\" 니클비 부인이 외치며 모자와 앞머리를 부랴부랴 매만졌어. \"혹시 그분들이라면 — 아이고, 이 한참을 복도에 세워 두다니 — 너는 어째서 가서 올라오시라고 안 했니, 이 멍청한 것아?\"\n\n처녀 아이가 그 심부름을 하러 간 사이, 니클비 부인은 먹고 마신 흔적을 죄다 후다닥 찬장 속에 쓸어 넣었어. 그러고는 채 자리에 앉아 최대한 차분한 표정을 짓기가 무섭게, 둘 다 생판 모르는 신사 둘이 모습을 드러냈지.\n\n\"안녕하시오?\" 한 신사가 물음의 끝말에 잔뜩 힘을 주며 말했어.\n\n\"안녕하시오?\" 다른 신사가 강세를 바꿔서 말했어. 인사에 변화를 주려는 모양이었지.\n\n니클비 부인은 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고 미소 지었어. 그러고는 또 한 번 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는, 두 손을 비비며 이렇게 운을 뗐어. 자기는 — 정말이지 — 두 분을 알게 되는 영광을 —\n\n\"누리지 못했다는 말씀이지요.\" 첫 번째 신사가 말했어. \"손해는 저희 쪽이 봤습니다, 니클비 부인. 손해를 본 게 우리 쪽이지, 파이크?\"\n\n\"그렇고말고, 플럭.\" 다른 신사가 대답했어.\n\n\"우리가 그걸 참 자주 안타까워했지, 그렇지, 파이크?\" 첫 번째 신사가 말했어.\n\n\"아주 자주 그랬지, 플럭.\" 두 번째 신사가 대답했어.\n\n\"하지만 이제는,\" 첫 번째 신사가 말했어. \"이제는 그토록 애타게 기다리던 행복을 누리게 됐어. 우리가 이 행복을 애타게 기다려 왔는가, 안 기다려 왔는가, 파이크?\"\n\n\"기다려 왔다는 걸 자네도 알잖나, 플럭.\" 파이크가 나무라듯 말했어.\n\n\"들으셨지요, 부인?\" 플럭 씨가 둘러보며 말했어. \"제 친구 파이크의 흠잡을 데 없는 증언을 들으셨지요 — 아, 그러고 보니, 격식이지요, 격식을 차리는 건 문명 사회에서 소홀히 해서는 안 되는 거니까요. 파이크 — 니클비 부인이시네.\"\n\n파이크 씨는 가슴에 손을 얹고 깊이 허리를 숙였어.\n\n\"제 소개도 똑같이 격식을 차려서 할지,\" 플럭 씨가 말했어. \"제 이름이 플럭이라고 제 입으로 말씀드릴지, 아니면 제 친구 파이크에게 — 이제 정식으로 소개를 받았으니 그럴 자격이 있지요 — 제 이름이 플럭이라고 부인께 대신 말해 달라고 부탁할지, 부인의 안녕에 제가 품은 그 깊은 관심을 그냥 내세워 알고 지내자고 청할지, 아니면 멀버리 호크 경의 친구로 저를 소개할지 — 니클비 부인, 이런 건 다 부인께서 정하시도록 맡기겠습니다.\"\n\n\"멀버리 호크 경의 친구라면 저한테는 그보다 더 좋은 소개가 필요 없지요.\" 니클비 부인이 너그럽게 말했어.\n\n\"그렇게 말씀해 주시니 정말 기쁩니다.\" 플럭 씨가 의자를 니클비 부인 가까이 끌어다 앉으며 말했어. \"제 훌륭한 친구 멀버리 경을 그렇게 높이 우러러보신다니 마음이 다 시원해지는군요. 귀에 대고 한마디만 하겠습니다, 니클비 부인. 멀버리 경이 이걸 알면 행복해할 겁니다 — 제 말씀은요, 니클비 부인, 행복한 사람이 될 거라고요. 파이크, 앉게나.\"\n\n\"제가 좋게 본다는 게,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. 그리고 이 가엾은 부인은 자기가 놀랍도록 영리하다는 생각에 우쭐했지. \"제가 좋게 본다는 게 멀버리 경 같은 신사분께야 별 대수롭지도 않은 일이겠지요.\"\n\n\"별 대수롭지 않다니요!\" 플럭 씨가 외쳤어. \"파이크, 니클비 부인이 좋게 봐 주시는 게 우리 친구 멀버리 경한테 얼마나 대수로운 일인가?\"\n\n\"얼마나 대수로운 일이냐고?\" 파이크가 따라 외쳤어.\n\n\"그래,\" 플럭이 다시 말했어. \"그게 무엇보다 대수로운 일이지 않나?\"\n\n\"무엇보다 더없이 대수로운 일이지.\" 파이크가 대답했어.\n\n\"니클비 부인께서도 모르실 리 없겠지요,\" 플럭 씨가 말했어. \"그 사랑스러운 따님이 남긴 그 엄청난 인상을 —\"\n\n\"플럭!\" 친구가 말했어. \"조심하게!\"\n\n\"파이크 말이 맞아.\" 플럭 씨가 잠깐 입을 다물었다가 중얼거렸어. \"그 얘긴 꺼내면 안 되는 거였지. 파이크 말이 아주 맞아. 고맙네, 파이크.\"\n\n'어머나, 이거 정말,' 니클비 부인은 속으로 생각했어. '저런 섬세함은 내 평생 처음 보네!'\n\n플럭 씨는 몇 분 동안 몹시 난처한 척하더니, 다시 말문을 열었어. 자기가 무심코 한 말은 부디 마음에 두지 말아 달라고 니클비 부인에게 사정했지. 자기를 경솔하고, 성급하고, 분별없는 사람으로 여겨 달라면서 말이야. 다만 자기에게 유리하게 한 가지만 부탁한다면, 자기 뜻만큼은 더없이 좋았다고 믿어 달라는 거였어.\n\n\"하지만,\" 플럭 씨가 말했어. \"이쪽에는 이토록 사랑스럽고 아름다운 분이 계시고, 저쪽에는 이토록 뜨겁고 헌신적인 마음이 있는 걸 보면, 저는 — 미안하네, 파이크, 그 얘길 또 꺼낼 생각은 없었어. 화제를 바꾸세, 파이크.\"\n\n\"멀버리 경과 프레더릭 경께 약속드렸거든요,\" 파이크가 말했어. \"오늘 아침에 들러서 어젯밤에 감기라도 드시지 않았는지 여쭤보겠다고요.\"\n\n\"어젯밤엔 조금도 그런 일 없었답니다, 선생님.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어. \"이렇게 여쭤봐 주시는 영광을 베푸시니 경과 멀버리 경께 깊이 감사드려요. 조금도 그런 일 없었어요 — 사실 더 신기한 게, 저는 정말이지 감기에 아주 잘 걸리거든요, 정말 잘 걸려요. 한번은 감기에 걸린 적이 있었는데,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"아마 천팔백십칠 년이었지 싶어요. 어디 보자, 사하고 오를 더하면 구고, 그리고 — 그래요, 천팔백십칠 년이었어요. 도무지 떨어지질 않을 줄 알았다니까요. 정말이지 진지하게, 영영 못 나을 줄 알았어요. 결국은 어떤 치료법으로 겨우 나았는데, 플럭 씨, 혹시 들어 보셨는지 모르겠네요. 견딜 수 있는 한 최대로 뜨거운 물 한 갤런에다, 소금 한 파운드하고, 육 펜스어치 고운 밀기울을 넣어요. 그러고는 매일 밤 잠들기 직전에 거기에 머리를 담그고 이십 분 동안 앉아 있는 거예요. 아니, 머리가 아니라 발을요. 정말 놀라운 치료법이에요 — 정말 놀라운 치료법이지요. 크리스마스 다음 날 처음 해 봤던 게 기억나는데, 이듬해 사월 중순쯤 되니까 감기가 싹 가셨더라고요. 가만 생각해 보면 거의 기적 같은 일이에요. 그 감기를 구월 초부터 달고 있었으니까요.\"\n\n\"이렇게 가슴 아픈 재난이 다 있나요!\" 파이크 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"정말 끔찍하네요!\" 플럭 씨가 외쳤어.\n\n\"하지만 니클비 부인이 다 나으셨다는 것만 알아도 그 고생담을 들을 만한 가치가 있지 않나, 플럭?\" 파이크 씨가 외쳤어.\n\n\"바로 그 점 때문에 이 이야기가 그토록 짜릿하게 흥미로운 거지.\" 플럭 씨가 대답했어.\n\n\"하지만 이런,\" 파이크가 갑자기 정신이 든 듯 말했어. \"이렇게 즐겁게 만나 뵙느라 우리 임무를 잊어선 안 되지. 저희는 임무를 띠고 왔답니다, 니클비 부인.\"\n\n\"임무라고요.\" 이 착한 부인이 외쳤어. 그녀의 머릿속에는 곧장 케이트에게 들어온 분명한 청혼이 생생한 빛깔로 떠올랐지.\n\n\"멀버리 경께서 보내셨어요.\" 파이크가 대답했어. \"여기서 무척 적적하시겠지요.\"\n\n\"좀 적적하긴 해요, 솔직히.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"멀버리 호크 경의 인사를 전해 드리려고요. 그리고 오늘 밤 연극을 보러 개인 관람석에 앉아 주십사 천 번이고 간청하신답니다.\" 플럭 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"어머나!\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"저는 외출이라곤 통 안 해요, 도통 안 한다니까요.\"\n\n\"바로 그게, 니클비 부인, 오늘 밤 외출을 하셔야 할 이유랍니다.\" 플럭 씨가 받아쳤어. \"파이크, 니클비 부인께 간청드리게.\"\n\n\"오, 제발 그렇게 해 주세요.\" 파이크가 말했어.\n\n\"꼭 그러셔야 합니다.\" 플럭이 채근했어.\n\n\"두 분 참 친절하시네요.\" 니클비 부인이 머뭇거리며 말했어. \"하지만 —\"\n\n\"이 일에 '하지만'은 없습니다, 니클비 부인.\" 플럭 씨가 따지듯 말했어. \"사전에 그런 말은 없는 거예요. 시동생분도 함께하시고, 프레더릭 경도 함께하시고, 멀버리 경도 함께하시고, 파이크도 함께합니다 — 거절은 있을 수 없어요. 멀버리 경께서 부인을 모시러 마차를 보내실 겁니다 — 일곱 시 이십 분 전에 딱 맞춰서요 — 일행 전부를 실망시키실 만큼 매정하진 않으시겠지요, 니클비 부인?\"\n\n\"하도 채근하시니 무슨 말을 해야 할지 모르겠네요.\" 그 갸륵한 부인이 대답했어.\n\n\"아무 말씀도 마세요. 한마디도, 한마디도 마세요, 더없이 친애하는 부인.\" 플럭 씨가 채근했어. \"니클비 부인.\" 이 훌륭한 신사가 목소리를 낮추며 말했어. \"지금 제가 드리려는 말씀은 더없이 사소한, 더없이 용서받을 만한 비밀 누설이긴 합니다만. 그래도 저기 제 친구 파이크가 엿듣기라도 하면 — 저 사람은 명예에 관한 한 어찌나 섬세한지요, 니클비 부인 — 저녁 식사 전에 저를 결투장으로 끌어낼 겁니다.\"\n\n니클비 부인은 호전적인 파이크 쪽을 불안한 눈길로 흘끔거렸어. 파이크는 창가로 걸어가 있었지. 그러자 플럭 씨가 부인의 손을 꼭 쥐고는 말을 이었어.\n\n\"따님께서 한 사람의 마음을 사로잡으셨답니다 — 제가 축하드릴 만한 정복이지요. 멀버리 경께서는요, 친애하는 부인, 멀버리 경께서는 따님께 헌신하는 종이 되셨답니다. 흠!\"\n\n\"하!\" 바로 그때 파이크 씨가 연극하듯 과장된 몸짓으로 벽난로 선반에서 뭔가를 낚아채며 외쳤어. \"이게 뭐지! 내가 지금 무엇을 보고 있는 거지!\"\n\n\"무얼 보고 있는데, 이 친구야?\" 플럭 씨가 물었어.\n\n\"이건 그 얼굴, 그 자태, 그 표정이야.\" 파이크 씨가 손에 세밀화를 든 채 의자에 털썩 주저앉으며 외쳤어. \"어설프게 그려졌고, 온전히 담아내진 못했지만, 그래도 분명 그 얼굴, 그 자태, 그 표정이라고.\"\n\n\"이 거리에서도 내가 알아보겠어!\" 플럭 씨가 한껏 열에 들떠 외쳤어. \"친애하는 부인, 이게 혹시 그분의 희미한 모습이 아닌지요 —\"\n\n\"그건 제 딸 초상화예요.\" 니클비 부인이 무척 자랑스럽게 말했어. 정말 그랬지. 라 크리비 아가씨가 봐 달라며 그걸 집에 가져온 게 바로 이틀 전 밤이었거든.\n\n파이크 씨는 자기 짐작이 딱 맞았다는 걸 확인하기가 무섭게, 그 거룩한 원본을 향해 더없이 호들갑스러운 찬사를 쏟아내기 시작했어. 그러고는 열에 들떠서 그 초상화에 천 번이고 입을 맞췄지. 그동안 플럭 씨는 니클비 부인의 손을 자기 가슴에 꼭 갖다 대고는, 그런 따님을 두신 걸 축하했어. 어찌나 진심 어리고 다정하게 하던지, 두 눈에 눈물이 고였지, 적어도 고인 것처럼 보였어. 가엾은 니클비 부인은 처음엔 더없이 흐뭇한 마음으로 듣고 있다가, 마침내 이 가족을 향한 깊은 존중과 애정의 표시에 그만 완전히 압도되고 말았어. 문틈으로 들여다보던 처녀 아이마저, 이 두 다정한 손님의 황홀경에 놀라서 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서 있었지.\n\n이 황홀경도 차츰 가라앉았어. 그러자 니클비 부인은 기울어 버린 가세를 한탄하며 손님들을 즐겁게 해 주기 시작했지. 시골에 있던 옛 집을 그림처럼 늘어놓으면서 말이야. 여러 방을 하나하나 빠짐없이 묘사했고, 자그마한 곳간도 잊지 않았어. 정원으로 내려가려면 계단을 몇 개나 밟아야 했는지, 거실 문으로 나오면 어느 쪽으로 꺾어야 했는지, 부엌에 얼마나 훌륭한 붙박이 세간이 있었는지도 생생하게 떠올렸지. 이 마지막 회상은 자연스레 부인을 세탁실로 이끌었고, 거기서 양조 도구에 발이 걸렸어. 그 도구들 사이를 한 시간은 헤맬 뻔했는데, 그 연장 이야기가 나오자마자 생각이 꼬리에 꼬리를 물어 파이크 씨가 '엄청나게 목이 마르다'는 걸 퍼뜩 떠올린 게 다행이었지.\n\n\"그럼 이렇게 하지요.\" 파이크 씨가 말했어. \"선술집에 사람을 보내서 우유 반 맥주 반 섞은 걸 한 단지 받아 오게 해 주시면, 진짜 정말로 제가 그걸 마시겠습니다.\"\n\n그러고는 진짜 정말로 파이크 씨는 그걸 마셨어. 플럭 씨도 거들었지. 니클비 부인은 두 가지에 번갈아 감탄하며 그 광경을 지켜봤어. 하나는 두 사람이 그렇게 격을 낮춰 주는 것, 또 하나는 백랍 단지에 척척 맞춰 마셔 대는 솜씨였지. 이 신기해 보이는 일을 풀어 보자면 이렇게 말할 수 있을 거야. 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨처럼 제 머리로 먹고사는 신사들은 — 아니, 어쩌면 제 머리가 있어서라기보다 남들 머리가 없는 걸 이용해 먹고사는 거지만 — 가끔은 아주 군색하고 옹색한 처지로 내몰리기도 하거든. 그런 때에는 아주 소박하고 원시적인 방식으로 즐기는 데 익숙해진단 말이지.\n\n\"그럼 일곱 시 이십 분 전에,\" 파이크 씨가 일어서며 말했어. \"마차가 여기 올 겁니다. 한 번만 더 — 아주 잠깐만 더 — 그 사랑스러운 얼굴을 보겠습니다. 아! 여기 있구나. 변함없이, 한결같이!\" 그런데 말이지, 이건 참 놀라운 일이었어. 세밀화란 게 표정이 그렇게 자주 바뀌게 마련인데도 말이야. \"오, 플럭! 플럭!\"\n\n플럭 씨는 다른 대답 없이 그저 니클비 부인의 손에 입을 맞췄어. 잔뜩 감정에 겨워하고 애정을 드러내는 시늉을 하면서 말이야. 파이크 씨도 똑같이 하고 나서, 두 신사는 황급히 물러갔어.\n\n니클비 부인은 평소 자기가 통찰력과 예리함을 제법 쓸 만하게 지녔다고 자부하는 버릇이 있었어. 그래도 그날만큼 제 눈썰미에 흡족했던 적은 없었지. 어젯밤에 다 알아챘잖아. 멀버리 경과 케이트가 함께 있는 걸 본 적도 없고 — 멀버리 경 이름조차 들어 본 적이 없었는데 — 그런데도 처음부터 속으로, 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 다 보인다고 말하지 않았던가? 이 얼마나 통쾌한 일이야. 이제 의심할 여지가 전혀 없으니 말이지. 자기한테 쏟아진 이 알랑거리는 관심이 충분한 증거가 아니라 해도, 멀버리 경의 막역한 친구가 그 비밀을 그렇게 또박또박 입 밖에 내고 말았잖아. \"난 그 다정한 플럭 씨한테 완전히 반해 버렸어, 정말이지.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어.\n\n이 행운 한가운데에도 큰 근심거리가 하나 있었어. 바로 이걸 털어놓을 사람이 곁에 아무도 없다는 거였지. 한두 번은 라 크리비 아가씨한테 곧장 걸어가 다 얘기해 버릴까 하고 마음을 먹기도 했어. '하지만 글쎄,' 니클비 부인은 생각했어. '그 사람은 아주 갸륵한 사람이긴 한데, 멀버리 경의 지체에 비하면 너무 낮아서 우리가 벗으로 삼기엔 좀 그렇지. 가엾기도 하지!' 이 엄숙한 고민 끝에 부인은 그 자그마한 초상화가를 믿고 속내를 털어놓는다는 생각을 접었어. 그러고는 처녀 아이한테 앞으로 출세시켜 주겠다는 막연하고 알쏭달쏭한 희망을 슬쩍슬쩍 흘리는 것으로 만족했지. 처녀 아이는 다가올 영화를 암시하는 이 알 듯 모를 듯한 말들을 깊은 존경과 우러름으로 받아들였어.\n\n약속한 마차는 시간에 딱 맞춰 왔어. 그건 삯마차가 아니라 개인 사두마차였지. 뒤에는 하인이 하나 서 있었는데, 그 다리가 몸에 비해 좀 굵긴 했지만, 그냥 다리 하나만 떼어 놓고 보면 왕립 아카데미의 모델로 나서도 될 만했어. 니클비 부인이 올라타자, 그 하인이 와락 요란하게 문을 쾅 닫고 뒤로 펄쩍 뛰어오르는 그 부산스러운 소리는 듣기만 해도 신이 났지. 그리고 그 하인이 긴 지팡이의 금장식 끝을 제 코에 갖다 대고는, 부인의 머리 너머로 마부에게 더없이 불손하게 신호를 보내는 걸 이 착한 부인은 전혀 알아채지 못했어. 그래서 부인은 잔뜩 뻣뻣하게 위엄을 차리고 앉아 있었지. 제 처지가 적잖이 자랑스러운 채로 말이야.\n\n극장 입구에서는 더 요란하게 문을 쾅 닫고 더 부산을 떨었어. 거기엔 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨가 부인을 관람석으로 모시려고 기다리고 있었지. 두 사람이 어찌나 깍듯하던지, 파이크 씨는 어쩌다 부인 앞길에 걸려 비틀거린 등불 든 노인을 '뭉개 버리겠다'고 온갖 험한 말로 으름장을 놓았어. 니클비 부인은 그게 어찌나 무서웠던지. 부인은 그 말의 어원을 미리 알고 있어서가 아니라 그저 파이크 씨가 잔뜩 흥분한 걸 보고, '뭉개 버린다'는 게 피를 보는 것과 결국 한가지일 거라고 짐작했거든. 그래서 무슨 일이 벌어질까 봐 이루 말할 수 없이 겁에 질렸지. 다행히 파이크 씨는 말로만 '뭉개는' 데서 그쳤어. 그래서 가는 길에 더 큰 방해라고는, 이 호전적인 신사가 좌석 번호를 잘못 알았다는 이유로 관람석 안내인 보조를 '박살 내겠다'고 한 것뿐이었지.\n\n니클비 부인을 관람석 커튼 뒤 안락의자에 막 앉혀 놓기가 무섭게, 멀버리 경과 베리소프트 경이 도착했어. 머리끝부터 장갑 끝까지, 또 장갑 끝부터 부츠 발끝까지 더없이 우아하고 값비싸게 차려입은 차림새였지. 멀버리 경은 전날보다 목소리가 좀 더 쉬어 있었고, 베리소프트 경은 졸린 듯 좀 멍한 얼굴이었어. 이런 기색에다, 두 사람 다 다리가 약간 휘청거리는 걸 보고, 니클비 부인은 두 사람이 저녁을 먹고 왔다고 옳게 결론을 내렸지.\n\n\"우리가 그러니까 — 그러니까 — 부인의 사랑스러운 따님을 위해 축배를 들었답니다, 니클비 부인.\" 멀버리 경이 부인 뒤에 앉으며 속삭였어.\n\n'호오!' 이 눈치 빠른 부인이 생각했어. '술이 들어가니 속말이 나오는구나. — 정말 친절하시네요, 멀버리 경.'\n\n\"아니, 아닙니다, 정말로!\" 멀버리 호크 경이 대답했어. \"친절한 건 부인이시지요, 정말이지 그렇습니다. 오늘 밤 이렇게 와 주시다니 어찌나 고마운지요.\"\n\n\"이렇게 초대해 주신 게 고마운 거겠지요, 멀버리 경.\" 니클비 부인이 고개를 까딱 젖히고는 더없이 영리한 척하며 대답했어.\n\n\"저는 부인을 알게 되기를 어찌나 바랐는지요, 부인이 저를 좋게 봐 주시기를 어찌나 바랐는지요, 우리 사이에 더없이 흐뭇하고 화목한 집안 같은 정이 흐르기를 어찌나 바랐는지요.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어. \"그러니 제가 하는 일에 사심이 없다고는 생각지 말아 주세요. 저는 끔찍하게 이기적인 놈이거든요. 정말이지 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"멀버리 경이 이기적일 리가 있나요!\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어. \"그러기에는 너무나 솔직하고 너그러운 얼굴을 지니셨는걸요.\"\n\n\"부인은 정말 비상한 관찰자시군요!\" 멀버리 호크 경이 말했어.\n\n\"아유, 아니에요, 저는 정말 사물을 그리 깊이 들여다보지 못해요, 멀버리 경.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어. 그런데 그 말투는 준남작더러 부인이 정말이지 아주 깊이 들여다본다고 짐작하게 만들었지.\n\n\"부인이 좀 무서울 지경입니다.\" 준남작이 말했어. \"정말이지,\" 멀버리 경이 일행을 둘러보며 거듭 말했어. \"니클비 부인이 무서워. 어찌나 엄청나게 예리하신지.\"\n\n파이크 씨와 플럭 씨는 알쏭달쏭하게 고개를 끄덕이며, 자기들도 진작에 그걸 알아챘다고 입을 모았어. 그러자 니클비 부인은 까르르 웃었고, 멀버리 경은 껄껄 웃었으며, 파이크와 플럭은 와하하 웃음을 터뜨렸지.\n\n\"그런데 우리 시동생은 어디 있나요, 멀버리 경?\" 니클비 부인이 물었어. \"그이 없이는 여기 안 왔을 텐데요. 오고 있겠지요?\"\n\n\"파이크,\" 멀버리 경이 이쑤시개를 꺼내 들고 의자에 등을 기대며 말했어. 이 질문에 답을 꾸며 낼 기운조차 없다는 듯 말이야. \"랄프 니클비는 어디 있지?\"\n\n\"플럭,\" 파이크가 준남작의 동작을 흉내 내며, 거짓말의 짐을 친구에게 넘기듯 말했어. \"랄프 니클비는 어디 있나?\"\n\n플럭 씨가 막 얼버무릴 대답을 하려는데, 옆 관람석으로 한 무리가 들어오며 부산을 떠는 통에 신사 넷의 시선이 죄다 그리로 쏠리는 듯했어. 네 사람은 의미심장한 눈빛을 주고받았지. 새로 온 무리가 자기들끼리 이야기를 나누기 시작하자, 멀버리 경은 별안간 더없이 귀를 쫑긋 세운 사람처럼 굴며, 친구들에게 숨도 쉬지 말라고 — 숨도 쉬지 말라고 — 사정했어.\n\n\"왜요?\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"무슨 일인데요?\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 멀버리 경이 부인의 팔에 손을 얹으며 대답했어. \"프레더릭 경, 저 목소리 알아듣겠나?\"\n\n\"맙소사, 니클비 아가씨 목소리가 아닌가 했네.\"\n\n\"어머, 경!\" 니클비 아가씨의 엄마가 커튼 너머로 머리를 쑥 내밀며 외쳤어. \"어머 정말 — 케이트, 얘야, 케이트.\"\n\n\"엄마가 여기 계셨어요! 어떻게 이런 일이!\"\n\n\"이런 일이라니, 얘야? 그래, 여기 있단다.\"\n\n\"그런데 누구 — 도대체 엄마랑 같이 계신 분이 누구예요?\" 케이트가 말했어. 웃으면서 손에 입을 맞추는 남자를 보고는 움찔 물러서며 말이야.\n\n\"누구일 것 같니, 얘야?\" 니클비 부인이 위티털리 부인 쪽으로 몸을 기울이며 대답했어. 그 부인 들으라고 목소리를 조금 높여서 말이야. \"파이크 씨, 플럭 씨, 멀버리 호크 경, 그리고 프레더릭 베리소프트 경이시란다.\"\n\n'아이고 맙소사!' 케이트가 황급히 생각했어. '엄마가 어쩌다 저런 사람들 틈에 끼셨담?'\n\n그런데 케이트는 이런 생각을 어찌나 황급히 했던지, 게다가 놀라움이 어찌나 컸던지, 더구나 랄프의 그 즐거웠던 만찬에서 있었던 일이 어찌나 생생하게 되살아났던지, 얼굴이 새파랗게 질리고 몹시 동요하는 듯 보였어. 이런 기색을 본 니클비 부인은, 이 예리한 부인은 그게 다 격렬한 사랑 때문에 일어난 거라고 단박에 결론지었지. 하지만 자기 눈썰미가 그렇게 빛을 발했다는 이 발견에 적잖이 흐뭇하긴 했어도, 케이트를 향한 어미의 걱정은 줄어들지 않았어. 그래서 부인은 잔뜩 안절부절못하며 제 관람석을 떠나 위티털리 부인의 관람석으로 부랴부랴 건너갔지. 위티털리 부인은 자기 교제 상대 가운데 경과 준남작이 끼어 있다는 그 영광에 어찌나 가슴이 뛰었던지, 지체 없이 위티털리 씨에게 문을 열라고 신호를 보냈어. 그렇게 해서 삼십 초도 안 되어 니클비 부인 일행은 위티털리 부인의 관람석으로 우르르 밀고 들어왔지. 관람석은 문간까지 꽉 들어찼고, 사실 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨는 머리하고 조끼나 겨우 들이밀 자리밖에 없었어.\n\n\"우리 케이트야.\" 니클비 부인이 딸에게 다정하게 입을 맞추며 말했어. \"조금 전엔 얼굴이 어쩜 그렇게 안 좋아 보였니! 정말이지 깜짝 놀랐잖아!\"\n\n\"그냥 기분 탓이에요, 엄마 — 그러니까 — 그러니까 — 조명이 비쳐서 그랬나 봐요.\" 케이트가 대답했어. 불안하게 주위를 흘끔거리면서 말이야. 무슨 주의나 설명을 속삭일 도리가 도무지 없었거든.\n\n\"멀버리 호크 경이 안 보이니, 얘야?\"\n\n케이트는 살짝 고개를 숙이고는, 입술을 깨물며 무대 쪽으로 고개를 돌렸어.\n\n하지만 멀버리 호크 경은 그렇게 호락호락 물러설 사람이 아니었지. 그가 손을 내밀며 다가왔거든. 그리고 니클비 부인이 주제넘게 이 사실을 케이트에게 일러 주는 바람에, 케이트는 어쩔 수 없이 제 손을 내밀어야 했어. 멀버리 경은 그 손을 붙잡고는 찬사를 줄줄이 늘어놓았지. 두 사람 사이에 오갔던 일을 떠올린 케이트는, 그 찬사를 그가 이미 자기에게 안긴 모욕에 모욕을 거듭 얹는 짓으로 옳게 받아들였어. 그다음엔 베리소프트 경과 인사를 나눴고, 그다음엔 파이크 씨의 인사, 그다음엔 플럭 씨의 인사가 이어졌어. 그리고 마침내, 이 아가씨의 굴욕을 마무리 짓기라도 하듯, 케이트는 위티털리 부인의 청에 따라 이 역겨운 자들을 소개하는 의식을 치러야만 했어. 더없이 분하고 진저리 나는 마음으로 바라보던 그자들을 말이야.\n\n\"위티털리 부인이 기뻐하고 있습니다.\" 위티털리 씨가 두 손을 비비며 말했어. \"기뻐하고 있고말고요, 경. 이렇게 알고 지낼 기회를 얻다니, 부디 앞으로 더 돈독해지길 바랍니다, 경. 줄리아, 여보, 너무 흥분하면 안 돼, 안 된다고. 정말 그러면 안 돼. 위티털리 부인은 어찌나 잘 흥분하는 성미인지요, 멀버리 경. 촛불 심지, 등잔 심지, 복숭아에 핀 솜털, 나비 날개의 보드라운 가루 같달까요. 후 불면 날아가 버릴 겁니다, 경. 후 불면 날아가 버릴 거예요.\"\n\n멀버리 경은 이 부인이 후 불어서 날아가 버린다면 아주 편리하겠다고 생각하는 눈치였어. 하지만 그는 기쁨은 서로 마찬가지라고 말했고, 베리소프트 경도 서로 마찬가지라고 덧붙였지. 그러자 멀리서 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨가 정말이지 서로 더없이 마찬가지라고 중얼거리는 소리가 들렸어.\n\n\"저는 관심이 있답니다, 경.\" 위티털리 부인이 희미하게 웃으며 말했어. \"연극에 그렇게나 관심이 많아요.\"\n\n\"그렇—군요. 아주 흥미롭지요.\" 베리소프트 경이 대답했어.\n\n\"저는 셰익스피어를 보고 나면 늘 앓아눕는답니다.\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어. \"다음 날엔 거의 살아 있지도 못해요. 비극을 보고 나면 그 후유증이 어찌나 큰지요, 경. 게다가 셰익스피어는 어찌나 황홀한 사람인지요.\"\n\n\"그렇—군요!\" 베리소프트 경이 대답했어. \"참 영리한 사람이었지요.\"\n\n\"있잖아요, 경.\" 위티털리 부인이 한참 입을 다물었다가 말했어. \"셰익스피어가 태어난 그 사랑스럽고 자그마한 적적한 집에 다녀온 뒤로는, 그이의 연극에 훨씬 더 관심이 가지 뭐예요! 거기 가 보신 적 있으세요, 경?\"\n\n\"아니요, 한 번도요.\" 베리소프트가 대답했어.\n\n\"그러면 정말 한번 가 보셔야 해요, 경.\" 위티털리 부인이 더없이 나른하고 느릿한 말투로 받았어. \"어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠지만, 그 장소를 보고 그 작은 방명록에 이름을 적고 나면, 어쩐지 영감이 솟는 것 같달까요. 마음속에 불길이 확 타오르는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그렇—군요!\" 베리소프트 경이 대답했어. \"꼭 가 봐야겠군요.\"\n\n\"줄리아, 내 사랑.\" 위티털리 씨가 끼어들었어. \"당신은 지금 경을 속이고 있는 거야 — 일부러 그런 건 아닙니다, 경, 이 사람이 경을 속이고 있다고요. 그건 다 당신의 시적인 기질이야, 여보 — 당신의 영묘한 영혼, 당신의 뜨거운 상상력이 당신을 천재의 열기와 흥분 속으로 몰아넣는 거라고. 그 장소엔 아무것도 없어, 여보 — 아무것도, 아무것도 없다고.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 그 장소에 뭔가 분명히 있는 것 같아요.\" 잠자코 듣고 있던 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"왜냐하면, 제가 결혼하고 얼마 안 됐을 때, 가엾고 사랑스러운 우리 니클비 씨하고 같이 버밍엄에서 역마차를 타고 스트랫퍼드에 갔거든요 — 그런데 그게 역마차였나? \" 니클비 부인이 곰곰이 생각하며 말했어. \"그래요, 역마차였던 게 틀림없어요. 그때 마부가 왼쪽 눈에 초록색 가리개를 하고 있다고 제가 한마디 했던 게 기억나니까요 — 버밍엄에서 역마차를 타고 갔는데, 셰익스피어의 무덤하고 생가를 보고 나서 거기 여관으로 돌아가 그날 밤을 묵었어요. 그런데 밤새도록 다른 건 하나도 안 나오고 오로지 어떤 까만 신사 꿈만 꿨던 게 기억나요. 회반죽으로 등신대로 만들어진 모습인데, 술 두 개로 묶은 접어 내린 깃을 달고, 기둥에 기대서 생각에 잠겨 있더라고요. 그래서 아침에 깨서 니클비 씨한테 그 모습을 얘기했더니, 그이가 그건 살아생전 셰익스피어 모습 그대로라고 하지 뭐예요. 정말이지 신기한 일이었어요. 스트랫퍼드 — 스트랫퍼드,\" 니클비 부인이 곰곰이 생각하며 말을 이었어. \"그래요, 저는 그것만은 확실해요. 왜냐하면 그때 제가 아들 니콜라스를 가진 몸이었던 게 기억나거든요. 그리고 바로 그날 아침에 어떤 이탈리아 석고상 파는 소년 때문에 무척 놀랐었지요. 사실 그건 참 다행스러운 일이었어요, 부인.\" 니클비 부인이 위티털리 부인에게 속삭이듯 덧붙였어. \"우리 아들이 셰익스피어가 되지 않았으니 말이에요. 그랬으면 얼마나 끔찍한 일이었겠어요!\"\n\n니클비 부인이 이 흥미로운 일화를 끝맺자, 늘 제 후원자의 일에 열심인 파이크와 플럭은 일행 가운데 한 패를 옆 관람석으로 옮기자고 제안했어. 어찌나 솜씨 좋게 사전 준비가 짜였던지, 케이트는 아무리 안 된다고 말하고 애를 써도 어쩔 도리가 없었어. 결국 멀버리 호크 경에게 이끌려 가는 걸 참고 따를 수밖에 없었지. 어머니와 플럭 씨가 그들을 따라갔는데, 이 갸륵한 부인은 제 분별력을 으스대느라 저녁 내내 딸을 쳐다보지조차 않으려고 각별히 신경을 썼어. 그러고는 플럭 씨의 농담과 이야기에 푹 빠진 척했지. 플럭 씨는 바로 그 일을 위해 니클비 부인의 보초로 임명된 터라, 자기 쪽에서도 부인의 주의를 독차지할 기회라면 하나도 놓치지 않았어.\n\n프레더릭 베리소프트 경은 옆 관람석에 남아 위티털리 부인의 이야기 상대가 되어 주었고, 파이크 씨는 곁에 붙어서 필요할 때마다 한두 마디 거들었지. 위티털리 씨로 말하자면, 그는 객석 한복판에서 충분히 바빴어. 마침 거기 와 있던 친구며 지인들에게, 위층에서 위티털리 부인과 이야기를 나누는 걸 봤던 그 두 신사가 바로 그 고명한 프레더릭 베리소프트 경과 그의 막역한 친구인 쾌활한 멀버리 호크 경이라고 알리고 다녔거든. 이 소식에 점잖은 집안 안주인 여럿이 더없이 질투와 분노에 불타올랐고, 시집 안 간 딸 열여섯 명이 절망의 벼랑 끝까지 내몰렸지.\n\n마침내 저녁 공연이 끝났어. 하지만 케이트는 아직도 그 끔찍한 멀버리 경의 손에 이끌려 아래층으로 내려가야 했지. 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨의 술수가 어찌나 교묘하게 펼쳐졌던지, 케이트와 준남작은 일행 가운데 맨 뒤에 남게 되었고, 게다가 — 일부러 꾸민 티도 안 나게 — 조금 떨어진 뒤쪽에 처지고 말았어.\n\n\"서두르지 마세요, 서두르지 마세요.\" 케이트가 걸음을 재촉하며 팔을 빼내려 하자 멀버리 경이 말했어.\n\n케이트는 아무 대꾸 없이 계속 앞으로 밀고 나갔어.\n\n\"이런, 그러면 —\" 멀버리 경이 태연하게 내뱉으며 케이트를 아예 멈춰 세웠어.\n\n\"저를 붙잡아 두려고 하지 않는 게 좋으실 거예요, 선생님!\" 케이트가 화를 내며 말했어.\n\n\"왜 안 되는데요?\" 멀버리 경이 받아쳤어. \"이봐요, 사랑스러운 아가씨, 어째서 이렇게 못마땅한 척을 계속하는 거지요?\"\n\n\"척이라니요!\" 케이트가 분개하며 되받았어. \"어떻게 감히 저한테 말을 거시는 거예요, 선생님 — 저한테 말을 붙이고 — 제 앞에 나타나시는 거예요?\"\n\n\"화를 내니 더 예뻐 보이는군요, 니클비 아가씨.\" 멀버리 호크 경이 케이트의 얼굴을 더 잘 보려고 허리를 숙이며 말했어.\n\n\"저는 당신을 더없이 쓰디쓴 혐오와 경멸로 대하고 있어요, 선생님.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"역겹고 진저리 나는 표정에서 무슨 매력을 느끼신다면, 당신은 — 당장 제 친구들한테 돌아가게 해 주세요, 선생님, 지금 당장이요. 지금껏 무엇이 저를 참게 했든 간에, 그걸 죄다 무시하고, 당장 저를 가게 해 주지 않으면 당신마저 뜨끔할 만한 행동을 하겠어요.\"\n\n멀버리 경은 빙긋 웃었어. 그러고는 여전히 케이트의 얼굴을 들여다보며 팔을 붙든 채 문 쪽으로 걸어갔지.\n\n\"제 처지가 여자라는 것도, 의지할 데 없는 신세라는 것도 당신이 이 거칠고 사내답지 못한 괴롭힘을 그만두게 만들지 못한다면,\" 케이트가 말했어. 격정에 휩싸여 제가 무슨 말을 하는지도 거의 모른 채 말이야. \"저한테는 오빠가 있어요. 언젠가 그 오빠가 이걸 톡톡히 갚아 줄 거예요.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 멀버리 경이 혼잣말하듯 조용히 내뱉으며 외쳤어. 그러면서 케이트의 허리에 팔을 둘렀지. \"이러니 더 아름다워 보이는걸. 눈을 내리깔고 얌전히 가만있을 때보다 이렇게 화를 낼 때가 더 마음에 들어!\"\n\n케이트가 어떻게 친구들이 기다리고 있는 로비까지 갔는지는 본인도 도무지 알 수 없었어. 하지만 케이트는 그들을 거들떠보지도 않고 로비를 가로질러 황급히 달려가, 별안간 동행에게서 몸을 빼내고는 마차로 뛰어올랐지. 그러고는 마차의 가장 어두운 구석에 몸을 던지며 울음을 터뜨렸어.\n\n파이크 씨와 플럭 씨는 제 차례를 알고 있던 터라, 곧장 마차를 부르라고 소리치고 죄 없는 구경꾼 몇몇과 격렬하게 시비를 붙어 일행을 큰 소동에 빠뜨렸어. 이 소란 한가운데서 두 사람은 겁에 질린 니클비 부인을 사두마차에 태웠지. 그리고 부인을 무사히 떠나보내고 나서는, 위티털리 부인에게로 생각을 돌렸어. 위티털리 부인도 더없는 어리둥절함과 경악 속에 빠뜨려 놓아서, 그 아가씨한테서 주의를 말끔히 떼어 놓은 참이었거든. 마침내 부인이 타고 온 마차도 짐을 싣고 굴러갔어. 그리고 현관 지붕 아래 덩그러니 남은 이 네 인물은 다 함께 한바탕 신나게 웃어 댔지.\n\n\"거 봐.\" 멀버리 경이 지체 높은 친구를 돌아보며 말했어. \"어젯밤에 내가 말하지 않았나? 내 하인을 시켜 저 집 하인을 매수해서 저들이 어디로 가는지 알아내고, 그다음에 어머니를 끼고 바짝 옆에 자리를 잡으면, 저 사람들 명예가 우리 손안에 들어온다고 말이야. 자 봐, 스물네 시간 만에 다 됐잖아.\"\n\n\"그렇—군.\" 속아 넘어간 친구가 대답했어. \"하지만 난 밤—새 그 늙은 부인한테 붙들려 있었잖나.\"\n\n\"이 친구 좀 보게.\" 멀버리 경이 두 친구를 돌아보며 말했어. \"이 불평불만 투성이 투덜이 좀 보라고. 이러니 다시는 이 친구 계략이고 음모고 안 돕겠다고 맹세할 만하지 않나? 이거 정말 괘씸한 노릇 아니냐고?\"\n\n파이크는 플럭에게 이게 정말 괘씸한 노릇 아니냐고 물었고, 플럭은 파이크에게 물었어. 하지만 둘 다 대답하지 않았지.\n\n\"그게 사실 아닌가?\" 베리소프트가 따졌어. \"그렇지 않았나?\"\n\n\"그렇지 않았느냐고!\" 멀버리 경이 되받았어. \"그럼 자네는 어떻게 했으면 좋았겠나? 자네가, 그 경께서, 그 집 어리석은 안주인 비위를 맞춰 주지 않았다면, 어떻게 처음 보자마자 그런 무제한 초대를 따낼 수 있었겠어 — 오고 싶을 때 오고, 가고 싶을 때 가고, 있고 싶은 만큼 있고, 하고 싶은 대로 하는 그런 초대를 말이야? 내가 이 아가씨한테 관심이라도 있는 줄 아나, 자네 친구라는 것 말고? 내가 자네를 위해서 자네 칭찬을 그 아가씨 귀에 줄곧 늘어놓고, 밤새 그 예쁜 토라짐이며 짜증을 다 받아 주지 않았느냐고? 자네는 내가 무슨 재료로 만들어졌다고 생각하나? 내가 아무한테나 이런 짓을 해 주겠어? 그 보답으로 고맙다는 말이라도 들을 자격이 없단 말이야?\"\n\n\"자네는 정말 끝내주게 좋은 친구야.\" 가엾은 젊은 경이 친구의 팔을 잡으며 말했어. \"정말이지 자네는 끝내주게 좋은 친구라고, 호크.\"\n\n\"그러니까 내가 옳게 한 거지, 그렇지?\" 멀버리 경이 따져 물었어.\n\n\"아주 옳—았네.\"\n\n\"그리고 나라는 게 그저 불쌍하고, 어수룩하고, 사람 좋고, 다정한 강아지 같은 놈이라는 거지, 응?\"\n\n\"그래—그래. 친구다운 친구지.\" 상대가 대답했어.\n\n\"그럼 됐어.\" 멀버리 경이 대답했어. \"난 만족해. 자, 이제 가서 그 독일 남작이랑 프랑스 놈한테 복수를 해 주자고. 어젯밤에 자네를 그렇게 깔끔하게 털어먹은 그 작자들 말이야.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 이 다정한 작자는 동행의 팔을 잡고 그를 데리고 갔어. 그러면서 몸을 반쯤 돌려, 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨에게 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하고 경멸 어린 웃음을 던졌지. 두 사람은 이 모든 일을 말없이 즐겁게 지켜본다는 표시로 손수건을 입에 틀어막고는, 제 후원자와 그 먹잇감을 조금 떨어져서 뒤따라갔어.\n\n# CHAPTER 28\n\n니클비 양이 멀베리 호크 경의 박해와 자신을 둘러싼 복잡한 곤경과 고통에 절망한 끝에, 최후의 수단으로 삼촌에게 보호를 호소하다\n\n멀버리 호크 경의 생각이라면 — 사실 이런 말을 그에게 쓸 수 있을까 싶긴 해. 그는 빈틈없이 계산하는 방탕한 사내였거든. 그가 느끼는 기쁨이며 후회며 고통이며 즐거움이 죄다 자기 자신에 관한 것뿐이었고, 그의 지적 능력이라곤 자기 자신을 더럽히고, 겉으로 뒤집어쓴 인간이라는 본성을 짓밟는 힘밖에 남지 않은 듯했으니까 — 아무튼 멀버리 호크 경의 생각은 케이트 니클비에게로 향했어. 간단히 말하면 이런 거였지. 그 아가씨는 의심할 여지 없이 아름답다는 것. 그 수줍음쯤이야 자기처럼 능숙하고 경험 많은 남자가 손쉽게 무너뜨릴 수 있다는 것. 그리고 이 정복은 틀림없이 자기 체면을 세워 주고, 세상에서의 평판을 크게 높여 줄 거라는 것이었어. 그런데 이 마지막 생각이 — 멀버리 경에게는 결코 사소하거나 부차적인 게 아니었지 — 누군가의 귀에는 이상하게 들릴지도 모르니, 이것만은 기억해 두자. 대개의 사람은 저마다의 세계 안에서 살아가고, 오직 그 좁은 울타리 안에서만 두각을 드러내고 박수받기를 바란다는 것 말이야. 멀버리 경의 세계는 방탕한 자들로 들끓었고, 그는 거기에 맞춰 행동했지.\n\n이런 식으로 부당한 일, 억압, 횡포, 그리고 더없이 터무니없는 편견이 우리 사이에서 날마다 끊임없이 벌어지는 거야. 사람들은 그 일을 저지른 주역들이 세상의 여론을 그토록 완전히 무시했다며 놀라워하고 감탄하기를 즐기지. 하지만 그것만큼 큰 착각이 없어. 그런 일이 애초에 벌어지는 것도, 큰 세상을 입 떡 벌리게 만드는 것도, 바로 그자들이 제 작은 세계의 여론만은 꼬박꼬박 살피기 때문이거든.\n\n니클비 부인의 생각은 더없이 자랑스럽고 흐뭇한 종류였어. 그리고 그 아주 기분 좋은 착각에 빠진 채로, 부인은 곧장 자리에 앉아 케이트에게 긴 편지를 써 내려갔지. 편지에서 부인은 딸이 한 그 훌륭한 선택을 전적으로 찬성한다고 했어. 그리고 멀버리 경을 하늘 높이 치켜세웠지. 딸의 마음을 한층 흐뭇하게 해 주려고, 만약 자기가 온 세상 남자들 중에서 골라잡을 수 있었다면 바로 그 사람을 사위로 골랐을 거라고까지 단언했어. 그러고는 이 착한 부인은, 자기가 세상을 이만큼 오래 살았으니 세상 물정을 모를 리 없다는 말을 먼저 깔아 둔 다음, 구애의 시기에 들어맞는 미묘한 가르침을 잔뜩 늘어놓았어. 그리고 그게 다 자기 경험으로 지혜를 입증한 것이라고 했지. 무엇보다도 부인은 처녀다운 엄격한 조심성을 칭찬했어. 그 자체로도 아주 칭찬받을 일일뿐더러, 연인의 열정을 단단히 키우고 부풀리는 데 큰 도움이 된다면서 말이야. \"그리고 나는 말이다,\" 니클비 부인은 덧붙였어. \"어젯밤에 내 사랑하는 딸의 분별력이 벌써 이걸 일러 주었더라는 걸 알고는, 평생 그렇게 기뻤던 적이 없었단다.\" 이런 말과 함께, 그리고 딸이 자기의 그 뛰어난 분별과 신중함을 이렇게나 많이 물려받았다는 걸 알게 되어 얼마나 기쁜지를 (조심하면 머지않아 딸도 거의 그 분별을 다 닮을 수 있겠지) 이렇게저렇게 넌지시 일러두면서, 니클비 부인은 아주 길고 알아보기 힘든 편지를 끝맺었어.\n\n가엾은 케이트는 그 편지를 받고 거의 정신이 나갈 지경이었어. 빽빽이 쓰고 가로세로로 겹쳐 쓴 넉 장짜리 축하 편지였는데, 하필이면 밤새 한숨도 못 자고 방에서 울며 뜬눈으로 지새우게 만든 바로 그 일을 두고 하는 말이었거든. 그런데 더 나쁘고 더 견디기 힘든 건 위티털리 부인의 비위를 맞춰야 한다는 거였어. 부인은 전날 밤의 피로 끝에 기분이 가라앉아 있었고, 그러니 당연히 자기 말동무가 (안 그러면 무엇 때문에 숙식과 봉급을 주겠어?) 더없이 기분 좋은 상태이기를 바랐지. 위티털리 씨로 말하자면, 귀족과 악수를 했고 그것도 모자라 자기 집에 와 달라고 청까지 했다는 사실에 온종일 기쁨으로 들떠 안절부절못하며 돌아다녔어. 정작 그 귀족 본인은 생각하는 능력 때문에 불편할 일이 도무지 없는 사람이라, 파이크와 플럭의 입담으로 흥을 돋우며 즐겼지. 두 사람은 그의 돈으로 값비싼 술을 실컷 들이켜며 입담을 더 날카롭게 갈았고 말이야.\n\n오후 네 시였어 — 그러니까 해와 시계가 가리키는, 그 흔해 빠진 오후 말이야. 위티털리 부인은 늘 그렇듯 응접실 소파에 비스듬히 기대 있었어. 한편 케이트는 「레이디 플라벨라」라는 제목의 세 권짜리 새 소설을 소리 내어 읽고 있었지. 그 미심쩍은 알퐁스가 바로 그날 아침 도서관에서 구해 온 책이었어. 그건 위티털리 부인이 앓는 병에 더없이 잘 맞는 작품이었어. 처음부터 끝까지 단 한 줄도, 아무리 가능성을 멀리 끌어와 봐도, 숨 쉬는 그 누구의 마음에 티끌만 한 흥분도 일으킬 만한 데가 없었거든.\n\n케이트는 계속 읽어 나갔어.\n\"'슈리제트,' 레이디 플라벨라가 생쥐처럼 자그마한 발을 푸른 새틴 슬리퍼에 밀어 넣으며 말했다. 바로 그 슬리퍼가, 지난밤 맹스펜유 공작의 무도회장에서 자기와 젊은 베필레르 대령 사이에 반은 장난스럽고 반은 화난 말다툼을 무심코 일으킨 것이었다. '슈리제트, 마 셰르, 도네무아 드 로드콜로뉴, 실 부 플레, 몽 앙팡.'\n\n\"'메르시 — 고마워,' 레이디 플라벨라가 말했다. 명랑하면서도 충직한 슈리제트가 그 향기로운 액체를 레이디 플라벨라의 손수건에 흠뻑 뿌리는 동안이었다. 그 손수건은 가장 고운 케임브릭 천에 더없이 화려한 레이스로 가장자리를 두르고, 네 귀퉁이마다 플라벨라 가문의 문장과 그 고귀한 가문의 찬란한 문장 장식을 수놓은 것이었다. '메르시 — 그만하면 됐다.'\n\n\"바로 그 순간, 레이디 플라벨라가 아직도 그 손수건을 곱지만 사려 깊게 깎아 놓은 듯한 코에 갖다 대고 그 그윽한 향기를 들이마시고 있을 때였다. 부두아르의 문이 — 이탈리아 하늘빛을 닮은 화려한 비단 다마스크 휘장으로 교묘히 가려져 있던 그 문이 — 활짝 열렸다. 그리고 복숭아꽃빛과 금빛의 호화로운 제복을 입은 두 시종이 소리 없는 걸음으로 방 안으로 들어왔다. 그 뒤를 비단 양말을 신은 시동이 따랐다. 두 시종이 얼마쯤 떨어진 곳에서 더없이 우아하게 절을 올리는 동안, 시동은 아름다운 여주인의 발치까지 나아가, 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고는, 호화롭게 새김 장식을 한 금쟁반에 향내 나는 쪽지 한 장을 받쳐 올렸다.\n\n\"레이디 플라벨라는 억누를 수 없는 떨림으로, 황급히 봉투를 뜯고 향기로운 봉인을 깨뜨렸다. 그것은 베필레르가 보낸 것이었다 — 젊고, 호리호리하고, 목소리가 나직한 — 다름 아닌 그녀의 베필레르 말이다.'\"\n\n\"어머, 멋져라!\" 이따금 문학에 심취하곤 하는 케이트의 후원자가 끼어들었어. \"정말이지 시적이야. 그 대목 다시 읽어 봐요, 니클비 양.\"\n\n케이트는 그대로 따랐어.\n\n\"정말 감미로워!\" 위티털리 부인이 한숨을 쉬며 말했어. \"어찌나 관능적인지, 안 그래? 어찌나 부드러운지?\"\n\n\"네, 그런 것 같아요.\" 케이트가 부드럽게 대답했어. \"아주 부드럽네요.\"\n\n\"책을 덮어요, 니클비 양.\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어. \"오늘은 더는 못 듣겠어. 그 감미로운 묘사의 여운을 흩뜨리고 싶지 않거든. 책을 덮어요.\"\n\n케이트는 그대로 따랐어. 마지못한 것도 아니었지. 그러는 동안 위티털리 부인은 나른한 손길로 안경을 들어 올리더니, 케이트가 창백해 보인다고 말했어.\n\n\"어젯밤의 그 — 그 소란과 혼란에 놀라서 그런가 봐요.\" 케이트가 말했어.\n\n\"어쩌면 그렇게 별날까!\" 위티털리 부인이 놀란 표정으로 외쳤어. 하긴 곰곰이 생각해 보면, 말동무의 마음을 무언가가 흔들어 놓는다는 건 정말이지 별난 일이긴 했어. 고장 난 증기기관이나 정교한 기계 장치쯤이야 그에 비하면 아무것도 아니었지.\n\n\"넌 프레더릭 경이랑 그 다른 멋진 분들을 어떻게 알게 됐니, 얘야?\" 위티털리 부인이 여전히 안경 너머로 케이트를 살피며 물었어.\n\n\"삼촌 댁에서 만났어요.\" 케이트가 말했어. 얼굴이 새빨개지는 걸 느끼고 속이 상했지만, 그 남자 생각만 하면 얼굴로 확 솟구치는 피를 억누를 수가 없었어.\n\n\"안 지는 오래됐고?\"\n\n\"아뇨.\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"오래되진 않았어요.\"\n\n\"그 점잖은 분, 네 어머니가 우리에게 그분들을 알게 될 기회를 주신 게 참 기뻤단다.\" 위티털리 부인이 거만한 투로 말했어. \"우리 지인 중에 막 우리를 소개해 주려던 분들이 있었거든. 그러니 더 놀라운 일이지.\"\n\n이 말은 니클비 양이 위세 있는 인물 넷을 (파이크와 플럭도 그 멋진 분들 축에 끼어 있었으니까) 알게 된 영광과 위엄에 으스댈까 봐 미리 못 박아 둔 거였어. 위티털리 부인 자신은 모르는 사람들이었거든. 그런데 그 일은 케이트의 마음에 이렇다 할 인상을 전혀 남기지 못했으니, 그 말의 효력도 케이트한테는 영 헛것이 되고 말았지.\n\n\"그분들이 찾아와도 되겠느냐고 청하더구나.\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어. \"물론 그러시라고 했지.\"\n\n\"오늘 오신대요?\" 케이트가 큰맘 먹고 물었어.\n\n위티털리 부인의 대답은 현관문을 쾅쾅 두드리는 요란한 소리에 묻혀 버렸어. 그리고 그 울림이 채 가시기도 전에, 멋진 이륜마차 한 대가 와서 멈췄고, 거기서 멀버리 호크 경과 그의 친구 베리소프트 경이 뛰어내렸지.\n\n\"이제 오셨네요.\" 케이트가 일어서며 서둘러 자리를 피하려 했어.\n\n\"니클비 양!\" 위티털리 부인이 외쳤어. 말동무가 자기 허락을 먼저 구해 받지도 않고 방을 나가려 하다니, 기가 막혀 어쩔 줄을 몰랐지. \"제발 갈 생각일랑 마요.\"\n\n\"정말 고맙습니다!\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"하지만 —\"\n\n\"제발 나한테 이렇게 말을 많이 시켜서 흥분하게 하지 말아요.\" 위티털리 부인이 아주 날카롭게 말했어. \"어머나, 니클비 양, 부탁이에요 —\"\n\n케이트가 몸이 안 좋다고 아무리 우겨 봐야 소용없었어. 누가 두드린 거든 간에 그 발소리가 벌써 계단을 올라오고 있었으니까. 케이트는 도로 자리에 앉았어. 앉기가 무섭게 그 미심쩍은 시동이 방으로 쏜살같이 들어와 한꺼번에 알렸지. 파이크 씨, 플럭 씨, 베리소프트 경, 그리고 멀버리 호크 경 행차요, 하고 말이야.\n\n\"세상에 이렇게 신기한 일이 다 있나요.\" 플럭 씨가 두 부인에게 더없이 다정하게 인사하며 말했어. \"정말이지 신기한 일이에요. 프레더릭 경과 멀버리 경이 마차를 몰고 문 앞에 닿는 바로 그 순간, 저랑 파이크가 막 문을 두드렸지 뭡니까.\"\n\n\"바로 그 순간에 두드렸지요.\" 파이크가 말했어.\n\n\"어떻게 오셨든 무슨 상관이겠어요. 이렇게 와 주셨는데요.\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어. 부인은 삼 년 반 동안 한 소파에 줄곧 누워 지낸 덕에 우아한 자세를 잔뜩 익혀 두었는데, 이제 그 모든 자세 중에서도 가장 인상적인 것을 골라 척 취하며 손님들을 놀라게 했지. \"정말이지 너무 기뻐요.\"\n\n\"니클비 양은 좀 어떤가요?\" 멀버리 호크 경이 케이트에게 다가가 낮은 목소리로 말을 건넸어. 그렇다고 위티털리 부인의 귀에 안 들릴 만큼 낮지는 않았지만 말이야.\n\n\"글쎄요, 어젯밤 놀란 게 아직 가시지 않는다네요.\" 부인이 말했어. \"하긴 그럴 만도 하지요. 제 신경도 갈가리 찢어졌는걸요.\"\n\n\"그런데도 부인은 보아하니,\" 멀버리 경이 몸을 돌리며 한마디 했어. \"그런데도 부인은 보아하니 —\"\n\n\"더없이 좋아 보이십니다.\" 파이크 씨가 자기 후원자를 거들고 나서며 말했어. 물론 플럭 씨도 똑같은 말을 했지.\n\n\"멀버리 경은 아첨꾼인가 봐요, 경.\" 위티털리 부인이 그 젊은 신사 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했어. 그는 잠자코 지팡이 머리를 빨면서 케이트를 빤히 쳐다보고 있었거든.\n\n\"오, 끝내주는군요!\" 베리소프트가 대답했어. 이 대단한 소감을 입 밖에 낸 다음, 그는 전처럼 다시 제 일에 골몰했지.\n\n\"니클비 양도 전보다 못해 보이지 않는군요.\" 멀버리 경이 대담한 시선을 케이트에게 던지며 말했어. \"이 아가씨는 늘 아름다웠지만, 맹세코 부인, 거기다 부인의 그 고운 자태까지 좀 나눠 주신 것 같습니다그려.\"\n\n이 말이 끝나고 가엾은 아가씨의 얼굴에 번진 그 홍조를 보면, 위티털리 부인이 자기 얼굴을 꾸민 그 인공의 화색을 케이트한테 좀 나눠 준 거라고 봐도 그럴듯할 만했어. 위티털리 부인은, 세상 그리 흔쾌한 낯빛은 아니었지만, 케이트가 정말 예뻐 보이긴 한다고 인정했어. 그리고 멀버리 경이 처음 생각만큼 그리 유쾌한 사람은 아니라는 생각이 들기 시작했지. 능숙한 아첨꾼이란 혼자 독차지할 수만 있다면 더없이 즐거운 말동무지만, 다른 사람을 칭찬하는 데 맛을 들이기 시작하면 그 안목이 영 미심쩍어지거든.\n\n\"파이크.\" 빈틈없는 플럭 씨가 니클비 양에 대한 칭찬이 일으킨 효과를 눈여겨보며 말했어.\n\n\"왜, 플럭.\" 파이크가 말했어.\n\n\"혹시 말이야,\" 플럭 씨가 알쏭달쏭하게 물었어. \"위티털리 부인의 옆얼굴을 보면 떠오르는 사람 없나, 자네가 아는 누구 말이야?\"\n\n\"떠오르는 사람!\" 파이크가 대답했어. \"물론 있고말고.\"\n\n\"누굴 말하는 건데?\" 플럭이 똑같이 알쏭달쏭한 투로 말했어. \"비 공작 부인?\"\n\n\"비 백작 부인이지.\" 파이크가 얼굴에 희미한 웃음기를 남긴 채 대답했어. \"그 아리따운 동생분이 백작 부인이잖아. 공작 부인이 아니라.\"\n\n\"맞아.\" 플럭이 말했어. \"비 백작 부인. 정말 놀랄 만큼 닮았어!\"\n\n\"그저 깜짝 놀랄 정도지.\" 파이크 씨가 말했어.\n\n이런 일이 다 있다니! 위티털리 부인이, 진실하고 안목 있는 두 증인의 증언에 따라, 영락없는 백작 부인의 판박이라고 선언된 거야! 이게 다 상류 사회에 발을 들인 덕이었지. 글쎄, 부인이 보잘것없는 사람들 틈에서 이십 년을 살았어도 그런 말은 한 번도 못 들어 봤을 거 아니겠어. 사실 그럴 수가 있었겠어? 그런 사람들이 백작 부인에 대해 뭘 알겠어?\n\n이 작은 미끼를 부인이 얼마나 게걸스레 삼키는지로 그 아첨에 대한 식욕의 크기를 가늠해 본 두 신사는, 이제 그 물건을 아주 듬뿍 들이부어 주기 시작했어. 그래서 멀버리 호크 경에게는 니클비 양을 이런저런 질문과 말로 들볶을 기회가 생겼지. 케이트는 거기에 어쩔 수 없이 무어라도 대꾸를 해야만 했고. 한편 베리소프트 경은 아무도 건드리지 않은 채로 지팡이 꼭대기의 금빛 손잡이가 주는 맛을 한껏 음미했어. 위티털리 씨가 집에 돌아와 화제를 자기가 좋아하는 쪽으로 돌리지만 않았더라면, 그는 면회가 끝날 때까지 그러고 있었을 거야.\n\n\"경.\" 위티털리 씨가 말했어. \"정말 기쁩니다 — 영광입니다 — 자랑스럽습니다. 다시 앉으시지요, 경, 제발요. 정말이지 자랑스럽습니다 — 더없이 자랑스러워요.\"\n\n위티털리 씨가 이 모든 말을 늘어놓는 게 그의 아내에게는 남몰래 짜증스러운 일이었어. 부인이 자부심과 거만함으로 터질 듯했던 건 사실이지만, 그래도 이 고귀한 손님들에게는 그들의 방문이 아주 흔한 일이고, 일주일 내내 날마다 귀족이며 준남작이 자기를 보러 온다고 믿게 하고 싶었거든. 그런데 위티털리 씨의 감정은 도저히 억누를 수 있는 게 아니었지.\n\n\"정말이지 영광입니다!\" 위티털리 씨가 말했어. \"줄리아, 여보, 당신 내일은 이것 때문에 고생 좀 하겠구려.\"\n\n\"고생이라뇨!\" 베리소프트 경이 외쳤어.\n\n\"그 반동 말입니다, 경, 반동이요.\" 위티털리 씨가 말했어. \"신경 계통에 이렇게 격한 부담을 주고 나면, 경, 그 뒤에 뭐가 따라오겠습니까? 가라앉음, 침울함, 처짐, 권태, 쇠약입니다. 경, 만약에 텀리 스너핌 경이 지금 이 순간 저 가녀린 사람을 본다면, 저 사람 목숨에 대해 이만큼도 — 이만큼도 — 값을 안 쳐 줄 겁니다.\" 이 말을 실감 나게 하려고, 위티털리 씨는 담뱃갑에서 코담배를 한 자밤 집어, 덧없음의 상징인 양 허공에 가볍게 휙 뿌렸어.\n\n\"이만큼도요.\" 위티털리 씨가 진지한 얼굴로 주위를 둘러보며 말했어. \"텀리 스너핌 경은 위티털리 부인의 목숨에 그만큼도 값을 안 쳐 줄 겁니다.\"\n\n위티털리 씨는 이 말을 점잖이 우쭐대며 했어. 마치 그렇게 위태로운 지경에 빠진 아내를 둔 게 결코 사소하지 않은 영예라도 되는 양 말이야. 그러자 위티털리 부인은 한숨을 쉬며 곁눈으로 보았지. 그 영예를 느끼긴 하지만 되도록 다소곳이 견디기로 작정했다는 듯이 말이야.\n\n\"위티털리 부인은,\" 그 남편이 말했어. \"텀리 스너핌 경이 가장 아끼는 환자랍니다. 감히 말씀드리건대, 켄싱턴 그래블 핏에서 한 가족을 몰살시켰다는 그 새 약을 맨 처음 먹은 사람이 바로 위티털리 부인일 겁니다. 분명 그랬을 거예요. 내가 틀렸거든, 줄리아 여보, 바로잡아 주구려.\"\n\n\"제가 처음이었던 것 같아요.\" 위티털리 부인이 힘없는 목소리로 말했어.\n\n이 대화에 어떻게 끼어드는 게 좋을지 자기 후원자가 좀 갈피를 못 잡는 듯하자, 지칠 줄 모르는 파이크 씨가 그 틈으로 뛰어들었어. 그리고 핵심을 찌르는 말이랍시고, 앞서 말한 그 약에 관해 — 맛이 괜찮더냐고 물었지.\n\n\"아니요, 안 괜찮았소. 그 약은 그런 장점조차 없었지요.\" 위티털리 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"위티털리 부인은 정말이지 순교자시군요.\" 파이크가 정중히 절을 하며 한마디 했어.\n\n\"제가 그런 것 같아요.\" 위티털리 부인이 미소 지으며 말했어.\n\n\"당신이 그렇소, 내 사랑 줄리아.\" 그 남편이 대답했어. 자기가 우쭐대는 건 아니지만 그래도 자기들 특권만은 기어이 내세워야겠다는 투였지. \"누구든, 경.\" 위티털리 씨가 그 귀족 쪽으로 휙 돌아서며 덧붙였어. \"위티털리 부인보다 더한 순교자를 제게 데려와 보시지요. 그러면 제가 드릴 말씀은, 그 순교자를 기꺼이 한번 뵙겠다는 것뿐입니다. 남자든 여자든 말입니다 — 그뿐이에요, 경.\"\n\n파이크와 플럭은 그보다 더 공정한 말은 없다고 얼른 맞장구를 쳤어. 그리고 이쯤 되자 방문이 어지간히 길어진 터라, 두 사람은 멀버리 경의 눈짓에 따라 일어나 가려고 했지. 그러자 멀버리 경 본인과 베리소프트 경도 따라 일어섰어. 우정의 다짐이며, 이렇게 기쁜 사귐에서 반드시 흘러나올 즐거움에 대한 기대의 말이 숱하게 오갔지. 그리고 손님들은, 언제 어느 때고 위티털리 댁이 자기들을 지붕 아래 맞이하는 영광을 누리게 될 거라는 거듭된 다짐을 남기고 떠났어.\n\n그들이 언제 어느 때고 찾아왔다는 것 — 하루는 거기서 점심을, 다음 날은 저녁을, 또 그다음 날은 다시 점심을 먹었고, 날마다 끊임없이 드나들었다는 것 — 공공장소를 구경하러 패를 짜기도 하고, 휴게실 같은 데서 우연히 마주치기도 했다는 것 — 이 모든 자리에서 니클비 양이 멀버리 호크 경의 끊임없고 그칠 줄 모르는 괴롭힘에 시달렸다는 것 — 그 경은 이제 케이트의 자존심을 꺾는 데 성공하느냐 마느냐에 자기 체면이 걸렸다고 여기기 시작했지, 심지어 제 두 부하의 평가 안에서도 말이야 — 그리고 케이트에게는 평화나 안식의 짬이라곤 없었다는 것, 다만 외로운 제 방에 앉아 그날의 시련을 두고 울 수 있는 그 시간만 빼면 말이야 — 이 모든 것이 멀버리 경의 잘 짜인 계략과, 그 부하 파이크와 플럭의 능란한 실행에서 자연히 흘러나온 결과였지.\n\n그렇게 이 주일 동안 일이 굴러갔어. 베리소프트 경이 비록 귀족이고 멀버리 호크 경이 비록 준남작이라 해도, 두 사람이 결코 더없이 좋은 말동무가 될 만한 부류가 아니라는 것, 그리고 그 습성이나 태도나 취향이나 말솜씨로 보아 숙녀들 모임에서 그리 환하게 빛날 만한 위인이 아니라는 것 — 이건 아주 어리석고 미욱한 사람이 아니고서야 한 번만 만나 봐도 알 수 있는 일이었으니, 굳이 말할 필요도 없을 거야. 하지만 위티털리 부인에게는 그 두 작위면 그걸로 충분했어. 거친 말투는 익살이 되었고, 천박함은 더없이 매력적인 별스러움으로 누그러졌지. 무례함은 격의 없이 스스럼없는 태도로 둔갑했는데, 그건 운 좋게 높은 분들과 어울려 본 사람만 익힐 수 있는 거라고 여겼거든.\n\n여주인이 새 친구들의 행동을 그런 식으로 받아들이는데, 말동무가 그들에게 무슨 항변을 할 수 있었겠어? 그들이 안주인 앞에서도 그토록 거리낌 없이 굴었으니, 돈 받고 부리는 그 아랫사람한테야 얼마나 더 제멋대로 말을 걸었겠어! 게다가 그게 최악도 아니었지. 그 역겨운 멀버리 호크 경이 점점 더 숨김없이 케이트에게 들러붙자, 위티털리 부인은 니클비 양이 자기보다 더 매력 있다는 데 질투를 느끼기 시작했어. 만약 이 감정 때문에 케이트가 그런 손님들이 와 있을 때 응접실에서 쫓겨났더라면, 케이트는 그렇게만 된다면 더없이 기쁘고 좋았을 거야. 그런데 케이트한테는 불행하게도, 그 타고난 우아함과 진정으로 기품 있는 몸가짐, 그리고 여성들의 자리에 가장 큰 매력을 더해 주는 그 이름 없는 천 가지 재주가 있었어. 이런 게 어디서든 값진 거라면, 안주인이 그저 움직이는 인형 같은 곳에서야 더더욱 그랬지. 그래서 케이트는 이중의 굴욕을 겪었어. 멀버리 경과 그 친구들이 와 있을 때는 그 자리에 없어서는 안 될 사람이 되고, 바로 그 이유 때문에, 그들이 가고 나면 위티털리 부인의 온갖 심술과 변덕에 고스란히 시달리는 거였지. 케이트는 완전히, 철저히 비참해졌어.\n\n위티털리 부인은 멀버리 경에 관해서만은 한 번도 가면을 벗은 적이 없었어. 다만 평소보다 유난히 심기가 틀어지면, 부인들이 더러 그러듯 그걸 신경의 탈 탓으로 돌렸지. 그런데 베리소프트 경마저도 케이트에게 다소 마음이 끌린 듯하다는, 그래서 자기 위티털리 부인은 완전히 뒷전 신세라는 그 끔찍한 생각이 부인의 머릿속에 어렴풋이 떠올라 차츰 또렷해지자, 부인은 더없이 당연하고 더없이 정숙한 분노로 가득 차게 됐어. 그리고 결혼한 부인이자 사회의 도덕적인 일원으로서, 그 일을 지체 없이 '그 젊은 사람'에게 일러 주는 게 자기 의무라고 느꼈지.\n\n그래서 위티털리 부인은 이튿날 아침, 소설을 읽다 잠시 멈춘 틈을 타 운을 뗐어.\n\n\"니클비 양.\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어. \"아주 진지하게 할 얘기가 있어요. 이런 말을 하게 돼서 유감이에요. 정말이지 무척 유감이고말고요. 하지만 니클비 양이 내게 달리 어쩔 도리를 안 남겨 주는걸요.\" 그러고는 위티털리 부인이 고개를 홱 젖혔어 — 격하게는 아니고 그저 점잖게 말이야. 그러고는 다소 흥분한 기색으로, 심장이 또 두근거리기 시작할까 봐 걱정이라고 했지.\n\n\"니클비 양, 양의 행동은,\" 부인이 말을 이었어. \"내 마음에 도무지 들지 않아요 — 도무지요. 나는 정말이지 양이 잘되기를 간절히 바라요. 하지만 분명히 말해 두는데, 니클비 양, 지금처럼 계속하다간 결코 잘되지 못할 거예요.\"\n\n\"부인!\" 케이트가 자존심 상한 듯 외쳤어.\n\n\"그런 식으로 말해서 날 흥분시키지 말아요, 니클비 양, 그러지 말라고요.\" 위티털리 부인이 다소 거칠게 말했어. \"안 그러면 종을 울리게 만들 테니까.\"\n\n케이트는 부인을 바라봤지만 아무 말도 하지 않았어.\n\n\"착각하지 말아요, 니클비 양.\" 위티털리 부인이 말을 이었어. \"양이 그런 눈으로 날 쳐다본다고 해서, 내가 하려는 말을 못 하리라고는 생각 마요. 나는 이게 종교적인 의무라고 느낀다고요. 그렇게 나를 빤히 쳐다볼 것도 없어요.\" 위티털리 부인이 별안간 앙심을 터뜨리며 말했어. \"나는 멀버리 경이 아니에요. 아니고말고요, 프레더릭 베리소프트 경도 아니고요, 니클비 양. 파이크 씨도 플럭 씨도 아니라고요.\"\n\n케이트는 부인을 다시 바라봤지만, 아까보다는 흔들리는 눈길이었어. 그러고는 식탁에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손으로 두 눈을 가렸지.\n\n\"내가 처녀였을 적에 이런 일이 있었더라면,\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어 (그건 그렇고, 그건 어지간히 오래전 일이었을 거야). \"아마 아무도 안 믿었을 거예요.\"\n\n\"안 믿었을 거예요.\" 케이트가 중얼거렸어. \"직접 겪어 보지 않고서야, 제가 겪을 운명에 놓인 듯한 이 일을 아무도 믿지 못할 거예요!\"\n\n\"제발 나한테 겪을 운명이니 뭐니 하는 말은 말아 줘요, 니클비 양.\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어. 그렇게 중한 환자치고는 자못 놀랄 만큼 날카로운 목소리였지. \"나는 말대꾸를 받지 않겠어요, 니클비 양. 나는 말대꾸를 받는 데 익숙하지 않고, 한순간도 그걸 용납하지 않을 거예요. 알아들어요?\" 부인은 다소 앞뒤가 안 맞게도 대답을 기다리며 덧붙였어.\n\n\"알아듣고 있어요, 부인.\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"놀라면서요 — 말로 다 못할 만큼 큰 놀라움으로요.\"\n\n\"나는 늘 양을 그 처지에 비하면 유난히 행실 바른 젊은이로 여겨 왔어요.\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어. \"그리고 양은 건강해 보이고 옷매무새도 단정하고 하니, 양에게 관심을 가져 온 거고, 지금도 그래요. 양의 어머니, 그 점잖은 노부인에게 일종의 의무가 있다고 생각해서 말이지요. 이런 이유들 때문에, 니클비 양, 이번 한 번만 똑똑히 말해 두겠어요. 그리고 내 말을 새겨듣길 바라요. 이 집을 찾는 신사분들에 대한 양의 그 지나치게 헤픈 행동을 당장 고쳐 줘야겠어요. 그건 정말 어울리지 않아요.\" 위티털리 부인이 말하며 그 정숙한 눈을 감았어. \"온당치 못해요 — 아주 온당치 못하다고요.\"\n\n\"오!\" 케이트가 위를 올려다보며 두 손을 맞잡고 외쳤어. \"이건, 이건 너무 잔인하고, 너무 견디기 힘들어요! 제가 밤낮없이 이렇게 시달려 온 것, 그런 사람들과 엮였다는 수치심에 제 자신을 보는 눈조차 거의 무너져 내린 것, 그것만으로도 모자라서, 이제 이렇게 부당하고 터무니없는 누명까지 뒤집어써야 하나요!\"\n\n\"부디 잘 새겨 두길 바라요, 니클비 양.\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어. \"양이 '부당하다'느니 '터무니없다'느니 하는 말을 쓸 때, 그건 결국 내가 사실이 아닌 걸 말하고 있다고 몰아붙이는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래요.\" 케이트가 정직한 분노로 말했어. \"이 비난을 부인 스스로 하시는 거든, 다른 사람들이 시켜서 하시는 거든, 저한테는 매한가지예요. 분명히 말하는데, 그건 비열하고, 터무니없고, 고의적인 거짓말이에요. 어떻게 이럴 수가 있죠!\" 케이트가 외쳤어. \"저와 같은 여자가 곁에 앉아 있으면서, 이 남자들이 제게 안긴 그 고통을 못 봤을 수가 있나요? 어떻게 부인이, 부인이 그 자리에 함께 있으면서, 그자들의 눈빛 하나하나에 묻어나는 그 모욕적인 무례함을 못 알아챘을 수가 있나요? 어떻게 부인이 못 보고 넘어갈 수가 있나요. 이 난봉꾼들이, 부인을 깡그리 무시하고, 신사다운 행실은 물론이고 예의마저 깡그리 저버린 채로, 단 한 가지 목적으로만 여기 발을 들였다는 걸요. 그건 친구도 없고 의지할 데도 없는 한 여자를 노린 제 흉계를 밀어붙이려는 거예요. 그 여자는, 이렇게 굴욕스럽게 털어놓지 않았더라면, 그렇게 자기보다 한참 나이 든 분에게서 여자다운 도움과 동정 비슷한 거라도 받기를 바랐을지 모르는데 말이에요. 저는 안 믿어요 — 도저히 못 믿겠어요!\"\n\n가엾은 케이트가 세상 물정을 조금이라도 알았더라면, 아무리 그렇게 격분으로 내몰린 상태였대도, 이런 분별없는 말은 결코 입에 담지 않았을 거야. 그 말의 효과는 좀 더 노련한 사람이라면 능히 내다봤을 그대로였지. 위티털리 부인은 자기 진실성에 대한 그 공격을 모범적인 침착함으로 받아넘겼어. 그리고 케이트가 자기 고통을 늘어놓는 동안에도 더없이 영웅적인 꿋꿋함으로 들었지. 하지만 신사들이 자기를 무시한다는 대목이 나오자 부인은 격한 감정을 드러냈어. 그리고 이 일격에 뒤이어 자기 나이에 관한 말까지 나오자마자, 부인은 소파에 벌렁 쓰러져 처량한 비명을 질렀지.\n\n\"무슨 일이야?\" 위티털리 씨가 방으로 튀어 들어오며 외쳤어. \"맙소사, 이게 웬일이야? 줄리아! 줄리아! 고개를 들어 봐, 여보, 고개를 들어 보라고!\"\n\n그런데 줄리아는 한사코 고개를 떨군 채 더 크게 비명을 질러 댔어. 그래서 위티털리 씨는 종을 울리고는, 위티털리 부인이 누운 소파 둘레를 미친 듯이 빙빙 돌며 춤을 추듯 했지. 텀리 스너핌 경을 끊임없이 부르짖으면서, 단 한 번도 멈춰서 눈앞의 이 광경에 대해 무슨 설명을 청하지도 않으면서 말이야.\n\n\"텀리 경을 모셔 와!\" 위티털리 씨가 시동에게 두 주먹을 휘두르며 외쳤어. \"내 그럴 줄 알았소, 니클비 양.\" 그가 처량한 승리감을 띤 채 둘러보며 말했어. \"사교계가 저 사람한테는 너무 벅찼던 거요. 이게 다 영혼 탓이오, 알겠소, 죄다 영혼 탓이라고.\" 이렇게 단언하고서 위티털리 씨는 늘어진 위티털리 부인의 몸을 들어 올려, 통째로 안고 침실로 데려갔어.\n\n케이트는 텀리 스너핌 경이 다녀가기를 기다렸어. 경은 들여다보고 나서, 자비로우신 섭리의 각별한 보살핌으로 (텀리 경의 말이 그랬어) 위티털리 부인이 잠이 들었노라고 알렸지. 그러자 케이트는 황급히 외출 차림을 하고는, 두어 시간 안에 돌아오겠다는 말을 남기고 삼촌 집을 향해 서둘러 떠났어.\n\n랄프 니클비에게는 운수 좋은 하루였어 — 그야말로 재수가 좋은 날이었지. 그는 두 손을 등 뒤로 맞잡고 자기 뒤편 작은 방을 이리저리 거닐면서, 아침나절부터 해 온 거래로 거둬들였거나 거둬들일 액수를 머릿속으로 죄다 더해 보고 있었어. 그의 입가에는 딱딱하고 매서운 미소가 그려졌지. 그 입을 이룬 선과 굴곡의 단단함이며, 차갑고 번득이는 눈의 음흉한 눈빛이며, 그 모두가 말해 주는 듯했어. 어떤 결단이나 술수로 이익을 더 불릴 수 있다면, 그는 그걸 위해 그것들을 기어이 불러낼 사람이라고 말이야.\n\n\"아주 좋아!\" 랄프가 말했어. 분명 그날 있었던 어떤 거래를 두고 한 말이었지. \"그자가 고리대금업자한테 대든단 말이지? 그래, 두고 보자고. '정직이 최선의 방책'이라? 그것도 한번 시험해 보자고.\"\n\n그는 멈춰 섰다가, 다시 걷기 시작했어.\n\n\"그자는 만족한다는 거지.\" 랄프가 미소를 띠며 말했어. \"제 알려진 인품이며 행실을 돈의 힘에 맞세우는 걸로 말이야 — 그자 말마따나 쓰레기 같은 돈에 말이지. 흥, 그 작자는 어지간히 멍청한 돌대가리가 틀림없어! 쓰레기라니, 돈이! 누구냐?\"\n\n\"접니다.\" 뉴먼 노그스가 들여다보며 말했어. \"조카 따님이요.\"\n\n\"그 애가 왜?\" 랄프가 날카롭게 물었어.\n\n\"여기 와 있습니다.\"\n\n\"여기에!\"\n뉴먼이 자기 작은 방 쪽으로 고개를 까딱여, 거기서 기다리고 있다는 걸 알렸어.\n\n\"무슨 일로 왔다더냐?\" 랄프가 물었어.\n\n\"모르겠는데요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어. \"물어볼까요?\" 그가 얼른 덧붙였지.\n\n\"아니.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"들여보내! 잠깐.\" 그는 식탁 위에 있던 자물쇠 채운 현금 상자를 황급히 치우고, 그 자리에 빈 지갑을 대신 놓았어. \"됐다.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"자, 이제 들어와도 좋아.\"\n\n뉴먼은 이 수작에 음울한 웃음을 지으며 아가씨에게 들어오라고 손짓했어. 그리고 의자를 하나 놓아 준 다음 물러갔지. 절뚝거리며 천천히 나가면서 어깨너머로 슬쩍 랄프를 돌아보면서 말이야.\n\n\"자.\" 랄프가 제법 퉁명스럽게 말했어. 그래도 다른 누구한테 보였을 것보다는 조금 더 다정한 구석이 묻어 있었지. \"자, 우리 — 얘야. 무슨 일이냐?\"\n\n케이트는 눈물이 그렁그렁한 눈을 들었어. 그리고 감정을 다잡으려 애쓰며 입을 떼려 했지만 헛일이었지. 그래서 다시 고개를 떨군 채 입을 다물고 있었어. 그 얼굴은 랄프의 눈에서 가려져 있었지만, 그래도 그는 케이트가 울고 있다는 걸 알 수 있었어.\n\n'이게 무슨 영문인지 짐작이 가는군!' 랄프는 한동안 말없이 케이트를 바라보다가 생각했어. '짐작이 가 — 짐작이 가고말고 — 영문을 알겠어. 흥! 흥!' 랄프는 그렇게 생각했어 — 아름다운 조카의 비통함을 지켜보며 순간 적잖이 당황했지. '뭐 해로울 게 있나? 그저 눈물 몇 방울일 뿐이고, 이건 저 애한테 더없이 좋은 교훈이야, 더없이 좋은 교훈이지.'\n\n\"무슨 일이냐?\" 랄프가 맞은편에 의자를 끌어다 앉으며 물었어.\n\n케이트가 별안간 단호하게 고개를 들고 대답하자, 그는 적잖이 움찔했지.\n\n\"저를 삼촌께 오게 만든 일은요,\" 케이트가 말했어. \"삼촌의 얼굴에 피가 솟구치게 하고, 듣기만 해도 화끈 달아오르게 만들 그런 일이에요. 제가 말하기조차 그러는 것처럼요. 저는 부당한 일을 당했어요. 제 감정이 짓밟히고, 모욕당하고, 도무지 아물 수 없을 만큼 상처 입었어요. 그것도 삼촌의 친구들 손에요.\"\n\n\"친구들이라니!\" 랄프가 매섭게 외쳤어. \"나한테 친구 따위 없다, 얘야.\"\n\n\"그럼 여기서 봤던 그 남자들 손에요.\" 케이트가 얼른 받아쳤어. \"그자들이 삼촌의 친구가 아니었고, 또 그자들이 어떤 자들인지 아셨다면 — 오, 그렇다면 삼촌, 저를 그들 틈에 데려간 게 더더욱 부끄러운 일이에요. 삼촌이 손님들을 잘못 믿었거나 잘 알지 못해서 저를 여기서 겪은 그런 일에 내몬 거라면, 그래도 무슨 그럴듯한 변명이라도 필요했겠지요. 하지만 만약 삼촌이 — 제가 지금 믿는 대로 — 그자들을 빤히 알면서도 그러신 거라면, 그건 더없이 비겁하고 잔인한 짓이에요.\"\n\n랄프는 이 거침없는 말에 어이가 없어 뒤로 물러서며, 더없이 매서운 눈으로 케이트를 노려봤어. 하지만 케이트는 당당하고 단단하게 그 시선을 맞받았지. 얼굴은 몹시 창백했지만, 그렇게 환히 빛이 어린 그 얼굴은 어느 때보다도 더 고결하고 아름다워 보였어.\n\n\"네 안에 그 녀석 피가 좀 흐르고 있구나.\" 랄프가 더없이 거친 어조로 말했어. 그 번득이는 눈빛에서 무언가가, 지난번 마지막으로 만났을 때의 니콜라스를 떠올리게 했거든.\n\n\"그랬으면 좋겠어요!\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"그렇다는 걸 알면 자랑스러울 거예요. 저는 어려요, 삼촌. 제 처지의 온갖 어려움과 비참함이 그걸 짓눌러 왔지만, 오늘은 도저히 견딜 수 없을 만큼 발끈 솟구쳤어요. 무슨 일이 닥치든, 저는 삼촌 동생의 자식으로서, 더는 이런 모욕을 견디지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"무슨 모욕이냐, 얘야?\" 랄프가 날카롭게 따져 물었어.\n\n\"여기서 무슨 일이 있었는지 떠올려 보고, 스스로에게 물어보세요.\" 케이트가 얼굴을 새빨갛게 붉히며 대답했어. \"삼촌, 부디 — 분명 그래 주시리라 믿어요 — 지금 제가 시달리는 그런 더럽고 욕스러운 동무에게서 저를 풀어 주셔야 해요. 화내고 거칠게 굴려는 게 아니에요.\" 케이트가 노인에게 다가가 그의 어깨에 팔을 얹으며 말했어. \"그렇게 보였다면 용서하세요, 삼촌. 하지만 삼촌은 제가 얼마나 괴로웠는지 모르세요, 정말 모르신다고요. 삼촌은 젊은 처녀의 마음이 어떤지 알 수가 없어요 — 제가 그걸 바랄 권리도 없지요. 하지만 제가 비참하고 가슴이 찢어진다고 말씀드리면, 삼촌은 분명 저를 도와주실 거예요. 분명, 분명 그러실 거예요!\"\n\n랄프는 잠시 케이트를 바라봤어. 그러고는 고개를 돌리고, 초조하게 발로 바닥을 굴렀지.\n\n\"저는 날이면 날마다 버텨 왔어요.\" 케이트가 그에게 몸을 숙이고, 자기 작은 손을 그의 손에 머뭇머뭇 얹으며 말했어. \"이 괴롭힘이 멎으리라는 희망으로요. 더없이 불행하면서도 즐거운 척 가장하지 않을 수 없는 채로, 날이면 날마다 버텨 왔어요. 저한테는 의논 상대도, 조언해 줄 사람도, 지켜 줄 사람도 없었어요. 엄마는 그들이 점잖고 부유하고 지체 높은 사람들인 줄로 알아요. 그런데 제가 어떻게 — 제가 어떻게 그 오해를 풀어 드리겠어요 — 엄마가 그런 자그마한 착각에 그토록 행복해하는데요. 엄마한테는 그게 유일한 행복인데 말이에요. 삼촌이 저를 맡기신 그 부인은 이렇게 미묘한 일을 털어놓을 만한 사람이 못 돼요. 그래서 마침내 삼촌께 온 거예요. 가까이에 있는 제 유일한 친구이자 — 사실상 제게 있는 단 하나뿐인 친구한테요 — 저를 도와 달라고 빌고 또 빌려고요.\"\n\n\"내가 너를 어떻게 돕겠느냐, 얘야?\" 랄프가 의자에서 일어나, 옛 버릇대로 방 안을 이리저리 거닐며 말했어.\n\n\"삼촌은 그 사람들 중 한 명한테 영향력이 있잖아요, 저 알아요.\" 케이트가 힘주어 받았어. \"삼촌의 한마디면 그자들이 이 사내답지 못한 짓을 그만두게 만들 수 있지 않나요?\"\n\n\"안 돼.\" 랄프가 별안간 돌아서며 말했어. \"적어도 — 그건 — 설령 그럴 수 있다 해도, 그 말은 못 해.\"\n\n\"못 하신다고요!\"\n\n\"못 해.\" 랄프가 우뚝 멈춰 서서 두 손을 등 뒤로 더 단단히 맞잡으며 말했어. \"그 말은 못 한다.\"\n\n케이트는 한두 걸음 물러서서, 자기가 제대로 들은 게 맞나 미심쩍어하는 듯 그를 바라봤어.\n\n\"우리는 사업으로 얽혀 있다.\" 랄프가 발끝과 뒤꿈치에 번갈아 몸을 싣고 흔들거리며, 조카의 얼굴을 차갑게 들여다보며 말했어. \"사업으로 말이야. 그러니 그들의 비위를 거스를 여유가 없어. 따지고 보면 이게 다 뭐냐? 사람은 누구나 제 시련이 있는 법이고, 이건 네 시련 가운데 하나인 거지. 어떤 처녀들은 그런 멋쟁이들을 제 발치에 거느리는 걸 자랑스러워할 거다.\"\n\n\"자랑스럽다고요!\" 케이트가 외쳤어.\n\n\"그자들을 경멸하는 게 잘못이라는 건 아니다.\" 랄프가 집게손가락을 치켜들며 받았어. \"아니지, 그 점에선 네가 분별 있게 처신하는 거고, 그야 처음부터 네가 그러리라는 걸 내가 알고 있었지. 그래. 다른 모든 점에서 너는 편안히 자리 잡고 있잖느냐. 견디기에 그리 대단한 일도 아니야. 설령 그 젊은 귀족이 네 뒤를 졸졸 쫓아다니며 시답잖은 헛소리를 네 귀에 속닥거린들, 그게 뭐 대수냐? 그건 떳떳치 못한 욕정이야. 그래 봤자지, 오래가지도 못해. 언젠가 또 다른 새것이 나타날 거고, 그러면 너는 풀려나는 거다. 그동안에는 —\"\n\n\"그동안에는,\" 케이트가 어울리는 자존심과 분노로 말을 가로챘어. \"저는 같은 여자들한테는 멸시받고, 남자들한테는 노리갯감이 되라는 거군요. 올바른 마음을 지닌 모든 여자에게 마땅히 손가락질당하고, 정직하고 떳떳한 모든 남자에게 업신여김당하면서요. 제 자신을 보는 눈에서도 무너지고, 저를 쳐다보는 모든 눈앞에서 더럽혀지면서요. 안 돼요, 손가락이 뼈가 드러나도록 일을 한대도, 더없이 거칠고 고된 노동에 내몰린대도, 그렇게는 못 해요. 저를 오해하지는 마세요. 삼촌의 추천을 욕되게 하진 않을게요. 계약 조건에 따라 떠날 자격이 생길 때까지는 삼촌이 들여보내 주신 그 집에 머물겠어요. 다만, 명심하세요, 그 남자들은 두 번 다시 안 볼 거예요. 그 집을 떠나면 저는 그들에게서도 삼촌에게서도 몸을 숨길 거예요. 그리고 고된 일로 엄마를 부양하려 애쓰면서, 적어도 마음 편히 살아갈 거예요. 하느님이 저를 도와주시리라 믿으면서요.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 케이트는 손을 한 번 내젓고 방을 나가 버렸어. 랄프 니클비를 조각상처럼 꼼짝 못 하게 남겨 둔 채로 말이야.\n\n케이트가 방문을 닫다가, 바로 그 곁에 뉴먼 노그스가 꼿꼿이 서 있는 걸 봤을 때, 그 놀라움이 어찌나 컸던지 하마터면 소리를 지를 뻔했어. 뉴먼은 벽의 작은 벽감 안에 마치 허수아비나, 겨울을 나려고 처박아 둔 가이 포크스 인형처럼 서 있었거든. 그런데 뉴먼이 손가락을 입에 갖다 대자, 케이트는 정신을 차리고 가까스로 참았어.\n\n\"그러지 마요.\" 뉴먼이 자기 구석에서 빠져나와 케이트와 함께 현관을 가로지르며 말했어. \"울지 마요, 울지 마요.\" 그런데 그렇게 말하는 뉴먼의 얼굴에는 아주 굵은 눈물 두 줄기가 흘러내리고 있었지.\n\n\"어떻게 된 일인지 다 압니다.\" 가엾은 노그스가 주머니에서 아주 낡은 걸레처럼 보이는 걸 꺼내 케이트의 눈을 닦아 주며 말했어. 마치 갓난아기를 다루듯 더없이 부드럽게 말이야. \"이제 마음이 풀어지는 거지요. 그래요, 그래요, 아주 좋아요. 그래야지요, 나는 그게 좋습니다. 그 사람 앞에서 무너지지 않은 건 잘한 일이에요. 그래요, 그래! 하하하! 오, 그렇고말고요. 가엾기도 하지!\"\n\n이런 두서없는 말들과 함께, 뉴먼은 앞서 말한 그 걸레로 제 눈을 닦았어. 그러고는 절뚝거리며 현관문으로 가서, 케이트를 내보내려고 문을 열어 주었지.\n\n\"이제 더는 울지 마요.\" 뉴먼이 속삭였어. \"곧 또 봅시다. 하! 하! 하! 그리고 다른 누군가도 그럴 겁니다. 그래요, 그래요. 호! 호!\"\n\n\"하느님의 축복이 함께하길 빌어요.\" 케이트가 서둘러 나가며 대답했어. \"하느님의 축복이 함께하길요.\"\n\n\"그쪽도요.\" 뉴먼이 그 말을 하려고 문을 다시 조금 열며 받았어. \"하, 하, 하! 호! 호! 호!\"\n\n그리고 뉴먼 노그스는 다시 한번 문을 열어 명랑하게 고개를 끄덕이고 웃었어 — 그러고는 문을 닫고서, 서글피 고개를 저으며 울었지.\n\n랄프는 문 닫히는 소리를 들을 때까지 똑같은 자세로 서 있었어. 그러다 어깨를 으쓱하더니, 방 안을 몇 바퀴 돌고는 — 처음에는 빠르다가 차츰 느려졌어. 자기 안으로 다시 가라앉으면서 말이야 — 책상 앞에 앉았지.\n\n이건 인간 본성의 수수께끼 가운데 하나야. 적어 둘 수는 있어도 풀어낼 수는 없는 그런 거 말이지. 랄프는 그 순간 순진하고 진실한 마음을 지닌 그 아가씨에게 한 짓을 두고 아무런 양심의 가책도 느끼지 않았어. 그 난봉꾼 고객들은 그가 예상했던 바로 그대로, 그가 가장 바라던 바로 그대로, 그에게 가장 이로울 바로 그대로 행동했지. 그런데도 그는 그들이 그런 짓을 했다는 것 때문에, 마음속 깊은 밑바닥에서부터 그들을 미워했어.\n\n\"으윽!\" 랄프가 인상을 잔뜩 찌푸리고 둘러보며, 두 난봉꾼의 얼굴이 머릿속에 떠오르자 움켜쥔 주먹을 부르르 떨며 말했어. \"이 대가를 치르게 해 주마. 오! 이 대가를 꼭 치르게 해 주마!\"\n\n고리대금업자가 위안을 얻으려고 자기 장부와 서류 쪽으로 돌아섰을 때, 그의 사무실 문밖에서는 한바탕 연극이 벌어지고 있었어. 만약 그가 무슨 수로든 그걸 알았더라면 적잖이 놀랐을 거야.\n\n뉴먼 노그스가 그 유일한 배우였지. 그는 문에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 문을 마주 보고 서 있었어. 그리고 외투 소매를 손목께까지 걷어붙인 채, 텅 빈 허공에다 더없이 힘차고, 정확하고, 거침없는 주먹질을 퍼붓고 있었지.\n\n언뜻 보면, 이건 그저 앉아서 일하는 사람이 가슴을 펴고 팔 근육을 단련하려는 현명한 운동쯤으로 보였을 거야. 하지만 땀으로 흠뻑 젖은 뉴먼 노그스의 얼굴에 떠오른 그 격렬한 열의와 기쁨, 그리고 바닥에서 다섯 자 여덟 치쯤 되는 특정한 한 패널을 향해 쉴 새 없이 연거푸 주먹을 날리면서도 더없이 지칠 줄 모르고 끈질기게 계속 휘둘러 대는 그 놀라운 기운을 보았다면, 눈썰미 있는 사람이라면 능히 알아챘을 거야. 뉴먼은 머릿속으로, 제 몸뚱이를 가장 부려 먹는 그 주인 랄프 니클비 씨를 숨이 끊어지기 직전까지 흠씬 두들겨 패고 있다는 걸 말이지.\n\n# CHAPTER 29\n\n포츠머스에서 벌인 그의 시도가 뜻밖에도 큰 성공을 거두고 인기를 끌자, 크럼블스 씨는 원래 머무르려고 잡아 둔 기간보다 두 주나 더 그 도시에 발길을 붙들어 두기로 했어. 그동안 니콜라스는 온갖 다양한 인물을 연기했는데, 그 성공이 조금도 시들지 않았지. 그리고 전에는 한 번도 극장에 나타난 적 없던 사람들을 어찌나 많이 끌어모았던지, 극단장은 니콜라스를 위한 자선 공연을 열면 꽤 짭짤한 벌이가 되겠다고 생각했어. 니콜라스가 제안받은 조건에 동의해서 자선 공연이 열렸고, 그걸로 그는 자그마치 이십 파운드라는 돈을 벌었지.\n\n이 뜻밖의 재산을 손에 쥐자 그가 가장 먼저 한 일은, 정직한 존 브로디에게 빌린 돈을 챙겨 보낸 거였어. 거기에 고맙다는 말과 존경의 인사를 잔뜩 곁들이고, 결혼 생활이 행복하기를 바란다는 진심 어린 축원도 함께 적었지. 뉴먼 노그스에게는 자기가 번 돈의 절반을 부쳤어. 그러면서 기회를 봐서 케이트한테 몰래 건네 달라고, 그리고 자기가 케이트를 얼마나 사랑하고 아끼는지 따뜻한 마음을 꼭 전해 달라고 부탁했지. 자기가 어떻게 지내고 있는지는 한마디도 하지 않았어. 그저 포츠머스 우체국으로 가명 앞으로 편지를 보내면 자기한테 잘 닿을 거라고만 알렸지. 그리고 그 고마운 친구에게, 어머니와 누이가 어떻게 지내는지 자세히 적어 달라고, 또 자기가 런던을 떠난 뒤로 랄프 니클비가 그들에게 베푼 그 대단한 일들이 무엇인지도 알려 달라고 간곡히 부탁했어.\n\n\"형은 기운이 없어 보여요.\" 편지를 부치고 난 다음 날 밤, 스마이크가 말했어.\n\n\"내가? 천만에!\" 니콜라스가 일부러 명랑한 척 대꾸했어. 솔직히 털어놓으면 이 아이가 밤새 마음 아파할 게 뻔했거든. \"누이 생각을 하고 있었어, 스마이크.\"\n\n\"누이요?\"\n\n\"응.\"\n\n\"형이랑 닮았어요?\" 스마이크가 물었어.\n\n\"글쎄, 다들 그렇다고들 하더라.\" 니콜라스가 웃으며 대답했어. \"다만 훨씬 더 예쁘지.\"\n\n\"그럼 정말 아름답겠네요.\" 스마이크가 두 손을 모으고 친구를 가만히 바라보며 한참 생각하더니 말했어.\n\n\"나만큼 너를 잘 모르는 사람이라면, 넌 아주 능숙한 아첨꾼이라고 할 거다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"그게 뭔지도 모르는걸요.\" 스마이크가 고개를 저으며 대답했어. \"나도 형 누이를 볼 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"그럼.\" 니콜라스가 소리쳤어. \"언젠가 우리 다 같이 모일 거야. 부자가 되면 말이야, 스마이크.\"\n\n\"나한테 이렇게 친절하고 잘해 주는 형한테는, 어째서 형한테 잘해 주는 사람이 아무도 없어요?\" 스마이크가 물었어. \"난 도무지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"그건 긴 이야기야.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그리고 네가 알아듣기엔 좀 어려울 것 같구나. 나한텐 적이 하나 있어. 적이 뭔지는 알지?\"\n\n\"아, 네, 그건 알아요.\" 스마이크가 말했어.\n\n\"그래, 다 그 사람 탓이야.\" 니콜라스가 말을 이었어. \"그 사람은 부자라서, 네 옛날 적인 스퀴어스처럼 쉽게 벌줄 수가 없어. 내 삼촌인데, 악당이지. 나한테 못된 짓을 했어.\"\n\n\"정말요?\" 스마이크가 몸을 바짝 앞으로 기울이며 물었어. \"이름이 뭐예요? 이름을 말해 줘요.\"\n\n\"랄프. 랄프 니클비.\"\n\n\"랄프 니클비.\" 스마이크가 따라 했어. \"랄프. 그 이름을 외워 둘래요.\"\n\n스마이크가 그 이름을 혼자 한 스무 번쯤 중얼거렸을 때, 문을 쾅쾅 두드리는 소리에 하던 일을 방해받았어. 그가 문을 열기도 전에, 무언극 배우인 폴레어 씨가 머리를 쑥 들이밀었지.\n\n폴레어 씨의 머리에는 보통 아주 동그란 모자가 얹혀 있었어. 모자 꼭대기가 유난히 높고 챙이 바짝 위로 말려 올라간 모양이었지. 이번에는 그 모자를 한쪽으로 잔뜩 기울여 썼는데, 뒷부분이 가장 덜 낡았다고 앞으로 돌려 쓴 거였어. 목에는 새빨간 털실 목도리를 둘렀는데, 흐트러진 끝자락이 다 닳은 뉴마켓 코트 아래로 삐죽 비어져 나와 있었지. 그 코트는 몸에 꼭 끼고 위까지 단추를 죄다 채운 것이었어. 손에는 아주 더러운 장갑 한 짝과 유리 손잡이가 달린 싸구려 정장용 지팡이를 들고 있었지. 한마디로 그의 차림새는 유난히 멋을 부린 것이었고, 평소 몸치장에 들이던 것보다 훨씬 더 공을 들였다는 게 보였어.\n\n\"안녕하세요.\" 폴레어 씨가 높다란 모자를 벗고 손가락으로 머리를 쓸어 넘기며 말했어. \"전할 말이 있어서 왔습니다. 흠!\"\n\n\"누구한테서, 무슨 일로?\" 니콜라스가 물었어. \"오늘 밤은 유난히 수상쩍게 구는군요.\"\n\n\"차갑다고요, 아마.\" 폴레어 씨가 대꾸했어. \"차갑겠죠, 아마. 그건 제 입장 탓이지 제 탓이 아닙니다, 존슨 씨. 양쪽 모두의 친구라는 제 입장이 그걸 요구하는 거예요.\" 폴레어 씨는 아주 인상적인 표정을 지으며 잠시 멈췄어. 그러고는 앞서 말한 그 모자 속을 뒤져, 묘하게 접은 누르스름한 갈색 종이쪽지를 꺼냈지. 그 안에서 그 종이가 깨끗하게 지켜 준 쪽지 한 장을 꺼내, 니콜라스에게 건네며 말했어.\n\n\"이걸 좀 읽어 주시겠습니까.\"\n\n니콜라스는 잔뜩 놀란 채로 쪽지를 받아 봉인을 뜯었어. 그러면서 폴레어 씨를 슬쩍 쳐다봤는데, 그는 눈썹을 찌푸리고 입을 잔뜩 오므린 채 위엄을 부리며 천장에 시선을 딱 고정하고 앉아 있었지.\n\n쪽지는 \"오거스터스 폴레어 귀하의 호의로, 존슨 귀하께\"라고 적혀 있었어. 니콜라스의 놀라움은 그 내용이 다음과 같은 간결한 문구로 쓰여 있다는 걸 알고도 조금도 가시지 않았지.\n\n\"렌빌 씨가 존슨 씨께 정중히 안부를 전하며, 내일 아침 몇 시에 극장에서 렌빌 씨를 만나는 게 가장 편하실지 알려 주시면 감사하겠습니다. 단원들이 보는 앞에서 존슨 씨의 코를 비틀어 주기 위함입니다.\n\n렌빌 씨는 존슨 씨께서 약속을 어기지 마시기를 부탁드립니다. 이 의식을 지켜볼 동료 두세 명을 초대해 두었기에, 무슨 일이 있어도 그들을 실망시킬 수 없기 때문입니다.\n포츠머스, 화요일 밤.\"\n\n이 무례함에 화가 치밀긴 했지만, 이런 식의 도전장이 어찌나 우습도록 황당하던지, 니콜라스는 입술을 깨물어야 했어. 그러고는 쪽지를 두세 번 거듭 읽고 나서야, 이 적의에 찬 전령에게 말을 걸 만큼 진지하고 엄한 표정을 겨우 지을 수 있었지. 그 전령은 여전히 천장에서 눈을 떼지 않았고, 얼굴 표정도 조금도 바꾸지 않았어.\n\n\"이 쪽지에 뭐가 적혀 있는지 아시오?\" 니콜라스가 마침내 물었어.\n\n\"네.\" 폴레어 씨가 잠깐 둘러보았다가 곧장 다시 천장으로 눈을 돌리며 대답했어.\n\n\"그런데 어떻게 이걸 감히 여기로 가져온 거요?\" 니콜라스가 쪽지를 잘게 찢어 전령을 향해 소나기처럼 휙 뿌리며 물었어. \"계단 아래로 걷어차일 거란 생각은 안 들었소?\"\n\n폴레어 씨는 이제 찢어진 쪽지 조각 몇 개가 얹힌 머리를 니콜라스 쪽으로 돌리더니, 똑같이 흔들림 없는 위엄으로 짧게 대답했어. \"안 들었는데요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 니콜라스가 그 높다란 모자를 집어 문 쪽으로 휙 던지며 말했어. \"당신 옷의 저 물건을 따라가는 게 좋을 거요. 안 그러면 단 십몇 초 안에 아주 불쾌하게 속았다는 걸 알게 될 테니까.\"\n\n\"이봐요, 존슨.\" 폴레어 씨가 갑자기 위엄을 죄다 잃고 항의했어. \"그러지 말아요. 신사 옷가지를 가지고 장난치는 거 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"방에서 나가시오.\" 니콜라스가 받아쳤어. \"어떻게 감히 이런 심부름을 하겠다고 여기 들이닥친 거요, 이 못된 작자야?\"\n\n\"쳇! 쳇!\" 폴레어 씨가 목도리를 풀고 천천히 거기서 빠져나오며 말했어. \"자, 이만하면 됐어요.\"\n\n\"됐다고!\" 니콜라스가 그에게 다가서며 소리쳤어. \"썩 꺼지시오.\"\n\n\"쳇! 쳇! 글쎄, 말하잖아요.\" 폴레어 씨가 더 이상의 분노를 막으려는 듯 손을 내저으며 대꾸했어. \"진심이 아니었다니까요. 그냥 장난삼아 가져온 거예요.\"\n\n\"다음번엔 그런 장난을 칠 때 조심하는 게 좋을 거요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"안 그러면 코를 비틀어 준다는 말이, 그 농담을 한 사람한테 꽤 위험한 경고가 될 수도 있으니까. 그런데 이건 장난삼아 쓴 거요?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 아뇨, 그게 제일 우스운 점이죠.\" 배우가 대답했어. \"완전히 진심이었어요. 정말이라니까요.\"\n\n니콜라스는 눈앞의 그 우스꽝스러운 꼴을 보고 웃음을 참을 수가 없었어. 어느 때고 화보다는 웃음을 자아내기 십상인 모습이었는데, 특히 그 순간이 그랬지. 폴레어 씨가 한쪽 무릎을 바닥에 대고 낡은 모자를 손 위에서 빙글빙글 돌리며, 모자의 보풀이 떨어져 나갔을까 봐 죽도록 괴로운 척하고 있었거든. 굳이 말할 필요도 없지만, 그 모자는 이미 여러 달 전부터 자랑할 만한 보풀 따위는 하나도 없었어.\n\n\"자, 그럼,\" 니콜라스가 저도 모르게 웃으며 말했어. \"어찌 된 일인지 좀 설명해 보시오.\"\n\n\"왜요, 어떻게 된 건지 말씀드리죠.\" 폴레어 씨가 아주 태연하게 의자에 앉으며 말했어. \"당신이 여기 온 뒤로 렌빌은 줄곧 단역만 맡았어요. 예전처럼 매일 밤 박수를 받기는커녕, 단원들이 그를 아무도 아닌 사람처럼 그냥 무대에 올려 보내는 거예요.\"\n\n\"박수를 받는다는 게 무슨 말이오?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 폴레어 씨가 외쳤어. \"당신은 정말 세상 물정 모르는 양치기로군요, 존슨! 처음 무대에 등장할 때 객석에서 터지는 갈채 말이에요. 그래서 그는 밤마다 무대에 올라도 박수 한 번 못 받았는데, 당신은 적어도 두 차례, 어떤 땐 세 차례나 받잖아요. 그러다 마침내 완전히 절망에 빠져서, 어젯밤엔 진짜 칼을 들고 티볼트를 연기하면서 당신을 콱 찌를까 하는 생각까지 절반쯤 했답니다. 위험하게는 아니고, 딱 한두 달 드러눕게 할 만큼만요.\"\n\n\"아주 사려 깊군요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"네, 사정을 보면 그렇다고 봐요. 자기 배우 평판이 걸린 일이니까요.\" 폴레어 씨가 자못 진지하게 말했어. \"그런데 막상 용기가 안 났던지, 당신을 괴롭히면서 동시에 자기는 인기를 끌 다른 방법을 찾기 시작했어요. 바로 그게 핵심이거든요. 유명세, 유명세, 그게 다예요. 이런, 그가 만약 당신을 콱 찔렀더라면,\" 폴레어 씨가 머릿속으로 계산을 하려고 잠시 멈췄어. \"그건 그한테 일주일에 — 아, 일주일에 팔구 실링은 됐을 거예요. 실수로 사람을 죽일 뻔한 배우를 보겠다고 온 동네 사람이 다 몰려왔을 테니까요. 어쩌면 런던에서 일자리를 얻었을지도 몰라요. 아무튼 인기를 끌 다른 방법을 찾아야 했고, 그러다 이 생각이 떠오른 거죠. 정말 영리한 생각이에요. 당신이 겁쟁이처럼 굴면서 코를 비틀게 내버려 뒀다면, 그건 신문에 났을 거예요. 당신이 그를 고발해서 보호를 청했어도 그것도 신문에 났을 거고, 그럼 당신만큼이나 그도 입에 오르내렸겠죠. 알겠어요?\"\n\n\"아, 그렇겠군.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어. \"그런데 내가 거꾸로 그의 코를 비틀어 버리면 어떻게 되오? 그래도 그가 한밑천 잡게 되오?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 그건 아닐걸요.\" 폴레어 씨가 머리를 긁적이며 대답했어. \"그러면 거기엔 아무 낭만도 없고, 좋은 쪽으로 이름이 알려지지도 않을 테니까요. 사실대로 말하면 그도 그건 별로 계산에 넣지 않았어요. 당신이 워낙 말씨가 부드럽고 여자들한테 인기가 많아서, 당신이 싸우려 들 줄은 우리도 의심하지 않았거든요. 하지만 당신이 정말 싸운다면, 그한텐 거기서 쉽게 빠져나올 방법이 있어요. 그건 틀림없어요.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어. \"내일 아침에 한번 해 봅시다. 그동안 우리가 만난 이야기는 당신 마음대로 좋을 대로 전하시오. 잘 자요.\"\n\n폴레어 씨는 짓궂은 장난을 즐기는 데다 양심이라곤 도무지 없는 사람으로 동료 배우들 사이에 꽤 알려져 있었어. 그래서 니콜라스는 그가 비극 배우를 몰래 부추겨 이런 짓을 하게 했으리라는 걸, 게다가 그 심부름을 받아들이는 뜻밖의 반응에 김이 새지만 않았더라면 아주 거만하게 굴었으리라는 걸 별로 의심하지 않았지. 하지만 그를 진지하게 상대할 가치는 없었어. 그래서 니콜라스는 또 한 번 무례를 저지르면 머리통이 깨지는 벌을 받을 거라고 슬쩍 귀띔하며 그 무언극 배우를 돌려보냈어. 그러자 폴레어 씨는 그 경고를 아주 기분 좋게 받아들이고, 자기 우두머리와 의논하러 걸어갔지. 그러고는 농담을 계속 이어 가기에 가장 좋겠다 싶은 식으로 자기가 한 일을 둘러댈 참이었어.\n\n폴레어 씨는 분명히 니콜라스가 잔뜩 겁에 질려 있다고 전했을 거야. 다음 날 아침 그 젊은이가 여느 때처럼 아주 느긋하게 극장으로 걸어 내려가 보니, 단원들이 죄다 모여 뭔가를 잔뜩 기대하는 눈치였거든. 게다가 렌빌 씨는 가장 험상궂은 무대용 표정을 짓고 탁자 위에 위풍당당하게 앉아, 도전하듯 휘파람을 불고 있었어.\n\n자, 여자들은 니콜라스 편이었고, 남자들은 (질투가 나서) 실망한 비극 배우 편이었어. 그래서 남자들은 그 무시무시한 렌빌 씨 둘레에 작은 무리를 이루었고, 여자들은 조금 떨어진 곳에서 떨리고 불안한 마음으로 지켜보고 있었지. 니콜라스가 걸음을 멈추고 인사하자, 렌빌 씨가 비웃는 웃음을 터뜨리며 강아지의 생태에 관한 무슨 일반적인 말을 한마디 던졌어.\n\n\"오!\" 니콜라스가 조용히 둘러보며 말했어. \"거기 있었군요?\"\n\n\"이 종놈!\" 렌빌 씨가 오른팔을 휘두르며 연극하듯 성큼성큼 니콜라스에게 다가서며 받아쳤어. 그런데 어쩐 일인지 바로 그 순간 그는 좀 흠칫 놀란 듯했어. 니콜라스가 자기가 기대한 만큼 겁에 질려 보이지 않았거든. 그래서 갑자기 어색하게 우뚝 멈춰 섰고, 모여 있던 여자들이 그 모습에 까르르 날카로운 웃음을 터뜨렸지.\n\n\"내 경멸과 증오의 대상!\" 렌빌 씨가 말했어. \"나는 너를 멸시하노라.\"\n\n니콜라스는 이 연기가 뜻밖에도 너무 재미있어서 웃음을 터뜨렸어. 그러자 여자들이 응원이라도 하듯 전보다 더 크게 웃었지. 그러자 렌빌 씨는 가장 신랄한 미소를 지으며, 그 여자들이 \"앞잡이들\"이라는 의견을 내뱉었어.\n\n\"하지만 저들이 너를 지켜 주진 못할 것이다!\" 비극 배우가 니콜라스를 위로 훑어보며 말했어. 부츠에서 시작해 정수리에서 끝나는 시선이었지. 그러고 나서 아래로 훑어보았는데, 정수리에서 시작해 부츠에서 끝나는 시선이었어. 누구나 알다시피 이 두 시선은 무대에서 도전을 뜻하지. \"저들이 너를 지켜 주진 못할 것이다, 애송이!\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 렌빌 씨는 팔짱을 끼고, 니콜라스에게 그 표정을 지어 보였어. 멜로드라마 공연에서 폭군 같은 왕들이 \"저놈을 성의 해자 아래 가장 깊은 지하 감옥으로 끌고 가라!\"라고 말할 때 그가 으레 짓던 그 표정 말이야. 거기에 쇠고랑 절그럭거리는 소리까지 곁들이면, 한때 엄청난 효과를 거두기로 유명한 표정이었지.\n\n쇠고랑이 없어서였는지 어쩐지는 몰라도, 어쨌든 그 표정은 렌빌 씨의 상대에게 별 깊은 인상을 주지 못했어. 오히려 그의 얼굴에 떠오른 좋은 기분을 더 돋우는 것 같았지. 결투가 이 단계에 이르자, 니콜라스의 코가 비틀리는 걸 보겠다고 일부러 나온 신사 한두 명이 조바심을 내기 시작했어. 어차피 할 거면 당장 하는 게 낫고, 렌빌 씨가 안 할 작정이면 그렇다고 말해서 사람들을 여기 마냥 기다리게 하지 말라고 투덜댔지. 이렇게 재촉당하자 비극 배우는 그 작업을 해치우려고 오른쪽 코트 소맷부리를 가다듬고, 아주 위풍당당하게 니콜라스에게 걸어갔어. 니콜라스는 그가 필요한 만큼 가까이 다가오도록 내버려 두었다가, 조금도 흐트러짐 없이 그를 때려눕혔지.\n\n당황한 비극 배우가 무대 바닥에서 미처 머리를 들기도 전에, 렌빌 부인이 (앞서 슬쩍 비쳤듯이 흥미로운 처지에 있었지) 여자들 뒷줄에서 뛰쳐나왔어. 그러고는 찢어지는 비명을 지르며 남편의 몸 위로 몸을 던졌지.\n\n\"이게 보이느냐, 괴물아? 이게 보이느냐?\" 렌빌 씨가 일어나 앉아, 허리를 꽉 끌어안고 엎드린 아내를 가리키며 소리쳤어.\n\n\"자,\" 니콜라스가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했어. \"어젯밤 나한테 보낸 그 건방진 쪽지를 사과하시오. 더 이상 말로 시간 끌지 말고.\"\n\n\"절대 안 한다!\" 렌빌 씨가 소리쳤어.\n\n\"해요, 해요, 해요!\" 아내가 비명을 질렀어. \"날 위해서, 날 위해서요, 렌빌. 부질없는 형식 따위는 다 버려요. 안 그러면 내가 당신 발치에서 시들어 가는 시체가 되는 꼴을 보게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"이거 참 가슴 아프군!\" 렌빌 씨가 주위를 둘러보며 손등으로 눈을 훔치며 말했어. \"혈육의 정은 강하구나. 약한 남편이자 아버지가, 장차 아버지가 될 사람이 누그러지노라. 사과하겠다.\"\n\n\"공손하고 순순히 말이오?\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"공손하고 순순히.\" 비극 배우가 위쪽을 노려보며 대답했어. \"하지만 오직 이 여자를 살리기 위해서다. 언젠가 때가 올 테니까.\"\n\n\"아주 좋소.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"렌빌 부인이 순산하시길 바라오. 그리고 그때가 와서 당신이 아버지가 되면, 용기가 있거든 사과를 도로 거두시오. 자. 다음번엔 당신 질투가 어디까지 당신을 끌고 가는지 조심하시오. 그리고 너무 멀리 나서기 전에 당신 경쟁자의 성질부터 알아보는 게 좋을 거요.\" 니콜라스는 이런 작별의 충고를 남기며, 렌빌 씨의 손에서 날아가 떨어진 물푸레나무 지팡이를 집어 들었어. 그러고는 그걸 반으로 부러뜨려 조각을 그에게 던지고, 걸어 나가며 구경꾼들에게 가볍게 고개 숙여 인사하고는 물러났지.\n\n그날 밤 니콜라스는 더없이 깊은 존경을 받았어. 아침에 그의 코가 비틀리는 걸 보고 싶어 안달하던 사람들이, 이때다 싶어 그를 한쪽으로 데려가서는 아주 감격스럽게 말했지. 그 렌빌이라는 작자를 그렇게 제대로 다뤄 줘서 얼마나 다행인지 모른다고, 그는 정말 못 봐줄 작자였다고 말이야. 그리고 놀랍게도 다들 하나같이, 자기들도 언젠가 한 번씩은 그 작자에게 마땅한 벌을 내릴까 생각했었는데, 그저 자비심 때문에 참았던 거라고들 했어. 사실 이 모든 이야기가 한결같이 똑같은 식으로 끝나는 걸 보면, 크럼블스 씨 극단의 남자 단원들만큼 인정 많고 마음씨 고운 사람들도 세상에 없을 것 같았지.\n\n니콜라스는 이 승리를, 극장이라는 작은 세계에서 거둔 성공과 마찬가지로 더없이 절제 있고 좋은 기분으로 받아들였어. 풀 죽은 렌빌 씨는 객석 위층 관람석으로 소년 하나를 들여보내 야유를 시키며 마지막 발악으로 복수를 노렸지만, 그 소년은 성난 관객들의 제물이 되어 입장료도 돌려받지 못한 채 곧장 쫓겨났지.\n\n\"자, 스마이크,\" 첫 작품이 끝나고 집에 갈 옷을 거의 다 갈아입었을 때 니콜라스가 말했어. \"편지는 아직 없었니?\"\n\n\"있어요.\" 스마이크가 대답했어. \"우체국에서 이걸 받아 왔어요.\"\n\n\"뉴먼 노그스한테서 왔구나.\" 니콜라스가 빽빽하게 적힌 수신인 글씨에 눈길을 던지며 말했어. \"그 사람 글씨는 알아보기가 쉽지 않아. 어디 보자, 어디 보자.\"\n\n반 시간이나 편지에 눈을 박고 들여다본 끝에, 그는 겨우 내용을 다 파악할 수 있었어. 그런데 그 내용은 분명 그의 마음을 편하게 해 줄 만한 게 아니었지. 뉴먼은 십 파운드를 도로 돌려보내며 이렇게 적었어. 알아보니 니클비 부인도 케이트도 지금 당장 돈이 아쉬운 처지는 아니라는 걸 확인했고, 머지않아 니콜라스가 그 돈이 더 필요할 때가 올지도 모른다고 말이야. 그는 자기가 이제부터 하려는 말에 놀라지 말라고 당부했어. 나쁜 소식은 없고, 다들 건강하다고 했지. 하지만 어떤 사정이 생길지도, 아니 이미 생기고 있을지도 모른다고, 그렇게 되면 케이트한테 오빠의 보호가 꼭 필요해질 거라고 했어. 그리고 만약 그렇게 되면, 다음 우편이나 그다음 우편으로 그렇게 알리는 편지를 쓰겠다고 뉴먼은 말했지.\n\n니콜라스는 이 대목을 몇 번이고 읽었어. 그리고 생각하면 할수록 랄프가 무슨 흉계를 꾸미고 있는 게 아닌가 하는 두려움이 들기 시작했지. 한두 번은 무슨 일이 있어도 한 시간도 지체하지 않고 당장 런던으로 떠나고 싶은 충동이 일었어. 하지만 잠깐 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 정말로 그런 조치가 필요했다면 뉴먼이 진작 터놓고 그렇게 말했으리라는 확신이 들었지.\n\n\"아무튼 여기 사람들한테는 내가 갑자기 떠날지도 모른다고 미리 일러 둬야겠어.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"지체 없이 그렇게 해야지.\" 이 생각이 떠오르자 그는 모자를 집어 들고 분장실로 서둘러 갔어.\n\n\"자, 존슨 씨,\" 화려한 왕족 의상을 입고 거기 앉아 있던 크럼블스 부인이, 신동 아이를 처녀 역할로 어머니 같은 품에 안은 채 말했어. \"다음 주엔 라이드로, 그다음엔 윈체스터로, 그다음엔—\"\n\n\"제가 걱정되는 게 있는데요.\" 니콜라스가 끼어들었어. \"여러분이 여길 떠나기 전에 제가 여러분과 함께하는 일을 그만두게 될 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그만두다니요!\" 크럼블스 부인이 놀라서 두 손을 치켜들며 소리쳤어.\n\n\"그만두다니요!\" 스네벨리치 양이 타이츠 속에서 어찌나 떨던지, 정말로 극단장 부인의 어깨에 손을 얹어 몸을 지탱하며 소리쳤어.\n\n\"아니, 설마 떠난다는 말은 아니겠지!\" 그러든 부인이 크럼블스 부인 쪽으로 다가서며 외쳤어. \"원, 별소릴! 말도 안 돼.\"\n\n신동 아이는 정이 많은 데다 잘 흥분하는 성격이라 큰 소리로 울음을 터뜨렸고, 벨바우니 양과 브라바사 양은 정말로 눈물을 흘렸어. 남자 배우들조차 이야기를 멈추고 \"떠난다고!\"라는 말을 따라 했지. 그래도 그 가운데 몇몇은 (그날 가장 큰 소리로 그를 축하해 주던 사람들이었어) 서로 눈짓을 주고받았어. 그렇게 잘나가는 경쟁자가 사라지는 게 영 싫지만은 않다는 듯이 말이야. 사실 정직한 폴레어 씨는 야만인 분장을 다 갖춘 채로, 함께 포터 한 잔을 나누던 악마 역 배우에게 바로 그런 의견을 대놓고 말로 다 털어놓았지.\n\n니콜라스는 아직 무어라 확실히 말할 수는 없지만 아무래도 그렇게 될 것 같다고 짤막하게 말했어. 그러고는 되도록 빨리 거기서 빠져나와 집으로 돌아갔지. 뉴먼의 편지를 한 번 더 곱씹어 보고, 다시 곰곰이 헤아려 보려고 말이야.\n\n그 잠 못 이루는 밤 내내, 여러 주 동안 그의 시간과 생각을 차지하던 모든 일이 그에게 얼마나 하찮게 느껴졌는지. 그리고 한 가지 생각이 얼마나 끊임없이 줄곧 그의 마음에 떠올랐는지. 케이트가 어떤 큰 고난과 고통 한복판에서, 바로 이 순간에도 그를 애타게 찾고 있을지도 모른다는, 그것도 헛되이 찾고 있을지도 모른다는 생각이었지!\n\n# CHAPTER 30\n\n빈센트 크럼블스 씨는 니콜라스가 곧 극단을 떠날지도 모른다고 공개적으로 알리자, 깊은 슬픔과 당황의 기색을 잔뜩 드러냈다. 그러고는 절망에 빠진 나머지, 정해진 급료를 곧 올려 주겠다는 막연한 약속까지 내비쳤다. 게다가 그가 쓴 작품에 딸린 부수입도 늘려 주겠다고 했다. 그래도 니콜라스가 극단을 떠나기로 굳게 마음먹은 걸 알자 — 니콜라스는 이제 결심을 굳혔거든. 뉴먼한테서 더 이상 소식이 오지 않더라도, 무슨 일이 있어도 런던으로 가서 동생이 정확히 어떤 처지인지 알아보고 마음을 놓겠다고 말이야 — 크럼블스 씨는 그가 다시 돌아올 가능성이나 따져 보며 마음을 달랠 수밖에 없었다. 그러면서 그가 떠나기 전에 최대한 써먹으려고 재빠르고 힘찬 수를 쓰기로 했다.\n\n\"어디 보자.\" 크럼블스 씨가 무법자 가발을 벗으며 말했다. 사건 전체를 차분한 머리로 살펴보려는 거였지. \"어디 보자. 오늘이 수요일 밤이지. 아침 일찍 포스터부터 붙여서, 내일 자네가 정말로 마지막 무대에 선다고 알리는 거야.\"\n\n\"하지만 그게 제 마지막 무대가 아닐 수도 있잖아요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다. \"갑자기 불려 가는 게 아니라면, 주말 전에 떠나서 폐를 끼치고 싶지는 않거든요.\"\n\n\"그럼 더 좋지.\" 크럼블스 씨가 대꾸했다. \"목요일에 정말로 자네 마지막 무대를 올리고 — 금요일엔 단 하룻밤 더 출연하기로 다시 계약하고 — 토요일엔 자리를 못 구해 아쉬워한 수많은 영향력 있는 후원자들의 바람에 따라 또 무대에 서는 거야. 그러면 사흘 다 제법 괜찮은 관객이 들겠지.\"\n\n\"그럼 제가 마지막 무대를 세 번 서는 거네요?\" 니콜라스가 웃으며 물었다.\n\n\"그래.\" 단장이 좀 짜증스러운 기색으로 머리를 긁적이며 대답했다. \"세 번으로는 부족하지. 더 많지 않으면 영 어설프고 격에도 안 맞아. 하지만 어쩔 수 없으면 어쩔 수 없는 거니까, 더 말해 봤자 소용없지. 뭔가 새로운 게 있으면 아주 좋을 텐데. 자네 조랑말 등에 올라타서 우스운 노래 한 곡 못 부르나?\"\n\n\"못 해요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다. \"정말로 못 합니다.\"\n\n\"그게 전에는 돈을 좀 벌어 줬거든.\" 크럼블스 씨가 실망한 표정으로 말했다. \"화려한 불꽃놀이는 어떻게 생각하나?\"\n\n\"돈이 꽤 들 것 같은데요.\" 니콜라스가 무덤덤하게 대답했다.\n\n\"십팔 펜스면 돼.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했다. \"자네가 사다리 꼭대기에 올라서고, 신동은 한쪽에서 멋진 자세를 잡고, 뒤쪽 투명 막에는 '안녕히!'라고 쓰는 거야. 그리고 양 옆에 아홉 사람이 손마다 폭죽을 하나씩 들고 — 그 열여덟 개를 한꺼번에 터뜨리는 거지 — 아주 웅장할 거야. 객석에서 보면 어마어마하지, 정말 어마어마해.\"\n\n니콜라스는 이 거창한 연출에 도무지 감동받은 기색이 아니었다. 오히려 아주 불경스러운 태도로 그 제안을 받아들이고는 한참 신나게 웃어 버렸다. 그러자 크럼블스 씨는 그 계획을 시작도 하기 전에 접어 버렸다. 그러고는 우울하게 말했다. 결투 장면이랑 혼파이프 춤으로 최대한 그럴듯한 프로그램을 짜서, 정통 연극으로 밀고 나가는 수밖에 없겠다고 말이야.\n\n이 계획을 당장 실행에 옮기려고, 단장은 곧장 옆에 붙은 자그마한 분장실로 갔다. 거기서는 크럼블스 부인이 멜로드라마 속 황후 의상을 십구 세기 평범한 부인네 옷으로 갈아입고 있었다. 그리고 이 부인과 솜씨 좋은 그러든 부인의 도움을 받아 — 그러든 부인은 프로그램 짜는 데 아주 천재적이었거든. 감탄 부호를 척척 집어넣는 솜씨가 대단했고, 오랜 경험으로 어디에 제일 큰 대문자를 박아야 하는지 정확히 알았지 — 그는 포스터 문구를 진지하게 짜기 시작했다.\n\n\"휴!\" 니콜라스가 프롬프터 의자에 등을 기대며 한숨을 쉬었다. 그는 막간극에서 비쩍 마른 재단사 역을 하던 스마이크에게 필요한 지시를 손짓으로 전해 둔 참이었다. 스마이크는 윗도리 자락이 한쪽밖에 없고, 큼지막한 구멍이 난 작은 손수건에 털실 잠자리 모자에 빨간 코까지, 무대 위 재단사 특유의 표시를 다 갖추고 있었지. \"휴! 이게 다 끝나 버렸으면 좋겠어.\"\n\n\"끝나다니요, 존슨 씨!\" 그의 뒤에서 여자 목소리가 무슨 서글픈 놀라움이 섞인 투로 되뇌었다.\n\n\"확실히 신사답지 못한 말이었네요.\" 니콜라스가 누가 말한 건지 보려고 고개를 들었다가 스네벨리치 양인 걸 알아보고는 말했다. \"듣고 계신 줄 알았다면 그런 말은 안 했을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"저 디그비 씨, 정말 사랑스럽지 않아요!\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했다. 재단사가 작품 끝에서 반대편으로 큰 박수를 받으며 퇴장하던 참이었다. (스마이크의 무대용 이름이 디그비였거든.)\n\n\"이따가 그 친구한테 당신이 그렇게 말했다고 전해 줄게요. 좋아할 테니까요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다.\n\n\"어머, 못된 사람 같으니!\" 스네벨리치 양이 받아쳤다. \"하긴 저 사람이 제 마음을 안다고 해서 별로 꺼릴 것도 없겠네요. 다른 어떤 사람한테라면, 정말이지, 좀 다를지도 모르지만요.\" 여기서 스네벨리치 양은 질문을 기다리는 듯 말을 멈췄다. 하지만 아무 질문도 오지 않았다. 니콜라스가 더 심각한 일을 생각하고 있었거든.\n\n\"참 친절도 하셔라.\" 스네벨리치 양이 잠깐 잠잠하다가 다시 입을 열었다. \"이렇게 밤마다, 밤마다, 아무리 피곤해도 여기 앉아서 저 사람을 기다려 주시고요. 저 사람한테 그렇게 공을 들이면서, 그걸 다 무슨 금이라도 캐는 것처럼 기쁘고 선뜻한 마음으로 해 주시잖아요!\"\n\n\"저 친구는 내가 베풀 수 있는 친절을 다 받을 만한 사람이에요. 그것보다 훨씬 더요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다. \"세상에 숨 쉬는 그 누구보다 고마움을 알고, 마음이 한결같고, 정 많은 사람이거든요.\"\n\n\"그런데 참 별나기도 하죠.\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했다. \"안 그래요?\"\n\n\"하느님이 저 친구를, 그리고 저 친구를 저렇게 만든 사람들을 도와주시기를. 정말 그렇긴 해요.\" 니콜라스가 고개를 저으며 대답했다.\n\n\"저 친구는 입이 어찌나 무거운지 정말 지독하다니까.\" 폴레어 씨가 말했다. 조금 전에 다가와서는 이제 이야기에 끼어든 거였다. \"아무도 저 친구한테서 뭘 캐낼 수가 없어.\"\n\n\"캐내긴 뭘 캐낸다는 겁니까?\" 니콜라스가 좀 퉁명스럽게 돌아서며 물었다.\n\n\"이런! 자넨 어쩜 그렇게 불같나, 존슨!\" 폴레어 씨가 무용화 뒤축을 끌어올리며 대꾸했다. \"난 그저 여기 사람들이 으레 품을 만한 궁금증을 말한 거야. 저 친구가 여태 살면서 뭘 하고 다녔는지 알고 싶어 한다는 거지.\"\n\n\"가엾은 친구! 내 생각엔 뻔하잖아요. 저 친구는 그 사람들한테든 누구한테든 대단할 만한 무슨 일을 하고 다닐 머리가 없는 사람이라는 거요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다.\n\n\"그래.\" 배우가 등 반사판에 비친 제 얼굴을 들여다보며 받아쳤다. \"하지만 바로 거기서 온갖 의문이 생기는 거지.\"\n\n\"무슨 의문요?\" 니콜라스가 물었다.\n\n\"왜, 저 친구가 누구고 뭐 하는 사람인지, 그리고 그렇게 다른 자네 둘이 어쩌다 그렇게 붙어 다니는 단짝이 됐는지 말이야.\" 폴레어 씨가 대답했다. 뭔가 거슬리는 말을 할 기회가 생겨 신이 난 거였다. \"그게 다들 입에 올리는 얘기거든.\"\n\n\"극장 안 '다들'이겠죠?\" 니콜라스가 경멸하듯 말했다.\n\n\"안에서도 밖에서도지.\" 배우가 대답했다. \"왜, 그 렌빌이 말하길—\"\n\n\"내가 그 사람 입은 확실히 다물게 한 줄 알았는데.\" 니콜라스가 얼굴이 붉어지며 끼어들었다.\n\n\"아마 그랬겠지.\" 끄떡도 않는 폴레어 씨가 받아쳤다. \"그랬다 치고, 그 사람이 입 다물기 전에 이런 말을 했거든. 렌빌 말로는 자네가 영락없는 막대기 같은 형편없는 배우인데, 자네를 둘러싼 수수께끼 덕분에 여기 사람들한테 인기를 끈 거고, 크럼블스가 제 잇속 때문에 그 수수께끼를 부추기는 거래. 하긴 렌빌은 거기에 아무것도 없다고 믿는다더군. 그냥 자네가 어디선가 무슨 짓을 저질러서 곤경에 빠지고 도망쳐 온 것 말고는 말이야.\"\n\n\"오!\" 니콜라스가 억지로 웃음을 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그건 그 사람이 한 말 중 일부야.\" 폴레어 씨가 덧붙였다. \"난 양쪽 다 친구니까 말해 주는 거고, 아주 비밀이야. 난 그 사람 생각에 동의 안 해, 알겠지. 그 사람은 디그비가 바보라기보다 악당에 가깝다고 보더라고. 그리고 그 무게 잡는 역을 맡는 늙은 플러거스 있잖아, 그 사람 말로는 재작년 시즌에 코벤트 가든에서 전갈을 전하고 다닐 때, 마차 정류장 근처를 어슬렁거리던 소매치기가 하나 있었는데 그 얼굴이 디그비랑 똑 닮았더래. 하긴 그 사람도 아주 옳은 말을 하더군. 디그비가 그 사람이 아닐 수도 있고, 그냥 그 형제나 가까운 친척일 수도 있다고 말이야.\"\n\n\"오!\" 니콜라스가 또 소리쳤다.\n\n\"그래.\" 폴레어 씨가 흔들림 없이 차분하게 말했다. \"다들 그렇게 말한다니까. 자네가 정말 알아야 할 것 같아서 말해 주는 거야. 오! 드디어 이 복덩이 신동이 나오네. 어휴, 요 깜찍한 사기꾼, 내가 정말—다 됐다, 아가야,—웃기시네—올려요, 그러든 부인, 인기 스타가 사람들 깨우게 해 줍시다.\"\n\n폴레어 씨는 마지막 말 가운데 영문 모르는 신동을 추켜세우는 대목은 큰 소리로 내뱉고, 나머지는 니콜라스에게 은밀히 '곁말'로 던졌다. 그러고는 올라가는 막을 눈으로 좇으며, 처녀 역으로 등장하는 크럼블스 양이 받는 환영을 비웃듯 바라보았다. 그러고는 더 효과적으로 등장하려고 한두 걸음 물러섰다가, 예비 울부짖음을 한 번 내지르고는 이를 딱딱 부딪치며 양철 도끼를 휘두르는 인디언 야만인 역으로 '등장했다'.\n\n'이런 게 사람들이 우리에 대해 지어내서 입에서 입으로 떠벌리는 이야기들이군!' 니콜라스는 생각했다. '크든 작든 어떤 사회에 절대 용서받지 못할 죄를 짓고 싶거든, 성공해 봐라. 그러면 어떤 죄든 다 용서해 줘도 그 죄만큼은 안 봐주거든.'\n\n\"설마 저 심술궂은 작자가 한 말을 마음에 두시는 건 아니죠, 존슨 씨?\" 스네벨리치 양이 더없이 다정한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다. \"여기 계속 남을 거라면 나서서 한바탕 붙어 볼 만하다 싶겠죠. 하지만 사정이 이러니, 목이 쉬도록 떠들라죠. 그런데 보세요.\" 스마이크가 다가오자 니콜라스가 덧붙였다. \"저기 그 사람들 입방아의 한 대상이 오네요. 그러니 저 친구랑 같이 작별 인사를 해야겠어요.\"\n\n\"안 돼요, 두 분 다 그런 말씀 못 하시게 할 거예요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 받아쳤다. \"저희 집에 오셔서 엄마를 뵈셔야 해요. 엄마가 오늘에야 포츠머스에 오셨는데, 당신을 어찌나 보고 싶어 하시는지 몰라요. 레드, 얘, 존슨 씨 좀 설득해 봐.\"\n\n\"어머, 정말이지.\" 레드룩 양이 꽤 발랄하게 받았다. \"네가 못 설득하면야—\" 레드룩 양은 더는 말을 잇지 않았지만, 능청스러운 장난기로 넌지시 알려 주었다. 스네벨리치 양이 못 설득하면 그 누구도 못 한다고 말이야.\n\n\"릴리빅 씨 부부가 저희 집에 방을 얻으셨는데, 당분간 저희 거실을 같이 쓰고 계세요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했다. \"그럼 마음이 좀 동하시지 않아요?\"\n\n\"그야,\" 니콜라스가 대답했다. \"당신 초대 말고 다른 무슨 끌릴 거리가 더 필요하겠어요.\"\n\n\"어머, 아니에요! 그러시겠죠.\" 스네벨리치 양이 받아쳤다. 그러자 레드룩 양이 \"어머, 세상에!\" 하고 말했다. 그 말에 스네벨리치 양이 레드룩 양더러 경박한 애라고 했고, 레드룩 양은 스네벨리치 양더러 그렇게 얼굴까지 붉힐 거 없다고 했다. 그러고는 스네벨리치 양이 레드룩 양을 찰싹 때리고, 레드룩 양도 스네벨리치 양을 찰싹 때렸다.\n\n\"가요.\" 레드룩 양이 말했다. \"이제 슬슬 가야 할 시간이에요. 안 그러면 가엾은 스네벨리치 부인이 당신이 따님을 데리고 도망친 줄 아실 거예요, 존슨 씨. 그럼 한바탕 난리가 나겠죠.\"\n\n\"얘, 레드.\" 스네벨리치 양이 나무랐다. \"넌 말을 어쩜 그렇게 하니!\"\n\n레드룩 양은 대꾸하지 않고 스마이크의 팔짱을 끼더니, 친구와 니콜라스가 알아서 따라오게 내버려 두었다. 두 사람은 — 아니 정확히는 니콜라스가 — 그렇게 하는 게 좋았다. 그런 상황에서 단둘이 마주 걷는 건 그리 내키지 않았거든. 그래서 곧장 따라나섰다.\n\n거리에 나서자 이야깃거리가 없지 않았다. 알고 보니 스네벨리치 양은 집까지 들고 갈 작은 바구니가 있었고, 레드룩 양은 작은 모자 상자가 있었거든. 둘 다 안에는 여배우들이 으레 저녁마다 들고 다니는 자잘한 무대 의상 따위가 들어 있었다. 니콜라스가 바구니를 들겠다고 우기고, 스네벨리치 양은 자기가 들겠다고 우겼다. 그래서 옥신각신 실랑이가 벌어졌고, 결국 니콜라스가 바구니를 빼앗고 모자 상자까지 차지했다. 그러고는 니콜라스가 바구니 안에 대체 뭐가 들었을까 궁금하다며 들여다보려고 했다. 그러자 스네벨리치 양이 비명을 지르며, 만약 그가 본 것 같으면 자기는 정말 기절해 버릴 거라고 외쳤다. 이 선언에 이어 모자 상자에도 똑같은 시도가 이어졌고, 레드룩 양 쪽에서도 똑같은 호들갑이 벌어졌다. 그러고는 두 여자가 다 같이, 니콜라스가 다시는 들여다보려 하지 않겠다고 약속할 때까지 한 발짝도 더 안 가겠다고 맹세했다. 마침내 니콜라스가 더는 호기심을 부리지 않겠다고 약속하자, 세 사람은 다시 걸었다. 두 여자는 연신 키득거리며, 살다 살다 이렇게 못된 사람은 처음 본다고, 정말이지 처음이라고 떠들었다.\n\n이런 농담으로 길을 가볍게 하는 사이, 그들은 어느새 재단사 집에 다다랐다. 그리고 여기서 제법 자그마한 모임이 꾸려졌다. 릴리빅 씨와 릴리빅 부인 말고도 스네벨리치 양의 엄마뿐 아니라 아빠까지 와 있었거든. 그런데 스네벨리치 양의 아빠는 유난히 잘생긴 남자였다. 매부리코에 흰 이마, 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리, 우뚝한 광대뼈까지, 전체적으로 꽤 잘생긴 얼굴이었지. 다만 술 탓인지 여드름이 좀 돋아 있었다. 스네벨리치 양의 아빠는 가슴팍이 아주 떡 벌어졌고, 다 닳은 파란 연미복을 입고 금박 단추로 꽉 끼게 채우고 있었다. 그는 니콜라스가 방에 들어오는 걸 보자마자, 오른손 검지와 중지 두 손가락을 가운데 단추 두 개 사이에 척 끼우고, 다른 팔은 우아하게 허리에 척 얹었다. 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. '자, 내가 바로 여기 있다, 젊은이. 그래, 나한테 무슨 할 말이 있나?'\n\n스네벨리치 양의 아빠는 바로 그런 사람이었고, 그런 자세로 앉아 있었다. 그는 크리스마스 무언극에서 열 살짜리 꼬마 도깨비 역을 처음 맡은 이래로 줄곧 이 바닥에 몸담아 온 사람이었다. 노래도 조금, 춤도 조금, 검술도 조금, 연기도 조금, 뭐든 다 조금씩은 했지만 어느 것 하나 대단치는 않았다. 런던의 온갖 극장에서 발레단에도 들었다 합창단에도 들었다 했고, 늘 그 체격 덕에 군인 손님이나 대사 없는 귀족 역으로 뽑혔다. 언제나 말쑥한 옷을 차려입고, 짧은 치마를 입은 말쑥한 여자와 팔짱을 끼고 등장했지. 그것도 어찌나 폼 나게 해냈던지, 일등석 관객들이 그를 무슨 대단한 사람인 줄 알고 \"브라보!\"를 몇 번이나 외친 일도 있었다. 스네벨리치 양의 아빠가 바로 그런 사람이었다. 시샘하는 사람들은 그가 가끔 스네벨리치 양의 엄마를 때린다는 험담을 씌우기도 했지. 엄마는 아직도 무용수였는데, 몸매가 곱고 미모도 좀 남아 있었지만, 무대 조명을 정면으로 받기엔 나이가 좀 든 편이라 지금은 춤을 출 때처럼 뒤편에 물러앉아 있었다.\n\n이 좋은 사람들에게 니콜라스가 아주 격식을 갖춰 소개되었다. 소개가 다 끝나자 스네벨리치 양의 아빠는 — 럼주에 물을 탄 냄새를 풍기는 사람이었지 — 이렇게나 재능이 뛰어난 신사를 알게 되어 기쁘다고 말했다. 그러고는 한마디 덧붙이기를, 이만한 성공은 — 아무렴, 자기 친구 글래보멜리 씨가 코버그 극장에 처음 섰던 이래로 없었다고 했다.\n\n\"그 사람 보셨소, 선생?\" 스네벨리치 양의 아빠가 물었다.\n\n\"아니요, 정말 본 적이 없네요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다.\n\n\"내 친구 글래보멜리를 못 보셨다고요, 선생!\" 스네벨리치 양의 아빠가 말했다. \"그럼 아직 진짜 연기를 못 보신 거요. 그 친구가 살아 있었더라면—\"\n\n\"오, 그분 돌아가셨군요?\" 니콜라스가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 스네벨리치 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 웨스트민스터 사원에 묻히진 못했지, 부끄러운 노릇이오. 그 친구는 정말—. 뭐, 됐소. 그는 어떤 나그네도 돌아오지 못하는 그 나라로 떠났소. 그곳에서는 제대로 인정받기를 바라오.\"\n\n그렇게 말하며 스네벨리치 양의 아빠는 아주 누런 비단 손수건으로 코끝을 문질렀다. 그러고는 이 추억들이 자기를 북받치게 한다는 걸 좌중에 알렸다.\n\n\"자, 릴리빅 씨.\" 니콜라스가 말했다. \"어떻게 지내십니까?\"\n\n\"아주 잘 지내오, 선생.\" 수금원이 대답했다. \"결혼한 처지만 한 게 없소, 선생, 정말이오.\"\n\n\"그렇습니까!\" 니콜라스가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"아! 그만한 게 없지, 선생.\" 릴리빅 씨가 엄숙하게 대답했다. \"어떻게 생각하시오.\" 수금원이 그를 한쪽으로 끌어당기며 속삭였다. \"오늘 밤 저 사람 어때 보이오?\"\n\n\"여전히 아름다우신데요.\" 니콜라스가 한때 페토커 양이던 부인을 흘끗 보며 대답했다.\n\n\"왜, 저 사람한테는 어떤 분위기가 있단 말이오, 선생.\" 수금원이 속삭였다. \"내가 누구한테서도 본 적 없는 그런 거 말이오. 지금 저기 주전자 올리려고 움직이는 것 좀 보시오. 저기! 정말 매혹적이지 않소, 선생?\"\n\n\"행복한 분이시네요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다.\n\n\"하, 하, 하!\" 수금원이 받아쳤다. \"아니. 그래도 그렇게 생각하시오, 응? 그럴지도, 그럴지도 모르지. 내 말은, 내가 젊은 사람이었더라도 이보다 더 잘하긴 어려웠을 거요, 안 그렇소? 당신도 이보다 더 잘하긴 어려웠을 거요, 안 그렇소—응—그렇지 않소?\" 이런 질문에다 또 이런 질문들을 잔뜩 늘어놓으며, 릴리빅 씨는 니콜라스의 옆구리를 팔꿈치로 쿡쿡 찔러댔다. 그러고는 흐뭇함을 억누르려 애쓰다 얼굴이 아주 시뻘게질 때까지 낄낄거렸다.\n\n이쯤 되자 모든 부인들의 합동 감독 아래 식탁보가 깔렸다. 식탁 두 개를 붙여 놓았는데, 하나는 높고 좁았고 다른 하나는 낮고 넓었다. 위쪽엔 굴, 아래쪽엔 소시지, 가운데엔 촛불 심지 가위 한 벌, 그리고 구운 감자는 놓기 편한 데마다 놓여 있었다. 침실에서 의자 두 개를 더 가져왔다. 스네벨리치 양은 식탁 상석에, 릴리빅 씨는 말석에 앉았다. 그리고 니콜라스는 스네벨리치 양 옆에 앉는 영광뿐 아니라, 오른편에 스네벨리치 양의 엄마를, 맞은편에 스네벨리치 양의 아빠를 두는 영광까지 누렸다. 한마디로 그는 이 잔치의 주인공이었다. 식탁이 치워지고 뭔가 따뜻한 게 나오자, 스네벨리치 양의 아빠가 일어나 그의 건강을 비는 축사를 했다. 그런데 그 안에 그가 곧 떠난다는 대목이 어찌나 가슴 아프게 들어 있었던지, 스네벨리치 양이 그만 울음을 터뜨리며 침실로 물러날 수밖에 없었다.\n\n\"쉿! 신경 쓰지 마세요.\" 레드룩 양이 침실에서 빼꼼 내다보며 말했다. \"쟤가 돌아오면, 너무 무리해서 그런 거라고 말해 주세요.\"\n\n레드룩 양은 다시 문을 닫기 전에 이 말에다 알 수 없는 끄덕임과 찡그림을 어찌나 잔뜩 덧붙였던지, 좌중에 깊은 침묵이 내려앉았다. 그동안 스네벨리치 양의 아빠는 정말이지 잔뜩 거들먹거리는 표정으로 — 실물보다 몇 배나 커진 듯한 얼굴로 — 모두를 차례로, 특히 니콜라스를 노려보았다. 그러면서 잔을 연신 비우고 또 채우고를 거듭했다. 그러다 부인들이 스네벨리치 양을 데리고 우르르 돌아왔다.\n\n\"조금도 놀라실 거 없어요, 스네벨리치 씨.\" 릴리빅 부인이 말했다. \"쟤가 그냥 좀 약하고 신경이 예민할 뿐이에요. 아침부터 내내 그랬거든요.\"\n\n\"오.\" 스네벨리치 씨가 말했다. \"그게 다요?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요, 그게 다예요. 호들갑 떨지 마세요.\" 부인들이 다 같이 외쳤다.\n\n그런데 이건 한 남자이자 아버지로서 스네벨리치 씨의 위신에 딱 어울리는 대답은 아니었다. 그래서 그는 불운한 스네벨리치 부인을 콕 집어, 대체 무슨 심보로 자기한테 그딴 식으로 말하느냐고 따졌다.\n\n\"아유, 여보.\" 스네벨리치 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"날 여보라고 부르지 마시오, 부인.\" 스네벨리치 씨가 말했다. \"제발.\"\n\n\"제발, 아빠, 그러지 마세요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"뭘 그러지 말라는 거냐, 얘야?\"\n\n\"그런 식으로 말하지 마시라고요.\"\n\n\"왜 안 되는데?\" 스네벨리치 씨가 말했다. \"설마 내가 내 맘대로 말하는 걸 막을 사람이 여기 있다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"아무도 막으려는 게 아니에요, 아빠.\" 딸이 받아쳤다.\n\n\"막으려고 해 봤자 못 막지.\" 스네벨리치 씨가 말했다. \"나는 나 자신이 부끄럽지 않아. 스네벨리치가 내 이름이다. 내가 시내에 있을 땐 보 거리의 브로드 코트에 가면 날 찾을 수 있어. 집에 없거든, 누구든 무대 출입문에서 날 찾으라고 해. 제기랄, 무대 출입문 사람들은 날 다 알 테니까. 길모퉁이 담배 가게에 가면 내 초상화를 본 사람도 수두룩하지. 전에 신문에 내 이름이 실린 적도 있어, 안 그래? 말이라! 내 말해 두지. 만약 어떤 놈이 내 딸 마음을 가지고 함부로 놀았다는 걸 알게 되면, 난 말 따위 안 해. 말 한마디 없이 그놈을 깜짝 놀라게 해 줄 거야. 그게 내 방식이거든.\"\n\n그렇게 말하며 스네벨리치 씨는 주먹을 꽉 쥐고 왼손 손바닥을 세 번 야무지게 내리쳤다. 그러고는 오른손 엄지와 검지로 허공의 코를 잡아 비틀고는, 또 한 잔을 단숨에 들이켰다. \"그게 내 방식이지.\" 스네벨리치 씨가 거듭 말했다.\n\n이름난 사람들은 대부분 약점이 있는 법이다. 그리고 사실인즉 스네벨리치 씨는 술을 좀 좋아하는 편이었다. 아니, 모든 사실을 다 털어놓자면 거의 맨정신인 적이 없었다고 해야겠지. 그는 취하면 뚜렷한 세 단계를 거쳤다. 점잖은 단계, 시비조 단계, 그리고 다정한 단계 말이다. 일로 무대에 설 때는 점잖은 단계를 절대 넘지 않았다. 하지만 사적인 자리에서는 세 단계를 다 거쳤는데, 어찌나 빨리 한 단계에서 다음 단계로 넘어가는지, 그를 잘 모르는 사람들은 종종 적잖이 어리둥절해했다.\n\n그래서 스네벨리치 씨는 또 한 잔을 들이켜자마자, 방금 시비조 기색을 보였던 걸 까맣게 잊은 채 행복하게 좌중 모두를 향해 미소 지었다. 그러고는 더없이 활기찬 태도로 건배를 제안했다. \"숙녀분들을 위하여! 그분들께 축복을!\"\n\n\"난 그분들을 사랑해.\" 스네벨리치 씨가 식탁을 둘러보며 말했다. \"사랑하고말고, 한 사람 한 사람 다.\"\n\n\"한 사람 한 사람 다는 아니겠죠.\" 릴리빅 씨가 부드럽게 따졌다.\n\n\"아니, 한 사람 한 사람 다요.\" 스네벨리치 씨가 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 결혼한 부인들까지 포함되는데 말이오.\" 릴리빅 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그분들도 사랑해요, 선생.\" 스네벨리치 씨가 말했다.\n\n수금원은 자못 놀란 표정으로 둘러앉은 얼굴들을 살펴보았다. 마치 '이거 참 대단한 사람이군!' 하고 말하는 듯했다. 그리고 릴리빅 부인의 태도에서 어떤 공포나 분노의 기색도 보이지 않자 좀 놀란 눈치였다.\n\n\"가는 정이 있으면 오는 정이 있는 법이지.\" 스네벨리치 씨가 말했다. \"내가 그분들을 사랑하고, 그분들도 날 사랑하거든.\" 그러고는 이 고백이 모든 도덕적 의무를 무시하고 거스르는 걸로도 모자라다는 듯, 스네벨리치 씨가 무슨 짓을 했게? 그가 윙크를 했다 — 대놓고 거리낌 없이 윙크를 했다. 오른쪽 눈으로 — 헨리에타 릴리빅을 향해서 말이다!\n\n수금원은 너무 놀란 나머지 의자에 털썩 기대고 말았다. 그 여자가 헨리에타 페토커였을 때 누가 그렇게 윙크를 했더라도 더없이 점잖지 못한 짓이었을 거다. 그런데 릴리빅 부인한테라니! 그가 식은땀을 흘리며 그 생각을 하면서, 혹시 내가 꿈을 꾸고 있는 게 아닐까 의아해하는 사이, 스네벨리치 씨가 다시 윙크를 했다. 그러고는 무언극처럼 릴리빅 부인을 향해 건배하더니, 진짜로 그녀에게 입맞춤을 날렸다! 릴리빅 씨가 의자에서 일어나 식탁 반대편 끝으로 곧장 걸어가더니, 그에게 덮쳐들었다 — 그야말로 덮쳐든 거다 — 순식간에 말이다. 릴리빅 씨는 결코 가벼운 몸집이 아니었다. 그래서 그가 스네벨리치 씨에게 덮쳐들자, 스네벨리치 씨가 식탁 밑으로 쓰러졌다. 릴리빅 씨도 그를 따라 들어갔고, 부인들이 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"이 양반들 왜 이러는 거예요! 미쳤어요?\" 니콜라스가 식탁 밑으로 뛰어들며 외쳤다. 그러고는 수금원을 온 힘을 다해 끌어내, 마치 박제 인형이라도 다루듯 잔뜩 웅크린 채로 의자에 밀어 넣었다. \"어쩌실 작정이세요? 뭘 하시려는 거예요? 도대체 왜 이러십니까?\"\n\n니콜라스가 수금원을 일으키는 사이, 스마이크가 스네벨리치 씨한테 똑같이 해 주었다. 스네벨리치 씨는 술 취한 얼굴로 방금 전 적수를 멍하니 바라보았다.\n\n\"여기 좀 보시오, 선생.\" 릴리빅 씨가 어리둥절해하는 아내를 가리키며 대답했다. \"여기 순결과 우아함을 두루 갖춘 사람이 있는데, 그 감정이 모욕당했소—짓밟혔단 말이오, 선생!\"\n\n\"세상에, 무슨 헛소리예요!\" 릴리빅 부인이 니콜라스의 묻는 눈빛에 대꾸하며 외쳤다. \"아무도 나한테 뭐라고 한 적 없어요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고 했냐고, 헨리에타!\" 수금원이 외쳤다. \"내가 저자가—\" 릴리빅 씨는 차마 그 말을 입 밖에 내지 못하고, 대신 눈을 깜빡이는 시늉을 흉내 냈다.\n\n\"흥!\" 릴리빅 부인이 외쳤다. \"그럼 아무도 날 쳐다보면 안 된다는 거예요? 그게 법이라면 결혼이란 참 좋은 거겠네요!\"\n\n\"당신은 괜찮았소?\" 수금원이 외쳤다.\n\n\"괜찮냐고요!\" 릴리빅 부인이 경멸하듯 되받았다. \"당신이야말로 무릎 꿇고 모두한테 용서를 빌어야 해요, 그래야 한다고요.\"\n\n\"용서를, 여보?\" 당황한 수금원이 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 나한테 먼저요.\" 릴리빅 부인이 대답했다. \"내가 뭐가 옳고 뭐가 그른지 제일 잘 판단할 줄 모른다고 생각해요?\"\n\n\"그럼요.\" 부인들이 다 같이 외쳤다. \"뭔가 짚고 넘어가야 할 일이 있었다면 저희가 제일 먼저 말했을 거 아니에요?\"\n\n\"저 사람들이 모를 줄 아시오, 선생?\" 스네벨리치 양의 아빠가 옷깃을 추켜올리며 말했다. 그러고는 머리통을 한 대 갈겨 주겠다는 둥, 나이를 생각해서 참는 거라는 둥 뭐라고 중얼거렸다. 그러면서 스네벨리치 양의 아빠는 몇 초 동안 릴리빅 씨를 똑바로 매섭게 노려보더니, 천천히 의자에서 일어나 부인들에게 빙 돌아가며 입을 맞췄다. 릴리빅 부인부터 시작해서 말이다.\n\n불행한 수금원은 가엾은 눈빛으로 아내를 바라보았다. 릴리빅 부인 속에 페토커 양의 모습이 한 가닥이라도 남아 있는지 살피려는 듯이 말이다. 그러나 그런 게 전혀 남아 있지 않다는 걸 너무도 확실히 깨닫고는, 아주 겸손하게 좌중 모두에게 용서를 빌었다. 그러고는 어찌나 기가 죽고 풀이 죽고 환상이 깨진 사람처럼 주저앉던지, 그 온갖 이기심과 노망에도 불구하고 정말이지 가엾기 짝이 없는 신세가 되고 말았다.\n\n스네벨리치 양의 아빠는 이 승리에, 그리고 이성에게 인기 있다는 반박할 수 없는 증거에 한껏 우쭐해져서, 금세 흥겨워졌다 — 아니, 떠들썩해졌다고 해야겠다. 자청해서 결코 짧지 않은 노래를 한 곡 이상 불렀다. 그리고 그 틈틈이 자기한테 연정을 품었던 걸로 짐작되는 갖가지 눈부신 여인들의 추억으로 좌중을 즐겁게 했다. 그중 몇몇은 이름까지 대며 건배했고, 그러는 김에 한마디 했다. 자기가 제 잇속을 좀 더 챙겼더라면 지금쯤 사두마차를 타고 굴러다니고 있었을 거라고 말이다. 이런 회상들도 스네벨리치 부인의 가슴엔 그리 쓰라린 아픔을 일으키지 못하는 듯했다. 부인은 니콜라스에게 제 딸의 갖가지 재주와 장점을 늘어놓느라 충분히 바빴거든. 젊은 아가씨 본인도 자기가 가진 최고의 매력을 펼쳐 보이는 데 전혀 뒤지지 않았다. 하지만 이런 매력들은, 레드룩 양의 갖은 꾀로 한껏 부추겨졌는데도, 니콜라스의 관심을 끄는 데는 조금도 효과가 없었다. 니콜라스는 스퀴어스 양의 일이 아직 생생하게 기억에 남아 있어서, 어떤 유혹에도 한결같이 버텼다. 그리고 자기 행동을 어찌나 엄격하게 단속했던지, 그가 작별 인사를 하고 떠나자 부인들은 입을 모아 그를 무감각의 화신 같은 사람이라고 평했다.\n\n이튿날 포스터가 차례대로 나붙었고, 대중은 무지개 온갖 빛깔로, 그리고 가능한 온갖 모양으로 척추가 굽은 듯 비뚤배뚤한 글자로 이런 소식을 접했다. 존슨 씨가 오늘 저녁 영광스럽게도 마지막 무대에 선다는 것, 그리고 그의 공연마다 따라붙는 엄청난 인파 때문에 일찌감치 자리를 예약하시기를 부탁드린다는 것 말이다. 연극사에서 주목할 만한 사실이자 오래전부터 이론의 여지 없이 확립된 사실이 하나 있는데, 사람들을 극장으로 끌어들이려면 먼저 그들이 도저히 자리를 못 구할 거라고 믿게 만들지 않고서는 가망이 없다는 거다.\n\n니콜라스는 밤에 극장에 들어서면서, 극단 사람들 모두의 얼굴에 보이는 평소답지 않은 동요와 흥분을 무엇 때문인지 도통 알 수가 없었다. 하지만 그 까닭을 오래 의아해하진 않았다. 그가 그 일에 대해 뭐라 물어보기도 전에 크럼블스 씨가 다가와, 흥분한 목소리로 박스석에 런던 극장 단장이 와 있다고 알려 줬거든.\n\n\"신동 때문이오, 틀림없어요, 선생.\" 크럼블스 씨가 니콜라스를 막에 난 작은 구멍으로 끌고 가서, 그리로 런던 단장을 엿볼 수 있게 해 주며 말했다. \"신동의 명성 때문인 게 조금도 의심할 여지가 없소—저 사람이오. 저기 외투 입고 셔츠 깃 없는 사람 말이오. 저 애한테 주급 십 파운드를 줘야 할 거요, 존슨. 한 푼이라도 덜 받고는 런던 무대에 안 세울 거요. 크럼블스 부인도 같이 데려가지 않으면 저 애도 못 데려가게 할 거고—둘이서 주급 이십 파운드. 아니면 이렇게 합시다, 나하고 두 아들까지 넣어서 온 가족을 삼십 파운드에 주겠소. 이보다 더 공정하게는 못 하지. 우리 중 누구도 다른 식구들 없이는 안 가니까, 데려가려면 우릴 다 데려가야 하오. 런던 사람들 중에 이렇게 하는 이들이 있는데, 늘 통하거든. 주급 삼십 파운드—너무 싸지, 존슨. 거저나 다름없소.\"\n\n니콜라스는 정말 그렇다고 대답했다. 그러자 빈센트 크럼블스 씨는 마음을 가라앉히려고 코담배를 큼지막하게 몇 번 들이마시더니, 부랴부랴 크럼블스 부인한테 가서 알렸다. 받아들일 수 있는 유일한 조건을 완전히 정해 놓았고, 한 푼도 깎지 않기로 결심했다고 말이다.\n\n다들 분장을 마치고 막이 오르자, 런던 단장이 와 있다는 데서 비롯된 흥분이 천 배로 커졌다. 다들 어쩐 일인지 런던 단장이 다름 아닌 바로 자기 연기를 보러 일부러 내려온 거라고 여겼고, 모두가 불안과 기대로 안절부절못했다. 첫 장면에 안 나오는 몇몇은 무대 옆으로 달려가 목을 길게 빼고 그를 엿보았다. 또 어떤 이들은 무대 출입문 위쪽에 있는 자그마한 개인 박스석 두 개로 살그머니 올라가, 거기서 런던 단장을 정탐했다. 한번은 런던 단장이 미소 짓는 게 보였다—크럼블스 부인이 한창 최고의 연기를 펼치고 있을 때, 익살꾼 시골뜨기가 쉬파리를 잡는 시늉을 하는 걸 보고 웃은 거였다. \"잘한다, 이 친구야.\" 크럼블스 씨가 그 익살꾼 시골뜨기가 무대에서 내려오자 주먹을 흔들며 말했다. \"자넨 다음 토요일 밤에 이 극단을 떠나게.\"\n\n마찬가지로, 무대에 선 사람은 누구나 단 한 사람 말고는 관객이 보이지 않았다. 다들 런던 단장을 향해 연기했다. 렌빌 씨가 갑자기 격분해서 황제더러 악당이라고 외치고는, 장갑을 깨물며 \"하지만 나는 속내를 감춰야 해\" 하고 말할 때, 이런 경우에 마땅히 그래야 하듯 침울하게 무대 바닥을 바라보며 자기 차례를 기다리는 대신, 그는 런던 단장한테 눈을 딱 박고 있었다. 브라바사 양이 연인을 향해 노래를 부를 때, 그 연인은 관례대로 절과 절 사이에 그녀와 악수하려고 대기하고 있었는데, 두 사람은 서로를 보는 게 아니라 런던 단장을 보고 있었다. 크럼블스 씨는 그를 정면으로 바라보며 죽었다. 그리고 위병 둘이 아주 처절한 죽음 끝에 시신을 옮기러 들어왔을 때, 그 시신이 눈을 뜨고 런던 단장을 흘끔거리는 게 보였다. 마침내 런던 단장이 잠들어 있는 게 발각되었다. 그러고는 얼마 안 가 그가 깨어나 가 버렸다. 그러자 극단 사람들이 다 그 불운한 익살꾼 시골뜨기한테 달려들어, 그의 어릿광대짓이 유일한 원인이라고 우겨댔다. 크럼블스 씨는 자기가 오랫동안 참아 왔지만 정말 더는 못 견디겠다며, 그러니 다른 일자리나 알아봐 주면 고맙겠다고 했다.\n\n이 모든 게 니콜라스한테는 큰 웃음거리였다. 이 일에 대한 그의 유일한 감정은, 그 대단한 양반이 자기가 등장하기 전에 가 버렸다는 데서 오는 진심 어린 만족뿐이었거든. 그는 마지막 두 작품에서 제 역을 최대한 활기차게 해냈다. 그러고는 한없는 호평과 전례 없는 박수를 받고서—한두 시간 전에 이미 찍어 놓은 다음 날 포스터에 그렇게 적혀 있었거든—스마이크의 팔을 잡고 집으로 걸어가 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n이튿날 아침 우편으로 뉴먼 노그스한테서 편지 한 통이 왔다. 잉크 자국투성이에, 아주 짧고, 아주 더럽고, 아주 작고, 아주 알쏭달쏭한 편지였다. 니콜라스더러 당장 런던으로 돌아오라고, 한순간도 지체하지 말라고, 되도록이면 그날 밤에 와 달라고 다그치는 내용이었다.\n\n\"가야지.\" 니콜라스가 말했다. \"하늘은 아시겠지, 내가 잘되라고, 내 뜻과는 정말 어긋나게 여기 남아 있었다는 걸. 하지만 지금도 너무 오래 꾸물거렸는지 몰라. 무슨 일이 생긴 걸까? 스마이크, 이봐—내 지갑 받아. 우리 짐 챙기고, 우리가 진 자잘한 빚 좀 갚아—얼른, 그래야 아침 마차를 탈 수 있어. 난 그저 우리가 떠난다고 사람들한테 말하고, 곧장 너한테 돌아올게.\"\n\n그렇게 말하며 그는 모자를 집어 들고, 크럼블스 씨의 숙소로 부랴부랴 달려가, 어찌나 우렁차게 노크를 했던지, 아직 침대에 누워 있던 그 양반을 깨우고 말았다. 그리고 도선사 벌프 씨가 어찌나 놀랐던지 아침 담뱃대를 입에서 거의 떨어뜨릴 뻔했다.\n\n문이 열리자, 니콜라스는 격식이고 뭐고 없이 위층으로 뛰어 올라가, 이층 앞쪽의 어두컴컴한 거실로 불쑥 들어갔다. 그랬더니 두 크럼블스 도련님이 소파 겸 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 잽싸게 옷을 입고 있는 게 아닌가. 한밤중인데 옆집에 불이 난 줄로 안 거였다.\n\n니콜라스가 미처 오해를 풀어 주기도 전에, 크럼블스 씨가 플란넬 가운에 잠자리 모자 차림으로 내려왔다. 니콜라스는 그에게 사정이 생겨서 당장 런던으로 가야 한다고 간단히 설명했다.\n\n\"그럼 안녕히 계세요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다. \"안녕히, 안녕히 계세요.\"\n\n크럼블스 씨가 놀란 마음을 가라앉히고 포스터에 대해 뭐라 겨우 헐떡이며 말을 꺼낼 무렵, 니콜라스는 벌써 계단을 반쯤 내려가 있었다.\n\n\"어쩔 수 없죠.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다. \"이번 주에 제가 번 게 있으면 그걸로 포스터 값을 치셔도 되고, 그걸로 모자라면 얼마면 되는지 바로 말씀하세요. 얼른요, 얼른.\"\n\n\"그건 그냥 없던 걸로 합시다.\" 크럼블스 씨가 대답했다. \"하지만 마지막으로 딱 하룻밤만 더 못 하겠나?\"\n\n\"한 시간도—일 분도 안 돼요.\" 니콜라스가 안타까운 듯 대답했다.\n\n\"크럼블스 부인한테 한마디 하고 가지 않겠나?\" 단장이 그를 따라 문까지 내려오며 물었다.\n\n\"제 목숨이 스무 해는 늘어난다 해도 멈출 수 없어요.\" 니콜라스가 받아쳤다. \"자, 제 손을 잡으세요. 그리고 이 손과 함께 제 진심 어린 감사도요.—오! 내가 여기서 빈둥거리고 있었다니!\"\n\n이 말과 함께 안타까운 듯 땅을 쿵 구르고는, 그는 자기를 붙잡으려는 단장의 손에서 몸을 뿌리쳤다. 그러고는 거리를 쏜살같이 내달려 순식간에 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n\"이런, 이런.\" 크럼블스 씨가 그가 방금 사라진 지점을 아쉬운 듯 바라보며 말했다. \"저 친구가 무대에서도 저렇게만 연기했으면 돈을 얼마나 끌어모았을까! 이 순회 공연단에 계속 붙어 있었어야 했는데. 나한테 아주 쓸모가 많았을 텐데 말이야. 그런데 저 친구는 제 앞길에 뭐가 좋은지를 몰라. 성마른 젊은이지. 젊은 것들은 경솔하다니까, 아주 경솔해.\"\n\n크럼블스 씨는 이렇게 교훈을 늘어놓을 기분이었던지라, 무심코 손을 조끼 주머니 쪽으로 가져가지 않았더라면 몇 분은 더 교훈을 읊었을지도 모른다. 평소 그가 코담배를 넣어 두던 자리 말이다. 그런데 늘 있던 자리에 주머니가 아예 없자, 문득 자기가 조끼를 안 입고 있다는 사실이 떠올랐다. 그러자 자기 옷차림이 너무 부실하다는 데까지 생각이 미쳐, 그는 문을 탁 닫고는 부랴부랴 위층으로 물러갔다.\n\n스마이크는 니콜라스가 없는 사이에 일을 부지런히 해 둬서, 그의 도움으로 떠날 채비가 금세 다 갖춰졌다. 두 사람은 아침을 한 입 먹을 새도 거의 없이, 반 시간이 채 안 되어 마차 사무소에 다다랐다. 제때 도착하려고 어찌나 서둘렀던지 둘 다 숨이 턱에 차 있었다. 아직 몇 분 여유가 있어서, 니콜라스는 자리를 잡아 두고는 바로 옆 헌옷 가게로 부랴부랴 들어가 스마이크한테 외투를 하나 사 줬다. 덩치 좋은 자영농한테도 좀 클 만한 옷이었다. 하지만 점원이 (제법 진심을 담아) 이만한 맞춤은 드물다고 우겨대니, 니콜라스는 마음이 급한 나머지 그게 두 배 크기였더라도 샀을 거다.\n\n이제 큰길에 나와 출발 준비를 다 마친 마차로 두 사람이 부랴부랴 다가가는데, 니콜라스는 적잖이 놀라고 말았다. 갑자기 어떤 사람이 꽉 끌어안는 바람에 거의 다리가 휘청할 뻔했거든. 그리고 그 놀라움은 크럼블스 씨가 외치는 목소리에 조금도 누그러지지 않았다. \"이 사람이야—내 친구, 내 친구라고!\"\n\n\"세상에.\" 니콜라스가 단장의 품에서 버둥거리며 외쳤다. \"지금 뭐 하시는 거예요?\"\n\n단장은 아무 대꾸도 없이 그를 다시 가슴에 꽉 끌어안고는, 그러면서 외쳤다. \"잘 가게, 내 고귀하고 사자처럼 용맹한 젊은이여!\"\n\n사실 크럼블스 씨는 직업적인 과시의 기회라면 결코 놓치는 법이 없는 사람이라, 일부러 니콜라스와 공개적인 작별을 하려고 나온 거였다. 그리고 그걸 더 그럴듯하게 만들려고, 이제 그 젊은이가 더없이 질색하는데도, 무대식 포옹을 잇달아 퍼붓고 있었다. 다들 알다시피 무대식 포옹이란 안는 사람이 제 턱을 사랑하는 상대의 어깨에 얹고 그 너머를 바라보는 식이다. 크럼블스 씨는 이걸 최고로 멜로드라마틱한 양식으로 해냈고, 그러면서 동시에 상비 작품들에서 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 구슬픈 작별의 말이란 말은 죄다 쏟아 냈다. 이게 다가 아니었다. 큰 크럼블스 도련님이 스마이크한테 똑같은 의식을 치르고 있었고, 한편 작은 퍼시 크럼블스 도련님은 아주 낡은 헌 낙타 모직 망토를 무대처럼 왼쪽 어깨에 걸치고는, 두 희생자를 처형대로 데려가려고 대기하는 부관 같은 자세로 그 옆에 서 있었거든.\n\n구경꾼들이 한바탕 신나게 웃어댔다. 그러니 좋게좋게 넘기는 편이 낫겠다 싶어, 니콜라스도 간신히 몸을 빼내고 나서는 따라 웃었다. 그러고는 어리둥절해하는 스마이크를 구해내, 그를 뒤따라 마차 지붕으로 기어 올라갔다. 그리고 마차가 굴러가기 시작하자, 자리에 없는 크럼블스 부인을 기리며 손에 입을 맞춰 보였다.\n\n# CHAPTER 31\n\n랄프 니클비는 까맣게 모르고 있었어. 자기 조카가 좋은 말 네 마리가 낼 수 있는 최고 속도로 자신의 활동 무대를 향해 달려오고 있다는 걸, 그리고 시간이 한 순간씩 지날 때마다 둘 사이의 거리가 점점 줄어들고 있다는 걸 말이야. 그날 아침 랄프는 늘 하던 일에 매달려 앉아 있었어. 그런데도 자꾸만 생각이 어제 조카딸과 나눈 그 만남으로 되돌아가는 걸 막을 수가 없었지. 그렇게 잠깐씩 멍하니 있다가 정신을 차리면, 랄프는 짜증스러운 소리를 한마디 중얼거리고는 다시 마음을 다잡아 앞에 놓인 장부에 매달렸어. 하지만 아무리 애를 써도 그 똑같은 생각의 줄기가 자꾸자꾸 되돌아왔지. 그러면 계산이 헝클어지고, 굽어보고 있던 숫자에서 정신이 온통 흩어졌어. 마침내 랄프는 펜을 내려놓고 의자에 등을 기대며 몸을 뒤로 젖혔어. 끈질기게 밀려드는 그 생각의 물줄기를 그냥 흐르게 내버려 두기로, 마음껏 흐르게 풀어 줘서 아예 깨끗이 떨쳐 버리기로 작정한 것 같았지.\n\n\"나는 예쁜 얼굴 하나에 마음이 흔들릴 사람이 아니야.\" 랄프가 매섭게 중얼거렸어. \"그 얼굴 밑에는 이를 드러내고 웃는 해골이 있지. 나처럼 겉이 아니라 속을 들여다보고 일하는 사람은 그걸 봐. 곱고 얇은 껍데기가 아니라 말이야. 그래도 그 애가 거의 마음에 들긴 해. 아니, 그렇게 도도하고 까탈스럽게 자라지만 않았더라면 정말 마음에 들었을 거야. 그 녀석이 물에 빠지거나 목이라도 매달려 죽고, 어미마저 죽어 버린다면, 이 집이 그 애 보금자리가 될 텐데. 진심으로 그랬으면 좋겠어, 온 마음을 다해서.\"\n\n랄프가 니콜라스에게 품은 그 지독한 증오에도 불구하고, 가엾은 니클비 부인을 비웃으며 쏟아 내던 그 모진 멸시에도 불구하고 — 그리고 케이트 자신에게 이제껏 보여 온, 또 지금도 보이고 있고, 제 잇속이 부추기면 앞으로도 또 보일 그 비열한 행동에도 불구하고 — 이상하게 들릴지 몰라도, 그 순간만큼은 그의 생각 속에 뭔가 사람다운, 심지어 부드러운 구석이 있었어. 그는 케이트가 곁에 있다면 자기 집이 어떤 모습일지 떠올려 봤어. 비어 있는 의자에 그 애를 앉혀 놓고, 그 애를 바라보고, 그 애가 말하는 걸 들었지. 떨리던 그 작은 손이 자기 팔을 부드럽게 누르던 느낌을 다시 느꼈어. 값비싼 방마다 여자가 머물며 일하는 자취가 묻은 자잘한 흔적들을 말없이 흩뿌려 놓았지. 그러다 다시 차가운 난롯가로, 적막하고 쓸쓸한 그 호화로움으로 돌아왔어. 비록 그게 이기적인 생각에서 태어난 것이긴 했지만, 더 나은 본성이 언뜻 비친 그 한순간에, 이 부자는 자기가 친구도 없고 자식도 없이 혼자라는 걸 느꼈어. 그 순간만큼은 황금이 그의 눈에서 빛을 잃었지. 황금으로는 결코 살 수 없는 마음의 보물이 셀 수 없이 많았으니까.\n\n이런 사람의 머릿속에서 이런 생각들을 몰아내는 데는 아주 사소한 일 하나면 충분했어. 랄프가 마당 너머 건너편 사무실 창문 쪽을 멍하니 바라보다가, 문득 뉴먼 노그스가 자기를 골똘히 지켜보고 있다는 걸 알아챘거든. 뉴먼은 빨간 코가 유리에 거의 닿을 듯이 붙은 채, 녹슨 칼 조각으로 펜을 다듬는 척하고 있었어. 그런데 실은 더없이 빈틈없고 열심인 눈빛으로 자기 주인을 빤히 쳐다보고 있었지.\n\n랄프는 멍하니 있던 자세를 평소의 일하는 자세로 바꿨어. 뉴먼의 얼굴이 사라지고, 생각의 줄기도 달아나 버렸지. 죄다 동시에, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 말이야.\n\n몇 분 뒤, 랄프가 종을 울렸어. 뉴먼이 부름에 답해 들어오자, 랄프는 슬그머니 눈을 들어 그의 얼굴을 봤어. 마치 거기서 자기가 방금 한 생각을 들켰을까 봐 두려워하는 것처럼 말이야.\n\n하지만 뉴먼 노그스의 얼굴에는 무슨 속내라곤 눈곱만큼도 보이지 않았어. 머리에 눈이 두 개나 달려 있고, 그 두 눈을 다 활짝 뜨고 있으면서도, 아무 데도 안 보고 아무것도 안 보는 사람을 떠올릴 수 있다면 말이야, 랄프 니클비가 쳐다보는 동안 뉴먼이 바로 그런 사람으로 보였어.\n\n\"무슨 일이야?\" 랄프가 으르렁거렸어.\n\n\"아!\" 뉴먼이 갑자기 눈에 생기를 한껏 채워 넣더니, 그 눈을 주인에게 떨어뜨리며 말했어. \"종을 울리신 줄 알았어요.\" 이 짤막한 말만 던지고는 뉴먼이 몸을 돌려 절뚝거리며 가 버렸어.\n\n\"멈춰!\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n뉴먼이 멈춰 섰어. 조금도 당황하는 기색이 없었지.\n\n\"종을 울린 게 맞아.\"\n\n\"그러신 줄 알았어요.\"\n\n\"그런데 그걸 알면서 왜 가려고 하는 거야?\"\n\n\"종을 안 울렸다고 말씀하시려고 종을 울리신 줄 알았죠.\" 뉴먼이 대꾸했어. \"곧잘 그러시잖아요.\"\n\n\"네놈이 어디서 감히 캐고, 들여다보고, 나를 빤히 쳐다봐, 이놈아?\" 랄프가 따졌어.\n\n\"쳐다보다뇨!\" 뉴먼이 외쳤어. \"당신을요! 하, 하!\" 뉴먼이 굳이 내놓은 해명이라곤 이게 다였어.\n\n\"조심해, 이 사람아.\" 랄프가 그를 똑바로 노려보며 말했어. \"여기서 술 취해 헛짓거리하는 꼴은 못 봐. 이 꾸러미 보이지?\"\n\n\"꽤 크긴 하네요.\" 뉴먼이 받아쳤어.\n\n\"이걸 시내로 가져가. 브로드 스트리트의 크로스한테 가서 거기 놓고 와 — 빨리. 알아들었어?\"\n\n뉴먼은 그렇다는 뜻으로 뚱하니 고개를 한 번 끄덕이고는, 잠깐 방을 나갔다가 모자를 들고 돌아왔어. 그 꾸러미를 — 사방 두 자쯤 되는 거였지 — 모자 통 안에 끼워 넣으려고 이리저리 애써 봤지만 다 헛수고였어. 그러자 뉴먼은 그걸 팔 밑에 끼고, 손가락 없는 장갑을 아주 정확하고 야무지게 꼈어. 그러는 내내 눈은 랄프 니클비 씨에게 딱 고정해 둔 채로 말이야. 그러고는 마치 그게 가장 비싼 최고급 새 모자라도 되는 것처럼 진짜든 시늉이든 정성을 다해 모자를 머리에 똑바로 얹고, 드디어 심부름을 떠났어.\n\n뉴먼은 맡은 일을 아주 잽싸고 빠릿빠릿하게 해치웠어. 딱 한 군데 술집에 삼십 초쯤 들른 게 다였지. 게다가 그것도 가는 길에 있었다고 할 만했어. 한쪽 문으로 들어가 다른 쪽 문으로 나왔으니까. 그런데 돌아오는 길에 집 쪽으로 스트랜드 거리까지 왔을 때, 뉴먼이 어정거리기 시작했어. 멈출지 그냥 곧장 갈지 마음을 정하지 못한 사람처럼 머뭇거리는 모습이었지. 아주 잠깐 고민하더니, 멈추고 싶은 마음이 이겼어. 그래서 마음속에 두고 있던 그곳을 향해 발걸음을 옮긴 뉴먼은, 라 크리비 양의 집 문을 점잖게 똑똑 두 번 두드렸어. 아니, 사실은 떨리는 손으로 딱 한 번 두드린 거였지.\n\n문을 연 건 낯선 하녀였어. 이 손님의 괴상한 행색이 하녀에게 그리 좋은 인상을 주지 못한 모양이었지. 하녀는 그를 보자마자 문을 거의 닫아 버리고는, 그 좁은 틈에 몸을 끼워 넣고 뭘 원하느냐고 물었거든. 그런데 뉴먼은 그저 \"노그스\"라는 한 마디만 내뱉었어. 마치 그게 무슨 주문이라도 되는 것처럼, 그 소리만 들으면 빗장이 절로 풀리고 문이 열리기라도 할 것처럼 말이야. 그러고는 휙 밀고 지나가서, 깜짝 놀란 하녀가 막아 볼 새도 없이 라 크리비 양의 거실 문 앞까지 가 버렸어.\n\n\"들어오세요.\" 뉴먼이 손마디로 두드리는 소리에 라 크리비 양이 대답했어. 그래서 그가 안으로 들어갔지.\n\n\"어머나!\" 뉴먼이 불쑥 뛰어들자 라 크리비 양이 화들짝 놀라며 외쳤어. \"무슨 일이세요, 선생님?\"\n\n\"저를 잊으셨군요.\" 뉴먼이 고개를 까딱하며 말했어. \"그럴 만도 하죠. 옛날에 저를 알던 사람들이 저를 기억 못 하는 거야 당연한 일이지만요. 그런데 요즘의 저를 한 번이라도 본 사람치고 저를 잊는 사람은 드물거든요.\" 그는 이렇게 말하며 자기의 허름한 옷차림과 마비된 다리를 흘끗 보고는, 고개를 살짝 저었어.\n\n\"정말이지 제가 당신을 잊었네요.\" 라 크리비 양이 뉴먼을 맞으려고 일어서며 말했어. 뉴먼이 그쪽으로 마주 다가왔지. \"제 자신이 부끄럽네요. 당신은 친절하고 마음씨 고운 분인데 말이에요, 노그스 씨. 앉으세요, 그리고 니클비 양 이야기를 다 들려주세요. 가엾은 것! 그 애를 못 본 지가 벌써 몇 주째예요.\"\n\n\"어쩌다 그렇게 됐죠?\" 뉴먼이 물었어.\n\n\"실은 말이죠, 노그스 씨.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"제가 어딜 좀 다녀왔거든요. 십오 년 만에 처음 가 본 나들이였어요.\"\n\n\"꽤 오래됐네요.\" 뉴먼이 서글프게 말했어.\n\n\"그래요, 햇수로 따지면 돌아보기에 아주 긴 세월이죠. 그래도 어찌 된 일인지, 고맙게도 그 외로운 날들이 그럭저럭 평온하고 행복하게 흘러갔어요.\" 미니어처 화가가 대답했어. \"저한테 오빠가 하나 있어요, 노그스 씨. 제 유일한 혈육이죠. 그런데 그 오랜 세월 동안 한 번도 못 만났어요. 우리가 다툰 건 절대 아니에요. 그냥 오빠가 시골로 일을 배우러 내려갔고, 거기서 결혼을 했거든요. 새로운 인연과 정이 둘레에 생기다 보니, 저 같은 가엾고 보잘것없는 여자는 잊어버린 거죠. 뭐, 그럴 만도 했어요, 아시잖아요. 제가 그걸 두고 불평한다고 생각하진 마세요. 저는 늘 혼자 이렇게 되뇌었거든요. '아주 당연한 일이야. 가엾은 우리 존이 세상에서 자리를 잡아 가고 있고, 근심 걱정을 털어놓을 아내도 있고, 이제 곁에서 뛰노는 아이들까지 있으니, 부디 오빠하고 그 식구들에게 신의 축복이 있기를. 그리고 언젠가 다 같이, 다시는 헤어지지 않을 그곳에서 만나게 되기를.' 그런데 어떻게 됐는지 아세요, 노그스 씨?\" 미니어처 화가가 얼굴이 환해지며 손뼉을 쳤어. \"바로 그 오빠가 마침내 런던에 올라와서는, 저를 찾아낼 때까지 도무지 가만있지를 않은 거예요. 그러고는 여기 와서 바로 저 의자에 앉아 어린아이처럼 엉엉 우는 거 있죠. 저를 만나서 너무 기쁘다면서요. 또 어땠는지 아세요? 저를 멀리 시골 자기 집까지 꼭 데려가겠다고 우기더라고요(아주 으리으리한 곳이에요, 노그스 씨. 커다란 정원이 있고, 밭은 또 몇 마지긴지 몰라요. 제복 입은 하인이 식탁 시중을 들고, 소며 말이며 돼지며, 또 뭐가 있었는지 다 모르겠네요). 그러고는 저를 꼬박 한 달이나 붙잡아 두고, 거기서 평생을 함께 살자고 졸랐어요. 네, 평생을요. 올케도 그러고, 아이들도 그랬어요. 아이들이 넷이었는데, 그중에 맏딸은요, 글쎄 그 애한테 제 이름을 붙여 줬더라고요. 그것도 팔 년이나 전에요, 정말이라니까요. 그렇게 행복했던 적이 없어요. 평생 단 한 번도요!\" 이 갸륵한 사람은 손수건에 얼굴을 묻고 큰 소리로 흐느꼈어. 가슴에 묻어 둔 걸 풀어놓을 첫 기회였으니, 그 마음이 멋대로 터져 나올 수밖에 없었지.\n\n\"하지만 어머나 세상에.\" 라 크리비 양이 잠깐 말을 멈췄다가 눈물을 닦고는, 손수건을 부산하고 잽싸게 주머니에 쑤셔 넣으며 말했어. \"제가 당신 눈엔 얼마나 어리석은 사람으로 보일까요, 노그스 씨! 이런 얘기까지 할 생각은 없었는데. 그냥 제가 왜 니클비 양을 못 봤는지 설명하고 싶었을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"그 노부인은 보셨나요?\" 뉴먼이 물었어.\n\n\"니클비 부인 말씀이세요?\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"그럼 한마디 해 둘게요, 노그스 씨. 그쪽 눈 밖에 안 나고 싶으시면, 이제 그분을 노부인이라고 부르지 않는 게 좋을 거예요. 그 말을 들으면 별로 안 좋아하실 것 같거든요. 네, 그저께 밤에 갔었어요. 그런데 무슨 일인지 잔뜩 콧대가 높아져서는, 어찌나 거창하고 알쏭달쏭하게 굴던지 도무지 종잡을 수가 없더라고요. 그래서 솔직히 말하면, 저도 거창하게 굴어야겠다 싶어서, 위세 좀 부리며 나와 버렸죠. 그동안 다시 누그러져서 찾아올 줄 알았는데, 여기 한 번도 안 왔어요.\"\n\n\"니클비 양 이야긴데요—\" 뉴먼이 말했어.\n\n\"아, 제가 없는 동안 두 번이나 여기 왔었대요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대답했어. \"제가 그 무슨 플레이스라는 데 사는 그 대단한 사람들 틈으로 찾아가는 걸 그 애가 안 좋아할까 봐 걱정이 돼서요. 그래서 하루 이틀 기다려 보고, 그래도 못 만나면 편지를 쓸 생각이었어요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 뉴먼이 손가락을 딱 꺾으며 소리쳤어.\n\n\"아무튼 그 식구들 소식을 다 듣고 싶네요.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"골든 스퀘어의 그 거칠고 모진 괴물은 잘 지내나요? 뭐, 당연히 잘 지내겠죠. 그런 사람들은 늘 그러니까요. 건강이 어떠냐는 게 아니라, 어떻게 지내느냐는 거예요. 요즘 어떻게 행동하고 있느냐고요.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을 인간!\" 뉴먼이 애지중지하던 모자를 바닥에 내동댕이치며 외쳤어. \"표리부동한 개 같은 놈이죠.\"\n\n\"어머나, 노그스 씨, 무서워 죽겠네요!\" 라 크리비 양이 새파랗게 질리며 외쳤어.\n\n\"어제 오후에 형편만 됐으면 그놈 낯짝을 아주 망가뜨려 놨을 거예요.\" 뉴먼이 안절부절못하고 왔다 갔다 하면서, 벽난로 위에 걸린 캐닝 씨의 초상화에 대고 주먹을 휘두르며 말했어. \"정말 코앞까지 갔었다고요. 두 손을 주머니에 푹 찔러 넣고 꽉 붙들고 있어야 했어요. 언젠가는 그 작은 뒷방에서 해치우고 말 거예요, 분명히요. 일을 더 그르칠까 봐 겁만 안 났어도 진작 했을 텐데. 죽기 전에 그놈하고 단둘이 문을 이중으로 걸어 잠그고 끝장을 보고 말 거예요, 정말이라니까요.\"\n\n\"진정 안 하시면 저 비명 지를 거예요, 노그스 씨.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"정말 참을 수가 없을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마세요.\" 뉴먼이 사납게 이리저리 휙휙 움직이며 받아쳤어. \"그놈이 오늘 밤 올라와요. 제가 편지로 알렸거든요. 그놈은 제가 알고 있는 줄은 꿈에도 몰라요. 제가 신경 쓰는 줄도 모르고요. 교활한 악당 같으니! 그놈은 그런 건 생각도 못 해요. 절대로요, 절대로. 두고 보세요, 제가 그놈을 막을 거예요. 바로 저, 뉴먼 노그스가요. 호, 호, 그 못된 놈!\"\n\n뉴먼 노그스는 스스로를 부추겨 터무니없을 만큼 격분한 상태로 몰아갔어. 그러고는 사람 몸으로 할 수 있는 가장 괴상한 동작으로 방 안을 들썩들썩 휘젓고 다녔지. 벽에 걸린 작은 미니어처들을 향해 주먹을 휘두르는가 하면, 마치 그 망상을 더 부추기기라도 하듯 제 머리를 세게 쥐어박기도 했어. 그러다 마침내 숨이 차고 기진맥진해서 아까 앉았던 자리에 풀썩 주저앉았지.\n\n\"자.\" 뉴먼이 모자를 집어 들며 말했어. \"이제 좀 풀렸네요. 한결 나아요. 이제 다 말씀드릴게요.\"\n\n라 크리비 양을 다시 안심시키는 데는 시간이 좀 걸렸어. 이 놀라운 소동에 거의 정신이 나갈 만큼 겁을 먹었으니까. 하지만 그게 끝나자, 뉴먼은 케이트와 그 삼촌 사이에 오갔던 일을 빠짐없이 충실히 들려줬어. 이야기를 시작하기 전에 자기가 진작부터 그 일을 의심해 왔다는 것과, 왜 그런 의심을 품게 됐는지를 먼저 밝혔지. 그리고 니콜라스에게 몰래 편지를 써 보냈다는 것까지 알려 주며 이야기를 맺었어.\n\n작은 라 크리비 양의 분노는 뉴먼처럼 그렇게 별나게 드러나지는 않았지만, 그 격렬함과 강도는 거의 못지않았어. 정말이지 그 순간 랄프 니클비가 그 방에 들어왔더라면, 뉴먼 노그스보다도 라 크리비 양이 더 위험한 적수라는 걸 알게 됐을지도 몰라.\n\n\"이런 말을 하다니 하느님 용서하소서.\" 라 크리비 양이 분풀이를 한바탕 쏟아 낸 끝에 말했어. \"그런데 정말이지 이걸로 그 인간을 푹 찔러 버려도 속이 시원할 것 같아요.\"\n\n라 크리비 양이 들고 있던 건 그리 무시무시한 무기가 아니었어. 사실 흑연 연필 하나, 딱 그거였거든. 그런데 자기 실수를 알아채고는, 이 작은 초상화가는 그걸 자개 손잡이가 달린 과일칼로 바꿔 들었어. 그러고는 자기가 얼마나 무서운 생각을 품고 있는지 보여 주려는 듯, 말을 하면서 그 칼로 한 번 푹 찔러 보였지. 하지만 그건 반쪽짜리 식빵 부스러기 하나 흐트러뜨리지 못할 시늉이었어.\n\n\"오늘 밤이 지나면 그 애가 거기 더는 안 있을 거예요.\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"그게 다행이죠.\"\n\n\"있긴요!\" 라 크리비 양이 외쳤어. \"몇 주 전에 진작 거기서 나왔어야 했어요.\"\n\n\"—우리가 이걸 알았더라면 말이죠.\" 뉴먼이 받아쳤어. \"그런데 몰랐잖아요. 그 애 어머니나 오빠 말고는 제대로 끼어들 사람이 없으니까요. 그 어머니는 마음이 약해요. 가엾게도, 약하죠. 그 착한 청년이 오늘 밤 올 거예요.\"\n\n\"어머나 세상에!\" 라 크리비 양이 외쳤어. \"한꺼번에 다 말해 버리면 그 사람이 무슨 무서운 일을 저지를 거예요, 노그스 씨.\"\n\n뉴먼이 손 비비던 걸 멈추고 생각에 잠긴 표정을 지었어.\n\n\"틀림없어요.\" 라 크리비 양이 진지하게 말했어. \"당신이 아주 조심해서 진실을 알려 주지 않으면, 그 사람이 삼촌이나 그 작자들 중 하나한테 무슨 난폭한 짓을 저지를 거예요. 그러면 제 머리 위에 끔찍한 재앙을 불러들이고, 우리 모두에게 슬픔과 비탄을 안기게 되겠죠.\"\n\n\"그건 미처 생각 못 했네요.\" 뉴먼이 점점 더 얼굴이 어두워지며 받아쳤어. \"저는 그 사람이 동생을 여기 데려올 경우에 좀 받아 달라고 부탁하러 온 거였는데, 그런데—\"\n\n\"하지만 이건 훨씬 더 중요한 문제예요.\" 라 크리비 양이 끼어들었어. \"그건 여기 오기 전에 이미 확신할 수 있는 일이었지만, 이 일의 끝은 아무도 내다볼 수 없어요. 당신이 아주 신중하고 조심하지 않는다면 말이에요.\"\n\n\"제가 뭘 할 수 있겠어요?\" 뉴먼이 잔뜩 골치 아프고 난감한 표정으로 머리를 긁적이며 외쳤어. \"그 사람이 그놈들을 다 권총으로 쏴 죽이겠다고 하면, 저는 어쩔 수 없이 이렇게 말할 거예요. '그럼요, 그래도 싸죠'라고요.\"\n\n이 말을 듣고 라 크리비 양은 가벼운 비명을 참지 못했어. 그러고는 곧장 뉴먼한테서 엄숙한 약속을 받아 내려고 했지. 니콜라스의 분노를 가라앉히는 데 온 힘을 다하겠다는 약속을 말이야. 몇 번 망설인 끝에 뉴먼이 그러기로 했어. 그런 다음 두 사람은 머리를 맞대고, 어떻게 하면 니콜라스에게 그를 와야만 했던 그 사정을 가장 안전하고 확실하게 전할 수 있을지 의논했어.\n\n\"그 사람이 무슨 일을 저지르기 전에 마음을 식힐 시간이 있어야 해요.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"그게 제일 중요해요. 밤늦게까지는 알려 줘선 안 돼요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 사람은 오늘 저녁 여섯 시에서 일곱 시 사이에 시내에 도착할 거예요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어. \"그 사람이 물으면 제가 숨길 수가 없다고요.\"\n\n\"그럼 나가 계셔야죠, 노그스 씨.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"일 때문에 발이 묶였던 걸로 하면 그만이잖아요. 그리고 자정 가까이 될 때까진 돌아오지 마세요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 사람이 곧장 여기로 올 거예요.\" 뉴먼이 되받았어.\n\n\"그렇겠죠.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"하지만 저도 집에 없을 거예요. 당신이 가시는 대로 저는 곧장 시내로 가서 니클비 부인하고 화해하고, 그분을 극장으로 데려갈 거니까요. 그러면 그 사람은 그의 여동생이 어디 사는지조차 모르게 되겠죠.\"\n\n이 문제를 더 의논해 보니, 이게 택할 수 있는 가장 안전하고 실행할 만한 방법인 듯했어. 그래서 결국 그렇게 하기로 결정이 났지. 그리고 뉴먼은 여러 가지 덧붙는 당부와 간곡한 부탁을 다 듣고 나서, 라 크리비 양과 작별하고 골든 스퀘어로 터덜터덜 돌아갔어. 가는 내내 머릿속에 빼곡히 들어찬 수많은 가능성과 불가능성을 곱씹으면서 말이야. 방금 끝낸 그 대화에서 비롯된 생각들이었지.\n\n# CHAPTER 32\n\n드디어 런던에 다다랐다. 니콜라스는 외투를 젖히며 한참 단잠에 빠져 있던 스마이크를 흔들어 깨웠어. \"드디어 런던이야! 영영 닿지 못할 것만 같았는데.\"\n\n\"그런 것치곤 꽤 빠른 속도로 달려오셨는데요.\" 마부가 어깨너머로 니콜라스를 돌아보며 말했어. 그다지 기분 좋은 얼굴은 아니었지.\n\n\"그래요, 알아요.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어. \"그저 목적지에 빨리 닿고 싶은 마음이 너무 간절해서, 길이 더 멀게 느껴졌던 거예요.\"\n\n\"흠, 댁이 타고 온 이런 말들 뒤에 앉아서도 길이 멀게 느껴졌다면, 어지간히도 마음이 급하셨던 모양이구먼.\" 마부가 이렇게 말하더니 채찍을 휙 풀어, 강조라도 하듯 옆에 있던 사내아이의 종아리를 슬쩍 후려쳤어.\n\n마차는 시끄럽고 분주하고 사람들로 북적이는 런던 거리를 덜컹덜컹 달려갔어. 거리에는 환하게 타오르는 가로등이 두 줄로 길게 늘어서 있었고, 군데군데 약국의 번쩍이는 불빛이 점점이 박혀 있었지. 게다가 가게 창문에서 쏟아져 나오는 눈부신 빛까지 거리를 환히 밝히고 있었어. 가게 안에는 반짝이는 보석이며, 더없이 화려한 빛깔의 비단과 벨벳이며, 입맛을 자극하는 진미며, 사치스러운 장식품들이 풍성하게 줄지어 반짝거렸어. 사람들의 물결은 끝도 없이 밀려들었어. 인파 속에서 서로 부딪치며 앞으로 서둘러 나아가면서도, 사방을 에워싼 그 온갖 보물에는 거의 눈길도 주지 않는 듯했지. 그러는 사이 온갖 모양과 종류의 탈것들이 흐르는 물처럼 한데 뒤엉켜 움직이며, 그 끊임없는 굉음으로 소음과 소란을 더했어.\n\n쉴 새 없이 바뀌고 변하는 풍경들을 휙휙 스쳐 지나갈 때면, 그것들이 눈앞을 얼마나 기묘한 행렬을 이루며 지나가는지 보는 게 신기했어. 세계 곳곳에서 들여온 옷감으로 지은 화려한 드레스를 파는 가게들. 물릴 대로 물린 입맛을 다시 돋우고 응석을 받아 주려는, 그리고 늘 똑같은 잔칫상에 새로운 맛을 더해 주려는 온갖 먹을거리가 그득한 유혹적인 상점들. 갖가지 정교한 모양의 꽃병과 접시와 술잔으로 빚어낸, 윤이 반들반들 나는 금은 그릇들. 총, 칼, 권총, 신식 살상 무기들. 굽은 몸을 펴 주는 나사와 쇠붙이, 갓난아기 옷, 병자를 위한 약, 죽은 이를 위한 관, 묻힌 이를 위한 교회 묘지. 이 모든 것이 서로 뒤섞여 나란히 모여서는, 마치 옛 네덜란드 화가가 그린 기괴한 무리들처럼 어지러운 춤을 추며 휙휙 스쳐 지나가는 것 같았어. 그리고 무심하고 정신없는 군중을 향해 그 그림들과 똑같은 엄숙한 교훈을 던지는 듯했지.\n\n그 풍경에 새로운 의미와 뜻을 더해 주는 건 군중 그 자체에도 모자람이 없었어. 금세공인의 보물을 비추는 화려한 불빛 속에서 초라한 거리 가수의 누더기가 펄럭였어. 입맛 당기는 음식이 놓인 창문 둘레로 창백하고 핼쑥한 얼굴들이 서성거렸지. 굶주린 눈은 얇은 유리 한 장이 — 그들에게는 쇠벽이나 다름없는 그 유리가 — 지키고 있는 산더미 같은 음식 위를 헤맸어. 반쯤 헐벗은 채 떨고 있는 사람들은 중국산 숄과 인도산 금빛 천을 멍하니 바라보려고 걸음을 멈췄어. 가장 큰 관 만드는 집에서는 세례 잔치가 벌어지고 있었어. 그런가 하면 가장 으리으리한 저택에서는 한창 진행되던 대단한 공사가 장례 문장 때문에 멈춰 서 있었지. 삶과 죽음이 손을 맞잡고 나란히 갔어. 부와 가난이 어깨를 나란히 하고 서 있었지. 배부름과 굶주림이 함께 몸을 누였어.\n\n하지만 그래도 런던이었어. 킹스턴을 한두 마일 앞두고서 마차 창밖으로 고개를 내밀던 시골 노부인이 있었지. 마부에게 분명 그곳을 지나쳐서 자기를 내려 주는 걸 깜빡한 거라며 소리치던 그 부인도, 마침내 만족했어.\n\n니콜라스는 마차가 멈춘 여관에 자기와 스마이크가 묵을 잠자리를 잡았어. 그러고는 한순간도 지체하지 않고 곧장 뉴먼 노그스의 하숙집으로 향했지. 시간이 일 분씩 흐를 때마다 그의 불안과 조바심은 점점 커져서, 이젠 거의 걷잡을 수 없을 지경이었거든.\n\n뉴먼의 다락방에는 불이 피워져 있었어. 촛불도 하나 켜진 채 남겨져 있었지. 바닥은 깨끗이 쓸려 있었고, 방은 그런 방이 갖출 수 있는 한 가장 아늑하게 정돈되어 있었어. 그리고 먹을 것과 마실 것이 탁자 위에 가지런히 놓여 있었지. 모든 게 뉴먼 노그스의 다정한 보살핌과 정성을 말해 주고 있었어. 그런데 정작 뉴먼은 거기 없었어.\n\n\"그분이 몇 시에 돌아오는지 아세요?\" 니콜라스는 뉴먼의 앞집 문을 두드리며 물었어.\n\n\"아, 존슨 씨!\" 크롤이 나타나며 말했어. \"어서 오세요, 선생님. 안색이 참 좋으시네요! 정말이지 믿기지가 않을 만큼—\"\n\n\"실례지만,\" 니콜라스가 말을 끊었어. \"제 질문에—저는 그게 너무 궁금해서요.\"\n\n\"그게 말이죠, 그 사람이 골치 아픈 일이 좀 생겨서,\" 크롤이 대답했어. \"열두 시 전에는 못 돌아올 겁니다. 정말 가기 싫어했어요, 내가 장담하는데. 그런데 어쩔 수가 없었지요. 그래도 돌아올 때까지 선생님더러 편히 계시라고, 또 내가 선생님을 대접하라고 일러두고 갔어요. 나야 기꺼이 그러지요.\"\n\n다 함께 즐겁게 지내려고 자기가 얼마나 발 벗고 나설 준비가 되어 있는지 증명이라도 하듯, 크롤 씨는 이렇게 말하면서 의자를 탁자 쪽으로 끌어당겼어. 그러고는 찬 고기를 듬뿍 자기 접시에 덜더니, 니콜라스와 스마이크에게도 자기처럼 하라고 권했지.\n\n실망스럽고 마음이 불편한 니콜라스는 음식에 손도 댈 수 없었어. 그래서 스마이크가 탁자에 편히 자리 잡은 걸 확인한 뒤, 밖으로 나갔지. 크롤 씨가 입에 음식을 가득 문 채로 여러 번 말렸지만 막무가내였어. 그러고는 혹시 뉴먼이 먼저 돌아오면 붙잡아 두라고 스마이크에게 일러두었어.\n\n라 크리비 양이 짐작했던 대로, 니콜라스는 곧장 그녀의 집으로 향했어. 그런데 그녀가 집에 없자, 한참 동안 마음속으로 고민했지. 어머니 집으로 갈까, 그러면 어머니를 랄프 니클비와 곤란한 처지에 빠뜨리는 게 아닐까 하고 말이야. 하지만 무언가 자기가 꼭 집에 있어야 할 절박한 이유가 없었다면 뉴먼이 자기더러 돌아오라고 청하지 않았을 거라는 확신이 들었어. 그래서 거기로 가기로 마음먹고, 있는 힘껏 동쪽으로 서둘러 갔지.\n\n니클비 부인은 열두 시가 넘어야, 어쩌면 더 늦어야 집에 돌아오신다고 하녀가 말했어. 니클비 양은 잘 지내는 것 같지만 이제는 집에서 살지 않고, 아주 가끔이 아니면 집에 오지도 않는다고 했지. 어디에 묵고 있는지는 모르겠지만, 마담 만탈리니네는 아니라고 했어. 그건 확실하다고 말이야.\n\n가슴이 거세게 두근거리는 데다 무슨 일인지도 모를 불행을 두려워하며, 니콜라스는 스마이크를 두고 온 곳으로 돌아왔어. 뉴먼은 아직 돌아오지 않았어. 열두 시 전에는 돌아올 리 없었지. 가망이 없었어. 잠깐만이라도 그를 데리러 사람을 보낼 수는 없을까, 아니면 글 한 줄이라도 적어 보내서 말로라도 답을 받을 수는 없을까? 그것도 도저히 불가능했어. 그는 골든 스퀘어에도 없었고, 아마 먼 곳으로 무슨 심부름을 보내진 모양이었어.\n\n니콜라스는 있는 자리에 가만히 있어 보려 했어. 그런데 너무 초조하고 들떠서 도무지 앉아 있을 수가 없었지. 움직이지 않으면 시간을 허비하는 것만 같았어. 어처구니없는 생각이라는 건 알았지만, 도저히 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었지. 그래서 모자를 집어 들고 다시 밖으로 나갔어.\n\n이번엔 서쪽으로 발길을 옮겼어. 서두르는 걸음으로 긴 거리들을 걸으면서, 도무지 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 수천 가지 불안과 걱정에 시달렸지. 그는 하이드 파크로 들어섰어. 이제는 인적도 없이 고요한 곳이었지. 그러고는 마치 자기 생각을 뒤에 떼어 놓고 가려는 듯 걸음을 더 빨리했어. 그런데 눈길을 끌 만한 게 더 이상 지나가지 않으니, 생각은 오히려 더 빽빽하게 밀려들었어. 그리고 한 가지 생각이 늘 마음을 짓눌렀지. 무언가 불행이 닥쳤는데, 그게 너무도 끔찍한 것이라 모두가 자기한테 털어놓기를 겁내는 거라는 생각이었어. 묵은 의문이 자꾸자꾸 고개를 들었어. 대체 무슨 일일까? 니콜라스는 지칠 때까지 걸었지만, 조금도 알아내지 못했어. 정말이지 마침내 공원을 나설 때는 들어갈 때보다 훨씬 더 머릿속이 어지럽고 헷갈렸지.\n\n그는 이른 아침 이후로 거의 아무것도 먹지도 마시지도 못해서, 완전히 녹초가 되고 기진맥진한 상태였어. 파크 레인과 본드 스트리트 사이에 놓인 큰길 하나를 따라 처음 출발했던 곳으로 맥없이 돌아오던 길에, 그는 어느 멋진 호텔을 지나치다가 무심코 걸음을 멈췄어.\n\n'비싼 데겠지.' 니콜라스는 생각했어. '하지만 포도주 한 잔에 비스킷 하나쯤이야, 어디서 먹든 대단한 사치는 아니잖아. 그래도 모르겠다.'\n\n그는 몇 걸음 더 걸었어. 그런데 눈앞에 길게 늘어선 가스등 줄을 아쉬운 듯 바라보면서, 저 끝까지 가려면 얼마나 걸릴까 생각했지. 게다가 마침 첫 충동에 가장 쉽게 휩쓸리는 그런 기분이기도 했어. 거기다 그 호텔에 강하게 끌리기도 했지. 한편으론 호기심 때문에, 또 한편으론 자기도 뭐라 설명하기 어려운 묘한 감정들이 뒤섞인 탓이었어. 그래서 니콜라스는 다시 발길을 돌려 커피룸으로 들어섰어.\n\n그곳은 아주 멋지게 꾸며져 있었어. 벽은 더없이 고급스러운 프랑스산 벽지로 장식되어 있었고, 우아하게 디자인된 금박 처마 장식으로 한층 화려해 보였지. 바닥은 값비싼 양탄자로 덮여 있었어. 그리고 멋진 거울이 두 개 있었는데, 하나는 벽난로 위에, 또 하나는 방 맞은편 끝에 바닥부터 천장까지 닿게 걸려 있었어. 그 거울들이 다른 아름다운 것들을 곱절로 비추고, 거기에 제 나름의 아름다움까지 보태어 전체 분위기를 더욱 돋우었지. 벽난로 옆 칸막이 자리에는 다소 시끄러운 신사 넷이 무리 지어 있었어. 그리고 다른 손님이라곤 단둘뿐이었는데, 둘 다 나이 지긋한 신사였고 둘 다 혼자였어.\n\n낯선 곳에 들어선 사람이 그곳을 한눈에 쓱 훑어볼 때처럼, 니콜라스도 이 모든 걸 단번에 살피고는 그 시끄러운 무리 바로 옆 칸막이 자리에 앉았어. 그들을 등진 채로 말이야. 그러고는 클라레 한 병을 주문하는 걸 잠시 미뤘지. 웨이터와 그 나이 든 신사 한 명이 메뉴판에 적힌 어떤 품목의 값을 두고 다투는 게 끝날 때까지 기다린 거야. 니콜라스는 신문을 집어 들고 읽기 시작했어.\n\n스무 줄도 채 읽지 못했고, 사실은 그 자신도 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있었어. 그런데 누군가 자기 여동생 이름을 입에 올리는 바람에 화들짝 놀랐지. '꼬마 케이트 니클비'라는 말이 그의 귀에 박혔거든. 그는 깜짝 놀라 고개를 들었어. 그러면서 맞은편 거울에 비친 모습으로, 등 뒤에 있던 무리 가운데 둘이 일어나 벽난로 앞에 서 있는 걸 봤지. '저 둘 중 한 명한테서 나온 말이 분명해.' 니콜라스는 생각했어. 그는 좀 분한 얼굴로 다음 말을 들으려 기다렸어. 말투가 도무지 존중이라곤 없었거든. 게다가 그 말을 한 사람이라고 짐작되는 자의 생김새도 거칠고 거만했지.\n\n그자는 — 니콜라스가 그 얼굴을 볼 수 있었던 바로 그 거울 속에서 관찰한 바로는 — 벽난로를 등지고 서서 더 젊은 남자와 이야기를 나누고 있었어. 그 젊은 남자는 일행을 등진 채 모자를 쓰고 서서, 거울을 보며 셔츠 깃을 매만지고 있었지. 둘은 소곤소곤 이야기하다가, 이따금 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸어. 하지만 니콜라스는 자기 귀를 사로잡았던 그 말도, 비슷하게 들리는 그 어떤 말도 다시 들을 수가 없었어.\n\n마침내 둘은 다시 자리에 앉았어. 그리고 포도주를 더 시키자, 무리는 한층 더 시끄럽게 떠들어 댔지. 그래도 여전히 그가 아는 누군가를 두고 하는 말은 없었어. 그래서 니콜라스는 자기 들뜬 머릿속이 그 소리를 통째로 지어냈거나, 아니면 다른 어떤 말을 그동안 줄곧 마음에 담아 둔 그 이름으로 바꿔 들은 거라고 믿게 되었지.\n\n'그것도 참 이상해.' 니콜라스는 생각했어. '그게 그냥 \"케이트\"나 \"케이트 니클비\"였다면 이렇게까지 놀라지도 않았을 텐데. 하필 \"꼬마 케이트 니클비\"라니!'\n\n마침 그때 포도주가 나오는 바람에 그는 생각을 끝맺지 못했어. 그는 한 잔을 쭉 들이켜고 다시 신문을 집어 들었지. 바로 그 순간—\n\n\"꼬마 케이트 니클비!\" 등 뒤에서 그 목소리가 외쳤어.\n\n\"역시 내 생각이 맞았어.\" 니콜라스는 신문을 손에서 떨어뜨리며 중얼거렸어. \"그리고 내가 짐작한 그 사람이야.\"\n\n\"술 찌꺼기로 그 아가씨를 위해 건배하는 건 영 안 될 말이지.\" 그 목소리가 말했어. \"그러니 새 큰 병의 첫 잔으로 그 아가씨를 위해 마시자고. 꼬마 케이트 니클비를 위하여!\"\n\n\"꼬마 케이트 니클비를 위하여.\" 나머지 셋이 외쳤어. 그리고 빈 잔들이 탁 내려놓아졌지.\n\n공공장소에서 자기 여동생 이름이 이토록 가볍고 함부로 입에 오르내리는 그 말투와 태도에, 니콜라스는 신경을 곤두세우고 있다가 단번에 발끈했어. 하지만 그는 안간힘을 써서 가만히 참았어. 심지어 고개조차 돌리지 않았지.\n\n\"그 깍쟁이!\" 아까 말했던 바로 그 목소리가 말했어. \"정말 영락없는 니클비야. 늙은 삼촌 랄프를 빼다 박은 후계자라니까. 더 애가 닳게 하려고 일부러 뒤로 빼는 거지. 그자도 그렇잖아. 랄프한테선 끈질기게 매달리지 않으면 아무것도 못 얻어 내. 그러다 돈이 들어오면 곱절로 반갑고, 흥정은 곱절로 빡빡하지. 이쪽은 애가 타는데 그쪽은 느긋하니까 말이야. 아아! 지독한 꿍꿍이라니까.\"\n\n\"지독한 꿍꿍이지.\" 두 목소리가 따라 했어.\n\n니콜라스는 완전히 애가 탔어. 맞은편의 나이 든 신사 둘이 차례로 일어나 나가 버렸거든. 그 바람에 한마디라도 놓칠까 봐 마음을 졸였지. 그런데 그들이 물러나는 동안 대화가 잠시 끊겼다가, 그들이 방을 나가고 나자 한층 더 거리낌 없이 다시 이어졌어.\n\n\"내 생각엔,\" 더 젊은 신사가 말했어. \"그 늙은 여편네가 질투가 나서, 그 애를 가둬 둔 게 아닐까 싶은데. 정말이지 딱 그런 것 같단 말이지.\"\n\n\"둘이 싸워서 꼬마 니클비가 제 어미한테 돌아가 버리면, 오히려 더 잘된 일이지.\" 처음 그 사람이 말했어. \"그 노부인은 내가 뭐든 마음대로 주무를 수 있거든. 내가 무슨 말을 하든 다 믿을 테니까.\"\n\n\"이런, 그건 맞는 말이야.\" 다른 목소리가 대꾸했어. \"하하하! 가엾은 양반 같으니!\"\n\n언제나 함께 끼어드는 그 두 목소리가 웃음을 받아 이었고, 웃음판은 니클비 부인을 안주 삼아 한껏 무르익었어. 니콜라스는 분노로 온몸이 화끈거렸어. 하지만 그 순간만큼은 꾹 참고, 더 들으려고 기다렸지.\n\n그가 들은 말을 여기서 다시 옮길 필요는 없겠어. 다만 이것만은 알아 둬. 포도주 잔이 도는 동안, 그는 자기가 엿들은 그 대화의 주인공들이 어떤 자들이며 무슨 꿍꿍이를 품고 있는지 충분히 알게 되었어. 랄프의 악행이 어디까지 뻗쳐 있는지, 또 자기가 런던으로 불려 온 진짜 이유가 무엇인지도 낱낱이 깨닫게 되었지. 그는 이 모든 것, 아니 그 이상을 들었어. 여동생이 겪는 고통이 조롱당하고, 그 애의 정숙한 행실이 비웃음을 사며 잔인하게 비틀려 곡해되는 걸 들었어. 그 애의 이름이 이 입 저 입으로 오르내리고, 그 애가 거칠고 무례한 내기와 함부로 지껄이는 말, 음탕한 농담의 거리가 되는 걸 들었지.\n\n맨 처음 입을 연 그 남자가 대화를 이끌었어. 사실상 거의 혼자 다 떠들었지. 그저 이따금 동료들 가운데 누군가가 슬쩍 한마디 거들어 부추길 뿐이었어. 그래서 니콜라스는, 그 무리 앞에 나서서 바싹 타들어 가는 목구멍에서 억지로 말을 짜낼 만큼 마음을 가다듬고 나자, 바로 그 남자에게 말을 걸었어.\n\n\"잠깐 얘기 좀 합시다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"나하고 말이오?\" 멀버리 호크 경이 경멸 어린 놀란 눈으로 그를 훑어보며 되받았어.\n\n\"당신하고라고 했소.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어. 분노가 목을 메이게 해서 말하기가 무척 힘들었지.\n\n\"이런, 정말이지 수수께끼 같은 낯선 분이로군!\" 멀버리 경이 술잔을 입가로 들어 올리며, 친구들을 둘러보며 외쳤어.\n\n\"잠깐 나하고 따로 좀 가겠소, 아니면 거절하는 거요?\" 니콜라스가 매섭게 말했어.\n\n멀버리 경은 그저 술을 마시려던 손길을 멈추고는, 용건을 말하든지 아니면 탁자에서 꺼지라고 했어.\n\n니콜라스는 주머니에서 명함을 한 장 꺼내, 그 앞에 휙 던졌어.\n\n\"자, 여기.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"내 용건은 짐작할 수 있겠지.\"\n\n이름을 읽는 멀버리 경의 얼굴에 한순간 놀라움이 스쳤어. 거기엔 약간의 당혹스러움도 섞여 있었지. 하지만 그는 곧장 그 기색을 가라앉히고는, 명함을 맞은편에 앉은 베리소프트 경에게 휙 던졌어. 그러고는 앞에 놓인 잔에서 이쑤시개를 하나 뽑아, 아주 느긋하게 입에 갖다 댔지.\n\n\"이름과 주소를?\" 니콜라스가 말했어. 분노가 타오르면서 얼굴이 점점 창백해졌지.\n\n\"둘 다 말해 줄 생각 없는데.\" 멀버리 경이 대꾸했어.\n\n\"이 자리에 신사가 한 분이라도 있다면,\" 니콜라스가 둘러보며 말했어. 핏기 없는 입술로 그 말을 겨우 만들어 내면서 말이야. \"이 사람의 이름과 사는 곳을 내게 알려 주겠지.\"\n\n쥐 죽은 듯한 침묵이 흘렀어.\n\n\"나는 방금 여기서 입에 오르내린 그 아가씨의 오빠요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"이 사람이 거짓말쟁이임을 고발하고, 비겁한 자임을 폭로하겠소. 만약 이 사람한테 친구가 있다면, 자기 이름을 숨기려는 그 치사한 짓의 망신을 면하게 해 주겠지. 게다가 다 부질없는 짓이오. 내가 기어이 알아낼 테고, 알아낼 때까지 절대 놓아주지 않을 테니까.\"\n\n멀버리 경은 그를 경멸하듯 바라보더니, 동료들에게 이렇게 말했어—\n\n\"이 친구가 떠들든 말든 내버려 둬. 이런 신분의 애송이한테 진지하게 할 말은 없으니까. 자정까지 떠들어 댄들, 제 예쁜 누이 덕에 머리통이 깨지는 건 면하게 될 거다.\"\n\n\"넌 비열하고 줏대 없는 악당이다!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"온 세상에 그렇게 알려질 거야. 기어이 네가 누군지 알아내겠어. 네가 아침까지 거리를 걷는다 해도, 네 집까지 따라가고 말 테니까.\"\n\n멀버리 경의 손이 저도 모르게 술병을 움켜쥐었어. 그러고는 한순간, 그걸 도전자의 머리에 내던질 듯한 기세였지. 하지만 그는 그저 잔을 채우고는 비웃듯 껄껄 웃었어.\n\n니콜라스는 그 무리 바로 맞은편에 자리를 잡고 앉았어. 그러고는 웨이터를 불러 자기 술값을 치렀지.\n\n\"저 사람 이름을 아시오?\" 니콜라스가 또렷이 들리는 목소리로 웨이터에게 물었어. 그러면서 멀버리 경을 손가락으로 가리켰지.\n\n멀버리 경이 다시 껄껄 웃었어. 그러자 언제나 함께 떠들던 두 목소리도 그 웃음을 따라 했지. 다만 좀 힘없는 웃음이었어.\n\n\"저 신사분 말입니까, 선생님?\" 웨이터가 대답했어. 분명 자기가 어떻게 처신해야 할지 잘 아는 자였지. 그래서 탈 없이 보일 수 있는 한 최대한 존중을 덜 담고, 최대한 건방을 떨며 대답했어. \"아니요, 선생님, 모릅니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"이봐, 자네.\" 웨이터가 물러나려는데 멀버리 경이 불렀어. \"자네는 저자 이름을 아나?\"\n\n\"이름요, 선생님? 모릅니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그럼 거기 보면 나와 있을 거야.\" 멀버리 경이 니콜라스의 명함을 그에게 휙 던지며 말했어. \"다 외우고 나거든, 그 판때기 쪼가리는 불 속에 던져 버려. 알아들었나?\"\n\n웨이터는 씩 웃었어. 그러고는 니콜라스를 미심쩍은 듯 흘끔거리더니, 명함을 벽난로 거울 틈에 끼워 두는 걸로 어정쩡하게 마무리했어. 그러고는 물러났지.\n\n니콜라스는 팔짱을 끼고 입술을 깨문 채, 아주 잠자코 앉아 있었어. 그래도 그 태도만큼은 분명히 말해 주고 있었지. 멀버리 경을 집까지 따라가겠다는 그 으름장을 기어이 끝까지 실행에 옮기겠다는 굳은 결심을 말이야.\n\n그 무리 가운데 더 젊은 자가 친구에게 말리는 투로 무어라 하는 걸로 봐서, 그가 이런 식의 처사에 반대하며 니콜라스의 요구를 들어주라고 권하는 게 분명했어. 하지만 멀버리 경은 술이 완전히 깨지도 않은 데다, 시무룩하니 고집스럽게 버티는 상태였지. 그래서 약해 빠진 젊은 친구의 만류를 금세 입막음해 버렸어. 게다가 — 마치 그 만류를 다시 듣기 싫다는 듯 — 자기를 혼자 내버려 두라고 우기는 것 같았지. 어쨌든 잠시 뒤에 그 젊은 신사와 언제나 함께 떠들던 두 사람이 정말로 일어나 가 버렸어. 그러고는 곧 물러가서, 친구를 니콜라스와 단둘이 남겨 두었지.\n\n니콜라스 같은 처지에 놓인 사람한테 그 몇 분이 정말이지 납덩이 같은 날개를 단 듯 더디게 흐르는 듯 느껴졌으리라는 건, 누구라도 쉽게 짐작할 수 있을 거야. 게다가 프랑스제 시계의 단조로운 똑딱 소리도, 십오 분마다 울리는 작은 종의 날카로운 소리도, 그 시간이 더 빨리 흐르게 해 주진 못했지. 그래도 그는 거기 앉아 있었어. 그리고 방 맞은편 제 자리에는 멀버리 호크 경이 비스듬히 기대어 있었지. 두 다리를 방석 위에 올려놓고, 손수건을 무릎 위에 아무렇게나 걸친 채로 말이야. 더없이 차갑고 무심하게 클라레 큰 병을 비우면서.\n\n이렇게 둘은 한 시간 넘게 완전한 침묵 속에 있었어. 니콜라스는 적어도 세 시간은 흘렀다고 여겼겠지만, 작은 종은 겨우 네 번밖에 울리지 않았지. 두세 번 그는 화가 나고 조바심이 나서 둘러보았어. 그런데 멀버리 경은 여전히 똑같은 자세로 이따금 잔을 입에 갖다 대고, 마치 살아 있는 사람이라곤 아무도 없는 듯 벽을 멍하니 바라보고 있었어.\n\n마침내 그는 하품을 하고는 기지개를 켜며 일어났어. 그러고는 느긋하게 거울 앞으로 걸어가 자기 모습을 살피더니, 몸을 돌려 니콜라스에게 길고 경멸 어린 눈길을 던지는 영광을 베풀었지. 니콜라스도 기꺼이 마주 노려봤어. 멀버리 경은 어깨를 으쓱하고 살짝 웃더니, 종을 울려 웨이터에게 외투 입는 걸 거들라고 시켰어.\n\n웨이터가 그렇게 해 주고는 문을 열어 잡았어.\n\n\"기다릴 것 없네.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어. 그리고 둘은 다시 단둘이 남았지.\n\n멀버리 경은 방 안을 몇 번이나 왔다 갔다 했어. 그러는 내내 무심하게 휘파람을 불었지. 몇 분 전에 따라 두었던 클라레 마지막 잔을 마저 비우려고 멈췄다가, 다시 걷고, 모자를 쓰고, 거울에 비추어 매만지고, 장갑을 끼고는, 마침내 천천히 걸어 나갔어. 니콜라스는 거의 미쳐 버릴 만큼 부글부글 속을 끓이고 있다가, 자리에서 박차고 일어나 그를 뒤따랐어. 어찌나 바싹 따라붙었던지, 멀버리 경이 빠져나간 뒤 문이 채 경첩에 부딪쳐 닫히기도 전에, 둘은 거리에 나란히 서 있었지.\n\n마차 한 대가 대기하고 있었어. 마부가 덮개를 열고는, 말 머리를 잡으려고 뛰어내렸지.\n\n\"내게 정체를 밝히겠소?\" 니콜라스가 억누른 목소리로 물었어.\n\n\"싫다.\" 상대가 사납게 대꾸했어. 그러고는 욕을 한마디 보태어 거절을 못 박았지. \"싫다고.\"\n\n\"네 말의 속도만 믿었다간 큰코다칠걸.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"내가 따라갈 테니까. 맹세컨대, 발판에 매달려서라도 따라가고 말 테다.\"\n\n\"그랬다간 채찍질을 당하게 될 거다.\" 멀버리 경이 받아쳤어.\n\n\"넌 악당이야.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"내가 알 게 뭐냐, 넌 심부름꾼 따위겠지.\" 멀버리 호크 경이 말했어.\n\n\"난 시골 신사의 아들이다.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어. \"출신이나 배움에서 너와 대등하고, 그 밖의 모든 면에서는 너보다 위라고 믿는다. 다시 말하지만, 니클비 양은 내 여동생이야. 네 사내답지 못하고 잔인한 행실에 대해, 책임을 지겠나 안 지겠나?\"\n\n\"제대로 된 상대에게라면—그러지. 너 같은 놈한테는—싫다.\" 멀버리 경이 고삐를 손에 쥐며 대꾸했어. \"비켜라, 이 개 같은 놈. 윌리엄, 말 머리를 놔라.\"\n\n\"그러지 않는 게 좋을걸.\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어. 멀버리 경이 마차에 뛰어오르는 순간, 그도 발판으로 뛰어올라 고삐를 움켜잡았지. \"저자는 말을 다룰 줄도 모른다고, 알아 둬. 못 가. 못 간다, 맹세코. 네가 누군지 말하기 전엔 말이야.\"\n\n마부가 머뭇거렸어. 그 암말은 혈기 넘치는 순종이라, 어찌나 사납게 날뛰는지 거의 붙들고 있을 수도 없었거든.\n\n\"놓으라니까!\" 그의 주인이 천둥처럼 호통쳤어.\n\n마부가 시키는 대로 했어. 그러자 말은 마차를 산산조각 내 버리기라도 할 듯 앞발을 치켜들고 날뛰었지. 하지만 니콜라스는 위험 따윈 안중에도 없이, 오직 자기 분노밖에 의식하지 못한 채 여전히 제자리를 지키며 고삐를 움켜쥐고 있었어.\n\n\"손을 놓지 못하겠나?\"\n\n\"네가 누군지 말하겠나?\"\n\n\"싫다!\"\n\n가장 빠른 혀로도 다 옮기지 못할 만큼 순식간에 이 말들이 오갔어. 그러더니 멀버리 경이 채찍을 짧게 고쳐 쥐고는, 니콜라스의 머리와 어깨를 미친 듯이 후려쳤지. 채찍은 몸싸움 중에 부러졌어. 니콜라스는 그 묵직한 손잡이를 빼앗아, 그걸로 상대의 한쪽 얼굴을 눈에서 입까지 쩍 갈라놓았어. 그는 그 상처를 봤어. 암말이 미친 듯이 사납게 내달리기 시작한 걸 알았지. 눈앞에 수백 개의 불빛이 어지럽게 춤추더니, 자기 몸이 땅바닥에 거세게 내동댕이쳐지는 게 느껴졌어.\n\n그는 어질어질하고 속이 메스꺼웠어. 하지만 곧장 비틀거리며 일어섰지. 거리를 마구 내달리며, 앞쪽 사람들에게 길을 비키라고 고래고래 소리치는 사람들 때문에 정신이 번쩍 들었거든. 사람들의 물결이 빠르게 곁을 스쳐 지나가는 게 느껴졌어. 고개를 들어 보니, 마차가 무시무시한 속도로 인도 위를 휩쓸며 달려가는 게 어렴풋이 보였지. 그러더니 커다란 비명이, 무언가 무거운 것이 부서지는 소리가, 그리고 유리 깨지는 소리가 들렸어. 그러고는 저 멀리서 군중이 빙 둘러 모여들었고, 그는 더 이상 아무것도 보거나 들을 수 없었어.\n\n사람들의 관심은 온통 그에게서 마차 안의 그 사람에게로 쏠려 있었어. 그래서 그는 완전히 혼자였지. 이런 상황에서 뒤쫓는 건 미친 짓이라고 옳게 판단한 그는, 가장 가까운 마차 정류장을 찾아 골목길로 접어들었어. 그러고는 일이 분쯤 지나서야 자기가 술 취한 사람처럼 비틀거리고 있다는 걸 알아챘지. 그리고 그제야 처음으로, 얼굴과 가슴을 타고 줄줄 흘러내리는 핏줄기를 깨달았어.\n\n# CHAPTER 33\n\n스마이크와 뉴먼 노그스는 약속한 시간보다 한참 일찍 안달이 나서 집으로 돌아와 있었어. 둘은 난롯가에 앉아 계단에서 들려오는 발소리 하나하나에, 집 안에서 부스럭거리는 작은 소리에까지 신경을 곤두세우며 니콜라스가 오기를 기다렸지. 시간은 자꾸 흘렀고, 어느덧 밤이 깊어 갔어. 니콜라스는 한 시간 안에 돌아오겠다고 했었거든. 그런데 이렇게 늦어지니 두 사람 마음에 불안이 점점 커졌지. 매번 기대가 어긋날 때마다 서로 멍하니 마주보는 그 표정만 봐도 그게 다 드러났어.\n\n마침내 마차가 멈춰 서는 소리가 들렸어. 뉴먼은 니콜라스가 계단을 올라오는 걸 밝혀 주려고 밖으로 뛰어나갔지. 그런데 지난 장 끝에 나온 그 꼴을 한 니콜라스를 보고는, 놀라고 기가 막혀서 그 자리에 멍하니 굳어 버렸어.\n\n\"놀라지 말아요.\" 니콜라스가 그를 떠밀어 방 안으로 들이며 말했어. \"물 한 대야면 씻어 낼 정도지, 큰일은 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"큰일이 없다고요!\" 뉴먼이 소리치며, 니콜라스의 등과 팔을 황급히 더듬어 봤어. 뼈라도 부러지지 않았나 확인하려는 거였지. \"대체 무슨 일이 있었던 거예요?\"\n\n\"전 다 알고 있어요.\" 니콜라스가 말을 잘랐어. \"한 부분은 들었고, 나머지는 짐작했죠. 하지만 이 얼룩을 한 점이라도 닦아 내기 전에, 당신한테서 전부 다 들어야겠어요. 보다시피 저는 침착해요. 마음도 굳혔고요. 자, 친구, 다 털어놔요. 이제 와서 둘러대거나 숨길 때는 지났으니까. 그리고 이제 랄프 니클비한테는 아무것도 소용없어요.\"\n\n\"옷이 여기저기 찢어졌어요. 다리도 절고 있고, 분명 아픈 데가 있을 텐데요.\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"먼저 다친 데부터 봅시다.\"\n\n\"좀 쑤시고 뻐근한 거 말곤 따로 볼 상처도 없어요. 그것도 곧 가라앉을 거고요.\" 니콜라스가 힘겹게 자리에 앉으며 말했어. \"하지만 설령 팔다리가 다 부러졌어도 정신만 멀쩡하다면, 내가 마땅히 알아야 할 걸 다 말해 주기 전엔 붕대 한 장도 못 감게 할 거예요. 자.\" 니콜라스가 노그스에게 손을 내밀며 말했어. \"언젠가 당신한테 누이가 있었다고 했죠. 당신이 불행해지기 전에 세상을 떠났다고요. 그 누이를 생각해서, 말해 줘요, 뉴먼.\"\n\n\"네, 할게요, 할게요.\" 노그스가 말했어. \"사실을 빠짐없이 다 말해 드리죠.\"\n\n뉴먼은 그렇게 했어. 니콜라스는 이미 주워들어 알고 있던 부분과 맞아떨어질 때마다 가끔씩 고개를 끄덕였지. 하지만 두 눈은 난롯불에 꽂은 채, 한 번도 돌아보지 않았어.\n\n이야기가 끝나자, 뉴먼은 어린 친구더러 외투를 벗고 다친 데를 제대로 치료받으라고 우겼어. 니콜라스는 몇 번 마다했지만 마침내 그러기로 했지. 그리고 팔과 어깨에 꽤 심하게 든 멍을 기름과 식초로, 또 뉴먼이 이런저런 하숙인들한테 빌려 온 갖가지 잘 듣는 약으로 문지르는 동안, 니콜라스는 그 멍이 어쩌다 생긴 건지 이야기했어. 그 이야기는 뉴먼의 뜨거운 상상력에 강한 인상을 남겼지. 니콜라스가 싸움의 격렬한 대목에 이르자, 뉴먼이 어찌나 세게 문질러 댔던지 니콜라스는 견딜 수 없이 아팠거든. 그래도 니콜라스는 죽어도 그 아픔을 내색하지 않았어. 그 순간 뉴먼이 멀버리 호크 경한테 손을 쓰고 있는 거나 다름없었고, 정작 진짜 환자는 까맣게 잊고 있었던 게 너무나 뻔했으니까.\n\n이 고행이 끝나자, 니콜라스는 뉴먼과 의논을 했어. 다음 날 아침 자기가 다른 일로 바쁜 사이에, 어머니가 지금 사는 집에서 곧장 떠날 수 있도록 채비를 해 두고, 라 크리비 양을 보내 어머니한테 그 소식을 전하기로 한 거지. 그러고 나서 니콜라스는 스마이크의 외투를 둘러 입고, 둘이 하룻밤 묵기로 한 여관으로 갔어. 그리고 거기서 (랄프에게 보낼 몇 줄을 써 두고, 그걸 다음 날 뉴먼이 전하기로 맡긴 뒤) 그토록 절실히 필요했던 휴식을 청해 보려 애썼지.\n\n사람들 말로는, 술 취한 사람은 벼랑에서 굴러떨어져도 정신이 돌아왔을 때 별다른 큰 탈을 못 느낀다고들 해. 어쩌면 이 말은 다른 격한 흥분 상태에서 입은 상처에도 들어맞을지 몰라. 아무튼 분명한 건, 니콜라스가 다음 날 아침 처음 눈을 떴을 땐 좀 아프긴 했지만, 시계가 일곱 시를 칠 때 별 어려움 없이 침대에서 벌떡 일어났고, 곧 아무 일도 없었던 것처럼 기운을 차렸다는 거야.\n\n니콜라스는 스마이크의 방을 잠깐 들여다보며 뉴먼 노그스가 곧 데리러 올 거라고만 일러두고는, 거리로 내려갔어. 그러고는 삯마차를 불러, 전날 밤 뉴먼이 일러 준 대로 위티털리 부인 댁으로 가자고 했지.\n\n캐도건 플레이스에 다다랐을 때는 여덟 시 십오 분 전이었어. 니콜라스는 이렇게 이른 시각에 일어나 있는 사람이 아무도 없으면 어쩌나 걱정이 들기 시작했지. 그런데 마침 문간 계단을 닦고 있는 하녀가 눈에 띄어 마음을 놓았어. 그 하녀는 그를 미덥잖은 사환에게 넘겼는데, 그 사환은 헝클어진 머리에 막 잠자리에서 빠져나온 사환답게 따끈따끈하고 번들거리는 얼굴로 나타났지.\n\n이 어린 신사한테서 니콜라스는 니클비 양이 지금 집 앞 정원에서 아침 산책을 하고 있다는 말을 들었어. 가서 누이를 찾아와 줄 수 있겠느냐고 묻자, 사환은 시무룩해지며 못 하겠다는 눈치였지. 하지만 일 실링이 쥐어지자 사환은 금세 밝아지더니 할 수 있을 것 같다고 했어.\n\n\"니클비 양한테 오빠가 와 있다고, 몹시 급히 보고 싶어 한다고 전해 주게.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n도금한 단추들이 평소답지 않게 날쌔게 사라졌어. 니콜라스는 안절부절못하며 방 안을 서성였지. 일 분의 기다림조차 견디기 힘든 상태였거든. 곧 그가 잘 아는 가벼운 발소리가 들렸어. 그리고 니콜라스가 미처 마중 나가기도 전에, 케이트가 그의 목을 끌어안고 울음을 터뜨렸지.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 동생아.\" 니콜라스가 케이트를 품에 안으며 말했어. \"얼굴이 왜 이렇게 핼쑥하니!\"\n\n\"여기서 너무 불행했어, 오빠.\" 가엾은 케이트가 흐느꼈어. \"정말, 정말 비참했어. 날 여기 두고 가지 마, 오빠. 안 그러면 가슴이 미어져서 죽고 말 거야.\"\n\n\"어디에도 널 두고 가지 않을 거야.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"다시는 안 그래, 케이트.\" 그는 케이트를 가슴에 꼭 끌어안으며, 자기도 모르게 마음이 북받쳐 외쳤지. \"내가 그때 최선을 다한 거라고 말해 줘. 우리가 헤어졌던 건 내가 네 머리 위에 불행을 끌어올까 봐 두려워서였다고. 그게 너만큼이나 나한테도 힘든 시련이었고, 혹시 내가 잘못했다면 그건 세상 물정을 몰라서, 모르고 한 일이었다고 말해 줘.\"\n\n\"우리가 그렇게 잘 아는 걸 왜 나더러 말하라는 거야?\" 케이트가 달래듯 대꾸했어. \"오빠, 사랑하는 오빠, 어쩌면 이렇게 무너질 수가 있어?\"\n\n\"네가 어떤 일을 겪었는지 아는 게 나한테는 이루 말할 수 없는 자책이야.\" 오빠가 대답했어. \"이렇게 많이 변한 너를 보면서도, 너는 여전히 이렇게 다정하고 잘 참아 내고 있으니. 맙소사!\" 니콜라스는 주먹을 불끈 쥐며 갑자기 말투와 태도를 바꿔 소리쳤어. \"다시 온몸의 피가 다 끓어오르는 것 같아. 너는 지금 당장 나랑 여기서 나가야 해. 어젯밤에도 여기서 자게 하면 안 되는 거였는데, 내가 이걸 다 너무 늦게 알았어. 우리가 떠나기 전에, 내가 누구한테 말을 하면 되지?\"\n\n이 물음은 더없이 때맞춰 나왔어. 바로 그 순간 위티털리 씨가 들어왔거든. 케이트가 그에게 오빠를 소개하자, 니콜라스는 곧바로 자기 목적을 밝히고, 그걸 미룰 수 없다는 뜻을 전했어.\n\n\"분기 통보 기간이,\" 떳떳한 처지에 선 사람다운 근엄한 태도로 위티털리 씨가 말했어. \"아직 절반도 안 지났습니다. 그러니까—\"\n\n\"그러니까,\" 니콜라스가 끼어들었어. \"분기 급료는 못 받게 되겠지요, 선생님. 이렇게 서두르는 걸 양해해 주십시오. 사정이 사정인지라 제 누이를 지금 곧장 데려가야 합니다. 한순간도 지체할 틈이 없어요. 누이가 여기에 가져온 물건은, 허락해 주신다면 오늘 안으로 사람을 보내 찾아가겠습니다.\"\n\n위티털리 씨는 고개를 숙였지만, 케이트가 당장 떠나는 데는 반대하지 않았어. 사실 그는 오히려 반기는 쪽이었지. 텀리 스너핌 경이, 케이트가 위티털리 부인의 체질에 좀 안 맞는 것 같다는 의견을 내놓았었거든.\n\n\"밀린 자잘한 급료 문제는,\" 위티털리 씨가 말했어. \"제가—\" 여기서 그는 격한 기침에 말이 끊겼어. \"제가— 니클비 양한테 빚진 걸로 해 두지요.\"\n\n짚고 넘어가야 할 게, 위티털리 씨는 자잘한 외상을 지고는 그걸 그냥 외상으로 남겨 두는 데 익숙한 사람이었어. 사람은 누구나 자기만의 자그마한 즐기는 방식이 있잖아. 이게 위티털리 씨의 방식이었지.\n\n\"그렇게 하시죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. 그러고는 이렇게 갑작스레 떠나게 된 걸 다시 한번 황급히 사과하고는, 케이트를 마차에 서둘러 태웠어. 그리고 마부에게 시내로 한껏 속력을 내 달라고 일렀지.\n\n그렇게 두 사람은 삯마차가 낼 수 있는 최대한의 속력으로 시내로 향했어. 그런데 마침 그 말들이 화이트채플에 살았고, 아침을 먹기는 먹는 날이면 거기서 먹는 버릇이 있었던지라, 으레 기대할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 빠르게 길을 내달렸지.\n\n니콜라스는 자기보다 몇 분 먼저 케이트를 위층으로 올려보냈어. 자기가 불쑥 나타나 어머니를 놀라게 하지 않으려는 거였지. 그렇게 미리 길을 닦아 둔 뒤, 그는 효성스럽고 다정한 모습으로 어머니 앞에 나섰어. 뉴먼도 빈둥대고 있진 않았어. 문 앞에 작은 짐수레가 와 있었고, 세간이 벌써 부지런히 밖으로 옮겨지고 있었거든.\n\n그런데 니클비 부인은 무슨 일이든 급하게 들어서 알아듣는 사람이 못 됐어. 아니, 좀 미묘하거나 중요한 일을 짧은 새에 알아차리는 사람이 못 됐다고 하는 편이 맞겠지. 그래서 이 착한 부인은 라 크리비 양에게 꼬박 한 시간이나 미리 마음의 준비를 시켜 받았고, 이제 니콜라스와 딸한테서 더없이 또렷한 말로 설명을 듣고 있었는데도, 묘하게 어리둥절하고 혼란스러운 상태였어. 이렇게 서둘러 일을 처리해야 할 까닭을 도무지 알아듣지를 못했지.\n\n\"얘, 니콜라스, 네 삼촌한테 대체 무슨 속셈으로 이러는 건지 물어보지 그러니?\" 니클비 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"어머니.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"이야기로 풀 때는 지났어요. 이제 할 일은 딱 하나, 그자가 마땅히 받아야 할 경멸과 분노로 그를 내치는 거예요. 어머니의 명예와 좋은 이름을 위해서라도, 그 추악한 짓이 드러난 마당에 단 한 시간이라도 그자한테 신세를 져선 안 돼요. 이 텅 빈 벽 아래 몸을 누이는 것조차도요.\"\n\n\"그래, 맞아.\" 니클비 부인이 서럽게 울며 말했어. \"그 사람은 짐승이야, 괴물이고. 게다가 이 벽들도 어찌나 휑한지, 페인트칠도 다시 해야 하는데. 나는 이 천장을 일 실링 육 펜스나 들여 회칠을 해 놨잖니. 그 돈이 다 네 삼촌 주머니로 들어간 셈이라고 생각하면 어찌나 속이 상하는지. 도저히 못 믿겠다, 정말이지 못 믿겠어.\"\n\n\"저도요, 다른 누구라도 못 믿을 거예요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"세상에, 맙소사!\" 니클비 부인이 외쳤어. \"그 멀버리 호크 경이 라 크리비 양 말마따나 그렇게 못된 인간이라니, 얘 니콜라스. 나는 날마다 속으로 흐뭇해하고 있었잖니. 그 사람이 우리 사랑스러운 케이트를 흠모한다고. 그 사람이 우리 집안과 인연을 맺어서 그 인맥으로 너한테 돈벌이 되는 관직이라도 하나 얻어 주면 집안에 얼마나 좋은 일일까 하고 말이야. 궁정 쪽에는 좋은 자리가 많거든, 내가 알기로는. 우리가 아는 사람 하나가 (케이트야, 엑서터의 크롭리 양 말이다, 기억하지) 그런 자리를 하나 얻었었는데, 그 사람 직무라는 게 주로 비단 양말을 신고, 검은 시계 주머니처럼 생긴 가발을 쓰는 거였단다. 그런데 그게 결국 이렇게 되고 말다니. 아이고, 정말이지, 사람 죽이는 일이야, 그렇고말고!\" 이렇게 슬픔을 토해 내며 니클비 부인은 다시 설움이 북받쳐 가엾게 울었어.\n\n이때쯤 니콜라스와 누이는 몇 안 되는 세간을 옮기는 걸 감독해야 했어. 그래서 라 크리비 양이 부인을 위로하는 일을 맡아, 더없이 다정한 태도로, 정말이지 기운을 좀 내셔야 한다고 타일렀지.\n\n\"아이고, 그러게요, 라 크리비 양.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어. 이 딱한 처지에선 그럴 만도 한 짜증 섞인 말투였지. \"기운 내라는 말이야 하기 쉽죠. 그렇지만 댁이 나만큼 기운 내야 할 일을 겪어 봤다면— 그리고 참.\" 니클비 부인이 말을 뚝 멈췄어. \"파이크 씨하고 플럭 씨를 생각해 봐요, 세상에 둘도 없는 더없이 완벽한 신사 양반들인데, 내가 그분들한테 뭐라고 해야 하나요— 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠어요? 아니, 내가 그분들한테 '댁의 친구 멀버리 경이 비열한 인간이라더군요' 하고 말하면, 나를 비웃을 거 아니에요.\"\n\n\"이젠 우리를 비웃을 일도 없을 겁니다.\" 니콜라스가 다가서며 말했어. \"자, 어머니, 문 앞에 마차가 와 있어요. 어쨌든 월요일까지는, 우리 옛 거처로 돌아가는 거예요.\"\n\n\"—거기엔 모든 게 다 준비돼 있고, 게다가 진심 어린 환영까지 기다리고 있답니다.\" 라 크리비 양이 덧붙였어. \"자, 제가 모시고 아래층으로 내려갈게요.\"\n\n하지만 니클비 부인은 그렇게 쉽게 움직일 사람이 아니었어. 우선 위층에 올라가 빠뜨린 게 없는지 봐야겠다고 우겼고, 그다음엔 아래층으로 내려가 모든 게 다 옮겨졌는지 봐야겠다고 했지. 그리고 마차에 오르려는 참엔, 뒷부엌 화덕 선반에 두고 온 커피 주전자가 눈앞에 어른거렸어. 그리고 마차 문이 닫힌 뒤엔, 어딘지 모를 어떤 문 뒤에 놓인 초록색 우산이 처량하게 떠올랐지. 마침내 니콜라스는 완전히 절망에 빠진 채로 마부에게 출발하라고 일렀어. 그런데 마차가 갑자기 출발하면서 예기치 못하게 덜컥하는 바람에, 니클비 부인은 짚더미 속에 일 실링을 떨어뜨렸어. 그래서 다행히도 부인은 그 동전에만 정신이 팔려, 다른 걸 떠올리기엔 너무 늦어 버릴 때까지 마차 안에만 신경을 쏟았지.\n\n모든 짐을 무사히 내가고, 하녀를 내보내고, 문을 잠그고 나서, 니콜라스는 이륜마차에 뛰어올라 골든 스퀘어 근처의 외진 곳으로 갔어. 노그스와 거기서 만나기로 약속해 뒀거든. 모든 일이 어찌나 빨리 처리됐던지, 그가 약속 장소에 다다랐을 때는 겨우 아홉 시 반이었어.\n\n\"여기 랄프한테 보낼 편지예요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그리고 이건 열쇠고요. 오늘 저녁 나한테 올 땐, 어젯밤 일은 한마디도 하지 말아요. 나쁜 소식은 빨리 퍼지는 법이니, 곧 알게 되겠죠. 그자가 많이 다쳤는지 들은 거 있어요?\"\n\n뉴먼이 고개를 저었어.\n\n\"그건 내가 직접 지체 없이 알아볼게요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"좀 쉬는 게 좋겠어요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어. \"열이 나고 몸도 안 좋잖아요.\"\n\n니콜라스는 아무렇지 않게 손을 내저었어. 그리고 자기를 버티게 해 주던 흥분이 가시고 나니 실제로 느껴지는 몸의 불편함을 감추면서, 뉴먼 노그스에게 황급히 작별을 고하고 떠났어.\n\n뉴먼은 골든 스퀘어에서 걸어서 삼 분도 안 걸리는 거리에 있었어. 그런데 그 삼 분 동안, 그는 적어도 스무 번은 모자에서 편지를 꺼냈다 다시 넣었다 했지. 처음엔 앞면, 다음엔 뒷면, 그다음엔 옆면, 그다음엔 겉봉의 수신인, 그다음엔 봉인까지, 죄다 뉴먼이 감탄하며 들여다보는 대상이 됐어. 그러고는 한 번에 흐뭇하게 전체를 훑어보려는 듯 편지를 팔 끝까지 쭉 뻗어 들었지. 그러고는 자기가 맡은 이 임무에 완전히 황홀해져서 두 손을 비벼 댔어.\n\n뉴먼은 사무실에 도착해 모자를 늘 걸던 못에 걸고, 편지와 열쇠를 책상 위에 올려놓은 채, 랄프 니클비가 나타나기를 안달하며 기다렸어. 몇 분 뒤, 계단에서 익숙한 그의 부츠 삐걱대는 소리가 들렸고, 이어서 종이 울렸지.\n\n\"우편물 왔나?\"\n\"아니요.\"\n\n\"다른 편지는?\"\n\n\"하나요.\" 뉴먼이 그를 유심히 살피며 편지를 책상 위에 내려놓았어.\n\n\"이건 뭔가?\" 랄프가 열쇠를 집어 들며 물었어.\n\n\"편지랑 같이 맡겨진 겁니다. 어떤 아이가 가져왔어요. 십오 분쯤 전에, 그보다 덜 됐나요.\"\n\n랄프는 수신인을 흘끗 보고는 편지를 뜯어 이렇게 읽었어.\n\n\"이제 나는 네 정체를 안다. 내가 네 머리 위에 아무리 많은 비난을 퍼붓는다 해도, 이 한마디가 네 가슴속에까지 불러일으킬 그 비굴한 수치심의 천분의 일에도 미치지 못할 것이다.\n\n네 동생의 미망인과 그 고아 자식은 네 지붕 아래 깃드는 것을 마다하고, 역겨움과 혐오로 너를 멀리한다. 네 혈육은 너를 등진다. 이름으로나마 너와 그들을 얽어매는 핏줄, 그것 말고는 부끄러울 게 없는 자들이니까.\n\n너는 늙은이다. 나는 너를 무덤에 내버려 두겠다. 네 삶의 온갖 기억이 그 거짓된 가슴에 매달려, 네 임종 자리에 그 어둠을 드리우기를.\"\n\n랄프 니클비는 이 편지를 두 번 읽고는, 잔뜩 미간을 찌푸린 채 깊은 생각에 잠겼어. 종이가 손에서 팔랑거리며 바닥에 떨어졌지만, 그는 여전히 그걸 쥐고 있는 양 손가락을 맞쥐고 있었지.\n\n문득 그가 자리에서 벌떡 일어났어. 그리고 그것을 다 구겨 주머니에 쑤셔 넣고는, 뉴먼 노그스를 향해 사납게 돌아섰어. 왜 아직 꾸물대고 있느냐고 따지려는 듯이 말이야. 하지만 뉴먼은 그에게 등을 돌린 채 꿈쩍도 않고 서 있었어. 닳고 시커메진 헌 펜 토막으로, 벽에 붙여 둔 이자 계산표의 숫자 몇 개를 짚어 가면서, 다른 건 전혀 안중에도 없다는 듯이 말이지.\n\n# CHAPTER 34\n\n\"이놈의 다 깨진 고물 주전자 같은 종을 댕그랑거리느라 얼마나 오래 기다리게 한 건지, 그 댕그랑 소리 한 번 한 번이 멀쩡한 사내도 시퍼렇게 경련을 일으키게 만들 정도였다고, 정말이지, 빌어먹을!\" 맨탈리니 씨가 랄프 니클비네 흙털개에 부츠를 문지르면서 뉴먼 노그스한테 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"종소리는 한 번밖에 못 들었는데요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어.\n\n\"그렇다면 자네는 엄청나게, 어처구니없을 만큼 귀가 먹은 거야.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 말했어. \"빌어먹을 말뚝처럼 귀가 멀었다고.\"\n\n이때쯤 맨탈리니 씨는 복도까지 들어와서, 별다른 격식도 차리지 않고 랄프의 사무실 문 쪽으로 가고 있었어. 그러자 뉴먼이 제 몸으로 길을 막아섰지. 그러고는 니클비 씨가 방해받고 싶어 하지 않는다는 걸 넌지시 비추면서, 손님 용건이 급한 거냐고 물었어.\n\n\"엄청나게 빌어먹을 만큼 급한 일이지.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 말했어. \"더러운 종잇조각 몇 장을 환하게 빛나고 짤랑짤랑 울리는 빌어먹을 돈으로 녹여 내는 일이거든.\"\n\n뉴먼은 의미심장하게 끙 소리를 냈어. 그러고는 맨탈리니 씨가 내미는 명함을 받아 들고, 그걸 들고 주인 사무실로 절뚝거리며 들어갔지. 문틈으로 고개를 들이미니, 랄프가 조카 편지를 읽고 나서 빠져들었던 그 생각에 잠긴 자세로 다시 돌아가 있는 게 보였어. 게다가 편지를 또 펼쳐 든 걸 보니, 그걸 다시 읽고 있었던 모양이야. 하지만 그건 잠깐 본 것뿐이었어. 방해를 받은 랄프가 무슨 일로 끼어드냐고 따지려고 몸을 돌렸거든.\n\n뉴먼이 사정을 말하는 동안, 그 일을 만든 장본인이 거들먹거리며 방 안으로 들어왔어. 그러고는 유난스러운 애정을 담아 랄프의 거친 손을 덥석 잡더니, 평생 이렇게 좋아 보인 적이 없다고 떠벌렸지.\n\n\"자네 빌어먹을 얼굴에 아주 꽃이 피었구먼.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 권하지도 않은 자리에 앉으며, 제 머리와 구레나룻을 매만지면서 말했어. \"아주 젊어 보이고 쾌활해 보여, 빌어먹을!\"\n\n\"우리뿐이오.\" 랄프가 쌀쌀맞게 대꾸했어. \"나한테 뭘 바라고 온 거요?\"\n\n\"좋아!\" 맨탈리니 씨가 이를 드러내며 외쳤어. \"내가 뭘 바라느냐고! 그래. 하, 하! 아주 좋아. 내가 뭘 바라느냐. 하, 하. 아이고 빌어먹을!\"\n\n\"뭘 원하는 거요, 이 사람아?\" 랄프가 엄하게 다그쳤어.\n\n\"빌어먹을 어음 할인이지.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 씩 웃으며, 익살스럽게 고개를 저으며 대답했어.\n\n\"돈이 귀해요.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"빌어먹게 귀하니까 나도 필요한 거 아닌가.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 끼어들었어.\n\n\"요즘 경기가 나빠서, 누굴 믿어야 할지 도통 모르겠소.\" 랄프가 말을 이었어. \"지금은 거래를 하고 싶지 않은데, 사실 안 하는 게 낫겠소. 하지만 자네는 친구니까 말이오. 어음이 몇 장이오?\"\n\n\"두 장.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 대답했어.\n\n\"전부 얼마요?\"\n\n\"빌어먹을 푼돈이지. 칠십오 파운드.\"\n\n\"날짜는?\"\n\n\"두 달짜리하고 넉 달짜리.\"\n\n\"자네 거라면 해 주지. 명심하시오, 자네 거니까. 다른 사람한테는 안 해 줘. 이십오 파운드에 해 주겠소.\" 랄프가 천천히 말했어.\n\n\"아이고 빌어먹을!\" 맨탈리니 씨가 외쳤어. 이 후한 제안에 그의 얼굴이 잔뜩 길어졌지.\n\n\"왜, 그래도 자네 손에 오십 파운드가 남잖소.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어. \"뭘 더 바라는 거요? 이름을 좀 봅시다.\"\n\n\"자넨 정말 빌어먹게 야박해, 니클비.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 따졌어.\n\n\"이름을 좀 보자니까.\" 랄프가 조바심을 내며 어음을 달라고 손을 내밀었어. \"음! 확실하진 않지만, 그런대로 안전하구먼. 조건에 동의하겠소, 돈을 받겠소? 나야 자네가 그러길 바라지 않소. 안 하는 편이 낫지.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을, 니클비, 자네 좀—\" 맨탈리니 씨가 입을 열었어.\n\n\"안 돼.\" 랄프가 말을 끊었어. \"안 된다고. 돈을 받겠소? 현금이오, 명심하시오. 미루지 말고, 시내로 나가서 있지도 않은, 애초에 있지도 않았던 딴 사람하고 흥정하는 척하지도 말고. 거래하겠소, 안 하겠소?\"\n\n랄프는 말하면서 서류 몇 장을 옆으로 밀쳐 놓고, 그저 우연인 척 무심하게 돈궤를 달그락거렸어. 그 소리에 맨탈리니 씨는 견딜 수가 없었지. 그 소리가 귀에 닿자마자 그는 바로 거래에 응했고, 랄프는 탁자 위에 돈을 세어 놓았어.\n\n랄프가 돈을 다 세어 놓기가 무섭게, 그리고 맨탈리니 씨가 그걸 다 그러모으기도 전에, 종소리가 울렸어. 그리고 곧이어 뉴먼이 다름 아닌 맨탈리니 부인을 모셔 들였지. 그 모습을 보자 맨탈리니 씨는 꽤나 당황한 기색을 보이며, 놀랄 만큼 잽싸게 돈을 호주머니에 쓸어 넣었어.\n\n\"아, 여기 계셨군요.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 고개를 홱 젖히며 말했어.\n\n\"그래, 내 목숨이자 영혼이여, 여기 있소.\" 남편이 무릎을 꿇으며, 새끼 고양이처럼 장난스럽게 굴러다니던 금화 한 닢을 덮치며 대답했어. \"여기 있소, 내 영혼의 기쁨이여, 톰 티들러의 땅에서 빌어먹을 금붙이 은붙이를 줍고 있다오.\"\n\n\"당신이 부끄럽군요.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 잔뜩 화가 나서 말했어.\n\n\"부끄럽다니, 내가? 내 기쁨이여? 빌어먹게 매혹적이고 달콤한 소리를 하고 있구려, 그런데 그건 못된 거짓말이지.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 대답했어. \"당신은 당신의 그 귀여운 임을 부끄러워하지 않으면서.\"\n\n이런 결과를 빚어낸 사정이 무엇이든 간에, 그 귀여운 임이 적어도 이번만큼은 부인의 애정 크기를 잘못 가늠한 게 분명해 보였어. 맨탈리니 부인은 대답 대신 경멸하는 표정만 지었거든. 그러고는 랄프 쪽으로 돌아서서, 갑작스레 들이닥친 걸 용서해 달라고 청했지.\n\n\"이게 다,\" 부인이 말했어. \"맨탈리니 씨의 형편없는 비행과 아주 부적절한 행동 탓이랍니다.\"\n\n\"내가, 내 둘도 없는 파인애플 즙이여!\"\n\n\"당신이요.\" 아내가 대꾸했어. \"하지만 난 더는 못 참아요. 어떤 남자의 사치와 방탕 때문에 거덜 나는 꼴은 안 당하겠어요. 내가 당신한테 하려는 일을 니클비 씨를 증인 삼아 말하겠어요.\"\n\n\"제발 저를 무슨 증인이니 하는 데 끌어들이지 마십시오, 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"두 분이 알아서 해결하세요, 두 분끼리 알아서요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 그래도 부탁 좀 드릴게요.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 말했어. \"제가 이 사람한테 단단히 마음먹은 일을 통보하는 걸 들어 주세요. 제 단단한 결심을요, 선생님.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 남편에게 화난 눈길을 쏘아 보내며 거듭 말했어.\n\n\"이 사람이 나한테 '선생님'이라고 부른 거요?\" 맨탈리니가 외쳤어. \"빌어먹게 뜨거운 정성으로 이 여자를 떠받드는 나한테! 순결한 천사 같은 방울뱀처럼 제 매력을 나한테 칭칭 휘감는 이 여자가! 이러다 내 감정이 다 거덜 나겠어. 이 여자가 나를 빌어먹을 지경으로 몰아넣고 있어.\"\n\n\"감정 얘기는 하지 마세요, 선생님.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 자리에 앉아 그에게 등을 돌리며 받아쳤어. \"당신은 내 감정은 안 헤아리잖아요.\"\n\n\"내가 당신 감정을 안 헤아린다고, 내 영혼이여!\" 맨탈리니 씨가 외쳤어.\n\n\"네.\" 아내가 대답했어.\n\n그리고 맨탈리니 씨가 갖은 아양을 떨었는데도, 맨탈리니 부인은 여전히 아니라고 했어. 게다가 어찌나 단호하고 결연하게 골을 내며 말하던지, 맨탈리니 씨는 영락없이 당황해 버렸지.\n\n\"이 사람 사치 말이에요, 니클비 씨.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 랄프한테 말을 건넸어. 랄프는 안락의자에 등을 기대고 두 손을 뒤로 한 채, 더없이 깊고 노골적인 경멸의 미소를 띠며 이 다정한 부부를 바라보고 있었지. \"이 사람 사치는 한도 끝도 없답니다.\"\n\n\"그럴 줄은 미처 몰랐군요.\" 랄프가 비꼬듯 대답했어.\n\n\"하지만 정말이라니까요, 니클비 씨.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 받았어. \"그것 때문에 내가 비참해 죽겠어요! 늘 마음을 졸이고, 늘 쪼들리고요. 게다가 이건,\" 맨탈리니 부인이 눈물을 닦으며 말했어. \"이건 최악도 아니에요. 오늘 아침엔 내 허락도 없이 내 책상에서 값나가는 서류 몇 장을 꺼내 갔다니까요.\"\n\n맨탈리니 씨가 가볍게 끙 소리를 내며, 바지 호주머니 단추를 잠갔어.\n\n\"우리가 요전에 겪은 불운 이후로,\" 맨탈리니 부인이 말을 이었어. \"낵 양한테 가게에 이름을 올려 둔 대가로 큰돈을 줘야만 해요. 그러니 정말이지 이 사람 낭비벽을 부추길 여유가 없어요. 니클비 씨, 이 사람이 제가 말한 그 서류를 돈으로 바꾸려고 곧장 여기로 왔을 게 틀림없으니, 그리고 전에도 우리를 자주 도와주셨고 이런 일에 우리하고 아주 깊이 얽혀 계시니, 이 사람 행실 때문에 제가 어쩔 수 없이 내린 결심을 좀 알아 주셨으면 해요.\"\n\n맨탈리니 씨가 아내 보닛 뒤에서 또 한 번 끙 소리를 냈어. 그러고는 한쪽 눈에 금화 한 닢을 끼워 넣고, 다른 쪽 눈으로 랄프한테 윙크를 했지. 이 묘기를 아주 솜씨 좋게 해치우고 나서, 그 동전을 호주머니에 휙 집어넣고는, 한층 더 뉘우치는 척하며 또 끙 소리를 냈어.\n\n\"마음을 굳혔어요.\" 랄프의 얼굴에 조바심이 드러나기 시작하자 맨탈리니 부인이 말했어. \"이 사람한테 생활비를 정해 주기로요.\"\n\n\"무얼 한다고, 내 기쁨이여?\" 맨탈리니 씨가 그 말을 못 알아들은 듯 물었어.\n\n\"이 사람한테,\" 맨탈리니 부인이 랄프를 바라보며 말했어. 남편의 그 숱한 매력에 결심이 흔들릴까 봐, 신중하게 남편 쪽으로는 눈길조차 주지 않았지. \"이 사람한테 정해진 생활비를 주려고요. 옷값하고 용돈으로 한 해에 백이십 파운드를 받는다면, 이 사람은 자기가 아주 운 좋은 사람인 줄 알아야 할 거예요.\"\n\n맨탈리니 씨는 정해 줄 생활비 액수를 들으려고 아주 점잖게 기다렸어. 그런데 그 액수가 귀에 들어오자, 모자와 지팡이를 바닥에 내던지고는, 손수건을 꺼내 처량하게 신음하며 감정을 쏟아냈지.\n\n\"빌어먹을!\" 맨탈리니 씨가 외쳤어. 그러고는 갑자기 의자에서 펄쩍 뛰어올랐다가, 또 그만큼 갑자기 도로 주저앉았어. 부인의 신경을 잔뜩 곤두서게 하면서 말이지. \"아니, 아니야. 이건 빌어먹을 끔찍한 꿈이야. 현실이 아니라고. 아니야!\"\n\n이 다짐으로 스스로를 위로하며, 맨탈리니 씨는 눈을 감고는 깨어날 때까지 참을성 있게 기다렸어.\n\n\"아주 현명한 처사군요.\" 랄프가 비웃으며 말했어. \"남편이 그 안에서 산다면 말입니다, 부인. 뭐, 틀림없이 그러겠지요만.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을!\" 맨탈리니 씨가 랄프의 목소리에 눈을 뜨며 외쳤어. \"이건 끔찍한 현실이야. 그녀가 내 앞에 앉아 있어. 저기 그녀의 우아한 몸매 윤곽이 있잖아. 잘못 볼 리가 없지. 저런 건 어디에도 없으니까. 그 두 백작 부인은 윤곽이라곤 아예 없었고, 그 노부인 윤곽은 빌어먹을 윤곽이었어. 어째서 그녀는 이토록 사무치게 아름다워서, 지금 이 순간에도 화를 낼 수가 없는 거지?\"\n\n\"당신이 자초한 일이에요, 앨프리드.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 대답했어. 여전히 나무라는 투였지만, 한결 누그러진 목소리였지.\n\n\"난 빌어먹을 악당이야!\" 맨탈리니 씨가 제 머리를 치며 외쳤어. \"호주머니를 금화 한 닢어치 반 페니 동전으로 가득 채우고 템스강에 빠져 죽어 버릴 테야. 하지만 그래도 그녀한테 화는 안 낼 거야. 가는 길에 이 페니짜리 우편으로 쪽지를 부쳐서, 시체가 어디 있는지 알려 줄 테니까. 그녀는 사랑스러운 과부가 되겠지. 나는 시체가 되고. 어떤 아리따운 여자들은 울 거야. 그녀는 빌어먹게 웃어 댈 테고.\"\n\n\"앨프리드, 당신은 잔인해요, 너무 잔인한 사람이에요.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 그 끔찍한 그림에 흐느끼며 말했어.\n\n\"그녀가 날 잔인하다고 하는군. 나를, 이 나를, 그녀를 위해서 빌어먹게 축축하고 눅눅한 시체가 되려는 이 나를!\" 맨탈리니 씨가 외쳤어.\n\n\"그런 얘기를 듣는 것만으로도 내 가슴이 거의 찢어지는 거 알잖아요.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 대답했어.\n\n\"내가 의심받으며 살 수 있겠어?\" 남편이 외쳤어. \"내 심장을 빌어먹게 엄청나게 많은 조각으로 잘라서, 하나씩 하나씩 죄다 그 작고 사람 홀리는 빌어먹을 매혹덩어리한테 줘 버린 내가, 그녀한테 의심받으며 살 수 있겠냐고? 빌어먹을, 아니, 난 못 살아.\"\n\n\"제가 말한 액수가 적당한지 니클비 씨한테 물어봐요.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 따지듯 말했어.\n\n\"난 어떤 액수도 바라지 않아.\" 풀이 죽은 남편이 대답했어. \"빌어먹을 생활비 따위 필요 없어. 난 시체가 될 거야.\"\n\n맨탈리니 씨가 이 끔찍한 협박을 또 되풀이하자, 맨탈리니 부인은 두 손을 비틀며 랄프 니클비한테 좀 말려 달라고 애원했어. 그러고는 한참 동안 눈물과 입씨름이 오가고, 맨탈리니 씨가 당장 제 몸에 해를 가하려는 듯 문 쪽으로 가려고 몇 번이나 시도한 끝에, 그 신사는 가까스로 시체가 되지 않겠다는 약속을 하도록 설득당했지. 이 큰 고비를 넘기자, 맨탈리니 부인은 생활비 문제를 따졌고, 맨탈리니 씨도 마찬가지로 따졌어. 그러면서 자기는 빵과 물만으로도 더없이 만족스럽게 살 수 있고 누더기를 걸치고도 지낼 수 있지만, 가장 헌신적이고 사심 없는 애정의 대상한테 의심받는 짐까지 더해서는 도저히 삶을 견딜 수 없다는 걸 보여 줄 기회로 삼았지. 이 말에 맨탈리니 부인의 눈에 다시 눈물이 고였어. 그녀의 눈은 맨탈리니 씨의 흠 몇 가지에 이제 막 뜨이기 시작한 참이라, 아주 조금밖에 안 떠진 데다 쉽게 도로 감겨 버릴 수 있었거든. 그 결과, 맨탈리니 부인은 생활비 문제를 완전히 접지는 않았지만 더 따지는 건 뒤로 미뤘어. 그리고 랄프는 똑똑히 알아챘지. 맨탈리니 씨가 그 편안한 삶을 새로 연장받았고, 적어도 한동안은 그의 몰락과 추락이 미뤄졌다는 걸 말이야.\n\n'하지만 머지않아 닥치고 말걸.' 랄프는 생각했어. '모든 사랑이란 — 흥! 내가 어린것들 입에서나 나올 소리를 하다니 — 다 덧없는 거지. 하긴 저 개코원숭이 같은 구레나룻 얼굴에 대한 감탄에 뿌리를 둔 사랑은, 아마 가장 오래 가긴 할 거야. 더 깊은 눈멂에서 시작해서 허영으로 길러지니까. 어쨌든 그 바보들이 내 방앗간에 곡식을 갖다 주는 셈이니, 멋대로 살게 두자. 길면 길수록 좋지.'\n\n이런 흐뭇한 생각이 랄프 니클비의 머릿속에 떠올랐어. 그가 생각하던 그 두 사람이, 안 보이는 줄 알고 자잘하게 쓰다듬고 어루만지며 정을 나누는 동안 말이지.\n\n\"니클비 씨한테 더 하실 말씀이 없으면, 여보,\" 맨탈리니 부인이 말했어. \"우린 그만 가요. 분명 너무 오래 붙잡아 둔 것 같으니까요.\"\n\n맨탈리니 씨는 우선 맨탈리니 부인의 코를 몇 번 톡톡 두드리는 걸로 대답을 대신했어. 그러고는 말로 더 할 얘기는 없다고 했지.\n\n\"빌어먹을! 그래도 있긴 있어.\" 그가 거의 곧바로 덧붙이며, 랄프를 구석으로 끌고 갔어. \"자네 친구 멀버리 경 일이야. 이런 빌어먹게 별나고 희한한 일이 또 없을 거야, 응?\"\n\n\"무슨 소리요?\" 랄프가 물었어.\n\n\"자네 모르나, 빌어먹을?\" 맨탈리니 씨가 물었어.\n\n\"신문에서 봤소만, 그가 어젯밤 이륜마차에서 떨어져서 크게 다쳤고, 목숨이 좀 위태롭다고 하더군.\" 랄프가 아주 침착하게 대답했어. \"하지만 거기 무슨 별난 게 있다는 건지 모르겠소. 사람이 험하게 살고 저녁 먹고 마차를 몰면, 사고란 기적 같은 일이 아니잖소.\"\n\n\"휘유!\" 맨탈리니 씨가 길고 날카롭게 휘파람을 불며 외쳤어. \"그럼 어쩌다 그렇게 됐는지는 모르나?\"\n\n\"방금 내가 짐작한 대로가 아니라면 모르지.\" 랄프가 무심하게 어깨를 으쓱하며 대답했어. 그 일에 도통 궁금한 게 없다는 걸 묻는 사람한테 알려 주려는 듯이 말이야.\n\n\"빌어먹을, 자네 정말 날 놀라게 하는군.\" 맨탈리니가 외쳤어.\n\n랄프는 다시 어깨를 으쓱했어. 맨탈리니 씨를 놀라게 하는 게 무슨 대단한 재주냐는 듯이 말이지. 그러고는 뉴먼 노그스의 얼굴을 아쉬운 듯 흘끔 쳐다봤어. 그 얼굴은 사무실 문에 끼운 유리 두 장 너머로 몇 번이나 나타났거든. 시시한 사람들이 찾아오면, 그들을 배웅하라고 종이 울린 줄로 착각하는 척하는 게 뉴먼의 일거리 가운데 하나였어. 그런 손님들한테 이제 갈 시간이라는 걸 넌지시 일러 주는 셈이었지.\n\n\"자네 모르나,\" 맨탈리니 씨가 랄프의 단추를 잡으며 말했어. \"그게 사고가 전혀 아니라, 자네 조카가 그 사람한테 가한 빌어먹게 사납고 사람 잡을 듯한 공격이었다는 걸?\"\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 랄프가 두 주먹을 불끈 쥐고 새파랗게 질리며 으르렁댔어.\n\n\"빌어먹을, 니클비, 자네도 그 사람만큼이나 대단한 호랑이로군.\" 맨탈리니가 이 기세에 놀라서 말했어.\n\n\"계속해 보시오.\" 랄프가 외쳤어. \"무슨 소린지 말해 보란 말이오. 이게 무슨 얘기요? 누가 그러던가? 말하시오.\" 랄프가 으르렁댔어. \"내 말 안 들리오?\"\n\n\"이런, 니클비.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 아내 쪽으로 물러서며 말했어. \"자넨 정말 빌어먹게 사나운 악령 영감이로군! 이 여자의 그 가냘프고 사랑스러운 정신줄을 다 놀라 날아가게 할 판이야. 단번에 이렇게 활활 타오르고 미쳐 날뛰는, 전에 없던 격분으로 치닫다니, 빌어먹을!\"\n\n\"쳇.\" 랄프가 억지웃음을 지으며 받아쳤어. \"그저 버릇일 뿐이오.\"\n\n\"그거 참 빌어먹게 거북한, 사설 정신병원 같은 버릇이로군.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 지팡이를 주워 들며 말했어.\n\n랄프는 웃는 척하면서, 맨탈리니 씨가 그 얘기를 누구한테 들었는지 다시 한번 물었어.\n\n\"파이크한테 들었지. 빌어먹게 멋지고 유쾌하고 신사다운 친구야.\" 맨탈리니가 대답했어. \"빌어먹게 즐거운 데다, 일류 멋쟁이고.\"\n\n\"그 사람이 뭐라던가?\" 랄프가 미간을 찌푸리며 물었어.\n\n\"이렇게 됐다는군. 자네 조카가 커피집에서 그 사람을 만나, 빌어먹게 사납게 덤벼들었다는 거야. 그러고는 마차까지 따라가서, 말 등에 올라타든 말 꼬리에 매달리든 같이 집까지 타고 가겠다고 우겼고. 그러다 그 얼굴을 박살 냈다더군. 본래는 빌어먹게 멋진 얼굴인데 말이지. 그러고 말을 놀라게 해서, 멀버리 경하고 저 자신을 마차 밖으로 내동댕이쳐 버리고, 그래서—\"\n\n\"그래서 죽었소?\" 랄프가 눈을 번득이며 끼어들었어. \"그랬소? 그가 죽었소?\"\n\n맨탈리니가 고개를 저었어.\n\n\"으.\" 랄프가 몸을 돌리며 말했어. \"그럼 아무 일도 안 한 거나 마찬가지군. 잠깐.\" 그가 다시 돌아보며 덧붙였어. \"다리나 팔을 부러뜨렸소, 아니면 어깨가 빠졌소, 아니면 쇄골이 부러졌소, 아니면 갈비뼈 한두 대가 으스러졌소? 목은 교수대용으로 무사히 건졌지만, 그 대가로 아프고 더디게 낫는 상처라도 입었소? 그랬소? 그 정도는 들었을 거 아니오.\"\n\n\"아니.\" 맨탈리니가 다시 고개를 저으며 대꾸했어. \"산산조각이 나서 가루처럼 날아가 버리기라도 했다면 모를까, 다친 데는 없어. 아주 조용하고 편안하게 떠났거든. 마치, 마치, 마치 빌어먹게 말이야.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 비유할 말을 못 찾아 더듬거리며 말했어.\n\n\"그런데,\" 랄프가 조금 머뭇거리며 말했어. \"싸움의 원인은 뭐였소?\"\n\n\"자넨 정말 빌어먹게 잘 아는 양반이야.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 감탄하는 투로 대답했어. \"가장 약삭빠르고, 가장 별나고, 가장 으뜸가는 늙은 여우라니까. 아이고 빌어먹을! 그게 그 작고 눈이 반짝이는 조카딸 때문인 걸 이제 와 모르는 척하다니. 그 더없이 보드랍고, 더없이 달콤하고, 더없이 어여쁜—\"\n\n\"앨프리드!\" 맨탈리니 부인이 끼어들었어.\n\n\"이 여자가 늘 옳지.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 달래듯 대꾸했어. \"이 여자가 갈 시간이라고 하면 갈 시간이고, 그러니 가야지. 그리고 이 여자가 제 튤립 같은 임하고 거리를 걸으면, 여자들은 부러워하며 말할 거야. 저 여자 빌어먹게 멋진 남편을 뒀다고. 그리고 남자들은 황홀해하며 말하겠지. 저 남자 빌어먹게 멋진 아내를 뒀다고. 그러면 둘 다 맞고 누구도 틀린 게 아니지, 내 목숨이자 영혼을 걸고. 아이고 빌어먹을!\"\n\n이런 말들을, 그리고 그에 못지않게 지적이고 핵심을 찌르는 말들을 잔뜩 늘어놓으며, 맨탈리니 씨는 장갑 낀 손가락에 입을 맞춰 랄프 니클비한테 인사를 보냈어. 그러고는 아내의 팔을 제 팔에 끼고, 점잔을 빼며 그녀를 데리고 나갔지.\n\n\"좋아, 좋아.\" 랄프가 의자에 털썩 주저앉으며 중얼거렸어. \"이 악마 같은 놈이 또 풀려나서, 타고난 팔자대로 사사건건 나를 가로막는군. 그놈이 언젠가 우리 사이에 결판을 낼 날이 올 거라고 했었지. 내가 그놈을 정확한 예언자로 만들어 주마. 그날은 틀림없이 오고 말 테니까.\"\n\n\"안에 계세요?\" 뉴먼이 불쑥 고개를 들이밀며 물었어.\n\n\"아니.\" 랄프가 똑같이 퉁명스럽게 대답했어.\n\n뉴먼은 고개를 도로 뺐다가, 다시 들이밀었어.\n\n\"안에 안 계신 거 확실하시죠, 그렇죠?\" 뉴먼이 말했어.\n\n\"이 멍청이가 무슨 소릴 하는 거야?\" 랄프가 짜증을 내며 외쳤어.\n\n\"저 사람들이 처음 들어왔을 때부터 거의 내내 기다리던 사람이 있어서요. 어쩌면 사장님 목소리를 들었을지도 모르고요. 그게 답니다.\" 뉴먼이 두 손을 비비며 말했어.\n\n\"누가?\" 랄프가 다그쳤어. 방금 들은 소식과 서기의 약 올리는 천연덕스러움에 짜증이 머리끝까지 치밀어 오른 참이었지.\n\n대답할 필요는 사라졌어. 뜻밖에도 세 번째 사람이 들어왔거든. 바로 그 문제의 인물이었지. 그는 외눈을 (그에겐 눈이 하나뿐이었으니까) 랄프 니클비한테 돌리고, 어기적어기적 인사를 잔뜩 하더니, 안락의자에 앉았어. 두 손을 무릎에 올리고서 말이야. 앉느라 끙끙대는 통에 짧은 검정 바지가 다리 위로 너무 높이 끌려 올라가서, 웰링턴 부츠 윗부분에도 겨우 닿을락 말락 했지.\n\n\"아니, 이거 놀랍구려!\" 랄프가 손님한테 시선을 모으며, 유심히 뜯어보면서 반쯤 미소 지었어. \"당신 얼굴, 알 것 같소만. 스퀴어스 씨.\"\n\n\"아!\" 그 양반이 대답했어. \"더 잘 알아보셨을 거요, 선생. 내가 겪은 그 모든 일만 없었어도 말이오. 저기 뒤 사무실 높은 걸상에서 저 꼬마 좀 내려놓고, 이리 들어오라고 해 주겠나, 이 사람아?\" 스퀴어스가 뉴먼한테 말했어. \"오, 자기가 알아서 내려왔구먼. 제 자식이오, 선생. 꼬마 왝퍼드라오. 어떻소, 선생, 두더보이즈 홀 먹성의 본보기로 말이오? 살이 쪄서 옷이 터지고 솔기가 뜯어지고, 단추가 다 튀어나갈 지경이지 않소? 이 살 좀 보쇼!\" 스퀴어스가 아이를 빙빙 돌리며, 통통한 부위마다 이리저리 쿡쿡 찌르고 쥐어박으며 외쳤어. 제 아들이자 후계자가 잔뜩 곤혹스러워하는데도 말이지. \"이 단단함 좀 보쇼, 이 야무짐 좀 보쇼! 글쎄, 손가락하고 엄지로 꼬집을 살을 어디서도 집어 올릴 수가 없을 정도라니까.\"\n\n왝퍼드 도련님 몸 상태가 아무리 좋았다 한들, 그렇게 놀랍도록 야무지지는 분명 못했어. 아버지가 그 말을 증명하려고 손가락하고 엄지를 오므리자, 아이가 날카롭게 비명을 지르며 더없이 자연스럽게 그 자리를 문질렀거든.\n\n\"음.\" 스퀴어스가 조금 머쓱해져서 말했어. \"이번엔 잘못 집었구먼. 그치만 그건 오늘 아침에 일찍 먹어서 그래. 아직 점심을 안 먹었거든. 글쎄, 이 녀석이 저녁만 먹었다 하면 문틈에 살점 하나 끼울 수가 없다오. 저 눈물 좀 보쇼, 선생.\" 왝퍼드 도련님이 윗옷 소매로 눈물을 닦자, 스퀴어스가 의기양양하게 말했어. \"기름기가 자르르하지 않소!\"\n\n\"정말 건강해 보이는군요.\" 랄프가 대답했어. 자기 나름의 속셈이 있어서, 이 교장 선생 비위를 맞추고 싶어 하는 눈치였지. \"그런데 스퀴어스 부인은 잘 계시오, 당신은 또 어떻고?\"\n\n\"스퀴어스 부인이야, 선생.\" 두더보이즈의 주인이 대답했어. \"늘 그대로라오. 그 애들한테 어머니 같은 사람이고, 그 사람을 아는 모든 이들한테 축복이자 위안이자 기쁨이지. 우리 애들 중 하나가, 그놈이 음식을 잔뜩 처먹고 곯아떨어졌거든. 그게 그놈들 버릇이지. 지난주에 종기가 났지 뭐요. 그걸 우리 부인이 주머니칼로 어떻게 처치했는지 봤어야 하는데! 아이고 맙소사!\" 스퀴어스가 한숨을 쉬며 고개를 몇 번이고 끄덕이며 말했어. \"그 사람이야말로 참 대단한 사회의 일원이라오!\"\n\n스퀴어스 씨는 한 십오 초쯤 지난 일을 회상하는 표정을 지었어. 부인의 그 훌륭함을 떠올리다 보니, 자연스레 마음이 요크셔 그레타 다리 근처 두더보이즈의 그 평화로운 마을로 흘러간 모양이었지. 그러고는 랄프를 쳐다봤어. 무슨 말이라도 해 주길 기다리는 듯이 말이야.\n\n\"그 악당 놈의 공격은 다 회복하셨소?\" 랄프가 물었어.\n\n\"이제 막 나았다오, 나은 거라면 말이지.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"온통 멍투성이였소, 선생.\" 스퀴어스가 먼저 머리뿌리를, 그다음 부츠 끝을 가리키며 말했어. \"여기서부터 저기까지 말이오. 식초하고 갈색 종이, 식초하고 갈색 종이를 아침부터 밤까지 붙였지. 처음부터 끝까지 갈색 종이를 반 연쯤은 내 몸에 붙였을 거요. 우리 집 부엌에 온통 종이를 바른 채 한 덩어리로 뻗어 있으니, 신음 소리만 잔뜩 들어찬 커다란 갈색 종이 꾸러미인 줄 알았을 거요. 내가 크게 신음했냐, 작게 신음했냐, 왝퍼드?\" 스퀴어스 씨가 아들한테 물었어.\n\n\"크게요.\" 왝퍼드가 대답했어.\n\n\"내가 그 끔찍한 꼴인 걸 보고 애들이 안타까워했냐, 왝퍼드, 아니면 좋아했냐?\" 스퀴어스 씨가 감상적인 투로 물었어.\n\n\"좋아—\"\n\n\"엉?\" 스퀴어스가 휙 돌아보며 외쳤어.\n\n\"안타까워했어요.\" 아들이 고쳐 말했어.\n\n\"오!\" 스퀴어스가 아이 귀싸대기를 호되게 한 대 후려치며 말했어. \"그럼 호주머니에서 손 빼고, 질문받으면 더듬거리지 마라. 신사분 사무실에서 시끄럽게 굴지 마, 안 그러면 식구들 버리고 도망쳐서 다시는 안 돌아올 거야. 그럼 자기 곁에 둘도 없는 친구도 없이 세상에 내팽개쳐질, 그 소중하고 가엾은 애들은 다 어떻게 되겠어?\"\n\n\"의사한테 진찰을 받아야 했소?\" 랄프가 물었어.\n\n\"아, 그랬지.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어. \"그 의사 양반이 청구서도 어마어마하게 들이밀었고. 그래도 내가 다 냈지만.\"\n\n랄프는 눈썹을 치켜올렸어. 동정으로도 놀라움으로도 받아들일 수 있는 그런 모양으로 말이야. 보는 사람 마음대로 받아들이라는 듯이.\n\n\"그래, 한 푼도 안 남기고 다 냈지.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. 자기가 상대하는 사람을 너무 잘 알아서, 이 문제를 어물쩍 넘긴다고 랄프가 비용을 보태 줄 리 없다는 걸 아는 눈치였지. \"그래도 결국 내 주머니에서 나간 건 없었지만.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"반 페니도 안 나갔지.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"실은 말이오, 우린 애들한테 추가로 받는 게 딱 하나뿐인데, 그게 필요할 때 부르는 의사 비용이거든. 그것도 손님한테 확실히 받아 낼 수 있을 때만 그렇고. 알아듣겠소?\"\n\n\"알겠소.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"아주 좋아.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어. \"그럼, 내 청구서가 쌓인 다음에, 우린 성홍열을 한 번도 안 앓은 꼬마 다섯을 골랐지. (돈 떼일 걱정 없는 영세 상인들 아들로 말이오.) 그러고 한 놈을 성홍열 걸린 집 오두막에 보냈더니, 그놈이 옮아 왔고, 그다음 나머지 넷을 그놈하고 같이 재웠더니, 그놈들도 옮았지. 그러고 의사가 와서 한꺼번에 다 봐 줬고, 우린 내 청구서 총액을 그놈들한테 나눠서, 각자 청구서에 얹었더니, 부모들이 다 냈지. 하, 하, 하!\"\n\n\"그것도 좋은 방법이군요.\" 랄프가 교장을 슬쩍 곁눈질하며 말했어.\n\n\"그럼요.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어. \"우린 늘 그렇게 하지. 글쎄, 스퀴어스 부인이 여기 이 꼬마 왝퍼드를 낳고 자리보전했을 때도, 백일해를 애들 대여섯한테 옮겨서, 산파까지 포함한 부인 비용을 그놈들한테 나눠 매겼다오. 하, 하, 하!\"\n\n랄프는 좀처럼 웃는 법이 없었지만, 이번만큼은 웃음에 최대한 가깝게 흉내를 냈어. 그러고는 스퀴어스 씨가 그 직업적인 농담을 실컷 즐길 때까지 기다렸다가, 무슨 일로 도시에 왔냐고 물었지.\n\n\"골치 아픈 법률 일 때문이오.\" 스퀴어스가 머리를 긁적이며 대답했어. \"그 사람들 말로 학생을 방치했다나 뭐라나, 그것 때문에 거는 소송하고 얽힌 일이지. 대체 뭘 어쩌라는 건지 모르겠소. 그 녀석은 우리 동네 어디 못지않게 좋은 풀밭을 누렸거든.\"\n\n랄프는 그 말을 잘 못 알아들은 듯한 표정을 지었어.\n\n\"풀밭 말이오.\" 스퀴어스가 목소리를 높이며 말했어. 랄프가 못 알아듣는 걸 보고, 귀가 먼 모양이라고 여겼거든. \"애가 약해지고 아프고 밥맛이 없어지면, 우린 식단을 바꿔 준다오. 하루에 한 시간쯤 이웃 순무밭에 내보내거나, 아니면 가끔, 좀 까다로운 경우엔 순무밭하고 당근밭을 번갈아 내보내서, 먹고 싶은 만큼 먹게 하지. 이 못된 녀석이 풀을 뜯은 데보다 더 좋은 땅은 이 나라에 없을 거요. 그런데도 가서 감기에 소화불량에 별걸 다 걸리더니, 그 녀석 친구들이 나한테 소송을 건단 말이오! 자, 설마 사람들 배은망덕이 그 지경까지 갈 줄은 미처 몰랐을 거요.\" 스퀴어스가 부당한 일을 당한 사람처럼 조바심을 내며 의자에서 몸을 들썩이며 덧붙였어. \"안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"참 딱한 일이군요.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"그렇게 말하는 게 딱 맞는 말이오.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"나만큼 아이들을 끔찍이 아끼는 사람이 또 있을 것 같진 않소. 지금 두더보이즈 홀엔 한 해에 팔백 파운드어치 아이들이 있다오. 천육백 파운드어치라도 데려올 수 있다면 데려와서, 그 한 명 한 명 이십 파운드짜리 하나하나를 무엇과도 견줄 수 없을 만큼 아껴 줄 텐데!\"\n\n\"전에 묵던 데 그대로요?\" 랄프가 물었어.\n\n\"그렇소, 사라센 여관에 있다오.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"이제 학기 끝날 날도 얼마 안 남았으니, 돈을 다 거둘 때까지, 또 새 학생들도 좀 받을 때까지 거기 계속 묵을 거요. 꼬마 왝퍼드를 일부러 데려온 것도 부모하고 보호자들한테 보여 주려고 그런 거지. 이번엔 광고에도 넣을 거요. 이 아이 좀 보쇼, 제 자신이 학생이오. 글쎄, 이 아이야말로 잘 먹인 기적이라니까!\"\n\n\"긴히 한마디 나누고 싶소만.\" 랄프가 말했어. 그는 한동안 말도 듣기도 기계적으로 해 왔고, 무슨 생각에 잠겨 있는 듯했지.\n\n\"얼마든지 말씀하시오, 선생.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어. \"왝퍼드, 너는 뒤 사무실에 가서 놀아라. 너무 많이 움직이진 말고, 안 그럼 살 빠진다, 그럼 안 되니까. 혹시 이 펜스 같은 거 가지고 계시오, 니클비 씨?\" 스퀴어스가 외투 호주머니 속 열쇠 꾸러미를 달그락거리며, 죄다 은화뿐이라고 우물거리며 말했어.\n\n\"있을, 거요, 아마.\" 랄프가 아주 천천히 말했어. 그러고는 낡은 서랍을 한참 뒤진 끝에, 일 페니짜리 하나에 반 페니짜리 하나, 사 분의 일 페니짜리 둘을 꺼냈어.\n\n\"고맙소.\" 스퀴어스가 그걸 아들한테 주며 말했어. \"자! 가서 타르트 하나 사 먹어라. 니클비 씨 일꾼이 어딘지 알려 줄 거야. 그리고 명심해라, 진한 걸로 사야 한다. 페이스트리는,\" 스퀴어스가 왝퍼드 도련님 뒤로 문을 닫으며 덧붙였어. \"이 녀석 살에 윤기를 꽤 내 주거든. 그럼 부모들은 그걸 건강한 표시로 여기지.\"\n\n이렇게 설명을 늘어놓고는, 그 말을 거들려고 유난히 의미심장한 표정을 지으며, 스퀴어스 씨는 의자를 옮겨서 랄프 니클비와 그리 멀지 않게 마주 보도록 했어. 그러고는 마음에 쏙 들게 자리를 잡고 앉았지.\n\n\"내 말 잘 들으시오.\" 랄프가 몸을 조금 앞으로 숙이며 말했어.\n\n스퀴어스가 고개를 끄덕였어.\n\n\"설마 당신이,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"당신한테 가해진 그 폭력이나, 그에 따른 망신을 그리 쉽게 용서하거나 잊을 멍청이라고는 생각하지 않소만?\"\n\n\"천만에.\" 스퀴어스가 쌀쌀맞게 대답했어.\n\n\"아니면 기회가 생기면 그걸 이자까지 쳐서 되갚아 줄 기회를 놓칠 사람도 아니고?\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"하나 보여 주고 시험해 보쇼.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"바로 그런 목적이 있어서 나를 찾아온 거 아니오?\" 랄프가 교장 얼굴로 눈을 들며 말했어.\n\n\"아, 아, 아니, 그건 잘 모르겠소.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"내 생각은, 당신이 보내 준 그 푼돈 말고도, 혹시 나한테 무슨 보상이라도 해 줄 능력이 있다면—\"\n\n\"아!\" 랄프가 그의 말을 끊으며 외쳤어. \"더 말 안 해도 되오.\"\n\n오랜 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 랄프는 생각에 잠긴 듯하다가, 이렇게 물으며 다시 침묵을 깼어.\n\n\"그놈이 데려간 그 애는 누구요?\"\n\n스퀴어스가 그 이름을 말했어.\n\n\"어렸소 나이가 들었소, 건강했소 병약했소, 고분고분했소 반항적이었소? 똑똑히 말해 보쇼, 이 사람아.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어.\n\n\"글쎄, 어리진 않았소.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"그러니까, 애치고는 어리진 않았단 말이오, 알겠소?\"\n\n\"그러니까, 애라고 할 수도 없었던 거로군?\" 랄프가 끼어들었어.\n\n\"음.\" 스퀴어스가 그 말에 안도한 듯 활기차게 대꾸했어. \"거의 스물쯤 됐을 거요. 그래도 모르는 사람들 눈엔 그렇게 늙어 보이진 않았소. 여기가 좀 모자랐거든.\" 그가 제 이마를 짚으며 말했어. \"아무리 두드려도 집에 아무도 없는 거지, 알겠소?\"\n\n\"그래서 당신이 꽤 자주 두드려 댔겠구먼, 내 짐작에?\" 랄프가 중얼거렸어.\n\n\"제법 그랬지.\" 스퀴어스가 씩 웃으며 대꾸했어.\n\n\"당신이 이 푼돈을, 당신 말마따나, 받았다고 확인하는 편지를 썼을 때,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그 애 친구들이 오래전에 버렸고, 그 애가 누군지 알아낼 단서나 흔적이라곤 털끝만큼도 없다고 했었지. 그게 사실이오?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 재수 없게도!\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. 랄프가 거리낌 없이 캐물을수록, 그의 태도도 점점 편하고 스스럼없어졌지. \"내 장부 기록으로 보면 십사 년 전 일이오. 어떤 낯선 남자가 어느 가을밤에 그 애를 우리 집에 데려와서 두고 갔지. 첫 분기 수업료로 오 파운드 오 실링을 선불로 내고 말이오. 그때 그 애는 대여섯 살쯤 됐을 거요. 그 이상은 아니고.\"\n\n\"그 애에 대해 더 아는 건 뭐요?\" 랄프가 다그쳤어.\n\n\"빌어먹게 별로 없소, 안타깝게도.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"수업료가 한 육칠 년쯤 들어오다가, 그러고 끊겼지. 이 사람이 런던 주소를 하나 주긴 했소만, 막상 따져 보니 당연히 아무도 그 사람을 아는 이가 없더군. 그래서 내가 그 녀석을, 그러니까, 그러니까—\"\n\n\"자선으로?\" 랄프가 무뚝뚝하게 거들었어.\n\n\"자선으로, 그렇지.\" 스퀴어스가 제 무릎을 문지르며 대꾸했어. \"그러다 그놈이 어떤 식으로든 좀 쓸모가 생길 만하니까, 그 니클비라는 젊은 악당 놈이 와서 데려가 버린 거요. 근데 이 일에서 제일 약 오르고 분통 터지는 건 말이오,\" 스퀴어스가 목소리를 낮추고 의자를 랄프 쪽으로 더 바짝 끌어당기며 말했어. \"마침내 그 애에 대해 이것저것 캐묻는 일이 생겼다는 거요. 나한테 직접 물은 건 아니고, 빙 둘러서 우리 마을 사람들한테 말이오. 그러니, 어쩌면 밀린 돈을 다 받아 낼 수 있었을 바로 그때, 어쩌면, 누가 알겠소? 우리 업계에선 전에도 이런 일이 있었거든. 그 애를 농부한테 보내거나 배에 태워 보내는 대가로 사례금까지 받을 수 있었을 텐데 말이오. 그 애가 우리 애들 상당수가 그렇듯 사생아라면, 다신 나타나서 제 부모 망신시키지 않게 말이오. 빌어먹을, 그 니클비라는 악당 놈이 대낮에 그 애 멱살을 잡아채서, 내 주머니에 노상강도질이나 다름없는 짓을 저지른 거요.\"\n\n\"우리 둘 다 머지않아 그놈하고 셈을 다 치르게 될 거요.\" 랄프가 요크셔 교장의 팔에 손을 얹으며 말했어.\n\n\"셈을 치른다!\" 스퀴어스가 따라 외쳤어. \"아! 나야 그놈 앞으로 작은 빚을 좀 남겨 두고 싶소만. 그놈이 갚을 수 있을 때 받게 말이오. 그저 우리 스퀴어스 부인이 그놈을 붙잡았으면 좋겠소. 아이고, 그 사람! 그놈을 죽여 버릴 거요, 니클비 씨. 그러고말고, 제 저녁밥 먹듯 거뜬히 말이오.\"\n\n\"이 얘긴 다음에 다시 합시다.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"생각할 시간이 좀 필요하니까. 그놈을 그놈 자신의 정과 환상을 통해 찌르자면—. 만약 내가 이 애를 통해 그놈을 칠 수만 있다면—\"\n\n\"맘대로 치쇼, 선생.\" 스퀴어스가 끼어들었어. \"그저 충분히 세게만 치쇼, 그게 다요. 그럼 이만 작별 인사 하겠소. 여기!—저기 구석 못에 걸린 저 꼬마 모자 좀 휙 던져 주고, 걸상에서 좀 내려놔 주겠나?\"\n\n뉴먼 노그스한테 이런 부탁을 큰 소리로 외치며, 스퀴어스 씨는 작은 뒤 사무실로 갔어. 그러고는 아비 된 마음으로 정성스레 제 아이한테 모자를 씌워 줬지. 그러는 동안 뉴먼은 펜을 귀에 꽂은 채, 걸상에 뻣뻣하게 꼼짝 않고 앉아서, 그 아비와 아들을 번갈아 빤히 쳐다보고 있었어.\n\n\"잘생긴 녀석이지, 안 그렇소?\" 스퀴어스가 고개를 살짝 한쪽으로 젖히고, 꼬마 왝퍼드의 균형을 더 잘 가늠하려고 책상 쪽으로 물러서며 말했어.\n\n\"아주요.\" 뉴먼이 말했어.\n\n\"제법 살이 통통하게 올랐지, 안 그렇소?\" 스퀴어스가 이어 말했어. \"이 녀석은 애 스무 명 살이 다 붙었다오.\"\n\n\"아!\" 뉴먼이 갑자기 제 얼굴을 스퀴어스 얼굴에 들이밀며 대꾸했어. \"그렇구먼. 스무 명 살이! 더 되겠어! 다 이 녀석이 가져갔구먼. 다른 애들은 하느님이 도우셔야지. 하! 하! 아이고 맙소사!\"\n\n이렇게 두서없는 말을 내뱉고는, 뉴먼이 책상에 엎드려 놀랄 만큼 빠른 속도로 글을 쓰기 시작했어.\n\n\"아니, 이 사람 무슨 소릴 하는 거요?\" 스퀴어스가 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤어. \"취했나?\"\n\n뉴먼은 아무 대답도 안 했어.\n\n\"미쳤나?\" 스퀴어스가 말했어.\n\n하지만 뉴먼은 여전히 제 자신 말고 누가 있다는 걸 전혀 알아채는 기색이 없었어. 그래서 스퀴어스 씨는 이 사람이 취한 데다 미치기까지 했다고 혼잣말하며 스스로를 달랬지. 그러고는 이 작별의 한마디를 남기고, 그 장래가 촉망되는 아들을 데리고 나갔어.\n\n케이트를 향한 마음이 안간힘을 쓰며 잔잔히 남아 있다는 걸 랄프 니클비가 의식하게 된 만큼, 꼭 그만큼 니콜라스를 향한 미움도 커졌어. 한 사람한테 마음이 기우는 그 약함을 만회하려고, 다른 누군가를 전보다 더 지독하게 미워해야 한다고 여긴 건지도 몰라. 어쨌든 그의 감정은 그렇게 흘러왔지. 그런데 이제, 도전받고 멸시당하고, 케이트 앞에서 가장 추악하고 역겨운 빛깔로 그려지고, 그녀가 자기를 미워하고 경멸하도록 배웠다는 걸 알게 되니, 자기 손길엔 병균이 있고 자기와 함께 있는 것만으로도 오염된다고 케이트가 느낀다는 걸 알게 되니, 이 모든 걸 알게 되고, 게다가 이 모든 일을 꾸민 장본인이 바로 그 애송이 가난뱅이 친척이라는 걸, 처음 만난 자리에서부터 자기를 비웃고, 그 뒤로도 드러내 놓고 자기한테 맞서고 대든 바로 그놈이라는 걸 알게 되니, 그의 조용하고 은밀한 악의는 어떤 지경까지 치달았어. 당장 보복할 길만 보였다면, 그걸 채우려고 무슨 위험이든 무릅쓰지 않았을 게 거의 없을 정도였지.\n\n하지만 니콜라스한테는 다행스럽게도, 랄프 니클비는 그 길을 못 봤어. 그날 하루 종일 궁리하고, 닥쳐오는 온갖 계략과 일거리 와중에도 머리 한구석을 그 한 가지 고민거리에 쉬지 않고 굴렸는데도, 밤이 되어서야 결국 그는 여전히 같은 주제를 곱씹으며, 여전히 그 부질없는 생각만 좇고 있었어.\n\n\"내 동생이 저놈만 할 때,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"우리 둘을 처음으로 견주기 시작했지. 늘 나한테 불리하게 말이야. 그 애는 트인 데다 너그럽고, 씩씩하고 쾌활했어. 나는 차갑고 고인 피가 흐르는 교활한 구두쇠였고, 아끼는 것 말곤 정열이라곤 없고, 돈 욕심 말곤 패기도 없었지. 처음 이 애송이를 봤을 때 그 일이 똑똑히 떠올랐는데, 지금은 더더욱 또렷이 떠오르는군.\"\n\n그는 니콜라스의 편지를 갈기갈기 찢고 있었어. 그러고는 말하면서, 그걸 자기 둘레에 자잘한 종잇조각 비처럼 흩뿌렸지.\n\n\"이런 기억들이,\" 랄프가 쓴웃음을 지으며 말을 이었어. \"내가 거기에 나를 내맡길 때면, 떼 지어, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 데서 몰려들어. 세상 사람들 일부가 돈의 힘을 깔보는 척하니, 내가 그게 어떤 건지 보여 줘야겠어.\"\n\n그리고 이때쯤엔 잠들기 좋은 흐뭇한 기분이 되어서, 랄프 니클비는 잠자리에 들었어.\n\n# CHAPTER 35\n\n어머니와 누이를 그 마음씨 고운 세밀화가의 집에 자리 잡게 하고, 멀버리 호크 경이 목숨을 잃을 위험은 없다는 걸 확인하고 나서, 니콜라스는 가엾은 스마이크에게 생각을 돌렸어. 스마이크는 뉴먼 노그스와 아침을 먹고 나서도 그 착한 사람의 하숙집에 풀이 죽은 채 머물러 있었어. 자기를 지켜 주는 사람의 소식을 더 듣고 싶어서 안절부절못하며 기다리고 있었지.\n\n'우리가 어디서 살든, 앞으로 어떤 운명이 기다리든, 저 친구는 우리 작은 식구 가운데 하나가 될 거야.' 니콜라스는 생각했어. '그러니 저 가엾은 친구를 제대로 소개해 줘야지. 식구들은 그 자체로 저 친구한테 잘해 줄 거야. 그게 안 되면, 그 이유만으로 내가 바라는 만큼 다 못 해 준다 해도, 나를 봐서라도 한 발짝 양보해 줄 거야. 틀림없어.'\n\n니콜라스는 '식구들'이라고 말했지만, 사실 마음에 걸리는 사람은 한 명뿐이었어. 케이트는 확신했지. 하지만 어머니의 별난 성격을 알고 있었거든. 그래서 스마이크가 니클비 부인의 눈에 들지 어떨지는 그리 자신할 수가 없었어.\n\n'그래도,' 자비로운 심부름을 떠나면서 니콜라스는 생각했어. '저 친구가 얼마나 헌신적인지 알면 어머니도 정이 안 들 리가 없지. 그리고 그걸 금방 알아채실 테니까, 시험 기간은 짧을 거야.'\n\n\"무슨 새로운 곤경에 빠지신 줄 알았어요.\" 친구를 다시 보고 너무 기뻐서 스마이크가 말했어. \"시간이 어찌나 길게 느껴지던지, 나중엔 형이 영영 못 돌아오는 게 아닌가 겁이 났어요.\"\n\n\"못 돌아온다고!\" 니콜라스가 명랑하게 대꾸했어. \"날 그렇게 쉽게 떼어 낼 순 없을걸, 약속하지. 난 앞으로도 수천 번은 더 물 위로 떠오를 거야. 그리고 날 밀어 내리는 손길이 거셀수록, 난 그만큼 더 빨리 튀어 오를 거라고, 스마이크. 자, 이리 와. 내가 여기 온 건 널 집으로 데려가려는 거야.\"\n\n\"집이요!\" 스마이크는 머뭇거리며 슬며시 뒤로 물러섰어.\n\n\"그래.\" 니콜라스가 그의 팔을 잡으며 대꾸했어. \"안 될 게 뭐 있어?\"\n\n\"한때는 그런 꿈도 꿨어요.\" 스마이크가 말했어. \"낮이고 밤이고, 낮이고 밤이고, 여러 해 동안요. 지칠 때까지 집을 그리워했고, 슬픔에 사무쳐 야위어 갔죠. 하지만 이제는……\"\n\n\"이제는 어떻다는 거야?\" 니콜라스가 그의 얼굴을 다정하게 들여다보며 물었어. \"이제 어떻다고, 친구?\"\n\n\"이제는 형하고 떨어져서는 이 세상 어떤 집에도 갈 수 없어요.\" 스마이크가 그의 손을 꼭 쥐며 대답했어. \"딱 한 곳만 빼고요, 딱 한 곳만요. 난 결코 노인이 되진 못할 거예요. 그리고 만약에 형 손으로 날 무덤에 묻어 준다면, 그리고 죽기 전에, 형이 가끔 그 무덤을 찾아와 그 다정한 미소 가운데 하나로 내려다봐 줄 거라고, 여름날, 모든 게 살아 숨 쉴 때 — 나처럼 죽은 게 아니라요 — 그렇게 생각할 수 있다면, 나는 거의 눈물 한 방울 없이 그 집으로 갈 수 있을 거예요.\"\n\n\"왜 그런 말을 하는 거야, 가엾은 녀석아, 나하고 사는 게 행복하다면서?\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"내가 변할 테니까요. 내 곁에 있는 사람들이 아니라요. 그리고 그 사람들이 날 잊는다 해도, 난 결코 알지 못할 테니까요.\" 스마이크가 대답했어. \"교회 묘지에서는 우리 모두 똑같아요. 하지만 여기엔 나 같은 사람이 없어요. 난 보잘것없는 녀석이에요. 하지만 그건 나도 알아요.\"\n\n\"넌 어리석고 바보 같은 녀석이야.\" 니콜라스가 쾌활하게 말했어. \"네 말 뜻이 그거라면, 그건 인정해 주지. 아니, 숙녀들과 함께할 자리에 이런 우울한 얼굴이라니! 게다가 네가 그렇게 자주 나한테 물어보던 내 예쁜 누이도 있는데 말이야. 이게 네 요크셔식 신사도냐? 부끄러운 줄 알아! 부끄러운 줄!\"\n\n스마이크는 얼굴이 환해지더니 미소를 지었어.\n\n\"내가 집 이야기를 할 때는,\" 니콜라스가 말을 이었어. \"내 집을 말하는 거야. 그러니까 당연히 네 집이기도 하지. 그게 특정한 벽 네 개와 지붕 하나로 정해진 거라면, 솔직히 그게 어디쯤 있는지 말하기가 꽤 난감할 거야. 하지만 내가 말하는 건 그런 게 아니야. 내가 집이라고 할 때는, 더 나은 데가 없으니, 내가 사랑하는 사람들이 한데 모인 곳을 말하는 거야. 그리고 그곳이 집시의 천막이든 헛간이든, 난 변함없이 그 좋은 이름으로 부를 거야. 자, 그럼 지금 내 집이 어떤 곳이냐 하면 — 네 기대가 아무리 거창하다 해도, 그 넓이로 널 놀라게 하지도, 그 웅장함으로 널 겁먹게 하지도 않을 거야!\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 니콜라스는 친구의 팔을 잡았어. 그리고 같은 뜻으로 훨씬 더 많은 이야기를 들려주며, 가는 길에 그의 흥미를 끌고 즐겁게 해 줄 이런저런 것들을 가리켜 보이면서, 미스 라 크리비의 집으로 앞장서 갔어.\n\n\"케이트, 이 사람이,\" 누이가 혼자 앉아 있는 방으로 들어서며 니콜라스가 말했어. \"내가 너한테 맞아 달라고 미리 말해 둔, 그 진실한 친구이자 다정한 길동무야.\"\n\n가엾은 스마이크는 처음엔 수줍고 어색하고 잔뜩 겁을 먹었어. 하지만 케이트가 어찌나 다정하게 다가와, 어찌나 고운 목소리로, 오빠한테 그 이야기를 다 듣고 나서 얼마나 만나고 싶었는지, 그토록 힘든 역경 속에서 오빠를 그렇게 크게 위로해 준 게 얼마나 고마운지를 말해 주는지, 그는 눈물을 흘려야 할지 말지 영 갈피를 못 잡고 더욱 허둥대고 말았어. 그래도 그는 떨리는 목소리로, 니콜라스가 자기의 유일한 친구이고, 그를 돕기 위해서라면 목숨이라도 내놓겠다고 간신히 말했어. 그리고 케이트는 그토록 친절하고 사려 깊으면서도 그의 괴로움과 당혹감을 전혀 눈치채지 못하는 듯 보였어. 그래서 그는 거의 곧바로 마음을 가다듬고 아주 편안해졌지.\n\n그때 미스 라 크리비가 들어왔어. 그래서 스마이크는 그녀에게도 소개되어야 했지. 미스 라 크리비도 무척 친절했고, 놀랄 만큼 말이 많았어. 스마이크한테가 아니라 — 그러면 처음엔 그가 불편해질 테니까 — 니콜라스와 그의 누이한테 말이지. 그러다가 얼마쯤 지나서는 이따금 스마이크한테도 직접 말을 걸었어. 그림 보는 안목이 있느냐고, 저 구석에 있는 그림이 자기랑 닮은 것 같으냐고, 자기를 열 살쯤 젊게 그렸으면 더 나았을 것 같지 않으냐고, 또 일반적으로 봐서 젊은 아가씨들이 그림 속에서뿐 아니라 그림 밖에서도 나이 든 사람들보다 더 예뻐 보이지 않느냐고 물었어. 그 밖에도 자잘한 농담과 익살스러운 말을 많이 늘어놓았는데, 어찌나 유쾌하고 명랑하게 풀어내는지, 스마이크는 속으로 그녀가 여태껏 본 가장 멋진 숙녀라고 생각했어. 빈센트 크럼블스 극단의 그러든 부인보다도 더 멋지다고 말이야. 그 부인도 멋진 숙녀였고, 어쩌면 말은 더 많이 했지만, 확실히 미스 라 크리비보다 목소리가 더 컸거든.\n\n마침내 문이 다시 열리더니, 상복을 입은 부인이 들어왔어. 그리고 니콜라스는 그 상복 입은 부인에게 다정하게 입을 맞추고 어머니라고 부르며, 부인이 방에 들어올 때 스마이크가 일어선 그 의자 쪽으로 그녀를 데려갔어.\n\n\"어머닌 늘 마음이 따뜻하시고, 짓눌린 사람들을 돕고 싶어 하시잖아요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그러니 이 친구한테도 잘 대해 주실 거예요, 저는 알아요.\"\n\n\"그럼, 그렇고말고, 우리 니콜라스.\" 니클비 부인은 새 친구를 아주 빤히 쳐다보며 대답했어. 그러고는 그 자리에 어울리는 것보다 좀 더 위엄을 담아 그에게 고개를 숙였지. \"네 친구라면 누구든, 당연히 그래야 마땅하고, 또 그래야만 하는 거지만, 나한테 큰 권리를 가지는 게 분명하지. 그리고 당연히, 네가 관심을 두는 사람을 소개받는 건 나한테는 아주 큰 기쁨이고. 그건 의심할 여지가 없어. 전혀 없지. 손톱만큼도 없어.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"그래도 한 가지 말해 둬야겠다, 니콜라스, 얘야. 네 가엾은 아버지한테 늘 하던 말인데, 그이가 집에 손님을 데려와 저녁을 먹이겠다는데 집에 아무것도 없을 때면 말이야, 그이가 그저께 왔더라면 — 아니, 지금 그저께를 말하는 게 아니라, 아마 재작년이라고 해야겠지 — 손님을 더 잘 대접할 수 있었을 거라고 했지.\"\n\n이렇게 한마디 하고서 니클비 부인은 딸 쪽으로 몸을 돌리더니, 들릴 만한 속삭임으로 저 신사가 밤새 묵을 거냐고 물었어.\n\n\"왜냐하면, 만약에 그렇다면 말이야, 케이트 얘야,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"저이가 어디서 잘 수 있을지 도무지 모르겠거든, 정말이야.\"\n\n케이트가 우아하게 앞으로 나서더니, 짜증이나 화난 기색은 조금도 없이, 어머니 귀에 몇 마디를 속삭였어.\n\n\"어머나, 케이트, 얘야,\" 니클비 부인이 흠칫 물러서며 말했어. \"너 사람을 왜 이렇게 간지럽히니! 당연히 나도 그쯤은 안다, 얘야, 네가 말 안 해도. 나도 니콜라스한테 똑같이 말했고, 정말 기쁘기도 하단다. 니콜라스, 얘야, 넌 나한테 말 안 해 줬구나,\" 니클비 부인은 아까보다 좀 덜 점잔 빼는 태도로 돌아서며 덧붙였어. \"네 친구 이름이 뭔지 말이야.\"\n\n\"얘 이름은요, 어머니,\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"스마이크예요.\"\n\n이 말이 가져온 효과는 전혀 예상 밖이었어. 그 이름이 입에서 나오기가 무섭게 니클비 부인이 의자에 털썩 주저앉더니, 와락 울음을 터뜨렸거든.\n\n\"무슨 일이세요?\" 니콜라스가 부인을 부축하러 달려가며 외쳤어.\n\n\"파이크하고 어쩜 그렇게 똑같니.\" 니클비 부인이 울먹였어. \"파이크하고 어쩌면 그렇게 빼다 박았는지. 아! 나한테 말 걸지 마라 — 곧 괜찮아질 테니까.\"\n\n그러고는 천천히 숨이 막혀 가는 온갖 단계의 증세를 다 보여 주고, 가득 찬 컵에서 찻숟가락 하나쯤 되는 물을 마시고 나머지는 흘린 다음, 니클비 부인은 괜찮아졌어. 그러고는 힘없는 미소를 띠며, 자기가 참 어리석다는 걸 안다고 말했어.\n\n\"우리 집안 내력이란다.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"그러니 당연히 내 탓은 아니지. 케이트야, 네 할머니도 꼭 이러셨어 — 정말로 똑같이. 조금만 흥분해도, 아주 살짝만 놀라도 — 곧바로 기절하셨지. 할머니가 자주, 아주 자주 말씀하셨는데, 처녀였을 때, 결혼하기 전에, 어느 날 옥스퍼드 거리로 모퉁이를 돌다가 자기 미용사하고 딱 부딪쳤다는 거야. 그런데 그 미용사가 곰한테서 도망치던 참이었다지 뭐냐 — 그렇게 갑작스럽게 마주친 것만으로도 곧장 기절을 하셨대. 가만, 그런데,\" 니클비 부인이 잠시 멈추고 곰곰이 생각하며 덧붙였어. \"내 말이 맞는지 좀 보자. 곰한테서 도망친 게 그 미용사였나, 아니면 미용실에서 도망친 게 곰이었나? 정말이지 지금은 기억이 안 나는구나. 그래도 그 미용사가 아주 잘생긴 남자였다는 건 알지. 매너도 아주 신사다웠고. 뭐, 그건 이야기의 핵심하고는 상관없는 일이지만.\"\n\n니클비 부인은 자기도 모르게 회상에 잠기는 기분에 빠져들더니, 그 순간부터 기분이 한결 나아졌어. 그러고는 가끔씩 슬쩍슬쩍 화제를 바꿔 가며, 지금 다루는 주제하고 딱 들어맞는 점에서 조금도 뒤지지 않는 이런저런 다른 일화들 속으로 미끄러져 들어갔지.\n\n\"스마이크 씨는 요크셔 출신이라고, 니콜라스, 얘야?\" 저녁을 먹고 한동안 말이 없다가 니클비 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"그럼요, 어머니.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"얘의 가슴 아픈 사연을 안 잊으셨군요.\"\n\n\"오, 그럼 안 잊었지.\" 니클비 부인이 외쳤어. \"아! 정말 가슴 아픈 일이지. 스마이크 씨, 혹시 노스 라이딩 어디쯤에 있는 그림블 홀의 그림블 가에서 식사를 한 적이 있나요?\" 착한 부인이 그에게 말을 걸었어. \"토머스 그림블 경이라고, 아주 자존심이 센 양반인데, 다 큰 딸이 여섯이나 되고 하나같이 어찌나 사랑스러운지, 그 고을에서 제일 좋은 정원을 가졌지요.\"\n\n\"어머니,\" 니콜라스가 타이르듯 말했어. \"요크셔 학교에서 불행하게 버려졌던 아이가 그 동네 귀족이나 신사들한테서 초대장을 그렇게 많이 받았을 것 같으세요?\"\n\n\"글쎄다, 얘야, 그게 왜 그렇게 별난 일인지 모르겠구나.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"나는 학교 다닐 때, 적어도 한 학기에 두 번씩은 톤턴 베일의 호킨스 가에 갔단다. 그 사람들은 그림블 가보다 훨씬 부자였고, 게다가 그림블 가하고 혼인으로 맺어진 사이였거든. 그러니 봐라, 그렇게 있을 법하지 않은 일도 아니잖니.\"\n\n이렇게 니콜라스를 보기 좋게 눌러 놓고서, 니클비 부인은 갑자기 스마이크의 진짜 이름을 까먹더니, 그를 슬래먼스 씨라고 부르고 싶은 걸 도무지 참을 수 없어 했어. 그 까닭을 부인은 두 이름이 소리에서 무척 비슷한 데 있다고 둘러댔지. 둘 다 에스로 시작하고, 게다가 엠 자가 들어간다는 거였어. 하지만 그 점이야 어떻든, 그가 더없이 훌륭한 청자라는 데는 의심할 여지가 없었어. 그래서 그 점이 둘 사이를 더없이 좋은 관계로 만드는 데 적잖은 영향을 미쳤고, 니클비 부인이 그의 전반적인 행실과 됨됨이에 최고의 평을 내리게 만들었지.\n\n이렇게 작은 식구들은 더없이 화목하고 즐거운 사이로 지냈어. 그러다 월요일 아침이 되자 니콜라스는 잠시 그 자리에서 물러났지. 자기 형편을 진지하게 돌아보고, 할 수만 있다면 어떤 삶의 길을 정하기 위해서였어. 오로지 자기 노력에만 기대 사는 사람들을 부양할 수 있는 길 말이야.\n\n크럼블스 씨가 한두 번 떠오르긴 했어. 하지만 케이트는 그 사람과 얽힌 사연을 죄다 알고 있어도 어머니는 몰랐거든. 그리고 자기가 무대에서 밥벌이를 한다고 하면 어머니가 천 가지나 되는 짜증 섞인 반대를 할 게 뻔히 보였어. 그 생활로 돌아가는 데는 더 무거운 이유들도 있었지. 벌이가 빠듯하고 불안정하다는 것, 그리고 지방 배우로서조차 큰 명성에 오르기를 바랄 수 없다는 자기 속마음에서 비롯된 이유들은 그렇다 치더라도, 어떻게 누이를 이 도시에서 저 도시로, 이곳저곳으로 끌고 다니며, 자기가 거의 가리지 않고 어울릴 수밖에 없는 그런 사람들 말고는 다른 누구와도 못 사귀게 한단 말인가? \"안 되겠어.\" 니콜라스가 고개를 저으며 말했어. \"다른 걸 찾아봐야지.\"\n\n이런 결심을 하기는 그걸 실행에 옮기기보다 훨씬 쉬웠어. 짧은 시도들 속에서 스스로 얻은 것 말고는 세상 경험이 없는 데다, 무모하고 경솔한 면도 적잖이 가진(그 나이 때 아주 부자연스러운 자질은 아니지만) 처지에, 돈도 쥐꼬리만 하고 친구는 더더욱 손에 꼽을 만큼밖에 없으니, 무엇을 할 수 있겠어? \"에라!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그 직업소개소나 다시 한번 가 봐야지.\"\n\n빠른 걸음으로 걸어가면서 그는 혼자 피식 웃었어. 바로 조금 전에 자기 경솔함을 속으로 탓했으니까 말이야. 그래도 웃으며 그 생각을 떨쳐 버리진 않았어. 계속 걸어갔거든. 그곳에 다가가면서, 온갖 근사한 가능성을, 그리고 따지고 보면 불가능한 것들까지 머릿속에 그려 보았어. 그리고 자기가 이렇게 들뜨고 낙천적인 기질을 타고난 게 — 어쩌면 그럴 만한 이유가 있어서겠지만 — 무척 운이 좋다고 생각했지.\n\n소개소는 지난번에 떠날 때하고 똑같아 보였어. 사실 한두 개만 빼면, 진열창에 붙은 광고지도 전에 봤던 바로 그것들 같았지. 흠잡을 데 없는 주인 나리와 마님들이 정직한 하인을 구하고 있었고, 그 정직한 하인들은 흠잡을 데 없는 주인 나리와 마님들을 구하고 있었어. 자본을 투자할 으리으리한 영지들도 그대로였고, 영지에 투자할 어마어마한 자본도 그대로였지. 한마디로, 한밑천 잡고 싶어 하는 사람들을 위한 온갖 기회가 그대로 있었던 거야. 그리고 사람들이 진작에 이런 좋은 기회들을 붙잡으려 들지 않았다는 게, 이 나라가 얼마나 번영하고 있는지를 보여 주는 더없이 놀라운 증거였지.\n\n니콜라스가 걸음을 멈추고 진열창을 들여다보는데, 마침 한 노신사도 멈춰 섰어. 니콜라스는 자기 처지에 딱 맞는 큼지막한 글씨의 광고지를 찾느라 진열창 유리를 왼쪽에서 오른쪽으로 죽 훑어 가다가, 이 노신사의 모습을 보게 됐어. 그러고는 저도 모르게 진열창에서 눈을 거두어 그를 좀 더 가까이 살펴봤지.\n\n그는 다부진 노인이었어. 폭이 넓고 자락이 큰 파란 코트를 입었는데, 넉넉하게 만들어져 허리가 따로 잡히지 않은 옷이었지. 굵은 다리에는 누런 반바지와 긴 각반을 둘렀고, 머리에는 부유한 목축업자가 쓸 법한, 챙이 넓고 모자통이 낮은 흰 모자를 쓰고 있었어. 코트는 단추를 채워 입었고, 보조개 패인 이중 턱은 흰 목도리 주름 속에 묻혀 있었지. 빳빳하게 풀 먹인, 졸도라도 할 것 같은 그런 넥타이가 아니라, 그대로 베고 자도 아무렇지 않을 만한, 편안하고 예스러운 흰 목도리였어. 그런데 니콜라스의 눈길을 무엇보다 끈 건 그 노신사의 눈이었어. 그렇게 맑고, 반짝이고, 정직하고, 유쾌하고, 행복해 보이는 눈은 처음이었지. 그는 거기 서서, 한 손은 코트 가슴팍에 찔러 넣고, 다른 손으로는 예스러운 금시계 줄을 만지작거리며, 약간 위쪽을 바라보고 있었어. 고개를 살짝 한쪽으로 기울이고, 모자는 머리보다 좀 더 한쪽으로 기울어져 있었지만(그건 분명 어쩌다 그런 거지, 평소 쓰는 모양은 아니었어), 입가에는 아주 흐뭇한 미소가 어려 있었어. 그리고 교활함과 천진함, 따뜻한 마음과 유쾌함이 한데 어우러진 익살스러운 표정이 그 명랑한 늙은 얼굴을 환히 밝히고 있어서, 니콜라스는 그 자리에 서서 저녁때까지 그를 바라봐도 좋겠다 싶었어. 그러면서 이 넓은 세상에 마음 비뚤어진 사람이나 심술궂은 얼굴 같은 게 있다는 것조차 까맣게 잊어버릴 만큼 말이야.\n\n하지만 그런 흐뭇함은 아주 잠깐도 누릴 수가 없었어. 노신사는 자기가 누군가의 구경거리가 됐다는 걸 전혀 모르는 듯하더니, 무심코 니콜라스 쪽을 쳐다봤거든. 그러자 니콜라스는 실례가 될까 봐 얼른 다시 진열창을 살피기 시작했어.\n\n그래도 노신사는 거기 서서 광고지를 하나하나 훑어보고 있었어. 니콜라스는 다시 그 얼굴로 눈길을 들지 않을 수가 없었지. 예스럽고 별난 그 차림새 위에는 뭐라 말할 수 없이 사람을 끄는 무언가가 새겨져 있었고, 어찌나 됨됨이가 훌륭해 보이는지, 게다가 입가와 눈가에는 자잘한 빛들이 어찌나 많이 떠도는지, 그를 보는 건 그저 재미있는 정도가 아니라 분명한 즐거움이자 기쁨이었어.\n\n이러니, 그 노인이 니콜라스를 그 짓을 하던 현장에서 한두 번 들킨 것도 당연한 일이지. 그럴 때마다 니콜라스는 얼굴을 붉히고 당황한 기색을 보였어. 실은, 혹시 이 낯선 이가 어쩌면 사무원이나 비서를 구하고 있는 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 들기 시작했거든. 그리고 그런 생각을 하니, 노신사도 분명 그걸 알 것만 같았던 거야.\n\n이걸 다 이야기하는 데는 오래 걸리지만, 실제로 지나간 시간은 이삼 분도 안 됐어. 낯선 이가 자리를 뜨려 할 때, 니콜라스는 다시 그와 눈이 마주쳤어. 그리고 어색한 그 순간에 더듬더듬 사과의 말을 내뱉었지.\n\n\"괜찮네. 아니, 괜찮아!\" 노인이 말했어.\n\n이 말이 어찌나 정겨운 어조로 나오는지, 그 목소리가 그런 사람한테서 나올 법한 바로 그 목소리였고, 그 태도에 어찌나 다정함이 묻어나는지, 니콜라스는 용기를 내어 다시 말을 걸었어.\n\n\"여기엔 기회가 참 많네요, 어르신.\" 그가 진열창 쪽으로 손짓하며 반쯤 웃으며 말했어.\n\n\"일자리를 구하고 싶어 안달이 난 사람들 가운데 그렇게 진지하게 생각한 이가 아주 많았겠지, 틀림없이.\" 노인이 대답했어. \"가엾은 사람들, 가엾은 사람들!\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 그는 자리를 떴어. 하지만 니콜라스가 막 말을 하려는 걸 보고는, 그의 말허리를 자르기 싫다는 듯 너그럽게 걸음을 늦췄지. 거리에서 목례를 주고받은 두 사람이 돌아서서 말을 걸까 말까 둘 다 망설일 때 흔히 보이는, 그런 머뭇거림이 잠시 흐른 뒤에, 니콜라스는 어느새 노인 곁에 가 있었어.\n\n\"뭔가 말하려던 참이었지, 젊은이. 무슨 말을 하려던 건가?\"\n\n\"그저, 저는 어쩌면 바랐어요 — 아니, 그러니까, 생각했어요 — 어르신이 저 광고들을 보시는 데 무슨 목적이 있으신가 하고요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"허, 그래? 무슨 목적? 무슨 목적이라는 건가?\" 노인이 니콜라스를 곁눈질하며 대꾸했어. \"이 사람이 지금 일자리를 구하는 줄 알았나, 응? 그렇게 생각한 게야?\"\n\n니콜라스가 고개를 저었어.\n\n\"하! 하!\" 노신사는 마치 손을 씻기라도 하는 것처럼 두 손과 손목을 비비며 웃었어. \"내가 저 광고들을 들여다보고 있는 걸 봤으니, 어쨌든 아주 자연스러운 생각이지. 나도 처음엔 자네를 그렇게 생각했다네. 정말이지 그랬어.\"\n\n\"끝까지 그렇게 생각하셨더라도, 어르신, 진실에서 그리 멀진 않았을 거예요.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"응?\" 노인이 그를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 훑어보며 외쳤어. \"뭐라고! 이런, 세상에! 아니, 아니. 행실 바른 젊은이가 그런 처지에 내몰리다니! 아니지, 아니야. 아니고말고.\"\n\n니콜라스는 고개 숙여 인사하고, 안녕히 가시라는 말과 함께 발길을 돌렸어.\n\n\"잠깐.\" 노인이 그를 옆 골목으로 불러들이며 말했어. 거기라면 방해를 덜 받고 이야기할 수 있었거든. \"그게 무슨 뜻인가, 응?\"\n\n\"그저, 어르신의 인자한 얼굴과 태도가 — 둘 다 제가 여태껏 본 누구하고도 너무 달라서요 — 저도 모르게 털어놓게 만든 거예요. 이 런던이라는 황무지에서 다른 낯선 사람한테는 꿈에도 하지 않았을 말을요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"황무지! 그래, 그렇지, 그래. 옳아! 여긴 황무지야.\" 노인이 무척 활기차게 말했어. \"나한테도 한때는 황무지였지. 난 맨발로 여기 왔다네. 한 번도 잊은 적이 없어. 고맙게도 말이야!\" 그러고는 머리에서 모자를 벗어 들고, 아주 엄숙한 표정을 지었어.\n\n\"무슨 일인가? 어찌 된 일이야? 다 어쩌다 그렇게 됐나?\" 노인이 니콜라스의 어깨에 손을 얹고, 그를 데리고 거리를 걸어 올라가며 말했어. \"자네 — 응?\" 그의 검은 코트 소맷자락에 손가락을 갖다 대며. \"누구 때문인가, 응?\"\n\n\"제 아버지요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"아!\" 노신사가 얼른 말했어. \"젊은이가 아버지를 잃는 건 안 좋은 일이지. 어머니는 혼자 되셨고, 그렇지?\"\n\n니콜라스가 한숨을 쉬었어.\n\n\"형제자매도 있고? 응?\"\n\n\"누이가 하나 있어요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"가엾어라, 가엾어! 자네는 학식도 있겠지, 틀림없이?\" 노인이 젊은이의 얼굴을 그윽이 들여다보며 말했어.\n\n\"그럭저럭 잘 배운 편이에요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"좋은 일이지.\" 노신사가 말했어. \"배움이란 대단한 거야. 아주 대단한 거지! 난 배운 게 없어서, 남이 가진 걸 더 우러러본다네. 아주 좋은 거지. 그래, 그래. 자네 이야기를 더 들려주게. 전부 들어 보세. 주제넘은 호기심은 아니야 — 아니, 아니고말고.\"\n\n이 모든 말을 하는 그 태도에는 어찌나 진실되고 꾸밈없는 데가 있는지, 또 온갖 형식적인 격식과 쌀쌀함을 어찌나 깡그리 무시하는지, 니콜라스는 도무지 거기에 저항할 수가 없었어. 건전하고 참된 자질을 지닌 사람들 사이에서는, 순수하게 열린 마음만큼 전염성 강한 것도 없거든. 니콜라스는 곧바로 그 마음에 전염됐고, 거리낌 없이 자기 짧은 이력의 요점들을 죽 풀어놓았어. 다만 이름들만 빼고, 삼촌이 케이트한테 한 짓은 되도록 가볍게 짚고 넘어갔지. 노인은 아주 골똘히 귀를 기울였어. 그리고 이야기가 끝나자, 그의 팔을 자기 팔에 다정하게 끼웠어.\n\n\"한마디도 더 하지 말게. 한마디도.\" 그가 말했어. \"나하고 같이 가세. 한순간도 허비해선 안 돼.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 노신사는 그를 다시 옥스퍼드 거리로 끌고 갔어. 그러고는 시내로 가는 합승마차를 불러 세우더니, 니콜라스를 먼저 밀어 넣고 자기도 뒤따라 탔지.\n\n그가 도무지 가만있지 못하는 들뜬 상태인 데다, 니콜라스가 말을 하려 들 때마다 \"한마디도 더 하지 말게, 이보게, 무슨 일이 있어도 — 한마디도\" 하고 곧장 가로막는 통에, 젊은이는 더 이상 끼어들지 않는 게 낫겠다고 생각했어. 그래서 두 사람은 아무 대화도 나누지 않고 시내로 들어갔지. 그리고 갈수록 니콜라스는 이 모험이 대체 어떻게 끝날지 점점 더 궁금해졌어.\n\n은행에 다다르자 노신사는 아주 잽싸게 내렸어. 그러고는 다시 한번 니콜라스의 팔을 잡고, 그를 데리고 스레드니들 거리를 따라 서둘러 갔지. 오른쪽으로 몇 갈래 골목과 통로를 지나, 마침내 조용하고 그늘진 작은 광장으로 나왔어. 그는 그 광장에서 가장 오래되고 가장 깨끗해 보이는 상점으로 앞장서 들어갔지. 문기둥에 적힌 글귀라곤 '치어리블 형제 상회'뿐이었어. 하지만 여기저기 놓인 짐짝들의 행선지를 얼핏 보고, 니콜라스는 치어리블 형제가 독일 상인이리라 짐작했지.\n\n번창하는 사업의 온갖 흔적이 묻어나는 창고를 지나, 치어리블 씨는(니콜라스는 지나친 창고지기와 짐꾼들이 보인 존경의 태도로 보아 그가 치어리블 씨라고 짐작했어) 그를 칸막이로 막아 놓은 작은 회계실로 데려갔어. 커다란 유리 상자 같은 곳이었지. 그 회계실 안에는 — 마치 유리 상자 뚜껑을 덮기 전에 그 안에 박혀서는 그 뒤로 한 번도 나온 적이 없는 양, 먼지 한 점 티 하나 없이 — 얼굴이 큼지막한 뚱뚱한 노년의 사무원이 앉아 있었어. 은테 안경을 끼고 머리에 분을 바른 사람이었지.\n\n\"내 동생은 자기 방에 있나, 팀?\" 치어리블 씨가 니콜라스한테 보인 것 못지않은 다정한 태도로 말했어.\n\n\"네, 계십니다, 어르신.\" 뚱뚱한 사무원이 안경알은 주인 쪽으로, 눈은 니콜라스 쪽으로 돌리며 대답했어. \"하지만 트리머스 씨가 같이 계세요.\"\n\n\"그래! 그 사람은 무슨 일로 왔나, 팀?\" 치어리블 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"오늘 아침 동인도 부두에서 사고로 죽은 사람의 미망인과 식구들을 위해 모금을 하고 있답니다, 어르신.\" 팀이 대답했어. \"설탕 통에 깔려 으스러졌다는군요, 어르신.\"\n\n\"착한 사람이야.\" 치어리블 씨가 무척 진지하게 말했어. \"마음씨 고운 사람이지. 트리머스한테 참 고마워. 트리머스는 우리한테 둘도 없는 친구 가운데 하나야. 우리 힘으로는 결코 알아내지 못했을 딱한 사정을 천 가지나 알려 주거든. 트리머스한테 정말 고마워.\" 이렇게 말하며 치어리블 씨는 더없이 흐뭇한 표정으로 두 손을 비볐어. 그리고 마침 그때 트리머스 씨가 나가는 길에 문 앞을 지나가자, 그가 쏜살같이 뒤쫓아 나가 그의 손을 붙잡았어.\n\n\"천 번 만 번 고맙네, 트리머스. 정말 고마워. 자네가 참 살갑게 해 줘서, 정말 살갑게 말이야.\" 치어리블 씨가 그를 한구석으로 끌고 가, 남들이 못 듣게 하며 말했어. \"아이가 몇이나 되나, 그리고 내 동생 네드는 얼마를 냈나, 트리머스?\"\n\n\"아이는 여섯이고요,\" 그 신사가 대답했어. \"동생분은 우리한테 이십 파운드를 주셨습니다.\"\n\n\"내 동생 네드는 착한 녀석이야. 그리고 자네도 착한 사람이고, 트리머스.\" 노인이 그의 두 손을 떨릴 만큼 열심히 흔들며 말했어. \"나도 이십 파운드 적어 주게 — 아니 — 잠깐만, 잠깐만. 너무 티 나게 굴면 안 되지. 나는 십 파운드만 적어 주고, 팀 링킨워터도 십 파운드 적어 주게. 팀, 트리머스 씨한테 이십 파운드짜리 수표를 끊어 주게. 신의 가호가 있기를, 트리머스 — 그리고 이번 주 어느 날 우리하고 저녁 먹으러 오게. 나이프하고 포크는 언제든 마련돼 있을 테니, 우리도 기쁠 걸세. 자, 이보게 — 팀, 링킨워터 씨 몫의 수표도. 설탕 통에 깔려 으스러지다니, 게다가 가엾은 아이가 여섯이라니 — 오, 저런, 저런, 저런!\"\n\n이런 식으로, 자기가 낸 큰 액수의 기부금을 두고 모금 담당자가 살갑게 만류라도 할까 봐, 치어리블 씨는 되도록 빠르게 말을 늘어놓았어. 그러고는 이 짧은 사이에 보고 들은 것에 똑같이 놀라고 감동한 니콜라스를 데리고, 반쯤 열린 또 다른 방문 앞으로 갔지.\n\n\"네드 동생,\" 치어리블 씨가 주먹으로 똑똑 두드리고 몸을 숙여 귀를 기울이며 말했어. \"바쁘니, 동생아, 아니면 나하고 한두 마디 나눌 시간이 좀 있을까?\"\n\n\"찰스 형, 형도 참,\" 안에서 목소리가 대답했는데, 그 어조가 방금 말한 사람하고 어찌나 똑같은지, 니콜라스는 흠칫 놀라며 같은 사람인 줄 알 뻔했어. \"그런 걸 묻지 말고, 어서 들어와요.\"\n\n두 사람은 더 묻고 따질 것 없이 안으로 들어갔어. 니콜라스를 데려온 사람이 다가가 또 다른 노신사와 따뜻하게 인사를 나누는데, 그가 자기하고 꼭 빼다 박은 본보기 같은 사람이었으니 니콜라스가 얼마나 놀랐겠어. 같은 얼굴, 같은 체격, 같은 코트와 조끼와 목도리, 같은 반바지와 각반 — 아니, 벽에 걸린 흰 모자까지 똑같은 거야!\n\n두 사람이 서로 손을 맞잡고 흔드는데, 각자의 얼굴이 다정함이 넘치는 환한 표정으로 빛났어. 그런 표정은 갓난아이들한테서 보면 더없이 흐뭇하겠지만, 이렇게 나이 든 사람들한테서 보니 말할 수 없이 뭉클했지. 니콜라스가 보니, 나중에 본 노신사가 형보다 좀 더 통통했어. 이 점하고, 걸음걸이와 체구에 살짝 덧붙은 굼뜬 기운 정도가, 둘 사이에서 알아볼 수 있는 유일한 차이였어. 두 사람이 쌍둥이 형제라는 걸 누구도 의심할 수 없었지.\n\n\"네드 동생,\" 니콜라스의 친구가 방문을 닫으며 말했어. \"여기 내가 도와줘야 할 젊은 친구가 하나 있다. 이 친구를 위해서나 우리를 위해서나 공정하게, 이 친구가 한 말을 제대로 알아봐야 해. 그리고 그게 사실로 밝혀지면 — 나는 분명히 그러리라 믿는다만 — 우리가 이 친구를 도와야지, 도와야 한다, 네드 동생.\"\n\n\"형이 도와야 한다고 하면, 그걸로 충분해요, 형.\" 동생이 대답했어. \"형이 그렇게 말하면, 더 알아볼 것도 없죠. 이 친구는 도움을 받게 될 거예요. 뭐가 필요하대요, 뭘 원하는데요? 팀 링킨워터는 어디 있죠? 그를 이리 부릅시다.\"\n\n여기서 한마디 덧붙이자면, 두 형제는 둘 다 말하는 투가 아주 힘 있고 진지했어. 둘 다 거의 같은 자리의 이가 빠져서, 말에 똑같은 특이함이 묻어났지. 그리고 둘 다 마치, 더없이 인자하고 의심을 모르는 천성이 줄 수 있는 가장 평온한 마음을 지닌 데다가, 운명의 가장 좋은 푸딩에서 건포도를 추려 내면서 지금 쓸 몇 알을 남겨 입에 물고 있기라도 한 것처럼 말했어.\n\n\"팀 링킨워터는 어디 있죠?\" 네드 동생이 말했어.\n\n\"잠깐, 잠깐, 잠깐!\" 찰스 형이 동생을 한쪽으로 데려가며 말했어. \"나한테 계획이 있다, 동생아, 계획이 있어. 팀도 이제 늙어 가고 있어. 그리고 팀은 충실한 일꾼이었지, 네드 동생. 팀의 어머니와 누이한테 연금을 주고, 그 가엾은 형이 죽었을 때 가족 묘를 조그맣게 하나 사 준 것만으로는, 그 충실한 봉사에 충분한 보답이 못 됐다는 생각이 든다.\"\n\n\"아냐, 아냐, 아냐.\" 동생이 대답했어. \"물론 모자라죠. 반의반도 안 돼요, 반의반도.\"\n\n\"우리가 팀의 일을 좀 덜어 줄 수 있다면,\" 노신사가 말했어. \"그리고 가끔 시골에 내려가도록, 게다가 일주일에 두세 번은 신선한 공기를 마시며 자도록 구슬릴 수 있다면(아침에 일을 한 시간 늦게 시작하면 그럴 수 있잖아), 늙은 팀 링킨워터도 머잖아 다시 젊어질 거야. 게다가 지금 우리보다 꼬박 세 살이나 위잖니. 늙은 팀 링킨워터가 다시 젊어진다! 응, 네드 동생, 응? 아니, 난 팀 링킨워터가 아주 꼬맹이였을 때가 기억나는데, 너는? 하, 하, 하! 가엾은 팀, 가엾은 팀!\"\n\n그러고는 이 멋진 노인들은 함께 정답게 웃었어. 둘 다 눈에 늙은 팀 링킨워터를 아끼는 눈물 한 방울씩을 그렁그렁 머금고 말이야.\n\n\"그래도 이것부터 들어 봐 — 이것부터, 네드 동생.\" 노인이 니콜라스 양옆에 의자를 하나씩 놓으며 서둘러 말했어. \"이건 내가 직접 너한테 얘기하마, 네드 동생. 이 젊은이는 겸손한 데다, 학식도 있어, 네드. 그러니 마치 거지라도 되는 양, 아니면 우리가 자기를 못 믿기라도 하는 양, 이 친구가 자기 사연을 몇 번이고 되풀이하게 하는 건 옳지 않다 싶구나. 아니지, 아니야, 아냐.\"\n\n\"아냐, 아냐, 아냐.\" 동생이 진지하게 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했어. \"지당해요, 형, 지당하죠.\"\n\n\"내가 잘못 말하면, 이 친구가 틀렸다고 일러 줄 거야.\" 니콜라스의 친구가 말했어. \"하지만 내가 그러든 안 그러든, 너는 무척 뭉클해질 거다, 네드 동생. 우리가 친구 하나 없는 두 소년이었던 시절, 이 큰 도시에서 처음으로 일 실링을 벌던 그때가 떠올라서 말이야.\"\n\n쌍둥이는 말없이 서로의 손을 꼭 쥐었어. 그리고 찰스 형은 자기 특유의 수수한 말투로, 니콜라스한테서 들은 사연을 들려줬지. 그 뒤로 이어진 대화는 길었어. 그리고 그게 끝나자, 네드 동생과 팀 링킨워터 사이에 또 다른 방에서 거의 그만큼 긴 비밀 회의가 벌어졌어. 두 형제와 마주 앉은 지 채 십 분도 안 되어, 니콜라스가 새로 베풀어지는 친절과 동정의 말이 나올 때마다 그저 손을 내저으며 어린아이처럼 흐느꼈다고 해서, 그를 깎아내릴 일은 아니지.\n\n마침내 네드 동생과 팀 링킨워터가 함께 돌아왔어. 그러자 팀이 곧장 니콜라스한테 걸어가, 아주 짤막한 한 문장을(팀은 평소 말이 적은 사람이었거든) 그의 귀에 속삭였어. 스트랜드의 주소를 적어 두었으니, 그날 저녁 여덟 시에 찾아가겠다는 거였지. 그러고 나서 팀은 치어리블 형제가 또 무슨 할 말이 있는지 들을 채비로, 안경을 닦아 다시 썼어.\n\n\"팀,\" 찰스 형이 말했어. \"우리가 이 젊은이를 회계실에 들일 작정이라는 거, 알겠지?\"\n\n네드 동생은 팀이 그 작정을 이미 알고 있고, 아주 찬성한다고 한마디 했어. 그리고 팀은 고개를 끄덕이며 그렇다고 하고는, 몸을 쭉 펴고서 유난히 뚱뚱하고 아주 중요한 사람처럼 굴었어. 그러고 나선 깊은 침묵이 흘렀지.\n\n\"난 아침에 한 시간 늦게 안 나올 거예요, 아시잖아요.\" 팀이 갑자기 다 쏟아 내듯, 아주 단호한 얼굴로 말했어. \"신선한 공기 마시며 잠도 안 잘 거고요. 아니, 시골에도 안 갈 거예요. 이 나이에 참 어울리는 일이네요, 정말. 흥!\"\n\n\"네 고집은 빌어먹을 노릇이다, 팀 링킨워터.\" 찰스 형이 노여움이라곤 손톱만큼도 없이, 그 늙은 사무원을 향한 애정으로 환히 빛나는 얼굴로 그를 바라보며 말했어. \"네 고집은 빌어먹을 노릇이야, 팀 링킨워터. 대체 무슨 소리냐, 응?\"\n\n\"올해로 사십사 년이에요.\" 팀이 펜으로 허공에 셈을 하면서, 합산하기 전에 가상의 선을 죽 그으며 말했어. \"오는 오월이면 사십사 년이죠. 제가 처음으로 치어리블 형제 상회의 장부를 맡은 지가요. 그동안 내내(일요일만 빼고) 매일 아침 시계가 아홉 시를 칠 때 금고를 열었고요, 매일 밤 열 시 반에(해외 우편 날만 빼고요, 그날은 열두 시 이십 분 전에) 온 집을 돌며 문이 잠겼는지, 불은 꺼졌는지 살폈죠. 뒤쪽 다락방을 벗어나 잔 적이 단 하루도 없어요. 창문 한가운데엔 똑같은 목서초 화분이 있고, 양쪽에 두 개씩, 똑같은 화분 네 개가 있는데, 다 제가 처음 왔을 때 가져온 거예요. 세상에 — 몇 번이고 말했고, 앞으로도 우길 거예요 — 세상에 이런 광장은 없어요. 없다는 걸 제가 안다고요.\" 팀이 갑자기 힘이 솟은 듯, 주위를 매섭게 둘러보며 말했어. \"단 하나도요. 일을 하든 즐기든, 여름이든 겨울이든 — 어느 쪽이든 상관없어요 — 이만한 데가 없어요. 영국에 저 아치 밑 펌프 같은 샘은 없어요. 영국에 제 창문에서 내다보는 것 같은 경치도 없고요. 면도하기 전에 매일 아침 봐 왔으니, 제가 그 정도는 좀 알죠. 저는 그 방에서 잤어요.\" 팀이 목소리를 좀 낮추며 덧붙였어. \"사십사 년 동안요. 그리고 폐가 안 되고 일에 지장만 안 된다면, 거기서 죽게 해 달라고 청하고 싶을 정도예요.\"\n\n\"네 이놈, 팀 링킨워터, 감히 죽는다는 소리를 입에 담아?\" 쌍둥이가 동시에 버럭 소리치며, 늙은 코를 요란하게 풀었어.\n\n\"제가 드릴 말씀은 그거예요, 에드윈 어른, 찰스 어른.\" 팀이 다시 어깨를 펴며 말했어. \"두 분이 저를 퇴직시키겠다는 얘기를 꺼낸 게 이번이 처음은 아니죠. 하지만 괜찮으시다면, 이번을 마지막으로 하고, 그 얘기는 영영 그만 접도록 하죠.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 팀 링킨워터는 성큼성큼 나가 버렸어. 그러고는 할 말을 다 했고 결코 꺾이지 않겠다고 단단히 마음먹은 사람의 기세로, 자기 유리 상자 안에 틀어박혔지.\n\n두 형제는 서로 눈짓을 주고받더니, 말없이 대여섯 번쯤 헛기침을 했어.\n\n\"저 친구한테 무슨 수를 써야겠다, 네드 동생.\" 형이 열을 내며 말했어. \"저 케케묵은 망설임은 무시해 버려야지. 그냥 두고 봐 줄 수도, 참아 줄 수도 없어. 저 친구를 동업자로 만들어야겠다, 네드 동생. 그리고 순순히 따르지 않겠다면, 우리가 힘이라도 써야지.\"\n\n\"옳은 말이에요.\" 네드 동생이 단단히 마음먹은 사람처럼 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했어. \"지당해요, 형. 저 친구가 사리를 안 듣겠다면, 우린 그 뜻을 거슬러서라도 해내야죠. 그리고 우리가 권한을 행사하기로 마음먹었다는 걸 보여 줘야죠. 우리가 저 친구하고 한바탕 싸워야겠어요, 찰스 형.\"\n\n\"그래야지. 우린 분명 팀 링킨워터하고 한판 붙어야 해.\" 형이 말했어. \"하지만 그동안에, 동생아, 우리가 우리 젊은 친구를 붙들어 두고 있구나. 그리고 그 가엾은 부인과 따님은 이 친구가 돌아오기를 애타게 기다리고 있을 거다. 그러니 일단은 작별 인사를 하자꾸나. 그리고 — 자, 자 — 그 상자 조심하게, 이보게 — 그리고 — 아니, 아니, 이젠 한마디도 말고. 그저 길 건널 때 조심하고 또 —\"\n\n이렇게 니콜라스가 고맙다는 말을 쏟아 내지 못하도록 두서없고 토막 난 말들을 늘어놓으며, 두 형제는 그를 서둘러 내보냈어. 가는 내내 그의 손을 흔들며, 그를 완전히 사로잡은 그 감정을 통 모르는 척하려 했지만 — 정말이지 거짓말엔 영 서툰 사람들이었어! — 영 신통치 않게 말이지.\n\n니콜라스는 가슴이 너무 벅차서, 어느 정도 마음을 가라앉히기 전엔 거리로 나설 수가 없었어. 마침내 그가 어쩔 수 없이 걸음을 멈췄던 그 어두운 문간 구석에서 슬며시 빠져나오는데, 쌍둥이가 유리 상자 한쪽 모퉁이로 살그머니 안을 엿보는 게 얼핏 눈에 들어왔어. 좀 전의 그 공격을 지체 없이 밀어붙일지, 아니면 일단은 그 꿈쩍 않는 팀 링킨워터를 향한 추가 공세를 미뤄 둘지, 영 마음을 정하지 못한 게 분명했지.\n\n방금 자세히 늘어놓은 이 일들이 미스 라 크리비의 집에서 불러일으킨 온갖 기쁨과 놀라움을, 그리고 그 일로 행해지고, 입에 오르고, 떠올려지고, 기대되고, 바라지고, 점쳐진 온갖 것을 일일이 늘어놓는 건, 지금 이 모험의 흐름과 목적에서 벗어난 일이야. 간단히 말해, 티머시 링킨워터 씨가 약속 시간에 딱 맞춰 도착했다는 것, 그가 별난 사람인 데다 주인들의 더없이 폭넓은 너그러움이 제대로 발휘되는지를 당연히 깐깐하게 따지는 사람이었음에도, 니콜라스에 대해 힘차고 따뜻하게 호의적인 보고를 했다는 것, 그리고 이튿날 니콜라스가 치어리블 형제 상회 회계실의 빈 의자에 임명되어, 당장 연봉 백이십 파운드를 받게 됐다는 것만 밝혀 두면 충분하지.\n\n\"그리고 내 생각엔 말이다, 동생아,\" 니콜라스의 첫 친구가 말했어. \"지금 비어 있는 보 근처 그 작은 오두막을, 보통 집세보다 좀 싸게 저들한테 빌려주면 어떨까, 응? 어떠냐, 네드 동생?\"\n\n\"공짜로 주죠.\" 네드 동생이 말했어. \"우린 부자인데, 이런 형편에 처한 사람들한테서 집세를 받는다는 건 부끄러운 일이죠. 팀 링킨워터는 어디 있죠? — 그냥 공짜로요, 형, 그냥 공짜로 줍시다.\"\n\n\"그래도 얼마쯤은 받는 게 나을지도 모른다, 네드 동생.\" 형이 부드럽게 제안했어. \"그래야 절약하는 습관을 지키는 데 도움이 되고, 또 감당 못 할 신세를 졌다는 괴로운 마음도 덜어 주거든. 십오 파운드나 이십 파운드쯤 받기로 하고, 그걸 또박또박 내면, 다른 방법으로 그만큼 채워 주면 돼. 그리고 내가 가구를 좀 들이게 슬쩍 작은 돈을 빌려주고, 너도 따로 슬쩍 작은 돈을 빌려주고, 네드 동생. 그러다 저들이 잘 지내는 걸 보면 — 분명 그럴 거야, 걱정 없어, 걱정 없지 — 그 빚을 선물로 바꿔 주면 돼. 조심조심, 네드 동생, 차근차근, 너무 부담을 주지 않으면서 말이야. 자, 이제 어떠냐, 동생아?\"\n\n네드 동생은 그 자리에서 손을 내밀어 그러자고 했어. 그러자고 말만 한 게 아니라 실제로 그렇게 해 버렸지. 그래서 짧은 한 주 만에, 니콜라스는 그 의자를 차지했고, 니클비 부인과 케이트는 그 집을 차지했어. 그리고 모든 게 희망과 분주함, 그리고 가벼운 마음으로 가득했지.\n\n정말이지 그 오두막에서의 첫 주만큼 발견과 놀라움이 가득한 한 주는 없었을 거야. 니콜라스가 집에 올 때마다 밤마다 새로운 게 발견됐어. 어느 날은 포도 덩굴이었고, 또 어느 날은 보일러였고, 또 어느 날은 물통 밑바닥에서 나온 앞쪽 응접실 벽장 열쇠였고, 그런 식으로 백 가지나 되는 것들이 줄줄이 나왔지. 그러고는 이 방은 모슬린 커튼으로 꾸며졌고, 저 방은 창문 블라인드로 제법 우아해졌고, 누구도 가능하리라 생각 못 했을 단장들이 이루어졌어. 그리고 미스 라 크리비도 있었지. 하루 이틀 머물며 거들려고 합승마차를 타고 나온 사람인데, 양철 압정이 든 아주 작은 갈색 종이 꾸러미하고 아주 큼지막한 망치를 줄곧 잃어버리고, 소맷부리를 걷어붙인 채 이리저리 뛰어다니고, 사다리에서 굴러떨어져 어디를 호되게 다치기도 했어 — 그리고 니클비 부인도 있었지. 쉴 새 없이 떠들면서 가끔 뭔가를 하긴 했지만, 자주는 아니었어 — 그리고 케이트도 있었지. 어디서나 소리 없이 부지런히 움직이며, 무엇이든 다 좋아했어 — 그리고 스마이크도 있었지. 정원을 보기만 해도 그저 감탄스러운 곳으로 만들어 놓았어 — 그리고 니콜라스도 있었지. 모두를 거들고 북돋워 주었어 — 집의 온갖 평화와 즐거움이 되살아났고, 검소한 기쁨 하나하나에 그토록 새로운 맛이 더해지고, 함께하는 매 순간이 그토록 기뻤으니, 그건 오직 불행과 이별만이 줄 수 있는 것이었지!\n\n한마디로, 가난한 니클비네 식구는 정답고 행복했어. 반면에 부유한 니클비는 외롭고 비참했지.\n\n# CHAPTER 36\n\n저녁 일곱 시쯤이었어. 골든 스퀘어 근처의 좁은 골목길에 어둠이 내려앉기 시작할 무렵이었지. 케이크위그스 씨는 가장 싼 흰 새끼염소 가죽 장갑 한 켤레를 사 오라고 사람을 보냈어. 십사 펜스짜리 장갑이었지. 그러고는 그중 제일 튼튼한 걸 골랐는데, 마침 오른손 장갑이었어. 그는 거드름을 피우면서, 한껏 들떠서 아래층으로 내려갔어. 그러고는 현관문 노커의 손잡이를 그 장갑으로 둘둘 감았지. 이 일을 아주 정성껏 해낸 다음, 케이크위그스 씨는 문을 끌어당겨 닫고 길 건너편으로 잠깐 가서 효과가 어떤지 살펴봤어. 도무지 이보다 더 멋질 수는 없다는 데 흡족해진 케이크위그스 씨는 다시 길을 건너왔어. 그러고는 열쇠 구멍에 대고 몰리나에게 문을 열라고 외친 다음, 집 안으로 사라져 더는 보이지 않았어.\n\n자, 그냥 따로 떼어 놓고 생각해 보면, 케이크위그스 씨가 굳이 이 노커를 감싸느라 수고를 들일 이유가 별로 없었어. 십 마일이나 떨어진 곳에 사는 어느 귀족이나 신사의 노커를 감싸는 것만큼이나 뜬금없는 일이었지. 왜냐하면 수많은 하숙인들이 편하게 드나들도록 현관문은 늘 활짝 열려 있었고, 노커는 한 번도 쓰인 적이 없었거든. 이 층도, 삼 층도, 사 층도 저마다 따로 초인종이 있었어. 다락방으로 말하자면, 거길 찾아오는 사람은 아무도 없었지. 그리고 누가 일 층 응접실에 볼일이 있으면, 그건 바로 코앞이라서 그냥 곧장 걸어 들어가면 그만이었어. 부엌은 또 지하로 내려가는 계단 쪽에 따로 입구가 있었고. 그러니 그저 필요하냐 쓸모가 있느냐만 따지면, 이 노커를 감싸는 일은 도무지 이해할 수 없는 짓이었지.\n\n하지만 노커란 그저 실용적인 목적 말고도 다른 까닭으로 감쌀 수 있는 거야. 이번 경우가 바로 그걸 똑똑히 보여 줬지. 문명사회에는 반드시 지켜야 할 점잖은 격식과 의례가 있거든. 안 그러면 사람들은 원래의 야만 상태로 되돌아가 버리고 마니까. 점잖은 부인이 해산을 하는데 — 아니, 점잖은 해산이라면 당연히 — 노커를 감싸는 상징이 따라붙지 않는 법은 결코 없었어. 케이크위그스 부인은 점잖음을 어느 정도 자처하는 부인이었고, 마침 해산을 하는 참이었지. 그래서 케이크위그스 씨는 이 집의 잠잠한 노커를 흰 새끼염소 가죽 장갑으로 묶어 둔 거야.\n\n\"근데 말이지,\" 케이크위그스 씨는 셔츠 깃을 매만지며 천천히 계단을 올라가면서 중얼거렸어. \"아들이니까, 이걸 신문에 내야 하나 말아야 하나, 아직 잘 모르겠단 말이야.\"\n\n이렇게 하는 게 과연 좋을지, 또 동네에 어떤 화젯거리가 될지 곰곰이 따져 보면서, 케이크위그스 씨는 거실로 들어갔어. 거기엔 아주 자그마한 옷가지들이 난로 앞 빨래걸이에 널려서 바람을 쐬고 있었고, 의사인 럼비 씨가 아기를 어르고 있었지. 그러니까 새 아기가 아니라 그 위의 아기를 말이야.\n\n\"훌륭한 사내아이로군요, 케이크위그스 씨.\" 의사 럼비 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"이 아이가 훌륭한 사내아이라고 보시는 거죠, 선생님?\" 케이크위그스 씨가 되받았어.\n\n\"내 평생 본 중에 가장 훌륭한 사내아이입니다.\" 의사가 말했어. \"이런 아기는 본 적이 없어요.\"\n\n생각해 보면 참 흐뭇한 일이야. 세상에 태어나는 아기는 하나같이 그 앞의 아기보다 더 훌륭하다니 말이지. 인류가 점점 퇴화한다고 우기는 사람들한테 이건 완벽한 대답이 되거든.\n\n\"이런 아기는 정말이지 본 적이 없어요.\" 의사 럼비 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"몰리나도 훌륭한 아기였죠.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 한마디 했어. 마치 그 말이 은근히 자기 집안을 깎아내리는 거라도 되는 양 말이야.\n\n\"다들 훌륭한 아기였죠.\" 럼비 씨가 말했어. 그러고는 럼비 씨는 생각에 잠긴 표정으로 계속 아기를 어르고 있었어. 어쩌면 청구서에 이 어르는 값을 어느 항목으로 매기는 게 제일 나을까 따져 보던 건지도 모르지. 그건 자기만 알 일이야.\n\n이 짤막한 대화가 오가는 동안, 몰리나 양은 집안의 맏이로서, 또 어머니가 자리에 누운 동안 어머니를 대신하는 사람으로서, 세 동생을 쉴 새 없이 떠밀고 찰싹찰싹 때리고 있었어. 이런 사려 깊고 다정한 행동에 케이크위그스 씨는 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁해졌지. 그러고는 저 아이는 철든 것이나 처신하는 것이나 어엿한 어른이라고 했어.\n\n\"저 아이는 시집가는 남자한테 보배가 될 겁니다, 선생님.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 반쯤 혼잣말처럼 말했어. \"내 생각엔 제 신분보다 높은 데로 시집갈 것 같아요, 럼비 씨.\"\n\n\"전혀 이상할 게 없죠.\" 의사가 대답했어.\n\n\"저 아이가 춤추는 거 한 번도 못 보셨죠, 선생님?\" 케이크위그스 씨가 물었어.\n\n의사는 고개를 저었어.\n\n\"저런!\" 케이크위그스 씨가 진심으로 그를 딱하게 여기는 듯이 말했어. \"그럼 저 아이가 어떤 재주가 있는지 모르시겠군요.\"\n\n이러는 내내 옆방에서는 사람들이 부산하게 들락날락했어. 문이 일 분에 스무 번쯤은 아주 조용히 열렸다 닫혔지. 케이크위그스 부인을 조용히 쉬게 해야 했으니까. 그리고 아기는 복도며 현관문 언저리에 모여든 여자 친구들의 선발 무리에서 보낸 대표단 스물 몇 팀에게 차례로 선을 보였어. 그 여자들은 이 사건을 온갖 방향에서 따져 보려고 모여 있었지. 사실 이 흥분은 온 거리로 퍼져 나갔어. 문가에 서 있는 부인네들 무리도 보였는데, 그중 몇몇은 케이크위그스 부인이 사람들 앞에 마지막으로 나타났을 때와 똑같은 흥미로운 상태였지. 그들은 저마다 비슷한 일을 겪은 경험담을 늘어놓고 있었어. 몇몇은 그저께 정확히 언제 그 일이 일어날지 미리 점쳤다며 큰 칭송을 받았지. 또 어떤 이들은, 케이크위그스 씨가 새파랗게 질려서 있는 힘껏 거리를 내달리는 걸 보자마자 무슨 일인지 단박에 알아챘다고 떠벌렸어. 이 사람은 이 말을 하고, 저 사람은 저 말을 했지. 하지만 다들 한꺼번에 떠들어 댔고, 두 가지 점에서는 모두 의견이 일치했어. 첫째, 케이크위그스 부인이 한 일은 아주 갸륵하고 더없이 칭찬받을 만하다는 것. 둘째, 저 럼비 선생만큼 솜씨 좋고 과학적인 의사는 일찍이 없었다는 것이었지.\n\n이 떠들썩한 소란 한가운데서, 럼비 선생은 앞서 말한 대로 이 층 앞방에 앉아, 자리에서 밀려난 아기를 어르며 케이크위그스 씨와 이야기를 나누고 있었어. 그는 퉁퉁하고 무뚝뚝해 보이는 신사였는데, 셔츠 깃이랄 것도 거의 없었고, 어제 아침부터 자란 수염이 까칠하게 돋아 있었지. 럼비 선생은 인기가 많았고 이 동네는 아기가 많이 태어나는 곳이었거든. 그래서 지난 마흔여덟 시간 사이에 노커를 감싼 집이 이 집 말고도 잇따라 세 군데나 더 있었던 거야.\n\n\"자, 케이크위그스 씨.\" 럼비 선생이 말했어. \"이걸로 여섯이로군요. 머잖아 식구가 아주 번창하시겠어요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"여섯이면 거의 충분한 것 같은데요, 선생님.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"쯧쯧! 무슨 말씀을!\" 의사가 말했어. \"터무니없는 소리! 절반도 안 됩니다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 의사는 웃었어. 그런데 케이크위그스 부인의 결혼한 친구 하나가 웃는 것의 절반도 안 되게 웃었지. 그 부인은 마침 산실에서 나와 경과를 알리고 브랜디 탄 물을 한 모금 홀짝이러 온 참이었는데, 이걸 세상에 나온 농담 중 가장 멋진 축에 든다고 여기는 모양이었어.\n\n\"이 아이들이 그저 운에만 매달려 사는 건 아니지요.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 둘째 딸을 무릎에 앉히며 말했어. \"이 아이들한테는 기대할 데가 있거든요.\"\n\n\"오, 그래요!\" 의사 럼비 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"게다가 아주 든든한 데죠? 안 그래요?\" 결혼한 부인이 물었어.\n\n\"글쎄요, 부인.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 말했어. \"그게 얼마나 될지 안 될지 콕 집어 말하는 건 제 처지에 할 일이 아니죠. 영광스럽게도 제가 연을 맺은 집안 자랑을 늘어놓는 것도 제가 할 일이 아니고요. 다만 케이크위그스 집안은 — 제가 드리고 싶은 말씀은,\" 케이크위그스 씨가 갑자기 목소리를 높이며 말했어. \"제 아이들이 어쩌면 한 명당 백 파운드쯤 손에 넣게 될지도 모른다는 겁니다. 어쩌면 그보다 더 될 수도 있고요, 하지만 그만큼은 확실하죠.\"\n\n\"아주 아담하고 어여쁜 재산이네요.\" 결혼한 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"케이크위그스 집안에는 친척이 몇 분 있는데요.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 의사의 코담배 통에서 코담배를 한 줌 집어 들고는, 익숙지가 않아서 아주 세게 재채기를 하며 말했어. \"그분들은 열 사람한테 백 파운드씩 남겨 주고도, 그러고 나서 동냥하러 다닐 일은 없는 분들이지요.\"\n\n\"아! 누구 말씀인지 알겠어요.\" 결혼한 부인이 고개를 끄덕이며 말했어.\n\n\"저는 아무 이름도 입에 올리지 않았고, 또 어떤 이름도 입에 올리고 싶지 않습니다.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 의미심장한 표정으로 말했어. \"제 친구들 중에 바로 이 방에서 케이크위그스 집안의 어떤 친척을 만나 본 분이 많은데, 그분은 어떤 자리에 가더라도 그 자리를 빛낼 분이지요. 제 말은 그게 다입니다.\"\n\n\"저도 그분을 만나 봤어요.\" 결혼한 부인이 럼비 선생 쪽을 흘긋 보며 말했어.\n\n\"그런 분이 제 아이들한테 입을 맞추고 귀여워해 주는 걸 보면, 아비 된 제 마음으로선 당연히 더없이 흐뭇하지요.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 말을 이었어. \"그런 분을 안다는 것만으로도 사내로서 제 마음은 당연히 더없이 흐뭇하고요. 그리고 그분께 이번 일을 알려 드리는 건 남편 된 제 마음으로선 당연히 더없이 흐뭇한 일이 될 겁니다.\"\n\n이런 식으로 자기 심정을 다 늘어놓은 다음, 케이크위그스 씨는 둘째 딸의 아마빛 땋은 머리를 매만지고는, 착한 아이가 되어서 언니 몰리나가 하는 말을 잘 들으라고 일렀어.\n\n\"저 아이는 날이 갈수록 제 엄마를 더 닮아 가네요.\" 럼비 씨가 갑자기 몰리나에게 열렬한 감탄이 솟구친 듯 말했어.\n\n\"거 봐요!\" 결혼한 부인이 맞장구를 쳤어. \"제가 늘 하는 말이에요. 늘 해 왔던 말이고요! 저 아이는 엄마를 빼다 박았다니까요.\" 이렇게 모두의 시선을 그 아가씨에게 쏠리게 한 다음, 결혼한 부인은 이 틈을 타 브랜디 탄 물을 한 모금 더 마셨어. 그것도 꽤나 긴 한 모금을 말이야.\n\n\"맞아요! 닮은 데가 있죠.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 말했어. \"하지만 케이크위그스 부인이 시집오기 전에 어땠는지! 세상에, 그런 여자가 또 없었다니까요!\"\n\n럼비 씨는 그녀가 어지간히 눈부신 미인이었나 보다 하는 듯이, 아주 엄숙하게 고개를 가로저었어.\n\n\"요정 얘기를 한다지만!\" 케이크위그스 씨가 외쳤어. \"그렇게 사뿐사뿐 가벼운 사람은 살아생전 본 적이 없어요, 한 번도. 게다가 그 몸가짐이라니, 그렇게 발랄하면서도 또 그렇게 빈틈없이 점잖았다니까요! 그 자태로 말하자면! 널리 알려진 일은 아니지만,\" 케이크위그스 씨가 목소리를 낮추며 말했어. \"그 시절 그 사람 자태가 얼마나 멋졌던지, 홀로웨이 로드 건너편 브리타니아 간판 그림을 그 사람을 본떠서 그렸을 정도라니까요!\"\n\n\"하지만 지금은 어떤지 좀 보세요.\" 결혼한 부인이 다그쳤어. \"저 사람이 여섯 아이의 엄마처럼 보이나요?\"\n\n\"말도 안 되죠.\" 의사가 외쳤어.\n\n\"오히려 제 딸이라고 해도 믿겠어요.\" 결혼한 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"그렇고말고요.\" 럼비 씨가 동의했어. \"훨씬 더 그렇죠.\"\n\n케이크위그스 씨가 이 의견에 힘을 보태려는 듯 뭔가 더 말을 보태려는 참이었어. 그때 또 다른 결혼한 부인이 — 케이크위그스 부인의 기운을 북돋우고, 또 돌아다니는 먹을 것 마실 것을 좀 치우는 걸 거들려고 들렀던 사람이지 — 머리를 들이밀고는, 자기가 방금 아래로 내려가 초인종에 응대했는데, 문 앞에 케이크위그스 씨를 \"꼭 좀\" 만나고 싶어 하는 신사 한 분이 와 있다고 알렸어.\n\n이 전갈을 듣자, 케이크위그스 씨의 머릿속에는 그 훌륭한 친척의 어렴풋한 모습이 어른거렸어. 그 영향을 받아서, 케이크위그스 씨는 당장 그 신사를 올려 보내라고 몰리나를 보냈지.\n\n\"아니, 이거 참.\" 케이크위그스 씨는 손님이 계단을 올라올 때 제일 먼저 얼굴을 볼 수 있도록 문 맞은편에 서서 말했어. \"존슨 씨 아니십니까! 어떻게 지내셨어요, 선생님?\"\n\n니콜라스는 악수를 하고, 옛 제자들에게 차례로 입을 맞추고는, 커다란 장난감 꾸러미 하나를 몰리나에게 맡겼어. 그리고 의사와 결혼한 부인들에게 인사를 하고는, 케이크위그스 부인의 안부를 진심 어린 어조로 물었어. 그 어조가 어찌나 살뜰했던지, 마침 작은 냄비에 무슨 알 수 없는 약을 데우러 난로 앞에 와 있던 산파의 마음 깊은 곳까지 울렸지.\n\n\"이런 때에 찾아와서 백번이라도 사과드려야 마땅하지요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그런데 초인종을 누르고 나서야 사정을 알았답니다. 게다가 요즘은 제가 시간이 너무 빠듯해서, 다시 들르려면 며칠은 걸릴까 봐 걱정이 됐거든요.\"\n\n\"지금만 한 때가 또 어디 있겠습니까, 선생님.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 말했어. \"케이크위그스 부인의 처지가, 선생님과 저 사이에 잠깐 이야기 나누는 데에 걸림돌이 되지는 않겠지요?\"\n\n\"참 너그러우십니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n바로 그때, 또 다른 결혼한 부인이 아기가 무섭게 먹기 시작했다고 외쳤어. 그러자 앞서 말한 두 결혼한 부인이 그 광경을 보겠다고 우르르 침실로 몰려갔지.\n\n\"사실은요.\" 니콜라스가 말을 이었어. \"제가 한동안 시골에 가 있었는데, 거길 떠나기 전에 선생님께 전갈 하나를 전해 드리기로 했거든요.\"\n\n\"아, 그래요?\" 케이크위그스 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"그런데 제가요,\" 니콜라스가 덧붙였어. \"벌써 며칠 전부터 시내에 와 있으면서도 그럴 기회가 통 없었네요.\"\n\n\"괜찮습니다, 선생님.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 말했어. \"좀 묵힌다고 나빠질 것도 없겠죠, 뭐. 시골에서 온 전갈이라!\" 케이크위그스 씨가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했어. \"그것참 묘하네요. 저는 시골에 아는 사람이 하나도 없는데요.\"\n\n\"피토커 양 말입니다.\" 니콜라스가 일러 줬어.\n\n\"오! 그 사람한테서 온 거로군요?\" 케이크위그스 씨가 말했어. \"아이고, 그렇죠. 아! 케이크위그스 부인이 그 사람 소식을 들으면 반가워할 거예요. 헨리에타 피토커 말이죠, 그렇죠? 세상일이 참 묘하게도 풀리네요! 선생님이 시골에서 그 사람을 만나다니! 거참!\"\n\n옛 친구의 이름이 나오는 걸 듣자, 케이크위그스 집안의 네 자매가 눈과 입을 떡 벌리고 니콜라스 둘레로 모여들어 더 들어 보려 했어. 케이크위그스 씨도 조금 궁금한 표정이었지만, 아주 편안하고 아무 의심도 없는 얼굴이었지.\n\n\"이 전갈은 집안일에 관한 겁니다.\" 니콜라스가 머뭇거리며 말했어.\n\n\"오, 괜찮아요.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 럼비 씨를 흘긋 보며 말했어. 럼비 씨는 경솔하게 어린 릴리빅을 떠맡았다가, 그 소중한 짐을 대신 받아 줄 사람을 아무도 찾지 못한 참이었지. \"여긴 다 친구들뿐이니까요.\"\n\n니콜라스는 한두 번 헛기침을 했어. 그러고는 말을 잇기가 좀 어려운 모양이었지.\n\n\"헨리에타 피토커는 포츠머스에 있지요.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 한마디 했어.\n\n\"네.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"릴리빅 씨도 거기 계세요.\"\n\n케이크위그스 씨의 얼굴이 새파래졌어. 하지만 그는 곧 정신을 추스르고는, 그것도 묘한 우연이라고 말했어.\n\n\"전갈은 그분에게서 온 겁니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n케이크위그스 씨는 다시 살아난 듯했어. 그는 자기 조카딸이 산달이 가까운 걸 알고 있었으니, 그분이 분명 자세한 사정을 알려 달라고 기별을 보낸 거라고 여겼지. 그래. 정말 친절한 분이야. 그분답기도 하고!\n\n\"그분이 저더러 가장 다정한 안부를 전해 달라고 하셨어요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"정말 고마운 분이지요. 너희들의 작은할아버지인 릴리빅 어른이시란다, 얘들아!\" 케이크위그스 씨가 자못 너그럽게 끼어들어 아이들에게 설명해 줬어.\n\n\"가장 다정한 안부를 전한다고요.\" 니콜라스가 말을 이었어. \"그리고 편지 쓸 짬이 없었지만, 자기가 피토커 양과 결혼했다는 말을 전해 달라고 하셨습니다.\"\n\n케이크위그스 씨는 돌처럼 굳어진 눈으로 자리에서 벌떡 일어났어. 그러고는 둘째 딸의 아마빛 땋은 머리를 움켜쥐고는, 손수건으로 얼굴을 가렸지. 몰리나는 어머니가 기절할 때 쓰러지던 모습을 본 그대로, 온몸을 뻣뻣하게 굳히고 아기 의자에 풀썩 쓰러졌어. 그리고 남은 두 어린 케이크위그스는 놀라서 비명을 질렀지.\n\n\"내 자식들, 사기당하고 등쳐 먹힌 내 어린것들!\" 케이크위그스 씨가 흥분에 못 이겨 둘째 딸의 아마빛 땋은 머리를 어찌나 세게 잡아당겼던지, 아이가 발끝으로 들려 올라가 몇 초 동안 그대로 매달려 있었어. \"악당, 멍청이, 배신자 같으니!\"\n\n\"이 양반이 정말!\" 산파가 화가 나서 둘러보며 외쳤어. \"여기서 이런 소동을 피우면 어쩌자는 거예요?\"\n\n\"조용히 하시오, 여편네!\" 케이크위그스 씨가 사납게 말했어.\n\n\"조용히 안 할 거예요.\" 산파가 받아쳤어. \"당신이나 조용히 해요, 이 못된 양반아. 당신 아기는 안중에도 없어요?\"\n\n\"없소!\" 케이크위그스 씨가 대답했어.\n\n\"그러니 더 부끄러운 줄 아세요.\" 산파가 쏘아붙였어. \"흥! 이 인정머리 없는 괴물 같으니.\"\n\n\"죽으라 그래.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 노여움이 쏟아져 나오는 와중에 외쳤어. \"죽으라 그래! 그 녀석은 이제 기대할 것도 없고, 물려받을 재산도 없어. 우리한텐 아기 따위 필요 없어.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 막무가내로 말했어. \"데려가, 다 데려가, 고아원으로 데려가 버려!\"\n\n이 끔찍한 말을 내뱉고는, 케이크위그스 씨는 의자에 털썩 앉아 산파한테 어디 한번 해 보라는 듯이 버텼어. 산파는 부랴부랴 옆방으로 들어가더니, 부인네들을 줄줄이 몰고 돌아왔지. 케이크위그스 씨가 자기 식구한테 못된 막말을 퍼부었으니, 분명 미쳐서 헛소리를 하는 게 틀림없다고 떠들면서 말이야.\n\n케이크위그스 씨에게 유리한 정황은 분명 아니었어. 그렇게 격하게 말을 쏟아 내면서도, 그 한탄이 케이크위그스 부인의 귀에 들어가지 않도록 목소리를 누르느라 애쓰다 보니, 그의 얼굴이 시커멓게 변해 버렸거든. 게다가 이 일로 한껏 들떠서, 그걸 자축하느라 평소 안 마시던 독한 술을 이것저것 들이켠 탓에, 얼굴이 영 보기 드물게 퉁퉁 부어오르고 벌게져 있었지. 하지만 니콜라스와 의사는 — 처음엔 케이크위그스 씨가 진심인지 어떤지 영 의심스러워 가만히 있었지만 — 그가 이렇게 된 직접적인 까닭을 설명하려고 끼어들었어. 그러자 부인네들의 분노는 동정으로 바뀌었고, 그들은 그에게 자뜩 마음을 담아, 조용히 가서 자라고 사정했지.\n\n\"내가 베푼 정성이라니,\" 케이크위그스 씨가 처량한 표정으로 둘러보며 말했어. \"그 인간한테 베푼 정성이라니! 이 집에서 그자가 먹어 치운 굴이며, 들이켠 에일이 몇 파인트인데—!\"\n\n\"정말 견디기 힘들고 참기 어려운 일이라는 거, 우리도 알아요.\" 결혼한 부인 중 하나가 말했어. \"하지만 사랑하는 부인을 생각하세요.\"\n\n\"오 그럼요, 그것도 바로 오늘 그 사람이 겪은 고생을 생각해 보세요.\" 여러 사람이 한꺼번에 외쳤어. \"착하시니까, 좀 그러세요.\"\n\n\"그 인간한테 안겨 준 선물이라니,\" 케이크위그스 씨가 다시 자기 비탄으로 돌아가 말했어. \"그 파이프며, 코담배 통이며, 육 실링 육 펜스나 주고 산 고무 덧신 한 켤레까지—\"\n\n\"아! 정말이지 생각만 해도 못 견딜 일이에요.\" 부인네들이 다 같이 외쳤어. \"하지만 그 인간한테도 죄다 되돌아갈 거예요, 걱정 마세요.\"\n\n케이크위그스 씨는 부인네들을 어두운 눈빛으로 바라봤어. 그래 봤자 자기한테 떨어질 게 하나도 없으니, 차라리 그게 다 자기한테 되돌아오는 편이 낫겠다는 듯이 말이야. 하지만 그는 아무 말도 않고, 손에 머리를 기댄 채 일종의 선잠 속으로 가라앉아 버렸어.\n\n그러자 부인네들은 다시, 이 착한 신사를 침대로 데려가는 게 좋겠다고 늘어놓았어. 내일이면 나아질 거라고, 또 케이크위그스 부인처럼 아내가 그날 그런 일을 겪을 때 어떤 남자들의 속이 얼마나 닳고 시달리는지 자기들도 안다고 말이야. 그리고 이건 그가 아주 갸륵한 거지, 부끄러워할 일이 결코 아니라고, 천만에, 오히려 보기 좋다고, 정말 보기 좋다고, 그건 마음이 착하다는 증거라고들 했지. 그리고 한 부인은 지금 이 경우에 딱 맞는 사례라며 이렇게 말했어. 자기 남편도 비슷한 일이 있을 때면 곧잘 걱정에 정신을 못 차린다고, 그래서 한번은 자기 어린 조니가 태어났을 때 거의 일주일이 지나서야 제정신으로 돌아왔는데, 그동안 내내 \"아들이오, 아들이오?\" 하고만 외쳐 댔다고, 듣는 사람들 마음을 다 울릴 만큼 그랬다고 말이야.\n\n마침내 몰리나가 — 아무도 자기를 알아봐 주지 않자 기절했던 일은 까맣게 잊어버린 채로 — 시름에 잠긴 아버지를 위해 방이 준비되었다고 알렸어. 케이크위그스 씨는 네 딸을 품에 너무 꼭 끌어안아 거의 숨이 막힐 지경으로 만든 다음, 한쪽으로는 의사의 팔에 기대고 다른 한쪽으로는 니콜라스의 부축을 받으며, 이번 일을 위해 마련해 둔 침실로 이 층으로 인도되어 갔지.\n\n니콜라스는 그가 푹 잠든 걸 확인하고, 더없이 만족스럽게 코를 고는 소리까지 듣고는, 거기다 장난감 나눠 주는 일까지 도맡아 어린 케이크위그스들이 모두 흡족해하는 걸 본 뒤에야 작별을 고했어. 부인네들은 하나둘씩 떠나갔지만, 밤새 묵기로 작정한 각별한 친구 예닐곱 명만은 남았지. 집집마다 불빛이 차츰 사라졌고, 케이크위그스 부인이 더 바랄 수 없을 만큼 잘 있다는 마지막 소식이 전해졌어. 그러고는 온 식구가 편히 쉬게 되었지.\n\n# CHAPTER 37\n\n치어리블 형제의 회계 사무소가 자리 잡은 그 광장은, 팀 링킨워터가 침이 마르도록 늘어놓는 찬사를 듣고 낯선 사람이 잔뜩 부풀려 그려 볼 법한 기대를 다 채워 주지는 못했어. 그래도 런던 같은 분주한 도시 한복판에 있는, 제법 그럴듯하고 아늑한 구석이긴 했지. 그리고 그 근처에 사는 점잖은 사람들 몇몇은 그곳을 애틋하게 기억하고 있었어. 다만 그 사람들의 기억은 훨씬 더 최근에 생긴 것이었고, 그곳을 향한 애착도 열성적인 팀의 기억과 애착에 비하면 한참 덜한 것이었지만 말이야.\n\n그로스베너 광장이나 하노버 광장의 귀족적인 무게감, 피츠로이 광장의 과부 같은 황량함과 쌀쌀맞음, 러셀 광장과 유스턴 광장의 자갈길과 정원 벤치에 익숙해진 사람들이라면, 팀 링킨워터나 이 동네를 그보다 덜 사랑하는 사람들의 애정이 잎사귀나 잔디 같은 것과의 정겨운 인연으로 깨어나 이어졌으리라고 생각하지는 말아야 해. 아무리 칙칙한 잎사귀라도, 아무리 헐벗고 듬성듬성한 잔디라도 말이야. 도시의 광장에는 한가운데에 가로등 기둥 말고는 따로 둘러친 울타리도 없고, 그 기둥 발치에 돋아난 잡초 말고는 잔디라고 할 것도 없거든. 거기는 조용하고 사람 발길이 드문, 외진 곳이야. 우울과 사색에, 그리고 오래 기다리는 약속에 딱 어울리는 곳이지. 그래서 약속한 사람은 광장의 사면을 위아래로 한 시간씩 멍하니 어슬렁거려. 반들반들 닳은 돌 위로 단조롭게 울리는 제 발소리로 메아리를 깨우면서, 처음엔 창문을, 그다음엔 자기를 빙 둘러싼 키 큰 침묵의 집들 벽돌까지 하나하나 세어 가면서 말이야. 겨울이면 분주한 거리와 큰길에서는 진작 녹아 버린 눈이 거기서는 오래오래 남아 있어. 여름 해도 그곳만큼은 좀 봐주는지, 광장 안으로는 밝은 햇살을 아껴서 비추고, 제 불타는 열기와 눈부심은 더 시끄럽고 덜 점잖은 동네에 쏟아붓거든. 어찌나 조용한지, 그 상쾌한 공기 속에서 더위를 식히려 걸음을 멈추면 제 회중시계 똑딱이는 소리까지 거의 들릴 정도야. 멀리서 웅웅거리는 소리가 들리긴 해. 벌레 소리가 아니라 마차 소리지. 하지만 그 말고는 어떤 소리도 광장의 고요를 깨뜨리지 않아. 짐꾼은 모퉁이 기둥에 한가로이 기대 있어. 푹푹 찌는 날인데도 그는 덥지 않고 그저 알맞게 따뜻하기만 하지. 그의 하얀 앞치마는 공중에서 나른하게 펄럭이고, 고개는 차츰 가슴께로 떨어지고, 두 눈을 한꺼번에 아주 길게 깜빡거려. 그조차도 이곳의 졸음을 부르는 기운을 견디지 못하고 차츰 잠에 빠져드는 거야. 그런데 갑자기 그가 화들짝 깨어나 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러서더니, 눈에 사납고 열띤 빛을 띠고 앞을 뚫어져라 바라봐. 일감이 생긴 건가, 아니면 구슬치기하는 아이가 나타난 건가? 유령이라도 본 건가, 풍금 소리라도 들은 건가? 아니야. 그보다 더 보기 드문 광경이지. 광장에 나비가 한 마리 있는 거야. 진짜로 살아 있는 나비가! 꽃과 단것들에서 길을 잃고, 먼지 낀 지하층 난간의 쇠창살 머리 사이를 팔랑팔랑 날아다니는 거지.\n\n하지만 치어리블 형제의 사무소 문밖에는 젊은 사무원의 눈길을 끌거나 생각을 흩어 놓을 만한 일이 그리 많지 않았어도, 문 안에는 그의 흥미를 돋우고 즐겁게 해 줄 것이 적지 않았어. 그곳에는 살아 있는 것이든 죽어 있는 것이든, 티머시 링킨워터 씨의 꼼꼼한 질서와 정확함을 어느 정도 닮지 않은 물건이 거의 없었거든. 그 늙은 사무원은 사무소 해시계만큼이나 정확했어. 그는 그 해시계가 런던에서 가장 정확한 시계라고 우겼지. 단, 근처에 숨어 있는, 아무도 모르는 어느 오래된 교회 시계 다음으로 말이야. (팀은 호스 가즈 시계가 좋다는 소문을, 시샘 많은 웨스트엔드 사람들이 지어낸 유쾌한 헛소리라고 여겼거든.) 그 늙은 사무원은 하루의 가장 자질구레한 행동 하나하나를, 또 그 작은 방의 가장 자질구레한 물건 하나하나를, 정확하고 규칙적인 순서대로 처리하고 정돈했어. 그 방이 정말로 진귀한 골동품을 넣어 둔 유리 진열장이었다 해도 그보다 더 잘 정돈할 수는 없었을 거야. 종이, 펜, 잉크, 자, 봉랍, 봉함지, 가루통, 끈 통, 부싯깃 통, 팀의 모자, 팀의 빈틈없이 개어 놓은 장갑, 팀의 또 다른 외투까지 — 벽에 걸려 있는 모양이 꼭 그의 뒷모습을 그대로 보는 것 같았지 — 모두가 정해진 자리를 한 치도 어김없이 차지하고 있었어. 시계를 빼고는, 문 뒤에 걸린 그 작은 온도계만큼 정확하고 흠잡을 데 없는 기구는 세상에 또 없었지. 그리고 온 세상을 다 뒤져도, 그 눈먼 검은지빠귀만큼 규칙적이고 사무적인 습관을 가진 새는 없었어. 그 새는 크고 아늑한 새장 안에서 꿈꾸듯 졸며 하루하루를 보냈는데, 팀이 처음 사 오기 여러 해 전에 이미 늙어서 목소리를 잃은 상태였어. 그리고 온갖 일화를 다 뒤져 봐도, 팀이 바로 그 새를 얻게 된 사연만큼 파란만장한 이야기는 없었지. 굶주려 고통받는 그 새의 처지가 가여워서, 그 비참한 목숨을 인정 많게 끝내 줄 셈으로 새를 사 왔다는 거야. 그러고는 사흘을 기다려 새가 살아나는지 보기로 마음먹었는데, 절반도 지나기 전에 새가 살아났다는 거지. 그리고 새는 점점 살아나서 입맛을 되찾고 보기 좋아지더니, 마침내 — \"지금 보시는 저 모습이 된 겁니다, 손님.\" 팀은 새장을 자랑스럽게 흘끔거리며 이렇게 말하곤 했어. 그러면서 팀은 곱고 맑은 소리로 짹짹 흉내를 내고는 \"딕!\" 하고 불렀지. 그러면 그때까지는 살아 있다는 기색이라곤 도무지 없어서, 그저 솜씨 없이 깎은 나무 지빠귀나 박제로밖에 안 보이던 딕이, 작은 깡충질 세 번으로 새장 옆으로 다가와서는, 부리를 창살 사이로 쑥 내밀고 앞 못 보는 머리를 옛 주인 쪽으로 돌리는 거야. 그 순간에는 둘 중 누가 더 행복한지, 그 새인지 팀 링킨워터인지 가리기가 참 어려웠어.\n\n이게 다가 아니었어. 게다가 모든 것이 형제의 다정한 마음씨를 어느 정도 비추고 있었지. 창고지기와 짐꾼들은 어찌나 튼튼하고 유쾌한 친구들인지, 보고 있으면 그저 흐뭇했어. 사무소 벽을 장식한 선박 공고문과 증기선 운항표 사이에는, 양로원 설계도며 자선 사업 명세서며 새 병원 도면이 끼어 있었어. 벽난로 위에는 나팔총 한 자루와 칼 두 자루가 걸려 있었지. 나쁜 짓 하는 자들을 겁주려는 것이었지만, 나팔총은 녹슬고 부서졌고, 칼은 부러지고 날도 없었어. 다른 데였다면 그런 꼴을 떡하니 걸어 놓은 게 웃음거리가 됐겠지만, 거기서는 그 사납고 위험한 무기들마저 그곳을 다스리는 기운에 물들어, 자비와 너그러움의 상징이 된 것만 같았어.\n\n니콜라스는 빈 의자를 처음 차지하고 앉던 날 아침에 이런 생각들이 아주 강하게 떠올랐어. 전에는 누려 보지 못한 여유로움으로 마음 편히 주위를 둘러보면서 말이야. 어쩌면 그런 생각들이 그를 북돋우고 일하게 만들었는지도 몰라. 그 뒤로 두 주 동안, 늦은 밤이든 이른 아침이든 그의 빈 시간은 모조리 부기와 그 밖의 몇 가지 상업 회계 방식의 비밀을 익히는 데 쉴 새 없이 바쳐졌거든. 그는 어찌나 꾸준하고 끈질기게 매달렸던지, 사실 그가 가진 사전 지식이라곤 학교 다닐 때 계산 공책에 적던 아주 긴 셈 두세 개에 대한 흐릿한 기억뿐이었는데도 — 그 공책은 글씨 선생이 손수 멋들어지게 그려 넣은 살찐 백조 그림으로 장식되어 부모님 검사를 무사히 통과하곤 했지 — 두 주 만에 링킨워터 씨에게 자신의 실력을 보고하고, 이제 니콜라스 니클비가 그의 더 중요한 일을 거들게 해 주겠다던 약속을 지켜 달라고 청할 만한 수준에 이르렀어.\n\n팀 링킨워터가 묵직한 원장과 일계장을 천천히 꺼내 오는 모습은 한번 볼만했어. 그것들을 이리저리 뒤집어 보고, 등과 옆면의 먼지를 애틋하게 털어 내고는, 여기저기 책장을 펼쳐 보며, 깨끗하고 얼룩 하나 없는 기입 내용을 반은 서글프게 반은 자랑스럽게 바라보는 거야.\n\n\"오는 오월이면 사십사 년째지!\" 팀이 말했어. \"그 뒤로 새 원장이 참 많기도 했어. 사십사 년이라니!\"\n\n팀은 다시 책을 덮었어.\n\n\"자, 자.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"어서 시작하고 싶어서 좀이 쑤시는걸요.\"\n\n팀 링킨워터는 부드럽게 나무라는 기색으로 고개를 저었어. 니클비 씨는 이 일이 얼마나 깊고 엄숙한 일인지를 충분히 깨닫지 못하고 있었거든. 혹시라도 잘못 적기라도 하면 — 그래서 지워 내야 하기라도 하면 어쩔 셈인가!\n\n젊은이는 무모한 법이지. 가끔 그들이 무슨 일에 겁 없이 덤벼드는지 보면 참 놀라워. 니콜라스는 의자에 앉는 조심성조차 부리지 않고, 책상 앞에 느긋하게 선 채로, 얼굴에 미소까지 띠고서 — 정말로 미소였어, 그건 틀림없었지, 링킨워터 씨가 나중에 자주 그 얘기를 했거든 — 앞에 놓인 잉크병에 펜을 푹 담그더니, 치어리블 형제의 장부 속으로 곧장 뛰어들었어!\n\n팀 링킨워터는 얼굴이 하얗게 질려서, 니콜라스 쪽에 가까운 의자 두 다리를 들어 기우뚱 기대고는, 숨도 못 쉴 만큼 마음을 졸이며 그의 어깨너머를 들여다봤어. 그때 찰스 형과 네드 형이 함께 사무소로 들어왔어. 하지만 팀 링킨워터는 돌아보지도 않고 손을 다급하게 내저어, 쥐 죽은 듯 조용히 해야 한다고 신호를 보냈어. 그러고는 그 서툰 펜촉의 움직임을 한껏 긴장한 간절한 눈으로 좇았지.\n\n형제는 웃는 얼굴로 지켜봤지만, 팀 링킨워터는 몇 분 동안 웃지도, 움직이지도 않았어. 마침내 그는 길고 느리게 숨을 한 번 내쉬고는, 여전히 기우뚱 기댄 의자 위에서 자세를 그대로 유지한 채, 찰스 형을 흘끔 보더니, 펜 깃털로 니콜라스를 슬쩍 가리키고는, 진지하고 단호하게 고개를 끄덕였어. \"이 친구, 되겠어.\" 하고 말하는 게 분명했지.\n\n찰스 형도 마주 고개를 끄덕이고는 네드 형과 웃음 띤 눈빛을 주고받았어. 그런데 바로 그때 니콜라스가 다른 쪽을 참고하려고 손을 멈췄지. 그러자 팀 링킨워터는 더 이상 흐뭇함을 참지 못하고 의자에서 내려와, 황홀해하며 그의 손을 덥석 잡았어.\n\n\"해냈어요!\" 팀이 고용주들을 돌아보며 의기양양하게 고개를 저으며 말했어. \"이 친구가 쓴 대문자 B하고 D가 제 것하고 똑같아요. 작은 i마다 점을 다 찍고, t를 쓸 때마다 가로획도 다 그어요. 온 런던을 다 뒤져도 이런 젊은이는 없어요.\" 팀은 니콜라스의 등을 탁탁 두드리며 말했어. \"단 한 명도요. 어림없는 소리! 이 도시는 이만한 짝을 못 만들어 내요. 어디 한번 만들어 보라고 시내 전체에 도전하겠어요!\"\n\n이렇게 도전장을 던지며, 팀 링킨워터는 꽉 쥔 주먹으로 책상을 어찌나 세게 내리쳤던지, 그 바람에 깜짝 놀란 늙은 검은지빠귀가 횃대에서 떨어지면서, 너무 놀란 나머지 정말로 가냘프게 깍 하고 울음소리까지 냈어.\n\n\"잘 말했네, 팀. 잘 말했어, 팀 링킨워터!\" 찰스 형이 외쳤어. 팀 못지않게 기뻐하며, 말하는 동안 부드럽게 손뼉을 쳤지. \"나는 우리 젊은 친구가 무던히 애쓸 줄 알았고, 금세 해낼 거라고 확신했지. 안 그런가, 네드?\"\n\n\"그렇게 말했지, 형님. 정말이지, 형님이 그렇게 말했고, 그 말이 딱 맞았어.\" 네드가 대답했어. \"딱 맞았지. 팀 링킨워터가 흥분하긴 했지만, 흥분할 만하니까, 마땅히 흥분할 만하니까 흥분한 거야. 팀은 참 좋은 친구야. 팀 링킨워터 씨, 자네는 참 좋은 사람이야.\"\n\n\"이거 생각만 해도 흐뭇한 일이지요!\" 팀은 자기한테 하는 이 말은 아랑곳하지 않고, 원장에서 형제에게로 안경을 들어 올리며 말했어. \"참 흐뭇한 일이에요. 제가 떠나고 나면 이 장부들이 어떻게 될까, 제가 그걸 자주 생각하지 않았을 것 같으세요? 제가 가고 나면 여기 일이 들쭉날쭉하고 엉망이 될지 모른다고 자주 걱정하지 않았을 것 같으세요? 그런데 이제는,\" 팀이 니콜라스 쪽으로 집게손가락을 뻗으며 말했어. \"이제는, 이 친구한테 조금만 더 가르쳐 놓으면, 마음이 놓여요. 제가 죽고 나서도 일은, 제가 살아 있을 때만큼 잘 굴러갈 거예요. 똑같이요. 그러면 저는, 치어리블 형제의 장부 같은 장부는 일찍이 없었다는 걸 알고 흐뭇하게 갈 수 있어요. 그런 장부는 없었어요! 아니, 앞으로도 그런 장부는 없을 거예요. 치어리블 형제의 장부 같은 건요.\"\n\n이렇게 속마음을 털어놓고 나서, 링킨워터 씨는 짧게 웃음을 한 번 터뜨렸어. 런던 시와 웨스트민스터 시 전체에 도전하는 듯한 웃음이었지. 그러고는 다시 책상으로 돌아앉아, 방금 더하던 마지막 세로 칸에서 칠십육을 조용히 받아 올리고는, 하던 일을 계속했어.\n\n\"팀 링킨워터 씨.\" 찰스 형이 말했어. \"손 좀 이리 주게. 오늘이 자네 생일이잖나. 자네한테 생일 축하 인사도 하기 전에, 어떻게 감히 딴 얘기를 꺼낼 수 있겠나, 팀 링킨워터? 하느님이 자네를 축복하시길, 팀! 하느님의 축복이 있기를!\"\n\n\"형님.\" 다른 노인이 팀의 빈 주먹을 마저 잡으며 말했어. \"팀 링킨워터가 지난 생일 때보다 열 살은 더 젊어 보이는데.\"\n\n\"네드, 이 친구야.\" 또 다른 노인이 대꾸했어. \"내 생각에 팀 링킨워터는 백쉰 살로 태어나서 차츰 스물다섯 살로 내려오고 있는 것 같아. 생일이 돌아올 때마다 작년보다 더 젊어지니 말이야.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고, 형님. 그렇고말고.\" 네드 형이 대답했어. \"거기엔 의심의 여지가 없지.\"\n\n\"잊지 말게, 팀.\" 찰스 형이 말했어. \"오늘은 두 시가 아니라 다섯 시 반에 저녁을 먹는다는 걸 말이야. 이 기념일에는 늘 평소 습관을 깨잖나, 자네도 잘 알다시피, 팀 링킨워터. 니클비 씨, 자네도 함께하게. 팀 링킨워터, 정 많고 충직한 이 녀석을 찰스 형하고 내가 기억할 수 있게, 자네 코담배갑을 주게. 그리고 그 대신 이걸 받게나. 우리의 존경과 애정을 담은 보잘것없는 표시일세. 잠자리에 들기 전에는 열어 보지 말고, 이 얘긴 더 이상 입에 올리지 말게. 안 그러면 저 검은지빠귀를 죽여 버릴 테니까. 아이고, 이 양반아! 저 새한테나 그 주인한테 조금이라도 더 기쁨이 됐을 거라면, 육 년 전에 진작 금새장을 사 줬어야 했어. 자, 네드, 이 친구야, 나는 준비됐네. 다섯 시 반이야, 잊지 말게, 니클비 씨! 팀 링킨워터 씨, 다섯 시 반에 니클비 씨 잘 챙기게. 자, 가세, 네드.\"\n\n이렇게 늘 하던 대로 쉴 새 없이 떠들어 댄 건, 상대편에서 고맙다거나 하는 인사를 도무지 꺼낼 틈을 주지 않으려는 것이었지. 쌍둥이 형제는 값비싼 금 코담배갑을 팀 링킨워터에게 안겨 주고는 — 그 안에는 갑값의 열 배도 넘는 은행권 한 장이 들어 있었어 — 팔짱을 끼고 종종걸음으로 떠났어.\n\n다섯 시 십오 분, 한 치도 어김없이 정각에, 해마다 그랬듯이 팀 링킨워터의 누이가 도착했어. 그리고 팀 링킨워터의 누이와 늙은 가정부 사이에는 한바탕 소동이 벌어졌지. 팀 링킨워터의 누이가 묵고 있는 집에서 심부름 아이 편에 보낸 모자가 아직 도착하지 않은 탓이었어. 모자는 모자 상자에 담겨 있었고, 그 상자는 손수건에 싸여 있었고, 그 손수건은 아이의 팔에 묶여 있었는데도 말이야. 게다가 모자를 보낼 곳을 낡은 편지 뒷면에 빠짐없이 길게 적어 두었고, 아이한테는 사람 눈으로는 미처 다 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 온갖 무시무시한 벌을 주겠다고 으름장을 놓으며, 되도록 빨리 갖다주고 도중에 한눈팔지 말라고 단단히 일러 두었는데도 그랬어. 팀 링킨워터의 누이는 탄식했고, 가정부는 위로했어. 그리고 둘은 아이가 \"오고 있는지\" 보려고 연신 이층 창밖으로 고개를 내밀었지. 모퉁이까지의 거리가 채 다섯 발짝도 안 됐으니, 아이가 오는 게 보이기만 하면 그건 아주 흡족한 일이었고, 따지고 보면 도착한 거나 마찬가지였거든. 그런데 바로 그때, 전혀 기대하지 않은 순간에, 심부름꾼이 모자 상자를 조심조심 받쳐 들고 정반대 방향에서 나타났어. 헉헉 숨을 몰아쉬며, 방금 한바탕 뛰어다닌 탓에 얼굴이 벌게져서 말이야. 그럴 만도 했지. 그 아이는 우선 캠버웰로 가는 삯마차 뒤꽁무니에 매달려 바람을 쐤고, 그다음엔 펀치 인형극 두 패를 졸졸 따라다녔고, 죽마 탄 광대들이 제 집 문 앞까지 가는 걸 배웅하고 왔거든. 그래도 모자는 멀쩡했으니 — 그게 하나의 위안이었지 — 그리고 야단쳐 봐야 소용없으니 — 그게 또 하나의 위안이었어. 그래서 아이는 신이 나서 제 갈 길을 갔고, 팀 링킨워터의 누이는 팀 링킨워터의 그 한 치도 틀림없는 시계로 정각 삼십 분이 친 지 딱 오 분 만에 아래층 손님들 앞에 모습을 드러냈어.\n\n손님은 치어리블 형제, 팀 링킨워터, 팀의 혈색 좋고 머리가 하얀 친구 한 명(은퇴한 은행 사무원이었지), 그리고 니콜라스로 이루어져 있었어. 니콜라스는 아주 진지하고 엄숙하게 팀 링킨워터의 누이에게 소개되었지. 이제 일행이 다 모이자, 네드 형이 종을 울려 저녁을 청했고, 곧 저녁 준비가 다 됐다는 알림이 오자, 팀 링킨워터의 누이를 옆방으로 이끌었어. 거기에는 푸짐하게 준비한 식사가 차려져 있었지. 그러고는 네드 형이 식탁 상석에, 찰스 형이 말석에 앉았어. 팀 링킨워터의 누이는 네드 형의 왼편에, 팀 링킨워터 본인은 그의 오른편에 앉았지. 그리고 중풍 걸린 듯한 인상에 다리가 아주 짧은 늙은 집사가 네드 형의 안락의자 뒤에 자리를 잡고는, 멋들어진 동작으로 음식 덮개를 벗길 채비로 오른팔을 한 번 휘젓고서, 꼿꼿이 선 채로 꼼짝도 하지 않았어.\n\n\"이것들과 그 밖의 모든 은혜에 대하여, 찰스 형.\" 네드가 말했어.\n\n\"주여, 우리를 진실로 감사하게 하소서, 네드.\" 찰스가 말했어.\n\n그러자 중풍 걸린 듯한 집사가 수프 그릇 뚜껑을 휙 벗기더니, 별안간 격렬하게 부산을 떨기 시작했어.\n\n대화는 넘쳐 났고, 끊길 걱정 따위는 거의 없었어. 그 멋진 노쌍둥이의 좋은 기분이 모두의 입을 열게 했거든. 그리고 팀 링킨워터의 누이는 샴페인 첫 잔을 마시자마자 팀 링킨워터의 어린 시절 이야기를 길고 자세하게 늘어놓기 시작했어. 자기는 팀보다 훨씬 어려서, 이 일들은 그저 집안에 전해 내려오는 이야기로만 알게 됐다는 걸 미리 밝히는 것도 잊지 않았지. 이 이야기가 끝나자, 네드 형이 다른 이야기를 꺼냈어. 정확히 삼십오 년 전에, 팀 링킨워터가 연애편지를 받은 것 같다는 의심을 산 적이 있었다는 거야. 그리고 그가 보기 드물게 어여쁜 노처녀와 칩사이드 거리를 걷는 걸 누가 봤다는 어렴풋한 소식이 사무소까지 들려왔다는 거지. 그 말에 한바탕 웃음이 터졌고, 팀 링킨워터는 얼굴을 붉혔다는 추궁을 받으며 해명하라는 요구를 받았어. 그러자 그는 그 비난이 사실이 아니라고 부인했고, 한술 더 떠 설령 사실이었다 한들 그게 무슨 흠 될 일이냐고 했지. 이 마지막 말에 은퇴한 은행 사무원이 어찌나 크게 웃어 댔는지, 평생 들어 본 것 중에 가장 멋진 말이라면서, 팀 링킨워터가 앞으로 무슨 말을 백 번을 한들 그 말을 이길 말은 못 할 거라고 했어.\n\n그날에만 있는 자그마한 의식이 하나 있었는데, 그 내용도 방식도 니콜라스에게 아주 깊은 인상을 남겼어. 식탁보가 걷히고 술병이 처음으로 한 바퀴 돌고 나자, 깊은 침묵이 뒤따랐어. 그리고 형제의 환한 얼굴에는, 완전한 우울은 아니지만, 잔칫상에서는 좀처럼 보기 힘든 조용한 사색의 빛이 떠올랐지. 니콜라스가 이 갑작스러운 변화에 마음이 쓰여 이게 무슨 뜻인가 궁금해하고 있는데, 형제가 함께 일어섰어. 그리고 식탁 상석에 앉은 쪽이 다른 쪽을 향해 몸을 기울이고는, 마치 그 한 사람에게만 말하듯 낮은 목소리로 이렇게 말했어.\n\n\"찰스 형, 이 친구야, 오늘과 얽혀 있는 또 다른 기억이 하나 있지. 그건 형이나 나나 결코 잊어서는 안 되고, 또 결코 잊을 수도 없는 거야. 오늘은 더없이 충직하고 훌륭하고 본받을 만한 한 친구를 이 세상에 데려온 날이지만, 동시에 더없이 자애롭고 가장 좋은 부모님을, 우리 둘에게 더없이 좋은 부모님을 이 세상에서 데려간 날이기도 해. 어머니가 우리가 잘된 모습을 보시고, 그걸 함께 누리시고, 우리가 가난한 두 소년이었을 때 그랬던 것처럼 그때도 우리가 어머니를 얼마나 사랑했는지 아셨더라면 얼마나 좋았을까. 하지만 그럴 수는 없었지. 형, 우리 어머니를 추억하며.\"\n\n'세상에,' 니콜라스는 생각했어. '이 모든 걸, 아니 이보다 만 배는 더한 것까지 다 알면서도, 이분들과 같은 신분의 사람 수십 명이 이분들을 저녁에 초대조차 않는단 말이지. 그저 칼로 음식을 떠먹고 학교를 안 다녔다는 이유로!'\n\n하지만 도덕을 곱씹을 새는 없었어. 다시 흥겨운 분위기가 아주 활기차게 살아났거든. 그리고 포트와인 병이 거의 비자, 네드 형이 종을 당겼고, 그 즉시 중풍 걸린 듯한 집사가 대답하며 나타났어.\n\n\"데이비드.\" 네드 형이 말했어.\n\n\"네, 어르신.\" 집사가 대답했어.\n\n\"더블 다이아몬드 큰 병으로 하나 가져오게, 데이비드. 링킨워터 씨의 건강을 위해 마실 테니.\"\n\n그러자 그 자리에 있는 모두가 감탄하는, 게다가 지난 몇 해 동안 해마다 감탄해 온 솜씨로, 중풍 걸린 듯한 집사가 등 뒤 허리께에서 왼손을 척 내밀었어. 그 손에는 이미 코르크 따개가 꽂힌 병이 들려 있었지. 그는 한 번에 코르크를 쑥 뽑고는, 자기 솜씨를 흐뭇하게 의식하는 위엄을 풍기며 큰 병과 코르크를 주인 앞에 내려놓았어.\n\n\"하!\" 네드 형이 먼저 코르크를 살펴보고는 잔을 채우며 말했어. 그동안 늙은 집사는 흐뭇하고 상냥하게 지켜보고 있었지. 마치 그게 다 제 것인데 손님들이 마음껏 들어도 좋다는 듯한 표정으로 말이야. \"이거 좋아 보이는데, 데이비드.\"\n\n\"좋을 수밖에요, 어르신.\" 데이비드가 대답했어. \"우리 더블 다이아몬드만 한 술은 어디 가서도 찾기 힘드실 겁니다. 그건 링킨워터 씨도 잘 아시고요. 이 술은 링킨워터 씨가 처음 오셨을 때 담가 둔 거예요. 바로 그 술입죠, 여러분.\"\n\n\"아니야, 데이비드, 아니야.\" 찰스 형이 끼어들었어.\n\n\"제가 직접 지하 저장고 장부에 적어 넣었습니다, 어르신, 죄송하지만요.\" 데이비드가, 제 사실에 한 치의 흔들림도 없다는 사람의 어조로 말했어. \"이 더블 다이아몬드 통을 담갔을 때, 링킨워터 씨는 여기 오신 지 딱 이십 년밖에 안 됐을 때였습니다, 어르신.\"\n\n\"데이비드 말이 딱 맞아, 딱 맞아, 찰스 형.\" 네드가 말했어. \"사람들 왔나, 데이비드?\"\n\n\"문밖에 와 있습니다, 어르신.\" 집사가 대답했어.\n\n\"들여보내게, 데이비드, 들여보내.\"\n\n이 분부에, 늙은 집사는 깨끗한 잔이 담긴 작은 쟁반을 주인 앞에 놓고는, 문을 열어 니콜라스가 아래층에서 봤던 그 유쾌한 짐꾼과 창고지기들을 들였어. 모두 넷이었는데, 그들이 절을 하고 싱글거리고 얼굴을 붉히며 들어오자, 그 뒤를 이어 가정부와 요리사와 하녀가 따라 들어왔지.\n\n\"일곱.\" 네드 형이 그만큼의 잔에 더블 다이아몬드를 채우며 말했어. \"그리고 데이비드까지 여덟. 자! 이제 모두들, 너희들의 가장 좋은 친구인 티머시 링킨워터 씨의 건강을 위해 마시거라. 그리고 그의 건강과 만수무강과, 오늘 같은 날이 여러 번 돌아오기를 빌어 주거라. 그를 위해서도, 또 그를 더없이 값진 보배로 여기는 너희 옛 주인들을 위해서도 말이야. 팀 링킨워터 씨, 자네의 건강을 위하여. 빌어먹을 친구 같으니, 팀 링킨워터 씨, 하느님의 축복이 있기를.\"\n\n이렇게 앞뒤가 안 맞는 묘한 말을 하며, 네드 형은 팀 링킨워터의 등을 한 대 철썩 때렸어. 그 바람에 팀은 그 순간 집사 못지않게 중풍이라도 걸린 듯한 얼굴이 됐지. 그러고는 네드 형은 눈 깜짝할 사이에 잔을 쭉 비웠어.\n\n팀 링킨워터를 위한 축배가 한껏 격식을 갖춰 다 들이켜지기도 전에, 그 가운데 가장 튼튼하고 유쾌한 아랫사람이 동료들보다 조금 앞으로 팔꿈치로 비집고 나섰어. 그러고는 아주 화끈거리고 벌게진 얼굴을 내보이며, 이마 한가운데 난 잿빛 머리카락 한 줌을 잡아당겨 좌중에 공손히 인사를 하고는, 이렇게 입을 열었어. 그러면서 파란 무명 손수건에 두 손바닥을 아주 세게 비벼 대면서 말이야.\n\n\"저희가 일 년에 한 번 버릇없이 굴어도 되는 날입죠, 여러분. 괜찮으시다면 지금 그래 보겠습니다. 지금만 한 때가 또 없고, 손안의 두 마리 새가 덤불 속 한 마리만 못하다는 건 다들 잘 아실 테니까요. 적어도 거꾸로 따지면 그렇다는 거고, 뜻은 매한가지입죠. (잠시 멈춤 — 집사는 납득하지 못함.) 저희가 드리려는 말씀은, 그러니까 일찍이 (집사를 보며) — 이렇게나 — (요리사를 보며) 고결하고 — 훌륭하고 — (여기저기 둘러보지만 아무도 안 봄) 너그럽고 마음 넓으신 주인 어르신들은 없었다는 겁니다. 오늘 저희를 이토록 후하게 대해 주신 분들 말입죠. 그리고 어디에나 한결같이 베풀어 주시는 그 모든 은혜에 감사드리며, 어르신들께서 오래오래 사시고 복되게 가시기를 비는 바입니다!\"\n\n이 연설이 끝나자 — 훨씬 더 우아하면서도 훨씬 덜 장황할 수도 있었겠지만 — 중풍 걸린 듯한 집사를 우두머리로 한 아랫사람들 전체가 조용히 만세 삼창을 했어. 그런데 그게 집사의 큰 분통을 살 만큼 영 가지런하지 못했어. 여자들이 박자는 아랑곳없이, 자기네들끼리 가늘고 높은 만세 소리를 끝도 없이 질러 댔거든. 이게 끝나자 그들은 물러갔어. 얼마 안 있어 팀 링킨워터의 누이도 물러갔고, 그러고 적당히 시간이 지나자 자리는 차와 커피, 그리고 다 함께 둘러앉아 하는 카드놀이를 위해 파했지.\n\n열 시 반에 — 이 광장으로서는 늦은 시각이지 — 샌드위치가 담긴 작은 쟁반 하나와 비숍 한 사발이 나왔어. 그 비숍이 더블 다이아몬드 위에 거기다 다른 흥까지 더해 들어가니, 팀 링킨워터한테 어떤 효과를 냈냐면, 그가 니콜라스를 한쪽으로 끌고 가더니, 은밀히 털어놨어. 보기 드물게 어여뻤다는 그 노처녀 이야기가 다 사실이라는 거야. 그리고 그 여자가 소문대로 더없이 아름다웠다고, 아니, 사실은 그 이상이었다고 했어. 하지만 그 여자가 너무 서둘러 처지를 바꾸려는 통에, 팀이 구애를 하며 자기 처지를 바꿔 볼까 고민하는 사이에, 다른 사람하고 결혼해 버렸다는 거지. \"따지고 보면 내 잘못이었겠지.\" 팀이 말했어. \"조만간 위층에 있는 판화 한 장을 보여 주겠네. 이십오 실링이나 주고 산 거야. 우리가 서먹해진 직후에 샀지. 남한텐 말하지 말게나, 그런데 그게 자네가 평생 본 것 중에 가장 기막히게 우연히 닮은 그림이거든. 바로 그 여자 초상이야, 손님!\"\n\n이 무렵엔 열한 시가 넘어 있었어. 그리고 팀 링킨워터의 누이가 한 시간도 더 전에 집에 갔어야 했다고 말하자, 마차를 한 대 불러 왔지. 네드 형이 아주 정중하게 누이를 마차에 태워 주는 동안, 찰스 형은 마부에게 가장 자세하게 길을 일러 줬어. 그리고 그 부인을 더없이 정성껏 모시라며 삯에 더해 일 실링을 얹어 주는 데다, 보기 드물게 독한 술을 한 잔 먹여 마부의 숨통을 막을 뻔했지. 그러고는 그 숨을 다시 불어넣어 주겠다고 기를 쓰며 마부의 몸에서 남은 숨까지 죄다 두들겨 빼낼 뻔했어.\n\n마침내 마차가 덜컹거리며 떠났고, 팀 링킨워터의 누이가 이제 무사히 집으로 가는 길에 오르자, 니콜라스와 팀 링킨워터의 친구는 함께 작별 인사를 하고는, 늙은 팀과 그 갸륵한 형제를 남겨 두고 떠났어. 그들이 편히 쉬도록 말이야.\n\n니콜라스는 걸어야 할 거리가 좀 되었던 터라, 집에 닿았을 때는 자정이 한참 지나 있었어. 집에서는 어머니와 스마이크가 자지 않고 그를 기다리고 있었지. 평소 잠자리에 들 시각을 한참 넘긴 데다, 두 사람은 아무리 늦어도 두 시간 전에는 그가 올 줄로 알았거든. 그래도 시간이 지루하지는 않았어. 니클비 부인이 외가 쪽 집안 내력을 족보 풀어 가며 스마이크한테 들려줬거든. 주요 인물들의 약전까지 곁들여서 말이야. 그리고 스마이크는 그게 다 무슨 얘긴지, 책에서 배운 건지 아니면 니클비 부인이 머릿속에서 지어낸 건지 궁금해하며 앉아 있었지. 그렇게 둘은 아주 즐겁게 시간을 보냈어.\n\n니콜라스는 치어리블 형제의 훌륭함과 후한 인심을 한참 늘어놓고, 그날 자기 노력이 거둔 큰 성공을 들려주지 않고서는 도무지 잠자리에 들 수가 없었어. 그런데 그가 채 열 마디도 하기 전에, 니클비 부인이 짐짓 눈을 찡긋찡긋, 고개를 끄덕끄덕하더니, 스마이크 씨가 분명 몹시 지쳤을 거라며, 단 한순간도 더 앉아 있게 둘 수 없다고 한사코 우겼어.\n\n\"참, 어찌나 말 잘 듣는 아이인지.\" 스마이크가 잘 자라는 인사를 하고 방을 나가자 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"네가 이해해 줄 줄 안다, 니콜라스. 그런데 나는 제삼자 앞에서 이걸 하는 게 영 싫구나. 사실 젊은 남자 앞이라면 그리 점잖은 일은 못 되지. 하긴 따지고 보면 그게 뭐 그리 흠 될 일인가 싶기도 하다만. 그저 그리 보기 좋은 건 아니니까. 그래도 어떤 사람들은 아주 보기 좋다고 하던데, 사실 잘 만들기만 하고 가장자리 주름만 곱게 잡으면 안 될 게 또 뭐냐 싶기도 하고. 물론 그건 만들기 나름이겠지만 말이다.\"\n\n이런 서두를 늘어놓고서, 니클비 부인은 아주 큰 기도서 책장 사이에 작게 접어 끼워 두었던 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자를 꺼내, 끈을 매기 시작했어. 그 내내 늘 하던 대로 두서없이 떠들어 대면서 말이야.\n\n\"남들이야 뭐라든,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"잠잘 때 쓰는 모자에는 큰 편안함이 있단다. 너도 분명 인정할 거다, 니콜라스. 네 것에도 끈만 달아서, 청색 자선학교 아이마냥 정수리에 얹기만 하지 말고, 사람답게 제대로 쓰기만 한다면 말이야. 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자에 까다롭게 구는 게 남자답지 못하다거나 우습다고 여길 건 없어. 네 가엾은 아버지하고, 또 저, 이름이 뭐더라 하는 그 목사님이 — 네가 태어나기 일주일 전날 밤에 풍향계가 바람에 날아가 버린, 그 묘하고 자그마한 첨탑이 달린 오래된 교회에서 기도문을 읽어 주시던 분 말이다 — 그분들이 이런 말씀 하시는 걸 자주 들었거든. 대학에 다니는 젊은이들은 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자에 유난히 까다롭다고. 그리고 옥스퍼드 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자는 튼튼하고 좋기로 아주 유명하다고. 어찌나 그런지, 젊은이들이 그걸 안 쓰고는 잠자리에 들 엄두도 못 낸다더구나. 그 사람들이 좋은 게 뭔지 알고, 제 몸 함부로 다루지 않는다는 건 누구나 인정하는 바라고 나는 믿는다.\"\n\n니콜라스는 웃었어. 그리고 이 길어진 일장 연설의 주제로는 더 이상 발을 들이지 않고, 자그마한 생일 잔치의 즐거운 이야기로 화제를 되돌렸어. 그러자 니클비 부인이 그 일에 부쩍 호기심을 보이며, 저녁으로 뭘 먹었는지, 상은 어떻게 차렸는지, 너무 익었는지 덜 익었는지, 누가 왔는지, '그 체리블 씨네 분들'은 뭐라고 했는지, 니콜라스는 뭐라고 했는지, 그가 그 말을 하니까 체리블 씨네 분들은 또 뭐라고 했는지, 수없이 캐물었어. 그래서 니콜라스는 잔치며 그날 아침의 일들까지 죄다 자세히 들려주었지.\n\n\"늦긴 했지만,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"케이트가 일어나 있어서 이 얘기를 다 들었더라면 싶어요. 저, 욕심이 좀 나는걸요. 오는 길 내내 동생한테 얘기해 주고 싶어서 좀이 쑤셨거든요.\"\n\n\"아, 케이트 말이냐.\" 니클비 부인이 두 발을 난로 가리개에 올리고, 의자를 바짝 끌어당기며 말했어. 긴 이야기를 할 채비를 하는 것처럼 말이야. \"케이트는 잠자리에 든 지 — 아! 두어 시간은 됐단다. 그리고 나는 그 애를 깨어 있지 않게 한 게 참 다행이구나, 니콜라스. 너한테 몇 마디 할 기회가 정말이지 무척 갖고 싶었거든. 이건 당연히 내가 걱정스러운 일이고, 또 믿고 의논할 수 있는 다 큰 아들이 있다는 건 더없이 기쁘고 위로가 되는 일이지. 사실 사람이 아들을 믿지 못한다면, 도대체 아들이 무슨 소용인가 싶다.\"\n\n니콜라스는 졸린 하품을 하다 말고 멈췄어. 어머니가 말을 꺼내자, 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라봤지.\n\n\"우리 동네에 한 부인이 살았는데,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"아들 얘기가 나오니까 생각나는구나. 우리가 돌리시 근처에 살 때 동네에 살던 부인인데, 이름이 로저스였던 것 같아. 아니, 머피가 아니라면 분명 로저스였어. 내가 헷갈리는 건 딱 그 하나뿐이니까.\"\n\n\"어머니가 저한테 하시려던 얘기가 그 부인에 관한 거예요?\" 니콜라스가 조용히 물었어.\n\n\"그 부인이라니!\" 니클비 부인이 외쳤어. \"아이고, 니콜라스, 어쩜 그리 엉뚱하니! 그런데 그게 꼭 네 가엾은 아버지 버릇이었어. 딱 그 버릇이지. 늘 딴 데로 새고, 두 시간은커녕 단 한 가지 일에도 생각을 붙들어 매지 못했어. 지금도 그이가 눈에 선하구나!\" 니클비 부인이 눈가를 훔치며 말했어. \"내가 그이 일에 대해 얘기하고 있으면, 그이는 나를 빤히 쳐다봤지. 머릿속 생각이 죄다 뒤죽박죽 엉켜 있는 사람처럼 말이야! 누가 우리한테 불쑥 들어왔다면, 내가 일을 더 분명하게 해 주기는커녕 그이를 헷갈리게 하고 정신을 흩어 놓는 줄로 알았을 거다. 정말이지 그랬을 거야.\"\n\n\"제가 이 안타까운 굼뜬 머리를 물려받았다니 정말 죄송해요, 어머니.\" 니콜라스가 다정하게 말했어. \"하지만 어머니가 곧장 본론만 말씀해 주신다면, 알아들으려고 최선을 다할게요. 정말로요.\"\n\n\"네 가엾은 아버지!\" 니클비 부인이 곰곰이 생각하며 말했어. \"그이는 너무 늦어 버릴 때까지도, 내가 그이한테 뭘 시키고 싶어 했는지 도통 몰랐단다!\"\n\n이건 의심할 바 없이 사실이었어. 고인이 된 니클비 씨가 죽을 때까지도 그걸 알아내지 못했으니까 말이야. 니클비 부인 자신도 몰랐고. 어찌 보면 그게 이 일에 대한 설명이 되겠지.\n\n\"하지만,\" 니클비 부인이 눈물을 닦으며 말했어. \"이건 옆집 그 신사하고는 아무 상관도 없어. 정말이지 손톱만큼도 상관없는 일이지.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 옆집 그 신사야말로 우리하고 상관이 거의 없을 것 같은데요.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"그가 신사라는 건,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"의심할 여지가 없어. 신사다운 몸가짐에, 신사다운 풍채를 지녔지. 무릎까지 오는 짧은 바지에 잿빛 털양말을 신긴 하다만. 그건 별난 취향일 수도 있고, 아니면 제 다리에 자부심이 있는 건지도 모르지. 그러면 안 될 게 또 뭐냐. 섭정 왕자도 제 다리에 자부심이 있었고, 대니얼 램버트도 그랬어. 그이는 뚱뚱보였는데도 제 다리를 자랑스러워했지. 비핀 양도 그랬어. 그 여자는 — 아니,\" 니클비 부인이 스스로 고쳐 말했어. \"그 여자는 발가락밖에 없었던 것 같다만, 그래도 이치는 매한가지지.\"\n\n니콜라스는 이 새로운 화제가 튀어나온 것에 어안이 벙벙해서 지켜봤어. 그런데 그건 니클비 부인이 그가 그러기를 바라던 바로 그 반응인 듯했지.\n\n\"놀랄 만도 하다, 니콜라스.\" 그녀가 말했어. \"나도 정말 놀랐으니까. 불꽃이 번쩍하듯 들이닥쳐서, 피가 다 얼어붙는 줄 알았어. 그 사람네 정원 끝이 우리 정원 끝하고 맞닿아 있잖니. 그래서 당연히 나는 그 사람이 자기 자그마한 정자에서 붉은강낭콩 사이에 앉아 있거나, 자그마한 온상에서 일하는 걸 여러 번 봤지. 좀 빤히 쳐다본다 싶긴 했다만, 우리가 갓 이사 온 사람들이니 우리가 어떤지 궁금한가 보다 하고 별 신경을 안 썼어. 그런데 그 사람이 자기 오이를 우리 담 너머로 던지기 시작하면서—\"\n\n\"오이를 우리 담 너머로 던지다니요!\" 니콜라스가 몹시 놀라 되뇌었어.\n\n\"그래, 니콜라스.\" 니클비 부인이 아주 진지한 어조로 대답했어. \"자기 오이를 우리 담 너머로 던졌어. 호박도 마찬가지고.\"\n\n\"그 뻔뻔한 작자 같으니!\" 니콜라스가 대번에 발끈하며 말했어. \"도대체 무슨 셈으로 그러는 거예요?\"\n\n\"내 생각엔 그게 무례하게 굴려는 건 전혀 아닌 것 같다.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어.\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"식구들이 제집 정원을 걷는데 그 머리 위로 오이며 호박이 날아드는데, 무례하게 굴려는 게 아니라고요! 아니, 어머니—\"\n\n니콜라스는 말을 뚝 끊었어. 니클비 부인의 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자 가장자리 사이로, 뭐라 말로 다 할 수 없는, 차분한 승리감이 수줍은 당혹감과 뒤섞인 표정이 어른거리고 있었거든. 그게 갑자기 그의 눈길을 붙들었지.\n\n\"그 사람은 분명 아주 약하고, 어리석고, 분별없는 사람이야.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"정말이지 나무랄 만한 사람이지. 적어도 남들은 그렇게 볼 게다. 물론 나야 그 점에 대해 뭐라 의견을 낼 처지는 못 되지. 특히나 남들이 네 가엾은 아버지가 나한테 청혼한 걸 나무랄 때도 나는 늘 그이 편을 들었으니까 말이야. 그리고 그 사람이 그걸 아주 별난 방식으로 드러낸 건 두말할 나위가 없지. 그래도 그와 동시에, 그 사람의 마음 씀은 — 그러니까, 어느 정도까지는, 또 일정 선까지는 물론 — 우쭐해지게 하는 데가 있긴 하단다. 그리고 케이트같이 사랑스러운 아이가 아직 자리를 못 잡고 있는 마당에 내가 다시 결혼할 생각 따위는 꿈에도 안 하지만—\"\n\n\"설마 어머니, 그런 생각이 단 한순간이라도 머릿속에 들어온 적은 없으시겠죠?\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"아이고 맙소사, 니콜라스.\" 그의 어머니가 짜증스러운 어조로 대꾸했어. \"내가 말을 좀 끝내게 둔다면, 그게 바로 내가 하려는 말 그대로 아니냐? 당연히 두 번 다시 생각도 안 했고, 네가 나를 그런 짓을 할 사람으로 여긴다니 놀랍고 기가 막힌다. 내가 하려는 말은 그저, 이 구애를 정중하고 곱게, 그러면서도 그 사람 마음을 너무 다치게 하거나 절망으로 몰아넣거나 하는 일 없이 물리치려면, 어떤 수가 가장 좋겠냐는 거야. 아이고 세상에!\" 니클비 부인이 반쯤 헤벌쭉 웃으며 외쳤어. \"그 사람이 제 몸에 무슨 무모한 짓이라도 하면 어쩌겠니. 그럼 내가 다시 행복할 수 있겠어, 니콜라스?\"\n\n니콜라스는 속이 상하고 걱정스러우면서도, 웃음을 참기가 좀처럼 어려웠어. 그래서 이렇게 받아쳤지. \"그래, 어머니, 아무리 모질게 퇴짜를 놓는다 한들 그런 결과가 정말 따라오겠어요?\"\n\n\"글쎄다, 얘야, 나도 모르겠다.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어. \"정말이지 모르겠어. 그저께 신문에, 프랑스 신문에서 옮겨 온 기사가 하나 있었던 게 분명해. 어느 구두장이 직공 얘긴데, 이웃 마을 처녀 하나한테 질투가 나서 그랬다는 거야. 그 처녀가 그 사람하고 함께 공기 안 통하는 사 층 방에 틀어박혀서 숯을 피워 같이 죽기를 마다했다는 이유로 말이야. 그래서 그 사람이 끝이 뾰족한 칼을 들고 숲속에 숨어 있다가, 그 처녀가 친구 몇이랑 지나갈 때 뛰쳐나와서, 자기가 먼저 죽고, 그다음 친구들을 다 죽이고, 그다음 그 처녀를 죽였다는 거야. 아니, 친구들을 먼저 다 죽이고, 그다음 자기가 죽고, 그다음 그 처녀를 — 생각만 해도 아주 끔찍하다. 그런데 어찌 된 영문인지,\" 니클비 부인이 잠시 말을 멈췄다가 덧붙였어. \"신문을 보면 프랑스에서 이런 짓을 하는 건 늘 구두장이 직공들이더구나. 왜 그런지는 모르겠다만 — 가죽에 뭔가 있나 보지.\"\n\n\"그런데 그 사람은 구두장이도 아니잖아요 — 그 사람이 뭘 했어요, 어머니, 뭐라고 했어요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어. 더는 참기 어려울 만큼 안달이 났지만, 겉으로는 니클비 부인 못지않게 체념한 듯 참을성 있게 보이면서 말이야. \"아시다시피, 오이를 정식 사랑 고백으로 바꿔 주는 채소의 언어 같은 건 없잖아요.\"\n\n\"얘야,\" 니클비 부인이 고개를 까딱 젖히고 난로 속 재를 바라보며 대답했어. \"그 사람은 별의별 짓을 다 하고, 별의별 말을 다 했어.\"\n\n\"어머니가 잘못 보신 건 아니고요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"잘못 보다니!\" 니클비 부인이 외쳤어. \"아이고, 니콜라스, 남자가 진심인지 아닌지 내가 모를 성싶으냐?\"\n\n\"알았어요, 알았어요!\" 니콜라스가 중얼거렸어.\n\n\"내가 창가에 갈 때마다,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"그 사람은 한 손에 입을 맞추고 다른 손은 가슴에 얹는단다. 물론 그러는 게 아주 어리석은 짓이고, 너는 분명 아주 잘못된 거라고 하겠지만, 그래도 그 사람이 그러는 게 아주 공손하단다. 정말이지 아주 공손해. 그리고 아주 다정하지, 더없이 다정해. 거기까지만 보면 칭찬받아 마땅하고, 그건 두말할 나위가 없어. 그리고 날마다 담 너머로 쏟아져 오는 선물들도 있는데, 정말이지 아주 훌륭해, 아주 훌륭하단다. 어제 저녁상에 그 오이 하나를 먹었고, 나머지는 다음 겨울에 쓰게 절여 둘까 한다. 그리고 어젯밤에는,\" 니클비 부인이 한층 더 당황해하며 덧붙였어. \"내가 정원을 거닐고 있는데 그 사람이 담 너머로 가만히 부르더니, 결혼하자고, 같이 도망가자고 청하더구나. 그 사람 목소리가 종소리나 유리 악기 소리처럼 맑아. 정말이지 유리 악기 소리하고 꼭 닮았어. 그래도 물론 나는 귀담아듣지 않았지. 그래서 문제는, 니콜라스, 내가 어떡해야 하느냐는 거다.\"\n\n\"케이트도 이걸 알아요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"아직 한마디도 안 했다.\" 그의 어머니가 대답했어.\n\n\"그렇다면, 제발,\" 니콜라스가 일어서며 받았어. \"말하지 마세요. 그 애가 무척 마음 아파할 테니까요. 그리고 어머니가 어떡하셔야 하느냐 하면, 어머니, 어머니의 그 분별과 마음씀씀이가, 또 아버지를 기리는 마음이 시키는 대로 하세요. 이 터무니없고 노망난 마음 씀을 싫어한다는 걸 드러낼 방법이야 수천 가지나 있어요. 어머니가 마땅히 그래야 하는 만큼 단호하게 처신하셨는데도 그게 계속되고, 그래서 어머니를 성가시게 한다면, 제가 당장 끝장내 드릴게요. 하지만 이렇게 우스꽝스러운 일에 제가 끼어들어서 거기다 무게를 실어 주는 짓은, 어머니가 먼저 스스로 떳떳하게 처신하시기 전에는 하지 않을래요. 여자들 대부분이 그쯤은 할 수 있고, 더구나 어머니 연세에 어머니 처지라면 더더욱 그렇죠. 게다가 진지하게 생각할 가치도 없는 이런 일에서는 말이에요. 제가 이걸 마음에 담아 두는 척하거나 단 한순간이라도 진지하게 받아들여서 어머니를 부끄럽게 하지는 않을 거예요. 어처구니없는 늙은 멍청이 같으니!\"\n\n이렇게 말하고서, 니콜라스는 어머니에게 입을 맞추고 잘 자라는 인사를 했어. 그리고 두 사람은 각자의 방으로 물러갔지.\n\n니클비 부인을 위해 공정하게 말하자면, 그녀가 자식들을 향해 품은 정만으로도 재혼을 진지하게 고려하는 일은 없었을 거야. 설령 죽은 남편에 대한 기억을 이겨 내고 그쪽으로 강한 마음이 동했다 하더라도 말이지. 그래도 니클비 부인의 마음에는 악의가 없고 진짜 이기심도 거의 없었지만, 그녀는 머리가 모자라고 또 허영심이 있었어. 그리고 이 나이에 와서 청혼을 받는다는 것에는 — 그것도 헛된 청혼이긴 했지만 — 어찌나 우쭐해지는 데가 있었던지, 니콜라스가 마땅하다고 여기는 듯한 그런 식으로, 그 정체 모를 신사의 연정을 그렇게 단번에 또 가볍게 떨쳐 버릴 수가 없었어.\n\n'그게 터무니없고, 노망났고, 우스꽝스럽다는 것에 대해선,' 니클비 부인이 제 방에서 혼자 곰곰이 따져 보며 생각했어. '나는 도무지 그렇게 안 보이는걸. 그 사람 쪽에서야 가망 없는 일인 건 분명하지. 하지만 왜 그 사람이 어처구니없는 늙은 멍청이여야 하는지, 솔직히 나는 모르겠어. 그 사람이 가망 없는 일이란 걸 안다고 볼 수도 없잖아. 가엾은 사람! 내 생각엔 측은히 여겨야 할 사람이야!'\n\n이렇게 곰곰이 생각하고 나서, 니클비 부인은 자그마한 화장 거울을 들여다보고는, 거기서 몇 걸음 뒤로 물러서서, 니콜라스가 스물한 살이 되면 제 아들이라기보다 오라비처럼 보일 거라고 누가 그러더라, 그게 누구였는지 떠올려 보려 애썼어. 그 말을 한 사람이 누구인지 도무지 생각나지 않자, 그녀는 촛불을 끄고 창문 가리개를 걷어, 이 무렵 이미 동트기 시작한 아침 햇살을 들였어.\n\n\"물건을 알아보기엔 영 어두운 빛이네.\" 니클비 부인이 정원을 들여다보며 중얼거렸어. \"내 눈이 그리 좋지도 않고 — 어려서부터 근시였으니까 — 그래도 정말이지, 지금 이 순간 담 꼭대기 깨진 유리병 위에 큼지막한 호박이 또 하나 떡하니 박혀 있는 것 같단 말이야!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 38\n\n조의를 표하러 간 방문에서 비롯된 몇 가지 사정이 담겨 있는데, 이건 나중에 중요하게 밝혀질지도 몰라. 스마이크는 뜻밖에 아주 오래된 친구와 마주치는데, 그 친구는 자기 집으로 가자며 어떤 거절도 듣지 않으려 들어.\n\n연정에 빠진 이웃의 그 갖가지 행동도, 그것이 케이트 어머니의 여린 가슴에 미친 영향도 케이트 니클비는 전혀 모르고 있었어. 그러는 동안 케이트는 차분하고 행복한 기분을 느끼기 시작했어. 오래도록 가끔, 그것도 잠깐씩만 맛볼 수 있었던 그런 마음이었지. 그토록 갑작스럽고 모질게 떨어져 있어야 했던 사랑하는 오빠와 한 지붕 아래 살게 되었고, 마음이 편안했고, 뺨을 붉히게 하거나 가슴을 찌르는 어떤 괴롭힘에서도 벗어나 있으니, 마치 새로운 삶으로 옮겨 온 것 같았어. 옛날의 명랑함이 되살아났고, 발걸음은 다시 탄력 있고 가벼워졌어. 뺨을 떠났던 핏기가 다시 찾아왔고, 케이트 니클비는 그 어느 때보다 아름다워 보였어.\n\n라 크리비 양이 곰곰이 생각하고 살펴본 끝에 이른 결론이 바로 그거였어. 그녀가 힘주어 말했듯이 오두막이 \"굴뚝 꼭대기부터 현관문 흙털개까지 구석구석 말끔하게 정리되고\" 나서, 이 부지런한 자그마한 여인이 마침내 잠깐 짬을 내어 집 식구들 생각을 할 수 있게 되었을 때 말이야.\n\n\"정말이지 여기 처음 내려온 뒤로 그럴 틈이 한 번도 없었거든요.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"아침이고 낮이고 밤이고 망치며 못이며 드라이버며 송곳 생각밖에 안 했으니까요.\"\n\n\"정작 자기 생각은 한 번도 안 하셨겠네요.\" 케이트가 미소 지으며 대꾸했어.\n\n\"세상에, 얘야, 생각할 즐거운 일이 그렇게 많은데 내 생각이나 하고 있으면 그게 바보지.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"그런데 말이지, 나도 누구 생각을 좀 하긴 했단다. 너 그거 아니? 이 집 식구 중 한 사람한테서 큰 변화를 봤어. 아주 예사롭지 않은 변화 말이야.\"\n\n\"누구한테요?\" 케이트가 걱정스레 물었어. \"설마—\"\n\n\"네 오빠는 아니란다, 얘야.\" 라 크리비 양이 말끝을 미리 짐작하고 대꾸했어. \"오빠야 처음 너희를 알았을 때나 지금이나 늘 한결같이 다정하고 마음씨 좋고 영리한 사람이지. 필요할 땐 그 누구라고는 말 안 하겠지만, 그 사람 닮은 매운맛도 좀 섞여 있고 말이야. 아니. 스마이크 말이야, 가엾게도 다들 그렇게만 부르지! 자기 이름 앞에 '미스터' 붙이는 걸 도무지 들으려 하질 않으니까. 그 애가 이 짧은 시간에도 아주 많이 달라졌어.\"\n\n\"어떻게요?\" 케이트가 물었어. \"몸이 안 좋은 건 아니죠?\"\n\n\"아, 아니. 꼭 몸이 안 좋은 건 아닐지도 몰라.\" 라 크리비 양이 잠깐 멈춰 생각하며 말했어. \"물론 야위고 기운 없는 애이긴 하고, 그 얼굴에는 만약 네 얼굴에 그런 게 있으면 내 가슴이 미어질 그런 게 어려 있긴 하지만. 아니, 몸이 안 좋은 건 아니야.\"\n\n\"그럼 뭔데요?\"\n\n\"나도 잘 모르겠어.\" 초상화가가 말했어. \"그렇지만 그 애를 지켜봤는데, 몇 번이나 내 눈에 눈물이 핑 돌게 하더구나. 하긴 나야 워낙 잘 우니까 그게 그리 어려운 일은 아니지만, 그래도 이번엔 그럴 만한 까닭이 있어서 그랬던 것 같아. 그 애가 여기 온 뒤로 무슨 강한 이유 때문인지, 자기 머리가 모자라다는 걸 점점 더 의식하게 된 게 분명해. 그걸 더 절실히 느끼는 거야. 가끔 정신이 멍하고 아주 간단한 것도 이해 못 한다는 걸 알게 되니까 더 괴로운 거지. 네가 없을 때 그 애를 지켜봤단다, 얘야. 혼자 앉아서 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있는데, 그 괴로운 표정이 어찌나 보기 힘들던지. 그러다 일어나서 방을 나가는데, 어찌나 서글프고 풀이 죽었던지, 내 마음이 얼마나 아팠는지 말로 다 못 한다. 삼 주도 안 됐는데, 그땐 마음 가벼운, 부지런한 애였잖아. 북적거리는 게 좋아서 신나하고, 온종일 행복해하던 애였는데. 지금은 딴사람이 됐어. 똑같이 잘 도우려 하고, 남 해칠 줄 모르고, 충직하고 사랑스럽긴 한데, 그것 말고는 다 변해 버렸어.\"\n\n\"분명 다 지나갈 거예요.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"가엾은 애!\"\n\n\"그러길 바라.\" 그녀의 자그마한 친구가, 평소답지 않게 무거운 어조로 대꾸했어. \"그러길 바란다. 그 가엾은 애를 위해서라도 그러길 바라. 어쨌든,\" 라 크리비 양이 평소 버릇대로 명랑하게 재잘대는 말투로 돌아오며 말했어. \"내 할 말은 다 했네. 어찌나 긴 말이었는지, 게다가 틀린 말도 많았을 거야, 하나도 놀랍지 않아. 어쨌든 오늘 밤엔 그 애 기운을 좀 북돋워 줄 거야. 스트랜드까지 내내 나를 바래다주는 호위 기사가 되어 줄 거라면, 뭔가에 웃음이 터질 때까지 쉴 새 없이 떠들고 또 떠들고 멈추지 않을 거니까. 그러니 그 애는 빨리 갈수록 좋고, 나도 빨리 갈수록 좋아. 안 그러면 우리 집 하녀가 누군가하고 싸돌아다니다가 집을 털릴지도 모르거든. 하긴 탁자랑 의자 말고 가져갈 게 뭐가 있을지 모르겠지만. 초상화들 빼고 말이야. 그런데 그걸 어디다 비싸게 팔아넘길 수 있는 사람이라면 정말 대단한 도둑이지. 나도 못 하는걸. 그게 솔직한 사실이야.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 자그마한 라 크리비 양은 아주 납작한 보닛 속에 얼굴을 파묻고, 아주 큰 숄로 몸을 감쌌어. 그러고는 커다란 핀으로 그 숄을 단단히 여미더니, 자기는 채비가 다 됐으니 합승마차가 오고 싶을 때 와도 좋다고 선언했어.\n\n하지만 아직 니클비 부인하고 작별 인사를 해야 했지. 그리고 이 마음씨 좋은 부인이 이 자리에 어울리는 옛 추억담을 미처 마치기도 전에 합승마차가 도착했어. 이 바람에 라 크리비 양은 몹시 부산해졌고, 그 통에 현관문 뒤에서 하녀에게 몰래 십팔 펜스를 쥐여 주려다가 그만 손지갑에서 십 펜스어치 반 페니 동전들을 쏟아 버렸어. 동전들이 복도 구석구석으로 다 굴러가 버려서, 그걸 줍느라 꽤 오랜 시간이 걸렸지. 이 의식이 끝나고 나니 당연히 케이트와 니클비 부인에게 두 번째로 입을 맞추고, 작은 바구니와 갈색 종이 꾸러미를 챙기는 일이 뒤따랐어. 그러는 동안에도 라 크리비 양 말마따나 \"그 합승마차가 어찌나 끔찍하게 욕을 해 대는지, 듣기에 정말 무서울 지경\"이었지. 마침내 마차가 떠나려는 시늉을 하자, 라 크리비 양이 후다닥 뛰쳐나갔다가 후다닥 올라타며 승객 모두에게 청산유수로 사과를 했어. 일부러 기다리게 한 건 절대로, 무슨 일이 있어도 아니라고 우기면서 말이야. 그녀가 앉을 만한 자리를 두리번거리는 사이, 차장이 스마이크를 밀어 넣고는 다 됐다고 외쳤어. 사실은 안 됐는데도 말이야. 그러고는 그 거대한 마차가 맥주 양조장 짐수레 적어도 대여섯 대는 굴러가는 듯한 요란한 소리를 내며 떠나갔어.\n\n그 마차는 앞서 말한 차장이 가고 싶은 대로 가게 내버려 두자. 그 차장은 뒤쪽 자기 작은 발판에 우아하게 기대어 향내 나는 시가를 피우고 있었지. 그가 적당하다 싶고 마땅하다 싶을 때 마차를 세우든 가든 내달리든 기어가든 마음대로 하게 내버려 두고, 이 이야기는 이참에 멀버리 호크 경의 상태가 어떤지, 앞서 자세히 이야기한 그 상황에서 이륜마차에서 거칠게 내동댕이쳐져 입은 부상을 그가 이제 얼마나 회복했는지 살펴보기로 하자.\n\n다리가 부러지고, 몸은 심하게 멍들고, 얼굴은 아물다 만 흉터로 일그러지고, 최근의 고통과 열병으로 기진맥진해 핼쑥해진 채, 멀버리 호크 경은 등을 대고 길게 누워 있었어. 앞으로도 몇 주는 더 갇혀 지내야 할 운명인 그 긴 의자에 말이야. 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨는 옆방에 앉아 진탕 술을 마시며, 단조롭게 웅얼대던 대화에 이따금 반쯤 죽인 웃음소리를 섞어 넣고 있었어. 한편 젊은 귀족은, 이 패거리 가운데 유일하게 완전히 구제 불능은 아니고 정말로 마음씨가 따뜻한 사람이었는데, 입에 시가를 문 채 스승 곁에 앉아 그날 신문에서 그가 흥미나 재미를 느낄 만한 기삿거리를 골라 등불 빛에 비춰 읽어 주고 있었어.\n\n\"저 개자식들 같으니!\" 환자가 옆방 쪽으로 짜증스럽게 고개를 돌리며 말했어. \"저 빌어먹을 목구멍을 막을 방법은 없는 거냐?\"\n\n파이크 씨와 플럭 씨는 그 외침을 듣고 곧바로 입을 다물었어. 그러면서 서로 눈짓을 주고받았지. 그리고 말을 빼앗긴 데 대한 보상이라도 되는 양 잔을 가장자리까지 가득 채웠어.\n\n\"제기랄!\" 병든 사내가 이를 악물고 중얼거리며 침대에서 짜증스럽게 몸부림쳤어. \"이 매트리스는 충분히 딱딱하지 않고, 이 방은 충분히 따분하지 않고, 통증은 충분히 지독하지 않다는 거냐. 저것들이 기어이 날 괴롭혀야겠어? 지금 몇 시야?\"\n\n\"여덟 시 반일세.\" 친구가 대답했어.\n\n\"이리, 탁자를 더 가까이 끌어다 놓고, 다시 카드를 하지.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어. \"피켓 한 판 더. 자.\"\n\n병든 사내가 고개를 좌우로 돌리는 것 말고는 자세를 바꿀 수도 없는 처지인데, 게임이 진행되는 내내 친구의 동작 하나하나를 얼마나 열심히 지켜보던지, 또 얼마나 열의와 흥미를 가지고 게임을 하면서도 얼마나 신중하고 침착하던지, 보고 있으면 묘했어. 그의 수완과 솜씨는 상대보다 스무 배는 더 나았어. 상대는 운이 좋아 좋은 패가 들어와도 — 그런 일은 자주 없었지만 — 도무지 당해 낼 수가 없었지. 멀버리 경은 매 판을 이겼어. 그러다 상대가 카드를 던지고 더는 안 하겠다고 하자, 그는 야윈 팔을 쭉 뻗어 판돈을 그러쥐며 으스대듯 욕설을 내뱉었어. 그리고 몇 달 전 랄프 니클비의 식당에서 울려 퍼졌던 것과 똑같은 그 쉰 웃음소리를, 비록 톤은 꽤 낮아졌지만 똑같이 내뱉었지.\n\n이러고 있는데 그의 하인이 나타나, 랄프 니클비 씨가 아래층에 와서 오늘 밤 그가 어떤지 알고 싶어 한다고 알렸어.\n\n\"나아졌다고 해.\" 멀버리 경이 짜증스럽게 말했어.\n\n\"니클비 씨가 알고 싶어 하시는데요, 나리—\"\n\n\"나아졌다니까.\" 멀버리 경이 탁자를 손바닥으로 내리치며 대꾸했어.\n\n하인은 잠깐 한두 차례 머뭇거리더니, 니클비 씨가 불편하지 않으시다면 멀버리 호크 경을 뵙게 해 달라고 청한다고 말했어.\n\n\"불편해. 못 봐. 아무도 못 만나.\" 주인이 아까보다 더 거칠게 말했어. \"알면서 그러는 거냐, 이 멍청아.\"\n\n\"정말 죄송합니다, 나리.\" 하인이 대꾸했어. \"그런데 니클비 씨가 하도 졸라 대셔서요, 나리—\"\n\n사실 랄프 니클비가 하인에게 돈을 찔러 준 거였어. 하인은 그 돈을 벌어 두면 앞으로 또 잘 봐줄까 싶어, 문손잡이를 잡은 채 감히 더 어물거리고 있었던 거지.\n\n\"무슨 볼일이 있어서 이야기하고 싶다던가?\" 멀버리 경이 잠깐 짜증스럽게 생각하더니 물었어.\n\n\"아니요, 나리. 그냥 나리를 뵙고 싶다고만 하셨습니다. 니클비 씨가 특별히 그렇게 말씀하셨습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"올라오라고 해. 이봐.\" 멀버리 경이 일그러진 얼굴을 손으로 쓸며, 나가려는 하인을 다시 불렀어. \"저 등불을 옮겨서 내 뒤 받침대에 놓아. 저 탁자는 치우고, 의자 하나를 거기다 둬. 더 멀찍이. 그렇게 둬.\"\n\n하인은 그 지시에 담긴 의도를 다 알아챘다는 듯 그대로 따르고는 방을 나갔어. 프레더릭 베리소프트 경은 곧 다시 들여다보겠다고 말하며 옆방으로 어슬렁어슬렁 들어가 접이문을 닫았어.\n\n그러자 계단에서 죽인 발소리가 들려왔어. 그리고 랄프 니클비가 모자를 손에 들고, 깊이 존경한다는 듯 몸을 앞으로 숙인 채, 그 갸륵한 고객의 얼굴에 눈길을 고정하고 살그머니 방으로 들어왔어.\n\n\"어, 니클비.\" 멀버리 경이 긴 의자 곁의 의자를 가리키며, 짐짓 무심한 척 손을 내저으며 말했어. \"보다시피 큰 사고를 당했지.\"\n\n\"그러게요.\" 랄프가 변함없이 가만히 응시하며 대꾸했어. \"정말 심하군요! 못 알아볼 뻔했습니다, 멀버리 경. 저런, 저런! 이거 정말 심합니다.\"\n\n랄프의 태도는 더없이 겸손하고 정중했어. 낮은 목소리는 병자를 가장 자상하게 배려하는 문병객이 짓는 그런 어조였지. 그런데 멀버리 경이 얼굴을 돌리고 있는 사이, 랄프의 얼굴 표정은 그것과 놀랍도록 딴판이었어. 그가 평소 자세 그대로 서서 눈앞에 누운 사람을 차분히 내려다보는데, 튀어나온 채 잔뜩 찌푸린 눈썹 그늘에 가리지 않은 얼굴 부분에는 죄다 비웃는 미소가 어려 있었어.\n\n\"앉지.\" 멀버리 경이 안간힘을 쓰듯 그를 향해 돌아보며 말했어. \"내가 무슨 구경거리라도 되나, 거기 서서 빤히 쳐다보게?\"\n\n그가 얼굴을 돌리자 랄프는 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러났어. 그러고는 도저히 놀라움을 감출 수 없지만 굳이 내색하지 않으려는 듯 시늉하며, 능청맞게 당황한 척하고 자리에 앉았어.\n\n\"매일 문간에서 안부를 여쭈었습니다, 멀버리 경.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"처음엔 하루에 두 번씩이나요. 그리고 오늘 밤엔 오랜 친분과, 우리가 어느 정도 서로 득을 본 지난 거래들을 믿고서, 침실로 들여보내 달라 청하지 않을 수가 없었습니다. 많이, 많이 아프셨습니까?\" 랄프가 몸을 앞으로 숙이며 물었어. 상대가 눈을 감자 그 얼굴에 또다시 모진 미소가 번지도록 내버려 두면서 말이야.\n\n\"내 마음에 들 만큼보다는 더, 그리고 자네하고 내가 아는 어떤 망해 빠진 한물간 자들 마음에 들 만큼보다는 덜 아팠지. 그자들은 제 신세 망친 걸 자네하고 내 탓으로 돌리고 있을 거야, 아마.\" 멀버리 경이 이불 위에서 팔을 안절부절못하며 휘저으며 대꾸했어.\n\n랄프는 어깨를 으쓱하며, 이 말에 담긴 격한 짜증을 못마땅해하는 시늉을 했어. 랄프의 말투와 태도에는 사람 부아를 돋우는 차갑고 또렷한 데가 있어서, 병든 사내가 도무지 견디기 힘들 만큼 신경을 긁었거든.\n\n\"그 '지난 거래들' 중에 대체 뭐 때문에 오늘 밤 여기 온 거지?\" 멀버리 경이 물었어.\n\n\"아무것도 아닙니다.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"각하의 어음 몇 장이 갱신이 필요하긴 하지만, 그건 나으실 때까지 미뤄 두지요. 저, 저는 왔습니다.\" 랄프가 더 천천히, 더 모질게 힘주어 말했어. \"제 친척이, 비록 제가 의절한 자이긴 합니다만, 경께 이런 벌을 가했다는 게 얼마나 가슴 아픈지 말씀드리러 왔습니다—\"\n\n\"벌이라고!\" 멀버리 경이 끼어들었어.\n\n\"가혹한 일이었다는 거 압니다.\" 랄프가 그 말허리를 일부러 곡해하며 말했어. \"그래서 더더욱 말씀드리고 싶었습니다. 저는 그 떠돌이를 의절했고, 그놈을 제 핏줄로 인정하지 않으며, 경께서든 그 누구에게서든 마땅한 응징을 받도록 내버려 둔다고요. 원하시면 그놈 목을 비틀어 버리셔도 됩니다. 저는 끼어들지 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 여기서 다들 나한테 떠들어 대는 그 이야기가 밖에 다 퍼졌단 말이지, 그래?\" 멀버리 경이 주먹과 이를 악물며 물었어.\n\n\"사방에 퍼졌습니다.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"클럽이며 도박장이며 죄다 그 이야기로 시끌벅적합니다. 듣자 하니 그걸로 그럴듯한 노래까지 만들어졌다더군요.\" 랄프가 질문한 사람을 뚫어지게 보며 말했어. \"제가 직접 들어 보진 못했습니다. 그런 데를 다닐 처지가 아니라서요. 그래도 듣기로는 인쇄까지 됐다더군요. 사적으로만 돌려 본다고는 하던데, 그래 봐야 온 장안에 다 퍼진 거나 마찬가지죠.\"\n\n\"거짓말이야!\" 멀버리 경이 말했어. \"전부 거짓말이라니까. 말이 놀란 거라고.\"\n\n\"그놈이 말을 놀라게 했다더군요.\" 랄프가 여전히 흔들림 없이 차분하게 말했어. \"어떤 사람들은 그놈이 경을 겁먹게 했다고도 하던데, 그건 거짓말이지요, 저도 압니다. 제가 대놓고 그렇게 말했습니다. 오, 스무 번도 넘게요! 저야 화평한 사람이지만, 사람들이 경에 대해 그렇게 떠드는 건 못 듣겠더군요. 아니, 아니고말고요.\"\n\n멀버리 경이 가까스로 말이 될 만한 소리를 짜내자, 랄프는 손을 귀에 갖다 대고 몸을 앞으로 숙였어. 얼굴은 그 모진 선 하나하나가 무쇠로 빚어진 듯 태연했지.\n\n\"이 빌어먹을 침대에서 일어나기만 하면,\" 환자가 격정에 사로잡혀 정말로 자기 부러진 다리를 후려치며 말했어. \"여태 그 누구도 한 적 없는 복수를 해 줄 테다. 맹세코 그러고말고. 운이 그놈 편을 들어 줘서 나한테 한두 주짜리 자국을 남겼다만, 나는 그놈한테 무덤까지 가져갈 자국을 남겨 줄 거야. 코하고 귀를 째 버리고, 채찍질하고, 평생 불구로 만들어 줄 테다. 그뿐인 줄 알아. 그 정숙함의 본보기, 그 새침함의 극치인 그 어여쁜 누이동생을 끌어다가—\"\n\n그 순간 랄프의 차가운 피마저 뺨에서 화끈 달아올랐는지도 몰라. 멀버리 경이 문득, 자기가 악당에 고리대금업자이긴 해도 갓난아기였던 그 옛날 언젠가는 그 애 아버지의 목을 끌어안았을 게 분명하다는 사실을 떠올렸는지도 모르지. 그는 말을 멈추더니, 손으로 위협하는 시늉을 하며 미처 내뱉지 못한 위협을 무시무시한 욕설로 마무리했어.\n\n\"부아가 치미는 일이지요.\" 랄프가 잠깐 입을 다물고 있다가 말했어. 그동안 그는 고통받는 사내를 날카롭게 살피고 있었지. \"장안의 한량, 난봉꾼, 탕아, 스무 시즌이나 닳고 닳은 노름꾼이 한낱 애송이 하나한테 이 지경이 되다니, 생각하면 말입니다!\"\n\n멀버리 경이 그에게 분노에 찬 눈길을 쏘았어. 하지만 랄프의 눈은 바닥을 향해 있었고, 그 얼굴에는 그저 생각에 잠긴 표정 말고는 아무것도 없었어.\n\n\"풋내기에 호리호리한 애송이가,\" 랄프가 말을 이었어. \"제 몸무게만으로도 그놈을 깔아뭉갤 수 있는 사내를 상대로 말입니다. 게다가 경의 그 솜씨야 말할 것도 없고요. 제 말이 맞지요, 아마.\" 랄프가 눈을 들며 말했어. \"예전에 권투계 후원자셨지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n병든 사내가 짜증스러운 몸짓을 했는데, 랄프는 그걸 굳이 수긍의 표시로 받아들이기로 했어.\n\n\"하!\" 그가 말했어. \"그럴 줄 알았습니다. 그건 제가 경을 알기 전 일이지만, 제가 틀릴 리 없다고 꽤나 확신했습니다. 그놈은 가볍고 날쌔겠지요, 아마. 하지만 그런 건 경의 장점에 비하면 보잘것없는 이점이지요. 운이지요, 운! 그 비럭질하는 천덕꾸러기들이 그걸 타고난단 말입니다.\"\n\n\"내가 다시 멀쩡해지면 그놈은 가진 운을 다 끌어 써야 할 거야.\" 멀버리 호크 경이 말했어. \"어디로 달아나든 말이지.\"\n\n\"오!\" 랄프가 잽싸게 대꾸했어. \"그놈은 그럴 생각이 꿈에도 없습니다. 그놈은 여기, 경께서 손볼 날을 기다리며 여기 런던에 있습니다. 한낮에 버젓이 거리를 활보하면서요. 으스대며 뽐내고 다닙니다. 맹세컨대 경을 찾고 있어요.\" 랄프가 말했어. 이 의기양양한 니콜라스의 모습이 떠오르자 그의 얼굴이 어두워졌고, 난생처음 제 증오심이 그를 휘어잡았지. \"안전하게 그럴 수 있는 나라 사람이기만 하다면, 저는 기꺼이 돈을 들여 그놈 심장에 칼을 꽂고, 개들이 물어뜯게 시궁창에 처박아 버리겠습니다.\"\n\n랄프가 오랜 고객을 다소 놀라게 하면서 이 작은 혈육의 정을 한바탕 쏟아 내고는 떠날 채비로 모자를 집어 들었을 때, 프레더릭 베리소프트 경이 들여다봤어.\n\n\"아니 대체 무슨 얘길 하고 있었던 거야, 호크, 자네하고 니클비 말이야?\" 젊은이가 말했어. \"이렇게 못 들어 줄 시끄러운 소란은 처음 들어 보네. 깍깍, 깍깍, 깍깍. 멍멍, 멍멍, 멍멍. 다 무슨 얘기였어?\"\n\n\"멀버리 경께서 화가 나셔서요, 각하.\" 랄프가 긴 의자 쪽을 보며 말했어.\n\n\"돈 얘긴 아니겠지? 사업에 뭐 잘못된 거 없지, 니클비?\"\n\n\"없습니다, 각하, 없어요.\" 랄프가 대꾸했어. \"그 점에선 저희가 늘 의견이 맞으니까요. 멀버리 경께서는 그 원인을 떠올리고 계셨던 겁니다—\"\n\n랄프가 말을 이을 필요도, 그럴 틈도 없었어. 멀버리 경이 그 화제를 받아 들고는, 거의 아까만큼이나 사납게 니콜라스를 향해 위협과 욕설을 퍼부었거든.\n\n여간한 관찰자가 아닌 랄프는, 이 장광설이 이어지는 동안 프레더릭 베리소프트 경의 태도가 완전히 달라지는 걸 보고 놀랐어. 처음에는 더없이 멋쟁이답고 나른한 태도로 구레나룻을 배배 꼬고 있던 그가 말이야. 그리고 멀버리 경이 말을 멈추자, 젊은 귀족이 성을 내며, 거의 꾸밈없는 태도로, 다시는 자기 앞에서 그 화제를 꺼내지 말라고 요구하는 데는 더욱 놀랐지.\n\n\"명심해, 호크!\" 그가 유난스러운 기세로 덧붙였어. \"나는 이 젊은 친구한테 비겁하게 덤벼드는 짓에 절대 끼지도 않을 거고, 내가 막을 수 있는 한 허락하지도 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"비겁하다고!\" 친구가 끼어들었어.\n\n\"그, 래.\" 상대가 그를 정면으로 돌아보며 말했어. \"자네가 그자한테 자네가 누군지 밝혔더라면, 명함을 건넸더라면, 그러고 나서 그자의 신분이나 처지 탓에 결투를 할 수 없다는 걸 알게 됐더라면, 그때도 충분히 못된 짓이었겠지. 맹세코 그때도 충분히 못된 짓이었을 거야. 그런데 실제로 자네가 한 짓은 잘못이었어. 나도 끼어들지 않은 게 잘못이었고, 그게 후회돼. 그 뒤에 자네한테 일어난 일은 계획했다기보다 사고에 가까운 거였고, 그자보다 자네 탓이 더 컸어. 그러니 내가 아는 한, 그걸 그자한테 잔인하게 되갚게 두진 않겠어, 절대 그러지 않을 거야.\"\n\n이렇게 자기가 맺음말을 힘주어 되풀이하고는, 젊은 귀족이 홱 돌아섰어. 그런데 옆방에 채 닿기도 전에 다시 돌아서더니, 아까보다 한층 더 격하게 말했어.\n\n\"나는 이제 정말로 믿어. 명예를 걸고 정말로 믿는다고. 그 누이동생이 아름다운 만큼이나 정숙하고 얌전한 아가씨라는 걸 말이야. 그리고 그 오빠에 대해서는 이렇게 말하겠어. 그자는 누이의 오빠로서 마땅히 해야 할 대로 행동했고, 사내답고 기개 있게 처신했다고. 나는 진심으로, 온 마음과 영혼을 다해 바라네. 우리 가운데 누구든 이 일에서 그자의 반만큼이라도 떳떳하게 빠져나왔으면 좋겠다고 말이야.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 프레더릭 베리소프트 경은 방을 걸어 나갔어. 랄프 니클비와 멀버리 경을 더없이 떨떠름한 놀라움 속에 남겨 둔 채로 말이야.\n\n\"저게 자네 제자인가?\" 랄프가 나직이 물었어. \"아니면 어느 시골 목사한테서 막 배워 온 건가?\"\n\n\"풋내기 바보들이 가끔 저런 발작을 하지.\" 멀버리 호크 경이 입술을 깨물며 문 쪽을 가리키며 대꾸했어. \"저놈은 나한테 맡겨 둬.\"\n\n랄프는 옛 지인과 허물없는 눈빛을 주고받았어. 이 섬뜩한 놀라움 속에서 둘은 별안간 다시 흉허물 없는 사이로 돌아왔거든. 그러고는 생각에 잠겨 천천히 집으로 향했어.\n\n이런 말들이 오가고 일들이 벌어지는 동안, 그리고 그게 다 끝나기 한참 전에, 합승마차는 라 크리비 양과 그 호위 기사를 토해 냈고, 둘은 그녀의 집 문 앞에 도착해 있었어. 그런데 이 자그마한 초상화가의 마음씨가 좋아서, 스마이크가 뭔가 속을 든든하게 할 것을 한 모금 마시고 모둠 비스킷이라도 좀 먹어 기운을 차리기 전에는 도무지 그를 다시 걸어 돌아가게 둘 수가 없었어. 스마이크도 속을 든든하게 할 한 모금이든 모둠 비스킷이든 마다할 생각이 없었고, 오히려 그것들이 보까지 걸어갈 채비로 아주 흐뭇하겠다 싶었지. 그러다 보니 그는 애초에 작정했던 것보다 훨씬 오래 머물게 되었고, 집으로 길을 나섰을 때는 땅거미가 진 지 반 시간쯤 지난 뒤였어.\n\n길을 잃을 염려는 없었어. 길이 그의 앞에 곧게 뻗어 있었고, 그는 거의 매일 니콜라스와 함께 시내로 걸어 들어왔다가 혼자 걸어 돌아갔으니까. 그래서 라 크리비 양과 그는 서로 믿음 가득한 마음으로 악수를 했고, 니클비 부인과 니클비 양에게 다정한 안부를 더 잔뜩 전해 달라는 부탁까지 받고, 스마이크는 출발했어.\n\n러드게이트 힐 기슭에서 그는 길을 살짝 벗어나, 뉴게이트를 한번 구경하고픈 호기심을 채우려 했어. 길 건너편에서 그 음침한 벽을 한참 동안 잔뜩 조심스럽고 두려운 마음으로 올려다보다가, 그는 다시 원래 가던 길로 돌아와 도심을 씩씩하게 걸어갔어. 이따금 멈춰 서서 유난히 눈길을 끄는 가게 진열창을 들여다보고, 또 잠깐 달려가다가, 또 멈춰 서고, 그러기를 되풀이하면서 말이야. 여느 시골 소년이라면 누구나 그러듯이.\n\n그는 한참 동안 어느 보석상 진열창을 들여다보고 있었어. 그 아름다운 장신구를 몇 개 집으로 선물로 가져갈 수 있으면 얼마나 좋을까 바라면서, 그럴 수 있다면 다들 얼마나 기뻐할까 그려 보면서 말이야. 그때 시계들이 여덟 시 사십오 분을 쳤어. 그 소리에 정신이 들어 그는 아주 빠른 걸음으로 서둘러 갔어. 그리고 어느 골목 모퉁이를 가로지르는데, 갑자기 어찌나 세게 홱 잡아채이는지 넘어지지 않으려고 가로등 기둥을 붙잡아야 했지. 바로 그 순간, 어린 사내아이 하나가 그의 다리를 꽉 끌어안았고, \"여기 있어요, 아버지! 만세!\" 하는 새된 외침이 그의 귀에 쟁쟁 울렸어.\n\n스마이크는 그 목소리를 너무도 잘 알았어. 그는 절망에 찬 눈으로 그 소리가 나온 형체를 내려다봤고, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 부르르 떨며 주위를 둘러봤어. 스퀴어스 씨가 우산 손잡이로 그의 코트 깃을 걸어, 반대쪽 끝에 온 힘을 다해 매달려 있었어. 그 의기양양한 외침은 와크퍼드 도련님한테서 나온 거였는데, 이 녀석은 스마이크가 아무리 발버둥치고 차도 아랑곳 않고 불도그처럼 끈질기게 그에게 들러붙어 있었지!\n\n한눈에 이 모든 게 그의 눈에 들어왔어. 그리고 그 한 번의 눈길에 겁에 질린 가엾은 그는 완전히 무력해져, 소리 한마디 낼 수 없게 되어 버렸어.\n\n\"이게 웬 횡재냐!\" 스퀴어스 씨가 우산을 손으로 한 칸 한 칸 옮겨 잡으며 내려오다가, 먹잇감의 깃을 단단히 움켜쥐고 나서야 우산을 풀며 외쳤어. \"이게 웬 기막힌 횡재야! 와크퍼드, 얘야, 저기 마차 한 대 불러라.\"\n\n\"마차요, 아버지!\" 어린 와크퍼드가 외쳤어.\n\n\"그래, 마차다, 이놈아.\" 스퀴어스가 스마이크의 얼굴을 두 눈에 실컷 담으며 대꾸했어. \"돈 따위 알 게 뭐냐. 이놈을 마차에 태우자.\"\n\n\"이놈이 뭘 했길래요?\" 벽돌을 짊어진 한 인부가 물었어. 스퀴어스 씨가 우산을 처음 홱 잡아챘을 때, 이 인부와 동료 인부 쪽으로 떠밀려 부딪혔거든.\n\n\"안 한 게 없지!\" 스퀴어스 씨가 옛 제자를 황홀경에 빠진 듯 뚫어지게 보며 대꾸했어. \"안 한 게 없어. 도망치고, 이놈아, 제 주인한테 피에 굶주린 공격을 함께 가하고, 나쁜 짓이란 나쁜 짓은 안 해 본 게 없지. 오, 이게 웬 기막힌 횡재냐, 맙소사!\"\n\n인부는 스퀴어스에게서 스마이크에게로 눈길을 옮겼어. 하지만 그 가엾은 친구가 지녔던 그나마의 정신머리는 깡그리 달아나 버린 뒤였지. 마차가 다가왔어. 와크퍼드 도련님이 올라탔어. 스퀴어스가 제 노획물을 밀어 넣고, 그 뒤를 바짝 따라 올라타더니 유리창을 끌어 올렸어. 마부가 마부석에 올라 천천히 마차를 몰고 떠나며, 이 장면을 유일하게 목격했던 벽돌공 둘과 늙은 사과 장수, 그리고 야간 학교에서 돌아오던 도회지 꼬마 하나를 남겨 두었어. 한가로이 이 일을 곱씹어 보라고 말이야.\n\n스퀴어스 씨는 불운한 스마이크의 맞은편 자리에 앉아, 두 손을 무릎에 단단히 짚고 한 오 분쯤 그를 바라봤어. 그러다 황홀경에서 깨어난 듯 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨리더니, 옛 제자의 뺨을 여러 차례 후려쳤어. 오른쪽 왼쪽 번갈아 가면서 말이야.\n\n\"꿈이 아니야!\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"이건 진짜 살과 피라고! 만져 보니 알겠어!\" 그리고 이 실험으로 자기 행운을 확실히 확인하고 나자, 스퀴어스 씨는 즐거움이 너무 똑같아 보일까 봐 귀싸대기도 몇 대 더 올려붙였어. 그러고는 한 대 칠 때마다 더 크게, 더 길게 웃었지.\n\n\"네 어머니가 이 소식을 들으면 좋아서 펄쩍펄쩍 뛸 거다, 얘야.\" 스퀴어스가 아들에게 말했어.\n\n\"안 그러겠어요, 아버지?\" 와크퍼드 도련님이 대꾸했어.\n\n\"생각해 봐라.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"너하고 내가 길모퉁이를 돌아 나오다가 딱 그 순간에 이놈하고 마주치다니. 그것도 우산을 단 한 번 휙 던졌을 뿐인데, 마치 갈고리로 콱 낚아챈 것처럼 이놈을 단단히 붙잡다니. 하, 하!\"\n\n\"제가 다리도 꽉 붙들었잖아요, 아버지?\" 어린 와크퍼드가 말했어.\n\n\"그래, 아주 잘했다, 얘야.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 아들 머리를 쓰다듬으며 말했어. \"그러니 다음에 새로 들어오는 애가 가져오는 것 중에 제일 좋은, 단추 다는 윗도리하고 조끼를 상으로 주마. 명심해라. 너는 늘 이 길을 그대로 걷고, 네 아버지가 하는 걸 보고 그대로 하거라. 그러면 죽어서 곧장 천국으로 직행할 거다, 아무도 토 안 달고 말이야.\"\n\n이런 말로 이 기회를 잘 써먹으면서, 스퀴어스 씨는 아들 머리를 다시 한번 쓰다듬고는 스마이크 머리도 쓰다듬었어. 다만 더 세게 말이야. 그러고는 놀리는 말투로 이제 좀 어떠냐고 물었어.\n\n\"집에 가야 해요.\" 스마이크가 미친 듯이 주위를 둘러보며 대답했어.\n\n\"그야 가야지. 거 옳은 말 했다.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 대꾸했어. \"곧 집에 가게 될 거다, 그럼. 일주일이 채 안 돼서 요크셔의 그 평화로운 마을 두더보이즈에 가 있게 될 거다, 젊은 친구. 그리고 다음번에 거기서 또 달아나거든, 그땐 영영 안 돌아와도 좋다고 내가 허락하마. 네가 도망칠 때 입고 간 옷은 어디 있냐, 이 배은망덕한 도둑놈아?\" 스퀴어스 씨가 매서운 목소리로 말했어.\n\n스마이크는 니콜라스가 보살펴 마련해 준 말끔한 옷차림을 흘끗 내려다보며 두 손을 비틀었어.\n\n\"그 재물을 빼돌린 죄로 내가 너를 올드 베일리 밖에 매달아 버릴 수도 있다는 거 알아?\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"남의 집에서 오 파운드어치가 넘는 걸 들고 튀는 게 교수형감이라는 거 알아? 게다가 해부용으로 넘겨질지도 모른다는 거, 나도 확실치는 않다만. 응? 그거 알아? 네가 입었던 그 옷이 얼마짜리였을 거 같아? 네가 신었던 그 웰링턴 부츠가 한 켤레에 이십팔 실링이었고, 구두는 칠 실링 육 펜스였다는 거 알아? 하지만 나한테 온 건 자비를 구하러 제대로 찾아온 거다. 그러니 너한테 그 자비를 베풀어 줄 사람이 바로 나라는 걸 네 별한테 감사하거라.\"\n\n스퀴어스 씨의 속을 모르는 사람이라면, 그가 누구에게든 내줄 그 물건을 잔뜩 쌓아 두기는커녕 아예 다 떨어진 줄로 알았을 거야. 그리고 그가 이 말끝에 우산 끝 쇠붙이로 스마이크의 가슴을 쿡쿡 찌르고, 같은 우산의 살로 그의 머리와 어깨에 매서운 소나기 같은 매질을 퍼붓는 걸 본대도, 의심 많은 사람들의 생각은 별로 달라지지 않았을 거야.\n\n\"내가 삯마차 안에서 애를 패 보긴 처음이군.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 쉬려고 손을 멈추며 말했어. \"불편하긴 한데, 그래도 새롭다 보니 또 색다른 맛이 있단 말이지!\"\n\n가엾은 스마이크! 그는 매를 할 수 있는 한 막아 내다가, 이제 마차 한구석에 웅크려 머리를 두 손에 묻고 팔꿈치를 무릎에 괴고 있었어. 멍하고 얼이 빠진 채로 말이야. 이제 그에게는 말을 건네거나 의논할 친구가 하나도 없으니, 자기가 무슨 짓을 한들 그 전능한 스퀴어스한테서 벗어날 수 있으리라는 생각은 조금도 들지 않았어. 니콜라스가 나타나기 전, 요크셔에서 보낸 그 지긋지긋한 세월 내내 그랬던 것처럼 말이야.\n\n여정은 끝이 없는 것 같았어. 거리 하나를 들어섰다가 또 하나를 지나치기를 거듭하며, 마차는 여전히 덜컹거리며 나아갔어. 마침내 스퀴어스 씨가 삼십 초마다 한 번씩 창밖으로 머리를 내밀며 마부에게 온갖 지시를 고래고래 외쳐 대기 시작했어. 그리고 집들의 생김새와 형편없는 도로 상태로 보아 최근에 지어진 듯한 초라한 거리 몇 군데를 어렵사리 지난 뒤, 스퀴어스 씨가 갑자기 신호 줄을 있는 힘껏 잡아당기며 \"서요!\" 하고 외쳤어.\n\n\"왜 남의 팔을 뽑아 버릴 듯이 잡아당기는 거요?\" 마부가 화난 눈으로 내려다보며 말했어.\n\n\"저 집이오.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어. \"저 단층짜리 작은 집 네 채 중에 두 번째, 초록색 덧창 달린 집 말이오. 문에 놋쇠 명패가 붙어 있고, 스놀리라는 이름이 적혀 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"남의 팔다리를 몸에서 비틀어 뽑지 않고서는 그 말을 못 하겠소?\" 마부가 따져 물었어.\n\n\"못 해!\" 스퀴어스 씨가 고래고래 외쳤어. \"한마디만 더 해 봐, 유리창 깨뜨린 죄로 고발해 버릴 테니. 서요!\"\n\n이 지시에 따라 마차는 스놀리 씨의 집 문 앞에 섰어. 스놀리 씨로 말할 것 같으면, 이 이야기 제4장에서 이야기했듯이, 의붓아들 둘을 스퀴어스 씨의 어버이 같은 보살핌에 맡긴 그 매끈하고 거룩한 척하는 신사로 기억할 거야. 스놀리 씨의 집은 서머스 타운에 잇닿은 새 주택지의 맨 변두리에 있었어. 스퀴어스 씨는 이번에 평소보다 오래 머물게 되어 그 집에 잠깐 셋방을 얻었는데, 늘 묵던 새러슨 여관이 와크퍼드 도련님의 식성을 익히 겪은 터라, 그를 다 큰 손님으로 치지 않고는 받아 주려 하지 않았거든.\n\n\"자, 다 왔다!\" 스퀴어스가 스마이크를 작은 응접실로 떠밀며 말했어. 거기서는 스놀리 씨 부부가 바닷가재로 저녁을 먹고 있었지. \"여기 그 부랑자가, 그 흉악범이, 그 반역자가, 그 배은망덕의 괴물이 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요! 도망쳤던 그 애 말입니까!\" 스놀리가 나이프와 포크를 식탁에 곧추세운 채, 눈을 한껏 휘둥그레 뜨고 외쳤어.\n\n\"바로 그 녀석이지요.\" 스퀴어스가 스마이크의 코앞에 주먹을 들이댔다가 도로 빼기를, 험상궂은 낯빛으로 여러 차례 되풀이하며 말했어. \"여기 부인만 안 계셨어도 한 대 이렇게 — : 됐고, 외상으로 달아 두지.\"\n\n그러고는 스퀴어스 씨가 이 도망자를 어떻게, 어떤 식으로, 언제, 어디서 붙잡았는지 이야기했어.\n\n\"이건 분명 거기에 하느님의 섭리가 있었던 겁니다, 선생.\" 스놀리 씨가 겸손한 척 눈을 내리깔고, 바닷가재 한 점을 꽂은 포크를 천장 쪽으로 치켜들며 말했어.\n\n\"섭리가 저놈한테 등을 돌린 건 틀림없지요.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 코를 긁적이며 대꾸했어. \"당연하지요. 그야 예상한 일이고요. 누구든 알 만한 일이었지요.\"\n\n\"모진 마음과 못된 짓은 절대 잘되는 법이 없지요, 선생.\" 스놀리 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"그런 일은 들어 본 적도 없습니다.\" 스퀴어스가 지갑에서 지폐 한 뭉치를 꺼내 다 무사한지 살펴보며 대꾸했어.\n\n\"내가 말이오, 스놀리 씨.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 이 점을 흡족히 확인하고 나서 말했어. \"내가 저놈의 은인이자, 밥을 먹여 준 사람이자, 가르쳐 준 사람이자, 옷을 입혀 준 사람입니다. 내가 저놈의 고전, 상업, 수학, 철학, 삼각법 친구였단 말입니다. 내 아들, 내 외아들 와크퍼드가 저놈의 형제였고요. 스퀴어스 부인은 저놈의 어머니이자, 할머니이자, 이모였고, 아! 삼촌 노릇까지 도맡았다 해도 좋아요. 그 사람은 댁의 그 사랑스럽고 마음에 쏙 드는 두 아드님 말고는, 그 누구한테도 이 녀석한테만큼 정을 준 적이 없습니다. 그런데 내가 받은 보답이 뭡니까? 내 인정의 젖이 어떻게 됐냐고요. 저놈을 보고 있으면 그게 응고된 우유 덩어리하고 유청으로 변해 버린단 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그럴 만도 하지요, 선생.\" 스놀리 부인이 말했어. \"오! 그럴 만도 하고말고요, 선생.\"\n\n\"이놈이 이때까지 어디 있었답니까?\" 스놀리가 물었어. \"혹시 같이 살았던 게—?\"\n\n\"아, 그래요, 선생!\" 스퀴어스가 다시 그와 마주서며 끼어들었어. \"너 그 빌어먹을 마귀 같은 니클비하고 같이 살았던 거지, 응?\"\n\n하지만 어떤 위협이나 매질로도 이 물음에 스마이크의 입에서 단 한마디 대답도 끌어낼 수 없었어. 다시 끌려가게 될 그 비참한 감옥에서 죽어 버릴지언정, 자기 첫 번째이자 진정한 친구를 끌어들일 단 한 음절도 내뱉지 않겠다고 속으로 다짐했거든. 그는 요크셔에서 함께 떠나올 때 니콜라스가 자기 과거에 대해 단단히 일러두었던 함구령을 벌써 떠올린 터였어. 게다가 자기 은인이 자기를 데리고 나오면서 무슨 끔찍한 죄를 저질렀을지도 모르고, 그게 발각되면 무거운 벌을 받게 될지도 모른다는 어수선하고 갈피 못 잡는 생각이, 그를 지금의 무기력하고 겁에 질린 상태로 몰아넣는 데 어느 정도 한몫했지.\n\n이런 게 스마이크의 마음속에 들어찬 생각들이었어. 쇠약해진 그의 머릿속을 떠도는, 그토록 어설프고 흐릿한 영상들에 그런 말을 갖다 붙일 수 있다면 말이지. 그게 그를 위협에도 설득에도 똑같이 귀먹게 만든 거야. 무슨 수를 써도 소용없다는 걸 알게 되자, 스퀴어스 씨는 그를 위층의 작은 뒷방으로 데려갔어. 거기서 밤을 지내게 한 거지. 그리고 그가 행여 달아날 엄두를 낼 만큼 기운을 그러모을까 봐, 신발과 코트와 조끼를 벗기고, 바깥에서 문을 잠그는 조심까지 한 뒤에, 이 갸륵한 신사는 그를 혼자 생각에 잠기게 내버려 두고 떠났어.\n\n그 생각들이 어떤 것이었는지, 그리고 자기가 떠나온 보금자리와, 거기 얽힌 정든 친구들과 낯익은 얼굴들을 떠올릴 때 — 아니, 그가 한순간이라도 그 생각을 멈춘 적이 있기나 했을까? — 그 가엾은 것의 가슴이 얼마나 철렁 내려앉았는지, 그건 다 말할 수가 없어. 마음이 그토록 무거운 잠에 들 채비를 하려면, 어린 시절에 모진 가혹함과 잔인함으로 그 자람이 멎어 버려야 해. 한 줄기 희망의 빛도 없이 비참함과 고통으로 점철된 세월이 있어야 하지. 다정함과 사랑의 목소리에 재빨리 화답해 뛰던 마음의 현들이, 그 은밀한 자리에서 녹슬고 끊어진 채, 사랑이나 친절의 옛말 한마디 그 메아리조차 남아 있지 않아야 해. 그런 지성의 밤에 앞선 짧은 낮은 참으로 음울했을 테고, 그 길고 긴 황혼은 참으로 흐릿했을 거야.\n\n그래도 그를 일깨워 줄 목소리들이 있긴 했어, 그 순간에도 말이야. 하지만 그 반가운 목소리들은 거기까지 닿을 수가 없었어. 그래서 그는, 니콜라스가 요크셔 학교에서 처음 발견했던 그때와 똑같이, 무기력하고 희망 없고 시들어 버린 모습 그대로 기어 들어가 잠자리에 누웠어.\n\n# CHAPTER 39\n\n한 가엾은 영혼에게 그토록 쓰라림으로 가득 찼던 밤이 물러가고, 밝고 구름 한 점 없는 여름 아침이 찾아왔어. 그 무렵 북부 지방에서 온 우편 마차 한 대가 아직 고요한 이즐링턴 거리를 경쾌한 소리를 내며 가로질렀지. 차장이 활기차게 나팔을 불어 마차가 다가온다는 걸 알리며, 우체국 바로 옆에 있는 정거장을 향해 덜커덕덜커덕 달려갔어.\n\n바깥 좌석에 탄 손님은 마부석에 앉은 건장하고 정직해 보이는 시골 사내 한 명뿐이었어. 그는 세인트 폴 대성당의 둥근 지붕에 눈을 박은 채 감탄에 푹 빠져 있었지. 어찌나 넋을 잃었던지 가방이며 소포를 꺼내는 그 부산한 소동을 도통 알아차리지 못할 정도였어. 그러다 마차 창문 하나가 탁 내려가자, 그제야 그는 고개를 돌렸어. 그리고 마침 그때 쑥 내밀어진 예쁜 여자 얼굴과 마주쳤지.\n\n\"저기 좀 봐, 색시!\" 시골 사내가 자기가 감탄하던 것을 가리키며 소리쳤어. \"저게 폴 성당이여. 워따, 엄청시리 큰 놈이네, 큰 놈이여.\"\n\n\"세상에, 존! 저 절반 크기도 안 될 줄 알았는데. 무슨 괴물 같네요!\"\n\n\"괴물이라!--그 말이 딱 맞구먼, 브로디 부인.\" 시골 사내가 커다란 외투를 걸친 채 느릿느릿 내려오며 사람 좋게 말했어. \"그라믄 저짝 건 뭐로 보이냐?--저기 길 건너편 거 말여. 일 년을 들여다봐도 못 맞힐 거구먼. 저게 다른 게 아니라 우체국이여! 호! 호! 두 배짜리 편지에 요금 매기는 게 당연하지. 우체국이라니! 어찌 생각혀? 워따, 저게 고작 우체국이믄 런던 시장은 대체 어떤 데서 사는지 한번 보고 잡네.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 존 브로디는 — 바로 그 사내였지 — 마차 문을 열었어. 그리고 안을 들여다보며 브로디 부인의, 그러니까 옛날 프라이스 양이었던 그녀의 뺨을 톡톡 두드리더니, 왁자하게 웃음을 터뜨렸어.\n\n\"이런!\" 존이 말했어. \"단추를 다 떼 버려도 좋아, 또 자고 있구먼!\"\n\n\"밤새 잤어요. 어제도 종일 자더라고요. 가끔 일이 분 깨는 것 말고는요.\" 존 브로디의 어여쁜 신부가 대답했어. \"깨었을 땐 어찌나 짜증을 부리던지, 차라리 안 깨었으면 싶었다니까요!\"\n\n이런 말의 주인공은 숄과 망토로 칭칭 싸매여 잠든 형체였어. 그 모습으로는 도무지 남자인지 여자인지 알아낼 도리가 없었지. 다만 머리를 장식한 갈색 비버 가죽 보닛과 초록색 베일이 있어서 겨우 짐작할 수 있었어. 그런데 그 보닛은 이백오십 마일을 오는 동안 — 바로 지금 이 숙녀의 코 고는 소리가 새어 나오는 마차의 그 한구석에서 — 짓눌리고 납작해진 탓에, 존 브로디의 불그레한 얼굴보다 훨씬 덜 잘 웃는 근육이라도 들썩이게 할 만큼 우스꽝스러운 꼴이 되어 있었어.\n\n\"여보쇼!\" 존이 늘어진 베일 한쪽 끝을 잡아당기며 외쳤어. \"자, 일어나 봐, 응?\"\n\n옛 구석 자리로 몇 번이나 더 파고들며 짜증과 피로에 찬 소리를 여러 번 내뱉은 끝에, 그 형체는 끙끙대며 일어나 앉았어. 그러자 구겨진 비버 가죽 더미 아래로, 파란 종이로 만 머리카락이 반원을 그리며 둘러싼 가운데, 패니 스퀴어스 양의 고운 이목구비가 드러났지.\n\n\"어머, 틸다!\" 스퀴어스 양이 외쳤어. \"이 멀쩡한 밤새 나를 어찌나 발로 차 댔는지!\"\n\n\"어머, 그것참 좋은 소리네.\" 친구가 웃으며 대꾸했어. \"마차를 거의 혼자 다 차지하고선 말이야.\"\n\n\"발뺌하지 마, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 자못 진지하게 말했어. \"분명히 그랬으니까. 안 그랬다고 우겨 봐야 소용없어. 잠결이라 몰랐겠지만, 틸다, 난 한숨도 못 붙였어. 그러니까 내 말을 믿어야 한다고 생각해.\"\n\n이렇게 대꾸하면서 스퀴어스 양은 보닛과 베일을 매만졌어. 초자연적인 힘이 끼어들어 자연의 법칙을 송두리째 멈추기 전에는 도무지 어떤 모양으로도 잡힐 수 없는 물건이었지. 그러고는 분명 자기 매무새가 유난히 단정해 보인다고 흐뭇해하면서, 무릎에 쌓인 샌드위치 부스러기와 비스킷 조각을 털어 냈어. 그리고 존 브로디가 내미는 팔에 의지해 마차에서 내려왔지.\n\n\"자,\" 합승 마차가 불려 오고 숙녀들과 짐이 서둘러 실리자 존이 말했어. \"사라네 머리로 가 주쇼.\"\n\n\"어디로요?\" 마부가 외쳤어.\n\n\"어머, 브로디 씨!\" 스퀴어스 양이 끼어들었어. \"별소릴 다! 새러슨즈 헤드예요.\"\n\n\"그렇지,\" 존이 말했어. \"사라네 아들 머리 어쩌고 하는 거였구먼. 그게 어디 있는지 아쇼?\"\n\n\"아, 예! 그건 알지요.\" 마부가 문을 쾅 닫으며 퉁명스레 대답했어.\n\n\"틸다, 정말이지.\" 스퀴어스 양이 항의했어. \"이러다 우리가 뭐로 보일지 모르겠어.\"\n\n\"보고 싶은 대로 보라지.\" 존 브로디가 말했어. \"우리가 런던에 즐기러 온 거 말고 뭐가 있어, 응?\"\n\n\"그러길 바라요, 브로디 씨.\" 스퀴어스 양이 유난히 시무룩한 얼굴로 대답했어.\n\n\"그럼 됐어.\" 존이 말했어. \"아무 상관 없잖여. 나야 결혼한 지 나흘밖에 안 됐어. 불쌍한 우리 아부지가 돌아가시는 바람에 미뤄졌거든. 여기 신혼 일행이 다 모였구먼--신부에 신부 들러리에 신랑까지. 이럴 때도 안 즐기믄 대체 언제 즐기겠어, 응? 젠장, 난 그게 알고 잡다고.\"\n\n그래서 한시도 허비하지 않고 당장 즐기기 시작하려고, 브로디 씨는 아내에게 진한 입맞춤을 했어. 그리고 스퀴어스 양에게서도 한 번 빼앗는 데 성공했지. 그 아가씨가 처녀답게 할퀴고 버둥대며 저항하긴 했지만 말이야. 그 실랑이는 일행이 새러슨즈 헤드에 도착할 때까지도 다 끝나지 않았어.\n\n여기서 일행은 곧장 쉬러 들어갔어. 그렇게 긴 여행 끝이라 잠으로 기운을 차리는 게 꼭 필요했거든. 그리고 정오 무렵에 다시 모여 든든한 아침을 먹었지. 존 브로디 씨가 시킨 대로 위층의 작은 별실에 차려진 식사였어. 그 방에서는 마구간이 한눈에 훤히 내려다보였지.\n\n이제 스퀴어스 양을 보면 말이지, 갈색 비버 가죽 보닛도, 초록 베일도, 파란 종이 말이도 다 벗어 던지고, 처녀다운 화려함으로 한껏 차려입은 모습이었어. 하얀 원피스에 짧은 윗옷을 걸치고, 하얀 모슬린 보닛을 썼는데, 그 안쪽엔 활짝 핀 다마스크 장미 모조품 한 송이가 달려 있었지. 풍성한 머리채는 어찌나 단단하게 말아 올렸는지 어떤 사고로도 풀릴 리 없어 보였어. 보닛 가장자리에는 작은 다마스크 장미들을 둘렀는데, 그건 큰 장미에서 돋아난 장래성 있는 햇가지들로 여겨도 좋을 법했지. 이 모든 걸 다 보고서, 게다가 그 집안의 큰 장미와 작은 장미들에 다 맞춘 넓은 다마스크 허리띠까지 봤다고 치자. 그 띠는 그녀의 가느다란 허리를 둘렀는데, 절묘한 솜씨로 뒤쪽 윗옷이 짧은 걸 가려 주었지. 이 모든 걸 다 바라보고, 거기다 손목을 감싼 산호 팔찌까지 셈에 넣어 보자. 구슬이 좀 모자라고 까만 실이 훤히 비치긴 했지만 말이야. 그리고 목에 걸린 산호 목걸이도 있었는데, 거기엔 원피스 바깥으로 외로운 홍옥수 하트 하나가 매달려 있었어. 그건 임자 없는 그녀의 애정을 상징하는 것이었지. 우리 본성의 가장 순수한 감정에 호소하는 이 말 없되 웅변적인 것들을 죄다 곰곰이 들여다본다면, 늙음의 서리도 녹아내리고, 젊음의 불길에 새롭고 끌 수 없는 땔감이 더해졌을 거야.\n\n웨이터도 마음이 흔들렸어. 웨이터라곤 해도 그에게도 사람의 정념과 감정이 있었거든. 그래서 머핀을 건네면서 스퀴어스 양을 뚫어지게 쳐다봤지.\n\n\"우리 아빠 와 계신지 아세요?\" 스퀴어스 양이 위엄 있게 물었어.\n\n\"뭐라고 하셨죠, 아가씨?\"\n\n\"우리 아빠 말이에요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 되풀이했어. \"와 계세요?\"\n\n\"어디에요, 아가씨?\"\n\n\"여기요--이 집에요!\" 스퀴어스 양이 대답했어. \"우리 아빠--워크퍼드 스퀴어스 씨요--여기 묵고 계시잖아요. 안에 계세요?\"\n\n\"그런 성함의 신사분이 이 집에 계신 줄은 몰랐는데요, 아가씨.\" 웨이터가 대답했어. \"커피실에 계실지도 모르겠네요.\"\n\n계실지도 모른다니. 거참, 듣기 좋은 소리네! 스퀴어스 양은 런던에 오는 내내 친구들에게 자기가 여기서 얼마나 제집처럼 편할지, 그리고 자기 이름과 인맥이 얼마나 깍듯한 대접을 받게 할지 보여 줄 생각만 하고 있었거든. 그런데 아버지가 거기 계실지도 모른다는 소리나 듣다니! \"무슨 떠돌이라도 되는 것처럼!\" 스퀴어스 양이 분에 차서 힘주어 한마디 했어.\n\n\"한번 알아보라구, 이 사람아.\" 존 브로디가 말했어. \"그라고 비둘기 파이 하나 더 갖다주겠어? 젠장할 놈.\" 웨이터가 물러나자 존이 빈 접시를 들여다보며 투덜거렸어. \"이걸 파이라고 부르나--새끼 비둘기 세 마리에 소고기 몇 점, 그리고 어찌나 가벼운 껍질인지 입에 들었는지 사라졌는지도 모르겠구먼. 아침 한 끼에 파이가 대체 몇 개나 드는지 모르겠네!\"\n\n존 브로디가 햄과 차가운 소고기 한 덩이를 먹어 치우는 동안 잠깐 틈이 있었어. 그러고 나서 웨이터가 파이를 하나 더 들고 돌아왔지. 그러면서 스퀴어스 씨는 이 집에 묵는 건 아니지만 날마다 여기 들른다고, 도착하면 바로 위층으로 안내하겠다고 일러 주었어. 그러고는 물러갔지. 그런데 물러난 지 이 분도 안 되어서, 스퀴어스 씨와 그의 미더운 아들을 데리고 다시 돌아왔어.\n\n\"아니, 이게 웬일이냐?\" 스퀴어스 씨가 일행에게 인사를 하고 딸에게서 집안의 은밀한 소식을 좀 전해 들은 뒤 말했어.\n\n\"웬일이긴요, 아빠!\" 그 아가씨가 앵돌아진 목소리로 대꾸했어. \"보세요, 틸다가 드디어 결혼했잖아요.\"\n\n\"내가 런던 구경 한턱내는 길이오, 선생.\" 존이 파이를 힘차게 공략하며 말했어.\n\n\"젊은 사내들이 결혼하면 하는 그런 짓 중 하나지.\" 스퀴어스가 말을 받았어. \"그리고 돈이 눈 깜짝할 새 다 빠져나가는 짓이고! 가령 그 돈을 모아 뒀다가 어린 사내애들 교육에 쓰면 얼마나 더 나으냐고! 그놈들은 말이지,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 훈계조로 말했어. \"당신이 미처 알아채기도 전에 들이닥친다구. 내 새끼들도 나한테 그랬으니까.\"\n\n\"좀 드시겠어?\" 존이 말했어.\n\n\"난 됐네.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"그래도 어린 워크퍼드한테 기름진 거 좀 먹게 해 주면 고맙겠구먼. 손가락으로 집어서 주게. 안 그러면 웨이터가 계산서에 올려 버리니까. 이런 음식은 그러지 않아도 이문이 많이 남거든. 웨이터 오는 소리 들리면, 얘야, 얼른 주머니에 쑤셔 넣고 창밖을 내다봐, 알겠지?\"\n\n\"깨어 있어요, 아버지.\" 효성 지극한 워크퍼드가 대답했어.\n\n\"자,\" 스퀴어스가 딸에게 돌아서며 말했어. \"이젠 네가 결혼할 차례다. 서둘러야지.\"\n\n\"어머, 난 안 급해요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 아주 쌀쌀맞게 말했어.\n\n\"아니라고, 패니?\" 옛 친구가 짓궂은 기색으로 외쳤어.\n\n\"그래, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 거세게 고개를 저으며 대답했어. \"난 기다릴 수 있어.\"\n\n\"젊은 사내들도 기다릴 수 있나 보네, 패니.\" 브로디 부인이 한마디 했어.\n\n\"내가 끌어들인 게 아니잖아, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 쏘아붙였어.\n\n\"그래.\" 친구가 받았어. \"그건 더없이 맞는 말이지.\"\n\n이 대답의 비꼬는 말투에 스퀴어스 양은 꽤 매서운 반박을 했을지도 몰라. 그녀는 타고나길 심술궂은 성미인 데다--지금은 여행과 방금까지의 덜컹거림으로 더 사나워진 참이었지--옛 기억과 브로디 씨를 향한 자기 계획이 실패한 일로 적잖이 약이 올라 있었거든. 그리고 그 매서운 반박은 또 다른 수많은 반박으로 이어졌을 테고, 그게 또 무슨 일로 번졌을지는 하늘만 알았겠지. 만약 바로 그 순간 스퀴어스 씨가 우연히 화제를 바꿔 놓지 않았더라면 말이야.\n\n\"이게 무슨 일인지 알겠나?\" 그 신사가 말했어. \"워크퍼드하고 내가 누구를 붙잡았는지 짐작이나 하겠어?\"\n\n\"아빠! 설마 그--?\" 스퀴어스 양은 말을 끝맺지 못했지만, 브로디 부인이 대신 끝맺으며 덧붙였어. \"니클비요?\"\n\n\"아니.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"그래도 그 바로 옆사람이긴 하지.\"\n\n\"설마 스마이크는 아니겠죠?\" 스퀴어스 양이 손뼉을 치며 외쳤어.\n\n\"아니, 바로 그놈이다.\" 아버지가 받았어. \"꽉 붙들어 놨지, 단단히.\"\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 존 브로디가 접시를 밀어 내며 외쳤어. \"그 불쌍한--빌어먹을 놈을 잡았다고? 어디서?\"\n\n\"왜, 내 하숙집 꼭대기 뒷방에 있지.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"한쪽엔 그놈, 한쪽엔 열쇠가 있고 말이야.\"\n\n\"당신 하숙집에! 당신 하숙집에 그놈을 잡아 뒀다고? 호! 호! 선생이 온 잉글랜드를 다 이겼구먼. 손 좀 줘 봐, 이 사람아. 빌어먹을, 이건 손이라도 한번 잡아 줘야겠어.--당신 하숙집에 잡아 뒀다고?\"\n\n\"그래.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. 건장한 요크셔 사내가 축하랍시고 가슴을 친 통에 의자째 휘청거리면서 말이야. \"고맙구먼. 또 그러진 말게. 호의로 그러는 줄은 알지만, 좀 아프단 말이야. 그래, 그놈이 거기 있어. 나쁘지 않지, 응?\"\n\n\"나쁘지 않냐고!\" 존 브로디가 되받았어. \"듣기만 해도 사람 간이 다 떨어지겠구먼.\"\n\n\"좀 놀랄 줄 알았지.\" 스퀴어스가 두 손을 비비며 말했어. \"꽤 깔끔하게 해치웠어. 게다가 아주 잽싸게 말이야.\"\n\n\"우찌 된 일이여?\" 존이 그 옆에 바싹 다가앉으며 물었어. \"다 얘기해 봐, 이 사람아. 어서, 빨리!\"\n\n존 브로디의 조급함을 다 따라가진 못했지만, 스퀴어스 씨는 스마이크가 어떤 운 좋은 우연으로 자기 손에 떨어졌는지 되도록 빨리 들려주었어. 그리고 듣는 이들의 감탄 어린 말이 끼어들 때 말고는, 이야기를 끝맺을 때까지 한 번도 멈추지 않았지.\n\n\"혹시라도 어찌어찌 그놈이 내 손을 빠져나갈까 봐,\" 스퀴어스가 이야기를 마치고 아주 교활한 표정을 지으며 말했어. \"내일 아침 바깥 좌석을 셋 끊어 뒀어--워크퍼드하고 그놈하고 나, 이렇게 말이야. 그리고 회계 정리하고 새 학생 받는 일은 대리인한테 맡기기로 해 놨지, 알겠어? 그러니 자네가 오늘 와서 정말 다행이야. 안 그랬으면 우릴 못 봤을 거 아닌가. 사정이 이러니, 오늘 밤에 와서 나하고 차라도 한잔하지 않으면, 우리가 떠나기 전엔 더 못 보게 될 거야.\"\n\n\"두말 말어.\" 요크셔 사내가 그의 손을 흔들며 대꾸했어. \"이십 마일 거리라도 가겠구먼.\"\n\n\"아니, 정말 그러겠어?\" 스퀴어스 씨가 받았어. 이렇게 선뜻 초대를 받아들일 줄은 미처 몰랐던 모양이야. 알았더라면 청하기 전에 두 번은 생각했겠지.\n\n존 브로디는 그저 그의 손을 한 번 더 꽉 쥐는 걸로 대답을 대신했어. 그리고 런던 구경은 내일부터 시작할 테니 여섯 시까지는 틀림없이 스놀리 씨 댁에 가 있겠노라고 다짐했지. 그러고서 좀 더 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 스퀴어스 씨와 아들은 떠났어.\n\n그날 나머지 시간 동안 브로디 씨는 아주 묘하고 들뜬 상태였어. 가끔 웃음을 펑 터뜨리고는, 모자를 집어 들고 마차 마당으로 달려 나가 혼자 실컷 웃어 댔지. 어찌나 안절부절못하던지, 끊임없이 들락날락하고, 손가락을 딱딱 튕기고, 투박한 시골 춤사위 한 토막씩을 추는가 하면, 한마디로 어찌나 유별나게 굴던지, 스퀴어스 양은 그가 미쳐 가는 거라고 단정했어. 그러고는 사랑하는 틸다더러 너무 속상해하지 말라면서, 자기 의심을 죄다 입 밖으로 털어놨지. 그렇지만 브로디 부인은 별로 놀라는 기색도 없이, 전에도 한 번 그가 이러는 걸 봤다고 했어. 그러고서 거의 틀림없이 앓아눕긴 하겠지만 그리 큰일은 아니라고, 그러니 그냥 혼자 내버려 두는 게 낫다고 했지.\n\n결과를 보니 그녀의 말이 완벽하게 맞았어. 그날 밤 다들 스놀리 씨네 응접실에 앉아 있을 때, 막 어둑어둑해지기 시작할 무렵, 존 브로디가 어찌나 심하게 앓는지, 머리가 무섭도록 어지럽다고 하는 통에, 온 일행이 더없는 경악에 빠졌거든. 그래도 그 자리에서 정신줄을 놓지 않은 사람은 그의 착한 아내뿐이었어. 그녀는 스퀴어스 씨의 침대에 한 시간쯤 눕혀 두고 완전히 혼자 내버려 두면, 앓아누운 만큼이나 빨리 틀림없이 도로 회복될 거라고 했지. 의사를 부르기 전에 이렇게 그럴듯한 제안의 효험을 한번 시험해 보자는데, 누구도 마다할 수 없었어. 그래서 존은 큰 어려움 끝에 부축을 받아 위층으로 올라갔지. 어찌나 엄청난 덩치였던지, 세 계단을 끌어 올릴 때마다 어김없이 두 계단씩 도로 미끄러져 내렸거든. 그리고 침대에 눕혀진 뒤 아내에게 맡겨졌어. 아내는 잠시 후 응접실에 다시 나타나, 그가 깊이 잠들었다는 반가운 소식을 전했지.\n\n그런데 사실인즉, 바로 그 순간 존 브로디는 침대에 앉아 있었어. 여태껏 본 적 없을 만큼 새빨개진 얼굴로, 베개 모서리를 입에 쑤셔 넣고 있었지. 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨리지 않으려고 말이야. 그 감정을 가까스로 억누르자마자 그는 신발을 벗어 던졌어. 그리고 포로가 갇혀 있는 옆방으로 살금살금 기어가, 바깥쪽에 꽂힌 열쇠를 돌렸지. 그러고는 안으로 쏜살같이 뛰어들어, 스마이크가 소리 한번 내기도 전에 그 커다란 손으로 입을 틀어막았어.\n\n\"이런, 날 모르겠어, 이 사람아?\" 요크셔 사내가 어리둥절한 청년에게 속삭였어. \"브로디여. 선생이 흠씬 두들겨 맞은 뒤에 자네를 만났던 그 사람 말이여.\"\n\n\"네, 네.\" 스마이크가 외쳤어. \"아! 절 좀 도와주세요.\"\n\n\"도와 달라고!\" 존이 이 말이 떨어지기 무섭게 다시 그의 입을 막으며 대꾸했어. \"자네가 세상천지 둘도 없는 멍청이만 아니었어도 도움 따위 필요 없었어. 그라믄 대체 여긴 뭐 하러 왔어?\"\n\n\"저 사람이 데려왔어요. 아! 저 사람이 절 데려왔다고요.\" 스마이크가 외쳤어.\n\n\"데려왔다고!\" 존이 받았어. \"그라믄 왜 그놈 머리통을 한 대 쥐어박거나, 땅바닥에 드러누워 발버둥 치면서 순경 불러 달라고 소리치지 그랬어? 나 같으믄 자네 나이 때 그런 놈 열둘은 두들겨 팼겠구먼. 하긴 자네는 가엾게도 다 부서져 버린 사람이지.\" 존이 서글프게 말했어. \"이 양반의 가장 약한 피조물 중 하나를 두고 큰소리쳤으니, 하느님 용서하소서!\"\n\n스마이크가 뭔가 말하려고 입을 열었지만, 존 브로디가 그를 막았어.\n\n\"가만있어.\" 요크셔 사내가 말했어. \"그라고 내가 말하랄 때까진 한마디도 입 떼지 말어.\"\n\n이렇게 주의를 준 뒤, 존 브로디는 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕였어. 그리고 주머니에서 드라이버를 꺼내, 아주 신중하고 능숙한 솜씨로 자물쇠 통을 떼어 냈지. 그러고는 그 연장과 함께 바닥에 내려놓았어.\n\n\"이거 보이지?\" 존이 말했어. \"이건 자네가 한 짓이여. 자, 튀어!\"\n\n스마이크는 그 말뜻을 못 알아듣는 듯, 그를 멍하니 쳐다봤어.\n\n\"튀라니까, 튀어.\" 존이 다급하게 되풀이했어. \"자네 사는 데 어딘지 알지? 알아? 좋아. 저게 자네 옷이여, 아니믄 선생 옷이여?\"\n\n\"제 거예요.\" 스마이크가 대답했어. 요크셔 사내가 그를 옆방으로 급히 끌고 가, 의자 위에 놓인 신발 한 켤레와 외투를 가리켰지.\n\n\"걸쳐.\" 존이 한쪽 팔을 엉뚱한 소매에 억지로 쑤셔 넣고, 외투 자락을 도망자의 목에 둘둘 감으며 말했어. \"자, 날 따라와. 그라고 바깥문에 나서면 오른쪽으로 돌아. 그라믄 자네가 지나가는 걸 못 볼 거여.\"\n\n\"하지만--하지만--제가 문 닫는 소리를 들을 거예요.\" 스마이크가 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨며 대꾸했어.\n\n\"그라믄 아예 닫지 말어.\" 존 브로디가 받았어. \"젠장, 설마 선생이 감기 들까 봐 겁나는 건 아니지?\"\n\n\"아--아뇨.\" 스마이크가 이를 딱딱 부딪치며 말했어. \"하지만 저 사람이 전에도 절 도로 끌고 왔어요. 또 그럴 거예요. 그럴 거예요, 정말 그럴 거라고요.\"\n\n\"그러긴 그래!\" 존이 답답하다는 듯 받았어. \"안 그래, 안 그런다니까. 들어 봐! 난 이걸 이웃끼리 좋게 처리하고 싶어. 자네가 혼자 알아서 도망친 걸로 믿게 만들고 싶다 이거여. 그래도 만약 자네가 빠져나가는 사이에 저놈이 저 응접실에서 나오기라도 하믄, 제 뼈다귀나 잘 간수해야 할 거여. 난 봐줄 생각 없으니까. 저놈이 곧 알아채면, 내가 엉뚱한 데로 냄새를 풍겨 줄 테니 걱정 말어. 그러니 자네가 마음만 단단히 먹으믄, 도망친 걸 저놈들이 알기도 전에 집에 가 있을 거여. 가자!\"\n\n스마이크는 이 말을 딱 격려라는 것만 알아들을 만큼은 알아듣고, 비틀거리는 걸음으로 따라나설 채비를 했어. 그때 존이 그의 귀에 대고 속삭였지.\n\n\"젊은 선생한테 이렇게 전해 줘. 내가 틸리 프라이스하고 짝이 됐고, 새러슨에 편지 보내면 소식 닿을 거라고. 그라고 난 그 양반 질투하지 않는다고 말이여--젠장, 그날 밤 생각만 하믄 배꼽이 빠질 것 같구먼! 워따, 지금도 눈에 선해. 그 얇은 버터 빵을 정신없이 먹어 치우던 꼴이!\"\n\n존에게는 그 순간 좀 위험한 기억이었어. 하마터면 큰 소리로 껄껄 웃음을 터뜨릴 뻔했거든. 그래도 가까스로 제때에 안간힘을 써서 참고는, 스마이크를 뒤에 끌고 살그머니 아래층으로 내려갔어. 그리고 누구든 먼저 나오는 사람과 맞닥뜨리려고 응접실 문 바로 옆에 자리를 잡고서, 그에게 어서 내빼라고 손짓했지.\n\n여기까지 오자 스마이크는 두 번 시킬 것도 없었어. 현관문을 살며시 열고, 고마움과 두려움이 뒤섞인 눈길을 자기 구원자에게 던지더니, 일러 준 방향으로 바람처럼 내달렸지.\n\n요크셔 사내는 몇 분 더 제자리를 지켰어. 그러다 안에서 이야기가 끊이지 않는 걸 알고는, 들키지 않게 도로 살금살금 기어 올라갔지. 그러고는 계단 난간에 기대 꼬박 한 시간을 귀 기울이고 서 있었어. 모든 게 더없이 조용하자, 그는 다시 스퀴어스 씨의 침대로 기어들었어. 그러고는 이불을 머리까지 끌어 올린 채, 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이 되도록 웃어 댔지.\n\n그 곁에 누군가 있어서 이불이 어떻게 들썩이는지, 또 요크셔 사내의 커다랗고 빨간 얼굴과 둥근 머리가 시트 위로 이따금 불쑥 솟아오르는 걸 봤더라면 말이야. 마치 어떤 유쾌한 괴물이 숨을 쉬러 수면 위로 떠올랐다가, 새로 터져 나오는 웃음에 다시 발작하듯 잠겨 드는 것 같았거든--그 누군가가 있었다면, 존 브로디 자신 못지않게 즐거워했을 거야.\n\n# CHAPTER 40\n\n니콜라스가 사랑에 빠지는 이야기. 그는 중재자를 한 사람 내세우는데, 그의 활약은 단 한 가지만 빼고 뜻밖의 성공을 거둔다.\n\n오랜 박해자의 손아귀에서 다시 한번 벗어나자, 스마이크가 끌어모을 수 있는 온 힘과 안간힘을 다 짜내는 데에 새삼스런 자극 따위는 필요 없었다. 자기가 가고 있는 길이 어디인지, 그게 집 쪽으로 향하는 길인지 반대 방향인지 따져 볼 겨를도 없이, 그는 놀라울 만큼 빠르고 한결같은 마음으로 달아났다. 오직 두려움만이 달 수 있는 날개를 단 채, 또 똑똑히 기억나는 스퀴어스의 목소리로 들려오는 환청 같은 고함 소리에 떠밀려서. 가엾은 그 사내의 어지러운 감각 속에서는, 스퀴어스가 한 무리의 추격자들과 함께 바짝 뒤를 쫓아오는 것만 같았다. 어떤 때는 그것이 한참 뒤로 멀어졌다가, 또 어떤 때는 점점 빠르게 가까워졌다. 희망과 공포가 번갈아 그를 뒤흔드는 대로 말이다. 그 소리들이 그저 흥분한 머릿속이 만들어 낸 것뿐이라는 걸 확실히 깨달은 뒤로도, 그는 한참을 더 내달렸다. 쇠약함과 탈진도 거의 늦추지 못할 만큼 빠른 걸음으로. 시골길의 어둠과 고요가 그를 바깥세상의 사물로 다시 끌어다 놓고, 머리 위의 별이 총총한 하늘이 시간이 얼마나 빠르게 흘렀는지 일러 주고 나서야, 그는 비로소 멈춰 섰다. 먼지를 뒤집어쓰고 숨을 헐떡이며, 귀를 기울이고 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n사방이 잠잠하고 고요했다. 멀리서 빛이 환하게 번지며 하늘에 따스한 노을빛을 드리운 곳, 거기가 거대한 도시가 자리한 자리였다. 울타리와 도랑으로 갈라진 외딴 들판들이, 그가 달아나면서 부딪치고 기어서 넘은 곳도 많았는데, 그 들판들이 그가 온 길 양옆으로, 또 길 건너편으로도 죽 펼쳐져 있었다. 어느새 밤이 깊었다. 그가 지나온 그런 샛길로는 추격자들도 그를 따라잡기 어려웠다. 그러니 그가 자기 거처로 돌아갈 수 있다면, 분명 바로 이런 시각에, 어둠을 틈타서여야 했다. 이 사실은 차츰차츰, 스마이크의 머릿속에도 꽤 또렷하게 떠올랐다. 처음에 그는 어렴풋하고 어린아이 같은 생각을 품었다. 시골로 한 십이 마일쯤 더 들어갔다가, 런던을 멀찍이 피해 크게 빙 돌아서 집으로 돌아가자는 거였다. 혼자 거리를 가로지르는 게 어찌나 무서웠던지, 또다시 그 무서운 적과 마주칠까 봐 두려웠던 것이다. 하지만 이런 생각들이 떠올려 준 결론에 따르기로 하고, 그는 발길을 돌렸다. 그리고 두렵고 미심쩍은 마음이 적잖았지만, 큰길로 나서서 다시 런던을 향해 걸었다. 스퀴어스의 그 임시 거처를 떠날 때 못지않게 빠른 걸음으로.\n\n그가 도시 서쪽 끝으로 다시 들어섰을 무렵엔 가게들이 거의 다 문을 닫은 뒤였다. 낮의 더위가 가시자 거리로 나왔던 그 많은 사람들 중에도 거리에 남은 이는 몇 안 되었고, 그나마도 어슬렁어슬렁 집으로 가는 길이었다. 하지만 그는 그 사람들에게 이따금 길을 물었고, 묻고 또 물은 끝에 마침내 뉴먼 노그스가 사는 곳에 다다랐다.\n\n그날 저녁 내내, 뉴먼은 지금 자기 집 문을 두드리는 바로 그 사람을 찾아 골목과 구석구석을 뒤지고 다녔다. 그러는 동안 니콜라스도 다른 방향으로 똑같이 찾아 헤맸다. 뉴먼이 우울한 얼굴로 초라한 저녁을 들고 있는데, 스마이크의 머뭇거리고 자신 없는 노크 소리가 그의 귀에 들려왔다. 불안하고 무언가를 기다리는 마음에 작은 소리에도 곤두서 있던 뉴먼은 서둘러 아래층으로 내려갔다. 그러고는 반갑고 놀란 외마디 소리를 지르며 이 고마운 손님을 복도로, 다시 위층으로 끌어올렸다. 한마디도 하지 않다가, 자기 다락방에 무사히 들여놓고 등 뒤로 문을 닫고 나서야, 진과 물을 큰 잔 가득 섞었다. 그리고 떼쓰는 아이 입에 약사발을 갖다 대듯 스마이크의 입에 갖다 대고는, 한 방울도 남기지 말고 다 마시라고 명령했다.\n\n그런데 스마이크가 그 귀한 혼합주에 입술만 살짝 대고 마는 걸 보고는, 뉴먼은 유난히 맥이 빠진 표정이 되었다. 그는 가엾은 친구의 약한 꼴에 깊은 동정의 한숨을 쉬며 그 잔을 자기 입으로 가져가던 참이었다. 그때 스마이크가 자기가 겪은 모험을 이야기하기 시작하는 바람에 그는 손을 멈췄고, 잔을 손에 든 채 가만히 서서 귀를 기울였다.\n\n스마이크가 이야기를 이어 가는 동안 뉴먼에게 일어난 변화를 보는 건 꽤나 우스운 노릇이었다. 처음에 그는 손등으로 입술을 문지르며 서 있었다. 한 모금 들이켤 마음의 준비를 하는 의식 같은 거였다. 그러다 스퀴어스 이야기가 나오자, 잔을 겨드랑이에 끼고 눈을 휘둥그렇게 뜨더니, 잔뜩 놀란 얼굴로 지켜보았다. 스마이크가 삯마차 안에서 자기를 덮쳤다는 대목에 이르자, 그는 황급히 잔을 탁자에 내려놓고는 방 안을 절뚝거리며 왔다 갔다 했다. 잔뜩 흥분한 채로, 이따금 더 귀를 기울이려는 듯 홱 멈춰 서기도 하면서. 존 브라우디 이야기가 나오자, 그는 천천히, 차츰차츰 의자에 주저앉았다. 그러고는 무릎에 두 손을 비벼 댔는데 — 이야기가 절정에 다다를수록 점점 더 빠르게 비벼 대다가 — 마침내 \"하! 하!\" 하고 한 번 크고 우렁차게 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그 웃음을 토해 내자마자 그의 얼굴은 곧바로 다시 어두워졌고, 더없이 불안한 얼굴로 물었다. 존 브라우디하고 스퀴어스가 주먹다짐을 벌였을 것 같으냐고.\n\n\"아니요! 그렇진 않을 거예요.\" 스마이크가 대답했다. \"제가 완전히 벗어날 때까지 그 사람은 제가 없어진 걸 몰랐을 거예요.\"\n\n뉴먼은 크게 실망한 외마디 소리와 함께 머리를 긁적였다. 그러고는 다시 한번 잔을 들어 그 속에 든 걸 입에 댔다. 그러면서 잔 가장자리 너머로 스마이크를 향해 음산하고 섬뜩한 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"여기 있어.\" 뉴먼이 말했다. \"너 지쳤잖아 — 녹초가 됐어. 네가 돌아왔다고 내가 알려 줄게. 다들 너 때문에 반쯤 미칠 지경이었거든. 니콜라스 도련님은—\"\n\n\"그분께 신의 축복이 있기를!\" 스마이크가 외쳤다.\n\n\"아멘!\" 뉴먼이 받아 말했다. \"그분은 잠시도 쉬거나 마음 편할 틈이 없었어. 노부인도, 케이트 아가씨도 마찬가지였고.\"\n\n\"네, 네. 그 아가씨가 제 생각을 했다고요?\" 스마이크가 말했다. \"정말 그랬어요? 아, 정말요, 정말요? 아니면 그렇다고 말하지 마세요.\"\n\n\"했어.\" 뉴먼이 외쳤다. \"그 아가씬 아름다운 만큼 마음씨도 고결하거든.\"\n\n\"네, 네!\" 스마이크가 외쳤다. \"맞는 말이에요!\"\n\n\"어찌나 온화하고 상냥한지.\" 뉴먼이 말했다.\n\n\"네, 네!\" 스마이크가 점점 더 간절하게 외쳤다.\n\n\"게다가 그렇게 진실하고 늠름한 기개까지 갖췄지.\" 뉴먼이 말을 이었다.\n\n그가 신이 나서 계속 떠들려는데, 마침 옆을 보니 스마이크가 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 있었다. 그리고 손가락 사이로 눈물이 흘러나오고 있었다.\n\n조금 전만 해도 그 소년의 눈은 여느 때 없던 불꽃으로 반짝였고, 얼굴 구석구석이 흥분으로 환하게 빛나, 그 순간만큼은 아주 딴사람처럼 보였는데 말이다.\n\n\"이런, 이런.\" 뉴먼은 조금 어리둥절한 듯 중얼거렸다. \"이런 천성을 타고난 애가 그런 시련을 겪었다니, 생각하면 나도 한두 번 마음이 아렸지. 이 가엾은 녀석도 — 그래, 그래 — 똑같이 느끼는 거야 — 그래서 마음이 약해진 거지 — 옛날 그 비참했던 시절이 떠오르는 거야. 허! 그런 거지? 그래, 그게—흠!\"\n\n이렇게 띄엄띄엄 내뱉은 혼잣말의 어조로 보아, 뉴먼 노그스가 정작 그 말들이 그런 감정을 불러일으킨 까닭을 만족스럽게 풀어 준다고 여겼는지는 도무지 분명하지 않았다. 그는 한동안 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 자세로 앉아, 이따금 불안하고 미심쩍은 눈길로 스마이크를 바라보았다. 그 눈길은 그가 그 생각거리와 그리 동떨어진 처지가 아니라는 걸 충분히 보여 주었다.\n\n마침내 그는 아까 한 제안을 되풀이했다. 스마이크는 그날 밤 여기 그대로 있고, 자기가 — 그러니까 노그스가 — 곧장 오두막으로 가서 식구들의 애타는 마음을 덜어 주겠다는 거였다. 하지만 스마이크가 친구들을 다시 보고 싶어 견딜 수 없다며 그 말을 듣지 않았다. 그래서 둘은 결국 함께 길을 나섰다. 그런데 이때쯤엔 밤이 한참 깊었고, 게다가 스마이크는 발이 어찌나 부르텄던지 기다시피 겨우 걸을 지경이라, 그들이 목적지에 다다른 건 해 뜨기 한 시간 전쯤이었다.\n\n집 밖에서 두 사람의 목소리가 처음 들려오자, 잃어버린 보호 대상을 되찾을 궁리를 짜내느라 한숨도 못 잔 니콜라스가 침대에서 벌떡 일어나, 기쁘게 둘을 맞아들였다. 시끌벅적한 이야기와 축하와 분개가 어찌나 오갔던지, 나머지 식구들도 곧 잠에서 깼다. 스마이크는 따뜻하고 다정한 환영을 받았다. 케이트뿐 아니라 니클비 부인한테서도 말이다. 부인은 앞으로도 그를 아끼고 잘 봐주겠다고 약속했다. 그러고는 친절하게도, 그와 둘러앉은 식구들의 흥을 돋워 주려고 아주 놀라운 이야기 하나를 들려주었다. 어느 책에서 읽은 거라는데 그 책 제목은 한 번도 안 적이 없었고, 어떤 감옥에서 기적처럼 탈출한 이야기라는데 무슨 감옥인지는 기억이 안 났고, 그 탈출을 해낸 건 어떤 장교라는데 이름은 잊어버렸으며, 그 장교가 무슨 죄로 갇혔던 건지도 또렷이 떠오르지 않는, 그런 이야기였다.\n\n처음에 니콜라스는 스마이크를 빼돌리려던 이 대담한 시도가(하마터면 성공할 뻔했다) 어느 정도는 삼촌의 짓이려니 했다. 하지만 좀 더 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에, 그 공로 전부가 스퀴어스 씨한테 돌아간다고 보는 쪽으로 마음이 기울었다. 일이 정말 어떻게 된 건지 존 브라우디를 통해 알아낼 수 있으면 알아내야겠다고 마음먹고, 그는 날마다 하는 일에 나섰다. 가는 길에 그는 그 요크셔 교장을 벌줄 갖가지 방법을 궁리했다. 하나같이 응보적 정의의 가장 엄정한 원칙에 바탕을 둔 것들이었지만, 죄다 도무지 실행할 수 없다는 단 하나의 흠이 있었다.\n\n\"날씨 좋은 아침이네요, 링킨워터 씨!\" 니콜라스가 사무실에 들어서며 말했다.\n\n\"아!\" 팀이 대꾸했다. \"시골 타령은 무슨! 자, 이런 날을 어떻게 생각하나 — 런던의 하루 말일세 — 어때?\"\n\n\"시내를 좀 벗어나면 공기가 조금 더 맑긴 하죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했다.\n\n\"맑다고!\" 팀 링킨워터가 따라 외쳤다. \"내 침실 창문에서 한번 봐야 하는데.\"\n\n\"제 방 창문에서도 봐야 하고요.\" 니콜라스가 웃으며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"쳇! 쳇!\" 팀 링킨워터가 말했다. \"내게 그런 소리 말게. 시골이라니!\" (팀에게는 보우 같은 데도 영락없는 시골이었다.) \"말도 안 돼! 시골에서 뭘 얻을 수 있겠나, 갓 낳은 달걀하고 꽃밖에 더 있어? 갓 낳은 달걀이야 아침 먹기 전에 아무 날이나 레든홀 시장에서 살 수 있지. 꽃으로 말하자면, 위층에 한달음에 올라가서 내 미뇨네트 냄새를 맡거나, 골목 6번지 뒤쪽 다락 창문에 핀 겹꽃 꽃무를 볼 만한 가치가 있다네.\"\n\n\"골목 6번지에 겹꽃 꽃무가 있단 말이죠?\" 니콜라스가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 있고말고!\" 팀이 대꾸했다. \"주둥이도 떨어져 나간 금 간 항아리에 심어져 있지. 지난봄엔 거기 히아신스도 피었는데, 어디 핀 거냐면 — 아니, 자넨 어차피 웃겠지.\"\n\n\"뭘 보고요?\"\n\n\"낡은 구두약 병에서 핀 걸 보고 말일세.\" 팀이 말했다.\n\n\"전 안 웃어요, 정말로요.\" 니콜라스가 받아 말했다.\n\n팀은 잠시 그를 그윽이 바라보았다. 마치 이 대답의 어조에 힘을 얻어 그 일에 대해 더 털어놓아도 되겠다 싶은 듯이. 그러고는 다듬던 펜을 귀 뒤에 꽂고, 칼을 딸깍 경쾌하게 접더니 말했다.\n\n\"그 꽃들은 병약하고 자리보전한 곱사등이 소년의 것이라네. 그리고 그게 그 애의 그 서글픈 삶에서 하나뿐인 낙인 것 같아, 니클비 군. 그 애를 처음 눈여겨본 게 몇 해 전인지,\" 팀이 곰곰이 헤아리며 말했다. \"조그만 목발 한 쌍에 몸을 끌면서 다니던, 아주 어린아이였을 때 말이야. 글쎄! 글쎄! 그리 오래되진 않았어. 하지만 다른 일들을 생각하면 아무것도 아닐 것 같은 그 세월이, 그 애를 생각하면 길고 긴 세월처럼 느껴진다네. 참 서글픈 일이지,\" 팀이 말을 끊었다가 이었다. \"조그맣게 몸이 비뚤어진 아이가 다른 아이들하고 떨어져 앉아서, 활기차고 즐거운 그 애들이 노는 걸 지켜보는 모습은 말이야. 자긴 끼어들 힘도 없는 그 놀이를. 그 애 때문에 내 가슴이 참 자주 아렸지.\"\n\n\"그렇게 매일같이 바쁜 일에서 마음을 떼어 그런 걸 헤아릴 줄 아는 건,\" 니콜라스가 말했다. \"선한 마음이지요. 아까 하시던 말씀이—\"\n\n\"그 꽃들이 그 가엾은 소년 거라는 얘기 말이지.\" 팀이 말했다. \"그게 다야. 날씨가 좋아서 침대에서 기어 나올 수 있으면, 그 애는 의자를 창문 가까이 끌어다 거기 앉아서, 하루 종일 꽃을 바라보고 매만진다네. 처음엔 그저 고개만 끄덕이곤 했는데, 그러다 우리는 말을 나누는 사이가 됐지. 예전엔 아침에 내가 부르면서 좀 어떠냐고 물으면, 그 애는 빙긋 웃으며 '더 나아졌어요!' 하고 답하곤 했어. 그런데 이젠 고개를 가로젓고는, 그저 자기 낡은 화분들 위로 더 가까이 몸을 숙일 뿐이라네. 그렇게 여러 달을 어두운 지붕들하고 흘러가는 구름이나 바라보고 있자면 따분할 텐데도, 그 애는 참 잘 견딘다네.\"\n\n\"집안에 그 애를 위로해 주거나 도와줄 사람이 아무도 없나요?\" 니콜라스가 물었다.\n\n\"아버지가 거기 사는 것 같긴 해.\" 팀이 대답했다. \"다른 사람들도 있고. 하지만 그 가엾고 병든 절름발이를 그리 마음에 두는 사람은 아무도 없는 듯해. 내가 아주 자주 물어봤다네, 너를 위해 내가 해 줄 게 없겠냐고. 그 애 대답은 늘 똑같아. '없어요.' 요즘 들어 목소리가 점점 약해지고 있지만, 그래도 늘 그 똑같은 대답을 하는 게 눈에 보이지. 이젠 침대를 떠나지도 못해서, 식구들이 침대를 창문 바로 옆으로 옮겨 줬어. 그래서 그 애는 하루 종일 거기 누워, 어떤 땐 하늘을 보고 어떤 땐 자기 꽃을 보지. 그 꽃을 아직도 제 가느다란 손으로 어떻게든 다듬고 물을 준다네. 밤이 되어 내 촛불이 보이면, 그 애는 커튼을 젖혀 두고 내가 잠자리에 들 때까지 그렇게 둬. 내가 거기 있다는 걸 아는 게 그 애한테 그렇게 큰 벗이 되는가 봐. 그래서 나도 한 시간이고 그 이상이고 자주 창가에 앉아 있곤 한다네. 내가 아직 깨어 있는 걸 그 애가 볼 수 있게 말이야. 그리고 가끔은 밤중에 일어나서, 그 애 작은 방의 흐릿하고 우울한 불빛을 바라보며, 그 애가 깨어 있을까 잠들었을까 헤아려 본다네.\n\n밤은 머지않아 올 거야.\" 팀이 말했다. \"그 애가 잠들어 다시는 이 땅에서 깨어나지 않을 밤 말이야. 우리는 평생 한 번도 손 한 번 맞잡은 적 없지만, 그래도 난 그 애를 오랜 친구처럼 그리워하게 될 걸세. 시골 꽃 중에 이 꽃들만큼 내 마음을 끄는 게 있겠나, 자네 생각엔? 아니면 말일세, 여태 만들어진 가장 어려운 라틴어 이름이 붙은 가장 귀한 꽃을 백 가지 종류 시들게 한들, 이 낡은 항아리와 병들이 잡동사니라고 쓸려 나갈 때 내가 느낄 아픔의 한 조각만큼이라도 줄 수 있겠나? 시골이라니!\" 팀이 경멸하듯 힘주어 외쳤다. \"내 침실 창문 아래에 이런 골목을 둘 수 있는 데가 런던 말고 어디 또 있는 줄 아나?\"\n\n이렇게 물으면서 팀은 등을 돌리고는, 장부에 푹 빠진 척하다가, 니콜라스가 딴 데를 보는 줄로 알 때 슬쩍 눈가를 훔칠 틈을 냈다.\n\n그날 아침따라 팀의 장부가 여느 때보다 유난히 복잡했던 탓인지, 아니면 그 추억들 때문에 평소의 차분함이 조금 흐트러진 탓인지, 어쨌든 이런 일이 벌어졌다. 니콜라스가 어떤 심부름을 다녀와서 찰스 치어리블 씨가 방에 혼자 있느냐고 묻자, 팀이 냉큼, 조금도 망설이지 않고 그렇다고 대답한 것이다. 십 분도 채 안 되어 누가 그 방으로 들어갔는데도 말이다. 게다가 팀은 형제 중 누구든 손님과 무슨 일이든 하고 있을 때 아무도 끼어들지 못하게 막는 걸 유난히, 특별히 자랑으로 여기는 사람이었는데도.\n\n\"그렇다면 이 편지를 지금 바로 갖다 드릴게요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다. 그러고는 그 방으로 걸어가 문을 두드렸다.\n\n대답이 없었다.\n\n다시 한번 두드렸지만, 여전히 대답이 없었다.\n\n'안 계신가 봐.' 니콜라스는 생각했다. '책상에 올려놓아야겠다.'\n\n그래서 니콜라스는 문을 열고 안으로 들어갔다. 그런데 곧바로 다시 나가려고 몸을 돌렸다. 어떤 젊은 아가씨가 치어리블 씨 발치에 무릎을 꿇고 있고, 치어리블 씨는 그녀에게 일어나라고 간청하고 있는 걸 보았기 때문이다. 더없이 놀랍고 난처한 광경이었다. 그리고 아가씨의 시중을 드는 하녀로 보이는 세 번째 사람한테, 자기를 도와 함께 설득해서 아가씨를 일으켜 달라고 사정하고 있었다.\n\n니콜라스는 어색하게 사과의 말을 더듬더듬 내뱉고는 황급히 물러나려 했다. 그때 아가씨가 고개를 살짝 돌리는 바람에, 그녀의 얼굴이 그의 눈에 들어왔다. 오래전 처음 찾아갔던 그날 소개소에서 보았던 바로 그 사랑스러운 아가씨였다. 그녀에게서 하녀에게로 눈길을 옮기니, 그때 그녀를 따라왔던 바로 그 굼뜬 하녀였다. 아가씨의 아름다움에 감탄하는 마음과, 이 뜻밖의 알아봄에서 온 당혹과 놀라움 사이에서, 그는 못 박힌 듯 꼼짝 못 하고 서 있었다. 어찌나 놀라고 어쩔 줄 몰랐던지, 그 순간만큼은 말을 할 힘도 움직일 힘도 완전히 잃은 채였다.\n\n\"이런 부인 — 아니 아가씨.\" 찰스 형제가 몹시 안절부절못하며 외쳤다. \"제발 — 한마디도 더 마시오, 부디 빌고 또 비오! 청하고 또 청하니 — 부디 일어나시오. 우리는 — 우리는 — 둘만 있는 게 아니라오.\"\n\n말을 하면서 그는 아가씨를 일으켰는데, 그녀는 비틀거리며 의자로 가더니 그만 정신을 잃고 말았다.\n\n\"기절하셨어요, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 다급히 앞으로 뛰쳐나오며 말했다.\n\n\"가엾어라, 가엾어라!\" 찰스 형제가 외쳤다. \"내 동생 네드는 어디 있나? 네드, 동생, 어서 이리 좀 와 주게.\"\n\n\"찰스 형님, 무슨 일이세요.\" 동생이 방으로 급히 들어오며 대답했다. \"무슨 — 아! 무슨—\"\n\n\"쉿! 쉿! — 무슨 일이 있어도 한마디도 하지 말게, 네드.\" 형이 받아 말했다. \"가정부를 부르는 종을 울려 주게, 동생 — 팀 링킨워터를 부르고! 여기, 팀 링킨워터 씨 — 니클비 군, 미안하네만 방에서 좀 나가 주게, 부탁하고 또 비네.\"\n\n\"이제 좀 나아지신 것 같아요.\" 니콜라스는 환자를 어찌나 애타게 지켜보고 있었던지, 그 부탁을 듣지도 못한 채 말했다.\n\n\"가엾은 새 같으니!\" 찰스 형제가 외치며 그녀의 손을 자기 손에 살며시 쥐고, 그녀의 머리를 자기 팔에 받쳐 주었다. \"네드, 동생, 업무 시간에 이런 일이 벌어진 걸 보고 자네가 놀랄 거 잘 아네만 — \" 여기서 그는 다시 니콜라스가 있다는 걸 떠올리고는, 그의 손을 흔들며 방에서 나가 달라고, 그리고 한순간도 지체 말고 팀 링킨워터를 보내 달라고 간곡히 청했다.\n\n니콜라스는 곧바로 물러났다. 그리고 사무실로 가는 길에 늙은 가정부와 팀 링킨워터를 둘 다 마주쳤는데, 둘은 복도에서 서로 밀치며 엄청난 속도로 사건 현장으로 달려가고 있었다. 팀 링킨워터는 그의 전갈을 들을 새도 없이 방으로 휙 뛰어들었고, 곧이어 니콜라스는 문이 닫히고 안에서 잠기는 소리를 들었다.\n\n이 발견을 곱씹어 볼 시간은 차고 넘쳤다. 팀 링킨워터가 거의 한 시간이나 자리를 비웠으니 말이다. 그 시간 내내 니콜라스는 오로지 그 아가씨 생각, 그녀의 빼어난 아름다움, 대체 무엇 때문에 그녀가 거기 온 것인지, 또 왜 그 일을 그렇게 쉬쉬하는지만 생각했다. 이 모든 걸 생각하면 할수록 더 어리둥절해졌고, 그녀가 누구이며 어떤 사람인지 알고 싶어 더욱 애가 탔다. '만 명 가운데 있어도 그녀를 알아봤을 거야.' 니콜라스는 생각했다. 그러고는 방 안을 왔다 갔다 하며, 그녀의 얼굴과 자태를(이건 유난히 또렷하게 기억하고 있었다) 떠올렸다. 다른 생각거리는 죄다 내던지고 오직 그 생각에만 매달렸다.\n\n마침내 팀 링킨워터가 돌아왔는데 — 얄미울 만큼 태연했다. 손에는 서류를 들고 입에는 펜을 문 채, 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이.\n\n\"그분 완전히 회복되셨어요?\" 니콜라스가 다급하게 물었다.\n\n\"누가?\" 팀 링킨워터가 되물었다.\n\n\"누구라뇨!\" 니콜라스가 되받았다. \"그 아가씨요.\"\n\n\"니클비 군, 자네가 계산해 보게.\" 팀이 입에서 펜을 빼며 말했다. \"사백이십칠 곱하기 삼천이백삼십팔이 얼만지 계산해 보겠나?\"\n\n\"아니,\" 니콜라스가 받아 말했다. \"제 질문에 먼저 답해 주세요. 제가 여쭌 건—\"\n\n\"그 아가씨 얘기지.\" 팀 링킨워터가 안경을 쓰며 말했다. \"그렇고말고. 그래. 아! 그분은 아주 잘 계시네.\"\n\n\"아주 잘 계신다고요?\" 니콜라스가 되받았다.\n\n\"아주 잘.\" 링킨워터 씨가 진지하게 대답했다.\n\n\"오늘 집에 돌아가실 수 있을까요?\" 니콜라스가 물었다.\n\n\"가셨네.\" 팀이 말했다.\n\n\"가셨다고요!\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\"먼 데까지 가시는 건 아니면 좋겠는데요?\" 니콜라스가 상대를 간절히 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그래.\" 꿈쩍도 않는 팀이 대답했다. \"나도 멀지 않으면 좋겠네.\"\n\n니콜라스는 한두 마디 더 슬쩍 던져 보았다. 하지만 팀 링킨워터에게는 그 화제를 피하는 나름의 까닭이 있다는 게, 그리고 그가 자기 젊은 친구의 가슴에 그토록 큰 호기심을 불러일으킨 그 아리따운 낯선 여인에 대해서는 더는 한마디도 알려 주지 않기로 단단히 작정했다는 게 빤히 보였다. 이 퇴짜에도 전혀 굴하지 않고, 니콜라스는 이튿날 다시 공세를 폈다. 링킨워터 씨가 아주 수다스럽고 말이 많은 기분이라는 점에 용기를 얻어서였다. 하지만 그가 그 화제를 다시 꺼내자마자, 팀은 더없이 얄미운 침묵 속으로 도로 빠져들었다. 한 마디 단어로만 답하다가, 나중엔 아예 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그저 몇 번 진지하게 고개를 끄덕이고 어깨를 으쓱하는 데서 짐작할 만한 것밖에는. 그건 이미 터무니없을 만큼 부풀어 오른 니콜라스의 그 소식에 대한 갈망을 더 돋울 뿐이었다.\n\n이런 시도에서 번번이 좌절하자, 그는 아가씨가 다음에 찾아오기를 기다리는 걸로 만족할 수밖에 없었다. 하지만 여기서도 그는 실망했다. 하루하루가 지나도 그녀는 다시 오지 않았다. 그는 모든 쪽지와 편지의 겉봉을 애타게 들여다봤지만, 그중에 그녀의 필체이지 싶은 건 하나도 없었다. 두세 번쯤 그는 멀리까지 다녀와야 하는 일을 맡았는데, 전에는 팀 링킨워터가 처리하던 일이었다. 니콜라스는 무슨 까닭에선지 자기가 일부러 자리를 비우도록 보내진 거고, 그가 없는 동안 아가씨가 거기 왔던 게 아닐까 하는 의심을 떨칠 수 없었다. 하지만 이 의심을 뒷받침할 만한 일은 아무것도 드러나지 않았고, 팀에게서는 그걸 조금이라도 뒷받침할 만한 어떤 자백이나 시인도 끌어낼 수 없었다.\n\n비밀과 실망이 사랑이 자라는 데 꼭 없어서는 안 될 것은 아니지만, 아주 흔히 사랑의 강력한 조력자가 되어 준다. '눈에서 멀어지면 마음에서도 멀어진다'는 말은 우정의 경우에 들어맞는 속담으로는 그럭저럭 괜찮다. 친구 사이라도 마음이 비는 데 꼭 떨어져 있어야 하는 건 아니고, 진실과 정직은 보석과 마찬가지로 멀리 떨어진 데서 가장 흉내 내기 쉬워서, 그 가짜가 종종 진짜로 통하긴 하지만 말이다. 그러나 사랑은 따뜻하고 활발한 상상력의 도움을 단단히 받는다. 그 상상력은 기억이 길고, 아주 보잘것없는 양식으로도 꽤 오랫동안 무성하게 자란다. 그래서 사랑은 흔히 떨어져 있을 때, 그리고 더없이 어려운 처지에서 가장 무성하게 자라난다. 그리하여 니콜라스도, 날이면 날마다 시간마다 그 낯선 아가씨 생각밖에 안 하다가, 마침내 자기가 그녀에게 형편없이 깊이 빠져 있으며, 자기만큼 학대받고 박해받은 연인은 세상에 없다고까지 여기게 되었다.\n\n그래도 그가 가장 정통적인 본보기대로 사랑하며 애태우면서, 케이트에게 그 사연을 털어놓는 것만큼은 그저 사소한 사정 때문에 망설이고 있었다 — 즉 평생 그 연모의 대상에게 말 한마디 건넨 적이 없고, 그녀를 본 것도 단 두 번뿐인데, 그 두 번 다 그녀가 번개처럼 — 아니 니콜라스 자신이 혼잣말로 수없이 되뇐 대로 하자면, 너무 눈부셔서 오래갈 수 없는 젊음과 아름다움의 환영처럼 — 왔다가 사라져 버렸다는 사정 말이다 — 어쨌든 그의 열정과 헌신은 보답을 받지 못한 채였다. 아가씨는 더 이상 나타나지 않았다. 그래서 엄청난 양의 사랑이 허비되었고(요즘 세상에 젊은 신사 대여섯 명쯤은 더없이 점잖게 차려 줄 만큼 충분한 양이었다), 그 덕에 한 톨이라도 더 똑똑해진 사람은 아무도 없었다. 니콜라스 자신조차 그랬다. 오히려 그는 날이 갈수록 더 멍하고 감상적이고 맥 빠진 사람이 되어 갔다.\n\n일이 이런 지경일 때, 독일에 있는 치어리블 형제의 한 거래처가 파산하는 바람에, 팀 링킨워터와 니콜라스는 아주 길고 복잡한, 꽤 긴 기간에 걸친 장부를 죄다 훑어봐야 하는 처지가 됐다. 그걸 더 빨리 끝내려고, 팀 링킨워터는 한 일주일쯤 밤 열 시까지 사무실에 남아 일하자고 제안했다. 친절한 후원자들을 위해 일하는 니콜라스의 열의는 그 무엇으로도 식힐 수 없었으므로 — 좀처럼 부지런한 습성과는 거리가 먼 연애 감정조차도 — 그는 흔쾌히 그러자고 했다. 이렇게 늦게까지 일하게 된 바로 그 첫날 밤, 정각 아홉 시에 누군가 찾아왔다. 아가씨 본인이 아니라 그녀의 하녀였다. 하녀는 한동안 찰스 형제와 단둘이 방에 들어가 있다가 돌아갔고, 이튿날 밤에도 같은 시각에 다시 왔고, 그다음 날도, 또 그다음 날도 왔다.\n\n이렇게 거듭되는 방문에 니콜라스의 호기심은 그야말로 극에 달했다. 견딜 수 없을 만큼 감질나고 들떴지만, 제 할 일을 게을리하지 않고는 그 비밀을 캐낼 도리가 없었다. 그래서 그는 그 비밀을 죄다 뉴먼 노그스에게 털어놓으며 사정했다. 다음 날 밤 망을 봐 달라고, 그 하녀를 따라 집까지 가 달라고, 의심을 사지 않는 선에서 그 여주인의 이름과 처지와 내력에 관해 알아볼 수 있는 데까지 알아봐 달라고, 그리고 그 결과를 되도록 빨리 자기에게 알려 달라고.\n\n이 임무를 더없이 자랑스러워하며, 뉴먼 노그스는 이튿날 저녁 약속된 시각보다 한 시간이나 일찍 광장에 자리를 잡았다. 펌프 뒤에 몸을 숨기고 모자를 눈까지 푹 눌러쓴 채, 거창하게 수상쩍어 보이는 자세로 망을 보기 시작했다. 누가 봐도 의심을 살 만한 폼이었다. 실제로 물을 길으러 온 여러 하녀들이며, 국자로 물을 마시려고 멈춰 선 여러 꼬마들이, 펌프 둘레를 슬그머니 살피는 뉴먼 노그스의 모습에 거의 혼비백산할 지경이었다. 얼굴 말고는 아무것도 안 보이는데, 그 얼굴이 또 생각에 잠긴 도깨비 같은 표정을 짓고 있었으니 말이다.\n\n시간을 어김없이 지켜, 그 심부름꾼이 다시 왔다. 그리고 여느 때보다 좀 더 긴 면담을 마친 뒤 떠났다. 뉴먼은 니콜라스와 두 번의 약속을 잡아 두었다. 하나는 일이 잘 풀린다는 조건으로 다음 날 저녁, 또 하나는 무슨 일이 있어도 지키기로 한 그다음 날 밤이었다. 첫째 날 밤엔 그가 약속 장소(시내와 골든 스퀘어 중간쯤에 있는 어느 선술집)에 나타나지 않았다. 하지만 둘째 날 밤엔 니콜라스보다 먼저 와 있다가, 두 팔을 벌려 그를 맞았다.\n\n\"다 잘됐어.\" 뉴먼이 속삭였다. \"앉아. 앉으라고, 착한 젊은이. 내가 다 얘기해 줄 테니까.\"\n\n니콜라스는 두 번 권할 것도 없이, 무슨 소식이냐고 다급히 물었다.\n\n\"소식이 한가득이야.\" 뉴먼이 의기양양하게 들떠 말했다. \"다 잘됐어. 조바심 내지 마. 어디서부터 얘기해야 할지 모르겠군. 그건 됐고. 기운 내. 다 잘됐다니까.\"\n\n\"그래서요?\" 니콜라스가 다급히 말했다. \"네?\"\n\n\"그래.\" 뉴먼이 대답했다. \"바로 그거지.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔데요?\" 니콜라스가 말했다. \"이름이요 — 이름 말이에요, 형!\"\n\n\"이름은 봅스터야.\" 뉴먼이 대답했다.\n\n\"봅스터요!\" 니콜라스가 발끈하며 되뇌었다.\n\n\"그게 이름이야.\" 뉴먼이 말했다. \"랍스터 가지고 외워 뒀지.\"\n\n\"봅스터라고요!\" 니콜라스가 아까보다 더 힘주어 되뇌었다. \"그건 하녀 이름이겠죠.\"\n\n\"아니, 그렇지 않아.\" 뉴먼이 아주 단호하게 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"세실리아 봅스터 아가씨.\"\n\n\"세실리아라, 응?\" 니콜라스가 받아 말하며, 그 두 이름을 갖가지 어조로 한데 묶어 거듭 중얼거리며 어떤 느낌인지 시험해 봤다. \"음, 세실리아는 예쁜 이름이네요.\"\n\n\"아주 예쁘지. 그리고 그 아가씨 본인도 예쁘고.\" 뉴먼이 말했다.\n\n\"누가요?\" 니콜라스가 말했다.\n\n\"봅스터 아가씨.\"\n\n\"아니, 형이 그 아가씨를 어디서 봤어요?\" 니콜라스가 따져 물었다.\n\n\"그건 신경 쓰지 마, 젊은이.\" 노그스가 그의 어깨를 툭 치며 받아 말했다. \"내가 그 아가씨를 봤어. 너도 보게 될 거야. 내가 다 손을 써 놨거든.\"\n\n\"뉴먼 형,\" 니콜라스가 그의 손을 꽉 잡으며 외쳤다. \"진심이에요?\"\n\n\"진심이지.\" 뉴먼이 대답했다. \"다 진심이야. 한마디 한마디 다. 너 내일 밤에 그 아가씨를 보게 될 거야. 그분이 네가 직접 마음을 고백하는 걸 들어주기로 했어. 내가 설득했지. 더없이 상냥하고, 착하고, 다정하고, 아름다운 분이야.\"\n\n\"그럴 줄 알았어요. 분명 그러실 거예요, 형!\" 니콜라스가 그의 손을 쥐어짜며 말했다.\n\n\"네 말이 맞아.\" 뉴먼이 받아 말했다.\n\n\"그 아가씨 어디 살아요?\" 니콜라스가 외쳤다. \"그분 내력은 뭘 알아냈어요? 아버지가 있어요 — 어머니는요 — 형제는 — 자매는요? 그 아가씬 뭐라고 했어요? 어쩌다 그분을 보게 된 거예요? 무척 놀라지 않았어요? 내가 그분과 얼마나 간절히 말하고 싶어 했는지 얘기해 줬어요? 내가 그분을 어디서 봤는지 말해 줬어요? 어떻게, 언제, 어디서, 얼마나 오래, 얼마나 자주 내가 그 고운 얼굴을 떠올렸는지 말해 줬어요? 더없이 쓰라리던 시절에 더 나은 세상을 잠깐 엿본 것처럼 내게 다가온 그 얼굴을 — 말해 줬어요, 형 — 그랬어요?\"\n\n가엾은 노그스는 이 질문의 홍수가 한꺼번에 들이닥치자 말 그대로 숨을 헐떡였다. 새 질문이 나올 때마다 의자에서 발작하듯 들썩이며, 그러는 내내 더없이 우스꽝스럽게 어리둥절한 표정으로 니콜라스를 빤히 쳐다봤다.\n\n\"아니,\" 뉴먼이 말했다. \"그건 말 안 했어.\"\n\n\"뭘 말 안 했다는 거예요?\" 니콜라스가 물었다.\n\n\"더 나은 세상을 엿봤다는 거.\" 뉴먼이 말했다. \"네가 누군지도 말 안 했고, 네가 그 아가씨를 어디서 봤는지도 말 안 했어. 그냥 네가 그분을 정신 못 차릴 만큼 사랑한다고 했지.\"\n\n\"그건 사실이에요, 형.\" 니콜라스가 특유의 격정으로 대답했다. \"하늘이 알아요, 정말 그래요!\"\n\n\"네가 오랫동안 남몰래 그분을 흠모해 왔다고도 했고.\" 뉴먼이 말했다.\n\n\"네, 네. 거기에 그분이 뭐라고 했어요?\" 니콜라스가 물었다.\n\n\"얼굴을 붉히던데.\" 뉴먼이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇겠죠. 당연히 그러셨을 거예요.\" 니콜라스가 흐뭇하게 말했다. 그러자 뉴먼은 이어서 말했다. 그 아가씨는 외동딸이고, 어머니는 돌아가셨으며, 아버지와 함께 살고 있는데, 자기한테 큰 영향을 미치는 하녀가 사정사정한 덕에 연인과 남몰래 만나는 데 응하게 됐다고. 그는 또 말했다. 아가씨를 이 지경까지 데려오는 데 어찌나 어르고 달래고 입에 침이 마르게 떠들어야 했는지. 그리고 그건 어디까지나 니콜라스가 자기 마음을 고백할 기회를 그저 마련해 주는 것일 뿐, 그분이 결코 그의 구애에 좋은 인상을 받기로 약속한 건 아니라는 점이 분명히 못 박혔다는 것도. 그녀가 치어리블 형제를 찾아간 까닭이라는 그 수수께끼는 전혀 풀리지 않은 채 남았다. 뉴먼이 그 일은 입에 올리지 않았기 때문이다. 하녀와 먼저 나눈 이야기에서도, 그 뒤 여주인과의 면담에서도. 그는 그저 자기가 그 처녀를 따라 집까지 가서 젊은 친구의 사정을 변호하라는 지시를 받았다고만 말했지, 그녀를 어디서부터 어디까지 뒤따랐는지는 말하지 않았다. 하지만 뉴먼은 넌지시 비쳤다. 그 측근에게서 흘러나온 말로 미루어, 그 아가씨가 아주 비참하고 불행한 삶을 살고 있는 듯하다는 것이다. 난폭하고 잔인한 성미를 지닌 하나뿐인 부모의 엄한 통제 아래서 말이다. 그는 이 사정이, 그녀가 형제의 보호와 우정을 구한 까닭과, 약속된 면담에 응하도록 설득당한 까닭 둘 다를 어느 정도 설명해 주지 않을까 생각했다. 그는 이 마지막 점을 전제에서 끌어낸 아주 논리적인 추론이라고 여겼다. 지금 처지가 그토록 부러울 게 없는 아가씨라면, 그걸 바꾸고 싶은 마음이 남보다 더 간절할 거라고 보는 게 자연스럽기 때문이었다.\n\n더 캐물어 보니 — 이 모든 걸 뉴먼 노그스한테서 끄집어내는 데는 아주 길고 힘겨운 과정이 필요했다 — 이런 게 드러났다. 뉴먼은 자기 초라한 행색을 둘러대느라, 이 은밀한 계략과 관련된 어떤 현명하고 꼭 필요한 목적 때문에 변장을 하고 있는 거라고 둘러댔다는 것이다. 그리고 어쩌다가 면담까지 주선하는, 그렇게 지나치게 임무를 넘어선 일을 했느냐고 묻자, 그는 이렇게 대답했다. 아가씨가 면담을 허락할 마음이 있어 보이기에, 도리로 보나 사내다운 의협심으로 보나, 니콜라스가 구애를 밀고 나갈 수 있게 해 줄 그 황금 같은 수단을 붙잡아야 마땅하다고 여겼다는 것이다. 이런 질문과 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 질문을 스무 번씩 묻고 답한 뒤에, 둘은 헤어졌다. 약속을 지키려고 이튿날 밤 열 시 반에 만나기로 하고서. 약속 시각은 열한 시였다.\n\n'일이란 게 참 묘하게 풀리는군!' 니콜라스는 집으로 걸어가며 생각했다. '이런 건 꿈에도 그려 본 적이 없는데. 이런 일이 가능하리라곤 상상도 못 했어. 그렇게 마음이 끌리던 사람의 삶을 조금이라도 알게 되는 것, 그분을 거리에서 보는 것, 그분이 사는 집 앞을 지나가는 것, 어쩌다 산책길에서 그분과 마주치는 것, 언젠가는 내 사랑을 그분께 말할 수 있는 처지가 될 날이 오기를 바라는 것 — 내 생각은 기껏해야 거기까지였는데. 그런데 이제는 — 아니, 내가 내 행운을 두고 불평한다면 그거야말로 바보 같은 짓이지!'\n\n그래도 니콜라스는 마음이 흡족하지 않았다. 그리고 그 불만 속엔 단순히 감정이 뒤바뀐 것 이상의 무언가가 있었다. 그는 그 아가씨가 그렇게 쉽게 넘어온 게 화가 났다. '왜냐하면,' 니콜라스는 따져 봤다. '그분이 그게 나인 줄 알고 그런 게 아니라, 아무나였어도 마찬가지였을 거 아냐.' 분명 유쾌한 노릇은 아니었다. 그러나 다음 순간, 그는 그런 생각을 품은 자신에게 화가 났다. 그런 신전 같은 사람 안엔 선함밖에 깃들 수 없으며, 형제가 그녀를 대하는 태도만 봐도 그분을 얼마나 높이 여기는지 충분히 알 수 있다고 따지면서. '사실 그분은 통째로 수수께끼야.' 니콜라스는 말했다. 이것도 앞서 한 생각보다 더 흡족하진 않았고, 그저 그를 새로운 억측과 추측의 바다로 떠밀어 냈을 뿐이다. 그는 거기서 마음이 몹시 불편한 채로 일렁이고 뒤척였다. 시계가 열 시를 칠 때까지, 그리고 약속 시각이 다가올 때까지.\n\n니콜라스는 아주 공들여 차려입었고, 뉴먼 노그스조차 자기 매무새를 조금 다듬었다. 그의 외투에는 단추 두 개가 잇달아 붙어 있는 진풍경이 펼쳐졌고, 모자라는 자리를 메운 핀들은 꽤 일정한 간격으로 꽂혀 있었다. 모자도 최신 멋에 맞춰 썼는데, 모자 꼭대기에 손수건을 끼우고 그 끄트머리를 꼬아 댕기머리처럼 뒤로 삐죽 내놓은 모양새였다. 하지만 이 마지막 장식을 고안해 낸 재간만큼은 그의 공으로 돌리기 어려웠다. 정작 본인은 그게 거기 있는 줄도 전혀 몰랐으니까. 그는 신경이 곤두서고 들뜬 상태라, 이번 원정의 그 큰 목표 말고는 모든 것에 도통 무감각했던 것이다.\n\n두 사람은 깊은 침묵 속에 거리를 가로질렀다. 그리고 한참을 빠른 걸음으로 걸은 끝에, 음침한 분위기에 인적도 거의 없는 어느 거리에 다다랐다. 에지웨어 로드 근처였다.\n\n\"12번지야.\" 뉴먼이 말했다.\n\n\"오!\" 니콜라스가 주위를 둘러보며 대답했다.\n\n\"괜찮은 거리지?\" 뉴먼이 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 니콜라스가 받아 말했다. \"좀 칙칙하긴 하네요.\"\n\n뉴먼은 이 말엔 대꾸하지 않았다. 그저 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 니콜라스를 어느 지하실 난간에 등을 댄 자세로 세워 놓고는, 주변에 아무도 없다는 게 확실히 확인될 때까지 손도 발도 까딱 말고 거기 기다리라고 일러 줬다. 그러고 나서 노그스는 아주 잰걸음으로 절뚝거리며 떠났다. 시시각각 어깨너머로 돌아보며 니콜라스가 자기 지시를 따르고 있는지 단단히 확인하면서. 그리고 여섯 집쯤 떨어진 어느 집 계단을 올라가더니 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n잠시 뒤, 그가 다시 나타나 절뚝거리며 돌아오다가, 중간쯤에서 멈춰 서서 니콜라스에게 따라오라고 손짓했다.\n\n\"어때요?\" 니콜라스가 발끝으로 살금살금 그에게 다가가며 말했다.\n\n\"다 됐어.\" 뉴먼이 신이 나서 대답했다. \"준비 끝. 집에 아무도 없어. 이보다 좋을 순 없지. 하! 하!\"\n\n이 든든한 장담과 함께, 그는 어느 현관문 앞을 살그머니 지나쳤다. 니콜라스는 그 문에 '봅스터'라고 아주 큼지막한 글자로 새겨진 놋쇠 명패가 붙어 있는 걸 언뜻 봤다. 그리고 그는 열려 있는 지하실 출입문 앞에서 멈춰, 젊은 친구에게 내려가라고 손짓했다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 짓이에요!\" 니콜라스가 뒷걸음치며 외쳤다. \"포크라도 훔치러 온 것처럼 부엌으로 슬그머니 기어 들어가야 한단 말이에요?\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 뉴먼이 대답했다. \"봅스터 영감 — 사나운 터키 사람이야. 그자라면 죄다 죽일 사람이야 — 아가씨 따귀를 갈긴다고 — 진짜로 — 자주.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 니콜라스가 잔뜩 화가 나서 외쳤다. \"어떤 작자가 감히 그런 분의 따귀를 갈긴단 말이에요, 그런—\"\n\n그러나 그때 그는 자기가 연모하는 여인을 칭송하는 노래를 부를 새가 없었다. 뉴먼이 그를 살짝 떠미는 바람에 하마터면 지하실 계단 바닥까지 곤두박질칠 뻔했으니까. 그 눈치를 순순히 받아들이는 게 상책이다 싶어, 니콜라스는 더는 군말 없이 내려갔다. 하지만 그 얼굴은 열정에 찬 연인의 기대나 황홀과는 거리가 먼, 다른 무언가를 드러내고 있었다. 뉴먼이 뒤따랐는데 — 니콜라스가 때맞춰 받쳐 주지 않았더라면 머리부터 곤두박질칠 뻔했다 — 그러고는 니콜라스의 손을 잡고, 칠흑같이 캄캄한 돌 통로를 지나, 더없이 새까맣고 칠흑같이 어두운 뒤편 부엌인지 지하 창고인지로 그를 이끌고 가서 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"자!\" 니콜라스가 불만스러운 목소리로 속삭였다. \"이게 다는 아니겠죠, 설마?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니지.\" 노그스가 받아 말했다. \"곧 그분들이 여기 올 거야. 다 잘됐어.\"\n\n\"그렇다니 다행이네요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다. \"솔직히 그럴 줄은 몰랐거든요.\"\n\n둘은 더 이상 말을 나누지 않았다. 니콜라스는 거기 선 채로 뉴먼 노그스의 거친 숨소리에 귀를 기울였다. 그리고 그를 에워싼 어둠 속에서도 그의 코가 벌겋게 단 숯덩이처럼 이글거리는 것만 같다고 생각했다. 문득 조심스러운 발소리가 그의 귀를 끌었고, 곧이어 여자 목소리가 신사분이 거기 계시냐고 물었다.\n\n\"네.\" 니콜라스가 그 목소리가 들려온 구석을 향해 돌아서며 대답했다. \"누구시죠?\"\n\n\"저예요, 선생님.\" 그 목소리가 대답했다. \"자, 이제 들어오시죠, 아가씨.\"\n\n그곳에 빛 한 줄기가 비쳐 들었고, 곧 하녀가 불을 들고 나타났다. 그 뒤로는 젊은 여주인이 따라왔는데, 부끄러움과 당혹감에 어쩔 줄 모르는 것 같았다.\n\n아가씨를 본 순간, 니콜라스는 흠칫하며 낯빛이 변했다. 가슴이 사납게 뛰었고, 그는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서 있었다. 바로 그 순간, 그녀가 도착하고 촛불이 들어온 것과 거의 동시에, 현관문을 쾅쾅 사납게 두드리는 큰 소리가 들려왔다. 그 소리에 뉴먼 노그스가, 걸터앉아 있던 맥주통에서 아주 날쌔게 펄쩍 뛰어오르더니, 잿빛으로 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 다급히 외쳤다. \"봅스터다, 맙소사!\"\n\n아가씨는 비명을 질렀고, 하녀는 두 손을 비틀어 짰고, 니콜라스는 멍하니 넋이 나간 듯 이쪽저쪽을 번갈아 바라봤다. 뉴먼은 이리저리 허둥대며 주머니란 주머니를 차례로 죄다 손으로 쑤셔 보고는, 갈피를 못 잡는 통에 주머니마다 안감을 끄집어냈다. 그저 한순간이었지만, 그 한순간에 몰아닥친 혼란은 어떤 상상으로도 부풀려 말할 수 없을 정도였다.\n\n\"제발, 이 집에서 나가세요! 우리가 잘못했어요, 이 꼴을 당해도 싸요.\" 아가씨가 외쳤다. \"이 집에서 나가세요, 안 그러면 전 영영 끝장이에요.\"\n\n\"제 말 딱 한마디만 들어주시겠어요?\" 니콜라스가 외쳤다. \"딱 한마디예요. 붙잡지 않을게요. 이 불상사에 대해 해명할 딱 한마디만 들어주시겠어요?\"\n\n하지만 니콜라스가 그 말을 바람에 대고 한 거나 다름없었다. 아가씨는 정신없는 표정으로 계단을 뛰어 올라가 버렸으니까. 그는 그녀를 뒤따르려 했지만, 뉴먼이 그의 외투 깃을 손으로 비틀어 쥐고는 그들이 들어왔던 통로 쪽으로 그를 끌어당겼다.\n\n\"놓으라고요, 뉴먼 형, 제발 좀!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤다. \"그분께 말해야 해요. 그러고 말 거예요! 그러기 전엔 이 집을 떠나지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"평판 — 체면 — 폭력 — 생각 좀 해.\" 뉴먼이 두 팔로 그를 끌어안고 매달리며 서둘러 끌고 갔다. \"저 사람들이 문을 열게 두자고. 문이 닫히는 대로 우리가 들어온 그대로 나가는 거야. 가자. 이쪽. 여기.\"\n\n뉴먼의 만류와, 처녀의 눈물과 애원과, 한순간도 멎지 않는 위층의 그 어마어마한 노크 소리에 짓눌려, 니콜라스는 끌려 나가도록 자신을 내맡겼다. 그리하여 봅스터 씨가 현관문으로 들어오는 바로 그 순간, 그와 노그스는 지하실 출입문으로 빠져나갔다.\n\n둘은 멈추지도 말하지도 않고 여러 거리를 지나 급히 달아났다. 마침내 둘은 걸음을 멈추고, 멍하고 처량한 얼굴로 서로를 마주 봤다.\n\n\"괜찮아.\" 뉴먼이 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"기죽지 마. 다 잘됐어. 다음번엔 운이 더 좋겠지. 어쩔 수 없는 일이었어. 난 내 몫은 다 했어.\"\n\n\"훌륭했어요.\" 니콜라스가 그의 손을 잡으며 대답했다. \"훌륭했어요. 형이 그렇게 진실하고 열성적인 친구다운 모습 그대로요. 다만 — 잘 들어요, 난 실망한 게 아니에요, 형. 그리고 형한테 고마운 마음도 똑같아요 — 다만 그분은 엉뚱한 아가씨였어요.\"\n\n\"응?\" 뉴먼 노그스가 외쳤다. \"하녀한테 속은 거냐?\"\n\n\"뉴먼 형, 뉴먼 형.\" 니콜라스가 그의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"그 하녀도 엉뚱한 하녀였어요.\"\n\n뉴먼의 아래턱이 툭 떨어졌고, 그는 멀쩡한 한쪽 눈을 머릿속에 박힌 듯 고정한 채 꼼짝 않고 니콜라스를 빤히 바라봤다.\n\n\"너무 마음 쓰지 말아요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다. \"별일 아니에요. 보다시피 난 신경 안 써요. 형은 엉뚱한 사람을 뒤따라간 거예요, 그게 다예요.\"\n\n정말 그게 다였다. 뉴먼 노그스가 펌프 둘레를 비스듬한 각도로 너무 오래 살피는 바람에 눈이 침침해진 건지, 아니면 시간이 남는 걸 알고 펌프가 내줄 수 있는 것보다 좀 더 독한 무언가로 몇 방울 기운을 차린 건지 — 어떤 까닭으로 그리됐든 간에, 이게 그의 실수였다. 그리고 니콜라스는 집으로 돌아가 그 일을 곱씹으며, 이제는 여전히 손에 닿지 않는 그 낯선 아가씨의 매력을 곰곰이 떠올렸다.\n\n# CHAPTER 41\n\n니콜라스와 그 중대한 대화를 나눈 그날 이후로, 니클비 부인은 자기 몸치장에 부쩍 정성을 들이기 시작했어. 그동안 늘 입고 다니던 점잖고 아낙네다운 옷차림에 이런저런 장식과 꾸밈을 차츰차츰 덧붙여 갔지. 하나하나 따지면 별것 아닐지 몰라도, 다 합쳐 놓고, 게다가 부인이 털어놓았던 그 사연과 견주어 보면 결코 가볍게 볼 일이 아니었어. 검은 드레스마저도 멋들어지게 차려입은 덕에 어딘가 죽은 듯하면서도 살아 있는 듯한 분위기를 풍겼지. 그리고 아직 남아 있던 매력에 한 가지를 더 보탰는데, 값나가지도 않는 자잘한 젊은 시절 장신구들을 여기저기 야무지게 달아 둔 거야. 바로 그 값어치 없다는 이유 하나로 살림이 거덜 날 때도 무사히 살아남아, 햇빛이 잘 들지 않는 낡은 서랍이며 상자 구석에서 곱게 잠자고 있던 것들이었지. 그 덕에 부인의 상복은 완전히 다른 성격을 띠게 됐어. 죽은 이를 향한 존경과 슬픔의 표시이던 것이, 산 사람을 향한 아주 살벌하고 치명적인 작전의 신호로 바뀐 거야.\n\n니클비 부인이 이런 일을 벌이게 된 건 어쩌면 드높은 의무감과, 더할 나위 없이 갸륵한 충동 때문이었을지도 몰라. 어쩌면 이 무렵에 이르러, 아무 쓸모도 없는 슬픔에 오래 빠져 있는 게 죄스럽다는 생각이 들었을 수도 있지. 아니면 한창 피어나는 딸에게 단정하고 예의 바른 본보기를 보여 줘야 한다는 생각이 들었거나. 의무니 책임이니 하는 건 다 제쳐 두더라도, 이 변화는 더없이 순수하고 사심 없는 자비심에서 비롯되었을 수도 있어. 니콜라스가 옆집 신사를 험담했잖아. 늙어 빠진 데다 머리까지 돈 멍청이라고 거칠게 낙인을 찍었지. 그런데 그 사람의 정신을 두고 그렇게 깎아내린 데에는 니클비 부인한테도 어느 정도 책임이 있었어. 부인은 어쩌면 이런 생각이 들었을 거야. 그 모욕당한 신사가 늙은이도 멍청이도 아니라는 걸 자기 힘껏 보여 주는 게 착한 기독교인의 도리라고 말이야. 그렇게 갸륵하고 칭찬받을 만한 목적을 위해서, 부인 자신이 직접 증명해 보이는 것보다 더 나은 방법이 어디 있겠어? 그 사람의 정념이야말로 세상에서 가장 합리적이고 이치에 맞는 것이며, 분별 있고 사려 깊은 사람이라면 누구나 능히 내다봤을 당연한 결과라는 걸 말이야. 부인이 무르익은 매력을 거리낌 없이, 그것도 열정 넘치고 마음 약한 남자의 바로 코앞에서 무심코 펼쳐 보였으니, 그렇게 될 수밖에 없었다는 걸 증명해 보이는 것보다 더 나은 방법이 어디 있겠어?\n\n\"아!\" 니클비 부인이 자못 심각하게 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했어. \"니콜라스가 알기만 했어도. 가엾은 제 아버지가 우리가 약혼하기 전에 얼마나 마음고생을 했는지, 내가 그이를 미워하던 시절에 말이야. 그걸 알았다면 그 애도 좀 더 헤아릴 줄 알 텐데. 그이가 내 양산을 들어 주겠다고 했을 때, 내가 그이를 비웃듯 쳐다보던 그 아침을 내가 어떻게 잊겠어? 아니면 내가 그이한테 눈살을 찌푸리던 그날 밤은 또 어떻고? 그이가 다른 나라로 떠나 버리지 않은 게 천만다행이지. 정말 거의 그럴 뻔했거든.\"\n\n세상을 떠난 그 양반이 차라리 총각 시절에 멀리 떠나 버렸으면 더 나았을지도 모른다는 문제는, 미망인이 굳이 따져 보려고 멈추지 않았어. 마침 그 생각이 이쯤 이르렀을 때 케이트가 반짇고리를 들고 방으로 들어왔거든. 게다가 이보다 훨씬 사소한 방해가 있었어도, 아니 아무 방해가 없었더라도, 니클비 부인의 생각은 어느 때든 금세 다른 곳으로 흘러가 버렸을 테지.\n\n\"케이트, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"어째서 그런지는 모르겠다만, 이렇게 따뜻하고 화창한 여름날, 사방에서 새들이 지저귀는 날이면 늘 통돼지구이가 떠오르는구나. 세이지와 양파 소스에 그레이비까지 곁들인 그 요리 말이야.\"\n\n\"그건 좀 이상한 연상 아니에요, 엄마?\"\n\n\"글쎄다, 얘야, 나도 모르겠구나.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어. \"통돼지구이라. 어디 보자. 네 세례식이 끝나고 다섯 주 뒤 되던 날, 우리가 통구이를 먹었는데. 아니, 그건 돼지는 아니었을 거야. 왜냐면 썰어야 할 게 두 마리였던 게 기억나거든. 가엾은 네 아버지랑 나랑 돼지를 두 마리나 놓고 앉을 생각은 도무지 못 했을 테니까. 그건 자고새였을 거야. 통돼지구이라! 가만 생각해 보니 우리가 통돼지구이를 먹은 적이 있기나 한지 모르겠구나. 네 아버지는 가게에 그게 걸려 있는 꼴을 도무지 못 봤거든. 그걸 보면 늘 아주 조그만 갓난아기가 떠오른다고 했어. 다만 돼지가 살결이 훨씬 더 곱다는 게 다르다고 했지. 그이는 갓난아기도 끔찍이 싫어했어. 식구가 늘어나는 걸 감당할 형편이 못 됐으니까. 그래서 그 얘기 자체를 본능적으로 꺼렸지. 참 이상하기도 하지, 대체 무엇 때문에 그게 머릿속에 떠올랐을까! 언젠가 베반 부인 댁에서 저녁을 먹은 적이 있는데, 마차 만드는 집 모퉁이를 돌면 나오는 그 널찍한 거리에 있었지. 거기서 술 취한 남자가 빈 집 지하실 뚜껑을 밟고 빠졌는데, 분기 결산일을 거의 일주일 앞두고 그랬는데도 새 세입자가 들어올 때까지 아무도 못 찾았다지 뭐냐. 아무튼 거기서 통돼지구이를 먹었어. 그게 떠올라서 그런가 보다, 그런 생각이 드는구나. 특히 그 방에 새 한 마리가 있어서 저녁 내내 줄곧 노래를 불러 댔거든. 아니, 새라기보다는 앵무새였지. 노래를 부른 것도 아니고 말을 했어. 그것도 무섭게 욕을 해 댔지. 아무튼 그것 때문일 거야. 정말 틀림없이 그래. 너도 그렇게 생각하지 않니, 얘야?\"\n\n\"의심할 여지가 없네요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 명랑하게 미소 지으며 대답했어.\n\n\"아니, 정말로 그렇게 생각하니, 케이트?\" 니클비 부인이 마치 더없이 급박하고 짜릿한 문제라도 되는 양 심각하게 물었어. \"안 그러면 안 그렇다고 당장 말하렴, 알겠지. 정확한 게 좋으니까. 특히 이런 종류의 문제는 아주 흥미로워서 생각난 김에 매듭지어 둘 만하거든.\"\n\n케이트는 웃으며, 자기도 완전히 확신한다고 대답했어. 그래도 엄마는 이 문제를 다시 꺼내는 게 꼭 필요한 일이 아닌가 하고 여전히 마음을 정하지 못한 눈치였지. 그래서 케이트는 일감을 챙겨 정자로 가서 화창한 오후의 아름다움을 즐기자고 했어. 니클비 부인은 선뜻 그러자고 했고, 두 사람은 더 이상 따지지 않고 정자로 향했지.\n\n\"그래, 내 이건 꼭 말해야겠다.\" 니클비 부인이 자리에 앉으며 말했어. \"스마이크 같은 착한 아이는 정말 없을 거야. 정말이지, 그 애가 이 작은 정자를 손질하고, 둘레에 더없이 어여쁜 꽃들을 길러 놓느라 들인 정성은 내가 도저히 뭐라 할 수가— 그런데 자갈을 죄다 네 쪽에만 깔아 놓고, 케이트, 얘야, 내 쪽에는 흙만 남겨 둔 건 좀 안 그랬으면 좋겠구나.\"\n\n\"엄마.\" 케이트가 얼른 말했어. \"이 자리로 앉으세요. 어서요. 저 좀 봐서요, 엄마.\"\n\n\"아니, 됐다, 얘야. 나는 내 자리를 지킬란다.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"어머나! 세상에!\"\n\n케이트가 묻는 눈빛으로 고개를 들었어.\n\n\"글쎄 그 애가,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"어디서 구해 왔는지 그 꽃 뿌리를 두어 포기 가져다 놨지 뭐냐. 내가 그저께 밤에 그렇게 좋아한다고 했던 그 꽃 말이야. 그러고는 너한테도 좋아하지 않느냐고 물었던— 아니, 네가 그저께 밤에 그렇게 좋아한다고 했던 거구나. 그러고는 나한테 안 좋아하느냐고 물었던— 뭐 마찬가지지. 자, 정말이지, 나는 그게 참 친절하고 세심한 일이라고 생각한다! 그런데,\" 니클비 부인이 주위를 꼼꼼히 둘러보며 덧붙였어. \"내 쪽에는 하나도 안 보이는구나. 하긴 그게 자갈 근처에서 제일 잘 자라나 보지. 틀림없이 그럴 거야, 케이트. 그래서 다 네 쪽에 있는 거고, 그 애가 거기다 자갈을 깐 것도 거기가 볕이 잘 드는 쪽이라서 그런 거지. 정말이지, 그거 참 영리하구나! 나라면 그 절반도 생각 못 했을 거야!\"\n\n\"엄마.\" 케이트가 일감 위로 고개를 숙여 얼굴이 거의 보이지 않게 한 채 말했어. \"엄마가 결혼하기 전에는—\"\n\n\"아이고, 케이트야.\" 니클비 부인이 가로막았어. \"도대체 무슨 영문으로 내가 결혼하기 전 얘기로 훌쩍 날아가는 거냐. 내가 지금 그 애가 나한테 얼마나 사려 깊고 세심한지를 얘기하고 있는데 말이야. 너는 이 정원에는 손톱만큼도 관심이 없는 모양이구나.\"\n\n\"어머! 엄마.\" 케이트가 다시 얼굴을 들며 말했어. \"관심 있는 거 아시잖아요.\"\n\n\"그럼, 얘야, 어째서 이 정원이 얼마나 단정하고 예쁘게 가꿔져 있는지 칭찬을 안 하는 거니?\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"너 참 이상하기도 하다, 케이트!\"\n\n\"칭찬하잖아요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 부드럽게 대답했어. \"가엾은 아이!\"\n\n\"내 귀에는 좀처럼 안 들리던데, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 받아쳤어. \"내가 할 말은 그게 다야.\" 이쯤 되자 이 착한 부인은 한 가지 화제에 한참 매달려 있었던 터라, 딸이 놓은 작은 덫에—그게 덫이라면 말이지만—단번에 걸려들고 말았어. 그러고는 무슨 말을 하려던 거냐고 물었지.\n\n\"뭐에 대해서요, 엄마?\" 케이트가 물었어. 자기가 화제를 돌렸던 걸 까맣게 잊은 모양이었지.\n\n\"아이고, 케이트야.\" 엄마가 대꾸했어. \"왜, 너 졸고 있거나 멍하구나! 내가 결혼하기 전 얘기 말이야.\"\n\n\"아 참!\" 케이트가 말했어. \"기억나요. 여쭤보려던 거예요, 엄마. 엄마가 결혼하기 전에 구혼자가 많았어요?\"\n\n\"구혼자라니, 얘야!\" 니클비 부인이 더없이 흐뭇한 미소를 지으며 외쳤어. \"처음부터 끝까지 따져 보면, 케이트, 적어도 열두 명은 됐을 거야.\"\n\n\"엄마!\" 케이트가 나무라는 투로 받았어.\n\n\"정말이란다, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"가엾은 네 아버지는 빼고 말이야. 그리고 그 시절 같은 무용 학원에 다니던 어떤 젊은 신사도 빼고. 그 사람은 금시계며 팔찌를 금테 두른 종이에 싸서 우리 집으로 보내 오곤 했지. (늘 도로 돌려보냈지만 말이야.) 그러다 나중에 안됐게도 사관 후보생 배를 타고 보타니 만으로 가게 됐는데— 아니, 죄수 호송선이었지. 그러다 덤불숲으로 달아나서 양들을 죽였어. (그 양들이 어떻게 거기 있었는지는 모르겠다만.) 그래서 교수형을 당할 뻔했는데, 그만 어쩌다 제 손으로 목이 막혀 버려서, 정부가 사면을 해 줬단다. 그리고 젊은 루킨이 있었지.\" 니클비 부인이 왼손 엄지부터 시작해 손가락을 하나씩 꼽으며 말했어. \"모글리— 팁슬락— 캐버리— 스미프서—\"\n\n이제 새끼손가락까지 다 꼽은 니클비 부인이 헤아림을 다른 손으로 넘기려던 참이었어. 그때 정원 담장 밑바닥에서 올라오는 듯한 커다란 \"에헴!\" 소리가 부인과 딸을 둘 다 화들짝 놀라게 했지.\n\n\"엄마! 저게 뭐죠?\" 케이트가 낮은 목소리로 말했어.\n\n\"정말이지, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 적잖이 놀라며 대꾸했어. \"옆집에 사는 그 신사가 아니라면, 대체 무엇 때문에 저런—\"\n\n\"에—헴!\" 똑같은 목소리가 외쳤어. 이번엔 흔히 목을 가다듬는 그런 소리가 아니라, 일종의 우렁찬 고함 같은 거였어. 동네의 메아리를 죄다 깨워 놓을 만큼 컸고, 어찌나 길게 끌었던지 보이지 않는 그 고함꾼은 얼굴이 새파래졌을 게 분명했지.\n\n\"이제 알겠다, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 케이트의 손에 손을 얹으며 말했어. \"놀라지 마라, 얘야. 너한테 하는 게 아니고, 누굴 겁주려는 것도 아니란다. 케이트, 누구든 제 몫은 인정해 줘야지. 나는 그렇게 말할 수밖에 없구나.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 니클비 부인은 고개를 끄덕이고는 딸의 손등을 여러 번 토닥였어. 그러고는 마음만 먹으면 굉장히 중요한 얘기를 들려줄 수도 있지만, 다행히도 자제할 줄 알아서 그러지 않겠다는 듯한 표정을 지었지.\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요, 엄마?\" 케이트가 눈에 띄게 놀라며 물었어.\n\n\"허둥대지 마라, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 정원 담장 쪽을 바라보며 대답했어. \"보다시피 나는 안 그러잖니. 누군가 허둥대도 봐줄 만한 사람이 있다면, 이 모든 사정을 고려할 때 그건 분명 나일 거야. 그래도 나는 안 그런단다, 케이트. 조금도 말이야.\"\n\n\"우리 주의를 끌려는 것 같아요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 말했어.\n\n\"우리 주의를 끌려는 거지, 얘야. 적어도,\" 니클비 부인이 몸을 곧추세우며, 아까보다 더 부드럽게 딸의 손을 토닥이며 받았어. \"우리 둘 중 하나의 주의를 끌려는 거야. 에헴! 너는 조금도 불편해할 것 없단다, 얘야.\"\n\n케이트가 몹시 어리둥절한 표정을 지으며 더 설명해 달라고 물으려던 참이었어. 그때 앞서 들렸던 소리와 같은 쪽에서 고함치고 발버둥치는 소리가 들려왔지. 나이 지긋한 신사가 헐거운 자갈 위에서 우렁차게 소리치며 다리를 마구 차 대는 듯한 소리였어. 그리고 그 소리가 잦아들기도 전에, 커다란 오이 하나가 폭죽처럼 빠르게 공중으로 솟구쳤어. 그러더니 빙글빙글 굴러떨어져 니클비 부인의 발치에 툭 떨어졌지.\n\n이 진기한 광경에 이어 똑같은 일이 또 한 번 일어났어. 그러더니 유난히 큼지막한 호박 하나가 공중으로 빙글빙글 솟았다가 비틀비틀 떨어졌지. 그다음엔 오이 여러 개가 한꺼번에 솟구쳤어. 그리고 마침내 양파와 무, 그 밖의 자잘한 채소들이 소나기처럼 쏟아져 하늘을 어둑하게 했지. 그것들은 사방으로 데굴데굴 구르고 흩어지고 부딪치며 떨어졌어.\n\n케이트가 좀 놀라서 자리에서 일어나 엄마 손을 잡고 집 안으로 달려가려는데, 도와주기는커녕 오히려 발목을 잡히는 듯한 느낌이 들었어. 그래서 니클비 부인의 시선을 따라가 보니, 낡은 검정 벨벳 모자 하나가 나타나 케이트는 그만 질겁하고 말았지. 그 모자는 마치 쓴 사람이 사다리나 계단을 한 칸씩 올라오는 것처럼 천천히, 자기네 정원과 옆집 오두막 정원을 가르는 담장 위로 솟아올랐어. (옆집 역시 자기네 집처럼 따로 떨어진 단독 건물이었지.) 그리고 그 뒤로 아주 커다란 머리통과 늙은 얼굴이 차츰 따라 올라왔어. 그 얼굴엔 더없이 기묘한 잿빛 두 눈이 박혀 있었지. 아주 사납고, 한껏 부릅뜬 채, 눈구멍 속에서 흐릿하고 게슴츠레하니 곁눈질하듯 굴러다니는데, 보기에 더없이 흉측했어.\n\n\"엄마!\" 케이트가 그 순간 정말로 겁에 질려 외쳤어. \"왜 멈춰 서세요, 왜 한순간도 머뭇거리세요? 엄마, 제발 들어가요!\"\n\n\"케이트, 얘야.\" 엄마가 여전히 버티며 대꾸했어. \"어쩜 그렇게 어리석을 수가 있니? 내가 다 창피하구나. 그렇게 겁쟁이여서야 어떻게 세상을 헤쳐 나갈 작정이니? 무슨 일이세요, 선생님?\" 니클비 부인이 야릇하게 새침을 떨며 못마땅한 투로 침입자에게 말을 건넸어. \"어떻게 감히 이 정원을 들여다보시는 거예요?\"\n\n\"내 영혼의 여왕이여.\" 낯선 사람이 두 손을 맞잡으며 대답했어. \"이 술잔을 한 모금 드시오!\"\n\n\"무슨 말도 안 되는 소리예요, 선생님.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"케이트, 얘야, 제발 좀 가만있으렴.\"\n\n\"이 술잔을 한 모금 안 드시겠소?\" 낯선 사람이 머리를 한쪽으로 애원하듯 기울이고 오른손을 가슴에 얹은 채 졸랐어. \"오, 부디 이 술잔을 한 모금 드시오!\"\n\n\"나는 그런 짓은 절대 하지 않을 거예요, 선생님.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"부디 물러가세요.\"\n\n\"어째서일까요.\" 노신사가 한 칸 더 올라와 마치 창밖을 내다보듯 더없이 흐뭇한 표정으로 담장에 팔꿈치를 괴며 말했어. \"어째서 아름다움이란 늘 이리도 매몰찰까요. 나처럼 흠모하는 마음이 이토록 떳떳하고 정중한데도 말이지요.\" 여기서 그는 미소를 짓고 손에 입을 맞추더니 여러 번 깊이 절을 했어. \"그건 꿀벌들 때문일까요? 꿀 철이 끝나 유황으로 죽임을 당했다고들 여겨질 때, 실은 바르바리로 날아가 사로잡힌 무어인들을 그 나른한 노래로 잠재운다는 그 꿀벌들 말이오. 아니면,\" 그가 목소리를 거의 속삭임으로 낮추며 덧붙였어. \"채링 크로스의 동상이 요전에 한밤중 증권 거래소에서 목격된 탓일까요? 승마복을 차려입고 올드게이트의 펌프와 팔짱을 끼고 거니는 모습으로 말이오.\"\n\n\"엄마.\" 케이트가 중얼거렸어. \"저 사람 말 들리세요?\"\n\n\"쉿, 얘야!\" 니클비 부인이 같은 투로 대답했어. \"저분은 아주 정중하시잖니. 게다가 방금 그건 시인의 글을 인용한 것 같구나. 제발 나를 그렇게 괴롭히지 마라— 내 팔을 시퍼렇게 꼬집어 놓겠다. 물러가세요, 선생님!\"\n\n\"아주 물러가라고요?\" 신사가 게슴츠레한 눈빛으로 말했어. \"오! 아주 물러가라고요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어. \"그럼요. 여기는 당신이 있을 곳이 아니에요. 여긴 사유지예요, 선생님. 그쯤은 아셔야죠.\"\n\n\"내가 알기로는,\" 노신사가 더없이 못마땅하게도 허물없는 태도로 콧등에 손가락을 얹으며 말했어. \"여기가 신성하고 마법에 걸린 곳이라는 것이오. 더없이 거룩한 매력이\"— 여기서 그는 또 손에 입을 맞추고 절을 했어. \"감미로움을 이웃집 정원까지 실어 보내, 과일과 채소를 철도 되기 전에 억지로 자라나게 하는 곳 말이오. 그 사실만큼은 내가 익히 알고 있소. 하지만 더없이 아리따운 분이여, 한 가지 물어봐도 되겠소? 마침 금성이 자리를 비운 틈에 말이오. 그분이 호스 가즈로 볼일을 보러 가셨거든. 안 그랬으면 당신의 더 빼어난 매력을 시샘해서 우리 사이를 가로막았을 텐데.\"\n\n\"케이트.\" 니클비 부인이 딸을 돌아보며 말했어. \"이거 참 난처하구나, 정말로. 이 신사분께 뭐라고 해야 할지 모르겠어. 너도 알다시피 예의는 지켜야 하잖니.\"\n\n\"엄마.\" 케이트가 받았어. \"저 사람한테 한마디도 하지 마시고, 우리 어서 달아나요. 그래서 니콜라스가 돌아올 때까지 문을 꼭 닫고 있어요.\"\n\n니클비 부인은 이 굴욕적인 제안에 거만하다 못해 경멸하는 듯한 표정을 지었어. 그러고는 이 속닥거림 내내 두 사람을 넋을 잃고 열심히 지켜보던 노신사를 돌아보며 말했지.\n\n\"선생님, 만약 당신이 내가 짐작하기에 마땅한 신사답게— 말씨로 보나, 그리고 그— 그— 풍채로 보나 그래 보이는데요. (한창때의 네 할아버지를 빼다박았구나, 케이트, 얘야.) 그렇게 처신하시고, 질문도 또렷한 말로 해 주신다면, 제가 대답해 드리지요.\"\n\n만약 니클비 부인의 그 훌륭한 아버지가 한창때 지금 담장 너머로 넘겨다보는 이 이웃과 닮았었다면, 적어도 한창 시절에 아주 괴상하게 생긴 노신사였던 게 틀림없었어. 어쩌면 케이트도 그렇게 생각했는지, 그 살아 있는 초상화를 어느 정도 유심히 살펴보았지. 신사가 검정 벨벳 모자를 벗고 완전히 벗어진 머리를 드러내며 줄줄이 절을 해 대는 동안 말이야. 절을 할 때마다 새로 손에 입을 맞추는 것도 잊지 않았어. 보아하니 이 고된 동작에 완전히 진이 빠진 듯하자, 그는 다시 머리에 모자를 쓰고는 귓등까지 아주 조심스럽게 끌어내렸어. 그러고는 아까의 자세로 돌아가 말했지.\n\n\"질문은—\"\n\n여기서 그는 말을 멈추고 사방을 둘러보았어. 근처에 엿듣는 사람이 없다는 걸 한 점 의심 없이 확인하려는 거였지. 아무도 없다고 안심하자, 그는 콧등을 여러 번 톡톡 두드렸어. 그러면서 교활한 눈빛을 곁들였는데, 마치 신중을 기한 자신을 대견해하는 듯했지. 그러더니 목을 쭉 빼고 큰 소리로 속삭였어.\n\n\"당신은 공주요?\"\n\n\"나를 놀리시는군요, 선생님.\" 니클비 부인이 집 쪽으로 물러나는 시늉을 하며 대답했어.\n\n\"아니, 정말로, 당신 공주요?\" 노신사가 말했어.\n\n\"아닌 거 아시잖아요, 선생님.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어.\n\n\"그럼 캔터베리 대주교와 친척이오?\" 노신사가 몹시 애가 타서 물었어. \"아니면 로마 교황과? 아니면 하원 의장과? 내가 틀렸다면 용서하시오만, 듣자 하니 당신이 포장 위원회의 조카요, 시장 어른과 시 참사회의 며느리라던데. 그렇다면 그 세 분 모두와 친척인 셈이 되니까 말이오.\"\n\n\"누가 그런 소문을 퍼뜨렸든, 선생님.\" 니클비 부인이 다소 발끈해서 받았어. \"내 이름을 가지고 아주 함부로 군 거예요. 우리 아들 니콜라스가 그걸 안다면 한순간도 가만두지 않을 거라고 장담해요. 별 소리를 다!\" 니클비 부인이 몸을 곧추세우며 말했어. \"포장 위원회의 조카라니!\"\n\n\"제발, 엄마, 가요!\" 케이트가 속삭였어.\n\n\"'제발 엄마!' 말도 안 되는 소리, 케이트.\" 니클비 부인이 성을 내며 말했어. \"꼭 그런 식이지. 누가 나더러 피리 소리 내는 멋쟁이새의 조카라고 했으면 네가 신경이나 썼겠니? 하지만 나를 알아주는 사람이 없구나.\" 니클비 부인이 훌쩍였어. \"기대도 안 한다만, 그게 한 가지 위안이지.\"\n\n\"눈물이군!\" 노신사가 어찌나 힘차게 펄쩍 뛰었던지 두세 칸을 미끄러져 내려가 턱을 담장에 긁히며 외쳤어. \"저 수정 방울을 받아라— 받으라니까— 병에 담아— 마개를 꽉 막고— 위에 봉랍을 발라— 큐피드 무늬로 봉인을 찍고— '최상품'이라고 딱지를 붙여— 그러고는 열네 번 칸에 넣어 둬. 위에 쇠막대를 얹어서 천둥이 못 들어오게 하고 말이야!\"\n\n이런 명령을 내리는 모습이, 마치 그 일을 부지런히 실행에 옮길 하인이 열두 명쯤 있기라도 한 것 같았어. 그러더니 그는 벨벳 모자를 뒤집어 까서 더없이 위엄 있게 눌러썼는데, 오른쪽 눈과 코의 사 분의 삼이 가려질 만큼 푹 눌러썼지. 그러고는 두 팔을 양옆구리에 척 짚고서 가까이 있는 참새 한 마리를 어찌나 사납게 노려보던지, 새가 날아가 버리고 말았어. 그제야 그는 더없이 흡족한 표정으로 모자를 주머니에 집어넣고는, 정중한 태도로 니클비 부인에게 말을 건넸어.\n\n\"아름다운 부인이여.\" 그의 말은 이랬어. \"내가 당신의 가문이나 친척에 대해 무슨 착오를 일으켰다면, 부디 너그러이 용서해 주시오. 내가 당신을 외국의 권력자들이나 본국의 위원회와 친척이라고 짐작했다면, 그건 당신에게 어떤 몸가짐과 자태, 그리고 기품이 있기 때문이오. 외람되지만, 당신 말고는 그 누구도— 굳이 따지자면 비극의 여신이 동인도 회사 앞에서 손풍금을 즉흥으로 연주할 때라면 모를까— 그 누구도 견줄 수 없는 기품 말이오. 보다시피 나는 풋내기가 아니오, 부인. 비록 당신 같은 분은 결코 늙는 법이 없겠지만, 외람되이 짐작건대 우리는 천생연분인 것 같소.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 케이트, 얘야!\" 니클비 부인이 힘없이 말하며 딴 데로 눈길을 돌렸어.\n\n\"나는 영지를 가지고 있소, 부인.\" 노신사가 그런 건 아주 대수롭지 않다는 듯 오른손을 한가로이 휘저으며 아주 빠르게 말했어. \"보석에, 등대에, 양어장에, 북해에 내 소유의 포경장이 있고, 태평양에는 이문이 두둑한 굴 양식장이 여럿 있소. 수고스럽지만 왕립 거래소로 내려가서 가장 뚱뚱한 안내인의 머리에서 삼각모를 벗겨 보시오. 그럼 모자 꼭대기 안감 속에 파란 종이에 싸인 내 명함이 들어 있을 거요. 내 지팡이도 하원 전속 목사에게 청하면 볼 수 있소. 그 목사는 그걸 보여 주고 돈을 받는 게 엄격히 금지돼 있지요. 내 둘레에는 적들이 있소, 부인.\" 그가 자기 집 쪽을 바라보며 아주 낮은 소리로 말했어. \"틈만 나면 나를 공격하고, 내 재산을 가로채려는 자들이오. 만약 당신이 손과 마음을 내게 주신다면, 당신은 대법관에게 청을 넣을 수도 있고, 필요하면 군대를 부를 수도 있소— 내 이쑤시개를 총사령관에게 보내면 충분할 거요— 그래서 예식을 치르기 전에 그자들을 집에서 싹 몰아낼 수 있지요. 그러고 나면, 사랑과 더없는 기쁨과 황홀함이오. 황홀함과 사랑과 더없는 기쁨이지. 내 사람이 되어 주오, 내 사람이 되어 주오!\"\n\n이 마지막 말을 더없는 황홀함과 열정으로 거듭 외치고는, 노신사는 검정 벨벳 모자를 다시 쓰고 황급히 하늘을 올려다보았어. 그러더니 자기가 기다리는 기구가 예정보다 좀 늦는다는, 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 말을 중얼거렸지.\n\n\"내 사람이 되어 주오, 내 사람이 되어 주오!\" 노신사가 거듭 외쳤어.\n\n\"케이트, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"말을 할 기운도 거의 없다만, 모두의 행복을 위해서 이 문제는 영영 매듭을 지어 둘 필요가 있구나.\"\n\n\"엄마가 한마디라도 하셔야 할 까닭이 정말 없잖아요?\" 케이트가 타일렀어.\n\n\"미안하다만, 얘야, 그건 내가 알아서 판단하게 해 다오.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"내 사람이 되어 주오, 내 사람이 되어 주오!\" 노신사가 외쳤어.\n\n\"낯선 분께,\" 니클비 부인이 다소곳이 눈을 땅에 떨군 채 말했어. \"이런 청혼에 내가 우쭐하고 고마운지 아닌지를 말해 드리리라고 기대하시기는 어렵겠지요, 선생님. 분명 아주 별난 상황에서 하신 청이긴 해요. 그래도 그것대로, 또 어느 정도까지는 물론\" (니클비 부인이 늘 붙이는 단서였지.) \"사람의 마음에 흐뭇하고 기껍지 않을 수 없는 일이긴 하지요.\"\n\n\"내 사람이 되어 주오, 내 사람이 되어 주오.\" 노신사가 외쳤어. \"고그와 마고그, 고그와 마고그. 내 사람이 되어 주오, 내 사람이 되어 주오!\"\n\n\"이 말만 하면 될 것 같군요, 선생님.\" 니클비 부인이 더없이 진지하게 말을 이었어. \"그리고 대답을 듣고 물러가는 게 마땅하다는 걸 당신도 분명 아실 거예요. 나는 과부로 남아서 아이들에게 헌신하기로 마음을 굳혔답니다. 당신은 내가 두 아이의 어미라고는 짐작 못 하실 거예요— 실제로 많은 이들이 그걸 의심했고, 세상 무엇으로도 그게 가능하다고는 도저히 못 믿겠다고들 했지요— 하지만 사실이고, 둘 다 다 컸어요. 우리는 당신을 이웃으로 두게 되면 무척 기쁠 거예요— 정말 기쁘지요. 더없이 반가운 일이고요— 하지만 그 외의 다른 사이로는 도저히 안 될 일이에요, 정말로요. 내가 다시 결혼할 만큼 젊으냐 하면, 어쩌면 그럴 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 단 한순간도 그런 건 생각할 수 없어요. 무슨 일이 있어도 말이에요. 나는 결코 안 그러겠다고 했고, 결코 안 그럴 거예요. 청혼을 거절해야 하는 건 참으로 가슴 아픈 일이라서, 아예 아무도 청혼하지 않았으면 좋겠어요. 그래도 이건 내가 오래전에 마음먹은 대답이고, 앞으로도 늘 할 대답이에요.\"\n\n이 말은 한편으로는 노신사에게, 한편으로는 케이트에게 한 것이었고, 또 한편으로는 혼잣말로 흘러나온 거였어. 말이 끝나갈 무렵, 구혼자는 더없이 불경스러울 만큼 딴청을 피웠어. 그리고 니클비 부인이 말을 채 끝내기도 전에, 그 부인과 딸을 둘 다 소스라치게 하더니, 그는 별안간 외투를 휙 벗어 던지고 담장 꼭대기로 펄쩍 뛰어올랐어. 그러고는 반바지와 잿빛 모직 양말을 한껏 자랑하는 자세를 취하더니, 마침내 한 발로 서서 그 좋아하는 고함을 더욱 우렁차게 거듭 질렀지.\n\n그가 여전히 그 마지막 음을 길게 끌며 거기에 한껏 멋을 부려 장식하고 있는데, 꾀죄죄한 손 하나가 담장 꼭대기를 따라 살그머니 재빠르게 미끄러져 가는 게 보였어. 마치 파리를 쫓는 것 같았지. 그러더니 더없이 날렵한 솜씨로 노신사의 발목 한쪽을 덥석 움켜잡았어. 그러고 나자 짝꿍 손이 나타나 다른 발목을 움켜잡았지.\n\n이렇게 발목을 붙잡힌 노신사는 두 다리를 어색하게 한두 번 들썩였어. 마치 아주 서투르고 엉성한 기계 부품이라도 되는 것처럼 말이야. 그러더니 담장 자기 쪽을 내려다보고는 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸어.\n\n\"너로구나, 그렇지?\" 노신사가 말했어.\n\n\"네, 접니다.\" 걸걸한 목소리가 대답했어.\n\n\"타타르 황제는 어떠시냐?\" 노신사가 말했어.\n\n\"오! 늘 그러시지요.\" 대답이 돌아왔어. \"더 좋지도 더 나쁘지도 않습니다.\"\n\n\"중국의 어린 왕자는,\" 노신사가 자못 관심을 보이며 말했어. \"그 장인인 위대한 감자 장수와 화해했느냐?\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 걸걸한 목소리가 대답했어. \"게다가 결코 화해 안 할 거라고 하던데요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 노신사가 말했어. \"아무래도 내려가는 게 낫겠구나.\"\n\n\"글쎄요.\" 담장 저쪽 사내가 말했어. \"그러시는 게 나을 것 같습니다, 아마도.\"\n\n그러자 한쪽 손이 조심스럽게 풀렸고, 노신사는 주저앉는 자세로 내려앉았어. 그러고는 니클비 부인에게 미소 짓고 절하려고 둘러보던 참에, 마치 누가 밑에서 다리를 잡아당기기라도 한 듯 후다닥 사라져 버렸지.\n\n그가 사라지자 한결 마음이 놓인 케이트가 엄마에게 말을 건네려고 돌아서는데, 그 꾀죄죄한 손이 다시 나타났어. 그리고 곧이어 거칠고 땅딸막한 사내의 모습이 따라 올라왔지. 조금 전 그 별난 이웃이 딛고 섰던 바로 그 계단을 밟고서 말이야.\n\n\"실례합니다, 아씨들.\" 새로 나타난 이가 씩 웃으며 모자에 손을 대고 말했어. \"저분이 두 분 중 누구한테 사랑 고백이라도 하던가요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 케이트가 말했어.\n\n\"아!\" 사내가 모자에서 손수건을 꺼내 얼굴을 닦으며 받았어. \"원래 늘 그러신답니다, 아시겠지만요. 사랑 고백은 무슨 수로도 못 말려요.\"\n\n\"가엾기도 하지, 저분이 정신이 온전치 않은 거냐고는 굳이 안 물어봐도 되겠네요.\" 케이트가 말했어.\n\n\"아무렴요.\" 사내가 모자 안을 들여다보고는 손수건을 한 번에 휙 던져 넣고 다시 쓰며 대답했어. \"그건 뻔히 보이지요, 그렇고말고요.\"\n\n\"오래되셨나요?\" 케이트가 물었어.\n\n\"오래됐지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 회복될 가망은 없는 건가요?\" 케이트가 가엾다는 듯 물었어.\n\n\"눈곱만큼도 없고, 그럴 자격도 없는 분이에요.\" 간수가 대답했어. \"정신이 있을 때보다 없을 때가 훨씬 나으시거든요. 숨 쉬는 사람 중에 그렇게 잔인하고, 그렇게 사악하고, 그렇게 지독한 늙은 구두쇠는 다시 없을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"정말이요?\" 케이트가 말했어.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 간수가 어찌나 힘주어 고개를 흔들었던지, 모자가 벗겨지지 않게 인상을 찌푸려야 할 정도였어. \"저런 망나니는 난생처음 봤어요. 제 동료도 똑같이 말하지요. 가엾은 아내는 속을 썩여 죽게 만들고, 딸들은 집에서 내쫓고, 아들들은 길거리로 내몰았지요. 그나마 끝에 가서 미쳐 버린 게 다행이었어요. 못된 성질에, 탐욕에, 이기심에, 게걸스레 처먹고 퍼마신 끝에 그리됐으니까요. 안 그랬으면 다른 사람들까지 여럿 미치게 했을 겁니다. 저분한테 가망이라니요, 저런 늙은 망나니한테! 세상에 가망이란 게 그리 흔하지도 않은데, 그나마 있는 가망은 저런 자보다 더 자격 있는 친구들 몫으로 남겨 둘 거라고, 내 한 닢 걸고 장담합니다.\"\n\n이런 신앙 고백을 늘어놓고서 간수는 또 한 번 고개를 흔들었어. 마치 일이 제대로 굴러가려면 이쯤은 돼야 한다는 듯이 말이야. 그러고는 시무룩하게 모자에 손을 댔지— 기분이 나빠서가 아니라, 그 화제가 마음을 거슬리게 한 거였어— 그러더니 사다리를 내려가 그걸 치워 버렸어.\n\n이 대화가 오가는 동안 니클비 부인은 그 사내를 매섭고도 한결같은 눈빛으로 바라보았어. 이제 부인은 깊은 한숨을 내쉬고는 입술을 꼭 다물고서, 천천히 미심쩍게 고개를 절레절레 흔들었지.\n\n\"가엾은 분!\" 케이트가 말했어.\n\n\"아! 정말 가엾지!\" 니클비 부인이 받았어. \"저런 일이 허용된다는 게 부끄러운 노릇이야. 부끄러운 일이고말고!\"\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 어쩌겠어요, 엄마?\" 케이트가 서글피 말했어. \"타고난 병약함이란—\"\n\n\"타고난 병약함이라니!\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"뭐라고! 너는 저 가엾은 신사가 정신이 나갔다고 생각하는 거니?\"\n\n\"저분을 본 사람이라면 누군들 달리 생각할 수 있겠어요, 엄마?\"\n\n\"그럼 내 너한테 딱 하나만 말해 주마, 케이트.\" 니클비 부인이 받았어. \"저분은 전혀 그런 게 아니야. 나는 네가 그렇게 속아 넘어갈 수 있다는 게 놀랍구나. 저건 저 사람들이 저분 재산을 가로채려는 무슨 음모야— 저분이 직접 그렇게 말했잖니? 저분이 좀 별나고 들떠 있을지는 모르지. 우리 중에도 그런 사람이 많잖니. 하지만 완전히 미쳤다니! 게다가 저렇게 정중하게, 그것도 아주 시적인 말씨로 자기 마음을 표현하고, 청혼도 저토록 신중하고 정성스럽고 사려 깊게 하시는데 말이야— 미친 사람이라면 길거리로 뛰쳐나가 처음 마주친 풋내기 계집애한테 무릎을 꿇었을 텐데, 저분은 그러지 않았잖니! 아니야, 아니야, 케이트. 저분의 광기에는 너무나 조리가 서 있어. 그건 믿어도 좋다, 얘야.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 42\n\n스노 힐의 보도는 온종일 더위에 굽고 지져졌어. 그 여관 입구를 지키는 사라센 머리 둘은—여관 이름이자 간판이기도 한 그 머리를 똑같이 본떠 둔 거였지—햇볕에 부풀어 오르고 그슬린 탓에 평소보다 더 사납게 보였어. 적어도 지치고 발이 부르튼 행인들 눈에는 그렇게 보였지. 바로 그때, 여관에서 가장 작은 응접실 하나에서는—열린 창문으로 김을 내뿜는 마차 말들의 구수한 냄새가 손에 잡힐 듯 모락모락 피어올랐어—찻상에 차려진 흔한 살림살이가 깔끔하고 먹음직스럽게 놓여 있었어. 그 둘레로는 큼직한 구운 고기와 삶은 고기 덩이, 우설 한 점, 비둘기 파이, 차가운 닭 한 마리, 에일 한 잔, 그 밖에 자잘한 것들이 늘어서 있었지. 이런 음식들은 타락한 마을이나 도시에서는 보통 든든한 점심이나 역마차 저녁상, 아니면 유난히 푸짐한 아침상에나 어울린다고 여겨지는 것들이었어.\n\n존 브라우디 씨는 주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣은 채 이 진수성찬 둘레를 안절부절못하고 맴돌았어. 이따금 멈춰 서서 아내 손수건으로 설탕 그릇에 앉은 파리를 휙휙 쫓기도 하고, 찻숟가락을 우유 단지에 담갔다 입으로 가져가기도 하고, 빵 껍질을 조금 잘라 내고 고기 한 귀퉁이를 베어 알약 두 알 삼키듯 두 번에 꿀꺽 넘기기도 했지. 이렇게 음식과 한 번씩 추파를 주고받을 때마다, 그는 시계를 꺼내 들고는 도무지 이 분도 더는 못 참겠다며 자못 처량할 만큼 진지하게 선언했어.\n\n\"틸리!\" 존이 자기 부인을 불렀어. 부인은 소파에 비스듬히 기댄 채 반쯤 깨고 반쯤 졸고 있었지.\n\n\"왜요, 존!\"\n\n\"왜요, 존이라니!\" 남편이 답답한 듯 받아쳤어. \"여보, 시장하지 않아?\"\n\n\"별로요.\" 브라우디 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"별로라니!\" 존이 천장으로 눈을 치켜뜨며 되뇌었어. \"세 시에 밥 먹고, 사람 달래기는커녕 약만 올리는 페이스트리로 점심을 때운 마당에 별로라니! 별로라고!\"\n\n\"손님 오셨습니다, 선생님.\" 웨이터가 안을 들여다보며 말했어.\n\n\"내, 뭐가 왔다고?\" 존이 마치 편지나 소포 얘긴 줄 안 것처럼 소리쳤어.\n\n\"손님이요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"이 친구야, 별 희한한 소릴 다 하네!\" 존이 말했어. \"와서 그게 무슨 소리야? 어서 모셔 와.\"\n\n\"안에 계십니까, 선생님?\"\n\n\"안에 있냐고!\" 존이 소리쳤어. \"있고말고, 두 시간 전에 차라도 마셨으면 좋았을 걸! 아니, 내가 딴 친구한테 문밖에서 똑똑히 지키고 섰다가 손님 오시면 곧장 알리라 했잖아, 우리가 배고파 죽을 지경이라고 말이야. 어서 모셔 와. 아하! 손 좀 줘 보쇼, 니클비 씨. 오늘은 내 평생에 둘도 없이 자랑스러운 날이오. 다들 잘 지내셨소? 어이쿠! 정말이지 기쁘구려!\"\n\n존 브라우디는 인사가 어찌나 진심에서 우러났던지 배고픈 것도 까맣게 잊고, 니콜라스의 손을 잡고 몇 번이고 흔들어 댔어. 흔들 때마다 그 손바닥을 어찌나 세게 철썩철썩 치던지, 환영하는 마음에 온기를 더하려는 셈이었지.\n\n\"아! 저기 있구려.\" 존이, 니콜라스가 아내 쪽으로 보내는 눈길을 알아채고 말했어. \"저기 있다오—이제 저 사람 두고 우리가 다툴 일은 없겠지요—안 그렇소? 야, 그때 일을 생각하면—아니, 그보다 뭐 좀 먹어야지. 자, 들어요, 어서 들어. 그리고 우리가 받으려는 이 음식에 대하여—\"\n\n식전 기도는 분명 제대로 끝까지 마쳤겠지만, 그 뒤로는 한마디도 들리지 않았어. 존이 벌써 칼과 포크를 어찌나 부지런히 놀려 대던지, 당분간은 말이 쑥 들어가 버렸거든.\n\n\"저는 늘 하던 대로 마음껏 굴겠습니다, 브라우디 씨.\" 니콜라스가 신부를 위해 의자를 놓아 주며 말했어.\n\n\"좋을 대로 하쇼.\" 존이 말했어. \"다 떨어지면 더 시키고.\"\n\n니콜라스는 굳이 설명할 것도 없이 얼굴이 발그레해진 브라우디 부인에게 입을 맞추고는 자리로 안내했어.\n\n\"이런,\" 존이 잠깐 어리둥절해서 말했어. \"아주 제 집처럼 편하게 굴구려, 응?\"\n\n\"그건 믿어도 좋습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"단, 한 가지 조건이 있어요.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔데요?\" 존이 물었어.\n\n\"대부가 필요할 일이 처음 생기면, 꼭 저를 대부로 삼아 주셔야 합니다.\"\n\n\"어! 저 소리 들었소?\" 존이 칼과 포크를 내려놓으며 외쳤어. \"대부라고! 하! 하! 하! 틸리—저 양반 말 좀 들어 봐—대부라네! 더는 한마디도 마쇼, 그건 도저히 못 당하겠소. 대부가 필요할 일이라니—대부라네! 하! 하! 하!\"\n\n점잖은 옛날 농담에 존 브라우디만큼 자지러지게 웃은 사람은 없을 거야. 그는 킥킥대다 와하하 터뜨리다, 큼직한 소고기 덩이를 웃으면서 들이마셔 숨통이 반쯤 막혔다가, 다시 웃어 젖히고, 그러면서도 기어이 먹기를 계속하고, 얼굴이 벌게지고 이마는 새카매지고, 기침을 하고, 눈물을 흘리고, 좀 나아졌다가, 속으로 또 웃음이 도지고, 더 심해지고, 목이 메어, 등을 두들겨 맞고, 발을 동동 구르고, 아내를 깜짝 놀라게 하고, 마침내 기진맥진한 끝에 눈에서 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 진정했어. 그러면서도 여전히 가냘프게 \"대부라네—대부라니, 틸리!\" 하고 내뱉었는데, 그 어떤 고통으로도 줄어들지 않을 만큼 그 농담을 절묘하게 음미하는 말투였지.\n\n\"우리가 처음 차를 마시던 그날 밤 기억하시죠?\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"내가 그걸 잊을 리가 있겠소?\" 존 브라우디가 대답했어.\n\n\"그날 밤 이분은 정말 무시무시한 사람이었지요, 안 그래요, 브라우디 부인?\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"완전히 괴물이었어요!\"\n\n\"집에 돌아가는 길에 이 사람이 하는 말을 들으셨으면, 정말 그렇게 말씀하셨을 거예요, 니클비 씨.\" 신부가 대답했어. \"평생 그렇게 무서웠던 적이 없다니까요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그건 말이지,\" 존이 입이 함박만 하게 벌어지도록 씩 웃으며 말했어. \"그게 아니란 걸 너도 잘 알잖아, 틸리.\"\n\n\"무서웠다니까요.\" 브라우디 부인이 대꾸했어. \"다시는 당신하고 말도 안 섞겠다고 마음을 거의 굳혔다고요.\"\n\n\"거의라!\" 존이 아까보다 더 입을 벌리고 웃으며 말했어. \"마음을 거의 굳혔다고! 그래 놓고 오는 내내 어르고 또 어르고, 살살 달래고 또 달래고 그랬지. '아니 너는 어쩌자고 저 친구가 너한테 수작 걸게 놔뒀냐?' 하고 내가 물었더니, '안 그랬어요, 존' 하고 내 팔을 꼭 쥐더라고. '안 그랬다고?' 하니까, '네' 하면서 또 한 번 꼭 쥐었지.\"\n\n\"어머나, 존!\" 예쁜 아내가 얼굴이 새빨개져서 끼어들었어. \"어쩜 그런 헛소릴 다 해요? 내가 그런 걸 꿈엔들 생각했겠어요!\"\n\n\"네가 그런 꿈을 꿨는지 어쨌는지야 내가 알 수 없지, 하긴 그럴 법도 하다 싶다만.\" 존이 받아쳤어. \"어쨌든 네가 그랬잖아. '너는 변덕스럽고 잘도 휙휙 도는 풍향계 같은 계집애야' 하니까, '변덕 아니에요, 존' 하더군. '맞아' 하고 내가 말했지. '변덕이야, 지독한 변덕이지. 학교 선생네 그 친구 두고 변덕 아니라곤 못할걸' 하니까, '그 사람이요!' 하고 막 째지게 소릴 지르더라고. '아! 그 사람!' 했더니, '아니, 존' 하면서—전보다 한참 더 바짝 다가와 더 세게 꼭 쥐고는—'당신 같은 번듯한 사람을 곁에 두고서 내가 저런 비실비실하고 빼빼 마른 애송이한테 넘어가는 게 말이 된다고 생각해요?' 하더라니까. 하! 하! 하! 애송이라잖아! '아이고!' 하고 내가 말했지. '그렇다면 날을 잡아서 후딱 해치워 버리자!' 하! 하! 하!\"\n\n니콜라스는 이 이야기에 진심으로 크게 웃었어. 그게 제 흉을 보는 얘기이기도 했고, 또 브라우디 부인의 얼굴 붉어짐을 덜어 주고 싶기도 했거든. 부인의 항변은 남편의 폭소에 묻혀 버렸지. 존의 좋은 성품 덕에 부인은 곧 마음이 편해졌어. 그리고 여전히 그 얘기를 아니라고 부인하면서도 어찌나 신나게 웃던지, 니콜라스는 그 얘기가 본질적으로는 한 치도 틀림없는 사실이라고 확신하고 흐뭇해졌지.\n\n\"우리가 함께 식사하는 건 이번이 두 번째군요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그리고 제가 두 분을 본 건 겨우 세 번째인데도, 정말이지 오랜 친구들 틈에 있는 것만 같습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇소!\" 요크셔 사나이가 말했어. \"내 말이 그 말이오.\"\n\n\"저도 정말 그래요.\" 젊은 아내가 덧붙였어.\n\n\"저야 누구보다 그렇게 느낄 까닭이 충분하지요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"제가 그런 걸 바라거나 기대할 처지가 전혀 아니었는데도, 좋은 친구분의 그 따뜻한 마음씨가 없었더라면, 제가 어찌 됐을지, 지금쯤 어떤 신세가 됐을지 모를 일이니까요.\"\n\n\"딴 얘길 하쇼.\" 존이 퉁명스럽게 대꾸했어. \"그런 걸로 사람 귀찮게 하지 말고.\"\n\n\"그럼 같은 곡조에 가사만 새로 붙여 부르지요.\" 니콜라스가 미소 지으며 말했어. \"편지에도 적었습니다만, 그 가엾은 소년을 향한 당신의 따뜻한 마음에 저는 깊이 감동했고 감탄했습니다. 당신 자신이 곤경과 어려움에 휘말릴 위험을 무릅쓰고 그 애를 풀어 주셨으니까요. 하지만 그 애와 제가, 또 당신이 알지 못하는 다른 사람들까지, 그 애를 가엾이 여겨 주신 당신께 얼마나 고마워하는지는 도저히 다 말로 못 합니다.\"\n\n\"아이고!\" 존 브라우디가 의자를 바짝 당기며 받았어. \"그리고 나도 당신한테 도저히 다 말 못 하겠소. 우리가 아는 어떤 사람들도, 내가 그 애를 가엾이 여겨 줬다는 걸 알면 똑같이 고마워할 거란 걸 말이오.\"\n\n\"아!\" 브라우디 부인이 탄성을 질렀어. \"그날 밤 내가 어떤 꼴이었는데요!\"\n\n\"그자들은 당신이 그 애 도망을 거든 걸 두고 의심이라도 했나요?\" 니콜라스가 존 브라우디에게 물었어.\n\n\"전혀.\" 요크셔 사나이가 입을 귀밑까지 쭉 벌리며 대답했어. \"날이 어두워진 뒤로도 한참, 나는 선생네 침대에 떡하니 누워 있었는데, 아무도 가까이 오질 않더라고. '음!' 하고 나는 생각했지. '저 녀석 출발 한번 그럴듯하게 했군. 지금쯤 집에 못 들어갔으면 영영 못 들어갈 테지. 그러니 올 테면 빨리들 와 보라지, 다 준비됐으니까'—그러니까, 알겠소만, 선생이 올지도 모른다는 얘기였지.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"이윽고,\" 존이 말을 이었어. \"선생이 정말 왔지. 아래층에서 문 닫히는 소리가 들리고, 그 양반이 어둠 속을 더듬더듬 올라오더군. '천천히, 차분하게.' 나는 속으로 말했지. '느긋하게 하쇼, 선생—서두를 거 없소.' 그가 문 앞에 와서 열쇠를 돌리더군—자물쇠를 채울 것도 없는데 열쇠를 돌리더니—'어이, 거기!' 하고 소리쳤어. '그래' 하고 나는 생각했지. '몇 번을 더 그래 봐야 아무도 안 깰 거요, 선생.' '어이, 거기' 하고 그가 또 부르고는 뚝 멈추더군. '날 약 올리지 않는 게 좋을 거다' 하고 잠시 뒤에 선생이 말했지. '뼈마디를 죄다 부러뜨려 놓겠다, 스마이크' 하고 또 한참 뒤에 말하더라고. 그러더니 갑자기 불을 가져오라고 빽 소리를 질렀어. 불이 오니까—야, 그 난리 법석이라니! '무슨 일이오?' 하고 내가 물었지. '그 녀석이 도망쳤어' 하더군—복수심에 완전히 미쳐 날뛰면서. '아무 소리도 못 들었나?' '들었소' 하고 내가 말했지. '바로 조금 전에 길 쪽 문이 닫히는 소리가 났소. 누가 저쪽으로 뛰어가는 소리도 들었고요'(딴 쪽을 가리키면서 말이야—응?). '사람 살려!' 하고 그가 외쳤어. '내가 도와주겠소' 하고 내가 말했지. 그래서 우리는 출발했어—엉뚱한 길로! 호! 호! 호!\"\n\n\"멀리까지 갔습니까?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"멀리!\" 존이 대답했어. \"십오 분 만에 그자 다리가 풀려 자빠지게 만들어 놨지. 모자도 안 쓴 선생이, 무릎까지 푹푹 빠지는 진흙탕과 물구덩이를 헤치며 휙휙 달리고, 울타리를 넘다 고꾸라지고, 도랑에 처박히고, 미친 사람처럼 고래고래 소리치고, 외눈으로는 그 녀석을 매섭게 찾으려 두리번거리고, 외투 자락은 뒤로 펄럭이고, 얼굴이고 어디고 온통 진흙투성이가 된 꼴이라니! 나는 그만 푹 쓰러져 웃다가 죽는 줄 알았소.\"\n\n존이 그저 떠올리기만 해도 어찌나 진심으로 웃어 대던지, 듣던 두 사람한테까지 그 웃음이 옮아서, 셋이 다 폭소를 터뜨렸어. 그 웃음은 몇 번이고 되살아나, 마침내 더는 웃을 수 없을 때까지 이어졌지.\n\n\"그자는 못된 인간이오.\" 존이 눈물을 닦으며 말했어. \"아주 못된 인간이지, 그 선생은.\"\n\n\"나는 그 사람 꼴도 보기 싫어요, 존.\" 아내가 말했어.\n\n\"이런,\" 존이 받아쳤어. \"그런 소리 잘도 하네, 그래. 너 아니었으면 우리가 그자를 알 일도 없었잖아. 네가 먼저 알았던 거 아니야, 틸리?\"\n\n\"패니 스퀴어스를 안 알 수가 있어야지요, 존.\" 아내가 대답했어. \"걔는 내 옛날 소꿉친구였잖아요.\"\n\n\"음,\" 존이 대답했어. \"내 말이 그 말 아니냐? 이웃끼리 사이좋게 지내고 옛 정분을 이어 가는 게 좋은 거지. 그리고 내가 늘 하는 말이, 웬만하면 다투지 말자는 거야. 안 그렇소, 니클비 씨?\"\n\n\"그럼요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그리고 그 잊지 못할 저녁 뒤에 길에서 말 타고 가시던 당신을 만났을 때, 당신은 바로 그 원칙대로 행동하셨지요.\"\n\n\"암, 그렇소.\" 존이 말했어. \"내가 한번 한 말은 끝까지 지키지요.\"\n\n\"그건 훌륭한 일이고, 사내답기도 합니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"비록 런던에서 '요크셔 식으로 사람을 속여먹는다'고 할 때 떠올리는 그런 건 아니지만요. 쪽지에 보니 스퀴어스 양이 댁에 와 있다고 하셨던데요.\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 존이 대답했어. \"틸리의 들러리지요. 그것도 별난 들러리고. 그 처녀, 신부가 되긴 한참 멀었을걸요, 내 짐작엔.\"\n\n\"부끄러운 줄 아세요, 존.\" 브라우디 부인이 말했어. 자기도 신부면서 그 농담의 뜻만은 예리하게 알아채고서 말이야.\n\n\"그 신랑은 복받은 사내일 거요.\" 존이 그 생각에 눈을 반짝이며 말했어. \"운수 대통한 거지, 그자는.\"\n\n\"있잖아요, 니클비 씨.\" 아내가 말했어. \"그 애가 여기 와 있는 까닭에, 존이 당신께 편지를 써서 오늘 밤으로 약속을 잡은 거예요. 지난 일이 있고 나서 두 분이 마주치는 게 유쾌하진 않을 거라고 생각했거든요.\"\n\n\"두말할 것 없지요. 잘 생각하셨습니다.\" 니콜라스가 끼어들며 말했어.\n\n\"특히,\" 브라우디 부인이 자못 짓궂은 표정으로 말했어. \"지난날 다 끝나 버린 연애 사건에 관해 우리가 아는 걸 생각하면 더더욱 그렇죠.\"\n\n\"우리가 알다마다요!\" 니콜라스가 고개를 저으며 말했어. \"그때 당신이 좀 짓궂게 굴었던 것 같던데요.\"\n\n\"그야 당연하지요.\" 존 브라우디가 큼직한 집게손가락으로 아내의 예쁜 곱슬머리 한 가닥을 쓸어 넘기며, 아내가 무척 자랑스럽다는 듯이 말했어. \"이 사람은 늘 어찌나 까불고 장난기가 넘치던지, 꼭—\"\n\n\"그래, 꼭 뭐 같은데요?\" 아내가 물었어.\n\n\"꼭 여자 같았지.\" 존이 대답했어. \"야! 그거 말고는 달리 비길 만한 게 없어서 말이야.\"\n\n\"스퀴어스 양 얘기를 하고 계셨지요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. 브라우디 부부 사이에 막 오가기 시작한 자잘한 부부간 정담을 멈추려는 뜻이었지. 그 정담 탓에 곁에 끼인 제삼자 처지가 좀 난처해졌고, 그 자리에 있는 게 거추장스럽게 느껴졌거든.\n\n\"아 참, 그래요.\" 브라우디 부인이 대답했어. \"존이 다 했어요. 존이 오늘 밤으로 잡은 건, 그 애가 아버지한테 가서 차를 마시겠다고 정해 놨기 때문이에요. 그리고 아무 탈도 없도록, 또 당신이 우리하고만 오붓하게 있도록 확실히 하려고, 존이 직접 거기 가서 그 애를 데려오기로 한 거예요.\"\n\n\"아주 잘 짜셨네요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그래도 저 때문에 이렇게 폐를 끼치게 돼서 죄송합니다.\"\n\n\"천만에요, 조금도요.\" 브라우디 부인이 대답했어. \"저희는 당신을 뵐 날을—존이랑 저랑—더없이 큰 기쁨으로 손꼽아 기다렸는걸요. 그런데 있잖아요, 니클비 씨,\" 브라우디 부인이 더없이 짓궂은 미소를 지으며 말했어. \"저는 정말이지 패니 스퀴어스가 당신을 무척 좋아했다고 생각해요.\"\n\n\"고마운 일이군요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"하지만 맹세코, 저는 그 처녀의 순결한 마음에 무슨 인상이라도 남기겠다고 바란 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"별말씀을 다 하시네!\" 브라우디 부인이 키득거렸어. \"아니, 그런데 정말로 있잖아요—이제 농담 다 빼고 진지하게요—제가 패니한테 직접 들은 바로는, 당신이 그 애한테 청혼을 했고, 두 사람이 아주 엄숙하고 정식으로 약혼을 하기로 했다지 뭐예요.\"\n\n\"당신이—당신이 그렇게 들었다고요?\" 날카로운 여자 목소리가 외쳤어. \"내가—이 내가—우리 아빠 피를 흘리게 한 살인마 도둑놈하고 약혼하려 했다는 말을 당신이 들었다고요? 당신은—당신은 정말로 생각하나요, 부인—내가 발밑의 흙처럼 천한 그자를, 약속이라도 했다간 손에 시커멓게 검댕만 묻을 그자를, 부엌 집게로도 차마 건드리기 싫은 그자를 무척 좋아했다고요? 그래요, 부인—정말 그래요? 아! 비열하고 천박한 틸다!\"\n\n이렇게 비난을 퍼부으며 스퀴어스 양이 문을 활짝 열어젖혔어. 그러고는 깜짝 놀란 브라우디 부부와 니콜라스의 눈앞에, 앞서 말한 그 순결한 흰옷(조금 더 더러워진)을 차려입은 자신의 균형 잡힌 자태뿐 아니라, 오라비와 아버지, 즉 워크퍼드 부자(父子)의 모습까지 드러내 보였지.\n\n\"이게 결말인가요, 응?\" 스퀴어스 양이 말을 이었어. 어찌나 흥분했던지 'ㅎ' 발음에 잔뜩 힘이 들어갔지. \"이게 결말인가요, 응? 그 두 얼굴 가진 것한테—그 독사한테—그 무슨—무슨—인어한테 내가 베푼 온갖 참을성과 우정의 결말이? (스퀴어스 양은 이 마지막 욕설을 한참 고르고 고르더니, 마침내 이게 모든 걸 못 박는다는 듯 의기양양하게 내뱉었어.) 이게 결말인가요, 응? 그 애의 속임수, 그 천박함, 그 거짓됨을, 또 천박한 마음들의 찬사나 낚으려고 갖은 교태를 부리는 그 꼴을, 내가—내 무엇을 위해 부끄러워 얼굴이 붉어지도록 참아 준 결말이?\"\n\n\"성별이지.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 구경꾼들을 악의에 찬 눈으로—말 그대로 한쪽 눈으로—노려보며 거들었어.\n\n\"그래요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어. \"하지만 우리 엄마도 같은—이라는 게 천만다행이죠—\"\n\n\"옳소, 옳소!\" 스퀴어스 씨가 한마디 했어. \"그 사람이 여기 와서 이 작자들을 한바탕 할퀴어 줬으면 좋겠구나.\"\n\n\"이게 결말인가요, 응?\" 스퀴어스 양이 고개를 홱 젖히고 바닥을 경멸하듯 내려다보며 말했어. \"내가 저 쓰레기 같은 것한테 관심을 베풀고, 내 몸을 낮춰 가며 돌봐 준 결말이?\"\n\n\"아이참, 그만해.\" 브라우디 부인이 자기를 말리려는 남편의 온갖 시도를 무시하고 맨 앞줄로 밀고 나서며 받아쳤어. \"그런 말도 안 되는 소리 좀 그만해.\"\n\n\"내가 너를 돌봐 주지 않았던가, 응?\" 스퀴어스 양이 따졌어.\n\n\"아니.\" 브라우디 부인이 대답했어.\n\n\"저런 데서 부끄러워 붉어지는 낯빛 따위는 바라지도 않겠어.\" 스퀴어스 양이 거만하게 말했어. \"저 얼굴은 수치심이랑 뻔뻔한 철면피 말고는 아무것도 모르니까.\"\n\n\"이봐요.\" 아내한테 쏟아지는 이 거듭된 공격에 약이 오른 존 브라우디가 끼어들었어. \"좀 점잖게 하쇼, 점잖게.\"\n\n\"당신은요, 브라우디 씨.\" 스퀴어스 양이 그의 말을 잽싸게 받았어. \"딱하게 여길 뿐이에요. 당신한테는 끝없는 연민 말고는 아무 감정도 없어요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"오!\" 존이 말했어.\n\n\"네.\" 스퀴어스 양이 제 아비를 곁눈질하며 말했어. \"비록 나는 별난 들러리고, 신부가 되긴 한참 멀었고, 또 내 남편 될 사람은 운수 대통할 거라지만요, 선생님께 품은 감정은 연민의 감정뿐이에요.\"\n\n이러면서 스퀴어스 양은 다시 아버지를 곁눈질했어. 아버지도 그녀를 곁눈질했지. 마치 '그래, 한 방 제대로 먹였다'는 투로 말이야.\n\n\"당신이 앞으로 어떤 일을 겪어야 할지 나는 알아요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 곱슬머리를 사납게 흔들며 말했어. \"당신 앞에 어떤 삶이 펼쳐질지 안다고요. 당신이 내 철천지원수라 해도, 그보다 더 나쁜 일을 빌어 줄 순 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그렇담 자기가 직접 저 사람하고 결혼하고 싶진 않아?\" 브라우디 부인이 더없이 상냥한 말투로 물었어.\n\n\"어머, 부인, 어쩜 그리 재치가 넘치실까.\" 스퀴어스 양이 낮게 절을 하며 받아쳤어. \"재치가 영악함에 거의 맞먹으시네요, 부인. 내가 아빠랑 차 마시러 가서 데리러 오기 전엔 절대 안 돌아올 게 뻔한 그 시간을 골라 잡다니, 어쩜 그리도 영악하실까요, 부인! 남들도 자기만큼 영악해서 그 계획을 망쳐 놓을 수 있다는 걸 한 번도 생각 못 하셨다니, 참 안됐네요!\"\n\n\"얘야, 그런 잘난 척으론 나를 화나게 못 한단다.\" 전날의 프라이스 양이 마님 행세를 하며 말했어.\n\n\"제발 나한테 '얘' 소리 마세요, 부인.\" 스퀴어스 양이 날카롭게 받았어. \"그건 못 참아요. 이게 결말—\"\n\n\"에잇, 빌어먹을.\" 존 브라우디가 답답한 듯 외쳤어. \"할 말 있으면 다 해 버려, 패니. 그게 결말인지 똑똑히 못 박고, 결말이냐 아니냐 남한테 묻지나 말고.\"\n\n\"청하지도 않은 충고 고맙기도 하네요, 브라우디 씨.\" 스퀴어스 양이 애써 정중하게 받아쳤어. \"내 세례명을 함부로 입에 올리는 주제넘은 짓일랑 삼가 주시지요. 아무리 연민을 베푼다 해도, 나 스스로에 대한 도리를 잊을 만큼은 아니니까요, 브라우디 씨. 틸다,\" 스퀴어스 양이 어찌나 갑자기 사납게 목소리를 높이던지 존이 신발 안에서 발을 흠칫했어. \"너를 영영 내쳐 버리겠어, 이 아가씨야. 너를 버리겠어. 너와 의절하겠어. 나는,\" 스퀴어스 양이 엄숙한 목소리로 외쳤어. \"무덤에서 구해 내는 한이 있어도 자식한테 틸다라는 이름은 절대 안 붙일 거야.\"\n\n\"그 일이라면,\" 존이 한마디 했어. \"그놈이 태어나거든 그때 이름을 생각해도 늦지 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"존!\" 아내가 끼어들었어. \"그만 약 올려요.\"\n\n\"오! 약을 올린다고요!\" 스퀴어스 양이 발끈 성을 내며 외쳤어. \"약을 올린다고! 흐, 흐! 약 올린다네! 아니, 약 올리지 마세요. 부디 저 사람 마음 좀 헤아려 주시지!\"\n\n\"엿듣는 사람은 제 흉밖에 못 듣는 게 정해진 이치라면,\" 브라우디 부인이 말했어. \"그야 나도 어쩔 수 없고, 그건 정말 미안해. 하지만 이건 말해 두겠어, 패니. 나는 네 등 뒤에서 수도 없이 네 얘기를 좋게 했고, 그 말들은 너조차 흠잡을 데 하나 없었을 거야.\"\n\n\"오, 그러셨겠지요, 부인!\" 스퀴어스 양이 또 한 번 절을 하며 외쳤어. \"그 갸륵한 마음씨에 깊이 감사드리고, 다음번엔 부디 저를 모질게 대하지 말아 주십사 빌고 또 빕니다!\"\n\n\"내가 지금 이 순간까지,\" 브라우디 부인이 말을 이었어. \"네 얘기를 그렇게 나쁘게 한 건 없는 것 같은데. 어쨌거나 내가 한 말은 다 사실이었어. 그래도 혹시 그랬다면, 정말 미안하고 용서를 빌게. 너는 수십 번도 더 나를 훨씬 더 나쁘게 말했지만, 패니, 나는 너한테 한 번도 앙심을 품은 적이 없어. 그러니 너도 나한테 앙심 품지 않았으면 좋겠어.\"\n\n스퀴어스 양은 이렇다 할 대꾸 대신, 옛 친구를 머리부터 발끝까지 훑어보고는 형언할 수 없는 경멸로 코를 치켜드는 게 다였어. 하지만 '요 계집', '여우 같은 것', '하찮은 것' 따위의 알아듣기 힘든 말들이 입에서 새어 나왔지. 게다가 입술을 사납게 깨물고, 침을 삼키기를 무척 힘겨워하고, 숨이 자꾸만 들락날락하는 걸 보니, 스퀴어스 양의 가슴속에서도 입 밖에 못 낼 만큼 거센 감정이 부풀어 오르는 모양이었어.\n\n위와 같은 대화가 오가는 동안, 워크퍼드 도련님은 아무도 자기를 안 보는 틈을 타, 압도적인 식욕이 강하게 도지는 걸 느끼고서, 조금씩 조금씩 식탁 쪽으로 슬그머니 다가갔어. 그러고는 접시 안쪽을 손가락으로 빙빙 돌려 훑고서 그 손가락을 더없이 맛나게 쪽쪽 빠는가 하면, 빵을 뜯어 그 조각을 버터 위에 쓱쓱 끌고 다니고, 줄곧 깊은 생각에 잠긴 척하면서 설탕 덩이를 주머니에 슬쩍 챙기는 식으로, 가벼운 잽을 날리며 음식을 공략하기 시작했지. 이 자잘한 방종에 아무도 간섭하지 않는 걸 알아채자, 그는 차차 더 큰 짓으로 올라갔어. 그리고 적당히 괜찮은 찬 음식을 한 접시 떠먹고 나서는, 이 무렵엔 파이 속에 깊숙이 빠져 있었지.\n\n이 모든 게 스퀴어스 씨의 눈을 비껴가진 않았어. 사람들의 관심이 다른 데 쏠려 있는 동안에는, 제 아들이자 후계자가 적의 비용으로 살을 찌우고 있다는 생각에 스퀴어스 씨는 속으로 흐뭇해했지. 그런데 이제 잠깐 조용해진 틈이 생겨, 어린 워크퍼드의 행각이 도저히 눈에 안 띌 수 없게 되자, 그는 마치 이제야 그 일을 처음 알아챈 척하며, 그 젊은 도련님의 뺨을 철썩 갈겼어. 그 바람에 찻잔들이 다 쟁그랑 울릴 정도였지.\n\n\"처먹어!\" 스퀴어스 씨가 외쳤어. \"그것도 제 아비 원수들이 남긴 걸! 너를 독살하고도 남을 음식이다, 이 후레자식 같은 놈.\"\n\n\"애한테 탈 안 나오.\" 싸움에 사내 하나가 끼어든다는 생각에 적잖이 안도한 기색으로 존이 말했어. \"먹게 놔두쇼. 차라리 그 학교 애들 전부가 여기 왔으면 좋겠소. 내 마지막 한 푼까지 털어서라도, 그 불쌍한 배들 좀 채워 줄 걸 먹였으면 좋겠다고!\"\n\n스퀴어스는 그 얼굴이 지을 수 있는 가장 흉악하고 악의에 찬 표정으로—그쪽으로는 또 어찌나 표정이 다채로운 얼굴인지—그를 노려보며 몰래 주먹을 흔들었어.\n\n\"이봐요, 이봐, 선생.\" 존이 말했어. \"바보짓 좀 마쇼. 내가 이걸 한 번만—딱 한 번만—흔들었다간, 그 바람에 당신이 푹 자빠질 테니까.\"\n\n\"네가, 응?\" 스퀴어스가 받아쳤어. \"내 도망친 녀석을 도와 빼돌린 게 네놈이야? 네놈이었어?\"\n\n\"나요!\" 존이 큰 소리로 받았어. \"그래, 나였소, 어쩔 거요? 나였다니까. 자, 그래서?\"\n\n\"이 애야, 저자가 제 입으로 했다고 하는 거 들었지!\" 스퀴어스가 딸한테 호소하며 말했어. \"저자가 제 입으로 했다고 하는 거 들었어!\"\n\n\"했고말고!\" 존이 외쳤어. \"한마디 더 해 주지, 이것도 들어 두쇼. 당신한테 도망친 녀석이 또 있으면, 나는 또 그렇게 해 줄 거요. 도망친 녀석이 스무 명 있으면, 스무 번을 또 해 주고, 거기다 스무 번을 더 해 주겠소. 그리고 한마디 더 하지.\" 존이 말했어. \"이젠 내 피가 끓어오르니 말인데, 당신은 늙은 악당이오. 그리고 당신이 늙은이라서 다행인 줄 아쇼, 안 그랬으면 가루가 되도록 빻아 놨을 거요. 당신이 점잖은 사람한테, 교회에서 그 불쌍한 녀석을 두들겨 팼다고 떠벌릴 때 말이오.\"\n\n\"점잖은 사람이라고!\" 스퀴어스가 비웃으며 외쳤어.\n\n\"아! 점잖은 사람이오.\" 존이 대꾸했어. \"당신 같은 작자랑 한 식탁에 다리를 들이미는 것만 빼면, 매사에 점잖은 사람이지.\"\n\n\"중상모략이다!\" 스퀴어스가 의기양양하게 말했어. \"여기 증인이 둘이야. 워크퍼드는 맹세가 뭔지 알지, 알고말고. 당신을 그걸로 걸어 주겠어, 선생. 악당이라고, 응?\" 스퀴어스 씨가 수첩을 꺼내 그걸 적어 넣었어. \"아주 좋아. 다음 순회재판에서 이건, 점잖다는 말은 빼고도, 족히 이십 파운드짜리는 되겠는걸, 선생.\"\n\n\"순회재판이라.\" 존이 외쳤어. \"나한테 순회재판 운운하지 않는 게 좋을 거요. 요크셔 학교들이 순회재판에서 까발려진 게 한두 번이 아니거든, 이 양반아. 그건 다시 들춰 봐야 좋을 게 없는 께름칙한 얘기지, 내 장담하는데.\"\n\n스퀴어스 씨는 위협하듯 고개를 흔들었어. 격분해서 얼굴이 새하얗게 질린 채 말이야. 그러고는 딸의 팔을 붙잡고 어린 워크퍼드의 손을 끌면서 문 쪽으로 물러났어.\n\n\"그리고 너로 말하면,\" 스퀴어스가 몸을 돌려 니콜라스를 향해 말했어. 니콜라스는 전에 한 번 이자를 호되게 따끔하게 해 준 적이 있던 터라, 일부러 이 언쟁에 끼어들지 않고 있었지. \"내가 머잖아 너를 가만 안 두나 봐라. 아이들을 납치하고 다니겠다, 그거지? 그 애비들이 나타나지 않게 조심하는 게 좋을 거다—명심해—그 애비들이 나타나서, 네놈이 아무리 막아도 그 애들을 도로 내게 보내 내 마음대로 하게 만들지 않도록 조심하란 말이다.\"\n\n\"그건 두렵지 않습니다.\" 니콜라스가 경멸하듯 어깨를 으쓱하고는 돌아서며 대꾸했어.\n\n\"안 두렵다고!\" 스퀴어스가 악마 같은 표정으로 받아쳤어. \"자, 그럼, 가자.\"\n\n\"나는 아빠랑 함께, 이런 무리를 영영 떠나겠어요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 경멸하듯 거만하게 둘러보며 말했어. \"이런 것들이랑 같은 공기를 마시는 것만으로도 더러워진다고요. 가엾은 브라우디 씨! 흐! 흐! 흐! 정말 딱해요, 정말로요. 저렇게 속고 있다니. 흐! 흐! 흐!—교활하고 음흉한 틸다!\"\n\n이렇게 갑작스레 다시 더없이 매섭고 위풍당당한 분노로 빠져들면서, 스퀴어스 양은 방을 휙 쓸고 나갔어. 그리고 마지막 순간까지 위엄을 지키더니, 복도에 나가서는 흐느끼고 비명을 지르며 발버둥 치는 소리가 들려왔지.\n\n존 브라우디는 식탁 뒤에 선 채, 입을 떡 벌리고 아내와 니콜라스를 번갈아 보고 또 봤어. 그러다 손이 우연히 에일 잔에 닿자, 그걸 집어 들고는 한동안 거기다 얼굴을 파묻었다가, 긴 숨을 한 번 내쉬고는 잔을 니콜라스에게 건네고 벨을 울렸지.\n\n\"이봐요, 웨이터.\" 존이 활기차게 말했어. \"여기 좀 봐요. 이것들 다 치우고, 열 시에 야식으로 뭐 좀 구워 와요—아주 든든하게, 푸짐하게 말이오. 브랜디랑 물도 좀 가져오고, 슬리퍼 한 켤레도—집에서 제일 큰 걸로—얼른 가져와요. 어이쿠!\" 존이 두 손을 비비며 말했어. \"이제 오늘 밤엔 누구 데리러 나갈 일도 없으니, 야, 이제 본격적으로 저녁 한번 제대로 즐겨 봅시다.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 43\n\n폭풍은 진작에 더없이 깊은 고요에 자리를 내주었고, 저녁도 꽤 깊어 있었다. 사실 저녁 식사는 이미 끝났고, 인체의 구조와 기능에 밝은 똑똑한 사람들이라면 누구나 그래야 마땅하다고 여길 만큼 — 그러니까 완전한 평온과 즐거운 대화, 그리고 적당량의 브랜디 물을 곁들인 가운데 — 소화도 순조롭게 진행되고 있던 참이었다. 그때 세 친구, 아니 시민적으로나 종교적으로나, 또 신성한 결혼의 상태에 마땅한 존중과 경의를 담아 말하자면 두 친구가 — 브라우디 부부는 하나로 쳐야 하니까 — 아래층에서 들려오는 사납고 성난 고함 소리에 깜짝 놀랐다. 그 소리는 곧 어찌나 높이 치솟던지, 게다가 어찌나 거창하고 살벌하고 험악한 말로 쏟아지던지, 정말로 그 여관에 사라센 사람의 머리가, 그것도 진짜로 살아 있고 분노에 차서 도무지 달랠 길 없는 사라센 사람의 몸통과 어깨 위에 얹힌 머리가 있었다 해도 그보다 더하지는 못했을 정도였다.\n\n이 소동은, 술집이든 입법 회의장이든 다른 어디서든 흔히 그러듯 처음 한바탕 터지고 나서 금세 그저 투덜대고 으르렁대는 말다툼으로 잦아드는 게 아니라, 시시각각 더 커져만 갔다. 그 온갖 소음이 폐 하나에서 나오는 것 같았는데도, 그 폐 하나가 어찌나 힘이 좋던지 \"악당\", \"불한당\", \"건방진 강아지\" 같은 말과, 상대를 추켜세우기로는 그에 못지않은 갖가지 욕설을 어찌나 신나고 우렁차게 되풀이하던지, 보통 때 같으면 열두 사람이 한꺼번에 목청을 높여도 그보다 소란은 훨씬 덜하고 일으키는 놀라움도 훨씬 작았을 정도였다.\n\n\"아니, 무슨 일이지?\" 니콜라스가 문 쪽으로 황급히 다가서며 말했다.\n\n존 브라우디도 같은 방향으로 성큼성큼 걸어가는데, 브라우디 부인이 얼굴이 새파래져서는 의자에 기댄 채 가냘픈 목소리로 그에게 일렀다. 만약 그가 위험한 데로 뛰어들면 자기는 당장 히스테리를 일으킬 작정이고, 그 결과가 그가 생각하는 것보다 훨씬 심각할 수도 있으니 명심하라는 거였다. 존은 이 소식에 좀 난처한 기색이었는데, 그러면서도 얼굴에는 슬그머니 웃음이 비쳤다. 하지만 도무지 싸움판에서 빠질 수가 없었던 그는 절충안을 찾았다. 아내의 팔을 자기 팔에 끼고는, 그렇게 동반한 채 니콜라스를 따라 있는 힘껏 빠르게 아래층으로 내려간 것이다.\n\n소란이 벌어진 곳은 커피룸 문밖 복도였다. 거기엔 커피룸 손님들과 종업원들이, 거기다 마당에서 온 마부와 일꾼 두세 명까지 한데 모여 있었다. 이들은 한 젊은이를 황급히 둘러싸고 있었는데, 그 젊은이는 보아하니 니콜라스보다 한두 살쯤 위인 듯했다. 그는 방금 말한 그런 호언장담을 늘어놓았을 뿐 아니라, 분이 더 치밀어 한층 더 나아간 모양이었다. 그의 발에는 양말 한 켤레 말고는 아무것도 신겨 있지 않았고, 슬리퍼 한 켤레는 맞은편 구석에 엎어진 사람의 머리에서 그리 멀지 않은 데 놓여 있었다. 그 엎어진 사람은 발길질에 차여 지금 그 자리로 처박힌 모양이었고, 그러고 나서 슬리퍼를 양쪽 귀에 얻어맞는 호사까지 누린 듯했다.\n\n커피룸 손님들도, 종업원들도, 마부들도, 일꾼들도 — 열린 미닫이창 너머로 구경하던 술집 아가씨는 말할 것도 없고 — 그 순간 그들의 눈짓과 끄덕임과 중얼거리는 소리로 미루어 보면, 양말 차림의 젊은 신사한테 단단히 등을 돌릴 기색이었다. 이걸 알아챈 니콜라스는, 그 젊은 신사가 자기와 거의 같은 나이인 데다 어디로 보나 싸움을 일삼는 사람 같지 않다는 것도 보고서, 가끔 젊은이들을 움직이게 하는 그런 감정에 떠밀려 약한 쪽 편을 들고 싶은 마음이 아주 강하게 솟구쳤다. 그래서 단번에 무리 한가운데로 비집고 들어가, 어쩌면 상황이 보장하는 것보다 좀 더 단호한 어조로, 대체 이 소란이 다 무슨 일이냐고 따져 물었다.\n\n\"어이!\" 마당에서 온 사람 하나가 말했다. \"이건 변장한 누구신가 보네, 이거.\"\n\n\"러시아 황제의 큰아드님 나가신다, 자리 좀 비키시오, 신사 여러분!\" 또 다른 녀석이 외쳤다.\n\n군중 속에서 제일 옷을 잘 차려입은 사람을 비웃는 농담이 으레 그렇듯 이 농지거리들도 유난히 잘 먹혀들었지만, 니콜라스는 그 말들을 무시하고 무심하게 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러고는 그새 슬리퍼를 주워 발을 꿴 그 젊은 신사를 향해, 공손한 태도로 아까 했던 질문을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"별것 아닙니다!\" 젊은이가 대답했다.\n\n이 말에 구경꾼들 사이에서 웅성거림이 일었다. 그중 대담한 몇몇이 외쳤다. \"허, 그래?— 정말 별것 아니라고?— 아무것도 아니라, 응?— 저게 별것 아니라는 거야, 정말?— 정말 별것 아니면 다행인 줄 알아.\" 이런 말과 함께 비아냥대는 못마땅한 소리들을 잔뜩 쏟아낸 다음, 마당에서 온 녀석 두세 명이 니콜라스와 소동을 일으킨 그 젊은 신사를 떠밀기 시작했다. 실수인 척 부딪치고, 발등을 밟고, 그런 식으로 말이다. 그런데 이건 둥근 놀이판 같은 거라서, 꼭 서너 명만 끼라는 법은 없었고, 존 브라우디한테도 열려 있었다. 그가 — 아내가 기겁하는 가운데 — 작은 무리 속으로 와락 뛰어들어 사방으로 몸을 부딪쳐 댔다. 오른쪽으로, 왼쪽으로, 앞으로, 뒤로, 그러다 유난히 설치던 키 큰 일꾼의 모자를 팔꿈치로 얼결에 푹 뚫어 버리니, 순식간에 형세가 아주 딴판으로 바뀌었다. 그러는 동안 우람한 녀석 여럿이 절뚝거리며 점잖게 거리를 두고 물러나, 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채 이 덩치 큰 요크셔 사람의 묵직한 걸음과 육중한 발을 저주했다.\n\n\"어디 또 한 번 해 보라지.\" 구석으로 차여 처박혔던 사내가 일어서며 말했다. 보아하니 방금 전의 적수와 대등하게 맞붙겠다는 마음보다는, 존 브라우디가 얼떨결에 자기를 밟을까 봐 무서워서 일어선 듯했다. \"어디 또 한 번 해 보라고. 그게 다야.\"\n\n\"그 소리 또 한 번 해 봐.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"그러면 네 그 대가리를 저 뒤에 있는 와인 잔들 속에다 처박아 줄 테니까.\"\n\n이때 그저 머리 깨지는 일만 벌어지는 동안엔 신이 나서 손을 비비며 구경하던 종업원 하나가, 어서 경찰을 불러오라고 구경꾼들에게 아주 간곡히 애원했다. 안 그러면 틀림없이 살인이 날 거라며, 이 집 안의 유리며 도자기는 죄다 자기 책임이라는 거였다.\n\n\"아무도 수고스럽게 움직일 것 없습니다.\" 젊은 신사가 말했다. \"저는 오늘 밤 이 집에 묵을 거고, 무슨 폭행 책임이라도 물을 게 있으면 아침에 여기서 저를 찾으면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"왜 저 사람을 친 거요?\" 곁에 선 사람 하나가 물었다.\n\n\"그래! 왜 저 사람을 쳤소?\" 다른 사람들도 따져 물었다.\n\n인심을 잃은 그 신사는 차분하게 둘러보고는, 니콜라스를 향해 말했다.\n\n\"방금 여기서 무슨 일이냐고 물으셨죠. 일은 간단합니다. 저기 저 사람이 말입니다, 제가 자기 전에 삼십 분쯤 커피룸에 앉아 있었는데 — 막 여행에서 돌아온 참이라, 내일까지는 아무도 저를 기다리지 않는 집으로 이 시간에 가느니 오늘 밤은 여기 묵는 게 낫겠다 싶었거든요 — 어떤 친구랑 술을 마시면서, 한 젊은 아가씨에 대해 아주 무례하고 건방지게 허물없는 말투로 떠들기 시작했습니다. 그 묘사며 다른 정황으로 보아 저는 그 아가씨가 누군지 알아봤고, 또 그 아가씨를 아는 영광을 누리고 있는 사람입니다. 그가 같이 있던 다른 손님들한테까지 들릴 만큼 큰 소리로 떠들기에, 저는 아주 정중하게 그 추측이 — 듣기에 거북한 추측이었지요 — 틀렸다고 일러 주고 그만두라고 청했습니다. 그가 잠깐은 그러더니, 방을 나가면서 또 아까보다 더 거북한 투로 그 얘기를 다시 꺼내지 뭡니까. 그래서 저는 그 뒤를 쫓아가 발길질로 떠나는 걸 도와주지 않을 수 없었고, 그래서 방금 보신 그 꼴이 된 겁니다. 제 일은 제가 제일 잘 안다고 봅니다.\" 아직 방금 전의 흥분이 완전히 가시지는 않은 젊은이가 말했다. \"여기 누구든 이 싸움을 자기 일로 삼고 싶은 분이 있다면, 저는 눈곱만큼도 반대하지 않습니다, 확실히 말씀드리지요.\"\n\n이렇게 자세히 들은 정황에서 취할 수 있는 온갖 행동 가운데, 그때의 니콜라스 마음 상태로는 이보다 더 칭찬할 만한 것은 분명 하나도 없었다. 그 순간 그의 가슴에 이보다 더 절절히 와닿을 다툼거리도 별로 없었다. 그 이름 모를 아가씨가 마음속에 늘 으뜸으로 자리하고 있었으니, 만약 무엄한 수다쟁이가 감히 자기 듣는 데서 그녀를 가볍게 입에 올렸다면 자기도 똑같이 했으리라는 생각이 절로 떠오른 것이다. 이런 마음에 이끌려 니콜라스는 그 젊은 신사의 싸움을 아주 뜨겁게 편들며, 잘했다고, 그래서 그를 존경한다고 힘주어 말했다. 그러자 존 브라우디도 — 비록 시비의 옳고 그름은 잘 가늠하지 못했지만 — 그에 못지않은 기세로 곧장 맞장구를 쳤다.\n\n\"조심하는 게 좋을 거야, 그게 다야.\" 패한 쪽이 말했다. 그는 방금 먼지투성이 바닥에 나동그라진 뒤 종업원한테 옷을 털리는 중이었다. \"공연히 나를 두들겨 패고 그냥 넘어갈 줄 알아, 두고 보라지. 사내가 예쁜 처녀 하나 칭찬했다고 박살이 나야 한다면, 세상 참 꼴좋겠군!\"\n\n이 말은 술집 카운터 안의 젊은 아가씨한테 크게 와닿은 모양이었다. 그녀는 (말하면서 모자를 매만지고 거울을 흘끔거리며) 정말이지 세상 꼴 한번 좋겠다고 했다. 그렇게 순진하고 자연스러운 행동으로 벌을 받아야 한다면, 때려눕히는 사람보다 얻어맞을 사람이 더 많아질 거라고, 대체 저 신사 양반은 무슨 속셈으로 그러는지 모르겠다고, 정말 모르겠다고 했다.\n\n\"이봐요, 아가씨.\" 젊은 신사가 미닫이창 쪽으로 다가서며 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"별말씀을요, 손님!\" 젊은 아가씨가 쌀쌀맞게 받았다. 그러면서도 고개를 옆으로 돌리며 미소 짓고 입술을 깨물었다. (이걸 보고, 아직 계단에 서 있던 브라우디 부인이 경멸하듯 그녀를 흘겨보고는 남편더러 이리 오라고 불렀다.)\n\n\"아니, 그래도 제 말 좀 들어 봐요.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"예쁜 얼굴에 반하는 게 죄라면, 저야말로 세상에서 제일 가망 없는 사람일 겁니다. 도무지 그걸 못 참거든요. 그게 저한테는 아주 기막힌 효력을 발휘해서, 제가 아무리 사납고 고집스러운 기분일 때도 저를 다잡고 다스려 줍니다. 아가씨 얼굴이 벌써 저한테 어떤 효력을 냈는지 보세요.\"\n\n\"어머, 말씀 참 예쁘게 하시네요.\" 젊은 아가씨가 고개를 살짝 젖히며 받았다. \"하지만—\"\n\n\"그럼요, 예쁜 줄 압니다.\" 젊은이가 감탄하는 표정으로 술집 아가씨의 얼굴을 바라보며 말했다. \"방금 그렇게 말했잖아요, 알다시피. 하지만 아름다움이란 존중하는 마음으로 말해야지요 — 존중하는 마음으로, 알맞은 말로, 그 가치와 빼어남에 걸맞은 마음가짐으로 말입니다. 그런데 저 작자는 그런 생각이라곤 손톱만큼도—\"\n\n이 대목에서 젊은 아가씨가 술집 창밖으로 머리를 쑥 내밀어 대화를 끊고는, 종업원에게 날카로운 목소리로 물었다. 얻어맞고 나동그라졌던 저 젊은이가 밤새 복도에 서 있을 작정이냐고, 아니면 다른 사람들 드나들게 입구를 좀 비워 둘 거냐고. 종업원들이 그 눈치를 채고 마부들한테도 일러 주자, 그들도 재빨리 태도를 바꾸었다. 그 결과 불운한 희생자는 눈 깜짝할 새에 밖으로 떠밀려 나갔다.\n\n\"분명 저 친구를 전에 본 적이 있는데.\" 니콜라스가 말했다.\n\n\"그래요?\" 새로 사귄 사람이 받았다.\n\n\"틀림없어요.\" 니콜라스가 곰곰이 생각하며 말을 멈췄다. \"어디서 봤더라— 가만— 그래, 맞아— 시내 서쪽 끝에 있는 직업 소개소 사람이에요. 얼굴이 기억난다 했지.\"\n\n그건 정말이지 못생긴 사무원, 톰이었다.\n\n\"거 참 묘하군!\" 니콜라스가 중얼거렸다. 그 직업 소개소가 가끔, 그것도 전혀 생각지도 못한 순간에 불쑥불쑥 튀어나와 자기 얼굴을 빤히 들여다보는 그 묘한 방식을 곱씹으면서 말이다.\n\n\"제 일이 변호인을 가장 필요로 할 때 그렇게 친절히 편들어 주셔서 정말 고맙습니다.\" 젊은이가 웃으며 호주머니에서 명함 한 장을 꺼냈다. \"어디로 가면 감사 인사를 드릴 수 있는지 알려 주시면 고맙겠습니다.\"\n\n니콜라스는 명함을 받아 들고, 답례 인사를 하며 무심코 거기에 눈길을 던졌다가 깜짝 놀랐다.\n\n\"프랭크 치어리블 씨!\" 니콜라스가 말했다. \"설마 내일 오기로 한 치어리블 형제 상회의 조카분은 아니시겠죠!\"\n\n\"보통 저를 그 상회의 조카라고는 부르지 않습니다만,\" 프랭크 씨가 사람 좋게 받았다. \"그 상회를 이루고 있는 두 훌륭한 분의 조카라는 건, 그래요, 자랑스럽게 말씀드릴 수 있습니다. 그리고 보아하니 당신은 제가 그토록 많이 들어 온 니클비 씨로군요! 정말 뜻밖의 만남입니다만, 그래서 더더욱 반갑습니다, 정말이에요.\"\n\n니콜라스도 같은 식의 인사로 화답했고, 두 사람은 따뜻하게 악수를 나누었다. 그러고 나서 니콜라스가 존 브라우디를 소개했다. 존은 술집 카운터의 젊은 아가씨가 그렇게 솜씨 좋게 옳은 편으로 넘어온 이후로 줄곧 감탄에 빠져 있던 참이었다. 그다음에 존 브라우디 부인을 소개했고, 마침내 다 함께 위층으로 올라가 다음 삼십 분을 더없이 흡족하게, 서로 즐겁게 어울리며 보냈다. 존 브라우디 부인은 자기가 여태 본 온갖 꾸민 것들 가운데 아래층의 그 젊은 여자가 제일 허영스럽고 제일 못났더라는 말로 이야기를 열었다.\n\n이 프랭크 치어리블 씨는, 방금 벌어진 일로 미루어 보면 다혈질의 젊은이였지만 (이게 자연에서 무슨 절대적인 기적이나 진기한 현상은 아니다), 발랄하고 사람 좋고 유쾌한 친구였다. 얼굴 생김새며 성품에 그 마음씨 고운 형제를 니콜라스에게 아주 강하게 떠올리게 하는 데가 많았다. 그의 태도는 그들 못지않게 꾸밈없었고, 거동에는 그 진심 어린 다정함이 가득했는데, 이는 마음속에 너그러운 구석이 조금이라도 있는 사람이라면 누구나 유난히 호감을 갖게 되는 그런 것이었다. 거기다 그는 잘생기고 영리했으며, 활기가 넘쳐 났고, 더없이 명랑했으며, 단 오 분 만에 존 브라우디의 온갖 별난 구석에 마치 어릴 적부터 알고 지낸 사이인 양 손쉽게 맞춰 주었다. 그러니 밤이 되어 헤어질 무렵, 그가 저 훌륭한 요크셔 사람과 그 아내뿐 아니라 니콜라스에게까지 더없이 좋은 인상을 남긴 것도 그리 놀랄 일은 아니었다. 니콜라스는 집으로 부지런히 걸어가며 이 모든 일을 마음속으로 곱씹은 끝에, 자기가 더없이 유쾌하고 바람직한 교제의 첫걸음을 떼었다는 결론에 이르렀다.\n\n\"그런데 그 직업 소개소 작자 일은 참 기막힌 노릇이야!\" 니콜라스는 생각했다. \"이 조카가 그 아름다운 아가씨에 대해 뭔가 알고 있을 가능성이 있을까? 며칠 전 팀 링컨워터가 이 사람이 여기 사업에 한몫 끼러 온다고 일러 줄 때, 사 년 동안 독일에서 사업을 관리했고, 지난 여섯 달 동안은 잉글랜드 북부에 대리점을 세우는 일을 맡고 있었다고 했지. 그러면 사 년 반— 사 년 반이야. 그 아가씨가 열일곱 살을 넘지는 않았을 거야— 많아야 열여덟이라고 치자. 그러면 그가 떠날 땐 아가씨가 아직 어린애였겠지. 내가 보기엔 그는 아가씨에 대해 아무것도 모르고 본 적도 없을 테니, 나한테 알려 줄 게 없을 거야. 어쨌든,\" 니콜라스는 자기 마음속 진짜 핵심에 이르러 생각했다. \"그쪽에서 그녀의 마음을 먼저 차지했을 위험은 없는 거지. 그건 아주 분명해.\"\n\n이기심이란 사랑이라 불리는 그 열정의 성분에 꼭 들어가야 하는 것일까, 아니면 사랑은 시인들이 의심할 바 없는 그들의 소명을 다하며 갖다 붙인 그 온갖 아름다운 말을 다 누릴 자격이 있는 것일까? 분명, 더없이 고결한 정황에서 신사들이 훌륭한 연적에게 숙녀를 양보하고 숙녀들이 신사를 양보한 진짜 사례들이 있긴 하다. 하지만 그런 숙녀와 신사 대다수가 어쩔 수 없는 일을 미덕으로 둔갑시켜, 어차피 자기 손에 닿지 않는 것을 고결하게 단념한 게 아니라고 정말 확실히 단정할 수 있을까? 사병이 결코 가터 훈장을 받지 않겠노라 맹세하는 것처럼, 또는 신앙심 깊고 학식 높지만 집안이라곤 — 그저 자식들만 잔뜩 거느린 큰 식구 말고는 — 변변찮은 가난한 부목사가 주교 자리를 마다하는 것처럼 말이다.\n\n여기 니콜라스 니클비를 보라. 그는 조카가 돌아온 지금 치어리블 형제의 총애나 재산에서 자기 처지가 나아질 가능성을 따져 본다는 생각 따위는 경멸했을 사람이건만, 어느새 그 조카가 이름 모를 그 미인의 마음을 두고 자기와 겨룰 만한지 깊이 계산하고 있었다. 그것도 그 한 가지만 빼면 다 정해진 일인 양 진지하게 혼자 그 문제를 따져 보고, 그 주제로 거듭거듭 되돌아가면서, 평생 단 한마디도 나눠 본 적 없는 사람한테 다른 누군가가 구애한다는 생각에 잔뜩 분개하며 부당한 일을 당한 기분까지 느꼈다. 사실 그는 새로 사귄 사람의 장점을 깎아내리기는커녕 오히려 부풀렸다. 그러면서도 그 사람한테 장점이라는 게 조금이라도 있다는 것을 — 그러니까 이 특정한 젊은 아가씨의 눈에 그렇다는 것을 — 일종의 개인적인 모욕으로 받아들였다. 다른 데서야 그가 원하는 만큼 장점을 얼마든지 가져도 좋았지만 말이다. 이 모든 데에 의심할 바 없는 이기심이 있었다. 그러나 니콜라스는 더없이 자유롭고 너그러운 천성을 지닌 사람이었고, 아마 어느 누구 못지않게 천하거나 더러운 생각이 적은 사람이었다. 그러니 사랑에 빠진 그가 같은 숭고한 처지에 놓인 다른 사람들과 다르게 느끼고 생각했으리라고 볼 까닭은 없다.\n\n하지만 그는 멈춰 서서 자기 생각의 갈피나 감정의 상태를 따져 보지는 않았다. 그저 집으로 가는 내내 그 생각에 잠겨 있었고, 밤새 같은 가락으로 꿈꾸듯 이어 갔다. 프랭크 치어리블이 그 수수께끼 같은 아가씨에 대해 알 리도, 아는 사이일 리도 없다고 스스로 납득하고 나니, 이번엔 자기마저 그녀를 영영 다시 못 볼지도 모른다는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 그 가정 위에 그는 아주 교묘하게 줄을 잇는 괴로운 상념들을 쌓아 올렸는데, 그것들은 프랭크 치어리블 씨라는 환영보다 오히려 그 목적에 더 잘 들어맞아, 깨어 있을 때나 잠들었을 때나 그를 애태우고 들볶았다.\n\n그와 반대로 아무리 많은 말과 노래가 있었다 한들, 아침이 그저 어떤 죄 없는 연인에게 심술을 부려 흡족함을 누리겠다고 한 시간쯤 그 다가옴을 늦추거나 앞당긴 적이 있다는, 제대로 입증된 사례는 하나도 없다. 옛 기록서들이 전하는 바에 따르면, 해는 자기 공적인 임무를 다하느라 어김없이 책력대로 떠올랐고, 결코 어떤 사사로운 고려에 휘둘리는 법이 없었다. 그리하여 아침은 여느 때처럼 찾아왔고, 그와 함께 업무 시간이, 그와 함께 프랭크 치어리블 씨가, 그와 함께 그 훌륭한 형제의 길게 이어지는 미소와 환대가, 그리고 티모시 링컨워터 씨의 좀 더 진중하고 사무원다운, 그러면서도 그에 못지않게 진심 어린 맞이가 따라왔다.\n\n\"프랭크 도련님하고 니클비 씨가 어젯밤에 만났다는 게 말이오,\" 팀 링컨워터가 천천히 걸상에서 내려와, 뭔가 아주 특별한 할 말이 있을 때면 으레 그러듯 책상에 등을 딱 붙이고 서서 회계실을 둘러보며 말했다. \"그 두 젊은이가 어젯밤 그런 식으로 만났다니, 이건 말이오, 우연이오, 놀라운 우연이란 말이지. 아니, 내 단언하건대,\" 팀이 안경을 벗고 잔잔한 자부심에 차서 미소 지으며 덧붙였다. \"온 세상에 런던만큼 우연이 잘 일어나는 곳은 없을 거요!\"\n\n\"글쎄, 그건 모르겠는데요.\" 프랭크 씨가 말했다. \"하지만—\"\n\n\"모르겠다니요, 프랜시스 도련님!\" 팀이 고집스러운 기색으로 말을 끊었다. \"그래, 어디 좀 압시다. 그런 일에 런던보다 나은 데가 있으면, 그게 어디요? 유럽에 있소? 아니, 없지. 아시아에 있소? 그야 당연히 없고말고. 아프리카에 있소? 어림도 없지. 아메리카에 있소? 그건 도련님이 더 잘 아실 테고요. 자, 그럼,\" 팀이 단호하게 팔짱을 끼며 말했다. \"대체 어디 있단 말이오?\"\n\n\"그 점을 따지려던 게 아니었어요, 팀.\" 젊은 치어리블이 웃으며 말했다. \"저도 그 정도로 이단은 아니랍니다. 제가 하려던 말은 그저, 제가 그 우연에 신세를 졌다고 여긴다는 것뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"오! 따지려는 게 아니라면,\" 팀이 아주 흡족해하며 말했다. \"그건 또 다른 얘기지요. 그래도 한마디 합시다. 도련님이 따졌더라면 좋았을 텐데. 도련님이든 누구든 따져 줬으면 좋겠단 말이오. 내가 그 사람을 아주 코를 납작하게 만들어 줄 텐데,\" 팀이 안경으로 왼손 집게손가락을 힘주어 톡톡 두드리며 말했다. \"논리로 그 사람을 아주 코를 납작하게—\"\n\n그렇게 무모한 작자가 팀 링컨워터와의 날 선 맞대결에서 얼마나 정신적으로 무너져 내릴지를 표현할 말이라곤 도무지 찾을 수가 없었다. 그래서 팀은 그저 말이 모자란 탓에 선언의 나머지를 포기하고는 다시 걸상에 올라앉았다.\n\n\"에드 동생, 우리는,\" 찰스가 팀 링컨워터의 등을 칭찬하듯 토닥인 뒤에 말했다. \"조카 프랭크하고 니클비 씨 같은 두 젊은이를 곁에 두었으니 정말 운이 좋다고 여겨도 되겠구나. 이건 우리한테 크나큰 만족과 기쁨의 샘이 되어야 마땅하지.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 찰스 형님, 그럼요.\" 다른 한 사람이 받았다.\n\n\"팀에 대해선,\" 에드 동생이 덧붙였다. \"내 아무 말도 하지 않겠소. 팀은 그저 어린애니까— 갓난애지— 우리가 도무지 생각도 안 하고 셈에도 안 넣는 아무것도 아닌 사람이거든. 팀, 이 악당아, 거기 대해 뭐라고 할 텐가, 응?\"\n\n\"전 두 사람 다 질투가 나서요,\" 팀이 말했다. \"다른 일자리를 알아볼 작정입니다. 그러니 두 분께서도 알아서 사람을 구해 두시지요, 괜찮으시다면요.\"\n\n팀은 이게 어찌나 절묘하고 둘도 없고 더없이 기막힌 농담이라고 여겼던지, 펜을 잉크병 위에 내려놓고는 여느 때처럼 신중하게 내려오는 게 아니라 걸상에서 거의 굴러떨어지다시피 하며, 기진맥진할 때까지 웃어 댔다. 그러면서 줄곧 고개를 흔들어 대니 머릿가루 부스러기가 사무실 안에 눈에 띄게 풀풀 날렸다. 형제도 결코 뒤지지 않았으니, 자기들과 늙은 팀이 자진해서 헤어진다는 그 우스꽝스러운 발상에 거의 그만큼이나 진심으로 웃어 댔다. 니콜라스와 프랭크 씨도 아주 떠들썩하게 웃었는데, 아마 이 작은 소동이 불러일으킨 다른 어떤 감정을 감추려는 것이었으리라 (사실 세 노인도 처음 한바탕 웃고 난 뒤에는 그랬다). 그러니 어쩌면 그 웃음 속에는, 어느 한 사람을 겨냥해 던진 더없이 신랄한 재담에서 아무리 점잖은 모임이 끌어냈을 즐거움 못지않은 날카로운 즐거움과 흥취가 통틀어 깃들어 있었는지도 모른다.\n\n\"니클비 씨,\" 찰스 형이 그를 한쪽으로 부르더니 다정하게 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"내가— 내가 말이오, 니클비 씨, 자네가 그 오두막에 제대로 편안히 자리 잡았는지 보고 싶어 안달이라네. 우리를 위해 그렇게 잘 일해 주는 사람이, 우리 힘으로 없애 줄 수 있는 어떤 궁핍이나 불편을 겪게 둘 수야 없지. 그리고 자네 어머님과 누이도 뵙고 싶고, 그분들과 알고 지내고 싶다네, 니클비 씨. 또 우리가 그분들께 해 드린 자그마한 도움이라는 게 자네가 보여 주는 열의와 열성으로 차고 넘치게 되갚아졌다고 안심시켜, 그분들 마음을 덜어 드릴 기회도 갖고 싶고 말일세. — 한마디도 마시게, 니클비 씨, 부탁이네. 내일이 일요일이지. 내 염치 불고하고 차 마실 시간에 나가 보겠네. 그래서 자네가 집에 있나 한번 보지. 만약 자네가 없거나, 아니면 부인들께서 불쑥 찾아오는 게 거북해서 지금은 나한테 알려지고 싶지 않으시다면, 그땐 또 다른 날에 오면 되니까. 나야 아무 때라도 괜찮다네. 그렇게 알고 있기로 하지. 에드 동생, 이보게, 이쪽으로 와서 잠깐 얘기 좀 하세.\"\n\n쌍둥이 형제는 팔짱을 끼고 사무실을 나섰다. 니콜라스는 이 친절한 처사와, 그날 아침 자기가 받은 다른 여러 친절에서, 그저 조카가 돌아온 마당에 형제가 자기 없는 자리에서 해 주었던 그 다정한 약속을 곱게 새로이 되새겨 주는 것일 뿐이라고 여겼다. 그러니 이렇게 유별난 배려에 아무리 감탄하고 고마워해도 모자랄 지경이었다.\n\n다음 날 손님이, 그것도 그런 손님이 온다는 소식은 니클비 부인의 가슴속에 환희와 아쉬움이 뒤섞인 감정을 불러일으켰다. 한편으로는 자기가 곧 좋은 사교계로, 또 거의 잊고 지내던 아침 방문과 저녁 차 모임의 즐거움으로 되돌아갈 징조라며 반겼지만, 다른 한편으로는 쓰라린 마음으로 떠올리지 않을 수 없었던 것이다. 뚜껑에 상아 손잡이가 달린 은제 찻주전자와 그에 어울리는 우유 단지가 이제 없다는 사실을. 그것들은 옛 시절 그녀의 자랑거리였고, 한 해가 다 가도록 무두질한 가죽에 싸여 어떤 맨 위 선반에 모셔져 있었는데, 그 선반이 이제 슬픔에 잠긴 그녀의 상상 속에 또렷한 빛깔로 떠올랐다.\n\n\"그 양념통은 누구 손에 들어갔을까.\" 니클비 부인이 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"왼쪽 구석에, 절인 양파에서 두 칸 옆에 놓여 있었지. 그 양념통 기억나니, 케이트?\"\n\n\"아주 잘 기억나요, 엄마.\"\n\n\"넌 기억 안 날 것 같은데, 케이트.\" 니클비 부인이 매서운 투로 받았다. \"그렇게 차갑고 무덤덤하게 말하는 걸 보니! 이런 잃어버린 것들 때문에 정작 그 물건을 잃은 것보다 더 속이 상하는 게 한 가지 있다면, 내 똑똑히 따지고 단언하건대,\" 니클비 부인이 격앙된 기색으로 코를 문지르며 말했다. \"그건 바로 곁에 있는 사람들이 매사를 이렇게 부아가 치밀 만큼 태평하게 받아들이는 거란다.\"\n\n\"엄마,\" 케이트가 슬며시 팔을 어머니 목에 두르며 말했다. \"엄마가 진심으로 그렇게 생각하지도, 그러려는 것도 아닌 줄 빤히 아는데 왜 그런 말씀을 하세요? 또 제가 행복하고 만족해한다고 왜 화를 내세요? 저한텐 엄마와 니콜라스 오빠가 남아 있고, 우리가 다시 함께 있게 되었잖아요. 그러니 평소에 아쉬울 일도 없는 자질구레한 물건 몇 개에 제가 무슨 미련을 두겠어요? 죽음이 가져올 수 있는 온갖 비참함과 황폐함을 다 겪어 보고, 사람들 틈에서도 외톨이로 홀로인 그 쓸쓸함을 알아 보고, 또 서로의 위로와 의지가 가장 절실하던 때에 슬픔과 가난 속에서 헤어져야 했던 그 모든 고통을 겪고 나서, 제가 이곳을 이토록 달콤한 고요와 안식의 자리로 여기는 게, 엄마가 곁에 있으니 더 바랄 것도 후회할 것도 없다고 여기는 게 그렇게 이상한가요? 한때, 그것도 그리 오래지 않은 때엔, 옛집의 그 온갖 안락함이 정말 자꾸자꾸 제게 되살아났어요, 솔직히요— 엄마가 생각하시는 것보다 더 자주요. 하지만 저는 아무렇지 않은 척했어요. 그러면 엄마가 그것들을 덜 아쉬워하시게 될까 싶어서요. 정말이지 저도 무덤덤하진 않았어요. 차라리 무덤덤했다면 더 행복했을지도 모르죠. 사랑하는 엄마,\" 케이트가 몹시 격해져서 말했다. \"저는 이 집과, 우리 모두가 그토록 오랜 세월 행복했던 그 집 사이에 아무런 차이도 모르겠어요. 다만 이 세상에서 아파 본 그 어떤 마음보다도 가장 다정하고 가장 따스했던 그 마음이 평안히 천국으로 가셨다는 것만 빼고요.\"\n\n\"케이트, 우리 케이트.\" 니클비 부인이 딸을 품에 끌어안으며 외쳤다.\n\n\"전 너무 자주 생각했어요,\" 케이트가 흐느꼈다. \"아빠의 그 다정한 말들을 전부요— 아빠가 침실로 올라가시면서 마지막으로 제 작은 방을 들여다보고 '잘 자거라, 우리 딸' 하고 말씀하시던 그 마지막 순간을요. 아빠 얼굴이 창백했어요, 엄마— 그 무너진 가슴이— 그런 거였어요— 그때는— 그런 줄 몰랐지만요—\"\n\n울음이 왈칵 터져 나와 케이트의 마음을 풀어 주었고, 케이트는 어머니의 가슴에 머리를 묻고 어린아이처럼 흐느껴 울었다.\n\n우리 본성에는 더없이 절묘하고 아름다운 점이 있으니, 어떤 잔잔한 행복이나 애틋한 정에 마음이 닿아 누그러질 때면, 세상을 떠난 이들에 대한 기억이 더없이 강하게, 거역할 수 없이 밀려든다는 것이다. 마치 우리의 더 선한 생각과 마음씀이 일종의 주문이라서, 그 힘으로 영혼이 우리가 살아생전 애틋이 사랑했던 이들의 혼령과 어떤 어렴풋하고 신비로운 교감을 나눌 수 있게 되는 것만 같다. 아아, 그 참을성 있는 천사들은 얼마나 자주, 또 얼마나 오래도록 우리 머리 위를 맴돌며, 그토록 좀처럼 읊어지지 않고 그토록 금세 잊히고 마는 그 주문을 기다리고 있을 것인가!\n\n가엾은 니클비 부인은 마음속에 떠오르는 것이라면 무엇이든 곧장 입 밖에 내는 데 익숙한 사람이라, 딸이 이런 생각들을 가슴속에 남몰래 품고 있을 수 있으리라곤 도무지 헤아려 본 적이 없었다. 더구나 무슨 모진 시련이나 푸념 섞인 원망으로 그런 속내가 케이트의 입에서 새어 나온 적도 없었기에 더욱 그랬다. 그런데 이제 니콜라스가 방금 들려준 모든 일의 행복과, 그들의 새롭고 평화로운 삶이, 그 기억들을 케이트에게 어찌나 강하게 불러일으켰던지 도저히 억누를 수가 없게 되자, 니클비 부인은 자기가 이따금 좀 생각이 모자랐구나 하는 깨달음이 어렴풋이 들기 시작했다. 그리고 딸을 끌어안고, 그런 대화가 자연히 일깨우는 감정에 자신을 내맡기며, 자책 비슷한 무언가를 느꼈다.\n\n그날 밤엔 굉장한 부산을 떨었고, 오기로 한 손님을 맞을 채비가 엄청났다. 근처 화원에서 아주 커다란 꽃다발을 가져와, 그걸 자잘한 꽃다발 여러 개로 잘라 나누었다. 니클비 부인은 그것으로 작은 거실을 꾸미려 했는데, 그 솜씨라면 분명 누구의 눈길이든 끌고도 남았을 게 틀림없다. 만약 케이트가 그 수고를 덜어 드리겠다고 나서서, 더없이 예쁘고 정갈하게 손수 꽂아 두지 않았다면 말이다. 오두막이 예뻐 보인 적이 있다면, 그건 다음 날처럼 그렇게 환하고 햇살 가득한 날이었으리라. 하지만 정원을 향한 스마이크의 자랑이나, 가구 상태를 향한 니클비 부인의 자랑이나, 매사를 향한 케이트의 자랑도, 케이트 자신을 바라보는 니콜라스의 자랑에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다. 그리고 분명, 잉글랜드에서 가장 값비싼 저택이라 한들, 그녀의 아름다운 얼굴과 우아한 자태에서 더없이 빼어나고 견줄 데 없는 장식을 찾았을 것이다.\n\n오후 여섯 시쯤, 니클비 부인은 오래 기다리던 문 두드리는 소리에 잔뜩 마음이 들떴다. 게다가 복도에서 부츠 두 켤레의 또렷한 발소리가 들려오니 그 들뜸은 도무지 가라앉질 않았다. 니클비 부인은 숨죽인 채, 저게 분명 '치어리블 형제 두 분'일 거라고 점쳤다. 정말 그렇긴 했지만, 니클비 부인이 기다리던 그 두 사람은 아니었다. 찰스 치어리블 씨와 그의 조카 프랭크 씨였으니까. 프랭크 씨는 불쑥 찾아온 데 대해 수없이 사과했고, 니클비 부인은 (다 같이 쓰고도 남을 만큼 찻숟가락이 넉넉했으므로) 더없이 우아하게 그를 맞았다. 이 뜻밖의 손님이 나타났다고 해서 (케이트만 빼고, 그것도 처음에 얼굴이 한두 번 붉어지는 정도였지만) 조금도 어색해지지 않았다. 늙은 신사가 어찌나 친절하고 다정한지, 또 젊은 신사가 그 점을 어찌나 잘 흉내 내는지, 첫 만남에 으레 따르는 그 뻣뻣함과 격식이 나타날 기미조차 보이지 않아서, 케이트는 정말이지 한 번 이상이나, 대체 그게 언제 시작되려나 하고 궁금해하고 있는 자기 자신을 알아채곤 했다.\n\n차 마시는 자리에선 아주 갖가지 주제로 이야기가 넘쳐 났고, 그런대로 우스갯거리 화젯거리도 빠지지 않았다. 젊은 치어리블 씨가 최근 독일에 머물렀던 일이 화제에 오르자, 늙은 치어리블 씨가 일동에게 일러 주기를, 앞서 말한 젊은 치어리블 씨가 어느 독일 시장님의 딸과 깊은 사랑에 빠진 게 아닌가 하는 의심을 산다는 것이었다. 이 혐의를 젊은 치어리블 씨가 더없이 분개하며 물리치자, 니클비 부인이 슬쩍 한마디 했다. 부인하는 그 열기로 미루어 보아 뭔가 있는 게 분명하다는 의심이 든다는 거였다. 그러자 젊은 치어리블 씨가 늙은 치어리블 씨에게, 다 농담이었다고 고백해 달라고 간곡히 졸랐다. 늙은 치어리블 씨가 마침내 그렇게 했는데, 젊은 치어리블 씨가 어찌나 진지하게 매달렸던지, 니클비 부인이 나중에 그 장면을 떠올리며 수천 번도 더 말했듯이, 그는 '얼굴이 아주 빨개졌다'. 부인은 이를 두고두고 새겨 둘 만하고 언급할 가치가 있는 사건으로 여겼는데, 젊은이들이란 본래 부류로 보아 겸손함이나 자제력으로 이름난 사람들이 아니거니와, 특히 거기 숙녀가 끼어 있을 때는 더더욱 그러하여, 얼굴을 붉히는 일이 있다 해도 대개는 자기가 붉어지기보다 이야기를 부풀려 그럴싸하게 꾸미기 일쑤이기 때문이다.\n\n차를 마신 뒤엔 정원을 거닐었고, 저녁이 무척 좋았던지라 정원 문을 나서서 몇몇 좁은 길과 샛길로 빠져나가, 아주 어두워질 때까지 이리저리 한가로이 거닐었다. 일행 모두에게 시간이 무척 빨리 흐르는 듯했다. 케이트가 오빠의 팔에 기대어 앞장서서, 오빠와 프랭크 치어리블 씨와 이야기를 나누며 걸었다. 그리고 니클비 부인과 나이 든 신사가 조금 뒤떨어져 따라갔다. 그 마음씨 좋은 상인의 친절함과, 니콜라스의 앞날에 대한 그의 관심과, 케이트를 향한 그의 감탄이 이 착한 부인의 마음에 어찌나 작용했던지, 평소 거침없이 흐르던 그녀의 말이 아주 좁고 옹색한 테두리 안에 갇혀 버렸다. 스마이크는 (평생 한 번이라도 관심의 대상이 된 적이 있다면, 바로 그날이 그런 날이었다) 그들과 함께하며, 어떤 때는 이쪽 무리에, 어떤 때는 저쪽 무리에 끼었다. 찰스 형이 그의 어깨에 손을 얹고 같이 걷자고 하기도 했고, 니콜라스가 미소 띤 얼굴로 돌아보며, 이리 와서 그를 가장 잘 이해하는 옛 친구와 이야기하자고 손짓하기도 했기 때문이다. 그 옛 친구는, 다른 누구도 못 할 때 그 시름에 찌든 얼굴에 미소를 끌어낼 줄 아는 사람이었다.\n\n자만은 일곱 가지 큰 죄 가운데 하나다. 하지만 그것이 자식을 향한 어머니의 자랑일 수는 없으니, 그것은 두 가지 으뜸가는 미덕, 곧 믿음과 희망이 어우러진 것이기 때문이다. 그날 밤 니클비 부인의 가슴을 벅차오르게 한 것이 바로 이 자랑이었다. 그리고 집으로 돌아왔을 때 그녀의 얼굴에, 불빛에 반짝이며 남아 있던 것도 바로 이것이었으니, 그것은 그녀가 여태껏 흘려 본 가장 고마운 눈물의 흔적이었다.\n\n조촐한 저녁 식사 자리엔 잔잔한 흥겨움이 감돌았는데, 이 감정의 결과 정확히 어우러졌다. 그러다 마침내 두 신사가 작별을 고했다. 그 작별 인사에는 한바탕 웃음과 농담을 자아낸 일이 한 가지 있었으니, 바로 프랭크 치어리블 씨가 케이트한테 손을 두 번이나 내밀었다는 것이다. 이미 그녀에게 작별 인사를 했다는 걸 까맣게 잊은 채 말이다. 늙은 치어리블 씨는 이를 두고, 그가 독일의 옛 연인을 생각하고 있다는 확실한 증거라고 했고, 그 농담에 엄청난 웃음이 터졌다. 가벼운 마음이란 이토록 쉽게 움직이는 법이다.\n\n요컨대, 그날은 맑고 고요한 행복의 하루였다. 그리고 우리 모두에게 — 바라건대 우리 가운데 많은 이에게는, 다른 여러 날들 틈에 섞여 — 유난한 기쁨으로 되돌아보는 어떤 환한 날이 있듯이, 이날도 그날을 함께한 이들의 달력 속에서 두드러진 자리를 차지한 날로, 그 뒤로 두고두고 되새겨졌다.\n\n단 하나 예외가 있었던가, 그것도 가장 행복해야 마땅했던 바로 그 사람이?\n\n자기 방의 고요 속에서 무릎을 꿇고, 첫 친구가 가르쳐 준 대로 기도를 올리려다가, 두 손을 모았다가 그것을 허공에 미친 듯이 내뻗고는, 쓰라린 슬픔의 격정에 휩싸여 얼굴을 땅에 묻고 엎드린 그 사람은 누구였던가?\n\n# CHAPTER 44\n\n세상에는 이런 사람들이 있어. 무슨 수를 써서든 자기 배만 불리겠다는 한 가지 목적으로 살아가면서, 그 목적을 위해 날마다 써먹는 수법이 얼마나 비열하고 못된 짓인지 자기들도 똑똑히 알고 있지. 그런데도 겉으로는 — 심지어 자기 자신한테까지 — 도덕적으로 아주 올바른 척하면서, 세상이 타락했다고 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 한숨을 쉰단 말이야. 이 땅 위를 걸어 다닌, 아니 걷는다는 건 적어도 똑바로 서서 사람답게 처신한다는 뜻이니까, 차라리 가장 더럽고 좁은 길로 평생을 기어 다닌 가장 교활한 악당들 가운데 몇몇은, 날마다 일기장에 그날그날의 일을 진지하게 적어 넣곤 하지. 그리고 하늘과 정식으로 빚지고 빚 받는 장부를 매기는데, 그 장부에는 늘 자기 쪽에 흑자가 떠 있어. 이게 그런 사람들의 거짓되고 속임수투성이인 삶에서 그나마 거저 하는 짓인지 — 거저 하는 유일한 짓 말이야 — 아니면 정말로 하늘마저 속여서, 이승에서 재물을 쌓게 해 준 바로 그 방법으로 저승에서도 보물을 쌓으려는 건지, 그건 따지지 않기로 하자. 어쨌든 그렇다는 거야. 그리고 그런 장부 매기기는 (세상을 일깨워 준 어떤 자서전들처럼) 한 가지 점에서는 분명 쓸모가 있을 거야. 기록하는 천사의 수고와 시간을 좀 덜어 줄 테니까.\n\n랄프 니클비는 그런 부류의 사람이 아니었어. 엄격하고, 고집스럽고, 끈질기고, 속을 도무지 알 수 없는 랄프는, 이승에서든 저승에서든 두 가지 욕망을 채우는 것 말고는 그 무엇에도 관심이 없었지. 하나는 탐욕이었어. 그의 천성에서 가장 먼저, 가장 크게 자리 잡은 욕구였지. 또 하나는 미움이었어. 그는 자기야말로 온 인류의 본보기일 뿐이라고 여기는 척하면서, 자기 본모습을 세상 사람들한테 굳이 감추려 들지도 않았어. 그리고 마음속으로는 못된 꿍꿍이가 떠오를 때마다 그걸 즐기고 아꼈지. 랄프 니클비가 글자 그대로 마음에 새긴 성경 말씀은 딱 하나, \"너 자신을 알라\"였어. 그는 자기 자신을 잘 알았어. 그러고는 온 인류가 다 자기와 같은 틀에서 찍혀 나왔다고 멋대로 생각하며 사람들을 미워했지. 사람은 누구도 자기 자신을 미워하진 않거든. 우리 가운데 가장 차가운 사람조차 그러기엔 자기애가 너무 크니까. 그런데도 대부분의 사람들은 저도 모르게 자기를 잣대 삼아 세상을 판단해. 그래서 흔히 보면, 입버릇처럼 인간 본성을 비웃고 깔보는 척하는 자들이야말로 그 본성의 가장 못나고 가장 정 안 가는 표본이란 말이지.\n\n그런데 지금 이 이야기에서 다룰 사람은 랄프 본인이야. 랄프는 잔뜩 찌푸린 얼굴로 뉴먼 노그스를 바라보고 서 있었어. 그러는 동안 그 충실한 사내는 손가락 부분이 없는 장갑을 벗더니, 왼손바닥에 조심스레 펼쳐 놓고는 오른손으로 주름을 펴며 꾹꾹 눌렀어. 그러고는 멍하니 그걸 돌돌 마는 거야. 마치 그 의식이 너무나 흥미로워서 다른 건 죄다 안중에 없다는 듯이 말이지.\n\n\"마을을 떠났다고!\" 랄프가 천천히 말했어. \"네가 잘못 안 거겠지. 다시 가 봐.\"\n\n\"잘못 안 거 아니에요.\" 뉴먼이 대꾸했어. \"떠날 참인 것도 아니고, 아예 떠나 버렸다고요.\"\n\n\"이놈이 계집애가 됐나, 아니면 갓난쟁이가 됐나?\" 랄프가 짜증 섞인 몸짓으로 중얼거렸어.\n\n\"그건 모르죠.\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"어쨌든 떠났어요.\"\n\n뉴먼 노그스는 \"떠났다\"는 말을 되풀이하는 게 이루 말할 수 없이 즐거운 모양이었어. 그 말이 랄프 니클비를 약 올리는 만큼 말이지. 뉴먼은 그 말에 묵직하게 힘을 잔뜩 실어서, 점잖게 봐줄 만한 한도까지 길게 끌었어. 그러고는 더 끌었다간 남이 눈치챌까 싶을 때쯤, 혼자 그 말을 숨죽여 되뇌며 서 있었지. 그것만으로도 만족스럽다는 듯이.\n\n\"그래서 어디로 갔는데?\" 랄프가 물었어.\n\n\"프랑스요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어. \"단독이 또 도질 위험이 있대요. 이번엔 더 심하게, 머리에요. 그래서 의사들이 떠나라고 했죠. 그래서 떠났어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 프레더릭 경은—?\" 랄프가 입을 뗐어.\n\n\"그분도 떠났어요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어.\n\n\"그래서 얻어터진 걸 고스란히 안고 가 버렸단 말이지?\" 랄프가 돌아서며 말했어. \"멍든 몸뚱이를 챙겨서, 말 한마디로 앙갚음하지도, 손톱만큼 보상을 받아 내려고도 하지 않고 슬그머니 꽁무니를 빼?\"\n\n\"너무 아프니까요.\" 뉴먼이 말했어.\n\n\"너무 아파!\" 랄프가 되받았어. \"나라면 죽어 가는 한이 있어도 받아 냈을 거다. 오히려 그럴 때일수록 더 악착같이, 그것도 당장 받아 냈겠지 — 내가 그 사람이라면 말이야. 그런데 너무 아프시다고! 가엾은 멀버리 경! 너무 아프시대!\"\n\n이 말을 더없는 경멸과 잔뜩 약 오른 태도로 내뱉고 나서, 랄프는 뉴먼한테 방에서 나가라고 황급히 손짓했어. 그러고는 의자에 털썩 몸을 던지더니, 초조하게 바닥을 발로 탁탁 굴렀지.\n\n\"그 녀석한테는 무슨 마법 같은 게 있어.\" 랄프가 이를 갈며 말했어. \"온갖 상황이 짜고서 그 녀석을 돕는단 말이야. 행운의 여신이 베푸는 은혜? 이런 악마 같은 운수에 비하면 돈 따위가 다 뭐야?\"\n\n랄프는 짜증스럽게 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣었어. 그런데 방금 그런 생각을 했는데도, 그 안에 무언가 위안거리가 있었던 모양이야. 얼굴이 조금 풀렸거든. 잔뜩 찌푸린 이마에는 여전히 깊은 주름이 잡혀 있었지만, 그건 실망이 아니라 셈을 굴리는 주름이었어.\n\n\"하지만 이 호크 녀석은 돌아올 거야.\" 랄프가 중얼거렸어. \"내가 그 사람을 안다면 — 이제쯤이면 알 만도 하지 — 그동안 그 분노는 조금도 사그라들지 않았을 테고. 어쩔 수 없이 틀어박혀 지내야 하는 데다, 그런 생활에 익숙한 사람한테 병실의 따분함이라니. 활개 치며 살지도 못하고, 술도 못 마시고, 노름도 못 하고, 좋아하고 그걸로 사는 게 죄다 막혀 버렸으니. 이 모든 일의 원인이 누군지를 잊을 위인이 아니지. 잊을 사람이야 드물겠지만, 하필 그 사람이? 아니, 아니고말고!\"\n\n랄프는 빙긋 웃고 고개를 저었어. 그러고는 턱을 손에 괴고 생각에 잠겼다가, 다시 웃었지. 한참 뒤에 자리에서 일어나 종을 울렸어.\n\n\"그 스퀴어스라는 자, 여기 왔었나?\" 랄프가 물었어.\n\n\"어젯밤에 왔었죠. 제가 집에 갈 때 여기 두고 갔는데요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어.\n\n\"그건 나도 알아, 이 멍청아. 모를까 봐?\" 랄프가 발끈해서 말했어. \"그 뒤에 또 왔냐고. 오늘 아침에 왔어?\"\n\n\"아뇨.\" 뉴먼이 아주 큰 소리로 외쳤어.\n\n\"내가 나가 있는 동안에 오거든 — 오늘 밤 아홉 시면 거의 틀림없이 올 텐데 — 기다리게 해. 그리고 같이 온 사람이 또 하나 있을 텐데, 아마—\" 랄프가 말을 끊었다가, \"그 사람도 기다리게 하고.\"\n\n\"둘 다 기다리게 하라고요?\" 뉴먼이 말했어.\n\n\"그래.\" 랄프가 성난 얼굴로 그를 홱 돌아보며 대답했어. \"이 짧은 외투나 입혀 줘. 그리고 까악거리는 앵무새처럼 내 말 따라 하지 마.\"\n\n\"제가 앵무새였으면 좋겠네요.\" 뉴먼이 뾰로통하게 말했어.\n\n\"나도 네가 앵무새였으면 좋겠다.\" 랄프가 외투를 걸치며 받아쳤어. \"그랬으면 진작에 네 모가지를 비틀어 버렸을 텐데.\"\n\n뉴먼은 이 칭찬에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았어. 다만 잠깐 랄프의 어깨너머를 들여다봤지. (그때 랄프는 외투 뒷깃을 매만지던 참이었어.) 마치 랄프의 코를 확 비틀어 주고 싶어서 안달이 난 듯이 말이야. 그런데 랄프와 눈이 마주치자, 뉴먼은 어슬렁대던 손가락을 얼른 거둬들이고는, 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 거칠게 자기 빨간 코를 문질렀어.\n\n이 별난 부하한테 위협하는 눈초리 한 번과, 조심해서 실수하지 말라는 당부 말고는 더 신경 쓰지 않은 채, 랄프는 모자와 장갑을 챙겨 들고 밖으로 나갔어.\n\n랄프는 아주 어마어마하고도 잡다한 인맥을 가진 듯했어. 그리고 그가 들르는 곳도 참 이상했지. 어떤 데는 으리으리하게 큰 부잣집이었고, 어떤 데는 자그마한 가난한 집이었어. 그래도 용건은 죄다 하나, 돈이었지. 좀 으스대는 고객들의 문지기나 하인들한테는 랄프의 얼굴이 부적이나 마찬가지였어. 그래서 그는 걸어서 터덜터덜 가는데도 척척 들여보내 줬지. 마차를 덜컹대며 문 앞까지 대고 온 다른 사람들은 거절당하는데도 말이야. 이런 데서 랄프는 더없이 부드럽고 굽실거리며 공손했어. 발걸음이 어찌나 가벼운지 두툼한 양탄자 위에서 거의 소리도 안 났고, 목소리가 어찌나 나직한지 상대방 말고는 들리지도 않을 정도였어. 그런데 가난한 집에서는 랄프가 딴사람이 됐지. 복도 마룻바닥에서 그의 구두가 삐걱대며 거침없이 들어섰고, 밀린 돈을 내놓으라고 다그칠 땐 목소리가 거칠고 컸어. 으름장도 험하고 성이 잔뜩 나 있었지. 또 다른 부류의 손님들한테 랄프는 또 딴사람이 됐어. 이들은 평판이 영 미심쩍은 변호사들이었지. 새 일거리를 물어다 주거나, 묵은 일에서 새 이익을 짜내 주는 자들이었어. 이런 자들한테 랄프는 스스럼없고 농담도 잘했어. 요즘 돌아가는 일에 익살을 떨었고, 특히 장사에 도움이 되는 파산이나 돈에 쪼들리는 사정 얘기가 나오면 신이 났지. 한마디로, 이렇게 갖가지로 변하는 모습만 봐서는 같은 사람이라고 알아보기 어려웠을 거야. 다만 두 가지는 한결같았지. 집집마다 주머니에서 어음과 차용증이 가득 든 두툼한 가죽 지갑을 꺼낸다는 것. 그리고 똑같은 푸념을 끊임없이 되풀이한다는 것이었어. (말투와 늘어놓는 방식만 그때그때 달랐지.) 세상은 자기를 부자로 알지만, 받을 걸 다 받았다면 모를까 그렇지도 않다는 거야. 일단 돈이 나가고 나면 원금이든 이자든 도무지 거둬들일 길이 없고, 살아가기가, 하루하루 입에 풀칠하기조차 여간 힘든 게 아니라는 거였지.\n\n이렇게 길게 이어진 방문이 (식당에서 변변찮은 저녁을 먹느라 한 번 끊긴 것 말고는) 핌리코에서 끝났을 때는 이미 저녁이었어. 랄프는 집으로 가는 길에 세인트제임스 공원을 따라 걸었지.\n\n랄프의 머릿속에는 깊은 꿍꿍이가 몇 가지 들어 있었어. 주름진 이마와 굳게 다문 입만 봐도 그건 넉넉히 알 만했지. 둘레의 것들에는 도통 무관심하다 못해 아예 의식도 못 하는 그 모습이 아니더라도 말이야. 어찌나 깊이 골똘했던지, 평소엔 누구 못지않게 눈썰미가 빠른 랄프가, 뒤를 밟는 비척대는 그림자를 알아채지 못했어. 그 그림자는 어떤 때는 발소리도 없이 랄프 뒤로 슬그머니 따라붙었고, 어떤 때는 그보다 몇 발짝 앞으로 살금살금 나아갔고, 또 어떤 때는 옆에 나란히 미끄러지듯 따라왔지. 그러면서 줄곧 어찌나 날카로운 눈으로, 어찌나 간절하고 빤히 살피는 표정으로 랄프를 쳐다봤는지, 그건 아무리 관심 많고 애타는 관찰자의 살핌이라기보다는, 무슨 강렬한 그림이나 또렷이 각인된 꿈속에 불쑥 끼어든 얼굴의 표정에 더 가까웠어.\n\n한동안 하늘이 잔뜩 내려앉아 어두컴컴하더니, 사나운 빗줄기가 쏟아지기 시작했어. 그 바람에 랄프는 나무 밑으로 비를 피했지. 그가 팔짱을 끼고 나무에 기대선 채 여전히 생각에 잠겨 있는데, 문득 눈을 들었다가, 나무줄기를 돌아 그의 얼굴을 샅샅이 들여다보는 한 사내의 눈과 딱 마주쳤어. 그 순간 이 고리대금업자의 표정에 무언가가 있었던 모양이야. 사내는 그걸 똑똑히 기억하는 눈치였고, 그 덕에 마음을 굳혔거든. 사내는 랄프에게 바짝 다가서더니 그의 이름을 불렀어.\n\n랄프는 순간 놀라서 두어 발짝 물러나며 그를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 훑어봤어. 마르고, 거무튀튀하고, 시들어 버린 사내였는데, 나이는 랄프와 엇비슷하고, 몸은 구부정했어. 게다가 아주 음흉한 얼굴이었지. 햇볕에 새카맣게 그을린 데다 홀쭉하고 굶주린 양 볼 때문에 더 험상궂어 보였어. 짙고 검은 눈썹은, 새하얗게 센 머리카락과 대비되어 더 새까매 보였지. 옷차림은 거칠고 후줄근했고, 모양새가 이상하고 어설펐어. 그리고 온몸에 뭐라 꼬집어 말하기 힘든 의기소침과 영락한 기색이 배어 있었지. 한순간엔 이게 그가 본 전부였어. 그런데 다시 들여다보니, 그 얼굴과 모습이 차츰 덜 낯설어지는 거야. 보는 사이에 자꾸 바뀌더니, 누그러지고 부드러워지면서 낯익은 윤곽으로 잡혔어. 그러다 마침내, 무슨 이상한 착시처럼, 여러 해 알고 지냈다가 그만큼이나 오래 잊고 잃어버렸던 어떤 사람의 얼굴로 또렷이 떠오른 거지.\n\n사내는 서로 알아봤다는 걸 눈치챘어. 그러고는 랄프에게 다시 나무 밑 아까 그 자리로 들어와, 쏟아지는 빗속에 서 있지 말라고 손짓했어. 정작 자기는 처음 놀란 통에 그 비를 까맣게 잊고 있었지만 말이야. 그러고는 쉰, 가냘픈 목소리로 랄프에게 말을 건넸어.\n\n\"제 목소리만 들어선 절 알아보기 힘드셨을 거예요, 니클비 씨.\" 사내가 말했어.\n\n\"그렇지.\" 랄프가 매서운 눈길을 그에게 던지며 대답했어. \"그래도 거기에 이제 보니 기억나는 게 좀 있군.\"\n\n\"제 모습에서 팔 년 전에도 있던 게 떠오를 만한 건 거의 없겠지요?\" 상대가 말했어.\n\n\"충분해.\" 랄프가 무심하게 얼굴을 돌리며 말했어. \"넘치도록 충분하지.\"\n\n\"제가 당신을 못 알아봤다 해도요, 니클비 씨.\" 사내가 말했어. \"이 푸대접하며 당신 태도를 보니 금방 알아봤을 거예요.\"\n\n\"다른 대접을 바라기라도 했나?\" 랄프가 쏘아붙였어.\n\n\"아뇨!\" 사내가 말했어.\n\n\"잘 알고 있군.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어. \"그러니 놀랍지도 않을 테고, 새삼 놀란 척할 것도 없겠지.\"\n\n\"니클비 씨.\" 사내가 잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤 퉁명스럽게 말했어. 그 잠깐 동안 그는 무슨 책망으로 받아치고 싶은 마음과 씨름하는 눈치였지. \"제가 드릴 말씀 몇 마디만 들어 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"비가 좀 멎을 때까지 여기 기다려야 하는 처지니까.\" 랄프가 먼 데를 바라보며 말했어. \"말을 하겠다면, 손가락으로 귀를 틀어막진 않겠네. 막은 거나 매한가지일 테지만.\"\n\n\"제가 한때 당신의 신임을 받던—\" 사내가 이렇게 말을 꺼냈어. 랄프는 돌아보며 저도 모르게 피식 웃었지.\n\n\"뭐, 그렇죠.\" 사내가 말했어. \"당신이 누구한테든 허락하는 만큼은, 저도 신임을 받았었죠.\"\n\n\"하!\" 랄프가 팔짱을 끼며 받아쳤어. \"그건 또 다른 얘기지, 아주 다른 얘기야.\"\n\n\"말꼬리 잡고 늘어지진 맙시다, 니클비 씨. 제발 인정이라도 좀 있게.\"\n\n\"뭐가 있게?\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"인정 말이에요.\" 사내가 엄숙하게 대답했어. \"전 굶주리고 궁핍합니다. 이렇게 오래 떨어져 있던 끝에 제게서 보실 수밖에 없는 이 변화가 — 보실 수밖에 없죠, 더디고 모진 단계를 거쳐 그 변화를 겪은 저 자신은 그걸 보고 잘 아니까요 — 그게 당신을 가엾게 여기게 못 한다면, 그럼 이거라도. 빵 말입니다. 주기도문에 나오는 그 일용할 양식 말고요. 이런 도시에서 그 기도를 올릴 땐, 부자한테는 세상 사치의 절반쯤을 다 끼워 넣은 거고, 가난뱅이한테는 목숨이나 부지할 만큼의 거친 음식이나 가리키는 거잖습니까. 그게 아니라, 그냥 빵, 마른 딱딱한 빵 한 조각조차 오늘 제 손에 안 들어옵니다. 다른 건 다 안 통해도, 이거라도 좀 마음에 와닿았으면 합니다.\"\n\n\"이게 자네가 늘 동냥하는 식이라면, 이보게.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"배역 공부 한번 잘했군그래. 그래도 세상 물정 좀 아는 사람의 충고를 듣겠다면, 좀 더 수그러진 어조를 권하지. 조금만 더 낮춰. 안 그러면 정말로 쫄쫄 굶어 죽기 딱 좋으니까.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 랄프는 오른손으로 왼쪽 손목을 꽉 움켜쥐고, 고개를 살짝 한쪽으로 기울이고 턱을 가슴에 떨군 채, 찌푸리고 뚱한 얼굴로 상대를 바라봤어. 그 무엇으로도 흔들거나 누그러뜨릴 수 없는 사내의 본보기 그대로였지.\n\n\"어제가 런던에 온 첫날이었습니다.\" 노인이 여행에 찌든 옷과 닳아빠진 신발을 흘끗 내려다보며 말했어.\n\n\"내 생각엔 그게 자네 마지막 날이었으면 더 좋았을 텐데.\" 랄프가 대답했어.\n\n\"이틀 동안 당신을 찾아다녔습니다. 당신이 있을 법한 곳들을 짚어 가면서요.\" 사내가 더 비굴하게 말을 이었어. \"그러다 마침내 여기서 당신을 만났네요. 당신을 마주칠 희망을 거의 접었을 때 말입니다, 니클비 씨.\"\n\n사내는 무슨 대답을 기다리는 눈치였어. 그런데 랄프가 아무 말도 안 하자, 그가 계속 말했어.\n\n\"전 더없이 비참하고 가련한 떠돌이입니다. 나이는 예순에 가깝지만, 여섯 살짜리 아이만큼이나 빈털터리에 무력하지요.\"\n\n\"나도 예순일세.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"그래도 빈털터리도 무력하지도 않아. 일을 해. 빵 타령 하면서 그럴듯한 연극 대사 늘어놓지 말고, 벌어.\"\n\n\"어떻게요?\" 사내가 부르짖었어. \"어디서요? 방법을 좀 일러 주십쇼. 그걸 제게 주시겠어요? 주실 거예요?\"\n\n\"한 번 준 적 있지.\" 랄프가 차분하게 대답했어. \"또 줄 거냐고는 굳이 안 물어봐도 알 텐데.\"\n\n\"이십 년 전, 아니 그보다 더 됐죠.\" 사내가 억눌린 목소리로 말했어. \"당신하고 제가 틀어진 게요. 기억하시죠? 제가 당신한테 물어다 준 어떤 일의 이익을 나눠 달라고 했어요. 그런데 제가 우기니까, 당신은 오래전에 빌려준 십 파운드 얼마에 — 오십 퍼센트쯤 되는 이자까지 쳐서 — 절 잡아 가뒀죠.\"\n\n\"그 비슷한 일이 기억나는군.\" 랄프가 무심하게 대답했어. \"그래서 뭐?\"\n\n\"그걸로 우리가 갈라선 건 아니죠.\" 사내가 말했어. \"전 빗장과 쇠창살 안에 갇힌 신세였으니 고분고분 굴복했고요. 당신도 그땐 지금처럼 자수성가한 인물이 아니어서, 그리 까다롭지 않고 당신이 하던 장사를 좀 아는 서기를 다시 들이는 게 반가웠을 테죠.\"\n\n\"자네가 빌고 또 빌어서, 내가 받아 줬지.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어. \"내가 친절했던 거야. 아마 자네가 필요하긴 했나 보지. 잊어버렸어. 그랬을 거야. 안 그랬으면 자넨 빌어도 헛수고였을 테니까. 쓸모가 있었지. 너무 정직하지도 않고, 너무 까다롭지도 않고, 손도 마음도 너무 깔끔하지 않은. 그래도 쓸모는 있었어.\"\n\n\"쓸모라, 그렇겠죠!\" 사내가 말했어. \"이거 봐요. 당신은 그 전에도 몇 해를 절 쥐어짜고 등쳐 먹었어요. 그래도 전 그때까지 충실하게 당신을 섬겼다고요. 그 개 같은 대접을 다 받으면서도 말이죠. 안 그랬습니까?\"\n\n랄프는 아무 대꾸도 안 했어.\n\n\"안 그랬냐고요?\" 사내가 다시 말했어.\n\n\"품삯을 받았잖나.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어. \"그리고 자네 일을 했고. 거기까진 우리가 서로 비긴 거지. 둘 다 빚진 거 없다고 할 수 있어.\"\n\n\"그땐 그랬죠. 하지만 그 뒤론 아니에요.\" 사내가 말했어.\n\n\"그 뒤론 당연히 아니지. 아니, 그때조차 아니야. (방금 자네가 말했듯이) 자넨 나한테 빚이 있었고, 지금도 그러니까.\" 랄프가 대답했어.\n\n\"그게 다가 아니에요.\" 사내가 다급하게 말했어. \"그게 다가 아니라고요. 잘 들어요. 저는 그 묵은 상처를 잊지 않았어요, 정말로요. 한편으론 그게 기억나서, 한편으론 언젠가 그걸로 돈을 벌어 보려는 속셈에서, 전 당신 곁에 있던 처지를 이용해서 당신의 약점을 하나 손에 넣었죠. 당신이 가진 걸 절반이라도 내놓고 알고 싶어 할, 그런데 저를 통하지 않고는 절대 알 수 없는 약점을요. 그러고 나서 전 당신을 떠났어요. 그게 한참 뒤였다는 거, 기억하세요. 그러다 법에 걸리는 자잘한 속임수를 좀 쳤다가 — 당신네 돈벌이꾼들이 법 테두리 바로 바깥에서 날마다 해 먹는 짓에 비하면 아무것도 아니었지만 — 칠 년 징역살이를 하러 끌려갔죠. 그러고는 지금 당신이 보는 이 꼴로 돌아왔습니다. 자, 니클비 씨.\" 사내가 비굴함과 무슨 힘을 쥐었다는 의식을 묘하게 뒤섞어 말했어. \"제게 무슨 도움을, 무슨 보탬을 주시겠어요? 까놓고 말해서, 입막음으로 얼마를 주실 거냐고요? 제 바람이 그리 터무니없진 않습니다. 그래도 살아야 하고, 살려면 먹고 마셔야죠. 돈은 당신 쪽에 있고, 굶주림과 목마름은 제 쪽에 있어요. 흥정하기엔 당신이 유리하지요.\"\n\n\"그게 단가?\" 랄프가 여전히 같은 흔들림 없는 눈길로 상대를 쏘아보며, 입술만 움직여 말했어.\n\n\"그게 단지 아닌지는 당신한테 달렸죠, 니클비 씨.\" 사내가 받아쳤어.\n\n\"그렇다면 잘 들어, 그—, 자네를 무슨 이름으로 불러야 할지 모르겠군.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"옛 이름으로 부르시죠, 원하신다면.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 잘 들어, 브루커 씨.\" 랄프가 더없이 험한 말투로 말했어. \"그리고 나한테서 또 한바탕 말이 나올 거라곤 기대하지 마. 잘 들어, 이 사람아. 난 자네가 옛날부터 약삭빠른 악당인 줄은 알았지만, 자네한텐 한 번도 배짱이란 게 없었지. 게다가 모진 노동에다, (어쩌면) 그 다리에 쇠사슬까지 차고, 내가 자넬 '쥐어짜고' '등쳐 먹던' 때보다 더 적게 먹었으니, 머리가 무뎌졌나 보군. 안 그러면 나한테 이런 얘길 들고 올 리가 없지. 자네가 내 약점을 쥐었다고! 쥐고 있든가, 아니면 세상에 까발리든가, 마음대로 해.\"\n\n\"그건 못 하죠.\" 브루커가 끼어들었어. \"그래선 제게 득될 게 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"안 돼?\" 랄프가 말했어. \"나한테 들고 온 거랑 딱 그만큼 득이 될 거다, 장담하지. 솔직히 말하면, 난 빈틈없는 사람이고 내 일은 속속들이 알아. 난 세상을 알고, 세상도 날 알지. 자네가 나를 섬길 적에 주워듣거나 보거나 알아낸 게 뭐든, 세상은 이미 다 알고 부풀려 떠들고 있어. 자네가 세상을 놀라게 할 만한 건 하나도 못 일러 줘. 그게 정말 내 신용이나 명예에 보탬이 되는 거라면 모를까, 그땐 오히려 자넬 거짓말쟁이라고 쫓아낼걸. 그런데도 내 장사는 한가하지 않고, 손님들도 까다롭게 굴지 않아. 오히려 그 반대지. 난 날마다 이놈저놈한테 욕먹고 위협받아.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그래도 일은 똑같이 굴러가고, 내가 더 가난해지지도 않지.\"\n\n\"전 욕하지도 위협하지도 않아요.\" 사내가 받아쳤어. \"전 당신한테 일러 줄 수 있어요. 제 짓 때문에 당신이 뭘 잃었는지, 오직 저만 되돌려 놓을 수 있는 게 뭔지, 그리고 제가 그걸 안 되돌려 놓고 죽으면 저랑 같이 죽어 영영 되찾을 수 없는 게 뭔지를요.\"\n\n\"난 내 돈을 꽤 정확히 세고, 대개는 내 손으로 간수하지.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"거래하는 사람들 대부분을 날카롭게 지켜보는데, 그중에서도 자넬 가장 날카롭게 지켜봤어. 자네가 나한테서 빼돌린 게 있다면, 죄다 자네나 가지라고.\"\n\n\"당신과 같은 이름을 쓰는 이들이 소중하긴 하오?\" 사내가 힘주어 말했어. \"만약 그렇다면—\"\n\n\"안 소중해.\" 랄프가 대답했어. 이렇게 끈질기게 구는 데다, 마지막 물음이 일깨운 니콜라스 생각에 화가 치밀어서 말이야. \"안 소중하다고. 자네가 그냥 흔한 거지로 왔다면, 옛날의 그 약삭빠른 악당이 떠올라 육 펜스쯤 던져 줬을지도 모르지. 그런데 진작에 더 잘 알았어야 할 사람한테 이런 케케묵은 수작을 떠넘기려 드니, 반 펜스도 안 내놓겠어. 자네가 썩어 문드러지는 걸 막을 수 있대도 안 줘. 그리고 이걸 명심해, 이 교수대를 모면한 놈아.\" 랄프가 그에게 손을 들어 위협하며 말했어. \"우리가 다시 만났을 때, 자네가 동냥하는 몸짓 하나라도 내게 보이기만 해 봐. 그땐 또 한 번 감옥 속을 구경하게 될 테고, 떠돌이한테 시키는 그 모진 노역 틈틈이 내 약점을 더 옥죄게 될 거야. 이게 자네 헛소리에 대한 내 대답이야. 가져가.\"\n\n랄프는 화풀이 상대를 경멸 어린 눈으로 노려봤어. 사내는 그 눈길을 받으면서도 한마디도 안 했지. 랄프는 늘 그렇듯 평소 걸음으로 걸어갔어. 방금 같이 있던 자가 어찌 되는지 조금도 궁금해하지 않았고, 정말 한 번도 뒤를 돌아보지 않았지. 사내는 그 자리에 그대로 서서, 멀어져 가는 랄프의 모습이 보이지 않을 때까지 눈을 떼지 않았어. 그러고는 축축한 기운과 굶주림이 차갑게 파고드는 듯 팔로 가슴팍을 감싸 안고, 구부정한 걸음으로 길가에서 서성이며 지나가는 사람들한테 동냥했어.\n\n랄프는 방금 일어난 일에 조금도 마음이 흔들리지 않았어. 이미 입 밖으로 다 내뱉은 것 말고는 말이야. 그는 또박또박 걸어 나가 공원을 벗어났어. 그리고 골든 스퀘어를 오른쪽으로 두고서, 도시 서쪽 끝의 거리 몇 개를 지나, 망탈리니 부인의 집이 있는 바로 그 거리에 다다랐지. 그 부인의 이름은 이제 번쩍이는 문패에 더 이상 보이지 않았어. 대신 내그 양의 이름이 들어가 있었지. 그래도 모자와 드레스 들은 여전히 이 층 창문 너머로 어렴풋이 보였어. 여름 저녁 스러져 가는 햇빛 속에서 말이야. 그리고 이렇게 겉으로 주인이 바뀐 것 말고는, 그 가게는 옛 모습 그대로였지.\n\n\"흠!\" 랄프가 감정가인 양 손으로 입을 쓱 훔치며 집을 위에서 아래까지 훑어보고는 중얼거렸어. \"이 사람들 꽤 잘되는 것 같군. 오래는 못 가겠지만. 그래도 망하는 때를 미리 알아 두면 난 안전하고, 짭짤한 이익도 챙기겠지. 이 사람들을 바짝 지켜봐야겠어. 그게 다야.\"\n\n그래서 아주 흡족하게 고개를 끄덕이며 랄프가 그 자리를 막 떠나려는데, 그의 빠른 귀에 무슨 어수선한 소리가 들렸어. 와글거리는 목소리에 계단을 우당탕 오르내리는 소리까지 뒤섞여서 말이야. 그것도 방금 그가 꼼꼼히 살펴보던 바로 그 집에서 나는 거였지. 그가 문을 두드릴까, 아니면 열쇠 구멍에 좀 더 귀를 대고 있을까 망설이는 사이에, 망탈리니 부인네 하녀 하나가 (랄프가 자주 본 아이였어) 문을 벌컥 열고 튀어나왔어. 파란 모자 끈을 바람에 휘날리면서 말이야.\n\n\"이봐, 거기. 멈춰!\" 랄프가 외쳤어. \"무슨 일이야? 나 여기 있다. 내가 두드리는 소리 못 들었어?\"\n\n\"어머! 니클비 씨, 나리.\" 하녀가 말했어. \"제발 좀 올라가 보세요. 주인 나리가 또 일을 저질렀어요.\"\n\n\"뭘 저질렀다는 거야?\" 랄프가 퉁명스럽게 말했어. \"무슨 소리야?\"\n\n\"이렇게 몰아세우면 저럴 줄 알았어요.\" 하녀가 부르짖었어. \"내내 그렇게 말했다고요.\"\n\n\"이리 와, 이 멍청한 계집애야.\" 랄프가 그녀의 손목을 붙잡으며 말했어. \"그리고 집안일을 이웃에 떠벌려서 가게 신용을 망치지 마. 이리 오라니까. 내 말 들리냐, 얘?\"\n\n랄프는 더 따질 것도 없이, 겁먹은 하녀를 집 안으로 이끌었어. 이끌었다기보다 끌어당겼다는 게 맞겠지. 그러고는 문을 닫았어. 그러고 나서 하녀더러 앞장서서 위층으로 올라가라 이르고는, 별 격식 없이 그 뒤를 따라갔어.\n\n여럿이 한꺼번에 떠들어 대는 소리를 길잡이 삼아, 랄프는 조바심에 몇 계단 못 올라가서 하녀를 앞질렀어. 그러고는 금세 안쪽 거실에 다다랐지. 그런데 거기서 느닷없이 맞닥뜨린 어수선하고 알 수 없는 광경에 적잖이 놀라고 말았어.\n\n거기엔 가게의 젊은 여공들이 죄다 모여 있었어. 모자를 쓴 애도 있고 안 쓴 애도 있었는데, 다들 놀라고 어쩔 줄 모르는 갖가지 자세였지. 몇몇은 한 의자에 앉아 눈물짓는 망탈리니 부인 둘레에 모여 있었고. 또 몇몇은 맞은편 다른 의자에 앉아 역시 눈물짓는 내그 양 둘레에 모여 있었어. 또 몇몇은 망탈리니 씨 둘레에 모여 있었는데, 이 무리 가운데 어쩌면 가장 눈에 띄는 인물이 바로 그였지. 망탈리니 씨의 두 다리는 바닥에 쭉 뻗어 있었고, 머리와 어깨는 키가 아주 큰 하인이 떠받치고 있었는데, 그 하인은 그걸 어찌해야 할지 모르는 눈치였어. 망탈리니 씨의 두 눈은 감겨 있었고, 얼굴은 핼쑥했고, 머리카락은 평소보다 곧게 펴져 있었어. 구레나룻과 콧수염은 축 늘어졌고, 이는 꽉 악물려 있었지. 오른손엔 작은 병을, 왼손엔 작은 찻숟가락을 쥐고 있었어. 그리고 손이며 팔이며 다리며 어깨가 죄다 뻣뻣하게 굳어 힘이 하나도 없었어. 그런데도 망탈리니 부인은 그 몸뚱이를 붙들고 울기는커녕, 의자에 앉아 거세게 잔소리를 퍼붓고 있었지. 게다가 이 모든 일이 귀가 멍멍할 만큼 떠들썩한 입씨름 한복판에서 벌어지고 있었고, 그 소동은 정말이지 가엾은 하인을 거의 미치기 직전까지 몰아붙인 듯했어.\n\n\"여기 무슨 일이야?\" 랄프가 앞으로 헤집고 나서며 말했어.\n\n이 물음에 소동은 스무 배로 커졌어. 그리고 귀청 떨어질 듯 날카롭게 엇갈리는 말들이 쏟아졌지. \"독약을 먹었어요\"—\"아니에요\"—\"의사를 불러요\"—\"부르지 마요\"—\"죽어 가요\"—\"아니에요, 죽는 척하는 거예요\"—온갖 다른 외침까지 어찌나 정신없이 빠르게 쏟아지는지. 그러다 망탈리니 부인이 랄프한테 말을 거는 게 보이자, 그녀가 무슨 말을 할지 궁금해하는 여자들의 호기심이 이겨 버렸어. 그러더니 약속이라도 한 듯, 속삭임 하나 없는 죽은 듯한 정적이 순식간에 뒤따랐지.\n\n\"니클비 씨.\" 망탈리니 부인이 말했어. \"무슨 일로 여기 오셨는지는 모르겠네요.\"\n\n이때 부글거리는 목소리가, 병든 사내의 헛소리처럼 \"지긋지긋한 달콤함!\" 하고 내뱉는 게 들렸어. 그런데 아무도 그 말에 신경 쓰지 않았지. 딱 그 하인만 빼고 말이야. 하인은 자기 손가락 사이에서 그런 끔찍한 소리가 나오는 걸 듣고 깜짝 놀라, 주인 머리를 꽤 큰 소리가 나게 바닥에 떨어뜨렸어. 그러고는 그걸 다시 들어 올릴 생각도 안 하고, 둘러선 사람들을 멀뚱히 바라봤지. 마치 영 못난 짓이 아니라 제법 잘난 짓이라도 한 것처럼 말이야.\n\n\"하지만 전요.\" 망탈리니 부인이 눈물을 훔치며 잔뜩 화가 난 목소리로 말을 이었어. \"당신 앞에서, 여기 모인 모든 사람 앞에서, 처음으로, 그리고 영영 단번에 말하겠어요. 저 인간의 낭비와 못된 짓을 다시는 절대 대 주지 않겠다고요. 저 인간한테 속아 넘어간 바보 노릇은 이제 할 만큼 했어요. 앞으로는 할 수 있으면 제 손으로 벌어먹으라죠. 그럼 그 돈으로 누구한테 어떻게 쓰든 마음대로 하든가요. 하지만 그게 제 돈은 아닐 거예요. 그러니 당신도 저 인간을 더 믿기 전에 잘 생각해 보는 게 좋을 거예요.\"\n\n이쯤에서 망탈리니 부인은, 약제사가 청산을 충분히 진하게 안 타 줘서 손대던 일을 마저 끝내려면 한두 병 더 먹어야겠다는 남편의 더없이 애처로운 푸념에도 조금도 흔들리지 않았어. 그러고는 이 사랑스러운 신사의 바람기며, 속임수며, 낭비며, 부정(특히 마지막 거)을 줄줄이 늘어놓기 시작했지. 그러고는 자기가 저 인간한테 손톱만큼이라도 애정을 품고 있다고 여겨선 안 된다고 못박으며 마무리했어. 그리고 자기 마음이 변했다는 증거로, 저 인간이 지난 보름 사이에 혼자서 무려 여섯 번이나 독약을 먹었는데도, 자기가 말 한마디 행동 하나로 목숨을 구하려 든 적이 한 번도 없었다는 사실을 들었지.\n\n\"그리고 저는 갈라서서 혼자 지낼 것을 요구해요.\" 망탈리니 부인이 흐느끼며 말했어. \"저 인간이 감히 별거를 거부한다면, 법대로 갈라설 거예요. 그럴 수 있다고요. 그리고 이 꼴사나운 구경거리를 본 모든 아가씨들한테 이게 경고가 되길 바라요.\"\n\n이 자리에서 누가 봐도 가장 나이 많은 아가씨인 내그 양이 아주 엄숙하게, 자기한테 경고가 되겠다고 말했어. 그러자 다른 젊은 아가씨들도 대체로 그렇다고 했지. 다만 한둘은, 그런 속삭임이 그렇게까지 나쁜 일일까 의심하는 눈치였어.\n\n\"왜 이런 얘길 이렇게 많은 사람 앞에서 하는 거요?\" 랄프가 낮은 목소리로 말했어. \"진심이 아니란 거 당신도 알잖소.\"\n\n\"전 진심이에요.\" 망탈리니 부인이 큰 소리로 대답하며 내그 양 쪽으로 물러섰어.\n\n\"그래도, 잘 생각해 보시오.\" 이 일에 큰 이해관계가 걸린 랄프가 타일렀어. \"곰곰이 따져 보는 게 좋아요. 결혼한 여자한텐 재산이 없는 법이니까.\"\n\n\"단 하나도 없지, 한 푼도 빌어먹게 없지, 내 영혼아.\" 망탈리니 씨가 팔꿈치로 몸을 일으키며 말했어.\n\n\"저도 그건 잘 알아요.\" 망탈리니 부인이 고개를 홱 젖히며 받아쳤어. \"그래서 전 가진 게 없죠. 사업도, 재고도, 이 집도, 그 안에 있는 모든 게 다 내그 양 거예요.\"\n\n\"맞는 말씀이에요, 망탈리니 부인.\" 내그 양이 말했어. 전 주인과 이 점에 대해 몰래 좋게 합의를 봐 둔 터였지. \"정말 맞는 말씀이고말고요, 망탈리니 부인—흠—정말 맞는 말씀이죠. 그리고 전 평생 이렇게 기뻤던 적이 없어요. 아무리 유리한 청혼이라도 다 뿌리칠 만큼 마음이 굳건했다는 게요. 지금 제 처지를, 그토록 불행하고 그토록 억울한 부인 처지와 견줘 보면 더욱 그렇답니다, 망탈리니 부인.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을!\" 망탈리니 씨가 아내 쪽으로 고개를 돌리며 외쳤어. \"이 녀석은 자기 달콤한 임을 감히 헐뜯는 저 샘 많은 늙은 과부를 찰싹 때리고 꼬집어 주지 않을 텐가?\"\n\n그런데 망탈리니 씨의 달콤한 말이 먹히던 시절은 이미 끝나 버렸어. \"내그 양은요, 여보.\" 그의 아내가 말했어. \"제 둘도 없는 친구예요.\" 망탈리니 씨가 눈알이 영영 제자리로 돌아오지 못할 만큼 곁눈질을 해 댔지만, 망탈리니 부인은 누그러질 기미가 전혀 없었지.\n\n훌륭한 내그 양을 두고 공평하게 말하자면, 이렇게 사정이 달라진 데에는 그녀의 공이 컸어. 매일 겪어 보니 망탈리니 씨가 씀씀이에 조금이라도 손을 대는 한 사업이 번창할 가망은커녕 명맥을 잇기도 글렀다는 걸 알게 됐거든. 게다가 이제 사업이 잘되는 데 적잖은 이해가 걸린 그녀는, 그 신사의 사생활에 얽힌 자잘한 일들을 부지런히 캐고 다녔지. 그러고는 그걸 아주 잘 밝혀내서 교묘하게 망탈리니 부인한테 일러 줬어. 그러니 몇 해에 걸친 그 어떤 빈틈없고 철학적인 설득보다도 더 톡톡히 부인의 눈을 뜨게 한 거지. 그 일에는 내그 양이 우연히 발견한 어떤 애틋한 편지가 더없이 절묘하게 한몫했어. 그 편지에서 망탈리니 부인은 \"늙은이\"에 \"평범한 여자\"라고 적혀 있었거든.\n\n그래도 그렇게 마음을 굳게 먹었으면서도, 망탈리니 부인은 아주 가엾게 울었어. 그러고는 내그 양에게 기댄 채 문 쪽으로 손짓했지. 그러자 그 젊은 아가씨와 다른 모든 젊은 아가씨들이 가엾어하는 얼굴로 부인을 부축해 데리고 나갔어.\n\n\"니클비.\" 망탈리니 씨가 눈물을 흘리며 말했어. \"당신은 이 빌어먹게 잔인한 짓을 똑똑히 목격했소. 세상에 둘도 없는 빌어먹게 매혹적인 마녀의 짓 말이오, 아 빌어먹을! 난 저 여자를 용서하오.\"\n\n\"용서한다고요!\" 망탈리니 부인이 성나서 되받았어.\n\n\"난 저 여자를 용서하오, 니클비.\" 망탈리니 씨가 말했어. \"당신은 날 탓하겠지, 세상이 날 탓하겠지, 여자들이 날 탓하겠지. 다들 비웃고, 비아냥대고, 미소 짓고, 빌어먹게 히죽댈 거요. 사람들이 이러겠지. '저 여잔 복을 받았는데 그걸 몰랐어. 그 남잔 너무 여렸어, 너무 착했어. 빌어먹게 멋진 사내였는데 사랑이 너무 깊었지. 아내가 토라져서 못된 욕을 퍼붓는 걸 견딜 수가 없었어. 빌어먹을 일이었지, 그보다 더 빌어먹을 일은 없었어.' 그래도 난 저 여자를 용서하오.\"\n\n이 가슴 저미는 연설을 마치고 망탈리니 씨는 다시 납작하게 쓰러졌어. 그러고는 정신도 움직임도 없는 듯 누워 있었지. 모든 여자들이 방을 나갈 때까지 말이야. 그러더니 슬그머니 일어나 앉는 자세를 취하고는, 더없이 멍한 얼굴로 랄프와 마주했어. 작은 병은 여전히 한 손에, 찻숟가락은 다른 손에 쥔 채로.\n\n\"이제 그 어릿광대짓은 집어치우고, 다시 머리 굴려서 먹고살 궁리나 하시오.\" 랄프가 태연히 모자를 쓰며 말했어.\n\n\"빌어먹을, 니클비, 진심은 아니지?\"\n\n\"난 좀처럼 농담을 안 하오.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"잘 자시오.\"\n\n\"아니, 근데 니클비—\" 망탈리니가 말했어.\n\n\"내가 틀렸을 수도 있겠지.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어. \"그러길 바라오. 당신이 제일 잘 알 테지. 잘 자시오.\"\n\n랄프는 좀 더 있으면서 의논 좀 해 달라는 망탈리니의 간청을 못 들은 척, 풀이 죽은 그를 명상에 빠지게 내버려 두고 조용히 집을 나섰어.\n\n\"오호!\" 그가 말했어. \"벌써 바람이 그쪽으로 부나? 반은 악당, 반은 바보, 그러고도 양쪽 다 들통이 났군? 당신 시대는 끝난 것 같소, 이 양반아.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 랄프는 수첩에 뭔가를 적어 넣었어. 거기엔 망탈리니 씨의 이름이 큼지막하게 들어가 있었지. 그러고는 시계를 보니 아홉 시에서 열 시 사이였어. 그래서 부리나케 집으로 돌아갔지.\n\n\"그 사람들 와 있나?\" 랄프가 뉴먼에게 던진 첫 물음이었어.\n\n뉴먼이 고개를 끄덕였어. \"삼십 분 전부터 와 있어요.\"\n\n\"둘이? 하나는 살집 있고 매끈한 사내?\"\n\n\"네.\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"지금 사장님 방에 있어요.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어. \"마차 한 대 불러 줘.\"\n\n\"마차요! 뭐, 사장님이—어딜—네?\" 뉴먼이 말을 더듬었어.\n\n랄프가 성난 목소리로 명령을 되풀이했어. 그러자 노그스는, 이런 색다르고 예사롭지 않은 일에 어리둥절해할 만도 했지만 (랄프가 마차 타는 걸 평생 한 번도 본 적이 없었거든), 시키는 대로 나갔다가 이내 마차를 끌고 돌아왔어.\n\n마차에 스퀴어스 씨와 랄프, 그리고 뉴먼 노그스가 한 번도 본 적 없는 세 번째 사내가 올라탔어. 뉴먼은 문간 계단에 서서 그들을 배웅했지. 그들이 어디로, 무슨 일로 가는지 굳이 궁금해하지도 않으면서 말이야. 그러다 그저 우연히, 마부더러 어디로 몰라고 랄프가 일러 주는 주소를 듣고 말았어.\n\n번개처럼 빠르게, 그리고 더없이 놀란 채로, 뉴먼은 모자를 가지러 자기 작은 사무실로 쏜살같이 달려 들어갔어. 그러고는 마차 뒤에 매달릴 작정인 듯 절뚝거리며 쫓아갔지. 그런데 이 계획은 틀어지고 말았어. 마차가 너무 앞서 출발한 데다 금세 가망 없이 멀어져서, 뉴먼은 텅 빈 거리에서 입을 떡 벌린 채 남겨졌거든.\n\n\"하긴 모르겠다.\" 노그스가 숨을 돌리려 멈춰 서서 말했어. \"나까지 갔어도 무슨 도움이 됐을지. 갔으면 사장님 눈에 띄었겠지. 저기로 가다니! 이게 무슨 일이람? 어제만 알았어도 일러 줄 수 있었을 텐데—저기로 가다니! 이건 무슨 못된 짓이야. 틀림없어.\"\n\n그의 생각은, 아주 눈에 띄지만 결코 호감 가지 않는 행색의 백발 사내 때문에 끊겼어. 그 사내는 슬그머니 그에게 다가와 적선을 청했지.\n\n뉴먼은 여전히 깊이 골똘한 채 돌아섰어. 그런데 사내가 그를 따라오며 어찌나 비참한 사연을 들이대는지, 뉴먼은 (동냥하기엔 가망 없는 사람으로 보였을 테고, 줄 것도 거의 없었지만) 모자 안을 들여다봤어. 평소 돈이 좀 생기면 손수건 한쪽 구석에 비틀어 묶어 두던 반 펜스 동전 몇 닢을 찾으려고 말이야.\n\n뉴먼이 이로 그 매듭을 부지런히 푸는 동안, 사내가 무슨 말을 했는데 그게 그의 주의를 끌었어. 그게 무슨 말이었든, 또 다른 말로 이어졌고, 결국 그와 뉴먼은 나란히 걸어가고 있었지—그 낯선 사내는 간절하게 얘기하고, 뉴먼은 귀 기울여 들으면서 말이야.\n\n\"우리는 내일 밤이면 런던을 떠날 거고, 내 평생 이렇게 행복했던 적이 또 있었나 싶은데, 니클비 씨, 에라! 우리 다음에 또 즐겁게 만날 날을 위해 한 잔 더 들어야겠소!\"\n\n존 브라우디는 이렇게 말하며, 무척 즐거운 듯 두 손을 비비고는, 그 말과 딱 어울리는 불그레하고 환한 얼굴로 둘레를 휘 둘러봤어.\n\n존이 이렇게 부러울 만한 기분에 젖어 있던 때는, 앞 장에서 이야기한 바로 그날 저녁이었어. 장소는 그 작은 집이었고, 모인 사람들은 니콜라스, 니클비 부인, 브라우디 부인, 케이트 니클비, 그리고 스마이크였지.\n\n다들 아주 즐거운 모임을 보내고 있었어. 니클비 부인은 정직한 요크셔 사람한테 아들이 신세를 졌다는 걸 알고 있었거든. 그래서 한참 망설인 끝에 브라우디 부부를 차 마시러 초대하자는 데 동의를 해 줬지. 그런데 이 일을 두고 처음엔 이런저런 어려움과 걸림돌이 있었어. 부인이 브라우디 부인한테 먼저 '방문'할 기회가 없었다는 게 문제였지. 니클비 부인은 까다로운 사람들이 흔히 그러듯, 자기는 거만하거나 격식 차리는 구석이 눈곱만큼도 없다고 흐뭇하게 자주 말하곤 했어. 그래도 품위와 예법에는 무척 까다로운 사람이었거든. 그러니 방문을 하기 전에는 — 점잖게 말해서, 또 사교계의 법도대로라면 — 브라우디 부인이라는 사람이 존재한다는 사실조차 알 수 없는 셈이라, 부인은 자기 처지가 유난히 미묘하고 곤란하다고 느꼈어.\n\n\"방문은 내가 먼저 시작해야 한단다, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"그건 꼭 그래야 하는 거야. 사실은 말이다, 얘야, 내 쪽에서 일종의 격을 낮춰 주는 게 필요해. 내가 그 젊은 여자를 기꺼이 봐주겠다는 걸 보여 줘야 한다는 거지. 아주 점잖아 보이는 젊은이가 하나 있는데,\" 니클비 부인이 잠깐 생각하더니 덧붙였어. \"여기를 지나다니는 합승마차 차장이야. 번들거리는 모자를 쓰고 다니지 — 너랑 나랑 그 사람을 아주 자주 봤잖니 — 코에 사마귀가 하나 있단다, 케이트, 너도 알지, 꼭 신사 집안 하인처럼 말이야.\"\n\n\"신사 집안 하인들은 다 코에 사마귀가 있나요, 어머니?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"니콜라스, 얘야, 너는 어쩜 그렇게 엉뚱하니.\" 어머니가 대꾸했어. \"당연히 내 말은 그 사람 번들거리는 모자가 신사 집안 하인 같다는 거지, 코에 난 사마귀가 그렇다는 게 아니야. 하지만 그것도 네가 생각하는 것만큼 우스운 일은 아니란다. 옛날에 우리 집에 사환 아이가 하나 있었는데, 사마귀뿐 아니라 혹까지 있었거든. 그것도 아주 큰 혹이었지. 그래서 그 아이는 그 때문에 돈이 많이 든다면서 품삯을 올려 달라고 했단다. 가만, 내가 무슨 얘기를 — 아 그래, 알겠다. 내가 생각하기에 제일 좋은 방법은, 그 젊은이 편에 카드 한 장이랑 내 인사를 써서('새러슨 위드 투 넥스'로 보내는 거지. 그 사람은 흑맥주 한 잔으로 알아들을 게 분명하지만 말이야) 보내는 거야. 종업원이 그 사람을 신사 집안 하인으로 봐 준다면 더 좋고. 그러면 브라우디 부인은 짐꾼 편에 카드를 도로 보내기만 하면 돼(그 사람이 문을 쾅쾅 두 번 두드리며 오면 되니까). 그러면 끝이지.\"\n\n\"어머니,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"이렇게 꾸밈없는 사람들은 자기 카드 같은 걸 가져 본 적도 없고, 앞으로 가질 일도 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇다면야, 니콜라스, 얘야,\" 니클비 부인이 대꾸했어. \"그건 또 다른 얘기지. 네가 그렇게 따진다면야, 나도 더 할 말이 없구나. 그 사람들이 아주 착한 사람들인 건 틀림없고, 차 마시러 오고 싶다면 오는 거 나는 아무 반대도 없고, 정말 온다면 아주 친절하게 대해 줄 거라는 것 말고는 말이다.\"\n\n이렇게 해서 이 문제가 깔끔하게 정리됐고, 니클비 부인은 자기 지위와 결혼한 햇수에 걸맞게 너그럽고 살짝 격을 낮춰 주는 자리에 자리 잡았지. 그래서 브라우디 부부는 초대를 받아 왔어. 부부는 니클비 부인한테 무척 깍듯했고, 부인의 대단함을 제대로 알아주는 듯했고, 모든 게 다 마음에 드는 모양이었어. 그래서 이 착한 부인은 케이트한테 한 번 이상 귓속말로, 자기가 본 사람 중에 제일 착한 사람들 같고 행실도 더없이 점잖다고 일러 줬지.\n\n이렇게 해서 존 브라우디는 저녁을 먹은 뒤 거실에서, 그러니까 밤 열한 시 이십 분 전쯤에, 평생 이렇게 행복했던 적이 없다고 떠들게 된 거야.\n\n브라우디 부인도 이 점에서는 남편에게 별로 뒤지지 않았어. 이 젊은 새댁의 시골스러운 아름다움은 케이트의 더 섬세한 사랑스러움과 아주 곱게 대비됐거든. 그렇다고 어느 쪽이 못나 보이는 것도 아니었어. 둘은 서로를 돋보이게 해 주고 꾸며 주는 셈이었으니까. 새댁은 젊은 아가씨의 부드럽고 사랑스러운 몸가짐과, 나이 든 부인의 상냥한 붙임성에 감탄해 마지않았어. 게다가 케이트는 화제를 잘 돌리는 재주가 있어서, 처음엔 낯선 자리에서 수줍어하던 시골 처녀가 편안함을 느낄 만한 이야기로 이끌어 갔지. 니클비 부인은 가끔 화제를 고르는 데 그만큼 능숙하지는 못했고, 브라우디 부인 말마따나 '생각이 좀 높은' 듯도 했지만, 그래도 그보다 더 다정할 수는 없었어. 그리고 부인이 이 젊은 부부에게 적잖이 관심을 두고 있다는 건, 브라우디 부인 귀에다 친절하게도 살림살이에 관한 기나긴 강의를 늘어놓는 데서 분명히 드러났지. 그 강의는 이 작은 집의 살림 형편을 이런저런 예로 들어 가며 곁들였는데, 정작 그 살림일은 (전부 케이트 몫이었거든) 이론으로든 실제로든 이 착한 부인이 끼어든 정도가, 세인트 폴 대성당 바깥을 장식하는 열두 사도 조각상 가운데 하나만큼이나 되었어.\n\n\"브라우디 씨는,\" 케이트가 그의 젊은 아내에게 말을 건넸어. \"제가 본 사람 중에 제일 마음씨 좋고, 제일 친절하고, 제일 다정한 분이에요. 제가 무슨 걱정거리에 얼마나 짓눌려 있든, 그분을 보기만 해도 행복해질 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 보아하니 더없이 훌륭한 분 같구나, 케이트.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"더없이 훌륭해. 그리고 브라우디 부인, 이렇게 수수하고 소박하게 우리 집에 와서 나를 봐 주는 건 언제든 정말 기쁜 일이에요 — 정말로 기쁘답니다. 우리는 거창하게 차리지 않아요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. 마음만 먹으면 엄청나게 거창하게 차릴 수도 있다는 걸 넌지시 비치는 듯한 투였지. \"야단법석도 없고, 준비도 없고요. 나는 그런 걸 용납하지 않거든요. 내가 그랬어요. '케이트, 얘야, 그러면 브라우디 부인이 불편해하기만 할 거다. 그건 얼마나 어리석고 경솔한 짓이겠니!'\"\n\n\"정말 감사합니다, 부인.\" 브라우디 부인이 고마운 마음으로 대답했어. \"거의 열한 시예요, 존. 부인을 너무 늦게까지 못 주무시게 하는 건 아닌지 모르겠네요, 부인.\"\n\n\"늦다니요!\" 니클비 부인이 날카롭고 가는 웃음을 터뜨리고는, 끝에 짧게 기침을 한 번 했어. 꼭 감탄 부호를 찍는 것처럼 말이야. \"우리한테 이건 아주 이른 시간이에요. 우리가 어떤 시간까지 깨어 있었는데요! 열두 시, 한 시, 두 시, 세 시도 우리한텐 아무것도 아니었답니다. 무도회, 만찬, 카드놀이 모임! 우리가 살던 동네 사람들처럼 그렇게 흥청거리는 사람들은 없었어요. 지금 생각하면 정말이지, 우리가 그걸 어떻게 다 견뎌 냈는지 참 놀라울 따름이에요. 그게 바로 인맥이 넓고 사람들이 많이 찾아 주는 데서 오는 폐단이지요. 나는 젊은 부부들한테 꼭 그걸 꿋꿋이 견디라고 권하고 싶어요. 물론, 또 아주 분명한 얘기지만, 그리고 참 다행스러운 일이라고 생각하는데, 그런 유혹에 노출될 수 있는 젊은 부부는 거의 없긴 하지요. 특히 한 집안이 있었는데, 우리 집에서 한 일 마일쯤 떨어진 데 살았어요 — 길을 곧장 내려가는 게 아니라, 플리머스 우편마차가 당나귀를 치고 지나간 그 통행료 받는 길에서 왼쪽으로 홱 꺾어 들어가야 했지요 — 그 사람들은 정말 유난스럽게 호화로운 잔치를 열곤 했어요. 조화에 샴페인에, 알록달록한 등불에, 한마디로 제아무리 까다로운 미식가라도 바랄 만한 온갖 진수성찬을 다 차렸지요. 그 펠티로거스네 같은 사람들은 또 없을 거예요. 펠티로거스네 기억나지, 케이트?\"\n\n케이트는 손님들이 편안하고 마음 놓이게 하려면 이제 이 추억의 물결을 막을 때가 됐다는 걸 알아챘어. 그래서 펠티로거스네에 대해 아주 생생하고 또렷이 기억한다고 대답했지. 그러고는 브라우디 씨가 저녁 일찍 요크셔 노래를 한 곡 불러 주기로 반쯤 약속했었다며, 그 약속을 꼭 지켜 줬으면 좋겠다고 했어. 그러면 어머니가 말로 다 못 할 만큼 즐겁고 흐뭇해하실 게 분명하다면서 말이야.\n\n니클비 부인은 더없이 우아하게 딸 말에 맞장구를 쳤어 — 거기에도 너그러이 봐주는 기색이, 또 자기가 그런 방면에 안목이 있고 제법 비평가쯤 된다는 듯한 투가 묻어 있었지 — 그러자 존 브라우디는 어느 북부 지방 가락의 가사를 떠올리려 애쓰며, 그 노래에 관해 아내의 기억을 빌렸어. 그러고는 의자에 앉은 채로 어색하게 이리저리 몸을 움직이더니, 천장에서 잠들어 있는 다른 파리들 가운데 파리 한 마리를 콕 골라 거기에 눈을 박고는, 부드러운 정감(사랑과 절망에 야위어 가는 점잖은 시골 총각이 읊는 것으로 되어 있는)을 천둥 같은 목소리로 우렁차게 뽑아 대기 시작했어.\n\n첫 절이 끝날 무렵, 마치 바깥에 있던 누군가가 그때까지 기다렸다가 제 소리를 들리게 하려는 듯, 길 쪽 현관문을 쾅쾅 사납게 두드리는 소리가 들렸어. 어찌나 크고 사납던지, 부인들은 마치 약속이라도 한 듯 한꺼번에 흠칫 놀랐고, 존 브라우디도 노래를 멈췄지.\n\n\"뭔가 잘못 온 거겠지요.\" 니콜라스가 대수롭잖게 말했어. \"이 시간에 여기 올 사람은 없으니까요.\"\n\n그래도 니클비 부인은 이런저런 짐작을 했어. 어쩌면 회계 사무소가 불타 버렸거나, 어쩌면 '치어리블 형제'가 니콜라스를 동업자로 들이려고 사람을 보냈거나(이 밤중에 그럴 가능성이 정말 높아 보였지), 어쩌면 링킨워터 씨가 재산을 가지고 달아났거나, 어쩌면 라 크리비 양이 속아 넘어갔거나, 어쩌면 —\n\n하지만 케이트가 황급히 내지른 소리에 부인의 짐작은 뚝 끊겼어. 그리고 랄프 니클비가 방 안으로 걸어 들어왔지.\n\n\"가만.\" 니콜라스가 일어서고 케이트가 그에게 다가가 팔에 매달리자, 랄프가 말했어. \"저 녀석이 한마디라도 하기 전에, 내 말을 들어라.\"\n\n니콜라스는 입술을 깨물고 위협하듯 고개를 저었지만, 그 순간만큼은 한마디도 내뱉을 수가 없는 듯했어. 케이트는 그의 팔에 더 바싹 매달렸고, 스마이크는 그들 뒤로 물러섰지. 그리고 랄프에 대해 들은 적이 있어서 그를 알아보는 데 별 어려움이 없어 보이던 존 브라우디가, 노인과 자기 젊은 친구 사이로 끼어들었어. 마치 둘 중 누구도 한 발짝 더 나아가지 못하게 막으려는 듯이 말이야.\n\n\"내 말을 들으라니까,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"저 녀석 말 말고.\"\n\n\"그럼 할 말이 있거든 하시오, 영감.\" 존이 받아쳤어. \"그라고 가라앉히는 게 나을 화딱지를 괜히 돋우지 않게 조심하슈.\"\n\n\"네놈이 누군지는,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그 말투를 보고 알겠다. 그리고 저놈은\" (스마이크를 가리키며) \"그 몰골을 보고 알겠고.\"\n\n\"저 사람한테 말 걸지 마요.\" 니콜라스가 목소리를 되찾으며 말했어. \"그건 못 참아요. 저 사람 말을 듣지 않을 겁니다. 나는 저 사람을 몰라요. 저 사람이 더럽히는 공기를 마실 수가 없어요. 저 사람이 여기 있는 건 내 누이에 대한 모욕이에요. 저 사람을 보는 것조차 수치스럽고요. 못 견디겠어요.\"\n\n\"가만있게!\" 존이 그의 가슴에 묵직한 손을 얹으며 소리쳤어.\n\n\"그럼 당장 저 사람을 물러가게 해요.\" 니콜라스가 버둥대며 말했어. \"내가 손을 대진 않을 거예요. 하지만 저 사람은 나가야 해요. 여기 두지 않겠어요. 존, 존 브라우디, 여기가 내 집이 아닙니까, 내가 어린애예요? 저 사람이 저렇게 서서,\" 니콜라스가 분노로 활활 타오르며 외쳤어. \"저 사람의 시커멓고 비열한 속을 다 아는 사람들을 저렇게 태연히 내려다보고 있으면, 나는 미쳐 버릴 거예요.\"\n\n이 모든 외침에 존 브라우디는 한마디도 대꾸하지 않았어. 그저 니콜라스를 붙든 손을 놓지 않았지. 그리고 니콜라스가 다시 잠잠해지자 입을 열었어.\n\n\"자네 생각보다 할 말도, 들을 말도 더 많아.\" 존이 말했어. \"나도 벌써 낌새를 챘다니께. 저기 문밖에 어른거리는 게 뭐여? 자, 선상님, 모습을 드러내 보슈, 양반. 부끄러워 말고. 자, 영감, 선상님 좀 나오게 합시다, 어서.\"\n\n이 호통을 듣자, 들어오기에 알맞은 때가 되어 효과 있게 나타날 수 있을 때까지 복도에서 머뭇거리던 스퀴어스 씨는, 별수 없이 좀 위신 없고 슬그머니 기어드는 꼴로 모습을 드러냈어. 그 꼴에 존 브라우디가 어찌나 날카롭고 진심 어린 즐거움으로 깔깔 웃어 대던지, 케이트조차 그 자리의 온갖 고통과 불안과 놀라움 속에서, 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채로도 따라 웃고 싶은 마음이 들 정도였지.\n\n\"실컷 즐기셨소, 양반?\" 마침내 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"지끔으로선 거진 다 즐겼소이다.\" 존이 대꾸했어.\n\n\"기다리지.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"천천히 하게나.\"\n\n랄프는 완전히 조용해질 때까지 기다렸어. 그러고는 니클비 부인 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸지만, 케이트한테 열렬한 눈길을 던졌어. 마치 케이트한테 미치는 효과를 더 살피고 싶다는 듯이 말이야. 그러고는 입을 열었어.\n\n\"자, 부인, 내 말을 들으시오. 댁의 그 아들 녀석이 나한테 보낸 그 거창한 일장 연설에 부인이 한몫 끼었다고는 생각하지 않소. 저 녀석한테 휘둘리는 처지에서 부인한테 손톱만큼이라도 자기 의지가 있다고는 믿지 않으니까. 부인의 충고든, 의견이든, 바람이든, 소원이든, 자연과 이치로 따져 마땅히 (안 그러면 그 풍부한 경험이 다 무슨 소용이오?) 저 녀석이 새겨들어야 할 것들이, 손톱만큼이라도 영향이나 무게가 있다고도, 한순간이라도 고려된다고도 믿지 않소.\"\n\n니클비 부인은 고개를 절레절레 젓고 한숨을 쉬었어. 거기에 분명 일리가 있다는 듯이 말이야.\n\n\"바로 그래서,\" 랄프가 말을 이었어. \"부인한테 말을 하는 거요. 한편으론 그래서고, 또 한편으론, 내가 어쩔 수 없이 의절해야 했던 비뚤어진 애송이, 그러고는 나중에 제 어린 위세를 부리며 — 하! 하! — 도리어 나를 의절한 척하는 그 녀석의 행실 때문에 망신당하고 싶지 않아서, 오늘 밤 여기 온 거요. 또 다른 동기도 있소. 인정에서 우러난 동기지. 나는 여기 왔소,\" 랄프가 신랄하고 의기양양한 미소를 띠고 둘레를 둘러보며 말했어. 마치 이 말을 하는 즐거움을 놓치기 싫다는 듯, 그 말 한마디 한마디를 음미하고 곱씹으면서 말이야. \"한 아비에게 그 자식을 돌려주려고 말이오. 그렇소, 양반.\" 그는 니콜라스의 낯빛이 변하는 걸 보고는, 열심히 앞으로 몸을 숙이며 니콜라스를 향해 말을 이었어. \"한 아비에게 그 자식을 돌려주려고 왔소. 그 아들을 말이야, 양반. 자네가 함정에 빠뜨리고, 가로채고, 굽이굽이마다 지키고 섰던 그 아들 말이야. 언젠가 저 애가 얼마 안 되는 비참한 돈푼이라도 갖게 되면 그걸 빼앗으려는 비열한 속셈으로 말이지.\"\n\n\"그건, 당신도 거짓말인 줄 알면서 하는 소리예요.\" 니콜라스가 당당하게 말했어.\n\n\"이건, 내가 진실을 말하는 줄 알면서 하는 소리지. 저 애 아비를 여기 데려왔다.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어.\n\n\"여기!\" 스퀴어스가 앞으로 나서며 비웃었어. \"들었나? 여기! 내가 그랬지, 저 애 아비가 나타나서 저 애를 나한테 돌려보낼지 모르니 조심하라고? 거 봐, 저 애 아비는 내 친구야. 저 애는 곧장 나한테 돌아올 거라고. 자, 이제 무슨 말을 할 텐가 — 엉! — 자 — 어디 — 이거에 뭐라고 할 거냔 말이야 — 괜히 헛수고한 게 분하지 않나? 안 분해? 안 분하냐고?\"\n\n\"당신 몸뚱이엔 내가 새겨 준 자국이 몇 개 있을 텐데,\" 니콜라스가 조용히 눈길을 돌리며 말했어. \"그걸 빌미로 떠들고 싶은 만큼 떠들어요. 그걸 지우려면 한참 걸릴 테니까, 스퀴어스 씨.\"\n\n방금 말한 그 훌륭하신 양반은 식탁을 흘끔 쳐다봤어. 이 대꾸에 발끈해서 물주전자나 병이라도 니콜라스 머리에 던질 작정인 양 말이야. 하지만 그런 작정이 있었다 한들 랄프가 가로막았어. 랄프는 스퀴어스의 팔꿈치를 툭 건드리며, 이제 아비를 나오게 해서 제 아들을 데려가라고 하라고 일렀거든.\n\n이건 순전히 즐거워서 하는 일이라, 스퀴어스 씨는 선뜻 따랐어. 그러려고 방을 나간 그는 거의 곧바로 돌아왔는데, 기름기 흐르는 얼굴에 매끈한 사내 하나를 부축하고 왔지. 그 사내는 스퀴어스한테서 떨어져 나오며 스놀리 씨의 모습과 얼굴을 드러냈어. 그러고는 곧장 스마이크한테 다가가, 그 가엾은 녀석의 머리를 제 팔 밑에 끼고 더없이 거칠고 어설프게 끌어안았지. 그러고는 챙 넓은 모자를 팔을 쭉 뻗어 공중에 높이 치켜들었어. 경건한 감사의 표시로 말이야. 그러면서 이렇게 외쳤지. \"이 녀석을 마지막으로 봤을 때, 이렇게 기쁜 만남을 어찌 짐작이나 했겠소! 오, 어찌 짐작이나 했겠소!\"\n\n\"진정하시오.\" 랄프가 무뚝뚝하게 동정하는 투로 말했어. \"이제 자식을 찾았잖소.\"\n\n\"찾았다! 오, 내가 이 애를 찾다니! 정말 내가 이 애를 찾은 거요?\" 스놀리 씨가 도무지 믿기지 않는 듯 외쳤어. \"그래, 여기 있구나, 피와 살이, 피와 살이.\"\n\n\"살은 쥐꼬리만큼밖에 없구먼.\" 존 브라우디가 말했어.\n\n스놀리 씨는 아비의 정에 너무 사로잡혀 이 말을 알아채지도 못했어. 그러고는 자식을 되찾았다는 걸 더 확실히 하려는 듯, 그 머리를 다시 제 팔 밑에 끼고는 거기서 빼지 않았지.\n\n\"대체 뭐였을까,\" 스놀리가 말했어. \"그 훌륭한 청소년 교육자께서 이 애를 우리 집에 데려왔을 때, 이 애한테 그토록 강한 관심이 일었던 게 말이오? 대체 뭐였길래, 제일 좋은 친구들, 자기 목사며 스승한테서 도망친 죄로 이 애를 호되게 벌하고 싶어 온몸이 다 달아올랐던 거요?\"\n\n\"그게 바로 부정(父情)의 본능이지요, 선생.\" 스퀴어스가 한마디 했어.\n\n\"바로 그거였소.\" 스놀리가 맞장구쳤어. \"그 고결한 감정, 옛 로마인과 그리스인의 감정, 또 들판의 짐승과 공중의 새들이 지닌 감정이지요. 가끔 제 새끼를 잡아먹는 토끼하고 수고양이만 빼고 말이오. 내 가슴이 이 애를 향해 사무쳤다오. 아비의 노여움에 휩싸여 이 애한테 무슨 짓을 — 무슨 짓인들 못 했을지 나도 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"그게 다 본성이라는 게 뭔지 보여 주는 거지요, 선생.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어. \"본성이란 참 별난 놈이거든요.\"\n\n\"본성은 거룩한 거지요, 선생.\" 스놀리가 말했어.\n\n\"옳은 말씀입니다.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 짐짓 점잖게 한숨을 쉬며 덧붙였어. \"본성 없이 우리가 대체 어떻게 살아갈지 모르겠어요. 본성이란,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 엄숙하게 말했어. \"말로 그리기보다 마음으로 그리기가 더 쉬운 거지요. 오, 본성 그대로의 상태에 있다는 건 얼마나 복된 일입니까, 선생!\"\n\n이 철학적인 이야기가 오가는 동안, 옆에 있던 사람들은 놀라움에 그저 얼이 빠져 있었어. 한편 니콜라스는 스놀리에게서 스퀴어스로, 스퀴어스에게서 랄프로 날카롭게 눈길을 옮기며, 혐오와 의심과 놀라움 사이에서 갈피를 못 잡고 있었지. 바로 그때, 스마이크가 아비한테서 빠져나와 니콜라스한테로 달아났어. 그러고는 더없이 애절한 말로, 제발 자기를 내주지 말고 당신 곁에서 살다 죽게 해 달라고 애원했지.\n\n\"당신이 이 애 아비라면,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"이 애가 얼마나 망가졌는지 보세요. 그러고도 내가 데려온 그 역겨운 소굴로 이 애를 돌려보낼 작정이라고 말할 수 있어요?\"\n\n\"또 험담이군!\" 스퀴어스가 외쳤어. \"잘 새겨 둬, 자넨 화약이고 총알이고 들일 가치도 없는 놈이지만, 이러나저러나 내 꼭 앙갚음하고 말 테니까.\"\n\n\"그만.\" 스놀리가 막 말을 꺼내려는데 랄프가 가로막았어. \"이 일을 짧게 끝냅시다. 머리 빈 난봉꾼들이랑 여기서 입씨름하지 말고. 이 애는 댁의 아들이오, 댁이 증명할 수 있듯이. 그리고 스퀴어스 씨, 당신은 이 애가 그토록 여러 해 스마이크라는 이름으로 당신한테 있던 바로 그 아이라는 걸 알지. 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"내가 아냐고요!\" 스퀴어스가 받았어. \"모를 리가요?\"\n\n\"좋소.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"여기선 몇 마디면 충분하겠군. 댁은 첫 아내한테서 아들을 하나 얻었소, 스놀리 씨?\"\n\n\"그랬지요.\" 그자가 대답했어. \"그리고 저기 그 애가 서 있고요.\"\n\n\"그건 곧 밝혀집시다.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"댁과 댁의 아내는 헤어졌고, 아이가 한 살 때 아내가 데려가 키웠지. 따로 산 지 한두 해쯤 됐을 때, 아내한테서 그 애가 죽었다는 기별을 받았고, 댁은 그걸 믿었소?\"\n\n\"당연히 믿었지요!\" 스놀리가 대답했어. \"오, 이 기쁨이란 —\"\n\n\"제발 정신 좀 차리시오.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"이건 일이오. 감격에 겨워하면 일에 방해가 된단 말이오. 그 아내는 한 일 년 반쯤 전에, 아니 그쯤 — 그보다 더는 아니고 — 어느 외진 곳에서 죽었소. 한 집안의 가정부로 있던 데서 말이오. 그렇소?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 스놀리가 대답했어.\n\n\"죽기 직전에 댁한테 바로 이 애에 관한 편지인지 고백인지를 써 놓았고, 그게 댁 이름 말고는 달리 받는 데가 적혀 있지 않아서 댁한테만, 그것도 빙 돌아 며칠 전에야 닿았소?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 스놀리가 말했어. \"하나하나 다 정확합니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 고백은,\" 랄프가 말을 이었어. \"이런 내용이지. 아이가 죽었다는 건 댁한테 상처를 주려고 아내가 꾸며 낸 거짓말이었다 — 한마디로, 두 사람이 서로한테 써먹은 듯한 그 괴롭힘의 한 수단이었던 거지 — 아이는 살아 있었지만 머리가 약하고 모자랐다 — 그래서 믿을 만한 사람 편에 요크셔의 어느 싼 학교로 보냈다 — 몇 해 동안 학비를 댔지만, 그러다 형편이 어려워지고 멀리 떠나게 되면서 차차 그 애를 버렸다, 그 일을 용서해 달라고 빌었다 — 그렇소?\"\n\n스놀리는 고개를 끄덕이고 눈을 닦았어. 끄덕이는 건 가볍게, 닦는 건 격렬하게.\n\n\"그 학교가 바로 스퀴어스 씨의 학교였고,\" 랄프가 말을 이었어. \"아이는 스마이크라는 이름으로 거기 맡겨졌소. 생김새가 죄다 빠짐없이 적혀 있고, 날짜도 스퀴어스 씨의 장부와 정확히 맞아떨어지지. 스퀴어스 씨는 지금 댁 집에 묵고 있고, 댁은 그 학교에 아들 둘을 더 보내 두었고. 댁은 이 모든 사실을 그한테 다 알렸고, 그는 제 자식을 납치한 자를 자기한테 소개해 준 사람이라며 댁을 나한테 데려왔고, 나는 댁을 여기로 데려왔소. 그렇소?\"\n\n\"선생은 꼭 좋은 책처럼 말씀하시는구려. 속에 진실 말고는 아무것도 안 든 그런 책 말이오.\" 스놀리가 대답했어.\n\n\"이건 댁의 수첩이고,\" 랄프가 외투에서 수첩 하나를 꺼내며 말했어. \"댁의 첫 혼인 증명서와 아이의 출생 증명서, 그리고 댁 아내의 편지 두 통, 그 밖에 이 진술들을 직접으로든 넌지시로든 뒷받침할 온갖 서류가 다 여기 들어 있소, 그렇소?\"\n\n\"하나도 빠짐없이요, 선생.\"\n\n\"그리고 댁은 이 서류들을 여기서 살펴보는 데 반대하지 않지. 이 사람들이, 댁이 법으로나 이치로나 당장 그 권리를 입증할 힘이 있다는 걸 똑똑히 알게, 또 댁이 더 미룰 것 없이 제 아들에 대한 권리를 되찾게 말이오. 내 말 알아듣겠소?\"\n\n\"제가 직접 말해도 이보다 더 잘 알아듣게는 못 했을 겁니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"그럼 자, 여기 있소.\" 랄프가 수첩을 식탁 위에 툭 던지며 말했어. \"보고 싶으면 보라고 하시오. 그게 원본 서류니까, 살펴보는 동안 댁이 가까이 서 있는 게 좋겠소. 안 그러면 몇 장 잃어버릴지도 모르니.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 랄프는 권하지도 않았는데 앉았어. 그러고는 잠깐 미소로 살짝 벌어졌던 입술을 꼭 다물고, 팔짱을 끼고는, 처음으로 조카를 쳐다봤어.\n\n니콜라스는 그 마지막 비꼼에 발끈해서 그에게 분노 어린 눈길을 쏘아붙였어. 하지만 최대한 자신을 다잡고는, 그 서류들을 꼼꼼히 살펴보기 시작했지. 존 브라우디가 거들었고. 그 서류엔 트집 잡을 만한 데가 하나도 없었어. 증명서들은 교구 장부에서 베껴 낸 것으로 제대로 서명이 되어 있었고, 첫 편지는 정말로 몇 해 전에 쓰여 보관되어 온 듯한 진짜배기 분위기가 났고, 둘째 편지의 글씨체도 그것과 정확히 들어맞았어(죽어 가는 사람이 쓴 거라는 점을 감안하면 말이야). 그 밖에도 뒷받침이 될 만한 자잘한 기록과 메모 쪼가리가 여럿 있었는데, 이것들도 똑같이 트집 잡기 어려웠지.\n\n\"오빠,\" 니콜라스 어깨너머로 불안하게 들여다보던 케이트가 속삭였어. \"이게 정말 사실일 수 있어? 이 진술이 진짜야?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같아.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"자넨 어떻게 보나, 존?\"\n\n존은 머리를 긁적이며 절레절레 흔들기만 했을 뿐, 아무 말도 하지 않았어.\n\n\"부인, 잘 보아 두시오.\" 랄프가 니클비 부인을 향해 말했어. \"이 애가 미성년인 데다 머리도 온전치 못하니, 우리는 오늘 밤 법의 권한으로 무장하고, 법의 졸개들을 한 떼 거느리고 여기 올 수도 있었소. 분명히 그렇게 했을 거요, 부인. 부인과 따님의 마음을 헤아리지 않았더라면 말이오.\"\n\n\"누이의 마음을 참 잘도 헤아려 주셨군요.\" 니콜라스가 누이를 제 쪽으로 끌어당기며 말했어.\n\n\"고맙군.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"자네 칭찬은, 양반, 정말이지 찬사로구먼.\"\n\n\"그래,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"이제 어쩐다? 우리가 움직일 생각을 안 하면 저 삯마차 말들이 감기 들겠는데. 지금 한 놈이 재채기를 하는 통에 현관문이 활짝 열리잖아. 오늘 일정은 뭐요? 스놀리 도련님은 우리랑 같이 가는 거요?\"\n\n\"싫어, 싫어, 싫어요.\" 스마이크가 뒷걸음치며 니콜라스에게 매달려 외쳤어. \"싫어요. 제발, 싫어요. 저 사람이랑은 당신 곁을 떠나지 않을 거예요. 싫어요, 싫어.\"\n\n\"이건 잔인한 짓이오.\" 스놀리가 자기 편들을 도와 달라는 듯 둘러보며 말했어. \"부모가 자식을 이러려고 세상에 낳소?\"\n\n\"부모가 자식을 저러려고 세상에 낳냔 말이여?\" 존 브라우디가 말하며 무뚝뚝하게 스퀴어스를 가리켰어.\n\n\"네놈은 상관 마.\" 그 양반이 비웃듯 제 코를 톡톡 두드리며 받아쳤어.\n\n\"내가 상관 마!\" 존이 말했어. \"그려, 아무도 상관 안 한다는 거 아녀, 선상님. 아무도 상관 안 헝께 너 같은 놈이 떠다니는 거여. 자 그럼, 어디로 오는 거여? 제기랄, 날 짓밟고 오지 말랑께, 양반아.\"\n\n말이 떨어지기 무섭게, 존 브라우디는 스마이크에게 다가오던 스퀴어스 씨의 가슴팍에 팔꿈치를 홱 박았어. 어찌나 잽쌌던지 그 학교 선생은 비틀비틀 뒷걸음치다 랄프 니클비한테 부딪쳤고, 몸을 가누지 못해 그 양반을 의자에서 넘어뜨리고는 그 위로 묵직하게 자빠졌지.\n\n이 우연한 사태가 아주 단호한 행동들의 신호탄이 됐어. 스마이크의 기도와 애원, 여자들의 비명과 외침, 사내들의 격앙이 뒤엉켜 큰 소란이 이는 가운데, 잃었던 아들을 힘으로 끌어내려는 시도가 벌어졌지. 스퀴어스가 정말로 스마이크를 끌어내기 시작했는데, 바로 그때 니콜라스가(그때까진 어떻게 해야 할지 분명히 마음을 정하지 못하고 있었어) 그의 멱살을 잡았어. 그러고는 몇 개 안 남은 이가 머릿속에서 딱딱 부딪칠 만큼 그를 흔들어 댄 뒤, 정중하게 방문까지 데려다주고는, 복도로 떠밀어 내고 문을 닫아 버렸지.\n\n\"자,\" 니콜라스가 나머지 두 사람에게 말했어. \"친절을 베풀어서 친구분을 따라가시지요.\"\n\n\"내 아들을 내놓으시오.\" 스놀리가 말했어.\n\n\"댁의 아들은,\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"스스로 고를 거요. 여기 남는 쪽을 골랐으니, 그렇게 될 거고요.\"\n\n\"애를 안 내놓겠다는 거요?\" 스놀리가 말했어.\n\n\"이 애가 개나 쥐라 해도, 본인 뜻을 거슬러서, 댁이 떠밀어 넣으려는 그런 잔인한 짓의 제물로 내주지는 않을 거요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"누가 촛대로 저 니클비 놈을 후려쳐 줘,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 열쇠 구멍에 대고 외쳤어. \"그리고 누가 내 모자 좀 내와 줘, 저놈이 훔칠 생각이 아니라면 말이야.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 너무 미안하구나.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. 부인은 브라우디 부인과 함께 한구석에서 울며 손가락을 깨물고 서 있었고, 케이트는(낯빛이 아주 창백했지만 더없이 차분하게) 되도록 오빠 곁에 바싹 붙어 있었지. \"이 모든 일이 정말이지 너무 미안하구나. 뭘 어떻게 하는 게 제일 좋을지 정말 모르겠다, 솔직히. 니콜라스가 제일 잘 판단할 테고, 그러길 바란다. 물론 남의 자식을 떠맡아 키운다는 건 힘든 일이지. 스놀리 도련님이 누구 못지않게 쓸모 있고 싹싹하긴 하다만 말이다. 하지만 무슨 다정한 방법으로 해결될 수만 있다면 — 이를테면 스놀리 영감이 저 애 먹고 자는 값으로 얼마쯤 딱 정해서 내기로 하고, 적당한 합의가 이뤄져서, 우리가 일주일에 생선을 두 번, 푸딩을 두 번, 아니면 경단이나 뭐 그런 걸 내기로 한다면 — 모두한테 아주 흡족하고 좋은 일이 될 것 같구나.\"\n\n이 타협안은 눈물과 한숨을 한껏 곁들여 내놓은 것이었지만, 정작 핵심에서는 어긋나 있었어. 그래서 아무도 거기에 신경 쓰지 않았지. 그러자 가엾은 니클비 부인은 브라우디 부인을 붙들고, 그런 방안이 얼마나 좋은지, 또 자기가 충고를 내놓았을 때 사람들이 귀담아듣지 않아서 늘 얼마나 불행한 결과가 빚어지는지를 일러 주기 시작했어.\n\n\"이놈아,\" 스놀리가 겁에 질린 스마이크를 향해 말했어. \"너는 천륜도 모르고, 은혜도 모르고, 사랑스럽지도 못한 놈이다. 내가 사랑해 주고 싶어도 사랑하게 두질 않는구나. 집에 안 갈 테냐, 안 갈 거야?\"\n\n\"싫어요, 싫어요, 싫어요.\" 스마이크가 움츠러들며 외쳤어.\n\n\"저놈은 누구도 사랑한 적이 없어.\" 스퀴어스가 열쇠 구멍에 대고 악을 썼어. \"나도 사랑한 적이 없지. 천사 바로 옆자리쯤 되는 워크퍼드도 사랑한 적이 없고. 그러니 제 아비를 사랑하길 어찌 바라겠나? 저놈은 절대 제 아비를 사랑 안 해, 절대. 아비가 있다는 게 뭔지도 몰라. 도통 이해를 못 해. 그런 게 저놈한텐 없거든.\"\n\n스놀리 씨는 일 분 내내 제 아들을 뚫어지게 바라봤어. 그러고는 손으로 두 눈을 가리고, 다시 한번 모자를 공중에 치켜들고는, 아들의 시커먼 배은망덕을 깊이 한탄하는 데 골몰한 척했지. 그런 다음 팔로 두 눈을 슥 훔치고는, 스퀴어스 씨의 모자를 집어 들었어. 그러고는 그 모자를 한쪽 팔에, 제 모자를 다른 쪽 팔에 끼고, 느릿느릿 서글프게 걸어 나갔지.\n\n\"자네의 그 로맨스는, 양반,\" 랄프가 잠깐 머뭇거리며 말했어. \"이걸로 다 깨진 셈이군. 정체 모를 사람도 아니고, 지체 높은 가문 후손이 박해받는 것도 아니고, 그저 가난하고 보잘것없는 장사꾼의 약하고 모자란 아들일 뿐이지. 자네의 그 동정심이 명백한 사실 앞에서 어떻게 녹아내리는지 두고 보겠네.\"\n\n\"두고 보시지요.\" 니콜라스가 문 쪽으로 손짓하며 말했어.\n\n\"그리고 내 말 믿게, 양반,\" 랄프가 덧붙였어. \"나는 자네가 오늘 밤 저 애를 내줄 거라곤 한 번도 생각 안 했네. 자존심, 고집, 고운 마음씨라는 평판, 그게 다 그걸 막았을 테니까. 그것들은 무너져야 하네, 양반. 낮아지고, 짓밟혀야 해. 머잖아 그렇게 될 거고. 가장 가혹한 형태의 법, 그 질질 끌고 진을 빼는 불안과 비용, 시시각각의 고문, 지치는 낮과 잠 못 드는 밤 — 그것들로 자네를 시험하고, 지금은 아무리 강하다고 자부하는 그 오만한 기개를 꺾어 놓겠네. 그리고 자네가 이 집을 지옥으로 만들고, 저기 저 가엾은 것한테 이 시련을 떠안길 때(자넨 그럴 테지, 내가 자넬 아니까), 또 지금 자네를 갓 날개 돋친 영웅쯤으로 여기는 사람들한테 그럴 때, 우리 둘 사이의 묵은 셈을 따져 보자고. 누가 빚진 쪽인지, 누가 끝에 가서, 온 세상 앞에서까지 잘되는지 어디 보세.\"\n\n랄프 니클비는 물러갔어. 하지만 이 마지막 한마디를 얼마쯤 들은 스퀴어스 씨는, 이때쯤 거의 전에 없던 무력한 악의로 잔뜩 약이 올라 있던 터라, 거실 문으로 도로 돌아오지 않을 수가 없었지. 그러고는 정말로 갖가지 찡그린 낯과 흉측한 표정을 지어 가며 열댓 번이나 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰어 댔어. 니콜라스의 몰락과 패배를 의기양양하게 확신한다는 표시로 말이야.\n\n이 전쟁 춤을 다 추고 나서 — 그 춤에서 짧은 바지와 큼지막한 장화가 아주 눈에 띄는 모양새를 했지 — 스퀴어스 씨는 친구들을 뒤따라갔어. 그리고 식구들만 남아 방금 벌어진 일들을 곱씹게 됐지.\n\n# CHAPTER 46\n\n니콜라스가 처한 괴롭고 난처한 처지를 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에, 그는 마음씨 좋은 형제에게 이 일을 솔직하게 털어놓는 데 한시도 지체하면 안 되겠다고 마음먹었어. 그래서 이튿날 일이 끝날 무렵 찰스 치어리블 씨와 단둘이 있게 된 첫 기회를 놓치지 않고, 스마이크의 짤막한 사연을 들려줬지. 그러고는 조심스럽지만 단호하게 자기 바람을 밝혔어. 자기가 말한 그런 사정이라면, 부모와 자식 사이에 끼어드는 극단적인 길을 택해서 아버지에게 불복하는 자식 편을 들어 준 일을 이 착한 노신사가 정당하다고 봐주기를 바란다고. 비록 그 아이가 아버지를 무서워하고 끔찍해하는 마음이 너무나 거슬리고 부자연스러운 일처럼 보이고, 또 틀림없이 그렇게 떠벌려질 테고, 그래서 그 아이를 거든 사람들이 온 세상의 미움과 손가락질을 받기 딱 좋은 처지가 된다 해도 말이야.\n\n\"그 사람을 무서워하는 마음이 어찌나 뿌리 깊은지,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"정말 그 사람 아들이 맞는지 저는 도무지 믿기지가 않습니다. 그 애 가슴속에는 아버지를 향한 애틋한 정이 단 한 조각도 자연이 심어 놓지 않은 것 같아요. 그런데 자연은 결코 잘못을 저지르지 않잖습니까.\"\n\n\"이보게,\" 찰스 형이 대답했어. \"자네는 아주 흔한 실수를 저지르고 있네. 자연과는 눈곱만큼도 상관없고 자연이 책임질 일도 아닌 것을 자연 탓으로 돌리고 있단 말이야. 사람들은 자연을 무슨 추상적인 것인 양 입에 올리면서, 정작 그러는 동안 무엇이 자연스러운지를 놓쳐 버리지. 여기 한 가엾은 아이가 있네. 부모의 보살핌이라곤 한 번도 받아 본 적 없고, 평생 고생과 슬픔 말고는 거의 아무것도 몰랐던 아이지. 그런 아이 앞에 아버지라는 사람이 나타나서, 첫마디로 그 짧은 행복을 끝장내겠다고, 옛날의 그 비참한 운명으로 다시 처넣겠다고, 그리고 평생 단 하나뿐인 친구 — 그게 바로 자네지 — 그 친구한테서 떼어 놓겠다고 으름장을 놓는단 말일세. 이런 마당에 자연이 그 아이 가슴속에 아버지에게로 끌리고 자네한테서 멀어지라고 부추기는 은밀한 속삭임을 단 하나라도 심어 놓는다면, 그건 자연이 거짓말쟁이에 바보라는 얘기지.\"\n\n니콜라스는 노신사가 이렇게 따뜻하게 말해 주는 게 너무 기뻤어. 그래서 같은 뜻으로 무슨 말을 더 해 주지 않을까 싶어 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았지.\n\n\"바로 그 똑같은 실수가 이런저런 모습으로 어디서나 내 눈에 띄거든,\" 찰스 형이 말했어. \"사랑을 보여 준 적 없는 부모가 자식들한테 타고난 정이 없다고 불평하지. 도리를 다한 적 없는 자식들이 부모한테 타고난 정이 없다고 불평하고. 또 그 둘이 다 너무 가엾은 처지라 그 정이라는 게 햇볕을 충분히 못 받아 자라지도 못했는데, 법을 만드는 사람들은 그 부모와 자식을 두고 떠들썩하게 훈계를 늘어놓으며 천륜이 무시당했다고 부르짖지. 이보게, 타고난 정과 본능이란 전능하신 분의 작품 가운데 가장 아름다운 것일세. 하지만 그분의 다른 아름다운 작품들과 마찬가지로, 잘 길러 주고 보살펴 줘야 하는 거야. 안 그러면 그게 아예 가려져 버리고 새로운 감정이 그 자리를 차지하는 게 오히려 자연스러운 일이지. 땅에서 나는 가장 달콤한 것들도 돌보지 않고 내버려 두면 잡초와 가시덤불에 뒤덮이고 마는 것처럼 말이야. 우리가 이 점을 좀 생각하게 됐으면 좋겠어. 그래서 마땅한 때에 천륜의 도리를 조금 더 기억하고, 엉뚱한 때에 그걸 조금 덜 떠들었으면 싶네.\"\n\n이렇게 말하다 보니 잔뜩 열이 오른 찰스 형은 잠시 멈춰 좀 식혔어. 그러고는 다시 말을 이었지.\n\n\"이보게, 자네 얘기를 듣고도 내가 별로 놀라지 않으니 자네가 의아할 거야. 그건 쉽게 풀리는 일일세. 자네 삼촌이 오늘 아침에 여기 왔었거든.\"\n\n니콜라스는 얼굴이 붉어지며 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러섰어.\n\n\"그래,\" 노신사가 책상을 콕콕 두드리며 말했어. \"여기, 이 방에 말이야. 그 사람은 이치도, 정도, 정의도 도무지 들으려 하지 않더군. 하지만 네드 형이 호되게 몰아붙였지. 이보게, 네드 형은 포장 돌멩이도 녹일 만한 사람이야.\"\n\n\"그분이 온 게—\" 니콜라스가 말을 꺼냈어.\n\n\"자네를 헐뜯으러 왔지,\" 찰스 형이 대답했어. \"비방과 거짓말로 우리 귀를 더럽히려고 말이야. 하지만 헛걸음이었고, 오히려 따끔한 진실 몇 마디를 귀에 담아 가지고 갔지. 이보게 니클비 군, 네드 형은 — 우리 네드 형은 완벽한 사자 같은 사람이야. 팀 링컨워터도 마찬가지지. 팀은 아주 사자 같은 친구야. 처음엔 팀을 들여보내 그 사람을 상대하게 했는데, '잭 로빈슨' 하고 말할 새도 없이 팀이 그 사람한테 달려들었다네.\"\n\n\"매일같이 제게 이렇게 깊은 은혜를 베푸시니, 제가 무엇으로 다 감사를 드려야 할지요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"이 일에 대해 입을 다물어 주면 되네,\" 찰스 형이 대답했어. \"자네는 바로잡힐 걸세. 적어도 부당한 일을 당하진 않을 거야. 자네와 관계된 누구도 부당한 일을 당하지 않을 걸세. 그들은 자네 머리카락 하나, 그 아이 머리카락 하나, 자네 어머니나 누이의 머리카락 하나도 건드리지 못해. 내가 그렇게 말했고, 네드 형도 그렇게 말했고, 팀 링컨워터도 그렇게 말했어. 우리 모두가 그렇게 말했으니, 다 같이 그렇게 할 걸세. 그 아버지라는 사람을 만나 봤어 — 정말 아버지가 맞다면 말이지. 뭐 맞긴 맞겠지. 그 사람은 야만인에다 위선자야, 니클비 군. 내가 그랬지. '당신은 야만인이오.' 그렇게 말했네. '당신은 야만인이오' 하고 말이야. 그리고 그렇게 말해 줘서 아주 기뻐. 그 사람더러 야만인이라고 말해 준 게 정말이지 아주 기쁘다네!\"\n\n이쯤 되자 찰스 형은 어찌나 화가 잔뜩 달아올랐던지, 니콜라스는 이제 한마디 끼어들어도 되겠다 싶었어. 그런데 막상 그러려는 순간, 치어리블 씨가 부드럽게 그의 팔에 손을 얹더니 의자를 가리켰어.\n\n\"이 얘기는 당분간 여기서 끝내세,\" 노신사가 얼굴을 닦으며 말했어. \"한마디도 다시 꺼내지 말게. 다른 얘기를 하려고 하네, 은밀한 얘기야, 니클비 군. 우리 다시 차분해져야 해, 차분해져야 한다고.\"\n\n방을 두세 번 가로질러 거닐던 그는 다시 자리에 앉았어. 그러고는 의자를 니콜라스가 앉은 의자 쪽으로 바짝 당기며 말했지.\n\n\"이보게, 자네한테 은밀하고 미묘한 임무를 하나 맡기려 하네.\"\n\n\"저보다 훨씬 유능한 심부름꾼이야 얼마든지 쓰실 수 있겠지요,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"하지만 저보다 더 믿음직하고 열성적인 사람은 못 찾으실 거라고, 감히 말씀드릴 수 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그건 나도 잘 알고 있네,\" 찰스 형이 대답했어. \"아주 잘 알지. 이 임무의 대상이 한 젊은 아가씨라고 말하면, 내가 그렇게 생각한다는 걸 자네도 인정해 줄 걸세.\"\n\n\"젊은 아가씨라고요?\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어. 더 듣고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 간절했던지, 그 순간 온몸이 떨릴 지경이었지.\n\n\"아주 아름다운 아가씨라네,\" 치어리블 씨가 진지하게 말했어.\n\n\"어서 말씀해 주세요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"어떻게 말을 꺼내야 하나 생각 중일세,\" 찰스 형이 말했어. 그 젊은 친구가 보기에는 어딘가 슬프고, 고통에 가까운 표정이었지. \"이보게, 어느 날 아침 자네가 이 방에서 한 젊은 아가씨가 정신을 잃고 쓰러진 걸 우연히 봤지. 기억하나? 어쩌면 잊었을지도 모르겠군.\"\n\n\"아뇨, 천만에요,\" 니콜라스가 다급히 대답했어. \"저는—저는—아주 또렷이 기억하고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그 아가씨가 바로 내가 말하는 사람이야,\" 찰스 형이 말했어. 그 유명한 앵무새처럼, 니콜라스는 머릿속으로는 온갖 생각을 다 했지만 한마디도 입 밖에 낼 수가 없었지.\n\n\"그 아가씨는 한 부인의 딸이라네,\" 치어리블 씨가 말했어. \"그 부인이 아름다운 처녀였고 내가 지금보다 한참 젊었을 때, 나는—이제 와서 이런 말을 입에 올리려니 참 이상하지만—그분을 무척 사랑했었네. 백발이 다 된 남자가 이런 얘길 하니 자네는 웃음이 날지도 모르지. 하지만 그래도 나는 화나지 않아. 내가 자네만큼 젊었을 때라면 나도 똑같이 그랬을 테니까.\"\n\n\"전 정말 그럴 마음 없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 네드 형은,\" 치어리블 씨가 말을 이었어. \"그 부인의 언니와 결혼할 사이였는데, 그 언니가 죽고 말았지. 이제 그 부인도 죽었어, 죽은 지 여러 해 됐네. 그분은 제가 고른 사람과 결혼했지. 그 뒤의 삶이, 하느님은 아시지만 내가 늘 빌었던 만큼 행복했다고 덧붙일 수 있으면 얼마나 좋겠나!\"\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀지만, 니콜라스는 그걸 깨려 들지 않았어.\n\n\"내 마음 가장 깊은 곳에서 (그분을 위해) 늘 바랐던 대로, 시련과 재앙이 그 사람 머리 위에 가볍게만 내렸더라면, 그 부인의 삶은 평화롭고 행복했을 걸세,\" 노신사가 차분하게 말했어. \"하지만 그렇지 못했다는 것만 말해 두면 충분하겠지. 그분은 행복하지 않았어. 둘은 얽히고설킨 고난과 어려움에 빠졌지. 그리고 그분은 죽기 열두 달 전에, 오랜 우정에 기대 보려고 나를 찾아왔네. 슬프게 변하고, 슬프게 달라져서, 고생과 학대에 기가 꺾이고, 마음이 거의 무너진 채로 말이야. 그 사람은 냉큼 돈을 받아 챙겼지. 그분 마음에 단 한 시간의 평화라도 주기 위해서라면 나는 물 쓰듯 아낌없이 내줬을 그 돈을 말이야 — 아니, 그 사람은 더 받아 오라고 그분을 자꾸 돌려보냈어 — 그래 놓고 그 돈을 흥청망청 쓰는 와중에도, 그분이 나한테 매달려 받아 낸 그 도움을 빌미로 모질게 비아냥대고 빈정거렸지. 그분이 자기 선택을 두고 쓰라린 후회에 잠겨 있는 걸 안다느니, 잇속과 허영 때문에 자기랑 결혼한 거라느니 하고 우겨 댔어 (그분이 남편으로 그를 골랐을 무렵 그는 주위에 대단한 친구들을 거느린 명랑한 청년이었거든). 한마디로 그 사람은 순전히 제 방탕 탓에 빚어진 그 파멸과 좌절의 쓰라림을 온갖 부당하고 모진 수단으로 그분에게 죄다 퍼부어 댔던 거야. 그 시절 이 젊은 아가씨는 그저 어린아이였네. 그 뒤로 나는 그 아가씨를 다시는 못 봤어, 자네도 본 그날 아침까지는 말이야. 그런데 내 조카 프랭크가—\"\n\n니콜라스는 흠칫 놀랐어. 그러고는 끼어든 것을 우물우물 사과하며, 어른께 어서 말씀을 이어 가시라고 청했지.\n\n\"—내 조카 프랭크 말일세,\" 치어리블 씨가 다시 말을 이었어. \"그 애가 영국에 돌아온 지 이틀도 안 돼서 우연히 그 아가씨와 마주쳤는데, 거의 일 분도 안 돼서 그만 놓쳐 버렸다네. 그 아가씨 아버지는 빚쟁이들을 피해 어딘가 은밀한 곳에 숨어 지냈지. 병과 가난 사이에서 죽음의 문턱까지 내몰린 채로 말이야. 그리고 그 아가씨는, 어린아이였는데 — 하늘의 모든 뜻이 얼마나 지혜로운지를 우리가 모른다면, 더 나은 사람한테 갔어야 할 아이라고 여길 뻔했지 — 그 아버지를 부양하려고 궁핍과 천대를, 그토록 어리고 여린 마음에 가장 끔찍할 온갖 것을 꿋꿋이 견뎌 냈던 거야. 이보게,\" 찰스 형이 말했어. \"그 아가씨는 이 역경 속에서 충직한 사람 하나의 시중을 받았네. 옛날에 그 집안에서 부엌데기로 일하던 가난한 처녀였는데, 그때는 단 하나 남은 하인이었지. 하지만 그 마음의 참됨과 충실함으로 치자면 — 그 처녀는 — 아! 팀 링컨워터의 아내가 되고도 남을 사람이었어, 그렇고말고!\"\n\n말로는 다 담을 수 없는 열정과 흐뭇함으로 그 가난한 하녀에 대한 찬사를 늘어놓던 찰스 형은, 의자에 등을 기대고는 남은 이야기를 한결 차분하게 들려주었어.\n\n요지인즉 이런 거였어. 그 아가씨는 돌아가신 어머니 친구들이 내미는 영구적인 도움과 부양 제안을 다 자존심을 지키며 마다했지. 그 제안들이 다 친구라곤 하나도 안 남은 비참한 아버지를 버리는 걸 조건으로 달았기 때문이야. 게다가 그 아가씨는, 아버지가 미워하던 그 참되고 고결한 마음 — 가장 크고 순수한 선함 때문에 도리어 오해와 험담에 휘말려 깊은 누명을 쓴 그 마음 — 에다 대고 아버지 대신 도움을 구하는 일을, 타고난 섬세함으로 본능처럼 꺼렸어. 그래서 이 어린 처녀는 홀로, 아무 도움 없이, 제 두 손으로 일해서 아버지를 먹여 살리려 발버둥을 쳤던 거야. 가난과 고난의 가장 깊은 바닥을 헤치며 고생하면서도, 그 일에서 한순간도 곁눈질하지 않았고, 지난날의 위안 어린 추억이나 앞날의 희망 한 자락 없이 짜증과 우울에 빠진 병자에게 시달리면서도 지치지 않았으며, 자기가 마다한 안락을 두고 투덜대거나 제 발로 떠안은 모진 팔자를 한탄하지도 않았지. 더 행복하던 시절에 익힌 자그마한 재주는 하나하나 다 이 목적에 동원되었고, 오직 이 한 가지 목표로 향했어. 꼬박 두 해 동안, 낮에는 물론 밤에도 자주 고생하며 바늘과 연필과 펜을 들고 일했지. 또 입주하지 않는 가정교사로 일하면서, 여자들이 — 게다가 딸까지 둔 여자들이 — 같은 여자가 그런 처지로 봉사할 때 곧잘 가하기 좋아하는 온갖 변덕과 모욕을 참아 냈어. 마치 어쩔 수 없이 부려야 하는 그 뛰어난 지성을 시샘하기라도 하듯이 말이야 — 그런 모욕은 백 번 가운데 아흔아홉 번은 자기들보다 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 잘난 사람들 머리 위에 퍼부어지는 것이고, 아무리 매정한 사기 도박꾼이라도 제 마부한테 차마 그러지 못할 만큼 지독한 것이지. 꼬박 두 해 동안 이 온갖 일을 죄다 해내며 어느 하나에도 지치지 않았건만, 그 아가씨는 제 삶의 단 하나뿐인 목표를 이루지 못했어. 그러다 쌓이고 쌓인 어려움과 좌절에 짓눌린 끝에, 어머니의 옛 친구를 찾아내, 터질 듯한 가슴을 안고 마침내 그에게 속을 털어놓을 수밖에 없었던 거지.\n\n\"내가 가난했더라면,\" 찰스 형이 눈을 반짝이며 말했어. \"이보게 니클비 군, 내가 가난했더라면 — 다행히 그렇지 않다만 — 그 아가씨를 돕기 위해 가장 흔한 생필품마저도 안 쓰고 견뎠을 걸세 (물론 그런 처지면 누구나 그러겠지). 그런데 사정이 사정이라, 이게 어려운 일이란 말이야. 그 아버지가 죽었다면 더없이 쉬울 텐데. 그러면 그 아가씨가 네드 형과 내가 마련해 줄 가장 행복한 보금자리를 함께 누리며 그 집을 환하게 밝힐 텐데, 마치 우리 딸이나 누이라도 되는 것처럼 말이야. 하지만 그 아버지가 아직 살아 있지. 아무도 그 사람은 도울 수가 없어. 수천 번도 더 시도해 봤지만 말이야. 다들 까닭 없이 그 사람을 저버린 게 아니라는 걸 나도 알아.\"\n\n\"그 아가씨를 설득해서—\" 니콜라스는 여기까지 말하다 멈칫했어.\n\n\"아버지를 떠나게 한단 말인가?\" 찰스 형이 말했어. \"자식더러 부모를 버리라고 누가 권할 수 있겠나? 가끔씩만 아버지를 보면 어떻겠느냐는, 그 정도로 한정한 권유는 그 아가씨한테 해 봤지 — 내가 한 건 아니고 — 하지만 늘 결과는 똑같았어.\"\n\n\"그 사람은 아가씨한테 다정합니까?\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"딸의 정을 보답해 줍니까?\"\n\n\"진정한 다정함, 사려 깊고 자기를 희생하는 다정함이란 그 사람 천성에 없어,\" 치어리블 씨가 대답했어. \"그래도 자기가 아는 만큼의 다정함으로는 딸을 대하는 것 같긴 해. 그 어머니는 온화하고 정 많고 사람을 잘 믿는 사람이었어. 그 사람이 결혼할 때부터 죽을 때까지 어떤 남자 못지않게 모질고 함부로 그분에게 상처를 줬건만, 그분은 그 사람 사랑하기를 끝내 멈추지 않았지. 그분은 임종의 자리에서 딸에게 그 사람을 보살펴 달라고 당부했어. 그 딸은 그 말을 한 번도 잊은 적 없고, 앞으로도 잊지 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"그 사람한테 영향력을 좀 미칠 순 없습니까?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"내가 말인가, 이보게! 세상에서 가장 안 될 사람이지. 그 사람은 나를 어찌나 시샘하고 미워하는지, 만약 제 딸이 나한테 속을 털어놨다는 걸 알면 갖은 비난으로 그 아가씨 삶을 비참하게 만들 걸세. 하기야 — 이게 그 사람 성격의 모순이자 이기심인데 — 그 아가씨가 가진 돈이 죄다 나한테서 나온 줄 알면서도, 그 얼마 안 되는 밑천을 제멋대로 펑펑 써서 채울 수 있는 자기 욕심이라면 단 하나도 마다하지 않을 위인이야.\"\n\n\"천륜도 모르는 악당이군요!\" 니콜라스가 분개하며 말했어.\n\n\"모진 말은 쓰지 마세,\" 찰스 형이 부드러운 목소리로 말했어. \"그저 이 아가씨가 놓인 처지에 우리를 맞추자고. 내가 겨우 설득해서 그 아가씨가 받게 한 도움도, 그 아가씨가 간곡히 청하는 바람에 아주 잔돈푼으로 조금씩 나눠 줄 수밖에 없었어. 그 아버지가 돈을 너무 쉽게 구할 수 있다는 걸 알면 평소보다 더 헤프게 흥청망청 써 댈까 봐서지. 그 아가씨는 이것마저도 받으려고 남몰래, 밤을 틈타 오가고 또 오갔어. 그런데 이보게 니클비 군, 나는 일이 이런 식으로 흘러가는 걸 도무지 못 견디겠어, 정말 못 견디겠단 말일세.\"\n\n그러고 나서 조금씩 조금씩 드러나는 거였어. 이 쌍둥이 형제가 그 착하고 늙은 머리를 맞대고, 이 젊은 아가씨를 가장 섬세하고 사려 깊은 방식으로 도울 갖가지 계획과 궁리를 짜내 왔다는 게 말이야. 그것도 아버지가 그 도움의 출처를 의심하지 못하게끔 말이지. 그래서 마침내 이런 결론에 이르렀던 거야. 가장 좋은 방법은, 그 아가씨가 그린 자그마한 그림이며 장식 세공품을 비싼 값에 사들이는 척하면서 그걸 끊임없이 주문해 주는 거라고. 그런데 이 목적과 의도를 이루려면 누군가가 그런 물건을 거래하는 상인 행세를 해야 했어. 그래서 한참 의논한 끝에, 이 역할을 맡을 사람으로 니콜라스를 꼽았던 거지.\n\n\"그 사람은 나를 알아,\" 찰스 형이 말했어. \"그리고 네드 형도 알지. 그러니 우리 둘 다 안 되네. 프랭크는 아주 좋은 녀석이야 — 아주 훌륭한 녀석이지 — 하지만 이런 미묘한 일에는 좀 들뜨고 경솔하지 않을까 걱정이야. 어쩌면 그 애가 — 한마디로, 너무 마음이 쉽게 흔들려서 (그 아가씨는 아름다운 사람이거든. 그 가엾은 어머니가 꼭 그랬듯이 말이야) 제 마음을 미처 추스르기도 전에 그 아가씨한테 반해 버려서는, 우리가 보잘것없는 손길로나마 차차 행복하게 해 주려는 그 순결한 가슴에 아픔과 슬픔을 가져다줄까 봐 그래. 그 애는 처음 우연히 그 아가씨와 마주쳤을 때 그분 처지에 유난히 마음을 쓰더군. 그 애한테 이것저것 물어본 걸로 미루어 보면, 그 애가 소동을 벌여서 자네랑 처음 알게 된 그 일도 바로 그 아가씨 때문이었던 것 같아.\"\n\n니콜라스는 자기도 전부터 그런 일이 있었을지 모른다고 짐작했었다고 더듬더듬 말했어. 그리고 그런 생각이 어쩌다 들었는지 설명하느라, 자기가 그 아가씨를 직접 본 게 언제 어디서였는지 이야기했지.\n\n\"자, 그래서 보다시피,\" 찰스 형이 말을 이었어. \"그 애는 안 되는 거야. 팀 링컨워터는 아예 말도 안 되고. 이보게, 팀은 워낙 불같은 친구라 도무지 참지를 못해서, 그 자리에 들어선 지 오 분도 안 돼서 아버지랑 한판 붙고 말 걸세. 자네는 모를 거야, 무언가가 팀의 감정을 세게 건드리면 그가 어떤 사람이 되는지를 말이야. 그땐 무시무시하다네, 팀 링컨워터는 정말 무시무시해. 그런데 자네라면 우리가 가장 굳게 믿을 수 있거든. 자네한테서는 — 적어도 내가 봤지, 뭐 그게 그거지만. 나랑 네드 형 사이엔 아무 차이도 없으니까. 다만 네드 형이 세상에 둘도 없이 훌륭한 사람이고, 온 세상에 그런 사람은 없고 앞으로도 없을 거란 것만 빼면 말이야 — 자네한테서는 가정적인 미덕과 정, 그리고 섬세한 감정을 봤어. 그게 바로 이런 일에 딱 맞는 자질이지. 그러니 자네가 적임자야.\"\n\n\"그 아가씨는 말입니다,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. 너무 멋쩍어서 아무 말이라도 꺼내기가 여간 힘들지 않았지. \"그분은—그분도—이 죄 없는 속임수에 한편입니까?\"\n\n\"그래, 그래,\" 치어리블 씨가 대답했어. \"적어도 자네가 우리 쪽에서 왔다는 건 알지. 하지만 자네가 이따금씩 사들이는 그 자그마한 작품들을 우리가 팔아 줄 거라는 건 몰라. 그리고 어쩌면, 자네가 그걸 아주 잘 해낸다면 (그러니까, 아주아주 잘 해낸다면 말일세) 어쩌면 그 아가씨가 우리가—우리가 그걸로 이문을 남긴다고 믿게 될지도 몰라. 응? 응?\"\n\n이렇게 천진하고 더없이 다정한 순박함 속에서 찰스 형은 너무도 행복해했어. 또 그 아가씨가 자기한테 신세 진 게 없다고 여기게 될지도 모른다는 그 가능성에, 그는 분명 한껏 들떠서 그렇게나 기뻐했지. 그래서 니콜라스는 그 일에 의심 한 자락도 내비치지 않았어.\n\n그런데 그러는 내내, 그의 혀끝에는 고백 하나가 맴돌고 있었어. 치어리블 씨가 조카를 이 일에 쓰면 안 된다며 든 바로 그 이유들이, 적어도 똑같은 무게와 타당성으로 자기 자신한테도 들어맞는다는 고백 말이야. 그래서 그는 백 번도 더 자기 진짜 마음을 털어놓고 이 일에서 빼 달라고 청할 뻔했지. 하지만 그 충동의 뒤꿈치를 밟듯 번번이 또 다른 충동이 따라붙어, 그만두라고, 비밀을 가슴속에만 묻어 두라고 부추겼어. '내가 왜,' 니콜라스는 생각했어. '내가 왜 이 인자하고 고결한 계획에 훼방을 놓아야 하지? 내가 이 착하고 사랑스러운 사람을 사랑하고 우러러본다 한들 어떻단 말인가. 그 아가씨가 나한테 반할 위험이 있다는 둥 정색하고 떠벌리면, 나야말로 더없이 거만하고 속 빈 멋쟁이로 보이지 않겠나? 게다가, 나 자신을 못 믿는단 말인가? 명예를 걸고 이런 생각을 억눌러야 하지 않나? 이 훌륭한 분이 내 최선의, 가장 진심 어린 봉사를 받을 자격이 있는데, 나 자신을 위한 어떤 잿속이 그걸 막아서야 되겠나?'\n\n이런 물음들을 스스로에게 던지며, 니콜라스는 마음속으로 아주 힘주어 \"안 돼!\" 하고 답했어. 그러고는 자기가 더없이 양심적이고 영광스러운 순교자라고 자신을 구슬리면서, 사실 제 마음을 조금만 더 꼼꼼히 들여다봤다면 도저히 거역할 수 없는 일임을 알았을 그 일을 기꺼이 떠맡기로 마음을 굳혔지. 우리가 스스로를 속여 넘기며, 제 약점을 굳건하고 더없이 너그러운 미덕으로 둔갑시키는 솜씨란 바로 이런 거야!\n\n물론 치어리블 씨는 그 젊은 친구의 머릿속에 이런 생각들이 떠오르고 있는 줄은 까맣게 몰랐어. 그래서 첫 방문에 필요한 소개장과 지시 사항을 일러 주었지. 그 방문은 이튿날 아침에 하기로 되어 있었어. 그렇게 사전 준비를 다 마치고 가장 철저한 비밀을 단단히 당부받고서, 니콜라스는 그날 밤 집으로 걸어가는 내내 정말이지 깊은 생각에 잠겼어.\n\n치어리블 씨가 그에게 일러 준 곳은, 킹스 벤치 감옥의 '룰스' 안에 있는 초라하고 그리 깨끗하지도 않은 집들이 늘어선 한 줄거리였어. 세인트 조지 들판에 선 오벨리스크에서 몇백 걸음 떨어지지 않은 곳이었지. 룰스란 감옥에 딸린 일종의 자유 구역으로, 열 몇 개쯤 되는 거리를 아우르는데, 큰 수수료를 낼 돈을 마련할 수 있는 채무자들이 거기에 살도록 허락된 곳이야. 그 수수료가 채권자들한테는 한 푼도 돌아가지 않는데도 말이지. 이게 다 저 똑똑한 법들의 어진 조항 덕분이라네. 그 똑같은 법이, 돈을 한 푼도 마련하지 못하는 채무자한테는, 인간을 더럽히는 가장 끔찍한 죄를 지은 흉악범에게도 베푸는 먹을 것, 입을 것, 잠자리, 온기조차 주지 않은 채 감옥에서 굶어 죽게 내버려 두지. 법에는 늘 작동하는 그럴듯한 허구가 많지만, 그 가운데 이만큼 그럴듯하고 우습기까지 한 것도 없어. 그 공평무사한 눈에는 사람이 죄다 똑같은 값어치를 지녔고, 주머니 사정 따위는 눈곱만큼도 따지지 않고 모든 법의 혜택을 누구나 똑같이 누릴 수 있다고 여기는, 그런 허구 말이야.\n\n니콜라스는 그런 일들로 머리를 그리 골치 썩이지 않고, 찰스 치어리블 씨가 일러 준 그 집들이 늘어선 줄거리로 발걸음을 옮겼어. 그러고는 무척 더럽고 먼지투성이인 변두리 — 싸구려 연극이며 조개며 진저비어며 짐수레며 채소 가게며 중고품 가게가 주된 풍경을 이루는 듯한 동네 — 를 가로지른 끝에, 마침내 두근거리는 가슴으로 그 집들 앞에 다다랐지. 집집마다 앞에는 자그마한 정원이 있었는데, 다른 건 죄다 손 놓고 내버려 둔 탓에 그저 먼지가 쌓이는 작은 우리 노릇이나 했어. 그러다 모퉁이를 돌아온 바람이 그 먼지를 길로 불어 날리곤 했지. 그 가운데 한 집 앞에 부서진 경첩에 위태롭게 매달린 채, 찾아온 손님을 반쯤은 들이고 반쯤은 밀어내는 흔들거리는 문을 밀어 열고, 니콜라스는 떨리는 손으로 현관문을 두드렸어.\n\n겉보기에 그건 정말이지 허름한 집이었어. 응접실 창은 어둑하고, 블라인드는 거의 없다시피 했지. 더럽고 얇은 무명 커튼이 헐겁고 축 늘어진 줄에 걸린 채 아래쪽 유리창을 가로질러 대롱대롱 매달려 있었어. 문이 열렸을 때도 안쪽이 겉모습의 약속을 저버리지 않더군. 계단에는 빛바랜 카펫이, 복도에는 빛바랜 유포가 깔려 있었거든. 게다가 그 불편함에 더해, 한 신사 '룰러'가 앞쪽 응접실에서 (아직 정오도 안 됐는데) 담배를 뻑뻑 피워 대고 있었어. 한편 집주인 여자는 뒤쪽 응접실 문간에서, 흐트러진 텐트 침대 조각들에 테레빈유를 칠하느라 분주했지. 운 좋게 그 방을 빌리게 된 새 하숙인을 맞을 채비라도 하는 듯이 말이야.\n\n니콜라스는 이런 것들을 두루 살펴볼 시간이 넉넉했어. 하숙인들 심부름을 다니는 어린 사내아이가 부엌 계단을 덜커덕거리며 내려가더니, 어느 깊은 지하실에서인 양 브레이 양의 하녀를 부르며 소리치는 게 들렸거든. 이내 하녀가 나타나서 따라오라고 했는데, 니콜라스는 그저 그 아가씨를 찾았을 뿐인데도 그 자연스러운 결과치고는 어울리지 않을 만큼 잔뜩 긴장하고 어쩔 줄 몰라 하는 기색을 내비쳤어.\n\n아무튼 그는 위층으로 올라갔고, 앞쪽 방으로 안내받았어. 그리고 거기, 창가의 자그마한 탁자 앞에 그 아름다운 처녀가 앉아 있었지. 탁자 위에는 그림 도구가 놓여 있었고, 그 처녀는 거기에 열중하고 있었어. 그토록 니콜라스의 마음을 사로잡았던 그 처녀가, 이제 그가 그 사연에 품게 된 새롭고 강렬한 온갖 관심에 둘러싸여, 그의 눈에는 여태껏 짐작했던 것보다 천 배는 더 아름다워 보였어.\n\n그런데 그 처녀가 초라하게 꾸며진 방 여기저기에 흩어 놓은 그 우아함과 고상함이라니, 니콜라스의 가슴을 어찌나 울렸는지! 꽃이며, 화초며, 새며, 하프며, 지난날엔 그 가락이 훨씬 더 감미롭게 울려 퍼졌을 낡은 피아노 — 집과 그 처녀를 아직 이어 주는 그 끊어진 사슬의 마지막 두 고리를 지켜 내느라 그 처녀가 얼마나 안간힘을 썼을까! 여가 시간에 만든 그 가냘픈 장식 하나하나에는, 여인의 손이 빚은 자그마한 정성스러운 작품마다 깃드는 그 우아한 매력이 가득했고, 거기엔 또 얼마나 많은 묵묵한 인내와 다정한 정이 엮여 있었는지! 그는 마치 그 작은 방에 하늘의 미소가 어린 것만 같았어. 그토록 어리고 여린 사람의 아름다운 헌신이, 둘레의 생명 없는 것들에까지 제 빛을 한 줄기 드리워 그것들을 저 자신처럼 아름답게 만든 것 같았지. 마치 옛 화가들이 죄 없는 세상의 빛나는 천사들을 둘러 그린 그 후광이, 영혼이 그 천사들과 닮은 한 존재의 둘레에 어른거리고, 그 빛이 그의 눈앞에 또렷이 보이는 것만 같았어.\n\n그런데도 니콜라스는 킹스 벤치 감옥의 룰스 안에 있었던 거야! 차라리 그곳이 이탈리아였고, 때가 해 질 녘이었고, 무대가 웅장한 테라스였더라면! 하지만 온 세상 위에 펼쳐진 하늘은 하나뿐이고, 그게 파랗든 흐리든 그 너머의 천국은 똑같으니, 어쩌면 그는 그렇게 생각했다고 양심에 거리낄 필요가 없었는지도 몰라.\n\n그가 모든 걸 한눈에 다 알아챘다고 여겨선 안 돼. 그때까지 그는 안락의자에 베개를 기대고 앉은 병든 남자가 있다는 걸 알아채지 못했거든. 그 남자가 자리에서 안절부절못하며 짜증스레 몸을 뒤척이는 통에, 그제야 그의 눈길을 끌었지.\n\n그는 기껏해야 쉰이 채 안 됐을 텐데, 어찌나 수척한지 훨씬 더 나이 들어 보였어. 그 이목구비에는 한때 잘생겼던 얼굴의 자취가 남아 있었지만, 거기엔 어떤 표정 — 훨씬 더 못난 얼굴조차 한결 호감 가게 만들어 줬을 그런 표정 — 보다, 강하고 격렬한 열정의 타다 남은 잿불이 오히려 더 또렷이 읽혔지. 그 행색은 무척 초췌했고, 팔다리와 몸은 글자 그대로 뼈만 남도록 야위었어. 하지만 그래도 움푹 꺼진 그 커다란 눈에는 옛날의 그 불길 같은 것이 어려 있었지. 그리고 그가, 자리에 앉은 몸을 지탱하는 데 쓰던 듯한 굵은 지팡이로 바닥을 짜증스레 두세 번 쾅쾅 내리치며 딸의 이름을 부를 때, 그 불길이 다시 타오르는 것 같았어.\n\n\"마들렌, 이 사람은 누구냐? 누가 여기서 뭘 원한다는 거야? 누가 낯선 사람한테 우리를 만나도 된다고 했어? 무슨 일이냐?\"\n\n\"제 생각엔—\" 젊은 아가씨가 입을 떼며, 니콜라스의 인사에 대답하느라 조금 당황한 기색으로 고개를 숙였어.\n\n\"넌 늘 생각만 하지,\" 아버지가 짜증스레 받아쳤어. \"무슨 일이냐고?\"\n\n이쯤 되자 니콜라스도 직접 입을 열 만큼 정신을 추슬렀어. 그래서 (미리 그렇게 말하기로 약속한 대로) 손가리개 한 쌍과 오토만에 씌울 채색 벨벳을 좀 사러 왔다고 말했지. 둘 다 더없이 우아한 도안이어야 하고, 시간이나 비용은 조금도 따지지 않는다고 말이야. 또 그는 그림 두 점에 대한 값도 치러야 한다며 거듭 고마워했어. 그러고는 자그마한 탁자로 다가가, 봉투에 접어 넣고 봉인한 지폐 한 장을 그 위에 내려놓았지.\n\n\"돈이 맞는지 봐라, 마들렌,\" 아버지가 말했어. \"그 봉투를 열어 봐, 얘야.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 맞아요, 아빠.\"\n\n\"이리 줘 봐!\" 브레이 씨가 손을 내밀며, 짜증스러운 조바심에 앙상한 손가락을 폈다 오므렸다 하며 말했어. \"어디 보자. 마들렌, 무슨 소릴 하는 거냐? 맞다고? 그런 걸 네가 어떻게 확신하느냐? 오 파운드 — 자, 이게 맞느냐?\"\n\n\"맞아요,\" 마들렌이 아버지 위로 몸을 굽히며 말했어. 그녀가 베개를 매만지느라 어찌나 분주했던지 니콜라스는 그 얼굴을 볼 수 없었어. 하지만 그녀가 몸을 숙일 때, 그는 눈물 한 방울이 떨어지는 걸 본 것 같았지.\n\n\"종을 울려라, 종을 울려,\" 병든 남자가 똑같이 안달하는 기색으로 말했어. 그쪽을 손짓하는데, 그 떨리는 손에 지폐가 바스락거리며 허공에서 소리를 냈지. \"그 애더러 이걸 잔돈으로 바꿔 오라고 해라. 신문도 한 부 사 오고, 포도도 좀 사 오고, 지난주에 마신 그 포도주도 한 병 더 사 오라고 — 그리고 — 그리고 — 지금 당장 필요한 게 절반은 생각이 안 나는구나, 하지만 그 애가 또 나가면 되지. 그것들부터 사 오라고 해, 그것들부터. 자, 마들렌, 얘야, 어서, 어서! 맙소사, 어쩜 이리 굼뜨냐!\"\n\n'그 아가씨한테 필요한 건 하나도 기억 못 하는군!' 니콜라스는 생각했어. 어쩌면 그가 생각한 게 얼마쯤 얼굴에 드러났는지도 몰라. 병든 남자가 잔뜩 가시 돋친 얼굴로 그를 돌아보더니, 영수증을 기다리는 거냐고 따져 물었거든.\n\n\"전혀 상관없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"상관없다고! 그게 무슨 소리요?\" 톡 쏘는 대꾸가 돌아왔어. \"상관없다고! 당신이 이 푼돈을 호의나 선물로 가져온 줄 아쇼? 아니면 거래로, 받은 값어치에 대한 대가로 가져온 줄 아쇼? 빌어먹을, 당신이 다루는 물건에 들어간 시간과 안목을 알아보지 못한다고 해서, 그래 당신 돈을 그냥 거저 주는 거라 생각하쇼? 당신이 지금 누구하고 얘기하고 있는지 알기나 하쇼? 한때는 당신 같은 사람 쉰 명에다 당신네 가진 거 전부를 다 사들이고도 남았을 신사란 말이오. 무슨 뜻으로 하는 소리요?\"\n\n\"저는 그저, 아가씨께서 너그러이 허락만 하신다면 앞으로 이 아가씨와 거래할 일이 많을 테니, 이런 격식으로 번거롭게 해 드리지 않으려는 것뿐입니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"그렇다면 나는, 미안하지만, 격식을 차릴 수 있는 만큼 죄다 차리겠소,\" 아버지가 받아쳤어. \"내 딸은 당신이든 누구든한테서 호의 따위 받을 필요 없소. 거래와 장사에만 딱 매여서, 그 선을 넘지 않도록 해 주시오. 이제 어중이떠중이 장사꾼마다 죄 이 애를 동정하기 시작하시겠다, 이거요? 원, 별꼴이군! 아주 좋아. 마들렌, 얘야, 이 사람한테 영수증을 써 줘라. 그리고 앞으로도 늘 그렇게 해.\"\n\n그녀가 영수증을 쓰는 척하고, 니콜라스가 이렇게 눈앞에 펼쳐진 별난, 하지만 결코 드물지 않은 인물됨을 곱씹는 동안, 때때로 몸이 몹시 아픈 듯하던 그 병자는 의자에 푹 기댄 채 힘없이 투덜거렸어. 그 애가 나간 지 한 시간이 됐다느니, 다들 짜고서 자기를 들들 볶는다느니 하고 말이야.\n\n\"언제,\" 니콜라스가 그 종잇장을 받아 들며 말했어. \"언제 다시 들를까요?\"\n\n이 말은 딸한테 한 거였지만, 아버지가 곧장 대답했어.\n\n\"당신더러 들르라고 청할 때, 그 전엔 안 되오. 귀찮게 들볶지 마시오. 마들렌, 얘야, 이 사람이 언제 다시 오면 되겠느냐?\"\n\n\"아, 한참 동안은 오지 마세요, 서너 주는요. 정말 그럴 필요 없어요. 없어도 돼요.\" 젊은 아가씨가 무척 간곡하게 말했어.\n\n\"아니, 없이 어떻게 지내려고?\" 아버지가 숨소리보다 크지 않은 목소리로 다그쳤어. \"서너 주라니, 마들렌! 서너 주라니!\"\n\n\"그럼 더 빨리, 더 빨리 오세요, 부탁이에요.\" 젊은 아가씨가 니콜라스를 돌아보며 말했어.\n\n\"서너 주라니!\" 아버지가 중얼거렸어. \"마들렌, 도대체 어쩌자고 — 서너 주씩이나 아무것도 안 하겠다니!\"\n\n\"긴 시간이네요, 아가씨.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"당신은 그렇게 생각하쇼?\" 아버지가 성을 내며 받아쳤어. \"내가 구걸이라도 하겠다고 마음먹고, 내가 경멸하는 인간들한테 굽실거리며 도움을 청하기로 한다면야, 서너 달도 긴 시간이 아닐 거요. 서너 해도 긴 시간이 아니지. 알아 두쇼, 그게 다 내가 남한테 기대 살기로 한다면 그렇다는 거요. 하지만 난 그럴 생각이 없으니, 일주일 뒤에 와도 좋소.\"\n\n니콜라스는 젊은 아가씨에게 깊숙이 고개 숙여 인사하고 물러났어. 브레이 씨가 말하는 독립이라는 게 어떤 건지 곱씹으면서, 그리고 인간이라는 천한 흙덩이에 그 사람처럼 독립적인 영혼들이 섞여 있는 일은 부디 드물기를 간절히 빌면서 말이야.\n\n계단을 내려오는데 위쪽에서 가벼운 발소리가 들렸어. 돌아보니 그 젊은 아가씨가 거기 서서 그를 머뭇머뭇 바라보고 있었지. 그를 다시 부를까 말까 망설이는 것 같았어. 이 물음을 푸는 가장 좋은 방법은 곧장 되돌아가는 거였고, 니콜라스는 그렇게 했지.\n\n\"이런 부탁을 드리는 게 옳은지 모르겠어요,\" 마들렌이 다급히 말했어. \"하지만 제발, 제발 오늘 여기서 있었던 일을 제 가엾은 어머니의 그 다정한 친구분들께 말씀하지 말아 주세요. 아버지는 고생을 많이 하셨고, 오늘 아침엔 상태가 더 안 좋으세요. 부탁드려요, 저를 위한 은혜로, 호의로 여기시고요.\"\n\n\"바라시는 걸 그저 한마디 비치기만 하셔도,\" 니콜라스가 뜨겁게 대답했어. \"저는 그걸 들어 드리려고 제 목숨이라도 걸겠습니다.\"\n\n\"성급히 말씀하시네요.\"\n\n\"진실로, 진심으로 드리는 말씀입니다,\" 니콜라스가 그 말을 빚어내는 동안 입술을 떨며 대꾸했어. \"여태껏 그 누가 진실을 말한 적이 있다면, 바로 이만큼 진실하게요. 저는 제 감정을 감추는 데 서툴고, 설사 그럴 줄 안다 해도 아가씨께만은 제 마음을 숨길 수가 없습니다. 아가씨, 저는 아가씨의 사연을 알고, 그런 일을 듣고 보는 사람과 천사라면 누구나 그러하듯 느끼는 사람으로서, 부디 믿어 주시기를 간청합니다. 저는 아가씨를 위해서라면 기꺼이 죽겠습니다.\"\n\n젊은 아가씨는 고개를 돌렸고, 누가 봐도 울고 있었어.\n\n\"용서하세요,\" 니콜라스가 정중하면서도 간절하게 말했어. \"제가 말이 지나쳤거나, 제게 맡겨진 그 믿음을 함부로 넘어선 것처럼 보였다면요. 하지만 제 관심과 동정이 오늘 맡은 그 일과 함께 끝나 버리기라도 하듯 아가씨 곁을 떠날 수는 없었습니다. 저는 이 순간부터 아가씨께 겸손히 헌신하는 충실한 종입니다. 저를 이리로 보내신 분께는 한 치의 거짓 없는 진실과 명예로, 또 아가씨께는 순수하고 올곧은 마음과 정중한 거리를 지켜 헌신하는 사람입니다. 만약 제 뜻이 이보다 더하거나 덜하다면, 저는 그분의 신임을 받을 자격이 없는 사람이고, 제가 지금 내뱉는 이 정직한 말을 부추기는 그 천성마저 저버리는 사람일 겁니다.\"\n\n그녀는 어서 가 달라고 청하며 손을 내저었지만, 한마디도 대답하지 않았어. 니콜라스는 더 이상 아무 말도 할 수 없어 말없이 물러났지. 그렇게 마들렌 브레이와의 첫 만남이 끝났어.\n\n# CHAPTER 47\n\n이웃 교회의 종소리에 귀를 기울이던 뉴먼 노그스가 중얼거렸다. \"벌써 사십오 분 종이 울리네! 내 점심시간은 두 시인데. 저 양반은 일부러 그러는 거야. 작정하고 그러는 거라고. 딱 저 양반답지.\"\n\n뉴먼이 이렇게 혼잣말을 한 건 자기만의 좁은 사무실, 그 높은 사무용 의자 위에서였다. 그리고 그 혼잣말은, 뉴먼이 투덜대는 혼잣말이 늘 그렇듯, 랄프 니클비를 두고 하는 소리였다.\n\n\"저 양반은 입맛이란 게 있었던 적이 없는 것 같아.\" 뉴먼이 말했다. \"파운드, 실링, 펜스 빼고는 말이지. 그런데 그걸 두고는 늑대처럼 게걸스럽거든. 영국 동전을 종류별로 하나씩 삼키게 만들어 봤으면 좋겠어. 일 펜스짜리는 삼키기 거북한 한 입이겠지만, 크라운짜리는, 하하! 하하!\"\n\n랄프 니클비가 어쩔 수 없이 오 실링짜리 동전을 삼키는 모습을 상상하니 기분이 어느 정도 풀렸다. 뉴먼은 책상에서 흔히 '포켓 권총'이라 불리는, 들고 다니는 작은 병 하나를 천천히 꺼냈다. 그러고는 귀에 바짝 대고 흔들어, 듣기에 시원하고 기분 좋은 찰랑찰랑 소리를 냈다. 그러자 굳었던 얼굴이 풀어졌고, 꿀꺽꿀꺽 한 모금 마시니 표정이 한층 더 풀어졌다. 코르크 마개를 도로 막고는, 아주 흡족하다는 듯 입맛을 두세 번 쩝쩝 다셨다. 그러다 이제 술맛이 사라지자, 다시 자기 신세 한탄으로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"세 시 오 분 전이군.\" 뉴먼이 으르렁거렸다. \"이쯤 됐으면 더 걸릴 것도 없겠지. 게다가 나는 여덟 시에 아침을 먹었어. 그것도 그런 아침을 말이야! 그리고 내 진짜 점심시간은 두 시라고! 지금 이 시간에 집에서는 뜨끈한 구운 고기 한 점이 식어 가며 망가지고 있을지도 모르는데, 저 양반이 그걸 어찌 알겠어? '내가 돌아올 때까지 가지 마라', '내가 돌아올 때까지 가지 마라', 날이면 날마다 그 소리지. 그러면 왜 늘 내 점심시간에 나가는 건데, 응? 그게 사람 약 올리는 짓 말고 뭐냐고, 응?\"\n\n이 말은 아주 큰 소리로 내뱉은 것이었지만, 듣는 거라곤 빈 허공뿐이었다. 하지만 자기 억울함을 늘어놓고 나니 뉴먼 노그스는 마음이 독해진 모양이었다. 그는 낡은 모자를 머리에 눌러쓰고, 늘 끼는 장갑을 당겨 끼더니, 무슨 일이 있어도 바로 이 순간 점심을 먹으러 가겠다고 아주 단호하게 선언했다.\n\n이 결심을 곧장 실행에 옮겨, 그가 복도까지 나왔을 때였다. 길 쪽 현관문에서 열쇠가 돌아가는 소리가 나는 바람에, 그는 부랴부랴 다시 자기 사무실로 후퇴했다.\n\n\"왔군.\" 뉴먼이 으르렁거렸다. \"누구랑 같이 왔어. 이제 '이 신사분 가실 때까지 있어라' 하겠지. 하지만 나는 안 그럴 거야. 그건 확실해.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 뉴먼은 반쪽짜리 문 두 짝이 달린, 키 큰 빈 벽장 속으로 슬쩍 들어가 안에 틀어박혔다. 랄프가 자기 방으로 무사히 들어가자마자 빠져나올 작정이었다.\n\n\"노그스!\" 랄프가 외쳤다. \"그 녀석 어디 갔어, 노그스?\"\n\n하지만 뉴먼은 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"가지 말라고 했는데 점심 먹으러 갔군, 이놈.\" 랄프가 사무실 안을 들여다보며 회중시계를 꺼내고는 중얼거렸다. \"흥! 그라이드, 자네는 이리 들어오는 게 낫겠어. 내 직원이 나갔고, 내 방은 햇볕이 뜨거워. 여기가 시원하고 그늘이 졌네, 좀 누추한 건 괜찮다면 말이지.\"\n\n\"전혀 괜찮습니다, 니클비 씨, 아 전혀요! 저는 어디든 다 똑같습니다, 선생님. 아! 아주 좋은데요. 오! 아주 좋아요!\"\n\n이렇게 대답한 노인은 일흔이나 일흔다섯쯤 된 작은 체구의 사람이었다. 몸이 무척 야위었고, 등이 많이 굽었으며 약간 비틀려 있었다. 그는 깃이 아주 좁은 회색 코트를 입고, 검은 비단으로 골이 진 구식 조끼를 걸쳤다. 그리고 어찌나 빈약한 바지인지, 쪼그라든 막대기 같은 정강이가 그 흉한 모습을 고스란히 드러냈다. 옷에서 자랑거리나 장식이라고는 딱 두 가지뿐이었다. 큼직한 금 도장이 몇 개 달린 강철 시곗줄, 그리고 요즘은 거의 보기 힘든 옛날식을 따라 회색 머리카락을 뒤로 묶은 검은 리본이었다. 그의 코와 턱은 날카롭고 뾰족했으며, 이가 빠져 턱이 안쪽으로 움푹 들어가 있었다. 얼굴은 쪼글쪼글하고 누랬는데, 마른 겨울 사과 빛이 줄무늬처럼 번진 두 뺨만 빼고는 그랬다. 그리고 수염이 났던 자리에는 아직 회색 수염 몇 가닥이 듬성듬성 남아 있었는데, 그건 너덜너덜한 눈썹과 마찬가지로 그것들이 돋아난 토양이 얼마나 메마른지를 말해 주는 것 같았다. 그 몸 전체에서 풍기는 분위기와 태도는 고양이처럼 살금살금 다가드는 비굴함이었다. 그리고 얼굴 전체의 표정은 주름진 곁눈질 하나에 다 모여 있었는데, 거기에는 교활함과 음탕함, 능청스러움과 탐욕이 한데 뒤섞여 있었다.\n\n이런 사람이 바로 늙은 아서 그라이드였다. 그의 얼굴에는 주름 하나, 옷에는 남는 주름이나 접힌 자락 하나 없었지만, 그 모든 것이 더없이 욕심 많고 인색한 궁핍함을 드러냈다. 그리고 그가 랄프 니클비가 속한 부류의 사람임을 충분히 일러 주었다. 이런 사람이 바로 늙은 아서 그라이드였다. 그는 낮은 의자에 앉아 랄프 니클비의 얼굴을 올려다보았고, 랄프는 높은 사무용 의자에 느긋이 걸터앉아 두 팔을 무릎에 올린 채 그를 내려다보았다. 그가 무슨 용건으로 왔든 만만치 않은 상대였다.\n\n\"그래, 그동안 어떻게 지내셨소?\" 그라이드가 랄프의 건강 상태에 무척 관심 있는 척하며 말했다. \"못 본 지가, 오! 정말로 못 본 지가.\"\n\n\"오랜만이지.\" 랄프가 묘한 미소를 지으며 말했다. 친구가 단지 안부나 물으러 온 게 아니라는 걸 잘 알고 있다는 뜻이었다. \"이렇게 만난 것도 아슬아슬한 거였어. 자네가 모퉁이를 돌 때 마침 내가 막 문 앞에 도착한 참이었거든.\"\n\n\"제가 운이 아주 좋군요.\" 그라이드가 말했다.\n\n\"다들 그렇게 말하지.\" 랄프가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다.\n\n나이 든 고리대금업자는 턱을 까딱이며 미소를 지었다. 그러나 새로 무슨 말을 꺼내지는 않았고, 두 사람은 한동안 말없이 앉아 있었다. 둘 다 상대의 허점을 잡으려고 살피는 중이었다.\n\n\"이봐, 그라이드.\" 마침내 랄프가 말했다. \"오늘은 무슨 바람이 분 건가?\"\n\n\"아하! 당신은 대담한 분이오, 니클비 씨.\" 상대가 외쳤다. 랄프가 먼저 본론으로 이끌어 주자 무척 안심한 기색이었다. \"이런, 이런, 정말 대담한 분이라니까!\"\n\n\"왜, 자네가 워낙 매끄럽고 슬그머니 구는 데가 있으니, 그에 비해 내가 그렇게 보이는 거겠지.\" 랄프가 받아쳤다. \"자네 방식이 더 나을지도 모르지만, 나는 그럴 만한 인내심이 없어서 말이야.\"\n\n\"당신은 천재로 태어난 분입니다, 니클비 씨.\" 늙은 아서가 말했다. \"깊고, 깊고, 깊어요. 아!\"\n\n\"충분히 깊지.\" 랄프가 되받았다. \"자네 같은 사람이 칭찬을 시작하면, 내가 가진 깊이를 다 끌어와야 한다는 걸 알 만큼은 말이야. 자네가 다른 사람들한테 아첨하고 알랑거릴 때 내가 옆에서 지켜봤잖아. 그게 늘 어떤 결말로 이어졌는지도 꽤 잘 기억하고 있어.\"\n\n\"하, 하, 하!\" 아서가 두 손을 비비며 응수했다. \"그렇지요, 그렇고말고요, 틀림없이. 그걸 당신보다 잘 아는 사람도 없지요. 자, 이제 당신이 옛 시절을 기억해 준다고 생각하니 참 기분 좋은 일입니다. 이런!\"\n\n\"자, 그럼.\" 랄프가 침착하게 말했다. \"무슨 바람이 분 거냐고 다시 묻겠네. 뭔가?\"\n\n\"저것 좀 보게!\" 상대가 외쳤다. \"지난 얘기를 나누는 중에도 일 얘기를 못 참는다니까. 이런, 이런, 정말 대단한 양반이야!\"\n\n\"옛날 일 가운데 뭘 들춰내고 싶은 건가?\" 랄프가 말했다. \"그 가운데 하나겠지, 아니면 그 얘기를 꺼낼 리가 없으니까.\"\n\n\"이 양반이 나까지 의심하네!\" 늙은 아서가 두 손을 치켜들며 외쳤다. \"나까지도! 이런, 나까지도. 정말 대단한 양반이야! 하, 하, 하! 정말 대단한 양반이라니까! 온 세상을 상대하는 니클비 씨. 저런 사람은 또 없지. 난쟁이들 틈의 거인이야, 거인, 거인이라고!\"\n\n랄프는 그 늙은 능구렁이가 이런 식으로 낄낄거리는 모습을 잔잔한 미소를 지으며 바라보았다. 그리고 벽장 속의 뉴먼 노그스는 점심을 먹을 가망이 점점 희미해져 가자 가슴이 철렁 내려앉는 기분이었다.\n\n\"그래도 비위를 맞춰 줘야지.\" 늙은 아서가 외쳤다. \"이 양반은 제 뜻대로 해야 직성이 풀리니까. 스코틀랜드 사람들 말마따나 고집 센 사람이거든. 뭐, 그래, 스코틀랜드 사람들은 현명한 민족이지. 이 양반은 일 얘기를 하려 들고, 제 시간을 거저 내주는 법이 없어. 아주 옳은 일이지. 시간이 돈이니까, 시간이 돈이니까.\"\n\n\"그 말은 우리 같은 사람이 만든 것 같은데.\" 랄프가 말했다. \"시간은 돈이지, 그것도 그걸로 이자를 따지는 사람한테는 아주 좋은 돈이고. 시간이 돈이라! 그래, 그리고 시간은 돈이 들기도 하지. 누군지 우리가 댈 수 있는 어떤 사람들한테는 꽤 비싼 물건이야, 안 그러면 내가 내 장사를 잊은 거고.\"\n\n이 비꼬는 말에 늙은 아서는 다시 두 손을 치켜들고, 다시 낄낄거리고, 다시 \"정말 대단한 양반이야!\" 하고 내뱉었다. 그러고는 낮은 의자를 랄프의 높은 의자 쪽으로 조금 더 끌어당기더니, 꿈쩍도 않는 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 만약, 내가 만약 결혼을 할 거라고 한다면, 당신은 뭐라고 하시겠소?\"\n\n\"내가 자네한테 이렇게 말하겠지.\" 랄프가 차갑게 그를 내려다보며 대답했다. \"자네 무슨 속셈이 있어서 거짓말을 하는 거라고. 그것도 이번이 처음이 아니고 마지막도 아닐 거라고. 나는 놀라지도 않았고 속아 넘어갈 사람도 아니라고 말이야.\"\n\n\"그러면 진지하게 말하리다, 나 정말 결혼하오.\" 늙은 아서가 말했다.\n\n\"그러면 나도 진지하게 말하겠네.\" 랄프가 받아쳤다. \"방금 한 그 말 그대로야. 가만. 어디 좀 보세. 자네 얼굴에 음탕한 악마가 들었군. 이게 무슨 일이야?\"\n\n\"당신을 속이려는 게 아니오, 알잖소.\" 아서 그라이드가 칭얼거렸다. \"그럴 수도 없거니와, 그러려고 들면 미친 짓이지. 내가, 내가 니클비 씨를 속이다니! 난쟁이가 거인을 속이려 들다니. 다시 묻겠소. 헤, 헤, 헤! 내가 만약 결혼을 할 거라고 한다면, 당신은 뭐라고 하시겠소?\"\n\n\"어디 늙은 노파한테?\" 랄프가 말했다.\n\n\"아니오, 아니오.\" 아서가 그의 말을 가로막으며, 황홀해서 두 손을 비비며 외쳤다. \"틀렸소, 또 틀렸소. 니클비 씨가 한 번은 헛짚었군. 빗나갔소, 완전히 빗나갔어! 젊고 아름다운 처녀한테요. 싱싱하고, 사랑스럽고, 호리는 듯하고, 아직 열아홉도 안 됐소. 검은 눈동자, 긴 속눈썹, 보기만 해도 입 맞추고 싶어지는 무르익은 붉은 입술, 손가락으로 만지작거리고 싶어 안달 나는 탐스럽게 굽이치는 머릿결, 그 둘레로 저절로 팔을 감고 싶어져 허공을 끌어안게 만드는 그런 허리, 어찌나 사뿐히 걷는지 땅을 밟는 것 같지도 않은 작은 발. 이 모든 걸 다 데리고 결혼하는 거요, 선생, 이걸, 헤, 헤!\"\n\n\"이건 흔한 헛소리보다 좀 더한데.\" 랄프가 그 늙은 죄인의 황홀경을 입술을 비죽이며 듣고는 말했다. \"그 처녀 이름이?\"\n\n\"오 깊다, 깊어! 자, 보게, 저게 얼마나 깊은지!\" 늙은 아서가 외쳤다. \"이 양반은 내가 자기 도움이 필요하다는 걸 알아. 자기가 그걸 줄 수 있다는 것도 알고, 그게 다 자기한테 이득이 되리란 것도 알지. 벌써 일이 어찌 돌아갈지 다 보이는 거야. 그 처녀 이름은, 근처에 듣는 사람 없소?\"\n\n\"아니, 도대체 여기 누가 있다고 그래?\" 랄프가 짜증스럽게 받아쳤다.\n\n\"혹시 누가 계단을 오르내리며 지나갈지도 모르잖소.\" 아서 그라이드가 문밖을 내다보고 조심스레 다시 닫고 나서 말했다. \"아니면 당신 직원이 돌아와서 밖에서 엿듣고 있을지도 모르고. 서기나 하인들은 엿듣는 버릇이 있거든. 노그스 씨가 만약.\"\n\n\"노그스 씨 따위 빌어먹을.\" 랄프가 날카롭게 말했다. \"할 얘기나 계속해.\"\n\n\"노그스 씨 따위 빌어먹을, 좋고말고요.\" 늙은 아서가 받았다. \"그거야 나도 조금도 반대할 게 없지요. 그 처녀 이름은.\"\n\n\"어서.\" 늙은 아서가 또 뜸을 들이는 바람에 잔뜩 짜증이 난 랄프가 말했다. \"뭐야?\"\n\n\"매들린 브레이요.\"\n\n이 이름이 랄프에게 어떤 영향을 미칠 만한 이유가 무엇이었든, 아서 그라이드는 그런 게 좀 있으리라고 짐작한 듯했다. 그리고 그 이름이 실제로 그에게 어떤 영향을 미쳤든, 랄프는 그것을 조금도 드러내지 않았다. 다만 차분하게 그 이름을 몇 번 되뇌었다. 마치 그것을 전에 언제 어디서 들었는지 떠올려 보는 것처럼.\n\n\"브레이.\" 랄프가 말했다. \"브레이, 어디의 젊은 브레이라는 사람이 있었지, 아니, 그 사람한테는 딸이 없었어.\"\n\n\"브레이 기억하지요?\" 아서 그라이드가 받았다.\n\n\"아니.\" 랄프가 멍하니 그를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"월터 브레이 말이오! 그 멋쟁이 사내, 잘난 마누라를 그렇게 못되게 다룬 자!\"\n\n\"그런 점을 들어서 어떤 특정한 멋쟁이 사내를 내 기억에 떠올리게 하려는 거라면,\" 랄프가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"내가 알았던 멋쟁이 사내들 열에 아홉과 헷갈릴 거야.\"\n\n\"쯧, 쯧. 지금 채무자 구역에 있는 그 브레이 말이오.\" 늙은 아서가 말했다. \"브레이를 잊었을 리가 없는데. 우리 둘 다 그자랑 거래했잖소. 아니, 그자가 당신한테 돈을 빚졌잖소!\"\n\n\"오 그 사람!\" 랄프가 받았다. \"그래, 그래. 이제 말이 되는군. 오! 그 사람 딸이라, 그런가?\"\n\n이 말은 아주 자연스럽게 나온 것 같았지만, 늙은 아서 그라이드 같은 동류의 인간이라면 알아챌 만큼은 자연스럽지 못했다. 랄프가 그를 부추겨, 그가 자진해서 털어놓았을 법한 것보다, 또는 랄프가 다른 어떤 수로 얻어 냈을 법한 것보다 훨씬 더 분명한 진술과 설명을 끌어내려는 속셈이었던 것이다. 그러나 늙은 아서는 자기 속셈에 너무 골몰해 있어서, 자기도 모르게 넘어가고 말았다. 좋은 친구가 진심으로 묻는 거라고만 믿어 의심치 않았다.\n\n\"잠깐만 생각해 보면 그자를 잊었을 리 없을 줄 알았소.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"자네 말이 맞아.\" 랄프가 대답했다. \"하지만 늙은 아서 그라이드와 결혼이라니, 정말 이상하게 어울리지 않는 말의 조합이군. 늙은 아서 그라이드와 검은 눈동자며 속눈썹이며, 보기만 해도 입 맞추고 싶어지는 입술이며, 만지작거리고 싶은 굽이치는 머릿결이며, 팔로 감고 싶은 허리며, 아무것도 밟지 않는 작은 발이라니, 늙은 아서 그라이드와 그런 것들이라니 더더욱 괴이쩍어. 그렇지만 늙은 아서 그라이드가 채무자 구역에 갇힌 망한 '멋쟁이 사내'의 딸과 결혼한다니, 그게 무엇보다도 괴이쩍고 믿기지 않는 일이야. 솔직히 말하자고, 아서 그라이드 친구. 자네가 이 일에 내 도움이 필요하다면, 당연히 그렇겠지, 안 그러면 여기 왔을 리 없으니까, 터놓고, 핵심을 말하게. 그리고 무엇보다도 이게 나한테 이득이 된다는 소리는 꺼내지도 마. 자네한테도 이득이 된다는 걸 내가 아니까, 그것도 두둑이 말이야. 안 그러면 자네가 이런 일에 손가락 하나 까딱할 리 없거든.\"\n\n랄프의 말 내용에도, 그가 내뱉은 목소리 가락에도, 거기 곁들인 눈빛에도, 그 늙은 고리대금업자의 차가운 피마저 끓게 하고 메마른 뺨마저 붉히게 할 만큼 신랄함과 비아냥이 가득했다. 그러나 아서는 분노를 조금도 드러내지 않았다. 그저 아까처럼 \"정말 대단한 양반이야!\" 하고 외치는 것으로 만족하며, 마음껏 자유와 익살을 즐기듯 머리를 좌우로 흔들었다. 하지만 랄프의 표정을 보고 되도록 빨리 본론으로 들어가는 게 상책임을 분명히 알아챈 그는, 좀 더 진지한 일을 위해 마음을 가다듬고는 협상의 알맹이로 들어갔다.\n\n먼저 그는 매들린 브레이가 이 세상에 다른 친구라고는 하나도 없는 단 하나뿐인 아비를 부양하고 보살피는 데 헌신하고 있으며, 그 아비의 모든 소원에 종처럼 매여 있다는 사실을 늘어놓았다. 그러자 랄프는 그런 비슷한 얘기는 전에도 들은 적이 있다고, 그 처녀가 세상 물정을 조금만 더 알았더라면 그런 바보짓은 안 했을 거라고 받아쳤다.\n\n둘째로 그는 그 처녀 아비의 성격에 대해 늘어놓았다. 설령 그자가 제 딴에 할 수 있는 한 가장 깊은 애정으로 딸을 사랑한다 치더라도, 자기 자신은 그보다 훨씬 더 사랑한다고 따지고 들었다. 그러자 랄프는 그건 더 말할 필요도 없다고, 아주 당연하고 충분히 있을 법한 일이라고 했다.\n\n셋째로 늙은 아서는 그 처녀가 가냘프고 아름다운 사람이며, 자기가 정말로 그녀를 아내로 삼고 싶어 안달이 났다고 운을 뗐다. 이에 랄프는 그저 모진 미소 한 번과, 자기 앞에 있는 쪼그라든 늙은이를 향한 눈길 한 번을 던질 뿐, 그 이상은 응수할 가치도 없다는 태도였다. 하지만 그것만으로도 뜻은 충분히 전해졌다.\n\n\"자.\" 그라이드가 말했다. \"이 일을 성사시키려고 내가 마음속에 품은 자그마한 계획에 대해 말하리다. 사실 나는 아직 그 아비한테도 청혼을 안 했소, 그걸 말했어야 했는데. 하지만 그건 벌써 눈치챘겠지? 아! 이런, 이런, 당신은 정말 날 선 연장 같은 사람이야!\"\n\n\"그럼 나를 가지고 놀지 말게.\" 랄프가 참지 못하고 말했다. \"속담 알잖아.\"\n\n\"늘 대답이 혀끝에 준비돼 있다니까!\" 늙은 아서가 감탄하여 두 손과 두 눈을 치켜들며 외쳤다. \"이 양반은 늘 만반의 준비가 돼 있어! 이런, 그렇게 잘 도는 머리에다, 그걸 뒷받침할 그렇게 넉넉한 돈까지 갖췄으니 얼마나 복인가!\" 그러더니 갑자기 어조를 바꿔 말을 이었다. \"지난 반년 동안 브레이의 거처를 여러 번 들락거렸소. 이 가냘픈 한 입을 처음 본 지 딱 반년이 됐지, 오 이런, 어찌나 가냘픈 한 입인지! 하지만 그건 지금 중요한 게 아니고. 나는 천칠백 파운드 빚으로 그자를 잡아 두고 있는 채권자요!\"\n\n\"마치 자네만 그자를 잡아 둔 채권자인 것처럼 말하는군.\" 랄프가 수첩을 꺼내며 말했다. \"나도 구백칠십오 파운드 사 실링 삼 펜스로 또 한 사람이야.\"\n\n\"오로지 당신 둘뿐이지요, 니클비 씨.\" 늙은 아서가 열을 올리며 말했다. \"오로지 둘뿐이오. 다른 누구도 구금 신청을 하는 비용을 들이지 않았소, 우리가 그자를 단단히 붙잡아 두리라 믿었던 거지, 내 장담하리다. 우리 둘 다 똑같은 덫에 걸린 거요. 오 이런, 어찌나 깊은 함정이던지, 그 때문에 나는 거의 망할 뻔했소! 어음으로 그자한테 돈을 빌려줬는데, 그자 이름 말고 보증인 이름은 딱 하나뿐이었소. 다들 틀림없이 믿을 만한 자라고 여겼고, 돈만큼이나 잘 유통됐지만, 결국 어찌 됐는지는 당신도 알잖소. 우리가 막 그자를 덮치려는 참에, 그자는 빈털터리로 죽어 버렸소. 아! 그 손해로 정말 망할 뻔했다니까!\"\n\n\"자네 계획이나 마저 말하게.\" 랄프가 말했다. \"지금 와서 우리 장사의 푸념을 늘어놓아 봐야 소용없어. 들어 줄 사람도 없잖나!\"\n\n\"그렇게 말하는 게 언제든 좋은 법이지요.\" 늙은 아서가 낄낄거리며 받았다. \"들을 사람이 있든 없든 말이오. 익숙해야 완벽해지는 거니까, 알잖소. 자, 내가 브레이한테 사위로 나서서, 단 한 가지 간단한 조건을 거는 거요. 내가 무사히 결혼하는 순간 그자를 조용히 풀어 주고, 강 건너편에서 신사처럼 살 수 있게 생활비를 대 준다는 조건이지. 그자는 오래 못 살아, 내가 의사한테 물어봤는데 그자 병이 심장병이라 가망이 없다고 딱 잘라 말하더군. 그리고 이 조건의 온갖 이점을 그자한테 제대로 짚어 가며 늘어놓으면, 그자가 나를 거절할 수 있겠소? 그리고 그자가 나를 거절하지 못한다면, 그 딸이 그자를 거역할 수 있겠소? 그러면 그 처녀를 아서 그라이드 부인으로, 예쁜 아서 그라이드 부인으로, 별미 같은, 앙증맞은 새끼 새 같은, 그 처녀를 아서 그라이드 부인으로 만들 수 있지 않겠소, 일주일이든 한 달이든 하루든, 내가 정하는 어느 때든 말이오?\"\n\n\"계속하게.\" 랄프가 천천히 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. 일부러 꾸민 듯한 그 차가운 어조는, 친구가 차츰차츰 끌어올린 황홀한 새된 소리와 묘한 대조를 이루었다. \"계속해. 자네가 나한테 그걸 물으러 온 건 아니잖아.\"\n\n\"오 이런, 말씀 한번 잘하시네!\" 늙은 아서가 랄프 쪽으로 몸을 더 바짝 붙이며 외쳤다. \"물론 아니지요, 그런 척도 안 하리다! 내가 온 건, 만약 그 아비한테서 일이 잘 풀린다면, 당신 그 빚을 두고 내게서 얼마를 받겠는지 물으려는 거요. 일 파운드당 오 실링? 육 실링 팔 펜스? 십 실링? 당신처럼 좋은 친구라면 십 실링까지도 내겠소, 우리가 늘 그렇게 좋은 사이였으니까. 하지만 당신은 그렇게까지 모질게 굴진 않을 거야, 나도 알아요. 자, 그러시겠지요?\"\n\n\"아직 할 말이 더 남았어.\" 랄프가 여전히 돌처럼 꿈쩍도 않고 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 그래요, 그렇소만, 시간을 안 주시잖소.\" 아서 그라이드가 받았다. \"이 일에 나는 뒤를 봐줄 사람이 필요하오. 말을 잘하고, 밀어붙이고, 요점을 다그칠 수 있는 사람 말이오, 당신만큼 그걸 잘하는 사람이 없거든. 나는 그걸 못 해, 나는 가엾고, 겁 많고, 신경 약한 인간이니까. 자, 당신이 오래전에 떼인 셈 친 이 빚을 두고 두둑한 합의금을 받게 된다면, 당신은 내 친구로서 나를 도와주겠지요. 안 그러겠소?\"\n\n\"할 말이 더 있어.\" 랄프가 말했다.\n\n\"아니오, 아니, 없소.\" 아서 그라이드가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래, 있어. 있다니까.\" 랄프가 말했다.\n\n\"오!\" 늙은 아서가 갑자기 깨달은 척하며 받았다. \"내 일과 내 속셈에 관해 할 말이 더 있다는 거구려. 아, 그렇지, 그렇고말고. 그걸 말해야 하오?\"\n\n\"그러는 게 낫겠지.\" 랄프가 무뚝뚝하게 받았다.\n\n\"그 얘기로 당신을 번거롭게 하고 싶지 않았소, 당신 관심이야 당신 몫이 걸린 일에서 끝날 거라고 여겼으니까.\" 아서 그라이드가 말했다. \"물어봐 주다니 친절하기도 하지. 오 이런, 어찌나 친절한지! 그러니까, 만약 내가 어떤 재산을, 자그마한 재산을, 아주 자그마한 재산을 알고 있다고 칩시다. 이 예쁜 새끼 새한테 권리가 있는 재산인데, 지금은 아무도 모르고 알 수도 없지만, 그 남편이 나만큼 알면 제 주머니로 싹 쓸어 담을 수 있는 그런 재산 말이오, 그러면 설명이 되지 않겠소?\"\n\n\"이 일 전체가 다 설명이 되지.\" 랄프가 불쑥 받았다. \"자, 이 문제를 한번 곰곰이 따져 보고, 내가 자네 성공을 도와준다면 얼마를 받아야 할지 생각해 봐야겠어.\"\n\n\"하지만 모질게 굴지 마시오.\" 늙은 아서가 애원하는 몸짓으로 두 손을 치켜들고 떨리는 목소리로 외쳤다. \"나한테 너무 모질게 굴지 마. 그건 아주 작은 재산이오, 정말로요. 십 실링으로 합시다, 그러고 흥정을 끝냅시다. 그건 내가 줘야 할 것보다 많은 거지만, 당신이 워낙 친절하니까, 십 실링으로 할까요? 그럽시다, 그래요.\"\n\n랄프는 이 애원에 아무런 반응도 보이지 않고, 삼사 분 동안 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채, 그 애원을 늘어놓는 사람을 곰곰이 바라보며 앉아 있었다. 충분히 머리를 굴린 뒤 그가 침묵을 깼는데, 쓸데없이 빙빙 돌려 말한다거나 본론을 곧장 짚지 못한다고 트집 잡힐 데는 분명 없었다.\n\n\"자네가 나 없이 이 처녀와 결혼한다면,\" 랄프가 말했다. \"자네는 내 빚을 전액 갚아야 해. 안 그러면 그 아비를 풀어 줄 수 없을 테니까. 그러니 분명한 건, 나는 전액을 받아야 한다는 거야, 한 푼도 깎거나 떼이는 일 없이 말이지. 안 그러면 나는 자네 비밀을 들어 준 덕에 손해를 보게 되는 셈이야, 이득을 보기는커녕. 그게 첫 번째 조항이야. 두 번째로, 이 협상하고 설득하고, 자네가 이 재산을 손에 넣게 도운 수고에 대해, 나는 오백 파운드를 받겠어. 그건 아주 적은 거지, 자네는 무르익은 입술이며 굽이치는 머릿결이며 그런 것들을 죄다 독차지하게 되니까 말이야. 세 번째이자 마지막 조항으로, 자네가 오늘 당장 나한테 증서를 써 줘야 해. 매들린 브레이와 결혼하는 날 정오 전에 이 두 금액을 지불하겠다는 증서 말이야. 자네가 나더러 요점을 다그치고 밀어붙일 수 있다고 했지. 나는 이걸 다그치겠어, 그리고 이 조건 말고는 한 푼도 깎아 주지 않겠어. 좋으면 받아들이고. 싫으면 자네 능력껏 나 없이 결혼하든가. 어쨌든 내 빚은 받아 낼 테니까.\"\n\n온갖 간청에도, 항변에도, 자기가 처음 내놓은 제안과 랄프의 제안 사이에서 절충을 보자는 제의에도, 랄프는 독사처럼 귀를 막았다. 그는 이 문제로 더 이상 논의하려 들지 않았다. 그리고 늙은 아서가 그 요구가 얼마나 터무니없는지 늘어놓으며 수정안을 내놓고, 차츰차츰 자기가 버티던 조건에 가까이 다가오는 동안에도, 랄프는 완전히 입을 다문 채 조용히 딴생각에 잠긴 표정으로 수첩에 적힌 항목과 서류만 들여다보고 있었다. 자기의 흔들림 없는 친구에게는 도무지 통하지 않는다는 걸 알자, 오기 전에 이미 이런 결과를 어느 정도 각오하고 있던 아서 그라이드는 무거운 마음으로 그 협상에 동의했다. 그러고는 그 자리에서 요구받은 증서를 채워 넣었다. 랄프는 그런 증서를 늘 가까이 두고 있었던 것이다. 다만 한 가지 조건을 내걸었는데, 니클비 씨가 바로 지금 자기와 함께 브레이의 거처로 가서, 정황이 좋고 그들 계획에 유리해 보이면 곧장 협상을 시작해 달라는 것이었다.\n\n이 마지막 합의에 따라, 두 훌륭한 신사는 곧 함께 밖으로 나갔다. 그러자 뉴먼 노그스가 병을 손에 든 채 벽장에서 나왔다. 그는 자기가 가장 관심 있는 대목이 논의될 때면, 들킬 위험을 무릅쓰고 벽장 위쪽 문틈으로 붉은 코를 한 번 이상 쑥 내밀었던 터였다.\n\n\"이젠 입맛이 없네.\" 뉴먼이 병을 주머니에 넣으며 말했다. \"내 점심은 다 먹었으니까.\"\n\n이 말을 아주 비통하고 구슬픈 어조로 내뱉고는, 뉴먼은 절름거리며 한걸음에 문까지 갔다가, 다시 한걸음에 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그 처녀가 누군지, 어떤 사람인지는 모르겠어.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 온 마음과 온 영혼으로 그 처녀가 가엾어. 그런데도 나는 그 처녀를 도울 수가 없어. 날마다 수백 가지 음모에 걸려드는 사람들 가운데 누구도 도울 수가 없지, 이렇게 비열한 음모는 또 없겠지만! 그래, 그게 내 고통만 더할 뿐, 그 사람들 고통을 더는 건 아니야. 내가 안다고 해서 일이 더 나빠지는 것도 아니고, 그저 그 사람들 못지않게 나까지 괴로울 뿐이지. 그라이드와 니클비라! 쌍두마차에 딱 맞는 한 쌍이군. 오 악당 놈들! 악당 놈들! 악당 놈들!\"\n\n이런 생각들과 함께, 마지막 말을 되풀이할 때마다 가엾은 모자 꼭대기를 호되게 한 대씩 내리치면서, 뉴먼 노그스는 길을 나섰다. 방금 숨어 있는 동안 포켓 권총의 내용물이 적잖이 그의 머릿속까지 흘러든 탓에 머리가 조금 어질어질했다. 그는 어느 싸구려 식당의 소고기와 채소에서나마 얼마간 위안을 얻어 보려고 발걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n한편 두 음모꾼은, 불과 며칠 전 아침 니콜라스가 처음 찾아갔던 바로 그 집으로 향했다. 그리고 브레이 씨를 만나는 데 성공했고, 마침 그 딸은 외출하고 없었다. 랄프의 온갖 재주를 짜낸 더없이 노련한 접근의 과정을 거쳐, 마침내 그들은 찾아온 진짜 목적을 털어놓았다.\n\n\"저기 앉아 있는 사람입니다, 브레이 씨.\" 랄프가 말했다. 아직 놀라움이 가시지 않은 병자는 의자에 기댄 채, 랄프와 아서 그라이드를 번갈아 바라보고 있었다. \"저 사람이 불운하게도 당신이 이곳에 갇힌 한 가지 원인이었다 한들 어떻습니까? 나도 또 다른 원인이었지요. 사람은 먹고살아야 하니까요. 당신은 그걸 제대로 볼 줄 모를 만큼 세상 물정에 어두운 분이 아니잖습니까. 우리는 우리가 할 수 있는 최선의 보상을 내놓는 겁니다. 보상이라! 여기, 작위 가진 아비라도 제 자식을 위해서라면 덥석 물 만한 청혼이 있습니다. 아서 그라이드 씨, 왕자만 한 재산을 가진 분이지요. 얼마나 큰 횡재인지 생각해 보십시오!\"\n\n\"내 딸은요, 선생.\" 브레이가 거만하게 받았다. \"내가 키운 그대로, 어떤 남자가 그 애 손을 얻는 대가로 내놓을 수 있는 가장 큰 재산에도 충분히 값하고도 남는 보답이오.\"\n\n\"바로 그게 내가 당신한테 한 말입니다.\" 교활한 랄프가 친구인 늙은 아서 쪽을 돌아보며 말했다. \"바로 그래서 이 일이 그토록 공평하고 수월하다고 본 거지요. 어느 쪽에도 빚지는 게 없습니다. 당신은 돈이 있고, 매들린 양은 아름다움과 인품이 있지요. 그쪽은 젊음이 있고, 당신은 돈이 있습니다. 그쪽은 돈이 없고, 당신은 젊음이 없지요. 주고받기, 비기는 셈, 하늘이 손수 맺어 준 짝입니다!\"\n\n\"짝은 하늘에서 맺어진다고들 하지요.\" 아서 그라이드가 자기가 탐내는 장인을 향해 흉측하게 곁눈질하며 덧붙였다. \"우리가 결혼한다면, 그 말대로 운명이겠지요.\"\n\n\"그러니 생각해 보십시오, 브레이 씨.\" 랄프가 이 논리를 얼른 좀 더 땅에 발 붙인 고려로 바꾸며 말했다. \"내 친구의 이 제안을 받아들이느냐 물리치느냐에 무엇이 걸려 있는지 생각해 보세요.\"\n\n\"내가 어떻게 받아들이거나 물리친단 말이오.\" 정말로 결정권이 자기한테 있다는 걸 짜증스럽게 의식하며 브레이 씨가 끼어들었다. \"받아들이거나 물리치는 건 내 딸의 몫이오. 내 딸의 몫이라고. 그건 당신도 알잖소.\"\n\n\"맞습니다.\" 랄프가 힘주어 말했다. \"하지만 당신한테는 여전히 조언할 힘이 있지요. 찬성과 반대의 이유를 늘어놓고, 바라는 바를 슬쩍 비칠 힘이요.\"\n\n\"바라는 바를 슬쩍 비치라고요, 선생!\" 채무자가 받아쳤다. 그는 자존심이 셌다가 비굴해졌다가 했지만, 언제나 이기적이었다. \"내가 그 애 아비요, 안 그렇소? 내가 왜 슬쩍 비치고 빙빙 돌려 말해야 하오? 당신은 그 애 어미의 친척들, 내 적들 말이오, 그것들 다 빌어먹을! 그것들처럼 그 애가 나한테 해 준 일이 의무가 아닌 다른 무엇이라고 생각하오, 선생, 그저 의무지! 아니면 내가 불운을 겪었으니 우리 부녀의 처지가 뒤바뀌어, 그 애가 명령하고 내가 따라야 마땅하다고 생각하오? 바라는 바를 비치라니! 당신은 아마, 내가 이런 데 있고 부축 없이는 이 의자에서 일어나기도 힘든 걸 보고는, 내가 무슨 기죽은 식객 신세라서, 제 자식을 위해 내가 최선이라 여기는 일을 할 용기도 힘도 없는 줄 아는가 보군. 바라는 바를 비칠 힘이라니! 그 정도야 있고말고!\"\n\n\"용서하시오.\" 랄프가 받았다. 그는 자기가 상대하는 사람을 속속들이 알았고, 그에 맞춰 입지를 정해 둔 터였다. \"내 말을 끝까지 안 들으시는군요. 내가 하려던 말은, 당신이 바라는 바를 비치는 것, 그저 비치기만 해도, 그건 분명 명령하는 것이나 다름없으리란 거였습니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 그야 당연하지.\" 브레이 씨가 격앙된 어조로 받아쳤다. \"혹시 당신이 그 일을 못 들었다면 말해 주리다, 선생, 한때는 말이오, 그 애 어미네 온 집안이 권력과 재산을 등에 업었어도, 내가 내 의지 하나만으로 그것들을 상대로 사사건건 보란 듯이 이긴 시절이 있었소.\"\n\n\"그래도,\" 랄프가 자기 천성이 허락하는 한 부드럽게 받았다. \"내 말을 끝까지 안 들으셨소. 당신은 아직 사교계에서 빛날 자격이 있는 분이오, 앞으로 살날도 많고요. 그러니까, 더 자유로운 공기 속에서, 더 밝은 하늘 아래에서, 직접 동무를 골라 가며 산다면 말입니다. 흥겨움이 당신 본바탕이지요, 전에도 거기서 빛났잖습니까. 당신한테는 멋과 자유가 어울려요. 프랑스, 그리고 거기서 호사스럽게 지낼 수 있는 연금이라면, 당신한테 새 삶을 빌려줄 거고, 당신을 새로운 인생으로 옮겨 줄 겁니다. 한때 당신의 호화로운 즐길 거리로 온 도시가 떠들썩했지요, 그러니 새 무대에서 다시 한번 화려하게 빛날 수도 있어요. 경험을 밑천 삼아, 남들이 당신 돈으로 살게 두는 대신, 남들 돈으로 좀 살아 가면서 말입니다. 그 반대편 그림에는 뭐가 있습니까? 뭐가 있냐고요? 어느 교회 묘지가 제일 가까운지는 모르겠으나, 거기 어딘가에 묘비 하나, 그리고 날짜 하나, 어쩌면 이 년 뒤, 어쩌면 이십 년 뒤겠지요. 그게 전부입니다.\"\n\n브레이 씨는 의자 팔걸이에 팔꿈치를 괴고, 한 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다.\n\n\"내가 이렇게 터놓고 말하는 건,\" 랄프가 그의 곁에 앉으며 말했다. \"마음이 절실하기 때문입니다. 당신이 딸을 내 친구 그라이드와 결혼시키는 게 내 이익이오, 그래야 저 사람이 내 빚을 갚아 줄 테니까요, 일부라도 말이죠. 숨기지 않겠습니다. 드러내 놓고 인정해요. 하지만 당신은 딸한테 그런 일을 권하는 데 무슨 이익이 있습니까? 그걸 잘 새겨 두세요. 따님은 싫다고 할 수도 있고, 항변할 수도 있고, 눈물을 흘릴 수도 있고, 저 사람이 너무 늙었다고 할 수도, 제 인생이 비참해질 거라고 호소할 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 지금은 어떻습니까?\"\n\n병자가 보인 몇 가지 자그마한 몸짓은, 이 논리가 그에게 헛되지 않았음을 드러냈다. 그의 그 자그마한 거동 하나하나가 랄프에게 헛되지 않았듯이 말이다.\n\n\"지금은 어떠냐 이겁니다.\" 교활한 고리대금업자가 말을 이었다. \"아니면 앞으로 어떻게 될 가망이 있느냐고요? 정말이지 당신이 죽으면, 당신이 미워하는 그 사람들이 따님을 행복하게 해 주겠지요. 그런데 당신은 그 생각을 견딜 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"못 하지!\" 브레이가 억누를 수 없는 앙심에 떠밀려 받았다.\n\n\"못 하시겠지요, 정말로!\" 랄프가 조용히 말했다. \"따님이 누군가의 죽음으로 덕을 본다면,\" 이 말은 좀 더 낮은 어조로 했다. \"그건 남편의 죽음이게 하시오. 따님이 당신의 죽음을 돌아보며, 그때부터 더 행복한 삶이 시작됐다고 헤아리게 만들지 마세요. 반대할 게 어디 있습니까? 어디 한번 들어 봅시다. 뭡니까? 구혼자가 늙은이라서? 아니, 가문 좋고 재산 있는 사람들, 당신 같은 핑계도 없이 삶의 온갖 수단과 사치를 다 누리는 사람들이, 얼마나 자주 제 딸을 늙은이한테, 아니 (그보다 더한 건) 머리도 마음도 없는 젊은 놈한테 시집보내는데요. 하찮은 허영을 채우려고, 어느 집안과의 이해관계를 다지려고, 의회 의석 하나를 챙기려고 말입니다! 따님을 위해 판단하세요, 선생, 따님을 위해 판단하시라고요. 당신이 가장 잘 알 테고, 따님은 살아서 당신한테 고마워할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"쉿! 쉿!\" 브레이 씨가 갑자기 벌떡 일어나 떨리는 손으로 랄프의 입을 막으며 외쳤다. \"그 애가 문간에 와 있는 소리가 들려요!\"\n\n이 황급한 행동의 부끄러움과 두려움 속에는 양심의 번득임이 있었다. 그것은 한순간에, 그 잔인한 계략을 덮고 있던 얇은 궤변의 껍질을 벗겨 내, 그 비열함과 무정한 추악함을 온전히 드러내고 말았다. 아비는 창백하게 떨며 의자에 주저앉았고, 아서 그라이드는 제 모자를 만지작거리며 잡아당길 뿐, 감히 바닥에서 눈을 들지 못했다. 랄프조차 그 순간만큼은 두들겨 맞은 사냥개처럼 움츠러들었다. 순결한 어린 처녀 하나 앞에서 기가 죽은 것이다!\n\n그 효과는 갑작스러웠던 만큼이나 거의 순식간에 끝났다. 랄프가 가장 먼저 정신을 차렸다. 매들린의 놀란 표정을 보고는, 그 가엾은 처녀에게 마음을 가라앉히라고, 두려워할 까닭은 전혀 없다고 안심시켜 주었다.\n\n\"갑작스러운 경련입니다.\" 랄프가 브레이 씨를 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"이젠 아주 멀쩡하세요.\"\n\n불과 일 분 전까지 그들이 확실한 불행을 꾸미고 있던 그 젊고 아름다운 사람이, 아비의 목을 끌어안고 다정한 위로와 사랑의 말을 쏟아 내는 모습은, 아비의 귀가 들을 수 있는, 자식의 입이 빚어낼 수 있는 가장 달콤한 말이었으니, 아무리 모질고 속물적인 마음이라도 움직일 만했다. 그러나 랄프는 차갑게 지켜보기만 했다. 그리고 흐릿한 눈으로 겉모습의 아름다움만 탐할 뿐 그 안에 깃든 정신은 보지 못하는 아서 그라이드는, 분명 어떤 야릇한 종류의 열기를 드러내긴 했지만, 그건 미덕을 바라볼 때 흔히 솟아나는 그런 종류의 따뜻한 감정은 결코 아니었다.\n\n\"매들린.\" 아비가 딸을 부드럽게 떼어 놓으며 말했다. \"아무것도 아니란다.\"\n\n\"하지만 어제도 그 경련을 일으키셨잖아요, 그렇게 아파하시는 걸 보면 저는 너무 무서워요. 제가 해 드릴 일이 정말 없어요?\"\n\n\"지금은 없단다. 여기 두 신사분이 와 계신다, 매들린, 한 분은 전에 본 적이 있지. 저 애가 늘 그랬소,\" 브레이 씨가 아서 그라이드를 향해 말을 덧붙였다. \"당신만 보면 내가 더 안 좋아진다고 말이오. 우리 사이와 그 결과에 대해 그 애가 아는 것만, 딱 그것만 알았으니 그럴 만도 했지. 뭐, 그래. 어쩌면 그 점에 대해 마음을 바꿀지도 모르오. 여자아이들은 마음을 바꿔도 되는 거니까, 알잖소. 너 많이 지쳤구나, 얘야.\"\n\n\"안 지쳤어요, 정말요.\"\n\n\"지쳤어, 정말로. 넌 너무 무리를 해.\"\n\n\"더 못 해 드리는 게 마음에 걸려요.\"\n\n\"네 마음은 안다, 하지만 넌 네 힘에 부치게 애를 쓰잖니. 날마다 고된 일에 시달리는 이 비참한 삶이, 얘야, 너한테는 견디기 버거운 거다, 분명 그래. 가엾은 매들린!\"\n\n이런 다정한 말을 비롯해 더 많은 말을 건네며, 브레이 씨는 딸을 끌어당겨 그 뺨에 애정 어린 입맞춤을 했다. 그동안 날카롭게 유심히 그를 지켜보던 랄프는 문 쪽으로 걸음을 옮기며, 그라이드에게 따라오라고 신호했다.\n\n\"다시 우리한테 연락 주시는 거지요?\" 랄프가 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 그래요.\" 브레이 씨가 황급히 딸을 옆으로 밀어내며 받았다. \"일주일 안에. 일주일만 주시오.\"\n\n\"일주일이오.\" 랄프가 동행을 돌아보며 말했다. \"오늘부터요. 그럼 이만. 매들린 양, 손에 입맞춤을 드립니다.\"\n\n\"악수를 합시다, 그라이드.\" 브레이 씨가 손을 내밀며 말했고, 늙은 아서는 고개를 숙였다. \"당신도 뜻은 좋겠지요, 틀림없이. 이제 그렇게 말하지 않을 수 없군. 내가 당신한테 돈을 빚졌다 한들, 그게 당신 잘못은 아니었으니. 매들린, 얘야, 여기 손을 다오.\"\n\n\"오 이런! 아가씨가 허락만 해 주신다면! 손가락 끝만이라도요.\" 아서가 머뭇거리며 반쯤 물러서며 말했다.\n\n매들린은 그 도깨비 같은 모습 앞에서 저도 모르게 움츠러들었다. 그래도 손가락 끝을 그의 손에 갖다 댔다가 곧바로 거두었다. 그것을 붙잡아 제 입술로 가져가려고 헛되이 한번 움켜쥐어 본 늙은 아서는, 제 손가락에 우물거리는 입맞춤을 하고는, 얼굴을 갖가지 음탕한 표정으로 일그러뜨리며 친구를 뒤쫓아 갔다. 그 무렵 친구는 이미 거리에 나와 있었다.\n\n\"뭐랍니까, 뭐래요? 거인은 난쟁이한테 뭐라고 합니까?\" 아서 그라이드가 절뚝거리며 랄프에게 다가가 물었다.\n\n\"난쟁이는 거인한테 뭐라고 하는데?\" 랄프가 눈썹을 치켜올리고 물어 오는 자를 내려다보며 받았다.\n\n\"이 양반은 뭐라고 해야 할지 모르겠소.\" 아서 그라이드가 대답했다. \"기대도 되고 겁도 나고. 한데 그 처녀, 별미 같지 않소?\"\n\n\"나는 미인에 별 취미가 없어.\" 랄프가 으르렁거렸다.\n\n\"하지만 나는 있지요.\" 아서가 두 손을 비비며 받았다. \"오 이런! 그 처녀가 아비한테 몸을 숙일 때 그 눈이 어찌나 고와 보이던지! 그렇게 긴 속눈썹, 그렇게 섬세한 가장자리라니! 그 처녀가, 그 처녀가 나를 그렇게 부드럽게 보더군.\"\n\n\"그리 사랑스러운 눈길은 아니었던 것 같은데.\" 랄프가 말했다. \"안 그래?\"\n\n\"아니, 안 그랬던 것 같소?\" 늙은 아서가 받았다. \"하지만 이 일이 잘 풀릴 거라고 보지 않소? 잘 풀릴 것 같지 않냐고요?\"\n\n랄프는 경멸 어린 찡그린 얼굴로 그를 보며, 비웃음을 머금고 이를 악문 채 대답했다.\n\n\"그자가 딸한테 지쳤다고, 너무 무리한다고, 힘에 부치게 애쓴다고 하던 거 눈여겨봤나?\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래. 그게 어쨌다고요?\"\n\n\"그자가 전에 그 애한테 언제 그런 말을 한 적이 있었을 것 같아? 이 삶은 그 애가 견디기 버겁다고 말이야. 그래, 그래. 그자가 딸을 위해 그 삶을 바꿔 줄 거야.\"\n\n\"이게 다 된 거라고 보오?\" 늙은 아서가 눈을 반쯤 감고 동행의 얼굴을 들여다보며 물었다.\n\n\"틀림없이 다 됐어.\" 랄프가 말했다. \"그자는 자기 자신을 속이려 들고 있어, 그것도 우리 눈앞에서, 벌써부터. 자기가 제 이익이 아니라 딸의 행복을 생각하는 척하고 있지. 미덕 있는 역할을 연기하는 거야, 어찌나 사려 깊고 다정한지, 선생, 그 딸이 제 아비를 못 알아볼 지경이었어. 그 애 눈에 놀란 눈물이 한 방울 비치는 걸 봤지. 머지않아 거기 놀란 눈물이 몇 방울 더 비칠 거야, 그건 다른 종류의 눈물이겠지만. 오! 우리는 자신만만하게 오늘부터 일주일을 기다리면 돼.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 48\n\n니콜라스는 무겁고 슬픈 마음으로, 괴로운 생각들에 짓눌린 채 다시 동쪽으로 발길을 돌렸어. 그러고는 치어리블 형제의 사무실로 향했지. 그동안 그가 헛되이 품어 보았던 희망이 어떤 것이었든, 마음속에 솟아올라 매들린 브레이의 고운 모습 둘레에 모여들던 즐거운 환상이 어떤 것이었든, 그것들은 이제 다 흩어져 버렸어. 그 환하고 기쁘던 기운은 흔적조차 남지 않았지.\n\n니콜라스는 매들린 브레이의 이름조차 모르던 시절, 그녀를 둘러싼 것만 같던 그 비밀이 마침내 풀렸어. 그것도 그런 식으로 풀렸지. 그렇다고 해서 그의 열정이 식거나 흠모의 마음이 차게 가라앉았다고 말하는 건, 니콜라스의 더 나은 본성을 깎아내리는 일이야. 그건 그가 받아 마땅한 평가도 결코 아니지. 전에 그가 그녀를 향해 품었던 마음이 그저 아름다움과 우아함에 끌린 젊은이들의 흔한 사랑 같은 것이었다면, 이제는 훨씬 더 깊고 강한 감정을 느끼고 있었거든. 하지만 그녀의 진실하고 순결한 마음에 대한 존경, 의지할 데 없고 외로운 처지에 대한 배려, 그토록 어리고 고운 사람이 겪는 시련에 대한 연민, 그리고 그녀의 크고 고결한 정신에 대한 감탄, 이 모든 것이 그녀를 그가 닿을 수 없는 저 높은 곳으로 끌어올리는 것만 같았어. 그리고 그것들이 그의 사랑에 새로운 깊이와 품위를 더해 주는 한편, 이 사랑은 가망이 없다고 속삭이는 것만 같았지.\n\n\"나는 그녀에게 한 약속을 꼭 지킬 거야.\" 니콜라스가 사내답게 말했어. \"내가 맡은 건 결코 가벼운 임무가 아니야. 나한테 지워진 이 두 가지 의무를 더없이 꼼꼼하고 철저하게 해낼 거야. 이런 일에서 내 은밀한 감정 따위는 고려할 가치가 없어. 그러니 조금도 고려하지 않겠어.\"\n\n그래도 그 은밀한 감정은 여전히 그대로 거기에 있었어. 그리고 니콜라스는 남몰래 그 감정을 누르기는커녕 오히려 부추기는 편이었지. 이렇게 따져 본 거야 — 따졌다고 한다면 말이지만. 그 감정을 마음속에만 두면 자기 말고는 누구한테도 해를 끼치지 않을 테고, 의무감 때문에 그걸 혼자만 간직한다면, 그 영웅적인 행동의 상으로 가끔 그 감정에 잠겨도 될 권리쯤은 더 생기는 거라고 말이야.\n\n이런 온갖 생각에, 그날 아침 본 광경과 다음 방문에 대한 기대까지 겹치니, 그는 더없이 멍하고 넋 나간 동무가 되어 버렸어. 어찌나 그랬던지 팀 링킨워터는 그가 어딘가에서 숫자 계산을 틀려 그게 마음에 걸리는 거라고 의심했지. 그래서 정말 그런 거라면 다 털어놓고 그 부분을 지워 버리라고 진지하게 권했어. 양심의 가책 때문에 평생을 괴로움 속에 보내느니 그게 낫다면서 말이야.\n\n그런데 팀의, 그리고 프랭크 씨의 이런 사려 깊은 말과 다른 여러 권유에도 니콜라스는 그저 한마디만 했어. 살면서 이보다 더 즐거운 적이 없다고 말이야. 그렇게 하루를 보냈고, 밤이 되자 그렇게 집으로 향했지. 여전히 똑같은 문제를 몇 번이고 되씹으면서, 똑같은 생각을 몇 번이고 거듭하면서, 그리고 몇 번이고 똑같은 결론에 다다르면서 말이야.\n\n이렇게 시름에 잠겨 갈피를 못 잡고 마음이 들떠 있을 때, 사람들은 까닭도 모른 채 어슬렁대고 빈둥거리기 마련이지. 벽에 붙은 광고를 잔뜩 들여다보면서도 그 내용은 한 단어도 머리에 안 들어오고, 가게 진열창을 뚫어지게 바라보면서도 거기 있는 물건은 하나도 안 보이는 거야. 니콜라스도 그랬어. 그가 집으로 가는 길에 지나야 했던 어느 작은 극장 바깥에 큼지막한 연극 광고가 걸려 있었는데, 그는 거기 푹 빠져 들여다보고 있었지. 곧 있을 어느 자선 공연에 출연하기로 한 남녀 배우들의 명단을 읽고 있었는데, 그 표정이 어찌나 진지했던지, 마치 운명의 책에서 가장 높은 자리에 오른 신사 숙녀들의 명단을 보며 거기서 제 이름을 애타게 찾고 있는 것만 같았어. 그러다 다시 걸음을 옮기려고 광고 맨 위쪽을 흘끗 보면서 자기가 그렇게 멍해 있는 게 우스워 빙긋 웃었지. 그런데 거기에 큼지막한 글자로, 글자 하나하나 사이에 큼지막한 간격을 두고 이렇게 적혀 있는 게 보였어. \"지방의 명배우 빈센트 크럼블스 씨, 정녕 마지막 무대!!!\"\n\n\"말도 안 돼!\" 니콜라스가 다시 돌아서며 말했어. \"그럴 리가 없는데.\"\n\n그런데 정말 그렇게 적혀 있었어. 한 줄에는 새 멜로드라마의 첫날 공연 소식이 따로 적혀 있었고, 또 한 줄에는 오래된 작품의 마지막 엿새 공연 소식이 따로 적혀 있었지. 세 번째 줄은 천하무적 아프리카 칼 삼키기 명인의 재출연에 바쳐졌는데, 이 사람은 친절하게도 설득에 못 이겨 지방 공연을 일주일 더 미뤄 주기로 했다는 거야. 네 번째 줄에는 스니틀 팀베리 씨가 최근의 심한 병환에서 회복하여 그날 저녁 영광스럽게 무대에 선다고 적혀 있었어. 다섯 번째 줄에는 밤마다 \"환호와 눈물과 웃음!\"이 넘친다고 했고, 여섯 번째 줄에는 지방의 명배우 빈센트 크럼블스 씨의 정녕 마지막 무대라고 적혀 있었지.\n\n\"분명 그 사람이 틀림없어.\" 니콜라스는 생각했어. \"빈센트 크럼블스가 둘일 리는 없잖아.\"\n\n이 문제를 확실히 매듭짓고 싶어서 그는 다시 광고를 살펴보았어. 그랬더니 첫 작품에 남작이 나오는데, 그 아들 로베르토 역을 어린 크럼블스 도련님이 맡았고, 조카 스팔레트로 역을 어린 퍼시 크럼블스 도련님이 맡았다는 거야. 그것도 그들의 마지막 무대라면서 말이지. 게다가 그 작품 중간에 등장인물들의 특색 있는 춤이 들어가고, 신동 아가씨의 캐스터네츠 독무가 있다는데, 이것도 그녀의 마지막 무대라는 거야. 이제 그는 더 이상 의심하지 않았어. 그래서 무대 출입문 앞에 가서 연필로 \"존슨 씨\"라고 적은 쪽지를 들여보냈지. 그러자 곧 허리에 아주 커다란 허리띠와 버클을 두르고 손에 아주 커다란 가죽 장갑을 낀 도둑 하나가 그를 옛 단장 앞으로 안내했어.\n\n크럼블스 씨는 그를 보고 진심으로 반가워했어. 작은 화장 거울 앞에 앉아 있다가 벌떡 일어났는데, 왼쪽 눈 위에는 아주 덥수룩한 눈썹 하나가 비뚜름하게 붙어 있었고, 한 손에는 짝이 되는 눈썹과 한쪽 다리의 종아리를 들고 있었지. 그러고는 그를 다정하게 끌어안으며, 떠나기 전에 작별 인사를 나누면 크럼블스 부인이 무척 기뻐할 거라고 했어.\n\n\"자네는 늘 그 사람이 아끼던 사람이었지, 존슨.\" 크럼블스가 말했어. \"처음부터 그랬어. 자네가 우리랑 처음 저녁을 먹던 그날부터 나는 자네에 대해선 마음이 아주 놓였다네. 크럼블스 부인이 마음에 들어 한 사람은 틀림없이 잘 풀리게 마련이거든. 아! 존슨, 그 사람은 정말 대단한 여자야!\"\n\n\"이 일이며 다른 모든 일에서 보여 주신 친절에 진심으로 감사드립니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그런데 작별 인사 얘기를 하시는 걸 보니, 대체 어디로 가시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"신문에서 못 봤나?\" 크럼블스가 제법 위엄 있게 말했어.\n\n\"못 봤습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"그것참 이상하군.\" 단장이 말했어. \"잡보란에 실렸는데. 여기 어딘가에 그 기사를 두었는데 — 글쎄, 어디 있더라 — 아, 그래, 여기 있군.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 크럼블스 씨는 잃어버린 줄 알았다는 시늉을 한참 하더니, 평소 입는 바지 주머니에서 한 치쯤 되는 신문 조각을 꺼냈어. 그 바지는 다른 신사 몇 사람의 평상복과 함께 방 안의 화장대 같은 데 여기저기 널려 있었지. 크럼블스 씨는 그 조각을 니콜라스에게 읽어 보라고 건넸어.\n\n\"재능 있는 빈센트 크럼블스 씨는 오래도록 지방 극단장이자 예사롭지 않은 기량의 배우로 이름을 떨쳐 온 분으로, 이제 연극 원정을 떠나 대서양을 건널 참이다. 듣자 하니 크럼블스 씨는 부인과 재능 넘치는 가족을 데리고 간다고 한다. 우리는 그만의 그 독특한 배역에서 크럼블스 씨보다 뛰어난 이를 알지 못하며, 공인으로서나 사인으로서나 이토록 폭넓은 친구들의 진심 어린 축복을 받으며 떠날 만한 사람도 알지 못한다. 크럼블스 씨는 반드시 성공할 것이다.\"\n\n\"여기 또 한 토막 있네.\" 크럼블스 씨가 더 작은 조각을 건네며 말했어. \"이건 독자 문의 답란에서 나온 거야, 이건.\"\n\n니콜라스는 그것을 소리 내어 읽었어. \"필로 드라마티쿠스 님께. 지방 극단장이자 배우인 크럼블스 씨는 마흔셋이나 마흔넷을 넘었을 리 없습니다. 또한 크럼블스 씨는 프로이센 사람이 아니며, 첼시 태생입니다.\" \"흠!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"참 묘한 기사로군요.\"\n\n\"아주 묘하지.\" 크럼블스가 콧등을 긁으며 대답했어. 그러고는 짐짓 아무렇지도 않은 척하며 니콜라스를 바라보았지. \"이런 걸 대체 누가 신문에 싣는지 모르겠단 말이야. 나는 안 그랬는데.\"\n\n크럼블스 씨는 여전히 니콜라스에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 더없이 진지한 표정으로 고개를 두세 번 절레절레 저었어. 그러고는 신문이 대체 어떻게 그런 걸 알아내는지 도무지 모르겠다고 한마디 하더니, 그 기사 조각들을 다시 접어 주머니에 넣었지.\n\n\"이 소식을 들으니 정말 놀랍군요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"미국으로 가시다니요! 제가 함께 있을 땐 그런 생각을 전혀 안 하셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 크럼블스가 대답했어. \"그땐 그런 생각이 없었지. 실은 말이야, 크럼블스 부인이 — 정말 대단한 여자라니까, 존슨.\" 그러고는 말을 끊더니 니콜라스의 귀에 대고 뭐라고 속삭였어.\n\n\"오!\" 니콜라스가 빙긋 웃으며 말했어. \"가족이 늘어날 참이라고요?\"\n\n\"일곱 번째 식구라네, 존슨.\" 크럼블스 씨가 엄숙하게 대답했어. \"신동 같은 애가 나왔으니 그게 마지막인 줄 알았는데, 또 하나 보게 생겼지 뭔가. 정말 비범한 여자야.\"\n\n\"축하드립니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"이 아이도 또 하나의 신동으로 자라기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 뭔가 예사롭지 않은 게 나오긴 할 거야, 아마.\" 크럼블스 씨가 대꾸했어. \"다른 셋의 재능은 주로 격투와 진지한 무언극에 있거든. 이 애는 소년 비극 쪽에 소질이 있으면 좋겠는데. 미국에서는 그런 걸 무척 원한다고 하더라고. 그래도 뭐, 나오는 대로 받아들여야지. 어쩌면 줄타기에 천재일지도 몰라. 한마디로 어떤 천재든 될 수 있지, 제 어미를 닮는다면 말이야, 존슨. 그 사람은 만능 천재거든. 어쨌든 천재성이 뭐든 간에, 그 천재성은 꼭 길러 줄 거야.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 나서 크럼블스 씨는 나머지 눈썹 한 짝과 두 다리의 종아리를 붙이고, 그다음 두 다리를 신었어. 그 다리는 누르스름한 살색이었는데, 무릎께가 꽤 더러워져 있었지. 저주하고 기도하고 마지막 발버둥을 치고, 그 밖에 격정적인 대목마다 그 관절로 자주 무릎을 꿇은 탓이었어.\n\n이 전직 단장은 화장을 마무리하면서 니콜라스에게 이런저런 이야기를 들려주었어. 운 좋게 꽤 괜찮은 공연 계약을 따낸 덕에 그 수익으로 미국에서 좋은 출발을 할 수 있을 거고, 또 자기와 크럼블스 부인이 영원히 무대에 설 수는 없는 노릇이라 — 명성의 숨결 속에서나, 비유적인 의미로나 모를까, 둘 다 불사신은 아니니까 — 그래서 그곳에 아주 눌러앉기로 마음먹었다는 거야. 자기 땅을 좀 마련해서 노년에 그것에 기대 살다가, 나중에 자식들에게 물려줄 수 있기를 바라면서 말이지. 니콜라스가 그 결심을 크게 칭찬하자, 크럼블스 씨는 두 사람이 함께 아는 친구들에 관해 재미있을 만한 소식을 마저 들려주었어. 그중에서도 스네벨리치 양이 극장에 양초를 대 주던 부유한 젊은 양초 장수와 행복하게 결혼했다는 것, 그리고 릴리비크 씨가 제 영혼이 제 것이라고 감히 말도 못 할 처지가 됐다는 것을 알려 줬지. 릴리비크 부인이 최고 권좌에 올라 무소불위로 군림하며 어찌나 폭군처럼 휘두르는지 말이야.\n\n니콜라스도 크럼블스 씨가 이렇게 속내를 털어놓아 준 데 답하여, 자기 본명과 처지와 앞날에 대해 그에게 털어놓았어. 그리고 두 사람이 처음 알게 된 사연도 되도록 짤막하게 두루뭉술한 말로 알려 주었지. 크럼블스 씨는 그의 형편이 나아진 것을 진심으로 따뜻하게 축하해 준 뒤, 다음 날 아침 자기와 식구들이 리버풀로 떠난다고 알려 주었어. 영국 해안을 떠나 그들을 실어 갈 배가 거기 정박해 있다면서 말이야. 그리고 니콜라스가 크럼블스 부인에게 마지막 작별 인사를 하고 싶다면, 그날 밤 자기와 함께 가야 한다고 했어. 근처 술집에서 가족을 위한 송별 만찬이 열리는데, 거기서 스니틀 팀베리 씨가 좌장을 맡고, 부좌장의 영예는 아프리카 칼 삼키기 명인이 떠맡을 거라면서 말이지.\n\n이때쯤 방은 무척 더워지고 사람도 좀 북적였어. 공연 중인 작품에서 방금 서로를 죽인 신사 넷이 들이닥친 탓이었지. 그래서 니콜라스는 초대를 받아들이고, 공연이 끝나면 돌아오겠다고 약속했어. 가스와 오렌지 껍질과 화약 냄새가 뒤섞여 가득한 그 덥고 번쩍이는 극장보다는, 바깥의 시원한 공기와 어스름이 더 좋았거든.\n\n그는 이 짬을 타서 은담뱃갑 하나를 샀어. 주머니 사정이 허락하는 한 가장 좋은 걸로 골랐지. 크럼블스 씨에게 줄 기념 선물이었어. 그 밖에 크럼블스 부인에게 줄 귀걸이 한 쌍, 신동 아가씨에게 줄 목걸이, 그리고 어린 신사들 하나하나에게 줄 화려한 셔츠 핀까지 사고는, 산책으로 기분을 좀 풀었지. 그리고 약속한 시간보다 조금 늦게 돌아와 보니, 불은 다 꺼졌고 극장은 텅 비었으며, 막은 그날 밤을 위해 내려져 있었어. 그리고 크럼블스 씨가 그가 오기를 기다리며 무대를 이리저리 거닐고 있었지.\n\n\"팀베리는 오래 안 걸릴 거야.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"오늘 밤 손님들을 다 내보낼 때까지 연기했거든. 마지막 작품에서 충직한 흑인 역을 하는데, 그래서 몸 씻는 데 시간이 좀 더 걸려.\"\n\n\"꽤 불쾌한 배역이겠는데요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"아니, 글쎄, 그렇지도 않아.\" 크럼블스 씨가 대답했어. \"지우기는 꽤 쉽거든. 얼굴이랑 목만 칠하니까. 예전에 우리 극단에 일급 비극 배우가 하나 있었는데, 그 친구는 오셀로를 연기할 때 온몸을 새까맣게 칠하곤 했지. 하지만 그건 배역에 푹 빠져 진짜처럼 들어가는 거야. 흔한 일은 아니지. 안타까운 노릇이지만 말이야.\"\n\n이제 스니틀 팀베리 씨가 아프리카 칼 삼키기 명인과 팔짱을 끼고 나타났어. 그리고 니콜라스에게 소개를 받자, 모자를 반 자나 들어 올리며 만나서 영광이라고 했지. 칼 삼키기 명인도 똑같이 말했는데, 생김새며 말투며 놀랄 만큼 아일랜드 사람 같았어.\n\n\"광고를 보니 편찮으셨다고 하던데요, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 팀베리 씨에게 말했어. \"오늘 밤 무리하신 탓에 더 나빠지지는 않으셨기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n팀베리 씨는 대답 대신 침울한 표정으로 고개를 절레절레 흔들더니, 의미심장하게 가슴을 여러 번 톡톡 두드렸어. 그러고는 망토를 몸에 더 단단히 여미며 말했지. \"하지만 괜찮습니다, 괜찮아요. 자, 갑시다!\"\n\n눈여겨볼 만한 점은, 무대 위 사람들이 기력이 다 빠져 더는 어쩔 수 없는 막다른 지경에 몰리면, 그때마다 어김없이 대단한 재주와 근력을 요하는 힘쓰는 묘기를 펼친다는 거야. 그러니까 피를 흘리며 죽어 가는 왕자나 산적 두목이, 너무 기진해서 더없이 부드러운 음악이 흐를 때나 겨우 움직이고 — 그것도 손과 무릎으로 기어서야 움직이는데 — 도움을 청하러 오두막 문으로 다가가는 장면을 보면 말이지. 어찌나 몸을 비틀고 꼬고, 다리를 오므렸다 펴고, 이리저리 뒹굴고, 일어났다가 또 고꾸라지는지, 그건 자세 잡기에 능한 아주 힘센 사람이 아니고서는 도저히 해낼 수 없는 거란 말이야. 스니틀 팀베리 씨에게는 이런 연기가 어찌나 자연스럽게 몸에 뱄던지, 극장을 나와 만찬이 열릴 술집으로 가는 길에 그는 최근의 병환이 얼마나 심했는지, 또 그게 신경계에 얼마나 큰 손상을 입혔는지를 일련의 체조 같은 동작으로 증명해 보였어. 그 모습은 지켜본 모두의 감탄을 자아냈지.\n\n\"세상에, 이건 정말 뜻밖의 기쁨이군요!\" 니콜라스가 소개되자 크럼블스 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"저도 그렇습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"이렇게 뵐 기회를 얻은 건 정말 우연이에요. 알았더라면 무슨 수를 써서라도 찾아왔을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"자네가 아는 아이가 여기 있네.\" 크럼블스 부인이 신동 아가씨를 앞으로 떠밀며 말했어. 아이는 자락을 잔뜩 댄 푸른 망사 원피스를 입고, 같은 천으로 된 바지를 입고 있었지. \"그리고 여기 또 하나, 또 하나.\" 부인은 어린 크럼블스 도련님들을 차례로 내보이며 말했어. \"그런데 자네 친구, 그 충직한 딕비는 어떻게 지내나?\"\n\n\"딕비요!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. 그 순간 이게 스마이크의 연극 무대 이름이었다는 걸 깜빡한 거야. \"아, 그래요. 그 친구는 아주 — 내가 무슨 소릴 하는 거지? — 그 친구는 영 좋지가 않습니다.\"\n\n\"세상에!\" 크럼블스 부인이 비극적으로 움찔하며 외쳤어.\n\n\"제 생각엔 말입니다.\" 니콜라스가 고개를 저으며 억지로 웃어 보이려 애썼어. \"지금 그 친구를 보시면 부군께서 예전보다 더 깊은 인상을 받으실 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻인가?\" 크럼블스 부인이 그 특유의 인기 있는 말투로 되물었어. \"어디서 이런 달라진 어조가 나오는 건가?\"\n\n\"제 비열한 원수 하나가 그 친구를 통해 저를 친 거예요. 그자는 저를 괴롭힌다고 여기면서, 그 친구한테 차마 말 못 할 공포와 불안의 고통을 안기고 있죠 — 죄송합니다, 정말로요.\" 니콜라스가 스스로 말을 멈췄어. \"이런 얘기는 절대 입에 올리면 안 되는데. 사정을 아는 사람들 말고는 한 적도 없고요. 잠깐 제 정신이 아니었네요.\"\n\n이렇게 황급히 사과하고 나서 니콜라스는 몸을 굽혀 신동 아가씨에게 인사하며 화제를 바꿨어. 속으로는 자신의 경솔함을 욕하면서, 크럼블스 부인이 이런 갑작스러운 폭발을 어떻게 생각할지 무척 궁금해하면서 말이지.\n\n그 부인은 그 일을 별로 마음에 두지 않는 눈치였어. 이때쯤 만찬상이 다 차려졌거든. 부인은 니콜라스에게 손을 내밀고는, 위엄 있는 걸음걸이로 스니틀 팀베리 씨의 왼편으로 가서 앉았지. 니콜라스는 영광스럽게도 그녀를 부축했고, 크럼블스 씨는 좌장의 오른편에 자리를 잡았어. 신동 아가씨와 어린 크럼블스 도련님들은 부좌석을 맡았지.\n\n손님은 모두 스물다섯에서 서른 명쯤 됐어. 당시 런던에서 일하거나 쉬고 있던 연극계 사람들 가운데, 크럼블스 부부와 가장 가까운 친구로 꼽히는 이들로 이루어져 있었지. 남자 손님과 여자 손님의 수는 거의 비슷하게 맞춰져 있었어. 잔치 비용은 남자 손님들이 댔는데, 저마다 여자 손님 하나를 자기 손님으로 초대할 권리가 있었거든.\n\n전체적으로 보면 꽤 쟁쟁한 모임이었어. 이날 스니틀 팀베리 씨 주위에 모여든 자잘한 연극계 인사들은 차치하고라도, 한 문인이 와 있었거든. 이 사람은 한창때 소설이 나오는 족족 무려 이백마흔일곱 편을 각색했는데 — 그중 몇 편은 소설이 나오기도 전에 각색했지 — 그 덕에 문인이 된 사람이었어.\n\n이 신사는 니콜라스의 왼편에 앉았는데, 그를 니콜라스에게 소개한 건 식탁 끝에 앉은 그의 친구 아프리카 칼 삼키기 명인이었어. 명인은 이 문인의 명성과 평판을 한껏 추켜세우며 소개했지.\n\n\"이렇게 명망 높은 분을 알게 되어 기쁩니다.\" 니콜라스가 정중하게 말했어.\n\n\"선생,\" 그 재담꾼이 대답했어. \"이쪽이야말로 환영입니다. 영광은 서로의 것이지요, 제가 책을 각색할 때 늘 하는 말처럼 말입니다. 선생, 명성이 뭔지 정의를 들어 본 적 있으십니까?\"\n\n\"여러 가지를 들어 봤지요.\" 니콜라스가 빙긋 웃으며 대답했어. \"선생의 정의는 뭡니까?\"\n\n\"제가 책을 각색하면 말입니다, 선생.\" 그 문인이 말했어. \"그게 바로 명성이지요. 그 책 지은이에게는요.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇군요!\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"그게 명성입니다, 선생.\" 그 문인이 말했어.\n\n\"그럼 리처드 터핀이며 톰 킹이며 제리 애버쇼는, 자기들이 가장 뻔뻔하게 강도질한 상대들의 이름을 후대에 길이 명성으로 남겨 준 셈이로군요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"그건 저도 잘 모르는 일입니다, 선생.\" 그 문인이 대답했어.\n\n\"셰익스피어가 전에 이미 활자로 나온 이야기들을 각색한 건 사실이지요.\" 니콜라스가 한마디 했어.\n\n\"빌 말씀이시군요, 선생?\" 그 문인이 말했어. \"그랬지요. 빌도 분명 각색을 한 사람이긴 합니다. 게다가 각색을 아주 잘했죠 — 그 시절치고는요.\"\n\n\"제가 하려던 말은 이겁니다.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어. \"셰익스피어는 두루 떠돌던 옛 설화와 전설에서 줄거리를 더러 따왔지요. 그런데 제가 보기엔, 요즘 선생 같은 직업을 가진 분들 가운데 몇몇은 그를 훨씬 앞질러 갔더군요 —\"\n\n\"아주 맞는 말씀입니다, 선생.\" 그 문인이 의자에 등을 기대고 이쑤시개를 놀리며 끼어들었어. \"인간의 지성은 말입니다, 선생, 그의 시대 이후로 발전해 왔고, 지금도 발전하고 있고, 앞으로도 발전할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"제 말은, 그를 앞질러 갔다는 게,\" 니콜라스가 다시 말을 이었어. \"전혀 다른 의미에서 그렇다는 겁니다. 그는 자기 천재성의 마법 같은 울타리 안으로 자기 목적에 꼭 맞는 전설들을 끌어들여, 흔하디흔한 것들을 별자리로 바꿔 놓았지요. 오랜 세월 세상을 밝혀 줄 별자리로 말입니다. 그런데 선생들은 자기네 둔함의 울타리 안으로, 무대에는 전혀 어울리지도 않는 소재를 끌고 들어와서, 그가 끌어올린 것을 도리어 깎아내리지요. 예를 들면, 선생들은 살아 있는 작가의 아직 끝나지도 않은 책을, 그 사람 손에서 나온 지 얼마 안 돼 인쇄 잉크도 채 안 마른 걸 가져다가, 선생네 배우들의 능력과 역량, 그리고 극장 사정에 맞춰 잘라 내고 토막 내고 도려내지요. 끝나지도 않은 작품을 끝내 버리고, 원작자가 아직 풀어내지도 않은 착상을 허둥지둥 어설프게 갖다 붙입니다. 그 착상이란 게 분명 그 사람이 며칠을 골똘히 고민하고 밤잠을 설치며 짜낸 것일 텐데 말입니다. 게다가 그가 보름 전에 마지막으로 써 둔 글귀까지 사건이며 대사를 죄다 견주어 가면서, 그의 줄거리를 앞질러 발표하려고 갖은 애를 다 쓰지요 — 이 모든 걸 그 사람 허락도 없이, 그 사람 뜻을 거슬러 가면서 말입니다. 그러고는 이 모든 짓의 마무리로, 그 작품에서 토막 낸 글들을 알맹이 없이 뒤죽박죽 섞은 걸 어느 시시한 소책자로 펴내면서, 거기에 선생 이름을 작가랍시고 박아 넣는 거지요. 똑같은 짓을 백 번도 더 저질렀다는 그 명예로운 칭호까지 덧붙여서요. 자, 이런 식의 좀도둑질과 길거리에서 남의 호주머니를 터는 게 뭐가 다른지 말씀해 보십시오. 그저 입법자들이 손수건은 끔찍이 아끼면서, 사람의 머릿속만큼은 — 폭력으로 두들겨 맞아 박살 나는 경우 말고는 — 알아서 제 앞가림하라고 내버려 두는 거라면 또 모르지만요.\"\n\n\"사람은 먹고살아야 하지 않습니까, 선생.\" 그 문인이 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했어.\n\n\"그건 두 경우 다 똑같이 그럴듯한 핑계겠지요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"하지만 굳이 그걸 근거로 삼으시겠다면, 저는 이 말밖에는 더 할 게 없습니다. 만약 제가 책을 쓰는 사람이고 선생이 목마른 극작가라면, 저는 선생의 술집 외상값을 여섯 달치나 — 그게 아무리 많더라도 — 대신 갚아 드리는 편을 택하겠습니다. 명예의 전당에서 육백 대를 두고 선생과 한자리를 차지하느니 말이지요. 그것도 제 좌대의 가장 초라한 구석 자리라 해도 말입니다.\"\n\n이쯤 이르자 대화가 다소 험악한 쪽으로 흘러갈 낌새였어. 그런데 마침 크럼블스 부인이 끼어들어 거친 충돌로 번지는 걸 막아 주었지. 부인은 그 문인에게 새 작품 여섯 편의 줄거리에 대해 이것저것 물었거든. 그건 아프리카 칼 삼키기 명인을 그 갖가지 천하무적 묘기 속에 등장시키려고 그가 계약을 맺고 쓴 작품들이었어. 그러자 그는 곧장 부인과 활기찬 대화에 빠져들었고, 거기에 정신이 팔린 나머지 방금 니콜라스와 벌인 논쟁 따위는 순식간에 까맣게 잊어버렸지.\n\n이제 식탁에서 든든한 음식들이 다 치워지고, 그 위에 펀치와 포도주와 독한 술이 놓여 두루 돌려졌어. 그러자 그전까지 서넛씩 작은 무리로 이야기를 나누던 손님들이 차츰 입을 다물어 쥐 죽은 듯 조용해졌지. 그리고 자리에 있던 사람들 대부분이 이따금 스니틀 팀베리 씨를 흘끔거렸고, 좀 더 대담한 이들은 손가락 마디로 식탁을 두드리는 것조차 서슴지 않으며 대놓고 기대를 내비쳤어. \"자, 팀,\" \"정신 차리세요, 좌장님,\" \"다들 잔 채웠습니다, 선생, 건배사만 기다리고 있어요\" 따위의 부추기는 말을 던지면서 말이지.\n\n이런 채근에도 팀베리 씨는 가슴을 두드리고 숨을 헐떡이는 것 말고는 어떤 대꾸도 하지 않았어. 그러고는 자기가 아직도 병환에 시달리고 있다는 걸 갖가지로 내비쳤지 — 무대에서든 무대 밖에서든 사람이 너무 값싸게 굴어서는 안 되는 법이니까. 한편 크럼블스 씨는 앞으로 나올 건배가 바로 자기를 위한 것임을 빤히 알고 있었어. 그래서 의자에 우아하게 앉아 한 팔을 등받이에 무심한 듯 걸치고 있다가, 이따금 잔을 입가로 들어 펀치를 조금씩 마셨지. 그 모습이 꼭 연회 장면에서 마분지 술잔으로 아무것도 안 든 걸 길게 들이켜던 그 태도 그대로였어.\n\n마침내 스니틀 팀베리 씨가 더없이 그럴듯한 자세로 일어섰어. 한 손은 조끼 가슴께에 찔러 넣고, 다른 손은 가장 가까이 있는 담뱃갑 위에 얹은 채로 말이지. 그러고는 큰 환호를 받으며, 인용구를 잔뜩 곁들여 자기 친구 빈센트 크럼블스 씨를 위해 건배를 제안했어. 꽤 긴 연설을 마무리하면서 그는 오른팔을 한쪽으로, 왼팔을 다른 쪽으로 쭉 뻗어, 크럼블스 씨와 크럼블스 부인에게 각각 그 손을 잡으라고 청했지. 이렇게 하고 나자 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 답사를 했고, 그게 끝나자 아프리카 칼 삼키기 명인이 감동적인 말로 빈센트 크럼블스 부인을 위해 건배를 제안했어. 그러자 크럼블스 부인과 여자 손님들에게서 크나큰 신음과 흐느낌이 터져 나왔지. 그런데도 그 영웅적인 여인은 한사코 직접 답사를 하겠다고 고집을 부렸고, 정말로 그렇게 했어. 그 태도와 연설은 그 누구도 능가한 적 없고, 견줄 만한 이조차 거의 없을 만한 것이었지. 그다음엔 스니틀 팀베리 씨가 어린 크럼블스 남매를 위해 건배를 제안할 차례였고, 그는 그렇게 했어. 그 뒤로 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 아버지로서 보충 연설로 일동에게 말을 건넸지. 아이들의 덕성과 사랑스러움과 빼어남을 한껏 늘어놓으면서, 이 아이들이 자리에 있는 신사 숙녀 모두의 아들딸이었으면 좋겠다고 바랐어. 이런 엄숙한 순서들이 끝난 뒤엔 음악을 비롯한 여러 여흥으로 흥겨워진 적당한 간격이 이어졌고, 그러고 나서 크럼블스 씨가 그 직업의 자랑인 아프리카 칼 삼키기 명인을 위해 건배를 제안했어. 자기의 더없이 소중한 벗이라고, 그렇게 불러도 된다면 말이지. 그렇게 부르도록 허락하는 데 딱히 거리낄 이유가 없었으니, 아프리카 칼 삼키기 명인은 흔쾌히 그러라고 했지. 그다음엔 그 문인을 위해 건배하려 했는데, 알고 보니 그는 또 다른 의미로 진작부터 취해 있었고, 그때 이미 계단에서 잠들어 있는 게 발견됐어. 그래서 그 뜻은 접고, 그 영예는 여자 손님들에게 돌렸지. 마침내 아주 오래 자리가 이어진 끝에 스니틀 팀베리 씨가 좌장 자리에서 일어났고, 일동은 숱한 작별 인사와 포옹을 나누며 흩어졌어.\n\n니콜라스는 작은 선물들을 건네려고 맨 마지막까지 남았어. 모두에게 작별을 고하고 크럼블스 씨 차례가 되자, 그는 이번 이별과 포츠머스에서의 작별이 사뭇 다르다는 걸 느끼지 않을 수 없었지. 그의 그 연극배우다운 태도는 한 톨도 남아 있지 않았어. 그가 내민 손짓에는 어떤 기색이 어려 있었는데, 만약 그걸 마음대로 불러낼 수만 있었다면 그는 그 시대 소박한 배역에서 으뜸가는 배우가 됐을 거야. 그리고 니콜라스가 진심으로 느끼는 따뜻함을 담아 그 손을 맞잡자, 그는 완전히 마음이 녹아내린 듯했지.\n\n\"우리는 참 행복한 작은 극단이었지, 존슨.\" 가엾은 크럼블스가 말했어. \"자네하고 나는 한 번도 다툰 적이 없었어. 내일 아침이면 자네를 다시 봤다는 걸 떠올리며 무척 기뻐하겠지. 그래도 지금은 차라리 자네가 안 왔더라면 싶을 만큼 마음이 그렇구먼.\"\n\n니콜라스가 막 밝은 대답을 하려는데, 그러던 그를 그루든 부인이 불쑥 나타나 몹시 당황하게 만들었어. 부인은 아침에 더 일찍 일어나려고 만찬에는 안 오겠다고 한 모양이었어. 그런데 이제 옆방 침실에서 아주 별난 흰 가운을 걸친 채 불쑥 뛰쳐나와, 그의 목에 두 팔을 두르고 무척이나 다정하게 끌어안았지.\n\n\"아니! 부인도 함께 가시는 겁니까?\" 니콜라스가 말했어. 그러면서도 마치 그녀가 세상에서 가장 어여쁜 처녀라도 되는 양 점잖게 받아 주었지.\n\n\"가느냐고?\" 그루든 부인이 대꾸했어. \"어이구, 맙소사, 내가 없으면 저 사람들이 어쩌겠나?\"\n\n니콜라스는 또 한 번 포옹을 받아 주었어. 그것도 아까보다 더, 그럴 수 있다면 말이지만, 한결 점잖은 태도로 말이야. 그러고는 되도록 쾌활하게 모자를 흔들어, 빈센트 크럼블스 일가에게 작별을 고했어.\n\n# CHAPTER 49\n\n니콜라스는 요즘 새롭게 눈앞에 펼쳐진 그 한 가지 일에만 온통 마음을 빼앗겨, 한가한 시간이면 매들린 브레이 생각에 잠기거나, 그녀를 위해 애쓰는 찰스 형제의 부탁을 들어주느라 그녀를 보고 또 봤어. 그리고 볼 때마다 마음의 평화가 점점 더 위태로워졌고, 굳게 세운 다짐도 점점 약해져 갔지. 그동안 니클비 부인과 케이트는 평화롭고 조용하게 지냈어. 스놀리 씨가 아들을 되찾겠다며 끈질기게 벌이는 소송과, 스마이크 본인을 걱정하는 마음 말고는 다른 근심거리가 없었지. 스마이크는 오래전부터 건강이 나빠지고 있었는데, 불안과 막막함에 시달리면서 상태가 점점 더 나빠졌어. 그래서 가끔은 그들과 니콜라스 모두 상당히 불안해했고, 심지어 깜짝 놀라기도 했지.\n\n그들을 이렇게 마음 졸이게 한 건 가엾은 그 청년의 불평이나 투정 때문이 아니었어. 스마이크는 자기가 할 수 있는 자잘한 일이라도 늘 기꺼이 맡으려 했고, 은인들에게 밝고 행복한 얼굴로 보답하려고 언제나 애썼거든. 그러니 그를 별로 아끼지 않는 사람의 눈에는 걱정할 만한 구석이 하나도 없어 보였을 거야. 하지만 어떤 때, 그것도 자주, 푹 꺼진 눈이 너무 반짝였고, 홀쭉한 뺨이 너무 발갛게 달아올랐고, 숨이 너무 거칠고 무겁게 오갔고, 몸이 너무 약하고 지쳐 보였어. 그 가족의 눈을 피할 수가 없을 만큼 말이야.\n\n세상에는 무서운 병이 하나 있어. 마치 희생자를 죽음에 맞게 다듬어 놓는 듯한 병이지. 그 추한 겉모습을 곱게 가다듬고, 낯익은 얼굴에 다가올 변화의 이 세상 것 같지 않은 기운을 둘러놓는 병이야. 영혼과 몸의 싸움이 어찌나 서서히, 조용히, 엄숙하게 이뤄지는지, 그리고 그 결과가 어찌나 확실한지, 하루하루, 한 알 한 알 죽어 가는 부분이 닳고 시들어 가. 그래서 짐이 가벼워질수록 영혼은 가뿐하고 밝아지지. 그리고 영원한 삶이 가까이 왔음을 느끼면서도 그것을 그저 새로운 인생의 한 단계쯤으로 여겨. 죽음과 삶이 어찌나 이상하게 뒤섞이는지, 죽음은 삶의 빛과 색을 띠고, 삶은 죽음의 앙상하고 끔찍한 형상을 띠는 병이야. 약으로도 결코 고친 적이 없고, 부자라고 막을 수도 없으며, 가난한 이라고 피할 수도 없는 병. 어떤 때는 성큼성큼 거인의 걸음으로 다가오고, 어떤 때는 굼뜨고 느릿느릿 다가오지만, 느리든 빠르든 언제나 어김없고 확실한 병이지.\n\n비록 자기 자신에게조차 결코 인정하려 하지 않았지만, 니콜라스는 마음 한구석에 이 병을 어렴풋이 떠올리며 이미 충실한 동무를 명망 높은 의사에게 데려갔었어. 의사는 당장 놀랄 일은 없다고 했어. 단정 지을 만한 증상이 지금은 없다는 거였지. 어릴 때 몸이 크게 시달리고 상하긴 했지만, 그래도 그 병이 아닐 수도 있다고 했어. 그게 전부였지.\n\n하지만 스마이크는 더 나빠지는 것 같지도 않았어. 그리고 그가 최근에 겪은 충격과 동요에서 이런 병색의 이유를 찾는 게 어렵지 않았으니, 니콜라스는 가엾은 친구가 곧 나으리라는 희망으로 스스로를 다독였지. 어머니와 누이도 그 희망을 함께 품었어. 그리고 모두가 한마음으로 걱정하는 그 당사자가 정작 본인은 불안해하거나 풀 죽은 기색이 없었거든. 오히려 날마다 조용히 미소 지으며 어제보다 한결 나은 것 같다고 대답했어. 그러니 그들의 두려움은 가라앉았고, 집안의 행복도 차츰 되살아났지.\n\n훗날 여러 해가 지나는 동안 니콜라스는 자기 인생의 이 시절을 몇 번이고 되돌아보았어. 옛날처럼 눈앞에 떠오르는 그 소박하고 조용하고 정겨운 장면들을 다시금 거닐었지. 몇 번이고, 여름 저녁 어스름 속에서, 혹은 가물거리는 겨울 난롯가에서 — 그때는 이만큼 자주도, 이만큼 슬프게도 떠오르진 않았지만 — 그의 생각은 그 옛 시절로 거슬러 올라가, 그 시절이 잔뜩 몰고 오는 자잘한 기억 하나하나에 즐거운 슬픔으로 머물곤 했어. 날이 어두워진 뒤에도 한참을 함께 앉아 그런 행복한 미래를 그려 보던 그 작은 방. 케이트의 명랑한 목소리와 즐거운 웃음소리. 케이트가 집을 비우면 식구들이 둘러앉아 그녀가 돌아오기를 기다리며, 케이트가 없으니 얼마나 적적한지 그 말이나 할 때 말고는 좀처럼 침묵을 깨지 않던 일. 가엾은 스마이크가 늘 앉아 있던 어두운 구석에서 신이 나서 벌떡 일어나 문을 열어 주러 달려가던 모습. 그리고 그의 얼굴에서 자주 보이던 눈물. 그 자신도 눈물이 흐르는 걸 보고 반쯤 의아해하면서도, 그렇게 기뻐하고 행복해하던 모습. 그 시절의 자잘한 사건 하나하나, 그때는 거의 마음에 두지 않았지만 분주한 근심과 시련을 까맣게 잊은 뒤에야 또렷이 떠오르는 사소한 말과 표정까지, 몇 번이고 생생하고도 무성하게 그의 앞에 되살아났어. 그리고 세월의 먼지 쌓인 더께 위로 살랑이며, 어제의 푸른 가지들이 되돌아왔지.\n\n하지만 이 추억들에 얽힌 다른 사람들도 있었고, 그 추억이 생기기까지 많은 변화가 있었어. 이 이야기를 풀어 나가기 위해 짚어 둬야 할 대목이지. 이 이야기는 곧 익숙한 흐름으로 가라앉아, 들뜬 예감이나 변덕스러운 곁길을 모두 피하고, 차분하고 점잖은 걸음을 이어 갈 거야.\n\n치어리블 형제는 니콜라스가 믿고 신뢰할 만한 사람임을 알게 되면서 날마다 그에게 새롭고 든든한 친절을 베풀었어. 그러면서 그에게 딸린 사람들도 못지않게 챙겼지. 니클비 부인에게 자잘한 선물을 이것저것 보냈는데, 늘 그 집에 가장 필요한 물건들이었어. 그래서 그 오두막은 적잖이 살림이 나아지고 보기에도 좋아졌지. 케이트의 자그마한 장신구함은 눈이 부실 만큼 가득 찼어. 그리고 말동무로 말할 것 같으면! 찰스 형제와 네드 형제가 일요일마다, 아니면 한 주에 한 번 저녁에 적어도 잠깐씩 들르지 않는 날이 없었어. 게다가 팀 링킨워터 씨가 있었지. 그는 평생 다른 사람과 여섯 명도 채 사귀어 본 적이 없는 사람인데, 새 친구들을 어찌나 좋아했는지 말로 다 할 수 없을 정도였어. 그래서 저녁 산책길에 끊임없이 오가며 쉬어 가곤 했지. 그런가 하면 프랭크 치어리블 씨도 무슨 묘한 우연들이 겹쳤는지, 무슨 볼일이 있다며 한 주에 적어도 사흘 밤은 그 집 문 앞을 지나가곤 했어.\n\n\"내 평생 그렇게 자상한 청년은 본 적이 없구나, 케이트.\" 어느 날 저녁 니클비 부인이 딸에게 말했어. 한참 동안 그 부인이 마지막에 말한 그 신사를 침이 마르도록 칭찬하는데도, 케이트는 입을 꾹 다물고 앉아만 있었거든.\n\n\"자상하다고요, 엄마?\" 케이트가 되물었어.\n\n\"어머나, 케이트!\" 니클비 부인이 늘 그렇듯 불쑥 소리쳤어. \"얼굴이 왜 그렇게 빨개졌니. 아니, 얼굴이 온통 달아올랐잖니!\"\n\n\"어머, 엄마! 별 이상한 생각을 다 하시네요!\"\n\n\"이상한 생각이 아니란다, 얘야, 내 분명히 봤다.\" 어머니가 대꾸했어. \"어쨌든 이젠 가셨으니까, 그랬는지 안 그랬는지는 그다지 중요하지 않구나. 우리가 무슨 얘길 하고 있었지? 아! 프랭크 씨 말이야. 내 평생 그렇게 자상한 사람은 본 적이 없어, 한 번도.\"\n\n\"설마 진심으로 하시는 말씀은 아니죠.\" 케이트가 다시 얼굴을 붉히며 대꾸했어. 이번에는 누가 봐도 분명했지.\n\n\"진심이 아니라니!\" 니클비 부인이 받아쳤어. \"왜 진심이 아니겠니? 이보다 더 진심인 적이 없었다. 그분이 나한테 보이는 그 예의와 자상함이야말로 아주 오랜만에 보는, 가장 흐뭇하고 보기 좋고 기분 좋은 일이라고 말해 두마. 요즘 젊은이들한테선 그런 행동을 좀처럼 보기 힘들지. 그러니까 막상 보게 되면 더 마음에 와닿는 거란다.\"\n\n\"아! 엄마한테 자상하단 말씀이군요.\" 케이트가 얼른 받았어. \"아, 네.\"\n\n\"세상에, 케이트.\" 니클비 부인이 되받았어. \"넌 참 별난 애로구나! 내가 그분이 다른 사람한테 자상하단 얘길 하고 있었겠니? 그분이 독일 아가씨를 사랑한다고 생각하니 정말이지 마음이 안됐구나, 그래.\"\n\n\"그분은 그런 거 아니라고 아주 분명히 말씀하셨잖아요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 대꾸했어. \"이 집에 처음 오신 날 밤에 그렇게 말씀하신 거 기억 안 나세요? 게다가,\" 케이트가 한결 부드러운 말투로 덧붙였어. \"설령 그렇다 한들 우리가 왜 안타까워해야 해요? 그게 우리랑 무슨 상관인데요, 엄마?\"\n\n\"우리랑은 상관없을지 몰라도, 케이트.\" 니클비 부인이 힘주어 말했어. \"나한테는 상관이 있단다, 솔직히 말하면. 나는 영국 사람이라면 철저히 영국 사람이었으면 좋겠어. 반쯤은 영국 사람이고 반쯤은 뭔지도 모를 그런 거 말고. 다음에 오면 대놓고 말해야겠다. 자기 나라 여자랑 결혼했으면 좋겠다고 말이야. 그러면 뭐라고 하는지 좀 보자꾸나.\"\n\n\"제발 그런 생각 마세요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 다급히 말했어. \"절대로요. 생각해 보세요. 그건 너무나—\"\n\n\"그래, 얘야, 너무나 뭐?\" 니클비 부인이 깜짝 놀라 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 말했어.\n\n케이트가 미처 대답하기도 전에, 묘하게 두 번 두드리는 노크 소리가 라 크리비 양이 그들을 보러 왔음을 알렸어. 그리고 라 크리비 양이 모습을 드러내자, 니클비 부인은 앞서 그 문제로 한바탕 따져 보고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았지만, 라 크리비 양이 타고 온 마차에 관한 온갖 추측을 쏟아 내느라 그 일을 까맣게 잊고 말았어. 마차를 몬 사람이 셔츠 바람의 그 남자였을 거라느니, 아니면 눈에 멍든 그 남자였을 거라느니, 누구였든 간에 지난주에 안에 두고 내린 그 양산은 못 찾았을 거라느니, 내려오는 길에 분명 하프웨이 하우스에서 한참 쉬었을 거라느니, 아니 어쩌면 손님이 꽉 차서 곧장 내처 왔을지도 모른다느니, 그리고 마지막으로 오는 길에 틀림없이 니콜라스를 지나쳤을 거라느니 하면서 말이야.\n\n\"그 사람은 못 봤어요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대답했어. \"그런데 그 정겨운 노인장, 링킨워터 씨는 봤지 뭐예요.\"\n\n\"저녁 산책을 하다가, 시내로 돌아가기 전에 여기 들러서 쉬려고 오는 거겠죠, 틀림없어요!\" 니클비 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"그런 것 같던데요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대답했어. \"특히 젊은 치어리블 씨도 함께 있었으니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇다고 링킨워터 씨가 여기 올 이유가 되진 않잖아요.\" 케이트가 말했어.\n\n\"아니, 난 그게 이유가 된다고 봐, 얘야.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"프랭크 씨는 젊은이치고 그리 잘 걷는 사람이 아니거든. 그런데 여기까지 오면 으레 지쳐서 한참을 푹 쉬어야 하더라고. 그런데 내 친구는 어디 있죠?\" 그 자그마한 여인이 케이트를 슬쩍 곁눈질하더니 두리번거리며 말했어. \"또 어디 끌려간 건 아니겠죠?\"\n\n\"아! 스마이크 씨는 어디 있지?\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"방금 전까지 여기 있었는데.\"\n\n좀 더 알아보니, 이 마음씨 좋은 부인이 깜짝 놀랄 만하게도, 스마이크는 방금 위층으로 자러 올라갔다는 거였어.\n\n\"어머나,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"정말 별난 사람이라니까! 지난 화요일에도—화요일이었나? 그래, 분명 화요일이었어. 기억나지, 케이트, 얘야, 젊은 치어리블 씨가 바로 지난번에 여기 왔던 날 말이야—지난 화요일 밤에도 똑같이 이상하게 사라졌잖니. 바로 문에서 노크 소리가 나던 그 순간에. 사람을 싫어해서 그런 건 아닐 거야. 니콜라스를 좋아하는 사람이면 누구든 늘 좋아하니까. 그리고 젊은 치어리블 씨가 그런 사람인 건 분명하고. 그런데 정말 이상한 건, 그 사람이 잠을 자러 가는 게 아니라는 거야. 그러니까 피곤해서 그런 것도 아니지. 잠자리에 안 드는 걸 내가 아는데, 내 방이 바로 옆방이거든. 지난 화요일에 그 사람보다 몇 시간이나 늦게 위층에 올라갔는데, 보니까 신발도 안 벗고 있더라니까. 게다가 촛불도 없었어. 그러니까 내내 어둠 속에 우두커니 앉아 있었던 거지. 정말이지,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"곰곰이 생각해 보니, 그거 참 희한한 일이구나!\"\n\n듣는 사람들이 이 말에 맞장구를 치기는커녕, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰라서인지, 아니면 말을 끊고 싶지 않아서인지 깊은 침묵에 잠겨 있자, 니클비 부인은 제 방식대로 이야기 실타래를 이어 갔어.\n\n\"이렇게 영문 모를 행동이,\" 그 부인이 말했어. \"혹시 침대에 드러누워 평생을 거기서 보내는 일의 시작은 아니었으면 좋겠구나. 터트베리의 목마른 여인이나 코크 골목의 유령처럼 말이야. 그런 별난 사람들 가운데 하나처럼. 그중 한 사람은 우리 집안과 무슨 인연이 있었지. 위층에 둔 오래된 편지들을 다시 들춰 보지 않고는 잘 모르겠는데, 우리 증조할아버지가 코크 골목의 유령과 같은 학교를 다녔던 건지, 아니면 터트베리의 목마른 여인이 우리 할머니와 같은 학교를 다녔던 건지 헷갈리는구나. 라 크리비 양, 당연히 아시겠죠. 목사님이 하는 말을 귀담아듣지 않았던 게 누구였죠? 코크 골목의 유령이었나요, 터트베리의 목마른 여인이었나요?\"\n\n\"코크 골목의 유령이었던 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"그럼 틀림없어요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"우리 증조할아버지가 같은 학교를 다닌 게 바로 그 유령이었던 거예요. 그분이 다닌 학교 선생이 비국교도였다는 걸 내가 아는데, 그러니까 코크 골목의 유령이 어른이 되어서 목사님한테 그렇게 버릇없이 군 게 다 그 탓이었던 거죠. 아! 유령을 가르치라—아니, 아이 말이에요—\"\n\n이 풍성한 주제에 대한 더 이상의 생각은 팀 링킨워터와 프랭크 치어리블 씨가 들이닥치는 바람에 뚝 끊기고 말았어. 그들을 맞이하느라 부산을 떠는 통에 니클비 부인은 다른 모든 걸 후딱 잊어버렸지.\n\n\"니콜라스가 집에 없어서 정말 안타깝네요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"케이트, 얘야, 네가 니콜라스 노릇도 하고 네 노릇도 해야겠다.\"\n\n\"니클비 양은 그저 자기 자신이기만 하면 됩니다.\" 프랭크가 말했어. \"저는—이렇게 말씀드려도 괜찮다면—그분이 조금이라도 바뀌는 건 반대입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 어쨌든 케이트가 더 계시라고 붙잡아야겠네요.\" 니클비 부인이 대꾸했어. \"링킨워터 씨는 십 분이라고 하시지만, 그렇게 빨리 보내 드릴 순 없죠. 니콜라스가 무척 서운해할 거예요, 분명히. 케이트, 얘야!\"\n\n유난히 의미심장한 고갯짓과 눈짓과 찌푸린 표정을 한껏 받은 케이트는 손님들에게 더 머물러 달라고 거들었어. 그런데 가만 보니 케이트는 그 청을 오로지 팀 링킨워터에게만 건네는 거였어. 게다가 그 태도에는 어딘가 어색한 구석이 있었지. 비록 그 어색함이 케이트의 우아함을 조금도 해치지 않았고, 뺨에 번진 발그레함이 미모를 조금도 깎아내리지 않았지만, 니클비 부인조차 한눈에 알아챌 정도였어. 하지만 그 신중한 부인은 자기 추측을 말로 옮겨 큰 소리로 떠벌릴 수 있는 경우가 아니고서는 그리 추리에 밝은 사람이 아니었거든. 그래서 그 묘한 기색을 두고, 딸이 마침 제일 좋은 옷을 안 입고 있어서 그러려니 하고 넘겨짚었어. \"그래도 저렇게 예뻐 보인 적이 없긴 한데, 그건 분명하지.\" 부인은 그러면서도 동시에 이렇게 생각했지. 이런 식으로 문제를 정리하고 나서, 이 경우든 다른 모든 경우든 자기 짐작이 틀림없이 옳다고 흐뭇하게 믿어 의심치 않으며, 니클비 부인은 그 생각을 머리에서 떨쳐 냈어. 그리고 자기가 이렇게 영민하고 눈치 빠른 사람이라는 데 속으로 흐뭇해했지.\n\n니콜라스는 집에 돌아오지 않았고, 스마이크도 다시 나타나지 않았어. 그렇지만 솔직히 말하면, 그 어느 쪽도 이 작은 모임에 별다른 영향을 끼치진 못했어. 다들 더없이 기분이 좋았거든. 정말이지 라 크리비 양과 팀 링킨워터 사이에는 제법 시시덕거리는 분위기가 피어올랐어. 팀이 우스갯소리와 익살을 수도 없이 늘어놓더니, 차츰 자상하다 못해 다정하다고 할 만큼 멋을 부렸지. 자그마한 라 크리비 양도 한껏 들떠서, 팀이 평생 그렇게 잘도 총각으로 지내 왔다고 놀려 댔어. 어찌나 그러는지 팀이 급기야, 누가 자기를 받아 주기만 한다면 지금이라도 처지를 바꿔 볼 수 있을지 모른다고 선언하기에 이르렀지. 라 크리비 양이 자기가 아는 부인을 진지하게 추천했는데, 링킨워터 씨에게 꼭 맞고 게다가 제 앞으로 꽤 넉넉한 재산도 있다는 거였어. 하지만 이 마지막 조건은 팀에게 별 효과가 없었어. 팀은 사내답게 펄쩍 뛰며, 자기한테 재산 따위는 중요하지 않다고, 사내가 아내에게서 찾아야 할 건 참된 됨됨이와 밝은 성품이라고, 그것만 갖추면 두 사람의 알맞은 살림에 쓸 돈이야 자기가 넉넉히 마련할 수 있다고 했지. 이 고백은 어찌나 팀의 명예를 드높이는 것이었는지, 니클비 부인도 라 크리비 양도 칭찬을 아끼지 않았어. 그리고 그 칭찬에 신이 난 팀은 자기 마음의 욕심 없음과 여성에 대한 깊은 헌신을 드러내는 다른 선언들을 줄줄이 쏟아 냈고, 그것 역시 못지않게 박수를 받았지. 이 모든 말과 행동에는 우스개와 진심이 익살스럽게 뒤섞여 있어서, 한바탕 웃음을 자아냈고, 정말이지 다들 즐거워졌어.\n\n평소 집안에서 대화의 활력이자 중심은 으레 케이트였어. 그런데 이날만큼은 평소보다 말수가 적었지(아마 팀과 라 크리비 양이 대화를 워낙 독차지해서였을 거야). 케이트는 이야기 무리에서 떨어져 창가에 앉아, 저녁이 깃들면서 짙어 가는 그림자들을 바라보며 밤의 고요한 아름다움을 즐기고 있었어. 그 아름다움은 프랭크에게도 그에 못지않게 끌리는 것이었나 봐. 그도 처음엔 곁을 서성이다가 이내 그녀 옆에 앉았거든. 여름 저녁에 어울리는 할 말이 무척 많다는 건 두말할 나위가 없지. 그리고 그 말들은 낮은 목소리로 하는 게 가장 좋다는 것도, 그게 그 시간의 평화와 고요에 가장 잘 어울리니까. 또 때때로 긴 침묵이 이어지고, 그러다 간절한 한두 마디가 오가고, 그러다 또 한 번 침묵이 찾아오는데 이게 어쩐지 침묵 같지가 않고, 어쩌면 이따금 황급히 고개를 돌린다거나 눈길을 바닥으로 떨군다거나 하는 거지. 이런 자잘한 일들이며, 촛불 들여오기를 꺼리는 마음이며, 시간을 분과 헷갈리는 버릇이며, 이 모든 게 다 그 시간이 부리는 조화일 뿐이라는 건, 사랑스러운 입술들이 숱하게 똑똑히 증언해 줄 거야. 그러니 마침내 촛불을 들여왔을 때 케이트의 반짝이는 눈이 그 빛을 견디지 못해 얼굴을 돌려야 했고, 심지어 잠시 방을 나가야 했던 걸 두고 니클비 부인이 놀랄 까닭은 조금도 없었어. 어둠 속에 그렇게 오래 앉아 있으면 촛불이 눈부신 법이거든. 그러니 그런 결과가 생기는 건 더없이 자연스러운 일이라는 걸, 세상 물정 아는 젊은이라면 누구나 알지. 그 점은 노인들도 알아, 아니 한때는 알았지. 그런데 가끔 이런 걸 잊어버리니, 그게 더 안타까운 노릇이야.\n\n하지만 이 마음씨 좋은 부인의 놀라움은 여기서 끝나지 않았어. 케이트가 저녁 식사에 입맛이 조금도 없다는 게 드러나자 놀라움은 한층 커졌지. 어찌나 큰일인지, 만약 바로 그때 모두의 시선이 아주 이상하고 흔치 않은 소리에 쏠리지 않았더라면, 니클비 부인의 걱정이 무슨 알 수 없는 웅변으로 터져 나왔을지 모를 일이었어. 그 소리는 얼굴이 새파랗게 질려 떨고 있는 하녀 아이의 말에 따르면, 그리고 모두의 귀에도 그렇게 들리는 듯했는데, 옆방 굴뚝에서 \"곧장 아래로\" 내려오는 소리였어.\n\n그게 아무리 기이하고 있을 법하지 않게 들려도, 어쨌든 그 소리가 정말로 그 굴뚝에서 나고 있다는 건 그 자리에 있는 모두가 똑똑히 알 수 있었어. 그리고 그 소리는(질질 끌고, 미끄러지고, 우르릉거리고, 버둥거리는 온갖 소리가 굴뚝에 막혀 한데 뒤섞인 묘한 소리였지) 여전히 계속됐어. 그래서 프랭크 치어리블이 촛불을 집어 들고, 팀 링킨워터는 부집게를 집어 들었지. 그들은 이 소동의 원인을 금세 밝혀냈을 거야. 니클비 부인이 그만 정신이 아득해져서는, 절대로 혼자 남지 않겠다고 버티지만 않았더라면 말이야. 그 바람에 잠깐 실랑이가 벌어졌는데, 결국 모두 한 무리가 되어 그 소란스러운 방으로 향하는 걸로 끝이 났어. 다만 라 크리비 양만 빠졌지. 하녀 아이가 어릴 적에 발작을 일으킨 적이 있다고 자진해서 털어놓는 바람에, 라 크리비 양이 그 애와 함께 남아 만일의 사태에 대비해 소리쳐 알리고 정신이 들게 해 주기로 했거든.\n\n그 수상한 방의 문 앞으로 다가가던 그들은 사람 목소리가 들려와 적잖이 놀랐어. 한껏 멋을 부린 구슬픈 가락으로, 게다가 마치 최고급 깃털 이불 대여섯 장 밑에 깔린 사람이 낼 법한 숨 막히는 목소리로, 한때 유행하던 \"내가 사모하는 그 아리따운 처녀, 그녀의 진심이 그예 식어 버렸나?\"라는 노래를 부르고 있었던 거야. 게다가 양해를 구할 새도 없이 방으로 들이닥쳐서도 그 놀라움은 가시지 않았어. 그 낭만적인 소리가 분명 굴뚝 위 어떤 남자의 목구멍에서 나오고 있었거든. 그런데 보이는 건 두 다리뿐이었어. 난로 위에 대롱대롱 매달린 채, 디딜 만한 맨 위 가로대를 어디 없나 하고 몹시 안달하며 더듬고 있는 것 같았지.\n\n이렇게 흔치 않고 사리에 안 맞는 광경에 팀 링킨워터는 그만 얼어붙고 말았어. 그는 낯선 이의 발목을 한두 번 살살 꼬집어 봤지만 아무 소용이 없자, 마치 다음번 공격을 위해 부집게를 벼리기라도 하는 양 부집게를 딱딱 맞부딪치며 서 있을 뿐, 다른 건 아무것도 하지 못했어.\n\n\"이건 분명 술 취한 사람일 거예요.\" 프랭크가 말했어. \"도둑이라면 이렇게 제 존재를 알리진 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n그는 잔뜩 분개해서 이렇게 말하며 두 다리를 좀 더 잘 보려고 촛불을 들어 올렸어. 그러고는 사정 봐줄 것 없이 그 다리를 끌어내리려고 앞으로 달려들던 참이었지. 그때 니클비 부인이 두 손을 맞잡더니, 비명인지 탄성인지 모를 날카로운 소리를 내며, 저 수상한 다리가 무릎바지에 회색 털양말을 신고 있는 게 아니냐고, 아니면 자기 눈이 잘못 본 거냐고 물었어.\n\n\"맞아요.\" 프랭크가 좀 더 가까이 들여다보며 외쳤어. \"무릎바지가 분명하고요, 그리고—그리고—거친 회색 양말도 신었네요. 아는 사람입니까, 부인?\"\n\n\"케이트, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 의자에 천천히 주저앉으며 말했어. 마치 이제 일이 막판에 이르렀으니 더 감추는 건 부질없다는 듯한, 자포자기 같은 체념이 서린 태도였지. \"얘야, 일이 정확히 어떻게 된 건지 좀 설명해 주련. 난 그분한테 부추긴 적이 없다—전혀, 눈곱만큼도 없어. 너도 잘 알잖니, 얘야. 그분이 고백할 때 아주 정중했어, 더없이 정중했지. 너도 봤잖니. 그래도 이런 식으로 시달려야 한다면, 채소 뭐시기들이며 온갖 텃밭 작물들이 바깥에서 내 길에 흩뿌려지고, 신사분이 집 안 굴뚝으로 기어들어 와 숨이 막힐 지경이 된다면, 난 정말 모르겠다—정말이지 모르겠어—내가 어찌 될지 말이다. 이건 정말 딱한 일이야—내가 네 가엾은 아빠랑 결혼하기 전에 겪었던 그 어떤 일보다 더 딱한 일이지. 그때도 꽤나 성가신 일을 겪긴 했다만—하기야 그건 으레 그러려니 하고 각오한 일이었으니까. 내가 지금 너보다 한참 어렸을 때, 교회에서 우리 옆자리에 앉던 젊은 신사가 있었단다. 그 사람은 거의 일요일마다 설교가 진행되는 동안 자기 신도석 앞쪽에다 내 이름을 큼지막한 글씨로 새기곤 했어. 물론 흐뭇한 일이었지, 자연스레 그렇기도 했고. 그래도 성가신 일이긴 했어. 그 신도석이 아주 눈에 띄는 자리였거든. 그 사람은 그러다가 여러 번 사람들 앞에서 교회지기한테 끌려 나갔단다. 하지만 그건 이 일에 비하면 아무것도 아니야. 이건 훨씬 더 고약하고, 훨씬 더 난처한 일이지. 차라리, 케이트, 얘야,\" 니클비 부인이 자못 엄숙하게, 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 말했어. \"차라리 돼지 얼굴을 한 여자로 사는 게 낫지, 이런 꼴을 당하느니 정말이지 그게 낫겠다!\"\n\n프랭크 치어리블과 팀 링킨워터는 도무지 놀라움을 참지 못해, 먼저 서로를 보았다가 케이트를 보았어. 케이트는 무슨 설명이 필요하다는 걸 알았지만, 그 다리의 출현에 대한 공포와, 그 임자가 숨이 막혀 죽지나 않을까 하는 걱정과, 이 수수께끼를 가능한 한 가장 우스꽝스럽지 않게 풀어 주고 싶은 마음 사이에서, 단 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 못했지.\n\n\"그분은 나한테 큰 고통을 준단다.\" 니클비 부인이 눈물을 닦으며 말을 이었어. \"큰 고통을 줘. 하지만 머리카락 한 올도 다치게 하지 말아 다오, 제발. 무슨 일이 있어도 머리카락 한 올 다치게 하면 안 돼.\"\n\n지금 형편으로 보면, 그 신사의 머리카락 한 올을 다치게 하기란 니클비 부인이 생각하는 것만큼 그리 쉬운 일이 아니었어. 그 머리 부분이 좁다란 굴뚝 안쪽 몇 자나 위에 박혀 있었으니까. 그런데 그동안 그 신사가 아리따운 처녀의 진심이 파산했다는 노래를 줄곧 멈추지 않더니, 이제는 아주 힘없이 꺽꺽거릴 뿐 아니라, 숨쉬기가 어려워졌는지 다리를 사납게 버둥거리기 시작했어. 그러자 프랭크 치어리블이 더는 머뭇거리지 않고 그 무릎바지와 털양말을 힘껏 잡아당겼지. 어찌나 세게 당겼는지, 그 신사는 프랭크가 예상한 것보다 훨씬 더 곤두박질치며 방 안으로 굴러떨어졌어.\n\n\"아! 네, 네.\" 이 별난 손님의 온몸이 이렇게 느닷없이 나타나자마자 케이트가 말했어. \"누군지 알겠어요. 제발 그분한테 거칠게 하지 마세요. 다쳤나요? 안 다쳤으면 좋겠는데. 아, 제발 다쳤는지 좀 봐 주세요.\"\n\n\"안 다쳤습니다, 정말이에요.\" 프랭크가 대답했어. 이 청을 듣고는, 자기를 놀라게 한 그 대상을 갑자기 다정하고 정중하게 다루면서 말이야. \"조금도 다치지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"더 가까이 못 오게 해 주세요.\" 케이트가 갈 수 있는 데까지 물러나며 말했어.\n\n\"아, 안 됩니다, 그러지 않을 거예요.\" 프랭크가 대꾸했어. \"보세요, 제가 여기 단단히 붙잡고 있잖아요. 그런데 이게 무슨 일인지 물어봐도 될까요? 그리고 이 노신사가 올 줄 알고 계셨나요?\"\n\n\"아, 아니요.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"당연히 몰랐죠. 하지만 그분은—엄마는 그렇게 생각 안 하시는 것 같지만—옆집에서 빠져나온 미친 신사분이에요. 여기 몰래 숨어들 기회를 잡으셨던 게 분명해요.\"\n\n\"케이트.\" 니클비 부인이 근엄한 위엄을 갖추고 끼어들었어. \"네가 그런다니 놀랍구나.\"\n\n\"엄마.\" 케이트가 부드럽게 항변했어.\n\n\"네가 그런다니 놀랍다.\" 니클비 부인이 거듭 말했어. \"정말이지, 케이트, 이 가엾은 신사분을 못살게 구는 사람들 편에 네가 끼어들다니 어이가 없구나. 그자들이 이분 재산을 노리는 비열한 속셈을 품고 있다는 걸, 그게 이 일의 모든 내막이라는 걸 너도 잘 알면서 말이야. 케이트, 차라리 링킨워터 씨나 치어리블 씨한테 이분 편을 들어 달라고, 이분이 억울한 일 안 당하게 해 달라고 부탁하는 게 훨씬 마음씨 고운 일이겠다. 네 감정에 휘둘려선 안 돼. 그건 옳지 않아, 전혀 옳지 않지. 그럼 내 심정은 어떻겠니, 생각해 봤니? 분개해야 할 사람이 있다면 누구겠어? 당연히 나지, 그게 마땅하고. 그래도 난 무슨 일이 있어도 그런 부당한 짓은 하지 않을 거야. 그래,\" 니클비 부인이 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우고는 수줍은 듯하면서도 위엄 있게 딴 데를 보며 말을 이었어. \"이 신사분도 내 말을 이해하실 거야. 내가 며칠 전에 드린 대답을 그대로 되풀이하겠다고. 앞으로도 늘 그렇게 되풀이하겠다고. 비록 이분이 나 때문에 이렇게 끔찍한 처지에 스스로 빠지는 걸 보면 진심이라는 건 믿지만 말이야. 그리고 이분께 곧장 점잖게 물러가 주십사 부탁드린다고. 안 그러면 이분 행동을 내 아들 니콜라스한테 비밀로 할 수가 없을 테니까. 고맙긴 해, 정말 고마운 분이지. 하지만 잠시라도 이분의 구애에 귀 기울일 수는 없어. 그건 도무지 안 될 일이야.\"\n\n이 말이 이어지는 동안, 그 노신사는 코와 뺨에 큼지막한 검댕 자국을 잔뜩 묻힌 채 팔짱을 끼고 바닥에 앉아, 깊은 침묵 속에서 자못 위엄 있는 태도로 구경꾼들을 바라보고 있었어. 그는 니클비 부인의 말에 조금도 관심을 두는 기색이 없었지. 그런데 부인이 말을 그치자, 그녀를 한참 빤히 쳐다보는 영광을 베풀더니, 이제 다 끝난 거냐고 물었어.\n\n\"더 할 말은 없어요.\" 그 부인이 다소곳이 대답했어. \"정말이지 더는 할 말이 없네요.\"\n\n\"아주 좋소.\" 노신사가 목소리를 높이며 말했어. \"그럼 병에 든 번개와 깨끗한 술잔, 그리고 병따개를 가져오시오.\"\n\n이 명령을 아무도 따르지 않자, 노신사는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 다시 목소리를 높여 천둥 샌드위치를 가져오라고 했어. 이것 역시 나오지 않자, 그는 장화 윗부분 프리카세에 금붕어 소스를 곁들여 달라고 청하더니, 호탕하게 웃으며 아주 길고, 아주 크고, 더없이 가락진 고함을 내질러 듣는 이들을 즐겁게 해 주었지.\n\n그래도 니클비 부인은 주위 사람들의 의미심장한 눈길에 답하듯 고개를 가로저었어. 마치 이 모든 일에서 자기는 아무것도 보지 못했다고, 굳이 따지자면 그저 약간 별난 구석 정도라고 안심시키려는 듯이 말이야. 부인은 생의 마지막 순간까지도 이 생각을 굳게 품고 있었을지 몰라. 자질구레하나마 사건의 양상을 통째로 뒤바꿔 놓은 일련의 일들만 없었더라면 말이지.\n\n마침 라 크리비 양은 자기가 돌보던 환자가 그리 위험한 상태가 아니라는 걸 알게 됐어. 그리고 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 보고 싶은 호기심에 못 이겨, 노신사가 한창 고함을 내지르고 있을 때 방 안으로 부산스레 들어왔지. 그런데 마침 그 노신사가 라 크리비 양을 본 순간, 그는 우뚝 멈추더니, 갑자기 폴짝 일어서서는 손에 미친 듯이 입을 맞추기 시작했어. 이렇게 태도가 돌변하는 통에 그 자그마한 초상화가는 거의 정신이 나갈 만큼 겁에 질려, 부랴부랴 팀 링킨워터 뒤로 도망쳐 숨었지.\n\n\"아하!\" 노신사가 두 손을 맞잡고 힘껏 맞비비며 외쳤어. \"이제야 그녀가 보이는구나. 이제야 보여! 내 사랑, 내 생명, 내 신부, 견줄 데 없는 미인이여. 마침내 그녀가 왔도다—마침내—이제 모든 게 가스등이요 각반이로다!\"\n\n니클비 부인은 잠시 다소 당황한 기색이었어. 하지만 곧 정신을 차리고는, 라 크리비 양과 다른 구경꾼들을 향해 몇 번이고 고개를 끄덕이고, 눈살을 찌푸렸다가, 진지하게 미소 지었지. 자기는 어디서 오해가 생겼는지 다 알고 있으며, 잠깐이면 다 바로잡겠다는 걸 알려 주려는 거였어.\n\n\"그녀가 왔도다!\" 노신사가 가슴에 손을 얹고 말했어. \"코모런과 블런더보어여! 그녀가 왔도다! 그녀가 나를 노예로 삼아 준다면 내가 가진 모든 재산이 다 그녀의 것이로다. 이만한 기품과 미모와 매혹이 또 어디 있단 말인가? 마다가스카르의 여제에게? 아니. 다이아몬드의 여왕에게? 아니. 매일 아침 칼리도르 목욕을 공짜로 하는 롤런드 부인에게? 아니지. 이들을 죄다 하나로 녹이고, 거기에 세 우미의 여신과 아홉 뮤즈, 옥스퍼드 거리의 비스킷 가게 딸 열넷을 더해 봐, 그래서 그 절반만큼이라도 사랑스러운 여인을 만들어 보라고. 흥! 어디 한번 해 보시지.\"\n\n이 황홀한 찬가를 늘어놓고 나서, 노신사는 손가락을 스무 번 서른 번쯤 딱딱 튕기더니, 라 크리비 양의 매력을 황홀하게 바라보는 명상에 잠겨 버렸어. 이 덕분에 니클비 부인은 설명할 좋은 기회를 잡았고, 곧장 그 일에 나섰지.\n\n\"제 생각엔,\" 그 마음씨 좋은 부인이 헛기침을 한 번 하고 운을 뗐어. \"이런 딱한 상황에서 다른 누군가가 저로 오해받는다는 건 정말 마음이 놓이는 일이에요—아주 큰 위안이죠. 이런 일은 전에 한 번도 없었거든요. 제가 제 딸 케이트로 오해받은 적은 여러 번 있었지만요. 그 사람들이 어지간히 어리석었던 게 분명하고, 좀 더 분별이 있었어야 마땅하지만, 그래도 어쨌든 저를 케이트로 본 거잖아요. 물론 그건 제 잘못이 아니고, 제가 그 책임을 져야 한다면 정말이지 너무 억울한 노릇이죠. 어쨌든 이번 경우엔, 당연히, 제가 누군가를—특히 제가 큰 신세를 진 분을—저 때문에 불편하게 만든다면 그건 제가 아주 잘못하는 일이라고 느끼지 않을 수 없어요. 그러니 저 신사분께 오해하셨다고 말씀드리는 게 제 도리라고 생각해요. 어떤 무례한 사람이 저를 두고 포장석 의회의 조카딸이라고 일러바쳤다던데, 제가 바로 그 부인이라고요. 그리고 제발 부탁이고 간청이니 조용히 가 주십사고요. 그게 다른 누구도 아닌,\" 여기서 니클비 부인은 새치름하게 웃으며 머뭇거렸어. \"저를 위해서라면 말이죠.\"\n\n이렇게 섬세하고 겸손하게 호소했으니, 노신사가 마음 깊이 감동받아 적어도 정중하고 알맞은 답이라도 돌려주었으리라 기대할 만했지. 그런데 니클비 부인이 받은 충격이 어떠했겠어. 노신사가 누가 봐도 부인을 똑바로 겨냥하며, 크고 쩌렁쩌렁한 목소리로 이렇게 대꾸했으니 말이야. \"썩 물러가라! 고양이야!\"\n\n\"이보세요!\" 니클비 부인이 기어드는 소리로 외쳤어.\n\n\"고양이!\" 노신사가 거듭 말했어. \"야옹이, 나비, 깡총이, 잿빛 마녀, 얼룩이, 줄무늬!\" 그러고는 이 사이로 쉭쉭 소리를 내뿜으며, 두 팔을 사납게 빙빙 돌리는 동시에, 니클비 부인에게 다가갔다 물러섰다 했어. 마치 장날에 사내아이들이 돼지나 양 같은 짐승이 엉뚱한 골목으로 고집스레 꺾어 들려 할 때 겁을 주느라 추는, 그런 야만스러운 춤을 추듯이 말이지.\n\n니클비 부인은 한마디 말도 더 하지 않고, 공포와 놀라움에 찬 비명을 지르더니, 곧장 까무러쳐 버렸어.\n\n\"엄마는 제가 볼게요.\" 케이트가 다급히 말했어. \"전 하나도 안 무서워요. 그러니 제발 저분을 데려가 주세요. 제발 데려가 주세요!\"\n\n프랭크는 이 부탁을 들어줄 수 있을지 영 자신이 없었어. 그러다 라 크리비 양을 몇 걸음 앞서 보내고 노신사더러 그녀를 따라가게 하는 꾀를 떠올렸지. 그 수가 기막히게 들어맞았어. 노신사는 한쪽엔 팀 링킨워터, 다른 한쪽엔 프랭크 본인이 단단히 호위하는 가운데, 황홀한 흠모에 젖어 따라 나갔거든.\n\n\"케이트.\" 위험이 사라지자 정신이 든 니클비 부인이 중얼거렸어. \"그 사람 갔니?\"\n\n갔다고 누군가 일러 주었지.\n\n\"난 평생 나 자신을 용서 못 할 거다, 케이트.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"절대로! 저 신사분이 정신을 잃은 거야. 그리고 그 가엾은 원인이 바로 나고.\"\n\n\"엄마가 원인이라고요!\" 케이트가 깜짝 놀라 말했어.\n\n\"그래, 나야, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 자포자기한 듯 차분하게 대답했어. \"며칠 전에 그 사람이 어떤지 너도 봤지. 그리고 지금 어떤지도 보잖니. 내가 몇 주, 몇 주 전에 네 오빠한테 말했단다, 케이트. 행여나 그 사람이 실연을 견디지 못할까 걱정된다고 말이야. 저렇게 폐인이 된 꼴을 좀 보렴. 좀 들떠 있긴 했어도, 정원에서 봤을 때 그 사람이 얼마나 조리 있고 분별 있고 점잖게 얘기했는지 너도 알잖니. 그런데 오늘 밤 그 사람이 저지른 그 끔찍한 헛소리며, 저 가엾고 불행한 자그마한 노처녀한테 들러붙던 꼴을 너도 들었지. 이게 다 어쩌다 이렇게 됐는지 누가 의심할 수 있겠니?\"\n\n\"의심할 사람은 거의 없을 것 같네요.\" 케이트가 부드럽게 말했어.\n\n\"나도 그럴 것 같구나.\" 어머니가 대꾸했어. \"그래! 내가 이 일의 불행한 원인이라면, 그래도 내가 탓할 일은 아니라는 걸 아니 그나마 마음이 놓인다. 내가 니콜라스한테 말했어. 이렇게 말했지. '니콜라스, 얘야, 우리 아주 조심해서 일을 처리해야 한다.' 그런데 그 애가 도통 내 말을 들으려 하질 않더구나. 처음부터 내가 바라던 대로 제대로 다뤘더라면 얼마나 좋았겠니! 그런데 너희 둘 다 어쩜 그렇게 너희 가엾은 아빠를 빼다 박았는지. 어쨌든 나한텐 나름의 위안이 있고, 그거면 됐다!\"\n\n이렇게 이 일에 대한 모든 책임을, 지난 일이든 지금 일이든 앞으로 올 일이든 다 손 털어 버리고 나서, 니클비 부인은 자기 자식들도 제 어미만큼만 스스로를 탓할 일이 있기를 바란다고 다정하게 덧붙였어. 그러고는 호위대를 맞을 채비를 했지. 그들은 곧 돌아와, 노신사를 무사히 집에 들여놓았다는 소식과, 친구들과 함께 흥에 겨워 놀고 있던 그의 보호인들이 그가 없어진 줄도 까맣게 모르고 있더라는 소식을 전했어.\n\n다시 고요가 찾아오자, 황홀한 반 시간이—훗날 집으로 걸어가는 길에 팀 링킨워터와 이야기를 나누며 프랭크가 그렇게 불렀지—이런저런 대화 속에 흘러갔어. 마침내 팀의 회중시계가 떠날 때가 한참 됐다고 일러 주자, 부인들만 남게 됐지. 그렇다고 프랭크가 그냥 간 건 아니야. 그는 밤이 아무리 깊더라도 니콜라스가 올 때까지 남아 있겠다고 몇 번이고 청했거든. 좀 전에 이웃이 그렇게 쳐들어왔으니, 혹시라도 둘만 남는 게 조금이라도 무섭다면 말이야. 하지만 두 사람이 더는 아무것도 두려워하지 않는 통에, 그가 보초를 서겠다고 우길 핑계가 없어졌어. 그래서 그는 어쩔 수 없이 그 성채를 떠나, 믿음직한 팀과 함께 물러갈 수밖에 없었지.\n\n거의 세 시간 동안 침묵이 흘렀어. 니콜라스가 돌아왔을 때, 케이트는 자기가 그토록 오래 혼자 앉아 제 생각에 잠겨 있었다는 걸 알고 얼굴을 붉혔지.\n\n\"정말이지 반 시간도 안 지난 줄 알았어.\" 케이트가 말했어.\n\n\"즐거운 생각이었나 보구나, 케이트.\" 니콜라스가 명랑하게 받았어. \"시간이 그렇게 훌쩍 지나가게 하다니. 그래, 무슨 생각이었는데?\"\n\n케이트는 당황했어. 탁자 위의 자잘한 물건을 만지작거리다가, 고개를 들어 미소 짓고, 다시 고개를 떨구며 눈물 한 방울을 흘렸지.\n\n\"이런, 케이트.\" 니콜라스가 누이를 끌어당겨 입을 맞추며 말했어. \"얼굴 좀 보자. 안 돼? 아! 그건 그저 흘끗이잖아. 그건 좀 너무하지. 그보다 더 오래 보자꾸나, 케이트. 자—그럼 내가 네 생각을 읽어 줄게.\"\n\n이 말에는, 비록 조금의 의식도 없이 무심코 던진 말이었지만, 어딘가 누이를 깜짝 놀라게 하는 구석이 있었어. 그래서 니콜라스는 웃으며 화제를 집안일로 돌렸지. 그리고 둘이 방을 나와 함께 위층으로 올라가는 동안, 스마이크가 밤새 얼마나 외로워했는지를 조금씩 알게 됐어. 그것도 아주 더디게야 알게 됐지. 이 일에 대해서도 케이트가 어딘가 말하기를 꺼리는 눈치였거든.\n\n\"가엾은 친구.\" 니콜라스가 그의 방문을 가만히 두드리며 말했어. \"대체 무엇 때문에 이러는 걸까?\"\n\n케이트는 오빠의 팔에 매달려 있었어. 문이 곧 열리는 바람에, 케이트는 미처 팔을 풀 새도 없었어. 얼굴이 몹시 창백하고 초췌한 스마이크가 옷을 다 차려입은 채 그들 앞에 마주 섰거든.\n\n\"아직 잠자리에 안 들었어?\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"아—아—안 잤어요.\" 그가 대답했지.\n\n니콜라스는 물러나려는 누이를 가만히 붙잡고는 물었어. \"왜 안 잤는데?\"\n\n\"잠이 안 와서요.\" 스마이크가 친구가 내미는 손을 꼭 잡으며 말했어.\n\n\"몸이 안 좋은 거야?\" 니콜라스가 다시 물었어.\n\n\"많이 나아졌어요, 정말로요. 아주 많이요.\" 스마이크가 얼른 말했어.\n\n\"그런데 왜 이렇게 자꾸 우울한 기분에 빠지는 거지?\" 니콜라스가 더없이 다정하게 물었어. \"아니면 왜 우리한테 그 까닭을 말하지 않는 거야? 넌 점점 딴사람이 되어 가, 스마이크.\"\n\n\"그래요, 저도 알아요.\" 그가 대답했어. \"언젠가 그 이유를 말할게요. 지금은 아니고요. 이런 제가 정말 밉네요. 다들 이렇게 좋고 친절하게 대해 주시는데. 그런데 어쩔 수가 없어요. 가슴이 너무 벅차요. 얼마나 벅찬지 모르실 거예요.\"\n\n그는 니콜라스의 손을 놓기 전에 한 번 꼭 쥐었어. 그러고는 함께 서 있는 오누이를 잠시 바라보았지. 마치 그 둘의 깊은 정에 무언가 가슴을 뭉클하게 하는 게 있다는 듯이. 그러더니 자기 방으로 들어갔어. 그리고 이내, 그 고요한 지붕 아래서 홀로 깨어 있는 사람이 되었지.\n\n햄프턴의 작은 경마장은 흥겨움이 한껏 무르익어 절정에 이르고 있었어. 날씨는 더없이 눈부셨지. 구름 한 점 없는 하늘 높이 해가 떠올라 가장 화려한 빛을 뿜어내고 있었어. 마차 좌석에서, 또 야단스러운 천막 꼭대기에서 바람에 펄럭이는 온갖 요란한 색깔들이 저마다 가장 화사한 빛을 내뿜었지. 낡고 칙칙하던 깃발이 새것처럼 되살아났고, 바래 가던 금박은 다시 반짝였어. 얼룩지고 다 삭은 천막 천도 새하얗게 보였지. 거지들의 누더기마저 산뜻해 보였어. 사람들은 이토록 그림 같은 가난에 푹 빠져 감탄하느라 자선이라는 마음씨조차 까맣게 잊고 말았지.\n\n가장 밝고 싱그러운 순간에 포착된, 생명과 활기가 넘치는 그런 풍경 가운데 하나였어. 보는 이의 마음에 들지 않을 수 없는 풍경이었지. 눈이 그 화려함과 번쩍임에 지친다 해도, 또 귀가 끝없이 이어지는 소음에 시달린다 해도, 눈은 거의 어디로 돌리든 들뜨고 행복하고 기대에 찬 얼굴들에서 쉴 수 있었거든. 그리고 귀는 그보다 더 거슬리는 소리에 대한 의식을, 즐겁고 흥겨운 소리 속에 묻어 버릴 수 있었어. 햇볕에 그을린 집시 아이들의 얼굴마저, 비록 반쯤 헐벗었다 해도, 한 줌의 위안을 안겨 주었지. 그 아이들에게 햇볕이 닿았다는 걸 보는 건 기분 좋은 일이야. 날마다 그 아이들에게 바람과 햇빛이 쏟아진다는 걸 아는 것도, 그 아이들이 정말 아이답게 아이의 삶을 살고 있다는 걸 느끼는 것도 말이지. 혹시 그 아이들의 베개가 젖어 있다면, 그건 눈물이 아니라 하늘에서 내린 이슬 때문이라는 것. 여자아이들의 팔다리가 자유롭고, 끔찍하게 뒤틀려 불구가 되거나, 제 성에 부자연스럽고 무시무시한 형벌을 짊어지는 일이 없다는 것. 그 아이들의 삶이 적어도 날마다 무시무시한 기계 한복판이 아니라 흔들리는 나무들 사이에서 흘러간다는 것. 그런 기계들은 어린아이가 어린 시절이 뭔지 알기도 전에 늙은이로 만들어 버리고, 늙음이 주는 죽음의 특권조차 없이 노년의 탈진과 쇠약만 안겨 주는데 말이야. 부디 옛날 옛적 동화가 사실이어서, 집시들이 그런 아이들을 수십 명씩 훔쳐 갔으면 좋겠어!\n\n그날의 큰 경주가 막 끝난 참이었어. 경주로 양옆에 빽빽이 늘어서 있던 사람들이 갑자기 흩어져 경주로 안으로 쏟아져 들어왔지. 그러자 풍경에 새로운 활기가 돌면서, 다시 온통 분주하게 움직였어. 어떤 이들은 우승한 말을 한번 보겠다고 다급하게 서둘러 갔어. 다른 이들은 더 좋은 자리를 찾아 떠나며 두고 온 마차를 찾느라 못지않게 다급하게 이리저리 내달렸지. 여기서는 한 무리가 콩과 골무 노름판 둘레에 모여, 어수룩한 풋내기 하나가 탈탈 털리는 걸 구경했어. 저기서는 또 다른 노름판 주인이 갖가지로 변장한 한패와 함께 있었지. 한 사람은 안경을 썼고, 또 한 사람은 외알 안경에 멋진 모자를 썼어. 세 번째 사람은 살림살이 넉넉한 농부 차림으로, 외투를 팔에 걸치고 큼지막한 가죽 지갑에 가짜 지폐를 잔뜩 넣고 있었어. 게다가 다들 손잡이가 묵직한 채찍을 들고서, 말을 타고 이곳까지 느긋이 달려온 더없이 순박한 시골 사람인 척했지. 그렇게 시끄럽게 떠들고 노름하는 척하며 방심한 손님 하나를 낚으려 했어. 그러는 동안 한패인 신사들은 — 깨끗한 셔츠에 좋은 옷차림으로 인상은 더 음흉했지 — 새로 오는 사람마다 불안하게 곁눈질하며 이 일에 깊은 관심이 있음을 드러냈어. 이들은 떠돌이 곡예사 둘레에 빙 둘러선 사람들 바깥쪽에 어슬렁거렸지. 그 곡예사는 또 시끄러운 악대나 '황소 고리 던지기'라는 옛날 놀이에 밀려나기도 했어. 한편 복화술사들은 나무 인형과 대화를 나누었고, 점치는 여자들은 진짜 아기들의 울음소리를 틀어막았지. 이들 또한 다른 많은 것들과 함께 사람들의 관심을 골고루 나눠 가졌어. 술 천막은 꽉 찼고, 마차 안에서는 잔이 부딪히기 시작했지. 광주리가 풀리고, 군침 도는 음식이 차려졌어. 나이프와 포크가 달그락거렸고, 샴페인 코르크가 펑펑 날아올랐지. 흐릿하지 않던 눈들이 더 환하게 빛났고, 소매치기들은 지난 경주 동안 챙긴 수확을 세고 있었어. 조금 전까지 한 가지 흥밋거리에 쏠려 있던 관심은 이제 백 가지로 흩어졌지. 어디를 보든, 잔치를 벌이고 웃고 떠들고 구걸하고 노름하고 익살을 부리는 잡다한 무리가 있었어.\n\n노름 천막은 잔뜩 늘어서 있었어. 바닥에 깔린 양탄자며, 줄무늬 휘장이며, 진홍색 천이며, 뾰족한 지붕이며, 제라늄 화분이며, 제복 입은 하인들이며, 온갖 화려함을 뽐내며 성업 중이었지. '이방인 클럽 회관', '아테나이움 클럽 회관', '햄프턴 클럽 회관', '세인트 제임스 클럽 회관'이 있었고, 그 안에서 노름할 수 있는 클럽 회관이 반 마일이나 늘어서 있었어. 또 '루주 에 누아', '프렌치 해저드', 그 밖에 즐길 노름들도 있었지. 우리 이야기가 향하는 곳이 바로 이 천막들 가운데 하나야.\n\n노름을 위해 탁자 세 개가 갖춰져 있었고, 노름꾼과 구경꾼으로 미어터졌어. 경마장에서 그런 곳 가운데 가장 큰 곳이었는데도, 안은 푹푹 쪘지. 바람이 더 들어오라고 천막 지붕 천을 한쪽 걷어 놓았고, 드나들기 편하게 문도 두 개나 있었는데도 말이야. 한두 사람은 왼손에 길게 만 반 크라운 동전 뭉치를 쥐고 — 그 사이사이에 소버린 금화가 몇 개 끼어 있었지 — 공이 구를 때마다 차분하게, 마치 일하듯 능숙하게 돈을 걸었어. 이건 그들이 노름에 익숙하고 하루 종일, 아마 그 전날도 하루 종일 노름을 했다는 걸 보여 주었지. 그런 한두 사람을 빼면, 노름꾼들에게 별달리 두드러진 점은 없었어. 대개 젊은이들이었는데, 호기심에 끌렸거나, 그날의 놀이 삼아 작은 돈을 걸고 따든 잃든 그리 크게 마음 쓰지 않는 듯했지. 하지만 그 자리에는 두 사람이 있었어. 어떤 부류의 유난히 좋은 본보기라서, 잠깐 짚고 넘어갈 만하지.\n\n이 가운데 한 사람은 쉰여섯에서 쉰여덟쯤 된 남자였어. 천막 입구 가운데 하나 가까이 의자에 앉아, 두 손을 지팡이 끝에 모으고 그 위로 턱을 괴고 있었지. 키가 크고 뚱뚱하며 몸통이 긴 남자였어. 연두색 외투를 목까지 단추로 채워서, 가뜩이나 긴 몸이 더 길어 보였지. 그 밖에 누르스름한 반바지에 각반을 둘렀고, 흰 목도리를 매고, 챙이 넓은 흰 모자를 썼어. 노름판의 온갖 웅성거림과 끊임없이 드나드는 사람들 한가운데서, 그는 더없이 차분하고 멍하니 있는 듯했지. 그 어떤 흥분의 기색도 손톱만큼 없었어. 지친 기색도, 또 무심히 보는 이의 눈에는 무언가에 관심 있는 기색도 드러내지 않았지. 그는 거기 앉아 아주 가만히, 차분히 있었어. 가끔, 하지만 아주 드물게, 지나가는 얼굴에 고개를 까딱하거나, 탁자 쪽에서 부르는 소리에 응하라고 종업원에게 손짓했지. 그러고는 다음 순간 다시 예전 상태로 가라앉았어. 그는 잠깐 쉬러 들어온 귀가 몹시 먼 노신사 같기도 했고, 누구의 존재도 전혀 의식하지 못한 채 친구를 끈기 있게 기다리는 사람 같기도 했지. 아니면 무아지경에 빠졌거나, 아편에 취한 사람 같기도 했어. 사람들이 돌아서서 그를 쳐다봤지만, 그는 아무런 몸짓도 하지 않았고, 누구와도 눈을 마주치지 않았어. 그들이 지나가게 두었고, 다른 이들이 와서 또 다른 이들로 이어지게 두면서, 아무것도 알아채지 못하는 척했지. 그가 막상 움직일 때면, 대체 무얼 보고 그러는 건지 신기할 정도였어. 사실이 그랬지. 하지만 드나드는 얼굴 가운데 이 남자가 못 보고 지나치는 얼굴은 하나도 없었어. 세 탁자 어느 곳에서든 그의 눈을 벗어나는 몸짓 하나 없었고, 물주들이 내뱉는 말 한마디도 그의 귀에 안 들어오는 게 없었지. 그가 짚어 내지 못하는 승자나 패자도 없었어. 그리고 그가 바로 이곳의 주인이었지.\n\n다른 한 사람은 '루주 에 누아' 탁자를 맡고 있었어. 아마 열 살쯤 젊은 듯했고, 통통하고 배가 나오고 다부진 사내였지. 아랫입술이 조금 오므라들어 있었는데, 돈을 치를 때마다 속으로 세는 버릇 때문이었어. 하지만 얼굴에 딱히 나쁜 표정은 없었고, 오히려 정직하고 쾌활한 편이었지. 날씨가 더워서 외투는 걸치지 않았어. 그는 탁자 뒤에 서 있었는데, 앞에는 크라운과 반 크라운 동전이 산더미처럼 쌓여 있었고, 지폐를 넣는 돈궤도 있었지. 이 노름은 쉼 없이 돌아갔어. 한 번에 스무 명쯤이 돈을 걸기도 했지. 이 남자는 공을 굴려야 했고, 돈이 놓이는 걸 지켜봐야 했어. 진 색깔에서 돈을 거둬들이고, 이긴 사람에게 돈을 치러야 했지. 그 모든 걸 더없이 재빠르게 해치우고, 다시 공을 굴려, 이 노름을 끝없이 살아 있게 해야 했어. 그는 그 모든 걸 그야말로 놀라운 속도로 해냈지. 머뭇거리는 법도, 실수하는 법도, 멈추는 법도 없었어. 그리고 다음과 같은 두서없는 말들을 쉴 새 없이 늘어놓았지. 반은 버릇이고 반은 뭔가 그럴듯하고 일하는 티가 나는 말을 하려고, 하루 종일 똑같이 단조로운 강조를 실어, 거의 똑같은 순서로 줄줄 쏟아 냈어.\n\n\"파리에서 온 루주 에 누아입니다! 신사 여러분, 돈을 거시고 소신껏 걸어 보세요. 공이 구르는 동안에는 언제든 됩니다. 파리에서 온 루주 에 누아, 신사 여러분, 프랑스 노름입니다, 신사 여러분, 제가 직접 들여왔어요, 정말이라니까요! 파리에서 온 루주 에 누아, 검정 승! 검정! 잠깐만요, 손님, 곧 치러 드리겠습니다. 거기 둘, 거기 반 파운드, 거기 셋, 그리고 거기 하나. 신사 여러분, 공이 구르고 있습니다, 공이 구르는 동안에는 언제든 됩니다, 손님! 이 노름의 묘미는 말이죠, 신사 여러분, 공이 구르는 동안에는 언제든 판돈을 두 배로 걸거나 돈을 내려놓을 수 있다는 겁니다. 또 검정! 검정 승! 이런 건 처음 봅니다. 평생 한 번도 본 적이 없어요, 정말이지 단 한 번도요. 지난 오 분 동안 누구든 검정에 걸었다면 공이 네 번 구르는 사이에 마흔다섯 파운드는 땄을 겁니다, 틀림없이요. 신사 여러분, 포트와인, 셰리주, 시가, 그리고 더없이 훌륭한 샴페인도 있습니다. 여기, 종업원, 샴페인 한 병 가져오고, 여기 시가도 열두 개나 열다섯 개쯤 가져와요. 편안하게들 즐기시죠, 신사 여러분. 깨끗한 잔도 좀 가져오고요. 공이 구르는 동안에는 언제든 됩니다! 어제 저는 공이 한 번 구르는 데 백서른일곱 파운드를 잃었습니다, 신사 여러분, 정말이라니까요! 안녕하십니까, 손님?\"(말을 멈추거나 목소리를 바꾸지 않고 아는 신사 하나를 알아보며, 우연인가 싶을 만큼 살짝 윙크를 보냈지.) \"셰리주 한 잔 드시겠습니까, 손님? 여기, 종업원! 깨끗한 잔 하나 가져와서 이 신사분께 셰리주를 따라 드리고, 빙 돌려 드려요, 종업원? 이게 파리에서 온 루주 에 누아입니다, 신사 여러분. 공이 구르는 동안에는 언제든 됩니다! 신사 여러분, 돈을 거시고 소신껏 걸어 보세요. 파리에서 온 루주 에 누아입니다, 아주 새로운 노름이죠, 제가 직접 들여왔어요, 정말이라니까요. 신사 여러분, 공이 구르고 있습니다!\"\n\n이 일꾼이 한창 제 일에 매달려 있는데, 예닐곱 사람이 천막을 어슬렁거리며 지나갔어. 그는 말도 일도 멈추지 않으면서, 그들에게 정중히 고개를 숙였지. 동시에 곁에 있던 사람에게 눈짓으로 그 무리 가운데 가장 키 큰 사람을 가리켰어. 그를 알아본 주인이 모자를 벗었지. 바로 멀버리 호크 경이었어. 그와 함께 그의 친구이자 제자, 그리고 신사 차림을 한 사내들 몇이 따라왔지. 정체가 모호하다기보다는 수상쩍은 자들이었어.\n\n주인이 낮은 목소리로 멀버리 경에게 안녕하시냐고 인사했어. 멀버리 경은 똑같이 낮은 목소리로 주인더러 꺼지라고 하고는, 돌아서서 친구들과 이야기를 나누었지.\n\n그가 호기심의 대상이 되었다는 걸 짜증스러워하며 의식하고 있는 게 분명했어. 자기에게 닥친 사고 이후 처음으로 사람들 앞에 모습을 드러내는 자리였으니까. 그리고 쉽게 알 수 있었지. 그날 그가 경마장에 나온 건 경기를 즐기려는 마음보다는, 자기를 아는 사람을 잔뜩 만나서 그 짜증스러운 일을 한꺼번에 최대한 넘겨 버리려는 속셈이었어. 얼굴에는 아직 옅은 흉터가 남아 있었지. 그리고 거의 매분마다 어슬렁거리며 드나드는 사람들에게 알아보일 때마다, 그는 장갑으로 그 흉터를 가리려고 안절부절못했어. 자기가 당한 망신을 얼마나 뼈저리게 느끼는지 드러내는 거였지.\n\n\"아! 호크.\" 무척 말쑥하게 차려입은 한 인물이 말했어. 뉴마켓 외투를 입고, 고급 목도리를 매고, 그 밖에 흠잡을 데 없는 온갖 장신구를 두르고 있었지. \"잘 지냈나, 친구?\"\n\n이자는 젊은 귀족과 신사들을 길들이는 일에서 경쟁자였어. 멀버리 경이 무엇보다 가장 싫어하고 마주치기를 두려워하는 자였지. 둘은 더없이 살갑게 악수를 나눴어.\n\n\"그래, 이제 좀 어떤가, 친구, 응?\"\n\n\"아주 좋아, 아주 좋아.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어.\n\n\"다행이군.\" 상대가 말했지. \"잘 지내나, 버리소프트? 우리 친구가 좀 풀이 죽었군. 아직 컨디션이 영 아닌가, 응?\"\n\n이 신사는 이가 무척 하얗다는 걸 짚어 둬야겠어. 그리고 웃을 핑계가 없을 때면, 대개 그 똑같은 외마디로 말을 맺었는데, 그걸 이가 드러나게 발음하곤 했지.\n\n\"이 친구 컨디션 아주 좋아. 아무 문제 없어.\" 젊은이가 무심하게 말했어.\n\n\"이거 정말 다행이군.\" 상대가 받아쳤지. \"브뤼셀에서 막 돌아왔나?\"\n\n\"어젯밤 늦게야 런던에 닿았어.\" 프레더릭 경이 말했어. 멀버리 경은 자기 일행 가운데 한 사람에게 말을 걸려고 돌아서며, 못 들은 척했지.\n\n\"이거 참, 정말이지,\" 친구가 속삭이는 척하며 말했어. \"이렇게 빨리 모습을 드러내다니 호크도 어지간히 대담하고 배짱 좋군. 내 신중히 하는 말일세. 거기엔 엄청난 용기가 필요하거든. 보다시피 그는 딱 호기심을 자극할 만큼만 시골에 처박혀 있었지, 사람들이 그 빌어먹게 불쾌한 일을 잊어버릴 만큼은 아니었어. 그건 그렇고, 자네야 물론 그 일의 진상을 알겠지? 어째서 그 망할 신문들에 거짓말이라고 따지지 않았나? 나야 신문을 거의 안 보지만, 그 일만큼은 신문에서 찾아봤지. 그런데 내가 정말이지—\"\n\n\"신문을 보게.\" 멀버리 경이 갑자기 홱 돌아서며 끼어들었어. \"내일—아니, 모레, 보겠나?\"\n\n\"이거 참, 친구, 나야 신문을 보는 일이 거의, 아니 아예 없지만,\" 상대가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했어. \"자네가 권하니 보겠네. 뭘 찾아보면 되나?\"\n\n\"그럼 잘 가게.\" 멀버리 경이 홱 발길을 돌리며 제자를 끌어당겼어. 그러고는 들어올 때처럼 다시 어슬렁어슬렁 무심한 걸음으로, 둘은 팔짱을 끼고 느릿느릿 빠져나갔지.\n\n\"그자한테 살인 사건을 읽으라고 주진 않겠어.\" 멀버리 경이 욕설을 섞어 중얼거렸지. \"하지만 채찍이 살을 가르고 몽둥이가 멍을 들게 한다면, 그거에 아주 가까운 걸 읽게 되겠지.\"\n\n그의 동행은 아무 말도 하지 않았어. 하지만 그 태도에 뭔가가 있어서, 멀버리 경은 자극을 받아 마치 그 친구가 니콜라스 본인이라도 되는 양 거의 똑같이 사납게 덧붙였지.\n\n\"오늘 아침 여덟 시도 되기 전에 젱킨스를 늙은 니클비한테 보냈어. 충직한 놈이야. 심부름꾼보다 먼저 나한테 돌아왔지. 처음 오 분 만에 다 알아냈어. 이 개자식을 어디서 만날 수 있는지 안다고. 시간도 장소도 다. 하지만 떠들 것 없어. 내일은 곧 올 테니까.\"\n\n\"그래서 내일 무, 무얼 할 셈인가?\" 프레더릭 경이 물었어.\n\n멀버리 호크 경은 성난 눈길을 한 번 던지는 걸로 답을 대신했지. 하지만 이 물음에 말로는 한마디도 답하지 않으려 했어. 둘은 마치 생각에 골똘히 잠긴 듯 시무룩하게 걸었지. 그러다 사람들 무리에서 완전히 벗어나 거의 단둘이 되자, 멀버리 경이 돌아가려고 몸을 휙 돌렸어.\n\n\"멈춰.\" 그의 동행이 말했어. \"자네한테 진지하게 할 말이 있어. 돌아가지 말게. 여기서 몇 분만 걷지.\"\n\n\"여기서가 아니라 저기서도 똑같이 할 수 있는 말을, 대체 나한테 뭐라고 하려는 건가?\" 그의 스승이 팔을 빼며 받아쳤어.\n\n\"호크.\" 상대가 받아쳤지. \"말해 주게. 난 꼭 알아야겠어.\"\n\n\"꼭 알아야겠다고.\" 상대가 깔보듯 끼어들었어. \"휘유! 말해 보게. 자네가 꼭 알아야겠다면, 물론 나야 빠져나갈 길이 없겠지. 꼭 알아야겠다라!\"\n\n\"그럼 꼭 물어야겠어.\" 프레더릭 경이 받아쳤지. \"그리고 솔직하고 분명한 답을 꼭 들어야겠어. 방금 자네가 한 말은, 그저 기분이 상하고 짜증이 나서 순간적으로 든 변덕인가, 아니면 자네의 진지한 뜻이고 실제로 마음먹은 일인가?\"\n\n\"아니, 그 일에 관해 어느 날 밤에 오간 이야기를 기억 못 하나? 내가 다리가 부러져 누워 있을 때 말이야.\" 멀버리 경이 비웃으며 말했어.\n\n\"아주 똑똑히 기억하지.\"\n\n\"그럼 그걸 답으로 알아, 빌어먹을,\" 멀버리 경이 대꾸했지. \"그리고 다른 답은 묻지 마.\"\n\n그가 제 봉에게 휘두르던 위세가 이만했고, 또 그 젊은이가 평소 굽히는 게 몸에 배어 있어서, 순간 젊은이는 그 일을 더 캐묻기를 반쯤 겁내는 듯했어. 하지만 그 마음이 잠시나마 그를 붙들었다 해도, 곧 그걸 떨쳐 내고 성을 내며 받아쳤지.\n\n\"자네가 말하는 그때 오간 이야기를 기억한다면, 난 이 일에 관해 강한 의견을 밝혔어. 내가 알거나 동의하는 한, 자네가 지금 으름장 놓는 그런 짓은 절대 못 한다고 말했지.\"\n\n\"자네가 날 막겠다고?\" 멀버리 경이 웃으며 물었어.\n\n\"그, 그래, 할 수 있다면.\" 상대가 곧장 대꾸했지.\n\n\"마지막 그 단서가 아주 적절하군.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어. \"또 자네한테 꼭 필요한 단서고. 오! 자네 일이나 신경 쓰고, 내 일은 나한테 맡겨 두게.\"\n\n\"이건 내 일이야.\" 프레더릭 경이 받아쳤지. \"내가 내 일로 삼겠어. 내 일로 삼고 말 거야. 이미 내 일이지. 지금만 해도 난 마땅한 것보다 더 깊이 얽혀 있어.\"\n\n\"자네 좋을 대로, 자네 하고 싶은 대로, 자네 자신을 위해 하게.\" 멀버리 경이 짐짓 느긋하고 너그러운 척하며 말했어. \"그럼 분명 만족스럽겠지! 날 위해서는 아무것도 하지 마. 그게 다야. 내가 하기로 마음먹은 일에 끼어들라고 누구한테도 권하지 않아. 자네는 날 그것보단 잘 알 텐데. 사실 보아하니 자네는 나한테 충고를 하려는 모양이군. 좋은 뜻인 건 의심하지 않아. 하지만 난 거절하네. 자, 괜찮다면 마차로 돌아가지. 여긴 즐길 게 없어, 오히려 그 반대야. 이 이야기를 더 끌면 다툴지도 몰라. 그건 자네한테든 나한테든 현명한 짓이 못 되지.\"\n\n이렇게 받아치고는, 더 이상 따질 틈도 주지 않고 멀버리 호크 경은 하품을 하더니 아주 느긋하게 발길을 돌렸어.\n\n이렇게 그를 다루는 방식에는 적잖은 요령과 젊은 귀족의 기질에 대한 이해가 담겨 있었어. 멀버리 경은 똑똑히 알았지. 자기 지배력이 이어지려면 지금 다져 놓아야 한다는 걸. 그는 자기가 거칠게 나가는 순간 젊은이도 거칠게 나오리라는 걸 알았어. 지배력을 약하게 만드는 무슨 일이 생길 때마다, 그는 여러 번 이런 냉정하고 짤막한 태도를 취해서 영향력을 다시 굳히곤 했지. 그래서 이번에도 거기에 기댔어. 그게 완전히 먹히리라는 걸 거의 의심하지 않으면서 말이야.\n\n하지만 그는 이렇게 하면서, 또 갈고닦은 재주로 지을 수 있는 가장 무심하고 심드렁한 태도를 꾸미면서, 속으로 결심했지. 감정을 억눌러야 했던 그 모든 굴욕을 니콜라스에게 한층 더 호되게 갚아 주겠다고. 그뿐 아니라 언젠가 어떤 식으로든 젊은 귀족도 그 대가를 톡톡히 치르게 만들겠다고 말이야. 젊은이가 자기 손안에서 고분고분한 도구일 때는, 멀버리 경은 그를 그저 경멸의 눈으로만 봤어. 하지만 이제 감히 자기와 반대되는 의견을 내세우고, 게다가 거만한 말투와 잘난 척하는 태도로 대드니, 그는 젊은이가 미워지기 시작했지. 더없이 천하고 하잘것없는 의미에서 자기가 그 약한 젊은 귀족에게 빌붙어 산다는 걸 알기에, 멀버리 경은 그의 손에 굴욕을 당하는 걸 더더욱 참을 수 없었어. 그리고 그가 젊은이를 싫어하기 시작하자, 그 미움의 크기를 — 사람들이 흔히 그러듯 — 자기가 그 상대에게 입힌 해악의 정도로 가늠했지. 멀버리 호크 경이 제자를 온갖 수단으로 등쳐 먹고, 속이고, 기만하고, 골려 먹었다는 걸 떠올려 보면, 그가 젊은이를 미워하기 시작하면서 진심으로 미워하게 된 것도 놀랄 일이 아니야.\n\n한편 젊은 귀족은 — 무슨 일에 대해서든 거의 하지 않던 짓이지만 — 니콜라스와의 일과 그 일로 이어진 정황을 곰곰이, 그것도 진지하게 생각해 본 끝에, 사내답고 정직한 결론에 다다랐어. 문제의 그 사건에서 멀버리 경이 보인 거칠고 모욕적인 행동이 그의 마음에 깊은 인상을 남겼거든. 멀버리 경이 제 속셈을 채우려고 자기를 부추겨 니클비 양을 쫓게 했다는 강한 의심이, 얼마 전부터 마음속에 도사리고 있었지. 그는 자기가 그 일에 끼었던 게 정말 부끄러웠고, 자기가 속아 넘어갔다는 께름칙함에 깊이 괴로웠어. 요즈음 시골에 물러나 있는 동안, 그는 이런 일들을 곱씹어 볼 시간이 넉넉했지. 그리고 그 무심하고 게으른 천성이 허락하는 때면, 이따금 그 기회를 활용했어. 자잘한 일들도 일어나서 그의 의심을 키웠지. 멀버리 경에 대한 그의 분노에 불을 붙이는 데는 아주 자잘한 일 하나면 충분했어. 그리고 방금 나눈 대화에서 멀버리 경이 보인 그 깔보고 거만한 말투가 — 멀버리 경이 언급한 그때 이후로 이 일에 관해 둘이 나눈 유일한 대화였지 — 바로 그 불을 댕긴 거였어.\n\n이렇게 둘은 친구들에게 돌아왔어. 저마다 상대를 향한 미움의 까닭을 가슴속에 사무치게 품은 채로 말이야. 게다가 젊은이는 니콜라스를 향해 으름장 놓아진 그 앙심에 찬 보복 생각에, 또 가능하다면 무슨 강한 수를 써서라도 그걸 막겠다는 결심에 시달렸지. 하지만 그게 다가 아니었어. 멀버리 경은 자기가 젊은이의 입을 확실히 막았다고 여기고는, 그 우쭐함을 누르지 못했어. 자기가 우위를 잡았다 싶은 걸 끝까지 밀어붙이지 않고는 못 배겼지. 거기엔 파이크 씨도 있었고, 플럭 씨도 있었어. 차우저 대령도, 같은 부류의 다른 신사들도 있었지. 멀버리 경에게는 자기가 영향력을 잃지 않았다는 걸 그들에게 보여 주는 게 큰일이었어. 처음에 젊은 귀족은 당장 그 관계에서 발을 빼기로 조용히 결심하는 걸로 만족했지. 하지만 차츰 화가 치밀었어. 몇 시간 전이었다면 재밋거리였을 농담과 허물없는 짓에 약이 올랐지. 그래도 그건 그에게 도움이 안 됐어. 일행한테 어울리는 그런 빈정거림이나 받아치기에서는 멀버리 경의 상대가 못 되었거든. 그래도 거칠게 틀어지는 일은 일어나지 않았어. 그들은 런던으로 돌아갔지. 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨, 그 밖의 신사들은 돌아가는 길에 자주 떠들어 댔어. 멀버리 경이 평생 이렇게 기분이 째진 적이 없었다고 말이야.\n\n그들은 함께 푸짐하게 저녁을 먹었어. 술이 거침없이 흘렀지, 사실 하루 종일 그랬듯이 말이야. 멀버리 경은 최근 술을 끊은 걸 벌충하려고 마셨고, 젊은 귀족은 분을 가라앉히려고 마셨어. 나머지 일행은 술이 최상품인 데다 자기들이 낼 돈이 없었으니까 마셨지. 그들이 술에 미친 듯 취하고, 피가 끓고, 머릿속이 불타오른 채 노름판으로 우르르 몰려 나간 건 거의 자정 무렵이었어.\n\n거기서 그들은 자기들처럼 미쳐 있는 또 다른 무리와 마주쳤어. 노름의 흥분과 후끈한 방, 번쩍이는 불빛은 그 흥분의 열기를 가라앉힐 만한 게 못 됐지. 그 어질어질한 소음과 혼란의 소용돌이 속에서, 사내들은 제정신이 아니었어. 이 순간의 사납게 취한 흥분 속에서 누가 돈이며 파산이며 내일을 떠올렸겠어? 술을 더 가져오라고 외쳤고, 잔이 연거푸 비워졌지. 바싹 마르고 타들어 가는 입은 갈증으로 갈라졌어. 술은 활활 타는 불에 기름을 붓듯 쏟아졌지. 그런데도 난동은 계속됐어. 흥청거림은 절정에 이르렀지. 손이 잔을 입까지 가져가지도 못하고 바닥에 내동댕이쳤어. 욕설을 내뱉을 말조차 제대로 빚어내지 못하는 입에서 욕설이 터져 나왔지. 술 취해 돈을 잃은 자들은 저주하고 고래고래 소리쳤어. 어떤 자들은 탁자 위에 올라가 술병을 머리 위로 흔들며 나머지에게 덤벼 보라고 했지. 어떤 자들은 춤추고, 어떤 자들은 노래하고, 어떤 자들은 카드를 찢으며 미친 듯이 날뛰었어. 소란과 광기가 천지를 뒤덮었지. 그때 다른 모든 소리를 삼켜 버리는 소음이 일었고, 두 남자가 서로의 멱살을 움켜쥔 채 몸싸움을 하며 방 한가운데로 휘청휘청 나왔어.\n\n그때까지 들리지 않던 열댓 명의 목소리가 둘을 떼어 놓으라고 큰 소리로 외쳤어. 따려고 정신을 차리고 있던 자들, 그런 판에서 밥벌이를 하는 자들이 싸우는 둘에게 달려들었지. 그러고는 둘을 억지로 갈라놓고 얼마쯤 떼어 놓았어.\n\n\"놓으시오!\" 멀버리 경이 걸걸하고 쉰 목소리로 외쳤어. \"이자가 날 쳤소! 들었소? 이자가 날 쳤다니까. 여기 내 편 없소? 이게 누구요? 웨스트우드. 이자가 날 쳤다고 내가 하는 말 들었소?\"\n\n\"들었어, 들었어.\" 그를 붙든 자들 가운데 하나가 대꾸했지. \"오늘 밤은 그만 가세!\"\n\n\"못 가, 절대로,\" 그가 대꾸했어. \"여기 열댓 명이 그 매질을 봤어.\"\n\n\"내일이면 시간은 넉넉해.\" 친구가 말했지.\n\n\"시간은 넉넉하지 않아!\" 멀버리 경이 외쳤어. \"오늘 밤, 당장, 여기서!\" 그의 격분이 어찌나 컸던지 말도 또박또박 못 하고, 주먹을 부르쥐고 머리카락을 쥐어뜯고 발을 쾅쾅 구르며 서 있었지.\n\n\"이게 무슨 일입니까, 각하?\" 그를 둘러싼 자들 가운데 하나가 말했어. \"매질이 오갔습니까?\"\n\n\"한 대 오갔지.\" 헐떡이며 답이 나왔어. \"내가 그를 쳤소. 여기 모두에게 알리는 바요! 내가 그를 쳤고, 그도 왜인지 알아. 그와 함께, 이 다툼을 지금 매듭짓겠소. 애덤스 대위,\" 젊은 귀족이 황급히 둘레를 둘러보며, 끼어들었던 자들 가운데 하나에게 말했어. \"이야기 좀 합시다, 부탁이오.\"\n\n말을 들은 사람이 앞으로 나서서 젊은이의 팔을 잡고, 둘은 함께 물러났어. 곧이어 멀버리 경과 그의 친구도 뒤따랐지.\n\n거긴 더없이 평판 나쁜 방탕한 소굴이었어. 그런 일이 어느 쪽이든 동정을 불러일으키거나, 더 이상의 만류나 끼어듦을 부를 만한 곳이 못 됐지. 다른 데였다면 그 일이 더 번지는 걸 당장 막고, 차분하고 냉정하게 돌아볼 틈을 주었을 거야. 하지만 거긴 아니었어. 흥청거림이 깨진 무리는 흩어졌지. 어떤 자들은 취해서 짐짓 점잔 빼는 얼굴로 비틀비틀 가 버렸고, 어떤 자들은 방금 벌어진 일을 시끄럽게 떠들며 물러났어. 남이 잃은 돈으로 먹고사는 명예로운 신사들은 나가면서 서로에게 호크가 명사수라고 했지. 그리고 가장 시끄럽게 굴던 자들은 소파에 곯아떨어져, 그 일은 더 생각지도 않았어.\n\n그러는 동안 이제 입회인이라 부를 만한 두 사람은, 저마다 제 당사자와 길게 이야기를 나눈 뒤 다른 방에서 만났어. 둘 다 더없이 매정했고, 둘 다 도시 한량이었지. 둘 다 도시의 더없이 못된 악덕에 푹 절어 있었고, 둘 다 빚에 깊이 빠져 있었어. 둘 다 한때 더 높은 자리에서 굴러떨어졌고, 둘 다 세상이 그럴듯한 이름을 붙여 주고 가장 타락한 관습을 핑계로 둘러대 주는 온갖 타락에 빠져 있었지. 그러니 당연히 그들 자신은 더없이 흠 없는 명예의 신사들이었고, 남의 명예에 대해서는 무척이나 까다로웠어.\n\n이 두 신사는 지금 유달리 들떠 있었어. 이 일은 꽤나 떠들썩해질 게 뻔했고, 그들의 명성을 드높여 줄 게 틀림없었거든.\n\n\"이거 거북한 일이군요, 애덤스.\" 웨스트우드 씨가 몸을 곧추세우며 말했어.\n\n\"아주요.\" 대위가 대꾸했지. \"매질이 오갔으니, 길은 당연히 하나뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"사과는 없겠지요?\" 웨스트우드 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"우리가 최후의 심판 날까지 떠든들, 제 쪽 사람한테서는 한마디도 안 나올 겁니다.\" 대위가 대꾸했지. \"제가 알기로 다툼의 본래 발단은 어떤 여자였답니다. 댁의 당사자가 그 여자한테 어떤 말을 했고, 프레더릭 경이 그 여자를 두둔하며 받아쳤지요. 한데 이게 온갖 아픈 주제와 비난, 맞비난으로 길게 번졌습니다. 멀버리 경은 빈정댔고, 프레더릭 경은 흥분했어요. 그리고 자극이 몹시 격해진 마당에, 화가 머리끝까지 치밀어 그를 친 겁니다. 멀버리 경 쪽에서 완전히 철회하지 않는 한, 프레더릭 경은 그 매질을 정당하다 내세울 작정입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 더 할 말은 없군요.\" 상대가 대꾸했지. \"만날 시간과 장소만 정하면 됩니다. 책임이 따르는 일이지요. 하지만 어서 끝내자는 마음이 강합니다. 해 뜰 무렵이라면 어떻습니까?\"\n\n\"빠듯하군요.\" 대위가 시계를 보며 대꾸했어. \"하지만 이 일이 오래 곪아 온 듯하고, 협상이란 말의 낭비일 뿐이니, 좋습니다.\"\n\n\"저쪽 방에서 벌어진 일이 있으니, 바깥에서 무슨 말이 나올지도 모릅니다. 그러니 지체 없이, 런던에서 완전히 벗어나는 게 좋겠습니다.\" 웨스트우드 씨가 말했어. \"트위크넘 맞은편 강가 풀밭 하나는 어떻습니까?\"\n\n대위는 반대할 게 없다고 봤어.\n\n\"피터셤에서 햄 하우스로 이어지는 가로수길에서 합류하고, 거기 도착해서 정확한 장소를 정할까요?\" 웨스트우드 씨가 말했지.\n\n대위도 여기에 동의했어. 몇 가지 다른 절차를 마찬가지로 짤막하게 거치고, 의심을 피하려고 양쪽이 갈 길을 정한 뒤, 둘은 헤어졌어.\n\n\"꼭 알맞게 여유가 있을 겁니다, 각하.\" 대위가 약속한 사항을 전한 뒤 말했지. \"제 방에 들러 권총 한 상자를 챙기고, 그런 다음 느긋하게 내려가시죠. 하인을 보내도 괜찮으시다면, 제 마차를 타죠. 각하 마차는 알아보일지도 모르니까요.\"\n\n거리에 나섰을 때, 그들이 방금 떠나온 그 광경과 어찌나 딴판이던지! 벌써 동이 트고 있었어. 안의 그 이글거리던 누런 불빛 대신, 맑고 환하고 눈부신 아침이 들어섰지. 후끈하고 답답한 공기, 꺼져 가는 등불 냄새가 배고 난동과 방탕의 김이 풍기던 그 공기 대신, 거리낌 없고 싱그럽고 건강한 공기가 들어섰어. 하지만 그 차가운 공기가 불어든 열에 들뜬 머리에는, 그 공기가 헛되이 보낸 시간과 흘려보낸 숱한 기회에 대한 회한을 잔뜩 싣고 오는 듯했지. 핏줄이 욱신거리고 살갗이 타오르며, 눈은 거칠고 무겁고, 생각은 다급하고 어지러운 채로, 그는 그 빛이 마치 자기를 꾸짖는 것만 같았어. 그래서 자기가 무슨 더럽고 흉측한 것이라도 되는 양, 저도 모르게 그 햇빛을 피해 움츠러들었지.\n\n\"떠시는군요?\" 대위가 말했어. \"추우신가 봅니다.\"\n\n\"좀 그러네.\"\n\n\"저 후끈한 방에서 나오니 쌀쌀하게 느껴지지요. 그 망토를 두르세요. 자, 그렇게. 자, 이제 갑시다.\"\n\n그들은 조용한 거리를 덜컹덜컹 지나, 대위의 숙소에 들렀고, 런던을 벗어나, 막힘이나 시비 없이 탁 트인 길로 나섰어.\n\n들판, 나무, 정원, 산울타리, 모든 게 무척 아름다워 보였어. 젊은이는 전에는 이것들을 거의 눈여겨본 적 없는 듯했지. 천 번도 더 똑같은 풍경을 지나쳤는데도 말이야. 그 모든 것 위에 평화와 고요가 깃들어 있었어. 어설프게 깬 그의 머릿속 어지러움과 혼란과는 묘하게 어긋났지만, 그래도 가슴에 와닿고 반가웠지. 그의 마음에 두려움은 없었어. 하지만 둘레를 둘러보니 노여움이 줄어들었지. 그리고 보잘것없던 옛 동행에 관한 온갖 헛된 믿음이 이제 다 걷혔지만, 그는 일이 이 지경에 이른 걸 떠올리기보다, 차라리 그자를 아예 몰랐더라면 싶었어.\n\n지난밤, 그 전날, 그 밖의 숱한 낮과 밤이 죄다 하나의 알 수 없고 갈피 없는 소용돌이 속에 뒤섞였어. 어느 때 일을 다른 때 일과 갈라낼 수가 없었지. 어느 순간엔 바퀴 소리가 그가 아는 가락의 토막들이 들리는 어떤 거친 곡조로 풀려나기도 했어. 또 어느 순간엔 귓속에 콸콸 흐르는 물 같은, 멍하고 어리둥절한 소리밖에 없었지. 하지만 동행이 너무 말이 없다고 핀잔을 주자, 둘은 시끌벅적 이야기하고 웃었어. 마차가 멈췄을 때, 그는 자기가 시가를 피우고 있는 걸 알고 좀 놀랐지. 하지만 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 언제 어디서 그 시가를 받았는지 떠올랐어.\n\n그들은 가로수길 어귀에 마차를 세우고 내렸어. 마차는 하인에게 맡겼지. 약삭빠른 녀석으로, 그런 일에 제 주인 못지않게 이골이 나 있었어. 멀버리 경과 그의 친구는 벌써 와 있었지. 넷은 깊은 침묵 속에 늘어선 위풍당당한 느릅나무들 사이의 길을 걸어 올라갔어. 머리 위 한참 높은 곳에서 맞닿은 나무들이, 고딕식 아치가 길게 이어지는 풍경을 이루었지. 마치 어떤 옛 폐허처럼, 그 끝은 탁 트인 하늘로 열려 있었어.\n\n잠시 멈춰 입회인들끼리 짤막하게 의논한 끝에, 그들은 마침내 오른쪽으로 돌았어. 작은 풀밭을 가로지르는 길을 따라, 햄 하우스를 지나 그 너머 들판으로 들어섰지. 그 가운데 한 들판에서 그들은 멈췄어. 거리를 재고, 흔히 거치는 절차 몇 가지를 마쳤지. 두 당사자가 합의한 거리만큼 떨어져 마주 보고 섰고, 멀버리 경은 처음으로 젊은 적수 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌렸어. 그는 무척 창백했지. 눈에는 핏발이 섰고, 옷매무새는 흐트러졌으며, 머리는 헝클어져 있었어. 얼굴은 사납고 흉악한 격정밖에 드러내지 않았지. 그는 손으로 눈을 가리고 적수를 한동안 뚫어지게 노려봤어. 그러고는 건네받은 무기를 들고 거기에 눈을 떨군 채, 신호가 떨어질 때까지 다시 고개를 들지 않았지. 그러다 신호가 떨어지자 곧바로 쏘았어.\n\n두 발의 총성은 거의 같은 순간에 울렸어. 그 순간, 젊은 귀족은 고개를 홱 옆으로 돌리더니, 끔찍한 눈길로 적수를 쏘아본 채, 신음도 비틀거림도 없이 쓰러져 죽었어.\n\n\"갔어!\" 웨스트우드가 외쳤어. 다른 입회인과 함께 시신으로 달려가, 그 곁에 한쪽 무릎을 꿇으며 말이야.\n\n\"제 피는 제 머리 위에 있을 뿐이지.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어. \"이건 제가 자초한 거야. 나한테 억지로 떠민 거라고.\"\n\n\"애덤스 대위,\" 웨스트우드가 황급히 외쳤어. \"이게 정정당당하게 된 일임을 증언해 주시오. 호크, 한순간도 지체할 수 없네. 당장 여길 떠나야 해. 브라이턴으로 내달려서, 있는 힘껏 빨리 프랑스로 건너가야 하네. 이건 고약한 일이야, 잠시라도 꾸물대면 더 고약해질지 몰라. 애덤스, 댁의 안전이나 챙기고, 여기 있지 마시오. 죽은 자보다 산 자가 먼저요. 잘 가시오!\"\n\n이 말과 함께 그는 멀버리 경의 팔을 붙들고 서둘러 끌고 갔어. 애덤스 대위는 — 결과가 죽음이라는 걸 한 점 의심 없이 확인하려고 잠깐 멈췄을 뿐 — 같은 방향으로 내달렸지. 자기 하인과 함께 시신을 옮길 방도를 짜고, 제 안전도 챙기려고 말이야.\n\n이렇게 프레더릭 버리소프트 경은 죽었어. 자기가 선물을 잔뜩 안겨 주고 천 번도 더 맞잡았던 그 손에. 그가 아니었다면, 또 그자 같은 자들이 아니었다면, 그는 행복한 사람으로 살다가 침대 둘레에 자식들 얼굴을 둘러두고 죽었을지도 모르는데, 바로 그자의 손에 말이야.\n\n해가 온 위엄을 떨치며 당당하게 떠올랐어. 고귀한 강은 굽이진 물길을 따라 흘렀지. 나뭇잎은 바람에 파르르 떨며 살랑거렸어. 새들은 나무마다 즐거운 노래를 쏟아 냈지. 덧없는 나비는 작은 날개를 팔랑였어. 낮의 온갖 빛과 생명이 깨어났지. 그리고 그 모든 것 한가운데, 잎새마다 스무 개의 자그마한 목숨을 품은 풀들을 짓누른 채, 죽은 사람이 누워 있었어. 뻣뻣하게 굳은 얼굴을 하늘로 향한 채 말이야.\n\n# CHAPTER 51\n\n음침하고 어둡고 먼지가 켜켜이 쌓인 낡은 집이 있었어. 그 집은 마치 주인을 꼭 닮은 듯, 그를 햇빛으로부터 꽁꽁 숨겨 두느라 — 그가 돈을 꽁꽁 숨겨 두었던 것처럼 — 누렇게 시들고 쪼그라든 것 같았지. 바로 거기에 아서 그라이드가 살고 있었어. 빈약하고 앙상한 모양에다 구두쇠 마음처럼 딱딱하고 차가운 낡은 의자와 탁자가, 음울한 벽을 따라 으스스하게 줄지어 늘어서 있었어. 속에 든 보물을 지키느라 비쩍 마르고 홀쭉해진 장롱들은, 도둑이 들까 늘 무섭고 두려운 듯 비틀거리며 어두운 구석에 웅크리고 있었지. 거기서는 바닥에 그림자조차 드리우지 않아서, 마치 남의 눈을 피해 숨고 움츠리는 것 같았어. 계단 위에는 키 크고 으스스한 시계가 있었어. 길고 가느다란 바늘에 굶주린 얼굴을 하고서, 조심스러운 속삭임처럼 똑딱거렸지. 그러다 시각을 알릴 때면, 늙은이 목소리 같은 가늘고 째지는 소리로, 배고픔에 꼬집힌 듯 덜덜거렸어.\n\n거기엔 편안히 쉬라고 손짓하는 난롯가 긴 의자 따위는 없었어. 팔걸이의자는 있었지만, 마음이 불편해 보였지. 의심스럽다는 듯 겁먹은 듯 팔걸이를 곧추세우고는, 경계를 늦추지 않았어. 어떤 것들은 기이하게 으스스하고 앙상했어. 키를 한껏 잔뜩 세우고 가장 사나운 표정을 지어, 들어오는 사람을 죄다 노려보며 기를 죽이려는 것 같았지. 또 어떤 것들은 옆엣것에 부딪히거나 벽에 기대어 몸을 지탱하고 있었어 — 좀 유난스럽게 말이야. 마치 자기들은 가져갈 가치가 없다고 온 세상 사람한테 증언이라도 시키려는 듯이. 어둡고 네모지고 육중한 침대들은 뒤숭숭한 꿈이나 꾸라고 만들어 놓은 것 같았어. 곰팡내 나는 휘장은 바람에 흔들릴 때면 듬성듬성 주름을 모으며 자기들끼리 소곤거리는 것 같았지. 어둡고 단단히 잠긴 벽장 속에 숨어 있는, 그 탐나는 물건들에 대한 떨리는 비밀을 말이야.\n\n이 빈약하고 굶주린 집 안에서도 가장 빈약하고 굶주린 방에서, 어느 날 아침 아서 그라이드 영감의 떨리는 목소리가 흘러나왔어. 잊힌 옛 노래의 끝자락을 힘없이 흥얼거리는 소리였는데, 후렴은 이랬지.\n\n타라란탄투, 낡은 신발을 던져라, 이 결혼에 행운이 깃들기를!\n\n그는 똑같이 새되고 떨리는 가락으로 이 대목을 몇 번이고 되풀이했어. 그러다 심한 기침이 터지는 바람에 그만둘 수밖에 없었고, 하던 일을 잠자코 계속했지.\n\n그 일이란, 좀먹은 옷장 선반에서 케케묵은 옷가지를 한 벌씩 꺼내는 거였어. 그러고는 옷을 햇빛에 비춰 보며 하나하나 꼼꼼하게 살핀 다음, 아주 정확하게 개어서 옆에 놓인 두 무더기 가운데 어느 한쪽에 올려놓았지. 그는 한 번에 옷 두 벌을 꺼내는 법이 없었어. 늘 한 벌씩만 꺼냈지. 그리고 선반에 손을 댈 때마다 어김없이 옷장 문을 닫고 열쇠를 돌려 잠갔어.\n\n\"코담배 빛깔 양복이라.\" 아서 그라이드가 닳아빠진 외투를 살펴보며 중얼거렸어. \"내가 코담배 빛이 잘 어울렸던가? 어디 보자.\"\n\n곰곰이 따져 본 결과는 영 신통치 않았던 모양이야. 그는 옷을 다시 개어 한쪽에 치워 두고, 또 한 벌을 꺼내려고 의자에 올라섰어. 그러면서 흥얼거렸지.\n\n젊고, 사랑스럽고, 어여뻐라, 오 그 얼마나 큰 행복인가! 이 결혼엔 틀림없이 행운이 깃들리!\n\n\"노래엔 늘 '젊고'를 넣지.\" 아서 영감이 말했어. \"하지만 노래야 운을 맞추려고 짓는 거잖아. 이건 내가 어릴 때 가난한 시골 사람들이 부르던 어리석은 노래고. 그래도 잠깐 — '젊다'는 것도 딱 맞는 말이지. 그건 신부를 뜻하니까 — 그래. 헤, 헤, 헤! 신부를 뜻하는 거야. 오 이런, 그것참 좋군. 아주 좋아. 게다가 사실이기도 하고, 아주 사실이지!\"\n\n이 발견에 흡족해진 그는, 곳곳에 떨림을 한두 번 곁들이고 표현을 한껏 살려 다시 한 번 그 구절을 불렀어. 그러고는 하던 일을 다시 시작했지.\n\n\"병 초록색이라.\" 아서 영감이 말했어. \"병 초록색은 입고 다니기에 끝내주는 양복이었지. 게다가 전당포에서 아주 싸게 샀고, 거기엔 — 헤, 헤, 헤! — 조끼 주머니에 빛바랜 일 실링짜리가 들어 있었단 말이야. 전당포 주인이 그 안에 일 실링이 든 줄도 몰랐다니! 난 알았거든! 옷감 품질을 살피면서 그게 만져졌단 말이지. 오, 그 멍청한 전당포 주인 같으니! 이 병 초록색은 운수 좋은 양복이기도 했어. 이걸 처음 입은 바로 그날, 늙은 맬로포드 경이 침대에서 불에 타 죽는 바람에, 사후 변제 어음이 죄다 굴러들었거든. 난 병 초록색을 입고 결혼하겠어. 페그. 페그 슬라이더스큐 — 난 병 초록색을 입을 테야!\"\n\n방문 앞에서 이렇게 두세 번 큰 소리로 부르자, 한 노파가 방 안으로 들어왔어. 키가 작고 비쩍 마르고 쪼글쪼글하고 눈이 짓무른, 중풍에 걸려 끔찍하게 못생긴 노파였지. 그 여자는 시든 얼굴을 더러운 앞치마에 닦으면서, 귀먹은 사람들이 흔히 쓰는 그 나직한 말투로 물었어.\n\n\"부르신 거예요, 아니면 시계가 친 거예요? 귀가 하도 어두워서, 어느 게 어느 건지 통 모르겠네요. 그래도 무슨 소리가 들리면, 이 집엔 그것 말고는 아무것도 움직이는 게 없으니까 둘 중 하나구나 하는 거죠.\"\n\n\"날세, 페그, 나야.\" 아서 그라이드가 알아듣게 하려고 자기 가슴을 톡톡 치며 말했어.\n\n\"영감님이요, 그래요?\" 페그가 되받았어. \"그래, 영감님은 뭘 원하시는데요?\"\n\n\"난 병 초록색을 입고 결혼하겠어.\" 아서 그라이드가 외쳤어.\n\n\"이건 결혼식에 입기엔 너무 멀쩡한데요, 나리.\" 양복을 잠깐 살펴본 페그가 대꾸했어. \"이것보다 더 허름한 건 없어요?\"\n\n\"쓸 만한 게 없어.\" 아서 영감이 대답했어.\n\n\"왜 쓸 만한 게 없어요?\" 페그가 쏘아붙였어. \"사내답게 그냥 평소 입는 옷을 입지 그래요 — 응?\"\n\n\"그건 어울림이 모자라, 페그.\" 주인이 대꾸했어.\n\n\"뭐가 모자라요?\" 페그가 물었어.\n\n\"어울림.\"\n\n\"뭐에 어울려요?\" 페그가 날카롭게 되물었어. \"입기엔 너무 늙어서 안 어울린단 말이에요?\"\n\n아서 그라이드는 가정부의 귀먹음에 대고 욕설을 중얼거리고는, 그 여자 귀에다 대고 버럭 소리쳤어.\n\n\"맵시가 모자란다고! 난 되도록 잘 보이고 싶단 말이야.\"\n\n\"잘 보여요?\" 페그가 외쳤어. \"그 여자가 영감님 말대로 그렇게 곱다면, 영감님은 거들떠보지도 않을걸요, 나리, 그건 맹세해도 좋아요. 그리고 영감님 모양새로 말하자면 — 후추소금 빛이든, 병 초록색이든, 하늘색이든, 격자무늬든 영감님한텐 그게 그거예요.\"\n\n이렇게 위로랍시고 한마디 던지고는, 페그 슬라이더스큐가 골라 둔 양복을 챙겨 들었어. 그러고는 깡마른 두 팔을 보따리 위에 포갠 채 서서, 입을 우물거리고 히죽거리며 눈물 고인 눈을 깜빡거렸지. 마치 어떤 괴이한 조각 작품에 새겨진 투박한 형상처럼 말이야.\n\n\"자네 기분이 별나구먼, 안 그런가, 페그?\" 아서가 영 마뜩잖은 태도로 말했어.\n\n\"왜요, 그럴 만하지 않아요?\" 노파가 되받았어. \"하지만 누가 날 누르고 부리려 들면, 난 곧장 토라질 거라고요. 그러니 미리 말해 둘게요, 나리. 이렇게 오랜 세월이 지났는데, 누구도 이 페그 슬라이더스큐 위에 올라설 순 없어요. 그건 영감님도 아실 테니 굳이 말 안 해도 되겠죠! 그런 건 나한테 안 통해요 — 안 돼요, 안 되고말고요, 영감님한테도 안 좋고요. 한 번만 그래 봐요, 그럼 망하는 거예요 — 망해요 — 망한다고요!\"\n\n\"오 이런, 이런, 난 절대 그러지 않을 거야.\" 아서 그라이드가 그 말에 질겁하며 말했어. \"무슨 일이 있어도 안 그래. 날 망하게 하는 건 아주 쉬운 일이지. 우린 아주 조심해야 해. 입 하나가 더 늘었으니, 전보다 더 아껴야 하고. 다만 우리는 — 우리는 그 여자가 고운 얼굴을 잃게 둬선 안 돼, 페그. 난 그걸 보는 게 좋거든.\"\n\n\"고운 얼굴이 돈깨나 든다는 걸 알게 되지 않도록 조심해요.\" 페그가 집게손가락을 흔들며 대꾸했어.\n\n\"하지만 그 여잔 스스로 돈을 벌 수도 있어, 페그.\" 아서 그라이드가 자기 말이 노파 얼굴에 어떤 효과를 내는지 열심히 살피며 말했어. \"그림도 그리고, 색칠도 하고, 걸상이며 의자 장식할 온갖 예쁜 것들을 만들 수 있단 말이야. 슬리퍼며, 페그, 시곗줄이며, 머리 사슬이며, 내가 이름의 절반도 못 댈 자잘하고 앙증맞은 물건을 천 가지나 말이지. 게다가 피아노도 칠 줄 알고 (게다가 더 좋은 건, 자기 피아노도 가졌어), 작은 새처럼 노래도 한단 말이야. 입히고 거두는 데 아주 돈이 적게 들 거야, 페그. 자네도 그렇게 생각하지?\"\n\n\"그 여자가 영감님을 바보로 만들게 두지만 않는다면, 그럴 수도 있겠죠.\" 페그가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"날 바보로 만든다고!\" 아서가 소리쳤어. \"이 늙은 주인이 고운 얼굴에 안 넘어갈 거라고 믿어 두라고, 페그. 아니, 아니, 아니야 — 못생긴 얼굴에도 안 넘어가지, 슬라이더스큐 부인.\" 그는 혼잣말처럼 나직이 덧붙였어.\n\n\"내가 못 듣게 뭔가 말하고 있잖아요.\" 페그가 말했어. \"그러고 있는 거 다 안다고요.\"\n\n\"오 이런! 이 여편네한테 마귀가 붙었나.\" 아서가 중얼거리고는, 능글맞게 곁눈질하며 덧붙였어. \"모든 걸 자네한테 맡긴다고 했어, 페그. 그게 다야.\"\n\n\"그렇게 하세요, 나리, 그럼 영감님 걱정은 다 끝나는 거예요.\" 페그가 흡족하게 말했어.\n\n'내가 그렇게 할 때면, 페그 슬라이더스큐,' 아서 그라이드는 속으로 생각했어. '자네 걱정도 다 끝나는 거지.'\n\n이걸 아주 또렷이 생각하면서도, 그는 노파가 눈치챌까 봐 감히 입술을 움직이지 못했어. 심지어 그 여자가 제 속마음을 읽었을까 봐 반쯤 겁이 난 것 같기도 했지. 그래서 그는 노파를 어르듯 곁눈질하며 큰 소리로 말했어.\n\n\"병 초록색에 풀린 솔기는 죄다 제일 좋은 검정 명주실로 꿰매. 제일 좋은 실 한 타래를 마련하고, 외투엔 새 단추도 달고 — 이건 좋은 생각이야, 페그, 자네도 좋아할 거야, 내 알지 — 내가 아직 그 여자한테 아무것도 안 줬으니, 그리고 처녀들이 그런 마음 씀씀이를 좋아하잖아, 위층에 반짝거리는 목걸이가 하나 있는데 그걸 자네가 닦아 둬. 그럼 결혼식 날 아침에 그 여자한테 줄 거야 — 내 손으로 그 매력적인 가느다란 목에 둘러 주고 — 그러고는 이튿날 도로 가져오는 거지. 헤, 헤, 헤! 그걸 그 여자 대신 넣어 잠가 두고, 잃어버리는 거야, 페그. 자, 누가 먼저 바보가 되는지 궁금하군 — 응, 페그?\"\n\n슬라이더스큐 부인은 이 기발한 꾀가 아주 마음에 드는 모양이었어. 그러고는 머리와 몸을 이리저리 들썩이고 씰룩거리며 흡족함을 드러냈는데, 그게 그 여자 매력을 조금도 더해 주진 못했지. 그 여자는 문가까지 절뚝거리며 갈 때까지 이걸 계속하다가, 이번엔 시큰둥하고 심술궂은 표정으로 바꿨어. 그러고는 아래턱을 좌우로 비틀며, 천천히 계단을 기어 내려가다가 거의 한 계단마다 숨을 고르면서, 장차 그라이드 부인이 될 여자에게 진심 어린 저주를 중얼거렸지.\n\n\"저 여잔 반쯤 마녀야, 내 보기엔.\" 아서 그라이드가 다시 혼자가 되자 말했어. \"하지만 아주 알뜰하고, 아주 귀가 먹었지. 저 여자 거두는 데 돈은 거의 안 들어. 게다가 열쇠 구멍에 귀를 대고 엿들어 봐야 소용없어, 못 들으니까. 참 좋은 여자지 — 이런 목적엔 말이야. 더없이 입이 무거운 늙은 가정부고, 제 무게만큼이나 값어치가 나가지 — 구리만큼이나 말이야.\"\n\n이렇게 거창한 말로 가정부의 미덕을 한껏 추켜세운 다음, 아서 영감은 다시 노래의 후렴으로 돌아갔어. 다가오는 혼례를 빛내 줄 양복도 골라 놨겠다, 그는 다른 옷들을 도로 제자리에 넣었지. 여러 해 동안 잠자코 잠들어 있던 곰팡내 나는 구석에서 옷을 꺼낼 때 못지않은 정성을 들여서 말이야.\n\n문에서 울린 초인종 소리에 깜짝 놀란 그는, 서둘러 이 작업을 끝내고 장롱을 잠갔어. 하지만 그렇게 서두를 필요는 없었지. 입 무거운 페그는 어쩌다 흐릿한 눈을 위로 들어 부엌 천장에 걸린 종이 흔들리는 걸 보지 않는 한, 종이 울린 줄도 좀처럼 몰랐거든. 그래도 잠깐 지나자, 페그가 비척비척 들어왔어. 그 뒤로 뉴먼 노그스가 따라 들어왔지.\n\n\"아! 노그스 씨!\" 아서 그라이드가 두 손을 비비며 외쳤어. \"내 좋은 친구, 노그스 씨, 무슨 소식을 가져오셨소?\"\n\n뉴먼은 흔들림 없는 무표정한 얼굴로, 가뜩이나 한곳에 박힌 눈을 더더욱 한곳에 박은 채, 말에 동작을 맞춰 대답했어. \"편지요. 니클비 씨한테서. 심부름꾼이 기다리고 있소.\"\n\n\"한—한—\"\n\n뉴먼이 고개를 들고 입맛을 다셨어.\n\n\"—의자에 좀 앉으시겠소?\" 아서 그라이드가 말했어.\n\n\"아니요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어. \"고맙소만.\"\n\n아서는 떨리는 손으로 편지를 뜯어, 그 내용을 더없이 게걸스럽게 삼키듯 읽었어. 황홀하게 낄낄거리며 몇 번이고 거듭 읽다가, 겨우 눈앞에서 편지를 떼어 놨지. 어찌나 읽고 또 읽었던지, 뉴먼은 자기가 와 있다는 걸 상기시켜 주는 게 낫겠다 싶었어.\n\n\"답장이오.\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"심부름꾼이 기다리고 있소.\"\n\n\"맞소.\" 아서 영감이 대답했어. \"그래—그래. 깜빡 잊을 뻔했군, 정말이지.\"\n\n\"잊으시는 줄 알았소.\" 뉴먼이 말했어.\n\n\"일깨워 줘서 고맙소, 노그스 씨. 오, 정말 잘했소.\" 아서가 말했어. \"그래. 몇 줄 적으리다. 내가—내가—좀 들떠서 말이오, 노그스 씨. 소식이—\"\n\n\"나쁜 거요?\" 뉴먼이 말을 잘랐어.\n\n\"아니오, 노그스 씨, 고맙소만. 좋은 거요, 좋아. 더없이 좋은 소식이지. 앉으시오. 펜과 잉크를 가져와서 몇 줄 답장을 쓰리다. 오래 붙들진 않으리다. 당신이 주인한테 보물 같은 사람이라는 건 나도 알아요, 노그스 씨. 그분이 가끔 어찌나 당신을 좋게 말하는지, 오 이런! 당신이 들으면 깜짝 놀랄 거요. 나도 그렇게 말한다고 해도 되겠지, 늘 그래 왔고. 난 늘 당신을 똑같이 말하거든.\"\n\n'당신이 그런다면, 그건 \"노그스 씨한테 진심으로 저주나 내려라!\"겠지.' 그라이드가 서둘러 나가자 뉴먼이 속으로 생각했어.\n\n편지가 바닥에 떨어져 있었어. 뉴먼은 잠깐 주위를 조심스레 살펴봤지. 자기 사무실 골방에서 엿들었던 그 음모가 어떻게 됐는지 알고 싶은 호기심에 못 이겨, 그는 편지를 집어 들고 재빨리 읽어 내려갔어. 내용은 이랬어.\n\"그라이드.\n\n오늘 아침에 브레이를 다시 만나, 자네가 일러 준 대로 모레로 결혼 날짜를 잡자고 했네. 그쪽은 반대가 없고, 그 딸한테는 어느 날이든 매한가지라네. 우리는 함께 갈 거고, 자네는 아침 일곱 시까지 내게 와 있어야 하네. 시간을 지키라는 말은 굳이 안 해도 되겠지.\n\n그동안 그 처녀한테는 더 이상 찾아가지 말게. 요즘 들어 자네는 가야 할 것보다 훨씬 자주 갔어. 그 여잔 자넬 그리워하는 게 아니니, 자칫 위험할 수도 있었네. 마흔여덟 시간만 그 젊은 혈기를 누르고, 처녀는 아비한테 맡겨 두게. 자넨 그 아비가 하는 일을, 그것도 잘하고 있는 일을 망쳐 놓을 뿐이야.\n\n자네의 벗,\n\n랄프 니클비.\"\n바깥에서 발소리가 들렸어. 뉴먼은 편지를 다시 그 자리에 떨어뜨리고, 날아가지 않게 발로 지그시 눌렀지. 그러고는 단번에 성큼 제자리로 돌아가, 세상 누구보다 멍하고 아무것도 모르는 얼굴을 했어. 아서 그라이드는 신경질적으로 주위를 두리번거리다가, 바닥에 떨어진 편지를 발견하고 집어 들었어. 그러고는 답장을 쓰려고 앉다가, 뉴먼 노그스를 흘끗 봤지. 뉴먼은 벽을 어찌나 뚫어지게 노려보고 있던지, 아서는 깜짝 놀라고 말았어.\n\n\"뭐 특별한 거라도 보이오, 노그스 씨?\" 아서가 뉴먼의 시선을 따라가 보려 애쓰며 말했어 — 그건 불가능한 일이었고, 여태 누구도 해낸 적 없는 일이었지.\n\n\"거미줄이오.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어.\n\n\"오! 그게 다요?\"\n\n\"아니오.\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"거기에 파리가 한 마리 걸려 있소.\"\n\n\"여긴 거미줄이 꽤 많소.\" 아서 그라이드가 말했어.\n\n\"우리 집도 그렇소.\" 뉴먼이 대꾸했어. \"파리도 많고.\"\n\n뉴먼은 이 응수에서 큰 재미를 보는 모양이었어. 그러고는 아서 그라이드의 신경을 잔뜩 거슬리게 하면서, 손가락 마디에서 날카로운 소리를 연거푸 냈는데, 멀리서 소총 일제 사격을 하는 소리 같았지. 그래도 아서는 랄프의 쪽지에 답장을 마저 다 써냈고, 마침내 그걸 그 별난 심부름꾼에게 건네며 전해 달라고 했어.\n\n\"이거요, 노그스 씨.\" 그라이드가 말했어.\n\n뉴먼은 고개를 끄덕이고, 그걸 모자 속에 넣고는, 발을 끌며 나가려 했어. 그런데 노망 든 기쁨에 어쩔 줄 모르던 그라이드가 그를 도로 불러서는, 얼굴 전체가 쪼글쪼글 일그러져 눈이 거의 안 보일 정도로 히죽거리며, 새된 목소리로 속삭였어.\n\n\"뭘—뭘 좀 한 방울 들지 않겠소—그저 맛만이라도?\"\n\n진짜 우정에서라면 (아서 그라이드한테 그런 게 있었다면 말이지) 뉴먼은 세상에서 가장 좋은 포도주라도 거품 한 방울 함께 마시지 않았을 거야. 하지만 그가 무슨 꿍꿍이인지 보려고, 또 할 수 있는 만큼 그를 골탕 먹이려고, 뉴먼은 그 제안을 냉큼 받아들였어.\n\n그래서 아서 그라이드는 다시 장롱으로 다가가, 키 큰 플랑드르식 술잔과 기묘한 병이 잔뜩 놓인 선반에서 — 어떤 병은 황새처럼 목이 길었고, 어떤 병은 네모반듯한 네덜란드식 몸통에 짧고 통통한 중풍 걸린 듯한 목을 하고 있었지 — 그럴듯해 보이는 먼지 낀 병 하나와, 신기할 만큼 자그마한 잔 둘을 꺼냈어.\n\n\"이건 한 번도 맛본 적 없을 거요.\" 아서가 말했어. \"이건 오 도르요—황금물이지. 난 그 이름 때문에 이걸 좋아하오. 멋진 이름이잖소. 황금의 물, 황금물! 오 이런, 이걸 마신다는 게 죄짓는 것 같구려!\"\n\n용기가 빠르게 사그라드는 모양이었어. 그가 병을 도로 제자리에 갖다 놓을 것처럼 마개를 만지작거리자, 뉴먼은 그 작은 잔 하나를 집어 들고는, 아직 한 잔도 못 받았다는 점잖은 채근으로 병에다 두세 번 쨍 부딪쳤지. 아서 그라이드는 깊은 한숨을 쉬며 천천히 잔을 채웠어—그래도 가장자리까지는 아니었지—그러고는 자기 잔도 채웠어.\n\n\"멈춰요, 멈춰. 아직 마시지 마시오.\" 그가 뉴먼의 손에 제 손을 얹으며 말했어. \"이건 이십 년 전에 받은 거요. 그리고 난 가끔, 아—주 드물게 한 모금 맛볼 때면, 미리 그걸 떠올리며 나 자신을 약 올리는 걸 좋아한다오. 우리 건배합시다. 건배합시다, 노그스 씨, 어떻소?\"\n\n\"아!\" 뉴먼이 제 작은 잔을 안달하며 쳐다봤어. \"빨리 합시다. 심부름꾼이 기다린다고.\"\n\n\"그럼, 내가 뭘 할지 말해 주리다.\" 아서가 킥킥거렸어. \"우리 건배합시다—헤, 헤, 헤!—우리 한 숙녀를 위해 건배합시다.\"\n\n\"숙녀분들 말이오?\" 뉴먼이 말했어.\n\n\"아니, 아니오, 노그스 씨.\" 그라이드가 그의 손을 멈추며 대답했어. \"한 숙녀요. 내가 한 숙녀라고 하니 이상하지요. 알아요, 알고말고. 자, 어린 매들린이오. 그게 건배사요, 노그스 씨. 어린 매들린!\"\n\n\"매들린!\" 뉴먼이 말하고는, 속으로 덧붙였어. '하느님 그녀를 도우소서!'\n\n뉴먼이 제 몫의 황금물을 어찌나 잽싸고 태평하게 비워 버렸던지, 영감은 큰 충격을 받았어. 그는 의자에 꼿꼿이 앉아, 그 광경에 숨이 멎기라도 한 듯 입을 떡 벌리고 그를 멍하니 바라봤지. 하지만 뉴먼은 눈 하나 깜짝 안 하고, 영감이야 천천히 제 잔을 홀짝이든 병에 도로 따라 넣든 내버려 둔 채 떠나 버렸어. 그러면서 복도에서 페그 슬라이더스큐를 스치고 지나가며, 사과 한마디 인사 한마디 없이 그 여자의 위엄을 단단히 짓밟아 놓고 말았지.\n\n그라이드 씨와 가정부는 둘만 남자마자, 곧장 방법과 수단을 따지는 위원회로 둔갑해, 어린 신부를 맞을 준비를 어떻게 할지 의논했어. 그런데 이들은 다른 몇몇 위원회들처럼, 논의가 지독히도 따분하고 장황했어. 그러니 이 이야기는 뉴먼 노그스의 발자취를 좇기로 하지. 그러면 필요한 일에 이로움까지 더해지는 셈이거든. 어차피 어떤 상황에서든 그렇게 해야 했을 테고, 세상이 다 알다시피 필요 앞엔 법도 없으니까.\n\n\"오래도 걸렸군.\" 뉴먼이 돌아오자 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"그쪽이 오래 걸렸지요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어.\n\n\"흥!\" 랄프가 안달하며 외쳤어. \"쪽지를 주거든 그 쪽지를 내놓고, 안 줬으면 전갈을 내놔. 그리고 가지 말게. 자네한테 할 말이 있으니, 이 사람아.\"\n\n뉴먼은 쪽지를 건네고, 주인이 봉인을 뜯어 쭉 훑어보는 동안 아주 점잖고 결백한 얼굴을 했어.\n\n\"틀림없이 올 거야.\" 랄프가 쪽지를 갈가리 찢으며 중얼거렸어. \"왜, 그야 당연히 그 자가 틀림없이 올 줄 알지. 그걸 뭐하러 적어? 노그스! 자, 이 사람아, 어젯밤 길에서 자네랑 같이 있던 그 사내는 누구였나?\"\n\n\"모릅니다.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어.\n\n\"기억을 좀 되살리는 게 좋을 거야, 이 사람아.\" 랄프가 위협하는 눈빛으로 말했어.\n\n\"말씀드리지만,\" 뉴먼이 대담하게 대꾸했어. \"전 모릅니다. 그 자는 여기 두 번 와서 사장님을 찾았죠. 사장님은 안 계셨고요. 그 자가 또 왔습니다. 사장님이 직접 쫓아 보냈고요. 브루커라고 이름을 댔죠.\"\n\n\"그건 나도 알아.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그래서?\"\n\n\"그래서요? 그야, 그 뒤로 그 자가 근처에서 어슬렁거리며 길에서 절 따라다닙니다. 밤마다 절 쫓아다니며, 사장님이랑 얼굴을 맞대게 해 달라고 졸라 대죠. 전에 한 번 그런 적이 있다나요, 그것도 얼마 안 됐다고. 사장님을 얼굴 맞대고 보고 싶다는 거예요. 그러면 사장님이 곧 자기 말을 끝까지 들으실 거라고 장담하더군요.\"\n\n\"그래서 자넨 뭐라고 했나?\" 랄프가 제 머슴을 날카롭게 쳐다보며 물었어.\n\n\"그건 제 알 바 아니니 안 한다고요. 그게 다라면 길에서 사장님을 붙들 수도 있을 거라고 했죠. 근데 안 된대요! 그건 안 된다는 거예요. 거기선 사장님이 한마디도 안 들으실 거라더군요. 문을 잠근 방에서 사장님과 단둘이 있어야 한다고요. 거기서라면 겁 없이 말할 수 있고, 그러면 사장님도 곧 태도가 달라져서 진득하게 자기 말을 들으실 거라나요.\"\n\n\"건방진 개 같은 놈!\" 랄프가 중얼거렸어.\n\n\"제가 아는 건 그게 다입니다.\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"다시 말씀드리지만, 전 그 자가 누군지 몰라요. 그 자도 자기가 누군지 모를 거라고 봅니다. 사장님은 그 자를 보셨으니, 어쩌면 사장님은 아실지도 모르죠.\"\n\n\"아는 것 같군.\" 랄프가 대답했어.\n\n\"흥.\" 뉴먼이 뚱하게 받아쳤어. \"저까지 그 자를 알기를 바라진 마세요. 그게 다예요. 다음엔 왜 이걸 진작 말 안 했냐고 물으시겠죠. 사람들이 사장님을 두고 떠드는 말을 제가 죄다 일러바치면 뭐라고 하실 건데요? 제가 가끔 그러면 절 뭐라고 부르세요? '짐승, 멍청이!' 하면서 용처럼 절 물어뜯잖아요.\"\n\n이건 꽤 사실이었어. 사실 뉴먼이 미리 짚은 그 질문이, 바로 그 순간 랄프의 입가에 막 떠올라 있었거든.\n\n\"그 자는 빈둥거리는 불한당이야.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"제 죄 때문에 바다 건너로 떠돌던 부랑자고. 제 목을 교수형 밧줄에 들이밀려고 풀려난 중죄인이지. 그리고 제 속을 빤히 아는 나한테 감히 수작을 부려 보려는 사기꾼이고. 그 자가 다음에 또 자넬 꼬드기거든, 거짓말과 협박으로 돈을 뜯어내려 했다고 경찰에 넘겨 버려—알겠나?—나머지는 나한테 맡기고. 잠시 감옥에서 발이나 식히게 해 주면, 나오고 나선 등쳐 먹을 다른 사람을 찾아 나설 게 틀림없어. 내 말 새겨듣는 건가, 응?\"\n\n\"듣고 있습니다.\" 뉴먼이 말했어.\n\n\"그럼 그렇게 해.\" 랄프가 대꾸했어. \"그럼 내 자네한테 상을 주지. 자, 이제 가도 좋아.\"\n\n뉴먼은 냉큼 그 허락을 받아들였어. 그러고는 자기 작은 사무실에 틀어박혀, 온종일 거기 앉아 아주 심각하게 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었지. 밤에 풀려나자, 그는 쓸 수 있는 모든 속도를 다해 도심으로 가서, 펌프 뒤 옛 자리를 잡고 니콜라스를 기다렸어. 뉴먼 노그스는 나름대로 자존심이 있어서, 자기가 떨어진 이 초라하고 비참한 꼴로 치어리블 형제 앞에서 니콜라스의 친구로 나서는 걸 차마 견딜 수 없었거든.\n\n이 자리를 잡은 지 몇 분 안 돼서, 그는 니콜라스가 다가오는 걸 보고 반가워하며, 매복 장소에서 뛰쳐나가 그를 맞이했어. 니콜라스도 한동안 못 본 친구를 만나게 되어 그에 못지않게 기뻤지. 그래서 둘의 인사는 따뜻했어.\n\n\"마침 그때 당신 생각을 하고 있었어요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"그것참 잘됐군.\" 뉴먼이 대꾸했어. \"나도 자네 생각을 했어. 오늘 밤엔 안 올 수가 없었네. 저기, 내가 뭔가 알아낼 것 같단 말이야.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔데요?\" 니콜라스가 이 묘한 말에 웃으며 되물었어.\n\n\"그게 뭘 수도 있는지, 뭐가 아닐 수도 있는지 나도 모르겠어.\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"자네 삼촌이 얽힌 무슨 비밀인데, 그게 뭔진 아직 못 알아냈어. 짚이는 데는 강하게 있지만 말이야. 지금은 안 떠벌릴 거야, 자네가 실망할까 봐.\"\n\n\"내가 실망한다고요!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어. \"나랑 관계가 있는 거예요?\"\n\n\"있는 것 같아.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어. \"그래야만 한다는 생각이 머릿속에 박혀 있거든. 분명 한꺼번에 다 털어놓고 싶어 하지 않는데, 그래도 더 많이 아는 사내를 하나 찾아냈어. 게다가 그 자가 벌써 나한테 어찌나 아리송한 말을 흘렸는지—그러니까, 날 어리둥절하게 만드는 말이지.\" 뉴먼은 빨간 코를 벅벅 긁어 시뻘겋게 부어오르게 하면서, 그러는 내내 있는 힘껏 니콜라스를 빤히 쳐다보며 말했어.\n\n대체 무엇이 친구를 이토록 수수께끼에 휩싸이게 만들었는지 궁금했던 니콜라스는, 잇따라 질문을 던지며 그 까닭을 밝혀 보려 했어. 하지만 헛수고였지. 뉴먼한테서는, 이미 던져 놓은 의문들을 되풀이하는 것과, 두서없는 일장 연설 말고는 더 분명한 말을 끌어낼 수 없었어. 그 연설인즉, 어떻게 최대한 조심해야 하는지, 어떻게 스라소니 눈을 한 랄프가 이미 그 정체 모를 상대와 함께 있는 자기를 봤는지, 또 어떻게 자기가 처음부터 그런 일에 대비해 두었던 덕에, 극도로 신중한 태도와 교묘한 말솜씨로 그 랄프를 따돌렸는지에 관한 거였어.\n\n뉴먼의 코가, 사실 봉화처럼 보는 사람 모두에게 끊임없이 경고를 보내는 그 버릇을 떠올린 니콜라스는, 그를 외진 선술집으로 데려갔어. 거기서 둘은 자기들 사귐의 시작과 그 흘러온 과정을 되짚어 봤지. 사람들이 가끔 그러듯이 말이야. 그러다 그 사귐에서 가장 또렷한 자취를 남긴 자잘한 일들을 더듬어 가던 끝에, 마침내 세실리아 봅스터 양 이야기까지 다다랐어.\n\n\"그러고 보니 생각났는데,\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"자넨 그 아가씨 진짜 이름을 한 번도 말 안 했어.\"\n\n\"매들린이에요!\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"매들린!\" 뉴먼이 외쳤어. \"무슨 매들린? 성을 말해. 성을 말해 보라고.\"\n\n\"브레이요.\" 니콜라스가 몹시 놀라며 말했어.\n\n\"바로 그 여자야!\" 뉴먼이 외쳤어. \"딱한 사연이지! 자넨 이 천륜에 어긋난 결혼이, 그 여자를 구하려는 시도 한 번 없이 벌어지는 걸 멀거니 보고만 있을 텐가?\"\n\n\"무슨 소리예요?\" 니콜라스가 벌떡 일어서며 소리쳤어. \"결혼이라뇨! 미친 거예요?\"\n\n\"자네가 미친 거 아닌가? 그 여자가? 자넨 눈멀고, 귀먹고, 정신 나가고, 죽었나?\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"하루 안에, 자네 삼촌 랄프의 손을 거쳐, 그 여자가 그자만큼이나 못된 놈한테, 아니 더 못된 놈이 있다면 그보다 더한 놈한테 시집간다는 걸 모르나? 하루 안에, 자네가 거기 살아 서 있는 게 확실한 것만큼이나 확실하게, 그 여자가 백발의 비열한 놈한테—태어날 때부터 마귀로 나서 마귀의 길에서 늙어 빠진 놈한테—제물로 바쳐진다는 걸 모르냐고?\"\n\n\"말조심해요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"제발 부탁이니 조심하라고요! 난 여기 혼자 남겨졌고, 그 여자를 구하러 손 내밀어 줄 사람들은 다 멀리 있어요. 대체 무슨 소리예요?\"\n\n\"난 그 여자 이름을 들은 적이 없었어.\" 뉴먼이 격해진 나머지 목이 메어 말했어. \"왜 나한테 말 안 했나? 내가 어떻게 알았겠어? 그랬으면 적어도 생각해 볼 시간이라도 좀 있었을 텐데!\"\n\n\"대체 무슨 소리냐고요?\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어.\n\n이 사정을 알아내기란 쉬운 일이 아니었어. 하지만 아무짝에도 도움 안 되는 야단스러운 손짓 발짓을 한바탕 거친 끝에, 뉴먼 노그스 못지않게 거의 제정신이 아니던 니콜라스가, 그를 의자에 억지로 눌러 앉히고 이야기를 시작할 때까지 꽉 붙들어 놨지.\n\n음모가 낱낱이 드러나자, 듣는 이의 가슴속으로 분노와 경악과 격분과 격정의 폭풍이 휘몰아쳤어. 그는 모든 걸 다 알아듣자마자, 잿빛으로 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 온 사지를 떨면서, 집 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔어.\n\n\"저 사람 잡아요!\" 뉴먼이 뒤쫓아 뛰쳐나가며 외쳤어. \"저 사람 무슨 끔찍한 짓을 저지를 거야. 누굴 죽일 거라고. 이봐요! 거기, 저 사람 좀 잡아요. 도둑이야! 도둑이야!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 52\n\n옛날 일을 다시 떠올려야겠다.\n\n뉴먼이 무슨 수를 써서라도 자기를 멈춰 세우려 한다는 걸 알아차린 니콜라스는, 혹시 \"도둑이야!\" 하는 소리에 마음씨 좋은 행인이 자기를 덥석 붙들어서 빠져나오기 힘든 곤란한 처지에 몰아넣지나 않을까 걱정이 되었다. 그래서 곧 걸음을 늦추고, 뉴먼 노그스가 따라붙도록 내버려 두었다. 따라붙은 뉴먼은 숨이 어찌나 가빴던지, 일 분만 더 갔으면 도저히 못 버텼을 것 같았다.\n\n\"브레이의 집으로 곧장 가겠어요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다. \"그 사람을 만나야겠어요. 그 가슴에 인정이 조금이라도 남아 있다면, 엄마도 친구도 없는 제 자식한테 마음 한 자락이라도 쓸 줄 안다면, 제가 그 마음을 일깨워 보겠어요.\"\n\n\"안 돼.\" 뉴먼이 대꾸했다. \"정말 안 돼.\"\n\n\"그럼,\" 니콜라스가 앞으로 밀고 나가며 말했다. \"처음 먹었던 마음대로 하겠어요. 곧장 랄프 니클비한테 가겠어요.\"\n\n\"네가 그 집에 닿을 때쯤이면 벌써 잠자리에 들어 있을 거다.\" 뉴먼이 말했다.\n\n\"잠자리에서 끌어내겠어요.\" 니콜라스가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"쯧, 쯧.\" 노그스가 말했다. \"정신 좀 차려라.\"\n\n\"당신은 제게 둘도 없는 친구예요, 뉴먼.\" 니콜라스가 잠시 말을 멈췄다가, 그의 손을 잡으며 다시 말했다. \"저는 많은 시련을 이겨 냈어요. 그렇지만 이번 일에는 다른 사람의 불행이, 그것도 이런 끔찍한 불행이 얽혀 있어요. 솔직히 말하면 저는 절망에 빠졌고, 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n사실 정말 가망 없는 일처럼 보였다. 뉴먼 노그스가 벽장 안에 숨어 있을 때 주워들은 그런 정보로는 아무것도 할 수가 없었다. 랄프 니클비와 그라이드가 짜고 든 거래가 있다는 사실만으로는 그 결혼을 무효로 만들 수도 없고, 브레이가 결혼을 마다하게 만들 수도 없었다. 브레이는 그런 밀약이 있다는 걸 정확히 알지는 못해도, 어렴풋이 눈치는 채고 있을 게 분명했다. 매들린을 속이려는 어떤 사기가 있다는 식의 암시가 있긴 했지만, 아서 그라이드가 워낙 흐릿하게 흘렸던 데다, 그걸 뉴먼 노그스가 전하면서 권총 연기 때문에 더 뿌예진 통에, 완전히 알아들을 수 없는 캄캄한 이야기가 되어 버렸다.\n\n\"한 줄기 희망도 안 보여요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다.\n\n\"그러니 더더욱 침착해야 한다. 이성을 잃지 말고, 잘 따져 보고, 곰곰이 생각해야지.\" 뉴먼이 한 단어 건너 한 번씩 말을 멈추며, 걱정스럽게 친구의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. \"그 형제분들은 어디 계시냐?\"\n\n\"두 분 다 급한 일로 떠나셨어요. 앞으로 일주일은 안 돌아오실 거예요.\"\n\n\"연락할 길이 없냐? 내일 밤까지 한 분이라도 여기로 모셔 올 방법이 없어?\"\n\n\"불가능해요!\" 니콜라스가 말했다. \"우리하고 그분들 사이에 바다가 가로놓여 있어요. 아무리 바람이 잘 불어 준다 해도, 갔다 오는 데만 사흘 낮밤이 걸려요.\"\n\n\"그분들 조카,\" 뉴먼이 말했다. \"아니면 그 늙은 서기.\"\n\n\"그 사람들이 한들, 제가 못 할 무슨 일을 할 수 있겠어요?\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했다. \"특히 그분들에 관해서는, 이 일을 철저히 입 다물라는 분부를 받았어요. 기적이 아니고서는 이 희생을 막을 길이 없는데, 저한테 맡겨진 비밀을 무슨 권리로 떠벌리겠어요?\"\n\n\"생각해 봐라.\" 뉴먼이 다그쳤다. \"정말 길이 없어?\"\n\n\"없어요.\" 니콜라스가 완전히 풀이 죽어 말했다. \"하나도요. 아버지는 몰아붙이고, 딸은 받아들였어요. 그 악마 같은 자들이 그 여자를 손아귀에 넣었어요. 법적 권리며, 힘이며, 권력이며, 돈이며, 온갖 영향력이 다 그들 편이에요. 제가 무슨 수로 그 여자를 구하기를 바라겠어요?\"\n\n\"끝까지 희망을 가져!\" 뉴먼이 그의 등을 탁 치며 말했다. \"언제나 희망을 가져, 착하지. 절대 희망을 놓지 마. 그래 봐야 소용없으니까. 내 말 알겠냐, 닉? 소용없는 짓이야. 손 안 댄 데가 없게 다 뒤집어 봐. 할 수 있는 만큼 다 했다는 걸 아는 것만으로도 늘 뭔가 남는 거야. 그렇지만 희망을 놓아 버리면, 무슨 일을 해도 다 헛수고다. 희망을 가져, 끝까지 희망을 가져!\"\n\n니콜라스에게는 격려가 필요했다. 두 고리대금업자의 계략이 너무도 갑자기 닥쳐온 것이며, 손쓸 시간이 얼마 남지 않은 것이며, 몇 시간만 지나면 매들린 브레이가 영영 손닿을 수 없는 곳으로 멀어져 말할 수 없는 불행에, 어쩌면 때 이른 죽음에까지 내던져지리라는 거의 확신에 가까운 짐작이며, 이 모든 것이 그를 얼떨떨하게 짓눌렀다. 자기도 모르게 품어 왔거나, 스스로 품게 내버려 두었던, 그 여자와 얽힌 모든 희망이 발치에 시들어 죽은 채 떨어지는 것 같았다. 그의 기억이나 상상이 그 여자를 둘러쌌던 온갖 매력이 하나하나 눈앞에 떠올라, 그저 그의 괴로움만 더하고 절망에 새로운 쓰라림을 보탤 뿐이었다. 그 여자의 외로운 처지에 대한 가엾은 마음과, 그 여자의 꿋꿋함과 의연함에 대한 감탄이, 온몸을 뒤흔드는 분노를 부추겨, 가슴이 터질 듯 부풀어 올랐다.\n\n그런데 니콜라스 자신의 마음이 그를 어쩔 줄 모르게 만든 그때, 뉴먼의 마음이 그를 구해 주었다. 늘 그렇듯 엉뚱하고 우스꽝스럽긴 했지만, 그의 충고에는 어찌나 진심이 담겨 있었고, 그 태도에는 어찌나 정성과 열의가 가득했던지, 그것이 니콜라스에게 새로운 굳건함을 불어넣어 주었다. 그래서 니콜라스는 잠시 말없이 걷다가 입을 열 수 있었다.\n\n\"당신이 제게 좋은 가르침을 주셨어요, 뉴먼. 그 가르침을 새기겠어요. 적어도 한 걸음은 내디딜 수 있어요. 아니, 꼭 내디뎌야만 하죠. 그 일에 내일부터 매달리겠어요.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔데?\" 노그스가 간절히 물었다. \"랄프를 협박하려는 건 아니지? 아버지를 만나려는 것도 아니고?\"\n\n\"그 딸을 만나려는 거예요, 뉴먼.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다. \"결국, 그 형제분들이 여기 계셨다면 하셨을 그 일을, 하늘이 그분들을 보내 주셨더라면 좋았을 텐데요, 제가 하려는 거예요! 그 끔찍한 결혼에 대해 그 여자와 이야기를 나누고, 그 여자가 지금 어쩌면 경솔하게, 깊이 생각해 보지도 않고 서둘러 뛰어드는 그 온갖 무서운 일들을 일러 주려는 거예요. 적어도 잠시만이라도 멈춰 보라고 사정해 보려고요. 그 여자한테는 자기를 위해 조언해 줄 사람이 하나도 없었을 거예요. 지금이 마지막 순간이고 그 여자가 파멸의 벼랑 끝에 서 있긴 하지만, 어쩌면 저라도 그 여자의 마음을 그 정도는 움직일 수 있을지 몰라요.\"\n\n\"훌륭한 말이다!\" 뉴먼이 말했다. \"잘했다, 잘했어! 그래. 아주 좋아.\"\n\n\"그리고 분명히 말하건대,\" 니콜라스가 진심에서 우러난 열의로 외쳤다. \"이번 일에서 저는 이기적이거나 사사로운 마음 때문에 움직이는 게 아니에요. 그 여자가 가엾고, 이 계략이 너무 끔찍하고 역겨워서 그러는 거예요. 그리고 만약 경쟁자가 스무 명이나 있고, 제가 그중 맨 꼴찌에 가장 인기 없는 사람이라 해도, 저는 똑같이 했을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그랬을 거라고 믿는다.\" 뉴먼이 말했다. \"그런데 지금 어디로 그렇게 서두르는 거냐?\"\n\n\"집으로요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다. \"같이 가시겠어요? 아니면 여기서 작별 인사를 드릴까요?\"\n\n\"네가 뛰지 말고 걸어 주기만 한다면, 조금 같이 가마.\" 노그스가 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 밤엔 걸을 수가 없어요, 뉴먼.\" 니콜라스가 다급하게 대답했다. \"빨리 움직여야지, 안 그러면 숨도 못 쉬겠어요. 제가 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 일을 했는지는 내일 알려 드릴게요.\"\n\n대답도 기다리지 않고 그는 빠른 걸음으로 내달려, 거리를 가득 메운 인파 속으로 뛰어들더니 이내 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n\"저 녀석은 가끔 너무 격해진단 말이야.\" 뉴먼이 그 뒷모습을 바라보며 말했다. \"그래도 난 그 점이 좋아. 지금이야 그럴 만도 하지, 안 그러면 별일이고. 희망이라! 내가 희망을 가지라고 했지! 랄프 니클비와 그라이드가 머리를 맞대고 있는데! 상대편한테 희망을 가지라니! 호! 호!\"\n\n뉴먼 노그스는 이 혼잣말을 아주 서글픈 웃음으로 맺었다. 그러고는 아주 서글프게 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, 몹시 침울한 얼굴로 돌아서서 터덜터덜 제 갈 길을 갔다.\n\n여느 때 같았으면 그는 작은 선술집이나 술집으로 발길을 옮겼을 것이다. 그게 여러모로 그의 버릇이었으니까. 그렇지만 뉴먼은 이 일에 너무 마음이 쓰이고 너무 걱정이 되어, 그런 데에 기댈 생각조차 못 한 채, 우울하고 침울한 생각에 잠겨 곧장 집으로 갔다.\n\n그날 오후, 모를리나 켄위그스 양에게 초대장이 한 장 와 있었다. 다음 날 웨스트민스터 다리에서 증기선을 타고 트위크넘에 있는 일파이 섬으로 놀러 가자는 것이었다. 거기서 찬 음식과 병맥주, 셔브, 새우를 곁들여 신나게 놀고, 그 자리를 위해 일부러 데려온 악단의 연주에 맞춰 야외에서 춤을 추기로 되어 있었다. 그 증기선은 인맥이 넓은 어느 무용 선생이 자기 수많은 제자들을 위해 따로 빌린 것이었다. 제자들은 옅은 파란색 표를 여러 장 사들이고 친구들한테도 사도록 권하는 것으로, 무용 선생의 노고에 대한 고마움을 나타냈다. 그 표가 있어야 이 나들이에 낄 수 있었던 것이다. 야심만만한 어느 이웃이 이 옅은 파란색 표 가운데 한 장을 모를리나 켄위그스 양에게 건네며, 자기 딸들과 함께 가자고 청했다. 켄위그스 부인은 이렇게 촉박한 통고에도 모를리나 양이 가장 화려한 차림으로 나서야 가문의 명예가 선다고 마땅히 여겼다. 또 그렇게 해서 그 무용 선생 말고도 다른 무용 선생이 있다는 걸 그에게 보여 주고, 그 자리에 모인 모든 아버지와 어머니들에게 자기네 자식 말고도 남의 자식 역시 점잖아질 수 있다는 걸 증명해 보이고 싶었다. 그래서 준비할 일이 어찌나 어마어마한지 두 번이나 까무러쳤지만, 가문의 이름을 지키지 못하면 차라리 죽고 말겠다는 각오로 버티며, 뉴먼 노그스가 집에 돌아왔을 때에도 여전히 한창 일에 매달려 있었다.\n\n그런데 이 일에 따라붙는 주름 장식 다리미질이며, 바지 자락 손질이며, 원피스 단장이며, 까무러쳤다 다시 깨어났다 하는 통에, 켄위그스 부인은 완전히 정신이 팔려 있었다. 그래서 불과 삼십 분 전에서야, 모를리나 양의 아마빛 머리 갈래가 말하자면 제멋대로 자라 부스스해졌다는 걸 알아차렸다. 솜씨 좋은 미용사의 손을 빌리지 않고서는, 모를리나가 다른 집 딸들을 보란 듯이 압도하는 그 눈부신 승리를 결코 거둘 수 없을 터였다. 그만 못한 것은 곧 패배나 마찬가지였다. 이 발견에 켄위그스 부인은 절망에 빠졌다. 미용실이 세 골목 너머, 위험한 건널목을 여덟 개나 지나야 하는 곳에 있었기 때문이다. 설령 그게 격식에 어긋나지 않는 일이라 쳐도 — 켄위그스 부인은 그 점도 미심쩍게 여겼다 — 모를리나 혼자 거기까지 가게 둘 수는 없었다. 켄위그스 씨는 아직 일에서 돌아오지 않았고, 모를리나를 데려갈 사람이 아무도 없었다. 그래서 켄위그스 부인은 우선 자기를 속상하게 만든 죄로 켄위그스 양을 찰싹 때렸다가, 이내 눈물을 흘렸다.\n\n\"이 은혜도 모르는 것아!\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했다. \"내가 오늘 밤 너 잘되라고 그 고생을 다 했는데.\"\n\n\"제 잘못이 아니잖아요, 엄마.\" 모를리나도 울면서 대꾸했다. \"머리가 자라는 걸 어떡해요.\"\n\n\"입 다물어, 이 못된 것아!\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했다. \"그만해! 너를 혼자 보내서 차에 안 치이고 무사히 간다 쳐도, 넌 분명 로라 촙킨스네로 쪼르르 달려가서,\" 로라는 그 야심만만한 이웃의 딸이었다. \"내일 무슨 옷을 입을 건지 죄다 일러바칠 거다, 분명히 그럴 거야. 너는 제 앞가림이라곤 모르고, 잠깐도 눈 밖에 두면 못 믿을 애야.\"\n\n이렇게 큰딸의 못된 심보를 한탄하면서, 켄위그스 부인은 눈에서 새로운 속상함의 눈물방울을 짜냈다. 그러고는 세상에 자기만큼 시달리며 사는 사람은 없을 거라고 단언했다. 그러자 모를리나 켄위그스도 다시 울음을 터뜨려, 둘이 함께 신세 한탄을 했다.\n\n일이 이 지경에 이르렀을 때, 뉴먼 노그스가 위층으로 올라가느라 절뚝거리며 문 앞을 지나가는 소리가 들렸다. 켄위그스 부인은 그 발소리에 새로운 희망을 얻어, 이렇게 짧은 사이에 지울 수 있는 만큼 방금 흘린 감정의 흔적을 얼른 얼굴에서 닦아 냈다. 그러고는 그의 앞에 나서서 자기네 딱한 사정을 늘어놓으며, 모를리나를 미용실까지 바래다 달라고 사정했다.\n\n\"노그스 씨가 얼마나 착하고 마음씨 따뜻한 분인지 몰랐다면, 이런 부탁 안 드렸을 거예요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했다. \"정말이지, 무슨 일이 있어도요. 저는 몸이 약하답니다, 노그스 씨. 그렇지만 제 자존심은, 거절당할 것 같은 데에 가서 부탁하는 것을 도저히 못 견뎌요. 제 아이들이 시샘과 천박함에 짓밟히고 깔리는 꼴을 보고만 있지 못하는 것처럼요!\"\n\n뉴먼은 켄위그스 부인이 이렇게 믿음을 드러내지 않았더라도 두말없이 응했을 만큼 마음씨가 고운 사람이었다. 그리하여 몇 분도 채 지나지 않아, 그와 모를리나 양은 미용실로 가는 길에 올라 있었다.\n\n엄밀히 말하면 그곳은 미용실이라기보다는, 거칠고 천박한 사람들이라면 이발소라고 불렀을 만한 곳이었다. 거기서는 숙녀들의 머리를 우아하게 자르고 곱슬곱슬 말아 주고, 아이들 머리를 정성껏 손질해 주었을 뿐 아니라, 신사들의 수염도 손쉽게 깎아 주었기 때문이다. 그래도 그곳은 무척 점잖은 가게였다. 사실 일류라 할 만했다. 진열창에는 다른 우아한 물건들과 함께, 살결 흰 여인과 거무스름한 신사의 밀랍 흉상이 놓여 있었는데, 온 동네의 감탄을 자아내는 것이었다. 정말이지 어떤 부인들은, 그 거무스름한 신사가 다름 아닌 그 패기 넘치는 젊은 주인의 초상이라고까지 우겨 댔다. 둘의 머리 모양이 어찌나 닮았는지 — 둘 다 반들반들한 머리카락을 한가운데로 곧게 가르마를 타고, 양옆으로는 납작한 동그란 곱슬머리를 잔뜩 늘어뜨리고 있었다 — 그런 생각을 부추겼다. 하지만 좀 더 사정에 밝은 여인들은 이 주장을 대수롭잖게 여겼다. 그들은 주인의 잘생긴 얼굴과 풍채를 흔쾌히 인정하긴 했지만 — 사실 아주 기꺼이 인정했다 — 진열창 속 거무스름한 신사의 얼굴만큼은 남성미의 더없이 빼어나고 추상적인 표상이라고 보았기 때문이다. 그런 얼굴은 어쩌면 천사들이나 군인들 가운데서는 이따금 실현될지 몰라도, 인간의 눈을 즐겁게 해 주려고 실물로 나타나는 일은 아주 드문 것이었다.\n\n뉴먼 노그스는 켄위그스 양을 이 가게까지 무사히 데려다주었다. 주인은 켄위그스 양에게 누이가 셋이나 있고, 저마다 아마빛 머리 갈래가 둘씩 있어 적어도 한 달에 한 번은 머리당 육 펜스씩 벌게 해 준다는 걸 알고 있었다. 그래서 막 비누 거품을 발라 면도를 시작하려던 노신사를 얼른 내팽개치고, 그 손님을 직공에게 넘겨 버린 채 — 그 직공은 뚱뚱하고 나이가 지긋한 탓에 숙녀들 사이에서 별로 인기가 없었다 — 몸소 그 아가씨를 맡았다.\n\n이렇게 손님이 바뀌자마자, 입에 파이프를 문 덩치 크고 우락부락하면서도 사람 좋아 보이는 석탄 일꾼 하나가 면도를 하러 나타났다. 그는 턱을 손으로 쓱 문지르며, 면도해 줄 사람이 언제 손이 비는지 알고 싶다고 했다.\n\n이 질문을 받은 직공은 미심쩍은 눈으로 젊은 주인을 쳐다보았고, 젊은 주인은 그 석탄 일꾼을 경멸하듯 바라보며 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"여기선 면도 못 해 드립니다, 손님.\"\n\n\"왜요?\" 석탄 일꾼이 말했다.\n\n\"우린 당신 같은 일 하는 분들 면도는 안 합니다.\" 젊은 주인이 한마디 했다.\n\n\"아니, 지난주에 창문으로 들여다봤더니 빵집 사람을 면도해 주던데요.\" 석탄 일꾼이 말했다.\n\n\"어딘가에 선은 그어야 하지 않겠습니까, 이 양반아.\" 주인이 대꾸했다. \"우린 거기에 선을 긋습니다. 빵집 사람보다 아래로는 안 내려가요. 빵집 사람보다 더 낮은 데까지 손대기 시작하면, 손님들이 다 떨어져 나가서 가게 문을 닫아야 할 겁니다. 다른 가게를 찾아보셔야겠어요, 손님. 여기선 안 됩니다.\"\n\n손님은 멀거니 쳐다보더니, 무척 재미있어하는 표정인 뉴먼 노그스를 보고 씩 웃었다. 그러고는 머릿기름 단지며 다른 물건들을 깔보는 듯 가게 안을 슬쩍 둘러보고는, 입에서 파이프를 빼 아주 큰 소리로 휘파람을 한 번 불더니, 다시 파이프를 물고 걸어 나갔다.\n\n방금 비누 거품을 바른 채, 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌리고 침울하게 앉아 있던 노신사는 이 소동을 전혀 알아차리지 못한 듯, 깊은 상념에 빠져 주위의 모든 것에 무감각한 모양이었다. 이따금 내쉬는 한숨으로 미루어, 무척이나 서글픈 상념에 잠겨 있는 것 같았다. 그 본보기에 영향을 받았는지, 주인은 켄위그스 양의 머리를 자르고, 직공은 노신사의 수염을 밀고, 뉴먼 노그스는 지난 일요일 신문을 읽으며, 셋 다 말없이 있었다. 그때 켄위그스 양이 가느다란 비명을 짧게 질렀다. 뉴먼이 눈을 들어 보니, 노신사가 고개를 돌리는 바람에 수금원 릴리비크 씨의 얼굴이 드러났던 것이다.\n\n분명 릴리비크 씨의 얼굴이긴 했지만, 묘하게 달라져 있었다. 언제나 깨끗하고 말끔하게 면도를 하고 사람들 앞에 나서기로 작정한 노신사가 있다면, 그게 바로 릴리비크 씨였다. 수금원답게 처신하며, 온 세상이 자기 장부에 올라 있고 그게 전부 두 분기치나 밀려 있기라도 한 듯, 모든 사람 앞에서 엄숙하고 위풍당당한 위엄을 떨치는 수금원이 있다면, 그게 바로 릴리비크 씨였다. 그런데 지금 그는 적어도 일주일은 묵은 수염 자국이 턱에 거치적거리고, 가슴 위에는 당당하게 빳빳이 선 게 아니라 더럽고 구겨진 셔츠 주름 장식이 말하자면 잔뜩 움츠러든 채, 너무도 풀이 죽고 처지고 낙담한, 그래서 굴욕과 슬픔과 부끄러움이 묻어나는 태도로 앉아 있었다. 요금을 안 내 수돗물이 끊긴 보잘것없는 셋방살이꾼 마흔 명의 넋을 한 몸뚱이에 죄다 모아 놓는다 해도, 지금 수금원 릴리비크 씨의 모습에 어린 그런 굴욕과 패배감만큼은 도저히 드러내지 못했을 것이다.\n\n뉴먼 노그스가 그의 이름을 부르자, 릴리비크 씨는 끙 신음을 했다. 그러고는 그걸 감추려고 기침을 했다. 하지만 신음은 제대로 된 신음이었고, 기침은 쌕쌕거리는 소리에 지나지 않았다.\n\n\"무슨 일이라도 있으세요?\" 뉴먼 노그스가 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이냐고요!\" 릴리비크 씨가 외쳤다. \"삶의 마개가 다 말라 버리고, 진흙만 남았소이다.\"\n\n이 말은 — 뉴먼이 보기에 릴리비크 씨가 요즘 연극쟁이들과 어울린 탓에 생긴 말투였다 — 도무지 무슨 뜻인지 알 수가 없었다. 뉴먼이 또 한 가지 물어보려는 듯한 표정을 짓자, 릴리비크 씨가 슬프게 그의 손을 흔들어 잡았다가 제 손을 휘저으며 그를 막았다.\n\n\"면도나 합시다!\" 릴리비크 씨가 말했다. \"모를리나가 끝나기 전에 마쳐야지. 모를리나 맞지요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 뉴먼이 말했다.\n\n\"켄위그스네는 사내아이를 봤지요, 안 그래요?\" 수금원이 물었다.\n\n뉴먼이 또 \"네.\" 하고 말했다.\n\n\"잘생긴 아이요?\" 수금원이 물었다.\n\n\"썩 못난 애는 아니에요.\" 뉴먼이 그 질문에 다소 거북해하며 대답했다.\n\n\"수전 켄위그스가 늘 그랬소.\" 수금원이 말했다. \"또 사내아이를 낳으면 나를 닮았으면 좋겠다고요. 이 아이가 나를 닮았소, 노그스 씨?\"\n\n이건 난처한 질문이었다. 하지만 뉴먼은, 그 아기가 어쩌면 시간이 지나면 그를 닮게 될지도 모르겠다고 릴리비크 씨에게 대답하며 슬쩍 피해 갔다.\n\n\"어떻게든 나를 닮은 사람이 하나 있으면 좋겠소.\" 릴리비크 씨가 말했다. \"내가 죽기 전에 말이오.\"\n\n\"아직 그러실 생각은 없으시잖아요?\" 뉴먼이 말했다.\n\n이에 릴리비크 씨는 엄숙한 목소리로 \"면도나 합시다!\" 하고 대답하고는, 다시 직공의 손에 몸을 맡긴 채 더는 말이 없었다.\n\n이건 별난 행동이었다. 모를리나 양이 보기에 어찌나 별나던지, 그 아가씨는 자칫 귀가 잘려 나갈지도 모르는 위험을 무릅쓰고, 방금 그 대화가 오가는 동안 스무 번도 넘게 고개를 돌려 돌아보지 않을 수 없었다. 그렇지만 릴리비크 씨는 그 애를 아랑곳하지 않았다. 오히려 (적어도 뉴먼 노그스가 보기에는) 그 애의 눈길을 피하려 애쓰며, 그 애가 자기를 쳐다볼 때마다 자기 속으로 움츠러들려는 듯했다. 뉴먼은 무엇이 수금원의 이 달라진 행동을 불러왔는지 몹시 궁금했다. 하지만 머잖아 알게 될 테고, 얼마든지 기다릴 수 있다고 차분히 생각하며, 그 노신사의 별난 처신에 별로 마음을 쓰지 않았다.\n\n마침내 머리 손질과 곱슬 말기가 끝나자, 한참을 기다리고 있던 노신사가 가려고 일어섰다. 그러고는 뉴먼, 그리고 뉴먼이 맡은 아이와 함께 걸어 나오며 뉴먼의 팔을 잡았다. 한동안 그는 아무 말도 없이 걸었다. 말수 적기로는 어디 가서 빠지지 않는 뉴먼도 굳이 침묵을 깨려 하지 않았다. 그렇게 둘은 걸어가다가 모를리나 양의 집에 거의 다 이르렀을 때, 릴리비크 씨가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"켄위그스네는 그 소식에 무척 충격을 받았소, 노그스 씨?\"\n\n\"무슨 소식 말씀이세요?\" 뉴먼이 되물었다.\n\n\"그게, 내가, 그러니까……\"\n\n\"결혼하셨다는 거요?\" 뉴먼이 말을 거들었다.\n\n\"아!\" 릴리비크 씨가 또 한 번 신음하며 대답했다. 이번에는 쌕쌕 기침으로 감추지도 않았다.\n\n\"엄마는 그 소식을 듣고 울었어요.\" 모를리나 양이 끼어들었다. \"그래도 우리가 엄마한테 한참 동안 숨겼어요. 아빠는 풀이 많이 죽었지만 지금은 나아지셨고요. 저도 많이 아팠는데, 저도 이제 나았어요.\"\n\n\"내가 청하면 이 작은할아버지 릴리비크한테 입맞춰 주겠니, 모를리나?\" 수금원이 조금 머뭇거리며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 릴리비크 할아버지, 해 드릴게요.\" 모를리나 양이 부모 둘의 기운을 합쳐 놓은 듯한 기세로 대답했다. \"그렇지만 릴리비크 할머니한테는 안 해요. 그 사람은 제 할머니가 아니에요. 절대 할머니라고 안 부를 거예요.\"\n\n이 말이 떨어지기가 무섭게, 릴리비크 씨는 모를리나 양을 두 팔로 번쩍 안아 올려 입을 맞추었다. 그러고는 이미 켄위그스 씨가 세 들어 사는 집 문 앞에 와 있었던지라 (앞서 말했듯이 그 문은 늘 활짝 열려 있었다), 곧장 켄위그스 씨의 거실로 걸어 올라가 모를리나 양을 한가운데에 내려놓았다. 켄위그스 부부는 저녁을 먹고 있었다. 배신한 친척의 모습을 보자, 켄위그스 부인은 정신이 아득해지며 핏기가 가셨고, 켄위그스 씨는 위풍당당하게 일어섰다.\n\n\"켄위그스,\" 수금원이 말했다. \"악수합시다.\"\n\n\"이보세요.\" 켄위그스 씨가 말했다. \"한때는 지금 나를 빤히 쳐다보는 저 사람 같은 분과 악수하는 게 자랑스럽던 시절이 있었지요. 한때는요.\" 켄위그스 씨가 말했다. \"저 사람이 찾아오면 나와 우리 식구 가슴에 자연스럽고도 가슴 벅찬 감정이 일던 시절이 있었습니다. 그런데 지금은, 저 사람을 무엇으로도 다 못 담을 감정으로 바라보면서 자문해 봅니다. 저 사람의 명예는 어디로 갔으며, 그 정직함은 어디로 갔으며, 그 인간된 도리는 어디로 갔느냐고요.\"\n\n\"수전 켄위그스.\" 릴리비크 씨가 조카에게 겸손하게 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"너는 나한테 아무 말도 안 하니?\"\n\n\"이 사람은 그럴 기운도 없습니다, 이보세요.\" 켄위그스 씨가 탁자를 힘주어 내리치며 말했다. \"튼튼한 갓난아기 젖 먹이랴, 당신의 그 매정한 처사를 곱씹으랴, 하루에 보리술 넉 파인트를 마셔도 몸을 가누기 힘들 지경이라고요.\"\n\n\"갓난아기가 튼튼하다니 기쁘구나.\" 가엾은 수금원이 풀죽은 목소리로 말했다. \"그것 참 다행이야.\"\n\n이건 켄위그스 부부의 가장 여린 곳을 건드린 셈이었다. 켄위그스 부인은 당장 울음을 터뜨렸고, 켄위그스 씨도 크게 마음이 동했다.\n\n\"그 아이를 기다리던 내내 내가 가장 기뻤던 건요.\" 켄위그스 씨가 서글프게 말했다. \"이런 생각을 한 거였지요. '사내아이라면, 부디 사내아이이길 바라지만, 그 애 외삼촌 릴리비크가 다음번엔 사내를 봤으면 좋겠다고 입버릇처럼 말씀하셨으니까, 사내아이라면 그 애 외삼촌 릴리비크가 뭐라고 하실까? 어떤 이름으로 불러 주길 바라실까? 피터일까, 알렉산더일까, 폼페이일까, 디오게네스일까, 아니면 뭐가 될까?' 그런데 지금 그 애를 들여다보면요. 아무것도 모르고 어쩔 줄 모르는 귀여운 갓난아기, 작은 팔로 할 줄 아는 거라곤 제 작은 모자를 잡아당기는 것뿐이고, 작은 다리로 할 줄 아는 거라곤 제 몸뚱이를 버둥거리는 것뿐인 아기를요. 그 애가 어미 무릎에 누워 옹알옹알 옹알이를 하며, 천진하게도 제 작은 주먹으로 제 목을 막다시피 하는 걸 보면요. 그 애가 그렇게 어린 갓난아기인 걸 보고, 한때 그 애를 그토록 예뻐하려던 외삼촌 릴리비크가 발길을 끊어 버렸다고 생각하면, 무슨 말로도 그려 낼 수 없는 복수심이 솟구쳐서, 저 거룩한 갓난아기조차 나더러 그를 미워하라고 일러 주는 것만 같단 말입니다.\"\n\n이 가슴 뭉클한 그림에 켄위그스 부인은 깊이 동했다. 솟구쳐 올라오려고 안간힘을 쓰다가 거센 눈물의 물살에 휩쓸려 떠내려가 버린 몇 마디 어설픈 말을 늘어놓은 끝에, 그녀가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"외삼촌.\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했다. \"외삼촌이 저와 제 사랑하는 아이들한테, 그리고 그 아이들을 있게 한 켄위그스한테 등을 돌리시다니요. 한때는 그토록 다정하고 정 깊으셨던 외삼촌이, 누가 외삼촌에 대해 그런 말을 했더라면 우리가 번개처럼 비웃으며 콧방귀를 뀌었을 그런 외삼촌이! 우리 첫째이자 맏아들 어린 릴리비크의 이름을 바로 그 제단 앞에서 외삼촌을 따 지었는데! 아이고, 세상에!\"\n\n\"우리가 돈을 바랐답니까?\" 켄위그스 씨가 말했다. \"우리가 재산을 한 번이라도 마음에 둔 적 있냐고요?\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 외쳤다. \"나는 그딴 거 우습게 봐요.\"\n\n\"나도 그래요.\" 켄위그스 씨가 말했다. \"늘 그랬고요.\"\n\n\"내 마음은 갈가리 찢겼어요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했다. \"괴로움에 내 가슴이 찢어졌고, 산후조리도 도로 망쳤고, 아무 죄 없는 갓난아기까지 보채고 칭얼대게 되었고, 모를리나는 비쩍 말라 버렸어요. 이 모든 걸 저는 잊고 용서하겠어요. 그리고 외삼촌하고는 절대 다투지 않을 거예요. 그렇지만 그 여자를 받아들이라는 말씀만은 하지 마세요, 절대로요, 외삼촌. 저는 안 할 거예요, 안 해요, 안 할 거예요, 안 할 거예요, 안 할 거라고요!\"\n\n\"수전, 여보.\" 켄위그스 씨가 말했다. \"당신 아이를 생각해요.\"\n\n\"그래요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 비명을 질렀다. \"내 아이를 생각하겠어요! 내 아이를 생각할 거예요! 어떤 외삼촌도 나한테서 빼앗지 못할 내 아이, 미움받고 멸시받고 버림받고 내쳐진 내 어린 아이를요.\" 여기서 켄위그스 부인의 감정이 어찌나 격해졌던지, 켄위그스 씨는 별수 없이 안으로는 사슴뿔 정기를 먹이고 밖으로는 식초를 발라 주었으며, 코르셋 끈 하나와 속치마 끈 넷, 자잘한 단추 여러 개를 못 쓰게 만들었다.\n\n뉴먼은 이 광경을 말없이 지켜보고 있었다. 릴리비크 씨가 그에게 나가지 말라고 신호했고, 켄위그스 씨도 고개를 끄덕여 함께 있어 달라고 청했기 때문이다. 켄위그스 부인이 어느 정도 정신을 차리고, 그녀에게 다소 영향력이 있는 사람으로서 뉴먼이 타이르며 진정하시라고 사정하자, 릴리비크 씨가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"나는 여기 누구한테도 내 — 굳이 그 말을 입에 올릴 것 없지. 무슨 말인지 알 테니까 — 받아들이라고 하지 않겠다. 켄위그스, 그리고 수전, 어제로 꼭 일주일 전에 그 여자가 반쯤 봉급 받는 퇴역 대위와 달아났단다!\"\n\n켄위그스 부부가 동시에 움찔했다.\n\n\"퇴역 대위와 달아났어.\" 릴리비크 씨가 되풀이했다. \"비열하고 거짓되게 퇴역 대위와 달아나 버렸지. 누구라도 안심하고 봤을 만한 술병코 대위와 말이야. 내가 헨리에타 페토커를 처음 본 게 바로 이 방이었지.\" 릴리비크 씨가 엄한 눈으로 둘러보며 말했다. \"그리고 그 여자를 영영 내치는 것도 바로 이 방이야.\"\n\n이 선언이 사태를 완전히 뒤바꿔 놓았다. 켄위그스 부인은 노신사의 목을 끌어안고, 조금 전의 매정함을 가슴 아프게 자책하며, 자기가 괴로웠다면 외삼촌의 괴로움은 오죽했겠느냐고 외쳤다! 켄위그스 씨는 그의 손을 꽉 잡고 영원한 우정과 뉘우침을 맹세했다. 켄위그스 부인은 자기가 헨리에타 페토커 같은 뱀이며 살무사며 독사며 구렁이며 비열한 악어를 가슴에 품고 키웠다는 생각에 소름이 끼쳤다. 켄위그스 씨는, 그 여자가 켄위그스 부인의 미덕을 그렇게 오래 곁에서 보고도 나아지지 않았다면 정말이지 못된 사람이었던 게 틀림없다고 따졌다. 켄위그스 부인은, 켄위그스 씨가 페토커 양의 행실이 영 미덥지 않다고 자주 말했던 것을 떠올리며, 자기가 어쩌다 그런 못된 것한테 눈이 멀었었는지 의아해했다. 켄위그스 씨는 자기도 의심을 품고 있었노라고 떠올렸지만, 켄위그스 부인이 의심하지 않았던 건 조금도 이상할 게 없다고 했다. 그녀는 더없이 정숙하고 순결하고 진실한 사람인 반면, 헨리에타는 더없이 비열하고 거짓되고 음흉한 사람이었으니 말이다. 그러고는 켄위그스 부부가 둘 다 북받치는 마음과 동정의 눈물로, 모든 일에는 다 그럴 만한 까닭이 있는 법이라고 하며, 부디 부질없는 슬픔에 빠지지 말고 언제나 두 팔과 가슴을 활짝 열고 있는 정 깊은 친척들과 어울리며 위안을 찾으시라고 착한 수금원에게 간청했다.\n\n\"수전과 켄위그스, 자네들을 아끼고 위하는 마음에서,\" 릴리비크 씨가 말했다. \"그 여자에 대한 복수나 앙심에서가 아니라네. 그 여자는 그럴 가치도 없으니까. 나는 내일 아침에 자네 아이들 앞으로 재산을 떼어 주겠네. 그리고 그 아이들이 성년이 되거나 결혼할 때 살아남은 아이들한테 지급되도록 하겠어. 한때 유언장에 남겨 주려던 그 돈을 말일세. 증서는 내일 작성할 거고, 노그스 씨가 증인 가운데 한 사람이 되어 줄 거야. 노그스 씨도 내가 이렇게 약속하는 걸 들었으니, 그 일이 이루어지는 것도 지켜봐 줄 거라네.\"\n\n이 고귀하고 너그러운 제안에 압도되어, 켄위그스 씨와 켄위그스 부인, 그리고 모를리나 켄위그스 양이 모두 함께 흐느끼기 시작했다. 그 흐느끼는 소리가 옆방까지 전해져, 거기 잠자리에 든 아이들마저 따라 울게 만들었다. 그러자 켄위그스 씨가 미친 듯이 뛰어 들어가, 아이들을 둘씩 둘씩 안아 데리고 나와, 잠자리 모자와 잠옷 바람으로 릴리비크 씨 발치에 우르르 내려놓고는, 외삼촌에게 고마워하고 복을 빌라고 일렀다.\n\n\"자, 이제.\" 가슴 미어지는 장면이 한바탕 지나가고 아이들이 다시 치워지자, 릴리비크 씨가 말했다. \"저녁이나 좀 주게. 이 일은 시내에서 이십 마일 떨어진 데서 벌어졌네. 나는 오늘 아침에 올라와서, 자네들을 보러 올 마음을 못 먹고 하루 종일 어슬렁거렸지. 나는 그 여자가 하자는 대로 다 해 줬고, 그 여자는 제멋대로였고, 하고 싶은 대로 다 했는데, 이제 와서 이런 짓을 한 거야. 찻숟가락 열두 개에 금화로 이십사 파운드가 있었는데, 그게 없어진 걸 제일 먼저 알았지. 정말 시련이야. 이제 순회를 돌 때 다시는 문을 두 번 두드릴 기운도 못 낼 것 같아. 제발 그 얘긴 더 하지 말아 주게. 그 숟가락들이 값이 나가는 거였는데, 아니, 됐네, 됐어!\"\n\n이렇게 중얼중얼 속을 털어놓으며 노신사는 몇 방울 눈물을 흘렸다. 하지만 식구들이 그를 안락의자에 앉히고, 그리 조르지 않아도 저녁을 든든히 들도록 했다. 그리고 그가 첫 파이프를 다 피우고, 켄위그스 씨가 그의 귀가를 축하하며 가족의 품으로 돌아온 것을 기려 시킨 큰 펀치 사발에서 여섯 잔쯤 비웠을 무렵에는, 여전히 무척 풀이 죽긴 했어도 제 운명에 꽤 체념한 듯 보였고, 아내가 달아난 것이 오히려 다행스럽다는 기색마저 들었다.\n\n\"저 사람을 보면 말이야.\" 켄위그스 씨가 한 손으로 켄위그스 부인의 허리를 감고, 다른 손으로는 파이프를 받쳐 들고 — 그는 담배를 안 피우는 사람이라 그 탓에 자꾸 눈을 깜빡이고 기침을 해 댔다 — 외삼촌 무릎에 앉은 모를리나에게 눈길을 두며 말했다. \"저 사람이 다시 한번 자기가 빛내던 그 자리에 어울려 들고, 그 정이 떳떳한 자리에서 펼쳐지는 걸 보면, 나는 저 사람의 됨됨이가 그렇게 고매하고 너른 만큼, 공인으로서 사회에 선 그 위치도 흠 하나 없다는 걸 느낍니다. 그리고 앞날을 보장받은 내 어린 자식들의 목소리가 나한테 가만히 속삭이는 것만 같아요. '이건 하늘 자체도 굽어보는 경사로구나!'\"\n\n# CHAPTER 53\n\n랄프 니클비 씨와 아서 그라이드 씨가 꾸민 음모가 어떻게 더 진행되는지를 담은 이야기\n\n극단적인 상황은 흔히 어떤 굳은 결심과 변치 않는 의지를 사람에게 불어넣곤 해. 매들린 브레이를 사모하는 청년보다 훨씬 덜 흥분하고 느긋한 기질을 가진 사람한테도 그런 힘이 작용하지. 니콜라스도 그랬어. 전날 밤 한숨도 자지 못한 채 뒤척이던 잠자리에서 동이 틀 무렵 벌떡 일어났어. 그러고는 마지막 호소를 하러 갈 채비를 했지. 매들린이 빠져나갈 단 하나 남은 희망이, 그 가늘고 약한 실 한 가닥에 매달려 있었거든.\n\n쉴 새 없이 움직이고 열정이 가득한 사람한테는 아침이 힘을 쏟고 활동하기에 알맞은 때일지도 몰라. 하지만 희망이 가장 크고 마음이 가장 들뜨고 가벼운 때가 늘 아침은 아니야. 힘들고 막막한 처지에 놓이면, 젊음과 습관, 우리를 둘러싼 어려움을 차분히 들여다보는 일, 그리고 그 어려움에 익숙해지는 일이 우리도 모르는 사이에 두려움을 조금씩 누그러뜨리지. 그러고는 어떤 막연하고 무모한 안도감까지는 아니어도, 그에 가까운 무심함을 만들어 내. 그 구원이 어떤 방법으로 어떤 모습으로 올지는 굳이 내다보려 하지도 않으면서 말이야. 그런데 아침에 새삼스레 그런 일들과 다시 마주하면, 우리와 어제 사이에 깜깜하고 고요한 틈이 벌어져 있는 거야. 희망이라는 약한 사슬의 고리 하나하나를 다시 단단히 박아야 하고, 뜨거운 열의는 가라앉아 그 자리에 차갑고 침착한 이성이 들어서지. 그러면 의심과 불안이 되살아나. 나그네가 낮에 가장 멀리 보고, 다정한 어둠이 눈과 마음에서 함께 가려 주었던 험한 산과 길 없는 들판을 비로소 알아차리는 것처럼, 고단한 인생길을 걷는 사람도 해가 다시 떠오를 때마다 넘어야 할 새 장애물, 올라야 할 새 봉우리를 보게 되는 거야. 어젯밤에는 거의 셈에 넣지도 않았던 먼 거리가 그 앞에 쭉 펼쳐지고, 온 자연을 환한 빛으로 물들이는 햇살마저 그저 그와 무덤 사이에 흩어져 있는 그 지긋지긋한 장애물 위로만 비치는 것 같아.\n\n니콜라스도 그렇게 생각했어. 그런 처지에 으레 따르는 조바심에 못 이겨 그는 살그머니 집을 나섰어. 침대에 누워 있는 건 더없이 귀한 시간을 허비하는 일 같고, 일어나서 움직이는 것이 어쩐지 그가 마음먹은 목적을 앞당기는 일 같았지. 그래서 런던 거리를 정처 없이 걸었어. 앞으로 몇 시간 동안은 매들린과 말을 나눌 수 없고, 그저 그 사이 시간이 얼른 지나가기만 바라는 것 말고는 할 일이 없다는 걸 뻔히 알면서도 말이야.\n\n그리고 지금도 그는 거리를 거닐며 점점 북적이고 하루를 준비하는 풍경을 멍하니 둘러보았어. 그런데 무엇 하나가 죄다 그에게 새로운 절망거리를 안겨 주는 것 같았어. 어젯밤만 해도, 젊고 다정하고 아름다운 사람을 그런 비열한 자에게, 그것도 그런 까닭으로 제물로 바친다는 게 너무나 끔찍해서 도무지 이루어질 수 없는 일처럼 보였어. 그리고 마음이 뜨거워질수록 어떤 일이 가로막고 들어서서 그녀를 그 손아귀에서 구해 낼 거라는 확신이 점점 굳어졌지. 그런데 지금은 이런 생각이 들었어. 세상일이 날마다 얼마나 한결같이 똑같은 굴레 속에서 돌아가는지. 젊음과 아름다움은 죽어 가는데 추하고 인색한 늙은이는 비틀거리며 살아남고, 약삭빠른 탐욕은 부자가 되는데 사내답고 정직한 마음은 가난하고 슬프지. 으리으리한 집에 사는 사람은 얼마나 적고, 또 얼마나 많은 사람들이 악취 나는 우리 같은 곳에 누워 지내는지. 날마다 아침에 일어나 밤에 다시 눕고, 살다 죽기를 아비와 아들, 어미와 자식, 대를 잇고 또 잇건만, 몸을 누일 집 한 칸 없고 단 한 사람도 그들을 도우려 힘쓰지 않지. 호화롭고 멋진 삶이 아니라 그저 가장 비참하고 모자란 입에 풀칠할 수단을 구하는 일조차, 그 한 도시 안에서 여자와 아이들이 계급으로 나뉘어, 귀족 가문이나 지체 높은 사람들만큼이나 꼬박꼬박 셈해지고 헤아려지지. 그러고는 갓난아기 때부터 가장 죄스럽고 무서운 일에 내몰리도록 길러진다는 거야. 무지는 벌을 받을 뿐 가르쳐지지는 않고, 수천 명을 향해 감옥 문이 입을 벌리고 교수대가 어른거리지. 그 사람들은 요람 머리맡부터 어둡게 드리운 사정에 떠밀려 그리로 가지만, 그 사정만 아니었다면 정직하게 밥벌이를 하며 평화롭게 살 수도 있었어. 얼마나 많은 사람이 영혼이 죽은 채로 살 기회조차 얻지 못하는지. 또 마음만 먹으면 얼마든지 악해질 수 있는데도 좀처럼 빗나가지 않을 처지에 있는 사람들이, 도무지 달리 어쩔 수 없어 짓밟히고 망가진 가엾은 자들에게서 거만하게 등을 돌리지. 그런데 그 가엾은 자들이 착하게 산다면, 도리어 그게 더 놀라운 일이야. 처지 좋은 사람들이 나쁘게 사는 것보다 말이야. 세상에 부당함과 비참함과 그름이 얼마나 많은지. 그런데도 세상은 해가 가고 또 가도 한결같이 무심하고 냉담하게 굴러가고, 그것을 바로잡거나 고치려는 사람은 하나도 없지. 이 모든 걸 떠올리다가, 그 무더기 속에서 자기 마음이 매달린 사소한 한 가지 일을 끄집어내자, 니콜라스는 정말로 희망을 걸 만한 구석이 거의 없다고 느꼈어. 그것이 거대한 고통과 슬픔의 더미 속 한 톨이 되어, 그 엄청난 양을 불리는 작고 보잘것없는 하나가 되지 못할 까닭이 없다고 말이야.\n\n하지만 젊음은 마음대로 바꿀 수 있는 그림의 가장 어두운 면만 들여다보고 있지는 않아. 해야 할 일을 곱씹고, 밤이 끊어 놓았던 생각의 줄기를 다시 되살리면서, 니콜라스는 차츰차츰 있는 힘을 다 끌어모았어. 그리고 아침이 그의 목적에 알맞을 만큼 무르익자, 그는 그 시간을 가장 잘 쓰는 것 말고는 다른 생각이 없었어. 서둘러 아침을 먹고, 바로 처리해야 할 일들을 마치고 나서, 그는 매들린 브레이가 사는 집으로 발걸음을 옮겼어. 한시도 지체하지 않고 그곳에 닿았지.\n\n그는 문득 이런 생각이 들었어. 그 아가씨가 면회를 거절당할지도 모른다고 말이야. 자기한테는 한 번도 그런 적이 없었지만. 그래서 그런 경우에 그녀를 만날 가장 확실한 방법이 무엇일까 아직 궁리하고 있었어. 그런데 집 문 앞에 다다라 보니 문이 살짝 열려 있는 거야. 아마 마지막에 나간 사람이 그렇게 두고 간 모양이었지. 그건 지나치게 격식을 차릴 만한 처지가 아니었어. 그래서 니콜라스는 이 기회를 이용해 조용히 위층으로 올라가, 늘 안내받던 그 방의 문을 두드렸어. 안에서 누군가 들어오라고 하자, 그는 문을 열고 들어갔지.\n\n브레이와 그의 딸이 거기 둘이서만 앉아 있었어. 그가 그녀를 마지막으로 본 지 거의 삼 주가 다 되어 갔지. 그런데 그의 앞에 있는 이 사랑스러운 아가씨에게는 어떤 변화가 있었어. 그 짧은 시간 동안 얼마나 큰 마음의 고통이 짓눌려 담겨 있었는지를, 니콜라스에게 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 똑똑히 말해 주는 변화였지. 그가 들어섰을 때 그를 향해 돌린 그 아름다운 얼굴의 완벽한 창백함, 그 맑고 투명한 흰빛은 무슨 말로도 표현할 수 없고, 무엇에도 견줄 수 없었어. 그녀의 머리카락은 짙고 깊은 갈색이었어. 하지만 그 얼굴에 그늘을 드리우고, 그에 못지않게 흰 목덜미 위로 흘러내리니, 그 강한 대비 탓에 까마귀처럼 새까매 보였지. 어두운 눈에는 어딘지 사납고 안절부절못하는 기운이 있었어. 하지만 그가 잘 기억하는 그 참을성 있는 눈빛, 그 부드러운 슬픔의 빛도 똑같이 어려 있었고, 눈물 자국은 한 방울도 없었어. 더없이 아름다웠어. 어쩌면 그 어느 때보다 더 아름다웠지. 그런데 그녀의 얼굴에는 그를 완전히 무너뜨리는 무언가가 있었어. 그건 가장 사나운 슬픔의 몸부림보다 훨씬 더 가슴을 저미는 것 같았지. 그건 그저 차분하고 침착한 게 아니라, 굳어 버린 듯 뻣뻣했어. 아버지의 눈앞에서 그 침착함을 불러내느라 쏟은 격렬한 안간힘이 다른 모든 생각을 억눌렀을 뿐 아니라, 그 안간힘이 얼굴에 잠깐 새겨 넣은 표정마저 가라앉지 못하게 막아, 마치 그 승리의 증거인 양 거기에 못 박아 둔 것 같았어.\n\n아버지는 그녀의 맞은편에 앉아 있었어. 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 보지는 않고, 명랑한 척하며 이야기하는 사이사이 그녀를 흘끔거렸지. 하지만 그 명랑한 태도는 속마음의 불안을 제대로 감추지 못했어. 그림 도구는 늘 놓이던 탁자 위에 없었고, 그녀가 평소 하던 일을 보여 주는 다른 흔적도 하나 보이지 않았어. 니콜라스가 늘 싱싱한 꽃으로 가득 차 있는 걸 보던 작은 꽃병들은 텅 비어 있거나, 시든 줄기와 잎사귀 몇 개만 꽂혀 있을 뿐이었어. 새는 잠잠했어. 밤에 새장을 덮어 두는 천도 걷히지 않은 채였지. 그 주인이 새를 잊어버린 거야.\n\n마음이 괴롭도록 예민해져 무엇이든 받아들이려 할 때면, 한눈에 많은 것을 알아챌 수 있는 법이지. 지금이 바로 그런 때였어. 니콜라스가 방 안을 한 번 휙 둘러보기만 했는데, 벌써 브레이 씨가 그를 알아보고는 짜증스럽게 말했거든.\n\n\"자, 무슨 일이오? 여기 온 용건을 얼른 말하시오. 부탁이니. 내 딸과 나는 당신이 온 일보다 훨씬 더 중요한 다른 일로 바쁘니까. 자, 당장 본론으로 들어가시오.\"\n\n니콜라스는 이 말투에 담긴 짜증과 조바심이 꾸며 낸 것이고, 브레이가 속으로는 딸의 주의를 끌어 줄 만한 일이라면 무엇이든 끼어드는 걸 반긴다는 걸 잘 알아챘어. 그는 말하는 그 아버지에게 저도 모르게 눈길을 던지고는 그의 불안을 눈여겨봤지. 브레이가 얼굴을 붉히며 고개를 돌려 버렸거든.\n\n하지만 그 수작이, 매들린을 끼어들게 하려는 수작인 한에서는 먹혀들었어. 그녀가 자리에서 일어나 니콜라스 쪽으로 다가오다 중간에 멈춰 서서, 편지라도 기다리는 듯 손을 내밀었어.\n\n\"매들린,\" 아버지가 짜증스럽게 말했어. \"얘야, 뭐 하는 거냐?\"\n\n\"브레이 양은 아마 동봉된 게 있나 하고 기다리시는 모양입니다.\" 니콜라스가 아주 또렷하게, 그녀가 좀처럼 못 알아들을 수 없을 만큼 힘주어 말했어. \"제 고용주가 지금 영국을 떠나 있어서요. 안 그랬으면 편지를 가져왔을 텐데요. 시간을 좀 주셨으면 합니다. 아주 조금만요. 정말 잠깐이면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그 일로 온 거라면,\" 브레이 씨가 말했어. \"그 점은 마음 놓아도 되오. 매들린, 얘야, 이 사람한테 네가 빚진 게 있는 줄은 몰랐구나.\"\n\n\"네, 그저 좀 사소한 거예요.\" 매들린이 힘없이 대답했어.\n\n\"이제 너는 이렇게 생각하겠지.\" 브레이가 의자를 빙 돌려 니콜라스와 마주 보며 말했어. \"내 딸이 제 시간을 그렇게 쓰기로 한 덕에 네가 여기 가져오는 그 보잘것없는 푼돈이 없으면, 우리가 굶어 죽기라도 할 거라고 말이야?\"\n\n\"그런 생각은 한 적 없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"생각한 적이 없다고!\" 병자가 비웃었어. \"분명 생각했으면서. 그렇게 생각했고, 여기 올 때마다 그렇게 생각하지. 젊은이, 내가 그런 졸렬한 장사치들 속을 모를 줄 알아? 어쩌다 운 좋게 신사보다 잠깐 윗자리에 서게 되면 — 아니면 윗자리에 섰다고 착각하면 — 으스대는 그런 부류 말이야.\"\n\n\"제 용무는,\" 니콜라스가 공손하게 말했어. \"한 숙녀분께 드리는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"신사의 딸한테겠지.\" 병든 자가 받아쳤어. \"그래도 그 째째한 근성은 매한가지야. 그런데 어쩌면 자네는 주문을 가져온 건가, 응? 내 딸한테 새 주문이라도 가져온 거냐고?\"\n\n니콜라스는 이 물음이 던져진 의기양양한 말투를 알아챘어. 하지만 자기가 꾸며 낸 신분을 지켜야 한다는 걸 떠올리고는, 종이쪽지 하나를 꺼냈어. 자기 고용주가 그려 달라고 한 그림 소재 몇 가지의 목록인 척하는 종이였지. 혹시나 이런 일이 생길까 봐 미리 준비해 둔 거였어.\n\n\"오!\" 브레이 씨가 말했어. \"이게 그 주문이라는 건가?\"\n\n\"굳이 그렇게 부르시겠다면, 네, 그렇습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"그럼 자네 주인한테 전하게.\" 브레이가 의기양양한 미소를 지으며 종이를 도로 던지며 말했어. \"내 딸, 매들린 브레이 양은 이제 이런 잡일 따위에 황송하게 제 몸을 쓰지 않겠다고. 그 사람 생각처럼 내 딸이 부르면 달려가는 사람이 아니라고. 우리가 그 사람 돈으로 사는 줄 제 멋대로 우쭐대지만 천만의 말씀이라고. 우리한테 갚을 게 있거든 가게 앞을 지나는 첫 거지한테나 주든지, 아니면 다음번 이익을 셈할 때 제 몫에다 보태라고 말이야. 그리고 나로서는 지옥에나 가라지. 그게 그자의 주문에 대한 내 대답일세!\"\n\n'딸을, 저 울고 있는 아가씨를 팔아넘기는 사내가 떠드는 독립이라는 게 고작 이거로구나!' 니콜라스는 생각했어.\n\n아버지는 자기 의기양양함에 너무 푹 빠진 나머지, 니콜라스가 한순간 짓고 만 경멸의 표정을 알아채지 못했어. 고문대에 묶였다 해도 억누를 수 없었을 그런 표정을 말이야. \"자,\" 그가 잠깐의 침묵 끝에 말을 이었어. \"전할 말을 들었으니 이제 물러가도 좋네. 더 — 하! — 더 전할 주문이 없다면 말이지.\"\n\n\"없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그리고 한때 당신이 가졌던 지위를 생각해서, 그 말이든 다른 어떤 말이든, 그 자체로는 아무리 거슬릴 게 없다 해도, 제 쪽의 권위나 당신 쪽의 종속을 내비치는 듯한 말은 쓰지 않았습니다. 저한테는 주문이 없습니다. 하지만 두려움이 있습니다. 당신이 아무리 화를 내셔도 말하고야 말 두려움이요. 저 아가씨가 손이 닳도록 일해서 죽도록 당신을 부양하는 것보다 더 못한 무언가에 떠밀리고 있는 게 아닐까 하는 두려움입니다. 이것이 제 두려움이고, 저는 바로 당신 자신의 태도에서 이 두려움을 느낍니다. 제가 그것을 옳게 읽었는지 아닌지는, 당신의 양심이 말해 줄 겁니다.\"\n\n\"제발요!\" 매들린이 깜짝 놀라 둘 사이에 끼어들며 외쳤어. \"기억하세요, 이분은 편찮으세요.\"\n\n\"편찮다고!\" 병자가 숨을 헐떡이며 가쁘게 외쳤어. \"편찮다니! 편찮다니! 가게 점원 따위한테 수염을 잡히고 윽박질림을 당하는데, 저 애는 그자한테 나를 불쌍히 여기고 내가 아프단 걸 기억해 달라고 빌고 있구나!\"\n\n그는 발작을 일으켰어. 어찌나 심한지 한순간 니콜라스가 그의 목숨이 위태로운가 싶어 겁이 날 정도였지. 하지만 그가 차츰 회복되기 시작하는 걸 보고, 니콜라스는 물러났어. 아가씨에게 손짓으로 중요하게 전할 말이 있다는 것, 방 밖에서 기다리겠다는 것을 알리고 나서 말이야. 병든 자가 차츰, 하지만 더디게 정신을 차리는 소리가 들렸어. 그리고 방금 있었던 일은 한마디도 입에 올리지 않은 채, 마치 아직 그 일을 또렷이 기억하지 못하는 듯, 혼자 있게 해 달라고 청하는 소리도 들렸지.\n\n'아!' 니콜라스는 생각했어. '이 가느다란 기회가 사라지지 않기를, 단 한 주의 시간과 다시 생각할 틈만이라도 얻어 낼 수 있다면!'\n\n\"제게 무슨 전할 말씀이 있으시다고요.\" 매들린이 몹시 떨면서 모습을 드러내며 말했어. \"지금은 그 말씀을 강요하지 말아 주세요, 빌고 또 비옵니다. 모레 — 그때 여기로 와 주세요.\"\n\n\"그땐 너무 늦습니다. 제가 드릴 말씀을 하기엔 너무 늦어요.\" 니콜라스가 받아 말했어. \"그리고 그땐 당신이 여기 안 계실 거고요. 오, 아가씨. 저를 여기 보낸 그분을 단 한 번이라도 생각해 보신다면, 당신 자신의 마음의 평화를 위한 마지막 미련이 단 한 가닥이라도 남아 있다면, 제발, 부디 제 말을 한 번 들어주십시오.\"\n\n그녀가 그를 지나치려 했지만, 니콜라스는 부드럽게 그녀를 붙들었어.\n\n\"들어주세요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그저 제 말을 들어주시기만을 청합니다. 저뿐만 아니라, 제가 대신해 말하는 그분을 위해서요. 그분은 멀리 떨어져 계셔서 당신의 위험을 모르십니다. 하느님의 이름으로 제 말을 들어주세요!\"\n\n울다 지쳐 눈이 붓고 빨개진 가엾은 시중꾼이 곁에 서 있었어. 니콜라스가 어찌나 간절한 말로 그녀에게 호소하던지, 그녀는 옆문을 열고 주인 아가씨를 부축해 옆방으로 데려가더니, 니콜라스에게 따라 들어오라고 손짓했지.\n\n\"저를 두고 가 주세요, 부탁이에요.\" 아가씨가 말했어.\n\n\"이대로는 당신을 두고 갈 수 없습니다. 가지 않겠습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"저는 다해야 할 도리가 있습니다. 그러니 여기서든, 방금 우리가 나온 그 방에서든, 브레이 씨에게 어떤 위험이나 화가 닥치든, 저는 당신께 다시 한번 간곡히 빌어야겠습니다. 당신이 떠밀려 온 그 무서운 길을 한 번만 더 헤아려 봐 달라고요.\"\n\n\"당신이 말하는 그 길이 무엇이며, 누가 저를 떠밀었다는 거죠?\" 아가씨가 도도하게 말하려 애쓰며 물었어.\n\n\"저는 이 결혼을 말하는 겁니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"내일로 정해진 이 결혼을요. 나쁜 목적이라면 한 번도 머뭇거린 적 없고, 좋은 일에는 손 한 번 보태 준 적 없는 자가 꾸민 결혼 말입니다. 그 내막을 저는 당신보다 훨씬, 훨씬 더 잘 알고 있는 이 결혼을요. 어떤 그물이 당신을 휘감고 있는지 저는 압니다. 이 음모를 꾸민 자들이 어떤 인간들인지도 알아요. 당신은 돈에 배신당하고 팔려 가는 겁니다. 동전 한 닢 한 닢이 눈물로 녹슬어 있는, 아니 제 미친 손으로 절망에 빠져 스러진 망한 이들의 피로 시뻘건 그 황금에 말입니다.\"\n\n\"당신은 다해야 할 도리가 있다고 했지요.\" 매들린이 말했어. \"저에게도 있습니다. 그리고 하늘의 도움으로 저는 그것을 다할 거예요.\"\n\n\"차라리 악마의 도움이라고 하시지요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"인간들의 도움이라고요. 그중 하나가 당신의 정해진 남편이고, 그자들은—\"\n\n\"이건 듣지 않겠어요.\" 아가씨가 외쳤어. 아서 그라이드를 슬쩍 언급한 것만으로도 솟아오르는 듯한 몸서리를 억누르려 애쓰면서 말이야. \"이 불행이, 그게 불행이라면, 제가 스스로 택한 거예요. 저는 누구한테도 이 길로 떠밀린 게 아니라, 제 자유의지로 따라가는 거예요. 보세요, 저는 강요받지도 떠밀리지도 않았어요. 이걸 전해 주세요.\" 매들린이 말했어. \"제 소중한 벗이자 은인께요. 그리고 그분과 당신을 위한 제 기도와 감사를 가지고, 영영 저를 떠나 주세요!\"\n\n\"저를 채우고 있는 이 온 마음의 진심과 열정을 다해 당신께 빌기 전에는 그럴 수 없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어. \"이 결혼을 단 한 주, 짧은 한 주만 미뤄 달라고 빌기 전에는요. 지금처럼 휘둘리는 채로가 아니라, 당신이 내디디려는 이 걸음을 지금껏 할 수 있었던 것보다 더 깊이 생각해 달라고 빌기 전에는요. 당신이 손을 내주려는 이 사내의 악랄함을 다 알지는 못하시겠지만, 그자가 한 짓 몇 가지는 아시잖아요. 그자가 말하는 걸 들으셨고, 그 얼굴을 보셨잖아요. 늦기 전에 헤아려 보세요, 헤아려 보시라고요. 마음으로는 도무지 함께할 수 없는 정절을 제단 앞에서 그자에게 맹세하는 그 우롱을요. 본성과 이성이 거스를 수밖에 없는 엄숙한 말을 입에 담는 일을요. 그자의 그 역겨운 인간됨이 날마다 점점 더 드러날수록, 당신 스스로 보기에 자신이 천해지는 그 일이 따라올 수밖에 없고, 또 날마다 더 심해질 수밖에 없다는 걸요. 이 비열한 자와 함께하는 끔찍한 동거를, 썩음과 병을 피하듯 피하세요. 정 그래야 한다면 수고와 고생을 견디세요. 하지만 그자만은 피하세요, 피하시고 행복하세요. 제 말을 믿으세요, 저는 진실을 말하고 있습니다. 가장 비참한 가난, 가장 처참한 처지의 삶이라도, 깨끗하고 올곧은 마음만 있다면, 이런 사내의 아내로 겪어야 할 그것에 비하면 행복일 거예요!\"\n\n니콜라스가 말을 마치기 한참 전부터, 아가씨는 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 눈물을 마음껏 쏟아 냈어. 처음에는 북받치는 감정에 말이 제대로 나오지 않았지만, 말을 이어 가면서 차츰 목소리에 힘을 되찾은 그녀가 그에게 대답했어.\n\n\"당신께 숨기지 않을게요. 어쩌면 숨겨야 할지도 모르지만요. 지난번 당신을 본 뒤로 저는 마음이 몹시 아팠고, 가슴이 거의 무너질 뻔했어요. 저는 이분을 사랑하지 않아요. 우리 사이의 나이며 취향이며 습관의 차이가 그걸 가로막죠. 이건 그분도 알아요. 그러면서도 제게 손을 내밀어요. 그 손을 받아들이는 것, 오직 그 한 걸음만이, 이곳에서 죽어 가는 제 아버지를 풀려나게 할 수 있어요. 어쩌면 아버지의 목숨을 여러 해 늘려 드릴 수도 있고요. 아버지를 다시 편안하게 — 거의 풍족하다고 해도 좋을 만큼 — 해 드릴 수 있어요. 그리고 한 너그러운 분을, 슬프게도 그 고귀한 마음을 거의 알아주지 못하는 사람을 돕는 짐에서 풀어 드릴 수 있어요. 저를 그렇게 못나게 여기지 말아 주세요. 느끼지도 않는 사랑을 꾸며 낸다고는 믿지 말아 주세요. 저에 대해 그렇게 나쁘게 전하지 말아 주세요. 그건 제가 견딜 수 없으니까요. 이 가엾은 손을 위해 이런 대가를 치르는 사람을 이성으로든 본성으로든 사랑할 수 없다 해도, 저는 아내의 도리는 다할 수 있어요. 그가 제게 바라는 모든 것이 될 수 있고, 또 되겠어요. 그는 저를 있는 그대로 받아들이는 데 만족해요. 저는 약속을 했고, 그러니 기뻐해야지 울어선 안 되겠죠. 정말 그래요. 저처럼 벗도 없고 의지할 데 없는 사람에게 당신이 보여 준 관심, 당신이 맡은 일을 다해 낸 그 섬세함, 저와 지켜 준 그 신의에 진심으로 감사드려요. 그리고 이렇게 마지막으로 변변찮은 감사를 드리면서, 보시다시피 저는 눈물이 나네요. 하지만 저는 후회하지 않아요. 불행하지도 않고요. 이렇게 쉽게 이뤄 낼 수 있는 모든 것을 생각하면 저는 행복해요. 다 끝나고 나서 돌이켜 볼 때면 더 행복할 거예요. 알아요.\"\n\n\"행복을 말하면서도 당신의 눈물은 더 빨리 흐르는군요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그리고 당신은 그 어두운 앞날을 들여다보길 피하고 있어요. 당신에게 그토록 많은 비참함을 안겨 줄 게 분명한 그 앞날을요. 이 결혼을 한 주만 미루세요. 단 한 주만요!\"\n\n\"방금 당신이 우리한테 들어왔을 때, 아버지는 이야기를 하고 계셨어요. 제가 옛날에 보던, 그러고는 정말 오래오래 보지 못했던 그런 미소를 띠고서요. 내일이면 찾아올 자유에 대해서요.\" 매들린이 한순간 굳센 목소리로 말했어. \"반가운 변화, 신선한 공기, 지친 몸에 새 생기를 불어넣어 줄 온갖 새로운 풍경과 것들에 대해서요. 그 생각에 아버지 눈이 반짝였고 얼굴이 환해졌어요. 저는 단 한 시간도 미루지 않겠어요.\"\n\n\"그건 당신을 떠밀려는 속임수와 꼼수일 뿐이에요.\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어.\n\n\"더는 듣지 않겠어요.\" 매들린이 황급히 말했어. \"이미 너무 많이 들었는걸요. 들어야 할 것보다 더 많이요. 제가 당신께 드린 말씀은, 당신이 정직하게 전해 주리라 믿는 그 소중한 벗께 드린 거예요. 얼마쯤 지나서, 제가 좀 더 마음이 가라앉고 새 삶에 익숙해지면, 그때까지 제가 산다면, 그분께 편지를 쓸게요. 그동안 온 거룩한 천사들이 그분의 머리 위에 축복을 쏟아붓고, 그분을 번성하게 하고 지켜 주기를 빌어요.\"\n\n그녀가 니콜라스를 지나쳐 급히 가려는데, 그가 그녀 앞에 몸을 던지며 그녀가 무모하게 서둘러 달려가는 그 운명을 단 한 번만 더 생각해 달라고 애원했어.\n\n\"물러설 길이 없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 애원에 몸부림치며 말했어. \"발을 뺄 수도 없어요! 모든 후회가 다 부질없을 거고, 그 후회는 깊고 쓰라릴 게 분명합니다. 이 마지막 순간에 당신을 멈추게 하려면 제가 무슨 말을 해야 할까요? 당신을 구하려면 제가 무엇을 할 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"아무것도요.\" 그녀가 두서없이 대답했어. \"이건 제가 겪은 가장 힘든 시련이에요. 제발 저를 가엾이 여겨 주세요, 빕니다. 이런 호소로 제 가슴을 찌르지 말아 주세요. 저— 저 아버지가 부르시는 소리가 들려요. 저— 저는 단 한순간도 더 여기 있어선 안 되고, 있지 않겠어요.\"\n\n\"만약 이것이 음모라면,\" 니콜라스가 그녀가 말하던 것과 똑같이 격하고 빠른 어조로 말했어. \"제가 아직 밝혀내진 못했지만 시간이 있으면 풀어낼 수도 있는 음모라면요. 만약 당신이 (모르고 계시지만) 당신 몫의 재산을 가질 권리가 있고, 그걸 되찾으면 이 결혼이 이뤄 낼 모든 걸 다 할 수 있다면, 당신은 물러서지 않으시겠어요?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요, 아니에요! 그건 불가능해요. 그건 어린애 이야기일 뿐이에요. 시간이 흐르면 아버지가 돌아가실 거예요. 또 부르시잖아요!\"\n\n\"이게 우리가 이 세상에서 만나는 마지막일지도 모릅니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"어쩌면 우리가 두 번 다시 만나지 않는 편이 제게는 나을지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"우리 둘 다에게요, 둘 다에게요.\" 매들린이 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모른 채 대답했어. \"이 한 번의 만남을 떠올리는 것만으로 제가 미쳐 버릴 것 같은 때가 올 거예요. 꼭 전해 주세요. 저를 차분하고 행복한 채로 두고 왔다고요. 그리고 하느님이 당신과 함께하시길, 제 감사한 마음과 축복도요!\"\n\n그녀는 가 버렸어. 니콜라스는 비틀거리며 집을 나서면서, 방금 자기 앞에서 막을 내린 그 다급한 장면을 떠올렸어. 마치 어떤 거칠고 어수선한 꿈의 허깨비 같았지. 하루가 지나갔어. 밤이 되어, 어느 정도 생각을 추스를 수 있게 되자, 그는 다시 밖으로 나섰어.\n\n그날 밤은 아서 그라이드의 총각 신세 마지막 밤이었는데, 그는 더없이 들떠서 무척 신이 나 있었어. 병 같은 초록빛 양복은 솔질이 되어 내일을 위해 준비되어 있었지. 페그 슬라이더스큐는 지난 살림살이의 계산을 내놓았어. 일 실링 육 펜스가 어김없이 다 셈해졌지(그녀한테는 한 번에 그보다 큰돈을 맡긴 적이 없었고, 계산은 보통 하루에 두 번 넘게 맞춰 보는 일이 없었거든). 다가올 잔치를 위한 채비는 다 되어 있었어. 그래서 아서는 앉아서 다가오는 행복을 그려 보고 있어도 좋았을 텐데, 그보다는 녹슨 걸쇠가 달린 더럽고 오래된 양피지 장부의 적힌 항목들을 들여다보며 앉아 있는 걸 더 좋아했어.\n\n\"아이고 좋다!\" 그가 키득거렸어. 바닥에 나사로 단단히 박아 둔 튼튼한 궤짝 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 거의 어깨까지 팔을 쑤셔 넣어 이 기름때 낀 책을 천천히 끄집어내면서 말이야. \"아이고, 이게 내 서재 전부지만, 여태껏 쓰인 책 중에 가장 재미있는 것 중 하나지! 신나는 책이고, 죄다 진짜고 사실이야 — 그게 제일 좋은 점이지 — 잉글랜드 은행만큼 참되고, 거기 금은만큼 진짜라고. 아서 그라이드가 썼지. 히, 히, 히! 너희 이야기책 나부랭이를 쓰는 작자들은 절대 이만큼 좋은 책은 못 만들어, 내가 장담하지. 이건 사사로이 돌려 보려고, 오직 나 혼자만 읽으려고 지은 거고, 다른 누구도 아니야. 히, 히, 히!\"\n\n이렇게 혼잣말을 중얼거리며 아서는 그 귀한 책을 탁자로 가져갔어. 그러고는 먼지 낀 책상에 책을 맞춰 놓고, 안경을 쓰고, 책장을 뚫어져라 들여다보기 시작했지.\n\n\"니클비 씨한테 갈 게 큰돈이군.\" 그가 구슬픈 목소리로 말했어. \"온전히 갚을 빚이 구백칠십오 파운드, 사 실링, 삼 펜스. 증서에 따라 더 줄 돈이 오백 파운드. 합쳐서 천사백칠십오 파운드, 사 실링, 삼 펜스를, 내일 열두 시에 말이지. 그래도 반대쪽엔, 이 예쁜 병아리 덕에 차감될 게 있지. 그런데 또 이런 의문이 들어. 이 모든 걸 나 혼자서 해낼 수도 있지 않았을까 하는 거지. '겁 많은 가슴은 미인을 얻은 적이 없다.' 내 가슴은 왜 그리 겁이 많았을까? 어째서 내가 직접 브레이한테 대담하게 속을 털어놓고, 천사백칠십오 파운드, 사 실링, 삼 펜스를 아끼지 못했을까?\"\n\n이런 생각들이 늙은 고리대금업자를 어찌나 풀이 죽게 했던지, 가슴에서 가냘픈 신음이 한두 번 새어 나왔고, 그는 두 손을 치켜들며 자기가 구빈원에서 죽고 말 거라고 내뱉었어. 하지만 더 곰곰이 따져 보니, 어떤 경우든 랄프의 빚은 자기가 갚거나 그럴듯하게 셈을 맞춰 줘야 했을 거라는 걸 떠올렸어. 게다가 이 일을 혼자 떠맡았다면 과연 성공했을지 도무지 자신이 없었지. 그래서 그는 다시 마음의 평정을 되찾고, 더 흐뭇한 항목들을 두고 재잘대며 입을 우물거렸어. 페그 슬라이더스큐가 들어오는 바람에 멈추기 전까지 말이야.\n\n\"오, 페그!\" 아서가 말했어. \"뭔가? 무슨 일이야, 페그?\"\n\n\"닭이에요.\" 페그가 작은, 아주 작은 닭 한 마리가 담긴 접시를 들어 보이며 대답했어. 그야말로 닭의 별종이었지. 어찌나 작고 비쩍 말랐던지.\n\n\"아름다운 새로구나!\" 아서가 값을 물어보고는 그 크기에 걸맞다는 걸 알고 나서 말했어. \"베이컨 한 점에다, 달걀을 깨 넣은 소스에다, 감자에, 채소에, 사과 푸딩에, 페그, 그리고 치즈 조금이면, 우리는 황제 같은 만찬을 먹는 거지. 그 애랑 나밖에 없을 거야 — 그리고 우리가 다 먹고 나면 너도, 페그.\"\n\n\"나중에 가서 돈 들었다고 투덜대지나 마세요.\" 슬라이더스큐 부인이 뚱하게 말했어.\n\n\"처음 한 주는 아무래도 돈을 좀 써야겠지.\" 아서가 끙 앓는 소리를 내며 대답했어. \"그러고 나선 그걸 메워야 해. 나는 어쩔 수 없는 것보다 더 먹진 않을 거야. 그리고 너도 네 늙은 주인을 너무 사랑해서, 어쩔 수 없는 것보다 더 먹진 않을 거지, 안 그러냐, 페그?\"\n\n\"내가 뭘 안 한다고요?\" 페그가 말했어.\n\n\"네 늙은 주인을 너무 사랑해서—\"\n\n\"아뇨, 손톱만큼도 너무는 아니에요.\" 페그가 말했어.\n\n\"오, 이런, 악마가 이 여편네를 데려가 버렸으면!\" 아서가 외쳤어. \"주인을 너무 사랑해서 그 주인 돈으로 어쩔 수 없는 것보다 더 먹진 않을 거란 말이야.\"\n\n\"그 주인 뭐로요?\" 페그가 말했어.\n\n\"오, 이런! 가장 중요한 말은 절대 못 듣고, 다른 건 죄다 듣는다니까!\" 그라이드가 칭얼대듯 말했어. \"주인 돈으로 말이야 — 이 못된 할망구야!\"\n\n슬라이더스큐 부인의 매력에 바치는 이 마지막 찬사는 속삭임으로 내뱉어졌으니, 그 부인은 거친 으르렁 소리로 그 일반적인 주장에 동의했어. 그러자 때맞춰 현관문에서 종소리가 울렸지.\n\n\"종이 울리네.\" 아서가 말했어.\n\n\"네, 네. 저도 알아요.\" 페그가 받아쳤어.\n\n\"그럼 왜 안 나가?\" 아서가 버럭 소리쳤어.\n\n\"어디를 나가요?\" 페그가 되받았어. \"저 여기서 나쁜 짓 하나도 안 하잖아요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n이에 아서 그라이드는 있는 힘껏 으르렁대며 '종' 소리라는 말을 되풀이했어. 그리고 현관문에서 종을 당기는 시늉의 손짓으로 그 뜻을 슬라이더스큐 부인의 둔한 귀에 더 알아듣게 전하자, 페그는 절뚝이며 나갔어. 그러면서 종이 울렸다고 진작 말하지 않고 그것과 아무 상관도 없는 온갖 잡소리만 늘어놓아, 자기가 마실 맥주 반 파인트를 계단에 기다리게 만들었다고 쏘아붙이고 나서 말이야.\n\n\"자네한테 뭔가 변한 게 있어, 페그 부인.\" 아서가 그녀가 나가는 걸 눈으로 좇으며 말했어. \"그게 뭔지는 잘 모르겠지만, 이게 계속되면 우리가 오래 사이좋게 지내진 못하겠는데. 자네 머리가 돌아가는 모양이야, 내 보기엔. 정말 그렇다면 자네는 알아서 나가야 해, 페그 부인 — 아니면 끌려 나가든지. 나야 어느 쪽이든 마찬가지지.\" 이렇게 중얼거리며 책장을 넘기던 그는, 곧 자기 눈길을 사로잡는 무언가에 닿았어. 그러고는 그 책장의 빠져드는 흥미에 페그 슬라이더스큐고 다른 무엇이고 죄다 잊어버렸지.\n\n방에는 흐릿하고 때 낀 등잔에서 나오는 빛 말고는 다른 빛이 없었어. 게으른 심지가 어두운 갓에 한층 더 가려진 채, 아주 좁은 데에만 그 희미한 빛을 던지고, 그 너머는 죄다 짙은 그림자 속에 남겨 두었지. 이 등잔을 고리대금업자가 어찌나 바짝 끌어다 놓았던지, 그것과 자기 사이에는 그가 굽어보는 책 하나 놓일 자리밖에 없었어. 그가 책상에 팔꿈치를 괴고, 날카로운 광대뼈를 두 손에 받친 채 앉아 있으니, 등잔불은 그저 그 추한 얼굴을, 그리고 그가 앉은 작은 탁자를 도드라지게 비춰 줄 뿐, 방의 나머지는 죄다 깊고 음침한 어둠 속에 묻어 버렸어. 무슨 셈을 머릿속으로 하느라 눈을 들어 이 어둠 속을 멍하니 바라보던 아서 그라이드는, 문득 한 사내의 못 박힌 듯한 응시와 마주쳤어.\n\n\"도둑이야! 도둑이야!\" 고리대금업자가 벌떡 일어나 책을 가슴에 끌어안으며 비명을 질렀어. \"강도야! 사람 살려!\"\n\n\"무슨 일입니까?\" 그 형체가 다가서며 말했어.\n\n\"가까이 오지 마!\" 벌벌 떠는 가련한 자가 외쳤어. \"사람이냐, 아니면 — 아니면—\"\n\n\"사람이 아니면 저를 뭐로 보십니까?\" 하는 물음이 돌아왔어.\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 아서 그라이드가 손으로 눈을 가리며 외쳤어. \"사람이지, 귀신이 아니야. 사람이야. 강도야! 강도야!\"\n\n\"무엇 때문에 이런 비명을 지르십니까? 혹시 저를 알아보고, 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있는 게 아니라면요?\" 낯선 이가 그에게 바짝 다가서며 말했어. \"저는 도둑이 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 뭐냐, 어떻게 여기 들어왔어?\" 그라이드가 다소 안심하면서도 여전히 손님에게서 물러나며 외쳤어. \"이름이 뭐고, 무슨 볼일이야?\"\n\n\"제 이름은 알 것 없습니다.\" 하는 대답이었어. \"당신네 하인이 길을 알려 줘서 여기 들어왔어요. 두세 번 말을 걸었는데, 당신이 책에 너무 깊이 빠져 있어 듣지 못하더군요. 그래서 당신이 좀 덜 정신을 놓을 때까지 잠자코 기다린 겁니다. 제가 뭘 원하는지는, 당신이 제 말을 듣고 알아들을 만큼 용기를 그러모았을 때 말씀드리지요.\"\n\n아서 그라이드는 큰맘 먹고 손님을 좀 더 주의 깊게 살펴봤어. 그러고는 그가 풍채와 거동이 점잖은 젊은이라는 걸 알아채고는, 자리로 돌아갔어. 그러고는 요즘 못된 자들이 들끓는데, 이번 일에다 전에도 집을 노린 일들이 있어 자기가 신경이 곤두서 있었다고 중얼거리며, 손님에게 앉으라고 권했어. 하지만 그는 사양했지.\n\n\"맙소사! 당신보다 유리한 자리를 차지하려고 서 있는 게 아닙니다.\" 니콜라스가 (바로 니콜라스였거든) 말했어. 그라이드가 겁먹은 몸짓을 하는 걸 보고서였지. \"제 말을 들으세요. 당신은 내일 아침 결혼합니다.\"\n\n\"아, 아, 아니야.\" 그라이드가 받아쳤어. \"누가 그래? 그걸 어떻게 알아?\"\n\n\"어떻게 아는지는 상관없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"저는 압니다. 당신에게 손을 내줄 그 아가씨는 당신을 미워하고 경멸합니다. 당신 이름만 들어도 피가 싸늘해진다고요. 독수리와 양, 쥐와 비둘기도 당신과 그녀보다 더 어울리지 않는 짝은 못 될 겁니다. 보십시오, 저는 그녀를 압니다.\"\n\n그라이드는 깜짝 놀라 돌처럼 굳어 버린 듯 그를 바라봤지만, 말은 하지 않았어. 어쩌면 말할 기운이 없었는지도 몰라.\n\n\"당신과 또 다른 사내, 랄프 니클비라는 자가 둘이서 이 음모를 꾸며 냈죠.\" 니콜라스가 말을 이었어. \"매들린 브레이를 이렇게 팔아넘기는 일에 그자가 한몫한 대가로 당신이 그자에게 돈을 치르는 거죠. 그렇잖습니까. 거짓말이 당신 입술에서 떨리는 게 보입니다.\"\n\n그가 말을 멈췄어. 하지만 아서가 아무 대꾸도 하지 않자, 다시 말을 이었지.\n\n\"당신은 그녀를 속여 가로채는 걸로 당신 몫을 챙기는 겁니다. 어떻게, 무슨 수로 — 거짓이나 속임수로 그녀의 일을 더럽히긴 싫으니 — 저는 모릅니다. 지금은 모르지만, 이 일에서 저는 혼자도 아니고 외톨이도 아닙니다. 인간의 힘으로 당신의 사기와 배신을 당신이 죽기 전에 밝혀낼 수 있다면, 재물과 복수와 정당한 증오로 당신이 빠져나가는 굽이굽이마다 쫓고 추적할 수 있다면, 당신은 이 일로 톡톡히 대가를 치르게 될 겁니다. 우리는 이미 냄새를 맡았어요. 우리가 모르는 걸 아는 당신이, 우리가 당신을 무너뜨릴 때가 언제일지 한번 점쳐 보시지요!\"\n\n그가 다시 말을 멈췄지만, 아서 그라이드는 여전히 말없이 그를 노려보고만 있었어.\n\n\"제가 호소해서 그 동정심이나 인간다움을 건드릴 가망이 조금이라도 있는 사람이라면,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"저는 이 아가씨의 무력함과 순결함과 젊음을 기억해 달라고 당신께 간곡히 권했을 겁니다. 그녀의 인품과 아름다움, 효심의 갸륵함, 그리고 마지막으로, 무엇보다 당신과 더 가까이 얽힌 일로서, 그녀가 당신의 자비와 사내다운 마음에 호소했다는 걸요. 하지만 저는 당신 같은 사람들에게 취할 수 있는 유일한 방법을 택해, 묻겠습니다. 돈을 얼마나 주면 당신이 손을 떼겠습니까. 당신이 처한 위험을 기억하세요. 보다시피 저는 아주 약간의 도움만 있으면 훨씬 더 많은 걸 알아낼 만큼 이미 알고 있어요. 당신이 모면하는 위험을 따져 기대 이익에서 얼마쯤 깎고, 당신의 값을 말해 보세요.\"\n\n늙은 아서 그라이드는 입술을 움직였지만, 그건 그저 흉한 미소를 지어 보일 뿐 다시 가만히 멈췄어.\n\n\"당신은 생각하겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그 값이 치러지지 않을 거라고요. 브레이 양에게는 부유한 벗들이 있어서, 이런 궁지에서 그녀를 구하기 위해서라면 제 심장이라도 돈으로 바꿀 사람들이지요. 값을 말하세요. 이 혼례를 단 며칠만 미루고, 제가 말한 그 사람들이 그 돈을 치르길 꺼리는지 어떤지 한번 보시라고요. 제 말 듣고 있습니까?\"\n\n니콜라스가 말을 꺼냈을 때 아서 그라이드는 처음엔 랄프 니클비가 자기를 배신했구나 싶었어. 하지만 그가 말을 이어 갈수록, 그가 가진 지식을 어떻게 손에 넣었든 그가 하는 역할은 진짜이고, 랄프와는 아무 상관이 없다는 확신이 들었지. 그가 확실히 아는 건 그저, 자기 그라이드가 랄프의 빚을 갚는다는 것뿐인 듯했어. 하지만 그건 브레이가 잡혀 있게 된 사정을 아는 사람이라면 — 랄프 자신의 말대로라면 브레이 본인에게까지도 — 누구에게나 빤히 알려진 일이었지. 매들린 본인을 속이는 사기에 대해서는, 이 손님이 그 본질이나 규모에 대해 아는 게 너무 적어서, 그건 운 좋은 짐작이거나 막무가내로 던진 비난일 수도 있었어. 어쨌든 그는 이 비밀의 열쇠를 분명 쥐고 있지 않았고, 그 비밀을 제 가슴속에 꽁꽁 간직한 그자를 해칠 수는 없었지. 벗들에 대한 언급이며 돈 제안이며, 그라이드는 그저 시간을 끌려는 속 빈 허풍으로 여겼어. '그리고 돈을 손에 넣을 수 있다 쳐도,' 아서 그라이드는 니콜라스를 흘끔 보며, 그 대담함과 뻔뻔함에 분노로 떨면서 생각했어. '저 곱상한 병아리를 내 아내로 삼고, 너한테서 가로채 주마, 이 풋내기 매끈둥이야!'\n\n오랜 세월 고객들이 하는 말을 꼼꼼히 재고 헤아리며, 머릿속으로 가능성을 정밀하게 저울질하고 면전에서 승산을 따지되 조금도 그러는 티를 내지 않는 버릇 덕에, 그라이드는 결론을 빨리 내리고, 헷갈리고 뒤얽히고 때로는 앞뒤가 맞지 않는 전제들에서 아주 교활한 추론을 끌어내는 데 능했어. 그래서 니콜라스가 말을 이어 가는 동안, 그는 자기 나름의 해석으로 바짝 뒤따라갔지. 그리고 니콜라스가 말을 멈췄을 때, 그는 마치 보름 동안 곰곰이 따진 것처럼 만반의 준비가 되어 있었어.\n\n\"듣고말고.\" 그가 자리에서 벌떡 일어나, 덧창의 걸쇠를 도로 젖히고 미닫이창을 확 올리며 외쳤어. \"여기 사람 살려! 사람 살려! 사람 살려!\"\n\n\"뭐 하는 겁니까?\" 니콜라스가 그의 팔을 붙잡으며 말했어.\n\n\"강도야, 도둑이야, 사람 죽인다 소리쳐서 동네를 깨우고, 너랑 몸싸움을 벌여 피를 좀 흘리고, 네가 날 털러 왔다고 맹세할 테다, 네가 내 집에서 나가지 않으면.\" 그라이드가 무시무시하게 이를 드러내며 고개를 안으로 들이밀며 대답했어. \"정말이야!\"\n\n\"이 가련한 인간!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어.\n\n\"감히 네 협박을 여기 들고 와, 그래?\" 그라이드가 말했어. 니콜라스에 대한 질투와 제 승리에 대한 감격이 그를 완전한 악귀로 바꿔 놓았지. \"너, 퇴짜 맞은 연인 양반? 이런 세상에! 히! 히! 히! 하지만 넌 그 애를 못 가져, 그 애도 널 못 가져. 그 앤 내 아내야, 나를 끔찍이 위하는 내 작은 마누라라고. 그 애가 널 그리워할 것 같아? 그 애가 울 것 같으냐고? 그 애가 우는 걸 한번 보고 싶구나, 난 개의치 않아. 눈물을 흘리면 더 예뻐 보이거든.\"\n\n\"이 악당!\" 니콜라스가 분노에 목이 메어 말했어.\n\n\"일 분만 더.\" 아서 그라이드가 외쳤어. \"그럼 온 거리를 비명으로 깨워 주마. 다른 누가 그런 비명을 질렀다면, 예쁜 매들린의 품에 안겨 있던 나라도 잠을 깰 만큼.\"\n\n\"이 개 같은 놈!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"네놈이 좀 더 젊은 자였다면—\"\n\n\"아, 그래!\" 아서 그라이드가 비웃었어. \"내가 좀 더 젊은 자였다면 이렇게까지는 아니겠지. 하지만 나처럼 이렇게 늙고 추한 자한테! 작은 매들린이 나 같은 자 때문에 너를 차 버리다니!\"\n\n\"내 말 들어.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그리고 내가 너를 거리로 내던지지 않을 만큼 나 자신을 다스리고 있는 걸 고맙게 여겨. 일단 너랑 맞붙으면 그 누구도 못 말릴 짓을 말이야. 나는 이 아가씨의 연인이었던 적이 없어. 우리 사이엔 어떤 언약도 약혼도 없었고, 사랑한다는 말 한마디 오간 적도 없어. 그녀는 내 이름조차 몰라.\"\n\n\"그래도 내가 물어볼 테다. 입을 맞춰 가며 그 애한테 졸라 댈 거야.\" 아서 그라이드가 말했어. \"그래, 그럼 그 애가 말해 주고, 입맞춤도 되돌려 주겠지. 그럼 우린 함께 웃고, 서로 부둥켜안고, 아주 즐거워하는 거야. 그 애를 갖고 싶어 했지만 이미 나한테 약속된 몸이라 못 가진 그 가엾은 풋내기를 떠올리면서 말이지!\"\n\n이 비웃음에 니콜라스의 얼굴에 어찌나 무서운 표정이 어렸던지, 아서 그라이드는 그게 곧 자기를 거리로 내던지겠다던 그 협박을 당장 실행에 옮길 전조라고 똑똑히 알아챘어. 그래서 그는 창밖으로 머리를 내밀고 두 손으로 단단히 매달려서는, 꽤나 요란하게 비명을 질러 댔지. 니콜라스는 그 소란의 결말을 기다릴 필요가 없다고 여기고, 분에 차서 한바탕 호통을 내뱉고는 방을 나와 집을 떠났어. 아서 그라이드는 그가 거리를 가로질러 가는 걸 지켜봤어. 그러고는 머리를 안으로 들이밀고, 전처럼 창문을 단단히 잠그고, 숨을 돌리려 앉았지.\n\n\"저 애가 언제고 토라지거나 심술을 부리면, 저 풋내기 얘기로 약을 올려 줘야지.\" 그가 마음을 가라앉히고 나서 말했어. \"내가 저놈을 안다는 건 꿈에도 모를 거야. 그리고 잘만 다루면, 이걸로 그 애 기를 꺾어 내 엄지손가락 밑에 둘 수 있겠지. 아무도 안 와서 다행이야. 너무 크게 부르진 않았으니. 감히 내 집에 들어와 나한테 들이대다니! 하지만 내일은 아주 멋진 승리를 거둘 테고, 저놈은 제 손가락을 물어뜯고 있겠지. 어쩌면 물에 빠져 죽거나 제 목을 그을지도 몰라! 그래도 난 놀라지 않을 거야! 그러면 아주 완벽해지지, 그래, 그야말로 완벽하게 말이야.\"\n\n다가오는 승리에 대한 이런저런 평을 늘어놓으며 평소의 상태로 돌아온 아서 그라이드는 책을 치웠어. 그리고 아주 조심스럽게 궤짝을 잠그고 나서, 부엌으로 내려가 페그 슬라이더스큐한테 잠자리에 들라고 이르고, 낯선 자를 그렇게 선뜻 들여보냈다고 야단을 쳤지.\n\n하지만 영문을 모르는 페그가 자기가 저지른 잘못을 도무지 알아듣지 못하자, 그는 그녀에게 불을 들고 있으라고 했어. 그동안 자기가 문단속을 한 바퀴 돌고, 현관문을 제 손으로 직접 잠갔지.\n\n\"위쪽 빗장,\" 아서가 말하면서 잠그며 중얼거렸어. \"아래쪽 빗장, 사슬, 가로대, 이중 자물쇠, 그리고 열쇠는 빼서 내 베개 밑에 둔다! 자, 퇴짜 맞은 구애자가 또 온다면, 열쇠 구멍으로나 들어오라지. 이제 다섯 시 반까지 자야겠다. 그때 일어나 결혼하러 가야 하니까, 페그!\"\n\n그러면서 그는 장난스럽게 슬라이더스큐 부인의 턱을 톡톡 두드렸어. 그러고는 잠깐, 그녀의 쪼글쪼글한 입술에 입맞춤을 찍어 총각 시절의 끝을 기념하고 싶은 마음이 든 듯했지. 하지만 다시 생각을 고쳐먹고는, 그 좀 더 따뜻한 친밀함 대신 그녀의 턱을 한 번 더 톡 두드리고, 슬그머니 잠자리로 갔어.\n\n결혼식 날 아침에 너무 늦게까지 침대에 누워 있거나 늦잠을 자는 사람은 그리 많지 않아. 옛날이야기 중에 정신이 딴 데 가 있기로 유명한 어떤 사람 얘기가 있어. 젊은 아내를 맞을 바로 그날 아침에 눈을 떴는데, 그 일을 까맣게 잊어버리고는 잔칫날을 위해 차려 놓은 그 좋은 옷을 두고 하인들을 나무랐다는 거야. 또 어떤 젊은 신사 이야기도 있지. 그런 경우를 위해 교회법이 정해 놓은 규율 따위는 안중에도 없이 자기 할머니한테 반해 버렸다지 뭐야. 두 경우 다 아주 별나고 특이한 일이라, 다음 세대 사람들이 널리 따를 만한 선례가 될 수 있을지는 자못 의심스러워.\n\n아서 그라이드는 한 시간도 더 전에 병녹색 결혼 예복을 차려입었어. 슬라이더스큐 부인이 깊은 잠을 떨치고 그의 방문을 두드리기 꼬박 한 시간도 더 전에 말이야. 그러고는 잔뜩 빼입은 채로 절뚝절뚝 아래층으로 내려가, 좋아하는 강장제를 찔끔 맛보며 입맛을 다셨지. 그 골동품 같은 노인네가 부엌에 모습을 드러내 빛을 비추기도 전에 말이야.\n\n\"퉤!\" 페그가 말했어. 집안일을 한답시고 녹슨 벽난로 안에 얼마 안 되는 잿더미를 뒤적이면서 말이야. \"결혼이라니! 거 참 대단한 결혼일세! 자기를 돌봐 줄 사람으로 늙은 페그보다 더 나은 누군가가 필요하시다 이거지? 그런데 나한테 몇 번이고 뭐라고 했더라? 적은 밥에, 쥐꼬리만 한 품삯에, 변변찮은 불이나 때면서 만족하라고 할 때 말이야. '내 뜻이야, 페그! 내 뜻이라고!' 그러셨지. '난 홀아비야 ― 친구도 없고 ― 친척도 없어, 페그.' 거짓말! 그래 놓고 이제 와서 새 안주인을 데려온다 이거지, 그것도 애 같은 얼굴을 한 계집애를! 아내가 필요했으면, 이 바보 영감탱이가, 어째서 제 나이에 어울리고 제 버릇도 잘 아는 여자를 못 골랐담? 그 여자는 내 일에 끼어들지 않을 거라고? 그래, 안 끼어들겠지, 그런데 그 까닭은 까맣게 모르시겠지, 아서 영감!\"\n\n슬라이더스큐 부인이 아래층에서 이렇게 투덜대는 동안 ― 아마 옛 주인이 다른 여자를 더 좋아한 탓에 생긴, 가시지 않은 실망과 무시당했다는 서운함 때문이었겠지 ― 아서 그라이드는 응접실에서 간밤에 있었던 일을 곰곰이 따져 보고 있었어.\n\n\"그자가 어떻게 알아낸 건지 도무지 모르겠단 말이야.\" 아서가 중얼거렸어. \"내가 뭔가 실수를 한 게 아니라면 ― 이를테면 브레이네 집에서 뭔가 흘렸는데 그걸 누가 엿들었다든가. 그럴지도 모르지. 그게 사실이라 해도 놀랍지 않아. 니클비 씨는 문밖으로 나가기 전에 내가 그 사람한테 떠드는 걸 자주 못마땅해했으니까. 그 부분은 말하지 말아야겠어. 안 그러면 기분 상하게 해서 하루 종일 나를 안절부절못하게 만들 거야.\"\n\n랄프는 모두에게 우러러보였어. 동료들 사이에서도 뛰어난 천재로 인정받았지. 그런데 아서 그라이드한테는 랄프의 엄하고 굽힐 줄 모르는 성격과 능수능란한 솜씨가 어찌나 깊은 인상을 남겼던지, 그는 정말로 랄프를 무서워했어. 천성이 뼛속까지 비굴하고 겁이 많은 아서 그라이드는 랄프 니클비 앞에서 흙바닥에 엎드리듯 자신을 한껏 낮췄어. 이런 공동의 이해관계가 없을 때조차도, 그는 랄프한테 말대꾸를 하거나 노예처럼 빌붙는 비굴한 아첨 말고 다른 마음으로 대들기는커녕, 차라리 그의 신발을 핥고 발치에 기어 다니려 들었지.\n\n아서 그라이드는 이제 약속대로 랄프 니클비를 찾아갔어. 그리고 랄프 니클비한테 이야기했지. 간밤에 한 번도 본 적 없는 어떤 젊고 으스대는 놈이 자기 집에 쳐들어와서, 예정된 결혼을 못 하게 겁을 주려 했다고 말이야. 한마디로, 니콜라스가 한 말과 행동을 다 들려준 거야. 단, 자기가 마음먹은 대로 살짝 빼놓은 부분만 빼고.\n\n\"그래, 그래서 어떻게 됐는데?\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"아! 그게 다예요.\" 그라이드가 대답했어.\n\n\"그자가 자네를 겁주려 했고,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"내 짐작엔 자네가 겁먹었겠지. 그런 거 아닌가?\"\n\n\"내가 도둑이야 살인이야 소리쳐서 그자를 겁줬는걸요.\" 그라이드가 대꾸했어. \"한순간은 진심이었어요, 정말이지. 그자가 협박을 했다고, 내 목숨이나 돈을 내놓으라 했다고 맹세할 마음이 반 넘게 들었거든요.\"\n\n\"오호!\" 랄프가 그를 곁눈질하며 말했어. \"질투까지 하는군!\"\n\n\"이런, 저것 좀 보게나!\" 아서가 두 손을 비비며 짐짓 웃는 척 외쳤어.\n\n\"이 사람아, 왜 그렇게 얼굴을 찡그리나?\" 랄프가 말했어. \"자네 질투하는 거 맞잖아 ― 게다가 그럴 만한 까닭도 있고 말이야.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니지. 그럴 만한 까닭은 없어요, 응? 그럴 만한 까닭이 있다고 생각하는 건 아니지요, 그렇지요?\" 아서가 우물거리며 외쳤어. \"그래도 그렇게 생각해요, 응?\"\n\n\"아니, 사실이 어떤데 그래?\" 랄프가 되받았어. \"여기 한 늙은이가 있어. 어린 처녀와 억지로 결혼하려는 참이지. 그런데 이 늙은이한테 잘생긴 젊은 놈이 찾아온 거야 ― 자네가 그놈더러 잘생겼다고 했지, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"아니!\" 아서 그라이드가 으르렁댔어.\n\n\"어!\" 랄프가 대꾸했어. \"그랬다고 한 줄 알았는데. 뭐! 잘생겼든 안 생겼든, 이 늙은이한테 젊은 놈이 찾아와서 온갖 험악한 도전을 그의 이빨에다 ― 아니, 잇몸에다 대고라고 해야겠군 ― 퍼부었지. 그러고는 대놓고 말했다지. 당신 신붓감이 당신을 미워한다고. 그놈이 왜 그랬겠어? 자선을 베푸느라?\"\n\n\"그 아가씨를 사랑해서는 아니에요.\" 그라이드가 대답했어. \"그놈이 그랬거든요. 둘 사이엔 사랑한다는 말이 ― 바로 그놈이 한 말이에요 ― 오간 적이 한 번도 없다고요.\"\n\n\"그놈이 그랬다!\" 랄프가 비웃듯 되뇌었어. \"하지만 한 가지 점에서는 그놈이 마음에 들어. 자네한테 친절히 미리 경고해 줬으니까. 자네의 ― 그게 뭐더라? ― 어여쁜 새 색시인지 앙증맞은 병아리인지 ― 어느 쪽이지? ― 를 단단히 자물쇠로 채워 두라고 말이야. 조심해, 그라이드, 조심하라고. 게다가 이건 승리야. 씩씩한 젊은 연적한테서 그 여자를 빼앗아 오는 거니까. 늙은이한테는 대단한 승리지! 이제 손에 넣고 나서 안전하게 지키는 일만 남았어 ― 그게 다야.\"\n\n\"참 대단한 사람이라니까!\" 아서 그라이드가 외쳤어. 괴로움이 극에 달했는데도 짐짓 무척 즐거운 척하면서 말이야. 그러고는 걱정스레 덧붙였지. \"그래요. 안전하게 지키는 거, 그게 다죠. 그건 별일 아니지요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"별일 아니라고!\" 랄프가 비웃으며 말했어. \"아니, 여자가 얼마나 다루기 쉽고 마음대로 휘두르기 쉬운지는 누구나 다 알잖아. 그건 그렇고, 이제 자네가 행복해질 시간이 거의 다 됐군. 나중에 번거롭지 않게, 지금 차용증 돈을 갚아 두는 게 좋겠지?\"\n\n\"아유, 정말 대단한 양반이라니까요!\" 아서가 갈라진 목소리로 말했어.\n\n\"안 될 거 있나?\" 랄프가 말했어. \"지금부터 열두 시 사이에 그 돈 이자를 자네한테 쳐줄 사람은 아무도 없을 텐데, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"하지만 당신한테 이자를 쳐줄 사람도 없잖아요, 안 그래요.\" 아서가 얼굴에 짜낼 수 있는 온갖 교활하고 능청스러운 빛을 띠며 랄프를 곁눈질하며 대꾸했어.\n\n\"게다가,\" 랄프가 입술 끝을 슬쩍 비틀어 웃으며 말했어. \"자네는 지금 그 돈을 갖고 있지도 않고, 이럴 줄 미리 대비하지도 않았지. 안 그랬으면 가지고 왔을 테니까. 그리고 자네가 나만큼 기꺼이 편의를 봐주고 싶은 사람도 없을 거고. 다 알겠어. 우리는 서로 비슷한 만큼 믿는 거지. 준비됐나?\"\n\n랄프의 이 마지막 말이 이어지는 동안 그라이드는 그저 히죽거리고 고개를 끄덕이고 종알대기만 했는데, 이윽고 그렇다고 대답했어. 그러고는 모자에서 커다란 흰 장식 리본 두 개를 꺼내, 하나를 제 가슴에 꽂고, 적잖이 애를 써서 친구한테도 똑같이 달게 했지. 이렇게 차림을 갖춘 두 사람은 랄프가 대기시켜 둔 삯마차에 올라타고, 아름답고 더없이 가엾은 신부의 집으로 달려갔어.\n\n그라이드는 집에 가까워지면 가까워질수록 점점 더 기운과 용기가 빠져나갔는데, 집 안에 감도는 구슬픈 정적에 완전히 겁을 먹고 풀이 죽어 버렸어. 그들이 마주친 단 한 사람, 가엾은 하녀의 얼굴은 눈물과 잠 못 잔 흔적으로 일그러져 있었지. 그들을 맞이하거나 반겨 주는 사람은 아무도 없었어. 두 사람은 살금살금 위층 여느 거실로 올라갔지. 신랑과 그 친구라기보다는 도둑 두 명 같은 꼴로 말이야.\n\n\"이건 마치,\" 랄프가 저도 모르게 낮고 가라앉은 목소리로 말했어. \"결혼식이 아니라 장례식이 치러지는 것 같군.\"\n\n\"히, 히!\" 친구가 킥킥거렸어. \"당신은 정말 ― 정말 우스운 양반이라니까요!\"\n\n\"우스워야지.\" 랄프가 무뚝뚝하게 말했어. \"여기가 좀 음침하고 으스스하니까 말이야. 좀 더 기운차게 굴게, 이 사람아, 그렇게 풀 죽은 꼴 하지 말고!\"\n\n\"네, 네, 그러죠.\" 그라이드가 말했어. \"그런데 ― 그런데 ― 그 여자가 벌써 오는 건 아니겠지요, 그렇지요?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 어쩔 수 없을 때까지는 안 나오겠지.\" 랄프가 시계를 보며 대꾸했어. \"아직 반 시간은 더 여유가 있어. 그 조바심 좀 다스리게.\"\n\n\"나 ― 나 ― 조바심 내는 거 아니에요.\" 아서가 더듬거렸어. \"그 여자한테 모질게 굴 생각은 결코 없어요. 아유, 원, 천만에. 그 여자가 천천히 하게 둬요 ― 제 시간껏 말이에요. 그 여자 시간은 곧 우리 시간이기도 하니까, 얼마든지요.\"\n\n랄프가 떨고 있는 친구를 날카로운 눈길로 쏘아봤어. 이 지극한 배려와 친절의 까닭을 완전히 꿰뚫고 있다는 듯한 눈빛이었지. 바로 그때 계단에서 발소리가 들렸어. 그리고 브레이가 직접 발끝으로 살금살금 방에 들어왔지. 마치 근처에 깨우면 안 되는 환자라도 있는 양, 조심하라는 손짓으로 손을 들어 올리면서 말이야.\n\n\"쉿!\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 말했어. \"그 애가 어젯밤 몹시 아팠어요. 가슴이 찢어지는 줄 알았다니까요. 지금은 옷을 다 차려입고 제 방에서 서럽게 울고 있어요. 하지만 한결 나아져서 아주 조용해요. 그거면 됐죠!\"\n\n\"준비가 됐다는 거지요?\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"다 됐어요.\" 아버지가 대답했어.\n\n\"기절이라든가 뭐 그런, 처녀 애들 특유의 나약한 짓으로 우리를 붙들어 둘 일은 없겠지요?\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"이젠 안심해도 돼요.\" 브레이가 대답했어. \"오늘 아침에 그 애와 이야기를 나눴거든요. 자! 이리 좀 와 봐요.\"\n\n그는 랄프 니클비를 방 저쪽 끝으로 데려가서는, 한쪽 구석에 잔뜩 웅크리고 앉아 외투 단추를 초조하게 만지작거리는 그라이드를 가리켰어. 그라이드의 얼굴은 그 음흉하고 비열한 표정 하나하나가, 불안과 두려움 탓에 더할 나위 없이 날카롭고 심하게 도드라져 있었지.\n\n\"저 사람을 봐요.\" 브레이가 힘주어 속삭였어. \"이건 아무래도 잔인한 짓이오.\"\n\n\"뭐가 잔인한 짓이라는 거요?\" 랄프가 물었어. 정말로 상대방 말뜻을 도무지 모르겠다는 듯, 무덤덤한 얼굴로 말이야.\n\n\"이 결혼 말이오.\" 브레이가 대답했어. \"뭐가 잔인하냐고 묻지 말아요. 나만큼 당신도 잘 알잖소.\"\n\n랄프는 어깨를 으쓱했어. 브레이의 조바심을 말없이 나무라는 거였지. 그러고는 눈썹을 치켜올리고 입술을 오므렸어. 어떤 말에 충분한 답을 마련해 두고도 그걸 꺼낼 더 좋은 기회를 기다리거나, 상대한테 굳이 대꾸할 가치도 없다 싶을 때 사람들이 하는 그런 몸짓이었어.\n\n\"저 사람을 봐요. 잔인해 보이지 않소?\" 브레이가 말했어.\n\n\"아니오!\" 랄프가 당당하게 대답했어.\n\n\"내 말은 잔인하다는 거요.\" 브레이가 잔뜩 짜증 난 기색으로 받아쳤어. \"이건 잔인한 짓이오, 온갖 못되고 배신스러운 것에 걸고 말이지!\"\n\n사람들이 어떤 부당한 짓을 저지르려 하거나 그런 짓을 거들려 할 때, 바로 그 대상이나 그와 비슷한 처지에 놓인 이를 두고 동정을 늘어놓는 일은 드물지 않아. 그러면서 그 순간 자기가 아주 고결하고 도덕적인 사람이라고, 동정심이라곤 전혀 드러내지 않는 자들보다 한참 낫다고 여기는 거지. 이건 행실보다 믿음을 떠받드는 격인데, 마음은 무척 편안하지. 랄프 니클비를 공정하게 말하자면, 그는 이런 식의 위선을 좀처럼 부리지 않았어. 하지만 그러는 사람들 속내는 다 꿰고 있었지. 그래서 브레이가 둘이 함께 아주 잔인한 짓을 하고 있다고 몇 번이고 열을 올려 떠들도록 내버려 뒀어. 다시 한마디 끼어들기 전까지 말이야.\n\n\"보다시피 저건 바짝 마르고 쪼그라들고 시들어 빠진 늙은 부스러기일 뿐이오.\" 마침내 상대가 입을 다물자 랄프가 대꾸했어. \"저 사람이 젊다면야 잔인할 수도 있겠지. 하지만 지금 형편이 이러니 ― 들어 봐요, 브레이 씨, 저 사람은 곧 죽을 거고, 그러면 그 애한테 젊고 부유한 과부 자리를 남겨 주는 거요! 매들린 양은 이번엔 당신 뜻을 따르는 거고, 다음번엔 제 뜻대로 하라고 하면 되지.\"\n\n\"맞아, 맞는 말이오.\" 브레이가 손톱을 깨물며 말했어. 영 마음이 편치 않은 게 빤히 보였지. \"그 애를 위해서 이 청혼을 받아들이라고 권하는 것보다 더 나은 일은 없지 않겠소? 자, 당신한테 묻겠소, 니클비, 세상 물정 아는 사람으로서 말이오. 더 나은 게 있겠소?\"\n\n\"당연히 없지요.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"내 말 들어 봐요, 선생. 여기서 오 마일 반경 안에 아버지가 백 명은 있을 거요. 형편이 넉넉하고, 좋은 집안에, 부유하고 든든한 사람들이지. 그 사람들이라면 기꺼이 제 딸을, 거기다 제 두 귀까지 얹어서, 저기 저 사람한테 줄 거요. 원숭이에 미라 같은 몰골이긴 해도 말이오.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇소!\" 브레이가 자신을 정당화해 줄 만한 것이라면 뭐든 덥석 붙들며 외쳤어. \"나도 그 애한테 그렇게 말했소, 어젯밤에도 오늘도 말이오.\"\n\n\"바른말을 한 거요.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"잘하셨소. 하지만 동시에 한마디 해야겠는데, 만약 나한테 딸이 있고, 내 자유와 즐거움, 아니 내 건강과 목숨까지도 그 애가 내가 점찍어 준 남편을 받아들이느냐에 달려 있다면, 내 바람에 따르라고 그 애를 설득하느라 다른 말을 더 늘어놓을 필요는 없었으면 좋겠소.\"\n\n브레이는 랄프가 진심으로 하는 말인지 살피려는 듯 그를 쳐다봤어. 그러고는 그 말에 두말없이 동의한다는 듯 두세 번 고개를 끄덕이더니 이렇게 말했어.\n\n\"옷매무새를 마저 다듬으러 잠깐 위층에 올라갔다 와야겠소. 내려올 때 매들린을 데리고 오리다. 그런데 말이오, 어젯밤에 아주 이상한 꿈을 꿨는데, 지금 이 순간까지 까맣게 잊고 있었지 뭐요. 꿈에서 바로 오늘 아침이었고, 당신과 내가 방금처럼 이야기를 나누고 있었소. 그러다 내가 위층으로 올라갔지. 지금 올라가려는 바로 그 일 때문에 말이오. 그런데 매들린의 손을 잡아 데리고 내려오려고 손을 뻗는 순간, 바닥이 나와 함께 푹 꺼지는 거요. 그리고 꿈에서나 그려 볼 수 있을, 상상조차 하기 힘든 어마어마한 높이에서 떨어진 끝에, 무덤 속에 내려앉았지 뭐요.\"\n\n\"그러다 잠에서 깨어 보니 똑바로 누워 있었거나, 머리가 침대 밖으로 늘어져 있었거나, 체해서 어디가 좀 아팠던 거겠지요?\" 랄프가 말했어. \"쳇, 브레이 씨! 나처럼 해 봐요. (이제 끊임없는 즐거움과 기쁨이 당신 앞에 펼쳐질 테니 기회가 있을 거요.) 낮에 좀 더 바쁘게 지내면서, 밤에 꾼 꿈 따위를 생각할 틈을 두지 마시오.\"\n\n랄프는 그가 문까지 가는 모습을 한결같은 눈길로 좇았어. 그러고는 다시 둘만 남게 되자 신랑 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했어.\n\n\"내 말 잘 새겨 두게, 그라이드. 자네는 저 사람한테 연금을 오래 낼 일은 없을 거야. 자네는 흥정에서는 늘 악마 같은 운을 타고났으니 말이야. 저 사람이 몇 달 안에 저세상으로 떠나는 긴 여행 명단에 오르지 않는다면, 내 머리에 오렌지를 얹고 다니겠네!\"\n\n귀에 어찌나 듣기 좋은 예언이었던지, 아서는 무척 기뻐 깔깔대는 것 말고는 아무 대답도 하지 않았어. 랄프가 의자에 털썩 몸을 던지자, 두 사람은 깊은 침묵 속에 앉아 기다렸지. 랄프는 입가에 비웃음을 띤 채, 그날 달라진 브레이의 태도를 생각하고 있었어. 나쁜 음모에 함께 발을 담그자 그자의 자존심이 어찌 그리 금세 꺾이고 둘 사이에 허물없는 사이가 생겨났는지를 말이야. 그런데 그때 귀 기울이고 있던 그의 귀에 계단을 스치는 여자 옷자락 소리와 한 남자의 발소리가 들려왔어.\n\n\"정신 차리게.\" 랄프가 조바심에 발을 쿵 구르며 말했어. \"산 사람처럼 좀 굴어 봐, 이 사람아, 응? 그들이 왔어. 그 메마른 늙은 뼈다귀들을 이리로 좀 끌고 오라고. 빨리, 이 사람아, 빨리!\"\n\n그라이드가 비척비척 앞으로 나와, 히죽거리고 굽실대며 랄프 곁에 바짝 붙어 섰어. 그때 문이 열리고 누군가 황급히 들어왔지 ― 그런데 브레이와 그 딸이 아니라, 니콜라스와 그의 누이 케이트였어.\n\n그림자의 세계에서 어떤 무시무시한 유령이 갑자기 눈앞에 나타났다 해도, 랄프 니클비가 이 뜻밖의 일에 받은 충격만큼 벼락을 맞은 듯하지는 않았을 거야. 두 손이 힘없이 옆구리로 툭 떨어졌고, 그는 비틀비틀 뒷걸음질 쳤지. 그러고는 입을 떡 벌리고 잿빛으로 새하얗게 질린 얼굴로, 말도 못 한 채 분노에 차서 그들을 노려보고 섰어. 두 눈이 어찌나 튀어나오고, 속에서 들끓는 격정에 얼굴이 어찌나 일그러지고 변했던지, 일 분 전만 해도 그였던 그 엄하고 침착하고 굳은 인상의 사내라고는 알아보기 어려울 정도였어.\n\n\"어젯밤에 나한테 찾아온 그놈이에요.\" 그라이드가 그의 팔꿈치를 잡아당기며 속삭였어. \"어젯밤에 나한테 찾아온 그놈!\"\n\n\"알아.\" 랄프가 중얼거렸어. \"안다고! 진작에 짐작했어야 했는데. 내가 가는 길마다, 모퉁이를 돌 때마다, 어딜 가든 무슨 짓을 하든, 저놈이 나타난단 말이야!\"\n\n얼굴에서 핏기가 싹 가신 것, 벌름거리는 콧구멍, 굳게 맞물렸으면서도 가만있지 못하고 떨리는 입술 ― 이런 것들이 니콜라스 안에서 어떤 감정들이 서로 주도권을 다투고 있는지 드러내 줬어. 하지만 그는 그것들을 꾹 눌렀어. 그리고 케이트를 안심시키려 그 팔을 살며시 잡아 주고는, 똑바로 서서 한 점 흔들림 없이, 못난 친척과 정면으로 마주 섰지.\n\n오누이가 나란히, 그들에게 잘 어울리는 늠름한 자태로 서 있으니, 둘 사이의 빼닮은 모습이 또렷이 드러났어. 따로따로 봤더라면 많은 사람들이 알아채지 못했을 그런 닮음이었지. 오빠의 분위기와 몸가짐, 그리고 그 눈빛과 표정까지도 누이에게 고스란히 비쳤어. 다만 여성스러운 섬세함과 매력의 가장 고운 경계까지 부드럽고 우아하게 다듬어져 있었지. 더 놀라운 건, 랄프의 얼굴에도 두 사람을 닮은 무언가가 어렴풋이 서려 있다는 거였어. 오누이가 그 어느 때보다 더 잘생겨 보이고 랄프는 그 어느 때보다 더 추해 보이는 순간, 오누이가 그 어느 때보다 더 당당하게 몸을 세우고 랄프는 그 절반만큼도 못 되게 움츠러든 순간, 바로 그때만큼 이 닮음이 또렷하게 드러난 적은 없었어. 또 사악한 생각들로 거칠고 험악해진 얼굴의 온갖 추한 특징이, 그때 절반만큼이라도 뚜렷하게 나타난 적도 없었지.\n\n\"꺼져!\" 그가 정말로 이를 갈며 처음으로 내뱉을 수 있었던 말이었어. \"꺼지라고! 여긴 무슨 일로 왔어? 거짓말쟁이, 악당, 비겁자, 도둑놈!\"\n\n\"내가 여기 온 건,\" 니콜라스가 낮고 깊은 목소리로 말했어. \"할 수만 있다면 당신 희생자를 구하기 위해서야. 당신이야말로 평생 하는 짓마다 거짓말쟁이에 악당이지. 도둑질은 당신 생업이고. 게다가 오늘 여기 와 있는 걸 보면 갑절로 비겁한 자임에 틀림없어. 모진 말로도 날 움직이지 못해. 모진 매질로도 마찬가지지. 나는 여기 서서, 내 할 일을 끝낼 때까지 떠나지 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"아가씨!\" 랄프가 말했어. \"물러가시지! 저놈한테는 힘을 쓸 수도 있지만, 너한테는 되도록 손대고 싶지 않으니까. 물러가라, 이 가냘프고 어리석은 계집애야. 이 개 같은 놈은 마땅한 대로 처리하게 내버려 두고 말이야.\"\n\n\"물러가지 않겠어요.\" 케이트가 눈을 번뜩이며, 두 볼에 붉은 피가 확 오르며 외쳤어. \"당신이 오빠한테 무슨 해를 끼친들 오빠가 갚지 못할 건 없어요. 나한테는 힘을 쓸 수도 있겠죠. 그러실 것 같네요. 나는 여자니까, 그게 당신한테 딱 어울리는 짓이겠죠. 하지만 나한테 여자의 연약함이 있다면, 여자의 마음도 있어요. 이런 일에서 그 마음을 본래 뜻에서 돌려놓을 수 있는 사람은 당신이 아니에요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 뜻이라는 게 뭔지 들어나 보지, 이 도도하기 짝이 없는 아가씨야?\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"당신의 배신에 희생된 그 불행한 사람한테, 이 마지막 순간에,\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"피난처와 보금자리를 내어 주려는 거야. 당신이 마련한 그런 남편이 코앞에 닥쳤다는 사실로도 그분 마음을 돌릴 수 없다면, 같은 여자의 간청과 호소에는 마음이 움직이기를 바라는 거지. 어찌 됐든 한번 해 볼 거야. 나도 직접, 누구한테서 와서 누구의 부탁을 받고 왔는지 그분 아버지한테 밝히겠어. 그래도 이 결혼을 기어이 밀어붙인다면, 그건 그 사람에게 더 큰 비열함이고 치사함이고 잔인함이 될 테니까. 나는 그분과 그분 따님을 만나려고 여기서 기다리는 거야. 이러려고 내가 왔고, 누이까지 당신 앞에 데려온 거라고. 우리 목적은 당신을 보거나 당신과 이야기하려는 게 아니야. 그러니 당신한테는 더 말하기도 아까워.\"\n\n\"그래?\" 랄프가 말했어. \"기어이 여기 남아 있겠다, 이거지, 아가씨?\"\n\n조카딸의 가슴은 그가 부추겨 놓은 분한 격정으로 들썩였지만, 그녀는 그에게 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았어.\n\n\"자, 그라이드, 여기 좀 보게.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"이놈은 ― 내 형의 아들이라고 말하기도 슬프지만 ― 온갖 비열하고 이기적인 죄로 더럽혀진, 타락하고 방탕한 놈이야. 이놈이 오늘 여기 와서 엄숙한 예식을 방해하는 거지. 이런 때 남의 집에 제 발로 들어와 기어이 버티고 있으면, 거리로 걷어차여 나가 제 꼴에 걸맞은 부랑자처럼 질질 끌려다닐 게 뻔한데도 말이야. 자, 잘 보게, 이놈이 제 누이를 방패 삼아 데려왔어. 우리가 어리석은 계집애를 ― 이놈한테야 새삼스럽지도 않은 ― 그 굴욕과 모욕에 내놓지 못할 거라고 여긴 거지. 게다가 내가 이 애한테 무슨 일이 닥칠지 경고까지 했는데도, 보다시피 이놈은 여태 이 애를 곁에 붙들어 두고, 겁쟁이 사내애가 제 엄마 치맛자락에 매달리듯 이 애의 앞치마 끈에 매달려 있단 말이야. 방금 자네가 들은 것처럼 큰소리치는 놈치고는 참 가관 아닌가?\"\n\n\"어젯밤에 내가 들은 것처럼 말이지요.\" 아서 그라이드가 말했어. \"어젯밤에 이놈이 살금살금 내 집에 기어들었다가 ― 히! 히! 히! ― 내가 죽도록 겁을 줬더니 금세 다시 슬그머니 빠져나갔을 때 말이에요. 그러면서 이놈도 매들린 양과 결혼하고 싶어 한다 이거지요! 아이고 참! 또 뭐 바라는 거 없답니까? 그 여자를 포기하는 거 말고, 우리가 또 뭘 해 주면 될까요? 빚을 갚아 주고, 집에 가구도 들여 주고, 면도라도 한다면 면도지로 쓸 지폐 몇 장 줄까요? 히! 히! 히!\"\n\n\"기어이 남겠다, 아가씨, 그렇지?\" 랄프가 다시 케이트를 돌아보며 말했어. \"여기 버티고 있으면, 술 취한 갈보처럼 계단 아래로 질질 끌려 내려가게 될 거야, 내 맹세코 그렇게 하고 말 테니. 대답이 없네! 그렇게 되면 네 오빠한테나 고마워해라. 그라이드, 브레이를 불러 내려오게 해 ― 딸은 말고. 그 애는 위층에 붙들어 두라고 하게.\"\n\n\"네 머리가 아깝거든,\" 니콜라스가 문 앞에 자리를 잡고 서서, 아까처럼 낮은 목소리로, 아까 드러낸 것 이상으로 겉으로 격정을 드러내지 않고 말했어. \"있는 자리에 그대로 있어!\"\n\n\"저놈 말고 내 말을 들어, 브레이를 불러 내려오게 하라고.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"우리 둘 중 누구 말도 듣지 말고 네 한 몸이나 챙겨. 그러니 있는 자리에 그대로 있어!\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"브레이를 불러 내려오게 할 텐가?\" 랄프가 외쳤어.\n\n\"내게 다가오면 무슨 일을 당할지 명심하라고.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n그라이드가 망설였어. 이때쯤 랄프는 함정에 빠진 호랑이처럼 사나워져서, 문 쪽으로 돌진했지. 그러고는 케이트를 지나치려다 그 팔을 손으로 거칠게 움켜쥐었어. 니콜라스가 두 눈에 불을 뿜으며 그의 멱살을 잡았지. 바로 그 순간, 위층에서 묵직한 것이 엄청난 소리를 내며 바닥에 쿵 쓰러졌어. 그리고 곧이어, 더없이 소름 끼치고 끔찍한 비명이 들려왔지.\n\n그들은 모두 멈춰 서서 서로를 바라봤어. 비명에 비명이 이어졌지. 뒤이어 우당탕탕 요란한 발소리가 들렸어. 그리고 여러 날카로운 목소리가 한꺼번에 아우성치며 외치는 게 들렸어. \"돌아가셨어!\"\n\n\"비켜!\" 니콜라스가 그때껏 억눌렀던 격정을 죄다 터뜨리며 외쳤어. \"내가 감히 바라기도 두려웠던 그 일이라면, 이 악당들, 너희는 너희가 놓은 덫에 걸린 거다.\"\n\n그는 방을 박차고 나가, 소리가 난 쪽을 향해 위층으로 내달렸어. 그리고 자그마한 침실을 꽉 메운 사람들을 헤치고 들어갔지. 거기엔 브레이가 바닥에 쓰러져 완전히 숨이 끊어진 채 누워 있었어. 그의 딸은 그 주검에 매달려 있었지.\n\n\"어쩌다 이렇게 됐습니까?\" 그가 사방을 미친 듯이 둘러보며 외쳤어.\n\n여러 목소리가 한꺼번에 대답했어. 반쯤 열린 문틈으로 보니 그가 이상하고 불편한 자세로 의자에 기대 있더라고. 몇 번이나 말을 걸어도 대답이 없어서 잠든 줄로만 알았는데, 누군가 들어가 팔을 흔들었더니 바닥으로 쿵 쓰러졌고, 그제야 숨이 끊어진 걸 알았다는 거야.\n\n\"이 집 주인이 누굽니까?\" 니콜라스가 다급히 말했어.\n\n사람들이 한 나이 든 여자를 가리켰어. 니콜라스는 무릎을 꿇고, 매들린의 두 팔을 그것이 휘감고 있던 싸늘한 주검에서 살며시 풀어내면서 그 여자한테 말했어. \"여기 이 아가씨의 하녀도 알다시피, 저는 이 아가씨의 가장 가까운 친구들을 대신해서 온 사람입니다. 이 끔찍한 자리에서 아가씨를 데려가야겠어요. 여기 이 사람은 제 누이로, 아가씨를 맡길 사람입니다. 제 이름과 주소는 저 명함에 적혀 있어요. 처리해야 할 일들에 필요한 지시는 제가 다 알려 드리겠습니다. 다들 비켜 주세요, 제발 제가 움직일 자리와 숨 쉴 틈을 좀 주세요!\"\n\n사람들이 뒤로 물러섰어. 방금 일어난 일 못지않게, 말하는 그 사람의 격앙되고 거센 기세에 놀란 거였지. 니콜라스는 정신을 잃은 아가씨를 두 팔로 안아 들고, 방에서 나와 방금 떠나온 아래층 방으로 데려갔어. 누이와 충직한 하녀가 그 뒤를 따랐지. 그는 하녀한테 당장 마차를 구해 오라고 일렀어. 그러는 동안 그와 케이트는 그 아름다운 아가씨 위로 몸을 숙이고 정신을 차리게 하려 애썼지만, 헛수고였지. 하녀가 어찌나 잽싸게 일을 해냈던지, 몇 분도 안 돼 마차가 준비됐어.\n\n랄프 니클비와 그라이드는 자신들의 계획을 그토록 갑작스레 뒤엎어 버린 그 끔찍한 사건에 충격받아 마비된 듯했어. (그렇지 않았다면 아마 그 일이 그들에게 그리 큰 충격을 주지는 못했겠지.) 그리고 앞을 가로막는 모든 걸 휩쓸어 버리는 니콜라스의 엄청난 기세와 다급함에 휩쓸려, 두 사람은 꿈을 꾸거나 넋이 나간 사람처럼 이 광경을 멍하니 바라보고 있었어. 매들린을 당장 데려갈 채비가 다 끝나고 나서야, 랄프가 침묵을 깨고 그 애를 데려가서는 안 된다고 선언했지.\n\n\"누가 그래?\" 니콜라스가 무릎을 펴고 일어나 그들과 맞서며 외쳤어. 그러면서도 매들린의 싸늘한 손은 여전히 제 손에 쥐고 있었지.\n\n\"내가!\" 랄프가 쉰 목소리로 대답했어.\n\n\"쉿, 쉿!\" 겁에 질린 그라이드가 다시 그의 팔을 붙잡으며 외쳤어. \"저놈이 하는 말을 들어 봐요.\"\n\n\"그래!\" 니콜라스가 잡히지 않은 한 손을 허공에 뻗으며 말했어. \"저 사람이 하는 말을 들어 봐. 당신들 두 사람의 빚이 다 자연이라는 그 큰 빚 하나로 갚아졌다고. 오늘 열두 시가 만기인 그 차용증은 이제 휴지 조각이 됐다고. 당신들이 꾸민 사기는 끝내 들통날 거라고. 당신들 계략은 세상에 다 알려졌고, 하늘이 그걸 뒤엎어 버렸다고. 이 비열한 자들아, 저 사람은 너희 둘한테 할 테면 어디 마음껏 해 보라고 도전하는 거야.\"\n\n\"이 사람이,\" 랄프가 거의 알아듣기 힘든 목소리로 말했어. \"이 사람이 자기 아내를 데려가겠다는데, 데려가게 둬야지.\"\n\n\"저 사람은 제 것도 아닌 걸 데려가겠다는 거고, 설령 쉰 사람이고 거기다 뒤를 받쳐 줄 쉰 사람이 더 있다 해도 데려가지 못해.\" 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"누가 못 데려가게 한다는 거냐?\"\n\n\"내가.\"\n\n\"무슨 권리로 그러는지 한번 들어 보자.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"무슨 권리로 그러느냐 말이다.\"\n\n\"이런 권리로. 내가 아는 사실이 있는 한, 당신은 감히 나를 더는 떠보지 못할 거야.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그리고 이 더 떳떳한 권리로. 내가 섬기는 분들, 당신이 나를 비열하게 속이고 해치려 들었던 그분들이야말로 이 아가씨의 가장 가깝고 가장 소중한 친구들이거든. 그분들의 이름으로 내가 아가씨를 여기서 데려가는 거야. 비켜!\"\n\n\"한마디만!\" 랄프가 입에 거품을 물고 외쳤어.\n\n\"한마디도 안 돼.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"한마디도 듣지 않겠어 ― 이 말만 빼고. 당신 앞가림이나 잘하고, 내가 하는 이 경고를 새겨들어! 당신 세상은 다 갔고, 밤이 다가오고 있으니까.\"\n\n\"내 저주를, 모질고 지독한 저주를, 네놈한테 퍼붓는다, 이놈!\"\n\n\"당신이 명령한다고 저주가 어디서 떨어지기라도 한답디까? 아니, 당신 같은 사람한테서 나온 저주든 축복이든 무슨 소용이 있겠어? 똑똑히 말해 두지. 불운과 파멸이 당신 머리 위로 점점 짙게 몰려들고 있어. 당신이 그 못된 평생에 걸쳐 쌓아 올린 그 모든 것들이 먼지로 부서지고 있다고. 당신이 가는 길에는 첩자들이 깔려 있고. 바로 오늘, 당신이 쌓아 둔 재산 중 만 파운드가 단번에 와르르 날아갔단 말이야!\"\n\n\"거짓말이다!\" 랄프가 움찔 뒷걸음질 치며 외쳤어.\n\n\"사실이야, 곧 그렇다는 걸 알게 될 거야. 더는 입 아프게 떠들 말도 없어. 문에서 비켜. 케이트, 네가 먼저 가. 이 아가씨한테든, 저 여자한테든, 나한테든 손 하나 대지 마. 지나갈 때 옷자락 하나 스치지도 말라고! ― 비켜서 지나가게 두나 했더니, 저자가 또 문을 막아서는군!\"\n\n아서 그라이드가 마침 문간에 서 있었어. 일부러 그런 건지 정신이 없어서 그런 건지는 분명치 않았지. 니콜라스가 그를 어찌나 거세게 밀쳐 냈던지, 그는 방 안을 빙그르르 돌다가 벽 모서리에 부딪혀 그대로 쿵 쓰러졌어. 그러고 나서 니콜라스는 그 아름다운 짐을 두 팔에 안고 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔지. 막으려는 사람이 있었다 해도, 아무도 그를 막으려 들지 않았어. 그 일의 소문을 듣고 집 주위에 몰려든 사람들 틈을 헤치고, 흥분한 나머지 매들린을 갓난아기라도 되는 양 가뿐히 안은 채, 그는 케이트와 하녀가 이미 기다리고 있는 마차에 다다랐어. 그러고는 자기가 안고 온 아가씨를 두 사람한테 맡기고, 마부 옆자리로 훌쩍 뛰어올라 어서 출발하라고 일렀어.\n\n# CHAPTER 55\n\n니클비 부인은 아들과 딸한테서 매들린 브레이의 사연 가운데 그들이 아는 건 빠짐없이 다 들어 두었어. 니콜라스가 떠맡게 된 막중한 처지도 차근차근 설명을 들었지. 그 아가씨를 자기 집에 들여야 할지도 모른다는, 일이 벌어지기 몇 분 전만 해도 도무지 그럴 성싶지 않던 일까지도 마음의 준비를 해 둔 참이었어. 그런데도 니클비 부인은 전날 밤 늦게 처음 이 비밀을 털어놓고 들은 순간부터, 영 개운치 않고 깊은 안갯속에 빠진 듯한 상태에서 헤어나지를 못했어. 아무리 설명을 하고 따져 줘도 풀어 줄 수가 없었고, 혼잣말과 곱씹는 생각이 새로 떠오를 때마다 점점 더 심해지기만 했지.\n\n\"아이고, 케이트야!\" 이 착한 부인은 이렇게 따져 들었어. \"치어리블 형제분이 이 아가씨가 결혼하는 걸 원치 않는다면, 어째서 대법관한테 소송을 걸어서 아가씨를 법정 후견 대상으로 만들고, 안전하게 플리트 감옥에 가둬 두지 않는 거니? 그런 얘기라면 신문에서 수백 번도 더 읽었다. 아니면, 니콜라스 말처럼 그분들이 아가씨를 그렇게나 끔찍이 아낀다면, 어째서 직접 결혼하지 않는 거야? 둘 중 한 분이 말이다. 그리고 설령 아가씨가 결혼하는 것도 원치 않고, 직접 결혼할 마음도 없다 쳐도, 도대체 어쩌자고 니콜라스가 온 세상을 돌아다니면서 남의 혼인을 막고 다닌단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"엄마가 잘 이해를 못 하시는 것 같아요.\" 케이트가 부드럽게 말했어.\n\n\"어머나 케이트야, 너 정말 말 한번 곱게 한다!\" 니클비 부인이 받아쳤어. \"나도 결혼이란 걸 해 봤고, 다른 사람들 결혼하는 것도 봐 왔다. 이해를 못 한다니, 원!\"\n\n\"엄마가 경험이 많으신 건 저도 알아요.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"제 말은 이번 일의 사정을 다 이해하지 못하실 수도 있다는 거예요. 저희가 어설프게 말씀드린 탓일 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래, 너희가 그랬겠지.\" 어머니가 톡 쏘아붙였어. \"충분히 그럴 만하지. 그게 내 탓은 아니잖니. 그래도 동시에, 사정이 다 뻔히 보이니까, 얘야, 내가 한마디 해야겠다. 나는 그 사정을 이해한다, 그것도 아주 똑똑히 말이다. 너랑 니콜라스가 아니라고 생각하든 말든 상관없어. 이 매그덜린 아가씨가 자기보다 나이 많은 사람하고 결혼한다고 해서 어째서 이렇게 야단법석을 떠는 거니? 너희 가엾은 아버지도 나보다 나이가 많았다, 네 살 반이나 위였어. 제인 디뱁스 말이다. 디뱁스 집안은 그 예쁘장한 단층 초가집에 살았지. 온통 담쟁이며 덩굴풀로 뒤덮이고, 인동덩굴이 휘감긴 아담한 현관이 딸려 있던 집 말이다. 여름 저녁이면 집게벌레가 찻잔에 툭툭 떨어져서는, 늘 등을 대고 자빠진 채 다리를 버둥거리며 발버둥을 쳤고, 밤새 묵을라치면 개구리가 골풀 등잔갓 속으로 기어들어 가서 떡하니 앉아서는 작은 구멍으로 사람처럼 내다보던 그 집 말이야. 그 제인 디뱁스가, 자기보다 한참 나이 많은 남자랑 결혼했어. 아무리 말려도 기어이 그 사람하고 결혼하겠다고 했지. 어찌나 그 남자를 끔찍이 아끼던지 견줄 데가 없을 정도였다. 제인 디뱁스 때는 아무도 야단법석을 안 떨었어. 게다가 그 남편은 더없이 점잖고 훌륭한 사람이라 누구나 좋게 말했지. 그런데 어째서 이 매그덜린 일로는 이렇게 호들갑을 떠는 거냐?\"\n\n\"그 아가씨 남편은 훨씬 나이가 많은 데다, 아가씨가 직접 고른 사람도 아니에요. 그 사람 성품도 방금 엄마가 말씀하신 거랑은 정반대고요. 두 경우가 확연히 다른 게 안 보이세요?\" 케이트가 말했어.\n\n이 말에 니클비 부인은 그저 이렇게 대꾸할 뿐이었어. 자기가 어지간히 멍청한 모양이다, 정말 의심할 여지가 없다, 제 자식들마저 날마다 거의 대놓고 그렇게 말하지 않느냐고 말이야. 하긴 자기가 저희보다 나이가 좀 많고, 어쩌면 어리석은 사람들은 그러니 당연히 자기가 제일 잘 알아야 한다고 여길지도 모르지만. 그래도 자기가 틀렸겠지, 당연히 틀렸을 거다, 늘 틀리니까, 옳을 리가 없고, 옳기를 바랄 수도 없다고. 그러니 더는 망신을 사지 않는 게 낫겠다는 거였어. 그러고는 그 뒤로 한 시간 동안 케이트가 아무리 달래고 양보해도, 착한 부인은 다른 대꾸 없이 그저 이렇게만 했어. 아이고, 그럼 그렇지, 어째서 나한테 묻는 거니, 내 의견이야 아무 소용도 없는데, 내가 무슨 말을 하든 상관없잖니, 하고 똑같은 식의 대꾸만 잔뜩 늘어놓는 거야.\n\n이런 마음 상태로 (말하기도 너무 단념한 지경이 되자, 고개를 끄덕이고 눈을 치뜨고 끙 하는 신음을 막 시작하다가, 남이 알아채면 얼른 짧은 헛기침으로 바꾸는 식으로 표현하면서) 니클비 부인은 니콜라스와 케이트가 걱정하던 그 아가씨를 데리고 돌아올 때까지 그러고 있었어. 그러다 이제는 자기 존재가 중요하다는 걸 확인한 데다, 그토록 젊고 아름다운 사람이 겪은 시련에 마음이 쓰이게 되니, 부인은 더없는 열성과 정성을 쏟았을 뿐 아니라, 아들이 택한 그 처사를 자기가 추천했노라며 큰 공을 자기 몫으로 돌렸어. 그러고는 의미심장한 표정으로 자주 이렇게 말하곤 했지. 일이 지금처럼 된 게 정말 다행이다, 자기가 크게 북돋고 지혜를 보태지 않았더라면 결코 여기까지 오지 못했을 거라고 넌지시 비치면서 말이야.\n\n니클비 부인이 일을 이렇게 끌어오는 데 정말 큰 몫을 했는지 안 했는지는 굳이 따지지 않더라도, 부인이 우쭐할 만한 확실한 근거가 있었던 건 틀림없어. 형제분은 돌아와서 니콜라스가 맡았던 역할을 어찌나 칭찬했던지, 게다가 상황이 달라지고 그 젊은 친구가 그토록 큰 시련과 위협스러운 위험에서 벗어난 데 어찌나 기뻐했던지, 부인은 딸에게 한두 번이 아니게 일러 주었듯이 이제 집안의 앞날이 \"거의 다 된 거나 마찬가지\"라고 여기게 되었지. 사실 찰스 치어리블 씨가 놀라움과 기쁨이 처음 북받쳐 오를 때 \"거의 다 된 거나 마찬가지\"라고 말씀하셨다고, 니클비 부인은 딱 잘라 주장했어. 이 단서가 정확히 무슨 뜻인지는 똑똑히 설명하지 못하면서도, 부인은 그 얘기만 나오면 그렇게 알 듯 말 듯 거드름 피우는 상태로 가라앉았어. 그리고 앞날에 펼쳐질 부와 위엄의 환상이 어찌나 어른거리던지 (희미하고 안개에 싸인 것이긴 했지만), 그럴 때면 정말로 으리으리한 규모로 평생 먹고살 길이 마련되기라도 한 것처럼 그만큼이나 행복해했지.\n\n부인이 받은 그 갑작스럽고도 끔찍한 충격에, 오랫동안 견뎌 온 크나큰 고통과 마음의 불안까지 겹치니, 매들린의 기력으로는 도저히 감당이 안 됐어. 아버지의 갑작스러운 죽음으로 다행히 빠져들었던 멍한 상태에서 깨어나기는 했지만, 그저 그 상태가 위태롭고 격렬한 병으로 바뀌었을 뿐이었지. 정신의 기운을 무리하게 쥐어짜고 결코 무너지지 않겠다는 굳은 결심으로 버텨 온 연약한 몸의 힘이 마침내 꺾일 때, 그 쇠약해지는 정도는 대개 그동안 그 몸을 떠받쳐 온 그 안간힘의 크기에 비례하는 법이야. 그래서 매들린에게 닥친 병은 가볍거나 잠깐 스쳐 가는 게 아니라, 한동안 그녀의 정신을 위협하고, 또 그에 못지않게 끔찍하게도 목숨 그 자체까지 위협하는 병이었어.\n\n그토록 모질고 위태로운 병에서 더디게 회복해 가는 사람이라면, 누군들 케이트처럼 다정하고 따뜻하고 진심 어린 간병인의 끊임없는 보살핌에 마음이 움직이지 않을 수 있겠어? 그 곱고 부드러운 목소리, 가벼운 발걸음, 섬세한 손길, 우리가 아플 때면 그토록 깊이 느끼다가 다 나으면 그토록 쉽게 잊어버리는 그 수천 가지 자잘한 친절과 위안을, 조용하고 밝고 소리 없이 베풀어 주는 그 손길이 마음 깊이 새겨지지 않을 사람이 어디 있겠어? 더구나 여자들이 소중히 여기는 온갖 순수하고 참된 애정으로 가득 찬, 그리고 같은 여자한테서 받는 다정함과 헌신이라곤 거의 모르고 자란 — 스스로 익힌 것 말고는 — 게다가 재앙과 고통을 겪으며, 그토록 오래 알지 못하고 그토록 오래 헛되이 찾아 헤매던 그 동정에 절절히 사무치게 된 젊은 마음이라면 더더욱 그렇지. 날들이 세월처럼 쌓이며 두 사람을 하나로 엮어 간들 무슨 이상할 게 있겠어! 건강이 돌아오는 시간마다, 케이트가 옛 장면을 떠올리며 — 이제는 옛일처럼, 몇 해 전에 있었던 일처럼 느껴졌지 — 제 오빠한테 아낌없이 늘어놓던 그 칭찬을, 매들린이 점점 더 강하고 달콤하게 받아들이게 된들 무슨 이상할 게 있겠어! 그 칭찬이 매들린의 가슴에 곧바로 메아리쳤다 한들, 또 케이트의 얼굴에 늘 니콜라스의 모습이 어른거려서 두 사람을 좀처럼 떼어 놓고 생각할 수 없게 된 나머지, 처음에 둘이 각각 불러일으켰던 그 감정을 누구한테 줘야 할지 똑같이 헷갈려 하다가, 케이트한테 품었던 그 더 따스한 감정의 얼마쯤을 니콜라스를 향한 고마움에 자기도 모르게 섞어 버렸다 한들, 그게 무슨 이상할 게 있었겠어?\n\n\"얘야,\" 니클비 부인은 방으로 들어오며 이렇게 말하곤 했어. 환자의 신경을 거스르지 않으려고 어찌나 공들여 조심하는지, 차라리 전속력으로 달려드는 기병이 들이닥치는 편이 더 나을 지경이었지. \"오늘 밤은 좀 어떠니? 한결 나았으면 좋겠구나.\"\n\n\"거의 다 나았어요, 엄마.\" 케이트는 하던 일감을 내려놓고 매들린의 손을 제 손에 쥐며 대답하곤 했어.\n\n\"케이트!\" 니클비 부인이 나무라듯 말하곤 했지. \"그렇게 큰 소리로 말하지 마라.\" (정작 이 착한 부인은, 아무리 담대한 남자라도 핏줄의 피가 얼어붙을 만큼 으스스한 속삭임으로 말하면서 말이야.)\n\n케이트는 이 꾸지람을 아주 잠자코 받아들이곤 했어. 그러면 니클비 부인은 살금살금 움직인답시고 마룻장이란 마룻장은 다 삐걱거리게 하고 실오라기 하나까지 바스락거리게 하면서 이렇게 덧붙이곤 했지.\n\n\"내 아들 니콜라스가 방금 집에 왔단다. 그래서 늘 하던 대로, 얘야, 네 입으로 직접 네가 정확히 어떤지 들으러 왔어. 그 애가 내 말은 통 안 믿고, 앞으로도 절대 안 믿을 테니까.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤은 평소보다 늦으셨네요.\" 매들린이 그렇게 대꾸하기도 했어. \"거의 반 시간이나요.\"\n\n\"세상에, 시간 따지는 데는 여기 너희 같은 사람들을 내 평생 본 적이 없다!\" 니클비 부인은 잔뜩 놀라서 외치곤 했지. \"정말이지 처음이야! 니콜라스가 늦었다는 건 나는 손톱만큼도 몰랐다, 눈곱만큼도. 니클비 씨가 늘 말했지, 네 가엾은 아버지 말이다, 케이트야, 식욕이야말로 세상에서 제일 좋은 시계라고 말이다. 그런데 너는 식욕이 없구나, 브레이 양, 좀 있었으면 좋으련만, 정말이지 식욕이 생길 만한 걸 좀 들어야 할 것 같다. 나도 잘은 모르지만, 토종 바닷가재를 두세 다스쯤 먹으면 식욕이 돈다고 들었다. 하긴 그것도 결국 같은 얘기지, 가재를 먹으려면 먼저 식욕이 있어야 할 테니까. 가재라고 했지만 굴 말이었어, 뭐 다 똑같지만. 그건 그렇고, 정말 너는 어떻게 니콜라스 일을 알았—\"\n\n\"마침 그 애 얘기를 하던 참이었어요, 엄마. 그래서요.\"\n\n\"내 보기엔 너는 늘 다른 얘기는 안 하는 것 같더라, 케이트야. 정말이지 네가 그렇게 생각이 없는 데는 놀랄 따름이다. 가끔은 얘깃거리를 얼마든지 찾아내면서, 브레이 양의 기운을 북돋고 흥미를 끌어 주는 게 얼마나 중요한지 뻔히 알면서, 어쩌면 그렇게 노상 똑같은 얘기만 주절주절, 주절주절, 시끌시끌, 시끌시끌 끝없이 늘어놓는지 정말 알다가도 모를 일이다. 너는 아주 친절한 간병인이고, 아주 솜씨도 좋고, 마음씨도 곱다는 건 안다. 그래도 이 말은 해야겠다. 내가 없었으면 브레이 양의 기운이 어떻게 됐을지 정말 모르겠다고 말이야. 그래서 의사 선생한테도 날마다 그렇게 말한단다. 선생은 내가 어떻게 내 기운을 그렇게 잘 챙기는지 신기해하던데, 나도 정말 어떻게 이렇게 버텨 내는지 스스로도 자주 신기할 지경이다. 물론 힘든 일이지, 그래도 이 집에서 나한테 얼마나 많은 게 달려 있는지 아니까, 안 할 수가 없는 거야. 칭찬받을 일은 아니지만, 꼭 필요한 일이니까 하는 거지.\"\n\n그러고 나서 니클비 부인은 의자를 끌어다 앉아서는, 한 사십오 분 동안 정신 사나운 온갖 화제를 더없이 정신 사나운 방식으로 줄줄이 늘어놓곤 했어. 그러다 마침내, 이제 니콜라스가 저녁을 먹는 동안 즐겁게 해 줘야 한다는 핑계로 자리를 떴지. 우선 환자가 분명히 더 나빠졌다는 소식으로 아들 기운을 한 번 북돋워 준 다음, 케이트가 어리석게도 오빠 얘기랑 집안일 말고는 통 다른 얘기를 안 하는 통에 브레이 양이 얼마나 따분하고 맥없고 풀 죽어 있는지 늘어놓으며 한층 더 기운을 차리게 해 주곤 했어. 이런저런 신나는 말로 니콜라스를 완전히 편안하게 만들어 준 다음, 그날 자기가 해낸 고된 일들을 길게 늘어놓곤 했지. 그러다 간혹, 혹시라도 자기한테 무슨 일이 생기면 이 집안이 자기 없이 어떻게 꾸려 갈까 싶어 눈물을 흘리기까지 했어.\n\n또 어떤 때는 니콜라스가 밤에 집에 올 때 프랭크 치어리블 씨를 데려오기도 했어. 그날 저녁 매들린이 어떤지 알아보라고 형제분이 보낸 거였지. 그럴 때면 (그런 일은 아주 자주 있었어) 니클비 부인은 정신을 바짝 차리는 게 특히 중요하다고 여겼어. 부인의 눈길을 끈 어떤 낌새와 조짐들로 미루어, 프랭크 씨가 삼촌들만큼이나 매들린한테 마음을 쓰는 양하지만 사실은 그 안부를 묻는 것 못지않게 케이트를 보러 오는 거라고 눈치 빠르게 짐작했거든. 더구나 형제분은 의사와 늘 연락을 주고받았고, 본인들도 아주 자주 들락거렸으며, 매일 아침 니콜라스한테서 자세한 보고를 받았으니 말이야. 니클비 부인한테는 이게 으쓱한 시절이었어. 부인만큼 신중하고 사려 깊은 사람도, 또 부인만큼 알 듯 말 듯 속을 안 내비치는 사람도 없었지. 그리고 자기 의심이 맞는지 알아내려고, 또 맞다면 프랭크 씨를 애간장 태워서 자기한테 속내를 털어놓고 자비를 빌게 만들려고 그한테 펼친 만큼 교묘한 작전과 헤아릴 수 없는 책략도 없었어. 이 거창한 계획을 밀어붙이려고 니클비 부인이 동원한 대포는, 무거운 것이며 가벼운 것이며 그 규모가 어마어마했어. 마음먹은 목적을 이루려고 쓴 수단도 가지각색이고 서로 정반대였지. 어떤 때는 온통 살갑고 편안하게 굴다가, 또 어떤 때는 온통 뻣뻣하고 쌀쌀맞게 굴었어. 어느 때는 그 가엾은 희생자한테 마음을 송두리째 열어 보이는 듯하다가도, 다음번에 만날 때는 더없이 서먹하고 짐짓 꾸민 듯한 태도로 맞이하곤 했지. 마치 무슨 새로운 깨달음이 번뜩 떠올라서 그의 속셈을 알아채고는, 그 싹을 미리 잘라 버리기로 작정한 듯이 말이야. 마치 스파르타인 같은 단호함으로 행동해서, 결코 이뤄질 수 없는 희망을 단번에 영원히 꺾어 버리는 게 자기의 마땅한 도리라고 느끼는 것처럼. 또 어떤 때는, 니콜라스가 엿들을 데 없고 케이트가 위층에서 아픈 친구를 부지런히 돌보고 있을 적이면, 이 착한 부인은 딸을 프랑스로 서너 해 보내겠다거나, 요즘 피로로 상한 건강을 다스리러 스코틀랜드로 보내겠다거나, 미국으로 방문을 보내겠다거나, 아무튼 길고 지루한 이별이 닥칠 만한 데로 보내겠다는 둥 알 수 없는 암시를 흘리곤 했어. 아니, 심지어 어렴풋이 이런 암시까지 했어. 옛 이웃의 아들 하나가 — 호레이쇼 펠티로거스라는 (그때 한 네 살쯤 됐을 법한 어린 도령이었지) — 제 딸한테 마음을 두고 있다고 말이야. 게다가 그건 두 집안 사이에 거의 다 정해진 일이나 마찬가지고, 딸의 최종 결정만 기다리면 교회의 축복 속에 성사되어 모든 식구가 이루 말할 수 없이 행복하고 흡족해질 거라는 식으로 떠벌리기까지 했어.\n\n어느 날 밤, 이 마지막 지뢰를 어찌나 멋들어지게 터뜨렸던지 그 우쭐함과 의기양양함이 한껏 부풀어 오른 김에, 니클비 부인은 잠자리에 들기 전 아들과 단둘이 남게 된 틈을 타서 줄곧 머릿속을 차지하던 그 화제로 아들의 속을 떠보았어. 이 일에 대해서야 둘의 의견이 다를 리 없다고 철석같이 믿으면서 말이야. 그래서 부인은 프랭크 치어리블 씨가 두루 얼마나 상냥한지 칭찬하는 적절한 말부터 이런저런 꺼내며 그 문제에 다가갔어.\n\n\"엄마 말씀이 옳아요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"정말 옳고말고요. 그 사람 멋진 친구죠.\"\n\n\"잘생기기도 했고.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어.\n\n\"확실히 잘생겼죠.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어.\n\n\"얘야, 그 사람 코는 뭐라고 해야 하니?\" 니클비 부인은 어떻게든 니콜라스가 이 화제에 흠뻑 빠져들게 하려고 계속 물었어.\n\n\"뭐라고 하다니요?\" 니콜라스가 되물었어.\n\n\"아!\" 어머니가 말을 받았어. \"어떤 모양의 코냐 말이다. 말하자면 어떤 양식의 건축이냐고. 내가 코에 대해선 그리 밝지 못해서. 로마식이라고 해야 하니, 그리스식이라고 해야 하니?\"\n\n\"맙소사, 엄마.\" 니콜라스가 웃으며 말했어. \"내 기억으로는, 일종의 혼합식이라고나, 뒤섞인 코라고나 해야 할 것 같은데요. 그런데 그 점에 대해선 그리 또렷한 기억이 없네요. 엄마가 그렇게 궁금하시다면, 좀 더 눈여겨봤다가 알려 드릴게요.\"\n\n\"그래 주면 좋겠구나, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 진지한 표정으로 말했어.\n\n\"좋아요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그럴게요.\"\n\n대화가 여기까지 이르자, 니콜라스는 읽던 책으로 다시 눈을 돌렸어. 니클비 부인은 잠깐 멈춰 생각하더니 다시 말을 이었지.\n\n\"그 사람은 너를 무척 따른단다, 니콜라스.\"\n\n니콜라스는 책을 덮으며 웃는 얼굴로, 그 말을 들으니 기쁘다고 했어. 그러고는 엄마가 벌써 그 새 친구의 속을 깊이 꿰뚫고 계신 모양이라고 한마디 했지.\n\n\"흠!\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"그건 모르겠다만, 얘야, 누군가는 그 사람 속을 알아 둘 필요가 꼭 있다고 생각한다. 아주 꼭 필요한 일이지.\"\n\n아들이 보내는 호기심 어린 눈빛에, 또 자기 혼자만 큰 비밀을 쥐고 있다는 뿌듯함에 우쭐해진 니클비 부인은 잔뜩 신이 나서 말을 이었어.\n\n\"정말이지, 니콜라스, 네가 어떻게 그걸 못 알아챘는지 나는 도무지 이해가 안 간다. 하긴 내가 왜 그런 말을 하는지도 모르겠다만, 물론 어느 정도까지는, 또 어느 선까지는, 이런 일에는, 특히 이런 초기 단계에는, 여자들 눈에는 아무리 뻔히 보여도 남자들 눈에는 그렇게까지 환히 보이기를 바라기 어려운 구석이 많은 법이거든. 내가 이런 일에 무슨 특별한 통찰력이 있다는 말은 아니야. 있을 수도 있지. 그건 내 곁에 있는 사람들이 제일 잘 알 테고, 아마 알고들 있겠지. 그 점에 대해선 내 의견을 말하지 않으련다. 내가 그러는 건 어울리지 않으니까, 어림도 없는 일이지, 어림도 없어.\"\n\n니콜라스는 촛불 심지를 다듬고, 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고는, 의자에 등을 기대고 꾹 참고 견디는 듯한, 우울하게 단념한 듯한 표정을 지었어.\n\n\"내가 아는 걸 너한테 말해 주는 게 도리라고 생각한다, 니콜라스.\" 어머니가 다시 말을 이었어. \"너도 그걸 알 권리가 있고, 이 집안에서 일어나는 일은 죄다 알 권리가 있어서만이 아니라, 네가 이 일을 크게 거들고 도울 수 있는 처지여서 그래. 그리고 이런 일은 빨리 분명하게 매듭짓는 게 어느 모로 보나 언제나 더 낫다는 데는 의심의 여지가 없거든. 네가 할 수 있는 일이 아주 많단다. 이를테면 가끔 정원을 거닌다거나, 잠시 위층 네 방에 앉아 있는다거나, 이따금 잠든 척한다거나, 무슨 볼일이 생각난 척하고 한 시간쯤 나갔다 오는 거지, 스마이크 씨를 데리고 말이야. 이게 아주 사소한 일처럼 보일 거다. 내가 이런 걸 그렇게 대단하게 여기니 너는 우습겠지. 그래도 얘야, 내 장담하건대 (그리고 너도 언젠가는 직접 알게 될 거다, 니콜라스, 네가 누군가를 사랑하게 되면 말이야. 나는 네가 꼭 그러기를 바라고 또 그러리라 믿는다. 물론 점잖고 행실 바른 여자라야지, 그렇지 않은 여자를 사랑하는 일이야 너로선 꿈에도 없을 테고) 이 사소한 일들에 네가 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 게 달려 있단다. 네 가엾은 아버지가 살아 있었다면, 두 사람을 단둘이 있게 두는 게 얼마나 중요한지 일러 줬을 거다. 물론 일부러 그러는 양 작정하고 방을 나가서는 안 되고, 아주 우연인 척해야 하고, 돌아올 때도 똑같이 해야 해. 문을 열기 전에 복도에서 헛기침을 한다거나, 무심한 척 휘파람을 분다거나, 콧노래를 흥얼거린다거나, 뭐 그런 식으로 네가 온다는 걸 알려 주면 늘 더 낫지. 왜냐하면, 물론 그건 자연스러울 뿐 아니라 그런 상황에선 더없이 옳고 마땅한 일이긴 해도, 젊은 사람들이 한창—한창 소파에 앉아서—그런 온갖 일을 하고 있을 때 끼어들면 아주 난처해지거든. 어쩌면 참 쓸데없는 짓이겠지만, 그래도 젊은 애들은 그러는 법이니까.\"\n\n이 긴 연설 내내 아들이 어머니를 바라보던 그 깊은 놀라움은, 연설이 절정으로 치달을수록 점점 커져만 갔어. 그런데도 니클비 부인은 조금도 당황하기는커녕, 오히려 제 영리함에 대한 자부심만 한껏 부풀어 올랐지. 그래서 부인은 흡족한 얼굴로, 네가 놀랄 줄 진작 알았다고 한마디 던져 두고는, 유난히 두서없고 종잡을 수 없는 정황 증거를 어마어마하게 늘어놓기 시작했어. 그 결론인즉, 프랭크 치어리블 씨가 케이트한테 홀딱 빠져 버렸다는 사실을 의심의 여지 없이 못 박는 거였지.\n\n\"누구한테요?\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어.\n\n니클비 부인은 케이트한테라고 다시 말했어.\n\n\"뭐라고요! 우리 케이트요! 제 누이동생이요!\"\n\n\"세상에, 니콜라스!\" 니클비 부인이 받아쳤어. \"우리 케이트가 아니면 누구네 케이트겠니. 게다가 그게 네 누이동생이 아니라 딴 사람이었다면 내가 왜 신경을 쓰고 관심을 두겠어?\"\n\n\"엄마.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"설마 그럴 리가요!\"\n\n\"좋아, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 자신만만하게 대답했어. \"두고 보면 알 거다.\"\n\n니콜라스는 그 순간까지 지금 들은 그런 일이 일어날 가능성이라곤 털끝만큼도 생각해 본 적이 없었어. 요즘 집을 비우는 일이 잦았고 다른 일들에 푹 매여 있었던 데다, 제 안의 질투 어린 두려움이 이런 의심을 부추겼거든. 프랭크 치어리블이 요즘 그렇게 자주 찾아오는 건, 매들린한테 자기와 비슷한 어떤 은밀한 마음을 품고 있어서가 아닐까 하고 말이야. 지금도, 이런 일에는 애가 타는 어머니의 관찰이 제 눈보다 훨씬 더 정확할 공산이 크다는 걸 알았고, 또 어머니가 일러 준 자잘한 정황들이 죄다 모아 놓고 보면 어머니가 의기양양하게 갖다 붙인 그 해석에 분명 들어맞기는 했지만, 그래도 그게 그저 사람 좋은 데서 나온 무심한 친절일 뿐, 젊고 사랑스러운 다른 어떤 처녀한테라도 똑같이 했을 행동이 아닐까 하는 생각을 떨치지 못했어. 어쨌든 그러기를 바랐고, 그래서 그렇게 믿으려 애썼지.\n\n\"엄마가 해 주신 얘기를 들으니 마음이 무척 어지럽네요.\" 니콜라스가 잠시 생각하더니 말했어. \"그래도 엄마가 잘못 보신 거였으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"네가 왜 그러기를 바라는지는 나도 모르겠다만, 솔직히.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"내가 잘못 본 게 아니라는 건 너도 믿어도 된다.\"\n\n\"케이트는요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"그게 말이다, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어. \"그게 바로 내가 아직 마음을 못 놓는 대목이야. 이번에 병치레하는 동안 케이트가 줄곧 매들린 곁을 지켰거든. 그 둘이 정이 든 만큼 서로 좋아하는 사이도 없을 거다. 그래서 솔직히 말하면, 니콜라스, 내가 가끔 일부러 케이트를 떼어 놓곤 했어. 그게 좋은 수라고 보거든. 젊은 남자를 다그치는 거지. 그래야 그 애가 너무 마음을 놓지 않으니까, 알겠니.\"\n\n부인이 이 말을 어찌나 한껏 들뜬 데다 제 잘한 일을 흐뭇해하며 하던지, 니콜라스로서는 그 희망을 꺾어 버리기가 이루 말할 수 없이 괴로웠어. 하지만 그는 자기 앞에 떳떳한 길은 하나뿐이고, 마땅히 그 길을 가야 한다고 느꼈지.\n\n\"엄마.\" 그가 다정하게 말했어. \"만약에 정말로 프랭크 씨가 케이트한테 진지한 마음을 품고 있는데, 우리가 잠깐이라도 그걸 부추기게 둔다면, 우리는 더없이 떳떳치 못하고 배은망덕한 짓을 하는 게 된다는 걸 모르시겠어요? 모르시겠냐고 여쭙긴 하지만, 엄마가 모르신다는 건 굳이 말씀 안 드려도 알아요. 아셨다면 더 단단히 조심하셨을 테니까요. 제 뜻을 설명해 드릴게요. 우리가 얼마나 가난한지 떠올려 보세요.\"\n\n니클비 부인은 고개를 저으며, 가난이 죄는 아니지 않느냐고 눈물 속에 말했어.\n\n\"그렇죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"바로 그렇기 때문에 가난은 정직한 자존심을 길러야 해요. 그래야 그게 우리를 떳떳치 못한 짓으로 이끌고 꾀어내지 못하고, 또 우리가 자존심을 지킬 수 있으니까요. 나무를 패고 물을 긷는 사람도 지킬 수 있는 자존심을, 그것도 임금이 제 자존심을 지키는 것보다 더 훌륭히 지킬 수 있는 그 자존심을요. 우리가 그 두 형제분께 얼마나 신세를 졌는지 생각해 보세요. 그분들이 해 주신 일, 그리고 날마다 우리를 위해 베푸시는 일을 떠올려 보세요. 우리가 평생을 바쳐 헌신해도 더없이 모자라고 어림없는 보답밖에 안 될 만큼 너그럽고 섬세하게 말이에요. 그분들이 친아들처럼 여기는 단 하나뿐인 친척, 그 조카가 — 그분들이 그가 받은 교육과 물려받을 재산에 걸맞은 계획을 세워 두지 않았으리라 여기는 건 어린애 같은 생각이죠 — 그런 조카가, 지참금 한 푼 없는 데다 우리와 이토록 가까운 처녀와 결혼하도록 우리가 내버려 둔다면, 그게 어떤 보답이 되겠어요? 그러면 누가 봐도 그가 어떤 음모에 걸려든 거라고, 이건 우리 셋이 짠 치밀한 계략이자 한탕 노린 짓이라고 짐작할 수밖에 없잖아요. 이 일을 똑똑히 그려 보세요, 엄마. 자, 만약 그 둘이 결혼하고, 그분들이 늘 그러시듯 친절한 볼일로 여기 들르셨을 때, 엄마가 그분들께 그 사실을 털어놓아야 한다면 기분이 어떠시겠어요? 마음이 편하시겠어요, 떳떳하게 행동했다는 생각이 드시겠어요?\"\n\n가엾은 니클비 부인은 점점 더 울먹이면서, 물론 프랭크 씨가 먼저 삼촌들의 허락을 구할 거라고 중얼거렸어.\n\n\"그야 그러면 그 사람 처지는 그분들 앞에서 나아지긴 하겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"하지만 우리는 여전히 똑같은 의심을 사게 돼요. 우리 사이의 격차는 여전히 그대로일 테고, 그 사람이 얻을 이득도 지금처럼 빤히 드러나 보일 테니까요. 어쩌면 우리가 이 모든 걸 혼자 김칫국부터 마시는 건지도 몰라요.\" 그가 한결 밝은 목소리로 덧붙였어. \"저는 그러기를 바라고, 거의 그렇다고 믿어요. 만약 그렇지 않다 해도, 저는 케이트를 믿으니까 그 애가 저와 똑같이 느끼리라는 걸 알아요. 그리고 엄마도 믿어요, 조금만 생각해 보시면 엄마도 똑같이 해 주시리라고요.\"\n\n니콜라스가 여러 번 거듭 설명하고 사정한 끝에, 니클비 부인한테서 약속을 받아 냈어. 자기도 아들처럼 생각하려고 최선을 다하겠다는 것, 그리고 만약 프랭크 씨가 계속 마음을 표하면 그걸 단념시키도록 애써 보겠다는 것, 아니면 적어도 그한테 아무런 호의나 도움도 보이지 않겠다는 약속이었지. 니콜라스는 정말로 케이트한테 그 얘기를 꺼낼 필요가 있다는 확신이 설 때까지는 그 일을 입에 담지 않기로 마음먹었어. 그리고 가까이서 직접 지켜보아 일이 정확히 어떤 형편인지 되도록 잘 가늠해 두기로 작정했지. 아주 현명한 결심이었지만, 그는 이걸 실행에 옮기지 못했어. 새로운 근심과 불안거리가 생겼거든.\n\n스마이크가 위태로울 만큼 앓아누웠어. 어찌나 쇠약하고 기진했던지, 부축 없이는 이 방에서 저 방으로 옮겨 가기도 힘들 지경이었지. 게다가 어찌나 야위고 수척했던지 바라보기가 안쓰러울 정도였어. 니콜라스는 처음에 의논했던 그 의사한테서 경고를 들었어. 그가 살아날 마지막 기회이자 희망은 당장 런던을 떠나는 데 달려 있다는 거였지. 니콜라스가 자란 데번셔의 그 지방이 가장 좋은 곳으로 꼽혔어. 하지만 이 충고에는 신중하게 이런 말이 따라붙었지. 그를 데려가는 사람이 누구든 최악의 경우를 각오해야 한다고 말이야. 빠르게 진행되는 폐병의 온갖 징후가 다 나타났으니, 어쩌면 그는 살아서 돌아오지 못할지도 모른다는 거였어.\n\n그 가엾은 청년의 슬픈 사연을 알고 있던 친절한 형제분은, 이 진찰 자리에 입회하라고 늙은 팀을 보냈어. 바로 그날 아침, 니콜라스는 형 찰스의 부름을 받고 그의 개인 방으로 갔는데, 형은 이렇게 말했어.\n\n\"이보게, 한시도 지체해선 안 되네. 우리가 쓸 수 있는 사람의 힘으로 이 아이의 목숨을 구할 수 있다면, 이 아이를 죽게 둬선 안 돼. 그리고 혼자, 낯선 데서 죽게 둬서도 안 되지. 내일 아침에 그 애를 데리고 가게. 그 애 처지에 필요한 온갖 편의를 다 갖춰 주고, 곁을 떠나지 말게. 떠나지 말게, 이보게, 당장의 위험이 더는 없다는 걸 알게 될 때까지는 말이야. 지금 자네 둘을 떼어 놓는 건 정말이지 모진 일일 테니까. 안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼! 오늘 밤 팀이 자네를 찾아갈 걸세. 오늘 밤 팀이 작별 인사 삼아 한두 마디 전하러 갈 거야. 네드, 이 사람아, 니클비 씨가 악수를 나누고 작별 인사를 하려고 기다리네. 니클비 씨가 오래 떠나 있진 않을 거야. 이 가엾은 친구도 곧 나을 거야, 아주 곧 나을 거고. 그러면 그 애를 맡길 만한 마음씨 좋고 소박한 시골 사람들을 찾아내서, 이따금 오갈 거야. 오가고 또 오가고 하는 거지, 그렇잖나 네드. 그러니 풀이 죽을 까닭이 없어. 그 애는 곧 나을 테니까, 아주 곧. 그렇잖나, 그렇잖나, 네드?\"\n\n팀 링킨워터가 그날 밤 무슨 말을 했는지, 또 무엇을 가지고 왔는지는 굳이 말할 필요가 없겠지. 이튿날 아침 니콜라스와 그 가냘픈 동행은 길을 나섰어.\n\n그리고 그 슬픈 작별에 어떤 마음의 고통이, 어떤 시들어 버린 생각이, 어떤 부질없는 슬픔이 깃들어 있었는지를, 누가 헤아릴 수 있었겠어? 그때 자기를 둘러싼 그 사람들이 아니었더라면 다정한 눈길 한 번 받아 본 적 없고 따뜻한 동정의 말 한마디 들어 본 적 없었을 바로 그 사람, 오직 그 사람 말고는 아무도 헤아릴 수 없었을 거야.\n\n\"보세요!\" 니콜라스가 마차 창밖을 내다보며 다급히 외쳤어. \"아직도 골목 모퉁이에 다들 서 계세요! 그리고 저기 케이트가, 가엾은 케이트가, 형이 차마 작별 인사를 못 하겠다던 그 애가 손수건을 흔들고 있어요. 케이트한테 작별 인사 한 번 없이 떠나면 안 되잖아요!\"\n\n\"난 못 하겠어!\" 떨고 있던 동행이 외치며 자리에 털썩 기대고는 두 눈을 가렸어. \"지금 그 애가 보여? 아직도 거기 있어?\"\n\n\"응, 응!\" 니콜라스가 간곡히 말했어. \"저기! 또 손을 흔드네! 내가 자네 대신 답해 줬어. 그리고 이제 안 보이게 됐어. 그렇게 서럽게 무너지지 마, 친구, 그러지 마. 자네는 그 사람들을 모두 다시 만날 거야.\"\n\n이렇게 격려를 받은 그는 시든 두 손을 들어 올려 간절히 맞잡았어.\n\n\"하늘에서요. 저는 하늘에 계신 하느님께 겸손히 기도드려요.\"\n\n그 말은 마치 부서진 가슴이 올리는 기도처럼 들렸어.\n\n# CHAPTER 56\n\n조카에게 마지막 계획을 가로막힌 랄프 니클비가 우연히 떠오른 복수의 음모를 꾸미고, 믿을 만한 옛 조력자를 끌어들이다\n\n이 모험들이 스스로 그려 가는 줄거리, 그리고 이야기를 들려주는 사람이 반드시 따라가야 할 그 줄거리가 이제 우리에게 요구하는 건, 지난 장이 시작되기 직전에 다다랐던 그 지점으로 되돌아가는 일이야. 그러니까 랄프 니클비와 아서 그라이드가, 죽음이 그토록 갑작스럽게 어둡고 무거운 깃발을 세운 그 집에 단둘이 남겨졌던 그때 말이지.\n\n두 주먹을 꽉 쥐고, 어떤 자물쇠로 턱을 채워도 그보다 더 단단히 맞물릴 수 없을 만큼 이를 악문 채로, 랄프는 조카에게 마지막으로 말을 던졌던 그 자세 그대로 한동안 서 있었어. 거칠게 숨을 몰아쉬면서도, 다른 데는 마치 청동 조각상이라도 된 양 빳빳하게 굳어 꼼짝도 안 했지. 한참이 지나자 그는 깊은 잠에서 깨어나는 사람처럼 아주 천천히, 조금씩 몸을 풀기 시작했어. 잠깐 그는 니콜라스가 사라진 문 쪽을 향해 쥐고 있던 주먹을 흔들었어. 그러더니 그 마지막 격정의 표시마저 억지로 눌러 버리려는 듯 주먹을 품속에 찔러 넣고는, 몸을 돌려 아직 바닥에서 일어나지도 못한, 자기보다 배짱이 약한 고리대금업자와 마주했어.\n\n그 잔뜩 움츠린 가엾은 자는 여전히 온몸을 사지마다 떨고 있었고, 머리에 몇 가닥 안 남은 흰머리까지 비참한 공포에 부르르 떨리고 있었어. 그러다 랄프의 눈과 마주치자 비틀비틀 일어섰지. 그러고는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 문 쪽으로 슬금슬금 기어가면서, 자기 잘못이 아니라고 하소연했어.\n\n\"누가 자네 잘못이라고 했나?\" 랄프가 억누른 목소리로 되받았어. \"누가 그랬어?\"\n\n\"꼭 제 탓이라고 여기시는 표정이라서요.\" 그라이드가 주뼛주뼛 말했어.\n\n\"쳇!\" 랄프가 억지로 웃음을 지으며 중얼거렸어. \"내가 탓하는 건 그자가 한 시간만 더 살지 못한 거야. 한 시간만 더 살았어도 충분했을 텐데. 다른 사람은 아무도 탓하지 않아.\"\n\n\"아, 아무도요?\" 그라이드가 물었어.\n\n\"이번 불운에 대해서는 말이지.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"자네 신부를 채 간 그 젊은 놈하고는 갚아야 할 묵은 빚이 있긴 해. 하지만 그건 방금 그놈이 떠들어 댄 거랑은 상관없는 일이야. 이 빌어먹을 사고만 없었다면 우리는 금세 그놈을 떼어 냈을 테니까.\"\n\n랄프 니클비가 말하는 그 침착함에는 어딘가 부자연스러운 데가 있었어. 게다가 그 얼굴, 그 표정과 어우러지니 더욱 그랬지. 아무리 애를 써도 감출 수 없는 경련으로 신경이며 근육이 죄다 씰룩이고 욱신거리며 순간순간 새롭고 끔찍한 모습을 빚어내고 있었거든. 거칠고 느리고 한결같은 그 목소리는 — 거의 단어마다 사이를 두고 멈추는, 어떻게든 또박또박 말하려고 안간힘을 쓰는 술 취한 사람처럼, 숨이 자꾸 멎는 것 말고는 흐트러짐이 없었어 — 그 목소리와, 더없이 격렬하고 사나운 격정의 흔적들, 그리고 그걸 억누르려고 그가 벌이는 사투 사이의 그 대조에는 어딘가 부자연스럽고 소름 끼치는 데가 있었어. 그래서 만약 위층에 누워 있는 그 시체가 그의 자리에 일어서서 잔뜩 움츠린 그라이드 앞에 섰더라도, 이보다 더 그를 떨게 할 광경을 보여 주지는 못했을 거야.\n\n\"마차 말이야.\" 강한 사람이 발작과 싸우듯 안간힘을 쓴 한참 뒤에 랄프가 입을 열었어. \"우리 마차 타고 왔잖아. 그거 기다리고 있나?\"\n\n그라이드는 잘됐다 싶어 핑계 삼아 창가로 가서 살폈어. 랄프는 얼굴을 줄곧 반대쪽으로 돌린 채, 품에 찔러 넣었던 손으로 셔츠를 잡아 뜯으며 쉰 목소리로 속삭이듯 중얼거렸어.\n\n\"만 파운드라니! 만 파운드라고 그랬어! 바로 어제 그 두 건의 저당 잡힌 돈으로 치른 그 액수가 딱 그만큼이었고, 내일이면 다시 비싼 이자를 붙여 나갈 돈이었는데. 그 상점이 무너졌고, 게다가 그놈이 그 소식을 제일 먼저 들고 왔단 말이지! 마차는 와 있나?\"\n\n\"네, 네.\" 그라이드가 그 사납게 캐묻는 말투에 놀라 대답했어. \"여기 있어요. 아이고, 아이고, 어쩜 이렇게 불같으신지!\"\n\n\"이리 와.\" 랄프가 그를 손짓해 부르며 말했어. \"동요하는 티를 내선 안 돼. 팔짱을 끼고 내려가자고.\"\n\n\"근데 멍이 들도록 꼬집으시잖아요.\" 그라이드가 항의했어.\n\n랄프는 짜증스럽게 그를 놓아주고는, 늘 그렇듯 단단하고 무거운 걸음으로 계단을 내려가 마차에 올랐어. 아서 그라이드가 뒤따랐지. 마부가 어디로 가느냐고 묻자 그라이드는 랄프를 미심쩍게 쳐다봤어. 그런데 랄프가 잠자코 아무 뜻도 내비치지 않자, 아서는 자기 집을 댔어. 그래서 둘은 그쪽으로 향했지.\n\n가는 동안 랄프는 가장 구석진 자리에 팔짱을 끼고 앉아 한 마디도 안 했어. 턱을 가슴에 묻고, 매듭처럼 뭉친 눈썹이 잔뜩 찌푸려져 내리뜬 눈을 완전히 가리고 있어서, 마차가 멈출 때까지는 깨어 있다는 기색이라곤 도무지 없어서 잠든 사람 같았어. 마차가 서자 그는 고개를 들고 창밖을 흘긋 보더니, 여기가 어디냐고 물었어.\n\n\"제 집이지요.\" 풀이 죽은 그라이드가 대답했어. 어쩌면 그 집의 외로운 모습에 마음이 흔들렸는지도 몰라. \"아이고! 제 집이에요.\"\n\n\"그렇군.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"오는 길을 살펴보질 못했어. 물 한 잔 마셨으면 좋겠는데. 집에 그건 있겠지?\"\n\n\"물이든, 뭐든 원하시는 건 다 드리지요.\" 그라이드가 신음하듯 대답했어. \"두드려 봐야 소용없어요, 마부 양반. 종을 울려요!\"\n\n마부는 종을 울리고, 또 울리고, 다시 울렸어. 그러고는 거리가 쩌렁쩌렁 울리도록 문을 두드렸지. 그다음에는 문의 열쇠 구멍에 귀를 댔어. 아무도 안 나왔어. 집은 무덤처럼 고요했지.\n\n\"어찌 된 거야?\" 랄프가 짜증스레 말했어.\n\n\"페그가 귀가 아주 어둡거든요.\" 그라이드가 불안하고 겁먹은 얼굴로 대답했어. \"아이고! 다시 울려요, 마부 양반. 종은 눈으로 보는 사람이니까요.\"\n\n마부는 다시 종을 울리고 두드리고, 또 두드리고 울렸어. 이웃 몇몇이 창문을 열어젖히고는 길 건너로 서로 소리쳤지. 늙은 그라이드네 가정부가 쓰러져 죽은 게 틀림없다고 말이야. 또 어떤 이들은 마차 둘레로 모여들어 이런저런 추측을 늘어놓았어. 누구는 잠들었을 거라고 하고, 누구는 불에 타 죽었다 하고, 누구는 술에 취했다고 했지. 그리고 아주 뚱뚱한 한 남자는, 먹을 게 눈에 띄었는데 (워낙 못 먹어 본지라) 너무 놀라서 기절했을 거라고 했어. 이 마지막 말이 구경꾼들을 어찌나 즐겁게 했던지, 다들 떠들썩하게 환호했고, 진짜인지 확인하겠다고 지하 입구로 내려가 부엌문을 부수려는 걸 가까스로 말렸을 정도였어. 그게 다가 아니었어. 아서가 그날 아침 결혼한다는 소문이 퍼진 터라, 신부가 어딨냐고 자꾸 캐묻는 거였어. 대다수는 신부가 랄프 니클비 씨로 변장하고 있는 거라고 우겼어. 그래서 부츠에 판탈롱 차림으로 신부가 공개적으로 나타났다며 농지거리 섞인 분개가 터져 나왔고, 야유와 신음 소리도 잔뜩 쏟아졌지. 마침내 두 고리대금업자는 옆집에 몸을 피해, 사다리를 빌려서 뒷마당 담을 — 그리 높진 않았어 — 기어 넘어, 반대편으로 무사히 내려갔어.\n\n\"솔직히 들어가기가 무서울 지경이오.\" 둘만 남게 되자 아서가 랄프를 돌아보며 말했어. \"그 여자가 살해당했으면 어쩌지. 부지깽이로 머리를 얻어맞아 골이 쏟아진 채 누워 있으면 말이야, 응?\"\n\n\"그러면 또 어때.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"내 말하지만, 나는 그런 일이 지금보다 더 흔하고 더 쉽게 일어났으면 좋겠어. 자네는 눈이 휘둥그레져서 벌벌 떨 테지. 나는 그러길 바란다고!\"\n\n그는 마당의 펌프로 다가갔어. 그러고는 물을 깊이 들이켜고 머리와 얼굴에 한 움큼 끼얹더니, 평소의 태도를 되찾고 앞장서서 집 안으로 들어갔어. 그라이드는 바짝 뒤꽁무니를 따라붙었지.\n\n여전히 똑같이 어두컴컴한 곳이었어. 방마다 늘 그렇듯 음침하고 고요했고, 으스스한 가구들도 죄다 제자리에 놓여 있었지. 그 음산한 낡은 시계의 무쇠 심장은 바깥의 온갖 소동에도 끄떡없이, 여전히 먼지 낀 통 속에서 무겁게 박동하고 있었어. 비틀거리는 찬장들은 늘 그렇듯 우중충한 구석으로 시야에서 슬그머니 숨어들었고, 발소리의 메아리는 똑같이 음울한 소리로 되돌아왔어. 다리가 긴 거미는 날쌘 걸음을 멈췄어. 그러고는 제 칙칙한 영토에 사람들이 들어온 걸 보고 겁을 먹고는, 그들이 지나갈 때까지 죽은 척하며 벽에 꼼짝 않고 매달려 있었지.\n\n지하실부터 다락방까지 두 고리대금업자는 삐걱대는 문을 죄다 열어 가며, 텅 빈 방마다 들여다봤어. 하지만 페그는 어디에도 없었지. 결국 둘은 아서 그라이드가 평소 지내던 방에 주저앉아, 한바탕 뒤진 뒤 숨을 돌렸어.\n\n\"그 노파가 자네 결혼 잔치 준비라도 하러 나갔나 보군.\" 랄프가 떠날 채비를 하며 말했어. \"이걸 봐! 내가 이 증서를 없애 버리겠네. 이제 이건 우리한테 필요 없을 테니까.\"\n\n방 안을 샅샅이 살피던 그라이드가 바로 그 순간 큰 궤짝 앞에 무릎을 꺾고 주저앉으며, 끔찍한 비명을 질렀어.\n\n\"또 왜 그래?\" 랄프가 매섭게 돌아보며 말했어.\n\n\"털렸어! 도둑맞았어!\" 아서 그라이드가 비명을 질렀어.\n\n\"털려? 돈을?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니야. 더 나빠! 훨씬 더 나쁘다고!\"\n\n\"그럼 뭘?\" 랄프가 다그쳤어.\n\n\"돈보다 더한 거, 돈보다 더한 거야!\" 노인이 짐승이 땅을 파헤치듯 궤짝에서 서류를 마구 끄집어 던지며 울부짖었어. \"차라리 돈을 훔쳐 갔으면 — 내 돈 전부를 — 많지도 않은데! 차라리 날 거지로 만들었으면, 이런 짓을 하느니 그게 나았다고!\"\n\n\"무슨 짓?\" 랄프가 말했어. \"무슨 짓을 했다는 거야, 이 노망난 악마 같은 늙은이야?\"\n\n그래도 그라이드는 대답 없이 서류 사이를 마구 찢고 헤집으며, 고통받는 악귀처럼 악을 쓰고 비명을 질러 댔어.\n\n\"뭔가 없어졌다는 거지.\" 랄프가 그의 멱살을 사납게 흔들며 말했어. \"그게 뭐야?\"\n\n\"서류, 증서야. 난 망했어. 잃어버렸어, 잃어버렸다고! 도둑맞았어, 난 끝장났어! 그 여자가 내가 그걸 읽는 걸 봤어 — 요새 그걸 읽었거든 — 자주 그랬지 — 그 여자가 날 지켜보고 있었어, 내가 그걸 이 안에 꼭 맞게 들어가는 상자에 넣는 걸 봤다고, 그 상자가 사라졌어, 그 여자가 훔쳐 갔어. 빌어먹을 년, 날 털어 갔어!\"\n\n\"뭘?\" 랄프가 소리쳤어. 그에게 갑자기 어떤 빛이 번뜩이는 듯, 두 눈이 번쩍이고 온몸이 흥분으로 떨렸어. 그러면서 그라이드의 앙상한 팔을 와락 움켜쥐었지. \"뭘 훔쳐 갔다고?\"\n\n\"그게 뭔지 그 여잔 몰라. 글을 못 읽으니까!\" 그라이드가 그 물음은 아랑곳없이 비명을 질렀어. \"그걸로 돈을 만들 길은 딱 하나뿐이야. 그 여자한테 가져가는 거지. 누군가 그 여자한테 읽어 주고, 뭘 해야 할지 일러 주겠지. 그 여자랑 한패가 그걸로 돈을 받아 챙기고, 게다가 풀려나기까지 할 거야. 자기들 공인 양 떠벌리겠지 — 자기들이 찾았다고, 알고 있었다고 — 그러고는 나한테 불리한 증언을 할 거야. 화를 다 뒤집어쓸 사람은 나뿐이라고, 나, 나 말이야!\"\n\n\"진정해!\" 랄프가 그를 더 꽉 움켜쥐고는, 곁눈으로 흘끔 그를 쏘아봤어. 그 눈길이 어찌나 고정되고 간절한지, 이제 하려는 말에 무슨 숨은 속셈이 있다는 게 충분히 드러났지. \"내 말 좀 들어 봐. 그 여자가 멀리 갔을 리는 없어. 내가 경찰을 부르겠네. 자네는 그 여자가 뭘 훔쳤는지만 신고하면 돼. 그러면 경찰이 그 여자를 붙잡을 거야, 나만 믿어. 여기요! 사람 좀!\"\n\n\"안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼!\" 노인이 랄프의 입에 손을 갖다 대며 비명을 질렀어. \"못 해, 못 한다고.\"\n\n\"사람 좀! 거기 누구 없소!\" 랄프가 외쳤어.\n\n\"안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼!\" 다른 쪽이 미친 사람의 기세로 바닥을 쾅쾅 구르며 비명을 질렀어. \"안 된다니까. 못 해, 못 한다고!\"\n\n\"이 도둑맞은 걸 공개적으로 못 알리겠다고?\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"못 해!\" 그라이드가 두 손을 비틀며 되받았어. \"쉿! 쉿! 이 일은 한 마디도 안 돼. 단 한 마디도 입에 올리면 안 돼. 난 끝장이야. 어느 쪽으로 돌아서든 난 끝장이라고. 난 배신당했어. 난 넘겨질 거야. 난 뉴게이트에서 죽게 될 거라고!\"\n\n이런 미친 듯한 외침들, 그리고 두려움과 슬픔과 분노가 기묘하게 뒤섞인 다른 숱한 외침들을 쏟아 내며, 공포에 질린 그 가엾은 자는 처음의 그 큰 부르짖음을 차츰 가라앉혔어. 그러다 마침내 낮고 절망적인 신음으로 잦아들었지. 궤짝에 남은 서류들을 뒤지다 또 새로 없어진 걸 발견할 때마다, 그 신음 사이사이로 이따금 울부짖음이 끼어들었어. 이렇게 갑자기 떠날 핑계랄 게 거의 없었지만, 랄프는 그를 두고 나왔어. 그러고는 집 밖의 어슬렁대던 사람들에게 아무 일도 없다고 말해 크게 실망시킨 다음, 마차에 올라 자기 집으로 향했지.\n\n탁자 위에 편지 한 통이 놓여 있었어. 그는 한동안 그걸 그대로 놔뒀어. 마치 뜯어 볼 용기가 안 나는 듯이 말이야. 그러다 마침내 뜯어 보더니, 새파랗게 질렸어.\n\n\"최악의 일이 터졌군.\" 그가 말했어. \"그 상점이 무너졌어. 알겠어. 그 소문이 어젯밤 시내에 퍼져서, 그 상인들 귀에까지 들어간 거야. 그래, 그래!\"\n\n그는 방 안을 사납게 오락가락하다가 다시 멈춰 섰어.\n\n\"만 파운드라니! 그것도 거기 단 하루 놓여 있었을 뿐인데 — 단 하루! 그 만 파운드를 긁어모으기까지 얼마나 많은 애태운 세월을, 얼마나 많은 쪼들린 낮과 잠 못 이룬 밤을 보냈는데! 만 파운드라니! 내가 그 만 파운드를 두 배로 불리는 동안, 잘난 척 분칠한 귀부인들이 얼마나 알랑거리고 미소 지었을 테며, 돈 헤프게 쓰는 멍청이들이 내 면전에서는 입에 발린 소리를 하고 속으로는 나를 욕했을까! 내가 이 궁한 돈 빌리는 자들을 짜내고 쥐어짜며 내 즐거움과 잇속을 위해 부려 먹는 동안, 그들이 나한테 얼마나 매끄러운 말과 공손한 표정과 정중한 편지를 바쳤을까! 거짓투성이 세상의 헛소리는, 나 같은 사람이 위선과 배신으로, 알랑거리고 굽실대고 비굴하게 굴어 재산을 모은다는 거야. 흥, 그 만 파운드가 나한테 얼마나 많은 거짓말을, 얼마나 비열하고 천한 핑계를, 얼마나 굽실대는 짓거리를 가져다줬겠어. 내 돈이 아니었으면 매일 저보다 나은 사람들을 발로 차듯 나를 밀쳐 냈을 그 벼락출세한 것들한테서 말이야! 내가 그걸 두 배로 불려 — 백 퍼센트를 남겨 — 금화 한 닢이 또 한 닢을 불러왔다 치자. 그 무더기 속 동전 한 닢 한 닢이 죄다 만 개의 비열하고 하찮은 거짓말을 나타내지 않는 게 없을 거야. 그것도 돈 빌려준 자가 한 거짓말이 아니라, 오 천만에! 돈 빌린 자들, 그 후하고 생각 없고 인심 좋고 화통한 작자들, 죽어도 육 펜스 한 푼 아껴 둘 만큼 쩨쩨하진 않은 그자들이 한 거짓말이지!\"\n\n이 다른 쓰디쓴 생각들로 자기 후회의 쓰라림을 얼마쯤 잊으려는 듯, 랄프는 계속 방 안을 서성거렸어. 그런데 마음이 차츰 잃어버린 돈으로 되돌아가면서, 그 태도에서 결연함이 점점 옅어졌어. 마침내 그는 안락의자에 털썩 주저앉아, 다시 삐걱댈 만큼 양옆을 단단히 움켜쥐고는 말했어.\n\n\"한때는 이 큰돈을 잃은 것만큼 나를 흔들 수 있는 게 없었지. 아무것도 없었어. 출생이며 죽음이며 결혼이며, 대다수 사람들에게 흥미로운 그 모든 일들이 (돈을 따거나 잃는 거랑 얽히지 않은 한) 나한테는 아무 흥미도 없었으니까. 하지만 이제는, 맹세컨대, 이 손해에다 그놈이 그걸 일러 주며 우쭐대던 게 뒤섞인단 말이야. 그놈이 이 일을 꾸민 거라면 — 거의 그놈이 그런 것만 같아 — 더 미워할 수도 없을 만큼 밉겠지. 그놈한테 복수만 할 수 있다면, 아무리 더디더라도 조금씩이라도 말이야 — 그놈을 한 발씩 이기기 시작만 할 수 있다면, 저울추를 돌려놓기만 할 수 있다면 — 그러면 나는 이걸 견딜 수 있어.\"\n\n그의 궁리는 길고도 깊었어. 그 끝에 그는 뉴먼을 시켜 편지 한 통을 보냈지. 사라센스 헤드에 묵는 스퀴어스 씨 앞으로 보내는 거였어. 그가 시내에 도착했는지 알아보고, 도착했으면 답을 기다리라고 일러 두었지. 뉴먼이 가져온 소식은, 스퀴어스 씨가 그날 아침 우편 마차로 와서 침대에서 그 편지를 받았다는 거였어. 그리고 안부를 전하며, 곧 일어나 니클비 씨를 찾아뵙겠다고 했대.\n\n이 전갈을 받은 때와 스퀴어스 씨가 도착한 때 사이의 틈은 아주 짧았어. 하지만 그가 오기 전에 랄프는 감정의 기색을 죄다 눌러 버리고, 다시 한번 그 단단하고 꿈쩍 않고 굽힐 줄 모르는, 몸에 밴 태도를 되찾았어. 어쩌면 그가 도덕에 관해 그리 강한 편견이 없는 숱한 사람들에게 거의 마음대로 부릴 수 있던 그 영향력의 적잖은 부분이, 바로 그런 태도 덕분이었는지도 몰라.\n\n\"그래, 스퀴어스 씨.\" 그가 늘 짓던 그 미소로 이 훌륭한 양반을 맞으며 말했어. 그 미소에는 날카로운 눈초리와 생각에 잠긴 듯한 찌푸림이 한 짝으로 따라붙어 있었지. \"어떻게 지내시나?\"\n\n\"아, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어. \"저야 그럭저럭 잘 지냅니다. 식구들도, 애들도 다 그렇고요. 다만 학교에 뭔 발진 같은 게 돌아서 애들이 통 입맛을 잃긴 했지만요. 그래도 아무한테도 득 안 되는 나쁜 바람은 없는 법이지요. 그게 애들한테 시련이 닥칠 때마다 제가 늘 하는 말입니다. 시련이란 게, 선생님, 죽을 운명을 타고난 자의 몫이지요. 죽음 그 자체가, 선생님, 하나의 시련이고요. 세상은 시련으로 꽉꽉 들어차 있어요. 그러니 애가 시련에 투덜대며 시끄럽게 굴어 사람을 불편하게 하면, 머리통을 한 대 쥐어박아야 하는 겁니다. 그게 바로 성경 말씀대로 하는 거지요, 그게.\"\n\n\"스퀴어스 씨.\" 랄프가 무뚝뚝하게 말했어.\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"이 귀하신 도덕 부스러기는 그만두고, 일 얘기나 합시다.\"\n\n\"기꺼이요, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 되받았어. \"그럼 먼저 제가 한마디 드리자면—\"\n\n\"먼저 내가 한마디 하지. — 노그스!\"\n\n뉴먼은 두세 번 거듭 부르고 나서야 나타나, 주인이 부르셨냐고 물었어.\n\n\"불렀지. 밥 먹으러 가게. 당장 가. 알아들었나?\"\n\n\"아직 시간이 안 됐습니다.\" 뉴먼이 고집스럽게 말했어. \"내 시간이 곧 자네 시간이고, 지금이 그 시간이라면 그런 거야.\" 랄프가 되받았어.\n\n\"날마다 시간을 바꾸시잖아요.\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"그건 공평치 않아요.\"\n\n\"자네가 무슨 요리사를 여럿 거느린 것도 아니니, 그자들한테 폐 끼친 거야 쉽게 사과하면 되겠지.\" 랄프가 쏘아붙였어. \"썩 나가게!\"\n\n랄프는 이 명령을 더없이 단호하게 내렸을 뿐 아니라, 작은 사무실에서 서류를 가지러 가는 척하면서 그게 지켜지는지 지켜봤어. 그리고 뉴먼이 집을 나서자, 그가 빗장열쇠로 몰래 되돌아올 가능성을 막으려고 문에 사슬을 채웠지.\n\n\"저놈을 의심할 만한 이유가 있단 말이야.\" 자기 사무실로 돌아오며 랄프가 말했어. \"그러니 저놈을 가장 빠르고 가장 손 덜 가게 망쳐 버릴 방법을 궁리해 낼 때까지는, 멀찍이 떼어 놓는 게 상책이지.\"\n\n\"그자를 망치는 데야 그리 많은 게 들지도 않을 것 같습니다만.\" 스퀴어스가 히죽 웃으며 말했어.\n\n\"아마 그렇겠지.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"내가 아는 숱한 사람들을 망치는 데도 별로 안 들 테고. 그래, 무슨 말을 하려던 거였소?\"\n\n랄프가 이런 예를 막힘없이 당연하다는 듯 들어 보이고, 그 뒤에 슬쩍 흘린 그 암시는, 스퀴어스 씨에게 분명 (틀림없이 그러려고 의도한 대로) 어떤 효과를 냈어. 그는 잠깐 망설이더니 한결 죽은 목소리로 말했어.\n\n\"아, 제가 하려던 말은요, 선생님. 그 배은망덕하고 매정한 작자, 스놀리 영감을 둘러싼 이 일이 저를 영 곤란하게 한다는 겁니다. 비할 데 없는 불편을 끼친다는 거지요. 게다가, 말하자면, 몇 주씩이나 스퀴어스 부인을 영락없는 과부 신세로 만들기도 하고요. 물론 선생님과 함께 일하는 건 저한테 기쁨이지만요.\"\n\n\"물론이지.\" 랄프가 무뚝뚝하게 말했어.\n\n\"네, 물론이라고 했지요.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 무릎을 문지르며 말을 이었어. \"하지만 동시에, 지금 저처럼 이백오십 마일도 넘게 와서 진술서에 선서를 하려니, 그게 사람을 꽤나 곤란하게 한단 말입니다. 위험은 차치하고서라도요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 위험이라는 게 대체 어디 있는데요, 스퀴어스 씨?\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"위험은 차치하고서라고 했습니다.\" 스퀴어스가 얼버무리며 대답했어.\n\n\"그래서 내가 묻잖소, 그 위험이 어디 있냐고?\"\n\n\"불평하는 건 아니에요, 아시잖아요, 니클비 씨.\" 스퀴어스가 사정조로 말했어. \"정말이지, 저는 이런 분은 처음—\"\n\n\"위험이 어디 있냐고 묻고 있잖소.\" 랄프가 힘주어 거듭 말했어.\n\n\"위험이 어디냐고요?\" 스퀴어스가 무릎을 더 세게 문지르며 되받았어. \"아니, 그거야 입에 올릴 필요가 없는 거지요. 어떤 얘긴 안 꺼내는 게 상책이고요. 아, 제가 무슨 위험을 말하는지 아시잖아요.\"\n\n\"내가 몇 번이나 말했고,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"또 몇 번이나 말해 줘야 하나, 당신한테는 아무 위험도 없다고? 당신이 선서한 게, 아니 선서하라고 요청받은 게 뭐요? 그저, 언제 어느 때 스마이크라는 이름의 아이가 당신한테 맡겨졌고, 그 아이가 몇 해 동안 당신 학교에 있었고, 이러저러한 사정으로 사라졌다가, 이제 다시 찾았고, 당신이 어디 어디 맡겨진 그 아이를 알아봤다는 것뿐이잖소? 이건 다 사실이지, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"네.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"그건 다 사실이지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 됐잖소.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"무슨 위험을 무릅쓴다는 거요? 거짓 선서를 하는 건 스놀리뿐이지, 내가 당신보다 훨씬 적게 돈을 준 그자 말이오.\"\n\n\"스놀리는 확실히 싸게 했지요, 그 사람은.\" 스퀴어스가 한마디 했어.\n\n\"싸게 했다고!\" 랄프가 짜증스레 받아쳤어. \"그래, 게다가 잘도 해냈지. 위선적인 얼굴에 거룩한 척하는 태도로 그걸 척척 해내고 있잖소. 근데 당신은! 위험이라니! 위험이라는 게 대체 무슨 소리요? 증명서는 죄다 진짜고, 스놀리한테 다른 아들이 있었고, 그는 두 번 결혼했고, 첫 부인은 죽었고, 그 여자가 그 편지를 안 썼다는 건 그 여자 귀신 말고는 아무도 못 밝히고, 이 아이가 제 아들이 아니라는 것도, 제 아들은 구더기 밥이 됐다는 것도, 스놀리 본인 말고는 아무도 못 밝힌단 말이오! 위증이라곤 스놀리가 하는 것뿐이고, 그자야 그런 거 꽤나 이골이 났을 거요. 당신 위험이 어디 있다는 거요?\"\n\n\"아니, 그게 말이죠.\" 스퀴어스가 의자에서 안절부절못하며 말했어. \"그렇게 따지자면, 저도 선생님 위험은 어디 있냐고 물을 수 있겠는데요.\"\n\n\"내 위험이 어디 있냐고 물을 수 있다!\" 랄프가 되받았어. \"그래, 내 위험이 어디 있냐고 물어도 좋소. 나는 이 일에 나서지도 않고, 당신도 마찬가지요. 스놀리한테 득이 되는 건 자기가 한 얘기를 끝까지 단단히 붙잡는 것뿐이고, 그자가 무릅쓰는 위험이라곤 그 얘기에서 조금이라도 벗어나는 것뿐이지. 그 음모에서 당신 위험을 운운하다니!\"\n\n\"이봐요.\" 스퀴어스가 불안하게 둘레를 살피며 항의했어. \"그걸 그렇게 부르지 마세요! 그저 부탁이니까, 그러지 마시라고요.\"\n\n\"부르고 싶은 대로 부르시오.\" 랄프가 짜증스레 말했어. \"하지만 내 말 잘 들어요. 이 이야기는 본디, 당신 장사를 망치고 당신을 거의 때려죽일 뻔한 한 놈을 골탕 먹이는 수단으로, 그리고 당신이 되찾고 싶어 하던 반쯤 죽은 일꾼 하나를 도로 손에 넣게 해 주려고 꾸며진 거였소. 당신이 그놈한테 그 일에 끼어든 죗값으로 앙갚음하는 동안, 그놈이 다시 당신 손아귀에 들어왔다는 걸 안다는 게, 당신 원수한테 줄 수 있는 가장 큰 벌이라는 걸 당신이 알았으니까. 그렇지 않소, 스퀴어스 씨?\"\n\n\"아, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 되받았어. 랄프가 모든 걸 자기한테 불리하게 돌려놓겠다고 마음먹은 그 결연함과, 그 매섭고 굽힐 줄 모르는 태도에 거의 압도된 채로 말이야. \"어느 정도는 그랬지요.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 뜻이오?\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"아니, 어느 정도라는 건요.\" 스퀴어스가 되받았어. \"어쩌면, 그게 다 저 때문만은 아니었다는 거지요. 선생님도 풀어야 할 묵은 원한이 좀 있었으니까요.\"\n\n\"내가 그게 없었다면,\" 랄프가 그 상기시키는 말에 조금도 무안해하지 않고 말했어. \"내가 당신을 도왔을 것 같소?\"\n\n\"아니, 그러진 않으셨겠지요.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"전 그저 그 점이 우리 사이에 깔끔하고 분명하게 정리됐으면 했던 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그게 어떻게 달리 될 수가 있겠소?\" 랄프가 되받았어. \"셈이 나한테 불리하다는 것 말고는 말이오. 나는 내 증오를 채우려고 돈을 쓰는데, 당신은 그걸 챙기면서 동시에 당신 증오까지 채우니까. 당신은 적어도 복수심만큼이나 탐욕스럽소. 나도 그렇고. 누가 더 이득이오? 같은 시간에 같은 과정으로 돈과 복수를 다 얻고, 복수는 못 해도 적어도 돈은 확실히 손에 쥐는 당신이오? 아니면 어떤 경우든 돈을 쓰는 것만 확실하고, 기껏해야 마지막에 알맹이뿐인 복수나 거머쥘 수 있는 나요?\"\n\n스퀴어스 씨는 이 물음에 그저 어깨를 으쓱하고 미소를 짓는 것밖에 대답할 게 없었어. 그러자 랄프는 그더러 잠자코 있으라고, 그만큼 형편이 좋은 걸 고맙게 여기라고 했지. 그러고는 그에게 눈을 단단히 고정한 채 말을 이었어.\n\n첫째, 니콜라스가 어떤 젊은 아가씨를 시집보내려고 자기가 짜 둔 계획을 가로막고는, 그 아가씨 아버지가 갑자기 죽으며 벌어진 혼란을 틈타 그 아가씨를 제 손에 넣어 의기양양하게 데려가 버렸다는 것.\n\n둘째, 어떤 유언장이나 양도 증서 — 분명 글로 쓰인 어떤 증서로, 거기엔 그 아가씨 이름이 들어 있을 테니, 일단 그게 보관된 곳에 들어가기만 한다면 다른 것들 사이에서 쉽게 골라낼 수 있는 그런 증서 — 에 따라, 그 아가씨가 어떤 재산을 받을 권리가 있다는 것. 그리고 이 증서의 존재를 그 아가씨가 알게 된다면, 그 남편을 (랄프는 니콜라스가 틀림없이 그 아가씨와 결혼할 거라고 단언했지) 부유하고 잘나가는 사람으로, 더없이 무서운 적으로 만들어 줄 거라는 것.\n\n셋째, 이 증서가 다른 것들과 함께, 그걸 손에 넣었거나 부정하게 감춰 둔 어떤 자에게서 도둑맞았는데, 그자는 그걸 되찾으려 무슨 손을 쓰는 걸 두려워한다는 것. 그리고 자기(랄프)가 그 도둑이 누군지 안다는 것.\n\n스퀴어스 씨는 이 모든 걸 한 음절 한 음절 집어삼킬 듯 탐욕스러운 귀로 들었어. 하나뿐인 눈과 입을 떡 벌린 채로 말이야. 무슨 특별한 까닭으로 자기가 랄프의 이런 깊은 신임을 입게 됐는지, 또 이 모든 게 다 무엇을 향한 건지 어리둥절해하면서 말이지.\n\n\"자.\" 랄프가 몸을 앞으로 기울이며 스퀴어스의 팔에 손을 얹고 말했어. \"내가 품은 계획을 들어 보시오. 내가 이걸 무르익게만 할 수 있다면, 반드시 — 내 말하지만, 반드시 — 실행에 옮겨야 할 계획이오. 이 증서로, 그게 뭐든 간에, 이득을 거둘 수 있는 사람은 그 아가씨 본인이나 그 남편뿐이오. 그리고 둘 중 누구라도 이득을 보려면, 이 증서를 손에 쥐는 게 반드시 필요하지. 그 점은 내가 한 치 의심의 여지 없이 알아냈소. 나는 그 증서를 여기로 가져왔으면 하오. 그래서 그걸 가져온 자한테 금화 오십 파운드를 주고, 그자 면전에서 그걸 재가 되도록 태워 버릴 작정이오.\"\n\n스퀴어스 씨는 랄프의 손이 벽난로 쪽을 가리키는 동작을, 마치 지금 당장 그 종이를 태워 없애기라도 하는 양 눈으로 좇았어. 그러더니 길게 숨을 내쉬며 말했지.\n\n\"네, 근데 그걸 누가 가져옵니까?\"\n\n\"어쩌면 아무도 못 할지도 모르지. 거기 손대기까지 할 일이 많으니까.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"하지만 만약 누군가가 한다면 — 바로 당신이오!\"\n\n스퀴어스 씨가 처음에 보인 그 경악한 기색이며, 그 일을 딱 잘라 못 하겠다고 손사래 친 것은, 대부분의 사람이라면 휘청하게 만들었을 거야. 그래서 곧장 그 제안을 아예 거두어들이게 했을 테지. 그런데 랄프한테는 조금도 영향을 못 미쳤어. 교장이 숨이 차도록 실컷 떠들고 나자, 랄프는 끊긴 적도 없었던 양 태연하게 말을 다시 이었어. 그러고는 가장 힘주어 강조하는 게 좋겠다 싶은 점들을 길게 늘어놓기 시작했지.\n\n그건 이런 거였어. 슬라이더스큐 부인이 나이가 많고 늙어 빠지고 약하다는 것. 한패는커녕 아는 사람조차 있을 가능성이 거의 없다는 것 — 그녀의 두문불출하는 습성과, 그라이드네 같은 집에서 오래 살아온 점을 미루어 보면 말이지. 이 도둑질이 미리 짜 둔 계획의 결과가 아니라고 볼 만한 강력한 근거가 있다는 것 — 안 그랬다면 그녀는 한몫 챙길 기회를 노렸을 테니까. 자기가 한 짓을 곰곰이 생각해 보다가, 무슨 내용인지 도무지 모르는 서류들을 잔뜩 짊어진 신세라는 걸 깨닫게 됐을 때 그녀가 처할 곤란한 처지. 그리고 그녀의 형편을 훤히 아는 누군가가 그녀에게 접근해, 필요하다면 그녀의 두려움을 부추겨 가며, 이런저런 핑계로 그녀의 신임을 파고들어 그 증서를 손에 넣는 게 비교적 쉬우리라는 것. 여기에 이런 점들도 덧붙였어. 스퀴어스 씨가 늘 런던에서 한참 떨어진 데 산다는 것 — 그러니 그가 슬라이더스큐 부인과 어울린다 해도 그냥 가장무도회 놀음 같은 거라, 그때든 나중이든 그를 알아볼 사람이 없으리라는 것. 랄프 본인은 그녀가 이미 얼굴을 아는 터라 이 일을 직접 맡을 수 없다는 것. 그리고 스퀴어스 씨의 보기 드문 수완과 경험에 관한 갖가지 칭찬 — 그 정도면 노파 하나 구워삶는 거야 애들 장난이자 심심풀이일 거라는 것. 이런 회유와 설득에 더해, 랄프는 온 솜씨와 힘을 다해, 만약 일이 잘되면 니콜라스가 겪을 낭패를 생생하게 그려 보였어. 상속녀와 결혼하리라 기대했던 자리에서 거지와 짝지어지는 꼴 말이야. 또 스퀴어스 같은 처지의 사람에게 자기 같은 친구를 곁에 두는 게 얼마나 헤아릴 수 없이 중요한지 슬쩍 내비쳤어. 처음 알고 지낸 이래로 베풀어 준 숱한 은혜를 길게 늘어놓기도 했지. 스퀴어스의 손에서 죽은 병약한 아이 하나를 두고, 그가 그 아이를 잘 보살폈다고 좋게 보고해 준 일도 (그 아이의 죽음은 랄프와 그의 고객들한테 아주 요긴했지만, 이 말은 안 했어) 들먹였어. 그리고 끝으로, 그 오십 파운드를 칠십오 파운드로 올려 줄 수도 있고, 일이 아주 크게 잘되면 백 파운드까지도 줄 수 있다고 넌지시 비쳤지.\n\n이 설득이 마침내 끝나자, 스퀴어스 씨는 다리를 꼬았다가 풀고, 머리를 긁고, 눈을 비비고, 손바닥을 들여다보고, 손톱을 물어뜯었어. 그렇게 안절부절못하고 망설이는 온갖 기색을 다 보이고 나서, \"백 파운드가 니클비 씨가 줄 수 있는 최고 액수냐\"고 물었지. 그렇다는 대답을 듣자 그는 다시 안절부절못했어. 그러더니 한참 생각한 끝에, \"오십 파운드를 더 못 올려 주시겠냐\"고 헛물을 켜 보고는, 친구를 위해서라면 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해 봐야겠다고, 그게 늘 자기 좌우명이라며 그 일을 맡겠다고 했어.\n\n\"근데 그 여자한테는 어떻게 다가갑니까?\" 그가 말했어. \"그게 바로 절 헷갈리게 하는 거란 말이죠.\"\n\n\"아예 못 다가갈 수도 있지.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"하지만 해 보겠소. 나는 전에도 이 도시에서, 그 여자보다 더 잘 숨은 사람들을 추적해 본 적이 있소. 그리고 기니 한두 닢을 잘만 쓰면 이보다 더 깜깜한 수수께끼도 곧잘 풀리는 동네들을 알지. 그래, 필요하면 입까지 단단히 다물게 해 두는 데도 말이오! 내 하인이 문에서 종을 울리는군. 이제 그만 헤어지는 게 낫겠소. 당신은 오락가락하지 말고, 내 연락이 갈 때까지 기다리는 게 좋겠소.\"\n\n\"좋습니다!\" 스퀴어스가 되받았어. \"참! 혹시 그 여자를 못 찾아내시면, 사라센에서 묵은 비용이랑, 시간 허비한 거 얼마쯤은 쳐 주시는 거죠?\"\n\n\"알았소.\" 랄프가 짜증스레 말했어. \"그래! 더 할 말 없소?\"\n\n스퀴어스가 고개를 젓자, 랄프는 그를 데리고 현관문까지 갔어. 그러고는 뉴먼더러 들으라는 듯, 왜 한밤중도 아닌데 문이 잠겨 있는지 모르겠다고 큰 소리로 의아해하면서, 뉴먼은 들이고 스퀴어스는 내보낸 뒤 제 방으로 돌아갔어.\n\n\"자!\" 그가 중얼거렸어. \"이제 무슨 일이 닥치든, 당장은 나는 단단하고 흔들림이 없어. 내 손해와 치욕의 이 작은 한 조각만이라도 되찾을 수 있다면, 그놈한테 틀림없이 가슴에 소중할 게 분명한 이 한 가지 희망에서만이라도 그놈을 꺾을 수 있다면, 이것만 해낼 수 있다면, 이건 내가 그놈을 칭칭 옭아맬 사슬의 첫 고리가 될 거야. 아직 그 누구도 벼려 낸 적 없는 그런 사슬 말이지.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 57\n\n깊어 가는 가을밤이었어. 어둡고 비가 내리고 음울한 밤이었지. 램버스 근처의 외진 거리, 아니 골목이라고 해야 할 곳에 자리 잡은 허름한 집의 위층 방에, 외눈박이 사내 하나가 혼자 앉아 있었어. 차림새가 우스꽝스러웠지. 더 나은 옷이 없어서였는지, 아니면 변장을 하려고 그랬는지, 헐렁한 외투 하나를 걸치고 있었거든. 소매는 제 팔보다 절반은 더 길었고, 품과 길이도 어찌나 넉넉한지 머리까지 통째로 그 안에 둘둘 말고도 남을 정도였어. 그것도 아주 손쉽게, 오래되고 기름때 낀 그 천을 조금도 잡아당기지 않고도 말이야.\n\n이런 차림을 하고, 평소 드나들던 곳이나 하던 일과는 한참 동떨어진 데에, 그것도 이렇게 초라하고 비참한 곳에 있으니, 아마 스퀴어스 부인조차 자기 남편을 알아보기가 쉽지 않았을 거야. 다정한 아내의 애틋한 그리움과 본능이 타고난 눈썰미를 한껏 곤두세웠을 텐데도 말이지. 하지만 그는 분명 스퀴어스 부인의 남편이었어. 그리고 스퀴어스 부인의 남편은 꽤나 울적한 기분에 잠겨 있는 듯했지. 옆 탁자에 놓인 검은 병에서 술을 따라 마시며, 방 안을 한 바퀴 둘러보는 눈길에는 눈앞의 것들에 대한 무관심이 또렷이 묻어났거든. 거기에 멀리 떨어진 풍경과 사람들을 떠올리는 아쉬움과 조바심도 뒤섞여 있었어.\n\n스퀴어스 씨의 눈길이 그렇게 못마땅하게 헤매던 방에도, 그가 창가로 다가가 볼 마음만 먹었다면 눈에 들어왔을 그 좁은 거리에도, 딱히 끌릴 만한 구석이라곤 하나도 없었어. 그가 앉아 있던 다락방은 휑하고 초라했지. 침대 틀이며 그 안에 놓인 몇 안 되는 살림살이는 죄다 가장 흔해 빠진 것들이었어. 다 부서져 가는 데다, 보기에도 영 정이 안 갔지. 거리는 진창에 더럽고 인적도 끊겨 있었어. 빠져나가는 길이 하나뿐이라 어느 때든 거기 사는 사람 말고는 지나는 이가 거의 없었지. 게다가 그날 밤은 누구나 집 안에 들어앉아 있고 싶어 하는 그런 밤이었어. 그래서 지금은 살아 있는 기척이라곤, 더러운 창문 너머로 새어 나오는 보잘것없는 촛불의 희미한 빛뿐이었지. 소리라곤 후두두 떨어지는 빗소리와, 가끔 삐걱대는 문이 쿵 하고 무겁게 닫히는 소리 정도였어.\n\n스퀴어스 씨는 계속 울적하게 주위를 둘러보았어. 그러고는 이 소리들에 깊은 침묵 속에서 귀를 기울였지. 그 침묵을 깨는 거라곤, 잔을 입으로 가져가려고 이따금 팔을 움직일 때 그 커다란 외투가 바스락거리는 소리뿐이었어. 스퀴어스 씨는 한동안 그러고 있었어. 그러다 점점 어둠이 짙어지자 촛불 심지를 잘라야겠다는 생각이 들었지. 이 일로 좀 정신이 든 듯, 그는 눈을 천장으로 들어 올렸어. 그러고는 지붕으로 스며든 비와 습기 때문에 천장에 그려진, 기괴하고 묘한 무늬에 눈길을 고정한 채 이렇게 혼잣말을 늘어놓기 시작했어.\n\n\"흠, 이거 참 꼴좋게 됐군, 이게 다 뭐람! 정말이지 꼴좋아! 내가 여기서 도대체 몇 주를 보낸 거야. 거진 여섯 주는 됐지. 이 빌어먹을 늙은 좀도둑 마나님 뒤꽁무니나 쫓아다니면서 말이야.\" 스퀴어스 씨는 이 호칭을 입에 올리느라 무척 애를 먹고 힘들어했어. \"그러는 동안 두더보이즈 홀은 알아서 척척 엉망이 돼 가고 있겠지! 그 늙은 니클비 같은 뻔뻔스러운 작자랑 엮이면 이게 제일 골치 아픈 점이라니까. 그자가 언제 너랑 손을 끊을지 도무지 알 수가 없거든. 그리고 한 푼이라도 발을 담갔다 하면, 결국 일 파운드까지 끌려 들어가는 거야.\"\n\n이 말을 하고 나니 스퀴어스 씨는 어쨌든 자기가 백 파운드에 발을 담그고 있다는 게 떠올랐는지도 몰라. 표정이 풀어졌어. 그러고는 아까보다 훨씬 흐뭇하게 술맛을 음미하는 기색으로 잔을 입으로 가져갔지.\n\n\"내 평생,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 혼잣말을 이어 갔어. \"저 늙은 니클비 같은 능구렁이는 본 적도, 마주친 적도 없어. 단 한 번도! 그자는 누구도 속을 헤아릴 수가 없다니까. 그자는, 뭐랄까, 진짜 독한 놈이야, 니클비 말이야. 어찌나 교활하고 음흉하게 파고드는지, 날이면 날마다 벌레처럼 기어들고 끈질기게 캐고 더듬고 돌고 휘감으면서, 마침내 이 귀하신 페그 마나님이 어디 숨었는지 알아내고는, 내가 일할 자리를 깔끔하게 닦아 놓잖아. 살금살금 기고 미끄러지듯 다니는 게, 꼭 추하고 늙은 데다 눈만 번뜩이고 피가 썩어 가는 독사 같다니까! 아! 우리 쪽 일에도 잘 어울리는 인물이었을 텐데, 그자한테는 너무 좁았을 거야. 그자의 재주는 어떤 굴레도 다 터뜨려 버리고, 어떤 장애물도 다 넘고, 앞을 가로막는 건 죄다 부숴 버려서, 마침내 스스로 우뚝 솟아올라 하나의 기념비가 되었을걸. 그 무슨 기념비냐면, 음, 나머지는 나중에 생각해 두었다가 적당한 때 말해야지.\"\n\n여기서 잠시 생각을 멈춘 스퀴어스 씨는 다시 잔을 입으로 가져갔어. 그러고는 주머니에서 꼬깃꼬깃한 편지 한 통을 꺼내, 그 내용을 찬찬히 훑어보기 시작했지. 여러 번 읽은 게 분명한 사람의 태도로 말이야. 무슨 새로운 정보를 얻으려는 게 아니라, 딱히 할 만한 다른 재미가 없어서 기억이나 되살리려는 듯한 모습이었어.\n\n\"돼지들은 잘 있고,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 읽었어. \"소들도 잘 있고, 아이들도 멀쩡하다. 어린 스프라우터 녀석이 눈을 찡긋거렸다고? 돌아가면 내가 그 눈을 찡긋하게 해 주지. '코비는 밥을 먹으면서 한사코 훌쩍거렸는데, 쇠고기가 너무 독해서 그렇다고 했다.' 좋아, 코비, 어디 쇠고기 없이도 네가 좀 훌쩍거리게 못 만드나 두고 보자. '피처가 또 열병에 걸렸고,' 당연히 그랬겠지. '친구들이 데리러 와서, 집에 도착한 다음 날 죽었다.' 당연히 그랬겠지, 약 올리려고 그런 거야. 이건 다 깊이 짜인 수작의 일부라니까. 학교에 그 녀석 말고 분기 끝나는 딱 그날에 죽을 놈은 또 없거든. 끝까지 나한테서 본전을 뽑아내고는, 제 심술을 끝장까지 부린 거지. '막내 팔머는 자기가 천국에 있었으면 좋겠다고 했다.' 나는 정말이지, 정말 그 녀석을 어찌해야 좋을지 모르겠어. 늘 끔찍한 소원만 빈다니까. 한번은 자기가 당나귀였으면 좋겠다고 했어. 그럼 자기를 사랑하지 않는 아버지가 없을 테니까! 여섯 살짜리 애가 참 고약하기도 하지!\"\n\n스퀴어스 씨는 이렇게 어린 녀석이 이토록 모진 마음을 품고 있다는 생각에 어찌나 심란해졌던지, 화가 나서 편지를 도로 집어넣었어. 그러고는 새로운 생각 줄기를 따라 위안거리를 찾으려 했지.\n\n\"런던에서 이렇게 오래 죽치고 있은 것도 참 길었어.\" 그가 말했어. \"게다가 여긴 와서 살기에 정말 끔찍한 소굴이라니까. 고작 한 주쯤이라 해도 말이야. 그래도 백 파운드면 애 다섯이야. 애 다섯이 백 파운드를 채우려면 꼬박 일 년이 걸리지. 거기다 그 녀석들 먹이고 재우는 비용까지 빼야 하고. 게다가 내가 여기 있어도 손해 볼 건 없어. 애들 돈은 내가 집에 있으나 없으나 똑같이 들어오고, 스퀴어스 부인이 알아서 애들을 잡고 있을 테니까. 물론 못 챙긴 시간은 메워야겠지. 밀린 매질도 처리해야 할 거고. 그래도 며칠이면 다 바로잡을 수 있어. 백 파운드를 위해서라면 좀 더 일하는 거야 별것 아니지. 이제 슬슬 그 노파를 보러 갈 때가 됐군. 어젯밤 그 여자가 한 말로 보아, 내가 성공할 거라면 오늘 밤에 할 것 같아. 그러니 반 잔만 더 마시면서 성공을 빌고, 기운을 좀 차려야지. 스퀴어스 부인, 여보, 당신의 건강을 위하여!\"\n\n마치 잔을 권하는 부인이 정말 거기 있기라도 한 듯, 스퀴어스 씨는 외눈으로 곁눈질을 했어. 그러고는 분명 그 흥에 겨워, 잔을 가득 채우더니 쭉 비워 버렸지. 그 술이 독한 독주인 데다 이미 같은 병에 한 번 이상 손을 댔으니, 이쯤 되어 그가 무척 들뜬 상태가 된 것도, 제 목적을 이룰 만큼 충분히 흥분한 것도 놀랄 일은 아니었어.\n\n그 목적이 무엇인지는 곧 드러났어. 그는 몸을 가누려고 방 안을 몇 바퀴 돈 다음, 병을 겨드랑이에 끼고 잔을 손에 들었지. 그러고는 한참 자리를 비울 작정인 듯 촛불을 불어 끄고, 계단으로 살그머니 나갔어. 자기 방 맞은편 문으로 가만가만 다가가서는, 가볍게 문을 두드렸지.\n\n\"근데 두드려 봐야 무슨 소용이람?\" 그가 말했어. \"어차피 못 들을 텐데. 뭐 별로 대단한 일을 하고 있진 않겠지. 설령 그렇대도, 내가 보기엔 별 상관 없고.\"\n\n이렇게 간단히 운을 떼고 나서, 스퀴어스 씨는 문고리에 손을 댔어. 그러고는 방금 나온 곳보다 훨씬 더 처참한 다락방 안으로 머리를 들이밀었지. 거기엔 노파 하나밖에 없었어. 그 여자는 초라한 불가에 몸을 숙이고 있었지. 날씨는 아직 따뜻했지만 저녁은 쌀쌀했거든. 그는 안으로 들어가 노파의 어깨를 톡톡 두드렸어.\n\n\"자, 내 슬라이더 양반.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 농담조로 말했어.\n\n\"당신이오?\" 페그가 물었어.\n\n\"아! 나요. 그리고 '나'는 일인칭 단수 주격이지. 동사 '~이오'와 호응하고, 생략된 스퀴어스의 지배를 받는단 말이야. 도토리 앞엔 '하나의', 한 시간 앞엔 '하나의'. 다만 h 소리가 날 때는 a만 쓰는 거지. '그리고' 앞엔 a, '기술' 앞엔 a, '큰길' 앞엔 a처럼.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 문법책에서 아무렇게나 끌어다 대답했어. \"뭐, 설령 틀렸어도 당신은 더 잘 알지도 못할 테고, 맞았다면 그건 어쩌다 그렇게 된 거고.\"\n\n이 대답을 늘 하던 목소리 크기로 했으니, 페그한테 들릴 리가 없었지. 스퀴어스 씨는 걸상 하나를 불가로 끌어다, 노파를 마주 보고 앉았어. 병과 잔은 둘 사이 바닥에 내려놓았지. 그러고는 다시 아주 큰 소리로 외쳤어.\n\n\"자, 내 슬라이더 양반!\"\n\n\"들려요.\" 페그가 그를 아주 정중하게 맞으며 말했어.\n\n\"약속대로 왔수다.\" 스퀴어스가 외쳤어.\n\n\"내가 살던 시골에선 그렇게들 말하곤 했지.\" 페그가 흐뭇하게 한마디 했어. \"그래도 난 기름이 더 낫다고 봐요.\"\n\n\"뭐보다 낫다고?\" 스퀴어스가 외쳤어. 그러고는 나직이 꽤 험한 말을 한마디 덧붙였지.\n\n\"아니,\" 페그가 말했어. \"당연히 아니지.\"\n\n\"내 평생 당신 같은 괴물은 처음 봐!\" 스퀴어스가 중얼거렸어. 그러면서도 한껏 상냥한 표정을 지으려 애썼지. 페그의 눈이 그를 향하고 있었고, 그 여자가 무슨 그럴듯한 재담이라도 던진 게 신나는지 무섭게 킬킬거리고 있었거든. \"이게 보여? 이건 병이야.\"\n\n\"보여요.\" 페그가 대답했어.\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 이건 보여?\" 스퀴어스가 고래고래 소리쳤어. \"이건 잔이야.\" 페그는 그것도 보였어.\n\n\"그럼 잘 봐.\" 스퀴어스가 말에 맞춰 적절히 행동까지 보태며 말했어. \"내가 병에서 잔에 따라. 그러고는 '당신의 건강을 위하여, 슬라이더' 하고 쭉 비우지. 그다음 점잖게 한 방울로 헹궈서, 어쩔 수 없이 불에 휙 던지는 거야. 어이쿠! 이러다 다음엔 굴뚝에 불이 붙겠네. 다시 채워서, 당신한테 건네주는 거야.\"\n\n\"당신의 건강을 위하여.\" 페그가 말했어.\n\n\"이건 그래도 알아듣는군.\" 스퀴어스가 중얼거렸어. 슬라이더스큐 부인이 제 몫을 단숨에 들이켜고는, 그러고 나서 끔찍하게 캑캑거리고 숨을 헐떡이는 걸 지켜보면서 말이야. \"자 그럼, 얘기 좀 해 봅시다. 그 류머티즘은 어때?\"\n\n슬라이더스큐 부인은 연신 눈을 깜빡이고 킬킬거리면서, 스퀴어스 씨와 그의 사람됨, 태도, 말솜씨에 푹 빠진 듯한 표정을 지었어. 그러고는 류머티즘이 좀 나아졌다고 대답했지.\n\n\"그 이유가 뭘까,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 병에서 새로운 익살을 끌어내며 말했어. \"류머티즘이 생기는 이유가 뭐냐고? 그게 무슨 뜻이지? 사람들은 왜 그런 걸 앓는 거야, 응?\"\n\n슬라이더스큐 부인은 모른다고 했어. 그래도 어쩔 도리가 없어서 그런 게 아닐까 하고 짐작은 했지.\n\n\"홍역, 류머티즘, 백일해, 열병, 학질, 그리고 요통,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어. \"이게 다 한통속으로 철학이라니까. 바로 그거야. 천체도 철학이고, 지상의 몸뚱이도 철학이지. 천체에 나사가 하나 헐거워지면, 그게 철학이야. 그리고 지상의 몸뚱이에 나사가 하나 헐거워지면, 그것도 철학이고. 아니면 가끔 거기 형이상학이 조금 섞여 있을 수도 있지만, 그건 자주 있는 일은 아니지. 철학이야말로 나한테 딱 맞는 녀석이야. 부모가 고전이니 상업이니 수학 쪽으로 뭘 물어보잖아? 그럼 내가 엄숙하게 이러는 거지. '저, 선생님, 우선 선생께서는 철학자이십니까?' 그럼 그 사람이 이러지. '아닙니다, 스퀴어스 씨. 저는 철학자가 아닙니다.' 그럼 내가 '그렇다면, 선생님, 안타깝군요. 제가 그걸 설명해 드릴 수가 없겠습니다' 하는 거야. 그러면 자연히 그 부모는 돌아가면서 자기도 철학자였으면 하고 바라게 되고, 또 그만큼 자연히 나를 철학자로 여기게 되지.\"\n\n이 말을, 그리고 그보다 훨씬 많은 말을, 술기운 어린 심오함과 진지한 듯 우스꽝스러운 태도로 늘어놓으며, 스퀴어스 씨는 내내 슬라이더스큐 부인한테서 눈을 떼지 않았어. 한마디도 못 알아듣는 그 여자한테 말이지. 그러고는 자기 잔을 채우고 병을 건네며 말을 맺었어. 페그는 거기에 합당한 경의를 표했지.\n\n\"바로 이 맛이야!\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어. \"당신, 아까보다 이십 파운드 십 실링어치는 더 좋아 보여.\"\n\n슬라이더스큐 부인은 또 킬킬거렸어. 하지만 차마 그 칭찬에 말로 맞장구를 칠 수는 없는 듯, 겸손을 떨었지.\n\n\"이십 파운드 십 실링어치는 더 좋아 보인다고.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 거듭 말했어. \"내가 처음 인사하러 왔던 그날보다 말이야. 모르겠어?\"\n\n\"아!\" 페그가 고개를 절레절레 저으며 말했어. \"그날 당신이 날 어찌나 놀라게 했는지.\"\n\n\"내가?\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"뭐, 좀 깜짝 놀랄 만하긴 했지. 낯선 사람이 와서는 다짜고짜 당신에 대해 다 안다고 하면서, 당신 이름이 뭔지, 왜 여기서 이렇게 조용히 지내는지, 뭘 슬쩍했는지, 또 누구한테서 슬쩍했는지까지 줄줄이 읊으며 인사를 했으니, 안 그래?\"\n\n페그는 그렇다는 듯 고개를 힘껏 끄덕였어.\n\n\"하지만 난 그런 식으로 벌어지는 일은 죄다 안단 말이지.\" 스퀴어스가 말을 이었어. \"그런 부류의 일은 내가 훤히 꿰지 못하는 게 하나도 없어. 난 말하자면 변호사 같은 사람이야, 슬라이더. 그것도 일류로, 그것도 머리까지 잘 돌아가는. 손가락이 너무 날래서 곤경에 빠진 거의 모든 남자, 여자, 아이의 둘도 없는 친구이자 은밀한 조언자가 바로 나라고. 나는 말이야……\"\n\n스퀴어스 씨가 늘어놓던 제 잘난 점과 재능의 목록은, 일부는 자신과 랄프 니클비 사이에 미리 짜 둔 계획에서 나온 것이고, 일부는 그 검은 병에서 흘러나온 것이었지. 그런데 여기서 슬라이더스큐 부인이 끼어드는 바람에 끊기고 말았어.\n\n\"하, 하, 하!\" 그 여자가 팔짱을 끼고 고개를 까딱거리며 소리쳤어. \"그래서 그 작자가 결국 결혼을 못 했다 이거지, 응? 결국 장가를 못 갔다고?\"\n\n\"그래.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"못 갔지!\"\n\n\"그리고 젊은 정인이 와서 신부를 채 갔다, 응?\" 페그가 말했어.\n\n\"그자 코앞에서 말이야.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"게다가 그 젊은 녀석이 험하게 굴기까지 했다더군. 창문을 다 깨부수고, 결혼식 꽃 장식을 억지로 삼키게 해서 그자가 하마터면 목이 막힐 뻔했다지.\"\n\n\"그 얘길 처음부터 다시 다 해 봐요.\" 페그가 소리쳤어. 옛 주인이 당한 낭패를 고소해하는 그 심술궂은 표정에, 타고난 흉한 얼굴이 정말이지 무시무시해 보였지. \"다시 한번 다 들어 봅시다. 이번엔 처음부터, 나한테 한 번도 얘기 안 한 것처럼 시작해 봐요. 한 마디 한 마디 다 들려줘요. 자, 자, 맨 처음부터 말이야. 그자가 그날 아침 그 집에 갔을 때부터!\"\n\n스퀴어스 씨는 슬라이더스큐 부인한테 술을 아낌없이 따라 주었어. 그리고 그렇게 큰 소리로 떠드는 수고를 견디려고 자기도 자주 술병에 손을 댔지. 그러고는 그 청을 들어주어, 아서 그라이드가 당한 망신을 묘사해 주었어. 그때그때 떠오르는 대로 진실에 적당히 살을 붙여 가면서 말이야. 사실 이렇게 그럴듯한 이야기를 지어내고 갖다 붙이는 솜씨가, 두 사람이 처음 알게 됐을 무렵 그가 노파의 환심을 사는 데 큰 몫을 했거든. 슬라이더스큐 부인은 기쁨에 겨워 어쩔 줄을 몰랐어. 고개를 이리저리 굴리고, 앙상한 어깨를 추켜올리고, 시체 같은 얼굴을 온갖 복잡하고 흉한 모양으로 일그러뜨렸지. 그 꼴에 스퀴어스 씨조차 끝없는 놀라움과 역겨움이 일 정도였어.\n\n\"그 작자는 음흉한 늙은 염소야.\" 페그가 말했어. \"교활한 수작이랑 거짓 약속으로 날 속여먹었지. 하지만 상관없어. 난 그자랑 비긴 셈이니까. 비긴 셈이지.\"\n\n\"비긴 것보다 더하지, 슬라이더.\" 스퀴어스가 받았어. \"그자가 결혼을 했다면 비긴 셈이었겠지만, 거기다 낭패까지 봤으니 당신이 한참 앞선 거야. 비교도 안 돼, 슬라이더, 아주 비교도 안 되게 앞섰지. 아, 그러고 보니 생각났는데,\" 그가 노파에게 잔을 건네며 덧붙였어. \"그 증서들에 대해 내 의견을 듣고 싶으면, 어떤 걸 챙기고 어떤 걸 태우는 게 좋을지 알고 싶으면, 지금이 딱 좋은 때야, 슬라이더.\"\n\n\"그건 서두를 거 없어요.\" 페그가 뭔가 안다는 듯 여러 번 눈짓을 하고 윙크를 하며 말했어.\n\n\"오! 좋을 대로!\" 스퀴어스가 한마디 했어. \"나야 상관없지. 당신이 부탁한 거잖아. 친구니까 한 푼도 안 받을 생각이었고. 물론 당신이 제일 잘 판단하겠지. 그래도 당신, 참 대담한 여자야, 슬라이더.\"\n\n\"대담하다니, 그게 무슨 소리요?\" 페그가 말했어.\n\n\"뭐, 내 말은 그냥, 나 같으면 자칫 내 목을 매달 수도 있는 서류를 여기저기 늘어놓진 않겠다는 거야. 돈으로 바꿀 수 있을 때 말이지. 쓸모없는 건 없애 버리고, 쓸모 있는 건 어디 안전한 데 잘 챙겨 두고. 그게 다야.\" 스퀴어스가 받았어. \"하지만 자기 일은 누구나 자기가 제일 잘 판단하는 법이지. 내가 하고 싶은 말은 그저, 슬라이더, 나라면 그렇게 안 둔다는 거야.\"\n\n\"이리 와요.\" 페그가 말했어. \"그럼 보여 줄게.\"\n\n\"보고 싶지 않은데.\" 스퀴어스가 시큰둥한 척하며 대답했어. \"무슨 대단한 구경거리라도 되는 양 말하지 말라고. 다른 사람한테 보여 주고 그 사람 조언이나 들으시지.\"\n\n스퀴어스 씨는 아마 좀 더 오래 삐친 척 연극을 이어 갔을 거야. 만약 슬라이더스큐 부인이 그의 호감 속에서 예전의 그 높은 자리를 되찾으려는 마음에 어찌나 애살스럽게 굴었던지, 그 애무에 숨이 막힐 위험에 처하지 않았더라면 말이지. 그는 되도록 점잖게 이 자질구레한 친밀함을 막아 냈어. 사실 그 친밀함은 슬라이더스큐 부인의 타고난 약점 못지않게, 적어도 그 검은 병 탓도 컸다고 볼 만했지. 그는 그저 농담이었을 뿐이라고 둘러댔어. 그리고 자기 기분이 조금도 상하지 않았다는 증거로, 그렇게 해서 이 어여쁜 친구의 마음에 조금이나마 흡족함이나 위안을 줄 수 있다면 당장이라도 증서들을 살펴보겠다고 했지.\n\n\"그래, 일어선 김에, 내 슬라이더 양반,\" 노파가 증서를 가지러 일어서자 스퀴어스가 고래고래 소리쳤어. \"문에 빗장을 걸어.\"\n\n페그는 종종걸음으로 문으로 가서, 빗장을 더듬더듬 만지작거렸어. 그러고는 방 반대편 끝으로 살금살금 가더니, 찬장 바닥을 가득 메운 석탄 밑에서 작은 전나무 상자 하나를 꺼냈어. 그걸 스퀴어스의 발치 바닥에 내려놓고는, 침대 베개 밑에서 작은 열쇠 하나를 가져왔지. 그러고는 그 신사에게 그것으로 상자를 열어 보라고 손짓했어. 노파의 동작을 하나하나 열심히 좇고 있던 스퀴어스 씨는, 이 눈짓에 지체 없이 따랐지. 뚜껑을 젖히고는, 그 안에 들어 있는 서류들을 황홀하게 바라봤어.\n\n\"자, 봐요.\" 페그가 그의 옆 바닥에 무릎을 꿇고 앉으며, 조바심치는 그의 손을 막고는 말했어. \"쓸모없는 건 우리가 태워 버리고, 돈이 될 만한 건 챙겨 두지. 그리고 혹시 그자를 곤경에 빠뜨릴 만한 게 있어서, 그 마음을 갈가리 찢어 놓고 시들시들 말라죽게 할 수 있는 거라면, 그런 건 각별히 잘 간수하는 거야. 그게 바로 내가 하고 싶은 거고, 그자를 떠날 때 내가 바라던 거니까.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"당신이 그자한테 별로 좋은 감정은 없는 줄 알았지. 근데 말이야, 왜 돈도 좀 같이 안 챙긴 거야?\"\n\n\"좀 뭐를?\" 페그가 물었어.\n\n\"돈 좀.\" 스퀴어스가 외쳤어. \"이 여자가 내 말을 알아듣고는, 내 핏줄이라도 터뜨리게 만들려는 게 분명해. 그래야 날 간호하는 재미를 누릴 테니까. 돈 좀, 슬라이더, 돈 말이야!\"\n\n\"아니, 그런 걸 다 묻다니 무슨 사람이 그래!\" 페그가 좀 깔보듯 소리쳤어. \"내가 아서 그라이드한테서 돈을 챙겼다면, 그자는 날 찾아내려고 온 세상을 다 뒤졌을 거야. 그래, 어떻게든 냄새를 맡고 캐냈을 거라고. 내가 그 돈을 잉글랜드에서 제일 깊은 우물 밑바닥에 묻었더라도 말이지. 아니, 아니야! 난 그보다는 똑똑했어. 난 그자의 비밀이 숨겨져 있을 만한 걸 챙겼지. 그자가 도저히 세상에 드러낼 수 없는 것들 말이야. 그게 아무리 큰돈이 되더라도 말이지. 그자는 늙은 개야. 교활하고 음흉하고 은혜도 모르는 늙은 개라고! 처음엔 날 굶기더니, 그다음엔 날 속여먹었어. 할 수만 있다면 난 그자를 죽여 버릴 거야.\"\n\n\"좋아, 아주 갸륵하군.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"근데 슬라이더, 무엇보다 먼저, 그 상자를 태워. 들통날 수 있는 물건은 절대 두면 안 돼. 늘 그걸 명심하라고. 그러니 당신이 그걸 조각조각 부수는 동안 — 아주 낡고 삐걱대니까 쉽게 부술 수 있을 거야 — 그리고 그 잔조각들을 태우는 동안, 내가 서류들을 훑어보고 그게 뭔지 말해 줄게.\"\n\n페그가 이 방식에 따르겠다고 하자, 스퀴어스 씨는 상자를 거꾸로 뒤집어 안에 든 것들을 바닥에 와르르 쏟았어. 그러고는 빈 상자를 그 여자한테 건넸지. 상자를 부수게 한 건 노파의 주의를 붙들어 두려고 즉석에서 꾸며 낸 수작이었어. 혹시 자기가 하는 일에서 그 여자의 시선을 돌릴 필요가 생길 경우를 대비해서 말이지.\n\n\"자!\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"당신은 그 조각들을 살창 사이에 쑤셔 넣고 불을 잘 지펴 봐. 그동안 난 읽을 테니까. 어디 보자, 어디 보자.\" 그러고는 촛불을 옆으로 끌어다 놓고, 스퀴어스 씨는 잔뜩 들떠서 얼굴 가득 음흉한 웃음을 띤 채 살펴보는 일에 착수했어.\n\n만약 그 노파가 그렇게 귀가 먹지 않았더라면, 마지막으로 문에 갔을 때 바로 그 뒤에 바짝 붙어 있던 두 사람의 숨소리를 분명 들었을 거야. 그리고 그 두 사람이 노파의 그 약점을 몰랐더라면, 아마 그 순간 모습을 드러내든지 아니면 달아나든지 둘 중 하나를 택했겠지. 하지만 자기들이 상대하는 게 누군지 잘 알았기에, 그들은 가만히 있었어. 그러고는 이제 문 앞에 들키지 않고 나타났을 뿐 아니라 — 그 문은 빗장이 걸려 있지 않았거든, 빗장에 고리가 없었으니까 — 조심스럽게, 발소리도 없이 방 안으로 들어왔지.\n\n그들이 살그머니, 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 조금씩 더 깊이 들어오는 동안, 그것도 숨도 쉬지 않는 듯 조심하면서 들어오는 동안, 늙은 마귀할멈과 스퀴어스는 그런 침입은 꿈에도 모른 채, 자기들 말고 가까이 누가 있다는 건 전혀 눈치채지 못하고 제 일에만 바삐 매달려 있었어. 노파는 주름진 얼굴을 난로 살창에 바짝 갖다 대고, 아직 장작에 옮겨붙지 않은 흐릿한 잉걸불을 후후 불고 있었지. 스퀴어스는 촛불 위로 몸을 숙이고 있었는데, 그 불빛이 그의 얼굴을 한껏 흉하게 드러냈어. 불빛이 옆에 있던 노파의 얼굴을 그렇게 비춘 것처럼 말이야. 둘 다 일에 정신이 팔려, 의기양양한 얼굴을 하고 있었지. 그 뒤에 있는 두 사람의 불안한 표정과는 너무도 대조되는 얼굴이었어. 그 둘은 아주 작은 소리라도 들리면 그 틈을 타 발걸음을 옮겼고, 한 치를 채 움직이기도 전에 다시 사방이 조용해지면 또 멈춰 섰지. 이 모든 게 휑하니 텅 빈 방, 축축한 벽, 가물거리는 미덥잖은 불빛과 어우러져 하나의 광경을 이루었어. 아무리 무심하고 무덤덤한 구경꾼이라도 — 거기 누가 있었더라면 말이지 — 거기서 어떤 흥미를 느끼지 않고는 못 배겼을, 그리고 쉽사리 잊지 못했을 그런 광경이었지.\n\n그 살금살금 들어온 사람들 가운데 하나는 프랭크 치어리블이었고, 다른 하나는 뉴먼 노그스였어. 뉴먼은 녹슨 주둥이를 잡고 낡은 풀무 한 짝을 집어 들었지. 그러고는 그걸 허공에 한 번 휘둘러 스퀴어스 씨의 머리통 위로 내리치려던 참이었어. 그 순간 프랭크가 진지한 몸짓으로 그의 팔을 붙들었어. 그러고는 한 걸음 더 앞으로 나아가, 그 교장 바로 뒤에 바짝 다가섰지. 살짝 몸을 앞으로 기울이면, 그가 눈앞에 들어 올린 글씨를 또렷이 알아볼 수 있을 정도로 가까이 말이야.\n\n스퀴어스 씨는 그리 학식이 깊은 편이 아니어서, 이 첫 번째 노획물 앞에서 꽤나 쩔쩔매는 듯했어. 그건 정자체로 쓰여 있었는데, 익숙한 눈이 아니고서는 알아보기가 영 쉽지 않았거든. 그는 왼쪽에서 오른쪽으로 읽어 보았어. 그다음엔 오른쪽에서 왼쪽으로도 읽어 봤지. 그런데 어느 쪽으로 읽어도 똑같이 알쏭달쏭하니까, 이번엔 거꾸로 뒤집어 봤어. 그래도 별 소득은 없었지.\n\n\"하, 하, 하!\" 페그가 킬킬거렸어. 불 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 앉아, 상자 조각들을 불에 넣어 주면서, 더없이 악마 같은 환희에 차서 이를 드러내고 웃었지. \"그게 무슨 글이래, 응?\"\n\n\"별거 아니야.\" 스퀴어스가 그것을 그 여자 쪽으로 휙 던지며 대답했어. \"내가 보기엔 그냥 오래된 임대 계약서야. 불에 던져.\"\n\n슬라이더스큐 부인은 시키는 대로 했어. 그러고는 다음 건 뭐냐고 물었지.\n\n\"이건,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"기한 지난 어음하고 갱신된 어음 한 묶음인데, 젊은 신사 예닐곱 명 거야. 근데 죄다 국회의원이라 누구한테도 쓸모가 없어. 불에 던져!\" 페그는 시키는 대로 하고는, 다음 걸 기다렸어.\n\n\"이건,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"캐셥 골짜기에 있는 퓨어처치 교구 목사직 임명권 매매 증서인 것 같군. 그건 잘 챙겨 둬, 슬라이더, 정말이지 신을 봐서라도 말이야. 경매장에 내놓으면 제값을 받을 거야.\"\n\n\"다음 건 뭐예요?\" 페그가 물었어.\n\n\"음, 이건,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"함께 든 편지 두 통으로 보아, 시골에 있는 어느 부목사가 이십 파운드를 빌리고 반년 치 봉급인 사십 파운드를 갚겠다고 쓴 채무 증서 같군. 그건 챙겨 둬. 그자가 안 갚으면, 그의 주교가 곧장 그를 들이닥쳐 닦달할 테니까. 우리야 낙타랑 바늘귀가 무슨 뜻인지 알지. 제 수입이 얼마든 그걸로 못 사는 사람은 어떤 값을 치러도 천국에 갈 생각은 하지 말아야지. 거 참 이상하네. 아직 그 비슷한 것도 안 보여.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이에요?\" 페그가 말했어.\n\n\"아무것도 아니야.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"그냥 내가 찾는 게……\"\n\n뉴먼이 다시 풀무를 들어 올렸어. 또 한 번, 프랭크가 소리 하나 없이 팔을 잽싸게 휘둘러 그를 멈춰 세웠지.\n\n\"여기 있군.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"채무 증서들이군. 챙겨 둬. 위임장. 그것도 챙겨 두고. 자백 증서 두 장. 챙겨 둬. 임대 및 해제 증서. 그건 태워. 아! '매덜린 브레이 — 성년이 되거나 결혼하면 — 앞서 말한 매덜린' — 자, 이건 태워!\"\n\n스퀴어스는 그러려고 양피지 한 장을 얼른 집어 노파한테 던졌어. 그리고 그 여자가 고개를 돌리는 사이, 방금 그 말이 눈에 들어왔던 그 증서를 자기 커다란 외투 품속에 쑤셔 넣었지. 그러고는 의기양양하게 함성을 내질렀어.\n\n\"찾았다!\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"찾았어! 만세! 가망 없는 도박이긴 했지만 계획은 좋았지. 그리고 마침내 이긴 거야!\"\n\n페그가 뭐가 그리 우습냐고 물었지만, 아무 대답도 돌아오지 않았어. 뉴먼의 팔은 더 이상 붙들어 둘 수가 없었지. 풀무가 묵직하게, 한 치도 빗나가지 않고 스퀴어스 씨의 머리통 한복판으로 내리꽂혔어. 그러고는 그를 바닥에 쓰러뜨려, 납작하게 뻗은 채 정신을 잃게 만들었지.\n\n옛 집에서 그토록 멀리 떠나오는 길에 자기가 돌보는 친구가 덜 지치고 덜 힘들도록, 니콜라스는 그 거리를 이틀 여정으로 나누었어. 그래서 집을 떠난 지 이틀째 저물녘에, 그는 제 삶에서 가장 행복했던 시절을 보낸 그곳에서 불과 몇 마일 떨어진 곳에 다다랐지. 그곳은 마음을 즐겁고 평온한 생각으로 가득 채워 주면서도, 한편으로는 아프고 또렷한 기억들을 도로 불러왔어. 그와 그의 식구가 옛 집을 떠나 거친 세상으로, 낯선 사람들의 인정에 기대어 떠돌게 된 그 시절의 기억 말이야.\n\n옛날에 대한 추억과, 어린 시절을 보낸 곳을 거니는 일이 아무리 무딘 사람의 마음에도 흔히 불러일으키는 그런 감상 같은 건, 니콜라스의 마음을 부드럽게 만들고 시들어 가는 친구를 평소보다 더 마음 쓰게 하는 데 굳이 필요하지도 않았어. 밤이고 낮이고, 언제 어느 때든, 늘 지켜보고 살피고 마음을 졸이면서, 친구도 없고 의지할 곳도 없는, 이제 삶의 모래가 빠르게 흘러내려 점점 줄어드는 그런 이를 위해 스스로 짊어진 의무를 한 번도 게을리하지 않고, 그는 늘 친구 곁에 있었어. 그는 한순간도 친구를 떠나지 않았지. 친구를 북돋고 기운 차리게 하고, 필요한 것을 챙겨 주고, 할 수 있는 한 최대로 부축하고 달래 주는 일이, 이제 그가 끊임없이 매달리는 일이 되었어.\n\n그들은 작은 농가에 소박한 거처를 구했어. 둘레에는 풀밭이 펼쳐져 있었는데, 니콜라스가 어릴 적 명랑한 학교 친구들과 어울려 자주 뛰어놀던 곳이었지. 그들은 거기서 머물기로 했어.\n\n처음에 스마이크는 짧은 거리쯤은 걸어 다닐 만큼 기운이 있었어. 니콜라스가 부축해 주는 것 말고는 다른 도움이 필요하지 않았지. 그 무렵 그가 무엇보다 좋아한 건, 옛날 니콜라스에게 가장 친숙했던 곳들을 찾아가 보는 일이었어. 니콜라스는 이 바람을 들어주었어. 그렇게 하면 병든 소년이 지루한 시간을 잊고, 게다가 뒤에 두고두고 생각하고 이야기할 거리가 늘 생긴다는 걸 알고는 기뻤거든. 그래서 니콜라스는 그런 곳들을 날마다 거니는 산책길로 삼았어. 작은 조랑말 수레에 친구를 태우고 이곳저곳 데려다주기도 하고, 함께 그 옛 추억의 자리들 사이를 천천히 거닐 때면 팔로 부축해 주기도 했지. 또 가장 고요하고 아름다운 곳에서는 햇살 속에 한참을 머물며 작별의 눈길을 길게 보내곤 했어.\n\n이런 때면 니콜라스는 거의 저도 모르게 옛 추억의 정에 빠져들어, 어떤 나무를 가리키곤 했어. 둥지 속 새끼 새들을 엿보려고 백 번도 더 기어올랐던 나무 말이야. 그리고 그 가지에서 어린 케이트한테 소리치곤 했다고도 했지. 케이트는 밑에 서서 오빠가 올라간 높이에 겁을 잔뜩 먹으면서도, 한편으로는 너무나 감탄한 나머지 더 높이 올라가라고 부추겼다는 거야. 옛 집도 있었어. 그들은 날마다 그 앞을 지났는데, 여름 아침이면 햇살이 흘러 들어와 니콜라스를 깨우던 그 자그마한 창을 올려다보곤 했지. 그때는 다 여름 아침뿐이었어. 정원 담을 기어올라 안을 들여다보면, 니콜라스의 눈에 바로 그 장미 덤불이 보였어. 어떤 어린 짝꿍이 케이트한테 선물로 보낸 걸, 케이트가 제 손으로 심은 것이었지. 남매가 함께 들꽃을 따곤 하던 산울타리도 있었고, 자주 거닐던 푸른 들판과 그늘진 오솔길도 있었어. 가까운 곳에는 골목 하나, 시냇물 하나, 작은 숲 하나, 오두막 하나도 어린 시절의 어떤 일과 얽히지 않은 게 없었어. 그러면 그 기억이 마음속에 되살아났지. 어린 시절의 일들이 다 그렇듯, 그 자체로는 별것도 아니었어. 말 한마디, 웃음 한 번, 눈길 한 번, 사소한 속상함, 스쳐 간 생각이나 두려움 같은 것이었지. 그런데도 일 년 전의 가장 모진 시련이나 가장 깊은 슬픔보다 훨씬 더 강하고 또렷하게 새겨져, 더 잘 기억에 남아 있었어.\n\n이런 나들이 가운데 한 번은 그들이 니콜라스 아버지의 무덤이 있는 교회 묘지를 지나게 되었어. \"여기서도,\" 니콜라스가 나직이 말했어. \"우리는 죽음이 뭔지도 모를 때부터 여길 어슬렁거렸지. 누구의 유해가 이 아래 묻힐지는 꿈에도 모르고 말이야. 그러다 이 고요함이 신기해서 쉬려고 앉아서는 숨죽여 소곤거리곤 했어. 한번은 케이트가 없어졌는데, 한 시간을 헛되이 찾아 헤맨 끝에 발견했지. 아버지 무덤에 그늘을 드리운 저 나무 아래에서 새근새근 잠들어 있었어. 아버지는 케이트를 무척 아끼셨어. 잠든 케이트를 팔에 안아 올리시며 말씀하셨지. 당신이 죽거든 사랑하는 어린 딸이 머리를 뉘었던 그 자리에 묻히고 싶다고. 봐, 그 소원은 잊히지 않았어.\"\n\n그때는 더 이상 별다른 말이 오가지 않았어. 그런데 그날 밤, 니콜라스가 침대 곁에 앉아 있는데, 잠든 듯하던 스마이크가 화들짝 깨어났어. 그러고는 니콜라스의 손에 제 손을 얹고, 얼굴 위로 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며, 한 가지만 굳게 약속해 달라고 빌었어.\n\n\"그게 뭔데?\" 니콜라스가 다정하게 말했어. \"내가 들어줄 수 있는 거라면, 아니 들어줄 가망이라도 있는 거라면, 들어줄 거란 거 너도 알잖아.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 들어줄 거예요.\" 그가 대답했어. \"내가 죽으면 그 나무 가까이에 묻어 주겠다고 약속해 줘요. 오늘 우리가 본 그 나무 말이에요, 무덤을 낼 수 있는 한 최대한 가까이요.\"\n\n니콜라스는 약속했어. 그 약속에 보탤 말은 많지 않았지만, 그 말들은 엄숙하고 진심이 담겨 있었지. 가엾은 친구는 니콜라스의 손을 제 손에 쥔 채로 잠을 청하려는 듯 돌아누웠어. 그런데 흐느낌을 삼키는 소리가 났어. 그리고 그 손은 그가 잠들어 천천히 쥔 손을 풀기까지, 한 번도, 두 번도, 세 번도 아니게 거듭 꼭 쥐어졌지.\n\n두 주가 지나자, 그는 너무 아파서 돌아다닐 수도 없게 되었어. 한두 번 니콜라스가 베개로 받쳐 그를 수레에 태워 데리고 나가 보았지만, 수레의 흔들림이 그에게는 고통스러웠고, 그 바람에 기절하는 일이 생겼지. 약해질 대로 약해진 그에게는 위험한 일이었어. 집에는 낡은 긴 의자가 하나 있었는데, 낮 동안 그가 가장 좋아하는 쉼터였어. 그래서 해가 나고 날이 따뜻하면, 니콜라스는 그 의자를 바로 옆에 있는 작은 과수원으로 밀고 갔지. 그리고 친구를 잘 싸매서 안고 나가서는, 거기 앉아 둘이서 몇 시간씩 함께 보내곤 했어.\n\n이렇게 보내던 어느 날, 한 가지 일이 벌어졌어. 그때 니콜라스는 그게 병으로 흐트러진 머릿속이 빚어낸 헛것에 지나지 않는다고 굳게 믿었지. 그런데 나중에 그게 실제로 진짜 있었던 일임을 알게 될, 너무도 충분한 이유가 생겼어.\n\n니콜라스는 스마이크를 품에 안아 데리고 나왔어. 가엾기도 하지! 이제는 아이라도 그를 안고 다닐 수 있을 정도였거든. 해 지는 걸 보여 주려고 데리고 나와서, 긴 의자를 잘 놓아 준 다음 그 곁에 자리를 잡았지. 그는 전날 밤을 꼬박 새운 데다 몸도 마음도 몹시 지쳐 있어서, 차츰 잠이 들고 말았어.\n\n눈을 감은 지 채 오 분도 안 됐을 텐데, 비명 소리에 깨어났어. 갑자기 깨어난 사람이 으레 느끼는 그런 두려움에 휩싸여 벌떡 일어난 그는, 깜짝 놀라고 말았지. 그가 돌보던 친구가 안간힘을 써서 일어나 앉아 있었거든. 눈은 거의 눈구멍에서 튀어나올 듯했고, 이마에는 차가운 식은땀이 맺혀 있었으며, 온몸을 뒤흔드는 떨림에 사로잡혀 그에게 살려 달라고 외치고 있었어.\n\n\"맙소사, 이게 무슨 일이야?\" 니콜라스가 그에게로 몸을 숙이며 말했어. \"진정해. 꿈을 꾼 거야.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니에요!\" 스마이크가 니콜라스에게 매달리며 외쳤어. \"꼭 붙잡아 줘요. 날 놓지 마요. 저기, 저기요. 나무 뒤예요!\"\n\n니콜라스는 그의 눈길을 따라갔어. 그 눈길은 조금 전 니콜라스가 막 일어난 그 의자 뒤편 멀찍한 곳을 향해 있었지. 그런데 거기엔 아무것도 없었어.\n\n\"이건 그냥 네 착각이야.\" 니콜라스가 그를 달래려 애쓰며 말했어. \"정말이지, 다른 건 없어.\"\n\n\"난 분명히 봤어요. 지금 형을 보는 것만큼이나 똑똑히 봤다고요.\" 그가 대답했어. \"오! 날 형 곁에 두겠다고 말해 줘요. 한순간도 날 떠나지 않겠다고 맹세해 줘요!\"\n\n\"내가 너를 떠난 적 있어?\" 니콜라스가 되받았어. \"다시 누워. 자! 봐, 내가 여기 있잖아. 자, 말해 봐. 그게 뭐였는데?\"\n\n\"기억나요?\" 스마이크가 낮은 목소리로, 겁에 질려 주위를 두리번거리며 말했어. \"나를 학교로 처음 데려간 그 남자 이야기, 내가 형한테 했던 거 기억나요?\"\n\n\"그럼, 기억하지.\"\n\n\"방금 저 나무 쪽으로 눈을 들었어요. 저기 줄기가 굵은 저 나무요. 그런데 거기, 그 남자가 나한테 눈을 박고 서 있었다고요!\"\n\n\"잠깐만 생각해 봐.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"설령 그자가 살아 있어서, 큰길에서 이렇게 멀찍이 떨어진 이런 외딴곳을 어슬렁거리고 있다고 치자. 이렇게 오랜 세월이 지났는데, 그 남자를 다시 알아볼 수 있을 것 같아?\"\n\n\"어디서든, 어떤 옷차림이든 알아봐요.\" 스마이크가 대답했어. \"그런데 방금 그자가 지팡이에 기대서 나를 보고 서 있었어요. 내가 기억한다고 형한테 말한 그 모습 그대로요. 걷느라 먼지투성이였고, 허름한 차림이었어요. 옷이 다 해진 것 같았어요. 그런데 그 사람을 보자마자, 그 비 오던 밤이며, 나를 떠날 때 그 사람 얼굴이며, 내가 혼자 남겨졌던 응접실이며, 거기 있던 사람들이며, 죄다 한꺼번에 되살아나는 것 같았어요. 내가 자기를 본 걸 알아채자 겁먹은 얼굴이 됐어요. 흠칫하더니 슬그머니 물러났거든요. 난 낮에는 그 사람을 생각하고, 밤에는 그 사람 꿈을 꿔요. 아주 어린아이였을 때도 그 사람은 내 잠 속을 들여다봤어요. 그리고 그 뒤로 줄곧, 방금처럼 내 잠 속을 들여다봐 왔어요.\"\n\n니콜라스는 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 말과 이치를 다 동원해서, 이 겁에 질린 친구에게 그건 상상이 자기를 속인 것이며, 꿈이 만들어 낸 모습과 봤다고 믿는 그 남자가 이렇게 닮았다는 게 오히려 그 증거라는 걸 납득시키려 애썼지. 하지만 다 헛수고였어. 한동안 그를 달래서 그 집 주인들에게 잠시 맡겨 둘 수 있게 되자, 니콜라스는 낯선 사람이 보인 적 없는지 꼼꼼히 캐물었어. 그리고 자기도 직접 나무 뒤며, 과수원 곳곳이며, 바로 옆에 붙은 땅이며, 사람이 숨을 만한 가까운 곳은 죄다 뒤졌지. 하지만 다 헛수고였어. 처음 짐작이 맞았다고 확신한 니콜라스는, 스마이크의 두려움을 가라앉히는 데 매달렸어. 한참 만에 어느 정도는 성공했지만, 그의 마음에 박힌 인상까지 지우지는 못했지. 그는 거듭, 또 거듭, 더없이 엄숙하고 간절하게, 자기가 말한 그것을 분명히 봤으며, 그게 진짜였다는 확신은 무엇으로도 절대 지울 수 없다고 단언했거든.\n\n이제 니콜라스는 알기 시작했어. 희망은 사라졌고, 가난을 함께 나누고 더 나은 운수도 함께 누렸던 이 동반자에게 세상이 빠르게 닫혀 가고 있다는 걸 말이야. 고통도 거의 없고, 괴로움도 거의 없었지만, 다시 기운을 차리는 일도, 안간힘도, 살려는 발버둥도 없었어. 그는 더할 나위 없이 야위고 쇠약해졌어. 목소리는 어찌나 낮게 잠겼는지, 말을 해도 거의 알아들을 수가 없었지. 자연의 힘은 완전히 바닥나 버렸고, 그는 죽으려고 자리에 누운 거였어.\n\n화창하고 온화한 어느 가을날, 모든 게 고요하고 평화롭던 그날, 부드럽고 향긋한 바람이 조용한 방의 열린 창으로 살그머니 스며들고, 나뭇잎이 가볍게 바스락거리는 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 들리지 않던 그날, 니콜라스는 침대 곁 늘 앉던 자리에 앉아 있었어. 그리고 그때가 거의 다가왔음을 알았지. 어찌나 고요했던지, 그는 이따금 귀를 가까이 숙여 잠든 친구의 숨소리에 귀를 기울이곤 했어. 아직 거기 생명이 있는지, 이 세상에서는 다시 깨어날 수 없는 그 깊은 잠에 빠져 버린 건 아닌지 확인이라도 하려는 듯이 말이야.\n\n니콜라스가 그러고 있는데, 감겨 있던 두 눈이 떠지더니, 창백한 얼굴 위로 잔잔한 미소가 번졌어.\n\n\"다행이다!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"잠을 자고 나니 한결 나아졌구나.\"\n\n\"정말 좋은 꿈을 꿨어요.\" 그가 대답했어. \"참 즐겁고 행복한 꿈을요!\"\n\n\"무슨 꿈?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n죽어 가는 소년은 그에게로 돌아누워, 그의 목에 팔을 두르고 대답했어. \"난 곧 거기 가게 될 거예요!\"\n\n잠깐 말이 없다가, 그가 다시 입을 열었어.\n\n\"난 죽는 게 두렵지 않아요.\" 그가 말했어. \"아주 만족스러워요. 이 침대에서 멀쩡하게 일어날 수 있다 해도, 이제는 그러고 싶지 않을 것 같다는 생각마저 들어요. 형이 우리가 다시 만나게 될 거라고 자주 말해 줬잖아요. 요즘 들어 정말 자주요. 그리고 지금은 그게 진실이라는 게 너무 강하게 느껴져서, 형과 헤어지는 것까지도 견딜 수 있을 것 같아요.\"\n\n떨리는 목소리와 눈물 어린 눈, 그리고 이 마지막 말과 함께 팔을 더 꼭 끌어안는 모습은, 그 말들이 말하는 이의 가슴을 얼마나 가득 채우고 있는지를 보여 주었어. 그리고 그 말을 듣는 이의 가슴을 얼마나 깊이 울렸는지도 드러나 있었지.\n\n\"네 말이 맞아.\" 마침내 니콜라스가 입을 열었어. \"그리고 내게 큰 위로가 된다, 친구야. 할 수 있다면, 네가 행복하다는 말을 들려주렴.\"\n\n\"먼저 말해 둘 게 있어요. 형한테 비밀을 두면 안 되니까요. 이런 때 형은 나를 탓하지 않을 거예요. 알아요.\"\n\n\"내가 너를 탓한다고!\" 니콜라스가 소리쳤어.\n\n\"형은 절대 안 그럴 거예요. 형은 내가 왜 그렇게 변했는지, 그리고, 그리고 왜 그렇게 자주 혼자 앉아 있었는지 물었죠. 그 까닭을 말해 줄까요?\"\n\n\"네가 괴롭다면 안 해도 돼.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"난 그저 할 수 있다면 너를 더 행복하게 해 주려고 물어본 것뿐이야.\"\n\n\"알아요. 그때 그렇게 느꼈어요.\" 그는 친구를 더 가까이 끌어당겼어. \"형은 날 용서해 줄 거예요. 어쩔 수가 없었어요. 난 그분을 행복하게 할 수 있다면 죽어도 좋았을 텐데, 그걸 보는 게 가슴이 찢어졌어요. 그분이 그녀를 끔찍이 아낀다는 걸 난 알아요. 오! 누가 나만큼 그걸 빨리 알아챘겠어요?\"\n\n이어진 말들은 가냘프고 희미하게 새어 나왔고, 긴 침묵으로 자꾸 끊겼어. 하지만 그 말에서 니콜라스는 처음으로 알게 되었지. 죽어 가는 이 소년이, 단 하나에 사로잡힌 가망 없고 은밀한 사랑에 온 본성을 쏟아붓는 그 열정으로, 누이 케이트를 사랑했다는 걸 말이야.\n\n그는 케이트의 머리카락 한 타래를 구해 두었더랬어. 그녀가 매던 가느다란 리본 한두 개에 싸서 가슴에 걸고 있었지. 그는 자기가 죽거든 니콜라스가 그걸 떼어 내어, 자기 눈 말고는 아무 눈에도 보이지 않게 해 달라고 빌었어. 그리고 자기가 관에 누여 땅에 묻히려 할 때, 다시 자기 목에 그걸 걸어 달라고 했지. 무덤 속에서도 자기와 함께 있도록 말이야.\n\n니콜라스는 무릎을 꿇고 그에게 이 약속을 했어. 그리고 그가 가리켰던 그 자리에 묻어 주겠다고 다시 한번 약속했지. 그들은 서로 끌어안고, 뺨에 입을 맞추었어.\n\n\"이제,\" 그가 가만히 중얼거렸어. \"난 행복해요.\"\n\n그는 가벼운 잠에 빠졌다가, 깨어나서는 아까처럼 미소 지었어. 그러고는 아름다운 정원 이야기를 했지. 그 정원이 자기 앞에 펼쳐져 있다고, 거기에는 남자며 여자며 수많은 아이들이 가득한데, 모두의 얼굴에 빛이 어려 있다고 했어. 그러더니 거기가 에덴이라고 속삭였어. 그렇게 그는 숨을 거두었어.\n\n# CHAPTER 59\n\n랄프는 혼자 앉아 있었어. 늘 끼니를 때우고, 돈벌이가 되는 일로 밖에 나갈 일이 없는 밤이면 앉아 있곤 하던 그 외딴 방에서 말이야. 그 앞에는 손도 안 댄 아침상이 놓여 있었어. 그리고 그의 손가락이 탁자 위를 안절부절 두드리는 곳 가까이에 회중시계가 놓여 있었지. 여러 해 동안 그 시계를 주머니에 넣고 또박또박한 걸음으로 아래층으로 내려가 그날의 일을 시작하던 시각이 한참 지났어. 그런데 그는 그 단조로운 경고에도, 눈앞의 음식과 마실 것에도 거의 신경을 쓰지 않았어. 한 손으로 머리를 받친 채, 우울한 눈을 바닥에 떨군 그대로 가만히 있었지.\n\n날마다 돈을 좇는 일에 그토록 한결같고 변함없던 사람이, 늘 지키던 규칙적인 습관에서 이렇게 벗어났다는 건, 그것만으로도 이 고리대금업자가 멀쩡하지 않다는 걸 거의 말해 주는 셈이었어. 그가 마음이든 몸이든 어딘가 탈이 나 있다는 것, 그것도 이런 사람을 이만큼 흔들어 놓을 정도면 결코 가벼운 탈이 아니라는 것은, 그의 핼쑥한 얼굴과 지친 기색, 그리고 푹 꺼지고 생기 없는 눈을 보면 충분히 알 수 있었어. 그러다 그는 마침내 흠칫 놀라며 눈을 들어 황급히 주위를 둘러봤어. 갑자기 잠에서 깨어 자기가 있는 곳을 얼른 알아보지 못하는 사람처럼 말이야.\n\n\"대체 뭐지,\" 그가 말했어. \"내 위에 드리워져서 도무지 떨쳐 낼 수가 없는 이게 뭐냔 말이야? 나는 한 번도 나 자신을 호강시킨 적이 없으니, 병이 날 까닭이 없는데. 나는 한 번도 시무룩하게 풀이 죽거나, 헛된 망상에 휘둘린 적이 없는데. 그렇지만 쉬지를 못하는데 사람이 무슨 수가 있겠어?\"\n\n그는 손으로 이마를 짓눌렀어.\n\n\"밤이 오고 또 가는데, 나는 도무지 쉬질 못해. 잠이 든다 한들, 그 잠이 무슨 쉼이 되겠어? 늘 같은 그 지긋지긋한 얼굴들이 떼를 지어 내 둘레로 몰려드는 꿈에 시달리는데. 같은 그 지긋지긋한 사람들이 온갖 모습으로, 내가 말하고 행하는 모든 일에 끼어들고, 늘 나를 무너뜨리는 그런 꿈에 말이야. 깨어 있은들 무슨 쉼이 있겠어? 이 무거운 그림자가, 정체가 뭔지도 모를 이게 끊임없이 나를 따라붙는데. 정체를 모른다는 게 가장 끔찍한 점이지. 나는 쉬어야 해. 하룻밤만이라도 끊기지 않고 푹 쉬면, 다시 사람다워질 텐데.\"\n\n말을 하면서 그는 음식 보는 것조차 진저리가 난다는 듯 탁자를 밀쳤어. 그러다 시계에 손이 닿았지. 바늘은 거의 정오를 가리키고 있었어.\n\n\"이거 이상한데!\" 그가 말했어. \"정오인데, 노그스가 안 와! 무슨 술주정뱅이 싸움에 휘말려서 못 오는 거지? 이번엔 뭐라도 내놓겠어. 그 끔찍한 손해를 보고 난 뒤지만, 돈이라도 내놓겠어. 그 녀석이 술집 싸움에서 누구를 칼로 찌르거나, 남의 집에 침입하거나, 소매치기를 하거나, 다리에 쇠고랑을 차고 끌려갈 짓을 해서 내게서 사라져 주기만 한다면 말이야. 더 좋기로는, 내가 미끼를 슬쩍 던져서 그 녀석이 나를 털도록 꾀어낼 수 있다면 좋겠어. 가져가는 거야 얼마든지 가져가라지, 내가 그 녀석한테 법의 심판을 안길 수만 있다면. 그 녀석은 배신자거든, 정말이야! 어떻게, 언제, 어디서 그랬는지는 모르겠지만, 짐작은 가.\"\n\n그는 다시 반 시간을 기다리고 나서, 집을 봐 주는 여자를 노그스의 하숙집으로 보냈어. 그가 아픈 건지, 왜 오지도 않고 기별도 없는 건지 알아보라고 말이야. 여자는 답을 가지고 돌아왔어. 그가 밤새 집에 들어오지 않았고, 그에 대해 아무것도 아는 사람이 없다는 거였어.\n\n\"그런데 어떤 신사분이 계세요, 나리.\" 여자가 말했어. \"아래층에요. 제가 들어올 때 문가에 서 계셨는데, 그분이 말씀하시기를······.\"\n\n\"뭐라고 하던가?\" 랄프가 그녀를 향해 화를 내며 따졌어. \"아무도 안 만난다고 했잖아.\"\n\n\"그분 말씀이,\" 여자가 그의 거친 말투에 주눅이 들어 대답했어. \"아주 특별한 일로 오셨다고, 핑계를 댈 수 없는 일이라고요. 그래서 제 생각엔 혹시 그게······.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐, 대체 무슨 일이라는 거야?\" 랄프가 말했어. \"넌 내 일을 캐고 넘겨짚고 다니는구나, 그런 거야?\"\n\n\"아유, 아니에요, 나리! 나리께서 마음 졸이시는 게 보여서, 혹시 노그스 씨 일인가 했어요. 그게 다예요.\"\n\n\"내가 마음 졸이는 게 보였다고!\" 랄프가 중얼거렸어. \"이제 다들 나를 감시하는군. 그 사람은 어디 있나? 내가 아직 안 내려왔다고 한 건 아니겠지?\"\n\n여자는 그가 작은 사무실에 있다고 대답했어. 주인이 바쁘다고 말해 두긴 했지만, 전갈은 전하겠다고 했다는 거야.\n\n\"좋아,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"내가 만나지. 넌 부엌으로 가서 거기 있어라. 내 말 알아들었나?\"\n\n풀려난 게 반가운 여자는 얼른 사라졌어. 랄프는 마음을 추슬렀어. 그리고 온 힘을 다해 평소의 태도를 최대한 끌어올린 채 계단을 내려갔어. 자물쇠에 손을 얹고 잠깐 멈춰 섰다가, 그는 노그스의 방으로 들어가 찰스 치어리블 씨와 마주 섰어.\n\n살아 있는 모든 사람 중에서, 이 사람이야말로 그가 어느 때든 가장 만나고 싶지 않은 축에 드는 사람이었어. 그런데 지금은 그가 니콜라스의 후원자이자 보호자로밖에 보이지 않으니, 차라리 유령을 보는 편이 나았을 거야. 그렇지만 이 만남이 그에게 한 가지 좋은 효과를 주긴 했어. 그 즉시 잠들어 있던 모든 기운을 깨워 일으킨 거야. 여러 해 동안 그의 가슴속에 무럭무럭 자라며 자리 잡고 있던 격정에 다시 불을 붙였지. 그의 분노와 증오와 악의를 모조리 불러냈어. 그의 입술에는 비웃음을, 이마에는 험상궂은 주름을 되살려 놨지. 그리고 겉모습만큼은 다시, 그토록 많은 사람들이 쓰라리게 기억하는 그 랄프 니클비로 만들어 놨어.\n\n\"흠!\" 랄프가 문가에 멈춰 서서 말했어. \"이거 뜻밖의 영광이군요, 선생.\"\n\n\"반갑지 않은 영광이지요.\" 찰스 형제가 말했어. \"반갑지 않다는 거, 나도 압니다.\"\n\n\"사람들이 그러더군요, 선생은 진실 그 자체라고.\" 랄프가 대꾸했어. \"어쨌든 지금 진실을 말씀하고 계시니, 나도 부정하지 않겠습니다. 적어도 이 영광이 뜻밖인 만큼이나 반갑지 않은 건 사실이니까요. 그 이상은 뭐라 할 말이 없군요.\"\n\n\"분명히 말씀드리자면, 선생······.\" 찰스 형제가 입을 열었어.\n\n\"분명히 말씀드리자면, 선생,\" 랄프가 가로막았어. \"나는 이 만남이 짧게 끝나기를, 시작한 자리에서 그대로 끝나기를 바랍니다. 무슨 얘길 하시려는지 짐작이 갑니다만, 나는 듣지 않겠습니다. 선생은 솔직한 걸 좋아하신다죠? 자, 솔직히 말씀드렸습니다. 보시다시피 저기 문이 있습니다. 우리가 가는 길은 아주 다른 방향이지요. 부디 선생 갈 길이나 가시고, 나는 조용히 내 길을 가게 내버려 두십시오.\"\n\n\"조용히!\" 찰스 형제가 부드럽게 되뇌었어. 나무람보다는 안쓰러움이 더 어린 눈으로 그를 바라보면서 말이야. \"조용히 자기 길을 가겠다니!\"\n\n\"내 뜻을 거슬러 가면서까지 내 집에 머무르시진 않겠지요, 설마.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"아니면, 선생이 무슨 말을 하든 귀를 닫아 버린 사람, 절대로 듣지 않겠다고 단단히 작정한 사람한테 무슨 영향을 줄 수 있다고 기대하시기도 어려울 테고요.\"\n\n\"니클비 씨, 선생.\" 찰스 형제가 대답했어. 아까처럼 부드럽게, 그러나 단호하기도 한 말투였지. \"나는 내 뜻을 거슬러 여기 왔습니다. 정말이지 괴롭고 마음 아프게도, 내 뜻을 거슬러서요. 이 집에 와 본 건 처음입니다. 그리고 솔직히 말씀드리자면, 이 집은 편하지도 마음 놓이지도 않아서, 다시는 오고 싶지 않군요. 선생은 내가 무슨 얘길 하러 왔는지 짐작 못 하십니다. 정말로 못 하세요. 분명합니다. 만약 짐작했다면 선생 태도가 아주 달랐을 테니까요.\"\n\n랄프는 날카롭게 그를 흘끔거렸어. 그렇지만 이 정직한 늙은 상인의 맑은 눈과 환한 얼굴은 표정 하나 바뀌지 않았어. 거리낌 없이 그의 눈길을 맞받았지.\n\n\"계속해도 될까요?\" 치어리블 씨가 말했어.\n\n\"아, 그러시죠, 정 그러시다면.\" 랄프가 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸했어. \"여기 말씀하실 벽이 있고, 선생, 책상 하나에 의자가 둘 있지요. 더없이 귀 기울여 줄 청중이고, 선생 말을 끊는 일도 절대 없을 겁니다. 어서 계속하시죠. 내 집을 선생 집처럼 쓰세요. 그러면 아마 내가 산책에서 돌아올 때쯤이면, 하실 말씀을 다 끝내시고 내게 집을 돌려주시겠지요.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고서 그는 외투 단추를 채웠어. 그러고는 복도로 돌아서 모자를 내려 들었지. 노신사가 뒤따라오며 막 입을 열려는데, 랄프가 짜증스럽게 손을 내저으며 말했어.\n\n\"한마디도 마세요. 분명히 말씀드립니다, 선생, 한마디도요. 아무리 덕망이 높으셔도, 선생은 아직 천사가 아니잖습니까. 사람들이 원하든 말든 남의 집에 나타나서, 듣기 싫다는 귀에 말을 쏟아붓는 그런 천사 말이오. 벽에다 설교하시라니까요. 내가 아니라!\"\n\n\"내가 천사가 아닌 거야, 하늘도 알지요.\" 찰스 형제가 고개를 저으며 대답했어. \"잘못도 저지르고 모자란 사람이지요. 그렇지만 모든 사람이 천사와 똑같이 가지고 있는 자질이 하나 있어요. 마음만 먹으면 누구나 베풀 수 있는 복된 기회 말입니다. 바로 자비지요. 나를 여기 데려온 건 자비라는 심부름입니다. 부디 그걸 다하게 해 주세요.\"\n\n\"나는 자비 따위 베풀지 않습니다.\" 랄프가 의기양양한 미소를 지으며 받아쳤어. \"그리고 구하지도 않고요. 선생의 어린애 같은 잘 속아 넘어가는 마음을 이용해 먹은 그 녀석을 위해서라면, 내게서 자비를 구하지 마세요. 내가 할 수 있는 최악의 일을 각오하라고 하십시오.\"\n\n\"그 애가 선생 손에서 자비를 구한다고요!\" 늙은 상인이 발끈해서 외쳤어. \"선생이야말로 그 애한테 자비를 구하셔야지요, 선생. 그 애한테요. 들을 수 있는 지금 내 말을 안 들으시겠다면, 들어야만 할 때 들으세요. 아니면 내가 할 말을 미리 헤아려서, 우리가 다시는 마주칠 일 없도록 손을 쓰시든가요. 선생 조카는 고귀한 청년입니다, 선생. 정직하고 고귀한 청년이에요. 선생이 어떤 사람인지는, 니클비 씨, 말하지 않겠습니다. 그렇지만 선생이 무슨 짓을 했는지는, 나도 압니다. 자, 선생, 요즘 손대신 그 일을 밀고 나가다가 막혀서 더는 못 하시겠거든, 나와 내 동생 네드, 그리고 팀 링킨워터한테 오세요, 선생. 우리가 풀어 설명해 드리지요. 어서 오세요, 안 그러면 너무 늦을지 모릅니다. 그땐 좀 더 거칠게, 좀 덜 점잖게 설명을 듣게 되실지도 모르니까요. 그리고 절대 잊지 마세요, 선생. 내가 오늘 아침 여기 온 건 선생을 향한 자비 때문이고, 나는 아직도 그 같은 마음으로 선생과 이야기할 준비가 되어 있다는 걸요.\"\n\n이 말을 아주 힘주어, 또 격앙된 감정으로 내뱉고서, 찰스 형제는 챙 넓은 모자를 썼어. 그러고는 다른 말 없이 랄프 니클비를 지나쳐, 잰걸음으로 거리로 나가 버렸어. 랄프는 그의 뒷모습을 바라봤어. 그렇지만 한동안 움직이지도 입을 열지도 않았지. 그러다 그는, 거의 멍하니 넋이 나간 듯한 그 침묵을 비웃는 웃음 한 번으로 깨뜨렸어.\n\n\"이건,\" 그가 말했어. \"하도 터무니없으니, 요즘 내 잠을 그렇게 망쳐 놓은 그 꿈들 중 하나가 분명해. 나를 향한 자비라니! 흥! 저 늙은 멍청이가 미쳐 버렸군.\"\n\n이렇게 비웃고 깔보는 투로 내뱉긴 했지만, 분명한 건, 랄프는 곰곰이 생각할수록 점점 더 불안해졌다는 거야. 그리고 무언가 막연한 근심과 두려움에 점점 더 시달렸어. 시간이 흘러도 뉴먼 노그스의 소식이 오지 않자, 그 근심은 점점 더 커졌지. 그는 오후 늦게까지 기다렸어. 온갖 불안과 의구심에, 또 마지막으로 만났을 때 조카가 했던 그 경고가 떠올라 괴로워하면서 말이야. 그 경고가 사실이라는 또 다른 증거가 이런 모습으로, 또 저런 모습으로 머릿속에 떠올라 끊임없이 그를 따라붙었거든. 그는 집을 나섰어. 그리고 자기도 왜인지 잘 모른 채, 그저 의심과 동요에 사로잡힌 기분에 이끌려 스놀리의 집으로 향했지. 그의 아내가 나왔어. 랄프는 그녀에게 남편이 집에 있느냐고 물었어.\n\n\"없어요.\" 그녀가 쏘아붙였어. \"정말로 없어요. 그리고 한참 동안은 집에 안 들어올 것 같네요. 그게 답이에요.\"\n\n\"내가 누군지 알겠소?\" 랄프가 물었어.\n\n\"아, 그럼요, 잘 알지요. 어쩌면 너무 잘 알아서 탈이지요. 그리고 우리 남편도 어쩌면 그럴 거고요. 이런 말을 해야 한다니 참 안타깝네요.\"\n\n\"방금 길을 건너오면서 위층 창문 가리개 너머로 그를 봤으니, 일이 있어서 그와 얘기를 하고 싶다고 전하시오.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"알아들었소?\"\n\n\"알아들었어요.\" 스놀리 부인이 그 부탁에는 더 신경도 쓰지 않고 대꾸했어.\n\n\"이 여자가 찬송가랑 성경 구절이나 들먹이는 위선자인 건 알고 있었지만,\" 랄프가 슬쩍 옆을 지나치며 말했어. \"술까지 마시는 줄은 미처 몰랐는걸.\"\n\n\"멈춰요! 여긴 못 들어와요.\" 스놀리 씨의 아내가 우람한 몸을 문간에 들이밀며 가로막았어. \"당신은 이미 우리 남편한테 일 얘기로 할 만큼은 다 했어요. 당신이랑 손잡고 당신 꿍꿍이를 받들다간 어떻게 되겠냐고, 내가 늘 그이한테 말해 왔다고요. 그 위조 편지를 꾸민 게 당신 아니면 그 교장이에요. 둘 중 하나, 아니면 둘이 짜고 한 짓이지! 그건 기억해 둬요! 그건 우리 남편이 한 짓이 아니니까, 그이한테 뒤집어씌우지 마요.\"\n\n\"입 다물어, 이 못된 것아.\" 랄프가 겁먹은 듯 주위를 둘러보며 말했어.\n\n\"흥, 나는 언제 입을 다물고 언제 말을 해야 하는지 잘 알아요, 니클비 씨.\" 부인이 받아쳤어. \"다른 사람들도 자기 입 다물 때를 잘 아는지나 조심하시지.\"\n\n\"이 화냥년아,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"네 남편이 멍청하게도 너한테 비밀을 털어놨다면, 잘 간직이나 해. 간직하라고, 이 마귀할멈 같은 것아!\"\n\n\"어쩌면 우리 남편 비밀이라기보다 남의 비밀일지도 모르죠.\" 여자가 받아쳤어. \"우리 남편 비밀이라기보다 당신 비밀 말이에요. 그 험상궂은 눈초리, 나한테 보내지 마요! 어쩌면 그거, 나중에 다 필요할 테니까. 아껴 두는 게 좋을걸요.\"\n\n\"그래,\" 랄프가 끓어오르는 화를 최대한 억누르며, 그녀의 손목을 꽉 움켜쥐고 말했어. \"네 남편한테 가서, 내가 그가 집에 있는 걸 알고 있으니 꼭 만나야겠다고 전하겠어? 그리고 너랑 그가 이 새로운 행동거지로 대체 뭘 어쩌겠다는 건지 나한테 말해 주겠어?\"\n\n\"싫어요.\" 여자가 거칠게 손을 뿌리치며 대답했어. \"둘 다 안 해요.\"\n\n\"나한테 대들겠다는 거냐, 그래?\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"네.\" 답이 돌아왔어. \"그러겠다고요.\"\n\n한순간 랄프는 손을 치켜들었어. 마치 그녀를 때리려는 것처럼 말이야. 그렇지만 스스로 멈추더니, 이 일을 잊지 않겠다고 그녀에게 다짐이라도 하듯 고개를 끄덕이며 뭐라고 중얼거리고는, 자리를 떴어.\n\n거기서 그는 곧장 스퀴어스 씨가 드나들던 여관으로 갔어. 그가 마지막으로 거기 들른 게 언제인지 물어봤지. 일이 잘됐든 안 됐든, 이쯤이면 그가 임무를 마치고 돌아와 다 무사하다고 안심시켜 줄 수 있지 않을까 하는 막연한 기대였어. 그런데 스퀴어스 씨는 열흘째 거기 나타나지 않았어. 사람들이 그에 대해 해 줄 수 있는 말이라곤, 그가 짐과 셈할 돈만 남겨 두고 갔다는 게 다였지.\n\n천 가지 두려움과 억측에 마음이 어지러워진 랄프는, 스퀴어스가 스놀리를 의심하고 있는지, 아니면 어떤 식으로든 이 달라진 행동에 한패로 끼어 있는지 알아내야겠다고 작정했어. 그래서 그는 극단적인 모험을 무릅쓰기로 했지. 램버스의 하숙집으로 그를 찾아가, 거기서라도 만나 보기로 한 거야. 이 목적에 골몰한 채, 한순간의 지체도 견디기 힘든 그런 기분으로, 그는 곧장 그곳으로 향했어. 그리고 들은 설명만으로도 그의 방 위치를 훤히 꿰고 있었기에, 살금살금 위층으로 올라가 문을 가만히 두드렸어.\n\n한 번, 두 번, 세 번을 두드려도, 아니 열두 번을 두드려도, 안에 아무도 없다는 사실을 랄프는 도무지 받아들이려 하지 않았어. 그는 그가 자고 있을지도 모른다고 생각했어. 그리고 귀를 기울이더니, 그가 숨 쉬는 소리가 들리는 것 같다고 거의 자기 자신을 믿게 만들 정도였지. 그가 거기 없는 게 확실해진 뒤에도, 그는 끈기 있게 부서진 계단에 앉아 기다렸어. 그가 무슨 자질구레한 심부름이라도 나간 거라서 곧 돌아올 게 분명하다고 우기면서 말이야.\n\n삐걱대는 계단으로 많은 발소리가 올라왔어. 그리고 그중 어떤 발소리는 그가 기다리는 사람의 것과 너무 비슷하게 들려서, 랄프는 그가 계단 끝에 다다르면 곧장 말을 걸 수 있게 자주 벌떡 일어서곤 했어. 그렇지만 사람들은 하나하나, 그가 진을 치고 있는 곳에 못 미친 어느 방으로 꺾어 들어갔어. 그렇게 실망할 때마다 그는 온몸이 으슬으슬 추워지고 외로워졌지.\n\n마침내 그는 더 머물러 봤자 가망이 없다는 걸 느꼈어. 그래서 다시 아래층으로 내려가, 하숙인 하나에게 스퀴어스 씨의 행방을 아는 게 없느냐고 물었어. 그 사람을, 둘이서 미리 정해 둔 가명으로 부르면서 말이야. 그 하숙인은 다른 사람을 가리켜 줬고, 그 사람은 또 다른 사람을 가리켜 줬어. 거기서 그는 이런 얘기를 들었지. 전날 밤늦게 그가 남자 둘과 황급히 나갔고, 그들이 얼마 뒤 같은 층에 사는 노파를 데리러 다시 왔다는 거야. 그리고 그 일이 일러 준 사람의 눈길을 끌긴 했지만, 그때 그들에게 말을 걸지도 않았고 나중에 캐묻지도 않았다는 거였어.\n\n이 말에 그는 한 가지 생각이 들었어. 어쩌면 페그 슬라이더스큐가 그 도둑질 때문에 붙잡혔고, 그 자리에 같이 있던 스퀴어스 씨도 공범으로 의심받아 같이 붙잡힌 게 아닐까 하는 거였지. 만약 그렇다면, 그 사실을 그라이드는 알고 있을 게 틀림없었어. 그래서 그는 그라이드의 집 쪽으로 발걸음을 옮겼어. 이제는 완전히 겁에 질려서, 정말로 음모가 진행되고 있는 게 아닌지, 자기를 곤경에 빠뜨리고 파멸시키려는 음모가 꾸며지고 있는 게 아닌지 두려워하면서 말이야.\n\n고리대금업자의 집에 다다른 그는, 창문이 꼭 닫혀 있고 거무칙칙한 가리개가 내려져 있는 걸 봤어. 모든 게 고요하고, 음울하고, 인기척이 없었지. 그렇지만 그건 늘 보던 그 집의 모습이었어. 그는 문을 두드렸어. 처음엔 살살, 그다음엔 크고 세차게 말이야. 아무도 나오지 않았어. 그는 연필로 카드에 몇 마디 적어 문 밑으로 밀어 넣고는 막 돌아서려 했어. 그런데 위쪽에서 무슨 소리가, 마치 창틀을 몰래 들어 올리는 것 같은 소리가 그의 귀를 붙잡았어. 올려다보니, 다락방 창문에서 집 난간 너머로 조심스레 내다보는 그라이드 본인의 얼굴이 어렴풋이 보였어. 아래에 누가 있는지 알아본 그는 얼굴을 도로 집어넣었지. 그런데 그렇게 빠르지는 못해서, 랄프는 그가 본 걸 알아챘다는 걸 그라이드에게 알리고 내려오라고 소리쳤어.\n\n부름이 거듭되자, 그라이드가 다시 내다봤어. 어찌나 조심스러운지 노인의 몸은 한 군데도 보이지 않을 정도였지. 날카로운 이목구비와 흰머리만 난간 위로 불쑥 솟아 있는 모습이, 마치 잘린 머리 하나가 벽을 장식하고 있는 것 같았어.\n\n\"쉿!\" 그가 외쳤어. \"가요, 가!\"\n\n\"내려와요.\" 랄프가 그에게 손짓하며 말했어.\n\n\"가라니―까!\" 그라이드가 일종의 미칠 듯한 조바심에 휩싸여 고개를 내저으며 끽끽거렸어. \"나한테 말 걸지 마요. 두드리지도 말고. 이 집에 시선 끌지 말고, 그냥 가요.\"\n\n\"맹세코 두드리겠어. 동네 사람들이 다 들고일어날 때까지 두드릴 테야.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"거기서 왜 숨어 있는지 말 안 하면 말이야, 이 징징대는 비겁한 놈아.\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 안 들려요. 나한테 말 걸지 마요. 안전하지 않아요. 가요, 가!\" 그라이드가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"내려오라니까. 내려올 거야 말 거야?\" 랄프가 사납게 말했어.\n\n\"안―돼―애―애.\" 그라이드가 으르렁댔어. 그는 머리를 도로 집어넣었어. 거리에 홀로 남겨진 랄프의 귀에, 창틀이 닫히는 소리가 들렸어. 열렸을 때처럼 가만가만, 조심스럽게 말이야.\n\n\"이게 어찌 된 일이지,\" 그가 말했어. \"다들 나한테서 떨어져 나가, 나를 역병처럼 피하다니. 내 발밑의 먼지까지 핥아 대던 이놈들이 말이야. 내 시절이 끝난 건가, 그리고 이게 정말 밤이 닥쳐오는 건가? 무슨 영문인지 알아내고 말겠어! 무슨 수를 써서라도 말이야. 나는 요즘 며칠보다 지금 더 단단하고, 더 나다워졌어.\"\n\n그는 문에서 돌아섰어. 처음 화가 치밀어 오를 때는, 그라이드가 겁에 질려 제 발로 열게 될 때까지 그 문을 두들겨 부술까 하는 생각도 했었지. 그는 시내 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌렸어. 그리고 그곳에서 쏟아져 나오는 인파를 (이때쯤이면 오후 다섯 시에서 여섯 시 사이였지) 꾸준히 헤치고 나아가, 치어리블 형제의 사업장으로 곧장 갔어. 유리 칸막이 안으로 머리를 들이밀어 보니, 팀 링킨워터가 혼자 있었어.\n\n\"내 이름은 니클비요.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"압니다.\" 팀이 안경 너머로 그를 훑어보며 대답했어.\n\n\"오늘 아침 나를 찾아온 게 당신네 회사 누구였소?\" 랄프가 따져 물었어.\n\n\"찰스 씨요.\"\n\n\"그럼 찰스 씨한테 내가 만나고 싶다고 전하시오.\"\n\n\"만나시게 될 겁니다.\" 팀이 아주 날렵하게 의자에서 내려오며 말했어. \"찰스 씨뿐 아니라 네드 씨까지 만나시게 될 거예요.\"\n\n팀은 멈춰 서더니, 랄프를 똑바로, 그리고 매섭게 바라봤어. 그리고 더 할 말이 좀 남아 있다는 듯한 무뚝뚝한 태도로 고개를 한 번 끄덕이고는 사라졌어. 잠시 뒤 그가 돌아왔어. 그리고 랄프를 두 형제 앞으로 안내한 다음, 자기도 그 방에 그대로 남아 있었지.\n\n\"오늘 아침 나한테 말한 사람, 당신과 얘기하고 싶소.\" 랄프가 자기가 말 거는 사람을 손가락으로 가리키며 말했어.\n\n\"나는 내 동생 네드한테도, 팀 링킨워터한테도 숨기는 게 없습니다.\" 찰스 형제가 조용히 말했어.\n\n\"나는 있소.\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"니클비 씨, 선생.\" 네드 형제가 말했어. \"오늘 아침 내 형 찰스가 선생을 찾아간 그 일은, 이미 우리 세 사람한테, 그리고 그 밖의 다른 사람들한테도 아주 잘 알려져 있습니다. 그리고 안타깝게도 머지않아 훨씬 더 많은 사람들이 알게 될 일이고요. 형이 오늘 아침 혼자 선생을 찾아간 건, 신중과 배려 차원에서였습니다. 그런데 지금 우리는 더 이상의 신중과 배려는 어울리지 않는다고 느낍니다. 그러니 우리가 함께 이야기를 나눈다면, 지금 이대로 함께 하든지, 아니면 아예 하지 않든지 둘 중 하나입니다.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 신사 양반들.\" 랄프가 입술을 비죽이며 말했어. \"수수께끼처럼 말하는 게 당신들 두 분 특유의 장기인가 보군요. 게다가 저 서기 양반도, 영리한 사람답게, 당신들 비위를 맞추려고 그 기술을 익혀 둔 모양이고. 여럿이 모여서 얘기하시구려, 신사 양반들, 어디 한번. 내가 맞춰 드리지요.\"\n\n\"맞춰 줘!\" 팀 링킨워터가 별안간 얼굴이 새빨개지며 소리쳤어. \"이 사람이 우리를 맞춰 준대! 치어리블 형제를 맞춰 준다고! 저 말 들으셨어요? 저 사람 말 들으셨냐고요? 치어리블 형제를 맞춰 주겠다는 저 말, 들으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"팀,\" 찰스와 네드가 동시에 말했어. \"제발, 팀, 제발 좀, 그만하게.\"\n\n팀은 그 뜻을 알아채고, 분을 최대한 억눌렀어. 그리고 그 분을 안경 너머로 새어 나가게 하면서, 이따금 짧고 발작적인 웃음이라는 추가 안전판까지 동원했지. 그게 그의 속을 어지간히 풀어 주는 듯했어.\n\n\"아무도 나한테 앉으라는 말이 없으니,\" 랄프가 둘러보며 말했어. \"내가 알아서 하나 차지하겠소. 걷느라 지쳤거든. 자, 이제, 괜찮으시다면, 신사 양반들, 나는 알고 싶소. 아니, 알기를 요구합니다. 그럴 권리가 있으니까요. 당신들이 그런 말투를 쓰는 게, 그리고 내 일에 그렇게 음흉하게 간섭하는 게, 무슨 근거가 있는지 말이오. 당신들이 그래 왔다고 믿을 만한 까닭이 내겐 있소. 분명히 말해 두지요, 신사 양반들. 내가 세상의 평판 따위는 (흔히들 하는 말로) 별로 신경 쓰지 않는 사람이지만, 중상과 악의에 가만히 굴복할 생각은 없소. 당신들이 너무 쉽게 속아 넘어가는 거든, 아니면 일부러 거기에 한패로 끼어든 거든, 내게 돌아오는 결과는 똑같소. 어느 쪽이든, 나 같은 단순한 사람한테서 많은 배려나 너그러움을 기대하진 마시오.\"\n\n이 말을 어찌나 차분하고 신중하게 하던지, 사정을 모르는 사람이라면 열에 아홉은 랄프가 정말로 억울한 피해자라고 여겼을 거야. 그는 팔짱을 끼고 거기 앉아 있었어. 평소보다 창백한 건 분명했고, 인상도 충분히 험악했지만, 아주 침착했어. 두 형제나 잔뜩 약이 오른 팀보다 훨씬 더 침착했지. 그리고 최악의 상황이라도 맞받아칠 태세였어.\n\n\"좋습니다, 선생.\" 찰스 형제가 말했어. \"아주 좋아요. 네드 동생, 종을 울려 주겠나?\"\n\n\"찰스, 형님! 잠깐만요.\" 다른 쪽이 대답했어. \"니클비 씨를 위해서나 우리 목적을 위해서나, 우리가 할 말을 다 마칠 때까지는, 가능하다면 저분이 잠자코 있는 편이 나을 겁니다. 그걸 저분이 알아주셨으면 합니다.\"\n\n\"옳은 말이야, 옳은 말이고말고.\" 찰스 형제가 말했어.\n\n랄프는 미소를 지었지만, 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았어. 종이 울렸어. 방문이 열렸어. 한 남자가 절뚝거리는 걸음으로 들어왔어. 둘러보던 랄프의 눈이 뉴먼 노그스의 눈과 마주쳤지. 그 순간부터, 그의 가슴이 무너지기 시작했어.\n\n\"이거 시작이 참 좋군.\" 그가 씁쓸하게 말했어. \"오! 시작이 아주 좋아. 당신들은 솔직하고, 정직하고, 마음이 열려 있고, 공정하게 거래하는 사람들이지! 당신들 같은 인품의 진짜 값어치를 내가 늘 알고 있었지! 이런 녀석을 꼬드기다니. 술이라면 (영혼이 있다면) 제 영혼이라도 팔아 치울 놈이고, 입만 열면 거짓말인 놈을 말이야. 이런 짓이 통한다면 안전할 사람이 어디 있겠소? 오, 시작 한번 좋다!\"\n\n\"내가 말하겠어요.\" 뉴먼이 외쳤어. 그를 막으려고 끼어든 팀의 머리 너머로 보려고 발끝으로 서서 말이야. \"이봐요, 당신―늙은 니클비!―당신이 '이런 녀석'이라고 할 때 그게 무슨 뜻이오? 누가 나를 '이런 녀석'으로 만들었소? 내가 술이라면 영혼이라도 팔 놈이라면, 왜 도둑이나 사기꾼이나 빈집털이나 뒷마당 좀도둑이나, 눈먼 사람의 개 밥그릇에서 푼돈이나 훔치는 놈이 안 되고, 당신 종놈에 짐말이 됐겠소? 내 입만 열면 거짓말이라면, 왜 당신이 아끼는 총애꾼이 안 됐겠소? 거짓말이라고! 내가 언제 당신한테 굽실거리고 아첨한 적 있소? 그 말이나 해 보시오! 나는 당신을 충실하게 섬겼소. 가난했으니까 더 많은 일을 했고, 당신과 당신 같은 작자들을 경멸했으니까 더 모진 말을 참아 냈소. 당신이 구빈원에서 데려올 수 있는 그 어떤 사람보다도 더 말이오. 정말이오. 내가 당신을 섬긴 건 자존심 때문이었소. 당신 곁에서 외톨이였으니까, 내 비참한 꼴을 볼 다른 종놈이 없었으니까. 그리고 내가 망한 사람이라는 걸, 늘 지금 같은 처지였던 게 아니라는 걸, 내가 바보처럼 굴어서 당신이며 다른 악당들 손아귀에 떨어지지만 않았으면 더 잘 살았을지도 모른다는 걸, 당신만큼 잘 아는 사람도 없었으니까. 그걸 부정하겠소?\"\n\n\"진정하게.\" 팀이 타일렀어. \"안 하겠다고 했잖나.\"\n\n\"안 하겠다고 했지!\" 뉴먼이 그를 밀치며 외쳤어. 팀이 움직이는 대로 손을 움직여 그를 팔 길이만큼 떼어 놓으면서 말이야. \"나한테 말하지 마! 이봐, 니클비! 내 말 신경 안 쓰는 척 마시오. 그래 봐야 소용없어. 내가 더 잘 아니까. 방금 꼬드기는 얘길 했지? 누가 요크셔 교장을 꼬드겼소? 그것도 그 종놈이 한마디도 못 엿듣게 밖으로 내보내 놓고서, 그렇게 지나치게 조심하면 도리어 의심을 살 수 있다는 걸, 그래서 종놈이 밤에 주인이 나가는 걸 지켜볼 수도 있고, 다른 눈을 시켜 교장을 지켜보게 할 수도 있다는 걸 까맣게 잊고서 말이오. 누가 이기적인 아비를 꼬드겨, 제 딸을 늙은 아서 그라이드한테 팔라고 부추겼소? 그리고 그라이드까지 꼬드겼고, 그것도 그 작은 사무실에서 그랬지. 방 안에 벽장이 딸린 그 사무실에서 말이오!\"\n\n랄프는 자신을 무던히도 다잡고 있었어. 그렇지만 만약 그 때문에 다음 순간 목이 잘리는 게 확실했다 해도, 살짝 움찔하는 것만은 억누르지 못했을 거야.\n\n\"아하!\" 뉴먼이 외쳤어. \"이제 내 말에 신경이 쓰이는 모양이지? 무엇 때문에 이 머슴이 주인 하는 짓에 의심을 품게 됐고, 막을 수 있을 때 막지 않았더라면 자기도 그자만큼, 아니 그보다 더 나쁜 놈이 됐을 거라고 느끼게 됐는 줄 아시오? 그건 그 주인이 제 핏줄한테 한 잔인한 짓 때문이었소. 그리고 한 어린 처녀를 노린 더러운 속셈 때문이었지. 그 처녀는 망가지고 술에 절고 비참한 그 부려 먹히는 종놈마저도 마음 쓰이게 했고, 그래서 그가 그 처녀한테 무슨 도움이라도 줄 수 있을까 하는 마음에 (감사하게도, 전에도 한두 번 다른 사람들한테 그래 본 적이 있었으니까) 그 일을 그만두지 못하고 머물게 했단 말이오. 안 그랬으면 그는 주인을 흠씬 두들겨 패서 속을 풀고, 그러고는 악마한테나 가 버렸을 거요. 정말 그랬을 거요. 그건 똑똑히 새겨 두시오. 그리고 이것도 새겨 두시오. 내가 지금 여기 있는 건, 이 신사분들이 그게 최선이라고 여겼기 때문이오. 내가 이분들을 찾아갔을 때 (정말 내가 찾아간 거요. 나를 꼬드긴 사람은 없었소), 나는 이분들한테 말했소. 당신을 찾아내고, 당신을 추적하고, 내가 시작한 일을 끝까지 해내고, 옳은 편을 돕는 데 도움이 필요하다고. 그리고 그 일을 다 해내면, 당신 방에 쳐들어가 모든 걸 다 말해 주겠다고. 얼굴을 마주하고, 사내 대 사내로, 사내답게 말이오. 자, 이제 내가 할 말은 다 했소. 다른 사람이 할 말 있으면 하라고 하시오. 어서 쏟아 내 보시오!\"\n\n이 마무리 한마디와 함께, 뉴먼 노그스는, 이 연설을 띄엄띄엄 토막토막 내뱉는 내내 줄곧 앉았다 일어서기를 되풀이했고, 그 격렬한 운동과 흥분이 한데 겹쳐 더없이 후끈 달아오른 상태였는데, 그 중간 단계라곤 하나도 거치지 않고 곧장 뻣뻣하게, 꼿꼿하게, 미동도 없이 굳어 버렸어. 그러고는 그대로 랄프 니클비를 있는 힘껏 노려보고 있었지.\n\n랄프는 그를 한순간, 딱 한순간만 바라봤어. 그러고는 손을 내젓고 발로 바닥을 구르며, 목멘 소리로 말했어.\n\n\"계속해 보시오, 신사 양반들, 계속해! 보다시피 나는 참을성이 있으니까. 법이라는 게 있소, 법이 있단 말이오. 내가 이 일을 두고 당신들한테 책임을 묻겠소. 말조심하시오. 내가 다 증명하게 만들 테니까.\"\n\n\"증거는 준비돼 있습니다.\" 찰스 형제가 대답했어. \"이미 우리 손에 다 갖춰져 있어요. 스놀리란 사람이 어젯밤 자백했습니다.\"\n\n\"'스놀리란 사람'이 누구일 수도 있고,\" 랄프가 받아쳤어. \"그 사람의 '자백'이라는 게 내 일과 무슨 상관이 있을 수도 있겠소?\"\n\n고집스럽게 꿈쩍도 않는 태도로 던진 이 물음에, 노신사는 아무 대답도 하지 않았어. 다만, 자기들이 얼마나 진심인지 그에게 보여 주려면, 그에게 어떤 혐의가 씌워졌는지뿐 아니라, 그 증거가 무엇이고 그 증거를 어떻게 손에 넣었는지까지 말해 줄 필요가 있겠다고 했어. 이렇게 문제를 통째로 펼쳐 놓자, 네드 형제와 팀 링킨워터, 그리고 뉴먼 노그스가 셋이서 한꺼번에 나섰어. 그들은 한참을 함께 떠들고 큰 혼란이 한바탕 벌어진 끝에, 랄프에게 다음과 같은 이야기를 또렷한 말로 펼쳐 놓았어.\n\n이러했어. 뉴먼은, 그때는 데려올 수 없는 어떤 사람으로부터, 스마이크가 스놀리의 아들이 아니라는 사실을 엄숙히 확언받았어. 그리고 그 사람이 필요하다면 그 점을 선서로 증언하겠다고까지 했지. 그들은 이 이야기를 듣고 비로소, 상대편이 내세운 그 주장을 의심하게 됐어. 그게 아니었더라면 그 주장을 의심할 까닭이 없었을 거야. 그건 그들이 반박할 힘이 없는 증거로 떠받쳐져 있었으니까. 일단 음모가 있다고 의심하기 시작하자, 그들은 어렵지 않게 그 뿌리를 거슬러 올라가, 랄프의 악의와 스퀴어스의 앙심과 탐욕에 다다랐어. 그렇지만 의심과 증거는 전혀 다른 거잖아. 그래서 그들은, 이런 일에서 명석하고 예리하기로 이름난 한 변호사의 조언을 받았어. 그 청년을 되찾으려고 상대편이 벌이는 소송 절차에 대해서는 최대한 느리고 교묘하게 맞서라는 것, 그러는 한편 스놀리를 (분명히 가장 큰 거짓말이 그에게 달려 있을 테니까) 집요하게 물고 늘어지라는 것이었지. 가능하다면 그를 앞뒤가 안 맞고 서로 어긋나는 진술로 몰아넣고, 동원할 수 있는 모든 수단으로 그를 괴롭히고, 그렇게 그의 두려움과 제 한 몸 지키려는 마음을 파고들어, 이 음모 전체를 다 털어놓게 만들고, 그를 고용한 자와 그가 끌어들일 수 있는 다른 누구든 다 넘기게 만들라는 거였어. 이 모든 게 솜씨 좋게 진행됐어. 그렇지만 스놀리는 비열한 술수와 모략의 기술에 능숙한 자라서, 그들의 모든 시도를 보기 좋게 따돌렸지. 그러다 뜻밖의 사건이 어젯밤 그를 무릎 꿇리게 만들 때까지 말이야.\n\n일은 이렇게 됐어. 뉴먼 노그스가, 스퀴어스가 다시 시내에 와 있고, 그와 랄프 사이에 어찌나 은밀한 만남이 이뤄졌던지 그가 한마디도 엿듣지 못하게 집 밖으로 내보내질 정도였다고 알려 왔어. 그래서 그 교장에게 감시가 붙었지. 의심스러운 그 음모에 무슨 실마리라도 던져 줄 뭔가가 밝혀지지 않을까 하는 기대에서 말이야. 그런데 그가 랄프와 더 이상 연락을 주고받지도 않고, 스놀리와도 아무 연락이 없으며, 아주 혼자 지낸다는 게 밝혀지자, 그들은 완전히 막막해졌어. 감시는 거둬졌지. 그리고 어느 날 밤 뉴먼이 거리에서 그와 랄프가 함께 있는 걸 들키지 않고 우연히 마주치는 일이 없었더라면, 그들은 더 이상 그의 움직임을 살피지 않았을 거야. 뉴먼은 그들을 뒤따라갔어. 그리고 놀랍게도, 그들이 여러 허름한 하숙집과, 망한 노름꾼들이 운영하는 술집들을 들락거린다는 걸 알아냈지. 그중 한 곳 넘게 랄프를 아는 사람이 있었어. 그리고 그들이 한 노파를 찾고 있다는 것도 알아냈는데, 그건 그들이 떠난 뒤 캐물어서 알아낸 거였어. 그 노파의 인상착의는 귀먹은 슬라이더스큐 부인의 것과 정확히 들어맞았지. 이제 사태가 한층 더 심각한 빛깔을 띠기 시작하는 듯 보이자, 감시는 한층 더 빈틈없이 다시 이뤄졌어. 한 수사관을 구해, 스퀴어스와 같은 술집에 묵게 했지. 그리고 그 수사관과 프랭크 치어리블이, 아무것도 모르는 교장의 발자취를 뒤쫓았어. 마침내 그가 램버스의 하숙집에 무사히 자리 잡을 때까지 말이야. 스퀴어스 씨가 하숙을 옮기자 수사관도 자기 하숙을 옮겼어. 그리고 같은 거리에, 사실 바로 맞은편 집에 숨어 있다가, 곧 스퀴어스 씨와 슬라이더스큐 부인이 끊임없이 연락을 주고받고 있다는 걸 알아냈지.\n\n이런 상황에서, 아서 그라이드에게 도움을 청했어. 그 도둑질은, 일부는 이웃들의 캐묻기 좋아하는 성미 탓에, 일부는 그 자신의 비탄과 분노 탓에, 진작에 알려진 일이었어. 그렇지만 그는 그 노파를 붙잡는 데 동의하거나 조금이라도 거들기를 딱 잘라 거절했어. 그리고 그녀에게 불리한 증언을 하라는 요청을 받을지도 모른다는 생각에 어찌나 겁에 질렸던지, 집에 꼭 틀어박혀 누구와도 연락을 끊어 버렸지. 이에 추적하던 이들이 함께 머리를 맞댔어. 그리고 진실에 아주 가까이 다가가, 이런 결론에 이르렀지. 그라이드와 랄프가, 스퀴어스를 도구 삼아, 도난당한 서류 중 빛을 볼 수 없는 어떤 것들을 되찾으려 흥정하고 있다는 것이었어. 그 서류들은 어쩌면 뉴먼이 엿들은 매들린에 관한 그 암시들을 설명해 줄지도 모를 것들이었지. 그래서 그들은, 슬라이더스큐 부인이 그 서류들을 넘기기 전에 그녀를 붙잡아 들이기로 결정했어. 그리고 스퀴어스도, 그에게 의심스러운 무엇이라도 걸 수 있다면 함께 붙잡기로 했지. 그리하여 수색 영장을 구하고 만반의 준비를 갖춘 다음, 스퀴어스 씨의 창문을 지켜봤어. 그의 불이 꺼지고, 미리 알아낸 대로 그가 보통 슬라이더스큐 부인을 찾아가는 시각이 올 때까지 말이야. 이렇게 한 뒤, 프랭크 치어리블과 뉴먼이 살금살금 위층으로 올라가 그들의 대화를 엿들었어. 그리고 가장 알맞은 때에 수사관에게 신호를 보내기로 했지. 그들이 얼마나 절묘한 순간에 다다랐는지, 어떻게 엿들었는지, 무엇을 들었는지는 이미 독자가 다 아는 일이야. 아직 반쯤 얼이 빠져 있는 스퀴어스 씨는 훔친 증서를 지닌 채 끌려갔고, 슬라이더스큐 부인도 마찬가지로 붙잡혔어. 스퀴어스가 붙잡혔다는 소식이 스놀리에게 곧장 전해졌지. 무슨 일로 붙잡혔는지는 알려 주지 않았어. 그러자 그 작자는, 자기는 처벌받지 않게 해 주겠다는 약속부터 받아 낸 다음, 스마이크에 관한 그 이야기 전부가 꾸며 낸 거짓이자 위조라고 털어놓았어. 그리고 랄프 니클비를 빠짐없이 끌어들였지. 스퀴어스 씨로 말하자면, 그날 아침 치안판사 앞에서 비공개 심문을 받았어. 그런데 그 증서를 어떻게 손에 넣었는지, 슬라이더스큐 부인과는 어떻게 한패가 됐는지를 흡족하게 설명하지 못해서, 그녀와 함께 일주일간 구류 처분을 받았어.\n\n이 모든 사실이 이제 랄프에게 자초지종이 낱낱이 다 이야기됐어. 그것들이 속으로 그에게 어떤 인상을 남겼든, 그는 어떤 감정의 기색도 새어 나가게 하지 않았어. 찌푸린 눈을 바닥에서 들지 않고, 한 손으로 입을 가린 채, 그저 가만히 앉아 있었지. 이야기가 끝나자, 그는 무슨 말을 하려는 듯 황급히 고개를 들었어. 그렇지만 찰스 형제가 다시 입을 열자, 그는 다시 아까의 그 자세로 가라앉았어.\n\n\"오늘 아침 선생한테 말씀드렸지요.\" 노신사가 동생의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했어. \"내가 자비를 품고 선생을 찾아왔다고요. 이 마지막 거래에 선생이 얼마나 깊이 연루돼 있는지, 또 지금 구류 중인 그 사람이 선생을 얼마나 죄에 끌어들일지는, 선생이 가장 잘 아실 겁니다. 그렇지만 이 가엾고, 아무 잘못 없고, 억울한 청년을 노린 음모에 가담한 자들에게는, 정의가 제 길을 가야 합니다. 그 결과에서 선생을 구해 드리는 건 내 힘으로도, 내 동생 네드의 힘으로도 안 됩니다. 우리가 할 수 있는 최선은, 선생에게 제때 경고를 해서 그 결과를 피할 기회를 드리는 거지요. 우리는 선생 같은 노인이 가까운 친척의 손에 망신을 당하고 벌을 받게 하고 싶지 않습니다. 또 그 친척이 선생처럼 핏줄과 천륜의 정을 죄다 잊어버리는 것도 바라지 않고요. 우리는 선생께 간청합니다. 네드 동생, 자네도 이 간청에 함께하지, 나도 아네. 그리고 팀 링킨워터, 자네도 그렇지. 자네야 고집불통인 척하면서 거기 앉아 그러지 않은 양 인상을 쓰고 있지만 말이야. 우리는 선생께 간청합니다. 런던을 떠나, 이 사악한 음모의 결과로부터 안전할 어딘가에 몸을 숨기시라고요. 그리고 거기서, 선생, 그 죄를 속죄하고 더 나은 사람이 될 시간을 가지시라고요.\"\n\n\"그래, 당신들 생각엔,\" 랄프가 일어서며 대답했어. \"당신들 생각엔, 그렇게 쉽게 나를 짓밟을 수 있을 것 같소? 잘 짜인 계략 백 가지든, 매수한 증인 백 명이든, 내 뒤꿈치를 물고 늘어지는 거짓된 똥개 백 마리든, 번지르르한 말로 가득 찬 위선적인 연설 백 마디든, 그게 나를 흔들 수 있을 것 같소? 당신들 꿍꿍이를 까발려 줘서 고맙군. 이제 내가 대비를 했으니. 당신들이 상대하는 사람은 당신들이 생각하는 그런 위인이 아니오. 어디 해 봐! 그리고 명심하시오. 나는 당신들의 번드르르한 말과 거짓된 수작에 침을 뱉고, 감히 당신들한테 요구하겠소. 아니, 부추기겠소. 약 올려서라도 시키겠소. 나한테 할 수 있는 짓 가운데 가장 지독한 짓을 해 보라고 말이오!\"\n\n이렇게 그들은 그때는 헤어졌어. 그렇지만 최악은 아직 오지 않았어.\n\n집으로 가는 대신, 랄프는 눈에 띄는 첫 번째 거리 마차에 몸을 던지듯 올라탔어. 그러고는 스퀴어스 씨가 봉변을 당한 그 동네 경찰서 쪽으로 마부에게 가자고 했지. 경찰서에서 조금 떨어진 곳에서 내려 마부를 돌려보내고, 나머지 길은 걸어서 갔어. 그가 그토록 걱정하던 사람의 행방을 물어보니, 마침 때를 잘 맞춰 온 셈이었어. 스퀴어스 씨는 바로 그 순간 미리 불러 둔 삯마차를 기다리고 있었거든. 그걸 타고 일주일짜리 유치 생활을 하러, 신사답게 떠날 작정이었지.\n\n죄수와 이야기 좀 하게 해 달라고 하자, 그는 대기실 같은 방으로 안내받았어. 스퀴어스 씨는 교사라는 직업과 남다른 점잖음 덕분에 거기서 하루를 보내도 좋다는 허락을 받은 모양이었어. 촛농을 흘리며 시커멓게 타들어 가는 촛불 하나에 의지해, 랄프는 저 멀리 구석 벤치에서 깊이 잠든 교장의 모습을 겨우 알아볼 수 있었어. 그의 앞 탁자에는 빈 잔이 놓여 있었지. 그 빈 잔에다 졸음에 푹 빠진 모양새, 그리고 브랜디와 물 냄새가 코를 찌르는 걸 보니, 스퀴어스 씨가 술기운에 기대 제 처지의 답답함을 잠시나마 잊어 보려 했다는 걸 손님은 미리 짐작할 수 있었지.\n\n그를 깨우는 일은 그리 쉽지가 않았어. 잠이 어찌나 깊고 무거운지 말이야. 흐릿하고 느릿하게 정신을 차리더니, 마침내 몸을 똑바로 일으켜 앉았어. 그러자 누런 얼굴에 새빨간 코, 뻣뻣한 수염이 드러났지. 게다가 피 얼룩이 진 더러운 흰 손수건을 머리 정수리에 둘러 턱 밑에서 묶어 놓은 탓에, 그 꼴이 한층 더 가관이었어. 그는 한참 말없이 랄프를 처량하게 쳐다보다가, 끓어오르는 심정을 이런 짤막한 한마디로 터뜨렸어.\n\n\"이봐, 젊은 양반, 이번엔 당신이 단단히 일을 저질렀어. 그래, 당신이 그랬다고!\"\n\n\"머리가 왜 그래?\" 랄프가 물었어.\n\n\"왜기는, 당신 부하가, 그 밀고하고 사람 납치하는 그 부하 놈이 깨 놨지.\" 스퀴어스가 뾰로통하게 대꾸했어. \"그래서 이 꼴이 된 거야. 이제야 왔구먼, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"왜 나한테 사람을 보내지 않았소?\" 랄프가 말했어. \"당신한테 무슨 일이 닥쳤는지 모르는데 내가 어떻게 올 수가 있겠소?\"\n\n\"우리 가족!\" 스퀴어스 씨가 딸꾹질을 하며 천장으로 눈을 치켜떴어. \"딱 한창 감수성이 활짝 꽃피어 오를 나이인 내 딸, 사사로운 삶에서야 어린 노발이요, 자식을 끔찍이 아끼는 마을의 자랑이자 꽃인 내 아들 ― 우리 가족한테 이 무슨 날벼락이냐고! 스퀴어스 집안의 문장이 찢기고, 그 집안의 해가 저 바다 물결 속으로 가라앉아 버렸단 말이다!\"\n\n\"당신 술 마셨군.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"아직 술이 안 깬 거고.\"\n\n\"당신 건강을 빌면서 마신 건 아니니까, 영감.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 대꾸했어. \"당신이 상관할 바 아니지.\"\n\n랄프는 교장의 달라진 데다 건방지기까지 한 태도에 치미는 분노를 꾹 눌렀어. 그러고는 왜 자기한테 사람을 보내지 않았느냐고 다시 물었어.\n\n\"당신한테 사람 보내서 내가 득 볼 게 뭐가 있는데?\" 스퀴어스가 받아쳤어. \"당신이랑 한패인 게 알려져 봐야 나한테 좋을 거 하나 없고, 이 사건 사정을 좀 더 알아내기 전까진 저들이 보석도 안 받아 줘. 그래서 나는 여기 꽁꽁 묶여 있고, 당신은 거기서 자유롭게 편하게 있는 거잖아.\"\n\n\"당신도 며칠이면 그렇게 될 거요.\" 랄프가 짐짓 기분 좋은 척하며 받아쳤어. \"저들이 당신을 어쩌지 못해.\"\n\n\"하긴, 어쩌다가 내가 그 송장 같은 늙은이 슬라이더랑 한패가 됐는지 설명만 하면, 나한테 큰일 못 하겠지.\" 스퀴어스가 독기를 품고 대꾸했어. \"그 할망구는 내가 엮이기 전에 차라리 죽어서 묻히고, 다시 파헤쳐져 해부당하고, 해부 박물관에 철사로 매달려 있었으면 좋겠다고. 오늘 아침에 그 머리에 분 칠한 양반이 한 말이 딱 이거였어. '죄인! 그대가 이 여자와 함께 있다가 붙잡혔고, 이 문서를 소지한 채 적발되었으며, 그 여자와 짜고 다른 문서들을 부정하게 없애려 했고, 그러면서 자기에 대한 변명도 시원찮으니, 조사를 하고 증거를 모으도록 일주일간 그대를 유치하겠노라. 그동안에는 그대의 출두를 보장하는 어떤 보석도 받지 않겠다.' 자, 그럼 내가 지금 하는 말은, 내가 나에 대해 시원하게 변명할 수 있다는 거야. 내 학원 명함을 내밀면서 이렇게 말하면 돼. '저는 여기 적힌 그 와크포드 스퀴어스올시다, 나리. 저로 말씀드리면, 흠잡을 데 없는 추천장으로 도덕성과 곧은 신념이 둘째가라면 서러운 사람이라고 보증받은 몸입니다. 이 일에서 뭐가 잘못됐든 그건 제 잘못이 아닙니다. 저는 나쁜 마음이라곤 없었어요, 나리. 무엇이 잘못됐는지조차 몰랐다고요. 저는 그저 친구한테, 골든 스퀘어에 사는 제 친구 랄프 니클비 씨한테 고용됐을 뿐입니다. 그 사람을 불러서 할 말 있으면 해 보라고 하십시오, 나리. 그 사람이 장본인이지 제가 아닙니다!'\"\n\n\"당신이 가지고 있던 문서가 뭐였소?\" 랄프가 방금 나온 이야기는 잠시 비껴가며 물었어.\n\n\"문서가 뭐냐고? 아니, 그 문서지.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"그 매들린 뭐시기 거 말이야. 유언장이었어, 그게 바로 유언장이었다고.\"\n\n\"어떤 성격의, 누구의 유언장이고, 작성된 날짜는 언제고, 그 여자한테 어떻게, 얼마나 이로운 거였소?\" 랄프가 다급하게 물었어.\n\n\"그 여자한테 유리한 유언장이라는 거, 그게 내가 아는 전부야.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어. \"그리고 그 풀무 같은 게 당신 머리에 떨어졌어도 그 정도밖에는 못 알았을걸. 저들이 그걸 손에 넣은 건 다 당신이 그 잘난 신중함을 부린 탓이야. 그냥 나한테 태우게 두고 없어졌다는 내 말만 믿었으면, 그건 지금쯤 벽난로 뒤에 잿더미가 됐을 거 아니냐고. 멀쩡하고 온전한 채로 내 외투 속에 들어 있는 대신에 말이야.\"\n\n\"가는 데마다 한 방씩 얻어맞는구나!\" 랄프가 중얼거렸어.\n\n\"아!\" 스퀴어스가 한숨을 내쉬었어. 브랜디와 물에다 깨진 머리까지 겹쳐서 정신이 묘하게 오락가락하고 있었거든. \"요크셔의 그레타 다리 근처, 그 즐거운 마을 두더보이즈에서는 말이야, 청소년들을 재워 주고, 입혀 주고, 책 사 주고, 씻겨 주고, 용돈 주고, 필요한 건 다 대 주고, 살아 있는 말 죽은 말 다 가르치고, 수학, 철자법, 기하학, 천문학, 삼각법까지 가르쳐 ― 이거야말로 삼각법의 변천이지, 이게 바로! 엘이 둘인 올(all) ― 전부, 모조리 ― 구두장이 연장. 위(up), 형용사, 다운(down) 아니고. 에스, 큐, 더블 이, 알, 에스 ― 스퀴어스, 명사, 청소년을 가르치는 사람. 합계, 스퀴어스는 이제 다 끝장!\"\n\n이렇게 그가 횡설수설하는 동안, 랄프는 정신을 가다듬을 틈을 얻었어. 그리고 곧 깨달았지. 교장의 불안을 최대한 가라앉히고, 입을 꾹 다물고 침묵을 지키는 게 가장 안전하고 현명한 길이라고 믿게 만들어야 한다는 걸 말이야.\n\n\"한 번 더 말하지만,\" 그가 말했어. \"저들은 당신을 어쩌지 못해. 당신은 불법 구금으로 소송을 걸어서, 오히려 이걸로 한몫 챙길 수도 있어. 이런 시시한 곤경쯤은 스무 번도 더 빠져나가게 해 줄 이야기를 우리가 지어내 줄게. 그리고 혹시 당신이 불려 나오게 될 경우에 대비해 천 파운드짜리 출두 보증을 원한다면, 그것도 마련해 주지. 당신이 할 일은 그저 진실을 감추는 것뿐이야. 오늘 밤엔 술기운이 좀 있어서 평소만큼 이걸 똑똑히 못 볼지도 몰라. 그래도 당신이 해야 할 일은 이거고, 정신을 바짝 차려야 해. 까딱 실수하면 곤란해지니까.\"\n\n\"오!\" 스퀴어스가 고개를 한쪽으로 비스듬히 기울인 채, 늙은 까마귀처럼 교활하게 그를 쳐다보며 말했어. \"그게 내가 할 일이다, 이거지? 그럼 이번엔 내 얘기 한두 마디 좀 들어 봐. 난 누가 나 대신 이야기 지어 주는 거 안 받을 거고, 그런 거에 매달릴 생각도 없어. 일이 나한테 불리하게 돌아간다 싶으면, 당신도 제 몫을 떠안길 바라겠어. 내가 꼭 그렇게 만들 거고. 당신은 위험에 대해선 한마디도 안 했어. 난 이런 곤경에 끌려들 거라곤 흥정한 적이 없고, 당신 생각만큼 얌전히 받아들일 마음도 없다고. 내가 당신한테 끌려서 이 일 저 일로 따라온 건, 우리가 어떤 식으로든 한데 얽혀 있었기 때문이야. 당신이 심술을 부릴 마음만 먹으면 아마 내 사업을 망칠 수도 있었을 거고, 좋게 나올 마음을 먹으면 나한테 적잖이 일거리를 던져 줄 수도 있었으니까. 자, 지금 다 잘 풀린다면야 그거 아주 좋고, 나도 불만 없어. 그런데 뭐가 하나라도 틀어지면, 그땐 사정이 달라지는 거야. 그땐 나한테 제일 득이 될 것 같은 말이면 뭐든 하고, 뭐든 하고, 누구 충고도 안 들을 거야. 그 녀석들에 대한 내 도덕적 권위가,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 한층 더 무게를 잡으며 덧붙였어. \"그 밑바닥부터 흔들리고 있다고. 먹을 게 없어 굶주리는 스퀴어스 부인, 내 딸, 내 아들 와크포드의 모습이 끊임없이 눈앞에 어른거려. 그 앞에서는 다른 생각이란 생각은 죄다 녹아 사라져 버려. 남편이자 아버지로서 내가 아는 산수에서 단 하나뿐인 숫자는, 이 지긋지긋한 마당에 일 번, 바로 나란 말이야!\"\n\n스퀴어스 씨가 얼마나 더 일장 연설을 늘어놓았을지, 아니면 그 연설이 얼마나 험악한 말다툼으로 번졌을지는 아무도 몰라. 마침 이 대목에서 마차가 도착하고 그를 데려갈 호송원이 나타나는 바람에 말이 끊겼거든. 그는 머리를 동여맨 손수건 위에다 모자를 위엄 있게 척 얹었어. 그러고는 한 손은 주머니에 찌르고 다른 손으로는 호송원의 팔을 붙든 채, 순순히 끌려 나갔지.\n\n'사람을 안 보낸 걸 보고 짐작했던 대로군!' 랄프는 생각했어. '이놈은, 술 취해 떠드는 그 헛소리 사이로 빤히 보이는데, 나한테 등을 돌리기로 작정했어. 내가 이렇게 사방에서 포위당하고 옴짝달싹 못 하게 몰리니까, 다들 겁에 질렸을 뿐 아니라, 우화 속 짐승들처럼 이제 나한테 한 방씩 먹이려 드는구나. 어제만 해도, 그래 바로 어제까지만 해도 다들 굽실거리며 고분고분했으면서 말이야. 하지만 저들이 날 흔들지는 못해. 난 물러서지 않아. 한 치도 꿈쩍 안 할 거다!'\n\n그는 집으로 갔어. 가정부가 몸이 아프다고 투덜대는 걸 보고는 차라리 다행이다 싶었지. 혼자 있을 핑계가 생기는 데다, 가까이 있는 그녀의 집으로 보내 버릴 구실이 됐으니까. 그러고는 촛불 하나 켜 놓고 그 앞에 앉아, 그날 일어난 모든 일을 그제야 처음으로 곰곰이 되짚어 보기 시작했어.\n\n그는 어젯밤 이후로 아무것도 먹지도 마시지도 않았어. 게다가 마음 졸인 것도 모자라 여러 시간 동안 거의 쉴 새 없이 이곳저곳을 돌아다녔지. 속이 메스껍고 기진맥진했지만, 물 한 잔 말고는 아무것도 입에 댈 수가 없었어. 그는 한 손에 머리를 괸 채 계속 앉아 있었어. 쉬는 것도 아니고 생각하는 것도 아니었지. 그저 둘 다 해 보려고 안간힘을 쓸 뿐이었어. 그리고 지금만큼은 피로와 황량함, 그 한 가지 감각 말고는 모든 감각이 다 마비된 것 같았어.\n\n거의 열 시가 다 됐을 무렵, 문 두드리는 소리가 들렸어. 그래도 그는 거기에 생각을 모을 수조차 없는 사람처럼 아까처럼 가만히 앉아 있었지. 두드리는 소리는 몇 번이고 되풀이됐어. 그리고 바깥에서 누군가가 창문에 불빛이 보인다고(그가 알기로는 자기가 켜 둔 촛불을 두고 하는 말이었지) 말하는 걸 여러 번 듣고 나서야, 그는 겨우 몸을 일으켜 아래층으로 내려갔어.\n\n\"니클비 씨, 당신한테 끔찍한 소식이 있습니다. 저는 당신더러 당장 저와 함께 가 주십사 부탁하러 왔어요.\" 어디서 들어 본 듯한 목소리였어. 그가 눈 위에 손을 얹고 내다보니, 계단에 팀 링킨워터가 서 있었어.\n\n\"어딜 가자는 거요?\" 랄프가 따져 물었어.\n\n\"우리 집으로요. 오늘 아침에 오셨던 곳 말입니다. 마차를 대 놨어요.\"\n\n\"내가 왜 거길 가야 하지?\" 랄프가 말했어.\n\n\"왜냐고 묻지 말고, 제발 저와 같이 가 주세요.\"\n\n\"오늘 일을 또 한 번 되풀이하자는 거군!\" 랄프가 문을 닫으려는 시늉을 하며 받아쳤어.\n\n\"아니, 아니에요!\" 팀이 그의 팔을 붙잡고 더없이 간절하게 외쳤어. \"그냥, 그동안 일어난 일을 좀 들으셔야 한다는 것뿐입니다. 아주 무서운 일이에요, 니클비 씨. 당신과 깊이 관련된 일이고요. 사실이 아니라면 제가 당신한테 이런 말을 하거나 이렇게 찾아왔겠습니까?\"\n\n랄프는 그를 더 찬찬히 들여다봤어. 그가 정말로 몹시 흥분해 있는 걸 보고는, 그도 흠칫 망설여졌어. 무슨 말을 해야 할지, 무슨 생각을 해야 할지 알 수가 없었지.\n\n\"이건 다른 어느 때보다 지금 들으시는 게 낫습니다.\" 팀이 말했어. \"당신한테 어떤 영향을 끼칠지도 모르고요. 제발 좀 와 주세요!\"\n\n다른 때였다면, 랄프의 고집과 반감이 그런 쪽 사람의 호소 따위는, 아무리 간절하게 졸라 댄들 끄떡없이 막아 냈을지도 몰라. 하지만 이번에는, 잠시 머뭇거리고 나서 그는 모자를 가지러 현관으로 들어갔어. 그러고는 돌아와 한마디 말도 없이 마차에 올라탔어.\n\n팀은 나중에 또렷이 기억했고, 두고두고 이야기했어. 랄프 니클비가 그러려고 집 안으로 들어갈 때, 의자에 내려놓은 촛불 빛에 비친 그가 술 취한 사람처럼 비틀비틀 휘청거리는 걸 봤다고 말이야. 그리고 또 똑똑히 기억했지. 랄프가 마차 발판에 발을 올려놓고는 몸을 돌려 자기를 쳐다봤는데, 그 얼굴이 어찌나 잿빛으로 창백하고 어찌나 사납고 텅 비어 있던지, 자기도 모르게 몸서리가 쳐졌고, 순간 따라가기조차 무서웠다고 말이야. 사람들은 그때 그에게 어떤 어두운 예감이 들었던 거라고 즐겨 이야기하곤 했어. 하지만 그 감정은, 따지고 보면 그날 그가 겪은 일들 탓이라고 보는 편이 더 그럴듯할지도 몰라.\n\n마차를 타고 가는 동안 깊은 침묵이 흘렀어. 목적지에 다다르자, 랄프는 안내인을 따라 집 안으로, 그리고 두 형제가 있는 방으로 들어갔어. 그들의 태도며 늙은 서기의 태도에 자기를 향한 어떤 말 없는 연민 같은 게 어려 있는 걸 보고, 그는 어찌나 어안이 벙벙했던지, 아니 두렵기까지 했던지, 입이 거의 떨어지지 않았어.\n\n그래도 자리에 앉고 나서, 그는 끊어지는 말투로나마 가까스로 입을 뗐어. \"무슨 ― 나한테 무슨 할 말이 있다는 거요 ― 아까 이미 한 말 말고 더?\"\n\n방은 낡고 컸어. 불빛은 영 시원찮았고, 한쪽 끝은 내닫이 창으로 막혀 있었지. 그 창가에는 묵직한 휘장이 드리워져 있었어. 말을 하며 그쪽으로 눈길을 던지던 그는, 거기서 한 남자의 거뭇한 형체를 알아본 것 같았어. 그 형체가 자기 시선을 받아 불편한 듯 움직이는 걸 보고, 그 느낌은 더욱 확실해졌지.\n\n\"저기 저게 누구요?\" 그가 말했어.\n\n\"지난 두 시간 사이에 우리한테 어떤 소식을 전해 준 사람이오. 당신을 부르게 만든 그 소식 말이오.\" 형 찰스가 대답했어. \"그냥 두시오, 선생. 지금은 그냥 두시오.\"\n\n\"또 수수께끼군!\" 랄프가 힘없이 말했어. \"그래서요, 선생?\"\n\n두 형제 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌리려면 그 창에서 눈을 떼야만 했어. 하지만 둘 중 누가 입을 열기도 전에, 그는 다시 그쪽을 돌아보고 말았지. 보이지 않는 그 사람이 있다는 게 그를 안절부절못하고 불편하게 만든 게 분명했어. 그는 이 동작을 몇 번이고 되풀이했거든. 그러다 마침내, 도무지 그쪽에서 눈을 떼지 못하게 만드는 신경증에라도 걸린 사람처럼, 아예 그 형체가 자기 정면에 오도록 자리를 잡고 앉았어. 그러고는 불빛이 눈부셔 못 견디겠다는 핑계를 중얼거렸지.\n\n두 형제는 잠깐 따로 떨어져 의논을 했어. 그 모습으로 보아 둘 다 마음이 흔들리고 있는 게 분명했지. 랄프는 그들을 두세 번 흘끔거리다가, 마침내 안간힘을 써서 침착함을 되찾으며 말했어. \"자, 이게 다 무슨 일이오? 이 야밤에 나를 집에서 끌어냈으면, 그만한 까닭이 있어야지. 나한테 무슨 할 말이 있는 거요?\" 잠깐 뜸을 들이더니 그가 덧붙였어. \"내 조카딸이 죽었소?\"\n\n그는 이야기를 꺼내기 한결 쉽게 만들어 주는 실마리를 건드린 셈이었어. 형 찰스가 몸을 돌리며, 자기들이 그에게 전해야 할 게 죽음에 관한 소식인 건 맞지만 그의 조카딸은 무사하다고 말했어.\n\n\"설마 그 애 오빠가 죽었다는 말은 아니겠지요.\" 랄프가 눈을 반짝이며 말했어. \"아니, 그건 너무 잘된 일이라. 그렇게 말해도 난 안 믿어요. 사실이라기엔 너무 반가운 소식이거든.\"\n\n\"부끄러운 줄 아시오, 이 모질고 인정머리 없는 사람아.\" 다른 형제가 발끈해서 외쳤어. \"마음 단단히 먹으시오. 당신 가슴에 사람다운 감정이 한 조각이라도 있다면, 당신마저 움츠러들고 떨게 만들 소식이니. 한 가엾고 불운한 소년이 ― 한 번도 그런 다정한 손길이나, 우리네 어린 시절을 평생토록 행복한 꿈처럼 추억하게 해 주는 그런 환한 시간 한 번 누려 보지 못했다는 것만 빼면 여느 아이와 다를 바 없던 아이가 ― 마음 따뜻하고, 해라곤 끼칠 줄 모르고, 정 많은 그 아이가, 당신한테 거슬리는 짓 한 번 한 적 없고 당신을 해코지한 적도 없는데, 당신이 조카한테 품은 악의와 증오를 죄다 그 아이한테 쏟아붓고, 그 아이를 조카에게 당신의 못된 화풀이를 해 대는 도구로 삼았는데 ― 그 아이가, 선생, 당신의 박해와, 햇수로는 짧으나 고통으로는 길었던 삶의 비참함과 학대에 짓눌려, 제 슬픈 사연을 하소연하러 떠나 버렸다고 말한다면 어쩌겠소? 그 사연 속 당신의 몫에 대해, 당신은 반드시 책임져야 할 그곳으로 말이오.\"\n\n\"그 애가 죽었다고 말해 준다면,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그 애가 죽었다고 말해 준다면, 나머지는 다 용서하리다. 그 애가 죽었다고 말해 준다면, 나는 당신들한테 빚진 거고 평생 신세를 진 거요. 죽었군! 당신들 얼굴에 다 쓰여 있어. 이제 누가 이긴 거지? 이게 당신들이 말하던 그 무서운 소식이오? 이게 그 끔찍한 소식이라고? 거 봐요, 이게 날 얼마나 움직이는지. 사람 보내길 잘했소. 바로 이런 때에 이 소식을 들을 수만 있다면, 나는 진흙탕이며 수렁이며 어둠을 헤치고 백 마일이라도 걸어왔을 거요.\"\n\n그렇게 야만스러운 기쁨에 들떠 있으면서도, 랄프는 두 형제의 얼굴에서 볼 수 있었어. 혐오와 공포의 표정에 뒤섞여, 아까 봤던 그 자기를 향한 무어라 말할 수 없는 연민 같은 게 어려 있는 걸 말이야.\n\n\"그래서 저 작자가 당신들한테 그 소식을 가져왔다, 이거지?\" 랄프가 아까 말한 그 움푹한 자리를 손가락으로 가리키며 말했어. \"그러고는 거기 앉아서, 그 소식에 내가 무너지고 짓눌리는 꼴을 구경할 속셈이었겠지! 하, 하, 하! 하지만 저자한테 말해 두지. 나는 앞으로 두고두고 오랜 세월 저자 옆구리에 박힌 날카로운 가시가 돼 줄 거라고. 그리고 당신들 둘한테도 다시 말하는데, 당신들은 아직 저자를 몰라. 저 부랑자한테 동정을 베푼 그날을 두고두고 후회하게 될 거야.\"\n\n\"당신은 나를 당신 조카로 착각하는군.\" 텅 빈 목소리가 말했어. \"내가 정말 그였다면, 당신한테도, 나한테도 더 나았을 텐데.\"\n\n그가 그토록 어렴풋이 봤던 그 형체가 자리에서 일어나, 천천히 다가왔어. 그는 흠칫 뒤로 물러섰어. 그가 마주한 건 짐작했던 니콜라스가 아니라 브루커였거든.\n\n랄프는 자기가 아는 한, 이 남자를 두려워할 이유가 없었어. 전에는 한 번도 두려워한 적이 없었지. 그런데 그날 밤 집을 나설 때 그의 얼굴에 어려 있던 그 창백함이 다시 그를 덮쳤어. 그가 떠는 게 보였어. 그리고 그자에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 달라진 목소리로 말했어.\n\n\"이 작자가 여기서 뭘 하는 거요? 이자가 전과자에, 중죄인에, 흔해 빠진 도둑놈이라는 걸 알기나 하시오?\"\n\n\"이자가 당신한테 할 말을 들어 보시오. 오, 니클비 씨, 이자가 누구든 간에, 그가 할 말을 좀 들어 보시오!\" 두 형제가 어찌나 힘주어 간절하게 외쳤던지, 랄프는 의아해하며 그들 쪽으로 돌아섰어. 그들이 브루커를 가리켰어. 랄프는 다시 그를 응시했어. 거의 기계처럼 말이야.\n\n\"저 소년은,\" 그 남자가 말했어. \"여기 두 분 신사께서 이야기하시던 저 소년은 ―\"\n\n\"저 소년은,\" 랄프가 멍하니 그를 바라보며 따라 말했어.\n\n\"제가 침대에 차갑게 식은 채 뻗어 죽어 있는 걸 보았고, 지금은 무덤 속에 있는 그 아이는 ―\"\n\n\"지금은 무덤 속에 있는,\" 랄프가 잠꼬대하는 사람처럼 되뇌었어.\n\n그 남자가 눈을 치켜뜨고, 두 손을 엄숙하게 맞잡았어.\n\n\"― 당신의 하나뿐인 아들이었소, 하늘에 계신 하느님께 맹세코!\"\n\n죽음 같은 침묵 한가운데서, 랄프는 두 손으로 관자놀이를 짓누르며 주저앉았어. 잠시 후 그가 손을 떼었는데, 어디 한 군데 상처로 일그러진 데도 없는 산 사람의 얼굴치고, 그때 그가 드러낸 것만큼 송장 같은 얼굴은 일찍이 본 적이 없었어. 그는 어느새 자기한테서 조금 떨어져 서 있던 브루커를 바라봤어. 하지만 한마디 말도, 조그만 소리도, 손짓 하나도 하지 않았어.\n\n\"여러분,\" 그 남자가 말했어. \"저는 제 변명 따위는 하지 않겠습니다. 그러기엔 너무 멀리 와 버렸으니까요. 이 일이 어쩌다 이렇게 됐는지 말씀드리면서, 제가 모질게 부려졌고 어쩌면 제 본래 성품에서 벗어나도록 내몰렸다고 한다면, 그건 그저 제 이야기에 빠질 수 없는 한 부분이라서일 뿐, 저를 감싸려는 게 아닙니다. 저는 죄지은 사람이에요.\"\n\n그가 무언가를 떠올리려는 듯 말을 멈추었어. 그리고 랄프에게서 눈길을 돌려 두 형제를 향해, 가라앉고 겸허한 어조로 이야기를 이어 갔어.\n\n\"한때 이 사람과 거래가 있던 이들 중에, 여러분 ― 한 이십 년, 아니 이십오 년쯤 전 일입니다 ― 한 사람이 있었습니다. 여우 사냥을 즐기고 술을 진탕 마셔 대는 거친 신사였는데, 제 재산은 이미 다 날려 먹고, 누이동생 재산까지 탕진해 버리려고 했죠. 둘 다 고아였고, 누이는 그 오빠랑 살면서 집안 살림을 맡고 있었어요. 본래 그 오빠의 영향력을 등에 업고 그 처녀를 설득해 넘기려던 거였는지 아닌지는 모르겠습니다만, 이 사람이,\" 그가 랄프를 가리켰어. \"레스터셔에 있는 그 집에 꽤 자주 내려가서, 한 번에 며칠씩 묵곤 했어요. 둘은 같이 한 거래가 아주 많았으니, 그중 어떤 일로 갔을 수도 있고, 아니면 폐허나 다름없던 그 의뢰인의 일을 수습하러 갔을 수도 있죠. 물론 가는 거야 잇속을 챙기려는 거였고요. 그 부인은 처녀라기엔 나이가 좀 있었지만, 제가 듣기로는 미인이었고, 꽤 큰 재산을 물려받을 자격이 있었답니다. 시간이 흐르면서, 이 사람이 그 여자와 결혼을 했어요. 그를 이 결혼으로 이끈 그 잇속에 대한 욕심이, 결혼을 철저히 비밀에 부치게 만든 원인이기도 했죠. 그 여자 아버지의 유언장에 이런 조항이 있었거든요. 만약 딸이 오빠의 동의 없이 결혼하면, 그 재산은 ― 딸이 결혼 안 하고 있는 동안에만 평생 수익권 정도를 갖던 그 재산은 ― 통째로 집안의 다른 갈래로 넘어간다는 거였어요. 오빠는 누이가 두둑이 값을 치르고 사지 않는 한 절대 동의를 안 해 줬고, 니클비 씨는 그런 희생은 죽어도 못 하겠다고 했죠. 그래서 둘은 결혼을 비밀로 한 채, 그 오빠가 목이 부러져 죽거나 열병으로 죽기만을 기다리며 지냈어요. 그런데 오빠는 둘 다 안 죽었고, 그러는 사이에 이 비밀 결혼의 결실로 아들이 하나 태어났습니다. 그 아이는 멀리 떨어진 곳으로 보내져 유모 손에 맡겨졌어요. 어머니는 그 아이를 한두 번밖에 못 봤고, 그것도 몰래였죠. 그리고 아버지는 ― 이제 거의 손아귀에 들어오는 듯한 그 돈을 어찌나 갈급하게 탐냈던지, 처남이 아주 위중해서 날이 갈수록 점점 더 쇠약해지고 있었거든요 ― 행여 의심이라도 살까 봐 그 아이 근처엔 얼씬도 안 했어요. 오빠는 질질 명을 이어 갔고, 니클비 씨의 아내는 자기들 결혼을 떳떳이 밝히자고 줄곧 졸랐지만, 그는 단호히 거절했어요. 그 여자는 적막한 시골집에 홀로 남겨져, 흥청대며 술 마시는 사냥꾼들 말고는 사람 구경을 거의 못 하고 지냈어요. 그는 런던에 살면서 제 사업에만 매달렸죠. 성난 말다툼과 서로 헐뜯는 일이 벌어졌고, 결혼한 지 칠 년이 다 되어 ― 오빠가 죽어서 모든 게 정리될 날을 몇 주 앞두고 ― 그 여자는 더 젊은 남자와 눈이 맞아 달아나, 그를 버리고 떠났습니다.\"\n\n여기서 그가 말을 멈췄어. 하지만 랄프는 꼼짝도 안 했지. 그러자 두 형제가 그에게 계속하라고 손짓했어.\n\n\"이런 사정들을 제가 그 사람 입으로 직접 알게 된 게 바로 그때였습니다. 그땐 이미 비밀도 아니었어요. 오빠며 다른 사람들도 다 알고 있었으니까요. 하지만 저한테 그 얘기를 한 건 그래서가 아니라, 제가 필요했기 때문이었죠. 그는 달아난 두 사람을 뒤쫓았어요. 누구는 아내의 수치를 빌미로 돈을 뜯어내려는 거라 했지만, 제 생각엔 무슨 끔찍한 복수를 하려던 거였어요. 그게 다른 쪽 못지않게, 아니 어쩌면 그 이상으로 그 사람 본성이었으니까요. 그는 두 사람을 못 찾았고, 그 여자는 얼마 안 가 죽었습니다. 그가 그 아이를 좋아하게 될지도 모른다고 생각하기 시작한 건지, 아니면 그 아이가 절대 제 어미 손에 들어가지 못하게 하고 싶었던 건지는 모르겠어요. 다만 떠나기 전에, 그는 그 아이를 집으로 데려오는 일을 저한테 맡겼습니다. 그리고 저는 그렇게 했고요.\"\n\n그는 이 대목에서부터 한층 더 겸허한 어조로, 아주 낮은 목소리로 말을 이어 갔어. 다시 이야기를 시작하면서 랄프를 가리켰지.\n\n\"그는 저를 모질게 ― 잔인하게 ― 부려 먹었어요. 얼마 전 길에서 그를 만났을 때 어떻게 그랬는지 일깨워 줬죠. 그래서 저는 그를 미워했습니다. 저는 그 아이를 그 사람 집으로 데려와, 앞쪽 다락방에 들였어요. 돌봄을 못 받아 아이는 몹시 병약했고, 저는 어쩔 수 없이 의사를 불렀습니다. 의사는 공기 좋은 데로 옮기지 않으면 아이가 죽을 거라고 했어요. 그 말이 제 머릿속에 처음으로 그 생각을 심어 준 것 같아요. 저는 그때 그 짓을 저질렀습니다. 그가 여섯 주 동안 집을 비웠다가 돌아왔을 때, 저는 그한테 말했어요 ― 정황을 빈틈없이 꾸미고 짜 맞춰서, 아무도 저를 의심할 수 없게 ― 그 아이가 죽어서 묻혔다고요. 그가 무슨 속셈을 품었다가 어그러진 건지, 아니면 천륜의 정 같은 게 있었던 건지, 어쨌든 그는 그 일에 슬퍼했어요. 그래서 저는 언젠가 이 비밀을 까발려서 그한테서 돈을 뜯어내는 수단으로 삼겠다는 결심을 굳혔죠. 저도 다른 사람들 대부분처럼 요크셔 학교 이야기를 들어 본 적이 있었어요. 저는 그 아이를 스퀴어스라는 사람이 운영하는 학교에 데려가서 거기 맡겼습니다. 아이한테 스마이크라는 이름을 지어 줬고요. 해마다, 육 년 동안 그 아이 몫으로 일 년에 이십 파운드씩 냈어요. 그러는 내내 비밀은 한마디도 입 밖에 안 냈죠. 또 한 번 모진 대접을 받고 나서 그의 일자리를 그만두었고, 그하고 또 다투었거든요. 저는 이 나라에서 쫓겨났어요. 거의 팔 년을 떠나 있었습니다. 다시 고국에 돌아오자마자, 저는 요크셔로 내려가서 저녁 무렵 그 마을에 몰래 숨어들어 학교 아이들에 대해 알아봤어요. 그러다 알게 됐죠. 제가 거기 맡겼던 그 아이가, 제 친아버지와 같은 이름을 가진 젊은이와 함께 달아나 버렸다는 걸요. 저는 런던에서 그 아버지를 찾아내서, 제가 그한테 해 줄 수 있는 이야기가 있다는 걸 넌지시 비치며 입에 풀칠할 약간의 돈을 얻어 보려 했어요. 하지만 그는 협박을 하며 저를 내쫓았죠. 그래서 저는 그의 서기를 찾아냈고, 조금씩 조금씩 다가가며, 저와 이야기를 나눌 만한 그럴듯한 까닭이 있다는 걸 그에게 보여 주면서, 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지를 알아냈습니다. 그 아이가 제 아버지라고 주장하는 그 사람의 아들이 아니라고 그에게 말해 준 게 바로 저였어요. 이 모든 동안 저는 그 아이를 한 번도 본 적이 없었습니다. 마침내 저는 바로 그 서기한테서, 그 아이가 몹시 아프다는 것과 어디 있는지를 들었어요. 저는 그곳으로 내려갔습니다. 할 수만 있다면 그 아이 기억 속에 저를 다시 떠올리게 해서 제 이야기를 뒷받침하려고요. 저는 느닷없이 그 아이 앞에 나타났어요. 그런데 제가 미처 말을 꺼내기도 전에 아이가 저를 알아봤죠 ― 그 가엾은 것이 저를 기억할 만한 사연이 충분했으니까요! ― 그리고 저도, 인도에서 그 아이를 만났대도 그 아이라고 맹세할 수 있었을 겁니다. 그 어린아이한테서 봤던 그 애처로운 얼굴을 알아본 거죠. 며칠을 망설이다가, 저는 그 아이를 돌보던 젊은 신사를 찾아갔어요. 그리고 그 아이가 죽었다는 걸 알게 됐습니다. 그분이 알아요. 그 아이가 얼마나 금방 저를 다시 알아봤는지, 저를, 그리고 제가 자기를 그 학교에 두고 떠난 일을 얼마나 자주 이야기했는지, 그리고 자기가 기억하는 다락방 이야기를 어떻게 그분한테 했는지 ― 그게 바로 제가 말씀드린 그 다락방이고, 지금 이날까지도 그 아이 아버지 집에 그대로 있는 곳입니다. 이게 제 이야기예요. 저는 저 교장과 얼굴을 맞대게 해 달라고 요구합니다. 이 이야기의 어느 부분이든 가능한 모든 증거를 들이대 보세요. 이게 너무나 사실이라는 것과, 제 영혼에 이 죄가 얹혀 있다는 걸 제가 보여 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"가엾은 사람 같으니!\" 형제들이 말했어. \"이 일을 어떻게 보상할 셈이오?\"\n\n\"못 합니다, 여러분, 못 해요! 저는 보상할 길도 없고, 이제 바랄 것도 없습니다. 저는 나이도 늙었고, 비참함과 시름으로는 더 늙어 버렸어요. 이 고백이 제게 가져다줄 거라곤 새로운 고통과 벌뿐입니다. 하지만 저는 고백하고, 무슨 일이 닥치든 거기에 따르겠습니다. 저는 한 사람의 머리 위로 이 끔찍한 응보가 떨어지게 만드는 도구로 쓰였어요. 못된 목적을 그악스럽게 좇다가 제 친자식을 박해하고 몰아세워 죽음에 이르게 한 사람의 머리 위로 말이죠. 그건 저한테도 떨어질 게 분명합니다. 떨어질 거라는 걸 압니다. 제 보상은 너무 늦게 왔어요. 그리고 이 세상에서도, 저세상에서도, 저는 다시는 희망을 가질 수 없습니다!\"\n\n그가 말을 채 끝내기도 전에, 랄프가 앉아 있던 자리 바로 옆 탁자 위에 놓인, 그 방의 단 하나뿐인 등불이 바닥으로 내동댕이쳐졌어. 그래서 그들은 어둠 속에 남겨졌지. 다른 불을 켜느라 약간의 소란이 있었어. 그 짬은 정말 아무것도 아닐 만큼 짧았지. 그런데 불이 다시 켜졌을 때, 랄프 니클비는 사라지고 없었어.\n\n마음씨 좋은 두 형제와 팀 링킨워터는 그가 돌아올지 어떨지를 두고 한동안 이야기를 나눴어. 그러다 그가 돌아오지 않으리라는 게 분명해지자, 그를 뒤쫓아 사람을 보낼지 말지 망설였지. 마침내, 그가 그 면담 내내 한 자세로 꼼짝도 않고 묘하게 입을 다문 채 앉아 있던 걸 떠올리고는, 어쩌면 그가 병이 났을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었어. 그래서 이제 시간이 아주 늦긴 했지만, 무슨 핑계를 대서라도 그의 집에 사람을 보내기로 마음먹었어. 브루커를 어찌 처리해야 할지, 그의 뜻을 물어보지 않고는 알 수가 없어서 그걸 핑계로 삼아, 잠자리에 들기 전에 이 결정을 실행에 옮기기로 했어.\n\n# CHAPTER 61\n\n브루커가 비밀을 털어놓은 다음 날 아침, 니콜라스는 집으로 돌아왔어. 그와 집에 남아 있던 사람들의 만남은 양쪽 모두에게 깊은 감정이 북받쳐 오르는 것이었지. 그들은 니콜라스의 편지를 통해 무슨 일이 있었는지 이미 알고 있었거든. 게다가 니콜라스의 슬픔이 곧 그들의 슬픔이기도 했고, 처음엔 외롭고 의지할 데 없는 처지 때문에 그들의 동정을 샀지만 날이 갈수록 진실한 마음과 고마워할 줄 아는 성실한 천성으로 점점 더 사랑받던 한 사람의 죽음을, 그들도 니콜라스와 함께 슬퍼했지.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 니클비 부인이 눈물을 훔치며 서럽게 흐느꼈어. \"나는 평생 내 곁에 있어 준 동무들 가운데 가장 착하고, 가장 열성적이고, 가장 세심한 아이를 잃었어. 물론 우리 니콜라스, 케이트, 그리고 너희 불쌍한 아빠랑, 빨래랑 작은 포크 열두 개를 가지고 달아난 그 얌전했던 보모는 빼고 말이야. 세상에 살았던 그 모든 순한 아이들, 한결같은 성품에 정 많고 충실한 아이들 가운데서도 그 애가 단연 으뜸이었다고 믿어. 그 애가 그렇게 자랑스러워하던 정원을 지금 둘러보거나, 그 애 방에 들어가서 우리 편하라고 만든 그 자잘한 물건들이 가득한 걸 보면 말이야. 그 애가 그렇게 만들기를 좋아했고, 그렇게 잘 만들었고, 미처 다 못 끝낸 채 떠날 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐을 그 물건들 말이야. 나는 도무지 견딜 수가 없어, 정말로 못 견디겠어. 아! 이건 나한테 큰 시련이야, 정말 큰 시련이지. 우리 니콜라스, 네가 그 애한테 늘 얼마나 다정하고 착하게 대해 줬는지 떠올리면 죽는 날까지 위로가 될 거야. 나도 그 애랑 늘 얼마나 좋은 사이였는지, 그 애가 나를 얼마나 따랐는지 생각하면 그게 위로가 되거든, 가엾은 것! 네가 그 애한테 정이 들었던 건 아주 당연한 일이야, 얘야. 아주 그렇지. 물론 너는 정이 들었고, 이번 일로 무척 마음이 무너졌겠지. 너를 보고 네가 얼마나 변했는지 보면 그건 단번에 알 수 있어. 그래도 내 심정이 어떤지는 아무도 몰라. 아무도 알 수가 없지. 도무지 불가능해!\"\n\n니클비 부인이 더없이 진심으로, 늘 자기를 맨 앞에 세우는 그 특유의 방식대로 슬픔을 쏟아 내는 동안, 그런 감정에 잠긴 사람은 부인만이 아니었어. 케이트는 남을 먼저 헤아릴 때면 으레 자기 자신을 잊는 데 익숙했지만, 이번만큼은 슬픔을 누를 수가 없었지. 매들린도 케이트 못지않게 마음이 흔들렸어. 그리고 마음씨 따뜻하고 정직한 가엾은 라크리비 아주머니는 니콜라스가 없는 사이에 들렀다가, 슬픈 소식이 전해진 뒤로는 모두를 위로하고 다독이는 일밖에 한 게 없었는데, 니콜라스가 문으로 들어오는 걸 보자마자 계단에 털썩 주저앉아 눈물을 펑펑 쏟으며 한참을 달래도 그치지 않았어.\n\n\"너무 마음이 아파서요,\" 가엾은 아주머니가 울먹였어. \"혼자 돌아오는 걸 보니까 말이에요. 저 사람이 얼마나 힘들었을까 하는 생각을 떨칠 수가 없어요. 차라리 좀 더 무너지면 이렇게까지 마음이 안 아플 텐데. 저렇게 꿋꿋하게 견디니까 더 그래요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그래야지요,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"안 그렇겠어요?\"\n\n\"맞아요, 맞아,\" 자그마한 아주머니가 대답했어. \"그 착한 마음씨에 복 받을 거예요! 하지만 나처럼 단순한 사람한테는 처음엔 이게 꼭 이런 것 같단 말이에요. 이런 말 하면 안 되는 거 알아요, 곧 후회할 거고요. 그래도 이건 당신이 한 그 모든 일에 비하면 너무 보잘것없는 보답 같단 말이에요.\"\n\n\"아니에요,\" 니콜라스가 부드럽게 말했어. \"그 애의 마지막 나날이 평화롭고 행복했다는 걸 아는 것, 그리고 내가 늘 그 애 곁을 지켰고, 백 가지 사정 때문에 못 그럴 수도 있었는데 그 애 곁에 있을 수 있었다는 걸 기억하는 것, 그보다 더 좋은 보답이 어디 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"그렇고말고요,\" 라크리비 아주머니가 흐느꼈어. \"정말 맞는 말이에요. 나야말로 은혜도 모르고 불경하고 못된 바보 늙은이지요, 알아요.\"\n\n그러더니 마음씨 착한 아주머니는 다시 울음을 터뜨렸고, 정신을 차려 보려고 웃으려 애를 썼어. 웃음과 울음이 그렇게 갑작스레 맞부딪쳐서 서로 주도권을 다투었지. 그 결과는 무승부였고, 라크리비 아주머니는 그만 히스테리를 일으키고 말았어.\n\n다들 어지간히 잠잠해지고 마음을 추스를 때까지 기다린 뒤, 긴 여행 끝에 쉴 필요가 있었던 니콜라스는 자기 방으로 물러갔어. 그러고는 옷을 입은 채 그대로 침대에 몸을 던지고는 깊은 잠에 빠졌지. 잠에서 깨어 보니 케이트가 침대맡에 앉아 있었어. 케이트는 니콜라스가 눈을 뜬 걸 보고 몸을 숙여 입을 맞췄지.\n\n\"오빠가 다시 집에 와서 얼마나 기쁜지 말해 주려고 왔어.\"\n\n\"그래도 내가 너를 보고 얼마나 기쁜지는 말로 못 한단다, 케이트.\"\n\n\"우린 오빠가 돌아오기를 어찌나 애타게 기다렸는지 몰라,\" 케이트가 말했어. \"엄마랑 나랑, 그리고 매들린도.\"\n\n\"네 지난번 편지에선 그분이 아주 잘 지낸다고 했지,\" 니콜라스가 다소 서둘러 말했고, 말하면서 얼굴이 붉어졌어. \"내가 없는 동안 그분 앞날에 대해 형제분들이 무슨 계획을 세우고 있다는 얘기는 없었니?\"\n\n\"아, 한마디도 없었어,\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"난 그 언니랑 헤어진다고 생각하면 슬퍼서 견딜 수가 없어. 오빠도 분명히 그러길 바라지 않잖아, 니콜라스!\"\n\n니콜라스는 다시 얼굴이 붉어졌어. 그러고는 창가의 작은 소파에 누이 옆으로 앉으며 말했어.\n\n\"그래, 케이트, 그래, 나도 바라지 않아. 다른 사람한테라면 내 진짜 마음을 숨기려고 애썼겠지만, 너한테는 말할게. 짧고 분명하게, 케이트, 나는 그 사람을 사랑해.\"\n\n케이트의 눈이 반짝였어. 뭔가 대답을 하려는데, 니콜라스가 케이트의 팔에 손을 얹고 말을 이었어.\n\n\"이건 너 말고는 아무도 몰라야 해. 그 사람은 누구보다도 마지막까지 몰라야 하고.\"\n\n\"오빠!\"\n\"누구보다도 마지막까지. 절대로, 절대라는 게 까마득히 먼 날이긴 하지만 말이야. 가끔은 내가 떳떳하게 이 마음을 그 사람한테 말할 수 있는 날이 올지도 모른다고 생각해 보려고 해. 그런데 그건 너무 멀어. 너무나 까마득해서, 그날이 오기까지 수많은 세월이 흘러야 하고, 그날이 정말 온다 해도 (온다면 말이지) 나는 지금의 나와는 아주 딴사람이 되어 있을 거야. 젊음과 낭만의 시절을 훌쩍 넘겨 버린 채로. 그 사람을 향한 사랑만큼은 끝나지 않았겠지만 말이야. 그러니 나조차도 그런 희망이 다 얼마나 헛된 꿈인지 느껴져. 차라리 내 손으로 거칠게 짓밟아 버리고 아픔을 한 번에 끝내는 게 낫지, 세월이 그 희망을 천천히 시들게 하면서 실망을 두고두고 남겨 두게 두느니 말이야. 안 돼, 케이트! 내가 떠나 있는 동안, 세상을 떠난 그 가엾은 아이가 늘 내 눈앞에 있었어. 그 고결한 형제분들의 넘치는 너그러움을 보여 주는 또 하나의 본보기로 말이야. 내가 할 수 있는 한, 나는 그 너그러움에 걸맞은 사람이 될 거야. 전에는 그분들에 대한 마땅한 도리에 흔들렸을지 몰라도, 이제는 그 도리를 엄격히 지키고, 더 이상의 망설임과 유혹은 내 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 치워 버리기로 마음먹었어.\"\n\n\"오빠가 한마디 더 하기 전에,\" 케이트가 새파랗게 질려서 말했어. \"내가 할 얘기를 들어야 해. 일부러 그 얘기를 하러 왔는데, 용기가 안 났어. 그런데 오빠가 지금 한 말이 나한테 새 용기를 줬어.\" 케이트는 머뭇거리다가 울음을 터뜨렸어.\n\n케이트의 그런 태도에는 다음에 무슨 말이 나올지 니콜라스가 짐작하게 하는 무언가가 있었어. 케이트가 말을 꺼내려 했지만 눈물 때문에 막혔지.\n\n\"자, 이 바보 같은 녀석,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"케이트, 케이트, 의젓하게 굴어야지! 네가 무슨 말을 하려는지 알 것 같아. 프랭크 씨 얘기지, 그렇지?\"\n\n케이트는 오빠 어깨에 고개를 묻고 \"응\" 하고 흐느꼈어.\n\n\"그리고 아마 내가 없는 동안 그 사람이 너한테 청혼했겠지,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그렇지? 그래. 그래, 그래. 봐, 막상 나한테 말하는 게 그렇게 어렵지도 않잖아. 그 사람이 청혼했지?\"\n\n\"내가 거절했어,\" 케이트가 말했어.\n\n\"그래, 그런데 왜?\"\n\n\"그 사람한테 말했어,\" 케이트가 떨리는 목소리로 말했어. \"오빠가 엄마한테 했던 그 얘기를 다 했어, 나중에야 알게 된 거지만. 그 사람한테도 숨길 수 없었고 오빠한테도 못 숨기겠는데, 그게, 그게 아프고 큰 시련이긴 했지만, 그래도 단호하게 그렇게 했어. 그리고 다시는 나를 보러 오지 말라고 부탁했어.\"\n\n\"그게 바로 내 용감한 케이트지!\" 니콜라스가 케이트를 가슴에 꼭 끌어안으며 말했어. \"네가 그럴 줄 알았어.\"\n\n\"그 사람은 내 결심을 돌려 보려고 했어,\" 케이트가 말했어. \"그리고 내 결정이 어떻든 간에, 자기가 한 일을 삼촌들한테 알릴 뿐 아니라 오빠한테도 돌아오는 대로 곧장 알리겠다고 했어. 내가 아무래도,\" 케이트는 잠깐 되찾았던 침착함을 다시 잃으며 덧붙였어. \"그렇게 사심 없는 사랑을 내가 얼마나 깊이 느꼈는지, 그 사람의 앞날의 행복을 얼마나 간절히 빌었는지, 충분히 강하게 말하지 못한 것 같아. 만약 오빠가 그 사람이랑 얘기하게 되면, 나는, 나는 그 사람이 그걸 알았으면 좋겠어.\"\n\n\"케이트, 너는 옳고 떳떳하다고 믿는 일을 위해 이렇게 희생을 했으면서, 내가 내 희생은 망설일 거라고 생각했니?\" 니콜라스가 다정하게 말했어.\n\n\"아, 아니! 오빠 처지가 나랑 같았다면 안 그랬겠지만, 그래도—\"\n\n\"그런데 똑같아,\" 니콜라스가 말을 잘랐어. \"매들린이 우리 은인분들의 가까운 친척은 아니지만, 그 못지않게 소중한 인연으로 그분들과 단단히 얽혀 있어. 그리고 내가 그분의 사연을 맨 처음 맡게 된 건, 그분들이 나를 한없이 믿고, 내가 강철처럼 진실하다고 믿어 주셨기 때문이야. 그분이 우리 집에 머물게 된 사정이나, 다행히 내가 그분께 해 드릴 수 있었던 그 자그마한 도움을 이용해서 그분의 마음을 얻으려 든다면, 그게 얼마나 비열한 짓이겠어. 만약 내가 성공한다면, 그 결과는 형제분들이 그분을 친자식으로 삼으려던 그 간절한 소망에서 실망하게 되는 거고, 또 내가 그토록 비열하고 떳떳하지 못하게 옭아맨 그 젊은 처자를 향한 그분들의 동정심에 내 출세를 기대려는 꼴이 되는 거잖아. 그분의 그 고마운 마음과 따뜻한 정마저 내 잇속과 셈속에 써먹고, 그분의 불행을 가지고 장사를 하는 거지! 게다가 나는, 케이트, 결코 잊지 않을 다른 책임들을 지는 게 내 의무이자 자랑이고 기쁨인 사람이야. 그리고 이미 편안하고 행복하게 살아갈 방편이 있으니, 그 너머를 넘볼 권리도 없는 사람이고! 나는 이 짐을 내 마음에서 치우기로 마음먹었어. 지금까지도 내가 잘못한 게 아닐까 싶어. 그래서 오늘, 나는 아무것도 숨기거나 얼버무리지 않고 체리블 씨께 내 진짜 이유를 털어놓고, 이 아가씨를 당장 다른 집 보호 아래로 옮겨 달라고 간청할 거야.\"\n\n\"오늘? 그렇게 빨리?\"\n\n\"몇 주 동안 이 생각을 했는데, 왜 미루겠어? 방금 내가 겪은 그 일이 나를 돌아보게 하고, 더 절실하고 신중하게 의무를 느끼도록 일깨워 줬다면, 그 인상이 식을 때까지 왜 기다리겠어? 너는 나를 말리진 않을 거지, 케이트, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"오빠도 알겠지만, 부자가 될 수도 있잖아,\" 케이트가 말했어.\n\n\"내가 부자가 될 수도 있다고!\" 니콜라스가 서글픈 미소를 지으며 되뇌었어. \"그래, 늙을 수도 있지! 그래도 부자든 가난뱅이든, 늙든 젊든, 우리 둘은 서로에게 언제나 똑같을 거고, 우리의 위안은 바로 거기에 있어. 우리한테 집이 하나뿐이라 한들 어때? 너와 나에게 그 집이 외로운 곳이 될 리는 없잖아. 우리가 이 첫 마음에 끝까지 충실해서 다른 인연을 안 맺는다 한들 어때? 그건 우리를 묶어 주는 그 단단한 사슬에 고리 하나가 더해지는 것뿐이야. 우리가 소꿉동무였던 게 어제 일 같은데, 케이트, 우리가 점잖은 노인이 되어 이 근심들을 돌아보게 되는 날도 내일 일처럼 금세 올 거야. 지금 우리가 어린 시절의 근심을 돌아보듯이 말이야. 그리고 한때는 이런 일들이 우리를 흔들 수 있었지, 하고 서글픈 즐거움으로 떠올리게 되겠지. 어쩌면 그때, 우리가 별난 노인이 되어 발걸음이 더 가볍고 머리가 세지 않았던 시절을 얘기할 때면, 우리를 이토록 서로에게 정들게 하고, 우리 삶을 그 평온하고 잔잔히 미끄러져 내려온 물줄기로 돌려놓은 그 시련들에 오히려 고마워하게 될지도 몰라. 그리고 우리 사연을 어렴풋이 전해 들은 우리 주변의 젊은이들이, 지금의 너와 나만큼 젊은 그 아이들이, 위로를 받으러 우리한테 찾아와서, 희망과 미숙함으로는 미처 절실히 느끼기도 어려운 괴로움을 늙은 노총각 오빠와 노처녀 누이의 너그러운 귀에 쏟아 놓겠지.\"\n\n니콜라스가 이런 그림을 그려 보이자 케이트는 눈물을 글썽이면서도 미소를 지었어. 하지만 그건 슬픔의 눈물은 아니었고, 니콜라스가 말을 멈춘 뒤에도 계속 흘러내렸지.\n\n\"내 말이 맞지, 케이트?\" 잠깐 침묵이 흐른 뒤에 니콜라스가 말했어.\n\n\"꼭 맞아, 정말 그래, 오빠. 그리고 내가 오빠가 바랐을 대로 행동했다는 게 얼마나 기쁜지 몰라.\"\n\n\"후회는 안 해?\"\n\n\"아, 아, 아니야,\" 케이트가 작은 발로 바닥에 무슨 무늬를 그리며 머뭇머뭇 말했어. \"물론 떳떳하고 옳은 일을 한 건 후회 안 해. 그래도 이런 일이 일어났다는 건 후회돼. 적어도 가끔은 후회가 돼, 그러다 또 가끔은, 나도 무슨 말을 하는지 모르겠어. 난 그저 연약한 계집애일 뿐이잖아, 니콜라스. 그래서 이 일로 무척 마음이 흔들렸어.\"\n\n이 순간 니콜라스에게 만 파운드가 있었다면, 그가 발그레한 뺨에 눈을 내리뜬 그 아이를 향한 너그러운 사랑으로, 자기 자신은 까맣게 잊은 채 케이트의 행복을 지키기 위해 그 마지막 한 푼까지 다 내주었으리라고 단언해도 결코 허풍이 아니야. 하지만 그가 할 수 있는 거라곤 다정한 말로 케이트를 다독이고 위로하는 것뿐이었지. 그 말들이 어찌나 사랑과 다정함으로, 또 밝은 격려로 가득했던지, 가엾은 케이트는 오빠 목을 끌어안고는 다시는 울지 않겠다고 했어.\n\n\"어떤 남자가,\" 니콜라스는 얼마 뒤 형제분들의 집으로 가는 길에 자랑스러운 마음으로 생각했어. \"케이트 같은 마음을 갖는 것만으로 어떤 재산의 희생이든 충분히 보상받지 못하겠는가. 마음이란 가볍고 금은이란 무겁다는 것만 빼면, 그 마음은 어떤 칭송으로도 모자란데 말이야. 프랭크한테는 돈이 있고, 더 바랄 것도 없지. 그 돈으로 케이트 같은 보물을 어디서 살 수 있겠어? 그런데도 어울리지 않는 결혼에서는 늘 부자 쪽이 큰 희생을 하고, 다른 쪽이 횡재를 하는 걸로들 여기지! 하지만 이건 사랑에 빠진 사람처럼, 아니면 멍청이처럼 하는 생각이군. 뭐, 그 둘은 거의 비슷한 거겠지만.\"\n\n지금 가는 길의 용건에는 도무지 어울리지 않는 그런 생각들을, 이런 자책과 그 못지않게 단단한 다른 자책들로 다잡으면서, 니콜라스는 가던 길을 계속 가서 팀 링킨워터 앞에 모습을 드러냈어.\n\n\"아! 니클비 씨!\" 팀이 외쳤어. \"복 받으세요! 어떻게 지내셨어요? 잘 지내셨죠? 아주 잘 지냈고 더없이 좋았다고 말해 봐요. 어서요.\"\n\n\"아주 잘 지냈어요,\" 니콜라스가 두 손으로 팀의 손을 맞잡으며 말했어.\n\n\"아!\" 팀이 말했어. \"그런데 막상 보니 피곤해 보이는데요. 들어 봐요! 저기 있어요, 들리세요? 저게 딕이에요, 검은새 말이에요. 당신이 떠난 뒤로는 영 제정신이 아니었답니다. 이제 당신 없이는 못 지내요. 나한테 그러는 것만큼이나 당신한테도 자연스럽게 따른다니까요.\"\n\n\"내가 당신만큼이나 자기 눈길을 받을 만한 가치가 있다고 여긴다면, 딕은 내가 생각했던 것보다 훨씬 영리하지 못한 녀석이군요,\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어.\n\n\"아니, 내가 말씀드릴게요,\" 팀이 즐겨 취하는 자세로 서서 펜 깃으로 새장을 가리키며 말했어. \"저 새한테 아주 신기한 점이 있는데요, 저 녀석이 조금이라도 눈길을 주는 사람은 오직 찰스 씨하고, 네드 씨하고, 당신하고, 나뿐이라는 거예요.\"\n\n여기서 팀은 말을 멈추고 걱정스레 니콜라스를 흘긋 보았어. 그러다 뜻밖에 니콜라스와 눈이 마주치자 다시 말했지. \"당신하고 나뿐이에요, 당신하고 나뿐이라고요.\" 그러고는 다시 니콜라스를 흘긋 보더니, 그의 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했어. \"나는 마음에 걸리는 걸 미루는 데는 영 젬병이라서요. 물어보지 않으려고 했는데, 그 가엾은 아이에 대해 좀 자세히 듣고 싶어요. 그 애가 체리블 형제 얘기를 조금이라도 하던가요?\"\n\n\"네,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"여러 번, 정말 여러 번 했지요.\"\n\n\"그 애답네요,\" 팀이 눈물을 훔치며 대꾸했어. \"정말 그 애다워요.\"\n\n\"그리고 당신 이름도 스무 번은 입에 올렸어요,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"링킨워터 씨께 자기 사랑을 전해 달라고 자주 부탁했지요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 정말 그랬어요?\" 팀이 대놓고 흐느끼며 되물었어. \"가엾은 녀석! 그 애를 시내에 묻을 수 있었으면 좋았을 텐데요. 광장 건너편의 그 자그마한 묘지만 한 곳이 런던을 통틀어 없거든요. 둘레에 회계 사무소들이 빙 둘러 있어서, 날씨 좋은 날 거기 들어가면 열린 창문 너머로 장부랑 금고가 다 보인답니다. 그 애가 나한테 사랑을 전했다고요? 그 애가 나를 떠올릴 줄은 몰랐는데요. 가엾은 녀석, 가엾은 녀석! 게다가 사랑을 전하다니!\"\n\n팀은 이 자그마한 기억의 표시에 어찌나 완전히 무너졌던지, 그 순간 더 이상 대화를 이어 갈 수가 없었어. 그래서 니콜라스는 조용히 빠져나와 찰스 형의 방으로 갔지.\n\n전까지 그가 꿋꿋함과 의연함을 지켜 왔다면, 그건 적잖은 아픔을 치르며 안간힘을 쓴 덕분이었어. 하지만 그 마음씨 좋은 노신사의 따뜻한 환대와 정겨운 태도, 꾸밈없는 소박한 위로에 그의 가슴이 뭉클해졌고, 아무리 속으로 안간힘을 써도 그걸 드러내지 않을 수가 없었지.\n\n\"자, 자, 이보게,\" 인정 많은 상인이 말했어. \"우리가 의기소침해지면 안 되네, 안 되지. 우리는 불행을 견디는 법을 배워야 하고, 죽음 속에도 위안의 샘이 많다는 걸 기억해야 해. 이 가엾은 아이는 하루하루 살아갈수록 세상에 점점 덜 어울리게 되고, 자기 부족함 때문에 점점 더 불행해졌을 거야. 이렇게 된 게 차라리 나아, 이보게. 그래, 그래, 그래, 이렇게 된 게 나아.\"\n\n\"그 점도 다 생각해 봤습니다, 어르신,\" 니콜라스가 목을 가다듬으며 대답했어. \"정말이지 저도 그렇게 느낍니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그게 잘된 거야,\" 체리블 씨가 대답했어. 그는 그렇게 위로하는 와중에도 정직한 늙은 팀 못지않게 마음이 흔들리고 있었지. \"그게 잘된 거야. 내 동생 네드는 어디 있나? 팀 링킨워터, 내 동생 네드는 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"트리머스 씨랑 같이 나가셨어요. 그 딱한 사람을 병원에 들여보내고, 그 사람 아이들한테 간호사를 보내는 일 때문에요,\" 팀이 말했어.\n\n\"내 동생 네드는 훌륭한 사람이야, 대단한 사람이지!\" 찰스 형은 문을 닫고 니콜라스에게 돌아오며 외쳤어. \"동생이 자네를 보면 무척 기뻐할 거야, 이보게. 우리는 날마다 자네 얘기를 했다네.\"\n\n\"사실 말씀드리면, 어르신께서 혼자 계셔서 다행입니다,\" 니콜라스가 자연스레 다소 머뭇거리며 말했어. \"긴히 드릴 말씀이 있어서요. 잠깐만 시간을 내주실 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"물론이지, 물론이고말고,\" 찰스 형이 걱정스러운 낯빛으로 니콜라스를 바라보며 대답했어. \"말해 보게, 이보게, 말해 봐.\"\n\n\"어디서, 어떻게 시작해야 할지 잘 모르겠습니다,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"만약 어떤 사람이 다른 사람에게 사랑과 존경으로 가득 찰 까닭이 있다면, 그를 위한 일이라면 아무리 힘든 봉사도 기쁨과 즐거움이 될 만한 그런 정으로, 또 자기 천성의 온 열성과 충성을 불러일으킬 만한 그런 고마운 기억으로 가득 찰 까닭이 있다면, 그게 바로 제가 어르신께 품은 마음입니다. 제 마음과 영혼을 다해 그렇습니다, 믿어 주십시오!\"\n\n\"믿네,\" 노신사가 대답했어. \"그렇게 믿으니 나도 행복하군. 한 번도 의심한 적 없고, 앞으로도 의심하지 않을 걸세. 정말 절대 그러지 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"그렇게 다정히 말씀해 주시니,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"용기를 내서 계속 말씀드리겠습니다. 처음 저를 믿고 브레이 양에게 심부름을 보내셨을 때, 저는 그분을 훨씬 전부터 보아 왔다고 말씀드렸어야 했습니다. 그분의 아름다움이 제게 지울 수 없는 인상을 남겼고, 그분을 찾아내 그 사연을 알아보려고 애썼지만 헛수고였다고 말입니다. 그런데도 말씀드리지 않았던 건, 제 약한 감정을 이겨 내고 어르신에 대한 의무를 위해 다른 모든 마음을 다스릴 수 있으리라고 부질없이 믿었기 때문입니다.\"\n\n\"니클비 군,\" 찰스 형이 말했어. \"자네는 내가 자네한테 둔 믿음을 저버리지 않았고, 그걸 떳떳지 못하게 이용하지도 않았어. 분명 그러지 않았다고 믿네.\"\n\n\"그러지 않았습니다,\" 니콜라스가 단호하게 말했어. \"스스로를 다잡고 억눌러야 할 필요가 날마다 더 절박해지고 그만큼 더 어려워졌지만, 저는 단 한순간도 어르신께서 곁에 계셨더라면 그랬을 것과 다르게 말하거나 바라본 적이 없습니다. 단 한순간도 제게 맡겨진 일을 저버린 적이 없고, 지금 이 순간까지도 그렇습니다. 그런데 이 사랑스러운 아가씨와 끊임없이 가까이 지내고 함께 있는 것이 제 마음의 평화에 치명적이고, 처음에 세워서 지금껏 충실히 지켜 온 제 결심마저 무너뜨릴지도 모르겠다는 걸 알았습니다. 한마디로, 어르신, 저는 제 자신을 믿을 수가 없습니다. 그러니 이 아가씨를 제 어머니와 누이의 보살핌에서 지체 없이 옮겨 주시기를 간청하고 또 애원합니다. 저 말고 다른 사람에게는, 그러니까 저와 이 아가씨 사이의 헤아릴 수 없는 거리를 아시는 어르신께는, 이제 어르신의 피후견인이자 각별한 보살핌을 받는 이 아가씨를 제가 마음으로나마 사랑한다는 게 더없는 경솔이자 주제넘은 짓으로 보일 줄 압니다. 그렇다는 걸 압니다. 하지만 제가 본 대로 그분을 보고, 그분의 삶이 어떠했는지 아는 사람이라면 누가 그분을 사랑하지 않을 수 있겠습니까? 제겐 그것 말고는 변명이 없습니다. 그리고 이 유혹에서 달아날 수도 없고, 그 대상이 끊임없이 눈앞에 있는데 이 마음을 억누를 수도 없으니, 그분을 옮겨 달라고, 그래서 제가 그분을 잊을 수 있게 해 달라고 비는 것 말고 제가 무엇을 할 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"니클비 군,\" 노신사가 잠깐 침묵한 뒤에 말했어. \"자네는 더 할 수 있는 게 없네. 자네 같은 젊은이를 이런 시련에 내몬 게 내 잘못이었어. 무슨 일이 벌어질지 미리 내다봤어야 했는데. 고맙네, 이보게, 고마워. 매들린은 옮기도록 하겠네.\"\n\n\"한 가지 부탁을 들어주신다면, 어르신, 그분께는 이 고백을 절대 알리지 마셔서, 그분이 저를 좋게 기억할 수 있게 해 주십시오—\"\n\n\"그렇게 하지,\" 체리블 씨가 말했어. \"그런데, 나한테 할 얘기가 이게 전부인가?\"\n\n\"아닙니다!\" 니콜라스가 그의 눈을 마주 보며 대답했어. \"전부가 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"나머지는 내가 아네,\" 체리블 씨가 이 선뜻한 대답에 적잖이 안도한 듯 말했어. \"언제 알게 됐나?\"\n\n\"오늘 아침 집에 도착했을 때입니다.\"\n\n\"곧장 나한테 와서, 자네 누이가 분명 일러 줬을 그 얘기를 하는 게 도리라고 느꼈군?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"프랭크 씨와 먼저 얘기를 나눴으면 했지만요.\"\n\n\"프랭크는 어젯밤에 나랑 같이 있었네,\" 노신사가 대답했어. \"자네는 잘했어, 니클비 군. 아주 잘했네, 이보게. 다시 한번 고맙네.\"\n\n이 대목에서 니콜라스는 몇 마디 덧붙일 수 있게 해 달라고 청했어. 그는 자기가 한 말이 케이트와 매들린의 사이를 갈라놓는 일로 이어지지 않기를 감히 바랐지. 두 사람은 서로 정이 들었는데, 그 사이가 끊긴다면 둘에게 큰 아픔이 될 거고, 무엇보다도 그 불행한 원인이 된 자기에게 회한과 아픔이 될 거라는 걸 그는 알았거든. 이 모든 일이 다 잊히고 나면, 프랭크와 자기가 여전히 따뜻한 벗으로 남기를, 그리고 자기의 보잘것없는 집이나, 그 집에 기꺼이 남아 자기의 조용한 살림을 함께 나누려는 그 사람에 대한 어떤 말이나 생각도 두 사람 사이의 화목을 다시는 흐트러뜨리지 않기를 바랐어. 그는 그날 아침 자기와 케이트 사이에 오간 일을 할 수 있는 한 그대로 들려주었어. 케이트를 이야기하는 그의 목소리에는 그토록 따뜻한 자랑과 애정이 묻어났고, 어떤 이기적인 미련도 이겨 내고 서로의 사랑 안에서 만족하고 행복하게 살아갈 수 있다는 확신을 그토록 밝게 펼쳐 보였기에, 그 얘기를 듣고 마음이 움직이지 않을 사람은 거의 없었을 거야. 그 자신도 그 어느 때보다 마음이 움직여서, 형제분들에 대한 헌신과, 그분들을 섬기며 살다가 죽기를 바라는 마음을, 서둘러 몇 마디로 — 어쩌면 가장 유창한 말 못지않게 절절하게 — 털어놓았지.\n\n이 모든 이야기를 찰스 형은 깊은 침묵 속에서 들었어. 의자를 니콜라스 쪽에서 돌려 앉아서 그 얼굴이 보이지 않았지. 그 역시 입을 열 때는 평소의 그 태도가 아니라, 그에게는 영 낯선 어떤 뻣뻣함과 어색함이 묻어났어. 니콜라스는 자기가 그분의 심기를 거스른 게 아닐까 두려웠어. 노신사는 \"아니, 아니야, 자네는 아주 잘했네\" 하고 말했지만, 그게 다였지.\n\n\"프랭크는 무심하고 어리석은 녀석이야,\" 니콜라스가 한동안 말을 멈춘 뒤에 노신사가 말했어. \"아주 무심하고 어리석은 녀석이지. 이 일이 지체 없이 마무리되도록 내가 챙기겠네. 이 얘기는 더 하지 마세, 나한테는 아주 괴로운 얘기니까. 삼십 분 뒤에 나한테 오게. 자네한테 들려줄 이상한 일들이 있고, 자네 삼촌이 오늘 오후에 나랑 같이 자기를 찾아오라고 정해 두었으니 말일세.\"\n\n\"삼촌을 찾아간다고요! 어르신과 함께요!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어.\n\n\"그래, 나와 함께,\" 노신사가 대답했어. \"삼십 분 뒤에 나한테 다시 오게. 그러면 더 얘기해 줌세.\"\n\n니콜라스는 말한 시간에 그를 다시 찾아갔고, 그제야 전날 있었던 일과, 랄프가 형제들과 잡은 약속에 대해 알려진 모든 것을 듣게 되었어. 그 약속은 그날 밤으로 잡혀 있었지. 그런데 이걸 제대로 이해하려면, 다시 거슬러 올라가 쌍둥이 형제의 집에서 나선 랄프 자신의 발자취를 따라가 볼 필요가 있어. 그러니 우리는 니콜라스를 여기 남겨 두기로 하지. 자기를 대하는 그분들의 태도에 다시 다정함이 돌아와 다소 마음이 놓이면서도, 그게 예전과는 어딘가 달라졌다는 걸 (어떤 점에서인지는 잘 몰랐지만) 느끼고 있는 니콜라스를 말이야. 그래서 그는 불안과 막막함과 심란함으로 가득 차 있었어.\n\n친애하는 독자 여러분... wait, no.\n\n집에서 살금살금 빠져나와 도둑처럼 슬그머니 자리를 떴어. 거리로 처음 나섰을 때는 마치 앞 못 보는 사람처럼 두 손으로 더듬거렸지. 그리고 서둘러 걸으면서 자꾸 어깨 너머를 돌아봤어. 상상 속에서든 실제로든 누군가가 따라오면서 자기를 붙잡고 캐물으려는 것만 같았거든. 그렇게 랄프 니클비는 도심을 뒤로하고 제 집으로 가는 길에 올랐어.\n\n밤은 어두웠고, 찬 바람이 불었어. 바람은 구름을 앞으로 사납고 빠르게 몰아댔지. 그런데 그를 따라오는 것만 같은 검고 음울한 구름 덩어리 하나가 있었어. 그것만은 다른 구름들과 함께 거센 추격에 끼어들지 않고, 뒤에서 시무룩하게 꾸물거리며 어둡고 은밀하게 미끄러져 오는 거야. 그는 그걸 자주 돌아봤어. 한두 번은 멈춰 서서 그게 지나가게 두기도 했지. 그런데 어찌 된 일인지 다시 앞으로 나아가면 그것은 여전히 뒤에 있었어. 그림자 같은 장례 행렬처럼 구슬프게 천천히 다가오면서 말이야.\n\n그는 초라하고 보잘것없는 묘지 하나를 지나야 했어. 거리보다 몇 피트 높이 솟아 있고, 낮은 난간 벽과 쇠 울타리로 거리와 갈라진 음산한 곳이었지. 무성하고 병들고 썩어 가는 자리였어. 풀과 잡초마저도 그 추레한 모습으로 이렇게 말하는 것 같았어. 자기들이 빈민들의 몸에서 돋아났고, 산 채로 김 오르는 골목과 술과 굶주림의 소굴에 절어 있던 사람들의 무덤에 뿌리를 박았다고 말이야. 그리고 정말로 그들은 거기 누워 있었어. 흙 한 줌과 널빤지 한두 장으로 산 자들과 갈라진 채, 빽빽하고 다닥다닥 누워 있었지. 마음이 썩었던 것처럼 몸도 썩어 가면서, 빽빽하고 추레한 무리로 말이야. 그들은 거기 누워 있었어. 삶과 뺨을 맞대고서. 날마다 그 위를 지나는 사람들의 발보다 깊지도 않은 곳에, 그들의 목까지 높이 쌓인 채로. 거기 그들은 누워 있었어. 소름 끼치는 한 가족으로. 그들이 땅에 묻혔을 때 그토록 후딱 일을 해치우던 혈색 좋은 목사의, 사랑하는 죽은 형제자매들이 죄다 거기 있었던 거야!\n\n이곳을 지나면서 랄프는 한 가지 일을 떠올렸어. 오래전에 자기 목을 그어 죽은 사람의 시신을 두고 배심원으로 참여한 적이 있었던 거야. 그리고 그 사람이 바로 이 자리에 묻혔다는 것도 말이야. 어째서 지금 와서 그게 떠오르는지 그는 알 수 없었어. 그동안 그렇게 자주 지나다니면서도 한 번도 그 사람을 생각한 적이 없었으니까. 또 어째서 이 일에 관심이 가는지도 알 수 없었지. 그래도 그는 떠올렸고, 관심도 갔어. 그리고 멈춰 서서 두 손으로 쇠 울타리를 움켜쥐고는 안을 골똘히 들여다봤어. 어느 게 그 사람 무덤일까 궁금해하면서 말이야.\n\n그가 이러고 있을 때, 고함과 노랫소리를 내며 술에 잔뜩 취한 사내 몇이 그를 향해 다가왔어. 그 뒤로는 그들에게 따지면서 조용히 집에 가라고 다그치는 다른 사람들이 따라왔지. 술꾼들은 기분이 한껏 좋았어. 그 가운데 작고 쪼글쪼글하고 등이 굽은 사내 하나가 춤을 추기 시작했어. 우스꽝스럽고 기괴한 모습이라, 몇 안 되는 구경꾼들이 웃었지. 랄프도 웃음이 나서, 곁에 서서 자기 얼굴을 돌아보는 사람의 웃음에 맞장구를 쳤어. 그들이 지나가 버리고 그가 다시 혼자 남았을 때, 그는 새로운 종류의 흥미를 느끼며 아까 생각을 다시 이어 갔어. 그 자살한 사람을 살아 있을 때 마지막으로 본 사람이, 그가 아주 즐거워하는 모습으로 헤어졌다고 한 게 떠올랐거든. 그리고 그때 자기와 다른 배심원들이 그 점을 얼마나 이상하게 여겼는지도 기억났어.\n\n그렇게 많은 무덤 더미 속에서 그 자리를 콕 집어낼 수는 없었어. 하지만 그 사람 자체에 대해서는 강렬하고 또렷한 모습을 머릿속에 떠올렸지. 그가 어떻게 생겼는지, 무엇 때문에 그런 짓을 했는지 말이야. 그 모든 걸 그는 쉽게 떠올렸어. 이 생각에 골똘히 매달린 탓에, 그는 떠나면서도 그 인상을 함께 데리고 갔어. 어릴 적에 누가 문에다 분필로 그려 놓은 도깨비 형상을 한번 본 뒤로 자주 눈앞에 어른거리던 것처럼 말이야. 그런데 집에 점점 가까워질수록 그는 그걸 또 잊어버렸어. 그리고 집 안이 얼마나 따분하고 쓸쓸할까 생각하기 시작했지.\n\n이 느낌이 마침내 어찌나 강해졌던지, 자기 집 문 앞에 이르렀을 때 그는 열쇠를 돌려 문을 열 마음을 좀처럼 먹지 못했어. 그래도 그걸 하고 복도로 들어섰을 땐, 문을 다시 닫으면 세상을 닫아 버리는 것 같은 기분이 들었어. 그래도 그는 문을 놓아 버렸고, 문은 요란한 소리를 내며 닫혔지. 불빛 하나 없었어. 어찌나 음산하고 차갑고 고요하던지!\n\n머리끝부터 발끝까지 부들부들 떨면서, 그는 위층으로 올라가 아까 마지막으로 방해를 받았던 방으로 갔어. 그는 자기 자신과 일종의 약속을 해 둔 터였어. 집에 도착할 때까지는 일어난 일을 생각하지 않겠다고 말이야. 이제 그는 집에 왔으니, 스스로에게 그 일을 곱씹도록 허락했지.\n\n자기 자식이었어, 자기 친자식! 그는 그 이야기를 한순간도 의심하지 않았어. 사실이라고 느꼈거든. 줄곧 그 일을 다 알고 있었던 것처럼, 이제 또렷이 알았던 거야. 자기 친자식이! 게다가 죽기까지 했어. 니콜라스 곁에서, 그를 사랑하고 마치 천사 같은 존재로 우러르면서 죽어 갔던 거야. 그게 제일 견디기 힘든 일이었어!\n\n그들은 모두 그를 외면하고 저버렸어. 그가 정작 도움이 가장 필요한 첫 순간에 말이야. 이제는 돈으로도 그들을 살 수 없었어. 모든 게 드러날 테고, 모두가 다 알게 될 테지. 젊은 귀족은 죽었고, 그 동행은 멀리 떠나 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있고, 만 파운드는 단번에 날아갔어. 그라이드와 짜 놓은 음모는 승리의 바로 그 순간에 뒤엎어졌고, 그 뒤에 꾸민 계략들도 들통났어. 자기 자신은 위험에 빠졌고, 자기가 괴롭히던 대상이자 니콜라스가 사랑하던 그 아이는, 다름 아닌 자기 가엾은 아들이었던 거야. 모든 게 무너져 내려 그를 덮쳤고, 그는 그 잔해에 짓눌려 흙먼지 속에서 엎드려 기었지.\n\n만약 자기 아이가 살아 있다는 걸 알았더라면, 아무런 속임수도 없었고 그 아이가 제 눈앞에서 자랐더라면, 그는 무심하고 데면데면하고 거칠고 모진 아버지였을지도 몰라. 그럴 법하지. 그도 그걸 느꼈어. 그래도 자기가 달랐을 수도 있다는 생각이 자꾸 들었어. 아들이 자기에게 위안이 되었을 수도 있고, 둘이 함께 행복했을 수도 있다고 말이야. 그는 이제 이런 생각이 들기 시작했어. 아들이 죽었다고 믿은 일과 아내가 달아난 일이, 자기를 이렇게 음울하고 모진 사람으로 만드는 데 한몫했을지도 모른다고. 자기가 지금처럼 거칠고 완고하지만은 않던 때가 있었던 것 같았어. 그리고 처음에 니콜라스를 미워하게 된 건, 그 애가 젊고 늠름한 데다, 어쩌면 제 머리 위에 불명예와 재산 손실을 안겨 준 그 풋내기와 닮아서였던 것 같다는 생각마저 들었지.\n\n하지만 격정과 회한이 휘몰아치는 가운데 떠오른 다정한 생각 하나, 또는 자연스러운 후회 하나는, 폭풍에 미쳐 날뛰는 바다에 떨어진 잔잔한 물방울 한 방울 같았어. 니콜라스를 향한 그의 증오는 자기 자신의 패배를 먹고 자랐어. 자기 계획에 끼어든 일을 양분 삼았고, 옛날의 그 반항과 성공으로 살을 찌웠지. 그 증오가 커진 데는 이유들이 있었어. 그것은 차츰차츰 자라고 굳세졌지. 이제 그것은 완전히 미친 광기의 높이에까지 다다랐어. 다른 누구도 아닌 그의 손이 자기 가엾은 아이를 구한 손이었다니. 그가 그 아이의 보호자이자 충실한 친구였다니. 그가 그 아이에게, 비참하게 태어난 순간부터 한 번도 받아 본 적 없는 그 사랑과 다정함을 베풀어 주었다니. 그가 그 아이에게 제 친아버지를 미워하고 그 이름마저 저주하라고 가르쳤다니. 그리고 이제 이 모든 걸 알고 느끼며 그 기억 속에서 의기양양해하다니. 이건 고리대금업자의 가슴에 쓸개즙이자 광기였어. 죽은 아이가 니콜라스를 사랑한 것, 그리고 니콜라스가 그 아이에게 정을 쏟은 것은 견딜 수 없는 고통이었어. 그 아이의 임종 장면이 떠올랐어. 니콜라스가 곁에서 돌보고 부축하고, 그 아이는 고맙다는 말을 내쉬며 그의 품에서 숨을 거두는 그 광경 말이야. 그는 그 둘이 서로 철천지원수가 되어 끝까지 미워하기를 바랐는데. 그 생각이 그를 미쳐 날뛰게 했어. 그는 이를 갈고 허공을 내리쳤어. 그리고 어둠 속에서 번뜩이는 눈으로 사납게 주위를 둘러보며 큰 소리로 외쳤지.\n\n\"나는 짓밟혀 망했어. 그 비열한 놈 말이 옳았어. 밤이 왔어! 저들에게서 더 이상의 승리를 빼앗고, 저들의 자비와 동정을 걷어찰 길은 없는 걸까? 나를 도와줄 악마는 없는 거야?\"\n\n그날 밤 그가 떠올렸던 그 형상이 다시 휙 그의 머릿속으로 미끄러져 들어왔어. 그것이 눈앞에 누워 있는 것 같았어. 이번엔 머리가 덮여 있었어. 그가 그걸 처음 봤을 때도 그랬지. 뻣뻣하게 위를 향한, 대리석 같은 발도 그는 잘 기억했어. 이어서 그의 눈앞에 검시 자리에서 그 사연을 들려주던, 창백하게 떨던 친척들이 떠올랐어. 여자들의 비명, 남자들의 말없는 두려움, 그 경악과 동요, 손 한 번 까딱하는 것으로 제 목숨을 끊고 그들 사이에 이런 소동을 일으킨 그 진흙 더미가 거둔 승리가 떠올랐던 거야.\n\n그는 더 이상 말하지 않았어. 그러다 잠시 뒤, 가만히 더듬어 방을 빠져나왔어. 그리고 메아리치는 계단을 따라 올라갔지. 맨 꼭대기까지, 앞쪽 다락방까지. 거기서 그는 등 뒤로 문을 닫고, 머물렀어.\n\n이제 그곳은 그저 잡동사니를 쌓아 두는 방일 뿐이었어. 그래도 거기엔 분해된 낡은 침대 틀 하나가 아직 남아 있었지. 그의 아들이 자던 그 침대였어. 그 방엔 다른 침대가 놓인 적이 한 번도 없었거든. 그는 황급히 그것을 피해, 거기서 될 수 있는 한 멀찍이 떨어진 곳에 앉았어.\n\n아래 거리의 불빛이 희미하게 비쳐 들었어. 그 빛을 막아 줄 블라인드도 커튼도 없는 창문을 통해 들어온 빛이었지. 그것만으로도 방의 분위기는 짐작할 만했어. 다만 여기저기 흩어진 갖가지 잡동사니, 줄로 묶은 낡은 트렁크들, 부서진 가구들을 죄다 환히 드러내 보일 만큼은 못 되었지. 천장은 비스듬한 경사 지붕이었어. 한쪽은 높고, 다른 쪽은 거의 바닥까지 내려와 있었지. 랄프는 그 가장 높은 쪽으로 눈길을 돌렸어. 그리고 몇 분 동안 거기에 눈을 가만히 박고 있다가 일어섰어. 그러고는 앉아 있던 낡은 궤짝을 그쪽으로 끌어다 그 위에 올라가서는, 두 손으로 머리 위쪽 벽을 더듬었어. 마침내 두 손에 커다란 쇠갈고리 하나가 닿았어. 들보 하나에 단단히 박혀 있는 것이었지.\n\n바로 그 순간, 아래층 문을 쾅쾅 두드리는 소리가 그를 멈춰 세웠어. 잠시 머뭇거리다가 그는 창문을 열고 누구냐고 물었지.\n\n\"니클비 씨를 뵈러 왔습니다.\" 한 목소리가 대답했어.\n\n\"그 사람한테 무슨 볼일인데?\"\n\n\"그건 니클비 씨 목소리가 아닌 것 같은데요?\" 상대가 되받았어.\n\n목소리가 닮지 않은 건 맞았어. 하지만 말한 사람은 랄프였으니, 그는 자기가 맞다고 했지.\n\n그 목소리가 대답하기를, 그 쌍둥이 형제분들이 오늘 밤 그가 만난 그 사람을 붙잡아 둬야 하는지 알고 싶어 한다고 했어. 그리고 지금이 한밤중이긴 하지만, 일을 제대로 처리하려는 마음에 사람을 보냈다고 했지.\n\n\"그래,\" 랄프가 외쳤어. \"그자를 내일까지 붙들어 둬. 그런 다음 그를 이리로 데려오라고 해. 그자랑 내 조카랑 말이야. 그리고 자기들도 직접 오라고 해. 내가 틀림없이 그들을 맞을 채비를 하고 있을 테니까.\"\n\n\"몇 시에요?\" 목소리가 물었어.\n\n\"아무 때나.\" 랄프가 사납게 대답했어. \"오후에 오라고 전해. 아무 때, 아무 분이든. 나한테는 어느 때나 다 똑같으니까.\"\n\n그는 그 사람이 멀어져 가는 발소리에 귀를 기울였어. 그 소리가 다 사라질 때까지 말이야. 그러고는 하늘을 올려다보다가, 자기를 집까지 따라오는 것만 같던 그 검은 구름을 봤어. 아니, 봤다고 생각했지. 그것은 이제 집 바로 위에 떠 있는 것처럼 보였어.\n\n\"이제 저게 무슨 뜻인지 알겠어.\" 그가 중얼거렸어. \"잠 못 이루던 밤들이며, 그 꿈들이며, 요즘 들어 내가 왜 그렇게 움츠러들었는지도 말이야. 다 이걸 가리키고 있었던 거야. 아! 제 영혼을 팔아 한동안 마음껏 날뛸 수만 있다면, 오늘 밤 나는 얼마나 짧은 동안이라도 좋으니 내 영혼을 내놓을 텐데!\"\n\n깊은 종소리 하나가 바람을 타고 들려왔어. 한 시였지.\n\n\"실컷 울려라!\" 고리대금업자가 외쳤어. \"그 쇠 혓바닥으로 말이야! 상속만 기다리는 자들 속을 뒤틀리게 하는 탄생을 위해서나, 지옥에서 맺어진 혼인을 위해서나 흥겹게 울려라. 그리고 신발이 벌써 다 닳아 버린 죽은 자들을 위해서나 구슬프게 조종을 울려라! 들키지 않아서 경건한 척하는 자들을 기도하러 불러 모으고, 이 저주받은 세상을 종말에 더 가까이 데려가는 해가 올 때마다 종을 울려 대라. 나한테는 종도 성경책도 필요 없어! 나를 두엄 더미에나 내던져, 거기서 썩어 공기나 더럽히게 두라고!\"\n\n광기와 증오와 절망이 무섭게 뒤섞인 사나운 눈길로 그는 주위를 둘러봤어. 그리고 여전히 어둡고 위협적인 머리 위 하늘을 향해 움켜쥔 주먹을 흔들고는, 창문을 닫았지.\n\n비와 우박이 유리창을 두드렸어. 굴뚝들이 흔들리고 휘청거렸지. 다 낡은 여닫이창은 바람에 덜컹거렸어. 마치 안에서 누군가 성난 손으로 그걸 박차고 열려는 것처럼 말이야. 하지만 거기엔 아무 손도 없었고, 그 창문은 다시는 열리지 않았어.\n\n\"이게 어찌 된 일이지?\" 한 사람이 외쳤어. \"그 신사분들이 그러는데, 아무리 불러도 사람이 나오질 않는다는 거예요. 두 시간째 두드려 봤다고요.\"\n\n\"그런데도 어젯밤에는 집에 들어왔어.\" 다른 사람이 말했어. \"저 위층 창문으로 누군가한테 말을 했으니까.\"\n\n그들은 몇 사람 모인 작은 무리였어. 창문 얘기가 나오자, 그들은 길로 나가 그 창문을 올려다봤지. 그러다 보니 집이 여전히 꼭 닫혀 있다는 걸 알아챘어. 가정부가 전날 밤에 그렇게 잠가 두고 나왔다고 한 그대로였지. 그 바람에 온갖 추측이 쏟아졌어. 결국엔 그 가운데 가장 대담한 두세 사람이 뒤로 돌아가, 창문으로 안에 들어가기로 했지. 나머지는 밖에 남아 애가 타서 결과를 기다리고 있었어.\n\n그들은 아래층 방을 죄다 들여다봤어. 들어가면서 덧문을 열어, 스러져 가는 빛을 들였지. 그래도 아무도 못 찾았고, 모든 게 조용하고 제자리에 놓여 있었어. 그들은 더 들어가 봐야 하나 망설였지. 그런데 한 사람이, 아직 다락방엔 안 가 봤다고, 그리고 그가 마지막으로 목격된 곳이 바로 거기라고 말했어. 그래서 그들은 거기도 들여다보기로 하고, 가만가만 올라갔어. 그 알 수 없는 분위기와 정적이 그들을 겁먹게 했거든.\n\n층계참에 잠시 서서 서로 눈치를 살핀 뒤, 수색을 거기까지 하자고 제안한 사람이 문손잡이를 돌렸어. 그리고 문을 밀어 열고는 그 틈으로 들여다보더니, 곧장 뒤로 물러섰지.\n\n\"거 참 이상하네.\" 그가 속삭였어. \"이 사람이 문 뒤에 숨어 있어! 봐!\"\n\n그들은 보려고 앞으로 몰려들었어. 그런데 그 가운데 한 사람이 큰 소리를 지르며 다른 사람들을 옆으로 밀쳐 내고는, 주머니에서 접이칼을 꺼내 방 안으로 뛰어들어 그 몸뚱이를 끊어 내렸어.\n\n그는 낡은 트렁크 하나에서 밧줄을 뜯어내, 천장의 들창 바로 아래 박힌 쇠갈고리에 목을 매단 거였어. 바로 그 자리, 십사 년 전 외롭고 쓸쓸한 어린것이던 그의 아들이, 어린아이다운 공포에 질려 그토록 자주 눈길을 던지던 그 자리였지.\n\n# CHAPTER 63\n\n치어리블 형제는 자신들을 위해서, 또 남들을 위해서 여러 가지를 선언한다. 팀 링킨워터는 자기 자신을 위해 선언을 한다.\n\n몇 주가 흘렀고, 그 일들로 받은 첫 충격도 가라앉았다. 매덜린은 떠났고, 프랭크는 자리를 비웠다. 니콜라스와 케이트는 자기들 슬픔을 진심으로 억누르려 애쓰기 시작했다. 서로를 위해, 또 어머니를 위해 살아 보려고 말이다. 그 가엾은 부인은 이 칙칙하고 달라진 살림살이에 도무지 적응하지 못했다. 그러던 어느 날 저녁, 링킨워터 씨가 친절하게도 형제의 초대장을 들고 왔다. 다음다음 날 저녁 식사에 와 달라는 것이었다. 니클비 부인과 케이트, 니콜라스뿐 아니라 라 크리비 양까지 초대했는데, 특히 라 크리비 양은 따로 콕 집어 언급해 두었다.\n\n\"자, 얘들아.\" 니클비 부인이 말했다. 초대에 합당한 예를 갖추고 팀이 떠난 뒤였다. \"이게 무슨 뜻일까?\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻이라뇨, 어머니?\" 니콜라스가 웃으며 물었다.\n\n\"내 말은 말이다, 얘야.\" 부인이 도무지 속을 알 수 없다는 표정으로 대꾸했다. \"이 저녁 식사 초대가 무슨 뜻이냐는 거야. 그 의도랑 목적이 뭐겠니?\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 그날 그 집에서 우리가 먹고 마신다는 뜻이고, 그 의도와 목적은 우리를 즐겁게 해 주려는 거겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했다.\n\n\"그게 다라고 생각하니, 얘야?\"\n\n\"아직 그보다 더 깊은 데까진 못 미쳤는데요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"그럼 내가 딱 한 가지만 말해 주마.\" 니클비 부인이 말했다. \"넌 좀 놀라게 될 거다, 그게 전부야. 이게 저녁 식사 말고 다른 뭔가를 뜻하는 거라고, 내 장담하마.\"\n\n\"차하고 야식 정도겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 넌지시 던졌다.\n\n\"내가 너라면 그런 우스운 소리는 안 하겠다, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 거만하게 대꾸했다. \"그건 도무지 어울리지도 않고, 너한테 전혀 안 맞아. 내 말은 말이다, 치어리블 형제분이 이렇게 격식까지 차려서 우리를 저녁 식사에 부르는 게 그냥 아무것도 아닌 일은 아니라는 거야. 됐다, 두고 보면 알아. 너야 물론 내 말은 한마디도 안 믿겠지. 기다리는 게 훨씬 나아, 훨씬 더. 그래야 모두가 만족하고, 다툴 일도 없거든. 내가 하고 싶은 말은, 지금 내가 한 말을 기억해 두라는 거야. 그리고 내가 그랬다고 하거든, 내가 안 그랬다고 하진 말아라.\"\n\n이렇게 못을 박아 둔 니클비 부인은, 밤이고 낮이고 한 가지 환상에 시달리고 있었다. 숨이 턱에 찬 전령이 문 앞으로 달려와 니콜라스가 동업자로 들어가게 됐다고 알리는 환상이었다. 부인은 그 화제의 가지를 거기서 접고, 새로운 화제로 넘어갔다.\n\n\"참 별일이야.\" 부인이 말했다. \"정말이지 별일도 다 있지, 라 크리비 양을 초대하다니. 정말 놀랍다, 진짜로 놀라워. 물론 그 사람이 초대받은 건 아주 반가운 일이지, 아주 반가워. 그 사람이 처신을 아주 잘하리란 것도 의심할 여지가 없고. 늘 그러니까. 우리가 그 사람을 그런 사교 자리에 끌어들이는 다리가 됐다고 생각하면 참 흐뭇하다. 정말 기뻐, 정말 기쁘다니까. 그 사람이 정말 더없이 예의 바르고 사람 좋은 자그마한 양반인 건 분명하니까. 누군가 친구가 그 사람한테 한마디 해 줬으면 싶긴 해. 모자 장식을 어쩌면 그렇게 형편없이 달았는지, 또 그 리본 매듭은 또 얼마나 터무니없는지 말이야. 하지만 그야 물론 안 될 일이지. 그 사람이 제 꼴을 망가뜨리고 싶다면, 그야 분명 그럴 권리가 있으니까. 우리는 우리 자신을 못 본단다. 한 번도 못 봐, 못 봤고. 앞으로도 못 보겠지.\"\n\n이런 교훈적인 생각을 하다 보니, 부인은 이번 자리에서 유난히 맵시 있게 차려입어야겠다는 데까지 생각이 미쳤다. 그래야 라 크리비 양과 균형을 맞추고, 자기가 그것을 효과적으로 상쇄하고 메워 줄 수 있을 테니까. 이 생각은 부인을 딸과의 의논으로 이끌었다. 어떤 리본과 장갑, 장식을 할지에 관한 의논이었다. 이건 복잡한 문제인 데다가 더없이 중대한 일이었으므로, 곧 앞의 화제를 몰아내고 멀리 쫓아 버렸다.\n\n대망의 날이 다가오자, 이 착한 부인은 아침을 먹고 한 시간쯤 뒤부터 케이트의 손에 자기 몸을 맡겼다. 그리고 천천히 단계를 밟아 가며 옷을 차려입어, 딸이 자기 채비를 할 만큼 시간을 충분히 남기고서 단장을 마쳤다. 케이트의 단장은 아주 간소해서 그리 오래 걸리지도 않았지만, 그 결과는 어찌나 만족스러웠던지 케이트가 그날처럼 매력적이고 사랑스러워 보인 적은 없었다. 라 크리비 양도 도착했는데, 모자 상자 두 개를 들고 왔다. 마차에서 건네받다가 그만 두 상자 다 밑바닥이 빠져 버렸다. 게다가 신문지에 싼 무언가도 들고 왔는데, 내려오는 길에 어떤 신사가 깔고 앉는 바람에 쓸 만하게 만들려고 다시 다림질을 해야 했다. 마침내 다들 옷을 갖춰 입었다. 그들을 데리러 집에 온 니콜라스까지 말이다. 그리고 형제가 일부러 보내 준 마차를 타고 떠났다. 니클비 부인은 저녁으로 뭐가 나올지 무척 궁금해하며, 니콜라스가 아침에 알아낸 게 어디까지인지 캐물었다. 자라 비슷한 냄새라도 좀 맡지 않았는지, 안 맡았다면 무슨 냄새를 맡았는지 말이다. 그러면서 한 이십 년 전에 갔던 만찬들 이야기로 대화에 변화를 주었는데, 음식만이 아니라 손님들까지 일일이 짚어 가며 이야기했다. 하지만 듣는 이들은 그다지 푹 빠져들 만큼 흥미를 느끼지 못했다. 그 이름들을 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없었으니까.\n\n늙은 집사가 깊은 존경과 숱한 미소로 그들을 맞이하더니, 응접실로 안내했다. 거기서 형제가 어찌나 다정하고 친절하게 그들을 맞아 주었던지, 니클비 부인은 그만 가슴이 두근거려서, 라 크리비 양에게 생색을 낼 정신조차 거의 없을 지경이었다. 케이트는 그 환대에 한층 더 마음이 흔들렸다. 형제가 자기와 프랭크 사이에 있었던 일을 죄다 알고 있다는 걸 아는 터라, 자기 처지가 더없이 미묘하고 견디기 힘들게 느껴졌기 때문이다. 케이트는 니콜라스의 팔에 매달려 떨고 있었는데, 그때 찰스 씨가 그녀를 자기 팔로 부축해서 방의 다른 쪽으로 데려갔다.\n\n\"매덜린을 본 적 있니, 얘야.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 애가 너희 집을 떠난 뒤로 말이다.\"\n\n\"아니요, 어르신!\" 케이트가 대답했다. \"한 번도요.\"\n\n\"소식도 못 들었고, 응? 소식도 못 들었어?\"\n\n\"편지를 딱 한 통 받았어요.\" 케이트가 부드럽게 대꾸했다. \"그렇게 빨리 저를 잊을 거라곤 생각 못 했는데요.\"\n\n\"아.\" 노인이 그녀의 머리를 토닥이며 말했다. 마치 그녀가 자기가 제일 아끼는 자식이라도 되는 양 다정한 목소리였다. \"가엾은 것! 이걸 어떻게 생각하나, 네드 형제? 매덜린이 케이트한테 딱 한 번밖에 안 썼대, 딱 한 번, 네드. 그리고 케이트는 그 애가 그렇게 빨리 자길 잊을 거라곤 생각 못 했다는군, 네드.\"\n\n\"오! 슬프다, 슬퍼. 정말 슬프구나!\" 네드가 말했다.\n\n형제는 서로 눈짓을 주고받았다. 그리고 잠시 말없이 케이트를 바라보더니, 손을 맞잡고 고개를 끄덕였다. 마치 무언가 아주 기쁜 일을 두고 서로 축하라도 하는 듯했다.\n\n\"자, 자.\" 찰스 형제가 말했다. \"저 방으로 들어가 보렴, 얘야. 저쪽 문 말이다. 거기 그 애한테서 온 편지가 없는지 보아라. 탁자 위에 한 통 있을 거다. 만약 있거든 서둘러 돌아올 것 없다, 얘야. 우리가 아직 식사를 시작하는 것도 아니고, 시간은 넉넉하니까. 아주 넉넉해.\"\n\n케이트는 시키는 대로 물러갔다. 찰스 형제는 그 우아한 자태를 눈으로 좇다가, 니클비 부인 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했다.\n\n\"실은 진짜 저녁 식사 시간보다 한 시간 일찍 모이자고 무례를 좀 부렸습니다, 부인. 잠깐 의논드릴 일이 좀 있어서요. 그 짬을 메울 만한 일이지요. 네드, 이보게, 우리가 합의한 걸 자네가 말해 주겠나? 니클비 씨, 괜찮으시면 나를 따라오시지요.\"\n\n더 이상 설명도 없이, 니클비 부인과 라 크리비 양, 그리고 네드 형제만 함께 남겨졌다. 니콜라스는 찰스 형제를 따라 그의 개인 방으로 들어갔다. 거기서 그는 깜짝 놀라고 말았다. 외국에 있는 줄로만 알았던 프랭크와 마주쳤기 때문이다.\n\n\"젊은이들.\" 치어리블 씨가 말했다. \"악수하게!\"\n\n\"그러라는 분부가 필요 없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"저도 그렇습니다.\" 프랭크가 그 손을 진심으로 꼭 잡으며 대꾸했다.\n\n이렇게 흐뭇하게 바라보는 두 사람보다 더 잘생기고 멋진 젊은이 둘이 나란히 설 수는 없으리라고, 노신사는 생각했다. 그는 잠시 말없이 두 사람에게 눈길을 두었다가, 책상 앞에 자리를 잡으며 말했다.\n\n\"나는 자네들이 친구가 되기를 바라네. 가깝고 단단한 친구 말이야. 만약 그렇지 않을 거라 여겼다면, 지금 하려는 말을 망설였을 걸세. 프랭크, 여기 좀 보게! 니클비 씨, 자네는 반대쪽으로 와 주겠나?\"\n\n두 젊은이는 찰스 형제의 양옆으로 다가섰다. 찰스 형제가 책상에서 서류 한 장을 꺼내 펼쳤다.\n\n\"이것은.\" 그가 말했다. \"매덜린의 외할아버지 유언장 사본이라네. 그 애한테 만 이천 파운드를 물려준다는 내용이지. 성년이 되거나 결혼할 때 지급한다는 조건으로 말이야. 사연인즉슨 이렇다네. 이 노신사는 매덜린한테 화가 나 있었어. 매덜린은 그의 유일한 혈육이었는데, 거듭된 권유에도 그의 보호 아래로 들어와 제 아버지 곁을 떠나려 하지 않았거든. 그래서 그는 이 재산을, 그게 그가 가진 전부였는데, 어느 자선 단체에 남긴다는 유언장을 만들었어. 그런데 그 결정을 후회했던 모양이야. 삼 주 뒤, 같은 달에 이 유언장을 작성했으니까. 그런데 그가 세상을 떠나자마자 누군가 이걸 부정한 수단으로 빼돌렸어. 그래서 다른 것, 그러니까 유일하게 발견된 유언장이 검인을 받고 집행됐지. 이 문서가 우리 손에 들어온 뒤로 우호적인 교섭이 진행됐는데, 그게 이제 막 마무리됐다네. 이게 진본이라는 데 의심의 여지가 없고, 증인들도 (꽤 애를 먹은 끝에) 찾아냈으니, 돈은 되돌려졌네. 그래서 매덜린은 자기 권리를 되찾은 거야. 내가 말한 두 가지 조건 가운데 하나가 일어나면, 그 애는 이 재산의 주인이 되네. 아니, 이미 주인이지. 내 말 알아듣겠나?\"\n\n프랭크는 그렇다고 대답했다. 니콜라스는 목소리가 떨리는 게 들킬까 봐 차마 입을 열지 못하고, 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"자, 프랭크.\" 노신사가 말했다. \"이 증서를 되찾는 데 직접적인 계기가 된 건 자네였네. 재산이 그리 큰 건 아니야. 하지만 우리는 매덜린을 사랑하네. 그러니 그게 어떻든, 우리는 그 세 배의 돈을 가진 어떤 처녀와 자네가 맺어지는 것보다, 그 애와 맺어지는 걸 더 보고 싶다네. 자네가 그 애한테 청혼하겠나?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 어르신. 제가 그 증서를 되찾는 일에 마음을 쓴 건, 매덜린의 손이 이미 어떤 사람에게 약속됐다고 믿었기 때문입니다. 저나 그 어떤 남자가 내세울 수 있는 것보다 천 배는 더, 그녀의 감사를 받을 자격이 있는 사람, 그리고 제가 잘못 본 게 아니라면 그녀의 마음까지 차지한 사람에게 말입니다. 이 점에선 제가 성급하게 판단한 모양이군요.\"\n\n\"자네는 늘 그렇지, 이 사람아!\" 찰스 형제가 짐짓 꾸민 위엄도 까맣게 잊고 외쳤다. \"늘 그래. 어떻게 감히 그런 생각을 하나, 프랭크. 젊음과 아름다움과 온갖 사랑스러운 미덕과 훌륭함을 사랑으로 얻을 수 있는데, 우리가 자네더러 돈 보고 결혼하라고 시킬 줄 알았나? 어떻게 감히, 프랭크, 자네가 어쩌려는지 우리한테 먼저 말도 안 하고, 우리가 자네 대신 나설 기회도 안 주고서, 니클비 씨의 여동생한테 마음을 줬느냔 말이야?\"\n\n\"저는 감히 바랄 엄두도 못 냈—\"\n\n\"감히 바랄 엄두도 못 냈다고! 그렇다면 더더욱 우리 도움을 받았어야지! 니클비 씨, 프랭크가 성급하게 판단하긴 했어도, 이번 한 번은 제대로 봤다네. 매덜린의 마음은 이미 임자가 있어. 자네 손을 이리 주게. 그 임자는 바로 자네야. 떳떳하고도 당연한 일이지. 이 재산은 자네 것이 될 운명이지만, 자네는 돈보다 그 애한테서 더 큰 재산을 얻는 걸세. 그 돈을 마흔 배로 불려도 못 미칠 재산을 말이야. 그 애가 자네를 택했네, 니클비 씨. 우리, 그 애의 가장 가까운 벗들이 바라던 그대로 택한 거지. 프랭크도 우리가 바라던 그대로 택했고. 자네 여동생이 스무 번을 거절했어도 프랭크는 그 작은 손을 차지해야 마땅하네. 암, 그래야 하고, 그렇게 될 거야! 자네는 우리 마음을 모르고서도 고귀하게 처신했네. 하지만 이제 우리 마음을 알았으니, 시키는 대로 해야 하네. 아니! 자네들은 훌륭한 신사의 자식들이야! 한때는 말일세, 나의 사랑하는 동생 네드와 내가 가난하고 순박한 두 소년이던 시절이 있었네. 거의 맨발로 떠돌며 살길을 찾던 시절이지. 그때 이후로 우리가 나이와 형편 말고 달라진 게 있던가? 아니, 천만에! 오, 네드, 네드, 네드, 오늘이 자네와 나한테 얼마나 행복한 날인가! 우리 가엾은 어머니가 지금의 우릴 볼 때까지 살아 계셨더라면, 네드, 그 다정한 마음이 끝내 얼마나 뿌듯했겠나!\"\n\n이렇게 부름을 받은 네드 형제는, 니클비 부인과 함께 들어와 있던 터라 젊은이들 눈에는 미처 띄지 않았는데, 앞으로 뛰쳐나와 찰스 형제를 두 팔로 와락 끌어안았다.\n\n\"내 어린 케이트를 데려오게.\" 찰스 형제가 잠시 입을 다물었다가 말했다. \"데려오게, 네드. 케이트를 보게 해 줘. 입을 맞추게 해 다오. 이제 나도 그럴 권리가 있으니까. 처음 그 애가 왔을 때도 입맞춤할 뻔했어. 여러 번 그럴 뻔했지. 아! 편지는 찾았느냐, 내 새 같은 것아? 매덜린이 직접 너를 기다리고 있던 걸 봤느냐? 그 애가 제 벗이자 간병인이자 다정한 동무를 아주 잊지는 않았다는 걸 알았느냐? 이런, 이게 무엇보다 제일 기쁜 일이로구나!\"\n\n\"자, 자.\" 네드가 말했다. \"프랭크가 질투하겠어. 저녁 식사 전에 칼부림이라도 나겠는걸.\"\n\n\"그럼 프랭크더러 데려가라고 하게, 네드, 데려가라고 해. 매덜린은 옆방에 있네. 사랑하는 것들은 죄다 비키게 하고, 할 말이 있거든 저희끼리 이야기하게 두자고. 다 내보내게, 네드, 하나도 빠짐없이!\"\n\n찰스 형제가 정리를 시작했다. 얼굴이 발그레해진 케이트를 문 앞으로 데려가, 입을 맞추고 내보낸 것이다. 프랭크도 그리 꾸물대지 않고 뒤를 따랐다. 니콜라스는 제일 먼저 사라진 뒤였다. 그래서 남은 건 니클비 부인과 라 크리비 양뿐이었는데, 둘 다 진심으로 흐느끼고 있었다. 그리고 두 형제, 또 이제 막 들어와 모두와 악수를 나누는 팀 링킨워터가 있었다. 그의 둥근 얼굴은 온통 환하게 빛나며 미소가 가득했다.\n\n\"자, 팀 링킨워터.\" 늘 대변인 노릇을 하는 찰스 형제가 말했다. \"이제 젊은이들이 행복해졌네.\"\n\n\"그래도 말씀하신 만큼 그렇게 오래 애태우진 않으셨네요.\" 팀이 짓궂게 대꾸했다. \"아니, 니콜라스 씨랑 프랭크 씨를 얼마나 오래 방에 붙들어 둘 거라고 하셨어요. 게다가 진실을 털어놓기 전에 두 사람한테 별의별 얘기를 다 늘어놓을 거라고 하지 않으셨습니까.\"\n\n\"이런 악당을 또 본 적 있나, 네드?\" 노신사가 말했다. \"팀 링킨워터 같은 악당을 또 본 적 있어? 나더러 성미가 급하다고 몰아세우다니. 정작 아침, 점심, 저녁으로 우릴 들들 볶은 게 바로 이 사람인데. 우리 계획이 절반도 갖춰지기 전에, 무얼 하나 제대로 정하지도 못했는데, 가서 무슨 일이 기다리고 있는지 알려 주게 해 달라고 우릴 들볶지 않았나. 배신을 일삼는 개야!\"\n\n\"맞고말고, 찰스 형제.\" 네드가 대꾸했다. \"팀은 배신을 일삼는 개야. 도무지 믿을 수가 없어. 팀은 철없는 젊은 녀석이거든. 진중함도 차분함도 없어. 한바탕 객기를 다 부려 봐야지. 그러고 나면 언젠가는 점잖은 사회 구성원이 될지도 모르지.\"\n\n이건 노형제와 팀 사이의 단골 농담 가운데 하나였으므로, 셋 다 아주 신나게 웃었다. 훨씬 더 오래 웃을 수도 있었겠지만, 형제는 니클비 부인이 자기 감정을 표현하려고 안간힘을 쓰고 있고, 이 행복한 시간에 진심으로 벅차오른 걸 보았다. 그래서 부인을 두 사람 사이에 모시고, 더없이 중대한 어떤 준비에 대해 의논해야 한다는 핑계로 방에서 데리고 나갔다.\n\n그런데 팀과 라 크리비 양은 그동안 아주 자주 만난 사이였다. 둘은 늘 수다스럽고 즐겁게 어울렸고, 늘 막역한 친구였다. 그러니 팀이 그녀가 여태 흐느끼는 걸 보고 달래 주려 애쓴 건 세상에서 가장 자연스러운 일이었다. 라 크리비 양이 둘이 앉아도 넉넉한 커다란 구식 창가 자리에 앉아 있었으니, 팀이 그 옆에 앉은 것도 자연스러운 일이었다. 그리고 그날 팀이 평소와 달리 옷차림에 유난히 신경 쓰고 멋을 부린 건 어떻고. 그야 큰 잔치이자 대단한 행사였으니, 그게 무엇보다도 가장 자연스러운 일이었다.\n\n팀은 라 크리비 양 옆에 앉아, 한쪽 다리를 다른 쪽 다리 위에 꼬았다. 그래야 자기 발이—그는 발 모양이 아주 잘생긴 데다, 마침 그날따라 더없이 말끔한 구두에 검은 비단 양말을 신고 있었다—그녀의 눈에 잘 들어올 테니까. 그러고는 달래는 투로 말했다.\n\n\"울지 마세요!\"\n\n\"우는 수밖에요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"아니, 울지 마요.\" 팀이 말했다. \"제발 울지 말아요. 부디요.\"\n\n\"제가 너무 행복해서요!\" 자그마한 여인이 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"그럼 웃어요.\" 팀이 말했다. \"웃으셔야죠.\"\n\n팀이 도대체 자기 팔로 무얼 하고 있었는지는 짐작조차 할 수 없지만, 그가 팔꿈치로 창문을 쳤다. 그것도 라 크리비 양의 한참 반대편 쪽에 있는 부분을 말이다. 그쪽에 팔꿈치가 있을 일이 전혀 없었던 게 분명한데도.\n\n\"웃으세요.\" 팀이 말했다. \"안 그러면 제가 울 겁니다.\"\n\n\"왜 우시려고요?\" 라 크리비 양이 미소 지으며 물었다.\n\n\"저도 행복하니까요.\" 팀이 말했다. \"우리 둘 다 행복하니, 저도 당신처럼 하고 싶거든요.\"\n\n분명 그때의 팀처럼 안절부절못한 남자는 또 없었으리라. 그가 또다시 창문을 쳤으니까. 거의 같은 자리를. 라 크리비 양은 그러다 창문 깨뜨리겠다고 했다.\n\n\"저는 알았어요.\" 팀이 말했다. \"당신이 이 장면을 보고 기뻐하실 줄 말이죠.\"\n\n\"저를 기억해 주시다니 참 사려 깊고 친절하셨어요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대꾸했다. \"이만큼 절 기쁘게 한 일은 또 없을 거예요.\"\n\n도대체 라 크리비 양과 팀 링킨워터는 무엇 때문에 이 모든 말을 속삭이며 했을까? 비밀도 아니었는데. 그리고 팀 링킨워터는 왜 그렇게 라 크리비 양을 뚫어지게 바라봤으며, 라 크리비 양은 또 왜 그렇게 땅바닥을 뚫어지게 내려다봤을까?\n\n\"기쁜 일이지요.\" 팀이 말했다. \"우리처럼 평생을 세상에 혼자 지내 온 사람들한테는요. 우리가 아끼는 젊은이들이 맺어져서, 앞에 그 많은 행복한 세월을 두고 있는 걸 보는 게 말입니다.\"\n\n\"아!\" 자그마한 여인이 진심을 담아 외쳤다. \"정말 그래요!\"\n\n\"그래도 말이죠.\" 팀이 말을 이었다. \"그래도 이러고 나면 영 외롭고 버림받은 기분이 들지 않나요? 안 그래요?\"\n\n라 크리비 양은 모르겠다고 했다. 한데 왜 모르겠다고 했을까? 그런지 안 그런지는 분명히 알고 있었을 텐데.\n\n\"이러다 우리도 결국 결혼하게 되는 거 아닐까요, 안 그래요?\" 팀이 말했다.\n\n\"오, 말도 안 돼요!\" 라 크리비 양이 웃으며 대꾸했다. \"우린 너무 늙었는걸요.\"\n\n\"천만에요.\" 팀이 말했다. \"혼자 살기엔 너무 늙었죠. 긴 겨울 저녁 내내 각자 외로운 난롯가에 앉아 있느니, 우리 둘 다 결혼하면 안 될 게 뭡니까? 그걸 하나의 난롯가로 만들어서, 서로 결혼하면 안 될 게 뭐냐고요?\"\n\n\"오, 링킨워터 씨, 농담이시군요!\"\n\n\"아니, 아니에요. 정말 농담 아닙니다.\" 팀이 말했다. \"당신이 그러겠다면 저도 그러겠어요. 그래 줘요, 그대!\"\n\n\"사람들이 한참 웃을 거예요.\"\n\n\"웃으라죠.\" 팀이 씩씩하게 외쳤다. \"우린 성미가 좋으니까, 우리도 같이 웃으면 됩니다. 아니, 우리가 서로 알고 지낸 뒤로 얼마나 실컷 웃었습니까!\"\n\n\"그건 그래요.\" 라 크리비 양이 외쳤다. 팀이 보기엔 마음이 조금 누그러진 것 같았다.\n\n\"제 평생 가장 행복한 시간이었어요. 적어도 회계 사무실이랑 치어리블 형제분을 떠나서는요.\" 팀이 말했다. \"그래 줘요, 그대! 어서 그러겠다고 해 줘요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니에요. 그런 생각은 하면 안 돼요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대꾸했다. \"형제분이 뭐라고 하시겠어요?\"\n\n\"아니, 이런 맙소사!\" 팀이 천연덕스럽게 외쳤다. \"내가 그분들 모르게 그런 일을 생각이나 할 것 같아요? 아니, 그분들이 일부러 우릴 여기 남겨 두셨는데요.\"\n\n\"전 다시는 그분들 얼굴을 못 볼 거예요!\" 라 크리비 양이 기어드는 소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"자, 봐요.\" 팀이 말했다. \"우리 단란한 부부가 됩시다. 내가 사십사 년을 지낸 이 오래된 집에서 함께 살고, 그 세월 내내 일요일 아침마다 다닌 그 오래된 교회에 함께 가는 거예요. 내 오랜 벗들도 죄다 우리 곁에 있을 거고요. 딕도, 아치형 통로도, 펌프도, 화분들도, 그리고 프랭크 씨의 아이들이랑 니콜라스 씨의 아이들도요. 우리는 그 애들한테 할아버지 할머니 같은 존재가 될 겁니다. 우리 단란한 부부가 되어서, 서로 돌봐 줍시다! 그리고 혹시 우리가 귀가 멀거나, 다리를 절거나, 눈이 멀거나, 자리에 눕게 되더라도, 늘 곁에서 이야기하고 함께 앉아 있어 줄, 아끼는 사람이 있다는 게 얼마나 기쁘겠어요! 우리 단란한 부부가 됩시다. 자, 그래 줘요, 그대!\"\n\n이 솔직하고 거침없는 말이 끝나고 오 분 뒤, 자그마한 라 크리비 양과 팀은 마치 이십 년을 부부로 살면서 그동안 단 한 번도 다툰 적 없는 사이처럼 즐겁게 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 그리고 다시 오 분 뒤, 라 크리비 양이 눈이 빨갛지 않은지 살피고 머리를 매만지러 부산하게 나가자, 팀은 위풍당당한 걸음걸이로 응접실 쪽으로 향하며 가는 길에 이렇게 외쳤다. \"온 런던을 통틀어도 저런 여자는 또 없어! 없고말고, 내 알지!\"\n\n이쯤 되니 졸도할 지경의 집사는 듣도 보도 못한 저녁 식사 연기 탓에 거의 발작이라도 일으킬 판이었다. 니콜라스는, 독자들이 저마다 알아서 짐작할 만한 식으로 바쁜 시간을 보내다가, 그 성난 부름에 따라 서둘러 계단을 내려가던 참이었다. 그때 또 한 번 뜻밖의 일과 마주쳤다.\n\n내려가던 길에, 그는 복도 한 군데에서 검은 옷을 단정하게 차려입은 낯선 사람을 따라잡았다. 그 사람도 식당 쪽으로 가고 있었다. 다리를 좀 저는지 천천히 걷는 통에, 니콜라스는 뒤에 처져 한 걸음 한 걸음 그를 따라가며 도대체 누구일까 궁금해하고 있었다. 그런데 그 사람이 갑자기 홱 돌아서서 니콜라스의 두 손을 덥석 잡았다.\n\n\"뉴먼 노그스!\" 니콜라스가 기쁨에 차서 외쳤다.\n\n\"아! 뉴먼, 자네의 뉴먼, 변함없이 충실한 자네의 옛 뉴먼이라네! 사랑하는 녀석, 사랑하는 닉, 기뻐하게. 건강도, 행복도, 온갖 축복도 다 누리게! 못 견디겠어. 너무 벅차, 사랑하는 녀석아. 내가 다 어린애가 되는구나!\"\n\n\"어디 계셨어요?\" 니콜라스가 말했다. \"뭘 하고 계셨고요? 제가 얼마나 자주 아저씨를 찾았는데요. 그때마다 머지않아 소식을 듣게 될 거란 말만 들었다고요!\"\n\n\"알아, 알아!\" 뉴먼이 대꾸했다. \"다들 행복이 한꺼번에 오길 바랐거든. 나도 그분들을 거들고 있었어. 나—나—날 좀 보게, 닉, 나를 좀 보라고!\"\n\n\"저한텐 한 번도 그렇게 차려입게 해 주신 적 없잖아요.\" 니콜라스가 부드럽게 나무라는 투로 말했다.\n\n\"그땐 내 꼴이 어떻든 상관없었어. 신사 옷을 걸칠 엄두가 안 났거든. 그런 옷은 옛날을 떠오르게 해서 날 비참하게 만들었을 테니까. 난 이제 딴사람이야, 닉. 사랑하는 녀석아, 말이 안 나오는구나. 나한테 아무 말도 하지 마. 이 눈물 때문에 날 나쁘게 보지도 말고. 자넨 내가 오늘 어떤 기분인지 모를 거야. 알 수도 없고, 앞으로도 영영 모르겠지!\"\n\n두 사람은 팔짱을 끼고 저녁 식사 자리로 걸어 들어가, 나란히 앉았다.\n\n세상이 시작된 이래 그런 만찬은 또 없었다. 팀 링킨워터의 친구인 퇴직한 은행 사무원이 있었고, 팀 링킨워터의 누이인 통통한 노부인도 있었다. 팀 링킨워터의 누이가 라 크리비 양에게 어찌나 살뜰히 마음을 쓰는지, 또 퇴직한 은행 사무원이 어찌나 농담을 많이 던지는지, 게다가 팀 링킨워터 본인은 또 어찌나 기분이 한껏 좋았는지, 자그마한 라 크리비 양은 어찌나 우스꽝스러운 모양새였는지, 이들만으로도 상상할 수 있는 가장 즐거운 모임이 이뤄졌을 정도였다. 게다가 니클비 부인은 또 얼마나 위엄 있고 흐뭇해했는지. 매덜린과 케이트는 또 얼마나 발그레하니 아름다웠는지. 니콜라스와 프랭크는 또 얼마나 헌신적이고 자랑스러워했는지. 그리고 그 넷은 또 얼마나 조용히, 또 떨리는 마음으로 행복해했는지. 뉴먼은 또 얼마나 차분하면서도 더없이 기뻐했는지. 그리고 쌍둥이 형제는 또 얼마나 기뻐하며 그런 눈빛을 주고받았는지, 늙은 하인은 주인의 의자 뒤에 못 박힌 듯 서서, 식탁을 빙 둘러보는 동안 두 눈이 흐릿해지는 걸 느낄 정도였다.\n\n만남의 첫 신기함이 가시고, 다들 자기들이 얼마나 행복한지 진정으로 느끼기 시작하자, 대화는 한층 두루두루 오갔고, 화목함과 즐거움도 가능하다면 더욱 커졌다. 형제는 더없이 황홀해했다. 그들이 부인들을 물러가게 하기 전에 한 사람씩 다 입맞춤하겠다고 고집을 부리는 바람에, 퇴직한 은행 사무원은 재치 있는 말을 어찌나 많이 쏟아냈는지, 자기 자신을 완전히 능가해서 익살의 천재로 여겨질 지경이었다.\n\n\"케이트, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 위층으로 올라가자마자 딸을 한쪽으로 데려가며 말했다. \"라 크리비 양이랑 링킨워터 씨 일이 정말로 사실이라고, 진짜로 그렇게 말하려는 건 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"정말 사실이에요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"아니, 내 평생 그런 얘긴 들어 본 적이 없다!\" 니클비 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"링킨워터 씨는 더없이 훌륭한 분이에요.\" 케이트가 조곤조곤 말했다. \"게다가 나이에 비하면 아직 아주 젊으신걸요.\"\n\n\"그분 나이에 비하면, 얘야!\" 니클비 부인이 대꾸했다. \"그래, 그분을 두고 흉보는 사람은 없지. 내가 본 사람 중에 가장 줏대 없고 가장 어리석은 사람이란 것만 빼면 말이다. 내가 말하는 건 그 여자 나이야. 그분이 가서 청혼을 했다는 게, 그것도 나보다—아, 나이가 절반은 더 많을 게 분명한 여자한테 말이야. 게다가 그 여자가 감히 그걸 받아들였다는 게! 됐다, 케이트. 난 그 여자한테 정나미가 떨어졌어!\"\n\n니클비 부인은 고개를 정말이지 단호하게 가로젓고는 휑하니 나가 버렸다. 그리고 그날 저녁 내내, 뒤이은 흥겨움과 즐거움 속에서, 그 한 가지만 빼고는 부인도 거리낌 없이 어울리면서도, 라 크리비 양에게만은 위엄 있고 쌀쌀맞게 굴었다. 그 여자의 처신이 얼마나 부적절한지를 똑똑히 알린다는 뜻이었고, 그 여자가 그토록 뻔뻔하게 저지른 비행에 대한 자신의 극심하고 매서운 못마땅함을 드러내려는 것이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 64\n\n니콜라스는 어려운 시절, 덜 운 좋던 날들을 함께한 친구들과 나누지 않으면 기쁨이 온전치 못한 사람이었어. 사랑과 희망의 온갖 매력에 둘러싸여 있으면서도, 그의 따뜻한 마음은 수수한 존 브로디를 향해 가닿았지. 그는 처음 만났던 일을 미소로 떠올렸고, 두 번째 만남은 눈물로 떠올렸어. 가엾은 스마이크가 어깨에 보따리를 메고 참을성 있게 곁에서 터덜터덜 걷던 모습이 다시 보였지. 그리고 런던으로 향하는 길에서 그들과 헤어지며 건넨, 그 정직한 요크셔 사내의 거친 격려의 말이 다시 들리는 것 같았어.\n\n매들린과 그는 여러 번 함께 앉아 편지를 쓰려고 했어. 존에게 바뀐 자기 형편을 낱낱이 알리고, 우정과 고마움을 전하는 편지였지. 그런데 어쩐 일인지 그 편지는 끝내 써지지가 않았어. 세상에서 가장 좋은 마음으로 매달려 보았지만, 매번 다른 이야기로 빠져 버리곤 했거든. 그리고 니콜라스가 혼자 써 보려고 해도, 하고 싶은 말의 절반도 적을 수가 없었어. 사실 무엇을 써 놓아도 다시 읽어 보면, 마음속에 품은 것에 비해 차갑고 못마땅하게만 보였지. 마침내 날마다 이렇게 끌면서 자꾸만 자신을 탓하던 끝에, 그는 마음을 정했어. 매들린이 강하게 권하기도 해서 더 선뜻 그러기로 했지. 요크셔로 후딱 다녀오기로, 그리고 아무 기별도 없이 브로디 부부 앞에 불쑥 나타나기로 한 거야.\n\n그래서 어느 날 저녁 일곱 시에서 여덟 시 사이, 그와 케이트는 사라센스 헤드 매표소에 가 있었어. 다음 날 아침 마차로 그레타 다리까지 가는 자리를 잡으려고 말이야. 두 사람은 여행에 필요한 자질구레한 것들을 사러 서쪽으로 가야 했어. 그리고 날이 좋아서, 갈 때는 걷고 올 때는 마차를 타기로 했지.\n\n방금 다녀온 곳이 워낙 많은 추억을 불러일으켰어. 케이트는 매들린 얘기를 잔뜩 늘어놓았고, 니콜라스는 프랭크 얘기를 잔뜩 늘어놓았지. 서로 상대방의 말에 푹 빠져 있었고, 둘 다 너무 행복하고 마음이 통했고, 할 얘기가 너무 많았어. 그러다 보니 두 사람은 세븐 다이얼스와 소호 사이에 얽힌 그 골목 미로 속을 꼬박 삼십 분이나 헤매 들어가고서도 큰길 하나 나오질 않았어. 그제야 니콜라스는 길을 잃었을지도 모른다는 생각이 들기 시작했지.\n\n그 가능성은 곧 확실한 사실이 되어 버렸어. 주위를 둘러보고, 길 이쪽 끝까지 갔다가 저쪽 끝까지 가 봐도, 알아볼 만한 표지 하나 찾을 수가 없었거든. 그래서 그는 길을 물어볼 만한 데를 찾아 다시 발길을 돌릴 수밖에 없었지.\n\n거기는 뒷골목이었어. 주위에도 사람이 없었고, 지나치는 몇 안 되는 초라한 가게에도 사람이 없었지. 지하실에서 인도 위로 희미한 빛 한 줄기가 새어 나오고 있었어. 니콜라스는 그쪽으로 다가갔지. 아래에 있는 사람들 눈에 자기 모습이 보이도록 두세 계단을 내려가 길을 물으려는 참이었어. 그런데 여자 목소리로 큰 소리로 야단치는 소리가 들려 그는 멈칫했지.\n\n\"아, 그냥 가요!\" 케이트가 말했어. \"싸우고 있잖아요. 오빠가 다칠지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"잠깐만, 케이트. 무슨 일인지 한번 들어 보자.\" 오빠가 대꾸했어. \"쉿!\"\n\n\"이 더럽고 게으르고 못돼 처먹은 쓸모없는 짐승아.\" 여자가 발을 쿵쿵 구르며 소리쳤어. \"왜 빨래 짜는 기계를 안 돌리는 거야?\"\n\n\"돌리고 있잖아, 내 목숨, 내 영혼아!\" 남자 목소리가 대답했어. \"나는 늘 돌리고 있어. 빌어먹을 방앗간에서 빌어먹을 늙은 말처럼 끝도 없이 돌리고 있다고. 내 인생은 그저 빌어먹게 끔찍한 노동이야!\"\n\n\"그럼 군대나 가서 입대해 버리지 그래?\" 여자가 쏘아붙였어. \"얼마든지 가도 돼.\"\n\n\"군대라니!\" 남자가 소리쳤어. \"군대라고! 그대의 기쁨이자 즐거움인 이 사람이, 꽁지 달린 거친 빨간 외투를 입은 꼴을 보고 싶어? 북 치는 놈들한테 빌어먹게 얻어맞고 두들겨 맞는 소리를 듣고 싶어? 진짜 총을 쏘게 하고, 머리를 깎이고, 콧수염을 밀리고, 두 눈을 좌우로 굴리게 하고, 바지에 흰 가루칠이나 하게 하고 싶다는 거야?\"\n\n\"니콜라스 오빠.\" 케이트가 속삭였어. \"저 사람이 누군지 모르죠. 만탈리니 씨가 분명해요.\"\n\n\"확인해 봐! 내가 길을 묻는 동안 슬쩍 들여다보렴.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"한두 계단만 내려와. 자!\"\n\n니콜라스는 케이트를 끌어당기며 계단을 살금살금 내려가, 판자로 둘러친 작은 지하실 안을 들여다봤어. 거기, 빨래 바구니와 옷가지 틈에서, 셔츠 소매까지 걷어붙이고 있는 사내가 있었지. 그래도 여전히 더없이 잘 만들어진 낡고 기운 바지를 입고 있었고, 한때 화려했던 조끼를 걸치고 있었어. 콧수염과 구레나룻도 예전 그대로였지만, 윤기 흐르던 염색만은 빠져 있었지. 거기, 풍만한 여자의 화를 달래려고 애쓰는 사내가 있었어. 그 여자는 정식 부인인 만탈리니 부인이 아니라, 그 가게의 주인이었지. 그러면서 그는 죽을힘을 다하듯 빨래 짜는 기계를 돌리고 있었어. 기계의 삐걱대는 소리가 여자의 새된 목소리와 뒤섞여, 그의 귀를 거의 멀게 할 지경이었지. 거기, 우아하고 멋스럽고 매혹적이며 한때 그토록 멋들어졌던 만탈리니가 있었어.\n\n\"이 거짓말쟁이 배신자!\" 여자가 만탈리니 씨의 얼굴에 손찌검을 하려고 들면서 소리쳤어.\n\n\"거짓말쟁이라니! 아, 제기랄! 자, 내 영혼아, 내 다정하고 사람 홀리고 마음 빼앗고 빌어먹게도 나를 사로잡는 귀여운 병아리야, 진정해.\" 만탈리니 씨가 굽실거리며 말했어.\n\n\"싫어!\" 여자가 비명을 질렀어. \"네 눈깔을 후벼 파 버릴 거야!\"\n\n\"아! 이 무슨 빌어먹게 사나운 양 같으니!\" 만탈리니 씨가 소리쳤어.\n\n\"당신은 도무지 믿을 수가 없어.\" 여자가 비명을 질렀어. \"어제는 하루 종일 나가 있었지. 어디서 시시덕거리고 다닌 거 다 알아. 당신도 알잖아! 내가 당신 빼내려고 이 파운드 십사 실링이나 내고, 감옥에서 꺼내서, 여기서 신사처럼 살게 해 줬는데, 그걸로 모자라서 이렇게 굴어야겠어? 게다가 내 가슴까지 찢어 놓으면서?\"\n\n\"절대로 그 마음을 찢어 놓지 않을게. 착한 아이가 될게. 다시는 안 그럴게. 다시는 못된 짓 안 할게. 그 작은 마음에 용서를 빌게.\" 만탈리니 씨가 빨래 기계 손잡이를 놓고 두 손바닥을 모으며 말했어. \"이 잘생긴 친구는 이제 끝장이 났어! 빌어먹을 개죽음이나 당하게 됐다고. 불쌍히 여겨 주겠지? 할퀴고 쥐어뜯지 말고, 쓰다듬고 달래 주겠지? 아, 제기랄!\"\n\n여자는 이 애틋한 호소에 거의 마음이 움직이지 않은 듯했어. 그 몸짓으로 봐서는 말이야. 막 화난 대답을 쏘아붙이려는 참이었지. 그때 니콜라스가 목소리를 높여 피카딜리로 가는 길을 물었어.\n\n만탈리니 씨가 몸을 돌렸어. 케이트를 보더니, 더는 한마디도 없이 문 뒤에 놓인 침대로 단번에 풀쩍 뛰어들었지. 그러고는 이불을 끌어다 얼굴을 덮고, 그러는 내내 발버둥을 쳐 댔어.\n\n\"제기랄.\" 그가 숨 막히는 목소리로 외쳤어. \"꼬마 니클비잖아! 문 닫아, 촛불 꺼, 나를 침대 속에 처박아 줘! 아, 제기, 제기, 제기랄!\"\n\n여자는 먼저 니콜라스를 보고, 그다음 만탈리니 씨를 봤어. 이 해괴한 짓을 누구한테 따져야 할지 갈피를 못 잡는 모양이었지. 그런데 만탈리니 씨가 운 나쁘게도 손님들이 갔는지 확인하고 싶은 마음에 이불 밑에서 코를 쑥 내밀었어. 그러자 여자는 느닷없이, 오랜 연습으로만 익혔을 법한 솜씨로, 제법 묵직한 빨래 바구니를 그에게 집어 던졌지. 어찌나 정확히 맞혔던지 그는 아까보다 더 사납게 발버둥을 쳤어. 하지만 완전히 파묻혀 버린 머리를 빼낼 엄두는 내지 못했지. 니콜라스는 여자의 분노가 쏟아져 내리기 전에 떠날 좋은 기회라고 생각했어. 그래서 케이트를 서둘러 데리고 나왔지. 이 뜻밖의 재회로 곤경에 빠진 가엾은 사내가 알아서 제 처신을 둘러대도록 내버려 두고 말이야.\n\n다음 날 아침 그는 길을 떠났어. 이제 날은 춥고 겨울 날씨였지. 그 길을 처음 갔을 때 어떤 처지였는지, 그 뒤로 얼마나 많은 우여곡절과 변화를 겪었는지가 새삼 또렷이 떠올랐어. 그는 길의 대부분을 마차 안에 혼자 있었지. 가끔 깜빡 졸다가 정신을 차리고 창밖을 내다보면, 잘 기억하는 곳이 눈에 들어왔어. 내려갈 때 지났거나, 가엾은 스마이크와 함께 오래 걸어 돌아올 때 지났던 곳이었지. 그럴 때면 그 뒤로 일어난 모든 일이 한낱 꿈만 같았어. 자기들은 여전히 세상을 앞에 두고 런던을 향해 지쳐 터벅터벅 걷고 있는 것만 같았지.\n\n이런 추억을 더 생생하게 만들려는 듯, 밤이 깃들자 눈이 내리기 시작했어. 스탬퍼드와 그랜섬을 지나고, 그로그즈위그의 용감한 남작 이야기를 들었던 작은 선술집을 지나칠 때, 모든 것이 어제 본 듯이 그대로였지. 지붕에 쌓인 흰 눈 껍질은 한 조각도 녹지 않은 것 같았어. 떼지어 몰려드는 생각들을 그대로 두자, 그는 거의 이렇게 믿을 뻔했어. 자기가 다시 마차 바깥에 스퀴어스며 아이들과 함께 앉아 있다고, 허공에 그들의 목소리가 들린다고 말이야. 그리고 그 옛날의 가라앉던 마음과 집을 향한 그리움이 다시 느껴졌어. 다만 지금은 아픔과 즐거움이 뒤섞인 느낌이었지. 이런 공상에 빠져들던 중에 그는 잠이 들었고, 매들린의 꿈을 꾸며 그것들을 잊어버렸어.\n\n그는 도착한 날 밤을 그레타 다리의 여관에서 묵었어. 다음 날 아침 아주 이른 시각에 일어나 장이 서는 마을까지 걸어가, 존 브로디의 집을 물었지. 존은 이제 가정을 꾸린 몸이라 마을 외곽에 살고 있었어. 다들 그를 알았기 때문에, 니콜라스는 그 집까지 안내해 주겠다는 소년 하나를 어렵잖게 찾았지.\n\n대문 앞에서 안내해 준 소년을 보내고, 니콜라스는 조급한 마음에 아담한 집이나 정원의 풍성한 모습을 감상할 새도 없이 부엌문 쪽으로 다가갔어. 그러고는 지팡이로 힘껏 문을 두드렸지.\n\n\"이봐!\" 안에서 목소리가 외쳤어. \"대체 무슨 일이여? 마을에 불이라도 난 겨? 어이쿠, 무슨 소리를 그리 시끄럽게 내고 그려!\"\n\n이렇게 외치며 존 브로디가 직접 문을 열었어. 그러고는 두 눈도 한껏 크게 뜨더니, 두 손을 탁 마주치며 호탕하게 웃음을 터뜨리며 소리쳤지.\n\n\"이런, 대부님이여, 대부님이여! 틸리, 여기 니클비 씨가 오셨어. 손 좀 줘 봐, 이 사람아. 어여 와, 어여 와. 들어와, 불가에 앉아. 이거 한 모금 마셔. 다 마실 때까정 한마디도 하지 말어! 쭉 들이켜, 이 사람아. 어이구! 자네를 보니께 참말로 반갑네그려.\"\n\n존은 말에 맞게 곧장 행동에 옮겼어. 니콜라스를 부엌으로 끌고 들어가, 활활 타오르는 불가의 커다란 긴 의자에 억지로 앉혔지. 그러고는 엄청나게 큰 병에서 술을 사분의 일 파인트쯤 따라 그의 손에 쥐여 줬어. 당장 마시라는 신호로 입을 떡 벌리고 고개를 뒤로 젖혀 보였지. 그러고는 그 큼지막한 붉은 얼굴에 환영의 미소를 활짝 띤 채, 유쾌한 거인처럼 서 있었어.\n\n\"나는 알아봤어야 혔는디.\" 존이 말했어. \"그렇게 두드리고 들어올 사람은 자네밖에 없다는 걸 말여. 그게 자네가 교장 선생 문 두드리던 식 아녀, 응? 하하하! 그나저나 말여, 교장 선생 얘기는 다 어찌 된 겨?\"\n\n\"그럼 그 일을 아시나요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어.\n\n\"어젯밤에 마을에서 다들 그 얘기를 허고 있더라고.\" 존이 대답했어. \"그런디 아무도 영 제대로 알아듣질 못하는 것 같더라고, 어째.\"\n\n\"여러 번 미뤄지고 끌었지만요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"훔친 유언장을 불법으로 가지고 있던 죄로 칠 년 유배형을 선고받았어요. 그리고 그 뒤엔 음모를 꾸민 죗값까지 치러야 하고요.\"\n\n\"휘유!\" 존이 외쳤어. \"음모라고! 화약 음모 같은 거여? 응? 가이 포크스 부류의 그런 거?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 아뇨, 아뇨, 그 학교와 얽힌 음모예요. 이따 설명해 드릴게요.\"\n\n\"그게 좋겄어!\" 존이 말했어. \"아침 먹고 설명혀, 지금 말고. 자네도 배고프고 나도 배고프니께. 그리고 틸리가 모든 설명에 끼어 있어야 혀. 그게 부부간의 믿음이라고 그러거든. 하하하! 어이구, 그 부부간의 믿음이란 게 참 별난 거여!\"\n\n브로디 부인이 깜찍한 모자를 쓰고 들어왔어. 부엌에서 아침을 먹다 들킨 걸 두고 거듭거듭 변명을 늘어놓으면서 말이야. 그 바람에 존은 이 진지한 화제에 대한 이야기를 멈추고 아침상을 서둘렀지. 아침상은 산더미 같은 토스트, 갓 낳은 달걀, 삶은 햄, 요크셔 파이, 그 밖에 차가운 든든한 음식들로 차려졌어. 아주 통통한 하녀의 손에서 그런 음식들이 다른 부엌으로부터 묵직하게 끊임없이 추가로 날라져 왔지. 춥고 황량한 아침에 더없이 어울리는 상이었고, 모두에게 톡톡히 대접받았어. 마침내 식사가 끝났지. 그동안 제일 좋은 응접실에 피워 둔 불이 활활 타올라서, 그들은 그쪽으로 자리를 옮겼어. 니콜라스가 들려줄 이야기를 들으려고 말이야.\n\n니콜라스는 모든 것을 다 이야기했어. 두 명의 열성적인 청중의 가슴에 그토록 많은 감정을 불러일으킨 이야기는 일찍이 없었지. 어떤 대목에서는 정직한 존이 공감하며 끙끙 앓는 소리를 냈고, 또 어떤 대목에서는 기뻐서 큰 소리로 웃었어. 어떤 때는 일부러 런던에 올라가 치어리블 형제를 한번 보겠노라 다짐했고, 또 어떤 때는 팀 링킨워터에게 마차로 운임까지 다 내고 햄을 보내 주겠다고 맹세했지. 인간의 칼로는 한 번도 썰어 본 적 없을 만큼 큰 햄을 말이야. 니콜라스가 매들린 이야기를 시작하자, 존은 입을 떡 벌린 채 앉아서 이따금 브로디 부인을 쿡쿡 찌르며 나직이 외쳤어. 그 아가씨가 \"꽤 괜찮은 처자\"임에 틀림없다고 말이야. 그리고 마침내 그 젊은 친구가 자기 행운을 알리고, 글로는 충분히 따뜻하게 전할 수 없던 그 모든 우정의 다짐을 전하려고 일부러 내려왔다는 말을 들었을 때, 이번 길의 유일한 목적이 자기 행복을 그들과 나누고, 결혼하면 꼭 올라와서 봐 달라고, 매들린도 자기만큼이나 그러길 바란다고 전하려는 것임을 들었을 때, 존은 더 이상 참지 못했어. 아내를 못마땅한 듯 노려보고는 대체 뭘 훌쩍거리느냐고 따져 묻더니, 외투 소매를 두 눈에 갖다 대고 엉엉 울어 버렸지.\n\n\"근디 말여.\" 양쪽에서 많은 이야기가 오간 뒤, 존이 진지하게 말했어. \"교장 선생 얘기로 돌아가서. 만약에 그놈 소식이 오늘 학교에 닿았으면, 그 늙은 마누라는 성한 뼈 하나 안 남을 거여. 패니도 마찬가지고.\"\n\n\"아, 존!\" 브로디 부인이 외쳤어.\n\n\"아, 아, 존이라고 또!\" 요크셔 사내가 대꾸했어. \"나는 그 녀석들이 무슨 짓을 할지 모른다 그 말여. 교장 선생이 곤경에 빠졌단 소문이 처음 돌았을 때, 어떤 부모들은 사람을 보내 제 자식들을 데려갔어. 남은 녀석들이 그놈한테 무슨 일이 닥쳤는지 알면, 그런 난리와 반란이 일어날 거여! 어이구! 내 생각엔 죄다 미쳐 가지고, 물처럼 피를 쏟아 낼 것 같다니께!\"\n\n사실 존 브로디는 어찌나 걱정이 컸던지, 지체 없이 학교로 말을 달려가기로 마음먹었어. 그러고는 니콜라스에게 같이 가자고 했지. 그런데 니콜라스는 거절했어. 자기가 나타나면 어쩌면 그들의 불행에 쓰라림만 더할지도 모른다는 핑계를 댔지.\n\n\"그건 맞는 말이여!\" 존이 말했어. \"나는 그 생각을 미처 못 혔네.\"\n\n\"저는 내일 돌아가야 해요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"하지만 오늘은 두 분과 함께 저녁을 먹으려고요. 그리고 브로디 부인께서 잠자리를 내주실 수 있다면—\"\n\n\"잠자리!\" 존이 외쳤어. \"자네가 침대 네 개에서 한꺼번에 잘 수 있으면 좋겄구먼. 어이구, 그럼 다 내주지. 내가 돌아올 때까정 있어. 돌아올 때까정만 있어. 그럼 어이구, 우리 하루 진하게 보내 보자고.\"\n\n존은 아내에게 진하게 입을 맞추고, 니콜라스에게는 그에 못지않게 진하게 손을 흔들어 잡았어. 그러고는 말에 올라타 달려갔지. 브로디 부인은 손님맞이 준비에 매달리도록 남겨 두고, 그 젊은 친구는 동네를 거닐며 수많은 비참한 기억으로 익숙해진 곳들을 다시 둘러보도록 두고 말이야.\n\n존은 가볍게 말을 달려 갔어. 두더보이즈 홀에 다다르자 말을 대문에 매어 두고 교실 문 쪽으로 갔지. 그런데 문은 안쪽에서 잠겨 있었어. 안에서 엄청난 소음과 소란이 일고 있었지. 벽에 난 적당한 틈에 눈을 갖다 대니, 그게 무슨 영문인지 알아채는 데는 오래 걸리지 않았어.\n\n스퀴어스 씨가 몰락했다는 소식이 두더보이즈에 닿은 거야. 그건 아주 분명했어. 보아하니 그 소식은 도련님들에게 아주 막 알려진 모양이었지. 반란이 방금 터졌으니 말이야.\n\n그날은 유황과 당밀을 먹이는 아침 가운데 하루였어. 스퀴어스 부인이 늘 하던 대로 큰 그릇과 숟가락을 들고 교실에 들어왔지. 그 뒤로 스퀴어스 양과 사랑스러운 워크퍼드가 따라왔어. 워크퍼드는 아버지가 없는 동안 자질구레한 집행 업무를 떠맡고 있었지. 못이 박힌 장화로 학생들을 걷어차고, 작은 아이들 머리채를 잡아당기고, 다른 아이들은 약 오르는 데를 꼬집고, 그 밖에 여러 비슷한 방식으로 제 어머니에게 큰 위안과 행복이 되는 일들 말이야. 이들이 들어선 것이, 미리 짠 것인지 동시에 솟구친 충동인지는 몰라도, 반란의 신호가 되었어. 한 무리가 문으로 달려가 문을 잠그고, 다른 무리가 책상과 걸상 위로 올라가는 동안, 가장 덩치 큰—따라서 가장 새로 온—아이가 회초리를 움켜쥐었어. 그러고는 험상궂은 얼굴로 스퀴어스 부인 앞에 맞서서, 그녀의 모자와 비버 가죽 보닛을 낚아채 제 머리에 썼지. 나무 숟가락으로 무장하고는, 죽기 싫으면 당장 무릎을 꿇고 약을 한 입 받아 먹으라고 명령했어. 그 갸륵한 부인이 정신을 차리거나 조금이라도 맞받아칠 새도 없이, 소리 지르는 괴롭힘꾼들 무리가 그녀를 무릎 꿇는 자세로 떠밀었지. 그러고는 그 역겨운 혼합물을 한 숟가락 삼키게 했어. 게다가 그 약은 워크퍼드 도련님의 머리가 그릇에 처박힌 덕에 평소보다 더 구수해진 것이었지. 워크퍼드를 그릇에 처박는 일은 또 다른 반란자에게 맡겨졌어. 이 첫 성공에 고무되어, 갖가지 핼쑥하고 굶주린 추한 얼굴들이 한데 모인 그 악의에 찬 무리는 더 험한 짓들로 나아갔어. 우두머리는 스퀴어스 부인에게 약을 한 번 더 먹으라고 우기고 있었고, 스퀴어스 도련님은 또 한 번 당밀에 처박히고 있었지. 그리고 스퀴어스 양에게도 거센 공격이 시작된 참이었어. 그때 존 브로디가 힘찬 발길질로 문을 박차고 구하러 달려들었지. 외침과 비명과 신음과 야유와 손뼉 소리가 갑자기 뚝 그치고, 죽은 듯한 정적이 흘렀어.\n\n\"너희 잘난 녀석들이로구나.\" 존이 차분히 둘러보며 말했어. \"여기서 뭣 하는 짓이여, 이 어린 개구쟁이들아?\"\n\n\"스퀴어스가 감옥에 갇혔어요, 우리는 도망칠 거예요!\" 스무 개쯤 되는 새된 목소리가 외쳤어. \"우리는 안 멈춰요, 안 멈춰요!\"\n\n\"그래, 그럼 멈추지 말어.\" 존이 대꾸했어. \"누가 너희한테 멈추랬어? 사내답게 도망쳐. 하지만 여자들은 다치게 하지 말어.\"\n\n\"만세!\" 새된 목소리들이 더더욱 새되게 외쳤어.\n\n\"만세?\" 존이 따라 말했어. \"그래, 만세도 사내답게 혀. 자 그럼, 잘 봐. 힙, 힙, 힙, 만세!\"\n\n\"만세!\" 목소리들이 외쳤어.\n\n\"만세! 다시!\" 존이 말했어. \"더 크게.\"\n\n아이들이 따랐어.\n\n\"한 번 더!\" 존이 말했어. \"겁먹지 말고. 제대로 한번 혀 보자고!\"\n\n\"만세!\"\n\n\"자 그럼.\" 존이 말했어. \"마지막으로 한 번만 더 하고, 그담엔 너희 좋을 대로 후딱 튀어. 자, 숨을 크게 들이쉬어. 스퀴어스는 감옥에 갇혔다. 학교는 끝장났다. 죄다 끝이여. 다 지나가고 사라졌다. 그걸 생각하면서, 진하게 한번 혀 보자고! 만세!\"\n\n두더보이즈 홀의 벽이 일찍이 한 번도 울려 본 적 없는, 그리고 다시는 응답할 일 없을 그런 환호가 터져 나왔어. 그 소리가 잦아들었을 때, 교실은 텅 비어 있었지. 오 분 전만 해도 그곳을 가득 메우던 분주하고 시끄러운 무리 가운데, 하나도 남지 않았어.\n\n\"잘하셨네요, 브로디 씨!\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어. 방금 전 소동으로 얼굴이 달아오르고 벌게져 있었지만, 끝까지 표독스러웠지. \"당신이 와서 우리 애들을 부추겨 도망치게 만들었어요. 그 대가를 톡톡히 치르게 해 드릴 테니 두고 보세요, 선생! 우리 아빠가 불운하게 원수들한테 짓밟혔다고 해서, 당신이랑 틸다한테 비열하게 비웃음당하고 짓눌릴 우리가 아니라고요.\"\n\n\"아니지!\" 존이 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸했어. \"너흰 그런 꼴 안 당해. 내 맹세허지. 우리를 좀 좋게 봐, 패니. 둘 다한테 말허는디, 나는 그 늙은이가 마침내 덜미 잡힌 게 기뻐. 더럽게 기뻐. 하지만 너흰 내 비웃음 없이도 충분히 고생할 거여. 나는 비웃을 사람이 아녀. 틸리도 그럴 처자가 아니고. 그러니께 똑똑히 말해 두는 거여. 그뿐 아녀, 지금 말허는디, 만약에 이곳을 떠날 때 도와줄 친구가 필요허면—코 쳐들지 말어, 패니, 그래도 돼—옛정을 떠올리며 손 내밀 준비가 된 틸리와 내가 있다는 걸 알게 될 거여. 그리고 이 말 헌다고 내가 한 짓을 부끄러워한다고는 생각 말어. 다시 한번 말허니께. 만세! 그리고 교장 선생, 망해 버려라. 자!\"\n\n작별의 말을 마치고, 존 브로디는 묵직하게 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔어. 다시 말에 올라타, 또 한 번 가볍게 박차를 가했지. 그러고는 옛 노래 몇 소절을 우렁차게 흥얼거리며 달려갔어. 말발굽이 그 노래에 흥겨운 장단을 맞춰 울렸지. 그렇게 그는 예쁜 아내와 니콜라스에게로 서둘러 돌아갔어.\n\n그 뒤로 며칠 동안, 이웃 고장은 소년들로 넘쳐 났어. 소문에 따르면 브로디 부부가 몰래 그들에게 빵과 고기로 든든한 한 끼를 챙겨 줬을 뿐 아니라, 가는 길에 보태라고 실링과 식스펜스도 이런저런 만큼 쥐여 줬다는 거였지. 이 소문에 존은 늘 단호히 아니라고 했어. 그런데 그러면서도 슬며시 씩 웃었지. 그 웃음 때문에 의심하던 이들은 헷갈렸고, 진작부터 믿던 이들은 확신을 굳혔어.\n\n겁 많은 어린아이들도 몇몇 있었어. 비참하게 지냈고, 그 끔찍한 학교에서 숱한 눈물을 흘렸으면서도, 다른 집을 알지 못해 거기에 일종의 정 같은 것을 붙인 아이들이었지. 그래서 더 대담한 녀석들이 달아날 때 울었고, 피난처인 양 거기에 매달렸어. 이 아이들 가운데 몇몇은 산울타리 밑이나 그런 곳에서 외로움에 겁먹은 채 울고 있는 것을 발견했지. 한 아이는 작은 새장에 죽은 새를 넣어 가지고 있었어. 그 아이는 거의 이십 마일이나 헤매다, 가엾은 자기 새가 죽자 용기를 잃고 그 옆에 드러누워 있었지. 또 다른 아이는 학교 근처 마당에서 발견됐어. 개와 함께 자고 있었는데, 그 개는 아이를 떼어 내려고 다가오는 사람들을 물려고 들었고, 잠든 아이의 핼쑥한 얼굴을 핥았어.\n\n아이들은 다시 데려와졌고, 다른 낙오자 몇몇도 거두어졌어. 하지만 차츰차츰 아이들은 누군가 데려가거나 다시 사라졌지. 그리고 세월이 흐르면서, 두더보이즈 홀과 그 마지막 해산은 이웃들에게 잊히기 시작했어. 아니면 그저 지나간 일들 가운데 하나로만 입에 오르내리게 되었지.\n\n마침내 상복을 벗을 때가 되자, 매들린은 니콜라스에게 자기 손과 재산을 함께 주었어. 그리고 같은 날 같은 시각에, 케이트는 프랭크 치어리블 부인이 되었지. 이날 팀 링킨워터와 라 크리비 양도 세 번째 짝이 될 거라고들 짐작했지만, 두 사람은 마다했어. 그러더니 두세 주쯤 지난 어느 날 아침, 식사도 하기 전에 함께 나갔다가 환한 얼굴로 돌아왔는데, 알고 보니 그날 둘이 조용히 결혼식을 올린 거였어.\n\n니콜라스는 아내 덕분에 얻은 돈을 치어리블 형제 상회에 투자했어. 프랭크가 그곳의 동업자가 되어 있었거든. 몇 해 지나지 않아 사업은 '치어리블 앤드 니클비'라는 이름으로 굴러가기 시작했어. 그러니 니클비 부인이 앞날을 내다보며 했던 기대가 마침내 이루어진 셈이지.\n\n쌍둥이 형제는 일선에서 물러났어. 그들이 행복했다는 걸 누가 새삼 말해 줄 필요가 있을까? 두 사람은 스스로 만들어 낸 행복에 둘러싸여 있었고, 그 행복을 더 키우는 것 말고는 다른 삶이 없었거든.\n\n팀 링킨워터는 거듭된 간청과 다그침 끝에 마지못해 상회의 지분을 받아들였어. 하지만 자기 이름을 동업자로 내거는 것만큼은 끝내 받아들이지 않았지. 그러고는 늘 사무원의 일을 한 치 어김없이 꼬박꼬박 해냈어.\n\n그와 아내는 옛집에서 살았어. 팀이 사십사 년 동안 잠들었던 바로 그 침실을 그대로 썼지. 아내는 나이를 먹을수록 더 명랑하고 마음 가벼운 작은 사람이 되어 갔어. 친구들 사이에서는 이런 말이 흔히 오갔어. 난롯가 한쪽에서 안락의자에 앉아 잔잔히 웃고 있는 팀과, 맞은편에서 재잘대고 웃으며 끊임없이 의자를 들락날락하는 자그마하고 발랄한 그의 아내 중 누가 더 행복해 보이는지 도무지 가릴 수가 없다고 말이야.\n\n검은지빠귀 딕은 회계실에서 옮겨져 거실의 따뜻한 구석 자리로 승진했어. 새장 아래에는 작은 초상화 두 점이 걸려 있었지. 링킨워터 부인이 직접 그린 그림이었어. 하나는 자기 모습이고 다른 하나는 팀이었는데, 둘 다 보는 사람마다 향해 아주 열심히 웃고 있었어. 팀의 머리는 십이일절 케이크처럼 분을 뿌린 듯했고 안경도 아주 정성껏 베껴 그려서, 낯선 사람들도 첫눈에 팀과 꼭 닮았다는 걸 알아봤어. 그러니 다른 한 점은 그의 아내일 거라고 짐작했고, 거리낌 없이 그렇게 말할 만큼 자신만만해졌지. 링킨워터 부인은 시간이 흐를수록 이 솜씨를 무척 자랑스러워했고, 자기가 그린 것 가운데 가장 잘 닮게 그린 그림으로 꼽았어. 팀도 그 그림을 깊이 믿었지. 이 일에서나 다른 모든 일에서나 두 사람은 늘 같은 생각이었거든. 세상에 '사이좋은 부부'가 있다면, 그건 바로 링킨워터 부부였어.\n\n랄프는 유언장도 남기지 않고 죽었어. 게다가 그토록 원수처럼 지내던 사람들 말고는 친척이 없었으니, 법대로 따지자면 그 사람들이 그의 상속자가 될 판이었지. 하지만 그들은 그렇게 손에 들어온 돈으로 부자가 된다는 생각을 견딜 수가 없었어. 그 돈으로는 결코 잘될 리 없다고 느꼈거든. 그래서 그들은 랄프의 재산에 아무런 권리도 주장하지 않았어. 랄프가 평생을 바쳐 모으고 그 많은 못된 짓으로 제 영혼을 짓눌러 가며 쌓은 재물은, 결국 국고 속으로 깡그리 쓸려 들어갔어. 그 돈으로 더 나아지거나 더 행복해진 사람은 아무도 없었지.\n\n아서 그라이드는 유언장을 불법으로 가지고 있었던 죄로 재판을 받았어. 그 유언장은 사람을 시켜 훔쳐 낸 것이거나, 아니면 그에 못지않게 나쁜 다른 수법으로 부정하게 손에 넣어 감춰 둔 것이었지. 교활한 변호사와 법의 허점 덕분에 그는 빠져나갔어. 하지만 그건 더 끔찍한 벌을 받기 위해서였을 뿐이야. 몇 해 뒤, 그가 엄청난 부자라는 소문에 혹한 강도들이 한밤중에 그의 집을 부수고 들어왔고, 그는 침대에서 살해된 채 발견되었거든.\n\n슬라이더스큐 부인은 스퀴어스와 거의 같은 무렵에 바다 건너로 떠났고, 순리대로 다시는 돌아오지 못했어. 브루커는 뉘우치며 죽었지. 멀버리 호크 경은 몇 해 동안 외국에서 살았어. 떠받들리고 귀여움받으며, 멋지고 잘나가는 사내로 명성이 자자했지. 그러다 끝내 이 나라로 돌아왔다가 빚 때문에 감옥에 갇혔고, 거기서 비참하게 죽고 말았어. 그런 한껏 우쭐대던 자들이 대개 그렇게 되듯이 말이야.\n\n니콜라스는 부유하고 번창하는 상인이 되자 가장 먼저 아버지의 옛집을 사들였어. 세월이 흐르고 그의 곁에 사랑스러운 아이들이 하나둘 모여들면서, 그 집은 고쳐지고 넓어졌지. 하지만 옛 방은 하나도 헐리지 않았어. 오래된 나무도 한 그루 뽑히지 않았고, 지난 시절의 추억이 깃든 것은 무엇 하나 치워지거나 바뀌지 않았어.\n\n돌을 던지면 닿을 만한 가까운 곳에 또 하나의 보금자리가 있었는데, 그곳에도 아이들의 즐거운 목소리가 넘쳤어. 여기엔 케이트가 살았지. 새로운 걱정거리와 할 일이 많았고, 그녀의 다정한 미소를 받고 싶어 하는 새 얼굴들도 많았어. 그중 하나는 어찌나 그녀를 닮았는지, 어머니 눈에는 케이트가 다시 어린아이가 된 것처럼 보였지. 케이트는 처녀 시절과 똑같았어. 한결같이 참되고 다정한 사람이었고, 한결같은 사랑스러운 누이였으며, 주위 모든 이의 사랑을 받는 것도 그대로였어.\n\n니클비 부인은 어떤 때는 딸과, 어떤 때는 아들과 함께 살았어. 사업상의 일로 두 집안이 다 런던에 머물러야 할 때면 둘 중 하나를 따라 런던으로 갔지. 그리고 늘 위엄 있는 태도를 한껏 지키며, 자기 경험담을 — 특히 아이를 다루고 키우는 일에 관해서라면 — 아주 엄숙하고 거창하게 늘어놓았어. 부인이 링킨워터 부인을 호의로 받아들이기까지는 아주 오랜 시간이 걸렸어. 게다가 부인이 그녀를 정말로 끝까지 용서했는지조차 알 수 없는 일이었지.\n\n흰머리에 조용하고 사람 좋은 신사가 한 명 있었어. 그는 겨울이고 여름이고 니콜라스의 집 바로 옆 작은 오두막에서 살았지. 그리고 니콜라스가 집을 비울 때면 집안일을 도맡아 챙겼어. 그가 가장 기뻐하고 즐거워하는 건 아이들과 함께 있는 일이었어. 아이들과 어울릴 때면 자기도 아이가 되어 놀이의 대장 노릇을 했지. 꼬마들은 사랑하는 뉴먼 노그스 없이는 아무것도 할 수가 없었어.\n\n세상을 떠난 그 소년의 무덤 위로는 풀이 푸르게 자랐어. 그 위를 밟는 발이 어찌나 작고 가벼운지, 그 무게에 고개를 숙이는 데이지 한 송이조차 없었지. 봄과 여름 내내, 어린 손들이 엮은 싱싱한 꽃다발이 그 비석 위에 놓였어. 그리고 아이들이 꽃이 시들어 그에게 더는 기쁨이 되지 못할까 봐 새것으로 갈아 주러 올 때면, 눈에는 눈물이 그렁그렁 고였어. 그러고는 가엾게 죽은 사촌 이야기를 나직나직 속삭였지.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-967"]=[[["There once lived, in a sequestered part of the county of Devonshire, one Mr. Godfrey Nickleby: a worthy gentleman, who, taking it into his head rather late in life that he must get married, and not being young enough or rich enough to aspire to the hand of a lady of fortune, had wedded an old flame out of mere attachment, who in her turn had taken him for the same reason. Thus two people who cannot afford to play cards for money, sometimes sit down to a quiet game for love.", "옛날 데번셔 지방의 외딴 마을에 고드프리 니클비라는 사람이 살았어. 점잖은 신사였지. 그런데 나이가 꽤 들어서야 결혼을 해야겠다는 생각이 머릿속에 떠올랐어. 젊지도 않고 부자도 아니어서 돈 많은 집안의 아가씨한테 청혼할 처지가 못 되었지. 그래서 그는 옛날에 좋아하던 여자와 그저 마음 하나로 결혼했어. 그 여자도 같은 이유로 그를 받아들였지. 이렇게 돈을 걸고 카드놀이를 할 형편이 안 되는 두 사람이, 가끔은 그냥 재미로 조용히 한 판을 벌이는 거야."]], [["Some ill-conditioned persons who sneer at the life-matrimonial, may perhaps suggest, in this place, that the good couple would be better likened to two principals in a sparring match, who, when fortune is low and backers scarce, will chivalrously set to, for the mere pleasure of the buffeting; and in one respect indeed this comparison would hold good; for, as the adventurous pair of the Fives’ Court will afterwards send round a hat, and trust to the bounty of the lookers-on for the means of regaling themselves, so Mr.", "결혼 생활을 비웃는 심술궂은 사람들은 아마 여기서 이렇게 말할지도 몰라. 이 착한 부부는 권투 시합의 두 선수에 비유하는 게 더 어울린다고 말이야."], ["Godfrey Nickleby and HIS partner, the honeymoon being over, looked out wistfully into the world, relying in no inconsiderable degree upon chance for the improvement of their means.", "돈도 떨어지고 후원자도 없을 때, 그저 치고받는 재미만으로 의리 있게 한판 붙는 그런 선수들 말이지. 사실 한 가지 점에서는 그 비유가 들어맞기도 해."], ["Mr.", "권투장의 그 용감한 두 사람은 시합이 끝나면 모자를 돌려 구경꾼들의 인심에 기대 한턱 낼 밑천을 마련하잖아. 그것처럼 고드프리 니클비와 그의 아내도 신혼이 끝나자 세상을 그리운 눈으로 둘러보았어."], ["Nickleby’s income, at the period of his marriage, fluctuated between sixty and eighty pounds PER ANNUM.", "형편이 나아지기를 적잖이 운에 맡기면서 말이야. 니클비 씨가 결혼할 무렵 그의 수입은 한 해에 육십에서 팔십 파운드 사이를 오갔어."]], [["There are people enough in the world, Heaven knows!", "세상에 사람은 넘쳐 나잖아, 하늘도 알다시피!"], ["and even in London (where Mr.", "니클비 씨가 그 시절 살던 런던도 마찬가지였어."], ["Nickleby dwelt in those days) but few complaints prevail, of the population being scanty.", "사람이 부족하다는 불평은 거의 없었지. 그 많은 사람들 틈에서 친구 얼굴 하나 찾지 못하고 한참을 두리번거리는 일이 얼마나 흔한지, 참 놀라운 노릇이지만 그게 사실이야."], ["It is extraordinary how long a man may look among the crowd without discovering the face of a friend, but it is no less true.", "니클비 씨는 보고 또 봤어. 눈이 마음만큼이나 아파 올 때까지 말이야."], ["Mr. Nickleby looked, and looked, till his eyes became sore as his heart, but no friend appeared; and when, growing tired of the search, he turned his eyes homeward, he saw very little there to relieve his weary vision.", "그래도 친구는 나타나지 않았어."], ["A painter who has gazed too long upon some glaring colour, refreshes his dazzled sight by looking upon a darker and more sombre tint; but everything that met Mr.", "찾기에 지쳐서 눈길을 집 쪽으로 돌렸지만, 거기서도 지친 눈을 달래 줄 만한 건 거의 보이지 않았어. 화가는 너무 오래 쳐다본 화려한 색에 눈이 부시면 더 어둡고 차분한 색을 보며 눈을 쉬게 하잖아."], ["Nickleby’s gaze wore so black and gloomy a hue, that he would have been beyond description refreshed by the very reverse of the contrast.", "그런데 니클비 씨의 눈에 들어오는 건 죄다 새까맣고 우울한 빛깔뿐이었어. 그러니 차라리 그 반대되는 걸 봤더라면 말할 수 없이 눈이 시원해졌을 거야."]], [["At length, after five years, when Mrs. Nickleby had presented her husband with a couple of sons, and that embarrassed gentleman, impressed with the necessity of making some provision for his family, was seriously revolving in his mind a little commercial speculation of insuring his life next quarter-day, and then falling from the top of the Monument by accident, there came, one morning, by the general post, a black-bordered letter to inform him how his uncle, Mr. Ralph Nickleby, was dead, and had left him the bulk of his little property, amounting in all to five thousand pounds sterling.", "그러다 오 년이 지났어. 니클비 부인은 남편에게 아들 둘을 안겨 주었지. 곤궁한 처지의 이 신사는 가족을 위해 뭔가 대비를 해 둬야겠다는 생각에 사로잡혔어. 그래서 다음 분기 결산일에 생명 보험을 든 다음 모뉴먼트 꼭대기에서 사고로 떨어지는, 그런 자그마한 돈벌이 궁리를 진지하게 머릿속에서 굴리고 있었지. 그러던 어느 날 아침, 일반 우편으로 검은 테두리를 두른 편지 한 통이 왔어. 그의 삼촌인 랄프 니클비 씨가 죽었다는 소식이었지. 그리고 삼촌이 자기 재산의 대부분을 그에게 남겼다는 거야. 다 합쳐 오천 파운드나 되는 돈이었어."]], [["As the deceased had taken no further notice of his nephew in his lifetime, than sending to his eldest boy (who had been christened after him, on desperate speculation) a silver spoon in a morocco case, which, as he had not too much to eat with it, seemed a kind of satire upon his having been born without that useful article of plate in his mouth, Mr. Godfrey Nickleby could, at first, scarcely believe the tidings thus conveyed to him. On examination, however, they turned out to be strictly correct. The amiable old gentleman, it seemed, had intended to leave the whole to the Royal Humane Society, and had indeed executed a will to that effect; but the Institution, having been unfortunate enough, a few months before, to save the life of a poor relation to whom he paid a weekly allowance of three shillings and sixpence, he had, in a fit of very natural exasperation, revoked the bequest in a codicil, and left it all to Mr. Godfrey Nickleby; with a special mention of his indignation, not only against the society for saving the poor relation’s life, but against the poor relation also, for allowing himself to be saved.", "세상을 떠난 그 삼촌은 살아 있는 동안 조카에게 더 이상 신경을 쓰지 않았어. 그저 큰조카한테 — 혹시나 하는 마음으로 삼촌 이름을 따서 세례명을 지은 아이였지 — 모로코가죽 상자에 든 은수저 하나를 보내 줬을 뿐이야. 그런데 그 수저로 떠먹을 음식이 넉넉지 못했으니, 그건 마치 입에 그 쓸 만한 은수저를 물고 태어나지 못한 걸 비꼬는 것 같았어. 그러니 고드프리 니클비 씨는 처음에는 이 소식을 도무지 믿을 수가 없었지. 그런데 알아보니 한 치도 틀림없는 사실이었어. 사연인즉 이래. 그 인심 좋은 노신사는 원래 전 재산을 왕립 인명 구조회에 남길 작정이었고, 실제로 그렇게 유언장까지 써 두었어. 그런데 그 구조회가 몇 달 전에 그만 운 나쁘게도, 노신사가 매주 삼 실링 육 펜스씩 용돈을 주던 가난한 친척의 목숨을 구해 준 거야. 그러자 노신사는 당연하다는 듯 발끈 화가 치밀어, 유언 보충서를 써서 그 유산을 도로 거둬들였어. 그러고는 전부 고드프리 니클비 씨에게 남겼지. 거기다 자기가 얼마나 분개했는지도 특별히 적어 두었어. 가난한 친척의 목숨을 구한 구조회에 대해서뿐 아니라, 제 목숨을 구하게 내버려 둔 그 가난한 친척한테도 말이야."]], [["With a portion of this property Mr.", "고드프리 니클비 씨는 이 재산의 일부로 데번셔의 돌리시 근처에 작은 농장을 샀어. 그리고 아내와 두 아이를 데리고 그곳으로 내려가 살았지."], ["Godfrey Nickleby purchased a small farm, near Dawlish in Devonshire, whither he retired with his wife and two children, to live upon the best interest he could get for the rest of his money, and the little produce he could raise from his land.", "남은 돈으로 받을 수 있는 최대한의 이자와, 그 땅에서 거둘 수 있는 얼마 안 되는 소출에 기대어 사는 거야. 부부는 함께 사이좋게 잘 살았어."], ["The two prospered so well together that, when he died, some fifteen years after this period, and some five after his wife, he was enabled to leave, to his eldest son, Ralph, three thousand pounds in cash, and to his youngest son, Nicholas, one thousand and the farm, which was as small a landed estate as one would desire to see.", "그래서 그가 죽을 때 — 이 무렵에서 한 십오 년 뒤였고, 아내가 죽고 오 년쯤 지난 때였지 — 큰아들 랄프에게는 현금 삼천 파운드를, 작은아들 니콜라스에게는 천 파운드와 농장을 남길 수 있었어. 그 농장은 더없이 자그마한 땅뙈기였지만 말이야."]], [["These two brothers had been brought up together in a school at Exeter; and, being accustomed to go home once a week, had often heard, from their mother’s lips, long accounts of their father’s sufferings in his days of poverty, and of their deceased uncle’s importance in his days of affluence: which recitals produced a very different impression on the two: for, while the younger, who was of a timid and retiring disposition, gleaned from thence nothing but forewarnings to shun the great world and attach himself to the quiet routine of a country life, Ralph, the elder, deduced from the often-repeated tale the two great morals that riches are the only true source of happiness and power, and that it is lawful and just to compass their acquisition by all means short of felony. ‘And,’ reasoned Ralph with himself, ‘if no good came of my uncle’s money when he was alive, a great deal of good came of it after he was dead, inasmuch as my father has got it now, and is saving it up for me, which is a highly virtuous purpose; and, going back to the old gentleman, good DID come of it to him too, for he had the pleasure of thinking of it all his life long, and of being envied and courted by all his family besides.’ And Ralph always wound up these mental soliloquies by arriving at the conclusion, that there was nothing like money.", "이 두 형제는 엑서터의 한 학교에서 함께 자랐어. 일주일에 한 번씩 집에 가는 게 버릇이 되어 있었고, 그때마다 어머니 입에서 긴 이야기를 자주 들었지. 가난하던 시절 아버지가 겪은 고생담과, 부유하던 시절 돌아가신 삼촌이 얼마나 대단했는지에 관한 이야기였어. 그런데 이 이야기들은 두 형제에게 아주 다른 인상을 남겼어. 동생은 겁이 많고 내성적인 성격이라, 거기서 큰 세상을 피하고 조용한 시골 생활에 정 붙이라는 경고만 새겨들었거든. 하지만 형인 랄프는 그 되풀이되는 이야기에서 두 가지 큰 교훈을 끌어냈어. 하나는 돈이야말로 행복과 권력의 유일한 참된 근원이라는 것. 또 하나는 중범죄만 아니라면 무슨 수를 써서든 돈을 손에 넣는 게 옳고 정당하다는 것이었지. \"게다가,\" 랄프는 혼자 이렇게 따져 봤어. \"삼촌이 살아 있을 땐 그 돈으로 좋은 일이 하나도 없었지만, 죽고 나니 좋은 일이 잔뜩 생겼잖아. 지금은 아버지가 그 돈을 가지고 있고, 나를 위해 모아 두고 계시니까. 이건 아주 갸륵한 일이지. 그리고 그 노신사한테도 좋은 일이긴 했어. 평생 그 돈 생각하는 재미를 누렸고, 게다가 온 식구의 부러움과 비위 맞추기를 받았으니까.\" 그리고 랄프는 늘 이런 속말의 끝을 한 가지 결론으로 마무리했어. 돈만 한 게 없다는 것이었지."]], [["Not confining himself to theory, or permitting his faculties to rust, even at that early age, in mere abstract speculations, this promising lad commenced usurer on a limited scale at school; putting out at good interest a small capital of slate-pencil and marbles, and gradually extending his operations until they aspired to the copper coinage of this realm, in which he speculated to considerable advantage. Nor did he trouble his borrowers with abstract calculations of figures, or references to ready-reckoners; his simple rule of interest being all comprised in the one golden sentence, ‘two-pence for every half-penny,’ which greatly simplified the accounts, and which, as a familiar precept, more easily acquired and retained in the memory than any known rule of arithmetic, cannot be too strongly recommended to the notice of capitalists, both large and small, and more especially of money-brokers and bill-discounters. Indeed, to do these gentlemen justice, many of them are to this day in the frequent habit of adopting it, with eminent success.", "이 장래가 촉망되는 소년은 이론에만 머물거나, 그 어린 나이에 머리를 그저 추상적인 궁리로 녹슬게 두지 않았어. 학교에서부터 작은 규모로 고리대금업을 시작했지. 석필이며 구슬이며 약간의 밑천을 두둑한 이자를 받고 빌려주었어. 그러다 점점 사업을 키워서 마침내 이 나라의 동전까지 다루게 되었고, 거기서 꽤 짭짤한 이득을 봤지. 돈을 빌리는 아이들한테 복잡한 숫자 계산이나 계산표 같은 걸로 골치 아프게 하지도 않았어. 그의 이자 규칙은 아주 간단해서, 황금 같은 한마디에 다 담겨 있었거든. \"반 펜스마다 이 펜스.\" 이러니 계산이 엄청 단순해졌지. 게다가 익숙한 격언처럼 외기 쉽고 기억에도 잘 남으니, 알려진 그 어떤 산수 규칙보다 낫단 말이야. 그러니 크든 작든 모든 자본가들, 특히 돈을 빌려주는 중개인과 어음 할인업자들이 이 규칙을 꼭 눈여겨봐야 해. 사실 이런 사람들 명예를 위해 말하자면, 오늘날에도 그 가운데 많은 이들이 이 규칙을 즐겨 써먹으며 크게 재미를 보고 있거든."]], [["In like manner, did young Ralph Nickleby avoid all those minute and intricate calculations of odd days, which nobody who has worked sums in simple-interest can fail to have found most embarrassing, by establishing the one general rule that all sums of principal and interest should be paid on pocket-money day, that is to say, on Saturday: and that whether a loan were contracted on the Monday, or on the Friday, the amount of interest should be, in both cases, the same. Indeed he argued, and with great show of reason, that it ought to be rather more for one day than for five, inasmuch as the borrower might in the former case be very fairly presumed to be in great extremity, otherwise he would not borrow at all with such odds against him. This fact is interesting, as illustrating the secret connection and sympathy which always exist between great minds. Though Master Ralph Nickleby was not at that time aware of it, the class of gentlemen before alluded to, proceed on just the same principle in all their transactions.", "마찬가지로 어린 랄프 니클비는 자질구레한 날수 계산도 다 피해 버렸어. 단리로 계산을 해 본 사람이라면 누구나 이게 가장 골치 아프다는 걸 알 거야. 랄프는 일반 규칙을 하나 정해 버렸지. 원금이든 이자든 모든 돈은 용돈 받는 날, 그러니까 토요일에 갚아야 한다는 거였어. 그리고 돈을 월요일에 빌렸든 금요일에 빌렸든, 이자는 두 경우 다 똑같이 매겼지. 사실 랄프는 그럴듯한 근거를 대며 따졌어. 하루 빌린 사람이 닷새 빌린 사람보다 오히려 이자를 더 내야 한다고 말이야. 하루 만에 빌린 사람은 그만큼 다급한 처지라고 봐도 무방하다는 거였지. 안 그러면 그렇게 불리한 조건으로 굳이 빌리지 않았을 테니까. 이 점은 흥미로워. 위대한 머리들 사이에는 늘 어떤 은밀한 연결과 공감이 있다는 걸 보여 주거든. 그때 랄프 니클비 도련님은 미처 몰랐지만, 앞에서 말한 그런 부류의 신사들도 거래할 때마다 꼭 똑같은 원칙을 따른단 말이지."]], [["From what we have said of this young gentleman, and the natural admiration the reader will immediately conceive of his character, it may perhaps be inferred that he is to be the hero of the work which we shall presently begin.", "이 젊은 신사에 대해 우리가 늘어놓은 이야기와, 독자가 당장 그의 인품에 품게 될 자연스러운 감탄을 보면, 아마 그가 곧 시작될 이 이야기의 주인공이라고 짐작할지도 몰라. 이 점만큼은 단번에, 또 영원히 짚고 넘어가야겠어."], ["To set this point at rest, for once and for ever, we hasten to undeceive them, and stride to its commencement.", "그러니 서둘러 오해를 풀어 주고, 곧장 이야기의 시작으로 성큼 들어가야지."]], [["On the death of his father, Ralph Nickleby, who had been some time before placed in a mercantile house in London, applied himself passionately to his old pursuit of money-getting, in which he speedily became so buried and absorbed, that he quite forgot his brother for many years; and if, at times, a recollection of his old playfellow broke upon him through the haze in which he lived--for gold conjures up a mist about a man, more destructive of all his old senses and lulling to his feelings than the fumes of charcoal--it brought along with it a companion thought, that if they were intimate he would want to borrow money of him. So, Mr. Ralph Nickleby shrugged his shoulders, and said things were better as they were.", "랄프 니클비는 아버지가 돌아가시기 얼마 전에 이미 런던의 한 상점에 들어가 있었어. 아버지가 죽자 그는 돈 모으는 옛 일에 미친 듯이 매달렸지. 어찌나 거기에 파묻혀 빠져들었던지, 여러 해 동안 동생을 까맣게 잊고 지냈어. 그리고 가끔 그가 사는 안개 속을 뚫고 옛 놀이 친구 생각이 떠오를 때도 — 황금이란 사람 둘레에 안개를 피워 올리거든. 숯 연기보다 더 사람의 옛 감각을 망가뜨리고 마음을 마비시키는 안개를 말이야 — 그럴 때면 늘 한 가지 생각이 따라붙었어. 동생과 친하게 지내면 동생이 돈을 빌려 달라고 할 거라는 생각이었지. 그래서 랄프 니클비 씨는 어깨를 으쓱하고는, 지금 이대로가 낫다고 했어."]], [["As for Nicholas, he lived a single man on the patrimonial estate until he grew tired of living alone, and then he took to wife the daughter of a neighbouring gentleman with a dower of one thousand pounds. This good lady bore him two children, a son and a daughter, and when the son was about nineteen, and the daughter fourteen, as near as we can guess--impartial records of young ladies’ ages being, before the passing of the new act, nowhere preserved in the registries of this country--Mr Nickleby looked about him for the means of repairing his capital, now sadly reduced by this increase in his family, and the expenses of their education.", "니콜라스는 어땠냐면, 물려받은 땅에서 혼자 지내다가 혼자 사는 게 지겨워질 무렵 결혼을 했어. 이웃 신사의 딸을 아내로 맞았지. 천 파운드의 지참금을 가진 여자였어. 이 착한 부인은 그에게 아들 하나 딸 하나, 두 아이를 낳아 주었어. 그리고 아들이 열아홉 살쯤, 딸이 열네 살쯤 됐을 때 — 우리가 짐작할 수 있는 건 대략 이 정도야. 새 법이 통과되기 전에는 이 나라의 어떤 호적부에도 아가씨들 나이가 공평하게 기록되어 있지 않았거든 — 니클비 씨는 밑천을 다시 채울 방법이 없을까 하고 주위를 둘러보았어. 식구가 늘고 아이들 교육비가 들면서 밑천이 안타깝게도 많이 줄어든 참이었지."]], [["‘Speculate with it,’ said Mrs.", "\"그걸로 투자를 해 봐요.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘Spec--u--late, my dear?’ said Mr.", "\"투, 자, 라고, 여보?\""], ["Nickleby, as though in doubt.", "니클비 씨가 미심쩍은 듯 되물었어."]], [["‘Why not?’ asked Mrs.", "\"왜 안 되는데요?\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 물었어."]], [["‘Because, my dear, if we SHOULD lose it,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"여보, 만약에 그걸 잃기라도 하면,\""], ["Nickleby, who was a slow and time-taking speaker, ‘if we SHOULD lose it, we shall no longer be able to live, my dear.’", "말이 느리고 뜸을 들이는 편인 니클비 씨가 대꾸했어. \"만약에 그걸 잃으면, 우리는 더 이상 살아갈 수가 없게 되잖소, 여보.\""]], [["‘Fiddle,’ said Mrs.", "\"쓸데없는 소리.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘I am not altogether sure of that, my dear,’ said Mr.", "\"글쎄, 나는 그게 영 확신이 안 서는데, 여보.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘There’s Nicholas,’ pursued the lady, ‘quite a young man--it’s time he was in the way of doing something for himself; and Kate too, poor girl, without a penny in the world. Think of your brother! Would he be what he is, if he hadn’t speculated?’", "\"니콜라스를 봐요.\" 부인이 말을 이었어. \"벌써 청년이잖아요. 이제 자기 앞가림을 할 때도 됐고요. 케이트도 그래요, 가엾은 것, 세상에 한 푼도 없잖아요. 당신 형님을 생각해 봐요! 투자를 안 했다면 지금의 그 사람이 됐겠어요?\""]], [["‘That’s true,’ replied Mr.", "\"그건 맞는 말이오.\" 니클비 씨가 대답했어."], ["Nickleby. ‘Very good, my dear.", "\"아주 좋아, 여보."], ["Yes.", "그래."], ["I WILL speculate, my dear.’", "투자를 하겠소, 여보.\""]], [["Speculation is a round game; the players see little or nothing of their cards at first starting; gains MAY be great--and so may losses.", "투자란 일종의 둥근 놀이판 같은 거야. 처음 시작할 땐 자기 패가 어떤지 거의, 아니 전혀 안 보이거든."], ["The run of luck went against Mr.", "이익이 클 수도 있어 — 그리고 손해도 그만큼 클 수 있지."], ["Nickleby.", "운은 니클비 씨를 비껴갔어."], ["A mania prevailed, a bubble burst, four stock-brokers took villa residences at Florence, four hundred nobodies were ruined, and among them Mr.", "광풍이 불었고, 거품이 터졌지. 주식 중개인 넷이 피렌체에 별장을 마련했고, 보잘것없는 사람 사백 명이 망했어."], ["Nickleby.", "그 가운데 니클비 씨도 끼어 있었지."]], [["‘The very house I live in,’ sighed the poor gentleman, ‘may be taken from me tomorrow.", "\"내가 사는 이 집조차,\" 가엾은 신사는 한숨을 쉬었어."], ["Not an article of my old furniture, but will be sold to strangers!’", "\"당장 내일이라도 빼앗길지 모르지. 내 낡은 가구 한 점까지 죄다 남의 손에 팔려 나갈 거야!\""]], [["The last reflection hurt him so much, that he took at once to his bed; apparently resolved to keep that, at all events.", "마지막 생각이 어찌나 그를 아프게 했던지, 그는 곧장 자리에 드러누워 버렸어. 적어도 침대만큼은 끝까지 지키기로 마음먹은 모양이었지."]], [["‘Cheer up, sir!’ said the apothecary.", "\"기운 내세요, 선생님!\" 약제사가 말했어."]], [["‘You mustn’t let yourself be cast down, sir,’ said the nurse.", "\"의기소침해지시면 안 돼요, 선생님.\" 간호사가 말했어."]], [["‘Such things happen every day,’ remarked the lawyer.", "\"이런 일은 날마다 일어난답니다.\" 변호사가 한마디 했어."]], [["‘And it is very sinful to rebel against them,’ whispered the clergyman.", "\"그리고 이런 일에 거역하는 건 아주 죄스러운 짓이지요.\" 목사가 속삭였어."]], [["‘And what no man with a family ought to do,’ added the neighbours.", "\"가족 있는 사람이라면 절대 해선 안 될 일이고요.\" 이웃들이 거들었어."]], [["Mr.", "니클비 씨는 고개를 저었어."], ["Nickleby shook his head, and motioning them all out of the room, embraced his wife and children, and having pressed them by turns to his languidly beating heart, sunk exhausted on his pillow.", "그러고는 모두에게 방에서 나가 달라고 손짓하고는, 아내와 아이들을 끌어안았지. 힘없이 뛰는 가슴에 하나씩 차례로 그들을 꼭 품었다가, 기진맥진해서 베개에 푹 가라앉았어."], ["They were concerned to find that his reason went astray after this; for he babbled, for a long time, about the generosity and goodness of his brother, and the merry old times when they were at school together.", "식구들은 그 뒤로 그의 정신이 오락가락하는 걸 보고 가슴이 아팠어. 그가 한참 동안 형의 너그러움과 착한 마음씨에 대해, 또 학교에서 함께 지내던 즐거운 옛 시절에 대해 횡설수설했거든."], ["This fit of wandering past, he solemnly commended them to One who never deserted the widow or her fatherless children, and, smiling gently on them, turned upon his face, and observed, that he thought he could fall asleep.", "이 헛소리가 잦아들자, 그는 과부와 아버지 없는 아이들을 한 번도 저버린 적 없는 분께 식구들을 엄숙히 맡겼어. 그러고는 식구들을 향해 부드럽게 미소 짓더니 얼굴을 옆으로 돌리며, 이제 잠이 들 것 같다고 말했어."]], [["# CHAPTER 2", "# CHAPTER 2"]], [["Of Mr.", "랄프 니클비 씨는 엄밀히 말하면 상인이라고 부를 만한 사람이 아니었어."], ["Ralph Nickleby, and his Establishments, and his Undertakings, and of a great Joint Stock Company of vast national Importance\nMr.", "은행가도 아니고, 변호사도 아니고, 법정 변론인도 아니고, 공증인도 아니었지. 장사꾼은 확실히 아니었고, 전문직 신사라는 이름을 붙일 처지는 더더욱 아니었어."], ["Ralph Nickleby was not, strictly speaking, what you would call a merchant, neither was he a banker, nor an attorney, nor a special pleader, nor a notary.", "그가 속한 번듯한 직업을 하나라도 대는 건 도무지 불가능했거든. 그래도 그는 골든 스퀘어의 널찍한 집에 살았어."], ["He was certainly not a tradesman, and still less could he lay any claim to the title of a professional gentleman; for it would have been impossible to mention any recognised profession to which he belonged.", "거리로 난 현관문에 놋쇠 명패가 붙어 있었고, 거기에 더해 왼쪽 문기둥에도 그보다 두 치수 반쯤 작은 놋쇠 명패가 또 하나 붙어 있었지. 그 명패에는 꼬챙이 조각을 움켜쥔 아기 주먹 모양의 놋쇠 모형이 둘레를 두르고 있었고, '사무소'라는 글자가 적혀 있었어."], ["Nevertheless, as he lived in a spacious house in Golden Square, which, in addition to a brass plate upon the street-door, had another brass plate two sizes and a half smaller upon the left hand door-post, surrounding a brass model of an infant’s fist grasping a fragment of a skewer, and displaying the word ‘Office,’ it was clear that Mr.", "그러니 랄프 니클비 씨가 무슨 사업인가를 하고 있거나, 적어도 하는 척하고 있다는 건 분명했지. 이 사실은 만약 더 자세한 정황 증거가 필요하다면, 한 사내의 모습으로 충분히 입증되었어."], ["Ralph Nickleby did, or pretended to do, business of some kind; and the fact, if it required any further circumstantial evidence, was abundantly demonstrated by the diurnal attendance, between the hours of half-past nine and five, of a sallow-faced man in rusty brown, who sat upon an uncommonly hard stool in a species of butler’s pantry at the end of the passage, and always had a pen behind his ear when he answered the bell.", "누렇게 뜬 얼굴에 빛바랜 갈색 옷을 입은 사내였는데, 매일 아홉 시 반부터 다섯 시까지 복도 끝의 집사용 식기 골방 같은 데서 유난히 딱딱한 걸상에 앉아 있었지. 그리고 종이 울려 응대하러 나갈 때면 늘 펜 한 자루를 귀 뒤에 꽂고 있었어."]], [["Although a few members of the graver professions live about Golden Square, it is not exactly in anybody’s way to or from anywhere.", "좀 더 점잖은 직업을 가진 사람 몇몇이 골든 스퀘어 근처에 살긴 하지만, 그곳은 누가 어딜 오가는 길목에 딱 들어맞는 동네는 아니야. 한때 잘나가던 광장 중 하나였지."], ["It is one of the squares that have been; a quarter of the town that has gone down in the world, and taken to letting lodgings.", "세상에서 한물간 동네라, 이제는 셋방이나 놓고 있었어. 일이 층 방 가운데 상당수가 가구까지 딸려 독신 신사들에게 세를 놓였지."], ["Many of its first and second floors are let, furnished, to single gentlemen; and it takes boarders besides.", "게다가 하숙도 쳤어. 외국인들이 많이 모이는 곳이기도 했지."], ["It is a great resort of foreigners.", "큼직한 반지를 끼고, 묵직한 시곗줄을 늘어뜨리고, 텁수룩한 구레나룻을 기른 가무잡잡한 사내들 말이야."], ["The dark-complexioned men who wear large rings, and heavy watch-guards, and bushy whiskers, and who congregate under the Opera Colonnade, and about the box-office in the season, between four and five in the afternoon, when they give away the orders,--all live in Golden Square, or within a street of it.", "공연철이면 오후 네 시에서 다섯 시 사이, 입장권을 나눠 줄 때 오페라 회랑 아래며 매표소 근처에 모여드는 그런 사내들. 그들 모두가 골든 스퀘어에 살거나, 거기서 한 골목 안쪽에 살았어."], ["Two or three violins and a wind instrument from the Opera band reside within its precincts.", "오페라 악단의 바이올린 두세 대와 관악기 하나도 그 구역 안에 자리 잡고 있었지. 하숙집들은 음악으로 가득했어."], ["Its boarding-houses are musical, and the notes of pianos and harps float in the evening time round the head of the mournful statue, the guardian genius of a little wilderness of shrubs, in the centre of the square.", "저녁이면 피아노와 하프 소리가 둥둥 떠다녔지. 광장 한가운데 작은 관목 숲의 수호신처럼 서 있는, 그 구슬픈 석상의 머리 둘레를 맴돌면서 말이야."], ["On a summer’s night, windows are thrown open, and groups of swarthy moustached men are seen by the passer-by, lounging at the casements, and smoking fearfully.", "여름밤이면 창문들이 활짝 열렸어. 그러면 가무잡잡하고 콧수염을 기른 사내들 무리가 보였지."], ["Sounds of gruff voices practising vocal music invade the evening’s silence; and the fumes of choice tobacco scent the air.", "창틀에 기대어 무시무시하게 담배를 피워 대는 모습을 지나가는 사람들이 봤어. 성악 연습을 하는 굵직한 목소리들이 저녁의 고요를 비집고 들어왔어."], ["There, snuff and cigars, and German pipes and flutes, and violins and violoncellos, divide the supremacy between them.", "그리고 고급 담배 연기가 공기에 향을 입혔지. 거기서는 코담배와 시가, 독일제 파이프와 플루트, 바이올린과 첼로가 저마다 위세를 나눠 가지고 있었어."], ["It is the region of song and smoke.", "노래와 연기의 고장이었지."], ["Street bands are on their mettle in Golden Square; and itinerant glee-singers quaver involuntarily as they raise their voices within its boundaries.", "거리 악단들도 골든 스퀘어에서는 신명을 냈어. 떠돌이 합창단도 그 경계 안에서 목청을 높일 때면 저도 모르게 떨림 가락을 넣었지."]], [["This would not seem a spot very well adapted to the transaction of business; but Mr.", "이런 곳은 사업을 하기에 그다지 잘 맞는 자리로 보이진 않았어."], ["Ralph Nickleby had lived there, notwithstanding, for many years, and uttered no complaint on that score.", "그런데도 랄프 니클비 씨는 여기서 오랜 세월을 살았고, 그 점에 대해 불평 한마디 하지 않았지."], ["He knew nobody round about, and nobody knew him, although he enjoyed the reputation of being immensely rich.", "그는 근처의 아무도 몰랐고, 아무도 그를 몰랐어. 엄청난 부자라는 평판은 누리고 있었지만 말이야."], ["The tradesmen held that he was a sort of lawyer, and the other neighbours opined that he was a kind of general agent; both of which guesses were as correct and definite as guesses about other people’s affairs usually are, or need to be.", "장사꾼들은 그를 일종의 변호사쯤으로 여겼고, 다른 이웃들은 그가 무슨 만물 중개인 같은 사람이라고 생각했지. 두 짐작 다 옳고 분명하기로는, 남의 일에 대한 짐작이 으레 그렇거나 그래야 하는 딱 그 정도였어."]], [["Mr. Ralph Nickleby sat in his private office one morning, ready dressed to walk abroad. He wore a bottle-green spencer over a blue coat; a white waistcoat, grey mixture pantaloons, and Wellington boots drawn over them. The corner of a small-plaited shirt-frill struggled out, as if insisting to show itself, from between his chin and the top button of his spencer; and the latter garment was not made low enough to conceal a long gold watch-chain, composed of a series of plain rings, which had its beginning at the handle of a gold repeater in Mr. Nickleby’s pocket, and its termination in two little keys: one belonging to the watch itself, and the other to some patent padlock. He wore a sprinkling of powder upon his head, as if to make himself look benevolent; but if that were his purpose, he would perhaps have done better to powder his countenance also, for there was something in its very wrinkles, and in his cold restless eye, which seemed to tell of cunning that would announce itself in spite of him. However this might be, there he was; and as he was all alone, neither the powder, nor the wrinkles, nor the eyes, had the smallest effect, good or bad, upon anybody just then, and are consequently no business of ours just now.", "어느 날 아침, 랄프 니클비 씨는 외출 채비를 다 마친 차림으로 자기 사무실에 앉아 있었어. 파란 코트 위에 병처럼 짙은 초록색 스펜서를 걸쳤지. 하얀 조끼에 회색 섞인 판탈롱 바지를 입고, 그 위로 웰링턴 부츠를 신었어. 잘게 주름 잡힌 셔츠 장식의 한쪽 끄트머리가, 턱과 스펜서 맨 윗단추 사이로 비집고 나와 있었지. 마치 제 모습을 꼭 보여 주겠다고 우기는 것 같았어. 게다가 그 스펜서는 길고 금빛 나는 시곗줄을 가릴 만큼 충분히 내려오질 않았어. 밋밋한 고리들을 죽 이어 만든 줄이었는데, 니클비 씨 주머니 속 금빛 시계의 손잡이에서 시작되어 작은 열쇠 두 개에서 끝났지. 하나는 시계 자체의 열쇠였고, 다른 하나는 무슨 특허받은 자물쇠 열쇠였어. 그는 머리에 분을 살짝 뿌리고 있었어. 자기 모습을 인자하게 보이게 하려는 것 같았지. 그런데 만약 그게 목적이었다면, 아마 얼굴에도 분을 발랐어야 했을 거야. 그 주름살에도, 그 차갑고 쉴 새 없이 움직이는 눈에도 뭔가가 있었거든. 본인이 아무리 감추려 해도 저절로 드러나고야 마는 교활함을 일러 주는 그런 것 말이야. 어쨌거나 그는 거기 있었지. 그런데 혼자뿐이었으니, 그 분도, 그 주름도, 그 눈도 그 순간 누구한테든 좋게든 나쁘게든 털끝만큼의 영향도 주지 않았어. 그러니 지금 우리가 신경 쓸 일도 아니지."]], [["Mr. Nickleby closed an account-book which lay on his desk, and, throwing himself back in his chair, gazed with an air of abstraction through the dirty window. Some London houses have a melancholy little plot of ground behind them, usually fenced in by four high whitewashed walls, and frowned upon by stacks of chimneys: in which there withers on, from year to year, a crippled tree, that makes a show of putting forth a few leaves late in autumn when other trees shed theirs, and, drooping in the effort, lingers on, all crackled and smoke-dried, till the following season, when it repeats the same process, and perhaps, if the weather be particularly genial, even tempts some rheumatic sparrow to chirrup in its branches. People sometimes call these dark yards ‘gardens’; it is not supposed that they were ever planted, but rather that they are pieces of unreclaimed land, with the withered vegetation of the original brick-field. No man thinks of walking in this desolate place, or of turning it to any account. A few hampers, half-a-dozen broken bottles, and such-like rubbish, may be thrown there, when the tenant first moves in, but nothing more; and there they remain until he goes away again: the damp straw taking just as long to moulder as it thinks proper: and mingling with the scanty box, and stunted everbrowns, and broken flower-pots, that are scattered mournfully about--a prey to ‘blacks’ and dirt.", "니클비 씨는 책상에 놓인 장부 하나를 덮었어. 그러고는 의자에 등을 기대고 뒤로 젖히며, 멍한 표정으로 더러운 창문 너머를 바라봤지. 런던 집들 가운데 어떤 건 뒤편에 우울하고 자그마한 땅뙈기가 딸려 있어. 대개 회칠한 높은 담 넷으로 둘러싸여 있고, 굴뚝 더미가 그 위로 찌푸린 듯 내려다보고 있지. 거기서 해마다 절름발이 나무 한 그루가 시들어 가. 다른 나무들이 잎을 떨구는 늦가을이 되어서야 잎 몇 장 내미는 시늉을 하다가, 그 안간힘에 축 늘어진 채 버티는 거야. 온통 쩍쩍 갈라지고 연기에 그을린 채로, 다음 철까지 근근이 견디지. 그러다 또 똑같은 짓을 되풀이하고, 어쩌면 날씨가 유난히 포근하면 류머티즘 걸린 참새 한 마리를 꾀어 그 가지에서 짹짹거리게 만들기도 해. 사람들은 가끔 이 어두침침한 뒤뜰을 '정원'이라고 불러. 하지만 거기에 뭔가 심어 본 적이 있을 거라고 여기진 않아. 오히려 개간되지 않은 땅뙈기, 원래 벽돌 굽던 터의 시든 풀이 남은 자리쯤으로 보는 거지. 누구도 이 황량한 자리를 거닐 생각을 하지 않아. 무슨 쓸모를 찾아볼 생각도 안 하지. 세입자가 처음 이사 올 때 바구니 몇 개, 깨진 병 대여섯 개, 그 비슷한 잡동사니나 거기 던져 둘 뿐 그 이상은 없어. 그리고 그가 다시 떠날 때까지 그것들은 그대로 남아 있지. 축축한 짚은 제멋대로 천천히 썩어 가고, 듬성듬성한 회양목과 비실비실한 상록수, 깨진 화분 들과 뒤섞여 가는 거야. 그 화분들은 구슬프게 여기저기 흩어진 채, 검댕과 먼지의 밥이 되어 있지."]], [["It was into a place of this kind that Mr.", "랄프 니클비 씨가 바라본 게 바로 이런 곳이었어."], ["Ralph Nickleby gazed, as he sat with his hands in his pockets looking out of the window.", "그는 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 창밖을 내다보고 앉아 있었지. 그의 눈길은 비틀어진 전나무 한 그루에 꽂혀 있었어."], ["He had fixed his eyes upon a distorted fir tree, planted by some former tenant in a tub that had once been green, and left there, years before, to rot away piecemeal.", "어떤 옛 세입자가 한때 초록색이었던 통에 심어 놓고는, 여러 해 전에 거기 내버려 두어 야금야금 썩어 가게 한 나무였지. 그 물체에 딱히 마음 끌릴 만한 건 없었어."], ["There was nothing very inviting in the object, but Mr Nickleby was wrapt in a brown study, and sat contemplating it with far greater attention than, in a more conscious mood, he would have deigned to bestow upon the rarest exotic.", "그런데 니클비 씨는 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있어서, 정신이 좀 더 또렷할 때라면 세상 가장 진귀한 외래종에도 베풀지 않았을 만한 정성으로 그걸 가만히 바라보고 앉아 있었지. 마침내 그의 눈길은 왼쪽의 작고 더러운 창문으로 옮겨 갔어."], ["At length, his eyes wandered to a little dirty window on the left, through which the face of the clerk was dimly visible; that worthy chancing to look up, he beckoned him to attend.", "그 너머로 서기의 얼굴이 흐릿하게 보였지. 그 갸륵한 사내가 마침 고개를 들었기에, 니클비 씨는 그에게 들어오라고 손짓했어."]], [["In obedience to this summons the clerk got off the high stool (to which he had communicated a high polish by countless gettings off and on), and presented himself in Mr. Nickleby’s room. He was a tall man of middle age, with two goggle eyes whereof one was a fixture, a rubicund nose, a cadaverous face, and a suit of clothes (if the term be allowable when they suited him not at all) much the worse for wear, very much too small, and placed upon such a short allowance of buttons that it was marvellous how he contrived to keep them on.", "이 부름에 따라 서기는 높은 걸상에서 내려왔어. 수도 없이 오르내린 통에 반들반들 윤이 나게 된 걸상이었지. 그러고는 니클비 씨 방에 들어와 섰어. 그는 중년의 키 큰 사내였는데, 부리부리한 두 눈 가운데 하나는 아예 한곳에 박혀 움직이질 않았어. 코는 불그스름했고, 얼굴은 송장처럼 핏기가 없었지. 그리고 옷 한 벌을 걸치고 있었는데 — 도무지 몸에 맞질 않으니 '옷 한 벌'이라는 말이 어울리는지 모르겠지만 — 한참을 입어 후줄근했고, 너무 작았어. 게다가 단추가 어찌나 인색하게 달려 있던지, 그걸 어떻게 여미고 다니는지 신기할 지경이었지."]], [["‘Was that half-past twelve, Noggs?’ said Mr.", "\"지금이 열두 시 반이었나, 노그스?\""], ["Nickleby, in a sharp and grating voice.", "니클비 씨가 날카롭고 거슬리는 목소리로 물었어."]], [["‘Not more than five-and-twenty minutes by the--’ Noggs was going to add public-house clock, but recollecting himself, substituted ‘regular time.’", "\"기껏해야 이십오 분 전인데요, 저—\" 노그스는 '술집 시계로'라고 덧붙이려다가, 정신을 차리고는 '정확한 시각으로'라고 바꿔 말했어."]], [["‘My watch has stopped,’ said Mr.", "\"내 시계가 멈췄어.\""], ["Nickleby; ‘I don’t know from what cause.’", "니클비 씨가 말했어. \"왜 그런지 모르겠군.\""]], [["‘Not wound up,’ said Noggs.", "\"태엽을 안 감으셨겠죠.\" 노그스가 말했어."]], [["‘Yes it is,’ said Mr.", "\"감았어.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Over-wound then,’ rejoined Noggs.", "\"그럼 너무 많이 감으셨네요.\" 노그스가 받아쳤어."]], [["‘That can’t very well be,’ observed Mr.", "\"그럴 리가 없는데.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 씨가 한마디 했어."]], [["‘Must be,’ said Noggs.", "\"틀림없어요.\" 노그스가 말했어."]], [["‘Well!’ said Mr.", "\"그래!\""], ["Nickleby, putting the repeater back in his pocket; ‘perhaps it is.’", "니클비 씨는 시계를 도로 주머니에 넣으며 말했어. \"그럴 수도 있겠지.\""]], [["Noggs gave a peculiar grunt, as was his custom at the end of all disputes with his master, to imply that he (Noggs) triumphed; and (as he rarely spoke to anybody unless somebody spoke to him) fell into a grim silence, and rubbed his hands slowly over each other: cracking the joints of his fingers, and squeezing them into all possible distortions.", "노그스는 묘한 소리로 끙 하고 내뱉었어. 주인과의 말다툼이 끝날 때마다 늘 하는 버릇이었지."], ["The incessant performance of this routine on every occasion, and the communication of a fixed and rigid look to his unaffected eye, so as to make it uniform with the other, and to render it impossible for anybody to determine where or at what he was looking, were two among the numerous peculiarities of Mr.", "자기(노그스)가 이겼다는 뜻이었어. 그러고는 (누가 먼저 말을 걸지 않으면 좀처럼 입을 열지 않는 사람이라) 험상궂은 침묵에 빠져들었고, 두 손을 천천히 맞비볐지."], ["Noggs, which struck an inexperienced observer at first sight.", "손가락 마디를 우두둑 꺾고, 온갖 모양으로 일그러뜨려 가면서 말이야. 무슨 일이 있을 때마다 이 짓을 끊임없이 되풀이하는 것, 그리고 멀쩡한 한쪽 눈에도 고정되고 뻣뻣한 눈빛을 입혀서 다른 쪽 눈과 똑같이 만들어 버리는 것 — 그래서 그가 대체 어디를, 무엇을 보고 있는지 아무도 알 수 없게 하는 것 — 이 두 가지가 노그스 씨의 수많은 별난 점 가운데서도, 처음 보는 사람의 눈에 대뜸 띄는 것들이었지."]], [["‘I am going to the London Tavern this morning,’ said Mr.", "\"오늘 아침엔 런던 태번에 갈 거야.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Public meeting?’ inquired Noggs.", "\"공개 집회요?\" 노그스가 물었어."]], [["Mr. Nickleby nodded.", "니클비 씨는 고개를 끄덕였어."], ["‘I expect a letter from the solicitor respecting that mortgage of Ruddle’s.", "\"러들의 그 저당 건으로 변호사한테서 편지가 오기로 돼 있어."], ["If it comes at all, it will be here by the two o’clock delivery.", "혹시라도 온다면, 두 시 배달로 여기 도착할 거야."], ["I shall leave the city about that time and walk to Charing Cross on the left-hand side of the way; if there are any letters, come and meet me, and bring them with you.’", "나는 그쯤 시내를 떠나서 길 왼편으로 채링 크로스까지 걸어갈 테니까. 편지가 오면 마중을 나와서 가지고 와.\""]], [["Noggs nodded; and as he nodded, there came a ring at the office bell.", "노그스가 고개를 끄덕였어. 그가 끄덕이는데, 사무실 종이 울렸지."], ["The master looked up from his papers, and the clerk calmly remained in a stationary position.", "주인은 서류에서 눈을 들었고, 서기는 태연히 제자리에 그대로 서 있었어."]], [["‘The bell,’ said Noggs, as though in explanation.", "\"종이에요.\" 노그스가 설명이라도 하듯 말했어."], ["‘At home?’", "\"댁에 계신 걸로요?\""]], [["‘Yes.’", "\"그래.\""]], [["‘To anybody?’", "\"누구한테든요?\""]], [["‘Yes.’", "\"그래.\""]], [["‘To the tax-gatherer?’", "\"세금 걷는 사람한테도요?\""]], [["‘No!", "\"아니!"], ["Let him call again.’", "그자는 다시 오라고 해.\""]], [["Noggs gave vent to his usual grunt, as much as to say ‘I thought so!’ and, the ring being repeated, went to the door, whence he presently returned, ushering in, by the name of Mr. Bonney, a pale gentleman in a violent hurry, who, with his hair standing up in great disorder all over his head, and a very narrow white cravat tied loosely round his throat, looked as if he had been knocked up in the night and had not dressed himself since.", "노그스는 늘 하던 대로 끙 소리를 냈어. '그럴 줄 알았다니까!' 하는 듯이 말이야. 그리고 종이 또 울리자 문으로 갔지. 거기서 곧 돌아오면서, '보니 씨'라는 이름으로 한 사람을 안내해 들였어. 몹시 서두르는 창백한 신사였지. 머리카락이 온통 헝클어진 채 사방으로 곤두서 있었고, 아주 가느다란 흰 넥타이를 목에 헐겁게 묶고 있었어. 밤중에 누가 깨운 뒤로 아직 옷도 제대로 못 챙겨 입은 사람 같아 보였지."]], [["‘My dear Nickleby,’ said the gentleman, taking off a white hat which was so full of papers that it would scarcely stick upon his head, ‘there’s not a moment to lose; I have a cab at the door. Sir Matthew Pupker takes the chair, and three members of Parliament are positively coming. I have seen two of them safely out of bed. The third, who was at Crockford’s all night, has just gone home to put a clean shirt on, and take a bottle or two of soda water, and will certainly be with us, in time to address the meeting. He is a little excited by last night, but never mind that; he always speaks the stronger for it.’", "\"이보게 니클비,\" 신사는 흰 모자를 벗으며 말했어. 모자 안에 서류가 어찌나 가득 들었던지 머리에 잘 얹히지도 않을 정도였지. \"한순간도 지체할 틈이 없네. 문 앞에 마차를 대 놨어. 매슈 퍼퍼 경이 의장을 맡고, 국회의원 셋이 틀림없이 오기로 했네. 둘은 내가 직접 침대에서 끌어내 챙겼어. 셋째는 밤새 크록퍼드에 있었는데, 방금 집에 가서 깨끗한 셔츠로 갈아입고 소다수를 한두 병 들이켜겠다고 했네. 집회에서 연설할 시간엔 틀림없이 와 있을 거야. 어젯밤 일로 좀 들떠 있긴 하지만, 그건 괜찮아. 그럴 때면 오히려 연설을 더 힘차게 하거든.\""]], [["‘It seems to promise pretty well,’ said Mr.", "\"제법 잘될 것 같군.\""], ["Ralph Nickleby, whose deliberate manner was strongly opposed to the vivacity of the other man of business.", "랄프 니클비 씨가 말했어. 그의 느긋한 태도는 또 다른 이 사업가의 활기참과는 영 딴판이었지."]], [["‘Pretty well!’ echoed Mr.", "\"제법이라니!\" 보니 씨가 되받았어."], ["Bonney. ‘It’s the finest idea that was ever started.", "\"이건 여태껏 나온 것 중에 가장 기막힌 발상이야."], ["“United Metropolitan Improved Hot Muffin and Crumpet Baking and Punctual Delivery Company.", "'연합 수도권 개량 따뜻한 머핀·크럼핏 제빵 및 정시 배달 회사."], ["Capital, five millions, in five hundred thousand shares of ten pounds each.”", "자본금 오백만 파운드, 십 파운드짜리 주식 오십만 주.'"], ["Why the very name will get the shares up to a premium in ten days.’", "아, 이 이름만으로도 열흘이면 주식이 웃돈 붙어 나갈걸.\""]], [["‘And when they ARE at a premium,’ said Mr.", "\"그리고 웃돈이 붙으면,\""], ["Ralph Nickleby, smiling.", "랄프 니클비 씨가 웃으며 말했어."]], [["‘When they are, you know what to do with them as well as any man alive, and how to back quietly out at the right time,’ said Mr.", "\"붙으면 말이지, 자네야 세상 누구 못지않게 그걸 어떻게 처리할지, 적당한 때 어떻게 조용히 발을 뺄지 잘 알지 않나.\""], ["Bonney, slapping the capitalist familiarly on the shoulder.", "보니 씨는 그 자본가의 어깨를 친근하게 툭 치며 말했어."], ["‘By-the-bye, what a VERY remarkable man that clerk of yours is.’", "\"그건 그렇고, 자네 저 서기는 참 별난 사람이군그래.\""]], [["‘Yes, poor devil!’ replied Ralph, drawing on his gloves.", "\"그래, 가엾은 친구지!\" 랄프는 장갑을 끼며 대답했어."], ["‘Though Newman Noggs kept his horses and hounds once.’", "\"그래도 뉴먼 노그스가 한때는 말과 사냥개를 거느렸던 사람이라네.\""]], [["‘Ay, ay?’ said the other carelessly.", "\"오, 그래?\" 상대는 무심하게 말했어."]], [["‘Yes,’ continued Ralph, ‘and not many years ago either; but he squandered his money, invested it anyhow, borrowed at interest, and in short made first a thorough fool of himself, and then a beggar. He took to drinking, and had a touch of paralysis, and then came here to borrow a pound, as in his better days I had--’", "\"그렇다네.\" 랄프가 말을 이었어. \"그것도 그리 오래된 일도 아니야. 그런데 돈을 흥청망청 써 버리고, 아무 데나 마구 투자하고, 이자 물어 가며 돈을 빌리고, 한마디로 처음엔 제대로 바보짓을 하더니 결국엔 거지가 됐지. 술독에 빠졌다가 중풍기까지 왔고, 그러고는 여기로 일 파운드를 꾸러 왔어. 형편이 나았던 시절에 내가 그 사람하고—\""]], [["‘Done business with him,’ said Mr.", "\"거래를 해 둔 사이였다는 거지.\""], ["Bonney with a meaning look.", "보니 씨가 뜻있는 눈길로 말했어."]], [["‘Just so,’ replied Ralph; ‘I couldn’t lend it, you know.’", "\"바로 그렇지.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"그러니 내가 빌려줄 수가 없었어, 알겠나.\""]], [["‘Oh, of course not.’", "\"오, 그야 당연하지.\""]], [["‘But as I wanted a clerk just then, to open the door and so forth, I took him out of charity, and he has remained with me ever since.", "\"그런데 마침 그때 서기가 하나 필요했거든. 문도 열어 주고 그런 일을 할 사람 말이야."], ["He is a little mad, I think,’ said Mr.", "그래서 자비를 베푸는 셈 치고 데려다 썼고, 그 뒤로 줄곧 나하고 있는 거지. 저 친구 머리가 좀 어떻게 된 것 같긴 해.\""], ["Nickleby, calling up a charitable look, ‘but he is useful enough, poor creature--useful enough.’", "니클비 씨가 자비로운 표정을 지어 보이며 말했어. \"그래도 쓸모는 충분하다네, 가엾은 사람—쓸모는 충분해.\""]], [["The kind-hearted gentleman omitted to add that Newman Noggs, being utterly destitute, served him for rather less than the usual wages of a boy of thirteen; and likewise failed to mention in his hasty chronicle, that his eccentric taciturnity rendered him an especially valuable person in a place where much business was done, of which it was desirable no mention should be made out of doors.", "이 마음씨 고운 신사는 한 가지를 덧붙이지 않았어. 뉴먼 노그스가 완전히 빈털터리라서, 열세 살짜리 소년한테 주는 보통 품삯보다도 적은 돈을 받고 그를 위해 일한다는 사실 말이야."], ["The other gentleman was plainly impatient to be gone, however, and as they hurried into the hackney cabriolet immediately afterwards, perhaps Mr.", "또 그 황급한 이야기 속에서 이 점도 빼먹었지. 노그스의 별난 과묵함 덕분에, 바깥에 새어 나가면 안 될 일이 많이 오가는 그런 곳에서 그가 특히 값진 사람이 되었다는 것 말이야."], ["Nickleby forgot to mention circumstances so unimportant.", "어쨌든 상대 신사는 어서 가고 싶어 안달이 난 게 뻔했어. 그래서 둘이 곧장 삯마차에 황급히 올라탔으니, 어쩌면 니클비 씨가 그렇게 사소한 사정들을 깜빡 잊고 말 안 한 건지도 모르지."]], [["There was a great bustle in Bishopsgate Street Within, as they drew up, and (it being a windy day) half-a-dozen men were tacking across the road under a press of paper, bearing gigantic announcements that a Public Meeting would be holden at one o’clock precisely, to take into consideration the propriety of petitioning Parliament in favour of the United Metropolitan Improved Hot Muffin and Crumpet Baking and Punctual Delivery Company, capital five millions, in five hundred thousand shares of ten pounds each; which sums were duly set forth in fat black figures of considerable size. Mr. Bonney elbowed his way briskly upstairs, receiving in his progress many low bows from the waiters who stood on the landings to show the way; and, followed by Mr. Nickleby, dived into a suite of apartments behind the great public room: in the second of which was a business-looking table, and several business-looking people.", "마차가 멈춰 설 무렵 비숍스게이트 거리 안쪽은 큰 소동이 벌어져 있었어. (바람 부는 날이었는데) 사내 대여섯이 종이를 잔뜩 짊어진 채 길을 가로질러 비척거리며 다니고 있었지. 거기엔 어마어마한 공고문이 적혀 있었어. 정확히 한 시에 공개 집회가 열린다는 거야. '연합 수도권 개량 따뜻한 머핀·크럼핏 제빵 및 정시 배달 회사', 자본금 오백만 파운드, 십 파운드짜리 주식 오십만 주를 위해 국회에 청원하는 게 옳은지를 의논하기 위해서 말이지. 그 금액들은 꽤 큼직하고 통통한 검은 숫자로 또박또박 적혀 있었어. 보니 씨는 팔꿈치로 헤치며 잰걸음으로 계단을 올라갔어. 올라가는 동안 층계참에 서서 길을 안내하던 종업원들한테서 굽실거리는 인사를 숱하게 받았지. 그리고 니클비 씨를 뒤에 거느린 채, 큰 공개 회의실 뒤편의 방들로 쑥 들어갔어. 그중 둘째 방에는 사무용으로 보이는 탁자가 하나, 그리고 사무용으로 보이는 사람 여럿이 있었지."]], [["‘Hear!’ cried a gentleman with a double chin, as Mr.", "\"옳소!\" 턱이 두 겹인 신사가 보니 씨가 모습을 드러내자 외쳤어."], ["Bonney presented himself. ‘Chair, gentlemen, chair!’", "\"의장석으로, 여러분, 의장석으로!\""]], [["The new-comers were received with universal approbation, and Mr. Bonney bustled up to the top of the table, took off his hat, ran his fingers through his hair, and knocked a hackney-coachman’s knock on the table with a little hammer: whereat several gentlemen cried ‘Hear!’ and nodded slightly to each other, as much as to say what spirited conduct that was. Just at this moment, a waiter, feverish with agitation, tore into the room, and throwing the door open with a crash, shouted ‘Sir Matthew Pupker!’", "새로 온 두 사람은 모두의 갈채를 받으며 맞아들여졌어. 보니 씨는 부산스레 탁자 상석으로 올라가 모자를 벗고는, 손가락으로 머리를 쓸어 넘겼어. 그러고는 작은 망치로 탁자를 탕탕 두드렸지. 삯마차 마부가 두드리듯이 말이야. 그러자 여러 신사가 \"옳소!\" 하고 외치며 서로에게 가볍게 고개를 끄덕였어. 참으로 기개 있는 행동이라는 듯이 말이지. 바로 그때 종업원 하나가 흥분으로 안달이 나서는 방 안으로 뛰어들었어. 그리고 쾅 소리 나게 문을 열어젖히며 외쳤지. \"매슈 퍼퍼 경 드십니다!\""]], [["The committee stood up and clapped their hands for joy, and while they were clapping them, in came Sir Matthew Pupker, attended by two live members of Parliament, one Irish and one Scotch, all smiling and bowing, and looking so pleasant that it seemed a perfect marvel how any man could have the heart to vote against them. Sir Matthew Pupker especially, who had a little round head with a flaxen wig on the top of it, fell into such a paroxysm of bows, that the wig threatened to be jerked off, every instant. When these symptoms had in some degree subsided, the gentlemen who were on speaking terms with Sir Matthew Pupker, or the two other members, crowded round them in three little groups, near one or other of which the gentlemen who were NOT on speaking terms with Sir Matthew Pupker or the two other members, stood lingering, and smiling, and rubbing their hands, in the desperate hope of something turning up which might bring them into notice. All this time, Sir Matthew Pupker and the two other members were relating to their separate circles what the intentions of government were, about taking up the bill; with a full account of what the government had said in a whisper the last time they dined with it, and how the government had been observed to wink when it said so; from which premises they were at no loss to draw the conclusion, that if the government had one object more at heart than another, that one object was the welfare and advantage of the United Metropolitan Improved Hot Muffin and Crumpet Baking and Punctual Delivery Company.", "위원들은 자리에서 일어나 기뻐서 손뼉을 쳤어. 그들이 손뼉을 치는 사이, 매슈 퍼퍼 경이 들어왔지. 살아 있는 국회의원 둘을 거느리고 말이야. 하나는 아일랜드 사람, 하나는 스코틀랜드 사람이었어. 다들 싱글벙글 웃으며 고개를 숙였고, 어찌나 유쾌해 보이던지 누가 저들에게 반대표를 던질 모진 마음을 먹을 수 있을까 싶을 만큼 신기할 정도였지. 특히 매슈 퍼퍼 경은 작고 둥근 머리에 아마빛 가발을 얹고 있었는데, 어찌나 정신없이 고개를 숙여 대는지 매 순간 가발이 홱 벗겨질 것만 같았어. 이런 기색이 어느 정도 가라앉자, 매슈 퍼퍼 경이나 다른 두 의원과 말을 트고 지내는 신사들이 세 무리로 갈라져 그들을 둘러쌌어. 그리고 그 어느 무리 곁에든, 매슈 퍼퍼 경이나 다른 두 의원과 말을 트지 못한 신사들이 서성거리며 머물러 있었지. 웃음 띤 얼굴로 두 손을 비비면서, 뭔가 일이 생겨 자기들도 눈에 띄게 되기를 간절히 바라면서 말이야. 그러는 내내 매슈 퍼퍼 경과 다른 두 의원은 저마다 자기 무리에게 정부의 속셈을 들려주고 있었어. 정부가 이 법안을 다룰 의향이 어떻다느니 하면서 말이지. 지난번 정부와 함께 식사할 때 정부가 귓속말로 뭐라고 했는지, 그 말을 하면서 정부가 어떻게 눈을 찡긋하는 게 보였는지까지 소상히 늘어놓았어. 그런 전제에서 그들은 어렵잖게 결론을 끌어냈지. 정부가 다른 무엇보다 마음 깊이 품은 목표가 하나 있다면, 그건 바로 '연합 수도권 개량 따뜻한 머핀·크럼핏 제빵 및 정시 배달 회사'의 번영과 이익이라고 말이야."]], [["Meanwhile, and pending the arrangement of the proceedings, and a fair division of the speechifying, the public in the large room were eyeing, by turns, the empty platform, and the ladies in the Music Gallery. In these amusements the greater portion of them had been occupied for a couple of hours before, and as the most agreeable diversions pall upon the taste on a too protracted enjoyment of them, the sterner spirits now began to hammer the floor with their boot-heels, and to express their dissatisfaction by various hoots and cries. These vocal exertions, emanating from the people who had been there longest, naturally proceeded from those who were nearest to the platform and furthest from the policemen in attendance, who having no great mind to fight their way through the crowd, but entertaining nevertheless a praiseworthy desire to do something to quell the disturbance, immediately began to drag forth, by the coat tails and collars, all the quiet people near the door; at the same time dealing out various smart and tingling blows with their truncheons, after the manner of that ingenious actor, Mr. Punch: whose brilliant example, both in the fashion of his weapons and their use, this branch of the executive occasionally follows.", "그러는 동안, 진행 순서를 정하고 연설을 공평하게 나누는 일이 마무리되기를 기다리며, 큰 회의실의 사람들은 번갈아 가며 텅 빈 단상과 음악 발코니의 부인들을 쳐다보고 있었어. 그들 대부분이 이미 두 시간 전부터 이런 구경거리로 시간을 보내고 있었지. 그런데 아무리 흥겨운 소일거리도 너무 오래 끌면 입맛이 떨어지는 법이라, 성미 급한 축들이 이제 구두 뒤축으로 바닥을 쿵쿵 치기 시작했어. 그리고 온갖 야유와 고함으로 못마땅함을 드러냈지. 가장 오래 있던 사람들 입에서 터져 나온 이 목청 소리는, 당연히 단상에 가장 가깝고 경비를 서는 경관들로부터는 가장 먼 자리에서 나왔어. 그 경관들은 군중을 헤치고 들어갈 마음은 별로 없으면서도, 그래도 이 소란을 가라앉히려고 뭐라도 해 보겠다는 갸륵한 마음만은 있었지. 그래서 곧장 문가에 있던 얌전한 사람들을 죄다 옷자락이며 멱살을 잡아 끌어냈어. 동시에 곤봉으로 따끔하고 얼얼한 매를 여기저기 갈겼지. 저 재주꾼 배우 펀치 씨의 방식대로 말이야. 무기의 생김새며 그 쓰는 법에 있어서, 이 행정 분야는 이따금 펀치 씨의 빛나는 본보기를 따라 하거든."]], [["Several very exciting skirmishes were in progress, when a loud shout attracted the attention even of the belligerents, and then there poured on to the platform, from a door at the side, a long line of gentlemen with their hats off, all looking behind them, and uttering vociferous cheers; the cause whereof was sufficiently explained when Sir Matthew Pupker and the two other real members of Parliament came to the front, amidst deafening shouts, and testified to each other in dumb motions that they had never seen such a glorious sight as that, in the whole course of their public career.", "아주 흥미진진한 몸싸움 몇 판이 벌어지고 있을 때였어. 우렁찬 함성이 울려 싸우던 사람들조차 정신이 그쪽으로 쏠렸지. 그러더니 옆문에서 신사들이 한 줄로 길게 단상으로 쏟아져 들어왔어. 다들 모자를 벗어 들고, 죄다 뒤를 돌아보며 목청껏 환호를 지르고 있었지. 그 까닭은 곧 충분히 밝혀졌어. 매슈 퍼퍼 경과 다른 진짜 국회의원 둘이 귀가 멍멍한 함성 속에서 앞으로 나왔거든. 그리고 자기들 공직 생활을 통틀어 이토록 영광스러운 광경은 본 적이 없다고, 서로에게 말없는 몸짓으로 증언했지."]], [["At length, and at last, the assembly left off shouting, but Sir Matthew Pupker being voted into the chair, they underwent a relapse which lasted five minutes. This over, Sir Matthew Pupker went on to say what must be his feelings on that great occasion, and what must be that occasion in the eyes of the world, and what must be the intelligence of his fellow-countrymen before him, and what must be the wealth and respectability of his honourable friends behind him, and lastly, what must be the importance to the wealth, the happiness, the comfort, the liberty, the very existence of a free and great people, of such an Institution as the United Metropolitan Improved Hot Muffin and Crumpet Baking and Punctual Delivery Company!", "마침내, 정말 마침내 사람들이 함성을 그쳤어. 그런데 매슈 퍼퍼 경이 의장으로 추대되자, 다시 도지기를 했고 그게 오 분이나 이어졌지. 이게 끝나자 매슈 퍼퍼 경이 말을 이어 갔어. 이 위대한 자리에서 자기 심정이 어떠할지, 이 자리가 세상 사람들 눈에 어떠할지, 자기 앞에 모인 동포들의 식견이 어떠할지, 자기 뒤에 선 존경하는 벗들의 재산과 명망이 어떠할지, 그리고 마지막으로, '연합 수도권 개량 따뜻한 머핀·크럼핏 제빵 및 정시 배달 회사' 같은 기관이 자유롭고 위대한 국민의 부와 행복과 안락과 자유와, 그 존립 자체에 얼마나 중요할지를 말이야!"]], [["Mr. Bonney then presented himself to move the first resolution; and having run his right hand through his hair, and planted his left, in an easy manner, in his ribs, he consigned his hat to the care of the gentleman with the double chin (who acted as a species of bottle-holder to the orators generally), and said he would read to them the first resolution--‘That this meeting views with alarm and apprehension, the existing state of the Muffin Trade in this Metropolis and its neighbourhood; that it considers the Muffin Boys, as at present constituted, wholly underserving the confidence of the public; and that it deems the whole Muffin system alike prejudicial to the health and morals of the people, and subversive of the best interests of a great commercial and mercantile community.’ The honourable gentleman made a speech which drew tears from the eyes of the ladies, and awakened the liveliest emotions in every individual present. He had visited the houses of the poor in the various districts of London, and had found them destitute of the slightest vestige of a muffin, which there appeared too much reason to believe some of these indigent persons did not taste from year’s end to year’s end. He had found that among muffin-sellers there existed drunkenness, debauchery, and profligacy, which he attributed to the debasing nature of their employment as at present exercised; he had found the same vices among the poorer class of people who ought to be muffin consumers; and this he attributed to the despair engendered by their being placed beyond the reach of that nutritious article, which drove them to seek a false stimulant in intoxicating liquors. He would undertake to prove before a committee of the House of Commons, that there existed a combination to keep up the price of muffins, and to give the bellmen a monopoly; he would prove it by bellmen at the bar of that House; and he would also prove, that these men corresponded with each other by secret words and signs as ‘Snooks,’ ‘Walker,’ ‘Ferguson,’ ‘Is Murphy right?’ and many others. It was this melancholy state of things that the Company proposed to correct; firstly, by prohibiting, under heavy penalties, all private muffin trading of every description; secondly, by themselves supplying the public generally, and the poor at their own homes, with muffins of first quality at reduced prices. It was with this object that a bill had been introduced into Parliament by their patriotic chairman Sir Matthew Pupker; it was this bill that they had met to support; it was the supporters of this bill who would confer undying brightness and splendour upon England, under the name of the United Metropolitan Improved Hot Muffin and Crumpet Baking and Punctual Delivery Company; he would add, with a capital of Five Millions, in five hundred thousand shares of ten pounds each.", "그러고 나서 보니 씨가 첫 번째 결의안을 발의하러 나섰어. 오른손으로 머리를 한 번 쓸어 넘기고, 왼손은 느긋하게 옆구리에 척 갖다 댄 다음, 자기 모자는 턱이 두 겹인 신사한테 맡겼지. (그 신사는 대체로 연사들의 시중꾼 노릇을 하고 있었어.) 그러고는 첫 번째 결의안을 읽어 주겠다고 했어. \"본 집회는 이 수도와 그 인근의 머핀 업계의 현 상태를 경악과 우려로써 바라보는 바이며, 현재의 머핀 소년들이 공중의 신뢰를 받을 자격이 전혀 없다고 여기는 바이며, 머핀 제도 전반이 국민의 건강과 도덕에 똑같이 해로우며, 위대한 상업·무역 공동체의 최선의 이익을 뒤엎는 것이라고 단정하는 바이다.\" 이 존경하는 신사는 연설을 했는데, 부인들 눈에서 눈물을 자아냈고 그 자리에 있는 한 사람 한 사람의 가슴에 더없이 생생한 감정을 불러일으켰지. 그는 런던 곳곳 동네의 가난한 이들의 집을 찾아가 봤다고 했어. 그런데 머핀의 흔적이라곤 티끌만큼도 없더라는 거야. 거기엔 이런 가난한 이들 중 더러는 한 해가 다 가도록 머핀 맛을 못 본다고 믿을 만한 까닭이 너무도 많아 보였지. 그는 머핀 장수들 사이에 술주정과 방탕과 난봉이 판친다는 걸 알아냈어. 그리고 그걸 지금처럼 영위되는 그들 일의 천한 성격 탓으로 돌렸지. 그는 머핀을 소비해야 마땅할 가난한 계층 사람들 사이에서도 똑같은 악덕을 발견했어. 그리고 이건 그들이 그 영양가 있는 식품을 손에 넣을 수 없는 처지에 놓여 생긴 절망 탓으로 돌렸지. 그 절망이 그들을 몰아붙여, 독한 술에서 거짓 자극을 찾게 만든다는 거야. 그는 하원 위원회 앞에서 증명해 보이겠다고 했어. 머핀 값을 높게 유지하고 종 치는 장수들에게 독점권을 주려는 담합이 있다는 걸 말이야. 종 치는 장수들을 하원의 증인석에 세워 그걸 증명하겠다고 했지. 또 이런 자들이 '스눅스', '워커', '퍼거슨', '머피는 제대로 하고 있나?' 같은 은밀한 암호와 신호로 서로 연락을 주고받는다는 것도 증명하겠다고 했어. 그밖에도 많다고 했지. 회사가 바로잡으려는 게 바로 이 서글픈 작태라는 거야. 첫째로, 온갖 종류의 모든 사적인 머핀 거래를 무거운 벌로써 금지함으로써, 둘째로, 회사가 직접 일반 대중에게, 또 가난한 이들에게는 그 집까지 찾아가, 최상급 머핀을 싼값에 공급함으로써 말이지. 바로 이 목적으로, 애국적인 의장 매슈 퍼퍼 경이 국회에 법안을 하나 제출해 두었다고 했어. 그들이 지지하려고 모인 게 바로 이 법안이라는 거야. 이 법안을 지지하는 이들이야말로 영국에 길이 빛날 광채와 영광을 안겨 줄 사람들이며, 그 이름은 '연합 수도권 개량 따뜻한 머핀·크럼핏 제빵 및 정시 배달 회사'라고 했지. 거기에 그는 덧붙였어. 자본금 오백만 파운드, 십 파운드짜리 주식 오십만 주라고 말이야."]], [["Mr. Ralph Nickleby seconded the resolution, and another gentleman having moved that it be amended by the insertion of the words ‘and crumpet’ after the word ‘muffin,’ whenever it occurred, it was carried triumphantly.", "랄프 니클비 씨가 그 결의안을 재청했어."], ["Only one man in the crowd cried ‘No!’ and he was promptly taken into custody, and straightway borne off.", "그리고 다른 신사 하나가, '머핀'이라는 말이 나올 때마다 그 뒤에 '및 크럼핏'이라는 말을 끼워 넣어 수정하자고 발의하자, 결의안은 의기양양하게 통과되었지. 군중 가운데 단 한 사람만이 \"반대!\"라고 외쳤는데, 그자는 즉각 붙들려 곧장 끌려 나갔어."]], [["The second resolution, which recognised the expediency of immediately abolishing ‘all muffin (or crumpet) sellers, all traders in muffins (or crumpets) of whatsoever description, whether male or female, boys or men, ringing hand-bells or otherwise,’ was moved by a grievous gentleman of semi-clerical appearance, who went at once into such deep pathetics, that he knocked the first speaker clean out of the course in no time. You might have heard a pin fall--a pin! a feather--as he described the cruelties inflicted on muffin boys by their masters, which he very wisely urged were in themselves a sufficient reason for the establishment of that inestimable company. It seemed that the unhappy youths were nightly turned out into the wet streets at the most inclement periods of the year, to wander about, in darkness and rain--or it might be hail or snow--for hours together, without shelter, food, or warmth; and let the public never forget upon the latter point, that while the muffins were provided with warm clothing and blankets, the boys were wholly unprovided for, and left to their own miserable resources. (Shame!) The honourable gentleman related one case of a muffin boy, who having been exposed to this inhuman and barbarous system for no less than five years, at length fell a victim to a cold in the head, beneath which he gradually sunk until he fell into a perspiration and recovered; this he could vouch for, on his own authority, but he had heard (and he had no reason to doubt the fact) of a still more heart-rending and appalling circumstance. He had heard of the case of an orphan muffin boy, who, having been run over by a hackney carriage, had been removed to the hospital, had undergone the amputation of his leg below the knee, and was now actually pursuing his occupation on crutches. Fountain of justice, were these things to last!", "두 번째 결의안은, '모든 머핀(또는 크럼핏) 장수, 그 종류를 막론한 모든 머핀(또는 크럼핏) 거래상, 남자든 여자든 소년이든 어른이든, 손 종을 울리든 안 울리든'을 즉각 폐지하는 게 마땅하다고 인정하는 내용이었어. 이걸 발의한 건 반쯤 성직자 같은 행색의, 비통해 보이는 신사였지. 그는 대뜸 어찌나 깊은 비애로 들어갔던지, 첫 번째 연사를 순식간에 완전히 무색하게 만들어 버렸어. 핀 하나 떨어지는 소리도 들렸을 거야 — 핀이라니! 깃털 하나 떨어지는 소리도 말이지 — 그가 머핀 소년들이 주인한테 당하는 학대를 묘사하는 동안 말이야. 그는 아주 현명하게도, 그 학대 자체만으로도 그 더없이 값진 회사를 세울 충분한 이유가 된다고 역설했지. 그 불쌍한 소년들은 한 해 중 가장 모진 철에도 밤마다 축축한 거리로 내쫓긴다는 거야. 어둠과 빗속을 — 어쩌면 우박이나 눈 속을 — 몇 시간씩 헤매고 다닌다는 거지. 비바람을 막을 곳도, 먹을 것도, 따뜻함도 없이 말이야. 그리고 마지막 점에 대해서는 대중이 절대 잊어선 안 된다고 했어. 머핀에는 따뜻한 보온재와 담요가 마련되어 있는데, 정작 소년들에게는 아무것도 마련되어 있지 않아, 제 한 몸 가엾은 처지에 내맡겨진다는 거야. (수치스럽다!) 이 존경하는 신사는 한 머핀 소년의 사례를 들려주었어. 무려 오 년 동안이나 이 비인간적이고 야만적인 제도에 시달린 끝에, 마침내 머리 감기에 걸려 쓰러진 소년이었지. 그 병 아래 점점 가라앉다가, 그만 땀을 흘리고는 회복되었다는 거야. 이건 자기가 직접 보증할 수 있다고 했어. 하지만 더 가슴 찢어지고 소름 끼치는 일도 들었다고 했지. (그리고 그 사실을 의심할 까닭이 없다고 했어.) 그는 한 고아 머핀 소년의 사례를 들었다고 했어. 삯마차에 치여 병원으로 실려 갔고, 무릎 아래로 다리를 잘라 내는 수술을 받았으며, 지금은 실제로 목발을 짚고 그 일을 계속하고 있다는 거야. 정의의 샘이여, 이런 일들이 계속되어야 한단 말인가!"]], [["This was the department of the subject that took the meeting, and this was the style of speaking to enlist their sympathies.", "집회의 마음을 사로잡은 게 바로 이 대목이었어. 그리고 사람들의 동정심을 끌어내는 게 바로 이런 연설 투였지."], ["The men shouted; the ladies wept into their pocket-handkerchiefs till they were moist, and waved them till they were dry; the excitement was tremendous; and Mr.", "사내들은 고함을 질렀어. 부인들은 손수건이 축축해질 때까지 거기에 눈물을 쏟더니, 마를 때까지 그걸 흔들어 댔지."], ["Nickleby whispered his friend that the shares were thenceforth at a premium of five-and-twenty per cent.", "흥분이 어마어마했어. 그리고 니클비 씨는 친구에게 귓속말로, 이제부터 주식이 이십오 퍼센트 웃돈이 붙었다고 말했지."]], [["The resolution was, of course, carried with loud acclamations, every man holding up both hands in favour of it, as he would in his enthusiasm have held up both legs also, if he could have conveniently accomplished it. This done, the draft of the proposed petition was read at length: and the petition said, as all petitions DO say, that the petitioners were very humble, and the petitioned very honourable, and the object very virtuous; therefore (said the petition) the bill ought to be passed into a law at once, to the everlasting honour and glory of that most honourable and glorious Commons of England in Parliament assembled.", "결의안은 물론 우렁찬 갈채 속에 통과되었어. 모두가 두 손을 번쩍 들어 찬성했지. 어찌나 열성이던지, 편하게 할 수만 있었다면 두 다리까지 들어 올렸을 거야. 이게 끝나자, 제출하기로 한 청원서 초안을 처음부터 끝까지 읽어 내려갔어. 그 청원서는, 모든 청원서가 으레 그러듯, 청원하는 자들은 더없이 비천하고, 청원받는 분들은 더없이 존귀하며, 그 목적은 더없이 갸륵하다고 했지. 그러므로 (청원서가 이르기를) 이 법안은 즉각 법으로 통과되어야 마땅하며, 그래야 국회에 모인 더없이 존귀하고 영광스러운 영국 하원의 영예와 영광이 영원하리라는 거야."]], [["Then, the gentleman who had been at Crockford’s all night, and who looked something the worse about the eyes in consequence, came forward to tell his fellow-countrymen what a speech he meant to make in favour of that petition whenever it should be presented, and how desperately he meant to taunt the parliament if they rejected the bill; and to inform them also, that he regretted his honourable friends had not inserted a clause rendering the purchase of muffins and crumpets compulsory upon all classes of the community, which he--opposing all half-measures, and preferring to go the extreme animal--pledged himself to propose and divide upon, in committee. After announcing this determination, the honourable gentleman grew jocular; and as patent boots, lemon-coloured kid gloves, and a fur coat collar, assist jokes materially, there was immense laughter and much cheering, and moreover such a brilliant display of ladies’ pocket-handkerchiefs, as threw the grievous gentleman quite into the shade.", "그러고 나서, 밤새 크록퍼드에 있었던 그 신사가 — 그 탓에 눈언저리가 좀 안 좋아 보였지 — 앞으로 나와 동포들에게 말했어. 이 청원서가 제출되기만 하면 자기가 그걸 지지하는 연설을 어떻게 할 작정인지, 만약 국회가 이 법안을 부결한다면 자기가 얼마나 무섭게 국회를 비꼴 작정인지를 말이지. 그리고 또 알려 주었어. 자기 존경하는 벗들이, 모든 계층의 국민에게 머핀과 크럼핏 구매를 의무로 정하는 조항을 끼워 넣지 않은 게 못내 아쉽다고 말이야. 그는 — 어떤 어중간한 방책도 반대하고, 끝까지 밀어붙이는 걸 좋아하는 사람이라 — 위원회에서 그 조항을 발의해 표결에 부치겠다고 다짐했지. 이 결심을 밝히고 나서, 이 존경하는 신사는 익살스러워졌어. 그리고 특허받은 부츠와 레몬빛 새끼염소 가죽 장갑과 모피 외투 깃은 농담에 큰 보탬이 되는 법이라, 엄청난 웃음과 많은 환호가 터졌지. 게다가 부인들의 손수건이 어찌나 눈부시게 펄럭였던지, 아까 그 비통한 신사가 완전히 빛을 잃을 정도였어."]], [["And when the petition had been read and was about to be adopted, there came forward the Irish member (who was a young gentleman of ardent temperament,) with such a speech as only an Irish member can make, breathing the true soul and spirit of poetry, and poured forth with such fervour, that it made one warm to look at him; in the course whereof, he told them how he would demand the extension of that great boon to his native country; how he would claim for her equal rights in the muffin laws as in all other laws; and how he yet hoped to see the day when crumpets should be toasted in her lowly cabins, and muffin bells should ring in her rich green valleys. And, after him, came the Scotch member, with various pleasant allusions to the probable amount of profits, which increased the good humour that the poetry had awakened; and all the speeches put together did exactly what they were intended to do, and established in the hearers’ minds that there was no speculation so promising, or at the same time so praiseworthy, as the United Metropolitan Improved Hot Muffin and Crumpet Baking and Punctual Delivery Company.", "그리고 청원서를 다 읽고 막 채택하려 할 때, 아일랜드 의원이 앞으로 나왔어. (열정적인 기질의 젊은 신사였지.) 오직 아일랜드 의원만이 할 수 있는 그런 연설을 가지고서 말이야. 시의 참된 혼과 정신이 숨 쉬는 연설이었고, 어찌나 열정적으로 쏟아 내던지 그를 보고만 있어도 몸이 뜨거워질 정도였지. 그 연설 가운데 그는 사람들에게 말했어. 자기는 이 위대한 은혜를 자기 고국에까지 넓혀 달라고 요구하겠노라고. 다른 모든 법에서와 마찬가지로 머핀 법에서도 고국을 위해 동등한 권리를 주장하겠노라고. 그리고 자기는 그날이 오기를 여전히 바란다고 했지. 고국의 누추한 오두막에서도 크럼핏이 구워지고, 풍요로운 푸른 골짜기마다 머핀 종이 울리는 그날을 말이야. 그 뒤를 이어 스코틀랜드 의원이 나왔어. 예상되는 수익 규모에 관해 이런저런 유쾌한 언급을 곁들여서, 시가 불러일으킨 좋은 기분을 한층 돋우었지. 그리고 모든 연설이 한데 어우러져, 의도했던 바를 정확히 이루어 냈어. 듣는 이들의 마음에, '연합 수도권 개량 따뜻한 머핀·크럼핏 제빵 및 정시 배달 회사'만큼 전망 밝고 동시에 칭송할 만한 투자처는 없다는 확신을 심어 준 거야."]], [["So, the petition in favour of the bill was agreed upon, and the meeting adjourned with acclamations, and Mr.", "그렇게 해서 법안을 지지하는 청원이 합의되었고, 집회는 갈채 속에 끝이 났어. 그리고 니클비 씨와 다른 이사들은 점심을 먹으러 사무소로 갔지."], ["Nickleby and the other directors went to the office to lunch, as they did every day at half-past one o’clock; and to remunerate themselves for which trouble, (as the company was yet in its infancy,) they only charged three guineas each man for every such attendance.", "매일 한 시 반이면 으레 그러듯이 말이야. 그리고 이 수고에 대한 보수로 (회사가 아직 걸음마 단계라) 그들은 그런 출석 한 번에 한 사람당 고작 삼 기니씩만 청구했단다."]], [["# CHAPTER 3", "# CHAPTER 3"]], [["Mr. Ralph Nickleby receives Sad Tidings of his Brother, but bears up nobly against the Intelligence communicated to him.", "랄프 니클비 씨는 점심을 해치우는 데 열성껏 손을 보탰어."], ["The Reader is informed how he liked Nicholas, who is herein introduced, and how kindly he proposed to make his Fortune at once\nHaving rendered his zealous assistance towards dispatching the lunch, with all that promptitude and energy which are among the most important qualities that men of business can possess, Mr.", "사업하는 사람들이 갖춰야 할 가장 중요한 자질 가운데 하나인 그 재빠르고 기운찬 솜씨로 말이야. 그러고는 함께 투기하던 동료들과 정답게 작별 인사를 나누고, 평소답지 않게 기분 좋은 얼굴로 서쪽을 향해 발걸음을 옮겼어."], ["Ralph Nickleby took a cordial farewell of his fellow-speculators, and bent his steps westward in unwonted good humour.", "세인트폴 성당을 지날 때 그는 문간 옆으로 비켜서서 시계를 맞추려 했어."], ["As he passed St Paul’s he stepped aside into a doorway to set his watch, and with his hand on the key and his eye on the cathedral dial, was intent upon so doing, when a man suddenly stopped before him.", "손은 태엽에 얹고 눈은 성당 시계판에 둔 채로 그 일에 열중하고 있는데, 갑자기 한 남자가 그의 앞을 가로막았지."], ["It was Newman Noggs.", "뉴먼 노그스였어."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Newman,’ said Mr.", "뉴먼.\""], ["Nickleby, looking up as he pursued his occupation.", "니클비 씨가 하던 일을 계속하며 고개를 들고 말했어."], ["‘The letter about the mortgage has come, has it?", "\"저당 잡힌 일로 편지가 온 게로군, 그렇지?"], ["I thought it would.’", "그럴 줄 알았어.\""]], [["‘Wrong,’ replied Newman.", "\"틀렸어요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어."]], [["‘What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["and nobody called respecting it?’ inquired Mr.", "그 일로 찾아온 사람도 없고?\""], ["Nickleby, pausing.", "니클비 씨가 손을 멈추고 물었어."], ["Noggs shook his head.", "노그스가 고개를 저었어."]], [["‘What HAS come, then?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그럼 대체 뭐가 왔다는 거야?\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 씨가 물었어."]], [["‘I have,’ said Newman.", "\"제가 왔지요.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."]], [["‘What else?’ demanded the master, sternly.", "\"그밖에는?\" 주인이 험악하게 다그쳤어."]], [["‘This,’ said Newman, drawing a sealed letter slowly from his pocket.", "\"이거요.\" 뉴먼이 봉인된 편지 한 통을 주머니에서 천천히 꺼내며 말했어."], ["‘Post-mark, Strand, black wax, black border, woman’s hand, C. N.", "\"소인은 스트랜드, 검은 봉랍, 검은 테두리, 여자 글씨, 한쪽 구석에 C."], ["in the corner.’", "N.\""]], [["‘Black wax?’ said Mr.", "\"검은 봉랍이라고?\""], ["Nickleby, glancing at the letter.", "니클비 씨가 편지를 흘끗 보며 말했어."], ["‘I know something of that hand, too.", "\"이 글씨도 어쩐지 낯이 익은데."], ["Newman, I shouldn’t be surprised if my brother were dead.’", "뉴먼, 내 동생이 죽었다 해도 나는 별로 놀라지 않을 것 같군.\""]], [["‘I don’t think you would,’ said Newman, quietly.", "\"그러실 것 같지 않네요.\" 뉴먼이 조용히 말했어."]], [["‘Why not, sir?’ demanded Mr.", "\"왜 안 그렇다는 거야?\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 씨가 따져 물었어."]], [["‘You never are surprised,’ replied Newman, ‘that’s all.’", "\"주인님은 결코 놀라는 법이 없으니까요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어. \"그것뿐입니다.\""]], [["Mr.", "니클비 씨는 조수에게서 편지를 낚아챘어."], ["Nickleby snatched the letter from his assistant, and fixing a cold look upon him, opened, read it, put it in his pocket, and having now hit the time to a second, began winding up his watch.", "그러고는 그에게 차가운 눈길을 한 번 박아 넣고는, 봉투를 뜯어 읽고 주머니에 집어넣었지. 이제 시간을 일 초까지 정확히 맞춘 다음, 시계태엽을 감기 시작했어."]], [["‘It is as I expected, Newman,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 예상한 대로야, 뉴먼.\""], ["Nickleby, while he was thus engaged.", "니클비 씨가 그 일을 하면서 말했어."], ["‘He IS dead.", "\"동생이 죽었어."], ["Dear me!", "저런!"], ["Well, that’s sudden thing.", "그것참, 갑작스러운 일이군. 정말이지, 생각도 못 했는데.\""], ["I shouldn’t have thought it, really.’ With these touching expressions of sorrow, Mr Nickleby replaced his watch in his fob, and, fitting on his gloves to a nicety, turned upon his way, and walked slowly westward with his hands behind him.", "이렇게 가슴 아픈 슬픔의 말을 늘어놓고는, 니클비 씨는 시계를 다시 회중 주머니에 넣었어. 그리고 장갑을 한 치 어긋남 없이 끼고는 가던 길로 몸을 돌려, 두 손을 등 뒤로 한 채 천천히 서쪽으로 걸어갔지."]], [["‘Children alive?’ inquired Noggs, stepping up to him.", "\"애들은 살아 있고요?\" 노그스가 그에게 다가서며 물었어."]], [["‘Why, that’s the very thing,’ replied Mr.", "\"이런, 바로 그게 문제야.\""], ["Nickleby, as though his thoughts were about them at that moment.", "마치 그 순간 그 애들 생각을 하고 있었다는 듯이 니클비 씨가 대답했어."], ["‘They are both alive.’", "\"둘 다 살아 있어.\""]], [["‘Both!’ repeated Newman Noggs, in a low voice.", "\"둘 다요!\" 뉴먼 노그스가 낮은 목소리로 되뇌었어."]], [["‘And the widow, too,’ added Mr.", "\"게다가 과부까지.\" 니클비 씨가 덧붙였어."], ["Nickleby, ‘and all three in London, confound them; all three here, Newman.’", "\"셋 다 런던에 있어, 빌어먹을. 셋 다 여기 있다고, 뉴먼.\""]], [["Newman fell a little behind his master, and his face was curiously twisted as by a spasm; but whether of paralysis, or grief, or inward laughter, nobody but himself could possibly explain. The expression of a man’s face is commonly a help to his thoughts, or glossary on his speech; but the countenance of Newman Noggs, in his ordinary moods, was a problem which no stretch of ingenuity could solve.", "뉴먼은 주인보다 조금 뒤로 처졌어. 그의 얼굴이 경련이라도 인 것처럼 묘하게 일그러졌지. 하지만 그게 마비 때문인지, 슬픔 때문인지, 아니면 속으로 터지는 웃음 때문인지는 그 자신 말고는 아무도 알 수 없었어. 사람의 얼굴 표정은 보통 그 속마음을 짐작하게 해 주거나 말뜻을 풀어 주는 실마리가 되잖아. 그런데 뉴먼 노그스의 평소 얼굴은 아무리 머리를 굴려도 풀 수 없는 수수께끼였어."]], [["‘Go home!’ said Mr.", "\"집에 가!\""], ["Nickleby, after they had walked a few paces: looking round at the clerk as if he were his dog.", "몇 걸음 걷고 나서 니클비 씨가 말했어. 마치 제 개를 보듯 조수를 돌아보면서 말이야."], ["The words were scarcely uttered when Newman darted across the road, slunk among the crowd, and disappeared in an instant.", "이 말이 떨어지기가 무섭게 뉴먼은 길을 가로질러 쏜살같이 달려가, 인파 속으로 슬그머니 끼어들더니 순식간에 사라져 버렸어."]], [["‘Reasonable, certainly!’ muttered Mr.", "\"하긴, 당연한 일이지!\""], ["Nickleby to himself, as he walked on, ‘very reasonable!", "니클비 씨가 걸어가며 혼자 중얼거렸어. \"지극히 당연한 일이고말고!"], ["My brother never did anything for me, and I never expected it; the breath is no sooner out of his body than I am to be looked to, as the support of a great hearty woman, and a grown boy and girl.", "동생은 나한테 해 준 게 하나도 없었고, 나도 바라지 않았어. 그런데 숨이 끊어지기가 무섭게, 떡 벌어진 건강한 여편네 하나에 다 큰 아들놈에 딸년까지 떠받쳐 줄 사람으로 나를 쳐다본다 이거지."], ["What are they to me!", "그것들이 나하고 무슨 상관이람!"], ["I never saw them.’", "얼굴 한번 본 적도 없는데.\""]], [["Full of these, and many other reflections of a similar kind, Mr.", "이런 생각과 그밖에 비슷한 여러 생각을 가득 품은 채, 니클비 씨는 부지런히 스트랜드로 향했어."], ["Nickleby made the best of his way to the Strand, and, referring to his letter as if to ascertain the number of the house he wanted, stopped at a private door about half-way down that crowded thoroughfare.", "그리고 찾는 집 번지수를 확인하려는 듯 편지를 들여다보며, 그 붐비는 큰길을 절반쯤 내려간 곳에 있는 어느 집 개인 출입문 앞에 멈춰 섰지."]], [["A miniature painter lived there, for there was a large gilt frame screwed upon the street-door, in which were displayed, upon a black velvet ground, two portraits of naval dress coats with faces looking out of them, and telescopes attached; one of a young gentleman in a very vermilion uniform, flourishing a sabre; and one of a literary character with a high forehead, a pen and ink, six books, and a curtain. There was, moreover, a touching representation of a young lady reading a manuscript in an unfathomable forest, and a charming whole length of a large-headed little boy, sitting on a stool with his legs fore-shortened to the size of salt-spoons. Besides these works of art, there were a great many heads of old ladies and gentlemen smirking at each other out of blue and brown skies, and an elegantly written card of terms with an embossed border.", "거기엔 세밀화 화가가 살았어. 길거리 쪽 문에 커다란 금박 액자가 나사로 박혀 있었거든. 그 안에는 검은 벨벳 바탕 위에 해군 정복 두 벌이 초상화로 걸려 있었어. 정복 위로 얼굴들이 빼꼼 내다보고 있었고, 망원경도 딸려 있었지. 하나는 새빨간 군복을 입은 젊은 신사가 군도를 휘두르는 그림이고, 또 하나는 이마가 훤한 문인 하나가 펜과 잉크, 책 여섯 권, 그리고 커튼과 함께 그려진 그림이었어. 그밖에도 가슴 뭉클한 그림이 하나 있었어. 끝 모를 깊은 숲속에서 원고를 읽고 있는 젊은 아가씨 그림이었지. 또 머리가 큰 작은 사내아이가 의자에 앉아 있는 매력적인 전신상도 있었어. 두 다리는 단축법으로 그려져 소금 숟가락만 한 크기로 줄어들어 있었지. 이런 예술 작품들 말고도, 파란 하늘과 갈색 하늘을 배경으로 서로를 향해 히죽거리는 노부인과 노신사의 얼굴 그림이 잔뜩 있었어. 그리고 양각 테두리를 두르고 우아한 글씨로 적은 요금표도 한 장 걸려 있었지."]], [["Mr.", "니클비 씨는 이 시시한 것들을 잔뜩 깔보는 눈으로 흘끗 보고는, 노크를 두 번 했어."], ["Nickleby glanced at these frivolities with great contempt, and gave a double knock, which, having been thrice repeated, was answered by a servant girl with an uncommonly dirty face.", "그걸 세 번이나 되풀이하고 나서야, 유난히 더러운 얼굴을 한 하녀가 문을 열어 주었지."]], [["‘Is Mrs.", "\"니클비 부인 집에 계시냐, 얘?\""], ["Nickleby at home, girl?’ demanded Ralph sharply.", "랄프가 날카롭게 물었어."]], [["‘Her name ain’t Nickleby,’ said the girl, ‘La Creevy, you mean.’", "\"여긴 니클비란 이름 안 살아요.\" 하녀가 말했어. \"라 크리비 말씀이시겠죠.\""]], [["Mr.", "니클비 씨는 이렇게 정정을 당하자 하녀에게 몹시 못마땅한 눈길을 보냈어."], ["Nickleby looked very indignant at the handmaid on being thus corrected, and demanded with much asperity what she meant; which she was about to state, when a female voice proceeding from a perpendicular staircase at the end of the passage, inquired who was wanted.", "그러고는 그게 무슨 소리냐고 잔뜩 가시 돋친 말투로 따졌지. 하녀가 막 설명을 하려는데, 복도 끝의 가파른 계단 쪽에서 여자 목소리가 들려와 누구를 찾느냐고 물었어."]], [["‘Mrs.", "\"니클비 부인.\""], ["Nickleby,’ said Ralph.", "랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘It’s the second floor, Hannah,’ said the same voice; ‘what a stupid thing you are!", "\"그 사람은 삼층이야, 해나.\" 같은 목소리가 말했어."], ["Is the second floor at home?’", "\"넌 어쩜 그렇게 멍청하니! 삼층에 사람 있니?\""]], [["‘Somebody went out just now, but I think it was the attic which had been a cleaning of himself,’ replied the girl.", "\"방금 누가 나가긴 했는데, 그건 위층 다락방 사람이 세수를 하고 나간 것 같은데요.\" 하녀가 대답했어."]], [["‘You had better see,’ said the invisible female. ‘Show the gentleman where the bell is, and tell him he mustn’t knock double knocks for the second floor; I can’t allow a knock except when the bell’s broke, and then it must be two single ones.’", "\"가서 살펴보는 게 좋겠다.\" 모습이 보이지 않는 여자가 말했어. \"이 신사분께 종이 어디 있는지 알려 드리고, 삼층 손님은 두 번 노크하면 안 된다고 말씀드려라. 나는 종이 고장 났을 때 말고는 노크를 허락하지 않아. 그때도 한 번씩 두 번만 쳐야 하고.\""]], [["‘Here,’ said Ralph, walking in without more parley, ‘I beg your pardon; is that Mrs.", "\"여보세요.\" 랄프가 더 이상 잔말 없이 안으로 걸어 들어가며 말했어."], ["La what’s-her-name?’", "\"실례합니다만, 라 뭐라고 하셨던 그분이십니까?\""]], [["‘Creevy--La Creevy,’ replied the voice, as a yellow headdress bobbed over the banisters.", "\"크리비, 라 크리비예요.\" 노란 머리 장식이 난간 위로 까딱 모습을 드러내며 목소리가 대답했어."]], [["‘I’ll speak to you a moment, ma’am, with your leave,’ said Ralph.", "\"괜찮으시다면 잠깐 말씀 좀 드리고 싶은데요, 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["The voice replied that the gentleman was to walk up; but he had walked up before it spoke, and stepping into the first floor, was received by the wearer of the yellow head-dress, who had a gown to correspond, and was of much the same colour herself. Miss La Creevy was a mincing young lady of fifty, and Miss La Creevy’s apartment was the gilt frame downstairs on a larger scale and something dirtier.", "목소리는 신사분더러 위로 올라오시라고 대답했어. 하지만 그가 말이 끝나기도 전에 이미 올라가 있었지. 그가 이 층으로 들어서자, 노란 머리 장식을 한 여자가 그를 맞았어. 머리 장식에 어울리는 가운을 입었고, 그 여자 자신도 거의 같은 색이었지. 라 크리비 양은 쉰 살 먹은, 점잔을 빼는 아가씨였어. 그리고 라 크리비 양의 방은 아래층 그 금박 액자를 더 큼직하게, 그리고 좀 더 지저분하게 키워 놓은 것 같았지."]], [["‘Hem!’ said Miss La Creevy, coughing delicately behind her black silk mitten.", "\"에헴!\" 라 크리비 양이 검은 비단 손싸개 뒤로 우아하게 기침을 하며 말했어."], ["‘A miniature, I presume.", "\"세밀화를 그리러 오셨나 보군요."], ["A very strongly-marked countenance for the purpose, sir.", "그 용도로는 아주 인상이 또렷한 얼굴이세요, 선생님."], ["Have you ever sat before?’", "전에 앉아 보신 적 있나요?\""]], [["‘You mistake my purpose, I see, ma’am,’ replied Mr.", "\"제 용건을 잘못 짚으신 모양이군요, 부인.\""], ["Nickleby, in his usual blunt fashion.", "니클비 씨가 평소의 무뚝뚝한 투로 대답했어."], ["‘I have no money to throw away on miniatures, ma’am, and nobody to give one to (thank God) if I had.", "\"저는 세밀화 따위에 내다 버릴 돈도 없고, 설령 있다 한들 (고맙게도) 그걸 줄 사람도 없습니다."], ["Seeing you on the stairs, I wanted to ask a question of you, about some lodgers here.’", "계단에서 부인을 뵙고, 여기 사는 어떤 세입자에 관해 한 가지 여쭤보고 싶었던 겁니다.\""]], [["Miss La Creevy coughed once more--this cough was to conceal her disappointment--and said, ‘Oh, indeed!’", "라 크리비 양은 다시 한 번 기침을 했어. 이 기침은 실망감을 감추려는 것이었지. 그러고는 \"아, 그러세요!\" 하고 말했어."]], [["‘I infer from what you said to your servant, that the floor above belongs to you, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "\"하녀에게 하시는 말씀을 들으니, 위층은 부인 거더군요.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 씨가 말했어."]], [["Yes it did, Miss La Creevy replied.", "그래요, 맞아요, 라고 라 크리비 양이 대답했어."], ["The upper part of the house belonged to her, and as she had no necessity for the second-floor rooms just then, she was in the habit of letting them.", "이 집 위층은 다 자기 거고, 마침 지금은 삼층 방이 필요 없어서 세를 놓는 습관이 있다고."], ["Indeed, there was a lady from the country and her two children in them, at that present speaking.", "사실 지금 거기엔 시골에서 올라온 부인 하나와 두 아이가 살고 있다고 했어."]], [["‘A widow, ma’am?’ said Ralph.", "\"과부입니까, 부인?\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Yes, she is a widow,’ replied the lady.", "\"네, 과부예요.\" 그 여자가 대답했어."]], [["‘A POOR widow, ma’am,’ said Ralph, with a powerful emphasis on that little adjective which conveys so much.", "\"가난한 과부 말이지요, 부인?\" 랄프가 그 짧은 형용사에 힘껏 무게를 실어 말했어. 그 한마디에 어찌나 많은 뜻이 담기던지."]], [["‘Well, I’m afraid she IS poor,’ rejoined Miss La Creevy.", "\"글쎄요, 가난하긴 한 것 같아요.\" 라 크리비 양이 받아 말했어."]], [["‘I happen to know that she is, ma’am,’ said Ralph.", "\"제가 가난하다는 걸 잘 알고 있습니다, 부인.\""], ["‘Now, what business has a poor widow in such a house as this, ma’am?’", "랄프가 말했어. \"그런데, 가난한 과부가 이런 집에 무슨 볼일이 있겠습니까, 부인?\""]], [["‘Very true,’ replied Miss La Creevy, not at all displeased with this implied compliment to the apartments.", "\"옳은 말씀이에요.\" 자기 방을 은근히 칭찬하는 말로 들렸는지, 라 크리비 양이 조금도 기분 나쁘지 않은 듯 대답했어."], ["‘Exceedingly true.’", "\"지당하신 말씀이고말고요.\""]], [["‘I know her circumstances intimately, ma’am,’ said Ralph; ‘in fact, I am a relation of the family; and I should recommend you not to keep them here, ma’am.’", "\"저는 그 댁 형편을 속속들이 알고 있습니다, 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"실은 제가 그 집안의 친척이거든요. 그러니 부인, 그들을 여기 두지 않으시는 게 좋을 겁니다.\""]], [["‘I should hope, if there was any incompatibility to meet the pecuniary obligations,’ said Miss La Creevy with another cough, ‘that the lady’s family would--’", "\"혹시라도 돈 문제를 감당하지 못할 사정이 생긴다면,\" 라 크리비 양이 또 한 번 기침을 하며 말했어. \"그 부인 친척 되시는 분들이—\""]], [["‘No they wouldn’t, ma’am,’ interrupted Ralph, hastily.", "\"아니요, 안 그럴 겁니다, 부인.\" 랄프가 황급히 끼어들었어."], ["‘Don’t think it.’", "\"그런 기대는 마세요.\""]], [["‘If I am to understand that,’ said Miss La Creevy, ‘the case wears a very different appearance.’", "\"그렇다면,\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"사정이 아주 달라지네요.\""]], [["‘You may understand it then, ma’am,’ said Ralph, ‘and make your arrangements accordingly. I am the family, ma’am--at least, I believe I am the only relation they have, and I think it right that you should know I can’t support them in their extravagances. How long have they taken these lodgings for?’", "\"그렇게 알아 두시면 됩니다, 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그리고 거기에 맞춰 준비하시는 거지요. 제가 그 집안입니다, 부인. 적어도 제가 그들의 유일한 친척인 것 같으니까요. 그러니 분명히 말씀드려야겠는데, 저는 그들의 사치를 떠받쳐 줄 생각이 없습니다. 그들이 이 방을 얼마 동안 빌리기로 했습니까?\""]], [["‘Only from week to week,’ replied Miss La Creevy.", "\"그저 일주일 단위로요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대답했어."], ["‘Mrs. Nickleby paid the first week in advance.’", "\"니클비 부인이 첫 주 방세는 미리 냈고요.\""]], [["‘Then you had better get them out at the end of it,’ said Ralph.", "\"그럼 그 주가 끝나면 내보내시는 게 좋겠습니다.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘They can’t do better than go back to the country, ma’am; they are in everybody’s way here.’", "\"그들은 시골로 돌아가는 게 상책이에요, 부인. 여기서는 누구한테나 거치적거리는 짐일 뿐이니까요.\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ said Miss La Creevy, rubbing her hands, ‘if Mrs.", "\"물론이지요.\" 라 크리비 양이 두 손을 비비며 말했어."], ["Nickleby took the apartments without the means of paying for them, it was very unbecoming a lady.’", "\"니클비 부인이 방세 낼 형편도 못 되면서 방을 얻었다면, 그건 숙녀로서 아주 꼴사나운 짓이고말고요.\""]], [["‘Of course it was, ma’am,’ said Ralph.", "\"당연히 그렇지요, 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘And naturally,’ continued Miss La Creevy, ‘I who am, AT PRESENT--hem--an unprotected female, cannot afford to lose by the apartments.’", "\"그리고 당연한 일이지만,\" 라 크리비 양이 말을 이었어. \"지금, 에헴, 의지할 데 없는 여자인 제가 이 방으로 손해를 볼 수는 없는 노릇이니까요.\""]], [["‘Of course you can’t, ma’am,’ replied Ralph.", "\"물론 그러실 수 없지요, 부인.\" 랄프가 대답했어."]], [["‘Though at the same time,’ added Miss La Creevy, who was plainly wavering between her good-nature and her interest, ‘I have nothing whatever to say against the lady, who is extremely pleasant and affable, though, poor thing, she seems terribly low in her spirits; nor against the young people either, for nicer, or better-behaved young people cannot be.’", "\"그렇다고 해도,\" 자기 착한 심성과 잇속 사이에서 갈팡질팡하는 게 빤히 보이는 라 크리비 양이 덧붙였어. \"그 부인을 두고 나쁘게 할 말은 하나도 없어요. 무척 상냥하고 싹싹한 분인데, 가엾게도 기운이 영 없어 보이긴 하더라고요. 그 댁 젊은이들도 마찬가지예요. 그보다 더 착하고 예의 바른 젊은이들은 없을 거예요.\""]], [["‘Very well, ma’am,’ said Ralph, turning to the door, for these encomiums on poverty irritated him; ‘I have done my duty, and perhaps more than I ought: of course nobody will thank me for saying what I have.’", "\"잘 알겠습니다, 부인.\" 가난을 이렇게 추켜세우는 소리가 신경에 거슬려, 랄프가 문 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했어. \"저는 제 도리를 다했고, 어쩌면 도리 이상을 한 셈이지요. 물론 제가 이런 말을 했다고 고마워할 사람은 아무도 없겠지만요.\""]], [["‘I am sure I am very much obliged to you at least, sir,’ said Miss La Creevy in a gracious manner.", "\"적어도 저는 정말 감사하게 생각해요, 선생님.\" 라 크리비 양이 상냥하게 말했어."], ["‘Would you do me the favour to look at a few specimens of my portrait painting?’", "\"제 초상화 작품 몇 점을 봐 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주시겠어요?\""]], [["‘You’re very good, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "\"고맙습니다만, 부인.\" 니클비 씨가 부리나케 자리를 뜨며 말했어."], ["Nickleby, making off with great speed; ‘but as I have a visit to pay upstairs, and my time is precious, I really can’t.’", "\"위층에 들를 데가 있고, 제 시간이 귀해서요. 정말이지 안 되겠습니다.\""]], [["‘At any other time when you are passing, I shall be most happy,’ said Miss La Creevy.", "\"다음에 지나시는 길에 언제든, 저는 더없이 기쁠 거예요.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어."], ["‘Perhaps you will have the kindness to take a card of terms with you?", "\"요금표 한 장 가져가시는 친절을 베풀어 주시겠어요? 고맙습니다."], ["Thank you--good-morning!’", "안녕히 가세요!\""]], [["‘Good-morning, ma’am,’ said Ralph, shutting the door abruptly after him to prevent any further conversation.", "\"안녕히 계세요, 부인.\" 랄프가 더 이상의 대화를 막으려고 등 뒤로 문을 휙 닫으며 말했어."], ["‘Now for my sister-in-law.", "\"이제 제수씨 차례로군."], ["Bah!’", "흥!\""]], [["Climbing up another perpendicular flight, composed with great mechanical ingenuity of nothing but corner stairs, Mr. Ralph Nickleby stopped to take breath on the landing, when he was overtaken by the handmaid, whom the politeness of Miss La Creevy had dispatched to announce him, and who had apparently been making a variety of unsuccessful attempts, since their last interview, to wipe her dirty face clean, upon an apron much dirtier.", "또 다른 가파른 계단을 올라갔어. 기계처럼 교묘하게 짜 맞춰져 죄다 모퉁이 계단뿐인 그런 층계였지. 랄프 니클비 씨는 층계참에서 숨을 돌리려고 멈춰 섰어. 그때 하녀가 그를 따라잡았지. 라 크리비 양이 친절을 베풀어 그가 왔다고 알리라며 보낸 거였어. 하녀는 아까 만난 뒤로 더러운 얼굴을 닦으려고 여러 번 애쓴 모양이었지만, 다 헛수고였지. 얼굴보다 훨씬 더 더러운 앞치마에 닦았으니 말이야."]], [["‘What name?’ said the girl.", "\"성함이 어떻게 되세요?\" 하녀가 물었어."]], [["‘Nickleby,’ replied Ralph.", "\"니클비.\" 랄프가 대답했어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Mrs.", "니클비 부인.\""], ["Nickleby,’ said the girl, throwing open the door, ‘here’s Mr Nickleby.’", "하녀가 문을 활짝 열어젖히며 말했어. \"여기 니클비 씨 오셨어요.\""]], [["A lady in deep mourning rose as Mr.", "랄프 니클비 씨가 들어서자, 짙은 상복을 입은 부인이 일어섰어."], ["Ralph Nickleby entered, but appeared incapable of advancing to meet him, and leant upon the arm of a slight but very beautiful girl of about seventeen, who had been sitting by her.", "하지만 그를 맞으러 나설 기력은 없는 것 같았어. 그래서 곁에 앉아 있던 가냘프지만 무척 아름다운, 열일곱 살쯤 된 아가씨의 팔에 기댔지."], ["A youth, who appeared a year or two older, stepped forward and saluted Ralph as his uncle.", "한두 살쯤 위로 보이는 청년 하나가 앞으로 나서더니, 랄프에게 삼촌이라며 인사를 했어."]], [["‘Oh,’ growled Ralph, with an ill-favoured frown, ‘you are Nicholas, I suppose?’", "\"오,\" 랄프가 험상궂게 인상을 쓰며 으르렁거렸어. \"네가 니콜라스인 게로구나?\""]], [["‘That is my name, sir,’ replied the youth.", "\"제 이름이 맞습니다.\" 청년이 대답했어."]], [["‘Put my hat down,’ said Ralph, imperiously.", "\"내 모자 좀 받아 둬라.\" 랄프가 거만하게 말했어."], ["‘Well, ma’am, how do you do?", "\"자, 제수씨, 어떻게 지내시오?"], ["You must bear up against sorrow, ma’am; I always do.’", "슬픔은 꿋꿋이 견뎌야 합니다, 제수씨. 나는 늘 그렇게 하지요.\""]], [["‘Mine was no common loss!’ said Mrs.", "\"제 슬픔은 흔한 슬픔이 아니에요!\""], ["Nickleby, applying her handkerchief to her eyes.", "니클비 부인이 손수건을 눈에 갖다 대며 말했어."]], [["‘It was no UNcommon loss, ma’am,’ returned Ralph, as he coolly unbuttoned his spencer.", "\"흔치 않은 슬픔도 아니지요, 제수씨.\" 랄프가 스펜서 외투의 단추를 차갑게 풀며 대꾸했어."], ["‘Husbands die every day, ma’am, and wives too.’", "\"남편은 날마다 죽고, 아내도 날마다 죽으니까요, 제수씨.\""]], [["‘And brothers also, sir,’ said Nicholas, with a glance of indignation.", "\"형제도 마찬가지지요.\" 니콜라스가 분개한 눈길을 던지며 말했어."]], [["‘Yes, sir, and puppies, and pug-dogs likewise,’ replied his uncle, taking a chair.", "\"그렇지, 강아지나 발바리도 날마다 죽고말고.\" 삼촌이 의자에 앉으며 대꾸했어."], ["‘You didn’t mention in your letter what my brother’s complaint was, ma’am.’", "\"제수씨 편지엔 내 동생이 무슨 병으로 갔는지 적혀 있지 않더군요.\""]], [["‘The doctors could attribute it to no particular disease,’ said Mrs Nickleby; shedding tears.", "\"의사들도 딱히 무슨 병이라고 짚어 내질 못했어요.\" 니클비 부인이 눈물을 흘리며 말했어."], ["‘We have too much reason to fear that he died of a broken heart.’", "\"상심해서 가슴이 무너져 돌아가신 거라고 볼 수밖에 없을 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘Pooh!’ said Ralph, ‘there’s no such thing. I can understand a man’s dying of a broken neck, or suffering from a broken arm, or a broken head, or a broken leg, or a broken nose; but a broken heart!--nonsense, it’s the cant of the day. If a man can’t pay his debts, he dies of a broken heart, and his widow’s a martyr.’", "\"쳇!\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그런 게 어디 있소. 목이 부러져 죽는다거나, 팔이 부러진다거나, 머리가 깨진다거나, 다리가 부러진다거나, 코가 부러진다거나 하는 건 이해가 가지요. 그런데 가슴이 무너져 죽는다니! 말도 안 되는 소리, 그건 요즘 유행하는 헛소리에 지나지 않아요. 빚을 못 갚으면 가슴이 무너져 죽었다고 하고, 그 과부는 순교자가 되는 거지요.\""]], [["‘Some people, I believe, have no hearts to break,’ observed Nicholas, quietly.", "\"제 생각엔, 무너질 가슴이 없는 사람도 있는 것 같은데요.\" 니콜라스가 조용히 한마디 했어."]], [["‘How old is this boy, for God’s sake?’ inquired Ralph, wheeling back his chair, and surveying his nephew from head to foot with intense scorn.", "\"이 녀석은 도대체 몇 살이오?\" 랄프가 의자를 뒤로 홱 돌리고는, 조카를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 잔뜩 깔보는 눈으로 훑어보며 물었어."]], [["‘Nicholas is very nearly nineteen,’ replied the widow.", "\"니콜라스는 곧 열아홉이 돼요.\" 과부가 대답했어."]], [["‘Nineteen, eh!’ said Ralph; ‘and what do you mean to do for your bread, sir?’", "\"열아홉이라, 흠!\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그래, 넌 밥벌이로 뭘 할 작정이냐?\""]], [["‘Not to live upon my mother,’ replied Nicholas, his heart swelling as he spoke.", "\"어머니께 얹혀살지는 않을 겁니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. 말하는 동안 가슴이 부풀어 올랐지."]], [["‘You’d have little enough to live upon, if you did,’ retorted the uncle, eyeing him contemptuously.", "\"네 어미한테 얹혀산댔자, 빌어먹을 게 별로 없을 텐데.\" 삼촌이 그를 깔보는 눈으로 보며 쏘아붙였어."]], [["‘Whatever it be,’ said Nicholas, flushed with anger, ‘I shall not look to you to make it more.’", "\"그게 뭐든,\" 니콜라스가 화가 나서 얼굴이 벌게져서 말했어. \"그걸 더 보태 달라고 삼촌을 쳐다보지는 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Nicholas, my dear, recollect yourself,’ remonstrated Mrs.", "\"니콜라스, 얘야, 정신 차리렴.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 타일렀어."]], [["‘Dear Nicholas, pray,’ urged the young lady.", "\"니콜라스 오빠, 제발.\" 아가씨도 다급히 말렸어."]], [["‘Hold your tongue, sir,’ said Ralph.", "\"입 다물어, 이 녀석아.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘Upon my word!", "\"원, 별일이군!"], ["Fine beginnings, Mrs Nickleby--fine beginnings!’", "시작이 아주 그럴듯하구려, 제수씨, 시작이 아주 그럴듯해!\""]], [["Mrs. Nickleby made no other reply than entreating Nicholas by a gesture to keep silent; and the uncle and nephew looked at each other for some seconds without speaking.", "니클비 부인은 다른 대꾸 없이, 그저 니콜라스에게 조용히 하라고 손짓으로 애원할 뿐이었어."], ["The face of the old man was stern, hard-featured, and forbidding; that of the young one, open, handsome, and ingenuous.", "그리고 삼촌과 조카는 몇 초 동안 말없이 서로를 마주 보았지. 노인의 얼굴은 험악하고 모질고 사나웠어."], ["The old man’s eye was keen with the twinklings of avarice and cunning; the young man’s bright with the light of intelligence and spirit.", "청년의 얼굴은 환하고 잘생기고 꾸밈없었지. 노인의 눈은 탐욕과 교활함으로 번득이며 날카로웠고, 청년의 눈은 지성과 패기의 빛으로 빛났어."], ["His figure was somewhat slight, but manly and well formed; and, apart from all the grace of youth and comeliness, there was an emanation from the warm young heart in his look and bearing which kept the old man down.", "그의 몸집은 다소 가냘팠지만 사내답고 균형이 잡혀 있었어. 그리고 젊음과 준수함이 주는 온갖 멋스러움은 차치하더라도, 그의 눈빛과 몸가짐에는 따뜻한 젊은 가슴에서 우러나는 무언가가 있어서, 그게 노인을 주눅 들게 했지."]], [["However striking such a contrast as this may be to lookers-on, none ever feel it with half the keenness or acuteness of perfection with which it strikes to the very soul of him whose inferiority it marks.", "이런 대비가 구경하는 사람들 눈엔 아무리 두드러지게 보인다 해도, 그걸 가장 날카롭고 뼈저리게 느끼는 건 바로 그 대비로 자기 못남이 드러나는 사람 자신이야. 그 누구도 그만큼 절절하게 느끼지는 못하지."], ["It galled Ralph to the heart’s core, and he hated Nicholas from that hour.", "그건 랄프의 가슴속 깊은 곳을 쓰라리게 후벼 팠어. 그리고 그는 그 순간부터 니콜라스를 미워했지."]], [["The mutual inspection was at length brought to a close by Ralph withdrawing his eyes, with a great show of disdain, and calling Nicholas ‘a boy.’ This word is much used as a term of reproach by elderly gentlemen towards their juniors: probably with the view of deluding society into the belief that if they could be young again, they wouldn’t on any account.", "이 서로의 살핌은 마침내 끝이 났어. 랄프가 잔뜩 경멸하는 티를 내며 눈길을 거두고는, 니콜라스를 \"애송이\"라고 불렀거든. 이 말은 나이 든 신사들이 손아랫사람을 나무랄 때 곧잘 쓰는 말이지. 아마도 자기들이 다시 젊어질 수 있다 해도 무슨 일이 있어도 그러지 않겠다고 세상 사람들을 속여 믿게 하려는 속셈이겠지."]], [["‘Well, ma’am,’ said Ralph, impatiently, ‘the creditors have administered, you tell me, and there’s nothing left for you?’", "\"자, 제수씨.\" 랄프가 조바심을 내며 말했어. \"채권자들이 재산을 다 처분해서, 제수씨한테 남은 게 하나도 없다는 말이지요?\""]], [["‘Nothing,’ replied Mrs.", "\"하나도 없어요.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 대답했어."]], [["‘And you spent what little money you had, in coming all the way to London, to see what I could do for you?’ pursued Ralph.", "\"그런데 그 얼마 안 되는 돈을 다 써 가며, 내가 무엇을 해 줄 수 있나 보려고 그 먼 런던까지 올라왔다는 거고요?\" 랄프가 캐물었어."]], [["‘I hoped,’ faltered Mrs.", "\"바라기로는,\""], ["Nickleby, ‘that you might have an opportunity of doing something for your brother’s children.", "니클비 부인이 머뭇거리며 말했어. \"도련님이 형의 아이들을 위해 뭔가 해 주실 기회가 있지 않을까 했어요."], ["It was his dying wish that I should appeal to you in their behalf.’", "도련님께 아이들을 위해 호소해 달라는 게 그이의 마지막 소원이었거든요.\""]], [["‘I don’t know how it is,’ muttered Ralph, walking up and down the room, ‘but whenever a man dies without any property of his own, he always seems to think he has a right to dispose of other people’s.", "\"어찌 된 노릇인지 모르겠군.\" 랄프가 방 안을 왔다 갔다 하며 중얼거렸어."], ["What is your daughter fit for, ma’am?’", "\"제 재산이라곤 한 푼도 없이 죽는 사람은, 어째 꼭 남의 재산을 마음대로 처분할 권리가 있다고 여긴단 말이야. 제수씨, 따님은 무슨 일을 할 수 있소?\""]], [["‘Kate has been well educated,’ sobbed Mrs.", "\"케이트는 교육을 잘 받았어요.\" 니클비 부인이 흐느꼈어."], ["Nickleby. ‘Tell your uncle, my dear, how far you went in French and extras.’", "\"삼촌께 말씀드리렴, 얘야, 프랑스어랑 다른 과목들을 어디까지 배웠는지.\""]], [["The poor girl was about to murmur something, when her uncle stopped her, very unceremoniously.", "가엾은 아가씨가 막 무어라 우물거리려는데, 삼촌이 아주 무례하게 말을 끊었어."]], [["‘We must try and get you apprenticed at some boarding-school,’ said Ralph.", "\"어디 기숙학교에 견습으로 들여보내 봐야겠군.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘You have not been brought up too delicately for that, I hope?’", "\"설마 그것도 못 할 만큼 곱게만 자라진 않았겠지?\""]], [["‘No, indeed, uncle,’ replied the weeping girl.", "\"네, 정말 안 그래요, 삼촌.\" 우는 아가씨가 대답했어."], ["‘I will try to do anything that will gain me a home and bread.’", "\"집과 밥을 얻을 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 해 보겠어요.\""]], [["‘Well, well,’ said Ralph, a little softened, either by his niece’s beauty or her distress (stretch a point, and say the latter). ‘You must try it, and if the life is too hard, perhaps dressmaking or tambour-work will come lighter. Have YOU ever done anything, sir?’ (turning to his nephew.)", "\"그래, 그래.\" 랄프가 조금 누그러져서 말했어. 조카딸의 아름다움 때문인지, 그 애처로운 모습 때문인지는 모르지만 (좋게 봐서, 후자라고 해 두자). \"한번 해 보거라. 그 일이 너무 고되면, 어쩌면 바느질이나 자수 일이 좀 더 수월할지도 모르지. 너는 무슨 일이든 해 본 적 있느냐?\" (조카를 돌아보며 물었어.)"]], [["‘No,’ replied Nicholas, bluntly.", "\"없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 퉁명스럽게 대답했어."]], [["‘No, I thought not!’ said Ralph.", "\"그래, 없을 줄 알았다!\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘This is the way my brother brought up his children, ma’am.’", "\"이게 내 동생이 제 자식들을 길러 온 꼴이지요, 제수씨.\""]], [["‘Nicholas has not long completed such education as his poor father could give him,’ rejoined Mrs.", "\"니콜라스는 가엾은 제 아버지가 시켜 줄 수 있는 만큼 공부를 마친 지 얼마 안 됐어요.\" 니클비 부인이 받아 말했어."], ["Nickleby, ‘and he was thinking of--’", "\"그리고 그이는 생각하기를—\""]], [["‘Of making something of him someday,’ said Ralph.", "\"언젠가 이 녀석을 뭔가로 만들 생각이었겠지요.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘The old story; always thinking, and never doing.", "\"늘 똑같은 얘기지. 늘 생각만 하고, 한 번도 하질 않아."], ["If my brother had been a man of activity and prudence, he might have left you a rich woman, ma’am: and if he had turned his son into the world, as my father turned me, when I wasn’t as old as that boy by a year and a half, he would have been in a situation to help you, instead of being a burden upon you, and increasing your distress.", "내 동생이 부지런하고 신중한 사람이었다면, 제수씨를 부자로 만들어 놓고 떠났을 거요. 그리고 내 아버지가 나를 세상에 내보냈듯이 — 내가 저 녀석보다 한 살 반이나 어렸을 때였지요 — 제 아들을 세상에 내보냈다면, 그 녀석은 제수씨한테 짐이 되어 곤경을 더하기는커녕, 오히려 제수씨를 도와줄 처지가 되어 있었을 거요."], ["My brother was a thoughtless, inconsiderate man, Mrs.", "내 동생은 생각 없고 분별없는 사람이었소, 제수씨."], ["Nickleby, and nobody, I am sure, can have better reason to feel that, than you.’", "그리고 그걸 제수씨보다 더 절절히 느낄 사람은 없을 거라고, 나는 확신하오.\""]], [["This appeal set the widow upon thinking that perhaps she might have made a more successful venture with her one thousand pounds, and then she began to reflect what a comfortable sum it would have been just then; which dismal thoughts made her tears flow faster, and in the excess of these griefs she (being a well-meaning woman enough, but weak withal) fell first to deploring her hard fate, and then to remarking, with many sobs, that to be sure she had been a slave to poor Nicholas, and had often told him she might have married better (as indeed she had, very often), and that she never knew in his lifetime how the money went, but that if he had confided in her they might all have been better off that day; with other bitter recollections common to most married ladies, either during their coverture, or afterwards, or at both periods. Mrs Nickleby concluded by lamenting that the dear departed had never deigned to profit by her advice, save on one occasion; which was a strictly veracious statement, inasmuch as he had only acted upon it once, and had ruined himself in consequence.", "이 말은 과부의 마음에 이런 생각을 불러일으켰어. 어쩌면 자기가 그 천 파운드로 좀 더 성공적인 투자를 할 수도 있었을지 모른다는. 그러자 부인은 그 돈이 지금 같은 때에 얼마나 든든한 액수였을까 곱씹기 시작했지. 이런 서글픈 생각에 눈물이 더 빨리 흘러내렸어. 그리고 이 슬픔이 북받친 나머지, 부인은 (마음씨는 충분히 착했지만 한편으론 마음이 약한 여자라) 처음엔 자기 기구한 팔자를 한탄하더니, 이어서 흐느껴 가며 이런 말을 늘어놓았어. 정말이지 자기는 가엾은 니콜라스의 노예나 다름없었고, 자기는 더 좋은 데로 시집갈 수도 있었다고 남편한테 곧잘 말하곤 했다고 (사실 정말로 자주 그랬지). 그리고 그이가 살아 있는 동안엔 돈이 어디로 새는지 도무지 알 수가 없었지만, 그이가 자기한테 터놓고 의논했더라면 오늘날 다들 형편이 더 나았을 거라고. 그밖에도 대다수 결혼한 부인들이 결혼 생활 중에든, 그 후에든, 아니면 양쪽 다든 흔히 떠올리는 쓰라린 기억들을 줄줄이 늘어놓았지. 니클비 부인은 결국, 그리운 고인이 한 번을 빼고는 자기 충고를 들어 덕을 보려 한 적이 없었다고 한탄하며 말을 맺었어. 이건 한 치도 틀림없는 사실이었지. 그이는 딱 한 번 그 충고대로 했고, 그 바람에 알거지가 되고 말았으니까."]], [["Mr.", "랄프 니클비 씨는 이 모든 이야기를 엷은 미소를 띤 채 들었어."], ["Ralph Nickleby heard all this with a half-smile; and when the widow had finished, quietly took up the subject where it had been left before the above outbreak.", "그리고 과부가 말을 마치자, 아까 그 푸념이 터지기 전에 멈췄던 화제를 조용히 다시 끄집어냈지."]], [["‘Are you willing to work, sir?’ he inquired, frowning on his nephew.", "\"일을 할 마음은 있느냐?\" 그가 조카를 향해 인상을 쓰며 물었어."]], [["‘Of course I am,’ replied Nicholas haughtily.", "\"당연히 있습니다.\" 니콜라스가 도도하게 대답했어."]], [["‘Then see here, sir,’ said his uncle.", "\"그럼 이걸 봐라.\" 삼촌이 말했어."], ["‘This caught my eye this morning, and you may thank your stars for it.’", "\"오늘 아침 이게 내 눈에 띄었지. 네 운수에 감사해야 할 거다.\""]], [["With this exordium, Mr.", "이렇게 운을 떼고 나서, 랄프 니클비 씨는 주머니에서 신문을 꺼냈어."], ["Ralph Nickleby took a newspaper from his pocket, and after unfolding it, and looking for a short time among the advertisements, read as follows:", "그러고는 신문을 펼쳐 잠깐 광고란을 훑어보더니, 다음과 같이 읽었지."]], [["‘“EDUCATION.--At Mr.", "\"'교육."], ["Wackford Squeers’s Academy, Dotheboys Hall, at the delightful village of Dotheboys, near Greta Bridge in Yorkshire, Youth are boarded, clothed, booked, furnished with pocket-money, provided with all necessaries, instructed in all languages living and dead, mathematics, orthography, geometry, astronomy, trigonometry, the use of the globes, algebra, single stick (if required), writing, arithmetic, fortification, and every other branch of classical literature.", "워크퍼드 스퀴어스 씨의 학원, 두더보이즈 홀. 요크셔 그레타 브리지 인근의 아름다운 마을 두더보이즈에 있습니다."], ["Terms, twenty guineas per annum.", "학생들에게 숙식과 의복, 교재, 용돈을 제공하고, 필요한 모든 물품을 갖춰 줍니다."], ["No extras, no vacations, and diet unparalleled. Mr.", "살아 있는 언어와 죽은 언어를 모두 가르치며, 수학, 정서법, 기하학, 천문학, 삼각법, 지구의 사용법, 대수학, (원한다면) 봉술, 글쓰기, 산수, 축성술, 그밖에 고전 문학의 모든 분야를 가르칩니다."], ["Squeers is in town, and attends daily, from one till four, at the Saracen’s Head, Snow Hill.", "수업료는 연 이십 기니. 추가 비용 없음, 방학 없음, 식사는 비할 데 없이 훌륭합니다."], ["N.B. An able assistant wanted.", "스퀴어스 씨는 지금 시내에 머물며, 매일 한 시부터 네 시까지 스노 힐의 새러슨스 헤드에서 면담을 받습니다."], ["Annual salary 5 pounds.", "추신. 유능한 조교 한 사람 구함."], ["A Master of Arts would be preferred.”", "연봉 오 파운드. 문학 석사 우대.'\""]], [["‘There!’ said Ralph, folding the paper again.", "\"자!\" 랄프가 신문을 다시 접으며 말했어."], ["‘Let him get that situation, and his fortune is made.’", "\"이 자리만 얻으면, 저 녀석 출셋길은 열린 거요.\""]], [["‘But he is not a Master of Arts,’ said Mrs.", "\"하지만 얘는 문학 석사가 아니잖아요.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘That,’ replied Ralph, ‘that, I think, can be got over.’", "\"그거야,\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"그건, 내 생각엔, 어떻게든 넘어갈 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["‘But the salary is so small, and it is such a long way off, uncle!’ faltered Kate.", "\"하지만 연봉이 너무 적고, 게다가 너무 멀리 떨어진 곳이에요, 삼촌!\" 케이트가 머뭇거리며 말했어."]], [["‘Hush, Kate my dear,’ interposed Mrs.", "\"쉿, 케이트, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 끼어들었어."], ["Nickleby; ‘your uncle must know best.’", "\"삼촌이 제일 잘 아실 거야.\""]], [["‘I say,’ repeated Ralph, tartly, ‘let him get that situation, and his fortune is made.", "\"내 말은,\" 랄프가 쌀쌀맞게 되풀이했어."], ["If he don’t like that, let him get one for himself.", "\"이 자리만 얻으면 저 녀석 출셋길은 열린다는 거요. 그게 싫다면, 제 손으로 자리를 구해 보라지."], ["Without friends, money, recommendation, or knowledge of business of any kind, let him find honest employment in London, which will keep him in shoe leather, and I’ll give him a thousand pounds.", "친구도, 돈도, 추천장도, 어떤 종류의 사업 지식도 없이, 런던에서 제 구두 가죽이라도 댈 만큼의 정직한 일자리를 찾아낸다면, 내가 천 파운드를 주겠소. 적어도,\""], ["At least,’ said Mr Ralph Nickleby, checking himself, ‘I would if I had it.’", "랄프 니클비 씨가 스스로 말을 가다듬으며 덧붙였어. \"그만한 돈이 있다면 그러겠다는 거요.\""]], [["‘Poor fellow!’ said the young lady.", "\"가엾은 오빠!\" 아가씨가 말했어."], ["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["uncle, must we be separated so soon!’", "삼촌, 우리가 이렇게 빨리 헤어져야 하나요!\""]], [["‘Don’t tease your uncle with questions when he is thinking only for our good, my love,’ said Mrs.", "\"삼촌이 오로지 우리 잘되라고 생각해 주시는데, 이것저것 물어서 귀찮게 해 드리지 마라, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby. ‘Nicholas, my dear, I wish you would say something.’", "\"니콜라스, 얘야, 너도 뭐라고 말 좀 했으면 좋겠구나.\""]], [["‘Yes, mother, yes,’ said Nicholas, who had hitherto remained silent and absorbed in thought.", "\"네, 어머니, 네.\" 그때까지 말없이 생각에 잠겨 있던 니콜라스가 입을 열었어."], ["‘If I am fortunate enough to be appointed to this post, sir, for which I am so imperfectly qualified, what will become of those I leave behind?’", "\"삼촌, 제가 운이 좋아서 이 자리에 — 자격도 이렇게 모자란 자리지만요 — 임명된다면, 제가 두고 가는 사람들은 어찌 됩니까?\""]], [["‘Your mother and sister, sir,’ replied Ralph, ‘will be provided for, in that case (not otherwise), by me, and placed in some sphere of life in which they will be able to be independent. That will be my immediate care; they will not remain as they are, one week after your departure, I will undertake.’", "\"네 어미와 누이 말이냐.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"그 경우엔 (그 경우에 한해서) 내가 보살펴서, 제힘으로 살아갈 수 있는 어떤 처지에 자리 잡게 해 주마. 그게 내가 당장 챙길 일이다. 네가 떠나고 일주일 안에, 그들이 지금 이대로 있게 두지는 않으마, 내가 책임지지.\""]], [["‘Then,’ said Nicholas, starting gaily up, and wringing his uncle’s hand, ‘I am ready to do anything you wish me. Let us try our fortune with Mr Squeers at once; he can but refuse.’", "\"그렇다면,\" 니콜라스가 기쁘게 벌떡 일어나 삼촌의 손을 꼭 잡으며 말했어. \"삼촌이 바라시는 일은 무엇이든 할 준비가 됐습니다. 당장 스퀴어스 씨한테 운을 한번 걸어 봐요. 거절당해 봤자 본전이니까요.\""]], [["‘He won’t do that,’ said Ralph.", "\"그자는 거절하지 않을 거다.\""], ["‘He will be glad to have you on my recommendation.", "랄프가 말했어. \"내 추천이라면 너를 기꺼이 데려갈 거야."], ["Make yourself of use to him, and you’ll rise to be a partner in the establishment in no time.", "그자에게 쓸모 있는 사람이 되어 봐라. 그러면 머지않아 그 학교의 동업자로 올라설 거다."], ["Bless me, only think!", "원, 세상에, 생각만 해 보렴!"], ["if he were to die, why your fortune’s made at once.’", "만약 그자가 죽기라도 하면, 네 출셋길은 단번에 열리는 셈이지.\""]], [["‘To be sure, I see it all,’ said poor Nicholas, delighted with a thousand visionary ideas, that his good spirits and his inexperience were conjuring up before him.", "\"그럼요, 다 그려져요.\" 가엾은 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘Or suppose some young nobleman who is being educated at the Hall, were to take a fancy to me, and get his father to appoint me his travelling tutor when he left, and when we come back from the continent, procured me some handsome appointment.", "좋은 기분과 세상 물정 모르는 마음이 눈앞에 불러일으킨 천 가지 헛된 공상에 들떠서 말이야. \"아니면 그 홀에서 교육받는 어떤 젊은 귀족이 저를 마음에 들어 해서, 학교를 떠날 때 자기 아버지를 졸라 저를 데리고 다닐 가정교사로 삼아 주는 거예요."], ["Eh!", "그러고는 대륙 여행에서 돌아오면, 저한테 그럴듯한 자리를 마련해 주고요."], ["uncle?’", "어때요, 삼촌?\""]], [["‘Ah, to be sure!’ sneered Ralph.", "\"아무렴, 그렇고말고!\" 랄프가 비웃었어."]], [["‘And who knows, but when he came to see me when I was settled (as he would of course), he might fall in love with Kate, who would be keeping my house, and--and marry her, eh!", "\"그리고 누가 알아요, 제가 자리를 잡고 나서 그분이 저를 보러 오면 (당연히 그러겠지요), 제 집안 살림을 맡아 줄 케이트한테 반해서, 또—또 케이트와 결혼할지도요, 어때요, 삼촌?"], ["uncle? Who knows?’", "누가 알겠어요?\""]], [["‘Who, indeed!’ snarled Ralph.", "\"그래, 누가 알겠냐!\" 랄프가 으르렁거렸어."]], [["‘How happy we should be!’ cried Nicholas with enthusiasm. ‘The pain of parting is nothing to the joy of meeting again. Kate will be a beautiful woman, and I so proud to hear them say so, and mother so happy to be with us once again, and all these sad times forgotten, and--’ The picture was too bright a one to bear, and Nicholas, fairly overpowered by it, smiled faintly, and burst into tears.", "\"우리가 얼마나 행복할까요!\" 니콜라스가 들떠서 외쳤어. \"헤어지는 아픔쯤이야, 다시 만나는 기쁨에 비하면 아무것도 아니지요. 케이트는 아름다운 여인이 되어 있을 거고, 사람들이 그렇게 말하는 걸 들으면 저는 어찌나 뿌듯할까요. 그리고 어머니는 우리와 다시 함께 지내게 되어 얼마나 행복하실까요. 이 슬픈 시절은 다 잊고요, 그리고—\" 그림이 너무 눈부셔서 도저히 견딜 수가 없었어. 그래서 니콜라스는 그 벅참에 그만 압도당해, 희미하게 미소 짓다가 그대로 울음을 터뜨렸지."]], [["This simple family, born and bred in retirement, and wholly unacquainted with what is called the world--a conventional phrase which, being interpreted, often signifieth all the rascals in it--mingled their tears together at the thought of their first separation; and, this first gush of feeling over, were proceeding to dilate with all the buoyancy of untried hope on the bright prospects before them, when Mr.", "이 단순한 가족은 세상과 동떨어진 곳에서 태어나 자랐고, 이른바 세상이라는 것을 도무지 몰랐어. 그 세상이라는 흔한 말은, 풀어서 해석하면 흔히 그 안의 온갖 악당들을 가리키는 말이기도 하지."], ["Ralph Nickleby suggested, that if they lost time, some more fortunate candidate might deprive Nicholas of the stepping-stone to fortune which the advertisement pointed out, and so undermine all their air-built castles.", "어쨌든 그들은 첫 이별을 생각하며 함께 눈물을 흘렸어. 그리고 이 첫 감정의 북받침이 가라앉자, 한 번도 시험해 본 적 없는 희망의 들뜬 마음으로 눈앞에 펼쳐진 밝은 앞날을 한껏 부풀려 그려 보기 시작했지."], ["This timely reminder effectually stopped the conversation.", "바로 그때 랄프 니클비 씨가 한마디 거들었어. 꾸물거리다가는 운 좋은 다른 지원자가 광고가 가리키는 그 출세의 디딤돌을 니콜라스한테서 가로채, 그들이 공중에 쌓아 올린 성을 죄다 허물어 버릴지도 모른다고."], ["Nicholas, having carefully copied the address of Mr.", "이 시의적절한 일깨움에 대화는 딱 멎었어."], ["Squeers, the uncle and nephew issued forth together in quest of that accomplished gentleman; Nicholas firmly persuading himself that he had done his relative great injustice in disliking him at first sight; and Mrs.", "니콜라스가 스퀴어스 씨의 주소를 꼼꼼히 옮겨 적은 뒤, 삼촌과 조카는 함께 그 훌륭한 신사를 찾아 길을 나섰지. 니콜라스는 첫눈에 삼촌을 싫어한 것이 그 친척한테 큰 잘못을 저지른 거라고 단단히 믿게 되었어."], ["Nickleby being at some pains to inform her daughter that she was sure he was a much more kindly disposed person than he seemed; which, Miss Nickleby dutifully remarked, he might very easily be.", "그리고 니클비 부인은 삼촌이 겉보기보다 훨씬 친절한 사람인 게 틀림없다고 딸한테 애써 일러 주었지. 그러자 니클비 양은 얌전히, 그야 충분히 그럴 수도 있겠다고 대꾸했어."]], [["To tell the truth, the good lady’s opinion had been not a little influenced by her brother-in-law’s appeal to her better understanding, and his implied compliment to her high deserts; and although she had dearly loved her husband, and still doted on her children, he had struck so successfully on one of those little jarring chords in the human heart (Ralph was well acquainted with its worst weaknesses, though he knew nothing of its best), that she had already begun seriously to consider herself the amiable and suffering victim of her late husband’s imprudence.", "사실대로 말하자면, 이 착한 부인의 생각은 시동생의 말에 적잖이 흔들린 거였어. 자기 판단력을 추켜세우고, 자기가 대단히 훌륭한 사람이라고 은근히 치켜세운 그 말에 말이야. 그리고 부인은 남편을 더없이 사랑했고 자식들을 여전히 끔찍이 아꼈지만, 랄프가 인간 마음속 그 어긋난 가락 가운데 하나를 어찌나 솜씨 좋게 건드렸던지 (랄프는 인간 마음의 가장 못된 약점은 훤히 꿰고 있었지만, 가장 좋은 점은 도무지 알지 못했어), 부인은 어느새 자기를 죽은 남편의 경솔함이 빚어낸 사랑스럽고 가여운 희생자라고 진지하게 여기기 시작했지."]], [["# CHAPTER 4", "# CHAPTER 4"]], [["Nicholas and his Uncle (to secure the Fortune without loss of time) wait upon Mr.", "스노 힐! 그 이름이 북부행 마차에 금박 글씨와 짙은 그림자로 또렷하게 새겨진 걸 보는 조용한 동네 사람들은, 스노 힐을 대체 어떤 곳이라고 생각할까?"], ["Wackford Squeers, the Yorkshire Schoolmaster\nSnow Hill!", "누구나 자주 눈에 띄거나 귀에 들리는 이름이 있으면, 그곳에 대해 어렴풋하고 흐릿한 생각을 품기 마련이지."], ["What kind of place can the quiet townspeople who see the words emblazoned, in all the legibility of gilt letters and dark shading, on the north-country coaches, take Snow Hill to be?", "그러니 바로 이 스노 힐을 두고 떠도는 제멋대로의 생각들이 또 얼마나 많겠어. 이름이 어찌나 그럴듯한지."], ["All people have some undefined and shadowy notion of a place whose name is frequently before their eyes, or often in their ears.", "스노 힐 — 게다가 그 스노 힐이 사라센스 헤드라는 마차와 짝을 이루니, 두 가지 생각이 겹쳐서 뭔가 험하고 거친 그림이 떠오르거든!"], ["What a vast number of random ideas there must be perpetually floating about, regarding this same Snow Hill. The name is such a good one.", "매서운 바람과 사나운 겨울 폭풍이 몰아치는 황량하고 쓸쓸한 벌판 — 낮에도 적막하고 밤에는 점잖은 사람이라면 떠올리기조차 꺼리는 어둡고 차갑고 음울한 황무지 — 외로운 나그네는 피해 다니고 흉악한 강도들이 모여드는 그런 곳."], ["Snow Hill--Snow Hill too, coupled with a Saracen’s Head: picturing to us by a double association of ideas, something stern and rugged!", "이런 것, 아니면 이와 비슷한 것이 그 멀고 외진 시골 사람들이 스노 힐에 대해 흔히 품는 생각일 거야."], ["A bleak desolate tract of country, open to piercing blasts and fierce wintry storms--a dark, cold, gloomy heath, lonely by day, and scarcely to be thought of by honest folks at night--a place which solitary wayfarers shun, and where desperate robbers congregate;--this, or something like this, should be the prevalent notion of Snow Hill, in those remote and rustic parts, through which the Saracen’s Head, like some grim apparition, rushes each day and night with mysterious and ghost-like punctuality; holding its swift and headlong course in all weathers, and seeming to bid defiance to the very elements themselves.", "그곳에는 사라센스 헤드가 마치 무시무시한 유령처럼 매일 밤낮으로 신비롭고 귀신같은 정확함으로 내달리거든. 어떤 날씨에도 빠르고 거침없이 길을 달리며, 마치 자연의 온갖 기운에 도전이라도 하는 듯이 말이야."]], [["The reality is rather different, but by no means to be despised notwithstanding.", "실제 모습은 그것과는 좀 다르지만, 그렇다고 결코 우습게 볼 곳은 아니야. 런던의 한복판, 그 활기와 사업의 심장부, 시끄러운 소음과 움직임이 소용돌이치는 그 가운데에 — 사방에서 끊임없이 흘러나와 그 성벽 아래에서 만나는 거대한 생명의 물결을 거스르듯이 — 뉴게이트가 서 있어."], ["There, at the very core of London, in the heart of its business and animation, in the midst of a whirl of noise and motion: stemming as it were the giant currents of life that flow ceaselessly on from different quarters, and meet beneath its walls: stands Newgate; and in that crowded street on which it frowns so darkly--within a few feet of the squalid tottering houses--upon the very spot on which the vendors of soup and fish and damaged fruit are now plying their trades--scores of human beings, amidst a roar of sounds to which even the tumult of a great city is as nothing, four, six, or eight strong men at a time, have been hurried violently and swiftly from the world, when the scene has been rendered frightful with excess of human life; when curious eyes have glared from casement and house-top, and wall and pillar; and when, in the mass of white and upturned faces, the dying wretch, in his all-comprehensive look of agony, has met not one--not one--that bore the impress of pity or compassion.", "그리고 그 감옥이 어둡게 내려다보는 그 북적이는 거리에서 — 다 쓰러져 가는 누추한 집들에서 불과 몇 걸음 떨어진 곳, 지금은 수프와 생선과 흠집 난 과일을 파는 장사꾼들이 장사를 벌이고 있는 바로 그 자리에서 — 큰 도시의 소란조차 아무것도 아닌 듯한 굉음 속에서, 수십 명의 사람들이, 한 번에 네 명, 여섯 명, 여덟 명의 건장한 사내들이, 거칠고 빠르게 세상에서 떠밀려 나갔지. 사람들이 너무 많이 몰려들어 그 광경이 끔찍해질 때, 호기심 어린 눈들이 창문에서, 지붕에서, 담벼락과 기둥에서 번뜩일 때, 그리고 위로 쳐들린 하얀 얼굴들 무더기 속에서, 죽어 가는 가엾은 자가 그 모든 고통을 담은 눈길을 두루 던졌지만 단 하나도 — 단 하나도 — 연민이나 동정의 빛을 띤 얼굴을 만나지 못했지."]], [["Near to the jail, and by consequence near to Smithfield also, and the Compter, and the bustle and noise of the city; and just on that particular part of Snow Hill where omnibus horses going eastward seriously think of falling down on purpose, and where horses in hackney cabriolets going westward not unfrequently fall by accident, is the coach-yard of the Saracen’s Head Inn; its portal guarded by two Saracens’ heads and shoulders, which it was once the pride and glory of the choice spirits of this metropolis to pull down at night, but which have for some time remained in undisturbed tranquillity; possibly because this species of humour is now confined to St James’s parish, where door knockers are preferred as being more portable, and bell-wires esteemed as convenient toothpicks. Whether this be the reason or not, there they are, frowning upon you from each side of the gateway. The inn itself garnished with another Saracen’s Head, frowns upon you from the top of the yard; while from the door of the hind boot of all the red coaches that are standing therein, there glares a small Saracen’s Head, with a twin expression to the large Saracens’ Heads below, so that the general appearance of the pile is decidedly of the Saracenic order.", "감옥 가까이에, 따라서 스미스필드와 콤프터, 그리고 도시의 북적임과 소음에서도 가까운 곳, 동쪽으로 가는 합승마차 말들이 진지하게 일부러 쓰러질까 궁리하고, 서쪽으로 가는 삯마차 말들이 심심찮게 정말로 넘어지곤 하는 바로 그 스노 힐의 한 자락에, 사라센스 헤드 여관의 마차 마당이 있어. 그 정문은 사라센 사람의 머리와 어깨 둘이 지키고 있는데, 한때는 이 도시의 별난 멋쟁이들이 밤에 그걸 뜯어내리는 걸 자랑이자 영광으로 여겼지만, 얼마 전부터는 아무도 건드리지 않아 잠잠하게 남아 있지. 아마 그런 부류의 장난이 이제는 세인트 제임스 교구에만 머물러서일 거야. 거기서는 문에 달린 노커가 들고 다니기 편하다고 더 인기고, 초인종 줄은 편리한 이쑤시개로 쳐주거든. 그게 이유든 아니든, 그것들은 거기에 그렇게 정문 양옆에서 너를 노려보고 있어. 여관 자체도 또 다른 사라센 머리로 장식되어 마당 꼭대기에서 너를 노려보고, 거기 서 있는 붉은 마차마다 뒤쪽 짐칸 문에서는 작은 사라센 머리가 아래쪽 큰 사라센 머리들과 똑 닮은 표정으로 노려보고 있지. 그래서 이 건물 전체의 분위기는 누가 봐도 사라센풍이라 할 만해."]], [["When you walk up this yard, you will see the booking-office on your left, and the tower of St Sepulchre’s church, darting abruptly up into the sky, on your right, and a gallery of bedrooms on both sides.", "이 마당을 걸어 올라가면 왼쪽에 예약 사무소가 보이고, 오른쪽에는 세인트 세펄커 교회의 탑이 하늘로 불쑥 솟아 있는 게 보여."], ["Just before you, you will observe a long window with the words ‘coffee-room’ legibly painted above it; and looking out of that window, you would have seen in addition, if you had gone at the right time, Mr.", "그리고 양옆으로는 침실들이 늘어선 회랑이 있지. 바로 앞에는 위에 '커피점'이라는 글자가 또렷하게 적힌 긴 창문 하나가 보일 거야."], ["Wackford Squeers with his hands in his pockets.", "그리고 그 창문 안을 들여다보면, 마침 알맞은 때에 갔다면, 주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣은 와크포드 스퀴어스 씨도 보였을 거야."]], [["Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨의 생김새는 호감을 주는 편이 아니었어."], ["Squeers’s appearance was not prepossessing.", "눈이 하나밖에 없었는데, 세상 사람들은 보통 둘이 있는 쪽을 더 좋아하잖아."], ["He had but one eye, and the popular prejudice runs in favour of two.", "그가 가진 한쪽 눈은 분명 쓸모는 있었지만 보기에는 영 아니었어. 푸르스름한 잿빛에다, 모양은 현관문 위에 달린 부채꼴 채광창을 닮았거든."], ["The eye he had, was unquestionably useful, but decidedly not ornamental: being of a greenish grey, and in shape resembling the fan-light of a street door.", "얼굴의 빈 쪽은 잔뜩 주름지고 오그라들어 있어서, 아주 음흉한 인상을 풍겼지. 특히 웃을 때가 그랬는데, 그럴 때 그의 표정은 거의 악당에 가까웠어."], ["The blank side of his face was much wrinkled and puckered up, which gave him a very sinister appearance, especially when he smiled, at which times his expression bordered closely on the villainous.", "머리카락은 끝부분만 빼고는 아주 납작하고 번들거렸어. 끝부분은 낮고 튀어나온 이마에서부터 빳빳하게 위로 빗어 올려져 있었는데, 그게 그의 거친 목소리며 상스러운 태도와 잘 어울렸지."], ["His hair was very flat and shiny, save at the ends, where it was brushed stiffly up from a low protruding forehead, which assorted well with his harsh voice and coarse manner.", "나이는 쉰둘이나 셋쯤 됐고, 키는 중간보다 조금 작았어. 끝이 긴 흰 목수건을 두르고, 학자풍의 검은 정장을 입고 있었지."], ["He was about two or three and fifty, and a trifle below the middle size; he wore a white neckerchief with long ends, and a suit of scholastic black; but his coat sleeves being a great deal too long, and his trousers a great deal too short, he appeared ill at ease in his clothes, and as if he were in a perpetual state of astonishment at finding himself so respectable.", "그런데 윗도리 소매는 너무 길고 바지는 너무 짧아서, 옷이 영 불편해 보였어. 마치 자기가 이렇게 점잖은 모습이라는 걸 늘 깜짝 놀라며 어색해하는 사람처럼 말이야."]], [["Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨는 커피점 벽난로 가운데 하나 옆 칸막이 자리에 서 있었어. 거기에는 커피점에서 흔히 보는 그런 탁자 하나와, 칸막이 모서리에 맞추어 만든 별나게 생기고 별난 크기의 탁자 둘이 놓여 있었지."], ["Squeers was standing in a box by one of the coffee-room fire-places, fitted with one such table as is usually seen in coffee-rooms, and two of extraordinary shapes and dimensions made to suit the angles of the partition.", "그 자리 한구석에는 아주 작은 전나무 궤짝 하나가 변변찮은 노끈으로 묶여 있었어. 그리고 그 궤짝 위에는 — 끈으로 매는 반장화와 코르덴 바지를 허공에 대롱대롱 늘어뜨린 채 — 자그마한 사내아이 하나가 올라앉아 있었어."], ["In a corner of the seat, was a very small deal trunk, tied round with a scanty piece of cord; and on the trunk was perched--his lace-up half-boots and corduroy trousers dangling in the air--a diminutive boy, with his shoulders drawn up to his ears, and his hands planted on his knees, who glanced timidly at the schoolmaster, from time to time, with evident dread and apprehension.", "어깨를 귀까지 잔뜩 움츠리고 두 손을 무릎에 얹은 채, 그 아이는 이따금 겁에 질린 눈으로 선생을 흘끔거렸지. 누가 봐도 두려움과 불안에 떨고 있었어."]], [["‘Half-past three,’ muttered Mr.", "\"세 시 반이군.\""], ["Squeers, turning from the window, and looking sulkily at the coffee-room clock.", "스퀴어스 씨가 창가에서 돌아서며 커피점 시계를 못마땅하게 바라보더니 중얼거렸어."], ["‘There will be nobody here today.’", "\"오늘은 아무도 안 오겠어.\""]], [["Much vexed by this reflection, Mr. Squeers looked at the little boy to see whether he was doing anything he could beat him for.", "이런 생각에 잔뜩 짜증이 난 스퀴어스 씨는, 그 사내아이가 때려 줄 만한 짓이라도 하고 있는지 보려고 아이를 쳐다봤어."], ["As he happened not to be doing anything at all, he merely boxed his ears, and told him not to do it again.", "그런데 아이는 마침 아무 짓도 하고 있지 않았지. 그래서 그냥 귀싸대기만 한 대 갈기고는, 두 번 다시 그러지 말라고 했어."]], [["‘At Midsummer,’ muttered Mr.", "\"한여름에는,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 다시 푸념을 늘어놓으며 중얼거렸어."], ["Squeers, resuming his complaint, ‘I took down ten boys; ten twenties is two hundred pound.", "\"열 명을 데려갔단 말이지. 열에 스무 곱이면 이백 파운드야."], ["I go back at eight o’clock tomorrow morning, and have got only three--three oughts is an ought--three twos is six--sixty pound.", "그런데 내일 아침 여덟 시에 돌아가는데, 겨우 셋밖에 못 구했어. 셋에 영을 곱하면 영, 셋에 둘을 곱하면 여섯, 육십 파운드라."], ["What’s come of all the boys?", "애들이 다 어디로 간 거야?"], ["what’s parents got in their heads?", "부모들 머릿속에 대체 뭐가 든 거지?"], ["what does it all mean?’", "이게 다 무슨 영문이람?\""]], [["Here the little boy on the top of the trunk gave a violent sneeze.", "이때 궤짝 위에 앉은 사내아이가 요란하게 재채기를 했어."]], [["‘Halloa, sir!’ growled the schoolmaster, turning round.", "\"이봐, 너!\" 선생이 홱 돌아서며 으르렁거렸어."], ["‘What’s that, sir?’", "\"그게 무슨 짓이야?\""]], [["‘Nothing, please sir,’ replied the little boy.", "\"아무것도 아니에요, 선생님.\" 사내아이가 대답했어."]], [["‘Nothing, sir!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"아무것도 아니라고!\""], ["Squeers.", "스퀴어스 씨가 버럭 소리쳤어."]], [["‘Please sir, I sneezed,’ rejoined the boy, trembling till the little trunk shook under him.", "\"저, 재채기를 했어요.\" 아이가 다시 말했어. 어찌나 떨었는지 작은 궤짝까지 들썩였지."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["sneezed, did you?’ retorted Mr.", "재채기를 했다고?\""], ["Squeers.", "스퀴어스 씨가 되받아쳤어."], ["‘Then what did you say “nothing” for, sir?’", "\"그럼 왜 '아무것도 아니'라고 했어, 응?\""]], [["In default of a better answer to this question, the little boy screwed a couple of knuckles into each of his eyes and began to cry, wherefore Mr Squeers knocked him off the trunk with a blow on one side of the face, and knocked him on again with a blow on the other.", "이 물음에 마땅한 대답이 떠오르지 않자, 사내아이는 양쪽 눈에 주먹을 한 쌍씩 비벼 넣더니 울기 시작했어. 그러자 스퀴어스 씨는 한쪽 뺨을 후려쳐 아이를 궤짝에서 떨어뜨리고, 다른 쪽 뺨을 후려쳐 다시 올려놓았지."]], [["‘Wait till I get you down into Yorkshire, my young gentleman,’ said Mr Squeers, ‘and then I’ll give you the rest.", "\"요크셔에 데려갈 때까지 기다려, 이 도련님아.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어."], ["Will you hold that noise, sir?’", "\"그때 나머지를 마저 주마. 그 시끄러운 소리 그칠 거야, 안 그칠 거야?\""]], [["‘Ye--ye--yes,’ sobbed the little boy, rubbing his face very hard with the Beggar’s Petition in printed calico.", "\"네, 네, 네.\" 사내아이가 흐느끼며, 무명천에 인쇄된 거지의 탄원서로 얼굴을 박박 문질렀어."]], [["‘Then do so at once, sir,’ said Squeers.", "\"그럼 당장 그쳐.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘Do you hear?’", "\"들었냐?\""]], [["As this admonition was accompanied with a threatening gesture, and uttered with a savage aspect, the little boy rubbed his face harder, as if to keep the tears back; and, beyond alternately sniffing and choking, gave no further vent to his emotions.", "이 호통에는 위협하는 몸짓이 따랐고, 그것도 사나운 표정으로 내뱉었으니, 사내아이는 눈물을 막으려는 듯 얼굴을 더 세게 문질렀어. 그러고는 번갈아 훌쩍이고 목메는 것 말고는, 더 이상 감정을 드러내지 않았지."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"스퀴어스 씨.\""], ["Squeers,’ said the waiter, looking in at this juncture; ‘here’s a gentleman asking for you at the bar.’", "마침 이때 종업원이 안을 들여다보며 말했어. \"어떤 신사분이 술집 쪽에서 찾으시는데요.\""]], [["‘Show the gentleman in, Richard,’ replied Mr.", "\"그 신사분을 들여보내게, 리처드.\""], ["Squeers, in a soft voice.", "스퀴어스 씨가 부드러운 목소리로 대답했어."], ["‘Put your handkerchief in your pocket, you little scoundrel, or I’ll murder you when the gentleman goes.’", "\"손수건 주머니에 넣어, 이 못된 녀석아. 안 그러면 손님 가고 나서 죽여 버릴 테니.\""]], [["The schoolmaster had scarcely uttered these words in a fierce whisper, when the stranger entered.", "선생이 이 말을 사납게 속삭이듯 내뱉기가 무섭게, 낯선 손님이 들어왔어."], ["Affecting not to see him, Mr. Squeers feigned to be intent upon mending a pen, and offering benevolent advice to his youthful pupil.", "스퀴어스 씨는 그를 못 본 척하며, 펜을 고치는 데 골몰한 척하면서 어린 제자에게 자애로운 충고를 건네는 시늉을 했어."]], [["‘My dear child,’ said Mr.", "\"얘야,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어."], ["Squeers, ‘all people have their trials.", "\"사람은 누구나 시련을 겪는 법이란다."], ["This early trial of yours that is fit to make your little heart burst, and your very eyes come out of your head with crying, what is it?", "어린 네 가슴이 터질 것 같고, 울다가 눈알이 다 빠질 것 같은 이 어린 시절의 시련, 그게 대체 뭐겠니? 아무것도 아니란다."], ["Nothing; less than nothing.", "아무것도 아닌 것보다 못한 거지."], ["You are leaving your friends, but you will have a father in me, my dear, and a mother in Mrs. Squeers.", "너는 지금 네 친구들과 헤어지고 있지만, 내가 네 아버지가 되어 주마, 얘야."], ["At the delightful village of Dotheboys, near Greta Bridge in Yorkshire, where youth are boarded, clothed, booked, washed, furnished with pocket-money, provided with all necessaries--’", "그리고 스퀴어스 부인이 네 어머니가 되어 주실 거야. 요크셔의 그레타 다리 근처, 그 아름다운 두더보이즈 마을에서는 아이들에게 숙식과 옷, 책, 빨래, 용돈, 그 밖에 필요한 모든 것을 다 마련해 주는데——\""]], [["‘It IS the gentleman,’ observed the stranger, stopping the schoolmaster in the rehearsal of his advertisement.", "\"바로 그 신사분이시군요.\" 낯선 손님이 선생이 광고문을 읊는 걸 막으며 말했어."], ["‘Mr. Squeers, I believe, sir?’", "\"스퀴어스 씨 맞으시죠?\""]], [["‘The same, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 선생님.\""], ["Squeers, with an assumption of extreme surprise.", "스퀴어스 씨가 몹시 놀란 척하며 말했어."]], [["‘The gentleman,’ said the stranger, ‘that advertised in the Times newspaper?’", "\"타임스 신문에 광고를 내신 그 신사분 맞으시죠?\" 낯선 손님이 물었어."]], [["‘--Morning Post, Chronicle, Herald, and Advertiser, regarding the Academy called Dotheboys Hall at the delightful village of Dotheboys, near Greta Bridge in Yorkshire,’ added Mr.", "\"——모닝 포스트, 크로니클, 헤럴드, 그리고 애드버타이저에도요. 요크셔의 그레타 다리 근처, 그 아름다운 두더보이즈 마을에 있는 두더보이즈 홀이라는 학교에 관한 광고죠.\""], ["Squeers. ‘You come on business, sir.", "스퀴어스 씨가 덧붙였어."], ["I see by my young friends.", "\"일이 있어서 오셨군요, 선생님."], ["How do you do, my little gentleman?", "제 어린 친구들을 보니 알겠습니다."], ["and how do you do, sir?’ With this salutation Mr.", "잘 있었니, 꼬마 신사? 너는 또 어떠니?\""], ["Squeers patted the heads of two hollow-eyed, small-boned little boys, whom the applicant had brought with him, and waited for further communications.", "이렇게 인사하며 스퀴어스 씨는 그 손님이 데려온, 눈이 퀭하고 뼈가 앙상한 두 사내아이의 머리를 쓰다듬었어. 그러고는 다음 이야기를 기다렸지."]], [["‘I am in the oil and colour way.", "\"저는 기름과 물감을 파는 사람입니다."], ["My name is Snawley, sir,’ said the stranger.", "이름은 스놀리라고 합니다.\" 낯선 손님이 말했어."]], [["Squeers inclined his head as much as to say, ‘And a remarkably pretty name, too.’", "스퀴어스는 마치 \"참으로 멋진 이름이군요\" 하고 말하듯 고개를 끄덕였어."]], [["The stranger continued.", "낯선 손님이 말을 이었어."], ["‘I have been thinking, Mr. Squeers, of placing my two boys at your school.’", "\"스퀴어스 씨, 제 두 아이를 선생님 학교에 보낼까 생각하고 있었습니다.\""]], [["‘It is not for me to say so, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"제 입으로 이런 말씀 드리긴 뭣하지만요, 선생님.\""], ["Squeers, ‘but I don’t think you could possibly do a better thing.’", "스퀴어스 씨가 대답했어. \"그보다 더 나은 결정은 없으실 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Hem!’ said the other.", "\"흠!\" 상대가 말했어."], ["‘Twenty pounds per annewum, I believe, Mr Squeers?’", "\"한 해에 이십 파운드라고 했지요, 스퀴어스 씨?\""]], [["‘Guineas,’ rejoined the schoolmaster, with a persuasive smile.", "\"기니입니다.\" 선생이 구슬리는 듯한 미소를 띠며 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Pounds for two, I think, Mr. Squeers,’ said Mr.", "\"둘이서 파운드로 알고 있는데요, 스퀴어스 씨.\""], ["Snawley, solemnly.", "스놀리 씨가 진지하게 말했어."]], [["‘I don’t think it could be done, sir,’ replied Squeers, as if he had never considered the proposition before.", "\"그건 어려울 것 같습니다만, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 마치 그런 제안은 처음 생각해 본다는 듯 대답했어."], ["‘Let me see; four fives is twenty, double that, and deduct the--well, a pound either way shall not stand betwixt us.", "\"어디 보자. 다섯에 넷을 곱하면 스물, 그 두 배에서 빼면——뭐, 파운드 하나쯤이 우리 사이를 가로막을 거야 있겠습니까."], ["You must recommend me to your connection, sir, and make it up that way.’", "대신 선생님께서 아는 분들께 저를 좀 추천해 주셔서 그렇게 메워 주십시오.\""]], [["‘They are not great eaters,’ said Mr.", "\"애들이 많이 먹지는 않습니다.\""], ["Snawley.", "스놀리 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오! 그건 전혀 상관없습니다.\""], ["that doesn’t matter at all,’ replied Squeers.", "스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"저희 학교에서는 아이들 식성 따위는 고려하지 않으니까요.\""], ["‘We don’t consider the boys’ appetites at our establishment.’ This was strictly true; they did not.", "이건 한 치도 틀림없는 사실이었어. 정말로 고려하지 않았거든."]], [["‘Every wholesome luxury, sir, that Yorkshire can afford,’ continued Squeers; ‘every beautiful moral that Mrs.", "\"요크셔에서 누릴 수 있는 건강에 좋은 온갖 호사가요, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 말을 이었어."], ["Squeers can instil; every--in short, every comfort of a home that a boy could wish for, will be theirs, Mr. Snawley.’", "\"스퀴어스 부인이 가르칠 수 있는 온갖 아름다운 도덕이며, 한마디로, 아이가 바랄 수 있는 가정의 온갖 안락함이 다 아이들 것이 될 겁니다, 스놀리 씨.\""]], [["‘I should wish their morals to be particularly attended to,’ said Mr Snawley.", "\"저는 특히 아이들 도덕 교육에 신경을 써 주셨으면 합니다.\" 스놀리 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘I am glad of that, sir,’ replied the schoolmaster, drawing himself up.", "\"그렇다니 다행입니다, 선생님.\" 선생이 몸을 쭉 펴며 대답했어."], ["‘They have come to the right shop for morals, sir.’", "\"도덕이라면 제대로 찾아오셨습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["‘You are a moral man yourself,’ said Mr.", "\"선생님 본인이 도덕적인 분이시군요.\""], ["Snawley.", "스놀리 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘I rather believe I am, sir,’ replied Squeers.", "\"저도 그런 편이라고 봅니다, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."]], [["‘I have the satisfaction to know you are, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"그러시다는 걸 알게 되어 흐뭇합니다, 선생님.\" 스놀리 씨가 말했어."], ["Snawley. ‘I asked one of your references, and he said you were pious.’", "\"선생님이 대 주신 보증인 한 분께 여쭤봤더니, 선생님이 독실하시다더군요.\""]], [["‘Well, sir, I hope I am a little in that line,’ replied Squeers.", "\"뭐, 선생님, 그 방면으로 조금은 그렇다고 봅니다.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."]], [["‘I hope I am also,’ rejoined the other.", "\"저도 그러기를 바라는 사람입니다.\""], ["‘Could I say a few words with you in the next box?’", "상대가 다시 말했어. \"옆 칸에서 몇 마디 나눌 수 있을까요?\""]], [["‘By all means,’ rejoined Squeers with a grin.", "\"물론입니다.\" 스퀴어스가 씩 웃으며 대답했어."], ["‘My dears, will you speak to your new playfellow a minute or two?", "\"얘들아, 새 놀이 친구하고 잠깐 얘기 좀 나누고 있을래?"], ["That is one of my boys, sir.", "저 애가 제 학생 중 하나입니다, 선생님."], ["Belling his name is,--a Taunton boy that, sir.’", "이름은 벨링이고요. 톤턴에서 온 아이지요.\""]], [["‘Is he, indeed?’ rejoined Mr.", "\"아, 그렇습니까?\""], ["Snawley, looking at the poor little urchin as if he were some extraordinary natural curiosity.", "스놀리 씨가 그 가엾은 꼬마를 무슨 진귀한 자연의 신기한 물건이라도 되는 양 바라보며 대꾸했어."]], [["‘He goes down with me tomorrow, sir,’ said Squeers.", "\"내일 저와 함께 내려갈 아이입니다, 선생님.\""], ["‘That’s his luggage that he is a sitting upon now.", "스퀴어스가 말했어. \"지금 깔고 앉아 있는 게 저 애 짐입니다."], ["Each boy is required to bring, sir, two suits of clothes, six shirts, six pair of stockings, two nightcaps, two pocket-handkerchiefs, two pair of shoes, two hats, and a razor.’", "학생마다 가져와야 하는 게요, 선생님, 옷 두 벌, 셔츠 여섯 장, 양말 여섯 켤레, 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자 둘, 손수건 둘, 신발 두 켤레, 모자 둘, 그리고 면도칼 하나입니다.\""]], [["‘A razor!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"면도칼이라고요!\""], ["Snawley, as they walked into the next box.", "스놀리 씨가 옆 칸으로 걸어 들어가며 소리쳤어."], ["‘What for?’", "\"그건 어디에 쓰려고요?\""]], [["‘To shave with,’ replied Squeers, in a slow and measured tone.", "\"면도를 하려는 거죠.\" 스퀴어스가 느리고 또박또박한 말투로 대답했어."]], [["There was not much in these three words, but there must have been something in the manner in which they were said, to attract attention; for the schoolmaster and his companion looked steadily at each other for a few seconds, and then exchanged a very meaning smile. Snawley was a sleek, flat-nosed man, clad in sombre garments, and long black gaiters, and bearing in his countenance an expression of much mortification and sanctity; so, his smiling without any obvious reason was the more remarkable.", "이 짧은 한마디에 별다른 게 담겨 있지는 않았지만, 그 말투에는 분명 사람의 주의를 끄는 뭔가가 있었나 봐. 선생과 그 일행이 잠깐 동안 서로를 빤히 바라보더니, 아주 의미심장한 미소를 주고받았거든. 스놀리는 매끈하고 코가 납작한 사내였어. 칙칙한 옷에 긴 검정 각반을 차고, 얼굴에는 잔뜩 풀이 죽고 거룩한 듯한 표정을 띠고 있었지. 그러니 뚜렷한 이유도 없이 그가 미소를 짓는 건 더더욱 눈에 띄는 일이었어."]], [["‘Up to what age do you keep boys at your school then?’ he asked at length.", "\"그러면 학교에서는 아이들을 몇 살까지 데리고 계십니까?\" 그가 마침내 물었어."]], [["‘Just as long as their friends make the quarterly payments to my agent in town, or until such time as they run away,’ replied Squeers. ‘Let us understand each other; I see we may safely do so. What are these boys;--natural children?’", "\"애들 보호자가 시내에 있는 제 대리인에게 분기마다 돈을 내는 한 계속이지요. 아니면 애들이 달아날 때까지고요.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"우리 서로 터놓고 얘기합시다. 그래도 될 것 같으니까요. 이 아이들은 어떤 애들입니까? 사생아인가요?\""]], [["‘No,’ rejoined Snawley, meeting the gaze of the schoolmaster’s one eye.", "\"아니요.\" 스놀리가 선생의 외눈과 눈을 맞추며 대꾸했어."], ["‘They ain’t.’", "\"그건 아닙니다.\""]], [["‘I thought they might be,’ said Squeers, coolly.", "\"그럴 수도 있겠다 싶었거든요.\" 스퀴어스가 태연하게 말했어."], ["‘We have a good many of them; that boy’s one.’", "\"그런 애들이 꽤 많습니다. 저 애도 그중 하나죠.\""]], [["‘Him in the next box?’ said Snawley.", "\"옆 칸에 있는 애 말입니까?\" 스놀리가 물었어."]], [["Squeers nodded in the affirmative; his companion took another peep at the little boy on the trunk, and, turning round again, looked as if he were quite disappointed to see him so much like other boys, and said he should hardly have thought it.", "스퀴어스가 그렇다고 고개를 끄덕였어. 그러자 그 일행은 궤짝 위의 사내아이를 다시 한번 흘끔 보더니, 도로 돌아앉으면서 그 애가 여느 아이들과 별로 다를 게 없어 영 실망스럽다는 표정을 짓고는, 그럴 줄은 몰랐다고 말했어."]], [["‘He is,’ cried Squeers.", "\"그렇다니까요.\" 스퀴어스가 소리쳤어."], ["‘But about these boys of yours; you wanted to speak to me?’", "\"그건 그렇고, 선생님 그 아이들 말입니다. 저한테 하실 말씀이 있다고요?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Snawley.", "\"네.\" 스놀리가 대답했어."], ["‘The fact is, I am not their father, Mr. Squeers.", "\"실은요, 스퀴어스 씨, 제가 저 애들 친아버지가 아닙니다."], ["I’m only their father-in-law.’", "그저 의붓아버지일 뿐이지요.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Is that it?’ said the schoolmaster.", "그렇습니까?\" 선생이 말했어."], ["‘That explains it at once.", "\"그러면 단번에 설명이 되는군요."], ["I was wondering what the devil you were going to send them to Yorkshire for.", "대체 왜 애들을 요크셔까지 보내려는 건가 했거든요."], ["Ha! ha!", "하하!"], ["Oh, I understand now.’", "오, 이제 알겠습니다.\""]], [["‘You see I have married the mother,’ pursued Snawley; ‘it’s expensive keeping boys at home, and as she has a little money in her own right, I am afraid (women are so very foolish, Mr. Squeers) that she might be led to squander it on them, which would be their ruin, you know.’", "\"보시다시피 제가 애들 어미와 결혼했지요.\" 스놀리가 말을 이었어. \"애들을 집에 데리고 있자니 돈이 많이 들고요, 게다가 그 여자가 제 명의로 가진 돈이 좀 있거든요. 그런데 (여자들이란 게 어찌나 어리석은지요, 스퀴어스 씨) 그 돈을 애들한테 쏟아부을까 봐 걱정입니다. 그랬다간 애들 신세를 망치는 거 아니겠습니까.\""]], [["‘I see,’ returned Squeers, throwing himself back in his chair, and waving his hand.", "\"알겠습니다.\" 스퀴어스가 의자에 등을 기대며 손을 내저었어."]], [["‘And this,’ resumed Snawley, ‘has made me anxious to put them to some school a good distance off, where there are no holidays--none of those ill-judged coming home twice a year that unsettle children’s minds so--and where they may rough it a little--you comprehend?’", "\"그래서요.\" 스놀리가 다시 말을 이었어. \"애들을 멀찌감치 떨어진 어디 학교에 보내고 싶어서 안달이 났습니다. 방학도 없고요——일 년에 두 번씩 집에 와서 애들 마음을 들쑤셔 놓는 그런 분별없는 짓도 없는 데요. 그리고 좀 거칠게 굴러도 되는 데 말입니다——무슨 말인지 아시겠지요?\""]], [["‘The payments regular, and no questions asked,’ said Squeers, nodding his head.", "\"돈은 꼬박꼬박, 그리고 이러쿵저러쿵 묻는 일은 없게요.\" 스퀴어스가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했어."]], [["‘That’s it, exactly,’ rejoined the other.", "\"바로 그겁니다.\" 상대가 대꾸했어."], ["‘Morals strictly attended to, though.’", "\"그래도 도덕만큼은 철저히 신경 써 주시고요.\""]], [["‘Strictly,’ said Squeers.", "\"철저히 하겠습니다.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."]], [["‘Not too much writing home allowed, I suppose?’ said the father-in-law, hesitating.", "\"집에 편지 쓰는 건 너무 많이는 허락하지 않으시겠지요?\" 의붓아버지가 머뭇거리며 말했어."]], [["‘None, except a circular at Christmas, to say they never were so happy, and hope they may never be sent for,’ rejoined Squeers.", "\"전혀요. 크리스마스에 회람 한 장 보내는 것 말고는요. 거기에 이렇게 어디 한가지로 행복한 적이 없으며, 부디 데려가지 마시기를 바란다고 적게 하지요.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Nothing could be better,’ said the father-in-law, rubbing his hands.", "\"그보다 좋을 순 없겠군요.\" 의붓아버지가 두 손을 비비며 말했어."]], [["‘Then, as we understand each other,’ said Squeers, ‘will you allow me to ask you whether you consider me a highly virtuous, exemplary, and well-conducted man in private life; and whether, as a person whose business it is to take charge of youth, you place the strongest confidence in my unimpeachable integrity, liberality, religious principles, and ability?’", "\"그럼 우리가 서로 통했으니 말입니다.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"제가 한 가지 여쭤봐도 될까요? 선생님께서는 저를 사생활에서도 대단히 덕망 있고, 모범적이며, 행실 바른 사람이라고 여기시는지요? 그리고 아이들을 맡아 돌보는 일을 하는 사람으로서, 제 흠잡을 데 없는 성실함과 너그러움, 신앙심, 그리고 능력에 가장 큰 신뢰를 두고 계시는지요?\""]], [["‘Certainly I do,’ replied the father-in-law, reciprocating the schoolmaster’s grin.", "\"물론입니다.\" 의붓아버지가 선생의 씩 웃는 미소를 똑같이 되돌려 주며 대답했어."]], [["‘Perhaps you won’t object to say that, if I make you a reference?’", "\"제가 선생님을 보증인으로 댄다면, 그렇게 말씀해 주시는 데 거리낌은 없으시겠지요?\""]], [["‘Not the least in the world.’", "\"조금도 없습니다.\""]], [["‘That’s your sort!’ said Squeers, taking up a pen; ‘this is doing business, and that’s what I like.’", "\"바로 그거죠!\" 스퀴어스가 펜을 집어 들며 말했어. \"이게 바로 일하는 거고, 제가 좋아하는 게 이런 겁니다.\""]], [["Having entered Mr.", "스놀리 씨의 주소를 적은 다음, 선생은 그보다 더 흐뭇한 일을 처리해야 했어. 첫 분기 수업료를 미리 받았다는 영수증을 적는 일이었지."], ["Snawley’s address, the schoolmaster had next to perform the still more agreeable office of entering the receipt of the first quarter’s payment in advance, which he had scarcely completed, when another voice was heard inquiring for Mr. Squeers.", "그걸 채 다 적기도 전에, 또 다른 목소리가 스퀴어스 씨를 찾는 게 들려왔어."]], [["‘Here he is,’ replied the schoolmaster; ‘what is it?’", "\"여기 있습니다.\" 선생이 대답했어. \"무슨 일이시죠?\""]], [["‘Only a matter of business, sir,’ said Ralph Nickleby, presenting himself, closely followed by Nicholas.", "\"그저 일이 좀 있어서요, 선생님.\" 랄프 니클비가 모습을 드러내며 말했어."], ["‘There was an advertisement of yours in the papers this morning?’", "그 바로 뒤에는 니콜라스가 따라왔지. \"오늘 아침 신문에 선생님 광고가 났던데요?\""]], [["‘There was, sir.", "\"났습니다, 선생님."], ["This way, if you please,’ said Squeers, who had by this time got back to the box by the fire-place.", "이쪽으로 오시지요.\" 그새 벽난로 옆 칸 자리로 돌아와 있던 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘Won’t you be seated?’", "\"앉으시겠습니까?\""]], [["‘Why, I think I will,’ replied Ralph, suiting the action to the word, and placing his hat on the table before him.", "\"음, 그러는 게 좋겠군요.\" 랄프가 말에 맞춰 행동하며, 모자를 앞 탁자에 올려놓고 대답했어."], ["‘This is my nephew, sir, Mr Nicholas Nickleby.’", "\"이쪽은 제 조카입니다, 선생님. 니콜라스 니클비입니다.\""]], [["‘How do you do, sir?’ said Squeers.", "\"안녕하십니까?\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."]], [["Nicholas bowed, said he was very well, and seemed very much astonished at the outward appearance of the proprietor of Dotheboys Hall: as indeed he was.", "니콜라스는 고개를 숙여 인사하며 잘 지낸다고 했어. 그러면서 두더보이즈 홀 교장의 겉모습에 무척 놀란 것 같았어. 실제로도 그랬고 말이야."]], [["‘Perhaps you recollect me?’ said Ralph, looking narrowly at the schoolmaster.", "\"혹시 저를 기억하십니까?\" 랄프가 선생을 빤히 들여다보며 말했어."]], [["‘You paid me a small account at each of my half-yearly visits to town, for some years, I think, sir,’ replied Squeers.", "\"몇 년 동안, 반년에 한 번씩 시내에 오실 때마다 제게 적은 액수를 치르신 분 같은데요, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."]], [["‘I did,’ rejoined Ralph.", "\"그랬지요.\" 랄프가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘For the parents of a boy named Dorker, who unfortunately--’", "\"도커라는 아이의 부모님을 위해서였죠. 그 애가 불행히도——\""]], [["‘--unfortunately died at Dotheboys Hall,’ said Ralph, finishing the sentence.", "\"——불행히도 두더보이즈 홀에서 죽었지요.\" 랄프가 문장을 마저 끝냈어."]], [["‘I remember very well, sir,’ rejoined Squeers.", "\"아주 잘 기억합니다, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어."], ["‘Ah! Mrs.", "\"아!"], ["Squeers, sir, was as partial to that lad as if he had been her own; the attention, sir, that was bestowed upon that boy in his illness!", "스퀴어스 부인이요, 선생님, 그 애를 꼭 제 자식처럼 아꼈답니다. 그 애가 앓을 때 쏟은 정성이라니, 선생님!"], ["Dry toast and warm tea offered him every night and morning when he couldn’t swallow anything--a candle in his bedroom on the very night he died--the best dictionary sent up for him to lay his head upon--I don’t regret it though.", "그 애가 아무것도 못 삼킬 때 밤이고 아침이고 마른 토스트와 따뜻한 차를 권했고요——죽던 바로 그날 밤에는 그 애 침실에 촛불까지 켜 줬지요——머리를 받치라고 제일 좋은 사전까지 올려보냈고요——그래도 저는 후회하지 않습니다."], ["It is a pleasant thing to reflect that one did one’s duty by him.’", "그 애한테 도리를 다했다고 돌아보는 건 흐뭇한 일이니까요.\""]], [["Ralph smiled, as if he meant anything but smiling, and looked round at the strangers present.", "랄프는 미소를 지었어. 그런데 미소 말고는 그 무엇을 뜻하는 듯한 미소였지. 그러고는 거기 있던 낯선 사람들을 둘러봤어."]], [["‘These are only some pupils of mine,’ said Wackford Squeers, pointing to the little boy on the trunk and the two little boys on the floor, who had been staring at each other without uttering a word, and writhing their bodies into most remarkable contortions, according to the custom of little boys when they first become acquainted. ‘This gentleman, sir, is a parent who is kind enough to compliment me upon the course of education adopted at Dotheboys Hall, which is situated, sir, at the delightful village of Dotheboys, near Greta Bridge in Yorkshire, where youth are boarded, clothed, booked, washed, furnished with pocket-money--’", "\"이 애들은 그저 제 학생들 몇입니다.\" 와크포드 스퀴어스가 궤짝 위의 사내아이와 바닥에 있는 두 사내아이를 가리키며 말했어. 그 둘은 말 한마디 없이 서로를 빤히 쳐다보면서, 처음 알게 된 사내아이들이 으레 그러듯 별별 희한한 모양으로 몸을 비비 꼬고 있었지. \"이 신사분은요, 선생님, 두더보이즈 홀에서 채택한 교육 방식을 친히 칭찬해 주시는 학부형이십니다. 두더보이즈 홀은요, 선생님, 요크셔의 그레타 다리 근처, 그 아름다운 두더보이즈 마을에 있는데요, 거기서는 아이들에게 숙식과 옷, 책, 빨래, 용돈을——\""]], [["‘Yes, we know all about that, sir,’ interrupted Ralph, testily.", "\"네, 그건 다 압니다, 선생님.\" 랄프가 짜증스럽게 끼어들었어."], ["‘It’s in the advertisement.’", "\"광고에 나와 있던데요.\""]], [["‘You are very right, sir; it IS in the advertisement,’ replied Squeers.", "\"맞는 말씀입니다, 선생님. 광고에 나와 있지요.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."]], [["‘And in the matter of fact besides,’ interrupted Mr. Snawley.", "\"게다가 사실이 그렇기도 하고요.\""], ["‘I feel bound to assure you, sir, and I am proud to have this opportunity OF assuring you, that I consider Mr.", "스놀리 씨가 끼어들었어. \"선생님께 꼭 확실히 말씀드려야겠습니다."], ["Squeers a gentleman highly virtuous, exemplary, well conducted, and--’", "그리고 이렇게 말씀드릴 기회가 있어서 자랑스럽습니다만, 저는 스퀴어스 씨를 대단히 덕망 있고, 모범적이며, 행실 바른 신사로——\""]], [["‘I make no doubt of it, sir,’ interrupted Ralph, checking the torrent of recommendation; ‘no doubt of it at all.", "\"의심하지 않습니다, 선생님.\" 랄프가 추천의 봇물을 막으며 끼어들었어."], ["Suppose we come to business?’", "\"조금도 의심하지 않아요. 그럼 본론으로 들어갈까요?\""]], [["‘With all my heart, sir,’ rejoined Squeers.", "\"기꺼이 그러지요, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어."], ["‘“Never postpone business,”", "\"'일을 미루지 말라.'"], ["is the very first lesson we instil into our commercial pupils.", "이게 바로 저희가 상업을 배우는 학생들에게 제일 먼저 새겨 주는 가르침이지요."], ["Master Belling, my dear, always remember that; do you hear?’", "벨링 군, 얘야, 그걸 늘 기억해라. 알겠니?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir,’ repeated Master Belling.", "\"네, 선생님.\" 벨링 군이 따라 말했어."]], [["‘He recollects what it is, does he?’ said Ralph.", "\"저 애가 그게 뭔지 기억하나요?\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Tell the gentleman,’ said Squeers.", "\"신사분께 말씀드려라.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."]], [["‘“Never,”’ repeated Master Belling.", "\"'말라.'\" 벨링 군이 따라 말했어."]], [["‘Very good,’ said Squeers; ‘go on.’", "\"아주 좋아.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"계속해.\""]], [["‘Never,’ repeated Master Belling again.", "\"말라.\" 벨링 군이 또 한 번 되풀이했어."]], [["‘Very good indeed,’ said Squeers.", "\"정말 잘했다.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘Yes.’", "\"그래.\""]], [["‘P,’ suggested Nicholas, good-naturedly.", "\"미.\" 니콜라스가 사람 좋게 거들었어."]], [["‘Perform--business!’ said Master Belling.", "\"미루지——일을!\" 벨링 군이 말했어."], ["‘Never--perform--business!’", "\"말라——미루지——일을!\""]], [["‘Very well, sir,’ said Squeers, darting a withering look at the culprit.", "\"좋아.\" 스퀴어스가 그 죄인에게 따끔한 눈길을 쏘아붙이며 말했어."], ["‘You and I will perform a little business on our private account by-and-by.’", "\"너랑 나는 이따가 우리끼리 따로 일을 좀 처리하자꾸나.\""]], [["‘And just now,’ said Ralph, ‘we had better transact our own, perhaps.’", "\"그리고 지금은,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"우리 일부터 처리하는 게 낫겠군요.\""]], [["‘If you please,’ said Squeers.", "\"그러시지요.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."]], [["‘Well,’ resumed Ralph, ‘it’s brief enough; soon broached; and I hope easily concluded. You have advertised for an able assistant, sir?’", "\"자.\" 랄프가 다시 말을 이었어. \"아주 간단합니다. 금방 꺼낼 수 있고, 쉽게 매듭지을 수 있기를 바랍니다. 선생님께서 유능한 조교를 구한다는 광고를 내셨지요?\""]], [["‘Precisely so,’ said Squeers.", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."]], [["‘And you really want one?’", "\"그리고 정말로 한 사람 필요하시고요?\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ answered Squeers.", "\"물론이지요.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Here he is!’ said Ralph. ‘My nephew Nicholas, hot from school, with everything he learnt there, fermenting in his head, and nothing fermenting in his pocket, is just the man you want.’", "\"여기 있습니다!\" 랄프가 말했어. \"제 조카 니콜라스입니다. 학교를 갓 나와서, 거기서 배운 게 죄다 머릿속에서 부글거리고 있지요. 그런데 주머니에서는 아무것도 부글거리지 않거든요. 선생님께 딱 맞는 사람입니다.\""]], [["‘I am afraid,’ said Squeers, perplexed with such an application from a youth of Nicholas’s figure, ‘I am afraid the young man won’t suit me.’", "\"글쎄요.\" 니콜라스 같은 풍채의 젊은이가 이런 자리에 지원한다는 게 어리둥절했던 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"이 젊은이는 저한테 안 맞을 것 같은데요.\""]], [["‘Yes, he will,’ said Ralph; ‘I know better. Don’t be cast down, sir; you will be teaching all the young noblemen in Dotheboys Hall in less than a week’s time, unless this gentleman is more obstinate than I take him to be.’", "\"아니요, 맞습니다.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"제가 더 잘 압니다. 풀 죽지 마십시오, 선생님. 일주일도 안 되어 두더보이즈 홀의 어린 귀족 자제들을 모조리 가르치게 될 겁니다. 이 신사분이 제 짐작보다 더 고집불통이 아니라면 말이지요.\""]], [["‘I fear, sir,’ said Nicholas, addressing Mr.", "\"제가 걱정되는 건요, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 스퀴어스 씨에게 말했어."], ["Squeers, ‘that you object to my youth, and to my not being a Master of Arts?’", "\"제 나이가 어리고, 제가 석사 학위가 없다는 점이 마음에 걸리시는 거지요?\""]], [["‘The absence of a college degree IS an objection,’ replied Squeers, looking as grave as he could, and considerably puzzled, no less by the contrast between the simplicity of the nephew and the worldly manner of the uncle, than by the incomprehensible allusion to the young noblemen under his tuition.", "\"대학 학위가 없다는 게 걸리긴 합니다.\" 스퀴어스가 최대한 진지한 표정을 지으며 대답했어. 그는 적잖이 어리둥절했지. 조카의 순박함과 삼촌의 약삭빠른 태도가 어찌나 대조적인지, 그리고 자기가 가르치는 어린 귀족 자제들이라는 알 수 없는 소리가 또 어찌나 영문 모를 일인지 말이야."]], [["‘Look here, sir,’ said Ralph; ‘I’ll put this matter in its true light in two seconds.’", "\"이보세요, 선생님.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"이 일을 제가 단 이 초 만에 제대로 밝혀 드리지요.\""]], [["‘If you’ll have the goodness,’ rejoined Squeers.", "\"그래 주신다면야.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘This is a boy, or a youth, or a lad, or a young man, or a hobbledehoy, or whatever you like to call him, of eighteen or nineteen, or thereabouts,’ said Ralph.", "\"이 애는 소년이라 하든, 청년이라 하든, 풋내기라 하든, 젊은이라 하든, 머슴애라 하든, 뭐라고 부르든 좋습니다만, 열여덟이나 열아홉쯤 됐습니다.\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘That I see,’ observed the schoolmaster.", "\"그건 저도 보면 압니다.\" 선생이 말했어."]], [["‘So do I,’ said Mr.", "\"저도 그렇고요.\""], ["Snawley, thinking it as well to back his new friend occasionally.", "스놀리 씨가 가끔은 새로 사귄 친구를 거들어 주는 게 좋겠다 싶어 말했어."]], [["‘His father is dead, he is wholly ignorant of the world, has no resources whatever, and wants something to do,’ said Ralph.", "\"아비는 죽었고, 세상 물정은 전혀 모르고, 가진 밑천이라곤 하나도 없고, 뭐든 할 일이 필요한 애지요.\""], ["‘I recommend him to this splendid establishment of yours, as an opening which will lead him to fortune if he turns it to proper account.", "랄프가 말했어. \"제대로만 활용하면 출세길로 이어질 자리라 여겨서, 선생님의 이 훌륭한 학교를 추천하는 겁니다."], ["Do you see that?’", "이해되시지요?\""]], [["‘Everybody must see that,’ replied Squeers, half imitating the sneer with which the old gentleman was regarding his unconscious relative.", "\"누가 봐도 그렇지요.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. 영문 모를 조카를 바라보는 노신사의 그 비웃음을 반쯤 따라 지으면서 말이야."]], [["‘I do, of course,’ said Nicholas, eagerly.", "\"물론 저도 그렇게 생각합니다.\" 니콜라스가 들떠서 말했어."]], [["‘He does, of course, you observe,’ said Ralph, in the same dry, hard manner.", "\"보다시피 저 애도 물론 그렇게 생각하고요.\" 랄프가 여전히 메마르고 딱딱한 투로 말했어."], ["‘If any caprice of temper should induce him to cast aside this golden opportunity before he has brought it to perfection, I consider myself absolved from extending any assistance to his mother and sister.", "\"혹시라도 변덕이 도져서 이 황금 같은 기회를 제대로 익히기도 전에 내팽개친다면, 저는 저 애 어미와 누이에게 도움을 줄 의무에서 완전히 벗어나는 거라고 봅니다. 저 애를 보세요."], ["Look at him, and think of the use he may be to you in half-a-dozen ways!", "그리고 저 애를 여러모로 어떻게 부려먹을 수 있을지 생각해 보십시오!"], ["Now, the question is, whether, for some time to come at all events, he won’t serve your purpose better than twenty of the kind of people you would get under ordinary circumstances.", "자, 문제는 이겁니다. 적어도 앞으로 한동안은, 저 애가 보통 때 같으면 구할 만한 그런 사람 스무 명보다 선생님 목적에 더 잘 맞지 않겠느냐는 거지요."], ["Isn’t that a question for consideration?’", "한번 생각해 볼 만한 문제 아닙니까?\""]], [["‘Yes, it is,’ said Squeers, answering a nod of Ralph’s head with a nod of his own.", "\"그렇군요, 그렇습니다.\" 스퀴어스가 랄프의 고갯짓에 자기도 고개를 끄덕여 답하며 말했어."]], [["‘Good,’ rejoined Ralph.", "\"좋습니다.\" 랄프가 대꾸했어."], ["‘Let me have two words with you.’", "\"잠깐 따로 두어 마디 나누지요.\""]], [["The two words were had apart; in a couple of minutes Mr.", "두 사람은 떨어져서 두어 마디를 나눴어."], ["Wackford Squeers announced that Mr. Nicholas Nickleby was, from that moment, thoroughly nominated to, and installed in, the office of first assistant master at Dotheboys Hall.", "그리고 몇 분 뒤, 와크포드 스퀴어스 씨는 이 순간부터 니콜라스 니클비 씨가 두더보이즈 홀의 수석 조교 자리에 정식으로 임명되고 그 직에 앉게 되었다고 선언했지."]], [["‘Your uncle’s recommendation has done it, Mr.", "\"삼촌분의 추천 덕분입니다, 니클비 씨.\""], ["Nickleby,’ said Wackford Squeers.", "와크포드 스퀴어스가 말했어."]], [["Nicholas, overjoyed at his success, shook his uncle’s hand warmly, and could almost have worshipped Squeers upon the spot.", "니콜라스는 일이 잘 풀린 게 너무 기뻐서 삼촌의 손을 따뜻하게 잡고 흔들었어. 그리고 그 자리에서 스퀴어스를 떠받들 뻔했지."]], [["‘He is an odd-looking man,’ thought Nicholas.", "'생김새가 좀 별나기는 해.' 니콜라스는 생각했어."], ["‘What of that?", "'하지만 그게 무슨 대수람?"], ["Porson was an odd-looking man, and so was Doctor Johnson; all these bookworms are.’", "포슨도 생김새가 별났고, 존슨 박사도 그랬잖아. 책벌레들이란 다 그런 거야.'"]], [["‘At eight o’clock tomorrow morning, Mr.", "\"내일 아침 여덟 시에요, 니클비 씨.\""], ["Nickleby,’ said Squeers, ‘the coach starts.", "스퀴어스가 말했어. \"마차가 출발합니다."], ["You must be here at a quarter before, as we take these boys with us.’", "이 애들을 데리고 가야 하니, 십오 분 전까지는 여기 와 계셔야 합니다.\""]], [["‘Certainly, sir,’ said Nicholas.", "\"알겠습니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘And your fare down, I have paid,’ growled Ralph.", "\"그리고 내려가는 마찻삯은 내가 다 치렀다.\""], ["‘So, you’ll have nothing to do but keep yourself warm.’", "랄프가 으르렁거렸어. \"그러니 너는 몸이나 따뜻하게 하고 있으면 돼.\""]], [["Here was another instance of his uncle’s generosity!", "이거야말로 삼촌의 또 한 번의 너그러움이 아니고 무엇이겠어!"], ["Nicholas felt his unexpected kindness so much, that he could scarcely find words to thank him; indeed, he had not found half enough, when they took leave of the schoolmaster, and emerged from the Saracen’s Head gateway.", "니콜라스는 뜻밖의 그 친절이 어찌나 가슴에 사무쳤던지, 고맙다는 말을 어찌 꺼내야 할지 모를 지경이었어. 정말이지 절반의 절반도 채 못 한 채, 두 사람은 선생과 작별하고 사라센스 헤드의 정문을 나섰지."]], [["‘I shall be here in the morning to see you fairly off,’ said Ralph.", "\"아침에 네가 무사히 떠나는 걸 보러 여기 오마.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘No skulking!’", "\"꽁무니 빼지 말고!\""]], [["‘Thank you, sir,’ replied Nicholas; ‘I never shall forget this kindness.’", "\"고맙습니다, 삼촌.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"이 친절은 절대 잊지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Take care you don’t,’ replied his uncle.", "\"잊지 않도록 해라.\" 삼촌이 대답했어."], ["‘You had better go home now, and pack up what you have got to pack.", "\"이제 집에 가서 챙겨 갈 것들을 꾸리는 게 좋겠다."], ["Do you think you could find your way to Golden Square first?’", "그 전에 골든 스퀘어까지 찾아갈 수 있겠니?\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ said Nicholas.", "\"그럼요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘I can easily inquire.’", "\"물어물어 가면 되지요.\""]], [["‘Leave these papers with my clerk, then,’ said Ralph, producing a small parcel, ‘and tell him to wait till I come home.’", "\"그럼 이 서류들을 내 서기에게 맡기고,\" 랄프가 작은 꾸러미 하나를 꺼내며 말했어. \"내가 집에 올 때까지 기다리라고 일러라.\""]], [["Nicholas cheerfully undertook the errand, and bidding his worthy uncle an affectionate farewell, which that warm-hearted old gentleman acknowledged by a growl, hastened away to execute his commission.", "니콜라스는 기꺼이 그 심부름을 맡았어. 그리고 마음씨 좋은 삼촌에게 다정하게 작별 인사를 했지. 그 따뜻한 노신사는 으르렁거리는 소리로 답하고 말이야. 니콜라스는 분부를 받들러 서둘러 떠났어."]], [["He found Golden Square in due course; Mr.", "이윽고 니콜라스는 골든 스퀘어를 찾아냈어."], ["Noggs, who had stepped out for a minute or so to the public-house, was opening the door with a latch-key, as he reached the steps.", "잠깐 술집에 다녀온 노그스 씨가, 마침 그가 계단에 다다랐을 때 걸쇠 열쇠로 문을 열고 있었지."]], [["‘What’s that?’ inquired Noggs, pointing to the parcel.", "\"그게 뭔가?\" 노그스가 꾸러미를 가리키며 물었어."]], [["‘Papers from my uncle,’ replied Nicholas; ‘and you’re to have the goodness to wait till he comes home, if you please.’", "\"삼촌이 주신 서류예요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그리고 삼촌이 집에 올 때까지 기다려 주시면 고맙겠다고 하셨어요.\""]], [["‘Uncle!’ cried Noggs.", "\"삼촌!\" 노그스가 소리쳤어."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"니클비 씨요.\""], ["Nickleby,’ said Nicholas in explanation.", "니콜라스가 설명했어."]], [["‘Come in,’ said Newman.", "\"들어오게.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."]], [["Without another word he led Nicholas into the passage, and thence into the official pantry at the end of it, where he thrust him into a chair, and mounting upon his high stool, sat, with his arms hanging, straight down by his sides, gazing fixedly upon him, as from a tower of observation.", "그는 더 말없이 니콜라스를 복도로 데려갔어. 그리고 복도 끝에 있는 사무실 식료품 방으로 안내했지. 거기서 니콜라스를 의자에 떠밀어 앉히고는, 자기는 높은 걸상에 올라앉아 두 팔을 옆구리에 똑바로 늘어뜨린 채 앉았어. 그러고는 마치 망루에서 내려다보듯 니콜라스를 빤히 바라봤지."]], [["‘There is no answer,’ said Nicholas, laying the parcel on a table beside him.", "\"답장은 없어요.\" 니콜라스가 꾸러미를 옆 탁자에 내려놓으며 말했어."]], [["Newman said nothing, but folding his arms, and thrusting his head forward so as to obtain a nearer view of Nicholas’s face, scanned his features closely.", "뉴먼은 아무 말도 하지 않았어. 그저 두 팔을 포개고 고개를 앞으로 쭉 내밀어 니콜라스의 얼굴을 더 가까이 보더니, 그 생김새를 찬찬히 뜯어봤지."]], [["‘No answer,’ said Nicholas, speaking very loud, under the impression that Newman Noggs was deaf.", "\"답장 없어요.\" 니콜라스가 뉴먼 노그스가 귀가 먹은 줄 알고 아주 크게 말했어."]], [["Newman placed his hands upon his knees, and, without uttering a syllable, continued the same close scrutiny of his companion’s face.", "뉴먼은 두 손을 무릎에 얹은 채, 한마디도 내뱉지 않고 상대의 얼굴을 똑같이 빤히 뜯어보길 계속했어."]], [["This was such a very singular proceeding on the part of an utter stranger, and his appearance was so extremely peculiar, that Nicholas, who had a sufficiently keen sense of the ridiculous, could not refrain from breaking into a smile as he inquired whether Mr.", "생판 모르는 사람이 이렇게 군다는 게 어찌나 별난 노릇인지, 게다가 그 생김새도 어찌나 별스러운지, 우스운 걸 제법 잘 알아채는 니콜라스는 그만 빙긋 웃음을 터뜨리고 말았어."], ["Noggs had any commands for him.", "그러면서 노그스 씨가 자기한테 무슨 시킬 일이라도 있느냐고 물었지."]], [["Noggs shook his head and sighed; upon which Nicholas rose, and remarking that he required no rest, bade him good-morning.", "노그스는 고개를 젓고 한숨을 쉬었어. 그러자 니콜라스가 일어서서, 자기는 쉬지 않아도 된다고 말하고는 잘 있으라고 인사했어."]], [["It was a great exertion for Newman Noggs, and nobody knows to this day how he ever came to make it, the other party being wholly unknown to him, but he drew a long breath and actually said, out loud, without once stopping, that if the young gentleman did not object to tell, he should like to know what his uncle was going to do for him.", "뉴먼 노그스로서는 엄청난 용기를 낸 거였어. 상대는 자기가 전혀 모르는 사람이었으니, 그가 대체 어쩌다 그런 말을 하게 됐는지는 오늘날까지도 아무도 몰라. 아무튼 뉴먼은 길게 숨을 들이쉬더니, 정말로 소리 내어, 한 번도 멈추지 않고 이렇게 말했어. 젊은이가 괜찮다면, 삼촌이 그를 위해 무엇을 해 줄 셈인지 알고 싶다고 말이야."]], [["Nicholas had not the least objection in the world, but on the contrary was rather pleased to have an opportunity of talking on the subject which occupied his thoughts; so, he sat down again, and (his sanguine imagination warming as he spoke) entered into a fervent and glowing description of all the honours and advantages to be derived from his appointment at that seat of learning, Dotheboys Hall.", "니콜라스로서는 그걸 마다할 까닭이 조금도 없었어. 오히려 머릿속을 가득 채운 그 일을 이야기할 기회가 생긴 게 반가웠지. 그래서 다시 자리에 앉아, (말을 하면서 그의 낙천적인 상상이 점점 달아올라서) 두더보이즈 홀이라는 그 배움의 터에 임명된 데서 얻게 될 온갖 명예와 이점을 열렬하고 신나게 늘어놓기 시작했어."]], [["‘But, what’s the matter--are you ill?’ said Nicholas, suddenly breaking off, as his companion, after throwing himself into a variety of uncouth attitudes, thrust his hands under the stool, and cracked his finger-joints as if he were snapping all the bones in his hands.", "\"그런데, 무슨 일이세요——어디 편찮으세요?\" 니콜라스가 갑자기 말을 뚝 끊으며 물었어. 상대가 별별 괴상한 자세를 취하더니 두 손을 걸상 밑으로 쑤셔 넣고는, 손뼈를 죄다 부러뜨리기라도 하듯 손가락 마디를 우두둑거렸거든."]], [["Newman Noggs made no reply, but went on shrugging his shoulders and cracking his finger-joints; smiling horribly all the time, and looking steadfastly at nothing, out of the tops of his eyes, in a most ghastly manner.", "뉴먼 노그스는 대꾸도 하지 않고, 계속 어깨를 으쓱거리며 손가락 마디를 우두둑거렸어. 그러면서 내내 끔찍하게 웃었고, 눈을 치켜뜬 채 더없이 섬뜩한 표정으로 허공의 아무것도 아닌 데를 뚫어지게 바라봤지."]], [["At first, Nicholas thought the mysterious man was in a fit, but, on further consideration, decided that he was in liquor, under which circumstances he deemed it prudent to make off at once.", "처음에 니콜라스는 그 알 수 없는 사내가 발작을 일으킨 줄 알았어. 그런데 다시 생각해 보니 술에 취한 거라는 결론을 내렸지."], ["He looked back when he had got the street-door open.", "그렇다면 당장 자리를 뜨는 게 현명하겠다 싶었어."], ["Newman Noggs was still indulging in the same extraordinary gestures, and the cracking of his fingers sounded louder that ever.", "그는 현관문을 열고서 뒤를 돌아봤어. 뉴먼 노그스는 여전히 그 별난 몸짓을 이어가고 있었고, 손가락 우두둑거리는 소리는 그 어느 때보다도 크게 울렸지."]], [["# CHAPTER 5", "# CHAPTER 5"]], [["Nicholas starts for Yorkshire.", "눈물방울이 트렁크에 떨어져서 그 주인을 슬픔과 불행으로부터 지켜 주는 부적이 된다면, 니콜라스 니클비는 더없이 행복한 조짐 속에서 여행을 시작하는 셈이었을 거야. 할 일은 너무 많은데 그 일을 할 시간은 너무 짧았어."], ["Of his Leave-taking and his Fellow-Travellers, and what befell them on the Road\nIf tears dropped into a trunk were charms to preserve its owner from sorrow and misfortune, Nicholas Nickleby would have commenced his expedition under most happy auspices.", "다정한 말은 그렇게 많이 떠올랐지만, 그 말이 솟아오르는 가슴마다 쓰라린 아픔이 가득해서 입 밖으로 나오는 걸 막았지. 그래서 여행을 위한 자그마한 준비조차 정말이지 슬픈 분위기 속에서 이루어졌어."], ["There was so much to be done, and so little time to do it in; so many kind words to be spoken, and such bitter pain in the hearts in which they rose to impede their utterance; that the little preparations for his journey were made mournfully indeed.", "어머니와 누이가 애써 챙기며 그의 편안함에 꼭 필요하다고 여긴 물건이 백 가지나 됐지만, 니콜라스는 그걸 두고 가겠다고 우겼어. 나중에 쓸모가 있을지도 모르고, 필요할 땐 돈으로 바꿀 수도 있다는 거였지."], ["A hundred things which the anxious care of his mother and sister deemed indispensable for his comfort, Nicholas insisted on leaving behind, as they might prove of some after use, or might be convertible into money if occasion required.", "이런 일들을 두고 다정한 실랑이가 백 번도 더 벌어졌어. 떠나기 전날, 그 슬픈 밤에 말이야."], ["A hundred affectionate contests on such points as these, took place on the sad night which preceded his departure; and, as the termination of every angerless dispute brought them nearer and nearer to the close of their slight preparations, Kate grew busier and busier, and wept more silently.", "그리고 화 한 점 없는 그 다툼이 한 번씩 끝날 때마다 보잘것없는 준비도 점점 마무리에 가까워졌어. 케이트는 점점 더 바삐 손을 놀렸고, 점점 더 소리 없이 울었지."]], [["The box was packed at last, and then there came supper, with some little delicacy provided for the occasion, and as a set-off against the expense of which, Kate and her mother had feigned to dine when Nicholas was out. The poor lad nearly choked himself by attempting to partake of it, and almost suffocated himself in affecting a jest or two, and forcing a melancholy laugh. Thus, they lingered on till the hour of separating for the night was long past; and then they found that they might as well have given vent to their real feelings before, for they could not suppress them, do what they would. So, they let them have their way, and even that was a relief.", "마침내 상자가 다 꾸려졌어. 그러고는 저녁 식사가 나왔어. 이날을 위해 마련한 자그마한 별미도 곁들였지. 그 비용을 메우려고 케이트와 어머니는 니콜라스가 밖에 나가 있을 때 밥을 먹은 척했어. 가엾은 소년은 그걸 좀 먹어 보려다 하마터면 목이 멜 뻔했어. 그리고 농담을 한두 마디 던지고 억지로 쓸쓸한 웃음을 지어 보이다가 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이 됐지. 그렇게 세 사람은 밤에 헤어질 시간이 한참 지나도록 자리에 머물러 있었어. 그러다 깨달았지. 진작 속마음을 다 털어놓는 편이 나았을 거라고 말이야. 아무리 애를 써도 도무지 감정을 억누를 수가 없었거든. 그래서 그들은 감정이 흐르는 대로 내버려 두었어. 그것만으로도 마음이 한결 가벼워졌지."]], [["Nicholas slept well till six next morning; dreamed of home, or of what was home once--no matter which, for things that are changed or gone will come back as they used to be, thank God! in sleep--and rose quite brisk and gay. He wrote a few lines in pencil, to say the goodbye which he was afraid to pronounce himself, and laying them, with half his scanty stock of money, at his sister’s door, shouldered his box and crept softly downstairs.", "니콜라스는 이튿날 아침 여섯 시까지 푹 잤어. 집 꿈을 꾸었지. 아니, 한때 집이었던 곳 꿈을. 어느 쪽이든 상관없었어. 변하거나 사라진 것도 꿈속에서는 옛 모습 그대로 돌아오니까. 정말 고마운 일이지! 그는 아주 기운차고 명랑한 기분으로 일어났어. 그리고 연필로 몇 줄을 적었어. 직접 입으로 꺼내기가 두려웠던 작별 인사를 쓴 거야. 그 쪽지를 얼마 안 되는 돈의 절반과 함께 누이의 방문 앞에 놓아두고는, 상자를 어깨에 둘러메고 살금살금 아래층으로 내려갔어."]], [["‘Is that you, Hannah?’ cried a voice from Miss La Creevy’s sitting-room, whence shone the light of a feeble candle.", "\"너니, 해나?\" 라 크리비 양의 거실에서 목소리가 흘러나왔어. 거기서 가냘픈 촛불 빛이 새어 나오고 있었지."]], [["‘It is I, Miss La Creevy,’ said Nicholas, putting down the box and looking in.", "\"저예요, 라 크리비 양.\" 니콜라스가 상자를 내려놓고 안을 들여다보며 말했어."]], [["‘Bless us!’ exclaimed Miss La Creevy, starting and putting her hand to her curl-papers.", "\"아이고머니!\" 라 크리비 양이 흠칫 놀라더니 머리에 만 종이를 손으로 가렸어."], ["‘You’re up very early, Mr. Nickleby.’", "\"정말 일찍 일어나셨네요, 니클비 씨.\""]], [["‘So are you,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"라 크리비 양도 그러신데요.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘It’s the fine arts that bring me out of bed, Mr.", "\"저는 예술 때문에 침대에서 일어난 거랍니다, 니클비 씨.\""], ["Nickleby,’ returned the lady.", "부인이 말했어."], ["‘I’m waiting for the light to carry out an idea.’", "\"어떤 아이디어를 살리려고 빛이 들기를 기다리는 중이거든요.\""]], [["Miss La Creevy had got up early to put a fancy nose into a miniature of an ugly little boy, destined for his grandmother in the country, who was expected to bequeath him property if he was like the family.", "라 크리비 양은 일찍 일어나서 못생긴 사내아이의 작은 초상화에 그럴듯한 코를 그려 넣으려던 참이었어. 그 그림은 시골에 사는 아이의 할머니에게 보낼 거였지. 아이가 집안 식구를 닮았으면 재산을 물려주기로 되어 있는 할머니 말이야."]], [["‘To carry out an idea,’ repeated Miss La Creevy; ‘and that’s the great convenience of living in a thoroughfare like the Strand.", "\"아이디어를 살린다는 거예요.\" 라 크리비 양이 다시 말했어."], ["When I want a nose or an eye for any particular sitter, I have only to look out of window and wait till I get one.’", "\"그게 바로 스트랜드 같은 큰길가에 사는 큰 이점이지요. 어떤 손님한테 어울리는 코나 눈이 필요할 때면, 창밖을 내다보면서 마음에 드는 게 나타날 때까지 기다리기만 하면 되거든요.\""]], [["‘Does it take long to get a nose, now?’ inquired Nicholas, smiling.", "\"그럼 코 하나 구하는 데 오래 걸리나요?\" 니콜라스가 미소 지으며 물었어."]], [["‘Why, that depends in a great measure on the pattern,’ replied Miss La Creevy. ‘Snubs and Romans are plentiful enough, and there are flats of all sorts and sizes when there’s a meeting at Exeter Hall; but perfect aquilines, I am sorry to say, are scarce, and we generally use them for uniforms or public characters.’", "\"글쎄요, 그건 모양에 따라 많이 다르답니다.\" 라 크리비 양이 대답했어. \"들창코나 매부리코는 아주 흔해요. 그리고 엑서터 홀에 무슨 모임이 있을 때면 온갖 크기의 납작코도 널렸지요. 하지만 완벽한 매부리코는, 안타깝게도 아주 드물어요. 그래서 그런 코는 보통 군복 입은 사람이나 유명 인사를 그릴 때나 쓴답니다.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ said Nicholas.", "\"그렇군요!\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘If I should meet with any in my travels, I’ll endeavour to sketch them for you.’", "\"제가 여행하다가 그런 코를 만나면, 당신을 위해 스케치해 보도록 애써 볼게요.\""]], [["‘You don’t mean to say that you are really going all the way down into Yorkshire this cold winter’s weather, Mr.", "\"이 추운 겨울 날씨에 정말로 요크셔까지 그 먼 길을 내려가신다는 거예요, 니클비 씨?\""], ["Nickleby?’ said Miss La Creevy.", "라 크리비 양이 물었어."], ["‘I heard something of it last night.’", "\"어젯밤에 얼핏 들었거든요.\""]], [["‘I do, indeed,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"정말 그래요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘Needs must, you know, when somebody drives.", "\"누군가 등을 떠밀면 어쩔 수 없잖아요."], ["Necessity is my driver, and that is only another name for the same gentleman.’", "저를 떠미는 건 다름 아닌 형편이지요. 그게 바로 그 신사의 또 다른 이름이고요.\""]], [["‘Well, I am very sorry for it; that’s all I can say,’ said Miss La Creevy; ‘as much on your mother’s and sister’s account as on yours. Your sister is a very pretty young lady, Mr. Nickleby, and that is an additional reason why she should have somebody to protect her. I persuaded her to give me a sitting or two, for the street-door case. ‘Ah! she’ll make a sweet miniature.’ As Miss La Creevy spoke, she held up an ivory countenance intersected with very perceptible sky-blue veins, and regarded it with so much complacency, that Nicholas quite envied her.", "\"그것참, 안됐네요. 제가 할 수 있는 말은 그뿐이에요.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"당신만큼이나 어머니와 누이를 생각하면 더 그렇고요. 당신 누이는 아주 예쁜 아가씨예요, 니클비 씨. 그러니 더더욱 곁에서 지켜 줄 사람이 있어야 하지요. 제가 그 아가씨를 설득해서 한두 번 그림 모델을 서 달라고 했어요. 현관문에 걸어 둘 작품으로요. 아! 정말 사랑스러운 초상화가 나올 거예요.\" 라 크리비 양은 이렇게 말하면서 상아빛 얼굴 하나를 들어 올렸어. 그 얼굴에는 하늘색 핏줄이 또렷이 가로질러 있었지. 부인이 어찌나 흐뭇하게 그걸 바라보던지, 니콜라스는 그만 부러운 마음이 들 정도였어."]], [["‘If you ever have an opportunity of showing Kate some little kindness,’ said Nicholas, presenting his hand, ‘I think you will.’", "\"언젠가 케이트한테 작은 친절이라도 베풀 기회가 생기거든,\" 니콜라스가 손을 내밀며 말했어. \"꼭 그래 주시리라 믿어요.\""]], [["‘Depend upon that,’ said the good-natured miniature painter; ‘and God bless you, Mr.", "\"그건 믿으셔도 돼요.\" 마음씨 고운 초상화가가 말했어."], ["Nickleby; and I wish you well.’", "\"그리고 신의 가호가 있기를 빌어요, 니클비 씨. 잘되길 바랄게요.\""]], [["It was very little that Nicholas knew of the world, but he guessed enough about its ways to think, that if he gave Miss La Creevy one little kiss, perhaps she might not be the less kindly disposed towards those he was leaving behind.", "니콜라스는 세상 물정을 거의 몰랐지만, 그래도 이 정도는 짐작할 만큼은 알았어. 라 크리비 양에게 작은 입맞춤 하나를 건네면, 그가 두고 떠나는 식구들에게 부인이 한결 더 살갑게 대해 줄지도 모른다는 거였지."], ["So, he gave her three or four with a kind of jocose gallantry, and Miss La Creevy evinced no greater symptoms of displeasure than declaring, as she adjusted her yellow turban, that she had never heard of such a thing, and couldn’t have believed it possible.", "그래서 그는 짐짓 장난스럽게 정중한 시늉을 하며 서너 번 입을 맞췄어. 라 크리비 양은 노란 터번을 매만지면서, 이런 일은 처음 들어 본다느니, 도무지 믿을 수 없는 일이라느니 하는 것 말고는 별다른 못마땅한 기색을 보이지 않았지."]], [["Having terminated the unexpected interview in this satisfactory manner, Nicholas hastily withdrew himself from the house. By the time he had found a man to carry his box it was only seven o’clock, so he walked slowly on, a little in advance of the porter, and very probably with not half as light a heart in his breast as the man had, although he had no waistcoat to cover it with, and had evidently, from the appearance of his other garments, been spending the night in a stable, and taking his breakfast at a pump.", "이렇게 뜻밖의 만남을 흐뭇하게 마무리한 니콜라스는 서둘러 집을 나섰어. 상자를 들어 줄 사람을 구하고 나니 겨우 일곱 시였지. 그래서 그는 짐꾼보다 조금 앞서서 천천히 걸어갔어. 아마 가슴속 마음만 따지면 짐꾼의 절반만큼도 가볍지 않았을 거야. 그 짐꾼은 가슴을 덮을 조끼 한 벌도 없는 데다, 다른 옷차림을 봐서는 분명 마구간에서 밤을 지새우고 펌프 앞에서 아침을 때운 듯한 행색이었는데도 말이지."]], [["Regarding, with no small curiosity and interest, all the busy preparations for the coming day which every street and almost every house displayed; and thinking, now and then, that it seemed rather hard that so many people of all ranks and stations could earn a livelihood in London, and that he should be compelled to journey so far in search of one; Nicholas speedily arrived at the Saracen’s Head, Snow Hill.", "거리마다, 또 거의 모든 집마다 다가오는 하루를 맞으려고 분주히 채비하는 모습을 니콜라스는 적잖은 호기심과 흥미로 바라봤어. 그리고 이따금 이런 생각도 들었지."], ["Having dismissed his attendant, and seen the box safely deposited in the coach-office, he looked into the coffee-room in search of Mr.", "신분도 처지도 제각각인 그 많은 사람들이 런던에서 먹고살 길을 찾는데, 정작 자기는 그 길을 찾으러 이렇게 먼 곳까지 떠나야 한다니 좀 야박하다 싶었어. 그렇게 니콜라스는 어느새 스노 힐의 사라센스 헤드에 다다랐어."], ["Squeers.", "짐꾼을 돌려보내고 상자가 마차 사무소에 무사히 맡겨진 걸 확인한 다음, 그는 스퀴어스 씨를 찾으러 커피실 안을 들여다봤지."]], [["He found that learned gentleman sitting at breakfast, with the three little boys before noticed, and two others who had turned up by some lucky chance since the interview of the previous day, ranged in a row on the opposite seat.", "그 학식 깊은 신사는 아침을 먹으며 앉아 있었어. 앞에는 전에 말했던 사내아이 셋과, 어제 그 만남 이후 무슨 운 좋은 우연으로 나타난 두 아이가 더 있어서, 맞은편 자리에 한 줄로 죽 앉아 있었지."], ["Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨 앞에는 자그마한 잔에 담긴 커피와 뜨거운 토스트 한 접시, 그리고 차게 식은 소고기 한 덩이가 놓여 있었어."], ["Squeers had before him a small measure of coffee, a plate of hot toast, and a cold round of beef; but he was at that moment intent on preparing breakfast for the little boys.", "하지만 그는 그 순간 아이들 아침을 차려 주는 데 온 정신이 팔려 있었지."]], [["‘This is twopenn’orth of milk, is it, waiter?’ said Mr.", "\"이게 이 펜스어치 우유 맞나, 웨이터?\""], ["Squeers, looking down into a large blue mug, and slanting it gently, so as to get an accurate view of the quantity of liquid contained in it.", "스퀴어스 씨가 커다란 파란 머그잔을 내려다보며 물었어. 그러고는 잔을 살살 기울여서 안에 든 우유 양을 정확히 가늠해 봤지."]], [["‘That’s twopenn’orth, sir,’ replied the waiter.", "\"그게 이 펜스어치예요, 손님.\" 웨이터가 대답했어."]], [["‘What a rare article milk is, to be sure, in London!’ said Mr.", "\"런던에서 우유라는 게 참 귀하기도 하지!\""], ["Squeers, with a sigh.", "스퀴어스 씨가 한숨을 쉬며 말했어."], ["‘Just fill that mug up with lukewarm water, William, will you?’", "\"윌리엄, 그 잔에 미지근한 물 좀 채워 주겠나?\""]], [["‘To the wery top, sir?’ inquired the waiter.", "\"잔 꼭대기까지요, 손님?\" 웨이터가 물었어."], ["‘Why, the milk will be drownded.’", "\"그럼 우유가 다 묽어져 버릴 텐데요.\""]], [["‘Never you mind that,’ replied Mr.", "\"그런 건 신경 쓸 거 없어.\""], ["Squeers. ‘Serve it right for being so dear.", "스퀴어스 씨가 대꾸했어."], ["You ordered that thick bread and butter for three, did you?’", "\"이렇게 비싸게 받으니 그래도 싸지. 두툼한 버터 빵은 셋 몫으로 시킨 거 맞나?\""]], [["‘Coming directly, sir.’", "\"곧 나옵니다, 손님.\""]], [["‘You needn’t hurry yourself,’ said Squeers; ‘there’s plenty of time. Conquer your passions, boys, and don’t be eager after vittles.’ As he uttered this moral precept, Mr. Squeers took a large bite out of the cold beef, and recognised Nicholas.", "\"서두를 거 없어.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어. \"시간은 넉넉하니까. 욕심을 누르거라, 얘들아. 먹을 거 앞에서 안달하지 말고.\" 이렇게 교훈을 한마디 늘어놓으면서, 스퀴어스 씨는 차게 식은 소고기를 큼지막하게 한 입 베어 물었어. 그러다 니콜라스를 알아봤지."]], [["‘Sit down, Mr.", "\"앉으시오, 니클비 씨.\""], ["Nickleby,’ said Squeers.", "스퀴어스 씨가 말했어."], ["‘Here we are, a breakfasting you see!’", "\"보다시피 우리 아침 먹는 중이오!\""]], [["Nicholas did NOT see that anybody was breakfasting, except Mr.", "니콜라스 눈에는 스퀴어스 씨 말고는 아무도 아침을 먹는 것 같지 않았어."], ["Squeers; but he bowed with all becoming reverence, and looked as cheerful as he could.", "하지만 그는 더없이 정중하게 고개를 숙이고는, 되도록 밝은 표정을 지어 보였지."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["that’s the milk and water, is it, William?’ said Squeers.", "그게 우유 탄 물인가, 윌리엄?\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어."], ["‘Very good; don’t forget the bread and butter presently.’", "\"아주 좋아. 이따 버터 빵 잊지 말게.\""]], [["At this fresh mention of the bread and butter, the five little boys looked very eager, and followed the waiter out, with their eyes; meanwhile Mr.", "버터 빵 얘기가 또 나오자, 다섯 아이는 눈을 잔뜩 빛내며 웨이터가 나가는 뒷모습을 눈으로 좇았어."], ["Squeers tasted the milk and water.", "그사이 스퀴어스 씨는 우유 탄 물을 맛봤지."]], [["‘Ah!’ said that gentleman, smacking his lips, ‘here’s richness!", "\"아!\" 그 신사가 입맛을 다시며 말했어."], ["Think of the many beggars and orphans in the streets that would be glad of this, little boys.", "\"이거 정말 진하구나! 거리에 있는 그 많은 거지와 고아들을 생각해 보거라, 얘들아."], ["A shocking thing hunger, isn’t it, Mr.", "이런 거라도 얻으면 얼마나 좋아하겠니."], ["Nickleby?’", "굶주림이란 참 끔찍한 거지요, 안 그렇소, 니클비 씨?\""]], [["‘Very shocking, sir,’ said Nicholas.", "\"정말 끔찍하지요, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘When I say number one,’ pursued Mr.", "\"내가 일 번이라고 하면,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 머그잔을 아이들 앞에 놓으며 말을 이었어."], ["Squeers, putting the mug before the children, ‘the boy on the left hand nearest the window may take a drink; and when I say number two, the boy next him will go in, and so till we come to number five, which is the last boy.", "\"창문 쪽에서 제일 가까운 왼쪽 끝 아이가 한 모금 마시는 거다. 그리고 내가 이 번이라고 하면 그 옆 아이가 마시고, 그렇게 오 번까지 가는 거야."], ["Are you ready?’", "오 번이 마지막 아이고. 준비됐니?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir,’ cried all the little boys with great eagerness.", "\"네, 선생님!\" 아이들이 모두 잔뜩 들떠서 외쳤어."]], [["‘That’s right,’ said Squeers, calmly getting on with his breakfast; ‘keep ready till I tell you to begin.", "\"좋아.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 태연히 자기 아침을 계속 먹으며 말했어."], ["Subdue your appetites, my dears, and you’ve conquered human natur.", "\"시작하라고 할 때까지 준비하고 있거라. 식욕을 다스리거라, 얘들아."], ["This is the way we inculcate strength of mind, Mr.", "그러면 인간의 본성을 정복한 거다."], ["Nickleby,’ said the schoolmaster, turning to Nicholas, and speaking with his mouth very full of beef and toast.", "이게 바로 우리가 정신력을 길러 주는 방식이지요, 니클비 씨.\" 교장은 니콜라스 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며, 소고기와 토스트를 입에 잔뜩 문 채로 말했어."]], [["Nicholas murmured something--he knew not what--in reply; and the little boys, dividing their gaze between the mug, the bread and butter (which had by this time arrived), and every morsel which Mr.", "니콜라스는 뭐라고 중얼거렸어. 자기도 무슨 말을 했는지 몰랐지."], ["Squeers took into his mouth, remained with strained eyes in torments of expectation.", "그리고 아이들은 머그잔과 버터 빵을(이때쯤엔 빵이 나와 있었어), 그리고 스퀴어스 씨가 입에 넣는 한 조각 한 조각을 번갈아 바라보면서, 기대로 애가 타서 눈을 잔뜩 부릅뜬 채 앉아 있었어."]], [["‘Thank God for a good breakfast,’ said Squeers, when he had finished.", "\"좋은 아침밥을 주셔서 감사합니다.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 식사를 마치고 말했어."], ["‘Number one may take a drink.’", "\"일 번이 한 모금 마셔도 좋다.\""]], [["Number one seized the mug ravenously, and had just drunk enough to make him wish for more, when Mr.", "일 번은 게걸스럽게 머그잔을 움켜쥐었어. 그리고 더 마시고 싶어질 만큼만 막 마셨을 때, 스퀴어스 씨가 이 번에게 신호를 보냈지."], ["Squeers gave the signal for number two, who gave up at the same interesting moment to number three; and the process was repeated until the milk and water terminated with number five.", "그러자 이 번은 바로 그 아쉬운 순간에 잔을 삼 번에게 넘겼어. 이렇게 차례차례 되풀이되다가, 우유 탄 물은 오 번에서 끝나 버렸어."]], [["‘And now,’ said the schoolmaster, dividing the bread and butter for three into as many portions as there were children, ‘you had better look sharp with your breakfast, for the horn will blow in a minute or two, and then every boy leaves off.’", "\"자, 이제.\" 교장은 셋 몫인 버터 빵을 아이들 수만큼 나누면서 말했어. \"어서 아침을 먹는 게 좋을 거다. 나팔이 일이 분 안에 울릴 테고, 그러면 모두 그만 먹어야 하니까.\""]], [["Permission being thus given to fall to, the boys began to eat voraciously, and in desperate haste: while the schoolmaster (who was in high good humour after his meal) picked his teeth with a fork, and looked smilingly on.", "이렇게 먹어도 좋다는 허락이 떨어지자, 아이들은 게걸스럽게, 그리고 죽을 둥 살 둥 허겁지겁 먹기 시작했어. 그사이 교장은(식사를 마치고 기분이 한껏 좋아져서) 포크로 이를 쑤시며 빙그레 웃으며 지켜봤지."], ["In a very short time, the horn was heard.", "얼마 지나지 않아 나팔 소리가 들려왔어."]], [["‘I thought it wouldn’t be long,’ said Squeers, jumping up and producing a little basket from under the seat; ‘put what you haven’t had time to eat, in here, boys!", "\"오래 안 걸릴 줄 알았지.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 벌떡 일어나 좌석 밑에서 작은 바구니를 꺼내며 말했어."], ["You’ll want it on the road!’", "\"미처 못 먹은 건 여기 담거라, 얘들아! 길에서 먹어야 할 테니까!\""]], [["Nicholas was considerably startled by these very economical arrangements; but he had no time to reflect upon them, for the little boys had to be got up to the top of the coach, and their boxes had to be brought out and put in, and Mr.", "니콜라스는 이렇게 알뜰한 처사에 적잖이 놀랐어. 하지만 그걸 곱씹어 볼 틈도 없었지."], ["Squeers’s luggage was to be seen carefully deposited in the boot, and all these offices were in his department.", "아이들을 마차 위로 올려 보내야 했고, 아이들 상자도 꺼내서 실어야 했고, 스퀴어스 씨 짐도 짐칸에 잘 넣어 두는 걸 챙겨야 했거든. 그리고 이 모든 일이 다 그의 몫이었어."], ["He was in the full heat and bustle of concluding these operations, when his uncle, Mr. Ralph Nickleby, accosted him.", "그가 이 일들을 마무리하느라 한창 열을 내며 부산을 떨고 있을 때, 삼촌인 랄프 니클비 씨가 그에게 말을 걸어왔어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["here you are, sir!’ said Ralph.", "여기 있었구나.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘Here are your mother and sister, sir.’", "\"네 어머니랑 누이가 여기 와 있다.\""]], [["‘Where?’ cried Nicholas, looking hastily round.", "\"어디요?\" 니콜라스가 황급히 둘레를 둘러보며 외쳤어."]], [["‘Here!’ replied his uncle.", "\"여기!\" 삼촌이 대답했어."], ["‘Having too much money and nothing at all to do with it, they were paying a hackney coach as I came up, sir.’", "\"돈이 너무 많아서 쓸 데가 없는지, 내가 올 때 보니 삯마차 값을 치르고 있더구나.\""]], [["‘We were afraid of being too late to see him before he went away from us,’ said Mrs.", "\"이 애가 우리 곁을 떠나기 전에 못 보고 보낼까 봐 겁이 났어요.\""], ["Nickleby, embracing her son, heedless of the unconcerned lookers-on in the coach-yard.", "니클비 부인이 아들을 끌어안으며 말했어. 마차 마당에서 무심히 쳐다보는 사람들 따위는 아랑곳하지 않았지."]], [["‘Very good, ma’am,’ returned Ralph, ‘you’re the best judge of course.", "\"좋습니다, 형수님.\" 랄프가 대꾸했어."], ["I merely said that you were paying a hackney coach.", "\"물론 형수님이 알아서 잘 판단하셨겠지요. 저는 그저 형수님이 삯마차 값을 치르고 있더라고 말했을 뿐입니다."], ["I never pay a hackney coach, ma’am; I never hire one.", "저는 삯마차 값을 치르는 법이 없어요, 형수님."], ["I haven’t been in a hackney coach of my own hiring, for thirty years, and I hope I shan’t be for thirty more, if I live as long.’", "빌려 타지를 않으니까요. 제 돈 내고 삯마차를 타 본 게 삼십 년 동안 한 번도 없고, 앞으로 삼십 년을 더 산다 해도 그럴 일은 없길 바랍니다.\""]], [["‘I should never have forgiven myself if I had not seen him,’ said Mrs Nickleby.", "\"이 애를 못 보고 보냈으면 나는 평생 나 자신을 용서 못 했을 거예요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["‘Poor dear boy--going away without his breakfast too, because he feared to distress us!’", "\"가엾은 것, 아침도 못 먹고 떠나는구나. 우리 마음 아플까 봐 그랬다니!\""]], [["‘Mighty fine certainly,’ said Ralph, with great testiness.", "\"그것참 갸륵하기도 하지요.\" 랄프가 잔뜩 짜증을 내며 말했어."], ["‘When I first went to business, ma’am, I took a penny loaf and a ha’porth of milk for my breakfast as I walked to the city every morning; what do you say to that, ma’am?", "\"제가 처음 일을 시작했을 때는요, 형수님, 매일 아침 시내로 걸어가면서 일 페니짜리 빵 한 덩이랑 반 페니어치 우유로 아침을 때웠습니다. 그건 어떻게 생각하십니까, 형수님?"], ["Breakfast!", "아침밥이라니!"], ["Bah!’", "흥!\""]], [["‘Now, Nickleby,’ said Squeers, coming up at the moment buttoning his greatcoat; ‘I think you’d better get up behind.", "\"자, 니클비.\" 그 순간 외투 단추를 채우며 다가온 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어."], ["I’m afraid of one of them boys falling off and then there’s twenty pound a year gone.’", "\"자네는 뒤쪽에 타는 게 좋겠어. 저 녀석들 중 하나라도 떨어지면, 그게 일 년에 이십 파운드가 날아가는 거니까 말이야.\""]], [["‘Dear Nicholas,’ whispered Kate, touching her brother’s arm, ‘who is that vulgar man?’", "\"오빠.\" 케이트가 오빠의 팔을 건드리며 속삭였어. \"저 천박한 사람은 누구야?\""]], [["‘Eh!’ growled Ralph, whose quick ears had caught the inquiry.", "\"응!\" 랄프가 으르렁거렸어."], ["‘Do you wish to be introduced to Mr.", "그의 빠른 귀가 그 물음을 놓치지 않았던 거야."], ["Squeers, my dear?’", "\"스퀴어스 씨를 소개받고 싶으냐, 얘야?\""]], [["‘That the schoolmaster!", "\"저 사람이 교장이라고요!"], ["No, uncle.", "아뇨, 삼촌."], ["Oh no!’ replied Kate, shrinking back.", "아, 아니에요!\" 케이트가 움찔 뒤로 물러서며 대답했어."]], [["‘I’m sure I heard you say as much, my dear,’ retorted Ralph in his cold sarcastic manner.", "\"내 분명히 네가 그렇게 말하는 걸 들었는데, 얘야.\" 랄프가 그 차갑고 비꼬는 투로 되받았어."], ["‘Mr.", "\"스퀴어스 씨, 여기 제 조카입니다."], ["Squeers, here’s my niece: Nicholas’s sister!’", "니콜라스의 누이지요!\""]], [["‘Very glad to make your acquaintance, miss,’ said Squeers, raising his hat an inch or two.", "\"만나서 정말 반갑습니다, 아가씨.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 모자를 한두 치쯤 들어 올리며 말했어."], ["‘I wish Mrs. Squeers took gals, and we had you for a teacher.", "\"우리 스퀴어스 부인이 여자애들도 받았더라면, 아가씨를 선생으로 모셨으면 좋았을 텐데요."], ["I don’t know, though, whether she mightn’t grow jealous if we had. Ha!", "하기야, 그랬다간 부인이 질투를 부릴지도 모르겠군요."], ["ha! ha!’", "하하하!\""]], [["If the proprietor of Dotheboys Hall could have known what was passing in his assistant’s breast at that moment, he would have discovered, with some surprise, that he was as near being soundly pummelled as he had ever been in his life.", "두더보이즈 홀의 주인이 그 순간 조수의 가슴속에 무슨 일이 일어나고 있었는지 알았더라면, 적잖이 놀라며 깨달았을 거야. 자기가 평생 처음으로 흠씬 두들겨 맞기 직전이라는 걸 말이지."], ["Kate Nickleby, having a quicker perception of her brother’s emotions, led him gently aside, and thus prevented Mr.", "케이트 니클비는 오빠의 감정을 누구보다 빠르게 알아채고는, 그를 슬며시 옆으로 데려갔어."], ["Squeers from being impressed with the fact in a peculiarly disagreeable manner.", "그래서 스퀴어스 씨는 그 사실을 아주 불쾌한 방식으로 실감하는 걸 면할 수 있었지."]], [["‘My dear Nicholas,’ said the young lady, ‘who is this man?", "\"오빠.\" 아가씨가 말했어."], ["What kind of place can it be that you are going to?’", "\"저 사람은 도대체 누구야? 오빠가 가는 곳은 대체 어떤 데일까?\""]], [["‘I hardly know, Kate,’ replied Nicholas, pressing his sister’s hand.", "\"나도 잘 모르겠어, 케이트.\" 니콜라스가 누이의 손을 꼭 잡으며 대답했어."], ["‘I suppose the Yorkshire folks are rather rough and uncultivated; that’s all.’", "\"요크셔 사람들이 좀 거칠고 안 다듬어진 모양이지. 그뿐이야.\""]], [["‘But this person,’ urged Kate.", "\"하지만 저 사람은.\" 케이트가 따지듯 말했어."]], [["‘Is my employer, or master, or whatever the proper name may be,’ replied Nicholas quickly; ‘and I was an ass to take his coarseness ill.", "\"내 고용주야. 아니면 주인이라고 해야 하나, 뭐 적당한 이름이 뭐든 간에.\""], ["They are looking this way, and it is time I was in my place.", "니콜라스가 얼른 대답했어. \"저 사람 거친 행동을 언짢게 받아들인 내가 바보였어."], ["Bless you, love, and goodbye!", "저쪽에서 이리 보고 있네."], ["Mother, look forward to our meeting again someday!", "나도 이제 자리에 가 있어야겠다. 잘 있어, 사랑하는 누이야."], ["Uncle, farewell!", "안녕! 어머니, 언젠가 우리 다시 만날 날을 기다려 주세요!"], ["Thank you heartily for all you have done and all you mean to do.", "삼촌, 안녕히 계세요! 해 주신 모든 일과 앞으로 해 주실 모든 일에 진심으로 감사드려요."], ["Quite ready, sir!’", "다 됐습니다, 선생님!\""]], [["With these hasty adieux, Nicholas mounted nimbly to his seat, and waved his hand as gallantly as if his heart went with it.", "이렇게 황급히 작별 인사를 하고는, 니콜라스는 날렵하게 자기 자리로 올라가 손을 흔들었어. 마치 가슴까지 함께 따라가는 것처럼 씩씩하게 말이야."]], [["At this moment, when the coachman and guard were comparing notes for the last time before starting, on the subject of the way-bill; when porters were screwing out the last reluctant sixpences, itinerant newsmen making the last offer of a morning paper, and the horses giving the last impatient rattle to their harness; Nicholas felt somebody pulling softly at his leg. He looked down, and there stood Newman Noggs, who pushed up into his hand a dirty letter.", "이 순간, 마부와 차장이 출발하기 전 마지막으로 운임 명세서를 두고 머리를 맞대고 있었어. 짐꾼들은 마지막 남은 육 펜스를 어떻게든 짜내려 하고, 떠돌이 신문팔이들은 마지막으로 조간신문을 사라고 들이밀고, 말들은 마지막으로 안달하듯 마구를 절그럭거렸지. 그때 니콜라스는 누군가 자기 다리를 슬며시 잡아당기는 걸 느꼈어. 내려다보니 거기 뉴먼 노그스가 서 있었지. 그는 니콜라스 손에 꾀죄죄한 편지 한 통을 쥐여 주었어."]], [["‘What’s this?’ inquired Nicholas.", "\"이게 뭐예요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘Hush!’ rejoined Noggs, pointing to Mr.", "\"쉿!\""], ["Ralph Nickleby, who was saying a few earnest words to Squeers, a short distance off: ‘Take it.", "노그스가 대꾸하며, 조금 떨어진 곳에서 스퀴어스 씨에게 뭔가 진지하게 몇 마디를 하고 있는 랄프 니클비 씨를 가리켰어. \"받아요."], ["Read it.", "읽어요."], ["Nobody knows.", "아무도 몰라요."], ["That’s all.’", "그게 다예요.\""]], [["‘Stop!’ cried Nicholas.", "\"잠깐만요!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어."]], [["‘No,’ replied Noggs.", "\"안 돼요.\" 노그스가 대답했어."]], [["Nicholas cried stop, again, but Newman Noggs was gone.", "니콜라스가 다시 잠깐만요, 하고 외쳤지만, 뉴먼 노그스는 이미 사라지고 없었어."]], [["A minute’s bustle, a banging of the coach doors, a swaying of the vehicle to one side, as the heavy coachman, and still heavier guard, climbed into their seats; a cry of all right, a few notes from the horn, a hasty glance of two sorrowful faces below, and the hard features of Mr Ralph Nickleby--and the coach was gone too, and rattling over the stones of Smithfield.", "잠깐의 부산함, 쾅 닫히는 마차 문, 한쪽으로 기우뚱하는 마차. 덩치 큰 마부와 그보다 더 덩치 큰 차장이 자리로 기어 올라가면서 그렇게 흔들렸지. 다 됐다는 외침, 나팔의 몇 가락, 아래쪽에서 슬픔에 잠긴 두 얼굴과 랄프 니클비 씨의 굳은 얼굴을 황급히 한 번 본 것. 그러고는 마차도 떠나 버렸어. 스미스필드의 돌바닥 위를 덜컹덜컹 굴러가면서 말이야."]], [["The little boys’ legs being too short to admit of their feet resting upon anything as they sat, and the little boys’ bodies being consequently in imminent hazard of being jerked off the coach, Nicholas had enough to do over the stones to hold them on.", "아이들 다리가 너무 짧아서 앉았을 때 발이 어디에도 닿지 않았어. 그래서 아이들 몸이 마차 밖으로 튕겨 나갈 위험이 시시각각 컸지."], ["Between the manual exertion and the mental anxiety attendant upon this task, he was not a little relieved when the coach stopped at the Peacock at Islington.", "그러니 니콜라스는 돌바닥 위를 지나는 내내 아이들을 붙잡느라 정신이 없었어. 손으로 끙끙 애쓰는 데다 이 일에 따른 마음 졸임까지 겹쳐서, 마차가 이즐링턴의 피콕에 멈춰 섰을 때 그는 적잖이 안도했어."], ["He was still more relieved when a hearty-looking gentleman, with a very good-humoured face, and a very fresh colour, got up behind, and proposed to take the other corner of the seat.", "그리고 더더욱 마음이 놓인 건, 아주 기분 좋아 보이는 얼굴에 혈색이 좋은 다부진 신사가 뒤쪽으로 올라타서 좌석의 나머지 한 귀퉁이를 차지하겠다고 했을 때였지."]], [["‘If we put some of these youngsters in the middle,’ said the new-comer, ‘they’ll be safer in case of their going to sleep; eh?’", "\"이 꼬마들 몇 명을 가운데로 넣으면,\" 새로 온 사람이 말했어. \"혹시 잠들어도 더 안전하지 않겠소? 응?\""]], [["‘If you’ll have the goodness, sir,’ replied Squeers, ‘that’ll be the very thing.", "\"그렇게 해 주시면, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 대답했어."], ["Mr.", "\"딱 좋겠군요."], ["Nickleby, take three of them boys between you and the gentleman.", "니클비 씨, 저 녀석들 셋을 자네랑 이 신사분 사이에 앉히게."], ["Belling and the youngest Snawley can sit between me and the guard.", "벨링이랑 막내 스놀리는 나랑 차장 사이에 앉으면 되고. 아이 셋은요,\""], ["Three children,’ said Squeers, explaining to the stranger, ‘books as two.’", "스퀴어스 씨가 낯선 신사에게 설명하듯 말했어. \"둘 몫으로 셉니다.\""]], [["‘I have not the least objection I am sure,’ said the fresh-coloured gentleman; ‘I have a brother who wouldn’t object to book his six children as two at any butcher’s or baker’s in the kingdom, I dare say. Far from it.’", "\"전혀 이의 없습니다, 정말로요.\" 혈색 좋은 신사가 말했어. \"제 형제 하나는 자기 아이 여섯을 이 나라 어느 푸줏간이든 빵집이든 둘 몫으로 적어 넣어도 마다하지 않을 사람이거든요. 정말이지요. 마다하기는커녕요.\""]], [["‘Six children, sir?’ exclaimed Squeers.", "\"아이가 여섯이라고요, 선생님?\" 스퀴어스 씨가 외쳤어."]], [["‘Yes, and all boys,’ replied the stranger.", "\"네, 그것도 다 사내아이지요.\" 낯선 신사가 대답했어."]], [["‘Mr. Nickleby,’ said Squeers, in great haste, ‘catch hold of that basket. Let me give you a card, sir, of an establishment where those six boys can be brought up in an enlightened, liberal, and moral manner, with no mistake at all about it, for twenty guineas a year each--twenty guineas, sir--or I’d take all the boys together upon a average right through, and say a hundred pound a year for the lot.’", "\"니클비 씨.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 잔뜩 서둘러 말했어. \"그 바구니 좀 잡아 주게. 선생님, 명함 한 장 드리지요. 그 여섯 아이를 깨인 정신과 너그럽고 도덕적인 방식으로 길러 줄 곳입니다. 그 점은 한 치도 틀림없고요. 한 명당 일 년에 이십 기니, 이십 기니입니다, 선생님. 아니면 여섯을 다 한꺼번에 평균을 내서, 통틀어 일 년에 백 파운드로 해 드리지요.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ said the gentleman, glancing at the card, ‘you are the Mr.", "\"오!\" 신사가 명함을 흘긋 보며 말했어."], ["Squeers mentioned here, I presume?’", "\"여기 적힌 그 스퀴어스 씨가 바로 당신인가 봅니다?\""]], [["‘Yes, I am, sir,’ replied the worthy pedagogue; ‘Mr.", "\"네, 그렇습니다, 선생님.\" 그 훌륭한 교육자가 대답했어."], ["Wackford Squeers is my name, and I’m very far from being ashamed of it.", "\"왁퍼드 스퀴어스가 제 이름이고, 그 이름을 부끄러워하기는커녕입니다. 이 아이들이 제 학생 중 일부고요, 선생님."], ["These are some of my boys, sir; that’s one of my assistants, sir--Mr.", "저쪽은 제 조수 중 한 사람입니다, 선생님. 니클비 씨인데, 신사의 아들이고 훌륭한 학자지요."], ["Nickleby, a gentleman’s son, and a good scholar, mathematical, classical, and commercial.", "수학, 고전, 상업 다 통달했고요. 우리 학교에선 일을 어중간하게 하는 법이 없습니다."], ["We don’t do things by halves at our shop.", "우리 아이들은 온갖 학문을 다 배워 갑니다, 선생님."], ["All manner of learning my boys take down, sir; the expense is never thought of; and they get paternal treatment and washing in.’", "비용은 따지지도 않고요. 게다가 친아버지 같은 보살핌에다 빨래까지 다 포함입니다.\""]], [["‘Upon my word,’ said the gentleman, glancing at Nicholas with a half-smile, and a more than half expression of surprise, ‘these are advantages indeed.’", "\"이거 정말이지,\" 신사가 반쯤 웃으며, 그리고 절반 넘게 놀란 표정으로 니콜라스를 흘긋 보며 말했어. \"굉장한 이점들이군요, 그래요.\""]], [["‘You may say that, sir,’ rejoined Squeers, thrusting his hands into his great-coat pockets.", "\"그렇게 말씀하셔도 됩니다, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 외투 주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣으며 대꾸했어."], ["‘The most unexceptionable references are given and required.", "\"더없이 나무랄 데 없는 신원 보증을 받고, 또 요구합니다."], ["I wouldn’t take a reference with any boy, that wasn’t responsible for the payment of five pound five a quarter, no, not if you went down on your knees, and asked me, with the tears running down your face, to do it.’", "저는 분기마다 오 파운드 오 실링을 책임지고 낼 사람이 보증을 서지 않으면, 어떤 아이도 받지 않습니다. 선생님이 무릎을 꿇고, 얼굴에 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 받아 달라고 빌어도, 안 됩니다.\""]], [["‘Highly considerate,’ said the passenger.", "\"무척 사려 깊으시군요.\" 승객이 말했어."]], [["‘It’s my great aim and end to be considerate, sir,’ rejoined Squeers.", "\"사려 깊은 게 제 가장 큰 목표이자 목적이지요, 선생님.\""], ["‘Snawley, junior, if you don’t leave off chattering your teeth, and shaking with the cold, I’ll warm you with a severe thrashing in about half a minute’s time.’", "스퀴어스 씨가 대꾸했어. \"스놀리, 너 이 작은 녀석아, 이를 딱딱 부딪치고 추워서 덜덜 떠는 거 안 그만두면, 한 삼십 초 안에 호된 매질로 몸을 따뜻하게 해 주마.\""]], [["‘Sit fast here, genelmen,’ said the guard as he clambered up.", "\"단단히 붙잡으시오, 손님들.\" 차장이 기어 올라오며 말했어."]], [["‘All right behind there, Dick?’ cried the coachman.", "\"뒤쪽 다 됐나, 딕?\" 마부가 외쳤어."]], [["‘All right,’ was the reply. ‘Off she goes!’ And off she did go--if coaches be feminine--amidst a loud flourish from the guard’s horn, and the calm approval of all the judges of coaches and coach-horses congregated at the Peacock, but more especially of the helpers, who stood, with the cloths over their arms, watching the coach till it disappeared, and then lounged admiringly stablewards, bestowing various gruff encomiums on the beauty of the turn-out.", "\"다 됐어.\" 대답이 돌아왔지. \"자, 떠난다!\" 그리고 마차는 정말로 떠났어. 차장의 나팔이 요란하게 한바탕 울리는 가운데, 피콕에 모여 있던 마차와 마차 끄는 말의 감정가들이 모두 차분히 고개를 끄덕이는 가운데 말이야. 특히 마구간 일꾼들이 그랬지. 그들은 팔에 천을 걸친 채 서서 마차가 사라질 때까지 지켜봤어. 그러고는 흐뭇한 얼굴로 어슬렁어슬렁 마구간 쪽으로 걸어가며, 멋진 행차 솜씨를 두고 투박한 칭찬을 이러쿵저러쿵 늘어놓았지."]], [["When the guard (who was a stout old Yorkshireman) had blown himself quite out of breath, he put the horn into a little tunnel of a basket fastened to the coach-side for the purpose, and giving himself a plentiful shower of blows on the chest and shoulders, observed it was uncommon cold; after which, he demanded of every person separately whether he was going right through, and if not, where he WAS going. Satisfactory replies being made to these queries, he surmised that the roads were pretty heavy arter that fall last night, and took the liberty of asking whether any of them gentlemen carried a snuff-box. It happening that nobody did, he remarked with a mysterious air that he had heard a medical gentleman as went down to Grantham last week, say how that snuff-taking was bad for the eyes; but for his part he had never found it so, and what he said was, that everybody should speak as they found. Nobody attempting to controvert this position, he took a small brown-paper parcel out of his hat, and putting on a pair of horn spectacles (the writing being crabbed) read the direction half-a-dozen times over; having done which, he consigned the parcel to its old place, put up his spectacles again, and stared at everybody in turn. After this, he took another blow at the horn by way of refreshment; and, having now exhausted his usual topics of conversation, folded his arms as well as he could in so many coats, and falling into a solemn silence, looked carelessly at the familiar objects which met his eye on every side as the coach rolled on; the only things he seemed to care for, being horses and droves of cattle, which he scrutinised with a critical air as they were passed upon the road.", "차장은(요크셔 출신의 다부진 노인이었어) 숨이 다 가쁘도록 나팔을 불고 나서, 그걸 마차 옆에 일부러 매달아 둔 작은 터널 같은 바구니에 도로 넣었어. 그러고는 가슴팍과 어깨를 마구 두들겨 대더니, 날이 어지간히 춥다고 한마디 했지. 그런 다음 한 사람 한 사람에게 따로 물었어. 끝까지 가는 거냐고, 안 그러면 어디까지 가느냐고 말이야. 그 물음에 다들 시원하게 대답하자, 그는 어젯밤 그 눈 때문에 길이 꽤 험할 거라고 짐작했어. 그러고는 손님들 중에 코담뱃갑을 가진 분이 있는지 외람되게 물어봤지. 마침 아무도 없자, 그는 알 듯 모를 듯한 표정으로 한마디 했어. 지난주에 그랜섬으로 내려가던 의사 양반이 코담배는 눈에 안 좋다고 하더라는 거야. 하지만 자기는 한 번도 그런 적이 없었다며, 사람은 누구나 제가 겪은 대로 말해야 한다고 했지. 아무도 이 말에 토를 달지 않자, 그는 모자에서 작은 갈색 종이 꾸러미를 하나 꺼내더니, 뿔테 안경을 끼고는(글씨가 비뚤배뚤했거든) 받는 사람 주소를 예닐곱 번이나 읽었어. 그러고 나서 꾸러미를 도로 제자리에 넣고, 안경도 다시 집어넣고는, 한 사람씩 차례로 빤히 쳐다봤지. 그런 다음 기운을 차리려는 듯 나팔을 또 한 번 불었어. 그리고 이제 평소 늘어놓던 이야깃거리가 다 떨어지자, 그 많은 외투를 껴입은 채로나마 팔짱을 끼고는 엄숙한 침묵에 빠져들었어. 마차가 굴러가는 동안 사방에서 눈에 들어오는 낯익은 풍경들을 무심히 바라보면서 말이야. 그가 유일하게 관심을 두는 건 말과 떼 지어 가는 소들이었지. 길에서 그것들을 지나칠 때마다 그는 평가하는 듯한 눈빛으로 꼼꼼히 뜯어봤어."]], [["The weather was intensely and bitterly cold; a great deal of snow fell from time to time; and the wind was intolerably keen.", "날씨는 지독하게, 살을 에듯이 추웠어. 이따금 눈도 펑펑 내렸고, 바람은 견딜 수 없을 만큼 매서웠지."], ["Mr. Squeers got down at almost every stage--to stretch his legs as he said--and as he always came back from such excursions with a very red nose, and composed himself to sleep directly, there is reason to suppose that he derived great benefit from the process.", "스퀴어스 씨는 정거장마다 거의 빠짐없이 내렸어."], ["The little pupils having been stimulated with the remains of their breakfast, and further invigorated by sundry small cups of a curious cordial carried by Mr.", "다리 좀 펴겠다는 거였지. 그리고 그렇게 다녀올 때마다 늘 코가 새빨개져서 돌아와서는 곧장 잠을 청했으니, 그가 그 과정에서 큰 덕을 봤다고 짐작할 만했어."], ["Squeers, which tasted very like toast-and-water put into a brandy bottle by mistake, went to sleep, woke, shivered, and cried, as their feelings prompted.", "어린 학생들은 남은 아침밥으로 기운을 차렸고, 거기다 스퀴어스 씨가 가지고 다니는 묘한 강장주를 작은 잔으로 몇 번 더 마시고는 기운이 좀 났지. 그 강장주는 꼭 토스트 우린 물을 실수로 브랜디 병에 넣은 것 같은 맛이었어."], ["Nicholas and the good-tempered man found so many things to talk about, that between conversing together, and cheering up the boys, the time passed with them as rapidly as it could, under such adverse circumstances.", "아이들은 잠들었다가 깨고, 덜덜 떨고, 또 기분 내키는 대로 울었어. 니콜라스와 그 마음씨 좋은 신사는 나눌 이야깃거리가 어찌나 많던지, 서로 이야기를 주고받고 아이들을 달래느라 이런 고약한 상황에서도 시간이 그나마 가장 빨리 흘러갔어."]], [["So the day wore on. At Eton Slocomb there was a good coach dinner, of which the box, the four front outsides, the one inside, Nicholas, the good-tempered man, and Mr. Squeers, partook; while the five little boys were put to thaw by the fire, and regaled with sandwiches. A stage or two further on, the lamps were lighted, and a great to-do occasioned by the taking up, at a roadside inn, of a very fastidious lady with an infinite variety of cloaks and small parcels, who loudly lamented, for the behoof of the outsides, the non-arrival of her own carriage which was to have taken her on, and made the guard solemnly promise to stop every green chariot he saw coming; which, as it was a dark night and he was sitting with his face the other way, that officer undertook, with many fervent asseverations, to do. Lastly, the fastidious lady, finding there was a solitary gentleman inside, had a small lamp lighted which she carried in reticule, and being after much trouble shut in, the horses were put into a brisk canter and the coach was once more in rapid motion.", "그렇게 하루가 저물어 갔어. 이튼 슬로컴에서는 괜찮은 마차 손님용 저녁이 나왔어. 마부석에 앉은 사람, 앞쪽 바깥 자리 네 사람, 안쪽 한 사람, 니콜라스, 그 마음씨 좋은 신사, 그리고 스퀴어스 씨가 그 저녁을 함께 먹었지. 그사이 다섯 아이는 불가에서 몸을 녹이도록 두고, 샌드위치로 요기를 시켰어. 한두 정거장 더 가자 등불이 켜졌어. 그리고 길가 여관에서 무척 까다로운 부인 하나를 태우느라 한바탕 소동이 벌어졌지. 부인은 온갖 종류의 망토며 자그마한 꾸러미를 끝도 없이 들고 있었어. 그러고는 바깥 자리 사람들 다 들으라는 듯이 큰 소리로 한탄했지. 자기를 데려가기로 했던 자기 마차가 안 왔다고 말이야. 그러고는 차장한테, 오는 길에 초록색 사륜마차가 보이거든 빠짐없이 다 세워 달라고 엄숙히 약속하게 했어. 그런데 캄캄한 밤인 데다 차장은 얼굴을 반대쪽으로 향하고 앉아 있었으니, 그 양반은 갖은 열렬한 맹세를 곁들여 그러겠노라 떠맡았지. 마지막으로 그 까다로운 부인은 안쪽에 신사가 혼자 타고 있는 걸 알고는, 손가방에 넣고 다니던 작은 등불을 켰어. 그리고 한참 애를 쓴 끝에 부인을 안에 들여보내자, 말들은 경쾌한 구보로 내달리기 시작했고, 마차는 다시 한번 빠르게 움직였지."]], [["The night and the snow came on together, and dismal enough they were.", "밤과 눈이 함께 몰려왔어. 둘 다 어지간히 음산했지."], ["There was no sound to be heard but the howling of the wind; for the noise of the wheels, and the tread of the horses’ feet, were rendered inaudible by the thick coating of snow which covered the ground, and was fast increasing every moment.", "바람이 윙윙거리는 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 들리지 않았어. 바퀴 소리도, 말발굽 디디는 소리도, 땅을 두껍게 덮은 눈에 다 묻혀 들리지 않았거든."], ["The streets of Stamford were deserted as they passed through the town; and its old churches rose, frowning and dark, from the whitened ground.", "그 눈은 시시각각 빠르게 쌓여 가고 있었지. 마차가 지나갈 때 스탬퍼드의 거리는 텅 비어 있었어."], ["Twenty miles further on, two of the front outside passengers, wisely availing themselves of their arrival at one of the best inns in England, turned in, for the night, at the George at Grantham.", "그리고 그 도시의 오래된 교회들이 하얗게 덮인 땅 위로 어둡게 잔뜩 찌푸린 채 솟아 있었지. 이십 마일을 더 가서, 앞쪽 바깥 자리 승객 둘이 영국에서 제일가는 여관 중 한 곳에 다다른 걸 슬기롭게 이용해, 그랜섬의 조지에서 밤을 묵으려고 들어갔어."], ["The remainder wrapped themselves more closely in their coats and cloaks, and leaving the light and warmth of the town behind them, pillowed themselves against the luggage, and prepared, with many half-suppressed moans, again to encounter the piercing blast which swept across the open country.", "남은 사람들은 외투며 망토를 더 단단히 여미고는, 도시의 불빛과 온기를 뒤로한 채, 짐에 기대어 몸을 누이고, 반쯤 억눌린 신음을 연신 내뱉으며, 탁 트인 들판을 가로질러 휘몰아치는 살을 에는 바람을 다시 맞을 채비를 했어."]], [["They were little more than a stage out of Grantham, or about halfway between it and Newark, when Nicholas, who had been asleep for a short time, was suddenly roused by a violent jerk which nearly threw him from his seat. Grasping the rail, he found that the coach had sunk greatly on one side, though it was still dragged forward by the horses; and while--confused by their plunging and the loud screams of the lady inside--he hesitated, for an instant, whether to jump off or not, the vehicle turned easily over, and relieved him from all further uncertainty by flinging him into the road.", "그랜섬을 벗어나 한 정거장쯤, 그러니까 그랜섬과 뉴어크의 중간쯤 왔을 때였어. 잠깐 잠들었던 니콜라스가 갑자기 격하게 흔들리는 바람에 화들짝 깼어. 하마터면 자리에서 튕겨 나갈 뻔했지. 난간을 움켜쥐고 보니, 마차가 한쪽으로 크게 기울어 있었어. 그래도 말들은 여전히 마차를 앞으로 끌고 가는 중이었지. 그리고 말들이 마구 날뛰는 데다 안에 탄 부인이 째지게 비명을 질러 대니 정신이 하나도 없었어. 그래서 뛰어내려야 할지 말지 잠깐 머뭇거리는 사이, 마차가 스르르 옆으로 넘어가 버렸어. 그러고는 니콜라스를 길바닥으로 내동댕이쳐서, 더 망설일 거리도 없게 만들어 버렸지."]], [["# CHAPTER 6", "# CHAPTER 6"]], [["In which the Occurrence of the Accident mentioned in the last Chapter, affords an Opportunity to a couple of Gentlemen to tell Stories against each other\n‘Wo ho!’ cried the guard, on his legs in a minute, and running to the leaders’ heads.", "\"워, 워!\" 차장이 순식간에 벌떡 일어나더니 앞장선 말들 머리 쪽으로 달려가며 소리쳤다."], ["‘Is there ony genelmen there as can len’ a hond here?", "\"거기 손 좀 빌려줄 신사분 안 계시오? 가만있어, 이놈!"], ["Keep quiet, dang ye! Wo ho!’", "워, 워!\""]], [["‘What’s the matter?’ demanded Nicholas, looking sleepily up.", "\"무슨 일이오?\" 니콜라스가 잠에 취한 눈을 들며 물었다."]], [["‘Matther mun, matter eneaf for one neight,’ replied the guard; ‘dang the wall-eyed bay, he’s gane mad wi’ glory I think, carse t’coorch is over.", "\"무슨 일이라니, 하룻밤치고는 일이 차고 넘치지요.\" 차장이 대꾸했다."], ["Here, can’t ye len’ a hond?", "\"저 눈깔 흰 밤색 말 좀 보소, 마차가 엎어진 게 무슨 자랑이라도 되는 양 미쳐 날뛰지 뭐요."], ["Dom it, I’d ha’ dean it if all my boans were brokken.’", "자, 손 좀 안 빌려주겠소? 빌어먹을, 내 뼈가 다 부러졌어도 내가 했을 거요.\""]], [["‘Here!’ cried Nicholas, staggering to his feet, ‘I’m ready.", "\"여기 있소!\" 니콜라스가 비틀거리며 일어섰다."], ["I’m only a little abroad, that’s all.’", "\"준비됐소. 잠깐 정신이 좀 나갔을 뿐이오.\""]], [["‘Hoold ‘em toight,’ cried the guard, ‘while ar coot treaces.", "\"꽉 붙들어요.\" 차장이 외쳤다."], ["Hang on tiv’em sumhoo.", "\"내가 끈을 자를 동안."], ["Well deane, my lod.", "어떻게든 매달리시오."], ["That’s it.", "잘했소, 양반."], ["Let’em goa noo.", "그렇지. 자, 이제 놔요."], ["Dang ‘em, they’ll gang whoam fast eneaf!’", "빌어먹을, 저놈들 알아서 집으로 잘도 돌아갈 거요!\""]], [["In truth, the animals were no sooner released than they trotted back, with much deliberation, to the stable they had just left, which was distant not a mile behind.", "과연 말들은 풀려나기가 무섭게 어슬렁어슬렁 방금 떠나온 마구간 쪽으로 되돌아 달려갔다. 그곳은 채 일 마일도 안 되는 뒤편에 있었다."]], [["‘Can you blo’ a harn?’ asked the guard, disengaging one of the coach-lamps.", "\"뿔나팔 불 줄 아시오?\" 차장이 마차 등 하나를 떼어 내며 물었다."]], [["‘I dare say I can,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"불 줄 알 것 같소.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다."]], [["‘Then just blo’ away into that ‘un as lies on the grund, fit to wakken the deead, will’ee,’ said the man, ‘while I stop sum o’ this here squealing inside.", "\"그럼 저기 땅바닥에 떨어진 등에다 대고 한번 힘껏 불어 보시오. 죽은 사람도 깨울 만큼 말이오.\""], ["Cumin’, cumin’.", "차장이 말했다. \"그동안 나는 안에서 꽥꽥대는 소리 좀 잠재울 테니."], ["Dean’t make that noise, wooman.’", "가요, 가. 그렇게 소리 좀 지르지 마쇼, 부인.\""]], [["As the man spoke, he proceeded to wrench open the uppermost door of the coach, while Nicholas, seizing the horn, awoke the echoes far and wide with one of the most extraordinary performances on that instrument ever heard by mortal ears. It had its effect, however, not only in rousing such of their fall, but in summoning assistance to their relief; for lights gleamed in the distance, and people were already astir.", "차장은 그렇게 말하며 마차의 맨 위쪽 문을 비틀어 열기 시작했다. 그사이 니콜라스는 뿔나팔을 쥐고는, 인간의 귀가 일찍이 들어 본 적 없는 가장 별난 연주로 사방의 메아리를 깨웠다. 그래도 효과는 있었다. 사고로 쓰러진 사람들을 깨웠을 뿐 아니라 그들을 도와줄 손길까지 불러 모았으니까. 멀리서 불빛이 반짝였고, 사람들이 벌써 움직이고 있었다."]], [["In fact, a man on horseback galloped down, before the passengers were well collected together; and a careful investigation being instituted, it appeared that the lady inside had broken her lamp, and the gentleman his head; that the two front outsides had escaped with black eyes; the box with a bloody nose; the coachman with a contusion on the temple; Mr. Squeers with a portmanteau bruise on his back; and the remaining passengers without any injury at all--thanks to the softness of the snow-drift in which they had been overturned. These facts were no sooner thoroughly ascertained, than the lady gave several indications of fainting, but being forewarned that if she did, she must be carried on some gentleman’s shoulders to the nearest public-house, she prudently thought better of it, and walked back with the rest.", "사실 승객들이 채 다 모이기도 전에 한 남자가 말을 타고 달려왔다. 그러고는 꼼꼼히 살펴본 끝에 이런 사정이 드러났다. 마차 안에 있던 부인은 등을 깨뜨렸고, 신사는 머리를 깨뜨렸다. 앞쪽 바깥자리에 앉았던 두 사람은 눈에 멍이 든 정도로 끝났고, 마부석 옆자리 사람은 코피가 났으며, 마부는 관자놀이에 타박상을 입었다. 스퀴어스 씨는 등에 여행 가방만 한 멍이 들었고, 나머지 승객들은 아무 데도 다치지 않았다. 그들이 처박힌 눈더미가 푹신했던 덕분이었다. 이 사실들이 다 밝혀지자마자 부인은 금방이라도 기절할 듯한 기색을 여러 번 내비쳤다. 그러나 만약 기절하면 어느 신사분 어깨에 업혀 가장 가까운 주막까지 가야 한다는 말을 미리 듣고는, 신중하게 마음을 고쳐먹고 다른 사람들과 함께 걸어서 돌아갔다."]], [["They found on reaching it, that it was a lonely place with no very great accommodation in the way of apartments--that portion of its resources being all comprised in one public room with a sanded floor, and a chair or two. However, a large faggot and a plentiful supply of coals being heaped upon the fire, the appearance of things was not long in mending; and, by the time they had washed off all effaceable marks of the late accident, the room was warm and light, which was a most agreeable exchange for the cold and darkness out of doors.", "주막에 닿아 보니 외딴곳이었고, 방이라고 할 만한 게 변변치 않았다. 그곳의 살림이라곤 모래를 깔아 둔 바닥에 의자 한두 개가 놓인 공동 거실 하나가 전부였다. 그래도 큼직한 장작 한 다발과 석탄을 넉넉히 불에 쌓아 올리니 사정은 금세 나아졌다. 그리고 그들이 사고 자국 가운데 지울 수 있는 건 다 씻어 냈을 무렵, 방은 따뜻하고 환해졌다. 바깥의 춥고 캄캄한 것과 견주면 더없이 반가운 변화였다."]], [["‘Well, Mr.", "\"자, 니클비 씨.\""], ["Nickleby,’ said Squeers, insinuating himself into the warmest corner, ‘you did very right to catch hold of them horses.", "스퀴어스가 가장 따뜻한 구석으로 슬그머니 파고들며 말했다. \"그 말들 붙잡은 건 아주 잘한 일이오."], ["I should have done it myself if I had come to in time, but I am very glad you did it.", "내가 제때 정신을 차렸으면 나도 그렇게 했겠지만, 어쨌든 자네가 해 줘서 참 다행이오."], ["You did it very well; very well.’", "아주 잘했소. 아주 잘했어.\""]], [["‘So well,’ said the merry-faced gentleman, who did not seem to approve very much of the patronising tone adopted by Squeers, ‘that if they had not been firmly checked when they were, you would most probably have had no brains left to teach with.’", "\"어찌나 잘했는지,\" 스퀴어스가 내세우는 거들먹거리는 말투가 영 마뜩잖아 보이는, 쾌활한 얼굴의 신사가 말했다. \"그때 말들이 단단히 붙들리지 않았더라면, 당신은 아이들 가르칠 머리도 남아나지 못했을 거요.\""]], [["This remark called up a discourse relative to the promptitude Nicholas had displayed, and he was overwhelmed with compliments and commendations.", "이 말에 니콜라스가 보여 준 재빠른 솜씨를 두고 이야기가 오갔고, 그는 칭찬과 찬사 세례에 파묻혔다."]], [["‘I am very glad to have escaped, of course,’ observed Squeers: ‘every man is glad when he escapes from danger; but if any one of my charges had been hurt--if I had been prevented from restoring any one of these little boys to his parents whole and sound as I received him--what would have been my feelings? Why the wheel a-top of my head would have been far preferable to it.’", "\"물론 무사히 빠져나와서 정말 기쁘오.\" 스퀴어스가 한마디 했다. \"위험에서 벗어나면 누구든 기쁜 법이지. 하지만 만약 내가 맡은 아이들 가운데 하나라도 다쳤더라면, 그러니까 이 어린 녀석들 중 누구 하나라도 받았을 때처럼 멀쩡하게 부모 품에 돌려보내지 못하게 됐더라면, 내 심정이 어땠겠소? 차라리 그 바퀴가 내 머리 위로 떨어지는 게 훨씬 나았을 거요.\""]], [["‘Are they all brothers, sir?’ inquired the lady who had carried the ‘Davy’ or safety-lamp.", "\"이 애들이 다 형제인가요, 선생님?\" '데이비' 등, 그러니까 안전등을 들고 왔던 부인이 물었다."]], [["‘In one sense they are, ma’am,’ replied Squeers, diving into his greatcoat pocket for cards.", "\"어떤 의미에서는 그렇지요, 부인.\" 스퀴어스가 명함을 찾으려고 외투 주머니를 뒤지며 대답했다."], ["‘They are all under the same parental and affectionate treatment.", "\"이 애들은 다 똑같이 부모처럼 아끼는 손길 아래 있으니까요."], ["Mrs. Squeers and myself are a mother and father to every one of ‘em.", "스퀴어스 부인과 나는 이 애들 하나하나에게 어머니이자 아버지랍니다."], ["Mr. Nickleby, hand the lady them cards, and offer these to the gentleman.", "니클비 씨, 부인께 이 명함을 건네드리고, 이건 신사분께 드리게나."], ["Perhaps they might know of some parents that would be glad to avail themselves of the establishment.’", "어쩌면 학교에 보내고 싶어 할 부모를 알고 계실지도 모르니까.\""]], [["Expressing himself to this effect, Mr.", "이런 말을 늘어놓으며, 거저 광고할 기회는 절대 놓치지 않는 스퀴어스 씨는 무릎에 손을 얹고는, 지을 수 있는 한 가장 자애로운 표정으로 학생들을 바라보았다."], ["Squeers, who lost no opportunity of advertising gratuitously, placed his hands upon his knees, and looked at the pupils with as much benignity as he could possibly affect, while Nicholas, blushing with shame, handed round the cards as directed.", "그사이 니콜라스는 부끄러움에 얼굴을 붉히며 시키는 대로 명함을 돌렸다."]], [["‘I hope you suffer no inconvenience from the overturn, ma’am?’ said the merry-faced gentleman, addressing the fastidious lady, as though he were charitably desirous to change the subject.", "\"마차가 엎어진 일로 불편한 데는 없으신지요, 부인?\" 쾌활한 얼굴의 신사가, 자비롭게도 화제를 바꿔 주려는 듯 까다로운 부인에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["‘No bodily inconvenience,’ replied the lady.", "\"몸이 불편한 데는 없어요.\" 부인이 대답했다."]], [["‘No mental inconvenience, I hope?’", "\"마음이 불편하지도 않으시길 바랍니다만.\""]], [["‘The subject is a very painful one to my feelings, sir,’ replied the lady with strong emotion; ‘and I beg you as a gentleman, not to refer to it.’", "\"그 이야기는 제 마음을 몹시 아프게 하는군요, 선생님.\" 부인이 격한 감정을 담아 대꾸했다. \"신사로서 부탁드리니, 그 일은 다시 입에 올리지 말아 주세요.\""]], [["‘Dear me,’ said the merry-faced gentleman, looking merrier still, ‘I merely intended to inquire--’", "\"이런.\" 쾌활한 얼굴의 신사가 더욱 쾌활해진 얼굴로 말했다. \"저는 그저 여쭤보려던 것뿐인데요.\""]], [["‘I hope no inquiries will be made,’ said the lady, ‘or I shall be compelled to throw myself on the protection of the other gentlemen. Landlord, pray direct a boy to keep watch outside the door--and if a green chariot passes in the direction of Grantham, to stop it instantly.’", "\"아무것도 묻지 않으셨으면 해요.\" 부인이 말했다. \"안 그러면 저는 다른 신사분들의 보호에 기댈 수밖에 없겠어요. 주인장, 부디 아이 하나를 시켜서 문밖을 지키게 해 줘요. 그리고 초록색 마차가 그랜섬 쪽으로 지나가거든 당장 세우라고요.\""]], [["The people of the house were evidently overcome by this request, and when the lady charged the boy to remember, as a means of identifying the expected green chariot, that it would have a coachman with a gold-laced hat on the box, and a footman, most probably in silk stockings, behind, the attentions of the good woman of the inn were redoubled. Even the box-passenger caught the infection, and growing wonderfully deferential, immediately inquired whether there was not very good society in that neighbourhood, to which the lady replied yes, there was: in a manner which sufficiently implied that she moved at the very tiptop and summit of it all.", "주막 사람들은 이 부탁에 단단히 압도된 게 분명했다. 그리고 부인이 그 기다리는 초록색 마차를 알아볼 표시라며, 마부석에는 금실 두른 모자를 쓴 마부가 앉아 있고 뒤쪽에는 십중팔구 비단 양말을 신은 하인이 서 있을 거라고 아이에게 일러두자, 마음씨 좋은 주막 안주인의 정성은 곱절로 늘어났다. 마부석 옆자리에 앉았던 승객까지 그 분위기에 물들었다. 그러더니 놀랄 만큼 공손해져서는, 이 동네에 상류 사회가 꽤 있지 않느냐고 대뜸 물었다. 부인은 그렇다고, 있다고 대답했는데, 그 말투가 자기야말로 그 사회의 맨 꼭대기, 정점에서 노니는 사람이라는 뜻을 충분히 풍겼다."]], [["‘As the guard has gone on horseback to Grantham to get another coach,’ said the good-tempered gentleman when they had been all sitting round the fire, for some time, in silence, ‘and as he must be gone a couple of hours at the very least, I propose a bowl of hot punch.", "\"차장이 마차를 새로 구하러 말을 타고 그랜섬까지 갔으니,\" 모두가 한동안 말없이 불가에 둘러앉아 있다가 그 사람 좋은 신사가 입을 열었다."], ["What say you, sir?’", "\"아무리 빨라도 두 시간은 걸릴 테니, 따뜻한 펀치 한 사발 어떻소? 어떻게 생각하시오, 선생?\""]], [["This question was addressed to the broken-headed inside, who was a man of very genteel appearance, dressed in mourning.", "이 물음은 머리를 다친 마차 안 승객을 향한 것이었다. 그는 아주 점잖은 차림에 상복을 입은 남자였다."], ["He was not past the middle age, but his hair was grey; it seemed to have been prematurely turned by care or sorrow.", "중년을 넘기지는 않았지만 머리는 희끗했다. 근심이나 슬픔 때문에 일찍 센 모양이었다."], ["He readily acceded to the proposal, and appeared to be prepossessed by the frank good-nature of the individual from whom it emanated.", "그는 흔쾌히 그 제안을 받아들였고, 제안을 꺼낸 사람의 꾸밈없는 선량함에 마음이 끌린 듯했다."]], [["This latter personage took upon himself the office of tapster when the punch was ready, and after dispensing it all round, led the conversation to the antiquities of York, with which both he and the grey-haired gentleman appeared to be well acquainted.", "펀치가 다 되자 이 사람 좋은 신사는 자청해서 술 따르는 일을 맡았다. 그러고는 모두에게 한 잔씩 돌린 뒤, 요크의 옛 유적 이야기로 화제를 이끌었다."], ["When this topic flagged, he turned with a smile to the grey-headed gentleman, and asked if he could sing.", "그와 희끗한 머리의 신사 둘 다 그 방면에 밝아 보였다. 이 화제가 시들해지자, 그는 빙긋 웃으며 희끗한 머리의 신사에게 노래를 부를 줄 아느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘I cannot indeed,’ replied gentleman, smiling in his turn.", "\"부를 줄 모릅니다, 정말로.\" 희끗한 머리의 신사도 마주 웃으며 대답했다."]], [["‘That’s a pity,’ said the owner of the good-humoured countenance.", "\"그것참 아쉽군요.\" 쾌활한 얼굴의 임자가 말했다."], ["‘Is there nobody here who can sing a song to lighten the time?’", "\"여기 누구 시간 좀 가볍게 보내게 노래 한 곡 불러 줄 분 안 계시오?\""]], [["The passengers, one and all, protested that they could not; that they wished they could; that they couldn’t remember the words of anything without the book; and so forth.", "승객들은 너 나 할 것 없이 한사코 못 부른다고, 부를 수 있으면 좋겠다고, 책 없이는 무슨 노래든 가사가 도무지 생각나지 않는다고, 그런 식으로 손사래를 쳤다."]], [["‘Perhaps the lady would not object,’ said the president with great respect, and a merry twinkle in his eye.", "\"어쩌면 부인께서는 마다하지 않으실지도 모르지요.\" 좌장은 더없이 정중하게, 눈에 짓궂은 빛을 반짝이며 말했다."], ["‘Some little Italian thing out of the last opera brought out in town, would be most acceptable I am sure.’", "\"런던에서 갓 올린 오페라에 나오는 이탈리아 노래 한 소절쯤이면 다들 더없이 반길 텐데요.\""]], [["As the lady condescended to make no reply, but tossed her head contemptuously, and murmured some further expression of surprise regarding the absence of the green chariot, one or two voices urged upon the president himself, the propriety of making an attempt for the general benefit.", "부인이 황송하게도 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고 그저 경멸하듯 고개를 홱 젖히며, 초록색 마차가 안 온다는 둥 또 무어라 놀라움을 중얼거리자, 한두 사람이 좌장 본인에게 모두를 위해 한번 나서 보는 게 어떻겠느냐고 권했다."]], [["‘I would if I could,’ said he of the good-tempered face; ‘for I hold that in this, as in all other cases where people who are strangers to each other are thrown unexpectedly together, they should endeavour to render themselves as pleasant, for the joint sake of the little community, as possible.’", "\"할 수 있으면 하지요.\" 사람 좋은 얼굴의 신사가 말했다. \"이런 경우든 다른 경우든, 서로 낯선 사람들이 뜻하지 않게 한자리에 모이게 되면, 이 작은 모임 모두를 위해서 되도록 서로 즐겁게 해 주려고 애써야 한다는 게 내 생각이오.\""]], [["‘I wish the maxim were more generally acted on, in all cases,’ said the grey-headed gentleman.", "\"그 말씀이 어떤 경우에든 좀 더 널리 지켜졌으면 좋겠군요.\" 희끗한 머리의 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘I’m glad to hear it,’ returned the other.", "\"그 말 들으니 기쁘오.\" 상대가 받았다."], ["‘Perhaps, as you can’t sing, you’ll tell us a story?’", "\"그럼 노래는 못 하신다니, 이야기라도 하나 들려주시겠소?\""]], [["‘Nay. I should ask you.’", "\"아니, 그건 제가 청해야 할 말인데요.\""]], [["‘After you, I will, with pleasure.’", "\"당신 다음에 나도 기꺼이 하리다.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ said the grey-haired gentleman, smiling, ‘Well, let it be so.", "\"정말이오?\" 희끗한 머리의 신사가 빙긋 웃으며 말했다."], ["I fear the turn of my thoughts is not calculated to lighten the time you must pass here; but you have brought this upon yourselves, and shall judge.", "\"좋소, 그럽시다. 다만 내 생각의 흐름이 여기서 보내실 시간을 가볍게 해 주지는 못할 것 같아 걱정이오."], ["We were speaking of York Minster just now.", "하지만 자초한 일이니, 판단은 여러분 몫이오. 우리가 방금 요크 대성당 이야기를 했지요."], ["My story shall have some reference to it.", "내 이야기도 거기에 얼마쯤 얽힌 거라오."], ["Let us call it", "제목을 이렇게 붙입시다."]], [["# THE FIVE SISTERS OF YORK", "# THE FIVE SISTERS OF YORK"]], [["After a murmur of approbation from the other passengers, during which the fastidious lady drank a glass of punch unobserved, the grey-headed gentleman thus went on:", "요크의 다섯 자매\n다른 승객들이 좋다는 듯 웅성거리는 사이, 까다로운 부인은 아무도 모르게 펀치를 한 잔 들이켰다. 그러고 나서 희끗한 머리의 신사는 이렇게 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["‘A great many years ago--for the fifteenth century was scarce two years old at the time, and King Henry the Fourth sat upon the throne of England--there dwelt, in the ancient city of York, five maiden sisters, the subjects of my tale.", "\"아주 오래전 일이오. 그때만 해도 십오 세기가 채 두 해도 안 됐고, 영국의 왕좌에는 헨리 사세가 앉아 있었지요. 그 무렵 유서 깊은 도시 요크에, 내 이야기의 주인공인 다섯 처녀 자매가 살고 있었소."]], [["‘These five sisters were all of surpassing beauty.", "이 다섯 자매는 하나같이 빼어나게 아름다웠소."], ["The eldest was in her twenty-third year, the second a year younger, the third a year younger than the second, and the fourth a year younger than the third.", "맏이는 스물세 살이었고, 둘째는 그보다 한 살 아래, 셋째는 둘째보다 한 살 아래, 넷째는 셋째보다 한 살 아래였소."], ["They were tall stately figures, with dark flashing eyes and hair of jet; dignity and grace were in their every movement; and the fame of their great beauty had spread through all the country round.", "키가 크고 기품 있는 자태에, 검은 눈은 반짝였고 머리칼은 칠흑 같았지요. 몸짓 하나하나에 위엄과 우아함이 깃들어 있었고, 그 대단한 미모의 명성은 온 고장에 퍼졌소."]], [["‘But, if the four elder sisters were lovely, how beautiful was the youngest, a fair creature of sixteen!", "하지만 위의 네 자매가 사랑스러웠다 한들, 막내는 또 얼마나 아름다웠겠소. 열여섯의 고운 처녀였지요."], ["The blushing tints in the soft bloom on the fruit, or the delicate painting on the flower, are not more exquisite than was the blending of the rose and lily in her gentle face, or the deep blue of her eye.", "과일에 부드럽게 피어오른 발그레한 빛깔도, 꽃에 섬세하게 입혀진 색채도, 그 다정한 얼굴에 어우러진 장미와 백합의 빛깔보다, 또 그 깊고 푸른 눈동자보다 더 곱지는 못했소."], ["The vine, in all its elegant luxuriance, is not more graceful than were the clusters of rich brown hair that sported round her brow.", "우아하게 흐드러진 포도 덩굴도, 그 이마를 감싸고 노니는 짙은 갈색 머리채만큼 고아하지는 않았지요."]], [["‘If we all had hearts like those which beat so lightly in the bosoms of the young and beautiful, what a heaven this earth would be!", "우리 모두가 젊고 아름다운 이들의 가슴속에서 그토록 가볍게 뛰는 그런 마음을 지녔다면, 이 땅이 얼마나 천국 같을까요!"], ["If, while our bodies grow old and withered, our hearts could but retain their early youth and freshness, of what avail would be our sorrows and sufferings!", "우리 몸은 늙고 시들어 가도 마음만은 처음의 젊음과 싱그러움을 간직할 수 있다면, 우리의 슬픔과 고통이 다 무슨 소용일까요!"], ["But, the faint image of Eden which is stamped upon them in childhood, chafes and rubs in our rough struggles with the world, and soon wears away: too often to leave nothing but a mournful blank remaining.", "하지만 어린 시절 마음에 새겨진 그 희미한 에덴의 모습은, 세상과 거칠게 부대끼는 가운데 닳고 쓸려 이내 사라지고 마오. 너무도 흔히, 슬픈 빈자리 말고는 아무것도 남기지 않은 채 말이오."]], [["‘The heart of this fair girl bounded with joy and gladness.", "이 고운 처녀의 마음은 기쁨과 즐거움으로 뛰놀았소."], ["Devoted attachment to her sisters, and a fervent love of all beautiful things in nature, were its pure affections.", "언니들을 향한 지극한 정과, 자연의 온갖 아름다운 것을 향한 뜨거운 사랑이 그 순수한 애정의 전부였지요."], ["Her gleesome voice and merry laugh were the sweetest music of their home.", "그 흥겨운 목소리와 명랑한 웃음소리는 집 안에서 가장 달콤한 음악이었소."], ["She was its very light and life.", "그 처녀야말로 그 집의 빛이자 생명이었지요."], ["The brightest flowers in the garden were reared by her; the caged birds sang when they heard her voice, and pined when they missed its sweetness.", "정원에서 가장 환한 꽃들은 그 손길에 길러졌고, 새장 속 새들은 그 목소리를 들으면 노래했다가 그 달콤함이 사라지면 시름시름 앓았소. 앨리스, 사랑스러운 앨리스."], ["Alice, dear Alice; what living thing within the sphere of her gentle witchery, could fail to love her!", "그 부드러운 마법이 미치는 곳에서, 살아 있는 그 무엇이 그를 사랑하지 않을 수 있었겠소!"]], [["‘You may seek in vain, now, for the spot on which these sisters lived, for their very names have passed away, and dusty antiquaries tell of them as of a fable. But they dwelt in an old wooden house--old even in those days--with overhanging gables and balconies of rudely-carved oak, which stood within a pleasant orchard, and was surrounded by a rough stone wall, whence a stout archer might have winged an arrow to St Mary’s Abbey. The old abbey flourished then; and the five sisters, living on its fair domains, paid yearly dues to the black monks of St Benedict, to which fraternity it belonged.", "이제 이 자매들이 살던 곳을 아무리 찾아본들 헛수고일 거요. 그들의 이름마저 잊혔고, 먼지투성이 골동품 학자들이나 옛 우화처럼 그들을 이야기할 뿐이니까. 하지만 그들은 낡은 나무집에서 살았소. 그 시절 기준으로도 낡은 집이었지요. 처마가 길게 드리워지고, 투박하게 새긴 떡갈나무 발코니가 달린 집이었소. 그 집은 아늑한 과수원 안에 서 있었고, 거친 돌담으로 둘러싸여 있었지요. 그 담에서라면 솜씨 좋은 궁수가 성 메리 수도원까지 화살을 날릴 만했소. 그 옛 수도원은 그때 한창 번성했고, 다섯 자매는 그 너른 영지 안에 살며 해마다 그곳에 딸린 성 베네딕트 수도회의 검은 옷 수도사들에게 세를 바쳤소."]], [["‘It was a bright and sunny morning in the pleasant time of summer, when one of those black monks emerged from the abbey portal, and bent his steps towards the house of the fair sisters.", "화창하고 햇살 좋은 어느 여름날 아침이었소. 그 검은 옷 수도사 가운데 하나가 수도원 정문을 나서더니 고운 자매들의 집 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼지요."], ["Heaven above was blue, and earth beneath was green; the river glistened like a path of diamonds in the sun; the birds poured forth their songs from the shady trees; the lark soared high above the waving corn; and the deep buzz of insects filled the air.", "위로는 하늘이 푸르렀고, 아래로는 땅이 푸르렀소. 강물은 햇빛 속에서 다이아몬드를 깔아 놓은 길처럼 반짝였지요."], ["Everything looked gay and smiling; but the holy man walked gloomily on, with his eyes bent upon the ground.", "새들은 그늘진 나무에서 노래를 쏟아 냈고, 종달새는 일렁이는 밀밭 위로 높이 솟아올랐으며, 벌레들의 깊은 윙윙거림이 공기를 가득 채웠소. 모든 것이 즐겁고 환하게 웃고 있었지요."], ["The beauty of the earth is but a breath, and man is but a shadow.", "하지만 그 거룩한 수도사는 눈을 땅에 박은 채 침울하게 걸어갔소. 땅의 아름다움이란 한낱 숨결에 지나지 않고, 사람이란 한낱 그림자에 불과하지요."], ["What sympathy should a holy preacher have with either?", "거룩한 설교자가 그 둘 가운데 어느 쪽엔들 마음을 둘 까닭이 있겠소?"]], [["‘With eyes bent upon the ground, then, or only raised enough to prevent his stumbling over such obstacles as lay in his way, the religious man moved slowly forward until he reached a small postern in the wall of the sisters’ orchard, through which he passed, closing it behind him. The noise of soft voices in conversation, and of merry laughter, fell upon his ears ere he had advanced many paces; and raising his eyes higher than was his humble wont, he descried, at no great distance, the five sisters seated on the grass, with Alice in the centre: all busily plying their customary task of embroidering.", "그렇게 눈을 땅에 박은 채, 아니면 앞을 가로막는 것에 걸려 넘어지지 않을 만큼만 겨우 눈을 들고서, 그 수도사는 자매들 과수원 담에 난 작은 쪽문에 이를 때까지 천천히 나아갔소. 그러고는 그리로 들어가 등 뒤로 문을 닫았지요. 몇 걸음 채 옮기기도 전에, 도란도란 정담을 나누는 부드러운 목소리와 명랑한 웃음소리가 그의 귀에 와닿았소. 평소의 겸손한 버릇보다 좀 더 높이 눈을 들자, 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 다섯 자매가 풀밭에 앉아 있는 게 보였소. 앨리스가 한가운데 앉아, 다들 늘 하던 자수 일에 부지런히 손을 놀리고 있었지요."]], [["‘“Save you, fair daughters!”", "'평안하시오, 고운 따님들이여!'"], ["said the friar; and fair in truth they were.", "수도사가 말했소. 정말이지 그들은 고왔지요."], ["Even a monk might have loved them as choice masterpieces of his Maker’s hand.", "수도사조차 창조주의 손이 빚어낸 빼어난 걸작이라 여겨 사랑할 만했소."]], [["‘The sisters saluted the holy man with becoming reverence, and the eldest motioned him to a mossy seat beside them.", "자매들은 알맞은 공경을 담아 그 거룩한 사람에게 인사했고, 맏이는 그에게 옆에 놓인 이끼 낀 자리를 권했소."], ["But the good friar shook his head, and bumped himself down on a very hard stone,--at which, no doubt, approving angels were gratified.", "하지만 마음씨 좋은 수도사는 고개를 가로젓더니, 아주 딱딱한 돌 위에 털썩 주저앉았지요. 틀림없이 흐뭇해하는 천사들이 그 모습을 보고 기뻐했을 거요."]], [["‘“Ye were merry, daughters,”", "'즐거워들 보이는구려, 따님들.'"], ["said the monk.", "수도사가 말했소."]], [["‘“You know how light of heart sweet Alice is,”", "'사랑스러운 앨리스가 얼마나 마음이 가벼운 아이인지 아시잖아요.'"], ["replied the eldest sister, passing her fingers through the tresses of the smiling girl.", "맏이가 웃고 있는 처녀의 머리채를 손으로 쓸어내리며 대답했소."]], [["‘“And what joy and cheerfulness it wakes up within us, to see all nature beaming in brightness and sunshine, father,”", "'그리고 온 자연이 환한 빛과 햇살 속에서 빛나는 걸 보면 저희 마음속에 얼마나 큰 기쁨과 즐거움이 일어나는데요, 신부님.'"], ["added Alice, blushing beneath the stern look of the recluse.", "앨리스가 그 은둔자의 엄한 눈길 아래 얼굴을 붉히며 덧붙였소."]], [["‘The monk answered not, save by a grave inclination of the head, and the sisters pursued their task in silence.", "수도사는 그저 무겁게 고개를 한 번 숙일 뿐 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았고, 자매들은 말없이 일을 이어 갔소."]], [["‘“Still wasting the precious hours,”", "'여전히 귀한 시간을 허비하는구려.'"], ["said the monk at length, turning to the eldest sister as he spoke, “still wasting the precious hours on this vain trifling.", "마침내 수도사가 맏이를 돌아보며 말했소. '여전히 이 헛된 장난질에 귀한 시간을 허비하다니."], ["Alas, alas!", "아아, 슬프구나!"], ["that the few bubbles on the surface of eternity--all that Heaven wills we should see of that dark deep stream--should be so lightly scattered!”", "영원의 표면에 떠 있는 그 몇 안 되는 거품들, 그 어둡고 깊은 강줄기 가운데 하늘이 우리에게 보도록 허락하신 그 전부를, 이토록 가벼이 흩뿌리다니!'"]], [["‘“Father,”", "'신부님.'"], ["urged the maiden, pausing, as did each of the others, in her busy task, “we have prayed at matins, our daily alms have been distributed at the gate, the sick peasants have been tended,--all our morning tasks have been performed.", "처녀가 다른 자매들처럼 바쁜 손길을 멈추며 말했소. '저희는 아침 기도를 올렸고, 날마다 베푸는 자선을 문간에서 나누었으며, 병든 농부들을 돌보았어요."], ["I hope our occupation is a blameless one?’", "오전에 할 일은 다 마쳤지요. 저희가 하는 일이 흠잡을 데 없는 일이기를 바라요.'"]], [["‘“See here,”", "'이걸 보시오.'"], ["said the friar, taking the frame from her hand, “an intricate winding of gaudy colours, without purpose or object, unless it be that one day it is destined for some vain ornament, to minister to the pride of your frail and giddy sex.", "수도사가 그의 손에서 수틀을 받아 들며 말했소. '아무 뜻도, 목적도 없이 화려한 색실만 복잡하게 얽어 놓았구려."], ["Day after day has been employed upon this senseless task, and yet it is not half accomplished.", "언젠가 어느 헛된 장식이 되어, 가냘프고 들뜬 그대들 여인네의 허영을 거들 운명이 아니라면 말이오."], ["The shade of each departed day falls upon our graves, and the worm exults as he beholds it, to know that we are hastening thither.", "날이면 날마다 이 부질없는 일에 매달리고도 아직 절반도 못 끝냈구려. 떠나간 하루하루의 그림자가 우리 무덤 위로 드리우고, 우리가 그리로 서둘러 가는 걸 지켜보며 벌레가 기뻐 날뛰지요."], ["Daughters, is there no better way to pass the fleeting hours?”", "따님들, 덧없이 흐르는 시간을 보낼 더 나은 길은 없겠소?'"]], [["‘The four elder sisters cast down their eyes as if abashed by the holy man’s reproof, but Alice raised hers, and bent them mildly on the friar.", "위의 네 자매는 거룩한 사람의 꾸짖음에 부끄러운 듯 눈을 내리깔았소. 하지만 앨리스는 눈을 들어 수도사를 부드럽게 바라보았지요."]], [["‘“Our dear mother,”", "'사랑하는 저희 어머니께서는.'"], ["said the maiden; “Heaven rest her soul!”", "처녀가 말했소. '하늘이 그 영혼을 편히 쉬게 하시기를!'"]], [["‘“Amen!”", "'아멘!'"], ["cried the friar in a deep voice.", "수도사가 깊은 목소리로 외쳤소."]], [["‘“Our dear mother,” faltered the fair Alice, “was living when these long tasks began, and bade us, when she should be no more, ply them in all discretion and cheerfulness, in our leisure hours; she said that if in harmless mirth and maidenly pursuits we passed those hours together, they would prove the happiest and most peaceful of our lives, and that if, in later times, we went forth into the world, and mingled with its cares and trials--if, allured by its temptations and dazzled by its glitter, we ever forgot that love and duty which should bind, in holy ties, the children of one loved parent--a glance at the old work of our common girlhood would awaken good thoughts of bygone days, and soften our hearts to affection and love.”", "'사랑하는 저희 어머니께서는,' 고운 앨리스가 떨리는 목소리로 이었소. '이 긴 일을 처음 시작했을 무렵 살아 계셨어요. 그러고는 당신이 세상에 없게 되거든, 한가한 시간에 늘 분별 있고 즐겁게 이 일을 해 나가라고 이르셨지요. 어머니께서는 이렇게 말씀하셨어요. 만약 저희가 죄 없는 즐거움과 처녀다운 일거리 속에서 그 시간을 함께 보낸다면, 그 시간이야말로 저희 삶에서 가장 행복하고 평화로운 때가 될 거라고요. 또 훗날 저희가 세상에 나가 그 근심과 시련에 섞이게 되면, 그러니까 세상의 유혹에 끌리고 그 화려함에 눈멀어, 한 사랑하는 부모의 자식들을 거룩한 끈으로 묶어야 할 그 사랑과 도리를 언젠가 잊게 된다면, 함께한 처녀 시절의 이 옛 작품을 한 번 들여다보는 것만으로도 지난날의 좋은 생각들이 되살아나, 마음이 누그러져 다시 다정함과 사랑으로 돌아오게 될 거라고요.'"]], [["‘“Alice speaks truly, father,”", "'앨리스 말이 옳아요, 신부님.'"], ["said the elder sister, somewhat proudly.", "맏이가 다소 자랑스러운 듯 말했소."], ["And so saying she resumed her work, as did the others.", "그러고는 다시 일을 시작했고, 다른 자매들도 그러했지요."]], [["‘It was a kind of sampler of large size, that each sister had before her; the device was of a complex and intricate description, and the pattern and colours of all five were the same.", "자매마다 앞에 큼직한 자수 견본 같은 것을 놓고 있었소. 무늬는 복잡하고 정교했으며, 다섯 자매 모두 본과 색이 똑같았지요."], ["The sisters bent gracefully over their work; the monk, resting his chin upon his hands, looked from one to the other in silence.", "자매들은 우아하게 일감 위로 몸을 숙였고, 수도사는 두 손에 턱을 괴고 말없이 이 사람 저 사람을 번갈아 바라보았소."]], [["‘“How much better,”", "'얼마나 더 나은 일이겠소.'"], ["he said at length, “to shun all such thoughts and chances, and, in the peaceful shelter of the church, devote your lives to Heaven!", "마침내 그가 말했소. '그런 온갖 생각과 우연을 피해, 교회의 평화로운 품 안에서 그대들의 삶을 하늘에 바친다면 말이오."], ["Infancy, childhood, the prime of life, and old age, wither as rapidly as they crowd upon each other.", "갓난 시절, 어린 시절, 한창때, 그리고 늙음이 서로 앞다투어 몰려오는 만큼이나 빠르게 시들어 가지요."], ["Think how human dust rolls onward to the tomb, and turning your faces steadily towards that goal, avoid the cloud which takes its rise among the pleasures of the world, and cheats the senses of their votaries.", "인간의 티끌이 어떻게 무덤을 향해 굴러가는지 생각해 보시오. 그리고 그 끝을 향해 한결같이 얼굴을 돌린 채, 세상의 쾌락에서 피어올라 그 신도들의 감각을 속이는 그 구름을 피하시오."], ["The veil, daughters, the veil!”", "면사포요, 따님들, 면사포!'"]], [["‘“Never, sisters,”", "'절대 안 돼요, 언니들.' 앨리스가 외쳤소."], ["cried Alice.", "'하늘의 빛과 공기를, 땅의 싱그러움을, 그리고 그 위에 숨 쉬는 온갖 아름다운 것을, 차디찬 수도원과 골방과 맞바꾸지 마요."], ["“Barter not the light and air of heaven, and the freshness of earth and all the beautiful things which breathe upon it, for the cold cloister and the cell.", "자연이 베푼 축복이야말로 삶의 마땅한 보물이고, 우리는 죄 없이 그것을 함께 누릴 수 있어요. 죽음은 우리에게 주어진 무거운 몫이지만, 아, 우리 둘레에 생명을 두고 죽어요."], ["Nature’s own blessings are the proper goods of life, and we may share them sinlessly together.", "우리 차가운 심장이 멎을 때, 곁에서 따뜻한 심장이 뛰게 해요. 우리의 마지막 눈길이 하느님께서 당신의 환한 하늘에 두신 그 경계에 닿게 해요."], ["To die is our heavy portion, but, oh, let us die with life about us; when our cold hearts cease to beat, let warm hearts be beating near; let our last look be upon the bounds which God has set to his own bright skies, and not on stone walls and bars of iron!", "돌담과 쇠창살이 아니라요! 사랑하는 언니들, 언니들이 원한다면 이 푸른 정원 안에서 살다 죽어요."], ["Dear sisters, let us live and die, if you list, in this green garden’s compass; only shun the gloom and sadness of a cloister, and we shall be happy.”", "다만 수도원의 음울함과 슬픔만은 피해요. 그러면 우리는 행복할 거예요.'"]], [["‘The tears fell fast from the maiden’s eyes as she closed her impassioned appeal, and hid her face in the bosom of her sister.", "처녀는 그 간절한 호소를 마치며 눈물을 비 오듯 흘렸고, 언니의 품에 얼굴을 묻었소."]], [["‘“Take comfort, Alice,”", "'마음 놓아라, 앨리스.'"], ["said the eldest, kissing her fair forehead.", "맏이가 그 고운 이마에 입 맞추며 말했소."], ["“The veil shall never cast its shadow on thy young brow.", "'면사포 따위가 네 젊은 이마에 그늘을 드리우는 일은 결코 없을 거야."], ["How say you, sisters?", "너희는 어떠니, 동생들?"], ["For yourselves you speak, and not for Alice, or for me.”", "너희 자신을 두고 하는 말이야. 앨리스나 나를 두고 하는 말이 아니라.'"]], [["‘The sisters, as with one accord, cried that their lot was cast together, and that there were dwellings for peace and virtue beyond the convent’s walls.", "자매들은 한목소리로 외쳤소. 우리 운명은 함께 묶여 있다고, 수도원 담장 너머에도 평화와 미덕이 머물 곳은 있다고 말이오."]], [["‘“Father,”", "'신부님.'"], ["said the eldest lady, rising with dignity, “you hear our final resolve.", "맏이가 위엄 있게 일어서며 말했소. '저희의 마지막 결심을 들으셨지요."], ["The same pious care which enriched the abbey of St Mary, and left us, orphans, to its holy guardianship, directed that no constraint should be imposed upon our inclinations, but that we should be free to live according to our choice.", "성 메리 수도원을 부유하게 하고, 고아가 된 저희를 그 거룩한 보살핌에 맡기신 그 경건한 배려는, 동시에 저희 뜻에 그 어떤 강요도 하지 말 것을, 저희가 택한 대로 자유로이 살게 할 것을 정해 두었어요."], ["Let us hear no more of this, we pray you.", "부디 이 이야기는 더 이상 듣지 않게 해 주세요."], ["Sisters, it is nearly noon.", "동생들아, 곧 정오야."], ["Let us take shelter until evening!”", "저녁까지 안에 들어가 쉬자!'"], ["With a reverence to the friar, the lady rose and walked towards the house, hand in hand with Alice; the other sisters followed.", "맏이는 수도사에게 정중히 절하고는 일어나, 앨리스와 손을 맞잡고 집 쪽으로 걸어갔소. 다른 자매들도 뒤를 따랐지요."]], [["‘The holy man, who had often urged the same point before, but had never met with so direct a repulse, walked some little distance behind, with his eyes bent upon the earth, and his lips moving AS IF in prayer.", "전에도 여러 번 같은 말을 했지만 이토록 단호하게 거절당한 적은 없던 그 거룩한 사람은, 눈을 땅에 박은 채 입술을 기도하듯 달싹이며 조금 뒤처져 걸었소."], ["As the sisters reached the porch, he quickened his pace, and called upon them to stop.", "자매들이 현관에 이르자, 그는 걸음을 재촉하며 멈추라고 소리쳤지요."]], [["‘“Stay!”", "'멈추시오!'"], ["said the monk, raising his right hand in the air, and directing an angry glance by turns at Alice and the eldest sister.", "수도사가 오른손을 허공에 치켜들고, 앨리스와 맏이를 번갈아 노려보며 말했소. '멈추고, 내 말을 들으시오."], ["“Stay, and hear from me what these recollections are, which you would cherish above eternity, and awaken--if in mercy they slumbered--by means of idle toys.", "그대들이 영원보다 더 소중히 여기겠다는, 그래서 자비롭게 잠들어 있는데도 한낱 부질없는 장난감으로 깨우려는 그 기억들이 무엇인지 말이오."], ["The memory of earthly things is charged, in after life, with bitter disappointment, affliction, death; with dreary change and wasting sorrow.", "세속의 것에 대한 기억은 훗날 쓰라린 환멸과 고통과 죽음으로, 황량한 변화와 사람을 갉아먹는 슬픔으로 무겁게 채워지지요. 언젠가 그날이 올 거요."], ["The time will one day come, when a glance at those unmeaning baubles will tear open deep wounds in the hearts of some among you, and strike to your inmost souls.", "그 뜻 없는 노리개를 한 번 들여다보는 것만으로 그대들 가운데 누군가의 가슴에 깊은 상처가 헤집어지고, 그 영혼 깊은 곳까지 사무치는 날이 말이오."], ["When that hour arrives--and, mark me, come it will--turn from the world to which you clung, to the refuge which you spurned.", "그때가 오거든, 그리고 명심하시오, 반드시 올 테니, 그토록 매달리던 세상에서 등을 돌려, 그대들이 비웃었던 이 안식처로 오시오."], ["Find me the cell which shall be colder than the fire of mortals grows, when dimmed by calamity and trial, and there weep for the dreams of youth.", "재앙과 시련에 그 빛이 바랠 때 인간의 불보다 차디찬 골방을 내게 찾아오시오. 그리고 거기서 젊은 날의 꿈을 두고 우시오."], ["These things are Heaven’s will, not mine,”", "이것은 하늘의 뜻이지 내 뜻이 아니오.'"], ["said the friar, subduing his voice as he looked round upon the shrinking girls.", "수도사가 움츠러든 처녀들을 둘러보며 목소리를 누그러뜨리고 말했소."], ["“The Virgin’s blessing be upon you, daughters!”", "'성모의 축복이 그대들에게 있기를, 따님들이여!'"]], [["‘With these words he disappeared through the postern; and the sisters hastening into the house were seen no more that day.", "이 말과 함께 그는 쪽문으로 사라졌소. 그리고 자매들은 서둘러 집 안으로 들어갔고, 그날은 더 이상 모습을 보이지 않았지요."]], [["‘But nature will smile though priests may frown, and next day the sun shone brightly, and on the next, and the next again.", "하지만 사제가 눈살을 찌푸린들 자연은 웃는 법이오. 이튿날 해가 환하게 비쳤고, 그다음 날도, 또 그다음 날도 그러했지요."], ["And in the morning’s glare, and the evening’s soft repose, the five sisters still walked, or worked, or beguiled the time by cheerful conversation, in their quiet orchard.", "그리고 아침의 눈부신 빛 속에서, 또 저녁의 부드러운 쉼 속에서, 다섯 자매는 여전히 그들의 조용한 과수원을 거닐고, 일하고, 즐거운 이야기로 시간을 보냈소."]], [["‘Time passed away as a tale that is told; faster indeed than many tales that are told, of which number I fear this may be one.", "세월은 한 편의 이야기처럼 흘러갔소. 사실 들려주는 여느 이야기보다도 빠르게 흘렀지요."], ["The house of the five sisters stood where it did, and the same trees cast their pleasant shade upon the orchard grass.", "어쩌면 이 이야기도 그런 부류일까 걱정이오만. 다섯 자매의 집은 그대로 그 자리에 서 있었고, 같은 나무들이 과수원 풀밭에 정겨운 그늘을 드리웠소."], ["The sisters too were there, and lovely as at first, but a change had come over their dwelling.", "자매들도 그곳에 있었고 처음처럼 사랑스러웠지만, 그들의 보금자리에는 변화가 찾아왔소."], ["Sometimes, there was the clash of armour, and the gleaming of the moon on caps of steel; and, at others, jaded coursers were spurred up to the gate, and a female form glided hurriedly forth, as if eager to demand tidings of the weary messenger.", "때로는 갑옷 부딪치는 소리가, 강철 투구 위에 어른거리는 달빛이 보였소. 또 때로는 지친 군마가 박차에 채여 대문까지 내달려 오면, 한 여인의 모습이 그 고단한 전령에게 소식을 다그쳐 묻고 싶은 양 서둘러 미끄러져 나왔지요."], ["A goodly train of knights and ladies lodged one night within the abbey walls, and next day rode away, with two of the fair sisters among them.", "어느 날 밤에는 기사와 귀부인의 멋진 행렬이 수도원 담장 안에 묵었다가, 이튿날 고운 자매 가운데 둘을 데리고 떠났소."], ["Then, horsemen began to come less frequently, and seemed to bring bad tidings when they did, and at length they ceased to come at all, and footsore peasants slunk to the gate after sunset, and did their errand there, by stealth.", "그 뒤로 말 탄 이들의 발길이 뜸해지기 시작했고, 와도 나쁜 소식을 가져오는 듯했으며, 마침내 아예 끊겨 버렸소. 그러더니 발이 부르튼 농부들이 해 진 뒤 대문으로 슬그머니 와서 몰래 볼일을 보고 갔지요."], ["Once, a vassal was dispatched in haste to the abbey at dead of night, and when morning came, there were sounds of woe and wailing in the sisters’ house; and after this, a mournful silence fell upon it, and knight or lady, horse or armour, was seen about it no more.", "한번은 한밤중에 하인 하나가 황급히 수도원으로 보내졌고, 날이 밝자 자매들의 집에서 비탄과 통곡 소리가 들려왔소. 그 뒤로는 슬픈 침묵이 그 집에 내려앉았고, 기사도 귀부인도, 말도 갑옷도 다시는 그 둘레에 보이지 않았지요."]], [["‘There was a sullen darkness in the sky, and the sun had gone angrily down, tinting the dull clouds with the last traces of his wrath, when the same black monk walked slowly on, with folded arms, within a stone’s-throw of the abbey.", "하늘에 음울한 어둠이 깔리고, 해가 성난 듯 저물며 흐린 구름을 그 마지막 노여움의 빛으로 물들이고 있을 때, 그 똑같은 검은 옷 수도사가 팔짱을 낀 채 수도원에서 돌 던지면 닿을 거리를 천천히 거닐고 있었소. 나무와 덤불에는 시들음이 덮쳤소."], ["A blight had fallen on the trees and shrubs; and the wind, at length beginning to break the unnatural stillness that had prevailed all day, sighed heavily from time to time, as though foretelling in grief the ravages of the coming storm.", "그리고 바람이 마침내 온종일 감돌던 부자연스러운 고요를 깨기 시작하더니, 이따금 무겁게 한숨지었지요. 다가올 폭풍의 황폐를 슬프게 예고하는 듯이 말이오."], ["The bat skimmed in fantastic flights through the heavy air, and the ground was alive with crawling things, whose instinct brought them forth to swell and fatten in the rain.", "박쥐가 그 무거운 공기를 가르며 괴이하게 날았고, 땅바닥은 기어 다니는 것들로 우글거렸소. 빗속에서 불어나고 살찌라는 본능이 그것들을 밖으로 불러낸 거였지요."]], [["‘No longer were the friar’s eyes directed to the earth; they were cast abroad, and roamed from point to point, as if the gloom and desolation of the scene found a quick response in his own bosom.", "이제 수도사의 눈은 땅을 향해 있지 않았소. 그 눈은 멀리 던져져, 이곳저곳을 헤맸지요."], ["Again he paused near the sisters’ house, and again he entered by the postern.", "그 장면의 음울함과 황량함이 제 가슴속에서 곧장 메아리를 찾기라도 한 듯이 말이오. 그는 다시 자매들의 집 가까이서 걸음을 멈췄고, 다시 쪽문으로 들어섰소."]], [["‘But not again did his ear encounter the sound of laughter, or his eyes rest upon the beautiful figures of the five sisters.", "하지만 다시는 그의 귀에 웃음소리가 닿지 않았고, 그의 눈이 다섯 자매의 아름다운 모습에 머무는 일도 없었소."], ["All was silent and deserted.", "모든 것이 고요하고 버려져 있었지요."], ["The boughs of the trees were bent and broken, and the grass had grown long and rank.", "나뭇가지들은 휘고 부러졌으며, 풀은 길고 무성하게 자라 있었소."], ["No light feet had pressed it for many, many a day.", "오랫동안, 아주 오랫동안 가벼운 발길이 그것을 밟은 적이 없었던 거요."]], [["‘With the indifference or abstraction of one well accustomed to the change, the monk glided into the house, and entered a low, dark room.", "그 변화에 어지간히 익숙해진 사람다운 무심함, 아니 골똘함으로, 수도사는 집 안으로 미끄러져 들어가 낮고 어두운 방으로 들어섰소."], ["Four sisters sat there.", "거기에 네 자매가 앉아 있었지요."], ["Their black garments made their pale faces whiter still, and time and sorrow had worked deep ravages.", "검은 옷이 그들의 창백한 얼굴을 더욱 희게 만들었고, 세월과 슬픔이 깊은 자국을 새겨 놓았소."], ["They were stately yet; but the flush and pride of beauty were gone.", "그들은 여전히 기품 있었지만, 미모의 발그레함과 자랑스러움은 사라지고 없었지요."]], [["‘And Alice--where was she?", "그럼 앨리스는, 어디 있었느냐고?"], ["In Heaven.", "하늘나라에."]], [["‘The monk--even the monk--could bear with some grief here; for it was long since these sisters had met, and there were furrows in their blanched faces which years could never plough.", "수도사는, 그 수도사조차, 이곳의 슬픔만큼은 차마 견딜 수 없었소. 이 자매들이 마지막으로 모인 지 오래되었고, 그 핏기 없는 얼굴에는 세월로도 결코 갈아 낼 수 없는 골이 패어 있었으니까."], ["He took his seat in silence, and motioned them to continue their speech.", "그는 말없이 자리에 앉아, 그들에게 하던 이야기를 계속하라고 손짓했소."]], [["‘“They are here, sisters,”", "'여기 있어요, 동생들.'"], ["said the elder lady in a trembling voice.", "맏이가 떨리는 목소리로 말했소."], ["“I have never borne to look upon them since, and now I blame myself for my weakness.", "'그 뒤로 차마 이걸 들여다본 적이 없었는데, 이제 내 나약함이 부끄럽구나."], ["What is there in her memory that we should dread?", "그 애를 떠올리는 게 뭐가 그리 두렵겠니?"], ["To call up our old days shall be a solemn pleasure yet.”", "우리 옛 시절을 다시 불러내는 건 그래도 엄숙한 기쁨이 될 거야.'"]], [["‘She glanced at the monk as she spoke, and, opening a cabinet, brought forth the five frames of work, completed long before.", "그는 그렇게 말하며 수도사를 흘끗 보고는, 장을 열어 오래전에 완성된 다섯 개의 자수틀을 꺼냈소. 걸음은 굳건했지만, 마지막 것을 꺼낼 때 그의 손은 떨렸지요."], ["Her step was firm, but her hand trembled as she produced the last one; and, when the feelings of the other sisters gushed forth at sight of it, her pent-up tears made way, and she sobbed “God bless her!”", "그리고 그것을 본 다른 자매들의 감정이 북받쳐 터져 나오자, 그는 눌러 두었던 눈물이 길을 트며 흐느꼈소. '하느님, 그 애를 축복하소서!'"]], [["‘The monk rose and advanced towards them.", "수도사가 일어나 그들 쪽으로 다가갔소."], ["“It was almost the last thing she touched in health,”", "'이건 그 애가 건강했을 때 거의 마지막으로 손댄 것이지요.'"], ["he said in a low voice.", "그가 나직한 목소리로 말했소."]], [["‘“It was,”", "'그래요.'"], ["cried the elder lady, weeping bitterly.", "맏이가 비통하게 울며 외쳤소."]], [["‘The monk turned to the second sister.", "수도사는 둘째 자매를 돌아보았소."]], [["‘“The gallant youth who looked into thine eyes, and hung upon thy very breath when first he saw thee intent upon this pastime, lies buried on a plain whereof the turf is red with blood.", "'그대가 이 일거리에 골몰한 모습을 처음 보고는, 그대 눈을 들여다보며 그대 숨결 하나에 매달리던 그 늠름한 젊은이는, 잔디가 피로 붉게 물든 벌판에 묻혀 있소."], ["Rusty fragments of armour, once brightly burnished, lie rotting on the ground, and are as little distinguishable for his, as are the bones that crumble in the mould!”", "한때 환히 빛나도록 닦였던 갑옷 조각들은 녹슨 채 땅바닥에 썩어 가고, 흙 속에서 부스러지는 뼈만큼이나 그것이 그의 것인지조차 알아볼 수 없소!'"]], [["‘The lady groaned, and wrung her hands.", "둘째 자매는 신음하며 두 손을 마주 비틀었소."]], [["‘“The policy of courts,”", "'궁정의 술책이,'"], ["he continued, turning to the two other sisters, “drew ye from your peaceful home to scenes of revelry and splendour.", "그가 나머지 두 자매를 돌아보며 이었소. '그대들을 평화로운 보금자리에서 끌어내 흥청거림과 화려함의 무대로 데려갔지요."], ["The same policy, and the restless ambition of--proud and fiery men, have sent ye back, widowed maidens, and humbled outcasts.", "그 똑같은 술책과, 거만하고 불같은 사내들의 그칠 줄 모르는 야망이, 과부가 된 처녀로, 또 천대받는 쫓겨난 자로 그대들을 돌려보냈소."], ["Do I speak truly?”", "내 말이 맞소?'"]], [["‘The sobs of the two sisters were their only reply.", "두 자매의 흐느낌만이 그 대답이었소."]], [["‘“There is little need,”", "'그 부질없는 짓에 시간을 허비할 까닭이 거의 없소.'"], ["said the monk, with a meaning look, “to fritter away the time in gewgaws which shall raise up the pale ghosts of hopes of early years.", "수도사가 뜻 있는 눈빛으로 말했소. '젊은 날 희망의 창백한 망령들이나 불러일으킬 그런 짓 말이오."], ["Bury them, heap penance and mortification on their heads, keep them down, and let the convent be their grave!”", "그것들을 묻으시오. 그 머리 위에 참회와 고행을 쌓아 누르고, 수도원을 그것들의 무덤으로 삼으시오!'"]], [["‘The sisters asked for three days to deliberate; and felt, that night, as though the veil were indeed the fitting shroud for their dead joys.", "자매들은 사흘만 생각할 말미를 달라고 했소. 그리고 그날 밤, 면사포야말로 죽은 기쁨을 덮을 알맞은 수의처럼 느껴졌지요."], ["But, morning came again, and though the boughs of the orchard trees drooped and ran wild upon the ground, it was the same orchard still.", "하지만 아침이 다시 왔소. 그리고 과수원 나뭇가지들이 축 늘어져 땅 위로 제멋대로 뻗어 있었어도, 그곳은 여전히 그 과수원이었소."], ["The grass was coarse and high, but there was yet the spot on which they had so often sat together, when change and sorrow were but names.", "풀은 거칠고 높이 자랐지만, 변화도 슬픔도 한낱 이름에 지나지 않던 시절 그토록 자주 함께 앉았던 그 자리가 아직 거기 있었지요."], ["There was every walk and nook which Alice had made glad; and in the minster nave was one flat stone beneath which she slept in peace.", "앨리스가 즐겁게 거닐던 모든 산책로와 후미진 구석이 거기 있었고, 대성당 본당 바닥에는 그가 평화로이 잠든 납작한 돌 하나가 있었소."]], [["‘And could they, remembering how her young heart had sickened at the thought of cloistered walls, look upon her grave, in garbs which would chill the very ashes within it?", "그 어린 마음이 수도원 담장이라는 생각만으로 얼마나 진저리쳤는지 기억하면서, 그 무덤 속 재마저 얼어붙게 할 옷차림으로 그 무덤을 내려다볼 수 있었겠소? 무릎 꿇어 기도하며, 온 하늘이 귀 기울여 들으려 할 때, 한 천사의 얼굴에 어두운 슬픔의 그늘을 드리울 수 있었겠소?"], ["Could they bow down in prayer, and when all Heaven turned to hear them, bring the dark shade of sadness on one angel’s face? No.", "아니지요."]], [["‘They sent abroad, to artists of great celebrity in those times, and having obtained the church’s sanction to their work of piety, caused to be executed, in five large compartments of richly stained glass, a faithful copy of their old embroidery work.", "그들은 멀리, 그 시절 이름난 장인들에게 사람을 보냈소. 그리고 이 경건한 일에 교회의 허락을 얻은 뒤, 화려하게 물들인 스테인드글라스 다섯 칸에 그들의 옛 자수 작품을 충실하게 옮겨 새기게 했지요."], ["These were fitted into a large window until that time bare of ornament; and when the sun shone brightly, as she had so well loved to see it, the familiar patterns were reflected in their original colours, and throwing a stream of brilliant light upon the pavement, fell warmly on the name of Alice.", "이것들은 그때까지 아무 장식도 없던 큼직한 창에 끼워 넣어졌소. 그리고 앨리스가 그토록 보기 좋아하던 그 햇살이 환히 비치면, 낯익은 무늬들이 본래의 색깔로 비쳐 들었고, 눈부신 빛줄기를 바닥에 던지며 앨리스라는 이름 위로 따스하게 내려앉았소."]], [["‘For many hours in every day, the sisters paced slowly up and down the nave, or knelt by the side of the flat broad stone.", "날마다 오랜 시간 동안 자매들은 본당을 천천히 오르내리거나, 그 납작하고 너른 돌 곁에 무릎 꿇었소."], ["Only three were seen in the customary place, after many years; then but two, and, for a long time afterwards, but one solitary female bent with age.", "여러 해가 지나자 그 늘 앉던 자리에 셋만 보였고, 그다음엔 둘뿐이었으며, 그 뒤로 오랫동안은 나이에 등 굽은 여인 단 하나뿐이었지요."], ["At length she came no more, and the stone bore five plain Christian names.", "마침내 그마저 더는 오지 않았고, 돌에는 다섯 개의 소박한 세례명이 새겨졌소."]], [["‘That stone has worn away and been replaced by others, and many generations have come and gone since then.", "그 돌은 닳아 없어져 다른 돌들로 바뀌었고, 그 뒤로 여러 세대가 오고 갔소. 세월이 색을 부드럽게 바래게 했지만, 똑같은 빛줄기가 지금도 그 잊힌 무덤 위로 떨어지지요."], ["Time has softened down the colours, but the same stream of light still falls upon the forgotten tomb, of which no trace remains; and, to this day, the stranger is shown in York Cathedral, an old window called the Five Sisters.’", "이제 그 무덤의 흔적은 남아 있지 않지만 말이오. 그리고 오늘날까지도 요크 대성당에 오면, 사람들은 다섯 자매라 불리는 옛 창문 하나를 낯선 이에게 보여 준다오.\""]], [["‘That’s a melancholy tale,’ said the merry-faced gentleman, emptying his glass.", "\"그것참 우울한 이야기로군요.\" 쾌활한 얼굴의 신사가 잔을 비우며 말했다."]], [["‘It is a tale of life, and life is made up of such sorrows,’ returned the other, courteously, but in a grave and sad tone of voice.", "\"삶에 관한 이야기지요. 그리고 삶이란 그런 슬픔으로 이루어진 것이고요.\" 상대가 정중하게, 그러나 무겁고 슬픈 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["‘There are shades in all good pictures, but there are lights too, if we choose to contemplate them,’ said the gentleman with the merry face.", "\"좋은 그림에는 다 그늘이 있지요. 하지만 우리가 마음먹고 들여다보면 빛도 있어요.\""], ["‘The youngest sister in your tale was always light-hearted.’", "쾌활한 얼굴의 신사가 말했다. \"당신 이야기 속 막내는 늘 마음이 가벼웠잖소.\""]], [["‘And died early,’ said the other, gently.", "\"그리고 일찍 죽었지요.\" 상대가 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["‘She would have died earlier, perhaps, had she been less happy,’ said the first speaker, with much feeling.", "\"덜 행복했더라면 어쩌면 더 일찍 죽었을지도 모르오.\" 처음 말한 사람이 한껏 감정을 실어 말했다."], ["‘Do you think the sisters who loved her so well, would have grieved the less if her life had been one of gloom and sadness?", "\"그를 그토록 사랑했던 자매들이, 만약 그의 삶이 음울하고 슬픈 것이었다면 덜 슬퍼했을 것 같소?"], ["If anything could soothe the first sharp pain of a heavy loss, it would be--with me--the reflection, that those I mourned, by being innocently happy here, and loving all about them, had prepared themselves for a purer and happier world.", "무거운 상실의 그 첫 날카로운 아픔을 달래 줄 게 있다면, 나로서는 이런 생각일 거요. 내가 애도하는 이들이 이 세상에서 죄 없이 행복하게 지내고 둘레의 모든 것을 사랑함으로써, 더 순결하고 더 행복한 세상을 맞을 채비를 해 두었다는 생각 말이오."], ["The sun does not shine upon this fair earth to meet frowning eyes, depend upon it.’", "햇살이 이 아름다운 땅을 비추는 건 찌푸린 얼굴을 만나려는 게 아니오. 그건 틀림없소.\""]], [["‘I believe you are right,’ said the gentleman who had told the story.", "\"당신 말이 옳은 것 같군요.\" 이야기를 들려준 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Believe!’ retorted the other, ‘can anybody doubt it?", "\"옳은 것 같다니!\" 상대가 받아쳤다."], ["Take any subject of sorrowful regret, and see with how much pleasure it is associated.", "\"이걸 누가 의심하겠소? 슬픈 회한의 소재를 아무거나 하나 떠올려 보시오."], ["The recollection of past pleasure may become pain--’", "거기에 얼마나 많은 즐거움이 함께 얽혀 있는지 보일 거요. 지난 기쁨의 추억이 아픔이 될 수도 있지요.\""]], [["‘It does,’ interposed the other.", "\"그렇지요.\" 상대가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Well; it does.", "\"그래요, 그렇소. 되돌릴 수 없는 행복을 떠올리는 건 아픈 일이오."], ["To remember happiness which cannot be restored, is pain, but of a softened kind.", "하지만 한결 누그러진 아픔이지요. 우리의 추억은 안타깝게도 한탄스러운 것들과, 또 쓰라리게 뉘우치는 수많은 행동과 뒤섞여 있소."], ["Our recollections are unfortunately mingled with much that we deplore, and with many actions which we bitterly repent; still in the most chequered life I firmly think there are so many little rays of sunshine to look back upon, that I do not believe any mortal (unless he had put himself without the pale of hope) would deliberately drain a goblet of the waters of Lethe, if he had it in his power.’", "그래도 아무리 얼룩진 삶이라도, 돌이켜볼 만한 자그마한 햇살 줄기가 그토록 많이 있다고 나는 굳게 믿소. 그러니 어떤 인간도, 제 스스로 희망의 울타리 밖으로 나선 자가 아니라면, 설령 그럴 힘이 있다 한들 망각의 강물을 일부러 들이켜지는 않을 거라 믿소.\""]], [["‘Possibly you are correct in that belief,’ said the grey-haired gentleman after a short reflection.", "\"그 믿음이 어쩌면 옳을지도 모르겠군요.\" 희끗한 머리의 신사가 잠시 생각하더니 말했다."], ["‘I am inclined to think you are.’", "\"그런 것 같다는 쪽으로 마음이 기우는군요.\""]], [["‘Why, then,’ replied the other, ‘the good in this state of existence preponderates over the bad, let miscalled philosophers tell us what they will.", "\"그렇다면,\" 상대가 받았다."], ["If our affections be tried, our affections are our consolation and comfort; and memory, however sad, is the best and purest link between this world and a better.", "\"이른바 철학자라는 자들이 뭐라 떠들든, 이 세상살이에서는 좋은 것이 나쁜 것을 앞지르는 법이오. 우리의 정이 시련을 겪더라도, 그 정이야말로 우리의 위안이요 안식이지요."], ["But come!", "그리고 기억은 아무리 슬프다 한들 이 세상과 더 나은 세상을 잇는 가장 좋고 순수한 끈이오."], ["I’ll tell you a story of another kind.’", "자, 그럼! 내가 또 다른 종류의 이야기를 하나 들려드리리다.\""]], [["After a very brief silence, the merry-faced gentleman sent round the punch, and glancing slyly at the fastidious lady, who seemed desperately apprehensive that he was going to relate something improper, began", "아주 잠깐 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 쾌활한 얼굴의 신사는 펀치를 한 차례 돌리고는, 무언가 점잖지 못한 이야기를 꺼낼까 봐 잔뜩 겁먹은 듯한 까다로운 부인을 곁눈질로 슬쩍 보며 이야기를 시작했다. 그로그즈위그의 남작"]], [["# THE BARON OF GROGZWIG", "# THE BARON OF GROGZWIG"]], [["‘The Baron Von Koeldwethout, of Grogzwig in Germany, was as likely a young baron as you would wish to see. I needn’t say that he lived in a castle, because that’s of course; neither need I say that he lived in an old castle; for what German baron ever lived in a new one? There were many strange circumstances connected with this venerable building, among which, not the least startling and mysterious were, that when the wind blew, it rumbled in the chimneys, or even howled among the trees in the neighbouring forest; and that when the moon shone, she found her way through certain small loopholes in the wall, and actually made some parts of the wide halls and galleries quite light, while she left others in gloomy shadow. I believe that one of the baron’s ancestors, being short of money, had inserted a dagger in a gentleman who called one night to ask his way, and it WAS supposed that these miraculous occurrences took place in consequence. And yet I hardly know how that could have been, either, because the baron’s ancestor, who was an amiable man, felt very sorry afterwards for having been so rash, and laying violent hands upon a quantity of stone and timber which belonged to a weaker baron, built a chapel as an apology, and so took a receipt from Heaven, in full of all demands.", "\"독일 그로그즈위그의 폰 쾰드웨트하우트 남작은 누구나 보고 싶어 할 만한 멋진 젊은 남작이었소. 그가 성에 살았다는 건 말할 것도 없지요. 당연한 일이니까. 게다가 낡은 성에 살았다는 것도 굳이 말할 필요가 없소. 새 성에 사는 독일 남작이 어디 있겠소? 이 유서 깊은 건물에는 별난 사정이 한둘이 아니었는데, 그중에서도 가장 놀랍고 알 수 없는 것은 이런 거였소. 바람이 불면 그 소리가 굴뚝에서 우르릉거리거나, 심지어 이웃한 숲의 나무들 사이에서 울부짖었지요. 또 달이 비치면 그 빛이 담벼락의 작은 틈새들을 뚫고 들어와, 너른 회랑과 복도의 어떤 부분은 환하게 밝히면서 다른 부분은 음침한 그림자 속에 남겨 두었소. 내가 알기로는, 남작의 어느 조상이 돈에 쪼들렸던 차에, 어느 날 밤 길을 묻겠다고 찾아온 신사에게 단검을 찔러 넣었다는데, 그 때문에 이런 기이한 일들이 벌어진 거라고들 했소. 하지만 그게 정말 그랬을까 싶기도 한 게, 그 남작의 조상은 마음씨 고운 사람이라 나중에는 그렇게 경솔하게 군 걸 몹시 후회했거든. 그래서 자기보다 약한 남작 소유의 돌과 목재를 잔뜩 강제로 빼앗아 사죄의 뜻으로 예배당을 지었고, 그렇게 하늘로부터 모든 빚을 청산했다는 영수증을 받아 낸 거지요."]], [["‘Talking of the baron’s ancestor puts me in mind of the baron’s great claims to respect, on the score of his pedigree. I am afraid to say, I am sure, how many ancestors the baron had; but I know that he had a great many more than any other man of his time; and I only wish that he had lived in these latter days, that he might have had more. It is a very hard thing upon the great men of past centuries, that they should have come into the world so soon, because a man who was born three or four hundred years ago, cannot reasonably be expected to have had as many relations before him, as a man who is born now. The last man, whoever he is--and he may be a cobbler or some low vulgar dog for aught we know--will have a longer pedigree than the greatest nobleman now alive; and I contend that this is not fair.", "남작의 조상 이야기를 하다 보니, 남작이 가문 덕에 마땅히 누리던 그 대단한 존경거리가 떠오르는구려. 남작에게 조상이 정확히 몇 명이나 있었는지는 차마 말 못 하겠소. 하지만 그가 당대의 누구보다도 훨씬 많은 조상을 두었다는 것만은 알지요. 다만 그가 요즘 같은 시대에 살았더라면 조상을 더 많이 둘 수 있었을 텐데 싶어 아쉬울 따름이오. 지난 세기의 위인들에게는 참 딱한 노릇이오. 너무 일찍 세상에 나온 셈이니까. 삼사백 년 전에 태어난 사람이, 지금 태어난 사람만큼 많은 선대 친족을 거느렸으리라 기대하는 건 무리잖소. 누구든 가장 마지막에 태어난 사람은, 그가 구두장이든 무슨 천한 개잡놈이든 간에, 지금 살아 있는 그 어떤 위대한 귀족보다도 긴 족보를 가지게 될 거요. 나는 이게 공평하지 않다고 보오."]], [["‘Well, but the Baron Von Koeldwethout of Grogzwig!", "자, 어쨌든 그로그즈위그의 폰 쾰드웨트하우트 남작 말이오!"], ["He was a fine swarthy fellow, with dark hair and large moustachios, who rode a-hunting in clothes of Lincoln green, with russet boots on his feet, and a bugle slung over his shoulder like the guard of a long stage.", "그는 거무스름하고 잘생긴 사내로, 검은 머리에 커다란 콧수염을 길렀고, 사냥할 때면 링컨 그린 빛깔 옷을 입고 적갈색 장화를 신은 채, 장거리 마차 차장처럼 어깨에 뿔나팔을 둘러메고 말을 달렸소. 그가 이 뿔나팔을 불면, 좀 더 거친 링컨 그린 옷에 밑창이 좀 더 두꺼운 적갈색 장화를 신은, 신분이 낮은 신사 스물넷이 대뜸 뛰쳐나왔지요."], ["When he blew this bugle, four-and-twenty other gentlemen of inferior rank, in Lincoln green a little coarser, and russet boots with a little thicker soles, turned out directly: and away galloped the whole train, with spears in their hands like lacquered area railings, to hunt down the boars, or perhaps encounter a bear: in which latter case the baron killed him first, and greased his whiskers with him afterwards.", "그러면 일행 전체가, 옻칠한 마당 난간 같은 창을 손에 들고 멧돼지를 잡으러, 어쩌면 곰과 맞붙으러 말을 달려 나갔소. 곰을 만났을 경우엔, 남작이 먼저 그놈을 죽인 다음 그 기름으로 제 콧수염을 발랐지요."]], [["‘This was a merry life for the Baron of Grogzwig, and a merrier still for the baron’s retainers, who drank Rhine wine every night till they fell under the table, and then had the bottles on the floor, and called for pipes.", "그로그즈위그 남작에게는 즐거운 삶이었고, 남작의 부하들에게는 한층 더 즐거운 삶이었소. 그들은 밤마다 라인 포도주를 마시다가 식탁 밑으로 굴러떨어졌고, 그러면 바닥에 술병을 갖다 놓고 담배를 청했지요."], ["Never were such jolly, roystering, rollicking, merry-making blades, as the jovial crew of Grogzwig.", "그로그즈위그의 그 유쾌한 패거리만큼 흥겹고 떠들썩하고 신명 나게 즐기는 한량들은 어디에도 없었소."]], [["‘But the pleasures of the table, or the pleasures of under the table, require a little variety; especially when the same five-and-twenty people sit daily down to the same board, to discuss the same subjects, and tell the same stories.", "하지만 식탁 위의 쾌락이든 식탁 밑의 쾌락이든, 좀 변화가 있어야 하는 법이오. 특히 똑같은 스물다섯 사람이 날마다 똑같은 식탁에 둘러앉아, 똑같은 화제를 떠들고 똑같은 이야기를 늘어놓을 때는 말이오."], ["The baron grew weary, and wanted excitement.", "남작은 점점 따분해졌고, 자극이 필요했소."], ["He took to quarrelling with his gentlemen, and tried kicking two or three of them every day after dinner.", "그는 부하 신사들과 시비를 거는 데에 맛을 들였고, 저녁을 먹고 나면 날마다 두세 명씩 걷어차 보려 했지요."], ["This was a pleasant change at first; but it became monotonous after a week or so, and the baron felt quite out of sorts, and cast about, in despair, for some new amusement.", "처음에는 이게 반가운 변화였소. 하지만 한 주쯤 지나니 지겨워졌고, 남작은 영 기분이 언짢아져서 새로운 놀거리를 찾아 절망 속에 두리번거렸소."]], [["‘One night, after a day’s sport in which he had outdone Nimrod or Gillingwater, and slaughtered “another fine bear,” and brought him home in triumph, the Baron Von Koeldwethout sat moodily at the head of his table, eyeing the smoky roof of the hall with a discontented aspect.", "어느 날 밤, 종일 사냥으로 님로드나 길링워터보다도 한 수 위인 솜씨를 뽐내며 '또 한 마리 멋진 곰'을 때려잡아 의기양양하게 집으로 끌고 온 폰 쾰드웨트하우트 남작은, 식탁 상석에 침울하게 앉아 못마땅한 얼굴로 회랑의 그을린 천장을 노려보고 있었소."], ["He swallowed huge bumpers of wine, but the more he swallowed, the more he frowned.", "그는 큼직한 잔으로 포도주를 들이켰지만, 마시면 마실수록 더 인상을 찌푸렸지요."], ["The gentlemen who had been honoured with the dangerous distinction of sitting on his right and left, imitated him to a miracle in the drinking, and frowned at each other.", "그의 오른쪽과 왼쪽에 앉는 그 위태로운 영예를 누리던 신사들은 술 마시는 데서는 신기할 만큼 그를 똑 닮게 따라 하며, 서로를 향해 인상을 찌푸렸소."]], [["‘“I will!”", "'내 그러리라!'"], ["cried the baron suddenly, smiting the table with his right hand, and twirling his moustache with his left.", "남작이 갑자기 오른손으로 식탁을 내리치고 왼손으로 콧수염을 비틀며 외쳤소."], ["“Fill to the Lady of Grogzwig!”", "'그로그즈위그의 안주인을 위해 잔을 채워라!'"]], [["‘The four-and-twenty Lincoln greens turned pale, with the exception of their four-and-twenty noses, which were unchangeable.", "스물넷의 링컨 그린들은 얼굴이 새파래졌소. 다만 스물넷의 코만은 예외였는데, 그 코들은 절대 변하는 법이 없었지요."]], [["‘“I said to the Lady of Grogzwig,”", "'내 그로그즈위그의 안주인을 위해서라고 했다.'"], ["repeated the baron, looking round the board.", "남작이 식탁을 빙 둘러보며 거듭 말했소."]], [["‘“To the Lady of Grogzwig!”", "'그로그즈위그의 안주인을 위하여!'"], ["shouted the Lincoln greens; and down their four-and-twenty throats went four-and-twenty imperial pints of such rare old hock, that they smacked their eight-and-forty lips, and winked again.", "링컨 그린들이 외쳤소. 그러고는 스물넷의 목구멍으로 진귀한 묵은 백포도주 스물넷 임페리얼 파인트가 넘어갔고, 그들은 마흔여덟 개의 입술을 쩝쩝거리며 다시 눈을 끔벅였소."]], [["‘“The fair daughter of the Baron Von Swillenhausen,”", "'폰 스윌렌하우젠 남작의 고운 따님 말이다.'"], ["said Koeldwethout, condescending to explain.", "쾰드웨트하우트가 황송하게도 설명을 곁들였소."], ["“We will demand her in marriage of her father, ere the sun goes down tomorrow.", "'내일 해가 지기 전에 그 아비에게 청혼하리라."], ["If he refuse our suit, we will cut off his nose.”", "만약 우리 청혼을 거절하거든, 그 코를 베어 버리겠다.'"]], [["‘A hoarse murmur arose from the company; every man touched, first the hilt of his sword, and then the tip of his nose, with appalling significance.", "좌중에서 쉰 웅성거림이 일었소. 사내들은 너 나 할 것 없이 먼저 칼자루에, 다음엔 제 코끝에 손을 대며 섬뜩한 뜻을 내비쳤지요."]], [["‘What a pleasant thing filial piety is to contemplate!", "자식의 효심이란 참 보기 좋은 것이오!"], ["If the daughter of the Baron Von Swillenhausen had pleaded a preoccupied heart, or fallen at her father’s feet and corned them in salt tears, or only fainted away, and complimented the old gentleman in frantic ejaculations, the odds are a hundred to one but Swillenhausen Castle would have been turned out at window, or rather the baron turned out at window, and the castle demolished.", "만약 폰 스윌렌하우젠 남작의 따님이 이미 마음 둔 데가 있다고 하소연했거나, 아버지 발치에 엎드려 짠 눈물로 그 발을 적셨거나, 아니면 그저 기절해 자빠지며 미친 듯한 비명으로 노신사를 떠받들었더라면, 백에 아흔아홉은 스윌렌하우젠 성이 창밖으로 내던져졌을 거요. 아니, 차라리 남작이 창밖으로 내던져지고 성이 헐려 버렸겠지요."], ["The damsel held her peace, however, when an early messenger bore the request of Von Koeldwethout next morning, and modestly retired to her chamber, from the casement of which she watched the coming of the suitor and his retinue.", "하지만 그 처녀는, 이튿날 아침 발 빠른 전령이 폰 쾰드웨트하우트의 청혼을 전했을 때 잠자코 있었소. 그러고는 얌전히 제 방으로 물러나, 그 창문으로 구혼자와 그 일행이 다가오는 걸 지켜보았지요."], ["She was no sooner assured that the horseman with the large moustachios was her proffered husband, than she hastened to her father’s presence, and expressed her readiness to sacrifice herself to secure his peace.", "그 커다란 콧수염을 기른 말 탄 사내가 자기에게 청혼한 남편감이라는 걸 확인하자마자, 처녀는 아버지 앞으로 한달음에 달려가, 아버지의 평안을 지키기 위해 자기를 기꺼이 희생하겠다고 했소."], ["The venerable baron caught his child to his arms, and shed a wink of joy.", "그러자 그 점잖은 남작은 딸을 와락 품에 안고, 기쁨의 눈물을 한 방울 찔끔 흘렸지요."]], [["‘There was great feasting at the castle, that day.", "그날 성에서는 큰 잔치가 벌어졌소. 폰 쾰드웨트하우트의 스물넷 링컨 그린은 폰 스윌렌하우젠의 열둘 링컨 그린과 영원한 우정을 맹세하며 잔을 나누었고, 노남작에게 '온통 새파래질 때까지'"], ["The four-and-twenty Lincoln greens of Von Koeldwethout exchanged vows of eternal friendship with twelve Lincoln greens of Von Swillenhausen, and promised the old baron that they would drink his wine “Till all was blue”--meaning probably until their whole countenances had acquired the same tint as their noses.", "그의 포도주를 마시겠노라 약속했소. 아마 얼굴 전체가 코와 똑같은 빛깔이 될 때까지라는 뜻이었겠지요."], ["Everybody slapped everybody else’s back, when the time for parting came; and the Baron Von Koeldwethout and his followers rode gaily home.", "헤어질 때가 되자 모두가 서로 등을 두드렸소. 그리고 폰 쾰드웨트하우트 남작과 그 부하들은 흥겹게 말을 달려 집으로 돌아갔지요."]], [["‘For six mortal weeks, the bears and boars had a holiday.", "꼬박 여섯 주 동안 곰과 멧돼지들은 휴일을 누렸소."], ["The houses of Koeldwethout and Swillenhausen were united; the spears rusted; and the baron’s bugle grew hoarse for lack of blowing.", "쾰드웨트하우트 가문과 스윌렌하우젠 가문이 하나로 맺어졌지요. 창은 녹슬었고, 남작의 뿔나팔은 불지 않은 탓에 목이 쉬었소."]], [["‘Those were great times for the four-and-twenty; but, alas!", "스물넷에게는 대단한 시절이었소. 하지만 아아!"], ["their high and palmy days had taken boots to themselves, and were already walking off.", "그들의 드높고 화려한 나날도 제풀에 장화를 챙겨 신고는, 벌써 걸어 나가는 중이었지요."]], [["‘“My dear,”", "'여보.'"], ["said the baroness.", "남작 부인이 말했소."]], [["‘“My love,”", "'여보.'"], ["said the baron.", "남작이 받았소."]], [["‘“Those coarse, noisy men--”", "'저 거칠고 시끄러운 사내들 말이에요.'"]], [["‘“Which, ma’am?”", "'어떤 자들 말이오?'"], ["said the baron, starting.", "남작이 흠칫 놀라며 물었소."]], [["‘The baroness pointed, from the window at which they stood, to the courtyard beneath, where the unconscious Lincoln greens were taking a copious stirrup-cup, preparatory to issuing forth after a boar or two.", "남작 부인은 두 사람이 서 있던 창문에서 아래 안마당을 가리켰소. 거기서는 아무것도 모르는 링컨 그린들이, 멧돼지 한두 마리쯤 잡으러 나서기 전에 이별주를 거나하게 들이켜고 있었지요."]], [["‘“My hunting train, ma’am,”", "'내 사냥 일행이오, 부인.'"], ["said the baron.", "남작이 말했소."]], [["‘“Disband them, love,”", "'해산시켜요, 여보.'"], ["murmured the baroness.", "남작 부인이 속삭였소."]], [["‘“Disband them!”", "'해산시키라고!'"], ["cried the baron, in amazement.", "남작이 기겁하며 외쳤소."]], [["‘“To please me, love,”", "'날 기쁘게 해 주려거든요, 여보.'"], ["replied the baroness.", "남작 부인이 대답했소."]], [["‘“To please the devil, ma’am,”", "'악마를 기쁘게 하지, 부인.'"], ["answered the baron.", "남작이 받아쳤소."]], [["‘Whereupon the baroness uttered a great cry, and swooned away at the baron’s feet.", "그러자 남작 부인은 큰 소리로 외마디 비명을 지르더니 남작의 발치에 풀썩 기절해 쓰러졌소."]], [["‘What could the baron do?", "남작이 어쩌겠소?"], ["He called for the lady’s maid, and roared for the doctor; and then, rushing into the yard, kicked the two Lincoln greens who were the most used to it, and cursing the others all round, bade them go--but never mind where.", "그는 부인의 몸종을 부르고 의사를 부르라 고래고래 소리쳤소. 그러고는 마당으로 뛰쳐나가, 가장 자주 걷어차여 본 링컨 그린 둘을 걷어차고, 나머지에게도 죄다 욕을 퍼부으며 꺼지라고 했소."], ["I don’t know the German for it, or I would put it delicately that way.", "어디로 꺼지라고 했는지는 신경 쓰지 마시오. 그 독일말을 모르니, 안다면 점잖게 옮겨 드렸으련만."]], [["‘It is not for me to say by what means, or by what degrees, some wives manage to keep down some husbands as they do, although I may have my private opinion on the subject, and may think that no Member of Parliament ought to be married, inasmuch as three married members out of every four, must vote according to their wives’ consciences (if there be such things), and not according to their own. All I need say, just now, is, that the Baroness Von Koeldwethout somehow or other acquired great control over the Baron Von Koeldwethout, and that, little by little, and bit by bit, and day by day, and year by year, the baron got the worst of some disputed question, or was slyly unhorsed from some old hobby; and that by the time he was a fat hearty fellow of forty-eight or thereabouts, he had no feasting, no revelry, no hunting train, and no hunting--nothing in short that he liked, or used to have; and that, although he was as fierce as a lion, and as bold as brass, he was decidedly snubbed and put down, by his own lady, in his own castle of Grogzwig.", "어떤 수로, 또 어느 정도까지, 어떤 아내들이 어떤 남편들을 그토록 꽉 잡고 사는지는 내가 말할 자리가 아니오. 하긴 이 문제에 대해 나름의 사견이 있고, 국회의원이란 모름지기 결혼해서는 안 된다고 생각하기는 하오. 결혼한 의원 넷 중 셋은 제 양심이 아니라 아내의 양심에 따라 표를 던질 수밖에 없으니까. 아내에게 그런 게 있다면 말이지만. 지금 내가 할 말은 이것뿐이오. 폰 쾰드웨트하우트 남작 부인은 어찌어찌하여 폰 쾰드웨트하우트 남작을 단단히 휘어잡게 되었다는 것. 그리고 조금씩, 차츰차츰, 날이 가고 해가 갈수록, 남작은 어떤 다툼에서든 늘 지는 쪽이 되거나, 오랜 취미라는 말에서 슬그머니 떨어져 내렸다는 것이오. 그래서 마흔여덟 살 안팎의 살집 좋고 혈색 좋은 사내가 되었을 무렵, 그에게는 잔치도, 흥청거림도, 사냥 일행도, 사냥도 없었소. 한마디로 그가 좋아하던 것, 늘 누리던 것이 하나도 남지 않았지요. 그리고 비록 사자처럼 사납고 황동처럼 뻔뻔했어도, 그는 자기 소유의 그로그즈위그 성에서 자기 아내에게 보기 좋게 면박당하고 짓눌려 살았소."]], [["‘Nor was this the whole extent of the baron’s misfortunes. About a year after his nuptials, there came into the world a lusty young baron, in whose honour a great many fireworks were let off, and a great many dozens of wine drunk; but next year there came a young baroness, and next year another young baron, and so on, every year, either a baron or baroness (and one year both together), until the baron found himself the father of a small family of twelve. Upon every one of these anniversaries, the venerable Baroness Von Swillenhausen was nervously sensitive for the well-being of her child the Baroness Von Koeldwethout; and although it was not found that the good lady ever did anything material towards contributing to her child’s recovery, still she made it a point of duty to be as nervous as possible at the castle of Grogzwig, and to divide her time between moral observations on the baron’s housekeeping, and bewailing the hard lot of her unhappy daughter. And if the Baron of Grogzwig, a little hurt and irritated at this, took heart, and ventured to suggest that his wife was at least no worse off than the wives of other barons, the Baroness Von Swillenhausen begged all persons to take notice, that nobody but she, sympathised with her dear daughter’s sufferings; upon which, her relations and friends remarked, that to be sure she did cry a great deal more than her son-in-law, and that if there were a hard-hearted brute alive, it was that Baron of Grogzwig.", "남작의 불행은 이게 다가 아니었소. 결혼한 지 한 해쯤 지나 우람한 어린 남작이 세상에 나왔고, 그를 기리며 불꽃놀이가 한가득 펼쳐지고 포도주 수십 병이 비워졌지요. 하지만 이듬해엔 어린 여남작이 태어났고, 그다음 해엔 또 어린 남작이, 그렇게 해마다 남작이든 여남작이든 태어났소. 어느 해엔 둘이 한꺼번에 태어나기도 했지요. 그러다 남작은 어느새 열둘이나 되는 자식을 둔 자그마한 한 집안의 가장이 되어 있었소. 그 돌맞이 때마다 점잖은 폰 스윌렌하우젠 노남작 부인은 제 딸 폰 쾰드웨트하우트 남작 부인의 안위를 두고 안절부절못했소. 그 마님이 딸의 회복을 위해 실제로 무슨 보탬이 될 만한 일을 했다는 얘기는 도통 들리지 않았지만, 그래도 그분은 그로그즈위그 성에 와서 되도록 안절부절못하는 걸 자기 본분으로 삼았지요. 그러고는 남작의 살림살이에 대해 도덕적 훈수를 두는 일과, 불행한 딸의 모진 팔자를 한탄하는 일로 시간을 둘로 쪼개 썼소. 그러다 그로그즈위그 남작이 이에 좀 상하고 짜증이 나서 용기를 내어, 적어도 내 아내가 다른 남작들 아내보다 못살지는 않잖느냐고 슬쩍 비치기라도 하면, 폰 스윌렌하우젠 남작 부인은 모두에게 똑똑히 봐 두라고 했소. 사랑하는 제 딸의 고통을 가엾이 여기는 사람은 자기밖에 없다고 말이오. 그러면 그분의 친척과 친구들은, 과연 그분이 사위보다 훨씬 더 많이 우는 게 분명하다고, 세상에 모진 짐승 같은 인간이 있다면 그건 바로 저 그로그즈위그 남작이라고 한마디씩 거들었지요."]], [["‘The poor baron bore it all as long as he could, and when he could bear it no longer lost his appetite and his spirits, and sat himself gloomily and dejectedly down.", "가엾은 남작은 견딜 수 있는 데까지 이 모든 걸 견뎠소. 그러다 더는 견딜 수 없게 되자 입맛도 기운도 잃고, 침울하고 풀이 죽은 채 주저앉아 버렸지요."], ["But there were worse troubles yet in store for him, and as they came on, his melancholy and sadness increased.", "하지만 그를 기다리는 더 큰 시련이 아직 남아 있었고, 그것들이 닥쳐오면서 그의 우울과 슬픔은 깊어만 갔소. 형편이 바뀌었소."], ["Times changed. He got into debt.", "그는 빚더미에 올라앉았지요."], ["The Grogzwig coffers ran low, though the Swillenhausen family had looked upon them as inexhaustible; and just when the baroness was on the point of making a thirteenth addition to the family pedigree, Von Koeldwethout discovered that he had no means of replenishing them.", "스윌렌하우젠 집안은 그로그즈위그의 금고가 마르지 않으리라 여겼지만, 그 금고가 바닥을 드러냈소. 그리고 마침 남작 부인이 가문의 족보에 열셋째를 보태려는 참에, 폰 쾰드웨트하우트는 그것을 다시 채울 길이 없다는 걸 알아차렸소."]], [["‘“I don’t see what is to be done,”", "'어찌해야 할지 모르겠군.'"], ["said the baron.", "남작이 말했소."], ["“I think I’ll kill myself.”", "'아무래도 죽어 버려야겠다.'"]], [["‘This was a bright idea.", "기막힌 묘안이었소."], ["The baron took an old hunting-knife from a cupboard hard by, and having sharpened it on his boot, made what boys call “an offer”", "남작은 가까운 찬장에서 낡은 사냥칼을 꺼내, 장화에다 쓱쓱 갈더니, 아이들 말로 제 목을 향해 '한번 들이대'"], ["at his throat.", "보았지요."]], [["‘“Hem!”", "'흠!'"], ["said the baron, stopping short.", "남작이 멈칫하며 말했소."], ["“Perhaps it’s not sharp enough.”", "'날이 영 덜 선 모양인데.'"]], [["‘The baron sharpened it again, and made another offer, when his hand was arrested by a loud screaming among the young barons and baronesses, who had a nursery in an upstairs tower with iron bars outside the window, to prevent their tumbling out into the moat.", "남작은 칼을 다시 갈아 또 한 번 들이댔소. 그런데 어린 남작들과 여남작들의 요란한 비명에 그의 손이 멈췄지요. 그 아이들은 위층 탑에 마련된 방을 썼는데, 해자로 떨어지지 않도록 창문 바깥에 쇠창살이 둘러쳐 있었소."]], [["‘“If I had been a bachelor,”", "'내가 독신이었더라면.'"], ["said the baron sighing, “I might have done it fifty times over, without being interrupted.", "남작이 한숨지으며 말했소. '아무 방해도 안 받고 쉰 번은 해치웠을 텐데."], ["Hallo!", "여봐라!"], ["Put a flask of wine and the largest pipe in the little vaulted room behind the hall.”", "회랑 뒤편 작은 둥근 천장 방에 포도주 한 병하고 제일 큰 담뱃대를 갖다 놓아라.'"]], [["‘One of the domestics, in a very kind manner, executed the baron’s order in the course of half an hour or so, and Von Koeldwethout being apprised thereof, strode to the vaulted room, the walls of which, being of dark shining wood, gleamed in the light of the blazing logs which were piled upon the hearth.", "하인 하나가 아주 친절한 태도로 반 시간쯤 만에 남작의 분부를 받들었고, 그 소식을 전해 들은 폰 쾰드웨트하우트는 둥근 천장 방으로 성큼성큼 걸어 들어갔소. 그 방의 벽은 거뭇하니 반들거리는 나무로 되어 있어, 난로에 쌓아 활활 타오르는 장작불 빛에 어른어른 빛났지요."], ["The bottle and pipe were ready, and, upon the whole, the place looked very comfortable.", "술병과 담뱃대가 준비되어 있었고, 전체적으로 그 방은 무척 아늑해 보였소."]], [["‘“Leave the lamp,”", "'램프는 두고 가라.'"], ["said the baron.", "남작이 말했소."]], [["‘“Anything else, my lord?”", "'더 필요하신 건 없으신지요, 나리?'"], ["inquired the domestic.", "하인이 물었소."]], [["‘“The room,”", "'방.'"], ["replied the baron.", "남작이 대답했소."], ["The domestic obeyed, and the baron locked the door.", "하인은 분부대로 나갔고, 남작은 문을 잠갔소."]], [["‘“I’ll smoke a last pipe,”", "'마지막으로 담배 한 대 피우고,'"], ["said the baron, “and then I’ll be off.”", "남작이 말했소. '그러고 나서 떠나야지.'"], ["So, putting the knife upon the table till he wanted it, and tossing off a goodly measure of wine, the Lord of Grogzwig threw himself back in his chair, stretched his legs out before the fire, and puffed away.", "그래서 그는 쓸 때까지 칼을 식탁에 올려 둔 채, 포도주를 거나하게 한 잔 들이켜고는, 의자에 등을 기대 불 앞으로 다리를 쭉 뻗고 뻐끔뻐끔 담배를 피웠소."]], [["‘He thought about a great many things--about his present troubles and past days of bachelorship, and about the Lincoln greens, long since dispersed up and down the country, no one knew whither: with the exception of two who had been unfortunately beheaded, and four who had killed themselves with drinking.", "그는 참 많은 것을 생각했소. 지금의 시련과 지난날의 독신 시절을, 그리고 이제는 어디로 갔는지 아무도 모르게 온 나라에 뿔뿔이 흩어진 링컨 그린들을 떠올렸지요."], ["His mind was running upon bears and boars, when, in the process of draining his glass to the bottom, he raised his eyes, and saw, for the first time and with unbounded astonishment, that he was not alone.", "다만 둘은 불행히도 목이 잘렸고, 넷은 술로 제 명을 끊었소. 그의 마음이 곰과 멧돼지를 좇고 있을 때, 잔을 바닥까지 비우려고 눈을 들었다가, 그는 난생처음으로 한없는 놀라움 속에 자기가 혼자가 아니라는 걸 알아챘소."]], [["‘No, he was not; for, on the opposite side of the fire, there sat with folded arms a wrinkled hideous figure, with deeply sunk and bloodshot eyes, and an immensely long cadaverous face, shadowed by jagged and matted locks of coarse black hair.", "그렇소, 혼자가 아니었지요. 난로 맞은편에, 팔짱을 낀 채 주름지고 흉측한 형상 하나가 앉아 있었으니까."], ["He wore a kind of tunic of a dull bluish colour, which, the baron observed, on regarding it attentively, was clasped or ornamented down the front with coffin handles.", "움푹 꺼져 핏발 선 눈에, 시체처럼 길쭉한 얼굴은 거칠고 검은 머리칼이 들쭉날쭉 헝클어져 그늘져 있었소. 그것은 흐릿한 푸르스름한 빛깔의 긴 윗옷 같은 걸 걸쳤는데, 남작이 유심히 들여다보니 그 앞섶을 따라 관 손잡이들이 단추처럼, 혹은 장식처럼 달려 있었소."], ["His legs, too, were encased in coffin plates as though in armour; and over his left shoulder he wore a short dusky cloak, which seemed made of a remnant of some pall.", "다리에도 갑옷처럼 관 장식판이 둘러져 있었고, 왼쪽 어깨에는 어느 관 덮개의 자투리로 지은 듯한 짧고 거뭇한 망토를 걸치고 있었지요."], ["He took no notice of the baron, but was intently eyeing the fire.", "그것은 남작에게는 눈길도 주지 않고, 그저 뚫어지게 불을 바라보고 있었소."]], [["‘“Halloa!”", "'이봐!'"], ["said the baron, stamping his foot to attract attention.", "남작이 주의를 끌려고 발을 쿵 굴렀소."]], [["‘“Halloa!”", "'이봐!'"], ["replied the stranger, moving his eyes towards the baron, but not his face or himself “What now?”", "그 형상이 얼굴이나 몸은 그대로 둔 채 눈만 남작 쪽으로 돌리며 대꾸했소. '왜 그러나?'"]], [["‘“What now!”", "'왜 그러냐니!'"], ["replied the baron, nothing daunted by his hollow voice and lustreless eyes.", "남작은 그 공허한 목소리와 빛 없는 눈에도 조금도 주눅 들지 않고 받아쳤소."], ["“I should ask that question.", "'그건 내가 물을 말이지."], ["How did you get here?”", "어떻게 여기 들어왔나?'"]], [["‘“Through the door,”", "'문으로.'"], ["replied the figure.", "그 형상이 대답했소."]], [["‘“What are you?”", "'정체가 뭐냐?'"], ["says the baron.", "남작이 물었소."]], [["‘“A man,”", "'사람이오.'"], ["replied the figure.", "그 형상이 대답했소."]], [["‘“I don’t believe it,”", "'못 믿겠는데.'"], ["says the baron.", "남작이 말했소."]], [["‘“Disbelieve it then,”", "'그럼 안 믿든가.'"], ["says the figure.", "그 형상이 말했소."]], [["‘“I will,”", "'안 믿지.'"], ["rejoined the baron.", "남작이 받았소."]], [["‘The figure looked at the bold Baron of Grogzwig for some time, and then said familiarly,", "그 형상은 한참 동안 용감한 그로그즈위그 남작을 바라보더니, 허물없이 말했소."]], [["‘“There’s no coming over you, I see.", "'당신은 도무지 속아 넘어가질 않는군."], ["I’m not a man!”", "나는 사람이 아니오!'"]], [["‘“What are you then?”", "'그럼 정체가 뭐냐?'"], ["asked the baron.", "남작이 물었소."]], [["‘“A genius,”", "'정령이오.'"], ["replied the figure.", "그 형상이 대답했소."]], [["‘“You don’t look much like one,”", "'별로 그렇게 안 보이는데.'"], ["returned the baron scornfully.", "남작이 비웃듯 받았소."]], [["‘“I am the Genius of Despair and Suicide,”", "'나는 절망과 자살의 정령이오.'"], ["said the apparition.", "그 유령이 말했소."], ["“Now you know me.”", "'이제 나를 알겠지.'"]], [["‘With these words the apparition turned towards the baron, as if composing himself for a talk--and, what was very remarkable, was, that he threw his cloak aside, and displaying a stake, which was run through the centre of his body, pulled it out with a jerk, and laid it on the table, as composedly as if it had been a walking-stick.", "이 말과 함께 유령은 한바탕 이야기라도 나눌 양 자세를 가다듬으며 남작 쪽으로 돌아앉았소. 그런데 참으로 놀라운 것은, 그가 망토를 옆으로 젖히더니 제 몸 한가운데를 꿰뚫고 있는 말뚝을 드러내고는, 그걸 휙 잡아 뽑아 마치 지팡이라도 되는 양 태연히 식탁 위에 올려놓는 거였소."]], [["‘“Now,”", "'자.'"], ["said the figure, glancing at the hunting-knife, “are you ready for me?”", "그 형상이 사냥칼을 흘끗 보며 말했소. '나를 맞을 준비가 됐나?'"]], [["‘“Not quite,”", "'아직.'"], ["rejoined the baron; “I must finish this pipe first.”", "남작이 받았소. '먼저 이 담배부터 다 피워야겠어.'"]], [["‘“Look sharp then,”", "'그럼 서두르시지.'"], ["said the figure.", "그 형상이 말했소."]], [["‘“You seem in a hurry,”", "'바쁜 모양이군.'"], ["said the baron.", "남작이 말했소."]], [["‘“Why, yes, I am,”", "'그래, 바쁘지.' 그 형상이 대답했소."], ["answered the figure; “they’re doing a pretty brisk business in my way, over in England and France just now, and my time is a good deal taken up.”", "'요즘 영국하고 프랑스 쪽에서 내 일감이 꽤 잘나가거든. 시간이 영 빠듯해.'"]], [["‘“Do you drink?”", "'한잔하겠나?'"], ["said the baron, touching the bottle with the bowl of his pipe.", "남작이 담뱃대 통으로 술병을 톡 건드리며 말했소."]], [["‘“Nine times out of ten, and then very hard,”", "'열에 아홉은 마시지, 그것도 아주 진탕.'"], ["rejoined the figure, drily.", "그 형상이 무뚝뚝하게 받았소."]], [["‘“Never in moderation?”", "'적당히는 안 마시고?'"], ["asked the baron.", "남작이 물었소."]], [["‘“Never,”", "'절대로.'"], ["replied the figure, with a shudder, “that breeds cheerfulness.”", "그 형상이 몸서리치며 대답했소. '적당히 마시면 기분이 좋아지거든.'"]], [["‘The baron took another look at his new friend, whom he thought an uncommonly queer customer, and at length inquired whether he took any active part in such little proceedings as that which he had in contemplation.", "남작은 새 친구를 다시 한번 훑어보며, 어지간히 별난 손님이라 여겼소. 그러고는 마침내, 지금 자기가 마음먹고 있는 그런 자그마한 일에 그가 직접 한몫 거드는지 물었소."]], [["‘“No,”", "'아니.'"], ["replied the figure evasively; “but I am always present.”", "그 형상이 얼버무리며 대답했소. '하지만 나는 늘 곁에 있지.'"]], [["‘“Just to see fair, I suppose?”", "'그저 공정하게 진행되나 보려고, 그런 거겠지?'"], ["said the baron.", "남작이 말했소."]], [["‘“Just that,”", "'바로 그거지.'"], ["replied the figure, playing with his stake, and examining the ferule.", "그 형상이 제 말뚝을 만지작거리며 그 쇠 물미를 살펴보며 대답했소."], ["“Be as quick as you can, will you, for there’s a young gentleman who is afflicted with too much money and leisure wanting me now, I find.”", "'되도록 빨리 좀 해 주겠나? 보아하니 지금 돈도 너무 많고 한가하기까지 한 어떤 젊은 양반이 날 기다리고 있거든.'"]], [["‘“Going to kill himself because he has too much money!”", "'돈이 너무 많아서 죽겠다고!'"], ["exclaimed the baron, quite tickled.", "남작이 어지간히 재미있어하며 소리쳤소."], ["“Ha! ha!", "'하하!"], ["that’s a good one.”", "그거 한번 웃기는군.'"], ["(This was the first time the baron had laughed for many a long day.)", "(남작이 이렇게 웃은 건 참으로 오랜만이었소.)"]], [["‘“I say,”", "'이봐.'"], ["expostulated the figure, looking very much scared; “don’t do that again.”", "그 형상이 몹시 겁먹은 얼굴로 항의했소. '그러지 좀 마.'"]], [["‘“Why not?”", "'왜 안 되는데?'"], ["demanded the baron.", "남작이 따져 물었소."]], [["‘“Because it gives me pain all over,”", "'온몸이 다 아프니까.'"], ["replied the figure.", "그 형상이 대답했소."], ["“Sigh as much as you please: that does me good.”", "'한숨이라면 얼마든지 쉬어도 좋아. 그건 나한테 보약이거든.'"]], [["‘The baron sighed mechanically at the mention of the word; the figure, brightening up again, handed him the hunting-knife with most winning politeness.", "남작은 그 말이 나오기가 무섭게 기계적으로 한숨을 쉬었소. 그러자 그 형상은 다시 밝아져서, 더없이 살가운 정중함으로 그에게 사냥칼을 건넸지요."]], [["‘“It’s not a bad idea though,”", "'그래도 꽤 괜찮은 발상이긴 하군.'"], ["said the baron, feeling the edge of the weapon; “a man killing himself because he has too much money.”", "남작이 칼날을 만져 보며 말했소. '돈이 너무 많아서 죽는다라.'"]], [["‘“Pooh!”", "'쳇!'"], ["said the apparition, petulantly, “no better than a man’s killing himself because he has none or little.”", "유령이 짜증스레 말했소. '돈이 없어서, 아니면 적어서 죽는 거나 나을 게 하나도 없지.'"]], [["‘Whether the genius unintentionally committed himself in saying this, or whether he thought the baron’s mind was so thoroughly made up that it didn’t matter what he said, I have no means of knowing.", "그 정령이 이 말을 얼떨결에 내뱉어 제 무덤을 판 건지, 아니면 남작의 마음이 워낙 확고하게 굳었으니 무슨 말을 하든 상관없으리라 여긴 건지, 나로서는 알 길이 없소."], ["I only know that the baron stopped his hand, all of a sudden, opened his eyes wide, and looked as if quite a new light had come upon him for the first time.", "다만 내가 아는 건, 남작이 별안간 손을 멈추고 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨더니, 난생처음 무슨 새로운 빛이라도 비친 듯한 표정을 지었다는 것뿐이오."]], [["‘“Why, certainly,”", "'아니, 그렇고말고.'"], ["said Von Koeldwethout, “nothing is too bad to be retrieved.”", "폰 쾰드웨트하우트가 말했소. '되돌릴 수 없을 만큼 나쁜 건 없지.'"]], [["‘“Except empty coffers,”", "'텅 빈 금고만 빼고.'"], ["cried the genius.", "정령이 외쳤소."]], [["‘“Well; but they may be one day filled again,”", "'글쎄, 하지만 그것도 언젠가 다시 채워질 수 있잖나.'"], ["said the baron.", "남작이 말했소."]], [["‘“Scolding wives,”", "'바가지 긁는 아내.'"], ["snarled the genius.", "정령이 으르렁댔소."]], [["‘“Oh!", "'아!"], ["They may be made quiet,”", "조용하게 만들 수도 있고.'"], ["said the baron.", "남작이 말했소."]], [["‘“Thirteen children,”", "'열셋이나 되는 자식.'"], ["shouted the genius.", "정령이 고함쳤소."]], [["‘“Can’t all go wrong, surely,”", "'설마 다 잘못되기야 하겠나.'"], ["said the baron.", "남작이 말했소."]], [["‘The genius was evidently growing very savage with the baron, for holding these opinions all at once; but he tried to laugh it off, and said if he would let him know when he had left off joking he should feel obliged to him.", "정령은 남작이 이런 생각들을 한꺼번에 쏟아 내자 어지간히 사나워진 게 분명했소. 하지만 그는 웃어넘기려 애쓰며, 농담을 다 끝내거든 좀 알려 주면 고맙겠다고 했지요."]], [["‘“But I am not joking; I was never farther from it,”", "'하지만 나는 농담하는 게 아닐세. 농담과는 거리가 멀어.'"], ["remonstrated the baron.", "남작이 따졌소."]], [["‘“Well, I am glad to hear that,”", "'그래, 그 말 들으니 다행이군.'"], ["said the genius, looking very grim, “because a joke, without any figure of speech, IS the death of me.", "정령이 아주 험상궂은 얼굴로 말했소. '비유고 뭐고 없이, 농담은 정말이지 나한테는 죽음이거든."], ["Come!", "자!"], ["Quit this dreary world at once.”", "어서 이 음울한 세상을 떠나시지.'"]], [["‘“I don’t know,”", "'글쎄.' 남작이 칼을 만지작거리며 말했소."], ["said the baron, playing with the knife; “it’s a dreary one certainly, but I don’t think yours is much better, for you have not the appearance of being particularly comfortable.", "'음울한 세상이긴 하지. 하지만 자네 쪽도 그리 나아 보이진 않는걸."], ["That puts me in mind--what security have I, that I shall be any the better for going out of the world after all!”", "자네야말로 딱히 편안해 보이지가 않으니 말이야. 그러고 보니 말인데, 내가 이 세상을 떠난들 정말로 조금이라도 나아지리란 보장이 어디 있나!'"], ["he cried, starting up; “I never thought of that.”", "그가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤소. '그 생각은 미처 못 했군.'"]], [["‘“Dispatch,”", "'어서!'"], ["cried the figure, gnashing his teeth.", "그 형상이 이를 갈며 외쳤소."]], [["‘“Keep off!”", "'물러서!' 남작이 말했소."], ["said the baron.", "'더는 비참한 일들을 곱씹지 않겠다. 좋은 낯으로 이 일을 헤쳐 나가, 다시 한번 신선한 바람과 곰들을 맛보겠어."], ["‘I’ll brood over miseries no longer, but put a good face on the matter, and try the fresh air and the bears again; and if that don’t do, I’ll talk to the baroness soundly, and cut the Von Swillenhausens dead.’ With this the baron fell into his chair, and laughed so loud and boisterously, that the room rang with it.", "그래도 안 되면, 마누라한테 따끔하게 한마디 하고, 폰 스윌렌하우젠 집안과는 아예 발을 끊어 버리지.' 이 말과 함께 남작은 의자에 털썩 주저앉아, 어찌나 크고 우렁차게 웃어 댔던지 방 안이 그 소리로 쩌렁쩌렁 울렸소."]], [["‘The figure fell back a pace or two, regarding the baron meanwhile with a look of intense terror, and when he had ceased, caught up the stake, plunged it violently into its body, uttered a frightful howl, and disappeared.", "그 형상은 한두 걸음 뒷걸음질 치며, 그동안 극심한 공포의 눈빛으로 남작을 노려보았소. 그러고는 남작이 웃음을 그치자, 말뚝을 움켜쥐고 제 몸에 사납게 푹 찔러 넣더니, 소름 끼치는 비명을 지르며 사라져 버렸소."]], [["‘Von Koeldwethout never saw it again. Having once made up his mind to action, he soon brought the baroness and the Von Swillenhausens to reason, and died many years afterwards: not a rich man that I am aware of, but certainly a happy one: leaving behind him a numerous family, who had been carefully educated in bear and boar-hunting under his own personal eye. And my advice to all men is, that if ever they become hipped and melancholy from similar causes (as very many men do), they look at both sides of the question, applying a magnifying-glass to the best one; and if they still feel tempted to retire without leave, that they smoke a large pipe and drink a full bottle first, and profit by the laudable example of the Baron of Grogzwig.’", "폰 쾰드웨트하우트는 다시는 그것을 보지 못했소. 한번 행동하기로 마음먹은 그는, 이내 남작 부인과 폰 스윌렌하우젠 집안을 정신 차리게 만들었고, 여러 해 뒤에 세상을 떠났소. 내가 알기로 부자는 아니었지만, 분명 행복한 사람이었지요. 그리고 곰과 멧돼지 사냥을 손수 곁에서 지켜보며 정성껏 가르친 많은 자식들을 남겼소. 그러니 모든 사람에게 주는 내 충고는 이거요. 혹시라도 비슷한 까닭으로 우울과 시름에 빠지거든 (참 많은 사람이 그러지요), 문제의 양면을 다 들여다보되 좋은 쪽에는 돋보기를 갖다 대시오. 그래도 여전히 허락 없이 이 세상을 뜨고 싶은 유혹이 든다면, 먼저 큰 담뱃대로 담배 한 대 피우고 술 한 병을 죄다 비운 다음, 그로그즈위그 남작의 갸륵한 본보기를 따르시오.\""]], [["‘The fresh coach is ready, ladies and gentlemen, if you please,’ said a new driver, looking in.", "\"새 마차가 준비됐습니다, 신사 숙녀 여러분, 채비하시지요.\" 새 마부가 안을 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["This intelligence caused the punch to be finished in a great hurry, and prevented any discussion relative to the last story. Mr.", "이 소식에 펀치는 부랴부랴 비워졌고, 마지막 이야기를 두고 더 왈가왈부할 틈도 없었다."], ["Squeers was observed to draw the grey-headed gentleman on one side, and to ask a question with great apparent interest; it bore reference to the Five Sisters of York, and was, in fact, an inquiry whether he could inform him how much per annum the Yorkshire convents got in those days with their boarders.", "스퀴어스 씨가 희끗한 머리의 신사를 한쪽으로 끌어당겨, 자못 흥미진진한 얼굴로 뭔가 묻는 게 보였다. 그건 요크의 다섯 자매에 관한 것이었는데, 사실은 그 시절 요크셔의 수도원들이 기숙생을 두고 한 해에 얼마씩 받았는지 알려 줄 수 있겠느냐는 물음이었다."]], [["The journey was then resumed.", "이윽고 여정이 다시 시작되었다."], ["Nicholas fell asleep towards morning, and, when he awoke, found, with great regret, that, during his nap, both the Baron of Grogzwig and the grey-haired gentleman had got down and were gone.", "니콜라스는 새벽녘에 잠이 들었고, 깨어나 보니 자기가 깜박 조는 사이에 그로그즈위그의 남작도 희끗한 머리의 신사도 둘 다 마차에서 내려 가 버린 걸 알고는 몹시 아쉬워했다."], ["The day dragged on uncomfortably enough.", "그날 하루는 꽤나 불편하게 더디 흘러갔다."], ["At about six o’clock that night, he and Mr. Squeers, and the little boys, and their united luggage, were all put down together at the George and New Inn, Greta Bridge.", "그날 밤 여섯 시쯤, 그와 스퀴어스 씨와 어린아이들, 그리고 그들의 짐 전부가 그레타 브리지의 조지 앤드 뉴 여관 앞에 한꺼번에 내려졌다."]], [["Mr. and Mrs.", "스퀴어스 씨는 무사히 마차에서 내리자, 니콜라스와 아이들을 짐과 함께 길가에 세워 두었어."], ["Squeers at Home\nMr.", "마차가 말을 바꾸는 동안 그거나 구경하며 시간을 보내라는 거였지."], ["Squeers, being safely landed, left Nicholas and the boys standing with the luggage in the road, to amuse themselves by looking at the coach as it changed horses, while he ran into the tavern and went through the leg-stretching process at the bar.", "그러고는 자기는 술집으로 뛰어 들어가 술집 카운터에서 다리를 쭉 펴는 일을 했어. 몇 분 뒤에 그가 돌아왔는데, 빨개진 코와 짧은 딸꾹질을 보면 다리를 아주 톡톡히 펴고 온 게 틀림없었지."], ["After some minutes, he returned, with his legs thoroughly stretched, if the hue of his nose and a short hiccup afforded any criterion; and at the same time there came out of the yard a rusty pony-chaise, and a cart, driven by two labouring men.", "그와 동시에 마당에서 낡은 조랑말 마차 한 대와 짐수레 한 대가 나왔어. 짐수레는 일꾼 둘이 몰고 있었지."]], [["‘Put the boys and the boxes into the cart,’ said Squeers, rubbing his hands; ‘and this young man and me will go on in the chaise.", "\"애들이랑 상자는 짐수레에 실어.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 손을 비비며 말했어."], ["Get in, Nickleby.’", "\"이 젊은 양반하고 나는 마차로 갈 테니까. 타게, 니클비.\""]], [["Nicholas obeyed. Mr.", "니콜라스는 시키는 대로 했어."], ["Squeers with some difficulty inducing the pony to obey also, they started off, leaving the cart-load of infant misery to follow at leisure.", "스퀴어스 씨가 조랑말도 시키는 대로 따르게 하느라 좀 애를 먹긴 했지만, 두 사람은 출발했지. 어린 비참함을 한가득 실은 짐수레는 천천히 뒤따라오게 내버려 두고 말이야."]], [["‘Are you cold, Nickleby?’ inquired Squeers, after they had travelled some distance in silence.", "\"춥나, 니클비?\" 한참을 말없이 달린 뒤에 스퀴어스가 물었어."]], [["‘Rather, sir, I must say.’", "\"네, 좀 그러네요, 솔직히 말하면요.\""]], [["‘Well, I don’t find fault with that,’ said Squeers; ‘it’s a long journey this weather.’", "\"뭐, 그건 탓할 일이 아니지.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"이런 날씨에 긴 여행이니까.\""]], [["‘Is it much farther to Dotheboys Hall, sir?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"두더보이즈 홀까지 아직 많이 남았나요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘About three mile from here,’ replied Squeers.", "\"여기서 한 삼 마일쯤.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."], ["‘But you needn’t call it a Hall down here.’", "\"그런데 여기 내려와서는 그걸 홀이라고 부르면 안 돼.\""]], [["Nicholas coughed, as if he would like to know why.", "니콜라스는 왜 그런지 알고 싶은 듯 헛기침을 했어."]], [["‘The fact is, it ain’t a Hall,’ observed Squeers drily.", "\"사실 홀이 아니거든.\" 스퀴어스가 무뚝뚝하게 말했어."]], [["‘Oh, indeed!’ said Nicholas, whom this piece of intelligence much astonished.", "\"아, 그래요!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. 이 소식에 적잖이 놀란 거야."]], [["‘No,’ replied Squeers. ‘We call it a Hall up in London, because it sounds better, but they don’t know it by that name in these parts. A man may call his house an island if he likes; there’s no act of Parliament against that, I believe?’", "\"그래.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"런던에서는 그걸 홀이라고 부르지. 그게 듣기에 더 그럴듯하니까. 그런데 이 동네에서는 그런 이름으로 안 통해. 사람이 자기 집을 섬이라고 부르고 싶으면 그래도 돼. 그걸 막는 법은 없으니까, 그렇지?\""]], [["‘I believe not, sir,’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"없겠지요.\" 니콜라스가 맞장구쳤어."]], [["Squeers eyed his companion slyly, at the conclusion of this little dialogue, and finding that he had grown thoughtful and appeared in nowise disposed to volunteer any observations, contented himself with lashing the pony until they reached their journey’s end.", "이 짧은 대화가 끝나자 스퀴어스는 슬쩍 곁눈질로 동행을 살폈어. 그런데 니콜라스가 생각에 잠긴 듯 더는 무슨 말을 꺼낼 기색이 없자, 그저 목적지에 다다를 때까지 조랑말한테 채찍질이나 하며 만족했지."]], [["‘Jump out,’ said Squeers.", "\"내려.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘Hallo there!", "\"이봐, 거기!"], ["Come and put this horse up.", "와서 이 말 좀 들여놔."], ["Be quick, will you!’", "빨리빨리, 알겠어!\""]], [["While the schoolmaster was uttering these and other impatient cries, Nicholas had time to observe that the school was a long, cold-looking house, one storey high, with a few straggling out-buildings behind, and a barn and stable adjoining. After the lapse of a minute or two, the noise of somebody unlocking the yard-gate was heard, and presently a tall lean boy, with a lantern in his hand, issued forth.", "선생이 이런저런 성급한 고함을 지르는 동안, 니콜라스는 학교를 둘러볼 틈이 있었어. 길쭉하고 추워 보이는 단층집이었지. 뒤에는 별채 몇 채가 띄엄띄엄 서 있었고, 헛간과 마구간이 붙어 있었어. 한두 분쯤 지나자 누가 마당 문 자물쇠를 따는 소리가 들렸어. 곧 키가 크고 비쩍 마른 소년이 손에 등불을 들고 나왔지."]], [["‘Is that you, Smike?’ cried Squeers.", "\"스마이크 너냐?\" 스퀴어스가 소리쳤어."]], [["‘Yes, sir,’ replied the boy.", "\"네.\" 소년이 대답했어."]], [["‘Then why the devil didn’t you come before?’", "\"그럼 왜 진작 안 나왔어, 이놈아?\""]], [["‘Please, sir, I fell asleep over the fire,’ answered Smike, with humility.", "\"죄송해요, 불 앞에서 잠이 들었어요.\" 스마이크가 주눅 든 목소리로 대답했어."]], [["‘Fire!", "\"불!"], ["what fire?", "무슨 불?"], ["Where’s there a fire?’ demanded the schoolmaster, sharply.", "불이 어디 있어?\" 선생이 날카롭게 따져 물었어."]], [["‘Only in the kitchen, sir,’ replied the boy.", "\"부엌에 있는 거요.\" 소년이 대답했어."], ["‘Missus said as I was sitting up, I might go in there for a warm.’", "\"사모님이, 제가 안 자고 기다리니까 거기 가서 좀 녹여도 된다고 하셨어요.\""]], [["‘Your missus is a fool,’ retorted Squeers.", "\"네 사모님은 바보야.\" 스퀴어스가 쏘아붙였어."], ["‘You’d have been a deuced deal more wakeful in the cold, I’ll engage.’", "\"추운 데 있었으면 훨씬 더 말똥말똥했을 거다, 내가 장담해.\""]], [["By this time Mr.", "이때쯤 스퀴어스 씨는 마차에서 내려 있었어."], ["Squeers had dismounted; and after ordering the boy to see to the pony, and to take care that he hadn’t any more corn that night, he told Nicholas to wait at the front-door a minute while he went round and let him in.", "소년한테 조랑말을 돌보라고, 그리고 오늘 밤엔 더 이상 여물을 주지 말라고 단단히 일러두고는, 니콜라스한테 잠깐 현관에서 기다리라고 했어. 자기가 돌아가서 문을 열어 주겠다는 거였지."]], [["A host of unpleasant misgivings, which had been crowding upon Nicholas during the whole journey, thronged into his mind with redoubled force when he was left alone.", "여행 내내 니콜라스를 짓누르던 갖가지 불길한 예감이, 혼자 남겨지자 곱절로 거세게 머릿속으로 밀려들었어."], ["His great distance from home and the impossibility of reaching it, except on foot, should he feel ever so anxious to return, presented itself to him in most alarming colours; and as he looked up at the dreary house and dark windows, and upon the wild country round, covered with snow, he felt a depression of heart and spirit which he had never experienced before.", "집에서 너무나 멀리 떨어져 있다는 것, 그리고 돌아가고 싶은 마음이 아무리 간절해도 걸어가는 것 말고는 닿을 방법이 없다는 것이, 더없이 섬뜩한 빛깔로 그에게 다가왔어. 그 음산한 집과 캄캄한 창문들, 그리고 눈으로 뒤덮인 사방의 거친 들판을 올려다보자니, 그는 일찍이 한 번도 느껴 본 적 없는 마음과 정신의 무거움을 느꼈어."]], [["‘Now then!’ cried Squeers, poking his head out at the front-door.", "\"자 어서!\" 스퀴어스가 현관문으로 고개를 쑥 내밀고 소리쳤어."], ["‘Where are you, Nickleby?’", "\"어디 있나, 니클비?\""]], [["‘Here, sir,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"여기요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Come in, then,’ said Squeers ‘the wind blows in, at this door, fit to knock a man off his legs.’", "\"그럼 들어와.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"이 문으로 바람이 들이치는데, 사람을 자빠뜨릴 지경이야.\""]], [["Nicholas sighed, and hurried in.", "니콜라스는 한숨을 쉬고 서둘러 들어갔어."], ["Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨는 문이 닫혀 있게 빗장을 지른 다음, 그를 작은 거실로 안내했지."], ["Squeers, having bolted the door to keep it shut, ushered him into a small parlour scantily furnished with a few chairs, a yellow map hung against the wall, and a couple of tables; one of which bore some preparations for supper; while, on the other, a tutor’s assistant, a Murray’s grammar, half-a-dozen cards of terms, and a worn letter directed to Wackford Squeers, Esquire, were arranged in picturesque confusion.", "가구라곤 의자 몇 개와 벽에 걸린 누런 지도 한 장, 그리고 탁자 두 개가 전부인 휑한 방이었어. 한쪽 탁자에는 저녁 준비가 좀 차려져 있었고, 다른 탁자에는 가정교사용 참고서 한 권, 머리의 문법책 한 권, 학비 안내 카드 대여섯 장, 그리고 '왁퍼드 스퀴어스 귀하'라고 적힌 닳아 빠진 편지 한 통이 그림처럼 어수선하게 놓여 있었지."]], [["They had not been in this apartment a couple of minutes, when a female bounced into the room, and, seizing Mr.", "두 사람이 이 방에 들어선 지 채 이 분도 안 되어, 한 여자가 방으로 불쑥 뛰어들었어."], ["Squeers by the throat, gave him two loud kisses: one close after the other, like a postman’s knock.", "그러고는 스퀴어스 씨의 목덜미를 와락 끌어안고 쪽쪽 소리 나게 입을 두 번 맞췄지."], ["The lady, who was of a large raw-boned figure, was about half a head taller than Mr.", "우체부가 문 두드리듯 연달아서 말이야. 이 부인은 뼈대가 굵고 우람한 체구였어."], ["Squeers, and was dressed in a dimity night-jacket; with her hair in papers; she had also a dirty nightcap on, relieved by a yellow cotton handkerchief which tied it under the chin.", "스퀴어스 씨보다 머리 반쯤은 더 컸고, 무명 잠옷을 걸치고 있었지. 머리는 종이로 말아 놓았고, 더러운 잠자리용 모자를 쓴 데다, 그걸 턱밑에서 묶은 노란 무명 손수건이 그나마 봐 줄 만하게 해 주고 있었어."]], [["‘How is my Squeery?’ said this lady in a playful manner, and a very hoarse voice.", "\"우리 스퀴어 자기, 잘 다녀왔어요?\" 부인이 장난스러운 투로, 아주 쉰 목소리로 말했어."]], [["‘Quite well, my love,’ replied Squeers.", "\"잘 다녀왔지, 여보.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."], ["‘How’s the cows?’", "\"소들은 어때?\""]], [["‘All right, every one of’em,’ answered the lady.", "\"다들 괜찮아요, 한 마리도 빠짐없이.\" 부인이 대답했어."]], [["‘And the pigs?’ said Squeers.", "\"돼지들은?\" 스퀴어스가 물었어."]], [["‘As well as they were when you went away.’", "\"당신 떠날 때랑 똑같아요.\""]], [["‘Come; that’s a blessing,’ said Squeers, pulling off his great-coat.", "\"거참, 다행이군.\" 스퀴어스가 외투를 벗으며 말했어."], ["‘The boys are all as they were, I suppose?’", "\"애들도 다 그대로겠지?\""]], [["‘Oh, yes, they’re well enough,’ replied Mrs.", "\"아, 그럼요, 다 멀쩡해요.\""], ["Squeers, snappishly.", "스퀴어스 부인이 쏘아붙이듯 대답했어."], ["‘That young Pitcher’s had a fever.’", "\"그 어린 피처란 놈이 열이 났어요.\""]], [["‘No!’ exclaimed Squeers.", "\"저런!\" 스퀴어스가 소리쳤어."], ["‘Damn that boy, he’s always at something of that sort.’", "\"빌어먹을 놈, 늘 무슨 일을 저질러요.\""]], [["‘Never was such a boy, I do believe,’ said Mrs.", "\"그런 애는 처음 봐요, 정말이지.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말했어."], ["Squeers; ‘whatever he has is always catching too.", "\"무슨 병에 걸리든 꼭 옮기기까지 한다니까요."], ["I say it’s obstinacy, and nothing shall ever convince me that it isn’t.", "내 말은 저게 고집이라는 거예요. 누가 뭐래도 아니라곤 안 믿어요."], ["I’d beat it out of him; and I told you that, six months ago.’", "나라면 매로 다스려서 빼 버렸을 거예요. 반년 전에도 그렇게 말했잖아요.\""]], [["‘So you did, my love,’ rejoined Squeers.", "\"그랬지, 여보.\" 스퀴어스가 맞장구쳤어."], ["‘We’ll try what can be done.’", "\"어떻게 할 수 있을지 한번 해 보자고.\""]], [["Pending these little endearments, Nicholas had stood, awkwardly enough, in the middle of the room: not very well knowing whether he was expected to retire into the passage, or to remain where he was.", "이 자그마한 애정 표현이 오가는 동안, 니콜라스는 어색하기 짝이 없게 방 한가운데 서 있었어. 복도로 물러가 있어야 하는 건지, 아니면 그냥 그 자리에 있어야 하는 건지 도무지 알 수가 없었거든."], ["He was now relieved from his perplexity by Mr. Squeers.", "그런데 이제 스퀴어스 씨가 그 난처함을 풀어 줬지."]], [["‘This is the new young man, my dear,’ said that gentleman.", "\"이 사람이 새로 온 젊은이요, 여보.\" 그가 말했어."]], [["‘Oh,’ replied Mrs.", "\"아.\""], ["Squeers, nodding her head at Nicholas, and eyeing him coldly from top to toe.", "스퀴어스 부인이 니콜라스를 향해 고개를 까딱하더니, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 차갑게 훑어보며 대답했어."]], [["‘He’ll take a meal with us tonight,’ said Squeers, ‘and go among the boys tomorrow morning.", "\"오늘 밤은 우리랑 한 끼 먹을 거고,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["You can give him a shake-down here, tonight, can’t you?’", "\"내일 아침부터 애들 사이로 들어갈 거요. 오늘 밤은 여기서 대충 잠자리를 봐 줄 수 있겠지?\""]], [["‘We must manage it somehow,’ replied the lady.", "\"어떻게든 해 봐야죠.\" 부인이 대답했어."], ["‘You don’t much mind how you sleep, I suppose, sir?’", "\"잠자리가 어떻든 별로 안 따지시죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["No, indeed,’ replied Nicholas, ‘I am not particular.’", "\"네, 그럼요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"저는 까다롭지 않습니다.\""]], [["‘That’s lucky,’ said Mrs.", "\"그거 잘됐네요.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말했어."], ["Squeers. And as the lady’s humour was considered to lie chiefly in retort, Mr.", "이 부인의 재치는 주로 쏘아붙이는 데 있다고들 여겨졌기에, 스퀴어스 씨는 껄껄 웃었어."], ["Squeers laughed heartily, and seemed to expect that Nicholas should do the same.", "그리고 니콜라스도 똑같이 웃기를 바라는 눈치였지."]], [["After some further conversation between the master and mistress relative to the success of Mr.", "주인 내외 사이에 이런저런 대화가 더 오갔어. 스퀴어스 씨의 이번 여행이 얼마나 잘됐는지, 누가 학비를 냈고 누가 안 냈는지에 관한 얘기였지."], ["Squeers’s trip and the people who had paid, and the people who had made default in payment, a young servant girl brought in a Yorkshire pie and some cold beef, which being set upon the table, the boy Smike appeared with a jug of ale.", "그러는 사이에 어린 하녀가 요크셔 파이와 식은 쇠고기를 좀 들고 들어왔어. 그것들이 탁자에 차려지자, 소년 스마이크가 맥주 한 주전자를 들고 나타났지."]], [["Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨는 외투 주머니에서 여러 아이들 앞으로 온 편지며, 자질구레한 서류들을 꺼내고 있었어."], ["Squeers was emptying his great-coat pockets of letters to different boys, and other small documents, which he had brought down in them.", "가져온 것들이었지. 소년은 그 종이들을 불안하고 겁먹은 표정으로 흘끔거렸어."], ["The boy glanced, with an anxious and timid expression, at the papers, as if with a sickly hope that one among them might relate to him.", "그중 하나가 혹시 자기와 관련된 것일지도 모른다는 가냘픈 희망을 품은 것처럼 말이야. 그 눈빛은 무척 아렸어."], ["The look was a very painful one, and went to Nicholas’s heart at once; for it told a long and very sad history.", "니콜라스의 가슴에 단번에 와닿았지. 길고도 아주 슬픈 사연을 들려주는 눈빛이었거든."]], [["It induced him to consider the boy more attentively, and he was surprised to observe the extraordinary mixture of garments which formed his dress. Although he could not have been less than eighteen or nineteen years old, and was tall for that age, he wore a skeleton suit, such as is usually put upon very little boys, and which, though most absurdly short in the arms and legs, was quite wide enough for his attenuated frame. In order that the lower part of his legs might be in perfect keeping with this singular dress, he had a very large pair of boots, originally made for tops, which might have been once worn by some stout farmer, but were now too patched and tattered for a beggar. Heaven knows how long he had been there, but he still wore the same linen which he had first taken down; for, round his neck, was a tattered child’s frill, only half concealed by a coarse, man’s neckerchief. He was lame; and as he feigned to be busy in arranging the table, glanced at the letters with a look so keen, and yet so dispirited and hopeless, that Nicholas could hardly bear to watch him.", "그 눈빛 때문에 니콜라스는 소년을 좀 더 찬찬히 살펴보게 되었어. 그러고는 소년의 옷차림을 이루는 옷가지들이 어찌나 별나게 뒤섞여 있는지 놀라고 말았지. 소년은 적어도 열여덟이나 열아홉은 됐을 게 분명했고, 그 나이치고도 키가 컸어. 그런데도 그는 아주 어린 사내애한테나 입히는 몸에 붙는 옷을 걸치고 있었어. 팔다리가 우스꽝스러울 만큼 짧았지만, 비쩍 마른 그의 몸에는 그래도 충분히 헐렁한 옷이었지. 종아리 아래쪽까지 이 별난 옷차림과 완벽하게 어울리게 하려는 듯, 그는 아주 커다란 장화 한 켤레를 신고 있었어. 원래는 위쪽을 접어 신는 식으로 만든 거였지. 한때 어느 건장한 농부가 신었을 법한 그 장화는, 이제는 너무 기워지고 너덜너덜해서 거지한테도 못 줄 지경이었어. 그가 거기 온 지 얼마나 됐는지는 하늘이나 알겠지만, 그는 여전히 처음 내려올 때 입었던 그 속옷을 그대로 걸치고 있었어. 목둘레에는 너덜너덜한 어린애용 주름 깃이 둘려 있었는데, 거친 어른용 목도리로 절반쯤만 가려져 있었거든. 그는 다리를 절었어. 그리고 탁자를 차리느라 바쁜 척하면서, 그 편지들을 흘끔거렸지. 어찌나 간절하면서도, 또 어찌나 풀 죽고 희망 없는 눈빛이던지, 니콜라스는 차마 그를 지켜볼 수가 없을 지경이었어."]], [["‘What are you bothering about there, Smike?’ cried Mrs.", "\"거기서 뭘 꾸물거려, 스마이크?\" 스퀴어스 부인이 소리쳤어."], ["Squeers; ‘let the things alone, can’t you?’", "\"그것들 좀 그냥 놔두지 못해?\""]], [["‘Eh!’ said Squeers, looking up.", "\"응?\" 스퀴어스가 고개를 들며 말했어."], ["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["it’s you, is it?’", "너냐?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir,’ replied the youth, pressing his hands together, as though to control, by force, the nervous wandering of his fingers.", "\"네.\" 소년이 두 손을 맞잡으며 대답했어."], ["‘Is there--’", "떨리는 손가락이 어쩔 줄 모르고 움직이는 걸 억지로 누르려는 듯이 말이야. \"혹시…\""]], [["‘Well!’ said Squeers.", "\"뭐!\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."]], [["‘Have you--did anybody--has nothing been heard--about me?’", "\"혹시… 누가… 아무 소식도 없었나요… 제 얘기요?\""]], [["‘Devil a bit,’ replied Squeers testily.", "\"코빼기도 없다.\" 스퀴어스가 짜증스럽게 대답했어."]], [["The lad withdrew his eyes, and, putting his hand to his face, moved towards the door.", "소년은 눈길을 거두고, 한 손을 얼굴에 갖다 댄 채 문 쪽으로 움직였어."]], [["‘Not a word,’ resumed Squeers, ‘and never will be.", "\"한마디도 없어.\" 스퀴어스가 말을 이었어."], ["Now, this is a pretty sort of thing, isn’t it, that you should have been left here, all these years, and no money paid after the first six--nor no notice taken, nor no clue to be got who you belong to?", "\"앞으로도 없을 거고. 자, 이게 참 꼴좋은 일 아니냐?"], ["It’s a pretty sort of thing that I should have to feed a great fellow like you, and never hope to get one penny for it, isn’t it?’", "그 오랜 세월 너를 여기 처박아 뒀는데, 처음 반년 치 빼곤 돈 한 푼 안 들어오고, 아무도 신경 안 쓰고, 네가 누구네 자식인지 알아낼 실마리 하나 없으니 말이야. 너 같은 큰 놈을 먹여 살려야 하는데, 그러고도 한 푼도 못 받을 게 뻔하니, 이거 참 꼴좋지 않으냐?\""]], [["The boy put his hand to his head as if he were making an effort to recollect something, and then, looking vacantly at his questioner, gradually broke into a smile, and limped away.", "소년은 무언가 떠올리려고 애쓰는 듯 손을 머리에 갖다 댔어. 그러고는 멍하니 묻는 이를 바라보다가, 차츰 미소를 짓더니 절뚝거리며 가 버렸지."]], [["‘I’ll tell you what, Squeers,’ remarked his wife as the door closed, ‘I think that young chap’s turning silly.’", "\"내가 하는 말인데, 스퀴어스.\" 문이 닫히자 부인이 말했어. \"저 녀석 점점 머리가 이상해지는 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘I hope not,’ said the schoolmaster; ‘for he’s a handy fellow out of doors, and worth his meat and drink, anyway.", "\"아니길 바라야지.\" 선생이 말했어."], ["I should think he’d have wit enough for us though, if he was.", "\"바깥일에 손이 야무지고, 어쨌든 밥값은 하는 놈이니까. 머리가 돌았어도 우리한테 써먹을 만큼은 영리할 거야."], ["But come; let’s have supper, for I am hungry and tired, and want to get to bed.’", "자, 어서 저녁이나 먹자고. 배도 고프고 피곤해서 빨리 자야겠어.\""]], [["This reminder brought in an exclusive steak for Mr.", "이 말에 스퀴어스 씨만을 위한 특별한 스테이크가 들어왔어."], ["Squeers, who speedily proceeded to do it ample justice.", "그는 곧장 그걸 아주 알뜰하게 해치우기 시작했지."], ["Nicholas drew up his chair, but his appetite was effectually taken away.", "니콜라스도 의자를 끌어당겼지만, 입맛은 싹 가셔 버린 뒤였어."]], [["‘How’s the steak, Squeers?’ said Mrs.", "\"스테이크 어때요, 스퀴어스?\""], ["S.", "스퀴어스 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘Tender as a lamb,’ replied Squeers.", "\"양고기처럼 연하군.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."], ["‘Have a bit.’", "\"당신도 좀 들어.\""]], [["‘I couldn’t eat a morsel,’ replied his wife.", "\"난 한 입도 못 먹겠어요.\" 부인이 대답했어."], ["‘What’ll the young man take, my dear?’", "\"이 젊은이는 뭘 먹겠대요, 여보?\""]], [["‘Whatever he likes that’s present,’ rejoined Squeers, in a most unusual burst of generosity.", "\"여기 있는 거 중에 뭐든 좋아하는 걸로.\" 스퀴어스가 아주 드물게 인심을 팍 쓰며 대답했어."]], [["‘What do you say, Mr.", "\"뭘 드시겠어요, 너클보이 씨?\""], ["Knuckleboy?’ inquired Mrs. Squeers.", "스퀴어스 부인이 물었어."]], [["‘I’ll take a little of the pie, if you please,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"괜찮으시면 파이를 좀 먹겠습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘A very little, for I’m not hungry.’", "\"아주 조금만요. 배가 별로 안 고파서요.\""]], [["Well, it’s a pity to cut the pie if you’re not hungry, isn’t it?’ said Mrs.", "\"뭐, 배도 안 고픈데 파이를 자르기는 아깝잖아요, 안 그래요?\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말했어."], ["Squeers. ‘Will you try a bit of the beef?’", "\"쇠고기를 좀 들어 볼래요?\""]], [["‘Whatever you please,’ replied Nicholas abstractedly; ‘it’s all the same to me.’", "\"주시는 대로 먹겠습니다.\" 니콜라스가 멍하니 대답했어. \"저는 아무거나 다 괜찮습니다.\""]], [["Mrs.", "이 대답을 듣자 스퀴어스 부인은 더없이 너그러운 표정이 되었어. 그리고 스퀴어스를 향해 고개를 끄덕였지."], ["Squeers looked vastly gracious on receiving this reply; and nodding to Squeers, as much as to say that she was glad to find the young man knew his station, assisted Nicholas to a slice of meat with her own fair hands.", "이 젊은이가 제 분수를 아는 걸 알게 되어 흐뭇하다는 듯이 말이야. 그러고는 제 고운 손수 니콜라스에게 고기 한 점을 덜어 주었어."]], [["‘Ale, Squeery?’ inquired the lady, winking and frowning to give him to understand that the question propounded, was, whether Nicholas should have ale, and not whether he (Squeers) would take any.", "\"맥주 줄까요, 스퀴어 자기?\" 부인이 물었어. 눈을 찡긋하고 인상을 찌푸려서, 그 질문이 니콜라스에게 맥주를 줄지 말지를 묻는 거지, 그(스퀴어스)가 맥주를 마실지 묻는 게 아니라는 걸 알리면서 말이야."]], [["‘Certainly,’ said Squeers, re-telegraphing in the same manner.", "\"그럼.\" 스퀴어스가 똑같이 신호를 되보내며 말했어."], ["‘A glassful.’", "\"한 잔 가득.\""]], [["So Nicholas had a glassful, and being occupied with his own reflections, drank it, in happy innocence of all the foregone proceedings.", "그래서 니콜라스는 한 잔 가득 받았어. 그러고는 제 생각에 빠진 채로, 앞서 오간 이 모든 수작을 까맣게 모른 채 천진하게 그걸 마셨지."]], [["‘Uncommon juicy steak that,’ said Squeers, as he laid down his knife and fork, after plying it, in silence, for some time.", "\"이 스테이크 보기 드물게 육즙이 좋군.\" 한동안 말없이 칼질을 부지런히 하던 스퀴어스가 칼과 포크를 내려놓으며 말했어."]], [["‘It’s prime meat,’ rejoined his lady.", "\"최상급 고기죠.\" 부인이 맞장구쳤어."], ["‘I bought a good large piece of it myself on purpose for--’", "\"내가 일부러 큼지막한 걸로 한 덩어리 샀거든요, 누구 주려고…\""]], [["‘For what!’ exclaimed Squeers hastily.", "\"누구 주려고!\" 스퀴어스가 황급히 외쳤어."], ["‘Not for the--’", "\"설마 그…\""]], [["‘No, no; not for them,’ rejoined Mrs.", "\"아니, 아니에요. 그 애들 주려는 게 아니라.\""], ["Squeers; ‘on purpose for you against you came home.", "스퀴어스 부인이 받아쳤어. \"당신 집에 돌아올 때 먹이려고 일부러 산 거예요."], ["Lor!", "어머!"], ["you didn’t think I could have made such a mistake as that.’", "내가 그런 실수를 했을 거라고 생각한 거예요?\""]], [["‘Upon my word, my dear, I didn’t know what you were going to say,’ said Squeers, who had turned pale.", "\"이런, 여보, 난 당신이 무슨 말을 하려는지 몰랐지.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. 그새 얼굴이 새파래져 있었지."]], [["‘You needn’t make yourself uncomfortable,’ remarked his wife, laughing heartily.", "\"마음 졸일 거 없어요.\" 부인이 껄껄 웃으며 말했어."], ["‘To think that I should be such a noddy!", "\"내가 그런 멍청이일 거라니!"], ["Well!’", "참!\""]], [["This part of the conversation was rather unintelligible; but popular rumour in the neighbourhood asserted that Mr.", "이 대목의 대화는 좀 알아듣기 어려웠어. 그런데 동네에 떠도는 소문에 따르면 이런 거야."], ["Squeers, being amiably opposed to cruelty to animals, not unfrequently purchased for boy consumption the bodies of horned cattle who had died a natural death; possibly he was apprehensive of having unintentionally devoured some choice morsel intended for the young gentlemen.", "스퀴어스 씨는 동물 학대에 곱게 반대하는 마음씨 좋은 사람이라, 제 명에 죽은 뿔 달린 짐승의 고기를 사다가 아이들한테 먹이는 일이 적잖이 있었다는 거지. 아마 그는 어린 신사분들 몫으로 마련해 둔 어느 맛난 고깃점을 자기도 모르게 먹어 치웠을까 봐 겁이 났던 모양이야."]], [["Supper being over, and removed by a small servant girl with a hungry eye, Mrs.", "저녁 식사가 끝나고, 배고픈 눈을 한 작은 하녀가 그릇을 치웠어. 스퀴어스 부인은 그걸 자물쇠 채워 두러, 그리고 방금 도착한 다섯 아이들의 옷도 안전하게 거둬 두러 자리를 떴지."], ["Squeers retired to lock it up, and also to take into safe custody the clothes of the five boys who had just arrived, and who were half-way up the troublesome flight of steps which leads to death’s door, in consequence of exposure to the cold.", "그 아이들은 추위에 시달린 탓에, 죽음의 문턱으로 이어지는 그 험한 계단을 절반쯤 올라가 있는 상태였어. 그러고는 멀건 죽으로 가벼운 저녁을 대접받고, 작은 침대 하나에 나란히 뉘어졌지."], ["They were then regaled with a light supper of porridge, and stowed away, side by side, in a small bedstead, to warm each other, and dream of a substantial meal with something hot after it, if their fancies set that way: which it is not at all improbable they did.", "서로 몸을 녹이며, 마음 내키면 든든한 한 끼와 그 뒤에 나오는 따뜻한 무언가를 꿈꾸라는 거였어. 그리고 정말로 그런 꿈을 꿨을 가능성도 아주 없진 않았지."]], [["Mr. Squeers treated himself to a stiff tumbler of brandy and water, made on the liberal half-and-half principle, allowing for the dissolution of the sugar; and his amiable helpmate mixed Nicholas the ghost of a small glassful of the same compound.", "스퀴어스 씨는 자기를 위해 브랜디에 물 탄 독한 한 잔을 만들었어."], ["This done, Mr.", "설탕 녹는 양까지 감안해서 반반씩 후하게 탄 거였지. 그리고 그의 다정한 반려는 니콜라스한테 같은 걸로 자그마한 한 잔의 그림자쯤 되게 타 줬어."], ["and Mrs.", "이렇게 하고 나서, 스퀴어스 부부는 불가로 바싹 다가앉았어. 발을 난로 망에 올린 채로, 머리를 맞대고 소곤소곤 은밀히 이야기를 나눴지."], ["Squeers drew close up to the fire, and sitting with their feet on the fender, talked confidentially in whispers; while Nicholas, taking up the tutor’s assistant, read the interesting legends in the miscellaneous questions, and all the figures into the bargain, with as much thought or consciousness of what he was doing, as if he had been in a magnetic slumber.", "그러는 동안 니콜라스는 가정교사용 참고서를 집어 들고, 잡다한 문제들 속의 흥미로운 이야기며 거기에 딸린 숫자들까지 죄다 읽었어. 그런데 자기가 뭘 하고 있는지 거의 생각도 의식도 없이, 마치 최면에 걸려 잠든 사람처럼 읽고 있었지."]], [["At length, Mr. Squeers yawned fearfully, and opined that it was high time to go to bed; upon which signal, Mrs.", "마침내 스퀴어스 씨는 무시무시하게 하품을 하더니, 이제 잘 시간이 됐다고 했어."], ["Squeers and the girl dragged in a small straw mattress and a couple of blankets, and arranged them into a couch for Nicholas.", "이 신호에 스퀴어스 부인과 하녀가 작은 짚 매트리스 하나와 담요 두 장을 끌고 들어와, 니콜라스를 위한 잠자리로 펴 주었지."]], [["‘We’ll put you into your regular bedroom tomorrow, Nickelby,’ said Squeers.", "\"내일은 자네 정식 침실에 넣어 주겠네, 니클비.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘Let me see!", "\"어디 보자!"], ["Who sleeps in Brooks’s bed, my dear?’", "브룩스 침대에는 누가 자지, 여보?\""]], [["‘In Brooks’s,’ said Mrs.", "\"브룩스 침대요?\""], ["Squeers, pondering.", "스퀴어스 부인이 곰곰 생각하며 말했어."], ["‘There’s Jennings, little Bolder, Graymarsh, and what’s his name.’", "\"제닝스, 어린 볼더, 그레이마시, 그리고 그 이름이 뭐더라.\""]], [["‘So there is,’ rejoined Squeers.", "\"맞아, 그렇지.\" 스퀴어스가 맞장구쳤어."], ["‘Yes!", "\"그래!"], ["Brooks is full.’", "브룩스 침대는 꽉 찼군.\""]], [["‘Full!’ thought Nicholas.", "'꽉 찼다고!'"], ["‘I should think he was.’", "니콜라스는 속으로 생각했어. '그렇겠지.'"]], [["‘There’s a place somewhere, I know,’ said Squeers; ‘but I can’t at this moment call to mind where it is.", "\"어딘가 자리가 하나 있긴 한데,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["However, we’ll have that all settled tomorrow.", "\"지금 당장은 그게 어딘지 생각이 안 나는군. 어쨌든 그건 내일 다 정리하면 돼."], ["Good-night, Nickleby.", "잘 자게, 니클비."], ["Seven o’clock in the morning, mind.’", "아침 일곱 시야, 명심하고.\""]], [["‘I shall be ready, sir,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"준비하고 있겠습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘Good-night.’", "\"안녕히 주무세요.\""]], [["‘I’ll come in myself and show you where the well is,’ said Squeers.", "\"내가 직접 와서 우물이 어딘지 알려 줌세.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘You’ll always find a little bit of soap in the kitchen window; that belongs to you.’", "\"부엌 창턱에 비누가 늘 조그맣게 있을 거야. 그게 자네 거야.\""]], [["Nicholas opened his eyes, but not his mouth; and Squeers was again going away, when he once more turned back.", "니콜라스는 눈은 휘둥그레졌지만 입은 열지 않았어. 스퀴어스가 다시 나가려다가, 또 한 번 뒤를 돌아봤지."]], [["‘I don’t know, I am sure,’ he said, ‘whose towel to put you on; but if you’ll make shift with something tomorrow morning, Mrs.", "\"자네한테 무슨 수건을 줄지는 나도 잘 모르겠네.\" 그가 말했어."], ["Squeers will arrange that, in the course of the day.", "\"그래도 내일 아침엔 아무거나로 대충 해결하면, 스퀴어스 부인이 낮 동안에 그건 마련해 줄 거야."], ["My dear, don’t forget.’", "여보, 잊지 말아요.\""]], [["‘I’ll take care,’ replied Mrs.", "\"내가 챙길게요.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 대답했어."], ["Squeers; ‘and mind YOU take care, young man, and get first wash.", "\"그리고 젊은이, 당신도 잘 챙겨서 제일 먼저 씻으라고."], ["The teacher ought always to have it; but they get the better of him if they can.’", "선생은 늘 먼저 씻어야 하는데, 그것들이 틈만 나면 선수를 친다니까.\""]], [["Mr. Squeers then nudged Mrs.", "그러고 나서 스퀴어스 씨는 브랜디 병을 치우라고 스퀴어스 부인을 슬쩍 찔렀어."], ["Squeers to bring away the brandy bottle, lest Nicholas should help himself in the night; and the lady having seized it with great precipitation, they retired together.", "니콜라스가 밤중에 멋대로 따라 마실까 봐서였지. 부인이 부랴부랴 그걸 낚아챘고, 둘은 함께 방을 나갔어."]], [["Nicholas, being left alone, took half-a-dozen turns up and down the room in a condition of much agitation and excitement; but, growing gradually calmer, sat himself down in a chair, and mentally resolved that, come what come might, he would endeavour, for a time, to bear whatever wretchedness might be in store for him, and that remembering the helplessness of his mother and sister, he would give his uncle no plea for deserting them in their need. Good resolutions seldom fail of producing some good effect in the mind from which they spring. He grew less desponding, and--so sanguine and buoyant is youth--even hoped that affairs at Dotheboys Hall might yet prove better than they promised.", "혼자 남겨진 니콜라스는 몹시 동요하고 흥분한 상태로 방 안을 대여섯 번 왔다 갔다 했어. 그러다 차츰 마음이 가라앉자, 의자에 앉아 마음속으로 다짐했지. 무슨 일이 닥치든, 한동안은 자기 앞에 놓인 어떤 비참함이라도 견뎌 보겠다고 말이야. 그리고 어머니와 누이의 처지가 얼마나 막막한지를 떠올리며, 삼촌에게 그들을 곤경 속에 내버려 둘 빌미를 주지 않겠다고 다짐했어. 좋은 결심은 그것이 우러난 마음에 얼마간 좋은 효과를 내기 마련이지. 그는 덜 우울해졌어. 그리고 젊음이란 워낙 희망차고 활기찬 것이라, 두더보이즈 홀의 사정이 보이는 것보다는 그래도 나을지 모른다고 기대하기까지 했지."]], [["He was preparing for bed, with something like renewed cheerfulness, when a sealed letter fell from his coat pocket.", "그가 새삼 다시 기운을 차린 듯한 기분으로 잘 채비를 하고 있는데, 봉인된 편지 한 통이 외투 주머니에서 툭 떨어졌어."], ["In the hurry of leaving London, it had escaped his attention, and had not occurred to him since, but it at once brought back to him the recollection of the mysterious behaviour of Newman Noggs.", "런던을 떠나느라 정신없는 통에 미처 챙기지 못했고, 그 뒤로도 생각이 안 났던 거지. 그런데 그게 단번에 그에게 뉴먼 노그스의 수상쩍던 행동을 떠올리게 했어."]], [["‘Dear me!’ said Nicholas; ‘what an extraordinary hand!’", "\"이런!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"글씨가 어쩜 이렇게 별나담!\""]], [["It was directed to himself, was written upon very dirty paper, and in such cramped and crippled writing as to be almost illegible.", "편지는 그 자신 앞으로 온 것이었고, 아주 더러운 종이에 쓰여 있었어. 게다가 글씨가 어찌나 비비 꼬이고 비틀어졌는지 거의 알아볼 수가 없을 지경이었지."], ["After great difficulty and much puzzling, he contrived to read as follows:--", "한참을 끙끙대고 갖은 애를 쓴 끝에, 그는 가까스로 이렇게 읽어 냈어."]], [["My dear young Man.", "친애하는 젊은이에게."]], [["I know the world.", "나는 세상을 압니다."], ["Your father did not, or he would not have done me a kindness when there was no hope of return.", "자네 아버지는 몰랐지. 그랬더라면 보답받을 가망이 전혀 없는데도 나한테 친절을 베풀지 않았을 테니까."], ["You do not, or you would not be bound on such a journey.", "자네도 세상을 모르네. 그랬더라면 이런 여행길에 오르지 않았을 테니까."]], [["If ever you want a shelter in London (don’t be angry at this, I once thought I never should), they know where I live, at the sign of the Crown, in Silver Street, Golden Square.", "혹시 런던에서 몸 둘 곳이 필요하면 (이런 말에 화내지 말게, 나도 한때는 그런 날이 절대 안 올 줄 알았으니까), 내가 사는 데를 그들이 알 거야. 골든 스퀘어의 실버 스트리트에 있는 크라운 간판을 단 집일세."], ["It is at the corner of Silver Street and James Street, with a bar door both ways.", "실버 스트리트와 제임스 스트리트가 만나는 모퉁이고, 양쪽으로 다 술집 문이 나 있어."], ["You can come at night.", "밤에 와도 돼."], ["Once, nobody was ashamed--never mind that.", "한때는 아무도 부끄러워하지 않았지. 그건 됐고."], ["It’s all over.", "다 지난 일이니까."]], [["Excuse errors.", "틀린 데가 있어도 봐주게."], ["I should forget how to wear a whole coat now.", "나는 이제 멀쩡한 외투를 어떻게 입는지조차 잊어버렸을 거야."], ["I have forgotten all my old ways.", "옛날 방식은 죄다 잊어버렸거든."], ["My spelling may have gone with them.", "철자법도 그것들이랑 같이 잊어버렸을지 모르겠네."]], [["# NEWMAN NOGGS.", "# NEWMAN NOGGS."]], [["P.S.", "뉴먼 노그스."], ["If you should go near Barnard Castle, there is good ale at the King’s Head.", "추신. 혹시 바너드 캐슬 근처에 가게 되면, 킹스 헤드에 좋은 맥주가 있어."], ["Say you know me, and I am sure they will not charge you for it.", "나를 안다고 말하게. 그럼 분명히 자네한테 돈을 안 받을 거야."], ["You may say Mr.", "거기선 노그스 씨라고 해도 돼."], ["Noggs there, for I was a gentleman then.", "그땐 내가 신사였으니까."], ["I was indeed.", "정말 그랬다네."]], [["It may be a very undignified circumstances to record, but after he had folded this letter and placed it in his pocket-book, Nicholas Nickleby’s eyes were dimmed with a moisture that might have been taken for tears.", "기록으로 남기기엔 영 체면이 안 서는 일일지도 모르지만, 이 편지를 접어 수첩에 넣고 나서, 니콜라스 니클비의 두 눈은 눈물이라 해도 좋을 만한 물기로 흐려져 있었어."]], [["Of the Internal Economy of Dotheboys Hall\nA ride of two hundred and odd miles in severe weather, is one of the best softeners of a hard bed that ingenuity can devise.", "혹독한 날씨에 이백 몇 마일을 달려온다는 건, 딱딱한 침대를 부드럽게 만드는 데 사람이 궁리해 낼 수 있는 가장 좋은 방법 가운데 하나야."], ["Perhaps it is even a sweetener of dreams, for those which hovered over the rough couch of Nicholas, and whispered their airy nothings in his ear, were of an agreeable and happy kind.", "어쩌면 꿈까지 달콤하게 해 주는지도 몰라. 니콜라스의 거친 잠자리 위를 맴돌며 귀에 대고 속삭이던 그 가벼운 꿈들은 즐겁고 행복한 것들이었거든."], ["He was making his fortune very fast indeed, when the faint glimmer of an expiring candle shone before his eyes, and a voice he had no difficulty in recognising as part and parcel of Mr Squeers, admonished him that it was time to rise.", "그가 정말로 빠르게 한밑천 모으고 있는데, 꺼져 가는 촛불의 희미한 불빛이 눈앞에 어른거렸어. 그리고 스퀴어스 씨의 일부라는 걸 어렵지 않게 알아챌 수 있는 목소리가, 이제 일어날 시간이라고 일러 주었지."]], [["‘Past seven, Nickleby,’ said Mr.", "\"일곱 시가 넘었어, 니클비.\""], ["Squeers.", "스퀴어스 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Has morning come already?’ asked Nicholas, sitting up in bed.", "\"벌써 아침이 됐나요?\" 니콜라스가 침대에서 일어나 앉으며 물었어."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["that has it,’ replied Squeers, ‘and ready iced too.", "됐고말고.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."], ["Now, Nickleby, come; tumble up, will you?’", "\"게다가 얼음처럼 차게 준비됐지. 자, 니클비, 어서, 벌떡 일어나라고, 알겠어?\""]], [["Nicholas needed no further admonition, but ‘tumbled up’ at once, and proceeded to dress himself by the light of the taper, which Mr.", "니콜라스는 더 이상 재촉이 필요 없었어. 곧장 \"벌떡 일어나서\""], ["Squeers carried in his hand.", "스퀴어스 씨가 손에 든 가느다란 촛불 빛에 옷을 챙겨 입기 시작했지."]], [["‘Here’s a pretty go,’ said that gentleman; ‘the pump’s froze.’", "\"이거 참 잘됐군.\" 그 신사가 말했어. \"펌프가 얼어붙었어.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ said Nicholas, not much interested in the intelligence.", "\"그래요?\" 니콜라스가 별로 관심도 없이 말했어."]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Squeers.", "\"그래.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."], ["‘You can’t wash yourself this morning.’", "\"오늘 아침엔 씻을 수가 없겠어.\""]], [["‘Not wash myself!’ exclaimed Nicholas.", "\"못 씻는다고요!\" 니콜라스가 소리쳤어."]], [["‘No, not a bit of it,’ rejoined Squeers tartly.", "\"그래, 한 방울도 못 써.\" 스퀴어스가 쌀쌀맞게 받아쳤어."], ["‘So you must be content with giving yourself a dry polish till we break the ice in the well, and can get a bucketful out for the boys.", "\"그러니 우물의 얼음을 깨서 애들 쓸 물을 한 통 길어 올릴 때까지는, 마른 채로 대충 문지르는 걸로 만족해야 해."], ["Don’t stand staring at me, but do look sharp, will you?’", "멀거니 날 쳐다보지 말고, 좀 서두르라고, 알겠어?\""]], [["Offering no further observation, Nicholas huddled on his clothes.", "니콜라스는 더 이상 토를 달지 않고 옷을 주섬주섬 걸쳤어. 그러는 동안 스퀴어스는 덧창을 열고 촛불을 불어 껐지."], ["Squeers, meanwhile, opened the shutters and blew the candle out; when the voice of his amiable consort was heard in the passage, demanding admittance.", "그때 그의 다정한 아내 목소리가 복도에서 들렸어. 문을 열어 달라는 거였지."]], [["‘Come in, my love,’ said Squeers.", "\"들어와요, 여보.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."]], [["Mrs.", "스퀴어스 부인이 들어왔어."], ["Squeers came in, still habited in the primitive night-jacket which had displayed the symmetry of her figure on the previous night, and further ornamented with a beaver bonnet of some antiquity, which she wore, with much ease and lightness, on the top of the nightcap before mentioned.", "전날 밤 그 몸매의 균형을 자랑했던 그 투박한 잠옷 윗도리를 여전히 걸친 채였고, 거기다 꽤 오래돼 보이는 비버 가죽 보닛까지 얹고 있었지. 앞에서 말한 그 잠자리용 모자 위에다 아주 가뿐하고 편안하게 쓰고 있었어."]], [["‘Drat the things,’ said the lady, opening the cupboard; ‘I can’t find the school spoon anywhere.’", "\"빌어먹을 물건들.\" 부인이 찬장을 열며 말했어. \"학교 숟가락을 도무지 못 찾겠네.\""]], [["‘Never mind it, my dear,’ observed Squeers in a soothing manner; ‘it’s of no consequence.’", "\"신경 쓰지 마, 여보.\" 스퀴어스가 달래듯 말했어. \"별일 아니야.\""]], [["‘No consequence, why how you talk!’ retorted Mrs.", "\"별일 아니라니, 무슨 그런 말을!\" 스퀴어스 부인이 날카롭게 받아쳤어."], ["Squeers sharply; ‘isn’t it brimstone morning?’", "\"오늘이 유황 먹이는 날이잖아요?\""]], [["‘I forgot, my dear,’ rejoined Squeers; ‘yes, it certainly is.", "\"깜빡했군, 여보.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어."], ["We purify the boys’ bloods now and then, Nickleby.’", "\"그래, 맞아, 그렇지. 우리는 가끔 애들 피를 깨끗하게 해 준단다, 니클비.\""]], [["‘Purify fiddlesticks’ ends,’ said his lady.", "\"피는 무슨 얼어 죽을 피야.\" 그의 아내가 말했어."], ["‘Don’t think, young man, that we go to the expense of flower of brimstone and molasses, just to purify them; because if you think we carry on the business in that way, you’ll find yourself mistaken, and so I tell you plainly.’", "\"젊은 양반, 우리가 그저 애들 피 깨끗하게 해 주자고 유황 가루랑 당밀에 돈을 쓰는 줄은 알지 마요. 우리가 그런 식으로 장사하는 줄 안다면 큰 오산이니까, 똑똑히 일러두는 거예요.\""]], [["‘My dear,’ said Squeers frowning.", "\"여보.\" 스퀴어스가 얼굴을 찌푸리며 말했어."], ["‘Hem!’", "\"흠!\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["nonsense,’ rejoined Mrs. Squeers.", "쓸데없는 소리.\""], ["‘If the young man comes to be a teacher here, let him understand, at once, that we don’t want any foolery about the boys.", "스퀴어스 부인이 받아쳤어. \"이 젊은 양반이 여기서 선생 노릇을 할 거라면, 단번에 알아 둬야지."], ["They have the brimstone and treacle, partly because if they hadn’t something or other in the way of medicine they’d be always ailing and giving a world of trouble, and partly because it spoils their appetites and comes cheaper than breakfast and dinner.", "우리는 애들한테 어리석은 짓 할 생각이 없다는 걸 말이에요. 애들한테 유황이랑 당밀을 먹이는 건, 첫째로 약 같은 걸 뭐라도 안 먹이면 늘 골골거리며 한없이 속을 썩일 테니 그렇고, 둘째로 그걸 먹으면 입맛이 떨어지는데다 아침이랑 저녁밥보다 싸게 먹히니까 그래요."], ["So, it does them good and us good at the same time, and that’s fair enough I’m sure.’", "그러니까 애들한테도 좋고 우리한테도 좋고, 동시에 그런 거지. 이만하면 공평하잖아요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["Having given this explanation, Mrs. Squeers put her head into the closet and instituted a stricter search after the spoon, in which Mr.", "이렇게 설명을 늘어놓고 스퀴어스 부인은 벽장에 머리를 들이밀고 숟가락을 더 샅샅이 뒤지기 시작했어."], ["Squeers assisted. A few words passed between them while they were thus engaged, but as their voices were partially stifled by the cupboard, all that Nicholas could distinguish was, that Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨도 거들었지."], ["Squeers said what Mrs. Squeers had said, was injudicious, and that Mrs.", "그러는 동안 둘 사이에 몇 마디가 오갔는데, 목소리가 찬장에 막혀 어렴풋하게만 들렸어."], ["Squeers said what Mr. Squeers said, was ‘stuff.’", "그래서 니콜라스가 알아들을 수 있는 거라곤, 스퀴어스 씨는 부인이 한 말이 분별없다고 했고, 스퀴어스 부인은 남편이 한 말이 \"헛소리\"라고 했다는 정도였지."]], [["A vast deal of searching and rummaging ensued, and it proving fruitless, Smike was called in, and pushed by Mrs.", "엄청난 뒤짐과 헤집기가 이어졌지만 헛수고로 끝나자, 스마이크가 불려 왔어."], ["Squeers, and boxed by Mr.", "스퀴어스 부인한테 떠밀리고 스퀴어스 씨한테 한 대 쥐어박혔지."], ["Squeers; which course of treatment brightening his intellects, enabled him to suggest that possibly Mrs.", "그런 대접을 받자 머리가 번쩍 든 모양인지, 어쩌면 숟가락이 스퀴어스 부인 주머니에 있을지도 모른다는 의견을 내놓았어. 그리고 정말로 그랬지."], ["Squeers might have the spoon in her pocket, as indeed turned out to be the case. As Mrs.", "그런데 스퀴어스 부인은 아까 그게 자기한테 없다고 분명히 우겼던 터라, 스마이크는 안주인 말에 토를 단 죄로 또 한 대 귀싸대기를 얻어맞았어."], ["Squeers had previously protested, however, that she was quite certain she had not got it, Smike received another box on the ear for presuming to contradict his mistress, together with a promise of a sound thrashing if he were not more respectful in future; so that he took nothing very advantageous by his motion.", "거기다 앞으로 좀 더 공손하게 굴지 않으면 흠씬 두들겨 패겠다는 으름장도 따라왔지. 그러니 자기 의견을 내놓고도 별로 득 볼 게 없었던 셈이야."]], [["‘A most invaluable woman, that, Nickleby,’ said Squeers when his consort had hurried away, pushing the drudge before her.", "\"정말 더없이 소중한 여자야, 저 사람은, 니클비.\" 아내가 그 머슴 같은 아이를 앞세워 떠밀고 서둘러 나가자 스퀴어스가 말했어."]], [["‘Indeed, sir!’ observed Nicholas.", "\"그러시겠지요, 선생님!\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘I don’t know her equal,’ said Squeers; ‘I do not know her equal. That woman, Nickleby, is always the same--always the same bustling, lively, active, saving creetur that you see her now.’", "\"저런 여자는 또 없어.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"저런 사람은 또 없지. 저 여자는 말이야, 니클비, 언제나 똑같아. 늘 지금처럼 부지런하고, 활기차고, 야무지고, 알뜰살뜰한 사람이지.\""]], [["Nicholas sighed involuntarily at the thought of the agreeable domestic prospect thus opened to him; but Squeers was, fortunately, too much occupied with his own reflections to perceive it.", "니콜라스는 그렇게 펼쳐진 즐거운 가정의 앞날을 떠올리며 저도 모르게 한숨을 내쉬었어. 그런데 다행히 스퀴어스는 제 생각에 너무 빠져 있어서 그걸 알아채지 못했지."]], [["‘It’s my way to say, when I am up in London,’ continued Squeers, ‘that to them boys she is a mother. But she is more than a mother to them; ten times more. She does things for them boys, Nickleby, that I don’t believe half the mothers going, would do for their own sons.’", "\"내가 런던에 올라가면 늘 이렇게 말한단다.\" 스퀴어스가 말을 이었어. \"저 사람은 애들한테 어머니 같은 존재라고 말이야. 아니, 어머니 이상이지. 열 배는 더 어머니 같아. 저 여자는 애들한테 이런 일들을 해 준단다, 니클비. 세상 어머니 절반은 제 친아들한테도 안 해 줄 그런 일들을 말이야.\""]], [["‘I should think they would not, sir,’ answered Nicholas.", "\"그렇겠지요, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["Now, the fact was, that both Mr. and Mrs.", "그런데 사실은 이래."], ["Squeers viewed the boys in the light of their proper and natural enemies; or, in other words, they held and considered that their business and profession was to get as much from every boy as could by possibility be screwed out of him.", "스퀴어스 씨와 부인은 둘 다 아이들을 자기네의 당연하고도 천연의 적으로 여겼어. 다시 말해, 두 사람은 자기네 일이자 직업이란 모든 아이한테서 짜낼 수 있는 만큼 최대한 짜내는 거라고 믿고 또 그렇게 여겼지."], ["On this point they were both agreed, and behaved in unison accordingly. The only difference between them was, that Mrs.", "이 점에서 둘은 의견이 딱 맞았고, 거기에 맞춰 한마음으로 움직였어."], ["Squeers waged war against the enemy openly and fearlessly, and that Squeers covered his rascality, even at home, with a spice of his habitual deceit; as if he really had a notion of someday or other being able to take himself in, and persuade his own mind that he was a very good fellow.", "두 사람의 유일한 차이라면, 스퀴어스 부인은 그 적을 향해 드러내 놓고 거리낌 없이 싸움을 벌였고, 스퀴어스는 집 안에서도 제 못된 짓을 늘 하던 버릇대로 약간의 거짓으로 덮었다는 거지. 마치 언젠가는 제 스스로를 속여서, 자기는 참 좋은 사람이라고 제 마음을 구슬릴 수 있을 거라는 생각이라도 정말 품은 것처럼 말이야."]], [["‘But come,’ said Squeers, interrupting the progress of some thoughts to this effect in the mind of his usher, ‘let’s go to the schoolroom; and lend me a hand with my school-coat, will you?’", "\"자, 됐고.\" 스퀴어스는 조교의 머릿속에서 그런 방향으로 흘러가던 생각의 갈래를 끊으며 말했어. \"교실로 가자고. 그리고 내 학교용 외투 입는 것 좀 거들어 줘, 응?\""]], [["Nicholas assisted his master to put on an old fustian shooting-jacket, which he took down from a peg in the passage; and Squeers, arming himself with his cane, led the way across a yard, to a door in the rear of the house.", "니콜라스는 주인이 외투 입는 걸 도왔어. 복도 못에 걸려 있던 걸 스퀴어스가 내린, 낡은 퍼스티언 사냥 재킷이었지. 그리고 스퀴어스는 회초리로 무장하더니, 앞장서서 마당을 가로질러 집 뒤쪽 문으로 갔어."]], [["‘There,’ said the schoolmaster as they stepped in together; ‘this is our shop, Nickleby!’", "\"자.\" 둘이 함께 안으로 들어서자 교장이 말했어. \"이게 우리 가게야, 니클비!\""]], [["It was such a crowded scene, and there were so many objects to attract attention, that, at first, Nicholas stared about him, really without seeing anything at all.", "어찌나 북적이는 광경인지, 또 눈길을 끄는 게 어찌나 많은지, 니콜라스는 처음엔 그저 멀거니 둘러보기만 했어. 사실 아무것도 제대로 보지 못한 채로 말이야."], ["By degrees, however, the place resolved itself into a bare and dirty room, with a couple of windows, whereof a tenth part might be of glass, the remainder being stopped up with old copy-books and paper.", "그러다 차츰 그곳의 모습이 또렷해졌지. 휑하고 지저분한 방이었고, 창문이 두어 개 있었는데, 그중 십분의 일쯤이나 유리일까, 나머지는 죄다 헌 공책이며 종이로 틀어막혀 있었어."], ["There were a couple of long old rickety desks, cut and notched, and inked, and damaged, in every possible way; two or three forms; a detached desk for Squeers; and another for his assistant.", "길고 낡은 책상이 두어 개 있었는데, 베이고 파이고 잉크가 묻고 온갖 식으로 망가져 있었지. 긴 의자도 두세 개 있었고, 스퀴어스용 책상이 따로 하나, 조교용 책상이 또 하나 있었어."], ["The ceiling was supported, like that of a barn, by cross-beams and rafters; and the walls were so stained and discoloured, that it was impossible to tell whether they had ever been touched with paint or whitewash.", "천장은 헛간처럼 들보와 서까래로 떠받쳐져 있었고, 벽은 어찌나 얼룩지고 색이 바랬는지, 페인트나 회칠을 한 번이라도 한 적이 있는지조차 알 수 없을 지경이었어."]], [["But the pupils--the young noblemen!", "그런데 그 학생들이라니, 그 어린 귀공자들이라니! 이 소굴에서 자기가 애쓴들 무슨 좋은 결과가 나올까 하던 마지막 희미한 희망의 자취, 가장 아득한 한 줄기 기대마저 어떻게 사라져 버렸는지 몰라."], ["How the last faint traces of hope, the remotest glimmering of any good to be derived from his efforts in this den, faded from the mind of Nicholas as he looked in dismay around!", "니콜라스가 망연자실해서 둘러보는 순간 말이야. 창백하고 수척한 얼굴들, 비쩍 마르고 뼈만 앙상한 몸들, 늙은이 같은 표정을 한 아이들, 팔다리에 쇠붙이를 댄 불구의 아이들, 자라다 만 아이들, 길고 깡마른 다리가 구부정한 몸뚱이를 간신히 지탱하는 아이들이 한꺼번에 눈앞에 몰려들었어."], ["Pale and haggard faces, lank and bony figures, children with the countenances of old men, deformities with irons upon their limbs, boys of stunted growth, and others whose long meagre legs would hardly bear their stooping bodies, all crowded on the view together; there were the bleared eye, the hare-lip, the crooked foot, and every ugliness or distortion that told of unnatural aversion conceived by parents for their offspring, or of young lives which, from the earliest dawn of infancy, had been one horrible endurance of cruelty and neglect.", "짓무른 눈, 언청이, 굽은 발, 그리고 부모가 제 자식한테 천륜을 거스르는 혐오를 품었음을 말해 주거나, 어린 목숨이 갓난아이 때부터 잔인함과 방치를 끔찍하게 견뎌 온 것임을 말해 주는 온갖 추함과 뒤틀림이 거기 있었지. 본래는 잘생겼을 작은 얼굴들이 부루퉁하고 고집스러운 고통의 찡그림으로 어두워져 있었어."], ["There were little faces which should have been handsome, darkened with the scowl of sullen, dogged suffering; there was childhood with the light of its eye quenched, its beauty gone, and its helplessness alone remaining; there were vicious-faced boys, brooding, with leaden eyes, like malefactors in a jail; and there were young creatures on whom the sins of their frail parents had descended, weeping even for the mercenary nurses they had known, and lonesome even in their loneliness.", "눈빛은 꺼지고 고움은 사라지고 무력함만 남은 어린 시절이 거기 있었지. 감옥의 죄수처럼 납빛 눈으로 음울하게 곱씹는, 험상궂은 얼굴의 아이들도 있었어."], ["With every kindly sympathy and affection blasted in its birth, with every young and healthy feeling flogged and starved down, with every revengeful passion that can fester in swollen hearts, eating its evil way to their core in silence, what an incipient Hell was breeding here!", "그리고 못난 부모의 죄를 물려받은 어린것들도 있었는데, 돈 받고 길러 준 유모를 그리워하며 울고, 외로움 속에서조차 외로워했지. 모든 다정한 정과 사랑이 싹트는 순간 짓밟히고, 모든 어리고 건강한 감정이 매질당하고 굶주려 꺾이고, 부어오른 가슴 속에서 곪을 수 있는 온갖 복수심이 말없이 그 마음 한가운데로 악한 길을 파먹어 들어가니, 여기서 얼마나 끔찍한 지옥의 씨앗이 자라나고 있었던지!"]], [["And yet this scene, painful as it was, had its grotesque features, which, in a less interested observer than Nicholas, might have provoked a smile. Mrs. Squeers stood at one of the desks, presiding over an immense basin of brimstone and treacle, of which delicious compound she administered a large instalment to each boy in succession: using for the purpose a common wooden spoon, which might have been originally manufactured for some gigantic top, and which widened every young gentleman’s mouth considerably: they being all obliged, under heavy corporal penalties, to take in the whole of the bowl at a gasp. In another corner, huddled together for companionship, were the little boys who had arrived on the preceding night, three of them in very large leather breeches, and two in old trousers, a something tighter fit than drawers are usually worn; at no great distance from these was seated the juvenile son and heir of Mr. Squeers--a striking likeness of his father--kicking, with great vigour, under the hands of Smike, who was fitting upon him a pair of new boots that bore a most suspicious resemblance to those which the least of the little boys had worn on the journey down--as the little boy himself seemed to think, for he was regarding the appropriation with a look of most rueful amazement. Besides these, there was a long row of boys waiting, with countenances of no pleasant anticipation, to be treacled; and another file, who had just escaped from the infliction, making a variety of wry mouths indicative of anything but satisfaction. The whole were attired in such motley, ill-assorted, extraordinary garments, as would have been irresistibly ridiculous, but for the foul appearance of dirt, disorder, and disease, with which they were associated.", "그래도 이 광경은, 괴롭긴 했지만, 어딘가 우스꽝스러운 구석도 있었어. 니콜라스만큼 마음이 쓰이지 않는 구경꾼이었다면 픽 웃음이 나올 만한 그런 면 말이야. 스퀴어스 부인이 책상 하나 앞에 서서, 어마어마하게 큰 대야에 담긴 유황과 당밀을 떡 맡고 있었어. 그 맛깔난 혼합물을 아이들한테 한 명씩 차례로 큼지막하게 한 술씩 떠 먹였지. 그러느라 쓰는 게 흔해 빠진 나무 숟가락이었는데, 원래는 무슨 거대한 팽이라도 만들려고 깎은 게 아닐까 싶을 만큼 컸어. 그래서 어린 신사들마다 입이 한참씩 벌어졌지. 다들 무거운 매질을 면하려면 한 술 가득을 단숨에 삼켜야 했거든. 또 다른 구석에는, 함께 모여 서로 의지하려고 옹기종기 붙어 앉은 어린아이들이 있었어. 전날 밤 도착한 아이들이었지. 그중 셋은 아주 큼지막한 가죽 반바지를 입었고, 둘은 헌 바지를 입었는데, 보통 속바지보다도 좀 더 꽉 끼는 거였어. 거기서 멀지 않은 데에는 스퀴어스 씨의 어린 아들이자 상속자가 앉아 있었어. 제 아비를 쏙 빼닮은 아이였지. 그 애가 스마이크의 손길 아래서 힘껏 발길질을 해 대고 있었는데, 스마이크가 그 애한테 새 장화를 신겨 주고 있었거든. 그런데 그 장화가, 내려오는 길에 어린아이들 중 제일 작은 애가 신었던 것이랑 어쩐지 영 수상쩍게 닮아 있었어. 그 어린아이 본인도 그렇게 생각하는 듯했지. 그게 가로채여 가는 걸 더없이 처량하고 어이없다는 눈빛으로 바라보고 있었거든. 이들 말고도, 길게 줄지어 차례를 기다리는 아이들이 있었어. 당밀을 받아먹을 차례인데, 도무지 달갑지 않은 표정들이었지. 또 한 줄은 방금 그 고역에서 벗어난 아이들이었는데, 만족과는 거리가 먼, 온갖 일그러진 입 모양을 짓고 있었어. 죄다 어찌나 잡다하고 안 어울리고 별스러운 옷을 걸쳤는지, 도저히 웃음을 참을 수 없을 지경이었어. 거기 따라붙은 더러움과 어수선함과 병색의 추한 몰골만 아니었더라면 말이지."]], [["‘Now,’ said Squeers, giving the desk a great rap with his cane, which made half the little boys nearly jump out of their boots, ‘is that physicking over?’", "\"자.\" 스퀴어스가 회초리로 책상을 쾅 내리치자, 어린아이들 절반이 화들짝 놀라 장화에서 튀어나올 뻔했어. \"약 먹이는 건 다 끝났나?\""]], [["‘Just over,’ said Mrs.", "\"방금 끝났어요.\""], ["Squeers, choking the last boy in her hurry, and tapping the crown of his head with the wooden spoon to restore him.", "스퀴어스 부인이 서두르다가 마지막 아이를 사레들리게 하고는, 나무 숟가락으로 그 애 정수리를 톡톡 두드려 정신 차리게 하며 말했어."], ["‘Here, you Smike; take away now.", "\"자, 스마이크, 이제 치워."], ["Look sharp!’", "서둘러!\""]], [["Smike shuffled out with the basin, and Mrs.", "스마이크가 대야를 들고 질질 끌듯이 나갔어."], ["Squeers having called up a little boy with a curly head, and wiped her hands upon it, hurried out after him into a species of wash-house, where there was a small fire and a large kettle, together with a number of little wooden bowls which were arranged upon a board.", "스퀴어스 부인은 곱슬머리 어린아이 하나를 불러다 거기에 손을 닦고는, 서둘러 그 애를 따라 일종의 세탁장 같은 데로 나갔어. 거기엔 작은 불이 피워져 있고 커다란 솥이 하나 있었고, 자그마한 나무 그릇들이 여러 개 판자 위에 죽 놓여 있었지."]], [["Into these bowls, Mrs.", "스퀴어스 부인은 굶주린 하인의 도움을 받아 이 그릇들에다 갈색 죽 같은 걸 부었어."], ["Squeers, assisted by the hungry servant, poured a brown composition, which looked like diluted pincushions without the covers, and was called porridge.", "겉을 벗겨 낸 바늘꽂이를 물에 탄 것처럼 생긴 거였는데, 죽이라고 불렀지. 그릇마다 갈색 빵을 작은 쐐기처럼 한 조각씩 꽂아 넣었어."], ["A minute wedge of brown bread was inserted in each bowl, and when they had eaten their porridge by means of the bread, the boys ate the bread itself, and had finished their breakfast; whereupon Mr.", "아이들은 그 빵으로 죽을 떠먹고, 다 떠먹은 다음엔 빵 자체를 먹어 치우는 걸로 아침 식사를 끝냈어. 그러자 스퀴어스 씨가 엄숙한 목소리로 말했지."], ["Squeers said, in a solemn voice, ‘For what we have received, may the Lord make us truly thankful!’--and went away to his own.", "\"우리가 받은 양식에 대해, 주께서 우리로 진실로 감사하게 하소서!\" 그러고는 자기 식사를 하러 가 버렸어."]], [["Nicholas distended his stomach with a bowl of porridge, for much the same reason which induces some savages to swallow earth--lest they should be inconveniently hungry when there is nothing to eat.", "니콜라스는 죽 한 그릇으로 배를 채웠어. 어떤 미개인들이 흙을 삼키는 것과 비슷한 이유에서였지."], ["Having further disposed of a slice of bread and butter, allotted to him in virtue of his office, he sat himself down, to wait for school-time.", "먹을 게 아무것도 없을 때 거북하게 배고프지 않으려고 말이야. 거기다 제 자리 덕에 받은 버터 바른 빵 한 조각까지 마저 해치우고는, 자리에 앉아 수업 시간을 기다렸어."]], [["He could not but observe how silent and sad the boys all seemed to be.", "아이들이 모두 어찌나 조용하고 슬퍼 보이는지 눈에 안 띌 수가 없었어."], ["There was none of the noise and clamour of a schoolroom; none of its boisterous play, or hearty mirth.", "교실의 떠들썩한 소음도, 와글거리는 소리도 없었지. 시끌벅적한 장난도, 신나는 웃음소리도 없었어."], ["The children sat crouching and shivering together, and seemed to lack the spirit to move about.", "아이들은 옹기종기 웅크리고 함께 떨면서, 돌아다닐 기운조차 없어 보였어."], ["The only pupil who evinced the slightest tendency towards locomotion or playfulness was Master Squeers, and as his chief amusement was to tread upon the other boys’ toes in his new boots, his flow of spirits was rather disagreeable than otherwise.", "조금이라도 움직이거나 장난칠 기색을 보이는 학생이라곤 스퀴어스 도련님 하나뿐이었지. 그런데 그 애의 주된 재미라는 게 새 장화로 다른 아이들 발가락을 밟는 거였으니, 그 넘쳐 나는 기운은 유쾌하기보다 오히려 거슬렸어."]], [["After some half-hour’s delay, Mr.", "삼십 분쯤 늦어진 뒤에 스퀴어스 씨가 다시 나타났고, 아이들은 제자리에 앉아 책을 펼쳤어."], ["Squeers reappeared, and the boys took their places and their books, of which latter commodity the average might be about one to eight learners.", "그런데 그 책이란 게 평균적으로 학생 여덟 명에 한 권쯤 돌아갈까 말까였지. 몇 분이 흘렀어."], ["A few minutes having elapsed, during which Mr.", "그동안 스퀴어스 씨는 아주 심오한 표정을 지었지."], ["Squeers looked very profound, as if he had a perfect apprehension of what was inside all the books, and could say every word of their contents by heart if he only chose to take the trouble, that gentleman called up the first class.", "마치 그 모든 책 속에 든 내용을 완벽하게 꿰뚫고 있고, 마음만 먹으면 거기 적힌 한 글자 한 글자를 죄다 외울 수 있다는 듯이 말이야. 그러더니 그 신사는 첫 번째 반을 불러냈어."]], [["Obedient to this summons there ranged themselves in front of the schoolmaster’s desk, half-a-dozen scarecrows, out at knees and elbows, one of whom placed a torn and filthy book beneath his learned eye.", "이 호출에 따라 교장 책상 앞에 허수아비 같은 아이 여섯이 죽 늘어섰어. 무릎이며 팔꿈치가 다 해진 차림이었지. 그중 하나가 찢어지고 더러운 책 한 권을 그 학식 깊은 눈앞에 갖다 놨어."]], [["‘This is the first class in English spelling and philosophy, Nickleby,’ said Squeers, beckoning Nicholas to stand beside him.", "\"이게 영어 철자법과 철학 첫 번째 반이야, 니클비.\" 스퀴어스가 니콜라스더러 옆에 서라고 손짓하며 말했어."], ["‘We’ll get up a Latin one, and hand that over to you.", "\"라틴어 반도 하나 꾸려서 자네한테 맡길 거야."], ["Now, then, where’s the first boy?’", "자, 그럼, 첫 번째 아이는 어디 있지?\""]], [["‘Please, sir, he’s cleaning the back-parlour window,’ said the temporary head of the philosophical class.", "\"저, 선생님, 그 애는 뒤쪽 응접실 창문을 닦고 있어요.\" 철학 반의 임시 우두머리가 말했어."]], [["‘So he is, to be sure,’ rejoined Squeers.", "\"그렇지, 맞아.\" 스퀴어스가 받아쳤어."], ["‘We go upon the practical mode of teaching, Nickleby; the regular education system.", "\"우리는 실전 방식으로 가르친단다, 니클비. 정통 교육 체계지."], ["C-l-e-a-n, clean, verb active, to make bright, to scour.", "닦, 다 — 닦다, 동사, 환하게 만들다, 문지르다."], ["W-i-n, win, d-e-r, der, winder, a casement.", "창, 문 — 창문, 여닫이창."], ["When the boy knows this out of book, he goes and does it.", "아이가 이걸 책에서 익히면, 가서 직접 그걸 하는 거야."], ["It’s just the same principle as the use of the globes.", "지구본을 쓰는 거랑 똑같은 원리지."], ["Where’s the second boy?’", "두 번째 아이는 어디 있나?\""]], [["‘Please, sir, he’s weeding the garden,’ replied a small voice.", "\"저, 선생님, 그 애는 정원 잡초를 뽑고 있어요.\" 작은 목소리가 대답했어."]], [["‘To be sure,’ said Squeers, by no means disconcerted.", "\"그렇지.\" 스퀴어스가 조금도 당황하지 않고 말했어."], ["‘So he is.", "\"맞아."], ["B-o-t, bot, t-i-n, tin, bottin, n-e-y, ney, bottinney, noun substantive, a knowledge of plants.", "식, 물, 학 — 식물학, 명사, 식물에 관한 지식."], ["When he has learned that bottinney means a knowledge of plants, he goes and knows ‘em.", "식물학이 식물에 관한 지식이란 걸 익히고 나면, 가서 식물들을 직접 아는 거지."], ["That’s our system, Nickleby: what do you think of it?’", "그게 우리 방식이야, 니클비. 어떻게 생각해?\""]], [["‘It’s very useful one, at any rate,’ answered Nicholas.", "\"하여간 아주 쓸모 있는 방식이네요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘I believe you,’ rejoined Squeers, not remarking the emphasis of his usher.", "\"그렇고말고.\" 스퀴어스가 조교의 말투에 실린 강조를 알아채지 못한 채 받았어."], ["‘Third boy, what’s horse?’", "\"세 번째 아이, 말이 뭐지?\""]], [["‘A beast, sir,’ replied the boy.", "\"짐승이요, 선생님.\" 그 아이가 대답했어."]], [["‘So it is,’ said Squeers.", "\"맞아.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘Ain’t it, Nickleby?’", "\"안 그런가, 니클비?\""]], [["‘I believe there is no doubt of that, sir,’ answered Nicholas.", "\"그 점은 의심할 여지가 없겠지요, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Of course there isn’t,’ said Squeers. ‘A horse is a quadruped, and quadruped’s Latin for beast, as everybody that’s gone through the grammar knows, or else where’s the use of having grammars at all?’", "\"당연히 없지.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"말은 네발짐승이고, 네발짐승은 짐승을 뜻하는 라틴어지. 문법을 뗀 사람이라면 누구나 아는 거야. 안 그러면 문법책이 다 무슨 소용이겠어?\""]], [["‘Where, indeed!’ said Nicholas abstractedly.", "\"정말, 무슨 소용이겠어요!\" 니콜라스가 멍하니 말했어."]], [["‘As you’re perfect in that,’ resumed Squeers, turning to the boy, ‘go and look after MY horse, and rub him down well, or I’ll rub you down. The rest of the class go and draw water up, till somebody tells you to leave off, for it’s washing-day tomorrow, and they want the coppers filled.’", "\"넌 그걸 완벽히 익혔으니까.\" 스퀴어스가 그 아이를 돌아보며 말을 이었어. \"가서 내 말이나 돌보고, 잘 문질러 닦아. 안 그러면 내가 널 문질러 줄 테니까. 나머지 반은 가서 물을 길어 올려. 그만하라고 할 때까지 말이야. 내일이 빨래하는 날이라서 가마솥을 가득 채워야 하거든.\""]], [["So saying, he dismissed the first class to their experiments in practical philosophy, and eyed Nicholas with a look, half cunning and half doubtful, as if he were not altogether certain what he might think of him by this time.", "이렇게 말하고 그는 첫 번째 반을 실전 철학 실습장으로 내보냈어. 그러고는 니콜라스를 반쯤 능청맞고 반쯤 미심쩍은 눈으로 살폈지. 마치 이쯤 됐으면 이 친구를 어떻게 봐야 할지 영 확신이 안 선다는 듯이 말이야."]], [["‘That’s the way we do it, Nickleby,’ he said, after a pause.", "\"우리는 이렇게 하는 거야, 니클비.\" 그가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말했어."]], [["Nicholas shrugged his shoulders in a manner that was scarcely perceptible, and said he saw it was.", "니콜라스는 알아챌까 말까 할 만큼만 어깨를 으쓱하고는, 그렇게 하는 걸 봤다고 말했어."]], [["‘And a very good way it is, too,’ said Squeers.", "\"게다가 아주 좋은 방식이지.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘Now, just take them fourteen little boys and hear them some reading, because, you know, you must begin to be useful.", "\"자, 그럼 저기 어린아이 열넷을 데리고 책 좀 읽혀 봐. 알다시피 자네도 슬슬 쓸모 있는 일을 시작해야 하니까."], ["Idling about here won’t do.’", "여기서 빈둥대는 건 안 되거든.\""]], [["Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨는 이 말을 마치 문득 떠오른 것처럼 했어."], ["Squeers said this, as if it had suddenly occurred to him, either that he must not say too much to his assistant, or that his assistant did not say enough to him in praise of the establishment.", "조교한테 말을 너무 많이 해선 안 되겠다는 생각이 들었거나, 아니면 조교가 이 시설에 대한 칭찬을 충분히 늘어놓지 않는다는 생각이 들었거나, 둘 중 하나였지. 아이들은 새 선생을 둘러싸고 반원으로 늘어섰어."], ["The children were arranged in a semicircle round the new master, and he was soon listening to their dull, drawling, hesitating recital of those stories of engrossing interest which are to be found in the more antiquated spelling-books.", "그리고 니콜라스는 곧 아이들이 더듬더듬 느릿느릿 머뭇거리며 읽어 내려가는 소리를 듣게 됐지. 좀 더 케케묵은 철자 교본에서나 찾아볼 수 있는, 그 흥미진진한 이야기들 말이야."]], [["In this exciting occupation, the morning lagged heavily on.", "이 신나는 일을 하는 동안 아침나절은 무겁게 흘러갔어."], ["At one o’clock, the boys, having previously had their appetites thoroughly taken away by stir-about and potatoes, sat down in the kitchen to some hard salt beef, of which Nicholas was graciously permitted to take his portion to his own solitary desk, to eat it there in peace.", "한 시가 되자 아이들은 — 이미 휘저은 죽이랑 감자로 입맛이 싹 가셔 버린 채 — 부엌에 앉아 딱딱한 소금에 절인 소고기를 먹었어. 니콜라스는 황송하게도 제 몫을 외딴 제 책상으로 가져가 거기서 편히 먹어도 좋다는 허락을 받았지."], ["After this, there was another hour of crouching in the schoolroom and shivering with cold, and then school began again.", "이게 끝난 뒤엔 또 한 시간 동안 교실에 웅크려 추위에 떨었고, 그러고 나서 수업이 다시 시작됐어."]], [["It was Mr. Squeer’s custom to call the boys together, and make a sort of report, after every half-yearly visit to the metropolis, regarding the relations and friends he had seen, the news he had heard, the letters he had brought down, the bills which had been paid, the accounts which had been left unpaid, and so forth.", "스퀴어스 씨한테는 버릇이 하나 있었어."], ["This solemn proceeding always took place in the afternoon of the day succeeding his return; perhaps, because the boys acquired strength of mind from the suspense of the morning, or, possibly, because Mr.", "반년에 한 번씩 런던에 다녀온 다음이면 아이들을 모아 놓고 일종의 보고를 하는 거였지. 자기가 만난 친척이며 친구들 얘기, 들은 소식, 가져온 편지들, 치러진 돈, 안 치러지고 밀린 외상값, 뭐 그런 것들에 관해서 말이야."], ["Squeers himself acquired greater sternness and inflexibility from certain warm potations in which he was wont to indulge after his early dinner.", "이 엄숙한 행사는 늘 그가 돌아온 다음 날 오후에 벌어졌어. 어쩌면 아이들이 오전 내내 마음 졸이며 단단히 각오를 다지라는 거였는지도 몰라."], ["Be this as it may, the boys were recalled from house-window, garden, stable, and cow-yard, and the school were assembled in full conclave, when Mr.", "아니면 스퀴어스 씨 자신이 이른 저녁을 먹은 뒤 으레 즐기던 따끈한 술 몇 잔 덕에 더 매섭고 단호해졌기 때문인지도 모르지."], ["Squeers, with a small bundle of papers in his hand, and Mrs. S.", "어쨌거나 아이들은 집 창문에서, 정원에서, 마구간에서, 외양간에서 불려 왔고, 학교 전체가 빠짐없이 모였어."], ["following with a pair of canes, entered the room and proclaimed silence.", "그때 스퀴어스 씨가 손에 종이 한 묶음을 들고, 뒤따라 스퀴어스 부인이 회초리 한 쌍을 들고 방에 들어와 조용히 하라고 선언했지."]], [["‘Let any boy speak a word without leave,’ said Mr.", "\"누구든 허락 없이 한마디라도 지껄이면,\""], ["Squeers mildly, ‘and I’ll take the skin off his back.’", "스퀴어스 씨가 부드럽게 말했어. \"등짝 가죽을 벗겨 버릴 테다.\""]], [["This special proclamation had the desired effect, and a deathlike silence immediately prevailed, in the midst of which Mr.", "이 특별 선언은 바라던 효과를 냈어. 곧장 죽은 듯한 침묵이 깔렸지."], ["Squeers went on to say:", "그 한가운데서 스퀴어스 씨가 말을 이었어."]], [["‘Boys, I’ve been to London, and have returned to my family and you, as strong and well as ever.’", "\"얘들아, 내가 런던에 다녀왔다. 그리고 변함없이 튼튼하고 멀쩡한 몸으로 내 가족과 너희한테 돌아왔지.\""]], [["According to half-yearly custom, the boys gave three feeble cheers at this refreshing intelligence.", "반년에 한 번 하는 관례대로, 아이들은 이 반가운 소식에 힘없는 만세를 세 번 외쳤어. 만세라니, 그런 만세를!"], ["Such cheers! Sights of extra strength with the chill on.", "으슬으슬 추위에 떨면서 짜낸 안간힘 같은 거였지."]], [["‘I have seen the parents of some boys,’ continued Squeers, turning over his papers, ‘and they’re so glad to hear how their sons are getting on, that there’s no prospect at all of their going away, which of course is a very pleasant thing to reflect upon, for all parties.’", "\"몇몇 아이들 부모를 만나고 왔다.\" 스퀴어스가 종이를 넘기며 말을 이었어. \"자기 아들이 어떻게 지내는지 듣고 어찌나 기뻐하던지, 데려갈 기색이 전혀 없더구나. 물론 이건 모두에게 아주 흐뭇한 일이지.\""]], [["Two or three hands went to two or three eyes when Squeers said this, but the greater part of the young gentlemen having no particular parents to speak of, were wholly uninterested in the thing one way or other.", "스퀴어스가 이렇게 말하자 두세 개의 손이 두세 개의 눈으로 갔어. 하지만 어린 신사들 대부분은 딱히 입에 올릴 만한 부모도 없는 터라, 그 일에 이러나저러나 도통 관심이 없었지."]], [["‘I have had disappointments to contend against,’ said Squeers, looking very grim; ‘Bolder’s father was two pound ten short.", "\"애를 먹은 일도 있었다.\" 스퀴어스가 아주 험상궂은 얼굴로 말했어."], ["Where is Bolder?’", "\"볼더 아버지가 이 파운드 십 실링을 덜 냈어. 볼더 어디 있나?\""]], [["‘Here he is, please sir,’ rejoined twenty officious voices.", "\"여기 있어요, 선생님.\" 스무 개쯤 되는 호들갑스러운 목소리가 받았어."], ["Boys are very like men to be sure.", "아이들이란 정말 어른이랑 꼭 닮았다니까."]], [["‘Come here, Bolder,’ said Squeers.", "\"이리 와, 볼더.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."]], [["An unhealthy-looking boy, with warts all over his hands, stepped from his place to the master’s desk, and raised his eyes imploringly to Squeers’s face; his own, quite white from the rapid beating of his heart.", "손마다 사마귀가 잔뜩 난, 병약해 보이는 아이가 제자리에서 나와 주인의 책상 쪽으로 갔어. 그러고는 애원하는 눈빛으로 스퀴어스의 얼굴을 올려다봤지. 그 애 자신의 얼굴은 심장이 쿵쾅쿵쾅 빨리 뛰는 통에 백지장처럼 하얬어."]], [["‘Bolder,’ said Squeers, speaking very slowly, for he was considering, as the saying goes, where to have him.", "\"볼더.\" 스퀴어스가 아주 느릿느릿 말했어."], ["‘Bolder, if you father thinks that because--why, what’s this, sir?’", "말마따나 이 녀석을 어디부터 잡을까 궁리하는 중이었거든. \"볼더, 네 아비가 만약에 — 어라, 이게 뭐냐, 이 녀석아?\""]], [["As Squeers spoke, he caught up the boy’s hand by the cuff of his jacket, and surveyed it with an edifying aspect of horror and disgust.", "스퀴어스는 말하면서 그 애 재킷 소맷부리를 잡아채 손을 끌어올리더니, 보란 듯이 끔찍하고 역겹다는 표정으로 그걸 들여다봤어."]], [["‘What do you call this, sir?’ demanded the schoolmaster, administering a cut with the cane to expedite the reply.", "\"이게 뭐냐, 이놈아?\" 교장이 다그치며, 대답을 재촉하려고 회초리로 한 대 후려쳤어."]], [["‘I can’t help it, indeed, sir,’ rejoined the boy, crying.", "\"저도 어쩔 수가 없어요, 정말이에요, 선생님.\" 그 아이가 울면서 대꾸했어."], ["‘They will come; it’s the dirty work I think, sir--at least I don’t know what it is, sir, but it’s not my fault.’", "\"자꾸 나는걸요. 더러운 일을 해서 그런 것 같아요, 선생님 — 아니, 저도 이게 뭔지 모르겠지만, 제 잘못은 아니에요.\""]], [["‘Bolder,’ said Squeers, tucking up his wristbands, and moistening the palm of his right hand to get a good grip of the cane, ‘you’re an incorrigible young scoundrel, and as the last thrashing did you no good, we must see what another will do towards beating it out of you.’", "\"볼더.\" 스퀴어스가 소맷동을 걷어 올리고, 회초리를 단단히 쥐려고 오른손 바닥에 침을 발랐어. \"넌 구제불능 망나니 녀석이야. 지난번 매질이 약발이 안 들었으니, 이번엔 또 한 번 해서 그 버릇을 두들겨 빼낼 수 있을지 봐야겠다.\""]], [["With this, and wholly disregarding a piteous cry for mercy, Mr.", "이렇게 말하고는, 살려 달라는 애처로운 울부짖음 따위는 깡그리 무시한 채, 스퀴어스 씨가 그 아이한테 달려들어 흠씬 매질을 했어."], ["Squeers fell upon the boy and caned him soundly: not leaving off, indeed, until his arm was tired out.", "사실 자기 팔이 지칠 때까지 멈추지 않았지."]], [["‘There,’ said Squeers, when he had quite done; ‘rub away as hard as you like, you won’t rub that off in a hurry.", "\"자.\""], ["Oh!", "완전히 끝내고 나서 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["you won’t hold that noise, won’t you?", "\"맘껏 문질러 봐라, 그게 그리 쉽게 지워지진 않을 테니. 오!"], ["Put him out, Smike.’", "그 소리 안 그칠 거냐, 응? 끌어내, 스마이크.\""]], [["The drudge knew better from long experience, than to hesitate about obeying, so he bundled the victim out by a side-door, and Mr.", "그 머슴 같은 아이는 오랜 경험으로 잘 알고 있었어. 시키는 걸 망설였다간 큰일 난다는 걸 말이야."], ["Squeers perched himself again on his own stool, supported by Mrs.", "그래서 그 가엾은 아이를 옆문으로 둘러메다시피 끌어냈지."], ["Squeers, who occupied another at his side.", "스퀴어스 씨는 다시 제 의자에 걸터앉았고, 옆에 또 다른 의자를 차지한 스퀴어스 부인이 곁을 지켰어."]], [["‘Now let us see,’ said Squeers.", "\"자, 어디 보자.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘A letter for Cobbey.", "\"코비 앞으로 온 편지로군."], ["Stand up, Cobbey.’", "일어서, 코비.\""]], [["Another boy stood up, and eyed the letter very hard while Squeers made a mental abstract of the same.", "또 다른 아이가 일어섰어. 그리고 스퀴어스가 그 편지 내용을 머릿속으로 간추리는 동안, 편지를 뚫어져라 쳐다봤지."]], [["‘Oh!’ said Squeers: ‘Cobbey’s grandmother is dead, and his uncle John has took to drinking, which is all the news his sister sends, except eighteenpence, which will just pay for that broken square of glass. Mrs Squeers, my dear, will you take the money?’", "\"오!\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"코비 할머니가 돌아가셨고, 존 삼촌은 술독에 빠졌단다. 누나가 전한 소식은 이게 다인데, 거기다 일 실링 육 펜스를 보냈군. 깨진 유리창 한 장 값으로 딱 맞겠어. 여보, 스퀴어스 부인, 이 돈 좀 받아 둘래요?\""]], [["The worthy lady pocketed the eighteenpence with a most business-like air, and Squeers passed on to the next boy, as coolly as possible.", "이 갸륵한 부인은 더없이 사무적인 태도로 그 일 실링 육 펜스를 주머니에 챙겼어. 스퀴어스는 더없이 태연하게 다음 아이로 넘어갔지."]], [["‘Graymarsh,’ said Squeers, ‘he’s the next.", "\"그레이마시.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["Stand up, Graymarsh.’", "\"다음은 이 녀석이군. 일어서, 그레이마시.\""]], [["Another boy stood up, and the schoolmaster looked over the letter as before.", "또 다른 아이가 일어섰어. 교장은 아까처럼 편지를 훑었지."]], [["‘Graymarsh’s maternal aunt,’ said Squeers, when he had possessed himself of the contents, ‘is very glad to hear he’s so well and happy, and sends her respectful compliments to Mrs.", "\"그레이마시 외숙모는.\" 스퀴어스가 내용을 다 파악하고 나서 말했어."], ["Squeers, and thinks she must be an angel. She likewise thinks Mr.", "\"이 녀석이 아주 잘 있고 행복하다는 소식에 무척 기뻐하고, 스퀴어스 부인께 정중한 안부를 전하며, 부인이 분명 천사일 거라고 여긴단다."], ["Squeers is too good for this world; but hopes he may long be spared to carry on the business.", "또 스퀴어스 씨는 이 세상에 두기엔 아까운 분이라고 생각하지만, 부디 오래오래 살아남아 이 사업을 계속해 주기를 바란다는구나."], ["Would have sent the two pair of stockings as desired, but is short of money, so forwards a tract instead, and hopes Graymarsh will put his trust in Providence.", "부탁한 양말 두 켤레를 보내려 했지만 돈이 부족해서, 대신 전도 책자를 한 권 보내고, 그레이마시가 하느님의 섭리를 믿기를 바란단다. 무엇보다도, 그레이마시가 매사에 스퀴어스 씨 내외를 기쁘게 해 드리려고 애쓰고, 두 분을 자기 유일한 친구로 여기기를 바란다는구나."], ["Hopes, above all, that he will study in everything to please Mr.", "또 스퀴어스 도련님을 사랑하고, 한 침대에서 다섯이 자는 걸 마다하지 않기를 바란다고."], ["and Mrs Squeers, and look upon them as his only friends; and that he will love Master Squeers; and not object to sleeping five in a bed, which no Christian should.", "기독교인이라면 그래선 안 되니까 말이지. 아!\""], ["Ah!’ said Squeers, folding it up, ‘a delightful letter.", "스퀴어스가 편지를 접으며 말했어. \"참 흐뭇한 편지로구나."], ["Very affecting indeed.’", "정말이지 가슴 뭉클해.\""]], [["It was affecting in one sense, for Graymarsh’s maternal aunt was strongly supposed, by her more intimate friends, to be no other than his maternal parent; Squeers, however, without alluding to this part of the story (which would have sounded immoral before boys), proceeded with the business by calling out ‘Mobbs,’ whereupon another boy rose, and Graymarsh resumed his seat.", "한편으로는 가슴 뭉클한 게 맞긴 했어. 사실 그레이마시의 외숙모는, 좀 더 가까운 친구들 사이에서는 다름 아닌 그 애의 친어머니라고 짐작되고 있었거든. 그렇지만 스퀴어스는 그 대목은 입에 올리지 않은 채 — 아이들 앞에서 했다간 부도덕하게 들렸을 테니까 — \"몹스\"를 불러 일을 계속 이어 갔어. 그러자 또 다른 아이가 일어섰고, 그레이마시는 다시 자리에 앉았지."]], [["‘Mobbs’s step-mother,’ said Squeers, ‘took to her bed on hearing that he wouldn’t eat fat, and has been very ill ever since.", "\"몹스 새어머니는.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["She wishes to know, by an early post, where he expects to go to, if he quarrels with his vittles; and with what feelings he could turn up his nose at the cow’s-liver broth, after his good master had asked a blessing on it.", "\"이 녀석이 비계를 안 먹는다는 소릴 듣고 자리에 드러누웠고, 그 뒤로 줄곧 몸져누워 있단다. 빠른 우편으로 알고 싶다는구나."], ["This was told her in the London newspapers--not by Mr.", "먹을 걸 가지고 투정을 부리면 도대체 어디로 가게 될 것 같은지 말이야. 그리고 착하신 선생님이 축복까지 빌어 주신 소 간 죽을, 무슨 심보로 코를 쳐들고 거들떠보지도 않았는지도."], ["Squeers, for he is too kind and too good to set anybody against anybody--and it has vexed her so much, Mobbs can’t think.", "이 일은 런던 신문을 통해 알게 된 거란다 — 스퀴어스 씨가 일러바친 건 아니야. 그분은 너무 친절하고 너무 착해서 누구를 누구한테 등 돌리게 할 사람이 아니거든 — 어쨌든 이 일로 어머니가 어찌나 속이 상했는지, 몹스는 상상도 못 할 거야."], ["She is sorry to find he is discontented, which is sinful and horrid, and hopes Mr.", "어머니는 이 녀석이 불만을 품은 걸 알고 마음 아파한단다. 그건 죄스럽고 끔찍한 일이니까."], ["Squeers will flog him into a happier state of mind; with which view, she has also stopped his halfpenny a week pocket-money, and given a double-bladed knife with a corkscrew in it to the Missionaries, which she had bought on purpose for him.’", "그래서 스퀴어스 씨가 회초리로 두들겨서 더 행복한 마음가짐으로 만들어 주기를 바란다는구나. 그런 뜻에서, 일주일 용돈 반 페니도 끊어 버렸고, 일부러 이 녀석한테 주려고 샀던 코르크 따개 달린 양날 칼은 선교사들한테 줘 버렸단다.\""]], [["‘A sulky state of feeling,’ said Squeers, after a terrible pause, during which he had moistened the palm of his right hand again, ‘won’t do.", "\"부루퉁한 마음가짐이란.\" 스퀴어스가 무시무시한 침묵 끝에 말했어."], ["Cheerfulness and contentment must be kept up.", "그동안 그는 오른손 바닥에 다시 침을 발랐지. \"안 될 일이지."], ["Mobbs, come to me!’", "명랑함과 만족스러움을 잃지 말아야 해. 몹스, 이리 와!\""]], [["Mobbs moved slowly towards the desk, rubbing his eyes in anticipation of good cause for doing so; and he soon afterwards retired by the side-door, with as good cause as a boy need have.", "몹스는 책상 쪽으로 천천히 다가갔어. 곧 그럴 만한 충분한 이유가 생길 걸 예상하며 미리 눈을 비비면서 말이야. 그리고 얼마 안 가, 한 아이가 가질 법한 충분한 이유를 안고 옆문으로 물러났지."]], [["Mr.", "그러고 나서 스퀴어스 씨는 잡다한 편지 꾸러미를 열기 시작했어."], ["Squeers then proceeded to open a miscellaneous collection of letters; some enclosing money, which Mrs.", "어떤 편지엔 돈이 들어 있어서 스퀴어스 부인이 \"챙겨 뒀고\", 또 어떤 편지엔 모자 같은 자잘한 옷가지 얘기가 있었지. 그런데 그것들은 죄다 부인 입으로 너무 크거나 너무 작다고, 어린 스퀴어스밖에 맞을 사람이 없다고 했어."], ["Squeers ‘took care of;’ and others referring to small articles of apparel, as caps and so forth, all of which the same lady stated to be too large, or too small, and calculated for nobody but young Squeers, who would appear indeed to have had most accommodating limbs, since everything that came into the school fitted him to a nicety.", "사실 그 애는 더없이 잘 맞는 팔다리를 가진 모양이었지. 학교에 들어오는 건 뭐든 그 애한테 딱 맞아떨어졌으니까."], ["His head, in particular, must have been singularly elastic, for hats and caps of all dimensions were alike to him.", "특히 그 애 머리는 유난히 잘 늘어났던 게 틀림없어. 온갖 크기의 모자란 모자가 죄다 그 애한테 똑같이 맞았거든."]], [["This business dispatched, a few slovenly lessons were performed, and Squeers retired to his fireside, leaving Nicholas to take care of the boys in the school-room, which was very cold, and where a meal of bread and cheese was served out shortly after dark.", "이 일이 끝나자 대충대충 몇 가지 수업이 진행됐고, 스퀴어스는 제 난롯가로 물러갔어. 교실에 남은 아이들 돌보는 일은 니콜라스한테 맡기고 말이야. 교실은 몹시 추웠고, 어두워진 뒤 얼마 안 돼 빵과 치즈로 끼니가 나왔어."]], [["There was a small stove at that corner of the room which was nearest to the master’s desk, and by it Nicholas sat down, so depressed and self-degraded by the consciousness of his position, that if death could have come upon him at that time, he would have been almost happy to meet it. The cruelty of which he had been an unwilling witness, the coarse and ruffianly behaviour of Squeers even in his best moods, the filthy place, the sights and sounds about him, all contributed to this state of feeling; but when he recollected that, being there as an assistant, he actually seemed--no matter what unhappy train of circumstances had brought him to that pass--to be the aider and abettor of a system which filled him with honest disgust and indignation, he loathed himself, and felt, for the moment, as though the mere consciousness of his present situation must, through all time to come, prevent his raising his head again.", "주인 책상에서 가장 가까운 방 구석에 작은 난로가 하나 있었어. 니콜라스는 그 곁에 앉았지. 제 처지를 자각하며 어찌나 의기소침하고 비참했던지, 만약 그때 죽음이 찾아온다면 차라리 기꺼이 맞이했을 거야. 본의 아니게 지켜봐야 했던 그 잔인함, 기분 좋을 때조차 거칠고 막돼먹은 스퀴어스의 행동, 더러운 그곳, 둘레의 광경과 소리, 이 모든 게 그런 심정을 만들었지. 그런데 자기가 거기 조교로 와 있는 이상, 어쩌다 그렇게 불운한 사정들에 떠밀려 그 지경에 이르렀든 간에, 사실상 자기가 — 정직한 혐오와 분노로 속이 뒤집히게 하는 그 체계의 — 조력자이자 방조자인 셈이라는 데 생각이 미치자, 그는 스스로가 역겨웠어. 그리고 그 순간, 마치 지금 제 처지를 자각하는 것만으로도 앞으로 영영 다시는 고개를 들 수 없을 것만 같았지."]], [["But, for the present, his resolve was taken, and the resolution he had formed on the preceding night remained undisturbed.", "하지만 지금으로선 마음을 굳혔어. 전날 밤에 세운 결심은 흔들리지 않았지."], ["He had written to his mother and sister, announcing the safe conclusion of his journey, and saying as little about Dotheboys Hall, and saying that little as cheerfully, as he possibly could.", "그는 어머니와 누이한테 편지를 써서, 여행이 무사히 끝났다는 걸 알렸어. 두더보이즈 홀에 대해서는 되도록 말을 아꼈고, 그 적은 말마저 되도록 밝게 적었지."], ["He hoped that by remaining where he was, he might do some good, even there; at all events, others depended too much on his uncle’s favour, to admit of his awakening his wrath just then.", "여기 머무르면, 이런 데서나마 뭔가 좋은 일을 할 수 있을지 모른다고 바랐거든. 어쨌거나 다른 식구들이 삼촌의 호의에 너무 많이 기대고 있어서, 지금 당장 그의 노여움을 살 수는 없었지."]], [["One reflection disturbed him far more than any selfish considerations arising out of his own position.", "그런데 자기 처지에서 비롯된 어떤 이기적인 걱정보다 그를 훨씬 더 괴롭히는 생각이 하나 있었어. 바로 누이 케이트가 어디로 가게 될까 하는 거였지."], ["This was the probable destination of his sister Kate.", "삼촌은 자기를 속였잖아. 그러니 누이도 어디 비참한 데로 보내 버리지 않을까?"], ["His uncle had deceived him, and might he not consign her to some miserable place where her youth and beauty would prove a far greater curse than ugliness and decrepitude?", "거기선 누이의 젊음과 아름다움이 추함과 노쇠함보다 훨씬 더 큰 저주가 될지도 모르는데. 손발이 묶인 채 갇힌 사람한테 이건 끔찍한 생각이었어 — 하지만 아니야, 그는 생각했어."], ["To a caged man, bound hand and foot, this was a terrible idea--but no, he thought, his mother was by; there was the portrait-painter, too--simple enough, but still living in the world, and of it.", "어머니가 곁에 계시잖아. 게다가 그 초상화가도 있고 — 좀 어수룩하긴 해도, 그래도 세상 속에 살면서 세상 돌아가는 걸 아는 사람이지."], ["He was willing to believe that Ralph Nickleby had conceived a personal dislike to himself.", "그는 랄프 니클비가 자기 개인한테 반감을 품었다고 믿고 싶었어."], ["Having pretty good reason, by this time, to reciprocate it, he had no great difficulty in arriving at this conclusion, and tried to persuade himself that the feeling extended no farther than between them.", "이쯤 되니 그 반감을 똑같이 되돌려 줄 만한 이유가 꽤 충분했던 터라, 그런 결론에 이르는 건 그리 어렵지 않았지. 그리고 그 감정이 자기랑 삼촌 둘 사이에서만 머문다고 스스로를 설득하려 애썼어."]], [["As he was absorbed in these meditations, he all at once encountered the upturned face of Smike, who was on his knees before the stove, picking a few stray cinders from the hearth and planting them on the fire.", "이런 생각에 빠져 있다가, 그는 문득 위를 올려다보는 스마이크의 얼굴과 마주쳤어. 스마이크는 난로 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 앉아, 화덕에서 흩어진 잿불 몇 개를 집어 불에 얹고 있었지."], ["He had paused to steal a look at Nicholas, and when he saw that he was observed, shrunk back, as if expecting a blow.", "그러다 잠시 멈추고 니콜라스를 몰래 훔쳐보던 참이었는데, 자기가 들킨 걸 알자 마치 매라도 날아올까 봐 움찔하며 움츠러들었어."]], [["‘You need not fear me,’ said Nicholas kindly.", "\"날 무서워할 것 없어.\" 니콜라스가 다정하게 말했어."], ["‘Are you cold?’\n‘N-n-o.’", "\"춥니?\""]], [["‘You are shivering.’", "\"아, 아, 아뇨.\""]], [["‘I am not cold,’ replied Smike quickly.", "\"떨고 있잖아.\" \"안 추워요.\""], ["‘I am used to it.’", "스마이크가 얼른 대답했어. \"익숙해서요.\""]], [["There was such an obvious fear of giving offence in his manner, and he was such a timid, broken-spirited creature, that Nicholas could not help exclaiming, ‘Poor fellow!’", "그 태도에는 행여 비위를 거스를까 두려워하는 기색이 너무나 또렷했어. 게다가 어찌나 겁 많고 기죽은 아이인지, 니콜라스는 저도 모르게 외치고 말았지. \"가엾은 녀석!\""]], [["If he had struck the drudge, he would have slunk away without a word.", "만약 그 머슴 같은 아이를 때렸다면, 그 애는 한마디도 없이 슬그머니 사라졌을 거야."], ["But, now, he burst into tears.", "그런데 지금은 그만 울음을 터뜨리고 말았어."]], [["‘Oh dear, oh dear!’ he cried, covering his face with his cracked and horny hands.", "\"아, 어떡해, 아, 어떡해!\" 그 애가 갈라지고 거칠어진 손으로 얼굴을 가리며 흐느꼈어."], ["‘My heart will break.", "\"가슴이 터질 것 같아요."], ["It will, it will.’", "정말, 정말로요.\""]], [["‘Hush!’ said Nicholas, laying his hand upon his shoulder.", "\"쉿!\" 니콜라스가 그 애 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했어."], ["‘Be a man; you are nearly one by years, God help you.’", "\"사내답게 굴어. 나이로 치면 거의 어른이잖아, 가엾기도 하지.\""]], [["‘By years!’ cried Smike.", "\"나이로 치면!\" 스마이크가 외쳤어."], ["‘Oh dear, dear, how many of them!", "\"아, 어떡해, 어떡해, 그게 얼마나 많은데요!"], ["How many of them since I was a little child, younger than any that are here now!", "제가 어린아이였을 때부터, 지금 여기 있는 어느 애들보다도 어렸을 때부터, 그게 얼마나 많은지요!"], ["Where are they all!’", "다들 어디로 가 버린 거예요!\""]], [["‘Whom do you speak of?’ inquired Nicholas, wishing to rouse the poor half-witted creature to reason.", "\"누구 얘기를 하는 거야?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."], ["‘Tell me.’", "이 가엾고 반쯤 정신 나간 아이를 정신 차리게 해 주고 싶었거든. \"말해 봐.\""]], [["‘My friends,’ he replied, ‘myself--my--oh!", "\"제 친구들요.\" 그 애가 대답했어."], ["what sufferings mine have been!’", "\"저 자신도요 — 제 — 아! 제가 겪은 고통이 얼마나 컸는지!\""]], [["‘There is always hope,’ said Nicholas; he knew not what to say.", "\"언제나 희망은 있어.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰랐거든."]], [["‘No,’ rejoined the other, ‘no; none for me.", "\"아뇨.\" 그 애가 받았어."], ["Do you remember the boy that died here?’", "\"아뇨, 저한텐 없어요. 여기서 죽은 그 애 기억하세요?\""]], [["‘I was not here, you know,’ said Nicholas gently; ‘but what of him?’", "\"난 그때 여기 없었잖아.\" 니콜라스가 부드럽게 말했어. \"그런데 그 애가 어쨌는데?\""]], [["‘Why,’ replied the youth, drawing closer to his questioner’s side, ‘I was with him at night, and when it was all silent he cried no more for friends he wished to come and sit with him, but began to see faces round his bed that came from home; he said they smiled, and talked to him; and he died at last lifting his head to kiss them. Do you hear?’", "\"그게요.\" 그 애가 묻는 사람 곁으로 더 바싹 다가서며 대답했어. \"밤에 제가 그 애랑 같이 있었거든요. 그러다 사방이 다 조용해지자, 그 애는 더 이상 옆에 와서 앉아 주길 바라던 친구들을 부르며 울지 않고, 침대 둘레로 집에서 온 얼굴들이 보이기 시작했어요. 그 애가 그러는데, 그 얼굴들이 자기한테 웃어 주고 말도 걸어 줬대요. 그리고 결국 그 애는 고개를 들어 그 얼굴들한테 입 맞추면서 죽었어요. 들었어요?\""]], [["‘Yes, yes,’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"그래, 그래.\" 니콜라스가 받았어."]], [["‘What faces will smile on me when I die!’ cried his companion, shivering.", "\"제가 죽을 땐 어떤 얼굴들이 저한테 웃어 줄까요!\" 그 애가 떨면서 외쳤어."], ["‘Who will talk to me in those long nights!", "\"그 긴긴 밤에 누가 저한테 말을 걸어 줄까요! 그 얼굴들은 집에서 올 수가 없어요."], ["They cannot come from home; they would frighten me, if they did, for I don’t know what it is, and shouldn’t know them.", "온다 해도 절 무섭게 만들 거예요. 전 그게 뭔지도 모르고, 그 얼굴들도 못 알아볼 테니까요."], ["Pain and fear, pain and fear for me, alive or dead.", "고통과 두려움, 저한텐 살아서도 죽어서도 고통과 두려움뿐이에요."], ["No hope, no hope!’", "희망은 없어요, 희망은 없다고요!\""]], [["The bell rang to bed: and the boy, subsiding at the sound into his usual listless state, crept away as if anxious to avoid notice.", "잠자리에 들라는 종이 울렸어. 그러자 그 아이는 그 소리에 다시 늘 그렇듯 멍하니 풀 죽은 상태로 가라앉아, 남의 눈에 띄지 않으려는 듯 슬그머니 사라졌어."], ["It was with a heavy heart that Nicholas soon afterwards--no, not retired; there was no retirement there--followed--to his dirty and crowded dormitory.", "무거운 마음으로 니콜라스도 얼마 안 가 — 아니, 잠자리에 들었다고는 못 하겠어. 거기엔 편히 쉴 데란 없었으니까 — 더럽고 북적이는 침실로 따라 들어갔지."]], [["# CHAPTER 9", "# CHAPTER 9"]], [["Of Miss Squeers, Mrs. Squeers, Master Squeers, and Mr.", "스퀴어스 선생이 밤이 되어 교실을 나선 뒤에는, 앞에서 말했듯이 자기 집 난롯가로 갔다."], ["Squeers; and of various Matters and Persons connected no less with the Squeerses than Nicholas Nickleby\nWhen Mr.", "그런데 그 난롯가는 니콜라스가 도착한 첫날 밤에 저녁을 먹었던 그 방이 아니라, 건물 뒤쪽의 더 작은 방이었어."], ["Squeers left the schoolroom for the night, he betook himself, as has been before remarked, to his own fireside, which was situated--not in the room in which Nicholas had supped on the night of his arrival, but in a smaller apartment in the rear of the premises, where his lady wife, his amiable son, and accomplished daughter, were in the full enjoyment of each other’s society; Mrs.", "거기서 그의 부인과 사랑스러운 아들, 그리고 재주 많은 딸이 서로의 정겨운 시간을 한껏 누리고 있었지. 스퀴어스 부인은 양말 깁기라는 안주인다운 일에 매달려 있었고, 어린 아가씨와 도련님은 식탁 너머로 주먹다짐을 벌이며 어린애다운 다툼을 정리하는 중이었어."], ["Squeers being engaged in the matronly pursuit of stocking-darning; and the young lady and gentleman being occupied in the adjustment of some youthful differences, by means of a pugilistic contest across the table, which, on the approach of their honoured parent, subsided into a noiseless exchange of kicks beneath it.", "그런데 존경하는 아버지가 다가오자, 그 다툼은 식탁 밑에서 소리 없이 발길질을 주고받는 걸로 잦아들었지."]], [["And, in this place, it may be as well to apprise the reader, that Miss Fanny Squeers was in her three-and-twentieth year. If there be any one grace or loveliness inseparable from that particular period of life, Miss Squeers may be presumed to have been possessed of it, as there is no reason to suppose that she was a solitary exception to an universal rule. She was not tall like her mother, but short like her father; from the former she inherited a voice of harsh quality; from the latter a remarkable expression of the right eye, something akin to having none at all.", "그리고 여기서 독자한테 미리 알려 두는 게 좋겠어. 패니 스퀴어스 양은 스물세 살이었지. 인생의 그 시기에 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 어떤 우아함이나 사랑스러움이 있다면, 스퀴어스 양도 그걸 갖추고 있었다고 봐도 될 거야. 그 여자가 만국 공통의 법칙에서 혼자만 예외였을 거라고 볼 이유는 없으니까. 그 여자는 어머니처럼 키가 크지 않고 아버지처럼 작았어. 어머니한테서는 거친 목소리를 물려받았고, 아버지한테서는 오른쪽 눈의 독특한 인상을 물려받았지. 그 눈은 아예 없는 것이나 다름없어 보였어."]], [["Miss Squeers had been spending a few days with a neighbouring friend, and had only just returned to the parental roof.", "스퀴어스 양은 이웃 친구네 집에서 며칠 지내다가 이제 막 친정 지붕 아래로 돌아온 참이었어."], ["To this circumstance may be referred, her having heard nothing of Nicholas, until Mr. Squeers himself now made him the subject of conversation.", "그런 사정 때문에, 스퀴어스 선생이 직접 니콜라스를 화제에 올리기 전까지 그 여자는 니콜라스에 대해 아무것도 듣지 못했던 거야."]], [["‘Well, my dear,’ said Squeers, drawing up his chair, ‘what do you think of him by this time?’", "\"자, 얘야,\" 스퀴어스가 의자를 끌어당기며 말했어. \"그 사람을 이제 어떻게 생각하느냐?\""]], [["‘Think of who?’ inquired Mrs.", "\"누구를 어떻게 생각하느냐고요?\""], ["Squeers; who (as she often remarked) was no grammarian, thank Heaven.", "스퀴어스 부인이 물었어. 부인은 (스스로 자주 말하듯이) 하늘에 감사하게도 문법 따위는 모르는 사람이었거든."]], [["‘Of the young man--the new teacher--who else could I mean?’", "\"그 젊은이 말이오, 새로 온 선생, 내가 누굴 말하겠소?\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["that Knuckleboy,’ said Mrs.", "그 너클보이.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 짜증스럽게 말했어."], ["Squeers impatiently. ‘I hate him.’", "\"난 그 작자가 싫어요.\""]], [["‘What do you hate him for, my dear?’ asked Squeers.", "\"왜 싫은데, 여보?\" 스퀴어스가 물었어."]], [["‘What’s that to you?’ retorted Mrs. Squeers.", "\"그게 당신이랑 무슨 상관이에요?\""], ["‘If I hate him, that’s enough, ain’t it?’", "스퀴어스 부인이 쏘아붙였어. \"내가 싫으면 그걸로 됐지, 안 그래요?\""]], [["‘Quite enough for him, my dear, and a great deal too much I dare say, if he knew it,’ replied Squeers in a pacific tone.", "\"그 사람한테야 그걸로 충분하지, 여보, 그가 그걸 안다면 차고 넘칠 만도 하고 말이야.\" 스퀴어스가 누그러진 어조로 대답했어."], ["‘I only ask from curiosity, my dear.’", "\"난 그냥 궁금해서 물어보는 거요, 여보.\""]], [["‘Well, then, if you want to know,’ rejoined Mrs.", "\"그럼 정 알고 싶으면,\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말을 받았어."], ["Squeers, ‘I’ll tell you.", "\"내 말해 주죠."], ["Because he’s a proud, haughty, consequential, turned-up-nosed peacock.’", "그 작자가 잘난 척하고, 거만하고, 거들먹거리고, 코를 치켜든 공작새 같은 놈이라서 그래요.\""]], [["Mrs.", "스퀴어스 부인은 흥분하면 험한 말을 쓰는 버릇이 있었어."], ["Squeers, when excited, was accustomed to use strong language, and, moreover, to make use of a plurality of epithets, some of which were of a figurative kind, as the word peacock, and furthermore the allusion to Nicholas’s nose, which was not intended to be taken in its literal sense, but rather to bear a latitude of construction according to the fancy of the hearers.", "게다가 여러 가지 별명을 한꺼번에 갖다 붙이곤 했는데, 그중에는 공작새 같은 비유적인 표현도 있었지. 그리고 니콜라스의 코에 대한 말도, 말 그대로 받아들이라는 게 아니라 듣는 사람 마음대로 폭넓게 해석하라는 뜻이었어."]], [["Neither were they meant to bear reference to each other, so much as to the object on whom they were bestowed, as will be seen in the present case: a peacock with a turned-up nose being a novelty in ornithology, and a thing not commonly seen.", "또 그 표현들은 서로 들어맞으라고 한 게 아니라, 그저 그 말을 받는 사람을 가리키려는 거였지. 이번 경우만 봐도 알 수 있어. 코를 치켜든 공작새라니, 조류학에서는 듣도 보도 못한 신기한 거였으니까."]], [["‘Hem!’ said Squeers, as if in mild deprecation of this outbreak.", "\"흠!\" 스퀴어스가 이 격한 말을 가볍게 나무라듯 말했어."], ["‘He is cheap, my dear; the young man is very cheap.’", "\"그 사람은 싸잖소, 여보. 그 젊은이는 아주 싸단 말이오.\""]], [["‘Not a bit of it,’ retorted Mrs.", "\"하나도 안 싸요.\""], ["Squeers.", "스퀴어스 부인이 받아쳤어."]], [["‘Five pound a year,’ said Squeers.", "\"한 해에 오 파운드인데.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."]], [["‘What of that; it’s dear if you don’t want him, isn’t it?’ replied his wife.", "\"그게 뭐 어때서요? 필요 없는 사람이면 그것도 비싼 거잖아요, 안 그래요?\" 부인이 대꾸했어."]], [["‘But we DO want him,’ urged Squeers.", "\"하지만 우리한텐 필요하잖소.\" 스퀴어스가 우겼어."]], [["‘I don’t see that you want him any more than the dead,’ said Mrs.", "\"죽은 사람이나 마찬가지로 필요 없어 보이는데요.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 말했어."], ["Squeers. ‘Don’t tell me.", "\"내 말 좀 들어 봐요."], ["You can put on the cards and in the advertisements, “Education by Mr.", "광고지에다 '왝퍼드 스퀴어스 선생과 유능한 조교들이 가르칩니다'"], ["Wackford Squeers and able assistants,” without having any assistants, can’t you?", "하고 적어 놓으면 되잖아요, 조교가 한 명도 없어도 말이에요, 안 그래요?"], ["Isn’t it done every day by all the masters about?", "다른 교장들도 다 날마다 그렇게 하잖아요?"], ["I’ve no patience with you.’", "정말 당신한테는 두 손 두 발 다 들었다니까요.\""]], [["‘Haven’t you!’ said Squeers, sternly.", "\"두 손 두 발 다 들었다고!\" 스퀴어스가 매섭게 말했어."], ["‘Now I’ll tell you what, Mrs Squeers.", "\"자, 그럼 내 말 좀 들어 봐요, 스퀴어스 부인. 선생을 두는 이 일만큼은, 미안하지만 내 방식대로 하겠소."], ["In this matter of having a teacher, I’ll take my own way, if you please.", "서인도제도의 노예 감독관도 자기 밑에 사람을 하나 둘 수 있단 말이오. 흑인들이 도망치거나 반란을 일으키지 못하게 지키라고 말이지."], ["A slave driver in the West Indies is allowed a man under him, to see that his blacks don’t run away, or get up a rebellion; and I’ll have a man under me to do the same with OUR blacks, till such time as little Wackford is able to take charge of the school.’", "나도 내 밑에 사람을 하나 둬서 우리 흑인들한테 똑같이 시킬 거요. 어린 왝퍼드가 학교를 맡을 수 있을 때까지 말이오.\""]], [["‘Am I to take care of the school when I grow up a man, father?’ said Wackford junior, suspending, in the excess of his delight, a vicious kick which he was administering to his sister.", "\"제가 어른이 되면 학교를 맡는 거예요, 아버지?\" 어린 왝퍼드가 너무 기쁜 나머지, 누이한테 막 날리려던 짓궂은 발길질을 멈추고 물었어."]], [["‘You are, my son,’ replied Mr.", "\"그렇단다, 아들아.\""], ["Squeers, in a sentimental voice.", "스퀴어스 선생이 감격에 찬 목소리로 대답했어."]], [["‘Oh my eye, won’t I give it to the boys!’ exclaimed the interesting child, grasping his father’s cane.", "\"우와, 그럼 제가 애들을 흠씬 패 줄 거예요!\" 그 사랑스러운 아이가 아버지의 회초리를 움켜쥐며 외쳤어."], ["‘Oh, father, won’t I make ‘em squeak again!’", "\"오, 아버지, 제가 그놈들을 또 찍소리 못 하게 만들 거라고요!\""]], [["It was a proud moment in Mr.", "스퀴어스 선생의 인생에서 그건 자랑스러운 순간이었어."], ["Squeers’s life, when he witnessed that burst of enthusiasm in his young child’s mind, and saw in it a foreshadowing of his future eminence.", "어린 자식의 마음속에서 그렇게 열정이 터져 나오는 걸 보고, 거기서 아이의 장래가 훤히 빛날 조짐을 읽었거든."], ["He pressed a penny into his hand, and gave vent to his feelings (as did his exemplary wife also), in a shout of approving laughter.", "그는 아이 손에 일 페니를 꼭 쥐여 주었어. 그러고는 (본받을 만한 부인도 그랬듯이) 흐뭇하게 껄껄 웃으며 감정을 쏟아 냈지."], ["The infantine appeal to their common sympathies, at once restored cheerfulness to the conversation, and harmony to the company.", "부모 둘의 공통된 마음을 건드린 그 어린애다운 호소 덕분에, 대화에는 다시 화기가, 자리에는 다시 화목이 돌아왔어."]], [["‘He’s a nasty stuck-up monkey, that’s what I consider him,’ said Mrs Squeers, reverting to Nicholas.", "\"그 작자는 거들먹거리는 더러운 원숭이예요, 내 보기엔 그래요.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 다시 니콜라스 이야기로 돌아가며 말했어."]], [["‘Supposing he is,’ said Squeers, ‘he is as well stuck up in our schoolroom as anywhere else, isn’t he?--especially as he don’t like it.’", "\"설령 그렇다 쳐도,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"우리 교실에 처박혀 거들먹거리는 게 다른 데서 그러는 것보다야 낫지 않소? 더구나 본인이 그걸 싫어하니까 말이오.\""]], [["‘Well,’ observed Mrs.", "\"하긴,\""], ["Squeers, ‘there’s something in that.", "스퀴어스 부인이 말했어. \"그건 일리가 있네요."], ["I hope it’ll bring his pride down, and it shall be no fault of mine if it don’t.’", "그 작자 콧대가 꺾였으면 좋겠어요. 안 꺾이면 그건 내 탓이 아닐 거예요.\""]], [["Now, a proud usher in a Yorkshire school was such a very extraordinary and unaccountable thing to hear of,--any usher at all being a novelty; but a proud one, a being of whose existence the wildest imagination could never have dreamed--that Miss Squeers, who seldom troubled herself with scholastic matters, inquired with much curiosity who this Knuckleboy was, that gave himself such airs.", "자, 그런데 요크셔의 학교에 콧대 높은 조교가 있다는 건 정말이지 듣도 보도 못한, 설명할 길 없는 일이었어. 조교라는 것 자체가 신기한 일인데, 콧대 높은 조교라니, 제아무리 엉뚱한 상상으로도 그런 존재는 꿈조차 못 꿀 정도였지. 그래서 평소에 학교 일에는 좀처럼 신경 쓰지 않던 스퀴어스 양도, 그렇게 거드름을 피운다는 그 너클보이가 대체 누구냐고 호기심에 차서 물었어."]], [["‘Nickleby,’ said Squeers, spelling the name according to some eccentric system which prevailed in his own mind; ‘your mother always calls things and people by their wrong names.’", "\"니클비란다.\" 스퀴어스가 자기 머릿속에만 있는 어떤 별난 방식으로 이름을 또박또박 말했어. \"네 어미는 늘 물건이고 사람이고 이름을 엉터리로 부른단 말이야.\""]], [["‘No matter for that,’ said Mrs.", "\"그건 아무래도 좋아요.\""], ["Squeers; ‘I see them with right eyes, and that’s quite enough for me.", "스퀴어스 부인이 말했어. \"난 멀쩡한 눈으로 그것들을 보니까, 그걸로 충분하다고요."], ["I watched him when you were laying on to little Bolder this afternoon.", "오늘 오후에 당신이 어린 볼더를 두들겨 팰 때 그 작자를 지켜봤어요."], ["He looked as black as thunder, all the while, and, one time, started up as if he had more than got it in his mind to make a rush at you.", "그동안 내내 천둥처럼 시커먼 얼굴을 하고 있더라고요. 한번은 벌떡 일어서기까지 했어요, 당신한테 달려들 작정이라도 한 것처럼 말이에요."], ["I saw him, though he thought I didn’t.’", "그 작자는 내가 못 본 줄 알았겠지만, 난 다 봤다고요.\""]], [["‘Never mind that, father,’ said Miss Squeers, as the head of the family was about to reply.", "\"그건 됐고요, 아버지.\" 집안의 가장이 막 대꾸하려는데 스퀴어스 양이 끼어들었어."], ["‘Who is the man?’", "\"그 사람이 누구예요?\""]], [["‘Why, your father has got some nonsense in his head that he’s the son of a poor gentleman that died the other day,’ said Mrs.", "\"글쎄, 네 아비가 무슨 말도 안 되는 생각을 머릿속에 담아 두고 있는데, 그 작자가 며칠 전에 죽은 어떤 가난한 신사의 아들이라는 거야.\""], ["Squeers.", "스퀴어스 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘The son of a gentleman!’", "\"신사의 아들이라고요!\""]], [["‘Yes; but I don’t believe a word of it.", "\"그래, 그렇지만 난 그 말을 한마디도 안 믿어."], ["If he’s a gentleman’s son at all, he’s a fondling, that’s my opinion.’", "그 작자가 정말로 신사의 아들이라면, 그건 사생애지, 그게 내 생각이야.\""]], [["‘Mrs.", "스퀴어스 부인은 '사생아'라고 말하려던 거였어."], ["Squeers intended to say ‘foundling,’ but, as she frequently remarked when she made any such mistake, it would be all the same a hundred years hence; with which axiom of philosophy, indeed, she was in the constant habit of consoling the boys when they laboured under more than ordinary ill-usage.", "하지만 그런 실수를 할 때마다 자주 말했듯이, 백 년 뒤에는 어차피 다 똑같다는 거였지. 사실 부인은 이 철학적 격언을 가지고, 아이들이 유난히 심한 학대에 시달릴 때면 늘 위로해 주는 버릇이 있었어."]], [["‘He’s nothing of the kind,’ said Squeers, in answer to the above remark, ‘for his father was married to his mother years before he was born, and she is alive now. If he was, it would be no business of ours, for we make a very good friend by having him here; and if he likes to learn the boys anything besides minding them, I have no objection I am sure.’", "\"그런 게 전혀 아니오.\" 스퀴어스가 위의 말에 대답했어. \"왜냐하면 그 애 아비는 그 애가 태어나기 한참 전에 그 애 어미랑 결혼했고, 그 어미는 지금도 살아 있으니까. 설령 사생아라 해도 우리가 알 바 아니지. 우린 그 사람을 여기 둠으로써 아주 좋은 친구를 얻는 셈이니까 말이야. 그리고 애들을 돌보는 것 말고도 뭔가 더 가르치고 싶다면, 나야 정말이지 반대할 게 없지.\""]], [["‘I say again, I hate him worse than poison,’ said Mrs.", "\"다시 말하지만, 난 그 작자가 독약보다 더 싫어요.\""], ["Squeers vehemently.", "스퀴어스 부인이 사납게 말했어."]], [["‘If you dislike him, my dear,’ returned Squeers, ‘I don’t know anybody who can show dislike better than you, and of course there’s no occasion, with him, to take the trouble to hide it.’", "\"당신이 그 사람을 싫어한다면, 여보,\" 스퀴어스가 받았어. \"싫은 티를 당신만큼 잘 내는 사람을 난 본 적이 없소. 그러니 그 사람한테야 굳이 애써 숨길 필요도 없겠지.\""]], [["‘I don’t intend to, I assure you,’ interposed Mrs.", "\"숨길 생각 없어요, 정말이지.\""], ["S.", "스퀴어스 부인이 끼어들었어."]], [["‘That’s right,’ said Squeers; ‘and if he has a touch of pride about him, as I think he has, I don’t believe there’s woman in all England that can bring anybody’s spirit down, as quick as you can, my love.’", "\"그게 옳지.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"그리고 내 생각엔 그 사람한테 콧대 같은 게 좀 있는 것 같은데, 영국 천지에 당신만큼 누군가의 기를 빨리 꺾을 수 있는 여자는 없을 거요, 여보.\""]], [["Mrs. Squeers chuckled vastly on the receipt of these flattering compliments, and said, she hoped she had tamed a high spirit or two in her day.", "스퀴어스 부인은 이 듣기 좋은 칭찬을 받고 한껏 낄낄 웃으며, 자기도 살면서 드센 기 한둘쯤은 길들여 봤기를 바란다고 말했어."], ["It is but due to her character to say, that in conjunction with her estimable husband, she had broken many and many a one.", "부인의 성격을 두고 공평하게 말하자면, 존경스러운 남편과 손발을 맞춰서 정말이지 수도 없이 많은 아이의 기를 꺾어 놓았지."]], [["Miss Fanny Squeers carefully treasured up this, and much more conversation on the same subject, until she retired for the night, when she questioned the hungry servant, minutely, regarding the outward appearance and demeanour of Nicholas; to which queries the girl returned such enthusiastic replies, coupled with so many laudatory remarks touching his beautiful dark eyes, and his sweet smile, and his straight legs--upon which last-named articles she laid particular stress; the general run of legs at Dotheboys Hall being crooked--that Miss Squeers was not long in arriving at the conclusion that the new usher must be a very remarkable person, or, as she herself significantly phrased it, ‘something quite out of the common.’ And so Miss Squeers made up her mind that she would take a personal observation of Nicholas the very next day.", "패니 스퀴어스 양은 이 이야기를, 또 같은 화제로 오간 더 많은 이야기를 잘 새겨 두었어. 그리고 밤이 되어 잠자리에 들 때, 굶주린 하녀를 붙잡고 니콜라스의 겉모습과 태도에 대해 시시콜콜 캐물었지. 하녀는 그 물음에 열을 올리며 대답했어. 그의 아름다운 검은 눈, 다정한 미소, 그리고 곧게 뻗은 다리에 대해 칭찬을 한가득 늘어놓았지. 특히 그 다리 이야기에 유난히 힘을 줬어. 두더보이즈 홀의 다리들은 대체로 휘어 있었으니까. 그러니 스퀴어스 양은 오래지 않아 결론에 다다랐지. 새로 온 조교는 아주 비범한 사람임이 틀림없다고, 아니 그 여자가 의미심장하게 표현한 대로 '아주 흔치 않은 무언가'임이 틀림없다고 말이야. 그래서 스퀴어스 양은 바로 다음 날 니콜라스를 직접 살펴보기로 마음먹었어."]], [["In pursuance of this design, the young lady watched the opportunity of her mother being engaged, and her father absent, and went accidentally into the schoolroom to get a pen mended: where, seeing nobody but Nicholas presiding over the boys, she blushed very deeply, and exhibited great confusion.", "이 계획을 실행에 옮기려고, 이 아가씨는 어머니가 일에 매여 있고 아버지가 자리를 비운 틈을 노렸어. 그러고는 펜을 고친다는 핑계로 우연인 척 교실에 들어갔지. 그런데 거기서 아이들을 맡고 있는 게 니콜라스밖에 없는 걸 보고는, 얼굴이 빨갛게 달아올라 어쩔 줄 몰라 했어."]], [["‘I beg your pardon,’ faltered Miss Squeers; ‘I thought my father was--or might be--dear me, how very awkward!’", "\"실례했어요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 더듬거렸어. \"저는 아버지가 계신 줄, 아니면 계실지도 모른다고 생각해서, 어머나, 정말 난처하네!\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"스퀴어스 선생님은 나가셨어요.\""], ["Squeers is out,’ said Nicholas, by no means overcome by the apparition, unexpected though it was.", "니콜라스가 말했어. 예기치 못한 등장이었지만, 그는 그 출현에 조금도 마음이 흔들리지 않았어."]], [["‘Do you know will he be long, sir?’ asked Miss Squeers, with bashful hesitation.", "\"오래 걸리실까요, 선생님?\" 스퀴어스 양이 수줍게 머뭇거리며 물었어."]], [["‘He said about an hour,’ replied Nicholas--politely of course, but without any indication of being stricken to the heart by Miss Squeers’s charms.", "\"한 시간쯤이라고 하셨어요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. 물론 예의 바르게였지만, 스퀴어스 양의 매력에 가슴이 찔린 기색은 전혀 없었지."]], [["‘I never knew anything happen so cross,’ exclaimed the young lady.", "\"이렇게 어긋나는 일은 처음이네요.\" 아가씨가 외쳤어."], ["‘Thank you!", "\"고마워요!"], ["I am very sorry I intruded, I am sure.", "방해해서 정말 죄송해요. 아버지가 여기 계신 줄 알았으니 망정이지, 안 그랬으면 무슨 일이 있어도, 정말 약 오르네, 너무 이상해 보이겠어요.\""], ["If I hadn’t thought my father was here, I wouldn’t upon any account have--it is very provoking--must look so very strange,’ murmured Miss Squeers, blushing once more, and glancing, from the pen in her hand, to Nicholas at his desk, and back again.", "스퀴어스 양이 다시 얼굴을 붉히며 중얼거렸어. 그러고는 손에 든 펜에서 책상에 앉은 니콜라스로, 또 다시 펜으로 눈길을 옮겼어."]], [["‘If that is all you want,’ said Nicholas, pointing to the pen, and smiling, in spite of himself, at the affected embarrassment of the schoolmaster’s daughter, ‘perhaps I can supply his place.’", "\"필요하신 게 그것뿐이라면,\" 니콜라스가 펜을 가리키며 말했어. 그리고 교장 딸의 꾸민 듯한 어색함에 저도 모르게 미소를 지었지. \"제가 선생님 대신 해 드릴 수 있을 것 같은데요.\""]], [["Miss Squeers glanced at the door, as if dubious of the propriety of advancing any nearer to an utter stranger; then round the schoolroom, as though in some measure reassured by the presence of forty boys; and finally sidled up to Nicholas and delivered the pen into his hand, with a most winning mixture of reserve and condescension.", "스퀴어스 양은 문 쪽을 흘끗 봤어. 생판 모르는 남자한테 더 가까이 다가가도 괜찮을지 망설이는 것 같았지. 그러다 교실을 한 바퀴 둘러봤어. 마흔 명이나 되는 아이들이 있다는 데 어느 정도 안심이 된 모양이었어. 그러고는 마침내 니콜라스 옆으로 슬그머니 다가가, 도도함과 너그러움이 더없이 매력적으로 뒤섞인 태도로 펜을 그의 손에 건넸지."]], [["‘Shall it be a hard or a soft nib?’ inquired Nicholas, smiling to prevent himself from laughing outright.", "\"펜촉을 단단하게 할까요, 부드럽게 할까요?\" 니콜라스가 그만 웃음을 터뜨리지 않으려고 미소를 지으며 물었어."]], [["‘He HAS a beautiful smile,’ thought Miss Squeers.", "'정말 아름다운 미소를 가졌어.' 스퀴어스 양은 생각했어."]], [["‘Which did you say?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"뭐라고 하셨죠?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘Dear me, I was thinking of something else for the moment, I declare,’ replied Miss Squeers.", "\"어머나, 잠깐 딴생각을 했나 봐요, 정말.\" 스퀴어스 양이 대답했어."], ["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["as soft as possible, if you please.’ With which words, Miss Squeers sighed.", "되도록 부드럽게 해 주세요, 부탁이에요.\" 이렇게 말하고 스퀴어스 양은 한숨을 쉬었어."], ["It might be, to give Nicholas to understand that her heart was soft, and that the pen was wanted to match.", "어쩌면 자기 마음이 부드러우니 펜도 거기 어울려야 한다는 걸 니콜라스한테 알려 주려는 거였는지도 몰라."]], [["Upon these instructions Nicholas made the pen; when he gave it to Miss Squeers, Miss Squeers dropped it; and when he stooped to pick it up, Miss Squeers stooped also, and they knocked their heads together; whereat five-and-twenty little boys laughed aloud: being positively for the first and only time that half-year.", "니콜라스는 그 주문대로 펜을 만들었어. 그가 펜을 건네자 스퀴어스 양이 그걸 떨어뜨렸지. 그가 주우려고 몸을 숙이자 스퀴어스 양도 같이 숙였고, 둘은 머리를 부딪쳤어. 그 바람에 어린 사내아이 스물다섯 명이 깔깔 웃음을 터뜨렸지. 그게 그 학기 통틀어 처음이자 단 한 번 있는 일이었어."]], [["‘Very awkward of me,’ said Nicholas, opening the door for the young lady’s retreat.", "\"제가 정말 서툴렀네요.\" 니콜라스가 아가씨가 나갈 수 있게 문을 열어 주며 말했어."]], [["‘Not at all, sir,’ replied Miss Squeers; ‘it was my fault.", "\"천만에요, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스 양이 대답했어."], ["It was all my foolish--a--a--good-morning!’", "\"제 잘못이에요. 다 제가 어리석어서, 어, 어, 안녕히 계세요!\""]], [["‘Goodbye,’ said Nicholas.", "\"안녕히 가세요.\""], ["‘The next I make for you, I hope will be made less clumsily.", "니콜라스가 말했어. \"다음에 만들어 드릴 땐 좀 덜 서툴렀으면 좋겠네요."], ["Take care!", "조심하세요!"], ["You are biting the nib off now.’", "지금 펜촉을 깨물고 계세요.\""]], [["‘Really,’ said Miss Squeers; ‘so embarrassing that I scarcely know what I--very sorry to give you so much trouble.’", "\"정말이지,\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어. \"너무 당황스러워서 제가 무슨, 이렇게 폐를 끼쳐서 정말 죄송해요.\""]], [["‘Not the least trouble in the world,’ replied Nicholas, closing the schoolroom door.", "\"조금도 폐될 거 없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 교실 문을 닫으며 대답했어."]], [["‘I never saw such legs in the whole course of my life!’ said Miss Squeers, as she walked away.", "\"내 평생 그런 다리는 처음 봤어!\" 스퀴어스 양이 걸어가며 말했어."]], [["In fact, Miss Squeers was in love with Nicholas Nickleby.", "사실 스퀴어스 양은 니콜라스 니클비에게 반해 버린 거였어."]], [["To account for the rapidity with which this young lady had conceived a passion for Nicholas, it may be necessary to state, that the friend from whom she had so recently returned, was a miller’s daughter of only eighteen, who had contracted herself unto the son of a small corn-factor, resident in the nearest market town. Miss Squeers and the miller’s daughter, being fast friends, had covenanted together some two years before, according to a custom prevalent among young ladies, that whoever was first engaged to be married, should straightway confide the mighty secret to the bosom of the other, before communicating it to any living soul, and bespeak her as bridesmaid without loss of time; in fulfilment of which pledge the miller’s daughter, when her engagement was formed, came out express, at eleven o’clock at night as the corn-factor’s son made an offer of his hand and heart at twenty-five minutes past ten by the Dutch clock in the kitchen, and rushed into Miss Squeers’s bedroom with the gratifying intelligence. Now, Miss Squeers being five years older, and out of her teens (which is also a great matter), had, since, been more than commonly anxious to return the compliment, and possess her friend with a similar secret; but, either in consequence of finding it hard to please herself, or harder still to please anybody else, had never had an opportunity so to do, inasmuch as she had no such secret to disclose. The little interview with Nicholas had no sooner passed, as above described, however, than Miss Squeers, putting on her bonnet, made her way, with great precipitation, to her friend’s house, and, upon a solemn renewal of divers old vows of secrecy, revealed how that she was--not exactly engaged, but going to be--to a gentleman’s son--(none of your corn-factors, but a gentleman’s son of high descent)--who had come down as teacher to Dotheboys Hall, under most mysterious and remarkable circumstances--indeed, as Miss Squeers more than once hinted she had good reason to believe, induced, by the fame of her many charms, to seek her out, and woo and win her.", "이 아가씨가 어쩌면 그렇게 빨리 니콜라스한테 마음을 품게 됐는지 설명하려면, 이 말부터 해 둬야겠어. 그 여자가 얼마 전에 다녀온 친구는 방앗간집 딸인데, 겨우 열여덟 살이었어. 그 애는 가장 가까운 장터 마을에 사는 작은 곡물 상인의 아들과 약혼한 사이였지. 스퀴어스 양과 방앗간집 딸은 단짝이었는데, 한 이 년쯤 전에 둘이서 약속을 했어. 젊은 아가씨들 사이에 흔한 풍습대로, 둘 중 누구든 먼저 약혼하게 되면 다른 누구한테 알리기 전에 곧장 그 엄청난 비밀을 상대에게 털어놓고, 지체 없이 신부 들러리로 청하기로 한 거였지. 그 약속대로 방앗간집 딸은 약혼이 성사되자마자 부랴부랴 달려왔어. 부엌의 네덜란드 시계로 열 시 이십오 분에 곡물 상인의 아들이 손과 마음을 바치겠다고 청혼했고, 밤 열한 시에 그 딸이 스퀴어스 양의 침실로 뛰어들어 그 기쁜 소식을 전한 거였지. 그런데 스퀴어스 양은 다섯 살이 더 많은 데다 이미 십 대를 벗어난 처지였어 (이것도 큰 문제였지). 그래서 그 뒤로 그 답례를 하고 싶어, 그러니까 비슷한 비밀로 친구를 놀라게 해 주고 싶어 보통 이상으로 안달이 났지. 하지만 자기 마음에 드는 상대를 찾기가 어려웠는지, 아니면 남의 마음에 들기가 더 어려웠는지, 그럴 기회를 도무지 잡지 못했어. 털어놓을 비밀이 아예 없었으니까. 그런데 앞에서 말한 대로 니콜라스와 그 짧은 만남을 가지자마자, 스퀴어스 양은 모자를 쓰고 부랴부랴 친구네 집으로 달려갔어. 그리고 비밀을 지키겠다는 묵은 맹세를 여러 번 엄숙히 새로 다진 뒤, 털어놓았지. 자기가, 정확히 약혼한 건 아니지만 곧 약혼하게 될 거라고, 그것도 신사의 아들과 (곡물 상인 따위가 아니라 가문 높은 신사의 아들이지) 말이야. 그 사람은 아주 신비롭고 놀라운 사정으로 두더보이즈 홀에 선생으로 내려왔는데, 사실 스퀴어스 양이 한두 번 넌지시 비친 바로는, 그 사람이 자기의 수많은 매력에 대한 소문에 이끌려 자기를 찾아와 구애하고 사로잡으려 온 거라고 믿을 만한 충분한 근거가 있다는 거였어."]], [["‘Isn’t it an extraordinary thing?’ said Miss Squeers, emphasising the adjective strongly.", "\"정말 놀라운 일 아니니?\" 스퀴어스 양이 그 형용사에 잔뜩 힘을 주며 말했어."]], [["‘Most extraordinary,’ replied the friend.", "\"정말 놀랍다.\" 친구가 대답했어."], ["‘But what has he said to you?’", "\"그런데 그 사람이 너한테 뭐라고 했는데?\""]], [["‘Don’t ask me what he said, my dear,’ rejoined Miss Squeers.", "\"무슨 말을 했는지는 묻지 마, 얘.\" 스퀴어스 양이 말을 받았어."], ["‘If you had only seen his looks and smiles!", "\"그 눈빛이랑 미소를 너도 봤어야 하는데!"], ["I never was so overcome in all my life.’", "내 평생 그렇게 마음이 흔들린 적은 처음이야.\""]], [["‘Did he look in this way?’ inquired the miller’s daughter, counterfeiting, as nearly as she could, a favourite leer of the corn-factor.", "\"이런 식으로 쳐다봤니?\" 방앗간집 딸이 곡물 상인이 즐겨 짓던 곁눈질을 할 수 있는 한 흉내 내며 물었어."]], [["‘Very like that--only more genteel,’ replied Miss Squeers.", "\"그거랑 아주 비슷한데, 좀 더 점잖았어.\" 스퀴어스 양이 대답했어."]], [["‘Ah!’ said the friend, ‘then he means something, depend on it.’", "\"아!\" 친구가 말했어. \"그럼 뭔가 마음이 있는 거야, 틀림없어.\""]], [["Miss Squeers, having slight misgivings on the subject, was by no means ill pleased to be confirmed by a competent authority; and discovering, on further conversation and comparison of notes, a great many points of resemblance between the behaviour of Nicholas, and that of the corn-factor, grew so exceedingly confidential, that she intrusted her friend with a vast number of things Nicholas had NOT said, which were all so very complimentary as to be quite conclusive. Then, she dilated on the fearful hardship of having a father and mother strenuously opposed to her intended husband; on which unhappy circumstance she dwelt at great length; for the friend’s father and mother were quite agreeable to her being married, and the whole courtship was in consequence as flat and common-place an affair as it was possible to imagine.", "스퀴어스 양은 이 일을 두고 살짝 미심쩍은 마음이 있던 터라, 믿을 만한 사람한테 확인을 받으니 영 싫지 않았어. 그리고 이야기를 더 나누고 서로 견주어 보면서, 니콜라스의 행동과 곡물 상인의 행동 사이에 닮은 점이 한둘이 아니라는 걸 알게 됐지. 그러자 어찌나 속을 다 터놓게 됐던지, 니콜라스가 하지도 않은 말을 잔뜩 친구한테 들려줬어. 그게 죄다 어찌나 듣기 좋은 말들이던지, 결정적인 증거나 다름없었지. 그러고는 자기의 장래 남편을 부모가 한사코 반대한다는, 그 무시무시한 고초에 대해 늘어놓았어. 이 가엾은 처지를 한참이나 곱씹었지. 친구의 부모는 딸이 결혼하는 데 아주 흔쾌히 찬성하는 터라, 그 연애 전체가 더없이 밋밋하고 평범한 일이었거든."]], [["‘How I should like to see him!’ exclaimed the friend.", "\"그 사람을 정말 보고 싶다!\" 친구가 외쳤어."]], [["‘So you shall, ‘Tilda,’ replied Miss Squeers.", "\"보게 될 거야, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 대답했어."], ["‘I should consider myself one of the most ungrateful creatures alive, if I denied you.", "\"내가 널 안 보여 주면 세상에서 제일 배은망덕한 사람일 거야. 어머니가 이틀쯤 아이들 데리러 떠나실 것 같아."], ["I think mother’s going away for two days to fetch some boys; and when she does, I’ll ask you and John up to tea, and have him to meet you.’", "그때 너랑 존을 차 마시러 부를게. 그 사람도 와서 너희를 만나게 할 거야.\""]], [["This was a charming idea, and having fully discussed it, the friends parted.", "이건 근사한 생각이었어. 그래서 둘이서 그 일을 실컷 의논한 뒤 헤어졌지."]], [["It so fell out, that Mrs.", "마침 일이 그렇게 됐어."], ["Squeers’s journey, to some distance, to fetch three new boys, and dun the relations of two old ones for the balance of a small account, was fixed that very afternoon, for the next day but one; and on the next day but one, Mrs.", "스퀴어스 부인이 새 아이 셋을 데려오고, 옛날 아이 둘의 친척한테 자잘한 외상값 잔금을 받아 내러 꽤 먼 길을 떠나는 게 바로 그날 오후에 정해졌거든. 그것도 모레 날짜로 말이야."], ["Squeers got up outside the coach, as it stopped to change at Greta Bridge, taking with her a small bundle containing something in a bottle, and some sandwiches, and carrying besides a large white top-coat to wear in the night-time; with which baggage she went her way.", "그리고 모레가 되자, 스퀴어스 부인은 그레타 다리에서 마차가 말을 바꾸려고 멈춘 사이에 마차 바깥자리에 올라탔어. 병에 든 무언가와 샌드위치가 든 작은 보따리를 챙기고, 거기다 밤에 입을 커다란 흰색 외투까지 들고서, 그 짐들을 갖고 길을 떠났지."]], [["Whenever such opportunities as these occurred, it was Squeers’s custom to drive over to the market town, every evening, on pretence of urgent business, and stop till ten or eleven o’clock at a tavern he much affected.", "이런 기회가 생길 때마다 스퀴어스는 매일 저녁 급한 일이 있다는 핑계로 장터 마을까지 마차를 몰고 가, 단골 술집에서 열 시나 열한 시까지 죽치는 버릇이 있었어. 그러니 이번 모임은 그에게 거슬리기는커녕 오히려 딸과 타협하는 구실이 됐지."], ["As the party was not in his way, therefore, but rather afforded a means of compromise with Miss Squeers, he readily yielded his full assent thereunto, and willingly communicated to Nicholas that he was expected to take his tea in the parlour that evening, at five o’clock.", "그래서 그는 선뜻 흔쾌히 동의해 주고, 니콜라스한테도 기꺼이 알렸어. 그날 저녁 다섯 시에 응접실에서 차를 마시러 와야 한다고 말이야."]], [["To be sure Miss Squeers was in a desperate flutter as the time approached, and to be sure she was dressed out to the best advantage: with her hair--it had more than a tinge of red, and she wore it in a crop--curled in five distinct rows, up to the very top of her head, and arranged dexterously over the doubtful eye; to say nothing of the blue sash which floated down her back, or the worked apron or the long gloves, or the green gauze scarf worn over one shoulder and under the other; or any of the numerous devices which were to be as so many arrows to the heart of Nicholas. She had scarcely completed these arrangements to her entire satisfaction, when the friend arrived with a whity-brown parcel--flat and three-cornered--containing sundry small adornments which were to be put on upstairs, and which the friend put on, talking incessantly. When Miss Squeers had ‘done’ the friend’s hair, the friend ‘did’ Miss Squeers’s hair, throwing in some striking improvements in the way of ringlets down the neck; and then, when they were both touched up to their entire satisfaction, they went downstairs in full state with the long gloves on, all ready for company.", "물론 시간이 다가오자 스퀴어스 양은 안절부절못하며 잔뜩 들떴어. 그리고 물론 한껏 멋을 부려 차려입었지. 머리는, 불그스름한 기가 꽤 도는 짧은 머리였는데, 다섯 줄로 또렷이 곱슬곱슬 말아 정수리 꼭대기까지 올렸어. 그리고 그 미심쩍은 눈을 솜씨 좋게 가리도록 매만졌지. 등 뒤로 늘어뜨린 파란 띠며, 수놓은 앞치마며, 긴 장갑이며, 한쪽 어깨 위와 다른 쪽 어깨 아래로 두른 초록 망사 스카프는 말할 것도 없었어. 그밖에도 니콜라스의 심장을 향해 쏠 화살이 되어 줄 온갖 장치들이 있었지. 그 여자가 이 차림을 완전히 흡족할 만큼 막 끝냈을 때, 친구가 도착했어. 누르스름한 갈색의, 납작하고 세모난 꾸러미를 들고서 말이야. 그 안에는 위층에서 달 자잘한 장식들이 들어 있었지. 친구는 쉴 새 없이 떠들며 그것들을 달았어. 스퀴어스 양이 친구 머리를 '해 주자', 친구도 스퀴어스 양 머리를 '해 줬어'. 목덜미로 곱슬머리를 늘어뜨리는 식으로 눈에 띄게 멋도 좀 더했지. 그러고는 둘 다 완전히 만족스럽게 단장을 마치자, 긴 장갑을 끼고 한껏 차려입은 채 손님 맞을 채비를 다 갖추고 아래층으로 내려갔어."]], [["‘Where’s John, ‘Tilda?’ said Miss Squeers.", "\"존은 어딨니, 틸다?\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어."]], [["‘Only gone home to clean himself,’ replied the friend.", "\"잠깐 씻으러 집에 갔어.\" 친구가 대답했어."], ["‘He will be here by the time the tea’s drawn.’", "\"차가 우려질 때쯤이면 올 거야.\""]], [["‘I do so palpitate,’ observed Miss Squeers.", "\"가슴이 너무 두근거려.\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["I know what it is,’ replied the friend.", "그게 어떤 건지 나도 알지.\" 친구가 대답했어."]], [["‘I have not been used to it, you know, ‘Tilda,’ said Miss Squeers, applying her hand to the left side of her sash.", "\"난 이런 거에 익숙하질 않잖아, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 띠의 왼쪽에 손을 갖다 대며 말했어."]], [["‘You’ll soon get the better of it, dear,’ rejoined the friend.", "\"곧 괜찮아질 거야, 얘.\" 친구가 받았어."], ["While they were talking thus, the hungry servant brought in the tea-things, and, soon afterwards, somebody tapped at the room door.", "둘이 이렇게 이야기하는 동안, 굶주린 하녀가 찻그릇을 들여왔어. 그리고 곧 누군가 방문을 똑똑 두드렸지."]], [["‘There he is!’ cried Miss Squeers.", "\"그 사람이야!\" 스퀴어스 양이 외쳤어."], ["‘Oh ‘Tilda!’", "\"오, 틸다!\""]], [["‘Hush!’ said ‘Tilda.", "\"쉿!\" 틸다가 말했어."], ["‘Hem!", "\"에헴!"], ["Say, come in.’", "어서, 들어오시라고 해.\""]], [["‘Come in,’ cried Miss Squeers faintly.", "\"들어오세요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 가냘프게 외쳤어."], ["And in walked Nicholas.", "그러자 니콜라스가 걸어 들어왔지."]], [["‘Good-evening,’ said that young gentleman, all unconscious of his conquest.", "\"안녕하세요.\" 그 젊은 신사가 자기가 마음을 사로잡았다는 걸 까맣게 모른 채 말했어."], ["‘I understood from Mr. Squeers that--’", "\"스퀴어스 선생님한테 듣기로는······.\""]], [["‘Oh yes; it’s all right,’ interposed Miss Squeers.", "\"아 네, 다 됐어요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 끼어들었어."], ["‘Father don’t tea with us, but you won’t mind that, I dare say.’ (This was said archly.)", "\"아버지는 저희랑 차를 안 드시는데, 괜찮으시겠죠.\" (이건 짐짓 새침하게 한 말이었어.)"]], [["Nicholas opened his eyes at this, but he turned the matter off very coolly--not caring, particularly, about anything just then--and went through the ceremony of introduction to the miller’s daughter with so much grace, that that young lady was lost in admiration.", "니콜라스는 이 말에 눈을 동그랗게 떴지만, 아주 무심하게 그냥 넘겼어. 그땐 딱히 무슨 일에도 마음 쓸 여유가 없었거든. 그러고는 방앗간집 딸한테 소개받는 의례를 어찌나 우아하게 치렀던지, 그 아가씨가 그만 넋을 잃고 감탄했지."]], [["‘We are only waiting for one more gentleman,’ said Miss Squeers, taking off the teapot lid, and looking in, to see how the tea was getting on.", "\"신사 한 분만 더 기다리면 돼요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 찻주전자 뚜껑을 열고 차가 어떻게 우러나는지 들여다보며 말했어."]], [["It was matter of equal moment to Nicholas whether they were waiting for one gentleman or twenty, so he received the intelligence with perfect unconcern; and, being out of spirits, and not seeing any especial reason why he should make himself agreeable, looked out of the window and sighed involuntarily.", "니콜라스한테는 한 사람을 기다리든 스무 명을 기다리든 매한가지였어. 그래서 그 소식을 아무 관심 없이 받아들였지. 게다가 기분도 가라앉아 있었고, 굳이 상냥하게 굴 까닭도 딱히 없어서, 창밖을 내다보며 저도 모르게 한숨을 쉬었어."]], [["As luck would have it, Miss Squeers’s friend was of a playful turn, and hearing Nicholas sigh, she took it into her head to rally the lovers on their lowness of spirits.", "운이 그렇게 따랐는지, 스퀴어스 양의 친구는 장난기가 많은 성격이었어. 니콜라스가 한숨 쉬는 걸 듣고는, 두 연인이 기운 없어 보인다고 놀려 줄 생각이 머릿속에 떠올랐지."]], [["‘But if it’s caused by my being here,’ said the young lady, ‘don’t mind me a bit, for I’m quite as bad. You may go on just as you would if you were alone.’", "\"근데 제가 여기 있어서 그런 거면,\" 아가씨가 말했어. \"저는 조금도 신경 쓰지 마세요. 저도 똑같은 처지니까요. 두 분만 있을 때처럼 그냥 하셔도 돼요.\""]], [["‘’Tilda,’ said Miss Squeers, colouring up to the top row of curls, ‘I am ashamed of you;’ and here the two friends burst into a variety of giggles, and glanced from time to time, over the tops of their pocket-handkerchiefs, at Nicholas, who from a state of unmixed astonishment, gradually fell into one of irrepressible laughter--occasioned, partly by the bare notion of his being in love with Miss Squeers, and partly by the preposterous appearance and behaviour of the two girls. These two causes of merriment, taken together, struck him as being so keenly ridiculous, that, despite his miserable condition, he laughed till he was thoroughly exhausted.", "\"틸다,\" 스퀴어스 양이 곱슬머리 맨 윗줄까지 빨개지며 말했어. \"너 때문에 창피해 죽겠어.\" 그러고는 두 친구가 갖가지로 키득키득 웃음을 터뜨렸어. 그리고 이따금 손수건 너머로 니콜라스를 흘끔흘끔 봤지. 니콜라스는 그저 어리둥절하던 상태에서 차츰 도무지 참을 수 없는 웃음으로 빠져들었어. 그건 한편으로는 자기가 스퀴어스 양과 사랑에 빠졌다는 그 황당한 생각 때문이었고, 또 한편으로는 두 아가씨의 어처구니없는 차림새와 행동 때문이었지. 이 두 가지 우스운 까닭이 한데 겹치니, 어찌나 지독히 우스꽝스럽게 느껴지던지, 그는 그 비참한 처지에도 완전히 지칠 때까지 웃어 댔어."]], [["‘Well,’ thought Nicholas, ‘as I am here, and seem expected, for some reason or other, to be amiable, it’s of no use looking like a goose.", "'그래,' 니콜라스는 생각했어."], ["I may as well accommodate myself to the company.’", "'내가 여기 와 있고, 어쩐 일인지 상냥하게 굴기를 기대받는 모양이니, 멍청이처럼 굴어 봤자 소용없지. 차라리 이 자리에 어울려 주는 게 낫겠어.'"]], [["We blush to tell it; but his youthful spirits and vivacity getting, for the time, the better of his sad thoughts, he no sooner formed this resolution than he saluted Miss Squeers and the friend with great gallantry, and drawing a chair to the tea-table, began to make himself more at home than in all probability an usher has ever done in his employer’s house since ushers were first invented.", "이런 말을 하기 부끄럽지만, 그의 젊은 혈기와 발랄함이 잠시나마 슬픈 생각을 이겨 버렸어. 그래서 그는 이 결심을 하자마자 스퀴어스 양과 친구에게 한껏 멋들어지게 인사를 건넸지. 그리고 찻상으로 의자를 끌어당겨, 조교라는 게 처음 생겨난 이래로 고용주 집에서 그 어떤 조교도 못 해 봤을 만큼 제집처럼 편하게 굴기 시작했어."]], [["The ladies were in the full delight of this altered behaviour on the part of Mr.", "니클비 씨의 이 달라진 행동에 두 아가씨가 한껏 즐거워하고 있을 때, 기다리던 그 청년이 도착했어."], ["Nickleby, when the expected swain arrived, with his hair very damp from recent washing, and a clean shirt, whereof the collar might have belonged to some giant ancestor, forming, together with a white waistcoat of similar dimensions, the chief ornament of his person.", "머리는 방금 씻어서 흠뻑 젖어 있었고, 깨끗한 셔츠를 입었는데 그 옷깃은 어느 거인 조상한테나 어울렸을 법했지. 그것이 비슷한 크기의 흰 조끼와 함께 그의 몸을 장식하는 가장 큰 치장이었어."]], [["‘Well, John,’ said Miss Matilda Price (which, by-the-bye, was the name of the miller’s daughter).", "\"왔구나, 존.\" 마틸다 프라이스 양이 말했어. (참고로 이게 방앗간집 딸의 이름이었어.)"]], [["‘Weel,’ said John with a grin that even the collar could not conceal.", "\"왔다.\" 존이 그 옷깃으로도 가릴 수 없는 활짝 웃음을 지으며 말했어."]], [["‘I beg your pardon,’ interposed Miss Squeers, hastening to do the honours.", "\"실례했어요.\""], ["‘Mr. Nickleby--Mr.", "스퀴어스 양이 얼른 손님 예우를 갖추려고 끼어들었어."], ["John Browdie.’", "\"니클비 씨, 이쪽은 존 브라우디 씨예요.\""]], [["‘Servant, sir,’ said John, who was something over six feet high, with a face and body rather above the due proportion than below it.", "\"처음 뵙겠습니다, 선생님.\" 존이 말했어. 그는 키가 육 피트를 좀 넘었고, 얼굴이며 몸이며 적당한 균형보다는 큰 편이었지."]], [["‘Yours to command, sir,’ replied Nicholas, making fearful ravages on the bread and butter.", "\"잘 부탁드립니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답하며, 버터 바른 빵을 무섭게 해치웠어."]], [["Mr.", "브라우디 씨는 말솜씨가 뛰어난 신사가 아니었어."], ["Browdie was not a gentleman of great conversational powers, so he grinned twice more, and having now bestowed his customary mark of recognition on every person in company, grinned at nothing in particular, and helped himself to food.", "그래서 두 번 더 활짝 웃었지. 그리고 이제 자리에 있는 사람들 모두에게 으레 하는 인사를 마치자, 딱히 뭘 보고 웃는 것도 아니게 그냥 씩 웃고는 음식을 가져다 먹었어."]], [["‘Old wooman awa’, bean’t she?’ said Mr.", "\"마누라가 집을 비웠지, 안 그러우?\""], ["Browdie, with his mouth full.", "브라우디 씨가 입에 음식을 가득 문 채 말했어."]], [["Miss Squeers nodded assent.", "스퀴어스 양이 고개를 끄덕여 그렇다고 했어."]], [["Mr. Browdie gave a grin of special width, as if he thought that really was something to laugh at, and went to work at the bread and butter with increased vigour.", "브라우디 씨는 그게 정말 웃을 만한 일이라도 되는 양 유난히 입을 활짝 벌려 씩 웃었어."], ["It was quite a sight to behold how he and Nicholas emptied the plate between them.", "그러고는 한층 더 힘차게 버터 바른 빵을 공략했지. 그와 니콜라스가 둘이서 접시를 비워 가는 꼴은 그야말로 구경거리였어."]], [["‘Ye wean’t get bread and butther ev’ry neight, I expect, mun,’ said Mr Browdie, after he had sat staring at Nicholas a long time over the empty plate.", "\"매일 밤 버터 바른 빵을 얻어먹지는 못할 거유, 양반.\" 브라우디 씨가 빈 접시 너머로 한참이나 니콜라스를 빤히 쳐다보고 나서 말했어."]], [["Nicholas bit his lip, and coloured, but affected not to hear the remark.", "니콜라스는 입술을 깨물고 얼굴을 붉혔지만, 그 말을 못 들은 척했어."]], [["‘Ecod,’ said Mr.", "\"허 참,\""], ["Browdie, laughing boisterously, ‘they dean’t put too much intiv’em.", "브라우디 씨가 요란하게 웃으며 말했어. \"여긴 음식을 넉넉히 안 주거든."], ["Ye’ll be nowt but skeen and boans if you stop here long eneaf.", "여기 오래 붙어 있다간 가죽하고 뼈만 남을걸. 허!"], ["Ho!", "허!"], ["ho! ho!’", "허!\""]], [["‘You are facetious, sir,’ said Nicholas, scornfully.", "\"농담도 잘하시는군요, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 경멸하듯 말했어."]], [["‘Na; I dean’t know,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니, 난 잘 모르겠소만,\""], ["Browdie, ‘but t’oother teacher, ‘cod he wur a learn ‘un, he wur.’ The recollection of the last teacher’s leanness seemed to afford Mr.", "브라우디 씨가 대답했어. \"전에 있던 그 선생, 참 그 사람 비쩍 골았었지, 정말로.\""], ["Browdie the most exquisite delight, for he laughed until he found it necessary to apply his coat-cuffs to his eyes.", "전임 선생이 비쩍 골았던 기억이 브라우디 씨한테는 더없이 절묘한 기쁨을 주는 모양이었어. 그가 어찌나 웃어 대던지, 마침내 외투 소매로 눈가를 닦아야 할 지경이었지."]], [["‘I don’t know whether your perceptions are quite keen enough, Mr Browdie, to enable you to understand that your remarks are offensive,’ said Nicholas in a towering passion, ‘but if they are, have the goodness to--’", "\"브라우디 씨, 당신 지각이 충분히 예민해서 자기 말이 무례하다는 걸 알아챌 수 있을지 모르겠지만,\" 니콜라스가 잔뜩 화가 치밀어 말했어. \"만약 그렇다면, 부디·····.\""]], [["‘If you say another word, John,’ shrieked Miss Price, stopping her admirer’s mouth as he was about to interrupt, ‘only half a word, I’ll never forgive you, or speak to you again.’", "\"한마디라도 더 하면, 존,\" 프라이스 양이 자기 흠모자가 막 끼어들려는 입을 막으며 새된 소리를 질렀어. \"딱 반 마디라도 더 하면, 다시는 안 봐 주고 말도 안 할 거야.\""]], [["‘Weel, my lass, I dean’t care aboot ‘un,’ said the corn-factor, bestowing a hearty kiss on Miss Matilda; ‘let ‘un gang on, let ‘un gang on.’", "\"알았어, 내 색시, 난 저 사람 신경 안 써.\" 곡물 상인이 마틸다 양한테 진하게 입을 맞추며 말했어. \"떠들게 둬, 떠들게 두라고.\""]], [["It now became Miss Squeers’s turn to intercede with Nicholas, which she did with many symptoms of alarm and horror; the effect of the double intercession was, that he and John Browdie shook hands across the table with much gravity; and such was the imposing nature of the ceremonial, that Miss Squeers was overcome and shed tears.", "이제는 스퀴어스 양이 니콜라스를 말릴 차례였어. 그 여자는 잔뜩 놀라고 끔찍해하는 기색을 보이며 그렇게 했지. 양쪽에서 말린 결과, 그와 존 브라우디는 식탁 너머로 아주 엄숙하게 악수를 나눴어. 그런데 그 의식이 어찌나 거창했던지, 스퀴어스 양은 그만 감격해서 눈물을 흘리고 말았지."]], [["‘What’s the matter, Fanny?’ said Miss Price.", "\"왜 그래, 패니?\" 프라이스 양이 말했어."]], [["‘Nothing, ‘Tilda,’ replied Miss Squeers, sobbing.", "\"아무것도 아니야, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 흐느끼며 대답했어."]], [["‘There never was any danger,’ said Miss Price, ‘was there, Mr.", "\"애초에 위험할 거 하나도 없었어.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어."], ["Nickleby?’", "\"그렇죠, 니클비 씨?\""]], [["‘None at all,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"전혀 없었죠.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘Absurd.’", "\"터무니없는 일이에요.\""]], [["‘That’s right,’ whispered Miss Price, ‘say something kind to her, and she’ll soon come round.", "\"그래야죠.\" 프라이스 양이 속삭였어."], ["Here!", "\"쟤한테 다정하게 한마디 해 줘요, 그럼 금방 풀릴 거예요."], ["Shall John and I go into the little kitchen, and come back presently?’", "자! 존이랑 제가 저쪽 작은 부엌에 가 있다가 이따 돌아올까요?\""]], [["‘Not on any account,’ rejoined Nicholas, quite alarmed at the proposition.", "\"무슨 일이 있어도 안 됩니다.\" 니콜라스가 그 제안에 잔뜩 놀라 받아쳤어."], ["‘What on earth should you do that for?’", "\"도대체 왜 그런 짓을 한다는 거예요?\""]], [["‘Well,’ said Miss Price, beckoning him aside, and speaking with some degree of contempt--‘you ARE a one to keep company.’", "\"원 참,\" 프라이스 양이 그를 옆으로 부르며 좀 깔보는 투로 말했어. \"교제하는 데 참 별나신 분이네요.\""]], [["‘What do you mean?’ said Nicholas; ‘I am not a one to keep company at all--here at all events.", "\"무슨 말씀이세요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["I can’t make this out.’", "\"전 교제 같은 거 전혀 안 하는데요, 적어도 여기서는요. 도무지 무슨 영문인지 모르겠네요.\""]], [["‘No, nor I neither,’ rejoined Miss Price; ‘but men are always fickle, and always were, and always will be; that I can make out, very easily.’", "\"네, 저도 모르겠어요.\" 프라이스 양이 받았어. \"하지만 남자들은 늘 변덕스럽고, 늘 그래 왔고, 앞으로도 늘 그럴 거예요. 그건 제가 아주 쉽게 알겠네요.\""]], [["‘Fickle!’ cried Nicholas; ‘what do you suppose?", "\"변덕스럽다고요!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어."], ["You don’t mean to say that you think--’", "\"도대체 무슨 생각을 하시는 거예요? 설마 제가······.\""]], [["‘Oh no, I think nothing at all,’ retorted Miss Price, pettishly.", "\"아, 아뇨, 전 아무 생각도 안 해요.\" 프라이스 양이 토라진 듯 받아쳤어."], ["‘Look at her, dressed so beautiful and looking so well--really ALMOST handsome.", "\"쟤 좀 봐요, 저렇게 예쁘게 차려입고 저렇게 고와 보이는데, 정말 거의 미인이잖아요."], ["I am ashamed at you.’", "당신 때문에 제가 다 창피하네요.\""]], [["‘My dear girl, what have I got to do with her dressing beautifully or looking well?’ inquired Nicholas.", "\"아니 아가씨, 저 사람이 예쁘게 차려입든 고와 보이든 그게 저랑 무슨 상관이에요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘Come, don’t call me a dear girl,’ said Miss Price--smiling a little though, for she was pretty, and a coquette too in her small way, and Nicholas was good-looking, and she supposed him the property of somebody else, which were all reasons why she should be gratified to think she had made an impression on him,--‘or Fanny will be saying it’s my fault. Come; we’re going to have a game at cards.’ Pronouncing these last words aloud, she tripped away and rejoined the big Yorkshireman.", "\"이봐요, 아가씨라고 부르지 마세요.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어. 그래도 살짝 미소를 지었지. 그 여자는 예뻤고, 나름의 방식으로 살짝 요염한 데도 있었거든. 게다가 니콜라스는 잘생겼고, 다른 사람의 임자라고 여겼으니, 그한테 자기가 인상을 남겼다고 생각하면 흐뭇할 만한 이유가 다 갖춰진 셈이었지. \"안 그러면 패니가 다 내 탓이라고 할 거예요. 자, 우리 카드놀이나 해요.\" 마지막 말을 큰 소리로 하며, 그 여자는 사뿐사뿐 걸어가 그 덩치 큰 요크셔 남자한테 돌아갔어."]], [["This was wholly unintelligible to Nicholas, who had no other distinct impression on his mind at the moment, than that Miss Squeers was an ordinary-looking girl, and her friend Miss Price a pretty one; but he had not time to enlighten himself by reflection, for the hearth being by this time swept up, and the candle snuffed, they sat down to play speculation.", "니콜라스한테는 이게 도무지 알 수 없는 일이었어. 그 순간 그의 머릿속에 또렷이 남은 인상이라고는, 스퀴어스 양은 평범하게 생긴 여자고 그 친구 프라이스 양은 예쁜 여자라는 것뿐이었지. 하지만 곰곰이 생각해 깨우칠 틈도 없었어. 그새 난롯가도 다 쓸리고 촛불 심지도 잘려서, 다들 자리에 앉아 스페큘레이션 카드놀이를 시작했거든."]], [["‘There are only four of us, ‘Tilda,’ said Miss Squeers, looking slyly at Nicholas; ‘so we had better go partners, two against two.’", "\"우리 넷밖에 없네, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 니콜라스를 슬쩍 훔쳐보며 말했어. \"그러니 둘씩 짝을 지어서 둘 대 둘로 하는 게 낫겠다.\""]], [["‘What do you say, Mr.", "\"어떠세요, 니클비 씨?\""], ["Nickleby?’ inquired Miss Price.", "프라이스 양이 물었어."]], [["‘With all the pleasure in life,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"더없이 좋습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["And so saying, quite unconscious of his heinous offence, he amalgamated into one common heap those portions of a Dotheboys Hall card of terms, which represented his own counters, and those allotted to Miss Price, respectively.", "그러고는 자기가 저지른 끔찍한 잘못을 까맣게 모른 채, 두더보이즈 홀의 요금표 한 장을 잘라 만든 패 가운데 자기 몫과 프라이스 양 몫을 한 무더기로 합쳐 버렸어."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"브라우디 씨,\""], ["Browdie,’ said Miss Squeers hysterically, ‘shall we make a bank against them?’", "스퀴어스 양이 신경질적으로 말했어. \"우리가 저쪽에 맞서 한편 먹을까요?\""]], [["The Yorkshireman assented--apparently quite overwhelmed by the new usher’s impudence--and Miss Squeers darted a spiteful look at her friend, and giggled convulsively.", "요크셔 남자는 그러자고 했어. 새 조교의 뻔뻔함에 아주 어안이 벙벙해진 모양이었지. 스퀴어스 양은 친구한테 심술궂은 눈길을 휙 던지고는, 발작하듯 키득거렸어."]], [["The deal fell to Nicholas, and the hand prospered.", "패를 돌릴 차례가 니콜라스한테 돌아왔는데, 패가 잘 풀렸어."]], [["‘We intend to win everything,’ said he.", "\"우리가 죄다 따 버릴 작정입니다.\" 그가 말했어."]], [["‘’Tilda HAS won something she didn’t expect, I think, haven’t you, dear?’ said Miss Squeers, maliciously.", "\"틸다는 기대도 안 한 걸 좀 땄지, 안 그러니, 얘?\" 스퀴어스 양이 짓궂게 말했어."]], [["‘Only a dozen and eight, love,’ replied Miss Price, affecting to take the question in a literal sense.", "\"고작 한 다스에 여덟이야, 얘.\" 프라이스 양이 그 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들이는 척하며 대답했어."]], [["‘How dull you are tonight!’ sneered Miss Squeers.", "\"너 오늘 밤 왜 이렇게 굼떠!\" 스퀴어스 양이 비아냥거렸어."]], [["‘No, indeed,’ replied Miss Price, ‘I am in excellent spirits.", "\"아니거든.\" 프라이스 양이 대답했어."], ["I was thinking YOU seemed out of sorts.’", "\"난 기분 아주 좋은데. 오히려 네가 영 시무룩해 보여서 그랬지.\""]], [["‘Me!’ cried Miss Squeers, biting her lips, and trembling with very jealousy.", "\"내가!\" 스퀴어스 양이 입술을 깨물고 질투에 부들부들 떨며 외쳤어."], ["‘Oh no!’", "\"오, 아니야!\""]], [["‘That’s well,’ remarked Miss Price.", "\"그럼 다행이고.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어."], ["‘Your hair’s coming out of curl, dear.’", "\"얘, 네 머리 곱슬이 풀리고 있어.\""]], [["‘Never mind me,’ tittered Miss Squeers; ‘you had better attend to your partner.’", "\"내 걱정은 말고,\" 스퀴어스 양이 깔깔거렸어. \"네 짝꿍이나 신경 쓰지 그래.\""]], [["‘Thank you for reminding her,’ said Nicholas.", "\"일러 줘서 고마워요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘So she had.’", "\"정말 그래야겠네요.\""]], [["The Yorkshireman flattened his nose, once or twice, with his clenched fist, as if to keep his hand in, till he had an opportunity of exercising it upon the features of some other gentleman; and Miss Squeers tossed her head with such indignation, that the gust of wind raised by the multitudinous curls in motion, nearly blew the candle out.", "요크셔 남자는 다른 신사의 얼굴짝에 한 방 먹일 기회가 올 때까지 손이 굳지 않게 하려는 듯, 꽉 쥔 주먹으로 자기 코를 한두 번 납작하게 눌렀어. 그리고 스퀴어스 양이 어찌나 분에 차서 고개를 홱홱 젖혔던지, 그 수많은 곱슬머리가 일으킨 바람결에 촛불이 꺼질 뻔했지."]], [["‘I never had such luck, really,’ exclaimed coquettish Miss Price, after another hand or two.", "\"정말이지 이런 행운은 처음이에요.\" 요염한 프라이스 양이 한두 판 더 치르고 나서 외쳤어."], ["‘It’s all along of you, Mr. Nickleby, I think.", "\"다 당신 덕분인가 봐요, 니클비 씨."], ["I should like to have you for a partner always.’", "늘 당신이 제 짝꿍이면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["‘I wish you had.’", "\"저도 그랬으면 좋겠네요.\""]], [["‘You’ll have a bad wife, though, if you always win at cards,’ said Miss Price.", "\"하지만 카드를 늘 따면 아내 복은 없을 거예요.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어."]], [["‘Not if your wish is gratified,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"당신 소원이 이루어진다면 그렇지 않겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘I am sure I shall have a good one in that case.’", "\"그렇게 되면 분명 좋은 아내를 얻을 테니까요.\""]], [["To see how Miss Squeers tossed her head, and the corn-factor flattened his nose, while this conversation was carrying on!", "이 대화가 오가는 동안 스퀴어스 양이 고개를 어떻게 홱홱 젖혔는지, 그리고 곡물 상인이 코를 어떻게 납작하게 눌렀는지!"], ["It would have been worth a small annuity to have beheld that; let alone Miss Price’s evident joy at making them jealous, and Nicholas Nickleby’s happy unconsciousness of making anybody uncomfortable.", "그 꼴을 봤더라면 자그마한 연금만큼의 값어치는 했을 거야. 게다가 프라이스 양이 두 사람을 질투 나게 만들며 대놓고 즐거워하던 것도, 또 자기가 누군가를 불편하게 만들고 있다는 걸 까맣게 모르고 행복해하던 니콜라스 니클비도 다 볼만했지."]], [["‘We have all the talking to ourselves, it seems,’ said Nicholas, looking good-humouredly round the table as he took up the cards for a fresh deal.", "\"보아하니 우리끼리만 죄다 떠드는 것 같네요.\" 니콜라스가 새 판을 돌리려고 카드를 집으며 식탁을 빙 둘러보고 너스레를 떨었어."]], [["‘You do it so well,’ tittered Miss Squeers, ‘that it would be a pity to interrupt, wouldn’t it, Mr.", "\"워낙 잘하시니까,\" 스퀴어스 양이 깔깔거렸어."], ["Browdie? He!", "\"끼어드는 게 아깝죠, 안 그래요, 브라우디 씨?"], ["he!", "히!"], ["he!’", "히! 히!\""]], [["‘Nay,’ said Nicholas, ‘we do it in default of having anybody else to talk to.’", "\"천만에요,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"달리 이야기 나눌 사람이 없어서 우리끼리 하는 거죠.\""]], [["‘We’ll talk to you, you know, if you’ll say anything,’ said Miss Price.", "\"하고 싶은 말 있으면 우리가 들어 드릴게요.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어."]], [["‘Thank you, ‘Tilda, dear,’ retorted Miss Squeers, majestically.", "\"고맙다, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 도도하게 받아쳤어."]], [["‘Or you can talk to each other, if you don’t choose to talk to us,’ said Miss Price, rallying her dear friend.", "\"우리랑 이야기 나누기 싫으면 둘이서 이야기하든가.\" 프라이스 양이 그 소중한 친구를 놀려 댔어."], ["‘John, why don’t you say something?’", "\"존, 너는 왜 아무 말도 안 해?\""]], [["‘Say summat?’ repeated the Yorkshireman.", "\"뭔 말을 하라고?\" 요크셔 남자가 되물었어."]], [["‘Ay, and not sit there so silent and glum.’", "\"그래, 그렇게 말없이 시무룩하게 앉아만 있지 말고.\""]], [["‘Weel, then!’ said the Yorkshireman, striking the table heavily with his fist, ‘what I say’s this--Dang my boans and boddy, if I stan’ this ony longer. Do ye gang whoam wi’ me, and do yon loight an’ toight young whipster look sharp out for a brokken head, next time he cums under my hond.’", "\"좋아, 그럼!\" 요크셔 남자가 주먹으로 식탁을 쾅 내리치며 말했어. \"내 할 말은 이거야. 빌어먹을, 내 뼈고 몸뚱이고 다 부서져도 더는 이 꼴 못 참겠다. 자네, 나랑 같이 집에 가지. 그리고 저 까불대고 으스대는 애송이 녀석, 다음에 내 손에 걸리면 대가리 깨질 줄 알고 단단히 조심하라고 해.\""]], [["‘Mercy on us, what’s all this?’ cried Miss Price, in affected astonishment.", "\"세상에, 이게 다 무슨 소리예요?\" 프라이스 양이 짐짓 놀란 척 외쳤어."]], [["‘Cum whoam, tell ‘e, cum whoam,’ replied the Yorkshireman, sternly.", "\"가자고, 집에, 집에 가자니까.\" 요크셔 남자가 매섭게 대꾸했어."], ["And as he delivered the reply, Miss Squeers burst into a shower of tears; arising in part from desperate vexation, and in part from an impotent desire to lacerate somebody’s countenance with her fair finger-nails.", "그가 이렇게 대꾸하자, 스퀴어스 양은 와락 눈물을 쏟았어. 한편으로는 지독한 분풀이에서 나온 것이었고, 또 한편으로는 고운 손톱으로 누군가의 얼굴을 할퀴고 싶은데 그러지 못해 솟구친 눈물이었지."]], [["This state of things had been brought about by divers means and workings. Miss Squeers had brought it about, by aspiring to the high state and condition of being matrimonially engaged, without good grounds for so doing; Miss Price had brought it about, by indulging in three motives of action: first, a desire to punish her friend for laying claim to a rivalship in dignity, having no good title: secondly, the gratification of her own vanity, in receiving the compliments of a smart young man: and thirdly, a wish to convince the corn-factor of the great danger he ran, in deferring the celebration of their expected nuptials; while Nicholas had brought it about, by half an hour’s gaiety and thoughtlessness, and a very sincere desire to avoid the imputation of inclining at all to Miss Squeers. So the means employed, and the end produced, were alike the most natural in the world; for young ladies will look forward to being married, and will jostle each other in the race to the altar, and will avail themselves of all opportunities of displaying their own attractions to the best advantage, down to the very end of time, as they have done from its beginning.", "이 사달은 여러 가지 까닭과 작용이 얽혀 일어난 거였어. 스퀴어스 양은 마땅한 근거도 없이 약혼한 몸이라는 높은 지위에 오르려고 욕심을 부려 이 일을 빚었어. 프라이스 양은 세 가지 동기에 빠져 이 일을 빚었지. 첫째는 친구가 자격도 없이 자기랑 품위를 견주려 든 걸 혼내 주고 싶은 마음, 둘째는 똑똑한 젊은이의 찬사를 받으며 자기 허영을 채우는 즐거움, 셋째는 곡물 상인한테 그가 자기들 혼례를 미루다가는 얼마나 큰 위험을 무릅쓰는 셈인지 일깨워 주고 싶은 마음이었어. 한편 니콜라스는 삼십 분쯤의 들뜬 기분과 경솔함, 그리고 자기가 스퀴어스 양한테 조금이라도 마음이 있다는 소리를 듣고 싶지 않은 아주 진심 어린 바람 때문에 이 일을 빚었지. 그러니 쓰인 수단도, 빚어진 결과도 다 세상에서 가장 자연스러운 거였어. 젊은 아가씨들은 결혼을 손꼽아 기다리고, 결혼식장으로 가는 경주에서 서로 밀치락거리고, 자기 매력을 가장 돋보이게 뽐낼 기회라면 무엇이든 잡으려 들 테니까. 세상이 시작될 때부터 그래 왔듯이, 세상이 끝나는 그날까지 말이야."]], [["‘Why, and here’s Fanny in tears now!’ exclaimed Miss Price, as if in fresh amazement.", "\"어머, 패니가 이제 울고 있네!\" 프라이스 양이 새삼 놀란 척 외쳤어."], ["‘What can be the matter?’", "\"무슨 일이람?\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["you don’t know, miss, of course you don’t know.", "너야 모르지, 당연히 모르겠지."], ["Pray don’t trouble yourself to inquire,’ said Miss Squeers, producing that change of countenance which children call making a face.", "굳이 수고스럽게 물어볼 거 없어.\" 스퀴어스 양이, 아이들이 찡그린다고 하는 그 표정을 지으며 말했어."]], [["‘Well, I’m sure!’ exclaimed Miss Price.", "\"어머, 별꼴이야 정말!\" 프라이스 양이 외쳤어."]], [["‘And who cares whether you are sure or not, ma’am?’ retorted Miss Squeers, making another face.", "\"네가 별꼴이라고 하든 말든 누가 신경이나 쓴대요, 아가씨?\" 스퀴어스 양이 또 한 번 얼굴을 찡그리며 받아쳤어."]], [["‘You are monstrous polite, ma’am,’ said Miss Price.", "\"참 엄청나게 공손하시네요, 아가씨.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어."]], [["‘I shall not come to you to take lessons in the art, ma’am!’ retorted Miss Squeers.", "\"그 기술을 배우러 당신한테 갈 일은 없을 거예요, 아가씨!\" 스퀴어스 양이 받아쳤어."]], [["‘You needn’t take the trouble to make yourself plainer than you are, ma’am, however,’ rejoined Miss Price, ‘because that’s quite unnecessary.’", "\"하지만 굳이 애써 지금보다 더 못나 보이려고 할 것 없어요, 아가씨.\" 프라이스 양이 받았어. \"그건 영 쓸데없는 짓이니까요.\""]], [["Miss Squeers, in reply, turned very red, and thanked God that she hadn’t got the bold faces of some people. Miss Price, in rejoinder, congratulated herself upon not being possessed of the envious feeling of other people; whereupon Miss Squeers made some general remark touching the danger of associating with low persons; in which Miss Price entirely coincided: observing that it was very true indeed, and she had thought so a long time.", "이에 스퀴어스 양은 얼굴이 새빨개져서는, 자기가 어떤 사람들처럼 뻔뻔한 낯짝을 가지지 않은 걸 하느님께 감사했어. 프라이스 양은 그 대꾸로, 자기가 다른 사람들 같은 시샘하는 마음을 가지지 않은 걸 다행으로 여겼지. 그러자 스퀴어스 양은 천박한 사람들과 어울리는 게 얼마나 위험한지에 대해 일반론을 한마디 했어. 프라이스 양은 거기에 전적으로 동의했지. 정말이지 옳은 말이라고, 자기도 오래전부터 그렇게 생각해 왔다고 하면서 말이야."]], [["‘’Tilda,’ exclaimed Miss Squeers with dignity, ‘I hate you.’", "\"틸다,\" 스퀴어스 양이 위엄을 부리며 외쳤어. \"난 네가 싫어.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["There’s no love lost between us, I assure you,’ said Miss Price, tying her bonnet strings with a jerk.", "우리 사이에 정 같은 건 진작 없었지, 정말이야.\" 프라이스 양이 모자 끈을 홱 묶으며 말했어."], ["‘You’ll cry your eyes out, when I’m gone; you know you will.’", "\"내가 가고 나면 넌 눈이 빠지게 울 거야. 너도 알잖아.\""]], [["‘I scorn your words, Minx,’ said Miss Squeers.", "\"네 말 따위 우습기만 해, 이 여우야.\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어."]], [["‘You pay me a great compliment when you say so,’ answered the miller’s daughter, curtseying very low.", "\"그렇게 말해 주니 정말 큰 칭찬인걸.\" 방앗간집 딸이 아주 깊숙이 절을 하며 대답했어."], ["‘Wish you a very good-night, ma’am, and pleasant dreams attend your sleep!’", "\"안녕히 주무세요, 아가씨, 단꿈 꾸시길 바라요!\""]], [["With this parting benediction, Miss Price swept from the room, followed by the huge Yorkshireman, who exchanged with Nicholas, at parting, that peculiarly expressive scowl with which the cut-and-thrust counts, in melodramatic performances, inform each other they will meet again.", "이 작별 인사를 끝으로 프라이스 양은 휙 방을 나갔어. 그 덩치 큰 요크셔 남자가 뒤따라 나갔지. 그는 나가면서 니콜라스와, 그 유난히 의미심장한 노려보는 눈빛을 주고받았어. 멜로드라마에서 칼싸움하는 자들이 또 만나자고 서로에게 알리는 그런 눈빛 말이야."]], [["They were no sooner gone, than Miss Squeers fulfilled the prediction of her quondam friend by giving vent to a most copious burst of tears, and uttering various dismal lamentations and incoherent words.", "그들이 나가자마자, 스퀴어스 양은 한때 친구였던 그 여자의 예언대로 펑펑 눈물을 쏟아 냈어. 그러고는 갖가지 처량한 한탄과 두서없는 말을 늘어놓았지."], ["Nicholas stood looking on for a few seconds, rather doubtful what to do, but feeling uncertain whether the fit would end in his being embraced, or scratched, and considering that either infliction would be equally agreeable, he walked off very quietly while Miss Squeers was moaning in her pocket-handkerchief.", "니콜라스는 몇 초간 가만히 지켜보며 어찌해야 할지 좀 망설였어. 하지만 이 발작이 끝나면 자기가 끌어안기게 될지 할퀴게 될지 알 수 없었고, 그 둘 중 어느 쪽이든 똑같이 달갑지 않다고 여겨져서, 스퀴어스 양이 손수건에 대고 흐느끼는 동안 아주 조용히 자리를 떴어."]], [["‘This is one consequence,’ thought Nicholas, when he had groped his way to the dark sleeping-room, ‘of my cursed readiness to adapt myself to any society in which chance carries me.", "'이게 바로 그 결과로구나,' 니콜라스는 더듬더듬 어두운 침실로 길을 찾아가며 생각했어."], ["If I had sat mute and motionless, as I might have done, this would not have happened.’", "'우연히 어떤 무리에 끼게 되든 거기에 척척 맞춰 버리는 이 빌어먹을 넉살의 결과야. 내가 입 꾹 다물고 가만히만 있었더라면, 그럴 수도 있었는데, 이런 일은 없었을 텐데.'"]], [["He listened for a few minutes, but all was quiet.", "그는 몇 분간 귀를 기울였지만, 사방이 고요했어."]], [["‘I was glad,’ he murmured, ‘to grasp at any relief from the sight of this dreadful place, or the presence of its vile master.", "'그래,' 그가 중얼거렸어."], ["I have set these people by the ears, and made two new enemies, where, Heaven knows, I needed none.", "'이 끔찍한 곳을 보는 것도, 이곳의 비열한 주인을 마주하는 것도 잠깐이나마 벗어날 수 있어서 좋았지."], ["Well, it is a just punishment for having forgotten, even for an hour, what is around me now!’", "그런데 난 이 사람들을 서로 으르렁대게 만들었고, 굳이 그럴 필요도 없는데, 하늘도 알다시피, 새 원수를 둘이나 만들었어. 그래, 단 한 시간이라도 지금 내 주위가 어떤 곳인지 잊어버린 데 대한 마땅한 벌이야!'"]], [["So saying, he felt his way among the throng of weary-hearted sleepers, and crept into his poor bed.", "이렇게 말하며, 그는 지쳐 잠든 아이들 무리를 손으로 더듬어 가며 자기의 초라한 침대로 기어 들어갔어."]], [["# CHAPTER 10", "# CHAPTER 10"]], [["How Mr. Ralph Nickleby provided for his Niece and Sister-in-Law\nOn the second morning after the departure of Nicholas for Yorkshire, Kate Nickleby sat in a very faded chair raised upon a very dusty throne in Miss La Creevy’s room, giving that lady a sitting for the portrait upon which she was engaged; and towards the full perfection of which, Miss La Creevy had had the street-door case brought upstairs, in order that she might be the better able to infuse into the counterfeit countenance of Miss Nickleby, a bright salmon flesh-tint which she had originally hit upon while executing the miniature of a young officer therein contained, and which bright salmon flesh-tint was considered, by Miss La Creevy’s chief friends and patrons, to be quite a novelty in art: as indeed it was.", "니콜라스가 요크셔로 떠난 지 이틀째 되는 날 아침이었어. 케이트 니클비는 라 크리비 양의 방에서, 먼지가 잔뜩 쌓인 받침대 위에 올려진 무척 낡은 의자에 앉아 있었지. 라 크리비 양이 그리고 있는 초상화를 위해 모델을 서 주고 있었던 거야. 그 그림을 더 완벽하게 만들려고 라 크리비 양은 길가 문에 걸어 두는 진열 상자까지 위층으로 가져왔어. 그래야 케이트 니클비 양의 그려진 얼굴에 밝은 연어빛 살색을 더 잘 입힐 수 있다고 생각했거든. 그 빛깔은 원래 그 상자 안에 든 어느 젊은 장교의 세밀화를 그릴 때 처음 떠올린 색이었어. 그리고 라 크리비 양의 주요 친구들과 후원자들은 그 밝은 연어빛 살색을 미술에서 아주 새로운 시도라고 여겼지. 사실 정말 그렇기도 했고."]], [["‘I think I have caught it now,’ said Miss La Creevy.", "\"이제 제대로 잡은 것 같아요.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어."], ["‘The very shade!", "\"딱 그 색조예요!"], ["This will be the sweetest portrait I have ever done, certainly.’", "이건 분명히 내가 그린 것 중에 가장 사랑스러운 초상화가 될 거예요.\""]], [["‘It will be your genius that makes it so, then, I am sure,’ replied Kate, smiling.", "\"그럼 그건 분명 선생님의 재능 덕분이겠죠.\" 케이트가 웃으며 대꾸했어."]], [["‘No, no, I won’t allow that, my dear,’ rejoined Miss La Creevy. ‘It’s a very nice subject--a very nice subject, indeed--though, of course, something depends upon the mode of treatment.’", "\"아니, 아니, 그렇게는 못 받아들여요, 아가씨.\" 라 크리비 양이 받아쳤어. \"아주 좋은 모델이라서 그래요. 정말 아주 좋은 모델이죠. 물론 다루는 방식에 따라 좀 달라지긴 하지만요.\""]], [["‘And not a little,’ observed Kate.", "\"적잖이 달라지죠.\" 케이트가 한마디 했어."]], [["‘Why, my dear, you are right there,’ said Miss La Creevy, ‘in the main you are right there; though I don’t allow that it is of such very great importance in the present case.", "\"아, 그래요, 그 점은 아가씨 말이 맞아요.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어."], ["Ah!", "\"대체로 맞는 말이에요. 하지만 이번 경우에 그게 그렇게 대단히 중요하다고는 못 하겠네요."], ["The difficulties of Art, my dear, are great.’", "아! 미술의 어려움이란, 아가씨, 정말 크답니다.\""]], [["‘They must be, I have no doubt,’ said Kate, humouring her good-natured little friend.", "\"틀림없이 그렇겠죠.\" 케이트는 마음씨 고운 작은 친구의 기분을 맞춰 주며 말했어."]], [["‘They are beyond anything you can form the faintest conception of,’ replied Miss La Creevy.", "\"아가씨가 아무리 어렴풋이 짐작해 봐도 그 어려움은 그 이상이에요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대답했어."], ["‘What with bringing out eyes with all one’s power, and keeping down noses with all one’s force, and adding to heads, and taking away teeth altogether, you have no idea of the trouble one little miniature is.’", "\"온 힘을 다해 눈을 살려내고, 또 온 힘을 다해 코를 눌러 주고, 머리는 보태고, 이는 아예 죄다 빼 버리고 말이죠. 작은 세밀화 한 점에 얼마나 손이 많이 가는지 아가씨는 짐작도 못 할 거예요.\""]], [["‘The remuneration can scarcely repay you,’ said Kate.", "\"보수로는 그 수고를 도저히 못 갚겠네요.\" 케이트가 말했어."]], [["‘Why, it does not, and that’s the truth,’ answered Miss La Creevy; ‘and then people are so dissatisfied and unreasonable, that, nine times out of ten, there’s no pleasure in painting them.", "\"그래요, 못 갚죠, 그게 사실이에요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대답했어."], ["Sometimes they say, “Oh, how very serious you have made me look, Miss La Creevy!”", "\"게다가 사람들이 어찌나 불만이 많고 막무가내인지, 열에 아홉은 그리는 재미가 하나도 없어요. 어떨 땐 '아이고, 라 크리비 양, 어쩜 이렇게 심각하게 그려 놓으셨어요!'"], ["and at others, “La, Miss La Creevy, how very smirking!”", "그러고, 또 어떨 땐 '어머, 라 크리비 양, 어쩜 이렇게 실실 웃는 얼굴이에요!' 그러죠."], ["when the very essence of a good portrait is, that it must be either serious or smirking, or it’s no portrait at all.’", "좋은 초상화의 핵심이란 게 바로 심각하거나 아니면 실실 웃거나 둘 중 하나여야 한다는 건데 말이에요. 안 그러면 초상화라고 할 수도 없거든요.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ said Kate, laughing.", "\"그래요!\" 케이트가 웃으며 말했어."]], [["‘Certainly, my dear; because the sitters are always either the one or the other,’ replied Miss La Creevy. ‘Look at the Royal Academy! All those beautiful shiny portraits of gentlemen in black velvet waistcoats, with their fists doubled up on round tables, or marble slabs, are serious, you know; and all the ladies who are playing with little parasols, or little dogs, or little children--it’s the same rule in art, only varying the objects--are smirking. In fact,’ said Miss La Creevy, sinking her voice to a confidential whisper, ‘there are only two styles of portrait painting; the serious and the smirk; and we always use the serious for professional people (except actors sometimes), and the smirk for private ladies and gentlemen who don’t care so much about looking clever.’", "\"그럼요, 아가씨. 모델들이 늘 그 둘 중 하나거든요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대답했어. \"왕립 미술원을 봐요! 검은 벨벳 조끼를 입고 둥근 탁자나 대리석 판 위에 주먹을 꽉 쥐고 있는 신사들의 그 아름답고 번들번들한 초상화들, 그게 다 심각한 쪽이에요. 그리고 작은 양산이나 작은 강아지, 작은 아이들을 데리고 노는 부인들은 죄다 실실 웃는 쪽이고요. 미술에선 다 같은 규칙이고, 그저 대상만 바뀔 뿐이에요. 사실은 말이죠,\" 라 크리비 양은 은밀한 속삭임으로 목소리를 낮췄어. \"초상화에는 딱 두 가지 양식밖에 없어요. 심각한 쪽하고 실실 웃는 쪽. 그래서 우리는 늘 전문직 사람들한테는 심각한 쪽을 쓰고 (가끔 배우들은 빼고요), 그리고 그렇게 똑똑해 보이는 데 별로 신경 안 쓰는 일반 신사 숙녀들한테는 실실 웃는 쪽을 쓰지요.\""]], [["Kate seemed highly amused by this information, and Miss La Creevy went on painting and talking, with immovable complacency.", "케이트는 이 이야기가 무척 재미있는 모양이었어. 그리고 라 크리비 양은 흔들림 없는 만족감에 젖어 그림을 그리며 이야기를 계속했지."]], [["‘What a number of officers you seem to paint!’ said Kate, availing herself of a pause in the discourse, and glancing round the room.", "\"장교를 정말 많이도 그리시나 봐요!\" 케이트는 이야기가 잠시 멈춘 틈을 타서, 방 안을 둘러보며 말했어."]], [["‘Number of what, child?’ inquired Miss La Creevy, looking up from her work. ‘Character portraits, oh yes--they’re not real military men, you know.’", "\"많이 뭐라고요, 아가씨?\" 라 크리비 양이 일하던 손을 멈추고 고개를 들며 물었어. \"성격 초상화 말이군요, 아 그래요. 저 사람들은 진짜 군인이 아니에요.\" \"아니라고요!\""]], [["‘No!’\n‘Bless your heart, of course not; only clerks and that, who hire a uniform coat to be painted in, and send it here in a carpet bag. Some artists,’ said Miss La Creevy, ‘keep a red coat, and charge seven-and-sixpence extra for hire and carmine; but I don’t do that myself, for I don’t consider it legitimate.’", "\"아이고, 당연히 아니죠. 그냥 사무원이나 뭐 그런 사람들이에요. 그림 그릴 때 입으려고 군복 외투를 빌려서, 가방에 넣어 여기로 보내는 거죠. 어떤 화가들은,\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"빨간 외투를 갖춰 놓고, 대여비랑 카민 물감 값으로 칠 실링 육 펜스를 더 받기도 해요. 하지만 나는 그러지 않아요. 그건 정당하지 않다고 보거든요.\""]], [["Drawing herself up, as though she plumed herself greatly upon not resorting to these lures to catch sitters, Miss La Creevy applied herself, more intently, to her task: only raising her head occasionally, to look with unspeakable satisfaction at some touch she had just put in: and now and then giving Miss Nickleby to understand what particular feature she was at work upon, at the moment; ‘not,’ she expressly observed, ‘that you should make it up for painting, my dear, but because it’s our custom sometimes to tell sitters what part we are upon, in order that if there’s any particular expression they want introduced, they may throw it in, at the time, you know.’", "라 크리비 양은 그런 미끼로 모델을 낚지 않는다는 게 무척 자랑스럽다는 듯 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우고는, 일에 더 골똘히 매달렸어. 가끔 고개를 들어 방금 칠한 어느 부분을 말할 수 없이 흐뭇하게 바라볼 뿐이었지. 그리고 이따금 지금 어느 부분을 그리는 중인지 케이트한테 일러 주기도 했어. \"그렇다고,\" 라 크리비 양은 굳이 덧붙였어. \"아가씨가 그림 그리기 좋게 표정을 꾸미라는 건 아니에요. 다만 우리 관습이 그래요. 모델한테 지금 어느 부분을 그리는 중인지 말해 주거든요. 혹시 꼭 넣고 싶은 표정이 있으면 그때 지어 보일 수 있게 말이죠.\""]], [["‘And when,’ said Miss La Creevy, after a long silence, to wit, an interval of full a minute and a half, ‘when do you expect to see your uncle again?’", "\"그런데,\" 라 크리비 양이 한참, 그러니까 일 분 반쯤이나 잠잠히 있다가 입을 열었어. \"삼촌은 언제 다시 만나기로 했어요?\""]], [["‘I scarcely know; I had expected to have seen him before now,’ replied Kate. ‘Soon I hope, for this state of uncertainty is worse than anything.’", "\"잘 모르겠어요. 진작 만났을 줄 알았는데요.\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"곧 만났으면 좋겠어요. 이렇게 막막하게 기다리는 게 무엇보다 힘들거든요.\""]], [["‘I suppose he has money, hasn’t he?’ inquired Miss La Creevy.", "\"돈은 좀 있는 분이겠죠?\" 라 크리비 양이 물었어."]], [["‘He is very rich, I have heard,’ rejoined Kate.", "\"아주 부자라고 들었어요.\" 케이트가 대답했어."], ["‘I don’t know that he is, but I believe so.’", "\"정말 그런지는 모르겠지만, 그런 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘Ah, you may depend upon it he is, or he wouldn’t be so surly,’ remarked Miss La Creevy, who was an odd little mixture of shrewdness and simplicity. ‘When a man’s a bear, he is generally pretty independent.’", "\"아, 틀림없이 부자일 거예요. 안 그러면 그렇게 무뚝뚝하지도 않겠죠.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. 영리함과 순박함이 묘하게 뒤섞인 작은 여자였거든. \"사내가 곰처럼 굴 땐, 대개 살림이 꽤 넉넉하다는 거예요.\""]], [["‘His manner is rough,’ said Kate.", "\"태도가 거칠긴 해요.\" 케이트가 말했어."]], [["‘Rough!’ cried Miss La Creevy, ‘a porcupine’s a featherbed to him!", "\"거칠다고요!\" 라 크리비 양이 소리쳤어."], ["I never met with such a cross-grained old savage.’", "\"그 사람한테 대면 호저도 깃털 침대 같겠어요! 그렇게 비뚤어진 늙은 야만인은 처음 봤다니까요.\""]], [["‘It is only his manner, I believe,’ observed Kate, timidly; ‘he was disappointed in early life, I think I have heard, or has had his temper soured by some calamity.", "\"그저 태도가 그럴 뿐일 거예요.\" 케이트가 조심스레 말했어."], ["I should be sorry to think ill of him until I knew he deserved it.’", "\"젊었을 때 무슨 실망스러운 일을 겪었다거나, 아니면 어떤 불행 때문에 성미가 비뚤어졌다고 들은 것 같아요. 그럴 만한 사람이라고 확실히 알기 전엔, 함부로 나쁘게 생각하고 싶지 않아요.\""]], [["‘Well; that’s very right and proper,’ observed the miniature painter, ‘and Heaven forbid that I should be the cause of your doing so!", "\"음, 그건 아주 옳고 마땅한 말이에요.\" 세밀화 화가가 말했어."], ["But, now, mightn’t he, without feeling it himself, make you and your mama some nice little allowance that would keep you both comfortable until you were well married, and be a little fortune to her afterwards?", "\"그리고 내가 아가씨를 그렇게 만드는 사람이 되지 않게 하늘이 막아 주시길! 하지만요, 삼촌이 자기는 별로 아쉬울 것도 없으면서, 아가씨랑 어머니한테 자그마한 용돈이라도 주실 순 없을까요?"], ["What would a hundred a year for instance, be to him?’", "아가씨가 좋은 데 시집갈 때까지 두 분이 편하게 지낼 만큼, 그리고 나중엔 어머니한테 작은 밑천이 될 만큼요. 예를 들어 한 해 백 파운드쯤이 그 사람한테 대수겠어요?\""]], [["‘I don’t know what it would be to him,’ said Kate, with energy, ‘but it would be that to me I would rather die than take.’", "\"그 사람한테 그게 어떤 의미인지는 모르겠어요.\" 케이트가 힘주어 말했어. \"하지만 저한테 그건, 받느니 차라리 죽는 게 낫겠다 싶은 거예요.\""]], [["‘Heyday!’ cried Miss La Creevy.", "\"이런 세상에!\" 라 크리비 양이 소리쳤어."]], [["‘A dependence upon him,’ said Kate, ‘would embitter my whole life.", "\"그 사람한테 기대고 사는 건,\" 케이트가 말했어."], ["I should feel begging a far less degradation.’", "\"제 삶을 통째로 쓰라리게 만들 거예요. 구걸하는 게 차라리 덜 굴욕적일 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘Well!’ exclaimed Miss La Creevy.", "\"이런!\" 라 크리비 양이 외쳤어."], ["‘This of a relation whom you will not hear an indifferent person speak ill of, my dear, sounds oddly enough, I confess.’", "\"남이 험담하는 것도 못 견디겠다던 그 친척에 대해, 정작 아가씨 입에서 이런 말이 나오니, 솔직히 좀 묘하게 들리네요.\""]], [["‘I dare say it does,’ replied Kate, speaking more gently, ‘indeed I am sure it must. I--I--only mean that with the feelings and recollection of better times upon me, I could not bear to live on anybody’s bounty--not his particularly, but anybody’s.’", "\"그러게요.\" 케이트가 한결 부드럽게 말했어. \"정말 그렇겠죠. 저는, 저는, 그저 이런 뜻이에요. 더 나았던 시절의 기억과 마음을 품고 있으니, 누구의 적선에도 기대어 살 수가 없다는 거예요. 그 사람뿐 아니라, 그 누구한테도요.\""]], [["Miss La Creevy looked slyly at her companion, as if she doubted whether Ralph himself were not the subject of dislike, but seeing that her young friend was distressed, made no remark.", "라 크리비 양은 친구를 슬쩍 곁눈질했어. 정말 랄프가 싫어서 그러는 건 아닌가 싶었던 거지. 하지만 어린 친구가 괴로워하는 걸 보고는, 아무 말도 하지 않았어."]], [["‘I only ask of him,’ continued Kate, whose tears fell while she spoke, ‘that he will move so little out of his way, in my behalf, as to enable me by his recommendation--only by his recommendation--to earn, literally, my bread and remain with my mother. Whether we shall ever taste happiness again, depends upon the fortunes of my dear brother; but if he will do this, and Nicholas only tells us that he is well and cheerful, I shall be contented.’", "\"제가 그 사람한테 바라는 건 딱 하나예요.\" 케이트는 말하면서 눈물을 흘렸어. \"저를 위해 아주 조금만 수고해 주시는 거요. 그 사람의 추천으로, 오직 추천만으로요, 말 그대로 제 밥벌이를 하면서 어머니 곁에 남아 있게 해 주는 거예요. 우리가 다시 행복을 맛볼 수 있을지는 사랑하는 오빠가 잘되느냐에 달렸어요. 하지만 그 사람이 이것만 해 준다면, 그리고 니콜라스가 잘 지내고 즐겁게 산다는 소식만 전해 준다면, 저는 만족할 거예요.\""]], [["As she ceased to speak, there was a rustling behind the screen which stood between her and the door, and some person knocked at the wainscot.’", "케이트가 말을 마쳤을 때, 그녀와 문 사이를 가린 가림막 뒤에서 바스락거리는 소리가 났어. 그리고 누군가 벽판을 똑똑 두드렸지."]], [["‘Come in, whoever it is!’ cried Miss La Creevy.", "\"누구든 들어오세요!\" 라 크리비 양이 소리쳤어."]], [["The person complied, and, coming forward at once, gave to view the form and features of no less an individual than Mr.", "그 사람은 시키는 대로 했어."], ["Ralph Nickleby himself.", "그러고는 곧장 앞으로 나오는데, 그 모습과 얼굴을 드러낸 사람은 다름 아닌 랄프 니클비 본인이었지."]], [["‘Your servant, ladies,’ said Ralph, looking sharply at them by turns.", "\"안녕하시오, 부인들.\" 랄프는 두 사람을 번갈아 날카롭게 쳐다보며 말했어."], ["‘You were talking so loud, that I was unable to make you hear.’", "\"어찌나 큰 소리로 얘기들을 하시는지, 들리게 할 수가 없더군요.\""]], [["When the man of business had a more than commonly vicious snarl lurking at his heart, he had a trick of almost concealing his eyes under their thick and protruding brows, for an instant, and then displaying them in their full keenness. As he did so now, and tried to keep down the smile which parted his thin compressed lips, and puckered up the bad lines about his mouth, they both felt certain that some part, if not the whole, of their recent conversation, had been overheard.", "이 사업가의 마음속에 평소보다 더 고약한 으르렁거림이 도사리고 있을 때면, 그는 한 가지 버릇이 있었어. 두툼하게 튀어나온 눈썹 밑으로 잠깐 눈을 거의 감추었다가, 그다음에 그 눈을 온전히 날카롭게 드러내는 거였지. 지금도 랄프는 그렇게 했어. 그리고 얇고 꾹 다문 입술을 가르고 입가의 험상궂은 주름을 잡히게 하는 미소를 애써 누르려 했지. 그 모습에 두 사람은, 방금 나눈 이야기의 일부가, 아니 어쩌면 전부가 그의 귀에 들어갔다는 걸 확신했어."]], [["‘I called in, on my way upstairs, more than half expecting to find you here,’ said Ralph, addressing his niece, and looking contemptuously at the portrait.", "\"위층으로 올라가는 길에 들렀소. 여기 있을 것 같다는 생각이 반은 넘게 들어서 말이오.\""], ["‘Is that my niece’s portrait, ma’am?’", "랄프는 조카에게 말하며, 초상화를 경멸하듯 바라봤어. \"저게 내 조카 초상화요, 부인?\""]], [["‘Yes it is, Mr.", "\"네, 그래요, 니클비 씨.\" 라 크리비 양이 무척 발랄한 태도로 말했어."], ["Nickleby,’ said Miss La Creevy, with a very sprightly air, ‘and between you and me and the post, sir, it will be a very nice portrait too, though I say it who am the painter.’", "\"그리고 우리끼리 얘기지만, 선생님, 이게 아주 멋진 초상화가 될 거예요. 그린 사람이 직접 하는 말이지만요.\""]], [["‘Don’t trouble yourself to show it to me, ma’am,’ cried Ralph, moving away, ‘I have no eye for likenesses.", "\"굳이 나한테 보여 줄 것 없소, 부인.\" 랄프가 자리를 옮기며 말했어."], ["Is it nearly finished?’", "\"나는 닮았는지 어쩐지 보는 눈이 없어서. 거의 다 됐소?\""]], [["‘Why, yes,’ replied Miss La Creevy, considering with the pencil end of her brush in her mouth.", "\"네, 그럼요.\" 라 크리비 양이 붓 자루 끝을 입에 문 채 생각하며 대답했어."], ["‘Two sittings more will--’", "\"두 번만 더 앉아 주면—\""]], [["‘Have them at once, ma’am,’ said Ralph.", "\"당장 다 끝내시오, 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘She’ll have no time to idle over fooleries after tomorrow.", "\"이 애는 내일부터는 시시한 짓에 빈둥거릴 시간이 없을 거요."], ["Work, ma’am, work; we must all work.", "일이오, 부인, 일. 우리는 다 일을 해야 하니까."], ["Have you let your lodgings, ma’am?’", "세놓을 방은 다 나갔소, 부인?\""]], [["‘I have not put a bill up yet, sir.’", "\"아직 광고는 안 붙였어요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Put it up at once, ma’am; they won’t want the rooms after this week, or if they do, can’t pay for them.", "\"당장 붙이시오, 부인. 이번 주 지나면 그 사람들도 방이 필요 없을 거요."], ["Now, my dear, if you’re ready, we’ll lose no more time.’", "아니면 필요해도 셋돈을 못 낼 테고. 자, 얘야, 준비됐으면 더 이상 시간 끌지 말자.\""]], [["With an assumption of kindness which sat worse upon him even than his usual manner, Mr.", "랄프 니클비는 친절한 척을 했는데, 그게 평소 태도보다 더 어색해 보였어."], ["Ralph Nickleby motioned to the young lady to precede him, and bowing gravely to Miss La Creevy, closed the door and followed upstairs, where Mrs.", "그는 조카에게 앞서 가라고 손짓하고는, 라 크리비 양에게 점잖게 고개를 숙여 인사했지. 그러고는 문을 닫고 위층으로 따라 올라갔어."], ["Nickleby received him with many expressions of regard.", "거기서 니클비 부인이 반가운 인사를 잔뜩 늘어놓으며 그를 맞았어."], ["Stopping them somewhat abruptly, Ralph waved his hand with an impatient gesture, and proceeded to the object of his visit.", "랄프는 다소 퉁명스럽게 그 인사를 끊더니, 못마땅한 듯 손을 내저으며 곧장 찾아온 용건으로 들어갔어."]], [["‘I have found a situation for your daughter, ma’am,’ said Ralph.", "\"따님의 일자리를 하나 구했소, 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Well,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby.", "\"어머나.\""], ["‘Now, I will say that that is only just what I have expected of you.", "니클비 부인이 대답했어. \"이제야 말하지만, 그게 바로 내가 당신한테 기대했던 거예요."], ["“Depend upon it,”", "'두고 봐라.'"], ["I said to Kate, only yesterday morning at breakfast, “that after your uncle has provided, in that most ready manner, for Nicholas, he will not leave us until he has done at least the same for you.”", "어제 아침 식사 때 케이트한테 그랬다니까요. '삼촌이 그렇게 척척 니콜라스를 챙겨 줬으니, 너한테도 적어도 그만큼은 해 주기 전엔 우리를 그냥 두지 않으실 거다.'"], ["These were my very words, as near as I remember.", "내가 한 말 그대로예요, 기억나는 한은요."], ["Kate, my dear, why don’t you thank your--’", "케이트야, 얘, 어서 삼촌한테 감사 인사를—\""]], [["‘Let me proceed, ma’am, pray,’ said Ralph, interrupting his sister-in-law in the full torrent of her discourse.", "\"제 말 좀 하게 해 주시오, 부인, 제발.\" 랄프가 제수의 말이 거침없이 쏟아지는 걸 끊으며 말했어."]], [["‘Kate, my love, let your uncle proceed,’ said Mrs.", "\"케이트야, 얘, 삼촌이 말씀하시게 두렴.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘I am most anxious that he should, mama,’ rejoined Kate.", "\"저도 삼촌이 말씀하시길 무척 바라고 있어요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 받아 말했어."]], [["‘Well, my dear, if you are anxious that he should, you had better allow your uncle to say what he has to say, without interruption,’ observed Mrs.", "\"그래, 얘, 삼촌이 말씀하시길 바란다면, 끼어들지 말고 삼촌이 하실 말씀을 다 하시게 두는 게 좋겠다.\" 니클비 부인이 자잘하게 고개를 끄덕이고 눈살을 찌푸리며 말했어."], ["Nickleby, with many small nods and frowns.", "\"삼촌은 시간이 무척 귀한 분이야, 얘."], ["‘Your uncle’s time is very valuable, my dear; and however desirous you may be--and naturally desirous, as I am sure any affectionate relations who have seen so little of your uncle as we have, must naturally be to protract the pleasure of having him among us, still, we are bound not to be selfish, but to take into consideration the important nature of his occupations in the city.’", "그리고 네가 아무리 바란다 해도, 사실 우리처럼 삼촌을 거의 못 뵙고 산 다정한 친척이라면 당연히 삼촌이 우리 곁에 더 오래 머무는 기쁨을 누리고 싶겠지만, 그래도 우리는 이기적으로 굴면 안 돼. 시내에서 하시는 그 중요한 일을 생각해야 한단다.\""]], [["‘I am very much obliged to you, ma’am,’ said Ralph with a scarcely perceptible sneer.", "\"무척 고맙소, 부인.\" 랄프가 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 비웃음을 띠며 말했어."], ["‘An absence of business habits in this family leads, apparently, to a great waste of words before business--when it does come under consideration--is arrived at, at all.’", "\"이 집안에는 사업하는 습관이 없다 보니, 정작 사업 얘기에 들어가기도 전에 말을 어마어마하게 낭비하게 되는 모양이군요. 그나마 사업 얘기가 나오기라도 한다면 말이오.\""]], [["‘I fear it is so indeed,’ replied Mrs.", "\"정말 그런가 봐요.\""], ["Nickleby with a sigh.", "니클비 부인이 한숨을 쉬며 대답했어."], ["‘Your poor brother--’", "\"가엾은 당신 동생도—\""]], [["‘My poor brother, ma’am,’ interposed Ralph tartly, ‘had no idea what business was--was unacquainted, I verily believe, with the very meaning of the word.’", "\"내 가엾은 동생은, 부인,\" 랄프가 매몰차게 끼어들었어. \"사업이 뭔지 도통 몰랐소. 정말이지 그 말뜻조차 모르고 살았을 거요.\""]], [["‘I fear he was,’ said Mrs.", "\"그랬나 봐요.\""], ["Nickleby, with her handkerchief to her eyes.", "니클비 부인이 손수건을 눈에 갖다 대며 말했어."], ["‘If it hadn’t been for me, I don’t know what would have become of him.’", "\"내가 없었으면 그이가 어떻게 됐을지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["What strange creatures we are!", "사람이란 참 이상한 존재야!"], ["The slight bait so skilfully thrown out by Ralph, on their first interview, was dangling on the hook yet.", "랄프가 처음 만났을 때 솜씨 좋게 던져 놓은 그 작은 미끼가, 아직도 낚싯바늘에 대롱대롱 매달려 있었거든."], ["At every small deprivation or discomfort which presented itself in the course of the four-and-twenty hours to remind her of her straitened and altered circumstances, peevish visions of her dower of one thousand pounds had arisen before Mrs.", "하루 사이에도 자기 처지가 옹색하게 바뀐 걸 일깨우는 사소한 결핍이나 불편이 닥칠 때마다, 니클비 부인의 머릿속엔 천 파운드 지참금에 대한 짜증 섞인 환상이 떠올랐어."], ["Nickleby’s mind, until, at last, she had come to persuade herself that of all her late husband’s creditors she was the worst used and the most to be pitied.", "그러다 마침내 그녀는, 죽은 남편의 모든 채권자 가운데 자기가 제일 푸대접을 받았고 제일 가엾은 신세라고 스스로를 설득하기에 이르렀지."], ["And yet, she had loved him dearly for many years, and had no greater share of selfishness than is the usual lot of mortals.", "그러면서도 그녀는 오랜 세월 남편을 끔찍이 사랑했고, 보통 사람들보다 더 이기적인 것도 아니었어."], ["Such is the irritability of sudden poverty.", "갑작스러운 가난이란 게 사람을 이렇게 못 견디게 만드는 법이지."], ["A decent annuity would have restored her thoughts to their old train, at once.", "변변한 연금 하나만 있었어도 그녀의 생각은 단번에 옛날 자리로 돌아갔을 거야."]], [["‘Repining is of no use, ma’am,’ said Ralph.", "\"한탄해 봐야 소용없소, 부인.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘Of all fruitless errands, sending a tear to look after a day that is gone is the most fruitless.’", "\"온갖 헛수고 중에서도, 지나간 날을 좇아 눈물 한 방울 흘려보내는 것만큼 헛된 일은 없소.\""]], [["‘So it is,’ sobbed Mrs.", "\"그래요.\" 니클비 부인이 흐느꼈어."], ["Nickleby. ‘So it is.’", "\"정말 그래요.\""]], [["‘As you feel so keenly, in your own purse and person, the consequences of inattention to business, ma’am,’ said Ralph, ‘I am sure you will impress upon your children the necessity of attaching themselves to it early in life.’", "\"부인이 직접 지갑과 몸으로 사업에 소홀했던 결과를 이토록 뼈저리게 느끼고 있으니,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"분명 자식들한테도 어려서부터 사업에 매달려야 한다는 걸 단단히 일러 주시겠지요.\""]], [["‘Of course I must see that,’ rejoined Mrs.", "\"물론 그래야죠.\" 니클비 부인이 받아 말했어."], ["Nickleby.", "\"쓰라린 경험이란 게 그렇잖아요, 아주버님."], ["‘Sad experience, you know, brother-in-law.--Kate, my dear, put that down in the next letter to Nicholas, or remind me to do it if I write.’", "케이트야, 얘, 그걸 다음에 니콜라스한테 보내는 편지에 적어 두렴. 아니면 내가 편지 쓸 때 잊지 않게 일러 주든지.\""]], [["Ralph paused for a few moments, and seeing that he had now made pretty sure of the mother, in case the daughter objected to his proposition, went on to say:", "랄프는 잠시 말을 멈췄어. 그리고 이제 딸이 자기 제안에 반대하더라도 어미만큼은 확실히 자기편으로 만들어 놨다는 걸 알고는, 말을 이어 갔어."]], [["‘The situation that I have made interest to procure, ma’am, is with--with a milliner and dressmaker, in short.’", "\"내가 힘을 써서 구한 그 자리는, 부인, 다름 아니라, 짧게 말해 어느 양품점 겸 양장점이오.\""]], [["‘A milliner!’ cried Mrs.", "\"양품점이라고요!\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 소리쳤어."]], [["‘A milliner and dressmaker, ma’am,’ replied Ralph.", "\"양품점 겸 양장점이오, 부인.\""], ["‘Dressmakers in London, as I need not remind you, ma’am, who are so well acquainted with all matters in the ordinary routine of life, make large fortunes, keep equipages, and become persons of great wealth and fortune.’", "랄프가 대답했어. \"런던의 양장점 주인들은, 일상사에 그토록 밝은 부인한테 굳이 일러 줄 것도 없지만, 큰돈을 벌고 마차를 굴리며 대단한 재산가가 된다오.\""]], [["Now, the first idea called up in Mrs. Nickleby’s mind by the words milliner and dressmaker were connected with certain wicker baskets lined with black oilskin, which she remembered to have seen carried to and fro in the streets; but, as Ralph proceeded, these disappeared, and were replaced by visions of large houses at the West end, neat private carriages, and a banker’s book; all of which images succeeded each other with such rapidity, that he had no sooner finished speaking, than she nodded her head and said ‘Very true,’ with great appearance of satisfaction.", "자, 양품점이니 양장점이니 하는 말에 니클비 부인의 머릿속에 처음 떠오른 건, 검은 방수포를 덧댄 버들 바구니들이었어. 거리에서 이리저리 실려 다니는 걸 본 기억이 있었거든. 하지만 랄프가 말을 이어 가자, 그 바구니들은 사라지고, 그 자리에 웨스트엔드의 큰 저택과 말끔한 자가용 마차, 그리고 은행 통장의 환상이 들어찼어. 이 모든 그림들이 어찌나 빠르게 줄지어 떠오르던지, 그가 말을 채 마치기도 전에 부인은 고개를 끄덕이며 무척 만족스러운 얼굴로 \"정말 그래요.\" 하고 말했어."]], [["‘What your uncle says is very true, Kate, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nickleby. ‘I recollect when your poor papa and I came to town after we were married, that a young lady brought me home a chip cottage-bonnet, with white and green trimming, and green persian lining, in her own carriage, which drove up to the door full gallop;--at least, I am not quite certain whether it was her own carriage or a hackney chariot, but I remember very well that the horse dropped down dead as he was turning round, and that your poor papa said he hadn’t had any corn for a fortnight.’", "\"삼촌 말씀이 정말 옳다, 케이트야, 얘.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"결혼하고 나서 가엾은 네 아빠랑 내가 시내에 처음 왔을 때가 생각나는구나. 어느 젊은 아가씨가 우리 집에 모자를 하나 갖다 줬는데, 하얗고 초록빛 장식에 초록빛 페르시아 안감을 댄 얇은 챙 모자였어. 자기 마차를 타고 와서는 전속력으로 문 앞까지 달려왔지. 적어도 자기 마차였는지 빌린 마차였는지는 확실치 않지만, 마차를 돌리다가 말이 그만 쓰러져 죽어 버린 건 아주 똑똑히 기억나. 그리고 네 가엾은 아빠가 그러더구나, 그 말이 보름 동안 여물을 한 톨도 못 먹었던 거라고.\""]], [["This anecdote, so strikingly illustrative of the opulence of milliners, was not received with any great demonstration of feeling, inasmuch as Kate hung down her head while it was relating, and Ralph manifested very intelligible symptoms of extreme impatience.", "양품점 주인들이 얼마나 부유한지를 이토록 인상적으로 보여 주는 이 일화는, 그다지 큰 호응을 얻지 못했어. 케이트는 이야기가 펼쳐지는 내내 고개를 푹 숙이고 있었고, 랄프는 누가 봐도 알 만큼 극도로 조바심을 내는 기색이었거든."]], [["‘The lady’s name,’ said Ralph, hastily striking in, ‘is Mantalini--Madame Mantalini.", "\"그 부인 이름은,\" 랄프가 황급히 끼어들며 말했어."], ["I know her.", "\"만탈리니요."], ["She lives near Cavendish Square.", "만탈리니 부인. 내가 아는 사람이오."], ["If your daughter is disposed to try after the situation, I’ll take her there directly.’", "캐번디시 광장 근처에 살지요. 따님이 그 자리를 한번 노려 볼 마음이 있다면, 당장 거기로 데려가겠소.\""]], [["‘Have you nothing to say to your uncle, my love?’ inquired Mrs.", "\"삼촌한테 할 말 없니, 얘?\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 물었어."]], [["‘A great deal,’ replied Kate; ‘but not now. I would rather speak to him when we are alone;--it will save his time if I thank him and say what I wish to say to him, as we walk along.’", "\"많아요.\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"하지만 지금은 아니에요. 단둘이 있을 때 말씀드리고 싶어요. 가는 길에 감사 인사도 드리고 하고 싶은 말도 하면, 삼촌 시간도 아낄 수 있으니까요.\""]], [["With these words, Kate hurried away, to hide the traces of emotion that were stealing down her face, and to prepare herself for the walk, while Mrs. Nickleby amused her brother-in-law by giving him, with many tears, a detailed account of the dimensions of a rosewood cabinet piano they had possessed in their days of affluence, together with a minute description of eight drawing-room chairs, with turned legs and green chintz squabs to match the curtains, which had cost two pounds fifteen shillings apiece, and had gone at the sale for a mere nothing.", "그 말을 남기고 케이트는 서둘러 자리를 떴어. 얼굴을 타고 슬며시 흐르는 감정의 흔적을 감추고, 외출 채비를 하려는 거였지. 그동안 니클비 부인은 아주버님을 붙들고, 눈물을 잔뜩 흘리며 옛 부유하던 시절에 갖고 있던 자단목 캐비닛 피아노의 치수를 자세히 늘어놓아 그를 즐겁게 해 줬어. 거기다 다리를 깎아 만들고 커튼에 맞춰 초록빛 친츠 방석을 댄 응접실 의자 여덟 개도 시시콜콜 묘사했지. 하나에 이 파운드 십오 실링이나 주고 산 건데, 경매에서는 거저나 다름없는 값에 넘어갔다는 거였어."]], [["These reminiscences were at length cut short by Kate’s return in her walking dress, when Ralph, who had been fretting and fuming during the whole time of her absence, lost no time, and used very little ceremony, in descending into the street.", "이 추억담은 마침내 외출복 차림으로 돌아온 케이트 덕분에 뚝 끊겼어. 케이트가 없는 동안 내내 안달하며 씩씩대던 랄프는 지체 없이, 그리고 격식이라곤 거의 차리지 않고 거리로 내려갔어."]], [["‘Now,’ he said, taking her arm, ‘walk as fast as you can, and you’ll get into the step that you’ll have to walk to business with, every morning.’ So saying, he led Kate off, at a good round pace, towards Cavendish Square.", "\"자.\" 그가 케이트의 팔을 잡으며 말했어. \"최대한 빨리 걸어라. 그래야 매일 아침 일터로 걸어갈 때 맞춰야 할 보폭에 익숙해질 테니.\" 그렇게 말하며 랄프는 케이트를 데리고 꽤 빠른 걸음으로 캐번디시 광장 쪽으로 향했어."]], [["‘I am very much obliged to you, uncle,’ said the young lady, after they had hurried on in silence for some time; ‘very.’", "\"정말 고마워요, 삼촌.\" 한동안 말없이 서둘러 걷고 나서 케이트가 말했어. \"정말로요.\""]], [["‘I’m glad to hear it,’ said Ralph.", "\"그렇다니 다행이구나.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘I hope you’ll do your duty.’", "\"네 본분을 다하길 바란다.\""]], [["‘I will try to please, uncle,’ replied Kate: ‘indeed I--’", "\"기쁘게 해 드리도록 애쓸게요, 삼촌.\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"정말이지 저는—\""]], [["‘Don’t begin to cry,’ growled Ralph; ‘I hate crying.’", "\"울지 마라.\" 랄프가 으르렁댔어. \"나는 우는 걸 질색해.\""]], [["‘It’s very foolish, I know, uncle,’ began poor Kate.", "\"바보 같은 짓인 거 저도 알아요, 삼촌.\" 가엾은 케이트가 입을 열었어."]], [["‘It is,’ replied Ralph, stopping her short, ‘and very affected besides.", "\"그래, 바보 같지.\" 랄프가 말을 뚝 끊으며 대답했어."], ["Let me see no more of it.’", "\"게다가 아주 꾸민 티가 나. 다신 그런 꼴 안 봤으면 좋겠다.\""]], [["Perhaps this was not the best way to dry the tears of a young and sensitive female, about to make her first entry on an entirely new scene of life, among cold and uninterested strangers; but it had its effect notwithstanding.", "차갑고 무심한 낯선 사람들 틈에서 완전히 새로운 인생의 무대에 처음 발을 들이려는 어리고 여린 처녀의 눈물을 말리는 데, 이게 최선의 방법은 아니었을 거야. 그래도 어쨌든 효과는 있었지."], ["Kate coloured deeply, breathed quickly for a few moments, and then walked on with a firmer and more determined step.", "케이트는 얼굴이 새빨개졌고, 잠깐 숨을 가쁘게 몰아쉬더니, 한결 단단하고 결연한 걸음으로 걸어갔어."]], [["It was a curious contrast to see how the timid country girl shrunk through the crowd that hurried up and down the streets, giving way to the press of people, and clinging closely to Ralph as though she feared to lose him in the throng; and how the stern and hard-featured man of business went doggedly on, elbowing the passengers aside, and now and then exchanging a gruff salutation with some passing acquaintance, who turned to look back upon his pretty charge, with looks expressive of surprise, and seemed to wonder at the ill-assorted companionship. But, it would have been a stranger contrast still, to have read the hearts that were beating side by side; to have laid bare the gentle innocence of the one, and the rugged villainy of the other; to have hung upon the guileless thoughts of the affectionate girl, and been amazed that, among all the wily plots and calculations of the old man, there should not be one word or figure denoting thought of death or of the grave. But so it was; and stranger still--though this is a thing of every day--the warm young heart palpitated with a thousand anxieties and apprehensions, while that of the old worldly man lay rusting in its cell, beating only as a piece of cunning mechanism, and yielding no one throb of hope, or fear, or love, or care, for any living thing.", "참 묘한 대조였어. 겁 많은 시골 처녀는 거리를 분주히 오가는 인파 속에서 잔뜩 움츠러들어, 밀려드는 사람들에게 길을 내주고, 군중 속에서 그를 놓칠세라 랄프에게 바짝 매달렸지. 한편 험상궂고 모진 얼굴의 사업가는 행인들을 팔꿈치로 밀쳐 가며 고집스레 나아갔어. 이따금 지나가는 아는 사람과 무뚝뚝하게 인사를 주고받기도 했는데, 그 사람들은 그가 데리고 가는 예쁜 처녀를 놀란 얼굴로 돌아보며, 어울리지 않는 이 동행을 의아해하는 듯했지. 하지만 나란히 뛰는 두 사람의 가슴속을 읽을 수 있었다면, 그건 한층 더 묘한 대조였을 거야. 한 사람의 부드러운 순수함과 다른 한 사람의 거친 악의를 낱낱이 드러내 본다면 말이야. 다정한 처녀의 티 없는 생각들을 들여다보다가, 늙은 사내의 그 온갖 교활한 음모와 계산 가운데, 죽음이나 무덤을 떠올리는 말 한마디 숫자 하나 없다는 데에 놀라게 됐을 거야. 하지만 사실이 그랬어. 그리고 더 묘한 건, 이건 날마다 일어나는 일이지만, 따뜻하고 젊은 가슴은 천 가지 근심과 걱정으로 두근거리는데, 늙고 세속에 찌든 사내의 가슴은 제 우리 속에 녹슨 채 누워, 그저 교활한 기계 부속처럼만 뛸 뿐, 살아 있는 그 무엇에게도 희망이나 두려움, 사랑이나 염려의 고동을 단 한 번도 내주지 않았다는 거야."]], [["‘Uncle,’ said Kate, when she judged they must be near their destination, ‘I must ask one question of you.", "\"삼촌.\" 이제 목적지에 거의 다 왔겠다 싶었을 때 케이트가 말했어."], ["I am to live at home?’", "\"한 가지만 여쭤볼게요. 저는 집에서 살게 되는 건가요?\""]], [["‘At home!’ replied Ralph; ‘where’s that?’", "\"집에서?\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"그게 어딘데?\""]], [["‘I mean with my mother--THE WIDOW,’ said Kate emphatically.", "\"어머니랑 같이요. 그 과부 말이에요.\" 케이트가 힘주어 말했어."]], [["‘You will live, to all intents and purposes, here,’ rejoined Ralph; ‘for here you will take your meals, and here you will be from morning till night--occasionally perhaps till morning again.’", "\"실상 너는 여기서 살게 될 거다.\" 랄프가 받아 말했어. \"여기서 밥을 먹고, 아침부터 밤까지 여기 있을 테니까. 가끔은 어쩌면 다시 아침이 될 때까지도 있을지 모르고.\""]], [["‘But at night, I mean,’ said Kate; ‘I cannot leave her, uncle. I must have some place that I can call a home; it will be wherever she is, you know, and may be a very humble one.’", "\"하지만 밤에요, 제 말은.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"저는 어머니를 떠날 수 없어요, 삼촌. 집이라고 부를 수 있는 곳이 있어야 해요. 그게 어머니가 계신 곳이라면 어디든 좋아요. 아주 누추한 곳이라도요.\""]], [["‘May be!’ said Ralph, walking faster, in the impatience provoked by the remark; ‘must be, you mean.", "\"누추한 곳이라도라니!\" 그 말에 발끈해 발걸음을 더 빨리하며 랄프가 말했어."], ["May be a humble one!", "\"누추한 곳일 수밖에 없다는 말이겠지. 누추한 곳이라도 좋다니!"], ["Is the girl mad?’", "이 애가 정신이 나갔나?\""]], [["‘The word slipped from my lips, I did not mean it indeed,’ urged Kate.", "\"말이 그냥 나와 버렸어요. 정말 그런 뜻은 아니었어요.\" 케이트가 다급히 말했어."]], [["‘I hope not,’ said Ralph.", "\"아니길 바란다.\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘But my question, uncle; you have not answered it.’", "\"그런데 제 질문에요, 삼촌. 아직 대답을 안 해 주셨어요.\""]], [["‘Why, I anticipated something of the kind,’ said Ralph; ‘and--though I object very strongly, mind--have provided against it. I spoke of you as an out-of-door worker; so you will go to this home that may be humble, every night.’", "\"그래, 그런 비슷한 걸 물을 줄 알았다.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그리고, 나는 아주 강하게 반대지만, 명심해라, 그래도 대비는 해 뒀다. 너를 통근하는 일꾼으로 얘기해 뒀으니까. 그러니 너는 매일 밤 그 누추할지도 모를 집으로 가게 될 거다.\""]], [["There was comfort in this.", "여기엔 위안이 있었어."], ["Kate poured forth many thanks for her uncle’s consideration, which Ralph received as if he had deserved them all, and they arrived without any further conversation at the dressmaker’s door, which displayed a very large plate, with Madame Mantalini’s name and occupation, and was approached by a handsome flight of steps.", "케이트는 삼촌의 배려에 감사 인사를 쏟아냈고, 랄프는 마치 그 인사를 다 받을 자격이 있다는 듯 받아들였지. 그러고 둘은 더 이상 별다른 말 없이 양장점 문 앞에 다다랐어."], ["There was a shop to the house, but it was let off to an importer of otto of roses.", "문에는 만탈리니 부인의 이름과 직업을 새긴 아주 큼직한 명패가 걸려 있었고, 멋진 계단을 올라가게 되어 있었어. 건물엔 가게도 하나 딸려 있었지만, 그건 장미 향유 수입상한테 세를 준 상태였지."], ["Madame Mantalini’s shows-rooms were on the first-floor: a fact which was notified to the nobility and gentry by the casual exhibition, near the handsomely curtained windows, of two or three elegant bonnets of the newest fashion, and some costly garments in the most approved taste.", "만탈리니 부인의 진열실은 이 층에 있었어. 멋들어진 커튼을 친 창가에 최신 유행하는 우아한 모자 두세 개와 더없이 세련된 값비싼 옷가지 몇 벌을 슬쩍 내걸어 둔 것으로, 귀족과 상류층에게 그 사실을 알려 주고 있었지."]], [["A liveried footman opened the door, and in reply to Ralph’s inquiry whether Madame Mantalini was at home, ushered them, through a handsome hall and up a spacious staircase, into the show saloon, which comprised two spacious drawing-rooms, and exhibited an immense variety of superb dresses and materials for dresses: some arranged on stands, others laid carelessly on sofas, and others again, scattered over the carpet, hanging on the cheval-glasses, or mingling, in some other way, with the rich furniture of various descriptions, which was profusely displayed.", "제복을 입은 하인이 문을 열었어. 만탈리니 부인이 집에 있느냐는 랄프의 물음에, 하인은 두 사람을 멋진 현관을 지나 널찍한 계단 위로 안내해서 진열실로 데려갔어. 그곳은 널따란 응접실 두 개로 이루어져 있었고, 엄청나게 다양한 화려한 드레스와 옷감들이 진열되어 있었지. 어떤 건 받침대 위에 정돈해 두고, 어떤 건 소파에 아무렇게나 던져 놓고, 또 어떤 건 양탄자 위에 흩어 놓거나 체경에 걸어 두거나, 아니면 여기저기 잔뜩 늘어놓은 갖가지 값진 가구들 사이에 뒤섞여 있었어."]], [["They waited here a much longer time than was agreeable to Mr.", "두 사람은 랄프 니클비가 견디기 어려울 만큼 오래 거기서 기다렸어."], ["Ralph Nickleby, who eyed the gaudy frippery about him with very little concern, and was at length about to pull the bell, when a gentleman suddenly popped his head into the room, and, seeing somebody there, as suddenly popped it out again.", "랄프는 주위의 번지르르한 싸구려 장식들을 무심히 둘러보다가, 마침내 종을 당기려던 참이었어. 그때 어떤 신사가 불쑥 방 안으로 머리를 들이밀더니, 누가 있는 걸 보고는 또 그만큼 불쑥 머리를 도로 빼 버렸지."]], [["‘Here.", "\"이봐요."], ["Hollo!’ cried Ralph.", "여보시오!\" 랄프가 소리쳤어."], ["‘Who’s that?’", "\"거기 누구요?\""]], [["At the sound of Ralph’s voice, the head reappeared, and the mouth, displaying a very long row of very white teeth, uttered in a mincing tone the words, ‘Demmit.", "랄프의 목소리에 그 머리가 다시 나타났어. 그리고 그 입은 아주 길게 늘어선 새하얀 이를 드러내며, 간드러진 말투로 이렇게 내뱉었어."], ["What, Nickleby!", "\"젠장. 아니, 니클비잖소!"], ["oh, demmit!’ Having uttered which ejaculations, the gentleman advanced, and shook hands with Ralph, with great warmth.", "아, 젠장!\" 이렇게 감탄사를 내뱉고 나서 그 신사는 다가와, 랄프와 아주 다정하게 악수를 했어."], ["He was dressed in a gorgeous morning gown, with a waistcoat and Turkish trousers of the same pattern, a pink silk neckerchief, and bright green slippers, and had a very copious watch-chain wound round his body.", "그는 화려한 실내복을 입고 있었는데, 조끼와 터키풍 바지도 같은 무늬였지. 분홍빛 비단 목수건을 두르고 밝은 초록빛 슬리퍼를 신었고, 아주 풍성한 시곗줄을 몸에 칭칭 두르고 있었어."], ["Moreover, he had whiskers and a moustache, both dyed black and gracefully curled.", "게다가 구레나룻과 콧수염을 길렀는데, 둘 다 까맣게 물들이고 멋들어지게 말아 올렸지."]], [["‘Demmit, you don’t mean to say you want me, do you, demmit?’ said this gentleman, smiting Ralph on the shoulder.", "\"젠장, 설마 내가 필요하다는 건 아니겠지요, 응, 젠장?\" 이 신사가 랄프의 어깨를 탁 치며 말했어."]], [["‘Not yet,’ said Ralph, sarcastically.", "\"아직은 아니오.\" 랄프가 비꼬듯 말했어."]], [["‘Ha!", "\"하! 하!"], ["ha!", "젠장.\""], ["demmit,’ cried the gentleman; when, wheeling round to laugh with greater elegance, he encountered Kate Nickleby, who was standing near.", "그 신사가 외쳤어. 그러고는 좀 더 우아하게 웃으려고 휙 몸을 돌리다가, 근처에 서 있던 케이트 니클비와 마주쳤어."]], [["‘My niece,’ said Ralph.", "\"내 조카요.\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘I remember,’ said the gentleman, striking his nose with the knuckle of his forefinger as a chastening for his forgetfulness.", "\"기억나는군요.\" 그 신사가 깜빡한 걸 벌하듯 검지 마디로 제 코를 톡 치며 말했어."], ["‘Demmit, I remember what you come for.", "\"젠장, 무슨 일로 왔는지 기억나요."], ["Step this way, Nickleby; my dear, will you follow me?", "이쪽으로 오시오, 니클비. 아가씨, 나를 따라오겠소?"], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["They all follow me, Nickleby; always did, demmit, always.’", "다들 나를 따라오지요, 니클비. 늘 그래요, 젠장, 늘 그렇다오.\""]], [["Giving loose to the playfulness of his imagination, after this fashion, the gentleman led the way to a private sitting-room on the second floor, scarcely less elegantly furnished than the apartment below, where the presence of a silver coffee-pot, an egg-shell, and sloppy china for one, seemed to show that he had just breakfasted.", "그 신사는 이런 식으로 제 익살을 마음껏 풀어 놓으며, 삼 층의 어느 개인 응접실로 앞장서 갔어. 아래층 방에 못지않게 우아하게 꾸며진 곳이었지. 거기엔 은제 커피포트와 달걀 껍데기, 그리고 일 인분짜리 어수선한 도자기 그릇이 놓여 있어서, 그가 방금 아침을 먹은 모양이라는 걸 알 수 있었어."]], [["‘Sit down, my dear,’ said the gentleman: first staring Miss Nickleby out of countenance, and then grinning in delight at the achievement.", "\"앉으시오, 아가씨.\" 그 신사가 말했어."], ["‘This cursed high room takes one’s breath away.", "그러고는 케이트가 무안해질 만큼 빤히 쳐다보더니, 그 짓에 성공했다는 듯 흐뭇하게 씩 웃었어. \"이 빌어먹게 높은 방은 사람 숨이 막힌다니까."], ["These infernal sky parlours--I’m afraid I must move, Nickleby.’", "이 망할 놈의 꼭대기 방 말이오. 아무래도 이사를 가야겠소, 니클비.\""]], [["‘I would, by all means,’ replied Ralph, looking bitterly round.", "\"꼭 그러시오.\" 랄프가 씁쓸하게 주위를 둘러보며 대답했어."]], [["‘What a demd rum fellow you are, Nickleby,’ said the gentleman, ‘the demdest, longest-headed, queerest-tempered old coiner of gold and silver ever was--demmit.’", "\"당신은 정말 빌어먹게 묘한 작자요, 니클비.\" 그 신사가 말했어. \"이제껏 본 중에 제일 빌어먹게 약아빠지고, 제일 잔머리 잘 굴리고, 제일 별난 성미를 지닌 늙은 금화 은화 주조꾼이라니까. 젠장.\""]], [["Having complimented Ralph to this effect, the gentleman rang the bell, and stared at Miss Nickleby until it was answered, when he left off to bid the man desire his mistress to come directly; after which, he began again, and left off no more until Madame Mantalini appeared.", "랄프를 이런 식으로 칭찬하고 나서, 그 신사는 종을 울리고는 사람이 올 때까지 케이트를 빤히 쳐다봤어. 사람이 오자 그는 시선을 거두고, 마님더러 당장 오시라고 전하라고 일렀지. 그러고는 다시 케이트를 빤히 쳐다보기 시작해서, 만탈리니 부인이 나타날 때까지 멈추지 않았어."]], [["The dressmaker was a buxom person, handsomely dressed and rather good-looking, but much older than the gentleman in the Turkish trousers, whom she had wedded some six months before. His name was originally Muntle; but it had been converted, by an easy transition, into Mantalini: the lady rightly considering that an English appellation would be of serious injury to the business. He had married on his whiskers; upon which property he had previously subsisted, in a genteel manner, for some years; and which he had recently improved, after patient cultivation by the addition of a moustache, which promised to secure him an easy independence: his share in the labours of the business being at present confined to spending the money, and occasionally, when that ran short, driving to Mr. Ralph Nickleby to procure discount--at a percentage--for the customers’ bills.", "이 양장점 주인은 풍만한 몸매에, 옷을 멋지게 차려입은 제법 잘생긴 여자였어. 하지만 한 여섯 달 전에 결혼한, 그 터키풍 바지를 입은 신사보다는 훨씬 나이가 많았지. 그의 원래 이름은 먼틀이었어. 그런데 그게 쉬운 변형을 거쳐 만탈리니로 바뀐 거야. 영국식 이름이 사업에 심각한 해가 될 거라고 부인이 옳게 판단했거든. 그는 제 구레나룻 덕에 결혼한 셈이었어. 그 구레나룻을 밑천 삼아 그는 여러 해 동안 점잖게 먹고살아 왔고, 최근엔 끈기 있게 가꾼 끝에 콧수염까지 보태 그 밑천을 개선해 놨지. 그 콧수염이 그에게 편안한 자립을 보장해 줄 것 같았어. 현재 그가 사업에서 맡은 몫이라곤, 돈을 쓰는 일과, 그 돈이 떨어졌을 때 가끔 랄프 니클비한테 마차를 몰고 가서 고객들의 어음을 일정 수수료를 떼고 할인받아 오는 일뿐이었거든."]], [["‘My life,’ said Mr.", "\"내 생명이여.\""], ["Mantalini, ‘what a demd devil of a time you have been!’", "만탈리니 씨가 말했어. \"어쩌면 그렇게 빌어먹게 지독히도 오래 걸렸소!\""]], [["‘I didn’t even know Mr.", "\"니클비 씨가 와 계신 줄도 몰랐어요, 여보.\""], ["Nickleby was here, my love,’ said Madame Mantalini.", "만탈리니 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘Then what a doubly demd infernal rascal that footman must be, my soul,’ remonstrated Mr.", "\"그렇다면 그 하인 녀석이 갑절로 빌어먹을 형편없는 악당이로군, 내 영혼이여.\""], ["Mantalini.", "만탈리니 씨가 항의했어."]], [["‘My dear,’ said Madame, ‘that is entirely your fault.’", "\"여보.\" 만탈리니 부인이 말했어. \"그건 전부 당신 잘못이에요.\""]], [["‘My fault, my heart’s joy?’", "\"내 잘못이라고, 내 마음의 기쁨이여?\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ returned the lady; ‘what can you expect, dearest, if you will not correct the man?’", "\"그럼요.\" 부인이 대꾸했어. \"당신이 그 사람을 야단치지 않으니, 뭘 기대해요, 여보?\""]], [["‘Correct the man, my soul’s delight!’", "\"그를 야단치라고, 내 영혼의 즐거움이여!\""]], [["‘Yes; I am sure he wants speaking to, badly enough,’ said Madame, pouting.", "\"네. 그 사람한테 따끔하게 말 좀 해 줘야겠어요, 정말로요.\" 부인이 입을 삐죽이며 말했어."]], [["‘Then do not vex itself,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 화내지 마시오.\""], ["Mantalini; ‘he shall be horse-whipped till he cries out demnebly.’ With this promise Mr.", "만탈리니 씨가 말했어. \"그놈을 빌어먹게 비명을 지를 때까지 채찍질해 주리다.\""], ["Mantalini kissed Madame Mantalini, and, after that performance, Madame Mantalini pulled Mr.", "이렇게 약속하고 만탈리니 씨는 만탈리니 부인에게 입을 맞췄어."], ["Mantalini playfully by the ear: which done, they descended to business.", "그러자 만탈리니 부인은 만탈리니 씨의 귀를 장난스레 잡아당겼지. 그러고 나서 둘은 본론으로 들어갔어."]], [["‘Now, ma’am,’ said Ralph, who had looked on, at all this, with such scorn as few men can express in looks, ‘this is my niece.’", "\"자, 부인.\" 이 모든 광경을, 어지간한 사내로선 표정에 담기 힘들 만큼의 경멸을 띠고 지켜보던 랄프가 말했어. \"이 애가 내 조카요.\""]], [["‘Just so, Mr.", "\"그렇군요, 니클비 씨.\""], ["Nickleby,’ replied Madame Mantalini, surveying Kate from head to foot, and back again.", "만탈리니 부인이 케이트를 머리끝부터 발끝까지, 다시 발끝부터 머리끝까지 훑어보며 대답했어."], ["‘Can you speak French, child?’", "\"프랑스어 할 줄 아니, 얘?\""]], [["‘Yes, ma’am,’ replied Kate, not daring to look up; for she felt that the eyes of the odious man in the dressing-gown were directed towards her.", "\"네, 부인.\" 케이트가 차마 고개를 들지 못한 채 대답했어. 실내복을 입은 그 역겨운 사내의 눈길이 자기를 향하고 있는 게 느껴졌거든."]], [["‘Like a demd native?’ asked the husband.", "\"빌어먹게 원어민처럼?\" 남편이 물었어."]], [["Miss Nickleby offered no reply to this inquiry, but turned her back upon the questioner, as if addressing herself to make answer to what his wife might demand.", "케이트는 이 물음엔 아무 대답도 하지 않고, 묻는 사람한테서 등을 돌려 버렸어. 마치 부인이 물을 만한 것에 답하려고 마음먹은 듯이 말이야."]], [["‘We keep twenty young women constantly employed in the establishment,’ said Madame.", "\"우리 가게에선 스무 명의 젊은 여공을 늘 부리고 있단다.\" 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘Indeed, ma’am!’ replied Kate, timidly.", "\"그렇군요, 부인!\" 케이트가 조심스레 대답했어."]], [["‘Yes; and some of ‘em demd handsome, too,’ said the master.", "\"그래, 그중엔 빌어먹게 예쁜 애들도 좀 있고.\" 주인이 말했어."]], [["‘Mantalini!’ exclaimed his wife, in an awful voice.", "\"만탈리니!\" 그의 아내가 무시무시한 목소리로 외쳤어."]], [["‘My senses’ idol!’ said Mantalini.", "\"내 감각의 우상이여!\" 만탈리니가 말했어."]], [["‘Do you wish to break my heart?’", "\"내 가슴을 찢어 놓을 작정이에요?\""]], [["‘Not for twenty thousand hemispheres populated with--with--with little ballet-dancers,’ replied Mantalini in a poetical strain.", "\"꼬마 발레 무희들로 우글거리는 반구가 이만 개 있다 해도 그럴 수야 없지.\" 만탈리니가 시적인 어조로 대답했어."]], [["‘Then you will, if you persevere in that mode of speaking,’ said his wife.", "\"계속 그런 식으로 말하면 정말 그렇게 될 거예요.\" 그의 아내가 말했어."], ["‘What can Mr. Nickleby think when he hears you?’", "\"니클비 씨가 들으면 어떻게 생각하시겠어요?\""]], [["‘Oh! Nothing, ma’am, nothing,’ replied Ralph. ‘I know his amiable nature, and yours,--mere little remarks that give a zest to your daily intercourse--lovers’ quarrels that add sweetness to those domestic joys which promise to last so long--that’s all; that’s all.’", "\"오! 아무 생각도 안 하오, 부인, 아무것도.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"나는 저 사람의 다정한 성품도 알고, 부인의 성품도 아오. 그저 매일의 정담에 흥취를 더해 주는 자그마한 말장난이지요. 그토록 오래 갈 것 같은 가정의 기쁨에 달콤함을 보태는 연인들의 다툼이오. 그게 다요. 그게 다지요.\""]], [["If an iron door could be supposed to quarrel with its hinges, and to make a firm resolution to open with slow obstinacy, and grind them to powder in the process, it would emit a pleasanter sound in so doing, than did these words in the rough and bitter voice in which they were uttered by Ralph.", "쇠문이 제 경첩과 다투기로 작정하고, 느리고 고집스럽게 열리면서 그 경첩을 갈아 가루로 만들어 버린다고 쳐 봐. 그렇다 해도 그 소리가, 랄프가 거칠고 쓰디쓴 목소리로 내뱉은 이 말보다는 듣기 좋았을 거야."], ["Even Mr.", "만탈리니 씨조차 그 영향을 느끼고는, 깜짝 놀라 몸을 돌리며 외쳤어."], ["Mantalini felt their influence, and turning affrighted round, exclaimed: ‘What a demd horrid croaking!’", "\"이 무슨 빌어먹게 끔찍한 까마귀 울음소리람!\""]], [["‘You will pay no attention, if you please, to what Mr.", "\"제발 만탈리니 씨가 하는 말엔 신경 쓰지 마라.\""], ["Mantalini says,’ observed his wife, addressing Miss Nickleby.", "그의 아내가 케이트에게 말했어."]], [["‘I do not, ma’am,’ said Kate, with quiet contempt.", "\"네, 안 써요, 부인.\" 케이트가 조용한 경멸을 담아 말했어."]], [["‘Mr. Mantalini knows nothing whatever about any of the young women,’ continued Madame, looking at her husband, and speaking to Kate.", "\"만탈리니 씨는 그 어떤 여공에 대해서도 아무것도 몰라.\""], ["‘If he has seen any of them, he must have seen them in the street, going to, or returning from, their work, and not here.", "부인이 남편을 쳐다보면서 케이트에게 말을 이었어. \"혹시 봤다 해도, 거리에서 일하러 오가는 걸 봤을 뿐이지 여기서 본 게 아니란다."], ["He was never even in the room.", "이 방에 들어와 본 적도 없어."], ["I do not allow it.", "내가 허락하질 않으니까."], ["What hours of work have you been accustomed to?’", "그래, 너는 몇 시간이나 일하는 데 익숙하니?\""]], [["‘I have never yet been accustomed to work at all, ma’am,’ replied Kate, in a low voice.", "\"아직 일이라곤 전혀 해 본 적이 없어요, 부인.\" 케이트가 낮은 목소리로 대답했어."]], [["‘For which reason she’ll work all the better now,’ said Ralph, putting in a word, lest this confession should injure the negotiation.", "\"그러니 이제 더 잘 배울 거요.\" 이 고백이 협상에 해가 될까 봐 랄프가 한마디 끼워 넣었어."]], [["‘I hope so,’ returned Madame Mantalini; ‘our hours are from nine to nine, with extra work when we’re very full of business, for which I allow payment as overtime.’", "\"그러길 바라요.\" 만탈리니 부인이 받아 말했어. \"우리 일과는 아홉 시부터 아홉 시까지란다. 일이 아주 밀릴 땐 추가로 일을 하는데, 그건 초과 근무로 따로 삯을 쳐 주지.\""]], [["Kate bowed her head, to intimate that she heard, and was satisfied.", "케이트는 알아들었고 만족한다는 뜻으로 고개를 숙였어."]], [["‘Your meals,’ continued Madame Mantalini, ‘that is, dinner and tea, you will take here. I should think your wages would average from five to seven shillings a week; but I can’t give you any certain information on that point, until I see what you can do.’", "\"끼니는,\" 만탈리니 부인이 말을 이었어. \"그러니까 점심과 차는 여기서 먹게 될 거야. 삯은 일주일에 다섯에서 일곱 실링쯤 될 것 같구나. 하지만 네 솜씨를 봐야 그 점은 확실히 말해 줄 수 있겠지.\""]], [["Kate bowed her head again.", "케이트는 다시 고개를 숙였어."]], [["‘If you’re ready to come,’ said Madame Mantalini, ‘you had better begin on Monday morning at nine exactly, and Miss Knag the forewoman shall then have directions to try you with some easy work at first.", "\"올 마음이 됐다면,\" 만탈리니 부인이 말했어."], ["Is there anything more, Mr.", "\"월요일 아침 아홉 시 정각에 시작하는 게 좋겠다. 그러면 작업반장인 내그 양한테 일러서 처음엔 쉬운 일부터 시켜 보게 하마."], ["Nickleby?’", "더 하실 말씀 있나요, 니클비 씨?\""]], [["‘Nothing more, ma’am,’ replied Ralph, rising.", "\"더는 없소, 부인.\" 랄프가 일어서며 대답했어."]], [["‘Then I believe that’s all,’ said the lady.", "\"그럼 이게 다인 것 같네요.\" 부인이 말했어."], ["Having arrived at this natural conclusion, she looked at the door, as if she wished to be gone, but hesitated notwithstanding, as though unwilling to leave to Mr Mantalini the sole honour of showing them downstairs.", "이렇게 자연스레 결말에 이르자, 그녀는 어서 가고 싶다는 듯 문을 바라봤어. 그래도 만탈리니 씨한테 두 사람을 아래층까지 배웅하는 영광을 혼자 차지하게 두긴 싫은 듯, 그래도 머뭇거렸지."], ["Ralph relieved her from her perplexity by taking his departure without delay: Madame Mantalini making many gracious inquiries why he never came to see them; and Mr.", "랄프가 지체 없이 떠나 줘서 그녀의 난처함을 덜어 줬어. 만탈리니 부인은 왜 한 번도 보러 안 오시느냐고 상냥하게 자꾸 물었지."], ["Mantalini anathematising the stairs with great volubility as he followed them down, in the hope of inducing Kate to look round,--a hope, however, which was destined to remain ungratified.", "그리고 만탈리니 씨는 두 사람을 따라 내려가면서, 케이트가 돌아봐 주길 바라는 마음에 계단에 대고 청산유수로 욕을 퍼부었어. 하지만 그 바람은 끝내 이루어질 운명이 아니었지."]], [["‘There!’ said Ralph when they got into the street; ‘now you’re provided for.’", "\"자!\" 거리로 나오자 랄프가 말했어. \"이제 네 앞가림은 마련됐다.\""]], [["Kate was about to thank him again, but he stopped her.", "케이트가 다시 감사 인사를 하려 했지만, 그가 막았어."]], [["‘I had some idea,’ he said, ‘of providing for your mother in a pleasant part of the country--(he had a presentation to some almshouses on the borders of Cornwall, which had occurred to him more than once)--but as you want to be together, I must do something else for her. She has a little money?’", "\"내 생각엔 말이다.\" 그가 말했어. \"네 어머니를 시골 어디 좋은 데 모실까 했었다.\" (그는 콘월 경계에 있는 어느 자선원에 한 사람을 들여보낼 권리가 있었는데, 그게 여러 번 머릿속에 떠올랐던 거야.) \"하지만 너희가 같이 있고 싶다니, 어머니한테는 다른 걸 해 줘야겠지. 네 어머니한테 돈이 좀 있느냐?\""]], [["‘A very little,’ replied Kate.", "\"아주 조금요.\" 케이트가 대답했어."]], [["‘A little will go a long way if it’s used sparingly,’ said Ralph.", "\"조금이라도 아껴 쓰면 오래 가는 법이지.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘She must see how long she can make it last, living rent free.", "\"집세 안 내고 살면서 그 돈을 얼마나 오래 버틸 수 있는지 봐야지."], ["You leave your lodgings on Saturday?’", "토요일에 그 셋방을 비우는 거지?\""]], [["‘You told us to do so, uncle.’", "\"삼촌이 그렇게 하라고 하셨잖아요.\""]], [["‘Yes; there is a house empty that belongs to me, which I can put you into till it is let, and then, if nothing else turns up, perhaps I shall have another. You must live there.’", "\"그래. 내 소유인데 비어 있는 집이 하나 있다. 세가 나갈 때까지 거기 너희를 들여보내 주마. 그리고 그때 가서 달리 뾰족한 수가 없으면, 어쩌면 또 다른 집이 생기겠지. 너희는 거기서 살아야 한다.\""]], [["‘Is it far from here, sir?’ inquired Kate.", "\"여기서 먼가요, 삼촌?\" 케이트가 물었어."]], [["‘Pretty well,’ said Ralph; ‘in another quarter of the town--at the East end; but I’ll send my clerk down to you, at five o’clock on Saturday, to take you there.", "\"꽤 멀지.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["Goodbye.", "\"이 도시의 다른 구역, 이스트엔드에 있어."], ["You know your way?", "하지만 토요일 다섯 시에 내 서기를 너희한테 보내 거기로 데려가게 하마. 잘 가라."], ["Straight on.’", "길은 알지? 곧장 가면 된다.\""]], [["Coldly shaking his niece’s hand, Ralph left her at the top of Regent Street, and turned down a by-thoroughfare, intent on schemes of money-getting.", "랄프는 조카의 손을 차갑게 한번 잡아 주고는, 리젠트 거리 꼭대기에서 케이트와 헤어졌어. 그러고는 돈벌이 궁리에 골몰하며 샛길로 접어들어 갔지."], ["Kate walked sadly back to their lodgings in the Strand.", "케이트는 슬픈 마음으로 스트랜드에 있는 셋방을 향해 터덜터덜 돌아갔어."]], [["# CHAPTER 11", "# CHAPTER 11"]], [["Newman Noggs inducts Mrs. and Miss Nickleby into their New Dwelling in the City\nMiss Nickleby’s reflections, as she wended her way homewards, were of that desponding nature which the occurrences of the morning had been sufficiently calculated to awaken.", "뉴먼 노그스가 니클비 부인과 케이트를 도시에 있는 새 거처로 안내하다 케이트의 머릿속을 채운 생각들은, 집으로 돌아가는 길에서 우울하기 짝이 없었다."], ["Her uncle’s was not a manner likely to dispel any doubts or apprehensions she might have formed, in the outset, neither was the glimpse she had had of Madame Mantalini’s establishment by any means encouraging.", "그날 아침에 겪은 일들을 떠올리면 그럴 만도 했지. 삼촌의 태도는 처음부터 그녀가 품게 된 의심이나 불안을 걷어 줄 만한 게 아니었고, 마담 만탈리니의 가게를 흘끗 본 것도 조금도 마음을 북돋아 주지 못했어."], ["It was with many gloomy forebodings and misgivings, therefore, that she looked forward, with a heavy heart, to the opening of her new career.", "그래서 케이트는 무거운 마음으로, 곧 시작될 새 일자리를 온갖 어두운 예감과 걱정 속에서 떠올렸던 거야."]], [["If her mother’s consolations could have restored her to a pleasanter and more enviable state of mind, there were abundance of them to produce the effect. By the time Kate reached home, the good lady had called to mind two authentic cases of milliners who had been possessed of considerable property, though whether they had acquired it all in business, or had had a capital to start with, or had been lucky and married to advantage, she could not exactly remember. However, as she very logically remarked, there must have been SOME young person in that way of business who had made a fortune without having anything to begin with, and that being taken for granted, why should not Kate do the same? Miss La Creevy, who was a member of the little council, ventured to insinuate some doubts relative to the probability of Miss Nickleby’s arriving at this happy consummation in the compass of an ordinary lifetime; but the good lady set that question entirely at rest, by informing them that she had a presentiment on the subject--a species of second-sight with which she had been in the habit of clenching every argument with the deceased Mr. Nickleby, and, in nine cases and three-quarters out of every ten, determining it the wrong way.", "어머니의 위로가 케이트를 한결 즐겁고 부러운 마음 상태로 되돌려 줄 수 있었다면, 그런 효과를 낼 위로는 차고 넘쳤어. 케이트가 집에 닿을 무렵, 이 마음씨 좋은 부인은 상당한 재산을 모은 옷가게 주인 두 명의 실제 사례를 떠올려 냈거든. 그 재산을 전부 장사로 벌었는지, 아니면 처음부터 밑천이 있었는지, 그것도 아니면 운이 좋아서 좋은 데로 시집을 갔는지는 정확히 기억나지 않았지만 말이야. 그래도 부인은 아주 논리적으로 이렇게 말했어. 그 장사 길에서 아무것도 없이 시작해 한밑천 잡은 젊은 사람이 분명히 있었을 테고, 그게 사실이라면 케이트라고 못할 게 뭐냐는 거였지. 이 작은 회의의 일원이던 미스 라 크리비가 슬며시 의심을 끼워 넣어 봤어. 보통 사람의 한평생 안에 미스 니클비가 그렇게 행복한 결말에 다다를 가능성이 있겠느냐고 말이야. 하지만 부인은 그 문제를 단번에 잠재워 버렸어. 자기는 이 일에 예감이 든다는 거였지. 일종의 예지력 같은 건데, 부인은 돌아가신 니클비 씨와 다툴 때마다 이걸로 마무리를 짓곤 했어. 그리고 열 번 가운데 아홉 번하고도 사 분의 삼은 틀린 쪽으로 결판이 났지."]], [["‘I am afraid it is an unhealthy occupation,’ said Miss La Creevy.", "\"옷 만드는 일이 건강에 안 좋을까 봐 걱정이에요.\" 미스 라 크리비가 말했어."], ["‘I recollect getting three young milliners to sit to me, when I first began to paint, and I remember that they were all very pale and sickly.’", "\"제가 처음 그림을 시작했을 때 젊은 옷가게 아가씨 셋을 모델로 앉혀 본 적이 있는데, 다들 얼굴이 아주 창백하고 병약했던 게 기억나거든요.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"어머! 그게 꼭 그런 건 아니에요.\""], ["that’s not a general rule by any means,’ observed Mrs.", "니클비 부인이 말했어. \"내가 어제 일처럼 또렷이 기억하는데, 빨간 망토가 유행이던 때에 특별히 추천받은 사람한테 빨간 망토를 하나 맡긴 적이 있거든요."], ["Nickleby; ‘for I remember, as well as if it was only yesterday, employing one that I was particularly recommended to, to make me a scarlet cloak at the time when scarlet cloaks were fashionable, and she had a very red face--a very red face, indeed.’", "그런데 그 사람 얼굴이 아주 빨갰어요. 정말이지 아주 빨갰다니까요.\""]], [["‘Perhaps she drank,’ suggested Miss La Creevy.", "\"술을 마셔서 그랬나 보죠.\" 미스 라 크리비가 넌지시 말했어."]], [["‘I don’t know how that may have been,’ returned Mrs.", "\"그건 어땠는지 모르겠지만,\" 니클비 부인이 대꾸했어."], ["Nickleby: ‘but I know she had a very red face, so your argument goes for nothing.’", "\"얼굴이 아주 빨갰던 건 분명해요. 그러니 댁 말은 아무 소용없는 거죠.\""]], [["In this manner, and with like powerful reasoning, did the worthy matron meet every little objection that presented itself to the new scheme of the morning.", "이런 식으로, 또 이만큼 강력한 논리로, 이 훌륭한 부인은 아침에 세운 새 계획에 들이대는 자잘한 반론을 하나하나 받아쳤어."], ["Happy Mrs. Nickleby!", "행복한 니클비 부인 같으니!"], ["A project had but to be new, and it came home to her mind, brightly varnished and gilded as a glittering toy.", "어떤 계획이든 새롭기만 하면, 부인의 머릿속에는 번쩍번쩍 광을 내고 금칠을 한 반짝이는 장난감처럼 다가왔거든."]], [["This question disposed of, Kate communicated her uncle’s desire about the empty house, to which Mrs. Nickleby assented with equal readiness, characteristically remarking, that, on the fine evenings, it would be a pleasant amusement for her to walk to the West end to fetch her daughter home; and no less characteristically forgetting, that there were such things as wet nights and bad weather to be encountered in almost every week of the year.", "이 문제가 정리되자, 케이트는 빈집에 관한 삼촌의 뜻을 전했어. 니클비 부인도 똑같이 선뜻 동의했지. 그러면서 자기다운 말을 했어. 날이 좋은 저녁이면 서쪽 끝까지 걸어가 딸을 집으로 데려오는 게 즐거운 소일거리가 되겠다는 거였지. 그리고 역시나 자기답게, 일 년 중 거의 매주 비 오는 밤이나 궂은 날씨를 만나야 한다는 사실은 까맣게 잊고 있었어."]], [["‘I shall be sorry--truly sorry to leave you, my kind friend,’ said Kate, on whom the good feeling of the poor miniature painter had made a deep impression.", "\"정말, 정말로 헤어지기가 아쉬워요, 친절하신 분.\" 케이트가 말했어. 이 가난한 세밀화가의 따뜻한 마음씨가 케이트에게 깊은 인상을 남겼던 거야."]], [["‘You shall not shake me off, for all that,’ replied Miss La Creevy, with as much sprightliness as she could assume.", "\"그래도 그렇게 쉽게 날 떼어 내진 못할걸요.\" 미스 라 크리비가 짐짓 기운을 내며 대꾸했어."], ["‘I shall see you very often, and come and hear how you get on; and if, in all London, or all the wide world besides, there is no other heart that takes an interest in your welfare, there will be one little lonely woman that prays for it night and day.’", "\"아주 자주 만날 거고, 찾아가서 어떻게 지내는지 들을 거예요. 그리고 온 런던, 아니 이 넓은 세상을 통틀어 아가씨가 잘되기를 바라는 마음이 하나도 없다 해도, 밤낮으로 그걸 빌어 주는 외로운 여자가 한 명은 있을 거예요.\""]], [["With this, the poor soul, who had a heart big enough for Gog, the guardian genius of London, and enough to spare for Magog to boot, after making a great many extraordinary faces which would have secured her an ample fortune, could she have transferred them to ivory or canvas, sat down in a corner, and had what she termed ‘a real good cry.’", "이 말과 함께, 런던의 수호신 고그를 다 품고도 매고그한테까지 나눠 줄 만큼 큰 마음을 가진 이 가엾은 사람은, 갖가지 유난스러운 표정을 잔뜩 지었어. 그 표정들을 상아나 캔버스에 옮길 수만 있었다면 두둑한 재산을 마련할 수 있었을 텐데 말이야. 그러고는 한쪽 구석에 앉아, 자기 표현으로 \"한바탕 시원하게 우는\" 시간을 가졌지."]], [["But no crying, or talking, or hoping, or fearing, could keep off the dreaded Saturday afternoon, or Newman Noggs either; who, punctual to his time, limped up to the door, and breathed a whiff of cordial gin through the keyhole, exactly as such of the church clocks in the neighbourhood as agreed among themselves about the time, struck five. Newman waited for the last stroke, and then knocked.", "하지만 아무리 울고, 떠들고, 바라고, 두려워해 봐도, 그 두려운 토요일 오후를 막을 수는 없었어. 뉴먼 노그스도 마찬가지였지. 그는 시간을 딱 맞춰 절뚝거리며 문 앞까지 와서는 열쇠 구멍으로 향긋한 진 냄새를 훅 풍겼어. 마침 그때, 동네 교회 시계들 가운데 서로 시간이 맞는 것들이 다섯 시를 쳤지. 뉴먼은 마지막 종소리를 기다렸다가 문을 두드렸어."]], [["‘From Mr.", "\"랄프 니클비 씨가 보내서 왔습니다.\""], ["Ralph Nickleby,’ said Newman, announcing his errand, when he got upstairs, with all possible brevity.", "위층에 올라온 뉴먼이 가능한 한 짧게 용건을 알렸어."]], [["‘We shall be ready directly,’ said Kate.", "\"금방 준비할게요.\" 케이트가 말했어."], ["‘We have not much to carry, but I fear we must have a coach.’", "\"옮길 짐은 많지 않은데, 그래도 마차가 있어야 할 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘I’ll get one,’ replied Newman.", "\"제가 한 대 잡아 오죠.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어."]], [["‘Indeed you shall not trouble yourself,’ said Mrs.", "\"정말 그렇게 수고하실 것 없어요.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘I will,’ said Newman.", "\"잡아 오겠습니다.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."]], [["‘I can’t suffer you to think of such a thing,’ said Mrs.", "\"그런 건 생각도 못 하게 하겠어요.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘You can’t help it,’ said Newman.", "\"어쩔 수 없으실걸요.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."]], [["‘Not help it!’", "\"어쩔 수 없다니요!\""]], [["‘No; I thought of it as I came along; but didn’t get one, thinking you mightn’t be ready.", "\"네, 오는 길에 생각은 했는데, 준비가 안 되셨을까 봐 안 잡아 왔거든요."], ["I think of a great many things.", "저는 여러 가지를 생각합니다."], ["Nobody can prevent that.’", "그건 누구도 못 막죠.\""]], [["‘Oh yes, I understand you, Mr.", "\"아, 알겠어요, 노그스 씨.\""], ["Noggs,’ said Mrs. Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["‘Our thoughts are free, of course.", "\"생각이야 물론 자유죠."], ["Everybody’s thoughts are their own, clearly.’", "누구든 자기 생각은 자기 거니까요.\""]], [["‘They wouldn’t be, if some people had their way,’ muttered Newman.", "\"어떤 사람들 뜻대로 됐다면 그렇지도 않았을 겁니다.\" 뉴먼이 중얼거렸어."]], [["‘Well, no more they would, Mr.", "\"맞아요, 정말 그렇지 않겠죠, 노그스 씨."], ["Noggs, and that’s very true,’ rejoined Mrs Nickleby.", "참 맞는 말이에요.\" 니클비 부인이 맞장구쳤어."], ["‘Some people to be sure are such--how’s your master?’", "\"어떤 사람들은 정말이지 그렇게나, 그런데 주인 어른은 잘 계세요?\""]], [["Newman darted a meaning glance at Kate, and replied with a strong emphasis on the last word of his answer, that Mr.", "뉴먼은 케이트에게 의미심장한 눈길을 던지고는, 대답 마지막 말에 잔뜩 힘을 주어 이렇게 말했어."], ["Ralph Nickleby was well, and sent his LOVE.", "랄프 니클비 씨는 잘 계시고, \"사랑\"을 전한다고 하셨다고."]], [["‘I am sure we are very much obliged to him,’ observed Mrs.", "\"정말 그분께 너무나 감사하네요.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘Very,’ said Newman.", "\"네, 그러시겠죠.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."], ["‘I’ll tell him so.’", "\"그렇게 전하겠습니다.\""]], [["It was no very easy matter to mistake Newman Noggs, after having once seen him, and as Kate, attracted by the singularity of his manner (in which on this occasion, however, there was something respectful and even delicate, notwithstanding the abruptness of his speech), looked at him more closely, she recollected having caught a passing glimpse of that strange figure before.", "뉴먼 노그스라는 사람은 한 번 보고 나면 잘못 알아볼 일이 없었어. 그래서 케이트는 그의 독특한 태도에 끌려 — 그런데 이번에는 말투가 무뚝뚝한데도 어딘가 정중하고 심지어 섬세한 데가 있었지 — 그를 좀 더 자세히 들여다봤어. 그러자 전에 그 이상한 모습을 흘끗 본 적이 있다는 게 떠올랐어."]], [["‘Excuse my curiosity,’ she said, ‘but did I not see you in the coachyard, on the morning my brother went away to Yorkshire?’", "\"제 궁금증을 너그러이 봐주세요.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"그런데 제 오빠가 요크셔로 떠나던 날 아침, 마차 마당에서 당신을 보지 않았나요?\""]], [["Newman cast a wistful glance on Mrs. Nickleby and said ‘No,’ most unblushingly.", "뉴먼은 니클비 부인을 슬쩍 곁눈질하더니, 낯빛 하나 안 변하고 \"아니요\"라고 했어."]], [["‘No!’ exclaimed Kate, ‘I should have said so anywhere.’", "\"아니라고요!\" 케이트가 외쳤어. \"어디서든 분명 당신이라고 했을 텐데요.\""]], [["‘You’d have said wrong,’ rejoined Newman.", "\"잘못 보신 겁니다.\" 뉴먼이 대꾸했어."], ["‘It’s the first time I’ve been out for three weeks.", "\"저는 삼 주 만에 처음 밖에 나왔어요."], ["I’ve had the gout.’", "통풍을 앓았거든요.\""]], [["Newman was very, very far from having the appearance of a gouty subject, and so Kate could not help thinking; but the conference was cut short by Mrs.", "뉴먼은 통풍 환자로는 도무지 보이지 않았어. 케이트도 그렇게 생각하지 않을 수 없었지."], ["Nickleby’s insisting on having the door shut, lest Mr.", "하지만 대화는 곧 끊겼어."], ["Noggs should take cold, and further persisting in sending the servant girl for a coach, for fear he should bring on another attack of his disorder.", "니클비 부인이 노그스 씨가 감기 들까 봐 문을 닫아야 한다고 우긴 데다, 그가 또 발작을 일으킬까 봐 하녀를 시켜 마차를 불러오게 하겠다고 고집을 부렸거든."], ["To both conditions, Newman was compelled to yield.", "두 가지 다 뉴먼은 따를 수밖에 없었어."], ["Presently, the coach came; and, after many sorrowful farewells, and a great deal of running backwards and forwards across the pavement on the part of Miss La Creevy, in the course of which the yellow turban came into violent contact with sundry foot-passengers, it (that is to say the coach, not the turban) went away again, with the two ladies and their luggage inside; and Newman, despite all Mrs.", "이윽고 마차가 왔고, 슬픈 작별 인사를 여러 번 나눈 뒤에, 미스 라 크리비가 보도를 이리저리 부산히 오가는 통에 노란 터번이 지나가던 행인 몇몇과 거세게 부딪치는 소동까지 겪은 끝에, 그것이 — 그러니까 터번이 아니라 마차가 — 다시 움직였어. 두 여자와 짐을 안에 싣고서 말이야."], ["Nickleby’s assurances that it would be his death--on the box beside the driver.", "그리고 뉴먼은, 그러다 죽는다는 니클비 부인의 거듭된 장담에도 아랑곳없이 마부 옆 좌석에 올라탔지."]], [["They went into the city, turning down by the river side; and, after a long and very slow drive, the streets being crowded at that hour with vehicles of every kind, stopped in front of a large old dingy house in Thames Street: the door and windows of which were so bespattered with mud, that it would have appeared to have been uninhabited for years.", "그들은 강가를 따라 내려가 시티 안으로 들어갔어. 그 시간에는 온갖 종류의 탈것이 거리에 가득해서, 길고 아주 느린 마차 여행 끝에 템스 거리의 크고 오래되고 거무튀튀한 집 앞에 멈춰 섰지. 그 집은 문이며 창이며 진흙이 잔뜩 튀어 있어서, 몇 년이나 사람이 안 산 것처럼 보였어."]], [["The door of this deserted mansion Newman opened with a key which he took out of his hat--in which, by-the-bye, in consequence of the dilapidated state of his pockets, he deposited everything, and would most likely have carried his money if he had had any--and the coach being discharged, he led the way into the interior of the mansion.", "뉴먼은 모자에서 꺼낸 열쇠로 이 버려진 저택의 문을 열었어. 참고로 그는 주머니가 너덜너덜해진 탓에 모든 걸 모자 안에 넣고 다녔거든. 돈이 있었다면 십중팔구 돈도 거기 넣어 다녔을 거야. 마차를 돌려보낸 뒤, 그는 앞장서서 저택 안으로 들어갔어."]], [["Old, and gloomy, and black, in truth it was, and sullen and dark were the rooms, once so bustling with life and enterprise.", "정말로 낡고 음침하고 시커먼 집이었어. 한때 그렇게 활기와 사업으로 북적이던 방들이 이제는 침침하고 어두웠지."], ["There was a wharf behind, opening on the Thames.", "집 뒤로는 템스강과 맞닿은 부두가 있었어."], ["An empty dog-kennel, some bones of animals, fragments of iron hoops, and staves of old casks, lay strewn about, but no life was stirring there.", "텅 빈 개집 하나, 짐승 뼈 몇 개, 부서진 쇠테 조각들, 낡은 통 널빤지가 여기저기 흩어져 있었지만, 살아 움직이는 건 아무것도 없었어."], ["It was a picture of cold, silent decay.", "그건 차갑고 조용한 쇠락의 풍경이었지."]], [["‘This house depresses and chills one,’ said Kate, ‘and seems as if some blight had fallen on it.", "\"이 집은 사람을 짓누르고 으스스하게 만들어요.\" 케이트가 말했어."], ["If I were superstitious, I should be almost inclined to believe that some dreadful crime had been perpetrated within these old walls, and that the place had never prospered since.", "\"마치 무슨 저주라도 내린 것 같아요. 제가 미신을 믿는 사람이었다면, 이 낡은 벽 안에서 무슨 끔찍한 죄가 저질러졌고, 그 뒤로 이곳이 한 번도 흥하지 못한 거라고 거의 믿어 버렸을 거예요."], ["How frowning and how dark it looks!’", "얼마나 험상궂고 컴컴해 보이는지!\""]], [["‘Lord, my dear,’ replied Mrs.", "\"얘야, 세상에.\" 니클비 부인이 대꾸했어."], ["Nickleby, ‘don’t talk in that way, or you’ll frighten me to death.’", "\"그런 식으로 말하지 마라. 안 그러면 무서워서 죽겠다.\""]], [["‘It is only my foolish fancy, mama,’ said Kate, forcing a smile.", "\"그냥 제 어리석은 상상이에요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 억지로 미소를 지으며 말했어."]], [["‘Well, then, my love, I wish you would keep your foolish fancy to yourself, and not wake up MY foolish fancy to keep it company,’ retorted Mrs.", "\"그래, 그럼 얘야, 그 어리석은 상상은 너 혼자 간직하고, 거기 짝지어 줄 내 어리석은 상상까지 깨우지는 말아 다오.\" 니클비 부인이 받아쳤어."], ["Nickleby.", "\"왜 이런 걸 미리 생각 안 했니, 넌 어쩜 그렇게 조심성이 없는지."], ["‘Why didn’t you think of all this before--you are so careless--we might have asked Miss La Creevy to keep us company or borrowed a dog, or a thousand things--but it always was the way, and was just the same with your poor dear father.", "미스 라 크리비한테 같이 있어 달라고 부탁하든가, 개라도 한 마리 빌리든가, 방법이야 수천 가지였잖니. 그런데 늘 이런 식이었어, 네 가엾은 아버지 때도 똑같았지."], ["Unless I thought of everything--’ This was Mrs.", "내가 모든 걸 다 생각하지 않으면—\" 이게 니클비 부인이 으레 늘어놓는 한탄의 시작이었어."], ["Nickleby’s usual commencement of a general lamentation, running through a dozen or so of complicated sentences addressed to nobody in particular, and into which she now launched until her breath was exhausted.", "딱히 누구를 향한 것도 아닌 복잡한 문장이 열 개 남짓 줄줄이 이어지는 그런 한탄 말이야. 부인은 이번에도 숨이 다 찰 때까지 그걸 쏟아 냈지."]], [["Newman appeared not to hear these remarks, but preceded them to a couple of rooms on the first floor, which some kind of attempt had been made to render habitable.", "뉴먼은 이런 말을 못 들은 척하고, 두 사람을 데리고 이층의 방 두 개로 앞장서 갔어. 그래도 사람이 지낼 만하게 어느 정도 손을 써 둔 방이었지."], ["In one, were a few chairs, a table, an old hearth-rug, and some faded baize; and a fire was ready laid in the grate.", "한 방에는 의자 몇 개와 탁자, 낡은 벽난로 깔개, 색 바랜 모직 천이 있었고, 벽난로에는 불을 피울 준비가 다 되어 있었어."], ["In the other stood an old tent bedstead, and a few scanty articles of chamber furniture.", "다른 방에는 오래된 천막식 침대 하나와 침실 가구 몇 점이 단출하게 놓여 있었지."]], [["‘Well, my dear,’ said Mrs.", "\"얘야, 자,\""], ["Nickleby, trying to be pleased, ‘now isn’t this thoughtful and considerate of your uncle?", "니클비 부인이 기뻐하려 애쓰며 말했어. \"네 삼촌이 이렇게 사려 깊고 자상하지 않니?"], ["Why, we should not have had anything but the bed we bought yesterday, to lie down upon, if it hadn’t been for his thoughtfulness!’", "그분이 신경을 안 써 주셨더라면, 우리는 어제 산 침대 말고는 누울 데도 없었을 거야!\""]], [["‘Very kind, indeed,’ replied Kate, looking round.", "\"정말 친절하시네요.\" 케이트가 방 안을 둘러보며 대답했어."]], [["Newman Noggs did not say that he had hunted up the old furniture they saw, from attic and cellar; or that he had taken in the halfpennyworth of milk for tea that stood upon a shelf, or filled the rusty kettle on the hob, or collected the woodchips from the wharf, or begged the coals.", "뉴먼 노그스는 두 사람이 보고 있는 그 낡은 가구를 자기가 다락과 지하실에서 일일이 뒤져 찾아냈다는 말은 하지 않았어. 선반에 놓인 반 페니어치 차 마실 우유를 자기가 받아 두었다는 것도, 화로 위 녹슨 주전자에 물을 채워 둔 것도, 부두에서 나뭇조각을 주워 모은 것도, 석탄을 얻어 온 것도 말하지 않았지."], ["But the notion of Ralph Nickleby having directed it to be done, tickled his fancy so much, that he could not refrain from cracking all his ten fingers in succession: at which performance Mrs.", "하지만 랄프 니클비가 이렇게 하라고 시켰다는 듯한 생각이 어찌나 우습던지, 그는 열 손가락을 차례로 우두둑 꺾지 않을 수 없었어."], ["Nickleby was rather startled at first, but supposing it to be in some remote manner connected with the gout, did not remark upon.", "그 모습에 니클비 부인은 처음엔 흠칫 놀랐지만, 어쩐지 통풍과 관련이 있겠거니 짐작하고는 아무 말도 하지 않았어."]], [["‘We need detain you no longer, I think,’ said Kate.", "\"이제 더 붙잡을 일은 없을 것 같네요.\" 케이트가 말했어."]], [["‘Is there nothing I can do?’ asked Newman.", "\"제가 해 드릴 일이 더 없을까요?\" 뉴먼이 물었어."]], [["‘Nothing, thank you,’ rejoined Miss Nickleby.", "\"없어요, 고맙습니다.\" 케이트가 대답했어."]], [["‘Perhaps, my dear, Mr.", "\"얘야, 노그스 씨가 우리 건강을 위해 한잔하고 싶어 하실지도 모르잖니.\""], ["Noggs would like to drink our healths,’ said Mrs Nickleby, fumbling in her reticule for some small coin.", "니클비 부인이 손지갑을 뒤적여 잔돈을 찾으며 말했어."]], [["‘I think, mama,’ said Kate hesitating, and remarking Newman’s averted face, ‘you would hurt his feelings if you offered it.’", "\"엄마, 제 생각엔,\" 케이트가 머뭇거리며, 뉴먼이 고개를 돌린 걸 보고 말했어. \"그걸 건네면 저분 마음이 상할 것 같아요.\""]], [["Newman Noggs, bowing to the young lady more like a gentleman than the miserable wretch he seemed, placed his hand upon his breast, and, pausing for a moment, with the air of a man who struggles to speak but is uncertain what to say, quitted the room.", "뉴먼 노그스는 보기엔 초라한 신세였지만, 그런 사람보다는 훨씬 신사답게 케이트에게 고개를 숙였어. 그러고는 가슴에 손을 얹고, 무슨 말을 하려고 애쓰지만 뭐라 해야 할지 몰라 망설이는 사람처럼 잠시 멈췄다가, 방을 나갔어."]], [["As the jarring echoes of the heavy house-door, closing on its latch, reverberated dismally through the building, Kate felt half tempted to call him back, and beg him to remain a little while; but she was ashamed to own her fears, and Newman Noggs was on his road homewards.", "육중한 현관문이 걸쇠에 닫히면서 그 거슬리는 메아리가 집 안에 음산하게 울려 퍼지자, 케이트는 그를 다시 불러 잠깐만 더 있어 달라고 부탁하고 싶은 마음이 반쯤 들었어. 하지만 자기가 무서워한다는 걸 털어놓기가 부끄러웠지. 그리고 뉴먼 노그스는 이미 집으로 가는 길에 올라 있었어."]], [["# CHAPTER 12", "# CHAPTER 12"]], [["Whereby the Reader will be enabled to trace the further course of Miss Fanny Squeer’s Love, and to ascertain whether it ran smooth or otherwise. It was a fortunate circumstance for Miss Fanny Squeers, that when her worthy papa returned home on the night of the small tea-party, he was what the initiated term ‘too far gone’ to observe the numerous tokens of extreme vexation of spirit which were plainly visible in her countenance. Being, however, of a rather violent and quarrelsome mood in his cups, it is not impossible that he might have fallen out with her, either on this or some imaginary topic, if the young lady had not, with a foresight and prudence highly commendable, kept a boy up, on purpose, to bear the first brunt of the good gentleman’s anger; which, having vented itself in a variety of kicks and cuffs, subsided sufficiently to admit of his being persuaded to go to bed. Which he did with his boots on, and an umbrella under his arm.", "이건 패니 스퀴어스 양에게는 아주 다행스러운 일이었어. 작은 다과회가 있던 그날 밤, 훌륭하신 아버지가 집에 돌아왔을 때 그는 이른바 술꾼들 말로 '갈 데까지 간' 상태였거든. 그래서 딸 얼굴에 또렷이 드러나 있던, 속이 잔뜩 뒤틀린 온갖 기색을 알아챌 처지가 못 되었지. 그런데 그는 술이 들어가면 좀 거칠고 시비를 거는 성미였어. 그러니 이 일로든 아니면 무슨 엉뚱한 트집으로든 딸과 한바탕 싸웠을지도 몰라. 다만 이 아가씨가 칭찬할 만큼 앞을 내다보고 영리하게 굴어서, 일부러 아이 하나를 안 재우고 깨워 두었지. 착하신 아버지의 분노가 처음 터질 때 그 화살을 대신 맞으라고 말이야. 그래서 그 화는 발길질이며 주먹질로 실컷 풀리고 나서야 가라앉았어. 겨우 잠자리로 가도록 달랠 수 있을 만큼 말이야. 그는 장화도 안 벗고, 우산도 옆구리에 낀 채로 자러 갔지."]], [["The hungry servant attended Miss Squeers in her own room according to custom, to curl her hair, perform the other little offices of her toilet, and administer as much flattery as she could get up, for the purpose; for Miss Squeers was quite lazy enough (and sufficiently vain and frivolous withal) to have been a fine lady; and it was only the arbitrary distinctions of rank and station which prevented her from being one.", "굶주린 하녀는 늘 하던 대로 스퀴어스 양 방으로 따라가서 머리를 곱슬곱슬 말아 주고, 단장에 필요한 자질구레한 시중을 들고, 그러면서 할 수 있는 만큼 아첨을 늘어놓았어. 다 그러려고 따라간 거였거든. 스퀴어스 양은 게으르기로 치면 (게다가 허영심 많고 경박하기로도) 귀부인이 되고도 남을 정도였어. 단지 신분과 지위라는 멋대로 정해진 차별 때문에 귀부인이 못 됐을 뿐이었지."]], [["‘How lovely your hair do curl tonight, miss!’ said the handmaiden.", "\"아가씨, 오늘 밤은 머리가 어쩜 이렇게 예쁘게 말리는지 몰라요!\" 하녀가 말했어."], ["‘I declare if it isn’t a pity and a shame to brush it out!’", "\"이걸 다시 빗어 푸는 게 정말 아깝고 죄스러울 지경이라니까요!\""]], [["‘Hold your tongue!’ replied Miss Squeers wrathfully.", "\"입 다물어!\" 스퀴어스 양이 성을 내며 대꾸했어."]], [["Some considerable experience prevented the girl from being at all surprised at any outbreak of ill-temper on the part of Miss Squeers.", "하녀는 그동안 겪을 만큼 겪어서, 스퀴어스 양이 짜증을 부려도 조금도 놀라지 않았어."], ["Having a half-perception of what had occurred in the course of the evening, she changed her mode of making herself agreeable, and proceeded on the indirect tack.", "그날 저녁에 무슨 일이 있었는지 어렴풋이 눈치챈 그녀는, 비위 맞추는 방식을 바꿔서 에둘러 가는 쪽으로 작전을 폈지."]], [["‘Well, I couldn’t help saying, miss, if you was to kill me for it,’ said the attendant, ‘that I never see nobody look so vulgar as Miss Price this night.’", "\"근데요, 아가씨, 절 죽이신대도 이 말은 안 할 수가 없네요.\" 하녀가 말했어. \"오늘 밤 프라이스 양처럼 천박해 보이는 사람은 난생처음 봤어요.\""]], [["Miss Squeers sighed, and composed herself to listen.", "스퀴어스 양은 한숨을 쉬더니, 마음을 가다듬고 귀를 기울였어."]], [["‘I know it’s very wrong in me to say so, miss,’ continued the girl, delighted to see the impression she was making, ‘Miss Price being a friend of your’n, and all; but she do dress herself out so, and go on in such a manner to get noticed, that--oh--well, if people only saw themselves!’", "\"제가 이런 말 하는 게 아주 잘못인 줄은 알아요, 아가씨.\" 하녀가 말을 이었어. 자기 말이 먹혀드는 게 보여서 신이 난 거야. \"프라이스 양이 아가씨 친구고 뭐고 다 그런데도요. 그래도 그 사람은 어찌나 차려입고, 어떻게든 눈에 띄려고 그렇게 굴던지요. 그러니까, 아휴, 글쎄, 사람들이 제 꼴을 좀 봐야 한다니까요!\""]], [["‘What do you mean, Phib?’ asked Miss Squeers, looking in her own little glass, where, like most of us, she saw--not herself, but the reflection of some pleasant image in her own brain.", "\"그게 무슨 말이야, 핍?\" 스퀴어스 양이 제 작은 거울을 들여다보며 물었어."], ["‘How you talk!’", "우리 대부분이 그렇듯, 그녀도 거울 속에서 자기 자신이 아니라 제 머릿속에 그려진 어떤 흐뭇한 모습을 비춰 보고 있었지. \"무슨 그런 말을 다 해!\""]], [["‘Talk, miss!", "\"무슨 말이라뇨, 아가씨!"], ["It’s enough to make a Tom cat talk French grammar, only to see how she tosses her head,’ replied the handmaid.", "그 사람이 머리를 쳐드는 꼴을 보면 수고양이도 프랑스어 문법을 떠들 지경인걸요.\" 하녀가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘She DOES toss her head,’ observed Miss Squeers, with an air of abstraction.", "\"하긴 머리를 쳐들긴 하더라.\" 스퀴어스 양이 딴 데 정신이 팔린 듯한 투로 말했어."]], [["‘So vain, and so very--very plain,’ said the girl.", "\"어찌나 잘난 척에다, 게다가 또 어찌나, 정말로, 정말로 못생겼는지요.\" 하녀가 말했어."]], [["‘Poor ‘Tilda!’ sighed Miss Squeers, compassionately.", "\"가엾은 틸다!\" 스퀴어스 양이 측은하다는 듯 한숨을 쉬었어."]], [["‘And always laying herself out so, to get to be admired,’ pursued the servant.", "\"그러면서 늘 칭찬받으려고 그렇게 애를 쓴다니까요.\" 하녀가 이어 갔어."], ["‘Oh, dear!", "\"어휴, 세상에!"], ["It’s positive indelicate.’", "정말이지 점잖지 못해요.\""]], [["‘I can’t allow you to talk in that way, Phib,’ said Miss Squeers.", "\"네가 그런 식으로 말하는 건 두고 볼 수 없어, 핍.\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어."], ["‘’Tilda’s friends are low people, and if she don’t know any better, it’s their fault, and not hers.’", "\"틸다 친구들이 천한 사람들이잖아. 그러니 그 애가 더 나은 걸 모른다면, 그건 그 사람들 탓이지 그 애 탓이 아니야.\""]], [["‘Well, but you know, miss,’ said Phoebe, for which name ‘Phib’ was used as a patronising abbreviation, ‘if she was only to take copy by a friend--oh! if she only knew how wrong she was, and would but set herself right by you, what a nice young woman she might be in time!’", "\"그야 그렇지만요, 아가씨.\" 피비가 말했어. '핍'이라는 이름은 그 애를 한 단계 낮춰 부르는 줄임말이었지. \"그 사람도 친구 하나만 본보기로 삼으면, 아휴! 자기가 얼마나 그른지만 알고, 아가씨를 보고 똑바로 고치기만 하면, 머지않아 얼마나 괜찮은 아가씨가 되겠어요!\""]], [["‘Phib,’ rejoined Miss Squeers, with a stately air, ‘it’s not proper for me to hear these comparisons drawn; they make ‘Tilda look a coarse improper sort of person, and it seems unfriendly in me to listen to them. I would rather you dropped the subject, Phib; at the same time, I must say, that if ‘Tilda Price would take pattern by somebody--not me particularly--’", "\"핍,\" 스퀴어스 양이 거드름을 피우며 받았어. \"이런 비교를 듣는 건 나한테 어울리지 않아. 그러면 틸다가 거칠고 점잖지 못한 사람처럼 보이잖니. 게다가 그런 말을 듣고 있는 건 친구로서 의리 없는 짓 같아. 그 얘기는 그만뒀으면 좋겠다, 핍. 그래도 이 말만은 해야겠어. 틸다 프라이스가 누군가를 본보기로 삼는다면 말이야, 꼭 나라는 건 아니지만 —\""]], [["‘Oh yes; you, miss,’ interposed Phib.", "\"아휴, 아니에요. 아가씨죠.\" 핍이 끼어들었어."]], [["‘Well, me, Phib, if you will have it so,’ said Miss Squeers.", "\"그래, 나라고 치자, 핍, 네가 굳이 그렇다면.\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어."], ["‘I must say, that if she would, she would be all the better for it.’", "\"이 말만은 해야겠어. 그 애가 그런다면, 훨씬 더 나아질 텐데 말이야.\""]], [["‘So somebody else thinks, or I am much mistaken,’ said the girl mysteriously.", "\"다른 누구도 그렇게 생각한다니까요. 제가 한참 잘못 본 게 아니라면요.\" 하녀가 알쏭달쏭하게 말했어."]], [["‘What do you mean?’ demanded Miss Squeers.", "\"그게 무슨 말이야?\" 스퀴어스 양이 따져 물었어."]], [["‘Never mind, miss,’ replied the girl; ‘I know what I know; that’s all.’", "\"신경 쓰지 마세요, 아가씨.\" 하녀가 대꾸했어. \"전 제가 아는 걸 알 뿐이에요. 그게 다예요.\""]], [["‘Phib,’ said Miss Squeers dramatically, ‘I insist upon your explaining yourself.", "\"핍,\" 스퀴어스 양이 자못 비장하게 말했어."], ["What is this dark mystery?", "\"똑똑히 설명을 해 봐. 이 어두운 비밀이 대체 뭐야?"], ["Speak.’", "말해.\""]], [["‘Why, if you will have it, miss, it’s this,’ said the servant girl.", "\"그럼 정 그러시다면, 아가씨, 이런 거예요.\" 하녀가 말했어."], ["‘Mr John Browdie thinks as you think; and if he wasn’t too far gone to do it creditable, he’d be very glad to be off with Miss Price, and on with Miss Squeers.’", "\"존 브라우디 씨도 아가씨랑 똑같이 생각해요. 그리고 그분이 갈 데까지 가서 제대로 못 할 처지만 아니었으면요, 프라이스 양은 떼어 버리고 스퀴어스 양이랑 어떻게 해 보고 싶어 안달했을걸요.\""]], [["‘Gracious heavens!’ exclaimed Miss Squeers, clasping her hands with great dignity.", "\"맙소사!\" 스퀴어스 양이 잔뜩 위엄을 부리며 두 손을 맞잡고 외쳤어."], ["‘What is this?’", "\"이게 무슨 소리야?\""]], [["‘Truth, ma’am, and nothing but truth,’ replied the artful Phib.", "\"진실이에요, 아가씨, 진실 말고는 아무것도 아니랍니다.\" 약삭빠른 핍이 대꾸했어."]], [["‘What a situation!’ cried Miss Squeers; ‘on the brink of unconsciously destroying the peace and happiness of my own ‘Tilda.", "\"이 무슨 처지람!\" 스퀴어스 양이 부르짖었어."], ["What is the reason that men fall in love with me, whether I like it or not, and desert their chosen intendeds for my sake?’", "\"나도 모르는 새에 우리 틸다의 평화와 행복을 망쳐 버릴 지경이라니. 대체 무슨 까닭으로 남자들이, 내가 좋아하든 말든 나한테 반해서, 정해 놓은 약혼녀까지 버리고 나한테로 오는 걸까?\""]], [["‘Because they can’t help it, miss,’ replied the girl; ‘the reason’s plain.’ (If Miss Squeers were the reason, it was very plain.)", "\"어쩔 수 없으니까 그렇죠, 아가씨.\" 하녀가 대꾸했어. \"이유는 뻔하잖아요.\" (스퀴어스 양이 그 이유라면야, 정말 뻔하긴 했지.)"]], [["‘Never let me hear of it again,’ retorted Miss Squeers.", "\"다시는 그런 말 내 귀에 들리게 하지 마.\" 스퀴어스 양이 쏘아붙였어."], ["‘Never!", "\"절대로! 알겠어?"], ["Do you hear?", "틸다 프라이스도 흠은 있어. 흠이 많지."], ["‘Tilda Price has faults--many faults--but I wish her well, and above all I wish her married; for I think it highly desirable--most desirable from the very nature of her failings--that she should be married as soon as possible.", "그래도 난 그 애가 잘되길 바라. 무엇보다 그 애가 시집가길 바란다고."], ["No, Phib. Let her have Mr.", "그 애 결점들의 성질로 봐서, 되도록 빨리 시집가는 게 더없이 바람직하다고 생각하거든."], ["Browdie.", "아니야, 핍. 그 애가 브라우디 씨를 갖게 둬."], ["I may pity HIM, poor fellow; but I have a great regard for ‘Tilda, and only hope she may make a better wife than I think she will.’", "난 그 사람이 가엾을지도 모르지, 딱한 양반. 하지만 난 틸다를 무척 아끼니까, 그저 그 애가 내 생각보다는 더 나은 아내가 되기만 바랄 뿐이야.\""]], [["With this effusion of feeling, Miss Squeers went to bed.", "이렇게 한바탕 감정을 쏟아 낸 뒤, 스퀴어스 양은 잠자리에 들었어."]], [["Spite is a little word; but it represents as strange a jumble of feelings, and compound of discords, as any polysyllable in the language. Miss Squeers knew as well in her heart of hearts that what the miserable serving-girl had said was sheer, coarse, lying flattery, as did the girl herself; yet the mere opportunity of venting a little ill-nature against the offending Miss Price, and affecting to compassionate her weaknesses and foibles, though only in the presence of a solitary dependant, was almost as great a relief to her spleen as if the whole had been gospel truth. Nay, more. We have such extraordinary powers of persuasion when they are exerted over ourselves, that Miss Squeers felt quite high-minded and great after her noble renunciation of John Browdie’s hand, and looked down upon her rival with a kind of holy calmness and tranquillity, that had a mighty effect in soothing her ruffled feelings.", "심술이란 건 짧은 단어야. 하지만 이 말은 이 언어의 그 어떤 긴 단어 못지않게, 이상하게 뒤엉킨 감정과 어긋난 마음들의 뒤범벅을 담고 있지. 스퀴어스 양은 가슴 깊은 곳에서 잘 알고 있었어. 그 가련한 하녀가 한 말이 죄다 새빨갛고 천박한 거짓 아첨이라는 걸 말이야. 그 하녀 자신이 아는 만큼이나 똑똑히 알았지. 그래도 미운 프라이스 양에게 심술을 좀 풀어 보고, 그 약점이며 흠을 가엾어하는 척해 보는 것만으로도, 비록 의지가지없는 하녀 하나 앞에서뿐이었지만, 그건 그 모든 게 복음서의 진리였던 것만큼이나 화를 푸는 데 큰 위안이 됐어. 아니, 그 이상이었지. 우리는 그 설득의 힘을 우리 자신에게 발휘할 때 어찌나 놀라운지, 스퀴어스 양은 존 브라우디의 손을 그토록 고결하게 마다하고 나니 제 마음이 아주 드높고 위대해진 것 같았어. 그래서 어떤 거룩한 평온과 고요함을 띠고 연적을 내려다보았지. 그게 헝클어진 마음을 달래는 데 엄청난 효험이 있었어."]], [["This happy state of mind had some influence in bringing about a reconciliation; for, when a knock came at the front-door next day, and the miller’s daughter was announced, Miss Squeers betook herself to the parlour in a Christian frame of spirit, perfectly beautiful to behold.", "이렇게 마음이 흐뭇해진 덕에 화해도 어느 정도 이루어졌어. 다음 날 현관문에서 노크 소리가 나고 방앗간 집 딸이 왔다고 알려 왔을 때, 스퀴어스 양은 보기에 더없이 아름다운, 그야말로 자비로운 마음가짐으로 응접실로 갔거든."]], [["‘Well, Fanny,’ said the miller’s daughter, ‘you see I have come to see you, although we HAD some words last night.’", "\"있잖아, 패니.\" 방앗간 집 딸이 말했어. \"어젯밤에 우리가 좀 말다툼을 하긴 했지만, 그래도 이렇게 너 보러 왔어.\""]], [["‘I pity your bad passions, ‘Tilda,’ replied Miss Squeers, ‘but I bear no malice.", "\"난 네 못된 성미가 가엾을 뿐이야, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 대꾸했어."], ["I am above it.’", "\"그래도 난 앙심 같은 건 없어. 난 그런 거 넘어선 사람이거든.\""]], [["‘Don’t be cross, Fanny,’ said Miss Price.", "\"성내지 마, 패니.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어."], ["‘I have come to tell you something that I know will please you.’", "\"네가 들으면 분명 좋아할 얘기를 해 주러 왔단 말이야.\""]], [["‘What may that be, ‘Tilda?’ demanded Miss Squeers; screwing up her lips, and looking as if nothing in earth, air, fire, or water, could afford her the slightest gleam of satisfaction.", "\"그게 뭔데, 틸다?\" 스퀴어스 양이 입술을 앙다물고 따져 물었어. 땅이든 하늘이든 불이든 물이든, 세상 그 무엇도 그녀에게 한 줄기 만족조차 줄 수 없다는 듯한 표정으로 말이야."]], [["‘This,’ rejoined Miss Price.", "\"이거야.\" 프라이스 양이 받았어."], ["‘After we left here last night John and I had a dreadful quarrel.’", "\"어젯밤 우리가 여기서 나간 뒤에, 존이랑 내가 무지하게 싸웠거든.\""]], [["‘That doesn’t please me,’ said Miss Squeers--relaxing into a smile though.", "\"그건 안 반가운데.\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어. 그래도 빙긋이 미소가 풀려 나오긴 했지."]], [["‘Lor!", "\"어머!"], ["I wouldn’t think so bad of you as to suppose it did,’ rejoined her companion.", "네가 그걸 반가워할 만큼 나쁜 애라고는 생각 안 해.\" 친구가 대꾸했어."], ["‘That’s not it.’", "\"그게 아니야.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ said Miss Squeers, relapsing into melancholy.", "\"아!\" 스퀴어스 양이 다시 우울해지며 말했어."], ["‘Go on.’", "\"계속해 봐.\""]], [["‘After a great deal of wrangling, and saying we would never see each other any more,’ continued Miss Price, ‘we made it up, and this morning John went and wrote our names down to be put up, for the first time, next Sunday, so we shall be married in three weeks, and I give you notice to get your frock made.’", "\"한참을 옥신각신하면서 다시는 서로 안 볼 거라고 하다가,\" 프라이스 양이 말을 이었어. \"결국 화해했어. 그래서 오늘 아침에 존이 가서 우리 이름을 적어 냈어. 이번 주 일요일에 처음으로 결혼 예고가 붙는 거야. 그러니까 우린 삼 주 뒤에 결혼해. 그러니 너도 드레스 맞추라고 미리 일러두는 거야.\""]], [["There was mingled gall and honey in this intelligence. The prospect of the friend’s being married so soon was the gall, and the certainty of her not entertaining serious designs upon Nicholas was the honey. Upon the whole, the sweet greatly preponderated over the bitter, so Miss Squeers said she would get the frock made, and that she hoped ‘Tilda might be happy, though at the same time she didn’t know, and would not have her build too much upon it, for men were strange creatures, and a great many married women were very miserable, and wished themselves single again with all their hearts; to which condolences Miss Squeers added others equally calculated to raise her friend’s spirits and promote her cheerfulness of mind.", "이 소식에는 쓴 쓸개즙과 단 꿀이 뒤섞여 있었어. 친구가 그렇게 곧 결혼한다는 게 쓸개즙이었고, 그 애가 니콜라스한테 진지한 속셈이 없다는 게 분명해진 건 꿀이었지. 따지고 보면 단맛이 쓴맛을 훨씬 앞섰어. 그래서 스퀴어스 양은 드레스를 맞추겠다고 했어. 그리고 틸다가 행복하길 바란다고 했지. 하지만 동시에, 그건 모를 일이고 너무 큰 기대는 걸지 말라고 했어. 남자란 묘한 생물이고, 결혼한 여자들 가운데 아주 많은 이들이 무척 비참해져서 다시 처녀로 돌아가고 싶어 한다고 진심으로 말이야. 이 위로에 스퀴어스 양은 다른 위로들도 보탰어. 친구의 기운을 북돋고 마음을 즐겁게 해 주려고 한, 그것들도 똑같이 계산된 말들이었지."]], [["‘But come now, Fanny,’ said Miss Price, ‘I want to have a word or two with you about young Mr.", "\"근데 있잖아, 패니.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어."], ["Nickleby.’", "\"젊은 니클비 씨 얘기를 너랑 한두 마디 나누고 싶은데.\""]], [["‘He is nothing to me,’ interrupted Miss Squeers, with hysterical symptoms.", "\"그 사람은 나랑 아무 사이도 아니야.\" 스퀴어스 양이 신경질적인 기색을 보이며 말을 끊었어."], ["‘I despise him too much!’", "\"난 그 사람 너무 경멸한다고!\""]], [["‘Oh, you don’t mean that, I am sure?’ replied her friend. ‘Confess, Fanny; don’t you like him now?’", "\"어머, 너 진심 아니지? 그럴 리 없어.\" 친구가 대꾸했어. \"솔직히 말해 봐, 패니. 지금도 그 사람 좋아하잖아?\""]], [["Without returning any direct reply, Miss Squeers, all at once, fell into a paroxysm of spiteful tears, and exclaimed that she was a wretched, neglected, miserable castaway.", "스퀴어스 양은 곧이곧대로 대답은 않고, 별안간 분에 받친 눈물을 쏟아 내며 발작하듯 외쳐 댔어. 자기는 가엾고, 버림받고, 비참한 신세라고 말이야."]], [["‘I hate everybody,’ said Miss Squeers, ‘and I wish that everybody was dead--that I do.’", "\"난 모두가 다 미워.\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어. \"다들 죽어 버렸으면 좋겠어. 정말이야.\""]], [["‘Dear, dear,’ said Miss Price, quite moved by this avowal of misanthropical sentiments.", "\"저런, 저런.\" 프라이스 양이 이렇게 사람 미워하는 마음을 털어놓는 데 적잖이 마음이 움직여서 말했어."], ["‘You are not serious, I am sure.’", "\"너 진심 아니지? 분명 그럴 거야.\""]], [["‘Yes, I am,’ rejoined Miss Squeers, tying tight knots in her pocket-handkerchief and clenching her teeth.", "\"아니, 진심이야.\" 스퀴어스 양이 손수건에 매듭을 꽉꽉 지으며, 이를 악물고 대꾸했어."], ["‘And I wish I was dead too.", "\"그리고 나도 죽었으면 좋겠어."], ["There!’", "자!\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["you’ll think very differently in another five minutes,’ said Matilda.", "오 분만 지나면 생각이 완전히 달라질걸.\" 마틸다가 말했어."], ["‘How much better to take him into favour again, than to hurt yourself by going on in that way.", "\"그렇게 굴면서 너 자신을 괴롭히느니, 그 사람을 다시 받아 주는 게 훨씬 낫잖아."], ["Wouldn’t it be much nicer, now, to have him all to yourself on good terms, in a company-keeping, love-making, pleasant sort of manner?’", "자, 이제 그 사람을 너 혼자 차지해서 사이좋게, 같이 어울리고, 사랑도 나누고, 즐겁게 지내는 게 훨씬 좋지 않겠어?\""]], [["‘I don’t know but what it would,’ sobbed Miss Squeers.", "\"그게 나을지도 모르지.\" 스퀴어스 양이 흐느꼈어."], ["‘Oh!", "\"아휴!"], ["‘Tilda, how could you have acted so mean and dishonourable!", "틸다, 어쩜 그렇게 비열하고 떳떳지 못하게 굴 수가 있었니!"], ["I wouldn’t have believed it of you, if anybody had told me.’", "누가 나한테 말해 줬어도 난 네가 그랬을 거라곤 안 믿었을 거야.\""]], [["‘Heyday!’ exclaimed Miss Price, giggling.", "\"어머나!\" 프라이스 양이 까르르 웃으며 외쳤어."], ["‘One would suppose I had been murdering somebody at least.’", "\"꼭 내가 누구라도 죽인 줄 알겠네.\""]], [["‘Very nigh as bad,’ said Miss Squeers passionately.", "\"그거나 거의 마찬가지지.\" 스퀴어스 양이 격하게 말했어."]], [["‘And all this because I happen to have enough of good looks to make people civil to me,’ cried Miss Price.", "\"이게 다, 내가 사람들이 친절하게 대해 줄 만큼 인물이 반반하다는 이유 때문이잖아.\" 프라이스 양이 외쳤어."], ["‘Persons don’t make their own faces, and it’s no more my fault if mine is a good one than it is other people’s fault if theirs is a bad one.’", "\"얼굴이야 자기가 만드는 게 아니지. 내 얼굴이 잘난 게 내 탓이 아니듯, 남들 얼굴이 못난 것도 그 사람들 탓이 아니야.\""]], [["‘Hold your tongue,’ shrieked Miss Squeers, in her shrillest tone; ‘or you’ll make me slap you, ‘Tilda, and afterwards I should be sorry for it!’", "\"입 닥쳐.\" 스퀴어스 양이 더없이 날카로운 목소리로 빽 소리쳤어. \"안 그러면 너 한 대 갈겨 버릴 거야, 틸다. 그러고 나면 내가 미안해지겠지만!\""]], [["It is needless to say, that, by this time, the temper of each young lady was in some slight degree affected by the tone of her conversation, and that a dash of personality was infused into the altercation, in consequence. Indeed, the quarrel, from slight beginnings, rose to a considerable height, and was assuming a very violent complexion, when both parties, falling into a great passion of tears, exclaimed simultaneously, that they had never thought of being spoken to in that way: which exclamation, leading to a remonstrance, gradually brought on an explanation: and the upshot was, that they fell into each other’s arms and vowed eternal friendship; the occasion in question making the fifty-second time of repeating the same impressive ceremony within a twelvemonth.", "이쯤 되니 두 아가씨의 성미가 저마다 대화의 어조에 어느 정도 물든 건 새삼 말할 것도 없지. 그 바람에 말다툼에 인신공격이 약간 끼어들었어. 사실 그 싸움은 사소하게 시작했다가 꽤 높이 치달았고, 아주 험악한 빛깔을 띠어 갔지. 그러다가 두 사람 다 펑펑 눈물을 쏟으며 동시에 외쳐 댔어. 자기가 그런 식으로 말을 들을 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐다고 말이야. 이 외침이 항변으로 이어졌고, 항변은 차츰 해명으로 번졌어. 그래서 결국 둘은 서로의 품에 안겨 영원한 우정을 맹세했지. 이번 일로 그 똑같이 감동적인 의식을 일 년 안에 쉰두 번째 되풀이하게 된 셈이었어."]], [["Perfect amicability being thus restored, a dialogue naturally ensued upon the number and nature of the garments which would be indispensable for Miss Price’s entrance into the holy state of matrimony, when Miss Squeers clearly showed that a great many more than the miller could, or would, afford, were absolutely necessary, and could not decently be dispensed with. The young lady then, by an easy digression, led the discourse to her own wardrobe, and after recounting its principal beauties at some length, took her friend upstairs to make inspection thereof. The treasures of two drawers and a closet having been displayed, and all the smaller articles tried on, it was time for Miss Price to return home; and as she had been in raptures with all the frocks, and had been stricken quite dumb with admiration of a new pink scarf, Miss Squeers said in high good humour, that she would walk part of the way with her, for the pleasure of her company; and off they went together: Miss Squeers dilating, as they walked along, upon her father’s accomplishments: and multiplying his income by ten, to give her friend some faint notion of the vast importance and superiority of her family.", "이렇게 더없는 화목이 되살아나자, 자연스레 이야기가 이어졌어. 프라이스 양이 거룩한 혼인의 길로 들어서는 데 빼놓을 수 없는 옷가지가 몇 벌이며 어떤 것들이냐 하는 거였지. 스퀴어스 양은, 방앗간 주인이 마련해 줄 수 있거나 마련해 주려는 것보다 훨씬 많은 옷이 반드시 필요하며, 체면상 그걸 빼놓을 수도 없다는 걸 또렷이 보여 줬어. 그러더니 슬쩍 화제를 돌려 자기 옷장 얘기로 끌고 갔어. 그러고는 그 주된 멋들을 한참 늘어놓더니, 친구를 위층으로 데려가서 직접 구경시켜 줬지. 서랍 두 개와 벽장 속 보물들을 죄다 펼쳐 보이고, 자잘한 물건들은 다 입어 보고 나니, 이제 프라이스 양이 집으로 돌아갈 때가 됐어. 프라이스 양이 드레스마다 황홀해하고, 새로 산 분홍색 스카프에는 감탄해서 말문이 막혀 버렸으니, 스퀴어스 양은 아주 기분이 좋아져서 같이 가 주는 게 즐거우니 가는 길을 얼마쯤 함께 걷겠다고 했어. 그래서 둘은 같이 나섰지. 걷는 동안 스퀴어스 양은 아버지의 잘난 점들을 한껏 늘어놓았어. 그리고 아버지 수입을 열 배로 부풀려서, 자기 집안이 얼마나 대단하고 우월한지를 친구가 어렴풋이나마 짐작하게 해 줬지."]], [["It happened that that particular time, comprising the short daily interval which was suffered to elapse between what was pleasantly called the dinner of Mr.", "그런데 마침 그때가, 듣기 좋게 스퀴어스 씨 학생들의 식사 시간이라고 부르는 그 시간이 끝난 뒤, 다시 유익한 공부로 돌아가기까지 날마다 허락되던 그 짧은 틈이었어. 바로 그 시간이, 니콜라스가 늘 우울한 산책을 나가던 때였지."], ["Squeers’s pupils, and their return to the pursuit of useful knowledge, was precisely the hour when Nicholas was accustomed to issue forth for a melancholy walk, and to brood, as he sauntered listlessly through the village, upon his miserable lot.", "그는 마을을 멍하니 어슬렁대며 자기 비참한 처지를 곱씹곤 했거든. 스퀴어스 양도 이걸 빤히 알고 있었어."], ["Miss Squeers knew this perfectly well, but had perhaps forgotten it, for when she caught sight of that young gentleman advancing towards them, she evinced many symptoms of surprise and consternation, and assured her friend that she ‘felt fit to drop into the earth.’", "그런데 깜빡했던 모양이야. 그 젊은 신사가 자기들 쪽으로 걸어오는 걸 보자, 그녀는 놀라고 당황한 기색을 잔뜩 드러내며 친구한테 '땅속으로 꺼져 버릴 것 같다'고 했거든."]], [["‘Shall we turn back, or run into a cottage?’ asked Miss Price.", "\"우리 돌아갈까, 아니면 어느 집으로 뛰어들까?\" 프라이스 양이 물었어."], ["‘He don’t see us yet.’", "\"아직 우릴 못 봤어.\""]], [["‘No, ‘Tilda,’ replied Miss Squeers, ‘it is my duty to go through with it, and I will!’", "\"아니야, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 대꾸했어. \"이건 내가 끝까지 견뎌 내야 할 일이야. 그래, 그럴 거야!\""]], [["As Miss Squeers said this, in the tone of one who has made a high moral resolution, and was, besides, taken with one or two chokes and catchings of breath, indicative of feelings at a high pressure, her friend made no further remark, and they bore straight down upon Nicholas, who, walking with his eyes bent upon the ground, was not aware of their approach until they were close upon him; otherwise, he might, perhaps, have taken shelter himself.", "스퀴어스 양이 이 말을, 무슨 드높은 도덕적 결심이라도 한 사람 같은 어조로 한 데다, 게다가 마음이 잔뜩 부풀어 오른 걸 드러내듯 한두 번 목이 메고 숨이 막히는 시늉까지 하니, 친구는 더 이상 아무 말도 하지 않았어. 그래서 둘은 곧장 니콜라스 쪽으로 밀고 갔지. 니콜라스는 눈을 땅에 떨군 채 걷고 있어서, 둘이 코앞에 닥쳐서야 다가오는 걸 알아챘어. 안 그랬으면 자기도 어디로든 피했을지 몰라."]], [["‘Good-morning,’ said Nicholas, bowing and passing by.", "\"안녕하세요.\" 니콜라스가 고개를 숙이고 지나가며 말했어."]], [["‘He is going,’ murmured Miss Squeers.", "\"가 버리잖아.\" 스퀴어스 양이 중얼거렸어."], ["‘I shall choke, ‘Tilda.’", "\"나 목이 막힐 것 같아, 틸다.\""]], [["‘Come back, Mr.", "\"돌아오세요, 니클비 씨, 어서요!\""], ["Nickleby, do!’ cried Miss Price, affecting alarm at her friend’s threat, but really actuated by a malicious wish to hear what Nicholas would say; ‘come back, Mr.", "프라이스 양이 외쳤어. 친구의 위협에 놀란 척했지만, 사실은 니콜라스가 뭐라고 할지 듣고 싶은 짓궂은 마음에서였지."], ["Nickleby!’", "\"돌아오세요, 니클비 씨!\""]], [["Mr. Nickleby came back, and looked as confused as might be, as he inquired whether the ladies had any commands for him.", "니클비 씨가 돌아와서는, 더없이 어리둥절한 얼굴로 두 분이 무슨 시키실 일이라도 있느냐고 물었어."]], [["‘Don’t stop to talk,’ urged Miss Price, hastily; ‘but support her on the other side.", "\"이야기하느라 멈추지 마시고요.\" 프라이스 양이 다급하게 재촉했어."], ["How do you feel now, dear?’", "\"반대쪽에서 이 애를 좀 부축해 주세요. 이제 좀 어떠니, 얘?\""]], [["‘Better,’ sighed Miss Squeers, laying a beaver bonnet of a reddish brown with a green veil attached, on Mr.", "\"좀 나아.\" 스퀴어스 양이 한숨을 쉬며, 초록색 베일이 달린 불그스름한 갈색 비버 가죽 보닛을 니클비 씨 어깨에 기대 놓았어."], ["Nickleby’s shoulder. ‘This foolish faintness!’", "\"이 바보 같은 어지럼증이라니!\""]], [["‘Don’t call it foolish, dear,’ said Miss Price: her bright eye dancing with merriment as she saw the perplexity of Nicholas; ‘you have no reason to be ashamed of it. It’s those who are too proud to come round again, without all this to-do, that ought to be ashamed.’", "\"바보 같다고 하지 마, 얘.\" 프라이스 양이 말했어. 니콜라스가 쩔쩔매는 꼴을 보며 그 반짝이는 눈이 재미로 춤을 추고 있었지. \"그게 부끄러워할 일은 아니야. 부끄러워해야 할 사람은, 너무 자존심이 세서 이런 소동 없이는 도로 마음을 못 돌리는 그런 사람이라고.\""]], [["‘You are resolved to fix it upon me, I see,’ said Nicholas, smiling, ‘although I told you, last night, it was not my fault.’", "\"날 기어이 거기다 엮으려고 작정하셨군요.\" 니콜라스가 미소 지으며 말했어. \"어젯밤에 제 잘못이 아니라고 말씀드렸는데도요.\""]], [["‘There; he says it was not his fault, my dear,’ remarked the wicked Miss Price.", "\"거 봐, 자기 잘못이 아니래, 얘.\" 짓궂은 프라이스 양이 말했어."], ["‘Perhaps you were too jealous, or too hasty with him?", "\"어쩌면 네가 너무 질투했거나, 너무 성급하게 대했던 거 아냐?"], ["He says it was not his fault.", "자기 잘못이 아니라잖아."], ["You hear; I think that’s apology enough.’", "너도 들었지. 이만하면 충분한 사과 같은데.\""]], [["‘You will not understand me,’ said Nicholas.", "\"제 말을 못 알아들으시네요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘Pray dispense with this jesting, for I have no time, and really no inclination, to be the subject or promoter of mirth just now.’", "\"제발 이런 농담은 그만두시죠. 저는 지금 그럴 시간도 없고, 솔직히 누구를 웃기는 거리가 되거나 누구를 웃기고 싶은 마음도 없으니까요.\""]], [["‘What do you mean?’ asked Miss Price, affecting amazement.", "\"그게 무슨 말씀이세요?\" 프라이스 양이 깜짝 놀란 척하며 물었어."]], [["‘Don’t ask him, ‘Tilda,’ cried Miss Squeers; ‘I forgive him.’", "\"저 사람한테 묻지 마, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 외쳤어. \"난 저 사람 용서할게.\""]], [["‘Dear me,’ said Nicholas, as the brown bonnet went down on his shoulder again, ‘this is more serious than I supposed.", "\"이런.\" 갈색 보닛이 다시 자기 어깨에 내려앉자 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["Allow me!", "\"이거 제 생각보다 일이 심각하군요."], ["Will you have the goodness to hear me speak?’", "잠깐만요! 제 말 좀 들어 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["Here he raised up the brown bonnet, and regarding with most unfeigned astonishment a look of tender reproach from Miss Squeers, shrunk back a few paces to be out of the reach of the fair burden, and went on to say:", "이렇게 말하며 그는 갈색 보닛을 들어 올렸어. 그러고는 스퀴어스 양이 보내는, 다정하면서도 나무라는 듯한 눈길을 더없이 진심 어린 놀라움으로 마주하더니, 그 아리따운 짐이 닿지 않게 몇 걸음 뒤로 물러서서 말을 이었어."]], [["‘I am very sorry--truly and sincerely sorry--for having been the cause of any difference among you, last night. I reproach myself, most bitterly, for having been so unfortunate as to cause the dissension that occurred, although I did so, I assure you, most unwittingly and heedlessly.’", "\"정말 미안합니다. 진심으로, 진정으로 미안해요. 어젯밤 두 분 사이에 어떤 다툼이 생긴 게 저 때문이라면요. 제가 운 나쁘게도 그 불화의 빌미가 된 걸 더없이 쓰라리게 자책하고 있어요. 비록 정말이지 전혀 뜻하지도, 생각지도 못한 채 그런 거지만요.\""]], [["‘Well; that’s not all you have got to say surely,’ exclaimed Miss Price as Nicholas paused.", "\"근데 그게 하고 싶은 말 전부는 아니겠죠?\" 니콜라스가 말을 멈추자 프라이스 양이 외쳤어."]], [["‘I fear there is something more,’ stammered Nicholas with a half-smile, and looking towards Miss Squeers, ‘it is a most awkward thing to say--but--the very mention of such a supposition makes one look like a puppy--still--may I ask if that lady supposes that I entertain any--in short, does she think that I am in love with her?’", "\"뭔가 더 있는 것 같긴 합니다.\" 니콜라스가 반쯤 웃으며 더듬더듬 말하고는, 스퀴어스 양 쪽을 바라봤어. \"이거 참 말하기 거북하네요. 하지만 — 이런 짐작을 입에 올리는 것만으로도 사람이 건방진 풋내기처럼 보이긴 하지만 — 그래도요 — 혹시 저 아가씨께서, 제가 무슨 마음을 품고 있다고 — 그러니까, 제가 자기를 사랑한다고 생각하시나요?\""]], [["‘Delightful embarrassment,’ thought Miss Squeers, ‘I have brought him to it, at last. Answer for me, dear,’ she whispered to her friend.", "'기분 좋게 쩔쩔매네.' 스퀴어스 양이 생각했어. '드디어 저 사람을 여기까지 몰고 왔구나. 나 대신 대답해 줘, 얘.' 그녀가 친구에게 속삭였어."]], [["‘Does she think so?’ rejoined Miss Price; ‘of course she does.’", "\"저 애가 그렇게 생각하느냐고요?\" 프라이스 양이 받았어. \"당연히 그렇게 생각하죠.\""]], [["‘She does!’ exclaimed Nicholas with such energy of utterance as might have been, for the moment, mistaken for rapture.", "\"그렇게요!\" 니콜라스가 어찌나 힘주어 내뱉었던지, 그 순간 황홀해하는 걸로 오해받을 만했어."]], [["‘Certainly,’ replied Miss Price", "\"그럼요.\" 프라이스 양이 대꾸했어."]], [["‘If Mr.", "\"니클비 씨가 그걸 의심하셨다면, 틸다.\""], ["Nickleby has doubted that, ‘Tilda,’ said the blushing Miss Squeers in soft accents, ‘he may set his mind at rest.", "스퀴어스 양이 얼굴을 붉히며 부드러운 어조로 말했어. \"마음 푹 놓으셔도 돼."], ["His sentiments are recipro--’", "그분 마음도 똑같—\""]], [["‘Stop,’ cried Nicholas hurriedly; ‘pray hear me. This is the grossest and wildest delusion, the completest and most signal mistake, that ever human being laboured under, or committed. I have scarcely seen the young lady half-a-dozen times, but if I had seen her sixty times, or am destined to see her sixty thousand, it would be, and will be, precisely the same. I have not one thought, wish, or hope, connected with her, unless it be--and I say this, not to hurt her feelings, but to impress her with the real state of my own--unless it be the one object, dear to my heart as life itself, of being one day able to turn my back upon this accursed place, never to set foot in it again, or think of it--even think of it--but with loathing and disgust.’", "\"멈추세요.\" 니콜라스가 황급히 외쳤어. \"제발 제 말을 들어 주세요. 이건 인간이 빠질 수 있는, 또 저지를 수 있는 가장 어처구니없고 터무니없는 착각이에요. 가장 완벽하고 명백한 오해라고요. 저는 저 아가씨를 여섯 번도 채 못 봤어요. 하지만 예순 번을 봤다 해도, 아니 앞으로 육만 번을 볼 운명이라 해도, 똑같았을 거고 똑같을 겁니다. 저는 저 아가씨와 얽힌 생각도, 바람도, 희망도 하나 없어요. 단 하나만 빼고요 — 이건 저 아가씨 마음을 아프게 하려는 게 아니라, 제 진짜 속마음을 똑똑히 알려 드리려고 하는 말입니다 — 제 목숨만큼이나 마음에 소중한 그 단 하나의 바람만 빼고요. 언젠가 이 빌어먹을 곳에 등을 돌리고 다시는 발도 들이지 않는 것, 다시는 이곳을 떠올리지조차 — 떠올리더라도 — 진저리와 혐오 없이는 떠올리지 않는 것 말입니다.\""]], [["With this particularly plain and straightforward declaration, which he made with all the vehemence that his indignant and excited feelings could bring to bear upon it, Nicholas waiting to hear no more, retreated.", "이 유난히 분명하고 거침없는 선언을, 그는 분하고 격앙된 마음에서 끌어올 수 있는 온 격렬함을 실어서 했어. 그러고는 더 들을 것도 없이 니콜라스는 물러갔지."]], [["But poor Miss Squeers!", "하지만 가엾은 스퀴어스 양! 그 분노며, 격분이며, 속이 뒤틀리는 심정이며, 그녀의 머릿속을 휘몰아친 쓰라리고 격한 감정들의 빠른 물결은 이루 말로 다 못 해."], ["Her anger, rage, and vexation; the rapid succession of bitter and passionate feelings that whirled through her mind; are not to be described.", "거절당하다니! 광고를 보고 주워 온, 일 년에 오 파운드를, 그것도 정해진 때도 없이 받는, 게다가 그 학생들이나 다름없이 먹고 자는 것까지 '얹혀'"], ["Refused!", "사는 교사 따위한테 거절당하다니."], ["refused by a teacher, picked up by advertisement, at an annual salary of five pounds payable at indefinite periods, and ‘found’ in food and lodging like the very boys themselves; and this too in the presence of a little chit of a miller’s daughter of eighteen, who was going to be married, in three weeks’ time, to a man who had gone down on his very knees to ask her.", "그것도 열여덟 살짜리 방앗간 집 딸 같은 풋내기 계집애 앞에서 말이야. 그 애는 삼 주 뒤면, 무릎까지 꿇고 청혼한 남자와 결혼할 참이었지."], ["She could have choked in right good earnest, at the thought of being so humbled.", "이렇게 망신을 당했다고 생각하니, 그녀는 정말이지 진심으로 숨이 막힐 지경이었어."]], [["But, there was one thing clear in the midst of her mortification; and that was, that she hated and detested Nicholas with all the narrowness of mind and littleness of purpose worthy a descendant of the house of Squeers.", "그래도 이 굴욕 한가운데서 한 가지만은 분명했어. 그건 그녀가, 스퀴어스 가문의 후손답게 좁아터진 마음과 옹졸한 속셈을 다 끌어모아 니콜라스를 미워하고 진저리 친다는 거였지."], ["And there was one comfort too; and that was, that every hour in every day she could wound his pride, and goad him with the infliction of some slight, or insult, or deprivation, which could not but have some effect on the most insensible person, and must be acutely felt by one so sensitive as Nicholas.", "그리고 위안거리도 하나 있었어. 그건 날마다 매시간, 그의 자존심을 후벼 파고, 무슨 무시든 모욕이든 빼앗음이든 안겨서 그를 들들 볶을 수 있다는 거였어."], ["With these two reflections uppermost in her mind, Miss Squeers made the best of the matter to her friend, by observing that Mr.", "그런 건 아무리 무딘 사람한테도 효과가 없을 수 없고, 니콜라스처럼 예민한 사람이라면 뼈저리게 느낄 게 분명했지. 이 두 가지 생각을 머릿속 맨 위에 올려놓고, 스퀴어스 양은 친구한테는 이 일을 그럴듯하게 둘러댔어."], ["Nickleby was such an odd creature, and of such a violent temper, that she feared she should be obliged to give him up; and parted from her.", "니클비 씨가 워낙 괴상한 사람인 데다 성미가 어찌나 사나운지, 아무래도 단념해야 할 것 같아서 걱정이라고 말이야. 그러고는 친구와 헤어졌지."]], [["And here it may be remarked, that Miss Squeers, having bestowed her affections (or whatever it might be that, in the absence of anything better, represented them) on Nicholas Nickleby, had never once seriously contemplated the possibility of his being of a different opinion from herself in the business. Miss Squeers reasoned that she was prepossessing and beautiful, and that her father was master, and Nicholas man, and that her father had saved money, and Nicholas had none, all of which seemed to her conclusive arguments why the young man should feel only too much honoured by her preference. She had not failed to recollect, either, how much more agreeable she could render his situation if she were his friend, and how much more disagreeable if she were his enemy; and, doubtless, many less scrupulous young gentlemen than Nicholas would have encouraged her extravagance had it been only for this very obvious and intelligible reason. However, he had thought proper to do otherwise, and Miss Squeers was outrageous.", "여기서 한마디 해 둘 게 있어. 스퀴어스 양은 니콜라스 니클비에게 애정을 (혹은 더 나은 게 없는 마당에 애정이랍시고 내세운 그 무엇이든) 쏟으면서도, 이 일에서 그가 자기와 다른 생각일 수도 있다는 가능성은 단 한 번도 진지하게 헤아려 본 적이 없었어. 스퀴어스 양의 계산은 이랬지. 자기는 매력 있고 예쁘다, 게다가 아버지는 주인이고 니콜라스는 일꾼이다, 아버지는 돈을 모았지만 니콜라스는 한 푼도 없다. 이 모든 게 그녀에게는, 저 젊은이가 자기 마음을 받았으니 오히려 분에 넘치게 영광스러워해야 할 결정적인 근거로 보였어. 게다가 그녀는 이것도 잊지 않고 떠올렸지. 자기가 그의 친구가 되어 주면 그의 처지를 얼마나 더 편하게 해 줄 수 있는지, 또 적이 되면 얼마나 더 고약하게 만들 수 있는지를 말이야. 그러니 분명, 니콜라스보다 덜 곧은 젊은이들이라면 많은 경우, 바로 이 뻔하고 알기 쉬운 이유 하나만으로도 그녀의 헛된 기대를 부추겨 줬을 거야. 하지만 그는 달리 처신하는 게 옳다고 여겼고, 그래서 스퀴어스 양은 길길이 날뛰었지."]], [["‘Let him see,’ said the irritated young lady, when she had regained her own room, and eased her mind by committing an assault on Phib, ‘if I don’t set mother against him a little more when she comes back!’", "\"두고 보라지.\" 화가 머리끝까지 난 이 아가씨가, 제 방으로 돌아와 핍한테 손찌검을 해서 분풀이를 하고 나서 말했어. \"엄마 돌아오시면 그 사람한테 더 단단히 등 돌리게 만들어 놓을 거야!\""]], [["It was scarcely necessary to do this, but Miss Squeers was as good as her word; and poor Nicholas, in addition to bad food, dirty lodging, and the being compelled to witness one dull unvarying round of squalid misery, was treated with every special indignity that malice could suggest, or the most grasping cupidity put upon him.", "이렇게까지 할 필요도 거의 없었지만, 스퀴어스 양은 말한 대로 했어. 그래서 가엾은 니콜라스는, 형편없는 음식에다 더러운 잠자리, 거기에 칙칙하고 변함없이 되풀이되는 비참함의 행렬을 억지로 지켜봐야 하는 것도 모자라, 악의가 짜낼 수 있는, 또 가장 지독한 탐욕이 안길 수 있는 온갖 별난 모욕까지 당하게 됐지."]], [["Nor was this all.", "이게 다가 아니었어."], ["There was another and deeper system of annoyance which made his heart sink, and nearly drove him wild, by its injustice and cruelty.", "또 다른, 더 뿌리 깊은 괴롭힘의 수법이 있었어. 그 부당함과 잔인함이 그의 가슴을 무너지게 했고, 거의 미쳐 버릴 지경으로 몰아갔지."]], [["The wretched creature, Smike, since the night Nicholas had spoken kindly to him in the schoolroom, had followed him to and fro, with an ever-restless desire to serve or help him; anticipating such little wants as his humble ability could supply, and content only to be near him. He would sit beside him for hours, looking patiently into his face; and a word would brighten up his care-worn visage, and call into it a passing gleam, even of happiness. He was an altered being; he had an object now; and that object was, to show his attachment to the only person--that person a stranger--who had treated him, not to say with kindness, but like a human creature.", "그 가련한 아이, 스마이크는, 니콜라스가 교실에서 다정하게 말을 걸어 준 그날 밤 이후로, 그를 졸졸 쫓아다녔어. 어떻게든 그를 섬기고 돕고 싶다는, 잠시도 가시지 않는 마음으로 말이야. 제 보잘것없는 능력으로 채워 줄 수 있는 자잘한 필요들을 미리 챙겼고, 그저 곁에 있는 것만으로 만족했지. 몇 시간이고 그 옆에 앉아 참을성 있게 그의 얼굴을 들여다봤어. 그러다 말 한마디면 그 근심 어린 얼굴이 환해지면서, 스쳐 가는 빛이, 행복이라 할 만한 빛까지 떠오르곤 했지. 그는 달라진 사람이었어. 이제 그에게는 목표가 생겼거든. 그 목표란, 자기를 — 친절하게는 그만두고라도 — 사람답게 대해 준 단 한 사람, 그것도 낯선 사람인 그에게 자기가 얼마나 마음을 붙였는지 보여 주는 거였지."]], [["Upon this poor being, all the spleen and ill-humour that could not be vented on Nicholas were unceasingly bestowed.", "니콜라스에게 풀 수 없는 온갖 심술과 짜증은, 죄다 이 가엾은 아이에게 끊임없이 쏟아졌어. 고된 막일이야 아무것도 아니었어."], ["Drudgery would have been nothing--Smike was well used to that.", "스마이크는 그런 데는 이골이 나 있었거든. 까닭 없이 얻어맞는 것도 마찬가지로 으레 있는 일이었지."], ["Buffetings inflicted without cause, would have been equally a matter of course; for to them also he had served a long and weary apprenticeship; but it was no sooner observed that he had become attached to Nicholas, than stripes and blows, stripes and blows, morning, noon, and night, were his only portion.", "그것도 길고 고달픈 세월을 거치며 단단히 익혀 둔 거였으니까. 그런데 그가 니콜라스를 따른다는 게 눈에 띄자마자, 회초리질과 주먹질, 또 회초리질과 주먹질이 아침에도 낮에도 밤에도 그의 유일한 몫이 됐어."], ["Squeers was jealous of the influence which his man had so soon acquired, and his family hated him, and Smike paid for both.", "스퀴어스는 제 일꾼이 그토록 빨리 얻은 영향력을 시샘했고, 그의 식구들은 그를 미워했지. 그래서 스마이크가 그 두 몫을 다 치른 거야."], ["Nicholas saw it, and ground his teeth at every repetition of the savage and cowardly attack.", "니콜라스는 그걸 지켜봤어. 그리고 그 야만스럽고 비겁한 매질이 되풀이될 때마다 이를 갈았지."]], [["He had arranged a few regular lessons for the boys; and one night, as he paced up and down the dismal schoolroom, his swollen heart almost bursting to think that his protection and countenance should have increased the misery of the wretched being whose peculiar destitution had awakened his pity, he paused mechanically in a dark corner where sat the object of his thoughts.", "그는 아이들을 위해 정규 수업을 몇 가지 짜 놓았어. 그러던 어느 날 밤, 그 음침한 교실을 이리저리 거닐다가, 그의 부푼 가슴이 터질 것만 같았어. 자기가 보살피고 편들어 준 것이 도리어 그 가련한 아이의 비참함을 키운 셈이라는 생각에 말이야. 유난히 딱한 그 처지가 그의 연민을 불러일으킨 아이였는데. 그는 자기도 모르게 어두운 구석에서 발을 멈췄어. 바로 그 생각의 주인공이 앉아 있는 곳이었지."]], [["The poor soul was poring hard over a tattered book, with the traces of recent tears still upon his face; vainly endeavouring to master some task which a child of nine years old, possessed of ordinary powers, could have conquered with ease, but which, to the addled brain of the crushed boy of nineteen, was a sealed and hopeless mystery. Yet there he sat, patiently conning the page again and again, stimulated by no boyish ambition, for he was the common jest and scoff even of the uncouth objects that congregated about him, but inspired by the one eager desire to please his solitary friend.", "그 가엾은 영혼은 누덕누덕한 책을 뚫어져라 들여다보고 있었어. 얼굴에는 방금 흘린 눈물 자국이 아직 남아 있었지. 평범한 머리를 가진 아홉 살짜리 아이라면 거뜬히 정복했을 어떤 과제를, 그는 헛되이 붙들고 씨름하는 중이었어. 하지만 짓눌릴 대로 짓눌린 이 열아홉 살 소년의 흐리멍덩한 머리에는, 그게 풀리지 않는, 가망 없는 수수께끼였지. 그래도 그는 거기 앉아, 참을성 있게 그 페이지를 읽고 또 읽었어. 무슨 소년다운 야심에 떠밀린 게 아니었어. 그는 제 둘레에 모여드는 그 거친 무리한테까지도 흔한 웃음거리이자 놀림감이었으니까. 그저 자기 유일한 친구를 기쁘게 해 주고 싶다는 그 간절한 바람 하나에서 비롯된 거였지."]], [["Nicholas laid his hand upon his shoulder.", "니콜라스가 그의 어깨에 손을 얹었어."]], [["‘I can’t do it,’ said the dejected creature, looking up with bitter disappointment in every feature.", "\"못 하겠어요.\" 풀이 죽은 아이가 얼굴마다 쓰라린 실망을 가득 담고 고개를 들며 말했어."], ["‘No, no.’", "\"안 돼요, 안 돼.\""]], [["‘Do not try,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"애쓰지 마.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어."]], [["The boy shook his head, and closing the book with a sigh, looked vacantly round, and laid his head upon his arm.", "아이는 고개를 저었어. 그러고는 한숨을 쉬며 책을 덮고는, 멍하니 주위를 둘러보다 팔에 머리를 묻었어."], ["He was weeping.", "울고 있었지."]], [["‘Do not for God’s sake,’ said Nicholas, in an agitated voice; ‘I cannot bear to see you.’", "\"제발, 부디 그러지 마.\" 니콜라스가 떨리는 목소리로 말했어. \"네가 그러는 걸 차마 못 보겠어.\""]], [["‘They are more hard with me than ever,’ sobbed the boy.", "\"이젠 그 어느 때보다 저한테 모질게 굴어요.\" 아이가 흐느꼈어."]], [["‘I know it,’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"나도 알아.\" 니콜라스가 받았어."], ["‘They are.’", "\"그래, 그래.\""]], [["‘But for you,’ said the outcast, ‘I should die. They would kill me; they would; I know they would.’", "\"형이 아니었으면,\" 그 버림받은 아이가 말했어. \"전 죽었을 거예요. 그 사람들이 절 죽였을걸요. 정말로요. 분명 그랬을 거예요.\""]], [["‘You will do better, poor fellow,’ replied Nicholas, shaking his head mournfully, ‘when I am gone.’", "\"넌 잘 지내게 될 거야, 가엾은 녀석.\" 니콜라스가 구슬프게 고개를 저으며 대꾸했어. \"내가 떠나고 나면 말이야.\""]], [["‘Gone!’ cried the other, looking intently in his face.", "\"떠난다고요!\" 그 아이가 그의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보며 외쳤어."]], [["‘Softly!’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"쉿!\" 니콜라스가 받았어."], ["‘Yes.’", "\"그래.\""]], [["‘Are you going?’ demanded the boy, in an earnest whisper.", "\"가시는 거예요?\" 아이가 간절하게 속삭이며 따져 물었어."]], [["‘I cannot say,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"그건 모르겠어.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어."], ["‘I was speaking more to my own thoughts, than to you.’", "\"너한테 한 말이라기보단, 내 생각을 그냥 입에 올린 거야.\""]], [["‘Tell me,’ said the boy imploringly, ‘oh do tell me, WILL you go--WILL you?’", "\"말해 줘요.\" 아이가 애원하듯 말했어. \"아, 제발 말해 줘요. 가실 거예요? 갈 거예요?\""]], [["‘I shall be driven to that at last!’ said Nicholas.", "\"결국 그러도록 내몰리겠지!\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘The world is before me, after all.’", "\"어쨌든 세상은 내 앞에 펼쳐져 있으니까.\""]], [["‘Tell me,’ urged Smike, ‘is the world as bad and dismal as this place?’", "\"말해 줘요.\" 스마이크가 재촉했어. \"바깥세상도 이곳만큼 나쁘고 음침한가요?\""]], [["‘Heaven forbid,’ replied Nicholas, pursuing the train of his own thoughts; ‘its hardest, coarsest toil, were happiness to this.’", "\"그럴 리가.\" 니콜라스가 제 생각의 갈피를 좇으며 대답했어. \"거기서 제일 고되고 험한 노동이라도, 여기에 비하면 행복일 거야.\""]], [["‘Should I ever meet you there?’ demanded the boy, speaking with unusual wildness and volubility.", "\"제가 거기서 형을 다시 만날 수 있을까요?\" 아이가 평소와 달리 거칠고 빠르게 쏟아 내며 물었어."]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Nicholas, willing to soothe him.", "\"그럼.\" 니콜라스가 그를 달래려는 마음에 대꾸했어."]], [["‘No, no!’ said the other, clasping him by the hand.", "\"아니, 아니에요!\" 아이가 그의 손을 꼭 붙잡으며 말했어."], ["‘Should I--should I--tell me that again.", "\"제가, 제가요, 그 말 다시 해 줘요."], ["Say I should be sure to find you.’", "형을 꼭 찾을 수 있다고 말해 줘요.\""]], [["‘You would,’ replied Nicholas, with the same humane intention, ‘and I would help and aid you, and not bring fresh sorrow on you as I have done here.’", "\"찾을 수 있어.\" 니콜라스가 똑같이 따뜻한 마음으로 대꾸했어. \"그러면 내가 너를 돕고 거들어 줄게. 여기서처럼 너한테 새 슬픔을 안기는 게 아니라 말이야.\""]], [["The boy caught both the young man’s hands passionately in his, and, hugging them to his breast, uttered a few broken sounds which were unintelligible.", "아이는 그 젊은이의 두 손을 격하게 제 손에 그러쥐었어. 그러고는 그 손을 가슴에 꼭 끌어안고, 알아들을 수 없는 끊어진 소리를 몇 마디 냈지."], ["Squeers entered at the moment, and he shrunk back into his old corner.", "바로 그때 스퀴어스가 들어왔고, 아이는 제 늘 있던 구석으로 움츠러들었어."]], [["# CHAPTER 13", "# CHAPTER 13"]], [["Nicholas varies the Monotony of Dothebys Hall by a most vigorous and remarkable proceeding, which leads to Consequences of some Importance", "니콜라스가 매우 과감하고 놀라운 행동으로 도더보이즈 홀의 단조로움을 깨뜨리니, 이는 적잖이 중요한 결과로 이어진다"]], [["The cold, feeble dawn of a January morning was stealing in at the windows of the common sleeping-room, when Nicholas, raising himself on his arm, looked among the prostrate forms which on every side surrounded him, as though in search of some particular object. It needed a quick eye to detect, from among the huddled mass of sleepers, the form of any given individual. As they lay closely packed together, covered, for warmth’s sake, with their patched and ragged clothes, little could be distinguished but the sharp outlines of pale faces, over which the sombre light shed the same dull heavy colour; with, here and there, a gaunt arm thrust forth: its thinness hidden by no covering, but fully exposed to view, in all its shrunken ugliness. There were some who, lying on their backs with upturned faces and clenched hands, just visible in the leaden light, bore more the aspect of dead bodies than of living creatures; and there were others coiled up into strange and fantastic postures, such as might have been taken for the uneasy efforts of pain to gain some temporary relief, rather than the freaks of slumber. A few--and these were among the youngest of the children--slept peacefully on, with smiles upon their faces, dreaming perhaps of home; but ever and again a deep and heavy sigh, breaking the stillness of the room, announced that some new sleeper had awakened to the misery of another day; and, as morning took the place of night, the smiles gradually faded away, with the friendly darkness which had given them birth.", "1월 아침의 차갑고 희미한 새벽빛이 공동 침실 창문으로 스며들 무렵, 니콜라스는 팔로 몸을 일으켜 사방으로 자신을 둘러싸고 엎드린 채 잠든 형체들 사이를 둘러보았다. 마치 어떤 특정한 사람을 찾는 듯한 모습이었다. 빽빽이 뒤엉킨 잠든 무리 속에서 누군가 한 사람의 모습을 가려내려면 날카로운 눈이 필요했다. 아이들은 추위를 견디려고 누덕누덕 기운 넝마 옷으로 몸을 덮고 다닥다닥 붙어 누워 있었다. 그래서 보이는 거라곤 창백한 얼굴들의 날카로운 윤곽뿐이었어. 그 위로 음울한 빛이 한결같이 흐릿하고 무거운 색을 드리웠지. 군데군데 앙상한 팔이 삐죽 나와 있었어. 그 마른 팔은 아무것도 덮지 못한 채, 쪼그라들 대로 쪼그라든 흉한 모습을 고스란히 드러내고 있었지. 어떤 아이들은 등을 대고 누워 얼굴을 위로 향한 채 두 손을 꽉 움켜쥐고 있었어. 납빛 어스름 속에 겨우 보이는 그 모습은 살아 있는 사람이라기보다 시신에 가까웠지. 또 어떤 아이들은 이상하고 기괴한 자세로 몸을 웅크리고 있었어. 잠결의 뒤척임이라기보다, 고통이 잠시라도 덜해지길 바라며 안간힘을 쓰는 몸짓 같았지. 몇몇은 — 그 가운데 가장 어린 축에 드는 아이들이었어 — 얼굴에 미소를 띤 채 평화롭게 잠들어 있었어. 아마 집 꿈을 꾸고 있었겠지. 그런데 이따금 깊고 무거운 한숨이 방 안의 고요를 깨뜨렸어. 또 한 아이가 잠에서 깨어, 새로운 하루의 괴로움과 마주하게 됐다는 소리였지. 그리고 밤이 아침으로 바뀌자, 그 미소도 차츰 사라져 갔어. 미소를 빚어낸 그 다정한 어둠과 함께 말이야."]], [["Dreams are the bright creatures of poem and legend, who sport on earth in the night season, and melt away in the first beam of the sun, which lights grim care and stern reality on their daily pilgrimage through the world.", "꿈은 시와 전설 속에 사는 환한 존재야. 밤이면 땅 위에서 노닐다가, 첫 햇살이 비치면 스르르 녹아 사라지지. 그 햇살은 무서운 근심과 모진 현실에 빛을 비추며, 세상을 헤쳐 나가는 날마다의 고된 여정을 밝히는 거야."]], [["Nicholas looked upon the sleepers; at first, with the air of one who gazes upon a scene which, though familiar to him, has lost none of its sorrowful effect in consequence; and, afterwards, with a more intense and searching scrutiny, as a man would who missed something his eye was accustomed to meet, and had expected to rest upon. He was still occupied in this search, and had half risen from his bed in the eagerness of his quest, when the voice of Squeers was heard, calling from the bottom of the stairs.", "니콜라스는 잠든 아이들을 바라보았어. 처음엔 익숙한 광경을 보는 사람의 눈빛이었지. 익숙해도 그 슬픈 기운만큼은 조금도 바래지 않은 그런 광경 말이야. 그러다 점점 더 골똘하고 살피는 눈으로 바뀌었어. 늘 눈에 들어오던 무언가가, 으레 거기 있으리라 여겼던 무언가가 보이지 않을 때 사람이 짓는 그런 눈빛이었지. 그는 여전히 누군가를 찾고 있었어. 어찌나 간절했던지 침대에서 반쯤 몸을 일으킨 참이었지. 그때 계단 아래에서 부르는 스퀴어스의 목소리가 들려왔어."]], [["‘Now then,’ cried that gentleman, ‘are you going to sleep all day, up there--’", "\"자, 이놈들아.\" 그 양반이 소리쳤어. \"거기 위에서 하루 종일 잠이나 잘 셈이냐——\""]], [["‘You lazy hounds?’ added Mrs.", "\"이 게으른 강아지들 같으니!\""], ["Squeers, finishing the sentence, and producing, at the same time, a sharp sound, like that which is occasioned by the lacing of stays.", "스퀴어스 부인이 말끝을 마저 채워 넣었어. 그러면서 코르셋 끈을 조일 때 나는 듯한 날카로운 소리를 함께 냈지."]], [["‘We shall be down directly, sir,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"곧 내려가겠습니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Down directly!’ said Squeers.", "\"곧 내려와!\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘Ah!", "\"흥!"], ["you had better be down directly, or I’ll be down upon some of you in less.", "당장 내려오는 게 좋을 거다. 안 그러면 내가 더 빨리 올라가서 몇 놈 손봐 줄 테니까."], ["Where’s that Smike?’", "그 스마이크 녀석은 어디 있어?\""]], [["Nicholas looked hurriedly round again, but made no answer.", "니콜라스는 다시 황급히 둘러보았지만, 아무 대답도 하지 않았어."]], [["‘Smike!’ shouted Squeers.", "\"스마이크!\" 스퀴어스가 고함쳤어."]], [["‘Do you want your head broke in a fresh place, Smike?’ demanded his amiable lady in the same key.", "\"머리통이 또 어디 새로 깨지고 싶은 게냐, 스마이크?\" 그 인자한 부인도 같은 가락으로 다그쳤어."]], [["Still there was no reply, and still Nicholas stared about him, as did the greater part of the boys, who were by this time roused.", "여전히 아무 대답도 없었어. 니콜라스는 계속 두리번거렸지. 이쯤 잠에서 깬 아이들 대부분도 마찬가지였어."]], [["‘Confound his impudence!’ muttered Squeers, rapping the stair-rail impatiently with his cane.", "\"건방진 놈 같으니!\" 스퀴어스가 지팡이로 계단 난간을 신경질적으로 탁탁 치며 중얼거렸어."], ["‘Nickleby!’", "\"니클비!\""]], [["‘Well, sir.’", "\"네, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Send that obstinate scoundrel down; don’t you hear me calling?’", "\"그 고집 센 망나니 놈 좀 내려보내. 내가 부르는 소리 안 들려?\""]], [["‘He is not here, sir,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"여기 없습니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Don’t tell me a lie,’ retorted the schoolmaster.", "\"거짓말 마라.\" 교장이 쏘아붙였어."], ["‘He is.’", "\"있잖아.\""]], [["‘He is not,’ retorted Nicholas angrily, ‘don’t tell me one.’", "\"없습니다.\" 니콜라스도 화가 나서 받아쳤어. \"저한테 거짓말하지 마세요.\""]], [["‘We shall soon see that,’ said Mr.", "\"곧 알게 되겠지.\""], ["Squeers, rushing upstairs.", "스퀴어스가 계단을 우당탕 올라오며 말했어."], ["‘I’ll find him, I warrant you.’", "\"내가 꼭 찾아내고 말 테다, 두고 봐라.\""]], [["With which assurance, Mr.", "이렇게 큰소리치고는 스퀴어스가 침실로 쿵 뛰어들었어."], ["Squeers bounced into the dormitory, and, swinging his cane in the air ready for a blow, darted into the corner where the lean body of the drudge was usually stretched at night.", "한 대 칠 기세로 지팡이를 허공에 휘두르며, 그 비쩍 마른 머슴애가 밤마다 몸을 뉘던 구석으로 쏜살같이 달려갔지."], ["The cane descended harmlessly upon the ground.", "지팡이는 애꿎은 바닥만 후려쳤어."], ["There was nobody there.", "거기엔 아무도 없었거든."]], [["‘What does this mean?’ said Squeers, turning round with a very pale face.", "\"이게 무슨 일이야?\" 스퀴어스가 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 홱 돌아서며 말했어."], ["‘Where have you hid him?’", "\"그놈을 어디 숨겼어?\""]], [["‘I have seen nothing of him since last night,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"어젯밤 이후로 한 번도 못 봤습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Come,’ said Squeers, evidently frightened, though he endeavoured to look otherwise, ‘you won’t save him this way. Where is he?’", "\"이봐.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. 아닌 척하려고 애썼지만 겁먹은 게 뻔히 보였지. \"이런다고 그놈을 못 구해. 어디 있어?\""]], [["‘At the bottom of the nearest pond for aught I know,’ rejoined Nicholas in a low voice, and fixing his eyes full on the master’s face.", "\"제가 알기로는 가장 가까운 연못 밑바닥에 있을지도 모르죠.\" 니콜라스가 낮은 목소리로 받아쳤어. 그러면서 교장의 얼굴을 똑바로 노려보았지."]], [["‘Damn you, what do you mean by that?’ retorted Squeers in great perturbation.", "\"빌어먹을, 그게 무슨 소리야?\" 스퀴어스가 크게 당황해서 쏘아붙였어."], ["Without waiting for a reply, he inquired of the boys whether any one among them knew anything of their missing schoolmate.", "그러고는 대답도 기다리지 않고, 아이들에게 사라진 친구에 대해 아는 게 있느냐고 물었어."]], [["There was a general hum of anxious denial, in the midst of which, one shrill voice was heard to say (as, indeed, everybody thought):", "여기저기서 불안하게 모른다고 웅성거리는 소리가 났어. 그 와중에 한 째지는 목소리가 (사실 다들 그렇게 생각하던 말이었지) 들렸어."]], [["‘Please, sir, I think Smike’s run away, sir.’", "\"저, 선생님, 스마이크가 도망친 것 같아요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Ha!’ cried Squeers, turning sharp round.", "\"하!\" 스퀴어스가 홱 돌아서며 소리쳤어."], ["‘Who said that?’", "\"누가 그런 말을 했어?\""]], [["‘Tomkins, please sir,’ rejoined a chorus of voices.", "\"톰킨스예요, 선생님.\" 여러 목소리가 한꺼번에 대답했어."], ["Mr.", "스퀴어스는 무리 속으로 풍덩 뛰어들었어."], ["Squeers made a plunge into the crowd, and at one dive, caught a very little boy, habited still in his night-gear, and the perplexed expression of whose countenance, as he was brought forward, seemed to intimate that he was as yet uncertain whether he was about to be punished or rewarded for the suggestion.", "그러고는 한 번에 잠옷 차림의 아주 조그만 아이를 낚아챘지. 그 아이는 앞으로 끌려 나오며 얼떨떨한 표정을 짓고 있었어."], ["He was not long in doubt.", "자기가 그 말을 한 대가로 벌을 받을지 상을 받을지 아직 모르겠다는 얼굴이었지. 의문은 오래가지 않았어."]], [["‘You think he has run away, do you, sir?’ demanded Squeers.", "\"그래, 그놈이 도망쳤다고 생각하는 거지, 응?\" 스퀴어스가 다그쳤어."]], [["‘Yes, please sir,’ replied the little boy.", "\"네, 선생님.\" 그 조그만 아이가 대답했어."]], [["‘And what, sir,’ said Squeers, catching the little boy suddenly by the arms and whisking up his drapery in a most dexterous manner, ‘what reason have you to suppose that any boy would want to run away from this establishment?", "\"그래서, 이놈아.\" 스퀴어스가 별안간 아이의 팔을 붙잡고 아주 능숙한 솜씨로 옷자락을 걷어 올리며 말했어."], ["Eh, sir?’", "\"이 학교에서 도망치고 싶어 할 놈이 있다고 생각할 무슨 근거가 있냐? 응, 이놈아?\""]], [["The child raised a dismal cry, by way of answer, and Mr.", "아이는 대답 대신 처량한 비명을 질렀어."], ["Squeers, throwing himself into the most favourable attitude for exercising his strength, beat him until the little urchin in his writhings actually rolled out of his hands, when he mercifully allowed him to roll away, as he best could.", "스퀴어스는 힘을 쓰기에 가장 좋은 자세를 잡고는, 그 꼬마 녀석이 몸부림치다 못해 손아귀에서 데굴데굴 빠져나갈 때까지 두들겨 팼어. 그러고는 인심 좋게도, 아이가 알아서 굴러가도록 내버려 두었지."]], [["‘There,’ said Squeers.", "\"자.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘Now if any other boy thinks Smike has run away, I shall be glad to have a talk with him.’", "\"이제 또 누가 스마이크가 도망쳤다고 생각하면, 기꺼이 나랑 한번 얘기해 보자.\""]], [["There was, of course, a profound silence, during which Nicholas showed his disgust as plainly as looks could show it.", "물론 깊은 침묵만 흘렀어. 그동안 니콜라스는 눈빛으로 드러낼 수 있는 한 분명하게 역겨움을 드러냈지."]], [["‘Well, Nickleby,’ said Squeers, eyeing him maliciously.", "\"그래, 니클비.\" 스퀴어스가 그를 심술궂게 흘겨보며 말했어."], ["‘YOU think he has run away, I suppose?’", "\"너는 그놈이 도망쳤다고 생각하겠지, 안 그래?\""]], [["‘I think it extremely likely,’ replied Nicholas, in a quiet manner.", "\"그럴 가능성이 아주 높다고 봅니다.\" 니콜라스가 차분하게 대답했어."]], [["‘Oh, you do, do you?’ sneered Squeers.", "\"오, 그래? 그렇게 생각해?\""], ["‘Maybe you know he has?’", "스퀴어스가 비웃었어. \"어쩌면 그놈이 도망친 걸 알고 있는지도 모르겠군?\""]], [["‘I know nothing of the kind.’", "\"그런 건 전혀 모릅니다.\""]], [["‘He didn’t tell you he was going, I suppose, did he?’ sneered Squeers.", "\"그놈이 간다는 말을 너한테 하진 않았겠지, 응?\" 스퀴어스가 비아냥거렸어."]], [["‘He did not,’ replied Nicholas; ‘I am very glad he did not, for it would then have been my duty to have warned you in time.’", "\"안 했습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"하지 않아서 정말 다행이라고 생각합니다. 했다면 미리 선생님께 알리는 게 제 도리였을 테니까요.\""]], [["‘Which no doubt you would have been devilish sorry to do,’ said Squeers in a taunting fashion.", "\"그런 도리라면 정말이지 끔찍하게 하기 싫었겠지.\" 스퀴어스가 빈정대듯 말했어."]], [["‘I should indeed,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"정말 그랬을 겁니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘You interpret my feelings with great accuracy.’", "\"제 마음을 아주 정확히 짚으셨네요.\""]], [["Mrs.", "스퀴어스 부인은 계단 아래에서 이 대화를 듣고 있었어."], ["Squeers had listened to this conversation, from the bottom of the stairs; but, now losing all patience, she hastily assumed her night-jacket, and made her way to the scene of action.", "그런데 이제 더는 못 참겠던지, 부랴부랴 잠옷 윗도리를 걸치고는 사건 현장으로 올라왔지."]], [["‘What’s all this here to-do?’ said the lady, as the boys fell off right and left, to save her the trouble of clearing a passage with her brawny arms. ‘What on earth are you a talking to him for, Squeery!’", "\"여기서 무슨 소란이야?\" 부인이 말했어. 아이들이 좌우로 흩어지며 길을 터 주었어. 그 우람한 팔로 직접 길을 헤칠 수고를 덜어 준 거지. \"도대체 뭐 하러 저런 놈하고 입씨름을 하고 있어요, 스퀴어리!\""]], [["‘Why, my dear,’ said Squeers, ‘the fact is, that Smike is not to be found.’", "\"그게 말이야, 여보.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"실은 스마이크가 보이질 않아.\""]], [["‘Well, I know that,’ said the lady, ‘and where’s the wonder? If you get a parcel of proud-stomached teachers that set the young dogs a rebelling, what else can you look for? Now, young man, you just have the kindness to take yourself off to the schoolroom, and take the boys off with you, and don’t you stir out of there till you have leave given you, or you and I may fall out in a way that’ll spoil your beauty, handsome as you think yourself, and so I tell you.’", "\"그건 나도 알아요.\" 부인이 말했어. \"그게 뭐 놀랄 일이에요? 자존심만 잔뜩 부풀어서 어린 강아지들을 부추겨 반항하게 만드는 선생을 두면, 그것 말고 뭘 바라겠어요? 자, 젊은 양반. 친절을 베풀어 교실로 썩 꺼져 주시지. 그리고 애들도 데리고 가요. 허락받기 전에는 거기서 한 발짝도 나오지 말고. 안 그러면 당신하고 나하고 한판 붙을 텐데, 잘난 척하는 그 얼굴이 아주 망가질걸. 내 분명히 말해 두는 거예요.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ said Nicholas.", "\"그래요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Yes; and indeed and indeed again, Mister Jackanapes,’ said the excited lady; ‘and I wouldn’t keep such as you in the house another hour, if I had my way.’", "\"그래, 그렇고말고, 거듭 그렇다니까, 이 건방진 애송이 양반아.\" 흥분한 부인이 말했어. \"내 마음대로 할 수 있다면 너 같은 놈은 이 집에 단 한 시간도 더 안 둘 거다.\""]], [["‘Nor would you if I had mine,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"제 마음대로 할 수 있다면 저도 마찬가지일 겁니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘Now, boys!’", "\"자, 얘들아!\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"흥!"], ["Now, boys,’ said Mrs.", "'자, 얘들아.'\""], ["Squeers, mimicking, as nearly as she could, the voice and manner of the usher.", "스퀴어스 부인이 그 보조 교사의 목소리와 말투를 최대한 흉내 내며 말했어."], ["‘Follow your leader, boys, and take pattern by Smike if you dare.", "\"우두머리를 따라가렴, 얘들아. 그리고 배짱 있으면 스마이크를 본받아 봐."], ["See what he’ll get for himself, when he is brought back; and, mind!", "그놈이 잡혀 오면 어떤 꼴을 당하는지 두고 봐. 그리고 명심해라!"], ["I tell you that you shall have as bad, and twice as bad, if you so much as open your mouths about him.’", "그놈 얘기를 입에 올리기만 해도 너희도 똑같이, 아니 두 배로 호되게 당할 줄 알아.\""]], [["‘If I catch him,’ said Squeers, ‘I’ll only stop short of flaying him alive.", "\"내가 그놈을 잡으면,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["I give you notice, boys.’", "\"산 채로 가죽을 벗기기 직전까지만 봐줄 거다. 분명히 일러두는 거다, 얘들아.\""]], [["‘IF you catch him,’ retorted Mrs.", "\"잡으면이라고요?\" 스퀴어스 부인이 경멸하듯 받아쳤어."], ["Squeers, contemptuously; ‘you are sure to; you can’t help it, if you go the right way to work.", "\"틀림없이 잡아요. 제대로만 나서면 안 잡힐 수가 없죠."], ["Come!", "자!"], ["Away with you!’", "다들 꺼져!\""]], [["With these words, Mrs.", "이 말과 함께 스퀴어스 부인은 아이들을 내보냈어."], ["Squeers dismissed the boys, and after a little light skirmishing with those in the rear who were pressing forward to get out of the way, but were detained for a few moments by the throng in front, succeeded in clearing the room, when she confronted her spouse alone.", "뒤쪽에서 빠져나가려고 앞으로 밀려드는데도 앞쪽 무리에 막혀 잠시 멈춰 있던 녀석들과 가볍게 한바탕 실랑이를 벌인 끝에, 부인은 마침내 방을 다 비워 냈지. 그러고는 남편과 단둘이 마주 섰어."]], [["‘He is off,’ said Mrs. Squeers.", "\"그놈은 멀리 갔어요.\""], ["‘The cow-house and stable are locked up, so he can’t be there; and he’s not downstairs anywhere, for the girl has looked.", "스퀴어스 부인이 말했어. \"외양간이랑 마구간은 잠겨 있으니 거기 있을 리 없고, 아래층 어디에도 없어요."], ["He must have gone York way, and by a public road too.’", "하녀가 다 살펴봤거든. 틀림없이 요크 쪽으로, 그것도 큰길로 갔을 거예요.\""]], [["‘Why must he?’ inquired Squeers.", "\"왜 그렇다는 거야?\" 스퀴어스가 물었어."]], [["‘Stupid!’ said Mrs.", "\"멍청하긴!\""], ["Squeers angrily.", "스퀴어스 부인이 화를 내며 말했어."], ["‘He hadn’t any money, had he?’", "\"그놈한테 돈이 한 푼이라도 있었어요?\""]], [["‘Never had a penny of his own in his whole life, that I know of,’ replied Squeers.", "\"내가 알기로는 평생 제 돈이라곤 한 푼도 가져 본 적이 없지.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."]], [["‘To be sure,’ rejoined Mrs.", "\"그렇고말고요.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 받았어."], ["Squeers, ‘and he didn’t take anything to eat with him; that I’ll answer for.", "\"게다가 먹을 것도 안 챙겨 갔어요. 그건 내가 장담해요."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["ha!’", "하!\""]], [["‘Ha!", "\"하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["ha!’ laughed Squeers.", "하!\" 스퀴어스도 웃었어."]], [["‘Then, of course,’ said Mrs.", "\"그러니 당연히,\""], ["S., ‘he must beg his way, and he could do that, nowhere, but on the public road.’", "스퀴어스 부인이 말했어. \"구걸하며 가야 하는데, 그건 큰길 말고는 어디서도 못 하죠.\""]], [["‘That’s true,’ exclaimed Squeers, clapping his hands.", "\"그건 맞아!\" 스퀴어스가 손뼉을 치며 외쳤어."]], [["‘True!", "\"맞고말고요! 그래도 내가 말 안 했으면 당신은 절대 거기까지 생각 못 했을걸요.\""], ["Yes; but you would never have thought of it, for all that, if I hadn’t said so,’ replied his wife.", "아내가 대꾸했어. \"자, 당신이 마차를 타고 한쪽 길로 가요."], ["‘Now, if you take the chaise and go one road, and I borrow Swallow’s chaise, and go the other, what with keeping our eyes open, and asking questions, one or other of us is pretty certain to lay hold of him.’", "나는 스왈로네 마차를 빌려 다른 길로 갈 테니. 둘 다 눈 똑바로 뜨고, 이것저것 물어 가며 다니면, 우리 둘 중 하나는 틀림없이 그놈을 붙잡을 거예요.\""]], [["The worthy lady’s plan was adopted and put in execution without a moment’s delay.", "이 훌륭한 부인의 계획은 곧장 받아들여졌고, 한순간도 지체 없이 실행에 옮겨졌어. 아주 서둘러 아침을 먹고, 마을에서 이것저것 수소문을 한 결과, 제대로 추적하고 있는 모양이라는 결론이 났지."], ["After a very hasty breakfast, and the prosecution of some inquiries in the village, the result of which seemed to show that he was on the right track, Squeers started forth in the pony-chaise, intent upon discovery and vengeance.", "스퀴어스는 발견과 복수를 벼르며 조랑말 마차를 타고 나섰어. 잠시 뒤, 스퀴어스 부인도 흰 외투를 걸치고 갖가지 숄과 손수건을 둘둘 두른 채 다른 마차를 타고 다른 방향으로 출발했어."], ["Shortly afterwards, Mrs.", "그러면서 큼지막한 몽둥이 하나, 튼튼한 밧줄 토막 몇 개, 그리고 건장한 일꾼 하나를 데려갔지."], ["Squeers, arrayed in the white top-coat, and tied up in various shawls and handkerchiefs, issued forth in another chaise and another direction, taking with her a good-sized bludgeon, several odd pieces of strong cord, and a stout labouring man: all provided and carried upon the expedition, with the sole object of assisting in the capture, and (once caught) insuring the safe custody of the unfortunate Smike.", "이 모든 건 단 하나의 목적을 위해 마련하고 실어 간 거였어. 가엾은 스마이크를 붙잡는 걸 돕고, 일단 잡으면 안전하게 가둬 두려는 것 말이야."]], [["Nicholas remained behind, in a tumult of feeling, sensible that whatever might be the upshot of the boy’s flight, nothing but painful and deplorable consequences were likely to ensue from it. Death, from want and exposure to the weather, was the best that could be expected from the protracted wandering of so poor and helpless a creature, alone and unfriended, through a country of which he was wholly ignorant. There was little, perhaps, to choose between this fate and a return to the tender mercies of the Yorkshire school; but the unhappy being had established a hold upon his sympathy and compassion, which made his heart ache at the prospect of the suffering he was destined to undergo. He lingered on, in restless anxiety, picturing a thousand possibilities, until the evening of next day, when Squeers returned, alone, and unsuccessful.", "니콜라스는 뒤에 남아 마음이 어지러웠어. 그 아이의 도주가 어떻게 끝나든, 거기서 나올 거라곤 가슴 아프고 통탄스러운 결말뿐이라는 걸 알았거든. 그렇게 가난하고 의지할 데 없는 아이가, 홀로 친구 하나 없이, 전혀 모르는 고장을 오래도록 헤매다가 기껏 맞을 수 있는 최선이라곤, 굶주림과 추위에 시달리다 죽는 것이었지. 어쩌면 그 운명과 요크셔 학교의 그 다정한 자비 속으로 돌아오는 것 사이에 별 차이가 없었을지도 몰라. 하지만 그 가엾은 아이는 니콜라스의 연민과 동정을 깊이 사로잡고 있었어. 그래서 그 아이가 겪게 될 고통을 떠올리니 가슴이 미어졌지. 니콜라스는 안절부절못한 채 머물러 있었어. 온갖 가능성을 천 가지나 그려 보면서 말이야. 그러다 이튿날 저녁, 스퀴어스가 혼자, 빈손으로 돌아왔어."]], [["‘No news of the scamp!’ said the schoolmaster, who had evidently been stretching his legs, on the old principle, not a few times during the journey.", "\"그 망나니 녀석 소식이 없어!\" 교장이 말했어."], ["‘I’ll have consolation for this out of somebody, Nickleby, if Mrs.", "그는 가는 동안 옛 방식대로 다리를 적잖이 풀고 온 게 분명했지."], ["Squeers don’t hunt him down; so I give you warning.’", "\"스퀴어스 부인이 그놈을 못 잡아 오면, 누구한테서든 이 분풀이를 받아 내고 말 거다, 니클비. 그러니 미리 일러두는 거다.\""]], [["‘It is not in my power to console you, sir,’ said Nicholas.", "\"제가 위로해 드릴 수 있는 일이 아닙니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘It is nothing to me.’", "\"저랑은 아무 상관 없는 일이니까요.\""]], [["‘Isn’t it?’ said Squeers in a threatening manner.", "\"그래?\" 스퀴어스가 위협하듯 말했어."], ["‘We shall see!’", "\"두고 보자!\""]], [["‘We shall,’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"두고 보죠.\" 니콜라스가 받아쳤어."]], [["‘Here’s the pony run right off his legs, and me obliged to come home with a hack cob, that’ll cost fifteen shillings besides other expenses,’ said Squeers; ‘who’s to pay for that, do you hear?’", "\"조랑말은 다리가 다 풀려 못 쓰게 됐고, 나는 삯말을 빌려 집에 와야 했어. 그게 다른 비용 빼고도 십오 실링이나 든단 말이야.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"그건 누가 물어 줄 거냐, 응?\""]], [["Nicholas shrugged his shoulders and remained silent.", "니콜라스는 어깨를 으쓱하고는 잠자코 있었어."]], [["‘I’ll have it out of somebody, I tell you,’ said Squeers, his usual harsh crafty manner changed to open bullying ‘None of your whining vapourings here, Mr.", "\"누구한테서든 받아 내고 말 거다, 분명히 말하지만.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["Puppy, but be off to your kennel, for it’s past your bedtime!", "늘 매섭고 교활하던 태도가 대놓고 으름장을 놓는 투로 바뀌어 있었지. \"여기서 징징대며 허세 부릴 생각 마라, 강아지 양반."], ["Come!", "네 개집으로 썩 꺼져. 잘 시간 지났으니까!"], ["Get out!’", "자! 나가!\""]], [["Nicholas bit his lip and knit his hands involuntarily, for his fingerends tingled to avenge the insult; but remembering that the man was drunk, and that it could come to little but a noisy brawl, he contented himself with darting a contemptuous look at the tyrant, and walked, as majestically as he could, upstairs: not a little nettled, however, to observe that Miss Squeers and Master Squeers, and the servant girl, were enjoying the scene from a snug corner; the two former indulging in many edifying remarks about the presumption of poor upstarts, which occasioned a vast deal of laughter, in which even the most miserable of all miserable servant girls joined: while Nicholas, stung to the quick, drew over his head such bedclothes as he had, and sternly resolved that the outstanding account between himself and Mr. Squeers should be settled rather more speedily than the latter anticipated.", "니콜라스는 입술을 깨물고 저도 모르게 두 손을 움켜쥐었어. 손끝이 그 모욕을 갚아 주고 싶어 근질거렸거든. 하지만 그 사람이 취해 있고, 결국 시끄러운 몸싸움밖에 안 될 거라는 걸 떠올리고는, 그 폭군에게 경멸의 눈길 한 번 던지는 걸로 만족했지. 그러고는 최대한 의젓하게 계단을 올라갔어. 그래도 적잖이 부아가 치밀었지. 스퀴어스 양과 스퀴어스 도련님, 그리고 하녀가 아늑한 구석에서 그 광경을 구경하며 즐기고 있었거든. 앞의 두 사람은 가난뱅이 벼락출세꾼의 주제넘음에 대해 점잖은 척 한마디씩 늘어놓았어. 그 말에 한바탕 웃음이 터졌고, 세상에서 가장 비참한 하녀까지도 거기 끼어 웃었지. 한편 니콜라스는 속이 잔뜩 상한 채, 가진 이불을 머리 위까지 끌어 덮었어. 그러고는 자기와 스퀴어스 사이의 묵은 셈을, 그자가 예상하는 것보다 훨씬 빨리 치러야겠다고 단단히 마음먹었어."]], [["Another day came, and Nicholas was scarcely awake when he heard the wheels of a chaise approaching the house. It stopped. The voice of Mrs Squeers was heard, and in exultation, ordering a glass of spirits for somebody, which was in itself a sufficient sign that something extraordinary had happened. Nicholas hardly dared to look out of the window; but he did so, and the very first object that met his eyes was the wretched Smike: so bedabbled with mud and rain, so haggard and worn, and wild, that, but for his garments being such as no scarecrow was ever seen to wear, he might have been doubtful, even then, of his identity.", "또 하루가 밝았어. 니콜라스가 채 잠에서 깨기도 전에, 마차 바퀴 소리가 집 쪽으로 다가오는 게 들렸지. 마차가 멈췄어. 스퀴어스 부인의 목소리가 들렸어. 의기양양하게 누군가에게 줄 독한 술 한 잔을 시키는 소리였지. 그것만으로도 뭔가 굉장한 일이 벌어졌다는 충분한 신호였어. 니콜라스는 차마 창밖을 내다볼 엄두가 안 났어. 그래도 내다봤지. 그러자 맨 먼저 눈에 들어온 게 그 가엾은 스마이크였어. 진흙과 빗물에 흠뻑 젖고, 어찌나 초췌하고 지치고 사나운 몰골이었던지, 어떤 허수아비도 입어 본 적 없을 그런 옷차림만 아니었다면, 그 순간조차 정말 그 아이가 맞는지 긴가민가했을 거야."]], [["‘Lift him out,’ said Squeers, after he had literally feasted his eyes, in silence, upon the culprit.", "\"끌어 내려.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘Bring him in; bring him in!’", "그는 잠자코 그 죄인을 실컷 눈요기한 뒤였지. \"안으로 데려와, 안으로 데려와!\""]], [["‘Take care,’ cried Mrs.", "\"조심해요.\""], ["Squeers, as her husband proffered his assistance.", "남편이 거들려고 하자 스퀴어스 부인이 외쳤어."], ["‘We tied his legs under the apron and made’em fast to the chaise, to prevent his giving us the slip again.’", "\"또 미끄러져 도망 못 가게, 다리를 앞치마 밑에 묶고 마차에 단단히 매어 놨으니까.\""]], [["With hands trembling with delight, Squeers unloosened the cord; and Smike, to all appearance more dead than alive, was brought into the house and securely locked up in a cellar, until such time as Mr.", "스퀴어스는 기쁨에 부들부들 떨리는 손으로 밧줄을 풀었어. 그리고 산 사람이라기보다 죽은 사람에 가까운 스마이크는 집 안으로 끌려 들어와, 지하실에 단단히 갇혔지."], ["Squeers should deem it expedient to operate upon him, in presence of the assembled school.", "스퀴어스가 학교 전체를 모아 놓고 그를 손봐 줄 적당한 때라고 여길 때까지 말이야."]], [["Upon a hasty consideration of the circumstances, it may be matter of surprise to some persons, that Mr.", "상황을 얼른 따져 보면, 어떤 사람들은 의아할지도 몰라. 스퀴어스 부부가 그렇게 큰 소리로 불평하던 골칫덩이를 도로 붙잡으려고 어쩌면 그토록 애를 썼을까 하고 말이야."], ["and Mrs.", "하지만 이 말을 들으면 그 의아함은 사라질 거야. 그 머슴애가 해 온 온갖 일을 만약 다른 사람이 했다면, 학교는 품삯으로 일주일에 십 실링이나 십이 실링쯤 들었을 테니까."], ["Squeers should have taken so much trouble to repossess themselves of an incumbrance of which it was their wont to complain so loudly; but their surprise will cease when they are informed that the manifold services of the drudge, if performed by anybody else, would have cost the establishment some ten or twelve shillings per week in the shape of wages; and furthermore, that all runaways were, as a matter of policy, made severe examples of, at Dotheboys Hall, inasmuch as, in consequence of the limited extent of its attractions, there was but little inducement, beyond the powerful impulse of fear, for any pupil, provided with the usual number of legs and the power of using them, to remain.", "게다가 두더보이즈 홀에서는, 도망친 녀석은 누구든 본보기로 호되게 다루는 게 방침이었어. 학교의 매력이라는 게 워낙 보잘것없다 보니, 다리가 멀쩡히 달려 있고 그걸 쓸 줄 아는 학생이라면 거기 남아 있을 이유라곤, 두려움이라는 강력한 충동 말고는 별로 없었거든."]], [["The news that Smike had been caught and brought back in triumph, ran like wild-fire through the hungry community, and expectation was on tiptoe all the morning. On tiptoe it was destined to remain, however, until afternoon; when Squeers, having refreshed himself with his dinner, and further strengthened himself by an extra libation or so, made his appearance (accompanied by his amiable partner) with a countenance of portentous import, and a fearful instrument of flagellation, strong, supple, wax-ended, and new,--in short, purchased that morning, expressly for the occasion.", "스마이크가 붙잡혀 의기양양하게 끌려왔다는 소식은 굶주린 무리 사이에 들불처럼 번졌어. 그래서 오전 내내 모두가 잔뜩 기대하며 마음을 졸였지. 하지만 그 기대는 오후까지 졸인 채로 있어야 했어. 스퀴어스가 점심으로 기운을 채우고, 술까지 한두 잔 더 걸쳐 한층 든든해진 뒤에야, 불길한 기색이 가득한 얼굴로 (그 인자한 짝과 함께) 나타났거든. 그러면서 무시무시한 매질 도구도 하나 들고 있었어. 튼튼하고 낭창낭창하고 끝에 밀랍을 먹인 새것이었지. 한마디로 그날 아침, 바로 이 일을 위해 사 온 거였어."]], [["‘Is every boy here?’ asked Squeers, in a tremendous voice.", "\"빠진 놈 없이 다 모였나?\" 스퀴어스가 우렁찬 목소리로 물었어."]], [["Every boy was there, but every boy was afraid to speak, so Squeers glared along the lines to assure himself; and every eye drooped, and every head cowered down, as he did so.", "다들 모여 있었지만 누구도 입을 떼지 못했어. 그래서 스퀴어스가 확인하려고 줄지어 선 아이들을 노려보았지. 그가 그러자 모든 눈이 내리깔리고, 모든 고개가 움츠러들었어."]], [["‘Each boy keep his place,’ said Squeers, administering his favourite blow to the desk, and regarding with gloomy satisfaction the universal start which it never failed to occasion.", "\"각자 제자리에 있어.\" 스퀴어스가 그 좋아하는 솜씨로 책상을 한 대 후려치며 말했어."], ["‘Nickleby!", "그러고는 그 소리에 어김없이 모두가 움찔하는 모습을 음울한 만족감으로 바라보았지."], ["to your desk, sir.’", "\"니클비! 자네 책상으로 가게.\""]], [["It was remarked by more than one small observer, that there was a very curious and unusual expression in the usher’s face; but he took his seat, without opening his lips in reply. Squeers, casting a triumphant glance at his assistant and a look of most comprehensive despotism on the boys, left the room, and shortly afterwards returned, dragging Smike by the collar--or rather by that fragment of his jacket which was nearest the place where his collar would have been, had he boasted such a decoration.", "그 보조 교사의 얼굴에 아주 묘하고 심상치 않은 표정이 떠올라 있다는 걸, 어린 구경꾼 여럿이 알아챘어. 하지만 니콜라스는 입 한 번 열어 대꾸하지 않고 자리에 앉았지. 스퀴어스는 조수에게 의기양양한 눈길을, 아이들에게는 더없이 위세 넘치는 눈길을 던지고는 방을 나갔어. 그러고는 곧 스마이크를 멱살을 잡아 끌고 돌아왔지. 아니, 정확히는 멱살이 있을 자리에 가장 가까운 웃옷 조각을 붙잡고 끌고 온 거였어. 그런 장식이 그에게 달려 있기나 했다면 말이지."]], [["In any other place, the appearance of the wretched, jaded, spiritless object would have occasioned a murmur of compassion and remonstrance.", "다른 곳이었다면, 그 가엾고 지치고 풀 죽은 모습은 동정과 항의의 웅성거림을 불러일으켰을 거야. 거기서도 얼마쯤은 그랬지."], ["It had some effect, even there; for the lookers-on moved uneasily in their seats; and a few of the boldest ventured to steal looks at each other, expressive of indignation and pity.", "구경하던 아이들이 불편한 듯 자리에서 움찔거렸거든. 그리고 가장 대담한 몇몇은 슬쩍슬쩍 서로 눈길을 주고받으며 분노와 연민을 드러냈어."]], [["They were lost on Squeers, however, whose gaze was fastened on the luckless Smike, as he inquired, according to custom in such cases, whether he had anything to say for himself.", "하지만 그건 스퀴어스에게는 통하지 않았어. 그의 시선은 운 나쁜 스마이크에게 못 박혀 있었거든. 그는 이런 경우의 관례대로, 할 말이 있느냐고 물었어."]], [["‘Nothing, I suppose?’ said Squeers, with a diabolical grin.", "\"아무 할 말 없겠지, 응?\" 스퀴어스가 악마 같은 미소를 지으며 말했어."]], [["Smike glanced round, and his eye rested, for an instant, on Nicholas, as if he had expected him to intercede; but his look was riveted on his desk.", "스마이크는 주위를 둘러보았어. 그리고 그 눈길이 잠시 니콜라스에게 멈췄지. 끼어들어 막아 주길 바라는 듯 말이야. 하지만 니콜라스의 눈은 책상에 붙박여 있었어."]], [["‘Have you anything to say?’ demanded Squeers again: giving his right arm two or three flourishes to try its power and suppleness.", "\"할 말 있어?\" 스퀴어스가 다시 다그쳤어."], ["‘Stand a little out of the way, Mrs.", "그러면서 매질하는 오른팔의 힘과 유연함을 시험하듯 두세 번 휘둘러 보았지."], ["Squeers, my dear; I’ve hardly got room enough.’", "\"조금만 비켜 줘, 여보. 팔 휘두를 자리가 영 좁아서 말이야.\""]], [["‘Spare me, sir!’ cried Smike.", "\"살려 주세요, 선생님!\" 스마이크가 외쳤어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["that’s all, is it?’ said Squeers.", "그게 다야?\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘Yes, I’ll flog you within an inch of your life, and spare you that.’", "\"그래, 목숨이 간당간당할 때까지 두들겨 패고, 딱 그 정도만 살려 주마.\""]], [["‘Ha, ha, ha,’ laughed Mrs.", "\"하, 하, 하.\" 스퀴어스 부인이 웃었어."], ["Squeers, ‘that’s a good ‘un!’", "\"거 한마디 잘했네!\""]], [["‘I was driven to do it,’ said Smike faintly; and casting another imploring look about him.", "\"어쩔 수 없어서 그랬어요.\" 스마이크가 힘없이 말했어. 그러고는 또 한 번 애원하는 눈길로 주위를 둘러보았지."]], [["‘Driven to do it, were you?’ said Squeers.", "\"어쩔 수 없어서 그랬다고?\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["it wasn’t your fault; it was mine, I suppose--eh?’", "네 잘못이 아니라, 내 잘못이란 말이지——응?\""]], [["‘A nasty, ungrateful, pig-headed, brutish, obstinate, sneaking dog,’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"고약하고 배은망덕하고 고집불통에다 짐승 같고 음흉한 개 같은 놈!\""], ["Squeers, taking Smike’s head under her arm, and administering a cuff at every epithet; ‘what does he mean by that?’", "스퀴어스 부인이 스마이크의 머리를 겨드랑이에 끼고 욕설을 한마디 할 때마다 한 대씩 후려치며 외쳤어. \"이놈이 그게 무슨 소리야?\""]], [["‘Stand aside, my dear,’ replied Squeers.", "\"비켜, 여보.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."], ["‘We’ll try and find out.’", "\"어디 한번 알아내 보자고.\""]], [["Mrs.", "스퀴어스 부인은 힘을 쓰느라 숨이 차서 물러났어. 스퀴어스는 그 아이를 단단히 움켜쥐었어."], ["Squeers, being out of breath with her exertions, complied.", "절박한 매질 한 대가 그 몸에 떨어졌고——스마이크는 채찍에 움찔하며 고통의 비명을 질렀지——채찍이 다시 들렸어. 그리고 다시 막 내리치려는 순간——니콜라스 니클비가 벌떡 일어나 \"멈춰!\""], ["Squeers caught the boy firmly in his grip; one desperate cut had fallen on his body--he was wincing from the lash and uttering a scream of pain--it was raised again, and again about to fall--when Nicholas Nickleby, suddenly starting up, cried ‘Stop!’ in a voice that made the rafters ring.", "하고 외쳤어. 서까래가 다 울릴 만큼 쩌렁쩌렁한 목소리였지."]], [["‘Who cried stop?’ said Squeers, turning savagely round.", "\"멈추라고 한 게 누구야?\" 스퀴어스가 사납게 홱 돌아서며 말했어."]], [["‘I,’ said Nicholas, stepping forward.", "\"접니다.\" 니콜라스가 앞으로 나서며 말했어."], ["‘This must not go on.’", "\"이건 계속해선 안 됩니다.\""]], [["‘Must not go on!’ cried Squeers, almost in a shriek.", "\"계속해선 안 된다고!\" 스퀴어스가 거의 비명을 지르듯 외쳤어."]], [["‘No!’ thundered Nicholas.", "\"안 됩니다!\" 니콜라스가 우레같이 소리쳤어."]], [["Aghast and stupefied by the boldness of the interference, Squeers released his hold of Smike, and, falling back a pace or two, gazed upon Nicholas with looks that were positively frightful.", "이 대담한 참견에 기겁하고 얼이 빠진 스퀴어스는 스마이크를 붙잡은 손을 놓았어. 그러고는 한두 걸음 뒷걸음치며, 정말이지 무시무시한 눈빛으로 니콜라스를 노려보았지."]], [["‘I say must not,’ repeated Nicholas, nothing daunted; ‘shall not.", "\"안 된다고 했습니다.\" 니콜라스가 조금도 주눅 들지 않고 거듭 말했어."], ["I will prevent it.’", "\"안 됩니다. 제가 막겠습니다.\""]], [["Squeers continued to gaze upon him, with his eyes starting out of his head; but astonishment had actually, for the moment, bereft him of speech.", "스퀴어스는 눈알이 튀어나올 듯한 눈으로 계속 그를 노려보았어. 하지만 너무 놀란 나머지, 그 순간만큼은 말문이 막혀 버렸지."]], [["‘You have disregarded all my quiet interference in the miserable lad’s behalf,’ said Nicholas; ‘you have returned no answer to the letter in which I begged forgiveness for him, and offered to be responsible that he would remain quietly here.", "\"선생님은 이 가엾은 아이를 위한 제 조용한 부탁들을 죄다 무시하셨습니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["Don’t blame me for this public interference.", "\"그 아이를 용서해 달라고 빌면서, 여기 얌전히 있도록 제가 책임지겠다고 한 편지에도 아무 답을 안 주셨고요. 이렇게 사람들 앞에서 끼어든다고 저를 탓하지 마세요."], ["You have brought it upon yourself; not I.’", "이건 선생님이 자초한 일이지, 제가 그런 게 아닙니다.\""]], [["‘Sit down, beggar!’ screamed Squeers, almost beside himself with rage, and seizing Smike as he spoke.", "\"앉아, 이 거지 같은 놈아!\" 스퀴어스가 분노로 거의 제정신이 아닌 채 비명을 질렀어. 그러면서 말이 끝나기 무섭게 스마이크를 다시 붙잡았지."]], [["‘Wretch,’ rejoined Nicholas, fiercely, ‘touch him at your peril!", "\"이 인간 말종.\" 니콜라스가 사납게 받아쳤어."], ["I will not stand by, and see it done.", "\"그 아이를 건드리면 큰일 날 줄 알아!"], ["My blood is up, and I have the strength of ten such men as you.", "가만히 서서 그 짓을 보고만 있진 않을 테니까. 나도 피가 끓어오른 데다, 너 같은 인간 열 명을 당할 힘이 있어."], ["Look to yourself, for by Heaven I will not spare you, if you drive me on!’", "몸조심해. 하늘에 맹세코, 네가 나를 끝까지 몰아붙이면 절대 봐주지 않을 테니!\""]], [["‘Stand back,’ cried Squeers, brandishing his weapon.", "\"물러서!\" 스퀴어스가 매를 휘두르며 외쳤어."]], [["‘I have a long series of insults to avenge,’ said Nicholas, flushed with passion; ‘and my indignation is aggravated by the dastardly cruelties practised on helpless infancy in this foul den. Have a care; for if you do raise the devil within me, the consequences shall fall heavily upon your own head!’", "\"갚아 줘야 할 모욕이 줄줄이 쌓였어.\" 니콜라스가 격분으로 얼굴이 벌게져서 말했어. \"게다가 이 더러운 소굴에서 의지할 데 없는 어린것들한테 저질러진 비겁한 만행 때문에 분노가 더 치미는 거야. 조심해. 정말로 내 속의 악마를 깨운다면, 그 대가가 네 머리 위로 호되게 떨어질 테니까!\""]], [["He had scarcely spoken, when Squeers, in a violent outbreak of wrath, and with a cry like the howl of a wild beast, spat upon him, and struck him a blow across the face with his instrument of torture, which raised up a bar of livid flesh as it was inflicted. Smarting with the agony of the blow, and concentrating into that one moment all his feelings of rage, scorn, and indignation, Nicholas sprang upon him, wrested the weapon from his hand, and pinning him by the throat, beat the ruffian till he roared for mercy.", "니콜라스가 채 말을 마치기도 전에, 스퀴어스가 노여움을 사납게 터뜨리며 들짐승 울부짖는 듯한 소리를 내질렀어. 그러고는 니콜라스에게 침을 뱉고, 그 고문 도구로 얼굴을 후려쳤지. 매가 닿은 자리에 시퍼런 살자국이 부풀어 올랐어. 그 매질의 아픔에 얼얼해진 니콜라스는, 분노와 경멸과 격분의 온갖 감정을 그 한순간에 몰아넣어 스퀴어스에게 덤벼들었어. 그자의 손에서 매를 빼앗고, 목을 틀어쥐고는, 그 악당이 살려 달라고 울부짖을 때까지 두들겨 팼지."]], [["The boys--with the exception of Master Squeers, who, coming to his father’s assistance, harassed the enemy in the rear--moved not, hand or foot; but Mrs. Squeers, with many shrieks for aid, hung on to the tail of her partner’s coat, and endeavoured to drag him from his infuriated adversary; while Miss Squeers, who had been peeping through the keyhole in expectation of a very different scene, darted in at the very beginning of the attack, and after launching a shower of inkstands at the usher’s head, beat Nicholas to her heart’s content; animating herself, at every blow, with the recollection of his having refused her proffered love, and thus imparting additional strength to an arm which (as she took after her mother in this respect) was, at no time, one of the weakest.", "아이들은——아버지를 도우러 와서 뒤에서 적을 괴롭히던 스퀴어스 도련님만 빼고——손끝 하나, 발끝 하나 움직이지 않았어. 하지만 스퀴어스 부인은 도와 달라고 연신 비명을 지르며 제 짝의 웃옷 자락에 매달려, 격분한 적수에게서 남편을 떼어 내려고 안간힘을 썼지. 한편 스퀴어스 양은 열쇠 구멍으로 아주 다른 장면을 기대하며 엿보고 있었는데, 공격이 시작되자마자 안으로 뛰어들었어. 그러고는 그 보조 교사의 머리에 잉크병을 빗발치듯 던지고는, 실컷 니콜라스를 두들겨 팼지. 한 대씩 칠 때마다, 자기가 내민 사랑을 그가 거절했다는 기억을 떠올리며 기운을 냈어. 그래서 그러잖아도 (이 점에서는 어머니를 닮아) 어느 때고 약한 편이 아니던 그 팔에, 한층 더 힘이 실렸지."]], [["Nicholas, in the full torrent of his violence, felt the blows no more than if they had been dealt with feathers; but, becoming tired of the noise and uproar, and feeling that his arm grew weak besides, he threw all his remaining strength into half-a-dozen finishing cuts, and flung Squeers from him with all the force he could muster.", "니콜라스는 격렬한 분노가 거세게 솟구치던 터라, 그 매질을 깃털로 맞는 정도로밖에 안 느꼈어. 하지만 그 소란과 아우성에 진력이 났고, 게다가 팔에 힘이 빠지는 게 느껴지자, 남은 힘을 모조리 끌어모아 마무리로 대여섯 대를 갈겼어."], ["The violence of his fall precipitated Mrs.", "그러고는 있는 힘껏 스퀴어스를 내동댕이쳤지."], ["Squeers completely over an adjacent form; and Squeers striking his head against it in his descent, lay at his full length on the ground, stunned and motionless.", "그가 어찌나 세게 나가떨어졌던지, 스퀴어스 부인까지 옆에 있던 의자 위로 완전히 나동그라졌어. 그리고 스퀴어스는 떨어지면서 그 의자에 머리를 부딪쳤고, 정신을 잃은 채 꼼짝 못 하고 바닥에 길게 뻗어 버렸어."]], [["Having brought affairs to this happy termination, and ascertained, to his thorough satisfaction, that Squeers was only stunned, and not dead (upon which point he had had some unpleasant doubts at first), Nicholas left his family to restore him, and retired to consider what course he had better adopt.", "이렇게 일을 흐뭇하게 마무리한 니콜라스는, 스퀴어스가 죽은 게 아니라 그저 기절했을 뿐이라는 걸 확인하고는 더없이 만족했어 (처음엔 이 점이 영 께름칙해 마음에 걸렸거든). 그는 식구들이 스퀴어스를 깨우게 내버려 두고, 이제 어떻게 하는 게 좋을지 곰곰 생각하러 물러났지."], ["He looked anxiously round for Smike, as he left the room, but he was nowhere to be seen.", "방을 나서며 그는 스마이크를 애타게 둘러보았어. 하지만 어디에도 보이지 않았어."]], [["After a brief consideration, he packed up a few clothes in a small leathern valise, and, finding that nobody offered to oppose his progress, marched boldly out by the front-door, and shortly afterwards, struck into the road which led to Greta Bridge.", "잠시 생각한 끝에, 그는 작은 가죽 가방에 옷가지 몇 벌을 챙겨 넣었어. 그리고 아무도 그의 발걸음을 막으려 들지 않자, 대담하게 정문으로 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔지. 그러고는 얼마 안 가, 그레타 다리로 이어지는 길로 접어들었어."]], [["When he had cooled sufficiently to be enabled to give his present circumstances some little reflection, they did not appear in a very encouraging light; he had only four shillings and a few pence in his pocket, and was something more than two hundred and fifty miles from London, whither he resolved to direct his steps, that he might ascertain, among other things, what account of the morning’s proceedings Mr.", "마음이 어지간히 가라앉아 지금 처지를 조금 곱씹어 볼 만해지자, 형편이 그리 희망적으로 보이지는 않았어. 주머니엔 사 실링에 잔돈 몇 푼이 다였고, 런던까지는 이백오십 마일이 넘었거든."], ["Squeers transmitted to his most affectionate uncle.", "그래도 런던으로 발길을 돌리기로 마음먹었어. 무엇보다 스퀴어스가 그 다정하기 그지없는 삼촌에게 오늘 아침 일을 어떻게 전했는지 알아봐야겠다 싶었던 거야."]], [["Lifting up his eyes, as he arrived at the conclusion that there was no remedy for this unfortunate state of things, he beheld a horseman coming towards him, whom, on nearer approach, he discovered, to his infinite chagrin, to be no other than Mr.", "이 딱한 처지를 어쩔 도리가 없다는 결론에 이르러 고개를 들었을 때, 한 말 탄 사람이 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 게 보였어. 가까이 와서 보니, 분하게도 다름 아닌 존 브라우디 씨였지."], ["John Browdie, who, clad in cords and leather leggings, was urging his animal forward by means of a thick ash stick, which seemed to have been recently cut from some stout sapling.", "코르덴 옷에 가죽 각반을 두른 그는, 굵은 물푸레나무 막대기로 말을 몰아 가고 있었어. 막 어느 튼튼한 어린나무에서 베어 낸 듯한 막대기였지."]], [["‘I am in no mood for more noise and riot,’ thought Nicholas, ‘and yet, do what I will, I shall have an altercation with this honest blockhead, and perhaps a blow or two from yonder staff.’", "'더 시끄럽게 싸우고 떠들고 싶은 기분이 아닌데.' 니콜라스는 생각했어. '그래도 어쩌겠어. 이 정직한 멍청이하고 한바탕 입씨름을 하게 생겼고, 어쩌면 저 막대기로 한두 대 맞을지도 모르겠군.'"]], [["In truth, there appeared some reason to expect that such a result would follow from the encounter, for John Browdie no sooner saw Nicholas advancing, than he reined in his horse by the footpath, and waited until such time as he should come up; looking meanwhile, very sternly between the horse’s ears, at Nicholas, as he came on at his leisure.", "사실 이 만남에서 그런 결과가 따를 거라고 예상할 만한 까닭이 좀 있긴 했어. 존 브라우디는 니콜라스가 다가오는 걸 보자마자, 길가에 말고삐를 당겨 세우고는 그가 올 때까지 기다렸거든. 그러면서 말 두 귀 사이로, 느긋이 걸어오는 니콜라스를 아주 험상궂게 노려보고 있었지."]], [["‘Servant, young genelman,’ said John.", "\"안녕하시오, 젊은 양반.\" 존이 말했어."]], [["‘Yours,’ said Nicholas.", "\"안녕하세요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Weel; we ha’ met at last,’ observed John, making the stirrup ring under a smart touch of the ash stick.", "\"음, 드디어 만났구먼.\" 존이 물푸레나무 막대기로 등자를 탁 쳐서 쩌렁 울리게 하며 말했어."]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Nicholas, hesitating. ‘Come!’ he said, frankly, after a moment’s pause, ‘we parted on no very good terms the last time we met; it was my fault, I believe; but I had no intention of offending you, and no idea that I was doing so. I was very sorry for it, afterwards. Will you shake hands?’", "\"그러게요.\" 니콜라스가 머뭇거리며 대답했어. \"저기요!\" 그는 잠깐 뜸을 들이더니 솔직하게 말했어. \"지난번에 만났을 땐 그리 좋게 헤어지지 못했죠. 제 잘못이었을 겁니다. 하지만 당신 기분을 상하게 할 마음은 없었고, 그러고 있는 줄도 몰랐어요. 나중에 정말 미안했습니다. 악수해 주시겠어요?\""]], [["‘Shake honds!’ cried the good-humoured Yorkshireman; ‘ah! that I weel;’ at the same time, he bent down from the saddle, and gave Nicholas’s fist a huge wrench: ‘but wa’at be the matther wi’ thy feace, mun? it be all brokken loike.’", "\"악수라고!\" 마음씨 좋은 그 요크셔 사내가 외쳤어. \"아! 그러고말고요.\" 그러면서 안장에서 몸을 숙여, 니콜라스의 손을 와락 비틀어 쥐었어. \"근데 얼굴이 왜 그래요, 젊은 양반? 죄다 터진 것 같은데.\""]], [["‘It is a cut,’ said Nicholas, turning scarlet as he spoke,--‘a blow; but I returned it to the giver, and with good interest too.’", "\"베인 거예요.\" 니콜라스가 말하면서 새빨개졌어. \"한 대 맞았죠. 하지만 때린 놈한테 도로 갚아 줬어요. 그것도 두둑한 이자까지 쳐서요.\""]], [["‘Noa, did ‘ee though?’ exclaimed John Browdie.", "\"아니, 그랬단 말이오?\" 존 브라우디가 외쳤어."], ["‘Well deane!", "\"잘했네!"], ["I loike ‘un for thot.’", "그래서 더 마음에 들어요.\""]], [["‘The fact is,’ said Nicholas, not very well knowing how to make the avowal, ‘the fact is, that I have been ill-treated.’", "\"실은 말이죠.\" 니콜라스가 어떻게 털어놔야 할지 잘 몰라 하며 말했어. \"실은 제가 못된 꼴을 당했어요.\""]], [["‘Noa!’ interposed John Browdie, in a tone of compassion; for he was a giant in strength and stature, and Nicholas, very likely, in his eyes, seemed a mere dwarf; ‘dean’t say thot.’", "\"저런!\" 존 브라우디가 안쓰러운 투로 끼어들었어. 그는 힘이며 덩치며 거인 같은 사내였고, 그 눈에는 아마 니콜라스가 한낱 난쟁이처럼 보였을 거야. \"그런 말 마요.\""]], [["‘Yes, I have,’ replied Nicholas, ‘by that man Squeers, and I have beaten him soundly, and am leaving this place in consequence.’", "\"정말이에요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그 스퀴어스라는 작자한테요. 그래서 제가 그놈을 흠씬 두들겨 패고, 그 길로 거기를 떠나는 길입니다.\""]], [["‘What!’ cried John Browdie, with such an ecstatic shout, that the horse quite shied at it.", "\"뭐라고!\" 존 브라우디가 어찌나 신나게 고함을 질렀던지, 말이 깜짝 놀라 펄쩍 뛸 정도였어."], ["‘Beatten the schoolmeasther!", "\"교장 놈을 패줬다고!"], ["Ho!", "호!"], ["ho!", "호!"], ["ho!", "호!"], ["Beatten the schoolmeasther!", "교장 놈을 패줬다니!"], ["who ever heard o’ the loike o’ that noo!", "세상에 그런 일이 다 있나!"], ["Giv’ us thee hond agean, yoongster.", "다시 한번 손 좀 주시오, 젊은 양반."], ["Beatten the schoolmeasther!", "교장 놈을 패주다니!"], ["Dang it, I loov’ thee for’t.’", "젠장, 그래서 당신이 더 좋소.\""]], [["With these expressions of delight, John Browdie laughed and laughed again--so loud that the echoes, far and wide, sent back nothing but jovial peals of merriment--and shook Nicholas by the hand meanwhile, no less heartily.", "이렇게 기뻐하며 존 브라우디는 웃고 또 웃었어——어찌나 크게 웃었던지, 멀고 가까운 메아리가 죄다 유쾌한 웃음소리만 되돌려 보낼 정도였지——그러면서 그 못지않게 진심으로 니콜라스의 손을 흔들어 댔어. 웃음이 잦아들자, 그는 니콜라스가 앞으로 어쩔 셈이냐고 물었어."], ["When his mirth had subsided, he inquired what Nicholas meant to do; on his informing him, to go straight to London, he shook his head doubtfully, and inquired if he knew how much the coaches charged to carry passengers so far.", "곧장 런던으로 갈 거라는 대답에, 그는 못 미덥다는 듯 고개를 절레절레 흔들었지. 그러고는 그렇게 먼 데까지 손님을 태우는 데 마차가 얼마나 받는지 아느냐고 물었어."]], [["‘No, I do not,’ said Nicholas; ‘but it is of no great consequence to me, for I intend walking.’", "\"아뇨, 모릅니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"하지만 저한테는 별로 상관없는 일이에요. 걸어갈 작정이거든요.\""]], [["‘Gang awa’ to Lunnun afoot!’ cried John, in amazement.", "\"런던까지 걸어간다고!\" 존이 깜짝 놀라 외쳤어."]], [["‘Every step of the way,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"한 걸음도 빼놓지 않고요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘I should be many steps further on by this time, and so goodbye!’", "\"이쯤이면 벌써 한참 더 갔어야 하는데. 그럼 안녕히 가세요!\""]], [["‘Nay noo,’ replied the honest countryman, reining in his impatient horse, ‘stan’ still, tellee.", "\"아니, 잠깐만.\" 그 정직한 시골 사내가 안달하는 말을 멈춰 세우며 말했어."], ["Hoo much cash hast thee gotten?’", "\"가만 좀 서 봐요. 돈은 얼마나 있소?\""]], [["‘Not much,’ said Nicholas, colouring, ‘but I can make it enough.", "\"많진 않아요.\" 니콜라스가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했어."], ["Where there’s a will, there’s a way, you know.’", "\"그래도 어떻게든 꾸려 갈 수 있어요. 뜻이 있는 곳에 길이 있다잖아요.\""]], [["John Browdie made no verbal answer to this remark, but putting his hand in his pocket, pulled out an old purse of solid leather, and insisted that Nicholas should borrow from him whatever he required for his present necessities.", "존 브라우디는 이 말에 입으로는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았어. 대신 주머니에 손을 넣어, 단단한 가죽으로 된 낡은 지갑을 꺼냈지. 그러고는 당장 필요한 만큼은 자기한테 꼭 빌려 가라고 우겼어."]], [["‘Dean’t be afeard, mun,’ he said; ‘tak’ eneaf to carry thee whoam.", "\"겁먹지 말아요, 젊은 양반.\" 그가 말했어."], ["Thee’lt pay me yan day, a’ warrant.’", "\"집까지 갈 만큼은 챙겨요. 언젠가 갚으면 되지, 내 장담하리다.\""]], [["Nicholas could by no means be prevailed upon to borrow more than a sovereign, with which loan Mr.", "니콜라스는 도무지 일 소버린 이상은 빌리려 들지 않았어."], ["Browdie, after many entreaties that he would accept of more (observing, with a touch of Yorkshire caution, that if he didn’t spend it all, he could put the surplus by, till he had an opportunity of remitting it carriage free), was fain to content himself.", "브라우디 씨는 더 받으라고 한참을 사정했지만 (요크셔 사람 특유의 신중함을 곁들여, 다 안 쓰면 남는 건 따로 두었다가 운임 없이 부칠 기회가 생길 때 돌려주면 된다고도 했지) 결국 그 정도로 만족할 수밖에 없었어."]], [["‘Tak’ that bit o’ timber to help thee on wi’, mun,’ he added, pressing his stick on Nicholas, and giving his hand another squeeze; ‘keep a good heart, and bless thee.", "\"이 막대기도 가져가요, 가는 데 도움 될 거요.\" 그가 막대기를 니콜라스에게 건네며 덧붙였어."], ["Beatten the schoolmeasther!", "그러고는 또 한 번 손을 꽉 쥐었지. \"기운 내고, 잘 가시오."], ["‘Cod it’s the best thing a’ve heerd this twonty year!’", "교장 놈을 패주다니! 이거 참, 이십 년 만에 들은 가장 통쾌한 소식이오!\""]], [["So saying, and indulging, with more delicacy than might have been expected from him, in another series of loud laughs, for the purpose of avoiding the thanks which Nicholas poured forth, John Browdie set spurs to his horse, and went off at a smart canter: looking back, from time to time, as Nicholas stood gazing after him, and waving his hand cheerily, as if to encourage him on his way. Nicholas watched the horse and rider until they disappeared over the brow of a distant hill, and then set forward on his journey.", "이렇게 말하고는, 그한테서 기대하기 어려울 만큼 섬세하게도, 존 브라우디는 또 한바탕 큰 소리로 웃어 댔어. 니콜라스가 쏟아 내는 고맙다는 말을 피하려는 거였지. 그러고는 말에 박차를 가해, 경쾌한 구보로 떠나갔어. 니콜라스가 서서 뒷모습을 바라보는 동안, 그는 이따금 뒤를 돌아보며 가는 길에 힘내라는 듯 명랑하게 손을 흔들어 주었지. 니콜라스는 말과 그 사람이 저 멀리 언덕마루 너머로 사라질 때까지 지켜보았어. 그러고 나서 길을 나섰지."]], [["He did not travel far that afternoon, for by this time it was nearly dark, and there had been a heavy fall of snow, which not only rendered the way toilsome, but the track uncertain and difficult to find, after daylight, save by experienced wayfarers. He lay, that night, at a cottage, where beds were let at a cheap rate to the more humble class of travellers; and, rising betimes next morning, made his way before night to Boroughbridge. Passing through that town in search of some cheap resting-place, he stumbled upon an empty barn within a couple of hundred yards of the roadside; in a warm corner of which, he stretched his weary limbs, and soon fell asleep.", "그날 오후엔 그리 멀리 가지 못했어. 이미 날이 거의 저문 데다 눈까지 많이 내렸거든. 그 눈 때문에 길이 고될 뿐 아니라, 날이 밝아도 길눈 밝은 나그네가 아니고서는 자취를 찾기 어렵고 헷갈렸지. 그날 밤 그는 한 오두막에서 묵었어. 가난한 축의 나그네들에게 싼값에 잠자리를 빌려주는 곳이었지. 이튿날 아침 일찍 일어나, 날이 저물기 전에 버러브리지에 다다랐어. 값싼 쉴 곳을 찾아 그 마을을 지나다가, 길가에서 이백 야드쯤 떨어진 빈 헛간을 우연히 발견했어. 그 따뜻한 한구석에 지친 몸을 뉘었고, 이내 잠이 들었지."]], [["When he awoke next morning, and tried to recollect his dreams, which had been all connected with his recent sojourn at Dotheboys Hall, he sat up, rubbed his eyes and stared--not with the most composed countenance possible--at some motionless object which seemed to be stationed within a few yards in front of him.", "이튿날 아침 잠에서 깬 그는, 꾼 꿈을 떠올려 보려 했어. 죄다 얼마 전 두더보이즈 홀에서 지낸 일과 얽힌 꿈이었지. 그는 자리에 일어나 앉아 눈을 비비며, 앞쪽 몇 야드 거리에 멈춰 선 듯한 어떤 꼼짝 않는 형체를 빤히 바라보았어——그리 차분한 얼굴은 아니었지."]], [["‘Strange!’ cried Nicholas; ‘can this be some lingering creation of the visions that have scarcely left me!", "\"이상하군!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어."], ["It cannot be real--and yet I--I am awake!", "\"이게 아직 채 가시지도 않은 꿈이 남긴 환영인가! 진짜일 리 없는데——그런데 나는——나는 깨어 있잖아!"], ["Smike!’", "스마이크!\""]], [["The form moved, rose, advanced, and dropped upon its knees at his feet.", "그 형체가 움직였어. 일어서서 다가오더니, 그의 발치에 무릎을 꿇었지."], ["It was Smike indeed.", "정말 스마이크였어."]], [["‘Why do you kneel to me?’ said Nicholas, hastily raising him.", "\"왜 나한테 무릎을 꿇는 거야?\" 니콜라스가 얼른 그를 일으키며 말했어."]], [["‘To go with you--anywhere--everywhere--to the world’s end--to the churchyard grave,’ replied Smike, clinging to his hand.", "\"형이랑 같이 가려고요——어디든——어디든지——세상 끝까지라도——교회 묘지의 무덤까지라도요.\" 스마이크가 그의 손을 붙잡으며 대답했어."], ["‘Let me, oh do let me.", "\"그러게 해 줘요, 오, 제발 그러게 해 줘요."], ["You are my home--my kind friend--take me with you, pray.’", "형이 제 집이에요——제 다정한 친구예요——저를 데려가 줘요, 제발요.\""]], [["‘I am a friend who can do little for you,’ said Nicholas, kindly.", "\"나는 너를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 게 별로 없는 친구야.\" 니콜라스가 다정하게 말했어."], ["‘How came you here?’", "\"어떻게 여기까지 왔어?\""]], [["He had followed him, it seemed; had never lost sight of him all the way; had watched while he slept, and when he halted for refreshment; and had feared to appear before, lest he should be sent back. He had not intended to appear now, but Nicholas had awakened more suddenly than he looked for, and he had had no time to conceal himself.", "알고 보니 그는 니콜라스를 따라온 거였어. 오는 내내 한순간도 그를 놓치지 않았다는 거지. 그가 잠잘 때도, 요기하러 멈췄을 때도 지켜보고 있었대. 그러면서도 진작 나서기가 무서웠다는 거야. 다시 돌려보내질까 봐서 말이지. 지금도 나설 생각은 아니었는데, 니콜라스가 생각보다 갑자기 깨는 바람에 몸을 숨길 틈이 없었던 거야."]], [["‘Poor fellow!’ said Nicholas, ‘your hard fate denies you any friend but one, and he is nearly as poor and helpless as yourself.’", "\"가엾은 녀석.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"모진 팔자 탓에 너한테는 친구라곤 단 하나뿐인데, 그마저도 너만큼이나 가난하고 의지할 데 없는 신세구나.\""]], [["‘May I--may I go with you?’ asked Smike, timidly.", "\"저——저 형이랑 같이 가도 돼요?\" 스마이크가 머뭇머뭇 물었어."], ["‘I will be your faithful hard-working servant, I will, indeed.", "\"충실하게, 부지런히 일하는 하인이 될게요, 정말이에요. 옷도 필요 없어요.\""], ["I want no clothes,’ added the poor creature, drawing his rags together; ‘these will do very well.", "그 가엾은 녀석이 제 넝마를 끌어모으며 덧붙였어. \"이거면 충분해요."], ["I only want to be near you.’", "그저 형 곁에 있고 싶을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["‘And you shall,’ cried Nicholas.", "\"그래, 그렇게 해.\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어."], ["‘And the world shall deal by you as it does by me, till one or both of us shall quit it for a better.", "\"그리고 세상은 나를 대하듯 너도 대할 거야. 우리 둘 중 하나가, 아니면 둘 다 더 나은 곳으로 떠나는 그날까지 말이야."], ["Come!’", "가자!\""]], [["With these words, he strapped his burden on his shoulders, and, taking his stick in one hand, extended the other to his delighted charge; and so they passed out of the old barn, together.", "이 말과 함께, 그는 짐을 둘러메고는 한 손에 막대기를 들고, 다른 손을 기뻐하는 그 아이에게 내밀었어. 그렇게 두 사람은 함께 그 낡은 헛간을 나섰어."]], [["# CHAPTER 14", "# CHAPTER 14"]], [["Having the Misfortune to treat of none but Common People, is necessarily of a Mean and Vulgar Character", "평민들만 다루는 불행을 안고 있어, 어쩔 수 없이 천하고 비속한 성격을 띤 장"]], [["In that quarter of London in which Golden Square is situated, there is a bygone, faded, tumble-down street, with two irregular rows of tall meagre houses, which seem to have stared each other out of countenance years ago. The very chimneys appear to have grown dismal and melancholy, from having had nothing better to look at than the chimneys over the way. Their tops are battered, and broken, and blackened with smoke; and, here and there, some taller stack than the rest, inclining heavily to one side, and toppling over the roof, seems to meditate taking revenge for half a century’s neglect, by crushing the inhabitants of the garrets beneath.", "골든 스퀘어가 자리 잡은 런던의 그 동네에는, 한물간 데다 빛바래고 다 쓰러져 가는 거리가 하나 있어. 길 양쪽으로 키만 멀쑥하고 비쩍 마른 집들이 들쭉날쭉 두 줄로 늘어서 있는데, 마치 오래전에 서로 노려보다가 기가 죽어 버린 것처럼 보였지. 굴뚝들마저 음울하고 우울해진 것 같았어. 건너편 굴뚝 말고는 달리 쳐다볼 만한 게 없었으니까. 굴뚝 꼭대기는 죄다 찌그러지고 부서지고 연기에 시커멓게 그을려 있었어. 그리고 여기저기 다른 것보다 유난히 높은 굴뚝 하나가 한쪽으로 심하게 기울어져 지붕 위로 넘어질 듯 휘청거렸지. 마치 반세기 동안 받아 온 무관심에 복수라도 하려는 듯이 말이야. 그 밑 다락방에 사는 사람들을 짓눌러 버리겠다는 듯이."]], [["The fowls who peck about the kennels, jerking their bodies hither and thither with a gait which none but town fowls are ever seen to adopt, and which any country cock or hen would be puzzled to understand, are perfectly in keeping with the crazy habitations of their owners. Dingy, ill-plumed, drowsy flutterers, sent, like many of the neighbouring children, to get a livelihood in the streets, they hop, from stone to stone, in forlorn search of some hidden eatable in the mud, and can scarcely raise a crow among them. The only one with anything approaching to a voice, is an aged bantam at the baker’s; and even he is hoarse, in consequence of bad living in his last place.", "하수구 둘레를 쪼아 대는 닭들은 몸을 이리저리 홱홱 트는 걸음걸이로 돌아다녔어. 그런 걸음은 도시 닭한테서만 볼 수 있지, 시골 수탉이나 암탉이라면 도무지 이해하지 못할 그런 걸음이었어. 그리고 그 닭들은 제 주인들의 다 허물어져 가는 집과 어찌나 잘 어울리는지. 거무튀튀하고 깃털도 볼품없고 졸린 듯 푸드덕거리는 이 새들은, 동네 아이들 여럿이 그렇듯 거리에 나가 먹고살라고 내몰린 신세였어. 돌멩이에서 돌멩이로 폴짝폴짝 뛰어다니며 진흙 속에 숨은 먹을거리를 가엾게 찾아 헤맸지만, 그 사이에서 제대로 우는 놈 하나 찾기 힘들었지. 목소리 비슷한 거라도 내는 건 빵집의 늙은 밴텀 닭 한 마리뿐이었어. 그런데 그놈마저도, 전에 살던 데서 못 먹고 지낸 탓에 목이 쉬어 있었지."]], [["To judge from the size of the houses, they have been, at one time, tenanted by persons of better condition than their present occupants; but they are now let off, by the week, in floors or rooms, and every door has almost as many plates or bell-handles as there are apartments within. The windows are, for the same reason, sufficiently diversified in appearance, being ornamented with every variety of common blind and curtain that can easily be imagined; while every doorway is blocked up, and rendered nearly impassable, by a motley collection of children and porter pots of all sizes, from the baby in arms and the half-pint pot, to the full-grown girl and half-gallon can.", "집들 크기로 짐작하건대, 한때는 지금 사는 사람들보다 형편이 나은 이들이 세 들어 살았던 것 같아. 하지만 지금은 층별로, 또는 방별로 주 단위로 세를 놓고 있었어. 그래서 문마다 안에 든 방 수만큼이나 많은 문패와 초인종 손잡이가 거의 다닥다닥 붙어 있었지. 창문도 같은 이유로 생김새가 가지각색이었어. 쉽게 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 흔한 블라인드와 커튼으로 장식되어 있었거든. 그리고 문간마다 알록달록한 아이들과 온갖 크기의 맥주 단지가 잔뜩 막아서서, 도무지 지나다닐 수가 없을 지경이었어. 품에 안긴 갓난아기와 반 파인트들이 단지부터, 다 큰 처녀와 반 갤런들이 통까지 죄다 있었지."]], [["In the parlour of one of these houses, which was perhaps a thought dirtier than any of its neighbours; which exhibited more bell-handles, children, and porter pots, and caught in all its freshness the first gust of the thick black smoke that poured forth, night and day, from a large brewery hard by; hung a bill, announcing that there was yet one room to let within its walls, though on what story the vacant room could be--regard being had to the outward tokens of many lodgers which the whole front displayed, from the mangle in the kitchen window to the flower-pots on the parapet--it would have been beyond the power of a calculating boy to discover.", "이런 집들 가운데 한 채의 일층 거실에 광고지 한 장이 붙어 있었어. 그 집은 어쩌면 이웃집들보다 조금 더 지저분했을지도 몰라. 초인종 손잡이도, 아이들도, 맥주 단지도 더 많았고, 바로 옆 큰 양조장에서 밤낮없이 쏟아져 나오는 짙고 검은 연기의 첫 줄기를 고스란히 들이마시는 자리였지. 그 광고지에는 이 집 안에 아직 세놓을 방이 하나 남았다고 적혀 있었어. 그런데 그 빈방이 몇 층에 있는지는, 아무리 셈 잘하는 아이라도 도무지 알아낼 도리가 없었을 거야. 부엌 창문의 빨래 짜는 기계부터 난간의 화분까지, 집 앞면 전체가 세입자가 많다는 증거를 죄다 내보이고 있었으니까."]], [["The common stairs of this mansion were bare and carpetless; but a curious visitor who had to climb his way to the top, might have observed that there were not wanting indications of the progressive poverty of the inmates, although their rooms were shut.", "이 저택의 공용 계단은 휑하니 카펫 한 장 깔려 있지 않았어. 하지만 꼭대기까지 기어 올라가야 하는 호기심 많은 손님이라면, 방문은 다 닫혀 있어도 사는 사람들이 위로 갈수록 점점 더 가난해진다는 표시를 알아챘을 거야."], ["Thus, the first-floor lodgers, being flush of furniture, kept an old mahogany table--real mahogany--on the landing-place outside, which was only taken in, when occasion required.", "그러니까 일층 세입자들은 가구가 넉넉해서, 진짜 마호가니로 만든 낡은 마호가니 탁자를 바깥 층계참에 내놓고 있었어. 그 탁자는 필요할 때만 안으로 들여놨지."], ["On the second story, the spare furniture dwindled down to a couple of old deal chairs, of which one, belonging to the back-room, was shorn of a leg, and bottomless.", "이층에 가면 남아도는 가구가 낡은 전나무 의자 두 개로 확 줄어들었어. 그 가운데 뒷방 의자는 다리 하나가 떨어져 나가고 앉는 자리도 뻥 뚫려 있었지."], ["The story above, boasted no greater excess than a worm-eaten wash-tub; and the garret landing-place displayed no costlier articles than two crippled pitchers, and some broken blacking-bottles.", "그 위층은 좀먹은 빨래통 하나밖에 더 자랑할 게 없었어. 그리고 다락방 층계참에는 깨진 물병 두 개와 부서진 구두약병 몇 개보다 값나가는 물건은 하나도 없었지."]], [["It was on this garret landing-place that a hard-featured square-faced man, elderly and shabby, stopped to unlock the door of the front attic, into which, having surmounted the task of turning the rusty key in its still more rusty wards, he walked with the air of legal owner.", "바로 이 다락방 층계참에서, 모나게 생긴 데다 얼굴이 네모진 한 남자가 걸음을 멈췄어. 나이가 지긋하고 행색이 초라했지. 그는 앞쪽 다락방 문을 따려고 멈춰 선 거였어. 안 그래도 녹슨 자물쇠 구멍에 더 녹슨 열쇠를 끼워 돌리는 일을 겨우 해치우고는, 법적인 주인이라도 되는 양 거드름을 피우며 안으로 들어갔어."]], [["This person wore a wig of short, coarse, red hair, which he took off with his hat, and hung upon a nail.", "이 사람은 짧고 뻣뻣한 빨간 머리 가발을 쓰고 있었는데, 모자랑 같이 벗어서 못에 걸어 두었어. 그러고는 그 자리에 더러운 무명 잠자리 모자를 눌러쓰고, 어두운 데서 손으로 더듬더듬 양초 토막 하나를 찾아냈지."], ["Having adopted in its place a dirty cotton nightcap, and groped about in the dark till he found a remnant of candle, he knocked at the partition which divided the two garrets, and inquired, in a loud voice, whether Mr. Noggs had a light.", "그런 다음 두 다락방을 가르는 칸막이벽을 두드리며, 노그스 씨한테 불 좀 있느냐고 큰 소리로 물었어."]], [["The sounds that came back were stifled by the lath and plaster, and it seemed moreover as though the speaker had uttered them from the interior of a mug or other drinking vessel; but they were in the voice of Newman, and conveyed a reply in the affirmative.", "돌아온 소리는 외벽 욋가지와 회반죽에 막혀 잔뜩 먹먹했어. 게다가 말하는 사람이 머그잔이나 다른 무슨 술잔 속에서 소리를 내뱉은 것처럼 들리기도 했지. 하지만 그건 뉴먼의 목소리였고, 그렇다는 대답을 전해 왔어."]], [["‘A nasty night, Mr.", "\"고약한 밤이네요, 노그스 씨!\""], ["Noggs!’ said the man in the nightcap, stepping in to light his candle.", "잠자리 모자를 쓴 남자가 양초에 불을 붙이려고 안으로 들어서며 말했어."]], [["‘Does it rain?’ asked Newman.", "\"비 와요?\" 뉴먼이 물었어."]], [["‘Does it?’ replied the other pettishly.", "\"비 오느냐고요?\" 상대가 짜증스럽게 대꾸했어."], ["‘I am wet through.’", "\"흠뻑 젖었구먼.\""]], [["‘It doesn’t take much to wet you and me through, Mr.", "\"당신이나 나나 젖는 데 비가 많이 와야 하는 것도 아니잖소, 크롤 씨.\""], ["Crowl,’ said Newman, laying his hand upon the lappel of his threadbare coat.", "뉴먼이 자기 닳고 닳은 외투 깃에 손을 얹으며 말했어."]], [["‘Well; and that makes it the more vexatious,’ observed Mr.", "\"그러니까 그게 더 약 오르는 거 아니오.\""], ["Crowl, in the same pettish tone.", "크롤 씨가 여전히 짜증스러운 말투로 말했어."]], [["Uttering a low querulous growl, the speaker, whose harsh countenance was the very epitome of selfishness, raked the scanty fire nearly out of the grate, and, emptying the glass which Noggs had pushed towards him, inquired where he kept his coals.", "낮게 투덜대며 불평을 내뱉는 이 남자는, 그 사납게 생긴 얼굴이 이기심을 그대로 빼다 박은 것 같았어. 그는 얼마 안 되는 불씨를 화로에서 거의 다 긁어내고는, 노그스가 밀어 준 잔을 쭉 비우더니 석탄을 어디다 두느냐고 물었어."]], [["Newman Noggs pointed to the bottom of a cupboard, and Mr.", "뉴먼 노그스가 찬장 밑을 가리키자, 크롤 씨가 부삽을 움켜쥐고 쟁여 둔 석탄의 절반을 불에 끼얹었어."], ["Crowl, seizing the shovel, threw on half the stock: which Noggs very deliberately took off again, without saying a word.", "그러자 노그스는 아무 말 없이 아주 천천히 그걸 도로 걷어 냈지."]], [["‘You have not turned saving, at this time of day, I hope?’ said Crowl.", "\"설마 이 나이에 와서 갑자기 짠돌이가 된 건 아니겠죠?\" 크롤이 말했어."]], [["Newman pointed to the empty glass, as though it were a sufficient refutation of the charge, and briefly said that he was going downstairs to supper.", "뉴먼은 그런 혐의를 반박하기에 충분하다는 듯 빈 잔을 가리켰어. 그러고는 아래층에 저녁 먹으러 내려갈 참이라고 짤막하게 말했지."]], [["‘To the Kenwigses?’ asked Crowl.", "\"켄위그스네로요?\" 크롤이 물었어."]], [["Newman nodded assent.", "뉴먼이 그렇다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였어."]], [["‘Think of that now!’ said Crowl.", "\"이거 참, 생각 좀 해 보쇼!\""], ["‘If I didn’t--thinking that you were certain not to go, because you said you wouldn’t--tell Kenwigs I couldn’t come, and make up my mind to spend the evening with you!’", "크롤이 말했어. \"내가 글쎄, 당신이 안 간다고 했으니 분명 안 갈 줄 알고 말이지, 켄위그스한테는 못 간다고 해 놓고, 저녁을 당신이랑 같이 보내기로 마음먹었단 말이오!\""]], [["‘I was obliged to go,’ said Newman.", "\"나는 갈 수밖에 없었소.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."], ["‘They would have me.’", "\"그쪽에서 한사코 오라고 했거든.\""]], [["‘Well; but what’s to become of me?’ urged the selfish man, who never thought of anybody else.", "\"그래, 그럼 나는 어쩌라고요?\" 남 생각이라고는 도무지 할 줄 모르는 이 이기적인 남자가 졸라 댔어."], ["‘It’s all your fault.", "\"다 당신 잘못이오."], ["I’ll tell you what--I’ll sit by your fire till you come back again.’", "내가 어떻게 할 거냐면 말이지, 당신이 돌아올 때까지 당신 불가에 앉아 있겠소.\""]], [["Newman cast a despairing glance at his small store of fuel, but, not having the courage to say no--a word which in all his life he never had said at the right time, either to himself or anyone else--gave way to the proposed arrangement.", "뉴먼은 얼마 안 되는 자기 땔감을 절망스럽게 바라봤어. 하지만 안 된다고 말할 용기가 없었지."], ["Mr.", "평생 단 한 번도, 자기한테든 남한테든, 그 말을 제때 해 본 적이 없는 사람이었거든."], ["Crowl immediately went about making himself as comfortable, with Newman Nogg’s means, as circumstances would admit of his being made.", "그래서 제안받은 대로 양보하고 말았어. 크롤 씨는 곧장 뉴먼 노그스의 살림을 가지고, 형편이 허락하는 한 최대한 편안하게 자리를 잡았지."]], [["The lodgers to whom Crowl had made allusion under the designation of ‘the Kenwigses,’ were the wife and olive branches of one Mr.", "크롤이 \"켄위그스네\"라고 부른 그 세입자들은, 켄위그스 씨라는 사람의 아내와 자식들이었어. 켄위그스 씨는 상아를 깎는 선반공이었는데, 이 집에서 꽤나 한자리하는 사람으로 여겨졌지."], ["Kenwigs, a turner in ivory, who was looked upon as a person of some consideration on the premises, inasmuch as he occupied the whole of the first floor, comprising a suite of two rooms.", "방 두 개가 죽 이어진 일층 전체를 다 차지하고 살았으니까. 켄위그스 부인도 몸가짐이 영락없는 귀부인이었고 집안도 아주 점잖았어."], ["Mrs.", "수도세를 걷는 삼촌이 있었거든."], ["Kenwigs, too, was quite a lady in her manners, and of a very genteel family, having an uncle who collected a water-rate; besides which distinction, the two eldest of her little girls went twice a week to a dancing school in the neighbourhood, and had flaxen hair, tied with blue ribbons, hanging in luxuriant pigtails down their backs; and wore little white trousers with frills round the ankles--for all of which reasons, and many more equally valid but too numerous to mention, Mrs.", "게다가 이런 영예 말고도, 어린 딸들 가운데 큰 두 아이가 일주일에 두 번씩 동네 무용 학원에 다녔지. 아이들은 아마빛 머리를 파란 리본으로 묶어 등 뒤로 탐스러운 댕기를 늘어뜨렸고, 발목에 주름 장식이 달린 자그마한 흰 바지를 입고 다녔어."], ["Kenwigs was considered a very desirable person to know, and was the constant theme of all the gossips in the street, and even three or four doors round the corner at both ends.", "이런 이유들 때문에, 그리고 여기 다 적기엔 너무 많지만 똑같이 그럴듯한 다른 이유들 때문에, 켄위그스 부인은 알고 지낼 만한 아주 좋은 사람으로 여겨졌어. 그래서 거리의 온갖 수다쟁이들 입에, 심지어 양쪽 모퉁이를 돌아 서너 집 떨어진 데서까지 늘 오르내리는 화젯거리였지."]], [["It was the anniversary of that happy day on which the Church of England as by law established, had bestowed Mrs.", "마침 그날은 법으로 정해진 영국 국교회가 켄위그스 부인을 켄위그스 씨에게 점지해 준, 그 행복한 날의 기념일이었어."], ["Kenwigs upon Mr. Kenwigs; and in grateful commemoration of the same, Mrs.", "그래서 켄위그스 부인은 그날을 고맙게 기리려고, 가려 뽑은 친구 몇몇을 일층으로 불러 카드놀이와 저녁 식사 자리를 마련했어."], ["Kenwigs had invited a few select friends to cards and a supper in the first floor, and had put on a new gown to receive them in: which gown, being of a flaming colour and made upon a juvenile principle, was so successful that Mr.", "손님을 맞으려고 새 드레스도 차려입었지."], ["Kenwigs said the eight years of matrimony and the five children seemed all a dream, and Mrs. Kenwigs younger and more blooming than on the very first Sunday he had kept company with her.", "그 드레스는 색이 화려하고 젊은 취향으로 만든 거였는데, 어찌나 잘 어울렸던지 켄위그스 씨는 결혼 팔 년에 아이 다섯이 다 한바탕 꿈만 같다고, 켄위그스 부인이 함께 사귀던 첫 일요일보다도 더 젊고 화사해 보인다고 했어."]], [["Beautiful as Mrs.", "켄위그스 부인이 차려입은 모습은 정말 아름다웠어. 게다가 어찌나 위엄이 넘치던지, 적어도 요리사 하나에 가정부 하나쯤은 두고 그저 부리기만 하면 되는 사람처럼 보일 정도였지."], ["Kenwigs looked when she was dressed though, and so stately that you would have supposed she had a cook and housemaid at least, and nothing to do but order them about, she had a world of trouble with the preparations; more, indeed, than she, being of a delicate and genteel constitution, could have sustained, had not the pride of housewifery upheld her.", "그래도 음식 준비하느라 어찌나 고생을 했던지. 정말이지, 곱고 점잖은 체질인 그가 살림하는 자부심에 떠받쳐지지 않았더라면 도저히 버텨 내지 못했을 만큼 말이야."], ["At last, however, all the things that had to be got together were got together, and all the things that had to be got out of the way were got out of the way, and everything was ready, and the collector himself having promised to come, fortune smiled upon the occasion.", "그래도 마침내 모아 와야 할 것들은 다 모아 왔고, 치워야 할 것들은 다 치웠고, 모든 게 준비됐어. 게다가 수도세 걷는 삼촌까지 직접 오겠다고 약속했으니, 이번 일에는 행운이 미소를 지어 준 셈이었지."]], [["The party was admirably selected.", "손님은 더없이 잘 골라 놨어."], ["There were, first of all, Mr. Kenwigs and Mrs.", "우선 켄위그스 씨와 켄위그스 부인, 그리고 저녁 식탁에 늦게까지 앉아 있는 켄위그스네 어린 네 자식이 있었지."], ["Kenwigs, and four olive Kenwigses who sat up to supper; firstly, because it was but right that they should have a treat on such a day; and secondly, because their going to bed, in presence of the company, would have been inconvenient, not to say improper.", "첫째로는, 이런 날이니 아이들도 한턱 누리는 게 마땅했기 때문이고, 둘째로는 손님들 앞에서 아이들을 재우러 보내는 게 불편한 데다, 점잖지 못하다고까지 할 수 있었기 때문이야."], ["Then, there was a young lady who had made Mrs.", "그다음에는 켄위그스 부인의 드레스를 만들어 준 젊은 아가씨가 있었어."], ["Kenwigs’s dress, and who--it was the most convenient thing in the world--living in the two-pair back, gave up her bed to the baby, and got a little girl to watch it.", "이 아가씨는 세상에 더없이 편리하게도 뒤쪽 삼층 방에 살아서, 자기 침대를 아기한테 내주고 작은 여자아이를 하나 구해다 아기를 지켜보게 했지. 그리고 이 아가씨와 짝이 맞게, 켄위그스 씨가 총각이던 시절부터 알고 지낸 젊은 남자도 있었어."], ["Then, to match this young lady, was a young man, who had known Mr.", "이 남자는 바람둥이라는 평판이 있어서 부인들 사이에서 꽤 인기가 많았지."], ["Kenwigs when he was a bachelor, and was much esteemed by the ladies, as bearing the reputation of a rake.", "여기에 갓 결혼한 부부도 더해졌는데, 켄위그스 부부가 연애하던 시절에 둘을 찾아온 적이 있는 사람들이었어. 또 켄위그스 부인의 여동생도 있었는데, 정말이지 미인이었지."], ["To these were added a newly-married couple, who had visited Mr.", "그 밖에도 또 다른 젊은 남자가 하나 있었는데, 방금 말한 그 아가씨한테 진지한 마음을 품고 있다는 사람이었어."], ["and Mrs. Kenwigs in their courtship; and a sister of Mrs.", "그리고 노그스 씨도 있었는데, 한때 신사였던 사람이라 초대할 만한 점잖은 인물이었지."], ["Kenwigs’s, who was quite a beauty; besides whom, there was another young man, supposed to entertain honourable designs upon the lady last mentioned; and Mr.", "또 뒷방에 사는 나이 지긋한 부인도 한 사람 있었고, 젊은 아가씨가 또 하나 있었어. 이 아가씨는 어쩌면 수도세 걷는 삼촌 다음으로 이 자리의 큰 구경거리였는데, 무언극에 \"출연\"하는 극장 소방수의 딸이었거든."], ["Noggs, who was a genteel person to ask, because he had been a gentleman once.", "게다가 전에 없던 무대 재능을 타고나서, 노래며 낭송이며 켄위그스 부인의 눈에 눈물이 핑 돌게 할 만큼 잘했지."], ["There were also an elderly lady from the back-parlour, and one more young lady, who, next to the collector, perhaps was the great lion of the party, being the daughter of a theatrical fireman, who ‘went on’ in the pantomime, and had the greatest turn for the stage that was ever known, being able to sing and recite in a manner that brought the tears into Mrs.", "이렇게 좋은 친구들을 보는 즐거움에 딱 한 가지 흠이 있었어. 뒷방에 사는 그 부인이, 살이 아주 많이 찌고 예순이 넘은 양반이 가슴이 깊이 팬 얇은 모슬린 드레스에 짧은 새끼 염소 가죽 장갑을 끼고 온 거였어."], ["Kenwigs’s eyes. There was only one drawback upon the pleasure of seeing such friends, and that was, that the lady in the back-parlour, who was very fat, and turned of sixty, came in a low book-muslin dress and short kid gloves, which so exasperated Mrs Kenwigs, that that lady assured her visitors, in private, that if it hadn’t happened that the supper was cooking at the back-parlour grate at that moment, she certainly would have requested its representative to withdraw.", "켄위그스 부인은 어찌나 약이 올랐던지, 손님들한테 살그머니 장담하기를, 마침 그때 저녁 음식이 뒷방 화로에서 익고 있는 중만 아니었다면 분명 그 양반한테 물러가 달라고 했을 거라고 했지."]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr.", "\"여보,\" 켄위그스 씨가 말했어."], ["Kenwigs, ‘wouldn’t it be better to begin a round game?’", "\"이제 다 같이 하는 놀이를 시작하는 게 낫지 않겠소?\""]], [["‘Kenwigs, my dear,’ returned his wife, ‘I am surprised at you.", "\"켄위그스, 여보,\" 아내가 대꾸했어."], ["Would you begin without my uncle?’", "\"당신한테 놀랐어요. 삼촌도 없이 시작하려고요?\""]], [["‘I forgot the collector,’ said Kenwigs; ‘oh no, that would never do.’", "\"수도세 삼촌을 깜빡했군.\" 켄위그스가 말했어. \"아, 안 되지, 그건 절대 안 될 일이오.\""]], [["‘He’s so particular,’ said Mrs.", "\"삼촌이 어찌나 까다로운지,\""], ["Kenwigs, turning to the other married lady, ‘that if we began without him, I should be out of his will for ever.’", "켄위그스 부인이 갓 결혼한 다른 부인 쪽으로 돌아보며 말했어. \"삼촌 없이 시작했다간 유언장에서 영영 빼 버릴 거예요.\""]], [["‘Dear!’ cried the married lady.", "\"어머나!\" 갓 결혼한 부인이 소리쳤어."]], [["‘You’ve no idea what he is,’ replied Mrs.", "\"삼촌이 어떤 사람인지 모르실 거예요.\""], ["Kenwigs; ‘and yet as good a creature as ever breathed.’", "켄위그스 부인이 대답했어. \"그래도 세상에 둘도 없이 착한 분이지요.\""]], [["‘The kindest-hearted man as ever was,’ said Kenwigs.", "\"세상에 그렇게 마음씨 고운 사람도 없지요.\" 켄위그스가 말했어."]], [["‘It goes to his heart, I believe, to be forced to cut the water off, when the people don’t pay,’ observed the bachelor friend, intending a joke.", "\"돈을 안 내는 사람들 수돗물을 끊어야 할 때면, 그분 가슴이 아플 거라니까요.\" 총각 친구가 농담 삼아 한마디 했어."]], [["‘George,’ said Mr.", "\"조지,\""], ["Kenwigs, solemnly, ‘none of that, if you please.’", "켄위그스 씨가 엄숙하게 말했어. \"제발 그런 소린 좀 그만하게.\""]], [["‘It was only my joke,’ said the friend, abashed.", "\"그냥 농담이었네.\" 친구가 머쓱해져서 말했어."]], [["‘George,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"조지,\" 켄위그스 씨가 받아쳤어."], ["Kenwigs, ‘a joke is a wery good thing--a wery good thing--but when that joke is made at the expense of Mrs.", "\"농담이야 아주 좋은 거지. 아주 좋은 거야."], ["Kenwigs’s feelings, I set my face against it.", "하지만 그 농담이 켄위그스 부인의 기분을 상하게 하는 거라면, 나는 단호히 반대하네."], ["A man in public life expects to be sneered at--it is the fault of his elewated sitiwation, and not of himself.", "공직에 몸담은 사람이라면 비웃음을 사는 거야 으레 있는 일이지. 그건 그 사람의 높으신 지위 탓이지, 그 사람 잘못이 아니야."], ["Mrs. Kenwigs’s relation is a public man, and that he knows, George, and that he can bear; but putting Mrs.", "켄위그스 부인의 친척은 공직에 계신 분이고, 그건 본인도 아는 일이지, 조지."], ["Kenwigs out of the question (if I COULD put Mrs.", "그리고 그건 견딜 수 있는 일이고."], ["Kenwigs out of the question on such an occasion as this), I have the honour to be connected with the collector by marriage; and I cannot allow these remarks in my--’ Mr.", "하지만 켄위그스 부인은 차치하고라도—이런 자리에서 내가 켄위그스 부인을 차치할 수 있을지 모르겠다만—나는 영광스럽게도 그 수도세 어른과 결혼으로 맺어진 사이란 말일세. 그러니 나는 내…에서 그런 말을 그냥 둘 수가 없네.\""], ["Kenwigs was going to say ‘house,’ but he rounded the sentence with ‘apartments’.", "켄위그스 씨는 \"집\"이라고 말하려다가, \"거처\"라는 말로 문장을 마무리했어."]], [["At the conclusion of these observations, which drew forth evidences of acute feeling from Mrs.", "이 말이 끝나자 켄위그스 부인은 북받치는 감정을 드러냈고, 그 자리의 사람들한테는 수도세 어른의 위엄을 깊이 새기게 하려던 의도가 그대로 먹혀들었어."], ["Kenwigs, and had the intended effect of impressing the company with a deep sense of the collector’s dignity, a ring was heard at the bell.", "그때 초인종 울리는 소리가 들렸지."]], [["‘That’s him,’ whispered Mr.", "\"그분이야.\""], ["Kenwigs, greatly excited.", "켄위그스 씨가 잔뜩 들떠서 속삭였어."], ["‘Morleena, my dear, run down and let your uncle in, and kiss him directly you get the door open.", "\"모를리나, 얘야, 어서 내려가서 삼촌을 들여라. 문 열자마자 바로 입맞춰 드리고."], ["Hem!", "흠!"], ["Let’s be talking.’", "자, 이야기들 좀 합시다.\""]], [["Adopting Mr.", "켄위그스 씨가 시킨 대로, 손님들은 편안하고 거리낌 없어 보이려고 아주 큰 소리로 떠들었어."], ["Kenwigs’s suggestion, the company spoke very loudly, to look easy and unembarrassed; and almost as soon as they had begun to do so, a short old gentleman in drabs and gaiters, with a face that might have been carved out of LIGNUM VITAE, for anything that appeared to the contrary, was led playfully in by Miss Morleena Kenwigs, regarding whose uncommon Christian name it may be here remarked that it had been invented and composed by Mrs.", "그런데 그렇게 떠들기 시작하자마자, 칙칙한 색 옷에 각반을 두른 키 작은 노신사가 모를리나 켄위그스 양의 손에 이끌려 장난스럽게 들어왔어. 그 노신사는 얼굴이 어찌나 무뚝뚝한지, 달리 볼 게 없으면 그냥 유창목을 깎아 만든 거라고 해도 믿을 정도였지."], ["Kenwigs previous to her first lying-in, for the special distinction of her eldest child, in case it should prove a daughter.", "그건 그렇고 모를리나라는 이 희한한 세례명에 대해 한마디 해 두자면, 켄위그스 부인이 첫 출산을 앞두고 직접 지어 만든 이름이었어. 혹시 첫째가 딸이면 그 아이만의 특별한 이름으로 쓰려고 말이야."]], [["‘Oh, uncle, I am SO glad to see you,’ said Mrs.", "\"어머, 삼촌, 정말 너무 반가워요.\""], ["Kenwigs, kissing the collector affectionately on both cheeks.", "켄위그스 부인이 수도세 어른의 양쪽 뺨에 다정하게 입을 맞추며 말했어."], ["‘So glad!’", "\"정말이지 반가워요!\""]], [["‘Many happy returns of the day, my dear,’ replied the collector, returning the compliment.", "\"오늘 같은 좋은 날이 해마다 돌아오길 빈다, 얘야.\" 수도세 어른이 인사에 답하며 말했어."]], [["Now, this was an interesting thing.", "자, 이건 흥미로운 일이었어."], ["Here was a collector of water-rates, without his book, without his pen and ink, without his double knock, without his intimidation, kissing--actually kissing--an agreeable female, and leaving taxes, summonses, notices that he had called, or announcements that he would never call again, for two quarters’ due, wholly out of the question.", "여기 수도세 걷는 사람이, 장부도 없이, 펜과 잉크도 없이, 두 번 쾅쾅 두드리는 노크도 없이, 으름장도 없이, 상냥한 여인에게 입을 맞추고 있었으니까. 정말로 입을 맞추고 있었지."], ["It was pleasant to see how the company looked on, quite absorbed in the sight, and to behold the nods and winks with which they expressed their gratification at finding so much humanity in a tax-gatherer.", "세금이며, 소환장이며, 들렀다 갔다는 통지서며, 두 분기치 밀린 돈 때문에 다시는 안 들르겠다는 고지서 따위는 죄다 까맣게 잊고서 말이야. 손님들이 그 광경에 완전히 빠져들어 구경하는 모습이며, 세금 걷는 사람한테 이렇게 인간미가 넘치는 걸 보고 흡족해하며 주고받는 끄덕임과 눈짓을 보는 건 흐뭇한 일이었어."]], [["‘Where will you sit, uncle?’ said Mrs.", "\"어디 앉으시겠어요, 삼촌?\""], ["Kenwigs, in the full glow of family pride, which the appearance of her distinguished relation occasioned.", "켄위그스 부인이 말했어. 이 대단한 친척이 나타난 덕에 집안에 대한 자부심으로 한껏 달아올라 있었지."]], [["‘Anywheres, my dear,’ said the collector, ‘I am not particular.’", "\"아무 데나 좋다, 얘야.\" 수도세 어른이 말했어. \"나는 가리는 게 없어.\""]], [["Not particular!", "가리는 게 없다니!"], ["What a meek collector!", "어쩌면 이렇게 겸손한 수도세 어른인지!"], ["If he had been an author, who knew his place, he couldn’t have been more humble.", "제 분수를 아는 작가였다 해도 이보다 더 겸손하진 못했을 거야."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"릴리빅 씨,\""], ["Lillyvick,’ said Kenwigs, addressing the collector, ‘some friends here, sir, are very anxious for the honour of--thank you--Mr.", "켄위그스가 수도세 어른을 부르며 말했어."], ["and Mrs Cutler, Mr. Lillyvick.’", "\"여기 친구들 몇이 영광스럽게도 인사를 무척—고맙습니다—이쪽은 커틀러 부부입니다, 릴리빅 씨.\""]], [["‘Proud to know you, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"뵙게 되어 영광입니다, 선생님.\""], ["Cutler; ‘I’ve heerd of you very often.’ These were not mere words of ceremony; for, Mr.", "커틀러 씨가 말했어. \"말씀 자주 들었습니다.\""], ["Cutler, having kept house in Mr.", "이건 그냥 예의상 하는 말이 아니었어."], ["Lillyvick’s parish, had heard of him very often indeed.", "커틀러 씨가 릴리빅 씨가 맡은 교구에서 살림을 차린 적이 있어서, 정말이지 그분 이야기를 아주 자주 들었거든."], ["His attention in calling had been quite extraordinary.", "들렀다 가시는 그 정성이 어찌나 대단했던지."]], [["‘George, you know, I think, Mr. Lillyvick,’ said Kenwigs; ‘lady from downstairs--Mr.", "\"조지는 아실 거예요, 릴리빅 씨.\""], ["Lillyvick. Mr.", "켄위그스가 말했어."], ["Snewkes--Mr. Lillyvick.", "\"아래층 부인이세요—릴리빅 씨."], ["Miss Green--Mr Lillyvick. Mr.", "스뉴크스 씨—릴리빅 씨."], ["Lillyvick--Miss Petowker of the Theatre Royal, Drury Lane.", "그린 양—릴리빅 씨. 릴리빅 씨—드루리 레인 왕립 극장의 페토커 양입니다."], ["Very glad to make two public characters acquainted!", "공직에 계신 두 분이 인사를 나누시게 되어 정말 기쁩니다!"], ["Mrs. Kenwigs, my dear, will you sort the counters?’", "켄위그스 부인, 여보, 패를 좀 나눠 줄 텐가?\""]], [["Mrs.", "켄위그스 부인은 뉴먼 노그스의 도움을 받아 시키는 대로 했어."], ["Kenwigs, with the assistance of Newman Noggs, (who, as he performed sundry little acts of kindness for the children, at all times and seasons, was humoured in his request to be taken no notice of, and was merely spoken about, in a whisper, as the decayed gentleman), did as he was desired; and the greater part of the guests sat down to speculation, while Newman himself, Mrs.", "뉴먼은 늘 때를 가리지 않고 아이들한테 자질구레한 친절을 베풀어 온 터라, 자기를 신경 쓰지 말아 달라는 부탁이 받아들여졌지. 그래서 그저 \"한물간 신사\"라고 귓속말로나 입에 오를 뿐이었어."], ["Kenwigs, and Miss Petowker of the Theatre Royal Drury Lane, looked after the supper-table.", "손님들 대부분은 자리에 앉아 카드놀이를 했고, 뉴먼 본인과 켄위그스 부인, 그리고 드루리 레인 왕립 극장의 페토커 양은 저녁 식탁을 챙겼어."]], [["While the ladies were thus busying themselves, Mr.", "부인들이 이렇게 부산을 떠는 동안, 릴리빅 씨는 한창 벌어지는 놀이에 푹 빠져 있었어. 수도세 걷는 사람의 그물에 걸리는 건 죄다 제 고기인 법이니, 이 사랑스러운 노신사는 남의 패를 제 것으로 챙기는 데 조금도 거리낌이 없었지."], ["Lillyvick was intent upon the game in progress, and as all should be fish that comes to a water-collector’s net, the dear old gentleman was by no means scrupulous in appropriating to himself the property of his neighbours, which, on the contrary, he abstracted whenever an opportunity presented itself, smiling good-humouredly all the while, and making so many condescending speeches to the owners, that they were delighted with his amiability, and thought in their hearts that he deserved to be Chancellor of the Exchequer at least.", "오히려 기회만 났다 하면 슬쩍슬쩍 가로채면서, 그러는 내내 사람 좋게 싱글벙글 웃었어. 게다가 그 임자들한테 너그러운 말을 어찌나 많이 건네던지, 사람들은 그 다정함에 흠뻑 빠져서 속으로 이 양반은 적어도 재무부 장관쯤은 될 만하다고 여겼지."]], [["After a great deal of trouble, and the administration of many slaps on the head to the infant Kenwigses, whereof two of the most rebellious were summarily banished, the cloth was laid with much elegance, and a pair of boiled fowls, a large piece of pork, apple-pie, potatoes and greens, were served; at sight of which, the worthy Mr.", "한참 애를 쓰고, 켄위그스네 어린것들 머리통을 여러 번 찰싹찰싹 때린 끝에—그 가운데 제일 말 안 듣는 둘은 즉결로 쫓겨났지—식탁보가 아주 우아하게 깔렸어. 그리고 삶은 닭 두 마리에 큼지막한 돼지고기 한 덩이, 사과 파이, 감자, 푸성귀가 차려졌지."], ["Lillyvick vented a great many witticisms, and plucked up amazingly: to the immense delight and satisfaction of the whole body of admirers.", "그 광경을 보고 훌륭하신 릴리빅 씨는 재담을 잔뜩 쏟아 내며 놀랄 만큼 기운을 차렸어. 따르는 무리 전체의 한없는 기쁨과 만족을 자아내면서 말이야."]], [["Very well and very fast the supper went off; no more serious difficulties occurring, than those which arose from the incessant demand for clean knives and forks; which made poor Mrs. Kenwigs wish, more than once, that private society adopted the principle of schools, and required that every guest should bring his own knife, fork, and spoon; which doubtless would be a great accommodation in many cases, and to no one more so than to the lady and gentleman of the house, especially if the school principle were carried out to the full extent, and the articles were expected, as a matter of delicacy, not to be taken away again.", "저녁 식사는 아주 순조롭고 빠르게 흘러갔어. 깨끗한 나이프와 포크를 끊임없이 달라는 통에 생기는 일 말고는 더 심각한 어려움은 없었지. 그 바람에 가엾은 켄위그스 부인은 한 번 이상 이런 생각을 했어. 사적인 모임도 학교 방식을 따라서, 손님마다 자기 나이프와 포크, 숟가락을 챙겨 오게 했으면 좋겠다고 말이야. 그렇게 하면 분명 여러 경우에 큰 도움이 될 텐데, 누구보다도 집주인 부부한테 그럴 거야. 특히 그 학교 방식을 끝까지 밀어붙여서, 예의상 그 물건들을 도로 가져가지 않는 게 당연하다고 친다면 더더욱 그렇겠지."]], [["Everybody having eaten everything, the table was cleared in a most alarming hurry, and with great noise; and the spirits, whereat the eyes of Newman Noggs glistened, being arranged in order, with water both hot and cold, the party composed themselves for conviviality; Mr.", "다들 차려진 음식을 죄다 먹어 치우자, 식탁은 무시무시할 만큼 허둥지둥, 또 요란한 소리를 내며 치워졌어. 그리고 뉴먼 노그스의 눈이 반짝거리게 만든 술이, 뜨거운 물과 찬물과 함께 가지런히 차려졌지."], ["Lillyvick being stationed in a large armchair by the fireside, and the four little Kenwigses disposed on a small form in front of the company with their flaxen tails towards them, and their faces to the fire; an arrangement which was no sooner perfected, than Mrs.", "손님들은 흥겹게 놀 채비를 했어. 릴리빅 씨는 난롯가의 커다란 안락의자에 자리를 잡았고, 켄위그스네 어린 넷은 손님들 앞 작은 긴 의자에 아마빛 댕기를 사람들 쪽으로 늘어뜨리고 얼굴은 불을 향한 채 앉았어."], ["Kenwigs was overpowered by the feelings of a mother, and fell upon the left shoulder of Mr. Kenwigs dissolved in tears.", "이렇게 자리가 다 잡히자마자, 켄위그스 부인은 어머니로서의 감정에 북받쳐 켄위그스 씨의 왼쪽 어깨에 쓰러지며 눈물을 펑펑 쏟았지."]], [["‘They are so beautiful!’ said Mrs.", "\"애들이 너무 예뻐요!\""], ["Kenwigs, sobbing.", "켄위그스 부인이 흐느끼며 말했어."]], [["‘Oh, dear,’ said all the ladies, ‘so they are! it’s very natural you should feel proud of that; but don’t give way, don’t.’", "\"어머나,\" 부인들이 다 같이 말했어. \"정말 그래요! 자랑스러우신 게 당연하지요. 하지만 너무 마음 쓰지 마세요, 진정하세요.\""]], [["‘I can--not help it, and it don’t signify,’ sobbed Mrs.", "\"어쩔 수가 없어요, 그래도 아무 소용없지만.\" 켄위그스 부인이 흐느꼈어."], ["Kenwigs; ‘oh!", "\"아!"], ["they’re too beautiful to live, much too beautiful!’", "살기엔 너무 예뻐요, 너무 예쁘다고요!\""]], [["On hearing this alarming presentiment of their being doomed to an early death in the flower of their infancy, all four little girls raised a hideous cry, and burying their heads in their mother’s lap simultaneously, screamed until the eight flaxen tails vibrated again; Mrs.", "이 아이들이 어린 꽃 같은 시절에 일찍 죽을 운명이라는 무시무시한 예감을 듣자, 어린 딸 넷이 일제히 끔찍한 울음을 터뜨렸어. 그러고는 동시에 어머니 무릎에 머리를 파묻고, 아마빛 댕기 여덟 가닥이 다시 부르르 떨릴 때까지 빽빽 울어 댔지."], ["Kenwigs meanwhile clasping them alternately to her bosom, with attitudes expressive of distraction, which Miss Petowker herself might have copied.", "그동안 켄위그스 부인은 아이들을 번갈아 가슴에 끌어안았어. 페토커 양마저도 그대로 본떴을 법한, 넋이 나간 듯한 몸짓으로 말이야."]], [["At length, the anxious mother permitted herself to be soothed into a more tranquil state, and the little Kenwigses, being also composed, were distributed among the company, to prevent the possibility of Mrs.", "마침내 애가 탄 어머니는 달램을 받아 좀 더 차분한 상태가 됐어. 켄위그스네 어린것들도 진정되자, 켄위그스 부인이 다시 그 한데 모인 미모의 광채에 압도되는 일이 없도록, 아이들을 손님들 사이로 나눠 앉혔지."], ["Kenwigs being again overcome by the blaze of their combined beauty.", "그러고 나자 신사 숙녀들이 입을 모아 예언했어."], ["This done, the ladies and gentlemen united in prophesying that they would live for many, many years, and that there was no occasion at all for Mrs.", "이 아이들은 오래오래, 아주 오래 살 거라고, 켄위그스 부인이 마음 졸일 까닭이 조금도 없다고 말이야."], ["Kenwigs to distress herself; which, in good truth, there did not appear to be; the loveliness of the children by no means justifying her apprehensions.", "사실 정말로 그럴 까닭은 없어 보였어. 아이들이 예쁘다고 해서 그 걱정이 들어맞을 리는 도무지 없었으니까."]], [["‘This day eight year,’ said Mr.", "\"이날이 팔 년 전이로군.\""], ["Kenwigs after a pause.", "켄위그스 씨가 잠시 말이 없다가 입을 열었어."], ["‘Dear me--ah!’", "\"이런—아!\""]], [["This reflection was echoed by all present, who said ‘Ah!’ first, and ‘dear me,’ afterwards.", "이 회상에 그 자리 사람들이 모두 맞장구를 쳤어. 먼저 \"아!\" 하더니, 뒤이어 \"이런\" 하고들 말했지."]], [["‘I was younger then,’ tittered Mrs.", "\"그때 난 더 젊었지요.\""], ["Kenwigs.", "켄위그스 부인이 까르르 웃으며 말했어."]], [["‘No,’ said the collector.", "\"아닐세.\" 수도세 어른이 말했어."]], [["‘Certainly not,’ added everybody.", "\"그럼요, 절대 아니지요.\" 모두가 거들었어."]], [["‘I remember my niece,’ said Mr.", "\"내 조카가 기억나는군.\""], ["Lillyvick, surveying his audience with a grave air; ‘I remember her, on that very afternoon, when she first acknowledged to her mother a partiality for Kenwigs.", "릴리빅 씨가 청중을 진중한 표정으로 둘러보며 말했어. \"기억나고말고, 바로 그날 오후에 그 애가 처음으로 제 어미한테 켄위그스가 좋다고 털어놓던 게 말이야."], ["“Mother,”", "'어머니,'"], ["she says, “I love him.”’", "그 애가 그러더군. '저 그이를 사랑해요.'\""]], [["‘“Adore him,” I said, uncle,’ interposed Mrs.", "\"'그이를 흠모해요'라고 했어요, 삼촌.\""], ["Kenwigs.", "켄위그스 부인이 끼어들었어."]], [["‘“Love him,”", "\"'사랑해요'였던 것 같은데, 얘야.\""], ["I think, my dear,’ said the collector, firmly.", "수도세 어른이 단호하게 말했어."]], [["‘Perhaps you are right, uncle,’ replied Mrs.", "\"삼촌 말씀이 맞을지도 모르겠네요.\""], ["Kenwigs, submissively.", "켄위그스 부인이 고분고분 대답했어."], ["‘I thought it was “adore.”’", "\"전 '흠모해요'였던 것 같았는데.\""]], [["‘“Love,” my dear,’ retorted Mr.", "\"'사랑해요'였다, 얘야.\""], ["Lillyvick.", "릴리빅 씨가 받아쳤어."], ["‘“Mother,”", "\"'어머니,'"], ["she says, “I love him!” “What do I hear?”", "그 애가 그러더군, '저 그이를 사랑해요!'"], ["cries her mother; and instantly falls into strong conwulsions.’", "'내가 지금 무슨 소리를 듣는 거냐?' 제 어미가 소리치더니, 그 자리에서 심한 경련을 일으키고 말았지.\""]], [["A general exclamation of astonishment burst from the company.", "그 자리 사람들이 일제히 놀라움의 탄성을 터뜨렸어."]], [["‘Into strong conwulsions,’ repeated Mr.", "\"심한 경련을 말일세.\""], ["Lillyvick, regarding them with a rigid look.", "릴리빅 씨가 사람들을 굳은 눈길로 바라보며 되풀이했어."], ["‘Kenwigs will excuse my saying, in the presence of friends, that there was a very great objection to him, on the ground that he was beneath the family, and would disgrace it.", "\"친구들 앞에서 이런 말을 해도 켄위그스가 이해해 주겠지만, 그땐 그 사람을 두고 아주 큰 반대가 있었네. 우리 집안에 비해 격이 떨어진다, 집안 망신이다, 그런 이유로 말이야."], ["You remember, Kenwigs?’", "기억하나, 켄위그스?\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ replied that gentleman, in no way displeased at the reminiscence, inasmuch as it proved, beyond all doubt, what a high family Mrs.", "\"물론이지요.\" 켄위그스 씨가 대답했어."], ["Kenwigs came of.", "이 회상이 켄위그스 부인이 얼마나 대단한 집안 출신인지를 더없이 분명하게 증명해 주는 거라, 조금도 언짢아하지 않으면서 말이야."]], [["‘I shared in that feeling,’ said Mr.", "\"나도 그 마음에 같이했지.\""], ["Lillyvick: ‘perhaps it was natural; perhaps it wasn’t.’", "릴리빅 씨가 말했어. \"그게 당연했을 수도 있고, 아닐 수도 있고.\""]], [["A gentle murmur seemed to say, that, in one of Mr. Lillyvick’s station, the objection was not only natural, but highly praiseworthy.", "부드러운 웅성거림이, 릴리빅 씨 정도 되는 지위에서는 그런 반대가 당연할 뿐 아니라 크게 칭찬받을 만한 일이었다고 말하는 듯했어."]], [["‘I came round to him in time,’ said Mr.", "\"나도 때가 되니 그 사람을 받아들였네.\""], ["Lillyvick.", "릴리빅 씨가 말했어."], ["‘After they were married, and there was no help for it, I was one of the first to say that Kenwigs must be taken notice of.", "\"둘이 결혼하고 나서, 이제 어쩔 도리가 없게 되니, 켄위그스를 챙겨 줘야 한다고 제일 먼저 나선 사람 가운데 하나가 나였지. 그래서 집안에서도 정말로 그 사람을 챙겨 주었네."], ["The family DID take notice of him, in consequence, and on my representation; and I am bound to say--and proud to say--that I have always found him a very honest, well-behaved, upright, respectable sort of man.", "내가 그렇게 말했으니까 말이야. 그리고 나는 꼭 말해야겠고—말하게 되어 자랑스럽고—그동안 나는 늘 그 사람이 아주 정직하고, 행실 바르고, 올곧고, 점잖은 사람이라는 걸 알게 되었네."], ["Kenwigs, shake hands.’", "켄위그스, 악수하세.\""]], [["‘I am proud to do it, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"영광입니다, 선생님.\""], ["Kenwigs.", "켄위그스 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘So am I, Kenwigs,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"나도 그렇네, 켄위그스.\""], ["Lillyvick.", "릴리빅 씨가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘A very happy life I have led with your niece, sir,’ said Kenwigs.", "\"댁의 조카와 아주 행복하게 살아왔습니다, 선생님.\" 켄위그스가 말했어."]], [["‘It would have been your own fault if you had not, sir,’ remarked Mr Lillyvick.", "\"안 그랬다면 그게 다 자네 잘못이었겠지, 선생.\" 릴리빅 씨가 한마디 했어."]], [["‘Morleena Kenwigs,’ cried her mother, at this crisis, much affected, ‘kiss your dear uncle!’", "\"모를리나 켄위그스,\" 그 어미가 이 대목에서 한껏 감격해 소리쳤어. \"사랑하는 삼촌께 입맞춰 드려라!\""]], [["The young lady did as she was requested, and the three other little girls were successively hoisted up to the collector’s countenance, and subjected to the same process, which was afterwards repeated on them by the majority of those present.", "이 아가씨는 시키는 대로 했고, 다른 어린 딸 셋도 차례차례 수도세 어른의 얼굴까지 들어 올려져 똑같은 절차를 치렀어. 그러고 나서는 그 자리에 있던 사람들 대부분이 또 그 아이들한테 그걸 되풀이했지."]], [["‘Oh dear, Mrs.", "\"어머나, 켄위그스 부인,\""], ["Kenwigs,’ said Miss Petowker, ‘while Mr.", "페토커 양이 말했어."], ["Noggs is making that punch to drink happy returns in, do let Morleena go through that figure dance before Mr. Lillyvick.’", "\"노그스 씨가 그 행복한 날을 기리며 마실 펀치를 만드는 동안, 모를리나한테 릴리빅 씨 앞에서 그 무용을 한번 추게 해 보세요.\""]], [["‘No, no, my dear,’ replied Mrs.", "\"아녜요, 안 돼요, 얘.\""], ["Kenwigs, ‘it will only worry my uncle.’", "켄위그스 부인이 대답했어. \"삼촌만 성가시게 할 뿐이에요.\""]], [["‘It can’t worry him, I am sure,’ said Miss Petowker.", "\"성가실 리가 없잖아요, 분명히.\" 페토커 양이 말했어."], ["‘You will be very much pleased, won’t you, sir?’", "\"아주 흐뭇해하실 거예요, 안 그러세요, 선생님?\""]], [["‘That I am sure I shall’ replied the collector, glancing at the punch-mixer.", "\"암, 그러고말고.\" 수도세 어른이 펀치 만드는 사람 쪽을 흘끗 보며 대답했어."]], [["‘Well then, I’ll tell you what,’ said Mrs.", "\"그럼 이렇게 하지요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했어."], ["Kenwigs, ‘Morleena shall do the steps, if uncle can persuade Miss Petowker to recite us the Blood-Drinker’s Burial, afterwards.’", "\"모를리나가 춤을 출 테니, 대신 삼촌이 페토커 양을 설득해서 그다음에 '피를 마시는 자의 장례'를 낭송하게 해 주세요.\""]], [["There was a great clapping of hands and stamping of feet, at this proposition; the subject whereof, gently inclined her head several times, in acknowledgment of the reception.", "이 제안에 손뼉을 치고 발을 구르는 소리가 요란하게 일었어. 그 주인공인 페토커 양은 환대에 답하느라 고개를 여러 번 살포시 숙였지."]], [["‘You know,’ said Miss Petowker, reproachfully, ‘that I dislike doing anything professional in private parties.’", "\"아시잖아요,\" 페토커 양이 나무라듯 말했어. \"저는 사적인 모임에서 무대 일 비슷한 걸 하는 걸 싫어한다는 거.\""]], [["‘Oh, but not here!’ said Mrs.", "\"어머, 여기선 괜찮아요!\""], ["Kenwigs.", "켄위그스 부인이 말했어."], ["‘We are all so very friendly and pleasant, that you might as well be going through it in your own room; besides, the occasion--’", "\"우리 다들 어찌나 정답고 즐거운지, 그냥 댁의 방에서 하는 거나 마찬가지일 거예요. 게다가 이런 날이니—\""]], [["‘I can’t resist that,’ interrupted Miss Petowker; ‘anything in my humble power I shall be delighted to do.’", "\"그럼 거절할 수가 없네요.\" 페토커 양이 끼어들었어. \"제 보잘것없는 재주로 할 수 있는 거라면 뭐든 기꺼이 하지요.\""]], [["Mrs.", "켄위그스 부인과 페토커 양은 둘이서 작은 공연 순서를 미리 짜 두었는데, 이게 바로 정해진 차례였어."], ["Kenwigs and Miss Petowker had arranged a small PROGRAMME of the entertainments between them, of which this was the prescribed order, but they had settled to have a little pressing on both sides, because it looked more natural.", "그래도 양쪽에서 조금씩 권하는 척하기로 미리 해 둔 거였지. 그래야 더 자연스러워 보이거든."], ["The company being all ready, Miss Petowker hummed a tune, and Morleena danced a dance; having previously had the soles of her shoes chalked, with as much care as if she were going on the tight-rope.", "손님들이 다 채비를 마치자, 페토커 양이 가락을 흥얼거렸고 모를리나가 춤을 추었어. 미리 신발 바닥에 분필 가루를 묻혀 두었는데, 마치 줄타기라도 하러 나가는 양 꼼꼼하게 발랐지."], ["It was a very beautiful figure, comprising a great deal of work for the arms, and was received with unbounded applause.", "아주 아름다운 춤이었고, 팔 동작이 상당히 많았어. 그리고 끝없는 박수갈채를 받았지."]], [["‘If I was blessed with a--a child--’ said Miss Petowker, blushing, ‘of such genius as that, I would have her out at the Opera instantly.’", "\"제게 만약 저런 재능을 타고난—아—아이가 있었다면,\" 페토커 양이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했어. \"당장 오페라 무대에 올렸을 거예요.\""]], [["Mrs. Kenwigs sighed, and looked at Mr.", "켄위그스 부인이 한숨을 쉬며 켄위그스 씨를 바라봤어."], ["Kenwigs, who shook his head, and observed that he was doubtful about it.", "그러자 켄위그스 씨는 고개를 저으며, 그건 좀 어떨지 모르겠다고 했지."]], [["‘Kenwigs is afraid,’ said Mrs.", "\"켄위그스가 걱정하는 거예요.\""], ["K.", "켄위그스 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘What of?’ inquired Miss Petowker, ‘not of her failing?’", "\"뭘 걱정하는데요?\" 페토커 양이 물었어. \"설마 실패할까 봐서는 아니죠?\""]], [["‘Oh no,’ replied Mrs.", "\"아, 그건 아녜요.\""], ["Kenwigs, ‘but if she grew up what she is now,--only think of the young dukes and marquises.’", "켄위그스 부인이 대답했어. \"하지만 이 애가 지금 모습 그대로 자라난다면—젊은 공작이며 후작 나리들을 한번 생각해 봐요.\""]], [["‘Very right,’ said the collector.", "\"맞는 말이오.\" 수도세 어른이 말했어."]], [["‘Still,’ submitted Miss Petowker, ‘if she took a proper pride in herself, you know--’", "\"그래도,\" 페토커 양이 조심스레 말했어. \"이 애가 스스로를 제대로 소중히 여길 줄만 안다면, 그러니까—\""]], [["‘There’s a good deal in that,’ observed Mrs.", "\"그 말에도 일리가 많네요.\""], ["Kenwigs, looking at her husband.", "켄위그스 부인이 남편을 보며 말했어."]], [["‘I only know--’ faltered Miss Petowker,--‘it may be no rule to be sure--but I have never found any inconvenience or unpleasantness of that sort.’", "\"저는 다만—\" 페토커 양이 머뭇거렸어. \"물론 다 그런 건 아닐 수도 있지만—저는 그런 종류의 불편한 일이나 거북한 일은 한 번도 겪어 본 적이 없거든요.\""]], [["Mr.", "켄위그스 씨는 신사다운 정중함으로, 그 말로 이 문제는 단번에 정리됐다고, 이 일을 진지하게 고려해 보겠다고 했어. 이렇게 결정이 나자, 사람들은 페토커 양한테 '피를 마시는 자의 장례'를 시작해 달라고 청했지."], ["Kenwigs, with becoming gallantry, said that settled the question at once, and that he would take the subject into his serious consideration.", "그러자 이 아가씨는 뒷머리를 풀어 내리고, 방 저쪽 끝에 자리를 잡았어. 총각 친구는 한쪽 구석에 서 있다가 \"죽음 속에 숨을 거두니\"라는 신호에 뛰쳐나가, 페토커 양이 미쳐 날뛰며 죽을 때 두 팔로 받아 안기로 했지."], ["This being resolved upon, Miss Petowker was entreated to begin the Blood-Drinker’s Burial; to which end, that young lady let down her back hair, and taking up her position at the other end of the room, with the bachelor friend posted in a corner, to rush out at the cue ‘in death expire,’ and catch her in his arms when she died raving mad, went through the performance with extraordinary spirit, and to the great terror of the little Kenwigses, who were all but frightened into fits.", "페토커 양은 놀라운 기백으로 그 공연을 해냈어. 켄위그스네 어린것들이 거의 기겁할 만큼 무서워서 발작을 일으킬 뻔할 정도로 말이야."]], [["The ecstasies consequent upon the effort had not yet subsided, and Newman (who had not been thoroughly sober at so late an hour for a long long time,) had not yet been able to put in a word of announcement, that the punch was ready, when a hasty knock was heard at the room-door, which elicited a shriek from Mrs.", "이 공연이 자아낸 황홀경은 아직 가라앉지 않았어. 그리고 뉴먼은—이렇게 늦은 시각에 이만큼 멀쩡하게 깬 적이 정말이지 오래오래간만이었는데—펀치가 다 됐다는 말을 미처 한마디도 끼워 넣지 못한 참이었지."], ["Kenwigs, who immediately divined that the baby had fallen out of bed.", "그때 방문을 다급하게 두드리는 소리가 들렸고, 켄위그스 부인이 비명을 질렀어. 아기가 침대에서 떨어졌다고 대번에 직감했거든."]], [["‘Who is that?’ demanded Mr.", "\"누구요?\""], ["Kenwigs, sharply.", "켄위그스 씨가 날카롭게 물었어."]], [["‘Don’t be alarmed, it’s only me,’ said Crowl, looking in, in his nightcap.", "\"놀라지 마쇼, 나요.\" 크롤이 잠자리 모자를 쓴 채 안을 들여다보며 말했어."], ["‘The baby is very comfortable, for I peeped into the room as I came down, and it’s fast asleep, and so is the girl; and I don’t think the candle will set fire to the bed-curtain, unless a draught was to get into the room--it’s Mr.", "\"아기는 아주 잘 있어요. 내려오다가 방을 살짝 들여다봤는데, 곤히 자고 있고 그 여자아이도 자고 있더군."], ["Noggs that’s wanted.’", "그리고 방에 바람만 안 들어오면 촛불이 침대 커튼에 옮겨붙을 일도 없을 거요—노그스 씨를 찾는 거요.\""]], [["‘Me!’ cried Newman, much astonished.", "\"나를요!\" 뉴먼이 깜짝 놀라 소리쳤어."]], [["‘Why, it IS a queer hour, isn’t it?’ replied Crowl, who was not best pleased at the prospect of losing his fire; ‘and they are queer-looking people, too, all covered with rain and mud.", "\"이거, 좀 이상한 시각이지요, 안 그래요?\" 자기 불을 잃을 처지가 영 마뜩잖은 크롤이 대꾸했어."], ["Shall I tell them to go away?’", "\"게다가 사람들 행색도 이상하더군요, 죄다 비랑 진흙투성이라. 가라고 할까요?\""]], [["‘No,’ said Newman, rising.", "\"아니요.\" 뉴먼이 일어서며 말했어."], ["‘People?", "\"사람들이라고요?"], ["How many?’", "몇이나?\""]], [["‘Two,’ rejoined Crowl.", "\"둘이요.\" 크롤이 대답했어."]], [["‘Want me?", "\"나를 찾는다고요?"], ["By name?’ asked Newman.", "이름까지 대고?\" 뉴먼이 물었어."]], [["‘By name,’ replied Crowl.", "\"이름까지 대고요.\" 크롤이 대답했어."], ["‘Mr. Newman Noggs, as pat as need be.’", "\"뉴먼 노그스 씨라고, 아주 또박또박.\""]], [["Newman reflected for a few seconds, and then hurried away, muttering that he would be back directly.", "뉴먼은 몇 초쯤 생각에 잠겼다가, 곧 돌아오겠다고 중얼거리며 서둘러 나갔어. 그러고는 정말 말한 대로였지."], ["He was as good as his word; for, in an exceedingly short time, he burst into the room, and seizing, without a word of apology or explanation, a lighted candle and tumbler of hot punch from the table, darted away like a madman.", "눈 깜짝할 새에 다시 방으로 뛰어들더니, 미안하다거나 무슨 일이라거나 한마디 말도 없이 식탁에서 불 켜진 양초 하나와 뜨거운 펀치 한 잔을 낚아채고는, 미친 사람처럼 쏜살같이 사라져 버렸어."]], [["‘What the deuce is the matter with him?’ exclaimed Crowl, throwing the door open.", "\"대체 저 사람한테 무슨 일이 생긴 거야?\" 크롤이 문을 활짝 열어젖히며 소리쳤어."], ["‘Hark!", "\"쉿!"], ["Is there any noise above?’", "위에서 무슨 소리 안 나요?\""]], [["The guests rose in great confusion, and, looking in each other’s faces with much perplexity and some fear, stretched their necks forward, and listened attentively.", "손님들은 잔뜩 어수선해져 일어났어. 그러고는 어쩔 줄 모르겠다는, 또 조금은 겁에 질린 얼굴로 서로를 마주 보다가, 목을 앞으로 쭉 빼고 귀를 기울였지."]], [["# CHAPTER 15", "# CHAPTER 15"]], [["Acquaints the Reader with the Cause and Origin of the Interruption described in the last Chapter, and with some other Matters necessary to be known", "지난 장에서 그려진 그 소동이 어떻게, 왜 일어났는지 독자에게 알려 주고, 더불어 꼭 알아야 할 몇 가지 다른 일들도 들려준다"]], [["Newman Noggs scrambled in violent haste upstairs with the steaming beverage, which he had so unceremoniously snatched from the table of Mr Kenwigs, and indeed from the very grasp of the water-rate collector, who was eyeing the contents of the tumbler, at the moment of its unexpected abstraction, with lively marks of pleasure visible in his countenance. He bore his prize straight to his own back-garret, where, footsore and nearly shoeless, wet, dirty, jaded, and disfigured with every mark of fatiguing travel, sat Nicholas and Smike, at once the cause and partner of his toil; both perfectly worn out by their unwonted and protracted exertion.", "뉴먼 노그스는 김이 모락모락 나는 그 음료를 들고 무섭게 서둘러 위층으로 허둥지둥 올라갔다. 그건 케이트… 아니, 케닉스 씨네 식탁에서, 그것도 수도세 징수원의 바로 손아귀에서 인사도 없이 낚아챈 것이었다. 음료를 빼앗긴 그 순간, 징수원은 잔 속의 내용물을 흐뭇한 표정 가득한 얼굴로 들여다보고 있던 참이었다. 뉴먼은 그 전리품을 곧장 자기 뒤편 다락방으로 가져갔다. 거기에는 니콜라스와 스마이크가 앉아 있었다. 그의 수고의 원인이자 동행자였던 둘은 발이 부르트고 신발도 다 떨어져 가는 데다, 비에 젖고 더럽고 지친 모습이었다. 고된 여행의 온갖 흔적을 다 뒤집어쓰고 있었지. 익숙지 않은 데다 오래 이어진 강행군에 두 사람 다 녹초가 되어 있었다."]], [["Newman’s first act was to compel Nicholas, with gentle force, to swallow half of the punch at a breath, nearly boiling as it was; and his next, to pour the remainder down the throat of Smike, who, never having tasted anything stronger than aperient medicine in his whole life, exhibited various odd manifestations of surprise and delight, during the passage of the liquor down his throat, and turned up his eyes most emphatically when it was all gone.", "뉴먼이 맨 먼저 한 일은, 거의 끓을 듯이 뜨거운 펀치의 절반을 니콜라스에게 부드럽게 떠밀어 단숨에 삼키게 한 것이었다. 그다음에는 나머지를 스마이크의 목구멍에 들이부었다. 스마이크는 평생 변비약보다 독한 건 입에 대 본 적이 없는 터라, 술이 목을 타고 넘어가는 동안 놀라움과 즐거움이 뒤섞인 갖가지 묘한 반응을 보였다. 그리고 다 넘어가고 나자 두 눈을 한껏 위로 치켜떴다."]], [["‘You are wet through,’ said Newman, passing his hand hastily over the coat which Nicholas had thrown off; ‘and I--I--haven’t even a change,’ he added, with a wistful glance at the shabby clothes he wore himself.", "\"흠뻑 젖었구나.\" 뉴먼이 니콜라스가 벗어 던진 외투에 황급히 손을 대 보며 말했다. \"그런데 나는… 나는… 갈아입을 옷도 없어.\" 그가 자기가 입고 있는 초라한 옷을 안타까운 눈길로 내려다보며 덧붙였다."]], [["‘I have dry clothes, or at least such as will serve my turn well, in my bundle,’ replied Nicholas. ‘If you look so distressed to see me, you will add to the pain I feel already, at being compelled, for one night, to cast myself upon your slender means for aid and shelter.’", "\"마른 옷이 있어요. 적어도 당장 쓸 만한 건 제 보따리에 있거든요.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했다. \"저를 보고 그렇게 안타까워하시면, 가뜩이나 마음 아픈 제가 더 괴로워져요. 단 하룻밤이라도 아저씨의 빠듯한 형편에 도움과 잠자리를 기댈 수밖에 없는 게 말이에요.\""]], [["Newman did not look the less distressed to hear Nicholas talking in this strain; but, upon his young friend grasping him heartily by the hand, and assuring him that nothing but implicit confidence in the sincerity of his professions, and kindness of feeling towards himself, would have induced him, on any consideration, even to have made him acquainted with his arrival in London, Mr.", "니콜라스가 이런 식으로 말하니 뉴먼은 조금도 덜 안타까워 보이지 않았다. 하지만 젊은 친구가 그의 손을 진심으로 꼭 잡고는, 그가 진심으로 자기를 위하는 마음과 그 따뜻한 정을 굳게 믿었기에, 무슨 일이 있어도 자기가 런던에 왔다는 걸 알린 거라고 단단히 못을 박자, 노그스 씨는 다시 얼굴이 환해졌다."], ["Noggs brightened up again, and went about making such arrangements as were in his power for the comfort of his visitors, with extreme alacrity.", "그러고는 손님들이 편히 지낼 수 있도록 자기 힘 닿는 데까지 부지런히 채비를 갖추기 시작했다."]], [["These were simple enough; poor Newman’s means halting at a very considerable distance short of his inclinations; but, slight as they were, they were not made without much bustling and running about. As Nicholas had husbanded his scanty stock of money, so well that it was not yet quite expended, a supper of bread and cheese, with some cold beef from the cook’s shop, was soon placed upon the table; and these viands being flanked by a bottle of spirits and a pot of porter, there was no ground for apprehension on the score of hunger or thirst, at all events. Such preparations as Newman had it in his power to make, for the accommodation of his guests during the night, occupied no very great time in completing; and as he had insisted, as an express preliminary, that Nicholas should change his clothes, and that Smike should invest himself in his solitary coat (which no entreaties would dissuade him from stripping off for the purpose), the travellers partook of their frugal fare, with more satisfaction than one of them at least had derived from many a better meal.", "채비라 해 봐야 아주 단출했다. 가엾은 뉴먼의 형편은 그 마음에 한참 못 미쳤으니까. 하지만 보잘것없는 채비였어도 부산하게 이리저리 뛰어다니지 않고는 마련할 수 없었다. 니콜라스가 얼마 안 되는 돈을 워낙 알뜰하게 아껴 둔 덕에 아직 다 떨어지지는 않았다. 그래서 빵과 치즈에다 음식점에서 사 온 찬 쇠고기까지 곁들인 저녁상이 금세 차려졌다. 거기에 독한 술 한 병과 흑맥주 한 단지까지 더해지니, 적어도 배고픔이나 목마름을 걱정할 일은 없었다. 손님들이 밤을 지낼 수 있도록 뉴먼이 마련할 수 있는 채비를 끝내는 데도 그리 오랜 시간이 걸리지는 않았다. 그리고 그는 먼저 한 가지를 못 박아 두었는데, 니콜라스는 옷을 갈아입고 스마이크는 자기 외투 하나를 걸치라는 것이었다(아무리 말려도 그는 그 외투를 벗어 주려고 기어이 벗어 버렸다). 그렇게 해서 나그네들은 그 소박한 음식을 나누어 먹었다. 적어도 둘 중 하나는 그동안 먹은 어떤 진수성찬보다도 더 만족스러워했다."]], [["They then drew near the fire, which Newman Noggs had made up as well as he could, after the inroads of Crowl upon the fuel; and Nicholas, who had hitherto been restrained by the extreme anxiety of his friend that he should refresh himself after his journey, now pressed him with earnest questions concerning his mother and sister.", "이윽고 그들은 난롯가로 다가앉았다. 크롤이 땔감을 축내고 간 뒤에 뉴먼 노그스가 그나마 할 수 있는 만큼 불을 지펴 놓은 참이었다. 니콜라스는 그때까지 여행에 지친 몸을 먼저 추스르라는 친구의 극진한 걱정에 가로막혀 있었지만, 이제는 어머니와 누이동생에 대해 간절히 물어 대기 시작했다."]], [["‘Well,’ replied Newman, with his accustomed taciturnity; ‘both well.’", "\"음.\" 뉴먼이 늘 그러듯 말수 적게 대꾸했다. \"둘 다 잘 있어.\""]], [["‘They are living in the city still?’ inquired Nicholas.", "\"아직 시내에 살고 계시죠?\" 니콜라스가 물었다."]], [["‘They are,’ said Newman.", "\"그래.\" 뉴먼이 말했다."]], [["‘And my sister,’--added Nicholas.", "\"그리고 제 누이동생은요.\""], ["‘Is she still engaged in the business which she wrote to tell me she thought she should like so much?’", "니콜라스가 덧붙였다. \"편지로, 정말 마음에 들 것 같다던 그 일을 아직 하고 있나요?\""]], [["Newman opened his eyes rather wider than usual, but merely replied by a gasp, which, according to the action of the head that accompanied it, was interpreted by his friends as meaning yes or no.", "뉴먼은 여느 때보다 눈을 좀 더 크게 떴지만, 그저 숨을 한 번 헉 들이쉬는 것으로 대답을 대신했다. 그 숨소리는 함께 따라오는 고갯짓에 따라, 친구들이 예 또는 아니요로 알아듣곤 했다."], ["In the present instance, the pantomime consisted of a nod, and not a shake; so Nicholas took the answer as a favourable one.", "이번에는 그 무언극이 고개를 끄덕이는 쪽이었지 가로젓는 쪽은 아니었다. 그래서 니콜라스는 그 대답을 좋은 뜻으로 받아들였다."]], [["‘Now listen to me,’ said Nicholas, laying his hand on Newman’s shoulder.", "\"이제 제 말 좀 들어 보세요.\" 니콜라스가 뉴먼의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다."], ["‘Before I would make an effort to see them, I deemed it expedient to come to you, lest, by gratifying my own selfish desire, I should inflict an injury upon them which I can never repair.", "\"두 사람을 만나려고 애쓰기 전에, 먼저 아저씨한테 오는 게 낫겠다고 생각했어요. 제 이기적인 바람을 채우려다 두 사람한테 영영 돌이킬 수 없는 해를 끼칠까 봐서요."], ["What has my uncle heard from Yorkshire?’", "제 삼촌이 요크셔에서 무슨 소식을 들었나요?\""]], [["Newman opened and shut his mouth, several times, as though he were trying his utmost to speak, but could make nothing of it, and finally fixed his eyes on Nicholas with a grim and ghastly stare.", "뉴먼은 입을 몇 번이고 벌렸다 닫았다 했다. 안간힘을 다해 말을 꺼내려는 것 같았지만 도무지 나오지 않았다. 그러더니 마침내 험상궂고 섬뜩한 눈길로 니콜라스를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["‘What has he heard?’ urged Nicholas, colouring.", "\"무슨 소식을 들었냐고요?\" 니콜라스가 얼굴을 붉히며 다그쳤다."], ["‘You see that I am prepared to hear the very worst that malice can have suggested.", "\"보시다시피 저는 그 악의가 지어낼 수 있는 가장 나쁜 얘기까지도 다 들을 각오가 돼 있어요."], ["Why should you conceal it from me?", "왜 저한테 숨기시는 거예요?"], ["I must know it sooner or later; and what purpose can be gained by trifling with the matter for a few minutes, when half the time would put me in possession of all that has occurred?", "어차피 조만간 알게 될 텐데요. 절반의 시간이면 무슨 일이 있었는지 다 알 수 있는데, 몇 분이나 이렇게 질질 끌어서 무슨 소용이 있겠어요?"], ["Tell me at once, pray.’", "제발 당장 말해 주세요.\""]], [["‘Tomorrow morning,’ said Newman; ‘hear it tomorrow.’", "\"내일 아침에.\" 뉴먼이 말했다. \"내일 듣게.\""]], [["‘What purpose would that answer?’ urged Nicholas.", "\"그게 무슨 소용이 있다고요?\" 니콜라스가 다그쳤다."]], [["‘You would sleep the better,’ replied Newman.", "\"잠을 더 잘 잘 수 있을 테니까.\" 뉴먼이 대꾸했다."]], [["‘I should sleep the worse,’ answered Nicholas, impatiently.", "\"오히려 더 못 잘걸요.\" 니콜라스가 안달하며 대답했다."], ["‘Sleep!", "\"잠이라뇨!"], ["Exhausted as I am, and standing in no common need of rest, I cannot hope to close my eyes all night, unless you tell me everything.’", "이렇게 녹초가 되어 누구보다 쉬어야 할 처지인데도, 아저씨가 다 말해 주지 않으면 밤새 눈 한번 못 붙일 거예요.\""]], [["‘And if I should tell you everything,’ said Newman, hesitating.", "\"내가 다 말해 준다 해도 말이지.\" 뉴먼이 망설이며 말했다."]], [["‘Why, then you may rouse my indignation or wound my pride,’ rejoined Nicholas; ‘but you will not break my rest; for if the scene were acted over again, I could take no other part than I have taken; and whatever consequences may accrue to myself from it, I shall never regret doing as I have done--never, if I starve or beg in consequence. What is a little poverty or suffering, to the disgrace of the basest and most inhuman cowardice! I tell you, if I had stood by, tamely and passively, I should have hated myself, and merited the contempt of every man in existence. The black-hearted scoundrel!’", "\"그럼, 제 분노를 들끓게 하거나 제 자존심에 상처를 줄 수는 있겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 받아쳤다. \"하지만 제 잠을 깨뜨리지는 못해요. 그 일이 다시 한번 똑같이 벌어진다 해도, 저는 그때 했던 것과 똑같이 했을 테니까요. 그 일로 저한테 어떤 결과가 닥치든, 제가 한 일을 절대 후회하지 않을 거예요. 그 때문에 굶주리거나 빌어먹게 되더라도 절대로요. 가장 비열하고 가장 잔인한 비겁함이 주는 치욕에 비하면, 약간의 가난이나 고생쯤이야 뭐 대수겠어요! 단언하건대, 만약 제가 가만히 손 놓고 모른 척 서 있었다면, 저는 저 자신을 미워했을 거고, 세상 모든 사람한테 멸시받아 마땅했을 거예요. 그 시커먼 속을 가진 악당 같으니!\""]], [["With this gentle allusion to the absent Mr.", "자리에 없는 스퀴어스 씨를 이렇게 점잖게 빗대고 나서, 니콜라스는 끓어오르는 분노를 가라앉혔다."], ["Squeers, Nicholas repressed his rising wrath, and relating to Newman exactly what had passed at Dotheboys Hall, entreated him to speak out without more pressing.", "그러고는 두더보이즈 홀에서 있었던 일을 뉴먼에게 그대로 들려준 다음, 더 다그치지 않을 테니 어서 털어놓으라고 간청했다. 이렇게 다짐을 받자 노그스 씨는 낡은 트렁크에서 종이 한 장을 꺼냈다."], ["Thus adjured, Mr.", "몹시 서둘러 갈겨쓴 듯한 종이였다."], ["Noggs took, from an old trunk, a sheet of paper, which appeared to have been scrawled over in great haste; and after sundry extraordinary demonstrations of reluctance, delivered himself in the following terms.", "그리고 한참을 별나게 꺼리는 시늉을 하고 나서, 다음과 같이 입을 열었다."]], [["‘My dear young man, you mustn’t give way to--this sort of thing will never do, you know--as to getting on in the world, if you take everybody’s part that’s ill-treated--Damn it, I am proud to hear of it; and would have done it myself!’", "\"이보게 젊은 친구, 이런 일에 너무 휘둘리면 안 돼. 이런 식으론 절대 안 된다는 거, 자네도 알잖나. 세상에서 출세하는 거 말일세. 학대당하는 사람마다 편을 들고 다니면… 에라, 빌어먹을, 나는 그 얘기를 들으니 오히려 자랑스럽네. 나라도 그렇게 했을 거야!\""]], [["Newman accompanied this very unusual outbreak with a violent blow upon the table, as if, in the heat of the moment, he had mistaken it for the chest or ribs of Mr.", "뉴먼은 이 아주 별난 격정을 식탁을 쾅 내리치는 것으로 마무리했다."], ["Wackford Squeers.", "흥분한 나머지 그 식탁을 와크포드 스퀴어스 씨의 가슴팍이나 갈비뼈로 착각하기라도 한 듯했다."], ["Having, by this open declaration of his feelings, quite precluded himself from offering Nicholas any cautious worldly advice (which had been his first intention), Mr. Noggs went straight to the point.", "이렇게 속마음을 대놓고 드러낸 통에, 노그스 씨는 니콜라스에게 신중한 세상살이 충고를 건넬 길을 스스로 막아 버린 셈이 되었지만(처음엔 그게 그의 속셈이었다), 그는 곧장 본론으로 들어갔다."]], [["‘The day before yesterday,’ said Newman, ‘your uncle received this letter.", "\"그저께 말이야.\" 뉴먼이 말했다."], ["I took a hasty copy of it, while he was out.", "\"자네 삼촌이 이 편지를 받았어. 삼촌이 나간 사이에 내가 후딱 베껴 뒀지."], ["Shall I read it?’", "읽어 줄까?\""]], [["‘If you please,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"네, 그래 주세요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다."], ["Newman Noggs accordingly read as follows:", "그러자 뉴먼 노그스는 이렇게 읽어 내려갔다. \"두더보이즈 홀에서, 목요일 아침."]], [["‘My pa requests me to write to you, the doctors considering it doubtful whether he will ever recuvver the use of his legs which prevents his holding a pen.", "귀하께,\n아빠가 저더러 편지를 쓰라고 하셨어요. 의사 선생님들이 아빠가 다리를 다시 쓰실 수 있을지 어떨지 모르겠다고 해서요. 그래서 펜을 못 잡으세요."]], [["‘We are in a state of mind beyond everything, and my pa is one mask of brooses both blue and green likewise two forms are steepled in his Goar.", "저희는 이루 말할 수 없는 심정이에요. 아빠는 온통 멍투성이에 파랗고 푸르딩딩하고, 의자도 두 개나 아빠의 피웅덩이에 처박혔어요."], ["We were kimpelled to have him carried down into the kitchen where he now lays.", "저희는 어쩔 수 없이 아빠를 부엌으로 옮겨 모셨고, 지금 거기 누워 계세요."], ["You will judge from this that he has been brought very low.", "이걸 보시면 아빠가 얼마나 형편없이 망가지셨는지 짐작하실 거예요."]], [["‘When your nevew that you recommended for a teacher had done this to my pa and jumped upon his body with his feet and also langwedge which I will not pollewt my pen with describing, he assaulted my ma with dreadful violence, dashed her to the earth, and drove her back comb several inches into her head.", "선생님이 교사라고 추천해 주신 그 조카가 아빠한테 이런 짓을 하고, 발로 아빠 몸 위를 짓밟고, 차마 제 펜으로 옮기기도 더러운 욕설까지 퍼붓고 나서는, 엄마한테 끔찍한 폭력을 휘둘렀어요. 엄마를 땅바닥에 내동댕이치고, 엄마 머리에 꽂혀 있던 빗을 몇 인치나 박아 넣었어요."], ["A very little more and it must have entered her skull.", "조금만 더했어도 머리뼈를 뚫고 들어갈 뻔했어요."], ["We have a medical certifiket that if it had, the tortershell would have affected the brain.", "만약 그랬다면 그 거북등껍질 빗이 뇌까지 망가뜨렸을 거라는 의사 진단서도 있어요."]], [["‘Me and my brother were then the victims of his feury since which we have suffered very much which leads us to the arrowing belief that we have received some injury in our insides, especially as no marks of violence are visible externally. I am screaming out loud all the time I write and so is my brother which takes off my attention rather and I hope will excuse mistakes.", "그러고 나서 저랑 남동생이 그 광기의 희생양이 되었고, 그 뒤로 저희는 무척 고통스러웠어요. 그래서 저희 몸속 어딘가가 다쳤다는 가슴 아픈 결론에 이르렀어요. 특히 겉으로는 폭력의 흔적이 하나도 안 보이니 더 그래요. 저는 이 편지를 쓰는 내내 큰 소리로 울부짖고 있고, 남동생도 그러고 있어서 정신이 좀 산만해요. 그래서 틀린 데가 있어도 너그러이 봐 주셨으면 해요."]], [["‘The monster having sasiated his thirst for blood ran away, taking with him a boy of desperate character that he had excited to rebellyon, and a garnet ring belonging to my ma, and not having been apprehended by the constables is supposed to have been took up by some stage-coach. My pa begs that if he comes to you the ring may be returned, and that you will let the thief and assassin go, as if we prosecuted him he would only be transported, and if he is let go he is sure to be hung before long which will save us trouble and be much more satisfactory. Hoping to hear from you when convenient", "그 괴물은 피에 대한 갈증을 다 채우고 나서 달아나 버렸어요. 자기가 부추겨 반항하게 만든 막돼먹은 성격의 남자애 하나랑, 엄마의 석류석 반지까지 가지고요. 그리고 순경들한테 붙잡히지 않은 걸 보니 어떤 역마차를 얻어 탄 게 분명해요. 아빠는, 만약 그자가 선생님께 찾아오거든 반지는 돌려받으시고, 그 도둑이자 살인자는 그냥 놓아 주십사 부탁하셨어요. 저희가 고소하면 그자는 기껏해야 유배나 갈 텐데, 그냥 놔두면 머지않아 틀림없이 교수형에 처해질 테니까요. 그게 저희 수고도 덜고 훨씬 더 후련할 거예요. 편하실 때 답장 주시길 바라며,"]], [["‘I remain ‘Yours and cetrer ‘FANNY SQUEERS.", "이만 줄입니다. 패니 스퀴어스 올림."]], [["‘P.S.", "추신."], ["I pity his ignorance and despise him.’", "저는 그자의 무식함이 가엾고, 그자를 경멸해요.\""]], [["A profound silence succeeded to the reading of this choice epistle, during which Newman Noggs, as he folded it up, gazed with a kind of grotesque pity at the boy of desperate character therein referred to; who, having no more distinct perception of the matter in hand, than that he had been the unfortunate cause of heaping trouble and falsehood upon Nicholas, sat mute and dispirited, with a most woe-begone and heart-stricken look.", "이 절묘한 편지를 다 읽고 나자 깊은 침묵이 뒤따랐다. 그동안 뉴먼 노그스는 편지를 접으면서, 거기 언급된 그 막돼먹은 성격의 남자애를 묘하게 가엾어하는 눈길로 바라보았다. 그 애는 지금 벌어지는 일에 대해, 자기가 불행히도 니콜라스에게 골칫거리와 거짓말을 잔뜩 안긴 빌미가 되었다는 것 말고는 뚜렷이 알지 못한 채, 더없이 처량하고 가슴 미어지는 표정으로 말없이 풀이 죽어 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"노그스 아저씨.\""], ["Noggs,’ said Nicholas, after a few moments’ reflection, ‘I must go out at once.’", "니콜라스가 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 말했다. \"저 당장 나가 봐야겠어요.\""]], [["‘Go out!’ cried Newman.", "\"나간다고!\" 뉴먼이 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Nicholas, ‘to Golden Square.", "\"네.\" 니콜라스가 말했다."], ["Nobody who knows me would believe this story of the ring; but it may suit the purpose, or gratify the hatred of Mr.", "\"골든 스퀘어로요. 저를 아는 사람이라면 누구도 이 반지 얘기를 믿지 않을 거예요."], ["Ralph Nickleby to feign to attach credence to it.", "하지만 랄프 니클비 씨한테는 이걸 믿는 척하는 게 그 속셈에 맞거나, 그 미움을 채워 줄지도 모르죠. 진실을 밝히는 건 마땅한 일이에요."], ["It is due--not to him, but to myself--that I should state the truth; and moreover, I have a word or two to exchange with him, which will not keep cool.’", "그 사람을 위해서가 아니라 저 자신을 위해서요. 게다가 그 사람한테 할 말이 한두 마디 있는데, 그건 식을 때까지 묵혀 둘 수가 없어요.\""]], [["‘They must,’ said Newman.", "\"묵혀 둬야 해.\" 뉴먼이 말했다."]], [["‘They must not, indeed,’ rejoined Nicholas firmly, as he prepared to leave the house.", "\"정말이지 그럴 순 없어요.\" 니콜라스가 집을 나설 채비를 하며 단호하게 받아쳤다."]], [["‘Hear me speak,’ said Newman, planting himself before his impetuous young friend.", "\"내 말 좀 들어 봐.\" 뉴먼이 성급한 젊은 친구 앞을 가로막고 서며 말했다."], ["‘He is not there.", "\"그 사람은 거기 없어."], ["He is away from town.", "시내를 떠났거든."], ["He will not be back for three days; and I know that letter will not be answered before he returns.’", "사흘은 지나야 돌아올 거야. 그리고 그 편지에 대한 답장도 그자가 돌아오기 전엔 없을 게 분명해.\""]], [["‘Are you sure of this?’ asked Nicholas, chafing violently, and pacing the narrow room with rapid strides.", "\"확실해요?\" 니콜라스가 몹시 안달하며, 좁은 방을 성큼성큼 빠르게 오가며 물었다."]], [["‘Quite,’ rejoined Newman.", "\"아주 확실하지.\" 뉴먼이 받아쳤다."], ["‘He had hardly read it when he was called away.", "\"그자는 그 편지를 채 다 읽기도 전에 불려 나갔어."], ["Its contents are known to nobody but himself and us.’", "그 내용을 아는 사람은 그자랑 우리밖에 없어.\""]], [["‘Are you certain?’ demanded Nicholas, precipitately; ‘not even to my mother or sister?", "\"틀림없어요?\" 니콜라스가 다급하게 캐물었다."], ["If I thought that they--I will go there--I must see them.", "\"제 어머니나 누이동생도 모르고요? 만약 두 사람이…"], ["Which is the way?", "그리로 가야겠어요. 만나 봐야겠어요."], ["Where is it?’", "어느 쪽이에요? 어디예요?\""]], [["‘Now, be advised by me,’ said Newman, speaking for the moment, in his earnestness, like any other man--‘make no effort to see even them, till he comes home.", "\"자, 내 말대로 해.\" 뉴먼이 그 순간만큼은 간절한 마음에 여느 사람처럼 또박또박 말했다."], ["I know the man. Do not seem to have been tampering with anybody.", "\"그자가 돌아올 때까지는 두 사람조차 만나려고 하지 마."], ["When he returns, go straight to him, and speak as boldly as you like.", "나는 그 사람을 잘 알아. 자네가 누군가하고 미리 입을 맞춘 것처럼 보이게 하면 안 돼."], ["Guessing at the real truth, he knows it as well as you or I.", "그자가 돌아오거든 곧장 찾아가서, 하고 싶은 만큼 당당하게 말하라고. 그자도 진실을 짐작하고 있고, 자네나 나만큼이나 잘 알고 있어."], ["Trust him for that.’", "그 점은 믿어도 돼.\""]], [["‘You mean well to me, and should know him better than I can,’ replied Nicholas, after some consideration.", "\"아저씨는 저를 위해 주시는 거고, 그 사람도 저보다 더 잘 아시겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 한참 생각하다 대답했다."], ["‘Well; let it be so.’", "\"좋아요. 그렇게 하죠.\""]], [["Newman, who had stood during the foregoing conversation with his back planted against the door, ready to oppose any egress from the apartment by force, if necessary, resumed his seat with much satisfaction; and as the water in the kettle was by this time boiling, made a glassful of spirits and water for Nicholas, and a cracked mug-full for the joint accommodation of himself and Smike, of which the two partook in great harmony, while Nicholas, leaning his head upon his hand, remained buried in melancholy meditation.", "뉴먼은 앞선 대화 내내 등을 문에 딱 붙이고 서서, 필요하면 힘으로라도 방에서 나가지 못하게 막을 채비를 하고 있었는데, 이제 흡족한 마음으로 다시 자리에 앉았다. 그새 주전자의 물이 끓었기에, 그는 니콜라스에게는 잔에 술과 물을 타서 한 잔 만들어 주고, 자기와 스마이크가 함께 마실 몫으로는 금이 간 머그잔에 가득 타 주었다. 둘은 사이좋게 그것을 나누어 마셨고, 그동안 니콜라스는 한 손에 머리를 괴고 우울한 생각에 잠긴 채 앉아 있었다."]], [["Meanwhile, the company below stairs, after listening attentively and not hearing any noise which would justify them in interfering for the gratification of their curiosity, returned to the chamber of the Kenwigses, and employed themselves in hazarding a great variety of conjectures relative to the cause of Mr.", "한편 아래층의 손님들은, 귀를 쫑긋 세우고 들어 봐도 호기심을 채우려고 끼어들 만한 소리가 들리지 않자, 케닉스 씨네 방으로 돌아갔다."], ["Noggs’ sudden disappearance and detention.", "그러고는 노그스 씨가 갑자기 사라져 붙들려 있는 까닭을 두고 온갖 추측을 늘어놓기 시작했다."]], [["‘Lor, I’ll tell you what,’ said Mrs. Kenwigs.", "\"어머, 내 말 좀 들어 봐요.\""], ["‘Suppose it should be an express sent up to say that his property has all come back again!’", "케닉스 부인이 말했다. \"혹시 그 사람 재산이 몽땅 도로 돌아왔다는 급한 전갈이 온 거면 어떡해요!\""]], [["‘Dear me,’ said Mr.", "\"이런.\" 케닉스 씨가 말했다."], ["Kenwigs; ‘it’s not impossible.", "\"그럴 수도 있겠는걸."], ["Perhaps, in that case, we’d better send up and ask if he won’t take a little more punch.’", "그렇다면 사람을 올려 보내서 펀치를 좀 더 들지 않겠냐고 물어보는 게 낫겠어.\""]], [["‘Kenwigs!’ said Mr.", "\"케닉스!\""], ["Lillyvick, in a loud voice, ‘I’m surprised at you.’", "릴리빅 씨가 큰 소리로 말했다. \"자네한테 놀랐네.\""]], [["‘What’s the matter, sir?’ asked Mr.", "\"무슨 일이세요, 어르신?\""], ["Kenwigs, with becoming submission to the collector of water-rates.", "케닉스 씨가 수도세 징수원에게 걸맞은 공손함으로 물었다."]], [["‘Making such a remark as that, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"그런 소릴 하다니, 이 사람아.\" 릴리빅 씨가 성을 내며 대꾸했다."], ["Lillyvick, angrily.", "\"그자는 이미 펀치를 마시지 않았나, 응? 내가 보기엔, 그 펀치를 그렇게 가로채 간 꼬락서니가, 이런 표현을 써도 된다면 말이지, 이 자리에 있는 모두를 아주 무시하는 짓이야."], ["‘He has had punch already, has he not, sir?", "괘씸하지, 아주 괘씸해. 이 집에선 그런 일을 그냥 넘기는 게 관습인지 몰라도, 내가 여태 보고 살아온 그런 행실은 아니야."], ["I consider the way in which that punch was cut off, if I may use the expression, highly disrespectful to this company; scandalous, perfectly scandalous.", "그러니 케닉스, 자네한테 한마디 해 두는 거네. 어떤 신사가 펀치 한 잔을 앞에 두고 막 입술을 갖다 대려는 참인데, 다른 사람이 와서 '실례합니다'라든가 '잠깐 좀 보겠습니다'"], ["It may be the custom to allow such things in this house, but it’s not the kind of behaviour that I’ve been used to see displayed, and so I don’t mind telling you, Kenwigs.", "한마디 없이 그 잔을 덥석 움켜쥐고는 들고 가 버린단 말이야. 이게 좋은 예절일지도 모르지."], ["A gentleman has a glass of punch before him to which he is just about to set his lips, when another gentleman comes and collars that glass of punch, without a “with your leave”, or “by your leave”, and carries that glass of punch away.", "그럴 수도 있겠지. 하지만 난 도무지 이해를 못 하겠어, 그뿐이야."], ["This may be good manners--I dare say it is--but I don’t understand it, that’s all; and what’s more, I don’t care if I never do.", "게다가 앞으로도 영영 이해 못 한대도 상관없어. 나는 할 말은 하고 사는 사람이야, 케닉스."], ["It’s my way to speak my mind, Kenwigs, and that is my mind; and if you don’t like it, it’s past my regular time for going to bed, and I can find my way home without making it later.’", "이게 내 생각이야. 자네가 마음에 안 든다면, 평소 자던 시간도 지났으니, 더 늦기 전에 알아서 집에 갈 수 있네.\""]], [["Here was an untoward event!", "이거 참 난감한 일이 벌어진 것이다!"], ["The collector had sat swelling and fuming in offended dignity for some minutes, and had now fairly burst out.", "징수원은 몇 분 동안 상한 위신을 안고 부글부글 끓어오르며 앉아 있다가, 이제 기어이 터뜨려 버린 것이었다."], ["The great man--the rich relation--the unmarried uncle--who had it in his power to make Morleena an heiress, and the very baby a legatee--was offended.", "그 대단한 인물, 그 돈 많은 친척, 그 결혼 안 한 외삼촌, 마음만 먹으면 모를리나를 상속녀로 만들 수도, 갓난아기까지 유산 상속인으로 삼을 수도 있는 그 사람이 단단히 삐친 것이다."], ["Gracious Powers, where was this to end!", "아이고 맙소사, 이 일을 대체 어떻게 마무리한단 말인가!"]], [["‘I am very sorry, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"정말 죄송합니다, 어르신.\""], ["Kenwigs, humbly.", "케닉스 씨가 굽실거리며 말했다."]], [["‘Don’t tell me you’re sorry,’ retorted Mr.", "\"죄송하단 소린 말게.\""], ["Lillyvick, with much sharpness.", "릴리빅 씨가 아주 날카롭게 받아쳤다."], ["‘You should have prevented it, then.’", "\"그럼 애초에 못 하게 막았어야지.\""]], [["The company were quite paralysed by this domestic crash.", "손님들은 이 집안의 충돌에 그만 얼어붙고 말았다."], ["The back-parlour sat with her mouth wide open, staring vacantly at the collector, in a stupor of dismay; the other guests were scarcely less overpowered by the great man’s irritation.", "뒷방에서 온 부인은 입을 떡 벌린 채 넋이 나가, 어쩔 줄 모르고 멍하니 징수원을 쳐다보았다. 다른 손님들도 그 대단한 인물의 노여움에 못지않게 짓눌려 있었다."], ["Mr. Kenwigs, not being skilful in such matters, only fanned the flame in attempting to extinguish it.", "케닉스 씨는 이런 일에 영 서툴렀던지라, 불을 끄려다 오히려 부채질만 하고 말았다."]], [["‘I didn’t think of it, I am sure, sir,’ said that gentleman.", "\"정말이지 거기까진 생각 못 했습니다, 어르신.\""], ["‘I didn’t suppose that such a little thing as a glass of punch would have put you out of temper.’", "케닉스 씨가 말했다. \"펀치 한 잔 같은 그 사소한 일로 어르신께서 화가 나실 줄은 몰랐어요.\""]], [["‘Out of temper!", "\"화가 나?"], ["What the devil do you mean by that piece of impertinence, Mr.", "케닉스, 그 무례한 소린 대체 무슨 뜻인가?\""], ["Kenwigs?’ said the collector.", "징수원이 말했다."], ["‘Morleena, child--give me my hat.’", "\"모를리나, 얘야, 내 모자 가져오너라.\""]], [["‘Oh, you’re not going, Mr.", "\"어머, 가시면 안 돼요, 릴리빅 어르신.\""], ["Lillyvick, sir,’ interposed Miss Petowker, with her most bewitching smile.", "페토커 양이 가장 매혹적인 미소를 지으며 끼어들었다."]], [["But still Mr.", "하지만 릴리빅 씨는 그 요부에게도 아랑곳없이 고집스럽게 외쳤다. \"모를리나, 내 모자!\""], ["Lillyvick, regardless of the siren, cried obdurately, ‘Morleena, my hat!’ upon the fourth repetition of which demand, Mrs Kenwigs sunk back in her chair, with a cry that might have softened a water-butt, not to say a water-collector; while the four little girls (privately instructed to that effect) clasped their uncle’s drab shorts in their arms, and prayed him, in imperfect English, to remain.", "그가 네 번째로 그렇게 다그치자 케닉스 부인은 의자에 풀썩 주저앉으며 울음을 터뜨렸는데, 그 소리는 수도세 징수원은 말할 것도 없고 빗물통이라도 누그러뜨릴 만했다. 그러는 사이 네 어린 딸들은(미리 그렇게 하라고 단단히 일러둔 터였다) 외삼촌의 황갈색 반바지를 팔로 부둥켜안고는, 서툰 말로 제발 가지 말아 달라고 애원했다."]], [["‘Why should I stop here, my dears?’ said Mr.", "\"내가 왜 여기 남아 있어야겠니, 얘들아?\""], ["Lillyvick; ‘I’m not wanted here.’", "릴리빅 씨가 말했다. \"여긴 날 반기지도 않는데.\""]], [["‘Oh, do not speak so cruelly, uncle,’ sobbed Mrs.", "\"아, 그렇게 매정하게 말씀하지 마세요, 외삼촌.\""], ["Kenwigs, ‘unless you wish to kill me.’", "케닉스 부인이 흐느꼈다. \"저를 죽이실 작정이 아니라면요.\""]], [["‘I shouldn’t wonder if some people were to say I did,’ replied Mr Lillyvick, glancing angrily at Kenwigs.", "\"내가 그러고 싶어 한다고 말할 사람도 있을 게다.\" 릴리빅 씨가 케닉스를 성난 눈으로 흘기며 대꾸했다."], ["‘Out of temper!’", "\"화가 났다니!\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["I cannot bear to see him look so, at my husband,’ cried Mrs Kenwigs.", "저이가 제 남편을 저렇게 노려보는 꼴은 차마 못 보겠어요.\" 케닉스 부인이 울부짖었다."], ["‘It’s so dreadful in families.", "\"한집안끼리 이러는 게 어찌나 끔찍한지."], ["Oh!’", "아!\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"릴리빅 어르신.\""], ["Lillyvick,’ said Kenwigs, ‘I hope, for the sake of your niece, that you won’t object to be reconciled.’", "케닉스가 말했다. \"조카딸을 봐서라도, 부디 화해를 마다하지 말아 주십시오.\""]], [["The collector’s features relaxed, as the company added their entreaties to those of his nephew-in-law.", "조카사위의 부탁에 손님들까지 거들고 나서자 징수원의 표정이 누그러졌다."], ["He gave up his hat, and held out his hand.", "그는 모자를 도로 내려놓고 손을 내밀었다."]], [["‘There, Kenwigs,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 케닉스.\" 릴리빅 씨가 말했다."], ["Lillyvick; ‘and let me tell you, at the same time, to show you how much out of temper I was, that if I had gone away without another word, it would have made no difference respecting that pound or two which I shall leave among your children when I die.’", "\"그리고 동시에 한마디 해 두지. 내가 얼마나 화가 났었는지 보여 주려고 하는 말인데, 내가 더 말없이 그냥 가 버렸다 해도, 내가 죽을 때 자네 아이들한테 남겨 줄 그 1, 2파운드에는 아무 변함이 없었을 거야.\""]], [["‘Morleena Kenwigs,’ cried her mother, in a torrent of affection. ‘Go down upon your knees to your dear uncle, and beg him to love you all his life through, for he’s more a angel than a man, and I’ve always said so.’", "\"모를리나 케닉스.\" 어미가 애정을 주체 못 하고 소리쳤다. \"어서 다정한 외삼촌 앞에 무릎 꿇고, 평생 너를 사랑해 달라고 빌어라. 외삼촌은 사람이라기보다 천사 같은 분이란다. 내가 늘 그렇게 말해 왔잖니.\""]], [["Miss Morleena approaching to do homage, in compliance with this injunction, was summarily caught up and kissed by Mr.", "이 분부에 따라 모를리나 양이 예를 갖추려고 다가가자, 릴리빅 씨는 대뜸 그 애를 번쩍 안아 올려 입을 맞추었다."], ["Lillyvick; and thereupon Mrs. Kenwigs darted forward and kissed the collector, and an irrepressible murmur of applause broke from the company who had witnessed his magnanimity.", "그러자 케닉스 부인이 앞으로 달려 나가 징수원에게 입을 맞추었고, 그의 너그러움을 지켜본 손님들 사이에서 참을 수 없는 갈채의 웅성거림이 터져 나왔다."]], [["The worthy gentleman then became once more the life and soul of the society; being again reinstated in his old post of lion, from which high station the temporary distraction of their thoughts had for a moment dispossessed him.", "이 훌륭한 신사는 이내 다시 모임의 활기이자 중심이 되었다. 잠깐 사람들의 정신이 딴 데로 쏠리는 바람에 잠시 밀려났던, 좌중의 주인공이라는 그 높은 자리에 다시 떡하니 올라앉은 것이다."], ["Quadruped lions are said to be savage, only when they are hungry; biped lions are rarely sulky longer than when their appetite for distinction remains unappeased.", "네발 달린 사자는 배고플 때만 사나워진다고들 한다. 두 발 달린 사자가 뾰로통해 있는 건, 자기를 떠받들어 달라는 욕심이 채워지지 않을 때뿐이고, 그것도 오래가는 법이 거의 없다."], ["Mr.", "릴리빅 씨는 그 어느 때보다 위세가 높아졌다."], ["Lillyvick stood higher than ever; for he had shown his power; hinted at his property and testamentary intentions; gained great credit for disinterestedness and virtue; and, in addition to all, was finally accommodated with a much larger tumbler of punch than that which Newman Noggs had so feloniously made off with.", "자기 힘을 보여 주었고, 자기 재산과 유언에 담을 뜻을 넌지시 비쳤으며, 사심 없고 덕 있는 사람이라는 큰 평판까지 얻었으니까. 게다가 그 모든 것에 더해, 마침내 뉴먼 노그스가 그토록 흉악하게 들고 가 버린 것보다 훨씬 큰 잔에 펀치를 받아 들기까지 했다."]], [["‘I say!", "\"저기요!"], ["I beg everybody’s pardon for intruding again,’ said Crowl, looking in at this happy juncture; ‘but what a queer business this is, isn’t it?", "또 끼어들어서 모두께 정말 죄송합니다.\" 크롤이 이 흐뭇한 순간에 안을 들여다보며 말했다."], ["Noggs has lived in this house, now going on for five years, and nobody has ever been to see him before, within the memory of the oldest inhabitant.’", "\"그런데 이거 참 묘한 일 아닙니까? 노그스가 이 집에 산 지 이제 오 년이 다 되어 가는데, 이 집에서 제일 오래 산 사람이 기억하기로도 여태 그를 찾아온 사람이 하나도 없었거든요.\""]], [["‘It’s a strange time of night to be called away, sir, certainly,’ said the collector; ‘and the behaviour of Mr.", "\"한밤중에 불려 나가다니, 참 이상한 시간이긴 하지요.\" 징수원이 말했다."], ["Noggs himself, is, to say the least of it, mysterious.’", "\"게다가 노그스 씨 본인의 행동도, 좋게 말해 봐야 수상쩍단 말이오.\""]], [["‘Well, so it is,’ rejoined Crowl; ‘and I’ll tell you what’s more--I think these two geniuses, whoever they are, have run away from somewhere.’", "\"맞아요, 그러게 말입니다.\" 크롤이 받아쳤다. \"한술 더 떠서 말씀드리자면, 제 생각엔 그 두 친구가, 그게 누구든 간에, 어디선가 도망쳐 온 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘What makes you think that, sir?’ demanded the collector, who seemed, by a tacit understanding, to have been chosen and elected mouthpiece to the company.", "\"왜 그렇게 생각하시오?\" 징수원이 캐물었다."], ["‘You have no reason to suppose that they have run away from anywhere without paying the rates and taxes due, I hope?’", "어느새 모두의 암묵적인 합의로 좌중의 대변인 자리에 뽑혀 앉은 듯한 그였다. \"혹시 그자들이 어딘가에서 마땅히 내야 할 세금이며 부과금을 안 내고 도망쳐 왔다고 보는 건 아니겠지요?\""]], [["Mr. Crowl, with a look of some contempt, was about to enter a general protest against the payment of rates or taxes, under any circumstances, when he was checked by a timely whisper from Kenwigs, and several frowns and winks from Mrs.", "크롤 씨는 다소 경멸 어린 표정으로, 어떤 경우에든 세금이나 부과금을 내는 것에 대해 한바탕 반대를 늘어놓으려던 참이었다."], ["K., which providentially stopped him.", "그런데 케닉스가 때맞춰 속삭여 말리고, 케닉스 부인이 몇 번이고 눈살을 찌푸리며 눈짓을 보내는 통에, 다행히도 입을 다물었다."]], [["‘Why the fact is,’ said Crowl, who had been listening at Newman’s door with all his might and main; ‘the fact is, that they have been talking so loud, that they quite disturbed me in my room, and so I couldn’t help catching a word here, and a word there; and all I heard, certainly seemed to refer to their having bolted from some place or other. I don’t wish to alarm Mrs. Kenwigs; but I hope they haven’t come from any jail or hospital, and brought away a fever or some unpleasantness of that sort, which might be catching for the children.’", "\"실은 말이죠.\" 있는 힘껏 뉴먼의 방문에 귀를 대고 엿듣던 크롤이 말했다. \"실은 두 사람이 어찌나 큰 소리로 얘기를 하던지, 제 방까지 다 들려서 영 거슬리더라고요. 그러다 보니 여기서 한마디, 저기서 한마디 안 들으려야 안 들을 수가 없었지요. 그런데 제가 들은 건 죄다 어디선가 줄행랑을 친 얘기 같았어요. 케닉스 부인을 놀라게 하고 싶진 않지만, 부디 그자들이 무슨 감옥이나 병원에서 온 게 아니었으면 좋겠네요. 열병이라든가 그 비슷한 고약한 걸 옮겨 와서, 애들한테 옮기기라도 하면 큰일이니까요.\""]], [["Mrs. Kenwigs was so overpowered by this supposition, that it needed all the tender attentions of Miss Petowker, of the Theatre Royal, Drury Lane, to restore her to anything like a state of calmness; not to mention the assiduity of Mr.", "케닉스 부인은 이 추측에 어찌나 압도됐는지, 드루어리 레인 왕립 극장의 페토커 양이 온갖 정성을 다해 보살핀 뒤에야 겨우 평정을 되찾을 수 있었다."], ["Kenwigs, who held a fat smelling-bottle to his lady’s nose, until it became matter of some doubt whether the tears which coursed down her face were the result of feelings or SAL VOLATILE.", "거기에 더해 케닉스 씨도 부지런히 거들었는데, 그는 통통한 각성제 병을 부인의 코에 갖다 대고 있었고, 급기야 그녀의 얼굴을 타고 흐르는 눈물이 정말 북받친 감정 탓인지 아니면 그 휘발성 각성제 탓인지 헷갈릴 지경이 되었다."]], [["The ladies, having expressed their sympathy, singly and separately, fell, according to custom, into a little chorus of soothing expressions, among which, such condolences as ‘Poor dear!’--‘I should feel just the same, if I was her’--‘To be sure, it’s a very trying thing’--and ‘Nobody but a mother knows what a mother’s feelings is,’ were among the most prominent, and most frequently repeated.", "부인들은 저마다 따로따로 안타까운 마음을 내비치더니, 으레 그러듯 위로의 말을 작은 합창처럼 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그중에서도 \"가엾어라!\"라든가, \"내가 저 사람이라도 꼭 같은 심정일 거예요\"라든가, \"그럼요, 참 견디기 힘든 일이지요\"라든가, \"어미 마음은 어미만이 아는 법이에요\""], ["In short, the opinion of the company was so clearly manifested, that Mr. Kenwigs was on the point of repairing to Mr.", "같은 말들이 가장 두드러졌고, 또 가장 자주 되풀이되었다."], ["Noggs’s room, to demand an explanation, and had indeed swallowed a preparatory glass of punch, with great inflexibility and steadiness of purpose, when the attention of all present was diverted by a new and terrible surprise.", "한마디로 좌중의 뜻이 어찌나 또렷이 드러났던지, 케닉스 씨는 노그스 씨의 방으로 가서 해명을 요구하려는 참이었다. 그래서 미리 펀치 한 잔까지 단단하고도 흔들림 없는 결의로 들이켠 터였는데, 바로 그때 새롭고도 끔찍한 사태가 벌어져 모두의 정신이 그리로 쏠렸다."]], [["This was nothing less than the sudden pouring forth of a rapid succession of the shrillest and most piercing screams, from an upper story; and to all appearance from the very two-pair back, in which the infant Kenwigs was at that moment enshrined.", "다름이 아니라, 위층에서 더없이 날카롭고 째지는 듯한 비명이 잇따라 터져 나온 것이었다."], ["They were no sooner audible, than Mrs.", "그것도 아무리 봐도 갓난 케닉스가 그 순간 고이 모셔져 있는 바로 그 뒷방에서 나는 소리 같았다."], ["Kenwigs, opining that a strange cat had come in, and sucked the baby’s breath while the girl was asleep, made for the door, wringing her hands, and shrieking dismally; to the great consternation and confusion of the company.", "그 소리가 들리기 무섭게 케닉스 부인은, 어떤 낯선 고양이가 들어와 보던 애가 잠든 사이 아기의 숨을 빨아들이고 있다고 단정하고는, 두 손을 부르쥐고 처절하게 비명을 지르며 문으로 달려갔다. 손님들은 그만 기겁하며 우왕좌왕했다."]], [["‘Mr. Kenwigs, see what it is; make haste!’ cried the sister, laying violent hands upon Mrs.", "\"케닉스 씨, 무슨 일인지 보고 와요, 어서요!\""], ["Kenwigs, and holding her back by force.", "부인의 동생이 케닉스 부인을 와락 붙들고 힘으로 끌어당기며 외쳤다."], ["‘Oh don’t twist about so, dear, or I can never hold you.’", "\"아, 그렇게 몸부림치지 마, 언니. 안 그러면 도저히 못 붙들겠어.\""]], [["‘My baby, my blessed, blessed, blessed, blessed baby!’ screamed Mrs Kenwigs, making every blessed louder than the last.", "\"내 아기, 내 귀하고 귀하고 귀하고 귀한 아기!\" 케닉스 부인이 '귀하고'를 갈수록 더 크게 외치며 비명을 질렀다."], ["‘My own darling, sweet, innocent Lillyvick--Oh let me go to him.", "\"내 사랑하는, 다정하고 순결한 릴리빅… 아, 그 애한테 가게 해 줘."], ["Let me go-o-o-o!’", "가게 해 줘어어어!\""]], [["Pending the utterance of these frantic cries, and the wails and lamentations of the four little girls, Mr.", "이렇게 미친 듯한 절규가 터지고 네 어린 딸들이 통곡하며 울부짖는 사이, 케닉스 씨는 소리가 나는 방을 향해 부리나케 위층으로 뛰어올라 갔다."], ["Kenwigs rushed upstairs to the room whence the sounds proceeded; at the door of which, he encountered Nicholas, with the child in his arms, who darted out with such violence, that the anxious father was thrown down six stairs, and alighted on the nearest landing-place, before he had found time to open his mouth to ask what was the matter.", "그런데 그 방문 앞에서 아기를 품에 안은 니콜라스와 맞닥뜨렸다. 니콜라스가 어찌나 거세게 뛰쳐나왔던지, 애가 탄 아비는 계단 여섯 칸을 굴러떨어져, 무슨 일이냐고 입을 떼 볼 새도 없이 가장 가까운 층계참에 나동그라지고 말았다."]], [["‘Don’t be alarmed,’ cried Nicholas, running down; ‘here it is; it’s all out, it’s all over; pray compose yourselves; there’s no harm done;’ and with these, and a thousand other assurances, he delivered the baby (whom, in his hurry, he had carried upside down), to Mrs. Kenwigs, and ran back to assist Mr. Kenwigs, who was rubbing his head very hard, and looking much bewildered by his tumble.", "\"놀라지 마세요.\" 니콜라스가 뛰어 내려오며 외쳤다. \"여기 있어요. 다 끝났어요, 다 지나갔다고요. 부디 진정하세요. 아무 일도 없어요.\" 그는 이런 말과 함께 수천 마디 안심시키는 말을 쏟아 내며, (서두르는 통에 거꾸로 안고 있던) 아기를 케닉스 부인에게 건넸다. 그러고는 다시 뛰어 올라가, 머리를 박박 문지르며 자기가 굴러떨어진 데에 어리둥절해하던 케닉스 씨를 부축했다."]], [["Reassured by this cheering intelligence, the company in some degree recovered from their fears, which had been productive of some most singular instances of a total want of presence of mind; thus, the bachelor friend had, for a long time, supported in his arms Mrs Kenwigs’s sister, instead of Mrs.", "이 반가운 소식에 마음을 놓은 손님들은 어느 정도 두려움에서 벗어났다."], ["Kenwigs; and the worthy Mr. Lillyvick had been actually seen, in the perturbation of his spirits, to kiss Miss Petowker several times, behind the room-door, as calmly as if nothing distressing were going forward.", "그 두려움 탓에 정신을 완전히 놓아 버린 아주 별난 일들이 몇 가지 벌어졌었는데, 가령 그 독신 친구는 한참 동안 케닉스 부인이 아니라 그 동생을 품에 안고 있었고, 점잖은 릴리빅 씨로 말하자면 마음이 어수선한 나머지 방문 뒤에서 페토커 양에게 몇 번이나 입을 맞추는 모습이 실제로 목격되었는데, 그것도 무슨 큰일이 벌어진 게 전혀 아니라는 듯 태연하기 짝이 없었다."]], [["‘It is a mere nothing,’ said Nicholas, returning to Mrs.", "\"별일 아니에요.\" 니콜라스가 케닉스 부인에게 돌아와 말했다."], ["Kenwigs; ‘the little girl, who was watching the child, being tired I suppose, fell asleep, and set her hair on fire.’", "\"아기를 보던 여자애가, 아마 졸렸나 봐요, 잠이 들었다가 제 머리카락에 불이 붙었지 뭐예요.\""]], [["‘Oh you malicious little wretch!’ cried Mrs.", "\"오, 이 악독한 못된 것 같으니!\""], ["Kenwigs, impressively shaking her forefinger at the small unfortunate, who might be thirteen years old, and was looking on with a singed head and a frightened face.", "케닉스 부인이 그 가엾은 어린 것에게 사뭇 험악하게 집게손가락을 흔들어 대며 소리쳤다. 열세 살쯤 되어 보이는 그 애는 머리가 그을린 채 겁에 질린 얼굴로 멀거니 보고 있었다."]], [["‘I heard her cries,’ continued Nicholas, ‘and ran down, in time to prevent her setting fire to anything else. You may depend upon it that the child is not hurt; for I took it off the bed myself, and brought it here to convince you.’", "\"그 애 비명을 듣고요.\" 니콜라스가 말을 이었다. \"뛰어 내려가서, 다른 데까지 불이 옮겨붙기 전에 막았어요. 아기는 정말 아무 데도 안 다쳤으니 믿으셔도 돼요. 제가 직접 침대에서 안아 내려서, 보여 드리려고 여기 데려온 거예요.\""]], [["This brief explanation over, the infant, who, as he was christened after the collector!", "이 짤막한 해명이 끝나자, 징수원의 이름을 따서 세례를 받아 릴리빅 케닉스라는 이름을 자랑스레 지니게 된 그 아기는, 사람들의 입맞춤 세례에 절반쯤 숨이 막히고 제 어미 가슴팍에 어찌나 꽉 눌렸던지 다시 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["rejoiced in the names of Lillyvick Kenwigs, was partially suffocated under the caresses of the audience, and squeezed to his mother’s bosom, until he roared again.", "그러고 나자 좌중의 관심은 자연스레, 감히 제 머리카락을 다 태워 먹은 그 어린 계집애에게로 옮겨 갔다."], ["The attention of the company was then directed, by a natural transition, to the little girl who had had the audacity to burn her hair off, and who, after receiving sundry small slaps and pushes from the more energetic of the ladies, was mercifully sent home: the ninepence, with which she was to have been rewarded, being escheated to the Kenwigs family.", "그 애는 더 극성스러운 부인들에게 자잘하게 몇 대 얻어맞고 떠밀린 끝에, 자비롭게도 집으로 돌려보내졌다. 다만 그 애한테 주려던 9펜스는 케닉스 집안 몫으로 몰수되었다."]], [["‘And whatever we are to say to you, sir,’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"그리고 우리가 선생님께 무슨 말씀을 드려야 할지,\""], ["Kenwigs, addressing young Lillyvick’s deliverer, ‘I am sure I don’t know.’", "케닉스 부인이 어린 릴리빅을 구해 준 사람을 향해 외쳤다. \"정말이지 모르겠네요.\""]], [["‘You need say nothing at all,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"아무 말씀도 하실 것 없어요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다."], ["‘I have done nothing to found any very strong claim upon your eloquence, I am sure.’", "\"부인의 그 고마운 말씀을 받을 만한 일을 한 것도 없는걸요, 정말로요.\""]], [["‘He might have been burnt to death, if it hadn’t been for you, sir,’ simpered Miss Petowker.", "\"선생님이 아니었으면 그 애는 불에 타 죽었을지도 몰라요.\" 페토커 양이 새치름하게 말했다."]], [["‘Not very likely, I think,’ replied Nicholas; ‘for there was abundance of assistance here, which must have reached him before he had been in any danger.’", "\"그렇게까지 되진 않았을 거예요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다. \"여기 도와줄 분들이 잔뜩 계셨으니, 그 애가 위험해지기 전에 분명 누군가 달려갔을 테니까요.\""]], [["‘You will let us drink your health, anyvays, sir!’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 그 보답으로 선생님 건강을 위해 한잔 들기라도 하게 해 주세요!\""], ["Kenwigs motioning towards the table.", "케닉스 씨가 식탁 쪽으로 손짓하며 말했다."]], [["‘--In my absence, by all means,’ rejoined Nicholas, with a smile.", "\"제가 없는 자리에서요, 그러시면 얼마든지요.\" 니콜라스가 빙긋 웃으며 받았다."], ["‘I have had a very fatiguing journey, and should be most indifferent company--a far greater check upon your merriment, than a promoter of it, even if I kept awake, which I think very doubtful.", "\"제가 여행에 몹시 지쳐서, 같이 있어 봐야 도무지 흥이 안 나는 사람일 거예요. 흥을 돋우기는커녕 깨기나 할 텐데, 깨어 있는다 해도 그렇고, 그나마 깨어 있을 수 있을지도 영 모르겠어요."], ["If you will allow me, I’ll return to my friend, Mr.", "괜찮으시면 저는 친구 노그스 씨한테 돌아가 보겠습니다."], ["Noggs, who went upstairs again, when he found nothing serious had occurred.", "그분은 별일 아닌 걸 아시고 다시 위층으로 올라가셨거든요."], ["Good-night.’", "안녕히들 계세요.\""]], [["Excusing himself, in these terms, from joining in the festivities, Nicholas took a most winning farewell of Mrs.", "이런 말로 잔치 자리에 끼는 걸 사양한 니콜라스는, 케닉스 부인과 다른 부인들에게 더없이 정겹게 작별을 고하고 물러갔다."], ["Kenwigs and the other ladies, and retired, after making a very extraordinary impression upon the company.", "좌중에 아주 비상한 인상을 남기고서."]], [["‘What a delightful young man!’ cried Mrs.", "\"어쩜 저렇게 멋진 청년이람!\""], ["Kenwigs.", "케닉스 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Uncommon gentlemanly, really,’ said Mr.", "\"보기 드물게 신사답더군, 정말로.\" 케닉스 씨가 말했다."], ["Kenwigs. ‘Don’t you think so, Mr Lillyvick?’", "\"안 그렇습니까, 릴리빅 어르신?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ said the collector, with a dubious shrug of his shoulders, ‘He is gentlemanly, very gentlemanly--in appearance.’", "\"그렇지.\" 징수원이 미심쩍은 듯 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"신사답긴 하지, 아주 신사다워. 겉보기엔 말이야.\""]], [["‘I hope you don’t see anything against him, uncle?’ inquired Mrs Kenwigs.", "\"혹시 저 청년한테 거슬리는 데라도 보이세요, 외삼촌?\" 케닉스 부인이 물었다."]], [["‘No, my dear,’ replied the collector, ‘no. I trust he may not turn out--well--no matter--my love to you, my dear, and long life to the baby!’", "\"아니다, 얘야.\" 징수원이 대답했다. \"아니야. 다만 저 청년이… 글쎄… 아무튼 됐다. 너한테 정을 보내마, 얘야. 그리고 아기는 오래오래 살거라!\""]], [["‘Your namesake,’ said Mrs.", "\"외삼촌 이름을 물려받은 아이잖아요.\""], ["Kenwigs, with a sweet smile.", "케닉스 부인이 상냥하게 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["‘And I hope a worthy namesake,’ observed Mr.", "\"부디 그 이름값을 하는 아이가 되었으면 합니다.\""], ["Kenwigs, willing to propitiate the collector.", "징수원의 비위를 맞추려는 마음에 케닉스 씨가 거들었다."], ["‘I hope a baby as will never disgrace his godfather, and as may be considered, in arter years, of a piece with the Lillyvicks whose name he bears.", "\"제 대부의 얼굴에 절대 먹칠하지 않는 아이, 그리고 훗날 자라서 제 이름의 주인인 릴리빅 가문 사람들과 어깨를 나란히 할 만한 아이로 여겨지길 바랍니다."], ["I do say--and Mrs. Kenwigs is of the same sentiment, and feels it as strong as I do--that I consider his being called Lillyvick one of the greatest blessings and Honours of my existence.’", "정말이지 저는, 그리고 케닉스 부인도 같은 마음이고 저만큼이나 절실히 느끼고 있습니다만, 그 애가 릴리빅이라 불리는 것이야말로 제 평생 가장 큰 축복이자 영광 가운데 하나라고 생각합니다.\""]], [["‘THE greatest blessing, Kenwigs,’ murmured his lady.", "\"가장 큰 축복이지요, 케닉스.\" 그의 아내가 나직이 거들었다."]], [["‘THE greatest blessing,’ said Mr.", "\"가장 큰 축복이지요.\""], ["Kenwigs, correcting himself.", "케닉스 씨가 자기 말을 고쳐 말했다."], ["‘A blessing that I hope, one of these days, I may be able to deserve.’", "\"언젠가 제가 그 축복을 누릴 자격을 갖출 수 있기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["This was a politic stroke of the Kenwigses, because it made Mr.", "이건 케닉스 부부의 영리한 한 수였다."], ["Lillyvick the great head and fountain of the baby’s importance.", "덕분에 릴리빅 씨가 그 아기의 귀함을 떠받치는 으뜸가는 머리이자 샘이 되었으니까."], ["The good gentleman felt the delicacy and dexterity of the touch, and at once proposed the health of the gentleman, name unknown, who had signalised himself, that night, by his coolness and alacrity.", "마음씨 좋은 그 신사는 이 손길의 세심함과 능란함을 알아채고는, 그 자리에서 이름은 모르지만 그날 밤 침착함과 민첩함으로 두드러진 활약을 보인 그 신사의 건강을 위해 건배를 청했다."]], [["‘Who, I don’t mind saying,’ observed Mr.", "\"한마디 덧붙이자면,\""], ["Lillyvick, as a great concession, ‘is a good-looking young man enough, with manners that I hope his character may be equal to.’", "릴리빅 씨가 큰 인심이라도 쓰듯 말했다. \"꽤 잘생긴 청년이고, 그 됨됨이도 부디 그 예의범절에 못지않기를 바라네.\""]], [["‘He has a very nice face and style, really,’ said Mrs.", "\"정말이지 얼굴도 아주 곱고 풍채도 좋더라고요.\""], ["Kenwigs.", "케닉스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘He certainly has,’ added Miss Petowker.", "\"정말 그래요.\" 페토커 양이 덧붙였다."], ["‘There’s something in his appearance quite--dear, dear, what’s that word again?’", "\"그 사람 어딘가에 아주… 어머나, 그 말이 뭐였더라?\""]], [["‘What word?’ inquired Mr.", "\"무슨 말이오?\""], ["Lillyvick.", "릴리빅 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Why--dear me, how stupid I am,’ replied Miss Petowker, hesitating.", "\"그게… 어머, 내 정신 좀 봐.\""], ["‘What do you call it, when Lords break off door-knockers and beat policemen, and play at coaches with other people’s money, and all that sort of thing?’", "페토커 양이 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"그, 귀족들이 문고리를 부수고 경관들을 두들겨 패고, 남의 돈으로 마차놀이를 하고, 그런 부류의 짓을 다 할 때 뭐라고 부르죠?\""]], [["‘Aristocratic?’ suggested the collector.", "\"귀족적이라고?\" 징수원이 거들었다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아! 귀족적이요.\""], ["aristocratic,’ replied Miss Petowker; ‘something very aristocratic about him, isn’t there?’", "페토커 양이 대답했다. \"그 사람한테 뭔가 아주 귀족적인 데가 있죠, 안 그래요?\""]], [["The gentleman held their peace, and smiled at each other, as who should say, ‘Well! there’s no accounting for tastes;’ but the ladies resolved unanimously that Nicholas had an aristocratic air; and nobody caring to dispute the position, it was established triumphantly.", "남자들은 입을 다문 채 서로를 보며 빙긋 웃었다. 마치 \"흠! 취향이야 뭐라 할 수 없는 거지\" 하고 말하려는 듯이. 하지만 부인들은 한목소리로 니콜라스에게 귀족적인 분위기가 있다고 결론지었다. 그리고 그 주장에 굳이 토를 다는 사람이 없으니, 그것은 보란 듯이 확정되었다."]], [["The punch being, by this time, drunk out, and the little Kenwigses (who had for some time previously held their little eyes open with their little forefingers) becoming fractious, and requesting rather urgently to be put to bed, the collector made a move by pulling out his watch, and acquainting the company that it was nigh two o’clock; whereat some of the guests were surprised and others shocked, and hats and bonnets being groped for under the tables, and in course of time found, their owners went away, after a vast deal of shaking of hands, and many remarks how they had never spent such a delightful evening, and how they marvelled to find it so late, expecting to have heard that it was half-past ten at the very latest, and how they wished that Mr.", "이쯤 되니 펀치도 다 마셔 버렸고, 어린 케닉스네 아이들도(아까부터 한참을 제 작은 집게손가락으로 작은 눈을 억지로 떠 받치고 있던 터였다) 보채기 시작하며 어서 재워 달라고 꽤 다급하게 졸라 댔다. 그러자 징수원이 회중시계를 꺼내 들고는, 곧 두 시가 다 되어 간다고 좌중에 알리며 자리를 뜰 채비를 했다."], ["and Mrs Kenwigs had a wedding-day once a week, and how they wondered by what hidden agency Mrs.", "그 말에 손님들 중 더러는 놀라고 더러는 기겁했다. 그리고 모자며 보닛을 식탁 밑이며 여기저기 더듬어 찾아 한참 만에야 찾아낸 뒤, 임자들은 길을 나섰다."], ["Kenwigs could possibly have managed so well; and a great deal more of the same kind. To all of which flattering expressions, Mr.", "수없이 손을 맞잡고, 이렇게 즐거운 저녁은 처음이라느니, 시간이 이토록 늦은 줄 몰랐다느니, 아무리 늦어도 열 시 반쯤일 줄 알았다느니, 케닉스 부부에게 일주일에 한 번씩 결혼기념일이 있으면 좋겠다느니, 케닉스 부인이 대체 무슨 숨은 재주로 이리도 잘 차려 냈는지 모르겠다느니, 그런 말들을 잔뜩 늘어놓으면서."], ["and Mrs. Kenwigs replied, by thanking every lady and gentleman, SERIATIM, for the favour of their company, and hoping they might have enjoyed themselves only half as well as they said they had.", "이 모든 듣기 좋은 말에 케닉스 부부는, 신사 숙녀 한 사람 한 사람에게 차례차례 와 주셔서 고맙다고 인사하고, 부디 말씀하신 만큼의 절반만이라도 즐거우셨기를 바란다고 답했다."]], [["As to Nicholas, quite unconscious of the impression he had produced, he had long since fallen asleep, leaving Mr.", "니콜라스로 말하자면, 자기가 남긴 인상은 전혀 모른 채 진작에 잠들어 버렸고, 뉴먼 노그스 씨와 스마이크에게 둘이서 술병을 비우도록 내맡겨 둔 터였다."], ["Newman Noggs and Smike to empty the spirit bottle between them; and this office they performed with such extreme good-will, that Newman was equally at a loss to determine whether he himself was quite sober, and whether he had ever seen any gentleman so heavily, drowsily, and completely intoxicated as his new acquaintance.", "그리고 둘은 어찌나 흔쾌히 그 일을 해치웠던지, 뉴먼은 제정신이 제대로 박혀 있는지조차 알 수 없을 지경이었고, 또 그 새 친구만큼 깊이, 나른하게, 흠뻑 취한 신사를 여태 본 적이 있던가 도무지 가늠이 안 될 정도였다."]], [["# CHAPTER 16", "# CHAPTER 16"]], [["Nicholas seeks to employ himself in a New Capacity, and being unsuccessful, accepts an engagement as Tutor in a Private Family\nThe first care of Nicholas, next morning, was, to look after some room in which, until better times dawned upon him, he could contrive to exist, without trenching upon the hospitality of Newman Noggs, who would have slept upon the stairs with pleasure, so that his young friend was accommodated.", "이튿날 아침, 니콜라스가 가장 먼저 한 일은 방을 하나 알아보는 거였어. 형편이 나아질 때까지 그럭저럭 지낼 만한 방 말이야. 뉴먼 노그스의 호의에 더 기대고 싶지 않았거든. 사실 뉴먼은 어린 친구가 편히 묵을 수만 있다면 기꺼이 계단에서라도 잘 사람이었지."]], [["The vacant apartment to which the bill in the parlour window bore reference, appeared, on inquiry, to be a small back-room on the second floor, reclaimed from the leads, and overlooking a soot-bespeckled prospect of tiles and chimney-pots. For the letting of this portion of the house from week to week, on reasonable terms, the parlour lodger was empowered to treat; he being deputed by the landlord to dispose of the rooms as they became vacant, and to keep a sharp look-out that the lodgers didn’t run away. As a means of securing the punctual discharge of which last service he was permitted to live rent-free, lest he should at any time be tempted to run away himself.", "응접실 창문에 붙은 광고가 가리키는 빈방은, 알아보니 삼층 뒤쪽의 작은 방이었어. 지붕 밑 공간을 막아 만든 방이라, 그을음 낀 기와지붕과 굴뚝들이 내다보이는 게 풍경의 전부였지. 이 방을 일주일 단위로 적당한 값에 세놓는 건 응접실에 사는 하숙인이 맡고 있었어. 집주인이 그에게 빈방이 생길 때마다 처리하라고 시킨 거야. 그리고 하숙인들이 도망가지 않게 잘 지켜보라고도 했지. 이 마지막 임무를 빈틈없이 해내라는 뜻에서, 그는 방세를 안 내고 살 수 있었어. 혹시라도 그 자신이 도망갈 마음이 들지 않게 하려는 거였지."]], [["Of this chamber, Nicholas became the tenant; and having hired a few common articles of furniture from a neighbouring broker, and paid the first week’s hire in advance, out of a small fund raised by the conversion of some spare clothes into ready money, he sat himself down to ruminate upon his prospects, which, like the prospect outside his window, were sufficiently confined and dingy. As they by no means improved on better acquaintance, and as familiarity breeds contempt, he resolved to banish them from his thoughts by dint of hard walking. So, taking up his hat, and leaving poor Smike to arrange and rearrange the room with as much delight as if it had been the costliest palace, he betook himself to the streets, and mingled with the crowd which thronged them.", "니콜라스는 이 방의 세입자가 되었어. 그러고는 근처 중고품 가게에서 흔한 가구 몇 개를 빌리고, 첫 주 방세를 미리 냈지. 여분의 옷가지를 돈으로 바꿔 마련한 얼마 안 되는 돈에서 낸 거야. 그러고는 자리에 앉아 앞날을 곰곰이 생각해 봤어. 그런데 그 앞날이라는 게, 창밖 풍경처럼 답답하고 칙칙하기 짝이 없었지. 들여다볼수록 나아지기는커녕, 자꾸 보면 정이 떨어진다는 말처럼 더 싫어지기만 했어. 그래서 그는 부지런히 걷기라도 해서 그 생각을 머릿속에서 몰아내기로 했지. 모자를 챙겨 들고, 가엾은 스마이크는 방에 남겨 둔 채 말이야. 스마이크는 그 방이 세상에서 제일 값진 궁전이라도 되는 양 신이 나서 가구를 이리저리 놓아 보고 있었거든. 니콜라스는 거리로 나서서 북적이는 사람들 틈에 섞여 들었어."]], [["Although a man may lose a sense of his own importance when he is a mere unit among a busy throng, all utterly regardless of him, it by no means follows that he can dispossess himself, with equal facility, of a very strong sense of the importance and magnitude of his cares. The unhappy state of his own affairs was the one idea which occupied the brain of Nicholas, walk as fast as he would; and when he tried to dislodge it by speculating on the situation and prospects of the people who surrounded him, he caught himself, in a few seconds, contrasting their condition with his own, and gliding almost imperceptibly back into his old train of thought again.", "바쁘게 오가는 사람들 틈에 끼어 아무도 거들떠보지 않는 한 점 같은 존재가 되면, 자기가 대단한 사람이라는 느낌은 사라질지도 몰라. 하지만 그렇다고 자기 걱정거리가 얼마나 무겁고 큰지에 대한 절절한 느낌까지 그렇게 쉽게 떨쳐 낼 수 있는 건 아니야. 아무리 빨리 걸어도, 니콜라스의 머릿속을 차지한 건 오직 자기 처지가 얼마나 딱한가 하는 생각 하나뿐이었지. 그래서 주위 사람들의 처지와 앞날을 헤아려 보며 그 생각을 밀어내 보려고 했어. 그런데 몇 초도 안 돼서, 어느새 그들의 형편을 자기 것과 견주고 있는 자신을 발견하는 거야. 그러고는 자기도 모르게 슬그머니 다시 원래의 생각으로 미끄러져 들어갔지."]], [["Occupied in these reflections, as he was making his way along one of the great public thoroughfares of London, he chanced to raise his eyes to a blue board, whereon was inscribed, in characters of gold, ‘General Agency Office; for places and situations of all kinds inquire within.’ It was a shop-front, fitted up with a gauze blind and an inner door; and in the window hung a long and tempting array of written placards, announcing vacant places of every grade, from a secretary’s to a foot-boy’s.", "이런 생각에 잠긴 채 런던의 큰 거리 하나를 걸어가던 그가, 우연히 파란 간판으로 눈길을 들었어. 거기엔 금빛 글씨로 이렇게 적혀 있었지. \"종합 직업소개소. 온갖 종류의 일자리와 자리, 안에서 문의 바람.\" 망사 차양과 안쪽 문이 달린 가게였어. 그리고 창문에는 손으로 쓴 광고지가 길게 줄지어 솔깃하게 걸려 있었지. 비서 자리부터 사환 자리까지, 온갖 등급의 빈자리를 알리는 광고였어."]], [["Nicholas halted, instinctively, before this temple of promise, and ran his eye over the capital-text openings in life which were so profusely displayed.", "니콜라스는 약속이 가득한 이 신전 앞에 저도 모르게 멈춰 섰어. 그러고는 큼직한 글씨로 잔뜩 내걸린 인생의 기회들을 쭉 훑어봤지."], ["When he had completed his survey he walked on a little way, and then back, and then on again; at length, after pausing irresolutely several times before the door of the General Agency Office, he made up his mind, and stepped in.", "다 보고 나서 그는 조금 걸어가다가, 다시 돌아왔다가, 또 걸어갔어. 그러다 마침내 소개소 문 앞에서 몇 번이나 망설이며 서성이다가, 마음을 굳히고 안으로 들어섰지."]], [["He found himself in a little floor-clothed room, with a high desk railed off in one corner, behind which sat a lean youth with cunning eyes and a protruding chin, whose performances in capital-text darkened the window. He had a thick ledger lying open before him, and with the fingers of his right hand inserted between the leaves, and his eyes fixed on a very fat old lady in a mob-cap--evidently the proprietress of the establishment--who was airing herself at the fire, seemed to be only waiting her directions to refer to some entries contained within its rusty clasps.", "그가 들어선 곳은 바닥에 천을 깐 작은 방이었어. 한쪽 구석엔 난간을 두른 높직한 책상이 있었고, 그 뒤에 마른 청년 하나가 앉아 있었지. 교활해 보이는 눈에 턱이 툭 튀어나온 그 청년이, 창문을 가린 그 큼직한 글씨들을 쓴 장본인이었어. 그 앞에는 두툼한 장부가 펼쳐져 있었어. 오른손 손가락을 책장 사이에 끼우고, 눈은 챙 달린 모자를 쓴 아주 뚱뚱한 노부인에게 박혀 있었지. 누가 봐도 이 가게의 주인이었어. 부인은 난롯가에서 몸을 쬐고 있었지. 청년은 부인의 지시만 떨어지면 녹슨 잠금쇠 속에 든 기록을 들춰 볼 태세였어."]], [["As there was a board outside, which acquainted the public that servants-of-all-work were perpetually in waiting to be hired from ten till four, Nicholas knew at once that some half-dozen strong young women, each with pattens and an umbrella, who were sitting upon a form in one corner, were in attendance for that purpose: especially as the poor things looked anxious and weary. He was not quite so certain of the callings and stations of two smart young ladies who were in conversation with the fat lady before the fire, until--having sat himself down in a corner, and remarked that he would wait until the other customers had been served--the fat lady resumed the dialogue which his entrance had interrupted.", "밖에는 안내판이 하나 걸려 있었어. 열 시부터 네 시까지 온갖 허드렛일을 할 하녀들이 언제든 고용을 기다리고 있다고 알리는 거였지. 그래서 니콜라스는 한쪽 구석 긴 의자에 앉아 있는 건장한 젊은 여자 여섯쯤이, 바로 그 목적으로 와 있다는 걸 대번에 알았어. 저마다 나막신과 우산을 챙긴 데다, 그 가엾은 여자들이 다들 불안하고 지친 기색이었거든. 하지만 난롯가에서 뚱뚱한 부인과 이야기를 나누는 말쑥한 젊은 아가씨 둘은 무슨 일을 하는 어떤 사람들인지 잘 알 수가 없었어. 그러다 니콜라스가 구석에 앉아, 다른 손님들이 다 끝날 때까지 기다리겠다고 말하자, 뚱뚱한 부인이 그가 들어오는 바람에 끊겼던 대화를 다시 이어 갔지."]], [["‘Cook, Tom,’ said the fat lady, still airing herself as aforesaid.", "\"요리사라고, 톰.\" 뚱뚱한 부인이 아까처럼 여전히 몸을 쬐며 말했어."]], [["‘Cook,’ said Tom, turning over some leaves of the ledger.", "\"요리사요.\" 톰이 장부 몇 장을 넘기며 말했어."], ["‘Well!’", "\"어디 보자!\""]], [["‘Read out an easy place or two,’ said the fat lady.", "\"쉬운 자리로 한두 개 읽어 봐.\" 뚱뚱한 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘Pick out very light ones, if you please, young man,’ interposed a genteel female, in shepherd’s-plaid boots, who appeared to be the client.", "\"아주 일 적은 데로 골라 주세요, 젊은 양반.\" 양치기 격자무늬 부츠를 신은 점잖은 여자가 끼어들었어. 손님인 모양이었지."]], [["‘“Mrs.", "\"마커 부인.\""], ["Marker,”’ said Tom, reading, ‘“Russell Place, Russell Square; offers eighteen guineas; tea and sugar found.", "톰이 읽었어. \"러셀 스퀘어의 러셀 플레이스."], ["Two in family, and see very little company.", "십팔 기니 제시. 차와 설탕 제공."], ["Five servants kept.", "식구는 둘, 손님 거의 없음."], ["No man.", "하인 다섯."], ["No followers.”’", "남자 하인 없음. 따라다니는 남자 없을 것.\""]], [["‘Oh Lor!’ tittered the client.", "\"어머나!\" 손님이 키득거렸어."], ["‘THAT won’t do.", "\"그건 안 되겠네."], ["Read another, young man, will you?’", "다른 거 읽어 줘 봐요, 젊은 양반.\""]], [["‘“Mrs.", "\"라이먹 부인.\""], ["Wrymug,”’ said Tom, ‘“Pleasant Place, Finsbury.", "톰이 말했어. \"핀스버리의 플레전트 플레이스."], ["Wages, twelve guineas.", "급료 십이 기니."], ["No tea, no sugar.", "차 없음, 설탕 없음."], ["Serious family--“’", "독실한 집안—\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["you needn’t mind reading that,’ interrupted the client.", "그건 안 읽어도 돼요.\" 손님이 말을 끊었어."]], [["‘“Three serious footmen,”’ said Tom, impressively.", "\"독실한 하인 셋.\" 톰이 무게를 잡고 말했어."]], [["‘Three?", "\"셋이라고?"], ["did you say?’ asked the client in an altered tone.", "셋이라 했어요?\" 손님이 어조를 바꿔 물었어."]], [["‘Three serious footmen,’ replied Tom. ‘“Cook, housemaid, and nursemaid; each female servant required to join the Little Bethel Congregation three times every Sunday--with a serious footman. If the cook is more serious than the footman, she will be expected to improve the footman; if the footman is more serious than the cook, he will be expected to improve the cook.”’", "\"독실한 하인 셋입니다.\" 톰이 대답했어. \"요리사, 하녀, 보모. 여자 하인은 저마다 일요일마다 세 번씩 리틀 베델 교회에 나가야 함. 독실한 하인과 함께. 만약 요리사가 하인보다 더 독실하면 하인을 일깨워 줄 것. 만약 하인이 요리사보다 더 독실하면 요리사를 일깨워 줄 것.\""]], [["‘I’ll take the address of that place,’ said the client; ‘I don’t know but what it mightn’t suit me pretty well.’", "\"그 집 주소를 받아 가야겠네.\" 손님이 말했어. \"나한테 꽤 잘 맞을지도 모르겠으니까.\""]], [["‘Here’s another,’ remarked Tom, turning over the leaves.", "\"여기 또 있어요.\" 톰이 책장을 넘기며 말했어."], ["‘“Family of Mr Gallanbile, MP.", "\"하원 의원 갤런바일 씨 댁."], ["Fifteen guineas, tea and sugar, and servants allowed to see male cousins, if godly.", "십오 기니에 차와 설탕 제공. 하인이 신앙심 깊은 사촌이라면 남자 사촌 면회 허용."], ["Note.", "참고."], ["Cold dinner in the kitchen on the Sabbath, Mr.", "안식일에는 부엌에서 찬 음식만."], ["Gallanbile being devoted to the Observance question.", "갤런바일 씨가 안식일 준수 문제에 헌신하기 때문임."], ["No victuals whatever cooked on the Lord’s Day, with the exception of dinner for Mr. and Mrs.", "주님의 날에는 어떤 음식도 조리 금지."], ["Gallanbile, which, being a work of piety and necessity, is exempted. Mr.", "단, 갤런바일 부부의 식사는 경건하고 꼭 필요한 일이므로 예외로 함."], ["Gallanbile dines late on the day of rest, in order to prevent the sinfulness of the cook’s dressing herself.”’", "갤런바일 씨는 안식일에 늦게 식사함. 요리사가 몸치장하는 죄를 막기 위해서임.\""]], [["‘I don’t think that’ll answer as well as the other,’ said the client, after a little whispering with her friend.", "\"그건 아까 그 집만큼 좋진 않을 것 같네.\" 손님이 친구와 잠깐 속닥거리고 나서 말했어."], ["‘I’ll take the other direction, if you please, young man.", "\"아까 그 주소를 받아 갈게요, 젊은 양반."], ["I can but come back again, if it don’t do.’", "안 맞으면 다시 오면 되니까.\""]], [["Tom made out the address, as requested, and the genteel client, having satisfied the fat lady with a small fee, meanwhile, went away accompanied by her friend.", "톰은 부탁받은 대로 주소를 적어 줬어. 그리고 점잖은 손님은 그새 뚱뚱한 부인에게 적은 수수료를 건네 만족시키고는, 친구와 함께 나갔지."]], [["As Nicholas opened his mouth, to request the young man to turn to letter S, and let him know what secretaryships remained undisposed of, there came into the office an applicant, in whose favour he immediately retired, and whose appearance both surprised and interested him.", "니콜라스가 입을 열어, 청년에게 에스 항목을 펼쳐서 아직 남아 있는 비서 자리가 있는지 알려 달라고 부탁하려는 참이었어. 그때 한 손님이 소개소로 들어왔지. 그는 곧바로 양보하고 물러섰어. 그런데 그 사람의 모습이 그를 놀라게 하는 동시에 흥미를 끌었지."]], [["This was a young lady who could be scarcely eighteen, of very slight and delicate figure, but exquisitely shaped, who, walking timidly up to the desk, made an inquiry, in a very low tone of voice, relative to some situation as governess, or companion to a lady. She raised her veil, for an instant, while she preferred the inquiry, and disclosed a countenance of most uncommon beauty, though shaded by a cloud of sadness, which, in one so young, was doubly remarkable. Having received a card of reference to some person on the books, she made the usual acknowledgment, and glided away.", "이제 막 열여덟쯤 됐을까 싶은 젊은 아가씨였어. 몸매가 아주 가냘프고 여렸지만, 더없이 곱게 빠진 모습이었지. 아가씨는 머뭇거리며 책상으로 다가가더니, 아주 나직한 목소리로 물었어. 가정교사나 귀부인의 말동무로 갈 만한 자리가 있는지 묻는 거였지. 아가씨는 묻는 동안 잠깐 베일을 들어 올렸어. 그러자 보기 드물게 아름다운 얼굴이 드러났지. 다만 그 얼굴엔 슬픔의 그림자가 어려 있었어. 그렇게 어린 사람한테 그런 그늘이 있다는 게 더욱 눈에 띄었지. 아가씨는 장부에 적힌 어떤 사람에게 보내는 소개장을 받아 들고는, 으레 하는 인사를 하고 미끄러지듯 나갔어."]], [["She was neatly, but very quietly attired; so much so, indeed, that it seemed as though her dress, if it had been worn by one who imparted fewer graces of her own to it, might have looked poor and shabby. Her attendant--for she had one--was a red-faced, round-eyed, slovenly girl, who, from a certain roughness about the bare arms that peeped from under her draggled shawl, and the half-washed-out traces of smut and blacklead which tattooed her countenance, was clearly of a kin with the servants-of-all-work on the form: between whom and herself there had passed various grins and glances, indicative of the freemasonry of the craft.", "아가씨는 단정했지만 아주 수수하게 차려입고 있었어. 어찌나 수수한지, 자기만의 우아함을 덜 지닌 사람이 그 옷을 입었더라면 초라하고 후줄근해 보였을 것 같을 정도였지. 아가씨에겐 시중드는 아이가 하나 있었어. 얼굴 붉고 눈이 동그란, 칠칠치 못한 여자아이였지. 끌려 내려온 숄 밑으로 드러난 맨팔이 거칠어 보였고, 얼굴엔 검댕과 흑연 자국이 채 안 씻겨 문신처럼 남아 있었어. 누가 봐도 긴 의자에 앉은 허드렛일 하녀들과 한패였지. 그 아이와 하녀들 사이에 오가는 갖가지 히죽거림과 눈짓이, 같은 일을 하는 사람들끼리의 은밀한 동지애를 드러내고 있었거든."]], [["This girl followed her mistress; and, before Nicholas had recovered from the first effects of his surprise and admiration, the young lady was gone.", "이 아이가 제 주인을 따라 나갔어. 그리고 니콜라스가 놀라움과 감탄에서 미처 헤어나기도 전에, 젊은 아가씨는 가 버렸지."], ["It is not a matter of such complete and utter improbability as some sober people may think, that he would have followed them out, had he not been restrained by what passed between the fat lady and her book-keeper.", "만약 뚱뚱한 부인과 장부 담당 청년 사이에 오간 말이 그를 붙잡아 두지 않았더라면, 그가 그들을 따라 나갔을지도 몰라. 이건 점잖은 사람들이 생각하는 것만큼 그렇게 말도 안 되는 일은 아니야."]], [["‘When is she coming again, Tom?’ asked the fat lady.", "\"저 아가씨 언제 다시 온대, 톰?\" 뚱뚱한 부인이 물었어."]], [["‘Tomorrow morning,’ replied Tom, mending his pen.", "\"내일 아침요.\" 톰이 펜을 다듬으며 대답했어."]], [["‘Where have you sent her to?’ asked the fat lady.", "\"어디로 보냈는데?\" 뚱뚱한 부인이 물었어."]], [["‘Mrs.", "\"클라크 부인 댁요.\""], ["Clark’s,’ replied Tom.", "톰이 대답했어."]], [["‘She’ll have a nice life of it, if she goes there,’ observed the fat lady, taking a pinch of snuff from a tin box.", "\"거기 가면 팔자 한번 편하겠구먼.\" 뚱뚱한 부인이 양철통에서 코담배를 한 줌 집으며 말했어."]], [["Tom made no other reply than thrusting his tongue into his cheek, and pointing the feather of his pen towards Nicholas--reminders which elicited from the fat lady an inquiry, of ‘Now, sir, what can we do for YOU?’", "톰은 별다른 대꾸 없이 혀를 볼 안쪽으로 밀어 넣고는, 펜 깃털로 니콜라스 쪽을 가리켰어. 그 신호에 뚱뚱한 부인이 물었지. \"자, 손님, 무슨 일로 오셨수?\""]], [["Nicholas briefly replied, that he wanted to know whether there was any such post to be had, as secretary or amanuensis to a gentleman.", "니콜라스는 짤막하게 대답했어. 신사의 비서나 필경사 같은 자리가 있는지 알고 싶다고 말이야."]], [["‘Any such!’ rejoined the mistress; ‘a-dozen-such.", "\"그런 자리요!\" 주인이 받았어."], ["An’t there, Tom?’", "\"그런 거라면 열 개도 넘지. 안 그래, 톰?\""]], [["‘I should think so,’ answered that young gentleman; and as he said it, he winked towards Nicholas, with a degree of familiarity which he, no doubt, intended for a rather flattering compliment, but with which Nicholas was most ungratefully disgusted.", "\"그럴걸요.\" 그 젊은 청년이 대답했어. 그러면서 그는 니콜라스를 향해 윙크를 했지. 친근함을 한껏 담은 윙크였는데, 본인은 분명 꽤나 듣기 좋은 인사라고 여겼겠지만, 니콜라스는 고맙기는커녕 진저리가 났어."]], [["Upon reference to the book, it appeared that the dozen secretaryships had dwindled down to one.", "장부를 들춰 보니, 열 개도 넘던 비서 자리가 결국 하나로 쪼그라들어 있었지."], ["Mr. Gregsbury, the great member of parliament, of Manchester Buildings, Westminster, wanted a young man, to keep his papers and correspondence in order; and Nicholas was exactly the sort of young man that Mr.", "웨스트민스터 맨체스터 빌딩에 사는 거물 하원 의원 그렉스버리 씨가, 서류와 서신을 정리해 줄 젊은이를 구하고 있다는 거였어."], ["Gregsbury wanted.", "그리고 니콜라스야말로 그렉스버리 씨가 찾는 바로 그런 젊은이였지."]], [["‘I don’t know what the terms are, as he said he’d settle them himself with the party,’ observed the fat lady; ‘but they must be pretty good ones, because he’s a member of parliament.’", "\"조건이 어떤지는 몰라요. 본인이 직접 당사자랑 정하겠다고 했으니까.\" 뚱뚱한 부인이 말했어. \"하지만 꽤 괜찮을 거유. 하원 의원이니까.\""]], [["Inexperienced as he was, Nicholas did not feel quite assured of the force of this reasoning, or the justice of this conclusion; but without troubling himself to question it, he took down the address, and resolved to wait upon Mr.", "니콜라스는 세상 경험이 없긴 했지만, 이 논리가 그렇게 그럴듯한지, 이 결론이 옳은지 영 확신이 안 섰어."], ["Gregsbury without delay.", "하지만 굳이 따지고 들 것 없이, 주소를 받아 적고는 곧장 그렉스버리 씨를 찾아가기로 마음먹었지."]], [["‘I don’t know what the number is,’ said Tom; ‘but Manchester Buildings isn’t a large place; and if the worst comes to the worst it won’t take you very long to knock at all the doors on both sides of the way till you find him out. I say, what a good-looking gal that was, wasn’t she?’", "\"번지수는 모르겠어요.\" 톰이 말했어. \"근데 맨체스터 빌딩이 큰 데가 아니거든요. 정 안 되면 양쪽 집 문을 죄다 두드려서 찾아도 그리 오래 안 걸릴 거예요. 그나저나, 아까 그 아가씨 참 예쁘지 않았어요?\""]], [["‘What girl?’ demanded Nicholas, sternly.", "\"무슨 아가씨?\" 니콜라스가 딱딱하게 물었어."]], [["‘Oh yes.", "\"아, 그래요."], ["I know--what gal, eh?’ whispered Tom, shutting one eye, and cocking his chin in the air.", "알지—무슨 아가씨, 응?\" 톰이 한쪽 눈을 감고 턱을 치켜들며 속삭였어."], ["‘You didn’t see her, you didn’t--I say, don’t you wish you was me, when she comes tomorrow morning?’", "\"못 봤죠, 못 봤어—참, 내일 아침 그 아가씨 오면, 나처럼 돼 봤으면 싶지 않아요?\""]], [["Nicholas looked at the ugly clerk, as if he had a mind to reward his admiration of the young lady by beating the ledger about his ears, but he refrained, and strode haughtily out of the office; setting at defiance, in his indignation, those ancient laws of chivalry, which not only made it proper and lawful for all good knights to hear the praise of the ladies to whom they were devoted, but rendered it incumbent upon them to roam about the world, and knock at head all such matter-of-fact and un-poetical characters, as declined to exalt, above all the earth, damsels whom they had never chanced to look upon or hear of--as if that were any excuse!", "니콜라스는 그 못생긴 점원을 노려봤어. 마치 아가씨를 향한 그의 감탄에 대한 보답으로 장부를 그의 귀싸대기에 패대기쳐 주고 싶은 표정이었지. 하지만 그는 참았어. 그러고는 거만하게 소개소를 성큼성큼 빠져나왔지. 분노에 찬 나머지, 옛 기사도의 법도 따위는 깡그리 무시한 채로 말이야. 그 법도에 따르면, 모든 훌륭한 기사는 자기가 흠모하는 귀부인을 칭송하는 말을 마땅히 들어야 옳고, 그건 적법한 일이었지. 게다가 세상을 떠돌며, 한 번 본 적도 들은 적도 없는 처녀를 세상 그 무엇보다 떠받들기를 거부하는, 그런 메마르고 시적이지 못한 자들을 죄다 때려눕히는 것이 기사의 의무이기도 했어. 마치 한 번도 본 적 없다는 게 무슨 핑계라도 된다는 듯이!"]], [["Thinking no longer of his own misfortunes, but wondering what could be those of the beautiful girl he had seen, Nicholas, with many wrong turns, and many inquiries, and almost as many misdirections, bent his steps towards the place whither he had been directed.", "니콜라스는 더 이상 자기 불행은 생각하지 않았어. 그저 자기가 본 그 아름다운 아가씨의 불행은 무엇일까 궁금해할 뿐이었지. 그러면서 그는 여러 번 길을 잘못 들고, 여러 번 길을 묻고, 거의 그만큼 잘못된 길 안내를 받아 가며, 일러 준 곳으로 발걸음을 옮겼어."]], [["Within the precincts of the ancient city of Westminster, and within half a quarter of a mile of its ancient sanctuary, is a narrow and dirty region, the sanctuary of the smaller members of Parliament in modern days. It is all comprised in one street of gloomy lodging-houses, from whose windows, in vacation-time, there frown long melancholy rows of bills, which say, as plainly as did the countenances of their occupiers, ranged on ministerial and opposition benches in the session which slumbers with its fathers, ‘To Let’, ‘To Let’. In busier periods of the year these bills disappear, and the houses swarm with legislators. There are legislators in the parlours, in the first floor, in the second, in the third, in the garrets; the small apartments reek with the breath of deputations and delegates. In damp weather, the place is rendered close, by the steams of moist acts of parliament and frouzy petitions; general postmen grow faint as they enter its infected limits, and shabby figures in quest of franks, flit restlessly to and fro like the troubled ghosts of Complete Letter-writers departed. This is Manchester Buildings; and here, at all hours of the night, may be heard the rattling of latch-keys in their respective keyholes: with now and then--when a gust of wind sweeping across the water which washes the Buildings’ feet, impels the sound towards its entrance--the weak, shrill voice of some young member practising tomorrow’s speech. All the livelong day, there is a grinding of organs and clashing and clanging of little boxes of music; for Manchester Buildings is an eel-pot, which has no outlet but its awkward mouth--a case-bottle which has no thoroughfare, and a short and narrow neck--and in this respect it may be typical of the fate of some few among its more adventurous residents, who, after wriggling themselves into Parliament by violent efforts and contortions, find that it, too, is no thoroughfare for them; that, like Manchester Buildings, it leads to nothing beyond itself; and that they are fain at last to back out, no wiser, no richer, not one whit more famous, than they went in.", "웨스트민스터라는 오래된 도시 구역 안, 그 옛 성역에서 십 분의 일 마일도 안 되는 곳에, 좁고 지저분한 동네가 하나 있어. 요즘 들어서는 군소 하원 의원들의 도피처가 된 곳이지. 음침한 하숙집들이 늘어선 거리 하나가 전부야. 의회가 쉬는 동안엔 그 집 창문마다 침울한 광고지가 길게 줄지어 인상을 찌푸리고 있어. 그 광고지들은, 마치 이제는 조상 곁에 잠든 회기에 여당석과 야당석에 늘어앉았던 의원들 얼굴이 그러했던 것처럼, 또렷하게 이렇게 말하지. \"세놓음\", \"세놓음.\" 한 해 중 좀 더 바쁜 철이 되면 이 광고지들은 사라지고, 집집마다 입법자들이 우글거려. 응접실에도, 이층에도, 삼층에도, 사층에도, 다락방에도 입법자들이 있지. 그 좁은 방들이 진정서를 들고 온 대표단과 사절들의 입김으로 후끈해. 축축한 날씨엔 이곳이 답답해지지. 눅눅한 법안과 퀴퀴한 청원서에서 김이 풀풀 올라오거든. 우체부들은 이 병든 구역에 발을 들이면 정신이 아득해지고, 무료 우편 허가를 얻으려는 추레한 사람들이 안절부절못하며 이리저리 떠돌아. 마치 죽어 버린 서간문집의 떠도는 유령들처럼 말이야. 이곳이 바로 맨체스터 빌딩이야. 그리고 여기서는 밤 어느 때고, 제각각의 열쇠 구멍에서 빗장 열쇠가 달그락거리는 소리가 들리지. 가끔은—빌딩 발치를 적시는 물 위로 바람이 휙 불어와 그 소리를 입구 쪽으로 떠밀 때면—내일 할 연설을 연습하는 어느 젊은 의원의 가냘프고 새된 목소리가 섞여 들기도 해. 온종일 손풍금 소리가 갈리듯 울리고, 자그마한 음악 상자들이 쨍그랑 댕그랑 부딪혀. 맨체스터 빌딩이란 어설픈 아가리 말고는 출구가 없는 장어 통발 같은 곳이거든. 통로 없이 짧고 좁은 목만 달린 사각 병 같은 곳이지. 이 점에서 이곳은, 좀 더 모험심 강한 거주자 몇몇의 운명을 상징하는지도 몰라. 그들은 격렬하게 몸부림치고 비틀어 가며 의회에 비집고 들어가지만, 곧 깨닫게 되거든. 의회 역시 자기들한테는 출구 없는 막다른 곳이라는 걸. 맨체스터 빌딩처럼, 의회도 그 자체 너머로는 어디로도 이어지지 않는다는 걸 말이야. 그래서 그들은 결국 뒷걸음질로 빠져나올 수밖에 없어. 들어갈 때보다 조금도 더 똑똑해지지도, 부유해지지도, 한 톨만큼도 더 유명해지지도 못한 채로."]], [["Into Manchester Buildings Nicholas turned, with the address of the great Mr.", "니콜라스는 거물 그렉스버리 씨의 주소를 손에 들고 맨체스터 빌딩으로 들어섰어."], ["Gregsbury in his hand.", "입구에서 멀지 않은 어느 허름한 집으로 사람들이 줄줄이 밀려 들어가고 있었지."], ["As there was a stream of people pouring into a shabby house not far from the entrance, he waited until they had made their way in, and then making up to the servant, ventured to inquire if he knew where Mr. Gregsbury lived.", "그래서 그는 그들이 다 들어갈 때까지 기다렸다가, 하인에게 다가가 그렉스버리 씨가 어디 사는지 아느냐고 조심스레 물었어."]], [["The servant was a very pale, shabby boy, who looked as if he had slept underground from his infancy, as very likely he had.", "하인은 아주 창백하고 추레한 소년이었어. 갓난아기 때부터 땅속에서 잠을 잔 듯한 몰골이었지."], ["‘Mr. Gregsbury?’ said he; ‘Mr.", "실제로 그랬을 가능성이 컸어."], ["Gregsbury lodges here.", "\"그렉스버리 씨요?\" 소년이 말했어."], ["It’s all right.", "\"그렉스버리 씨는 여기 사세요. 맞아요."], ["Come in!’", "들어오세요!\""]], [["Nicholas thought he might as well get in while he could, so in he walked; and he had no sooner done so, than the boy shut the door, and made off.", "니콜라스는 들어갈 수 있을 때 들어가는 게 낫겠다 싶어, 안으로 걸어 들어갔어. 그런데 그가 들어서기 무섭게, 소년이 문을 닫고 사라져 버렸지."]], [["This was odd enough: but what was more embarrassing was, that all along the passage, and all along the narrow stairs, blocking up the window, and making the dark entry darker still, was a confused crowd of persons with great importance depicted in their looks; who were, to all appearance, waiting in silent expectation of some coming event. From time to time, one man would whisper to his neighbour, or a little group would whisper together, and then the whisperers would nod fiercely to each other, or give their heads a relentless shake, as if they were bent upon doing something very desperate, and were determined not to be put off, whatever happened.", "이것만 해도 꽤 이상한 노릇이었어. 그런데 더 난처한 건, 복도를 따라, 또 좁은 계단을 따라, 창문을 막아서서 그렇잖아도 어두운 입구를 한층 더 어둡게 만들며, 사람들이 한데 뒤엉켜 북적이고 있다는 거였지. 다들 얼굴에 잔뜩 무게를 잡고 있었어. 보아하니 곧 닥칠 무슨 일을 말없이 기다리는 모양이었지. 이따금 한 사람이 옆 사람에게 귓속말을 하거나, 몇몇이 한데 모여 수군거렸어. 그러고는 수군대던 자들이 서로를 향해 사납게 고개를 끄덕이거나, 매정하게 고개를 절레절레 흔드는 거야. 마치 무슨 비장한 일을 작정하고서, 무슨 일이 있어도 물러서지 않겠다고 다짐한 사람들 같았지."]], [["As a few minutes elapsed without anything occurring to explain this phenomenon, and as he felt his own position a peculiarly uncomfortable one, Nicholas was on the point of seeking some information from the man next him, when a sudden move was visible on the stairs, and a voice was heard to cry, ‘Now, gentleman, have the goodness to walk up!’", "몇 분이 흘러도 이 광경을 설명해 줄 일이 아무것도 일어나지 않았어. 게다가 니콜라스는 자기 처지가 유난히 불편하게 느껴졌지. 그래서 그가 옆 사람에게 뭐라도 물어보려는 참이었어. 그때 계단 쪽에서 갑자기 움직임이 일더니, 누군가 외치는 소리가 들렸지. \"자, 여러분, 어서 올라오시지요!\""]], [["So far from walking up, the gentlemen on the stairs began to walk down with great alacrity, and to entreat, with extraordinary politeness, that the gentlemen nearest the street would go first; the gentlemen nearest the street retorted, with equal courtesy, that they couldn’t think of such a thing on any account; but they did it, without thinking of it, inasmuch as the other gentlemen pressing some half-dozen (among whom was Nicholas) forward, and closing up behind, pushed them, not merely up the stairs, but into the very sitting-room of Mr. Gregsbury, which they were thus compelled to enter with most unseemly precipitation, and without the means of retreat; the press behind them, more than filling the apartment.", "그런데 계단 위 사람들은 올라가기는커녕 잽싸게 내려오기 시작했어. 그러고는 더없이 정중하게, 거리에 가장 가까이 선 사람들더러 먼저 들어가시라고 청하는 거야. 거리에 가장 가까이 선 사람들도 똑같이 공손하게, 그런 일은 결코 있을 수 없다고 받아쳤지. 하지만 결국 본의 아니게 그렇게 되고 말았어. 다른 사람들이 여섯 명쯤(그중에 니콜라스도 있었지)을 앞으로 떠밀고 뒤에서 바짝 죄어 들었거든. 그래서 그들은 계단 위로 떠밀린 정도가 아니라, 그렉스버리 씨의 거실 안으로까지 떠밀려 들어갔어. 더없이 꼴사납게 허둥지둥, 빠져나갈 길도 없이 말이야. 뒤에서 밀려드는 사람들이 방을 넘치도록 가득 채웠지."]], [["‘Gentlemen,’ said Mr.", "\"여러분.\""], ["Gregsbury, ‘you are welcome.", "그렉스버리 씨가 말했어. \"잘 오셨습니다."], ["I am rejoiced to see you.’", "뵙게 되어 기쁩니다.\""]], [["For a gentleman who was rejoiced to see a body of visitors, Mr.", "손님들을 보고 기뻐하는 신사치고는, 그렉스버리 씨는 더없이 불편한 표정이었어. 하지만 어쩌면 그건 의원다운 근엄함과, 감정을 다스리는 정치가다운 습관 탓이었는지도 몰라."], ["Gregsbury looked as uncomfortable as might be; but perhaps this was occasioned by senatorial gravity, and a statesmanlike habit of keeping his feelings under control.", "그는 다부지고 우람하며 머리가 둔한 신사였어. 목소리는 우렁차고, 태도는 거들먹거렸지."], ["He was a tough, burly, thick-headed gentleman, with a loud voice, a pompous manner, a tolerable command of sentences with no meaning in them, and, in short, every requisite for a very good member indeed.", "알맹이 없는 말을 그럴싸하게 늘어놓는 솜씨도 제법이었어. 한마디로, 아주 훌륭한 의원이 되는 데 필요한 자질을 죄다 갖춘 셈이었지."]], [["‘Now, gentlemen,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 여러분.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 발치의 등나무 바구니에 두툼한 서류 뭉치를 던져 넣으며 말했어."], ["Gregsbury, tossing a great bundle of papers into a wicker basket at his feet, and throwing himself back in his chair with his arms over the elbows, ‘you are dissatisfied with my conduct, I see by the newspapers.’", "그러고는 팔걸이에 양팔을 걸친 채 의자에 등을 기댔지. \"신문을 보니, 여러분이 내 처신에 불만이 있으신 모양이군요.\""]], [["‘Yes, Mr.", "\"그렇습니다, 그렉스버리 씨, 불만입니다.\""], ["Gregsbury, we are,’ said a plump old gentleman in a violent heat, bursting out of the throng, and planting himself in the front.", "통통한 노신사 하나가 잔뜩 열을 올리며 군중 사이에서 튀어나와 맨 앞에 떡 버티고 섰어."]], [["‘Do my eyes deceive me,’ said Mr.", "\"내 눈이 잘못된 건가?\""], ["Gregsbury, looking towards the speaker, ‘or is that my old friend Pugstyles?’", "그렉스버리 씨가 말한 사람을 바라보며 말했어. \"아니면 저게 내 오랜 친구 퍼그스타일스인가?\""]], [["‘I am that man, and no other, sir,’ replied the plump old gentleman.", "\"바로 그 사람이올시다, 다른 누구도 아니고요.\" 통통한 노신사가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Give me your hand, my worthy friend,’ said Mr.", "\"악수합시다, 친애하는 벗이여.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어."], ["Gregsbury. ‘Pugstyles, my dear friend, I am very sorry to see you here.’", "\"퍼그스타일스, 친애하는 친구, 자네를 여기서 보다니 참으로 유감일세.\""]], [["‘I am very sorry to be here, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"저야말로 여기 오게 되어 유감입니다.\""], ["Pugstyles; ‘but your conduct, Mr.", "퍼그스타일스 씨가 말했어."], ["Gregsbury, has rendered this deputation from your constituents imperatively necessary.’", "\"하지만 그렉스버리 씨, 당신의 처신 때문에 유권자들이 보낸 이 대표단이 어쩔 수 없이 필요하게 된 거지요.\""]], [["‘My conduct, Pugstyles,’ said Mr. Gregsbury, looking round upon the deputation with gracious magnanimity--‘my conduct has been, and ever will be, regulated by a sincere regard for the true and real interests of this great and happy country. Whether I look at home, or abroad; whether I behold the peaceful industrious communities of our island home: her rivers covered with steamboats, her roads with locomotives, her streets with cabs, her skies with balloons of a power and magnitude hitherto unknown in the history of aeronautics in this or any other nation--I say, whether I look merely at home, or, stretching my eyes farther, contemplate the boundless prospect of conquest and possession--achieved by British perseverance and British valour--which is outspread before me, I clasp my hands, and turning my eyes to the broad expanse above my head, exclaim, “Thank Heaven, I am a Briton!”’", "\"내 처신이라.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 너그러운 도량을 풍기며 대표단을 둘러보며 말했어. \"내 처신은 지금껏 그래 왔고 앞으로도 언제나, 이 위대하고 행복한 나라의 진정하고 참된 이익을 향한 진심 어린 마음으로 이끌어져 왔소. 안으로 눈을 돌리든, 밖으로 눈을 돌리든 말이오. 우리 섬나라의 평화롭고 부지런한 마을들을 바라보든. 증기선으로 뒤덮인 강, 기관차로 뒤덮인 도로, 마차로 뒤덮인 거리, 그리고 이 나라든 다른 어느 나라든 항공의 역사에서 일찍이 없던 위력과 규모의 기구로 뒤덮인 하늘을 바라보든. 내가 말하건대, 그저 안으로 눈을 돌리든, 아니면 더 멀리 눈을 뻗어, 영국인의 끈기와 영국인의 용맹으로 이룩한, 내 앞에 펼쳐진 저 끝없는 정복과 영토의 전망을 바라보든. 나는 두 손을 맞잡고, 머리 위로 드넓게 펼쳐진 창공으로 눈을 들어 이렇게 외친다오. '하늘이여 감사합니다, 나는 영국인입니다!'\""]], [["The time had been, when this burst of enthusiasm would have been cheered to the very echo; but now, the deputation received it with chilling coldness. The general impression seemed to be, that as an explanation of Mr. Gregsbury’s political conduct, it did not enter quite enough into detail; and one gentleman in the rear did not scruple to remark aloud, that, for his purpose, it savoured rather too much of a ‘gammon’ tendency.", "예전 같았으면 이런 열변은 메아리가 울리도록 환호를 받았겠지. 하지만 지금 대표단은 그 말을 싸늘하게 받아들였어. 다들 받은 인상은 이런 것 같았지. 그렉스버리 씨의 정치적 처신에 대한 해명치고는 너무 두루뭉술하다는 거였어. 그리고 뒤쪽에 있던 한 신사는 큰 소리로 한마디 내뱉기를 서슴지 않았지. 자기가 보기엔 그 말이 너무 '허튼소리' 냄새가 난다고 말이야."]], [["‘The meaning of that term--gammon,’ said Mr.", "\"그 말—허튼소리라는 말의 뜻은,\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어."], ["Gregsbury, ‘is unknown to me.", "\"나로선 알 수가 없군요."], ["If it means that I grow a little too fervid, or perhaps even hyperbolical, in extolling my native land, I admit the full justice of the remark.", "만약 그게 내가 조국을 칭송하느라 좀 지나치게 열을 올렸다거나, 어쩌면 과장이 좀 심했다는 뜻이라면, 나는 그 지적이 전적으로 옳다고 인정하겠소."], ["I AM proud of this free and happy country.", "나는 이 자유롭고 행복한 나라가 자랑스럽소이다."], ["My form dilates, my eye glistens, my breast heaves, my heart swells, my bosom burns, when I call to mind her greatness and her glory.’", "이 나라의 위대함과 영광을 떠올리면, 내 몸은 부풀어 오르고, 내 눈은 빛나고, 내 가슴은 들썩이고, 내 심장은 부풀고, 내 가슴속은 불타오른단 말이오.\""]], [["‘We wish, sir,’ remarked Mr.", "\"저희는, 선생님,\""], ["Pugstyles, calmly, ‘to ask you a few questions.’", "퍼그스타일스 씨가 차분히 말했어. \"몇 가지 질문을 드리고 싶습니다.\""]], [["‘If you please, gentlemen; my time is yours--and my country’s--and my country’s--’ said Mr.", "\"좋으실 대로 하시오, 여러분. 내 시간은 여러분의 것이오."], ["Gregsbury.", "그리고 내 조국의 것—내 조국의—\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어."]], [["This permission being conceded, Mr.", "이 허락이 떨어지자, 퍼그스타일스 씨는 안경을 쓰고 주머니에서 꺼낸 종이를 펼쳤어."], ["Pugstyles put on his spectacles, and referred to a written paper which he drew from his pocket; whereupon nearly every other member of the deputation pulled a written paper from HIS pocket, to check Mr.", "그러자 대표단의 거의 모든 사람이 저마다 주머니에서 종이를 꺼냈지."], ["Pugstyles off, as he read the questions.", "퍼그스타일스 씨가 질문을 읽어 나가는 대로 하나씩 짚어 가며 확인하려는 거였어."]], [["This done, Mr. Pugstyles proceeded to business.", "이렇게 준비를 마치고, 퍼그스타일스 씨가 본론으로 들어갔어."]], [["‘Question number one.--Whether, sir, you did not give a voluntary pledge previous to your election, that in event of your being returned, you would immediately put down the practice of coughing and groaning in the House of Commons.", "\"질문 제일 번. 당신은 선거 전에 자진해서 이렇게 약속하지 않았습니까?"], ["And whether you did not submit to be coughed and groaned down in the very first debate of the session, and have since made no effort to effect a reform in this respect?", "만약 당선되면 곧바로 하원에서 기침하고 신음하는 짓거리를 뿌리 뽑겠다고요. 그런데 회기 첫 토론에서부터 기침과 신음에 묻혀 입도 못 떼고 물러서지 않았습니까?"], ["Whether you did not also pledge yourself to astonish the government, and make them shrink in their shoes?", "그리고 그 뒤로 이걸 고치려고 아무 노력도 안 하지 않았습니까? 또 정부를 깜짝 놀라게 해서 벌벌 떨게 만들겠다고도 약속하지 않았습니까?"], ["And whether you have astonished them, and made them shrink in their shoes, or not?’", "그래서 정부를 놀라게 해 벌벌 떨게 만들었습니까, 아닙니까?\""]], [["‘Go on to the next one, my dear Pugstyles,’ said Mr.", "\"다음 질문으로 넘어가게, 친애하는 퍼그스타일스.\""], ["Gregsbury.", "그렉스버리 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Have you any explanation to offer with reference to that question, sir?’ asked Mr.", "\"그 질문에 대해 하실 해명이 있으십니까, 선생님?\""], ["Pugstyles.", "퍼그스타일스 씨가 물었어."]], [["‘Certainly not,’ said Mr.", "\"전혀 없소.\""], ["Gregsbury.", "그렉스버리 씨가 말했어."]], [["The members of the deputation looked fiercely at each other, and afterwards at the member.", "대표단 사람들이 서로를 사납게 노려보더니, 이어서 의원을 노려봤어."], ["‘Dear Pugstyles’ having taken a very long stare at Mr. Gregsbury over the tops of his spectacles, resumed his list of inquiries.", "'친애하는 퍼그스타일스'는 안경 너머로 그렉스버리 씨를 한참 동안 빤히 쏘아보고는, 다시 질문 목록을 이어 갔지."]], [["‘Question number two.--Whether, sir, you did not likewise give a voluntary pledge that you would support your colleague on every occasion; and whether you did not, the night before last, desert him and vote upon the other side, because the wife of a leader on that other side had invited Mrs.", "\"질문 제이 번. 당신은 마찬가지로, 무슨 일이 있어도 당신 동료를 지지하겠다고 자진해서 약속하지 않았습니까?"], ["Gregsbury to an evening party?’", "그런데 그저께 밤에 그를 저버리고 반대편에 표를 던지지 않았습니까? 반대편 지도자의 부인이 그렉스버리 부인을 야회에 초대했다는 이유로 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Go on,’ said Mr.", "\"계속하시오.\""], ["Gregsbury.", "그렉스버리 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Nothing to say on that, either, sir?’ asked the spokesman.", "\"그것에 대해서도 하실 말씀이 없으십니까, 선생님?\" 대변인이 물었어."]], [["‘Nothing whatever,’ replied Mr.", "\"전혀 없소.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 대답했어."], ["Gregsbury. The deputation, who had only seen him at canvassing or election time, were struck dumb by his coolness.", "대표단은 선거 운동 때나 선거철에만 그를 봤던 터라, 그의 태연함에 말문이 막혔지."], ["He didn’t appear like the same man; then he was all milk and honey; now he was all starch and vinegar.", "그는 딴사람 같았어. 그때는 온통 우유와 꿀처럼 다정하더니, 지금은 온통 풀 먹인 옷과 식초처럼 빳빳하고 시큰둥했거든."], ["But men ARE so different at different times!", "하지만 사람이란 때에 따라 이렇게 다른 법이지!"]], [["‘Question number three--and last,’ said Mr.", "\"질문 제삼 번, 마지막 질문이오.\""], ["Pugstyles, emphatically.", "퍼그스타일스 씨가 힘주어 말했어."], ["‘Whether, sir, you did not state upon the hustings, that it was your firm and determined intention to oppose everything proposed; to divide the house upon every question, to move for returns on every subject, to place a motion on the books every day, and, in short, in your own memorable words, to play the very devil with everything and everybody?’ With this comprehensive inquiry, Mr.", "\"당신은 선거 연단에서, 제안되는 건 무엇이든 반대하겠다고 단호하고 굳게 마음먹었다고 말하지 않았습니까? 모든 안건마다 표결로 의회를 갈라놓고, 사사건건 보고서를 요구하고, 날마다 동의안을 의사록에 올리고, 한마디로 당신 자신의 그 잊지 못할 표현대로, 모든 일과 모든 사람을 상대로 한바탕 난장판을 벌이겠다고 말입니다.\""], ["Pugstyles folded up his list of questions, as did all his backers.", "이 포괄적인 질문을 끝으로, 퍼그스타일스 씨는 질문 목록을 접었어. 그를 지지하는 사람들도 다 같이 접었지."]], [["Mr.", "그렉스버리 씨는 곰곰이 생각하더니 코를 풀고, 의자에 더 깊숙이 등을 기댔어. 그러다 다시 앞으로 몸을 내밀어 두 팔꿈치를 탁자에 괴고는, 양 엄지와 양 검지로 삼각형을 만들었지."], ["Gregsbury reflected, blew his nose, threw himself further back in his chair, came forward again, leaning his elbows on the table, made a triangle with his two thumbs and his two forefingers, and tapping his nose with the apex thereof, replied (smiling as he said it), ‘I deny everything.’", "그러고는 그 꼭짓점으로 코를 톡톡 두드리며 대답했어(말하면서 빙긋 웃었지). \"전부 부인하오.\""]], [["At this unexpected answer, a hoarse murmur arose from the deputation; and the same gentleman who had expressed an opinion relative to the gammoning nature of the introductory speech, again made a monosyllabic demonstration, by growling out ‘Resign!’ Which growl being taken up by his fellows, swelled into a very earnest and general remonstrance.", "이 뜻밖의 대답에, 대표단 사이에서 쉰 웅성거림이 일었어. 그리고 아까 첫 연설이 허튼소리 같다고 의견을 냈던 바로 그 신사가, 다시 한 단어로 시위를 했지. \"사퇴하시오!\"라고 으르렁댄 거야. 이 으르렁거림을 동료들이 받아 이어가더니, 아주 진지하고 한목소리 같은 항의로 부풀어 올랐어."]], [["‘I am requested, sir, to express a hope,’ said Mr.", "\"저는, 선생님, 한 가지 바람을 전해 달라는 부탁을 받았습니다.\" 퍼그스타일스 씨가 서먹하게 인사하며 말했어."], ["Pugstyles, with a distant bow, ‘that on receiving a requisition to that effect from a great majority of your constituents, you will not object at once to resign your seat in favour of some candidate whom they think they can better trust.’", "\"유권자의 절대다수가 그런 요구서를 보낸다면, 당신이 즉각 의석을 내놓는 데 반대하지 않으셨으면 한다는 겁니다. 그들이 더 믿을 만하다고 여기는 다른 후보를 위해서 말입니다.\""]], [["To this, Mr.", "이에 그렉스버리 씨는 다음과 같은 답을 읽어 줬어. 이런 요구를 미리 내다보고 편지 형식으로 미리 써 둔 거였지."], ["Gregsbury read the following reply, which, anticipating the request, he had composed in the form of a letter, whereof copies had been made to send round to the newspapers.", "게다가 신문사마다 돌리려고 사본까지 만들어 둔 거였어. \"친애하는 퍼그스타일스 씨께."]], [["‘Next to the welfare of our beloved island--this great and free and happy country, whose powers and resources are, I sincerely believe, illimitable--I value that noble independence which is an Englishman’s proudest boast, and which I fondly hope to bequeath to my children, untarnished and unsullied. Actuated by no personal motives, but moved only by high and great constitutional considerations; which I will not attempt to explain, for they are really beneath the comprehension of those who have not made themselves masters, as I have, of the intricate and arduous study of politics; I would rather keep my seat, and intend doing so.", "우리의 사랑하는 섬나라—이 위대하고 자유롭고 행복한 나라, 그 힘과 자원이 내가 진심으로 믿건대 무한한 이 나라의 안녕 다음으로, 나는 영국인의 가장 자랑스러운 긍지인 그 고귀한 독립을 소중히 여깁니다. 그리고 그것을 한 점 흠도 없이, 한 점 더럼도 없이 내 자식들에게 물려주기를 간절히 바랍니다. 사사로운 동기에서가 아니라, 오직 높고 위대한 헌정적 고려에 따라 움직이는 것입니다. 그 고려가 무엇인지는 굳이 설명하지 않겠습니다. 나처럼 복잡하고 고된 정치 공부를 통달하지 못한 사람들로서는 도저히 이해할 수 없는 것이니까요. 나는 차라리 내 의석을 지키고자 하며, 또 그럴 작정입니다."]], [["‘Will you do me the favour to present my compliments to the constituent body, and acquaint them with this circumstance?", "부디 유권자 여러분께 내 안부를 전하고, 이 사정을 알려 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주시겠습니까?"]], [["‘With great esteem, ‘My dear Mr. Pugstyles, ‘&c.&c.’", "깊은 존경을 담아, 친애하는 퍼그스타일스 씨께, 등등 등등.\""]], [["‘Then you will not resign, under any circumstances?’ asked the spokesman.", "\"그러니까 어떤 경우에도 사퇴하지 않으시겠다는 거지요?\" 대변인이 물었어."]], [["Mr. Gregsbury smiled, and shook his head.", "그렉스버리 씨는 빙긋 웃으며 고개를 저었어."]], [["‘Then, good-morning, sir,’ said Pugstyles, angrily.", "\"그럼, 안녕히 계십시오, 선생님.\" 퍼그스타일스가 화가 나서 말했어."]], [["‘Heaven bless you!’ said Mr. Gregsbury.", "\"하늘의 축복이 함께하기를!\""], ["And the deputation, with many growls and scowls, filed off as quickly as the narrowness of the staircase would allow of their getting down.", "그렉스버리 씨가 말했어. 그러자 대표단은 갖은 으르렁거림과 험상궂은 얼굴로, 좁은 계단이 허락하는 한 최대한 빠르게 줄지어 내려갔지."]], [["The last man being gone, Mr.", "마지막 사람까지 가 버리자, 그렉스버리 씨는 두 손을 비비며 낄낄댔어. 보통 이상으로 잘한 말이나 일을 했다고 여길 때 유쾌한 사람들이 그러듯이 말이야."], ["Gregsbury rubbed his hands and chuckled, as merry fellows will, when they think they have said or done a more than commonly good thing; he was so engrossed in this self-congratulation, that he did not observe that Nicholas had been left behind in the shadow of the window-curtains, until that young gentleman, fearing he might otherwise overhear some soliloquy intended to have no listeners, coughed twice or thrice, to attract the member’s notice.", "그는 이 자화자찬에 어찌나 푹 빠져 있었던지, 니콜라스가 창문 커튼 그늘에 남아 있는 걸 알아채지 못했지. 그러다 그 젊은이가, 그러지 않으면 들으라고 한 게 아닌 혼잣말을 엿듣게 될까 봐, 의원의 주의를 끌려고 두세 번 헛기침을 했어."]], [["‘What’s that?’ said Mr.", "\"그게 뭐요?\""], ["Gregsbury, in sharp accents.", "그렉스버리 씨가 날카로운 목소리로 말했어."]], [["Nicholas stepped forward, and bowed.", "니콜라스가 앞으로 나서서 고개를 숙였어."]], [["‘What do you do here, sir?’ asked Mr.", "\"여기서 뭐 하는 거요, 선생?\""], ["Gregsbury; ‘a spy upon my privacy!", "그렉스버리 씨가 물었어. \"내 사생활을 캐는 첩자로군!"], ["A concealed voter!", "숨어든 유권자야!"], ["You have heard my answer, sir. Pray follow the deputation.’", "내 대답은 들었을 테니, 부디 대표단을 따라가시오.\""]], [["‘I should have done so, if I had belonged to it, but I do not,’ said Nicholas.", "\"제가 대표단 소속이었다면 그랬겠지요. 하지만 저는 아닙니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Then how came you here, sir?’ was the natural inquiry of Mr.", "\"그럼 어떻게 여기 들어왔단 말이오, 선생?\""], ["Gregsbury, MP.", "하원 의원 그렉스버리 씨가 당연히 물을 만한 질문이었지."], ["‘And where the devil have you come from, sir?’ was the question which followed it.", "\"그리고 대체 어디서 굴러들어 온 거요, 선생?\" 이어진 질문이었어."]], [["‘I brought this card from the General Agency Office, sir,’ said Nicholas, ‘wishing to offer myself as your secretary, and understanding that you stood in need of one.’", "\"종합 직업소개소에서 이 소개장을 받아 왔습니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"선생님의 비서로 저를 추천하려고요. 선생님께서 비서가 필요하시다기에요.\""]], [["‘That’s all you have come for, is it?’ said Mr.", "\"고작 그것 때문에 왔단 말이오?\""], ["Gregsbury, eyeing him in some doubt.", "그렉스버리 씨가 미심쩍은 눈으로 그를 훑어보며 말했어."]], [["Nicholas replied in the affirmative.", "니콜라스는 그렇다고 대답했어."]], [["‘You have no connection with any of those rascally papers have you?’ said Mr.", "\"혹시 그 못된 신문사 어디랑 엮인 건 아니겠지?\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어."], ["Gregsbury. ‘You didn’t get into the room, to hear what was going forward, and put it in print, eh?’", "\"여기서 무슨 일이 벌어지나 들으려고 방에 들어와서, 그걸 활자로 찍어 낼 속셈은 아니고, 응?\""]], [["‘I have no connection, I am sorry to say, with anything at present,’ rejoined Nicholas,--politely enough, but quite at his ease.", "\"안타깝게도 저는 지금 그 무엇과도 엮인 데가 없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 받아쳤어. 충분히 정중하면서도, 아주 느긋하게 말이야."]], [["‘Oh!’ said Mr. Gregsbury.", "\"오!\""], ["‘How did you find your way up here, then?’", "그렉스버리 씨가 말했어. \"그럼 여긴 어떻게 올라온 거요?\""]], [["Nicholas related how he had been forced up by the deputation.", "니콜라스는 대표단에 떠밀려 올라오게 된 사정을 이야기했어."]], [["‘That was the way, was it?’ said Mr.", "\"그렇게 된 거였군, 그래?\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어."], ["Gregsbury. ‘Sit down.’", "\"앉으시오.\""]], [["Nicholas took a chair, and Mr.", "니콜라스가 의자에 앉았어."], ["Gregsbury stared at him for a long time, as if to make certain, before he asked any further questions, that there were no objections to his outward appearance.", "그러자 그렉스버리 씨는 한참을 그를 빤히 쳐다봤지. 더 묻기 전에, 그의 겉모습에 흠잡을 데가 없는지 확인하려는 듯이 말이야."]], [["‘You want to be my secretary, do you?’ he said at length.", "\"내 비서가 되고 싶다, 그 말이오?\" 마침내 그가 말했어."]], [["‘I wish to be employed in that capacity, sir,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"그 자리에 채용되고 싶습니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Well,’ said Mr.", "\"좋소.\""], ["Gregsbury; ‘now what can you do?’", "그렉스버리 씨가 말했어. \"그럼, 뭘 할 줄 아시오?\""]], [["‘I suppose,’ replied Nicholas, smiling, ‘that I can do what usually falls to the lot of other secretaries.’", "\"제 생각엔,\" 니콜라스가 빙긋 웃으며 대답했어. \"보통 다른 비서들이 맡는 일은 할 수 있을 것 같습니다.\""]], [["‘What’s that?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그게 뭔데?\""], ["Gregsbury.", "그렉스버리 씨가 물었어."]], [["‘What is it?’ replied Nicholas.", "\"그게 뭐냐고요?\" 니콜라스가 되물었어."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"그래!"], ["What is it?’ retorted the member, looking shrewdly at him, with his head on one side.", "그게 뭐냐고.\" 의원이 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃하며 빈틈없는 눈으로 그를 바라보며 받아쳤어."]], [["‘A secretary’s duties are rather difficult to define, perhaps,’ said Nicholas, considering.", "\"비서의 일이란 게 딱 잘라 말하기는 좀 어렵습니다.\" 니콜라스가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했어."], ["‘They include, I presume, correspondence?’", "\"제가 보기엔, 우선 서신 처리가 들어가겠지요?\""]], [["‘Good,’ interposed Mr.", "\"좋소.\""], ["Gregsbury.", "그렉스버리 씨가 끼어들었어."]], [["‘The arrangement of papers and documents?’", "\"서류와 문서 정리도 있고요?\""]], [["‘Very good.’", "\"아주 좋소.\""]], [["‘Occasionally, perhaps, the writing from your dictation; and possibly, sir,’ said Nicholas, with a half-smile, ‘the copying of your speech for some public journal, when you have made one of more than usual importance.’", "\"그리고 가끔은 받아쓰기도 하겠지요. 또 어쩌면, 선생님,\" 니콜라스가 슬쩍 미소를 띠며 말했어. \"선생님께서 평소보다 더 중요한 연설을 하셨을 때, 어느 신문에 실으려고 그 연설을 베껴 두는 일도 있겠고요.\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"물론이오.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 받았어."], ["Gregsbury. ‘What else?’", "\"또 뭐가 있소?\""]], [["‘Really,’ said Nicholas, after a moment’s reflection, ‘I am not able, at this instant, to recapitulate any other duty of a secretary, beyond the general one of making himself as agreeable and useful to his employer as he can, consistently with his own respectability, and without overstepping that line of duties which he undertakes to perform, and which the designation of his office is usually understood to imply.’", "\"솔직히,\" 니콜라스가 잠시 생각하고 나서 말했어. \"지금 당장은 비서의 다른 임무를 더 꼽지는 못하겠습니다. 다만 한 가지 두루 통하는 일이라면, 자기 품위를 잃지 않는 선에서, 또 자기가 맡기로 한 일—그러니까 그 직책의 이름이 으레 뜻하는 그 일의 범위를 넘지 않는 선에서, 고용주에게 되도록 마음에 들고 쓸모 있게 처신하는 것이겠지요.\""]], [["Mr. Gregsbury looked fixedly at Nicholas for a short time, and then glancing warily round the room, said in a suppressed voice:", "그렉스버리 씨는 잠시 니콜라스를 빤히 쳐다보더니, 방 안을 조심스레 둘러보고는 목소리를 낮춰 말했어."]], [["‘This is all very well, Mr--what is your name?’", "\"다 좋은 말이오, 미스터—이름이 뭐라고 했소?\""]], [["‘Nickleby.’", "\"니클비입니다.\""]], [["‘This is all very well, Mr.", "\"다 좋은 말이고 아주 그럴듯하오, 니클비 씨."], ["Nickleby, and very proper, so far as it goes--so far as it goes, but it doesn’t go far enough.", "거기까지는—거기까지는 좋은데, 충분히 멀리 가질 못하는군."], ["There are other duties, Mr. Nickleby, which a secretary to a parliamentary gentleman must never lose sight of.", "의회에 몸담은 신사의 비서라면 결코 놓쳐선 안 될 다른 임무가 있단 말이오, 니클비 씨."], ["I should require to be crammed, sir.’", "나는 머릿속을 채워 줄 사람이 필요하오, 선생.\""]], [["‘I beg your pardon,’ interposed Nicholas, doubtful whether he had heard aright.", "\"죄송하지만요.\" 니콜라스가 끼어들었어. 제대로 들은 건지 긴가민가했거든."]], [["‘--To be crammed, sir,’ repeated Mr.", "\"—머릿속을 채워 줄 사람 말이오, 선생.\""], ["Gregsbury.", "그렉스버리 씨가 거듭 말했어."]], [["‘May I beg your pardon again, if I inquire what you mean, sir?’ said Nicholas.", "\"한 번 더 죄송합니다만, 무슨 뜻인지 여쭤봐도 될까요, 선생님?\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘My meaning, sir, is perfectly plain,’ replied Mr.", "\"내 말뜻은 더없이 분명하오, 선생.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 자못 엄숙한 얼굴로 대답했어."], ["Gregsbury with a solemn aspect.", "\"내 비서라면, 신문에 비치는 대로 세계 각국의 외교 정세를 훤히 꿰고 있어야 하오."], ["‘My secretary would have to make himself master of the foreign policy of the world, as it is mirrored in the newspapers; to run his eye over all accounts of public meetings, all leading articles, and accounts of the proceedings of public bodies; and to make notes of anything which it appeared to him might be made a point of, in any little speech upon the question of some petition lying on the table, or anything of that kind.", "모든 공개 집회 기사, 모든 사설, 그리고 공공 기관의 활동 보고를 죄다 훑어봐야 하고. 그리고 탁자에 놓인 어떤 청원에 관한 자그마한 연설이라든가, 뭐 그런 데서 한번 짚고 넘어갈 만하다 싶은 게 있으면 뭐든 메모해 둬야 하지."], ["Do you understand?’", "알아듣겠소?\""]], [["‘I think I do, sir,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"알 것 같습니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Then,’ said Mr. Gregsbury, ‘it would be necessary for him to make himself acquainted, from day to day, with newspaper paragraphs on passing events; such as “Mysterious disappearance, and supposed suicide of a potboy,” or anything of that sort, upon which I might found a question to the Secretary of State for the Home Department. Then, he would have to copy the question, and as much as I remembered of the answer (including a little compliment about independence and good sense); and to send the manuscript in a frank to the local paper, with perhaps half-a-dozen lines of leader, to the effect, that I was always to be found in my place in parliament, and never shrunk from the responsible and arduous duties, and so forth. You see?’", "\"그러니,\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어. \"내 비서는 날마다 일어나는 사건에 관한 신문 기사를 꿰고 있어야 할 거요. 이를테면 '술집 사환의 수상한 실종, 자살 추정' 같은 거 말이오. 뭐 그런 종류의 기사를 가지고, 내가 내무부 장관에게 던질 질문의 근거로 삼는 거지. 그러면 비서는 그 질문을 베끼고, 답변도 내가 기억하는 만큼 베껴야 하오(독립심과 양식에 관한 자그마한 찬사도 곁들여서). 그러고는 그 원고를 무료 우편으로 그 지역 신문에 보내는 거요. 어쩌면 사설 대여섯 줄도 함께 말이오. 내용인즉, 나는 의회의 내 자리를 늘 지키고 있으며, 책임지고 고된 임무를 결코 마다하지 않는다, 뭐 그런 식이지. 알겠소?\""]], [["Nicholas bowed.", "니콜라스는 고개를 숙였어."]], [["‘Besides which,’ continued Mr. Gregsbury, ‘I should expect him, now and then, to go through a few figures in the printed tables, and to pick out a few results, so that I might come out pretty well on timber duty questions, and finance questions, and so on; and I should like him to get up a few little arguments about the disastrous effects of a return to cash payments and a metallic currency, with a touch now and then about the exportation of bullion, and the Emperor of Russia, and bank notes, and all that kind of thing, which it’s only necessary to talk fluently about, because nobody understands it. Do you take me?’", "\"그뿐 아니라,\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말을 이었어. \"이따금 인쇄된 통계표의 숫자 몇 개를 짚어 보고, 결과 몇 가지를 뽑아 줬으면 하오. 그래야 내가 목재 관세 문제, 재정 문제 같은 걸로 그럴듯하게 한몫 잡을 수 있거든. 그리고 현금 지불과 금속 화폐로 되돌아가는 게 얼마나 끔찍한 결과를 부르는지에 관한 자그마한 논거 몇 개도 마련해 줬으면 하오. 가끔은 정금 수출이라든가 러시아 황제라든가 은행권 같은 걸 슬쩍 곁들이고 말이오. 그런 건 그저 술술 떠들어 대기만 하면 되는 거요. 아무도 못 알아들으니까. 내 말 알아듣겠소?\""]], [["‘I think I understand,’ said Nicholas.", "\"알아들은 것 같습니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘With regard to such questions as are not political,’ continued Mr Gregsbury, warming; ‘and which one can’t be expected to care a curse about, beyond the natural care of not allowing inferior people to be as well off as ourselves--else where are our privileges?--I should wish my secretary to get together a few little flourishing speeches, of a patriotic cast. For instance, if any preposterous bill were brought forward, for giving poor grubbing devils of authors a right to their own property, I should like to say, that I for one would never consent to opposing an insurmountable bar to the diffusion of literature among THE PEOPLE,--you understand?--that the creations of the pocket, being man’s, might belong to one man, or one family; but that the creations of the brain, being God’s, ought as a matter of course to belong to the people at large--and if I was pleasantly disposed, I should like to make a joke about posterity, and say that those who wrote for posterity should be content to be rewarded by the approbation OF posterity; it might take with the house, and could never do me any harm, because posterity can’t be expected to know anything about me or my jokes either--do you see?’", "\"정치와 상관없는 문제들로 말하자면,\" 그렉스버리 씨가 열을 올리며 말을 이었어. \"그런 거야 우리가 눈곱만큼도 신경 쓸 까닭이 없지. 우리보다 못한 사람들이 우리만큼 잘사는 꼴은 두고 보지 않겠다는, 그 당연한 마음 말고는 말이오—안 그러면 우리 특권이 무슨 소용이겠소?—그런 문제라면, 내 비서가 화려한 자그마한 연설 몇 개를, 애국심 넘치는 투로 엮어 줬으면 하오. 이를테면, 글이나 끄적이는 가난뱅이 작가 놈들한테 제 작품에 대한 권리를 준다는 터무니없는 법안이라도 나오면, 나는 이렇게 말하고 싶거든. 적어도 나는 백성들 사이에 문학이 퍼지는 데 결코 넘을 수 없는 벽을 세우는 일에는 절대로 찬성하지 않겠노라고—알아듣겠소?—주머니에서 나온 것은 사람의 것이니 한 사람, 한 집안의 것이 될 수 있다. 하지만 머리에서 나온 것은 하느님의 것이니, 당연히 만백성의 것이 되어야 마땅하다고 말이오. 그리고 기분이 좋으면, 후세에 관해 농담 한마디 던지고 싶거든. 후세를 위해 글을 쓰는 자들은 후세의 칭찬으로 보답받는 데 만족해야 한다고 말이오. 의회에서 먹힐 만한 말이지. 게다가 나한테 해될 것도 전혀 없어. 후세가 나에 대해서든 내 농담에 대해서든 뭘 알 리가 없으니까. 무슨 말인지 알겠소?\""]], [["‘I see that, sir,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"그건 알겠습니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘You must always bear in mind, in such cases as this, where our interests are not affected,’ said Mr.", "\"이런 경우, 그러니까 우리 이익이 걸려 있지 않은 경우엔 늘 명심해야 하오.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 말했어."], ["Gregsbury, ‘to put it very strong about the people, because it comes out very well at election-time; and you could be as funny as you liked about the authors; because I believe the greater part of them live in lodgings, and are not voters.", "\"백성에 대해서는 아주 세게 말하는 거요. 선거철에 아주 잘 먹히거든."], ["This is a hasty outline of the chief things you’d have to do, except waiting in the lobby every night, in case I forgot anything, and should want fresh cramming; and, now and then, during great debates, sitting in the front row of the gallery, and saying to the people about--‘You see that gentleman, with his hand to his face, and his arm twisted round the pillar--that’s Mr.", "그리고 작가들에 대해서는 마음껏 우스갯거리로 삼아도 좋소. 내가 알기로 그치들 태반은 셋방살이를 하니, 유권자도 아니거든."], ["Gregsbury--the celebrated Mr.", "이게 자네가 해야 할 주된 일들의 대략적인 윤곽이오. 그 밖에 매일 밤 로비에서 대기하는 것도 있고—내가 뭘 빠뜨려서 머릿속을 새로 채워야 할 때가 있으니 말이오."], ["Gregsbury,’--with any other little eulogium that might strike you at the moment.", "그리고 가끔, 큰 토론이 있을 때는 방청석 맨 앞줄에 앉아서, 주위 사람들한테 이렇게 말하는 것도 있지. '저 신사분 보이시오?"], ["And for salary,’ said Mr.", "손을 얼굴에 대고, 팔을 기둥에 휘감은 저분—저분이 그렉스버리 씨요—그 유명한 그렉스버리 씨.' 그때그때 떠오르는 자그마한 찬사도 곁들이고 말이오."], ["Gregsbury, winding up with great rapidity; for he was out of breath--‘and for salary, I don’t mind saying at once in round numbers, to prevent any dissatisfaction--though it’s more than I’ve been accustomed to give--fifteen shillings a week, and find yourself.", "그리고 급료로 말하자면,\" 그렉스버리 씨가 숨이 차서 헐떡이며 무척 빠르게 마무리했어."], ["There!’", "\"급료로 말하자면, 괜한 불만이 생기지 않게 단도직입으로 어림수로 말해 두겠소—내가 여태 줘 본 것보다 많은 액수이긴 하지만—주급 십오 실링이고, 끼니는 알아서 해결하시오. 자, 어떻소!\""]], [["With this handsome offer, Mr.", "이 후한 제안을 던지고, 그렉스버리 씨는 다시 의자에 등을 기댔어."], ["Gregsbury once more threw himself back in his chair, and looked like a man who had been most profligately liberal, but is determined not to repent of it notwithstanding.", "더없이 헤프게 인심을 쓴 사람이지만, 그래도 결코 후회하지는 않겠다고 작정한 사람 같은 표정이었지."]], [["‘Fifteen shillings a week is not much,’ said Nicholas, mildly.", "\"주급 십오 실링은 많지 않은데요.\" 니콜라스가 부드럽게 말했어."]], [["‘Not much!", "\"많지 않다고!"], ["Fifteen shillings a week not much, young man?’ cried Mr Gregsbury.", "주급 십오 실링이 많지 않다니, 젊은이?\" 그렉스버리 씨가 외쳤어."], ["‘Fifteen shillings a--’", "\"주급 십오 실—\""]], [["‘Pray do not suppose that I quarrel with the sum, sir,’ replied Nicholas; ‘for I am not ashamed to confess, that whatever it may be in itself, to me it is a great deal. But the duties and responsibilities make the recompense small, and they are so very heavy that I fear to undertake them.’", "\"제가 그 액수를 두고 시비를 거는 거라고 생각하진 말아 주십시오, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"솔직히 털어놓건대, 그 자체가 어떻든 저에게는 큰돈입니다. 부끄러울 것 없어요. 하지만 그 임무와 책임에 비하면 보수가 적습니다. 게다가 그 임무가 어찌나 무거운지, 제가 감당할 수 있을지 두렵습니다.\""]], [["‘Do you decline to undertake them, sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"맡지 않겠다는 거요, 선생?\""], ["Gregsbury, with his hand on the bell-rope.", "그렉스버리 씨가 종 끈에 손을 얹으며 물었어."]], [["‘I fear they are too great for my powers, however good my will may be, sir,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"제 의지가 아무리 좋다 해도, 제 힘에 부치는 일인 것 같아 두렵습니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘That is as much as to say that you had rather not accept the place, and that you consider fifteen shillings a week too little,’ said Mr Gregsbury, ringing.", "\"그건 곧 이 자리를 받지 않겠다는 거고, 주급 십오 실링이 너무 적다고 여긴다는 말이군.\" 그렉스버리 씨가 종을 울리며 말했어."], ["‘Do you decline it, sir?’", "\"거절하는 거요, 선생?\""]], [["‘I have no alternative but to do so,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"그럴 수밖에 없을 것 같습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Door, Matthews!’ said Mr.", "\"문, 매슈스!\""], ["Gregsbury, as the boy appeared.", "그렉스버리 씨가 소년이 나타나자 말했어."]], [["‘I am sorry I have troubled you unnecessarily, sir,’ said Nicholas.", "\"공연히 폐를 끼쳐 죄송합니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘I am sorry you have,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"그러게 말이오.\""], ["Gregsbury, turning his back upon him.", "그렉스버리 씨가 그에게서 등을 돌리며 받아쳤어."], ["‘Door, Matthews!’", "\"문, 매슈스!\""]], [["‘Good-morning, sir,’ said Nicholas.", "\"안녕히 계십시오, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Door, Matthews!’ cried Mr.", "\"문, 매슈스!\""], ["Gregsbury.", "그렉스버리 씨가 외쳤어."]], [["The boy beckoned Nicholas, and tumbling lazily downstairs before him, opened the door, and ushered him into the street.", "소년이 니콜라스에게 손짓을 하고는, 앞장서서 게으름 부리듯 비척비척 계단을 내려가 문을 열고, 그를 거리로 내보냈어."], ["With a sad and pensive air, he retraced his steps homewards.", "니콜라스는 슬프고 시름에 잠긴 얼굴로 집을 향해 발걸음을 되짚었지."]], [["Smike had scraped a meal together from the remnant of last night’s supper, and was anxiously awaiting his return.", "스마이크는 간밤에 먹다 남은 것을 그러모아 끼니를 차려 놓고, 그가 돌아오기를 애타게 기다리고 있었어. 아침에 겪은 일들 때문에 니콜라스는 입맛이 돌지 않았지."], ["The occurrences of the morning had not improved Nicholas’s appetite, and, by him, the dinner remained untasted.", "그래서 그 음식은 손도 대지 않은 채 그대로 남아 있었어. 그가 생각에 잠긴 자세로 앉아 있었지."], ["He was sitting in a thoughtful attitude, with the plate which the poor fellow had assiduously filled with the choicest morsels, untouched, by his side, when Newman Noggs looked into the room.", "가엾은 친구가 가장 좋은 부분만 골라 정성껏 수북이 담아 둔 접시도, 손도 안 댄 채 곁에 놓여 있었어. 그때 뉴먼 노그스가 방 안을 들여다봤지."]], [["‘Come back?’ asked Newman.", "\"돌아왔나?\" 뉴먼이 물었어."]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Nicholas, ‘tired to death: and, what is worse, might have remained at home for all the good I have done.’", "\"네.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"녹초가 됐어요. 게다가 더 딱한 건, 한 일도 없으니 차라리 집에 있을 걸 그랬다는 거예요.\""]], [["‘Couldn’t expect to do much in one morning,’ said Newman.", "\"한나절 만에 큰 걸 바랄 순 없지.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."]], [["‘Maybe so, but I am sanguine, and did expect,’ said Nicholas, ‘and am proportionately disappointed.’ Saying which, he gave Newman an account of his proceedings.", "\"그럴지도 모르죠. 하지만 저는 희망에 부풀어서 정말 기대를 했거든요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그러니 그만큼 실망도 큰 거죠.\" 이렇게 말하면서, 그는 뉴먼에게 그동안 있었던 일을 들려줬어."]], [["‘If I could do anything,’ said Nicholas, ‘anything, however slight, until Ralph Nickleby returns, and I have eased my mind by confronting him, I should feel happier. I should think it no disgrace to work, Heaven knows. Lying indolently here, like a half-tamed sullen beast, distracts me.’", "\"무슨 일이든 할 수 있다면,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"아무리 하찮은 일이라도요. 랄프 니클비가 돌아와서, 제가 그자와 맞서 속이라도 풀 때까지 말이에요. 그때까지라도 뭐든 할 수 있다면 마음이 더 편할 것 같아요. 하늘이 아시지만, 저는 일하는 걸 부끄럽게 여기지 않아요. 길든 만 사나운 짐승처럼 여기 빈둥대며 누워 있는 게 저를 미치게 합니다.\""]], [["‘I don’t know,’ said Newman; ‘small things offer--they would pay the rent, and more--but you wouldn’t like them; no, you could hardly be expected to undergo it--no, no.’", "\"글쎄,\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"자그마한 일거리가 하나 있긴 한데—방세를 내고도 남을 정도지만—자네 마음에는 안 들 거야. 그래, 자네한테 그걸 견디라고 할 순 없지—아니, 아니야.\""]], [["‘What could I hardly be expected to undergo?’ asked Nicholas, raising his eyes.", "\"제가 뭘 견디기 어렵다는 거죠?\" 니콜라스가 눈을 들며 물었어."], ["‘Show me, in this wide waste of London, any honest means by which I could even defray the weekly hire of this poor room, and see if I shrink from resorting to them!", "\"이 넓디넓은 런던 황무지에서, 이 초라한 방의 주급 방세나마 댈 수 있는 정직한 방도를 보여 주세요. 그래도 제가 거기에 손대길 꺼리는지 한번 보시라고요!"], ["Undergo!", "견디라고요!"], ["I have undergone too much, my friend, to feel pride or squeamishness now.", "저는 너무 많은 걸 견뎌 봤어요, 친구. 이제 와 자존심을 내세우거나 까다롭게 굴 처지가 아니에요."], ["Except--’ added Nicholas hastily, after a short silence, ‘except such squeamishness as is common honesty, and so much pride as constitutes self-respect.", "다만—\" 니콜라스가 잠깐 입을 다물었다가 서둘러 덧붙였어."], ["I see little to choose, between assistant to a brutal pedagogue, and toad-eater to a mean and ignorant upstart, be he member or no member.’", "\"다만 그저 정직함에서 나오는 까다로움과, 자존심을 지키는 만큼의 자부심은 빼고요. 잔인한 교사의 조수 노릇이나, 비열하고 무식한 벼락출세자의 아첨꾼 노릇이나, 의원이든 아니든, 별로 다를 게 없다고 봅니다.\""]], [["‘I hardly know whether I should tell you what I heard this morning, or not,’ said Newman.", "\"오늘 아침에 들은 얘기를 자네한테 해야 할지 말아야 할지 모르겠군.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."]], [["‘Has it reference to what you said just now?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"방금 하신 말씀과 관계가 있는 건가요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘It has.’", "\"그렇다네.\""]], [["‘Then in Heaven’s name, my good friend, tell it me,’ said Nicholas. ‘For God’s sake consider my deplorable condition; and, while I promise to take no step without taking counsel with you, give me, at least, a vote in my own behalf.’", "\"그럼 제발, 친애하는 친구, 말씀해 주세요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"제발 제 딱한 처지를 헤아려 주세요. 그리고 약속할게요. 당신과 의논하지 않고는 한 발짝도 움직이지 않겠다고요. 그러니 적어도 제 일에 저도 한 표는 행사하게 해 주세요.\""]], [["Moved by this entreaty, Newman stammered forth a variety of most unaccountable and entangled sentences, the upshot of which was, that Mrs.", "이 간청에 마음이 움직인 뉴먼은, 도무지 종잡을 수 없고 뒤엉킨 문장들을 더듬더듬 늘어놓았어. 그 요지인즉 이랬지."], ["Kenwigs had examined him, at great length that morning, touching the origin of his acquaintance with, and the whole life, adventures, and pedigree of, Nicholas; that Newman had parried these questions as long as he could, but being, at length, hard pressed and driven into a corner, had gone so far as to admit, that Nicholas was a tutor of great accomplishments, involved in some misfortunes which he was not at liberty to explain, and bearing the name of Johnson.", "그날 아침 켄위그스 부인이 그를 한참 동안 캐물었다는 거였어. 니콜라스와 어떻게 알게 됐는지, 또 니콜라스의 일생과 모험담, 그리고 가문 내력까지 죄다 캐물었다는 거지."], ["That Mrs.", "뉴먼은 이 질문들을 할 수 있는 데까지 막아 냈지만, 결국 거세게 몰아붙임을 당해 구석에 몰린 끝에, 이렇게까지 털어놓고 말았다는 거야. 니콜라스는 학식이 대단한 가정교사이며, 자기가 함부로 밝힐 수 없는 어떤 불행에 휘말려 있고, 존슨이라는 이름을 쓴다고 말이지."], ["Kenwigs, impelled by gratitude, or ambition, or maternal pride, or maternal love, or all four powerful motives conjointly, had taken secret conference with Mr.", "그러자 켄위그스 부인은 고마움 때문인지, 야심 때문인지, 어미로서의 자부심 때문인지, 어미로서의 사랑 때문인지, 아니면 이 네 가지 강력한 동기가 한꺼번에 작용한 탓인지, 켄위그스 씨와 은밀히 의논을 했다는 거야. 그러고는 마침내 이런 제안을 들고 돌아왔다는 거지."], ["Kenwigs, and had finally returned to propose that Mr.", "존슨 씨가 네 명의 켄위그스 아가씨들에게 프랑스 사람이 쓰는 그대로의 프랑스어를 가르쳐 줬으면 한다고 말이야. 주급으로는 이 나라 통화로 오 실링을 주겠다는 거였어."], ["Johnson should instruct the four Miss Kenwigses in the French language as spoken by natives, at the weekly stipend of five shillings, current coin of the realm; being at the rate of one shilling per week, per each Miss Kenwigs, and one shilling over, until such time as the baby might be able to take it out in grammar.", "켄위그스 아가씨 한 명당 주급 일 실링씩에, 일 실링이 남는 셈이었지. 그 남는 일 실링은, 갓난아기가 문법 공부로 그 값을 받아 갈 만큼 자랄 때까지를 위한 거였어."]], [["‘Which, unless I am very much mistaken,’ observed Mrs.", "\"내가 크게 잘못 본 게 아니라면 말이에요,\" 켄위그스 부인이 이 제안을 하며 말했어."], ["Kenwigs in making the proposition, ‘will not be very long; for such clever children, Mr Noggs, never were born into this world, I do believe.’", "\"그렇게 오래 걸리지도 않을 거예요. 노그스 씨, 이렇게 영리한 아이들은 정말이지 세상에 태어난 적이 없을 테니까요.\""]], [["‘There,’ said Newman, ‘that’s all.", "\"자, 그게 다일세.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."], ["It’s beneath you, I know; but I thought that perhaps you might--’", "\"자네한테 격에 안 맞는 일인 줄은 알아. 하지만 혹시나 자네가—\""]], [["‘Might!’ cried Nicholas, with great alacrity; ‘of course I shall.", "\"혹시나라뇨!\" 니콜라스가 한껏 들떠 외쳤어."], ["I accept the offer at once.", "\"당연히 하죠. 당장 그 제안을 받겠습니다."], ["Tell the worthy mother so, without delay, my dear fellow; and that I am ready to begin whenever she pleases.’", "그 갸륵한 어머님께 지체 없이 그렇게 전해 주세요, 친구. 그리고 부인이 원하실 때 언제든 시작할 준비가 되어 있다고요.\""]], [["Newman hastened, with joyful steps, to inform Mrs.", "뉴먼은 기쁜 발걸음으로 서둘러 켄위그스 부인에게 친구가 응했다는 소식을 전하러 갔어. 그러고는 이내 돌아와 이렇게 전했지."], ["Kenwigs of his friend’s acquiescence, and soon returning, brought back word that they would be happy to see him in the first floor as soon as convenient; that Mrs.", "부인은 편하실 때 이층에서 그를 뵙게 되면 기쁘겠다고요. 또 부인이 그 즉시 사람을 보내, 중고 프랑스어 문법책과 회화책을 구해 오게 했다는 거야."], ["Kenwigs had, upon the instant, sent out to secure a second-hand French grammar and dialogues, which had long been fluttering in the sixpenny box at the bookstall round the corner; and that the family, highly excited at the prospect of this addition to their gentility, wished the initiatory lesson to come off immediately.", "길모퉁이 책방의 육 펜스짜리 헌책 상자 속에서 오래도록 나풀거리던 책이었지. 그리고 가족 모두가 이렇게 품위를 더하게 됐다는 기대에 잔뜩 들떠서, 첫 수업을 당장 시작하고 싶어 한다는 거였어."]], [["And here it may be observed, that Nicholas was not, in the ordinary sense of the word, a young man of high spirit. He would resent an affront to himself, or interpose to redress a wrong offered to another, as boldly and freely as any knight that ever set lance in rest; but he lacked that peculiar excess of coolness and great-minded selfishness, which invariably distinguish gentlemen of high spirit. In truth, for our own part, we are disposed to look upon such gentleman as being rather incumbrances than otherwise in rising families: happening to be acquainted with several whose spirit prevents their settling down to any grovelling occupation, and only displays itself in a tendency to cultivate moustachios, and look fierce; and although moustachios and ferocity are both very pretty things in their way, and very much to be commended, we confess to a desire to see them bred at the owner’s proper cost, rather than at the expense of low-spirited people.", "여기서 한 가지 짚고 넘어가자면, 니콜라스는 흔히 말하는 그런 의미의 혈기 넘치는 젊은이는 아니었어. 자기가 모욕을 당하면 발끈하고, 남이 부당한 일을 당하면 끼어들어 바로잡아 주기는 했지. 일찍이 창을 겨눠 본 그 어떤 기사 못지않게 대담하고 거리낌 없이 말이야. 하지만 혈기 넘치는 신사들에게서 어김없이 보이는, 그 유별난 냉정함과 거창한 이기심은 없었어. 사실 우리로서는, 그런 신사들이 일어서려는 집안에 도움이 되기는커녕 짐이나 된다고 보는 쪽이야. 우리가 아는 사람 중에 그런 이가 여럿 있는데, 그들은 그 혈기 탓에 비천한 일에는 도무지 안착하질 못하지. 그 혈기라는 게 고작 콧수염이나 기르고 사나운 표정이나 짓는 데서나 드러날 뿐이야. 물론 콧수염과 사나움도 그 나름으로는 꽤 보기 좋고 칭찬할 만한 것들이지. 하지만 우리는 솔직히 바라는 바가 있어. 그런 것들을 기를 거면 혈기 없는 사람들의 돈이 아니라, 제 주머니를 털어 제대로 기르라는 거지."]], [["Nicholas, therefore, not being a high-spirited young man according to common parlance, and deeming it a greater degradation to borrow, for the supply of his necessities, from Newman Noggs, than to teach French to the little Kenwigses for five shillings a week, accepted the offer with the alacrity already described, and betook himself to the first floor with all convenient speed.", "그러니 니콜라스는 세간에서 말하는 그런 혈기 넘치는 젊은이가 아니었어. 그리고 그는 제 필요를 메우려고 뉴먼 노그스에게 돈을 꾸는 것이, 주급 오 실링에 어린 켄위그스 아이들에게 프랑스어를 가르치는 것보다 더 떳떳지 못한 일이라고 여겼지. 그래서 앞서 말한 대로 선뜻 그 제안을 받아들이고는, 되도록 서둘러 이층으로 올라갔어."]], [["Here, he was received by Mrs. Kenwigs with a genteel air, kindly intended to assure him of her protection and support; and here, too, he found Mr Lillyvick and Miss Petowker; the four Miss Kenwigses on their form of audience; and the baby in a dwarf porter’s chair with a deal tray before it, amusing himself with a toy horse without a head; the said horse being composed of a small wooden cylinder, not unlike an Italian iron, supported on four crooked pegs, and painted in ingenious resemblance of red wafers set in blacking.", "거기서 그는 켄위그스 부인의 점잖은 영접을 받았어. 자기가 그를 보호하고 후원해 주겠다는 뜻을 친절히 담은 태도였지. 그리고 거기엔 릴리빅 씨와 페토커 양도 있었어. 네 명의 켄위그스 아가씨들은 청강석 같은 긴 의자에 앉아 있었고, 갓난아기는 앞에 전나무 쟁반이 달린 자그마한 짐꾼용 의자에 앉아, 머리 없는 장난감 말을 가지고 혼자 놀고 있었지. 그 말이라는 게, 이탈리아식 다리미를 닮은 작은 나무 원통에 구부러진 못 네 개를 받쳐 세운 거였어. 게다가 까만 구두약 위에 빨간 봉랍을 박아 놓은 듯 영리하게 칠해져 있었지."]], [["‘How do you do, Mr.", "\"안녕하세요, 존슨 씨?\""], ["Johnson?’ said Mrs. Kenwigs.", "켄위그스 부인이 말했어."], ["‘Uncle--Mr. Johnson.’", "\"외삼촌—존슨 씨예요.\""]], [["‘How do you do, sir?’ said Mr.", "\"안녕하시오, 선생?\""], ["Lillyvick--rather sharply; for he had not known what Nicholas was, on the previous night, and it was rather an aggravating circumstance if a tax collector had been too polite to a teacher.", "릴리빅 씨가 다소 퉁명스럽게 말했어. 전날 밤에는 니콜라스가 어떤 사람인지 몰랐던 데다, 세금 징수원이 한낱 교사에게 너무 정중하게 굴었다면 그건 좀 분통 터지는 일이었거든."]], [["‘Mr. Johnson is engaged as private master to the children, uncle,’ said Mrs.", "\"존슨 씨가 아이들의 개인 교사로 채용됐어요, 외삼촌.\""], ["Kenwigs.", "켄위그스 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘So you said just now, my dear,’ replied Mr.", "\"방금 그렇게 말하지 않았느냐, 얘야.\""], ["Lillyvick.", "릴리빅 씨가 대답했어."]], [["‘But I hope,’ said Mrs.", "\"하지만 저는 바라요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 자세를 꼿꼿이 세우며 말했어."], ["Kenwigs, drawing herself up, ‘that that will not make them proud; but that they will bless their own good fortune, which has born them superior to common people’s children.", "\"이 일로 아이들이 우쭐대지 않기를요. 그저 자기네 행운에 감사하기를 바랄 뿐이에요."], ["Do you hear, Morleena?’", "보통 사람들 자식보다 나은 신분으로 태어났다는 그 행운에요. 듣고 있니, 몰리나?\""]], [["‘Yes, ma,’ replied Miss Kenwigs.", "\"네, 엄마.\" 켄위그스 양이 대답했어."]], [["‘And when you go out in the streets, or elsewhere, I desire that you don’t boast of it to the other children,’ said Mrs. Kenwigs; ‘and that if you must say anything about it, you don’t say no more than “We’ve got a private master comes to teach us at home, but we ain’t proud, because ma says it’s sinful.” Do you hear, Morleena?’", "\"그리고 길에 나가거나 어디 가서든, 다른 아이들한테 이걸 자랑하지 마라.\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했어. \"혹시 꼭 무슨 말을 해야 한다면, 그냥 이렇게만 말하렴. '우리는 집으로 와서 가르쳐 주는 개인 교사가 있어. 하지만 우린 우쭐대지 않아. 엄마가 그건 죄받을 일이라고 했거든.' 알아들었니, 몰리나?\""]], [["‘Yes, ma,’ replied Miss Kenwigs again.", "\"네, 엄마.\" 켄위그스 양이 또 대답했어."]], [["‘Then mind you recollect, and do as I tell you,’ said Mrs.", "\"그럼 꼭 기억하고, 엄마가 시킨 대로 하렴.\""], ["Kenwigs. ‘Shall Mr.", "켄위그스 부인이 말했어."], ["Johnson begin, uncle?’", "\"존슨 씨더러 시작하라고 할까요, 외삼촌?\""]], [["‘I am ready to hear, if Mr. Johnson is ready to commence, my dear,’ said the collector, assuming the air of a profound critic.", "\"존슨 씨가 시작할 준비가 됐다면, 나야 들을 준비가 됐다, 얘야.\""], ["‘What sort of language do you consider French, sir?’", "징수원이 자못 깊이 있는 비평가 같은 태도를 지으며 말했어. \"선생은 프랑스어를 어떤 언어라고 보시오?\""]], [["‘How do you mean?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"무슨 말씀이신지요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘Do you consider it a good language, sir?’ said the collector; ‘a pretty language, a sensible language?’", "\"좋은 언어라고 보냐는 말이오, 선생?\" 징수원이 말했어. \"예쁜 언어, 똑똑한 언어 말이오.\""]], [["‘A pretty language, certainly,’ replied Nicholas; ‘and as it has a name for everything, and admits of elegant conversation about everything, I presume it is a sensible one.’", "\"예쁜 언어인 건 분명합니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그리고 무엇이든 부르는 이름이 있고, 무엇에 대해서든 우아하게 대화할 수 있으니, 똑똑한 언어라고 봐도 될 것 같습니다.\""]], [["‘I don’t know,’ said Mr.", "\"글쎄요.\""], ["Lillyvick, doubtfully.", "릴리빅 씨가 미심쩍은 듯 말했어."], ["‘Do you call it a cheerful language, now?’", "\"그럼, 그게 명랑한 언어라고 보시오?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Nicholas, ‘I should say it was, certainly.’", "\"네.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그건 분명히 그렇다고 말할 수 있겠습니다.\""]], [["‘It’s very much changed since my time, then,’ said the collector, ‘very much.’", "\"그럼 내 시절하고는 아주 많이 달라졌구먼.\" 징수원이 말했어. \"아주 많이 말이오.\""]], [["‘Was it a dismal one in your time?’ asked Nicholas, scarcely able to repress a smile.", "\"선생님 시절엔 침울한 언어였나요?\" 니콜라스가 웃음을 간신히 참으며 물었어."]], [["‘Very,’ replied Mr.", "\"아주 그랬지.\""], ["Lillyvick, with some vehemence of manner.", "릴리빅 씨가 자못 격하게 대답했어. \"내가 말하는 건 전쟁 때요."], ["‘It’s the war time that I speak of; the last war.", "지난번 전쟁 말이오. 명랑한 언어일지도 모르지."], ["It may be a cheerful language.", "누구 말을 반박하고 싶진 않소. 하지만 이것만은 말할 수 있소."], ["I should be sorry to contradict anybody; but I can only say that I’ve heard the French prisoners, who were natives, and ought to know how to speak it, talking in such a dismal manner, that it made one miserable to hear them.", "나는 프랑스 포로들이 하는 말을 들어 봤거든. 그치들은 토박이니 프랑스어를 제대로 할 줄 알 텐데, 어찌나 침울하게 말하는지, 듣고 있으면 사람이 다 비참해질 지경이었소."], ["Ay, that I have, fifty times, sir--fifty times!’", "그렇다니까, 그런 적이 쉰 번도 넘소, 선생—쉰 번도 넘게!\""]], [["Mr. Lillyvick was waxing so cross, that Mrs.", "릴리빅 씨가 어찌나 심사가 뒤틀려 가는지, 켄위그스 부인은 니콜라스에게 아무 말도 하지 말라고 눈짓하는 게 상책이라고 여겼지."], ["Kenwigs thought it expedient to motion to Nicholas not to say anything; and it was not until Miss Petowker had practised several blandishments, to soften the excellent old gentleman, that he deigned to break silence by asking,", "그리고 페토커 양이 이 훌륭한 노신사를 누그러뜨리려고 갖은 알랑거림을 부린 뒤에야, 그가 비로소 침묵을 깨고 입을 열어 물었어."]], [["‘What’s the water in French, sir?’", "\"프랑스어로 물이 뭐요, 선생?\""]], [["‘L’EAU,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"로(L'EAU)입니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Ah!’ said Mr.", "\"아!\""], ["Lillyvick, shaking his head mournfully, ‘I thought as much.", "릴리빅 씨가 구슬프게 고개를 저으며 말했어. \"그럴 줄 알았소."], ["Lo, eh?", "로, 응?"], ["I don’t think anything of that language--nothing at all.’", "그딴 언어는 아무것도 아니라고 보오—전혀 아무것도 아니지.\""]], [["‘I suppose the children may begin, uncle?’ said Mrs.", "\"그럼 아이들 시작해도 되겠지요, 외삼촌?\""], ["Kenwigs.", "켄위그스 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘Oh yes; they may begin, my dear,’ replied the collector, discontentedly.", "\"오, 그래. 시작해도 된다, 얘야.\""], ["‘I have no wish to prevent them.’", "징수원이 마뜩잖게 대답했어. \"내가 못 하게 막을 생각은 없으니까.\""]], [["This permission being conceded, the four Miss Kenwigses sat in a row, with their tails all one way, and Morleena at the top: while Nicholas, taking the book, began his preliminary explanations.", "이 허락이 떨어지자, 네 명의 켄위그스 아가씨들이 한 줄로 앉았어. 댕기를 모두 한쪽으로 늘어뜨리고, 맨 위에 몰리나가 앉았지."], ["Miss Petowker and Mrs.", "그동안 니콜라스는 책을 집어 들고 첫 설명을 시작했어."], ["Kenwigs looked on, in silent admiration, broken only by the whispered assurances of the latter, that Morleena would have it all by heart in no time; and Mr.", "페토커 양과 켄위그스 부인은 말없이 감탄하며 지켜봤지. 그 침묵을 깨는 건 오직, 몰리나가 금세 죄다 외워 버릴 거라는 부인의 속삭이는 장담뿐이었어."], ["Lillyvick regarded the group with frowning and attentive eyes, lying in wait for something upon which he could open a fresh discussion on the language.", "그리고 릴리빅 씨는 인상을 찌푸린 채 주의 깊은 눈으로 그 무리를 바라봤지. 이 언어를 두고 새로 논쟁을 벌일 만한 꼬투리가 없나 호시탐탐 노리면서 말이야."]], [["Follows the Fortunes of Miss Nickleby\nIt was with a heavy heart, and many sad forebodings which no effort could banish, that Kate Nickleby, on the morning appointed for the commencement of her engagement with Madame Mantalini, left the city when its clocks yet wanted a quarter of an hour of eight, and threaded her way alone, amid the noise and bustle of the streets, towards the west end of London.", "케이트 니클비가 마담 맨탈리니와 일을 시작하기로 정해진 날 아침, 그 무거운 마음을 안고 길을 나섰어. 아무리 애를 써도 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 슬픈 예감이 잔뜩 들어찬 채였지. 시내 시계들이 여덟 시까지 아직 십오 분이 남았을 무렵, 케이트는 도시를 빠져나왔어. 그리고 시끌벅적하고 북적이는 거리를 혼자서 헤치고, 런던 서쪽 끝을 향해 걸어갔지."]], [["At this early hour many sickly girls, whose business, like that of the poor worm, is to produce, with patient toil, the finery that bedecks the thoughtless and luxurious, traverse our streets, making towards the scene of their daily labour, and catching, as if by stealth, in their hurried walk, the only gasp of wholesome air and glimpse of sunlight which cheer their monotonous existence during the long train of hours that make a working day. As she drew nigh to the more fashionable quarter of the town, Kate marked many of this class as they passed by, hurrying like herself to their painful occupation, and saw, in their unhealthy looks and feeble gait, but too clear an evidence that her misgivings were not wholly groundless.", "이 이른 시간에 병약한 처녀들이 많이도 거리를 누벼. 그들이 하는 일이란, 가엾은 누에처럼 끈기 있게 애써서 생각 없고 사치스러운 사람들을 꾸며 줄 고운 옷가지를 만들어 내는 거야. 그들은 하루 일터를 향해 걸음을 재촉하면서, 그 바쁜 걸음 가운데 마치 몰래 훔치듯이 맑은 공기 한 모금과 햇빛 한 줄기를 챙기지. 일하는 하루를 이루는 그 긴 시간들 동안, 그것만이 단조로운 삶을 기쁘게 해 주는 유일한 거니까. 케이트는 좀 더 멋진 동네로 다가가면서, 자기처럼 고된 일터로 서둘러 가는 이런 부류의 처녀들을 많이도 눈여겨봤어. 그리고 그들의 병색 짙은 얼굴과 비틀거리는 걸음걸이에서, 자기 불길한 예감이 아주 근거 없는 건 아니라는 너무도 또렷한 증거를 보았지."]], [["She arrived at Madame Mantalini’s some minutes before the appointed hour, and after walking a few times up and down, in the hope that some other female might arrive and spare her the embarrassment of stating her business to the servant, knocked timidly at the door: which, after some delay, was opened by the footman, who had been putting on his striped jacket as he came upstairs, and was now intent on fastening his apron.", "케이트는 약속한 시간보다 몇 분 일찍 마담 맨탈리니의 집에 닿았어. 혹시 다른 여자가 와서, 하인에게 용건을 말해야 하는 어색함을 덜어 줄까 싶어 몇 번 왔다 갔다 한 다음, 조심스럽게 문을 두드렸지. 한참 만에 하인이 문을 열었어. 그는 계단을 올라오면서 줄무늬 윗도리를 걸치던 참이었고, 이제는 앞치마를 매느라 정신이 없었어."]], [["‘Is Madame Mantalini in?’ faltered Kate.", "\"마담 맨탈리니 계신가요?\" 케이트가 더듬더듬 물었어."]], [["‘Not often out at this time, miss,’ replied the man in a tone which rendered “Miss,”", "\"이 시간에 나가 계시는 일은 잘 없죠, 아가씨.\""], ["something more offensive than “My dear.”", "하인이 대답했는데, 그 말투 때문에 \"아가씨\"라는 말이 \"이봐요\"보다도 더 거슬리게 들렸지."]], [["‘Can I see her?’ asked Kate.", "\"뵐 수 있을까요?\" 케이트가 물었어."]], [["‘Eh?’ replied the man, holding the door in his hand, and honouring the inquirer with a stare and a broad grin, ‘Lord, no.’", "\"네?\" 하인이 문손잡이를 잡은 채 대꾸했어. 그러고는 묻는 사람을 빤히 쳐다보더니 입을 헤벌쭉 벌리며 빈정대듯 말했지. \"원, 안 되죠.\""]], [["‘I came by her own appointment,’ said Kate; ‘I am--I am--to be employed here.’", "\"마님이 직접 약속하셔서 왔어요.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"저는요, 저는, 여기서 일하게 될 사람이거든요.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["you should have rung the worker’s bell,’ said the footman, touching the handle of one in the door-post.", "그럼 일꾼용 초인종을 눌렀어야죠.\" 하인이 문기둥에 달린 초인종 하나의 손잡이를 만지며 말했어."], ["‘Let me see, though, I forgot--Miss Nickleby, is it?’", "\"그런데 가만, 내가 깜빡했네. 니클비 아가씨, 맞죠?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Kate.", "\"네.\" 케이트가 대답했어."]], [["‘You’re to walk upstairs then, please,’ said the man. ‘Madame Mantalini wants to see you--this way--take care of these things on the floor.’", "\"그럼 위층으로 올라가셔야 해요.\" 하인이 말했어. \"마담 맨탈리니가 보자고 하셨거든요. 이쪽이에요. 바닥에 있는 것들 조심하시고요.\""]], [["Cautioning her, in these terms, not to trip over a heterogeneous litter of pastry-cook’s trays, lamps, waiters full of glasses, and piles of rout seats which were strewn about the hall, plainly bespeaking a late party on the previous night, the man led the way to the second story, and ushered Kate into a back-room, communicating by folding-doors with the apartment in which she had first seen the mistress of the establishment.", "이런 말로 케이트한테 조심하라고 일러 준 거야. 현관 바닥에는 제과점 쟁반이며 등잔이며 잔이 잔뜩 실린 받침이며 연회용 의자 더미가 여기저기 어수선하게 널려 있었거든. 누가 봐도 전날 밤 늦게까지 파티가 있었다는 걸 알 수 있었지. 하인은 앞장서서 이층으로 케이트를 데려갔어. 그리고 안쪽 방으로 안내했는데, 그 방은 접이문으로 다른 방과 통했어. 케이트가 처음에 그 가게 안주인을 만났던 바로 그 방이었지."]], [["‘If you’ll wait here a minute,’ said the man, ‘I’ll tell her presently.’ Having made this promise with much affability, he retired and left Kate alone.", "\"여기서 잠깐 기다리세요.\" 하인이 말했어. \"곧 마님께 알릴 테니까요.\" 이렇게 아주 상냥하게 약속하고는 물러갔고, 케이트만 혼자 남았어."]], [["There was not much to amuse in the room; of which the most attractive feature was, a half-length portrait in oil, of Mr.", "방 안에는 별로 눈요깃거리가 없었어. 그나마 제일 눈길을 끄는 건, 맨탈리니 씨를 그린 반신 유화 한 점이었지."], ["Mantalini, whom the artist had depicted scratching his head in an easy manner, and thus displaying to advantage a diamond ring, the gift of Madame Mantalini before her marriage.", "화가는 그를 한가롭게 머리를 긁적이는 모습으로 그려 놨는데, 덕분에 다이아몬드 반지가 보기 좋게 드러났어. 마담 맨탈리니가 결혼 전에 선물한 반지였지."], ["There was, however, the sound of voices in conversation in the next room; and as the conversation was loud and the partition thin, Kate could not help discovering that they belonged to Mr and Mrs.", "그런데 옆방에서 두런두런 이야기 나누는 소리가 들렸어."], ["Mantalini.", "게다가 대화 소리는 컸고 칸막이는 얇았으니, 케이트는 그게 맨탈리니 씨 부부의 목소리라는 걸 알아챌 수밖에 없었지."]], [["‘If you will be odiously, demnebly, outrigeously jealous, my soul,’ said Mr.", "\"내 영혼이여, 당신이 그렇게 밉살스럽고 빌어먹게 어처구니없이 질투를 부린다면,\" 맨탈리니 씨가 말했어."], ["Mantalini, ‘you will be very miserable--horrid miserable--demnition miserable.’ And then, there was a sound as though Mr.", "\"당신은 아주 비참해질 거야. 끔찍하게 비참하지."], ["Mantalini were sipping his coffee.", "빌어먹게 비참할 거라고.\" 그러더니 맨탈리니 씨가 커피를 홀짝이는 듯한 소리가 났어."]], [["‘I AM miserable,’ returned Madame Mantalini, evidently pouting.", "\"나는 정말 비참하다고요.\" 마담 맨탈리니가 입을 삐죽 내밀고 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Then you are an ungrateful, unworthy, demd unthankful little fairy,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 당신은 은혜도 모르고, 자격도 없고, 빌어먹게 고마운 줄도 모르는 작은 요정이야.\""], ["Mantalini.", "맨탈리니 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘I am not,’ returned Madame, with a sob.", "\"아니거든요.\" 마담이 흐느끼며 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Do not put itself out of humour,’ said Mr.", "\"기분 상해하지 말라고.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 달걀을 깨며 말했어."], ["Mantalini, breaking an egg.", "\"그건 예쁘고 사람 홀리는 빌어먹게 깜찍한 얼굴이야. 그러니 기분 상해하면 안 돼."], ["‘It is a pretty, bewitching little demd countenance, and it should not be out of humour, for it spoils its loveliness, and makes it cross and gloomy like a frightful, naughty, demd hobgoblin.’", "그러면 그 사랑스러움이 다 망가지거든. 그리고 무섭고 못된 빌어먹을 도깨비처럼 심술궂고 침울해 보인다고.\""]], [["‘I am not to be brought round in that way, always,’ rejoined Madame, sulkily.", "\"나는 늘 그런 식으로 넘어가지는 않아요.\" 마담이 토라진 채 받아쳤어."]], [["‘It shall be brought round in any way it likes best, and not brought round at all if it likes that better,’ retorted Mr.", "\"제일 좋아하는 방식으로 풀어 주지. 풀어 주지 않는 게 더 좋다면 아예 안 풀어 줘도 되고.\""], ["Mantalini, with his egg-spoon in his mouth.", "맨탈리니 씨가 달걀 숟가락을 입에 문 채 받아쳤어."]], [["‘It’s very easy to talk,’ said Mrs.", "\"말이야 쉽죠.\""], ["Mantalini.", "마담 맨탈리니가 말했어."]], [["‘Not so easy when one is eating a demnition egg,’ replied Mr.", "\"빌어먹을 달걀을 먹고 있을 땐 그리 쉽지가 않다고.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 대답했어."], ["Mantalini; ‘for the yolk runs down the waistcoat, and yolk of egg does not match any waistcoat but a yellow waistcoat, demmit.’", "\"노른자가 조끼에 줄줄 흐르거든. 그런데 달걀노른자는 노란 조끼 말고는 어떤 조끼하고도 안 어울려, 젠장.\""]], [["‘You were flirting with her during the whole night,’ said Madame Mantalini, apparently desirous to lead the conversation back to the point from which it had strayed.", "\"당신은 밤새도록 그 여자랑 시시덕거렸어요.\" 마담 맨탈리니가 말했어. 빗나갔던 대화를 다시 원래 자리로 돌려놓고 싶은 모양이었지."]], [["‘No, no, my life.’", "\"아니야, 아니야, 내 생명이여.\""]], [["‘You were,’ said Madame; ‘I had my eye upon you all the time.’", "\"맞아요.\" 마담이 말했어. \"내가 내내 당신을 지켜봤다고요.\""]], [["‘Bless the little winking twinkling eye; was it on me all the time!’ cried Mantalini, in a sort of lazy rapture.", "\"깜빡깜빡 반짝이는 그 작은 눈에 복이 있기를. 그게 내내 나를 보고 있었다고!\""], ["‘Oh, demmit!’", "맨탈리니가 어쩐지 나른한 황홀경에 빠져 외쳤어. \"오, 젠장!\""]], [["‘And I say once more,’ resumed Madame, ‘that you ought not to waltz with anybody but your own wife; and I will not bear it, Mantalini, if I take poison first.’", "\"그리고 다시 한번 말하는데,\" 마담이 말을 이었어. \"당신은 당신 아내 말고는 누구하고도 왈츠를 추면 안 돼요. 안 그러면 가만있지 않을 거예요, 맨탈리니. 내가 먼저 독약을 마셔 버릴 테니까.\""]], [["‘She will not take poison and have horrid pains, will she?’ said Mantalini; who, by the altered sound of his voice, seemed to have moved his chair, and taken up his position nearer to his wife. ‘She will not take poison, because she had a demd fine husband who might have married two countesses and a dowager--’", "\"독약을 마시고 끔찍한 고통을 겪지는 않을 거지, 그렇지?\" 맨탈리니가 말했어. 목소리가 달라진 걸 보니, 의자를 옮겨서 아내 가까이 자리를 잡은 모양이었지. \"독약은 안 마실 거야. 왜냐하면 당신한테는 빌어먹게 멋진 남편이 있으니까. 백작 부인 둘하고 미망인 하나하고도 결혼할 수 있었던 남편이 말이야.\""]], [["‘Two countesses,’ interposed Madame.", "\"백작 부인 둘이라고요.\" 마담이 끼어들었어."], ["‘You told me one before!’", "\"전에는 하나라고 했잖아요!\""]], [["‘Two!’ cried Mantalini.", "\"둘이야!\" 맨탈리니가 외쳤어."], ["‘Two demd fine women, real countesses and splendid fortunes, demmit.’", "\"빌어먹게 멋진 여자 둘. 진짜 백작 부인에다 어마어마한 재산까지, 젠장.\""]], [["‘And why didn’t you?’ asked Madame, playfully.", "\"그런데 왜 안 했어요?\" 마담이 장난스럽게 물었어."]], [["‘Why didn’t I!’ replied her husband.", "\"왜 안 했냐고!\" 남편이 대답했어."], ["‘Had I not seen, at a morning concert, the demdest little fascinator in all the world, and while that little fascinator is my wife, may not all the countesses and dowagers in England be--’", "\"내가 어느 날 오전 음악회에서, 온 세상에서 제일 빌어먹게 사람 홀리는 작은 여자를 보지 않았겠어. 그리고 그 작은 마법사가 내 아내인 한, 잉글랜드의 백작 부인이고 미망인이고 다 같이 ─\""]], [["Mr.", "맨탈리니 씨는 그 문장을 끝맺지 않았어. 대신 마담 맨탈리니한테 아주 요란한 입맞춤을 했지."], ["Mantalini did not finish the sentence, but he gave Madame Mantalini a very loud kiss, which Madame Mantalini returned; after which, there seemed to be some more kissing mixed up with the progress of the breakfast.", "마담 맨탈리니도 마주 입맞춤을 했어. 그러고 나서는 아침 식사가 이어지는 와중에 입맞춤이 좀 더 섞여 드는 것 같았지."]], [["‘And what about the cash, my existence’s jewel?’ said Mantalini, when these endearments ceased.", "\"그런데 돈은 어떻게 됐어, 내 인생의 보석?\" 이 살가운 짓들이 잦아들자 맨탈리니가 말했어."], ["‘How much have we in hand?’", "\"수중에 얼마나 있어?\""]], [["‘Very little indeed,’ replied Madame.", "\"정말 얼마 없어요.\" 마담이 대답했어."]], [["‘We must have some more,’ said Mantalini; ‘we must have some discount out of old Nickleby to carry on the war with, demmit.’", "\"좀 더 있어야 하는데.\" 맨탈리니가 말했어. \"늙은 니클비한테서 할인을 좀 받아서 이 싸움을 계속해 나가야지, 젠장.\""]], [["‘You can’t want any more just now,’ said Madame coaxingly.", "\"지금 당장 더 필요할 리는 없잖아요.\" 마담이 어르듯 말했어."]], [["‘My life and soul,’ returned her husband, ‘there is a horse for sale at Scrubbs’s, which it would be a sin and a crime to lose--going, my senses’ joy, for nothing.’", "\"내 생명이자 영혼이여,\" 남편이 대답했어. \"스크럽스네 가게에 팔려고 내놓은 말이 한 마리 있는데, 그걸 놓치는 건 죄악이자 범죄야. 내 감각의 기쁨이여, 거저나 다름없이 넘어가는 거라고.\""]], [["‘For nothing,’ cried Madame, ‘I am glad of that.’", "\"거저나 다름없다고요.\" 마담이 외쳤어. \"그것참 다행이네요.\""]], [["‘For actually nothing,’ replied Mantalini.", "\"정말로 거저나 다름없어.\" 맨탈리니가 대답했어."], ["‘A hundred guineas down will buy him; mane, and crest, and legs, and tail, all of the demdest beauty.", "\"현금 백 기니면 그 말을 살 수 있지. 갈기며 목덜미며 다리며 꼬리며, 전부 빌어먹게 아름다워."], ["I will ride him in the park before the very chariots of the rejected countesses.", "차여 버린 백작 부인들 마차 바로 앞에서 그 말을 타고 공원을 달릴 거야. 그 빌어먹을 늙은 미망인은 슬픔과 분노로 기절할 거고."], ["The demd old dowager will faint with grief and rage; the other two will say “He is married, he has made away with himself, it is a demd thing, it is all up!”", "나머지 둘은 이렇게 말하겠지. '저 남자는 결혼했어, 제 발로 사라져 버렸어, 빌어먹을 일이야, 다 끝났어!'"], ["They will hate each other demnebly, and wish you dead and buried.", "그 둘은 서로를 빌어먹게 미워하면서, 당신이 죽어 묻혀 버리기를 바랄걸."], ["Ha! ha!", "하하!"], ["Demmit.’", "젠장.\""]], [["Madame Mantalini’s prudence, if she had any, was not proof against these triumphal pictures; after a little jingling of keys, she observed that she would see what her desk contained, and rising for that purpose, opened the folding-door, and walked into the room where Kate was seated.", "마담 맨탈리니가 신중함이라는 걸 갖고 있었다 해도, 그 신중함은 이런 의기양양한 그림 앞에서는 버티지 못했어. 그녀는 열쇠를 짤랑짤랑 흔들더니, 책상 안에 뭐가 들었는지 봐야겠다고 했지. 그러고는 그러려고 일어나, 접이문을 열고 케이트가 앉아 있는 방으로 걸어 들어왔어."]], [["‘Dear me, child!’ exclaimed Madame Mantalini, recoiling in surprise.", "\"어머나, 얘야!\" 마담 맨탈리니가 깜짝 놀라 뒤로 물러서며 소리쳤어."], ["‘How came you here?’", "\"너 여기 어떻게 왔니?\""]], [["‘Child!’ cried Mantalini, hurrying in.", "\"얘야!\" 맨탈리니가 서둘러 들어오며 외쳤어."], ["‘How came--eh!--oh--demmit, how d’ye do?’", "\"어떻게 왔, 엥! 오, 젠장, 안녕하시오?\""]], [["‘I have been waiting, here some time, ma’am,’ said Kate, addressing Madame Mantalini.", "\"여기서 한참을 기다렸어요, 마님.\" 케이트가 마담 맨탈리니를 향해 말했어."], ["‘The servant must have forgotten to let you know that I was here, I think.’", "\"하인이 제가 와 있는 걸 알려 드리는 걸 깜빡한 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘You really must see to that man,’ said Madame, turning to her husband.", "\"그 사람을 정말 단단히 일러둬야겠어요.\" 마담이 남편을 돌아보며 말했어."], ["‘He forgets everything.’", "\"그자는 뭐든지 다 깜빡한다니까요.\""]], [["‘I will twist his demd nose off his countenance for leaving such a very pretty creature all alone by herself,’ said her husband.", "\"이렇게 예쁜 아가씨를 혼자 내버려 둔 죄로, 그자의 빌어먹을 코를 얼굴에서 비틀어 떼어 내야겠군.\" 남편이 말했어."]], [["‘Mantalini,’ cried Madame, ‘you forget yourself.’", "\"맨탈리니,\" 마담이 외쳤어. \"당신 정신 좀 차려요.\""]], [["‘I don’t forget you, my soul, and never shall, and never can,’ said Mantalini, kissing his wife’s hand, and grimacing aside, to Miss Nickleby, who turned away.", "\"당신을 잊은 적은 없어, 내 영혼이여. 앞으로도 결코 없을 거고, 그럴 수도 없지.\" 맨탈리니가 아내의 손에 입을 맞추며 말했어. 그러고는 옆으로 고개를 돌려 니클비 아가씨한테 우스꽝스러운 표정을 지어 보였지만, 케이트는 외면해 버렸어."]], [["Appeased by this compliment, the lady of the business took some papers from her desk which she handed over to Mr.", "이 칭찬에 누그러진 가게 안주인은 책상에서 서류 몇 장을 꺼내 맨탈리니 씨한테 건넸어."], ["Mantalini, who received them with great delight.", "맨탈리니 씨는 그걸 아주 기쁘게 받았지."], ["She then requested Kate to follow her, and after several feints on the part of Mr.", "그러고 나서 마담은 케이트더러 따라오라고 했어."], ["Mantalini to attract the young lady’s attention, they went away: leaving that gentleman extended at full length on the sofa, with his heels in the air and a newspaper in his hand.", "그리고 맨탈리니 씨가 그 젊은 아가씨의 관심을 끌어 보려고 몇 번 수작을 부린 다음, 두 사람은 자리를 떴어. 그 신사는 소파 위에 발뒤꿈치를 허공에 올린 채 길게 드러누워, 손에 신문 한 장을 들고 있었지."]], [["Madame Mantalini led the way down a flight of stairs, and through a passage, to a large room at the back of the premises where were a number of young women employed in sewing, cutting out, making up, altering, and various other processes known only to those who are cunning in the arts of millinery and dressmaking.", "마담 맨탈리니가 앞장서서 계단을 하나 내려가더니, 복도를 지나 건물 뒤쪽에 있는 큰 방으로 갔어. 거기에는 젊은 여자들이 여럿 있었는데, 바느질하고, 마름질하고, 옷을 짓고, 고치고, 그 밖에 모자나 옷 만드는 재주에 익숙한 사람들이나 알 법한 갖가지 일을 하고 있었지."], ["It was a close room with a skylight, and as dull and quiet as a room need be.", "천장에 채광창이 하나 달린 답답한 방이었어. 그리고 방이라고 하면 으레 그러려니 싶을 만큼 칙칙하고 조용했지."]], [["On Madame Mantalini calling aloud for Miss Knag, a short, bustling, over-dressed female, full of importance, presented herself, and all the young ladies suspending their operations for the moment, whispered to each other sundry criticisms upon the make and texture of Miss Nickleby’s dress, her complexion, cast of features, and personal appearance, with as much good breeding as could have been displayed by the very best society in a crowded ball-room.", "마담 맨탈리니가 큰 소리로 미스 내그를 부르자, 키 작고 부산스럽고 옷을 잔뜩 차려입은 여자가 나타났어. 자기가 대단한 사람이라는 티가 잔뜩 났지. 그러자 젊은 아가씨들이 다들 하던 일을 잠깐 멈추고, 니클비 아가씨의 옷 모양새며 옷감이며 얼굴빛이며 이목구비며 차림새에 대해 이러쿵저러쿵 서로 소곤거렸어. 사람으로 붐비는 무도회장에서 최고 상류 사회 사람들이 보여 줄 법한 만큼의 점잖음을 갖추고서 말이야."]], [["‘Oh, Miss Knag,’ said Madame Mantalini, ‘this is the young person I spoke to you about.’", "\"오, 미스 내그,\" 마담 맨탈리니가 말했어. \"이 아가씨가 내가 당신한테 말했던 그 사람이에요.\""]], [["Miss Knag bestowed a reverential smile upon Madame Mantalini, which she dexterously transformed into a gracious one for Kate, and said that certainly, although it was a great deal of trouble to have young people who were wholly unused to the business, still, she was sure the young person would try to do her best--impressed with which conviction she (Miss Knag) felt an interest in her, already.", "미스 내그는 마담 맨탈리니한테 공손한 미소를 지어 보이더니, 그 미소를 능숙하게 바꿔서 케이트한테 친절한 미소로 보냈어. 그러고는 이렇게 말했지. 일에 전혀 익숙지 않은 젊은 사람을 데리고 있는 게 무척 성가신 일이긴 하지만, 그래도 이 아가씨가 최선을 다하려고 애쓸 거라고 믿는다고. 그런 확신이 들고 보니, 자기는(미스 내그 말이야) 벌써 이 아가씨한테 관심이 간다고 말이야."]], [["‘I think that, for the present at all events, it will be better for Miss Nickleby to come into the show-room with you, and try things on for people,’ said Madame Mantalini.", "\"내 생각엔 적어도 당분간은, 니클비 아가씨를 당신과 함께 진열실에 두고 손님들한테 옷을 입혀 보는 일을 맡기는 게 낫겠어요.\" 마담 맨탈리니가 말했어."], ["‘She will not be able for the present to be of much use in any other way; and her appearance will--’", "\"당분간은 다른 일로는 별로 쓸모가 없을 테니까요. 그리고 저 아가씨 생김새가 ─\""]], [["‘Suit very well with mine, Madame Mantalini,’ interrupted Miss Knag. ‘So it will; and to be sure I might have known that you would not be long in finding that out; for you have so much taste in all those matters, that really, as I often say to the young ladies, I do not know how, when, or where, you possibly could have acquired all you know--hem--Miss Nickleby and I are quite a pair, Madame Mantalini, only I am a little darker than Miss Nickleby, and--hem--I think my foot may be a little smaller. Miss Nickleby, I am sure, will not be offended at my saying that, when she hears that our family always have been celebrated for small feet ever since--hem--ever since our family had any feet at all, indeed, I think. I had an uncle once, Madame Mantalini, who lived in Cheltenham, and had a most excellent business as a tobacconist--hem--who had such small feet, that they were no bigger than those which are usually joined to wooden legs--the most symmetrical feet, Madame Mantalini, that even you can imagine.’", "\"제 생김새하고 아주 잘 어울리죠, 마담 맨탈리니.\" 미스 내그가 끼어들었어. \"그럼요, 그렇고말고요. 사실 마님이 그걸 금방 알아채실 거라는 걸 제가 진작 알았어야 했는데 말이죠. 마님은 그런 일에 안목이 어찌나 뛰어나신지, 정말이지 제가 젊은 아가씨들한테 자주 하는 말이지만, 마님이 알고 계신 그 모든 걸 도대체 어떻게, 언제, 어디서 익히셨는지 저는 도무지 모르겠다니까요. 흠. 니클비 아가씨하고 저는 그야말로 한 쌍이에요, 마담 맨탈리니. 다만 제가 니클비 아가씨보다 살빛이 좀 더 짙고, 흠, 제 생각엔 발도 제 발이 좀 더 작을 것 같네요. 우리 집안은 옛날부터, 흠, 사실 우리 집안에 발이라는 게 달리기 시작한 그때부터 늘 작은 발로 유명했다는 걸 니클비 아가씨가 들으면, 제가 이런 말을 해도 기분 나빠하지 않을 거예요. 저한테 옛날에 삼촌이 한 분 계셨는데요, 마담 맨탈리니, 첼튼엄에 사셨고 담배 가게로 아주 잘되는 장사를 하셨죠. 흠. 그 삼촌 발이 어찌나 작았던지, 흔히 의족에 붙어 있는 발보다도 크지 않았다니까요. 마님조차 상상하실 수 있는 한 제일 균형 잡힌 발이었죠, 마담 맨탈리니.\""]], [["‘They must have had something of the appearance of club feet, Miss Knag,’ said Madame.", "\"그럼 그건 안짱발 비슷하게 생겼겠네요, 미스 내그.\" 마담이 말했어."]], [["‘Well now, that is so like you,’ returned Miss Knag, ‘Ha!", "\"어머나, 그게 바로 마님다운 말씀이에요.\" 미스 내그가 받아쳤어."], ["ha!", "\"하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["Of club feet!", "하! 안짱발이라니!"], ["Oh very good!", "오, 정말 멋져요!"], ["As I often remark to the young ladies, “Well I must say, and I do not care who knows it, of all the ready humour--hem--I ever heard anywhere”--and I have heard a good deal; for when my dear brother was alive (I kept house for him, Miss Nickleby), we had to supper once a week two or three young men, highly celebrated in those days for their humour, Madame Mantalini--“Of all the ready humour,”", "제가 젊은 아가씨들한테 자주 하는 말이지만, '글쎄, 누가 알든 상관없이 말하겠는데, 내가 어디서든 들어 본 그 모든 즉석 재치 중에서, 흠' 사실 저는 어지간히 많이 들어 봤거든요."], ["I say to the young ladies, “I ever heard, Madame Mantalini’s is the most remarkable--hem.", "우리 사랑하는 오빠가 살아 있을 때(제가 오빠 집 살림을 맡았었죠, 니클비 아가씨), 일주일에 한 번씩 그 시절에 재치로 아주 유명했던 젊은 남자 두세 명을 저녁 식사에 초대했으니까요, 마담 맨탈리니. '내가 들어 본 그 모든 즉석 재치 중에서', 제가 젊은 아가씨들한테 이렇게 말하죠."], ["It is so gentle, so sarcastic, and yet so good-natured (as I was observing to Miss Simmonds only this morning), that how, or when, or by what means she acquired it, is to me a mystery indeed.”’", "'마담 맨탈리니의 재치가 가장 놀라워, 흠. 어찌나 부드럽고, 어찌나 풍자적이면서도, 또 어찌나 너그러운지(오늘 아침에도 미스 시먼즈한테 한 말이지만), 그걸 어떻게, 언제, 무슨 수로 익히셨는지 나로서는 정말 수수께끼야'라고요.\""]], [["Here Miss Knag paused to take breath, and while she pauses it may be observed--not that she was marvellously loquacious and marvellously deferential to Madame Mantalini, since these are facts which require no comment; but that every now and then, she was accustomed, in the torrent of her discourse, to introduce a loud, shrill, clear ‘hem!’ the import and meaning of which, was variously interpreted by her acquaintance; some holding that Miss Knag dealt in exaggeration, and introduced the monosyllable when any fresh invention was in course of coinage in her brain; others, that when she wanted a word, she threw it in to gain time, and prevent anybody else from striking into the conversation. It may be further remarked, that Miss Knag still aimed at youth, although she had shot beyond it, years ago; and that she was weak and vain, and one of those people who are best described by the axiom, that you may trust them as far as you can see them, and no farther.", "여기서 미스 내그는 숨을 돌리려고 말을 멈췄어. 그녀가 잠깐 멈춘 사이에 한 가지 짚어 둘 게 있어. 그녀가 놀랄 만큼 수다스럽고 마담 맨탈리니한테 놀랄 만큼 굽실거린다는 건 굳이 말할 것도 없는 사실이니까 빼고 말이야. 그녀는 말을 폭포처럼 쏟아내는 와중에 이따금 크고 새되고 또렷한 \"흠!\" 소리를 끼워 넣는 버릇이 있었거든. 그 소리가 무슨 뜻인지는 그녀를 아는 사람마다 제각각 해석했어. 어떤 사람들은 미스 내그가 과장을 일삼는데, 머릿속에서 뭔가 새로운 거짓말을 지어내는 중일 때 그 외마디를 끼워 넣는다고 했지. 또 어떤 사람들은, 그녀가 단어가 생각 안 날 때 시간을 벌고 다른 사람이 끼어들지 못하게 하려고 그 소리를 던지는 거라고 했어. 한 가지 더 짚어 두자면, 미스 내그는 젊음에서 이미 몇 해 전에 한참 벗어났는데도 여전히 젊은 척했다는 거야. 그리고 그녀는 마음이 약하고 허영심이 많아서, 이런 격언으로 가장 잘 설명되는 부류였지. 눈에 보이는 데까지만 믿을 수 있고, 그 이상은 못 믿을 사람이라는 격언 말이야."]], [["‘You’ll take care that Miss Nickleby understands her hours, and so forth,’ said Madame Mantalini; ‘and so I’ll leave her with you.", "\"니클비 아가씨가 근무 시간이며 그런 것들을 잘 알아듣도록 챙겨 주세요.\" 마담 맨탈리니가 말했어."], ["You’ll not forget my directions, Miss Knag?’", "\"그럼 이 아가씨를 당신한테 맡길게요. 내 지시 잊지 않으실 거죠, 미스 내그?\""]], [["Miss Knag of course replied, that to forget anything Madame Mantalini had directed, was a moral impossibility; and that lady, dispensing a general good-morning among her assistants, sailed away.", "미스 내그는 물론 이렇게 대답했지. 마담 맨탈리니가 지시한 걸 잊어버리는 건 도덕적으로 불가능한 일이라고. 그러자 마담은 직원들한테 두루 아침 인사를 건네고는 미끄러지듯 떠나갔어."]], [["‘Charming creature, isn’t she, Miss Nickleby?’ said Miss Knag, rubbing her hands together.", "\"매력적인 분이지, 안 그래, 니클비 아가씨?\" 미스 내그가 두 손을 비비며 말했어."]], [["‘I have seen very little of her,’ said Kate.", "\"제가 그분을 거의 못 봬서요.\" 케이트가 말했어."], ["‘I hardly know yet.’", "\"아직 잘 모르겠어요.\""]], [["‘Have you seen Mr.", "\"맨탈리니 씨는 봤어?\""], ["Mantalini?’ inquired Miss Knag.", "미스 내그가 물었어."]], [["‘Yes; I have seen him twice.’", "\"네, 두 번 뵀어요.\""]], [["‘Isn’t HE a charming creature?’", "\"그분도 매력적인 분 아니야?\""]], [["‘Indeed he does not strike me as being so, by any means,’ replied Kate.", "\"제가 보기엔 아무래도 그렇지가 않은데요.\" 케이트가 대답했어."]], [["‘No, my dear!’ cried Miss Knag, elevating her hands.", "\"어머나, 얘야!\" 미스 내그가 두 손을 치켜들며 외쳤어."], ["‘Why, goodness gracious mercy, where’s your taste?", "\"아니, 세상에 맙소사, 네 안목은 어디다 둔 거니?"], ["Such a fine tall, full-whiskered dashing gentlemanly man, with such teeth and hair, and--hem--well now, you DO astonish me.’", "그렇게 늘씬하고 키 크고 구레나룻이 멋진 데다 세련된 신사양반을, 그런 치아에 그런 머릿결을, 흠, 글쎄, 너 정말 날 놀라게 하는구나.\""]], [["‘I dare say I am very foolish,’ replied Kate, laying aside her bonnet; ‘but as my opinion is of very little importance to him or anyone else, I do not regret having formed it, and shall be slow to change it, I think.’", "\"제가 어리석은가 봐요.\" 케이트가 보닛을 내려놓으며 대답했어. \"하지만 제 의견이라는 게 그분한테든 누구한테든 별로 중요하지도 않으니, 그렇게 생각한 걸 후회하지는 않아요. 그리고 그 생각을 쉽게 바꾸지도 않을 것 같고요.\""]], [["‘He is a very fine man, don’t you think so?’ asked one of the young ladies.", "\"그분 정말 멋진 분 아니에요?\" 젊은 아가씨 하나가 물었어."]], [["‘Indeed he may be, for anything I could say to the contrary,’ replied Kate.", "\"그럴지도 모르죠, 아니라고 할 만한 게 저한테는 없으니까요.\" 케이트가 대답했어."]], [["‘And drives very beautiful horses, doesn’t he?’ inquired another.", "\"그리고 아주 아름다운 말들도 모시잖아요, 안 그래요?\" 또 다른 아가씨가 물었어."]], [["‘I dare say he may, but I never saw them,’ answered Kate.", "\"그럴지도 모르죠, 하지만 저는 그 말들을 본 적이 없어요.\" 케이트가 대답했어."]], [["‘Never saw them!’ interposed Miss Knag.", "\"본 적이 없다고!\" 미스 내그가 끼어들었어."], ["‘Oh, well!", "\"오, 저런!"], ["There it is at once you know; how can you possibly pronounce an opinion about a gentleman--hem--if you don’t see him as he turns out altogether?’", "자, 그러니까 당장 이게 문제라는 거야. 신사를 그분이 차려입고 나선 모습 전부로 보지 않으면, 흠, 그분에 대해 무슨 의견을 내놓을 수가 있겠니?\""]], [["There was so much of the world--even of the little world of the country girl--in this idea of the old milliner, that Kate, who was anxious, for every reason, to change the subject, made no further remark, and left Miss Knag in possession of the field.", "이 늙은 모자 장수의 생각에는 세상 물정이라는 게 잔뜩 들어 있었어. 시골 처녀의 작은 세상에 비춰 봐도 그랬지. 무슨 까닭에서든 화제를 돌리고 싶었던 케이트는 더 이상 말을 잇지 않고, 미스 내그한테 마당을 통째로 내줬어."]], [["After a short silence, during which most of the young people made a closer inspection of Kate’s appearance, and compared notes respecting it, one of them offered to help her off with her shawl, and the offer being accepted, inquired whether she did not find black very uncomfortable wear.", "잠깐 침묵이 흘렀어. 그동안 젊은 사람들 대부분이 케이트의 차림새를 좀 더 자세히 뜯어보고 그것에 대해 서로 의견을 견줬지. 그러다 그중 하나가 케이트의 숄 벗는 걸 도와주겠다고 했어. 케이트가 그 호의를 받아들이자, 그 아가씨는 검은 옷이 입기에 아주 불편하지 않냐고 물었어."]], [["‘I do indeed,’ replied Kate, with a bitter sigh.", "\"정말 그래요.\" 케이트가 쓰디쓴 한숨을 쉬며 대답했어."]], [["‘So dusty and hot,’ observed the same speaker, adjusting her dress for her.", "\"먼지도 많이 타고 덥기도 하고요.\" 같은 사람이 케이트의 옷매무새를 바로잡아 주며 말했어."]], [["Kate might have said, that mourning is sometimes the coldest wear which mortals can assume; that it not only chills the breasts of those it clothes, but extending its influence to summer friends, freezes up their sources of good-will and kindness, and withering all the buds of promise they once so liberally put forth, leaves nothing but bared and rotten hearts exposed.", "케이트는 이렇게 말할 수도 있었을 거야. 상복이란 때로 사람이 걸칠 수 있는 가장 차가운 옷이라고."], ["There are few who have lost a friend or relative constituting in life their sole dependence, who have not keenly felt this chilling influence of their sable garb.", "그건 그 옷을 입은 사람의 가슴을 얼릴 뿐 아니라, 그 영향이 좋은 시절의 친구들한테까지 뻗쳐서 그들의 호의와 친절의 샘까지 얼려 버리지. 그리고 그들이 전에 그토록 아낌없이 피워 냈던 약속의 꽃봉오리를 죄다 시들게 하고는, 헐벗고 썩은 마음만 드러낸 채 남겨 두는 거야."], ["She had felt it acutely, and feeling it at the moment, could not quite restrain her tears.", "살면서 자기가 유일하게 기댔던 친구나 친척을 잃은 사람치고, 검은 옷의 이 차가운 영향을 사무치게 느껴 보지 않은 사람은 드물어. 케이트는 그걸 뼈저리게 느꼈고, 그 순간에도 느끼면서, 눈물을 차마 다 참지 못했지."]], [["‘I am very sorry to have wounded you by my thoughtless speech,’ said her companion.", "\"생각 없이 한 말로 아가씨 마음을 아프게 해서 정말 미안해요.\""], ["‘I did not think of it.", "그 동료가 말했어."], ["You are in mourning for some near relation?’", "\"거기까지는 생각을 못 했어요. 가까운 친척 상중인가요?\""]], [["‘For my father,’ answered Kate.", "\"아버지요.\" 케이트가 대답했어."]], [["‘For what relation, Miss Simmonds?’ asked Miss Knag, in an audible voice.", "\"무슨 친척이래, 미스 시먼즈?\" 미스 내그가 들으라는 듯한 목소리로 물었어."]], [["‘Her father,’ replied the other softly.", "\"아버지래요.\" 다른 아가씨가 나직이 대답했어."]], [["‘Her father, eh?’ said Miss Knag, without the slightest depression of her voice.", "\"아버지라고, 응?\" 미스 내그가 목소리를 조금도 낮추지 않은 채 말했어."], ["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["A long illness, Miss Simmonds?’", "오래 앓으셨대, 미스 시먼즈?\""]], [["‘Hush,’ replied the girl; ‘I don’t know.’", "\"쉿,\" 그 아가씨가 대답했어. \"저도 몰라요.\""]], [["‘Our misfortune was very sudden,’ said Kate, turning away, ‘or I might perhaps, at a time like this, be enabled to support it better.’", "\"갑작스러운 불행이었어요.\" 케이트가 고개를 돌리며 말했어. \"안 그랬다면 이런 때에 제가 좀 더 잘 견뎌 낼 수 있었을지도 모르는데.\""]], [["There had existed not a little desire in the room, according to invariable custom, when any new ‘young person’ came, to know who Kate was, and what she was, and all about her; but, although it might have been very naturally increased by her appearance and emotion, the knowledge that it pained her to be questioned, was sufficient to repress even this curiosity; and Miss Knag, finding it hopeless to attempt extracting any further particulars just then, reluctantly commanded silence, and bade the work proceed.", "그 방에서는 으레 그렇듯이, 새 '아가씨'가 오면 케이트가 누구인지, 무엇을 하던 사람인지, 그녀에 관한 모든 걸 알고 싶어 하는 마음이 적잖이 있었어. 그리고 그 호기심은 케이트의 생김새와 감정 때문에 아주 자연스럽게 더 커졌을 법도 했지. 하지만 케이트가 질문받는 걸 아파한다는 걸 알게 되자, 그것만으로도 그 호기심을 누르기에 충분했어. 미스 내그는 당장 더 캐물어 봤자 별 소득이 없겠다 싶었던지, 마지못해 조용히 하라고 명하고는 일을 계속하라고 했어."]], [["In silence, then, the tasks were plied until half-past one, when a baked leg of mutton, with potatoes to correspond, were served in the kitchen. The meal over, and the young ladies having enjoyed the additional relaxation of washing their hands, the work began again, and was again performed in silence, until the noise of carriages rattling through the streets, and of loud double knocks at doors, gave token that the day’s work of the more fortunate members of society was proceeding in its turn.", "그렇게 한 시 반까지 다들 말없이 일에 매달렸어. 한 시 반이 되자 구운 양 다릿살에다 거기 맞춘 감자가 부엌에 차려졌지. 식사가 끝나고 젊은 아가씨들이 손 씻는 여유까지 누리고 나자, 일은 다시 시작됐어. 그리고 또 말없이 진행됐지. 마차들이 거리를 덜컹덜컹 달리는 소리와 문마다 요란하게 두 번씩 두드리는 소리가 들리기 전까지 말이야. 그 소리는 세상에서 좀 더 운 좋은 사람들의 하루 일과가 이제 막 시작됐다는 신호였어."]], [["One of these double knocks at Madame Mantalini’s door, announced the equipage of some great lady--or rather rich one, for there is occasionally a distinction between riches and greatness--who had come with her daughter to approve of some court-dresses which had been a long time preparing, and upon whom Kate was deputed to wait, accompanied by Miss Knag, and officered of course by Madame Mantalini.", "마담 맨탈리니네 문을 그렇게 두 번 두드린 소리 가운데 하나는, 어느 대단한 귀부인의, 아니 차라리 돈 많은 부인의 마차가 왔다는 걸 알리는 거였어. 부유함과 대단함은 가끔 별개니까 말이야. 그 부인은 딸을 데리고 왔는데, 오랫동안 준비해 온 궁정 예복 몇 벌을 살펴보러 온 거였지. 케이트는 그 부인의 시중을 들도록 차출됐어. 미스 내그가 함께했고, 물론 마담 맨탈리니가 지휘를 맡았지."]], [["Kate’s part in the pageant was humble enough, her duties being limited to holding articles of costume until Miss Knag was ready to try them on, and now and then tying a string, or fastening a hook-and-eye. She might, not unreasonably, have supposed herself beneath the reach of any arrogance, or bad humour; but it happened that the lady and daughter were both out of temper that day, and the poor girl came in for her share of their revilings. She was awkward--her hands were cold--dirty--coarse--she could do nothing right; they wondered how Madame Mantalini could have such people about her; requested they might see some other young woman the next time they came; and so forth.", "이 볼거리에서 케이트가 맡은 역할은 보잘것없었어. 그녀가 하는 일이란 미스 내그가 옷을 입혀 볼 준비가 될 때까지 옷가지를 들고 있는 것, 그리고 가끔 끈을 매거나 걸쇠를 채우는 것뿐이었으니까. 케이트는 자기가 어떤 거만함이나 심술도 닿지 못할 자리에 있다고 생각했을 만도 해. 그게 무리한 생각도 아니었고. 그런데 마침 그 부인과 딸이 그날 둘 다 기분이 언짢았던 거야. 그래서 가엾은 케이트도 그들의 욕설에서 제 몫을 받게 됐지. 케이트는 굼뜨다는 둥, 손이 차다는 둥, 더럽다는 둥, 거칠다는 둥, 제대로 하는 게 하나도 없다는 둥. 그들은 마담 맨탈리니가 어떻게 이런 사람들을 곁에 둘 수 있냐며 의아해했어. 다음에 올 때는 다른 아가씨를 보게 해 달라고 했고, 그런 식이었지."]], [["So common an occurrence would be hardly deserving of mention, but for its effect.", "이렇게 흔한 일은 그 결과만 아니라면 굳이 입에 올릴 만한 것도 못 돼. 케이트는 그 사람들이 가고 나자 쓰디쓴 눈물을 많이도 흘렸어."], ["Kate shed many bitter tears when these people were gone, and felt, for the first time, humbled by her occupation.", "그리고 자기 일 때문에 처음으로 모욕감을 느꼈지. 케이트가 힘든 잡일과 고된 노동을 앞두고 주춤했던 건 사실이야."], ["She had, it is true, quailed at the prospect of drudgery and hard service; but she had felt no degradation in working for her bread, until she found herself exposed to insolence and pride.", "하지만 빵을 벌려고 일하는 것에서는 아무런 비참함도 느끼지 않았었지. 무례함과 오만함에 노출되기 전까지는 말이야."], ["Philosophy would have taught her that the degradation was on the side of those who had sunk so low as to display such passions habitually, and without cause: but she was too young for such consolation, and her honest feeling was hurt.", "철학이라면 케이트한테 이렇게 가르쳐 줬을 거야. 비참함은 오히려 까닭도 없이 그런 감정을 버릇처럼 드러낼 만큼 천하게 굴러떨어진 사람들 쪽에 있는 거라고."], ["May not the complaint, that common people are above their station, often take its rise in the fact of UNcommon people being below theirs?", "하지만 케이트는 그런 위안을 받기에는 너무 어렸고, 그녀의 진솔한 감정은 상처를 입었어. 평범한 사람들이 제 분수를 넘어선다는 불평은, 사실 평범하지 않은 사람들이 제 분수에 못 미치는 데서 비롯되는 경우가 많지 않을까?"]], [["In such scenes and occupations the time wore on until nine o’clock, when Kate, jaded and dispirited with the occurrences of the day, hastened from the confinement of the workroom, to join her mother at the street corner, and walk home:--the more sadly, from having to disguise her real feelings, and feign to participate in all the sanguine visions of her companion.", "이런 장면과 일 속에서 시간은 아홉 시까지 흘러갔어. 그제야 케이트는 그날 있었던 일로 지치고 풀이 죽은 채, 답답한 작업실에서 서둘러 빠져나왔지. 길모퉁이에서 어머니와 만나 집까지 걸어가려고 말이야. 그런데 그 걸음이 더 서글펐던 건, 진짜 속마음을 감추고 어머니의 온갖 장밋빛 환상에 함께 들떠 있는 척해야 했기 때문이야."]], [["‘Bless my soul, Kate,’ said Mrs.", "\"어머나 세상에, 케이트,\""], ["Nickleby; ‘I’ve been thinking all day what a delightful thing it would be for Madame Mantalini to take you into partnership--such a likely thing too, you know!", "니클비 부인이 말했어. \"내가 오늘 하루 종일 무슨 생각을 했냐면, 마담 맨탈리니가 너를 동업자로 들이면 얼마나 좋을까 하는 거였단다."], ["Why, your poor dear papa’s cousin’s sister-in-law--a Miss Browndock--was taken into partnership by a lady that kept a school at Hammersmith, and made her fortune in no time at all.", "그것도 충분히 있을 법한 일이고 말이야, 알지! 아니, 너희 가엾은 아빠의 사촌의 시누이가 말이다, 미스 브라운독이라는 분인데, 해머스미스에서 학교를 하던 어떤 부인 밑으로 동업자가 들어가서 눈 깜짝할 새에 떼돈을 벌었거든."], ["I forget, by-the-bye, whether that Miss Browndock was the same lady that got the ten thousand pounds prize in the lottery, but I think she was; indeed, now I come to think of it, I am sure she was.", "그건 그렇고 그 미스 브라운독이 복권에서 만 파운드 상금을 탄 그 부인하고 같은 사람인지 아닌지는 깜빡했지만, 같은 사람인 것 같아. 사실 지금 생각해 보니 틀림없이 그래."], ["“Mantalini and Nickleby”, how well it would sound!--and if Nicholas has any good fortune, you might have Doctor Nickleby, the head-master of Westminster School, living in the same street.’", "'맨탈리니 앤드 니클비', 얼마나 듣기 좋니! 그리고 니콜라스한테 무슨 좋은 운이라도 따른다면, 웨스트민스터 학교 교장 선생님 니클비 박사님이 같은 거리에 사시게 될 수도 있고 말이야.\""]], [["‘Dear Nicholas!’ cried Kate, taking from her reticule her brother’s letter from Dotheboys Hall.", "\"사랑하는 니콜라스!\" 케이트가 손가방에서 두더보이즈 홀에서 온 오빠의 편지를 꺼내며 외쳤어."], ["‘In all our misfortunes, how happy it makes me, mama, to hear he is doing well, and to find him writing in such good spirits!", "\"우리가 이 모든 불행을 겪는 와중에도, 엄마, 오빠가 잘 지낸다는 소식을 들으니, 그리고 그렇게 기운찬 글을 쓴 걸 보니 얼마나 마음이 놓이는지 몰라요!"], ["It consoles me for all we may undergo, to think that he is comfortable and happy.’", "오빠가 편안하고 행복하다고 생각하면, 우리가 앞으로 무슨 일을 겪든 다 위로가 돼요.\""]], [["Poor Kate!", "가엾은 케이트!"], ["she little thought how weak her consolation was, and how soon she would be undeceived.", "그녀는 그 위안이 얼마나 허약한지, 그리고 얼마나 빨리 그 착각에서 깨어나게 될지 까맣게 몰랐어."]], [["# CHAPTER 18", "# CHAPTER 18"]], [["Miss Knag, after doting on Kate Nickleby for three whole Days, makes up her Mind to hate her for evermore.", "케이트 니클비를 사흘 내내 끔찍이 아끼던 내그 양이, 이제부터 영원히 그녀를 미워하기로 마음을 정한다."], ["The Causes which led Miss Knag to form this Resolution", "내그 양이 이런 결심을 하게 된 까닭들"]], [["There are many lives of much pain, hardship, and suffering, which, having no stirring interest for any but those who lead them, are disregarded by persons who do not want thought or feeling, but who pamper their compassion and need high stimulants to rouse it.", "세상에는 고통과 고난과 괴로움으로 가득한 삶이 참 많아. 그런데 그런 삶을 직접 사는 사람 말고는 누구한테도 별 흥미를 끌지 못해서, 다들 그냥 지나쳐 버리지. 생각이나 감정이 메마른 사람들이 아닌데도 말이야. 그저 자기 동정심을 곱게 받들어 모시느라, 그 동정심을 일깨우려면 아주 자극이 센 게 필요한 사람들이지."]], [["There are not a few among the disciples of charity who require, in their vocation, scarcely less excitement than the votaries of pleasure in theirs; and hence it is that diseased sympathy and compassion are every day expended on out-of-the-way objects, when only too many demands upon the legitimate exercise of the same virtues in a healthy state, are constantly within the sight and hearing of the most unobservant person alive. In short, charity must have its romance, as the novelist or playwright must have his. A thief in fustian is a vulgar character, scarcely to be thought of by persons of refinement; but dress him in green velvet, with a high-crowned hat, and change the scene of his operations, from a thickly-peopled city, to a mountain road, and you shall find in him the very soul of poetry and adventure. So it is with the one great cardinal virtue, which, properly nourished and exercised, leads to, if it does not necessarily include, all the others. It must have its romance; and the less of real, hard, struggling work-a-day life there is in that romance, the better.", "자선을 베푸는 이들 가운데에는, 자기 일을 하면서 쾌락을 좇는 사람들 못지않게 강한 흥분을 바라는 이들이 적지 않아. 그래서 병든 공감과 동정심이 날마다 엉뚱한 데로 쏟아지지. 정작 건강한 상태에서 그 미덕을 제대로 베풀어야 할 곳이 넘쳐 나는데도 말이야. 그런 곳은 세상에서 제일 무심한 사람의 눈과 귀에도 늘 닿을 만큼 가까이 있는데도. 한마디로, 자선에도 낭만이 있어야 하는 거야. 소설가나 극작가한테 낭만이 필요한 것처럼 말이지. 거친 무명천 옷을 입은 도둑은 천박한 인물이라, 점잖은 사람들은 거들떠보지도 않아. 그런데 그 도둑한테 초록색 벨벳 옷을 입히고 챙이 높은 모자를 씌운 다음, 무대를 사람 북적이는 도시에서 산속 길로 옮겨 봐. 그러면 그 안에서 시와 모험의 정수를 발견하게 될걸. 그 위대한 으뜸 미덕도 마찬가지야. 잘 길러서 제대로 베풀면, 다른 모든 미덕으로 이어지거나, 적어도 그 미덕들을 다 품게 되거든. 거기에도 낭만이 있어야 해. 그리고 그 낭만 속에 실제의, 고되고, 발버둥치는 하루하루의 삶이 적게 담길수록 더 좋은 거지."]], [["The life to which poor Kate Nickleby was devoted, in consequence of the unforeseen train of circumstances already developed in this narrative, was a hard one; but lest the very dulness, unhealthy confinement, and bodily fatigue, which made up its sum and substance, should deprive it of any interest with the mass of the charitable and sympathetic, I would rather keep Miss Nickleby herself in view just now, than chill them in the outset, by a minute and lengthened description of the establishment presided over by Madame Mantalini.", "가엾은 케이트 니클비가 떠밀려 들어간 삶은, 이 이야기에서 이미 펼쳐 보인 뜻밖의 사정들 때문에, 참 고된 삶이었어. 그런데 그 삶을 이루는 알맹이라곤 따분함과, 답답하고 몸에 해로운 갇힘과, 몸이 녹초가 되는 피로뿐이었지. 자칫하면 그 때문에 자선심 많고 공감 잘하는 사람들이 이 이야기에 흥미를 잃을지도 몰라. 그러니 나는 차라리 지금은 니클비 양 그 자신한테 눈길을 두려고 해. 마담 만탈리니가 다스리는 그 가게를 시시콜콜 길게 늘어놓아서 처음부터 독자들 마음을 식혀 버리는 것보다는 말이야."]], [["‘Well, now, indeed, Madame Mantalini,’ said Miss Knag, as Kate was taking her weary way homewards on the first night of her novitiate; ‘that Miss Nickleby is a very creditable young person--a very creditable young person indeed--hem--upon my word, Madame Mantalini, it does very extraordinary credit even to your discrimination that you should have found such a very excellent, very well-behaved, very--hem--very unassuming young woman to assist in the fitting on. I have seen some young women when they had the opportunity of displaying before their betters, behave in such a--oh, dear--well--but you’re always right, Madame Mantalini, always; and as I very often tell the young ladies, how you do contrive to be always right, when so many people are so often wrong, is to me a mystery indeed.’", "\"자, 정말이지, 마담 만탈리니,\" 케이트가 견습 첫날 밤 지친 걸음으로 집을 향해 가고 있을 때 내그 양이 말했어. \"저 니클비 양은 아주 믿음직한 아가씨예요. 정말이지 아주 믿음직해요. 흠. 정말이지, 마담 만탈리니, 저렇게 훌륭하고, 행실 바르고, 아주, 흠, 아주 겸손한 아가씨를 옷 맞추는 일에 보탬으로 뽑으셨다니, 마담의 안목에 정말 대단히 감탄할 일이에요. 저는 어떤 아가씨들은 윗사람들 앞에서 자기를 뽐낼 기회만 생기면 어찌나 굴던지, 아이고 참, 글쎄. 하지만 마담 만탈리니는 늘 옳으시죠, 늘요. 제가 아가씨들한테 자주 하는 말인데, 그 많은 사람들이 그렇게 자주 틀리는데 마담께서는 어떻게 늘 옳으실 수가 있는지, 저한테는 정말 수수께끼라니까요.\""]], [["‘Beyond putting a very excellent client out of humour, Miss Nickleby has not done anything very remarkable today--that I am aware of, at least,’ said Madame Mantalini in reply.", "\"아주 훌륭한 손님 한 분 비위를 거스른 것 말고는, 니클비 양이 오늘 그렇게 대단한 일을 한 건 없는데. 적어도 내가 아는 한은 말이야.\" 마담 만탈리니가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Oh, dear!’ said Miss Knag; ‘but you must allow a great deal for inexperience, you know.’", "\"아이고 참!\" 내그 양이 말했어. \"하지만 경험이 없으니 많이 봐주셔야죠, 아시잖아요.\""]], [["‘And youth?’ inquired Madame.", "\"그럼 어리니까도?\" 마담이 물었어."]], [["‘Oh, I say nothing about that, Madame Mantalini,’ replied Miss Knag, reddening; ‘because if youth were any excuse, you wouldn’t have--’", "\"아, 그 점에 대해선 아무 말 안 할게요, 마담 만탈리니.\" 내그 양이 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했어. \"왜냐하면 어린 게 변명이 된다면, 마담께서도——\""]], [["‘Quite so good a forewoman as I have, I suppose,’ suggested Madame.", "\"나도 지금 같은 훌륭한 작업반장은 못 두었겠지, 그 말이겠지.\" 마담이 거들었어."]], [["‘Well, I never did know anybody like you, Madame Mantalini,’ rejoined Miss Knag most complacently, ‘and that’s the fact, for you know what one’s going to say, before it has time to rise to one’s lips.", "\"글쎄, 마담 만탈리니 같은 분은 제가 처음 봐요.\" 내그 양이 더없이 흐뭇한 얼굴로 받았어."], ["Oh, very good!", "\"정말이라니까요. 무슨 말을 하려고 하면 그 말이 입에 채 오르기도 전에 다 아시잖아요."], ["Ha, ha, ha!’", "아, 정말 대단해요! 하하하!\""]], [["‘For myself,’ observed Madame Mantalini, glancing with affected carelessness at her assistant, and laughing heartily in her sleeve, ‘I consider Miss Nickleby the most awkward girl I ever saw in my life.’", "\"내 생각엔 말이야,\" 마담 만탈리니가 짐짓 무심한 척 자기 조수를 흘끗 보며, 속으로는 실컷 웃으면서 말했어. \"니클비 양은 내 평생 본 아가씨 중에 제일 굼떠.\""]], [["‘Poor dear thing,’ said Miss Knag, ‘it’s not her fault. If it was, we might hope to cure it; but as it’s her misfortune, Madame Mantalini, why really you know, as the man said about the blind horse, we ought to respect it.’", "\"가엾은 것,\" 내그 양이 말했어. \"그게 그 애 잘못은 아니죠. 잘못이라면 고쳐 볼 희망이라도 있겠지만, 그건 그 애 불운이니까요. 그러니, 마담 만탈리니, 정말이지 아시잖아요. 눈먼 말을 두고 누가 그랬듯이, 우리가 그걸 존중해 줘야죠.\""]], [["‘Her uncle told me she had been considered pretty,’ remarked Madame Mantalini.", "\"그 애 삼촌은 그 애가 예쁘다는 소릴 들었다고 하던데.\" 마담 만탈리니가 한마디 했어."], ["‘I think her one of the most ordinary girls I ever met with.’", "\"내 보기엔 내가 만난 아가씨 중에 제일 평범한 축이야.\""]], [["‘Ordinary!’ cried Miss Knag with a countenance beaming delight; ‘and awkward! Well, all I can say is, Madame Mantalini, that I quite love the poor girl; and that if she was twice as indifferent-looking, and twice as awkward as she is, I should be only so much the more her friend, and that’s the truth of it.’", "\"평범하다고요!\" 내그 양이 기쁨에 환해진 얼굴로 외쳤어. \"게다가 굼뜨고요! 글쎄, 제가 할 수 있는 말은 이것뿐이에요, 마담 만탈리니. 저는 그 가엾은 애가 정말 좋아요. 그리고 그 애가 지금보다 두 배로 못나 보이고 두 배로 굼떠도, 저는 그만큼 더 그 애 편이 돼 줄 거예요. 그게 진심이에요.\""]], [["In fact, Miss Knag had conceived an incipient affection for Kate Nickleby, after witnessing her failure that morning, and this short conversation with her superior increased the favourable prepossession to a most surprising extent; which was the more remarkable, as when she first scanned that young lady’s face and figure, she had entertained certain inward misgivings that they would never agree.", "사실 내그 양은 그날 아침 케이트가 실수하는 걸 본 뒤로 슬그머니 정이 싹트기 시작했어. 그리고 윗사람과 나눈 이 짧은 대화가 그 호감을 놀랄 만큼 키워 놓았지. 더 놀라운 건, 처음 그 아가씨의 얼굴과 몸매를 훑어봤을 때만 해도, 내그 양은 둘이 절대 잘 지내지 못할 거라는 속마음을 품고 있었다는 점이야."]], [["‘But now,’ said Miss Knag, glancing at the reflection of herself in a mirror at no great distance, ‘I love her--I quite love her--I declare I do!’", "\"그래도 이제는,\" 내그 양이 멀지 않은 거울에 비친 자기 모습을 흘끗 보며 말했어. \"그 애가 좋아. 정말 좋다니까. 정말이야!\""]], [["Of such a highly disinterested quality was this devoted friendship, and so superior was it to the little weaknesses of flattery or ill-nature, that the kind-hearted Miss Knag candidly informed Kate Nickleby, next day, that she saw she would never do for the business, but that she need not give herself the slightest uneasiness on this account, for that she (Miss Knag), by increased exertions on her own part, would keep her as much as possible in the background, and that all she would have to do, would be to remain perfectly quiet before company, and to shrink from attracting notice by every means in her power. This last suggestion was so much in accordance with the timid girl’s own feelings and wishes, that she readily promised implicit reliance on the excellent spinster’s advice: without questioning, or indeed bestowing a moment’s reflection upon, the motives that dictated it.", "이 한결같은 우정은 어찌나 이해타산이라곤 없는지, 아첨이나 심술 같은 자잘한 약점보다 어찌나 한 수 위인지, 마음씨 고운 내그 양은 다음 날 케이트 니클비한테 솔직하게 일러 줬어. 보아하니 이 일에는 영 안 맞는 것 같다고 말이야. 하지만 그것 때문에 조금도 마음 쓸 것 없다고 했어. 자기(내그 양)가 더 애를 써서 그 애를 되도록 뒤에 숨겨 줄 테니까. 그러니 그 애가 할 일은 그저, 손님들 앞에서 얌전히 가만있고, 온갖 수를 다 써서 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 움츠리고 있는 것뿐이라고 했지. 이 마지막 충고는 겁 많은 케이트의 마음과 바람에 어찌나 딱 들어맞던지, 케이트는 그 훌륭한 노처녀의 충고를 무조건 따르겠다고 선뜻 약속했어. 그 충고가 어떤 속셈에서 나온 건지 따져 보지도, 한순간 곱씹어 보지도 않고 말이야."]], [["‘I take quite a lively interest in you, my dear soul, upon my word,’ said Miss Knag; ‘a sister’s interest, actually.", "\"내가 정말이지 너한테 활짝 정이 들었단다, 얘야.\" 내그 양이 말했어."], ["It’s the most singular circumstance I ever knew.’", "\"정말 친언니 같은 마음이야. 내 평생 이렇게 별난 일은 처음이라니까.\""]], [["Undoubtedly it was singular, that if Miss Knag did feel a strong interest in Kate Nickleby, it should not rather have been the interest of a maiden aunt or grandmother; that being the conclusion to which the difference in their respective ages would have naturally tended. But Miss Knag wore clothes of a very youthful pattern, and perhaps her feelings took the same shape.", "확실히 별난 일이긴 했어. 내그 양이 정말로 케이트 니클비한테 강한 정을 느꼈다면, 그건 차라리 노처녀 이모나 할머니의 마음이어야 어울렸을 테니까. 두 사람의 나이 차이로 보면 자연히 그런 결론이 나올 법했거든. 하지만 내그 양은 아주 젊어 보이는 옷을 입고 다녔어. 그러니 어쩌면 그 마음도 같은 모양을 띠었는지도 모르지."]], [["‘Bless you!’ said Miss Knag, bestowing a kiss upon Kate at the conclusion of the second day’s work, ‘how very awkward you have been all day.’", "\"아이고!\" 내그 양이 이튿날 일이 끝날 무렵 케이트한테 입을 맞추며 말했어. \"넌 오늘 하루 종일 어쩜 그렇게 굼떴니.\""]], [["‘I fear your kind and open communication, which has rendered me more painfully conscious of my own defects, has not improved me,’ sighed Kate.", "\"제 단점을 더 뼈아프게 깨닫게 해 주신 그 친절하고 솔직한 말씀이, 저를 나아지게 하지는 못한 것 같아 걱정이에요.\" 케이트가 한숨을 쉬었어."]], [["‘No, no, I dare say not,’ rejoined Miss Knag, in a most uncommon flow of good humour.", "\"아니, 아니, 그렇진 않을 거야.\" 내그 양이 보기 드물게 기분이 한껏 좋아져서 받았어."], ["‘But how much better that you should know it at first, and so be able to go on, straight and comfortable!", "\"하지만 처음부터 알아 두는 게 얼마나 나으냐. 그래야 마음 편히 똑바로 나아갈 수 있잖니!"], ["Which way are you walking, my love?’", "넌 어느 쪽으로 가니, 얘야?\""]], [["‘Towards the city,’ replied Kate.", "\"시내 쪽이에요.\" 케이트가 대답했어."]], [["‘The city!’ cried Miss Knag, regarding herself with great favour in the glass as she tied her bonnet.", "\"시내!\" 내그 양이 모자 끈을 매며 거울 속 자기 모습을 흐뭇하게 바라보면서 외쳤어."], ["‘Goodness gracious me!", "\"어머나 세상에!"], ["now do you really live in the city?’", "너 정말로 시내에 사니?\""]], [["‘Is it so very unusual for anybody to live there?’ asked Kate, half smiling.", "\"누가 거기 산다는 게 그렇게 이상한 일인가요?\" 케이트가 반쯤 미소 지으며 물었어."]], [["‘I couldn’t have believed it possible that any young woman could have lived there, under any circumstances whatever, for three days together,’ replied Miss Knag.", "\"어떤 처지에서든, 젊은 아가씨가 거기서 사흘을 내리 살 수 있다는 게 나는 믿기지가 않는걸.\" 내그 양이 대답했어."]], [["‘Reduced--I should say poor people,’ answered Kate, correcting herself hastily, for she was afraid of appearing proud, ‘must live where they can.’", "\"형편이 어려운——그러니까 가난한 사람들은,\" 케이트가 얼른 말을 고쳤어. 잘난 척하는 것처럼 보일까 봐 겁이 났거든. \"살 수 있는 데서 살아야죠.\""]], [["‘Ah! very true, so they must; very proper indeed!’ rejoined Miss Knag with that sort of half-sigh, which, accompanied by two or three slight nods of the head, is pity’s small change in general society; ‘and that’s what I very often tell my brother, when our servants go away ill, one after another, and he thinks the back-kitchen’s rather too damp for ‘em to sleep in. These sort of people, I tell him, are glad to sleep anywhere! Heaven suits the back to the burden. What a nice thing it is to think that it should be so, isn’t it?’", "\"아! 맞는 말이야, 그래야지. 정말 옳은 말이고말고!\" 내그 양이 그 반쯤 한숨 같은 소리를 내며 받았어. 고개를 두어 번 까딱이는 그 한숨이란 게, 점잖은 자리에서 동정심이 내미는 잔돈 같은 거였지. \"그게 바로 내가 우리 오빠한테 자주 하는 말이야. 우리 집 하인들이 줄줄이 병들어 나갈 때 말이지. 오빠는 뒤채 부엌이 걔들 자기엔 너무 축축하지 않나 하거든. 그럼 내가 그러지. 이런 부류 사람들은 어디서든 자게만 해 줘도 고마워한다고! 하늘이 짐에 맞게 등을 만들어 주시는 거지. 그게 그렇다는 게 참 좋은 일 아니니, 안 그래?\""]], [["‘Very,’ replied Kate.", "\"정말 그래요.\" 케이트가 대답했어."]], [["‘I’ll walk with you part of the way, my dear,’ said Miss Knag, ‘for you must go very near our house; and as it’s quite dark, and our last servant went to the hospital a week ago, with St Anthony’s fire in her face, I shall be glad of your company.’", "\"가는 길에 좀 같이 걸어 줄게, 얘야.\" 내그 양이 말했어. \"넌 우리 집 아주 가까이로 지나가야 하니까. 게다가 깜깜하기도 하고, 우리 집 마지막 하인이 일주일 전에 얼굴에 단독이 나서 병원에 가 버렸거든. 네가 같이 가 주면 나도 좋지.\""]], [["Kate would willingly have excused herself from this flattering companionship; but Miss Knag having adjusted her bonnet to her entire satisfaction, took her arm with an air which plainly showed how much she felt the compliment she was conferring, and they were in the street before she could say another word.", "케이트는 이 영광스러운 동행을 기꺼이 사양하고 싶었어. 하지만 내그 양은 모자를 흡족하게 매만진 다음, 자기가 베푸는 호의를 얼마나 대단히 여기는지 빤히 드러나는 태도로 케이트의 팔짱을 꼈지. 그리고 케이트가 한마디 더 꺼내기도 전에 둘은 이미 거리로 나와 있었어."]], [["‘I fear,’ said Kate, hesitating, ‘that mama--my mother, I mean--is waiting for me.’", "\"걱정이에요.\" 케이트가 망설이며 말했어. \"엄마가——제 어머니 말이에요——저를 기다리고 계실까 봐요.\""]], [["‘You needn’t make the least apology, my dear,’ said Miss Knag, smiling sweetly as she spoke; ‘I dare say she is a very respectable old person, and I shall be quite--hem--quite pleased to know her.’", "\"조금도 미안해할 거 없단다, 얘야.\" 내그 양이 다정하게 미소 지으며 말했어. \"분명 아주 점잖은 노부인이시겠지. 나도 그분을 알게 되면 정말, 흠, 정말 기쁠 거야.\""]], [["As poor Mrs.", "가엾은 니클비 부인이 길모퉁이에서 발만이 아니라 온몸을 식히고 있었던 터라, 케이트로서는 어머니를 내그 양한테 소개하는 수밖에 없었어."], ["Nickleby was cooling--not her heels alone, but her limbs generally at the street corner, Kate had no alternative but to make her known to Miss Knag, who, doing the last new carriage customer at second-hand, acknowledged the introduction with condescending politeness.", "내그 양은 방금 본 새 마차 손님 흉내를 어설프게 내며, 거들먹거리는 정중함으로 인사를 받았지."], ["The three then walked away, arm in arm: with Miss Knag in the middle, in a special state of amiability.", "그러고 나서 셋은 팔짱을 끼고 걸어갔어. 내그 양이 가운데서, 유난히 상냥한 기분에 젖어서 말이야."]], [["‘I have taken such a fancy to your daughter, Mrs.", "\"제가 댁의 따님을 어찌나 마음에 들어 하는지, 니클비 부인, 상상도 못 하실 거예요.\""], ["Nickleby, you can’t think,’ said Miss Knag, after she had proceeded a little distance in dignified silence.", "위엄 있는 침묵 속에 한참 걸어가다가 내그 양이 입을 열었어."]], [["‘I am delighted to hear it,’ said Mrs.", "\"그 말씀 들으니 기쁘네요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby; ‘though it is nothing new to me, that even strangers should like Kate.’", "\"하지만 낯선 분들까지 케이트를 좋아하는 게 저한테 새삼스러운 일은 아니랍니다.\""]], [["‘Hem!’ cried Miss Knag.", "\"흠!\" 내그 양이 소리를 냈어."]], [["‘You will like her better when you know how good she is,’ said Mrs Nickleby.", "\"그 애가 얼마나 착한지 아시면 더 좋아하실 거예요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["‘It is a great blessing to me, in my misfortunes, to have a child, who knows neither pride nor vanity, and whose bringing-up might very well have excused a little of both at first.", "\"이런 불운 속에서, 교만도 허영도 모르는 아이를 둔 건 저한테 큰 복이지요. 그 애 자라온 형편으로 보면 처음엔 교만이나 허영이 조금 묻어도 흉이 아니었을 텐데 말이에요."], ["You don’t know what it is to lose a husband, Miss Knag.’", "남편을 잃는다는 게 어떤 건지 내그 양은 모르실 거예요.\""]], [["As Miss Knag had never yet known what it was to gain one, it followed, very nearly as a matter of course, that she didn’t know what it was to lose one; so she said, in some haste, ‘No, indeed I don’t,’ and said it with an air intending to signify that she should like to catch herself marrying anybody--no, no, she knew better than that.", "내그 양은 아직 남편을 얻는다는 게 어떤 건지도 모르는 처지였으니, 남편을 잃는다는 게 어떤 건지 모르는 것도 거의 당연한 노릇이었지. 그래서 그녀는 다소 황급히, \"그럼요, 정말 모르죠.\" 하고 대답했어. 그러면서 자기가 누구하고든 결혼하는 꼴을 누가 한번 보기나 하라지——아니, 아니, 나는 그렇게 어리석진 않아, 하는 뜻을 풍기는 태도를 지었어."]], [["‘Kate has improved even in this little time, I have no doubt,’ said Mrs Nickleby, glancing proudly at her daughter.", "\"이 짧은 동안에도 케이트는 분명 많이 늘었을 거예요.\" 니클비 부인이 딸을 자랑스럽게 흘끗 보며 말했어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["of course,’ said Miss Knag.", "그럼요.\" 내그 양이 말했어."]], [["‘And will improve still more,’ added Mrs.", "\"그리고 앞으로 더 늘 거고요.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 덧붙였어."]], [["‘That she will, I’ll be bound,’ replied Miss Knag, squeezing Kate’s arm in her own, to point the joke.", "\"정말 그럴 거예요, 장담해요.\" 내그 양이 농담조로 케이트의 팔을 제 팔로 꼭 끼며 대답했어."]], [["‘She always was clever,’ said poor Mrs.", "\"그 애는 늘 영리했어요.\""], ["Nickleby, brightening up, ‘always, from a baby.", "가엾은 니클비 부인이 환해져서 말했어. \"늘 그랬죠, 갓난아기 때부터요."], ["I recollect when she was only two years and a half old, that a gentleman who used to visit very much at our house--Mr Watkins, you know, Kate, my dear, that your poor papa went bail for, who afterwards ran away to the United States, and sent us a pair of snow shoes, with such an affectionate letter that it made your poor dear father cry for a week.", "기억나는데, 그 애가 겨우 두 살 반이었을 때, 우리 집에 자주 드나들던 신사분이 있었어요——왓킨스 씨 말이야, 케이트, 얘야, 네 가엾은 아빠가 보증을 서 줬던 그분. 나중에 미국으로 도망가서 우리한테 눈신발 한 켤레를 보내 줬잖니."], ["You remember the letter?", "어찌나 다정한 편지를 같이 보냈던지 네 가엾은 아빠가 일주일을 울었지."], ["In which he said that he was very sorry he couldn’t repay the fifty pounds just then, because his capital was all out at interest, and he was very busy making his fortune, but that he didn’t forget you were his god-daughter, and he should take it very unkind if we didn’t buy you a silver coral and put it down to his old account?", "그 편지 기억나니? 그때 당장 오십 파운드를 못 갚아서 정말 미안하다, 자기 돈을 죄다 이자 받게 굴려 놓은 데다 재산 불리느라 너무 바빠서 그렇다, 하지만 네가 자기 대녀라는 걸 잊지 않고 있으니, 우리가 너한테 은제 산호 노리개를 사 주고 그걸 자기 외상 장부에 달아 놓지 않으면 아주 섭섭하겠다, 그렇게 썼던 편지 말이야."], ["Dear me, yes, my dear, how stupid you are!", "어머나, 얘야, 너 어쩜 그렇게 둔하니!"], ["and spoke so affectionately of the old port wine that he used to drink a bottle and a half of every time he came.", "그리고 자기가 올 때마다 한 병 반씩 마시던 그 묵은 포트 와인 얘기를 어찌나 다정하게 했던지."], ["You must remember, Kate?’", "기억나야지, 케이트?\""]], [["‘Yes, yes, mama; what of him?’", "\"네, 네, 엄마. 그분이 어쨌다는 거예요?\""]], [["‘Why, that Mr. Watkins, my dear,’ said Mrs.", "\"아, 그러니까 그 왓킨스 씨가 말이다, 얘야.\""], ["Nickleby slowly, as if she were making a tremendous effort to recollect something of paramount importance; ‘that Mr.", "니클비 부인이, 무슨 엄청나게 중요한 걸 떠올리려고 안간힘을 쓰듯 느릿느릿 말했어."], ["Watkins--he wasn’t any relation, Miss Knag will understand, to the Watkins who kept the Old Boar in the village; by-the-bye, I don’t remember whether it was the Old Boar or the George the Third, but it was one of the two, I know, and it’s much the same--that Mr.", "\"그 왓킨스 씨가——내그 양도 아시겠지만, 그분은 마을에서 올드 보어를 하던 그 왓킨스하고는 아무 친척도 아니에요. 그런데 가만, 그게 올드 보어였는지 조지 더 서드였는지 기억이 안 나네."], ["Watkins said, when you were only two years and a half old, that you were one of the most astonishing children he ever saw.", "하지만 둘 중 하나였던 건 분명해요, 어차피 거기서 거기지만——그 왓킨스 씨가, 네가 겨우 두 살 반일 때, 너더러 자기가 본 아이들 중에 제일 놀라운 아이라고 했단다."], ["He did indeed, Miss Knag, and he wasn’t at all fond of children, and couldn’t have had the slightest motive for doing it.", "정말 그랬어요, 내그 양. 게다가 그분은 아이를 도통 좋아하지도 않았으니, 그런 말을 할 까닭이 전혀 없었거든요."], ["I know it was he who said so, because I recollect, as well as if it was only yesterday, his borrowing twenty pounds of her poor dear papa the very moment afterwards.’", "그 말을 한 게 그분인 건 분명해요. 바로 그다음 순간에 그분이 그 애 가엾은 아빠한테 이십 파운드를 빌려 간 게 어제 일처럼 또렷이 기억나니까요.\""]], [["Having quoted this extraordinary and most disinterested testimony to her daughter’s excellence, Mrs.", "자기 딸의 훌륭함을 두고 이런 놀랍고도 더없이 사심 없는 증언을 끌어다 댄 다음, 니클비 부인은 숨을 돌리려고 말을 멈췄어."], ["Nickleby stopped to breathe; and Miss Knag, finding that the discourse was turning upon family greatness, lost no time in striking in, with a small reminiscence on her own account.", "그러자 내그 양은 이야기가 집안의 대단함 쪽으로 흘러가는 걸 보고, 지체 없이 끼어들었지. 자기 나름의 자그마한 추억거리를 가지고 말이야."]], [["‘Don’t talk of lending money, Mrs.", "\"돈 빌려준 얘기는 꺼내지도 마세요, 니클비 부인.\" 내그 양이 말했어."], ["Nickleby,’ said Miss Knag, ‘or you’ll drive me crazy, perfectly crazy.", "\"안 그러면 제가 미쳐 버릴 거예요, 완전히 미쳐 버린다니까요. 우리 엄마가——흠——세상에 다시없을 사랑스럽고 아름다운 분이었거든요."], ["My mama--hem--was the most lovely and beautiful creature, with the most striking and exquisite--hem--the most exquisite nose that ever was put upon a human face, I do believe, Mrs Nickleby (here Miss Knag rubbed her own nose sympathetically); the most delightful and accomplished woman, perhaps, that ever was seen; but she had that one failing of lending money, and carried it to such an extent that she lent--hem--oh!", "그렇게 또렷하고 빼어난——흠——사람 얼굴에 붙은 것 중에 그렇게 빼어난 코는 다시없었을 거예요, 정말이에요, 니클비 부인 (여기서 내그 양은 동질감에 젖어 자기 코를 문질렀어). 아마 세상에 다시없을 더없이 사랑스럽고 교양 있는 분이었죠."], ["thousands of pounds, all our little fortunes, and what’s more, Mrs.", "그런데 그 돈 빌려주는 버릇 하나가 흠이었어요. 그걸 어찌나 심하게 했던지——흠——아이고!"], ["Nickleby, I don’t think, if we were to live till--till--hem--till the very end of time, that we should ever get them back again.", "수천 파운드를 빌려줬다니까요, 우리 자그마한 재산을 죄다요. 게다가 말이에요, 니클비 부인, 우리가 살고 또 살아서——흠——이 세상 끝까지 산다 해도 그 돈을 다시 받아 낼 것 같지가 않아요."], ["I don’t indeed.’", "정말이지 그래요.\""]], [["After concluding this effort of invention without being interrupted, Miss Knag fell into many more recollections, no less interesting than true, the full tide of which, Mrs. Nickleby in vain attempting to stem, at length sailed smoothly down by adding an under-current of her own recollections; and so both ladies went on talking together in perfect contentment; the only difference between them being, that whereas Miss Knag addressed herself to Kate, and talked very loud, Mrs. Nickleby kept on in one unbroken monotonous flow, perfectly satisfied to be talking and caring very little whether anybody listened or not.", "이렇게 지어낸 이야기를 한 번도 끊기지 않고 끝마친 다음, 내그 양은 더 많은 추억 속으로 빠져들었어. 하나같이 못지않게 흥미롭고, 또 못지않게 사실인 추억들이었지. 그 도도하게 밀려드는 물길을 니클비 부인은 막아 보려 했지만 헛수고였어. 결국 부인은 자기 나름의 추억이라는 밑물을 흘려보내며 매끄럽게 그 흐름을 따라 떠갔지. 그렇게 두 부인은 더없이 흡족하게 함께 이야기를 이어 갔어. 다만 한 가지 차이가 있다면, 내그 양은 케이트를 향해 아주 큰 소리로 떠들어 댔고, 니클비 부인은 한결같이 단조롭게 줄줄 이어 나갔다는 거야. 그저 떠드는 것만으로 흡족해서, 누가 듣든 말든 별로 개의치 않으면서 말이지."]], [["In this manner they walked on, very amicably, until they arrived at Miss Knag’s brother’s, who was an ornamental stationer and small circulating library keeper, in a by-street off Tottenham Court Road; and who let out by the day, week, month, or year, the newest old novels, whereof the titles were displayed in pen-and-ink characters on a sheet of pasteboard, swinging at his door-post. As Miss Knag happened, at the moment, to be in the middle of an account of her twenty-second offer from a gentleman of large property, she insisted upon their all going in to supper together; and in they went.", "이런 식으로 두 사람은 아주 화기애애하게 걸어가서, 마침내 내그 양 오빠네 집에 다다랐어. 그는 토튼햄 코트 로드에서 갈라진 골목길에서 고급 문구점 겸 자그마한 대여 도서관을 하고 있었지. 가장 새로 나온 헌 소설들을 하루, 일주일, 한 달, 또는 일 년 단위로 빌려주었는데, 그 제목들은 펜과 잉크로 적혀 마분지 한 장에 나붙어 문기둥에 대롱대롱 매달려 있었어. 마침 그때 내그 양은 큰 재산가인 어떤 신사한테서 받은 스물두 번째 청혼 이야기를 한창 늘어놓던 참이라, 모두 함께 들어가서 저녁을 먹자고 우겼지. 그래서 다들 안으로 들어갔어."]], [["‘Don’t go away, Mortimer,’ said Miss Knag as they entered the shop.", "\"가지 마, 모티머.\" 그들이 가게로 들어서자 내그 양이 말했어."], ["‘It’s only one of our young ladies and her mother.", "\"우리 가게 아가씨 하나하고 그 어머니야."], ["Mrs. and Miss Nickleby.’", "니클비 부인하고 니클비 양.\""]], [["‘Oh, indeed!’ said Mr.", "\"오, 그래!\""], ["Mortimer Knag.", "모티머 내그 씨가 말했어."], ["‘Ah!’", "\"아!\""]], [["Having given utterance to these ejaculations with a very profound and thoughtful air, Mr.", "이 감탄사들을 더없이 깊고 사색에 잠긴 태도로 내뱉은 뒤, 내그 씨는 계산대 위 부엌 양초 두 개의 심지를 천천히 잘랐어."], ["Knag slowly snuffed two kitchen candles on the counter, and two more in the window, and then snuffed himself from a box in his waistcoat pocket.", "그리고 창가의 양초 두 개도 더 잘랐지. 그러고는 조끼 주머니에서 코담배 갑을 꺼내 자기 코에다 한 줌 들이켰어."]], [["There was something very impressive in the ghostly air with which all this was done; and as Mr.", "이 모든 걸 해내는 그 유령 같은 분위기에는 사람을 압도하는 무언가가 있었어. 게다가 내그 씨는 엄숙한 이목구비에 안경을 쓴, 키 크고 깡마른 신사였지."], ["Knag was a tall lank gentleman of solemn features, wearing spectacles, and garnished with much less hair than a gentleman bordering on forty, or thereabouts, usually boasts, Mrs Nickleby whispered her daughter that she thought he must be literary.", "마흔 줄에 접어든, 아니 그 언저리의 신사가 보통 자랑할 만한 것보다 머리숱이 훨씬 적은 사람이었어. 그래서 니클비 부인은 딸한테, 저분은 분명 문학을 하는 사람일 거라고 속삭였지."]], [["‘Past ten,’ said Mr.", "\"열 시가 넘었군.\""], ["Knag, consulting his watch.", "내그 씨가 회중시계를 들여다보며 말했어."], ["‘Thomas, close the warehouse.’", "\"토머스, 창고 문 닫아라.\""]], [["Thomas was a boy nearly half as tall as a shutter, and the warehouse was a shop about the size of three hackney coaches.", "토머스는 키가 덧문 절반쯤 되는 소년이었고, 창고라는 건 합승 마차 세 대만 한 크기의 가게였어."]], [["‘Ah!’ said Mr.", "\"아!\""], ["Knag once more, heaving a deep sigh as he restored to its parent shelf the book he had been reading.", "내그 씨가 읽던 책을 제자리 선반에 도로 꽂으며 다시 한 번 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었어."], ["‘Well--yes--I believe supper is ready, sister.’", "\"그럼——그래——저녁이 준비됐을 거다, 누이.\""]], [["With another sigh Mr.", "또 한 번 한숨을 쉬며 내그 씨는 계산대에서 부엌 양초들을 집어 들었어."], ["Knag took up the kitchen candles from the counter, and preceded the ladies with mournful steps to a back-parlour, where a charwoman, employed in the absence of the sick servant, and remunerated with certain eighteenpences to be deducted from her wages due, was putting the supper out.", "그리고 구슬픈 발걸음으로 부인들을 안내해 뒤채 거실로 들어갔지. 거기에는 병든 하인 대신 고용된 잡일꾼 아주머니가, 밀린 임금에서 일정한 십팔 펜스씩을 떼는 조건으로 삯을 받으며, 저녁상을 차리고 있었어."]], [["‘Mrs.", "\"블록슨 부인,\""], ["Blockson,’ said Miss Knag, reproachfully, ‘how very often I have begged you not to come into the room with your bonnet on!’", "내그 양이 나무라듯 말했어. \"내가 그 모자를 쓴 채로 방에 들어오지 말아 달라고 얼마나 자주 부탁했어요!\""]], [["‘I can’t help it, Miss Knag,’ said the charwoman, bridling up on the shortest notice. ‘There’s been a deal o’cleaning to do in this house, and if you don’t like it, I must trouble you to look out for somebody else, for it don’t hardly pay me, and that’s the truth, if I was to be hung this minute.’", "\"어쩔 수 없잖아요, 내그 양.\" 잡일꾼 아주머니가 대뜸 발끈해서 말했어. \"이 집엔 청소할 게 산더미라고요. 그게 마음에 안 드시면 다른 사람을 알아보시는 수밖에 없어요. 이래선 품삯도 안 나온다고요, 그게 사실이에요, 지금 당장 목이 매달린다 해도요.\""]], [["‘I don’t want any remarks if YOU please,’ said Miss Knag, with a strong emphasis on the personal pronoun.", "\"이러쿵저러쿵 말은 듣고 싶지 않아요, 부인은.\" 내그 양이 인칭대명사에 잔뜩 힘을 주며 말했어."], ["‘Is there any fire downstairs for some hot water presently?’", "\"이따 더운물 쓰게 아래층에 불은 좀 있어요?\""]], [["‘No there is not, indeed, Miss Knag,’ replied the substitute; ‘and so I won’t tell you no stories about it.’", "\"없어요, 정말로요, 내그 양.\" 대신 온 아주머니가 대답했어. \"그러니 거짓말은 안 할래요.\""]], [["‘Then why isn’t there?’ said Miss Knag.", "\"그럼 왜 없는 거예요?\" 내그 양이 말했어."]], [["‘Because there arn’t no coals left out, and if I could make coals I would, but as I can’t I won’t, and so I make bold to tell you, Mem,’ replied Mrs.", "\"내놓은 석탄이 없으니까요. 제가 석탄을 만들 수 있으면 만들겠지만, 못 만드니까 안 만드는 거예요."], ["Blockson.", "그래서 이렇게 대놓고 말씀드리는 거고요, 마님.\" 블록슨 부인이 대답했어."]], [["‘Will you hold your tongue--female?’ said Mr.", "\"입 좀 다물지 못하겠소——이 아낙네야?\""], ["Mortimer Knag, plunging violently into this dialogue.", "모티머 내그 씨가 거칠게 이 대화에 끼어들었어."]], [["‘By your leave, Mr.", "\"실례지만, 내그 씨.\""], ["Knag,’ retorted the charwoman, turning sharp round.", "잡일꾼 아주머니가 홱 돌아서며 쏘아붙였어. \"저야 이 집에서 말 안 하는 게 더없이 좋다고요."], ["‘I’m only too glad not to speak in this house, excepting when and where I’m spoke to, sir; and with regard to being a female, sir, I should wish to know what you considered yourself?’", "누가 묻는 데서 묻는 때 말고는요. 그리고 아낙네라고 하셨는데, 그럼 선생님은 자기를 뭐라고 여기시는지 알고 싶네요.\""]], [["‘A miserable wretch,’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"비참한 인간이지.\""], ["Knag, striking his forehead.", "내그 씨가 자기 이마를 치며 외쳤어."], ["‘A miserable wretch.’", "\"비참한 인간.\""]], [["‘I’m very glad to find that you don’t call yourself out of your name, sir,’ said Mrs.", "\"자기를 본명이 아닌 딴 이름으로 부르지 않으시니 다행이네요, 선생님.\""], ["Blockson; ‘and as I had two twin children the day before yesterday was only seven weeks, and my little Charley fell down a airy and put his elber out, last Monday, I shall take it as a favour if you’ll send nine shillings, for one week’s work, to my house, afore the clock strikes ten tomorrow.’", "블록슨 부인이 말했어. \"그리고 제가 그저께가 일곱 주째였던 날에 쌍둥이를 낳았고, 우리 막내 찰리가 지난 월요일에 환기 구덩이에 떨어져서 팔꿈치가 빠졌으니, 한 주 일한 삯 구 실링을 내일 시계가 열 시를 치기 전에 우리 집으로 보내 주시면 고맙겠습니다.\""]], [["With these parting words, the good woman quitted the room with great ease of manner, leaving the door wide open; Mr.", "이런 작별의 말을 남기고, 이 착한 부인은 아주 태연한 태도로 방을 나갔어. 문은 활짝 열어 둔 채로 말이지."], ["Knag, at the same moment, flung himself into the ‘warehouse,’ and groaned aloud.", "바로 그 순간 내그 씨는 '창고'로 몸을 내던지더니 큰 소리로 끙끙 앓았어."]], [["‘What is the matter with that gentleman, pray?’ inquired Mrs.", "\"저 신사분한테 무슨 일이 있는 거예요, 네?\""], ["Nickleby, greatly disturbed by the sound.", "니클비 부인이 그 소리에 몹시 마음이 어지러워져 물었어."]], [["‘Is he ill?’ inquired Kate, really alarmed.", "\"아프신 거예요?\" 케이트가 정말로 놀라서 물었어."]], [["‘Hush!’ replied Miss Knag; ‘a most melancholy history.", "\"쉿!\" 내그 양이 대답했어."], ["He was once most devotedly attached to--hem--to Madame Mantalini.’", "\"참 서글픈 사연이지. 저 사람이 한때 어떤 분한테 끔찍이 빠져 있었거든——흠——마담 만탈리니한테.\""]], [["‘Bless me!’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"어머나!\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 외쳤어."]], [["‘Yes,’ continued Miss Knag, ‘and received great encouragement too, and confidently hoped to marry her.", "\"맞아요,\" 내그 양이 말을 이었어."], ["He has a most romantic heart, Mrs.", "\"게다가 상당한 부추김도 받아서, 그분과 결혼할 거라고 철석같이 바랐죠. 저 사람은 더없이 낭만적인 마음을 지녔거든요, 니클비 부인."], ["Nickleby, as indeed--hem--as indeed all our family have, and the disappointment was a dreadful blow.", "사실——흠——사실 우리 집안사람들이 다 그래요. 그러니 그 실망이 끔찍한 타격이었죠."], ["He is a wonderfully accomplished man--most extraordinarily accomplished--reads--hem--reads every novel that comes out; I mean every novel that--hem--that has any fashion in it, of course.", "저 사람은 굉장히 교양 있는 사람이에요——유달리 교양이 깊어서——읽거든요——흠——나오는 소설이란 소설은 다 읽어요. 그러니까 무슨——흠——물론 좀 유행을 타는 소설은 다 말이에요."], ["The fact is, that he did find so much in the books he read, applicable to his own misfortunes, and did find himself in every respect so much like the heroes--because of course he is conscious of his own superiority, as we all are, and very naturally--that he took to scorning everything, and became a genius; and I am quite sure that he is, at this very present moment, writing another book.’", "사실은 이래요, 저 사람이 읽는 책 속에서 제 불운에 딱 들어맞는 대목을 어찌나 많이 찾았던지, 그리고 모든 면에서 자기가 그 주인공들하고 어찌나 닮았다고 느꼈던지——물론 자기가 남보다 잘났다는 걸 스스로 알고 있죠, 우리 다 그렇듯이, 그건 아주 당연한 일이고요——그러다 보니 만사를 다 깔보게 됐고, 천재가 돼 버린 거예요. 그리고 저는 저 사람이 바로 이 순간에도 또 다른 책을 쓰고 있다고 확신해요.\""]], [["‘Another book!’ repeated Kate, finding that a pause was left for somebody to say something.", "\"또 다른 책을요!\" 케이트가, 누군가 한마디 하라고 잠깐 틈이 비었다는 걸 알아채고 따라 말했어."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Miss Knag, nodding in great triumph; ‘another book, in three volumes post octavo. Of course it’s a great advantage to him, in all his little fashionable descriptions, to have the benefit of my--hem--of my experience, because, of course, few authors who write about such things can have such opportunities of knowing them as I have. He’s so wrapped up in high life, that the least allusion to business or worldly matters--like that woman just now, for instance--quite distracts him; but, as I often say, I think his disappointment a great thing for him, because if he hadn’t been disappointed he couldn’t have written about blighted hopes and all that; and the fact is, if it hadn’t happened as it has, I don’t believe his genius would ever have come out at all.’", "\"맞아요,\" 내그 양이 의기양양하게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했어. \"또 다른 책, 팔절판 세 권짜리예요. 물론 저 사람이 그 자잘한 유행 묘사를 다 할 때 제——흠——제 경험의 덕을 보니 큰 보탬이 되죠. 왜냐하면 물론, 그런 걸 다루는 작가 가운데 그런 걸 저만큼 알 기회를 가진 사람은 거의 없으니까요. 저 사람은 상류 생활에 푹 빠져 있어서, 장사나 세속적인 일을 조금만 들먹여도——이를테면 방금 그 여자처럼요——아주 정신이 산란해져요. 하지만 제가 자주 하는 말인데, 저는 그 실망이 저 사람한테 큰 약이 됐다고 봐요. 실망을 안 했더라면 시든 희망이니 뭐니 하는 걸 쓰지 못했을 테니까요. 사실은요, 일이 이렇게 되지 않았더라면, 저 사람 천재성은 영영 빛을 못 봤을 거예요.\""]], [["How much more communicative Miss Knag might have become under more favourable circumstances, it is impossible to divine, but as the gloomy one was within ear-shot, and the fire wanted making up, her disclosures stopped here.", "좀 더 좋은 형편이었다면 내그 양이 얼마나 더 입을 놀렸을지는 헤아릴 길이 없어. 하지만 그 우울한 양반이 말소리가 들리는 거리에 있었고, 불도 지펴야 했으니, 그녀의 폭로는 거기서 멈췄지."], ["To judge from all appearances, and the difficulty of making the water warm, the last servant could not have been much accustomed to any other fire than St Anthony’s; but a little brandy and water was made at last, and the guests, having been previously regaled with cold leg of mutton and bread and cheese, soon afterwards took leave; Kate amusing herself, all the way home, with the recollection of her last glimpse of Mr.", "모든 정황으로 보아, 또 물을 데우기가 그렇게 어려웠던 걸로 보아, 그 마지막 하인은 단독 말고 다른 불이라곤 별로 다뤄 본 적이 없는 것 같았어. 그래도 마침내 브랜디에 물을 탄 게 조금 마련됐어."], ["Mortimer Knag deeply abstracted in the shop; and Mrs.", "손님들은 그에 앞서 차가운 양 다리 고기와 빵과 치즈를 대접받은 터라, 곧이어 작별 인사를 했지."], ["Nickleby by debating within herself whether the dressmaking firm would ultimately become ‘Mantalini, Knag, and Nickleby’, or ‘Mantalini, Nickleby, and Knag’.", "케이트는 집에 가는 내내, 가게에서 깊은 생각에 빠져 있던 모티머 내그 씨의 마지막 모습을 떠올리며 즐거워했어. 그리고 니클비 부인은, 이 양장점 회사가 결국 '만탈리니, 내그, 그리고 니클비'가 될지, 아니면 '만탈리니, 니클비, 그리고 내그'가 될지 속으로 따져 보며 즐거워했지."]], [["At this high point, Miss Knag’s friendship remained for three whole days, much to the wonderment of Madame Mantalini’s young ladies who had never beheld such constancy in that quarter, before; but on the fourth, it received a check no less violent than sudden, which thus occurred.", "내그 양의 우정은 이렇게 한껏 무르익은 상태로 사흘 내내 이어졌어. 마담 만탈리니네 아가씨들은 그쪽에서 그런 한결같음을 일찍이 본 적이 없던 터라 무척 신기해했지. 그런데 나흘째 되는 날, 그 우정은 못지않게 격렬하고 못지않게 갑작스러운 제동이 걸렸어. 일은 이렇게 벌어졌지."]], [["It happened that an old lord of great family, who was going to marry a young lady of no family in particular, came with the young lady, and the young lady’s sister, to witness the ceremony of trying on two nuptial bonnets which had been ordered the day before, and Madame Mantalini announcing the fact, in a shrill treble, through the speaking-pipe, which communicated with the workroom, Miss Knag darted hastily upstairs with a bonnet in each hand, and presented herself in the show-room, in a charming state of palpitation, intended to demonstrate her enthusiasm in the cause. The bonnets were no sooner fairly on, than Miss Knag and Madame Mantalini fell into convulsions of admiration.", "대단한 가문의 늙은 귀족이 별로 내세울 가문도 없는 젊은 아가씨와 결혼하려는 참이었는데, 그가 그 젊은 아가씨와 그 아가씨의 언니를 데리고 찾아왔어. 전날 주문한 결혼식용 모자 두 개를 써 보는 광경을 보러 온 거지. 마담 만탈리니가 작업실로 통하는 전성관에 대고 날카로운 고음으로 그 사실을 알리자, 내그 양은 양손에 모자를 하나씩 들고 부랴부랴 위층으로 달려 올라갔어. 그러고는 그 일에 얼마나 열성인지를 보여 주려고 매혹적으로 가슴을 두근거리는 모습으로 진열실에 나타났지. 모자를 제대로 씌우자마자, 내그 양과 마담 만탈리니는 감탄의 발작에 빠졌어."]], [["‘A most elegant appearance,’ said Madame Mantalini.", "\"더없이 우아한 모습이에요.\" 마담 만탈리니가 말했어."]], [["‘I never saw anything so exquisite in all my life,’ said Miss Knag.", "\"제 평생 이렇게 빼어난 건 처음 봐요.\" 내그 양이 말했어."]], [["Now, the old lord, who was a VERY old lord, said nothing, but mumbled and chuckled in a state of great delight, no less with the nuptial bonnets and their wearers, than with his own address in getting such a fine woman for his wife; and the young lady, who was a very lively young lady, seeing the old lord in this rapturous condition, chased the old lord behind a cheval-glass, and then and there kissed him, while Madame Mantalini and the other young lady looked, discreetly, another way.", "자, 그 늙은 귀족은 아주아주 늙은 귀족이라, 아무 말도 없이 그저 우물우물 입을 놀리며 크게 흐뭇해서 낄낄거렸어. 그 결혼식용 모자와 그걸 쓴 사람들 못지않게, 이렇게 어여쁜 여자를 아내로 얻어 낸 자기 수완에도 흡족해하면서 말이지. 그리고 아주 발랄한 그 젊은 아가씨는, 늙은 귀족이 이렇게 황홀해하는 꼴을 보고는, 그를 큰 거울 뒤로 쫓아가더니 거기서 다짜고짜 입을 맞췄어. 그동안 마담 만탈리니와 다른 젊은 아가씨는 점잖게 딴 데로 눈길을 돌렸지."]], [["But, pending the salutation, Miss Knag, who was tinged with curiosity, stepped accidentally behind the glass, and encountered the lively young lady’s eye just at the very moment when she kissed the old lord; upon which the young lady, in a pouting manner, murmured something about ‘an old thing,’ and ‘great impertinence,’ and finished by darting a look of displeasure at Miss Knag, and smiling contemptuously.", "그런데 그 입맞춤이 진행되는 동안, 호기심에 살짝 물든 내그 양이 어쩌다 거울 뒤로 발을 들였다가, 그 발랄한 젊은 아가씨가 늙은 귀족한테 입 맞추는 바로 그 순간 그 아가씨의 눈과 딱 마주쳤어. 그러자 그 젊은 아가씨는 입을 삐죽이며 '늙은 것'이니 '대단한 무례'니 하고 뭐라고 중얼거렸어. 그러고는 마지막으로 내그 양한테 못마땅한 눈길을 쏘아붙이고는 경멸하듯 빙긋 웃었지."]], [["‘Madame Mantalini,’ said the young lady.", "\"마담 만탈리니.\" 그 젊은 아가씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Ma’am,’ said Madame Mantalini.", "\"네, 손님.\" 마담 만탈리니가 말했어."]], [["‘Pray have up that pretty young creature we saw yesterday.’", "\"어제 본 그 예쁜 아가씨 좀 올라오라고 해 주세요.\""]], [["‘Oh yes, do,’ said the sister.", "\"아, 그래요, 그렇게 해 줘요.\" 자매가 말했어."]], [["‘Of all things in the world, Madame Mantalini,’ said the lord’s intended, throwing herself languidly on a sofa, ‘I hate being waited upon by frights or elderly persons.", "\"세상 무엇보다도, 마담 만탈리니,\" 귀족의 신붓감이 소파에 나른하게 몸을 던지며 말했어."], ["Let me always see that young creature, I beg, whenever I come.’", "\"저는 흉한 사람이나 나이 든 사람한테 시중받는 게 질색이에요. 제가 올 때마다 늘 그 젊은 아가씨를 보게 해 주세요, 부탁이에요.\""]], [["‘By all means,’ said the old lord; ‘the lovely young creature, by all means.’", "\"물론이지,\" 늙은 귀족이 말했어. \"그 사랑스러운 젊은 아가씨를, 물론이고말고.\""]], [["‘Everybody is talking about her,’ said the young lady, in the same careless manner; ‘and my lord, being a great admirer of beauty, must positively see her.’", "\"다들 그 애 얘기를 하더라고요.\" 젊은 아가씨가 여전히 무심한 태도로 말했어. \"그리고 우리 귀족님은 미인을 대단히 좋아하시니, 꼭 그 애를 봐야 하시죠.\""]], [["‘She IS universally admired,’ replied Madame Mantalini.", "\"그 애는 누구한테나 칭송을 받지요.\" 마담 만탈리니가 대답했어."], ["‘Miss Knag, send up Miss Nickleby.", "\"내그 양, 니클비 양을 올려보내요."], ["You needn’t return.’", "당신은 다시 안 와도 돼요.\""]], [["‘I beg your pardon, Madame Mantalini, what did you say last?’ asked Miss Knag, trembling.", "\"실례지만, 마담 만탈리니, 마지막에 뭐라고 하셨죠?\" 내그 양이 부들부들 떨며 물었어."]], [["‘You needn’t return,’ repeated the superior, sharply. Miss Knag vanished without another word, and in all reasonable time was replaced by Kate, who took off the new bonnets and put on the old ones: blushing very much to find that the old lord and the two young ladies were staring her out of countenance all the time.", "\"다시 안 와도 된다고요.\" 윗사람이 날카롭게 되풀이했어. 내그 양은 더 말 한마디 없이 사라졌고, 알맞은 시간이 지나 그 자리에 케이트가 대신 들어섰어. 케이트는 새 모자들을 벗기고 헌 모자들을 도로 씌웠어. 늙은 귀족과 두 젊은 아가씨가 그동안 내내 자기를 빤히 쳐다보며 무안하게 만드는 걸 알고 얼굴이 새빨개졌지."]], [["‘Why, how you colour, child!’ said the lord’s chosen bride.", "\"어머, 얘 좀 봐, 얼굴이 어쩜 그렇게 붉어지니!\" 귀족이 점찍은 신부가 말했어."]], [["‘She is not quite so accustomed to her business, as she will be in a week or two,’ interposed Madame Mantalini with a gracious smile.", "\"이 애가 아직 일에 그리 익숙지 않아서요, 한두 주만 지나면 익숙해질 거예요.\" 마담 만탈리니가 우아하게 웃으며 끼어들었어."]], [["‘I am afraid you have been giving her some of your wicked looks, my lord,’ said the intended.", "\"우리 귀족님이 그 짓궂은 눈길을 좀 보내셨나 봐요.\" 신붓감이 말했어."]], [["‘No, no, no,’ replied the old lord, ‘no, no, I’m going to be married, and lead a new life.", "\"아니, 아니, 아니야.\" 늙은 귀족이 대답했어."], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "\"아니, 아니, 나는 결혼해서 새 삶을 살 거야."], ["a new life, a new life!", "하하하! 새 삶이라네, 새 삶!"], ["ha, ha, ha!’", "하하하!\""]], [["It was a satisfactory thing to hear that the old gentleman was going to lead a new life, for it was pretty evident that his old one would not last him much longer.", "이 늙은 신사가 새 삶을 살 거라는 말은 듣기에 흐뭇한 일이었어. 왜냐하면 그의 옛 삶이 그리 오래 가지 못할 게 빤히 보였으니까."], ["The mere exertion of protracted chuckling reduced him to a fearful ebb of coughing and gasping; it was some minutes before he could find breath to remark that the girl was too pretty for a milliner.", "그저 한참 낄낄거리는 데에 힘을 쓴 것만으로도 그는 무섭게 기침하고 헐떡이는 지경으로 떨어졌어. 그 아가씨가 양장점 점원치고는 너무 예쁘다고 한마디 할 숨을 고르기까지 몇 분이나 걸렸지."]], [["‘I hope you don’t think good looks a disqualification for the business, my lord,’ said Madame Mantalini, simpering.", "\"설마 예쁜 얼굴이 이 일에 흠이 된다고 여기시는 건 아니죠, 우리 귀족님?\" 마담 만탈리니가 간드러지게 웃으며 말했어."]], [["‘Not by any means,’ replied the old lord, ‘or you would have left it long ago.’", "\"천만에.\" 늙은 귀족이 대답했어. \"그랬다면 부인은 진작에 이 일을 그만뒀겠지.\""]], [["‘You naughty creature,’ said the lively lady, poking the peer with her parasol; ‘I won’t have you talk so.", "\"이 짓궂은 양반 같으니.\" 발랄한 아가씨가 파라솔로 그 귀족을 콕 찌르며 말했어."], ["How dare you?’", "\"그렇게 말하시면 안 돼요. 어쩜 그러세요?\""]], [["This playful inquiry was accompanied with another poke, and another, and then the old lord caught the parasol, and wouldn’t give it up again, which induced the other lady to come to the rescue, and some very pretty sportiveness ensued.", "이 장난스러운 물음에는 또 한 번, 또 한 번 콕콕 찌르는 짓이 따라붙었어. 그러다 늙은 귀족이 파라솔을 덥석 붙들고 도로 내주려 하지 않자, 다른 아가씨가 구하러 나섰지. 그래서 아주 귀여운 장난이 한바탕 벌어졌어."]], [["‘You will see that those little alterations are made, Madame Mantalini,’ said the lady.", "\"그 자잘한 손질들 꼭 해 주셔야 해요, 마담 만탈리니.\" 아가씨가 말했어."], ["‘Nay, you bad man, you positively shall go first; I wouldn’t leave you behind with that pretty girl, not for half a second.", "\"아니에요, 이 못된 양반, 당신이 꼭 먼저 가셔야 해요. 저 예쁜 아가씨하고 단 반 초도 당신을 두고 가지 않을 거예요."], ["I know you too well.", "제가 당신을 너무 잘 알거든요."], ["Jane, my dear, let him go first, and we shall be quite sure of him.’", "제인, 얘야, 저분을 먼저 보내자. 그래야 우리가 저분을 확실히 잡아 둘 수 있어.\""]], [["The old lord, evidently much flattered by this suspicion, bestowed a grotesque leer upon Kate as he passed; and, receiving another tap with the parasol for his wickedness, tottered downstairs to the door, where his sprightly body was hoisted into the carriage by two stout footmen.", "이 의심에 분명 무척 우쭐해진 늙은 귀족은, 지나가면서 케이트한테 괴상한 곁눈질을 던졌어. 그리고 그 짓궂음 때문에 파라솔로 또 한 대 톡 얻어맞고는, 비틀비틀 아래층 문간까지 내려갔지. 거기서 그 기운찬 몸뚱이는 건장한 하인 둘에게 들려 마차에 실렸어."]], [["‘Foh!’ said Madame Mantalini, ‘how he ever gets into a carriage without thinking of a hearse, I can’t think.", "\"흥!\" 마담 만탈리니가 말했어."], ["There, take the things away, my dear, take them away.’", "\"저 양반이 마차에 탈 때마다 어떻게 영구차 생각을 안 하는지 모르겠다니까. 자, 그것들 치워라, 얘야, 치워.\""]], [["Kate, who had remained during the whole scene with her eyes modestly fixed upon the ground, was only too happy to avail herself of the permission to retire, and hasten joyfully downstairs to Miss Knag’s dominion.", "이 모든 장면이 펼쳐지는 동안 내내 다소곳이 바닥에 눈길을 둔 채 서 있던 케이트는, 물러가도 좋다는 허락을 너무나 반갑게 받아들였어. 그리고 기뻐하며 부랴부랴 아래층 내그 양의 영토로 내려갔지."]], [["The circumstances of the little kingdom had greatly changed, however, during the short period of her absence.", "그런데 그녀가 잠깐 자리를 비운 짧은 사이에, 그 작은 왕국의 사정은 크게 바뀌어 있었어. 내그 양은 늘 앉던 자리에서 마담 만탈리니 대리인으로서의 온갖 위엄과 권위를 지키고 있는 게 아니었어."], ["In place of Miss Knag being stationed in her accustomed seat, preserving all the dignity and greatness of Madame Mantalini’s representative, that worthy soul was reposing on a large box, bathed in tears, while three or four of the young ladies in close attendance upon her, together with the presence of hartshorn, vinegar, and other restoratives, would have borne ample testimony, even without the derangement of the head-dress and front row of curls, to her having fainted desperately.", "그 훌륭한 양반은 큰 상자 위에 늘어져서 눈물범벅이 되어 있었지. 그동안 아가씨 서너 명이 그녀 곁에 바짝 붙어 시중을 들고 있었고, 거기에 사슴뿔 정기며 식초며 다른 정신 차리게 하는 약들까지 곁들여진 걸 보면——머리 장식이 흐트러지고 앞줄 곱슬머리가 헝클어진 것까지 보지 않더라도——그녀가 정신을 잃고 까무러쳤다는 게 넉넉히 짐작이 갔어."]], [["‘Bless me!’ said Kate, stepping hastily forward, ‘what is the matter?’", "\"어머나!\" 케이트가 황급히 앞으로 나서며 말했어. \"무슨 일이에요?\""]], [["This inquiry produced in Miss Knag violent symptoms of a relapse; and several young ladies, darting angry looks at Kate, applied more vinegar and hartshorn, and said it was ‘a shame.’", "이 물음은 내그 양한테 도로 까무러칠 듯한 격렬한 기미를 일으켰어. 그리고 아가씨 몇몇은 케이트한테 성난 눈길을 쏘아붙이며 식초와 사슴뿔 정기를 더 발라 주고는, '망신스러운 일'이라고 말했지."]], [["‘What is a shame?’ demanded Kate.", "\"뭐가 망신스러운 일이라는 거예요?\""], ["‘What is the matter?", "케이트가 따져 물었어. \"무슨 일이에요?"], ["What has happened?", "무슨 일이 있었던 거예요?"], ["tell me.’", "말해 주세요.\""]], [["‘Matter!’ cried Miss Knag, coming, all at once, bolt upright, to the great consternation of the assembled maidens; ‘matter!", "\"무슨 일이냐고!\" 내그 양이 모여 있던 아가씨들이 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 별안간 벌떡 꼿꼿이 일어나며 외쳤어."], ["Fie upon you, you nasty creature!’", "\"무슨 일이냐고! 어디 감히, 이 고약한 것아!\""]], [["‘Gracious!’ cried Kate, almost paralysed by the violence with which the adjective had been jerked out from between Miss Knag’s closed teeth; ‘have I offended you?’", "\"어머나!\" 케이트가, 내그 양이 앙다문 이 사이로 그 형용사를 홱 내뱉는 그 사나움에 거의 얼어붙어 외쳤어. \"제가 뭘 기분 상하게 해 드렸나요?\""]], [["‘YOU offended me!’ retorted Miss Knag, ‘YOU!", "\"네가 나를 기분 상하게 했냐고!\" 내그 양이 되받아쳤어."], ["a chit, a child, an upstart nobody!", "\"네가! 풋내기, 어린것, 굴러들어 온 보잘것없는 것이!"], ["Oh, indeed!", "아, 그래!"], ["Ha, ha!’", "하, 하!\""]], [["Now, it was evident, as Miss Knag laughed, that something struck her as being exceedingly funny; and as the young ladies took their tone from Miss Knag--she being the chief--they all got up a laugh without a moment’s delay, and nodded their heads a little, and smiled sarcastically to each other, as much as to say how very good that was!", "자, 내그 양이 웃는 걸 보니, 뭔가가 그녀한테 몹시 우습게 여겨지는 게 분명했어. 그리고 아가씨들은 내그 양한테 장단을 맞추던 터라——그녀가 우두머리였으니까——단 한순간도 지체 없이 다 같이 웃음을 터뜨렸지. 그리고 고개를 살짝 끄덕이며 서로 비웃듯 미소 지었어. 그거 참 멋지다는 듯이 말이야!"]], [["‘Here she is,’ continued Miss Knag, getting off the box, and introducing Kate with much ceremony and many low curtseys to the delighted throng; ‘here she is--everybody is talking about her--the belle, ladies--the beauty, the--oh, you bold-faced thing!’", "\"여기 이 애예요.\" 내그 양이 상자에서 내려와, 들떠 모여든 무리한테 케이트를 거창하게, 또 몇 번이고 깊이 절을 해 가며 소개했어. \"여기 이 애가——다들 이 애 얘기를 하더라죠——미녀랍니다, 여러분——미인이요, 이——아이고, 이 뻔뻔한 것아!\""]], [["At this crisis, Miss Knag was unable to repress a virtuous shudder, which immediately communicated itself to all the young ladies; after which, Miss Knag laughed, and after that, cried.", "이 고비에 내그 양은 의로운 몸서리를 도저히 억누르지 못했어. 그 몸서리는 곧장 아가씨들 모두한테로 옮겨 갔지. 그러고 나서 내그 양은 웃었고, 그다음엔 울었어."]], [["‘For fifteen years,’ exclaimed Miss Knag, sobbing in a most affecting manner, ‘for fifteen years have I been the credit and ornament of this room and the one upstairs. Thank God,’ said Miss Knag, stamping first her right foot and then her left with remarkable energy, ‘I have never in all that time, till now, been exposed to the arts, the vile arts, of a creature, who disgraces us with all her proceedings, and makes proper people blush for themselves. But I feel it, I do feel it, although I am disgusted.’", "\"십오 년 동안,\" 내그 양이 더없이 가슴 저미게 흐느끼며 외쳤어. \"십오 년 동안 나는 이 방과 위층 방의 자랑이요 꽃이었어. 하느님 감사하게도,\" 내그 양이 오른발을 굴렀다가 다음엔 왼발을 놀라운 기세로 구르며 말했어. \"그 긴 세월 내내 이제껏 단 한 번도, 하는 짓마다 우리를 망신시키고 점잖은 사람들이 부끄러워 얼굴 붉히게 만드는 그런 것의 수작에, 그 비열한 수작에 당한 적이 없었지. 그런데 나는 느껴, 정말로 느껴진다고, 이렇게 진저리가 나는데도 말이야.\""]], [["Miss Knag here relapsed into softness, and the young ladies renewing their attentions, murmured that she ought to be superior to such things, and that for their part they despised them, and considered them beneath their notice; in witness whereof, they called out, more emphatically than before, that it was a shame, and that they felt so angry, they did, they hardly knew what to do with themselves.", "여기서 내그 양은 다시 누그러져 부드러워졌어. 그러자 아가씨들이 다시 시중을 들며, 그런 것쯤은 초연하셔야 한다고, 자기들 같으면 그런 걸 경멸하고 거들떠보지도 않을 거라고 소곤거렸지. 그 증거로, 아가씨들은 아까보다 더 힘주어 망신스러운 일이라고 외쳤어. 그리고 어찌나 화가 나는지 자기들도 어쩔 줄을 모르겠다고 했지."]], [["‘Have I lived to this day to be called a fright!’ cried Miss Knag, suddenly becoming convulsive, and making an effort to tear her front off.", "\"내가 흉한 것이라는 소리를 들으려고 이 나이까지 살았다니!\" 내그 양이 별안간 발작을 일으키며, 자기 앞머리를 쥐어뜯으려 애쓰며 외쳤어."]], [["‘Oh no, no,’ replied the chorus, ‘pray don’t say so; don’t now!’", "\"아니에요, 아니에요.\" 합창이 받았어. \"제발 그런 말씀 마세요, 어서요!\""]], [["‘Have I deserved to be called an elderly person?’ screamed Miss Knag, wrestling with the supernumeraries.", "\"내가 나이 든 사람이라는 소리를 들어 마땅하다는 거니?\" 내그 양이 곁꾼들과 몸싸움을 벌이며 비명을 질렀어."]], [["‘Don’t think of such things, dear,’ answered the chorus.", "\"그런 건 생각도 마세요, 언니.\" 합창이 대답했어."]], [["‘I hate her,’ cried Miss Knag; ‘I detest and hate her. Never let her speak to me again; never let anybody who is a friend of mine speak to her; a slut, a hussy, an impudent artful hussy!’ Having denounced the object of her wrath, in these terms, Miss Knag screamed once, hiccuped thrice, gurgled in her throat several times, slumbered, shivered, woke, came to, composed her head-dress, and declared herself quite well again.", "\"나는 그 애가 미워.\" 내그 양이 외쳤어. \"그 애가 진절머리 나게 밉다고. 다시는 그 애가 나한테 말 못 걸게 해. 내 친구라면 누구도 그 애한테 말 걸지 못하게 해. 칠칠치 못한 것, 화냥년, 뻔뻔하고 교활한 화냥년!\" 분노의 대상을 이런 말로 매도하고 나서, 내그 양은 한 번 비명을 지르고, 세 번 딸꾹질을 하고, 목구멍에서 여러 번 그르렁 소리를 내고, 잠깐 늘어졌다가, 부르르 떨고, 깨어나, 정신을 차리고, 머리 장식을 매만지고는, 이제 아주 멀쩡해졌다고 선언했어."]], [["Poor Kate had regarded these proceedings, at first, in perfect bewilderment.", "가엾은 케이트는 처음엔 이 소동을 어리둥절한 마음으로 그저 바라보기만 했어."], ["She had then turned red and pale by turns, and once or twice essayed to speak; but, as the true motives of this altered behaviour developed themselves, she retired a few paces, and looked calmly on without deigning a reply.", "그러다 얼굴이 붉어졌다 창백해졌다 하기를 거듭했고, 한두 번 말을 꺼내 보려 했지. 하지만 이 달라진 태도의 진짜 속내가 드러나자, 그녀는 몇 걸음 물러서서, 대꾸도 하지 않은 채 차분히 바라보았어."], ["Nevertheless, although she walked proudly to her seat, and turned her back upon the group of little satellites who clustered round their ruling planet in the remotest corner of the room, she gave way, in secret, to some such bitter tears as would have gladdened Miss Knag’s inmost soul, if she could have seen them fall.", "그래도 그녀는 당당하게 자기 자리로 걸어가, 방의 가장 외진 구석에서 자기들 주관 행성 둘레에 몰려든 그 작은 위성 무리한테 등을 돌렸지만, 남몰래 쓰디쓴 눈물을 흘리고 말았어. 만약 내그 양이 그 눈물이 흐르는 걸 볼 수 있었다면, 그 마음속 깊은 곳까지 기뻐했을 그런 눈물을 말이야."]], [["# CHAPTER 19", "# CHAPTER 19"]], [["Descriptive of a Dinner at Mr. Ralph Nickleby’s, and of the Manner in which the Company entertained themselves, before Dinner, at Dinner, and after Dinner.", "랄프 니클비 씨 댁의 만찬, 그리고 손님들이 만찬 전과 만찬 중, 만찬 후에 스스로를 즐겁게 만든 방식에 대하여."]], [["The bile and rancour of the worthy Miss Knag undergoing no diminution during the remainder of the week, but rather augmenting with every successive hour; and the honest ire of all the young ladies rising, or seeming to rise, in exact proportion to the good spinster’s indignation, and both waxing very hot every time Miss Nickleby was called upstairs; it will be readily imagined that that young lady’s daily life was none of the most cheerful or enviable kind. She hailed the arrival of Saturday night, as a prisoner would a few delicious hours’ respite from slow and wearing torture, and felt that the poor pittance for her first week’s labour would have been dearly and hardly earned, had its amount been trebled.", "훌륭한 미스 내그의 심술과 원한은 그 주가 다 가도록 조금도 수그러들지 않았어. 오히려 시간이 갈수록 점점 더 부풀어 올랐지. 그리고 모든 젊은 아가씨들의 정직한 분노도 미스 내그의 화에 정확히 비례해서 치솟거나, 치솟는 척했어. 미스 니클비가 위층으로 불려 올라갈 때마다 양쪽 다 부글부글 끓어올랐지. 그러니 케이트의 하루하루가 그다지 즐겁거나 부러울 만한 게 아니었으리라는 건 쉽게 짐작할 수 있을 거야. 케이트는 토요일 밤이 오는 걸 반겼어. 마치 죄수가 더디고 진을 빼는 고문에서 잠깐 풀려나 달콤한 몇 시간을 맞듯이 말이야. 그리고 첫 주 노동의 대가로 받은 그 쥐꼬리만 한 보수가, 설령 세 배였다 해도 참 비싸고 힘들게 번 돈이라는 생각이 들었지."]], [["When she joined her mother, as usual, at the street corner, she was not a little surprised to find her in conversation with Mr.", "평소처럼 길모퉁이에서 어머니를 만났을 때, 케이트는 어머니가 랄프 니클비 씨와 이야기를 나누고 있는 걸 보고 적잖이 놀랐어. 그런데 그 놀라움은 곧 두 배로 커졌지."], ["Ralph Nickleby; but her surprise was soon redoubled, no less by the matter of their conversation, than by the smoothed and altered manner of Mr. Nickleby himself.", "두 사람이 나누는 이야기 내용도 그렇고, 니클비 씨 자신의 부드러워지고 달라진 태도 때문이기도 했어."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아! 얘야!\""], ["my dear!’ said Ralph; ‘we were at that moment talking about you.’", "랄프가 말했어. \"지금 막 네 얘기를 하던 참이었단다.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ replied Kate, shrinking, though she scarce knew why, from her uncle’s cold glistening eye.", "\"그러셨어요!\" 케이트가 대답했어. 왜인지도 모른 채 삼촌의 차갑게 번득이는 눈빛 앞에서 움츠러들면서."]], [["‘That instant,’ said Ralph. ‘I was coming to call for you, making sure to catch you before you left; but your mother and I have been talking over family affairs, and the time has slipped away so rapidly--’", "\"바로 지금 말이다.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"너를 데리러 가던 길이었지. 네가 떠나기 전에 꼭 붙잡으려고 말이야. 그런데 네 어머니랑 집안일 이야기를 하다 보니 시간이 어찌나 빨리 흘렀는지—\""]], [["‘Well, now, hasn’t it?’ interposed Mrs.", "\"어머, 정말 그렇지 않니?\""], ["Nickleby, quite insensible to the sarcastic tone of Ralph’s last remark.", "니클비 부인이 끼어들었어. 랄프의 마지막 말에 담긴 빈정거리는 투는 전혀 알아채지 못한 채로 말이야."], ["‘Upon my word, I couldn’t have believed it possible, that such a--Kate, my dear, you’re to dine with your uncle at half-past six o’clock tomorrow.’", "\"정말이지, 이렇게나—케이트, 얘야, 너 내일 여섯 시 반에 삼촌 댁에서 저녁을 먹게 됐단다.\""]], [["Triumphing in having been the first to communicate this extraordinary intelligence, Mrs.", "이 놀라운 소식을 제일 먼저 전했다는 데 우쭐해진 니클비 부인은 고개를 끄덕이며 몇 번이고 빙긋빙긋 웃었어."], ["Nickleby nodded and smiled a great many times, to impress its full magnificence on Kate’s wondering mind, and then flew off, at an acute angle, to a committee of ways and means.", "그 굉장함을 어리둥절해하는 케이트의 마음에 한껏 새겨 주려는 거였지. 그러더니 곧장 예각으로 방향을 꺾어 살림살이 궁리로 넘어갔어."]], [["‘Let me see,’ said the good lady.", "\"가만 보자.\" 착한 부인이 말했어."], ["‘Your black silk frock will be quite dress enough, my dear, with that pretty little scarf, and a plain band in your hair, and a pair of black silk stock--Dear, dear,’ cried Mrs Nickleby, flying off at another angle, ‘if I had but those unfortunate amethysts of mine--you recollect them, Kate, my love--how they used to sparkle, you know--but your papa, your poor dear papa--ah!", "\"네 검정 비단 드레스면 차림으로 충분할 거야, 얘야. 그 예쁘장한 작은 스카프를 두르고, 머리는 수수한 띠로 묶고, 검정 비단 양말 한 켤레—아이고, 아이고,\""], ["there never was anything so cruelly sacrificed as those jewels were, never!’ Overpowered by this agonising thought, Mrs.", "니클비 부인이 또 다른 방향으로 꺾으며 외쳤어. \"그 안타까운 자수정만 있었더라면—기억나지, 케이트, 얘야—그게 얼마나 반짝였는지, 알잖니—그런데 네 아빠가, 가엾은 네 아빠가—아!"], ["Nickleby shook her head, in a melancholy manner, and applied her handkerchief to her eyes.", "그 보석들만큼 그렇게 모질게 희생당한 건 세상에 없었어, 정말!\" 이 가슴 아픈 생각에 짓눌려 니클비 부인은 서글프게 고개를 가로젓고는 손수건을 눈가에 가져다 댔어."]], [["I don’t want them, mama, indeed,’ said Kate.", "\"전 그거 필요 없어요, 엄마, 정말로요.\" 케이트가 말했어."], ["‘Forget that you ever had them.’", "\"그런 게 있었다는 것도 잊으세요.\""]], [["‘Lord, Kate, my dear,’ rejoined Mrs.", "\"세상에, 케이트, 얘야,\""], ["Nickleby, pettishly, ‘how like a child you talk!", "니클비 부인이 토라진 듯 받아쳤어. \"어쩜 그렇게 애처럼 말하니!"], ["Four-and-twenty silver tea-spoons, brother-in-law, two gravies, four salts, all the amethysts--necklace, brooch, and ear-rings--all made away with, at the same time, and I saying, almost on my bended knees, to that poor good soul, “Why don’t you do something, Nicholas?", "은 찻숟가락 스물네 개에, 시동생, 그레이비 그릇 둘, 소금 그릇 넷, 자수정은 다—목걸이며 브로치며 귀걸이까지—한꺼번에 다 날아가 버렸지. 그러는 내내 나는 거의 무릎까지 꿇다시피 하면서 그 가엾고 착한 양반한테 말했어."], ["Why don’t you make some arrangement?”", "'여보, 뭐라도 좀 해 봐요, 니콜라스? 무슨 방도를 좀 마련해 봐요?'"], ["I am sure that anybody who was about us at that time, will do me the justice to own, that if I said that once, I said it fifty times a day.", "그때 우리 곁에 있던 사람이라면 누구든 인정해 줄 거야. 내가 그 말을 한 번만 한 게 아니라 하루에 쉰 번은 했다는 걸."], ["Didn’t I, Kate, my dear?", "안 그러니, 케이트, 얘야?"], ["Did I ever lose an opportunity of impressing it on your poor papa?’", "내가 네 가엾은 아빠한테 그걸 일깨워 줄 기회를 한 번이라도 놓친 적이 있더냐?\""]], [["‘No, no, mama, never,’ replied Kate.", "\"아니요, 아니요, 엄마, 한 번도요.\""], ["And to do Mrs.", "케이트가 대답했어."], ["Nickleby justice, she never had lost--and to do married ladies as a body justice, they seldom do lose--any occasion of inculcating similar golden percepts, whose only blemish is, the slight degree of vagueness and uncertainty in which they are usually enveloped.", "그리고 니클비 부인을 공평하게 말하자면, 부인은 그런 황금 같은 교훈을 새겨 줄 기회를 한 번도 놓친 적이 없었어—그리고 결혼한 부인네들 전체를 공평하게 말하자면, 그들은 그런 기회를 좀처럼 놓치는 법이 없지. 그 교훈의 유일한 흠이라면, 으레 거기에 살짝 모호하고 불확실한 기운이 감돈다는 것뿐이었어."]], [["‘Ah!’ said Mrs.", "\"아!\""], ["Nickleby, with great fervour, ‘if my advice had been taken at the beginning--Well, I have always done MY duty, and that’s some comfort.’", "니클비 부인이 무척 열을 올리며 말했어. \"처음에 내 충고만 들었더라면—그래도, 나는 늘 내 도리는 다했으니까, 그게 그나마 위안이지.\""]], [["When she had arrived at this reflection, Mrs.", "이 생각에 다다르자 니클비 부인은 한숨을 쉬고 두 손을 비비고 눈을 치켜뜨더니, 마침내 온순하고 차분한 표정을 지었어."], ["Nickleby sighed, rubbed her hands, cast up her eyes, and finally assumed a look of meek composure; thus importing that she was a persecuted saint, but that she wouldn’t trouble her hearers by mentioning a circumstance which must be so obvious to everybody.", "그러니까 자기가 박해받는 성인이긴 하지만, 누구나 빤히 아는 그런 사정을 굳이 입에 올려 듣는 사람을 성가시게 하진 않겠다, 뭐 그런 뜻이었지."]], [["‘Now,’ said Ralph, with a smile, which, in common with all other tokens of emotion, seemed to skulk under his face, rather than play boldly over it--‘to return to the point from which we have strayed. I have a little party of--of--gentlemen with whom I am connected in business just now, at my house tomorrow; and your mother has promised that you shall keep house for me. I am not much used to parties; but this is one of business, and such fooleries are an important part of it sometimes. You don’t mind obliging me?’", "\"자,\" 랄프가 미소를 지으며 말했어. 그런데 그 미소는, 다른 모든 감정의 기색이 그렇듯, 얼굴 위에서 대담하게 노니는 게 아니라 오히려 얼굴 밑으로 슬그머니 숨어드는 것 같았어. \"우리가 벗어났던 본론으로 돌아가자꾸나. 내일 우리 집에서 작은 모임이 있단다—요즘 사업으로 엮인—이런저런—신사분들 모임이지. 그래서 네 어머니가 네가 내 집의 안주인 노릇을 해 주기로 약속했단다. 나는 모임 같은 데는 별로 익숙지 않아. 하지만 이건 사업상의 모임이고, 가끔은 그런 바보 같은 짓도 사업의 중요한 일부거든. 나 좀 도와주는 거, 싫지 않지?\""]], [["‘Mind!’ cried Mrs.", "\"싫다니요!\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 외쳤어."], ["‘My dear Kate, why--’", "\"얘야 케이트, 어째서—\""]], [["‘Pray,’ interrupted Ralph, motioning her to be silent.", "\"제발,\" 랄프가 조용히 하라는 손짓을 하며 가로막았어."], ["‘I spoke to my niece.’", "\"조카한테 한 말입니다.\""]], [["‘I shall be very glad, of course, uncle,’ replied Kate; ‘but I am afraid you will find me awkward and embarrassed.’", "\"물론 기꺼이 하겠어요, 삼촌.\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"하지만 제가 어설프고 쩔쩔매서 삼촌을 곤란하게 할까 봐 걱정이에요.\""]], [["‘Oh no,’ said Ralph; ‘come when you like, in a hackney coach--I’ll pay for it.", "\"아, 그럴 일 없다.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["Good-night--a--a--God bless you.’", "\"편한 시간에 오너라, 삯마차를 타고 말이다—삯은 내가 내마. 잘 자거라—그—그—하느님의 가호가 있기를.\""]], [["The blessing seemed to stick in Mr.", "그 축복의 말은 랄프 니클비 씨의 목구멍에 턱 걸린 것 같았어."], ["Ralph Nickleby’s throat, as if it were not used to the thoroughfare, and didn’t know the way out.", "마치 그 길을 다녀 본 적이 없어서 빠져나갈 길을 모르는 것처럼 말이야."], ["But it got out somehow, though awkwardly enough; and having disposed of it, he shook hands with his two relatives, and abruptly left them.", "그래도 어쨌든, 어설프기 짝이 없긴 했지만, 어떻게든 밖으로 나오긴 했지. 그러고는 그 말을 처리하고 나서, 그는 두 친척과 악수를 나누고 휭하니 떠나 버렸어."]], [["‘What a very strongly marked countenance your uncle has!’ said Mrs Nickleby, quite struck with his parting look.", "\"네 삼촌은 얼굴 윤곽이 어찌나 또렷한지!\" 니클비 부인이 그의 작별 표정에 자못 강한 인상을 받은 듯 말했어."], ["‘I don’t see the slightest resemblance to his poor brother.’", "\"가엾은 네 아빠랑은 닮은 구석이 조금도 안 보이는구나.\""]], [["‘Mama!’ said Kate reprovingly.", "\"엄마!\" 케이트가 나무라듯 말했어."], ["‘To think of such a thing!’", "\"그런 생각을 하시다니요!\""]], [["‘No,’ said Mrs.", "\"그래,\""], ["Nickleby, musing.", "니클비 부인이 곰곰 생각하며 말했어."], ["‘There certainly is none.", "\"확실히 하나도 안 닮았어."], ["But it’s a very honest face.’", "그래도 아주 정직한 얼굴이야.\""]], [["The worthy matron made this remark with great emphasis and elocution, as if it comprised no small quantity of ingenuity and research; and, in truth, it was not unworthy of being classed among the extraordinary discoveries of the age.", "이 훌륭한 부인은 이 말을 무척 힘주어, 또박또박 했어. 마치 거기에 적잖은 기지와 탐구가 담겨 있기라도 한 듯이 말이지."], ["Kate looked up hastily, and as hastily looked down again.", "그리고 사실, 그 말은 이 시대의 놀라운 발견 축에 끼어도 손색이 없었어. 케이트는 황급히 고개를 들었다가, 또 그만큼 황급히 다시 고개를 떨궜어."]], [["‘What has come over you, my dear, in the name of goodness?’ asked Mrs Nickleby, when they had walked on, for some time, in silence.", "\"대체 무슨 일이니, 얘야, 도무지 모르겠구나?\" 니클비 부인이 물었어. 둘이 한동안 말없이 걸은 뒤였지."]], [["‘I was only thinking, mama,’ answered Kate.", "\"그냥 생각 좀 하고 있었어요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 대답했어."]], [["‘Thinking!’ repeated Mrs.", "\"생각이라고!\" 니클비 부인이 되뇌었어."], ["Nickleby. ‘Ay, and indeed plenty to think about, too.", "\"그래, 그것도 생각할 게 참 많지."], ["Your uncle has taken a strong fancy to you, that’s quite clear; and if some extraordinary good fortune doesn’t come to you, after this, I shall be a little surprised, that’s all.’", "네 삼촌이 너를 무척 마음에 들어 하시는 게 분명해. 그러니 이 일이 있고 나서 너한테 무슨 굉장한 행운이 찾아오지 않는다면, 나는 좀 놀랄 거야, 그게 다야.\""]], [["With this she launched out into sundry anecdotes of young ladies, who had had thousand-pound notes given them in reticules, by eccentric uncles; and of young ladies who had accidentally met amiable gentlemen of enormous wealth at their uncles’ houses, and married them, after short but ardent courtships; and Kate, listening first in apathy, and afterwards in amusement, felt, as they walked home, something of her mother’s sanguine complexion gradually awakening in her own bosom, and began to think that her prospects might be brightening, and that better days might be dawning upon them. Such is hope, Heaven’s own gift to struggling mortals; pervading, like some subtle essence from the skies, all things, both good and bad; as universal as death, and more infectious than disease!", "이 말과 함께 부인은 갖가지 일화를 줄줄이 늘어놓기 시작했어. 괴짜 삼촌들한테서 손가방에 천 파운드짜리 지폐를 받은 아가씨들 이야기며, 삼촌 집에서 우연히 어마어마한 부자인 다정한 신사를 만나 짧지만 뜨거운 구애 끝에 결혼한 아가씨들 이야기 말이야. 케이트는 처음엔 시큰둥하게, 나중엔 재미있어하며 들었어. 그리고 집으로 걸어가는 동안 어머니의 그 낙천적인 기질 같은 게 자기 가슴속에서도 차츰 깨어나는 걸 느꼈지. 어쩌면 자기 앞날이 환해질지도, 더 나은 날들이 밝아 올지도 모른다는 생각이 들기 시작한 거야. 희망이란 게 그런 거지, 발버둥 치는 인간에게 하늘이 내린 선물 말이야. 하늘에서 내려온 어떤 미묘한 정기처럼 좋은 것이든 나쁜 것이든 모든 것에 스며들고, 죽음만큼이나 보편적이며, 병보다 더 잘 옮는 그런 것 말이지!"]], [["The feeble winter’s sun--and winter’s suns in the city are very feeble indeed--might have brightened up, as he shone through the dim windows of the large old house, on witnessing the unusual sight which one half-furnished room displayed. In a gloomy corner, where, for years, had stood a silent dusty pile of merchandise, sheltering its colony of mice, and frowning, a dull and lifeless mass, upon the panelled room, save when, responding to the roll of heavy waggons in the street without, it quaked with sturdy tremblings and caused the bright eyes of its tiny citizens to grow brighter still with fear, and struck them motionless, with attentive ear and palpitating heart, until the alarm had passed away--in this dark corner, was arranged, with scrupulous care, all Kate’s little finery for the day; each article of dress partaking of that indescribable air of jauntiness and individuality which empty garments--whether by association, or that they become moulded, as it were, to the owner’s form--will take, in eyes accustomed to, or picturing, the wearer’s smartness. In place of a bale of musty goods, there lay the black silk dress: the neatest possible figure in itself. The small shoes, with toes delicately turned out, stood upon the very pressure of some old iron weight; and a pile of harsh discoloured leather had unconsciously given place to the very same little pair of black silk stockings, which had been the objects of Mrs. Nickleby’s peculiar care. Rats and mice, and such small gear, had long ago been starved, or had emigrated to better quarters: and, in their stead, appeared gloves, bands, scarfs, hair-pins, and many other little devices, almost as ingenious in their way as rats and mice themselves, for the tantalisation of mankind. About and among them all, moved Kate herself, not the least beautiful or unwonted relief to the stern, old, gloomy building.", "힘없는 겨울 햇살이—그리고 도시의 겨울 햇살이란 정말이지 무척 힘이 없거든—그 크고 낡은 집의 흐릿한 창문을 뚫고 들어와, 절반쯤만 꾸며진 어느 방이 펼쳐 보이는 흔치 않은 광경을 목격하고는 한결 밝아졌는지도 몰라. 음침한 한구석에는, 여러 해 동안 먼지투성이 상품 더미가 말없이 쌓여 있었어. 그 안에 생쥐 무리를 품고서, 칸막이를 댄 방을 향해 둔하고 생기 없는 덩어리로 잔뜩 찌푸리고 있었지. 다만 바깥 거리에서 무거운 짐마차가 굴러갈 때면 거기에 응답하듯 묵직하게 부르르 떨었고, 그러면 그 안의 자그마한 주민들의 반짝이는 눈은 겁에 질려 한층 더 반짝였어. 그러고는 꼼짝도 못한 채 귀를 쫑긋 세우고 가슴을 졸이며, 그 소란이 다 지나갈 때까지 그대로 굳어 있었지—바로 이 어두운 구석에, 그날 케이트가 걸칠 자그마한 차림새가 하나하나 정성껏 차려져 있었어. 옷가지마다 그 뭐라 말로 다 못할 산뜻하고 개성 있는 분위기가 배어 있었지. 빈 옷이란 게—입던 사람과 함께 떠올라서든, 아니면 주인의 몸에 맞게 본을 떴기 때문이든—그걸 입은 사람의 맵시에 익숙하거나 그 맵시를 그려 보는 눈에는 으레 그런 분위기를 띠게 마련이거든. 곰팡내 나는 물건 더미가 있던 자리에는 검정 비단 드레스가 놓여 있었어. 그 자체로 더없이 단정한 자태였지. 발끝이 곱게 바깥으로 휜 작은 신발은, 옛날에 쇳덩이 추가 짓누르던 바로 그 자리에 놓여 있었어. 그리고 거칠고 빛바랜 가죽 더미가 있던 자리는, 어느새 니클비 부인이 유난히 애지중지하던 바로 그 검정 비단 양말 한 켤레에 자리를 내주고 말았지. 쥐며 생쥐며 그런 자잘한 무리들은 진작에 굶어 죽었거나 더 나은 거처로 옮겨 가 버린 뒤였어. 그리고 그 자리에는 장갑이며 머리띠며 스카프며 머리핀이며, 인간을 애태우는 데는 그 나름대로 쥐나 생쥐 못지않게 교묘한 자잘한 물건들이 잔뜩 나타났지. 그 모든 것 사이로, 또 그 한복판으로 케이트 자신이 거닐고 있었어. 그 엄숙하고 낡고 음침한 건물에는, 결코 덜 아름답거나 덜 진귀하지 않은 빛이었지."]], [["In good time, or in bad time, as the reader likes to take it--for Mrs Nickleby’s impatience went a great deal faster than the clocks at that end of the town, and Kate was dressed to the very last hair-pin a full hour and a half before it was at all necessary to begin to think about it--in good time, or in bad time, the toilet was completed; and it being at length the hour agreed upon for starting, the milkman fetched a coach from the nearest stand, and Kate, with many adieux to her mother, and many kind messages to Miss La Creevy, who was to come to tea, seated herself in it, and went away in state, if ever anybody went away in state in a hackney coach yet. And the coach, and the coachman, and the horses, rattled, and jangled, and whipped, and cursed, and swore, and tumbled on together, until they came to Golden Square.", "좋은 시간에든 나쁜 시간에든, 그건 독자가 어떻게 받아들이든 좋아—니클비 부인의 조바심은 그쪽 동네 시계들보다 훨씬 빨리 앞서 나갔거든. 그래서 케이트는 그런 채비를 생각하기 시작해도 될 시각보다 꼬박 한 시간 반이나 일찍 머리핀 하나까지 빈틈없이 차려입었지—좋은 시간에든 나쁜 시간에든, 어쨌든 단장은 다 끝났어. 그리고 마침내 출발하기로 약속한 시각이 되자, 우유 배달부가 제일 가까운 정류장에서 마차를 한 대 불러왔지. 케이트는 어머니에게 몇 번이고 작별 인사를 하고, 차 마시러 오기로 한 미스 라크리비에게도 다정한 안부를 잔뜩 남긴 뒤, 그 마차에 올라타 위풍당당하게 떠나갔어. 삯마차를 타고 위풍당당하게 떠나간 사람이 여태 있었다면 말이지만 말이야. 그리고 마차와 마부와 말들은 덜컹거리고 삐걱대고 채찍질하고 욕을 퍼붓고 악담을 하며 함께 굴러갔어. 골든 스퀘어에 다다를 때까지 말이지."]], [["The coachman gave a tremendous double knock at the door, which was opened long before he had done, as quickly as if there had been a man behind it, with his hand tied to the latch. Kate, who had expected no more uncommon appearance than Newman Noggs in a clean shirt, was not a little astonished to see that the opener was a man in handsome livery, and that there were two or three others in the hall. There was no doubt about its being the right house, however, for there was the name upon the door; so she accepted the laced coat-sleeve which was tendered her, and entering the house, was ushered upstairs, into a back drawing-room, where she was left alone.", "마부가 현관문을 어찌나 요란하게 두 번 두들겼는지, 그가 다 두들기기도 한참 전에 문이 열렸어. 마치 문 뒤에 어떤 남자가 손을 빗장에 묶어 두고 서 있기라도 한 것처럼 잽싸게 말이야. 케이트는 깨끗한 셔츠를 입은 뉴먼 노그스보다 더 별난 모습은 없으리라 여겼던 터라, 문을 연 사람이 멋진 제복을 입은 남자이고 현관에 그런 사람이 두셋이나 더 있는 걸 보고는 적잖이 놀랐어. 그래도 제대로 찾아온 집이라는 데는 의심의 여지가 없었지. 문에 이름이 적혀 있었으니까. 그래서 케이트는 자기에게 내밀어진 레이스 달린 코트 소매를 잡고 집 안으로 들어섰어. 그러고는 위층으로 안내되어 뒤쪽 응접실로 들어갔고, 거기 혼자 남겨졌지."]], [["If she had been surprised at the apparition of the footman, she was perfectly absorbed in amazement at the richness and splendour of the furniture.", "하인이 나타난 데 놀랐다면, 케이트는 가구의 호화롭고 으리으리한 모습에는 그야말로 넋을 잃었어."], ["The softest and most elegant carpets, the most exquisite pictures, the costliest mirrors; articles of richest ornament, quite dazzling from their beauty and perplexing from the prodigality with which they were scattered around; encountered her on every side.", "더없이 부드럽고 우아한 양탄자, 더없이 정교한 그림들, 더없이 값비싼 거울들. 그 아름다움에 눈이 부시고, 사방에 함부로 뿌려 놓은 듯한 그 사치스러움에 어리둥절할 만큼 화려한 장식품들이 사방에서 그녀를 맞이했어."], ["The very staircase nearly down to the hall-door, was crammed with beautiful and luxurious things, as though the house were brimful of riches, which, with a very trifling addition, would fairly run over into the street.", "현관문 바로 아래까지 이어진 계단조차 아름답고 호사스러운 물건들로 가득 차 있었지. 마치 집 안이 온갖 부로 넘쳐 나서, 조금만 더 보태면 그게 거리로 흘러넘칠 것만 같았어."]], [["Presently, she heard a series of loud double knocks at the street-door, and after every knock some new voice in the next room; the tones of Mr Ralph Nickleby were easily distinguishable at first, but by degrees they merged into the general buzz of conversation, and all she could ascertain was, that there were several gentlemen with no very musical voices, who talked very loud, laughed very heartily, and swore more than she would have thought quite necessary. But this was a question of taste.", "이내 케이트는 현관문에서 요란하게 두 번씩 두드리는 소리가 잇따라 들려오는 걸 들었어. 그리고 두드리는 소리가 날 때마다 옆방에서 새로운 목소리가 하나씩 들려왔지. 처음엔 랄프 니클비 씨의 목소리가 쉽게 구별됐지만, 차츰 두런두런하는 이야기 소리에 묻혀 버렸어. 그녀가 알아챌 수 있는 거라곤, 그다지 곱지 않은 목소리를 지닌 신사가 여럿 있다는 것뿐이었지. 그들은 아주 크게 떠들고, 아주 호탕하게 웃고, 그녀가 보기엔 굳이 그럴 필요까진 없을 만큼 욕을 해 댔어. 하지만 그거야 취향 문제니까."]], [["At length, the door opened, and Ralph himself, divested of his boots, and ceremoniously embellished with black silks and shoes, presented his crafty face.", "마침내 문이 열리고, 랄프가 직접 모습을 드러냈어. 부츠는 벗고, 검정 비단과 구두로 격식 있게 차려입은 채로, 교활한 얼굴을 들이밀었지."]], [["‘I couldn’t see you before, my dear,’ he said, in a low tone, and pointing, as he spoke, to the next room.", "\"아까는 너를 보러 올 수가 없었구나, 얘야.\" 그가 나직한 목소리로 말하며 옆방을 가리켰어."], ["‘I was engaged in receiving them.", "\"손님들 맞느라 바빴거든."], ["Now--shall I take you in?’", "자, 이제—너를 들여보내 줄까?\""]], [["‘Pray, uncle,’ said Kate, a little flurried, as people much more conversant with society often are, when they are about to enter a room full of strangers, and have had time to think of it previously, ‘are there any ladies here?’", "\"저, 삼촌,\" 케이트가 조금 안절부절못하며 말했어. 사교계에 훨씬 익숙한 사람들도 낯선 이들로 가득한 방에 들어서기 직전, 미리 그걸 생각할 틈이 있었을 때면 흔히 그러듯이 말이야. \"거기 여자 분들도 계신가요?\""]], [["‘No,’ said Ralph, shortly, ‘I don’t know any.’", "\"아니,\" 랄프가 짤막하게 말했어. \"내가 아는 여자는 없구나.\""]], [["‘Must I go in immediately?’ asked Kate, drawing back a little.", "\"바로 들어가야 하나요?\" 케이트가 조금 뒷걸음치며 물었어."]], [["‘As you please,’ said Ralph, shrugging his shoulders.", "\"네 마음대로 하렴.\" 랄프가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했어."], ["‘They are all come, and dinner will be announced directly afterwards--that’s all.’", "\"다들 와 있고, 곧 저녁이 차려질 거라는 것만 알아 두렴—그게 다야.\""]], [["Kate would have entreated a few minutes’ respite, but reflecting that her uncle might consider the payment of the hackney-coach fare a sort of bargain for her punctuality, she suffered him to draw her arm through his, and to lead her away.", "케이트는 몇 분만 말미를 달라고 사정하고 싶었어. 하지만 삼촌이 삯마차 삯을 낸 걸 자기가 시간을 잘 지키기로 한 일종의 거래쯤으로 여길지도 모른다는 생각이 들었지. 그래서 삼촌이 자기 팔을 그의 팔에 끼우고 데려가게 내버려 두었어."]], [["Seven or eight gentlemen were standing round the fire when they went in, and, as they were talking very loud, were not aware of their entrance until Mr.", "두 사람이 들어섰을 때 신사 일고여덟 명이 난롯가에 둘러서 있었어."], ["Ralph Nickleby, touching one on the coat-sleeve, said in a harsh emphatic voice, as if to attract general attention--", "그리고 그들은 아주 크게 떠들고 있던 터라, 랄프 니클비 씨가 그중 한 사람의 코트 소매를 건드리며 모두의 주의를 끌려는 듯 거칠고 힘준 목소리로 말할 때까지 두 사람이 들어온 줄도 몰랐지—"]], [["‘Lord Frederick Verisopht, my niece, Miss Nickleby.’", "\"프레더릭 베리소프트 경, 제 조카 미스 니클비입니다.\""]], [["The group dispersed, as if in great surprise, and the gentleman addressed, turning round, exhibited a suit of clothes of the most superlative cut, a pair of whiskers of similar quality, a moustache, a head of hair, and a young face.", "무리가 깜짝 놀라기라도 한 듯 흩어졌어. 그리고 호명을 받은 신사가 돌아서자, 더없이 빼어나게 재단된 옷 한 벌과 그에 못지않은 구레나룻, 콧수염, 한 머리 가득한 머리카락, 그리고 젊은 얼굴이 드러났지."]], [["‘Eh!’ said the gentleman.", "\"어!\" 그 신사가 말했어."], ["‘What--the--deyvle!’", "\"이런—세상에—맙소사!\""]], [["With which broken ejaculations, he fixed his glass in his eye, and stared at Miss Nickleby in great surprise.", "이 토막 난 탄성과 함께, 그는 외알 안경을 눈에 끼우고 깜짝 놀란 얼굴로 미스 니클비를 빤히 쳐다봤어."]], [["‘My niece, my lord,’ said Ralph.", "\"제 조카입니다, 경.\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Then my ears did not deceive me, and it’s not wa-a-x work,’ said his lordship. ‘How de do? I’m very happy.’ And then his lordship turned to another superlative gentleman, something older, something stouter, something redder in the face, and something longer upon town, and said in a loud whisper that the girl was ‘deyvlish pitty.’", "\"그렇다면 내 귀가 날 속인 게 아니로군. 밀랍 인형도 아니고 말이지.\" 경이 말했어. \"안녕하시오? 참으로 반갑소이다.\" 그러고는 경은 또 다른 빼어난 차림의 신사 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸어. 좀 더 나이가 들고, 좀 더 살집이 있고, 얼굴이 좀 더 불그스레하고, 도시 물을 좀 더 오래 먹은 신사였지. 그러고는 그 처녀가 \"지독하게 예쁘군\"이라고 큰 소리로 속닥였어."]], [["‘Introduce me, Nickleby,’ said this second gentleman, who was lounging with his back to the fire, and both elbows on the chimneypiece.", "\"나도 소개해 주게, 니클비.\" 이 두 번째 신사가 말했어. 그는 난롯불에 등을 대고 양 팔꿈치를 벽난로 선반에 올린 채 어슬렁거리고 있었지."]], [["‘Sir Mulberry Hawk,’ said Ralph.", "\"멀베리 호크 경입니다.\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Otherwise the most knowing card in the pa-ack, Miss Nickleby,’ said Lord Frederick Verisopht.", "\"달리 말하면 한 무리 가운데 가장 닳고 닳은 패라는 분이죠, 미스 니클비.\" 프레더릭 베리소프트 경이 말했어."]], [["‘Don’t leave me out, Nickleby,’ cried a sharp-faced gentleman, who was sitting on a low chair with a high back, reading the paper.", "\"나도 빼놓지 말게, 니클비.\" 등받이가 높은 낮은 의자에 앉아 신문을 읽고 있던, 얼굴이 날카로운 신사가 외쳤어."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"파이크 씨입니다.\""], ["Pyke,’ said Ralph.", "랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Nor me, Nickleby,’ cried a gentleman with a flushed face and a flash air, from the elbow of Sir Mulberry Hawk.", "\"나도, 니클비.\" 멀베리 호크 경의 팔꿈치께에서, 얼굴이 벌겋고 야단스러운 분위기를 풍기는 신사가 외쳤어."]], [["‘Mr. Pluck,’ said Ralph.", "\"플럭 씨입니다.\""], ["Then wheeling about again, towards a gentleman with the neck of a stork and the legs of no animal in particular, Ralph introduced him as the Honourable Mr.", "랄프가 말했어. 그러고는 다시 휙 돌아서서, 황새 같은 목에 어느 짐승의 것도 아닌 다리를 지닌 신사를 향해, 그를 스놉 씨라고 소개했어."], ["Snobb; and a white-headed person at the table as Colonel Chowser.", "그리고 식탁에 앉은 백발의 사람은 차우저 대령이라고 했지."], ["The colonel was in conversation with somebody, who appeared to be a make-weight, and was not introduced at all.", "대령은 어떤 사람과 이야기를 나누고 있었는데, 그 사람은 그저 머릿수나 채우는 인물처럼 보였고 아예 소개도 받지 못했어."]], [["There were two circumstances which, in this early stage of the party, struck home to Kate’s bosom, and brought the blood tingling to her face.", "이 모임 초반에 케이트의 가슴을 콕콕 찌르며 피를 얼굴로 화끈 솟구치게 한 일이 두 가지 있었어. 하나는 손님들이 자기 삼촌을 대놓고 깔보는 그 경박한 태도였고, 다른 하나는 자기를 향한 그 거리낌 없는 무례함이었지."], ["One was the flippant contempt with which the guests evidently regarded her uncle, and the other, the easy insolence of their manner towards herself.", "첫 번째 낌새가 두 번째를 한층 더 심하게 만들기 쉬우리라는 건, 굳이 대단한 통찰력이 없어도 내다볼 수 있었어."], ["That the first symptom was very likely to lead to the aggravation of the second, it needed no great penetration to foresee. And here Mr.", "그리고 여기서 랄프 니클비 씨는 잘못 계산한 셈이었지."], ["Ralph Nickleby had reckoned without his host; for however fresh from the country a young lady (by nature) may be, and however unacquainted with conventional behaviour, the chances are, that she will have quite as strong an innate sense of the decencies and proprieties of life as if she had run the gauntlet of a dozen London seasons--possibly a stronger one, for such senses have been known to blunt in this improving process.", "아무리 시골에서 갓 올라온 (천성이 그런) 아가씨라 해도, 또 사교 예법에 아무리 서툴다 해도, 십중팔구 그 아가씨는 세상살이의 예의범절에 대한 타고난 감각을, 런던에서 열 번이나 사교철을 거친 사람 못지않게—어쩌면 그보다 더 강하게—지니고 있게 마련이거든. 그런 감각이란 그렇게 닳고 닳는 과정에서 무뎌지는 법이라고들 하니까."]], [["When Ralph had completed the ceremonial of introduction, he led his blushing niece to a seat.", "랄프가 소개 절차를 다 마치고 나서, 그는 얼굴이 발그레해진 조카를 자리로 안내했어."], ["As he did so, he glanced warily round as though to assure himself of the impression which her unlooked-for appearance had created.", "그러면서 그는 경계하듯 주위를 슬쩍 둘러봤지. 뜻밖에 나타난 그녀가 어떤 인상을 불러일으켰는지 가늠해 보려는 것처럼 말이야."]], [["‘An unexpected playsure, Nickleby,’ said Lord Frederick Verisopht, taking his glass out of his right eye, where it had, until now, done duty on Kate, and fixing it in his left, to bring it to bear on Ralph.", "\"뜻밖의 즐거움이군, 니클비.\" 프레더릭 베리소프트 경이 말했어. 그러면서 지금껏 케이트를 살피느라 끼고 있던 오른쪽 눈의 외알 안경을 빼서 왼쪽 눈에 끼웠지. 랄프를 겨눠 보려고 말이야."]], [["‘Designed to surprise you, Lord Frederick,’ said Mr.", "\"경을 놀래 드리려고 마련한 거랍니다, 프레더릭 경.\""], ["Pluck.", "플럭 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Not a bad idea,’ said his lordship, ‘and one that would almost warrant the addition of an extra two and a half per cent.’", "\"나쁘지 않은 발상이군.\" 경이 말했어. \"이건 거의 이 점 오 퍼센트쯤 더 붙여도 될 만한 일이야.\""]], [["‘Nickleby,’ said Sir Mulberry Hawk, in a thick coarse voice, ‘take the hint, and tack it on the other five-and-twenty, or whatever it is, and give me half for the advice.’", "\"니클비,\" 멀베리 호크 경이 굵고 거친 목소리로 말했어. \"그 귀띔을 받아들여서 나머지 스물다섯이든 얼마든 거기에 덧붙이게. 그리고 그 조언값으로 나한테 절반을 주고 말이야.\""]], [["Sir Mulberry garnished this speech with a hoarse laugh, and terminated it with a pleasant oath regarding Mr.", "멀베리 경은 이 말에 쉰 웃음을 곁들이고는, 니클비 씨의 사지에 대고 유쾌한 욕설 한마디로 마무리했어."], ["Nickleby’s limbs, whereat Messrs Pyke and Pluck laughed consumedly.", "그러자 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨가 죽어라 웃어 댔지."]], [["These gentlemen had not yet quite recovered the jest, when dinner was announced, and then they were thrown into fresh ecstasies by a similar cause; for Sir Mulberry Hawk, in an excess of humour, shot dexterously past Lord Frederick Verisopht who was about to lead Kate downstairs, and drew her arm through his up to the elbow.", "이 신사들이 그 농담에서 채 헤어나기도 전에 저녁 식사가 알려졌어. 그러더니 그들은 비슷한 일로 또 한바탕 황홀경에 빠졌지. 멀베리 호크 경이 흥에 겨운 나머지, 케이트를 아래층으로 데려가려던 프레더릭 베리소프트 경의 곁을 잽싸게 스쳐 지나가더니, 케이트의 팔을 팔꿈치까지 자기 팔에 끼워 버렸거든."]], [["‘No, damn it, Verisopht,’ said Sir Mulberry, ‘fair play’s a jewel, and Miss Nickleby and I settled the matter with our eyes ten minutes ago.’", "\"안 되지, 빌어먹을, 베리소프트,\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"정정당당함이야말로 보배 아닌가. 게다가 미스 니클비랑 나는 십 분 전에 이미 눈으로 이 문제를 매듭지었다네.\""]], [["‘Ha, ha, ha!’ laughed the honourable Mr.", "\"하, 하, 하!\" 스놉 씨가 웃었어."], ["Snobb, ‘very good, very good.’", "\"아주 좋군, 아주 좋아.\""]], [["Rendered additionally witty by this applause, Sir Mulberry Hawk leered upon his friends most facetiously, and led Kate downstairs with an air of familiarity, which roused in her gentle breast such burning indignation, as she felt it almost impossible to repress.", "이 갈채에 한층 더 재치가 솟은 멀베리 호크 경은 친구들을 향해 더없이 익살맞게 곁눈질하고는, 허물없는 태도로 케이트를 아래층으로 데려갔어. 그 태도에 케이트의 여린 가슴속에서는 도무지 억누르기 힘들 만큼 활활 타오르는 분노가 치밀었지."], ["Nor was the intensity of these feelings at all diminished, when she found herself placed at the top of the table, with Sir Mulberry Hawk and Lord Frederick Verisopht on either side.", "그리고 그 격한 감정은 조금도 누그러들지 않았어. 자기가 식탁 상석에 앉혀지고, 양옆에 멀베리 호크 경과 프레더릭 베리소프트 경이 앉은 걸 알았을 때 말이야."]], [["‘Oh, you’ve found your way into our neighbourhood, have you?’ said Sir Mulberry as his lordship sat down.", "\"오, 우리 동네까지 길을 찾아오셨군그래?\" 경이 자리에 앉자 멀베리 경이 말했어."]], [["‘Of course,’ replied Lord Frederick, fixing his eyes on Miss Nickleby, ‘how can you a-ask me?’", "\"당연하지,\" 프레더릭 경이 미스 니클비에게 눈길을 고정한 채 대답했어. \"그걸 어찌 물어보나?\""]], [["‘Well, you attend to your dinner,’ said Sir Mulberry, ‘and don’t mind Miss Nickleby and me, for we shall prove very indifferent company, I dare say.’", "\"자, 자넨 저녁이나 들게.\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"그리고 미스 니클비랑 나는 신경 쓰지 말게. 우리야 아주 시시한 말동무가 될 테니까 말이야, 아마도.\""]], [["‘I wish you’d interfere here, Nickleby,’ said Lord Frederick.", "\"여기 좀 끼어들어 주게, 니클비.\" 프레더릭 경이 말했어."]], [["‘What is the matter, my lord?’ demanded Ralph from the bottom of the table, where he was supported by Messrs Pyke and Pluck.", "\"무슨 일입니까, 경?\" 식탁 끝에서 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨의 호위를 받고 있던 랄프가 물었어."]], [["‘This fellow, Hawk, is monopolising your niece,’ said Lord Frederick.", "\"이 친구, 호크가 자네 조카를 독차지하고 있단 말이야.\" 프레더릭 경이 말했어."]], [["‘He has a tolerable share of everything that you lay claim to, my lord,’ said Ralph with a sneer.", "\"경께서 차지하려는 것이라면 무엇이든 저 친구가 웬만큼은 한몫 챙기지요, 경.\" 랄프가 비웃듯 말했어."]], [["‘’Gad, so he has,’ replied the young man; ‘deyvle take me if I know which is master in my house, he or I.’", "\"맞아, 정말 그렇지.\" 젊은이가 대답했어. \"맙소사, 우리 집에서 누가 주인인지 모르겠다니까, 저 친구인지 나인지 말이야.\""]], [["‘I know,’ muttered Ralph.", "\"저는 압니다.\" 랄프가 중얼거렸어."]], [["‘I think I shall cut him off with a shilling,’ said the young nobleman, jocosely.", "\"저 친구를 한 푼만 남기고 잘라 버릴까 봐.\" 젊은 귀족이 농담조로 말했어."]], [["‘No, no, curse it,’ said Sir Mulberry.", "\"아니, 아니, 천만에,\" 멀베리 경이 말했어."], ["‘When you come to the shilling--the last shilling--I’ll cut you fast enough; but till then, I’ll never leave you--you may take your oath of it.’", "\"자네가 그 한 푼—마지막 한 푼에 다다르면, 내가 잽싸게 자네를 잘라 버릴 걸세. 하지만 그때까진 절대 자네 곁을 안 떠날 거야—그건 맹세코 장담하지.\""]], [["This sally (which was strictly founded on fact) was received with a general roar, above which, was plainly distinguishable the laughter of Mr.", "이 농담은 (엄연히 사실에 바탕을 둔 거였지만) 한바탕 웃음으로 받아들여졌어. 그 위로 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨의 웃음소리가 또렷이 구별됐지."], ["Pyke and Mr. Pluck, who were, evidently, Sir Mulberry’s toads in ordinary.", "누가 봐도 이 둘은 멀베리 경에게 늘 따라붙는 아첨꾼이었거든."], ["Indeed, it was not difficult to see, that the majority of the company preyed upon the unfortunate young lord, who, weak and silly as he was, appeared by far the least vicious of the party.", "사실 그 자리의 손님 대다수가 그 가엾은 젊은 경을 등쳐 먹고 있다는 건 어렵잖게 알 수 있었어. 그 젊은 경은 나약하고 어리석긴 해도, 무리 중에서는 단연 가장 덜 못된 축에 들어 보였으니까."], ["Sir Mulberry Hawk was remarkable for his tact in ruining, by himself and his creatures, young gentlemen of fortune--a genteel and elegant profession, of which he had undoubtedly gained the head.", "멀베리 호크 경은 자기와 자기 졸개들을 부려서 재산깨나 있는 젊은 신사들을 거덜 내는 데 능란하기로 유명했어—점잖고 우아한 직업이었고, 그가 그 분야에서 으뜸가는 자리에 올랐다는 데는 의심의 여지가 없었지. 그는 독창적인 천재다운 온갖 대담함으로, 흔한 방식과는 완전히 동떨어진 아주 새로운 수법을 고안해 냈어."], ["With all the boldness of an original genius, he had struck out an entirely new course of treatment quite opposed to the usual method; his custom being, when he had gained the ascendancy over those he took in hand, rather to keep them down than to give them their own way; and to exercise his vivacity upon them openly, and without reserve.", "그가 손아귀에 넣은 자들 위에 일단 군림하게 되면, 그들을 제멋대로 하게 두기보다는 오히려 짓눌러 두는 게 그의 버릇이었지. 그리고 그들에게 대놓고, 거리낌 없이 자기 재기를 휘둘렀어."], ["Thus, he made them butts, in a double sense, and while he emptied them with great address, caused them to ring with sundry well-administered taps, for the diversion of society.", "그렇게 그는 두 가지 뜻에서 그들을 표적으로 삼은 거야. 능란한 솜씨로 그들을 텅 비게 만드는가 하면, 사람들 좋으라고 적당히 두드려서 그들이 텅텅 울리게 만들었으니까."]], [["The dinner was as remarkable for the splendour and completeness of its appointments as the mansion itself, and the company were remarkable for doing it ample justice, in which respect Messrs Pyke and Pluck particularly signalised themselves; these two gentlemen eating of every dish, and drinking of every bottle, with a capacity and perseverance truly astonishing. They were remarkably fresh, too, notwithstanding their great exertions: for, on the appearance of the dessert, they broke out again, as if nothing serious had taken place since breakfast.", "만찬은 그 으리으리하고 빈틈없는 차림새가 저택 자체만큼이나 대단했어. 그리고 손님들은 그 만찬을 한껏 정당하게 대접하는 데 대단했지. 그 점에선 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨가 특히 두드러졌어. 이 두 신사는 요리란 요리는 죄다 먹고 술병이란 술병은 죄다 비웠는데, 그 양과 끈기가 정말이지 놀라울 지경이었지. 게다가 그렇게 무진 애를 썼는데도 두 사람은 놀랄 만큼 멀쩡했어. 후식이 나오자, 마치 아침 식사 이후로 별일 없었다는 듯 다시 또 시작했으니까."]], [["‘Well,’ said Lord Frederick, sipping his first glass of port, ‘if this is a discounting dinner, all I have to say is, deyvle take me, if it wouldn’t be a good pla-an to get discount every day.’", "\"자,\" 프레더릭 경이 첫 포트와인 잔을 홀짝이며 말했어. \"이게 어음 할인 만찬이라면, 내가 할 말은 이것뿐이야. 맙소사, 날마다 할인을 받는 것도 괜찮은 수단이겠는걸.\""]], [["‘You’ll have plenty of it, in your time,’ returned Sir Mulberry Hawk; ‘Nickleby will tell you that.’", "\"그럴 기회야 살면서 실컷 누리실 겁니다, 경.\" 멀베리 호크 경이 받아쳤어. \"니클비가 그렇다고 말해 줄 거고요.\""]], [["‘What do you say, Nickleby?’ inquired the young man; ‘am I to be a good customer?’", "\"어떻게 말하겠나, 니클비?\" 젊은이가 물었어. \"내가 좋은 고객이 될까?\""]], [["‘It depends entirely on circumstances, my lord,’ replied Ralph.", "\"그건 전적으로 사정에 달렸지요, 경.\" 랄프가 대답했어."]], [["‘On your lordship’s circumstances,’ interposed Colonel Chowser of the Militia--and the race-courses.", "\"경의 사정에 달렸지요.\" 의용군—그리고 경마장—소속의 차우저 대령이 끼어들었어."]], [["The gallant colonel glanced at Messrs Pyke and Pluck as if he thought they ought to laugh at his joke; but those gentlemen, being only engaged to laugh for Sir Mulberry Hawk, were, to his signal discomfiture, as grave as a pair of undertakers. To add to his defeat, Sir Mulberry, considering any such efforts an invasion of his peculiar privilege, eyed the offender steadily, through his glass, as if astonished at his presumption, and audibly stated his impression that it was an ‘infernal liberty,’ which being a hint to Lord Frederick, he put up HIS glass, and surveyed the object of censure as if he were some extraordinary wild animal then exhibiting for the first time. As a matter of course, Messrs Pyke and Pluck stared at the individual whom Sir Mulberry Hawk stared at; so, the poor colonel, to hide his confusion, was reduced to the necessity of holding his port before his right eye and affecting to scrutinise its colour with the most lively interest.", "이 늠름한 대령은 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨가 자기 농담에 웃어 줘야 한다고 여기는 듯 그쪽을 흘끔 봤어. 하지만 그 신사들은 오로지 멀베리 호크 경을 위해서만 웃기로 되어 있던 터라, 대령으로선 톡톡히 망신스럽게도, 장의사 둘처럼 근엄하기만 했지. 게다가 대령의 패배를 더하기라도 하듯, 멀베리 경은 그런 시도를 죄다 자기만의 고유한 특권을 침범하는 짓으로 여겨, 외알 안경 너머로 그 무엄한 자를 빤히 노려봤어. 마치 그 주제넘음에 어이가 없다는 듯이 말이지. 그러고는 이건 \"괘씸한 무례\"라는 자기 생각을 다 들리게 떠벌렸어. 그게 프레더릭 경에겐 일종의 신호가 됐고, 경도 자기 외알 안경을 들어 올려, 그 비난의 대상을 마치 처음 전시되는 무슨 진귀한 야생 짐승이라도 되는 양 살펴봤지. 당연한 일이지만, 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨도 멀베리 호크 경이 노려보는 그자를 빤히 쳐다봤어. 그래서 가엾은 대령은 자기 당혹감을 감추려고, 어쩔 수 없이 포트와인 잔을 오른쪽 눈앞에 들고는 더없이 흥미진진하다는 듯 그 빛깔을 들여다보는 척하는 처지가 되고 말았지."]], [["All this while, Kate had sat as silently as she could, scarcely daring to raise her eyes, lest they should encounter the admiring gaze of Lord Frederick Verisopht, or, what was still more embarrassing, the bold looks of his friend Sir Mulberry.", "이러는 내내 케이트는 되도록 조용히 앉아 있었어. 좀처럼 눈을 들 엄두도 못 냈지."], ["The latter gentleman was obliging enough to direct general attention towards her.", "프레더릭 베리소프트 경의 흠모하는 눈길이나, 그보다 더 거북한, 그의 친구 멀베리 경의 뻔뻔한 시선과 마주칠까 봐서 말이야. 그런데 이 후자의 신사가 친절하게도 모두의 시선을 그녀 쪽으로 돌려 줬지."]], [["‘Here is Miss Nickleby,’ observed Sir Mulberry, ‘wondering why the deuce somebody doesn’t make love to her.’", "\"여기 미스 니클비가,\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"어째서 아무도 자기한테 사랑 고백을 안 하나 궁금해하고 있군그래.\""]], [["‘No, indeed,’ said Kate, looking hastily up, ‘I--’ and then she stopped, feeling it would have been better to have said nothing at all.", "\"아니에요, 정말로요.\" 케이트가 황급히 고개를 들며 말했어. \"전—\" 그러고는 입을 다물었어. 아예 아무 말도 하지 않는 편이 나았겠다 싶어서였지."]], [["‘I’ll hold any man fifty pounds,’ said Sir Mulberry, ‘that Miss Nickleby can’t look in my face, and tell me she wasn’t thinking so.’", "\"내 누구한테든 오십 파운드를 걸겠네,\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"미스 니클비가 내 얼굴을 똑바로 보면서, 자기가 그런 생각을 안 하고 있었다고 말하진 못할 거라고 말이야.\""]], [["‘Done!’ cried the noble gull.", "\"좋아!\" 그 고귀한 봉이 외쳤어."], ["‘Within ten minutes.’", "\"십 분 안에.\""]], [["‘Done!’ responded Sir Mulberry.", "\"좋네!\" 멀베리 경이 응했어."], ["The money was produced on both sides, and the Honourable Mr. Snobb was elected to the double office of stake-holder and time-keeper.", "양쪽에서 돈이 나왔고, 스놉 씨가 판돈 관리인 겸 시간 기록원이라는 두 직책에 뽑혔어."]], [["‘Pray,’ said Kate, in great confusion, while these preliminaries were in course of completion.", "\"제발,\" 케이트가 몹시 당황한 채로 말했어."], ["‘Pray do not make me the subject of any bets.", "이 준비가 한창 진행되는 동안이었지. \"제발 저를 무슨 내기의 대상으로 삼지 말아 주세요."], ["Uncle, I cannot really--’", "삼촌, 정말 저는—\""]], [["‘Why not, my dear?’ replied Ralph, in whose grating voice, however, there was an unusual huskiness, as though he spoke unwillingly, and would rather that the proposition had not been broached. ‘It is done in a moment; there is nothing in it. If the gentlemen insist on it--’", "\"왜 안 되니, 얘야?\" 랄프가 대답했어. 그런데 그 거슬리는 목소리에는 웬일로 낯선 쉰 기운이 섞여 있었어. 마지못해 말하는 듯, 차라리 그런 제안이 나오지 않았기를 바라는 듯 말이야. \"금방 끝나는 일이야. 별것 아니란다. 신사분들이 굳이 하시겠다면—\""]], [["‘I don’t insist on it,’ said Sir Mulberry, with a loud laugh. ‘That is, I by no means insist upon Miss Nickleby’s making the denial, for if she does, I lose; but I shall be glad to see her bright eyes, especially as she favours the mahogany so much.’", "\"내가 굳이 하자는 건 아닐세.\" 멀베리 경이 큰 소리로 웃으며 말했어. \"그러니까, 미스 니클비더러 꼭 그 부정을 하라고 우기는 건 결코 아니야. 그렇게 하면 내가 지는 거니까. 다만 나는 저 빛나는 눈을 보면 기쁘겠다는 거지. 특히나 저렇게 마호가니 식탁만 어여삐 봐 주고 계시니 말이야.\""]], [["‘So she does, and it’s too ba-a-d of you, Miss Nickleby,’ said the noble youth.", "\"정말 그렇소, 그건 너무하시오, 미스 니클비.\" 그 고귀한 젊은이가 말했어."]], [["‘Quite cruel,’ said Mr.", "\"너무 잔인하시군요.\""], ["Pyke.", "파이크 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Horrid cruel,’ said Mr.", "\"끔찍하게 잔인해요.\""], ["Pluck.", "플럭 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘I don’t care if I do lose,’ said Sir Mulberry; ‘for one tolerable look at Miss Nickleby’s eyes is worth double the money.’", "\"내기에 진다 해도 상관없네.\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"미스 니클비의 눈을 그럴듯하게 한 번 보는 것만 해도 그 돈의 곱절은 되니까.\""]], [["‘More,’ said Mr.", "\"그 이상이죠.\""], ["Pyke.", "파이크 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Far more,’ said Mr.", "\"훨씬 그 이상이죠.\""], ["Pluck.", "플럭 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘How goes the enemy, Snobb?’ asked Sir Mulberry Hawk.", "\"적군은 어찌 돼 가나, 스놉?\" 멀베리 호크 경이 물었어."]], [["‘Four minutes gone.’", "\"사 분 지났습니다.\""]], [["‘Bravo!’", "\"브라보!\""]], [["‘Won’t you ma-ake one effort for me, Miss Nickleby?’ asked Lord Frederick, after a short interval.", "\"날 위해 한 번 애써 봐 주지 않겠소, 미스 니클비?\" 잠깐 사이를 두고 프레더릭 경이 물었어."]], [["‘You needn’t trouble yourself to inquire, my buck,’ said Sir Mulberry; ‘Miss Nickleby and I understand each other; she declares on my side, and shows her taste.", "\"굳이 물어볼 것도 없네, 이 친구야.\" 멀베리 경이 말했어."], ["You haven’t a chance, old fellow.", "\"미스 니클비랑 나는 서로 통하거든. 저분은 내 편을 들고, 그렇게 자기 안목을 보여 주고 있다네."], ["Time, Snobb?’", "자넨 가망이 없어, 친구. 시간, 스놉?\""]], [["‘Eight minutes gone.’", "\"팔 분 지났습니다.\""]], [["‘Get the money ready,’ said Sir Mulberry; ‘you’ll soon hand over.’", "\"돈이나 준비해 두게.\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"곧 건네주게 될 테니.\""]], [["‘Ha, ha, ha!’ laughed Mr.", "\"하, 하, 하!\""], ["Pyke.", "파이크 씨가 웃었어."]], [["Mr. Pluck, who always came second, and topped his companion if he could, screamed outright.", "언제나 둘째로 나서서, 할 수만 있으면 자기 동료를 능가하려 드는 플럭 씨는 아예 째지게 비명을 질렀지."]], [["The poor girl, who was so overwhelmed with confusion that she scarcely knew what she did, had determined to remain perfectly quiet; but fearing that by so doing she might seem to countenance Sir Mulberry’s boast, which had been uttered with great coarseness and vulgarity of manner, raised her eyes, and looked him in the face. There was something so odious, so insolent, so repulsive in the look which met her, that, without the power to stammer forth a syllable, she rose and hurried from the room. She restrained her tears by a great effort until she was alone upstairs, and then gave them vent.", "그 가엾은 처녀는 당혹감에 어찌나 휩싸였던지 자기가 무얼 하는지도 모를 지경이었어. 그래서 그냥 가만히 있기로 마음먹었지. 하지만 그렇게 하면, 그렇게 거칠고 천박한 태도로 내뱉어진 멀베리 경의 그 호언장담을 자기가 인정하는 꼴로 비칠까 봐 두려웠어. 그래서 눈을 들어 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 봤지. 그런데 그녀와 마주친 그 눈빛에는 어찌나 역겹고, 어찌나 무례하고, 어찌나 진저리 나는 무언가가 서려 있었던지, 그녀는 한 마디도 더듬더듬 내뱉을 힘조차 없이 벌떡 일어나 방에서 뛰쳐나가고 말았어. 그녀는 위층에서 혼자가 될 때까지 안간힘을 다해 눈물을 참았어. 그러고는 비로소 터뜨렸지."]], [["‘Capital!’ said Sir Mulberry Hawk, putting the stakes in his pocket.", "\"훌륭해!\" 멀베리 호크 경이 판돈을 주머니에 챙기며 말했어."]], [["‘That’s a girl of spirit, and we’ll drink her health.’", "\"기개 있는 처녀로군, 저 아가씨의 건강을 위해 한잔하지.\""]], [["It is needless to say, that Pyke and Co.", "파이크 일당이 이 제안에 무척 후끈한 태도로 호응했다는 건, 또 그 건배가 멀베리 경의 정복이 얼마나 완벽한지에 대한 그 패거리의 자잘한 빈정거림과 함께 이루어졌다는 건 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠지."], ["responded, with great warmth of manner, to this proposal, or that the toast was drunk with many little insinuations from the firm, relative to the completeness of Sir Mulberry’s conquest.", "랄프는 다른 손님들의 주의가 앞 장면의 주역들에게 쏠려 있는 동안, 그들을 늑대처럼 노려보고 있었어. 그런데 조카가 가 버리자 이제야 한결 편하게 숨 쉬는 것 같았지."], ["Ralph, who, while the attention of the other guests was attracted to the principals in the preceding scene, had eyed them like a wolf, appeared to breathe more freely now his niece was gone; the decanters passing quickly round, he leaned back in his chair, and turned his eyes from speaker to speaker, as they warmed with wine, with looks that seemed to search their hearts, and lay bare, for his distempered sport, every idle thought within them.", "술병들이 빠르게 돌고 도는 가운데, 그는 의자에 등을 기댄 채, 술기운이 오르는 사람들을 하나하나 차례로 눈길로 좇았어. 마치 그들의 속내를 헤집어, 그 비뚤어진 재미를 위해 그 안에 든 부질없는 생각이란 생각을 다 까발리려는 듯한 눈빛으로 말이야."]], [["Meanwhile Kate, left wholly to herself, had, in some degree, recovered her composure.", "그러는 동안 케이트는 완전히 혼자 남겨진 채 어느 정도 평정을 되찾았어."], ["She had learnt from a female attendant, that her uncle wished to see her before she left, and had also gleaned the satisfactory intelligence, that the gentlemen would take coffee at table.", "하녀에게서, 삼촌이 자기가 떠나기 전에 보고 싶어 한다는 말을 들었지. 또 신사들이 식탁에서 커피를 마실 거라는 반가운 소식도 알아냈어."], ["The prospect of seeing them no more, contributed greatly to calm her agitation, and, taking up a book, she composed herself to read.", "그들을 더는 안 봐도 된다는 생각에 그녀의 동요는 한결 가라앉았지. 그래서 책을 한 권 집어 들고, 마음을 가다듬고 읽기 시작했어."]], [["She started sometimes, when the sudden opening of the dining-room door let loose a wild shout of noisy revelry, and more than once rose in great alarm, as a fancied footstep on the staircase impressed her with the fear that some stray member of the party was returning alone. Nothing occurring, however, to realise her apprehensions, she endeavoured to fix her attention more closely on her book, in which by degrees she became so much interested, that she had read on through several chapters without heed of time or place, when she was terrified by suddenly hearing her name pronounced by a man’s voice close at her ear.", "식당 문이 갑자기 열려 떠들썩한 흥청거림의 사나운 함성이 쏟아져 나올 때면, 그녀는 이따금 흠칫했어. 그리고 한 번 이상 크게 놀라 일어나기도 했지. 계단에서 발소리가 들린 것 같아서, 그 패거리 중 누군가가 길을 잘못 들어 혼자 돌아오는 게 아닐까 겁이 났거든. 하지만 그녀의 우려를 현실로 만들 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았어. 그래서 그녀는 책에 좀 더 바짝 정신을 쏟으려고 애썼지. 그리고 차츰 거기에 어찌나 빠져들었던지, 시간도 장소도 잊은 채 몇 장이나 죽 읽어 내려갔어. 그러다 별안간 어떤 남자의 목소리가 귓가 바로 옆에서 자기 이름을 부르는 걸 듣고 소스라치게 놀랐지."]], [["The book fell from her hand.", "책이 손에서 떨어졌어."], ["Lounging on an ottoman close beside her, was Sir Mulberry Hawk, evidently the worse--if a man be a ruffian at heart, he is never the better--for wine.", "바로 곁의 긴 의자에 비스듬히 기대어, 멀베리 호크 경이 있었어. 누가 봐도 술에 더 추해진 모습이었지—본디 마음속이 불한당인 사람은, 술이 들어가도 결코 나아지는 법이 없으니까."]], [["‘What a delightful studiousness!’ said this accomplished gentleman.", "\"이 얼마나 사랑스러운 학구열인지!\" 이 세련된 신사가 말했어."], ["‘Was it real, now, or only to display the eyelashes?’", "\"그런데 그게 진짜였소, 아니면 그저 속눈썹이나 뽐내려던 거였소?\""]], [["Kate, looking anxiously towards the door, made no reply.", "케이트는 불안한 눈길로 문 쪽을 바라볼 뿐, 아무 대답도 하지 않았어."]], [["‘I have looked at ‘em for five minutes,’ said Sir Mulberry.", "\"내 그 속눈썹을 오 분이나 들여다봤다오.\" 멀베리 경이 말했어."], ["‘Upon my soul, they’re perfect.", "\"맹세코, 완벽하더군."], ["Why did I speak, and destroy such a pretty little picture?’", "내 어쩌자고 입을 열어 그런 어여쁜 그림을 망쳐 버렸을까?\""]], [["‘Do me the favour to be silent now, sir,’ replied Kate.", "\"부디 이제 좀 조용히 해 주세요.\" 케이트가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘No, don’t,’ said Sir Mulberry, folding his crushed hat to lay his elbow on, and bringing himself still closer to the young lady; ‘upon my life, you oughtn’t to. Such a devoted slave of yours, Miss Nickleby--it’s an infernal thing to treat him so harshly, upon my soul it is.’", "\"아니, 그러지 마시오.\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. 그러고는 자기 찌그러진 모자를 접어 팔꿈치를 괴고, 처녀에게 더더욱 바짝 다가들었지. \"맹세코, 그러시면 안 되지. 당신께 이렇게 충실한 종을 그토록 모질게 대하다니—맙소사, 그건 정말 못된 짓이오, 맹세코 그렇다오.\""]], [["‘I wish you to understand, sir,’ said Kate, trembling in spite of herself, but speaking with great indignation, ‘that your behaviour offends and disgusts me.", "\"이걸 분명히 알아 두셨으면 좋겠어요.\" 케이트가 자기도 모르게 떨면서도 무척 분개한 어조로 말했어."], ["If you have a spark of gentlemanly feeling remaining, you will leave me.’", "\"당신의 행동이 저를 불쾌하게 하고 진저리 나게 한다는 걸요. 신사다운 마음이 조금이라도 남아 있다면, 저를 내버려 두실 거예요.\""]], [["‘Now why,’ said Sir Mulberry, ‘why will you keep up this appearance of excessive rigour, my sweet creature?", "\"아니 어째서,\" 멀베리 경이 말했어."], ["Now, be more natural--my dear Miss Nickleby, be more natural--do.’", "\"어째서 자꾸 그렇게 지나치게 도도한 척하는 거요, 나의 어여쁜 그대여? 자, 좀 더 자연스럽게 굴어 보시오—나의 사랑스러운 미스 니클비, 좀 더 자연스럽게—제발 좀.\""]], [["Kate hastily rose; but as she rose, Sir Mulberry caught her dress, and forcibly detained her.", "케이트는 황급히 일어섰어. 그런데 그녀가 일어서는 순간, 멀베리 경이 그녀의 드레스를 붙잡고 억지로 붙들어 놓았지."]], [["‘Let me go, sir,’ she cried, her heart swelling with anger.", "\"놓으세요.\" 그녀가 분노에 가슴이 부풀어 오른 채 소리쳤어."], ["‘Do you hear?", "\"들리세요?"], ["Instantly--this moment.’", "당장—이 순간.\""]], [["‘Sit down, sit down,’ said Sir Mulberry; ‘I want to talk to you.’", "\"앉으시오, 앉아요.\" 멀베리 경이 말했어. \"당신과 얘기를 좀 나누고 싶소.\""]], [["‘Unhand me, sir, this instant,’ cried Kate.", "\"이 손 놓으세요, 당장요.\" 케이트가 외쳤어."]], [["‘Not for the world,’ rejoined Sir Mulberry.", "\"무슨 일이 있어도 안 되지.\" 멀베리 경이 받아쳤어."], ["Thus speaking, he leaned over, as if to replace her in her chair; but the young lady, making a violent effort to disengage herself, he lost his balance, and measured his length upon the ground.", "이렇게 말하면서 그는, 그녀를 다시 의자에 앉히려는 듯 몸을 기울였어. 그런데 처녀가 거기서 벗어나려고 격렬하게 몸부림치는 바람에, 그는 균형을 잃고 바닥에 벌렁 나자빠지고 말았지."], ["As Kate sprung forward to leave the room, Mr Ralph Nickleby appeared in the doorway, and confronted her.", "케이트가 방을 나가려고 앞으로 뛰쳐나가는 순간, 랄프 니클비 씨가 문간에 나타나 그녀와 마주 섰어."]], [["‘What is this?’ said Ralph.", "\"이게 무슨 일이냐?\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘It is this, sir,’ replied Kate, violently agitated: ‘that beneath the roof where I, a helpless girl, your dead brother’s child, should most have found protection, I have been exposed to insult which should make you shrink to look upon me.", "\"이런 일이에요, 삼촌.\" 케이트가 몹시 격앙되어 대답했어."], ["Let me pass you.’", "\"저는 삼촌의 돌아가신 동생의 자식이고, 의지할 데 없는 처녀인데, 그 어떤 지붕 아래보다도 마땅히 보호를 받아야 할 이 지붕 밑에서, 삼촌이 저를 차마 똑바로 보지도 못할 만큼 모욕을 당했다는 거예요. 비켜 주세요.\""]], [["Ralph DID shrink, as the indignant girl fixed her kindling eye upon him; but he did not comply with her injunction, nevertheless: for he led her to a distant seat, and returning, and approaching Sir Mulberry Hawk, who had by this time risen, motioned towards the door.", "랄프는 정말로 움찔했어. 분노한 처녀가 불타는 눈으로 그를 쏘아봤을 때 말이야. 하지만 그래도 그는 그녀의 명령을 따르진 않았어. 그녀를 멀찍한 자리로 데려가서 앉히고는, 다시 돌아와 그새 일어선 멀베리 호크 경에게 다가가더니, 문 쪽을 가리켰거든."]], [["‘Your way lies there, sir,’ said Ralph, in a suppressed voice, that some devil might have owned with pride.", "\"당신이 갈 길은 저쪽이오.\" 랄프가 억누른 목소리로 말했어. 어떤 악마라도 자랑스러워했을 법한 목소리로 말이야."]], [["‘What do you mean by that?’ demanded his friend, fiercely.", "\"그게 무슨 소린가?\" 그의 친구가 사납게 따져 물었어."]], [["The swoln veins stood out like sinews on Ralph’s wrinkled forehead, and the nerves about his mouth worked as though some unendurable emotion wrung them; but he smiled disdainfully, and again pointed to the door.", "랄프의 주름진 이마에는 부푼 핏줄이 힘줄처럼 도드라졌고, 입가의 신경은 무슨 견딜 수 없는 감정에 쥐어짜이기라도 하는 듯 꿈틀거렸어. 하지만 그는 경멸하듯 미소 짓고는, 다시 문을 가리켰지."]], [["‘Do you know me, you old madman?’ asked Sir Mulberry.", "\"내가 누군지 알기나 하나, 이 늙은 미치광이야?\" 멀베리 경이 물었어."]], [["‘Well,’ said Ralph.", "\"잘 알지.\""], ["The fashionable vagabond for the moment quite quailed under the steady look of the older sinner, and walked towards the door, muttering as he went.", "랄프가 말했어. 이 한량은 한순간 그 더 늙은 죄인의 흔들림 없는 눈빛에 완전히 기가 꺾여, 가는 길에 뭐라 중얼거리며 문 쪽으로 걸어갔어."]], [["‘You wanted the lord, did you?’ he said, stopping short when he reached the door, as if a new light had broken in upon him, and confronting Ralph again.", "\"자넨 그 경을 노린 거였군, 안 그래?\" 그가 문에 다다르자 별안간 우뚝 멈춰 서며 말했어."], ["‘Damme, I was in the way, was I?’", "마치 무슨 새로운 깨달음이 머릿속에 번뜩 비친 듯, 다시 랄프와 마주 섰지. \"빌어먹을, 내가 거치적거렸던 거로군, 그렇지?\""]], [["Ralph smiled again, but made no answer.", "랄프는 다시 미소 지었지만, 아무 대답도 하지 않았어."]], [["‘Who brought him to you first?’ pursued Sir Mulberry; ‘and how, without me, could you ever have wound him in your net as you have?’", "\"그 경을 자네한테 맨 처음 데려온 게 누군가?\" 멀베리 경이 다그쳤어. \"그리고 내가 없었다면, 자네가 어떻게 지금처럼 그를 자네 그물에 옭아 넣을 수 있었겠나?\""]], [["‘The net is a large one, and rather full,’ said Ralph.", "\"그물은 크고, 꽤 가득 찼지.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘Take care that it chokes nobody in the meshes.’", "\"그 그물코에 누가 목 졸리는 일이나 없게 조심하게.\""]], [["‘You would sell your flesh and blood for money; yourself, if you have not already made a bargain with the devil,’ retorted the other.", "\"자넨 돈을 위해서라면 자기 피붙이도 팔아넘길 위인이지. 이미 악마와 거래를 맺지 않았다면야 제 몸뚱이라도 팔아넘길 거고.\""], ["‘Do you mean to tell me that your pretty niece was not brought here as a decoy for the drunken boy downstairs?’", "상대가 받아쳤어. \"자네 그 어여쁜 조카가 아래층의 그 취한 애송이를 꾀는 미끼로 이리 끌려온 게 아니라고 우길 셈인가?\""]], [["Although this hurried dialogue was carried on in a suppressed tone on both sides, Ralph looked involuntarily round to ascertain that Kate had not moved her position so as to be within hearing.", "이 다급한 대화는 양쪽 다 억누른 목소리로 이어지고 있었지만, 랄프는 저도 모르게 주위를 둘러봤어. 케이트가 자리를 옮겨 들리는 데까지 와 있지는 않은지 확인하려고 말이야."], ["His adversary saw the advantage he had gained, and followed it up.", "그의 적수는 자기가 잡은 유리한 고지를 알아채고, 거기에 한층 밀어붙였지."]], [["‘Do you mean to tell me,’ he asked again, ‘that it is not so?", "\"이게 그렇지 않다고 우길 셈이냐고 묻는 거야.\" 그가 다시 물었어."], ["Do you mean to say that if he had found his way up here instead of me, you wouldn’t have been a little more blind, and a little more deaf, and a little less flourishing, than you have been?", "\"만약 그 경이 나 대신 여기 위로 길을 찾아 올라왔더라면, 자네가 지금보다 좀 더 눈감아 주고, 좀 더 못 들은 척하고, 좀 덜 기세등등했을 거라는 걸 부인하겠다는 거냐고."], ["Come, Nickleby, answer me that.’", "자, 니클비, 그것 좀 대답해 보게.\""]], [["‘I tell you this,’ replied Ralph, ‘that if I brought her here, as a matter of business--’", "\"내 자네한테 이건 말해 두지.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"내가 사업상의 일로 저 애를 이리 데려왔다면—\""]], [["‘Ay, that’s the word,’ interposed Sir Mulberry, with a laugh.", "\"그래, 바로 그 말이야.\" 멀베리 경이 웃으며 끼어들었어."], ["‘You’re coming to yourself again now.’", "\"이제야 자네 본모습으로 돌아오는군.\""]], [["‘--As a matter of business,’ pursued Ralph, speaking slowly and firmly, as a man who has made up his mind to say no more, ‘because I thought she might make some impression on the silly youth you have taken in hand and are lending good help to ruin, I knew--knowing him--that it would be long before he outraged her girl’s feelings, and that unless he offended by mere puppyism and emptiness, he would, with a little management, respect the sex and conduct even of his usurer’s niece. But if I thought to draw him on more gently by this device, I did not think of subjecting the girl to the licentiousness and brutality of so old a hand as you. And now we understand each other.’", "\"—사업상의 일로 말이야.\" 랄프가 더는 입을 열지 않기로 마음먹은 사람처럼, 느리고 단호하게 말을 이었어. \"자네가 손아귀에 넣고 거덜 내는 데 톡톡히 손을 보태고 있는 그 멍청한 애송이한테 저 애가 무슨 인상이라도 남길까 싶어서였지. 나는 알았어—그 애송이를 아니까—그가 저 애의 처녀다운 마음을 짓밟기까지는 한참 걸릴 거라는 걸. 그리고 그가 그저 풋내기다운 어리석음과 알맹이 없는 짓으로 저 애를 거스르지만 않는다면, 조금만 다루기 나름으로, 제 고리대금업자의 조카라 해도 그 여인의 정숙함과 행실만큼은 존중하리라는 걸 말이야. 그런데 내가 이 수법으로 그를 좀 더 슬슬 끌어들이려 했을지언정, 저 애를 자네 같은 닳고 닳은 인간의 음탕함과 야만에 내맡길 생각은 없었어. 자, 이제 우리 서로 알 만큼 알았군.\""]], [["‘Especially as there was nothing to be got by it--eh?’ sneered Sir Mulberry.", "\"특히나 그래 봤자 얻을 게 아무것도 없었으니까—그렇지?\" 멀베리 경이 비웃었어."]], [["‘Exactly so,’ said Ralph.", "\"바로 그렇지.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["He had turned away, and looked over his shoulder to make this last reply.", "그는 몸을 돌린 채, 어깨너머로 이 마지막 대꾸를 던졌어. 두 잘난 자의 눈이 마주쳤지."], ["The eyes of the two worthies met, with an expression as if each rascal felt that there was no disguising himself from the other; and Sir Mulberry Hawk shrugged his shoulders and walked slowly out.", "마치 두 악당이 저마다, 상대 앞에서는 제 본색을 감출 길이 없다는 걸 느끼는 듯한 표정으로 말이야. 그러고는 멀베리 호크 경이 어깨를 으쓱하고는 느릿느릿 걸어 나갔어."]], [["His friend closed the door, and looked restlessly towards the spot where his niece still remained in the attitude in which he had left her.", "그의 친구는 문을 닫고, 조카가 아직도 자기가 두고 간 그 자세 그대로 있는 자리 쪽을 불안하게 바라봤어."], ["She had flung herself heavily upon the couch, and with her head drooping over the cushion, and her face hidden in her hands, seemed to be still weeping in an agony of shame and grief.", "그녀는 긴 의자에 무겁게 몸을 내던진 채, 머리를 쿠션 위로 떨구고 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻고 있었지. 여전히 수치심과 비탄의 고통 속에서 울고 있는 것 같았어."]], [["Ralph would have walked into any poverty-stricken debtor’s house, and pointed him out to a bailiff, though in attendance upon a young child’s death-bed, without the smallest concern, because it would have been a matter quite in the ordinary course of business, and the man would have been an offender against his only code of morality.", "랄프라면, 어떤 가난에 찌든 채무자의 집에 걸어 들어가, 그 사람이 어린 자식의 임종을 지키고 있다 한들, 그를 집달리에게 가리켜 보이고도 눈곱만큼도 개의치 않았을 거야. 그건 그저 지극히 흔한 사업상의 일이고, 그 사람은 그가 가진 유일한 도덕률을 어긴 죄인일 테니까."], ["But, here was a young girl, who had done no wrong save that of coming into the world alive; who had patiently yielded to all his wishes; who had tried hard to please him--above all, who didn’t owe him money--and he felt awkward and nervous.", "하지만 여기 한 어린 처녀가 있었어. 살아서 세상에 태어난 것 말고는 아무 잘못도 저지르지 않은, 그의 온갖 바람에 묵묵히 따라 준, 그를 기쁘게 하려고 무던히도 애쓴—무엇보다도, 그에게 빚진 돈이 없는 처녀가 말이야—그래서 그는 어색하고 불안했어."]], [["Ralph took a chair at some distance; then, another chair a little nearer; then, moved a little nearer still; then, nearer again, and finally sat himself on the same sofa, and laid his hand on Kate’s arm.", "랄프는 좀 떨어진 곳에 의자를 하나 가져다 놓았어. 그러더니 또 다른 의자를 조금 더 가까이 가져다 놓았지. 그러고는 조금 더 가까이 다가갔고, 그다음엔 또 조금 더 다가갔어. 그러더니 마침내 같은 소파에 앉아, 케이트의 팔에 손을 얹었지."]], [["‘Hush, my dear!’ he said, as she drew it back, and her sobs burst out afresh.", "\"쉿, 얘야!\" 그녀가 팔을 거두며 다시 흐느낌을 터뜨리자 그가 말했어."], ["‘Hush, hush!", "\"쉿, 쉿!"], ["Don’t mind it, now; don’t think of it.’", "이젠 신경 쓰지 말거라. 그 일은 생각도 하지 마.\""]], [["‘Oh, for pity’s sake, let me go home,’ cried Kate.", "\"오, 제발 부탁이에요, 집에 가게 해 주세요.\" 케이트가 외쳤어."], ["‘Let me leave this house, and go home.’", "\"이 집을 떠나서, 집에 가게 해 주세요.\""]], [["‘Yes, yes,’ said Ralph.", "\"그래, 그래.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘You shall.", "\"그러렴."], ["But you must dry your eyes first, and compose yourself.", "하지만 먼저 눈물부터 닦고 마음을 좀 가라앉혀야지."], ["Let me raise your head.", "머리 좀 들어 보렴."], ["There--there.’", "자—옳지.\""]], [["‘Oh, uncle!’ exclaimed Kate, clasping her hands.", "\"오, 삼촌!\" 케이트가 두 손을 맞잡으며 부르짖었어."], ["‘What have I done--what have I done--that you should subject me to this?", "\"제가 무슨 짓을 했기에—제가 무슨 짓을 했기에—저를 이런 일에 내모신 거예요? 제가 생각으로든, 말로든, 행동으로든 삼촌께 잘못을 저질렀다면, 그래도 이건 저한테 너무나 모진 일이었을 거예요."], ["If I had wronged you in thought, or word, or deed, it would have been most cruel to me, and the memory of one you must have loved in some old time; but--’", "또 삼촌이 옛 시절 어느 땐가 사랑하셨을 그 사람의 기억에도요. 하지만—\""]], [["‘Only listen to me for a moment,’ interrupted Ralph, seriously alarmed by the violence of her emotions.", "\"잠깐만 내 말을 들어 보렴.\" 랄프가 끼어들었어."], ["‘I didn’t know it would be so; it was impossible for me to foresee it.", "그녀의 격한 감정에 정말로 겁이 났던 거지. \"나는 이렇게 될 줄 몰랐다."], ["I did all I could.--Come, let us walk about.", "내가 이걸 미리 내다보기란 불가능했어."], ["You are faint with the closeness of the room, and the heat of these lamps.", "나는 할 수 있는 건 다 했단다.—자, 우리 좀 걸어 보자꾸나. 방이 답답하고 이 등불들이 뜨거워서 네가 어지러운 거야."], ["You will be better now, if you make the slightest effort.’", "아주 조금만 기운을 내면 한결 나아질 게다.\""]], [["‘I will do anything,’ replied Kate, ‘if you will only send me home.’", "\"무엇이든 하겠어요.\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"저를 집에만 보내 주신다면요.\""]], [["‘Well, well, I will,’ said Ralph; ‘but you must get back your own looks; for those you have, will frighten them, and nobody must know of this but you and I.", "\"그래, 그래, 보내 주마.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["Now let us walk the other way.", "\"하지만 네 본래 얼굴빛을 되찾아야 한다. 지금 그 얼굴로는 그 사람들이 놀랄 테고, 이 일은 너와 나 말고는 아무도 몰라야 하니까."], ["There.", "자, 이번엔 반대쪽으로 걸어 보자."], ["You look better even now.’", "옳지. 지금도 한결 나아 보이는구나.\""]], [["With such encouragements as these, Ralph Nickleby walked to and fro, with his niece leaning on his arm; actually trembling beneath her touch.", "이런 식으로 다독이며, 랄프 니클비는 조카를 자기 팔에 기대게 한 채 이리저리 거닐었어. 그녀의 손길 아래서 정말로 떨면서 말이야."]], [["In the same manner, when he judged it prudent to allow her to depart, he supported her downstairs, after adjusting her shawl and performing such little offices, most probably for the first time in his life.", "마찬가지로, 이제 그녀를 보내도 되겠다 싶었을 때, 그는 그녀의 숄을 매만져 주고 그런 자잘한 시중을 들어 준 뒤—아마 평생 처음이었을 거야—그녀를 부축해 아래층으로 내려갔어."], ["Across the hall, and down the steps, Ralph led her too; nor did he withdraw his hand until she was seated in the coach.", "현관을 가로질러, 계단을 내려가는 동안에도 랄프는 그녀를 이끌었지. 그리고 그녀가 마차에 앉을 때까지 손을 거두지 않았어."]], [["As the door of the vehicle was roughly closed, a comb fell from Kate’s hair, close at her uncle’s feet; and as he picked it up, and returned it into her hand, the light from a neighbouring lamp shone upon her face. The lock of hair that had escaped and curled loosely over her brow, the traces of tears yet scarcely dry, the flushed cheek, the look of sorrow, all fired some dormant train of recollection in the old man’s breast; and the face of his dead brother seemed present before him, with the very look it bore on some occasion of boyish grief, of which every minutest circumstance flashed upon his mind, with the distinctness of a scene of yesterday.", "마차 문이 거칠게 닫힐 때, 케이트의 머리에서 빗 하나가 떨어졌어. 그녀의 삼촌 발치 바로 옆에 말이야. 그리고 그가 그걸 주워 그녀의 손에 돌려주는데, 옆에 있던 가로등 불빛이 그녀의 얼굴을 비췄지. 흘러내려 이마 위로 느슨하게 곱슬진 머리채, 아직 채 마르지도 않은 눈물 자국, 발그레한 뺨, 슬픔에 잠긴 표정—이 모든 게 그 노인의 가슴속에 잠들어 있던 어떤 기억의 실타래에 불을 댕겼어. 그리고 죽은 동생의 얼굴이 그의 눈앞에 떠오르는 것 같았지. 어릴 적 무언가 슬픈 일이 있었을 때 짓던 바로 그 표정 그대로 말이야. 그때의 자질구레한 정황 하나하나가, 마치 어제 일처럼 또렷하게 그의 머릿속에 번뜩 떠올랐어."]], [["Ralph Nickleby, who was proof against all appeals of blood and kindred--who was steeled against every tale of sorrow and distress--staggered while he looked, and went back into his house, as a man who had seen a spirit from some world beyond the grave.", "피붙이와 혈육의 그 어떤 호소에도 끄떡없던 랄프 니클비, 슬픔과 고난의 그 어떤 사연에도 강철처럼 단단하던 그가, 그 얼굴을 바라보며 휘청거렸어. 그러고는 무덤 너머 어느 세상에서 온 망령이라도 본 사람처럼, 자기 집 안으로 되돌아 들어갔지."]], [["# CHAPTER 20", "# CHAPTER 20"]], [["Wherein Nicholas at length encounters his Uncle, to whom he expresses his Sentiments with much Candour.", "라 크리비 양은 월요일 이른 아침, 그러니까 만찬이 있었던 다음 날, 마을 서쪽 끝의 여러 거리를 종종걸음으로 바삐 가로질러 갔어."], ["His Resolution.", "아주 중요한 심부름을 맡았거든."], ["Little Miss La Creevy trotted briskly through divers streets at the west end of the town, early on Monday morning--the day after the dinner--charged with the important commission of acquainting Madame Mantalini that Miss Nickleby was too unwell to attend that day, but hoped to be enabled to resume her duties on the morrow.", "니클비 양이 오늘은 몸이 너무 안 좋아서 일을 나갈 수 없지만 내일은 다시 일을 시작할 수 있길 바란다는 말을, 마담 만탈리니한테 전하는 일이었지. 라 크리비 양은 걸어가면서 머릿속으로 점잖고 우아한 표현들을 이리저리 굴려 봤어."], ["And as Miss La Creevy walked along, revolving in her mind various genteel forms and elegant turns of expression, with a view to the selection of the very best in which to couch her communication, she cogitated a good deal upon the probable causes of her young friend’s indisposition.", "그 가운데 가장 멋진 말로 소식을 전하려고 말이야. 그러면서 어린 친구가 왜 몸져누웠을까, 그 까닭에 대해서도 한참을 곰곰이 생각했어."]], [["‘I don’t know what to make of it,’ said Miss La Creevy.", "\"도무지 모르겠단 말이야.\" 라 크리비 양이 중얼거렸어."], ["‘Her eyes were decidedly red last night.", "\"어젯밤에 분명히 눈이 빨갰거든."], ["She said she had a headache; headaches don’t occasion red eyes.", "두통이 있다고 했지만, 두통 때문에 눈이 빨개지진 않잖아."], ["She must have been crying.’", "분명히 울었던 거야.\""]], [["Arriving at this conclusion, which, indeed, she had established to her perfect satisfaction on the previous evening, Miss La Creevy went on to consider--as she had done nearly all night--what new cause of unhappiness her young friend could possibly have had.", "이 결론에 다다른 라 크리비 양은 — 사실 어젯밤에 이미 스스로 흡족할 만큼 확신해 둔 결론이었지 — 계속 생각을 이어 갔어. 거의 밤새 그랬던 것처럼 말이야. 어린 친구한테 또 무슨 새로운 슬픔거리가 생겼을까 하고 말이지."]], [["‘I can’t think of anything,’ said the little portrait painter.", "\"아무리 생각해도 모르겠어.\" 자그마한 초상화가가 말했어."], ["‘Nothing at all, unless it was the behaviour of that old bear.", "\"전혀 모르겠다고. 그 늙은 곰탱이가 못되게 굴었다면 모를까."], ["Cross to her, I suppose?", "케이트한테 심술을 부렸나 봐."], ["Unpleasant brute!’", "못된 짐승 같으니!\""]], [["Relieved by this expression of opinion, albeit it was vented upon empty air, Miss La Creevy trotted on to Madame Mantalini’s; and being informed that the governing power was not yet out of bed, requested an interview with the second in command; whereupon Miss Knag appeared.", "이렇게 자기 생각을 입 밖에 내고 나니 한결 마음이 가벼워졌어. 비록 허공에 대고 한 말이긴 했지만 말이야. 라 크리비 양은 종종걸음으로 마담 만탈리니의 가게에 닿았어. 그런데 우두머리는 아직 침대에서 일어나지 않았다는 거야. 그래서 이인자를 만나게 해 달라고 했지. 그랬더니 내그 양이 나타났어."]], [["‘So far as I am concerned,’ said Miss Knag, when the message had been delivered, with many ornaments of speech; ‘I could spare Miss Nickleby for evermore.’", "\"제 입장에서 말씀드리자면,\" 라 크리비 양이 잔뜩 꾸민 말투로 용건을 전하자 내그 양이 말했어. \"니클비 양 없이도 저는 얼마든지 잘 지낼 수 있답니다.\""]], [["‘Oh, indeed, ma’am!’ rejoined Miss La Creevy, highly offended.", "\"오, 그러시군요, 부인!\" 라 크리비 양이 잔뜩 기분이 상해 받아쳤어."], ["‘But, you see, you are not mistress of the business, and therefore it’s of no great consequence.’", "\"하지만 보시다시피, 부인이 이 가게 주인은 아니잖아요. 그러니 별로 중요한 말은 아니네요.\""]], [["‘Very good, ma’am,’ said Miss Knag.", "\"아주 좋습니다, 부인.\" 내그 양이 말했어."], ["‘Have you any further commands for me?’", "\"제게 더 하실 분부라도 있으신가요?\""]], [["‘No, I have not, ma’am,’ rejoined Miss La Creevy.", "\"아니요, 없어요, 부인.\" 라 크리비 양이 받아쳤어."]], [["‘Then good-morning, ma’am,’ said Miss Knag.", "\"그럼 안녕히 가세요, 부인.\" 내그 양이 말했어."]], [["‘Good-morning to you, ma’am; and many obligations for your extreme politeness and good breeding,’ rejoined Miss La Creevy.", "\"안녕히 계세요, 부인. 그리고 그 지극한 예의와 훌륭한 교양에 깊이 감사드려요.\" 라 크리비 양이 되받아쳤어."]], [["Thus terminating the interview, during which both ladies had trembled very much, and been marvellously polite--certain indications that they were within an inch of a very desperate quarrel--Miss La Creevy bounced out of the room, and into the street.", "이렇게 해서 만남이 끝났어. 그동안 두 부인은 부들부들 떨면서도 놀랄 만큼 예의를 차렸지. 이건 두 사람이 아주 끔찍한 싸움 직전에 와 있다는 분명한 신호였어. 라 크리비 양은 방을 박차고 나와 거리로 튀어나갔지."]], [["‘I wonder who that is,’ said the queer little soul.", "\"저 여자는 대체 누굴까.\" 별난 자그마한 사람이 중얼거렸어."], ["‘A nice person to know, I should think!", "\"알고 지내면 참 좋은 사람이겠네! 내가 저 여자 초상화를 그리게 됐으면 좋겠어."], ["I wish I had the painting of her: I’D do her justice.’ So, feeling quite satisfied that she had said a very cutting thing at Miss Knag’s expense, Miss La Creevy had a hearty laugh, and went home to breakfast in great good humour.", "아주 똑같이 그려 줄 텐데.\" 그러고는 내그 양을 두고 아주 톡 쏘는 말을 했다는 생각에 마냥 흐뭇해진 라 크리비 양은, 한바탕 신나게 웃고는 무척 기분 좋게 아침을 먹으러 집으로 돌아갔어."]], [["Here was one of the advantages of having lived alone so long!", "이런 게 바로 오래 혼자 살아서 좋은 점 가운데 하나였지! 부산스럽고 활기차고 명랑한 이 자그마한 사람은 온전히 자기 안에서 살았어."], ["The little bustling, active, cheerful creature existed entirely within herself, talked to herself, made a confidante of herself, was as sarcastic as she could be, on people who offended her, by herself; pleased herself, and did no harm.", "혼자 말하고, 혼자한테 속을 털어놓고, 자기 기분을 상하게 한 사람들한테 혼자서 할 수 있는 만큼 빈정대고, 혼자 흡족해하면서도 아무한테도 해를 끼치지 않았지. 흉을 봐도 누구의 평판이 깎이는 일이 없었고, 작은 복수를 즐겨도 세상 그 누구도 털끝만큼 손해 볼 일이 없었어."], ["If she indulged in scandal, nobody’s reputation suffered; and if she enjoyed a little bit of revenge, no living soul was one atom the worse.", "형편이 빠듯한 탓에 바라던 사람들과 어울리지 못하고, 어울릴 수 있는 사람들과는 섞이고 싶지 않은 — 그런 많은 사람들 가운데 하나였던 거지. 그런 사람들한테 런던은 시리아 들판만큼이나 완전히 외로운 곳이야."], ["One of the many to whom, from straitened circumstances, a consequent inability to form the associations they would wish, and a disinclination to mix with the society they could obtain, London is as complete a solitude as the plains of Syria, the humble artist had pursued her lonely, but contented way for many years; and, until the peculiar misfortunes of the Nickleby family attracted her attention, had made no friends, though brimful of the friendliest feelings to all mankind.", "이 소박한 화가는 외롭지만 만족스러운 길을 여러 해 동안 걸어왔어. 그리고 니클비 가족의 별난 불운이 눈길을 끌기 전까지는, 온 인류를 향한 다정한 마음으로 가슴이 가득 차 있었으면서도 친구 하나 사귀지 못했지."], ["There are many warm hearts in the same solitary guise as poor little Miss La Creevy’s.", "가엾고 자그마한 라 크리비 양처럼 외로운 모습으로 사는 따뜻한 가슴이 세상엔 참 많아."]], [["However, that’s neither here nor there, just now. She went home to breakfast, and had scarcely caught the full flavour of her first sip of tea, when the servant announced a gentleman, whereat Miss La Creevy, at once imagining a new sitter transfixed by admiration at the street-door case, was in unspeakable consternation at the presence of the tea-things.", "하지만 그건 지금 여기서 할 얘기는 아니야. 라 크리비 양은 아침을 먹으러 집에 돌아갔어. 그런데 첫 모금 차의 향을 채 음미하기도 전에, 하녀가 한 신사가 왔다고 알렸어. 라 크리비 양은 곧장 새 손님이 길가에 내놓은 진열장에 감탄해서 넋을 잃고 서 있는 거라고 짐작했지. 그래서 찻잔들이 놓여 있는 걸 보고는 말할 수 없이 당황했어."]], [["‘Here, take ‘em away; run with ‘em into the bedroom; anywhere,’ said Miss La Creevy. ‘Dear, dear; to think that I should be late on this particular morning, of all others, after being ready for three weeks by half-past eight o’clock, and not a soul coming near the place!’", "\"자, 이것들 좀 치워 줘. 침실로 얼른 가져가. 어디로든.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"어머, 어머나. 하필이면 오늘 아침에 늦다니, 다른 날도 많은데. 삼 주 동안이나 여덟 시 반이면 채비를 다 갖추고 있었는데도 코빼기 하나 안 비치더니!\""]], [["‘Don’t let me put you out of the way,’ said a voice Miss La Creevy knew.", "\"저 때문에 번거롭게 마세요.\" 라 크리비 양이 아는 목소리가 말했어."], ["‘I told the servant not to mention my name, because I wished to surprise you.’", "\"하녀한테 제 이름은 말하지 말라고 했어요. 깜짝 놀라게 해 드리고 싶어서요.\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"니콜라스 도련님!\""], ["Nicholas!’ cried Miss La Creevy, starting in great astonishment.", "라 크리비 양이 깜짝 놀라며 소리쳤어."], ["‘You have not forgotten me, I see,’ replied Nicholas, extending his hand.", "\"절 잊지 않으셨군요.\" 니콜라스가 손을 내밀며 말했어."]], [["‘Why, I think I should even have known you if I had met you in the street,’ said Miss La Creevy, with a smile.", "\"어머, 길에서 마주쳤어도 알아봤을 것 같은데요.\" 라 크리비 양이 미소 지으며 말했어."], ["‘Hannah, another cup and saucer.", "\"한나, 잔하고 받침 하나 더 가져와."], ["Now, I’ll tell you what, young man; I’ll trouble you not to repeat the impertinence you were guilty of, on the morning you went away.’", "그런데 말이에요, 젊은 양반. 떠나던 날 아침에 저지른 그 무례한 짓, 다시는 하지 말아 줬으면 좋겠네요.\""]], [["‘You would not be very angry, would you?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"그렇게 많이 화내진 않으실 거죠?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘Wouldn’t I!’ said Miss La Creevy.", "\"안 낼 것 같아요!\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어."], ["‘You had better try; that’s all!’", "\"한번 해 보든가. 그게 다예요!\""]], [["Nicholas, with becoming gallantry, immediately took Miss La Creevy at her word, who uttered a faint scream and slapped his face; but it was not a very hard slap, and that’s the truth.", "니콜라스는 신사다운 멋을 부리며 곧장 라 크리비 양의 말대로 했어. 라 크리비 양은 가느다란 비명을 지르고는 그의 뺨을 찰싹 때렸지. 하지만 그렇게 세게 때린 건 아니었어. 그게 사실이야."]], [["‘I never saw such a rude creature!’ exclaimed Miss La Creevy.", "\"이렇게 버릇없는 사람은 처음 봐!\" 라 크리비 양이 외쳤어."]], [["‘You told me to try,’ said Nicholas.", "\"한번 해 보라고 하셨잖아요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Well; but I was speaking ironically,’ rejoined Miss La Creevy.", "\"그야, 그건 비꼬는 말이었지요.\" 라 크리비 양이 받아쳤어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오! 그건 또 다른 얘기네요.\""], ["that’s another thing,’ said Nicholas; ‘you should have told me that, too.’", "니콜라스가 말했어. \"그것도 미리 말씀해 주셨어야죠.\""]], [["‘I dare say you didn’t know, indeed!’ retorted Miss La Creevy.", "\"정말이지 몰랐다는 거예요!\" 라 크리비 양이 쏘아붙였어."], ["‘But, now I look at you again, you seem thinner than when I saw you last, and your face is haggard and pale.", "\"그건 그렇고, 다시 보니 지난번 봤을 때보다 야위었네요. 얼굴도 핼쑥하고 창백하고요."], ["And how come you to have left Yorkshire?’", "그런데 요크셔는 어쩌다 떠나게 됐어요?\""]], [["She stopped here; for there was so much heart in her altered tone and manner, that Nicholas was quite moved.", "라 크리비 양은 여기서 말을 멈췄어. 달라진 그 말투와 태도에 어찌나 진심이 담겨 있었던지, 니콜라스가 그만 마음이 뭉클해졌거든."]], [["‘I need look somewhat changed,’ he said, after a short silence; ‘for I have undergone some suffering, both of mind and body, since I left London.", "\"많이 변해 보일 만도 하죠.\" 니콜라스가 잠시 말이 없다가 입을 열었어."], ["I have been very poor, too, and have even suffered from want.’", "\"런던을 떠난 뒤로 마음으로나 몸으로나 고생을 좀 했거든요. 무척 가난하기도 했고, 굶주려 본 적도 있어요.\""]], [["‘Good Heaven, Mr.", "\"세상에, 니콜라스 도련님!\""], ["Nicholas!’ exclaimed Miss La Creevy, ‘what are you telling me?’", "라 크리비 양이 외쳤어. \"지금 무슨 말을 하는 거예요?\""]], [["‘Nothing which need distress you quite so much,’ answered Nicholas, with a more sprightly air; ‘neither did I come here to bewail my lot, but on matter more to the purpose.", "\"그렇게까지 마음 아파하실 일은 아니에요.\" 니콜라스가 한결 밝은 표정으로 대답했어."], ["I wish to meet my uncle face to face.", "\"제 신세를 한탄하러 온 것도 아니고요. 더 중요한 일로 왔어요."], ["I should tell you that first.’", "삼촌과 얼굴을 마주하고 만나고 싶어요. 그 말씀부터 드려야겠네요.\""]], [["‘Then all I have to say about that is,’ interposed Miss La Creevy, ‘that I don’t envy you your taste; and that sitting in the same room with his very boots, would put me out of humour for a fortnight.’", "\"그 일에 대해 제가 할 말은 이거 하나예요.\" 라 크리비 양이 끼어들었어. \"그런 취향은 부럽지가 않네요. 그 사람 장화하고 같은 방에 앉아 있는 것만으로도 저는 보름 동안 기분이 잡칠 거예요.\""]], [["‘In the main,’ said Nicholas, ‘there may be no great difference of opinion between you and me, so far; but you will understand, that I desire to confront him, to justify myself, and to cast his duplicity and malice in his throat.’", "\"대체로는,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"거기까진 도련님과 제 생각이 크게 다르지 않을 거예요. 하지만 이해해 주세요. 저는 삼촌과 맞서서 제 결백을 밝히고, 그 두 얼굴과 악의를 그 목구멍에 도로 처넣어 주고 싶어요.\""]], [["‘That’s quite another matter,’ rejoined Miss La Creevy.", "\"그건 완전히 다른 얘기네요.\" 라 크리비 양이 받아쳤어."], ["‘Heaven forgive me; but I shouldn’t cry my eyes quite out of my head, if they choked him.", "\"하늘이 절 용서하시길. 하지만 누가 그 사람 목을 졸라 버린다 해도, 저는 눈물 한 방울 흘리지 않을 거예요."], ["Well?’", "그래서요?\""]], [["‘To this end, I called upon him this morning,’ said Nicholas.", "\"그래서 오늘 아침에 삼촌을 찾아갔어요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘He only returned to town on Saturday, and I knew nothing of his arrival until late last night.’", "\"삼촌은 토요일에야 시내로 돌아왔는데, 저는 어젯밤 늦게야 그 소식을 알았거든요.\""]], [["‘And did you see him?’ asked Miss La Creevy.", "\"그래서 만났어요?\" 라 크리비 양이 물었어."]], [["‘No,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"아뇨.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘He had gone out.’", "\"외출하고 없었어요.\""]], [["‘Hah!’ said Miss La Creevy; ‘on some kind, charitable business, I dare say.’", "\"하!\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"무슨 친절한 자선 사업이라도 하러 갔겠죠, 아마.\""]], [["‘I have reason to believe,’ pursued Nicholas, ‘from what has been told me, by a friend of mine who is acquainted with his movements, that he intends seeing my mother and sister today, and giving them his version of the occurrences that have befallen me.", "\"제 생각엔,\" 니콜라스가 말을 이었어."], ["I will meet him there.’", "\"삼촌의 움직임을 잘 아는 제 친구한테 들은 바로는, 오늘 어머니와 누이를 만나서 제게 일어난 일들을 자기 식대로 들려줄 작정인 것 같아요. 거기서 삼촌을 만나려고요.\""]], [["‘That’s right,’ said Miss La Creevy, rubbing her hands. ‘And yet, I don’t know,’ she added, ‘there is much to be thought of--others to be considered.’", "\"잘 생각했어요.\" 라 크리비 양이 두 손을 비비며 말했어. \"그렇긴 한데, 글쎄요.\" 그러고는 덧붙였어. \"생각할 게 많아요. 다른 사람들도 고려해야 하고요.\""]], [["‘I have considered others,’ rejoined Nicholas; ‘but as honesty and honour are both at issue, nothing shall deter me.’", "\"다른 사람들도 생각해 봤어요.\" 니콜라스가 받아쳤어. \"하지만 정직과 명예가 둘 다 걸린 문제니까, 그 무엇도 절 막을 수 없어요.\""]], [["‘You should know best,’ said Miss La Creevy.", "\"그건 도련님이 제일 잘 알겠죠.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어."]], [["‘In this case I hope so,’ answered Nicholas.", "\"이 일만큼은 그러길 바라요.\""], ["‘And all I want you to do for me, is, to prepare them for my coming.", "니콜라스가 대답했어. \"제가 부탁드리고 싶은 건 딱 하나예요."], ["They think me a long way off, and if I went wholly unexpected, I should frighten them.", "제가 간다는 걸 미리 식구들한테 알려 주세요. 식구들은 제가 멀리 있는 줄 알거든요."], ["If you can spare time to tell them that you have seen me, and that I shall be with them in a quarter of an hour afterwards, you will do me a great service.’", "아무 예고도 없이 들이닥치면 깜짝 놀랄 거예요. 시간을 좀 내서, 저를 만났고 십오 분쯤 뒤에 제가 갈 거라고 전해 주시면, 큰 도움이 될 거예요.\""]], [["‘I wish I could do you, or any of you, a greater,’ said Miss La Creevy; ‘but the power to serve, is as seldom joined with the will, as the will is with the power, I think.’", "\"도련님한테든 식구들 누구한테든 더 큰 도움을 드릴 수 있으면 좋으련만.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"하지만 돕고 싶은 마음에 도울 힘이 따라 주는 일은, 도울 힘에 마음이 따라 주는 것만큼이나 드문 것 같네요.\""]], [["Talking on very fast and very much, Miss La Creevy finished her breakfast with great expedition, put away the tea-caddy and hid the key under the fender, resumed her bonnet, and, taking Nicholas’s arm, sallied forth at once to the city.", "라 크리비 양은 아주 빠르고 많이 떠들어 대면서 후딱 아침을 먹어 치웠어. 그러고는 차 통을 치우고 열쇠를 난로 망 밑에 감춘 다음, 다시 보닛을 쓰고 니콜라스의 팔을 잡고는 곧장 시내로 나섰지."], ["Nicholas left her near the door of his mother’s house, and promised to return within a quarter of an hour.", "니콜라스는 어머니 집 문 가까이에서 그녀와 헤어지면서, 십오 분 안에 돌아오겠다고 약속했어."]], [["It so chanced that Ralph Nickleby, at length seeing fit, for his own purposes, to communicate the atrocities of which Nicholas had been guilty, had (instead of first proceeding to another quarter of the town on business, as Newman Noggs supposed he would) gone straight to his sister-in-law.", "그런데 마침 랄프 니클비는, 제 속셈에 따라 니콜라스가 저질렀다는 못된 짓들을 알려야겠다고 마음먹고는, (뉴먼 노그스가 짐작한 대로 일 때문에 시내 다른 곳에 먼저 들르는 대신) 곧장 형수한테 가 있었던 거야."], ["Hence, when Miss La Creevy, admitted by a girl who was cleaning the house, made her way to the sitting-room, she found Mrs Nickleby and Kate in tears, and Ralph just concluding his statement of his nephew’s misdemeanours.", "그래서 집 청소를 하던 여자아이의 안내를 받아 라 크리비 양이 거실로 들어섰을 때, 니클비 부인과 케이트는 눈물을 흘리고 있었고, 랄프는 막 조카가 저지른 비행에 대한 이야기를 끝맺는 참이었어."], ["Kate beckoned her not to retire, and Miss La Creevy took a seat in silence.", "케이트가 나가지 말라고 손짓을 했고, 라 크리비 양은 말없이 자리에 앉았지."]], [["‘You are here already, are you, my gentleman?’ thought the little woman.", "'벌써 와 있었군, 이 양반아.' 자그마한 부인이 속으로 생각했어."], ["‘Then he shall announce himself, and see what effect that has on you.’", "'그럼 도련님이 직접 등장해서, 그게 당신한테 어떤 효과를 내는지 보게 해 주지.'"]], [["‘This is pretty,’ said Ralph, folding up Miss Squeers’s note; ‘very pretty.", "\"이것 참 그럴듯하군요.\" 랄프가 스퀴어스 양의 쪽지를 접으며 말했어."], ["I recommend him--against all my previous conviction, for I knew he would never do any good--to a man with whom, behaving himself properly, he might have remained, in comfort, for years.", "\"아주 그럴듯해요. 내가 그 녀석을 추천해 줬는데 말이죠."], ["What is the result?", "제 모든 예전 확신을 거스르면서요. 그 녀석이 결코 잘되지 못할 걸 알면서도 말입니다."], ["Conduct for which he might hold up his hand at the Old Bailey.’", "처신만 똑바로 했다면 몇 년이고 편안하게 지낼 수 있는 사람한테 보냈는데, 결과가 뭡니까? 올드 베일리 법정에서 손을 들고 서 있어야 할 짓을 저질렀지요.\""]], [["‘I never will believe it,’ said Kate, indignantly; ‘never. It is some base conspiracy, which carries its own falsehood with it.’", "\"전 절대 안 믿을 거예요.\" 케이트가 분개해서 말했어. \"절대요. 이건 무슨 비열한 음모예요. 거짓이 빤히 들여다보이는 음모라고요.\""]], [["‘My dear,’ said Ralph, ‘you wrong the worthy man. These are not inventions. The man is assaulted, your brother is not to be found; this boy, of whom they speak, goes with him--remember, remember.’", "\"얘야,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"넌 그 훌륭한 분을 욕하는 거다. 이건 지어낸 얘기가 아니야. 그 사람은 폭행을 당했고, 네 오빠는 행방이 묘연해. 그들이 말하는 그 애도 네 오빠랑 같이 갔어. 명심해, 명심하라고.\""]], [["‘It is impossible,’ said Kate.", "\"그럴 리 없어요.\" 케이트가 말했어."], ["‘Nicholas!--and a thief too!", "\"니콜라스가 — 게다가 도둑이라니요!"], ["Mama, how can you sit and hear such statements?’", "엄마, 어떻게 가만히 앉아서 그런 말을 듣고 있어요?\""]], [["Poor Mrs.", "가엾은 니클비 부인은 언제 봐도 머리가 그리 또렷하다고 할 만한 사람이 아니었어. 게다가 요즘 들어 형편이 바뀌면서 더없이 복잡한 혼란 속에 빠져 있었지."], ["Nickleby, who had, at no time, been remarkable for the possession of a very clear understanding, and who had been reduced by the late changes in her affairs to a most complicated state of perplexity, made no other reply to this earnest remonstrance than exclaiming from behind a mass of pocket-handkerchief, that she never could have believed it--thereby most ingeniously leaving her hearers to suppose that she did believe it.", "그래서 이 간절한 항변에 대해, 손수건 더미 뒤에서 자기는 도무지 믿을 수가 없다고 외칠 뿐 다른 대답을 하지 못했어. 그렇게 해서 아주 교묘하게도, 듣는 사람들이 도리어 그녀가 믿고 있다고 짐작하게 만든 거야."]], [["‘It would be my duty, if he came in my way, to deliver him up to justice,’ said Ralph, ‘my bounden duty; I should have no other course, as a man of the world and a man of business, to pursue. And yet,’ said Ralph, speaking in a very marked manner, and looking furtively, but fixedly, at Kate, ‘and yet I would not. I would spare the feelings of his--of his sister. And his mother of course,’ added Ralph, as though by an afterthought, and with far less emphasis.", "\"만약 그 애가 내 앞에 나타난다면, 그 애를 법의 심판에 넘기는 게 내 도리겠지요.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"내 마땅한 의무니까요. 세상 물정 아는 사람이자 사업가로서, 그 길밖에는 따를 도리가 없어요. 그렇지만,\" 랄프는 아주 의미심장한 투로 말하면서 케이트를 슬쩍, 그러나 뚫어지게 바라보았어. \"그렇지만 난 그러지 않을 겁니다. 그 애의 — 그 애 누이의 마음을 헤아려 줄 테니까요. 물론 어머니 마음도요.\" 랄프는 마치 뒤늦게 생각났다는 듯, 훨씬 힘을 빼고 덧붙였어."]], [["Kate very well understood that this was held out as an additional inducement to her to preserve the strictest silence regarding the events of the preceding night.", "케이트는 이게 어젯밤 일에 대해 철저히 입을 다물라고 자기한테 던지는 또 하나의 미끼라는 걸 잘 알았어."], ["She looked involuntarily towards Ralph as he ceased to speak, but he had turned his eyes another way, and seemed for the moment quite unconscious of her presence.", "랄프가 말을 멈추자 케이트는 저도 모르게 그를 쳐다봤지만, 그는 눈길을 다른 데로 돌린 채 잠시 케이트의 존재를 까맣게 잊은 듯했어."]], [["‘Everything,’ said Ralph, after a long silence, broken only by Mrs Nickleby’s sobs, ‘everything combines to prove the truth of this letter, if indeed there were any possibility of disputing it.", "\"모든 게,\" 니클비 부인의 흐느낌 말고는 아무 소리 없는 긴 침묵 끝에 랄프가 입을 열었어."], ["Do innocent men steal away from the sight of honest folks, and skulk in hiding-places, like outlaws?", "\"모든 게 이 편지가 사실임을 증명하고 있어요. 설령 이걸 반박할 여지가 조금이라도 있다 쳐도 말이죠."], ["Do innocent men inveigle nameless vagabonds, and prowl with them about the country as idle robbers do?", "죄 없는 사람이 정직한 이들의 눈을 피해 슬그머니 빠져나가서, 무법자처럼 은신처에 숨어 다니던가요? 죄 없는 사람이 이름도 모를 부랑자를 꾀어, 게으른 도둑들처럼 그자와 함께 시골을 어슬렁거리던가요?"], ["Assault, riot, theft, what do you call these?’", "폭행, 소동, 절도, 이런 걸 뭐라고 부릅니까?\""]], [["‘A lie!’ cried a voice, as the door was dashed open, and Nicholas came into the room.", "\"거짓말이야!\" 한 목소리가 외쳤어. 문이 벌컥 열리더니 니콜라스가 방 안으로 들어왔지."]], [["In the first moment of surprise, and possibly of alarm, Ralph rose from his seat, and fell back a few paces, quite taken off his guard by this unexpected apparition. In another moment, he stood, fixed and immovable with folded arms, regarding his nephew with a scowl; while Kate and Miss La Creevy threw themselves between the two, to prevent the personal violence which the fierce excitement of Nicholas appeared to threaten.", "깜짝 놀란 첫 순간, 어쩌면 겁이 났던지도 모르지, 랄프는 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 몇 걸음 뒤로 물러섰어. 이 뜻밖의 출현에 완전히 허를 찔린 거야. 다음 순간, 그는 팔짱을 낀 채 꼼짝 않고 굳어 서서, 조카를 노려보았어. 한편 케이트와 라 크리비 양은 두 사람 사이로 몸을 던졌어. 니콜라스의 격렬한 흥분이 당장이라도 폭력으로 터질 것 같았거든."]], [["‘Dear Nicholas,’ cried his sister, clinging to him.", "\"오빠.\" 누이가 그를 붙들고 매달리며 외쳤어."], ["‘Be calm, consider--’", "\"진정해, 잘 생각해 봐 —\""]], [["‘Consider, Kate!’ cried Nicholas, clasping her hand so tight in the tumult of his anger, that she could scarcely bear the pain.", "\"잘 생각하라고, 케이트!\" 니콜라스가 분노에 차서 누이의 손을 어찌나 꽉 움켜쥐었던지, 케이트는 아파서 견디기 힘들 정도였어."], ["‘When I consider all, and think of what has passed, I need be made of iron to stand before him.’", "\"이 모든 걸 생각하고, 그동안 있었던 일을 떠올리면, 저 사람 앞에 서 있으려면 나는 쇠로 만들어진 사람이라도 돼야 할 판이야.\""]], [["‘Or bronze,’ said Ralph, quietly; ‘there is not hardihood enough in flesh and blood to face it out.’", "\"청동으로 만든 사람이거나.\" 랄프가 조용히 말했어. \"살과 피로 된 인간한테는 이 일을 끝까지 버텨 낼 만한 뻔뻔함이 모자라거든.\""]], [["‘Oh dear, dear!’ cried Mrs.", "\"어머, 어머나!\""], ["Nickleby, ‘that things should have come to such a pass as this!’", "니클비 부인이 외쳤어. \"일이 이 지경까지 오다니!\""]], [["‘Who speaks in a tone, as if I had done wrong, and brought disgrace on them?’ said Nicholas, looking round.", "\"누가 내가 잘못이라도 한 것처럼, 식구들한테 망신이라도 안긴 것처럼 말하는 겁니까?\" 니콜라스가 둘러보며 말했어."]], [["‘Your mother, sir,’ replied Ralph, motioning towards her.", "\"네 어머니다.\" 랄프가 그녀 쪽을 가리키며 대답했어."]], [["‘Whose ears have been poisoned by you,’ said Nicholas; ‘by you--who, under pretence of deserving the thanks she poured upon you, heaped every insult, wrong, and indignity upon my head. You, who sent me to a den where sordid cruelty, worthy of yourself, runs wanton, and youthful misery stalks precocious; where the lightness of childhood shrinks into the heaviness of age, and its every promise blights, and withers as it grows. I call Heaven to witness,’ said Nicholas, looking eagerly round, ‘that I have seen all this, and that he knows it.’", "\"당신 때문에 귀가 더럽혀진 거예요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"당신 때문에요. 당신은 어머니가 쏟아 낸 감사를 받을 자격이 있는 척하면서, 온갖 모욕과 부당함과 치욕을 내 머리 위에 들이부었지요. 당신은 나를 소굴로 보냈어요. 당신한테 딱 어울리는 추악한 잔인함이 제멋대로 날뛰고, 어린 비참함이 조숙하게 활보하는 그런 곳으로요. 거기선 아이다운 발랄함이 늙은이의 무거움 속으로 쪼그라들고, 자라면서 모든 희망이 시들고 말라 버려요. 하늘에 대고 증언하겠어요.\" 니콜라스가 간절히 둘러보며 말했어. \"내가 이 모든 걸 봤다는 걸, 그리고 저 사람도 그걸 다 안다는 걸요.\""]], [["‘Refute these calumnies,’ said Kate, ‘and be more patient, so that you may give them no advantage. Tell us what you really did, and show that they are untrue.’", "\"그 비방을 반박해 봐.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"그리고 좀 더 참아. 저들한테 빌미를 주면 안 돼. 오빠가 실제로 무슨 짓을 했는지 말해 줘. 그리고 저게 다 거짓이라는 걸 보여 줘.\""]], [["‘Of what do they--or of what does he--accuse me?’ said Nicholas.", "\"저들이 — 아니, 저 사람이 — 나를 뭐라고 고발하는 건데?\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘First, of attacking your master, and being within an ace of qualifying yourself to be tried for murder,’ interposed Ralph.", "\"우선, 네 고용주를 공격해서 살인죄로 재판받기 직전까지 갔다는 거다.\" 랄프가 끼어들었어."], ["‘I speak plainly, young man, bluster as you will.’", "\"분명히 말해 두는데, 젊은이, 아무리 큰소리쳐 봐야 소용없어.\""]], [["‘I interfered,’ said Nicholas, ‘to save a miserable creature from the vilest cruelty. In so doing, I inflicted such punishment upon a wretch as he will not readily forget, though far less than he deserved from me. If the same scene were renewed before me now, I would take the same part; but I would strike harder and heavier, and brand him with such marks as he should carry to his grave, go to it when he would.’", "\"저는 끼어들었을 뿐이에요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"가엾은 한 생명을 더없이 비열한 잔인함에서 구하려고요. 그러면서 그 못된 인간한테 쉽게 잊지 못할 벌을 줬지요. 사실 그자가 내게 받아 마땅한 것에 비하면 훨씬 가벼웠지만요. 지금 내 앞에서 똑같은 장면이 다시 벌어진다면, 나는 똑같이 할 거예요. 다만 더 세게, 더 모질게 때려서, 그자가 무덤까지, 언제 가든 그날까지 지니고 갈 만한 자국을 남겨 줄 거예요.\""]], [["‘You hear?’ said Ralph, turning to Mrs.", "\"들리세요?\" 랄프가 니클비 부인 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했어."], ["Nickleby. ‘Penitence, this!’", "\"이게 뉘우치는 겁니까!\""]], [["‘Oh dear me!’ cried Mrs.", "\"어머, 어쩜!\""], ["Nickleby, ‘I don’t know what to think, I really don’t.’", "니클비 부인이 외쳤어. \"어떻게 생각해야 할지 정말 모르겠어, 정말로.\""]], [["‘Do not speak just now, mama, I entreat you,’ said Kate. ‘Dear Nicholas, I only tell you, that you may know what wickedness can prompt, but they accuse you of--a ring is missing, and they dare to say that--’", "\"제발 지금은 아무 말도 하지 마세요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"오빠, 한 가지만 말할게. 악의가 사람을 어디까지 몰고 가는지 오빠가 알아야 하니까. 저들이 오빠를 고발하는 건 — 반지가 하나 없어졌다면서, 감히 이렇게 말하는 거야 —\""]], [["‘The woman,’ said Nicholas, haughtily, ‘the wife of the fellow from whom these charges come, dropped--as I suppose--a worthless ring among some clothes of mine, early in the morning on which I left the house. At least, I know that she was in the bedroom where they lay, struggling with an unhappy child, and that I found it when I opened my bundle on the road. I returned it, at once, by coach, and they have it now.’", "\"그 여자가,\" 니콜라스가 거만하게 말했어. \"이 혐의들을 제기한 그 작자의 아내가, 내가 그 집을 떠나던 날 이른 아침에 — 내 짐작으로는 — 내 옷가지 틈에 값어치도 없는 반지를 하나 떨어뜨린 거예요. 적어도 나는 그 여자가 옷이 놓인 침실에 있었다는 걸 알아요. 가엾은 아이랑 실랑이를 벌이면서 말이죠. 그리고 길에서 짐 보따리를 풀었을 때 그 반지를 발견했고요. 나는 곧장 마차 편으로 그걸 돌려보냈어요. 지금 그들 손에 있을 거예요.\""]], [["‘I knew, I knew,’ said Kate, looking towards her uncle.", "\"그럴 줄 알았어, 그럴 줄 알았다고.\" 케이트가 삼촌 쪽을 바라보며 말했어."], ["‘About this boy, love, in whose company they say you left?’", "\"오빠, 저들이 오빠랑 같이 떠났다는 그 애에 대해서는?\""]], [["‘The boy, a silly, helpless creature, from brutality and hard usage, is with me now,’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"그 애는 어수룩하고 의지할 데 없는 아이예요. 잔인함과 모진 학대 때문에 그렇게 된 거죠. 지금 나랑 함께 있어요.\" 니콜라스가 받아쳤어."]], [["‘You hear?’ said Ralph, appealing to the mother again, ‘everything proved, even upon his own confession. Do you choose to restore that boy, sir?’", "\"들리세요?\" 랄프가 다시 니클비 부인에게 호소했어. \"모든 게 증명됐어요. 본인 입으로 자백까지 했고요. 자네, 그 아이를 돌려보낼 건가?\""]], [["‘No, I do not,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"아뇨, 안 돌려보냅니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘You do not?’ sneered Ralph.", "\"안 돌려보내겠다고?\" 랄프가 비웃었어."]], [["‘No,’ repeated Nicholas, ‘not to the man with whom I found him. I would that I knew on whom he has the claim of birth: I might wring something from his sense of shame, if he were dead to every tie of nature.’", "\"네.\" 니콜라스가 되풀이했어. \"내가 그 애를 데려온 그 작자한테는요. 그 애가 누구한테 핏줄로 기댈 수 있는지 알았으면 좋겠어요. 만약 그자가 인간으로서 모든 정을 다 잃은 사람이라 해도, 그 수치심에서 뭐라도 짜낼 수 있을지 모르니까요.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ said Ralph.", "\"그것참!\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘Now, sir, will you hear a word or two from me?’", "\"자, 젊은이, 이제 내가 한두 마디 하는 걸 들어 보겠나?\""]], [["‘You can speak when and what you please,’ replied Nicholas, embracing his sister.", "\"하고 싶을 때 하고 싶은 말은 뭐든 하세요.\" 니콜라스가 누이를 끌어안으며 대답했어."], ["‘I take little heed of what you say or threaten.’", "\"당신이 무슨 말을 하든, 무슨 위협을 하든 저는 별로 개의치 않아요.\""]], [["‘Mighty well, sir,’ retorted Ralph; ‘but perhaps it may concern others, who may think it worth their while to listen, and consider what I tell them. I will address your mother, sir, who knows the world.’", "\"아주 좋아, 젊은이.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어. \"하지만 어쩌면 다른 사람들한테는 관계되는 일일지도 모르지. 귀 기울여 듣고, 내가 하는 말을 곱씹어 볼 만하다고 여기는 사람들 말이야. 자네 어머니한테 말씀드려야겠군. 세상 물정을 아는 분이니.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["and I only too dearly wish I didn’t,’ sobbed Mrs.", "차라리 몰랐으면 얼마나 좋을까.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 흐느꼈어."]], [["There really was no necessity for the good lady to be much distressed upon this particular head; the extent of her worldly knowledge being, to say the least, very questionable; and so Ralph seemed to think, for he smiled as she spoke. He then glanced steadily at her and Nicholas by turns, as he delivered himself in these words:", "이 점에 대해 이 착한 부인이 그렇게까지 괴로워할 필요는 사실 전혀 없었어. 그녀가 아는 세상 물정이란 게, 적게 잡아도 무척 미심쩍은 수준이었거든. 랄프도 그렇게 생각하는 모양이었어. 부인이 말하는 동안 그가 빙긋 웃었으니까. 그러고는 부인과 니콜라스를 번갈아 가며 가만히 쳐다보면서, 이렇게 말을 늘어놓았지."]], [["‘Of what I have done, or what I meant to do, for you, ma’am, and my niece, I say not one syllable.", "\"제가 부인과 제 조카딸을 위해 무엇을 해 줬는지, 또는 무엇을 해 주려 했는지, 그에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않겠습니다."], ["I held out no promise, and leave you to judge for yourself.", "저는 어떤 약속도 하지 않았으니, 부인 스스로 판단하시도록 맡기지요. 지금 어떤 협박을 하려는 것도 아닙니다."], ["I hold out no threat now, but I say that this boy, headstrong, wilful and disorderly as he is, should not have one penny of my money, or one crust of my bread, or one grasp of my hand, to save him from the loftiest gallows in all Europe.", "다만 이 말은 하겠어요. 이 녀석은, 이렇게 고집 세고 제멋대로에다 무질서한 이 녀석은, 온 유럽에서 가장 높은 교수대에서 목숨을 건진다 해도, 내 돈 한 푼도, 내 빵 한 조각도, 내 손길 한 번도 받지 못할 겁니다."], ["I will not meet him, come where he comes, or hear his name.", "이 녀석이 어디로 오든 만나지 않을 거고, 그 이름도 듣지 않을 겁니다."], ["I will not help him, or those who help him.", "이 녀석도, 이 녀석을 돕는 자들도 돕지 않겠어요."], ["With a full knowledge of what he brought upon you by so doing, he has come back in his selfish sloth, to be an aggravation of your wants, and a burden upon his sister’s scanty wages.", "자기가 그렇게 함으로써 부인한테 어떤 일이 닥칠지 빤히 알면서도, 이 녀석은 이기적인 게으름에 빠져 돌아왔지요. 부인의 궁핍을 더 무겁게 하고, 제 누이의 쥐꼬리만 한 임금에 짐이 되러 말입니다."], ["I regret to leave you, and more to leave her, now, but I will not encourage this compound of meanness and cruelty, and, as I will not ask you to renounce him, I see you no more.’", "부인을 떠나는 게, 더구나 지금 조카딸을 두고 떠나는 게 안타깝습니다만, 저는 이 비열함과 잔인함 덩어리를 부추기지 않을 겁니다. 그리고 부인한테 이 녀석을 내치라고 요구하지는 않을 테니, 저는 다시는 부인을 뵙지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["If Ralph had not known and felt his power in wounding those he hated, his glances at Nicholas would have shown it him, in all its force, as he proceeded in the above address.", "랄프가 미워하는 사람들에게 상처를 주는 자기 힘을 모르거나 못 느꼈다 해도, 위의 말을 늘어놓는 동안 니콜라스를 흘끔거리는 눈길만 봤어도 그 힘이 얼마나 대단한지 똑똑히 알았을 거야."], ["Innocent as the young man was of all wrong, every artful insinuation stung, every well-considered sarcasm cut him to the quick; and when Ralph noted his pale face and quivering lip, he hugged himself to mark how well he had chosen the taunts best calculated to strike deep into a young and ardent spirit.", "이 청년은 아무런 잘못도 없었지만, 교묘한 빗댐 하나하나가 그를 콕콕 찔렀고, 잘 벼린 빈정거림 하나하나가 그의 마음을 후벼 팠어. 그리고 랄프는 니콜라스의 창백한 얼굴과 떨리는 입술을 알아채고는, 젊고 뜨거운 영혼을 가장 깊이 찌르도록 계산된 조롱을 자기가 얼마나 잘 골랐는지 흐뭇해하며 속으로 쾌재를 불렀어."]], [["‘I can’t help it,’ cried Mrs. Nickleby. ‘I know you have been very good to us, and meant to do a good deal for my dear daughter. I am quite sure of that; I know you did, and it was very kind of you, having her at your house and all--and of course it would have been a great thing for her and for me too. But I can’t, you know, brother-in-law, I can’t renounce my own son, even if he has done all you say he has--it’s not possible; I couldn’t do it; so we must go to rack and ruin, Kate, my dear. I can bear it, I dare say.’ Pouring forth these and a perfectly wonderful train of other disjointed expressions of regret, which no mortal power but Mrs. Nickleby’s could ever have strung together, that lady wrung her hands, and her tears fell faster.", "\"나도 어쩔 수가 없네요.\" 니클비 부인이 외쳤어. \"당신이 우리한테 무척 잘해 줬다는 거, 내 사랑하는 딸을 위해 많은 걸 해 주려 했다는 거 알아요. 그건 정말 확실해요. 알고 있다고요. 그 애를 당신 집에 데려가고 한 것도 무척 친절한 일이었어요. 물론 그 애한테도, 나한테도 큰 도움이 됐을 거고요. 하지만 도련님, 나는 그럴 수가 없어요. 내 친아들을 내칠 수는 없어요. 설령 그 애가 당신 말대로 그 모든 짓을 다 했다 쳐도요. 그건 안 될 일이에요. 난 못 해요. 그러니 우린 망해서 거덜이 나는 수밖에요, 케이트, 얘야. 그래도 난 견딜 수 있을 거야, 아마.\" 이런 말들과, 그리고 니클비 부인 말고는 그 누구의 힘으로도 결코 엮어 낼 수 없는, 정말 놀라우리만치 두서없는 한탄들을 줄줄이 쏟아 내면서, 부인은 두 손을 비틀어 댔고 눈물은 더 빨리 흘러내렸어."]], [["‘Why do you say “IF Nicholas has done what they say he has,”", "\"엄마, 왜 '만약에 니콜라스가 저들 말대로 했다면'이라고 말하는 거예요?\""], ["mama?’ asked Kate, with honest anger.", "케이트가 솔직하게 화를 내며 물었어."], ["‘You know he has not.’", "\"오빠가 안 했다는 거 엄마도 알잖아요.\""]], [["‘I don’t know what to think, one way or other, my dear,’ said Mrs Nickleby; ‘Nicholas is so violent, and your uncle has so much composure, that I can only hear what he says, and not what Nicholas does.", "\"이러나저러나 어떻게 생각해야 할지 모르겠어, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Never mind, don’t let us talk any more about it.", "\"니콜라스는 저렇게 격하고, 네 삼촌은 저렇게 침착하니, 나는 그저 삼촌이 하는 말만 들리고 니콜라스가 하는 말은 안 들리는구나. 됐다, 그 얘기는 이제 그만하자."], ["We can go to the Workhouse, or the Refuge for the Destitute, or the Magdalen Hospital, I dare say; and the sooner we go the better.’ With this extraordinary jumble of charitable institutions, Mrs.", "우린 구빈원에든, 빈민 보호소에든, 막달레나 수용소에든 가면 되겠지, 아마. 그리고 빨리 갈수록 좋고.\""], ["Nickleby again gave way to her tears.", "자선 기관들을 이렇게 엉뚱하게 뒤섞어 늘어놓고는, 니클비 부인은 다시 눈물을 쏟았어."]], [["‘Stay,’ said Nicholas, as Ralph turned to go. ‘You need not leave this place, sir, for it will be relieved of my presence in one minute, and it will be long, very long, before I darken these doors again.’", "\"잠깐만요.\" 랄프가 가려고 돌아서자 니콜라스가 말했어. \"굳이 여기서 나가실 필요 없어요. 일 분 안에 제가 사라져서 이 자리가 홀가분해질 테니까요. 그리고 제가 다시 이 문턱을 넘기까지는 오래, 아주 오랜 시간이 걸릴 거예요.\""]], [["‘Nicholas,’ cried Kate, throwing herself on her brother’s shoulder, ‘do not say so.", "\"오빠.\" 케이트가 오빠의 어깨에 몸을 던지며 외쳤어."], ["My dear brother, you will break my heart.", "\"그런 말 하지 마. 사랑하는 오빠, 내 가슴이 찢어질 것 같아."], ["Mama, speak to him.", "엄마, 오빠한테 말 좀 해 봐요."], ["Do not mind her, Nicholas; she does not mean it, you should know her better.", "엄마 말은 신경 쓰지 마, 오빠. 엄마도 진심이 아니야."], ["Uncle, somebody, for Heaven’s sake speak to him.’", "오빠가 엄마를 더 잘 알아야지. 삼촌이든, 누구든, 제발 오빠한테 말 좀 해 줘요.\""]], [["‘I never meant, Kate,’ said Nicholas, tenderly, ‘I never meant to stay among you; think better of me than to suppose it possible. I may turn my back on this town a few hours sooner than I intended, but what of that? We shall not forget each other apart, and better days will come when we shall part no more. Be a woman, Kate,’ he whispered, proudly, ‘and do not make me one, while HE looks on.’", "\"케이트, 난 처음부터 너희들 곁에 머물 생각이 없었어.\" 니콜라스가 다정하게 말했어. \"내가 그럴 마음이라곤 꿈에도 생각하지 마. 나를 좀 더 좋게 봐 줘. 내가 작정했던 것보다 몇 시간 일찍 이 마을에 등을 돌리는 것뿐이야. 그게 뭐 대수겠어? 떨어져 있어도 우린 서로를 잊지 않을 거고, 다시는 헤어지지 않을 좋은 날들이 올 거야. 의젓해져, 케이트.\" 그가 자랑스럽다는 듯 속삭였어. \"그리고 저 사람이 보고 있는데, 날 울보로 만들지 마.\""]], [["‘No, no, I will not,’ said Kate, eagerly, ‘but you will not leave us. Oh! think of all the happy days we have had together, before these terrible misfortunes came upon us; of all the comfort and happiness of home, and the trials we have to bear now; of our having no protector under all the slights and wrongs that poverty so much favours, and you cannot leave us to bear them alone, without one hand to help us.’", "\"응, 안 그럴게, 안 그럴 거야.\" 케이트가 간절하게 말했어. \"하지만 우릴 두고 떠나진 마. 오! 이 끔찍한 불운이 닥치기 전에, 우리가 함께 보낸 그 행복한 날들을 생각해 봐. 집에서 누리던 온갖 편안함과 행복을, 그리고 지금 우리가 견뎌야 하는 이 시련들을 말이야. 가난이 그토록 부추기는 온갖 멸시와 부당함 속에서 우릴 지켜 줄 사람 하나 없는데, 오빠가 우릴 두고 갈 순 없어. 도와줄 손길 하나 없이, 우리 둘만 이 일을 견디게 내버려 둘 순 없잖아.\""]], [["‘You will be helped when I am away,’ replied Nicholas hurriedly.", "\"내가 떠나면 도움을 받게 될 거야.\" 니콜라스가 다급하게 대답했어."], ["‘I am no help to you, no protector; I should bring you nothing but sorrow, and want, and suffering.", "\"난 너희들한테 아무 도움도 못 되고, 지켜 주지도 못해. 내가 곁에 있어 봐야 슬픔과 궁핍과 고통밖에 가져다줄 게 없어."], ["My own mother sees it, and her fondness and fears for you, point to the course that I should take.", "우리 어머니도 그걸 보고 있고, 어머니가 너를 아끼고 너 때문에 걱정하는 마음이, 내가 가야 할 길을 가리키고 있어. 그러니 케이트, 모든 착한 천사들이 너를 지켜 주길 빌어."], ["And so all good angels bless you, Kate, till I can carry you to some home of mine, where we may revive the happiness denied to us now, and talk of these trials as of things gone by.", "내가 너를 어딘가 내 보금자리로 데려갈 수 있을 때까지 말이야. 거기서 우린 지금 누리지 못하는 행복을 되살리고, 이 시련들을 다 지나간 일처럼 이야기할 수 있을 거야."], ["Do not keep me here, but let me go at once.", "날 여기 붙들지 말고, 지금 당장 가게 해 줘. 자."], ["There. Dear girl--dear girl.’", "사랑하는 동생아, 사랑하는 동생아.\""]], [["The grasp which had detained him relaxed, and Kate swooned in his arms.", "그를 붙들고 있던 손이 스르르 풀렸고, 케이트는 그의 품에서 정신을 잃었어."], ["Nicholas stooped over her for a few seconds, and placing her gently in a chair, confided her to their honest friend.", "니콜라스는 잠시 누이 위로 몸을 숙였다가, 케이트를 의자에 가만히 앉히고는 정직한 친구한테 맡겼어."]], [["‘I need not entreat your sympathy,’ he said, wringing her hand, ‘for I know your nature.", "\"굳이 동정을 부탁드리지 않을게요.\" 그가 라 크리비 양의 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했어."], ["You will never forget them.’", "\"당신 성품을 아니까요. 당신은 결코 이 사람들을 잊지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["He stepped up to Ralph, who remained in the same attitude which he had preserved throughout the interview, and moved not a finger.", "그는 랄프에게 다가갔어. 랄프는 만남 내내 지켜 온 그 자세 그대로 손가락 하나 까딱하지 않고 서 있었지."]], [["‘Whatever step you take, sir,’ he said, in a voice inaudible beyond themselves, ‘I shall keep a strict account of.", "\"당신이 무슨 짓을 하든,\" 니콜라스가 두 사람한테만 들릴 만큼 낮은 목소리로 말했어."], ["I leave them to you, at your desire.", "\"제가 빠짐없이 똑똑히 적어 두겠어요. 당신이 원하니 이 사람들을 당신한테 맡깁니다."], ["There will be a day of reckoning sooner or later, and it will be a heavy one for you if they are wronged.’", "머지않아 셈을 치를 날이 올 거예요. 만약 이 사람들이 부당한 일을 당한다면, 그날은 당신한테 아주 무거운 날이 될 거고요.\""]], [["Ralph did not allow a muscle of his face to indicate that he heard one word of this parting address.", "랄프는 이 작별 인사를 한마디라도 들었다는 기색을 얼굴 근육 하나에도 내비치지 않았어."], ["He hardly knew that it was concluded, and Mrs.", "그 인사가 끝났는지조차 거의 알아채지 못한 듯했지."], ["Nickleby had scarcely made up her mind to detain her son by force if necessary, when Nicholas was gone.", "그리고 니클비 부인이 필요하면 억지로라도 아들을 붙잡아야겠다고 채 마음먹기도 전에, 니콜라스는 이미 가 버렸어."]], [["As he hurried through the streets to his obscure lodging, seeking to keep pace, as it were, with the rapidity of the thoughts which crowded upon him, many doubts and hesitations arose in his mind, and almost tempted him to return. But what would they gain by this? Supposing he were to put Ralph Nickleby at defiance, and were even fortunate enough to obtain some small employment, his being with them could only render their present condition worse, and might greatly impair their future prospects; for his mother had spoken of some new kindnesses towards Kate which she had not denied. ‘No,’ thought Nicholas, ‘I have acted for the best.’", "니콜라스는 자기 초라한 거처를 향해 거리를 서둘러 지나갔어. 머릿속으로 몰려드는 생각들의 속도에 보조를 맞추려는 듯 말이야. 그러는 동안 마음속에 온갖 의심과 망설임이 일어, 그를 돌아가고 싶게 만들 뻔했지. 하지만 그렇게 한들 무얼 얻겠어? 설령 랄프 니클비한테 맞서고, 운 좋게 작은 일자리라도 하나 구한다 쳐도, 그가 식구들과 함께 있으면 지금 형편을 더 나쁘게 만들 뿐이고, 앞날의 가망마저 크게 망쳐 놓을지 몰라. 어머니가 케이트한테 베풀 새로운 친절에 대해 말했는데, 케이트가 그걸 거절하지 않았으니 말이야. '아니야.' 니콜라스는 생각했어. '난 가장 잘한 거야.'"]], [["But, before he had gone five hundred yards, some other and different feeling would come upon him, and then he would lag again, and pulling his hat over his eyes, give way to the melancholy reflections which pressed thickly upon him. To have committed no fault, and yet to be so entirely alone in the world; to be separated from the only persons he loved, and to be proscribed like a criminal, when six months ago he had been surrounded by every comfort, and looked up to, as the chief hope of his family--this was hard to bear. He had not deserved it either. Well, there was comfort in that; and poor Nicholas would brighten up again, to be again depressed, as his quickly shifting thoughts presented every variety of light and shade before him.", "하지만 오백 야드도 채 가기 전에, 또 다른 마음이 그를 덮쳐 왔어. 그러면 그는 다시 걸음을 늦추고, 모자를 눈까지 푹 눌러쓴 채, 빽빽이 밀려드는 우울한 생각에 잠겼지. 아무 잘못도 저지르지 않았는데도 세상에 이토록 철저히 혼자라니. 사랑하는 단 하나의 사람들과 떨어져, 죄인처럼 쫓겨나는 신세라니. 여섯 달 전만 해도 온갖 편안함에 둘러싸여, 집안의 가장 큰 희망으로 우러름받던 그가 말이야. 이건 견디기 힘든 일이었어. 게다가 그는 이런 일을 당할 만한 짓을 한 적도 없었어. 음, 거기에 위안이 있었지. 그래서 가엾은 니콜라스는 다시 마음이 밝아졌다가, 또 가라앉았어. 빠르게 바뀌는 생각들이 그의 앞에 온갖 빛과 그늘을 차례차례 펼쳐 놓았거든."]], [["Undergoing these alternations of hope and misgiving, which no one, placed in a situation of ordinary trial, can fail to have experienced, Nicholas at length reached his poor room, where, no longer borne up by the excitement which had hitherto sustained him, but depressed by the revulsion of feeling it left behind, he threw himself on the bed, and turning his face to the wall, gave free vent to the emotions he had so long stifled.", "평범한 시련에 처한 사람이라면 누구나 겪어 봤을 이런 희망과 불안의 엇갈림을 거치면서, 니콜라스는 마침내 그 초라한 방에 닿았어. 거기서 그는, 여태 자기를 지탱해 주던 흥분이 더 이상 떠받쳐 주지 않게 되자, 도리어 그 흥분이 남기고 간 감정의 반동에 짓눌려, 침대에 몸을 던졌지. 그러고는 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌리고, 그토록 오래 억눌러 온 감정을 마음껏 쏟아 냈어."]], [["He had not heard anybody enter, and was unconscious of the presence of Smike, until, happening to raise his head, he saw him, standing at the upper end of the room, looking wistfully towards him.", "그는 누가 들어오는 소리도 못 들었고, 스마이크가 와 있는 것도 알아채지 못했어. 그러다 우연히 고개를 들었을 때, 방 저쪽 끝에 서서 안타까운 눈으로 자기를 바라보고 있는 스마이크를 봤지."], ["He withdrew his eyes when he saw that he was observed, and affected to be busied with some scanty preparations for dinner.", "스마이크는 자기가 들킨 걸 알자 눈길을 거두고는, 변변찮은 저녁 준비를 하느라 바쁜 척했어."]], [["‘Well, Smike,’ said Nicholas, as cheerfully as he could speak, ‘let me hear what new acquaintances you have made this morning, or what new wonder you have found out, in the compass of this street and the next one.’", "\"음, 스마이크.\" 니콜라스가 애써 밝은 목소리로 말했어. \"오늘 아침엔 어떤 새 친구를 사귀었는지, 아니면 이 거리하고 옆 거리를 다니면서 어떤 새 신기한 걸 알아냈는지, 어디 한번 들어 보자.\""]], [["‘No,’ said Smike, shaking his head mournfully; ‘I must talk of something else today.’", "\"아뇨.\" 스마이크가 슬프게 고개를 저으며 말했어. \"오늘은 다른 얘길 해야겠어요.\""]], [["‘Of what you like,’ replied Nicholas, good-humouredly.", "\"하고 싶은 얘기 해.\" 니콜라스가 다정하게 대답했어."]], [["‘Of this,’ said Smike.", "\"이거예요.\" 스마이크가 말했어."], ["‘I know you are unhappy, and have got into great trouble by bringing me away.", "\"형이 불행하다는 거, 저를 데리고 나온 탓에 큰 곤경에 빠졌다는 거 알아요. 진작 그걸 알고 뒤에 남았어야 했는데."], ["I ought to have known that, and stopped behind--I would, indeed, if I had thought it then.", "정말이지, 그때 그 생각을 했더라면 그랬을 거예요. 형은 — 형은 — 부자가 아니잖아요."], ["You--you--are not rich; you have not enough for yourself, and I should not be here.", "형 한 몸 건사할 것도 모자란데, 제가 여기 있으면 안 되는 거였어요. 형은,\""], ["You grow,’ said the lad, laying his hand timidly on that of Nicholas, ‘you grow thinner every day; your cheek is paler, and your eye more sunk.", "스마이크가 머뭇거리며 니콜라스의 손에 자기 손을 얹었어. \"형은 날마다 야위어 가요."], ["Indeed I cannot bear to see you so, and think how I am burdening you.", "뺨은 더 창백해지고, 눈은 더 푹 꺼지고요. 정말이지 형이 그러는 걸 보면서, 내가 형한테 얼마나 짐이 되는지 생각하면 견딜 수가 없어요."], ["I tried to go away today, but the thought of your kind face drew me back.", "오늘 떠나려고 했는데, 형의 다정한 얼굴이 떠올라서 발길이 돌아왔어요. 한마디 말도 없이 형을 두고 떠날 순 없었어요.\""], ["I could not leave you without a word.’ The poor fellow could say no more, for his eyes filled with tears, and his voice was gone.", "가엾은 녀석은 더 말을 잇지 못했어. 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁 차오르고, 목소리가 사라져 버렸거든."]], [["‘The word which separates us,’ said Nicholas, grasping him heartily by the shoulder, ‘shall never be said by me, for you are my only comfort and stay.", "\"우릴 갈라놓는 그런 말은,\" 니콜라스가 스마이크의 어깨를 진심으로 꽉 붙들며 말했어."], ["I would not lose you now, Smike, for all the world could give.", "\"내 입에서는 결코 나오지 않을 거야. 넌 내 유일한 위안이고 의지니까."], ["The thought of you has upheld me through all I have endured today, and shall, through fifty times such trouble.", "이제 와서 온 세상을 다 준다 해도 난 널 잃고 싶지 않아, 스마이크. 오늘 내가 견뎌 낸 그 모든 걸 버티게 해 준 건 바로 너를 생각하는 마음이었어."], ["Give me your hand. My heart is linked to yours.", "그리고 그 쉰 곱절의 고난을 겪는다 해도 그럴 거야."], ["We will journey from this place together, before the week is out.", "손 이리 줘. 내 마음은 네 마음과 이어져 있어."], ["What, if I am steeped in poverty?", "우린 이번 주가 가기 전에 함께 이곳을 떠날 거야. 내가 가난에 찌들었다 한들 어때?"], ["You lighten it, and we will be poor together.’", "네가 그 가난을 덜어 주잖아. 우린 함께 가난하게 지내면 돼.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 21", "# CHAPTER 21"]], [["Madam Mantalini finds herself in a Situation of some Difficulty, and Miss Nickleby finds herself in no Situation at all\nThe agitation she had undergone, rendered Kate Nickleby unable to resume her duties at the dressmaker’s for three days, at the expiration of which interval she betook herself at the accustomed hour, and with languid steps, to the temple of fashion where Madame Mantalini reigned paramount and supreme.", "케이트 니클비는 그동안 겪은 마음고생 때문에 사흘 동안은 양장점 일을 다시 할 수가 없었어. 그러다 사흘이 지나서야 늘 가던 시간에, 무거운 발걸음으로, 마담 만탈리니가 절대 권력을 휘두르는 그 패션의 전당으로 향했지."]], [["The ill-will of Miss Knag had lost nothing of its virulence in the interval.", "미스 내그의 심술은 그동안에도 독기가 조금도 빠지지 않았어."], ["The young ladies still scrupulously shrunk from all companionship with their denounced associate; and when that exemplary female arrived a few minutes afterwards, she was at no pains to conceal the displeasure with which she regarded Kate’s return.", "젊은 아가씨들은 여전히 손가락질받는 동료와는 어울리길 꺼리며 슬슬 피했지. 그리고 그 본보기 같은 여자가 몇 분 뒤에 도착했을 때, 그녀는 케이트가 돌아온 걸 보고 느낀 못마땅함을 굳이 숨기려 들지도 않았어."]], [["‘Upon my word!’ said Miss Knag, as the satellites flocked round, to relieve her of her bonnet and shawl; ‘I should have thought some people would have had spirit enough to stop away altogether, when they know what an incumbrance their presence is to right-minded persons.", "\"세상에 원!\" 미스 내그는 부하들이 우르르 몰려와 모자며 숄을 벗겨 주는 동안 이렇게 말했어."], ["But it’s a queer world; oh!", "\"어떤 사람들은 자기가 제대로 된 사람들한테 얼마나 짐이 되는지 알면, 아예 안 나올 만한 배짱쯤은 있을 줄 알았는데. 하지만 참 묘한 세상이야."], ["it’s a queer world!’", "오! 묘한 세상이지!\""]], [["Miss Knag, having passed this comment on the world, in the tone in which most people do pass comments on the world when they are out of temper, that is to say, as if they by no means belonged to it, concluded by heaving a sigh, wherewith she seemed meekly to compassionate the wickedness of mankind.", "미스 내그는 화가 났을 때 사람들이 흔히 그러는 투로, 그러니까 자기는 그 세상에 절대 속하지 않는다는 듯한 투로, 세상에 대해 이런 한마디를 던졌어. 그러고는 한숨으로 말을 맺었지. 마치 인류의 사악함을 너그러이 가엾어하는 것 같은 한숨이었어."]], [["The attendants were not slow to echo the sigh, and Miss Knag was apparently on the eve of favouring them with some further moral reflections, when the voice of Madame Mantalini, conveyed through the speaking-tube, ordered Miss Nickleby upstairs to assist in the arrangement of the show-room; a distinction which caused Miss Knag to toss her head so much, and bite her lips so hard, that her powers of conversation were, for the time, annihilated.", "부하들도 곧장 그 한숨을 따라 쉬었어. 미스 내그가 막 그들에게 도덕적인 훈수를 좀 더 늘어놓으려던 참에, 전성관을 타고 마담 만탈리니의 목소리가 흘러나왔어. 미스 니클비더러 위층으로 올라와 진열실 정리를 도우라는 거였지. 이런 특별 대우에 미스 내그는 어찌나 머리를 휙휙 젖히고 입술을 꽉 깨물던지, 한동안 말할 기운이 싹 사라져 버렸어."]], [["‘Well, Miss Nickleby, child,’ said Madame Mantalini, when Kate presented herself; ‘are you quite well again?’", "\"자, 니클비 양, 얘야,\" 케이트가 올라오자 마담 만탈리니가 말했어. \"이제 몸은 다 나았니?\""]], [["‘A great deal better, thank you,’ replied Kate.", "\"많이 좋아졌어요, 감사합니다.\" 케이트가 대답했어."]], [["‘I wish I could say the same,’ remarked Madame Mantalini, seating herself with an air of weariness.", "\"나도 그렇다고 말할 수 있으면 좋으련만.\" 마담 만탈리니가 지친 기색으로 자리에 앉으며 말했어."]], [["‘Are you ill?’ asked Kate.", "\"어디 편찮으세요?\" 케이트가 물었어."], ["‘I am very sorry for that.’", "\"그렇다면 정말 안됐네요.\""]], [["‘Not exactly ill, but worried, child--worried,’ rejoined Madame.", "\"꼭 아픈 건 아니고, 그냥 골치가 아파서 그래, 얘야. 골치가 아파.\" 마담이 대꾸했어."]], [["‘I am still more sorry to hear that,’ said Kate, gently.", "\"그 말씀을 들으니 더 안타깝네요.\" 케이트가 조용히 말했어."], ["‘Bodily illness is more easy to bear than mental.’", "\"몸이 아픈 건 마음이 아픈 것보다 견디기 쉬운데요.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["and it’s much easier to talk than to bear either,’ said Madame, rubbing her nose with much irritability of manner.", "게다가 둘 다 직접 견디는 것보다 말로 하는 게 훨씬 쉽지.\" 마담이 신경질적으로 코를 문지르며 말했어."], ["‘There, get to your work, child, and put the things in order, do.’", "\"자, 어서 일이나 해라, 얘야. 물건들을 좀 정리해 둬, 어서.\""]], [["While Kate was wondering within herself what these symptoms of unusual vexation portended, Mr.", "케이트가 이런 평소답지 않은 신경질이 무슨 뜻일까 속으로 궁금해하고 있을 때였어."], ["Mantalini put the tips of his whiskers, and, by degrees, his head, through the half-opened door, and cried in a soft voice--", "만탈리니 씨가 반쯤 열린 문틈으로 수염 끝을, 그리고 점점 머리까지 들이밀더니 부드러운 목소리로 외쳤어."]], [["‘Is my life and soul there?’", "\"내 목숨이자 영혼이 여기 있나?\""]], [["‘No,’ replied his wife.", "\"없어요.\" 그의 아내가 대답했어."]], [["‘How can it say so, when it is blooming in the front room like a little rose in a demnition flower-pot?’ urged Mantalini.", "\"앞방에서 그 끝내주는 화분에 핀 작은 장미처럼 활짝 피어 있으면서 어떻게 없다고 하지?\" 만탈리니가 졸랐어."], ["‘May its poppet come in and talk?’", "\"그 사람의 귀여운 인형이 들어가서 얘기 좀 해도 될까?\""]], [["‘Certainly not,’ replied Madame: ‘you know I never allow you here.", "\"절대 안 돼요.\" 마담이 대답했어."], ["Go along!’", "\"여기 들어오는 거 내가 절대 허락 안 하는 거 알잖아요. 어서 가요!\""]], [["The poppet, however, encouraged perhaps by the relenting tone of this reply, ventured to rebel, and, stealing into the room, made towards Madame Mantalini on tiptoe, blowing her a kiss as he came along.", "하지만 그 귀여운 인형은 누그러진 대답의 말투에 용기를 얻었는지 반항을 감행했어. 슬그머니 방으로 기어들어 와 발끝으로 살금살금 마담 만탈리니에게 다가가면서, 오는 내내 입맞춤을 날려 보냈지."]], [["‘Why will it vex itself, and twist its little face into bewitching nutcrackers?’ said Mantalini, putting his left arm round the waist of his life and soul, and drawing her towards him with his right.", "\"어쩌자고 속을 끓이면서 그 작은 얼굴을 그렇게 매혹적인 호두까기 모양으로 찌푸리고 그러나?\" 만탈리니가 자기 목숨이자 영혼의 허리를 왼팔로 감고는, 오른팔로 끌어당기며 말했어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I can’t bear you,’ replied his wife.", "당신은 정말 못 봐 주겠어요.\" 그의 아내가 대답했어."]], [["‘Not--eh, not bear ME!’ exclaimed Mantalini.", "\"못, 어, 나를 못 봐 주겠다고!\" 만탈리니가 소리쳤어."], ["‘Fibs, fibs.", "\"거짓말, 거짓말."], ["It couldn’t be.", "그럴 리가 없지."], ["There’s not a woman alive, that could tell me such a thing to my face--to my own face.’ Mr.", "그런 소릴 내 면전에서, 바로 내 얼굴에 대고 할 수 있는 여자는 세상에 한 명도 없어.\""], ["Mantalini stroked his chin, as he said this, and glanced complacently at an opposite mirror.", "만탈리니 씨는 이렇게 말하며 턱을 쓰다듬더니, 맞은편 거울을 흐뭇하게 바라봤어."]], [["‘Such destructive extravagance,’ reasoned his wife, in a low tone.", "\"그렇게 사람 망치는 낭비를 해 놓고.\" 그의 아내가 낮은 목소리로 따졌어."]], [["‘All in its joy at having gained such a lovely creature, such a little Venus, such a demd, enchanting, bewitching, engrossing, captivating little Venus,’ said Mantalini.", "\"그건 다 이렇게 사랑스러운 사람을, 이렇게 작은 비너스를, 이렇게 끝내주게 황홀하고 매혹적이고 사람 넋을 빼놓는 작은 비너스를 손에 넣은 기쁨에 겨워서 그런 거지.\" 만탈리니가 말했어."]], [["‘See what a situation you have placed me in!’ urged Madame.", "\"내가 지금 어떤 꼴이 됐는지 좀 봐요!\" 마담이 다그쳤어."]], [["‘No harm will come, no harm shall come, to its own darling,’ rejoined Mr. Mantalini. ‘It is all over; there will be nothing the matter; money shall be got in; and if it don’t come in fast enough, old Nickleby shall stump up again, or have his jugular separated if he dares to vex and hurt the little--’", "\"내 사랑스러운 사람한테는 아무 해도 안 와, 아무 해도 안 올 거야.\" 만탈리니 씨가 대꾸했어. \"다 끝난 일이야. 아무 일도 없을 거라고. 돈은 들어올 테니까. 그리고 그게 빨리 안 들어오면, 늙은 니클비가 또 돈을 내놓겠지. 아니면 감히 이 작은 사람을 괴롭히고 아프게 했다간 목줄기를 따 버릴 거야—\""]], [["‘Hush!’ interposed Madame.", "\"쉿!\" 마담이 끼어들었어."], ["‘Don’t you see?’", "\"안 보여요?\""]], [["Mr. Mantalini, who, in his eagerness to make up matters with his wife, had overlooked, or feigned to overlook, Miss Nickleby hitherto, took the hint, and laying his finger on his lip, sunk his voice still lower.", "만탈리니 씨는 아내와 화해하려는 데 정신이 팔려 지금까지 미스 니클비를 못 본 척, 혹은 정말로 못 봤는데, 이 눈치를 알아채고는 손가락을 입술에 갖다 대고 목소리를 더 낮췄어."], ["There was, then, a great deal of whispering, during which Madame Mantalini appeared to make reference, more than once, to certain debts incurred by Mr.", "그러고는 한참 동안 속닥속닥 소곤거렸지. 그러는 동안 마담 만탈리니는 자기와 결혼하기 전에 만탈리니 씨가 진 빚 얘기를 한 번이 아니라 여러 번 들먹이는 것 같았어."], ["Mantalini previous to her coverture; and also to an unexpected outlay of money in payment of the aforesaid debts; and furthermore, to certain agreeable weaknesses on that gentleman’s part, such as gaming, wasting, idling, and a tendency to horse-flesh; each of which matters of accusation Mr.", "또 그 빚을 갚느라 예상치 못하게 돈이 나간 얘기도 했지. 게다가 그 신사의 사랑스러운 약점들, 그러니까 도박이며, 낭비며, 빈둥거림이며, 경마에 빠지는 버릇까지 들먹였어."], ["Mantalini disposed of, by one kiss or more, as its relative importance demanded.", "그런데 만탈리니 씨는 이런 비난들을 하나하나, 그 경중에 따라 입맞춤 한 번이나 그 이상으로 다 처리해 버렸어."], ["The upshot of it all was, that Madame Mantalini was in raptures with him, and that they went upstairs to breakfast.", "결국 이 모든 일의 결말은, 마담 만탈리니가 그에게 홀딱 빠져 버렸다는 것, 그리고 둘이 위층으로 아침을 먹으러 올라갔다는 거였어."]], [["Kate busied herself in what she had to do, and was silently arranging the various articles of decoration in the best taste she could display, when she started to hear a strange man’s voice in the room, and started again, to observe, on looking round, that a white hat, and a red neckerchief, and a broad round face, and a large head, and part of a green coat were in the room too.", "케이트는 자기가 할 일에 몰두하며, 여러 장식품들을 자기가 낼 수 있는 최고의 안목으로 조용히 정리하고 있었어. 그런데 방 안에서 낯선 남자 목소리가 들려와 깜짝 놀랐지. 그리고 돌아보니, 흰 모자에 빨간 목수건에 넓적하고 둥근 얼굴에 커다란 머리에다, 초록색 코트 자락 일부가 방 안에 들어와 있는 걸 보고 또 한 번 놀랐어."]], [["‘Don’t alarm yourself, miss,’ said the proprietor of these appearances.", "\"놀라지 마세요, 아가씨.\" 이런 모습을 한 그 임자가 말했어."], ["‘I say; this here’s the mantie-making consarn, an’t it?’", "\"저기, 여기가 부인복 짓는 가게 맞죠?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ rejoined Kate, greatly astonished.", "\"네.\" 케이트가 몹시 놀라며 대꾸했어."], ["‘What did you want?’", "\"무슨 일이세요?\""]], [["The stranger answered not; but, first looking back, as though to beckon to some unseen person outside, came, very deliberately, into the room, and was closely followed by a little man in brown, very much the worse for wear, who brought with him a mingled fumigation of stale tobacco and fresh onions. The clothes of this gentleman were much bespeckled with flue; and his shoes, stockings, and nether garments, from his heels to the waist buttons of his coat inclusive, were profusely embroidered with splashes of mud, caught a fortnight previously--before the setting-in of the fine weather.", "낯선 남자는 대답하지 않았어. 그저 먼저 뒤를 돌아보더니, 밖에 있는 보이지 않는 누군가에게 손짓하는 듯하다가, 아주 느긋하게 방으로 들어왔어. 그 뒤를 갈색 옷을 입은 자그마한 남자가 바짝 따라 들어왔는데, 어찌나 후줄근한지 형편없었지. 이 남자는 묵은 담배 냄새와 갓 까낸 양파 냄새가 뒤섞인 악취를 풍기며 들어왔어. 이 신사의 옷에는 보푸라기가 잔뜩 묻어 있었지. 그리고 구두며 양말이며 아래쪽 옷가지들은, 발뒤꿈치부터 코트 허리 단추까지 죄다 진흙 얼룩이 잔뜩 수놓여 있었어. 보름 전에, 그러니까 날씨가 좋아지기 전에 튄 진흙이었지."]], [["Kate’s very natural impression was, that these engaging individuals had called with the view of possessing themselves, unlawfully, of any portable articles that chanced to strike their fancy.", "케이트는 아주 자연스럽게 이런 인상을 받았어. 이 매력적인 인간들이 눈에 드는 들고 갈 만한 물건이라면 뭐든지 슬쩍 가져갈 작정으로 찾아온 거라고 말이야."], ["She did not attempt to disguise her apprehensions, and made a move towards the door.", "그녀는 두려운 기색을 숨기려 들지도 않고, 문 쪽으로 발을 옮겼어."]], [["‘Wait a minnit,’ said the man in the green coat, closing it softly, and standing with his back against it.", "\"잠깐만요.\" 초록색 코트를 입은 남자가 문을 살며시 닫고 그 앞에 등을 기대고 서며 말했어."], ["‘This is a unpleasant bisness.", "\"이거 좀 거북한 일인데요."], ["Vere’s your govvernor?’", "댁의 영감님은 어디 계시오?\""]], [["‘My what--did you say?’ asked Kate, trembling; for she thought ‘governor’ might be slang for watch or money.", "\"제 뭐가—뭐라고 하셨어요?\" 케이트가 떨면서 물었어. '영감님'이라는 게 시계나 돈을 가리키는 은어일지도 모른다고 생각했거든."]], [["‘Mister Muntlehiney,’ said the man.", "\"먼틀하이니 씨 말이오.\" 남자가 말했어."], ["‘Wot’s come on him?", "\"그 양반 어찌 됐소?"], ["Is he at home?’", "집에 있소?\""]], [["‘He is above stairs, I believe,’ replied Kate, a little reassured by this inquiry.", "\"위층에 계실 거예요.\" 케이트가 이 질문에 조금 안심하며 대답했어."], ["‘Do you want him?’", "\"그분을 찾으세요?\""]], [["‘No,’ replied the visitor.", "\"아뇨.\" 방문객이 대답했어."], ["‘I don’t ezactly want him, if it’s made a favour on.", "\"꼭 그 양반을 찾는 건 아니오, 굳이 따지자면. 그냥 그 양반한테 이 명함이나 건네주쇼."], ["You can jist give him that ‘ere card, and tell him if he wants to speak to ME, and save trouble, here I am; that’s all.’", "그리고 만약에 나하고 얘기하고 싶고 일을 줄이고 싶거든, 내가 여기 있다고 전해 주쇼. 그게 다요.\""]], [["With these words, the stranger put a thick square card into Kate’s hand, and, turning to his friend, remarked, with an easy air, ‘that the rooms was a good high pitch;’ to which the friend assented, adding, by way of illustration, ‘that there was lots of room for a little boy to grow up a man in either on ‘em, vithout much fear of his ever bringing his head into contract vith the ceiling.’", "이 말과 함께 낯선 남자는 두툼한 네모 명함 한 장을 케이트의 손에 쥐여 줬어. 그러고는 친구 쪽으로 돌아서서 태평한 태도로 한마디 했지. \"방 천장이 꽤 높네.\" 그러자 친구도 맞장구를 치며, 예를 들어 이렇게 덧붙였어. \"어느 방이든 어린애 하나가 어른이 될 때까지 자라도 천장에 머리 부딪칠 걱정은 없겠어. 그만큼 공간이 넉넉하다 이거지.\""]], [["After ringing the bell which would summon Madame Mantalini, Kate glanced at the card, and saw that it displayed the name of ‘Scaley,’ together with some other information to which she had not had time to refer, when her attention was attracted by Mr. Scaley himself, who, walking up to one of the cheval-glasses, gave it a hard poke in the centre with his stick, as coolly as if it had been made of cast iron.", "케이트는 마담 만탈리니를 부를 종을 울린 다음 명함을 흘끗 봤어. 거기에는 '스케일리'라는 이름과 함께 다른 정보들도 적혀 있었지. 그런데 그걸 미처 다 읽기도 전에, 스케일리 씨한테 그녀의 시선이 쏠렸어. 그가 전신 거울 하나로 걸어가더니 한가운데를 지팡이로 푹 찔러 본 거야. 마치 그게 무쇠로 만들어지기라도 한 듯이 아주 태연하게 말이야."]], [["‘Good plate this here, Tix,’ said Mr.", "\"여기 이 유리 좋은데, 틱스.\""], ["Scaley to his friend.", "스케일리 씨가 친구에게 말했어."]], [["‘Ah!’ rejoined Mr.", "\"아!\""], ["Tix, placing the marks of his four fingers, and a duplicate impression of his thumb, on a piece of sky-blue silk; ‘and this here article warn’t made for nothing, mind you.’", "틱스 씨가 하늘색 비단에 손가락 네 개 자국과 엄지 자국 하나를 또렷이 찍으며 대꾸했어. \"그리고 이 물건도 거저 만든 게 아니란 말이지, 알아둬.\""]], [["From the silk, Mr. Tix transferred his admiration to some elegant articles of wearing apparel, while Mr.", "비단에서 틱스 씨의 감탄은 멋진 옷가지 몇 점으로 옮겨 갔어."], ["Scaley adjusted his neckcloth, at leisure, before the glass, and afterwards, aided by its reflection, proceeded to the minute consideration of a pimple on his chin; in which absorbing occupation he was yet engaged, when Madame Mantalini, entering the room, uttered an exclamation of surprise which roused him.", "한편 스케일리 씨는 거울 앞에서 느긋하게 목수건을 매만지더니, 그다음엔 거울에 비친 모습을 보며 턱에 난 여드름을 꼼꼼히 들여다보기 시작했어. 그렇게 거기에 푹 빠져 있는 와중에, 마담 만탈리니가 방으로 들어오면서 깜짝 놀라 소리를 질렀고, 그제야 그는 정신이 들었어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Is this the missis?’ inquired Scaley.", "이분이 마님이오?\" 스케일리가 물었어."]], [["‘It is Madame Mantalini,’ said Kate.", "\"마담 만탈리니세요.\" 케이트가 말했어."]], [["‘Then,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼,\" 스케일리 씨가 주머니에서 작은 서류를 꺼내 아주 천천히 펼치며 말했어."], ["Scaley, producing a small document from his pocket and unfolding it very slowly, ‘this is a writ of execution, and if it’s not conwenient to settle we’ll go over the house at wunst, please, and take the inwentory.’", "\"이건 집행 영장이오. 그리고 변제하기가 편치 않으시면, 우리가 당장 집을 둘러보고 재산 목록을 작성하겠소이다.\""]], [["Poor Madame Mantalini wrung her hands for grief, and rung the bell for her husband; which done, she fell into a chair and a fainting fit, simultaneously.", "가엾은 마담 만탈리니는 슬픔에 두 손을 비틀었어. 그러고는 남편을 부르려고 종을 울렸지."], ["The professional gentlemen, however, were not at all discomposed by this event, for Mr.", "그러고 나서 의자에 쓰러지는 동시에 기절해 버렸어."], ["Scaley, leaning upon a stand on which a handsome dress was displayed (so that his shoulders appeared above it, in nearly the same manner as the shoulders of the lady for whom it was designed would have done if she had had it on), pushed his hat on one side and scratched his head with perfect unconcern, while his friend Mr.", "하지만 이 일에도 그 직업적인 신사들은 조금도 동요하지 않았어. 스케일리 씨는 멋진 드레스가 걸려 있는 받침대에 기댔는데(그러니까 그 드레스를 입을 부인이 입었더라면 어깨가 솟아 보일 그 자리에, 딱 그 모양새로 그의 어깨가 솟아 있었지), 모자를 한쪽으로 밀어 올리고 아주 태연하게 머리를 긁적였어."], ["Tix, taking that opportunity for a general survey of the apartment preparatory to entering on business, stood with his inventory-book under his arm and his hat in his hand, mentally occupied in putting a price upon every object within his range of vision.", "한편 친구 틱스 씨는 그 틈을 타 일에 들어가기 전에 방 안을 한 바퀴 죽 둘러봤지. 재산 목록 장부를 겨드랑이에 끼고 모자를 손에 든 채로, 눈에 들어오는 물건마다 머릿속으로 값을 매기느라 바빴어."]], [["Such was the posture of affairs when Mr.", "만탈리니 씨가 허둥지둥 들어왔을 때 일이 이렇게 돌아가고 있었어."], ["Mantalini hurried in; and as that distinguished specimen had had a pretty extensive intercourse with Mr.", "이 잘난 표본은 총각 시절에 스케일리 씨 같은 부류와 꽤 폭넓게 어울려 봤거든."], ["Scaley’s fraternity in his bachelor days, and was, besides, very far from being taken by surprise on the present agitating occasion, he merely shrugged his shoulders, thrust his hands down to the bottom of his pockets, elevated his eyebrows, whistled a bar or two, swore an oath or two, and, sitting astride upon a chair, put the best face upon the matter with great composure and decency.", "게다가 이 골치 아픈 상황에서도 전혀 놀라지 않았지. 그저 어깨를 으쓱하고, 두 손을 주머니 깊숙이 찔러 넣고, 눈썹을 치켜올리고, 한두 소절 휘파람을 불고, 욕을 한두 마디 내뱉더니, 의자에 다리를 벌리고 걸터앉아 아주 침착하고 점잖게 사태에 최선의 얼굴을 들이밀었어."]], [["‘What’s the demd total?’ was the first question he asked.", "\"빌어먹을 총액이 얼마요?\" 그가 던진 첫 질문이었어."]], [["‘Fifteen hundred and twenty-seven pound, four and ninepence ha’penny,’ replied Mr.", "\"천오백이십칠 파운드 사 실링 구 펜스 반이올시다.\""], ["Scaley, without moving a limb.", "스케일리 씨가 손가락 하나 까딱하지 않고 대답했어."]], [["‘The halfpenny be demd,’ said Mr.", "\"반 펜스는 빌어먹게 떼 버리쇼.\""], ["Mantalini, impatiently.", "만탈리니 씨가 짜증스레 말했어."]], [["‘By all means if you vish it,’ retorted Mr.", "\"정 원하신다면 그러죠.\" 스케일리 씨가 받아쳤어."], ["Scaley; ‘and the ninepence.’", "\"구 펜스도요.\""]], [["‘It don’t matter to us if the fifteen hundred and twenty-seven pound went along with it, that I know on,’ observed Mr.", "\"천오백이십칠 파운드까지 같이 떼 줘도 우리한텐 상관없는 일이지요, 내가 아는 한은.\""], ["Tix.", "틱스 씨가 한마디 했어."]], [["‘Not a button,’ said Scaley.", "\"손톱만큼도 상관없지.\" 스케일리가 말했어."]], [["‘Well,’ said the same gentleman, after a pause, ‘wot’s to be done--anything?", "\"자,\" 그 신사가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말했어."], ["Is it only a small crack, or a out-and-out smash?", "\"그럼 어찌할 거요—뭐 방법이 있소? 살짝 금만 간 거요, 아니면 아주 완전히 거덜 난 거요?"], ["A break-up of the constitootion is it?--werry good.", "살림이 완전히 끝장난 거요?—아주 좋소. 그럼 토머스 틱스 나리, 당신은 천사 같은 마누라와 사랑스러운 식구들한테 알려야겠소."], ["Then Mr.", "앞으로 사흘 밤은 집에서 못 잔다고 말이오."], ["Tom Tix, esk-vire, you must inform your angel wife and lovely family as you won’t sleep at home for three nights to come, along of being in possession here.", "여기 들어앉아 지켜야 하니까."], ["Wot’s the good of the lady a fretting herself?’ continued Mr Scaley, as Madame Mantalini sobbed.", "마님이 저렇게 속을 끓여 봐야 무슨 소용이오?\" 스케일리 씨는 마담 만탈리니가 흐느끼자 말을 이었어."], ["‘A good half of wot’s here isn’t paid for, I des-say, and wot a consolation oughtn’t that to be to her feelings!’", "\"여기 있는 물건 절반은 아마 값도 안 치렀을 거요, 내 장담하리다. 그러니 그게 얼마나 위안이 되겠소!\""]], [["With these remarks, combining great pleasantry with sound moral encouragement under difficulties, Mr.", "이렇게 큰 익살과 함께 어려운 처지에 건전한 도덕적 격려까지 곁들인 말을 남기고서, 스케일리 씨는 재산 목록 작성에 들어갔어."], ["Scaley proceeded to take the inventory, in which delicate task he was materially assisted by the uncommon tact and experience of Mr. Tix, the broker.", "이 섬세한 작업에는 중개인 틱스 씨의 보기 드문 요령과 경험이 큰 도움이 됐지."]], [["‘My cup of happiness’s sweetener,’ said Mantalini, approaching his wife with a penitent air; ‘will you listen to me for two minutes?’", "\"내 행복의 잔을 달콤하게 해 주는 사람아.\" 만탈리니가 뉘우치는 기색으로 아내에게 다가가며 말했어. \"내 말 좀 이 분만 들어 주겠어?\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["don’t speak to me,’ replied his wife, sobbing.", "내게 말 걸지 마요.\" 그의 아내가 흐느끼며 대답했어."], ["‘You have ruined me, and that’s enough.’", "\"당신이 날 망쳐 놨어요. 그걸로 충분해요.\""]], [["Mr.", "만탈리니 씨는 분명 자기 배역을 미리 잘 짜 뒀던 모양이야."], ["Mantalini, who had doubtless well considered his part, no sooner heard these words pronounced in a tone of grief and severity, than he recoiled several paces, assumed an expression of consuming mental agony, rushed headlong from the room, and was, soon afterwards, heard to slam the door of an upstairs dressing-room with great violence.", "슬픔과 매서움이 담긴 이 말이 떨어지기가 무섭게, 그는 몇 걸음 뒤로 물러서더니, 속이 타들어 가는 듯한 표정을 짓고는, 방에서 쏜살같이 뛰쳐나갔어. 그러고는 곧이어 위층 화장실 문을 쾅 하고 거세게 닫는 소리가 들려왔지."]], [["‘Miss Nickleby,’ cried Madame Mantalini, when this sound met her ear, ‘make haste, for Heaven’s sake, he will destroy himself!", "\"니클비 양.\" 이 소리가 귀에 들려오자 마담 만탈리니가 외쳤어."], ["I spoke unkindly to him, and he cannot bear it from me.", "\"제발 빨리, 어서요. 저 사람이 자살할 거예요!"], ["Alfred, my darling Alfred.’", "내가 모질게 말했더니, 저 사람은 나한테서 그런 말을 못 견뎌요. 알프레드, 내 사랑하는 알프레드.\""]], [["With such exclamations, she hurried upstairs, followed by Kate who, although she did not quite participate in the fond wife’s apprehensions, was a little flurried, nevertheless.", "이렇게 외치며 그녀는 위층으로 허둥지둥 올라갔어. 케이트도 그 뒤를 따랐는데, 이 다정한 아내만큼 걱정하는 건 아니었지만 그래도 조금은 마음이 다급했지."], ["The dressing-room door being hastily flung open, Mr.", "화장실 문이 급히 벌컥 열리자 만탈리니 씨의 모습이 드러났어."], ["Mantalini was disclosed to view, with his shirt-collar symmetrically thrown back: putting a fine edge to a breakfast knife by means of his razor strop.", "셔츠 깃을 좌우 대칭으로 가지런히 젖힌 채, 면도 가죽띠에다 아침 식사용 칼을 슥슥 갈아 날을 세우고 있었지."]], [["‘Ah!’ cried Mr.", "\"아!\""], ["Mantalini, ‘interrupted!’ and whisk went the breakfast knife into Mr.", "만탈리니 씨가 외쳤어. \"방해받았군!\""], ["Mantalini’s dressing-gown pocket, while Mr.", "그러고는 그 아침 식사용 칼이 휙 하고 만탈리니 씨의 실내복 주머니 속으로 들어갔어."], ["Mantalini’s eyes rolled wildly, and his hair floating in wild disorder, mingled with his whiskers.", "만탈리니 씨의 두 눈은 마구 굴렀고, 머리카락은 제멋대로 흐트러져 수염과 뒤엉켜 있었지."]], [["‘Alfred,’ cried his wife, flinging her arms about him, ‘I didn’t mean to say it, I didn’t mean to say it!’", "\"알프레드.\" 그의 아내가 두 팔로 그를 끌어안으며 외쳤어. \"그런 말 할 생각은 아니었어요, 그런 말 할 생각은 정말 아니었다고요!\""]], [["‘Ruined!’ cried Mr.", "\"망했어!\" 만탈리니 씨가 외쳤어."], ["Mantalini. ‘Have I brought ruin upon the best and purest creature that ever blessed a demnition vagabond!", "\"이 빌어먹을 떠돌이한테 축복을 내려 준, 세상에서 가장 착하고 순수한 사람을 내가 망쳐 놨다니!"], ["Demmit, let me go.’ At this crisis of his ravings Mr.", "빌어먹을, 날 놔둬.\""], ["Mantalini made a pluck at the breakfast knife, and being restrained by his wife’s grasp, attempted to dash his head against the wall--taking very good care to be at least six feet from it.", "이렇게 미친 듯이 떠들어 대던 절정의 순간에, 만탈리니 씨는 아침 식사용 칼을 홱 잡아 빼려고 했어. 그러다 아내의 손에 붙들리자, 벽에다 머리를 들이받으려 했지—다만 적어도 벽에서 육 피트는 떨어져 있도록 아주 조심하면서 말이야."]], [["‘Compose yourself, my own angel,’ said Madame.", "\"진정해요, 내 천사.\" 마담이 말했어."], ["‘It was nobody’s fault; it was mine as much as yours, we shall do very well yet.", "\"누구 잘못도 아니에요. 당신 잘못만큼이나 내 잘못이기도 해요."], ["Come, Alfred, come.’", "우린 그래도 아주 잘 살 수 있어요. 자, 알프레드, 자.\""]], [["Mr. Mantalini did not think proper to come to, all at once; but, after calling several times for poison, and requesting some lady or gentleman to blow his brains out, gentler feelings came upon him, and he wept pathetically.", "만탈리니 씨는 단번에 정신을 차리는 게 좋겠다고는 생각하지 않았어."], ["In this softened frame of mind he did not oppose the capture of the knife--which, to tell the truth, he was rather glad to be rid of, as an inconvenient and dangerous article for a skirt pocket--and finally he suffered himself to be led away by his affectionate partner.", "몇 번이나 독약을 달라고 외치고, 어느 신사분이든 숙녀분이든 자기 머리를 쏴 달라고 부탁한 다음에야, 좀 부드러운 감정이 솟아나 가엾게 흐느껴 울었어. 이렇게 마음이 누그러진 상태에서 그는 칼을 빼앗기는 데도 저항하지 않았지—사실 솔직히 말하면, 치마 주머니에 넣고 다니기엔 거추장스럽고 위험한 물건이라 차라리 치워 버려 다행이라 여겼거든—그리고 마침내 다정한 아내에게 이끌려 자리를 떴어."]], [["After a delay of two or three hours, the young ladies were informed that their services would be dispensed with until further notice, and at the expiration of two days, the name of Mantalini appeared in the list of bankrupts: Miss Nickleby received an intimation per post, on the same morning, that the business would be, in future, carried on under the name of Miss Knag, and that her assistance would no longer be required--a piece of intelligence with which Mrs. Nickleby was no sooner made acquainted, than that good lady declared she had expected it all along and cited divers unknown occasions on which she had prophesied to that precise effect.", "두세 시간을 끈 끝에, 젊은 아가씨들은 다음 통보가 있을 때까지 일을 그만둬도 된다는 말을 들었어. 그리고 이틀이 지나자 만탈리니라는 이름이 파산자 명단에 올랐지. 미스 니클비는 같은 날 아침 우편으로 통지 한 장을 받았어. 앞으로 이 가게는 미스 내그라는 이름으로 운영될 거고, 그녀의 도움은 더 이상 필요 없다는 내용이었지. 니클비 부인은 이 소식을 듣자마자, 자기는 진작부터 이렇게 될 줄 알았다고 큰소리쳤어. 그러고는 자기가 바로 이런 일을 예언했던 알 수 없는 여러 경우를 줄줄이 들먹였지."]], [["‘And I say again,’ remarked Mrs.", "\"그러니까 내가 다시 말하지만,\" 니클비 부인이 한마디 했어(굳이 짚을 것도 없이, 부인은 전에 이런 말을 한 적이 한 번도 없었어)."], ["Nickleby (who, it is scarcely necessary to observe, had never said so before), ‘I say again, that a milliner’s and dressmaker’s is the very last description of business, Kate, that you should have thought of attaching yourself to.", "\"내가 다시 말하지만, 부인복점이나 양장점이라는 건, 케이트, 네가 몸담을 일로 가장 마지막에나 생각했어야 할 거야. 너를 나무라는 건 아니다, 얘야."], ["I don’t make it a reproach to you, my love; but still I will say, that if you had consulted your own mother--’", "그래도 이 말은 해야겠어. 네가 네 어미하고 상의만 했더라면—\""]], [["‘Well, well, mama,’ said Kate, mildly: ‘what would you recommend now?’", "\"알았어요, 알았어요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 부드럽게 말했어. \"그럼 이제 뭘 추천하시겠어요?\""]], [["‘Recommend!’ cried Mrs.", "\"추천이라니!\" 니클비 부인이 외쳤어."], ["Nickleby, ‘isn’t it obvious, my dear, that of all occupations in this world for a young lady situated as you are, that of companion to some amiable lady is the very thing for which your education, and manners, and personal appearance, and everything else, exactly qualify you?", "\"뻔하잖니, 얘야. 너 같은 처지의 젊은 아가씨한테 이 세상 모든 일 중에서, 점잖은 부인의 말동무가 되는 일이야말로 네 교육이며, 몸가짐이며, 외모며, 그 밖에 모든 걸로 봐서 딱 맞는 일이지 않니?"], ["Did you never hear your poor dear papa speak of the young lady who was the daughter of the old lady who boarded in the same house that he boarded in once, when he was a bachelor--what was her name again?", "너희 가엾은 아버지가 그 젊은 아가씨 얘기 하는 걸 못 들어 봤니? 네 아버지가 총각 시절에 하숙하던 그 집에서 같이 하숙하던 노부인의 딸 말이야—이름이 뭐였더라?"], ["I know it began with a B, and ended with g, but whether it was Waters or--no, it couldn’t have been that, either; but whatever her name was, don’t you know that that young lady went as companion to a married lady who died soon afterwards, and that she married the husband, and had one of the finest little boys that the medical man had ever seen--all within eighteen months?’", "B로 시작해서 g로 끝났던 건 아는데, 그게 워터스였나—아니, 그건 또 아니었을 거야. 아무튼 이름이야 뭐였든, 그 젊은 아가씨가 어느 유부녀의 말동무로 갔는데, 그 부인이 얼마 안 가 죽었고, 그 남편하고 결혼해서, 의사가 평생 본 것 중 가장 멋진 사내아이를 낳았다는 거 모르니—그게 다 일 년 반 만에 일어난 일이라잖아?\""]], [["Kate knew, perfectly well, that this torrent of favourable recollection was occasioned by some opening, real or imaginary, which her mother had discovered, in the companionship walk of life. She therefore waited, very patiently, until all reminiscences and anecdotes, bearing or not bearing upon the subject, had been exhausted, and at last ventured to inquire what discovery had been made. The truth then came out. Mrs Nickleby had, that morning, had a yesterday’s newspaper of the very first respectability from the public-house where the porter came from; and in this yesterday’s newspaper was an advertisement, couched in the purest and most grammatical English, announcing that a married lady was in want of a genteel young person as companion, and that the married lady’s name and address were to be known, on application at a certain library at the west end of the town, therein mentioned.", "케이트는 이 호의적인 추억담의 봇물이 어디서 터졌는지 잘 알았어. 엄마가 말동무라는 직업 쪽에서 실제든 상상이든 무슨 기회를 찾아낸 거였지. 그래서 케이트는 아주 참을성 있게 기다렸어. 주제에 들어맞든 안 맞든 온갖 추억담과 일화가 다 바닥날 때까지 말이야. 그러고는 마침내 무슨 발견을 했는지 조심스레 물어봤지. 그제야 진실이 드러났어. 니클비 부인은 그날 아침, 포터 맥주를 받아 오는 술집에서 어제 자 신문 한 부를 얻어 왔는데, 더없이 점잖은 신문이었지. 그리고 이 어제 자 신문에 광고 하나가 실려 있었어. 더없이 순수하고 문법에 딱 맞는 영어로 쓰인 광고였지. 어느 유부녀가 말동무로 점잖은 젊은 여성을 구한다는 내용이었어. 그리고 그 유부녀의 이름과 주소는 광고에 적힌, 시내 서쪽 끝의 어느 도서관에 문의하면 알 수 있다는 거였지."]], [["‘And I say,’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"그러니까 내가 하는 말이,\""], ["Nickleby, laying the paper down in triumph, ‘that if your uncle don’t object, it’s well worth the trial.’", "니클비 부인이 의기양양하게 신문을 내려놓으며 외쳤어. \"네 삼촌만 반대하지 않으면, 한번 해 볼 만한 일이라는 거다.\""]], [["Kate was too sick at heart, after the rough jostling she had already had with the world, and really cared too little at the moment what fate was reserved for her, to make any objection.", "케이트는 이미 세상과 거칠게 부대낀 뒤라 마음이 너무 지쳐 있었어. 게다가 그 순간엔 자기한테 어떤 운명이 닥칠지 정말로 별로 신경 쓰이지도 않아서, 반대할 마음이 없었지."], ["Mr.", "랄프 니클비 씨도 반대하지 않았어."], ["Ralph Nickleby offered none, but, on the contrary, highly approved of the suggestion; neither did he express any great surprise at Madame Mantalini’s sudden failure, indeed it would have been strange if he had, inasmuch as it had been procured and brought about chiefly by himself.", "오히려 그 제안을 크게 찬성했지. 마담 만탈리니가 갑자기 망한 일에도 별로 놀라는 기색이 없었어."], ["So, the name and address were obtained without loss of time, and Miss Nickleby and her mama went off in quest of Mrs.", "사실 놀랐다면 그게 이상한 일이었지. 그 일을 꾸미고 부추긴 게 대부분 그 자신이었으니까."], ["Wititterly, of Cadogan Place, Sloane Street, that same forenoon.", "그래서 이름과 주소는 지체 없이 알아냈고, 미스 니클비와 그녀의 엄마는 그날 오전 곧장 슬론 가 캐도건 플레이스에 사는 위티털리 부인을 찾아 나섰어."]], [["Cadogan Place is the one slight bond that joins two great extremes; it is the connecting link between the aristocratic pavements of Belgrave Square, and the barbarism of Chelsea.", "캐도건 플레이스는 두 극단을 잇는 단 하나의 가느다란 끈이야. 벨그레이브 광장의 귀족적인 보도와 첼시의 야만 사이를 이어 주는 연결 고리지."], ["It is in Sloane Street, but not of it.", "슬론 가에 있긴 하지만, 슬론 가에 속하지는 않아."], ["The people in Cadogan Place look down upon Sloane Street, and think Brompton low.", "캐도건 플레이스 사람들은 슬론 가를 깔보고, 브롬프턴은 천하다고 여기지. 그들도 유행을 흉내 내면서, 뉴 로드는 대체 어디 있느냐는 듯 굴어."], ["They affect fashion too, and wonder where the New Road is.", "그렇다고 벨그레이브 광장이며 그로스브너 플레이스의 상류층과 똑같은 처지라고 주장하는 건 아니야."], ["Not that they claim to be on precisely the same footing as the high folks of Belgrave Square and Grosvenor Place, but that they stand, with reference to them, rather in the light of those illegitimate children of the great who are content to boast of their connections, although their connections disavow them.", "다만 그들에 견주면, 명문가의 사생아 같은 위치에 서 있달까. 그러니까 자기 혈연을 자랑하는 데 만족하는 사생아들 말이야."], ["Wearing as much as they can of the airs and semblances of loftiest rank, the people of Cadogan Place have the realities of middle station.", "정작 그 혈연 쪽에서는 자기들을 모른 척하는데도 말이지. 캐도건 플레이스 사람들은 최고위 신분의 분위기와 겉모습을 할 수 있는 한 다 걸치고 다니지만, 실제로는 중간 계급의 처지야."], ["It is the conductor which communicates to the inhabitants of regions beyond its limit, the shock of pride of birth and rank, which it has not within itself, but derives from a fountain-head beyond; or, like the ligament which unites the Siamese twins, it contains something of the life and essence of two distinct bodies, and yet belongs to neither.", "캐도건 플레이스는 자기 안에는 없는 출신과 신분의 자랑이라는 충격을, 자기보다 더 높은 곳의 원천에서 끌어다가, 그 경계 너머에 사는 사람들에게 전해 주는 전도체 같은 거야. 아니면 샴쌍둥이를 잇는 인대처럼, 두 개의 별개 몸뚱이의 생명과 본질을 조금씩 품고 있으면서도, 정작 어느 쪽에도 속하지 않는 거지."]], [["Upon this doubtful ground, lived Mrs.", "이렇게 어정쩡한 땅에 위티털리 부인이 살았어. 그리고 위티털리 부인의 집 문을, 케이트 니클비는 떨리는 손으로 두드렸지."], ["Wititterly, and at Mrs.", "문을 연 건 머리에 밀가루를 뿌렸는지, 분필 칠을 했는지, 아무튼 무슨 식으로든 칠을 한(진짜 분 같지는 않았어) 덩치 큰 하인이었어."], ["Wititterly’s door Kate Nickleby knocked with trembling hand.", "그 덩치 큰 하인은 소개 명함을 받더니, 그걸 작은 사환에게 건넸지. 그 사환이 어찌나 작은지, 보통 차림으로는 사환 복장에 꼭 필요한 그 작은 단추들을 그 몸뚱이에 다 달 수가 없을 정도였어."], ["The door was opened by a big footman with his head floured, or chalked, or painted in some way (it didn’t look genuine powder), and the big footman, receiving the card of introduction, gave it to a little page; so little, indeed, that his body would not hold, in ordinary array, the number of small buttons which are indispensable to a page’s costume, and they were consequently obliged to be stuck on four abreast.", "그래서 단추들을 어쩔 수 없이 네 개씩 나란히 붙여 놓아야 했지. 이 젊은 신사는 쟁반에 명함을 받쳐 들고 위층으로 올라갔어."], ["This young gentleman took the card upstairs on a salver, and pending his return, Kate and her mother were shown into a dining-room of rather dirty and shabby aspect, and so comfortably arranged as to be adapted to almost any purpose rather than eating and drinking.", "그가 돌아오기를 기다리는 동안, 케이트와 엄마는 좀 지저분하고 허름해 보이는 식당으로 안내됐지. 그 방은 어찌나 안락하게 꾸며져 있던지, 먹고 마시는 일만 빼고는 거의 다른 무슨 용도에든 딱 맞아 보였어."]], [["Now, in the ordinary course of things, and according to all authentic descriptions of high life, as set forth in books, Mrs.", "자, 일이 평범하게 돌아갔다면, 그리고 책에 적힌 상류층 생활에 관한 온갖 믿을 만한 묘사대로라면, 위티털리 부인은 자기 규방에 있어야 마땅했어."], ["Wititterly ought to have been in her BOUDOIR; but whether it was that Mr. Wititterly was at that moment shaving himself in the BOUDOIR or what not, certain it is that Mrs.", "그런데 마침 위티털리 씨가 그 규방에서 면도를 하고 있었는지 어쨌는지, 아무튼 분명한 건 위티털리 부인은 응접실에서 손님을 맞이했다는 거야."], ["Wititterly gave audience in the drawing-room, where was everything proper and necessary, including curtains and furniture coverings of a roseate hue, to shed a delicate bloom on Mrs.", "거기엔 적절하고 필요한 게 다 갖춰져 있었지."], ["Wititterly’s complexion, and a little dog to snap at strangers’ legs for Mrs Wititterly’s amusement, and the afore-mentioned page, to hand chocolate for Mrs. Wititterly’s refreshment.", "위티털리 부인의 얼굴빛에 은은한 화색을 드리워 줄 장밋빛 커튼과 가구 덮개도 있었고, 위티털리 부인의 심심풀이로 낯선 이의 다리를 물어뜯을 작은 개도 있었고, 위티털리 부인의 기운을 돋워 줄 초콜릿을 받쳐 올릴 앞서 말한 그 사환도 있었어."]], [["The lady had an air of sweet insipidity, and a face of engaging paleness; there was a faded look about her, and about the furniture, and about the house.", "부인은 달콤하면서도 밋밋한 분위기에, 사람을 끄는 창백한 얼굴을 하고 있었어. 부인한테도, 가구한테도, 집한테도 어딘지 빛바랜 기색이 감돌았지."], ["She was reclining on a sofa in such a very unstudied attitude, that she might have been taken for an actress all ready for the first scene in a ballet, and only waiting for the drop curtain to go up.", "부인은 아주 꾸밈없는 자세로 소파에 비스듬히 기대 있었는데, 어찌나 그런지 발레의 첫 장면을 위해 만반의 준비를 마치고, 그저 막이 오르기만 기다리는 여배우로 봐도 될 정도였어."]], [["‘Place chairs.’", "\"의자를 내드려라.\""]], [["The page placed them.", "사환이 의자를 내놨어."]], [["‘Leave the room, Alphonse.’", "\"방에서 나가거라, 알퐁스.\""]], [["The page left it; but if ever an Alphonse carried plain Bill in his face and figure, that page was the boy.", "사환이 나갔어. 그런데 평범한 빌이라는 이름을 얼굴과 몸뚱이에 그대로 써 붙인 알퐁스가 세상에 있다면, 그 사환 소년이 바로 그랬지."]], [["‘I have ventured to call, ma’am,’ said Kate, after a few seconds of awkward silence, ‘from having seen your advertisement.’", "\"실례를 무릅쓰고 찾아왔어요, 부인.\" 케이트가 어색한 침묵이 몇 초 흐른 뒤에 말했어. \"광고를 보고요.\""]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Mrs.", "\"네.\""], ["Wititterly, ‘one of my people put it in the paper--Yes.’", "위티털리 부인이 대답했어. \"우리 집 사람 중 하나가 신문에 냈죠—네.\""]], [["‘I thought, perhaps,’ said Kate, modestly, ‘that if you had not already made a final choice, you would forgive my troubling you with an application.’", "\"혹시,\" 케이트가 조심스레 말했어. \"아직 최종 결정을 안 하셨다면, 제가 지원하느라 폐를 끼쳐도 너그러이 봐주시지 않을까 했어요.\""]], [["‘Yes,’ drawled Mrs.", "\"네.\""], ["Wititterly again.", "위티털리 부인이 또 느릿하게 말했어."]], [["‘If you have already made a selection--’", "\"이미 사람을 고르셨다면—\""]], [["‘Oh dear no,’ interrupted the lady, ‘I am not so easily suited.", "\"오 아니에요.\" 부인이 말을 끊었어."], ["I really don’t know what to say.", "\"난 그렇게 쉽게 맞는 사람을 못 찾아요."], ["You have never been a companion before, have you?’", "정말 뭐라 해야 할지 모르겠네. 말동무 일은 전에 해 본 적이 없죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["Mrs. Nickleby, who had been eagerly watching her opportunity, came dexterously in, before Kate could reply.", "니클비 부인은 기회를 호시탐탐 노리고 있다가, 케이트가 대답하기도 전에 능숙하게 끼어들었어."], ["‘Not to any stranger, ma’am,’ said the good lady; ‘but she has been a companion to me for some years.", "\"낯선 분 곁에서는 없어요, 부인.\" 이 착한 부인이 말했어."], ["I am her mother, ma’am.’", "\"하지만 몇 년 동안 제 말동무 노릇은 해 왔답니다. 제가 이 애 어미예요, 부인.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ said Mrs.", "\"오!\""], ["Wititterly, ‘I apprehend you.’", "위티털리 부인이 말했어. \"이해했어요.\""]], [["‘I assure you, ma’am,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘that I very little thought, at one time, that it would be necessary for my daughter to go out into the world at all, for her poor dear papa was an independent gentleman, and would have been at this moment if he had but listened in time to my constant entreaties and--’", "\"정말이지요, 부인.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"한때는 제 딸이 세상에 나가 일을 해야 할 거라곤 꿈에도 생각 못 했답니다. 이 애의 가엾은 아버지가 자기 재산이 있는 신사분이었거든요. 그리고 제가 늘 간곡히 부탁할 때 제때 귀를 기울이기만 했어도 지금까지 그랬을 텐데—\""]], [["‘Dear mama,’ said Kate, in a low voice.", "\"엄마.\" 케이트가 낮은 목소리로 말했어."]], [["‘My dear Kate, if you will allow me to speak,’ said Mrs.", "\"케이트야, 내가 말 좀 하게 해 주련.\""], ["Nickleby, ‘I shall take the liberty of explaining to this lady--’", "니클비 부인이 말했어. \"내가 실례를 무릅쓰고 이분께 설명을 좀—\""]], [["‘I think it is almost unnecessary, mama.’", "\"그건 거의 필요 없을 것 같아요, 엄마.\""]], [["And notwithstanding all the frowns and winks with which Mrs.", "니클비 부인은 이 일을 단번에 매듭지을 무슨 말을 하려 한다는 듯 온갖 눈살을 찌푸리고 눈짓을 보냈어."], ["Nickleby intimated that she was going to say something which would clench the business at once, Kate maintained her point by an expressive look, and for once Mrs.", "그래도 케이트는 의미심장한 눈빛으로 자기 뜻을 굽히지 않았지."], ["Nickleby was stopped upon the very brink of an oration.", "그래서 니클비 부인은 한 번쯤은 일장 연설을 막 시작하려던 바로 그 문턱에서 멈추고 말았어."]], [["‘What are your accomplishments?’ asked Mrs.", "\"무슨 재주가 있나요?\""], ["Wititterly, with her eyes shut.", "위티털리 부인이 눈을 감은 채 물었어."]], [["Kate blushed as she mentioned her principal acquirements, and Mrs Nickleby checked them all off, one by one, on her fingers; having calculated the number before she came out.", "케이트는 얼굴을 붉히며 자기가 익힌 주된 재주들을 말했어. 그리고 니클비 부인은 그것들을 하나하나 손가락으로 꼽으며 짚어 갔지."], ["Luckily the two calculations agreed, so Mrs.", "나오기 전에 그 개수를 미리 계산해 뒀거든."], ["Nickleby had no excuse for talking.", "다행히 두 계산이 맞아떨어져서, 니클비 부인은 또 입을 놀릴 핑계가 없었어."]], [["‘You are a good temper?’ asked Mrs.", "\"성격은 좋은가요?\""], ["Wititterly, opening her eyes for an instant, and shutting them again.", "위티털리 부인이 잠깐 눈을 떴다가 다시 감으며 물었어."]], [["‘I hope so,’ rejoined Kate.", "\"그러길 바라요.\" 케이트가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘And have a highly respectable reference for everything, have you?’", "\"그리고 모든 면에서 아주 점잖은 신원 보증인이 있나요?\""]], [["Kate replied that she had, and laid her uncle’s card upon the table.", "케이트는 있다고 대답하며, 삼촌의 명함을 탁자 위에 놓았어."]], [["‘Have the goodness to draw your chair a little nearer, and let me look at you,’ said Mrs.", "\"의자를 좀 더 가까이 당겨서, 내가 좀 볼 수 있게 해 줘요.\""], ["Wititterly; ‘I am so very nearsighted that I can’t quite discern your features.’", "위티털리 부인이 말했어. \"내가 근시가 아주 심해서, 얼굴이 잘 안 보이거든요.\""]], [["Kate complied, though not without some embarrassment, with this request, and Mrs.", "케이트는 좀 쑥스럽긴 했지만 이 부탁대로 의자를 당겼어."], ["Wititterly took a languid survey of her countenance, which lasted some two or three minutes.", "그러자 위티털리 부인은 나른하게 그녀의 얼굴을 살폈는데, 이게 한 이삼 분쯤 이어졌지."]], [["‘I like your appearance,’ said that lady, ringing a little bell.", "\"외모가 마음에 드는군요.\" 그 부인이 작은 종을 울리며 말했어."], ["‘Alphonse, request your master to come here.’", "\"알퐁스, 주인 어른더러 이리 오시라고 해라.\""]], [["The page disappeared on this errand, and after a short interval, during which not a word was spoken on either side, opened the door for an important gentleman of about eight-and-thirty, of rather plebeian countenance, and with a very light head of hair, who leant over Mrs Wititterly for a little time, and conversed with her in whispers.", "사환이 이 심부름을 하러 사라졌어. 그리고 잠깐 동안 양쪽 다 한마디도 없던 사이가 지난 뒤, 사환이 문을 열어 한 거물 신사를 들였지. 서른여덟쯤 된 신사였는데, 얼굴은 좀 평민 같았고, 머리숱은 아주 옅었어. 그는 잠시 위티털리 부인 위로 몸을 숙이고는, 속삭이며 그녀와 이야기를 나눴어."]], [["‘Oh!’ he said, turning round, ‘yes. This is a most important matter. Mrs Wititterly is of a very excitable nature; very delicate, very fragile; a hothouse plant, an exotic.’", "\"오!\" 그가 돌아서며 말했어. \"그래. 이건 아주 중요한 문제요. 위티털리 부인은 아주 예민한 성품이라서. 아주 섬세하고, 아주 연약하지. 온실 속 화초요, 외래종이라고.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Henry, my dear,’ interposed Mrs.", "헨리, 여보.\""], ["Wititterly.", "위티털리 부인이 끼어들었어."]], [["‘You are, my love, you know you are; one breath--’ said Mr.", "\"당신은 그렇잖소, 여보, 당신도 알면서. 숨 한 번이면—\""], ["W., blowing an imaginary feather away.", "위티털리 씨가 보이지 않는 깃털 하나를 후 불어 날리며 말했어."], ["‘Pho!", "\"휴!"], ["you’re gone!’", "당신은 가 버린다고!\""]], [["The lady sighed.", "부인이 한숨을 쉬었어."]], [["‘Your soul is too large for your body,’ said Mr.", "\"당신의 영혼은 몸에 비해 너무 크오.\" 위티털리 씨가 말했어."], ["Wititterly.", "\"당신의 지성이 당신을 닳게 하지."], ["‘Your intellect wears you out; all the medical men say so; you know that there is not a physician who is not proud of being called in to you.", "의사들도 다 그렇게 말하잖소. 당신한테 왕진 와 달라는 부름을 자랑스러워하지 않는 의사가 없다는 걸 당신도 알 거요."], ["What is their unanimous declaration?", "그들이 입을 모아 하는 말이 뭐겠소?"], ["“My dear doctor,”", "'선생,'"], ["said I to Sir Tumley Snuffim, in this very room, the very last time he came.", "내가 바로 이 방에서, 텀리 스너핌 경이 마지막으로 왔을 때 그에게 말했지. '선생, 내 아내의 병이 대체 뭐요?"], ["“My dear doctor, what is my wife’s complaint?", "다 말해 주시오. 나는 견딜 수 있소."], ["Tell me all.", "신경이오?'"], ["I can bear it.", "'여보게,'"], ["Is it nerves?”", "그가 말했지."], ["“My dear fellow,”", "'그 부인을 자랑스러워하게."], ["he said, “be proud of that woman; make much of her; she is an ornament to the fashionable world, and to you.", "그분을 소중히 여기게. 그분은 사교계의 보배이자, 자네의 보배일세."], ["Her complaint is soul.", "그분의 병은 영혼이라네."], ["It swells, expands, dilates--the blood fires, the pulse quickens, the excitement increases--Whew!”’ Here Mr.", "그게 부풀고, 팽창하고, 늘어나면서—피가 끓고, 맥박이 빨라지고, 흥분이 치솟고—휴!'\""], ["Wititterly, who, in the ardour of his description, had flourished his right hand to within something less than an inch of Mrs. Nickleby’s bonnet, drew it hastily back again, and blew his nose as fiercely as if it had been done by some violent machinery.", "여기서 위티털리 씨는, 묘사에 열을 올린 나머지 오른손을 휘두르다 니클비 부인의 모자에서 채 한 치도 안 되는 데까지 갖다 댔다가, 황급히 다시 거둬들이고는, 무슨 사나운 기계로 푼 것처럼 거세게 코를 풀었어."]], [["‘You make me out worse than I am, Henry,’ said Mrs.", "\"당신은 날 실제보다 더 심하게 만드네요, 헨리.\""], ["Wititterly, with a faint smile.", "위티털리 부인이 희미하게 미소 지으며 말했어."]], [["‘I do not, Julia, I do not,’ said Mr. W.", "\"아니오, 줄리아, 아니오.\""], ["‘The society in which you move--necessarily move, from your station, connection, and endowments--is one vortex and whirlpool of the most frightful excitement.", "위티털리 씨가 말했어. \"당신이 드나드는—당신의 신분이며, 인맥이며, 타고난 재능 때문에 어쩔 수 없이 드나드는—사교계는 가장 무시무시한 흥분의 소용돌이이자 회오리란 말이오."], ["Bless my heart and body, can I ever forget the night you danced with the baronet’s nephew at the election ball, at Exeter!", "맙소사, 엑서터의 선거 무도회에서 당신이 준남작의 조카와 춤춘 그날 밤을 내가 어찌 잊겠소!"], ["It was tremendous.’", "정말 대단했지.\""]], [["‘I always suffer for these triumphs afterwards,’ said Mrs.", "\"난 그런 영광스러운 일을 치르고 나면 늘 뒤에 고생을 해요.\""], ["Wititterly.", "위티털리 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘And for that very reason,’ rejoined her husband, ‘you must have a companion, in whom there is great gentleness, great sweetness, excessive sympathy, and perfect repose.’", "\"바로 그 이유 때문에,\" 그녀의 남편이 대꾸했어. \"당신한테는 더없이 온화하고, 더없이 다정하고, 한없이 공감해 주고, 완벽하게 차분한 말동무가 있어야 하는 거요.\""]], [["Here, both Mr. and Mrs.", "여기서 위티털리 씨 부부는—두 사람은 서로한테라기보다는 니클비 모녀를 향해 이야기하고 있었지—말을 멈추고는, 두 청중을 바라봤어."], ["Wititterly, who had talked rather at the Nicklebys than to each other, left off speaking, and looked at their two hearers, with an expression of countenance which seemed to say, ‘What do you think of all this?’", "'이 모든 게 어떠세요?' 하고 말하는 듯한 표정으로 말이야."]], [["‘Mrs. Wititterly,’ said her husband, addressing himself to Mrs.", "\"위티털리 부인은,\""], ["Nickleby, ‘is sought after and courted by glittering crowds and brilliant circles.", "그녀의 남편이 니클비 부인을 향해 말했어. \"번쩍이는 무리와 화려한 모임들이 모셔 가려 하고 떠받드는 사람이오."], ["She is excited by the opera, the drama, the fine arts, the--the--the--’", "부인은 오페라며, 연극이며, 미술이며, 그—그—그—\""]], [["‘The nobility, my love,’ interposed Mrs.", "\"귀족들이죠, 여보.\""], ["Wititterly.", "위티털리 부인이 끼어들었어."]], [["‘The nobility, of course,’ said Mr.", "\"물론, 귀족들이지.\" 위티털리 씨가 말했어."], ["Wititterly. ‘And the military.", "\"그리고 군인들도."], ["She forms and expresses an immense variety of opinions on an immense variety of subjects.", "부인은 어마어마하게 다양한 주제에 대해 어마어마하게 다양한 견해를 짓고 또 펼치지."], ["If some people in public life were acquainted with Mrs Wititterly’s real opinion of them, they would not hold their heads, perhaps, quite as high as they do.’", "공직에 있는 어떤 사람들이 위티털리 부인이 자기들을 정말 어떻게 보는지 안다면, 아마 지금처럼 그렇게 고개를 빳빳이 들고 다니진 못할 거요.\""]], [["‘Hush, Henry,’ said the lady; ‘this is scarcely fair.’", "\"쉿, 헨리.\" 부인이 말했어. \"이건 좀 온당치 못해요.\""]], [["‘I mention no names, Julia,’ replied Mr.", "\"이름은 대지 않겠소, 줄리아.\" 위티털리 씨가 대답했어."], ["Wititterly; ‘and nobody is injured.", "\"그러니 다칠 사람은 아무도 없지."], ["I merely mention the circumstance to show that you are no ordinary person, that there is a constant friction perpetually going on between your mind and your body; and that you must be soothed and tended.", "난 그저 당신이 평범한 사람이 아니라는 걸, 당신의 마음과 몸 사이에 끊임없이 마찰이 일어나고 있다는 걸, 그러니 당신은 어루만져 주고 돌봐 줄 사람이 필요하다는 걸 보여 주려고 이 일을 언급한 것뿐이오."], ["Now let me hear, dispassionately and calmly, what are this young lady’s qualifications for the office.’", "자, 이제 차분하고 냉정하게 들려주시오. 이 젊은 아가씨가 그 일에 어떤 자질을 갖췄는지 말이오.\""]], [["In obedience to this request, the qualifications were all gone through again, with the addition of many interruptions and cross-questionings from Mr.", "이 요청에 따라, 자질들을 처음부터 다시 죽 늘어놓았어. 거기다 위티털리 씨의 숱한 끼어듦과 꼬치꼬치 캐묻는 질문까지 덧붙여서 말이지."], ["Wititterly. It was finally arranged that inquiries should be made, and a decisive answer addressed to Miss Nickleby under cover of her uncle, within two days.", "결국, 신원 조회를 해 본 다음, 이틀 안에 미스 니클비에게 삼촌 앞으로 결정적인 답을 보내기로 합의됐어."], ["These conditions agreed upon, the page showed them down as far as the staircase window; and the big footman, relieving guard at that point, piloted them in perfect safety to the street-door.", "이런 조건들에 합의가 되자, 사환이 계단 창문 있는 데까지 그들을 안내했어. 그리고 그 지점에서 보초를 교대한 덩치 큰 하인이, 현관문까지 그들을 무사히 인도했지."]], [["‘They are very distinguished people, evidently,’ said Mrs.", "\"보아하니 아주 지체 높은 사람들이구나.\""], ["Nickleby, as she took her daughter’s arm.", "니클비 부인이 딸의 팔짱을 끼며 말했어."], ["‘What a superior person Mrs. Wititterly is!’", "\"위티털리 부인은 정말 우월한 분이지 뭐냐!\""]], [["‘Do you think so, mama?’ was all Kate’s reply.", "\"그렇게 생각하세요, 엄마?\" 케이트의 대답은 이게 다였어."]], [["‘Why, who can help thinking so, Kate, my love?’ rejoined her mother.", "\"아니, 어떻게 그렇게 생각 안 할 수가 있겠니, 케이트야?\" 엄마가 대꾸했어."], ["‘She is pale though, and looks much exhausted.", "\"물론 얼굴은 창백하고, 무척 지쳐 보이긴 하더라."], ["I hope she may not be wearing herself out, but I am very much afraid.’", "부디 몸이 닳도록 무리하는 게 아니어야 할 텐데, 아무래도 걱정이구나.\""]], [["These considerations led the deep-sighted lady into a calculation of the probable duration of Mrs.", "이런 생각이 이 통찰력 깊은 부인을 한 가지 계산으로 이끌었어. 위티털리 부인이 앞으로 얼마나 더 살까, 그리고 그 슬픔에 잠긴 홀아비가 자기 딸에게 청혼할 가능성은 얼마나 될까 하는 계산이었지."], ["Wititterly’s life, and the chances of the disconsolate widower bestowing his hand on her daughter.", "집에 닿기도 전에, 부인은 위티털리 부인의 영혼을 온갖 육체의 속박에서 풀어 주었고, 케이트를 하노버 광장의 세인트 조지 성당에서 아주 성대하게 결혼시켰어."], ["Before reaching home, she had freed Mrs.", "그리고 딱 하나 사소한 문제만 결정을 못 내렸지."], ["Wititterly’s soul from all bodily restraint; married Kate with great splendour at St George’s, Hanover Square; and only left undecided the minor question, whether a splendid French-polished mahogany bedstead should be erected for herself in the two-pair back of the house in Cadogan Place, or in the three-pair front: between which apartments she could not quite balance the advantages, and therefore adjusted the question at last, by determining to leave it to the decision of her son-in-law.", "자기를 위한 프랑스식으로 광을 낸 멋진 마호가니 침대를 캐도건 플레이스 집 뒤편 삼 층에 놓을까, 아니면 앞쪽 사 층에 놓을까 하는 문제였어. 이 두 방의 장점을 도무지 저울질하지 못해서, 결국 이 문제는 사위의 결정에 맡기기로 하고 매듭지었지."]], [["The inquiries were made.", "신원 조회가 이루어졌어."], ["The answer--not to Kate’s very great joy--was favourable; and at the expiration of a week she betook herself, with all her movables and valuables, to Mrs.", "그 답은—케이트한테는 그리 큰 기쁨이 아니었지만—호의적이었지. 그리고 일주일이 지나자 케이트는 자기 짐이며 귀중품을 다 챙겨 위티털리 부인의 저택으로 갔어."], ["Wititterly’s mansion, where for the present we will leave her.", "우리는 일단 그녀를 거기 남겨 두기로 하자."]], [["# CHAPTER 22", "# CHAPTER 22"]], [["Nicholas, accompanied by Smike, sallies forth to seek his Fortune. He encounters Mr.", "니콜라스가 집세를 치르고, 그 초라한 가구를 빌려 준 중개인과 셈을 마치고 나니, 손에 쥔 돈이든 앞으로 들어올 돈이든, 그가 가질 권리가 있는 전 재산은 다 합쳐 봐야 이십 실링에서 반 페니 몇 푼 더 되는 게 고작이었어."], ["Vincent Crummles; and who he was, is herein made manifest\nThe whole capital which Nicholas found himself entitled to, either in possession, reversion, remainder, or expectancy, after paying his rent and settling with the broker from whom he had hired his poor furniture, did not exceed, by more than a few halfpence, the sum of twenty shillings.", "그런데도 그는 런던을 떠나기로 마음먹은 그날 아침을 가벼운 마음으로 맞았어. 그리고 톡톡 튀는 기운으로 침대에서 벌떡 일어났지."], ["And yet he hailed the morning on which he had resolved to quit London, with a light heart, and sprang from his bed with an elasticity of spirit which is happily the lot of young persons, or the world would never be stocked with old ones.", "이런 활기야말로 젊은 사람들이 누리는 다행스러운 복이야. 안 그랬으면 이 세상에 늙은이가 채워질 일도 없었을 테니까."]], [["It was a cold, dry, foggy morning in early spring. A few meagre shadows flitted to and fro in the misty streets, and occasionally there loomed through the dull vapour, the heavy outline of some hackney coach wending homewards, which, drawing slowly nearer, rolled jangling by, scattering the thin crust of frost from its whitened roof, and soon was lost again in the cloud. At intervals were heard the tread of slipshod feet, and the chilly cry of the poor sweep as he crept, shivering, to his early toil; the heavy footfall of the official watcher of the night, pacing slowly up and down and cursing the tardy hours that still intervened between him and sleep; the rambling of ponderous carts and waggons; the roll of the lighter vehicles which carried buyers and sellers to the different markets; the sound of ineffectual knocking at the doors of heavy sleepers--all these noises fell upon the ear from time to time, but all seemed muffled by the fog, and to be rendered almost as indistinct to the ear as was every object to the sight. The sluggish darkness thickened as the day came on; and those who had the courage to rise and peep at the gloomy street from their curtained windows, crept back to bed again, and coiled themselves up to sleep.", "초봄의 춥고 메마른, 안개 낀 아침이었어. 안개 자욱한 거리에는 야윈 그림자 몇이 이리저리 어른거렸어. 그리고 가끔씩 그 칙칙한 안개 속으로 집으로 돌아가는 삯마차의 묵직한 윤곽이 어렴풋이 떠올랐지. 마차는 천천히 가까이 다가오더니, 덜커덩거리며 굴러 지나갔어. 하얗게 서리 앉은 지붕에서 얇은 서리 껍질을 흩뿌리면서 말이야. 그러고는 이내 다시 안개 속으로 사라져 버렸지. 이따금 질질 끄는 발소리가 들렸어. 그리고 가난한 굴뚝 청소부가 떨면서, 이른 일을 하러 기어 나오며 내지르는 추운 외침 소리도 들렸지. 밤을 지키는 야경꾼의 묵직한 발소리도 났어. 그는 천천히 오르락내리락 걸으며, 잠들기 전까지 아직 남은 더딘 시간을 욕했어. 육중한 짐수레와 마차들이 덜컹거리는 소리, 시장마다 사고파는 사람들을 실어 나르는 가벼운 수레들이 굴러가는 소리도 났어. 곤히 잠든 이들의 문을 두드려 봐야 헛수고인 소리도 났지. 이런 온갖 소리가 때때로 귓가에 닿았어. 하지만 죄다 안개에 파묻힌 듯했지. 그래서 눈에 보이는 모든 것이 흐릿하듯, 귀에 들리는 모든 것도 거의 그만큼 분간하기 어려웠어. 날이 밝아 오면서 굼뜬 어둠은 오히려 짙어졌어. 그리고 용기를 내어 일어나 커튼 친 창문 너머로 음울한 거리를 들여다본 사람들은, 다시 슬그머니 침대로 기어들어 몸을 웅크리고 잠을 청했어."]], [["Before even these indications of approaching morning were rife in busy London, Nicholas had made his way alone to the city, and stood beneath the windows of his mother’s house.", "바쁜 런던에 이런 아침의 기척이 채 퍼지기도 전에, 니콜라스는 혼자서 시내로 들어와 어머니가 사는 집 창문 아래 섰어. 칙칙하고 휑한 모습이었지."], ["It was dull and bare to see, but it had light and life for him; for there was at least one heart within its old walls to which insult or dishonour would bring the same blood rushing, that flowed in his own veins.", "하지만 그에게는 빛과 생기가 있는 곳이었어. 그 낡은 벽 안에는 적어도 하나, 모욕이나 치욕을 당하면 그의 핏줄에 흐르는 것과 똑같은 피가 솟구쳐 오를 심장이 있었으니까."]], [["He crossed the road, and raised his eyes to the window of the room where he knew his sister slept.", "그는 길을 건너 여동생이 자는 줄로 아는 방 창문으로 눈을 들었어. 창은 닫혀 있고 어두웠지."], ["It was closed and dark.", "'가엾은 것.'"], ["‘Poor girl,’ thought Nicholas, ‘she little thinks who lingers here!’", "니콜라스는 생각했어. '누가 여기 서성이고 있는지도 모르겠지!'"]], [["He looked again, and felt, for the moment, almost vexed that Kate was not there to exchange one word at parting. ‘Good God!’ he thought, suddenly correcting himself, ‘what a boy I am!’", "그는 다시 한 번 올려다봤어. 그리고 잠깐이지만, 케이트가 거기 없어서 작별 인사 한마디 나눌 수 없다는 게 거의 야속하게 느껴졌어. '맙소사!' 그는 퍼뜩 스스로를 다잡으며 생각했지. '나도 참 어린애구나!'"]], [["‘It is better as it is,’ said Nicholas, after he had lounged on, a few paces, and returned to the same spot. ‘When I left them before, and could have said goodbye a thousand times if I had chosen, I spared them the pain of leave-taking, and why not now?’ As he spoke, some fancied motion of the curtain almost persuaded him, for the instant, that Kate was at the window, and by one of those strange contradictions of feeling which are common to us all, he shrunk involuntarily into a doorway, that she might not see him. He smiled at his own weakness; said ‘God bless them!’ and walked away with a lighter step.", "\"이게 나아.\" 니콜라스는 몇 걸음 어슬렁어슬렁 걷다가 같은 자리로 되돌아와 말했어. \"전에 식구들을 떠날 때도, 마음만 먹었으면 작별 인사를 천 번이라도 할 수 있었지만, 헤어지는 아픔을 덜어 주었잖아. 그러니 지금이라고 안 될 게 뭐람?\" 그가 이렇게 말하는데, 커튼이 움직이는 듯한 착각이 잠깐 그를 사로잡았어. 케이트가 창가에 있는 것만 같았지. 그러자 우리 모두에게 흔한, 그 묘하게 어긋나는 감정 가운데 하나로, 그는 저도 모르게 출입구 안으로 몸을 숨겼어. 케이트가 자기를 보지 못하도록 말이야. 그는 자신의 약한 마음에 피식 웃었어. 그러고는 \"신의 가호가 있기를!\" 하고 말한 뒤, 한결 가벼운 걸음으로 걸어갔지."]], [["Smike was anxiously expecting him when he reached his old lodgings, and so was Newman, who had expended a day’s income in a can of rum and milk to prepare them for the journey. They had tied up the luggage, Smike shouldered it, and away they went, with Newman Noggs in company; for he had insisted on walking as far as he could with them, overnight.", "니콜라스가 옛 하숙집에 닿았을 때 스마이크가 애타게 그를 기다리고 있었어. 뉴먼도 마찬가지였지. 뉴먼은 그들이 길을 떠날 채비를 하라고, 하루치 벌이를 럼주와 우유 한 통에 다 써 버렸어. 그들은 짐을 꾸려 묶었어. 스마이크가 그걸 둘러멨고, 셋은 길을 나섰지. 뉴먼 노그스도 함께였어. 전날 밤, 그가 갈 수 있는 데까지 같이 걷겠다고 우겼거든."]], [["‘Which way?’ asked Newman, wistfully.", "\"어느 쪽으로 가나?\" 뉴먼이 아쉬운 듯 물었어."]], [["‘To Kingston first,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"우선 킹스턴으로.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘And where afterwards?’ asked Newman.", "\"그다음엔 어디로?\" 뉴먼이 물었어."], ["‘Why won’t you tell me?’", "\"왜 말을 안 해 주는 거야?\""]], [["‘Because I scarcely know myself, good friend,’ rejoined Nicholas, laying his hand upon his shoulder; ‘and if I did, I have neither plan nor prospect yet, and might shift my quarters a hundred times before you could possibly communicate with me.’", "\"나도 잘 모르거든, 친구.\" 니콜라스가 그의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 대꾸했어. \"안다 해도, 아직 계획도 전망도 없어. 자네가 연락할 새도 없이 거처를 백 번은 옮길지도 모르고.\""]], [["‘I am afraid you have some deep scheme in your head,’ said Newman, doubtfully.", "\"자네 머릿속에 무슨 깊은 꿍꿍이가 있는 것 같은데.\" 뉴먼이 미심쩍은 듯 말했어."]], [["‘So deep,’ replied his young friend, ‘that even I can’t fathom it.", "\"어찌나 깊은지,\" 젊은 친구가 대답했어."], ["Whatever I resolve upon, depend upon it I will write you soon.’", "\"나조차도 그 속을 못 헤아릴 정도야. 무얼 결정하든, 곧 편지하겠으니 믿어 줘.\""]], [["‘You won’t forget?’ said Newman.", "\"잊으면 안 돼.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."]], [["‘I am not very likely to,’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"잊을 리가 없지.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어."], ["‘I have not so many friends that I shall grow confused among the number, and forget my best one.’", "\"친구가 그렇게 많아서 그 수에 헷갈려 가장 좋은 친구를 잊을 처지도 못 되거든.\""]], [["Occupied in such discourse, they walked on for a couple of hours, as they might have done for a couple of days if Nicholas had not sat himself down on a stone by the wayside, and resolutely declared his intention of not moving another step until Newman Noggs turned back. Having pleaded ineffectually first for another half-mile, and afterwards for another quarter, Newman was fain to comply, and to shape his course towards Golden Square, after interchanging many hearty and affectionate farewells, and many times turning back to wave his hat to the two wayfarers when they had become mere specks in the distance.", "이런 이야기를 나누며 그들은 두어 시간을 걸었어. 니콜라스가 길가 돌 위에 주저앉지만 않았더라면, 이틀이라도 그렇게 걸었을지 몰라. 그는 뉴먼 노그스가 돌아가기 전에는 한 발짝도 더 움직이지 않겠다고 단호히 못 박았지. 뉴먼은 처음엔 반 마일만 더, 그다음엔 사분의 일 마일만 더 가자고 졸랐지만 소용없었어. 그래서 하는 수 없이 따랐지. 마음에서 우러난 다정한 작별 인사를 몇 번이고 주고받은 뒤, 그는 골든 스퀘어 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸어. 두 나그네가 멀리 점처럼 작아질 때까지 몇 번이고 돌아서서 모자를 흔들면서 말이야."]], [["‘Now listen to me, Smike,’ said Nicholas, as they trudged with stout hearts onwards.", "\"자, 내 말 좀 들어 봐, 스마이크.\" 둘이 씩씩한 마음으로 터벅터벅 앞으로 나아가며 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘We are bound for Portsmouth.’", "\"우리는 포츠머스로 가는 거야.\""]], [["Smike nodded his head and smiled, but expressed no other emotion; for whether they had been bound for Portsmouth or Port Royal would have been alike to him, so they had been bound together.", "스마이크는 고개를 끄덕이며 웃었어. 하지만 그 밖에 다른 감정은 드러내지 않았지. 포츠머스로 가든 포트 로열로 가든, 그에게는 다 똑같았으니까. 둘이 함께 가기만 한다면 말이야."]], [["‘I don’t know much of these matters,’ resumed Nicholas; ‘but Portsmouth is a seaport town, and if no other employment is to be obtained, I should think we might get on board some ship.", "\"나도 이런 일을 잘 아는 건 아니야.\" 니콜라스가 말을 이었어."], ["I am young and active, and could be useful in many ways.", "\"하지만 포츠머스는 항구 도시야. 다른 일자리를 못 구하면 어느 배에든 탈 수 있지 않을까 싶어."], ["So could you.’", "나는 젊고 몸도 잰 편이니까, 여러모로 쓸모가 있을 거야. 너도 그렇고.\""]], [["‘I hope so,’ replied Smike.", "\"그랬으면 좋겠어.\" 스마이크가 대답했어."], ["‘When I was at that--you know where I mean?’", "\"내가 그… 어딘지 알지?\""]], [["‘Yes, I know,’ said Nicholas.", "\"그래, 알아.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘You needn’t name the place.’", "\"그곳 이름은 말할 것 없어.\""]], [["‘Well, when I was there,’ resumed Smike; his eyes sparkling at the prospect of displaying his abilities; ‘I could milk a cow, and groom a horse, with anybody.’", "\"그러니까, 내가 거기 있을 때,\" 스마이크가 말을 이었어. 제 솜씨를 뽐낼 생각에 눈을 반짝이면서 말이야. \"나는 누구 못지않게 소젖도 짜고, 말도 손질할 수 있었거든.\""]], [["‘Ha!’ said Nicholas, gravely.", "\"허!\" 니콜라스가 점잖게 말했어."], ["‘I am afraid they don’t keep many animals of either kind on board ship, Smike, and even when they have horses, that they are not very particular about rubbing them down; still you can learn to do something else, you know.", "\"배 위에선 그런 짐승을 많이 안 키울걸, 스마이크. 말이 있다 해도 빗질에 그리 까다롭지도 않을 거고."], ["Where there’s a will, there’s a way.’", "그래도 다른 일을 배우면 되잖아. 뜻이 있는 곳에 길이 있는 법이니까.\""]], [["‘And I am very willing,’ said Smike, brightening up again.", "\"나는 정말 마음은 굴뚝같아.\" 스마이크가 다시 환하게 밝아지며 말했어."]], [["‘God knows you are,’ rejoined Nicholas; ‘and if you fail, it shall go hard but I’ll do enough for us both.’", "\"네 마음이야 하늘이 알지.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어. \"혹시 네가 안 되더라도, 어지간하면 내가 우리 둘 몫을 다 해낼 테니까.\""]], [["‘Do we go all the way today?’ asked Smike, after a short silence.", "\"오늘 다 가는 거야?\" 잠시 잠잠하다가 스마이크가 물었어."]], [["‘That would be too severe a trial, even for your willing legs,’ said Nicholas, with a good-humoured smile.", "\"그건 네 부지런한 다리에도 너무 가혹한 시험이지.\" 니콜라스가 사람 좋게 웃으며 말했어."], ["‘No.", "\"아니야."], ["Godalming is some thirty and odd miles from London--as I found from a map I borrowed--and I purpose to rest there.", "고댈밍이 런던에서 삼십 몇 마일쯤 돼. 빌린 지도를 보고 알았지."], ["We must push on again tomorrow, for we are not rich enough to loiter.", "거기서 쉴 작정이야. 내일 다시 부지런히 가야 해."], ["Let me relieve you of that bundle!", "우리는 어슬렁댈 만큼 넉넉하지가 않으니까. 그 짐은 내가 좀 들게!"], ["Come!’", "이리 줘!\""]], [["‘No, no,’ rejoined Smike, falling back a few steps.", "\"아니, 아니.\" 스마이크가 몇 걸음 물러서며 대꾸했어."], ["‘Don’t ask me to give it up to you.’", "\"그걸 달라고 하지 마.\""]], [["‘Why not?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"왜 안 돼?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘Let me do something for you, at least,’ said Smike.", "\"적어도 너한테 뭐라도 하게 해 줘.\" 스마이크가 말했어."], ["‘You will never let me serve you as I ought.", "\"넌 내가 마땅히 해야 할 시중조차 들게 해 주질 않잖아."], ["You will never know how I think, day and night, of ways to please you.’", "내가 밤낮으로 어떻게 하면 너를 기쁘게 할까 궁리하는지 넌 영영 모를 거야.\""]], [["‘You are a foolish fellow to say it, for I know it well, and see it, or I should be a blind and senseless beast,’ rejoined Nicholas. ‘Let me ask you a question while I think of it, and there is no one by,’ he added, looking him steadily in the face. ‘Have you a good memory?’", "\"그런 말을 하다니 바보 같긴. 나도 잘 알고 다 보고 있는걸. 안 그러면 내가 눈멀고 분별없는 짐승이게?\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어. \"생각난 김에, 그리고 옆에 아무도 없으니 한 가지 물어볼게.\" 그는 스마이크의 얼굴을 가만히 들여다보며 덧붙였어. \"너 기억력이 좋니?\""]], [["‘I don’t know,’ said Smike, shaking his head sorrowfully. ‘I think I had once; but it’s all gone now--all gone.’", "\"모르겠어.\" 스마이크가 슬픈 듯 고개를 저으며 말했어. \"옛날엔 좋았던 것 같아. 그런데 지금은 다 사라졌어. 죄다 사라졌어.\""]], [["‘Why do you think you had once?’ asked Nicholas, turning quickly upon him as though the answer in some way helped out the purport of his question.", "\"옛날엔 좋았던 것 같다는 건 왜 그렇게 생각해?\" 니콜라스가 그 대답이 어쩐지 자기 질문의 속뜻을 풀어 주기라도 하는 듯, 휙 그를 돌아보며 물었어."]], [["‘Because I could remember, when I was a child,’ said Smike, ‘but that is very, very long ago, or at least it seems so. I was always confused and giddy at that place you took me from; and could never remember, and sometimes couldn’t even understand, what they said to me. I--let me see--let me see!’", "\"어릴 적엔 기억할 수 있었거든.\" 스마이크가 말했어. \"그게 아주, 아주 오래전이긴 해. 적어도 그렇게 느껴져. 네가 날 데려온 그곳에선 늘 머리가 어지럽고 멍했어. 사람들이 나한테 한 말을 도무지 기억할 수가 없었고, 가끔은 무슨 말인지 알아듣지도 못했어. 나… 가만, 가만있어 봐!\""]], [["‘You are wandering now,’ said Nicholas, touching him on the arm.", "\"지금 또 정신이 딴 데 가 있네.\" 니콜라스가 그의 팔을 건드리며 말했어."]], [["‘No,’ replied his companion, with a vacant look ‘I was only thinking how--’ He shivered involuntarily as he spoke.", "\"아니야.\" 그의 길동무가 멍한 눈빛으로 대답했어. \"그냥 어떻게…\" 그는 말하면서 저도 모르게 몸을 부르르 떨었어."]], [["‘Think no more of that place, for it is all over,’ retorted Nicholas, fixing his eyes full upon that of his companion, which was fast settling into an unmeaning stupefied gaze, once habitual to him, and common even then. ‘What of the first day you went to Yorkshire?’", "\"그곳 생각은 이제 그만해. 다 끝난 일이니까.\" 니콜라스가 길동무의 눈을 똑바로 들여다보며 대꾸했어. 그 눈은 어느새 아무 뜻도 없는 멍한 응시로 가라앉고 있었지. 예전에 늘 그랬고, 그때도 흔히 짓던 표정이었어. \"요크셔에 처음 갔던 날은 어땠어?\""]], [["‘Eh!’ cried the lad.", "\"응?\" 소년이 외쳤어."]], [["‘That was before you began to lose your recollection, you know,’ said Nicholas quietly.", "\"그건 네 기억을 잃기 전이잖아.\" 니콜라스가 조용히 말했어."], ["‘Was the weather hot or cold?’", "\"날씨가 더웠어, 추웠어?\""]], [["‘Wet,’ replied the boy. ‘Very wet. I have always said, when it has rained hard, that it was like the night I came: and they used to crowd round and laugh to see me cry when the rain fell heavily. It was like a child, they said, and that made me think of it more. I turned cold all over sometimes, for I could see myself as I was then, coming in at the very same door.’", "\"비가 왔어.\" 소년이 대답했어. \"엄청 많이 왔지. 비가 세차게 올 때면 나는 늘 말하곤 했어. 내가 처음 오던 그날 밤 같다고. 그러면 사람들이 빙 둘러서서 비 쏟아질 때 내가 우는 걸 보고 깔깔거렸어. 꼭 어린애 같다고들 했지. 그 말에 그날이 더 생각났어. 가끔은 온몸이 싸늘하게 식었어. 그때의 내 모습이 보였거든. 바로 그 똑같은 문으로 들어오는 모습 말이야.\""]], [["‘As you were then,’ repeated Nicholas, with assumed carelessness; ‘how was that?’", "\"그때의 네 모습이라.\" 니콜라스가 짐짓 무심한 척 되뇌었어. \"어땠는데?\""]], [["‘Such a little creature,’ said Smike, ‘that they might have had pity and mercy upon me, only to remember it.’", "\"아주 조그만 아이였어.\" 스마이크가 말했어. \"그 모습만 기억했어도, 사람들이 나한테 좀 가엾게 여기고 마음을 베풀어 줬을 텐데.\""]], [["‘You didn’t find your way there, alone!’ remarked Nicholas.", "\"거기까지 혼자 찾아간 건 아니지!\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘No,’ rejoined Smike, ‘oh no.’", "\"아니야.\" 스마이크가 대꾸했어. \"응, 아니야.\""]], [["‘Who was with you?’", "\"누구랑 같이 갔어?\""]], [["‘A man--a dark, withered man.", "\"한 남자랑. 거무튀튀하고 비쩍 마른 남자였어."], ["I have heard them say so, at the school, and I remembered that before.", "학교에서 사람들이 그렇게 말하는 걸 들었고, 나도 그건 전부터 기억하고 있었어."], ["I was glad to leave him, I was afraid of him; but they made me more afraid of them, and used me harder too.’", "그 남자랑 헤어져서 기뻤어, 그 사람이 무서웠거든. 그런데 사람들은 나를 그 사람보다 더 무섭게 했고, 더 모질게 부려 먹었어.\""]], [["‘Look at me,’ said Nicholas, wishing to attract his full attention.", "\"날 봐.\" 니콜라스가 스마이크의 온 정신을 끌어모으려고 말했어."], ["‘There; don’t turn away.", "\"그래, 고개 돌리지 말고."], ["Do you remember no woman, no kind woman, who hung over you once, and kissed your lips, and called you her child?’", "너 한 번이라도 너를 굽어보며 네 입술에 입 맞추고 너를 제 자식이라 부른 여자, 다정한 여자를 기억 못 해?\""]], [["‘No,’ said the poor creature, shaking his head, ‘no, never.’", "\"못 해.\" 가엾은 아이가 고개를 저으며 말했어. \"응, 한 번도.\""]], [["‘Nor any house but that house in Yorkshire?’", "\"요크셔의 그 집 말고 다른 집은?\""]], [["‘No,’ rejoined the youth, with a melancholy look; ‘a room--I remember I slept in a room, a large lonesome room at the top of a house, where there was a trap-door in the ceiling. I have covered my head with the clothes often, not to see it, for it frightened me: a young child with no one near at night: and I used to wonder what was on the other side. There was a clock too, an old clock, in one corner. I remember that. I have never forgotten that room; for when I have terrible dreams, it comes back, just as it was. I see things and people in it that I had never seen then, but there is the room just as it used to be; THAT never changes.’", "\"없어.\" 소년이 우울한 얼굴로 대꾸했어. \"방 하나… 방 하나에서 잤던 건 기억나. 집 꼭대기의 크고 외로운 방이었어. 천장에 들창이 하나 나 있었지. 그게 무서워서, 안 보려고 자주 이불로 머리를 덮곤 했어. 밤이면 곁에 아무도 없는 어린아이였으니까. 그리고 그 들창 너머엔 뭐가 있을까 늘 궁금했어. 시계도 하나 있었어, 한구석에 낡은 시계가. 그건 기억나. 그 방은 한 번도 잊은 적이 없어. 무서운 꿈을 꿀 때면 그 방이 그때 모습 그대로 돌아오거든. 그 안에서 그때는 본 적도 없는 것들과 사람들이 보이지만, 방만큼은 늘 있던 그대로야. 그건 절대 안 변해.\""]], [["‘Will you let me take the bundle now?’ asked Nicholas, abruptly changing the theme.", "\"이제 그 짐 내가 들면 안 될까?\" 니콜라스가 화제를 불쑥 바꾸며 물었어."]], [["‘No,’ said Smike, ‘no.", "\"안 돼.\" 스마이크가 말했어."], ["Come, let us walk on.’", "\"안 돼. 자, 계속 걷자.\""]], [["He quickened his pace as he said this, apparently under the impression that they had been standing still during the whole of the previous dialogue.", "그는 이렇게 말하며 걸음을 재촉했어. 앞선 이야기를 나누는 내내 둘이 가만히 서 있기라도 했던 것처럼 말이야."], ["Nicholas marked him closely, and every word of this conversation remained upon his memory.", "니콜라스는 그를 유심히 지켜봤어. 그리고 이 대화의 한마디 한마디가 그의 기억에 고스란히 남았지."]], [["It was, by this time, within an hour of noon, and although a dense vapour still enveloped the city they had left, as if the very breath of its busy people hung over their schemes of gain and profit, and found greater attraction there than in the quiet region above, in the open country it was clear and fair. Occasionally, in some low spots they came upon patches of mist which the sun had not yet driven from their strongholds; but these were soon passed, and as they laboured up the hills beyond, it was pleasant to look down, and see how the sluggish mass rolled heavily off, before the cheering influence of day. A broad, fine, honest sun lighted up the green pastures and dimpled water with the semblance of summer, while it left the travellers all the invigorating freshness of that early time of year. The ground seemed elastic under their feet; the sheep-bells were music to their ears; and exhilarated by exercise, and stimulated by hope, they pushed onward with the strength of lions.", "이때쯤엔 정오가 한 시간도 채 안 남았어. 그들이 떠나온 도시 위로는 여전히 짙은 안개가 자욱이 덮여 있었지. 마치 바삐 사는 사람들의 숨결 자체가 이익과 이윤을 노리는 그들의 궁리 위에 걸려, 저 위 고요한 하늘보다 거기에 더 끌리기라도 하는 것처럼 말이야. 하지만 탁 트인 시골은 맑고 화창했어. 가끔 움푹한 곳에서는 햇살이 아직 몰아내지 못한 안개 조각과 마주치기도 했지. 하지만 그것도 금세 지나쳤어. 그 너머 언덕을 힘겹게 오르다 아래를 내려다보면, 그 굼뜬 안개 덩어리가 낮의 환한 기운 앞에서 묵직하게 굴러 물러나는 모습이 보였어. 보기에 참 좋았지. 넓고 멋지고 정직한 햇살이 푸른 목초지와 잔물결 이는 물을 마치 여름처럼 환히 비추었어. 그러면서도 길손들에게는 이 이른 계절 특유의 생기 돋는 상쾌함을 그대로 남겨 주었지. 발밑의 땅은 탄력 있게 느껴졌어. 양들의 방울 소리는 그들의 귀에 음악 같았지. 그리고 걷느라 활기가 솟고 희망에 들떠서, 그들은 사자 같은 힘으로 앞으로 밀고 나아갔어."]], [["The day wore on, and all these bright colours subsided, and assumed a quieter tint, like young hopes softened down by time, or youthful features by degrees resolving into the calm and serenity of age. But they were scarcely less beautiful in their slow decline, than they had been in their prime; for nature gives to every time and season some beauties of its own; and from morning to night, as from the cradle to the grave, is but a succession of changes so gentle and easy, that we can scarcely mark their progress.", "날이 저물자, 이 모든 밝은 빛깔이 가라앉으며 한결 차분한 색조로 바뀌었어. 마치 세월에 누그러진 젊은 날의 희망처럼, 또 점점 노년의 평온과 고요로 가라앉는 젊은 얼굴처럼 말이야. 그래도 그 더딘 저묾은 한창때 못지않게 아름다웠어. 자연은 어느 때, 어느 철에든 저마다의 아름다움을 베푸니까. 아침부터 밤까지가, 요람에서 무덤까지가 그러하듯, 이어지는 변화가 어찌나 부드럽고 수월한지 우리는 그 흐름을 좀처럼 알아채지도 못하지."]], [["To Godalming they came at last, and here they bargained for two humble beds, and slept soundly. In the morning they were astir: though not quite so early as the sun: and again afoot; if not with all the freshness of yesterday, still, with enough of hope and spirit to bear them cheerily on.", "마침내 그들은 고댈밍에 닿았어. 거기서 소박한 침대 두 개를 흥정해 빌리고, 곤히 잠들었지. 아침에 그들은 일어나 움직였어. 해만큼 이르지는 않았지만 말이야. 그리고 다시 길을 나섰지. 어제만큼 생생하지는 않았어도, 그들을 즐겁게 받쳐 줄 만큼의 희망과 기운은 여전히 남아 있었어."]], [["It was a harder day’s journey than yesterday’s, for there were long and weary hills to climb; and in journeys, as in life, it is a great deal easier to go down hill than up. However, they kept on, with unabated perseverance, and the hill has not yet lifted its face to heaven that perseverance will not gain the summit of at last.", "어제보다 더 힘든 하루 길이었어. 길고 고된 언덕을 올라야 했거든. 여행도 인생과 마찬가지로, 언덕은 오르는 것보다 내려가는 게 훨씬 쉬운 법이지. 그래도 그들은 꺾이지 않는 끈기로 계속 나아갔어. 끈기로 끝내 그 꼭대기에 오르지 못할 언덕은, 아직 이 세상에 솟아오른 적이 없거든."]], [["They walked upon the rim of the Devil’s Punch Bowl; and Smike listened with greedy interest as Nicholas read the inscription upon the stone which, reared upon that wild spot, tells of a murder committed there by night. The grass on which they stood, had once been dyed with gore; and the blood of the murdered man had run down, drop by drop, into the hollow which gives the place its name. ‘The Devil’s Bowl,’ thought Nicholas, as he looked into the void, ‘never held fitter liquor than that!’", "그들은 데블스 펀치 볼의 가장자리를 걸었어. 그 거친 곳에 세워진 돌에는, 한밤중에 거기서 벌어진 살인 사건을 알리는 글귀가 새겨져 있었지. 니콜라스가 그걸 읽어 주자, 스마이크는 빨려들 듯한 호기심으로 귀를 기울였어. 그들이 밟고 선 풀밭은 한때 피로 물들었던 곳이야. 살해당한 사람의 피가 한 방울씩 흘러내려, 이 자리에 이름을 붙여 준 그 움푹한 곳으로 떨어졌지. '데블스 볼이라.' 니콜라스는 그 텅 빈 곳을 들여다보며 생각했어. '이보다 더 어울리는 술을 담아 본 적은 없겠군!'"]], [["Onward they kept, with steady purpose, and entered at length upon a wide and spacious tract of downs, with every variety of little hill and plain to change their verdant surface. Here, there shot up, almost perpendicularly, into the sky, a height so steep, as to be hardly accessible to any but the sheep and goats that fed upon its sides, and there, stood a mound of green, sloping and tapering off so delicately, and merging so gently into the level ground, that you could scarce define its limits. Hills swelling above each other; and undulations shapely and uncouth, smooth and rugged, graceful and grotesque, thrown negligently side by side, bounded the view in each direction; while frequently, with unexpected noise, there uprose from the ground a flight of crows, who, cawing and wheeling round the nearest hills, as if uncertain of their course, suddenly poised themselves upon the wing and skimmed down the long vista of some opening valley, with the speed of light itself.", "그들은 한결같은 마음으로 계속 나아갔어. 그리고 마침내 넓고 광활한 구릉지에 들어섰지. 자그마한 언덕과 평지가 갖가지로 어우러져, 그 푸른 표면을 바꿔 가는 곳이었어. 어떤 곳에는 거의 수직으로 하늘을 향해 솟구친 봉우리가 있었어. 어찌나 가파른지 비탈에서 풀 뜯는 양과 염소가 아니고는 좀처럼 오를 수도 없었지. 또 어떤 곳에는 초록빛 둔덕이 서 있었어. 어찌나 곱게 비탈지고 가늘어지며, 어찌나 부드럽게 평지로 녹아드는지 그 경계를 가늠하기조차 어려웠지. 언덕들이 서로 겹쳐 부풀어 올랐어. 맵시 있는 것과 투박한 것, 매끈한 것과 거친 것, 우아한 것과 기괴한 것이 한데 무심히 나란히 놓여, 사방으로 시야를 둘러쌌어. 그러다 가끔 뜻밖의 소리와 함께 땅에서 까마귀 떼가 솟구쳐 올랐지. 까악까악 울며 가장 가까운 언덕 둘레를 빙빙 돌다가, 마치 갈 길을 못 정한 듯하더니, 별안간 날갯짓을 가다듬고 길게 열린 골짜기를 따라 빛처럼 빠르게 미끄러져 내려갔어."]], [["By degrees, the prospect receded more and more on either hand, and as they had been shut out from rich and extensive scenery, so they emerged once again upon the open country.", "차츰차츰 양옆의 경치가 점점 멀어져 갔어. 그렇게 풍요롭고 드넓은 풍광에서 벗어나, 그들은 다시 한 번 탁 트인 시골로 나왔지."], ["The knowledge that they were drawing near their place of destination, gave them fresh courage to proceed; but the way had been difficult, and they had loitered on the road, and Smike was tired.", "목적지가 가까워졌다는 생각에 다시 나아갈 새 힘이 솟았어. 하지만 길이 험했던 데다 도중에 어정거리기도 해서, 스마이크는 지쳐 있었어."], ["Thus, twilight had already closed in, when they turned off the path to the door of a roadside inn, yet twelve miles short of Portsmouth.", "그래서 그들이 길에서 벗어나 어느 길가 여관 문 앞으로 들어설 무렵엔, 이미 땅거미가 내려앉아 있었어. 포츠머스까지는 아직 십이 마일이나 남은 곳이었지."]], [["‘Twelve miles,’ said Nicholas, leaning with both hands on his stick, and looking doubtfully at Smike.", "\"십이 마일이라.\" 니콜라스가 두 손을 지팡이에 짚고 기대어, 스마이크를 미심쩍게 바라보며 말했어."]], [["‘Twelve long miles,’ repeated the landlord.", "\"멀고 먼 십이 마일이지요.\" 여관 주인이 되풀이했어."]], [["‘Is it a good road?’ inquired Nicholas.", "\"길은 좋은가요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘Very bad,’ said the landlord.", "\"아주 나빠요.\" 주인이 말했어."], ["As of course, being a landlord, he would say.", "여관 주인이니, 당연히 그렇게 말하겠지."]], [["‘I want to get on,’ observed Nicholas, hesitating.", "\"계속 가긴 가야 하는데.\" 니콜라스가 망설이며 중얼거렸어."], ["‘I scarcely know what to do.’", "\"어찌해야 할지 모르겠네요.\""]], [["‘Don’t let me influence you,’ rejoined the landlord.", "\"제 말에 휘둘리진 마세요.\" 주인이 대꾸했어."], ["‘I wouldn’t go on if it was me.’", "\"저 같으면 안 갑니다.\""]], [["‘Wouldn’t you?’ asked Nicholas, with the same uncertainty.", "\"안 가신다고요?\" 니콜라스가 여전히 갈팡질팡하며 물었어."]], [["‘Not if I knew when I was well off,’ said the landlord.", "\"제 처지가 나은 줄 안다면야 안 가지요.\""], ["And having said it he pulled up his apron, put his hands into his pockets, and, taking a step or two outside the door, looked down the dark road with an assumption of great indifference.", "주인이 말했어. 그러고는 앞치마를 추켜올리고 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣더니, 문밖으로 두어 걸음 나가 짐짓 무관심한 척 어두운 길을 내려다봤어."]], [["A glance at the toil-worn face of Smike determined Nicholas, so without any further consideration he made up his mind to stay where he was.", "니콜라스는 고생에 찌든 스마이크의 얼굴을 한 번 보고는 마음을 정했어. 그래서 더는 따지지 않고, 여기 머물기로 결심했지."]], [["The landlord led them into the kitchen, and as there was a good fire he remarked that it was very cold.", "주인은 그들을 부엌으로 안내했어. 그리고 불이 잘 타고 있는데도 날이 무척 춥다고 한마디 했지."], ["If there had happened to be a bad one he would have observed that it was very warm.", "만일 불이 변변치 못했더라면, 날이 무척 따뜻하다고 했을 거야."]], [["‘What can you give us for supper?’ was Nicholas’s natural question.", "\"저녁으로 뭘 주실 수 있나요?\" 니콜라스가 당연히 그렇게 물었어."]], [["‘Why--what would you like?’ was the landlord’s no less natural answer.", "\"글쎄요, 뭘 드시고 싶으신데요?\" 주인의 대답도 그에 못지않게 당연했지."]], [["Nicholas suggested cold meat, but there was no cold meat--poached eggs, but there were no eggs--mutton chops, but there wasn’t a mutton chop within three miles, though there had been more last week than they knew what to do with, and would be an extraordinary supply the day after tomorrow.", "니콜라스는 차가운 고기를 청해 봤어. 그런데 차가운 고기는 없었지. 수란을 청했지만 달걀도 없었어. 양고기 토막을 청했지만, 삼 마일 안엔 양고기 토막 하나 없었어. 지난주엔 주체할 수 없을 만큼 많았고, 모레는 또 엄청나게 들어올 거라는데도 말이야."]], [["‘Then,’ said Nicholas, ‘I must leave it entirely to you, as I would have done, at first, if you had allowed me.’", "\"그럼,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"당신한테 다 맡길게요. 처음부터 그러라고 하셨으면 그랬을 텐데요.\""]], [["‘Why, then I’ll tell you what,’ rejoined the landlord.", "\"그럼 이러면 어떨까요.\" 주인이 대꾸했어."], ["‘There’s a gentleman in the parlour that’s ordered a hot beef-steak pudding and potatoes, at nine.", "\"안쪽 방에 한 신사분이 계신데, 아홉 시에 뜨거운 비프스테이크 푸딩이랑 감자를 시켜 놓으셨거든요."], ["There’s more of it than he can manage, and I have very little doubt that if I ask leave, you can sup with him.", "혼자 다 드시기엔 양이 많아요. 제가 여쭤보면, 손님이 같이 드셔도 된다고 하실 게 거의 틀림없습니다."], ["I’ll do that, in a minute.’", "얼른 가서 여쭤보고 오지요.\""]], [["‘No, no,’ said Nicholas, detaining him.", "\"아니, 아니요.\" 니콜라스가 그를 붙잡으며 말했어."], ["‘I would rather not.", "\"그건 좀 사양하고 싶군요. 나…"], ["I--at least--pshaw!", "그러니까… 에잇!"], ["why cannot I speak out?", "왜 시원하게 말을 못 할까?"], ["Here; you see that I am travelling in a very humble manner, and have made my way hither on foot.", "자, 보시다시피 나는 아주 초라하게 여행하는 데다, 여기까지 걸어서 왔어요."], ["It is more than probable, I think, that the gentleman may not relish my company; and although I am the dusty figure you see, I am too proud to thrust myself into his.’", "그 신사분이 내가 같이 앉는 걸 안 좋아하실 게 거의 분명해요. 이렇게 먼지투성이 꼴이긴 하지만, 그래도 그분한테 비집고 들어갈 만큼 염치없진 못하답니다.\""]], [["‘Lord love you,’ said the landlord, ‘it’s only Mr.", "\"세상에, 손님도 참.\" 주인이 말했어."], ["Crummles; HE isn’t particular.’", "\"그저 크럼블스 씨인걸요. 그분은 까다로운 분이 아니에요.\""]], [["‘Is he not?’ asked Nicholas, on whose mind, to tell the truth, the prospect of the savoury pudding was making some impression.", "\"그렇지 않다고요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어. 사실대로 말하자면, 구수한 푸딩 생각이 그의 마음에 슬슬 와닿고 있었거든."]], [["‘Not he,’ replied the landlord.", "\"천만에요.\" 주인이 대답했어."], ["‘He’ll like your way of talking, I know.", "\"그분은 손님 말솜씨를 분명히 좋아하실 겁니다."], ["But we’ll soon see all about that.", "어쨌든 곧 알게 되겠지요."], ["Just wait a minute.’", "잠깐만 기다리세요.\""]], [["The landlord hurried into the parlour, without staying for further permission, nor did Nicholas strive to prevent him: wisely considering that supper, under the circumstances, was too serious a matter to be trifled with.", "주인은 더 허락을 구할 것도 없이 서둘러 안쪽 방으로 들어갔어. 니콜라스도 굳이 말리려 들지 않았지."], ["It was not long before the host returned, in a condition of much excitement.", "이런 처지에 저녁 한 끼는 가벼이 여길 수 없는 자못 진지한 문제라고, 현명하게 판단한 거야. 얼마 지나지 않아 주인이 잔뜩 들뜬 채 돌아왔어."]], [["‘All right,’ he said in a low voice.", "\"됐습니다.\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 말했어."], ["‘I knew he would.", "\"그러실 줄 알았어요."], ["You’ll see something rather worth seeing, in there.", "저 안에서 볼 만한 구경거리도 좀 보실 겁니다."], ["Ecod, how they are a-going of it!’", "어이쿠, 어찌나 신명 나게들 하던지!\""]], [["There was no time to inquire to what this exclamation, which was delivered in a very rapturous tone, referred; for he had already thrown open the door of the room; into which Nicholas, followed by Smike with the bundle on his shoulder (he carried it about with him as vigilantly as if it had been a sack of gold), straightway repaired.", "이 외침이, 그것도 잔뜩 황홀한 말투로 내뱉은 외침이 무엇을 두고 한 말인지 물어볼 새도 없었어. 주인이 벌써 방문을 활짝 열어젖혔거든. 니콜라스는 곧장 그리로 들어갔지. 어깨에 짐을 멘 스마이크가 그 뒤를 따랐고. 스마이크는 그 짐을 마치 금자루라도 되는 양 한시도 눈을 떼지 않고 지니고 다녔어."]], [["Nicholas was prepared for something odd, but not for something quite so odd as the sight he encountered. At the upper end of the room, were a couple of boys, one of them very tall and the other very short, both dressed as sailors--or at least as theatrical sailors, with belts, buckles, pigtails, and pistols complete--fighting what is called in play-bills a terrific combat, with two of those short broad-swords with basket hilts which are commonly used at our minor theatres. The short boy had gained a great advantage over the tall boy, who was reduced to mortal strait, and both were overlooked by a large heavy man, perched against the corner of a table, who emphatically adjured them to strike a little more fire out of the swords, and they couldn’t fail to bring the house down, on the very first night.", "니콜라스는 뭔가 별난 광경을 각오하고는 있었어. 하지만 눈앞에 펼쳐진 만큼 별난 건 미처 생각지 못했지. 방 안쪽 끝에는 사내아이 둘이 있었어. 하나는 아주 키가 컸고 다른 하나는 아주 작았는데, 둘 다 선원 차림이었지. 적어도 연극에 나오는 선원 차림이었어. 허리띠며 버클이며 땋은 머리며 권총까지 빠짐없이 갖췄지. 둘은 연극 광고지에 흔히 적히는 그 '치열한 결투'를 벌이고 있었어. 우리네 작은 극장에서 흔히 쓰는, 바구니 모양 손잡이가 달린 짤막하고 넓적한 칼 두 자루를 들고 말이야. 작은 아이가 큰 아이를 크게 몰아붙여, 큰 아이는 죽기 직전의 막다른 처지에 몰려 있었지. 그리고 둘을 굽어보는 덩치 크고 묵직한 사내가 탁자 모서리에 걸터앉아 있었어. 그는 칼에서 불꽃을 좀 더 튀기라고 힘주어 다그쳤지. 그렇게만 하면 첫날밤부터 객석을 들썩이게 할 게 틀림없다면서 말이야."]], [["‘Mr. Vincent Crummles,’ said the landlord with an air of great deference.", "\"빈센트 크럼블스 씨입니다.\""], ["‘This is the young gentleman.’", "여관 주인이 자못 공손한 태도로 말했어. \"이쪽이 그 젊은 신사분이고요.\""]], [["Mr.", "빈센트 크럼블스 씨는 고개를 한 번 까딱하며 니콜라스를 맞았어."], ["Vincent Crummles received Nicholas with an inclination of the head, something between the courtesy of a Roman emperor and the nod of a pot companion; and bade the landlord shut the door and begone.", "로마 황제의 예법과 술친구의 끄덕임 사이쯤 되는 인사였지. 그러고는 주인더러 문을 닫고 나가라고 했어."]], [["‘There’s a picture,’ said Mr.", "\"멋진 한 장면이지요.\""], ["Crummles, motioning Nicholas not to advance and spoil it.", "크럼블스 씨가 니콜라스더러 앞으로 나와 장면을 망치지 말라고 손짓하며 말했어."], ["‘The little ‘un has him; if the big ‘un doesn’t knock under, in three seconds, he’s a dead man.", "\"작은 녀석이 큰 녀석을 잡았어요. 큰 녀석이 삼 초 안에 항복하지 않으면 죽은 목숨이지요."], ["Do that again, boys.’", "얘들아, 한 번 더 해 봐라.\""]], [["The two combatants went to work afresh, and chopped away until the swords emitted a shower of sparks: to the great satisfaction of Mr Crummles, who appeared to consider this a very great point indeed. The engagement commenced with about two hundred chops administered by the short sailor and the tall sailor alternately, without producing any particular result, until the short sailor was chopped down on one knee; but this was nothing to him, for he worked himself about on the one knee with the assistance of his left hand, and fought most desperately until the tall sailor chopped his sword out of his grasp. Now, the inference was, that the short sailor, reduced to this extremity, would give in at once and cry quarter, but, instead of that, he all of a sudden drew a large pistol from his belt and presented it at the face of the tall sailor, who was so overcome at this (not expecting it) that he let the short sailor pick up his sword and begin again. Then, the chopping recommenced, and a variety of fancy chops were administered on both sides; such as chops dealt with the left hand, and under the leg, and over the right shoulder, and over the left; and when the short sailor made a vigorous cut at the tall sailor’s legs, which would have shaved them clean off if it had taken effect, the tall sailor jumped over the short sailor’s sword, wherefore to balance the matter, and make it all fair, the tall sailor administered the same cut, and the short sailor jumped over HIS sword. After this, there was a good deal of dodging about, and hitching up of the inexpressibles in the absence of braces, and then the short sailor (who was the moral character evidently, for he always had the best of it) made a violent demonstration and closed with the tall sailor, who, after a few unavailing struggles, went down, and expired in great torture as the short sailor put his foot upon his breast, and bored a hole in him through and through.", "두 결투자는 다시 한판 붙었어. 그리고 칼에서 불꽃이 소나기처럼 쏟아질 때까지 마구 후려쳤지. 크럼블스 씨는 무척 흡족해했어. 그는 이걸 정말 중요한 대목이라 여기는 모양이었지. 결투는 작은 선원과 큰 선원이 번갈아 한 이백 번쯤 후려치는 걸로 시작됐어. 별다른 결판이 안 나다가, 작은 선원이 한쪽 무릎을 꿇으며 베여 쓰러졌지. 하지만 그게 그한테는 아무것도 아니었어. 왼손을 짚어 한쪽 무릎으로 빙빙 몸을 틀면서 죽을힘을 다해 싸웠거든. 그러다 큰 선원이 그의 손에서 칼을 쳐서 떨어뜨렸어. 자, 이쯤 되면 막다른 처지에 몰린 작은 선원이 곧장 항복하고 살려 달라 외칠 거라 짐작하겠지. 그런데 그러기는커녕, 그는 별안간 허리띠에서 커다란 권총을 뽑아 큰 선원의 얼굴에 들이댔어. 큰 선원은 (미처 예상치 못해서) 이에 어찌나 당황했던지, 작은 선원이 칼을 다시 주워 들고 또 시작하게 내버려 뒀지. 그러고는 다시 후려치기가 시작됐어. 양쪽에서 갖가지 멋들어진 칼질이 오갔지. 왼손으로 휘두르는 칼질, 다리 밑으로 넣는 칼질, 오른쪽 어깨 너머로, 왼쪽 어깨 너머로 넘기는 칼질 따위 말이야. 작은 선원이 큰 선원의 다리를 향해 힘껏 베었는데, 제대로 맞았더라면 다리가 싹둑 잘려 나갔을 그 칼질을, 큰 선원이 펄쩍 뛰어넘었어. 그래서 균형을 맞춰 공평하게 하느라, 큰 선원도 똑같이 베었고 작은 선원이 그 칼을 펄쩍 뛰어넘었지. 그러고 나서는 한참 동안 이리저리 피하고, 멜빵이 없어서 흘러내리는 바지를 추켜올리고 했어. 그러다 작은 선원이 (보아하니 분명 의로운 인물이었지. 늘 우위를 차지했으니까) 격렬한 공세를 펴며 큰 선원에게 달려들었어. 큰 선원은 몇 번 헛되이 발버둥 치다 쓰러졌지. 그러고는 작은 선원이 그의 가슴을 발로 밟고 몸을 푹 꿰뚫어 구멍을 내자, 끔찍한 고통 속에 숨을 거두었어."]], [["‘That’ll be a double ENCORE if you take care, boys,’ said Mr. Crummles.", "\"잘만 하면 두 번 앙코르를 받겠다, 얘들아.\""], ["‘You had better get your wind now and change your clothes.’", "크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"이제 숨 좀 돌리고 옷을 갈아입어라.\""]], [["Having addressed these words to the combatants, he saluted Nicholas, who then observed that the face of Mr. Crummles was quite proportionate in size to his body; that he had a very full under-lip, a hoarse voice, as though he were in the habit of shouting very much, and very short black hair, shaved off nearly to the crown of his head--to admit (as he afterwards learnt) of his more easily wearing character wigs of any shape or pattern.", "이 말을 결투자들에게 건넨 뒤, 그는 니콜라스에게 인사했어. 그제야 니콜라스는 크럼블스 씨의 얼굴이 몸집에 딱 어울리게 큼직하다는 걸 보았지. 그는 아랫입술이 무척 두툼했어. 목소리는 쉬어 있었는데, 평소 어지간히 소리를 질러 대는 버릇이 있는 것 같았어. 그리고 새까만 머리는 아주 짧았어. 정수리 가까이까지 거의 밀어 버린 상태였지. 나중에 알게 된 거지만, 어떤 모양이나 무늬의 분장용 가발이든 더 수월하게 쓰려고 그런 거였어."]], [["‘What did you think of that, sir?’ inquired Mr.", "\"어떻게 보셨습니까, 선생?\""], ["Crummles.", "크럼블스 씨가 물었어."]], [["‘Very good, indeed--capital,’ answered Nicholas.", "\"정말 훌륭했어요. 일품이던데요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘You won’t see such boys as those very often, I think,’ said Mr Crummles.", "\"저런 아이들은 그리 흔히 못 보실 겁니다.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어."]], [["Nicholas assented--observing that if they were a little better match--", "니콜라스도 맞다고 했어. 그러면서 두 아이가 조금만 더 어울리는 짝이었으면 하고 한마디 보탰지."]], [["‘Match!’ cried Mr.", "\"짝이라니!\""], ["Crummles.", "크럼블스 씨가 외쳤어."]], [["‘I mean if they were a little more of a size,’ said Nicholas, explaining himself.", "\"제 말은, 둘이 덩치가 좀 더 비슷했더라면 하는 거였어요.\" 니콜라스가 제 뜻을 풀어 설명했어."]], [["‘Size!’ repeated Mr.", "\"덩치라니!\""], ["Crummles; ‘why, it’s the essence of the combat that there should be a foot or two between them.", "크럼블스 씨가 되받았어. \"아니, 둘 사이에 한두 자쯤 차이가 나야 그게 결투의 묘미지요."], ["How are you to get up the sympathies of the audience in a legitimate manner, if there isn’t a little man contending against a big one?--unless there’s at least five to one, and we haven’t hands enough for that business in our company.’", "작은 사람이 큰 사람을 상대로 맞서는 게 아니면, 어떻게 관객의 마음을 제대로 끌어올리겠어요? 적어도 다섯 대 하나는 돼야 하는데, 우리 극단엔 그럴 만큼 일손이 없거든요.\""]], [["‘I see,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"그렇군요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘I beg your pardon.", "\"죄송합니다."], ["That didn’t occur to me, I confess.’", "솔직히 거기까진 미처 생각을 못 했네요.\""]], [["‘It’s the main point,’ said Mr.", "\"그게 핵심입니다.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어."], ["Crummles. ‘I open at Portsmouth the day after tomorrow.", "\"나는 모레 포츠머스에서 막을 올려요."], ["If you’re going there, look into the theatre, and see how that’ll tell.’", "그쪽으로 가신다면 극장에 한번 들러서, 그게 얼마나 먹히는지 보세요.\""]], [["Nicholas promised to do so, if he could, and drawing a chair near the fire, fell into conversation with the manager at once. He was very talkative and communicative, stimulated perhaps, not only by his natural disposition, but by the spirits and water he sipped very plentifully, or the snuff he took in large quantities from a piece of whitey-brown paper in his waistcoat pocket. He laid open his affairs without the smallest reserve, and descanted at some length upon the merits of his company, and the acquirements of his family; of both of which, the two broad-sword boys formed an honourable portion. There was to be a gathering, it seemed, of the different ladies and gentlemen at Portsmouth on the morrow, whither the father and sons were proceeding (not for the regular season, but in the course of a wandering speculation), after fulfilling an engagement at Guildford with the greatest applause.", "니콜라스는 가능하면 그러겠다고 약속했어. 그러고는 불 가까이 의자를 끌어다 앉아, 곧장 단장과 이야기를 나누기 시작했지. 단장은 무척 수다스럽고 거리낌 없이 말을 늘어놨어. 어쩌면 타고난 성품 때문만이 아니라, 그가 넉넉히 홀짝이는 술과 물, 또 조끼 주머니 속 누르스름한 종이에서 한 움큼씩 집어 들이켜는 코담배 덕분이었는지도 몰라. 그는 조금도 숨김없이 자기 사정을 털어놨어. 그러고는 자기 극단의 장점이며 식구들의 재주를 한참 늘어놓았지. 그 둘 모두에서 넓적칼을 휘두르던 두 아이가 자랑스러운 한몫을 차지했어. 듣자 하니 이튿날 포츠머스에서 여러 단원들이 모이기로 돼 있었어. 아버지와 두 아들은 그리로 가는 길이었지. (정규 공연철 때문이 아니라, 떠돌이 흥행을 다니던 중이었어.) 길퍼드에서 한 차례 공연을 큰 갈채 속에 마치고 오는 길이었지."]], [["‘You are going that way?’ asked the manager.", "\"그쪽으로 가신다고요?\" 단장이 물었어."]], [["‘Ye-yes,’ said Nicholas.", "\"네… 네.\""], ["‘Yes, I am.’", "니콜라스가 말했어. \"네, 그렇습니다.\""]], [["‘Do you know the town at all?’ inquired the manager, who seemed to consider himself entitled to the same degree of confidence as he had himself exhibited.", "\"그 동네는 좀 아세요?\" 단장이 물었어. 자기가 그만큼 속을 터놓았으니, 그만한 신뢰를 받을 자격이 있다고 여기는 모양이었지. \"아니요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘No,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"한 번도 안 가 보셨어요?\""]], [["‘Never there?’", "\"한 번도요.\""]], [["‘Never.’\nMr. Vincent Crummles gave a short dry cough, as much as to say, ‘If you won’t be communicative, you won’t;’ and took so many pinches of snuff from the piece of paper, one after another, that Nicholas quite wondered where it all went to.", "빈센트 크럼블스 씨는 짤막하게 마른기침을 했어. '터놓고 말 안 할 거면 말고요' 하는 듯한 기침이었지. 그러고는 그 종이에서 코담배를 한 움큼씩 연거푸 집어 들이켰어. 어찌나 많이 집던지, 니콜라스는 그게 다 어디로 들어가는지 어리둥절할 지경이었어."]], [["While he was thus engaged, Mr. Crummles looked, from time to time, with great interest at Smike, with whom he had appeared considerably struck from the first.", "이러는 동안 크럼블스 씨는 이따금 스마이크를 무척 흥미로운 눈길로 바라봤어."], ["He had now fallen asleep, and was nodding in his chair.", "처음부터 그를 꽤 눈여겨본 듯했지. 스마이크는 어느새 잠이 들어 의자에 앉은 채 꾸벅거리고 있었어."]], [["‘Excuse my saying so,’ said the manager, leaning over to Nicholas, and sinking his voice, ‘but what a capital countenance your friend has got!’", "\"이런 말 해서 미안합니다만,\" 단장이 니콜라스 쪽으로 몸을 기울이며 목소리를 낮춰 말했어. \"선생 친구 얼굴이 정말 일품이로군요!\""]], [["‘Poor fellow!’ said Nicholas, with a half-smile, ‘I wish it were a little more plump, and less haggard.’", "\"가엾은 친구지요!\" 니콜라스가 씁쓸하게 웃으며 말했어. \"좀 더 살이 오르고, 덜 핼쑥했으면 싶은데요.\""]], [["‘Plump!’ exclaimed the manager, quite horrified, ‘you’d spoil it for ever.’", "\"살이라니요!\" 단장이 화들짝 놀라 외쳤어. \"그랬다간 영영 망쳐 버려요.\""]], [["‘Do you think so?’", "\"그렇게 생각하세요?\""]], [["‘Think so, sir!", "\"생각하다마다요, 선생!"], ["Why, as he is now,’ said the manager, striking his knee emphatically; ‘without a pad upon his body, and hardly a touch of paint upon his face, he’d make such an actor for the starved business as was never seen in this country.", "지금 저 모습 그대로면,\" 단장이 제 무릎을 탁 치며 말했어."], ["Only let him be tolerably well up in the Apothecary in Romeo and Juliet, with the slightest possible dab of red on the tip of his nose, and he’d be certain of three rounds the moment he put his head out of the practicable door in the front grooves O.P.’", "\"몸에 패드 하나 안 대고, 얼굴에 분칠 한 번 안 해도, 굶주린 역할로는 이 나라에서 둘도 없는 배우가 될 겁니다. 그저 〈로미오와 줄리엣〉의 약제사 역만 그럭저럭 익히게 해서, 코끝에 빨간색을 아주 살짝만 찍어 주면, 무대 앞 오른쪽 진짜 문으로 머리만 내밀어도 단번에 박수 세 차례는 따 놓은 당상이지요.\""]], [["‘You view him with a professional eye,’ said Nicholas, laughing.", "\"전문가의 눈으로 보시는군요.\" 니콜라스가 웃으며 말했어."]], [["‘And well I may,’ rejoined the manager.", "\"그럴 만도 하지요.\" 단장이 대꾸했어."], ["‘I never saw a young fellow so regularly cut out for that line, since I’ve been in the profession.", "\"이 바닥에 발 들인 이래로 그 역할에 그렇게 딱 맞아떨어지는 젊은이는 처음 봅니다."], ["And I played the heavy children when I was eighteen months old.’", "나로 말할 것 같으면 생후 열여덟 달 때부터 비중 있는 아역을 맡았던 사람이거든요.\""]], [["The appearance of the beef-steak pudding, which came in simultaneously with the junior Vincent Crummleses, turned the conversation to other matters, and indeed, for a time, stopped it altogether.", "비프스테이크 푸딩이 나오는 바람에 — 크럼블스 집안의 두 아들도 때맞춰 들어왔지 — 이야기는 다른 데로 흘렀어. 사실 한동안은 아예 말이 끊겼지."], ["These two young gentlemen wielded their knives and forks with scarcely less address than their broad-swords, and as the whole party were quite as sharp set as either class of weapons, there was no time for talking until the supper had been disposed of.", "이 두 젊은 신사는 넓적칼 못지않게 능숙하게 나이프와 포크를 놀렸어. 그리고 일행 모두가 어느 쪽 무기 못지않게 입맛이 칼칼하게 서 있던 터라, 저녁상을 다 비울 때까지는 이야기할 새가 없었지."]], [["The Master Crummleses had no sooner swallowed the last procurable morsel of food, than they evinced, by various half-suppressed yawns and stretchings of their limbs, an obvious inclination to retire for the night, which Smike had betrayed still more strongly: he having, in the course of the meal, fallen asleep several times while in the very act of eating. Nicholas therefore proposed that they should break up at once, but the manager would by no means hear of it; vowing that he had promised himself the pleasure of inviting his new acquaintance to share a bowl of punch, and that if he declined, he should deem it very unhandsome behaviour.", "크럼블스 형제는 마지막 한 입까지 삼키자마자, 어떻게든 참으려는 하품을 거듭 흘리고 팔다리를 늘이며, 잠자리에 들고 싶은 기색을 역력히 드러냈어. 그건 스마이크가 한층 더 뚜렷하게 보여 준 기색이었지. 그는 식사하는 내내 음식을 먹는 그 와중에도 몇 번이나 잠들곤 했거든. 그래서 니콜라스가 이제 그만 자리를 파하자고 제안했어. 하지만 단장은 도무지 들으려 하지 않았지. 새로 사귄 벗에게 펀치 한 사발을 함께하자고 청하는 즐거움을 스스로에게 약속했는데, 만일 거절한다면 아주 인정머리 없는 처사로 여기겠다며 맹세하듯 우겼어."]], [["‘Let them go,’ said Mr.", "\"애들은 자게 두지요.\""], ["Vincent Crummles, ‘and we’ll have it snugly and cosily together by the fire.’", "빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"우리끼리 난롯가에서 오붓하고 아늑하게 한잔합시다.\""]], [["Nicholas was not much disposed to sleep--being in truth too anxious--so, after a little demur, he accepted the offer, and having exchanged a shake of the hand with the young Crummleses, and the manager having on his part bestowed a most affectionate benediction on Smike, he sat himself down opposite to that gentleman by the fireside to assist in emptying the bowl, which soon afterwards appeared, steaming in a manner which was quite exhilarating to behold, and sending forth a most grateful and inviting fragrance.", "니콜라스는 그다지 잠이 오지 않았어. 사실 너무 마음이 졸여서였지. 그래서 잠깐 망설인 끝에 그 청을 받아들였어. 그리고 어린 크럼블스 형제와 악수를 나눴지. 단장은 단장대로 스마이크에게 더없이 다정한 축복을 내려 주었어. 니콜라스는 난롯가에 그 신사와 마주 앉아, 사발을 비우는 걸 거들기로 했지. 그 사발은 곧 들어왔는데, 보기만 해도 신명 나게 김을 모락모락 피워 올리며, 더없이 구수하고 군침 도는 향을 풍기고 있었어."]], [["But, despite the punch and the manager, who told a variety of stories, and smoked tobacco from a pipe, and inhaled it in the shape of snuff, with a most astonishing power, Nicholas was absent and dispirited. His thoughts were in his old home, and when they reverted to his present condition, the uncertainty of the morrow cast a gloom upon him, which his utmost efforts were unable to dispel. His attention wandered; although he heard the manager’s voice, he was deaf to what he said; and when Mr. Vincent Crummles concluded the history of some long adventure with a loud laugh, and an inquiry what Nicholas would have done under the same circumstances, he was obliged to make the best apology in his power, and to confess his entire ignorance of all he had been talking about.", "하지만 펀치도, 갖가지 이야기를 들려주고 파이프로 담배를 피우다가 코담배로도 들이마시던 그 놀라운 능력의 단장도 소용없었어. 니콜라스는 넋이 나간 채 의기소침해 있었지. 그의 마음은 옛집에 가 있었어. 그러다 지금 처지로 생각이 돌아오면, 앞날의 막막함이 그를 어둡게 짓눌렀어. 아무리 안간힘을 써도 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었지. 정신은 자꾸 딴 데로 흩어졌어. 단장의 목소리는 들렸지만, 무슨 말을 하는지는 귀에 들어오지 않았어. 그러다 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 어느 길고 긴 모험담을 큰 웃음으로 마무리하며, 같은 처지였다면 니콜라스는 어떻게 했겠느냐고 물었어. 니콜라스는 할 수 있는 한 그럴듯하게 사과하는 수밖에 없었지. 그가 늘어놓은 이야기를 자기가 죄다 못 알아들었다고 털어놓으면서 말이야."]], [["‘Why, so I saw,’ observed Mr.", "\"아, 그래 보이더군요.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어."], ["Crummles. ‘You’re uneasy in your mind.", "\"마음이 편치 않으신 거죠."], ["What’s the matter?’", "무슨 일입니까?\""]], [["Nicholas could not refrain from smiling at the abruptness of the question; but, thinking it scarcely worth while to parry it, owned that he was under some apprehensions lest he might not succeed in the object which had brought him to that part of the country.", "니콜라스는 그 불쑥 던진 물음에 웃음을 참을 수가 없었어. 하지만 굳이 피해 갈 것도 없다 싶어, 자기를 이 고장까지 데려온 그 목적을 못 이룰까 봐 좀 걱정하던 참이라고 털어놨어."]], [["‘And what’s that?’ asked the manager.", "\"그게 뭔데요?\" 단장이 물었어."]], [["‘Getting something to do which will keep me and my poor fellow-traveller in the common necessaries of life,’ said Nicholas.", "\"나와 이 가엾은 길동무가 먹고살 만한 기본 생계라도 꾸려 갈 일거리를 구하는 거지요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘That’s the truth.", "\"그게 사실입니다."], ["You guessed it long ago, I dare say, so I may as well have the credit of telling it you with a good grace.’", "진작 알아채셨겠지만요. 그러니 차라리 내 입으로 깔끔하게 말씀드리는 게 낫겠다 싶네요.\""]], [["‘What’s to be got to do at Portsmouth more than anywhere else?’ asked Mr Vincent Crummles, melting the sealing-wax on the stem of his pipe in the candle, and rolling it out afresh with his little finger.", "\"포츠머스에 다른 데보다 더 할 일이 있겠어요?\" 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 물었어. 촛불에 파이프 대롱의 봉랍을 녹여서, 새끼손가락으로 다시 굴려 매만지면서 말이야."]], [["‘There are many vessels leaving the port, I suppose,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"항구를 떠나는 배가 많을 것 같아서요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘I shall try for a berth in some ship or other.", "\"어느 배든 자리 하나를 구해 볼 작정입니다."], ["There is meat and drink there at all events.’", "거기라면 그래도 먹을 것 마실 것은 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["‘Salt meat and new rum; pease-pudding and chaff-biscuits,’ said the manager, taking a whiff at his pipe to keep it alight, and returning to his work of embellishment.", "\"소금에 절인 고기에 갓 빚은 럼주, 완두콩 푸딩에 왕겨 비스킷이지요.\" 단장이 파이프 불이 꺼지지 않게 한 모금 빨면서, 다시 봉랍 매만지는 일로 돌아가며 말했어."]], [["‘One may do worse than that,’ said Nicholas.", "\"그것보다 못한 일도 많지요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘I can rough it, I believe, as well as most young men of my age and previous habits.’", "\"내 또래에 이런 처지로 자란 젊은이들 못지않게, 나도 고생쯤은 견딜 수 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["‘You need be able to,’ said the manager, ‘if you go on board ship; but you won’t.’", "\"그래야 할 거예요.\" 단장이 말했어. \"배를 탄다면 말이지요. 하지만 못 타실 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Why not?’", "\"왜요?\""]], [["‘Because there’s not a skipper or mate that would think you worth your salt, when he could get a practised hand,’ replied the manager; ‘and they as plentiful there, as the oysters in the streets.’", "\"숙련된 일꾼을 구할 수 있는데, 어느 선장이나 항해사가 선생을 밥값 한다고 보겠어요?\" 단장이 대답했어. \"거기엔 그런 일꾼이 길거리 굴 껍데기만큼이나 흔하거든요.\""]], [["‘What do you mean?’ asked Nicholas, alarmed by this prediction, and the confident tone in which it had been uttered.", "\"그게 무슨 말씀이세요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."], ["‘Men are not born able seamen.", "이 예언과, 그걸 내뱉는 자신만만한 말투에 덜컥 겁이 났거든."], ["They must be reared, I suppose?’", "\"사람이 처음부터 능숙한 뱃사람으로 태어나는 건 아니잖아요. 길러져야 하는 거 아닌가요?\""]], [["Mr. Vincent Crummles nodded his head.", "빈센트 크럼블스 씨는 고개를 끄덕였어."], ["‘They must; but not at your age, or from young gentlemen like you.’", "\"길러져야지요. 하지만 선생 나이엔 아니고, 선생 같은 젊은 신사한테서 길러지는 것도 아니에요.\""]], [["There was a pause.", "잠시 말이 끊겼어."], ["The countenance of Nicholas fell, and he gazed ruefully at the fire.", "니콜라스의 낯빛이 어두워졌지. 그는 처량하게 불을 들여다봤어."]], [["‘Does no other profession occur to you, which a young man of your figure and address could take up easily, and see the world to advantage in?’ asked the manager.", "\"선생 같은 풍채와 말솜씨를 지닌 젊은이가 쉽게 시작할 만하고, 거기서 덕을 보며 세상 구경도 할 만한 다른 직업이 떠오르진 않으세요?\" 단장이 물었어."]], [["‘No,’ said Nicholas, shaking his head.", "\"없네요.\" 니콜라스가 고개를 저으며 말했어."]], [["‘Why, then, I’ll tell you one,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 내가 하나 일러 드리지요.\""], ["Crummles, throwing his pipe into the fire, and raising his voice.", "크럼블스 씨가 파이프를 불 속에 던지고 목소리를 높이며 말했어."], ["‘The stage.’", "\"무대입니다.\""]], [["‘The stage!’ cried Nicholas, in a voice almost as loud.", "\"무대라고요!\" 니콜라스가 거의 그만큼 큰 목소리로 외쳤어."]], [["‘The theatrical profession,’ said Mr.", "\"연극이라는 직업 말이에요.\""], ["Vincent Crummles.", "빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 말했어."], ["‘I am in the theatrical profession myself, my wife is in the theatrical profession, my children are in the theatrical profession.", "\"나도 연극을 업으로 삼고 있고, 내 아내도 연극이 업이고, 내 아이들도 다 연극이 업입니다. 강아지 때부터 평생 연극을 하다 죽은 개도 있었어요."], ["I had a dog that lived and died in it from a puppy; and my chaise-pony goes on, in Timour the Tartar.", "내 마차 끄는 조랑말도 〈타타르의 티무르〉에 나간답니다. 선생도 무대에 세워 드리지요."], ["I’ll bring you out, and your friend too.", "친구분도요. 한마디만 하시면 됩니다."], ["Say the word. I want a novelty.’", "나는 새 얼굴이 필요하거든요.\""]], [["‘I don’t know anything about it,’ rejoined Nicholas, whose breath had been almost taken away by this sudden proposal.", "\"저는 그런 거 아무것도 모르는데요.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어."], ["‘I never acted a part in my life, except at school.’", "이 느닷없는 제안에 그는 숨이 턱 막힐 지경이었거든. \"학교 때 말고는 평생 배역 한 번 맡아 본 적이 없어요.\""]], [["‘There’s genteel comedy in your walk and manner, juvenile tragedy in your eye, and touch-and-go farce in your laugh,’ said Mr.", "\"선생 걸음걸이와 몸가짐엔 점잖은 희극이 있고, 눈빛엔 풋풋한 비극이 있고, 웃음엔 가볍게 톡톡 튀는 소극이 있어요.\""], ["Vincent Crummles.", "빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 말했어."], ["‘You’ll do as well as if you had thought of nothing else but the lamps, from your birth downwards.’", "\"태어나면서부터 줄곧 무대 조명 말고는 아무것도 생각해 본 적 없는 사람만큼이나 잘 해내실 겁니다.\""]], [["Nicholas thought of the small amount of small change that would remain in his pocket after paying the tavern bill; and he hesitated.", "니콜라스는 여관 셈을 치르고 나면 주머니에 남을 자잘한 동전이 얼마 안 된다는 걸 떠올렸어. 그래서 망설였지."]], [["‘You can be useful to us in a hundred ways,’ said Mr.", "\"선생은 여러모로 우리한테 쓸모가 있어요.\""], ["Crummles.", "크럼블스 씨가 말했어."], ["‘Think what capital bills a man of your education could write for the shop-windows.’", "\"선생 같은 학식 있는 사람이 상점 진열창에 붙일 광고문을 써 준다고 생각해 보세요. 얼마나 근사하겠습니까.\""]], [["‘Well, I think I could manage that department,’ said Nicholas.", "\"음, 그 일이라면 제가 맡을 수 있을 것 같네요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘To be sure you could,’ replied Mr.", "\"맡으시고말고요.\" 크럼블스 씨가 대답했어."], ["Crummles. ‘“For further particulars see small hand-bills”--we might have half a volume in every one of ‘em.", "\"'자세한 내용은 작은 전단지 참조'라고 써 놓고, 그 전단지 하나하나에 책 반 권 분량을 담을 수도 있지요."], ["Pieces too; why, you could write us a piece to bring out the whole strength of the company, whenever we wanted one.’", "작품도 마찬가지예요. 필요할 때마다 우리 극단의 온 역량을 끌어낼 작품을 써 주실 수 있잖아요.\""]], [["‘I am not quite so confident about that,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"그건 그렇게 자신은 없는데요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘But I dare say I could scribble something now and then, that would suit you.’", "\"하지만 가끔 당신네한테 어울릴 만한 걸 끄적여 볼 수는 있을 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘We’ll have a new show-piece out directly,’ said the manager. ‘Let me see--peculiar resources of this establishment--new and splendid scenery--you must manage to introduce a real pump and two washing-tubs.’", "\"새 흥행작을 당장 하나 내봅시다.\" 단장이 말했어. \"어디 보자… 우리 극단만의 특별한 자산… 새롭고 화려한 무대 장치라… 진짜 펌프 하나랑 빨래통 두 개를 어떻게든 집어넣어 주셔야 해요.\""]], [["‘Into the piece?’ said Nicholas.", "\"작품 속에다 말인가요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Yes,’ replied the manager.", "\"그렇지요.\" 단장이 대답했어."], ["‘I bought ‘em cheap, at a sale the other day, and they’ll come in admirably.", "\"며칠 전 경매에서 싸게 사 뒀는데, 아주 요긴하게 쓰일 겁니다."], ["That’s the London plan.", "그게 런던식이에요."], ["They look up some dresses, and properties, and have a piece written to fit ‘em.", "의상이랑 소품을 먼저 마련해 놓고, 거기 맞춰 작품을 쓰게 하는 거지요."], ["Most of the theatres keep an author on purpose.’", "극장 대부분이 일부러 작가를 따로 둔답니다.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ cried Nicholas.", "\"정말요!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어."]], [["‘Oh, yes,’ said the manager; ‘a common thing. It’ll look very well in the bills in separate lines--Real pump!--Splendid tubs!--Great attraction! You don’t happen to be anything of an artist, do you?’", "\"아, 그럼요.\" 단장이 말했어. \"흔한 일이에요. 광고지에 한 줄씩 따로 박아 놓으면 아주 보기 좋지요. 진짜 펌프! 화려한 빨래통! 굉장한 볼거리! 혹시 그림 솜씨가 좀 있진 않으세요?\""]], [["‘That is not one of my accomplishments,’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"그건 제 재주가 아니라서요.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아! 그럼 어쩔 수 없지요.\""], ["Then it can’t be helped,’ said the manager.", "단장이 말했어. \"그림을 그릴 줄 아셨으면, 포스터에 마지막 장면을 큼직한 목판화로 넣을 수 있었거든요."], ["‘If you had been, we might have had a large woodcut of the last scene for the posters, showing the whole depth of the stage, with the pump and tubs in the middle; but, however, if you’re not, it can’t be helped.’", "무대 안쪽 깊이를 죄다 보여 주면서, 한가운데 펌프랑 빨래통을 박아 넣고요. 하지만 못 그리신다니, 어쩔 수 없네요.\""]], [["‘What should I get for all this?’ inquired Nicholas, after a few moments’ reflection.", "\"이 일을 다 하면 제가 얼마를 받게 되나요?\" 니콜라스가 잠깐 생각하다 물었어."], ["‘Could I live by it?’", "\"이걸로 먹고살 수 있을까요?\""]], [["‘Live by it!’ said the manager.", "\"먹고사느냐고요!\""], ["‘Like a prince!", "단장이 말했어."], ["With your own salary, and your friend’s, and your writings, you’d make--ah!", "\"왕족처럼 살지요! 선생 봉급에다 친구분 봉급, 거기다 글값까지 합치면…"], ["you’d make a pound a week!’", "아, 일주일에 일 파운드는 버실 겁니다!\""]], [["‘You don’t say so!’", "\"설마요!\""]], [["‘I do indeed, and if we had a run of good houses, nearly double the money.’", "\"정말이에요. 게다가 손님이 줄줄이 들기라도 하면, 그 갑절에 가까운 돈을 버시고요.\""]], [["Nicholas shrugged his shoulders; but sheer destitution was before him; and if he could summon fortitude to undergo the extremes of want and hardship, for what had he rescued his helpless charge if it were only to bear as hard a fate as that from which he had wrested him? It was easy to think of seventy miles as nothing, when he was in the same town with the man who had treated him so ill and roused his bitterest thoughts; but now, it seemed far enough. What if he went abroad, and his mother or Kate were to die the while?", "니콜라스는 어깨를 으쓱했어. 하지만 눈앞엔 영락없는 빈털터리 신세가 기다리고 있었지. 게다가 설령 그가 굶주림과 고생의 끝을 견뎌 낼 굳은 마음을 끌어모은다 한들, 이 의지가지없는 아이를 무엇 하러 구해 냈겠어? 결국 자기가 끌어내 준 그 처지만큼이나 모진 운명을 또 짊어지게 할 거라면 말이야. 자기를 그렇게 못되게 굴어 가장 쓰라린 마음을 들쑤셔 놓은 그 사람과 같은 도시에 있을 때는, 칠십 마일쯤이야 아무것도 아니라고 여기기 쉬웠어. 하지만 지금은 그게 까마득히 멀게만 느껴졌지. 만일 그가 멀리 떠나 있는 동안 어머니나 케이트가 죽기라도 하면 어쩐담?"]], [["Without more deliberation, he hastily declared that it was a bargain, and gave Mr.", "그는 더 따져 볼 것도 없이, 서둘러 그렇게 하기로 했다고 말했어."], ["Vincent Crummles his hand upon it.", "그러고는 빈센트 크럼블스 씨에게 그 약조의 표시로 손을 내밀었지."]], [["# CHAPTER 23", "# CHAPTER 23"]], [["Treats of the Company of Mr. Vincent Crummles, and of his Affairs, Domestic and Theatrical\nAs Mr. Crummles had a strange four-legged animal in the inn stables, which he called a pony, and a vehicle of unknown design, on which he bestowed the appellation of a four-wheeled phaeton, Nicholas proceeded on his journey next morning with greater ease than he had expected: the manager and himself occupying the front seat: and the Master Crummleses and Smike being packed together behind, in company with a wicker basket defended from wet by a stout oilskin, in which were the broad-swords, pistols, pigtails, nautical costumes, and other professional necessaries of the aforesaid young gentlemen.", "크럼블스 씨는 여관 마구간에 다리 넷 달린 이상한 동물 한 마리를 두고 있었어. 그는 그걸 조랑말이라고 불렀지. 또 정체를 알 수 없는 탈것도 한 대 있었는데, 그건 네 바퀴 사두마차라고 부르더라고. 덕분에 니콜라스는 이튿날 아침, 생각보다 훨씬 편하게 길을 떠날 수 있었어. 단장과 니콜라스가 앞자리를 차지하고, 크럼블스 도련님들과 스마이크는 뒤에 한데 끼어 앉았지. 그리고 그 옆에는 두툼한 방수포로 비를 막아 둔 버드나무 바구니가 있었어. 그 안에는 앞서 말한 어린 신사들의 양날 검이며 권총, 변발 가발, 선원 의상, 그 밖의 직업상 필요한 물건들이 들어 있었지."]], [["The pony took his time upon the road, and--possibly in consequence of his theatrical education--evinced, every now and then, a strong inclination to lie down.", "조랑말은 길에서 제멋대로 늑장을 부렸어. 그리고 — 아마 연극판에서 받은 교육 탓이었는지 — 이따금 드러눕고 싶어 안달하는 기색을 강하게 보였지."], ["However, Mr. Vincent Crummles kept him up pretty well, by jerking the rein, and plying the whip; and when these means failed, and the animal came to a stand, the elder Master Crummles got out and kicked him.", "그래도 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 고삐를 홱홱 당기고 채찍을 휘둘러서 그럭저럭 일으켜 세웠어."], ["By dint of these encouragements, he was persuaded to move from time to time, and they jogged on (as Mr.", "그리고 이 방법이 통하지 않아 동물이 아예 멈춰 서 버리면, 큰아들 크럼블스가 내려서 발길질을 했지. 이런 격려 덕분에 조랑말은 가끔씩 다시 움직이도록 설득당했어."], ["Crummles truly observed) very comfortably for all parties.", "그래서 (크럼블스 씨가 딱 맞게 말한 대로) 다들 아주 편안하게 덜컹덜컹 나아갔지."]], [["‘He’s a good pony at bottom,’ said Mr.", "\"이놈이 속은 착한 조랑말이라오.\""], ["Crummles, turning to Nicholas.", "크럼블스 씨가 니콜라스 쪽으로 돌아보며 말했어."]], [["He might have been at bottom, but he certainly was not at top, seeing that his coat was of the roughest and most ill-favoured kind.", "속은 그럴지도 몰라. 하지만 겉은 분명히 아니었지."], ["So, Nicholas merely observed that he shouldn’t wonder if he was.", "털가죽이 거칠대로 거칠고 볼품이라고는 없었거든. 그래서 니콜라스는 그냥, 그럴 만도 하겠다고만 했어."]], [["‘Many and many is the circuit this pony has gone,’ said Mr.", "\"이 조랑말이 돌아다닌 순회 길이 얼마나 많은지 모른다오.\""], ["Crummles, flicking him skilfully on the eyelid for old acquaintance’ sake.", "크럼블스 씨가 옛정을 생각하며 능숙하게 눈꺼풀을 툭 쳐 주며 말했어."], ["‘He is quite one of us.", "\"이놈도 우리 식구나 다름없지."], ["His mother was on the stage.’", "어미가 무대에 섰거든.\""]], [["‘Was she?’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"그랬어요?\" 니콜라스가 받아 물었어."]], [["‘She ate apple-pie at a circus for upwards of fourteen years,’ said the manager; ‘fired pistols, and went to bed in a nightcap; and, in short, took the low comedy entirely. His father was a dancer.’", "\"서커스에서 십사 년 넘게 사과 파이를 먹었지.\" 단장이 말했어. \"권총도 쏘고, 잠잘 땐 나이트캡까지 쓰고 잤다오. 한마디로 저급 희극을 도맡았던 거지. 아비는 무용수였고.\""]], [["‘Was he at all distinguished?’", "\"아비는 좀 이름이 났었나요?\""]], [["‘Not very,’ said the manager.", "\"별로.\" 단장이 말했어."], ["‘He was rather a low sort of pony.", "\"오히려 좀 천한 축에 드는 조랑말이었지."], ["The fact is, he had been originally jobbed out by the day, and he never quite got over his old habits.", "사실 처음에 날품으로 빌려 쓰이던 놈이라, 그 옛 버릇을 끝내 못 고쳤어."], ["He was clever in melodrama too, but too broad--too broad.", "멜로드라마에도 솜씨가 있긴 했는데, 너무 과해서 — 너무 과했지."], ["When the mother died, he took the port-wine business.’", "어미가 죽고 나선 포트와인 일을 맡았다오.\""]], [["‘The port-wine business!’ cried Nicholas.", "\"포트와인 일이라뇨!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어."]], [["‘Drinking port-wine with the clown,’ said the manager; ‘but he was greedy, and one night bit off the bowl of the glass, and choked himself, so his vulgarity was the death of him at last.’", "\"광대랑 같이 포트와인을 마시는 거지.\" 단장이 말했어. \"그런데 이놈이 욕심이 많아서, 어느 날 밤 잔의 둥근 부분을 물어뜯어 버렸다가 목이 막혀 버렸어. 그러니 결국 그 천박함이 제 명을 재촉한 셈이지.\""]], [["The descendant of this ill-starred animal requiring increased attention from Mr.", "이 기구한 동물의 후손은 그날의 일과가 진행될수록 점점 더 손이 많이 갔어."], ["Crummles as he progressed in his day’s work, that gentleman had very little time for conversation.", "그래서 크럼블스 씨는 이야기를 나눌 틈이 거의 없었지. 덕분에 니콜라스는 한가로이 제 생각에 잠겨 시간을 보낼 수 있었어."], ["Nicholas was thus left at leisure to entertain himself with his own thoughts, until they arrived at the drawbridge at Portsmouth, when Mr. Crummles pulled up.", "그러다 포츠머스의 도개교에 다다르자 크럼블스 씨가 말을 세웠어."]], [["‘We’ll get down here,’ said the manager, ‘and the boys will take him round to the stable, and call at my lodgings with the luggage.", "\"여기서 내립시다.\" 단장이 말했어."], ["You had better let yours be taken there, for the present.’", "\"애들이 이놈을 마구간으로 데려갔다가, 짐을 내 숙소에 갖다 놓을 거요. 당신 짐도 우선은 거기로 가져다 두는 게 좋겠소.\""]], [["Thanking Mr.", "빈센트 크럼블스 씨의 친절한 제안에 고맙다고 한 뒤, 니콜라스는 마차에서 폴짝 뛰어내렸어."], ["Vincent Crummles for his obliging offer, Nicholas jumped out, and, giving Smike his arm, accompanied the manager up High Street on their way to the theatre; feeling nervous and uncomfortable enough at the prospect of an immediate introduction to a scene so new to him.", "그러고는 스마이크에게 팔을 내주고, 단장을 따라 하이 스트리트를 올라 극장으로 향했지. 그토록 낯선 곳에 곧장 들어선다고 생각하니 어지간히 떨리고 불편한 기분이었어."]], [["They passed a great many bills, pasted against the walls and displayed in windows, wherein the names of Mr.", "두 사람은 벽에 붙고 창문에 내걸린 수많은 벽보 곁을 지났어. 거기에는 빈센트 크럼블스 씨, 빈센트 크럼블스 부인, 크럼블스 도련님, P."], ["Vincent Crummles, Mrs.", "크럼블스 도련님, 크럼블스 양의 이름이 아주 큼직한 글씨로 박혀 있었지."], ["Vincent Crummles, Master Crummles, Master P.", "나머지는 죄다 아주 작은 글씨였고. 그러다 마침내 어느 입구로 꺾어 들었는데, 거기서는 오렌지 껍질과 등유 냄새가 진하게 풍겼고, 그 밑으로 톱밥 냄새가 깔려 있었어."], ["Crummles, and Miss Crummles, were printed in very large letters, and everything else in very small ones; and, turning at length into an entry, in which was a strong smell of orange-peel and lamp-oil, with an under-current of sawdust, groped their way through a dark passage, and, descending a step or two, threaded a little maze of canvas screens and paint pots, and emerged upon the stage of the Portsmouth Theatre.", "두 사람은 어두운 통로를 더듬어 나아가, 한두 계단을 내려간 뒤, 천 가림막과 물감통이 빚어 놓은 작은 미로를 헤치고 빠져나왔지. 그러자 포츠머스 극장 무대 위로 나왔어."]], [["‘Here we are,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 다 왔소.\""], ["Crummles.", "크럼블스 씨가 말했어."]], [["It was not very light, but Nicholas found himself close to the first entrance on the prompt side, among bare walls, dusty scenes, mildewed clouds, heavily daubed draperies, and dirty floors.", "그리 밝지는 않았어. 니콜라스는 어느새 무대 우측 첫 번째 출입구 가까이에 서 있었는데, 둘레는 텅 빈 벽이며 먼지 낀 배경판, 곰팡이 핀 구름 그림, 두껍게 칠해 댄 휘장, 더러운 바닥뿐이었지."], ["He looked about him; ceiling, pit, boxes, gallery, orchestra, fittings, and decorations of every kind,--all looked coarse, cold, gloomy, and wretched.", "그는 둘레를 둘러봤어. 천장, 일층석, 박스석, 위층석, 오케스트라석, 온갖 비품과 장식들 — 죄다 거칠고, 썰렁하고, 음침하고, 초라해 보였어."]], [["‘Is this a theatre?’ whispered Smike, in amazement; ‘I thought it was a blaze of light and finery.’", "\"이게 극장이라고요?\" 스마이크가 어리둥절해서 속삭였어. \"저는 환한 불빛에 화려한 곳인 줄 알았는데요.\""]], [["‘Why, so it is,’ replied Nicholas, hardly less surprised; ‘but not by day, Smike--not by day.’", "\"맞아, 그렇지.\" 니콜라스도 못지않게 놀라며 대꾸했어. \"하지만 낮엔 아니야, 스마이크 — 낮엔 아니라고.\""]], [["The manager’s voice recalled him from a more careful inspection of the building, to the opposite side of the proscenium, where, at a small mahogany table with rickety legs and of an oblong shape, sat a stout, portly female, apparently between forty and fifty, in a tarnished silk cloak, with her bonnet dangling by the strings in her hand, and her hair (of which she had a great quantity) braided in a large festoon over each temple.", "단장의 목소리에 니콜라스는 건물을 더 꼼꼼히 살펴보다 말고 정신을 차렸어. 그쪽을 보니 무대 앞 반대편에, 다리가 흔들거리는 길쭉한 마호가니 작은 탁자가 있었지. 그 앞에는 살집 좋고 풍채 넉넉한 여인이 앉아 있었어. 보아하니 마흔에서 쉰 사이쯤이었지. 빛바랜 비단 망토를 두르고, 보닛은 끈을 손에 쥔 채 늘어뜨리고 있었어. 그리고 머리카락은 — 숱이 어마어마했는데 — 양쪽 관자놀이 위로 큼직하게 화환처럼 땋아 늘어뜨렸더라고."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"존슨 씨.\""], ["Johnson,’ said the manager (for Nicholas had given the name which Newman Noggs had bestowed upon him in his conversation with Mrs Kenwigs), ‘let me introduce Mrs.", "단장이 말했어 (니콜라스는 뉴먼 노그스가 케닉스 부인과 이야기할 때 지어 준 그 이름을 댔거든)."], ["Vincent Crummles.’", "\"빈센트 크럼블스 부인을 소개하리다.\""]], [["‘I am glad to see you, sir,’ said Mrs.", "\"만나서 반가워요.\""], ["Vincent Crummles, in a sepulchral voice.", "빈센트 크럼블스 부인이 무덤 속에서 울려 나오는 듯한 목소리로 말했어."], ["‘I am very glad to see you, and still more happy to hail you as a promising member of our corps.’", "\"정말 반가워요. 게다가 우리 극단의 촉망받는 일원으로 맞이하게 되어 더없이 기쁘답니다.\""]], [["The lady shook Nicholas by the hand as she addressed him in these terms; he saw it was a large one, but had not expected quite such an iron grip as that with which she honoured him.", "부인은 이렇게 말하면서 니콜라스의 손을 잡고 흔들었어. 손이 큼직한 건 보였지만, 이렇게 쇠집게 같은 악력으로 영광을 베풀 줄은 미처 예상하지 못했지."]], [["‘And this,’ said the lady, crossing to Smike, as tragic actresses cross when they obey a stage direction, ‘and this is the other. You too, are welcome, sir.’", "\"그리고 이쪽은.\" 부인이 스마이크 쪽으로 걸어갔어. 비극 여배우가 무대 지시에 따라 걸음을 옮길 때처럼 말이야. \"이쪽은 또 한 사람이군요. 당신도 환영해요.\""]], [["‘He’ll do, I think, my dear?’ said the manager, taking a pinch of snuff.", "\"이 친구도 쓸 만하겠지, 여보?\" 단장이 코담배를 한 줌 집으며 말했어."]], [["‘He is admirable,’ replied the lady.", "\"훌륭해요.\" 부인이 대답했어."], ["‘An acquisition indeed.’", "\"정말 좋은 인재예요.\""]], [["As Mrs. Vincent Crummles recrossed back to the table, there bounded on to the stage from some mysterious inlet, a little girl in a dirty white frock with tucks up to the knees, short trousers, sandaled shoes, white spencer, pink gauze bonnet, green veil and curl papers; who turned a pirouette, cut twice in the air, turned another pirouette, then, looking off at the opposite wing, shrieked, bounded forward to within six inches of the footlights, and fell into a beautiful attitude of terror, as a shabby gentleman in an old pair of buff slippers came in at one powerful slide, and chattering his teeth, fiercely brandished a walking-stick.", "빈센트 크럼블스 부인이 다시 탁자 쪽으로 걸음을 돌렸을 때였어. 어딘가 알 수 없는 입구에서 어린 여자아이 하나가 무대 위로 폴짝 뛰어나왔지. 무릎까지 주름을 잡은 더러운 흰 원피스에, 짧은 바지, 끈 달린 샌들, 흰 스펜서 재킷, 분홍 망사 보닛, 초록 면사포, 그리고 종이로 만 곱슬머리를 하고 있었어. 그 아이는 한 바퀴 피루엣을 돌더니, 공중에서 두 번 발을 부딪치고, 또 한 번 피루엣을 돌았지. 그러고는 반대편 무대 옆을 바라보며 비명을 지르고, 앞으로 뛰어나와 각광 등에서 십오 센티미터쯤 되는 곳에서 멈춰 서더니, 아름다운 공포의 자세로 쓰러졌어. 그때 낡은 황갈색 슬리퍼를 신은 허름한 신사가 한 번에 힘차게 미끄러져 들어왔지. 그러고는 이를 딱딱 부딪치며 지팡이를 사납게 휘둘렀어."]], [["‘They are going through the Indian Savage and the Maiden,’ said Mrs Crummles.", "\"지금 '인디언 야만인과 처녀'를 연습하는 중이에요.\" 크럼블스 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘Oh!’ said the manager, ‘the little ballet interlude.", "\"오!\" 단장이 말했어."], ["Very good, go on.", "\"그 짧은 발레 막간극이로군."], ["A little this way, if you please, Mr.", "아주 좋아, 계속해. 이쪽으로 조금만 와요, 존슨 씨."], ["Johnson. That’ll do.", "그래, 됐어."], ["Now!’", "자!\""]], [["The manager clapped his hands as a signal to proceed, and the savage, becoming ferocious, made a slide towards the maiden; but the maiden avoided him in six twirls, and came down, at the end of the last one, upon the very points of her toes. This seemed to make some impression upon the savage; for, after a little more ferocity and chasing of the maiden into corners, he began to relent, and stroked his face several times with his right thumb and four fingers, thereby intimating that he was struck with admiration of the maiden’s beauty. Acting upon the impulse of this passion, he (the savage) began to hit himself severe thumps in the chest, and to exhibit other indications of being desperately in love, which being rather a prosy proceeding, was very likely the cause of the maiden’s falling asleep; whether it was or no, asleep she did fall, sound as a church, on a sloping bank, and the savage perceiving it, leant his left ear on his left hand, and nodded sideways, to intimate to all whom it might concern that she WAS asleep, and no shamming. Being left to himself, the savage had a dance, all alone. Just as he left off, the maiden woke up, rubbed her eyes, got off the bank, and had a dance all alone too--such a dance that the savage looked on in ecstasy all the while, and when it was done, plucked from a neighbouring tree some botanical curiosity, resembling a small pickled cabbage, and offered it to the maiden, who at first wouldn’t have it, but on the savage shedding tears relented. Then the savage jumped for joy; then the maiden jumped for rapture at the sweet smell of the pickled cabbage. Then the savage and the maiden danced violently together, and, finally, the savage dropped down on one knee, and the maiden stood on one leg upon his other knee; thus concluding the ballet, and leaving the spectators in a state of pleasing uncertainty, whether she would ultimately marry the savage, or return to her friends.", "단장이 시작하라는 신호로 손뼉을 쳤어. 그러자 야만인이 사나워지며 처녀를 향해 미끄러져 갔지. 하지만 처녀는 여섯 번 빙글빙글 돌며 그를 피했고, 마지막 회전 끝에 발끝으로 사뿐히 내려섰어. 이게 야만인에게 좀 인상을 남긴 모양이었어. 야만인은 조금 더 사납게 굴며 처녀를 구석으로 몰아붙이다가, 마음이 누그러지기 시작했거든. 그러고는 오른손 엄지와 네 손가락으로 제 얼굴을 몇 번 쓰다듬었어. 처녀의 아름다움에 감탄했다는 뜻이었지. 이 격정에 사로잡힌 야만인은 제 가슴을 세게 쾅쾅 두드리기 시작했고, 또 죽도록 사랑에 빠졌다는 온갖 표시를 내보였어. 그런데 이게 좀 따분하게 늘어진 탓이었는지, 아마 그래서 처녀가 잠들어 버린 것 같았어. 그게 이유였든 아니든, 처녀는 잠이 들었어. 비탈진 둔덕 위에서 교회처럼 새근새근 깊이 잠들었지. 야만인은 그걸 알아채고는 왼손에 왼쪽 귀를 기대고 고개를 옆으로 끄덕였어. 관계된 모든 이에게 처녀가 정말로 잠들었지, 자는 척하는 게 아니라는 걸 알리려는 거였지. 혼자 남은 야만인은 홀로 춤을 췄어. 그가 막 춤을 멈추자, 처녀가 깨어나 눈을 비비고 둔덕에서 내려와 또 혼자 춤을 췄지. 어찌나 멋진 춤이었던지 야만인은 내내 황홀하게 바라봤어. 춤이 끝나자 그는 옆의 나무에서 무슨 식물 같은 신기한 걸 뜯어 왔는데, 작은 절인 양배추처럼 생긴 거였어. 그걸 처녀에게 내밀었지. 처녀는 처음엔 받지 않으려 했지만, 야만인이 눈물을 흘리자 마음을 누그러뜨렸어. 그러자 야만인은 기뻐서 펄쩍 뛰었지. 이어서 처녀도 그 절인 양배추의 달콤한 냄새에 황홀해져 펄쩍 뛰었어. 그러고는 야만인과 처녀가 격렬하게 함께 춤을 췄어. 마침내 야만인이 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고, 처녀가 그의 다른 쪽 무릎 위에 한 발로 섰지. 이렇게 발레가 끝났어. 그리고 관객들은 즐거운 궁금증에 잠긴 채 남겨졌지. 처녀가 끝내 야만인과 결혼할지, 아니면 제 친구들에게 돌아갈지 말이야."]], [["‘Very well indeed,’ said Mr.", "\"정말 훌륭해.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어."], ["Crummles; ‘bravo!’", "\"브라보!\""]], [["‘Bravo!’ cried Nicholas, resolved to make the best of everything.", "\"브라보!\" 니콜라스도 외쳤어."], ["‘Beautiful!’", "뭐든 좋게 보려고 마음먹은 거지. \"아름답습니다!\""]], [["‘This, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"이 아이가 말이오.\""], ["Vincent Crummles, bringing the maiden forward, ‘this is the infant phenomenon--Miss Ninetta Crummles.’", "빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 처녀를 앞으로 데려오며 말했어. \"이 아이가 바로 그 어린 신동 — 니네타 크럼블스 양이라오.\""]], [["‘Your daughter?’ inquired Nicholas.", "\"따님인가요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘My daughter--my daughter,’ replied Mr.", "\"내 딸이지, 내 딸.\""], ["Vincent Crummles; ‘the idol of every place we go into, sir.", "빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 대답했어. \"우리가 가는 곳마다 우상이라오."], ["We have had complimentary letters about this girl, sir, from the nobility and gentry of almost every town in England.’", "이 아이에 대해 영국 거의 모든 마을의 귀족과 신사들한테서 칭찬 편지를 받았다니까.\""]], [["‘I am not surprised at that,’ said Nicholas; ‘she must be quite a natural genius.’", "\"그럴 만도 하네요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"타고난 천재가 틀림없어요.\""]], [["‘Quite a--!’ Mr.", "\"그야말로 —!\""], ["Crummles stopped: language was not powerful enough to describe the infant phenomenon.", "크럼블스 씨가 말을 멈췄어. 이 어린 신동을 표현할 만큼 강한 말이 없었던 거지."], ["‘I’ll tell you what, sir,’ he said; ‘the talent of this child is not to be imagined.", "\"내 말해 두지만 말이오.\" 그가 말했어."], ["She must be seen, sir--seen--to be ever so faintly appreciated.", "\"이 아이의 재능은 도무지 상상도 못 할 정도라오. 직접 봐야만 — 봐야만 — 그나마 어렴풋이라도 알아볼 수 있다오."], ["There; go to your mother, my dear.’", "자, 어머니한테 가 보거라, 얘야.\""]], [["‘May I ask how old she is?’ inquired Nicholas.", "\"실례지만 몇 살인지 물어봐도 될까요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘You may, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"물어봐도 되고말고.\""], ["Crummles, looking steadily in his questioner’s face, as some men do when they have doubts about being implicitly believed in what they are going to say.", "크럼블스 씨가 대답했어. 자기 말이 곧이곧대로 믿어질지 의심스러울 때 어떤 사람들이 그러듯, 질문한 사람의 얼굴을 똑바로 응시하면서 말이야."], ["‘She is ten years of age, sir.’", "\"열 살이라오.\" \"그것밖에 안 됐다고요!\""]], [["‘Not more!’\n‘Not a day.’", "\"하루도 더 안 됐지.\""]], [["‘Dear me!’ said Nicholas, ‘it’s extraordinary.’", "\"아이고!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"정말 놀랍군요.\""]], [["It was; for the infant phenomenon, though of short stature, had a comparatively aged countenance, and had moreover been precisely the same age--not perhaps to the full extent of the memory of the oldest inhabitant, but certainly for five good years. But she had been kept up late every night, and put upon an unlimited allowance of gin-and-water from infancy, to prevent her growing tall, and perhaps this system of training had produced in the infant phenomenon these additional phenomena.", "놀랍긴 했어. 이 어린 신동은 키는 작았지만 얼굴은 비교적 나이 들어 보였거든. 게다가 정확히 이 나이로 머물러 있은 지가 — 가장 나이 많은 마을 사람의 기억을 다 더듬을 정도까지는 아니어도 — 확실히 오 년은 족히 됐단 말이지. 하지만 이 아이는 키가 자라지 못하도록 밤마다 늦게까지 깨어 있었고, 갓난아기 때부터 진과 물을 무제한으로 마셔 댔어. 아마 이런 훈련 방식이 어린 신동에게 그 추가적인 신기한 현상들을 만들어 낸 모양이야."]], [["While this short dialogue was going on, the gentleman who had enacted the savage, came up, with his walking shoes on his feet, and his slippers in his hand, to within a few paces, as if desirous to join in the conversation.", "이 짧은 대화가 오가는 동안, 야만인 역을 맡았던 신사가 다가왔어. 발에는 산책용 구두를 신고, 손에는 슬리퍼를 든 채로 말이야."], ["Deeming this a good opportunity, he put in his word.", "대화에 끼고 싶은 듯 몇 걸음 앞까지 와 멈췄지. 그러고는 이때다 싶었는지 한마디 끼워 넣었어."]], [["‘Talent there, sir!’ said the savage, nodding towards Miss Crummles.", "\"저 아이, 재능이 대단하지요!\" 야만인이 크럼블스 양 쪽으로 고갯짓하며 말했어."]], [["Nicholas assented.", "니콜라스도 맞장구쳤어."]], [["‘Ah!’ said the actor, setting his teeth together, and drawing in his breath with a hissing sound, ‘she oughtn’t to be in the provinces, she oughtn’t.’", "\"아!\" 배우가 이를 악물고 쉬익 소리를 내며 숨을 들이켰어. \"저 아이는 지방에 있을 애가 아닌데, 아니라고요.\""]], [["‘What do you mean?’ asked the manager.", "\"그게 무슨 소린가?\" 단장이 물었어."]], [["‘I mean to say,’ replied the other, warmly, ‘that she is too good for country boards, and that she ought to be in one of the large houses in London, or nowhere; and I tell you more, without mincing the matter, that if it wasn’t for envy and jealousy in some quarter that you know of, she would be.", "\"내 말은 말입니다.\" 상대방이 열을 올리며 대답했어."], ["Perhaps you’ll introduce me here, Mr.", "\"저 아이는 시골 무대엔 너무 아까운 인재라, 런던의 큰 극장 가운데 하나에 서든가, 아니면 아예 어디에도 안 서는 게 맞다는 거지요. 게다가 한마디 더 보태자면, 에두르지 않고 말해서, 당신도 아는 어떤 쪽의 시기와 질투만 아니었으면 진작 그렇게 됐을 거란 말입니다."], ["Crummles.’", "그건 그렇고, 저를 좀 소개해 주시지요, 크럼블스 씨.\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"폴레어 씨라오.\""], ["Folair,’ said the manager, presenting him to Nicholas.", "단장이 그를 니콜라스에게 소개했어."]], [["‘Happy to know you, sir.’ Mr.", "\"만나서 반갑소이다.\""], ["Folair touched the brim of his hat with his forefinger, and then shook hands.", "폴레어 씨가 집게손가락으로 모자챙을 건드리고 나서 악수를 청했어."], ["‘A recruit, sir, I understand?’", "\"새로 들어온 분이라고요?\""]], [["‘An unworthy one,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"보잘것없는 신참이지요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Did you ever see such a set-out as that?’ whispered the actor, drawing him away, as Crummles left them to speak to his wife.", "\"저런 꼴 본 적 있소?\" 배우가 니콜라스를 한쪽으로 끌고 가며 속삭였어. 마침 크럼블스가 아내에게 말을 걸려고 두 사람 곁을 떠났거든."]], [["‘As what?’", "\"어떤 꼴 말입니까?\""]], [["Mr. Folair made a funny face from his pantomime collection, and pointed over his shoulder.", "폴레어 씨는 자기 무언극 밑천에서 우스꽝스러운 표정 하나를 골라 짓더니, 어깨너머를 가리켰어."]], [["‘You don’t mean the infant phenomenon?’", "\"설마 그 어린 신동을 말하는 건 아니지요?\""]], [["‘Infant humbug, sir,’ replied Mr. Folair.", "\"신동이 아니라 사기예요.\""], ["‘There isn’t a female child of common sharpness in a charity school, that couldn’t do better than that.", "폴레어 씨가 대답했어. \"자선 학교에 있는, 그저 그만큼 영악한 여자애라도 저것보단 잘하겠소."], ["She may thank her stars she was born a manager’s daughter.’", "저 애는 단장 딸로 태어난 제 팔자에 감사나 해야지.\""]], [["‘You seem to take it to heart,’ observed Nicholas, with a smile.", "\"단단히 마음에 두고 계신 모양이군요.\" 니콜라스가 웃으며 말했어."]], [["‘Yes, by Jove, and well I may,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇소, 정말이지 그럴 만하잖소.\""], ["Folair, drawing his arm through his, and walking him up and down the stage.", "폴레어 씨가 니콜라스의 팔에 제 팔을 끼고 무대 위를 왔다 갔다 거닐며 말했어."], ["‘Isn’t it enough to make a man crusty to see that little sprawler put up in the best business every night, and actually keeping money out of the house, by being forced down the people’s throats, while other people are passed over?", "\"저 조그만 게 밤마다 제일 좋은 자리에 떡하니 올라가서는, 사람들 목구멍에 억지로 밀어 넣어지는 통에 오히려 손님을 쫓아낸단 말이오. 그 사이에 다른 사람들은 그냥 지나쳐지고."], ["Isn’t it extraordinary to see a man’s confounded family conceit blinding him, even to his own interest?", "이러니 사람 심사가 안 뒤틀리겠소? 게다가 한 인간이 제 집안 잘난 척에 눈이 멀어서, 제 잇속까지 못 보는 걸 지켜보는 게 어디 보통 일이오?"], ["Why I KNOW of fifteen and sixpence that came to Southampton one night last month, to see me dance the Highland Fling; and what’s the consequence?", "글쎄, 내 알기로 지난달 어느 날 밤, 내가 하이랜드 플링 추는 걸 보겠다고 사우샘프턴에서 십오 실링 육 펜스가 들어왔단 말이오. 그런데 그 결과가 뭐였소?"], ["I’ve never been put up in it since--never once--while the “infant phenomenon”", "그 뒤로 난 그 춤판에 한 번도 못 올라갔소 — 단 한 번도 — 그동안 저 '어린 신동'은 매일 밤 조화 사이로 이를 드러내고 웃어 댔지."], ["has been grinning through artificial flowers at five people and a baby in the pit, and two boys in the gallery, every night.’", "일층석에 손님 다섯에 갓난애 하나, 위층석에 사내애 둘 앞에서 말이오.\""]], [["‘If I may judge from what I have seen of you,’ said Nicholas, ‘you must be a valuable member of the company.’", "\"제가 본 바로 미루어 짐작컨대.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"선생은 이 극단에서 아주 값진 단원이 틀림없겠군요.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ replied Mr.", "\"오!\""], ["Folair, beating his slippers together, to knock the dust out; ‘I CAN come it pretty well--nobody better, perhaps, in my own line--but having such business as one gets here, is like putting lead on one’s feet instead of chalk, and dancing in fetters without the credit of it.", "폴레어 씨가 슬리퍼를 맞부딪쳐 먼지를 털며 대답했어. \"내 솜씨야 꽤 괜찮소 — 적어도 내 분야에선 나만 한 사람도 없을 거요 — 하지만 여기서 맡는 역이라는 게, 발에 분필 대신 납덩이를 매단 꼴이오."], ["Holloa, old fellow, how are you?’", "인정도 못 받으면서 족쇄를 차고 춤추는 거나 마찬가지지. 어이, 친구, 잘 지냈나?\""]], [["The gentleman addressed in these latter words was a dark-complexioned man, inclining indeed to sallow, with long thick black hair, and very evident inclinations (although he was close shaved) of a stiff beard, and whiskers of the same deep shade. His age did not appear to exceed thirty, though many at first sight would have considered him much older, as his face was long, and very pale, from the constant application of stage paint. He wore a checked shirt, an old green coat with new gilt buttons, a neckerchief of broad red and green stripes, and full blue trousers; he carried, too, a common ash walking-stick, apparently more for show than use, as he flourished it about, with the hooked end downwards, except when he raised it for a few seconds, and throwing himself into a fencing attitude, made a pass or two at the side-scenes, or at any other object, animate or inanimate, that chanced to afford him a pretty good mark at the moment.", "이 마지막 말이 향한 신사는 가무잡잡한 사내였어. 사실 누르스름한 쪽에 가까웠지. 길고 숱 많은 검은 머리에, 말끔하게 면도를 했는데도 뻣뻣한 수염 자국이 또렷이 드러나 있었고, 구레나룻도 같은 짙은 빛이었어. 나이는 서른을 넘지 않아 보였지만, 처음 보면 훨씬 더 들어 보인다고 여길 사람도 많았을 거야. 무대 분장을 끊임없이 한 탓에 얼굴이 길쭉하고 아주 창백했거든. 그는 체크무늬 셔츠에, 새 금빛 단추를 단 낡은 초록 코트를 입고, 붉고 푸른 굵은 줄무늬 목도리를 두르고, 통 넓은 파란 바지를 입고 있었어. 또 평범한 물푸레나무 지팡이도 들고 있었는데, 쓰임새보다는 폼을 위한 거였지. 갈고리 진 끝을 아래로 향한 채 휘두르다가, 가끔 몇 초쯤 들어 올려서는 펜싱 자세를 잡고 무대 옆 배경이나, 그 순간 꽤 그럴듯한 표적이 되어 준 다른 무언가를 향해 — 살아 있든 죽어 있든 — 찌르는 시늉을 한두 번 하곤 했어."]], [["‘Well, Tommy,’ said this gentleman, making a thrust at his friend, who parried it dexterously with his slipper, ‘what’s the news?’", "\"어, 토미.\" 이 신사가 친구를 향해 한 번 찔러 보며 말했어. 친구는 슬리퍼로 능숙하게 막아 냈지. \"뭐 새로운 소식 없나?\""]], [["‘A new appearance, that’s all,’ replied Mr.", "\"새 얼굴 하나 들어왔지, 그게 다야.\""], ["Folair, looking at Nicholas.", "폴레어 씨가 니콜라스를 보며 대답했어."]], [["‘Do the honours, Tommy, do the honours,’ said the other gentleman, tapping him reproachfully on the crown of the hat with his stick.", "\"예의를 차려야지, 토미, 예의를 차려.\" 다른 신사가 지팡이로 폴레어의 모자 꼭대기를 나무라듯 톡톡 치며 말했어."]], [["‘This is Mr. Lenville, who does our first tragedy, Mr.", "\"이쪽은 우리 일급 비극을 맡은 렌빌 씨예요, 존슨 씨.\""], ["Johnson,’ said the pantomimist.", "무언극 배우가 말했어."]], [["‘Except when old bricks and mortar takes it into his head to do it himself, you should add, Tommy,’ remarked Mr.", "\"가끔 벽돌이랑 회반죽 영감이 손수 하고 싶어 할 때만 빼고지, 그렇게 덧붙여야겠네, 토미.\" 렌빌 씨가 한마디 했어."], ["Lenville. ‘You know who bricks and mortar is, I suppose, sir?’", "\"벽돌이랑 회반죽이 누군지는 아시겠지요?\""]], [["‘I do not, indeed,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"실은 모릅니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘We call Crummles that, because his style of acting is rather in the heavy and ponderous way,’ said Mr. Lenville. ‘I mustn’t be cracking jokes though, for I’ve got a part of twelve lengths here, which I must be up in tomorrow night, and I haven’t had time to look at it yet; I’m a confounded quick study, that’s one comfort.’", "\"우리가 크럼블스를 그렇게 부른다오. 연기 스타일이 좀 무겁고 둔중한 편이라서.\" 렌빌 씨가 말했어. \"하지만 농담이나 하고 있을 처지가 아니지. 여기 열두 장짜리 대사를 받았는데, 내일 밤까지 다 외워야 하거든. 아직 들여다볼 틈도 없었소. 그래도 내가 외우는 건 지독히 빨라서, 그게 그나마 위안이오.\""]], [["Consoling himself with this reflection, Mr.", "이런 생각으로 스스로를 달래며, 렌빌 씨는 코트 주머니에서 기름때 묻고 구깃구깃한 원고를 꺼냈어."], ["Lenville drew from his coat pocket a greasy and crumpled manuscript, and, having made another pass at his friend, proceeded to walk to and fro, conning it to himself and indulging occasionally in such appropriate action as his imagination and the text suggested.", "그러고는 친구에게 한 번 더 찌르는 시늉을 하더니, 이리저리 거닐기 시작했지. 혼잣말로 대사를 외면서, 가끔 자기 상상과 본문이 일러 주는 대로 어울리는 동작을 곁들였어."]], [["A pretty general muster of the company had by this time taken place; for besides Mr. Lenville and his friend Tommy, there were present, a slim young gentleman with weak eyes, who played the low-spirited lovers and sang tenor songs, and who had come arm-in-arm with the comic countryman--a man with a turned-up nose, large mouth, broad face, and staring eyes. Making himself very amiable to the infant phenomenon, was an inebriated elderly gentleman in the last depths of shabbiness, who played the calm and virtuous old men; and paying especial court to Mrs Crummles was another elderly gentleman, a shade more respectable, who played the irascible old men--those funny fellows who have nephews in the army and perpetually run about with thick sticks to compel them to marry heiresses. Besides these, there was a roving-looking person in a rough great-coat, who strode up and down in front of the lamps, flourishing a dress cane, and rattling away, in an undertone, with great vivacity for the amusement of an ideal audience. He was not quite so young as he had been, and his figure was rather running to seed; but there was an air of exaggerated gentility about him, which bespoke the hero of swaggering comedy. There was, also, a little group of three or four young men with lantern jaws and thick eyebrows, who were conversing in one corner; but they seemed to be of secondary importance, and laughed and talked together without attracting any attention.", "이때쯤이면 극단원들이 꽤 두루 모여 있었어. 렌빌 씨와 그 친구 토미 말고도, 눈이 약한 호리호리한 젊은 신사가 와 있었지. 그는 풀죽은 연인 역을 맡고 테너 노래를 불렀는데, 코미디 시골뜨기 역과 팔짱을 끼고 들어왔어. 그 시골뜨기 역은 들창코에 입이 크고 얼굴이 넓적하고 눈을 부릅뜬 사내였지. 어린 신동에게 살갑게 구는 건, 누추함의 극치에 이른 술 취한 늙은 신사였어. 그는 차분하고 덕 있는 노인 역을 맡았지. 그리고 크럼블스 부인에게 유난히 비위를 맞추는 건 또 다른 늙은 신사였는데, 그쪽보다는 한결 점잖아 보였어. 그는 성마른 노인 역을 맡았지 — 군대에 조카를 둬서 그 조카더러 상속녀와 결혼하라고 굵은 지팡이를 들고 줄곧 쫓아다니는, 그런 우스운 친구들 말이야. 이들 말고도 거친 외투를 걸친, 떠돌이 같은 인상의 사내가 하나 있었어. 그는 각광 등 앞을 성큼성큼 오가며 정장용 지팡이를 휘두르고, 가상의 관객을 즐겁게 하려는 듯 낮은 소리로 활기차게 떠들어 댔지. 한창때만큼 젊지는 않았고, 몸도 좀 망가져 가는 참이었지만, 어딘가 과장된 점잔이 풍겨서 거들먹거리는 희극의 주인공임을 알 수 있었어. 또 한구석에는 턱이 길쭉하고 눈썹이 짙은 젊은이 서넛이 작게 무리 지어 이야기를 나누고 있었지. 하지만 이들은 그리 중요한 축은 아닌 듯, 아무 눈길도 끌지 못한 채 자기들끼리 웃고 떠들었어."]], [["The ladies were gathered in a little knot by themselves round the rickety table before mentioned.", "여자들은 앞서 말한 그 흔들거리는 탁자 둘레에 따로 작은 무리를 이루어 모여 있었어."], ["There was Miss Snevellicci--who could do anything, from a medley dance to Lady Macbeth, and also always played some part in blue silk knee-smalls at her benefit--glancing, from the depths of her coal-scuttle straw bonnet, at Nicholas, and affecting to be absorbed in the recital of a diverting story to her friend Miss Ledrook, who had brought her work, and was making up a ruff in the most natural manner possible.", "거기엔 스네벨리치 양이 있었지 — 그녀는 메들리 댄스부터 맥베스 부인 역까지 못 하는 게 없었고, 게다가 자기 후원 공연 때면 늘 파란 비단 반바지 차림으로 어떤 역이든 하나는 맡았어 — 석탄통처럼 생긴 밀짚 보닛 속 깊은 데서 니콜라스를 힐끔거리면서, 친구 레드룩 양에게 무슨 재미난 이야기를 들려주는 데 푹 빠진 척하고 있었지. 레드룩 양은 바느질거리를 가져와서, 더없이 자연스러운 솜씨로 주름 깃을 만들고 있었어."], ["There was Miss Belvawney--who seldom aspired to speaking parts, and usually went on as a page in white silk hose, to stand with one leg bent, and contemplate the audience, or to go in and out after Mr.", "또 벨바우니 양도 있었어 — 그녀는 대사 있는 역엔 좀처럼 욕심내지 않고, 보통은 흰 비단 양말을 신은 시동 역으로 나와서 한쪽 다리를 굽히고 서서 관객을 바라보거나, 위풍당당한 비극에서 크럼블스 씨를 따라 들락날락했지 — 그 벨바우니 양이 아름다운 브라바사 양의 곱슬머리를 말아 올리고 있었어."], ["Crummles in stately tragedy--twisting up the ringlets of the beautiful Miss Bravassa, who had once had her likeness taken ‘in character’ by an engraver’s apprentice, whereof impressions were hung up for sale in the pastry-cook’s window, and the greengrocer’s, and at the circulating library, and the box-office, whenever the announce bills came out for her annual night.", "브라바사 양은 언젠가 판화 견습공한테 '배역 차림으로' 초상을 찍은 적이 있었는데, 그 인쇄본이 그녀의 연례 공연 벽보가 나붙을 때마다 제과점 진열창이며 채소 가게, 대출 도서관, 매표소에 내걸려 팔리곤 했지."], ["There was Mrs.", "또 렌빌 부인도 있었어."], ["Lenville, in a very limp bonnet and veil, decidedly in that way in which she would wish to be if she truly loved Mr.", "아주 축 늘어진 보닛에 면사포를 쓰고서, 만약 그녀가 정말로 렌빌 씨를 사랑한다면 바라 마지않을 바로 그런 상태로 말이야."], ["Lenville; there was Miss Gazingi, with an imitation ermine boa tied in a loose knot round her neck, flogging Mr.", "또 가징기 양도 있었는데, 모조 담비 목도리를 헐겁게 매듭지어 목에 두르고는, 그 양쪽 끝으로 어린 크럼블스를 장난삼아 후려치고 있었어. 마지막으로 그러든 부인이 있었어."], ["Crummles, junior, with both ends, in fun.", "갈색 모직 외투에 비버 가죽 보닛 차림이었지."], ["Lastly, there was Mrs. Grudden in a brown cloth pelisse and a beaver bonnet, who assisted Mrs.", "그녀는 크럼블스 부인의 집안일을 거들고, 문간에서 입장료를 받고, 여배우들 옷을 입혀 주고, 극장을 쓸고, 마지막 장면에 다들 무대에 나가 있을 땐 프롬프트 대본을 들었어."], ["Crummles in her domestic affairs, and took money at the doors, and dressed the ladies, and swept the house, and held the prompt book when everybody else was on for the last scene, and acted any kind of part on any emergency without ever learning it, and was put down in the bills under any name or names whatever, that occurred to Mr. Crummles as looking well in print.", "또 어떤 비상시에든 외운 적도 없는 아무 역이나 척척 해냈고, 벽보엔 크럼블스 씨가 인쇄로 보기 좋다 싶은 아무 이름이나, 이름이 몇 개든 갖다 붙여 올렸지."]], [["Mr. Folair having obligingly confided these particulars to Nicholas, left him to mingle with his fellows; the work of personal introduction was completed by Mr.", "폴레어 씨가 친절하게 이런 자세한 사정을 니콜라스에게 일러 준 뒤, 동료들과 어울리러 가 버렸어."], ["Vincent Crummles, who publicly heralded the new actor as a prodigy of genius and learning.", "그리고 사람들에게 일일이 소개해 주는 일은 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 마무리했지. 그는 이 새 배우를 천재와 학식의 신동이라고 공공연히 알렸어."]], [["‘I beg your pardon,’ said Miss Snevellicci, sidling towards Nicholas, ‘but did you ever play at Canterbury?’", "\"실례지만요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 니콜라스 쪽으로 슬며시 다가오며 말했어. \"혹시 캔터베리에서 무대에 선 적 있으세요?\""]], [["‘I never did,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘I recollect meeting a gentleman at Canterbury,’ said Miss Snevellicci, ‘only for a few moments, for I was leaving the company as he joined it, so like you that I felt almost certain it was the same.’", "\"제가 캔터베리에서 어떤 신사분을 만난 기억이 나서요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했어. \"그저 잠깐이었지요. 그분이 극단에 들어올 때 저는 떠나는 참이었거든요. 당신하고 어찌나 닮았던지, 같은 사람이 거의 틀림없다 싶었어요.\""]], [["‘I see you now for the first time,’ rejoined Nicholas with all due gallantry.", "\"저는 지금 처음 뵙는걸요.\" 니콜라스가 합당한 정중함을 다해 대꾸했어."], ["‘I am sure I never saw you before; I couldn’t have forgotten it.’", "\"분명히 전엔 뵌 적이 없습니다. 봤다면 잊었을 리가 없지요.\""]], [["‘Oh, I’m sure--it’s very flattering of you to say so,’ retorted Miss Snevellicci with a graceful bend.", "\"어머, 정말이지 — 그렇게 말씀해 주시니 무척 으쓱하네요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 우아하게 허리를 굽히며 받아쳤어."], ["‘Now I look at you again, I see that the gentleman at Canterbury hadn’t the same eyes as you--you’ll think me very foolish for taking notice of such things, won’t you?’", "\"이제 다시 보니, 캔터베리의 그 신사분은 당신하고 눈이 다르네요 — 이런 걸 일일이 눈여겨본다고 저를 무척 어리석다 여기시겠죠, 안 그래요?\""]], [["‘Not at all,’ said Nicholas.", "\"천만에요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘How can I feel otherwise than flattered by your notice in any way?’", "\"어떤 식으로든 당신이 저를 눈여겨봐 주시는데 으쓱하지 않을 리가 있겠습니까?\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["you men are such vain creatures!’ cried Miss Snevellicci.", "남자분들은 어쩜 그리 다들 허영이 넘치는지!\""], ["Whereupon, she became charmingly confused, and, pulling out her pocket-handkerchief from a faded pink silk reticule with a gilt clasp, called to Miss Ledrook--", "스네벨리치 양이 외쳤어. 그러고는 사랑스럽게 당황하더니, 금빛 잠금쇠가 달린 빛바랜 분홍 비단 손가방에서 손수건을 꺼내며 레드룩 양을 불렀어."]], [["‘Led, my dear,’ said Miss Snevellicci.", "\"레드, 얘.\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했어."]], [["‘Well, what is the matter?’ said Miss Ledrook.", "\"왜, 무슨 일인데?\" 레드룩 양이 말했어."]], [["‘It’s not the same.’", "\"그 사람 아니야.\""]], [["‘Not the same what?’", "\"뭐가 아니라는 거야?\""]], [["‘Canterbury--you know what I mean.", "\"캔터베리 — 무슨 말인지 알잖아."], ["Come here!", "이리 와 봐!"], ["I want to speak to you.’", "너한테 할 말이 있어.\""]], [["But Miss Ledrook wouldn’t come to Miss Snevellicci, so Miss Snevellicci was obliged to go to Miss Ledrook, which she did, in a skipping manner that was quite fascinating; and Miss Ledrook evidently joked Miss Snevellicci about being struck with Nicholas; for, after some playful whispering, Miss Snevellicci hit Miss Ledrook very hard on the backs of her hands, and retired up, in a state of pleasing confusion.", "하지만 레드룩 양은 스네벨리치 양에게 오려 하지 않았어. 그래서 스네벨리치 양이 레드룩 양에게 갈 수밖에 없었지. 그녀는 깡충깡충 뛰는 듯한 걸음으로 갔는데, 그게 어찌나 매력적이던지. 그리고 레드룩 양은 분명 스네벨리치 양이 니콜라스에게 반했다고 놀려 댔어. 장난스레 한참 속닥인 끝에, 스네벨리치 양이 레드룩 양의 손등을 아주 세게 한 대 때리고는, 즐겁게 당황한 채 뒤로 물러났거든."]], [["‘Ladies and gentlemen,’ said Mr.", "\"신사 숙녀 여러분.\""], ["Vincent Crummles, who had been writing on a piece of paper, ‘we’ll call the Mortal Struggle tomorrow at ten; everybody for the procession.", "빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 종이쪽에 뭔가 적고 나서 말했어. \"내일 열 시에 '필사의 격투'를 연습합시다."], ["Intrigue, and Ways and Means, you’re all up in, so we shall only want one rehearsal.", "다들 행진 장면에 나오고. '음모'랑 '수단과 방법'은 다들 외워 두었으니, 한 번만 리허설하면 되겠소."], ["Everybody at ten, if you please.’", "모두 열 시까지 와 주시오.\""]], [["‘Everybody at ten,’ repeated Mrs.", "\"모두 열 시.\""], ["Grudden, looking about her.", "그러든 부인이 둘레를 둘러보며 따라 말했어."]], [["‘On Monday morning we shall read a new piece,’ said Mr.", "\"월요일 아침엔 새 작품을 읽겠소.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어."], ["Crummles; ‘the name’s not known yet, but everybody will have a good part. Mr.", "\"제목은 아직 안 정해졌지만, 다들 좋은 역을 맡을 거요."], ["Johnson will take care of that.’", "그건 존슨 씨가 챙길 테니.\""]], [["‘Hallo!’ said Nicholas, starting.", "\"엥!\" 니콜라스가 흠칫 놀라며 말했어."], ["‘I--’", "\"제가 —\""]], [["‘On Monday morning,’ repeated Mr.", "\"월요일 아침이오.\""], ["Crummles, raising his voice, to drown the unfortunate Mr.", "크럼블스 씨가 가엾은 존슨 씨의 항의를 묻어 버리려고 목소리를 높여 되풀이했어."], ["Johnson’s remonstrance; ‘that’ll do, ladies and gentlemen.’", "\"그럼 됐소, 신사 숙녀 여러분.\""]], [["The ladies and gentlemen required no second notice to quit; and, in a few minutes, the theatre was deserted, save by the Crummles family, Nicholas, and Smike.", "신사 숙녀들은 두 번 말할 것도 없이 자리를 떴어. 그리고 몇 분 만에 극장에는 크럼블스 가족과 니콜라스, 스마이크만 남았지."]], [["‘Upon my word,’ said Nicholas, taking the manager aside, ‘I don’t think I can be ready by Monday.’", "\"솔직히 말씀드리자면.\" 니콜라스가 단장을 한쪽으로 데려가며 말했어. \"월요일까지는 준비가 못 될 것 같습니다.\""]], [["‘Pooh, pooh,’ replied Mr.", "\"쯧쯧, 무슨 소리.\""], ["Crummles.", "크럼블스 씨가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘But really I can’t,’ returned Nicholas; ‘my invention is not accustomed to these demands, or possibly I might produce--’", "\"하지만 정말 못 합니다.\" 니콜라스가 받았어. \"제 창작력이 이런 요구에 익숙지 않아서요. 아니면 어쩌면 만들어 낼 수도 —\""]], [["‘Invention!", "\"창작력!"], ["what the devil’s that got to do with it!’ cried the manager hastily.", "그게 도대체 이거랑 무슨 상관이오!\" 단장이 다급히 외쳤어."]], [["‘Everything, my dear sir.’", "\"전부 다 상관있지요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Nothing, my dear sir,’ retorted the manager, with evident impatience.", "\"아무 상관도 없소, 선생.\" 단장이 대놓고 못 참겠다는 듯 받아쳤어."], ["‘Do you understand French?’", "\"프랑스어 할 줄 아시오?\""]], [["‘Perfectly well.’", "\"아주 잘합니다.\""]], [["‘Very good,’ said the manager, opening the table drawer, and giving a roll of paper from it to Nicholas.", "\"아주 좋아.\" 단장이 탁자 서랍을 열어 그 안에서 둘둘 만 종이 한 뭉치를 꺼내 니콜라스에게 주며 말했어."], ["‘There!", "\"자!"], ["Just turn that into English, and put your name on the title-page.", "이걸 영어로 옮기고, 표지에 당신 이름만 적어 넣으면 되오. 빌어먹을.\""], ["Damn me,’ said Mr.", "크럼블스 씨가 화난 듯 말했어. \"내가 노상 말해 왔잖소."], ["Crummles, angrily, ‘if I haven’t often said that I wouldn’t have a man or woman in my company that wasn’t master of the language, so that they might learn it from the original, and play it in English, and save all this trouble and expense.’", "우리 극단엔 그 언어를 통달한 남자나 여자가 아니면 두지 않겠다고. 그래야 원본에서 직접 배워서, 영어로 공연하고, 이 온갖 수고와 비용을 다 덜 수 있을 거 아니오.\""]], [["Nicholas smiled and pocketed the play.", "니콜라스는 웃으며 그 희곡을 주머니에 넣었어."]], [["‘What are you going to do about your lodgings?’ said Mr.", "\"숙소는 어떻게 할 거요?\""], ["Crummles.", "크럼블스 씨가 물었어."]], [["Nicholas could not help thinking that, for the first week, it would be an uncommon convenience to have a turn-up bedstead in the pit, but he merely remarked that he had not turned his thoughts that way.", "니콜라스는 한 주쯤은 일층석에 접이식 침대를 놓는 게 더없이 편하겠다는 생각을 떨칠 수 없었지만, 그저 거기까진 미처 생각해 보지 못했다고만 했어."]], [["‘Come home with me then,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 나랑 같이 갑시다.\""], ["Crummles, ‘and my boys shall go with you after dinner, and show you the most likely place.’", "크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"저녁 먹고 나서 우리 애들이 따라가서, 가장 괜찮을 만한 데를 알려 줄 테니.\""]], [["The offer was not to be refused; Nicholas and Mr.", "거절할 수 없는 제안이었어."], ["Crummles gave Mrs Crummles an arm each, and walked up the street in stately array.", "니콜라스와 크럼블스 씨가 크럼블스 부인에게 한쪽씩 팔을 내주고, 위풍당당한 행렬을 이루어 거리를 올라갔지."], ["Smike, the boys, and the phenomenon, went home by a shorter cut, and Mrs Grudden remained behind to take some cold Irish stew and a pint of porter in the box-office.", "스마이크와 아이들과 신동은 지름길로 집에 갔고, 그러든 부인은 매표소에 남아 차게 식은 아일랜드식 스튜와 포터 맥주 한 파인트를 들기로 했어."]], [["Mrs.", "크럼블스 부인은 마치 곧장 처형장으로 끌려가는 사람처럼 보도를 밟았어."], ["Crummles trod the pavement as if she were going to immediate execution with an animating consciousness of innocence, and that heroic fortitude which virtue alone inspires.", "결백하다는 가슴 벅찬 자각과, 오직 미덕만이 불어넣는 그 영웅적 의연함을 품고서 말이야. 반대로 크럼블스 씨는 잔인무도한 폭군의 표정과 걸음걸이를 취했지."], ["Mr. Crummles, on the other hand, assumed the look and gait of a hardened despot; but they both attracted some notice from many of the passers-by, and when they heard a whisper of ‘Mr.", "하지만 두 사람 다 지나가는 사람들 사이에서 적잖은 눈길을 끌었어."], ["and Mrs.", "그리고 \"크럼블스 부부다!\""], ["Crummles!’ or saw a little boy run back to stare them in the face, the severe expression of their countenances relaxed, for they felt it was popularity.", "하는 속삭임이 들리거나, 어린 사내애가 얼굴을 빤히 보려고 되돌아 달려오면, 그들의 근엄한 표정이 스르르 풀렸어. 그게 바로 인기라는 걸 느꼈거든."]], [["Mr.", "크럼블스 씨는 세인트 토머스 거리, 벌프라는 도선사의 집에 살았어."], ["Crummles lived in St Thomas’s Street, at the house of one Bulph, a pilot, who sported a boat-green door, with window-frames of the same colour, and had the little finger of a drowned man on his parlour mantelshelf, with other maritime and natural curiosities.", "벌프는 배처럼 초록색 문을 자랑스레 달아 두었는데, 창틀도 똑같은 색이었지. 또 응접실 벽난로 선반에는 물에 빠져 죽은 사람의 새끼손가락을 다른 바다 관련 물건이며 기이한 자연물들과 함께 올려 두었어."], ["He displayed also a brass knocker, a brass plate, and a brass bell-handle, all very bright and shining; and had a mast, with a vane on the top of it, in his back yard.", "게다가 놋쇠 문고리, 놋쇠 문패, 놋쇠 초인종 손잡이도 내걸었는데, 죄다 아주 반짝반짝 윤이 났지. 뒷마당에는 꼭대기에 풍향계를 단 돛대까지 세워 두었어."]], [["‘You are welcome,’ said Mrs.", "\"어서 오세요.\""], ["Crummles, turning round to Nicholas when they reached the bow-windowed front room on the first floor.", "크럼블스 부인이 이 층의 활처럼 둥근 창이 난 앞방에 다다르자, 니콜라스 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했어."]], [["Nicholas bowed his acknowledgments, and was unfeignedly glad to see the cloth laid.", "니콜라스는 고개 숙여 감사를 표했어. 그리고 식탁보가 깔려 있는 걸 보고 정말이지 반가웠지."]], [["‘We have but a shoulder of mutton with onion sauce,’ said Mrs.", "\"양 어깨살에 양파 소스밖에 없어요.\""], ["Crummles, in the same charnel-house voice; ‘but such as our dinner is, we beg you to partake of it.’", "크럼블스 부인이 그 납골당 같은 목소리로 말했어. \"하지만 변변찮은 저녁이나마 함께 드시기를 청합니다.\""]], [["‘You are very good,’ replied Nicholas, ‘I shall do it ample justice.’", "\"정말 친절하시군요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"남김없이 잘 먹겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Vincent,’ said Mrs.", "\"빈센트.\""], ["Crummles, ‘what is the hour?’", "크럼블스 부인이 말했어. \"지금 몇 시지요?\""]], [["‘Five minutes past dinner-time,’ said Mr.", "\"저녁 시간 오 분 지났소.\""], ["Crummles.", "크럼블스 씨가 말했어."]], [["Mrs. Crummles rang the bell.", "크럼블스 부인이 종을 울렸어."], ["‘Let the mutton and onion sauce appear.’", "\"양고기와 양파 소스를 등장시키세요.\""]], [["The slave who attended upon Mr. Bulph’s lodgers, disappeared, and after a short interval reappeared with the festive banquet.", "벌프 씨네 하숙인들 시중을 드는 하녀가 사라졌다가, 잠깐 사이에 그 잔칫상을 들고 다시 나타났어."], ["Nicholas and the infant phenomenon opposed each other at the pembroke-table, and Smike and the master Crummleses dined on the sofa bedstead.", "니콜라스와 어린 신동이 펨브로크 식탁을 사이에 두고 마주 앉았고, 스마이크와 크럼블스 도련님들은 소파 겸 침대에서 저녁을 먹었지."]], [["‘Are they very theatrical people here?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"여기 사람들은 연극을 무척 좋아하나요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘No,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니오.\""], ["Crummles, shaking his head, ‘far from it--far from it.’", "크럼블스 씨가 고개를 저으며 대답했어. \"택도 없지 — 어림도 없어.\""]], [["‘I pity them,’ observed Mrs.", "\"가엾기도 하지.\""], ["Crummles.", "크럼블스 부인이 한마디 했어."]], [["‘So do I,’ said Nicholas; ‘if they have no relish for theatrical entertainments, properly conducted.’", "\"저도 그래요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"제대로 된 연극 공연에 흥미가 없다니 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Then they have none, sir,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"흥미라곤 도무지 없다오, 선생.\" 크럼블스 씨가 받았어."], ["Crummles.", "\"작년에 신동의 후원 공연이 있었지요."], ["‘To the infant’s benefit, last year, on which occasion she repeated three of her most popular characters, and also appeared in the Fairy Porcupine, as originally performed by her, there was a house of no more than four pound twelve.’", "그날 우리 애가 자기 인기 배역을 셋이나 다시 선보였고, 또 자기가 처음 맡았던 그대로 '요정 호저' 역에도 나왔는데, 그날 극장 수입이 사 파운드 십이 실링밖에 안 됐다오.\""]], [["‘Is it possible?’ cried Nicholas.", "\"그게 가능한 일인가요?\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어."]], [["‘And two pound of that was trust, pa,’ said the phenomenon.", "\"그중 이 파운드는 외상이었고요, 아빠.\" 신동이 말했어."]], [["‘And two pound of that was trust,’ repeated Mr.", "\"그중 이 파운드는 외상이었지.\""], ["Crummles. ‘Mrs.", "크럼블스 씨가 따라 말했어."], ["Crummles herself has played to mere handfuls.’", "\"크럼블스 부인 본인도 손가락으로 셀 만큼 적은 관객 앞에서 연기한 적이 있다오.\""]], [["‘But they are always a taking audience, Vincent,’ said the manager’s wife.", "\"그래도 관객들이 늘 잘 받아 주잖아요, 빈센트.\" 단장의 아내가 말했어."]], [["‘Most audiences are, when they have good acting--real good acting--the regular thing,’ replied Mr.", "\"좋은 연기, 정말 좋은 연기, 제대로 된 진짜를 보여 주면 관객은 대개 그렇기 마련이지.\""], ["Crummles, forcibly.", "크럼블스 씨가 힘주어 대답했어."]], [["‘Do you give lessons, ma’am?’ inquired Nicholas.", "\"부인은 레슨도 하시나요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘I do,’ said Mrs.", "\"하지요.\""], ["Crummles.", "크럼블스 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘There is no teaching here, I suppose?’", "\"여기선 가르칠 사람이 없겠군요?\""]], [["‘There has been,’ said Mrs.", "\"있긴 했어요.\" 크럼블스 부인이 말했어."], ["Crummles. ‘I have received pupils here.", "\"여기서 제자를 받은 적이 있지요."], ["I imparted tuition to the daughter of a dealer in ships’ provision; but it afterwards appeared that she was insane when she first came to me.", "선박 식료품 상인의 딸을 가르쳤거든요. 그런데 알고 보니 그 아이가 저한테 처음 왔을 때 이미 정신이 온전치 않았더라고요."], ["It was very extraordinary that she should come, under such circumstances.’", "그런 상태로 찾아오다니 정말 별난 일이지요.\""]], [["Not feeling quite so sure of that, Nicholas thought it best to hold his peace.", "거기엔 그리 확신이 서지 않았던 니콜라스는, 잠자코 있는 게 상책이다 싶었어."]], [["‘Let me see,’ said the manager cogitating after dinner.", "\"가만있자.\" 저녁을 먹고 나서 단장이 골똘히 궁리하며 말했어."], ["‘Would you like some nice little part with the infant?’", "\"신동이랑 같이 하는 자그마하고 괜찮은 역을 해 보겠소?\""]], [["‘You are very good,’ replied Nicholas hastily; ‘but I think perhaps it would be better if I had somebody of my own size at first, in case I should turn out awkward. I should feel more at home, perhaps.’", "\"정말 친절하시네요.\" 니콜라스가 다급히 대답했어. \"하지만 처음엔 제 또래 사람이랑 하는 게 나을 것 같습니다. 혹시 제가 서툴게 굴까 봐서요. 그래야 좀 더 편할 듯해서요.\""]], [["‘True,’ said the manager.", "\"그도 그렇군.\" 단장이 말했어."], ["‘Perhaps you would.", "\"그럴 수도 있겠소."], ["And you could play up to the infant, in time, you know.’", "그러다 차차 신동을 받쳐 주면 되지, 안 그렇소.\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ replied Nicholas: devoutly hoping that it would be a very long time before he was honoured with this distinction.", "\"물론입니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. 속으로는 그 영광을 누리게 되는 날이 아주아주 멀기를 간절히 바라면서 말이야."]], [["‘Then I’ll tell you what we’ll do,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 이렇게 합시다.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어."], ["Crummles. ‘You shall study Romeo when you’ve done that piece--don’t forget to throw the pump and tubs in by-the-bye--Juliet Miss Snevellicci, old Grudden the nurse.--Yes, that’ll do very well.", "\"그 작품을 끝내고 나면 로미오를 연구하시오 — 그건 그렇고, 펌프랑 물통 던지는 장면도 잊지 말고 넣고 — 줄리엣은 스네벨리치 양, 늙은 그러든이 유모 역."], ["Rover too;--you might get up Rover while you were about it, and Cassio, and Jeremy Diddler.", "그래, 그게 아주 좋겠군. 로버 역도 — 하는 김에 로버 역도 익혀 두면 되겠고, 또 카시오, 제레미 디들러도."], ["You can easily knock them off; one part helps the other so much.", "금방 해치울 수 있다오. 역들이 서로 워낙 도움이 되니까."], ["Here they are, cues and all.’", "자, 여기 있소, 큐 사인까지 다 적혀 있는 걸로.\""]], [["With these hasty general directions Mr.", "크럼블스 씨는 이렇게 서둘러 두루뭉술한 지시를 내리며, 작은 책 여러 권을 니콜라스의 머뭇거리는 손에 떠안겼어."], ["Crummles thrust a number of little books into the faltering hands of Nicholas, and bidding his eldest son go with him and show where lodgings were to be had, shook him by the hand, and wished him good night.", "그러고는 맏아들더러 같이 가서 숙소 구할 데를 알려 주라 이르고는, 니콜라스의 손을 흔들며 잘 자라고 인사했지."]], [["There is no lack of comfortable furnished apartments in Portsmouth, and no difficulty in finding some that are proportionate to very slender finances; but the former were too good, and the latter too bad, and they went into so many houses, and came out unsuited, that Nicholas seriously began to think he should be obliged to ask permission to spend the night in the theatre, after all.", "포츠머스에는 가구 딸린 안락한 셋방이 모자라지 않았어. 아주 빠듯한 주머니 사정에 맞는 곳을 찾기도 어렵지 않았지. 그런데 앞쪽은 너무 좋았고, 뒤쪽은 너무 형편없었어. 두 사람은 숱한 집에 들어갔다가 마음에 안 들어 도로 나오기를 거듭했지. 그러다 니콜라스는 결국 극장에서 하룻밤 지내게 해 달라고 부탁해야 하는 거 아닌가 진지하게 생각하기 시작했어."]], [["Eventually, however, they stumbled upon two small rooms up three pair of stairs, or rather two pair and a ladder, at a tobacconist’s shop, on the Common Hard: a dirty street leading down to the dockyard. These Nicholas engaged, only too happy to have escaped any request for payment of a week’s rent beforehand.", "하지만 마침내 두 사람은 작은 방 두 칸을 우연히 찾아냈어. 세 층 계단을 올라간 곳, 아니 정확히는 두 층 계단에 사다리 하나를 더 올라간 곳이었지. 담배 가게 위층이었고, 조선소로 내려가는 더러운 거리, 코먼 하드에 있었어. 니콜라스는 이 방을 빌렸어. 한 주치 방세를 미리 내라는 소리를 듣지 않은 게 그저 더없이 다행스러웠지."]], [["‘There!", "\"자! 우리 짐을 내려놓자, 스마이크.\""], ["Lay down our personal property, Smike,’ he said, after showing young Crummles downstairs.", "어린 크럼블스를 아래층까지 바래다준 뒤 니콜라스가 말했어. \"참 묘한 시절에 들어섰구나."], ["‘We have fallen upon strange times, and Heaven only knows the end of them; but I am tired with the events of these three days, and will postpone reflection till tomorrow--if I can.’", "끝이 어떻게 될지는 하느님만 아실 테고. 하지만 이 사흘 동안 벌어진 일들로 지쳤으니, 생각은 내일로 미뤄야겠다 — 미룰 수 있다면 말이지.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 24", "# CHAPTER 24"]], [["Of the Great Bespeak for Miss Snevellicci, and the first Appearance of Nicholas upon any Stage\nNicholas was up betimes in the morning; but he had scarcely begun to dress, notwithstanding, when he heard footsteps ascending the stairs, and was presently saluted by the voices of Mr.", "니콜라스는 아침 일찍 일어났어. 그런데 옷을 채 입기도 전에 계단을 올라오는 발소리가 들렸지."], ["Folair the pantomimist, and Mr. Lenville, the tragedian.", "곧이어 무언극 배우 폴레어 씨와 비극 배우 렌빌 씨의 목소리가 들려왔어."]], [["‘House, house, house!’ cried Mr.", "\"집주인! 집주인!"], ["Folair.", "집주인 어디 있소!\" 폴레어 씨가 외쳤어."]], [["‘What, ho!", "\"여어, 이봐!"], ["within there,’ said Mr.", "안에 있나!\""], ["Lenville, in a deep voice.", "렌빌 씨가 굵은 목소리로 말했어."]], [["‘Confound these fellows!’ thought Nicholas; ‘they have come to breakfast, I suppose. I’ll open the door directly, if you’ll wait an instant.’", "'이 사람들 정말!' 니콜라스는 속으로 생각했어. '아침을 같이 먹으러 온 모양이군. 잠깐만 기다려요. 곧 문을 열어 줄 테니.'"]], [["The gentlemen entreated him not to hurry himself; and, to beguile the interval, had a fencing bout with their walking-sticks on the very small landing-place: to the unspeakable discomposure of all the other lodgers downstairs.", "두 사람은 서두르지 말라고 했어. 그러고는 기다리는 동안 심심풀이로, 그 좁디좁은 층계참에서 지팡이를 휘두르며 펜싱 시합을 벌였지. 아래층에 사는 다른 하숙인들이 이루 말할 수 없이 불편해할 정도였어."]], [["‘Here, come in,’ said Nicholas, when he had completed his toilet.", "\"자, 들어와요.\" 단장을 마친 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘In the name of all that’s horrible, don’t make that noise outside.’", "\"제발, 끔찍한 그 소동 좀 밖에서 벌이지 말고요.\""]], [["‘An uncommon snug little box this,’ said Mr.", "\"여기 참 아늑한 작은 방이로군.\""], ["Lenville, stepping into the front room, and taking his hat off, before he could get in at all.", "렌빌 씨가 앞방으로 들어서며 말했어. 그런데 다 들어서기도 전에 모자부터 벗어야 했지."], ["‘Pernicious snug.’", "\"기막히게 아늑해.\""]], [["‘For a man at all particular in such matters, it might be a trifle too snug,’ said Nicholas; ‘for, although it is, undoubtedly, a great convenience to be able to reach anything you want from the ceiling or the floor, or either side of the room, without having to move from your chair, still these advantages can only be had in an apartment of the most limited size.’", "\"그런 걸 따지는 사람한테는 좀 지나치게 아늑할지도 모르죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"물론, 의자에서 꼼짝 안 하고도 천장이든 바닥이든 방 어느 쪽이든 손만 뻗으면 다 닿으니 엄청 편하긴 하죠. 하지만 그런 편리함은 더없이 비좁은 방에서나 누릴 수 있는 거잖아요.\""]], [["‘It isn’t a bit too confined for a single man,’ returned Mr.", "\"혼자 사는 남자한테는 조금도 답답하지 않아.\" 렌빌 씨가 받았어."], ["Lenville. ‘That reminds me,--my wife, Mr.", "\"그러고 보니 생각났는데, 존슨 씨, 내 아내 말이오."], ["Johnson,--I hope she’ll have some good part in this piece of yours?’", "이번 자네 작품에서 좋은 역 하나 맡았으면 좋겠는데.\""]], [["‘I glanced at the French copy last night,’ said Nicholas.", "\"어젯밤에 프랑스어 원본을 훑어봤어요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘It looks very good, I think.’", "\"꽤 좋아 보이던데요.\""]], [["‘What do you mean to do for me, old fellow?’ asked Mr.", "\"나한테는 뭘 해 줄 셈인가, 친구?\""], ["Lenville, poking the struggling fire with his walking-stick, and afterwards wiping it on the skirt of his coat.", "렌빌 씨가 지팡이로 시들시들 타는 불을 쑤시더니, 그 끝을 코트 자락에 닦으며 물었어."], ["‘Anything in the gruff and grumble way?’", "\"거칠게 으르렁대는 그런 역할이라도 있나?\""]], [["‘You turn your wife and child out of doors,’ said Nicholas; ‘and, in a fit of rage and jealousy, stab your eldest son in the library.’", "\"아내와 아이를 집에서 내쫓는 거예요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그리고 분노와 질투에 휩싸여, 서재에서 큰아들을 칼로 찌르고요.\""]], [["‘Do I though!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"내가 그런다고!\" 렌빌 씨가 소리쳤어."], ["Lenville. ‘That’s very good business.’", "\"그거 아주 괜찮은 역이군.\""]], [["‘After which,’ said Nicholas, ‘you are troubled with remorse till the last act, and then you make up your mind to destroy yourself. But, just as you are raising the pistol to your head, a clock strikes--ten.’", "\"그 뒤에,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"마지막 막까지 죄책감에 시달리다가, 결국 스스로 목숨을 끊기로 마음먹어요. 그런데 막 권총을 머리에 갖다 대는 순간, 시계가 울려요. 열 시를 알리는 종소리가요.\""]], [["‘I see,’ cried Mr.", "\"알겠어.\""], ["Lenville.", "렌빌 씨가 외쳤어."], ["‘Very good.’", "\"아주 좋아.\""]], [["‘You pause,’ said Nicholas; ‘you recollect to have heard a clock strike ten in your infancy. The pistol falls from your hand--you are overcome--you burst into tears, and become a virtuous and exemplary character for ever afterwards.’", "\"멈칫하는 거예요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"어릴 적에 들었던 열 시 종소리가 떠오르는 거죠. 권총이 손에서 떨어지고, 당신은 무너져 내리며 울음을 터뜨려요. 그러고는 그 뒤로 영원히 착하고 모범적인 사람이 되는 거죠.\""]], [["‘Capital!’ said Mr.", "\"최고로군!\""], ["Lenville: ‘that’s a sure card, a sure card.", "렌빌 씨가 말했어. \"그건 확실한 패야, 확실한 패."], ["Get the curtain down with a touch of nature like that, and it’ll be a triumphant success.’", "그런 자연스러운 감동으로 막을 내리면, 대성공이 따 놓은 당상이지.\""]], [["‘Is there anything good for me?’ inquired Mr.", "\"나한테 좋은 역은 없나?\""], ["Folair, anxiously.", "폴레어 씨가 걱정스레 물었어."]], [["‘Let me see,’ said Nicholas.", "\"어디 보자.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘You play the faithful and attached servant; you are turned out of doors with the wife and child.’", "\"당신은 충직하고 헌신적인 하인을 맡아요. 아내와 아이와 함께 집에서 쫓겨나죠.\""]], [["‘Always coupled with that infernal phenomenon,’ sighed Mr.", "\"늘 그 지긋지긋한 천재 신동이랑 짝지어진다니까.\" 폴레어 씨가 한숨을 쉬었어."], ["Folair; ‘and we go into poor lodgings, where I won’t take any wages, and talk sentiment, I suppose?’", "\"그래서 우리는 허름한 하숙집으로 가고, 거기서 나는 품삯도 안 받겠다며 감상적인 소리나 늘어놓겠지?\""]], [["‘Why--yes,’ replied Nicholas: ‘that is the course of the piece.’", "\"뭐, 그렇죠.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그게 작품 줄거리니까요.\""]], [["‘I must have a dance of some kind, you know,’ said Mr.", "\"알다시피 나는 무슨 춤이든 한 번은 춰야 해.\""], ["Folair.", "폴레어 씨가 말했어."], ["‘You’ll have to introduce one for the phenomenon, so you’d better make a PAS DE DEUX, and save time.’", "\"신동을 위해서도 춤을 하나 넣어야 할 테니, 차라리 둘이 추는 춤으로 만드는 게 좋을 거야. 그래야 시간도 아끼고.\""]], [["‘There’s nothing easier than that,’ said Mr.", "\"그것보다 쉬운 게 어디 있나.\""], ["Lenville, observing the disturbed looks of the young dramatist.", "렌빌 씨가 젊은 극작가의 난처한 표정을 보고 말했어."]], [["‘Upon my word I don’t see how it’s to be done,’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"솔직히 그걸 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠는데요.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Why, isn’t it obvious?’ reasoned Mr.", "\"아니, 뻔하지 않나?\""], ["Lenville.", "렌빌 씨가 따지듯 말했어."], ["‘Gadzooks, who can help seeing the way to do it?--you astonish me!", "\"이런, 그 방법을 모를 사람이 어디 있겠어? 자네 정말 놀라운 사람이군!"], ["You get the distressed lady, and the little child, and the attached servant, into the poor lodgings, don’t you?--Well, look here.", "곤경에 빠진 부인이랑 어린아이랑 헌신적인 하인을 그 허름한 하숙집에 들여보내잖아? 그렇지?"], ["The distressed lady sinks into a chair, and buries her face in her pocket-handkerchief.", "자, 봐. 곤경에 빠진 부인이 의자에 푹 주저앉아서 손수건에 얼굴을 파묻어."], ["“What makes you weep, mama?”", "'엄마, 왜 우세요?' 아이가 말하지."], ["says the child.", "'엄마, 울지 마세요."], ["“Don’t weep, mama, or you’ll make me weep too!”--“And me!”", "안 그러면 저도 울어요!' '저도요!'"], ["says the favourite servant, rubbing his eyes with his arm.", "충직한 하인이 팔로 눈을 비비며 말해. '사랑하는 엄마, 어떻게 하면 기운을 차리시겠어요?'"], ["“What can we do to raise your spirits, dear mama?”", "어린아이가 말하지. '그래, 우리가 뭘 할 수 있을까?'"], ["says the little child.", "충직한 하인이 말해. '오, 피에르!'"], ["“Ay, what CAN we do?”", "곤경에 빠진 부인이 말하지. '이 괴로운 생각들을 떨쳐 버릴 수만 있다면.'"], ["says the faithful servant.", "'해 보세요, 부인. 해 보세요.'"], ["“Oh, Pierre!”", "충직한 하인이 말해."], ["says the distressed lady; “would that I could shake off these painful thoughts.”--“Try, ma’am, try,”", "'기운을 내세요, 부인. 즐거운 일을 해 보세요.'"], ["says the faithful servant; “rouse yourself, ma’am; be amused.”--“I will,”", "'그래야지.' 부인이 말해."], ["says the lady, “I will learn to suffer with fortitude.", "'꿋꿋하게 견디는 법을 배워야지. 충직한 친구여, 그 춤을 기억하나?"], ["Do you remember that dance, my honest friend, which, in happier days, you practised with this sweet angel?", "행복했던 시절에 자네가 이 사랑스러운 천사와 함께 추던 그 춤 말일세. 그땐 그 춤을 보면 늘 마음이 가라앉았지."], ["It never failed to calm my spirits then.", "오! 죽기 전에 한 번만 더 보고 싶구나!'"], ["Oh!", "자, 바로 그거야."], ["let me see it once again before I die!”--There it is--cue for the band, BEFORE I DIE,--and off they go.", "악단한테 신호를 주는 거지, '죽기 전에'에서 말이야. 그럼 둘이 춤을 추는 거야."], ["That’s the regular thing; isn’t it, Tommy?’", "그게 정석이지. 안 그래, 토미?\""]], [["‘That’s it,’ replied Mr.", "\"바로 그거야.\" 폴레어 씨가 대답했어."], ["Folair. ‘The distressed lady, overpowered by old recollections, faints at the end of the dance, and you close in with a picture.’", "\"곤경에 빠진 부인이 옛 추억에 북받쳐서 춤이 끝날 때 기절하고, 자네는 한 폭의 그림 같은 장면으로 마무리하는 거지.\""]], [["Profiting by these and other lessons, which were the result of the personal experience of the two actors, Nicholas willingly gave them the best breakfast he could, and, when he at length got rid of them, applied himself to his task: by no means displeased to find that it was so much easier than he had at first supposed. He worked very hard all day, and did not leave his room until the evening, when he went down to the theatre, whither Smike had repaired before him to go on with another gentleman as a general rebellion.", "이런저런 가르침을 얻었는데, 다 두 배우가 몸소 겪어서 터득한 것들이었지. 니콜라스는 기꺼이 자기가 차릴 수 있는 가장 좋은 아침을 대접했어. 그리고 마침내 두 사람을 보내고 나서야 자기 일에 매달렸지. 그 일이 처음 생각했던 것보다 훨씬 쉽다는 걸 알고는 영 싫지 않았어. 그날 하루 종일 열심히 일했고, 저녁이 되어서야 방을 나서 극장으로 내려갔어. 스마이크는 다른 배우와 함께 반란군 무리 역을 맡아, 니콜라스보다 먼저 극장에 가 있었지."]], [["Here all the people were so much changed, that he scarcely knew them.", "극장에 와 보니 사람들이 어찌나 달라졌는지, 니콜라스는 누가 누군지 거의 알아보지 못했어. 가짜 머리, 가짜 분칠, 가짜 종아리, 가짜 근육까지, 다들 완전히 딴사람이 되어 있었거든."], ["False hair, false colour, false calves, false muscles--they had become different beings.", "렌빌 씨는 더없이 균형 잡힌 몸매를 뽐내는 혈기왕성한 전사가 되어 있었어. 크럼블스 씨는 그 큼지막한 얼굴을 새까만 머리카락으로 잔뜩 가리고, 더없이 위풍당당한 하일랜드 무법자로 변해 있었지."], ["Mr.", "늙은 신사 한 명은 간수가, 다른 한 명은 위엄 있는 노족장이 되었어."], ["Lenville was a blooming warrior of most exquisite proportions; Mr.", "익살스러운 시골뜨기는 용맹무쌍한 데다 유머까지 한 줌 곁들인 싸움꾼이 되었고, 어린 크럼블스 형제는 저마다 어엿한 왕자가 되었어."], ["Crummles, his large face shaded by a profusion of black hair, a Highland outlaw of most majestic bearing; one of the old gentlemen a jailer, and the other a venerable patriarch; the comic countryman, a fighting-man of great valour, relieved by a touch of humour; each of the Master Crummleses a prince in his own right; and the low-spirited lover, a desponding captive.", "그리고 풀죽은 연인 역의 배우는 절망에 빠진 포로가 되었지. 3막을 위해 화려한 연회상도 차려져 있었어."], ["There was a gorgeous banquet ready spread for the third act, consisting of two pasteboard vases, one plate of biscuits, a black bottle, and a vinegar cruet; and, in short, everything was on a scale of the utmost splendour and preparation.", "마분지 꽃병 두 개, 비스킷 한 접시, 검은 병 하나, 식초 병 하나로 이루어진 상이었지. 요컨대, 모든 것이 더없이 호화롭고 빈틈없이 준비되어 있었어."]], [["Nicholas was standing with his back to the curtain, now contemplating the first scene, which was a Gothic archway, about two feet shorter than Mr. Crummles, through which that gentleman was to make his first entrance, and now listening to a couple of people who were cracking nuts in the gallery, wondering whether they made the whole audience, when the manager himself walked familiarly up and accosted him.", "니콜라스는 막을 등진 채 서 있었어. 한편으로는 첫 장면을 바라보고 있었지. 고딕풍 아치문이었는데, 크럼블스 씨보다 한 육십 센티미터쯤 낮았어. 크럼블스 씨가 그 문으로 처음 등장할 참이었지. 또 한편으로는 위층 관람석에서 견과를 까먹고 있는 두 사람의 소리에 귀를 기울이며, 저 둘이 관객의 전부인가 하고 궁금해하고 있었어. 그때 단장이 직접 친근하게 다가와 말을 걸었지."]], [["‘Been in front tonight?’ said Mr.", "\"오늘 밤 객석은 둘러봤나?\""], ["Crummles.", "크럼블스 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘No,’ replied Nicholas, ‘not yet.", "\"아니요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["I am going to see the play.’", "\"아직요. 이제 연극을 보러 가려고요.\""]], [["‘We’ve had a pretty good Let,’ said Mr.", "\"표가 꽤 잘 팔렸어.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어."], ["Crummles. ‘Four front places in the centre, and the whole of the stage-box.’", "\"가운데 앞자리 네 개에다, 무대 옆 특별석을 통째로 팔았지.\""]], [["‘Oh, indeed!’ said Nicholas; ‘a family, I suppose?’", "\"오, 정말요!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"어느 가족인가 보죠?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Mr.", "\"그래.\" 크럼블스 씨가 대답했어."], ["Crummles, ‘yes.", "\"그래."], ["It’s an affecting thing.", "그게 참 가슴 뭉클한 일이야."], ["There are six children, and they never come unless the phenomenon plays.’", "그 집 아이가 여섯인데, 신동이 출연하지 않으면 절대 안 온단 말이지.\""]], [["It would have been difficult for any party, family, or otherwise, to have visited the theatre on a night when the phenomenon did NOT play, inasmuch as she always sustained one, and not uncommonly two or three, characters, every night; but Nicholas, sympathising with the feelings of a father, refrained from hinting at this trifling circumstance, and Mr Crummles continued to talk, uninterrupted by him.", "사실 어떤 일행이든, 가족이든 누구든, 신동이 출연하지 않는 밤에 극장을 찾기란 어려운 일이었어. 그 아이는 매일 밤 한 역을, 흔하게는 두세 역을 맡았으니까. 하지만 니콜라스는 아버지의 마음에 공감해서, 이 사소한 사정은 입에 올리지 않았어. 그래서 크럼블스 씨는 방해받지 않고 계속 떠들 수 있었지."]], [["‘Six,’ said that gentleman; ‘pa and ma eight, aunt nine, governess ten, grandfather and grandmother twelve. Then, there’s the footman, who stands outside, with a bag of oranges and a jug of toast-and-water, and sees the play for nothing through the little pane of glass in the box-door--it’s cheap at a guinea; they gain by taking a box.’", "\"여섯이지.\" 그가 말했어. \"아빠 엄마까지 여덟, 이모까지 아홉, 가정교사까지 열, 할아버지 할머니까지 열둘이야. 거기다 하인도 하나 있는데, 그놈은 밖에 서서 오렌지 한 자루랑 구운 빵 띄운 물 한 주전자를 들고, 특별석 문에 난 작은 유리창으로 공짜로 연극을 본단 말이지. 일 기니로 그 정도면 싼 거야. 특별석을 잡으니 그만큼 이득이거든.\""]], [["‘I wonder you allow so many,’ observed Nicholas.", "\"그렇게 많이 들여보내시는 게 의외네요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘There’s no help for it,’ replied Mr.", "\"어쩔 도리가 없어.\" 크럼블스 씨가 대답했어."], ["Crummles; ‘it’s always expected in the country.", "\"시골에서는 늘 그러려니 하거든."], ["If there are six children, six people come to hold them in their laps.", "아이가 여섯이면, 무릎에 앉히려고 어른 여섯이 따라오는 거지."], ["A family-box carries double always.", "가족 특별석은 늘 두 배로 잡힌단 말이야."], ["Ring in the orchestra, Grudden!’", "그루든, 악단 들여보내!\""]], [["That useful lady did as she was requested, and shortly afterwards the tuning of three fiddles was heard. Which process having been protracted as long as it was supposed that the patience of the audience could possibly bear it, was put a stop to by another jerk of the bell, which, being the signal to begin in earnest, set the orchestra playing a variety of popular airs, with involuntary variations.", "그 쓸모 있는 여자가 시키는 대로 했어. 그러자 곧 바이올린 세 대를 조율하는 소리가 들려왔지. 관객의 인내심이 도저히 더는 못 견디겠다 싶을 때까지 이 작업을 질질 끌다가, 종을 한 번 더 흔들어 멈췄어. 그게 본격적으로 시작하라는 신호였지. 그러자 악단이 인기 있는 곡들을 이것저것 연주하기 시작했어. 본의 아닌 변주를 곁들여서 말이야."]], [["If Nicholas had been astonished at the alteration for the better which the gentlemen displayed, the transformation of the ladies was still more extraordinary. When, from a snug corner of the manager’s box, he beheld Miss Snevellicci in all the glories of white muslin with a golden hem, and Mrs. Crummles in all the dignity of the outlaw’s wife, and Miss Bravassa in all the sweetness of Miss Snevellicci’s confidential friend, and Miss Belvawney in the white silks of a page doing duty everywhere and swearing to live and die in the service of everybody, he could scarcely contain his admiration, which testified itself in great applause, and the closest possible attention to the business of the scene. The plot was most interesting. It belonged to no particular age, people, or country, and was perhaps the more delightful on that account, as nobody’s previous information could afford the remotest glimmering of what would ever come of it. An outlaw had been very successful in doing something somewhere, and came home, in triumph, to the sound of shouts and fiddles, to greet his wife--a lady of masculine mind, who talked a good deal about her father’s bones, which it seemed were unburied, though whether from a peculiar taste on the part of the old gentleman himself, or the reprehensible neglect of his relations, did not appear. This outlaw’s wife was, somehow or other, mixed up with a patriarch, living in a castle a long way off, and this patriarch was the father of several of the characters, but he didn’t exactly know which, and was uncertain whether he had brought up the right ones in his castle, or the wrong ones; he rather inclined to the latter opinion, and, being uneasy, relieved his mind with a banquet, during which solemnity somebody in a cloak said ‘Beware!’ which somebody was known by nobody (except the audience) to be the outlaw himself, who had come there, for reasons unexplained, but possibly with an eye to the spoons. There was an agreeable little surprise in the way of certain love passages between the desponding captive and Miss Snevellicci, and the comic fighting-man and Miss Bravassa; besides which, Mr. Lenville had several very tragic scenes in the dark, while on throat-cutting expeditions, which were all baffled by the skill and bravery of the comic fighting-man (who overheard whatever was said all through the piece) and the intrepidity of Miss Snevellicci, who adopted tights, and therein repaired to the prison of her captive lover, with a small basket of refreshments and a dark lantern. At last, it came out that the patriarch was the man who had treated the bones of the outlaw’s father-in-law with so much disrespect, for which cause and reason the outlaw’s wife repaired to his castle to kill him, and so got into a dark room, where, after a good deal of groping in the dark, everybody got hold of everybody else, and took them for somebody besides, which occasioned a vast quantity of confusion, with some pistolling, loss of life, and torchlight; after which, the patriarch came forward, and observing, with a knowing look, that he knew all about his children now, and would tell them when they got inside, said that there could not be a more appropriate occasion for marrying the young people than that; and therefore he joined their hands, with the full consent of the indefatigable page, who (being the only other person surviving) pointed with his cap into the clouds, and his right hand to the ground; thereby invoking a blessing and giving the cue for the curtain to come down, which it did, amidst general applause.", "남자 배우들이 보여 준 멋진 변신에 니콜라스가 놀랐다면, 여자 배우들의 변신은 더더욱 놀라웠어. 단장의 특별석 한쪽 아늑한 구석에서, 니콜라스는 금실로 단을 두른 새하얀 모슬린을 차려입고 한껏 빛나는 스네벨리치 양을 바라봤어. 또 무법자 아내의 위엄을 한껏 갖춘 크럼블스 부인과, 스네벨리치 양의 절친한 친구로서 더없는 상냥함을 풍기는 브라바사 양도 봤지. 그리고 시동 역의 새하얀 비단옷을 입고 사방을 누비며, 모두를 위해 살고 죽겠다고 맹세하는 벨바우니 양도 봤어. 니콜라스는 감탄을 참지 못해 큰 박수를 보냈고, 장면 하나하나에 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울였어. 줄거리는 정말 흥미진진했지. 어느 시대, 어느 민족, 어느 나라의 이야기도 아니었는데, 그래서 오히려 더 즐거웠는지도 몰라. 아무도 미리 알던 정보로는 이 이야기가 어떻게 흘러갈지 손톱만큼도 짐작할 수 없었으니까. 어떤 무법자가 어딘가에서 무언가를 멋지게 해냈어. 그러고는 함성과 바이올린 소리에 맞춰 의기양양하게 집으로 돌아와 아내를 맞이하지. 그 아내는 사내대장부 같은 성격이었는데, 자기 아버지의 뼈 이야기를 줄곧 늘어놓았어. 그 뼈가 아직 묻히지 못한 모양이었지. 그게 노신사 본인의 별난 취향 때문인지, 아니면 친척들의 괘씸한 무관심 탓인지는 분명치 않았어. 이 무법자의 아내는 어찌어찌해서, 저 멀리 성에 사는 한 족장과 얽히게 돼. 그 족장은 등장인물 여럿의 아버지였는데, 정작 누가 자기 자식인지는 정확히 몰랐어. 자기 성에서 키운 아이들이 진짜 자식인지 가짜 자식인지조차 확신하지 못했지. 그는 오히려 가짜 자식이라는 쪽으로 마음이 기울어, 불안한 나머지 연회를 열어 마음을 달랬어. 그 엄숙한 자리에서 망토를 두른 누군가가 \"조심하라!\" 하고 외쳤지. 그게 누군지는 아무도 몰랐어. 관객만 빼고 말이야. 사실 그건 바로 그 무법자였어. 까닭은 밝혀지지 않았지만 거기 와 있었지. 어쩌면 은수저를 노리고 왔는지도 몰라. 그리고 절망에 빠진 포로와 스네벨리치 양 사이, 또 익살스러운 싸움꾼과 브라바사 양 사이에 사랑 이야기가 펼쳐지는 즐겁고 작은 깜짝 장면도 있었어. 그뿐만 아니라 렌빌 씨는 어둠 속에서 목을 베러 다니는 아주 비극적인 장면을 여러 번 연기했어. 그런데 그 시도는 익살스러운 싸움꾼의 솜씨와 용기에 번번이 좌절됐지. 이 싸움꾼은 극 내내 무슨 말이든 다 엿들었거든. 거기에 스네벨리치 양의 대담함도 한몫했어. 그녀는 몸에 딱 붙는 옷을 입고, 다과 바구니 하나와 어두운 등불 하나를 들고 포로가 된 연인의 감옥을 찾아갔지. 마침내 밝혀진 사실은, 그 족장이 바로 무법자 장인의 뼈를 그토록 함부로 다룬 장본인이라는 거였어. 그 까닭에 무법자의 아내는 그를 죽이려고 그의 성을 찾아갔어. 그러다 어두운 방에 들어가게 됐지. 거기서 한참을 어둠 속에서 더듬거리다가, 모두가 서로를 붙잡고는 엉뚱한 다른 사람으로 착각하는 거야. 그 바람에 엄청난 혼란이 벌어졌어. 권총질도 좀 있었고, 목숨을 잃기도 했고, 횃불도 등장했지. 그 뒤에 족장이 앞으로 나와, 알겠다는 표정으로 이제 자기 자식들에 관해 다 알았다고, 안에 들어가면 다 말해 주겠다고 말했어. 그러고는 이만한 자리에서 젊은이들을 결혼시키는 것보다 더 알맞은 때가 없다고 했지. 그래서 그는 두 사람의 손을 맞잡아 주었어. 지칠 줄 모르는 시동도 흔쾌히 동의했지. 그 시동은 살아남은 유일한 다른 사람이었는데, 모자로 하늘을 가리키고 오른손으로 땅을 가리켰어. 그렇게 축복을 빌며 막을 내리라는 신호를 줬지. 그러자 막이 내렸어. 우레와 같은 박수 속에서 말이야."]], [["‘What did you think of that?’ asked Mr.", "\"어땠나?\" 니콜라스가 다시 무대 뒤로 돌아가자 크럼블스 씨가 물었어."], ["Crummles, when Nicholas went round to the stage again.", "크럼블스 씨는 얼굴이 시뻘겋게 달아오르고 땀범벅이었어."], ["Mr. Crummles was very red and hot, for your outlaws are desperate fellows to shout.", "무법자 역이란 게 워낙 목청껏 소리를 질러 대야 하는 거였거든."]], [["‘I think it was very capital indeed,’ replied Nicholas; ‘Miss Snevellicci in particular was uncommonly good.’", "\"정말 훌륭했어요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"특히 스네벨리치 양이 유난히 좋았어요.\""]], [["‘She’s a genius,’ said Mr.", "\"그 애는 천재야.\""], ["Crummles; ‘quite a genius, that girl.", "크럼블스 씨가 말했어. \"정말 천재라니까, 저 아이는."], ["By-the-bye, I’ve been thinking of bringing out that piece of yours on her bespeak night.’", "그건 그렇고, 자네 그 작품을 그 애의 자선 공연 밤에 올릴까 생각 중이었어.\""]], [["‘When?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"언제요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘The night of her bespeak.", "\"그 애가 예약 공연을 하는 밤 말이야."], ["Her benefit night, when her friends and patrons bespeak the play,’ said Mr.", "자선 공연 밤인데, 그 애 친구들이랑 후원자들이 연극을 예약하는 거지.\""], ["Crummles.", "크럼블스 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["I understand,’ replied Nicholas.", "알겠어요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘You see,’ said Mr.", "\"있잖아,\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어."], ["Crummles, ‘it’s sure to go, on such an occasion, and even if it should not work up quite as well as we expect, why it will be her risk, you know, and not ours.’", "\"그런 자리에서는 분명 성공할 거야. 설령 우리 기대만큼 잘 안 풀린다 해도, 그건 그 애가 떠안을 위험이지 우리 위험이 아니거든.\""]], [["‘Yours, you mean,’ said Nicholas.", "\"단장님 위험이겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘I said mine, didn’t I?’ returned Mr.", "\"내 위험이라고 했잖나, 안 그래?\""], ["Crummles. ‘Next Monday week.", "크럼블스 씨가 대꾸했어."], ["What do you say?", "\"다음다음 주 월요일이야. 어떤가?"], ["You’ll have done it, and are sure to be up in the lover’s part, long before that time.’", "그때까지 작품도 다 끝내고, 연인 역도 틀림없이 다 외울 수 있을 거야. 그것도 한참 전에 말이지.\""]], [["‘I don’t know about “long before,”’ replied Nicholas; ‘but BY that time I think I can undertake to be ready.’", "\"한참 전이라곤 못 하겠는데요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"하지만 그때까지는 준비를 마치겠다고 약속할 수 있을 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘Very good,’ pursued Mr.", "\"아주 좋아.\""], ["Crummles, ‘then we’ll call that settled.", "크럼블스 씨가 말을 이었어."], ["Now, I want to ask you something else.", "\"그럼 그렇게 정한 걸로 하지."], ["There’s a little--what shall I call it?--a little canvassing takes place on these occasions.’", "자, 한 가지 더 부탁할 게 있는데. 이런 자리에는 조그만, 뭐랄까, 조그만 표 모으는 일이 따라온단 말이지.\""]], [["‘Among the patrons, I suppose?’ said Nicholas.", "\"후원자들 사이에서요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Among the patrons; and the fact is, that Snevellicci has had so many bespeaks in this place, that she wants an attraction.", "\"후원자들 사이에서지. 사실은 말이야, 스네벨리치가 이 동네에서 예약 공연을 워낙 많이 해서, 이제 끌어들일 거리가 필요해."], ["She had a bespeak when her mother-in-law died, and a bespeak when her uncle died; and Mrs.", "시어머니가 죽었을 때도 예약 공연을 했고, 삼촌이 죽었을 때도 했지."], ["Crummles and myself have had bespeaks on the anniversary of the phenomenon’s birthday, and our wedding-day, and occasions of that description, so that, in fact, there’s some difficulty in getting a good one.", "게다가 나랑 크럼블스 부인도 신동의 생일 기념일이며 우리 결혼기념일이며, 그런 갖가지 날에 예약 공연을 했단 말이야. 그러니 사실 이제 괜찮은 명분 하나 잡기가 영 어렵단 말이지."], ["Now, won’t you help this poor girl, Mr.", "자, 이 가엾은 아가씨를 좀 도와주지 않겠나, 존슨 씨?\""], ["Johnson?’ said Crummles, sitting himself down on a drum, and taking a great pinch of snuff, as he looked him steadily in the face.", "크럼블스 씨가 북 위에 앉아 코담배를 한 줌 크게 집으며 말했어. 그러고는 니콜라스의 얼굴을 가만히 들여다봤어."]], [["‘How do you mean?’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"무슨 말씀이세요?\" 니콜라스가 되물었어."]], [["‘Don’t you think you could spare half an hour tomorrow morning, to call with her at the houses of one or two of the principal people?’ murmured the manager in a persuasive tone.", "\"내일 아침에 반 시간만 내서, 그 애랑 같이 주요 인사 한두 명의 집에 들러 줄 수 없겠나?\" 단장이 설득하는 어조로 나직이 말했어."]], [["‘Oh dear me,’ said Nicholas, with an air of very strong objection, ‘I shouldn’t like to do that.’", "\"이런, 어쩌죠.\" 니콜라스가 영 내키지 않는 기색으로 말했어. \"그건 정말 하고 싶지 않은데요.\""]], [["‘The infant will accompany her,’ said Mr.", "\"아이도 같이 갈 거야.\""], ["Crummles.", "크럼블스 씨가 말했어."], ["‘The moment it was suggested to me, I gave permission for the infant to go.", "\"그 얘기가 나오자마자, 내가 아이도 가도 좋다고 허락했지. 조금도 점잖지 못한 일은 없을 거야."], ["There will not be the smallest impropriety--Miss Snevellicci, sir, is the very soul of honour.", "스네벨리치 양은 그야말로 명예의 화신이거든. 그게 큰 도움이 될 거야."], ["It would be of material service--the gentleman from London--author of the new piece--actor in the new piece--first appearance on any boards--it would lead to a great bespeak, Mr. Johnson.’", "런던에서 온 신사, 새 작품의 작가, 새 작품의 배우, 무대에 처음 서는 사람이라니, 그러면 큰 예약 공연으로 이어질 거야, 존슨 씨.\""]], [["‘I am very sorry to throw a damp upon the prospects of anybody, and more especially a lady,’ replied Nicholas; ‘but really I must decidedly object to making one of the canvassing party.’", "\"누구의 앞날에든 찬물을 끼얹게 되어 정말 미안해요. 더구나 숙녀분의 일이라면 더더욱요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"하지만 정말이지 그 표 모으는 무리에 끼는 건 단호히 거절해야겠어요.\""]], [["‘What does Mr. Johnson say, Vincent?’ inquired a voice close to his ear; and, looking round, he found Mrs.", "\"존슨 씨가 뭐라고 하나요, 빈센트?\""], ["Crummles and Miss Snevellicci herself standing behind him.", "귓가에서 목소리가 들려왔어. 니콜라스가 돌아보니, 크럼블스 부인과 스네벨리치 양 본인이 그의 뒤에 서 있었지."]], [["‘He has some objection, my dear,’ replied Mr.", "\"뭔가 못마땅한 게 있다는구려, 여보.\""], ["Crummles, looking at Nicholas.", "크럼블스 씨가 니콜라스를 보며 대답했어."]], [["‘Objection!’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"못마땅하다고요!\" 크럼블스 부인이 외쳤어."], ["Crummles. ‘Can it be possible?’", "\"그럴 리가 있나요?\""]], [["‘Oh, I hope not!’ cried Miss Snevellicci. ‘You surely are not so cruel--oh, dear me!--Well, I--to think of that now, after all one’s looking forward to it!’", "\"오, 제발 아니라고 해 주세요!\" 스네벨리치 양이 외쳤어. \"설마 그렇게 모질지는 않으시겠죠. 오, 이를 어째! 그래, 내가, 이런 일을 다 겪다니, 그렇게나 기대했는데!\""]], [["‘Mr. Johnson will not persist, my dear,’ said Mrs.", "\"존슨 씨는 고집부리지 않을 거예요, 여보.\""], ["Crummles. ‘Think better of him than to suppose it.", "크럼블스 부인이 말했어."], ["Gallantry, humanity, all the best feelings of his nature, must be enlisted in this interesting cause.’", "\"그런 분이라고 의심하지 말아요. 신사다움이며 인정이며, 저분 천성의 가장 고운 마음씨가 모두 이 갸륵한 일에 동원될 거예요.\""]], [["‘Which moves even a manager,’ said Mr.", "\"단장의 마음마저 움직이는 일이지.\""], ["Crummles, smiling.", "크럼블스 씨가 미소 지으며 말했어."]], [["‘And a manager’s wife,’ added Mrs.", "\"단장 아내의 마음까지요.\""], ["Crummles, in her accustomed tragedy tones.", "크럼블스 부인이 늘 그렇듯 비극 배우 같은 목소리로 덧붙였어."], ["‘Come, come, you will relent, I know you will.’", "\"자, 자, 마음을 누그러뜨리실 거예요. 그러실 줄 알아요.\""]], [["‘It is not in my nature,’ said Nicholas, moved by these appeals, ‘to resist any entreaty, unless it is to do something positively wrong; and, beyond a feeling of pride, I know nothing which should prevent my doing this.", "\"제 천성이,\" 니콜라스가 이 호소들에 마음이 움직여 말했어."], ["I know nobody here, and nobody knows me.", "\"어떤 간청도 뿌리치지 못하는 편이라서요. 그게 명백히 그릇된 일만 아니라면요."], ["So be it then.", "그리고 자존심 하나 빼고는, 이 일을 마다할 까닭이 없네요. 여기서는 제가 아는 사람도 없고, 저를 아는 사람도 없으니까요."], ["I yield.’", "그럼 그렇게 하죠. 제가 졌어요.\""]], [["Miss Snevellicci was at once overwhelmed with blushes and expressions of gratitude, of which latter commodity neither Mr.", "스네벨리치 양은 단번에 얼굴이 빨개지며 고맙다는 말을 쏟아 냈어. 그 고맙다는 말이라면 크럼블스 씨도 부인도 결코 아끼지 않았지."], ["nor Mrs.", "니콜라스가 이튿날 아침 열한 시에 그녀의 하숙집으로 찾아가기로 정해졌어."], ["Crummles was by any means sparing.", "그러고는 곧 헤어졌지. 니콜라스는 집필을 하러 집으로 돌아갔고, 스네벨리치 양은 막간극 의상을 갈아입으러 갔어."], ["It was arranged that Nicholas should call upon her, at her lodgings, at eleven next morning, and soon after they parted: he to return home to his authorship: Miss Snevellicci to dress for the after-piece: and the disinterested manager and his wife to discuss the probable gains of the forthcoming bespeak, of which they were to have two-thirds of the profits by solemn treaty of agreement.", "그리고 사심 없는 단장과 그 아내는 다가올 예약 공연으로 얼마나 벌이가 될지를 의논했어. 엄숙한 합의에 따라 그 수익의 삼분의 이를 두 사람이 가져가기로 되어 있었거든."]], [["At the stipulated hour next morning, Nicholas repaired to the lodgings of Miss Snevellicci, which were in a place called Lombard Street, at the house of a tailor.", "이튿날 아침 약속한 시각에, 니콜라스는 스네벨리치 양의 하숙집으로 찾아갔어. 롬바드 거리라는 곳에 있는, 어느 재단사의 집이었지."], ["A strong smell of ironing pervaded the little passage; and the tailor’s daughter, who opened the door, appeared in that flutter of spirits which is so often attendant upon the periodical getting up of a family’s linen.", "좁은 복도에는 다림질 냄새가 진동했어. 문을 열어 준 재단사의 딸은, 온 식구의 빨랫감을 정기적으로 손질할 때면 으레 따라오는 그 들뜬 기색이었지."]], [["‘Miss Snevellicci lives here, I believe?’ said Nicholas, when the door was opened.", "\"스네벨리치 양이 여기 사는 게 맞죠?\" 문이 열리자 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["The tailor’s daughter replied in the affirmative.", "재단사의 딸은 그렇다고 대답했어."]], [["‘Will you have the goodness to let her know that Mr.", "\"존슨이 왔다고 좀 전해 주시겠어요?\""], ["Johnson is here?’ said Nicholas.", "니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Oh, if you please, you’re to come upstairs,’ replied the tailor’s daughter, with a smile.", "\"오, 괜찮으시면 위층으로 올라오세요.\" 재단사의 딸이 웃으며 대답했어."]], [["Nicholas followed the young lady, and was shown into a small apartment on the first floor, communicating with a back-room; in which, as he judged from a certain half-subdued clinking sound, as of cups and saucers, Miss Snevellicci was then taking her breakfast in bed.", "니콜라스는 그 아가씨를 따라갔어. 그리고 이 층의 작은 방으로 안내받았지. 그 방은 안쪽 방과 통해 있었어. 어렴풋이 짤그락거리는 소리, 찻잔과 받침이 부딪치는 듯한 소리로 미루어, 스네벨리치 양은 그때 침대에서 아침을 먹고 있는 모양이었지."]], [["‘You’re to wait, if you please,’ said the tailor’s daughter, after a short period of absence, during which the clinking in the back-room had ceased, and been succeeded by whispering--‘She won’t be long.’", "\"괜찮으시면 좀 기다리세요.\" 잠깐 자리를 비웠다 돌아온 재단사의 딸이 말했어. 그새 안쪽 방의 짤그락거리는 소리는 멎고, 속삭이는 소리로 바뀌어 있었지. \"곧 나오실 거예요.\""]], [["As she spoke, she pulled up the window-blind, and having by this means (as she thought) diverted Mr.", "말을 하면서 그녀는 창문 가리개를 끌어 올렸어. 그렇게 해서 (스스로 생각하기에) 존슨 씨의 눈길을 방에서 거리로 돌려놓은 다음, 난로망에 널어 말리던 물건 몇 개를 잽싸게 집어 들었어."], ["Johnson’s attention from the room to the street, caught up some articles which were airing on the fender, and had very much the appearance of stockings, and darted off.", "영락없이 스타킹처럼 보이는 것들이었지. 그러고는 휙 사라졌어."]], [["As there were not many objects of interest outside the window, Nicholas looked about the room with more curiosity than he might otherwise have bestowed upon it. On the sofa lay an old guitar, several thumbed pieces of music, and a scattered litter of curl-papers; together with a confused heap of play-bills, and a pair of soiled white satin shoes with large blue rosettes. Hanging over the back of a chair was a half-finished muslin apron with little pockets ornamented with red ribbons, such as waiting-women wear on the stage, and (by consequence) are never seen with anywhere else. In one corner stood the diminutive pair of top-boots in which Miss Snevellicci was accustomed to enact the little jockey, and, folded on a chair hard by, was a small parcel, which bore a very suspicious resemblance to the companion smalls.", "창밖에 눈길 끌 만한 게 별로 없어서, 니콜라스는 방 안을 둘러봤어. 평소 같으면 그러지 않았을 텐데, 호기심이 더 일었지. 소파에는 낡은 기타 하나와, 손때 묻은 악보 몇 장과, 여기저기 흩어진 종이 머리 마는 도구가 놓여 있었어. 거기다 어수선하게 쌓인 공연 전단지 더미와, 커다란 파란 장미 매듭이 달린 더러운 흰 새틴 신발 한 켤레도 있었지. 의자 등받이에는 반쯤 만들다 만 모슬린 앞치마가 걸려 있었어. 빨간 리본으로 장식한 작은 주머니가 달린 거였지. 무대에서 시녀들이 두르는, 그래서 (당연히) 다른 데서는 절대 볼 수 없는 그런 앞치마였어. 한쪽 구석에는 자그마한 장화 한 켤레가 놓여 있었어. 스네벨리치 양이 어린 기수 역을 할 때 신던 거였지. 그 옆 의자에는 작은 꾸러미 하나가 개켜져 있었는데, 짝을 이루는 짧은 바지를 영락없이 닮았더라고."]], [["But the most interesting object of all was, perhaps, the open scrapbook, displayed in the midst of some theatrical duodecimos that were strewn upon the table; and pasted into which scrapbook were various critical notices of Miss Snevellicci’s acting, extracted from different provincial journals, together with one poetic address in her honour, commencing--", "하지만 무엇보다 흥미로운 건, 아마 펼쳐져 있던 스크랩북이었을 거야. 탁자 위에 흩어진 작은 연극 책들 한가운데에 놓여 있었지. 그 스크랩북에는 스네벨리치 양의 연기에 관한 갖가지 비평이 붙어 있었어. 여러 지방 신문에서 오려 낸 것들이었지. 거기에 그녀를 기리는 시 한 편도 같이 붙어 있었는데, 이렇게 시작했어."]], [["Sing, God of Love, and tell me in what dearth Thrice-gifted SNEVELLICCI came on earth, To thrill us with her smile, her tear, her eye, Sing, God of Love, and tell me quickly why.", "노래하라, 사랑의 신이여, 그리고 말해 다오, 어떤 메마른 시절에 세 겹의 재능을 타고난 스네벨리치가 이 땅에 왔는지를. 그 미소로, 그 눈물로, 그 눈빛으로 우리를 떨리게 하려고, 노래하라, 사랑의 신이여, 그리고 어서 그 까닭을 말해 다오."]], [["Besides this effusion, there were innumerable complimentary allusions, also extracted from newspapers, such as--‘We observe from an advertisement in another part of our paper of today, that the charming and highly-talented Miss Snevellicci takes her benefit on Wednesday, for which occasion she has put forth a bill of fare that might kindle exhilaration in the breast of a misanthrope. In the confidence that our fellow-townsmen have not lost that high appreciation of public utility and private worth, for which they have long been so pre-eminently distinguished, we predict that this charming actress will be greeted with a bumper.’ ‘To Correspondents.--J.S. is misinformed when he supposes that the highly-gifted and beautiful Miss Snevellicci, nightly captivating all hearts at our pretty and commodious little theatre, is NOT the same lady to whom the young gentleman of immense fortune, residing within a hundred miles of the good city of York, lately made honourable proposals. We have reason to know that Miss Snevellicci IS the lady who was implicated in that mysterious and romantic affair, and whose conduct on that occasion did no less honour to her head and heart, than do her histrionic triumphs to her brilliant genius.’ A copious assortment of such paragraphs as these, with long bills of benefits all ending with ‘Come Early’, in large capitals, formed the principal contents of Miss Snevellicci’s scrapbook.", "이 격정 어린 시 말고도, 신문에서 오려 낸 칭찬의 글이 수도 없이 많았어. 이런 식이었지. \"오늘 신문 다른 면의 광고에서, 매력적이고 재능 넘치는 스네벨리치 양이 수요일에 자선 공연을 연다는 소식을 보았다. 이날을 위해 그녀가 내놓은 공연 목록은 인간 혐오자의 가슴에도 흥을 지필 만하다. 우리 이웃 시민들이 공익에 대한 높은 안목과 개인의 진가를 알아보는 식견을 여전히 잃지 않았으리라 믿는다. 그들은 오랫동안 그 점에서 더없이 두드러져 왔으니. 우리는 이 매력적인 여배우가 만원사례의 환대를 받으리라 예언하는 바이다.\" \"독자께 알림. J. S. 씨께서는 잘못 알고 계신다. 우리의 아담하고 편리한 작은 극장에서 매일 밤 모든 이의 마음을 사로잡는, 재능 넘치고 아름다운 스네벨리치 양이, 요크 시 백 마일 이내에 사는 막대한 재산의 젊은 신사가 최근 정중히 청혼한 그 숙녀가 아니라고 말이다. 우리는 알고 있다. 스네벨리치 양이야말로 그 신비롭고 낭만적인 사건에 얽혀 있던 그 숙녀라는 것을. 그리고 그때 그녀가 보인 처신은, 그녀의 빛나는 천재성을 증명하는 연기 위의 승리만큼이나, 그녀의 머리와 마음에 더없는 영예를 안겨 주었다.\" 이런 글들이 잔뜩 모여 있었고, 거기에 큼지막한 대문자로 죄다 \"일찍 오세요\"로 끝나는 긴 자선 공연 전단지들까지. 그게 스네벨리치 양 스크랩북의 주된 내용이었어."]], [["Nicholas had read a great many of these scraps, and was absorbed in a circumstantial and melancholy account of the train of events which had led to Miss Snevellicci’s spraining her ankle by slipping on a piece of orange-peel flung by a monster in human form, (so the paper said,) upon the stage at Winchester,--when that young lady herself, attired in the coal-scuttle bonnet and walking-dress complete, tripped into the room, with a thousand apologies for having detained him so long after the appointed time.", "니콜라스는 이런 오려 낸 글들을 꽤 많이 읽었어. 그러다 어떤 자세하고 구슬픈 사연에 푹 빠졌지. 스네벨리치 양이 발목을 삐게 된 일련의 사건에 관한 이야기였어. 윈체스터의 무대에서 사람 탈을 쓴 괴물이 (신문에는 그렇게 적혀 있었어) 던진 오렌지 껍질을 밟고 미끄러진 거였지. 그때 바로 그 아가씨가, 석탄 통처럼 챙이 깊은 보닛에 외출복까지 완벽하게 차려입고 사뿐사뿐 방으로 들어왔어. 약속한 시간보다 한참 늦게 그를 붙잡아 둔 데 대해 천 번이나 사과하면서 말이야."]], [["‘But really,’ said Miss Snevellicci, ‘my darling Led, who lives with me here, was taken so very ill in the night that I thought she would have expired in my arms.’", "\"그게요, 정말이지,\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했어. \"여기서 저랑 같이 사는 사랑하는 레드가 밤새 어찌나 심하게 앓던지, 제 품에서 숨이 넘어가는 줄 알았어요.\""]], [["‘Such a fate is almost to be envied,’ returned Nicholas, ‘but I am very sorry to hear it nevertheless.’", "\"그런 운명이라면 차라리 부럽기까지 한데요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그래도 그 얘기를 들으니 정말 안타깝네요.\""]], [["‘What a creature you are to flatter!’ said Miss Snevellicci, buttoning her glove in much confusion.", "\"어쩜 그렇게 아부를 잘하세요!\" 스네벨리치 양이 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 장갑 단추를 채우면서 말했어."]], [["‘If it be flattery to admire your charms and accomplishments,’ rejoined Nicholas, laying his hand upon the scrapbook, ‘you have better specimens of it here.’", "\"당신의 매력과 재능에 감탄하는 게 아부라면,\" 니콜라스가 스크랩북에 손을 올리며 대꾸했어. \"여기 그보다 더 나은 아부의 본보기들이 잔뜩 있는걸요.\""]], [["‘Oh you cruel creature, to read such things as those!", "\"오, 짓궂으셔라. 그런 걸 다 읽으시다니!"], ["I’m almost ashamed to look you in the face afterwards, positively I am,’ said Miss Snevellicci, seizing the book and putting it away in a closet.", "이제 얼굴을 마주 볼 낯이 없어요, 정말이에요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 책을 낚아채 벽장 안에 치우며 말했어."], ["‘How careless of Led!", "\"레드도 참 칠칠치 못하지!"], ["How could she be so naughty!’", "어쩜 그렇게 못된 짓을!\""]], [["‘I thought you had kindly left it here, on purpose for me to read,’ said Nicholas.", "\"제가 읽으라고 일부러 친절히 여기 놔두신 줄 알았는데요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["And really it did seem possible.", "사실 그럴 법도 했지."]], [["‘I wouldn’t have had you see it for the world!’ rejoined Miss Snevellicci.", "\"무슨 일이 있어도 그건 보여 드리고 싶지 않았어요!\" 스네벨리치 양이 대꾸했어."], ["‘I never was so vexed--never!", "\"이렇게 속상한 적은 없었어요, 한 번도요!"], ["But she is such a careless thing, there’s no trusting her.’", "그 애가 워낙 칠칠치 못해서 도무지 믿을 수가 없다니까요.\""]], [["The conversation was here interrupted by the entrance of the phenomenon, who had discreetly remained in the bedroom up to this moment, and now presented herself, with much grace and lightness, bearing in her hand a very little green parasol with a broad fringe border, and no handle. After a few words of course, they sallied into the street.", "여기서 대화가 끊겼어. 신동이 들어왔거든. 이 순간까지 얌전히 침실에 머물러 있던 신동은, 이제 더없이 우아하고 사뿐하게 모습을 드러냈어. 손에는 아주 자그마한 초록색 양산을 들고 있었지. 넓은 술 장식이 둘러진, 손잡이 없는 양산이었어. 으레 하는 인사 몇 마디를 나눈 뒤, 그들은 거리로 나섰어."]], [["The phenomenon was rather a troublesome companion, for first the right sandal came down, and then the left, and these mischances being repaired, one leg of the little white trousers was discovered to be longer than the other; besides these accidents, the green parasol was dropped down an iron grating, and only fished up again with great difficulty and by dint of much exertion. However, it was impossible to scold her, as she was the manager’s daughter, so Nicholas took it all in perfect good humour, and walked on, with Miss Snevellicci, arm-in-arm on one side, and the offending infant on the other.", "신동은 꽤나 성가신 동행이었어. 먼저 오른쪽 샌들이 벗겨지고, 다음엔 왼쪽이 벗겨졌거든. 그걸 고쳐 신기고 나니, 이번엔 작은 흰 바지의 한쪽 가랑이가 다른 쪽보다 길다는 게 드러났지. 게다가 초록색 양산이 쇠창살 틈으로 떨어져서, 한참 애를 쓰고 갖은 수를 써서야 겨우 건져 올렸어. 그래도 야단을 칠 수는 없었지. 단장의 딸이었으니까. 그래서 니콜라스는 이 모든 걸 너그러이 받아들이고 계속 걸었어. 한쪽 팔에는 스네벨리치 양이 팔짱을 끼고, 다른 쪽에는 말썽쟁이 아이를 데리고서 말이야."]], [["The first house to which they bent their steps, was situated in a terrace of respectable appearance.", "그들이 가장 먼저 발길을 향한 집은, 점잖아 보이는 연립 주택가에 있었어."], ["Miss Snevellicci’s modest double-knock was answered by a foot-boy, who, in reply to her inquiry whether Mrs.", "스네벨리치 양이 점잖게 문을 두 번 두드리자, 사환 소년이 나왔지."], ["Curdle was at home, opened his eyes very wide, grinned very much, and said he didn’t know, but he’d inquire.", "커들 부인이 집에 계시냐는 물음에, 소년은 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 히죽히죽 웃으며, 잘 모르겠지만 여쭤보겠다고 했어. 그러고는 그들을 응접실로 안내해 기다리게 했지."], ["With this he showed them into a parlour where he kept them waiting, until the two women-servants had repaired thither, under false pretences, to see the play-actors; and having compared notes with them in the passage, and joined in a vast quantity of whispering and giggling, he at length went upstairs with Miss Snevellicci’s name.", "그새 하녀 두 명이 핑계를 대고 그리로 와서는, 연극배우들을 구경했어. 그러고는 복도에서 소년이랑 서로 정보를 나누고, 잔뜩 속닥거리고 킥킥대더니, 마침내 소년이 스네벨리치 양의 이름을 들고 위층으로 올라갔어."]], [["Now, Mrs.", "자, 이런 일에 밝은 사람들 사이에서 커들 부인은 문학이며 연극에 관한 한 그야말로 런던 취향을 지녔다고 여겨졌어."], ["Curdle was supposed, by those who were best informed on such points, to possess quite the London taste in matters relating to literature and the drama; and as to Mr.", "그리고 커들 씨로 말하자면, 그는 팔 절판 육십사 쪽짜리 소책자를 한 권 쓴 적이 있었지."], ["Curdle, he had written a pamphlet of sixty-four pages, post octavo, on the character of the Nurse’s deceased husband in Romeo and Juliet, with an inquiry whether he really had been a ‘merry man’ in his lifetime, or whether it was merely his widow’s affectionate partiality that induced her so to report him.", "「로미오와 줄리엣」에 나오는 유모의 죽은 남편의 성격에 관한 거였어. 그가 생전에 정말로 \"유쾌한 사람\"이었는지, 아니면 그저 미망인이 애틋한 정에 끌려 그렇게 전한 것뿐인지 따져 보는 내용이었지."], ["He had likewise proved, that by altering the received mode of punctuation, any one of Shakespeare’s plays could be made quite different, and the sense completely changed; it is needless to say, therefore, that he was a great critic, and a very profound and most original thinker.", "그는 또 통용되는 구두점 찍는 방식을 바꾸면, 셰익스피어의 어떤 희곡이든 완전히 다른 작품으로 만들어 뜻을 송두리째 바꿀 수 있다는 것도 입증했어. 그러니 두말할 것도 없이, 그는 대단한 비평가요, 더없이 심오하고 독창적인 사상가였지."]], [["‘Well, Miss Snevellicci,’ said Mrs.", "\"어머, 스네벨리치 양.\""], ["Curdle, entering the parlour, ‘and how do YOU do?’", "커들 부인이 응접실로 들어서며 말했어. \"그래, 잘 지냈어요?\""]], [["Miss Snevellicci made a graceful obeisance, and hoped Mrs. Curdle was well, as also Mr.", "스네벨리치 양은 우아하게 인사하고는, 커들 부인이 안녕하시기를, 또 마침 같이 나타난 커들 씨도 안녕하시기를 바랐어."], ["Curdle, who at the same time appeared. Mrs.", "커들 부인은 실내복 차림에, 머리 꼭대기에 작은 모자를 얹고 있었어."], ["Curdle was dressed in a morning wrapper, with a little cap stuck upon the top of her head. Mr.", "커들 씨는 등에 헐렁한 가운을 걸치고, 오른손 검지를 이마에 댄 자세였지."], ["Curdle wore a loose robe on his back, and his right forefinger on his forehead after the portraits of Sterne, to whom somebody or other had once said he bore a striking resemblance.", "스턴의 초상화를 본뜬 거였어. 누군가가 그에게 스턴과 놀랄 만큼 닮았다고 한 적이 있었거든."]], [["‘I venture to call, for the purpose of asking whether you would put your name to my bespeak, ma’am,’ said Miss Snevellicci, producing documents.", "\"제 예약 공연에 이름을 올려 주실 수 있을까 여쭈러 외람되이 찾아왔어요, 부인.\" 스네벨리치 양이 서류를 꺼내며 말했어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I really don’t know what to say,’ replied Mrs.", "정말 뭐라고 해야 할지 모르겠네요.\" 커들 부인이 대답했어."], ["Curdle.", "\"연극이 한창 흥하고 번창하던 시절도 아니고."], ["‘It’s not as if the theatre was in its high and palmy days--you needn’t stand, Miss Snevellicci--the drama is gone, perfectly gone.’", "서 있지 말아요, 스네벨리치 양. 연극은 갔어요, 완전히 갔어요.\""]], [["‘As an exquisite embodiment of the poet’s visions, and a realisation of human intellectuality, gilding with refulgent light our dreamy moments, and laying open a new and magic world before the mental eye, the drama is gone, perfectly gone,’ said Mr.", "\"시인의 환상을 더없이 정교하게 구현한 것이자, 인간 지성을 실현한 것으로서, 우리의 몽롱한 순간을 찬란한 빛으로 물들이고, 마음의 눈앞에 새롭고 마법 같은 세계를 펼쳐 보이던 연극은, 갔어요, 완전히 갔어요.\""], ["Curdle.", "커들 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘What man is there, now living, who can present before us all those changing and prismatic colours with which the character of Hamlet is invested?’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"지금 살아 있는 사람 가운데, 햄릿이라는 인물에 깃든 그 변화무쌍하고 영롱한 빛깔들을 우리 앞에 펼쳐 보일 수 있는 자가 어디 있겠어요?\""], ["Curdle.", "커들 부인이 외쳤어."]], [["‘What man indeed--upon the stage,’ said Mr.", "\"정말이지 누가 있겠소, 무대 위에 말이오.\""], ["Curdle, with a small reservation in favour of himself.", "커들 씨가 자기만은 살짝 예외로 두며 말했어."], ["‘Hamlet!", "\"햄릿이라!"], ["Pooh!", "흥!"], ["ridiculous!", "우스운 일이지!"], ["Hamlet is gone, perfectly gone.’", "햄릿은 갔어요, 완전히 갔어요.\""]], [["Quite overcome by these dismal reflections, Mr. and Mrs.", "이 음울한 상념에 그만 압도되어, 커들 씨 부부는 한숨을 쉬고는 한동안 말없이 앉아 있었어."], ["Curdle sighed, and sat for some short time without speaking. At length, the lady, turning to Miss Snevellicci, inquired what play she proposed to have.", "마침내 부인이 스네벨리치 양을 돌아보며, 어떤 연극을 올릴 생각이냐고 물었어."]], [["‘Quite a new one,’ said Miss Snevellicci, ‘of which this gentleman is the author, and in which he plays; being his first appearance on any stage.", "\"완전히 새 작품이에요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했어."], ["Mr.", "\"여기 이 신사분이 작가시고, 이분이 직접 출연도 하세요."], ["Johnson is the gentleman’s name.’", "무대에 처음 서시는 거고요. 존슨 씨라는 분이에요.\""]], [["‘I hope you have preserved the unities, sir?’ said Mr.", "\"삼일치는 지키셨겠지요, 선생?\""], ["Curdle.", "커들 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘The original piece is a French one,’ said Nicholas.", "\"원작은 프랑스 작품이에요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘There is abundance of incident, sprightly dialogue, strongly-marked characters--’", "\"사건도 풍성하고, 대사도 발랄하고, 인물도 뚜렷하게 그려져 있죠.\""]], [["‘--All unavailing without a strict observance of the unities, sir,’ returned Mr.", "\"삼일치를 엄격히 지키지 않으면 다 소용없어요, 선생.\" 커들 씨가 받았어."], ["Curdle. ‘The unities of the drama, before everything.’", "\"연극의 삼일치, 그게 무엇보다 먼저요.\""]], [["‘Might I ask you,’ said Nicholas, hesitating between the respect he ought to assume, and his love of the whimsical, ‘might I ask you what the unities are?’", "\"여쭤봐도 될까요.\" 니콜라스가 마땅히 차려야 할 예의와 짓궂은 걸 좋아하는 마음 사이에서 망설이며 말했어. \"그 삼일치라는 게 뭔지 여쭤봐도 될까요?\""]], [["Mr. Curdle coughed and considered. ‘The unities, sir,’ he said, ‘are a completeness--a kind of universal dovetailedness with regard to place and time--a sort of a general oneness, if I may be allowed to use so strong an expression. I take those to be the dramatic unities, so far as I have been enabled to bestow attention upon them, and I have read much upon the subject, and thought much. I find, running through the performances of this child,’ said Mr. Curdle, turning to the phenomenon, ‘a unity of feeling, a breadth, a light and shade, a warmth of colouring, a tone, a harmony, a glow, an artistical development of original conceptions, which I look for, in vain, among older performers--I don’t know whether I make myself understood?’", "커들 씨가 헛기침을 하고는 곰곰이 생각했어. \"삼일치란 말이오, 선생.\" 그가 말했어. \"하나의 완결성이지요. 장소와 시간에 관한 일종의 보편적인 짜맞춤이라고 할까. 이렇게 강한 표현을 써도 된다면, 일종의 전반적인 하나됨이오. 내가 거기에 주의를 기울일 수 있었던 한에서는, 그것이 바로 연극의 삼일치라고 보오. 나는 이 주제에 관해 많이 읽었고, 많이 생각했소. 이 아이의 연기를 보면,\" 커들 씨가 신동을 돌아보며 말했어. \"거기엔 감정의 통일성과, 넓이와, 명암과, 따뜻한 색조와, 음색과, 조화와, 빛남과, 독창적 착상의 예술적 전개가 흐르고 있소. 나이 든 배우들에게서는 아무리 찾으려 해도 찾을 수 없는 것들이지. 내 말을 알아듣겠소?\""]], [["‘Perfectly,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"완벽히요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Just so,’ said Mr.", "\"바로 그렇소.\""], ["Curdle, pulling up his neckcloth.", "커들 씨가 목도리를 끌어 올리며 말했어."], ["‘That is my definition of the unities of the drama.’", "\"그게 내가 내리는 연극 삼일치의 정의요.\""]], [["Mrs. Curdle had sat listening to this lucid explanation with great complacency.", "커들 부인은 이 명쾌한 설명에 흡족해하며 귀를 기울이고 앉아 있었어."], ["It being finished, she inquired what Mr. Curdle thought, about putting down their names.", "설명이 끝나자, 부인은 이름을 적는 일에 대해 커들 씨가 어떻게 생각하는지 물었어."]], [["‘I don’t know, my dear; upon my word I don’t know,’ said Mr.", "\"모르겠소, 여보. 정말이지 모르겠소.\""], ["Curdle. ‘If we do, it must be distinctly understood that we do not pledge ourselves to the quality of the performances.", "커들 씨가 말했어."], ["Let it go forth to the world, that we do not give THEM the sanction of our names, but that we confer the distinction merely upon Miss Snevellicci.", "\"만약 적는다면, 우리가 공연의 질을 보증하는 게 아니라는 걸 분명히 못 박아 둬야 하오. 세상에 알려야 한단 말이오."], ["That being clearly stated, I take it to be, as it were, a duty, that we should extend our patronage to a degraded stage, even for the sake of the associations with which it is entwined.", "우리가 그들에게 우리 이름의 인정을 내주는 게 아니라, 그저 스네벨리치 양에게만 그 영예를 베푸는 거라고. 그 점을 분명히 한다면, 비록 그 연극에 얽힌 추억을 봐서라도, 타락한 무대에 우리의 후원을 베푸는 게, 말하자면 하나의 도리라고 보오."], ["Have you got two-and-sixpence for half-a-crown, Miss Snevellicci?’ said Mr.", "반 크라운 거슬러 줄 이 실링 육 펜스 있소, 스네벨리치 양?\""], ["Curdle, turning over four of those pieces of money.", "커들 씨가 그 동전 네 닢을 뒤적이며 말했어."]], [["Miss Snevellicci felt in all the corners of the pink reticule, but there was nothing in any of them.", "스네벨리치 양은 분홍색 손가방 구석구석을 더듬어 봤지만, 어느 구석에도 아무것도 없었어."], ["Nicholas murmured a jest about his being an author, and thought it best not to go through the form of feeling in his own pockets at all.", "니콜라스는 자기는 작가일 뿐이라며 농담을 중얼거렸고, 자기 주머니를 뒤지는 시늉조차 아예 안 하는 게 낫겠다고 생각했지."]], [["‘Let me see,’ said Mr.", "\"어디 보자.\" 커들 씨가 말했어."], ["Curdle; ‘twice four’s eight--four shillings a-piece to the boxes, Miss Snevellicci, is exceedingly dear in the present state of the drama--three half-crowns is seven-and-six; we shall not differ about sixpence, I suppose?", "\"이 곱하기 사는 팔이고, 한 자리에 사 실링짜리 특별석이라니, 지금처럼 연극이 형편없는 때에는 너무 비싸군, 스네벨리치 양. 반 크라운 세 닢이면 칠 실링 육 펜스이고."], ["Sixpence will not part us, Miss Snevellicci?’", "육 펜스 가지고는 다투지 않겠지, 그렇지? 육 펜스 때문에 갈라서지는 않겠지, 스네벨리치 양?\""]], [["Poor Miss Snevellicci took the three half-crowns, with many smiles and bends, and Mrs.", "가엾은 스네벨리치 양은 연신 웃고 고개를 숙이며 반 크라운 세 닢을 받았어."], ["Curdle, adding several supplementary directions relative to keeping the places for them, and dusting the seat, and sending two clean bills as soon as they came out, rang the bell, as a signal for breaking up the conference.", "그리고 커들 부인은 자리를 잡아 둘 것이며, 좌석을 닦아 둘 것이며, 새 전단지가 나오는 대로 깨끗한 걸로 두 장 보내 줄 것이며, 이런저런 당부를 몇 마디 덧붙이고는, 종을 울렸어. 면담을 끝내자는 신호였지."]], [["‘Odd people those,’ said Nicholas, when they got clear of the house.", "\"별난 사람들이네요.\" 그 집에서 벗어나자 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘I assure you,’ said Miss Snevellicci, taking his arm, ‘that I think myself very lucky they did not owe all the money instead of being sixpence short. Now, if you were to succeed, they would give people to understand that they had always patronised you; and if you were to fail, they would have been quite certain of that from the very beginning.’", "\"정말이지,\" 스네벨리치 양이 그의 팔을 잡으며 말했어. \"그분들이 육 펜스 모자란 게 아니라 돈을 다 빚지지 않은 게 다행이다 싶어요. 자, 만약 당신이 성공하면, 그분들은 자기들이 늘 당신을 후원했다고 떠벌리고 다닐 거예요. 그러다 당신이 실패하면, 처음부터 그럴 줄 알았다고 했겠죠.\""]], [["At the next house they visited, they were in great glory; for, there, resided the six children who were so enraptured with the public actions of the phenomenon, and who, being called down from the nursery to be treated with a private view of that young lady, proceeded to poke their fingers into her eyes, and tread upon her toes, and show her many other little attentions peculiar to their time of life.", "다음으로 찾아간 집에서, 그들은 큰 영광을 누렸어. 거기엔 신동의 공연에 그토록 푹 빠진 그 여섯 아이가 살고 있었거든. 아이들은 그 아가씨를 따로 구경하라고 육아실에서 불려 내려왔어. 그러고는 신동의 눈을 손가락으로 쿡쿡 찌르고, 발가락을 밟아 대고, 그 나이대 특유의 자잘한 친절을 잔뜩 베풀었지."]], [["‘I shall certainly persuade Mr.", "\"보럼 씨를 설득해서 꼭 개인 특별석을 잡게 할게요.\""], ["Borum to take a private box,’ said the lady of the house, after a most gracious reception.", "더없이 정중히 맞이한 뒤에 그 집 안주인이 말했어."], ["‘I shall only take two of the children, and will make up the rest of the party, of gentlemen--your admirers, Miss Snevellicci.", "\"아이는 둘만 데려가고, 나머지 일행은 신사분들로 채울 거예요. 스네벨리치 양, 당신을 흠모하는 분들로요."], ["Augustus, you naughty boy, leave the little girl alone.’", "오거스터스, 이 못된 녀석, 어린 아가씨 좀 가만 놔둬라.\""]], [["This was addressed to a young gentleman who was pinching the phenomenon behind, apparently with a view of ascertaining whether she was real.", "이 말은 한 젊은 신사를 향한 거였어. 그 아이는 신동의 등 뒤를 꼬집고 있었지. 그게 진짜 사람인지 확인해 보려는 모양이었어."]], [["‘I am sure you must be very tired,’ said the mama, turning to Miss Snevellicci.", "\"분명 무척 피곤하실 거예요.\" 엄마가 스네벨리치 양을 돌아보며 말했어."], ["‘I cannot think of allowing you to go, without first taking a glass of wine.", "\"포도주 한 잔도 안 드시고 가시게 둘 수는 없죠."], ["Fie, Charlotte, I am ashamed of you!", "어머나, 샬럿, 너 부끄러운 줄 알아라!"], ["Miss Lane, my dear, pray see to the children.’", "레인 양, 미안하지만 아이들 좀 봐 줘요.\""]], [["Miss Lane was the governess, and this entreaty was rendered necessary by the abrupt behaviour of the youngest Miss Borum, who, having filched the phenomenon’s little green parasol, was now carrying it bodily off, while the distracted infant looked helplessly on.", "레인 양은 가정교사였어. 이런 부탁이 필요했던 건, 막내 보럼 아가씨의 난데없는 행동 때문이었지. 그 아이가 신동의 작은 초록색 양산을 슬쩍해서는, 그대로 들고 도망가고 있었거든. 정신이 쏙 빠진 신동은 어쩔 줄 몰라 멀거니 바라보기만 했어."]], [["‘I am sure, where you ever learnt to act as you do,’ said good-natured Mrs. Borum, turning again to Miss Snevellicci, ‘I cannot understand (Emma, don’t stare so); laughing in one piece, and crying in the next, and so natural in all--oh, dear!’", "\"도대체 어디서 그렇게 연기하는 법을 배웠는지,\" 마음씨 좋은 보럼 부인이 다시 스네벨리치 양을 돌아보며 말했어. \"도무지 알 수가 없어요. (엠마, 그렇게 빤히 쳐다보지 마라.) 이 장면에서 웃다가, 저 장면에서 울다가, 다 어쩜 그렇게 자연스러운지. 오, 세상에!\""]], [["‘I am very happy to hear you express so favourable an opinion,’ said Miss Snevellicci.", "\"그렇게 좋게 봐 주시니 정말 기뻐요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했어."], ["‘It’s quite delightful to think you like it.’", "\"마음에 드신다니 더없이 즐겁네요.\""]], [["‘Like it!’ cried Mrs.", "\"마음에 들다뿐이겠어요!\""], ["Borum. ‘Who can help liking it?", "보럼 부인이 외쳤어."], ["I would go to the play, twice a week if I could: I dote upon it--only you’re too affecting sometimes.", "\"어떻게 마음에 안 들 수가 있어요? 할 수만 있다면 일주일에 두 번이라도 연극을 보러 가고 싶어요."], ["You do put me in such a state--into such fits of crying!", "정말 푹 빠졌다니까요. 다만 가끔 너무 가슴을 울려서 탈이에요."], ["Goodness gracious me, Miss Lane, how can you let them torment that poor child so!’", "어찌나 사람을 그런 지경으로 만드는지, 펑펑 울게 만든다니까요! 어머나, 세상에, 레인 양, 어떻게 저 가엾은 아이를 저렇게 들볶게 두는 거예요!\""]], [["The phenomenon was really in a fair way of being torn limb from limb; for two strong little boys, one holding on by each of her hands, were dragging her in different directions as a trial of strength. However, Miss Lane (who had herself been too much occupied in contemplating the grown-up actors, to pay the necessary attention to these proceedings) rescued the unhappy infant at this juncture, who, being recruited with a glass of wine, was shortly afterwards taken away by her friends, after sustaining no more serious damage than a flattening of the pink gauze bonnet, and a rather extensive creasing of the white frock and trousers.", "신동은 정말이지 사지가 찢겨 나갈 판이었어. 기운 센 사내아이 둘이 신동의 양손을 하나씩 붙잡고, 누가 더 센지 겨루느라 서로 반대 방향으로 잡아당기고 있었거든. 하지만 마침 레인 양이 그 가엾은 아이를 구해 냈어. 사실 레인 양은 다 큰 배우들을 구경하느라 너무 정신이 팔려서, 이 일에 마땅한 주의를 기울이지 못했던 거였지. 신동은 포도주 한 잔으로 기운을 차린 뒤, 곧 친구들 손에 이끌려 돌아갔어. 다행히 분홍 망사 보닛이 납작해지고, 흰 원피스와 바지가 꽤 심하게 구겨진 것 말고는 더 큰 손상은 입지 않았지."]], [["It was a trying morning; for there were a great many calls to make, and everybody wanted a different thing. Some wanted tragedies, and others comedies; some objected to dancing; some wanted scarcely anything else. Some thought the comic singer decidedly low, and others hoped he would have more to do than he usually had. Some people wouldn’t promise to go, because other people wouldn’t promise to go; and other people wouldn’t go at all, because other people went. At length, and by little and little, omitting something in this place, and adding something in that, Miss Snevellicci pledged herself to a bill of fare which was comprehensive enough, if it had no other merit (it included among other trifles, four pieces, divers songs, a few combats, and several dances); and they returned home, pretty well exhausted with the business of the day.", "힘든 아침이었어. 들러야 할 집이 워낙 많았는데, 사람마다 원하는 게 제각각이었거든. 어떤 이는 비극을, 어떤 이는 희극을 원했어. 어떤 이는 춤은 빼라고 했고, 어떤 이는 춤 말고는 별로 보고 싶은 게 없다고 했지. 어떤 이는 희극 가수가 딱 봐도 격이 떨어진다고 했고, 어떤 이는 그 가수가 평소보다 출연을 더 많이 했으면 좋겠다고 했어. 어떤 사람들은 가겠다는 약속을 안 했어. 다른 사람들이 가겠다는 약속을 안 했다는 이유로 말이야. 또 어떤 사람들은 아예 안 가겠다고 했지. 다른 사람들이 간다는 이유로 말이야. 마침내, 조금씩 조금씩, 여기서는 뭘 빼고 저기서는 뭘 더하면서, 스네벨리치 양은 한 공연 목록을 약속하기에 이르렀어. 다른 장점은 없을지언정 내용 하나는 풍성한 목록이었지. (다른 자질구레한 것들 가운데 연극 네 편, 노래 여러 곡, 결투 몇 차례, 춤 여러 번이 들어 있었어.) 그러고는 두 사람은 집으로 돌아왔어. 그날 일로 꽤 녹초가 되어서 말이야."]], [["Nicholas worked away at the piece, which was speedily put into rehearsal, and then worked away at his own part, which he studied with great perseverance and acted--as the whole company said--to perfection. And at length the great day arrived. The crier was sent round, in the morning, to proclaim the entertainments with the sound of bell in all the thoroughfares; and extra bills of three feet long by nine inches wide, were dispersed in all directions, flung down all the areas, thrust under all the knockers, and developed in all the shops. They were placarded on all the walls too, though not with complete success, for an illiterate person having undertaken this office during the indisposition of the regular bill-sticker, a part were posted sideways, and the remainder upside down.", "니콜라스는 작품을 써 나갔어. 작품은 곧바로 연습에 들어갔지. 그러고 나서 그는 자기 배역을 파고들었어. 엄청난 끈기로 익혀서, 온 극단이 입을 모아 말하기를, 완벽하게 연기해 냈어. 그리고 마침내 그 큰 날이 왔지. 아침에 거리마다 포고꾼을 보내, 종소리와 함께 공연을 알렸어. 그리고 길이 삼 피트에 너비 구 인치짜리 추가 전단지를 사방에 뿌렸지. 모든 지하 입구에 던져 넣고, 모든 문고리 밑에 끼워 넣고, 모든 상점에 펼쳐 놨어. 벽이란 벽에도 다 붙였지. 그런데 완전히 성공적이지는 못했어. 본래 전단 붙이는 사람이 아파서 글 모르는 사람이 그 일을 맡았거든. 그래서 일부는 옆으로 돌아가게 붙었고, 나머지는 거꾸로 붙었지."]], [["At half-past five, there was a rush of four people to the gallery-door; at a quarter before six, there were at least a dozen; at six o’clock the kicks were terrific; and when the elder Master Crummles opened the door, he was obliged to run behind it for his life.", "다섯 시 반에 위층 관람석 문으로 네 사람이 몰려왔어. 여섯 시 십오 분 전에는 적어도 열두 명은 됐지."], ["Fifteen shillings were taken by Mrs.", "여섯 시가 되자 문을 차 대는 소리가 무시무시했어. 그래서 큰 크럼블스 도련님이 문을 열었을 때는, 목숨을 부지하려고 문 뒤로 뛰어들어야 했지."], ["Grudden in the first ten minutes.", "첫 십 분 만에 그루든 부인이 십오 실링을 거뒀어."]], [["Behind the scenes, the same unwonted excitement prevailed.", "무대 뒤에서도 똑같이 색다른 흥분이 감돌았어."], ["Miss Snevellicci was in such a perspiration that the paint would scarcely stay on her face.", "스네벨리치 양은 어찌나 땀을 흘리던지, 얼굴에 분이 도무지 붙어 있질 않았어."], ["Mrs. Crummles was so nervous that she could hardly remember her part.", "크럼블스 부인은 어찌나 긴장했던지, 자기 대사를 거의 기억하지 못했지."], ["Miss Bravassa’s ringlets came out of curl with the heat and anxiety; even Mr.", "브라바사 양의 곱슬머리는 열기와 불안에 그만 다 풀려 버렸어."], ["Crummles himself kept peeping through the hole in the curtain, and running back, every now and then, to announce that another man had come into the pit.", "심지어 크럼블스 씨 본인까지 막에 난 구멍으로 자꾸 엿보고는, 이따금 뛰어 들어와서 일 층 좌석에 한 사람이 더 들어왔다고 알렸지."]], [["At last, the orchestra left off, and the curtain rose upon the new piece. The first scene, in which there was nobody particular, passed off calmly enough, but when Miss Snevellicci went on in the second, accompanied by the phenomenon as child, what a roar of applause broke out! The people in the Borum box rose as one man, waving their hats and handkerchiefs, and uttering shouts of ‘Bravo!’ Mrs. Borum and the governess cast wreaths upon the stage, of which, some fluttered into the lamps, and one crowned the temples of a fat gentleman in the pit, who, looking eagerly towards the scene, remained unconscious of the honour; the tailor and his family kicked at the panels of the upper boxes till they threatened to come out altogether; the very ginger-beer boy remained transfixed in the centre of the house; a young officer, supposed to entertain a passion for Miss Snevellicci, stuck his glass in his eye as though to hide a tear. Again and again Miss Snevellicci curtseyed lower and lower, and again and again the applause came down, louder and louder. At length, when the phenomenon picked up one of the smoking wreaths and put it on, sideways, over Miss Snevellicci’s eye, it reached its climax, and the play proceeded.", "마침내 악단이 연주를 멈추고, 새 작품의 막이 올랐어. 별다른 사람이 안 나오는 첫 장면은 그런대로 조용히 지나갔지. 그런데 둘째 장면에서 스네벨리치 양이, 아이 역의 신동을 데리고 등장하자, 그야말로 박수갈채가 터져 나왔어! 보럼 가족석의 사람들이 일제히 일어서서, 모자와 손수건을 흔들며 \"브라보!\"를 외쳤어. 보럼 부인과 가정교사는 무대 위로 화환을 던졌어. 그 가운데 몇 개는 조명등 속으로 펄럭이며 떨어졌고, 하나는 일 층 좌석에 앉은 뚱뚱한 신사의 머리에 씌워졌지. 그 신사는 무대 쪽을 뚫어져라 바라보느라, 그 영예를 까맣게 모르고 있었어. 재단사와 그 식구는 위층 특별석 칸막이를 어찌나 차 대던지, 통째로 떨어져 나올 지경이었지. 진저비어 파는 소년조차 극장 한복판에 못 박힌 듯 서 있었어. 스네벨리치 양에게 연정을 품은 것으로 여겨지는 한 젊은 장교는, 눈물을 가리려는 듯 안경을 눈에 갖다 댔지. 스네벨리치 양은 거듭거듭 점점 더 깊이 절을 했고, 박수는 거듭거듭 점점 더 크게 쏟아졌어. 마침내 신동이 김이 모락모락 나는 화환 하나를 집어서, 스네벨리치 양의 눈 위로 비스듬히 씌우자, 갈채가 절정에 이르렀지. 그러고서 연극은 계속됐어."]], [["But when Nicholas came on for his crack scene with Mrs. Crummles, what a clapping of hands there was!", "그런데 니콜라스가 크럼블스 부인과의 명장면을 위해 등장하자, 그 박수 소리란!"], ["When Mrs. Crummles (who was his unworthy mother), sneered, and called him ‘presumptuous boy,’ and he defied her, what a tumult of applause came on!", "크럼블스 부인이 (그의 못난 어머니 역이었지) 코웃음 치며 그를 \"건방진 녀석\"이라 부르고, 그가 맞서자, 박수가 그야말로 떠나갈 듯 터졌어!"], ["When he quarrelled with the other gentleman about the young lady, and producing a case of pistols, said, that if he WAS a gentleman, he would fight him in that drawing-room, until the furniture was sprinkled with the blood of one, if not of two--how boxes, pit, and gallery, joined in one most vigorous cheer!", "그가 그 아가씨를 두고 다른 신사와 다투며 권총 한 상자를 꺼내, 네가 정녕 신사라면 이 응접실에서 한 사람의, 아니 어쩌면 두 사람의 피가 가구에 흩뿌려질 때까지 결투를 벌이겠다고 하자, 특별석이고 일 층이고 위층 관람석이고 다 같이 더없이 우렁찬 환호를 보냈어!"], ["When he called his mother names, because she wouldn’t give up the young lady’s property, and she relenting, caused him to relent likewise, and fall down on one knee and ask her blessing, how the ladies in the audience sobbed!", "그가 어머니가 그 아가씨의 재산을 내놓지 않는다며 욕을 퍼붓다가, 어머니가 누그러지자 자기도 누그러져서, 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 축복을 빌자, 객석의 여인들이 어찌나 흐느꼈는지!"], ["When he was hid behind the curtain in the dark, and the wicked relation poked a sharp sword in every direction, save where his legs were plainly visible, what a thrill of anxious fear ran through the house!", "그가 어둠 속에서 막 뒤에 숨고, 못된 친척이 날카로운 칼을 사방으로 쑤셔 대는데, 정작 그의 다리가 빤히 보이는 데만 빼고 찌르자, 극장 안에 불안한 두려움의 전율이 좍 퍼졌어!"], ["His air, his figure, his walk, his look, everything he said or did, was the subject of commendation.", "그의 분위기며, 자태며, 걸음걸이며, 표정이며, 그가 말하고 행하는 모든 게 다 찬사의 대상이었지."], ["There was a round of applause every time he spoke.", "그가 입을 열 때마다 박수가 한바탕 쏟아졌어."], ["And when, at last, in the pump-and-tub scene, Mrs Grudden lighted the blue fire, and all the unemployed members of the company came in, and tumbled down in various directions--not because that had anything to do with the plot, but in order to finish off with a tableau--the audience (who had by this time increased considerably) gave vent to such a shout of enthusiasm as had not been heard in those walls for many and many a day.", "그리고 마침내, 펌프와 물통 장면에서, 그루든 부인이 파란 불을 켜고, 출연 없는 단원들이 모두 들어와 사방으로 나뒹굴자, 그건 줄거리와는 아무 상관도 없고 그저 한 폭의 그림 같은 장면으로 마무리하려는 거였지만, 그새 부쩍 늘어난 관객은 그야말로 열광에 찬 함성을 내질렀어. 그 벽 안에서 그토록 오랫동안 들어 본 적 없는 함성이었지."]], [["In short, the success both of new piece and new actor was complete, and when Miss Snevellicci was called for at the end of the play, Nicholas led her on, and divided the applause.", "요컨대, 새 작품도 새 배우도 완벽한 성공이었어. 그리고 연극이 끝나고 스네벨리치 양이 무대로 불려 나오자, 니콜라스가 그녀를 이끌고 나와, 박수갈채를 함께 나눴지."]], [["# CHAPTER 25", "# CHAPTER 25"]], [["Concerning a young Lady from London, who joins the Company, and an elderly Admirer who follows in her Train; with an affecting Ceremony consequent on their Arrival", "런던에서 온 젊은 아가씨가 극단에 합류하는 일과, 그녀를 따라 함께 온 나이 든 흠모자에 관한 이야기. 그리고 두 사람이 도착한 뒤 벌어진 감동적인 예식에 관하여"]], [["The new piece being a decided hit, was announced for every evening of performance until further notice, and the evenings when the theatre was closed, were reduced from three in the week to two.", "새 작품이 확실한 흥행작으로 떠오르면서, 추후 공지가 있을 때까지 공연하는 저녁마다 무대에 올린다고 알려졌어. 그리고 극장 문을 닫는 저녁도 일주일에 사흘에서 이틀로 줄었지."], ["Nor were these the only tokens of extraordinary success; for, on the succeeding Saturday, Nicholas received, by favour of the indefatigable Mrs.", "엄청난 성공의 표시는 이것만이 아니었어. 다음 토요일에 니콜라스는 지칠 줄 모르는 그러든 부인의 호의로 무려 삼십 실링이나 되는 돈을 받았거든."], ["Grudden, no less a sum than thirty shillings; besides which substantial reward, he enjoyed considerable fame and honour: having a presentation copy of Mr.", "이 두둑한 보상 말고도 그는 적잖은 명성과 영예까지 누렸어. 컬들 씨의 소책자 증정본이 극장으로 보내져 왔는데, 속표지에 그 신사가 직접 쓴 사인이 적혀 있었지."], ["Curdle’s pamphlet forwarded to the theatre, with that gentleman’s own autograph (in itself an inestimable treasure) on the fly-leaf, accompanied with a note, containing many expressions of approval, and an unsolicited assurance that Mr.", "그 자체로 값을 매길 수 없는 보물이었어. 거기에는 칭찬의 말을 잔뜩 담은 쪽지도 함께 들어 있었어."], ["Curdle would be very happy to read Shakespeare to him for three hours every morning before breakfast during his stay in the town.", "게다가 청하지도 않았는데, 니콜라스가 이 도시에 머무는 동안 매일 아침 식사 전 세 시간씩 셰익스피어를 읽어 주면 정말 기쁘겠다는 다짐까지 적혀 있었지."]], [["‘I’ve got another novelty, Johnson,’ said Mr.", "\"또 새로운 게 생겼어, 존슨.\""], ["Crummles one morning in great glee.", "어느 날 아침 크럼믈스 씨가 잔뜩 신이 나서 말했어."]], [["‘What’s that?’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"그게 뭔데요?\" 니콜라스가 되물었어."], ["‘The pony?’", "\"조랑말인가요?\""]], [["‘No, no, we never come to the pony till everything else has failed,’ said Mr.", "\"아니, 아니. 조랑말은 다른 게 다 안 통할 때나 꺼내 드는 거야.\""], ["Crummles.", "크럼믈스 씨가 말했어."], ["‘I don’t think we shall come to the pony at all, this season.", "\"이번 시즌엔 아예 조랑말까지 갈 일이 없을 것 같아."], ["No, no, not the pony.’", "아니, 아니, 조랑말은 아니야.\""]], [["‘A boy phenomenon, perhaps?’ suggested Nicholas.", "\"그럼 신동 소년인가요?\" 니콜라스가 짐작해 봤어."]], [["‘There is only one phenomenon, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"신동은 하나뿐이야, 자네.\" 크럼믈스 씨가 묵직하게 대답했어."], ["Crummles impressively, ‘and that’s a girl.’", "\"그리고 그건 여자아이지.\""]], [["‘Very true,’ said Nicholas.", "\"맞아요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘I beg your pardon.", "\"죄송합니다."], ["Then I don’t know what it is, I am sure.’", "그럼 정말 뭔지 모르겠는데요.\""]], [["‘What should you say to a young lady from London?’ inquired Mr.", "\"런던에서 온 젊은 아가씨라면 어떤가?\" 크럼믈스 씨가 물었어."], ["Crummles. ‘Miss So-and-so, of the Theatre Royal, Drury Lane?’", "\"드루어리 레인 왕립 극장의 아무개 양 말이야.\""]], [["‘I should say she would look very well in the bills,’ said Nicholas.", "\"전단지에 아주 보기 좋겠는데요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘You’re about right there,’ said Mr.", "\"제법 맞췄어.\" 크럼믈스 씨가 말했어."], ["Crummles; ‘and if you had said she would look very well upon the stage too, you wouldn’t have been far out.", "\"거기다 무대에서도 아주 보기 좋을 거라고 했으면, 그것도 틀린 말은 아니었을 거야."], ["Look here; what do you think of this?’", "자, 여기. 이걸 어떻게 생각해?\""]], [["With this inquiry Mr.", "이렇게 물으면서 크럼믈스 씨는 빨간 포스터, 파란 포스터, 노란 포스터를 차례로 펼쳐 보였어."], ["Crummles unfolded a red poster, and a blue poster, and a yellow poster, at the top of each of which public notification was inscribed in enormous characters--‘First appearance of the unrivalled Miss Petowker of the Theatre Royal, Drury Lane!’", "저마다 맨 위에는 커다란 글자로 이렇게 알리는 문구가 박혀 있었지. \"드루어리 레인 왕립 극장의 견줄 데 없는 페토커 양, 첫 출연!\""]], [["‘Dear me!’ said Nicholas, ‘I know that lady.’", "\"이런!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"저 아가씨를 제가 알아요.\""]], [["‘Then you are acquainted with as much talent as was ever compressed into one young person’s body,’ retorted Mr.", "\"그렇다면 자네는 한 젊은 사람 몸뚱이에 꽉꽉 눌러 담긴, 둘도 없는 재능을 알고 있는 셈이지.\""], ["Crummles, rolling up the bills again; ‘that is, talent of a certain sort--of a certain sort.", "크럼믈스 씨가 포스터를 다시 둘둘 말며 대꾸했어. \"그러니까 어떤 종류의 재능 말이야."], ["“The Blood Drinker,”’ added Mr.", "어떤 종류의 재능. '피를 마시는 자.'\""], ["Crummles with a prophetic sigh, ‘“The Blood Drinker”", "크럼믈스 씨가 예언이라도 하듯 한숨을 쉬며 덧붙였어."], ["will die with that girl; and she’s the only sylph I ever saw, who could stand upon one leg, and play the tambourine on her other knee, LIKE a sylph.’", "\"'피를 마시는 자'는 저 아가씨와 함께 죽고 말 거야. 그리고 저 애만큼 한쪽 다리로 서서 다른 쪽 무릎에 탬버린을 치며, 그것도 요정처럼 해내는 요정은 내 평생 본 적이 없어.\""]], [["‘When does she come down?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"언제 내려온대요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘We expect her today,’ replied Mr.", "\"오늘쯤 올 거야.\" 크럼믈스 씨가 대답했어."], ["Crummles. ‘She is an old friend of Mrs Crummles’s.", "\"크럼믈스 부인의 오랜 친구거든."], ["Mrs. Crummles saw what she could do--always knew it from the first.", "크럼믈스 부인이 저 애 솜씨를 알아봤지."], ["She taught her, indeed, nearly all she knows.", "처음부터 죽 알고 있었어. 사실 저 애가 아는 건 거의 다 부인이 가르쳐 준 거야."], ["Mrs. Crummles was the original Blood Drinker.’", "크럼믈스 부인이 바로 원조 '피를 마시는 자'였거든.\""]], [["‘Was she, indeed?’", "\"그래요?\""]], [["‘Yes.", "\"그래."], ["She was obliged to give it up though.’", "하지만 그만둘 수밖에 없었지.\""]], [["‘Did it disagree with her?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"부인한테 안 맞았나요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘Not so much with her, as with her audiences,’ replied Mr.", "\"부인보다는 관객들한테 안 맞았어.\" 크럼믈스 씨가 대답했어."], ["Crummles. ‘Nobody could stand it.", "\"아무도 견뎌 내질 못했거든."], ["It was too tremendous.", "너무 어마어마했지."], ["You don’t quite know what Mrs. Crummles is yet.’", "자네는 아직 크럼믈스 부인이 어떤 사람인지 잘 몰라.\""]], [["Nicholas ventured to insinuate that he thought he did.", "니콜라스는 자기는 안다고 생각하는데, 하고 슬쩍 끼워 넣어 봤어."]], [["‘No, no, you don’t,’ said Mr.", "\"아니, 아니, 자네는 몰라.\""], ["Crummles; ‘you don’t, indeed.", "크럼믈스 씨가 말했어. \"정말 몰라."], ["I don’t, and that’s a fact.", "나도 몰라, 그게 사실이야."], ["I don’t think her country will, till she is dead.", "부인이 죽기 전까지는 이 나라도 모를걸."], ["Some new proof of talent bursts from that astonishing woman every year of her life.", "저 놀라운 여자한테선 살아온 해마다 무슨 새로운 재능이 터져 나온다니까. 저 사람을 봐."], ["Look at her--mother of six children--three of ‘em alive, and all upon the stage!’", "여섯 아이의 어머니야. 그 가운데 셋이 살아 있고, 다 무대에 서고 있지!\""]], [["‘Extraordinary!’ cried Nicholas.", "\"대단하네요!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["extraordinary indeed,’ rejoined Mr.", "정말 대단하지.\""], ["Crummles, taking a complacent pinch of snuff, and shaking his head gravely.", "크럼믈스 씨가 흐뭇하게 코담배를 한 줌 집으며, 진지하게 고개를 끄덕이며 대꾸했어."], ["‘I pledge you my professional word I didn’t even know she could dance, till her last benefit, and then she played Juliet, and Helen Macgregor, and did the skipping-rope hornpipe between the pieces.", "\"직업인의 명예를 걸고 말하는데, 부인이 마지막 후원 공연을 하기 전까진 춤도 출 줄 아는지 몰랐어. 그때 부인이 줄리엣도 하고 헬렌 맥그리거도 하더니, 막간에는 줄넘기 혼파이프까지 추더라고."], ["The very first time I saw that admirable woman, Johnson,’ said Mr.", "내가 그 훌륭한 여자를 처음 봤을 때는 말이야, 존슨.\""], ["Crummles, drawing a little nearer, and speaking in the tone of confidential friendship, ‘she stood upon her head on the butt-end of a spear, surrounded with blazing fireworks.’", "크럼믈스 씨가 조금 더 가까이 다가와, 은밀한 우정을 나누는 말투로 말했어. \"활활 타오르는 불꽃놀이에 둘러싸여서, 창 자루 끝에 물구나무를 서 있었지.\""]], [["‘You astonish me!’ said Nicholas.", "\"놀라운데요!\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘SHE astonished ME!’ returned Mr.", "\"내가 놀랐다니까!\""], ["Crummles, with a very serious countenance.", "크럼믈스 씨가 아주 진지한 얼굴로 대꾸했어."], ["‘Such grace, coupled with such dignity!", "\"그렇게 우아하면서도 그렇게 위엄이 있다니!"], ["I adored her from that moment!’", "나는 그 순간부터 부인을 흠모했어!\""]], [["The arrival of the gifted subject of these remarks put an abrupt termination to Mr.", "이런 말들의 재능 넘치는 주인공이 도착하는 바람에 크럼믈스 씨의 찬사는 뚝 끊기고 말았어."], ["Crummles’s eulogium.", "거의 곧바로 퍼시 크럼믈스 도련님이 편지 한 통을 들고 들어왔거든."], ["Almost immediately afterwards, Master Percy Crummles entered with a letter, which had arrived by the General Post, and was directed to his gracious mother; at sight of the superscription whereof, Mrs.", "일반 우편으로 온 거였고, 자애로운 그의 어머니 앞으로 온 편지였지. 겉봉의 글씨를 보자마자 크럼믈스 부인이 외쳤어."], ["Crummles exclaimed, ‘From Henrietta Petowker, I do declare!’ and instantly became absorbed in the contents.", "\"헨리에타 페토커한테서 왔잖아, 분명히!\" 그러고는 곧장 편지 내용에 푹 빠져들었어."]], [["‘Is it--?’ inquired Mr.", "\"그게 정말―?\""], ["Crummles, hesitating.", "크럼믈스 씨가 머뭇거리며 물었어."]], [["‘Oh, yes, it’s all right,’ replied Mrs.", "\"아, 그럼요, 다 됐어요.\""], ["Crummles, anticipating the question.", "크럼믈스 부인이 질문을 앞질러 대답했어."], ["‘What an excellent thing for her, to be sure!’", "\"저 애한테는 참말로 잘된 일이지요!\""]], [["‘It’s the best thing altogether, that I ever heard of, I think,’ said Mr Crummles; and then Mr.", "\"내가 듣기로는 이게 통틀어 제일 잘된 일인 것 같소.\""], ["Crummles, Mrs.", "크럼믈스 씨가 말했어."], ["Crummles, and Master Percy Crummles, all fell to laughing violently.", "그러고는 크럼믈스 씨도, 크럼믈스 부인도, 퍼시 크럼믈스 도련님도 다 같이 요란하게 웃기 시작했어. 니콜라스는 그들끼리 즐겁게 웃도록 내버려 두고 하숙집으로 걸어갔어."], ["Nicholas left them to enjoy their mirth together, and walked to his lodgings; wondering very much what mystery connected with Miss Petowker could provoke such merriment, and pondering still more on the extreme surprise with which that lady would regard his sudden enlistment in a profession of which she was such a distinguished and brilliant ornament.", "페토커 양과 관련된 무슨 비밀이 저렇게 웃음을 자아내는 걸까 몹시 궁금했지. 그리고 그 아가씨가 이렇게나 빛나고 뛰어난 장식 같은 존재로 있는 직업에, 자기가 느닷없이 발을 들였다는 걸 알면 얼마나 놀랄까 하는 생각에 더 골똘히 잠겼어."]], [["But, in this latter respect he was mistaken; for--whether Mr.", "하지만 이 뒤쪽 일에 대해서는 그가 잘못 짚었어."], ["Vincent Crummles had paved the way, or Miss Petowker had some special reason for treating him with even more than her usual amiability--their meeting at the theatre next day was more like that of two dear friends who had been inseparable from infancy, than a recognition passing between a lady and gentleman who had only met some half-dozen times, and then by mere chance.", "빈센트 크럼믈스 씨가 미리 길을 닦아 둔 건지, 아니면 페토커 양이 평소보다도 더 다정하게 그를 대할 무슨 특별한 이유가 있었던 건지, 어쨌든 이튿날 극장에서 두 사람이 만난 모습은 그저 여섯 번쯤, 그것도 우연히 마주쳤을 뿐인 신사 숙녀의 인사라기보다는, 갓난아기 때부터 떨어진 적 없는 절친한 두 친구의 만남 같았거든."], ["Nay, Miss Petowker even whispered that she had wholly dropped the Kenwigses in her conversations with the manager’s family, and had represented herself as having encountered Mr.", "아니, 페토커 양은 단장 식구들과 이야기할 때 켄위그스네 식구 얘기는 통째로 빼 버렸다고, 그리고 자기가 존슨 씨를 처음부터 가장 세련된 사교계에서 만난 양 둘러댔다고 귓속말까지 했어."], ["Johnson in the very first and most fashionable circles; and on Nicholas receiving this intelligence with unfeigned surprise, she added, with a sweet glance, that she had a claim on his good nature now, and might tax it before long.", "니콜라스가 진짜로 놀라서 이 말을 듣자, 그녀는 달콤한 눈길을 보내며 이제 자기가 그의 너그러움에 기댈 일이 있다고, 머지않아 그 신세를 좀 질지도 모른다고 덧붙였어."]], [["Nicholas had the honour of playing in a slight piece with Miss Petowker that night, and could not but observe that the warmth of her reception was mainly attributable to a most persevering umbrella in the upper boxes; he saw, too, that the enchanting actress cast many sweet looks towards the quarter whence these sounds proceeded; and that every time she did so, the umbrella broke out afresh. Once, he thought that a peculiarly shaped hat in the same corner was not wholly unknown to him; but, being occupied with his share of the stage business, he bestowed no great attention upon this circumstance, and it had quite vanished from his memory by the time he reached home.", "그날 밤 니콜라스는 페토커 양과 함께 가벼운 작품에 출연하는 영광을 누렸어. 그런데 그녀가 그렇게 뜨거운 환영을 받은 게 주로 위쪽 박스석에서 끈질기게 두드려 대는 우산 하나 덕분이라는 걸 알아챌 수밖에 없었지. 게다가 그 매혹적인 여배우가 그 소리가 나는 쪽으로 달콤한 눈길을 잔뜩 보낸다는 것도 봤어. 그리고 그녀가 그럴 때마다 그 우산이 다시 요란하게 두드려 댄다는 것도 봤지. 한번은 그 구석에 있는 유난히 묘하게 생긴 모자가 어쩐지 낯설지 않다는 생각이 들었어. 하지만 자기 무대 일에 정신이 팔려 있던 터라 그 일에 크게 마음을 두지 않았고, 집에 닿을 무렵엔 기억에서 완전히 사라지고 말았지."]], [["He had just sat down to supper with Smike, when one of the people of the house came outside the door, and announced that a gentleman below stairs wished to speak to Mr. Johnson.", "그가 스마이크와 막 저녁을 먹으려고 앉았을 때, 하숙집 사람 하나가 문밖에 와서 아래층에 어떤 신사가 존슨 씨와 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한다고 알렸어."]], [["‘Well, if he does, you must tell him to come up; that’s all I know,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"그래요, 그렇다면 올라오시라고 하세요. 내가 아는 건 그게 다예요.\""], ["‘One of our hungry brethren, I suppose, Smike.’", "니콜라스가 대답했어. \"배곯는 우리 동료 중 하나겠지, 스마이크.\""]], [["His fellow-lodger looked at the cold meat in silent calculation of the quantity that would be left for dinner next day, and put back a slice he had cut for himself, in order that the visitor’s encroachments might be less formidable in their effects.", "함께 사는 그 친구는 차가운 고기를 물끄러미 바라보며, 내일 저녁거리로 얼마나 남겨질지 속으로 가늠해 봤어. 그러고는 손님이 헤집어 놓을 피해가 좀 덜하도록, 제 몫으로 베어 두었던 고기 한 조각을 도로 내려놓았지."]], [["‘It is not anybody who has been here before,’ said Nicholas, ‘for he is tumbling up every stair.", "\"전에 여기 왔던 사람은 아니야.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["Come in, come in.", "\"계단마다 비틀거리며 올라오는 걸 보니 말이야."], ["In the name of wonder!", "들어오세요, 들어오세요. 세상에, 이게 누구야!"], ["Mr Lillyvick?’", "릴리빅 씨?\""]], [["It was, indeed, the collector of water-rates who, regarding Nicholas with a fixed look and immovable countenance, shook hands with most portentous solemnity, and sat himself down in a seat by the chimney-corner.", "정말로 수도세 징수원이었어. 그는 니콜라스를 뚫어지게 바라보며 꼼짝 않는 얼굴로, 무슨 큰일이라도 난 듯 엄숙하게 악수를 하고는 굴뚝 옆 자리에 앉았어."]], [["‘Why, when did you come here?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"아니, 언제 여기 오셨어요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘This morning, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"오늘 아침에 왔지, 자네.\""], ["Lillyvick.", "릴리빅 씨가 대답했어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["I see; then you were at the theatre tonight, and it was your umb--’", "알겠어요. 그럼 오늘 밤 극장에 계셨던 거고, 그 우산이―\""]], [["‘This umbrella,’ said Mr.", "\"이 우산이지.\""], ["Lillyvick, producing a fat green cotton one with a battered ferrule.", "릴리빅 씨가 끝이 찌그러진, 두툼한 초록색 무명 우산을 꺼내 보이며 말했어."], ["‘What did you think of that performance?’", "\"그 공연을 어떻게 봤나?\""]], [["‘So far as I could judge, being on the stage,’ replied Nicholas, ‘I thought it very agreeable.’", "\"무대에 서 있어서 제대로 판단하긴 어려웠지만.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"아주 보기 좋았어요.\""]], [["‘Agreeable!’ cried the collector.", "\"보기 좋았다고!\" 징수원이 외쳤어."], ["‘I mean to say, sir, that it was delicious.’", "\"내 말은, 자네, 그건 황홀했다는 거야.\""]], [["Mr.", "릴리빅 씨는 마지막 단어를 더 힘주어 말하려고 몸을 앞으로 숙였어."], ["Lillyvick bent forward to pronounce the last word with greater emphasis; and having done so, drew himself up, and frowned and nodded a great many times.", "그러고는 몸을 다시 곧추세우더니 인상을 찌푸리고 여러 번 고개를 끄덕였지."]], [["‘I say, delicious,’ repeated Mr. Lillyvick.", "\"황홀했어, 내 말은.\""], ["‘Absorbing, fairy-like, toomultuous,’ and again Mr.", "릴리빅 씨가 되풀이했어. \"넋을 빼놓고, 요정 같고, 가슴 벅찼지.\""], ["Lillyvick drew himself up, and again he frowned and nodded.", "그러고는 릴리빅 씨가 다시 몸을 곧추세우더니, 또 인상을 찌푸리고 고개를 끄덕였어."]], [["‘Ah!’ said Nicholas, a little surprised at these symptoms of ecstatic approbation.", "\"아!\" 니콜라스는 이 황홀한 칭찬의 기미에 조금 놀라며 말했어."], ["‘Yes--she is a clever girl.’", "\"네, 영리한 아가씨예요.\""]], [["‘She is a divinity,’ returned Mr. Lillyvick, giving a collector’s double knock on the ground with the umbrella before-mentioned. ‘I have known divine actresses before now, sir, I used to collect--at least I used to CALL for--and very often call for--the water-rate at the house of a divine actress, who lived in my beat for upwards of four year but never--no, never, sir of all divine creatures, actresses or no actresses, did I see a diviner one than is Henrietta Petowker.’", "\"여신이지.\" 릴리빅 씨가 앞서 말한 그 우산으로 바닥을 두 번 똑똑 두드리며 대꾸했어. 징수원다운 두드림이었지. \"나도 전에 여신 같은 여배우들을 알았어, 자네. 옛날에 수도세를 거두러―적어도 받으러 다녔거든. 아주 자주 받으러 갔지. 한 여신 같은 여배우 집에 말이야. 사 년 넘게 내 구역에 살았던 여자였어. 하지만 내 평생, 아니, 단 한 번도, 여신 같은 존재 가운데, 여배우든 아니든 간에, 헨리에타 페토커보다 더 여신 같은 사람은 본 적이 없어.\""]], [["Nicholas had much ado to prevent himself from laughing; not trusting himself to speak, he merely nodded in accordance with Mr.", "니콜라스는 웃음이 터져 나오는 걸 참느라 무진 애를 썼어."], ["Lillyvick’s nods, and remained silent.", "입을 열 자신이 없어서, 그저 릴리빅 씨의 끄덕임에 맞춰 같이 고개만 끄덕이며 입을 다물고 있었지."]], [["‘Let me speak a word with you in private,’ said Mr.", "\"자네하고 단둘이 할 얘기가 있어.\""], ["Lillyvick.", "릴리빅 씨가 말했어."]], [["Nicholas looked good-humouredly at Smike, who, taking the hint, disappeared.", "니콜라스가 스마이크를 향해 싱긋 웃자, 스마이크는 눈치를 채고 사라졌어."]], [["‘A bachelor is a miserable wretch, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"독신 남자란 비참한 신세야, 자네.\""], ["Lillyvick.", "릴리빅 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Is he?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"그래요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘He is,’ rejoined the collector.", "\"그렇지.\" 징수원이 대꾸했어."], ["‘I have lived in the world for nigh sixty year, and I ought to know what it is.’", "\"내가 이 세상을 육십 년 가까이 살아 봤으니, 그게 어떤 건지 알 만하지 않겠나.\""]], [["‘You OUGHT to know, certainly,’ thought Nicholas; ‘but whether you do or not, is another question.’", "'아는 게 당연하긴 하죠.' 니콜라스는 속으로 생각했어. '하지만 진짜 아시는지 어떤지는 또 다른 문제고요.'"]], [["‘If a bachelor happens to have saved a little matter of money,’ said Mr Lillyvick, ‘his sisters and brothers, and nephews and nieces, look TO that money, and not to him; even if, by being a public character, he is the head of the family, or, as it may be, the main from which all the other little branches are turned on, they still wish him dead all the while, and get low-spirited every time they see him looking in good health, because they want to come into his little property. You see that?’", "\"독신 남자가 어쩌다 돈푼이라도 좀 모아 두면 말이야.\" 릴리빅 씨가 말했어. \"그 형제자매며 조카들이 죄다 그 남자가 아니라 그 돈만 쳐다본다니까. 공인이라서 집안의 가장이라거나, 말하자면 다른 자잘한 가지들이 죄다 거기서 뻗어 나오는 원줄기라 해도, 그래도 그자들은 늘 그 남자가 죽기를 바란단 말이야. 그 남자가 정정해 보일 때마다 풀이 죽고. 그 쥐꼬리만 한 재산을 차지하고 싶어서지. 알겠나?\""]], [["‘Oh yes,’ replied Nicholas: ‘it’s very true, no doubt.’", "\"아, 네.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"정말 그렇겠네요, 틀림없이.\""]], [["‘The great reason for not being married,’ resumed Mr. Lillyvick, ‘is the expense; that’s what’s kept me off, or else--Lord!’ said Mr. Lillyvick, snapping his fingers, ‘I might have had fifty women.’", "\"결혼을 안 하는 가장 큰 이유는 말이야.\" 릴리빅 씨가 말을 이었어. \"돈이 드는 거야. 그게 날 막아 온 거지. 안 그랬으면―어휴!\" 릴리빅 씨가 손가락을 딱 튕기며 말했어. \"여자 쉰 명은 거뜬했을걸.\""]], [["‘Fine women?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"멋진 여자들로요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘Fine women, sir!’ replied the collector; ‘ay! not so fine as Henrietta Petowker, for she is an uncommon specimen, but such women as don’t fall into every man’s way, I can tell you. Now suppose a man can get a fortune IN a wife instead of with her--eh?’", "\"멋진 여자들이지, 자네!\" 징수원이 대답했어. \"암! 헨리에타 페토커만큼 멋지진 않지. 그 여자는 흔치 않은 보배니까. 하지만 아무 남자한테나 굴러오는 그런 여자들은 아니었다고, 내 말해 두지. 자, 한 남자가 여자한테 딸려 오는 재산이 아니라, 여자 자체가 재산인 여자를 얻을 수 있다고 쳐 봐. 응?\""]], [["‘Why, then, he’s a lucky fellow,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"그야, 그럼 그 남자는 운 좋은 사람이죠.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘That’s what I say,’ retorted the collector, patting him benignantly on the side of the head with his umbrella; ‘just what I say. Henrietta Petowker, the talented Henrietta Petowker has a fortune in herself, and I am going to--’", "\"내 말이 그거야.\" 징수원이 우산으로 그의 머리통 옆을 다정하게 톡톡 두드리며 대꾸했어. \"딱 내 말이 그거라고. 헨리에타 페토커, 그 재능 넘치는 헨리에타 페토커는 그 자체가 재산이야. 그래서 내가 그 여자를―\""]], [["‘To make her Mrs.", "\"릴리빅 부인으로 만드시려고요?\""], ["Lillyvick?’ suggested Nicholas.", "니콜라스가 거들었어."]], [["‘No, sir, not to make her Mrs.", "\"아니, 자네, 릴리빅 부인으로 만드는 건 아니야.\" 징수원이 대답했어."], ["Lillyvick,’ replied the collector.", "\"여배우들은 말이야, 자네, 늘 처녀 적 이름을 그대로 쓰거든. 그게 관례지."], ["‘Actresses, sir, always keep their maiden names--that’s the regular thing--but I’m going to marry her; and the day after tomorrow, too.’", "하지만 어쨌든 난 그 여자와 결혼할 거야. 그것도 모레.\""]], [["‘I congratulate you, sir,’ said Nicholas.", "\"축하드립니다, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Thank you, sir,’ replied the collector, buttoning his waistcoat. ‘I shall draw her salary, of course, and I hope after all that it’s nearly as cheap to keep two as it is to keep one; that’s a consolation.’", "\"고맙네, 자네.\" 징수원이 조끼 단추를 채우며 대답했어. \"물론 그 여자 급료는 내가 받아 챙길 거야. 그리고 어쨌든 둘을 건사하는 게 하나를 건사하는 거랑 얼추 비슷하게 싸게 먹히면 좋겠어. 그게 위안거리지.\""]], [["‘Surely you don’t want any consolation at such a moment?’ observed Nicholas.", "\"설마 이런 순간에 무슨 위안 같은 게 필요하신 건 아니죠?\" 니콜라스가 한마디 했어."]], [["‘No,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니.\""], ["Lillyvick, shaking his head nervously: ‘no--of course not.’", "릴리빅 씨가 초조하게 고개를 저으며 대답했어. \"아니―당연히 아니지.\""]], [["‘But how come you both here, if you’re going to be married, Mr Lillyvick?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"그런데 결혼을 하실 거라면, 어떻게 두 분이 다 여기 와 계신 거예요, 릴리빅 씨?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘Why, that’s what I came to explain to you,’ replied the collector of water-rate.", "\"아, 그걸 자네한테 설명하러 온 거야.\" 수도세 징수원이 대답했어."], ["‘The fact is, we have thought it best to keep it secret from the family.’", "\"사실은 말이야, 우리가 식구들한테는 비밀로 하는 게 제일 낫겠다고 생각했거든.\""]], [["‘Family!’ said Nicholas.", "\"식구요!\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘What family?’", "\"무슨 식구요?\""]], [["‘The Kenwigses of course,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"당연히 켄위그스네지.\" 릴리빅 씨가 대꾸했어."], ["Lillyvick.", "\"내가 떠나오기 전에 조카딸이랑 애들이 이 일을 한마디라도 알았더라면, 내 발치에 쓰러져 발작을 일으켰을 거고, 아무하고도 결혼 안 하겠다고 내가 맹세할 때까지 발작에서 안 깨어났을 거야."], ["‘If my niece and the children had known a word about it before I came away, they’d have gone into fits at my feet, and never have come out of ‘em till I took an oath not to marry anybody--or they’d have got out a commission of lunacy, or some dreadful thing,’ said the collector, quite trembling as he spoke.", "아니면 정신 이상자라고 위원회라도 꾸려서 무슨 끔찍한 짓을 벌였겠지.\" 징수원이 말하면서 부들부들 떨었어."]], [["‘To be sure,’ said Nicholas.", "\"그렇겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘Yes; they would have been jealous, no doubt.’", "\"네, 분명 샘을 냈을 거예요, 틀림없이.\""]], [["‘To prevent which,’ said Mr.", "\"그걸 막으려고 말이야.\" 릴리빅 씨가 말했어."], ["Lillyvick, ‘Henrietta Petowker (it was settled between us) should come down here to her friends, the Crummleses, under pretence of this engagement, and I should go down to Guildford the day before, and join her on the coach there, which I did, and we came down from Guildford yesterday together.", "\"헨리에타 페토커가―우리끼리 그렇게 정한 거지―이 출연을 핑계로 자기 친구들인 크럼믈스네한테 먼저 내려오고, 나는 그 전날 길퍼드로 내려가서 거기서 마차에 같이 타기로 했어. 그렇게 했지."], ["Now, for fear you should be writing to Mr.", "그래서 우리는 어제 길퍼드에서 함께 내려온 거야. 자, 혹시 자네가 노그스 씨한테 편지를 써서 우리 얘기를 할까 봐, 자네한테 이 비밀을 알려 두는 게 제일 낫겠다 싶었어."], ["Noggs, and might say anything about us, we have thought it best to let you into the secret.", "우린 크럼믈스네 하숙집에서 식을 올릴 거야. 자네가 와 주면 정말 기쁘겠어."], ["We shall be married from the Crummleses’ lodgings, and shall be delighted to see you--either before church or at breakfast-time, which you like.", "식 전이든 아침 식사 때든, 자네 좋을 대로. 돈이 많이 드는 건 아니야, 알지.\""], ["It won’t be expensive, you know,’ said the collector, highly anxious to prevent any misunderstanding on this point; ‘just muffins and coffee, with perhaps a shrimp or something of that sort for a relish, you know.’", "징수원이 이 점에 대해 행여 오해가 있을까 봐 몹시 안달하며 말했어. \"그냥 머핀에 커피, 그리고 입맛 돋우게 새우 한 마리쯤 곁들이는 정도지, 알겠나.\""]], [["‘Yes, yes, I understand,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"네, 네, 알겠어요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘Oh, I shall be most happy to come; it will give me the greatest pleasure.", "\"아, 기꺼이 가야죠. 더없이 기쁜 일인걸요."], ["Where’s the lady stopping--with Mrs.", "그 아가씨는 어디 묵고 있어요?"], ["Crummles?’", "크럼믈스 부인하고요?\""]], [["‘Why, no,’ said the collector; ‘they couldn’t very well dispose of her at night, and so she is staying with an acquaintance of hers, and another young lady; they both belong to the theatre.’", "\"아니, 그건 아니야.\" 징수원이 말했어. \"밤에 그 여자를 재울 데가 마땅찮아서, 자기 아는 사람하고 다른 젊은 아가씨하고 같이 지내고 있어. 둘 다 극단 사람이지.\""]], [["‘Miss Snevellicci, I suppose?’ said Nicholas.", "\"스네벨리치 양 말이군요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Yes, that’s the name.’", "\"그래, 그 이름이야.\""]], [["‘And they’ll be bridesmaids, I presume?’ said Nicholas.", "\"그럼 그 사람들이 신부 들러리를 서겠네요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Why,’ said the collector, with a rueful face, ‘they WILL have four bridesmaids; I’m afraid they’ll make it rather theatrical.’", "\"그게 말이야.\" 징수원이 울상을 지으며 말했어. \"들러리를 넷이나 세우겠다는 거야. 아무래도 일이 좀 연극처럼 되어 버릴까 봐 걱정이야.\""]], [["‘Oh no, not at all,’ replied Nicholas, with an awkward attempt to convert a laugh into a cough.", "\"아, 아니에요, 전혀요.\" 니콜라스가 웃음을 헛기침으로 바꾸려 어설프게 애쓰며 대답했어."], ["‘Who may the four be?", "\"그 넷이 누군데요?"], ["Miss Snevellicci of course--Miss Ledrook--’", "당연히 스네벨리치 양이고―레드룩 양이고―\""]], [["‘The--the phenomenon,’ groaned the collector.", "\"그―그 신동이지.\" 징수원이 끙 앓는 소리를 냈어."]], [["‘Ha, ha!’ cried Nicholas.", "\"하하!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어."], ["‘I beg your pardon, I don’t know what I’m laughing at--yes, that’ll be very pretty--the phenomenon--who else?’", "\"죄송해요, 제가 왜 웃는지도 모르겠네요―네, 그거 아주 예쁘겠어요―그 신동이라―또 누구죠?\""]], [["‘Some young woman or other,’ replied the collector, rising; ‘some other friend of Henrietta Petowker’s.", "\"무슨 젊은 여자 하나 더 있어.\" 징수원이 일어서며 대답했어."], ["Well, you’ll be careful not to say anything about it, will you?’", "\"헨리에타 페토커의 또 다른 친구라더군. 자, 이 일에 대해 아무 말도 안 하게 조심해 주겠지, 응?\""]], [["‘You may safely depend upon me,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"믿으셔도 돼요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘Won’t you take anything to eat or drink?’", "\"뭐 좀 드시거나 마시지 않으실래요?\""]], [["‘No,’ said the collector; ‘I haven’t any appetite.", "\"아니.\" 징수원이 말했어."], ["I should think it was a very pleasant life, the married one, eh?’", "\"입맛이 없어. 결혼 생활이란 게 아주 즐거운 거겠지, 응?\""]], [["‘I have not the least doubt of it,’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"조금도 의심할 여지가 없죠.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Yes,’ said the collector; ‘certainly.", "\"그래.\" 징수원이 말했어."], ["Oh yes.", "\"그렇고말고."], ["No doubt.", "아, 그럼."], ["Good night.’", "틀림없지. 잘 자게.\""]], [["With these words, Mr. Lillyvick, whose manner had exhibited through the whole of this interview a most extraordinary compound of precipitation, hesitation, confidence and doubt, fondness, misgiving, meanness, and self-importance, turned his back upon the room, and left Nicholas to enjoy a laugh by himself if he felt so disposed.", "이 말과 함께 릴리빅 씨는―이 면담 내내 서두름과 망설임, 자신감과 의심, 애정과 불안, 인색함과 거드름이 뒤섞인 더없이 별난 모습을 보여 주었지―방에 등을 돌리고 나가, 니콜라스가 내키면 혼자 실컷 웃도록 내버려 두었어."]], [["Without stopping to inquire whether the intervening day appeared to Nicholas to consist of the usual number of hours of the ordinary length, it may be remarked that, to the parties more directly interested in the forthcoming ceremony, it passed with great rapidity, insomuch that when Miss Petowker awoke on the succeeding morning in the chamber of Miss Snevellicci, she declared that nothing should ever persuade her that that really was the day which was to behold a change in her condition.", "그 사이에 낀 하루가 니콜라스에게 여느 때처럼 평범한 길이의 시간들로 채워진 것처럼 느껴졌는지 어떤지는 굳이 따져 묻지 않더라도, 곧 다가올 예식에 더 직접 얽혀 있는 사람들에게는 그날이 무척이나 빠르게 흘러갔다는 것만은 짚어 둘 만해. 그래서 이튿날 아침 페토커 양이 스네벨리치 양의 방에서 눈을 떴을 때, 그녀는 오늘이 정말로 자기 처지가 바뀌는 그날이라는 걸 무슨 수로도 믿을 수 없다고 선언했지."]], [["‘I never will believe it,’ said Miss Petowker; ‘I cannot really. It’s of no use talking, I never can make up my mind to go through with such a trial!’", "\"난 절대로 못 믿겠어.\" 페토커 양이 말했어. \"정말로 못 믿겠다고. 말해 봐야 소용없어. 이런 시련을 끝까지 겪어 낼 마음이 도무지 안 잡혀!\""]], [["On hearing this, Miss Snevellicci and Miss Ledrook, who knew perfectly well that their fair friend’s mind had been made up for three or four years, at any period of which time she would have cheerfully undergone the desperate trial now approaching if she could have found any eligible gentleman disposed for the venture, began to preach comfort and firmness, and to say how very proud she ought to feel that it was in her power to confer lasting bliss on a deserving object, and how necessary it was for the happiness of mankind in general that women should possess fortitude and resignation on such occasions; and that although for their parts they held true happiness to consist in a single life, which they would not willingly exchange--no, not for any worldly consideration--still (thank God), if ever the time SHOULD come, they hoped they knew their duty too well to repine, but would the rather submit with meekness and humility of spirit to a fate for which Providence had clearly designed them with a view to the contentment and reward of their fellow-creatures.", "이 말을 듣고 스네벨리치 양과 레드룩 양은―두 사람은 자기네 어여쁜 친구의 마음이 이미 삼사 년 전부터 굳어져 있었고, 그동안 언제든 모험에 나설 만한 어엿한 신사를 찾을 수만 있었다면 지금 다가오는 이 절박한 시련을 기꺼이 겪어 냈으리란 걸 너무도 잘 알고 있었지―위로와 굳센 마음을 설교하기 시작했어. 그리고 자격 있는 사람에게 영원한 행복을 안겨 줄 힘이 자기한테 있다는 게 얼마나 자랑스러운 일이냐고, 이런 때에 여자가 꿋꿋함과 체념의 미덕을 갖추는 게 인류 전체의 행복을 위해 얼마나 꼭 필요한 일이냐고 했지. 그리고 자기네로 말하자면 참된 행복은 독신 생활에 있다고 여기며, 그 무엇과도 기꺼이 바꾸지 않을 거라고―그래, 세상 그 어떤 것을 준대도―그래도 (하느님 감사하게도) 행여 그런 때가 온다면, 자기네는 투덜대기엔 도리를 너무 잘 알기에, 그보다는 동료 인간들의 만족과 보답을 위해 하늘이 분명히 점지해 준 운명에 온순하고 겸손한 마음으로 따르기를 바란다고 했어."]], [["‘I might feel it was a great blow,’ said Miss Snevellicci, ‘to break up old associations and what-do-you-callems of that kind, but I would submit, my dear, I would indeed.’", "\"오랜 인연이며 그런 뭐랄까 하는 것들을 끊어 내야 한다는 게 나한테는 큰 타격일지도 몰라.\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했어. \"하지만 난 따를 거야, 얘. 정말로 따를 거라고.\""]], [["‘So would I,’ said Miss Ledrook; ‘I would rather court the yoke than shun it. I have broken hearts before now, and I’m very sorry for it: for it’s a terrible thing to reflect upon.’", "\"나도 그래.\" 레드룩 양이 말했어. \"난 그 멍에를 피하느니 차라리 자청하겠어. 전에도 남자 마음을 여럿 아프게 한 적이 있는데, 그게 참 미안해. 돌이켜 생각하면 끔찍한 일이거든.\""]], [["‘It is indeed,’ said Miss Snevellicci. ‘Now Led, my dear, we must positively get her ready, or we shall be too late, we shall indeed.’", "\"정말 그래.\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했어. \"자, 레드, 얘, 우리가 무슨 일이 있어도 얘 채비를 시켜야지. 안 그러면 너무 늦어. 정말 늦는다니까.\""]], [["This pious reasoning, and perhaps the fear of being too late, supported the bride through the ceremony of robing, after which, strong tea and brandy were administered in alternate doses as a means of strengthening her feeble limbs and causing her to walk steadier.", "이 경건한 설득과, 어쩌면 너무 늦을지 모른다는 두려움이 신부를 떠받쳐 옷 입는 예식을 견디게 해 줬어. 그러고 나서는 진한 차와 브랜디를 번갈아 가며 한 모금씩 먹였지. 후들거리는 다리에 힘을 넣어 좀 더 똑바로 걷게 하려는 거였어."]], [["‘How do you feel now, my love?’ inquired Miss Snevellicci.", "\"이제 좀 어때, 얘?\" 스네벨리치 양이 물었어."]], [["‘Oh Lillyvick!’ cried the bride.", "\"오, 릴리빅!\" 신부가 외쳤어."], ["‘If you knew what I am undergoing for you!’", "\"내가 당신을 위해 무슨 일을 겪고 있는지 안다면!\""]], [["‘Of course he knows it, love, and will never forget it,’ said Miss Ledrook.", "\"그야 당연히 그분이 알지, 얘. 절대 잊지 않으실 거야.\" 레드룩 양이 말했어."]], [["‘Do you think he won’t?’ cried Miss Petowker, really showing great capability for the stage.", "\"안 잊을 것 같아?\" 페토커 양이, 정말이지 무대에 어울리는 대단한 솜씨를 뽐내며 외쳤어."], ["‘Oh, do you think he won’t?", "\"오, 정말 안 잊을 것 같아?"], ["Do you think Lillyvick will always remember it--always, always, always?’", "릴리빅이 그걸 늘 기억할 것 같아? 늘, 늘, 늘 말이야?\""]], [["There is no knowing in what this burst of feeling might have ended, if Miss Snevellicci had not at that moment proclaimed the arrival of the fly, which so astounded the bride that she shook off divers alarming symptoms which were coming on very strong, and running to the glass adjusted her dress, and calmly declared that she was ready for the sacrifice.", "이 북받치는 감정이 어디까지 치달았을지는 알 수 없는 노릇이었어. 마침 그 순간 스네벨리치 양이 마차가 왔다고 알리지 않았더라면 말이지. 그 말에 신부는 어찌나 화들짝 놀랐던지, 잔뜩 몰려오던 갖가지 심상찮은 증세를 떨쳐 버리고는, 거울로 달려가 옷매무새를 가다듬더니, 차분하게 이 희생을 치를 채비가 됐다고 선언했어."]], [["She was accordingly supported into the coach, and there ‘kept up’ (as Miss Snevellicci said) with perpetual sniffs of SAL VOLATILE and sips of brandy and other gentle stimulants, until they reached the manager’s door, which was already opened by the two Master Crummleses, who wore white cockades, and were decorated with the choicest and most resplendent waistcoats in the theatrical wardrobe. By the combined exertions of these young gentlemen and the bridesmaids, assisted by the coachman, Miss Petowker was at length supported in a condition of much exhaustion to the first floor, where she no sooner encountered the youthful bridegroom than she fainted with great decorum.", "그래서 사람들은 그녀를 부축해 마차에 태웠어. 그 안에서도 (스네벨리치 양 말마따나) 각성 소금을 연신 들이켜고 브랜디며 다른 순한 강장제를 홀짝이게 하며 그녀를 '추스르게' 했지. 그렇게 단장의 집 문 앞에 닿았는데, 문은 벌써 두 크럼믈스 도련님이 열어 두고 있었어. 둘은 하얀 장식 매듭을 달고, 극단 의상실에서 제일 좋고 제일 화려한 조끼로 차려입었더라고. 이 두 젊은 신사와 들러리들이, 마부의 거듦까지 받아 가며 한데 힘을 모은 끝에, 페토커 양은 기진맥진한 채로 가까스로 이층까지 부축을 받아 올라갔어. 그리고 거기서 젊은 신랑과 마주치자마자, 아주 예의 바르게 까무러쳤지."]], [["‘Henrietta Petowker!’ said the collector; ‘cheer up, my lovely one.’", "\"헨리에타 페토커!\" 징수원이 말했어. \"기운 내요, 내 사랑스러운 사람.\""]], [["Miss Petowker grasped the collector’s hand, but emotion choked her utterance.", "페토커 양이 징수원의 손을 꼭 잡았지만, 벅찬 감정에 말문이 막혔어."]], [["‘Is the sight of me so dreadful, Henrietta Petowker?’ said the collector.", "\"내 모습이 그렇게 끔찍하오, 헨리에타 페토커?\" 징수원이 말했어."]], [["‘Oh no, no, no,’ rejoined the bride; ‘but all the friends--the darling friends--of my youthful days--to leave them all--it is such a shock!’", "\"오, 아니, 아니에요.\" 신부가 대꾸했어. \"하지만 그 모든 친구들―내 젊은 날의 사랑스러운 친구들―그들을 다 두고 떠난다는 게―이렇게 충격이라니!\""]], [["With such expressions of sorrow, Miss Petowker went on to enumerate the dear friends of her youthful days one by one, and to call upon such of them as were present to come and embrace her. This done, she remembered that Mrs.", "이런 슬픔의 말들과 함께, 페토커 양은 젊은 날의 다정한 친구들을 하나하나 꼽기 시작했고, 그 자리에 있는 사람들을 향해 다가와 안아 달라고 청했어."], ["Crummles had been more than a mother to her, and after that, that Mr. Crummles had been more than a father to her, and after that, that the Master Crummleses and Miss Ninetta Crummles had been more than brothers and sisters to her.", "이걸 마치고 나서는, 크럼믈스 부인이 자기에게 어머니나 다름없었다는 게 떠올랐고, 그다음엔 크럼믈스 씨가 아버지나 다름없었다는 게, 또 그다음엔 크럼믈스 도련님들과 니네타 크럼믈스 양이 형제자매나 다름없었다는 게 떠올랐지."], ["These various remembrances being each accompanied with a series of hugs, occupied a long time, and they were obliged to drive to church very fast, for fear they should be too late.", "이 갖가지 추억마다 줄줄이 포옹이 따라붙는 통에 한참이 걸렸고, 결국 너무 늦을까 봐 교회까지 마차를 아주 빨리 몰아야 했어."]], [["The procession consisted of two flys; in the first of which were Miss Bravassa (the fourth bridesmaid), Mrs.", "행렬은 마차 두 대로 이루어졌어."], ["Crummles, the collector, and Mr Folair, who had been chosen as his second on the occasion. In the other were the bride, Mr.", "첫 번째 마차에는 브라바사 양(네 번째 들러리)과 크럼믈스 부인, 징수원, 그리고 이 자리에서 신랑의 들러리로 뽑힌 폴레어 씨가 탔지."], ["Crummles, Miss Snevellicci, Miss Ledrook, and the phenomenon. The costumes were beautiful.", "다른 마차에는 신부와 크럼믈스 씨, 스네벨리치 양, 레드룩 양, 그리고 그 신동이 탔어."], ["The bridesmaids were quite covered with artificial flowers, and the phenomenon, in particular, was rendered almost invisible by the portable arbour in which she was enshrined.", "의상은 아름다웠어. 들러리들은 조화로 온통 뒤덮여 있었고, 특히 그 신동은 자기를 모셔 놓은 들고 다니는 꽃 정자에 파묻혀 거의 안 보일 지경이었지."], ["Miss Ledrook, who was of a romantic turn, wore in her breast the miniature of some field-officer unknown, which she had purchased, a great bargain, not very long before; the other ladies displayed several dazzling articles of imitative jewellery, almost equal to real, and Mrs Crummles came out in a stern and gloomy majesty, which attracted the admiration of all beholders.", "낭만적인 기질의 레드룩 양은 어느 알 수 없는 영관급 장교의 초상화를 가슴에 달았는데, 그리 오래되지 않은 때에 엄청 싼값에 사들인 거였어. 다른 숙녀들은 진짜에 거의 맞먹는 휘황찬란한 모조 장신구를 여럿 뽐냈고, 크럼믈스 부인은 엄숙하고 음울한 위엄을 풍기며 등장해 보는 이마다 감탄을 자아냈지."]], [["But, perhaps the appearance of Mr.", "하지만 일행 가운데 누구보다도 더 인상적이고 어울리는 모습을 보인 건 어쩌면 크럼믈스 씨였을 거야."], ["Crummles was more striking and appropriate than that of any member of the party.", "신부의 아버지 역을 맡은 이 신사는, 기발하고 독창적인 착상에 따라 그 역에 맞게 '분장'을 했어."], ["This gentleman, who personated the bride’s father, had, in pursuance of a happy and original conception, ‘made up’ for the part by arraying himself in a theatrical wig, of a style and pattern commonly known as a brown George, and moreover assuming a snuff-coloured suit, of the previous century, with grey silk stockings, and buckles to his shoes.", "흔히 브라운 조지라고 불리는 모양새의 연극용 가발을 쓰고, 거기다 지난 세기풍의 담배색 양복을 차려입었지. 회색 비단 양말에 신발에는 버클까지 달았더라고."], ["The better to support his assumed character he had determined to be greatly overcome, and, consequently, when they entered the church, the sobs of the affectionate parent were so heart-rending that the pew-opener suggested the propriety of his retiring to the vestry, and comforting himself with a glass of water before the ceremony began.", "자기가 맡은 인물을 한층 더 그럴듯하게 받쳐 주려고, 그는 감정에 크게 휩싸이기로 작정했어. 그래서 일행이 교회에 들어섰을 때, 이 정 많은 아버지의 흐느낌이 어찌나 가슴을 미어지게 하던지, 좌석 안내인이 식이 시작되기 전에 성구실로 물러나 물 한 잔 마시며 마음을 좀 추스르는 게 좋겠다고 권할 정도였지."]], [["The procession up the aisle was beautiful.", "통로를 따라 올라가는 행렬은 아름다웠어."], ["The bride, with the four bridesmaids, forming a group previously arranged and rehearsed; the collector, followed by his second, imitating his walk and gestures to the indescribable amusement of some theatrical friends in the gallery; Mr.", "신부는 네 명의 들러리와 함께, 미리 짜고 연습해 둔 무리를 이루었지. 징수원은 그 뒤를 들러리가 따랐는데, 위층 관람석에 있던 극단 친구 몇몇이 형언 못 할 만큼 재미있어할 정도로 징수원의 걸음걸이와 몸짓을 흉내 냈어."], ["Crummles, with an infirm and feeble gait; Mrs. Crummles advancing with that stage walk, which consists of a stride and a stop alternately--it was the completest thing ever witnessed.", "크럼믈스 씨는 약하고 비틀거리는 걸음으로, 크럼믈스 부인은 성큼 한 발 내딛고 멈추기를 번갈아 하는 그 무대용 걸음으로 나아갔지."], ["The ceremony was very quickly disposed of, and all parties present having signed the register (for which purpose, when it came to his turn, Mr.", "여태껏 본 적 없는 더없이 완벽한 장면이었어. 식은 아주 후딱 끝났고, 참석한 사람들이 모두 혼인부에 서명을 마치자(이를 위해 자기 차례가 오자 크럼믈스 씨는 어마어마하게 큰 안경을 정성껏 닦아서 꼈지), 다들 기분이 한껏 들떠 아침 식사를 하러 돌아왔어."], ["Crummles carefully wiped and put on an immense pair of spectacles), they went back to breakfast in high spirits. And here they found Nicholas awaiting their arrival.", "그리고 여기서 그들은 자기네가 오기를 기다리고 있던 니콜라스를 만났지."]], [["‘Now then,’ said Crummles, who had been assisting Mrs.", "\"자, 그럼.\" 크럼믈스가 말했어."], ["Grudden in the preparations, which were on a more extensive scale than was quite agreeable to the collector.", "그는 그러든 부인을 도와 준비를 거들고 있었는데, 그 준비가 징수원이 흡족해할 만한 수준보다 좀 더 푸짐한 규모였지."], ["‘Breakfast, breakfast.’", "\"아침 식사, 아침 식사.\""]], [["No second invitation was required.", "두 번 청할 것도 없었어."], ["The company crowded and squeezed themselves at the table as well as they could, and fell to, immediately: Miss Petowker blushing very much when anybody was looking, and eating very much when anybody was NOT looking; and Mr.", "사람들은 식탁에 되도록 빽빽이 끼어 비집고 앉아서는 곧장 음식에 달려들었지. 페토커 양은 누가 쳐다보면 얼굴을 잔뜩 붉혔고, 아무도 안 볼 때는 잔뜩 먹어 댔어."], ["Lillyvick going to work as though with the cool resolve, that since the good things must be paid for by him, he would leave as little as possible for the Crummleses to eat up afterwards.", "그리고 릴리빅 씨는, 어차피 이 맛있는 음식 값을 자기가 치러야 하니 크럼믈스네가 나중에 먹어 치울 게 되도록 적게 남게 하겠다고 작정한 듯, 냉정하게 음식에 손을 댔지."]], [["‘It’s very soon done, sir, isn’t it?’ inquired Mr.", "\"이거 금방 끝나는 거지요, 선생님, 안 그래요?\""], ["Folair of the collector, leaning over the table to address him.", "폴레어 씨가 식탁 너머로 몸을 기울여 징수원에게 물었어."]], [["‘What is soon done, sir?’ returned Mr.", "\"뭐가 금방 끝난다는 건가, 자네?\""], ["Lillyvick.", "릴리빅 씨가 되물었어."]], [["‘The tying up--the fixing oneself with a wife,’ replied Mr.", "\"매듭짓는 거―아내한테 자기를 묶는 거 말이에요.\" 폴레어 씨가 대답했어."], ["Folair. ‘It don’t take long, does it?’", "\"오래 안 걸리잖아요, 그렇죠?\""]], [["‘No, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"안 걸리지, 자네.\""], ["Lillyvick, colouring.", "릴리빅 씨가 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했어."], ["‘It does not take long.", "\"오래 안 걸려."], ["And what then, sir?’", "그래서 뭐 어쨌다는 건가, 자네?\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["nothing,’ said the actor.", "아무것도요.\" 배우가 말했어."], ["‘It don’t take a man long to hang himself, either, eh?", "\"사람이 목매다는 것도 오래 안 걸리잖아요, 안 그래요?"], ["ha, ha!’", "하하!\""]], [["Mr. Lillyvick laid down his knife and fork, and looked round the table with indignant astonishment.", "릴리빅 씨는 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고 분개한 놀라움에 차서 식탁을 둘러보았어."]], [["‘To hang himself!’ repeated Mr.", "\"목을 매단다고!\""], ["Lillyvick.", "릴리빅 씨가 되받았어."]], [["A profound silence came upon all, for Mr.", "깊은 침묵이 모두에게 내려앉았어."], ["Lillyvick was dignified beyond expression.", "릴리빅 씨가 말로 다 못 할 만큼 위엄에 차 있었거든."]], [["‘To hang himself!’ cried Mr. Lillyvick again.", "\"목을 매단다고!\""], ["‘Is any parallel attempted to be drawn in this company between matrimony and hanging?’", "릴리빅 씨가 다시 외쳤어. \"이 자리에서 결혼과 목매다는 걸 견주려는 셈인가?\""]], [["‘The noose, you know,’ said Mr.", "\"올가미 말이에요, 그러니까.\""], ["Folair, a little crest-fallen.", "폴레어 씨가 조금 풀이 죽어서 말했어."]], [["‘The noose, sir?’ retorted Mr.", "\"올가미라고, 자네?\" 릴리빅 씨가 되받았어."], ["Lillyvick. ‘Does any man dare to speak to me of a noose, and Henrietta Pe--’", "\"감히 누가 나한테, 그리고 헨리에타 페―와 한 입으로 올가미 얘기를 한단 말인가―\""]], [["‘Lillyvick,’ suggested Mr.", "\"릴리빅.\""], ["Crummles.", "크럼믈스 씨가 거들었어."]], [["‘--And Henrietta Lillyvick in the same breath?’ said the collector.", "\"―그리고 헨리에타 릴리빅을 한 입으로?\""], ["‘In this house, in the presence of Mr.", "징수원이 말했어."], ["and Mrs. Crummles, who have brought up a talented and virtuous family, to be blessings and phenomenons, and what not, are we to hear talk of nooses?’", "\"이 집에서, 재능 있고 덕망 있는 식구를 길러 내 축복이며 신동이며 뭐며 다 이뤄 낸 크럼믈스 씨 부부 앞에서, 우리가 올가미 얘기를 들어야 한단 말인가?\""]], [["‘Folair,’ said Mr.", "\"폴레어.\""], ["Crummles, deeming it a matter of decency to be affected by this allusion to himself and partner, ‘I’m astonished at you.’", "크럼믈스 씨가 자기와 아내를 빗댄 이 말에 감동받는 게 도리라 여기고 말했어. \"자네한테 놀랐네.\""]], [["‘What are you going on in this way at me for?’ urged the unfortunate actor.", "\"왜들 이렇게 나한테 몰아붙이는 거예요?\" 가엾은 배우가 따졌어."], ["‘What have I done?’", "\"내가 뭘 어쨌다고?\""]], [["‘Done, sir!’ cried Mr.", "\"어쨌느냐고, 자네!\""], ["Lillyvick, ‘aimed a blow at the whole framework of society--’", "릴리빅 씨가 외쳤어. \"사회의 골조 전체에 한 방 먹였잖나―\""]], [["‘And the best and tenderest feelings,’ added Crummles, relapsing into the old man.", "\"그리고 가장 곱고 가장 여린 감정에도.\" 크럼믈스가 다시 그 노인 역으로 빠져들며 덧붙였어."]], [["‘And the highest and most estimable of social ties,’ said the collector.", "\"그리고 가장 드높고 가장 값진 사회적 인연에도 말이야.\" 징수원이 말했어."], ["‘Noose!", "\"올가미라니!"], ["As if one was caught, trapped into the married state, pinned by the leg, instead of going into it of one’s own accord and glorying in the act!’", "제 발로 들어가서 그 일을 영광스러워하는 게 아니라, 마치 누가 덫에 걸려 결혼이라는 처지에 빠지고, 다리를 핀으로 꽂힌 것처럼 말이지!\""]], [["‘I didn’t mean to make it out, that you were caught and trapped, and pinned by the leg,’ replied the actor.", "\"덫에 걸리고 잡혀서 다리가 핀에 꽂혔다는 뜻으로 한 말은 아니에요.\" 배우가 대답했어."], ["‘I’m sorry for it; I can’t say any more.’", "\"죄송합니다. 더는 할 말이 없네요.\""]], [["‘So you ought to be, sir,’ returned Mr.", "\"그래야 마땅하지, 자네.\""], ["Lillyvick; ‘and I am glad to hear that you have enough of feeling left to be so.’", "릴리빅 씨가 대꾸했어. \"그리고 자네한테 그럴 만한 감정이 아직 남아 있다니 다행이군.\""]], [["The quarrel appearing to terminate with this reply, Mrs. Lillyvick considered that the fittest occasion (the attention of the company being no longer distracted) to burst into tears, and require the assistance of all four bridesmaids, which was immediately rendered, though not without some confusion, for the room being small and the table-cloth long, a whole detachment of plates were swept off the board at the very first move.", "이 대답으로 다툼이 끝나는 듯하자, 릴리빅 부인은 (이제 사람들의 주의가 더는 흐트러지지 않았으니) 지금이 울음을 터뜨려 네 들러리의 부축을 모두 청하기에 가장 알맞은 때라고 여겼어."], ["Regardless of this circumstance, however, Mrs.", "그 부축은 곧장 베풀어졌지만, 약간의 소동이 없진 않았지. 방은 좁고 식탁보는 긴 탓에, 첫 움직임에 접시 한 무더기가 식탁에서 쓸려 떨어졌거든."], ["Lillyvick refused to be comforted until the belligerents had passed their words that the dispute should be carried no further, which, after a sufficient show of reluctance, they did, and from that time Mr.", "하지만 그런 건 아랑곳없이, 릴리빅 부인은 다툰 두 사람이 더는 시비를 키우지 않겠다고 약속할 때까지 위로받기를 거부했어. 두 사람은 한참 마지못한 기색을 보인 뒤 그렇게 약속했지."], ["Folair sat in moody silence, contenting himself with pinching Nicholas’s leg when anything was said, and so expressing his contempt both for the speaker and the sentiments to which he gave utterance.", "그때부터 폴레어 씨는 뚱하니 입을 다물고 앉아서, 무슨 말이 나올 때마다 니콜라스의 다리를 꼬집는 걸로 만족했어. 그렇게 말하는 사람도, 그 사람이 내뱉는 소리도 둘 다 경멸한다는 뜻을 나타낸 거지."]], [["There were a great number of speeches made; some by Nicholas, and some by Crummles, and some by the collector; two by the Master Crummleses in returning thanks for themselves, and one by the phenomenon on behalf of the bridesmaids, at which Mrs. Crummles shed tears. There was some singing, too, from Miss Ledrook and Miss Bravassa, and very likely there might have been more, if the fly-driver, who stopped to drive the happy pair to the spot where they proposed to take steamboat to Ryde, had not sent in a peremptory message intimating, that if they didn’t come directly he should infallibly demand eighteen-pence over and above his agreement.", "연설이 아주 많이 나왔어. 더러는 니콜라스가, 더러는 크럼믈스가, 더러는 징수원이 했지. 크럼믈스 도련님들이 자기네 몫으로 두 번 답례 연설을 했고, 신동이 들러리들을 대표해 한 번 했는데, 거기서 크럼믈스 부인은 눈물을 흘렸어. 노래도 좀 있었지. 레드룩 양과 브라바사 양이 불렀어. 노래가 더 이어졌을지도 모를 일이었지만, 행복한 한 쌍을 라이드행 증기선을 탈 곳까지 태워다 주려고 기다리던 마차꾼이 단호한 전갈을 보내왔거든. 당장 안 나오면 약속한 삯에 일 실링 육 펜스를 더 받아 내고 말겠다는 거였지."]], [["This desperate threat effectually broke up the party.", "이 절박한 으름장에 모임은 결국 흩어지고 말았어."], ["After a most pathetic leave-taking, Mr.", "더없이 애절한 작별 인사를 마친 뒤, 릴리빅 씨와 그의 신부는 라이드로 떠났어."], ["Lillyvick and his bride departed for Ryde, where they were to spend the next two days in profound retirement, and whither they were accompanied by the infant, who had been appointed travelling bridesmaid on Mr.", "거기서 이틀 동안 깊은 칩거에 들어갈 작정이었지. 그리고 그 길에는 갓난아기도 함께 갔는데, 릴리빅 씨가 못 박아 둔 조건에 따라 여행용 들러리로 임명된 거였지."], ["Lillyvick’s express stipulation: as the steamboat people, deceived by her size, would (he had previously ascertained) transport her at half-price.", "증기선 사람들이 그 애 덩치에 속아서 반값에 태워 주리란 걸 (그가 미리 알아 두었거든) 노린 거였어."]], [["As there was no performance that night, Mr.", "그날 밤은 공연이 없어서, 크럼믈스 씨는 마실 게 다 동날 때까지 흥을 이어 가겠다고 선언했어."], ["Crummles declared his intention of keeping it up till everything to drink was disposed of; but Nicholas having to play Romeo for the first time on the ensuing evening, contrived to slip away in the midst of a temporary confusion, occasioned by the unexpected development of strong symptoms of inebriety in the conduct of Mrs. Grudden.", "하지만 니콜라스는 이튿날 저녁 처음으로 로미오를 연기해야 했기에, 그러든 부인의 행동에서 만취의 뚜렷한 징후가 느닷없이 드러나는 통에 잠깐 소란이 인 틈을 타 슬그머니 빠져나왔지."]], [["To this act of desertion he was led, not only by his own inclinations, but by his anxiety on account of Smike, who, having to sustain the character of the Apothecary, had been as yet wholly unable to get any more of the part into his head than the general idea that he was very hungry, which--perhaps from old recollections--he had acquired with great aptitude.", "이렇게 자리를 뜬 데에는 자기 마음 때문만이 아니라 스마이크에 대한 걱정도 한몫했어. 스마이크는 약제사 역을 맡아야 했는데, 아직껏 대사라곤 자기가 무척 배가 고프다는 큰 줄거리 말고는 도무지 머릿속에 넣질 못했거든. 그건 ― 어쩌면 옛 기억 덕분인지 ― 아주 척척 익혔지만 말이야."]], [["‘I don’t know what’s to be done, Smike,’ said Nicholas, laying down the book.", "\"어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠다, 스마이크.\" 니콜라스가 책을 내려놓으며 말했어."], ["‘I am afraid you can’t learn it, my poor fellow.’", "\"이걸 못 외울까 봐 걱정이야, 가엾은 친구.\""]], [["‘I am afraid not,’ said Smike, shaking his head.", "\"나도 못 외울까 봐 걱정이에요.\" 스마이크가 고개를 저으며 말했어."], ["‘I think if you--but that would give you so much trouble.’", "\"형이 만약에―그런데 그러면 형이 너무 수고스러울 텐데.\""]], [["‘What?’ inquired Nicholas.", "\"뭐?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."], ["‘Never mind me.’", "\"내 걱정은 말고.\""]], [["‘I think,’ said Smike, ‘if you were to keep saying it to me in little bits, over and over again, I should be able to recollect it from hearing you.’", "\"제 생각엔요.\" 스마이크가 말했어. \"형이 저한테 짧게 토막토막 끊어서 자꾸자꾸 되풀이해 말해 주면, 듣고서 그걸 외울 수 있을 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘Do you think so?’ exclaimed Nicholas.", "\"그럴 것 같아?\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어."], ["‘Well said.", "\"좋아."], ["Let us see who tires first.", "누가 먼저 지치나 보자."], ["Not I, Smike, trust me.", "난 안 지쳐, 스마이크, 날 믿어."], ["Now then.", "자, 그럼."], ["Who calls so loud?’", "누가 이리 큰 소리로 부르느냐?\""]], [["‘“Who calls so loud?”’ said Smike.", "\"'누가 이리 큰 소리로 부르느냐?'\" 스마이크가 말했어."]], [["‘“Who calls so loud?”’ repeated Nicholas.", "\"'누가 이리 큰 소리로 부르느냐?'\" 니콜라스가 되풀이했어."]], [["‘“Who calls so loud?”’ cried Smike.", "\"'누가 이리 큰 소리로 부르느냐?'\" 스마이크가 외쳤어."]], [["Thus they continued to ask each other who called so loud, over and over again; and when Smike had that by heart Nicholas went to another sentence, and then to two at a time, and then to three, and so on, until at midnight poor Smike found to his unspeakable joy that he really began to remember something about the text.", "이렇게 둘은 누가 이리 큰 소리로 부르느냐고 서로에게 묻기를 몇 번이고 되풀이했어. 그러다 스마이크가 그걸 다 외우자 니콜라스는 다음 문장으로 넘어갔고, 그다음엔 한 번에 두 문장, 그다음엔 세 문장, 이런 식으로 계속해 나갔지. 그러다 자정 무렵, 가엾은 스마이크는 자기가 정말로 대사를 좀 기억하기 시작했다는 걸 알고 이루 말할 수 없이 기뻐했어."]], [["Early in the morning they went to it again, and Smike, rendered more confident by the progress he had already made, got on faster and with better heart.", "이른 아침에 둘은 다시 그 일에 매달렸어. 그리고 이미 이뤄 낸 진척에 한층 자신감을 얻은 스마이크는 더 빠르게, 더 힘차게 나아갔지."], ["As soon as he began to acquire the words pretty freely, Nicholas showed him how he must come in with both hands spread out upon his stomach, and how he must occasionally rub it, in compliance with the established form by which people on the stage always denote that they want something to eat.", "그가 대사를 제법 술술 익히기 시작하자, 니콜라스는 그에게 양손을 배 위에 쫙 펴고 들어와야 한다는 것과, 가끔 배를 문질러야 한다는 것을 알려 줬어. 무대 위 사람들이 뭔가 먹고 싶다는 걸 늘 그렇게 나타내는 정해진 방식에 맞춘 거였지."], ["After the morning’s rehearsal they went to work again, nor did they stop, except for a hasty dinner, until it was time to repair to the theatre at night.", "아침 연습이 끝나고 둘은 또다시 일에 매달렸고, 부랴부랴 점심을 먹을 때 말고는 쉬지 않다가, 밤에 극장으로 갈 시간이 되어서야 그쳤어."]], [["Never had master a more anxious, humble, docile pupil.", "이보다 더 마음 졸이고, 더 겸손하고, 더 고분고분한 제자를 둔 스승은 없었어."], ["Never had pupil a more patient, unwearying, considerate, kindhearted master.", "그리고 이보다 더 참을성 있고, 더 지칠 줄 모르고, 더 사려 깊고, 더 마음 따뜻한 스승을 둔 제자도 없었지."]], [["As soon as they were dressed, and at every interval when he was not upon the stage, Nicholas renewed his instructions.", "옷을 다 차려입자마자, 그리고 무대에 서지 않는 막간마다, 니콜라스는 거듭 일러 주었어."], ["They prospered well.", "일은 잘 풀렸지."], ["The Romeo was received with hearty plaudits and unbounded favour, and Smike was pronounced unanimously, alike by audience and actors, the very prince and prodigy of Apothecaries.", "로미오는 진심 어린 갈채와 끝없는 호평을 받았어. 그리고 스마이크는 관객과 배우 모두에게 한목소리로, 약제사 중의 더없는 왕이자 신동이라는 평을 받았지."]], [["# CHAPTER 26", "# CHAPTER 26"]], [["Is fraught with some Danger to Miss Nickleby’s Peace of Mind", "니클비 양의 마음의 평온에 적잖은 위험이 닥치다"]], [["The place was a handsome suite of private apartments in Regent Street; the time was three o’clock in the afternoon to the dull and plodding, and the first hour of morning to the gay and spirited; the persons were Lord Frederick Verisopht, and his friend Sir Mulberry Hawk.", "장소는 리젠트 거리에 있는 멋진 개인 거처였어. 시간은 게으르고 굼뜬 사람들에게는 오후 세 시였지만, 흥청대고 활기찬 사람들에게는 아침 첫 시간이었지. 그곳에 있는 사람은 프레더릭 베리소프트 경과 그의 친구 멀버리 호크 경이었어."]], [["These distinguished gentlemen were reclining listlessly on a couple of sofas, with a table between them, on which were scattered in rich confusion the materials of an untasted breakfast. Newspapers lay strewn about the room, but these, like the meal, were neglected and unnoticed; not, however, because any flow of conversation prevented the attractions of the journals from being called into request, for not a word was exchanged between the two, nor was any sound uttered, save when one, in tossing about to find an easier resting-place for his aching head, uttered an exclamation of impatience, and seemed for a moment to communicate a new restlessness to his companion.", "이 점잖은 두 신사는 소파 두 개에 나른하게 기대 누워 있었어. 그 사이에는 탁자가 하나 놓여 있었는데, 손도 대지 않은 아침 식사 재료가 어수선하게 잔뜩 흩어져 있었지. 방 안에는 신문이 여기저기 널려 있었지만, 식사처럼 신문도 거들떠보지도 않고 내버려져 있었어. 그렇다고 둘 사이에 대화가 오가서 신문에 눈길 줄 틈이 없었던 것도 아니야. 두 사람 사이엔 단 한마디도 오가지 않았거든. 아무 소리도 나지 않았지. 다만 둘 중 하나가 지끈거리는 머리를 좀 더 편히 누일 자리를 찾느라 몸을 뒤척이며 짜증스러운 소리를 내뱉을 때만 빼고 말이야. 그러면 잠깐 동안 그 새로운 안절부절못함이 곁의 친구한테로 옮아가는 것 같았어."]], [["These appearances would in themselves have furnished a pretty strong clue to the extent of the debauch of the previous night, even if there had not been other indications of the amusements in which it had been passed.", "이런 모습만 봐도 지난밤 흥청망청 놀아 댄 정도를 꽤 또렷하게 짐작할 수 있었어. 어떻게 밤을 보냈는지 보여 주는 다른 흔적이 없었다 해도 말이야."], ["A couple of billiard balls, all mud and dirt, two battered hats, a champagne bottle with a soiled glove twisted round the neck, to allow of its being grasped more surely in its capacity of an offensive weapon; a broken cane; a card-case without the top; an empty purse; a watch-guard snapped asunder; a handful of silver, mingled with fragments of half-smoked cigars, and their stale and crumbled ashes;--these, and many other tokens of riot and disorder, hinted very intelligibly at the nature of last night’s gentlemanly frolics.", "진흙과 먼지가 잔뜩 묻은 당구공 두 개, 찌그러진 모자 두 개, 목에 더러운 장갑이 칭칭 감긴 샴페인 병 하나 — 공격 무기로 쓸 때 더 단단히 움켜쥐려고 그랬던 거지 — 부러진 지팡이, 뚜껑 없는 명함첩, 빈 지갑, 두 동강 난 시계줄, 반쯤 피우다 만 시가 토막과 그 케케묵은 부스러진 재가 한데 섞인 은화 한 줌. 이런 것들에다 난동과 무질서를 보여 주는 다른 여러 흔적들까지, 지난밤 신사들의 놀이가 어떤 것이었는지 아주 알기 쉽게 일러 주고 있었지."]], [["Lord Frederick Verisopht was the first to speak.", "프레더릭 베리소프트 경이 먼저 입을 열었어."], ["Dropping his slippered foot on the ground, and, yawning heavily, he struggled into a sitting posture, and turned his dull languid eyes towards his friend, to whom he called in a drowsy voice.", "슬리퍼 신은 발을 바닥에 떨어뜨리고 크게 하품을 하더니, 끙끙대며 겨우 몸을 일으켜 앉았어. 그러고는 흐리멍덩하고 나른한 눈을 친구 쪽으로 돌리고는, 졸린 목소리로 불렀지."]], [["‘Hallo!’ replied Sir Mulberry, turning round.", "\"여어!\" 멀버리 경이 돌아누우며 대답했어."]], [["‘Are we going to lie here all da-a-y?’ said the lord.", "\"우리 하루 종일 여기 이러고 누워 있을 거야아?\" 경이 말했어."]], [["‘I don’t know that we’re fit for anything else,’ replied Sir Mulberry; ‘yet awhile, at least.", "\"우리가 달리 뭘 할 수 있을 것 같지도 않은데.\" 멀버리 경이 대꾸했어."], ["I haven’t a grain of life in me this morning.’", "\"적어도 지금은 말이야. 오늘 아침엔 내 몸에 기운이라곤 한 톨도 없어.\""]], [["‘Life!’ cried Lord Verisopht.", "\"기운!\" 베리소프트 경이 소리쳤어."], ["‘I feel as if there would be nothing so snug and comfortable as to die at once.’", "\"지금 당장 죽어 버리는 것보다 더 아늑하고 편안한 게 없을 것 같은 기분이야.\""]], [["‘Then why don’t you die?’ said Sir Mulberry.", "\"그럼 왜 안 죽어?\" 멀버리 경이 말했어."]], [["With which inquiry he turned his face away, and seemed to occupy himself in an attempt to fall asleep.", "이렇게 묻고는 얼굴을 돌려 버렸어. 그러고는 잠을 청해 보려는 데만 골몰하는 것 같았지."]], [["His hopeful friend and pupil drew a chair to the breakfast-table, and essayed to eat; but, finding that impossible, lounged to the window, then loitered up and down the room with his hand to his fevered head, and finally threw himself again on his sofa, and roused his friend once more.", "그의 장래가 촉망되는 친구이자 제자는 의자를 아침 식탁 쪽으로 끌어다 놓고 뭐라도 먹어 보려 했어. 하지만 도저히 안 되겠던지, 어슬렁어슬렁 창가로 갔지. 그러고는 열에 들뜬 머리에 손을 얹은 채 방 안을 어정버정 오갔어. 결국 다시 소파에 몸을 던지고는, 한 번 더 친구를 깨웠지."]], [["‘What the devil’s the matter?’ groaned Sir Mulberry, sitting upright on the couch.", "\"도대체 뭐가 문제냐고?\" 멀버리 경이 끙 소리를 내며 소파에 똑바로 일어나 앉았어."]], [["Although Sir Mulberry said this with sufficient ill-humour, he did not seem to feel himself quite at liberty to remain silent; for, after stretching himself very often, and declaring with a shiver that it was ‘infernal cold,’ he made an experiment at the breakfast-table, and proving more successful in it than his less-seasoned friend, remained there.", "멀버리 경은 이 말을 잔뜩 기분 나쁜 투로 내뱉었지만, 그렇다고 가만히 입 다물고 있을 마음은 들지 않는 모양이었어. 몇 번이고 기지개를 켜고는, 부르르 떨면서 \"끔찍하게 춥군.\" 하고 투덜대더니, 아침 식탁에서 한번 먹어 보려고 했지. 그러고는 덜 단련된 친구보다 더 성공을 거두어서, 그 자리에 그대로 눌러앉았어."]], [["‘Suppose,’ said Sir Mulberry, pausing with a morsel on the point of his fork, ‘suppose we go back to the subject of little Nickleby, eh?’", "\"이봐,\" 멀버리 경이 포크 끝에 음식 한 점을 꽂은 채 멈추고는 말했어. \"우리 니클비네 딸 얘기로 다시 돌아가 볼까, 응?\""]], [["‘Which little Nickleby; the money-lender or the ga-a-l?’ asked Lord Verisopht.", "\"어느 니클비? 그 돈놀이꾼 말이야, 아니면 그 처어녀 말이야?\" 베리소프트 경이 물었어."]], [["‘You take me, I see,’ replied Sir Mulberry.", "\"무슨 말인지 알아듣는구먼.\" 멀버리 경이 대꾸했어."], ["‘The girl, of course.’", "\"당연히 그 처녀지.\""]], [["‘You promised me you’d find her out,’ said Lord Verisopht.", "\"네가 그 여자를 찾아 주겠다고 약속했잖아.\" 베리소프트 경이 말했어."]], [["‘So I did,’ rejoined his friend; ‘but I have thought further of the matter since then.", "\"그랬지.\" 친구가 받아쳤어."], ["You distrust me in the business--you shall find her out yourself.’", "\"그런데 그 뒤로 이 일을 좀 더 생각해 봤거든. 너는 이 일에서 나를 못 믿잖아 — 그러니 네가 직접 그 여자를 찾아내.\""]], [["‘Na-ay,’ remonstrated Lord Verisopht.", "\"아아니.\" 베리소프트 경이 항의했어."]], [["‘But I say yes,’ returned his friend.", "\"아니, 그래야 해.\" 친구가 대꾸했어."], ["‘You shall find her out yourself.", "\"네가 직접 그 여자를 찾아내라고."], ["Don’t think that I mean, when you can--I know as well as you that if I did, you could never get sight of her without me.", "네가 찾을 수 있을 때 찾으라는 뜻이 아니야 — 나 없이는 네가 그 여자 얼굴 한번 못 본다는 걸 너만큼이나 나도 잘 알아. 아니지."], ["No. I say you shall find her out--SHALL--and I’ll put you in the way.’", "내 말은 네가 직접 찾아내야 한다는 거야 — 반드시 — 그리고 그 길을 내가 일러 주지.\""]], [["‘Now, curse me, if you ain’t a real, deyvlish, downright, thorough-paced friend,’ said the young lord, on whom this speech had produced a most reviving effect.", "\"맙소사, 너야말로 진짜, 더럽게, 틀림없는, 끝내주는 친구로구먼.\" 젊은 경이 말했어. 이 말에 그는 완전히 기운이 되살아난 듯했지."]], [["‘I’ll tell you how,’ said Sir Mulberry.", "\"어떻게 하는지 알려 주지.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어."], ["‘She was at that dinner as a bait for you.’", "\"그 처녀가 그날 저녁 식사 자리에 있었던 건 너를 낚으려는 미끼였어.\""]], [["‘No!’ cried the young lord.", "\"설마!\" 젊은 경이 소리쳤어."], ["‘What the dey--’", "\"도대체 무슨—\""]], [["‘As a bait for you,’ repeated his friend; ‘old Nickleby told me so himself.’", "\"너를 낚으려는 미끼였다고.\" 친구가 되풀이했어. \"늙은 니클비가 직접 나한테 그렇게 말했어.\""]], [["‘What a fine old cock it is!’ exclaimed Lord Verisopht; ‘a noble rascal!’", "\"정말 대단한 늙은이로군!\" 베리소프트 경이 외쳤어. \"기막힌 악당이야!\""]], [["‘Yes,’ said Sir Mulberry, ‘he knew she was a smart little creature--’", "\"그래.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어. \"그자는 그 처녀가 영리하고 깜찍한 것임을 알고 있었지—\""]], [["‘Smart!’ interposed the young lord.", "\"영리하다고!\" 젊은 경이 끼어들었어."], ["‘Upon my soul, Hawk, she’s a perfect beauty--a--a picture, a statue, a--a--upon my soul she is!’", "\"맹세컨대, 호크, 그 여잔 완벽한 미인이야 — 한 폭의 그림, 조각상, 그야말로—맹세컨대 그렇다고!\""]], [["‘Well,’ replied Sir Mulberry, shrugging his shoulders and manifesting an indifference, whether he felt it or not; ‘that’s a matter of taste; if mine doesn’t agree with yours, so much the better.’", "\"글쎄.\" 멀버리 경이 어깨를 으쓱하며, 정말 그렇든 아니든 무심한 척하며 대꾸했어. \"그건 취향 문제지. 내 취향이 네 취향이랑 안 맞는다면, 그거야 잘된 일이고.\""]], [["‘Confound it!’ reasoned the lord, ‘you were thick enough with her that day, anyhow.", "\"제기랄!\" 경이 따졌어."], ["I could hardly get in a word.’", "\"어쨌든 그날 너는 그 여자랑 어지간히 죽이 잘 맞던데. 나는 끼어들 틈도 거의 없었다고.\""]], [["‘Well enough for once, well enough for once,’ replied Sir Mulberry; ‘but not worth the trouble of being agreeable to again. If you seriously want to follow up the niece, tell the uncle that you must know where she lives and how she lives, and with whom, or you are no longer a customer of his. He’ll tell you fast enough.’", "\"한 번쯤은 충분히 그럴 만했지, 한 번쯤은.\" 멀버리 경이 대꾸했어. \"하지만 다시 비위 맞춰 줄 만큼 수고할 가치는 없어. 네가 정말로 그 조카딸을 쫓아가 볼 생각이라면, 그 삼촌한테 말해. 그 여자가 어디 사는지, 어떻게 사는지, 누구랑 사는지 꼭 알아야겠다고. 안 그러면 더 이상 네 거래는 없다고 말이야. 그러면 그자가 냉큼 일러 줄 거야.\""]], [["‘Why didn’t you say this before?’ asked Lord Verisopht, ‘instead of letting me go on burning, consuming, dragging out a miserable existence for an a-age!’", "\"왜 이걸 진작 말 안 했어?\" 베리소프트 경이 물었어. \"나를 이렇게 한 세에월이나 애태우고, 속 끓이고, 비참한 꼴로 질질 끌게 내버려 두고 말이야!\""]], [["‘I didn’t know it, in the first place,’ answered Sir Mulberry carelessly; ‘and in the second, I didn’t believe you were so very much in earnest.’", "\"우선 첫째로, 나도 몰랐거든.\" 멀버리 경이 무심하게 대답했어. \"그리고 둘째로, 네가 그렇게까지 진심인 줄 몰랐고.\""]], [["Now, the truth was, that in the interval which had elapsed since the dinner at Ralph Nickleby’s, Sir Mulberry Hawk had been furtively trying by every means in his power to discover whence Kate had so suddenly appeared, and whither she had disappeared. Unassisted by Ralph, however, with whom he had held no communication since their angry parting on that occasion, all his efforts were wholly unavailing, and he had therefore arrived at the determination of communicating to the young lord the substance of the admission he had gleaned from that worthy. To this he was impelled by various considerations; among which the certainty of knowing whatever the weak young man knew was decidedly not the least, as the desire of encountering the usurer’s niece again, and using his utmost arts to reduce her pride, and revenge himself for her contempt, was uppermost in his thoughts. It was a politic course of proceeding, and one which could not fail to redound to his advantage in every point of view, since the very circumstance of his having extorted from Ralph Nickleby his real design in introducing his niece to such society, coupled with his extreme disinterestedness in communicating it so freely to his friend, could not but advance his interests in that quarter, and greatly facilitate the passage of coin (pretty frequent and speedy already) from the pockets of Lord Frederick Verisopht to those of Sir Mulberry Hawk.", "사실인즉 이래. 랄프 니클비의 저녁 식사 자리 이후로 흐른 동안, 멀버리 호크 경은 케이트가 어디서 그렇게 갑자기 나타났다가 또 어디로 사라졌는지 알아내려고 온갖 수단을 동원해 은밀히 애써 왔어. 하지만 랄프의 도움 없이는 — 그날 화를 내며 헤어진 뒤로 그와는 한 번도 연락을 주고받지 않았거든 — 그 모든 노력이 죄다 헛수고였지. 그래서 멀버리 경은 그 훌륭한 양반한테서 캐낸 자백의 알맹이를 젊은 경에게 알려 주기로 마음먹은 거야. 여기엔 여러 가지 속셈이 작용했는데, 그중에서도 이 미욱한 젊은이가 아는 건 뭐든 자기도 확실히 알아 둔다는 점이 결코 가볍지 않았지. 무엇보다 그 돈놀이꾼의 조카딸을 다시 만나서, 온갖 수완을 다 부려 그 여자의 자존심을 꺾고, 자기를 깔본 데 앙갚음하겠다는 마음이 머릿속에 제일 크게 자리 잡고 있었거든. 이건 영악한 처사였어. 어느 모로 보나 자기한테 이로울 수밖에 없는 수였지. 랄프 니클비가 조카딸을 그런 사람들 틈에 끌어들인 진짜 속셈을 자기가 캐냈다는 사실에다, 그걸 친구한테 이렇게 선뜻 일러 주는 더없는 무사심까지 더하면, 그쪽으로 자기 잇속이 늘 수밖에 없었거든. 그리고 프레더릭 베리소프트 경의 주머니에서 멀버리 호크 경의 주머니로 돈이 (이미 꽤 자주, 또 빠르게 넘어오고 있었지만) 더 수월하게 옮겨 오게 만들 테니까."]], [["Thus reasoned Sir Mulberry, and in pursuance of this reasoning he and his friend soon afterwards repaired to Ralph Nickleby’s, there to execute a plan of operations concerted by Sir Mulberry himself, avowedly to promote his friend’s object, and really to attain his own.", "멀버리 경은 이렇게 따져 보았어. 그리고 이 계산에 따라, 그와 친구는 곧 랄프 니클비네로 향했지. 거기서 멀버리 경이 직접 짜 놓은 작전을 실행하려는 거였어. 겉으로는 친구의 목적을 이루어 준다는 명분이었지만, 실은 자기 잇속을 채우려는 거였지."]], [["They found Ralph at home, and alone.", "두 사람은 랄프가 집에 혼자 있는 걸 보았어. 랄프가 그들을 응접실로 안내할 때, 그곳에서 벌어졌던 그 장면이 떠오른 모양이었어."], ["As he led them into the drawing-room, the recollection of the scene which had taken place there seemed to occur to him, for he cast a curious look at Sir Mulberry, who bestowed upon it no other acknowledgment than a careless smile.", "호기심 어린 눈길로 멀버리 경을 흘끔 보았거든. 그런데 멀버리 경은 그저 무심한 미소로만 그 눈길을 받아넘겼지."]], [["They had a short conference upon some money matters then in progress, which were scarcely disposed of when the lordly dupe (in pursuance of his friend’s instructions) requested with some embarrassment to speak to Ralph alone.", "세 사람은 마침 진행 중인 돈 문제를 놓고 잠깐 이야기를 나누었어. 그 얘기가 채 끝나기도 전에, 그 귀족 호구가 (친구의 지시에 따라) 좀 멋쩍어하며 랄프와 단둘이 얘기하고 싶다고 했지."]], [["‘Alone, eh?’ cried Sir Mulberry, affecting surprise.", "\"단둘이서, 응?\" 멀버리 경이 놀란 척하며 외쳤어."], ["‘Oh, very good.", "\"오, 아주 좋아."], ["I’ll walk into the next room here.", "나는 여기 옆방으로 가 있지."], ["Don’t keep me long, that’s all.’", "너무 오래 붙들어 두지나 마, 그것뿐이야.\""]], [["So saying, Sir Mulberry took up his hat, and humming a fragment of a song disappeared through the door of communication between the two drawing-rooms, and closed it after him.", "이렇게 말하고 멀버리 경은 모자를 집어 들었어. 그러고는 노래 한 자락을 흥얼거리며, 두 응접실 사이를 잇는 문으로 사라지더니 등 뒤로 문을 닫았지."]], [["‘Now, my lord,’ said Ralph, ‘what is it?’", "\"자, 경.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"무슨 일이십니까?\""]], [["‘Nickleby,’ said his client, throwing himself along the sofa on which he had been previously seated, so as to bring his lips nearer to the old man’s ear, ‘what a pretty creature your niece is!’", "\"니클비.\" 그의 고객이, 아까 앉았던 소파에 길게 몸을 누이며 늙은이의 귀에 입을 더 가까이 가져갔어. \"댁의 조카딸이 어찌나 예쁜 처녀던지!\""]], [["‘Is she, my lord?’ replied Ralph.", "\"그렇습니까, 경?\" 랄프가 대꾸했어."], ["‘Maybe--maybe--I don’t trouble my head with such matters.’", "\"글쎄요 — 그럴지도요 — 저는 그런 일에 머리를 쓰지 않습니다만.\""]], [["‘You know she’s a deyvlish fine girl,’ said the client.", "\"그 여자가 끝내주게 멋진 처녀라는 거 너도 알잖아.\" 고객이 말했어."], ["‘You must know that, Nickleby.", "\"그건 너도 알 거야, 니클비."], ["Come, don’t deny that.’", "자, 아니라고는 못 할걸.\""]], [["‘Yes, I believe she is considered so,’ replied Ralph.", "\"예, 그렇게들 여기는 줄로 압니다.\" 랄프가 대답했어."], ["‘Indeed, I know she is.", "\"사실 그렇다는 걸 알고 있지요."], ["If I did not, you are an authority on such points, and your taste, my lord--on all points, indeed--is undeniable.’", "설령 제가 몰랐다 해도, 경께서는 그런 점에 권위자이시니까요. 그리고 경의 안목은 — 사실 모든 점에서 — 두말할 나위가 없으니까요.\""]], [["Nobody but the young man to whom these words were addressed could have been deaf to the sneering tone in which they were spoken, or blind to the look of contempt by which they were accompanied.", "이 말이 어떤 비웃는 투로 던져졌는지 알아채지 못할 사람, 또 그 말에 따라붙은 경멸의 눈빛을 못 알아볼 사람은, 그 말을 들은 그 젊은이 말고는 아무도 없었을 거야."], ["But Lord Frederick Verisopht was both, and took them to be complimentary.", "그런데 프레더릭 베리소프트 경은 그 둘 다였어. 그래서 그 말을 칭찬으로 받아들였지."]], [["‘Well,’ he said, ‘p’raps you’re a little right, and p’raps you’re a little wrong--a little of both, Nickleby. I want to know where this beauty lives, that I may have another peep at her, Nickleby.’", "\"글쎄.\" 그가 말했어. \"네 말이 좀 맞기도 하고, 좀 틀리기도 해 — 둘 다 조금씩이지, 니클비. 나는 이 미인이 어디 사는지 알고 싶어. 한 번 더 그 여자를 몰래 들여다보게 말이야, 니클비.\""]], [["‘Really--’ Ralph began in his usual tones.", "\"실은요—\" 랄프가 평소 말투로 입을 열었어."]], [["‘Don’t talk so loud,’ cried the other, achieving the great point of his lesson to a miracle.", "\"그렇게 큰 소리로 말하지 마.\" 그가 외쳤어."], ["‘I don’t want Hawk to hear.’", "배운 대로 그 중요한 대목을 기막히게 해낸 거지. \"호크한테 들리는 거 싫으니까.\""]], [["‘You know he is your rival, do you?’ said Ralph, looking sharply at him.", "\"그자가 경의 연적이라는 거, 아십니까?\" 랄프가 그를 날카롭게 쳐다보며 말했어."]], [["‘He always is, d-a-amn him,’ replied the client; ‘and I want to steal a march upon him.", "\"그자는 늘 그러지, 빌어머어글.\""], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "고객이 대꾸했어."], ["He’ll cut up so rough, Nickleby, at our talking together without him.", "\"그래서 나는 그자보다 한발 앞서고 싶은 거야. 하하하!"], ["Where does she live, Nickleby, that’s all?", "그자는 우리 둘이 자기 빼고 얘기했다는 걸 알면 엄청 까칠하게 굴 거야, 니클비."], ["Only tell me where she lives, Nickleby.’", "그 여자가 어디 사느냐고, 그것뿐이야. 어디 사는지만 말해 줘, 니클비.\""]], [["‘He bites,’ thought Ralph.", "'걸려들었군.' 랄프가 생각했어."], ["‘He bites.’", "'걸려들었어.'"]], [["‘Eh, Nickleby, eh?’ pursued the client.", "\"응, 니클비, 응?\" 고객이 다그쳤어."], ["‘Where does she live?’", "\"그 여자가 어디 살아?\""]], [["‘Really, my lord,’ said Ralph, rubbing his hands slowly over each other, ‘I must think before I tell you.’", "\"실은요, 경.\" 랄프가 두 손을 천천히 비비며 말했어. \"말씀드리기 전에 좀 생각을 해 봐야겠습니다.\""]], [["‘No, not a bit of it, Nickleby; you mustn’t think at all,’ replied Verisopht.", "\"아냐, 절대 안 돼, 니클비. 생각 따윈 하지 마.\""], ["‘Where is it?’", "베리소프트 경이 대꾸했어. \"어디냐고?\""]], [["‘No good can come of your knowing,’ replied Ralph.", "\"아셔 봐야 좋을 게 없습니다.\" 랄프가 대답했어."], ["‘She has been virtuously and well brought up; to be sure she is handsome, poor, unprotected!", "\"그 아이는 정숙하고 반듯하게 자랐지요. 아닌 게 아니라 예쁘긴 하지만, 가난하고, 지켜 줄 사람도 없으니까요!"], ["Poor girl, poor girl.’", "가엾은 것, 가엾은 것.\""]], [["Ralph ran over this brief summary of Kate’s condition as if it were merely passing through his own mind, and he had no intention to speak aloud; but the shrewd sly look which he directed at his companion as he delivered it, gave this poor assumption the lie.", "랄프는 케이트의 처지를 이렇게 짤막하게 늘어놓았는데, 마치 자기 머릿속을 그냥 스쳐 가는 생각일 뿐 입 밖에 낼 마음은 없었던 것처럼 굴었어. 그런데 그 말을 하면서 곁의 사람을 향해 던진 그 약삭빠르고 음흉한 눈길이, 이 어설픈 시늉을 거짓으로 들통내고 말았지."]], [["‘I tell you I only want to see her,’ cried his client.", "\"내 말은, 나는 그저 그 여자를 보고 싶을 뿐이라고.\""], ["‘A ma-an may look at a pretty woman without harm, mayn’t he?", "고객이 외쳤어. \"남자가 예쁜 여자 좀 본다고 해될 게 뭐 있어, 안 그래?"], ["Now, where DOES she live?", "자, 그 여자가 도대체 어디 살아?"], ["You know you’re making a fortune out of me, Nickleby, and upon my soul nobody shall ever take me to anybody else, if you only tell me this.’", "너도 나한테서 한밑천 잡고 있는 거 알잖아, 니클비. 맹세컨대, 이것만 말해 주면 나는 절대 누가 다른 사람한테로 데려간다 해도 절대 안 갈 거야.\""]], [["‘As you promise that, my lord,’ said Ralph, with feigned reluctance, ‘and as I am most anxious to oblige you, and as there’s no harm in it--no harm--I’ll tell you. But you had better keep it to yourself, my lord; strictly to yourself.’ Ralph pointed to the adjoining room as he spoke, and nodded expressively.", "\"그렇게 약속하시니까, 경.\" 랄프가 마지못한 척하며 말했어. \"그리고 저도 경의 뜻을 들어드리고 싶어 안달이 나 있고, 또 해될 것도 없으니까요 — 해될 게 없지요 — 말씀드리겠습니다. 하지만 이건 경 혼자만 알고 계시는 게 좋습니다. 철저히 혼자만요.\" 랄프는 이렇게 말하며 옆방을 가리키고는, 뜻이 통하게끔 고개를 끄덕였어."]], [["The young lord, feigning to be equally impressed with the necessity of this precaution, Ralph disclosed the present address and occupation of his niece, observing that from what he heard of the family they appeared very ambitious to have distinguished acquaintances, and that a lord could, doubtless, introduce himself with great ease, if he felt disposed.", "젊은 경도 이 조심이 꼭 필요하다는 데 똑같이 마음을 쓰는 척하자, 랄프는 조카딸이 지금 어디 사는지, 무슨 일을 하는지 알려 주었어. 그러고는 자기가 들은 바로는 그 식구가 이름난 사람들과 사귀고 싶어 안달이 난 것 같다고, 그러니 귀족쯤 되시면 마음만 먹으면 틀림없이 아주 쉽게 자기소개를 할 수 있을 거라고 덧붙였지."]], [["‘Your object being only to see her again,’ said Ralph, ‘you could effect it at any time you chose by that means.’", "\"경의 목적이 그저 그 아이를 다시 보는 것뿐이라면,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그 방법으로 원하실 때 언제든 이룰 수 있으실 겁니다.\""]], [["Lord Verisopht acknowledged the hint with a great many squeezes of Ralph’s hard, horny hand, and whispering that they would now do well to close the conversation, called to Sir Mulberry Hawk that he might come back.", "베리소프트 경은 랄프의 딱딱하고 거친 손을 여러 번 꼭 쥐며 그 귀띔에 고마움을 표했어. 그러고는 이제 이쯤에서 이야기를 끝내는 게 좋겠다고 속삭이고는, 멀버리 호크 경더러 돌아오라고 불렀지."]], [["‘I thought you had gone to sleep,’ said Sir Mulberry, reappearing with an ill-tempered air.", "\"잠든 줄 알았네.\" 멀버리 경이 못마땅한 기색으로 다시 나타나며 말했어."]], [["‘Sorry to detain you,’ replied the gull; ‘but Nickleby has been so ama-azingly funny that I couldn’t tear myself away.’", "\"붙들어 둬서 미안하네.\" 호구가 대꾸했어. \"근데 니클비가 어찌나 우우스운지 도무지 자리를 뜰 수가 있어야지.\""]], [["‘No, no,’ said Ralph; ‘it was all his lordship.", "\"아닙니다, 아닙니다.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["You know what a witty, humorous, elegant, accomplished man Lord Frederick is.", "\"다 경께서 그러신 거지요. 프레더릭 경께서 얼마나 재치 있고, 익살스럽고, 품위 있고, 교양 넘치는 분이신지 아시잖습니까."], ["Mind the step, my lord--Sir Mulberry, pray give way.’", "발밑 조심하십시오, 경 — 멀버리 경, 부디 먼저 가시지요.\""]], [["With such courtesies as these, and many low bows, and the same cold sneer upon his face all the while, Ralph busied himself in showing his visitors downstairs, and otherwise than by the slightest possible motion about the corners of his mouth, returned no show of answer to the look of admiration with which Sir Mulberry Hawk seemed to compliment him on being such an accomplished and most consummate scoundrel.", "이런 정중한 인사와 수없이 굽실대는 절을 곁들이며, 그러면서도 줄곧 그 차가운 비웃음을 얼굴에 띤 채, 랄프는 손님들을 아래층으로 배웅하느라 분주했어. 멀버리 호크 경은 이렇게 교양 있고 더없이 완벽한 악당이 되었다며 감탄 어린 눈길로 그를 추켜세우는 듯했는데, 랄프는 입꼬리를 아주 살짝 움직이는 것 말고는 거기에 아무런 대꾸도 내보이지 않았지."]], [["There had been a ring at the bell a few minutes before, which was answered by Newman Noggs just as they reached the hall.", "몇 분 전에 초인종이 울렸는데, 그들이 막 현관에 다다랐을 때 뉴먼 노그스가 거기에 응했어."], ["In the ordinary course of business Newman would have either admitted the new-comer in silence, or have requested him or her to stand aside while the gentlemen passed out.", "평소 같으면 뉴먼은 새로 온 사람을 말없이 들이거나, 신사들이 나가는 동안 옆으로 비켜서 있어 달라고 청했을 거야. 그런데 누가 왔는지 보자마자, 뭔가 자기 나름의 속셈이라도 있는 듯, 영업 시간 중 랄프네 저택의 정해진 관례를 대담하게 깨 버렸지."], ["But he no sooner saw who it was, than as if for some private reason of his own, he boldly departed from the established custom of Ralph’s mansion in business hours, and looking towards the respectable trio who were approaching, cried in a loud and sonorous voice, ‘Mrs Nickleby!’", "다가오는 그 점잖은 세 사람 쪽을 바라보며, 크고 우렁찬 목소리로 외친 거야. \"니클비 부인!\""]], [["‘Mrs.", "\"니클비 부인!\""], ["Nickleby!’ cried Sir Mulberry Hawk, as his friend looked back, and stared him in the face.", "멀버리 호크 경이 외쳤어. 그러자 친구가 뒤돌아 그의 얼굴을 빤히 쳐다봤지."]], [["It was, indeed, that well-intentioned lady, who, having received an offer for the empty house in the city directed to the landlord, had brought it post-haste to Mr.", "정말로 그 마음씨 착한 부인이었어."], ["Nickleby without delay.", "도시에 있는 빈집을 사겠다는 제안이 집주인 앞으로 왔는데, 그걸 지체 없이 니클비 씨한테 부랴부랴 들고 온 거였지."]], [["‘Nobody YOU know,’ said Ralph.", "\"자네가 아는 사람이 아니네.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘Step into the office, my--my--dear.", "\"사무실로 들어가 계시구려, 형—형수님."], ["I’ll be with you directly.’", "곧 가겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Nobody I know!’ cried Sir Mulberry Hawk, advancing to the astonished lady.", "\"제가 아는 사람이 아니라고요!\" 멀버리 호크 경이 어리둥절해하는 부인 쪽으로 다가서며 외쳤어."], ["‘Is this Mrs. Nickleby--the mother of Miss Nickleby--the delightful creature that I had the happiness of meeting in this house the very last time I dined here?", "\"이분이 니클비 부인 — 니클비 양의 어머니 — 제가 이 댁에서 마지막으로 식사하던 바로 그날, 만나 뵙는 행복을 누렸던 그 사랑스러운 분이신가요?"], ["But no;’ said Sir Mulberry, stopping short.", "아니, 아니지.\" 멀버리 경이 우뚝 멈추며 말했어."], ["‘No, it can’t be.", "\"아니, 그럴 리가 없어."], ["There is the same cast of features, the same indescribable air of--But no; no.", "똑같은 이목구비에, 똑같이 뭐라 말로 다 할 수 없는 분위기까지 — 하지만 아니야, 아니지."], ["This lady is too young for that.’", "이분은 그러기엔 너무 젊으셔.\""]], [["‘I think you can tell the gentleman, brother-in-law, if it concerns him to know,’ said Mrs.", "\"제부, 그 신사분이 알아 둘 만한 일이라면 직접 말씀드려도 좋겠네요.\""], ["Nickleby, acknowledging the compliment with a graceful bend, ‘that Kate Nickleby is my daughter.’", "니클비 부인이 그 칭찬을 우아하게 고개 숙여 받으며 말했어. \"케이트 니클비가 제 딸이라고요.\""]], [["‘Her daughter, my lord!’ cried Sir Mulberry, turning to his friend.", "\"부인의 따님이라고요, 경!\" 멀버리 경이 친구 쪽을 돌아보며 외쳤어."], ["‘This lady’s daughter, my lord.’", "\"이분의 따님이라네요, 경.\""]], [["‘My lord!’ thought Mrs.", "'경이라고?' 니클비 부인이 생각했어."], ["Nickleby. ‘Well, I never did--’", "'어머나, 세상에—'"]], [["‘This, then, my lord,’ said Sir Mulberry, ‘is the lady to whose obliging marriage we owe so much happiness.", "\"그렇다면, 경.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어."], ["This lady is the mother of sweet Miss Nickleby.", "\"이분이 바로 그 고마운 결혼을 해 주신 덕에 우리가 이토록 큰 행복을 입게 된 그 부인이로군요."], ["Do you observe the extraordinary likeness, my lord?", "이분이 그 사랑스러운 니클비 양의 어머니라니. 이 놀라운 닮은꼴이 보이십니까, 경?"], ["Nickleby--introduce us.’", "니클비 — 우리를 좀 소개해 주시오.\""]], [["Ralph did so, in a kind of desperation.", "랄프는 자포자기한 심정으로 그렇게 했어."]], [["‘Upon my soul, it’s a most delightful thing,’ said Lord Frederick, pressing forward.", "\"맹세컨대, 정말 더없이 기쁜 일입니다.\" 프레더릭 경이 앞으로 나서며 말했어."], ["‘How de do?’", "\"안녕하십니까?\""]], [["Mrs.", "니클비 부인은 이 유난히 다정한 인사에 너무 당황한 데다, 다른 보닛을 쓰고 오지 못한 게 못내 아쉬워서, 당장 무슨 대답을 할 수가 없었어."], ["Nickleby was too much flurried by these uncommonly kind salutations, and her regrets at not having on her other bonnet, to make any immediate reply, so she merely continued to bend and smile, and betray great agitation.", "그저 연신 고개를 숙이고 미소만 지으며, 무척 들뜬 기색을 내비칠 뿐이었지."]], [["‘A--and how is Miss Nickleby?’ said Lord Frederick.", "\"그—그래, 니클비 양은 어떻게 지내시나요?\" 프레더릭 경이 말했어."], ["‘Well, I hope?’", "\"잘 지내시지요?\""]], [["‘She is quite well, I’m obliged to you, my lord,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby, recovering. ‘Quite well. She wasn’t well for some days after that day she dined here, and I can’t help thinking, that she caught cold in that hackney coach coming home. Hackney coaches, my lord, are such nasty things, that it’s almost better to walk at any time, for although I believe a hackney coachman can be transported for life, if he has a broken window, still they are so reckless, that they nearly all have broken windows. I once had a swelled face for six weeks, my lord, from riding in a hackney coach--I think it was a hackney coach,’ said Mrs Nickleby reflecting, ‘though I’m not quite certain whether it wasn’t a chariot; at all events I know it was a dark green, with a very long number, beginning with a nought and ending with a nine--no, beginning with a nine, and ending with a nought, that was it, and of course the stamp-office people would know at once whether it was a coach or a chariot if any inquiries were made there--however that was, there it was with a broken window and there was I for six weeks with a swelled face--I think that was the very same hackney coach, that we found out afterwards, had the top open all the time, and we should never even have known it, if they hadn’t charged us a shilling an hour extra for having it open, which it seems is the law, or was then, and a most shameful law it appears to be--I don’t understand the subject, but I should say the Corn Laws could be nothing to THAT act of Parliament.’", "\"잘 지내요, 고마워요, 경.\" 니클비 부인이 정신을 차리며 대답했어. \"아주 잘 지낸답니다. 그 애가 여기서 식사하던 그날 이후로 며칠 몸이 안 좋았는데, 아무래도 집에 오는 길에 그 삯마차에서 감기에 걸린 것 같다는 생각이 자꾸 들어요. 삯마차란 게, 경, 어찌나 더러운 물건인지, 언제든 차라리 걷는 게 나을 정도예요. 삯마차 마부는 창문이 깨져 있으면 종신 유배형까지 받을 수 있다고 알고 있는데도, 어찌나들 막무가내인지, 거의 다 창문이 깨져 있거든요. 저도 한번은 삯마차를 탔다가 얼굴이 여섯 주나 부어 있었답니다, 경 — 그게 삯마차였던 것 같아요.\" 니클비 부인이 곰곰이 생각하며 말했어. \"그게 사륜마차가 아니었는지는 확실치 않지만요. 어쨌든 짙은 초록색이었고, 번호가 아주 길었는데, 영으로 시작해서 구로 끝났어요 — 아니, 구로 시작해서 영으로 끝났던가, 그게 맞아요. 그러니까 누가 마차 등록소에 물어보면 그게 삯마차였는지 사륜마차였는지 단번에 알 텐데요 — 아무튼 그게, 창문이 깨진 채로 거기 있었고, 저는 얼굴이 부은 채로 여섯 주를 보냈답니다 — 그게 바로 그 삯마차였던 것 같아요. 나중에 알고 보니 그동안 내내 지붕이 열려 있었던 건데, 지붕 열어 둔 값이라며 한 시간에 일 실링씩 더 물리지만 않았어도 우리는 그런 줄도 몰랐을 거예요. 그게 법이라나요, 적어도 그때는요. 참 부끄러운 법인 것 같아요 — 제가 그 일은 잘 모르지만, 그 의회 법에 비하면 곡물법쯤은 아무것도 아닐 거예요.\""]], [["Having pretty well run herself out by this time, Mrs.", "이쯤에서 니클비 부인은 어지간히 할 말이 다 떨어졌는지, 시작할 때처럼 갑자기 입을 다물었어."], ["Nickleby stopped as suddenly as she had started off; and repeated that Kate was quite well.", "그러고는 케이트가 아주 잘 지낸다는 말을 다시 한번 했지. \"정말이지,\""], ["‘Indeed,’ said Mrs.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby, ‘I don’t think she ever was better, since she had the hooping-cough, scarlet-fever, and measles, all at the same time, and that’s the fact.’", "\"백일해랑 성홍열이랑 홍역을 한꺼번에 앓고 난 뒤로, 지금처럼 건강한 적이 없었던 것 같아요. 정말이에요.\""]], [["‘Is that letter for me?’ growled Ralph, pointing to the little packet Mrs.", "\"그 편지, 나한테 온 거요?\""], ["Nickleby held in her hand.", "랄프가 니클비 부인이 손에 든 작은 꾸러미를 가리키며 으르렁대듯 말했어."]], [["‘For you, brother-in-law,’ replied Mrs.", "\"제부한테 온 거예요.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어."], ["Nickleby, ‘and I walked all the way up here on purpose to give it you.’", "\"이걸 드리려고 일부러 여기까지 그 먼 길을 걸어왔답니다.\""]], [["‘All the way up here!’ cried Sir Mulberry, seizing upon the chance of discovering where Mrs.", "\"여기까지 그 먼 길을요!\" 멀버리 경이, 니클비 부인이 어디서 왔는지 알아낼 기회를 잡으며 외쳤어."], ["Nickleby had come from.", "\"지긋지긋하게 먼 거리지요!"], ["‘What a confounded distance! How far do you call it now?’", "자, 그게 얼마나 멀다고 보십니까?\""]], [["‘How far do I call it?’ said Mrs.", "\"얼마나 멀다고 보냐고요?\""], ["Nickleby. ‘Let me see.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["It’s just a mile from our door to the Old Bailey.’", "\"어디 보자. 우리 집 문에서 올드 베일리까지 딱 일 마일이에요.\""]], [["‘No, no.", "\"아니, 아니요."], ["Not so much as that,’ urged Sir Mulberry.", "그 정도까진 안 됩니다.\" 멀버리 경이 우겼어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["It is indeed,’ said Mrs.", "정말 그렇다니까요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby. ‘I appeal to his lordship.’", "\"여기 경께 여쭤볼게요.\""]], [["‘I should decidedly say it was a mile,’ remarked Lord Frederick, with a solemn aspect.", "\"제가 보기엔 분명 일 마일은 됩니다.\" 프레더릭 경이 엄숙한 얼굴로 한마디 했어."]], [["‘It must be; it can’t be a yard less,’ said Mrs.", "\"틀림없어요. 일 야드도 모자라지 않을걸요.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["‘All down Newgate Street, all down Cheapside, all up Lombard Street, down Gracechurch Street, and along Thames Street, as far as Spigwiffin’s Wharf.", "\"뉴게이트 거리를 쭉 내려가서, 칩사이드를 쭉 내려가서, 롬바드 거리를 쭉 올라가서, 그레이스처치 거리를 내려가서, 템스 거리를 따라 스피그위핀스 부두까지요. 어머!"], ["Oh! It’s a mile.’", "일 마일은 된다니까요.\""]], [["‘Yes, on second thoughts I should say it was,’ replied Sir Mulberry.", "\"예, 다시 생각해 보니 그렇겠군요.\" 멀버리 경이 대꾸했어."], ["‘But you don’t surely mean to walk all the way back?’", "\"그런데 설마 그 먼 길을 다시 걸어서 돌아가실 생각은 아니시겠지요?\""]], [["‘Oh, no,’ rejoined Mrs.", "\"어머, 아니에요.\""], ["Nickleby. ‘I shall go back in an omnibus.", "니클비 부인이 받아쳤어."], ["I didn’t travel about in omnibuses, when my poor dear Nicholas was alive, brother-in-law.", "\"옴니버스를 타고 돌아갈 거예요. 제 가엾은 남편 니콜라스가 살아 있을 땐 옴니버스 같은 거 타고 돌아다니지 않았답니다, 제부."], ["But as it is, you know--’", "하지만 사정이 이러니까요, 아시잖아요—\""]], [["‘Yes, yes,’ replied Ralph impatiently, ‘and you had better get back before dark.’", "\"예, 예.\" 랄프가 못 참겠다는 듯 대꾸했어. \"그러니 날 저물기 전에 돌아가시는 게 좋겠소.\""]], [["‘Thank you, brother-in-law, so I had,’ returned Mrs.", "\"고마워요, 제부, 그래야겠네요.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어."], ["Nickleby. ‘I think I had better say goodbye, at once.’", "\"지금 당장 작별 인사를 하는 게 낫겠어요.\""]], [["‘Not stop and--rest?’ said Ralph, who seldom offered refreshments unless something was to be got by it.", "\"좀 머물다 — 쉬어 가지 않고요?\" 랄프가 말했어. 그는 뭔가 얻을 게 있지 않고서야 좀처럼 음식 대접 같은 건 권하지 않았지."]], [["‘Oh dear me no,’ returned Mrs.", "\"어머, 무슨, 됐어요.\""], ["Nickleby, glancing at the dial.", "니클비 부인이 시계판을 흘끔 보며 대답했어."]], [["‘Lord Frederick,’ said Sir Mulberry, ‘we are going Mrs.", "\"프레더릭 경.\""], ["Nickleby’s way.", "멀버리 경이 말했어."], ["We’ll see her safe to the omnibus?’", "\"우리도 니클비 부인 가시는 길로 가는 참이잖아. 옴니버스까지 무사히 모셔다 드릴까?\""]], [["‘By all means.", "\"물론이지."], ["Ye-es.’", "그—러—세.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["I really couldn’t think of it!’ said Mrs.", "정말이지 그러실 것까진 없는데요!\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."]], [["But Sir Mulberry Hawk and Lord Verisopht were peremptory in their politeness, and leaving Ralph, who seemed to think, not unwisely, that he looked less ridiculous as a mere spectator, than he would have done if he had taken any part in these proceedings, they quitted the house with Mrs.", "하지만 멀버리 호크 경과 베리소프트 경은 막무가내로 정중하게 굴었어. 그래서 랄프는 — 이런 일에 끼어드느니 그냥 구경꾼으로 있는 편이 덜 우스워 보인다고 여긴 모양인데, 그게 그리 어리석은 생각은 아니었지 — 남겨 둔 채, 둘은 니클비 부인을 가운데 끼고 집을 나섰어."], ["Nickleby between them; that good lady in a perfect ecstasy of satisfaction, no less with the attentions shown her by two titled gentlemen, than with the conviction that Kate might now pick and choose, at least between two large fortunes, and most unexceptionable husbands.", "그 착한 부인은 더없는 만족감에 푹 빠져 있었지. 작위 가진 두 신사가 자기한테 이렇게 떠받들어 주는 게 흐뭇하기도 했지만, 이제 케이트가 적어도 두 큰 재산과, 흠잡을 데 없는 두 신랑감 사이에서 골라잡을 수 있겠구나 하는 확신이 못지않게 기뻤거든."]], [["As she was carried away for the moment by an irresistible train of thought, all connected with her daughter’s future greatness, Sir Mulberry Hawk and his friend exchanged glances over the top of the bonnet which the poor lady so much regretted not having left at home, and proceeded to dilate with great rapture, but much respect on the manifold perfections of Miss Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 잠시 딸의 앞날에 펼쳐질 영화와 얽힌 거역할 수 없는 생각의 흐름에 빠져 넋이 나가 있는 사이, 멀버리 호크 경과 그의 친구는 그 가엾은 부인이 집에 두고 오지 않은 걸 그토록 아쉬워하던 보닛 너머로 눈짓을 주고받았어. 그러고는 잔뜩 황홀해하면서도, 한껏 정중한 태도로 니클비 양의 갖가지 빼어난 점에 대해 늘어놓기 시작했지."]], [["‘What a delight, what a comfort, what a happiness, this amiable creature must be to you,’ said Sir Mulberry, throwing into his voice an indication of the warmest feeling.", "\"이렇게 사랑스러운 아가씨가 부인께는 얼마나 큰 기쁨이고, 위안이고, 행복이겠습니까.\" 멀버리 경이 더없이 따뜻한 감정이 묻어나게 목소리를 꾸미며 말했어."]], [["‘She is indeed, sir,’ replied Mrs.", "\"정말 그렇답니다, 선생님.\""], ["Nickleby; ‘she is the sweetest-tempered, kindest-hearted creature--and so clever!’", "니클비 부인이 대답했어. \"그 애는 더없이 상냥하고, 마음씨 곱고 — 게다가 어찌나 똑똑한지요!\""]], [["‘She looks clayver,’ said Lord Verisopht, with the air of a judge of cleverness.", "\"똑똑해 보이긴 하더군요.\" 베리소프트 경이 똑똑함을 가리는 감정가라도 되는 듯한 투로 말했어."]], [["‘I assure you she is, my lord,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby. ‘When she was at school in Devonshire, she was universally allowed to be beyond all exception the very cleverest girl there, and there were a great many very clever ones too, and that’s the truth--twenty-five young ladies, fifty guineas a year without the et-ceteras, both the Miss Dowdles the most accomplished, elegant, fascinating creatures--Oh dear me!’ said Mrs Nickleby, ‘I never shall forget what pleasure she used to give me and her poor dear papa, when she was at that school, never--such a delightful letter every half-year, telling us that she was the first pupil in the whole establishment, and had made more progress than anybody else! I can scarcely bear to think of it even now. The girls wrote all the letters themselves,’ added Mrs. Nickleby, ‘and the writing-master touched them up afterwards with a magnifying glass and a silver pen; at least I think they wrote them, though Kate was never quite certain about that, because she didn’t know the handwriting of hers again; but anyway, I know it was a circular which they all copied, and of course it was a very gratifying thing--very gratifying.’", "\"틀림없다니까요, 경.\" 니클비 부인이 받아쳤어. \"그 애가 데번셔에서 학교에 다닐 때, 거기서 두말할 것 없이 가장 똑똑한 아이라고 다들 인정했답니다. 게다가 똑똑한 아이들도 아주 많았는데 말이에요. 정말이라니까요 — 아가씨가 스물다섯 명, 한 해 학비가 잡비 빼고 오십 기니에, 다우들 집안 자매도 둘이나 있었는데 더없이 교양 있고, 우아하고, 매력적인 아이들이었지요 — 어머나!\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"그 애가 그 학교에 다닐 때 저랑 가엾은 그 애 아빠한테 안겨 준 기쁨을 저는 절대 못 잊을 거예요, 절대로 — 반년마다 그렇게 기쁜 편지가 왔답니다. 자기가 학교 전체에서 일등이고, 누구보다 더 많이 발전했다고요! 지금 생각해도 가슴이 벅차서 견디기가 힘들 정도예요. 편지는 아이들이 다 직접 썼어요.\" 니클비 부인이 덧붙였어. \"글씨 선생님이 나중에 돋보기랑 은펜으로 손을 봐 주긴 했지만요. 적어도 아이들이 썼다고는 생각하는데, 케이트는 그것조차 영 확실치가 않대요. 자기 글씨를 다시 봐도 못 알아보겠다나요. 어쨌든 그게 다들 베껴 쓴 똑같은 회람용 편지였던 건 알아요. 그래도 물론 아주 기쁜 일이었지요 — 정말 기쁜 일이었어요.\""]], [["With similar recollections Mrs.", "니클비 부인은 이런 추억들로 지루한 길을 달래 가며, 마침내 옴니버스에 다다랐어."], ["Nickleby beguiled the tediousness of the way, until they reached the omnibus, which the extreme politeness of her new friends would not allow them to leave until it actually started, when they took their hats, as Mrs.", "새 친구들은 어찌나 극진히 정중한지, 마차가 실제로 출발할 때까지 자리를 뜨려 하지 않았지. 그러고는 출발하는 순간, 모자를 — 니클비 부인이 그 뒤로 여러 차례 듣는 이들에게 엄숙히 장담했듯이 — \"완전히 벗어 들고,\""], ["Nickleby solemnly assured her hearers on many subsequent occasions, ‘completely off,’ and kissed their straw-coloured kid gloves till they were no longer visible.", "그 짚빛 새끼 염소가죽 장갑이 더 이상 보이지 않을 때까지 입을 맞췄어."]], [["Mrs. Nickleby leant back in the furthest corner of the conveyance, and, closing her eyes, resigned herself to a host of most pleasing meditations. Kate had never said a word about having met either of these gentlemen; ‘that,’ she thought, ‘argues that she is strongly prepossessed in favour of one of them.’ Then the question arose, which one could it be. The lord was the youngest, and his title was certainly the grandest; still Kate was not the girl to be swayed by such considerations as these. ‘I will never put any constraint upon her inclinations,’ said Mrs. Nickleby to herself; ‘but upon my word I think there’s no comparison between his lordship and Sir Mulberry--Sir Mulberry is such an attentive gentlemanly creature, so much manner, such a fine man, and has so much to say for himself. I hope it’s Sir Mulberry--I think it must be Sir Mulberry!’ And then her thoughts flew back to her old predictions, and the number of times she had said, that Kate with no fortune would marry better than other people’s daughters with thousands; and, as she pictured with the brightness of a mother’s fancy all the beauty and grace of the poor girl who had struggled so cheerfully with her new life of hardship and trial, her heart grew too full, and the tears trickled down her face.", "니클비 부인은 마차의 가장 안쪽 구석에 등을 기댔어. 그러고는 눈을 감고, 더없이 흐뭇한 온갖 상념에 몸을 내맡겼지. 케이트는 이 신사들 중 누구를 만났다는 말을 한마디도 한 적이 없었어. '저건,' 부인은 생각했어. '저 애가 둘 중 한 사람한테 단단히 마음이 기울었다는 뜻이지.' 그러자 그게 둘 중 누구일까 하는 물음이 떠올랐어. 경이 더 젊었고, 작위도 분명 더 대단했지. 그래도 케이트는 그런 걸 따져서 마음이 흔들릴 애가 아니었어. '나는 그 애 마음에 절대 억지를 부리지 않을 거야.' 니클비 부인은 혼자 생각했어. '하지만 정말이지, 그 경하고 멀버리 경은 비교가 안 되는 것 같단 말이야 — 멀버리 경은 어찌나 자상하고 신사다운 분인지, 풍채도 좋고, 인물도 훤하고, 또 말솜씨도 좋고. 멀버리 경이었으면 좋겠어 — 멀버리 경이 틀림없어!' 그러자 부인의 생각은 오래전 자기가 했던 예언으로 날아갔어. 케이트가 한 푼 없이도, 수천 파운드를 가진 남의 집 딸들보다 더 좋은 데로 시집갈 거라고 몇 번이나 말했던가 하는 거였지. 그리고 가난한 새 삶의 고난과 시련을 그토록 씩씩하게 헤쳐 온 그 가엾은 딸의 온갖 아름다움과 우아함을 어머니의 환한 상상으로 그려 보자니, 가슴이 너무 벅차올라 눈물이 주르륵 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸어."]], [["Meanwhile, Ralph walked to and fro in his little back-office, troubled in mind by what had just occurred.", "한편 랄프는 작은 뒤쪽 사무실을 이리저리 거닐고 있었어. 방금 일어난 일로 마음이 어지러웠거든."], ["To say that Ralph loved or cared for--in the most ordinary acceptation of those terms--any one of God’s creatures, would be the wildest fiction.", "랄프가 — 그 말의 가장 평범한 뜻으로 — 신이 지으신 어느 한 사람이라도 사랑했다거나 아꼈다고 하면, 그건 더없이 터무니없는 꾸며 낸 이야기일 거야. 그래도 어쩐 일인지, 이따금 조카딸 생각이 동정과 연민이 어린 채로 그의 마음에 슬며시 스며들곤 했어."], ["Still, there had somehow stolen upon him from time to time a thought of his niece which was tinged with compassion and pity; breaking through the dull cloud of dislike or indifference which darkened men and women in his eyes, there was, in her case, the faintest gleam of light--a most feeble and sickly ray at the best of times--but there it was, and it showed the poor girl in a better and purer aspect than any in which he had looked on human nature yet.", "그의 눈에 비치는 남자며 여자를 죄다 어둡게 덮어 버리는 그 칙칙한 미움과 무관심의 구름을 뚫고, 그 아이의 경우엔 아주 희미한 한 줄기 빛이 새어 들었던 거야 — 아무리 좋을 때라도 더없이 가냘프고 병약한 빛줄기였지만 — 어쨌든 그건 거기 있었어. 그리고 그 빛은 그 가엾은 아이를, 그가 여태 인간 본성을 바라보며 보아 온 그 어떤 모습보다도 더 곱고 더 맑은 모습으로 비춰 주었지."]], [["‘I wish,’ thought Ralph, ‘I had never done this.", "'내가,' 랄프는 생각했어."], ["And yet it will keep this boy to me, while there is money to be made.", "'이 짓을 하지 말걸 그랬어. 그래도 돈벌이가 되는 한, 이걸로 그 애송이를 내 곁에 붙들어 둘 수 있겠지."], ["Selling a girl--throwing her in the way of temptation, and insult, and coarse speech.", "계집애를 팔아넘기는 거지 — 유혹과 모욕과 상스러운 말 앞에 그 애를 내던지는 거야."], ["Nearly two thousand pounds profit from him already though.", "하지만 벌써 그 녀석한테서 이천 파운드 가까이 벌었잖아."], ["Pshaw!", "흥!"], ["match-making mothers do the same thing every day.’", "딸 시집보내려는 어미들이 날마다 하는 짓이 바로 이거야.'"]], [["He sat down, and told the chances, for and against, on his fingers.", "그는 자리에 앉아, 손가락을 꼽아 가며 이러쿵저러쿵 가능성을 따져 보았어."]], [["‘If I had not put them in the right track today,’ thought Ralph, ‘this foolish woman would have done so. Well.", "'오늘 내가 그자들을 옳은 길로 들여놓지 않았어도,'"], ["If her daughter is as true to herself as she should be from what I have seen, what harm ensues?", "랄프는 생각했어. '저 어리석은 여편네가 그렇게 했을 거야."], ["A little teasing, a little humbling, a few tears.", "그래. 그 딸이 내가 본 대로 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 제 몸을 지킬 줄 안다면, 무슨 해가 생기겠어?"], ["Yes,’ said Ralph, aloud, as he locked his iron safe.", "약간의 놀림, 약간의 망신, 눈물 몇 방울이지. 그래.'"], ["‘She must take her chance.", "랄프가 쇠금고를 잠그며 소리 내어 말했어."], ["She must take her chance.’", "'그 애도 운에 맡겨야지. 운에 맡겨야 하고말고.'"]], [["# CHAPTER 27", "# CHAPTER 27"]], [["Mrs. Nickleby becomes acquainted with Messrs Pyke and Pluck, whose Affection and Interest are beyond all Bounds\nMrs.", "니클비 부인은 집에 돌아온 뒤로 며칠 동안 느껴 보지 못한 자부심과 우쭐함에 흠뻑 젖었어."], ["Nickleby had not felt so proud and important for many a day, as when, on reaching home, she gave herself wholly up to the pleasant visions which had accompanied her on her way thither.", "집으로 오는 내내 함께했던 그 즐거운 상상에 푹 빠져 있었거든. 멀버리 호크 부인 — 머릿속을 떠나지 않는 생각이 바로 그거였지."], ["Lady Mulberry Hawk--that was the prevalent idea.", "멀버리 호크 부인!"], ["Lady Mulberry Hawk!--On Tuesday last, at St George’s, Hanover Square, by the Right Reverend the Bishop of Llandaff, Sir Mulberry Hawk, of Mulberry Castle, North Wales, to Catherine, only daughter of the late Nicholas Nickleby, Esquire, of Devonshire.", "지난 화요일, 하노버 광장의 세인트 조지 교회에서 랜다프 주교의 주례로, 북웨일스 멀버리 성의 멀버리 호크 경이, 데번셔의 고 니콜라스 니클비 씨의 외동딸 캐서린과 혼인하였습니다. \"어머나!\""], ["‘Upon my word!’ cried Mrs.", "니콜라스 니클비 부인이 외쳤어."], ["Nicholas Nickleby, ‘it sounds very well.’", "\"참 그럴듯하게 들리네.\""]], [["Having dispatched the ceremony, with its attendant festivities, to the perfect satisfaction of her own mind, the sanguine mother pictured to her imagination a long train of honours and distinctions which could not fail to accompany Kate in her new and brilliant sphere.", "식과 그에 딸린 잔치까지 흡족하게 마음속에서 다 치르고 나자, 희망에 부푼 어머니는 케이트가 새로 발 디딜 그 눈부신 세상에서 누리게 될 온갖 영예와 영광을 길게 그려 보았어. 물론 케이트는 궁중에 소개되겠지."], ["She would be presented at court, of course.", "케이트의 생일은 칠월 십구일인데 (니클비 부인은 괄호 치듯 속으로 덧붙였어. '새벽 세 시 십 분이었지."], ["On the anniversary of her birthday, which was upon the nineteenth of July [‘at ten minutes past three o’clock in the morning,’ thought Mrs.", "몇 시냐고 물어봤던 게 기억나니까'), 그날이 돌아오면 멀버리 경은 소작인들 모두에게 성대한 잔치를 베풀 거야. 그리고 지난 반년 치 소작료의 삼 점 오 퍼센트를 돌려주겠지."], ["Nickleby in a parenthesis, ‘for I recollect asking what o’clock it was’), Sir Mulberry would give a great feast to all his tenants, and would return them three and a half per cent on the amount of their last half-year’s rent, as would be fully described and recorded in the fashionable intelligence, to the immeasurable delight and admiration of all the readers thereof.", "그 모든 게 사교계 소식란에 빠짐없이 실리고 기록되어, 읽는 이들마다 한없는 기쁨과 감탄에 잠길 테고. 케이트의 초상화도 적어도 대여섯 권의 화보집에 실릴 거야."], ["Kate’s picture, too, would be in at least half-a-dozen of the annuals, and on the opposite page would appear, in delicate type, ‘Lines on contemplating the Portrait of Lady Mulberry Hawk.", "그리고 그 맞은편 면에는 고운 활자로 이렇게 적혀 있겠지. '멀버리 호크 부인의 초상을 바라보며 짓다."], ["By Sir Dingleby Dabber.’ Perhaps some one annual, of more comprehensive design than its fellows, might even contain a portrait of the mother of Lady Mulberry Hawk, with lines by the father of Sir Dingleby Dabber.", "딩글비 대버 경.' 어쩌면 그 가운데 한 권쯤은, 다른 것들보다 더 폭넓은 구성을 자랑하느라, 멀버리 호크 부인의 어머니 초상화까지 실어 줄지도 몰라."], ["More unlikely things had come to pass.", "거기엔 딩글비 대버 경의 아버지가 지은 시가 붙어 있겠지. 이보다 더 있을 법하지 않은 일도 일어나곤 했잖아."], ["Less interesting portraits had appeared.", "이보다 덜 흥미로운 초상화도 실린 적이 있었고. 이런 생각이 떠오르자 이 착한 부인의 얼굴에는 저도 모르게 그런 묘한 표정이 떠올랐어."], ["As this thought occurred to the good lady, her countenance unconsciously assumed that compound expression of simpering and sleepiness which, being common to all such portraits, is perhaps one reason why they are always so charming and agreeable.", "새침하게 웃는 듯하면서도 졸린 듯한 표정 말이야. 그런 초상화에는 죄다 그런 표정이 담기는데, 어쩌면 그래서 그런 초상화들이 늘 그렇게 매력적이고 보기 좋은지도 몰라."]], [["With such triumphs of aerial architecture did Mrs.", "니클비 부인은 랄프의 지체 높은 친구들에게 우연히 소개받은 그날 저녁 내내 이런 허공에 짓는 누각의 영광에 푹 잠겨 있었어."], ["Nickleby occupy the whole evening after her accidental introduction to Ralph’s titled friends; and dreams, no less prophetic and equally promising, haunted her sleep that night.", "그리고 그날 밤 잠자리에서도 그 못지않게 예언 같고 희망찬 꿈이 그녀를 떠나지 않았지. 다음 날 부인은 여전히 같은 생각에 잠긴 채 — 잠과 햇빛에 어쩌면 조금쯤 누그러졌겠지만 — 소박한 저녁상을 차리고 있었어."], ["She was preparing for her frugal dinner next day, still occupied with the same ideas--a little softened down perhaps by sleep and daylight--when the girl who attended her, partly for company, and partly to assist in the household affairs, rushed into the room in unwonted agitation, and announced that two gentlemen were waiting in the passage for permission to walk upstairs.", "그때 부인의 시중을 드는 처녀 아이가 — 말동무도 되고 집안일도 거들려고 두었지 — 평소답지 않게 잔뜩 들떠서 방으로 뛰어들어 왔어. 그러고는 신사 두 분이 복도에서 위층으로 올라가도 되는지 허락을 기다리고 있다고 알렸지."]], [["‘Bless my heart!’ cried Mrs.", "\"어머나 세상에!\""], ["Nickleby, hastily arranging her cap and front, ‘if it should be--dear me, standing in the passage all this time--why don’t you go and ask them to walk up, you stupid thing?’", "니클비 부인이 외치며 모자와 앞머리를 부랴부랴 매만졌어. \"혹시 그분들이라면 — 아이고, 이 한참을 복도에 세워 두다니 — 너는 어째서 가서 올라오시라고 안 했니, 이 멍청한 것아?\""]], [["While the girl was gone on this errand, Mrs.", "처녀 아이가 그 심부름을 하러 간 사이, 니클비 부인은 먹고 마신 흔적을 죄다 후다닥 찬장 속에 쓸어 넣었어."], ["Nickleby hastily swept into a cupboard all vestiges of eating and drinking; which she had scarcely done, and seated herself with looks as collected as she could assume, when two gentlemen, both perfect strangers, presented themselves.", "그러고는 채 자리에 앉아 최대한 차분한 표정을 짓기가 무섭게, 둘 다 생판 모르는 신사 둘이 모습을 드러냈지."]], [["‘How do you DO?’ said one gentleman, laying great stress on the last word of the inquiry.", "\"안녕하시오?\" 한 신사가 물음의 끝말에 잔뜩 힘을 주며 말했어."]], [["‘HOW do you do?’ said the other gentleman, altering the emphasis, as if to give variety to the salutation.", "\"안녕하시오?\" 다른 신사가 강세를 바꿔서 말했어. 인사에 변화를 주려는 모양이었지."]], [["Mrs.", "니클비 부인은 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고 미소 지었어."], ["Nickleby curtseyed and smiled, and curtseyed again, and remarked, rubbing her hands as she did so, that she hadn’t the--really--the honour to--", "그러고는 또 한 번 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는, 두 손을 비비며 이렇게 운을 뗐어. 자기는 — 정말이지 — 두 분을 알게 되는 영광을 —"]], [["‘To know us,’ said the first gentleman.", "\"누리지 못했다는 말씀이지요.\" 첫 번째 신사가 말했어."], ["‘The loss has been ours, Mrs Nickleby.", "\"손해는 저희 쪽이 봤습니다, 니클비 부인."], ["Has the loss been ours, Pyke?’", "손해를 본 게 우리 쪽이지, 파이크?\""]], [["‘It has, Pluck,’ answered the other gentleman.", "\"그렇고말고, 플럭.\" 다른 신사가 대답했어."]], [["‘We have regretted it very often, I believe, Pyke?’ said the first gentleman.", "\"우리가 그걸 참 자주 안타까워했지, 그렇지, 파이크?\" 첫 번째 신사가 말했어."]], [["‘Very often, Pluck,’ answered the second.", "\"아주 자주 그랬지, 플럭.\" 두 번째 신사가 대답했어."]], [["‘But now,’ said the first gentleman, ‘now we have the happiness we have pined and languished for.", "\"하지만 이제는,\" 첫 번째 신사가 말했어."], ["Have we pined and languished for this happiness, Pyke, or have we not?’", "\"이제는 그토록 애타게 기다리던 행복을 누리게 됐어. 우리가 이 행복을 애타게 기다려 왔는가, 안 기다려 왔는가, 파이크?\""]], [["‘You know we have, Pluck,’ said Pyke, reproachfully.", "\"기다려 왔다는 걸 자네도 알잖나, 플럭.\" 파이크가 나무라듯 말했어."]], [["‘You hear him, ma’am?’ said Mr.", "\"들으셨지요, 부인?\""], ["Pluck, looking round; ‘you hear the unimpeachable testimony of my friend Pyke--that reminds me,--formalities, formalities, must not be neglected in civilised society.", "플럭 씨가 둘러보며 말했어. \"제 친구 파이크의 흠잡을 데 없는 증언을 들으셨지요 — 아, 그러고 보니, 격식이지요, 격식을 차리는 건 문명 사회에서 소홀히 해서는 안 되는 거니까요."], ["Pyke--Mrs. Nickleby.’", "파이크 — 니클비 부인이시네.\""]], [["Mr. Pyke laid his hand upon his heart, and bowed low.", "파이크 씨는 가슴에 손을 얹고 깊이 허리를 숙였어."]], [["‘Whether I shall introduce myself with the same formality,’ said Mr Pluck--‘whether I shall say myself that my name is Pluck, or whether I shall ask my friend Pyke (who being now regularly introduced, is competent to the office) to state for me, Mrs.", "\"제 소개도 똑같이 격식을 차려서 할지,\" 플럭 씨가 말했어."], ["Nickleby, that my name is Pluck; whether I shall claim your acquaintance on the plain ground of the strong interest I take in your welfare, or whether I shall make myself known to you as the friend of Sir Mulberry Hawk--these, Mrs Nickleby, are considerations which I leave to you to determine.’", "\"제 이름이 플럭이라고 제 입으로 말씀드릴지, 아니면 제 친구 파이크에게 — 이제 정식으로 소개를 받았으니 그럴 자격이 있지요 — 제 이름이 플럭이라고 부인께 대신 말해 달라고 부탁할지, 부인의 안녕에 제가 품은 그 깊은 관심을 그냥 내세워 알고 지내자고 청할지, 아니면 멀버리 호크 경의 친구로 저를 소개할지 — 니클비 부인, 이런 건 다 부인께서 정하시도록 맡기겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Any friend of Sir Mulberry Hawk’s requires no better introduction to me,’ observed Mrs.", "\"멀버리 호크 경의 친구라면 저한테는 그보다 더 좋은 소개가 필요 없지요.\""], ["Nickleby, graciously.", "니클비 부인이 너그럽게 말했어."]], [["‘It is delightful to hear you say so,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇게 말씀해 주시니 정말 기쁩니다.\""], ["Pluck, drawing a chair close to Mrs. Nickleby, and sitting himself down.", "플럭 씨가 의자를 니클비 부인 가까이 끌어다 앉으며 말했어."], ["‘It is refreshing to know that you hold my excellent friend, Sir Mulberry, in such high esteem.", "\"제 훌륭한 친구 멀버리 경을 그렇게 높이 우러러보신다니 마음이 다 시원해지는군요."], ["A word in your ear, Mrs. Nickleby.", "귀에 대고 한마디만 하겠습니다, 니클비 부인."], ["When Sir Mulberry knows it, he will be a happy man--I say, Mrs. Nickleby, a happy man.", "멀버리 경이 이걸 알면 행복해할 겁니다 — 제 말씀은요, 니클비 부인, 행복한 사람이 될 거라고요."], ["Pyke, be seated.’", "파이크, 앉게나.\""]], [["‘MY good opinion,’ said Mrs.", "\"제가 좋게 본다는 게,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby, and the poor lady exulted in the idea that she was marvellously sly,--‘my good opinion can be of very little consequence to a gentleman like Sir Mulberry.’", "그리고 이 가엾은 부인은 자기가 놀랍도록 영리하다는 생각에 우쭐했지. \"제가 좋게 본다는 게 멀버리 경 같은 신사분께야 별 대수롭지도 않은 일이겠지요.\""]], [["‘Of little consequence!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"별 대수롭지 않다니요!\""], ["Pluck.", "플럭 씨가 외쳤어."], ["‘Pyke, of what consequence to our friend, Sir Mulberry, is the good opinion of Mrs. Nickleby?’", "\"파이크, 니클비 부인이 좋게 봐 주시는 게 우리 친구 멀버리 경한테 얼마나 대수로운 일인가?\""]], [["‘Of what consequence?’ echoed Pyke.", "\"얼마나 대수로운 일이냐고?\" 파이크가 따라 외쳤어."]], [["‘Ay,’ repeated Pluck; ‘is it of the greatest consequence?’", "\"그래,\" 플럭이 다시 말했어. \"그게 무엇보다 대수로운 일이지 않나?\""]], [["‘Of the very greatest consequence,’ replied Pyke.", "\"무엇보다 더없이 대수로운 일이지.\" 파이크가 대답했어."]], [["‘Mrs. Nickleby cannot be ignorant,’ said Mr.", "\"니클비 부인께서도 모르실 리 없겠지요,\""], ["Pluck, ‘of the immense impression which that sweet girl has--’", "플럭 씨가 말했어. \"그 사랑스러운 따님이 남긴 그 엄청난 인상을 —\""]], [["‘Pluck!’ said his friend, ‘beware!’", "\"플럭!\" 친구가 말했어. \"조심하게!\""]], [["‘Pyke is right,’ muttered Mr.", "\"파이크 말이 맞아.\""], ["Pluck, after a short pause; ‘I was not to mention it.", "플럭 씨가 잠깐 입을 다물었다가 중얼거렸어. \"그 얘긴 꺼내면 안 되는 거였지."], ["Pyke is very right.", "파이크 말이 아주 맞아."], ["Thank you, Pyke.’", "고맙네, 파이크.\""]], [["‘Well now, really,’ thought Mrs.", "'어머나, 이거 정말,'"], ["Nickleby within herself.", "니클비 부인은 속으로 생각했어."], ["‘Such delicacy as that, I never saw!’", "'저런 섬세함은 내 평생 처음 보네!'"]], [["Mr. Pluck, after feigning to be in a condition of great embarrassment for some minutes, resumed the conversation by entreating Mrs.", "플럭 씨는 몇 분 동안 몹시 난처한 척하더니, 다시 말문을 열었어."], ["Nickleby to take no heed of what he had inadvertently said--to consider him imprudent, rash, injudicious.", "자기가 무심코 한 말은 부디 마음에 두지 말아 달라고 니클비 부인에게 사정했지. 자기를 경솔하고, 성급하고, 분별없는 사람으로 여겨 달라면서 말이야."], ["The only stipulation he would make in his own favour was, that she should give him credit for the best intentions.", "다만 자기에게 유리하게 한 가지만 부탁한다면, 자기 뜻만큼은 더없이 좋았다고 믿어 달라는 거였어."]], [["‘But when,’ said Mr.", "\"하지만,\" 플럭 씨가 말했어."], ["Pluck, ‘when I see so much sweetness and beauty on the one hand, and so much ardour and devotion on the other, I--pardon me, Pyke, I didn’t intend to resume that theme.", "\"이쪽에는 이토록 사랑스럽고 아름다운 분이 계시고, 저쪽에는 이토록 뜨겁고 헌신적인 마음이 있는 걸 보면, 저는 — 미안하네, 파이크, 그 얘길 또 꺼낼 생각은 없었어."], ["Change the subject, Pyke.’", "화제를 바꾸세, 파이크.\""]], [["‘We promised Sir Mulberry and Lord Frederick,’ said Pyke, ‘that we’d call this morning and inquire whether you took any cold last night.’", "\"멀버리 경과 프레더릭 경께 약속드렸거든요,\" 파이크가 말했어. \"오늘 아침에 들러서 어젯밤에 감기라도 드시지 않았는지 여쭤보겠다고요.\""]], [["‘Not the least in the world last night, sir,’ replied Mrs.", "\"어젯밤엔 조금도 그런 일 없었답니다, 선생님.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어."], ["Nickleby, ‘with many thanks to his lordship and Sir Mulberry for doing me the honour to inquire; not the least--which is the more singular, as I really am very subject to colds, indeed--very subject.", "\"이렇게 여쭤봐 주시는 영광을 베푸시니 경과 멀버리 경께 깊이 감사드려요. 조금도 그런 일 없었어요 — 사실 더 신기한 게, 저는 정말이지 감기에 아주 잘 걸리거든요, 정말 잘 걸려요."], ["I had a cold once,’ said Mrs.", "한번은 감기에 걸린 적이 있었는데,\""], ["Nickleby, ‘I think it was in the year eighteen hundred and seventeen; let me see, four and five are nine, and--yes, eighteen hundred and seventeen, that I thought I never should get rid of; actually and seriously, that I thought I never should get rid of.", "니클비 부인이 말했어. \"아마 천팔백십칠 년이었지 싶어요."], ["I was only cured at last by a remedy that I don’t know whether you ever happened to hear of, Mr.", "어디 보자, 사하고 오를 더하면 구고, 그리고 — 그래요, 천팔백십칠 년이었어요. 도무지 떨어지질 않을 줄 알았다니까요."], ["Pluck.", "정말이지 진지하게, 영영 못 나을 줄 알았어요."], ["You have a gallon of water as hot as you can possibly bear it, with a pound of salt, and sixpen’orth of the finest bran, and sit with your head in it for twenty minutes every night just before going to bed; at least, I don’t mean your head--your feet.", "결국은 어떤 치료법으로 겨우 나았는데, 플럭 씨, 혹시 들어 보셨는지 모르겠네요. 견딜 수 있는 한 최대로 뜨거운 물 한 갤런에다, 소금 한 파운드하고, 육 펜스어치 고운 밀기울을 넣어요."], ["It’s a most extraordinary cure--a most extraordinary cure.", "그러고는 매일 밤 잠들기 직전에 거기에 머리를 담그고 이십 분 동안 앉아 있는 거예요. 아니, 머리가 아니라 발을요."], ["I used it for the first time, I recollect, the day after Christmas Day, and by the middle of April following the cold was gone.", "정말 놀라운 치료법이에요 — 정말 놀라운 치료법이지요. 크리스마스 다음 날 처음 해 봤던 게 기억나는데, 이듬해 사월 중순쯤 되니까 감기가 싹 가셨더라고요."], ["It seems quite a miracle when you come to think of it, for I had it ever since the beginning of September.’", "가만 생각해 보면 거의 기적 같은 일이에요. 그 감기를 구월 초부터 달고 있었으니까요.\""]], [["‘What an afflicting calamity!’ said Mr.", "\"이렇게 가슴 아픈 재난이 다 있나요!\""], ["Pyke.", "파이크 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Perfectly horrid!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"정말 끔찍하네요!\""], ["Pluck.", "플럭 씨가 외쳤어."]], [["‘But it’s worth the pain of hearing, only to know that Mrs.", "\"하지만 니클비 부인이 다 나으셨다는 것만 알아도 그 고생담을 들을 만한 가치가 있지 않나, 플럭?\""], ["Nickleby recovered it, isn’t it, Pluck?’ cried Mr. Pyke.", "파이크 씨가 외쳤어."]], [["‘That is the circumstance which gives it such a thrilling interest,’ replied Mr.", "\"바로 그 점 때문에 이 이야기가 그토록 짜릿하게 흥미로운 거지.\""], ["Pluck.", "플럭 씨가 대답했어."]], [["‘But come,’ said Pyke, as if suddenly recollecting himself; ‘we must not forget our mission in the pleasure of this interview.", "\"하지만 이런,\" 파이크가 갑자기 정신이 든 듯 말했어."], ["We come on a mission, Mrs.", "\"이렇게 즐겁게 만나 뵙느라 우리 임무를 잊어선 안 되지."], ["Nickleby.’", "저희는 임무를 띠고 왔답니다, 니클비 부인.\""]], [["‘On a mission,’ exclaimed that good lady, to whose mind a definite proposal of marriage for Kate at once presented itself in lively colours.", "\"임무라고요.\" 이 착한 부인이 외쳤어. 그녀의 머릿속에는 곧장 케이트에게 들어온 분명한 청혼이 생생한 빛깔로 떠올랐지."]], [["‘From Sir Mulberry,’ replied Pyke.", "\"멀버리 경께서 보내셨어요.\" 파이크가 대답했어."], ["‘You must be very dull here.’", "\"여기서 무척 적적하시겠지요.\""]], [["‘Rather dull, I confess,’ said Mrs.", "\"좀 적적하긴 해요, 솔직히.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘We bring the compliments of Sir Mulberry Hawk, and a thousand entreaties that you’ll take a seat in a private box at the play tonight,’ said Mr.", "\"멀버리 호크 경의 인사를 전해 드리려고요. 그리고 오늘 밤 연극을 보러 개인 관람석에 앉아 주십사 천 번이고 간청하신답니다.\""], ["Pluck.", "플럭 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Oh dear!’ said Mrs.", "\"어머나!\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby, ‘I never go out at all, never.’", "\"저는 외출이라곤 통 안 해요, 도통 안 한다니까요.\""]], [["‘And that is the very reason, my dear Mrs.", "\"바로 그게, 니클비 부인, 오늘 밤 외출을 하셔야 할 이유랍니다.\""], ["Nickleby, why you should go out tonight,’ retorted Mr. Pluck.", "플럭 씨가 받아쳤어."], ["‘Pyke, entreat Mrs. Nickleby.’", "\"파이크, 니클비 부인께 간청드리게.\""]], [["‘Oh, pray do,’ said Pyke.", "\"오, 제발 그렇게 해 주세요.\" 파이크가 말했어."]], [["‘You positively must,’ urged Pluck.", "\"꼭 그러셔야 합니다.\" 플럭이 채근했어."]], [["‘You are very kind,’ said Mrs.", "\"두 분 참 친절하시네요.\""], ["Nickleby, hesitating; ‘but--’", "니클비 부인이 머뭇거리며 말했어. \"하지만 —\""]], [["‘There’s not a but in the case, my dear Mrs.", "\"이 일에 '하지만'은 없습니다, 니클비 부인.\""], ["Nickleby,’ remonstrated Mr Pluck; ‘not such a word in the vocabulary.", "플럭 씨가 따지듯 말했어. \"사전에 그런 말은 없는 거예요."], ["Your brother-in-law joins us, Lord Frederick joins us, Sir Mulberry joins us, Pyke joins us--a refusal is out of the question.", "시동생분도 함께하시고, 프레더릭 경도 함께하시고, 멀버리 경도 함께하시고, 파이크도 함께합니다 — 거절은 있을 수 없어요."], ["Sir Mulberry sends a carriage for you--twenty minutes before seven to the moment--you’ll not be so cruel as to disappoint the whole party, Mrs. Nickleby?’", "멀버리 경께서 부인을 모시러 마차를 보내실 겁니다 — 일곱 시 이십 분 전에 딱 맞춰서요 — 일행 전부를 실망시키실 만큼 매정하진 않으시겠지요, 니클비 부인?\""]], [["‘You are so very pressing, that I scarcely know what to say,’ replied the worthy lady.", "\"하도 채근하시니 무슨 말을 해야 할지 모르겠네요.\" 그 갸륵한 부인이 대답했어."]], [["‘Say nothing; not a word, not a word, my dearest madam,’ urged Mr.", "\"아무 말씀도 마세요. 한마디도, 한마디도 마세요, 더없이 친애하는 부인.\""], ["Pluck. ‘Mrs.", "플럭 씨가 채근했어."], ["Nickleby,’ said that excellent gentleman, lowering his voice, ‘there is the most trifling, the most excusable breach of confidence in what I am about to say; and yet if my friend Pyke there overheard it--such is that man’s delicate sense of honour, Mrs.", "\"니클비 부인.\" 이 훌륭한 신사가 목소리를 낮추며 말했어."], ["Nickleby--he’d have me out before dinner-time.’", "\"지금 제가 드리려는 말씀은 더없이 사소한, 더없이 용서받을 만한 비밀 누설이긴 합니다만. 그래도 저기 제 친구 파이크가 엿듣기라도 하면 — 저 사람은 명예에 관한 한 어찌나 섬세한지요, 니클비 부인 — 저녁 식사 전에 저를 결투장으로 끌어낼 겁니다.\""]], [["Mrs. Nickleby cast an apprehensive glance at the warlike Pyke, who had walked to the window; and Mr.", "니클비 부인은 호전적인 파이크 쪽을 불안한 눈길로 흘끔거렸어."], ["Pluck, squeezing her hand, went on:", "파이크는 창가로 걸어가 있었지. 그러자 플럭 씨가 부인의 손을 꼭 쥐고는 말을 이었어."]], [["‘Your daughter has made a conquest--a conquest on which I may congratulate you.", "\"따님께서 한 사람의 마음을 사로잡으셨답니다 — 제가 축하드릴 만한 정복이지요."], ["Sir Mulberry, my dear ma’am, Sir Mulberry is her devoted slave.", "멀버리 경께서는요, 친애하는 부인, 멀버리 경께서는 따님께 헌신하는 종이 되셨답니다."], ["Hem!’", "흠!\""]], [["‘Hah!’ cried Mr.", "\"하!\""], ["Pyke at this juncture, snatching something from the chimney-piece with a theatrical air.", "바로 그때 파이크 씨가 연극하듯 과장된 몸짓으로 벽난로 선반에서 뭔가를 낚아채며 외쳤어. \"이게 뭐지!"], ["‘What is this! what do I behold!’", "내가 지금 무엇을 보고 있는 거지!\""]], [["‘What DO you behold, my dear fellow?’ asked Mr.", "\"무얼 보고 있는데, 이 친구야?\""], ["Pluck.", "플럭 씨가 물었어."]], [["‘It is the face, the countenance, the expression,’ cried Mr.", "\"이건 그 얼굴, 그 자태, 그 표정이야.\""], ["Pyke, falling into his chair with a miniature in his hand; ‘feebly portrayed, imperfectly caught, but still THE face, THE countenance, THE expression.’", "파이크 씨가 손에 세밀화를 든 채 의자에 털썩 주저앉으며 외쳤어. \"어설프게 그려졌고, 온전히 담아내진 못했지만, 그래도 분명 그 얼굴, 그 자태, 그 표정이라고.\""]], [["‘I recognise it at this distance!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"이 거리에서도 내가 알아보겠어!\""], ["Pluck in a fit of enthusiasm.", "플럭 씨가 한껏 열에 들떠 외쳤어."], ["‘Is it not, my dear madam, the faint similitude of--’", "\"친애하는 부인, 이게 혹시 그분의 희미한 모습이 아닌지요 —\""]], [["‘It is my daughter’s portrait,’ said Mrs.", "\"그건 제 딸 초상화예요.\""], ["Nickleby, with great pride.", "니클비 부인이 무척 자랑스럽게 말했어."], ["And so it was.", "정말 그랬지."], ["And little Miss La Creevy had brought it home for inspection only two nights before.", "라 크리비 아가씨가 봐 달라며 그걸 집에 가져온 게 바로 이틀 전 밤이었거든."]], [["Mr. Pyke no sooner ascertained that he was quite right in his conjecture, than he launched into the most extravagant encomiums of the divine original; and in the warmth of his enthusiasm kissed the picture a thousand times, while Mr.", "파이크 씨는 자기 짐작이 딱 맞았다는 걸 확인하기가 무섭게, 그 거룩한 원본을 향해 더없이 호들갑스러운 찬사를 쏟아내기 시작했어."], ["Pluck pressed Mrs.", "그러고는 열에 들떠서 그 초상화에 천 번이고 입을 맞췄지. 그동안 플럭 씨는 니클비 부인의 손을 자기 가슴에 꼭 갖다 대고는, 그런 따님을 두신 걸 축하했어."], ["Nickleby’s hand to his heart, and congratulated her on the possession of such a daughter, with so much earnestness and affection, that the tears stood, or seemed to stand, in his eyes. Poor Mrs.", "어찌나 진심 어리고 다정하게 하던지, 두 눈에 눈물이 고였지, 적어도 고인 것처럼 보였어."], ["Nickleby, who had listened in a state of enviable complacency at first, became at length quite overpowered by these tokens of regard for, and attachment to, the family; and even the servant girl, who had peeped in at the door, remained rooted to the spot in astonishment at the ecstasies of the two friendly visitors.", "가엾은 니클비 부인은 처음엔 더없이 흐뭇한 마음으로 듣고 있다가, 마침내 이 가족을 향한 깊은 존중과 애정의 표시에 그만 완전히 압도되고 말았어. 문틈으로 들여다보던 처녀 아이마저, 이 두 다정한 손님의 황홀경에 놀라서 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서 있었지."]], [["By degrees these raptures subsided, and Mrs.", "이 황홀경도 차츰 가라앉았어. 그러자 니클비 부인은 기울어 버린 가세를 한탄하며 손님들을 즐겁게 해 주기 시작했지."], ["Nickleby went on to entertain her guests with a lament over her fallen fortunes, and a picturesque account of her old house in the country: comprising a full description of the different apartments, not forgetting the little store-room, and a lively recollection of how many steps you went down to get into the garden, and which way you turned when you came out at the parlour door, and what capital fixtures there were in the kitchen.", "시골에 있던 옛 집을 그림처럼 늘어놓으면서 말이야. 여러 방을 하나하나 빠짐없이 묘사했고, 자그마한 곳간도 잊지 않았어."], ["This last reflection naturally conducted her into the wash-house, where she stumbled upon the brewing utensils, among which she might have wandered for an hour, if the mere mention of those implements had not, by an association of ideas, instantly reminded Mr.", "정원으로 내려가려면 계단을 몇 개나 밟아야 했는지, 거실 문으로 나오면 어느 쪽으로 꺾어야 했는지, 부엌에 얼마나 훌륭한 붙박이 세간이 있었는지도 생생하게 떠올렸지. 이 마지막 회상은 자연스레 부인을 세탁실로 이끌었고, 거기서 양조 도구에 발이 걸렸어."], ["Pyke that he was ‘amazing thirsty.’", "그 도구들 사이를 한 시간은 헤맬 뻔했는데, 그 연장 이야기가 나오자마자 생각이 꼬리에 꼬리를 물어 파이크 씨가 '엄청나게 목이 마르다'는 걸 퍼뜩 떠올린 게 다행이었지."]], [["‘And I’ll tell you what,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 이렇게 하지요.\" 파이크 씨가 말했어."], ["Pyke; ‘if you’ll send round to the public-house for a pot of milk half-and-half, positively and actually I’ll drink it.’", "\"선술집에 사람을 보내서 우유 반 맥주 반 섞은 걸 한 단지 받아 오게 해 주시면, 진짜 정말로 제가 그걸 마시겠습니다.\""]], [["And positively and actually Mr.", "그러고는 진짜 정말로 파이크 씨는 그걸 마셨어. 플럭 씨도 거들었지."], ["Pyke DID drink it, and Mr.", "니클비 부인은 두 가지에 번갈아 감탄하며 그 광경을 지켜봤어."], ["Pluck helped him, while Mrs.", "하나는 두 사람이 그렇게 격을 낮춰 주는 것, 또 하나는 백랍 단지에 척척 맞춰 마셔 대는 솜씨였지. 이 신기해 보이는 일을 풀어 보자면 이렇게 말할 수 있을 거야."], ["Nickleby looked on in divided admiration of the condescension of the two, and the aptitude with which they accommodated themselves to the pewter-pot; in explanation of which seeming marvel it may be here observed, that gentlemen who, like Messrs Pyke and Pluck, live upon their wits (or not so much, perhaps, upon the presence of their own wits as upon the absence of wits in other people) are occasionally reduced to very narrow shifts and straits, and are at such periods accustomed to regale themselves in a very simple and primitive manner.", "파이크 씨와 플럭 씨처럼 제 머리로 먹고사는 신사들은 — 아니, 어쩌면 제 머리가 있어서라기보다 남들 머리가 없는 걸 이용해 먹고사는 거지만 — 가끔은 아주 군색하고 옹색한 처지로 내몰리기도 하거든. 그런 때에는 아주 소박하고 원시적인 방식으로 즐기는 데 익숙해진단 말이지."]], [["‘At twenty minutes before seven, then,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 일곱 시 이십 분 전에,\" 파이크 씨가 일어서며 말했어."], ["Pyke, rising, ‘the coach will be here.", "\"마차가 여기 올 겁니다."], ["One more look--one little look--at that sweet face.", "한 번만 더 — 아주 잠깐만 더 — 그 사랑스러운 얼굴을 보겠습니다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["here it is.", "여기 있구나. 변함없이, 한결같이!\""], ["Unmoved, unchanged!’ This, by the way, was a very remarkable circumstance, miniatures being liable to so many changes of expression--‘Oh, Pluck!", "그런데 말이지, 이건 참 놀라운 일이었어. 세밀화란 게 표정이 그렇게 자주 바뀌게 마련인데도 말이야."], ["Pluck!’", "\"오, 플럭! 플럭!\""]], [["Mr. Pluck made no other reply than kissing Mrs.", "플럭 씨는 다른 대답 없이 그저 니클비 부인의 손에 입을 맞췄어."], ["Nickleby’s hand with a great show of feeling and attachment; Mr.", "잔뜩 감정에 겨워하고 애정을 드러내는 시늉을 하면서 말이야."], ["Pyke having done the same, both gentlemen hastily withdrew.", "파이크 씨도 똑같이 하고 나서, 두 신사는 황급히 물러갔어."]], [["Mrs. Nickleby was commonly in the habit of giving herself credit for a pretty tolerable share of penetration and acuteness, but she had never felt so satisfied with her own sharp-sightedness as she did that day.", "니클비 부인은 평소 자기가 통찰력과 예리함을 제법 쓸 만하게 지녔다고 자부하는 버릇이 있었어."], ["She had found it all out the night before.", "그래도 그날만큼 제 눈썰미에 흡족했던 적은 없었지."], ["She had never seen Sir Mulberry and Kate together--never even heard Sir Mulberry’s name--and yet hadn’t she said to herself from the very first, that she saw how the case stood?", "어젯밤에 다 알아챘잖아. 멀버리 경과 케이트가 함께 있는 걸 본 적도 없고 — 멀버리 경 이름조차 들어 본 적이 없었는데 — 그런데도 처음부터 속으로, 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 다 보인다고 말하지 않았던가?"], ["and what a triumph it was, for there was now no doubt about it.", "이 얼마나 통쾌한 일이야. 이제 의심할 여지가 전혀 없으니 말이지."], ["If these flattering attentions to herself were not sufficient proofs, Sir Mulberry’s confidential friend had suffered the secret to escape him in so many words.", "자기한테 쏟아진 이 알랑거리는 관심이 충분한 증거가 아니라 해도, 멀버리 경의 막역한 친구가 그 비밀을 그렇게 또박또박 입 밖에 내고 말았잖아. \"난 그 다정한 플럭 씨한테 완전히 반해 버렸어, 정말이지.\""], ["‘I am quite in love with that dear Mr Pluck, I declare I am,’ said Mrs. Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."]], [["There was one great source of uneasiness in the midst of this good fortune, and that was the having nobody by, to whom she could confide it. Once or twice she almost resolved to walk straight to Miss La Creevy’s and tell it all to her. ‘But I don’t know,’ thought Mrs Nickleby; ‘she is a very worthy person, but I am afraid too much beneath Sir Mulberry’s station for us to make a companion of. Poor thing!’ Acting upon this grave consideration she rejected the idea of taking the little portrait painter into her confidence, and contented herself with holding out sundry vague and mysterious hopes of preferment to the servant girl, who received these obscure hints of dawning greatness with much veneration and respect.", "이 행운 한가운데에도 큰 근심거리가 하나 있었어. 바로 이걸 털어놓을 사람이 곁에 아무도 없다는 거였지. 한두 번은 라 크리비 아가씨한테 곧장 걸어가 다 얘기해 버릴까 하고 마음을 먹기도 했어. '하지만 글쎄,' 니클비 부인은 생각했어. '그 사람은 아주 갸륵한 사람이긴 한데, 멀버리 경의 지체에 비하면 너무 낮아서 우리가 벗으로 삼기엔 좀 그렇지. 가엾기도 하지!' 이 엄숙한 고민 끝에 부인은 그 자그마한 초상화가를 믿고 속내를 털어놓는다는 생각을 접었어. 그러고는 처녀 아이한테 앞으로 출세시켜 주겠다는 막연하고 알쏭달쏭한 희망을 슬쩍슬쩍 흘리는 것으로 만족했지. 처녀 아이는 다가올 영화를 암시하는 이 알 듯 모를 듯한 말들을 깊은 존경과 우러름으로 받아들였어."]], [["Punctual to its time came the promised vehicle, which was no hackney coach, but a private chariot, having behind it a footman, whose legs, although somewhat large for his body, might, as mere abstract legs, have set themselves up for models at the Royal Academy. It was quite exhilarating to hear the clash and bustle with which he banged the door and jumped up behind after Mrs. Nickleby was in; and as that good lady was perfectly unconscious that he applied the gold-headed end of his long stick to his nose, and so telegraphed most disrespectfully to the coachman over her very head, she sat in a state of much stiffness and dignity, not a little proud of her position.", "약속한 마차는 시간에 딱 맞춰 왔어. 그건 삯마차가 아니라 개인 사두마차였지. 뒤에는 하인이 하나 서 있었는데, 그 다리가 몸에 비해 좀 굵긴 했지만, 그냥 다리 하나만 떼어 놓고 보면 왕립 아카데미의 모델로 나서도 될 만했어. 니클비 부인이 올라타자, 그 하인이 와락 요란하게 문을 쾅 닫고 뒤로 펄쩍 뛰어오르는 그 부산스러운 소리는 듣기만 해도 신이 났지. 그리고 그 하인이 긴 지팡이의 금장식 끝을 제 코에 갖다 대고는, 부인의 머리 너머로 마부에게 더없이 불손하게 신호를 보내는 걸 이 착한 부인은 전혀 알아채지 못했어. 그래서 부인은 잔뜩 뻣뻣하게 위엄을 차리고 앉아 있었지. 제 처지가 적잖이 자랑스러운 채로 말이야."]], [["At the theatre entrance there was more banging and more bustle, and there were also Messrs Pyke and Pluck waiting to escort her to her box; and so polite were they, that Mr.", "극장 입구에서는 더 요란하게 문을 쾅 닫고 더 부산을 떨었어. 거기엔 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨가 부인을 관람석으로 모시려고 기다리고 있었지."], ["Pyke threatened with many oaths to ‘smifligate’ a very old man with a lantern who accidentally stumbled in her way--to the great terror of Mrs.", "두 사람이 어찌나 깍듯하던지, 파이크 씨는 어쩌다 부인 앞길에 걸려 비틀거린 등불 든 노인을 '뭉개 버리겠다'고 온갖 험한 말로 으름장을 놓았어."], ["Nickleby, who, conjecturing more from Mr.", "니클비 부인은 그게 어찌나 무서웠던지."], ["Pyke’s excitement than any previous acquaintance with the etymology of the word that smifligation and bloodshed must be in the main one and the same thing, was alarmed beyond expression, lest something should occur.", "부인은 그 말의 어원을 미리 알고 있어서가 아니라 그저 파이크 씨가 잔뜩 흥분한 걸 보고, '뭉개 버린다'는 게 피를 보는 것과 결국 한가지일 거라고 짐작했거든. 그래서 무슨 일이 벌어질까 봐 이루 말할 수 없이 겁에 질렸지."], ["Fortunately, however, Mr.", "다행히 파이크 씨는 말로만 '뭉개는'"], ["Pyke confined himself to mere verbal smifligation, and they reached their box with no more serious interruption by the way, than a desire on the part of the same pugnacious gentleman to ‘smash’ the assistant box-keeper for happening to mistake the number.", "데서 그쳤어. 그래서 가는 길에 더 큰 방해라고는, 이 호전적인 신사가 좌석 번호를 잘못 알았다는 이유로 관람석 안내인 보조를 '박살 내겠다'고 한 것뿐이었지."]], [["Mrs. Nickleby had scarcely been put away behind the curtain of the box in an armchair, when Sir Mulberry and Lord Verisopht arrived, arrayed from the crowns of their heads to the tips of their gloves, and from the tips of their gloves to the toes of their boots, in the most elegant and costly manner.", "니클비 부인을 관람석 커튼 뒤 안락의자에 막 앉혀 놓기가 무섭게, 멀버리 경과 베리소프트 경이 도착했어."], ["Sir Mulberry was a little hoarser than on the previous day, and Lord Verisopht looked rather sleepy and queer; from which tokens, as well as from the circumstance of their both being to a trifling extent unsteady upon their legs, Mrs.", "머리끝부터 장갑 끝까지, 또 장갑 끝부터 부츠 발끝까지 더없이 우아하고 값비싸게 차려입은 차림새였지. 멀버리 경은 전날보다 목소리가 좀 더 쉬어 있었고, 베리소프트 경은 졸린 듯 좀 멍한 얼굴이었어."], ["Nickleby justly concluded that they had taken dinner.", "이런 기색에다, 두 사람 다 다리가 약간 휘청거리는 걸 보고, 니클비 부인은 두 사람이 저녁을 먹고 왔다고 옳게 결론을 내렸지."]], [["‘We have been--we have been--toasting your lovely daughter, Mrs Nickleby,’ whispered Sir Mulberry, sitting down behind her.", "\"우리가 그러니까 — 그러니까 — 부인의 사랑스러운 따님을 위해 축배를 들었답니다, 니클비 부인.\" 멀버리 경이 부인 뒤에 앉으며 속삭였어."]], [["‘Oh, ho!’ thought that knowing lady; ‘wine in, truth out.--You are very kind, Sir Mulberry.’", "'호오!' 이 눈치 빠른 부인이 생각했어. '술이 들어가니 속말이 나오는구나. — 정말 친절하시네요, 멀버리 경.'"]], [["‘No, no upon my soul!’ replied Sir Mulberry Hawk.", "\"아니, 아닙니다, 정말로!\" 멀버리 호크 경이 대답했어."], ["‘It’s you that’s kind, upon my soul it is.", "\"친절한 건 부인이시지요, 정말이지 그렇습니다."], ["It was so kind of you to come tonight.’", "오늘 밤 이렇게 와 주시다니 어찌나 고마운지요.\""]], [["‘So very kind of you to invite me, you mean, Sir Mulberry,’ replied Mrs Nickleby, tossing her head, and looking prodigiously sly.", "\"이렇게 초대해 주신 게 고마운 거겠지요, 멀버리 경.\" 니클비 부인이 고개를 까딱 젖히고는 더없이 영리한 척하며 대답했어."]], [["‘I am so anxious to know you, so anxious to cultivate your good opinion, so desirous that there should be a delicious kind of harmonious family understanding between us,’ said Sir Mulberry, ‘that you mustn’t think I’m disinterested in what I do. I’m infernal selfish; I am--upon my soul I am.’", "\"저는 부인을 알게 되기를 어찌나 바랐는지요, 부인이 저를 좋게 봐 주시기를 어찌나 바랐는지요, 우리 사이에 더없이 흐뭇하고 화목한 집안 같은 정이 흐르기를 어찌나 바랐는지요.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어. \"그러니 제가 하는 일에 사심이 없다고는 생각지 말아 주세요. 저는 끔찍하게 이기적인 놈이거든요. 정말이지 그렇습니다.\""]], [["‘I am sure you can’t be selfish, Sir Mulberry!’ replied Mrs.", "\"멀버리 경이 이기적일 리가 있나요!\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어."], ["Nickleby. ‘You have much too open and generous a countenance for that.’", "\"그러기에는 너무나 솔직하고 너그러운 얼굴을 지니셨는걸요.\""]], [["‘What an extraordinary observer you are!’ said Sir Mulberry Hawk.", "\"부인은 정말 비상한 관찰자시군요!\" 멀버리 호크 경이 말했어."]], [["‘Oh no, indeed, I don’t see very far into things, Sir Mulberry,’ replied Mrs.", "\"아유, 아니에요, 저는 정말 사물을 그리 깊이 들여다보지 못해요, 멀버리 경.\""], ["Nickleby, in a tone of voice which left the baronet to infer that she saw very far indeed.", "니클비 부인이 대답했어. 그런데 그 말투는 준남작더러 부인이 정말이지 아주 깊이 들여다본다고 짐작하게 만들었지."]], [["‘I am quite afraid of you,’ said the baronet.", "\"부인이 좀 무서울 지경입니다.\" 준남작이 말했어."], ["‘Upon my soul,’ repeated Sir Mulberry, looking round to his companions; ‘I am afraid of Mrs Nickleby.", "\"정말이지,\" 멀버리 경이 일행을 둘러보며 거듭 말했어."], ["She is so immensely sharp.’", "\"니클비 부인이 무서워. 어찌나 엄청나게 예리하신지.\""]], [["Messrs Pyke and Pluck shook their heads mysteriously, and observed together that they had found that out long ago; upon which Mrs.", "파이크 씨와 플럭 씨는 알쏭달쏭하게 고개를 끄덕이며, 자기들도 진작에 그걸 알아챘다고 입을 모았어."], ["Nickleby tittered, and Sir Mulberry laughed, and Pyke and Pluck roared.", "그러자 니클비 부인은 까르르 웃었고, 멀버리 경은 껄껄 웃었으며, 파이크와 플럭은 와하하 웃음을 터뜨렸지."]], [["‘But where’s my brother-in-law, Sir Mulberry?’ inquired Mrs.", "\"그런데 우리 시동생은 어디 있나요, 멀버리 경?\" 니클비 부인이 물었어."], ["Nickleby. ‘I shouldn’t be here without him.", "\"그이 없이는 여기 안 왔을 텐데요."], ["I hope he’s coming.’", "오고 있겠지요?\""]], [["‘Pyke,’ said Sir Mulberry, taking out his toothpick and lolling back in his chair, as if he were too lazy to invent a reply to this question.", "\"파이크,\" 멀버리 경이 이쑤시개를 꺼내 들고 의자에 등을 기대며 말했어."], ["‘Where’s Ralph Nickleby?’", "이 질문에 답을 꾸며 낼 기운조차 없다는 듯 말이야. \"랄프 니클비는 어디 있지?\""]], [["‘Pluck,’ said Pyke, imitating the baronet’s action, and turning the lie over to his friend, ‘where’s Ralph Nickleby?’", "\"플럭,\" 파이크가 준남작의 동작을 흉내 내며, 거짓말의 짐을 친구에게 넘기듯 말했어. \"랄프 니클비는 어디 있나?\""]], [["Mr. Pluck was about to return some evasive reply, when the hustle caused by a party entering the next box seemed to attract the attention of all four gentlemen, who exchanged glances of much meaning.", "플럭 씨가 막 얼버무릴 대답을 하려는데, 옆 관람석으로 한 무리가 들어오며 부산을 떠는 통에 신사 넷의 시선이 죄다 그리로 쏠리는 듯했어."], ["The new party beginning to converse together, Sir Mulberry suddenly assumed the character of a most attentive listener, and implored his friends not to breathe--not to breathe.", "네 사람은 의미심장한 눈빛을 주고받았지. 새로 온 무리가 자기들끼리 이야기를 나누기 시작하자, 멀버리 경은 별안간 더없이 귀를 쫑긋 세운 사람처럼 굴며, 친구들에게 숨도 쉬지 말라고 — 숨도 쉬지 말라고 — 사정했어."]], [["‘Why not?’ said Mrs.", "\"왜요?\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby. ‘What is the matter?’", "\"무슨 일인데요?\""]], [["‘Hush!’ replied Sir Mulberry, laying his hand on her arm.", "\"쉿!\" 멀버리 경이 부인의 팔에 손을 얹으며 대답했어."], ["‘Lord Frederick, do you recognise the tones of that voice?’", "\"프레더릭 경, 저 목소리 알아듣겠나?\""]], [["‘Deyvle take me if I didn’t think it was the voice of Miss Nickleby.’", "\"맙소사, 니클비 아가씨 목소리가 아닌가 했네.\""]], [["‘Lor, my lord!’ cried Miss Nickleby’s mama, thrusting her head round the curtain.", "\"어머, 경!\" 니클비 아가씨의 엄마가 커튼 너머로 머리를 쑥 내밀며 외쳤어."], ["‘Why actually--Kate, my dear, Kate.’", "\"어머 정말 — 케이트, 얘야, 케이트.\""]], [["‘YOU here, mama!", "\"엄마가 여기 계셨어요!"], ["Is it possible!’", "어떻게 이런 일이!\""]], [["‘Possible, my dear?", "\"이런 일이라니, 얘야?"], ["Yes.’", "그래, 여기 있단다.\""]], [["‘Why who--who on earth is that you have with you, mama?’ said Kate, shrinking back as she caught sight of a man smiling and kissing his hand.", "\"그런데 누구 — 도대체 엄마랑 같이 계신 분이 누구예요?\" 케이트가 말했어. 웃으면서 손에 입을 맞추는 남자를 보고는 움찔 물러서며 말이야."]], [["‘Who do you suppose, my dear?’ replied Mrs.", "\"누구일 것 같니, 얘야?\" 니클비 부인이 위티털리 부인 쪽으로 몸을 기울이며 대답했어."], ["Nickleby, bending towards Mrs Wititterly, and speaking a little louder for that lady’s edification. ‘There’s Mr.", "그 부인 들으라고 목소리를 조금 높여서 말이야."], ["Pyke, Mr. Pluck, Sir Mulberry Hawk, and Lord Frederick Verisopht.’", "\"파이크 씨, 플럭 씨, 멀버리 호크 경, 그리고 프레더릭 베리소프트 경이시란다.\""]], [["‘Gracious Heaven!’ thought Kate hurriedly.", "'아이고 맙소사!' 케이트가 황급히 생각했어."], ["‘How comes she in such society?’", "'엄마가 어쩌다 저런 사람들 틈에 끼셨담?'"]], [["Now, Kate thought thus SO hurriedly, and the surprise was so great, and moreover brought back so forcibly the recollection of what had passed at Ralph’s delectable dinner, that she turned extremely pale and appeared greatly agitated, which symptoms being observed by Mrs.", "그런데 케이트는 이런 생각을 어찌나 황급히 했던지, 게다가 놀라움이 어찌나 컸던지, 더구나 랄프의 그 즐거웠던 만찬에서 있었던 일이 어찌나 생생하게 되살아났던지, 얼굴이 새파랗게 질리고 몹시 동요하는 듯 보였어. 이런 기색을 본 니클비 부인은, 이 예리한 부인은 그게 다 격렬한 사랑 때문에 일어난 거라고 단박에 결론지었지."], ["Nickleby, were at once set down by that acute lady as being caused and occasioned by violent love.", "하지만 자기 눈썰미가 그렇게 빛을 발했다는 이 발견에 적잖이 흐뭇하긴 했어도, 케이트를 향한 어미의 걱정은 줄어들지 않았어."], ["But, although she was in no small degree delighted by this discovery, which reflected so much credit on her own quickness of perception, it did not lessen her motherly anxiety in Kate’s behalf; and accordingly, with a vast quantity of trepidation, she quitted her own box to hasten into that of Mrs. Wititterly.", "그래서 부인은 잔뜩 안절부절못하며 제 관람석을 떠나 위티털리 부인의 관람석으로 부랴부랴 건너갔지."], ["Mrs. Wititterly, keenly alive to the glory of having a lord and a baronet among her visiting acquaintance, lost no time in signing to Mr.", "위티털리 부인은 자기 교제 상대 가운데 경과 준남작이 끼어 있다는 그 영광에 어찌나 가슴이 뛰었던지, 지체 없이 위티털리 씨에게 문을 열라고 신호를 보냈어."], ["Wititterly to open the door, and thus it was that in less than thirty seconds Mrs. Nickleby’s party had made an irruption into Mrs.", "그렇게 해서 삼십 초도 안 되어 니클비 부인 일행은 위티털리 부인의 관람석으로 우르르 밀고 들어왔지."], ["Wititterly’s box, which it filled to the very door, there being in fact only room for Messrs Pyke and Pluck to get in their heads and waistcoats.", "관람석은 문간까지 꽉 들어찼고, 사실 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨는 머리하고 조끼나 겨우 들이밀 자리밖에 없었어."]], [["‘My dear Kate,’ said Mrs.", "\"우리 케이트야.\""], ["Nickleby, kissing her daughter affectionately.", "니클비 부인이 딸에게 다정하게 입을 맞추며 말했어."], ["‘How ill you looked a moment ago!", "\"조금 전엔 얼굴이 어쩜 그렇게 안 좋아 보였니!"], ["You quite frightened me, I declare!’", "정말이지 깜짝 놀랐잖아!\""]], [["‘It was mere fancy, mama,--the--the--reflection of the lights perhaps,’ replied Kate, glancing nervously round, and finding it impossible to whisper any caution or explanation.", "\"그냥 기분 탓이에요, 엄마 — 그러니까 — 그러니까 — 조명이 비쳐서 그랬나 봐요.\" 케이트가 대답했어. 불안하게 주위를 흘끔거리면서 말이야. 무슨 주의나 설명을 속삭일 도리가 도무지 없었거든."]], [["‘Don’t you see Sir Mulberry Hawk, my dear?’", "\"멀버리 호크 경이 안 보이니, 얘야?\""]], [["Kate bowed slightly, and biting her lip turned her head towards the stage.", "케이트는 살짝 고개를 숙이고는, 입술을 깨물며 무대 쪽으로 고개를 돌렸어."]], [["But Sir Mulberry Hawk was not to be so easily repulsed, for he advanced with extended hand; and Mrs.", "하지만 멀버리 호크 경은 그렇게 호락호락 물러설 사람이 아니었지. 그가 손을 내밀며 다가왔거든."], ["Nickleby officiously informing Kate of this circumstance, she was obliged to extend her own.", "그리고 니클비 부인이 주제넘게 이 사실을 케이트에게 일러 주는 바람에, 케이트는 어쩔 수 없이 제 손을 내밀어야 했어."], ["Sir Mulberry detained it while he murmured a profusion of compliments, which Kate, remembering what had passed between them, rightly considered as so many aggravations of the insult he had already put upon her.", "멀버리 경은 그 손을 붙잡고는 찬사를 줄줄이 늘어놓았지. 두 사람 사이에 오갔던 일을 떠올린 케이트는, 그 찬사를 그가 이미 자기에게 안긴 모욕에 모욕을 거듭 얹는 짓으로 옳게 받아들였어."], ["Then followed the recognition of Lord Verisopht, and then the greeting of Mr.", "그다음엔 베리소프트 경과 인사를 나눴고, 그다음엔 파이크 씨의 인사, 그다음엔 플럭 씨의 인사가 이어졌어."], ["Pyke, and then that of Mr Pluck, and finally, to complete the young lady’s mortification, she was compelled at Mrs.", "그리고 마침내, 이 아가씨의 굴욕을 마무리 짓기라도 하듯, 케이트는 위티털리 부인의 청에 따라 이 역겨운 자들을 소개하는 의식을 치러야만 했어."], ["Wititterly’s request to perform the ceremony of introducing the odious persons, whom she regarded with the utmost indignation and abhorrence.", "더없이 분하고 진저리 나는 마음으로 바라보던 그자들을 말이야."]], [["‘Mrs. Wititterly is delighted,’ said Mr.", "\"위티털리 부인이 기뻐하고 있습니다.\""], ["Wititterly, rubbing his hands; ‘delighted, my lord, I am sure, with this opportunity of contracting an acquaintance which, I trust, my lord, we shall improve.", "위티털리 씨가 두 손을 비비며 말했어. \"기뻐하고 있고말고요, 경."], ["Julia, my dear, you must not allow yourself to be too much excited, you must not.", "이렇게 알고 지낼 기회를 얻다니, 부디 앞으로 더 돈독해지길 바랍니다, 경. 줄리아, 여보, 너무 흥분하면 안 돼, 안 된다고."], ["Indeed you must not.", "정말 그러면 안 돼."], ["Mrs. Wititterly is of a most excitable nature, Sir Mulberry.", "위티털리 부인은 어찌나 잘 흥분하는 성미인지요, 멀버리 경."], ["The snuff of a candle, the wick of a lamp, the bloom on a peach, the down on a butterfly.", "촛불 심지, 등잔 심지, 복숭아에 핀 솜털, 나비 날개의 보드라운 가루 같달까요."], ["You might blow her away, my lord; you might blow her away.’", "후 불면 날아가 버릴 겁니다, 경. 후 불면 날아가 버릴 거예요.\""]], [["Sir Mulberry seemed to think that it would be a great convenience if the lady could be blown away.", "멀버리 경은 이 부인이 후 불어서 날아가 버린다면 아주 편리하겠다고 생각하는 눈치였어."], ["He said, however, that the delight was mutual, and Lord Verisopht added that it was mutual, whereupon Messrs Pyke and Pluck were heard to murmur from the distance that it was very mutual indeed.", "하지만 그는 기쁨은 서로 마찬가지라고 말했고, 베리소프트 경도 서로 마찬가지라고 덧붙였지. 그러자 멀리서 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨가 정말이지 서로 더없이 마찬가지라고 중얼거리는 소리가 들렸어."]], [["‘I take an interest, my lord,’ said Mrs.", "\"저는 관심이 있답니다, 경.\""], ["Wititterly, with a faint smile, ‘such an interest in the drama.’", "위티털리 부인이 희미하게 웃으며 말했어. \"연극에 그렇게나 관심이 많아요.\""]], [["‘Ye--es.", "\"그렇—군요."], ["It’s very interesting,’ replied Lord Verisopht.", "아주 흥미롭지요.\" 베리소프트 경이 대답했어."]], [["‘I’m always ill after Shakespeare,’ said Mrs.", "\"저는 셰익스피어를 보고 나면 늘 앓아눕는답니다.\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어."], ["Wititterly.", "\"다음 날엔 거의 살아 있지도 못해요."], ["‘I scarcely exist the next day; I find the reaction so very great after a tragedy, my lord, and Shakespeare is such a delicious creature.’", "비극을 보고 나면 그 후유증이 어찌나 큰지요, 경. 게다가 셰익스피어는 어찌나 황홀한 사람인지요.\""]], [["‘Ye--es!’ replied Lord Verisopht.", "\"그렇—군요!\" 베리소프트 경이 대답했어."], ["‘He was a clayver man.’", "\"참 영리한 사람이었지요.\""]], [["‘Do you know, my lord,’ said Mrs.", "\"있잖아요, 경.\""], ["Wititterly, after a long silence, ‘I find I take so much more interest in his plays, after having been to that dear little dull house he was born in!", "위티털리 부인이 한참 입을 다물었다가 말했어. \"셰익스피어가 태어난 그 사랑스럽고 자그마한 적적한 집에 다녀온 뒤로는, 그이의 연극에 훨씬 더 관심이 가지 뭐예요!"], ["Were you ever there, my lord?’", "거기 가 보신 적 있으세요, 경?\""]], [["‘No, nayver,’ replied Verisopht.", "\"아니요, 한 번도요.\" 베리소프트가 대답했어."]], [["‘Then really you ought to go, my lord,’ returned Mrs.", "\"그러면 정말 한번 가 보셔야 해요, 경.\""], ["Wititterly, in very languid and drawling accents.", "위티털리 부인이 더없이 나른하고 느릿한 말투로 받았어."], ["‘I don’t know how it is, but after you’ve seen the place and written your name in the little book, somehow or other you seem to be inspired; it kindles up quite a fire within one.’", "\"어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠지만, 그 장소를 보고 그 작은 방명록에 이름을 적고 나면, 어쩐지 영감이 솟는 것 같달까요. 마음속에 불길이 확 타오르는 거예요.\""]], [["‘Ye--es!’ replied Lord Verisopht, ‘I shall certainly go there.’", "\"그렇—군요!\" 베리소프트 경이 대답했어. \"꼭 가 봐야겠군요.\""]], [["‘Julia, my life,’ interposed Mr.", "\"줄리아, 내 사랑.\" 위티털리 씨가 끼어들었어."], ["Wititterly, ‘you are deceiving his lordship--unintentionally, my lord, she is deceiving you.", "\"당신은 지금 경을 속이고 있는 거야 — 일부러 그런 건 아닙니다, 경, 이 사람이 경을 속이고 있다고요."], ["It is your poetical temperament, my dear--your ethereal soul--your fervid imagination, which throws you into a glow of genius and excitement.", "그건 다 당신의 시적인 기질이야, 여보 — 당신의 영묘한 영혼, 당신의 뜨거운 상상력이 당신을 천재의 열기와 흥분 속으로 몰아넣는 거라고."], ["There is nothing in the place, my dear--nothing, nothing.’", "그 장소엔 아무것도 없어, 여보 — 아무것도, 아무것도 없다고.\""]], [["‘I think there must be something in the place,’ said Mrs.", "\"제 생각엔 그 장소에 뭔가 분명히 있는 것 같아요.\" 잠자코 듣고 있던 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby, who had been listening in silence; ‘for, soon after I was married, I went to Stratford with my poor dear Mr.", "\"왜냐하면, 제가 결혼하고 얼마 안 됐을 때, 가엾고 사랑스러운 우리 니클비 씨하고 같이 버밍엄에서 역마차를 타고 스트랫퍼드에 갔거든요 — 그런데 그게 역마차였나? \""], ["Nickleby, in a post-chaise from Birmingham--was it a post-chaise though?’ said Mrs.", "니클비 부인이 곰곰이 생각하며 말했어. \"그래요, 역마차였던 게 틀림없어요."], ["Nickleby, considering; ‘yes, it must have been a post-chaise, because I recollect remarking at the time that the driver had a green shade over his left eye;--in a post-chaise from Birmingham, and after we had seen Shakespeare’s tomb and birthplace, we went back to the inn there, where we slept that night, and I recollect that all night long I dreamt of nothing but a black gentleman, at full length, in plaster-of-Paris, with a lay-down collar tied with two tassels, leaning against a post and thinking; and when I woke in the morning and described him to Mr Nickleby, he said it was Shakespeare just as he had been when he was alive, which was very curious indeed.", "그때 마부가 왼쪽 눈에 초록색 가리개를 하고 있다고 제가 한마디 했던 게 기억나니까요 — 버밍엄에서 역마차를 타고 갔는데, 셰익스피어의 무덤하고 생가를 보고 나서 거기 여관으로 돌아가 그날 밤을 묵었어요. 그런데 밤새도록 다른 건 하나도 안 나오고 오로지 어떤 까만 신사 꿈만 꿨던 게 기억나요."], ["Stratford--Stratford,’ continued Mrs.", "회반죽으로 등신대로 만들어진 모습인데, 술 두 개로 묶은 접어 내린 깃을 달고, 기둥에 기대서 생각에 잠겨 있더라고요. 그래서 아침에 깨서 니클비 씨한테 그 모습을 얘기했더니, 그이가 그건 살아생전 셰익스피어 모습 그대로라고 하지 뭐예요."], ["Nickleby, considering.", "정말이지 신기한 일이었어요. 스트랫퍼드 — 스트랫퍼드,\""], ["‘Yes, I am positive about that, because I recollect I was in the family way with my son Nicholas at the time, and I had been very much frightened by an Italian image boy that very morning.", "니클비 부인이 곰곰이 생각하며 말을 이었어. \"그래요, 저는 그것만은 확실해요."], ["In fact, it was quite a mercy, ma’am,’ added Mrs.", "왜냐하면 그때 제가 아들 니콜라스를 가진 몸이었던 게 기억나거든요. 그리고 바로 그날 아침에 어떤 이탈리아 석고상 파는 소년 때문에 무척 놀랐었지요."], ["Nickleby, in a whisper to Mrs.", "사실 그건 참 다행스러운 일이었어요, 부인.\" 니클비 부인이 위티털리 부인에게 속삭이듯 덧붙였어."], ["Wititterly, ‘that my son didn’t turn out to be a Shakespeare, and what a dreadful thing that would have been!’", "\"우리 아들이 셰익스피어가 되지 않았으니 말이에요. 그랬으면 얼마나 끔찍한 일이었겠어요!\""]], [["When Mrs. Nickleby had brought this interesting anecdote to a close, Pyke and Pluck, ever zealous in their patron’s cause, proposed the adjournment of a detachment of the party into the next box; and with so much skill were the preliminaries adjusted, that Kate, despite all she could say or do to the contrary, had no alternative but to suffer herself to be led away by Sir Mulberry Hawk.", "니클비 부인이 이 흥미로운 일화를 끝맺자, 늘 제 후원자의 일에 열심인 파이크와 플럭은 일행 가운데 한 패를 옆 관람석으로 옮기자고 제안했어."], ["Her mother and Mr.", "어찌나 솜씨 좋게 사전 준비가 짜였던지, 케이트는 아무리 안 된다고 말하고 애를 써도 어쩔 도리가 없었어."], ["Pluck accompanied them, but the worthy lady, pluming herself upon her discretion, took particular care not so much as to look at her daughter during the whole evening, and to seem wholly absorbed in the jokes and conversation of Mr.", "결국 멀버리 호크 경에게 이끌려 가는 걸 참고 따를 수밖에 없었지. 어머니와 플럭 씨가 그들을 따라갔는데, 이 갸륵한 부인은 제 분별력을 으스대느라 저녁 내내 딸을 쳐다보지조차 않으려고 각별히 신경을 썼어."], ["Pluck, who, having been appointed sentry over Mrs Nickleby for that especial purpose, neglected, on his side, no possible opportunity of engrossing her attention.", "그러고는 플럭 씨의 농담과 이야기에 푹 빠진 척했지. 플럭 씨는 바로 그 일을 위해 니클비 부인의 보초로 임명된 터라, 자기 쪽에서도 부인의 주의를 독차지할 기회라면 하나도 놓치지 않았어."]], [["Lord Frederick Verisopht remained in the next box to be talked to by Mrs Wititterly, and Mr.", "프레더릭 베리소프트 경은 옆 관람석에 남아 위티털리 부인의 이야기 상대가 되어 주었고, 파이크 씨는 곁에 붙어서 필요할 때마다 한두 마디 거들었지."], ["Pyke was in attendance to throw in a word or two when necessary. As to Mr.", "위티털리 씨로 말하자면, 그는 객석 한복판에서 충분히 바빴어."], ["Wititterly, he was sufficiently busy in the body of the house, informing such of his friends and acquaintance as happened to be there, that those two gentlemen upstairs, whom they had seen in conversation with Mrs.", "마침 거기 와 있던 친구며 지인들에게, 위층에서 위티털리 부인과 이야기를 나누는 걸 봤던 그 두 신사가 바로 그 고명한 프레더릭 베리소프트 경과 그의 막역한 친구인 쾌활한 멀버리 호크 경이라고 알리고 다녔거든."], ["W., were the distinguished Lord Frederick Verisopht and his most intimate friend, the gay Sir Mulberry Hawk--a communication which inflamed several respectable house-keepers with the utmost jealousy and rage, and reduced sixteen unmarried daughters to the very brink of despair.", "이 소식에 점잖은 집안 안주인 여럿이 더없이 질투와 분노에 불타올랐고, 시집 안 간 딸 열여섯 명이 절망의 벼랑 끝까지 내몰렸지."]], [["The evening came to an end at last, but Kate had yet to be handed downstairs by the detested Sir Mulberry; and so skilfully were the manoeuvres of Messrs Pyke and Pluck conducted, that she and the baronet were the last of the party, and were even--without an appearance of effort or design--left at some little distance behind.", "마침내 저녁 공연이 끝났어. 하지만 케이트는 아직도 그 끔찍한 멀버리 경의 손에 이끌려 아래층으로 내려가야 했지. 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨의 술수가 어찌나 교묘하게 펼쳐졌던지, 케이트와 준남작은 일행 가운데 맨 뒤에 남게 되었고, 게다가 — 일부러 꾸민 티도 안 나게 — 조금 떨어진 뒤쪽에 처지고 말았어."]], [["‘Don’t hurry, don’t hurry,’ said Sir Mulberry, as Kate hastened on, and attempted to release her arm.", "\"서두르지 마세요, 서두르지 마세요.\" 케이트가 걸음을 재촉하며 팔을 빼내려 하자 멀버리 경이 말했어."]], [["She made no reply, but still pressed forward.", "케이트는 아무 대꾸 없이 계속 앞으로 밀고 나갔어."]], [["‘Nay, then--’ coolly observed Sir Mulberry, stopping her outright.", "\"이런, 그러면 —\" 멀버리 경이 태연하게 내뱉으며 케이트를 아예 멈춰 세웠어."]], [["‘You had best not seek to detain me, sir!’ said Kate, angrily.", "\"저를 붙잡아 두려고 하지 않는 게 좋으실 거예요, 선생님!\" 케이트가 화를 내며 말했어."]], [["‘And why not?’ retorted Sir Mulberry.", "\"왜 안 되는데요?\" 멀버리 경이 받아쳤어."], ["‘My dear creature, now why do you keep up this show of displeasure?’", "\"이봐요, 사랑스러운 아가씨, 어째서 이렇게 못마땅한 척을 계속하는 거지요?\""]], [["‘SHOW!’ repeated Kate, indignantly.", "\"척이라니요!\" 케이트가 분개하며 되받았어."], ["‘How dare you presume to speak to me, sir--to address me--to come into my presence?’", "\"어떻게 감히 저한테 말을 거시는 거예요, 선생님 — 저한테 말을 붙이고 — 제 앞에 나타나시는 거예요?\""]], [["‘You look prettier in a passion, Miss Nickleby,’ said Sir Mulberry Hawk, stooping down, the better to see her face.", "\"화를 내니 더 예뻐 보이는군요, 니클비 아가씨.\" 멀버리 호크 경이 케이트의 얼굴을 더 잘 보려고 허리를 숙이며 말했어."]], [["‘I hold you in the bitterest detestation and contempt, sir,’ said Kate.", "\"저는 당신을 더없이 쓰디쓴 혐오와 경멸로 대하고 있어요, 선생님.\""], ["‘If you find any attraction in looks of disgust and aversion, you--let me rejoin my friends, sir, instantly.", "케이트가 말했어. \"역겹고 진저리 나는 표정에서 무슨 매력을 느끼신다면, 당신은 — 당장 제 친구들한테 돌아가게 해 주세요, 선생님, 지금 당장이요."], ["Whatever considerations may have withheld me thus far, I will disregard them all, and take a course that even YOU might feel, if you do not immediately suffer me to proceed.’", "지금껏 무엇이 저를 참게 했든 간에, 그걸 죄다 무시하고, 당장 저를 가게 해 주지 않으면 당신마저 뜨끔할 만한 행동을 하겠어요.\""]], [["Sir Mulberry smiled, and still looking in her face and retaining her arm, walked towards the door.", "멀버리 경은 빙긋 웃었어. 그러고는 여전히 케이트의 얼굴을 들여다보며 팔을 붙든 채 문 쪽으로 걸어갔지."]], [["‘If no regard for my sex or helpless situation will induce you to desist from this coarse and unmanly persecution,’ said Kate, scarcely knowing, in the tumult of her passions, what she said,--‘I have a brother who will resent it dearly, one day.’", "\"제 처지가 여자라는 것도, 의지할 데 없는 신세라는 것도 당신이 이 거칠고 사내답지 못한 괴롭힘을 그만두게 만들지 못한다면,\" 케이트가 말했어. 격정에 휩싸여 제가 무슨 말을 하는지도 거의 모른 채 말이야. \"저한테는 오빠가 있어요. 언젠가 그 오빠가 이걸 톡톡히 갚아 줄 거예요.\""]], [["‘Upon my soul!’ exclaimed Sir Mulberry, as though quietly communing with himself; passing his arm round her waist as he spoke, ‘she looks more beautiful, and I like her better in this mood, than when her eyes are cast down, and she is in perfect repose!’", "\"맙소사!\" 멀버리 경이 혼잣말하듯 조용히 내뱉으며 외쳤어. 그러면서 케이트의 허리에 팔을 둘렀지. \"이러니 더 아름다워 보이는걸. 눈을 내리깔고 얌전히 가만있을 때보다 이렇게 화를 낼 때가 더 마음에 들어!\""]], [["How Kate reached the lobby where her friends were waiting she never knew, but she hurried across it without at all regarding them, and disengaged herself suddenly from her companion, sprang into the coach, and throwing herself into its darkest corner burst into tears.", "케이트가 어떻게 친구들이 기다리고 있는 로비까지 갔는지는 본인도 도무지 알 수 없었어. 하지만 케이트는 그들을 거들떠보지도 않고 로비를 가로질러 황급히 달려가, 별안간 동행에게서 몸을 빼내고는 마차로 뛰어올랐지. 그러고는 마차의 가장 어두운 구석에 몸을 던지며 울음을 터뜨렸어."]], [["Messrs Pyke and Pluck, knowing their cue, at once threw the party into great commotion by shouting for the carriages, and getting up a violent quarrel with sundry inoffensive bystanders; in the midst of which tumult they put the affrighted Mrs.", "파이크 씨와 플럭 씨는 제 차례를 알고 있던 터라, 곧장 마차를 부르라고 소리치고 죄 없는 구경꾼 몇몇과 격렬하게 시비를 붙어 일행을 큰 소동에 빠뜨렸어. 이 소란 한가운데서 두 사람은 겁에 질린 니클비 부인을 사두마차에 태웠지."], ["Nickleby in her chariot, and having got her safely off, turned their thoughts to Mrs.", "그리고 부인을 무사히 떠나보내고 나서는, 위티털리 부인에게로 생각을 돌렸어."], ["Wititterly, whose attention also they had now effectually distracted from the young lady, by throwing her into a state of the utmost bewilderment and consternation.", "위티털리 부인도 더없는 어리둥절함과 경악 속에 빠뜨려 놓아서, 그 아가씨한테서 주의를 말끔히 떼어 놓은 참이었거든."], ["At length, the conveyance in which she had come rolled off too with its load, and the four worthies, being left alone under the portico, enjoyed a hearty laugh together.", "마침내 부인이 타고 온 마차도 짐을 싣고 굴러갔어. 그리고 현관 지붕 아래 덩그러니 남은 이 네 인물은 다 함께 한바탕 신나게 웃어 댔지."]], [["‘There,’ said Sir Mulberry, turning to his noble friend.", "\"거 봐.\" 멀버리 경이 지체 높은 친구를 돌아보며 말했어."], ["‘Didn’t I tell you last night that if we could find where they were going by bribing a servant through my fellow, and then established ourselves close by with the mother, these people’s honour would be our own?", "\"어젯밤에 내가 말하지 않았나? 내 하인을 시켜 저 집 하인을 매수해서 저들이 어디로 가는지 알아내고, 그다음에 어머니를 끼고 바짝 옆에 자리를 잡으면, 저 사람들 명예가 우리 손안에 들어온다고 말이야."], ["Why here it is, done in four-and-twenty hours.’", "자 봐, 스물네 시간 만에 다 됐잖아.\""]], [["‘Ye--es,’ replied the dupe.", "\"그렇—군.\" 속아 넘어간 친구가 대답했어."], ["‘But I have been tied to the old woman all ni-ight.’", "\"하지만 난 밤—새 그 늙은 부인한테 붙들려 있었잖나.\""]], [["‘Hear him,’ said Sir Mulberry, turning to his two friends.", "\"이 친구 좀 보게.\" 멀버리 경이 두 친구를 돌아보며 말했어."], ["‘Hear this discontented grumbler.", "\"이 불평불만 투성이 투덜이 좀 보라고."], ["Isn’t it enough to make a man swear never to help him in his plots and schemes again?", "이러니 다시는 이 친구 계략이고 음모고 안 돕겠다고 맹세할 만하지 않나?"], ["Isn’t it an infernal shame?’", "이거 정말 괘씸한 노릇 아니냐고?\""]], [["Pyke asked Pluck whether it was not an infernal shame, and Pluck asked Pyke; but neither answered.", "파이크는 플럭에게 이게 정말 괘씸한 노릇 아니냐고 물었고, 플럭은 파이크에게 물었어. 하지만 둘 다 대답하지 않았지."]], [["‘Isn’t it the truth?’ demanded Verisopht.", "\"그게 사실 아닌가?\" 베리소프트가 따졌어."], ["‘Wasn’t it so?’", "\"그렇지 않았나?\""]], [["‘Wasn’t it so!’ repeated Sir Mulberry.", "\"그렇지 않았느냐고!\" 멀버리 경이 되받았어."], ["‘How would you have had it?", "\"그럼 자네는 어떻게 했으면 좋았겠나?"], ["How could we have got a general invitation at first sight--come when you like, go when you like, stop as long as you like, do what you like--if you, the lord, had not made yourself agreeable to the foolish mistress of the house?", "자네가, 그 경께서, 그 집 어리석은 안주인 비위를 맞춰 주지 않았다면, 어떻게 처음 보자마자 그런 무제한 초대를 따낼 수 있었겠어 — 오고 싶을 때 오고, 가고 싶을 때 가고, 있고 싶은 만큼 있고, 하고 싶은 대로 하는 그런 초대를 말이야?"], ["Do I care for this girl, except as your friend?", "내가 이 아가씨한테 관심이라도 있는 줄 아나, 자네 친구라는 것 말고?"], ["Haven’t I been sounding your praises in her ears, and bearing her pretty sulks and peevishness all night for you?", "내가 자네를 위해서 자네 칭찬을 그 아가씨 귀에 줄곧 늘어놓고, 밤새 그 예쁜 토라짐이며 짜증을 다 받아 주지 않았느냐고?"], ["What sort of stuff do you think I’m made of?", "자네는 내가 무슨 재료로 만들어졌다고 생각하나?"], ["Would I do this for every man?", "내가 아무한테나 이런 짓을 해 주겠어?"], ["Don’t I deserve even gratitude in return?’", "그 보답으로 고맙다는 말이라도 들을 자격이 없단 말이야?\""]], [["‘You’re a deyvlish good fellow,’ said the poor young lord, taking his friend’s arm.", "\"자네는 정말 끝내주게 좋은 친구야.\" 가엾은 젊은 경이 친구의 팔을 잡으며 말했어."], ["‘Upon my life you’re a deyvlish good fellow, Hawk.’", "\"정말이지 자네는 끝내주게 좋은 친구라고, 호크.\""]], [["‘And I have done right, have I?’ demanded Sir Mulberry.", "\"그러니까 내가 옳게 한 거지, 그렇지?\" 멀버리 경이 따져 물었어."]], [["‘Quite ri-ght.’", "\"아주 옳—았네.\""]], [["‘And like a poor, silly, good-natured, friendly dog as I am, eh?’", "\"그리고 나라는 게 그저 불쌍하고, 어수룩하고, 사람 좋고, 다정한 강아지 같은 놈이라는 거지, 응?\""]], [["‘Ye--es, ye--es; like a friend,’ replied the other.", "\"그래—그래. 친구다운 친구지.\" 상대가 대답했어."]], [["‘Well then,’ replied Sir Mulberry, ‘I’m satisfied. And now let’s go and have our revenge on the German baron and the Frenchman, who cleaned you out so handsomely last night.’", "\"그럼 됐어.\" 멀버리 경이 대답했어. \"난 만족해. 자, 이제 가서 그 독일 남작이랑 프랑스 놈한테 복수를 해 주자고. 어젯밤에 자네를 그렇게 깔끔하게 털어먹은 그 작자들 말이야.\""]], [["With these words the friendly creature took his companion’s arm and led him away, turning half round as he did so, and bestowing a wink and a contemptuous smile on Messrs Pyke and Pluck, who, cramming their handkerchiefs into their mouths to denote their silent enjoyment of the whole proceedings, followed their patron and his victim at a little distance.", "이 말과 함께 이 다정한 작자는 동행의 팔을 잡고 그를 데리고 갔어. 그러면서 몸을 반쯤 돌려, 파이크 씨와 플럭 씨에게 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하고 경멸 어린 웃음을 던졌지. 두 사람은 이 모든 일을 말없이 즐겁게 지켜본다는 표시로 손수건을 입에 틀어막고는, 제 후원자와 그 먹잇감을 조금 떨어져서 뒤따라갔어."]], [["# CHAPTER 28", "# CHAPTER 28"]], [["Miss Nickleby, rendered desperate by the Persecution of Sir Mulberry Hawk, and the Complicated Difficulties and Distresses which surround her, appeals, as a last resource, to her Uncle for Protection", "니클비 양이 멀베리 호크 경의 박해와 자신을 둘러싼 복잡한 곤경과 고통에 절망한 끝에, 최후의 수단으로 삼촌에게 보호를 호소하다"]], [["The ensuing morning brought reflection with it, as morning usually does; but widely different was the train of thought it awakened in the different persons who had been so unexpectedly brought together on the preceding evening, by the active agency of Messrs Pyke and Pluck. The reflections of Sir Mulberry Hawk--if such a term can be applied to the thoughts of the systematic and calculating man of dissipation, whose joys, regrets, pains, and pleasures, are all of self, and who would seem to retain nothing of the intellectual faculty but the power to debase himself, and to degrade the very nature whose outward semblance he wears--the reflections of Sir Mulberry Hawk turned upon Kate Nickleby, and were, in brief, that she was undoubtedly handsome; that her coyness MUST be easily conquerable by a man of his address and experience, and that the pursuit was one which could not fail to redound to his credit, and greatly to enhance his reputation with the world. And lest this last consideration--no mean or secondary one with Sir Mulberry--should sound strangely in the ears of some, let it be remembered that most men live in a world of their own, and that in that limited circle alone are they ambitious for distinction and applause. Sir Mulberry’s world was peopled with profligates, and he acted accordingly.", "멀버리 호크 경의 생각이라면 — 사실 이런 말을 그에게 쓸 수 있을까 싶긴 해. 그는 빈틈없이 계산하는 방탕한 사내였거든. 그가 느끼는 기쁨이며 후회며 고통이며 즐거움이 죄다 자기 자신에 관한 것뿐이었고, 그의 지적 능력이라곤 자기 자신을 더럽히고, 겉으로 뒤집어쓴 인간이라는 본성을 짓밟는 힘밖에 남지 않은 듯했으니까 — 아무튼 멀버리 호크 경의 생각은 케이트 니클비에게로 향했어. 간단히 말하면 이런 거였지. 그 아가씨는 의심할 여지 없이 아름답다는 것. 그 수줍음쯤이야 자기처럼 능숙하고 경험 많은 남자가 손쉽게 무너뜨릴 수 있다는 것. 그리고 이 정복은 틀림없이 자기 체면을 세워 주고, 세상에서의 평판을 크게 높여 줄 거라는 것이었어. 그런데 이 마지막 생각이 — 멀버리 경에게는 결코 사소하거나 부차적인 게 아니었지 — 누군가의 귀에는 이상하게 들릴지도 모르니, 이것만은 기억해 두자. 대개의 사람은 저마다의 세계 안에서 살아가고, 오직 그 좁은 울타리 안에서만 두각을 드러내고 박수받기를 바란다는 것 말이야. 멀버리 경의 세계는 방탕한 자들로 들끓었고, 그는 거기에 맞춰 행동했지."]], [["Thus, cases of injustice, and oppression, and tyranny, and the most extravagant bigotry, are in constant occurrence among us every day.", "이런 식으로 부당한 일, 억압, 횡포, 그리고 더없이 터무니없는 편견이 우리 사이에서 날마다 끊임없이 벌어지는 거야. 사람들은 그 일을 저지른 주역들이 세상의 여론을 그토록 완전히 무시했다며 놀라워하고 감탄하기를 즐기지."], ["It is the custom to trumpet forth much wonder and astonishment at the chief actors therein setting at defiance so completely the opinion of the world; but there is no greater fallacy; it is precisely because they do consult the opinion of their own little world that such things take place at all, and strike the great world dumb with amazement.", "하지만 그것만큼 큰 착각이 없어. 그런 일이 애초에 벌어지는 것도, 큰 세상을 입 떡 벌리게 만드는 것도, 바로 그자들이 제 작은 세계의 여론만은 꼬박꼬박 살피기 때문이거든."]], [["The reflections of Mrs.", "니클비 부인의 생각은 더없이 자랑스럽고 흐뭇한 종류였어."], ["Nickleby were of the proudest and most complacent kind; and under the influence of her very agreeable delusion she straightway sat down and indited a long letter to Kate, in which she expressed her entire approval of the admirable choice she had made, and extolled Sir Mulberry to the skies; asserting, for the more complete satisfaction of her daughter’s feelings, that he was precisely the individual whom she (Mrs.", "그리고 그 아주 기분 좋은 착각에 빠진 채로, 부인은 곧장 자리에 앉아 케이트에게 긴 편지를 써 내려갔지. 편지에서 부인은 딸이 한 그 훌륭한 선택을 전적으로 찬성한다고 했어."], ["Nickleby) would have chosen for her son-in-law, if she had had the picking and choosing from all mankind.", "그리고 멀버리 경을 하늘 높이 치켜세웠지. 딸의 마음을 한층 흐뭇하게 해 주려고, 만약 자기가 온 세상 남자들 중에서 골라잡을 수 있었다면 바로 그 사람을 사위로 골랐을 거라고까지 단언했어."], ["The good lady then, with the preliminary observation that she might be fairly supposed not to have lived in the world so long without knowing its ways, communicated a great many subtle precepts applicable to the state of courtship, and confirmed in their wisdom by her own personal experience.", "그러고는 이 착한 부인은, 자기가 세상을 이만큼 오래 살았으니 세상 물정을 모를 리 없다는 말을 먼저 깔아 둔 다음, 구애의 시기에 들어맞는 미묘한 가르침을 잔뜩 늘어놓았어. 그리고 그게 다 자기 경험으로 지혜를 입증한 것이라고 했지."], ["Above all things she commended a strict maidenly reserve, as being not only a very laudable thing in itself, but as tending materially to strengthen and increase a lover’s ardour.", "무엇보다도 부인은 처녀다운 엄격한 조심성을 칭찬했어. 그 자체로도 아주 칭찬받을 일일뿐더러, 연인의 열정을 단단히 키우고 부풀리는 데 큰 도움이 된다면서 말이야."], ["‘And I never,’ added Mrs Nickleby, ‘was more delighted in my life than to observe last night, my dear, that your good sense had already told you this.’ With which sentiment, and various hints of the pleasure she derived from the knowledge that her daughter inherited so large an instalment of her own excellent sense and discretion (to nearly the full measure of which she might hope, with care, to succeed in time), Mrs.", "\"그리고 나는 말이다,\" 니클비 부인은 덧붙였어."], ["Nickleby concluded a very long and rather illegible letter.", "\"어젯밤에 내 사랑하는 딸의 분별력이 벌써 이걸 일러 주었더라는 걸 알고는, 평생 그렇게 기뻤던 적이 없었단다.\" 이런 말과 함께, 그리고 딸이 자기의 그 뛰어난 분별과 신중함을 이렇게나 많이 물려받았다는 걸 알게 되어 얼마나 기쁜지를 (조심하면 머지않아 딸도 거의 그 분별을 다 닮을 수 있겠지) 이렇게저렇게 넌지시 일러두면서, 니클비 부인은 아주 길고 알아보기 힘든 편지를 끝맺었어."]], [["Poor Kate was well-nigh distracted on the receipt of four closely-written and closely-crossed sides of congratulation on the very subject which had prevented her closing her eyes all night, and kept her weeping and watching in her chamber; still worse and more trying was the necessity of rendering herself agreeable to Mrs.", "가엾은 케이트는 그 편지를 받고 거의 정신이 나갈 지경이었어. 빽빽이 쓰고 가로세로로 겹쳐 쓴 넉 장짜리 축하 편지였는데, 하필이면 밤새 한숨도 못 자고 방에서 울며 뜬눈으로 지새우게 만든 바로 그 일을 두고 하는 말이었거든."], ["Wititterly, who, being in low spirits after the fatigue of the preceding night, of course expected her companion (else wherefore had she board and salary?) to be in the best spirits possible.", "그런데 더 나쁘고 더 견디기 힘든 건 위티털리 부인의 비위를 맞춰야 한다는 거였어. 부인은 전날 밤의 피로 끝에 기분이 가라앉아 있었고, 그러니 당연히 자기 말동무가 (안 그러면 무엇 때문에 숙식과 봉급을 주겠어?) 더없이 기분 좋은 상태이기를 바랐지."], ["As to Mr. Wititterly, he went about all day in a tremor of delight at having shaken hands with a lord, and having actually asked him to come and see him in his own house.", "위티털리 씨로 말하자면, 귀족과 악수를 했고 그것도 모자라 자기 집에 와 달라고 청까지 했다는 사실에 온종일 기쁨으로 들떠 안절부절못하며 돌아다녔어."], ["The lord himself, not being troubled to any inconvenient extent with the power of thinking, regaled himself with the conversation of Messrs Pyke and Pluck, who sharpened their wit by a plentiful indulgence in various costly stimulants at his expense.", "정작 그 귀족 본인은 생각하는 능력 때문에 불편할 일이 도무지 없는 사람이라, 파이크와 플럭의 입담으로 흥을 돋우며 즐겼지. 두 사람은 그의 돈으로 값비싼 술을 실컷 들이켜며 입담을 더 날카롭게 갈았고 말이야."]], [["It was four in the afternoon--that is, the vulgar afternoon of the sun and the clock--and Mrs.", "오후 네 시였어 — 그러니까 해와 시계가 가리키는, 그 흔해 빠진 오후 말이야. 위티털리 부인은 늘 그렇듯 응접실 소파에 비스듬히 기대 있었어."], ["Wititterly reclined, according to custom, on the drawing-room sofa, while Kate read aloud a new novel in three volumes, entitled ‘The Lady Flabella,’ which Alphonse the doubtful had procured from the library that very morning.", "한편 케이트는 「레이디 플라벨라」라는 제목의 세 권짜리 새 소설을 소리 내어 읽고 있었지. 그 미심쩍은 알퐁스가 바로 그날 아침 도서관에서 구해 온 책이었어."], ["And it was a production admirably suited to a lady labouring under Mrs.", "그건 위티털리 부인이 앓는 병에 더없이 잘 맞는 작품이었어."], ["Wititterly’s complaint, seeing that there was not a line in it, from beginning to end, which could, by the most remote contingency, awaken the smallest excitement in any person breathing. Kate read on.", "처음부터 끝까지 단 한 줄도, 아무리 가능성을 멀리 끌어와 봐도, 숨 쉬는 그 누구의 마음에 티끌만 한 흥분도 일으킬 만한 데가 없었거든."]], [["‘“Cherizette,”", "케이트는 계속 읽어 나갔어. \"'슈리제트,'"], ["said the Lady Flabella, inserting her mouse-like feet in the blue satin slippers, which had unwittingly occasioned the half-playful half-angry altercation between herself and the youthful Colonel Befillaire, in the Duke of Mincefenille’s SALON DE DANSE on the previous night.", "레이디 플라벨라가 생쥐처럼 자그마한 발을 푸른 새틴 슬리퍼에 밀어 넣으며 말했다. 바로 그 슬리퍼가, 지난밤 맹스펜유 공작의 무도회장에서 자기와 젊은 베필레르 대령 사이에 반은 장난스럽고 반은 화난 말다툼을 무심코 일으킨 것이었다."], ["“CHERIZETTE, MA CHERE, DONNEZ-MOI DE L’EAU-DE-COLOGNE, S’IL VOUS PLAIT, MON ENFANT.”", "'슈리제트, 마 셰르, 도네무아 드 로드콜로뉴, 실 부 플레, 몽 앙팡.'"]], [["‘“MERCIE--thank you,”", "\"'메르시 — 고마워,' 레이디 플라벨라가 말했다."], ["said the Lady Flabella, as the lively but devoted Cherizette plentifully besprinkled with the fragrant compound the Lady Flabella’s MOUCHOIR of finest cambric, edged with richest lace, and emblazoned at the four corners with the Flabella crest, and gorgeous heraldic bearings of that noble family.", "명랑하면서도 충직한 슈리제트가 그 향기로운 액체를 레이디 플라벨라의 손수건에 흠뻑 뿌리는 동안이었다. 그 손수건은 가장 고운 케임브릭 천에 더없이 화려한 레이스로 가장자리를 두르고, 네 귀퉁이마다 플라벨라 가문의 문장과 그 고귀한 가문의 찬란한 문장 장식을 수놓은 것이었다."], ["“MERCIE--that will do.”", "'메르시 — 그만하면 됐다.'"]], [["‘At this instant, while the Lady Flabella yet inhaled that delicious fragrance by holding the MOUCHOIR to her exquisite, but thoughtfully-chiselled nose, the door of the BOUDOIR (artfully concealed by rich hangings of silken damask, the hue of Italy’s firmament) was thrown open, and with noiseless tread two VALETS-DE-CHAMBRE, clad in sumptuous liveries of peach-blossom and gold, advanced into the room followed by a page in BAS DE SOIE--silk stockings--who, while they remained at some distance making the most graceful obeisances, advanced to the feet of his lovely mistress, and dropping on one knee presented, on a golden salver gorgeously chased, a scented BILLET.", "\"바로 그 순간, 레이디 플라벨라가 아직도 그 손수건을 곱지만 사려 깊게 깎아 놓은 듯한 코에 갖다 대고 그 그윽한 향기를 들이마시고 있을 때였다. 부두아르의 문이 — 이탈리아 하늘빛을 닮은 화려한 비단 다마스크 휘장으로 교묘히 가려져 있던 그 문이 — 활짝 열렸다. 그리고 복숭아꽃빛과 금빛의 호화로운 제복을 입은 두 시종이 소리 없는 걸음으로 방 안으로 들어왔다. 그 뒤를 비단 양말을 신은 시동이 따랐다. 두 시종이 얼마쯤 떨어진 곳에서 더없이 우아하게 절을 올리는 동안, 시동은 아름다운 여주인의 발치까지 나아가, 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고는, 호화롭게 새김 장식을 한 금쟁반에 향내 나는 쪽지 한 장을 받쳐 올렸다."]], [["‘The Lady Flabella, with an agitation she could not repress, hastily tore off the ENVELOPE and broke the scented seal.", "\"레이디 플라벨라는 억누를 수 없는 떨림으로, 황급히 봉투를 뜯고 향기로운 봉인을 깨뜨렸다."], ["It WAS from Befillaire--the young, the slim, the low-voiced--HER OWN Befillaire.’", "그것은 베필레르가 보낸 것이었다 — 젊고, 호리호리하고, 목소리가 나직한 — 다름 아닌 그녀의 베필레르 말이다.'\""]], [["‘Oh, charming!’ interrupted Kate’s patroness, who was sometimes taken literary.", "\"어머, 멋져라!\" 이따금 문학에 심취하곤 하는 케이트의 후원자가 끼어들었어."], ["‘Poetic, really.", "\"정말이지 시적이야."], ["Read that description again, Miss Nickleby.’", "그 대목 다시 읽어 봐요, 니클비 양.\""]], [["Kate complied.", "케이트는 그대로 따랐어."]], [["‘Sweet, indeed!’ said Mrs.", "\"정말 감미로워!\""], ["Wititterly, with a sigh.", "위티털리 부인이 한숨을 쉬며 말했어."], ["‘So voluptuous, is it not--so soft?’", "\"어찌나 관능적인지, 안 그래? 어찌나 부드러운지?\""]], [["‘Yes, I think it is,’ replied Kate, gently; ‘very soft.’", "\"네, 그런 것 같아요.\" 케이트가 부드럽게 대답했어. \"아주 부드럽네요.\""]], [["‘Close the book, Miss Nickleby,’ said Mrs.", "\"책을 덮어요, 니클비 양.\""], ["Wititterly.", "위티털리 부인이 말했어."], ["‘I can hear nothing more today; I should be sorry to disturb the impression of that sweet description.", "\"오늘은 더는 못 듣겠어. 그 감미로운 묘사의 여운을 흩뜨리고 싶지 않거든."], ["Close the book.’", "책을 덮어요.\""]], [["Kate complied, not unwillingly; and, as she did so, Mrs.", "케이트는 그대로 따랐어. 마지못한 것도 아니었지."], ["Wititterly raising her glass with a languid hand, remarked, that she looked pale.", "그러는 동안 위티털리 부인은 나른한 손길로 안경을 들어 올리더니, 케이트가 창백해 보인다고 말했어."]], [["‘It was the fright of that--that noise and confusion last night,’ said Kate.", "\"어젯밤의 그 — 그 소란과 혼란에 놀라서 그런가 봐요.\" 케이트가 말했어."]], [["‘How very odd!’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"어쩌면 그렇게 별날까!\""], ["Wititterly, with a look of surprise.", "위티털리 부인이 놀란 표정으로 외쳤어."], ["And certainly, when one comes to think of it, it WAS very odd that anything should have disturbed a companion.", "하긴 곰곰이 생각해 보면, 말동무의 마음을 무언가가 흔들어 놓는다는 건 정말이지 별난 일이긴 했어."], ["A steam-engine, or other ingenious piece of mechanism out of order, would have been nothing to it.", "고장 난 증기기관이나 정교한 기계 장치쯤이야 그에 비하면 아무것도 아니었지."]], [["‘How did you come to know Lord Frederick, and those other delightful creatures, child?’ asked Mrs.", "\"넌 프레더릭 경이랑 그 다른 멋진 분들을 어떻게 알게 됐니, 얘야?\""], ["Wititterly, still eyeing Kate through her glass.", "위티털리 부인이 여전히 안경 너머로 케이트를 살피며 물었어."]], [["‘I met them at my uncle’s,’ said Kate, vexed to feel that she was colouring deeply, but unable to keep down the blood which rushed to her face whenever she thought of that man.", "\"삼촌 댁에서 만났어요.\" 케이트가 말했어. 얼굴이 새빨개지는 걸 느끼고 속이 상했지만, 그 남자 생각만 하면 얼굴로 확 솟구치는 피를 억누를 수가 없었어."]], [["‘Have you known them long?’", "\"안 지는 오래됐고?\""]], [["‘No,’ rejoined Kate.", "\"아뇨.\" 케이트가 대답했어."], ["‘Not long.’", "\"오래되진 않았어요.\""]], [["‘I was very glad of the opportunity which that respectable person, your mother, gave us of being known to them,’ said Mrs.", "\"그 점잖은 분, 네 어머니가 우리에게 그분들을 알게 될 기회를 주신 게 참 기뻤단다.\""], ["Wititterly, in a lofty manner.", "위티털리 부인이 거만한 투로 말했어."], ["‘Some friends of ours were on the very point of introducing us, which makes it quite remarkable.’", "\"우리 지인 중에 막 우리를 소개해 주려던 분들이 있었거든. 그러니 더 놀라운 일이지.\""]], [["This was said lest Miss Nickleby should grow conceited on the honour and dignity of having known four great people (for Pyke and Pluck were included among the delightful creatures), whom Mrs.", "이 말은 니클비 양이 위세 있는 인물 넷을 (파이크와 플럭도 그 멋진 분들 축에 끼어 있었으니까) 알게 된 영광과 위엄에 으스댈까 봐 미리 못 박아 둔 거였어. 위티털리 부인 자신은 모르는 사람들이었거든."], ["Wititterly did not know. But as the circumstance had made no impression one way or other upon Kate’s mind, the force of the observation was quite lost upon her.", "그런데 그 일은 케이트의 마음에 이렇다 할 인상을 전혀 남기지 못했으니, 그 말의 효력도 케이트한테는 영 헛것이 되고 말았지."]], [["‘They asked permission to call,’ said Mrs.", "\"그분들이 찾아와도 되겠느냐고 청하더구나.\""], ["Wititterly.", "위티털리 부인이 말했어."], ["‘I gave it them of course.’", "\"물론 그러시라고 했지.\""]], [["‘Do you expect them today?’ Kate ventured to inquire.", "\"오늘 오신대요?\" 케이트가 큰맘 먹고 물었어."]], [["Mrs.", "위티털리 부인의 대답은 현관문을 쾅쾅 두드리는 요란한 소리에 묻혀 버렸어."], ["Wititterly’s answer was lost in the noise of a tremendous rapping at the street-door, and before it had ceased to vibrate, there drove up a handsome cabriolet, out of which leaped Sir Mulberry Hawk and his friend Lord Verisopht.", "그리고 그 울림이 채 가시기도 전에, 멋진 이륜마차 한 대가 와서 멈췄고, 거기서 멀버리 호크 경과 그의 친구 베리소프트 경이 뛰어내렸지."]], [["‘They are here now,’ said Kate, rising and hurrying away.", "\"이제 오셨네요.\" 케이트가 일어서며 서둘러 자리를 피하려 했어."]], [["‘Miss Nickleby!’ cried Mrs.", "\"니클비 양!\""], ["Wititterly, perfectly aghast at a companion’s attempting to quit the room, without her permission first had and obtained.", "위티털리 부인이 외쳤어. 말동무가 자기 허락을 먼저 구해 받지도 않고 방을 나가려 하다니, 기가 막혀 어쩔 줄을 몰랐지."], ["‘Pray don’t think of going.’", "\"제발 갈 생각일랑 마요.\""]], [["‘You are very good!’ replied Kate.", "\"정말 고맙습니다!\" 케이트가 대답했어."], ["‘But--’", "\"하지만 —\""]], [["‘For goodness’ sake, don’t agitate me by making me speak so much,’ said Mrs.", "\"제발 나한테 이렇게 말을 많이 시켜서 흥분하게 하지 말아요.\""], ["Wititterly, with great sharpness.", "위티털리 부인이 아주 날카롭게 말했어."], ["‘Dear me, Miss Nickleby, I beg--’", "\"어머나, 니클비 양, 부탁이에요 —\""]], [["It was in vain for Kate to protest that she was unwell, for the footsteps of the knockers, whoever they were, were already on the stairs.", "케이트가 몸이 안 좋다고 아무리 우겨 봐야 소용없었어. 누가 두드린 거든 간에 그 발소리가 벌써 계단을 올라오고 있었으니까."], ["She resumed her seat, and had scarcely done so, when the doubtful page darted into the room and announced, Mr.", "케이트는 도로 자리에 앉았어. 앉기가 무섭게 그 미심쩍은 시동이 방으로 쏜살같이 들어와 한꺼번에 알렸지."], ["Pyke, and Mr. Pluck, and Lord Verisopht, and Sir Mulberry Hawk, all at one burst.", "파이크 씨, 플럭 씨, 베리소프트 경, 그리고 멀버리 호크 경 행차요, 하고 말이야."]], [["‘The most extraordinary thing in the world,’ said Mr.", "\"세상에 이렇게 신기한 일이 다 있나요.\""], ["Pluck, saluting both ladies with the utmost cordiality; ‘the most extraordinary thing.", "플럭 씨가 두 부인에게 더없이 다정하게 인사하며 말했어. \"정말이지 신기한 일이에요."], ["As Lord Frederick and Sir Mulberry drove up to the door, Pyke and I had that instant knocked.’", "프레더릭 경과 멀버리 경이 마차를 몰고 문 앞에 닿는 바로 그 순간, 저랑 파이크가 막 문을 두드렸지 뭡니까.\""]], [["‘That instant knocked,’ said Pyke.", "\"바로 그 순간에 두드렸지요.\" 파이크가 말했어."]], [["‘No matter how you came, so that you are here,’ said Mrs.", "\"어떻게 오셨든 무슨 상관이겠어요. 이렇게 와 주셨는데요.\""], ["Wititterly, who, by dint of lying on the same sofa for three years and a half, had got up quite a little pantomime of graceful attitudes, and now threw herself into the most striking of the whole series, to astonish the visitors.", "위티털리 부인이 말했어. 부인은 삼 년 반 동안 한 소파에 줄곧 누워 지낸 덕에 우아한 자세를 잔뜩 익혀 두었는데, 이제 그 모든 자세 중에서도 가장 인상적인 것을 골라 척 취하며 손님들을 놀라게 했지."], ["‘I am delighted, I am sure.’", "\"정말이지 너무 기뻐요.\""]], [["‘And how is Miss Nickleby?’ said Sir Mulberry Hawk, accosting Kate, in a low voice--not so low, however, but that it reached the ears of Mrs Wititterly.", "\"니클비 양은 좀 어떤가요?\" 멀버리 호크 경이 케이트에게 다가가 낮은 목소리로 말을 건넸어. 그렇다고 위티털리 부인의 귀에 안 들릴 만큼 낮지는 않았지만 말이야."]], [["‘Why, she complains of suffering from the fright of last night,’ said the lady.", "\"글쎄요, 어젯밤 놀란 게 아직 가시지 않는다네요.\" 부인이 말했어."], ["‘I am sure I don’t wonder at it, for my nerves are quite torn to pieces.’", "\"하긴 그럴 만도 하지요. 제 신경도 갈가리 찢어졌는걸요.\""]], [["‘And yet you look,’ observed Sir Mulberry, turning round; ‘and yet you look--’", "\"그런데도 부인은 보아하니,\" 멀버리 경이 몸을 돌리며 한마디 했어. \"그런데도 부인은 보아하니 —\""]], [["‘Beyond everything,’ said Mr.", "\"더없이 좋아 보이십니다.\""], ["Pyke, coming to his patron’s assistance.", "파이크 씨가 자기 후원자를 거들고 나서며 말했어."], ["Of course Mr. Pluck said the same.", "물론 플럭 씨도 똑같은 말을 했지."]], [["‘I am afraid Sir Mulberry is a flatterer, my lord,’ said Mrs.", "\"멀버리 경은 아첨꾼인가 봐요, 경.\""], ["Wititterly, turning to that young gentleman, who had been sucking the head of his cane in silence, and staring at Kate.", "위티털리 부인이 그 젊은 신사 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했어. 그는 잠자코 지팡이 머리를 빨면서 케이트를 빤히 쳐다보고 있었거든."]], [["‘Oh, deyvlish!’ replied Verisopht.", "\"오, 끝내주는군요!\" 베리소프트가 대답했어."], ["Having given utterance to which remarkable sentiment, he occupied himself as before.", "이 대단한 소감을 입 밖에 낸 다음, 그는 전처럼 다시 제 일에 골몰했지."]], [["‘Neither does Miss Nickleby look the worse,’ said Sir Mulberry, bending his bold gaze upon her.", "\"니클비 양도 전보다 못해 보이지 않는군요.\" 멀버리 경이 대담한 시선을 케이트에게 던지며 말했어."], ["‘She was always handsome, but upon my soul, ma’am, you seem to have imparted some of your own good looks to her besides.’", "\"이 아가씨는 늘 아름다웠지만, 맹세코 부인, 거기다 부인의 그 고운 자태까지 좀 나눠 주신 것 같습니다그려.\""]], [["To judge from the glow which suffused the poor girl’s countenance after this speech, Mrs. Wititterly might, with some show of reason, have been supposed to have imparted to it some of that artificial bloom which decorated her own.", "이 말이 끝나고 가엾은 아가씨의 얼굴에 번진 그 홍조를 보면, 위티털리 부인이 자기 얼굴을 꾸민 그 인공의 화색을 케이트한테 좀 나눠 준 거라고 봐도 그럴듯할 만했어."], ["Mrs. Wititterly admitted, though not with the best grace in the world, that Kate DID look pretty.", "위티털리 부인은, 세상 그리 흔쾌한 낯빛은 아니었지만, 케이트가 정말 예뻐 보이긴 한다고 인정했어."], ["She began to think, too, that Sir Mulberry was not quite so agreeable a creature as she had at first supposed him; for, although a skilful flatterer is a most delightful companion if you can keep him all to yourself, his taste becomes very doubtful when he takes to complimenting other people.", "그리고 멀버리 경이 처음 생각만큼 그리 유쾌한 사람은 아니라는 생각이 들기 시작했지. 능숙한 아첨꾼이란 혼자 독차지할 수만 있다면 더없이 즐거운 말동무지만, 다른 사람을 칭찬하는 데 맛을 들이기 시작하면 그 안목이 영 미심쩍어지거든."]], [["‘Pyke,’ said the watchful Mr.", "\"파이크.\""], ["Pluck, observing the effect which the praise of Miss Nickleby had produced.", "빈틈없는 플럭 씨가 니클비 양에 대한 칭찬이 일으킨 효과를 눈여겨보며 말했어."]], [["‘Well, Pluck,’ said Pyke.", "\"왜, 플럭.\" 파이크가 말했어."]], [["‘Is there anybody,’ demanded Mr.", "\"혹시 말이야,\" 플럭 씨가 알쏭달쏭하게 물었어."], ["Pluck, mysteriously, ‘anybody you know, that Mrs. Wititterly’s profile reminds you of?’", "\"위티털리 부인의 옆얼굴을 보면 떠오르는 사람 없나, 자네가 아는 누구 말이야?\""]], [["‘Reminds me of!’ answered Pyke.", "\"떠오르는 사람!\" 파이크가 대답했어."], ["‘Of course there is.’", "\"물론 있고말고.\""]], [["‘Who do you mean?’ said Pluck, in the same mysterious manner.", "\"누굴 말하는 건데?\" 플럭이 똑같이 알쏭달쏭한 투로 말했어."], ["‘The D. of B.?’", "\"비 공작 부인?\""]], [["‘The C.", "\"비 백작 부인이지.\""], ["of B.,’ replied Pyke, with the faintest trace of a grin lingering in his countenance.", "파이크가 얼굴에 희미한 웃음기를 남긴 채 대답했어."], ["‘The beautiful sister is the countess; not the duchess.’", "\"그 아리따운 동생분이 백작 부인이잖아. 공작 부인이 아니라.\""]], [["‘True,’ said Pluck, ‘the C.", "\"맞아.\" 플럭이 말했어."], ["of B.", "\"비 백작 부인."], ["The resemblance is wonderful!’", "정말 놀랄 만큼 닮았어!\""]], [["‘Perfectly startling,’ said Mr.", "\"그저 깜짝 놀랄 정도지.\""], ["Pyke.", "파이크 씨가 말했어."]], [["Here was a state of things!", "이런 일이 다 있다니!"], ["Mrs. Wititterly was declared, upon the testimony of two veracious and competent witnesses, to be the very picture of a countess!", "위티털리 부인이, 진실하고 안목 있는 두 증인의 증언에 따라, 영락없는 백작 부인의 판박이라고 선언된 거야!"], ["This was one of the consequences of getting into good society.", "이게 다 상류 사회에 발을 들인 덕이었지."], ["Why, she might have moved among grovelling people for twenty years, and never heard of it.", "글쎄, 부인이 보잘것없는 사람들 틈에서 이십 년을 살았어도 그런 말은 한 번도 못 들어 봤을 거 아니겠어."], ["How could she, indeed?", "사실 그럴 수가 있었겠어?"], ["what did THEY know about countesses?", "그런 사람들이 백작 부인에 대해 뭘 알겠어?"]], [["The two gentlemen having, by the greediness with which this little bait was swallowed, tested the extent of Mrs.", "이 작은 미끼를 부인이 얼마나 게걸스레 삼키는지로 그 아첨에 대한 식욕의 크기를 가늠해 본 두 신사는, 이제 그 물건을 아주 듬뿍 들이부어 주기 시작했어."], ["Wititterly’s appetite for adulation, proceeded to administer that commodity in very large doses, thus affording to Sir Mulberry Hawk an opportunity of pestering Miss Nickleby with questions and remarks, to which she was absolutely obliged to make some reply.", "그래서 멀버리 호크 경에게는 니클비 양을 이런저런 질문과 말로 들볶을 기회가 생겼지. 케이트는 거기에 어쩔 수 없이 무어라도 대꾸를 해야만 했고."], ["Meanwhile, Lord Verisopht enjoyed unmolested the full flavour of the gold knob at the top of his cane, as he would have done to the end of the interview if Mr.", "한편 베리소프트 경은 아무도 건드리지 않은 채로 지팡이 꼭대기의 금빛 손잡이가 주는 맛을 한껏 음미했어."], ["Wititterly had not come home, and caused the conversation to turn to his favourite topic.", "위티털리 씨가 집에 돌아와 화제를 자기가 좋아하는 쪽으로 돌리지만 않았더라면, 그는 면회가 끝날 때까지 그러고 있었을 거야."]], [["‘My lord,’ said Mr.", "\"경.\" 위티털리 씨가 말했어."], ["Wititterly, ‘I am delighted--honoured--proud.", "\"정말 기쁩니다 — 영광입니다 — 자랑스럽습니다."], ["Be seated again, my lord, pray.", "다시 앉으시지요, 경, 제발요."], ["I am proud, indeed--most proud.’", "정말이지 자랑스럽습니다 — 더없이 자랑스러워요.\""]], [["It was to the secret annoyance of his wife that Mr.", "위티털리 씨가 이 모든 말을 늘어놓는 게 그의 아내에게는 남몰래 짜증스러운 일이었어."], ["Wititterly said all this, for, although she was bursting with pride and arrogance, she would have had the illustrious guests believe that their visit was quite a common occurrence, and that they had lords and baronets to see them every day in the week.", "부인이 자부심과 거만함으로 터질 듯했던 건 사실이지만, 그래도 이 고귀한 손님들에게는 그들의 방문이 아주 흔한 일이고, 일주일 내내 날마다 귀족이며 준남작이 자기를 보러 온다고 믿게 하고 싶었거든."], ["But Mr. Wititterly’s feelings were beyond the power of suppression.", "그런데 위티털리 씨의 감정은 도저히 억누를 수 있는 게 아니었지."]], [["‘It is an honour, indeed!’ said Mr.", "\"정말이지 영광입니다!\" 위티털리 씨가 말했어."], ["Wititterly. ‘Julia, my soul, you will suffer for this tomorrow.’", "\"줄리아, 여보, 당신 내일은 이것 때문에 고생 좀 하겠구려.\""]], [["‘Suffer!’ cried Lord Verisopht.", "\"고생이라뇨!\" 베리소프트 경이 외쳤어."]], [["‘The reaction, my lord, the reaction,’ said Mr.", "\"그 반동 말입니다, 경, 반동이요.\" 위티털리 씨가 말했어."], ["Wititterly. ‘This violent strain upon the nervous system over, my lord, what ensues?", "\"신경 계통에 이렇게 격한 부담을 주고 나면, 경, 그 뒤에 뭐가 따라오겠습니까?"], ["A sinking, a depression, a lowness, a lassitude, a debility.", "가라앉음, 침울함, 처짐, 권태, 쇠약입니다."], ["My lord, if Sir Tumley Snuffim was to see that delicate creature at this moment, he would not give a--a--THIS for her life.’ In illustration of which remark, Mr Wititterly took a pinch of snuff from his box, and jerked it lightly into the air as an emblem of instability.", "경, 만약에 텀리 스너핌 경이 지금 이 순간 저 가녀린 사람을 본다면, 저 사람 목숨에 대해 이만큼도 — 이만큼도 — 값을 안 쳐 줄 겁니다.\" 이 말을 실감 나게 하려고, 위티털리 씨는 담뱃갑에서 코담배를 한 자밤 집어, 덧없음의 상징인 양 허공에 가볍게 휙 뿌렸어."]], [["‘Not THAT,’ said Mr.", "\"이만큼도요.\""], ["Wititterly, looking about him with a serious countenance.", "위티털리 씨가 진지한 얼굴로 주위를 둘러보며 말했어."], ["‘Sir Tumley Snuffim would not give that for Mrs Wititterly’s existence.’", "\"텀리 스너핌 경은 위티털리 부인의 목숨에 그만큼도 값을 안 쳐 줄 겁니다.\""]], [["Mr.", "위티털리 씨는 이 말을 점잖이 우쭐대며 했어."], ["Wititterly told this with a kind of sober exultation, as if it were no trifling distinction for a man to have a wife in such a desperate state, and Mrs.", "마치 그렇게 위태로운 지경에 빠진 아내를 둔 게 결코 사소하지 않은 영예라도 되는 양 말이야. 그러자 위티털리 부인은 한숨을 쉬며 곁눈으로 보았지."], ["Wititterly sighed and looked on, as if she felt the honour, but had determined to bear it as meekly as might be.", "그 영예를 느끼긴 하지만 되도록 다소곳이 견디기로 작정했다는 듯이 말이야."]], [["‘Mrs. Wititterly,’ said her husband, ‘is Sir Tumley Snuffim’s favourite patient.", "\"위티털리 부인은,\""], ["I believe I may venture to say, that Mrs.", "그 남편이 말했어. \"텀리 스너핌 경이 가장 아끼는 환자랍니다."], ["Wititterly is the first person who took the new medicine which is supposed to have destroyed a family at Kensington Gravel Pits.", "감히 말씀드리건대, 켄싱턴 그래블 핏에서 한 가족을 몰살시켰다는 그 새 약을 맨 처음 먹은 사람이 바로 위티털리 부인일 겁니다."], ["I believe she was.", "분명 그랬을 거예요."], ["If I am wrong, Julia, my dear, you will correct me.’", "내가 틀렸거든, 줄리아 여보, 바로잡아 주구려.\""]], [["‘I believe I was,’ said Mrs.", "\"제가 처음이었던 것 같아요.\""], ["Wititterly, in a faint voice.", "위티털리 부인이 힘없는 목소리로 말했어."]], [["As there appeared to be some doubt in the mind of his patron how he could best join in this conversation, the indefatigable Mr.", "이 대화에 어떻게 끼어드는 게 좋을지 자기 후원자가 좀 갈피를 못 잡는 듯하자, 지칠 줄 모르는 파이크 씨가 그 틈으로 뛰어들었어."], ["Pyke threw himself into the breach, and, by way of saying something to the point, inquired--with reference to the aforesaid medicine--whether it was nice.", "그리고 핵심을 찌르는 말이랍시고, 앞서 말한 그 약에 관해 — 맛이 괜찮더냐고 물었지."]], [["‘No, sir, it was not.", "\"아니요, 안 괜찮았소. 그 약은 그런 장점조차 없었지요.\""], ["It had not even that recommendation,’ said Mr. W.", "위티털리 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Mrs.", "\"위티털리 부인은 정말이지 순교자시군요.\""], ["Wititterly is quite a martyr,’ observed Pyke, with a complimentary bow.", "파이크가 정중히 절을 하며 한마디 했어."]], [["‘I THINK I am,’ said Mrs.", "\"제가 그런 것 같아요.\""], ["Wititterly, smiling.", "위티털리 부인이 미소 지으며 말했어."]], [["‘I think you are, my dear Julia,’ replied her husband, in a tone which seemed to say that he was not vain, but still must insist upon their privileges. ‘If anybody, my lord,’ added Mr. Wititterly, wheeling round to the nobleman, ‘will produce to me a greater martyr than Mrs Wititterly, all I can say is, that I shall be glad to see that martyr, whether male or female--that’s all, my lord.’", "\"당신이 그렇소, 내 사랑 줄리아.\" 그 남편이 대답했어. 자기가 우쭐대는 건 아니지만 그래도 자기들 특권만은 기어이 내세워야겠다는 투였지. \"누구든, 경.\" 위티털리 씨가 그 귀족 쪽으로 휙 돌아서며 덧붙였어. \"위티털리 부인보다 더한 순교자를 제게 데려와 보시지요. 그러면 제가 드릴 말씀은, 그 순교자를 기꺼이 한번 뵙겠다는 것뿐입니다. 남자든 여자든 말입니다 — 그뿐이에요, 경.\""]], [["Pyke and Pluck promptly remarked that certainly nothing could be fairer than that; and the call having been by this time protracted to a very great length, they obeyed Sir Mulberry’s look, and rose to go.", "파이크와 플럭은 그보다 더 공정한 말은 없다고 얼른 맞장구를 쳤어. 그리고 이쯤 되자 방문이 어지간히 길어진 터라, 두 사람은 멀버리 경의 눈짓에 따라 일어나 가려고 했지."], ["This brought Sir Mulberry himself and Lord Verisopht on their legs also.", "그러자 멀버리 경 본인과 베리소프트 경도 따라 일어섰어."], ["Many protestations of friendship, and expressions anticipative of the pleasure which must inevitably flow from so happy an acquaintance, were exchanged, and the visitors departed, with renewed assurances that at all times and seasons the mansion of the Wititterlys would be honoured by receiving them beneath its roof.", "우정의 다짐이며, 이렇게 기쁜 사귐에서 반드시 흘러나올 즐거움에 대한 기대의 말이 숱하게 오갔지. 그리고 손님들은, 언제 어느 때고 위티털리 댁이 자기들을 지붕 아래 맞이하는 영광을 누리게 될 거라는 거듭된 다짐을 남기고 떠났어."]], [["That they came at all times and seasons--that they dined there one day, supped the next, dined again on the next, and were constantly to and fro on all--that they made parties to visit public places, and met by accident at lounges--that upon all these occasions Miss Nickleby was exposed to the constant and unremitting persecution of Sir Mulberry Hawk, who now began to feel his character, even in the estimation of his two dependants, involved in the successful reduction of her pride--that she had no intervals of peace or rest, except at those hours when she could sit in her solitary room, and weep over the trials of the day--all these were consequences naturally flowing from the well-laid plans of Sir Mulberry, and their able execution by the auxiliaries, Pyke and Pluck.", "그들이 언제 어느 때고 찾아왔다는 것 — 하루는 거기서 점심을, 다음 날은 저녁을, 또 그다음 날은 다시 점심을 먹었고, 날마다 끊임없이 드나들었다는 것 — 공공장소를 구경하러 패를 짜기도 하고, 휴게실 같은 데서 우연히 마주치기도 했다는 것 — 이 모든 자리에서 니클비 양이 멀버리 호크 경의 끊임없고 그칠 줄 모르는 괴롭힘에 시달렸다는 것 — 그 경은 이제 케이트의 자존심을 꺾는 데 성공하느냐 마느냐에 자기 체면이 걸렸다고 여기기 시작했지, 심지어 제 두 부하의 평가 안에서도 말이야 — 그리고 케이트에게는 평화나 안식의 짬이라곤 없었다는 것, 다만 외로운 제 방에 앉아 그날의 시련을 두고 울 수 있는 그 시간만 빼면 말이야 — 이 모든 것이 멀버리 경의 잘 짜인 계략과, 그 부하 파이크와 플럭의 능란한 실행에서 자연히 흘러나온 결과였지."]], [["And thus for a fortnight matters went on.", "그렇게 이 주일 동안 일이 굴러갔어."], ["That any but the weakest and silliest of people could have seen in one interview that Lord Verisopht, though he was a lord, and Sir Mulberry Hawk, though he was a baronet, were not persons accustomed to be the best possible companions, and were certainly not calculated by habits, manners, tastes, or conversation, to shine with any very great lustre in the society of ladies, need scarcely be remarked.", "베리소프트 경이 비록 귀족이고 멀버리 호크 경이 비록 준남작이라 해도, 두 사람이 결코 더없이 좋은 말동무가 될 만한 부류가 아니라는 것, 그리고 그 습성이나 태도나 취향이나 말솜씨로 보아 숙녀들 모임에서 그리 환하게 빛날 만한 위인이 아니라는 것 — 이건 아주 어리석고 미욱한 사람이 아니고서야 한 번만 만나 봐도 알 수 있는 일이었으니, 굳이 말할 필요도 없을 거야."], ["But with Mrs.", "하지만 위티털리 부인에게는 그 두 작위면 그걸로 충분했어."], ["Wititterly the two titles were all sufficient; coarseness became humour, vulgarity softened itself down into the most charming eccentricity; insolence took the guise of an easy absence of reserve, attainable only by those who had had the good fortune to mix with high folks.", "거친 말투는 익살이 되었고, 천박함은 더없이 매력적인 별스러움으로 누그러졌지. 무례함은 격의 없이 스스럼없는 태도로 둔갑했는데, 그건 운 좋게 높은 분들과 어울려 본 사람만 익힐 수 있는 거라고 여겼거든."]], [["If the mistress put such a construction upon the behaviour of her new friends, what could the companion urge against them?", "여주인이 새 친구들의 행동을 그런 식으로 받아들이는데, 말동무가 그들에게 무슨 항변을 할 수 있었겠어?"], ["If they accustomed themselves to very little restraint before the lady of the house, with how much more freedom could they address her paid dependent!", "그들이 안주인 앞에서도 그토록 거리낌 없이 굴었으니, 돈 받고 부리는 그 아랫사람한테야 얼마나 더 제멋대로 말을 걸었겠어!"], ["Nor was even this the worst.", "게다가 그게 최악도 아니었지."], ["As the odious Sir Mulberry Hawk attached himself to Kate with less and less of disguise, Mrs.", "그 역겨운 멀버리 호크 경이 점점 더 숨김없이 케이트에게 들러붙자, 위티털리 부인은 니클비 양이 자기보다 더 매력 있다는 데 질투를 느끼기 시작했어."], ["Wititterly began to grow jealous of the superior attractions of Miss Nickleby.", "만약 이 감정 때문에 케이트가 그런 손님들이 와 있을 때 응접실에서 쫓겨났더라면, 케이트는 그렇게만 된다면 더없이 기쁘고 좋았을 거야."], ["If this feeling had led to her banishment from the drawing-room when such company was there, Kate would have been only too happy and willing that it should have existed, but unfortunately for her she possessed that native grace and true gentility of manner, and those thousand nameless accomplishments which give to female society its greatest charm; if these be valuable anywhere, they were especially so where the lady of the house was a mere animated doll.", "그런데 케이트한테는 불행하게도, 그 타고난 우아함과 진정으로 기품 있는 몸가짐, 그리고 여성들의 자리에 가장 큰 매력을 더해 주는 그 이름 없는 천 가지 재주가 있었어. 이런 게 어디서든 값진 거라면, 안주인이 그저 움직이는 인형 같은 곳에서야 더더욱 그랬지."], ["The consequence was, that Kate had the double mortification of being an indispensable part of the circle when Sir Mulberry and his friends were there, and of being exposed, on that very account, to all Mrs.", "그래서 케이트는 이중의 굴욕을 겪었어. 멀버리 경과 그 친구들이 와 있을 때는 그 자리에 없어서는 안 될 사람이 되고, 바로 그 이유 때문에, 그들이 가고 나면 위티털리 부인의 온갖 심술과 변덕에 고스란히 시달리는 거였지."], ["Wititterly’s ill-humours and caprices when they were gone. She became utterly and completely miserable.", "케이트는 완전히, 철저히 비참해졌어."]], [["Mrs. Wititterly had never thrown off the mask with regard to Sir Mulberry, but when she was more than usually out of temper, attributed the circumstance, as ladies sometimes do, to nervous indisposition.", "위티털리 부인은 멀버리 경에 관해서만은 한 번도 가면을 벗은 적이 없었어."], ["However, as the dreadful idea that Lord Verisopht also was somewhat taken with Kate, and that she, Mrs.", "다만 평소보다 유난히 심기가 틀어지면, 부인들이 더러 그러듯 그걸 신경의 탈 탓으로 돌렸지."], ["Wititterly, was quite a secondary person, dawned upon that lady’s mind and gradually developed itself, she became possessed with a large quantity of highly proper and most virtuous indignation, and felt it her duty, as a married lady and a moral member of society, to mention the circumstance to ‘the young person’ without delay.", "그런데 베리소프트 경마저도 케이트에게 다소 마음이 끌린 듯하다는, 그래서 자기 위티털리 부인은 완전히 뒷전 신세라는 그 끔찍한 생각이 부인의 머릿속에 어렴풋이 떠올라 차츰 또렷해지자, 부인은 더없이 당연하고 더없이 정숙한 분노로 가득 차게 됐어. 그리고 결혼한 부인이자 사회의 도덕적인 일원으로서, 그 일을 지체 없이 '그 젊은 사람'에게 일러 주는 게 자기 의무라고 느꼈지."]], [["Accordingly Mrs. Wititterly broke ground next morning, during a pause in the novel-reading.", "그래서 위티털리 부인은 이튿날 아침, 소설을 읽다 잠시 멈춘 틈을 타 운을 뗐어."]], [["‘Miss Nickleby,’ said Mrs.", "\"니클비 양.\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어."], ["Wititterly, ‘I wish to speak to you very gravely.", "\"아주 진지하게 할 얘기가 있어요. 이런 말을 하게 돼서 유감이에요."], ["I am sorry to have to do it, upon my word I am very sorry, but you leave me no alternative, Miss Nickleby.’ Here Mrs.", "정말이지 무척 유감이고말고요. 하지만 니클비 양이 내게 달리 어쩔 도리를 안 남겨 주는걸요.\""], ["Wititterly tossed her head--not passionately, only virtuously--and remarked, with some appearance of excitement, that she feared that palpitation of the heart was coming on again.", "그러고는 위티털리 부인이 고개를 홱 젖혔어 — 격하게는 아니고 그저 점잖게 말이야. 그러고는 다소 흥분한 기색으로, 심장이 또 두근거리기 시작할까 봐 걱정이라고 했지."]], [["‘Your behaviour, Miss Nickleby,’ resumed the lady, ‘is very far from pleasing me--very far. I am very anxious indeed that you should do well, but you may depend upon it, Miss Nickleby, you will not, if you go on as you do.’", "\"니클비 양, 양의 행동은,\" 부인이 말을 이었어. \"내 마음에 도무지 들지 않아요 — 도무지요. 나는 정말이지 양이 잘되기를 간절히 바라요. 하지만 분명히 말해 두는데, 니클비 양, 지금처럼 계속하다간 결코 잘되지 못할 거예요.\""]], [["‘Ma’am!’ exclaimed Kate, proudly.", "\"부인!\" 케이트가 자존심 상한 듯 외쳤어."]], [["‘Don’t agitate me by speaking in that way, Miss Nickleby, don’t,’ said Mrs.", "\"그런 식으로 말해서 날 흥분시키지 말아요, 니클비 양, 그러지 말라고요.\""], ["Wititterly, with some violence, ‘or you’ll compel me to ring the bell.’", "위티털리 부인이 다소 거칠게 말했어. \"안 그러면 종을 울리게 만들 테니까.\""]], [["Kate looked at her, but said nothing.", "케이트는 부인을 바라봤지만 아무 말도 하지 않았어."]], [["‘You needn’t suppose,’ resumed Mrs.", "\"착각하지 말아요, 니클비 양.\" 위티털리 부인이 말을 이었어."], ["Wititterly, ‘that your looking at me in that way, Miss Nickleby, will prevent my saying what I am going to say, which I feel to be a religious duty.", "\"양이 그런 눈으로 날 쳐다본다고 해서, 내가 하려는 말을 못 하리라고는 생각 마요. 나는 이게 종교적인 의무라고 느낀다고요."], ["You needn’t direct your glances towards me,’ said Mrs.", "그렇게 나를 빤히 쳐다볼 것도 없어요.\" 위티털리 부인이 별안간 앙심을 터뜨리며 말했어."], ["Wititterly, with a sudden burst of spite; ‘I am not Sir Mulberry, no, nor Lord Frederick Verisopht, Miss Nickleby, nor am I Mr.", "\"나는 멀버리 경이 아니에요. 아니고말고요, 프레더릭 베리소프트 경도 아니고요, 니클비 양."], ["Pyke, nor Mr. Pluck either.’", "파이크 씨도 플럭 씨도 아니라고요.\""]], [["Kate looked at her again, but less steadily than before; and resting her elbow on the table, covered her eyes with her hand.", "케이트는 부인을 다시 바라봤지만, 아까보다는 흔들리는 눈길이었어. 그러고는 식탁에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손으로 두 눈을 가렸지."]], [["‘If such things had been done when I was a young girl,’ said Mrs Wititterly (this, by the way, must have been some little time before), ‘I don’t suppose anybody would have believed it.’", "\"내가 처녀였을 적에 이런 일이 있었더라면,\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어 (그건 그렇고, 그건 어지간히 오래전 일이었을 거야). \"아마 아무도 안 믿었을 거예요.\""]], [["‘I don’t think they would,’ murmured Kate.", "\"안 믿었을 거예요.\" 케이트가 중얼거렸어."], ["‘I do not think anybody would believe, without actually knowing it, what I seem doomed to undergo!’", "\"직접 겪어 보지 않고서야, 제가 겪을 운명에 놓인 듯한 이 일을 아무도 믿지 못할 거예요!\""]], [["‘Don’t talk to me of being doomed to undergo, Miss Nickleby, if you please,’ said Mrs.", "\"제발 나한테 겪을 운명이니 뭐니 하는 말은 말아 줘요, 니클비 양.\""], ["Wititterly, with a shrillness of tone quite surprising in so great an invalid.", "위티털리 부인이 말했어. 그렇게 중한 환자치고는 자못 놀랄 만큼 날카로운 목소리였지."], ["‘I will not be answered, Miss Nickleby.", "\"나는 말대꾸를 받지 않겠어요, 니클비 양."], ["I am not accustomed to be answered, nor will I permit it for an instant.", "나는 말대꾸를 받는 데 익숙하지 않고, 한순간도 그걸 용납하지 않을 거예요."], ["Do you hear?’ she added, waiting with some apparent inconsistency FOR an answer.", "알아들어요?\" 부인은 다소 앞뒤가 안 맞게도 대답을 기다리며 덧붙였어."]], [["‘I do hear you, ma’am,’ replied Kate, ‘with surprise--with greater surprise than I can express.’", "\"알아듣고 있어요, 부인.\" 케이트가 대답했어. \"놀라면서요 — 말로 다 못할 만큼 큰 놀라움으로요.\""]], [["‘I have always considered you a particularly well-behaved young person for your station in life,’ said Mrs.", "\"나는 늘 양을 그 처지에 비하면 유난히 행실 바른 젊은이로 여겨 왔어요.\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어."], ["Wititterly; ‘and as you are a person of healthy appearance, and neat in your dress and so forth, I have taken an interest in you, as I do still, considering that I owe a sort of duty to that respectable old female, your mother.", "\"그리고 양은 건강해 보이고 옷매무새도 단정하고 하니, 양에게 관심을 가져 온 거고, 지금도 그래요. 양의 어머니, 그 점잖은 노부인에게 일종의 의무가 있다고 생각해서 말이지요."], ["For these reasons, Miss Nickleby, I must tell you once for all, and begging you to mind what I say, that I must insist upon your immediately altering your very forward behaviour to the gentlemen who visit at this house.", "이런 이유들 때문에, 니클비 양, 이번 한 번만 똑똑히 말해 두겠어요. 그리고 내 말을 새겨듣길 바라요."], ["It really is not becoming,’ said Mrs.", "이 집을 찾는 신사분들에 대한 양의 그 지나치게 헤픈 행동을 당장 고쳐 줘야겠어요. 그건 정말 어울리지 않아요.\""], ["Wititterly, closing her chaste eyes as she spoke; ‘it is improper--quite improper.’", "위티털리 부인이 말하며 그 정숙한 눈을 감았어. \"온당치 못해요 — 아주 온당치 못하다고요.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ cried Kate, looking upwards and clasping her hands; ‘is not this, is not this, too cruel, too hard to bear!", "\"오!\" 케이트가 위를 올려다보며 두 손을 맞잡고 외쳤어."], ["Is it not enough that I should have suffered as I have, night and day; that I should almost have sunk in my own estimation from very shame of having been brought into contact with such people; but must I also be exposed to this unjust and most unfounded charge!’", "\"이건, 이건 너무 잔인하고, 너무 견디기 힘들어요! 제가 밤낮없이 이렇게 시달려 온 것, 그런 사람들과 엮였다는 수치심에 제 자신을 보는 눈조차 거의 무너져 내린 것, 그것만으로도 모자라서, 이제 이렇게 부당하고 터무니없는 누명까지 뒤집어써야 하나요!\""]], [["‘You will have the goodness to recollect, Miss Nickleby,’ said Mrs Wititterly, ‘that when you use such terms as “unjust”, and “unfounded”, you charge me, in effect, with stating that which is untrue.’", "\"부디 잘 새겨 두길 바라요, 니클비 양.\" 위티털리 부인이 말했어. \"양이 '부당하다'느니 '터무니없다'느니 하는 말을 쓸 때, 그건 결국 내가 사실이 아닌 걸 말하고 있다고 몰아붙이는 거예요.\""]], [["‘I do,’ said Kate with honest indignation.", "\"그래요.\" 케이트가 정직한 분노로 말했어."], ["‘Whether you make this accusation of yourself, or at the prompting of others, is alike to me.", "\"이 비난을 부인 스스로 하시는 거든, 다른 사람들이 시켜서 하시는 거든, 저한테는 매한가지예요. 분명히 말하는데, 그건 비열하고, 터무니없고, 고의적인 거짓말이에요."], ["I say it IS vilely, grossly, wilfully untrue.", "어떻게 이럴 수가 있죠!\" 케이트가 외쳤어."], ["Is it possible!’ cried Kate, ‘that anyone of my own sex can have sat by, and not have seen the misery these men have caused me?", "\"저와 같은 여자가 곁에 앉아 있으면서, 이 남자들이 제게 안긴 그 고통을 못 봤을 수가 있나요? 어떻게 부인이, 부인이 그 자리에 함께 있으면서, 그자들의 눈빛 하나하나에 묻어나는 그 모욕적인 무례함을 못 알아챘을 수가 있나요?"], ["Is it possible that you, ma’am, can have been present, and failed to mark the insulting freedom that their every look bespoke?", "어떻게 부인이 못 보고 넘어갈 수가 있나요. 이 난봉꾼들이, 부인을 깡그리 무시하고, 신사다운 행실은 물론이고 예의마저 깡그리 저버린 채로, 단 한 가지 목적으로만 여기 발을 들였다는 걸요."], ["Is it possible that you can have avoided seeing, that these libertines, in their utter disrespect for you, and utter disregard of all gentlemanly behaviour, and almost of decency, have had but one object in introducing themselves here, and that the furtherance of their designs upon a friendless, helpless girl, who, without this humiliating confession, might have hoped to receive from one so much her senior something like womanly aid and sympathy?", "그건 친구도 없고 의지할 데도 없는 한 여자를 노린 제 흉계를 밀어붙이려는 거예요. 그 여자는, 이렇게 굴욕스럽게 털어놓지 않았더라면, 그렇게 자기보다 한참 나이 든 분에게서 여자다운 도움과 동정 비슷한 거라도 받기를 바랐을지 모르는데 말이에요."], ["I do not--I cannot believe it!’", "저는 안 믿어요 — 도저히 못 믿겠어요!\""]], [["If poor Kate had possessed the slightest knowledge of the world, she certainly would not have ventured, even in the excitement into which she had been lashed, upon such an injudicious speech as this.", "가엾은 케이트가 세상 물정을 조금이라도 알았더라면, 아무리 그렇게 격분으로 내몰린 상태였대도, 이런 분별없는 말은 결코 입에 담지 않았을 거야."], ["Its effect was precisely what a more experienced observer would have foreseen. Mrs.", "그 말의 효과는 좀 더 노련한 사람이라면 능히 내다봤을 그대로였지."], ["Wititterly received the attack upon her veracity with exemplary calmness, and listened with the most heroic fortitude to Kate’s account of her own sufferings.", "위티털리 부인은 자기 진실성에 대한 그 공격을 모범적인 침착함으로 받아넘겼어. 그리고 케이트가 자기 고통을 늘어놓는 동안에도 더없이 영웅적인 꿋꿋함으로 들었지."], ["But allusion being made to her being held in disregard by the gentlemen, she evinced violent emotion, and this blow was no sooner followed up by the remark concerning her seniority, than she fell back upon the sofa, uttering dismal screams.", "하지만 신사들이 자기를 무시한다는 대목이 나오자 부인은 격한 감정을 드러냈어. 그리고 이 일격에 뒤이어 자기 나이에 관한 말까지 나오자마자, 부인은 소파에 벌렁 쓰러져 처량한 비명을 질렀지."]], [["‘What is the matter?’ cried Mr.", "\"무슨 일이야?\""], ["Wititterly, bouncing into the room.", "위티털리 씨가 방으로 튀어 들어오며 외쳤어."], ["‘Heavens, what do I see?", "\"맙소사, 이게 웬일이야?"], ["Julia!", "줄리아!"], ["Julia!", "줄리아!"], ["look up, my life, look up!’", "고개를 들어 봐, 여보, 고개를 들어 보라고!\""]], [["But Julia looked down most perseveringly, and screamed still louder; so Mr.", "그런데 줄리아는 한사코 고개를 떨군 채 더 크게 비명을 질러 댔어."], ["Wititterly rang the bell, and danced in a frenzied manner round the sofa on which Mrs.", "그래서 위티털리 씨는 종을 울리고는, 위티털리 부인이 누운 소파 둘레를 미친 듯이 빙빙 돌며 춤을 추듯 했지."], ["Wititterly lay; uttering perpetual cries for Sir Tumley Snuffim, and never once leaving off to ask for any explanation of the scene before him.", "텀리 스너핌 경을 끊임없이 부르짖으면서, 단 한 번도 멈춰서 눈앞의 이 광경에 대해 무슨 설명을 청하지도 않으면서 말이야."]], [["‘Run for Sir Tumley,’ cried Mr.", "\"텀리 경을 모셔 와!\""], ["Wititterly, menacing the page with both fists.", "위티털리 씨가 시동에게 두 주먹을 휘두르며 외쳤어."], ["‘I knew it, Miss Nickleby,’ he said, looking round with an air of melancholy triumph, ‘that society has been too much for her.", "\"내 그럴 줄 알았소, 니클비 양.\" 그가 처량한 승리감을 띤 채 둘러보며 말했어."], ["This is all soul, you know, every bit of it.’ With this assurance Mr.", "\"사교계가 저 사람한테는 너무 벅찼던 거요. 이게 다 영혼 탓이오, 알겠소, 죄다 영혼 탓이라고.\""], ["Wititterly took up the prostrate form of Mrs. Wititterly, and carried her bodily off to bed.", "이렇게 단언하고서 위티털리 씨는 늘어진 위티털리 부인의 몸을 들어 올려, 통째로 안고 침실로 데려갔어."]], [["Kate waited until Sir Tumley Snuffim had paid his visit and looked in with a report, that, through the special interposition of a merciful Providence (thus spake Sir Tumley), Mrs.", "케이트는 텀리 스너핌 경이 다녀가기를 기다렸어. 경은 들여다보고 나서, 자비로우신 섭리의 각별한 보살핌으로 (텀리 경의 말이 그랬어) 위티털리 부인이 잠이 들었노라고 알렸지."], ["Wititterly had gone to sleep. She then hastily attired herself for walking, and leaving word that she should return within a couple of hours, hurried away towards her uncle’s house.", "그러자 케이트는 황급히 외출 차림을 하고는, 두어 시간 안에 돌아오겠다는 말을 남기고 삼촌 집을 향해 서둘러 떠났어."]], [["It had been a good day with Ralph Nickleby--quite a lucky day; and as he walked to and fro in his little back-room with his hands clasped behind him, adding up in his own mind all the sums that had been, or would be, netted from the business done since morning, his mouth was drawn into a hard stern smile; while the firmness of the lines and curves that made it up, as well as the cunning glance of his cold, bright eye, seemed to tell, that if any resolution or cunning would increase the profits, they would not fail to be excited for the purpose.", "랄프 니클비에게는 운수 좋은 하루였어 — 그야말로 재수가 좋은 날이었지. 그는 두 손을 등 뒤로 맞잡고 자기 뒤편 작은 방을 이리저리 거닐면서, 아침나절부터 해 온 거래로 거둬들였거나 거둬들일 액수를 머릿속으로 죄다 더해 보고 있었어. 그의 입가에는 딱딱하고 매서운 미소가 그려졌지. 그 입을 이룬 선과 굴곡의 단단함이며, 차갑고 번득이는 눈의 음흉한 눈빛이며, 그 모두가 말해 주는 듯했어. 어떤 결단이나 술수로 이익을 더 불릴 수 있다면, 그는 그걸 위해 그것들을 기어이 불러낼 사람이라고 말이야."]], [["‘Very good!’ said Ralph, in allusion, no doubt, to some proceeding of the day.", "\"아주 좋아!\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘He defies the usurer, does he?", "분명 그날 있었던 어떤 거래를 두고 한 말이었지. \"그자가 고리대금업자한테 대든단 말이지?"], ["Well, we shall see.", "그래, 두고 보자고."], ["“Honesty is the best policy,”", "'정직이 최선의 방책'이라?"], ["is it? We’ll try that too.’", "그것도 한번 시험해 보자고.\""]], [["He stopped, and then walked on again.", "그는 멈춰 섰다가, 다시 걷기 시작했어."]], [["‘He is content,’ said Ralph, relaxing into a smile, ‘to set his known character and conduct against the power of money--dross, as he calls it.", "\"그자는 만족한다는 거지.\" 랄프가 미소를 띠며 말했어."], ["Why, what a dull blockhead this fellow must be!", "\"제 알려진 인품이며 행실을 돈의 힘에 맞세우는 걸로 말이야 — 그자 말마따나 쓰레기 같은 돈에 말이지. 흥, 그 작자는 어지간히 멍청한 돌대가리가 틀림없어!"], ["Dross to, dross!", "쓰레기라니, 돈이!"], ["Who’s that?’", "누구냐?\""]], [["‘Me,’ said Newman Noggs, looking in.", "\"접니다.\" 뉴먼 노그스가 들여다보며 말했어."], ["‘Your niece.’", "\"조카 따님이요.\""]], [["‘What of her?’ asked Ralph sharply.", "\"그 애가 왜?\" 랄프가 날카롭게 물었어."]], [["‘She’s here.’\n‘Here!’", "\"여기 와 있습니다.\""]], [["Newman jerked his head towards his little room, to signify that she was waiting there.", "\"여기에!\" 뉴먼이 자기 작은 방 쪽으로 고개를 까딱여, 거기서 기다리고 있다는 걸 알렸어."]], [["‘What does she want?’ asked Ralph.", "\"무슨 일로 왔다더냐?\" 랄프가 물었어."]], [["‘I don’t know,’ rejoined Newman.", "\"모르겠는데요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어."], ["‘Shall I ask?’ he added quickly.", "\"물어볼까요?\" 그가 얼른 덧붙였지."]], [["‘No,’ replied Ralph.", "\"아니.\" 랄프가 대답했어."], ["‘Show her in!", "\"들여보내!"], ["Stay.’ He hastily put away a padlocked cash-box that was on the table, and substituted in its stead an empty purse.", "잠깐.\" 그는 식탁 위에 있던 자물쇠 채운 현금 상자를 황급히 치우고, 그 자리에 빈 지갑을 대신 놓았어."], ["‘There,’ said Ralph.", "\"됐다.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘NOW she may come in.’", "\"자, 이제 들어와도 좋아.\""]], [["Newman, with a grim smile at this manoeuvre, beckoned the young lady to advance, and having placed a chair for her, retired; looking stealthily over his shoulder at Ralph as he limped slowly out.", "뉴먼은 이 수작에 음울한 웃음을 지으며 아가씨에게 들어오라고 손짓했어. 그리고 의자를 하나 놓아 준 다음 물러갔지. 절뚝거리며 천천히 나가면서 어깨너머로 슬쩍 랄프를 돌아보면서 말이야."]], [["‘Well,’ said Ralph, roughly enough; but still with something more of kindness in his manner than he would have exhibited towards anybody else.", "\"자.\" 랄프가 제법 퉁명스럽게 말했어."], ["‘Well, my--dear.", "그래도 다른 누구한테 보였을 것보다는 조금 더 다정한 구석이 묻어 있었지. \"자, 우리 — 얘야."], ["What now?’", "무슨 일이냐?\""]], [["Kate raised her eyes, which were filled with tears; and with an effort to master her emotion strove to speak, but in vain.", "케이트는 눈물이 그렁그렁한 눈을 들었어. 그리고 감정을 다잡으려 애쓰며 입을 떼려 했지만 헛일이었지."], ["So drooping her head again, she remained silent.", "그래서 다시 고개를 떨군 채 입을 다물고 있었어."], ["Her face was hidden from his view, but Ralph could see that she was weeping.", "그 얼굴은 랄프의 눈에서 가려져 있었지만, 그래도 그는 케이트가 울고 있다는 걸 알 수 있었어."]], [["‘I can guess the cause of this!’ thought Ralph, after looking at her for some time in silence.", "'이게 무슨 영문인지 짐작이 가는군!' 랄프는 한동안 말없이 케이트를 바라보다가 생각했어."], ["‘I can--I can--guess the cause.", "'짐작이 가 — 짐작이 가고말고 — 영문을 알겠어. 흥!"], ["Well!", "흥!'"], ["Well!’ thought Ralph--for the moment quite disconcerted, as he watched the anguish of his beautiful niece.", "랄프는 그렇게 생각했어 — 아름다운 조카의 비통함을 지켜보며 순간 적잖이 당황했지."], ["‘Where is the harm?", "'뭐 해로울 게 있나?"], ["only a few tears; and it’s an excellent lesson for her, an excellent lesson.’", "그저 눈물 몇 방울일 뿐이고, 이건 저 애한테 더없이 좋은 교훈이야, 더없이 좋은 교훈이지.'"]], [["‘What is the matter?’ asked Ralph, drawing a chair opposite, and sitting down.", "\"무슨 일이냐?\" 랄프가 맞은편에 의자를 끌어다 앉으며 물었어."]], [["He was rather taken aback by the sudden firmness with which Kate looked up and answered him.", "케이트가 별안간 단호하게 고개를 들고 대답하자, 그는 적잖이 움찔했지."]], [["‘The matter which brings me to you, sir,’ she said, ‘is one which should call the blood up into your cheeks, and make you burn to hear, as it does me to tell. I have been wronged; my feelings have been outraged, insulted, wounded past all healing, and by your friends.’", "\"저를 삼촌께 오게 만든 일은요,\" 케이트가 말했어. \"삼촌의 얼굴에 피가 솟구치게 하고, 듣기만 해도 화끈 달아오르게 만들 그런 일이에요. 제가 말하기조차 그러는 것처럼요. 저는 부당한 일을 당했어요. 제 감정이 짓밟히고, 모욕당하고, 도무지 아물 수 없을 만큼 상처 입었어요. 그것도 삼촌의 친구들 손에요.\""]], [["‘Friends!’ cried Ralph, sternly.", "\"친구들이라니!\" 랄프가 매섭게 외쳤어."], ["‘I have no friends, girl.’", "\"나한테 친구 따위 없다, 얘야.\""]], [["‘By the men I saw here, then,’ returned Kate, quickly.", "\"그럼 여기서 봤던 그 남자들 손에요.\" 케이트가 얼른 받아쳤어."], ["‘If they were no friends of yours, and you knew what they were,--oh, the more shame on you, uncle, for bringing me among them.", "\"그자들이 삼촌의 친구가 아니었고, 또 그자들이 어떤 자들인지 아셨다면 — 오, 그렇다면 삼촌, 저를 그들 틈에 데려간 게 더더욱 부끄러운 일이에요."], ["To have subjected me to what I was exposed to here, through any misplaced confidence or imperfect knowledge of your guests, would have required some strong excuse; but if you did it--as I now believe you did--knowing them well, it was most dastardly and cruel.’", "삼촌이 손님들을 잘못 믿었거나 잘 알지 못해서 저를 여기서 겪은 그런 일에 내몬 거라면, 그래도 무슨 그럴듯한 변명이라도 필요했겠지요. 하지만 만약 삼촌이 — 제가 지금 믿는 대로 — 그자들을 빤히 알면서도 그러신 거라면, 그건 더없이 비겁하고 잔인한 짓이에요.\""]], [["Ralph drew back in utter amazement at this plain speaking, and regarded Kate with the sternest look.", "랄프는 이 거침없는 말에 어이가 없어 뒤로 물러서며, 더없이 매서운 눈으로 케이트를 노려봤어."], ["But she met his gaze proudly and firmly, and although her face was very pale, it looked more noble and handsome, lighted up as it was, than it had ever appeared before.", "하지만 케이트는 당당하고 단단하게 그 시선을 맞받았지. 얼굴은 몹시 창백했지만, 그렇게 환히 빛이 어린 그 얼굴은 어느 때보다도 더 고결하고 아름다워 보였어."]], [["‘There is some of that boy’s blood in you, I see,’ said Ralph, speaking in his harshest tones, as something in the flashing eye reminded him of Nicholas at their last meeting.", "\"네 안에 그 녀석 피가 좀 흐르고 있구나.\" 랄프가 더없이 거친 어조로 말했어. 그 번득이는 눈빛에서 무언가가, 지난번 마지막으로 만났을 때의 니콜라스를 떠올리게 했거든."]], [["‘I hope there is!’ replied Kate.", "\"그랬으면 좋겠어요!\" 케이트가 대답했어."], ["‘I should be proud to know it.", "\"그렇다는 걸 알면 자랑스러울 거예요. 저는 어려요, 삼촌."], ["I am young, uncle, and all the difficulties and miseries of my situation have kept it down, but I have been roused today beyond all endurance, and come what may, I WILL NOT, as I am your brother’s child, bear these insults longer.’", "제 처지의 온갖 어려움과 비참함이 그걸 짓눌러 왔지만, 오늘은 도저히 견딜 수 없을 만큼 발끈 솟구쳤어요. 무슨 일이 닥치든, 저는 삼촌 동생의 자식으로서, 더는 이런 모욕을 견디지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["‘What insults, girl?’ demanded Ralph, sharply.", "\"무슨 모욕이냐, 얘야?\" 랄프가 날카롭게 따져 물었어."]], [["‘Remember what took place here, and ask yourself,’ replied Kate, colouring deeply.", "\"여기서 무슨 일이 있었는지 떠올려 보고, 스스로에게 물어보세요.\" 케이트가 얼굴을 새빨갛게 붉히며 대답했어."], ["‘Uncle, you must--I am sure you will--release me from such vile and degrading companionship as I am exposed to now.", "\"삼촌, 부디 — 분명 그래 주시리라 믿어요 — 지금 제가 시달리는 그런 더럽고 욕스러운 동무에게서 저를 풀어 주셔야 해요. 화내고 거칠게 굴려는 게 아니에요.\""], ["I do not mean,’ said Kate, hurrying to the old man, and laying her arm upon his shoulder; ‘I do not mean to be angry and violent--I beg your pardon if I have seemed so, dear uncle,--but you do not know what I have suffered, you do not indeed.", "케이트가 노인에게 다가가 그의 어깨에 팔을 얹으며 말했어. \"그렇게 보였다면 용서하세요, 삼촌."], ["You cannot tell what the heart of a young girl is--I have no right to expect you should; but when I tell you that I am wretched, and that my heart is breaking, I am sure you will help me.", "하지만 삼촌은 제가 얼마나 괴로웠는지 모르세요, 정말 모르신다고요. 삼촌은 젊은 처녀의 마음이 어떤지 알 수가 없어요 — 제가 그걸 바랄 권리도 없지요."], ["I am sure, I am sure you will!’", "하지만 제가 비참하고 가슴이 찢어진다고 말씀드리면, 삼촌은 분명 저를 도와주실 거예요. 분명, 분명 그러실 거예요!\""]], [["Ralph looked at her for an instant; then turned away his head, and beat his foot nervously upon the ground.", "랄프는 잠시 케이트를 바라봤어. 그러고는 고개를 돌리고, 초조하게 발로 바닥을 굴렀지."]], [["‘I have gone on day after day,’ said Kate, bending over him, and timidly placing her little hand in his, ‘in the hope that this persecution would cease; I have gone on day after day, compelled to assume the appearance of cheerfulness, when I was most unhappy. I have had no counsellor, no adviser, no one to protect me. Mama supposes that these are honourable men, rich and distinguished, and how CAN I--how can I undeceive her--when she is so happy in these little delusions, which are the only happiness she has? The lady with whom you placed me, is not the person to whom I could confide matters of so much delicacy, and I have come at last to you, the only friend I have at hand--almost the only friend I have at all--to entreat and implore you to assist me.’", "\"저는 날이면 날마다 버텨 왔어요.\" 케이트가 그에게 몸을 숙이고, 자기 작은 손을 그의 손에 머뭇머뭇 얹으며 말했어. \"이 괴롭힘이 멎으리라는 희망으로요. 더없이 불행하면서도 즐거운 척 가장하지 않을 수 없는 채로, 날이면 날마다 버텨 왔어요. 저한테는 의논 상대도, 조언해 줄 사람도, 지켜 줄 사람도 없었어요. 엄마는 그들이 점잖고 부유하고 지체 높은 사람들인 줄로 알아요. 그런데 제가 어떻게 — 제가 어떻게 그 오해를 풀어 드리겠어요 — 엄마가 그런 자그마한 착각에 그토록 행복해하는데요. 엄마한테는 그게 유일한 행복인데 말이에요. 삼촌이 저를 맡기신 그 부인은 이렇게 미묘한 일을 털어놓을 만한 사람이 못 돼요. 그래서 마침내 삼촌께 온 거예요. 가까이에 있는 제 유일한 친구이자 — 사실상 제게 있는 단 하나뿐인 친구한테요 — 저를 도와 달라고 빌고 또 빌려고요.\""]], [["‘How can I assist you, child?’ said Ralph, rising from his chair, and pacing up and down the room in his old attitude.", "\"내가 너를 어떻게 돕겠느냐, 얘야?\" 랄프가 의자에서 일어나, 옛 버릇대로 방 안을 이리저리 거닐며 말했어."]], [["‘You have influence with one of these men, I KNOW,’ rejoined Kate, emphatically.", "\"삼촌은 그 사람들 중 한 명한테 영향력이 있잖아요, 저 알아요.\" 케이트가 힘주어 받았어."], ["‘Would not a word from you induce them to desist from this unmanly course?’", "\"삼촌의 한마디면 그자들이 이 사내답지 못한 짓을 그만두게 만들 수 있지 않나요?\""]], [["‘No,’ said Ralph, suddenly turning; ‘at least--that--I can’t say it, if it would.’", "\"안 돼.\" 랄프가 별안간 돌아서며 말했어. \"적어도 — 그건 — 설령 그럴 수 있다 해도, 그 말은 못 해.\""]], [["‘Can’t say it!’", "\"못 하신다고요!\""]], [["‘No,’ said Ralph, coming to a dead stop, and clasping his hands more tightly behind him.", "\"못 해.\" 랄프가 우뚝 멈춰 서서 두 손을 등 뒤로 더 단단히 맞잡으며 말했어."], ["‘I can’t say it.’", "\"그 말은 못 한다.\""]], [["Kate fell back a step or two, and looked at him, as if in doubt whether she had heard aright.", "케이트는 한두 걸음 물러서서, 자기가 제대로 들은 게 맞나 미심쩍어하는 듯 그를 바라봤어."]], [["‘We are connected in business,’ said Ralph, poising himself alternately on his toes and heels, and looking coolly in his niece’s face, ‘in business, and I can’t afford to offend them.", "\"우리는 사업으로 얽혀 있다.\" 랄프가 발끝과 뒤꿈치에 번갈아 몸을 싣고 흔들거리며, 조카의 얼굴을 차갑게 들여다보며 말했어."], ["What is it after all?", "\"사업으로 말이야."], ["We have all our trials, and this is one of yours.", "그러니 그들의 비위를 거스를 여유가 없어. 따지고 보면 이게 다 뭐냐?"], ["Some girls would be proud to have such gallants at their feet.’", "사람은 누구나 제 시련이 있는 법이고, 이건 네 시련 가운데 하나인 거지. 어떤 처녀들은 그런 멋쟁이들을 제 발치에 거느리는 걸 자랑스러워할 거다.\""]], [["‘Proud!’ cried Kate.", "\"자랑스럽다고요!\" 케이트가 외쳤어."]], [["‘I don’t say,’ rejoined Ralph, raising his forefinger, ‘but that you do right to despise them; no, you show your good sense in that, as indeed I knew from the first you would.", "\"그자들을 경멸하는 게 잘못이라는 건 아니다.\" 랄프가 집게손가락을 치켜들며 받았어."], ["Well. In all other respects you are comfortably bestowed.", "\"아니지, 그 점에선 네가 분별 있게 처신하는 거고, 그야 처음부터 네가 그러리라는 걸 내가 알고 있었지."], ["It’s not much to bear.", "그래. 다른 모든 점에서 너는 편안히 자리 잡고 있잖느냐."], ["If this young lord does dog your footsteps, and whisper his drivelling inanities in your ears, what of it?", "견디기에 그리 대단한 일도 아니야. 설령 그 젊은 귀족이 네 뒤를 졸졸 쫓아다니며 시답잖은 헛소리를 네 귀에 속닥거린들, 그게 뭐 대수냐?"], ["It’s a dishonourable passion.", "그건 떳떳치 못한 욕정이야."], ["So be it; it won’t last long.", "그래 봤자지, 오래가지도 못해."], ["Some other novelty will spring up one day, and you will be released.", "언젠가 또 다른 새것이 나타날 거고, 그러면 너는 풀려나는 거다."], ["In the mean time--’", "그동안에는 —\""]], [["‘In the mean time,’ interrupted Kate, with becoming pride and indignation, ‘I am to be the scorn of my own sex, and the toy of the other; justly condemned by all women of right feeling, and despised by all honest and honourable men; sunken in my own esteem, and degraded in every eye that looks upon me. No, not if I work my fingers to the bone, not if I am driven to the roughest and hardest labour. Do not mistake me. I will not disgrace your recommendation. I will remain in the house in which it placed me, until I am entitled to leave it by the terms of my engagement; though, mind, I see these men no more. When I quit it, I will hide myself from them and you, and, striving to support my mother by hard service, I will live, at least, in peace, and trust in God to help me.’", "\"그동안에는,\" 케이트가 어울리는 자존심과 분노로 말을 가로챘어. \"저는 같은 여자들한테는 멸시받고, 남자들한테는 노리갯감이 되라는 거군요. 올바른 마음을 지닌 모든 여자에게 마땅히 손가락질당하고, 정직하고 떳떳한 모든 남자에게 업신여김당하면서요. 제 자신을 보는 눈에서도 무너지고, 저를 쳐다보는 모든 눈앞에서 더럽혀지면서요. 안 돼요, 손가락이 뼈가 드러나도록 일을 한대도, 더없이 거칠고 고된 노동에 내몰린대도, 그렇게는 못 해요. 저를 오해하지는 마세요. 삼촌의 추천을 욕되게 하진 않을게요. 계약 조건에 따라 떠날 자격이 생길 때까지는 삼촌이 들여보내 주신 그 집에 머물겠어요. 다만, 명심하세요, 그 남자들은 두 번 다시 안 볼 거예요. 그 집을 떠나면 저는 그들에게서도 삼촌에게서도 몸을 숨길 거예요. 그리고 고된 일로 엄마를 부양하려 애쓰면서, 적어도 마음 편히 살아갈 거예요. 하느님이 저를 도와주시리라 믿으면서요.\""]], [["With these words, she waved her hand, and quitted the room, leaving Ralph Nickleby motionless as a statue.", "이 말과 함께 케이트는 손을 한 번 내젓고 방을 나가 버렸어. 랄프 니클비를 조각상처럼 꼼짝 못 하게 남겨 둔 채로 말이야."]], [["The surprise with which Kate, as she closed the room-door, beheld, close beside it, Newman Noggs standing bolt upright in a little niche in the wall like some scarecrow or Guy Faux laid up in winter quarters, almost occasioned her to call aloud.", "케이트가 방문을 닫다가, 바로 그 곁에 뉴먼 노그스가 꼿꼿이 서 있는 걸 봤을 때, 그 놀라움이 어찌나 컸던지 하마터면 소리를 지를 뻔했어. 뉴먼은 벽의 작은 벽감 안에 마치 허수아비나, 겨울을 나려고 처박아 둔 가이 포크스 인형처럼 서 있었거든."], ["But, Newman laying his finger upon his lips, she had the presence of mind to refrain.", "그런데 뉴먼이 손가락을 입에 갖다 대자, 케이트는 정신을 차리고 가까스로 참았어."]], [["‘Don’t,’ said Newman, gliding out of his recess, and accompanying her across the hall.", "\"그러지 마요.\" 뉴먼이 자기 구석에서 빠져나와 케이트와 함께 현관을 가로지르며 말했어."], ["‘Don’t cry, don’t cry.’ Two very large tears, by-the-bye, were running down Newman’s face as he spoke.", "\"울지 마요, 울지 마요.\" 그런데 그렇게 말하는 뉴먼의 얼굴에는 아주 굵은 눈물 두 줄기가 흘러내리고 있었지."]], [["‘I see how it is,’ said poor Noggs, drawing from his pocket what seemed to be a very old duster, and wiping Kate’s eyes with it, as gently as if she were an infant.", "\"어떻게 된 일인지 다 압니다.\" 가엾은 노그스가 주머니에서 아주 낡은 걸레처럼 보이는 걸 꺼내 케이트의 눈을 닦아 주며 말했어."], ["‘You’re giving way now.", "마치 갓난아기를 다루듯 더없이 부드럽게 말이야."], ["Yes, yes, very good; that’s right, I like that.", "\"이제 마음이 풀어지는 거지요. 그래요, 그래요, 아주 좋아요."], ["It was right not to give way before him.", "그래야지요, 나는 그게 좋습니다. 그 사람 앞에서 무너지지 않은 건 잘한 일이에요."], ["Yes, yes!", "그래요, 그래!"], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하하하!"], ["Oh, yes.", "오, 그렇고말고요."], ["Poor thing!’", "가엾기도 하지!\""]], [["With these disjointed exclamations, Newman wiped his own eyes with the afore-mentioned duster, and, limping to the street-door, opened it to let her out.", "이런 두서없는 말들과 함께, 뉴먼은 앞서 말한 그 걸레로 제 눈을 닦았어. 그러고는 절뚝거리며 현관문으로 가서, 케이트를 내보내려고 문을 열어 주었지."]], [["‘Don’t cry any more,’ whispered Newman.", "\"이제 더는 울지 마요.\" 뉴먼이 속삭였어."], ["‘I shall see you soon.", "\"곧 또 봅시다."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["And so shall somebody else too.", "그리고 다른 누군가도 그럴 겁니다."], ["Yes, yes.", "그래요, 그래요."], ["Ho!", "호!"], ["ho!’", "호!\""]], [["‘God bless you,’ answered Kate, hurrying out, ‘God bless you.’", "\"하느님의 축복이 함께하길 빌어요.\" 케이트가 서둘러 나가며 대답했어. \"하느님의 축복이 함께하길요.\""]], [["‘Same to you,’ rejoined Newman, opening the door again a little way to say so.", "\"그쪽도요.\" 뉴먼이 그 말을 하려고 문을 다시 조금 열며 받았어."], ["‘Ha, ha, ha!", "\"하, 하, 하!"], ["Ho!", "호!"], ["ho!", "호!"], ["ho!’", "호!\""]], [["And Newman Noggs opened the door once again to nod cheerfully, and laugh--and shut it, to shake his head mournfully, and cry.", "그리고 뉴먼 노그스는 다시 한번 문을 열어 명랑하게 고개를 끄덕이고 웃었어 — 그러고는 문을 닫고서, 서글피 고개를 저으며 울었지."]], [["Ralph remained in the same attitude till he heard the noise of the closing door, when he shrugged his shoulders, and after a few turns about the room--hasty at first, but gradually becoming slower, as he relapsed into himself--sat down before his desk.", "랄프는 문 닫히는 소리를 들을 때까지 똑같은 자세로 서 있었어. 그러다 어깨를 으쓱하더니, 방 안을 몇 바퀴 돌고는 — 처음에는 빠르다가 차츰 느려졌어. 자기 안으로 다시 가라앉으면서 말이야 — 책상 앞에 앉았지."]], [["It is one of those problems of human nature, which may be noted down, but not solved;--although Ralph felt no remorse at that moment for his conduct towards the innocent, true-hearted girl; although his libertine clients had done precisely what he had expected, precisely what he most wished, and precisely what would tend most to his advantage, still he hated them for doing it, from the very bottom of his soul.", "이건 인간 본성의 수수께끼 가운데 하나야. 적어 둘 수는 있어도 풀어낼 수는 없는 그런 거 말이지. 랄프는 그 순간 순진하고 진실한 마음을 지닌 그 아가씨에게 한 짓을 두고 아무런 양심의 가책도 느끼지 않았어. 그 난봉꾼 고객들은 그가 예상했던 바로 그대로, 그가 가장 바라던 바로 그대로, 그에게 가장 이로울 바로 그대로 행동했지. 그런데도 그는 그들이 그런 짓을 했다는 것 때문에, 마음속 깊은 밑바닥에서부터 그들을 미워했어."]], [["‘Ugh!’ said Ralph, scowling round, and shaking his clenched hand as the faces of the two profligates rose up before his mind; ‘you shall pay for this.", "\"으윽!\" 랄프가 인상을 잔뜩 찌푸리고 둘러보며, 두 난봉꾼의 얼굴이 머릿속에 떠오르자 움켜쥔 주먹을 부르르 떨며 말했어."], ["Oh!", "\"이 대가를 치르게 해 주마."], ["you shall pay for this!’", "오! 이 대가를 꼭 치르게 해 주마!\""]], [["As the usurer turned for consolation to his books and papers, a performance was going on outside his office door, which would have occasioned him no small surprise, if he could by any means have become acquainted with it.", "고리대금업자가 위안을 얻으려고 자기 장부와 서류 쪽으로 돌아섰을 때, 그의 사무실 문밖에서는 한바탕 연극이 벌어지고 있었어. 만약 그가 무슨 수로든 그걸 알았더라면 적잖이 놀랐을 거야."]], [["Newman Noggs was the sole actor.", "뉴먼 노그스가 그 유일한 배우였지."], ["He stood at a little distance from the door, with his face towards it; and with the sleeves of his coat turned back at the wrists, was occupied in bestowing the most vigorous, scientific, and straightforward blows upon the empty air.", "그는 문에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 문을 마주 보고 서 있었어. 그리고 외투 소매를 손목께까지 걷어붙인 채, 텅 빈 허공에다 더없이 힘차고, 정확하고, 거침없는 주먹질을 퍼붓고 있었지."]], [["At first sight, this would have appeared merely a wise precaution in a man of sedentary habits, with the view of opening the chest and strengthening the muscles of the arms.", "언뜻 보면, 이건 그저 앉아서 일하는 사람이 가슴을 펴고 팔 근육을 단련하려는 현명한 운동쯤으로 보였을 거야. 하지만 땀으로 흠뻑 젖은 뉴먼 노그스의 얼굴에 떠오른 그 격렬한 열의와 기쁨, 그리고 바닥에서 다섯 자 여덟 치쯤 되는 특정한 한 패널을 향해 쉴 새 없이 연거푸 주먹을 날리면서도 더없이 지칠 줄 모르고 끈질기게 계속 휘둘러 대는 그 놀라운 기운을 보았다면, 눈썰미 있는 사람이라면 능히 알아챘을 거야."], ["But the intense eagerness and joy depicted in the face of Newman Noggs, which was suffused with perspiration; the surprising energy with which he directed a constant succession of blows towards a particular panel about five feet eight from the ground, and still worked away in the most untiring and persevering manner, would have sufficiently explained to the attentive observer, that his imagination was thrashing, to within an inch of his life, his body’s most active employer, Mr. Ralph Nickleby.", "뉴먼은 머릿속으로, 제 몸뚱이를 가장 부려 먹는 그 주인 랄프 니클비 씨를 숨이 끊어지기 직전까지 흠씬 두들겨 패고 있다는 걸 말이지."]], [["# CHAPTER 29", "# CHAPTER 29"]], [["Of the Proceedings of Nicholas, and certain Internal Divisions in the Company of Mr. Vincent Crummles\nThe unexpected success and favour with which his experiment at Portsmouth had been received, induced Mr.", "포츠머스에서 벌인 그의 시도가 뜻밖에도 큰 성공을 거두고 인기를 끌자, 크럼블스 씨는 원래 머무르려고 잡아 둔 기간보다 두 주나 더 그 도시에 발길을 붙들어 두기로 했어."], ["Crummles to prolong his stay in that town for a fortnight beyond the period he had originally assigned for the duration of his visit, during which time Nicholas personated a vast variety of characters with undiminished success, and attracted so many people to the theatre who had never been seen there before, that a benefit was considered by the manager a very promising speculation.", "그동안 니콜라스는 온갖 다양한 인물을 연기했는데, 그 성공이 조금도 시들지 않았지. 그리고 전에는 한 번도 극장에 나타난 적 없던 사람들을 어찌나 많이 끌어모았던지, 극단장은 니콜라스를 위한 자선 공연을 열면 꽤 짭짤한 벌이가 되겠다고 생각했어."], ["Nicholas assenting to the terms proposed, the benefit was had, and by it he realised no less a sum than twenty pounds.", "니콜라스가 제안받은 조건에 동의해서 자선 공연이 열렸고, 그걸로 그는 자그마치 이십 파운드라는 돈을 벌었지."]], [["Possessed of this unexpected wealth, his first act was to enclose to honest John Browdie the amount of his friendly loan, which he accompanied with many expressions of gratitude and esteem, and many cordial wishes for his matrimonial happiness. To Newman Noggs he forwarded one half of the sum he had realised, entreating him to take an opportunity of handing it to Kate in secret, and conveying to her the warmest assurances of his love and affection. He made no mention of the way in which he had employed himself; merely informing Newman that a letter addressed to him under his assumed name at the Post Office, Portsmouth, would readily find him, and entreating that worthy friend to write full particulars of the situation of his mother and sister, and an account of all the grand things that Ralph Nickleby had done for them since his departure from London.", "이 뜻밖의 재산을 손에 쥐자 그가 가장 먼저 한 일은, 정직한 존 브로디에게 빌린 돈을 챙겨 보낸 거였어. 거기에 고맙다는 말과 존경의 인사를 잔뜩 곁들이고, 결혼 생활이 행복하기를 바란다는 진심 어린 축원도 함께 적었지. 뉴먼 노그스에게는 자기가 번 돈의 절반을 부쳤어. 그러면서 기회를 봐서 케이트한테 몰래 건네 달라고, 그리고 자기가 케이트를 얼마나 사랑하고 아끼는지 따뜻한 마음을 꼭 전해 달라고 부탁했지. 자기가 어떻게 지내고 있는지는 한마디도 하지 않았어. 그저 포츠머스 우체국으로 가명 앞으로 편지를 보내면 자기한테 잘 닿을 거라고만 알렸지. 그리고 그 고마운 친구에게, 어머니와 누이가 어떻게 지내는지 자세히 적어 달라고, 또 자기가 런던을 떠난 뒤로 랄프 니클비가 그들에게 베푼 그 대단한 일들이 무엇인지도 알려 달라고 간곡히 부탁했어."]], [["‘You are out of spirits,’ said Smike, on the night after the letter had been dispatched.", "\"형은 기운이 없어 보여요.\" 편지를 부치고 난 다음 날 밤, 스마이크가 말했어."]], [["‘Not I!’ rejoined Nicholas, with assumed gaiety, for the confession would have made the boy miserable all night; ‘I was thinking about my sister, Smike.’", "\"내가? 천만에!\" 니콜라스가 일부러 명랑한 척 대꾸했어. 솔직히 털어놓으면 이 아이가 밤새 마음 아파할 게 뻔했거든. \"누이 생각을 하고 있었어, 스마이크.\""]], [["‘Sister!’", "\"누이요?\""]], [["‘Ay.’", "\"응.\""]], [["‘Is she like you?’ inquired Smike.", "\"형이랑 닮았어요?\" 스마이크가 물었어."]], [["‘Why, so they say,’ replied Nicholas, laughing, ‘only a great deal handsomer.’", "\"글쎄, 다들 그렇다고들 하더라.\" 니콜라스가 웃으며 대답했어. \"다만 훨씬 더 예쁘지.\""]], [["‘She must be VERY beautiful,’ said Smike, after thinking a little while with his hands folded together, and his eyes bent upon his friend.", "\"그럼 정말 아름답겠네요.\" 스마이크가 두 손을 모으고 친구를 가만히 바라보며 한참 생각하더니 말했어."]], [["‘Anybody who didn’t know you as well as I do, my dear fellow, would say you were an accomplished courtier,’ said Nicholas.", "\"나만큼 너를 잘 모르는 사람이라면, 넌 아주 능숙한 아첨꾼이라고 할 거다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘I don’t even know what that is,’ replied Smike, shaking his head.", "\"그게 뭔지도 모르는걸요.\" 스마이크가 고개를 저으며 대답했어."], ["‘Shall I ever see your sister?’", "\"나도 형 누이를 볼 수 있을까요?\""]], [["‘To be sure,’ cried Nicholas; ‘we shall all be together one of these days--when we are rich, Smike.’", "\"그럼.\" 니콜라스가 소리쳤어. \"언젠가 우리 다 같이 모일 거야. 부자가 되면 말이야, 스마이크.\""]], [["‘How is it that you, who are so kind and good to me, have nobody to be kind to you?’ asked Smike.", "\"나한테 이렇게 친절하고 잘해 주는 형한테는, 어째서 형한테 잘해 주는 사람이 아무도 없어요?\" 스마이크가 물었어."], ["‘I cannot make that out.’", "\"난 도무지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["‘Why, it is a long story,’ replied Nicholas, ‘and one you would have some difficulty in comprehending, I fear. I have an enemy--you understand what that is?’", "\"그건 긴 이야기야.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그리고 네가 알아듣기엔 좀 어려울 것 같구나. 나한텐 적이 하나 있어. 적이 뭔지는 알지?\""]], [["‘Oh, yes, I understand that,’ said Smike.", "\"아, 네, 그건 알아요.\" 스마이크가 말했어."]], [["‘Well, it is owing to him,’ returned Nicholas.", "\"그래, 다 그 사람 탓이야.\" 니콜라스가 말을 이었어."], ["‘He is rich, and not so easily punished as YOUR old enemy, Mr. Squeers.", "\"그 사람은 부자라서, 네 옛날 적인 스퀴어스처럼 쉽게 벌줄 수가 없어."], ["He is my uncle, but he is a villain, and has done me wrong.’", "내 삼촌인데, 악당이지. 나한테 못된 짓을 했어.\""]], [["‘Has he though?’ asked Smike, bending eagerly forward.", "\"정말요?\" 스마이크가 몸을 바짝 앞으로 기울이며 물었어."], ["‘What is his name?", "\"이름이 뭐예요?"], ["Tell me his name.’", "이름을 말해 줘요.\""]], [["‘Ralph--Ralph Nickleby.’", "\"랄프. 랄프 니클비.\""]], [["‘Ralph Nickleby,’ repeated Smike.", "\"랄프 니클비.\" 스마이크가 따라 했어."], ["‘Ralph.", "\"랄프."], ["I’ll get that name by heart.’", "그 이름을 외워 둘래요.\""]], [["He had muttered it over to himself some twenty times, when a loud knock at the door disturbed him from his occupation.", "스마이크가 그 이름을 혼자 한 스무 번쯤 중얼거렸을 때, 문을 쾅쾅 두드리는 소리에 하던 일을 방해받았어."], ["Before he could open it, Mr. Folair, the pantomimist, thrust in his head.", "그가 문을 열기도 전에, 무언극 배우인 폴레어 씨가 머리를 쑥 들이밀었지."]], [["Mr.", "폴레어 씨의 머리에는 보통 아주 동그란 모자가 얹혀 있었어."], ["Folair’s head was usually decorated with a very round hat, unusually high in the crown, and curled up quite tight in the brims.", "모자 꼭대기가 유난히 높고 챙이 바짝 위로 말려 올라간 모양이었지. 이번에는 그 모자를 한쪽으로 잔뜩 기울여 썼는데, 뒷부분이 가장 덜 낡았다고 앞으로 돌려 쓴 거였어."], ["On the present occasion he wore it very much on one side, with the back part forward in consequence of its being the least rusty; round his neck he wore a flaming red worsted comforter, whereof the straggling ends peeped out beneath his threadbare Newmarket coat, which was very tight and buttoned all the way up.", "목에는 새빨간 털실 목도리를 둘렀는데, 흐트러진 끝자락이 다 닳은 뉴마켓 코트 아래로 삐죽 비어져 나와 있었지. 그 코트는 몸에 꼭 끼고 위까지 단추를 죄다 채운 것이었어."], ["He carried in his hand one very dirty glove, and a cheap dress cane with a glass handle; in short, his whole appearance was unusually dashing, and demonstrated a far more scrupulous attention to his toilet than he was in the habit of bestowing upon it.", "손에는 아주 더러운 장갑 한 짝과 유리 손잡이가 달린 싸구려 정장용 지팡이를 들고 있었지. 한마디로 그의 차림새는 유난히 멋을 부린 것이었고, 평소 몸치장에 들이던 것보다 훨씬 더 공을 들였다는 게 보였어."]], [["‘Good-evening, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"안녕하세요.\""], ["Folair, taking off the tall hat, and running his fingers through his hair.", "폴레어 씨가 높다란 모자를 벗고 손가락으로 머리를 쓸어 넘기며 말했어."], ["‘I bring a communication.", "\"전할 말이 있어서 왔습니다."], ["Hem!’", "흠!\""]], [["‘From whom and what about?’ inquired Nicholas.", "\"누구한테서, 무슨 일로?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."], ["‘You are unusually mysterious tonight.’", "\"오늘 밤은 유난히 수상쩍게 구는군요.\""]], [["‘Cold, perhaps,’ returned Mr.", "\"차갑다고요, 아마.\" 폴레어 씨가 대꾸했어."], ["Folair; ‘cold, perhaps.", "\"차갑겠죠, 아마."], ["That is the fault of my position--not of myself, Mr.", "그건 제 입장 탓이지 제 탓이 아닙니다, 존슨 씨. 양쪽 모두의 친구라는 제 입장이 그걸 요구하는 거예요.\""], ["Johnson. My position as a mutual friend requires it, sir.’ Mr.", "폴레어 씨는 아주 인상적인 표정을 지으며 잠시 멈췄어."], ["Folair paused with a most impressive look, and diving into the hat before noticed, drew from thence a small piece of whity-brown paper curiously folded, whence he brought forth a note which it had served to keep clean, and handing it over to Nicholas, said--", "그러고는 앞서 말한 그 모자 속을 뒤져, 묘하게 접은 누르스름한 갈색 종이쪽지를 꺼냈지. 그 안에서 그 종이가 깨끗하게 지켜 준 쪽지 한 장을 꺼내, 니콜라스에게 건네며 말했어."]], [["‘Have the goodness to read that, sir.’", "\"이걸 좀 읽어 주시겠습니까.\""]], [["Nicholas, in a state of much amazement, took the note and broke the seal, glancing at Mr.", "니콜라스는 잔뜩 놀란 채로 쪽지를 받아 봉인을 뜯었어."], ["Folair as he did so, who, knitting his brow and pursing up his mouth with great dignity, was sitting with his eyes steadily fixed upon the ceiling.", "그러면서 폴레어 씨를 슬쩍 쳐다봤는데, 그는 눈썹을 찌푸리고 입을 잔뜩 오므린 채 위엄을 부리며 천장에 시선을 딱 고정하고 앉아 있었지."]], [["It was directed to blank Johnson, Esq., by favour of Augustus Folair, Esq.; and the astonishment of Nicholas was in no degree lessened, when he found it to be couched in the following laconic terms:--", "쪽지는 \"오거스터스 폴레어 귀하의 호의로, 존슨 귀하께\"라고 적혀 있었어. 니콜라스의 놀라움은 그 내용이 다음과 같은 간결한 문구로 쓰여 있다는 걸 알고도 조금도 가시지 않았지."]], [["“Mr. Lenville presents his kind regards to Mr. Johnson, and will feel obliged if he will inform him at what hour tomorrow morning it will be most convenient to him to meet Mr. L. at the Theatre, for the purpose of having his nose pulled in the presence of the company.", "\"렌빌 씨가 존슨 씨께 정중히 안부를 전하며, 내일 아침 몇 시에 극장에서 렌빌 씨를 만나는 게 가장 편하실지 알려 주시면 감사하겠습니다. 단원들이 보는 앞에서 존슨 씨의 코를 비틀어 주기 위함입니다."]], [["“Mr. Lenville requests Mr.", "렌빌 씨는 존슨 씨께서 약속을 어기지 마시기를 부탁드립니다."], ["Johnson not to neglect making an appointment, as he has invited two or three professional friends to witness the ceremony, and cannot disappoint them upon any account whatever.", "이 의식을 지켜볼 동료 두세 명을 초대해 두었기에, 무슨 일이 있어도 그들을 실망시킬 수 없기 때문입니다. 포츠머스, 화요일 밤.\""]], [["Indignant as he was at this impertinence, there was something so exquisitely absurd in such a cartel of defiance, that Nicholas was obliged to bite his lip and read the note over two or three times before he could muster sufficient gravity and sternness to address the hostile messenger, who had not taken his eyes from the ceiling, nor altered the expression of his face in the slightest degree.", "이 무례함에 화가 치밀긴 했지만, 이런 식의 도전장이 어찌나 우습도록 황당하던지, 니콜라스는 입술을 깨물어야 했어. 그러고는 쪽지를 두세 번 거듭 읽고 나서야, 이 적의에 찬 전령에게 말을 걸 만큼 진지하고 엄한 표정을 겨우 지을 수 있었지. 그 전령은 여전히 천장에서 눈을 떼지 않았고, 얼굴 표정도 조금도 바꾸지 않았어."]], [["‘Do you know the contents of this note, sir?’ he asked, at length.", "\"이 쪽지에 뭐가 적혀 있는지 아시오?\" 니콜라스가 마침내 물었어."]], [["‘Yes,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"네.\""], ["Folair, looking round for an instant, and immediately carrying his eyes back again to the ceiling.", "폴레어 씨가 잠깐 둘러보았다가 곧장 다시 천장으로 눈을 돌리며 대답했어."]], [["‘And how dare you bring it here, sir?’ asked Nicholas, tearing it into very little pieces, and jerking it in a shower towards the messenger.", "\"그런데 어떻게 이걸 감히 여기로 가져온 거요?\" 니콜라스가 쪽지를 잘게 찢어 전령을 향해 소나기처럼 휙 뿌리며 물었어."], ["‘Had you no fear of being kicked downstairs, sir?’", "\"계단 아래로 걷어차일 거란 생각은 안 들었소?\""]], [["Mr.", "폴레어 씨는 이제 찢어진 쪽지 조각 몇 개가 얹힌 머리를 니콜라스 쪽으로 돌리더니, 똑같이 흔들림 없는 위엄으로 짧게 대답했어."], ["Folair turned his head--now ornamented with several fragments of the note--towards Nicholas, and with the same imperturbable dignity, briefly replied ‘No.’", "\"안 들었는데요.\""]], [["‘Then,’ said Nicholas, taking up the tall hat and tossing it towards the door, ‘you had better follow that article of your dress, sir, or you may find yourself very disagreeably deceived, and that within a dozen seconds.’", "\"그렇다면,\" 니콜라스가 그 높다란 모자를 집어 문 쪽으로 휙 던지며 말했어. \"당신 옷의 저 물건을 따라가는 게 좋을 거요. 안 그러면 단 십몇 초 안에 아주 불쾌하게 속았다는 걸 알게 될 테니까.\""]], [["‘I say, Johnson,’ remonstrated Mr.", "\"이봐요, 존슨.\""], ["Folair, suddenly losing all his dignity, ‘none of that, you know.", "폴레어 씨가 갑자기 위엄을 죄다 잃고 항의했어. \"그러지 말아요."], ["No tricks with a gentleman’s wardrobe.’", "신사 옷가지를 가지고 장난치는 거 아닙니다.\""]], [["‘Leave the room,’ returned Nicholas.", "\"방에서 나가시오.\" 니콜라스가 받아쳤어."], ["‘How could you presume to come here on such an errand, you scoundrel?’", "\"어떻게 감히 이런 심부름을 하겠다고 여기 들이닥친 거요, 이 못된 작자야?\""]], [["‘Pooh!", "\"쳇!"], ["pooh!’ said Mr.", "쳇!\""], ["Folair, unwinding his comforter, and gradually getting himself out of it.", "폴레어 씨가 목도리를 풀고 천천히 거기서 빠져나오며 말했어."], ["‘There--that’s enough.’", "\"자, 이만하면 됐어요.\""]], [["‘Enough!’ cried Nicholas, advancing towards him.", "\"됐다고!\" 니콜라스가 그에게 다가서며 소리쳤어."], ["‘Take yourself off, sir.’", "\"썩 꺼지시오.\""]], [["‘Pooh!", "\"쳇!"], ["pooh!", "쳇!"], ["I tell you,’ returned Mr.", "글쎄, 말하잖아요.\""], ["Folair, waving his hand in deprecation of any further wrath; ‘I wasn’t in earnest.", "폴레어 씨가 더 이상의 분노를 막으려는 듯 손을 내저으며 대꾸했어. \"진심이 아니었다니까요."], ["I only brought it in joke.’", "그냥 장난삼아 가져온 거예요.\""]], [["‘You had better be careful how you indulge in such jokes again,’ said Nicholas, ‘or you may find an allusion to pulling noses rather a dangerous reminder for the subject of your facetiousness.", "\"다음번엔 그런 장난을 칠 때 조심하는 게 좋을 거요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["Was it written in joke, too, pray?’", "\"안 그러면 코를 비틀어 준다는 말이, 그 농담을 한 사람한테 꽤 위험한 경고가 될 수도 있으니까. 그런데 이건 장난삼아 쓴 거요?\""]], [["‘No, no, that’s the best of it,’ returned the actor; ‘right down earnest--honour bright.’", "\"아뇨, 아뇨, 그게 제일 우스운 점이죠.\" 배우가 대답했어. \"완전히 진심이었어요. 정말이라니까요.\""]], [["Nicholas could not repress a smile at the odd figure before him, which, at all times more calculated to provoke mirth than anger, was especially so at that moment, when with one knee upon the ground, Mr.", "니콜라스는 눈앞의 그 우스꽝스러운 꼴을 보고 웃음을 참을 수가 없었어. 어느 때고 화보다는 웃음을 자아내기 십상인 모습이었는데, 특히 그 순간이 그랬지."], ["Folair twirled his old hat round upon his hand, and affected the extremest agony lest any of the nap should have been knocked off--an ornament which it is almost superfluous to say, it had not boasted for many months.", "폴레어 씨가 한쪽 무릎을 바닥에 대고 낡은 모자를 손 위에서 빙글빙글 돌리며, 모자의 보풀이 떨어져 나갔을까 봐 죽도록 괴로운 척하고 있었거든. 굳이 말할 필요도 없지만, 그 모자는 이미 여러 달 전부터 자랑할 만한 보풀 따위는 하나도 없었어."]], [["‘Come, sir,’ said Nicholas, laughing in spite of himself.", "\"자, 그럼,\" 니콜라스가 저도 모르게 웃으며 말했어."], ["‘Have the goodness to explain.’", "\"어찌 된 일인지 좀 설명해 보시오.\""]], [["‘Why, I’ll tell you how it is,’ said Mr.", "\"왜요, 어떻게 된 건지 말씀드리죠.\""], ["Folair, sitting himself down in a chair with great coolness.", "폴레어 씨가 아주 태연하게 의자에 앉으며 말했어."], ["‘Since you came here Lenville has done nothing but second business, and, instead of having a reception every night as he used to have, they have let him come on as if he was nobody.’", "\"당신이 여기 온 뒤로 렌빌은 줄곧 단역만 맡았어요. 예전처럼 매일 밤 박수를 받기는커녕, 단원들이 그를 아무도 아닌 사람처럼 그냥 무대에 올려 보내는 거예요.\""]], [["‘What do you mean by a reception?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"박수를 받는다는 게 무슨 말이오?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘Jupiter!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"맙소사!\" 폴레어 씨가 외쳤어."], ["Folair, ‘what an unsophisticated shepherd you are, Johnson!", "\"당신은 정말 세상 물정 모르는 양치기로군요, 존슨! 처음 무대에 등장할 때 객석에서 터지는 갈채 말이에요."], ["Why, applause from the house when you first come on.", "그래서 그는 밤마다 무대에 올라도 박수 한 번 못 받았는데, 당신은 적어도 두 차례, 어떤 땐 세 차례나 받잖아요."], ["So he has gone on night after night, never getting a hand, and you getting a couple of rounds at least, and sometimes three, till at length he got quite desperate, and had half a mind last night to play Tybalt with a real sword, and pink you--not dangerously, but just enough to lay you up for a month or two.’", "그러다 마침내 완전히 절망에 빠져서, 어젯밤엔 진짜 칼을 들고 티볼트를 연기하면서 당신을 콱 찌를까 하는 생각까지 절반쯤 했답니다. 위험하게는 아니고, 딱 한두 달 드러눕게 할 만큼만요.\""]], [["‘Very considerate,’ remarked Nicholas.", "\"아주 사려 깊군요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Yes, I think it was under the circumstances; his professional reputation being at stake,’ said Mr.", "\"네, 사정을 보면 그렇다고 봐요. 자기 배우 평판이 걸린 일이니까요.\""], ["Folair, quite seriously.", "폴레어 씨가 자못 진지하게 말했어."], ["‘But his heart failed him, and he cast about for some other way of annoying you, and making himself popular at the same time--for that’s the point.", "\"그런데 막상 용기가 안 났던지, 당신을 괴롭히면서 동시에 자기는 인기를 끌 다른 방법을 찾기 시작했어요. 바로 그게 핵심이거든요."], ["Notoriety, notoriety, is the thing.", "유명세, 유명세, 그게 다예요."], ["Bless you, if he had pinked you,’ said Mr.", "이런, 그가 만약 당신을 콱 찔렀더라면,\""], ["Folair, stopping to make a calculation in his mind, ‘it would have been worth--ah, it would have been worth eight or ten shillings a week to him.", "폴레어 씨가 머릿속으로 계산을 하려고 잠시 멈췄어. \"그건 그한테 일주일에 — 아, 일주일에 팔구 실링은 됐을 거예요."], ["All the town would have come to see the actor who nearly killed a man by mistake; I shouldn’t wonder if it had got him an engagement in London.", "실수로 사람을 죽일 뻔한 배우를 보겠다고 온 동네 사람이 다 몰려왔을 테니까요. 어쩌면 런던에서 일자리를 얻었을지도 몰라요."], ["However, he was obliged to try some other mode of getting popular, and this one occurred to him.", "아무튼 인기를 끌 다른 방법을 찾아야 했고, 그러다 이 생각이 떠오른 거죠. 정말 영리한 생각이에요."], ["It’s a clever idea, really.", "당신이 겁쟁이처럼 굴면서 코를 비틀게 내버려 뒀다면, 그건 신문에 났을 거예요."], ["If you had shown the white feather, and let him pull your nose, he’d have got it into the paper; if you had sworn the peace against him, it would have been in the paper too, and he’d have been just as much talked about as you--don’t you see?’", "당신이 그를 고발해서 보호를 청했어도 그것도 신문에 났을 거고, 그럼 당신만큼이나 그도 입에 오르내렸겠죠. 알겠어요?\""]], [["‘Oh, certainly,’ rejoined Nicholas; ‘but suppose I were to turn the tables, and pull HIS nose, what then?", "\"아, 그렇겠군.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어."], ["Would that make his fortune?’", "\"그런데 내가 거꾸로 그의 코를 비틀어 버리면 어떻게 되오? 그래도 그가 한밑천 잡게 되오?\""]], [["‘Why, I don’t think it would,’ replied Mr.", "\"글쎄, 그건 아닐걸요.\""], ["Folair, scratching his head, ‘because there wouldn’t be any romance about it, and he wouldn’t be favourably known.", "폴레어 씨가 머리를 긁적이며 대답했어. \"그러면 거기엔 아무 낭만도 없고, 좋은 쪽으로 이름이 알려지지도 않을 테니까요."], ["To tell you the truth though, he didn’t calculate much upon that, for you’re always so mild-spoken, and are so popular among the women, that we didn’t suspect you of showing fight.", "사실대로 말하면 그도 그건 별로 계산에 넣지 않았어요. 당신이 워낙 말씨가 부드럽고 여자들한테 인기가 많아서, 당신이 싸우려 들 줄은 우리도 의심하지 않았거든요."], ["If you did, however, he has a way of getting out of it easily, depend upon that.’", "하지만 당신이 정말 싸운다면, 그한텐 거기서 쉽게 빠져나올 방법이 있어요. 그건 틀림없어요.\""]], [["‘Has he?’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"그래요?\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어."], ["‘We will try, tomorrow morning.", "\"내일 아침에 한번 해 봅시다."], ["In the meantime, you can give whatever account of our interview you like best.", "그동안 우리가 만난 이야기는 당신 마음대로 좋을 대로 전하시오."], ["Good-night.’", "잘 자요.\""]], [["As Mr.", "폴레어 씨는 짓궂은 장난을 즐기는 데다 양심이라곤 도무지 없는 사람으로 동료 배우들 사이에 꽤 알려져 있었어."], ["Folair was pretty well known among his fellow-actors for a man who delighted in mischief, and was by no means scrupulous, Nicholas had not much doubt but that he had secretly prompted the tragedian in the course he had taken, and, moreover, that he would have carried his mission with a very high hand if he had not been disconcerted by the very unexpected demonstrations with which it had been received.", "그래서 니콜라스는 그가 비극 배우를 몰래 부추겨 이런 짓을 하게 했으리라는 걸, 게다가 그 심부름을 받아들이는 뜻밖의 반응에 김이 새지만 않았더라면 아주 거만하게 굴었으리라는 걸 별로 의심하지 않았지."], ["It was not worth his while to be serious with him, however, so he dismissed the pantomimist, with a gentle hint that if he offended again it would be under the penalty of a broken head; and Mr.", "하지만 그를 진지하게 상대할 가치는 없었어. 그래서 니콜라스는 또 한 번 무례를 저지르면 머리통이 깨지는 벌을 받을 거라고 슬쩍 귀띔하며 그 무언극 배우를 돌려보냈어."], ["Folair, taking the caution in exceedingly good part, walked away to confer with his principal, and give such an account of his proceedings as he might think best calculated to carry on the joke.", "그러자 폴레어 씨는 그 경고를 아주 기분 좋게 받아들이고, 자기 우두머리와 의논하러 걸어갔지. 그러고는 농담을 계속 이어 가기에 가장 좋겠다 싶은 식으로 자기가 한 일을 둘러댈 참이었어."]], [["He had no doubt reported that Nicholas was in a state of extreme bodily fear; for when that young gentleman walked with much deliberation down to the theatre next morning at the usual hour, he found all the company assembled in evident expectation, and Mr.", "폴레어 씨는 분명히 니콜라스가 잔뜩 겁에 질려 있다고 전했을 거야. 다음 날 아침 그 젊은이가 여느 때처럼 아주 느긋하게 극장으로 걸어 내려가 보니, 단원들이 죄다 모여 뭔가를 잔뜩 기대하는 눈치였거든."], ["Lenville, with his severest stage face, sitting majestically on a table, whistling defiance.", "게다가 렌빌 씨는 가장 험상궂은 무대용 표정을 짓고 탁자 위에 위풍당당하게 앉아, 도전하듯 휘파람을 불고 있었어."]], [["Now the ladies were on the side of Nicholas, and the gentlemen (being jealous) were on the side of the disappointed tragedian; so that the latter formed a little group about the redoubtable Mr.", "자, 여자들은 니콜라스 편이었고, 남자들은 (질투가 나서) 실망한 비극 배우 편이었어."], ["Lenville, and the former looked on at a little distance in some trepidation and anxiety. On Nicholas stopping to salute them, Mr.", "그래서 남자들은 그 무시무시한 렌빌 씨 둘레에 작은 무리를 이루었고, 여자들은 조금 떨어진 곳에서 떨리고 불안한 마음으로 지켜보고 있었지."], ["Lenville laughed a scornful laugh, and made some general remark touching the natural history of puppies.", "니콜라스가 걸음을 멈추고 인사하자, 렌빌 씨가 비웃는 웃음을 터뜨리며 강아지의 생태에 관한 무슨 일반적인 말을 한마디 던졌어."]], [["‘Oh!’ said Nicholas, looking quietly round, ‘are you there?’", "\"오!\" 니콜라스가 조용히 둘러보며 말했어. \"거기 있었군요?\""]], [["‘Slave!’ returned Mr.", "\"이 종놈!\""], ["Lenville, flourishing his right arm, and approaching Nicholas with a theatrical stride.", "렌빌 씨가 오른팔을 휘두르며 연극하듯 성큼성큼 니콜라스에게 다가서며 받아쳤어. 그런데 어쩐 일인지 바로 그 순간 그는 좀 흠칫 놀란 듯했어."], ["But somehow he appeared just at that moment a little startled, as if Nicholas did not look quite so frightened as he had expected, and came all at once to an awkward halt, at which the assembled ladies burst into a shrill laugh.", "니콜라스가 자기가 기대한 만큼 겁에 질려 보이지 않았거든. 그래서 갑자기 어색하게 우뚝 멈춰 섰고, 모여 있던 여자들이 그 모습에 까르르 날카로운 웃음을 터뜨렸지."]], [["‘Object of my scorn and hatred!’ said Mr.", "\"내 경멸과 증오의 대상!\" 렌빌 씨가 말했어."], ["Lenville, ‘I hold ye in contempt.’", "\"나는 너를 멸시하노라.\""]], [["Nicholas laughed in very unexpected enjoyment of this performance; and the ladies, by way of encouragement, laughed louder than before; whereat Mr.", "니콜라스는 이 연기가 뜻밖에도 너무 재미있어서 웃음을 터뜨렸어. 그러자 여자들이 응원이라도 하듯 전보다 더 크게 웃었지."], ["Lenville assumed his bitterest smile, and expressed his opinion that they were ‘minions’.", "그러자 렌빌 씨는 가장 신랄한 미소를 지으며, 그 여자들이 \"앞잡이들\"이라는 의견을 내뱉었어."]], [["‘But they shall not protect ye!’ said the tragedian, taking an upward look at Nicholas, beginning at his boots and ending at the crown of his head, and then a downward one, beginning at the crown of his head, and ending at his boots--which two looks, as everybody knows, express defiance on the stage. ‘They shall not protect ye--boy!’", "\"하지만 저들이 너를 지켜 주진 못할 것이다!\" 비극 배우가 니콜라스를 위로 훑어보며 말했어. 부츠에서 시작해 정수리에서 끝나는 시선이었지. 그러고 나서 아래로 훑어보았는데, 정수리에서 시작해 부츠에서 끝나는 시선이었어. 누구나 알다시피 이 두 시선은 무대에서 도전을 뜻하지. \"저들이 너를 지켜 주진 못할 것이다, 애송이!\""]], [["Thus speaking, Mr.", "이렇게 말하며 렌빌 씨는 팔짱을 끼고, 니콜라스에게 그 표정을 지어 보였어."], ["Lenville folded his arms, and treated Nicholas to that expression of face with which, in melodramatic performances, he was in the habit of regarding the tyrannical kings when they said, ‘Away with him to the deepest dungeon beneath the castle moat;’ and which, accompanied with a little jingling of fetters, had been known to produce great effects in its time.", "멜로드라마 공연에서 폭군 같은 왕들이 \"저놈을 성의 해자 아래 가장 깊은 지하 감옥으로 끌고 가라!\"라고 말할 때 그가 으레 짓던 그 표정 말이야. 거기에 쇠고랑 절그럭거리는 소리까지 곁들이면, 한때 엄청난 효과를 거두기로 유명한 표정이었지."]], [["Whether it was the absence of the fetters or not, it made no very deep impression on Mr.", "쇠고랑이 없어서였는지 어쩐지는 몰라도, 어쨌든 그 표정은 렌빌 씨의 상대에게 별 깊은 인상을 주지 못했어. 오히려 그의 얼굴에 떠오른 좋은 기분을 더 돋우는 것 같았지."], ["Lenville’s adversary, however, but rather seemed to increase the good-humour expressed in his countenance; in which stage of the contest, one or two gentlemen, who had come out expressly to witness the pulling of Nicholas’s nose, grew impatient, murmuring that if it were to be done at all it had better be done at once, and that if Mr Lenville didn’t mean to do it he had better say so, and not keep them waiting there.", "결투가 이 단계에 이르자, 니콜라스의 코가 비틀리는 걸 보겠다고 일부러 나온 신사 한두 명이 조바심을 내기 시작했어. 어차피 할 거면 당장 하는 게 낫고, 렌빌 씨가 안 할 작정이면 그렇다고 말해서 사람들을 여기 마냥 기다리게 하지 말라고 투덜댔지."], ["Thus urged, the tragedian adjusted the cuff of his right coat sleeve for the performance of the operation, and walked in a very stately manner up to Nicholas, who suffered him to approach to within the requisite distance, and then, without the smallest discomposure, knocked him down.", "이렇게 재촉당하자 비극 배우는 그 작업을 해치우려고 오른쪽 코트 소맷부리를 가다듬고, 아주 위풍당당하게 니콜라스에게 걸어갔어. 니콜라스는 그가 필요한 만큼 가까이 다가오도록 내버려 두었다가, 조금도 흐트러짐 없이 그를 때려눕혔지."]], [["Before the discomfited tragedian could raise his head from the boards, Mrs.", "당황한 비극 배우가 무대 바닥에서 미처 머리를 들기도 전에, 렌빌 부인이 (앞서 슬쩍 비쳤듯이 흥미로운 처지에 있었지) 여자들 뒷줄에서 뛰쳐나왔어."], ["Lenville (who, as has been before hinted, was in an interesting state) rushed from the rear rank of ladies, and uttering a piercing scream threw herself upon the body.", "그러고는 찢어지는 비명을 지르며 남편의 몸 위로 몸을 던졌지."]], [["‘Do you see this, monster?", "\"이게 보이느냐, 괴물아?"], ["Do you see THIS?’ cried Mr.", "이게 보이느냐?\""], ["Lenville, sitting up, and pointing to his prostrate lady, who was holding him very tight round the waist.", "렌빌 씨가 일어나 앉아, 허리를 꽉 끌어안고 엎드린 아내를 가리키며 소리쳤어."]], [["‘Come,’ said Nicholas, nodding his head, ‘apologise for the insolent note you wrote to me last night, and waste no more time in talking.’", "\"자,\" 니콜라스가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했어. \"어젯밤 나한테 보낸 그 건방진 쪽지를 사과하시오. 더 이상 말로 시간 끌지 말고.\""]], [["‘Never!’ cried Mr.", "\"절대 안 한다!\""], ["Lenville.", "렌빌 씨가 소리쳤어."]], [["‘Yes--yes--yes!’ screamed his wife. ‘For my sake--for mine, Lenville--forego all idle forms, unless you would see me a blighted corse at your feet.’", "\"해요, 해요, 해요!\" 아내가 비명을 질렀어. \"날 위해서, 날 위해서요, 렌빌. 부질없는 형식 따위는 다 버려요. 안 그러면 내가 당신 발치에서 시들어 가는 시체가 되는 꼴을 보게 될 거예요.\""]], [["‘This is affecting!’ said Mr.", "\"이거 참 가슴 아프군!\""], ["Lenville, looking round him, and drawing the back of his hand across his eyes.", "렌빌 씨가 주위를 둘러보며 손등으로 눈을 훔치며 말했어."], ["‘The ties of nature are strong.", "\"혈육의 정은 강하구나."], ["The weak husband and the father--the father that is yet to be--relents.", "약한 남편이자 아버지가, 장차 아버지가 될 사람이 누그러지노라."], ["I apologise.’", "사과하겠다.\""]], [["‘Humbly and submissively?’ said Nicholas.", "\"공손하고 순순히 말이오?\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Humbly and submissively,’ returned the tragedian, scowling upwards.", "\"공손하고 순순히.\" 비극 배우가 위쪽을 노려보며 대답했어."], ["‘But only to save her,--for a time will come--’", "\"하지만 오직 이 여자를 살리기 위해서다. 언젠가 때가 올 테니까.\""]], [["‘Very good,’ said Nicholas; ‘I hope Mrs.", "\"아주 좋소.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["Lenville may have a good one; and when it does come, and you are a father, you shall retract it if you have the courage.", "\"렌빌 부인이 순산하시길 바라오. 그리고 그때가 와서 당신이 아버지가 되면, 용기가 있거든 사과를 도로 거두시오."], ["There.", "자."], ["Be careful, sir, to what lengths your jealousy carries you another time; and be careful, also, before you venture too far, to ascertain your rival’s temper.’ With this parting advice Nicholas picked up Mr.", "다음번엔 당신 질투가 어디까지 당신을 끌고 가는지 조심하시오. 그리고 너무 멀리 나서기 전에 당신 경쟁자의 성질부터 알아보는 게 좋을 거요.\""], ["Lenville’s ash stick which had flown out of his hand, and breaking it in half, threw him the pieces and withdrew, bowing slightly to the spectators as he walked out.", "니콜라스는 이런 작별의 충고를 남기며, 렌빌 씨의 손에서 날아가 떨어진 물푸레나무 지팡이를 집어 들었어. 그러고는 그걸 반으로 부러뜨려 조각을 그에게 던지고, 걸어 나가며 구경꾼들에게 가볍게 고개 숙여 인사하고는 물러났지."]], [["The profoundest deference was paid to Nicholas that night, and the people who had been most anxious to have his nose pulled in the morning, embraced occasions of taking him aside, and telling him with great feeling, how very friendly they took it that he should have treated that Lenville so properly, who was a most unbearable fellow, and on whom they had all, by a remarkable coincidence, at one time or other contemplated the infliction of condign punishment, which they had only been restrained from administering by considerations of mercy; indeed, to judge from the invariable termination of all these stories, there never was such a charitable and kind-hearted set of people as the male members of Mr. Crummles’s company.", "그날 밤 니콜라스는 더없이 깊은 존경을 받았어. 아침에 그의 코가 비틀리는 걸 보고 싶어 안달하던 사람들이, 이때다 싶어 그를 한쪽으로 데려가서는 아주 감격스럽게 말했지. 그 렌빌이라는 작자를 그렇게 제대로 다뤄 줘서 얼마나 다행인지 모른다고, 그는 정말 못 봐줄 작자였다고 말이야. 그리고 놀랍게도 다들 하나같이, 자기들도 언젠가 한 번씩은 그 작자에게 마땅한 벌을 내릴까 생각했었는데, 그저 자비심 때문에 참았던 거라고들 했어. 사실 이 모든 이야기가 한결같이 똑같은 식으로 끝나는 걸 보면, 크럼블스 씨 극단의 남자 단원들만큼 인정 많고 마음씨 고운 사람들도 세상에 없을 것 같았지."]], [["Nicholas bore his triumph, as he had his success in the little world of the theatre, with the utmost moderation and good humour.", "니콜라스는 이 승리를, 극장이라는 작은 세계에서 거둔 성공과 마찬가지로 더없이 절제 있고 좋은 기분으로 받아들였어."], ["The crestfallen Mr. Lenville made an expiring effort to obtain revenge by sending a boy into the gallery to hiss, but he fell a sacrifice to popular indignation, and was promptly turned out without having his money back.", "풀 죽은 렌빌 씨는 객석 위층 관람석으로 소년 하나를 들여보내 야유를 시키며 마지막 발악으로 복수를 노렸지만, 그 소년은 성난 관객들의 제물이 되어 입장료도 돌려받지 못한 채 곧장 쫓겨났지."]], [["‘Well, Smike,’ said Nicholas when the first piece was over, and he had almost finished dressing to go home, ‘is there any letter yet?’", "\"자, 스마이크,\" 첫 작품이 끝나고 집에 갈 옷을 거의 다 갈아입었을 때 니콜라스가 말했어. \"편지는 아직 없었니?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Smike, ‘I got this one from the post-office.’", "\"있어요.\" 스마이크가 대답했어. \"우체국에서 이걸 받아 왔어요.\""]], [["‘From Newman Noggs,’ said Nicholas, casting his eye upon the cramped direction; ‘it’s no easy matter to make his writing out.", "\"뉴먼 노그스한테서 왔구나.\" 니콜라스가 빽빽하게 적힌 수신인 글씨에 눈길을 던지며 말했어."], ["Let me see--let me see.’", "\"그 사람 글씨는 알아보기가 쉽지 않아. 어디 보자, 어디 보자.\""]], [["By dint of poring over the letter for half an hour, he contrived to make himself master of the contents, which were certainly not of a nature to set his mind at ease.", "반 시간이나 편지에 눈을 박고 들여다본 끝에, 그는 겨우 내용을 다 파악할 수 있었어. 그런데 그 내용은 분명 그의 마음을 편하게 해 줄 만한 게 아니었지."], ["Newman took upon himself to send back the ten pounds, observing that he had ascertained that neither Mrs.", "뉴먼은 십 파운드를 도로 돌려보내며 이렇게 적었어. 알아보니 니클비 부인도 케이트도 지금 당장 돈이 아쉬운 처지는 아니라는 걸 확인했고, 머지않아 니콜라스가 그 돈이 더 필요할 때가 올지도 모른다고 말이야."], ["Nickleby nor Kate was in actual want of money at the moment, and that a time might shortly come when Nicholas might want it more.", "그는 자기가 이제부터 하려는 말에 놀라지 말라고 당부했어. 나쁜 소식은 없고, 다들 건강하다고 했지."], ["He entreated him not to be alarmed at what he was about to say;--there was no bad news--they were in good health--but he thought circumstances might occur, or were occurring, which would render it absolutely necessary that Kate should have her brother’s protection, and if so, Newman said, he would write to him to that effect, either by the next post or the next but one.", "하지만 어떤 사정이 생길지도, 아니 이미 생기고 있을지도 모른다고, 그렇게 되면 케이트한테 오빠의 보호가 꼭 필요해질 거라고 했어. 그리고 만약 그렇게 되면, 다음 우편이나 그다음 우편으로 그렇게 알리는 편지를 쓰겠다고 뉴먼은 말했지."]], [["Nicholas read this passage very often, and the more he thought of it the more he began to fear some treachery upon the part of Ralph.", "니콜라스는 이 대목을 몇 번이고 읽었어. 그리고 생각하면 할수록 랄프가 무슨 흉계를 꾸미고 있는 게 아닌가 하는 두려움이 들기 시작했지."], ["Once or twice he felt tempted to repair to London at all hazards without an hour’s delay, but a little reflection assured him that if such a step were necessary, Newman would have spoken out and told him so at once.", "한두 번은 무슨 일이 있어도 한 시간도 지체하지 않고 당장 런던으로 떠나고 싶은 충동이 일었어. 하지만 잠깐 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 정말로 그런 조치가 필요했다면 뉴먼이 진작 터놓고 그렇게 말했으리라는 확신이 들었지."]], [["‘At all events I should prepare them here for the possibility of my going away suddenly,’ said Nicholas; ‘I should lose no time in doing that.’ As the thought occurred to him, he took up his hat and hurried to the green-room.", "\"아무튼 여기 사람들한테는 내가 갑자기 떠날지도 모른다고 미리 일러 둬야겠어.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"지체 없이 그렇게 해야지.\" 이 생각이 떠오르자 그는 모자를 집어 들고 분장실로 서둘러 갔어."]], [["‘Well, Mr. Johnson,’ said Mrs.", "\"자, 존슨 씨,\""], ["Crummles, who was seated there in full regal costume, with the phenomenon as the Maiden in her maternal arms, ‘next week for Ryde, then for Winchester, then for--’", "화려한 왕족 의상을 입고 거기 앉아 있던 크럼블스 부인이, 신동 아이를 처녀 역할로 어머니 같은 품에 안은 채 말했어. \"다음 주엔 라이드로, 그다음엔 윈체스터로, 그다음엔—\""]], [["‘I have some reason to fear,’ interrupted Nicholas, ‘that before you leave here my career with you will have closed.’", "\"제가 걱정되는 게 있는데요.\" 니콜라스가 끼어들었어. \"여러분이 여길 떠나기 전에 제가 여러분과 함께하는 일을 그만두게 될 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘Closed!’ cried Mrs.", "\"그만두다니요!\""], ["Crummles, raising her hands in astonishment.", "크럼블스 부인이 놀라서 두 손을 치켜들며 소리쳤어."]], [["‘Closed!’ cried Miss Snevellicci, trembling so much in her tights that she actually laid her hand upon the shoulder of the manageress for support.", "\"그만두다니요!\" 스네벨리치 양이 타이츠 속에서 어찌나 떨던지, 정말로 극단장 부인의 어깨에 손을 얹어 몸을 지탱하며 소리쳤어."]], [["‘Why he don’t mean to say he’s going!’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"아니, 설마 떠난다는 말은 아니겠지!\""], ["Grudden, making her way towards Mrs. Crummles.", "그러든 부인이 크럼블스 부인 쪽으로 다가서며 외쳤어."], ["‘Hoity toity!", "\"원, 별소릴!"], ["Nonsense.’", "말도 안 돼.\""]], [["The phenomenon, being of an affectionate nature and moreover excitable, raised a loud cry, and Miss Belvawney and Miss Bravassa actually shed tears.", "신동 아이는 정이 많은 데다 잘 흥분하는 성격이라 큰 소리로 울음을 터뜨렸고, 벨바우니 양과 브라바사 양은 정말로 눈물을 흘렸어. 남자 배우들조차 이야기를 멈추고 \"떠난다고!\"라는 말을 따라 했지."], ["Even the male performers stopped in their conversation, and echoed the word ‘Going!’ although some among them (and they had been the loudest in their congratulations that day) winked at each other as though they would not be sorry to lose such a favoured rival; an opinion, indeed, which the honest Mr.", "그래도 그 가운데 몇몇은 (그날 가장 큰 소리로 그를 축하해 주던 사람들이었어) 서로 눈짓을 주고받았어. 그렇게 잘나가는 경쟁자가 사라지는 게 영 싫지만은 않다는 듯이 말이야."], ["Folair, who was ready dressed for the savage, openly stated in so many words to a demon with whom he was sharing a pot of porter.", "사실 정직한 폴레어 씨는 야만인 분장을 다 갖춘 채로, 함께 포터 한 잔을 나누던 악마 역 배우에게 바로 그런 의견을 대놓고 말로 다 털어놓았지."]], [["Nicholas briefly said that he feared it would be so, although he could not yet speak with any degree of certainty; and getting away as soon as he could, went home to con Newman’s letter once more, and speculate upon it afresh.", "니콜라스는 아직 무어라 확실히 말할 수는 없지만 아무래도 그렇게 될 것 같다고 짤막하게 말했어. 그러고는 되도록 빨리 거기서 빠져나와 집으로 돌아갔지. 뉴먼의 편지를 한 번 더 곱씹어 보고, 다시 곰곰이 헤아려 보려고 말이야."]], [["How trifling all that had been occupying his time and thoughts for many weeks seemed to him during that sleepless night, and how constantly and incessantly present to his imagination was the one idea that Kate in the midst of some great trouble and distress might even then be looking--and vainly too--for him!", "그 잠 못 이루는 밤 내내, 여러 주 동안 그의 시간과 생각을 차지하던 모든 일이 그에게 얼마나 하찮게 느껴졌는지. 그리고 한 가지 생각이 얼마나 끊임없이 줄곧 그의 마음에 떠올랐는지. 케이트가 어떤 큰 고난과 고통 한복판에서, 바로 이 순간에도 그를 애타게 찾고 있을지도 모른다는, 그것도 헛되이 찾고 있을지도 모른다는 생각이었지!"]], [["# CHAPTER 30", "# CHAPTER 30"]], [["Festivities are held in honour of Nicholas, who suddenly withdraws himself from the Society of Mr. Vincent Crummles and his Theatrical Companions\nMr.", "빈센트 크럼블스 씨는 니콜라스가 곧 극단을 떠날지도 모른다고 공개적으로 알리자, 깊은 슬픔과 당황의 기색을 잔뜩 드러냈다."], ["Vincent Crummles was no sooner acquainted with the public announcement which Nicholas had made relative to the probability of his shortly ceasing to be a member of the company, than he evinced many tokens of grief and consternation; and, in the extremity of his despair, even held out certain vague promises of a speedy improvement not only in the amount of his regular salary, but also in the contingent emoluments appertaining to his authorship.", "그러고는 절망에 빠진 나머지, 정해진 급료를 곧 올려 주겠다는 막연한 약속까지 내비쳤다. 게다가 그가 쓴 작품에 딸린 부수입도 늘려 주겠다고 했다."], ["Finding Nicholas bent upon quitting the society--for he had now determined that, even if no further tidings came from Newman, he would, at all hazards, ease his mind by repairing to London and ascertaining the exact position of his sister--Mr.", "그래도 니콜라스가 극단을 떠나기로 굳게 마음먹은 걸 알자 — 니콜라스는 이제 결심을 굳혔거든. 뉴먼한테서 더 이상 소식이 오지 않더라도, 무슨 일이 있어도 런던으로 가서 동생이 정확히 어떤 처지인지 알아보고 마음을 놓겠다고 말이야 — 크럼블스 씨는 그가 다시 돌아올 가능성이나 따져 보며 마음을 달랠 수밖에 없었다."], ["Crummles was fain to content himself by calculating the chances of his coming back again, and taking prompt and energetic measures to make the most of him before he went away.", "그러면서 그가 떠나기 전에 최대한 써먹으려고 재빠르고 힘찬 수를 쓰기로 했다."]], [["‘Let me see,’ said Mr.", "\"어디 보자.\""], ["Crummles, taking off his outlaw’s wig, the better to arrive at a cool-headed view of the whole case.", "크럼블스 씨가 무법자 가발을 벗으며 말했다. 사건 전체를 차분한 머리로 살펴보려는 거였지."], ["‘Let me see.", "\"어디 보자."], ["This is Wednesday night.", "오늘이 수요일 밤이지."], ["We’ll have posters out the first thing in the morning, announcing positively your last appearance for tomorrow.’", "아침 일찍 포스터부터 붙여서, 내일 자네가 정말로 마지막 무대에 선다고 알리는 거야.\""]], [["‘But perhaps it may not be my last appearance, you know,’ said Nicholas.", "\"하지만 그게 제 마지막 무대가 아닐 수도 있잖아요.\""], ["‘Unless I am summoned away, I should be sorry to inconvenience you by leaving before the end of the week.’", "니콜라스가 말했다. \"갑자기 불려 가는 게 아니라면, 주말 전에 떠나서 폐를 끼치고 싶지는 않거든요.\""]], [["‘So much the better,’ returned Mr.", "\"그럼 더 좋지.\" 크럼블스 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["Crummles. ‘We can have positively your last appearance, on Thursday--re-engagement for one night more, on Friday--and, yielding to the wishes of numerous influential patrons, who were disappointed in obtaining seats, on Saturday.", "\"목요일에 정말로 자네 마지막 무대를 올리고 — 금요일엔 단 하룻밤 더 출연하기로 다시 계약하고 — 토요일엔 자리를 못 구해 아쉬워한 수많은 영향력 있는 후원자들의 바람에 따라 또 무대에 서는 거야."], ["That ought to bring three very decent houses.’", "그러면 사흘 다 제법 괜찮은 관객이 들겠지.\""]], [["‘Then I am to make three last appearances, am I?’ inquired Nicholas, smiling.", "\"그럼 제가 마지막 무대를 세 번 서는 거네요?\" 니콜라스가 웃으며 물었다."]], [["‘Yes,’ rejoined the manager, scratching his head with an air of some vexation; ‘three is not enough, and it’s very bungling and irregular not to have more, but if we can’t help it we can’t, so there’s no use in talking. A novelty would be very desirable. You couldn’t sing a comic song on the pony’s back, could you?’", "\"그래.\" 단장이 좀 짜증스러운 기색으로 머리를 긁적이며 대답했다. \"세 번으로는 부족하지. 더 많지 않으면 영 어설프고 격에도 안 맞아. 하지만 어쩔 수 없으면 어쩔 수 없는 거니까, 더 말해 봤자 소용없지. 뭔가 새로운 게 있으면 아주 좋을 텐데. 자네 조랑말 등에 올라타서 우스운 노래 한 곡 못 부르나?\""]], [["‘No,’ replied Nicholas, ‘I couldn’t indeed.’", "\"못 해요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다. \"정말로 못 합니다.\""]], [["‘It has drawn money before now,’ said Mr.", "\"그게 전에는 돈을 좀 벌어 줬거든.\""], ["Crummles, with a look of disappointment.", "크럼블스 씨가 실망한 표정으로 말했다."], ["‘What do you think of a brilliant display of fireworks?’", "\"화려한 불꽃놀이는 어떻게 생각하나?\""]], [["‘That it would be rather expensive,’ replied Nicholas, drily.", "\"돈이 꽤 들 것 같은데요.\" 니콜라스가 무덤덤하게 대답했다."]], [["‘Eighteen-pence would do it,’ said Mr.", "\"십팔 펜스면 돼.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했다."], ["Crummles. ‘You on the top of a pair of steps with the phenomenon in an attitude; “Farewell!”", "\"자네가 사다리 꼭대기에 올라서고, 신동은 한쪽에서 멋진 자세를 잡고, 뒤쪽 투명 막에는 '안녕히!'라고 쓰는 거야."], ["on a transparency behind; and nine people at the wings with a squib in each hand--all the dozen and a half going off at once--it would be very grand--awful from the front, quite awful.’", "그리고 양 옆에 아홉 사람이 손마다 폭죽을 하나씩 들고 — 그 열여덟 개를 한꺼번에 터뜨리는 거지 — 아주 웅장할 거야. 객석에서 보면 어마어마하지, 정말 어마어마해.\""]], [["As Nicholas appeared by no means impressed with the solemnity of the proposed effect, but, on the contrary, received the proposition in a most irreverent manner, and laughed at it very heartily, Mr. Crummles abandoned the project in its birth, and gloomily observed that they must make up the best bill they could with combats and hornpipes, and so stick to the legitimate drama.", "니콜라스는 이 거창한 연출에 도무지 감동받은 기색이 아니었다. 오히려 아주 불경스러운 태도로 그 제안을 받아들이고는 한참 신나게 웃어 버렸다. 그러자 크럼블스 씨는 그 계획을 시작도 하기 전에 접어 버렸다. 그러고는 우울하게 말했다. 결투 장면이랑 혼파이프 춤으로 최대한 그럴듯한 프로그램을 짜서, 정통 연극으로 밀고 나가는 수밖에 없겠다고 말이야."]], [["For the purpose of carrying this object into instant execution, the manager at once repaired to a small dressing-room, adjacent, where Mrs.", "이 계획을 당장 실행에 옮기려고, 단장은 곧장 옆에 붙은 자그마한 분장실로 갔다."], ["Crummles was then occupied in exchanging the habiliments of a melodramatic empress for the ordinary attire of matrons in the nineteenth century. And with the assistance of this lady, and the accomplished Mrs.", "거기서는 크럼블스 부인이 멜로드라마 속 황후 의상을 십구 세기 평범한 부인네 옷으로 갈아입고 있었다."], ["Grudden (who had quite a genius for making out bills, being a great hand at throwing in the notes of admiration, and knowing from long experience exactly where the largest capitals ought to go), he seriously applied himself to the composition of the poster.", "그리고 이 부인과 솜씨 좋은 그러든 부인의 도움을 받아 — 그러든 부인은 프로그램 짜는 데 아주 천재적이었거든. 감탄 부호를 척척 집어넣는 솜씨가 대단했고, 오랜 경험으로 어디에 제일 큰 대문자를 박아야 하는지 정확히 알았지 — 그는 포스터 문구를 진지하게 짜기 시작했다."]], [["‘Heigho!’ sighed Nicholas, as he threw himself back in the prompter’s chair, after telegraphing the needful directions to Smike, who had been playing a meagre tailor in the interlude, with one skirt to his coat, and a little pocket-handkerchief with a large hole in it, and a woollen nightcap, and a red nose, and other distinctive marks peculiar to tailors on the stage.", "\"휴!\" 니콜라스가 프롬프터 의자에 등을 기대며 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["‘Heigho!", "그는 막간극에서 비쩍 마른 재단사 역을 하던 스마이크에게 필요한 지시를 손짓으로 전해 둔 참이었다. 스마이크는 윗도리 자락이 한쪽밖에 없고, 큼지막한 구멍이 난 작은 손수건에 털실 잠자리 모자에 빨간 코까지, 무대 위 재단사 특유의 표시를 다 갖추고 있었지."], ["I wish all this were over.’", "\"휴! 이게 다 끝나 버렸으면 좋겠어.\""]], [["‘Over, Mr.", "\"끝나다니요, 존슨 씨!\""], ["Johnson!’ repeated a female voice behind him, in a kind of plaintive surprise.", "그의 뒤에서 여자 목소리가 무슨 서글픈 놀라움이 섞인 투로 되뇌었다."]], [["‘It was an ungallant speech, certainly,’ said Nicholas, looking up to see who the speaker was, and recognising Miss Snevellicci.", "\"확실히 신사답지 못한 말이었네요.\" 니콜라스가 누가 말한 건지 보려고 고개를 들었다가 스네벨리치 양인 걸 알아보고는 말했다."], ["‘I would not have made it if I had known you had been within hearing.’", "\"듣고 계신 줄 알았다면 그런 말은 안 했을 텐데요.\""]], [["‘What a dear that Mr.", "\"저 디그비 씨, 정말 사랑스럽지 않아요!\""], ["Digby is!’ said Miss Snevellicci, as the tailor went off on the opposite side, at the end of the piece, with great applause.", "스네벨리치 양이 말했다. 재단사가 작품 끝에서 반대편으로 큰 박수를 받으며 퇴장하던 참이었다."], ["(Smike’s theatrical name was Digby.)", "(스마이크의 무대용 이름이 디그비였거든.)"]], [["‘I’ll tell him presently, for his gratification, that you said so,’ returned Nicholas.", "\"이따가 그 친구한테 당신이 그렇게 말했다고 전해 줄게요. 좋아할 테니까요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh you naughty thing!’ rejoined Miss Snevellicci. ‘I don’t know though, that I should much mind HIS knowing my opinion of him; with some other people, indeed, it might be--’ Here Miss Snevellicci stopped, as though waiting to be questioned, but no questioning came, for Nicholas was thinking about more serious matters.", "\"어머, 못된 사람 같으니!\" 스네벨리치 양이 받아쳤다. \"하긴 저 사람이 제 마음을 안다고 해서 별로 꺼릴 것도 없겠네요. 다른 어떤 사람한테라면, 정말이지, 좀 다를지도 모르지만요.\" 여기서 스네벨리치 양은 질문을 기다리는 듯 말을 멈췄다. 하지만 아무 질문도 오지 않았다. 니콜라스가 더 심각한 일을 생각하고 있었거든."]], [["‘How kind it is of you,’ resumed Miss Snevellicci, after a short silence, ‘to sit waiting here for him night after night, night after night, no matter how tired you are; and taking so much pains with him, and doing it all with as much delight and readiness as if you were coining gold by it!’", "\"참 친절도 하셔라.\" 스네벨리치 양이 잠깐 잠잠하다가 다시 입을 열었다. \"이렇게 밤마다, 밤마다, 아무리 피곤해도 여기 앉아서 저 사람을 기다려 주시고요. 저 사람한테 그렇게 공을 들이면서, 그걸 다 무슨 금이라도 캐는 것처럼 기쁘고 선뜻한 마음으로 해 주시잖아요!\""]], [["‘He well deserves all the kindness I can show him, and a great deal more,’ said Nicholas.", "\"저 친구는 내가 베풀 수 있는 친절을 다 받을 만한 사람이에요. 그것보다 훨씬 더요.\""], ["‘He is the most grateful, single-hearted, affectionate creature that ever breathed.’", "니콜라스가 말했다. \"세상에 숨 쉬는 그 누구보다 고마움을 알고, 마음이 한결같고, 정 많은 사람이거든요.\""]], [["‘So odd, too,’ remarked Miss Snevellicci, ‘isn’t he?’", "\"그런데 참 별나기도 하죠.\" 스네벨리치 양이 말했다. \"안 그래요?\""]], [["‘God help him, and those who have made him so; he is indeed,’ rejoined Nicholas, shaking his head.", "\"하느님이 저 친구를, 그리고 저 친구를 저렇게 만든 사람들을 도와주시기를. 정말 그렇긴 해요.\" 니콜라스가 고개를 저으며 대답했다."]], [["‘He is such a devilish close chap,’ said Mr.", "\"저 친구는 입이 어찌나 무거운지 정말 지독하다니까.\""], ["Folair, who had come up a little before, and now joined in the conversation.", "폴레어 씨가 말했다. 조금 전에 다가와서는 이제 이야기에 끼어든 거였다."], ["‘Nobody can ever get anything out of him.’", "\"아무도 저 친구한테서 뭘 캐낼 수가 없어.\""]], [["‘What SHOULD they get out of him?’ asked Nicholas, turning round with some abruptness.", "\"캐내긴 뭘 캐낸다는 겁니까?\" 니콜라스가 좀 퉁명스럽게 돌아서며 물었다."]], [["‘Zooks!", "\"이런!"], ["what a fire-eater you are, Johnson!’ returned Mr.", "자넨 어쩜 그렇게 불같나, 존슨!\""], ["Folair, pulling up the heel of his dancing shoe.", "폴레어 씨가 무용화 뒤축을 끌어올리며 대꾸했다."], ["‘I’m only talking of the natural curiosity of the people here, to know what he has been about all his life.’", "\"난 그저 여기 사람들이 으레 품을 만한 궁금증을 말한 거야. 저 친구가 여태 살면서 뭘 하고 다녔는지 알고 싶어 한다는 거지.\""]], [["‘Poor fellow!", "\"가엾은 친구! 내 생각엔 뻔하잖아요."], ["it is pretty plain, I should think, that he has not the intellect to have been about anything of much importance to them or anybody else,’ said Nicholas.", "저 친구는 그 사람들한테든 누구한테든 대단할 만한 무슨 일을 하고 다닐 머리가 없는 사람이라는 거요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다."]], [["‘Ay,’ rejoined the actor, contemplating the effect of his face in a lamp reflector, ‘but that involves the whole question, you know.’", "\"그래.\" 배우가 등 반사판에 비친 제 얼굴을 들여다보며 받아쳤다. \"하지만 바로 거기서 온갖 의문이 생기는 거지.\""]], [["‘What question?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"무슨 의문요?\" 니콜라스가 물었다."]], [["‘Why, the who he is and what he is, and how you two, who are so different, came to be such close companions,’ replied Mr.", "\"왜, 저 친구가 누구고 뭐 하는 사람인지, 그리고 그렇게 다른 자네 둘이 어쩌다 그렇게 붙어 다니는 단짝이 됐는지 말이야.\""], ["Folair, delighted with the opportunity of saying something disagreeable.", "폴레어 씨가 대답했다. 뭔가 거슬리는 말을 할 기회가 생겨 신이 난 거였다."], ["‘That’s in everybody’s mouth.’", "\"그게 다들 입에 올리는 얘기거든.\""]], [["‘The “everybody”", "\"극장 안 '다들'이겠죠?\""], ["of the theatre, I suppose?’ said Nicholas, contemptuously.", "니콜라스가 경멸하듯 말했다."]], [["‘In it and out of it too,’ replied the actor.", "\"안에서도 밖에서도지.\" 배우가 대답했다."], ["‘Why, you know, Lenville says--’", "\"왜, 그 렌빌이 말하길—\""]], [["‘I thought I had silenced him effectually,’ interrupted Nicholas, reddening.", "\"내가 그 사람 입은 확실히 다물게 한 줄 알았는데.\" 니콜라스가 얼굴이 붉어지며 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Perhaps you have,’ rejoined the immovable Mr. Folair; ‘if you have, he said this before he was silenced: Lenville says that you’re a regular stick of an actor, and that it’s only the mystery about you that has caused you to go down with the people here, and that Crummles keeps it up for his own sake; though Lenville says he don’t believe there’s anything at all in it, except your having got into a scrape and run away from somewhere, for doing something or other.’", "\"아마 그랬겠지.\" 끄떡도 않는 폴레어 씨가 받아쳤다. \"그랬다 치고, 그 사람이 입 다물기 전에 이런 말을 했거든. 렌빌 말로는 자네가 영락없는 막대기 같은 형편없는 배우인데, 자네를 둘러싼 수수께끼 덕분에 여기 사람들한테 인기를 끈 거고, 크럼블스가 제 잇속 때문에 그 수수께끼를 부추기는 거래. 하긴 렌빌은 거기에 아무것도 없다고 믿는다더군. 그냥 자네가 어디선가 무슨 짓을 저질러서 곤경에 빠지고 도망쳐 온 것 말고는 말이야.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ said Nicholas, forcing a smile.", "\"오!\" 니콜라스가 억지로 웃음을 지으며 말했다."]], [["‘That’s a part of what he says,’ added Mr.", "\"그건 그 사람이 한 말 중 일부야.\""], ["Folair.", "폴레어 씨가 덧붙였다."], ["‘I mention it as the friend of both parties, and in strict confidence.", "\"난 양쪽 다 친구니까 말해 주는 거고, 아주 비밀이야. 난 그 사람 생각에 동의 안 해, 알겠지."], ["I don’t agree with him, you know.", "그 사람은 디그비가 바보라기보다 악당에 가깝다고 보더라고. 그리고 그 무게 잡는 역을 맡는 늙은 플러거스 있잖아, 그 사람 말로는 재작년 시즌에 코벤트 가든에서 전갈을 전하고 다닐 때, 마차 정류장 근처를 어슬렁거리던 소매치기가 하나 있었는데 그 얼굴이 디그비랑 똑 닮았더래."], ["He says he takes Digby to be more knave than fool; and old Fluggers, who does the heavy business you know, HE says that when he delivered messages at Covent Garden the season before last, there used to be a pickpocket hovering about the coach-stand who had exactly the face of Digby; though, as he very properly says, Digby may not be the same, but only his brother, or some near relation.’", "하긴 그 사람도 아주 옳은 말을 하더군. 디그비가 그 사람이 아닐 수도 있고, 그냥 그 형제나 가까운 친척일 수도 있다고 말이야.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ cried Nicholas again.", "\"오!\" 니콜라스가 또 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Mr.", "\"그래.\""], ["Folair, with undisturbed calmness, ‘that’s what they say.", "폴레어 씨가 흔들림 없이 차분하게 말했다. \"다들 그렇게 말한다니까."], ["I thought I’d tell you, because really you ought to know.", "자네가 정말 알아야 할 것 같아서 말해 주는 거야."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["here’s this blessed phenomenon at last.", "드디어 이 복덩이 신동이 나오네."], ["Ugh, you little imposition, I should like to--quite ready, my darling,--humbug--Ring up, Mrs. G., and let the favourite wake ‘em.’", "어휴, 요 깜찍한 사기꾼, 내가 정말—다 됐다, 아가야,—웃기시네—올려요, 그러든 부인, 인기 스타가 사람들 깨우게 해 줍시다.\""]], [["Uttering in a loud voice such of the latter allusions as were complimentary to the unconscious phenomenon, and giving the rest in a confidential ‘aside’ to Nicholas, Mr.", "폴레어 씨는 마지막 말 가운데 영문 모르는 신동을 추켜세우는 대목은 큰 소리로 내뱉고, 나머지는 니콜라스에게 은밀히 '곁말'로 던졌다."], ["Folair followed the ascent of the curtain with his eyes, regarded with a sneer the reception of Miss Crummles as the Maiden, and, falling back a step or two to advance with the better effect, uttered a preliminary howl, and ‘went on’ chattering his teeth and brandishing his tin tomahawk as the Indian Savage.", "그러고는 올라가는 막을 눈으로 좇으며, 처녀 역으로 등장하는 크럼블스 양이 받는 환영을 비웃듯 바라보았다. 그러고는 더 효과적으로 등장하려고 한두 걸음 물러섰다가, 예비 울부짖음을 한 번 내지르고는 이를 딱딱 부딪치며 양철 도끼를 휘두르는 인디언 야만인 역으로 '등장했다'."]], [["‘So these are some of the stories they invent about us, and bandy from mouth to mouth!’ thought Nicholas.", "'이런 게 사람들이 우리에 대해 지어내서 입에서 입으로 떠벌리는 이야기들이군!'"], ["‘If a man would commit an inexpiable offence against any society, large or small, let him be successful.", "니콜라스는 생각했다. '크든 작든 어떤 사회에 절대 용서받지 못할 죄를 짓고 싶거든, 성공해 봐라."], ["They will forgive him any crime but that.’", "그러면 어떤 죄든 다 용서해 줘도 그 죄만큼은 안 봐주거든.'"]], [["‘You surely don’t mind what that malicious creature says, Mr.", "\"설마 저 심술궂은 작자가 한 말을 마음에 두시는 건 아니죠, 존슨 씨?\""], ["Johnson?’ observed Miss Snevellicci in her most winning tones.", "스네벨리치 양이 더없이 다정한 어조로 말했다."]], [["‘Not I,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"아니에요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다."], ["‘If I were going to remain here, I might think it worth my while to embroil myself.", "\"여기 계속 남을 거라면 나서서 한바탕 붙어 볼 만하다 싶겠죠. 하지만 사정이 이러니, 목이 쉬도록 떠들라죠."], ["As it is, let them talk till they are hoarse.", "그런데 보세요.\" 스마이크가 다가오자 니콜라스가 덧붙였다."], ["But here,’ added Nicholas, as Smike approached, ‘here comes the subject of a portion of their good-nature, so let he and I say good night together.’", "\"저기 그 사람들 입방아의 한 대상이 오네요. 그러니 저 친구랑 같이 작별 인사를 해야겠어요.\""]], [["‘No, I will not let either of you say anything of the kind,’ returned Miss Snevellicci.", "\"안 돼요, 두 분 다 그런 말씀 못 하시게 할 거예요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 받아쳤다."], ["‘You must come home and see mama, who only came to Portsmouth today, and is dying to behold you.", "\"저희 집에 오셔서 엄마를 뵈셔야 해요. 엄마가 오늘에야 포츠머스에 오셨는데, 당신을 어찌나 보고 싶어 하시는지 몰라요."], ["Led, my dear, persuade Mr Johnson.’", "레드, 얘, 존슨 씨 좀 설득해 봐.\""]], [["‘Oh, I’m sure,’ returned Miss Ledrook, with considerable vivacity, ‘if YOU can’t persuade him--’ Miss Ledrook said no more, but intimated, by a dexterous playfulness, that if Miss Snevellicci couldn’t persuade him, nobody could.", "\"어머, 정말이지.\" 레드룩 양이 꽤 발랄하게 받았다. \"네가 못 설득하면야—\" 레드룩 양은 더는 말을 잇지 않았지만, 능청스러운 장난기로 넌지시 알려 주었다. 스네벨리치 양이 못 설득하면 그 누구도 못 한다고 말이야."]], [["‘Mr. and Mrs.", "\"릴리빅 씨 부부가 저희 집에 방을 얻으셨는데, 당분간 저희 거실을 같이 쓰고 계세요.\""], ["Lillyvick have taken lodgings in our house, and share our sitting-room for the present,’ said Miss Snevellicci.", "스네벨리치 양이 말했다."], ["‘Won’t that induce you?’", "\"그럼 마음이 좀 동하시지 않아요?\""]], [["‘Surely,’ returned Nicholas, ‘I can require no possible inducement beyond your invitation.’", "\"그야,\" 니콜라스가 대답했다. \"당신 초대 말고 다른 무슨 끌릴 거리가 더 필요하겠어요.\""]], [["‘Oh no! I dare say,’ rejoined Miss Snevellicci. And Miss Ledrook said, ‘Upon my word!’ Upon which Miss Snevellicci said that Miss Ledrook was a giddy thing; and Miss Ledrook said that Miss Snevellicci needn’t colour up quite so much; and Miss Snevellicci beat Miss Ledrook, and Miss Ledrook beat Miss Snevellicci.", "\"어머, 아니에요! 그러시겠죠.\" 스네벨리치 양이 받아쳤다. 그러자 레드룩 양이 \"어머, 세상에!\" 하고 말했다. 그 말에 스네벨리치 양이 레드룩 양더러 경박한 애라고 했고, 레드룩 양은 스네벨리치 양더러 그렇게 얼굴까지 붉힐 거 없다고 했다. 그러고는 스네벨리치 양이 레드룩 양을 찰싹 때리고, 레드룩 양도 스네벨리치 양을 찰싹 때렸다."]], [["‘Come,’ said Miss Ledrook, ‘it’s high time we were there, or we shall have poor Mrs.", "\"가요.\" 레드룩 양이 말했다."], ["Snevellicci thinking that you have run away with her daughter, Mr.", "\"이제 슬슬 가야 할 시간이에요. 안 그러면 가엾은 스네벨리치 부인이 당신이 따님을 데리고 도망친 줄 아실 거예요, 존슨 씨."], ["Johnson; and then we should have a pretty to-do.’", "그럼 한바탕 난리가 나겠죠.\""]], [["‘My dear Led,’ remonstrated Miss Snevellicci, ‘how you do talk!’", "\"얘, 레드.\" 스네벨리치 양이 나무랐다. \"넌 말을 어쩜 그렇게 하니!\""]], [["Miss Ledrook made no answer, but taking Smike’s arm in hers, left her friend and Nicholas to follow at their pleasure; which it pleased them, or rather pleased Nicholas, who had no great fancy for a TETE-A-TETE under the circumstances, to do at once.", "레드룩 양은 대꾸하지 않고 스마이크의 팔짱을 끼더니, 친구와 니콜라스가 알아서 따라오게 내버려 두었다. 두 사람은 — 아니 정확히는 니콜라스가 — 그렇게 하는 게 좋았다. 그런 상황에서 단둘이 마주 걷는 건 그리 내키지 않았거든. 그래서 곧장 따라나섰다."]], [["There were not wanting matters of conversation when they reached the street, for it turned out that Miss Snevellicci had a small basket to carry home, and Miss Ledrook a small bandbox, both containing such minor articles of theatrical costume as the lady performers usually carried to and fro every evening. Nicholas would insist upon carrying the basket, and Miss Snevellicci would insist upon carrying it herself, which gave rise to a struggle, in which Nicholas captured the basket and the bandbox likewise. Then Nicholas said, that he wondered what could possibly be inside the basket, and attempted to peep in, whereat Miss Snevellicci screamed, and declared that if she thought he had seen, she was sure she should faint away. This declaration was followed by a similar attempt on the bandbox, and similar demonstrations on the part of Miss Ledrook, and then both ladies vowed that they wouldn’t move a step further until Nicholas had promised that he wouldn’t offer to peep again. At last Nicholas pledged himself to betray no further curiosity, and they walked on: both ladies giggling very much, and declaring that they never had seen such a wicked creature in all their born days--never.", "거리에 나서자 이야깃거리가 없지 않았다. 알고 보니 스네벨리치 양은 집까지 들고 갈 작은 바구니가 있었고, 레드룩 양은 작은 모자 상자가 있었거든. 둘 다 안에는 여배우들이 으레 저녁마다 들고 다니는 자잘한 무대 의상 따위가 들어 있었다. 니콜라스가 바구니를 들겠다고 우기고, 스네벨리치 양은 자기가 들겠다고 우겼다. 그래서 옥신각신 실랑이가 벌어졌고, 결국 니콜라스가 바구니를 빼앗고 모자 상자까지 차지했다. 그러고는 니콜라스가 바구니 안에 대체 뭐가 들었을까 궁금하다며 들여다보려고 했다. 그러자 스네벨리치 양이 비명을 지르며, 만약 그가 본 것 같으면 자기는 정말 기절해 버릴 거라고 외쳤다. 이 선언에 이어 모자 상자에도 똑같은 시도가 이어졌고, 레드룩 양 쪽에서도 똑같은 호들갑이 벌어졌다. 그러고는 두 여자가 다 같이, 니콜라스가 다시는 들여다보려 하지 않겠다고 약속할 때까지 한 발짝도 더 안 가겠다고 맹세했다. 마침내 니콜라스가 더는 호기심을 부리지 않겠다고 약속하자, 세 사람은 다시 걸었다. 두 여자는 연신 키득거리며, 살다 살다 이렇게 못된 사람은 처음 본다고, 정말이지 처음이라고 떠들었다."]], [["Lightening the way with such pleasantry as this, they arrived at the tailor’s house in no time; and here they made quite a little party, there being present besides Mr. Lillyvick and Mrs. Lillyvick, not only Miss Snevellicci’s mama, but her papa also. And an uncommonly fine man Miss Snevellicci’s papa was, with a hook nose, and a white forehead, and curly black hair, and high cheek bones, and altogether quite a handsome face, only a little pimply as though with drinking. He had a very broad chest had Miss Snevellicci’s papa, and he wore a threadbare blue dress-coat buttoned with gilt buttons tight across it; and he no sooner saw Nicholas come into the room, than he whipped the two forefingers of his right hand in between the two centre buttons, and sticking his other arm gracefully a-kimbo seemed to say, ‘Now, here I am, my buck, and what have you got to say to me?’", "이런 농담으로 길을 가볍게 하는 사이, 그들은 어느새 재단사 집에 다다랐다. 그리고 여기서 제법 자그마한 모임이 꾸려졌다. 릴리빅 씨와 릴리빅 부인 말고도 스네벨리치 양의 엄마뿐 아니라 아빠까지 와 있었거든. 그런데 스네벨리치 양의 아빠는 유난히 잘생긴 남자였다. 매부리코에 흰 이마, 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리, 우뚝한 광대뼈까지, 전체적으로 꽤 잘생긴 얼굴이었지. 다만 술 탓인지 여드름이 좀 돋아 있었다. 스네벨리치 양의 아빠는 가슴팍이 아주 떡 벌어졌고, 다 닳은 파란 연미복을 입고 금박 단추로 꽉 끼게 채우고 있었다. 그는 니콜라스가 방에 들어오는 걸 보자마자, 오른손 검지와 중지 두 손가락을 가운데 단추 두 개 사이에 척 끼우고, 다른 팔은 우아하게 허리에 척 얹었다. 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. '자, 내가 바로 여기 있다, 젊은이. 그래, 나한테 무슨 할 말이 있나?'"]], [["Such was, and in such an attitude sat Miss Snevellicci’s papa, who had been in the profession ever since he had first played the ten-year-old imps in the Christmas pantomimes; who could sing a little, dance a little, fence a little, act a little, and do everything a little, but not much; who had been sometimes in the ballet, and sometimes in the chorus, at every theatre in London; who was always selected in virtue of his figure to play the military visitors and the speechless noblemen; who always wore a smart dress, and came on arm-in-arm with a smart lady in short petticoats,--and always did it too with such an air that people in the pit had been several times known to cry out ‘Bravo!’ under the impression that he was somebody. Such was Miss Snevellicci’s papa, upon whom some envious persons cast the imputation that he occasionally beat Miss Snevellicci’s mama, who was still a dancer, with a neat little figure and some remains of good looks; and who now sat, as she danced,--being rather too old for the full glare of the foot-lights,--in the background.", "스네벨리치 양의 아빠는 바로 그런 사람이었고, 그런 자세로 앉아 있었다. 그는 크리스마스 무언극에서 열 살짜리 꼬마 도깨비 역을 처음 맡은 이래로 줄곧 이 바닥에 몸담아 온 사람이었다. 노래도 조금, 춤도 조금, 검술도 조금, 연기도 조금, 뭐든 다 조금씩은 했지만 어느 것 하나 대단치는 않았다. 런던의 온갖 극장에서 발레단에도 들었다 합창단에도 들었다 했고, 늘 그 체격 덕에 군인 손님이나 대사 없는 귀족 역으로 뽑혔다. 언제나 말쑥한 옷을 차려입고, 짧은 치마를 입은 말쑥한 여자와 팔짱을 끼고 등장했지. 그것도 어찌나 폼 나게 해냈던지, 일등석 관객들이 그를 무슨 대단한 사람인 줄 알고 \"브라보!\"를 몇 번이나 외친 일도 있었다. 스네벨리치 양의 아빠가 바로 그런 사람이었다. 시샘하는 사람들은 그가 가끔 스네벨리치 양의 엄마를 때린다는 험담을 씌우기도 했지. 엄마는 아직도 무용수였는데, 몸매가 곱고 미모도 좀 남아 있었지만, 무대 조명을 정면으로 받기엔 나이가 좀 든 편이라 지금은 춤을 출 때처럼 뒤편에 물러앉아 있었다."]], [["To these good people Nicholas was presented with much formality.", "이 좋은 사람들에게 니콜라스가 아주 격식을 갖춰 소개되었다. 소개가 다 끝나자 스네벨리치 양의 아빠는 — 럼주에 물을 탄 냄새를 풍기는 사람이었지 — 이렇게나 재능이 뛰어난 신사를 알게 되어 기쁘다고 말했다."], ["The introduction being completed, Miss Snevellicci’s papa (who was scented with rum-and-water) said that he was delighted to make the acquaintance of a gentleman so highly talented; and furthermore remarked, that there hadn’t been such a hit made--no, not since the first appearance of his friend Mr. Glavormelly, at the Coburg.", "그러고는 한마디 덧붙이기를, 이만한 성공은 — 아무렴, 자기 친구 글래보멜리 씨가 코버그 극장에 처음 섰던 이래로 없었다고 했다."]], [["‘You have seen him, sir?’ said Miss Snevellicci’s papa.", "\"그 사람 보셨소, 선생?\" 스네벨리치 양의 아빠가 물었다."]], [["‘No, really I never did,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"아니요, 정말 본 적이 없네요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다."]], [["‘You never saw my friend Glavormelly, sir!’ said Miss Snevellicci’s papa.", "\"내 친구 글래보멜리를 못 보셨다고요, 선생!\" 스네벨리치 양의 아빠가 말했다."], ["‘Then you have never seen acting yet.", "\"그럼 아직 진짜 연기를 못 보신 거요."], ["If he had lived--’", "그 친구가 살아 있었더라면—\""]], [["‘Oh, he is dead, is he?’ interrupted Nicholas.", "\"오, 그분 돌아가셨군요?\" 니콜라스가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘He is,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇소.\""], ["Snevellicci, ‘but he isn’t in Westminster Abbey, more’s the shame.", "스네벨리치 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 웨스트민스터 사원에 묻히진 못했지, 부끄러운 노릇이오."], ["He was a--. Well, no matter.", "그 친구는 정말—."], ["He is gone to that bourne from whence no traveller returns.", "뭐, 됐소. 그는 어떤 나그네도 돌아오지 못하는 그 나라로 떠났소."], ["I hope he is appreciated THERE.’", "그곳에서는 제대로 인정받기를 바라오.\""]], [["So saying Miss Snevellicci’s papa rubbed the tip of his nose with a very yellow silk handkerchief, and gave the company to understand that these recollections overcame him.", "그렇게 말하며 스네벨리치 양의 아빠는 아주 누런 비단 손수건으로 코끝을 문질렀다. 그러고는 이 추억들이 자기를 북받치게 한다는 걸 좌중에 알렸다."]], [["‘Well, Mr.", "\"자, 릴리빅 씨.\""], ["Lillyvick,’ said Nicholas, ‘and how are you?’", "니콜라스가 말했다. \"어떻게 지내십니까?\""]], [["‘Quite well, sir,’ replied the collector.", "\"아주 잘 지내오, 선생.\" 수금원이 대답했다."], ["‘There is nothing like the married state, sir, depend upon it.’", "\"결혼한 처지만 한 게 없소, 선생, 정말이오.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ said Nicholas, laughing.", "\"그렇습니까!\" 니콜라스가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["nothing like it, sir,’ replied Mr.", "그만한 게 없지, 선생.\" 릴리빅 씨가 엄숙하게 대답했다."], ["Lillyvick solemnly.", "\"어떻게 생각하시오.\""], ["‘How do you think,’ whispered the collector, drawing him aside, ‘how do you think she looks tonight?’", "수금원이 그를 한쪽으로 끌어당기며 속삭였다. \"오늘 밤 저 사람 어때 보이오?\""]], [["‘As handsome as ever,’ replied Nicholas, glancing at the late Miss Petowker.", "\"여전히 아름다우신데요.\" 니콜라스가 한때 페토커 양이던 부인을 흘끗 보며 대답했다."]], [["‘Why, there’s air about her, sir,’ whispered the collector, ‘that I never saw in anybody.", "\"왜, 저 사람한테는 어떤 분위기가 있단 말이오, 선생.\" 수금원이 속삭였다."], ["Look at her now she moves to put the kettle on.", "\"내가 누구한테서도 본 적 없는 그런 거 말이오. 지금 저기 주전자 올리려고 움직이는 것 좀 보시오."], ["There!", "저기!"], ["Isn’t it fascination, sir?’", "정말 매혹적이지 않소, 선생?\""]], [["‘You’re a lucky man,’ said Nicholas.", "\"행복한 분이시네요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다."]], [["‘Ha, ha, ha!’ rejoined the collector.", "\"하, 하, 하!\" 수금원이 받아쳤다."], ["‘No.", "\"아니."], ["Do you think I am though, eh?", "그래도 그렇게 생각하시오, 응?"], ["Perhaps I may be, perhaps I may be.", "그럴지도, 그럴지도 모르지."], ["I say, I couldn’t have done much better if I had been a young man, could I?", "내 말은, 내가 젊은 사람이었더라도 이보다 더 잘하긴 어려웠을 거요, 안 그렇소?"], ["You couldn’t have done much better yourself, could you--eh--could you?’ With such inquires, and many more such, Mr.", "당신도 이보다 더 잘하긴 어려웠을 거요, 안 그렇소—응—그렇지 않소?\""], ["Lillyvick jerked his elbow into Nicholas’s side, and chuckled till his face became quite purple in the attempt to keep down his satisfaction.", "이런 질문에다 또 이런 질문들을 잔뜩 늘어놓으며, 릴리빅 씨는 니콜라스의 옆구리를 팔꿈치로 쿡쿡 찔러댔다. 그러고는 흐뭇함을 억누르려 애쓰다 얼굴이 아주 시뻘게질 때까지 낄낄거렸다."]], [["By this time the cloth had been laid under the joint superintendence of all the ladies, upon two tables put together, one being high and narrow, and the other low and broad.", "이쯤 되자 모든 부인들의 합동 감독 아래 식탁보가 깔렸다. 식탁 두 개를 붙여 놓았는데, 하나는 높고 좁았고 다른 하나는 낮고 넓었다."], ["There were oysters at the top, sausages at the bottom, a pair of snuffers in the centre, and baked potatoes wherever it was most convenient to put them.", "위쪽엔 굴, 아래쪽엔 소시지, 가운데엔 촛불 심지 가위 한 벌, 그리고 구운 감자는 놓기 편한 데마다 놓여 있었다."], ["Two additional chairs were brought in from the bedroom: Miss Snevellicci sat at the head of the table, and Mr.", "침실에서 의자 두 개를 더 가져왔다. 스네벨리치 양은 식탁 상석에, 릴리빅 씨는 말석에 앉았다."], ["Lillyvick at the foot; and Nicholas had not only the honour of sitting next Miss Snevellicci, but of having Miss Snevellicci’s mama on his right hand, and Miss Snevellicci’s papa over the way.", "그리고 니콜라스는 스네벨리치 양 옆에 앉는 영광뿐 아니라, 오른편에 스네벨리치 양의 엄마를, 맞은편에 스네벨리치 양의 아빠를 두는 영광까지 누렸다. 한마디로 그는 이 잔치의 주인공이었다."], ["In short, he was the hero of the feast; and when the table was cleared and something warm introduced, Miss Snevellicci’s papa got up and proposed his health in a speech containing such affecting allusions to his coming departure, that Miss Snevellicci wept, and was compelled to retire into the bedroom.", "식탁이 치워지고 뭔가 따뜻한 게 나오자, 스네벨리치 양의 아빠가 일어나 그의 건강을 비는 축사를 했다. 그런데 그 안에 그가 곧 떠난다는 대목이 어찌나 가슴 아프게 들어 있었던지, 스네벨리치 양이 그만 울음을 터뜨리며 침실로 물러날 수밖에 없었다."]], [["‘Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["Don’t take any notice of it,’ said Miss Ledrook, peeping in from the bedroom.", "신경 쓰지 마세요.\" 레드룩 양이 침실에서 빼꼼 내다보며 말했다."], ["‘Say, when she comes back, that she exerts herself too much.’", "\"쟤가 돌아오면, 너무 무리해서 그런 거라고 말해 주세요.\""]], [["Miss Ledrook eked out this speech with so many mysterious nods and frowns before she shut the door again, that a profound silence came upon all the company, during which Miss Snevellicci’s papa looked very big indeed--several sizes larger than life--at everybody in turn, but particularly at Nicholas, and kept on perpetually emptying his tumbler and filling it again, until the ladies returned in a cluster, with Miss Snevellicci among them.", "레드룩 양은 다시 문을 닫기 전에 이 말에다 알 수 없는 끄덕임과 찡그림을 어찌나 잔뜩 덧붙였던지, 좌중에 깊은 침묵이 내려앉았다. 그동안 스네벨리치 양의 아빠는 정말이지 잔뜩 거들먹거리는 표정으로 — 실물보다 몇 배나 커진 듯한 얼굴로 — 모두를 차례로, 특히 니콜라스를 노려보았다. 그러면서 잔을 연신 비우고 또 채우고를 거듭했다. 그러다 부인들이 스네벨리치 양을 데리고 우르르 돌아왔다."]], [["‘You needn’t alarm yourself a bit, Mr.", "\"조금도 놀라실 거 없어요, 스네벨리치 씨.\""], ["Snevellicci,’ said Mrs. Lillyvick.", "릴리빅 부인이 말했다."], ["‘She is only a little weak and nervous; she has been so ever since the morning.’", "\"쟤가 그냥 좀 약하고 신경이 예민할 뿐이에요. 아침부터 내내 그랬거든요.\""]], [["‘Oh,’ said Mr.", "\"오.\""], ["Snevellicci, ‘that’s all, is it?’", "스네벨리치 씨가 말했다. \"그게 다요?\""]], [["‘Oh yes, that’s all.", "\"오, 그럼요, 그게 다예요."], ["Don’t make a fuss about it,’ cried all the ladies together.", "호들갑 떨지 마세요.\" 부인들이 다 같이 외쳤다."]], [["Now this was not exactly the kind of reply suited to Mr.", "그런데 이건 한 남자이자 아버지로서 스네벨리치 씨의 위신에 딱 어울리는 대답은 아니었다."], ["Snevellicci’s importance as a man and a father, so he picked out the unfortunate Mrs Snevellicci, and asked her what the devil she meant by talking to him in that way.", "그래서 그는 불운한 스네벨리치 부인을 콕 집어, 대체 무슨 심보로 자기한테 그딴 식으로 말하느냐고 따졌다."]], [["‘Dear me, my dear!’ said Mrs.", "\"아유, 여보.\""], ["Snevellicci.", "스네벨리치 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Don’t call me your dear, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "\"날 여보라고 부르지 마시오, 부인.\""], ["Snevellicci, ‘if you please.’", "스네벨리치 씨가 말했다. \"제발.\""]], [["‘Pray, pa, don’t,’ interposed Miss Snevellicci.", "\"제발, 아빠, 그러지 마세요.\" 스네벨리치 양이 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Don’t what, my child?’", "\"뭘 그러지 말라는 거냐, 얘야?\""]], [["‘Talk in that way.’", "\"그런 식으로 말하지 마시라고요.\""]], [["‘Why not?’ said Mr.", "\"왜 안 되는데?\""], ["Snevellicci.", "스네벨리치 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I hope you don’t suppose there’s anybody here who is to prevent my talking as I like?’", "\"설마 내가 내 맘대로 말하는 걸 막을 사람이 여기 있다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지?\""]], [["‘Nobody wants to, pa,’ rejoined his daughter.", "\"아무도 막으려는 게 아니에요, 아빠.\" 딸이 받아쳤다."]], [["‘Nobody would if they did want to,’ said Mr.", "\"막으려고 해 봤자 못 막지.\" 스네벨리치 씨가 말했다."], ["Snevellicci.", "\"나는 나 자신이 부끄럽지 않아."], ["‘I am not ashamed of myself, Snevellicci is my name; I’m to be found in Broad Court, Bow Street, when I’m in town.", "스네벨리치가 내 이름이다. 내가 시내에 있을 땐 보 거리의 브로드 코트에 가면 날 찾을 수 있어."], ["If I’m not at home, let any man ask for me at the stage-door.", "집에 없거든, 누구든 무대 출입문에서 날 찾으라고 해."], ["Damme, they know me at the stage-door I suppose.", "제기랄, 무대 출입문 사람들은 날 다 알 테니까."], ["Most men have seen my portrait at the cigar shop round the corner.", "길모퉁이 담배 가게에 가면 내 초상화를 본 사람도 수두룩하지."], ["I’ve been mentioned in the newspapers before now, haven’t I?", "전에 신문에 내 이름이 실린 적도 있어, 안 그래?"], ["Talk!", "말이라!"], ["I’ll tell you what; if I found out that any man had been tampering with the affections of my daughter, I wouldn’t talk.", "내 말해 두지. 만약 어떤 놈이 내 딸 마음을 가지고 함부로 놀았다는 걸 알게 되면, 난 말 따위 안 해."], ["I’d astonish him without talking; that’s my way.’", "말 한마디 없이 그놈을 깜짝 놀라게 해 줄 거야. 그게 내 방식이거든.\""]], [["So saying, Mr. Snevellicci struck the palm of his left hand three smart blows with his clenched fist; pulled a phantom nose with his right thumb and forefinger, and swallowed another glassful at a draught.", "그렇게 말하며 스네벨리치 씨는 주먹을 꽉 쥐고 왼손 손바닥을 세 번 야무지게 내리쳤다."], ["‘That’s my way,’ repeated Mr.", "그러고는 오른손 엄지와 검지로 허공의 코를 잡아 비틀고는, 또 한 잔을 단숨에 들이켰다. \"그게 내 방식이지.\""], ["Snevellicci.", "스네벨리치 씨가 거듭 말했다."]], [["Most public characters have their failings; and the truth is that Mr Snevellicci was a little addicted to drinking; or, if the whole truth must be told, that he was scarcely ever sober. He knew in his cups three distinct stages of intoxication,--the dignified--the quarrelsome--the amorous. When professionally engaged he never got beyond the dignified; in private circles he went through all three, passing from one to another with a rapidity of transition often rather perplexing to those who had not the honour of his acquaintance.", "이름난 사람들은 대부분 약점이 있는 법이다. 그리고 사실인즉 스네벨리치 씨는 술을 좀 좋아하는 편이었다. 아니, 모든 사실을 다 털어놓자면 거의 맨정신인 적이 없었다고 해야겠지. 그는 취하면 뚜렷한 세 단계를 거쳤다. 점잖은 단계, 시비조 단계, 그리고 다정한 단계 말이다. 일로 무대에 설 때는 점잖은 단계를 절대 넘지 않았다. 하지만 사적인 자리에서는 세 단계를 다 거쳤는데, 어찌나 빨리 한 단계에서 다음 단계로 넘어가는지, 그를 잘 모르는 사람들은 종종 적잖이 어리둥절해했다."]], [["Thus Mr.", "그래서 스네벨리치 씨는 또 한 잔을 들이켜자마자, 방금 시비조 기색을 보였던 걸 까맣게 잊은 채 행복하게 좌중 모두를 향해 미소 지었다."], ["Snevellicci had no sooner swallowed another glassful than he smiled upon all present in happy forgetfulness of having exhibited symptoms of pugnacity, and proposed ‘The ladies!", "그러고는 더없이 활기찬 태도로 건배를 제안했다."], ["Bless their hearts!’ in a most vivacious manner.", "\"숙녀분들을 위하여! 그분들께 축복을!\""]], [["‘I love ‘em,’ said Mr.", "\"난 그분들을 사랑해.\""], ["Snevellicci, looking round the table, ‘I love ‘em, every one.’", "스네벨리치 씨가 식탁을 둘러보며 말했다. \"사랑하고말고, 한 사람 한 사람 다.\""]], [["‘Not every one,’ reasoned Mr.", "\"한 사람 한 사람 다는 아니겠죠.\""], ["Lillyvick, mildly.", "릴리빅 씨가 부드럽게 따졌다."]], [["‘Yes, every one,’ repeated Mr.", "\"아니, 한 사람 한 사람 다요.\""], ["Snevellicci.", "스네벨리치 씨가 거듭 말했다."]], [["‘That would include the married ladies, you know,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 결혼한 부인들까지 포함되는데 말이오.\""], ["Lillyvick.", "릴리빅 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I love them too, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"그분들도 사랑해요, 선생.\""], ["Snevellicci.", "스네벨리치 씨가 말했다."]], [["The collector looked into the surrounding faces with an aspect of grave astonishment, seeming to say, ‘This is a nice man!’ and appeared a little surprised that Mrs.", "수금원은 자못 놀란 표정으로 둘러앉은 얼굴들을 살펴보았다. 마치 '이거 참 대단한 사람이군!'"], ["Lillyvick’s manner yielded no evidences of horror and indignation.", "하고 말하는 듯했다. 그리고 릴리빅 부인의 태도에서 어떤 공포나 분노의 기색도 보이지 않자 좀 놀란 눈치였다."]], [["‘One good turn deserves another,’ said Mr.", "\"가는 정이 있으면 오는 정이 있는 법이지.\""], ["Snevellicci.", "스네벨리치 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I love them and they love me.’ And as if this avowal were not made in sufficient disregard and defiance of all moral obligations, what did Mr.", "\"내가 그분들을 사랑하고, 그분들도 날 사랑하거든.\" 그러고는 이 고백이 모든 도덕적 의무를 무시하고 거스르는 걸로도 모자라다는 듯, 스네벨리치 씨가 무슨 짓을 했게?"], ["Snevellicci do?", "그가 윙크를 했다 — 대놓고 거리낌 없이 윙크를 했다."], ["He winked--winked openly and undisguisedly; winked with his right eye--upon Henrietta Lillyvick!", "오른쪽 눈으로 — 헨리에타 릴리빅을 향해서 말이다!"]], [["The collector fell back in his chair in the intensity of his astonishment.", "수금원은 너무 놀란 나머지 의자에 털썩 기대고 말았다."], ["If anybody had winked at her as Henrietta Petowker, it would have been indecorous in the last degree; but as Mrs.", "그 여자가 헨리에타 페토커였을 때 누가 그렇게 윙크를 했더라도 더없이 점잖지 못한 짓이었을 거다. 그런데 릴리빅 부인한테라니!"], ["Lillyvick! While he thought of it in a cold perspiration, and wondered whether it was possible that he could be dreaming, Mr.", "그가 식은땀을 흘리며 그 생각을 하면서, 혹시 내가 꿈을 꾸고 있는 게 아닐까 의아해하는 사이, 스네벨리치 씨가 다시 윙크를 했다."], ["Snevellicci repeated the wink, and drinking to Mrs. Lillyvick in dumb show, actually blew her a kiss!", "그러고는 무언극처럼 릴리빅 부인을 향해 건배하더니, 진짜로 그녀에게 입맞춤을 날렸다!"], ["Mr. Lillyvick left his chair, walked straight up to the other end of the table, and fell upon him--literally fell upon him--instantaneously.", "릴리빅 씨가 의자에서 일어나 식탁 반대편 끝으로 곧장 걸어가더니, 그에게 덮쳐들었다 — 그야말로 덮쳐든 거다 — 순식간에 말이다."], ["Mr. Lillyvick was no light weight, and consequently when he fell upon Mr.", "릴리빅 씨는 결코 가벼운 몸집이 아니었다."], ["Snevellicci, Mr. Snevellicci fell under the table.", "그래서 그가 스네벨리치 씨에게 덮쳐들자, 스네벨리치 씨가 식탁 밑으로 쓰러졌다."], ["Mr. Lillyvick followed him, and the ladies screamed.", "릴리빅 씨도 그를 따라 들어갔고, 부인들이 비명을 질렀다."]], [["‘What is the matter with the men!", "\"이 양반들 왜 이러는 거예요! 미쳤어요?\""], ["Are they mad?’ cried Nicholas, diving under the table, dragging up the collector by main force, and thrusting him, all doubled up, into a chair, as if he had been a stuffed figure.", "니콜라스가 식탁 밑으로 뛰어들며 외쳤다. 그러고는 수금원을 온 힘을 다해 끌어내, 마치 박제 인형이라도 다루듯 잔뜩 웅크린 채로 의자에 밀어 넣었다."], ["‘What do you mean to do?", "\"어쩌실 작정이세요?"], ["What do you want to do?", "뭘 하시려는 거예요?"], ["What is the matter with you?’", "도대체 왜 이러십니까?\""]], [["While Nicholas raised up the collector, Smike had performed the same office for Mr.", "니콜라스가 수금원을 일으키는 사이, 스마이크가 스네벨리치 씨한테 똑같이 해 주었다."], ["Snevellicci, who now regarded his late adversary in tipsy amazement.", "스네벨리치 씨는 술 취한 얼굴로 방금 전 적수를 멍하니 바라보았다."]], [["‘Look here, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"여기 좀 보시오, 선생.\""], ["Lillyvick, pointing to his astonished wife, ‘here is purity and elegance combined, whose feelings have been outraged--violated, sir!’", "릴리빅 씨가 어리둥절해하는 아내를 가리키며 대답했다. \"여기 순결과 우아함을 두루 갖춘 사람이 있는데, 그 감정이 모욕당했소—짓밟혔단 말이오, 선생!\""]], [["‘Lor, what nonsense he talks!’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"세상에, 무슨 헛소리예요!\""], ["Lillyvick in answer to the inquiring look of Nicholas.", "릴리빅 부인이 니콜라스의 묻는 눈빛에 대꾸하며 외쳤다."], ["‘Nobody has said anything to me.’", "\"아무도 나한테 뭐라고 한 적 없어요.\""]], [["‘Said, Henrietta!’ cried the collector.", "\"뭐라고 했냐고, 헨리에타!\" 수금원이 외쳤다."], ["‘Didn’t I see him--’ Mr Lillyvick couldn’t bring himself to utter the word, but he counterfeited the motion of the eye.", "\"내가 저자가—\" 릴리빅 씨는 차마 그 말을 입 밖에 내지 못하고, 대신 눈을 깜빡이는 시늉을 흉내 냈다."]], [["‘Well!’ cried Mrs.", "\"흥!\" 릴리빅 부인이 외쳤다."], ["Lillyvick. ‘Do you suppose nobody is ever to look at me?", "\"그럼 아무도 날 쳐다보면 안 된다는 거예요?"], ["A pretty thing to be married indeed, if that was law!’", "그게 법이라면 결혼이란 참 좋은 거겠네요!\""]], [["‘You didn’t mind it?’ cried the collector.", "\"당신은 괜찮았소?\" 수금원이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Mind it!’ repeated Mrs.", "\"괜찮냐고요!\""], ["Lillyvick contemptuously.", "릴리빅 부인이 경멸하듯 되받았다."], ["‘You ought to go down on your knees and beg everybody’s pardon, that you ought.’", "\"당신이야말로 무릎 꿇고 모두한테 용서를 빌어야 해요, 그래야 한다고요.\""]], [["‘Pardon, my dear?’ said the dismayed collector.", "\"용서를, 여보?\" 당황한 수금원이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, and mine first,’ replied Mrs. Lillyvick.", "\"그래요, 나한테 먼저요.\""], ["‘Do you suppose I ain’t the best judge of what’s proper and what’s improper?’", "릴리빅 부인이 대답했다. \"내가 뭐가 옳고 뭐가 그른지 제일 잘 판단할 줄 모른다고 생각해요?\""]], [["‘To be sure,’ cried all the ladies.", "\"그럼요.\" 부인들이 다 같이 외쳤다."], ["‘Do you suppose WE shouldn’t be the first to speak, if there was anything that ought to be taken notice of?’", "\"뭔가 짚고 넘어가야 할 일이 있었다면 저희가 제일 먼저 말했을 거 아니에요?\""]], [["‘Do you suppose THEY don’t know, sir?’ said Miss Snevellicci’s papa, pulling up his collar, and muttering something about a punching of heads, and being only withheld by considerations of age.", "\"저 사람들이 모를 줄 아시오, 선생?\" 스네벨리치 양의 아빠가 옷깃을 추켜올리며 말했다."], ["With which Miss Snevellicci’s papa looked steadily and sternly at Mr.", "그러고는 머리통을 한 대 갈겨 주겠다는 둥, 나이를 생각해서 참는 거라는 둥 뭐라고 중얼거렸다."], ["Lillyvick for some seconds, and then rising deliberately from his chair, kissed the ladies all round, beginning with Mrs.", "그러면서 스네벨리치 양의 아빠는 몇 초 동안 릴리빅 씨를 똑바로 매섭게 노려보더니, 천천히 의자에서 일어나 부인들에게 빙 돌아가며 입을 맞췄다."], ["Lillyvick.", "릴리빅 부인부터 시작해서 말이다."]], [["The unhappy collector looked piteously at his wife, as if to see whether there was any one trait of Miss Petowker left in Mrs.", "불행한 수금원은 가엾은 눈빛으로 아내를 바라보았다. 릴리빅 부인 속에 페토커 양의 모습이 한 가닥이라도 남아 있는지 살피려는 듯이 말이다."], ["Lillyvick, and finding too surely that there was not, begged pardon of all the company with great humility, and sat down such a crest-fallen, dispirited, disenchanted man, that despite all his selfishness and dotage, he was quite an object of compassion.", "그러나 그런 게 전혀 남아 있지 않다는 걸 너무도 확실히 깨닫고는, 아주 겸손하게 좌중 모두에게 용서를 빌었다. 그러고는 어찌나 기가 죽고 풀이 죽고 환상이 깨진 사람처럼 주저앉던지, 그 온갖 이기심과 노망에도 불구하고 정말이지 가엾기 짝이 없는 신세가 되고 말았다."]], [["Miss Snevellicci’s papa being greatly exalted by this triumph, and incontestable proof of his popularity with the fair sex, quickly grew convivial, not to say uproarious; volunteering more than one song of no inconsiderable length, and regaling the social circle between-whiles with recollections of divers splendid women who had been supposed to entertain a passion for himself, several of whom he toasted by name, taking occasion to remark at the same time that if he had been a little more alive to his own interest, he might have been rolling at that moment in his chariot-and-four. These reminiscences appeared to awaken no very torturing pangs in the breast of Mrs. Snevellicci, who was sufficiently occupied in descanting to Nicholas upon the manifold accomplishments and merits of her daughter. Nor was the young lady herself at all behind-hand in displaying her choicest allurements; but these, heightened as they were by the artifices of Miss Ledrook, had no effect whatever in increasing the attentions of Nicholas, who, with the precedent of Miss Squeers still fresh in his memory, steadily resisted every fascination, and placed so strict a guard upon his behaviour that when he had taken his leave the ladies were unanimous in pronouncing him quite a monster of insensibility.", "스네벨리치 양의 아빠는 이 승리에, 그리고 이성에게 인기 있다는 반박할 수 없는 증거에 한껏 우쭐해져서, 금세 흥겨워졌다 — 아니, 떠들썩해졌다고 해야겠다. 자청해서 결코 짧지 않은 노래를 한 곡 이상 불렀다. 그리고 그 틈틈이 자기한테 연정을 품었던 걸로 짐작되는 갖가지 눈부신 여인들의 추억으로 좌중을 즐겁게 했다. 그중 몇몇은 이름까지 대며 건배했고, 그러는 김에 한마디 했다. 자기가 제 잇속을 좀 더 챙겼더라면 지금쯤 사두마차를 타고 굴러다니고 있었을 거라고 말이다. 이런 회상들도 스네벨리치 부인의 가슴엔 그리 쓰라린 아픔을 일으키지 못하는 듯했다. 부인은 니콜라스에게 제 딸의 갖가지 재주와 장점을 늘어놓느라 충분히 바빴거든. 젊은 아가씨 본인도 자기가 가진 최고의 매력을 펼쳐 보이는 데 전혀 뒤지지 않았다. 하지만 이런 매력들은, 레드룩 양의 갖은 꾀로 한껏 부추겨졌는데도, 니콜라스의 관심을 끄는 데는 조금도 효과가 없었다. 니콜라스는 스퀴어스 양의 일이 아직 생생하게 기억에 남아 있어서, 어떤 유혹에도 한결같이 버텼다. 그리고 자기 행동을 어찌나 엄격하게 단속했던지, 그가 작별 인사를 하고 떠나자 부인들은 입을 모아 그를 무감각의 화신 같은 사람이라고 평했다."]], [["Next day the posters appeared in due course, and the public were informed, in all the colours of the rainbow, and in letters afflicted with every possible variation of spinal deformity, how that Mr.", "이튿날 포스터가 차례대로 나붙었고, 대중은 무지개 온갖 빛깔로, 그리고 가능한 온갖 모양으로 척추가 굽은 듯 비뚤배뚤한 글자로 이런 소식을 접했다."], ["Johnson would have the honour of making his last appearance that evening, and how that an early application for places was requested, in consequence of the extraordinary overflow attendant on his performances,--it being a remarkable fact in theatrical history, but one long since established beyond dispute, that it is a hopeless endeavour to attract people to a theatre unless they can be first brought to believe that they will never get into it.", "존슨 씨가 오늘 저녁 영광스럽게도 마지막 무대에 선다는 것, 그리고 그의 공연마다 따라붙는 엄청난 인파 때문에 일찌감치 자리를 예약하시기를 부탁드린다는 것 말이다. 연극사에서 주목할 만한 사실이자 오래전부터 이론의 여지 없이 확립된 사실이 하나 있는데, 사람들을 극장으로 끌어들이려면 먼저 그들이 도저히 자리를 못 구할 거라고 믿게 만들지 않고서는 가망이 없다는 거다."]], [["Nicholas was somewhat at a loss, on entering the theatre at night, to account for the unusual perturbation and excitement visible in the countenances of all the company, but he was not long in doubt as to the cause, for before he could make any inquiry respecting it Mr.", "니콜라스는 밤에 극장에 들어서면서, 극단 사람들 모두의 얼굴에 보이는 평소답지 않은 동요와 흥분을 무엇 때문인지 도통 알 수가 없었다. 하지만 그 까닭을 오래 의아해하진 않았다."], ["Crummles approached, and in an agitated tone of voice, informed him that there was a London manager in the boxes.", "그가 그 일에 대해 뭐라 물어보기도 전에 크럼블스 씨가 다가와, 흥분한 목소리로 박스석에 런던 극장 단장이 와 있다고 알려 줬거든."]], [["‘It’s the phenomenon, depend upon it, sir,’ said Crummles, dragging Nicholas to the little hole in the curtain that he might look through at the London manager.", "\"신동 때문이오, 틀림없어요, 선생.\" 크럼블스 씨가 니콜라스를 막에 난 작은 구멍으로 끌고 가서, 그리로 런던 단장을 엿볼 수 있게 해 주며 말했다."], ["‘I have not the smallest doubt it’s the fame of the phenomenon--that’s the man; him in the great-coat and no shirt-collar.", "\"신동의 명성 때문인 게 조금도 의심할 여지가 없소—저 사람이오."], ["She shall have ten pound a week, Johnson; she shall not appear on the London boards for a farthing less.", "저기 외투 입고 셔츠 깃 없는 사람 말이오. 저 애한테 주급 십 파운드를 줘야 할 거요, 존슨."], ["They shan’t engage her either, unless they engage Mrs.", "한 푼이라도 덜 받고는 런던 무대에 안 세울 거요."], ["Crummles too--twenty pound a week for the pair; or I’ll tell you what, I’ll throw in myself and the two boys, and they shall have the family for thirty.", "크럼블스 부인도 같이 데려가지 않으면 저 애도 못 데려가게 할 거고—둘이서 주급 이십 파운드. 아니면 이렇게 합시다, 나하고 두 아들까지 넣어서 온 가족을 삼십 파운드에 주겠소."], ["I can’t say fairer than that.", "이보다 더 공정하게는 못 하지."], ["They must take us all, if none of us will go without the others.", "우리 중 누구도 다른 식구들 없이는 안 가니까, 데려가려면 우릴 다 데려가야 하오."], ["That’s the way some of the London people do, and it always answers.", "런던 사람들 중에 이렇게 하는 이들이 있는데, 늘 통하거든."], ["Thirty pound a week--it’s too cheap, Johnson.", "주급 삼십 파운드—너무 싸지, 존슨."], ["It’s dirt cheap.’", "거저나 다름없소.\""]], [["Nicholas replied, that it certainly was; and Mr.", "니콜라스는 정말 그렇다고 대답했다."], ["Vincent Crummles taking several huge pinches of snuff to compose his feelings, hurried away to tell Mrs.", "그러자 빈센트 크럼블스 씨는 마음을 가라앉히려고 코담배를 큼지막하게 몇 번 들이마시더니, 부랴부랴 크럼블스 부인한테 가서 알렸다."], ["Crummles that he had quite settled the only terms that could be accepted, and had resolved not to abate one single farthing.", "받아들일 수 있는 유일한 조건을 완전히 정해 놓았고, 한 푼도 깎지 않기로 결심했다고 말이다."]], [["When everybody was dressed and the curtain went up, the excitement occasioned by the presence of the London manager increased a thousand-fold.", "다들 분장을 마치고 막이 오르자, 런던 단장이 와 있다는 데서 비롯된 흥분이 천 배로 커졌다."], ["Everybody happened to know that the London manager had come down specially to witness his or her own performance, and all were in a flutter of anxiety and expectation.", "다들 어쩐 일인지 런던 단장이 다름 아닌 바로 자기 연기를 보러 일부러 내려온 거라고 여겼고, 모두가 불안과 기대로 안절부절못했다."], ["Some of those who were not on in the first scene, hurried to the wings, and there stretched their necks to have a peep at him; others stole up into the two little private boxes over the stage-doors, and from that position reconnoitred the London manager.", "첫 장면에 안 나오는 몇몇은 무대 옆으로 달려가 목을 길게 빼고 그를 엿보았다. 또 어떤 이들은 무대 출입문 위쪽에 있는 자그마한 개인 박스석 두 개로 살그머니 올라가, 거기서 런던 단장을 정탐했다."], ["Once the London manager was seen to smile--he smiled at the comic countryman’s pretending to catch a blue-bottle, while Mrs Crummles was making her greatest effect.", "한번은 런던 단장이 미소 짓는 게 보였다—크럼블스 부인이 한창 최고의 연기를 펼치고 있을 때, 익살꾼 시골뜨기가 쉬파리를 잡는 시늉을 하는 걸 보고 웃은 거였다."], ["‘Very good, my fine fellow,’ said Mr.", "\"잘한다, 이 친구야.\""], ["Crummles, shaking his fist at the comic countryman when he came off, ‘you leave this company next Saturday night.’", "크럼블스 씨가 그 익살꾼 시골뜨기가 무대에서 내려오자 주먹을 흔들며 말했다. \"자넨 다음 토요일 밤에 이 극단을 떠나게.\""]], [["In the same way, everybody who was on the stage beheld no audience but one individual; everybody played to the London manager.", "마찬가지로, 무대에 선 사람은 누구나 단 한 사람 말고는 관객이 보이지 않았다."], ["When Mr.", "다들 런던 단장을 향해 연기했다."], ["Lenville in a sudden burst of passion called the emperor a miscreant, and then biting his glove, said, ‘But I must dissemble,’ instead of looking gloomily at the boards and so waiting for his cue, as is proper in such cases, he kept his eye fixed upon the London manager.", "렌빌 씨가 갑자기 격분해서 황제더러 악당이라고 외치고는, 장갑을 깨물며 \"하지만 나는 속내를 감춰야 해\" 하고 말할 때, 이런 경우에 마땅히 그래야 하듯 침울하게 무대 바닥을 바라보며 자기 차례를 기다리는 대신, 그는 런던 단장한테 눈을 딱 박고 있었다."], ["When Miss Bravassa sang her song at her lover, who according to custom stood ready to shake hands with her between the verses, they looked, not at each other, but at the London manager. Mr.", "브라바사 양이 연인을 향해 노래를 부를 때, 그 연인은 관례대로 절과 절 사이에 그녀와 악수하려고 대기하고 있었는데, 두 사람은 서로를 보는 게 아니라 런던 단장을 보고 있었다."], ["Crummles died point blank at him; and when the two guards came in to take the body off after a very hard death, it was seen to open its eyes and glance at the London manager.", "크럼블스 씨는 그를 정면으로 바라보며 죽었다. 그리고 위병 둘이 아주 처절한 죽음 끝에 시신을 옮기러 들어왔을 때, 그 시신이 눈을 뜨고 런던 단장을 흘끔거리는 게 보였다."], ["At length the London manager was discovered to be asleep, and shortly after that he woke up and went away, whereupon all the company fell foul of the unhappy comic countryman, declaring that his buffoonery was the sole cause; and Mr.", "마침내 런던 단장이 잠들어 있는 게 발각되었다. 그러고는 얼마 안 가 그가 깨어나 가 버렸다."], ["Crummles said, that he had put up with it a long time, but that he really couldn’t stand it any longer, and therefore would feel obliged by his looking out for another engagement.", "그러자 극단 사람들이 다 그 불운한 익살꾼 시골뜨기한테 달려들어, 그의 어릿광대짓이 유일한 원인이라고 우겨댔다. 크럼블스 씨는 자기가 오랫동안 참아 왔지만 정말 더는 못 견디겠다며, 그러니 다른 일자리나 알아봐 주면 고맙겠다고 했다."]], [["All this was the occasion of much amusement to Nicholas, whose only feeling upon the subject was one of sincere satisfaction that the great man went away before he appeared.", "이 모든 게 니콜라스한테는 큰 웃음거리였다. 이 일에 대한 그의 유일한 감정은, 그 대단한 양반이 자기가 등장하기 전에 가 버렸다는 데서 오는 진심 어린 만족뿐이었거든."], ["He went through his part in the two last pieces as briskly as he could, and having been received with unbounded favour and unprecedented applause--so said the bills for next day, which had been printed an hour or two before--he took Smike’s arm and walked home to bed.", "그는 마지막 두 작품에서 제 역을 최대한 활기차게 해냈다. 그러고는 한없는 호평과 전례 없는 박수를 받고서—한두 시간 전에 이미 찍어 놓은 다음 날 포스터에 그렇게 적혀 있었거든—스마이크의 팔을 잡고 집으로 걸어가 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["With the post next morning came a letter from Newman Noggs, very inky, very short, very dirty, very small, and very mysterious, urging Nicholas to return to London instantly; not to lose an instant; to be there that night if possible.", "이튿날 아침 우편으로 뉴먼 노그스한테서 편지 한 통이 왔다. 잉크 자국투성이에, 아주 짧고, 아주 더럽고, 아주 작고, 아주 알쏭달쏭한 편지였다. 니콜라스더러 당장 런던으로 돌아오라고, 한순간도 지체하지 말라고, 되도록이면 그날 밤에 와 달라고 다그치는 내용이었다."]], [["‘I will,’ said Nicholas.", "\"가야지.\" 니콜라스가 말했다."], ["‘Heaven knows I have remained here for the best, and sorely against my own will; but even now I may have dallied too long.", "\"하늘은 아시겠지, 내가 잘되라고, 내 뜻과는 정말 어긋나게 여기 남아 있었다는 걸. 하지만 지금도 너무 오래 꾸물거렸는지 몰라."], ["What can have happened?", "무슨 일이 생긴 걸까?"], ["Smike, my good fellow, here--take my purse.", "스마이크, 이봐—내 지갑 받아."], ["Put our things together, and pay what little debts we owe--quick, and we shall be in time for the morning coach.", "우리 짐 챙기고, 우리가 진 자잘한 빚 좀 갚아—얼른, 그래야 아침 마차를 탈 수 있어."], ["I will only tell them that we are going, and will return to you immediately.’", "난 그저 우리가 떠난다고 사람들한테 말하고, 곧장 너한테 돌아올게.\""]], [["So saying, he took his hat, and hurrying away to the lodgings of Mr Crummles, applied his hand to the knocker with such hearty good-will, that he awakened that gentleman, who was still in bed, and caused Mr Bulph the pilot to take his morning’s pipe very nearly out of his mouth in the extremity of his surprise.", "그렇게 말하며 그는 모자를 집어 들고, 크럼블스 씨의 숙소로 부랴부랴 달려가, 어찌나 우렁차게 노크를 했던지, 아직 침대에 누워 있던 그 양반을 깨우고 말았다. 그리고 도선사 벌프 씨가 어찌나 놀랐던지 아침 담뱃대를 입에서 거의 떨어뜨릴 뻔했다."]], [["The door being opened, Nicholas ran upstairs without any ceremony, and bursting into the darkened sitting-room on the one-pair front, found that the two Master Crummleses had sprung out of the sofa-bedstead and were putting on their clothes with great rapidity, under the impression that it was the middle of the night, and the next house was on fire.", "문이 열리자, 니콜라스는 격식이고 뭐고 없이 위층으로 뛰어 올라가, 이층 앞쪽의 어두컴컴한 거실로 불쑥 들어갔다. 그랬더니 두 크럼블스 도련님이 소파 겸 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 잽싸게 옷을 입고 있는 게 아닌가. 한밤중인데 옆집에 불이 난 줄로 안 거였다."]], [["Before he could undeceive them, Mr.", "니콜라스가 미처 오해를 풀어 주기도 전에, 크럼블스 씨가 플란넬 가운에 잠자리 모자 차림으로 내려왔다."], ["Crummles came down in a flannel gown and nightcap; and to him Nicholas briefly explained that circumstances had occurred which rendered it necessary for him to repair to London immediately.", "니콜라스는 그에게 사정이 생겨서 당장 런던으로 가야 한다고 간단히 설명했다."]], [["‘So goodbye,’ said Nicholas; ‘goodbye, goodbye.’", "\"그럼 안녕히 계세요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다. \"안녕히, 안녕히 계세요.\""]], [["He was half-way downstairs before Mr. Crummles had sufficiently recovered his surprise to gasp out something about the posters.", "크럼블스 씨가 놀란 마음을 가라앉히고 포스터에 대해 뭐라 겨우 헐떡이며 말을 꺼낼 무렵, 니콜라스는 벌써 계단을 반쯤 내려가 있었다."]], [["‘I can’t help it,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"어쩔 수 없죠.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다."], ["‘Set whatever I may have earned this week against them, or if that will not repay you, say at once what will.", "\"이번 주에 제가 번 게 있으면 그걸로 포스터 값을 치셔도 되고, 그걸로 모자라면 얼마면 되는지 바로 말씀하세요."], ["Quick, quick.’", "얼른요, 얼른.\""]], [["‘We’ll cry quits about that,’ returned Crummles.", "\"그건 그냥 없던 걸로 합시다.\" 크럼블스 씨가 대답했다."], ["‘But can’t we have one last night more?’", "\"하지만 마지막으로 딱 하룻밤만 더 못 하겠나?\""]], [["‘Not an hour--not a minute,’ replied Nicholas, impatiently.", "\"한 시간도—일 분도 안 돼요.\" 니콜라스가 안타까운 듯 대답했다."]], [["‘Won’t you stop to say something to Mrs.", "\"크럼블스 부인한테 한마디 하고 가지 않겠나?\""], ["Crummles?’ asked the manager, following him down to the door.", "단장이 그를 따라 문까지 내려오며 물었다."]], [["‘I couldn’t stop if it were to prolong my life a score of years,’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"제 목숨이 스무 해는 늘어난다 해도 멈출 수 없어요.\" 니콜라스가 받아쳤다."], ["‘Here, take my hand, and with it my hearty thanks.--Oh!", "\"자, 제 손을 잡으세요. 그리고 이 손과 함께 제 진심 어린 감사도요.—오!"], ["that I should have been fooling here!’", "내가 여기서 빈둥거리고 있었다니!\""]], [["Accompanying these words with an impatient stamp upon the ground, he tore himself from the manager’s detaining grasp, and darting rapidly down the street was out of sight in an instant.", "이 말과 함께 안타까운 듯 땅을 쿵 구르고는, 그는 자기를 붙잡으려는 단장의 손에서 몸을 뿌리쳤다. 그러고는 거리를 쏜살같이 내달려 순식간에 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["‘Dear me, dear me,’ said Mr.", "\"이런, 이런.\""], ["Crummles, looking wistfully towards the point at which he had just disappeared; ‘if he only acted like that, what a deal of money he’d draw!", "크럼블스 씨가 그가 방금 사라진 지점을 아쉬운 듯 바라보며 말했다. \"저 친구가 무대에서도 저렇게만 연기했으면 돈을 얼마나 끌어모았을까!"], ["He should have kept upon this circuit; he’d have been very useful to me.", "이 순회 공연단에 계속 붙어 있었어야 했는데. 나한테 아주 쓸모가 많았을 텐데 말이야."], ["But he don’t know what’s good for him.", "그런데 저 친구는 제 앞길에 뭐가 좋은지를 몰라."], ["He is an impetuous youth.", "성마른 젊은이지."], ["Young men are rash, very rash.’", "젊은 것들은 경솔하다니까, 아주 경솔해.\""]], [["Mr.", "크럼블스 씨는 이렇게 교훈을 늘어놓을 기분이었던지라, 무심코 손을 조끼 주머니 쪽으로 가져가지 않았더라면 몇 분은 더 교훈을 읊었을지도 모른다."], ["Crummles being in a moralising mood, might possibly have moralised for some minutes longer if he had not mechanically put his hand towards his waistcoat pocket, where he was accustomed to keep his snuff.", "평소 그가 코담배를 넣어 두던 자리 말이다. 그런데 늘 있던 자리에 주머니가 아예 없자, 문득 자기가 조끼를 안 입고 있다는 사실이 떠올랐다."], ["The absence of any pocket at all in the usual direction, suddenly recalled to his recollection the fact that he had no waistcoat on; and this leading him to a contemplation of the extreme scantiness of his attire, he shut the door abruptly, and retired upstairs with great precipitation.", "그러자 자기 옷차림이 너무 부실하다는 데까지 생각이 미쳐, 그는 문을 탁 닫고는 부랴부랴 위층으로 물러갔다."]], [["Smike had made good speed while Nicholas was absent, and with his help everything was soon ready for their departure.", "스마이크는 니콜라스가 없는 사이에 일을 부지런히 해 둬서, 그의 도움으로 떠날 채비가 금세 다 갖춰졌다."], ["They scarcely stopped to take a morsel of breakfast, and in less than half an hour arrived at the coach-office: quite out of breath with the haste they had made to reach it in time.", "두 사람은 아침을 한 입 먹을 새도 거의 없이, 반 시간이 채 안 되어 마차 사무소에 다다랐다. 제때 도착하려고 어찌나 서둘렀던지 둘 다 숨이 턱에 차 있었다."], ["There were yet a few minutes to spare, so, having secured the places, Nicholas hurried into a slopseller’s hard by, and bought Smike a great-coat.", "아직 몇 분 여유가 있어서, 니콜라스는 자리를 잡아 두고는 바로 옆 헌옷 가게로 부랴부랴 들어가 스마이크한테 외투를 하나 사 줬다."], ["It would have been rather large for a substantial yeoman, but the shopman averring (and with considerable truth) that it was a most uncommon fit, Nicholas would have purchased it in his impatience if it had been twice the size.", "덩치 좋은 자영농한테도 좀 클 만한 옷이었다. 하지만 점원이 (제법 진심을 담아) 이만한 맞춤은 드물다고 우겨대니, 니콜라스는 마음이 급한 나머지 그게 두 배 크기였더라도 샀을 거다."]], [["As they hurried up to the coach, which was now in the open street and all ready for starting, Nicholas was not a little astonished to find himself suddenly clutched in a close and violent embrace, which nearly took him off his legs; nor was his amazement at all lessened by hearing the voice of Mr.", "이제 큰길에 나와 출발 준비를 다 마친 마차로 두 사람이 부랴부랴 다가가는데, 니콜라스는 적잖이 놀라고 말았다. 갑자기 어떤 사람이 꽉 끌어안는 바람에 거의 다리가 휘청할 뻔했거든."], ["Crummles exclaim, ‘It is he--my friend, my friend!’", "그리고 그 놀라움은 크럼블스 씨가 외치는 목소리에 조금도 누그러지지 않았다. \"이 사람이야—내 친구, 내 친구라고!\""]], [["‘Bless my heart,’ cried Nicholas, struggling in the manager’s arms, ‘what are you about?’", "\"세상에.\" 니콜라스가 단장의 품에서 버둥거리며 외쳤다. \"지금 뭐 하시는 거예요?\""]], [["The manager made no reply, but strained him to his breast again, exclaiming as he did so, ‘Farewell, my noble, my lion-hearted boy!’", "단장은 아무 대꾸도 없이 그를 다시 가슴에 꽉 끌어안고는, 그러면서 외쳤다. \"잘 가게, 내 고귀하고 사자처럼 용맹한 젊은이여!\""]], [["In fact, Mr.", "사실 크럼블스 씨는 직업적인 과시의 기회라면 결코 놓치는 법이 없는 사람이라, 일부러 니콜라스와 공개적인 작별을 하려고 나온 거였다."], ["Crummles, who could never lose any opportunity for professional display, had turned out for the express purpose of taking a public farewell of Nicholas; and to render it the more imposing, he was now, to that young gentleman’s most profound annoyance, inflicting upon him a rapid succession of stage embraces, which, as everybody knows, are performed by the embracer’s laying his or her chin on the shoulder of the object of affection, and looking over it.", "그리고 그걸 더 그럴듯하게 만들려고, 이제 그 젊은이가 더없이 질색하는데도, 무대식 포옹을 잇달아 퍼붓고 있었다. 다들 알다시피 무대식 포옹이란 안는 사람이 제 턱을 사랑하는 상대의 어깨에 얹고 그 너머를 바라보는 식이다."], ["This Mr. Crummles did in the highest style of melodrama, pouring forth at the same time all the most dismal forms of farewell he could think of, out of the stock pieces.", "크럼블스 씨는 이걸 최고로 멜로드라마틱한 양식으로 해냈고, 그러면서 동시에 상비 작품들에서 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 구슬픈 작별의 말이란 말은 죄다 쏟아 냈다."], ["Nor was this all, for the elder Master Crummles was going through a similar ceremony with Smike; while Master Percy Crummles, with a very little second-hand camlet cloak, worn theatrically over his left shoulder, stood by, in the attitude of an attendant officer, waiting to convey the two victims to the scaffold.", "이게 다가 아니었다. 큰 크럼블스 도련님이 스마이크한테 똑같은 의식을 치르고 있었고, 한편 작은 퍼시 크럼블스 도련님은 아주 낡은 헌 낙타 모직 망토를 무대처럼 왼쪽 어깨에 걸치고는, 두 희생자를 처형대로 데려가려고 대기하는 부관 같은 자세로 그 옆에 서 있었거든."]], [["The lookers-on laughed very heartily, and as it was as well to put a good face upon the matter, Nicholas laughed too when he had succeeded in disengaging himself; and rescuing the astonished Smike, climbed up to the coach roof after him, and kissed his hand in honour of the absent Mrs.", "구경꾼들이 한바탕 신나게 웃어댔다. 그러니 좋게좋게 넘기는 편이 낫겠다 싶어, 니콜라스도 간신히 몸을 빼내고 나서는 따라 웃었다."], ["Crummles as they rolled away.", "그러고는 어리둥절해하는 스마이크를 구해내, 그를 뒤따라 마차 지붕으로 기어 올라갔다. 그리고 마차가 굴러가기 시작하자, 자리에 없는 크럼블스 부인을 기리며 손에 입을 맞춰 보였다."]], [["# CHAPTER 31", "# CHAPTER 31"]], [["Of Ralph Nickleby and Newman Noggs, and some wise Precautions, the success or failure of which will appear in the Sequel\nIn blissful unconsciousness that his nephew was hastening at the utmost speed of four good horses towards his sphere of action, and that every passing minute diminished the distance between them, Ralph Nickleby sat that morning occupied in his customary avocations, and yet unable to prevent his thoughts wandering from time to time back to the interview which had taken place between himself and his niece on the previous day. At such intervals, after a few moments of abstraction, Ralph would mutter some peevish interjection, and apply himself with renewed steadiness of purpose to the ledger before him, but again and again the same train of thought came back despite all his efforts to prevent it, confusing him in his calculations, and utterly distracting his attention from the figures over which he bent. At length Ralph laid down his pen, and threw himself back in his chair as though he had made up his mind to allow the obtrusive current of reflection to take its own course, and, by giving it full scope, to rid himself of it effectually.", "랄프 니클비는 까맣게 모르고 있었어. 자기 조카가 좋은 말 네 마리가 낼 수 있는 최고 속도로 자신의 활동 무대를 향해 달려오고 있다는 걸, 그리고 시간이 한 순간씩 지날 때마다 둘 사이의 거리가 점점 줄어들고 있다는 걸 말이야. 그날 아침 랄프는 늘 하던 일에 매달려 앉아 있었어. 그런데도 자꾸만 생각이 어제 조카딸과 나눈 그 만남으로 되돌아가는 걸 막을 수가 없었지. 그렇게 잠깐씩 멍하니 있다가 정신을 차리면, 랄프는 짜증스러운 소리를 한마디 중얼거리고는 다시 마음을 다잡아 앞에 놓인 장부에 매달렸어. 하지만 아무리 애를 써도 그 똑같은 생각의 줄기가 자꾸자꾸 되돌아왔지. 그러면 계산이 헝클어지고, 굽어보고 있던 숫자에서 정신이 온통 흩어졌어. 마침내 랄프는 펜을 내려놓고 의자에 등을 기대며 몸을 뒤로 젖혔어. 끈질기게 밀려드는 그 생각의 물줄기를 그냥 흐르게 내버려 두기로, 마음껏 흐르게 풀어 줘서 아예 깨끗이 떨쳐 버리기로 작정한 것 같았지."]], [["‘I am not a man to be moved by a pretty face,’ muttered Ralph sternly.", "\"나는 예쁜 얼굴 하나에 마음이 흔들릴 사람이 아니야.\" 랄프가 매섭게 중얼거렸어."], ["‘There is a grinning skull beneath it, and men like me who look and work below the surface see that, and not its delicate covering.", "\"그 얼굴 밑에는 이를 드러내고 웃는 해골이 있지. 나처럼 겉이 아니라 속을 들여다보고 일하는 사람은 그걸 봐."], ["And yet I almost like the girl, or should if she had been less proudly and squeamishly brought up.", "곱고 얇은 껍데기가 아니라 말이야. 그래도 그 애가 거의 마음에 들긴 해."], ["If the boy were drowned or hanged, and the mother dead, this house should be her home.", "아니, 그렇게 도도하고 까탈스럽게 자라지만 않았더라면 정말 마음에 들었을 거야. 그 녀석이 물에 빠지거나 목이라도 매달려 죽고, 어미마저 죽어 버린다면, 이 집이 그 애 보금자리가 될 텐데."], ["I wish they were, with all my soul.’", "진심으로 그랬으면 좋겠어, 온 마음을 다해서.\""]], [["Notwithstanding the deadly hatred which Ralph felt towards Nicholas, and the bitter contempt with which he sneered at poor Mrs Nickleby--notwithstanding the baseness with which he had behaved, and was then behaving, and would behave again if his interest prompted him, towards Kate herself--still there was, strange though it may seem, something humanising and even gentle in his thoughts at that moment. He thought of what his home might be if Kate were there; he placed her in the empty chair, looked upon her, heard her speak; he felt again upon his arm the gentle pressure of the trembling hand; he strewed his costly rooms with the hundred silent tokens of feminine presence and occupation; he came back again to the cold fireside and the silent dreary splendour; and in that one glimpse of a better nature, born as it was in selfish thoughts, the rich man felt himself friendless, childless, and alone. Gold, for the instant, lost its lustre in his eyes, for there were countless treasures of the heart which it could never purchase.", "랄프가 니콜라스에게 품은 그 지독한 증오에도 불구하고, 가엾은 니클비 부인을 비웃으며 쏟아 내던 그 모진 멸시에도 불구하고 — 그리고 케이트 자신에게 이제껏 보여 온, 또 지금도 보이고 있고, 제 잇속이 부추기면 앞으로도 또 보일 그 비열한 행동에도 불구하고 — 이상하게 들릴지 몰라도, 그 순간만큼은 그의 생각 속에 뭔가 사람다운, 심지어 부드러운 구석이 있었어. 그는 케이트가 곁에 있다면 자기 집이 어떤 모습일지 떠올려 봤어. 비어 있는 의자에 그 애를 앉혀 놓고, 그 애를 바라보고, 그 애가 말하는 걸 들었지. 떨리던 그 작은 손이 자기 팔을 부드럽게 누르던 느낌을 다시 느꼈어. 값비싼 방마다 여자가 머물며 일하는 자취가 묻은 자잘한 흔적들을 말없이 흩뿌려 놓았지. 그러다 다시 차가운 난롯가로, 적막하고 쓸쓸한 그 호화로움으로 돌아왔어. 비록 그게 이기적인 생각에서 태어난 것이긴 했지만, 더 나은 본성이 언뜻 비친 그 한순간에, 이 부자는 자기가 친구도 없고 자식도 없이 혼자라는 걸 느꼈어. 그 순간만큼은 황금이 그의 눈에서 빛을 잃었지. 황금으로는 결코 살 수 없는 마음의 보물이 셀 수 없이 많았으니까."]], [["A very slight circumstance was sufficient to banish such reflections from the mind of such a man.", "이런 사람의 머릿속에서 이런 생각들을 몰아내는 데는 아주 사소한 일 하나면 충분했어. 랄프가 마당 너머 건너편 사무실 창문 쪽을 멍하니 바라보다가, 문득 뉴먼 노그스가 자기를 골똘히 지켜보고 있다는 걸 알아챘거든."], ["As Ralph looked vacantly out across the yard towards the window of the other office, he became suddenly aware of the earnest observation of Newman Noggs, who, with his red nose almost touching the glass, feigned to be mending a pen with a rusty fragment of a knife, but was in reality staring at his employer with a countenance of the closest and most eager scrutiny.", "뉴먼은 빨간 코가 유리에 거의 닿을 듯이 붙은 채, 녹슨 칼 조각으로 펜을 다듬는 척하고 있었어. 그런데 실은 더없이 빈틈없고 열심인 눈빛으로 자기 주인을 빤히 쳐다보고 있었지."]], [["Ralph exchanged his dreamy posture for his accustomed business attitude: the face of Newman disappeared, and the train of thought took to flight, all simultaneously, and in an instant.", "랄프는 멍하니 있던 자세를 평소의 일하는 자세로 바꿨어. 뉴먼의 얼굴이 사라지고, 생각의 줄기도 달아나 버렸지. 죄다 동시에, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 말이야."]], [["After a few minutes, Ralph rang his bell.", "몇 분 뒤, 랄프가 종을 울렸어."], ["Newman answered the summons, and Ralph raised his eyes stealthily to his face, as if he almost feared to read there, a knowledge of his recent thoughts.", "뉴먼이 부름에 답해 들어오자, 랄프는 슬그머니 눈을 들어 그의 얼굴을 봤어. 마치 거기서 자기가 방금 한 생각을 들켰을까 봐 두려워하는 것처럼 말이야."]], [["There was not the smallest speculation, however, in the countenance of Newman Noggs.", "하지만 뉴먼 노그스의 얼굴에는 무슨 속내라곤 눈곱만큼도 보이지 않았어."], ["If it be possible to imagine a man, with two eyes in his head, and both wide open, looking in no direction whatever, and seeing nothing, Newman appeared to be that man while Ralph Nickleby regarded him.", "머리에 눈이 두 개나 달려 있고, 그 두 눈을 다 활짝 뜨고 있으면서도, 아무 데도 안 보고 아무것도 안 보는 사람을 떠올릴 수 있다면 말이야, 랄프 니클비가 쳐다보는 동안 뉴먼이 바로 그런 사람으로 보였어."]], [["‘How now?’ growled Ralph.", "\"무슨 일이야?\" 랄프가 으르렁거렸어."]], [["‘Oh!’ said Newman, throwing some intelligence into his eyes all at once, and dropping them on his master, ‘I thought you rang.’ With which laconic remark Newman turned round and hobbled away.", "\"아!\" 뉴먼이 갑자기 눈에 생기를 한껏 채워 넣더니, 그 눈을 주인에게 떨어뜨리며 말했어. \"종을 울리신 줄 알았어요.\" 이 짤막한 말만 던지고는 뉴먼이 몸을 돌려 절뚝거리며 가 버렸어."]], [["‘Stop!’ said Ralph.", "\"멈춰!\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["Newman stopped; not at all disconcerted.", "뉴먼이 멈춰 섰어. 조금도 당황하는 기색이 없었지."]], [["‘I did ring.’", "\"종을 울린 게 맞아.\""]], [["‘I knew you did.’", "\"그러신 줄 알았어요.\""]], [["‘Then why do you offer to go if you know that?’", "\"그런데 그걸 알면서 왜 가려고 하는 거야?\""]], [["‘I thought you rang to say you didn’t ring,’ replied Newman.", "\"종을 안 울렸다고 말씀하시려고 종을 울리신 줄 알았죠.\" 뉴먼이 대꾸했어."], ["‘You often do.’", "\"곧잘 그러시잖아요.\""]], [["‘How dare you pry, and peer, and stare at me, sirrah?’ demanded Ralph.", "\"네놈이 어디서 감히 캐고, 들여다보고, 나를 빤히 쳐다봐, 이놈아?\" 랄프가 따졌어."]], [["‘Stare!’ cried Newman, ‘at YOU! Ha, ha!’ which was all the explanation Newman deigned to offer.", "\"쳐다보다뇨!\" 뉴먼이 외쳤어. \"당신을요! 하, 하!\" 뉴먼이 굳이 내놓은 해명이라곤 이게 다였어."]], [["‘Be careful, sir,’ said Ralph, looking steadily at him.", "\"조심해, 이 사람아.\" 랄프가 그를 똑바로 노려보며 말했어."], ["‘Let me have no drunken fooling here.", "\"여기서 술 취해 헛짓거리하는 꼴은 못 봐."], ["Do you see this parcel?’", "이 꾸러미 보이지?\""]], [["‘It’s big enough,’ rejoined Newman.", "\"꽤 크긴 하네요.\" 뉴먼이 받아쳤어."]], [["‘Carry it into the city; to Cross, in Broad Street, and leave it there--quick.", "\"이걸 시내로 가져가. 브로드 스트리트의 크로스한테 가서 거기 놓고 와 — 빨리."], ["Do you hear?’", "알아들었어?\""]], [["Newman gave a dogged kind of nod to express an affirmative reply, and, leaving the room for a few seconds, returned with his hat.", "뉴먼은 그렇다는 뜻으로 뚱하니 고개를 한 번 끄덕이고는, 잠깐 방을 나갔다가 모자를 들고 돌아왔어."], ["Having made various ineffective attempts to fit the parcel (which was some two feet square) into the crown thereof, Newman took it under his arm, and after putting on his fingerless gloves with great precision and nicety, keeping his eyes fixed upon Mr.", "그 꾸러미를 — 사방 두 자쯤 되는 거였지 — 모자 통 안에 끼워 넣으려고 이리저리 애써 봤지만 다 헛수고였어. 그러자 뉴먼은 그걸 팔 밑에 끼고, 손가락 없는 장갑을 아주 정확하고 야무지게 꼈어."], ["Ralph Nickleby all the time, he adjusted his hat upon his head with as much care, real or pretended, as if it were a bran-new one of the most expensive quality, and at last departed on his errand.", "그러는 내내 눈은 랄프 니클비 씨에게 딱 고정해 둔 채로 말이야. 그러고는 마치 그게 가장 비싼 최고급 새 모자라도 되는 것처럼 진짜든 시늉이든 정성을 다해 모자를 머리에 똑바로 얹고, 드디어 심부름을 떠났어."]], [["He executed his commission with great promptitude and dispatch, only calling at one public-house for half a minute, and even that might be said to be in his way, for he went in at one door and came out at the other; but as he returned and had got so far homewards as the Strand, Newman began to loiter with the uncertain air of a man who has not quite made up his mind whether to halt or go straight forwards. After a very short consideration, the former inclination prevailed, and making towards the point he had had in his mind, Newman knocked a modest double knock, or rather a nervous single one, at Miss La Creevy’s door.", "뉴먼은 맡은 일을 아주 잽싸고 빠릿빠릿하게 해치웠어. 딱 한 군데 술집에 삼십 초쯤 들른 게 다였지. 게다가 그것도 가는 길에 있었다고 할 만했어. 한쪽 문으로 들어가 다른 쪽 문으로 나왔으니까. 그런데 돌아오는 길에 집 쪽으로 스트랜드 거리까지 왔을 때, 뉴먼이 어정거리기 시작했어. 멈출지 그냥 곧장 갈지 마음을 정하지 못한 사람처럼 머뭇거리는 모습이었지. 아주 잠깐 고민하더니, 멈추고 싶은 마음이 이겼어. 그래서 마음속에 두고 있던 그곳을 향해 발걸음을 옮긴 뉴먼은, 라 크리비 양의 집 문을 점잖게 똑똑 두 번 두드렸어. 아니, 사실은 떨리는 손으로 딱 한 번 두드린 거였지."]], [["It was opened by a strange servant, on whom the odd figure of the visitor did not appear to make the most favourable impression possible, inasmuch as she no sooner saw him than she very nearly closed it, and placing herself in the narrow gap, inquired what he wanted. But Newman merely uttering the monosyllable ‘Noggs,’ as if it were some cabalistic word, at sound of which bolts would fly back and doors open, pushed briskly past and gained the door of Miss La Creevy’s sitting-room, before the astonished servant could offer any opposition.", "문을 연 건 낯선 하녀였어. 이 손님의 괴상한 행색이 하녀에게 그리 좋은 인상을 주지 못한 모양이었지. 하녀는 그를 보자마자 문을 거의 닫아 버리고는, 그 좁은 틈에 몸을 끼워 넣고 뭘 원하느냐고 물었거든. 그런데 뉴먼은 그저 \"노그스\"라는 한 마디만 내뱉었어. 마치 그게 무슨 주문이라도 되는 것처럼, 그 소리만 들으면 빗장이 절로 풀리고 문이 열리기라도 할 것처럼 말이야. 그러고는 휙 밀고 지나가서, 깜짝 놀란 하녀가 막아 볼 새도 없이 라 크리비 양의 거실 문 앞까지 가 버렸어."]], [["‘Walk in if you please,’ said Miss La Creevy in reply to the sound of Newman’s knuckles; and in he walked accordingly.", "\"들어오세요.\" 뉴먼이 손마디로 두드리는 소리에 라 크리비 양이 대답했어. 그래서 그가 안으로 들어갔지."]], [["‘Bless us!’ cried Miss La Creevy, starting as Newman bolted in; ‘what did you want, sir?’", "\"어머나!\" 뉴먼이 불쑥 뛰어들자 라 크리비 양이 화들짝 놀라며 외쳤어. \"무슨 일이세요, 선생님?\""]], [["‘You have forgotten me,’ said Newman, with an inclination of the head.", "\"저를 잊으셨군요.\" 뉴먼이 고개를 까딱하며 말했어."], ["‘I wonder at that.", "\"그럴 만도 하죠. 옛날에 저를 알던 사람들이 저를 기억 못 하는 거야 당연한 일이지만요."], ["That nobody should remember me who knew me in other days, is natural enough; but there are few people who, seeing me once, forget me NOW.’ He glanced, as he spoke, at his shabby clothes and paralytic limb, and slightly shook his head.", "그런데 요즘의 저를 한 번이라도 본 사람치고 저를 잊는 사람은 드물거든요.\" 그는 이렇게 말하며 자기의 허름한 옷차림과 마비된 다리를 흘끗 보고는, 고개를 살짝 저었어."]], [["‘I did forget you, I declare,’ said Miss La Creevy, rising to receive Newman, who met her half-way, ‘and I am ashamed of myself for doing so; for you are a kind, good creature, Mr.", "\"정말이지 제가 당신을 잊었네요.\" 라 크리비 양이 뉴먼을 맞으려고 일어서며 말했어."], ["Noggs.", "뉴먼이 그쪽으로 마주 다가왔지."], ["Sit down and tell me all about Miss Nickleby.", "\"제 자신이 부끄럽네요. 당신은 친절하고 마음씨 고운 분인데 말이에요, 노그스 씨."], ["Poor dear thing!", "앉으세요, 그리고 니클비 양 이야기를 다 들려주세요."], ["I haven’t seen her for this many a week.’", "가엾은 것! 그 애를 못 본 지가 벌써 몇 주째예요.\""]], [["‘How’s that?’ asked Newman.", "\"어쩌다 그렇게 됐죠?\" 뉴먼이 물었어."]], [["‘Why, the truth is, Mr.", "\"실은 말이죠, 노그스 씨.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어."], ["Noggs,’ said Miss La Creevy, ‘that I have been out on a visit--the first visit I have made for fifteen years.’", "\"제가 어딜 좀 다녀왔거든요. 십오 년 만에 처음 가 본 나들이였어요.\""]], [["‘That is a long time,’ said Newman, sadly.", "\"꽤 오래됐네요.\" 뉴먼이 서글프게 말했어."]], [["‘So it is a very long time to look back upon in years, though, somehow or other, thank Heaven, the solitary days roll away peacefully and happily enough,’ replied the miniature painter. ‘I have a brother, Mr Noggs--the only relation I have--and all that time I never saw him once. Not that we ever quarrelled, but he was apprenticed down in the country, and he got married there; and new ties and affections springing up about him, he forgot a poor little woman like me, as it was very reasonable he should, you know. Don’t suppose that I complain about that, because I always said to myself, “It is very natural; poor dear John is making his way in the world, and has a wife to tell his cares and troubles to, and children now to play about him, so God bless him and them, and send we may all meet together one day where we shall part no more.” But what do you think, Mr. Noggs,’ said the miniature painter, brightening up and clapping her hands, ‘of that very same brother coming up to London at last, and never resting till he found me out; what do you think of his coming here and sitting down in that very chair, and crying like a child because he was so glad to see me--what do you think of his insisting on taking me down all the way into the country to his own house (quite a sumptuous place, Mr. Noggs, with a large garden and I don’t know how many fields, and a man in livery waiting at table, and cows and horses and pigs and I don’t know what besides), and making me stay a whole month, and pressing me to stop there all my life--yes, all my life--and so did his wife, and so did the children--and there were four of them, and one, the eldest girl of all, they--they had named her after me eight good years before, they had indeed. I never was so happy; in all my life I never was!’ The worthy soul hid her face in her handkerchief, and sobbed aloud; for it was the first opportunity she had had of unburdening her heart, and it would have its way.", "\"그래요, 햇수로 따지면 돌아보기에 아주 긴 세월이죠. 그래도 어찌 된 일인지, 고맙게도 그 외로운 날들이 그럭저럭 평온하고 행복하게 흘러갔어요.\" 미니어처 화가가 대답했어. \"저한테 오빠가 하나 있어요, 노그스 씨. 제 유일한 혈육이죠. 그런데 그 오랜 세월 동안 한 번도 못 만났어요. 우리가 다툰 건 절대 아니에요. 그냥 오빠가 시골로 일을 배우러 내려갔고, 거기서 결혼을 했거든요. 새로운 인연과 정이 둘레에 생기다 보니, 저 같은 가엾고 보잘것없는 여자는 잊어버린 거죠. 뭐, 그럴 만도 했어요, 아시잖아요. 제가 그걸 두고 불평한다고 생각하진 마세요. 저는 늘 혼자 이렇게 되뇌었거든요. '아주 당연한 일이야. 가엾은 우리 존이 세상에서 자리를 잡아 가고 있고, 근심 걱정을 털어놓을 아내도 있고, 이제 곁에서 뛰노는 아이들까지 있으니, 부디 오빠하고 그 식구들에게 신의 축복이 있기를. 그리고 언젠가 다 같이, 다시는 헤어지지 않을 그곳에서 만나게 되기를.' 그런데 어떻게 됐는지 아세요, 노그스 씨?\" 미니어처 화가가 얼굴이 환해지며 손뼉을 쳤어. \"바로 그 오빠가 마침내 런던에 올라와서는, 저를 찾아낼 때까지 도무지 가만있지를 않은 거예요. 그러고는 여기 와서 바로 저 의자에 앉아 어린아이처럼 엉엉 우는 거 있죠. 저를 만나서 너무 기쁘다면서요. 또 어땠는지 아세요? 저를 멀리 시골 자기 집까지 꼭 데려가겠다고 우기더라고요(아주 으리으리한 곳이에요, 노그스 씨. 커다란 정원이 있고, 밭은 또 몇 마지긴지 몰라요. 제복 입은 하인이 식탁 시중을 들고, 소며 말이며 돼지며, 또 뭐가 있었는지 다 모르겠네요). 그러고는 저를 꼬박 한 달이나 붙잡아 두고, 거기서 평생을 함께 살자고 졸랐어요. 네, 평생을요. 올케도 그러고, 아이들도 그랬어요. 아이들이 넷이었는데, 그중에 맏딸은요, 글쎄 그 애한테 제 이름을 붙여 줬더라고요. 그것도 팔 년이나 전에요, 정말이라니까요. 그렇게 행복했던 적이 없어요. 평생 단 한 번도요!\" 이 갸륵한 사람은 손수건에 얼굴을 묻고 큰 소리로 흐느꼈어. 가슴에 묻어 둔 걸 풀어놓을 첫 기회였으니, 그 마음이 멋대로 터져 나올 수밖에 없었지."]], [["‘But bless my life,’ said Miss La Creevy, wiping her eyes after a short pause, and cramming her handkerchief into her pocket with great bustle and dispatch; ‘what a foolish creature I must seem to you, Mr.", "\"하지만 어머나 세상에.\" 라 크리비 양이 잠깐 말을 멈췄다가 눈물을 닦고는, 손수건을 부산하고 잽싸게 주머니에 쑤셔 넣으며 말했어."], ["Noggs!", "\"제가 당신 눈엔 얼마나 어리석은 사람으로 보일까요, 노그스 씨!"], ["I shouldn’t have said anything about it, only I wanted to explain to you how it was I hadn’t seen Miss Nickleby.’", "이런 얘기까지 할 생각은 없었는데. 그냥 제가 왜 니클비 양을 못 봤는지 설명하고 싶었을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["‘Have you seen the old lady?’ asked Newman.", "\"그 노부인은 보셨나요?\" 뉴먼이 물었어."]], [["‘You mean Mrs.", "\"니클비 부인 말씀이세요?\""], ["Nickleby?’ said Miss La Creevy.", "라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"그럼 한마디 해 둘게요, 노그스 씨."], ["‘Then I tell you what, Mr Noggs, if you want to keep in the good books in that quarter, you had better not call her the old lady any more, for I suspect she wouldn’t be best pleased to hear you.", "그쪽 눈 밖에 안 나고 싶으시면, 이제 그분을 노부인이라고 부르지 않는 게 좋을 거예요. 그 말을 들으면 별로 안 좋아하실 것 같거든요."], ["Yes, I went there the night before last, but she was quite on the high ropes about something, and was so grand and mysterious, that I couldn’t make anything of her: so, to tell you the truth, I took it into my head to be grand too, and came away in state.", "네, 그저께 밤에 갔었어요. 그런데 무슨 일인지 잔뜩 콧대가 높아져서는, 어찌나 거창하고 알쏭달쏭하게 굴던지 도무지 종잡을 수가 없더라고요."], ["I thought she would have come round again before this, but she hasn’t been here.’", "그래서 솔직히 말하면, 저도 거창하게 굴어야겠다 싶어서, 위세 좀 부리며 나와 버렸죠. 그동안 다시 누그러져서 찾아올 줄 알았는데, 여기 한 번도 안 왔어요.\""]], [["‘About Miss Nickleby--’ said Newman.", "\"니클비 양 이야긴데요—\" 뉴먼이 말했어."]], [["‘Why, she was here twice while I was away,’ returned Miss La Creevy.", "\"아, 제가 없는 동안 두 번이나 여기 왔었대요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대답했어."], ["‘I was afraid she mightn’t like to have me calling on her among those great folks in what’s-its-name Place, so I thought I’d wait a day or two, and if I didn’t see her, write.’", "\"제가 그 무슨 플레이스라는 데 사는 그 대단한 사람들 틈으로 찾아가는 걸 그 애가 안 좋아할까 봐 걱정이 돼서요. 그래서 하루 이틀 기다려 보고, 그래도 못 만나면 편지를 쓸 생각이었어요.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ exclaimed Newman, cracking his fingers.", "\"아!\" 뉴먼이 손가락을 딱 꺾으며 소리쳤어."]], [["‘However, I want to hear all the news about them from you,’ said Miss La Creevy.", "\"아무튼 그 식구들 소식을 다 듣고 싶네요.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어."], ["‘How is the old rough and tough monster of Golden Square?", "\"골든 스퀘어의 그 거칠고 모진 괴물은 잘 지내나요?"], ["Well, of course; such people always are.", "뭐, 당연히 잘 지내겠죠. 그런 사람들은 늘 그러니까요."], ["I don’t mean how is he in health, but how is he going on: how is he behaving himself?’", "건강이 어떠냐는 게 아니라, 어떻게 지내느냐는 거예요. 요즘 어떻게 행동하고 있느냐고요.\""]], [["‘Damn him!’ cried Newman, dashing his cherished hat on the floor; ‘like a false hound.’", "\"빌어먹을 인간!\" 뉴먼이 애지중지하던 모자를 바닥에 내동댕이치며 외쳤어. \"표리부동한 개 같은 놈이죠.\""]], [["‘Gracious, Mr.", "\"어머나, 노그스 씨, 무서워 죽겠네요!\""], ["Noggs, you quite terrify me!’ exclaimed Miss La Creevy, turning pale.", "라 크리비 양이 새파랗게 질리며 외쳤어."]], [["‘I should have spoilt his features yesterday afternoon if I could have afforded it,’ said Newman, moving restlessly about, and shaking his fist at a portrait of Mr.", "\"어제 오후에 형편만 됐으면 그놈 낯짝을 아주 망가뜨려 놨을 거예요.\" 뉴먼이 안절부절못하고 왔다 갔다 하면서, 벽난로 위에 걸린 캐닝 씨의 초상화에 대고 주먹을 휘두르며 말했어."], ["Canning over the mantelpiece.", "\"정말 코앞까지 갔었다고요."], ["‘I was very near it. I was obliged to put my hands in my pockets, and keep ‘em there very tight.", "두 손을 주머니에 푹 찔러 넣고 꽉 붙들고 있어야 했어요."], ["I shall do it some day in that little back-parlour, I know I shall.", "언젠가는 그 작은 뒷방에서 해치우고 말 거예요, 분명히요."], ["I should have done it before now, if I hadn’t been afraid of making bad worse.", "일을 더 그르칠까 봐 겁만 안 났어도 진작 했을 텐데."], ["I shall double-lock myself in with him and have it out before I die, I’m quite certain of it.’", "죽기 전에 그놈하고 단둘이 문을 이중으로 걸어 잠그고 끝장을 보고 말 거예요, 정말이라니까요.\""]], [["‘I shall scream if you don’t compose yourself, Mr.", "\"진정 안 하시면 저 비명 지를 거예요, 노그스 씨.\""], ["Noggs,’ said Miss La Creevy; ‘I’m sure I shan’t be able to help it.’", "라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"정말 참을 수가 없을 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘Never mind,’ rejoined Newman, darting violently to and fro.", "\"신경 쓰지 마세요.\" 뉴먼이 사납게 이리저리 휙휙 움직이며 받아쳤어."], ["‘He’s coming up tonight: I wrote to tell him.", "\"그놈이 오늘 밤 올라와요. 제가 편지로 알렸거든요."], ["He little thinks I know; he little thinks I care.", "그놈은 제가 알고 있는 줄은 꿈에도 몰라요. 제가 신경 쓰는 줄도 모르고요."], ["Cunning scoundrel!", "교활한 악당 같으니!"], ["he don’t think that.", "그놈은 그런 건 생각도 못 해요."], ["Not he, not he.", "절대로요, 절대로."], ["Never mind, I’ll thwart him--I, Newman Noggs.", "두고 보세요, 제가 그놈을 막을 거예요. 바로 저, 뉴먼 노그스가요."], ["Ho, ho, the rascal!’", "호, 호, 그 못된 놈!\""]], [["Lashing himself up to an extravagant pitch of fury, Newman Noggs jerked himself about the room with the most eccentric motion ever beheld in a human being: now sparring at the little miniatures on the wall, and now giving himself violent thumps on the head, as if to heighten the delusion, until he sank down in his former seat quite breathless and exhausted.", "뉴먼 노그스는 스스로를 부추겨 터무니없을 만큼 격분한 상태로 몰아갔어. 그러고는 사람 몸으로 할 수 있는 가장 괴상한 동작으로 방 안을 들썩들썩 휘젓고 다녔지. 벽에 걸린 작은 미니어처들을 향해 주먹을 휘두르는가 하면, 마치 그 망상을 더 부추기기라도 하듯 제 머리를 세게 쥐어박기도 했어. 그러다 마침내 숨이 차고 기진맥진해서 아까 앉았던 자리에 풀썩 주저앉았지."]], [["‘There,’ said Newman, picking up his hat; ‘that’s done me good. Now I’m better, and I’ll tell you all about it.’", "\"자.\" 뉴먼이 모자를 집어 들며 말했어. \"이제 좀 풀렸네요. 한결 나아요. 이제 다 말씀드릴게요.\""]], [["It took some little time to reassure Miss La Creevy, who had been almost frightened out of her senses by this remarkable demonstration; but that done, Newman faithfully related all that had passed in the interview between Kate and her uncle, prefacing his narrative with a statement of his previous suspicions on the subject, and his reasons for forming them; and concluding with a communication of the step he had taken in secretly writing to Nicholas.", "라 크리비 양을 다시 안심시키는 데는 시간이 좀 걸렸어. 이 놀라운 소동에 거의 정신이 나갈 만큼 겁을 먹었으니까. 하지만 그게 끝나자, 뉴먼은 케이트와 그 삼촌 사이에 오갔던 일을 빠짐없이 충실히 들려줬어. 이야기를 시작하기 전에 자기가 진작부터 그 일을 의심해 왔다는 것과, 왜 그런 의심을 품게 됐는지를 먼저 밝혔지. 그리고 니콜라스에게 몰래 편지를 써 보냈다는 것까지 알려 주며 이야기를 맺었어."]], [["Though little Miss La Creevy’s indignation was not so singularly displayed as Newman’s, it was scarcely inferior in violence and intensity.", "작은 라 크리비 양의 분노는 뉴먼처럼 그렇게 별나게 드러나지는 않았지만, 그 격렬함과 강도는 거의 못지않았어."], ["Indeed, if Ralph Nickleby had happened to make his appearance in the room at that moment, there is some doubt whether he would not have found Miss La Creevy a more dangerous opponent than even Newman Noggs himself.", "정말이지 그 순간 랄프 니클비가 그 방에 들어왔더라면, 뉴먼 노그스보다도 라 크리비 양이 더 위험한 적수라는 걸 알게 됐을지도 몰라."]], [["‘God forgive me for saying so,’ said Miss La Creevy, as a wind-up to all her expressions of anger, ‘but I really feel as if I could stick this into him with pleasure.’", "\"이런 말을 하다니 하느님 용서하소서.\" 라 크리비 양이 분풀이를 한바탕 쏟아 낸 끝에 말했어. \"그런데 정말이지 이걸로 그 인간을 푹 찔러 버려도 속이 시원할 것 같아요.\""]], [["It was not a very awful weapon that Miss La Creevy held, it being in fact nothing more nor less than a black-lead pencil; but discovering her mistake, the little portrait painter exchanged it for a mother-of-pearl fruit knife, wherewith, in proof of her desperate thoughts, she made a lunge as she spoke, which would have scarcely disturbed the crumb of a half-quartern loaf.", "라 크리비 양이 들고 있던 건 그리 무시무시한 무기가 아니었어. 사실 흑연 연필 하나, 딱 그거였거든. 그런데 자기 실수를 알아채고는, 이 작은 초상화가는 그걸 자개 손잡이가 달린 과일칼로 바꿔 들었어. 그러고는 자기가 얼마나 무서운 생각을 품고 있는지 보여 주려는 듯, 말을 하면서 그 칼로 한 번 푹 찔러 보였지. 하지만 그건 반쪽짜리 식빵 부스러기 하나 흐트러뜨리지 못할 시늉이었어."]], [["‘She won’t stop where she is after tonight,’ said Newman.", "\"오늘 밤이 지나면 그 애가 거기 더는 안 있을 거예요.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."], ["‘That’s a comfort.’", "\"그게 다행이죠.\""]], [["‘Stop!’ cried Miss La Creevy, ‘she should have left there, weeks ago.’", "\"있긴요!\" 라 크리비 양이 외쳤어. \"몇 주 전에 진작 거기서 나왔어야 했어요.\""]], [["‘--If we had known of this,’ rejoined Newman.", "\"—우리가 이걸 알았더라면 말이죠.\" 뉴먼이 받아쳤어."], ["‘But we didn’t.", "\"그런데 몰랐잖아요."], ["Nobody could properly interfere but her mother or brother.", "그 애 어머니나 오빠 말고는 제대로 끼어들 사람이 없으니까요."], ["The mother’s weak--poor thing--weak.", "그 어머니는 마음이 약해요. 가엾게도, 약하죠."], ["The dear young man will be here tonight.’", "그 착한 청년이 오늘 밤 올 거예요.\""]], [["‘Heart alive!’ cried Miss La Creevy.", "\"어머나 세상에!\" 라 크리비 양이 외쳤어."], ["‘He will do something desperate, Mr Noggs, if you tell him all at once.’", "\"한꺼번에 다 말해 버리면 그 사람이 무슨 무서운 일을 저지를 거예요, 노그스 씨.\""]], [["Newman left off rubbing his hands, and assumed a thoughtful look.", "뉴먼이 손 비비던 걸 멈추고 생각에 잠긴 표정을 지었어."]], [["‘Depend upon it,’ said Miss La Creevy, earnestly, ‘if you are not very careful in breaking out the truth to him, he will do some violence upon his uncle or one of these men that will bring some terrible calamity upon his own head, and grief and sorrow to us all.’", "\"틀림없어요.\" 라 크리비 양이 진지하게 말했어. \"당신이 아주 조심해서 진실을 알려 주지 않으면, 그 사람이 삼촌이나 그 작자들 중 하나한테 무슨 난폭한 짓을 저지를 거예요. 그러면 제 머리 위에 끔찍한 재앙을 불러들이고, 우리 모두에게 슬픔과 비탄을 안기게 되겠죠.\""]], [["‘I never thought of that,’ rejoined Newman, his countenance falling more and more.", "\"그건 미처 생각 못 했네요.\" 뉴먼이 점점 더 얼굴이 어두워지며 받아쳤어."], ["‘I came to ask you to receive his sister in case he brought her here, but--’", "\"저는 그 사람이 동생을 여기 데려올 경우에 좀 받아 달라고 부탁하러 온 거였는데, 그런데—\""]], [["‘But this is a matter of much greater importance,’ interrupted Miss La Creevy; ‘that you might have been sure of before you came, but the end of this, nobody can foresee, unless you are very guarded and careful.’", "\"하지만 이건 훨씬 더 중요한 문제예요.\" 라 크리비 양이 끼어들었어. \"그건 여기 오기 전에 이미 확신할 수 있는 일이었지만, 이 일의 끝은 아무도 내다볼 수 없어요. 당신이 아주 신중하고 조심하지 않는다면 말이에요.\""]], [["‘What CAN I do?’ cried Newman, scratching his head with an air of great vexation and perplexity.", "\"제가 뭘 할 수 있겠어요?\" 뉴먼이 잔뜩 골치 아프고 난감한 표정으로 머리를 긁적이며 외쳤어."], ["‘If he was to talk of pistoling ‘em all, I should be obliged to say, “Certainly--serve ‘em right.”’", "\"그 사람이 그놈들을 다 권총으로 쏴 죽이겠다고 하면, 저는 어쩔 수 없이 이렇게 말할 거예요. '그럼요, 그래도 싸죠'라고요.\""]], [["Miss La Creevy could not suppress a small shriek on hearing this, and instantly set about extorting a solemn pledge from Newman that he would use his utmost endeavours to pacify the wrath of Nicholas; which, after some demur, was conceded. They then consulted together on the safest and surest mode of communicating to him the circumstances which had rendered his presence necessary.", "이 말을 듣고 라 크리비 양은 가벼운 비명을 참지 못했어. 그러고는 곧장 뉴먼한테서 엄숙한 약속을 받아 내려고 했지. 니콜라스의 분노를 가라앉히는 데 온 힘을 다하겠다는 약속을 말이야. 몇 번 망설인 끝에 뉴먼이 그러기로 했어. 그런 다음 두 사람은 머리를 맞대고, 어떻게 하면 니콜라스에게 그를 와야만 했던 그 사정을 가장 안전하고 확실하게 전할 수 있을지 의논했어."]], [["‘He must have time to cool before he can possibly do anything,’ said Miss La Creevy.", "\"그 사람이 무슨 일을 저지르기 전에 마음을 식힐 시간이 있어야 해요.\""], ["‘That is of the greatest consequence.", "라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"그게 제일 중요해요."], ["He must not be told until late at night.’", "밤늦게까지는 알려 줘선 안 돼요.\""]], [["‘But he’ll be in town between six and seven this evening,’ replied Newman.", "\"하지만 그 사람은 오늘 저녁 여섯 시에서 일곱 시 사이에 시내에 도착할 거예요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어."], ["‘I can’t keep it from him when he asks me.’", "\"그 사람이 물으면 제가 숨길 수가 없다고요.\""]], [["‘Then you must go out, Mr.", "\"그럼 나가 계셔야죠, 노그스 씨.\""], ["Noggs,’ said Miss La Creevy.", "라 크리비 양이 말했어."], ["‘You can easily have been kept away by business, and must not return till nearly midnight.’", "\"일 때문에 발이 묶였던 걸로 하면 그만이잖아요. 그리고 자정 가까이 될 때까진 돌아오지 마세요.\""]], [["‘Then he will come straight here,’ retorted Newman.", "\"그럼 그 사람이 곧장 여기로 올 거예요.\" 뉴먼이 되받았어."]], [["‘So I suppose,’ observed Miss La Creevy; ‘but he won’t find me at home, for I’ll go straight to the city the instant you leave me, make up matters with Mrs. Nickleby, and take her away to the theatre, so that he may not even know where his sister lives.’", "\"그렇겠죠.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"하지만 저도 집에 없을 거예요. 당신이 가시는 대로 저는 곧장 시내로 가서 니클비 부인하고 화해하고, 그분을 극장으로 데려갈 거니까요. 그러면 그 사람은 그의 여동생이 어디 사는지조차 모르게 되겠죠.\""]], [["Upon further discussion, this appeared the safest and most feasible mode of proceeding that could possibly be adopted. Therefore it was finally determined that matters should be so arranged, and Newman, after listening to many supplementary cautions and entreaties, took his leave of Miss La Creevy and trudged back to Golden Square; ruminating as he went upon a vast number of possibilities and impossibilities which crowded upon his brain, and arose out of the conversation that had just terminated.", "이 문제를 더 의논해 보니, 이게 택할 수 있는 가장 안전하고 실행할 만한 방법인 듯했어. 그래서 결국 그렇게 하기로 결정이 났지. 그리고 뉴먼은 여러 가지 덧붙는 당부와 간곡한 부탁을 다 듣고 나서, 라 크리비 양과 작별하고 골든 스퀘어로 터덜터덜 돌아갔어. 가는 내내 머릿속에 빼곡히 들어찬 수많은 가능성과 불가능성을 곱씹으면서 말이야. 방금 끝낸 그 대화에서 비롯된 생각들이었지."]], [["# CHAPTER 32", "# CHAPTER 32"]], [["Relating chiefly to some remarkable Conversation, and some remarkable Proceedings to which it gives rise\n‘London at last!’ cried Nicholas, throwing back his greatcoat and rousing Smike from a long nap.", "드디어 런던에 다다랐다. 니콜라스는 외투를 젖히며 한참 단잠에 빠져 있던 스마이크를 흔들어 깨웠어."], ["‘It seemed to me as though we should never reach it.’", "\"드디어 런던이야! 영영 닿지 못할 것만 같았는데.\""]], [["‘And yet you came along at a tidy pace too,’ observed the coachman, looking over his shoulder at Nicholas with no very pleasant expression of countenance.", "\"그런 것치곤 꽤 빠른 속도로 달려오셨는데요.\" 마부가 어깨너머로 니콜라스를 돌아보며 말했어. 그다지 기분 좋은 얼굴은 아니었지."]], [["‘Ay, I know that,’ was the reply; ‘but I have been very anxious to be at my journey’s end, and that makes the way seem long.’", "\"그래요, 알아요.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어. \"그저 목적지에 빨리 닿고 싶은 마음이 너무 간절해서, 길이 더 멀게 느껴졌던 거예요.\""]], [["‘Well,’ remarked the coachman, ‘if the way seemed long with such cattle as you’ve sat behind, you MUST have been most uncommon anxious;’ and so saying, he let out his whip-lash and touched up a little boy on the calves of his legs by way of emphasis.", "\"흠, 댁이 타고 온 이런 말들 뒤에 앉아서도 길이 멀게 느껴졌다면, 어지간히도 마음이 급하셨던 모양이구먼.\" 마부가 이렇게 말하더니 채찍을 휙 풀어, 강조라도 하듯 옆에 있던 사내아이의 종아리를 슬쩍 후려쳤어."]], [["They rattled on through the noisy, bustling, crowded street of London, now displaying long double rows of brightly-burning lamps, dotted here and there with the chemists’ glaring lights, and illuminated besides with the brilliant flood that streamed from the windows of the shops, where sparkling jewellery, silks and velvets of the richest colours, the most inviting delicacies, and most sumptuous articles of luxurious ornament, succeeded each other in rich and glittering profusion. Streams of people apparently without end poured on and on, jostling each other in the crowd and hurrying forward, scarcely seeming to notice the riches that surrounded them on every side; while vehicles of all shapes and makes, mingled up together in one moving mass, like running water, lent their ceaseless roar to swell the noise and tumult.", "마차는 시끄럽고 분주하고 사람들로 북적이는 런던 거리를 덜컹덜컹 달려갔어. 거리에는 환하게 타오르는 가로등이 두 줄로 길게 늘어서 있었고, 군데군데 약국의 번쩍이는 불빛이 점점이 박혀 있었지. 게다가 가게 창문에서 쏟아져 나오는 눈부신 빛까지 거리를 환히 밝히고 있었어. 가게 안에는 반짝이는 보석이며, 더없이 화려한 빛깔의 비단과 벨벳이며, 입맛을 자극하는 진미며, 사치스러운 장식품들이 풍성하게 줄지어 반짝거렸어. 사람들의 물결은 끝도 없이 밀려들었어. 인파 속에서 서로 부딪치며 앞으로 서둘러 나아가면서도, 사방을 에워싼 그 온갖 보물에는 거의 눈길도 주지 않는 듯했지. 그러는 사이 온갖 모양과 종류의 탈것들이 흐르는 물처럼 한데 뒤엉켜 움직이며, 그 끊임없는 굉음으로 소음과 소란을 더했어."]], [["As they dashed by the quickly-changing and ever-varying objects, it was curious to observe in what a strange procession they passed before the eye. Emporiums of splendid dresses, the materials brought from every quarter of the world; tempting stores of everything to stimulate and pamper the sated appetite and give new relish to the oft-repeated feast; vessels of burnished gold and silver, wrought into every exquisite form of vase, and dish, and goblet; guns, swords, pistols, and patent engines of destruction; screws and irons for the crooked, clothes for the newly-born, drugs for the sick, coffins for the dead, and churchyards for the buried--all these jumbled each with the other and flocking side by side, seemed to flit by in motley dance like the fantastic groups of the old Dutch painter, and with the same stern moral for the unheeding restless crowd.", "쉴 새 없이 바뀌고 변하는 풍경들을 휙휙 스쳐 지나갈 때면, 그것들이 눈앞을 얼마나 기묘한 행렬을 이루며 지나가는지 보는 게 신기했어. 세계 곳곳에서 들여온 옷감으로 지은 화려한 드레스를 파는 가게들. 물릴 대로 물린 입맛을 다시 돋우고 응석을 받아 주려는, 그리고 늘 똑같은 잔칫상에 새로운 맛을 더해 주려는 온갖 먹을거리가 그득한 유혹적인 상점들. 갖가지 정교한 모양의 꽃병과 접시와 술잔으로 빚어낸, 윤이 반들반들 나는 금은 그릇들. 총, 칼, 권총, 신식 살상 무기들. 굽은 몸을 펴 주는 나사와 쇠붙이, 갓난아기 옷, 병자를 위한 약, 죽은 이를 위한 관, 묻힌 이를 위한 교회 묘지. 이 모든 것이 서로 뒤섞여 나란히 모여서는, 마치 옛 네덜란드 화가가 그린 기괴한 무리들처럼 어지러운 춤을 추며 휙휙 스쳐 지나가는 것 같았어. 그리고 무심하고 정신없는 군중을 향해 그 그림들과 똑같은 엄숙한 교훈을 던지는 듯했지."]], [["Nor were there wanting objects in the crowd itself to give new point and purpose to the shifting scene. The rags of the squalid ballad-singer fluttered in the rich light that showed the goldsmith’s treasures, pale and pinched-up faces hovered about the windows where was tempting food, hungry eyes wandered over the profusion guarded by one thin sheet of brittle glass--an iron wall to them; half-naked shivering figures stopped to gaze at Chinese shawls and golden stuffs of India. There was a christening party at the largest coffin-maker’s and a funeral hatchment had stopped some great improvements in the bravest mansion. Life and death went hand in hand; wealth and poverty stood side by side; repletion and starvation laid them down together.", "그 풍경에 새로운 의미와 뜻을 더해 주는 건 군중 그 자체에도 모자람이 없었어. 금세공인의 보물을 비추는 화려한 불빛 속에서 초라한 거리 가수의 누더기가 펄럭였어. 입맛 당기는 음식이 놓인 창문 둘레로 창백하고 핼쑥한 얼굴들이 서성거렸지. 굶주린 눈은 얇은 유리 한 장이 — 그들에게는 쇠벽이나 다름없는 그 유리가 — 지키고 있는 산더미 같은 음식 위를 헤맸어. 반쯤 헐벗은 채 떨고 있는 사람들은 중국산 숄과 인도산 금빛 천을 멍하니 바라보려고 걸음을 멈췄어. 가장 큰 관 만드는 집에서는 세례 잔치가 벌어지고 있었어. 그런가 하면 가장 으리으리한 저택에서는 한창 진행되던 대단한 공사가 장례 문장 때문에 멈춰 서 있었지. 삶과 죽음이 손을 맞잡고 나란히 갔어. 부와 가난이 어깨를 나란히 하고 서 있었지. 배부름과 굶주림이 함께 몸을 누였어."]], [["But it was London; and the old country lady inside, who had put her head out of the coach-window a mile or two this side Kingston, and cried out to the driver that she was sure he must have passed it and forgotten to set her down, was satisfied at last.", "하지만 그래도 런던이었어. 킹스턴을 한두 마일 앞두고서 마차 창밖으로 고개를 내밀던 시골 노부인이 있었지. 마부에게 분명 그곳을 지나쳐서 자기를 내려 주는 걸 깜빡한 거라며 소리치던 그 부인도, 마침내 만족했어."]], [["Nicholas engaged beds for himself and Smike at the inn where the coach stopped, and repaired, without the delay of another moment, to the lodgings of Newman Noggs; for his anxiety and impatience had increased with every succeeding minute, and were almost beyond control.", "니콜라스는 마차가 멈춘 여관에 자기와 스마이크가 묵을 잠자리를 잡았어. 그러고는 한순간도 지체하지 않고 곧장 뉴먼 노그스의 하숙집으로 향했지. 시간이 일 분씩 흐를 때마다 그의 불안과 조바심은 점점 커져서, 이젠 거의 걷잡을 수 없을 지경이었거든."]], [["There was a fire in Newman’s garret; and a candle had been left burning; the floor was cleanly swept, the room was as comfortably arranged as such a room could be, and meat and drink were placed in order upon the table. Everything bespoke the affectionate care and attention of Newman Noggs, but Newman himself was not there.", "뉴먼의 다락방에는 불이 피워져 있었어. 촛불도 하나 켜진 채 남겨져 있었지. 바닥은 깨끗이 쓸려 있었고, 방은 그런 방이 갖출 수 있는 한 가장 아늑하게 정돈되어 있었어. 그리고 먹을 것과 마실 것이 탁자 위에 가지런히 놓여 있었지. 모든 게 뉴먼 노그스의 다정한 보살핌과 정성을 말해 주고 있었어. 그런데 정작 뉴먼은 거기 없었어."]], [["‘Do you know what time he will be home?’ inquired Nicholas, tapping at the door of Newman’s front neighbour.", "\"그분이 몇 시에 돌아오는지 아세요?\" 니콜라스는 뉴먼의 앞집 문을 두드리며 물었어."]], [["‘Ah, Mr.", "\"아, 존슨 씨!\""], ["Johnson!’ said Crowl, presenting himself.", "크롤이 나타나며 말했어. \"어서 오세요, 선생님."], ["‘Welcome, sir. How well you’re looking!", "안색이 참 좋으시네요!"], ["I never could have believed--’", "정말이지 믿기지가 않을 만큼—\""]], [["‘Pardon me,’ interposed Nicholas.", "\"실례지만,\" 니콜라스가 말을 끊었어."], ["‘My question--I am extremely anxious to know.’", "\"제 질문에—저는 그게 너무 궁금해서요.\""]], [["‘Why, he has a troublesome affair of business,’ replied Crowl, ‘and will not be home before twelve o’clock. He was very unwilling to go, I can tell you, but there was no help for it. However, he left word that you were to make yourself comfortable till he came back, and that I was to entertain you, which I shall be very glad to do.’", "\"그게 말이죠, 그 사람이 골치 아픈 일이 좀 생겨서,\" 크롤이 대답했어. \"열두 시 전에는 못 돌아올 겁니다. 정말 가기 싫어했어요, 내가 장담하는데. 그런데 어쩔 수가 없었지요. 그래도 돌아올 때까지 선생님더러 편히 계시라고, 또 내가 선생님을 대접하라고 일러두고 갔어요. 나야 기꺼이 그러지요.\""]], [["In proof of his extreme readiness to exert himself for the general entertainment, Mr.", "다 함께 즐겁게 지내려고 자기가 얼마나 발 벗고 나설 준비가 되어 있는지 증명이라도 하듯, 크롤 씨는 이렇게 말하면서 의자를 탁자 쪽으로 끌어당겼어."], ["Crowl drew a chair to the table as he spoke, and helping himself plentifully to the cold meat, invited Nicholas and Smike to follow his example.", "그러고는 찬 고기를 듬뿍 자기 접시에 덜더니, 니콜라스와 스마이크에게도 자기처럼 하라고 권했지."]], [["Disappointed and uneasy, Nicholas could touch no food, so, after he had seen Smike comfortably established at the table, he walked out (despite a great many dissuasions uttered by Mr.", "실망스럽고 마음이 불편한 니콜라스는 음식에 손도 댈 수 없었어. 그래서 스마이크가 탁자에 편히 자리 잡은 걸 확인한 뒤, 밖으로 나갔지."], ["Crowl with his mouth full), and left Smike to detain Newman in case he returned first.", "크롤 씨가 입에 음식을 가득 문 채로 여러 번 말렸지만 막무가내였어. 그러고는 혹시 뉴먼이 먼저 돌아오면 붙잡아 두라고 스마이크에게 일러두었어."]], [["As Miss La Creevy had anticipated, Nicholas betook himself straight to her house.", "라 크리비 양이 짐작했던 대로, 니콜라스는 곧장 그녀의 집으로 향했어."], ["Finding her from home, he debated within himself for some time whether he should go to his mother’s residence, and so compromise her with Ralph Nickleby.", "그런데 그녀가 집에 없자, 한참 동안 마음속으로 고민했지. 어머니 집으로 갈까, 그러면 어머니를 랄프 니클비와 곤란한 처지에 빠뜨리는 게 아닐까 하고 말이야."], ["Fully persuaded, however, that Newman would not have solicited him to return unless there was some strong reason which required his presence at home, he resolved to go there, and hastened eastwards with all speed.", "하지만 무언가 자기가 꼭 집에 있어야 할 절박한 이유가 없었다면 뉴먼이 자기더러 돌아오라고 청하지 않았을 거라는 확신이 들었어. 그래서 거기로 가기로 마음먹고, 있는 힘껏 동쪽으로 서둘러 갔지."]], [["Mrs. Nickleby would not be at home, the girl said, until past twelve, or later.", "니클비 부인은 열두 시가 넘어야, 어쩌면 더 늦어야 집에 돌아오신다고 하녀가 말했어."], ["She believed Miss Nickleby was well, but she didn’t live at home now, nor did she come home except very seldom.", "니클비 양은 잘 지내는 것 같지만 이제는 집에서 살지 않고, 아주 가끔이 아니면 집에 오지도 않는다고 했지."], ["She couldn’t say where she was stopping, but it was not at Madame Mantalini’s.", "어디에 묵고 있는지는 모르겠지만, 마담 만탈리니네는 아니라고 했어."], ["She was sure of that.", "그건 확실하다고 말이야."]], [["With his heart beating violently, and apprehending he knew not what disaster, Nicholas returned to where he had left Smike.", "가슴이 거세게 두근거리는 데다 무슨 일인지도 모를 불행을 두려워하며, 니콜라스는 스마이크를 두고 온 곳으로 돌아왔어."], ["Newman had not been home.", "뉴먼은 아직 돌아오지 않았어."], ["He wouldn’t be, till twelve o’clock; there was no chance of it.", "열두 시 전에는 돌아올 리 없었지. 가망이 없었어."], ["Was there no possibility of sending to fetch him if it were only for an instant, or forwarding to him one line of writing to which he might return a verbal reply?", "잠깐만이라도 그를 데리러 사람을 보낼 수는 없을까, 아니면 글 한 줄이라도 적어 보내서 말로라도 답을 받을 수는 없을까?"], ["That was quite impracticable.", "그것도 도저히 불가능했어."], ["He was not at Golden Square, and probably had been sent to execute some commission at a distance.", "그는 골든 스퀘어에도 없었고, 아마 먼 곳으로 무슨 심부름을 보내진 모양이었어."]], [["Nicholas tried to remain quietly where he was, but he felt so nervous and excited that he could not sit still.", "니콜라스는 있는 자리에 가만히 있어 보려 했어. 그런데 너무 초조하고 들떠서 도무지 앉아 있을 수가 없었지."], ["He seemed to be losing time unless he was moving.", "움직이지 않으면 시간을 허비하는 것만 같았어."], ["It was an absurd fancy, he knew, but he was wholly unable to resist it.", "어처구니없는 생각이라는 건 알았지만, 도저히 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었지."], ["So, he took up his hat and rambled out again.", "그래서 모자를 집어 들고 다시 밖으로 나갔어."]], [["He strolled westward this time, pacing the long streets with hurried footsteps, and agitated by a thousand misgivings and apprehensions which he could not overcome. He passed into Hyde Park, now silent and deserted, and increased his rate of walking as if in the hope of leaving his thoughts behind. They crowded upon him more thickly, however, now there were no passing objects to attract his attention; and the one idea was always uppermost, that some stroke of ill-fortune must have occurred so calamitous in its nature that all were fearful of disclosing it to him. The old question arose again and again--What could it be? Nicholas walked till he was weary, but was not one bit the wiser; and indeed he came out of the Park at last a great deal more confused and perplexed than when he went in.", "이번엔 서쪽으로 발길을 옮겼어. 서두르는 걸음으로 긴 거리들을 걸으면서, 도무지 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 수천 가지 불안과 걱정에 시달렸지. 그는 하이드 파크로 들어섰어. 이제는 인적도 없이 고요한 곳이었지. 그러고는 마치 자기 생각을 뒤에 떼어 놓고 가려는 듯 걸음을 더 빨리했어. 그런데 눈길을 끌 만한 게 더 이상 지나가지 않으니, 생각은 오히려 더 빽빽하게 밀려들었어. 그리고 한 가지 생각이 늘 마음을 짓눌렀지. 무언가 불행이 닥쳤는데, 그게 너무도 끔찍한 것이라 모두가 자기한테 털어놓기를 겁내는 거라는 생각이었어. 묵은 의문이 자꾸자꾸 고개를 들었어. 대체 무슨 일일까? 니콜라스는 지칠 때까지 걸었지만, 조금도 알아내지 못했어. 정말이지 마침내 공원을 나설 때는 들어갈 때보다 훨씬 더 머릿속이 어지럽고 헷갈렸지."]], [["He had taken scarcely anything to eat or drink since early in the morning, and felt quite worn out and exhausted.", "그는 이른 아침 이후로 거의 아무것도 먹지도 마시지도 못해서, 완전히 녹초가 되고 기진맥진한 상태였어."], ["As he returned languidly towards the point from which he had started, along one of the thoroughfares which lie between Park Lane and Bond Street, he passed a handsome hotel, before which he stopped mechanically.", "파크 레인과 본드 스트리트 사이에 놓인 큰길 하나를 따라 처음 출발했던 곳으로 맥없이 돌아오던 길에, 그는 어느 멋진 호텔을 지나치다가 무심코 걸음을 멈췄어."]], [["‘An expensive place, I dare say,’ thought Nicholas; ‘but a pint of wine and a biscuit are no great debauch wherever they are had.", "'비싼 데겠지.' 니콜라스는 생각했어."], ["And yet I don’t know.’", "'하지만 포도주 한 잔에 비스킷 하나쯤이야, 어디서 먹든 대단한 사치는 아니잖아. 그래도 모르겠다.'"]], [["He walked on a few steps, but looking wistfully down the long vista of gas-lamps before him, and thinking how long it would take to reach the end of it and being besides in that kind of mood in which a man is most disposed to yield to his first impulse--and being, besides, strongly attracted to the hotel, in part by curiosity, and in part by some odd mixture of feelings which he would have been troubled to define--Nicholas turned back again, and walked into the coffee-room.", "그는 몇 걸음 더 걸었어. 그런데 눈앞에 길게 늘어선 가스등 줄을 아쉬운 듯 바라보면서, 저 끝까지 가려면 얼마나 걸릴까 생각했지. 게다가 마침 첫 충동에 가장 쉽게 휩쓸리는 그런 기분이기도 했어. 거기다 그 호텔에 강하게 끌리기도 했지. 한편으론 호기심 때문에, 또 한편으론 자기도 뭐라 설명하기 어려운 묘한 감정들이 뒤섞인 탓이었어. 그래서 니콜라스는 다시 발길을 돌려 커피룸으로 들어섰어."]], [["It was very handsomely furnished.", "그곳은 아주 멋지게 꾸며져 있었어."], ["The walls were ornamented with the choicest specimens of French paper, enriched with a gilded cornice of elegant design.", "벽은 더없이 고급스러운 프랑스산 벽지로 장식되어 있었고, 우아하게 디자인된 금박 처마 장식으로 한층 화려해 보였지. 바닥은 값비싼 양탄자로 덮여 있었어."], ["The floor was covered with a rich carpet; and two superb mirrors, one above the chimneypiece and one at the opposite end of the room reaching from floor to ceiling, multiplied the other beauties and added new ones of their own to enhance the general effect.", "그리고 멋진 거울이 두 개 있었는데, 하나는 벽난로 위에, 또 하나는 방 맞은편 끝에 바닥부터 천장까지 닿게 걸려 있었어. 그 거울들이 다른 아름다운 것들을 곱절로 비추고, 거기에 제 나름의 아름다움까지 보태어 전체 분위기를 더욱 돋우었지."], ["There was a rather noisy party of four gentlemen in a box by the fire-place, and only two other persons present--both elderly gentlemen, and both alone.", "벽난로 옆 칸막이 자리에는 다소 시끄러운 신사 넷이 무리 지어 있었어. 그리고 다른 손님이라곤 단둘뿐이었는데, 둘 다 나이 지긋한 신사였고 둘 다 혼자였어."]], [["Observing all this in the first comprehensive glance with which a stranger surveys a place that is new to him, Nicholas sat himself down in the box next to the noisy party, with his back towards them, and postponing his order for a pint of claret until such time as the waiter and one of the elderly gentlemen should have settled a disputed question relative to the price of an item in the bill of fare, took up a newspaper and began to read.", "낯선 곳에 들어선 사람이 그곳을 한눈에 쓱 훑어볼 때처럼, 니콜라스도 이 모든 걸 단번에 살피고는 그 시끄러운 무리 바로 옆 칸막이 자리에 앉았어. 그들을 등진 채로 말이야. 그러고는 클라레 한 병을 주문하는 걸 잠시 미뤘지. 웨이터와 그 나이 든 신사 한 명이 메뉴판에 적힌 어떤 품목의 값을 두고 다투는 게 끝날 때까지 기다린 거야. 니콜라스는 신문을 집어 들고 읽기 시작했어."]], [["He had not read twenty lines, and was in truth himself dozing, when he was startled by the mention of his sister’s name.", "스무 줄도 채 읽지 못했고, 사실은 그 자신도 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있었어. 그런데 누군가 자기 여동생 이름을 입에 올리는 바람에 화들짝 놀랐지."], ["‘Little Kate Nickleby’ were the words that caught his ear.", "'꼬마 케이트 니클비'라는 말이 그의 귀에 박혔거든. 그는 깜짝 놀라 고개를 들었어."], ["He raised his head in amazement, and as he did so, saw by the reflection in the opposite glass, that two of the party behind him had risen and were standing before the fire.", "그러면서 맞은편 거울에 비친 모습으로, 등 뒤에 있던 무리 가운데 둘이 일어나 벽난로 앞에 서 있는 걸 봤지. '저 둘 중 한 명한테서 나온 말이 분명해.'"], ["‘It must have come from one of them,’ thought Nicholas.", "니콜라스는 생각했어. 그는 좀 분한 얼굴로 다음 말을 들으려 기다렸어."], ["He waited to hear more with a countenance of some indignation, for the tone of speech had been anything but respectful, and the appearance of the individual whom he presumed to have been the speaker was coarse and swaggering.", "말투가 도무지 존중이라곤 없었거든. 게다가 그 말을 한 사람이라고 짐작되는 자의 생김새도 거칠고 거만했지."]], [["This person--so Nicholas observed in the same glance at the mirror which had enabled him to see his face--was standing with his back to the fire conversing with a younger man, who stood with his back to the company, wore his hat, and was adjusting his shirt-collar by the aid of the glass.", "그자는 — 니콜라스가 그 얼굴을 볼 수 있었던 바로 그 거울 속에서 관찰한 바로는 — 벽난로를 등지고 서서 더 젊은 남자와 이야기를 나누고 있었어. 그 젊은 남자는 일행을 등진 채 모자를 쓰고 서서, 거울을 보며 셔츠 깃을 매만지고 있었지."], ["They spoke in whispers, now and then bursting into a loud laugh, but Nicholas could catch no repetition of the words, nor anything sounding at all like the words, which had attracted his attention.", "둘은 소곤소곤 이야기하다가, 이따금 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸어. 하지만 니콜라스는 자기 귀를 사로잡았던 그 말도, 비슷하게 들리는 그 어떤 말도 다시 들을 수가 없었어."]], [["At length the two resumed their seats, and more wine being ordered, the party grew louder in their mirth.", "마침내 둘은 다시 자리에 앉았어. 그리고 포도주를 더 시키자, 무리는 한층 더 시끄럽게 떠들어 댔지."], ["Still there was no reference made to anybody with whom he was acquainted, and Nicholas became persuaded that his excited fancy had either imagined the sounds altogether, or converted some other words into the name which had been so much in his thoughts.", "그래도 여전히 그가 아는 누군가를 두고 하는 말은 없었어. 그래서 니콜라스는 자기 들뜬 머릿속이 그 소리를 통째로 지어냈거나, 아니면 다른 어떤 말을 그동안 줄곧 마음에 담아 둔 그 이름으로 바꿔 들은 거라고 믿게 되었지."]], [["‘It is remarkable too,’ thought Nicholas: ‘if it had been “Kate”", "'그것도 참 이상해.'"], ["or “Kate Nickleby,”", "니콜라스는 생각했어."], ["I should not have been so much surprised: but “little Kate Nickleby!”’", "'그게 그냥 \"케이트\"나 \"케이트 니클비\"였다면 이렇게까지 놀라지도 않았을 텐데. 하필 \"꼬마 케이트 니클비\"라니!'"]], [["The wine coming at the moment prevented his finishing the sentence.", "마침 그때 포도주가 나오는 바람에 그는 생각을 끝맺지 못했어."], ["He swallowed a glassful and took up the paper again.", "그는 한 잔을 쭉 들이켜고 다시 신문을 집어 들었지."], ["At that instant--", "바로 그 순간—"]], [["‘Little Kate Nickleby!’ cried the voice behind him.", "\"꼬마 케이트 니클비!\" 등 뒤에서 그 목소리가 외쳤어."]], [["‘I was right,’ muttered Nicholas as the paper fell from his hand.", "\"역시 내 생각이 맞았어.\" 니콜라스는 신문을 손에서 떨어뜨리며 중얼거렸어."], ["‘And it was the man I supposed.’", "\"그리고 내가 짐작한 그 사람이야.\""]], [["‘As there was a proper objection to drinking her in heel-taps,’ said the voice, ‘we’ll give her the first glass in the new magnum.", "\"술 찌꺼기로 그 아가씨를 위해 건배하는 건 영 안 될 말이지.\" 그 목소리가 말했어."], ["Little Kate Nickleby!’", "\"그러니 새 큰 병의 첫 잔으로 그 아가씨를 위해 마시자고. 꼬마 케이트 니클비를 위하여!\""]], [["‘Little Kate Nickleby,’ cried the other three.", "\"꼬마 케이트 니클비를 위하여.\" 나머지 셋이 외쳤어."], ["And the glasses were set down empty.", "그리고 빈 잔들이 탁 내려놓아졌지."]], [["Keenly alive to the tone and manner of this slight and careless mention of his sister’s name in a public place, Nicholas fired at once; but he kept himself quiet by a great effort, and did not even turn his head.", "공공장소에서 자기 여동생 이름이 이토록 가볍고 함부로 입에 오르내리는 그 말투와 태도에, 니콜라스는 신경을 곤두세우고 있다가 단번에 발끈했어. 하지만 그는 안간힘을 써서 가만히 참았어. 심지어 고개조차 돌리지 않았지."]], [["‘The jade!’ said the same voice which had spoken before. ‘She’s a true Nickleby--a worthy imitator of her old uncle Ralph--she hangs back to be more sought after--so does he; nothing to be got out of Ralph unless you follow him up, and then the money comes doubly welcome, and the bargain doubly hard, for you’re impatient and he isn’t. Oh! infernal cunning.’", "\"그 깍쟁이!\" 아까 말했던 바로 그 목소리가 말했어. \"정말 영락없는 니클비야. 늙은 삼촌 랄프를 빼다 박은 후계자라니까. 더 애가 닳게 하려고 일부러 뒤로 빼는 거지. 그자도 그렇잖아. 랄프한테선 끈질기게 매달리지 않으면 아무것도 못 얻어 내. 그러다 돈이 들어오면 곱절로 반갑고, 흥정은 곱절로 빡빡하지. 이쪽은 애가 타는데 그쪽은 느긋하니까 말이야. 아아! 지독한 꿍꿍이라니까.\""]], [["‘Infernal cunning,’ echoed two voices.", "\"지독한 꿍꿍이지.\" 두 목소리가 따라 했어."]], [["Nicholas was in a perfect agony as the two elderly gentlemen opposite, rose one after the other and went away, lest they should be the means of his losing one word of what was said.", "니콜라스는 완전히 애가 탔어. 맞은편의 나이 든 신사 둘이 차례로 일어나 나가 버렸거든."], ["But the conversation was suspended as they withdrew, and resumed with even greater freedom when they had left the room.", "그 바람에 한마디라도 놓칠까 봐 마음을 졸였지. 그런데 그들이 물러나는 동안 대화가 잠시 끊겼다가, 그들이 방을 나가고 나자 한층 더 거리낌 없이 다시 이어졌어."]], [["‘I am afraid,’ said the younger gentleman, ‘that the old woman has grown jea-a-lous, and locked her up.", "\"내 생각엔,\" 더 젊은 신사가 말했어."], ["Upon my soul it looks like it.’", "\"그 늙은 여편네가 질투가 나서, 그 애를 가둬 둔 게 아닐까 싶은데. 정말이지 딱 그런 것 같단 말이지.\""]], [["‘If they quarrel and little Nickleby goes home to her mother, so much the better,’ said the first.", "\"둘이 싸워서 꼬마 니클비가 제 어미한테 돌아가 버리면, 오히려 더 잘된 일이지.\" 처음 그 사람이 말했어."], ["‘I can do anything with the old lady.", "\"그 노부인은 내가 뭐든 마음대로 주무를 수 있거든."], ["She’ll believe anything I tell her.’", "내가 무슨 말을 하든 다 믿을 테니까.\""]], [["‘Egad that’s true,’ returned the other voice.", "\"이런, 그건 맞는 말이야.\" 다른 목소리가 대꾸했어."], ["‘Ha, ha, ha!", "\"하하하!"], ["Poor deyvle!’", "가엾은 양반 같으니!\""]], [["The laugh was taken up by the two voices which always came in together, and became general at Mrs. Nickleby’s expense.", "언제나 함께 끼어드는 그 두 목소리가 웃음을 받아 이었고, 웃음판은 니클비 부인을 안주 삼아 한껏 무르익었어."], ["Nicholas turned burning hot with rage, but he commanded himself for the moment, and waited to hear more.", "니콜라스는 분노로 온몸이 화끈거렸어. 하지만 그 순간만큼은 꾹 참고, 더 들으려고 기다렸지."]], [["What he heard need not be repeated here.", "그가 들은 말을 여기서 다시 옮길 필요는 없겠어. 다만 이것만은 알아 둬."], ["Suffice it that as the wine went round he heard enough to acquaint him with the characters and designs of those whose conversation he overhead; to possess him with the full extent of Ralph’s villainy, and the real reason of his own presence being required in London.", "포도주 잔이 도는 동안, 그는 자기가 엿들은 그 대화의 주인공들이 어떤 자들이며 무슨 꿍꿍이를 품고 있는지 충분히 알게 되었어. 랄프의 악행이 어디까지 뻗쳐 있는지, 또 자기가 런던으로 불려 온 진짜 이유가 무엇인지도 낱낱이 깨닫게 되었지."], ["He heard all this and more.", "그는 이 모든 것, 아니 그 이상을 들었어."], ["He heard his sister’s sufferings derided, and her virtuous conduct jeered at and brutally misconstrued; he heard her name bandied from mouth to mouth, and herself made the subject of coarse and insolent wagers, free speech, and licentious jesting.", "여동생이 겪는 고통이 조롱당하고, 그 애의 정숙한 행실이 비웃음을 사며 잔인하게 비틀려 곡해되는 걸 들었어. 그 애의 이름이 이 입 저 입으로 오르내리고, 그 애가 거칠고 무례한 내기와 함부로 지껄이는 말, 음탕한 농담의 거리가 되는 걸 들었지."]], [["The man who had spoken first, led the conversation, and indeed almost engrossed it, being only stimulated from time to time by some slight observation from one or other of his companions.", "맨 처음 입을 연 그 남자가 대화를 이끌었어. 사실상 거의 혼자 다 떠들었지."], ["To him then Nicholas addressed himself when he was sufficiently composed to stand before the party, and force the words from his parched and scorching throat.", "그저 이따금 동료들 가운데 누군가가 슬쩍 한마디 거들어 부추길 뿐이었어. 그래서 니콜라스는, 그 무리 앞에 나서서 바싹 타들어 가는 목구멍에서 억지로 말을 짜낼 만큼 마음을 가다듬고 나자, 바로 그 남자에게 말을 걸었어."]], [["‘Let me have a word with you, sir,’ said Nicholas.", "\"잠깐 얘기 좀 합시다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘With me, sir?’ retorted Sir Mulberry Hawk, eyeing him in disdainful surprise.", "\"나하고 말이오?\" 멀버리 호크 경이 경멸 어린 놀란 눈으로 그를 훑어보며 되받았어."]], [["‘I said with you,’ replied Nicholas, speaking with great difficulty, for his passion choked him.", "\"당신하고라고 했소.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어. 분노가 목을 메이게 해서 말하기가 무척 힘들었지."]], [["‘A mysterious stranger, upon my soul!’ exclaimed Sir Mulberry, raising his wine-glass to his lips, and looking round upon his friends.", "\"이런, 정말이지 수수께끼 같은 낯선 분이로군!\" 멀버리 경이 술잔을 입가로 들어 올리며, 친구들을 둘러보며 외쳤어."]], [["‘Will you step apart with me for a few minutes, or do you refuse?’ said Nicholas sternly.", "\"잠깐 나하고 따로 좀 가겠소, 아니면 거절하는 거요?\" 니콜라스가 매섭게 말했어."]], [["Sir Mulberry merely paused in the act of drinking, and bade him either name his business or leave the table.", "멀버리 경은 그저 술을 마시려던 손길을 멈추고는, 용건을 말하든지 아니면 탁자에서 꺼지라고 했어."]], [["Nicholas drew a card from his pocket, and threw it before him.", "니콜라스는 주머니에서 명함을 한 장 꺼내, 그 앞에 휙 던졌어."]], [["‘There, sir,’ said Nicholas; ‘my business you will guess.’", "\"자, 여기.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"내 용건은 짐작할 수 있겠지.\""]], [["A momentary expression of astonishment, not unmixed with some confusion, appeared in the face of Sir Mulberry as he read the name; but he subdued it in an instant, and tossing the card to Lord Verisopht, who sat opposite, drew a toothpick from a glass before him, and very leisurely applied it to his mouth.", "이름을 읽는 멀버리 경의 얼굴에 한순간 놀라움이 스쳤어. 거기엔 약간의 당혹스러움도 섞여 있었지. 하지만 그는 곧장 그 기색을 가라앉히고는, 명함을 맞은편에 앉은 베리소프트 경에게 휙 던졌어. 그러고는 앞에 놓인 잔에서 이쑤시개를 하나 뽑아, 아주 느긋하게 입에 갖다 댔지."]], [["‘Your name and address?’ said Nicholas, turning paler as his passion kindled.", "\"이름과 주소를?\" 니콜라스가 말했어. 분노가 타오르면서 얼굴이 점점 창백해졌지."]], [["‘I shall give you neither,’ replied Sir Mulberry.", "\"둘 다 말해 줄 생각 없는데.\" 멀버리 경이 대꾸했어."]], [["‘If there is a gentleman in this party,’ said Nicholas, looking round and scarcely able to make his white lips form the words, ‘he will acquaint me with the name and residence of this man.’", "\"이 자리에 신사가 한 분이라도 있다면,\" 니콜라스가 둘러보며 말했어. 핏기 없는 입술로 그 말을 겨우 만들어 내면서 말이야. \"이 사람의 이름과 사는 곳을 내게 알려 주겠지.\""]], [["There was a dead silence.", "쥐 죽은 듯한 침묵이 흘렀어."]], [["‘I am the brother of the young lady who has been the subject of conversation here,’ said Nicholas.", "\"나는 방금 여기서 입에 오르내린 그 아가씨의 오빠요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘I denounce this person as a liar, and impeach him as a coward.", "\"이 사람이 거짓말쟁이임을 고발하고, 비겁한 자임을 폭로하겠소. 만약 이 사람한테 친구가 있다면, 자기 이름을 숨기려는 그 치사한 짓의 망신을 면하게 해 주겠지."], ["If he has a friend here, he will save him the disgrace of the paltry attempt to conceal his name--and utterly useless one--for I will find it out, nor leave him until I have.’", "게다가 다 부질없는 짓이오. 내가 기어이 알아낼 테고, 알아낼 때까지 절대 놓아주지 않을 테니까.\""]], [["Sir Mulberry looked at him contemptuously, and, addressing his companions, said--", "멀버리 경은 그를 경멸하듯 바라보더니, 동료들에게 이렇게 말했어—"]], [["‘Let the fellow talk, I have nothing serious to say to boys of his station; and his pretty sister shall save him a broken head, if he talks till midnight.’", "\"이 친구가 떠들든 말든 내버려 둬. 이런 신분의 애송이한테 진지하게 할 말은 없으니까. 자정까지 떠들어 댄들, 제 예쁜 누이 덕에 머리통이 깨지는 건 면하게 될 거다.\""]], [["‘You are a base and spiritless scoundrel!’ said Nicholas, ‘and shall be proclaimed so to the world. I WILL know you; I will follow you home if you walk the streets till morning.’", "\"넌 비열하고 줏대 없는 악당이다!\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"온 세상에 그렇게 알려질 거야. 기어이 네가 누군지 알아내겠어. 네가 아침까지 거리를 걷는다 해도, 네 집까지 따라가고 말 테니까.\""]], [["Sir Mulberry’s hand involuntarily closed upon the decanter, and he seemed for an instant about to launch it at the head of his challenger.", "멀버리 경의 손이 저도 모르게 술병을 움켜쥐었어. 그러고는 한순간, 그걸 도전자의 머리에 내던질 듯한 기세였지."], ["But he only filled his glass, and laughed in derision.", "하지만 그는 그저 잔을 채우고는 비웃듯 껄껄 웃었어."]], [["Nicholas sat himself down, directly opposite to the party, and, summoning the waiter, paid his bill.", "니콜라스는 그 무리 바로 맞은편에 자리를 잡고 앉았어. 그러고는 웨이터를 불러 자기 술값을 치렀지."]], [["‘Do you know that person’s name?’ he inquired of the man in an audible voice; pointing out Sir Mulberry as he put the question.", "\"저 사람 이름을 아시오?\" 니콜라스가 또렷이 들리는 목소리로 웨이터에게 물었어. 그러면서 멀버리 경을 손가락으로 가리켰지."]], [["Sir Mulberry laughed again, and the two voices which had always spoken together, echoed the laugh; but rather feebly.", "멀버리 경이 다시 껄껄 웃었어. 그러자 언제나 함께 떠들던 두 목소리도 그 웃음을 따라 했지. 다만 좀 힘없는 웃음이었어."]], [["‘That gentleman, sir?’ replied the waiter, who, no doubt, knew his cue, and answered with just as little respect, and just as much impertinence as he could safely show: ‘no, sir, I do not, sir.’", "\"저 신사분 말입니까, 선생님?\" 웨이터가 대답했어. 분명 자기가 어떻게 처신해야 할지 잘 아는 자였지. 그래서 탈 없이 보일 수 있는 한 최대한 존중을 덜 담고, 최대한 건방을 떨며 대답했어. \"아니요, 선생님, 모릅니다, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Here, you sir,’ cried Sir Mulberry, as the man was retiring; ‘do you know THAT person’s name?’", "\"이봐, 자네.\" 웨이터가 물러나려는데 멀버리 경이 불렀어. \"자네는 저자 이름을 아나?\""]], [["‘Name, sir?", "\"이름요, 선생님?"], ["No, sir.’", "모릅니다, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Then you’ll find it there,’ said Sir Mulberry, throwing Nicholas’s card towards him; ‘and when you have made yourself master of it, put that piece of pasteboard in the fire--do you hear me?’", "\"그럼 거기 보면 나와 있을 거야.\" 멀버리 경이 니콜라스의 명함을 그에게 휙 던지며 말했어. \"다 외우고 나거든, 그 판때기 쪼가리는 불 속에 던져 버려. 알아들었나?\""]], [["The man grinned, and, looking doubtfully at Nicholas, compromised the matter by sticking the card in the chimney-glass.", "웨이터는 씩 웃었어. 그러고는 니콜라스를 미심쩍은 듯 흘끔거리더니, 명함을 벽난로 거울 틈에 끼워 두는 걸로 어정쩡하게 마무리했어."], ["Having done this, he retired.", "그러고는 물러났지."]], [["Nicholas folded his arms, and biting his lip, sat perfectly quiet; sufficiently expressing by his manner, however, a firm determination to carry his threat of following Sir Mulberry home, into steady execution.", "니콜라스는 팔짱을 끼고 입술을 깨문 채, 아주 잠자코 앉아 있었어. 그래도 그 태도만큼은 분명히 말해 주고 있었지. 멀버리 경을 집까지 따라가겠다는 그 으름장을 기어이 끝까지 실행에 옮기겠다는 굳은 결심을 말이야."]], [["It was evident from the tone in which the younger member of the party appeared to remonstrate with his friend, that he objected to this course of proceeding, and urged him to comply with the request which Nicholas had made.", "그 무리 가운데 더 젊은 자가 친구에게 말리는 투로 무어라 하는 걸로 봐서, 그가 이런 식의 처사에 반대하며 니콜라스의 요구를 들어주라고 권하는 게 분명했어. 하지만 멀버리 경은 술이 완전히 깨지도 않은 데다, 시무룩하니 고집스럽게 버티는 상태였지."], ["Sir Mulberry, however, who was not quite sober, and who was in a sullen and dogged state of obstinacy, soon silenced the representations of his weak young friend, and further seemed--as if to save himself from a repetition of them--to insist on being left alone.", "그래서 약해 빠진 젊은 친구의 만류를 금세 입막음해 버렸어. 게다가 — 마치 그 만류를 다시 듣기 싫다는 듯 — 자기를 혼자 내버려 두라고 우기는 것 같았지."], ["However this might have been, the young gentleman and the two who had always spoken together, actually rose to go after a short interval, and presently retired, leaving their friend alone with Nicholas.", "어쨌든 잠시 뒤에 그 젊은 신사와 언제나 함께 떠들던 두 사람이 정말로 일어나 가 버렸어. 그러고는 곧 물러가서, 친구를 니콜라스와 단둘이 남겨 두었지."]], [["It will be very readily supposed that to one in the condition of Nicholas, the minutes appeared to move with leaden wings indeed, and that their progress did not seem the more rapid from the monotonous ticking of a French clock, or the shrill sound of its little bell which told the quarters. But there he sat; and in his old seat on the opposite side of the room reclined Sir Mulberry Hawk, with his legs upon the cushion, and his handkerchief thrown negligently over his knees: finishing his magnum of claret with the utmost coolness and indifference.", "니콜라스 같은 처지에 놓인 사람한테 그 몇 분이 정말이지 납덩이 같은 날개를 단 듯 더디게 흐르는 듯 느껴졌으리라는 건, 누구라도 쉽게 짐작할 수 있을 거야. 게다가 프랑스제 시계의 단조로운 똑딱 소리도, 십오 분마다 울리는 작은 종의 날카로운 소리도, 그 시간이 더 빨리 흐르게 해 주진 못했지. 그래도 그는 거기 앉아 있었어. 그리고 방 맞은편 제 자리에는 멀버리 호크 경이 비스듬히 기대어 있었지. 두 다리를 방석 위에 올려놓고, 손수건을 무릎 위에 아무렇게나 걸친 채로 말이야. 더없이 차갑고 무심하게 클라레 큰 병을 비우면서."]], [["Thus they remained in perfect silence for upwards of an hour--Nicholas would have thought for three hours at least, but that the little bell had only gone four times.", "이렇게 둘은 한 시간 넘게 완전한 침묵 속에 있었어. 니콜라스는 적어도 세 시간은 흘렀다고 여겼겠지만, 작은 종은 겨우 네 번밖에 울리지 않았지."], ["Twice or thrice he looked angrily and impatiently round; but there was Sir Mulberry in the same attitude, putting his glass to his lips from time to time, and looking vacantly at the wall, as if he were wholly ignorant of the presence of any living person.", "두세 번 그는 화가 나고 조바심이 나서 둘러보았어. 그런데 멀버리 경은 여전히 똑같은 자세로 이따금 잔을 입에 갖다 대고, 마치 살아 있는 사람이라곤 아무도 없는 듯 벽을 멍하니 바라보고 있었어."]], [["At length he yawned, stretched himself, and rose; walked coolly to the glass, and having surveyed himself therein, turned round and honoured Nicholas with a long and contemptuous stare.", "마침내 그는 하품을 하고는 기지개를 켜며 일어났어. 그러고는 느긋하게 거울 앞으로 걸어가 자기 모습을 살피더니, 몸을 돌려 니콜라스에게 길고 경멸 어린 눈길을 던지는 영광을 베풀었지."], ["Nicholas stared again with right good-will; Sir Mulberry shrugged his shoulders, smiled slightly, rang the bell, and ordered the waiter to help him on with his greatcoat.", "니콜라스도 기꺼이 마주 노려봤어. 멀버리 경은 어깨를 으쓱하고 살짝 웃더니, 종을 울려 웨이터에게 외투 입는 걸 거들라고 시켰어."]], [["The man did so, and held the door open.", "웨이터가 그렇게 해 주고는 문을 열어 잡았어."]], [["‘Don’t wait,’ said Sir Mulberry; and they were alone again.", "\"기다릴 것 없네.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어. 그리고 둘은 다시 단둘이 남았지."]], [["Sir Mulberry took several turns up and down the room, whistling carelessly all the time; stopped to finish the last glass of claret which he had poured out a few minutes before, walked again, put on his hat, adjusted it by the glass, drew on his gloves, and, at last, walked slowly out. Nicholas, who had been fuming and chafing until he was nearly wild, darted from his seat, and followed him: so closely, that before the door had swung upon its hinges after Sir Mulberry’s passing out, they stood side by side in the street together.", "멀버리 경은 방 안을 몇 번이나 왔다 갔다 했어. 그러는 내내 무심하게 휘파람을 불었지. 몇 분 전에 따라 두었던 클라레 마지막 잔을 마저 비우려고 멈췄다가, 다시 걷고, 모자를 쓰고, 거울에 비추어 매만지고, 장갑을 끼고는, 마침내 천천히 걸어 나갔어. 니콜라스는 거의 미쳐 버릴 만큼 부글부글 속을 끓이고 있다가, 자리에서 박차고 일어나 그를 뒤따랐어. 어찌나 바싹 따라붙었던지, 멀버리 경이 빠져나간 뒤 문이 채 경첩에 부딪쳐 닫히기도 전에, 둘은 거리에 나란히 서 있었지."]], [["There was a private cabriolet in waiting; the groom opened the apron, and jumped out to the horse’s head.", "마차 한 대가 대기하고 있었어. 마부가 덮개를 열고는, 말 머리를 잡으려고 뛰어내렸지."]], [["‘Will you make yourself known to me?’ asked Nicholas in a suppressed voice.", "\"내게 정체를 밝히겠소?\" 니콜라스가 억누른 목소리로 물었어."]], [["‘No,’ replied the other fiercely, and confirming the refusal with an oath.", "\"싫다.\" 상대가 사납게 대꾸했어."], ["‘No.’", "그러고는 욕을 한마디 보태어 거절을 못 박았지. \"싫다고.\""]], [["‘If you trust to your horse’s speed, you will find yourself mistaken,’ said Nicholas.", "\"네 말의 속도만 믿었다간 큰코다칠걸.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘I will accompany you.", "\"내가 따라갈 테니까."], ["By Heaven I will, if I hang on to the foot-board.’", "맹세컨대, 발판에 매달려서라도 따라가고 말 테다.\""]], [["‘You shall be horsewhipped if you do,’ returned Sir Mulberry.", "\"그랬다간 채찍질을 당하게 될 거다.\" 멀버리 경이 받아쳤어."]], [["‘You are a villain,’ said Nicholas.", "\"넌 악당이야.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘You are an errand-boy for aught I know,’ said Sir Mulberry Hawk.", "\"내가 알 게 뭐냐, 넌 심부름꾼 따위겠지.\" 멀버리 호크 경이 말했어."]], [["‘I am the son of a country gentleman,’ returned Nicholas, ‘your equal in birth and education, and your superior I trust in everything besides.", "\"난 시골 신사의 아들이다.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어."], ["I tell you again, Miss Nickleby is my sister.", "\"출신이나 배움에서 너와 대등하고, 그 밖의 모든 면에서는 너보다 위라고 믿는다."], ["Will you or will you not answer for your unmanly and brutal conduct?’", "다시 말하지만, 니클비 양은 내 여동생이야. 네 사내답지 못하고 잔인한 행실에 대해, 책임을 지겠나 안 지겠나?\""]], [["‘To a proper champion--yes.", "\"제대로 된 상대에게라면—그러지."], ["To you--no,’ returned Sir Mulberry, taking the reins in his hand.", "너 같은 놈한테는—싫다.\" 멀버리 경이 고삐를 손에 쥐며 대꾸했어."], ["‘Stand out of the way, dog.", "\"비켜라, 이 개 같은 놈."], ["William, let go her head.’", "윌리엄, 말 머리를 놔라.\""]], [["‘You had better not,’ cried Nicholas, springing on the step as Sir Mulberry jumped in, and catching at the reins. ‘He has no command over the horse, mind. You shall not go--you shall not, I swear--till you have told me who you are.’", "\"그러지 않는 게 좋을걸.\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어. 멀버리 경이 마차에 뛰어오르는 순간, 그도 발판으로 뛰어올라 고삐를 움켜잡았지. \"저자는 말을 다룰 줄도 모른다고, 알아 둬. 못 가. 못 간다, 맹세코. 네가 누군지 말하기 전엔 말이야.\""]], [["The groom hesitated, for the mare, who was a high-spirited animal and thorough-bred, plunged so violently that he could scarcely hold her.", "마부가 머뭇거렸어. 그 암말은 혈기 넘치는 순종이라, 어찌나 사납게 날뛰는지 거의 붙들고 있을 수도 없었거든."]], [["‘Leave go, I tell you!’ thundered his master.", "\"놓으라니까!\" 그의 주인이 천둥처럼 호통쳤어."]], [["The man obeyed.", "마부가 시키는 대로 했어."], ["The animal reared and plunged as though it would dash the carriage into a thousand pieces, but Nicholas, blind to all sense of danger, and conscious of nothing but his fury, still maintained his place and his hold upon the reins.", "그러자 말은 마차를 산산조각 내 버리기라도 할 듯 앞발을 치켜들고 날뛰었지. 하지만 니콜라스는 위험 따윈 안중에도 없이, 오직 자기 분노밖에 의식하지 못한 채 여전히 제자리를 지키며 고삐를 움켜쥐고 있었어."]], [["‘Will you unclasp your hand?’", "\"손을 놓지 못하겠나?\""]], [["‘Will you tell me who you are?’", "\"네가 누군지 말하겠나?\""]], [["‘No!’\n‘No!’", "\"싫다!\""]], [["In less time than the quickest tongue could tell it, these words were exchanged, and Sir Mulberry shortening his whip, applied it furiously to the head and shoulders of Nicholas. It was broken in the struggle; Nicholas gained the heavy handle, and with it laid open one side of his antagonist’s face from the eye to the lip. He saw the gash; knew that the mare had darted off at a wild mad gallop; a hundred lights danced in his eyes, and he felt himself flung violently upon the ground.", "가장 빠른 혀로도 다 옮기지 못할 만큼 순식간에 이 말들이 오갔어. 그러더니 멀버리 경이 채찍을 짧게 고쳐 쥐고는, 니콜라스의 머리와 어깨를 미친 듯이 후려쳤지. 채찍은 몸싸움 중에 부러졌어. 니콜라스는 그 묵직한 손잡이를 빼앗아, 그걸로 상대의 한쪽 얼굴을 눈에서 입까지 쩍 갈라놓았어. 그는 그 상처를 봤어. 암말이 미친 듯이 사납게 내달리기 시작한 걸 알았지. 눈앞에 수백 개의 불빛이 어지럽게 춤추더니, 자기 몸이 땅바닥에 거세게 내동댕이쳐지는 게 느껴졌어."]], [["He was giddy and sick, but staggered to his feet directly, roused by the loud shouts of the men who were tearing up the street, and screaming to those ahead to clear the way. He was conscious of a torrent of people rushing quickly by--looking up, could discern the cabriolet whirled along the foot-pavement with frightful rapidity--then heard a loud cry, the smashing of some heavy body, and the breaking of glass--and then the crowd closed in in the distance, and he could see or hear no more.", "그는 어질어질하고 속이 메스꺼웠어. 하지만 곧장 비틀거리며 일어섰지. 거리를 마구 내달리며, 앞쪽 사람들에게 길을 비키라고 고래고래 소리치는 사람들 때문에 정신이 번쩍 들었거든. 사람들의 물결이 빠르게 곁을 스쳐 지나가는 게 느껴졌어. 고개를 들어 보니, 마차가 무시무시한 속도로 인도 위를 휩쓸며 달려가는 게 어렴풋이 보였지. 그러더니 커다란 비명이, 무언가 무거운 것이 부서지는 소리가, 그리고 유리 깨지는 소리가 들렸어. 그러고는 저 멀리서 군중이 빙 둘러 모여들었고, 그는 더 이상 아무것도 보거나 들을 수 없었어."]], [["The general attention had been entirely directed from himself to the person in the carriage, and he was quite alone. Rightly judging that under such circumstances it would be madness to follow, he turned down a bye-street in search of the nearest coach-stand, finding after a minute or two that he was reeling like a drunken man, and aware for the first time of a stream of blood that was trickling down his face and breast.", "사람들의 관심은 온통 그에게서 마차 안의 그 사람에게로 쏠려 있었어. 그래서 그는 완전히 혼자였지. 이런 상황에서 뒤쫓는 건 미친 짓이라고 옳게 판단한 그는, 가장 가까운 마차 정류장을 찾아 골목길로 접어들었어. 그러고는 일이 분쯤 지나서야 자기가 술 취한 사람처럼 비틀거리고 있다는 걸 알아챘지. 그리고 그제야 처음으로, 얼굴과 가슴을 타고 줄줄 흘러내리는 핏줄기를 깨달았어."]], [["# CHAPTER 33", "# CHAPTER 33"]], [["In which Mr.", "스마이크와 뉴먼 노그스는 약속한 시간보다 한참 일찍 안달이 나서 집으로 돌아와 있었어."], ["Ralph Nickleby is relieved, by a very expeditious Process, from all Commerce with his Relations\nSmike and Newman Noggs, who in his impatience had returned home long before the time agreed upon, sat before the fire, listening anxiously to every footstep on the stairs, and the slightest sound that stirred within the house, for the approach of Nicholas.", "둘은 난롯가에 앉아 계단에서 들려오는 발소리 하나하나에, 집 안에서 부스럭거리는 작은 소리에까지 신경을 곤두세우며 니콜라스가 오기를 기다렸지. 시간은 자꾸 흘렀고, 어느덧 밤이 깊어 갔어."], ["Time had worn on, and it was growing late.", "니콜라스는 한 시간 안에 돌아오겠다고 했었거든."], ["He had promised to be back in an hour; and his prolonged absence began to excite considerable alarm in the minds of both, as was abundantly testified by the blank looks they cast upon each other at every new disappointment.", "그런데 이렇게 늦어지니 두 사람 마음에 불안이 점점 커졌지. 매번 기대가 어긋날 때마다 서로 멍하니 마주보는 그 표정만 봐도 그게 다 드러났어."]], [["At length a coach was heard to stop, and Newman ran out to light Nicholas up the stairs.", "마침내 마차가 멈춰 서는 소리가 들렸어. 뉴먼은 니콜라스가 계단을 올라오는 걸 밝혀 주려고 밖으로 뛰어나갔지."], ["Beholding him in the trim described at the conclusion of the last chapter, he stood aghast in wonder and consternation.", "그런데 지난 장 끝에 나온 그 꼴을 한 니콜라스를 보고는, 놀라고 기가 막혀서 그 자리에 멍하니 굳어 버렸어."]], [["‘Don’t be alarmed,’ said Nicholas, hurrying him back into the room.", "\"놀라지 말아요.\" 니콜라스가 그를 떠밀어 방 안으로 들이며 말했어."], ["‘There is no harm done, beyond what a basin of water can repair.’", "\"물 한 대야면 씻어 낼 정도지, 큰일은 없으니까요.\""]], [["‘No harm!’ cried Newman, passing his hands hastily over the back and arms of Nicholas, as if to assure himself that he had broken no bones.", "\"큰일이 없다고요!\" 뉴먼이 소리치며, 니콜라스의 등과 팔을 황급히 더듬어 봤어."], ["‘What have you been doing?’", "뼈라도 부러지지 않았나 확인하려는 거였지. \"대체 무슨 일이 있었던 거예요?\""]], [["‘I know all,’ interrupted Nicholas; ‘I have heard a part, and guessed the rest.", "\"전 다 알고 있어요.\" 니콜라스가 말을 잘랐어."], ["But before I remove one jot of these stains, I must hear the whole from you.", "\"한 부분은 들었고, 나머지는 짐작했죠. 하지만 이 얼룩을 한 점이라도 닦아 내기 전에, 당신한테서 전부 다 들어야겠어요."], ["You see I am collected.", "보다시피 저는 침착해요. 마음도 굳혔고요."], ["My resolution is taken.", "자, 친구, 다 털어놔요."], ["Now, my good friend, speak out; for the time for any palliation or concealment is past, and nothing will avail Ralph Nickleby now.’", "이제 와서 둘러대거나 숨길 때는 지났으니까. 그리고 이제 랄프 니클비한테는 아무것도 소용없어요.\""]], [["‘Your dress is torn in several places; you walk lame, and I am sure you are suffering pain,’ said Newman.", "\"옷이 여기저기 찢어졌어요. 다리도 절고 있고, 분명 아픈 데가 있을 텐데요.\""], ["‘Let me see to your hurts first.’", "뉴먼이 말했어. \"먼저 다친 데부터 봅시다.\""]], [["‘I have no hurts to see to, beyond a little soreness and stiffness that will soon pass off,’ said Nicholas, seating himself with some difficulty.", "\"좀 쑤시고 뻐근한 거 말곤 따로 볼 상처도 없어요. 그것도 곧 가라앉을 거고요.\""], ["‘But if I had fractured every limb, and still preserved my senses, you should not bandage one till you had told me what I have the right to know.", "니콜라스가 힘겹게 자리에 앉으며 말했어. \"하지만 설령 팔다리가 다 부러졌어도 정신만 멀쩡하다면, 내가 마땅히 알아야 할 걸 다 말해 주기 전엔 붕대 한 장도 못 감게 할 거예요."], ["Come,’ said Nicholas, giving his hand to Noggs.", "자.\" 니콜라스가 노그스에게 손을 내밀며 말했어."], ["‘You had a sister of your own, you told me once, who died before you fell into misfortune.", "\"언젠가 당신한테 누이가 있었다고 했죠. 당신이 불행해지기 전에 세상을 떠났다고요."], ["Now think of her, and tell me, Newman.’", "그 누이를 생각해서, 말해 줘요, 뉴먼.\""]], [["‘Yes, I will, I will,’ said Noggs.", "\"네, 할게요, 할게요.\" 노그스가 말했어."], ["‘I’ll tell you the whole truth.’", "\"사실을 빠짐없이 다 말해 드리죠.\""]], [["Newman did so.", "뉴먼은 그렇게 했어."], ["Nicholas nodded his head from time to time, as it corroborated the particulars he had already gleaned; but he fixed his eyes upon the fire, and did not look round once.", "니콜라스는 이미 주워들어 알고 있던 부분과 맞아떨어질 때마다 가끔씩 고개를 끄덕였지. 하지만 두 눈은 난롯불에 꽂은 채, 한 번도 돌아보지 않았어."]], [["His recital ended, Newman insisted upon his young friend’s stripping off his coat and allowing whatever injuries he had received to be properly tended. Nicholas, after some opposition, at length consented, and, while some pretty severe bruises on his arms and shoulders were being rubbed with oil and vinegar, and various other efficacious remedies which Newman borrowed from the different lodgers, related in what manner they had been received. The recital made a strong impression on the warm imagination of Newman; for when Nicholas came to the violent part of the quarrel, he rubbed so hard, as to occasion him the most exquisite pain, which he would not have exhibited, however, for the world, it being perfectly clear that, for the moment, Newman was operating on Sir Mulberry Hawk, and had quite lost sight of his real patient.", "이야기가 끝나자, 뉴먼은 어린 친구더러 외투를 벗고 다친 데를 제대로 치료받으라고 우겼어. 니콜라스는 몇 번 마다했지만 마침내 그러기로 했지. 그리고 팔과 어깨에 꽤 심하게 든 멍을 기름과 식초로, 또 뉴먼이 이런저런 하숙인들한테 빌려 온 갖가지 잘 듣는 약으로 문지르는 동안, 니콜라스는 그 멍이 어쩌다 생긴 건지 이야기했어. 그 이야기는 뉴먼의 뜨거운 상상력에 강한 인상을 남겼지. 니콜라스가 싸움의 격렬한 대목에 이르자, 뉴먼이 어찌나 세게 문질러 댔던지 니콜라스는 견딜 수 없이 아팠거든. 그래도 니콜라스는 죽어도 그 아픔을 내색하지 않았어. 그 순간 뉴먼이 멀버리 호크 경한테 손을 쓰고 있는 거나 다름없었고, 정작 진짜 환자는 까맣게 잊고 있었던 게 너무나 뻔했으니까."]], [["This martyrdom over, Nicholas arranged with Newman that while he was otherwise occupied next morning, arrangements should be made for his mother’s immediately quitting her present residence, and also for dispatching Miss La Creevy to break the intelligence to her.", "이 고행이 끝나자, 니콜라스는 뉴먼과 의논을 했어. 다음 날 아침 자기가 다른 일로 바쁜 사이에, 어머니가 지금 사는 집에서 곧장 떠날 수 있도록 채비를 해 두고, 라 크리비 양을 보내 어머니한테 그 소식을 전하기로 한 거지."], ["He then wrapped himself in Smike’s greatcoat, and repaired to the inn where they were to pass the night, and where (after writing a few lines to Ralph, the delivery of which was to be intrusted to Newman next day), he endeavoured to obtain the repose of which he stood so much in need.", "그러고 나서 니콜라스는 스마이크의 외투를 둘러 입고, 둘이 하룻밤 묵기로 한 여관으로 갔어. 그리고 거기서 (랄프에게 보낼 몇 줄을 써 두고, 그걸 다음 날 뉴먼이 전하기로 맡긴 뒤) 그토록 절실히 필요했던 휴식을 청해 보려 애썼지."]], [["Drunken men, they say, may roll down precipices, and be quite unconscious of any serious personal inconvenience when their reason returns.", "사람들 말로는, 술 취한 사람은 벼랑에서 굴러떨어져도 정신이 돌아왔을 때 별다른 큰 탈을 못 느낀다고들 해."], ["The remark may possibly apply to injuries received in other kinds of violent excitement: certain it is, that although Nicholas experienced some pain on first awakening next morning, he sprung out of bed as the clock struck seven, with very little difficulty, and was soon as much on the alert as if nothing had occurred.", "어쩌면 이 말은 다른 격한 흥분 상태에서 입은 상처에도 들어맞을지 몰라. 아무튼 분명한 건, 니콜라스가 다음 날 아침 처음 눈을 떴을 땐 좀 아프긴 했지만, 시계가 일곱 시를 칠 때 별 어려움 없이 침대에서 벌떡 일어났고, 곧 아무 일도 없었던 것처럼 기운을 차렸다는 거야."]], [["Merely looking into Smike’s room, and telling him that Newman Noggs would call for him very shortly, Nicholas descended into the street, and calling a hackney coach, bade the man drive to Mrs.", "니콜라스는 스마이크의 방을 잠깐 들여다보며 뉴먼 노그스가 곧 데리러 올 거라고만 일러두고는, 거리로 내려갔어."], ["Wititterly’s, according to the direction which Newman had given him on the previous night.", "그러고는 삯마차를 불러, 전날 밤 뉴먼이 일러 준 대로 위티털리 부인 댁으로 가자고 했지."]], [["It wanted a quarter to eight when they reached Cadogan Place.", "캐도건 플레이스에 다다랐을 때는 여덟 시 십오 분 전이었어."], ["Nicholas began to fear that no one might be stirring at that early hour, when he was relieved by the sight of a female servant, employed in cleaning the door-steps.", "니콜라스는 이렇게 이른 시각에 일어나 있는 사람이 아무도 없으면 어쩌나 걱정이 들기 시작했지. 그런데 마침 문간 계단을 닦고 있는 하녀가 눈에 띄어 마음을 놓았어."], ["By this functionary he was referred to the doubtful page, who appeared with dishevelled hair and a very warm and glossy face, as of a page who had just got out of bed.", "그 하녀는 그를 미덥잖은 사환에게 넘겼는데, 그 사환은 헝클어진 머리에 막 잠자리에서 빠져나온 사환답게 따끈따끈하고 번들거리는 얼굴로 나타났지."]], [["By this young gentleman he was informed that Miss Nickleby was then taking her morning’s walk in the gardens before the house.", "이 어린 신사한테서 니콜라스는 니클비 양이 지금 집 앞 정원에서 아침 산책을 하고 있다는 말을 들었어."], ["On the question being propounded whether he could go and find her, the page desponded and thought not; but being stimulated with a shilling, the page grew sanguine and thought he could.", "가서 누이를 찾아와 줄 수 있겠느냐고 묻자, 사환은 시무룩해지며 못 하겠다는 눈치였지. 하지만 일 실링이 쥐어지자 사환은 금세 밝아지더니 할 수 있을 것 같다고 했어."]], [["‘Say to Miss Nickleby that her brother is here, and in great haste to see her,’ said Nicholas.", "\"니클비 양한테 오빠가 와 있다고, 몹시 급히 보고 싶어 한다고 전해 주게.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["The plated buttons disappeared with an alacrity most unusual to them, and Nicholas paced the room in a state of feverish agitation which made the delay even of a minute insupportable. He soon heard a light footstep which he well knew, and before he could advance to meet her, Kate had fallen on his neck and burst into tears.", "도금한 단추들이 평소답지 않게 날쌔게 사라졌어. 니콜라스는 안절부절못하며 방 안을 서성였지. 일 분의 기다림조차 견디기 힘든 상태였거든. 곧 그가 잘 아는 가벼운 발소리가 들렸어. 그리고 니콜라스가 미처 마중 나가기도 전에, 케이트가 그의 목을 끌어안고 울음을 터뜨렸지."]], [["‘My darling girl,’ said Nicholas as he embraced her.", "\"내 사랑하는 동생아.\" 니콜라스가 케이트를 품에 안으며 말했어."], ["‘How pale you are!’", "\"얼굴이 왜 이렇게 핼쑥하니!\""]], [["‘I have been so unhappy here, dear brother,’ sobbed poor Kate; ‘so very, very miserable. Do not leave me here, dear Nicholas, or I shall die of a broken heart.’", "\"여기서 너무 불행했어, 오빠.\" 가엾은 케이트가 흐느꼈어. \"정말, 정말 비참했어. 날 여기 두고 가지 마, 오빠. 안 그러면 가슴이 미어져서 죽고 말 거야.\""]], [["‘I will leave you nowhere,’ answered Nicholas--‘never again, Kate,’ he cried, moved in spite of himself as he folded her to his heart. ‘Tell me that I acted for the best. Tell me that we parted because I feared to bring misfortune on your head; that it was a trial to me no less than to yourself, and that if I did wrong it was in ignorance of the world and unknowingly.’", "\"어디에도 널 두고 가지 않을 거야.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"다시는 안 그래, 케이트.\" 그는 케이트를 가슴에 꼭 끌어안으며, 자기도 모르게 마음이 북받쳐 외쳤지. \"내가 그때 최선을 다한 거라고 말해 줘. 우리가 헤어졌던 건 내가 네 머리 위에 불행을 끌어올까 봐 두려워서였다고. 그게 너만큼이나 나한테도 힘든 시련이었고, 혹시 내가 잘못했다면 그건 세상 물정을 몰라서, 모르고 한 일이었다고 말해 줘.\""]], [["‘Why should I tell you what we know so well?’ returned Kate soothingly.", "\"우리가 그렇게 잘 아는 걸 왜 나더러 말하라는 거야?\" 케이트가 달래듯 대꾸했어."], ["‘Nicholas--dear Nicholas--how can you give way thus?’", "\"오빠, 사랑하는 오빠, 어쩌면 이렇게 무너질 수가 있어?\""]], [["‘It is such bitter reproach to me to know what you have undergone,’ returned her brother; ‘to see you so much altered, and yet so kind and patient--God!’ cried Nicholas, clenching his fist and suddenly changing his tone and manner, ‘it sets my whole blood on fire again. You must leave here with me directly; you should not have slept here last night, but that I knew all this too late. To whom can I speak, before we drive away?’", "\"네가 어떤 일을 겪었는지 아는 게 나한테는 이루 말할 수 없는 자책이야.\" 오빠가 대답했어. \"이렇게 많이 변한 너를 보면서도, 너는 여전히 이렇게 다정하고 잘 참아 내고 있으니. 맙소사!\" 니콜라스는 주먹을 불끈 쥐며 갑자기 말투와 태도를 바꿔 소리쳤어. \"다시 온몸의 피가 다 끓어오르는 것 같아. 너는 지금 당장 나랑 여기서 나가야 해. 어젯밤에도 여기서 자게 하면 안 되는 거였는데, 내가 이걸 다 너무 늦게 알았어. 우리가 떠나기 전에, 내가 누구한테 말을 하면 되지?\""]], [["This question was most opportunely put, for at that instant Mr Wititterly walked in, and to him Kate introduced her brother, who at once announced his purpose, and the impossibility of deferring it.", "이 물음은 더없이 때맞춰 나왔어. 바로 그 순간 위티털리 씨가 들어왔거든. 케이트가 그에게 오빠를 소개하자, 니콜라스는 곧바로 자기 목적을 밝히고, 그걸 미룰 수 없다는 뜻을 전했어."]], [["‘The quarter’s notice,’ said Mr.", "\"분기 통보 기간이,\""], ["Wititterly, with the gravity of a man on the right side, ‘is not yet half expired.", "떳떳한 처지에 선 사람다운 근엄한 태도로 위티털리 씨가 말했어. \"아직 절반도 안 지났습니다."], ["Therefore--’", "그러니까—\""]], [["‘Therefore,’ interposed Nicholas, ‘the quarter’s salary must be lost, sir. You will excuse this extreme haste, but circumstances require that I should immediately remove my sister, and I have not a moment’s time to lose. Whatever she brought here I will send for, if you will allow me, in the course of the day.’", "\"그러니까,\" 니콜라스가 끼어들었어. \"분기 급료는 못 받게 되겠지요, 선생님. 이렇게 서두르는 걸 양해해 주십시오. 사정이 사정인지라 제 누이를 지금 곧장 데려가야 합니다. 한순간도 지체할 틈이 없어요. 누이가 여기에 가져온 물건은, 허락해 주신다면 오늘 안으로 사람을 보내 찾아가겠습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Wititterly bowed, but offered no opposition to Kate’s immediate departure; with which, indeed, he was rather gratified than otherwise, Sir Tumley Snuffim having given it as his opinion, that she rather disagreed with Mrs.", "위티털리 씨는 고개를 숙였지만, 케이트가 당장 떠나는 데는 반대하지 않았어."], ["Wititterly’s constitution.", "사실 그는 오히려 반기는 쪽이었지. 텀리 스너핌 경이, 케이트가 위티털리 부인의 체질에 좀 안 맞는 것 같다는 의견을 내놓았었거든."]], [["‘With regard to the trifle of salary that is due,’ said Mr. Wititterly, ‘I will’--here he was interrupted by a violent fit of coughing--‘I will--owe it to Miss Nickleby.’", "\"밀린 자잘한 급료 문제는,\" 위티털리 씨가 말했어. \"제가—\" 여기서 그는 격한 기침에 말이 끊겼어. \"제가— 니클비 양한테 빚진 걸로 해 두지요.\""]], [["Mr. Wititterly, it should be observed, was accustomed to owe small accounts, and to leave them owing.", "짚고 넘어가야 할 게, 위티털리 씨는 자잘한 외상을 지고는 그걸 그냥 외상으로 남겨 두는 데 익숙한 사람이었어."], ["All men have some little pleasant way of their own; and this was Mr.", "사람은 누구나 자기만의 자그마한 즐기는 방식이 있잖아."], ["Wititterly’s.", "이게 위티털리 씨의 방식이었지."]], [["‘If you please,’ said Nicholas.", "\"그렇게 하시죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["And once more offering a hurried apology for so sudden a departure, he hurried Kate into the vehicle, and bade the man drive with all speed into the city.", "그러고는 이렇게 갑작스레 떠나게 된 걸 다시 한번 황급히 사과하고는, 케이트를 마차에 서둘러 태웠어. 그리고 마부에게 시내로 한껏 속력을 내 달라고 일렀지."]], [["To the city they went accordingly, with all the speed the hackney coach could make; and as the horses happened to live at Whitechapel and to be in the habit of taking their breakfast there, when they breakfasted at all, they performed the journey with greater expedition than could reasonably have been expected.", "그렇게 두 사람은 삯마차가 낼 수 있는 최대한의 속력으로 시내로 향했어. 그런데 마침 그 말들이 화이트채플에 살았고, 아침을 먹기는 먹는 날이면 거기서 먹는 버릇이 있었던지라, 으레 기대할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 빠르게 길을 내달렸지."]], [["Nicholas sent Kate upstairs a few minutes before him, that his unlooked-for appearance might not alarm his mother, and when the way had been paved, presented himself with much duty and affection. Newman had not been idle, for there was a little cart at the door, and the effects were hurrying out already.", "니콜라스는 자기보다 몇 분 먼저 케이트를 위층으로 올려보냈어. 자기가 불쑥 나타나 어머니를 놀라게 하지 않으려는 거였지. 그렇게 미리 길을 닦아 둔 뒤, 그는 효성스럽고 다정한 모습으로 어머니 앞에 나섰어. 뉴먼도 빈둥대고 있진 않았어. 문 앞에 작은 짐수레가 와 있었고, 세간이 벌써 부지런히 밖으로 옮겨지고 있었거든."]], [["Now, Mrs.", "그런데 니클비 부인은 무슨 일이든 급하게 들어서 알아듣는 사람이 못 됐어."], ["Nickleby was not the sort of person to be told anything in a hurry, or rather to comprehend anything of peculiar delicacy or importance on a short notice.", "아니, 좀 미묘하거나 중요한 일을 짧은 새에 알아차리는 사람이 못 됐다고 하는 편이 맞겠지."], ["Wherefore, although the good lady had been subjected to a full hour’s preparation by little Miss La Creevy, and was now addressed in most lucid terms both by Nicholas and his sister, she was in a state of singular bewilderment and confusion, and could by no means be made to comprehend the necessity of such hurried proceedings.", "그래서 이 착한 부인은 라 크리비 양에게 꼬박 한 시간이나 미리 마음의 준비를 시켜 받았고, 이제 니콜라스와 딸한테서 더없이 또렷한 말로 설명을 듣고 있었는데도, 묘하게 어리둥절하고 혼란스러운 상태였어. 이렇게 서둘러 일을 처리해야 할 까닭을 도무지 알아듣지를 못했지."]], [["‘Why don’t you ask your uncle, my dear Nicholas, what he can possibly mean by it?’ said Mrs.", "\"얘, 니콜라스, 네 삼촌한테 대체 무슨 속셈으로 이러는 건지 물어보지 그러니?\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘My dear mother,’ returned Nicholas, ‘the time for talking has gone by.", "\"어머니.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["There is but one step to take, and that is to cast him off with the scorn and indignation he deserves.", "\"이야기로 풀 때는 지났어요. 이제 할 일은 딱 하나, 그자가 마땅히 받아야 할 경멸과 분노로 그를 내치는 거예요."], ["Your own honour and good name demand that, after the discovery of his vile proceedings, you should not be beholden to him one hour, even for the shelter of these bare walls.’", "어머니의 명예와 좋은 이름을 위해서라도, 그 추악한 짓이 드러난 마당에 단 한 시간이라도 그자한테 신세를 져선 안 돼요. 이 텅 빈 벽 아래 몸을 누이는 것조차도요.\""]], [["‘To be sure,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, crying bitterly, ‘he is a brute, a monster; and the walls are very bare, and want painting too, and I have had this ceiling whitewashed at the expense of eighteen-pence, which is a very distressing thing, considering that it is so much gone into your uncle’s pocket. I never could have believed it--never.’", "\"그래, 맞아.\" 니클비 부인이 서럽게 울며 말했어. \"그 사람은 짐승이야, 괴물이고. 게다가 이 벽들도 어찌나 휑한지, 페인트칠도 다시 해야 하는데. 나는 이 천장을 일 실링 육 펜스나 들여 회칠을 해 놨잖니. 그 돈이 다 네 삼촌 주머니로 들어간 셈이라고 생각하면 어찌나 속이 상하는지. 도저히 못 믿겠다, 정말이지 못 믿겠어.\""]], [["‘Nor I, nor anybody else,’ said Nicholas.", "\"저도요, 다른 누구라도 못 믿을 거예요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Lord bless my life!’ exclaimed Mrs. Nickleby. ‘To think that that Sir Mulberry Hawk should be such an abandoned wretch as Miss La Creevy says he is, Nicholas, my dear; when I was congratulating myself every day on his being an admirer of our dear Kate’s, and thinking what a thing it would be for the family if he was to become connected with us, and use his interest to get you some profitable government place. There are very good places to be got about the court, I know; for a friend of ours (Miss Cropley, at Exeter, my dear Kate, you recollect), he had one, and I know that it was the chief part of his duty to wear silk stockings, and a bag wig like a black watch-pocket; and to think that it should come to this after all--oh, dear, dear, it’s enough to kill one, that it is!’ With which expressions of sorrow, Mrs. Nickleby gave fresh vent to her grief, and wept piteously.", "\"세상에, 맙소사!\" 니클비 부인이 외쳤어. \"그 멀버리 호크 경이 라 크리비 양 말마따나 그렇게 못된 인간이라니, 얘 니콜라스. 나는 날마다 속으로 흐뭇해하고 있었잖니. 그 사람이 우리 사랑스러운 케이트를 흠모한다고. 그 사람이 우리 집안과 인연을 맺어서 그 인맥으로 너한테 돈벌이 되는 관직이라도 하나 얻어 주면 집안에 얼마나 좋은 일일까 하고 말이야. 궁정 쪽에는 좋은 자리가 많거든, 내가 알기로는. 우리가 아는 사람 하나가 (케이트야, 엑서터의 크롭리 양 말이다, 기억하지) 그런 자리를 하나 얻었었는데, 그 사람 직무라는 게 주로 비단 양말을 신고, 검은 시계 주머니처럼 생긴 가발을 쓰는 거였단다. 그런데 그게 결국 이렇게 되고 말다니. 아이고, 정말이지, 사람 죽이는 일이야, 그렇고말고!\" 이렇게 슬픔을 토해 내며 니클비 부인은 다시 설움이 북받쳐 가엾게 울었어."]], [["As Nicholas and his sister were by this time compelled to superintend the removal of the few articles of furniture, Miss La Creevy devoted herself to the consolation of the matron, and observed with great kindness of manner that she must really make an effort, and cheer up.", "이때쯤 니콜라스와 누이는 몇 안 되는 세간을 옮기는 걸 감독해야 했어. 그래서 라 크리비 양이 부인을 위로하는 일을 맡아, 더없이 다정한 태도로, 정말이지 기운을 좀 내셔야 한다고 타일렀지."]], [["‘Oh I dare say, Miss La Creevy,’ returned Mrs.", "\"아이고, 그러게요, 라 크리비 양.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어."], ["Nickleby, with a petulance not unnatural in her unhappy circumstances, ‘it’s very easy to say cheer up, but if you had as many occasions to cheer up as I have had--and there,’ said Mrs.", "이 딱한 처지에선 그럴 만도 한 짜증 섞인 말투였지. \"기운 내라는 말이야 하기 쉽죠."], ["Nickleby, stopping short. ‘Think of Mr.", "그렇지만 댁이 나만큼 기운 내야 할 일을 겪어 봤다면— 그리고 참.\""], ["Pyke and Mr Pluck, two of the most perfect gentlemen that ever lived, what am I too say to them--what can I say to them?", "니클비 부인이 말을 뚝 멈췄어. \"파이크 씨하고 플럭 씨를 생각해 봐요, 세상에 둘도 없는 더없이 완벽한 신사 양반들인데, 내가 그분들한테 뭐라고 해야 하나요— 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠어요?"], ["Why, if I was to say to them, “I’m told your friend Sir Mulberry is a base wretch,”", "아니, 내가 그분들한테 '댁의 친구 멀버리 경이 비열한 인간이라더군요'"], ["they’d laugh at me.’", "하고 말하면, 나를 비웃을 거 아니에요.\""]], [["‘They will laugh no more at us, I take it,’ said Nicholas, advancing.", "\"이젠 우리를 비웃을 일도 없을 겁니다.\" 니콜라스가 다가서며 말했어."], ["‘Come, mother, there is a coach at the door, and until Monday, at all events, we will return to our old quarters.’", "\"자, 어머니, 문 앞에 마차가 와 있어요. 어쨌든 월요일까지는, 우리 옛 거처로 돌아가는 거예요.\""]], [["‘--Where everything is ready, and a hearty welcome into the bargain,’ added Miss La Creevy.", "\"—거기엔 모든 게 다 준비돼 있고, 게다가 진심 어린 환영까지 기다리고 있답니다.\" 라 크리비 양이 덧붙였어."], ["‘Now, let me go with you downstairs.’", "\"자, 제가 모시고 아래층으로 내려갈게요.\""]], [["But Mrs.", "하지만 니클비 부인은 그렇게 쉽게 움직일 사람이 아니었어."], ["Nickleby was not to be so easily moved, for first she insisted on going upstairs to see that nothing had been left, and then on going downstairs to see that everything had been taken away; and when she was getting into the coach she had a vision of a forgotten coffee-pot on the back-kitchen hob, and after she was shut in, a dismal recollection of a green umbrella behind some unknown door.", "우선 위층에 올라가 빠뜨린 게 없는지 봐야겠다고 우겼고, 그다음엔 아래층으로 내려가 모든 게 다 옮겨졌는지 봐야겠다고 했지. 그리고 마차에 오르려는 참엔, 뒷부엌 화덕 선반에 두고 온 커피 주전자가 눈앞에 어른거렸어."], ["At last Nicholas, in a condition of absolute despair, ordered the coachman to drive away, and in the unexpected jerk of a sudden starting, Mrs.", "그리고 마차 문이 닫힌 뒤엔, 어딘지 모를 어떤 문 뒤에 놓인 초록색 우산이 처량하게 떠올랐지. 마침내 니콜라스는 완전히 절망에 빠진 채로 마부에게 출발하라고 일렀어."], ["Nickleby lost a shilling among the straw, which fortunately confined her attention to the coach until it was too late to remember anything else.", "그런데 마차가 갑자기 출발하면서 예기치 못하게 덜컥하는 바람에, 니클비 부인은 짚더미 속에 일 실링을 떨어뜨렸어. 그래서 다행히도 부인은 그 동전에만 정신이 팔려, 다른 걸 떠올리기엔 너무 늦어 버릴 때까지 마차 안에만 신경을 쏟았지."]], [["Having seen everything safely out, discharged the servant, and locked the door, Nicholas jumped into a cabriolet and drove to a bye place near Golden Square where he had appointed to meet Noggs; and so quickly had everything been done, that it was barely half-past nine when he reached the place of meeting.", "모든 짐을 무사히 내가고, 하녀를 내보내고, 문을 잠그고 나서, 니콜라스는 이륜마차에 뛰어올라 골든 스퀘어 근처의 외진 곳으로 갔어. 노그스와 거기서 만나기로 약속해 뒀거든. 모든 일이 어찌나 빨리 처리됐던지, 그가 약속 장소에 다다랐을 때는 겨우 아홉 시 반이었어."]], [["‘Here is the letter for Ralph,’ said Nicholas, ‘and here the key.", "\"여기 랄프한테 보낼 편지예요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["When you come to me this evening, not a word of last night.", "\"그리고 이건 열쇠고요. 오늘 저녁 나한테 올 땐, 어젯밤 일은 한마디도 하지 말아요."], ["Ill news travels fast, and they will know it soon enough.", "나쁜 소식은 빨리 퍼지는 법이니, 곧 알게 되겠죠."], ["Have you heard if he was much hurt?’", "그자가 많이 다쳤는지 들은 거 있어요?\""]], [["Newman shook his head.", "뉴먼이 고개를 저었어."]], [["‘I will ascertain that myself without loss of time,’ said Nicholas.", "\"그건 내가 직접 지체 없이 알아볼게요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘You had better take some rest,’ returned Newman.", "\"좀 쉬는 게 좋겠어요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어."], ["‘You are fevered and ill.’", "\"열이 나고 몸도 안 좋잖아요.\""]], [["Nicholas waved his hand carelessly, and concealing the indisposition he really felt, now that the excitement which had sustained him was over, took a hurried farewell of Newman Noggs, and left him.", "니콜라스는 아무렇지 않게 손을 내저었어. 그리고 자기를 버티게 해 주던 흥분이 가시고 나니 실제로 느껴지는 몸의 불편함을 감추면서, 뉴먼 노그스에게 황급히 작별을 고하고 떠났어."]], [["Newman was not three minutes’ walk from Golden Square, but in the course of that three minutes he took the letter out of his hat and put it in again twenty times at least.", "뉴먼은 골든 스퀘어에서 걸어서 삼 분도 안 걸리는 거리에 있었어. 그런데 그 삼 분 동안, 그는 적어도 스무 번은 모자에서 편지를 꺼냈다 다시 넣었다 했지."], ["First the front, then the back, then the sides, then the superscription, then the seal, were objects of Newman’s admiration.", "처음엔 앞면, 다음엔 뒷면, 그다음엔 옆면, 그다음엔 겉봉의 수신인, 그다음엔 봉인까지, 죄다 뉴먼이 감탄하며 들여다보는 대상이 됐어."], ["Then he held it at arm’s length as if to take in the whole at one delicious survey, and then he rubbed his hands in a perfect ecstasy with his commission.", "그러고는 한 번에 흐뭇하게 전체를 훑어보려는 듯 편지를 팔 끝까지 쭉 뻗어 들었지. 그러고는 자기가 맡은 이 임무에 완전히 황홀해져서 두 손을 비벼 댔어."]], [["He reached the office, hung his hat on its accustomed peg, laid the letter and key upon the desk, and waited impatiently until Ralph Nickleby should appear.", "뉴먼은 사무실에 도착해 모자를 늘 걸던 못에 걸고, 편지와 열쇠를 책상 위에 올려놓은 채, 랄프 니클비가 나타나기를 안달하며 기다렸어."], ["After a few minutes, the well-known creaking of his boots was heard on the stairs, and then the bell rung.", "몇 분 뒤, 계단에서 익숙한 그의 부츠 삐걱대는 소리가 들렸고, 이어서 종이 울렸지."]], [["‘Has the post come in?’", "\"우편물 왔나?\" \"아니요.\""]], [["‘No.’\n‘Any other letters?’", "\"다른 편지는?\""]], [["‘One.’ Newman eyed him closely, and laid it on the desk.", "\"하나요.\" 뉴먼이 그를 유심히 살피며 편지를 책상 위에 내려놓았어."]], [["‘What’s this?’ asked Ralph, taking up the key.", "\"이건 뭔가?\" 랄프가 열쇠를 집어 들며 물었어."]], [["‘Left with the letter;--a boy brought them--quarter of an hour ago, or less.’", "\"편지랑 같이 맡겨진 겁니다. 어떤 아이가 가져왔어요. 십오 분쯤 전에, 그보다 덜 됐나요.\""]], [["Ralph glanced at the direction, opened the letter, and read as follows:--", "랄프는 수신인을 흘끗 보고는 편지를 뜯어 이렇게 읽었어."]], [["‘You are known to me now.", "\"이제 나는 네 정체를 안다."], ["There are no reproaches I could heap upon your head which would carry with them one thousandth part of the grovelling shame that this assurance will awaken even in your breast.", "내가 네 머리 위에 아무리 많은 비난을 퍼붓는다 해도, 이 한마디가 네 가슴속에까지 불러일으킬 그 비굴한 수치심의 천분의 일에도 미치지 못할 것이다."]], [["‘Your brother’s widow and her orphan child spurn the shelter of your roof, and shun you with disgust and loathing.", "네 동생의 미망인과 그 고아 자식은 네 지붕 아래 깃드는 것을 마다하고, 역겨움과 혐오로 너를 멀리한다."], ["Your kindred renounce you, for they know no shame but the ties of blood which bind them in name with you.", "네 혈육은 너를 등진다. 이름으로나마 너와 그들을 얽어매는 핏줄, 그것 말고는 부끄러울 게 없는 자들이니까."]], [["‘You are an old man, and I leave you to the grave.", "너는 늙은이다. 나는 너를 무덤에 내버려 두겠다."], ["May every recollection of your life cling to your false heart, and cast their darkness on your death-bed.’", "네 삶의 온갖 기억이 그 거짓된 가슴에 매달려, 네 임종 자리에 그 어둠을 드리우기를.\""]], [["Ralph Nickleby read this letter twice, and frowning heavily, fell into a fit of musing; the paper fluttered from his hand and dropped upon the floor, but he clasped his fingers, as if he held it still.", "랄프 니클비는 이 편지를 두 번 읽고는, 잔뜩 미간을 찌푸린 채 깊은 생각에 잠겼어. 종이가 손에서 팔랑거리며 바닥에 떨어졌지만, 그는 여전히 그걸 쥐고 있는 양 손가락을 맞쥐고 있었지."]], [["Suddenly, he started from his seat, and thrusting it all crumpled into his pocket, turned furiously to Newman Noggs, as though to ask him why he lingered. But Newman stood unmoved, with his back towards him, following up, with the worn and blackened stump of an old pen, some figures in an Interest-table which was pasted against the wall, and apparently quite abstracted from every other object.", "문득 그가 자리에서 벌떡 일어났어. 그리고 그것을 다 구겨 주머니에 쑤셔 넣고는, 뉴먼 노그스를 향해 사납게 돌아섰어. 왜 아직 꾸물대고 있느냐고 따지려는 듯이 말이야. 하지만 뉴먼은 그에게 등을 돌린 채 꿈쩍도 않고 서 있었어. 닳고 시커메진 헌 펜 토막으로, 벽에 붙여 둔 이자 계산표의 숫자 몇 개를 짚어 가면서, 다른 건 전혀 안중에도 없다는 듯이 말이지."]], [["# CHAPTER 34", "# CHAPTER 34"]], [["Wherein Mr. Ralph Nickleby is visited by Persons with whom the Reader has been already made acquainted\n‘What a demnition long time you have kept me ringing at this confounded old cracked tea-kettle of a bell, every tinkle of which is enough to throw a strong man into blue convulsions, upon my life and soul, oh demmit,’--said Mr.", "\"이놈의 다 깨진 고물 주전자 같은 종을 댕그랑거리느라 얼마나 오래 기다리게 한 건지, 그 댕그랑 소리 한 번 한 번이 멀쩡한 사내도 시퍼렇게 경련을 일으키게 만들 정도였다고, 정말이지, 빌어먹을!\""], ["Mantalini to Newman Noggs, scraping his boots, as he spoke, on Ralph Nickleby’s scraper.", "맨탈리니 씨가 랄프 니클비네 흙털개에 부츠를 문지르면서 뉴먼 노그스한테 이렇게 말했다."]], [["‘I didn’t hear the bell more than once,’ replied Newman.", "\"종소리는 한 번밖에 못 들었는데요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어."]], [["‘Then you are most immensely and outr-i-geously deaf,’ said Mr Mantalini, ‘as deaf as a demnition post.’", "\"그렇다면 자네는 엄청나게, 어처구니없을 만큼 귀가 먹은 거야.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 말했어. \"빌어먹을 말뚝처럼 귀가 멀었다고.\""]], [["Mr. Mantalini had got by this time into the passage, and was making his way to the door of Ralph’s office with very little ceremony, when Newman interposed his body; and hinting that Mr.", "이때쯤 맨탈리니 씨는 복도까지 들어와서, 별다른 격식도 차리지 않고 랄프의 사무실 문 쪽으로 가고 있었어."], ["Nickleby was unwilling to be disturbed, inquired whether the client’s business was of a pressing nature.", "그러자 뉴먼이 제 몸으로 길을 막아섰지. 그러고는 니클비 씨가 방해받고 싶어 하지 않는다는 걸 넌지시 비추면서, 손님 용건이 급한 거냐고 물었어."]], [["‘It is most demnebly particular,’ said Mr. Mantalini.", "\"엄청나게 빌어먹을 만큼 급한 일이지.\""], ["‘It is to melt some scraps of dirty paper into bright, shining, chinking, tinkling, demd mint sauce.’", "맨탈리니 씨가 말했어. \"더러운 종잇조각 몇 장을 환하게 빛나고 짤랑짤랑 울리는 빌어먹을 돈으로 녹여 내는 일이거든.\""]], [["Newman uttered a significant grunt, and taking Mr.", "뉴먼은 의미심장하게 끙 소리를 냈어."], ["Mantalini’s proffered card, limped with it into his master’s office.", "그러고는 맨탈리니 씨가 내미는 명함을 받아 들고, 그걸 들고 주인 사무실로 절뚝거리며 들어갔지."], ["As he thrust his head in at the door, he saw that Ralph had resumed the thoughtful posture into which he had fallen after perusing his nephew’s letter, and that he seemed to have been reading it again, as he once more held it open in his hand.", "문틈으로 고개를 들이미니, 랄프가 조카 편지를 읽고 나서 빠져들었던 그 생각에 잠긴 자세로 다시 돌아가 있는 게 보였어. 게다가 편지를 또 펼쳐 든 걸 보니, 그걸 다시 읽고 있었던 모양이야."], ["The glance was but momentary, for Ralph, being disturbed, turned to demand the cause of the interruption.", "하지만 그건 잠깐 본 것뿐이었어. 방해를 받은 랄프가 무슨 일로 끼어드냐고 따지려고 몸을 돌렸거든."]], [["As Newman stated it, the cause himself swaggered into the room, and grasping Ralph’s horny hand with uncommon affection, vowed that he had never seen him looking so well in all his life.", "뉴먼이 사정을 말하는 동안, 그 일을 만든 장본인이 거들먹거리며 방 안으로 들어왔어. 그러고는 유난스러운 애정을 담아 랄프의 거친 손을 덥석 잡더니, 평생 이렇게 좋아 보인 적이 없다고 떠벌렸지."]], [["‘There is quite a bloom upon your demd countenance,’ said Mr.", "\"자네 빌어먹을 얼굴에 아주 꽃이 피었구먼.\""], ["Mantalini, seating himself unbidden, and arranging his hair and whiskers.", "맨탈리니 씨가 권하지도 않은 자리에 앉으며, 제 머리와 구레나룻을 매만지면서 말했어."], ["‘You look quite juvenile and jolly, demmit!’", "\"아주 젊어 보이고 쾌활해 보여, 빌어먹을!\""]], [["‘We are alone,’ returned Ralph, tartly.", "\"우리뿐이오.\" 랄프가 쌀쌀맞게 대꾸했어."], ["‘What do you want with me?’", "\"나한테 뭘 바라고 온 거요?\""]], [["‘Good!’ cried Mr.", "\"좋아!\""], ["Mantalini, displaying his teeth.", "맨탈리니 씨가 이를 드러내며 외쳤어."], ["‘What did I want!", "\"내가 뭘 바라느냐고!"], ["Yes.", "그래."], ["Ha, ha!", "하, 하!"], ["Very good.", "아주 좋아."], ["WHAT did I want.", "내가 뭘 바라느냐."], ["Ha, ha.", "하, 하."], ["Oh dem!’", "아이고 빌어먹을!\""]], [["‘What DO you want, man?’ demanded Ralph, sternly.", "\"뭘 원하는 거요, 이 사람아?\" 랄프가 엄하게 다그쳤어."]], [["‘Demnition discount,’ returned Mr.", "\"빌어먹을 어음 할인이지.\""], ["Mantalini, with a grin, and shaking his head waggishly.", "맨탈리니 씨가 씩 웃으며, 익살스럽게 고개를 저으며 대답했어."]], [["‘Money is scarce,’ said Ralph.", "\"돈이 귀해요.\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Demd scarce, or I shouldn’t want it,’ interrupted Mr.", "\"빌어먹게 귀하니까 나도 필요한 거 아닌가.\""], ["Mantalini.", "맨탈리니 씨가 끼어들었어."]], [["‘The times are bad, and one scarcely knows whom to trust,’ continued Ralph. ‘I don’t want to do business just now, in fact I would rather not; but as you are a friend--how many bills have you there?’", "\"요즘 경기가 나빠서, 누굴 믿어야 할지 도통 모르겠소.\" 랄프가 말을 이었어. \"지금은 거래를 하고 싶지 않은데, 사실 안 하는 게 낫겠소. 하지만 자네는 친구니까 말이오. 어음이 몇 장이오?\""]], [["‘Two,’ returned Mr.", "\"두 장.\""], ["Mantalini.", "맨탈리니 씨가 대답했어."]], [["‘What is the gross amount?’", "\"전부 얼마요?\""]], [["‘Demd trifling--five-and-seventy.’", "\"빌어먹을 푼돈이지. 칠십오 파운드.\""]], [["‘And the dates?’", "\"날짜는?\""]], [["‘Two months, and four.’", "\"두 달짜리하고 넉 달짜리.\""]], [["‘I’ll do them for you--mind, for YOU; I wouldn’t for many people--for five-and-twenty pounds,’ said Ralph, deliberately.", "\"자네 거라면 해 주지. 명심하시오, 자네 거니까. 다른 사람한테는 안 해 줘. 이십오 파운드에 해 주겠소.\" 랄프가 천천히 말했어."]], [["‘Oh demmit!’ cried Mr.", "\"아이고 빌어먹을!\""], ["Mantalini, whose face lengthened considerably at this handsome proposal.", "맨탈리니 씨가 외쳤어. 이 후한 제안에 그의 얼굴이 잔뜩 길어졌지."]], [["‘Why, that leaves you fifty,’ retorted Ralph.", "\"왜, 그래도 자네 손에 오십 파운드가 남잖소.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어."], ["‘What would you have?", "\"뭘 더 바라는 거요?"], ["Let me see the names.’", "이름을 좀 봅시다.\""]], [["‘You are so demd hard, Nickleby,’ remonstrated Mr.", "\"자넨 정말 빌어먹게 야박해, 니클비.\""], ["Mantalini.", "맨탈리니 씨가 따졌어."]], [["‘Let me see the names,’ replied Ralph, impatiently extending his hand for the bills.", "\"이름을 좀 보자니까.\" 랄프가 조바심을 내며 어음을 달라고 손을 내밀었어."], ["‘Well!", "\"음!"], ["They are not sure, but they are safe enough.", "확실하진 않지만, 그런대로 안전하구먼."], ["Do you consent to the terms, and will you take the money?", "조건에 동의하겠소, 돈을 받겠소?"], ["I don’t want you to do so.", "나야 자네가 그러길 바라지 않소."], ["I would rather you didn’t.’", "안 하는 편이 낫지.\""]], [["‘Demmit, Nickleby, can’t you--’ began Mr.", "\"빌어먹을, 니클비, 자네 좀—\""], ["Mantalini.", "맨탈리니 씨가 입을 열었어."]], [["‘No,’ replied Ralph, interrupting him.", "\"안 돼.\" 랄프가 말을 끊었어."], ["‘I can’t.", "\"안 된다고. 돈을 받겠소?"], ["Will you take the money--down, mind; no delay, no going into the city and pretending to negotiate with some other party who has no existence, and never had.", "현금이오, 명심하시오. 미루지 말고, 시내로 나가서 있지도 않은, 애초에 있지도 않았던 딴 사람하고 흥정하는 척하지도 말고."], ["Is it a bargain, or is it not?’", "거래하겠소, 안 하겠소?\""]], [["Ralph pushed some papers from him as he spoke, and carelessly rattled his cash-box, as though by mere accident.", "랄프는 말하면서 서류 몇 장을 옆으로 밀쳐 놓고, 그저 우연인 척 무심하게 돈궤를 달그락거렸어."], ["The sound was too much for Mr Mantalini.", "그 소리에 맨탈리니 씨는 견딜 수가 없었지."], ["He closed the bargain directly it reached his ears, and Ralph told the money out upon the table.", "그 소리가 귀에 닿자마자 그는 바로 거래에 응했고, 랄프는 탁자 위에 돈을 세어 놓았어."]], [["He had scarcely done so, and Mr.", "랄프가 돈을 다 세어 놓기가 무섭게, 그리고 맨탈리니 씨가 그걸 다 그러모으기도 전에, 종소리가 울렸어."], ["Mantalini had not yet gathered it all up, when a ring was heard at the bell, and immediately afterwards Newman ushered in no less a person than Madame Mantalini, at sight of whom Mr Mantalini evinced considerable discomposure, and swept the cash into his pocket with remarkable alacrity.", "그리고 곧이어 뉴먼이 다름 아닌 맨탈리니 부인을 모셔 들였지. 그 모습을 보자 맨탈리니 씨는 꽤나 당황한 기색을 보이며, 놀랄 만큼 잽싸게 돈을 호주머니에 쓸어 넣었어."]], [["‘Oh, you ARE here,’ said Madame Mantalini, tossing her head.", "\"아, 여기 계셨군요.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 고개를 홱 젖히며 말했어."]], [["‘Yes, my life and soul, I am,’ replied her husband, dropping on his knees, and pouncing with kitten-like playfulness upon a stray sovereign.", "\"그래, 내 목숨이자 영혼이여, 여기 있소.\" 남편이 무릎을 꿇으며, 새끼 고양이처럼 장난스럽게 굴러다니던 금화 한 닢을 덮치며 대답했어."], ["‘I am here, my soul’s delight, upon Tom Tiddler’s ground, picking up the demnition gold and silver.’", "\"여기 있소, 내 영혼의 기쁨이여, 톰 티들러의 땅에서 빌어먹을 금붙이 은붙이를 줍고 있다오.\""]], [["‘I am ashamed of you,’ said Madame Mantalini, with much indignation.", "\"당신이 부끄럽군요.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 잔뜩 화가 나서 말했어."]], [["‘Ashamed--of ME, my joy?", "\"부끄럽다니, 내가? 내 기쁨이여?"], ["It knows it is talking demd charming sweetness, but naughty fibs,’ returned Mr.", "빌어먹게 매혹적이고 달콤한 소리를 하고 있구려, 그런데 그건 못된 거짓말이지.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 대답했어."], ["Mantalini. ‘It knows it is not ashamed of its own popolorum tibby.’", "\"당신은 당신의 그 귀여운 임을 부끄러워하지 않으면서.\""]], [["Whatever were the circumstances which had led to such a result, it certainly appeared as though the popolorum tibby had rather miscalculated, for the nonce, the extent of his lady’s affection.", "이런 결과를 빚어낸 사정이 무엇이든 간에, 그 귀여운 임이 적어도 이번만큼은 부인의 애정 크기를 잘못 가늠한 게 분명해 보였어. 맨탈리니 부인은 대답 대신 경멸하는 표정만 지었거든."], ["Madame Mantalini only looked scornful in reply; and, turning to Ralph, begged him to excuse her intrusion.", "그러고는 랄프 쪽으로 돌아서서, 갑작스레 들이닥친 걸 용서해 달라고 청했지."]], [["‘Which is entirely attributable,’ said Madame, ‘to the gross misconduct and most improper behaviour of Mr.", "\"이게 다,\" 부인이 말했어."], ["Mantalini.’", "\"맨탈리니 씨의 형편없는 비행과 아주 부적절한 행동 탓이랍니다.\""]], [["‘Of me, my essential juice of pineapple!’", "\"내가, 내 둘도 없는 파인애플 즙이여!\""]], [["‘Of you,’ returned his wife.", "\"당신이요.\" 아내가 대꾸했어."], ["‘But I will not allow it.", "\"하지만 난 더는 못 참아요."], ["I will not submit to be ruined by the extravagance and profligacy of any man.", "어떤 남자의 사치와 방탕 때문에 거덜 나는 꼴은 안 당하겠어요."], ["I call Mr Nickleby to witness the course I intend to pursue with you.’", "내가 당신한테 하려는 일을 니클비 씨를 증인 삼아 말하겠어요.\""]], [["‘Pray don’t call me to witness anything, ma’am,’ said Ralph.", "\"제발 저를 무슨 증인이니 하는 데 끌어들이지 마십시오, 부인.\""], ["‘Settle it between yourselves, settle it between yourselves.’", "랄프가 말했어. \"두 분이 알아서 해결하세요, 두 분끼리 알아서요.\""]], [["‘No, but I must beg you as a favour,’ said Madame Mantalini, ‘to hear me give him notice of what it is my fixed intention to do--my fixed intention, sir,’ repeated Madame Mantalini, darting an angry look at her husband.", "\"아니에요, 그래도 부탁 좀 드릴게요.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 말했어. \"제가 이 사람한테 단단히 마음먹은 일을 통보하는 걸 들어 주세요. 제 단단한 결심을요, 선생님.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 남편에게 화난 눈길을 쏘아 보내며 거듭 말했어."]], [["‘Will she call me “Sir”?’ cried Mantalini.", "\"이 사람이 나한테 '선생님'이라고 부른 거요?\""], ["‘Me who dote upon her with the demdest ardour!", "맨탈리니가 외쳤어. \"빌어먹게 뜨거운 정성으로 이 여자를 떠받드는 나한테!"], ["She, who coils her fascinations round me like a pure angelic rattlesnake!", "순결한 천사 같은 방울뱀처럼 제 매력을 나한테 칭칭 휘감는 이 여자가!"], ["It will be all up with my feelings; she will throw me into a demd state.’", "이러다 내 감정이 다 거덜 나겠어. 이 여자가 나를 빌어먹을 지경으로 몰아넣고 있어.\""]], [["‘Don’t talk of feelings, sir,’ rejoined Madame Mantalini, seating herself, and turning her back upon him.", "\"감정 얘기는 하지 마세요, 선생님.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 자리에 앉아 그에게 등을 돌리며 받아쳤어."], ["‘You don’t consider mine.’", "\"당신은 내 감정은 안 헤아리잖아요.\""]], [["‘I do not consider yours, my soul!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"내가 당신 감정을 안 헤아린다고, 내 영혼이여!\""], ["Mantalini.", "맨탈리니 씨가 외쳤어."]], [["‘No,’ replied his wife.", "\"네.\" 아내가 대답했어."]], [["And notwithstanding various blandishments on the part of Mr.", "그리고 맨탈리니 씨가 갖은 아양을 떨었는데도, 맨탈리니 부인은 여전히 아니라고 했어."], ["Mantalini, Madame Mantalini still said no, and said it too with such determined and resolute ill-temper, that Mr. Mantalini was clearly taken aback.", "게다가 어찌나 단호하고 결연하게 골을 내며 말하던지, 맨탈리니 씨는 영락없이 당황해 버렸지."]], [["‘His extravagance, Mr.", "\"이 사람 사치 말이에요, 니클비 씨.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 랄프한테 말을 건넸어."], ["Nickleby,’ said Madame Mantalini, addressing herself to Ralph, who leant against his easy-chair with his hands behind him, and regarded the amiable couple with a smile of the supremest and most unmitigated contempt,--‘his extravagance is beyond all bounds.’", "랄프는 안락의자에 등을 기대고 두 손을 뒤로 한 채, 더없이 깊고 노골적인 경멸의 미소를 띠며 이 다정한 부부를 바라보고 있었지. \"이 사람 사치는 한도 끝도 없답니다.\""]], [["‘I should scarcely have supposed it,’ answered Ralph, sarcastically.", "\"그럴 줄은 미처 몰랐군요.\" 랄프가 비꼬듯 대답했어."]], [["‘I assure you, Mr. Nickleby, however, that it is,’ returned Madame Mantalini.", "\"하지만 정말이라니까요, 니클비 씨.\""], ["‘It makes me miserable!", "맨탈리니 부인이 받았어."], ["I am under constant apprehensions, and in constant difficulty.", "\"그것 때문에 내가 비참해 죽겠어요! 늘 마음을 졸이고, 늘 쪼들리고요."], ["And even this,’ said Madame Mantalini, wiping her eyes, ‘is not the worst.", "게다가 이건,\" 맨탈리니 부인이 눈물을 닦으며 말했어."], ["He took some papers of value out of my desk this morning without asking my permission.’", "\"이건 최악도 아니에요. 오늘 아침엔 내 허락도 없이 내 책상에서 값나가는 서류 몇 장을 꺼내 갔다니까요.\""]], [["Mr. Mantalini groaned slightly, and buttoned his trousers pocket.", "맨탈리니 씨가 가볍게 끙 소리를 내며, 바지 호주머니 단추를 잠갔어."]], [["‘I am obliged,’ continued Madame Mantalini, ‘since our late misfortunes, to pay Miss Knag a great deal of money for having her name in the business, and I really cannot afford to encourage him in all his wastefulness. As I have no doubt that he came straight here, Mr Nickleby, to convert the papers I have spoken of, into money, and as you have assisted us very often before, and are very much connected with us in this kind of matters, I wish you to know the determination at which his conduct has compelled me to arrive.’", "\"우리가 요전에 겪은 불운 이후로,\" 맨탈리니 부인이 말을 이었어. \"낵 양한테 가게에 이름을 올려 둔 대가로 큰돈을 줘야만 해요. 그러니 정말이지 이 사람 낭비벽을 부추길 여유가 없어요. 니클비 씨, 이 사람이 제가 말한 그 서류를 돈으로 바꾸려고 곧장 여기로 왔을 게 틀림없으니, 그리고 전에도 우리를 자주 도와주셨고 이런 일에 우리하고 아주 깊이 얽혀 계시니, 이 사람 행실 때문에 제가 어쩔 수 없이 내린 결심을 좀 알아 주셨으면 해요.\""]], [["Mr. Mantalini groaned once more from behind his wife’s bonnet, and fitting a sovereign into one of his eyes, winked with the other at Ralph.", "맨탈리니 씨가 아내 보닛 뒤에서 또 한 번 끙 소리를 냈어."], ["Having achieved this performance with great dexterity, he whipped the coin into his pocket, and groaned again with increased penitence.", "그러고는 한쪽 눈에 금화 한 닢을 끼워 넣고, 다른 쪽 눈으로 랄프한테 윙크를 했지. 이 묘기를 아주 솜씨 좋게 해치우고 나서, 그 동전을 호주머니에 휙 집어넣고는, 한층 더 뉘우치는 척하며 또 끙 소리를 냈어."]], [["‘I have made up my mind,’ said Madame Mantalini, as tokens of impatience manifested themselves in Ralph’s countenance, ‘to allowance him.’", "\"마음을 굳혔어요.\" 랄프의 얼굴에 조바심이 드러나기 시작하자 맨탈리니 부인이 말했어. \"이 사람한테 생활비를 정해 주기로요.\""]], [["‘To do that, my joy?’ inquired Mr.", "\"무얼 한다고, 내 기쁨이여?\""], ["Mantalini, who did not seem to have caught the words.", "맨탈리니 씨가 그 말을 못 알아들은 듯 물었어."]], [["‘To put him,’ said Madame Mantalini, looking at Ralph, and prudently abstaining from the slightest glance at her husband, lest his many graces should induce her to falter in her resolution, ‘to put him upon a fixed allowance; and I say that if he has a hundred and twenty pounds a year for his clothes and pocket-money, he may consider himself a very fortunate man.’", "\"이 사람한테,\" 맨탈리니 부인이 랄프를 바라보며 말했어. 남편의 그 숱한 매력에 결심이 흔들릴까 봐, 신중하게 남편 쪽으로는 눈길조차 주지 않았지. \"이 사람한테 정해진 생활비를 주려고요. 옷값하고 용돈으로 한 해에 백이십 파운드를 받는다면, 이 사람은 자기가 아주 운 좋은 사람인 줄 알아야 할 거예요.\""]], [["Mr.", "맨탈리니 씨는 정해 줄 생활비 액수를 들으려고 아주 점잖게 기다렸어."], ["Mantalini waited, with much decorum, to hear the amount of the proposed stipend, but when it reached his ears, he cast his hat and cane upon the floor, and drawing out his pocket-handkerchief, gave vent to his feelings in a dismal moan.", "그런데 그 액수가 귀에 들어오자, 모자와 지팡이를 바닥에 내던지고는, 손수건을 꺼내 처량하게 신음하며 감정을 쏟아냈지."]], [["‘Demnition!’ cried Mr.", "\"빌어먹을!\" 맨탈리니 씨가 외쳤어."], ["Mantalini, suddenly skipping out of his chair, and as suddenly skipping into it again, to the great discomposure of his lady’s nerves.", "그러고는 갑자기 의자에서 펄쩍 뛰어올랐다가, 또 그만큼 갑자기 도로 주저앉았어. 부인의 신경을 잔뜩 곤두서게 하면서 말이지."], ["‘But no.", "\"아니, 아니야."], ["It is a demd horrid dream.", "이건 빌어먹을 끔찍한 꿈이야."], ["It is not reality.", "현실이 아니라고."], ["No!’", "아니야!\""]], [["Comforting himself with this assurance, Mr. Mantalini closed his eyes and waited patiently till such time as he should wake up.", "이 다짐으로 스스로를 위로하며, 맨탈리니 씨는 눈을 감고는 깨어날 때까지 참을성 있게 기다렸어."]], [["‘A very judicious arrangement,’ observed Ralph with a sneer, ‘if your husband will keep within it, ma’am--as no doubt he will.’", "\"아주 현명한 처사군요.\" 랄프가 비웃으며 말했어. \"남편이 그 안에서 산다면 말입니다, 부인. 뭐, 틀림없이 그러겠지요만.\""]], [["‘Demmit!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"빌어먹을!\""], ["Mantalini, opening his eyes at the sound of Ralph’s voice, ‘it is a horrid reality.", "맨탈리니 씨가 랄프의 목소리에 눈을 뜨며 외쳤어. \"이건 끔찍한 현실이야."], ["She is sitting there before me.", "그녀가 내 앞에 앉아 있어."], ["There is the graceful outline of her form; it cannot be mistaken--there is nothing like it.", "저기 그녀의 우아한 몸매 윤곽이 있잖아. 잘못 볼 리가 없지."], ["The two countesses had no outlines at all, and the dowager’s was a demd outline.", "저런 건 어디에도 없으니까. 그 두 백작 부인은 윤곽이라곤 아예 없었고, 그 노부인 윤곽은 빌어먹을 윤곽이었어."], ["Why is she so excruciatingly beautiful that I cannot be angry with her, even now?’", "어째서 그녀는 이토록 사무치게 아름다워서, 지금 이 순간에도 화를 낼 수가 없는 거지?\""]], [["‘You have brought it upon yourself, Alfred,’ returned Madame Mantalini--still reproachfully, but in a softened tone.", "\"당신이 자초한 일이에요, 앨프리드.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 대답했어. 여전히 나무라는 투였지만, 한결 누그러진 목소리였지."]], [["‘I am a demd villain!’ cried Mr.", "\"난 빌어먹을 악당이야!\" 맨탈리니 씨가 제 머리를 치며 외쳤어."], ["Mantalini, smiting himself on the head.", "\"호주머니를 금화 한 닢어치 반 페니 동전으로 가득 채우고 템스강에 빠져 죽어 버릴 테야."], ["‘I will fill my pockets with change for a sovereign in halfpence and drown myself in the Thames; but I will not be angry with her, even then, for I will put a note in the twopenny-post as I go along, to tell her where the body is.", "하지만 그래도 그녀한테 화는 안 낼 거야. 가는 길에 이 페니짜리 우편으로 쪽지를 부쳐서, 시체가 어디 있는지 알려 줄 테니까."], ["She will be a lovely widow.", "그녀는 사랑스러운 과부가 되겠지."], ["I shall be a body.", "나는 시체가 되고."], ["Some handsome women will cry; she will laugh demnebly.’", "어떤 아리따운 여자들은 울 거야. 그녀는 빌어먹게 웃어 댈 테고.\""]], [["‘Alfred, you cruel, cruel creature,’ said Madame Mantalini, sobbing at the dreadful picture.", "\"앨프리드, 당신은 잔인해요, 너무 잔인한 사람이에요.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 그 끔찍한 그림에 흐느끼며 말했어."]], [["‘She calls me cruel--me--me--who for her sake will become a demd, damp, moist, unpleasant body!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"그녀가 날 잔인하다고 하는군. 나를, 이 나를, 그녀를 위해서 빌어먹게 축축하고 눅눅한 시체가 되려는 이 나를!\""], ["Mantalini.", "맨탈리니 씨가 외쳤어."]], [["‘You know it almost breaks my heart, even to hear you talk of such a thing,’ replied Madame Mantalini.", "\"그런 얘기를 듣는 것만으로도 내 가슴이 거의 찢어지는 거 알잖아요.\" 맨탈리니 부인이 대답했어."]], [["‘Can I live to be mistrusted?’ cried her husband.", "\"내가 의심받으며 살 수 있겠어?\" 남편이 외쳤어."], ["‘Have I cut my heart into a demd extraordinary number of little pieces, and given them all away, one after another, to the same little engrossing demnition captivater, and can I live to be suspected by her?", "\"내 심장을 빌어먹게 엄청나게 많은 조각으로 잘라서, 하나씩 하나씩 죄다 그 작고 사람 홀리는 빌어먹을 매혹덩어리한테 줘 버린 내가, 그녀한테 의심받으며 살 수 있겠냐고?"], ["Demmit, no I can’t.’", "빌어먹을, 아니, 난 못 살아.\""]], [["‘Ask Mr.", "\"제가 말한 액수가 적당한지 니클비 씨한테 물어봐요.\""], ["Nickleby whether the sum I have mentioned is not a proper one,’ reasoned Madame Mantalini.", "맨탈리니 부인이 따지듯 말했어."]], [["‘I don’t want any sum,’ replied her disconsolate husband; ‘I shall require no demd allowance.", "\"난 어떤 액수도 바라지 않아.\" 풀이 죽은 남편이 대답했어."], ["I will be a body.’", "\"빌어먹을 생활비 따위 필요 없어. 난 시체가 될 거야.\""]], [["On this repetition of Mr. Mantalini’s fatal threat, Madame Mantalini wrung her hands, and implored the interference of Ralph Nickleby; and after a great quantity of tears and talking, and several attempts on the part of Mr.", "맨탈리니 씨가 이 끔찍한 협박을 또 되풀이하자, 맨탈리니 부인은 두 손을 비틀며 랄프 니클비한테 좀 말려 달라고 애원했어."], ["Mantalini to reach the door, preparatory to straightway committing violence upon himself, that gentleman was prevailed upon, with difficulty, to promise that he wouldn’t be a body.", "그러고는 한참 동안 눈물과 입씨름이 오가고, 맨탈리니 씨가 당장 제 몸에 해를 가하려는 듯 문 쪽으로 가려고 몇 번이나 시도한 끝에, 그 신사는 가까스로 시체가 되지 않겠다는 약속을 하도록 설득당했지. 이 큰 고비를 넘기자, 맨탈리니 부인은 생활비 문제를 따졌고, 맨탈리니 씨도 마찬가지로 따졌어."], ["This great point attained, Madame Mantalini argued the question of the allowance, and Mr Mantalini did the same, taking occasion to show that he could live with uncommon satisfaction upon bread and water, and go clad in rags, but that he could not support existence with the additional burden of being mistrusted by the object of his most devoted and disinterested affection.", "그러면서 자기는 빵과 물만으로도 더없이 만족스럽게 살 수 있고 누더기를 걸치고도 지낼 수 있지만, 가장 헌신적이고 사심 없는 애정의 대상한테 의심받는 짐까지 더해서는 도저히 삶을 견딜 수 없다는 걸 보여 줄 기회로 삼았지. 이 말에 맨탈리니 부인의 눈에 다시 눈물이 고였어."], ["This brought fresh tears into Madame Mantalini’s eyes, which having just begun to open to some few of the demerits of Mr. Mantalini, were only open a very little way, and could be easily closed again.", "그녀의 눈은 맨탈리니 씨의 흠 몇 가지에 이제 막 뜨이기 시작한 참이라, 아주 조금밖에 안 떠진 데다 쉽게 도로 감겨 버릴 수 있었거든."], ["The result was, that without quite giving up the allowance question, Madame Mantalini, postponed its further consideration; and Ralph saw, clearly enough, that Mr.", "그 결과, 맨탈리니 부인은 생활비 문제를 완전히 접지는 않았지만 더 따지는 건 뒤로 미뤘어. 그리고 랄프는 똑똑히 알아챘지."], ["Mantalini had gained a fresh lease of his easy life, and that, for some time longer at all events, his degradation and downfall were postponed.", "맨탈리니 씨가 그 편안한 삶을 새로 연장받았고, 적어도 한동안은 그의 몰락과 추락이 미뤄졌다는 걸 말이야."]], [["‘But it will come soon enough,’ thought Ralph; ‘all love--bah! that I should use the cant of boys and girls--is fleeting enough; though that which has its sole root in the admiration of a whiskered face like that of yonder baboon, perhaps lasts the longest, as it originates in the greater blindness and is fed by vanity. Meantime the fools bring grist to my mill, so let them live out their day, and the longer it is, the better.’", "'하지만 머지않아 닥치고 말걸.' 랄프는 생각했어. '모든 사랑이란 — 흥! 내가 어린것들 입에서나 나올 소리를 하다니 — 다 덧없는 거지. 하긴 저 개코원숭이 같은 구레나룻 얼굴에 대한 감탄에 뿌리를 둔 사랑은, 아마 가장 오래 가긴 할 거야. 더 깊은 눈멂에서 시작해서 허영으로 길러지니까. 어쨌든 그 바보들이 내 방앗간에 곡식을 갖다 주는 셈이니, 멋대로 살게 두자. 길면 길수록 좋지.'"]], [["These agreeable reflections occurred to Ralph Nickleby, as sundry small caresses and endearments, supposed to be unseen, were exchanged between the objects of his thoughts.", "이런 흐뭇한 생각이 랄프 니클비의 머릿속에 떠올랐어. 그가 생각하던 그 두 사람이, 안 보이는 줄 알고 자잘하게 쓰다듬고 어루만지며 정을 나누는 동안 말이지."]], [["‘If you have nothing more to say, my dear, to Mr.", "\"니클비 씨한테 더 하실 말씀이 없으면, 여보,\""], ["Nickleby,’ said Madame Mantalini, ‘we will take our leaves.", "맨탈리니 부인이 말했어. \"우린 그만 가요."], ["I am sure we have detained him much too long already.’", "분명 너무 오래 붙잡아 둔 것 같으니까요.\""]], [["Mr.", "맨탈리니 씨는 우선 맨탈리니 부인의 코를 몇 번 톡톡 두드리는 걸로 대답을 대신했어."], ["Mantalini answered, in the first instance, by tapping Madame Mantalini several times on the nose, and then, by remarking in words that he had nothing more to say.", "그러고는 말로 더 할 얘기는 없다고 했지."]], [["‘Demmit!", "\"빌어먹을!"], ["I have, though,’ he added almost immediately, drawing Ralph into a corner.", "그래도 있긴 있어.\" 그가 거의 곧바로 덧붙이며, 랄프를 구석으로 끌고 갔어."], ["‘Here’s an affair about your friend Sir Mulberry.", "\"자네 친구 멀버리 경 일이야."], ["Such a demd extraordinary out-of-the-way kind of thing as never was--eh?’", "이런 빌어먹게 별나고 희한한 일이 또 없을 거야, 응?\""]], [["‘What do you mean?’ asked Ralph.", "\"무슨 소리요?\" 랄프가 물었어."]], [["‘Don’t you know, demmit?’ asked Mr.", "\"자네 모르나, 빌어먹을?\""], ["Mantalini.", "맨탈리니 씨가 물었어."]], [["‘I see by the paper that he was thrown from his cabriolet last night, and severely injured, and that his life is in some danger,’ answered Ralph with great composure; ‘but I see nothing extraordinary in that--accidents are not miraculous events, when men live hard, and drive after dinner.’", "\"신문에서 봤소만, 그가 어젯밤 이륜마차에서 떨어져서 크게 다쳤고, 목숨이 좀 위태롭다고 하더군.\" 랄프가 아주 침착하게 대답했어. \"하지만 거기 무슨 별난 게 있다는 건지 모르겠소. 사람이 험하게 살고 저녁 먹고 마차를 몰면, 사고란 기적 같은 일이 아니잖소.\""]], [["‘Whew!’ cried Mr.", "\"휘유!\""], ["Mantalini in a long shrill whistle.", "맨탈리니 씨가 길고 날카롭게 휘파람을 불며 외쳤어."], ["‘Then don’t you know how it was?’", "\"그럼 어쩌다 그렇게 됐는지는 모르나?\""]], [["‘Not unless it was as I have just supposed,’ replied Ralph, shrugging his shoulders carelessly, as if to give his questioner to understand that he had no curiosity upon the subject.", "\"방금 내가 짐작한 대로가 아니라면 모르지.\" 랄프가 무심하게 어깨를 으쓱하며 대답했어. 그 일에 도통 궁금한 게 없다는 걸 묻는 사람한테 알려 주려는 듯이 말이야."]], [["‘Demmit, you amaze me,’ cried Mantalini.", "\"빌어먹을, 자네 정말 날 놀라게 하는군.\" 맨탈리니가 외쳤어."]], [["Ralph shrugged his shoulders again, as if it were no great feat to amaze Mr. Mantalini, and cast a wistful glance at the face of Newman Noggs, which had several times appeared behind a couple of panes of glass in the room door; it being a part of Newman’s duty, when unimportant people called, to make various feints of supposing that the bell had rung for him to show them out: by way of a gentle hint to such visitors that it was time to go.", "랄프는 다시 어깨를 으쓱했어. 맨탈리니 씨를 놀라게 하는 게 무슨 대단한 재주냐는 듯이 말이지. 그러고는 뉴먼 노그스의 얼굴을 아쉬운 듯 흘끔 쳐다봤어. 그 얼굴은 사무실 문에 끼운 유리 두 장 너머로 몇 번이나 나타났거든. 시시한 사람들이 찾아오면, 그들을 배웅하라고 종이 울린 줄로 착각하는 척하는 게 뉴먼의 일거리 가운데 하나였어. 그런 손님들한테 이제 갈 시간이라는 걸 넌지시 일러 주는 셈이었지."]], [["‘Don’t you know,’ said Mr.", "\"자네 모르나,\""], ["Mantalini, taking Ralph by the button, ‘that it wasn’t an accident at all, but a demd, furious, manslaughtering attack made upon him by your nephew?’", "맨탈리니 씨가 랄프의 단추를 잡으며 말했어. \"그게 사고가 전혀 아니라, 자네 조카가 그 사람한테 가한 빌어먹게 사납고 사람 잡을 듯한 공격이었다는 걸?\""]], [["‘What!’ snarled Ralph, clenching his fists and turning a livid white.", "\"뭐라고!\" 랄프가 두 주먹을 불끈 쥐고 새파랗게 질리며 으르렁댔어."]], [["‘Demmit, Nickleby, you’re as great a tiger as he is,’ said Mantalini, alarmed at these demonstrations.", "\"빌어먹을, 니클비, 자네도 그 사람만큼이나 대단한 호랑이로군.\" 맨탈리니가 이 기세에 놀라서 말했어."]], [["‘Go on,’ cried Ralph.", "\"계속해 보시오.\" 랄프가 외쳤어."], ["‘Tell me what you mean.", "\"무슨 소린지 말해 보란 말이오."], ["What is this story?", "이게 무슨 얘기요?"], ["Who told you?", "누가 그러던가?"], ["Speak,’ growled Ralph.", "말하시오.\" 랄프가 으르렁댔어."], ["‘Do you hear me?’", "\"내 말 안 들리오?\""]], [["‘’Gad, Nickleby,’ said Mr.", "\"이런, 니클비.\""], ["Mantalini, retreating towards his wife, ‘what a demneble fierce old evil genius you are!", "맨탈리니 씨가 아내 쪽으로 물러서며 말했어. \"자넨 정말 빌어먹게 사나운 악령 영감이로군!"], ["You’re enough to frighten the life and soul out of her little delicious wits--flying all at once into such a blazing, ravaging, raging passion as never was, demmit!’", "이 여자의 그 가냘프고 사랑스러운 정신줄을 다 놀라 날아가게 할 판이야. 단번에 이렇게 활활 타오르고 미쳐 날뛰는, 전에 없던 격분으로 치닫다니, 빌어먹을!\""]], [["‘Pshaw,’ rejoined Ralph, forcing a smile.", "\"쳇.\" 랄프가 억지웃음을 지으며 받아쳤어."], ["‘It is but manner.’", "\"그저 버릇일 뿐이오.\""]], [["‘It is a demd uncomfortable, private-madhouse-sort of a manner,’ said Mr Mantalini, picking up his cane.", "\"그거 참 빌어먹게 거북한, 사설 정신병원 같은 버릇이로군.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 지팡이를 주워 들며 말했어."]], [["Ralph affected to smile, and once more inquired from whom Mr. Mantalini had derived his information.", "랄프는 웃는 척하면서, 맨탈리니 씨가 그 얘기를 누구한테 들었는지 다시 한번 물었어."]], [["‘From Pyke; and a demd, fine, pleasant, gentlemanly dog it is,’ replied Mantalini.", "\"파이크한테 들었지. 빌어먹게 멋지고 유쾌하고 신사다운 친구야.\""], ["‘Demnition pleasant, and a tip-top sawyer.’", "맨탈리니가 대답했어. \"빌어먹게 즐거운 데다, 일류 멋쟁이고.\""]], [["‘And what said he?’ asked Ralph, knitting his brows.", "\"그 사람이 뭐라던가?\" 랄프가 미간을 찌푸리며 물었어."]], [["‘That it happened this way--that your nephew met him at a coffeehouse, fell upon him with the most demneble ferocity, followed him to his cab, swore he would ride home with him, if he rode upon the horse’s back or hooked himself on to the horse’s tail; smashed his countenance, which is a demd fine countenance in its natural state; frightened the horse, pitched out Sir Mulberry and himself, and--’", "\"이렇게 됐다는군. 자네 조카가 커피집에서 그 사람을 만나, 빌어먹게 사납게 덤벼들었다는 거야. 그러고는 마차까지 따라가서, 말 등에 올라타든 말 꼬리에 매달리든 같이 집까지 타고 가겠다고 우겼고. 그러다 그 얼굴을 박살 냈다더군. 본래는 빌어먹게 멋진 얼굴인데 말이지. 그러고 말을 놀라게 해서, 멀버리 경하고 저 자신을 마차 밖으로 내동댕이쳐 버리고, 그래서—\""]], [["‘And was killed?’ interposed Ralph with gleaming eyes.", "\"그래서 죽었소?\" 랄프가 눈을 번득이며 끼어들었어."], ["‘Was he?", "\"그랬소?"], ["Is he dead?’", "그가 죽었소?\""]], [["Mantalini shook his head.", "맨탈리니가 고개를 저었어."]], [["‘Ugh,’ said Ralph, turning away.", "\"으.\" 랄프가 몸을 돌리며 말했어."], ["‘Then he has done nothing.", "\"그럼 아무 일도 안 한 거나 마찬가지군."], ["Stay,’ he added, looking round again.", "잠깐.\" 그가 다시 돌아보며 덧붙였어."], ["‘He broke a leg or an arm, or put his shoulder out, or fractured his collar-bone, or ground a rib or two?", "\"다리나 팔을 부러뜨렸소, 아니면 어깨가 빠졌소, 아니면 쇄골이 부러졌소, 아니면 갈비뼈 한두 대가 으스러졌소?"], ["His neck was saved for the halter, but he got some painful and slow-healing injury for his trouble?", "목은 교수대용으로 무사히 건졌지만, 그 대가로 아프고 더디게 낫는 상처라도 입었소?"], ["Did he?", "그랬소?"], ["You must have heard that, at least.’", "그 정도는 들었을 거 아니오.\""]], [["‘No,’ rejoined Mantalini, shaking his head again.", "\"아니.\" 맨탈리니가 다시 고개를 저으며 대꾸했어."], ["‘Unless he was dashed into such little pieces that they blew away, he wasn’t hurt, for he went off as quiet and comfortable as--as--as demnition,’ said Mr.", "\"산산조각이 나서 가루처럼 날아가 버리기라도 했다면 모를까, 다친 데는 없어. 아주 조용하고 편안하게 떠났거든."], ["Mantalini, rather at a loss for a simile.", "마치, 마치, 마치 빌어먹게 말이야.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 비유할 말을 못 찾아 더듬거리며 말했어."]], [["‘And what,’ said Ralph, hesitating a little, ‘what was the cause of quarrel?’", "\"그런데,\" 랄프가 조금 머뭇거리며 말했어. \"싸움의 원인은 뭐였소?\""]], [["‘You are the demdest, knowing hand,’ replied Mr. Mantalini, in an admiring tone, ‘the cunningest, rummest, superlativest old fox--oh dem!--to pretend now not to know that it was the little bright-eyed niece--the softest, sweetest, prettiest--’", "\"자넨 정말 빌어먹게 잘 아는 양반이야.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 감탄하는 투로 대답했어. \"가장 약삭빠르고, 가장 별나고, 가장 으뜸가는 늙은 여우라니까. 아이고 빌어먹을! 그게 그 작고 눈이 반짝이는 조카딸 때문인 걸 이제 와 모르는 척하다니. 그 더없이 보드랍고, 더없이 달콤하고, 더없이 어여쁜—\""]], [["‘Alfred!’ interposed Madame Mantalini.", "\"앨프리드!\" 맨탈리니 부인이 끼어들었어."]], [["‘She is always right,’ rejoined Mr. Mantalini soothingly, ‘and when she says it is time to go, it is time, and go she shall; and when she walks along the streets with her own tulip, the women shall say, with envy, she has got a demd fine husband; and the men shall say with rapture, he has got a demd fine wife; and they shall both be right and neither wrong, upon my life and soul--oh demmit!’", "\"이 여자가 늘 옳지.\" 맨탈리니 씨가 달래듯 대꾸했어. \"이 여자가 갈 시간이라고 하면 갈 시간이고, 그러니 가야지. 그리고 이 여자가 제 튤립 같은 임하고 거리를 걸으면, 여자들은 부러워하며 말할 거야. 저 여자 빌어먹게 멋진 남편을 뒀다고. 그리고 남자들은 황홀해하며 말하겠지. 저 남자 빌어먹게 멋진 아내를 뒀다고. 그러면 둘 다 맞고 누구도 틀린 게 아니지, 내 목숨이자 영혼을 걸고. 아이고 빌어먹을!\""]], [["With which remarks, and many more, no less intellectual and to the purpose, Mr.", "이런 말들을, 그리고 그에 못지않게 지적이고 핵심을 찌르는 말들을 잔뜩 늘어놓으며, 맨탈리니 씨는 장갑 낀 손가락에 입을 맞춰 랄프 니클비한테 인사를 보냈어."], ["Mantalini kissed the fingers of his gloves to Ralph Nickleby, and drawing his lady’s arm through his, led her mincingly away.", "그러고는 아내의 팔을 제 팔에 끼고, 점잔을 빼며 그녀를 데리고 나갔지."]], [["‘So, so,’ muttered Ralph, dropping into his chair; ‘this devil is loose again, and thwarting me, as he was born to do, at every turn.", "\"좋아, 좋아.\" 랄프가 의자에 털썩 주저앉으며 중얼거렸어."], ["He told me once there should be a day of reckoning between us, sooner or later.", "\"이 악마 같은 놈이 또 풀려나서, 타고난 팔자대로 사사건건 나를 가로막는군. 그놈이 언젠가 우리 사이에 결판을 낼 날이 올 거라고 했었지."], ["I’ll make him a true prophet, for it shall surely come.’", "내가 그놈을 정확한 예언자로 만들어 주마. 그날은 틀림없이 오고 말 테니까.\""]], [["‘Are you at home?’ asked Newman, suddenly popping in his head.", "\"안에 계세요?\" 뉴먼이 불쑥 고개를 들이밀며 물었어."]], [["‘No,’ replied Ralph, with equal abruptness.", "\"아니.\" 랄프가 똑같이 퉁명스럽게 대답했어."]], [["Newman withdrew his head, but thrust it in again.", "뉴먼은 고개를 도로 뺐다가, 다시 들이밀었어."]], [["‘You’re quite sure you’re not at home, are you?’ said Newman.", "\"안에 안 계신 거 확실하시죠, 그렇죠?\" 뉴먼이 말했어."]], [["‘What does the idiot mean?’ cried Ralph, testily.", "\"이 멍청이가 무슨 소릴 하는 거야?\" 랄프가 짜증을 내며 외쳤어."]], [["‘He has been waiting nearly ever since they first came in, and may have heard your voice--that’s all,’ said Newman, rubbing his hands.", "\"저 사람들이 처음 들어왔을 때부터 거의 내내 기다리던 사람이 있어서요. 어쩌면 사장님 목소리를 들었을지도 모르고요. 그게 답니다.\" 뉴먼이 두 손을 비비며 말했어."]], [["‘Who has?’ demanded Ralph, wrought by the intelligence he had just heard, and his clerk’s provoking coolness, to an intense pitch of irritation.", "\"누가?\" 랄프가 다그쳤어. 방금 들은 소식과 서기의 약 올리는 천연덕스러움에 짜증이 머리끝까지 치밀어 오른 참이었지."]], [["The necessity of a reply was superseded by the unlooked-for entrance of a third party--the individual in question--who, bringing his one eye (for he had but one) to bear on Ralph Nickleby, made a great many shambling bows, and sat himself down in an armchair, with his hands on his knees, and his short black trousers drawn up so high in the legs by the exertion of seating himself, that they scarcely reached below the tops of his Wellington boots.", "대답할 필요는 사라졌어. 뜻밖에도 세 번째 사람이 들어왔거든. 바로 그 문제의 인물이었지. 그는 외눈을 (그에겐 눈이 하나뿐이었으니까) 랄프 니클비한테 돌리고, 어기적어기적 인사를 잔뜩 하더니, 안락의자에 앉았어. 두 손을 무릎에 올리고서 말이야. 앉느라 끙끙대는 통에 짧은 검정 바지가 다리 위로 너무 높이 끌려 올라가서, 웰링턴 부츠 윗부분에도 겨우 닿을락 말락 했지."]], [["‘Why, this IS a surprise!’ said Ralph, bending his gaze upon the visitor, and half smiling as he scrutinised him attentively; ‘I should know your face, Mr.", "\"아니, 이거 놀랍구려!\" 랄프가 손님한테 시선을 모으며, 유심히 뜯어보면서 반쯤 미소 지었어."], ["Squeers.’", "\"당신 얼굴, 알 것 같소만. 스퀴어스 씨.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ replied that worthy, ‘and you’d have know’d it better, sir, if it hadn’t been for all that I’ve been a-going through.", "\"아!\" 그 양반이 대답했어."], ["Just lift that little boy off the tall stool in the back-office, and tell him to come in here, will you, my man?’ said Squeers, addressing himself to Newman.", "\"더 잘 알아보셨을 거요, 선생. 내가 겪은 그 모든 일만 없었어도 말이오."], ["‘Oh, he’s lifted his-self off.", "저기 뒤 사무실 높은 걸상에서 저 꼬마 좀 내려놓고, 이리 들어오라고 해 주겠나, 이 사람아?\" 스퀴어스가 뉴먼한테 말했어."], ["My son, sir, little Wackford.", "\"오, 자기가 알아서 내려왔구먼. 제 자식이오, 선생."], ["What do you think of him, sir, for a specimen of the Dotheboys Hall feeding?", "꼬마 왝퍼드라오. 어떻소, 선생, 두더보이즈 홀 먹성의 본보기로 말이오?"], ["Ain’t he fit to bust out of his clothes, and start the seams, and make the very buttons fly off with his fatness?", "살이 쪄서 옷이 터지고 솔기가 뜯어지고, 단추가 다 튀어나갈 지경이지 않소? 이 살 좀 보쇼!\""], ["Here’s flesh!’ cried Squeers, turning the boy about, and indenting the plumpest parts of his figure with divers pokes and punches, to the great discomposure of his son and heir.", "스퀴어스가 아이를 빙빙 돌리며, 통통한 부위마다 이리저리 쿡쿡 찌르고 쥐어박으며 외쳤어. 제 아들이자 후계자가 잔뜩 곤혹스러워하는데도 말이지."], ["‘Here’s firmness, here’s solidness!", "\"이 단단함 좀 보쇼, 이 야무짐 좀 보쇼!"], ["Why you can hardly get up enough of him between your finger and thumb to pinch him anywheres.’", "글쎄, 손가락하고 엄지로 꼬집을 살을 어디서도 집어 올릴 수가 없을 정도라니까.\""]], [["In however good condition Master Squeers might have been, he certainly did not present this remarkable compactness of person, for on his father’s closing his finger and thumb in illustration of his remark, he uttered a sharp cry, and rubbed the place in the most natural manner possible.", "왝퍼드 도련님 몸 상태가 아무리 좋았다 한들, 그렇게 놀랍도록 야무지지는 분명 못했어. 아버지가 그 말을 증명하려고 손가락하고 엄지를 오므리자, 아이가 날카롭게 비명을 지르며 더없이 자연스럽게 그 자리를 문질렀거든."]], [["‘Well,’ remarked Squeers, a little disconcerted, ‘I had him there; but that’s because we breakfasted early this morning, and he hasn’t had his lunch yet. Why you couldn’t shut a bit of him in a door, when he’s had his dinner. Look at them tears, sir,’ said Squeers, with a triumphant air, as Master Wackford wiped his eyes with the cuff of his jacket, ‘there’s oiliness!’", "\"음.\" 스퀴어스가 조금 머쓱해져서 말했어. \"이번엔 잘못 집었구먼. 그치만 그건 오늘 아침에 일찍 먹어서 그래. 아직 점심을 안 먹었거든. 글쎄, 이 녀석이 저녁만 먹었다 하면 문틈에 살점 하나 끼울 수가 없다오. 저 눈물 좀 보쇼, 선생.\" 왝퍼드 도련님이 윗옷 소매로 눈물을 닦자, 스퀴어스가 의기양양하게 말했어. \"기름기가 자르르하지 않소!\""]], [["‘He looks well, indeed,’ returned Ralph, who, for some purposes of his own, seemed desirous to conciliate the schoolmaster.", "\"정말 건강해 보이는군요.\" 랄프가 대답했어."], ["‘But how is Mrs Squeers, and how are you?’", "자기 나름의 속셈이 있어서, 이 교장 선생 비위를 맞추고 싶어 하는 눈치였지. \"그런데 스퀴어스 부인은 잘 계시오, 당신은 또 어떻고?\""]], [["‘Mrs. Squeers, sir,’ replied the proprietor of Dotheboys, ‘is as she always is--a mother to them lads, and a blessing, and a comfort, and a joy to all them as knows her. One of our boys--gorging his-self with vittles, and then turning in; that’s their way--got a abscess on him last week. To see how she operated upon him with a pen-knife! Oh Lor!’ said Squeers, heaving a sigh, and nodding his head a great many times, ‘what a member of society that woman is!’", "\"스퀴어스 부인이야, 선생.\" 두더보이즈의 주인이 대답했어. \"늘 그대로라오. 그 애들한테 어머니 같은 사람이고, 그 사람을 아는 모든 이들한테 축복이자 위안이자 기쁨이지. 우리 애들 중 하나가, 그놈이 음식을 잔뜩 처먹고 곯아떨어졌거든. 그게 그놈들 버릇이지. 지난주에 종기가 났지 뭐요. 그걸 우리 부인이 주머니칼로 어떻게 처치했는지 봤어야 하는데! 아이고 맙소사!\" 스퀴어스가 한숨을 쉬며 고개를 몇 번이고 끄덕이며 말했어. \"그 사람이야말로 참 대단한 사회의 일원이라오!\""]], [["Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨는 한 십오 초쯤 지난 일을 회상하는 표정을 지었어. 부인의 그 훌륭함을 떠올리다 보니, 자연스레 마음이 요크셔 그레타 다리 근처 두더보이즈의 그 평화로운 마을로 흘러간 모양이었지."], ["Squeers indulged in a retrospective look, for some quarter of a minute, as if this allusion to his lady’s excellences had naturally led his mind to the peaceful village of Dotheboys near Greta Bridge in Yorkshire; and then looked at Ralph, as if waiting for him to say something.", "그러고는 랄프를 쳐다봤어. 무슨 말이라도 해 주길 기다리는 듯이 말이야."]], [["‘Have you quite recovered that scoundrel’s attack?’ asked Ralph.", "\"그 악당 놈의 공격은 다 회복하셨소?\" 랄프가 물었어."]], [["‘I’ve only just done it, if I’ve done it now,’ replied Squeers.", "\"이제 막 나았다오, 나은 거라면 말이지.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."], ["‘I was one blessed bruise, sir,’ said Squeers, touching first the roots of his hair, and then the toes of his boots, ‘from HERE to THERE.", "\"온통 멍투성이였소, 선생.\" 스퀴어스가 먼저 머리뿌리를, 그다음 부츠 끝을 가리키며 말했어."], ["Vinegar and brown paper, vinegar and brown paper, from morning to night.", "\"여기서부터 저기까지 말이오. 식초하고 갈색 종이, 식초하고 갈색 종이를 아침부터 밤까지 붙였지."], ["I suppose there was a matter of half a ream of brown paper stuck upon me, from first to last.", "처음부터 끝까지 갈색 종이를 반 연쯤은 내 몸에 붙였을 거요."], ["As I laid all of a heap in our kitchen, plastered all over, you might have thought I was a large brown-paper parcel, chock full of nothing but groans.", "우리 집 부엌에 온통 종이를 바른 채 한 덩어리로 뻗어 있으니, 신음 소리만 잔뜩 들어찬 커다란 갈색 종이 꾸러미인 줄 알았을 거요."], ["Did I groan loud, Wackford, or did I groan soft?’ asked Mr.", "내가 크게 신음했냐, 작게 신음했냐, 왝퍼드?\""], ["Squeers, appealing to his son.", "스퀴어스 씨가 아들한테 물었어."]], [["‘Loud,’ replied Wackford.", "\"크게요.\" 왝퍼드가 대답했어."]], [["‘Was the boys sorry to see me in such a dreadful condition, Wackford, or was they glad?’ asked Mr.", "\"내가 그 끔찍한 꼴인 걸 보고 애들이 안타까워했냐, 왝퍼드, 아니면 좋아했냐?\""], ["Squeers, in a sentimental manner.", "스퀴어스 씨가 감상적인 투로 물었어."]], [["‘Gl--’", "\"좋아—\""]], [["‘Eh?’ cried Squeers, turning sharp round.", "\"엉?\" 스퀴어스가 휙 돌아보며 외쳤어."]], [["‘Sorry,’ rejoined his son.", "\"안타까워했어요.\" 아들이 고쳐 말했어."]], [["‘Oh!’ said Squeers, catching him a smart box on the ear.", "\"오!\" 스퀴어스가 아이 귀싸대기를 호되게 한 대 후려치며 말했어."], ["‘Then take your hands out of your pockets, and don’t stammer when you’re asked a question.", "\"그럼 호주머니에서 손 빼고, 질문받으면 더듬거리지 마라."], ["Hold your noise, sir, in a gentleman’s office, or I’ll run away from my family and never come back any more; and then what would become of all them precious and forlorn lads as would be let loose on the world, without their best friend at their elbers?’", "신사분 사무실에서 시끄럽게 굴지 마, 안 그러면 식구들 버리고 도망쳐서 다시는 안 돌아올 거야. 그럼 자기 곁에 둘도 없는 친구도 없이 세상에 내팽개쳐질, 그 소중하고 가엾은 애들은 다 어떻게 되겠어?\""]], [["‘Were you obliged to have medical attendance?’ inquired Ralph.", "\"의사한테 진찰을 받아야 했소?\" 랄프가 물었어."]], [["‘Ay, was I,’ rejoined Squeers, ‘and a precious bill the medical attendant brought in too; but I paid it though.’", "\"아, 그랬지.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어. \"그 의사 양반이 청구서도 어마어마하게 들이밀었고. 그래도 내가 다 냈지만.\""]], [["Ralph elevated his eyebrows in a manner which might be expressive of either sympathy or astonishment--just as the beholder was pleased to take it.", "랄프는 눈썹을 치켜올렸어. 동정으로도 놀라움으로도 받아들일 수 있는 그런 모양으로 말이야. 보는 사람 마음대로 받아들이라는 듯이."]], [["‘Yes, I paid it, every farthing,’ replied Squeers, who seemed to know the man he had to deal with, too well to suppose that any blinking of the question would induce him to subscribe towards the expenses; ‘I wasn’t out of pocket by it after all, either.’", "\"그래, 한 푼도 안 남기고 다 냈지.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. 자기가 상대하는 사람을 너무 잘 알아서, 이 문제를 어물쩍 넘긴다고 랄프가 비용을 보태 줄 리 없다는 걸 아는 눈치였지. \"그래도 결국 내 주머니에서 나간 건 없었지만.\""]], [["‘No!’ said Ralph.", "\"그래요?\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Not a halfpenny,’ replied Squeers.", "\"반 페니도 안 나갔지.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."], ["‘The fact is, we have only one extra with our boys, and that is for doctors when required--and not then, unless we’re sure of our customers.", "\"실은 말이오, 우린 애들한테 추가로 받는 게 딱 하나뿐인데, 그게 필요할 때 부르는 의사 비용이거든. 그것도 손님한테 확실히 받아 낼 수 있을 때만 그렇고."], ["Do you see?’", "알아듣겠소?\""]], [["‘I understand,’ said Ralph.", "\"알겠소.\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Very good,’ rejoined Squeers.", "\"아주 좋아.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어."], ["‘Then, after my bill was run up, we picked out five little boys (sons of small tradesmen, as was sure pay) that had never had the scarlet fever, and we sent one to a cottage where they’d got it, and he took it, and then we put the four others to sleep with him, and THEY took it, and then the doctor came and attended ‘em once all round, and we divided my total among ‘em, and added it on to their little bills, and the parents paid it.", "\"그럼, 내 청구서가 쌓인 다음에, 우린 성홍열을 한 번도 안 앓은 꼬마 다섯을 골랐지. (돈 떼일 걱정 없는 영세 상인들 아들로 말이오.) 그러고 한 놈을 성홍열 걸린 집 오두막에 보냈더니, 그놈이 옮아 왔고, 그다음 나머지 넷을 그놈하고 같이 재웠더니, 그놈들도 옮았지."], ["Ha! ha!", "그러고 의사가 와서 한꺼번에 다 봐 줬고, 우린 내 청구서 총액을 그놈들한테 나눠서, 각자 청구서에 얹었더니, 부모들이 다 냈지."], ["ha!’", "하, 하, 하!\""]], [["‘And a good plan too,’ said Ralph, eyeing the schoolmaster stealthily.", "\"그것도 좋은 방법이군요.\" 랄프가 교장을 슬쩍 곁눈질하며 말했어."]], [["‘I believe you,’ rejoined Squeers.", "\"그럼요.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어."], ["‘We always do it.", "\"우린 늘 그렇게 하지."], ["Why, when Mrs Squeers was brought to bed with little Wackford here, we ran the hooping-cough through half-a-dozen boys, and charged her expenses among ‘em, monthly nurse included. Ha!", "글쎄, 스퀴어스 부인이 여기 이 꼬마 왝퍼드를 낳고 자리보전했을 때도, 백일해를 애들 대여섯한테 옮겨서, 산파까지 포함한 부인 비용을 그놈들한테 나눠 매겼다오."], ["ha! ha!’", "하, 하, 하!\""]], [["Ralph never laughed, but on this occasion he produced the nearest approach to it that he could, and waiting until Mr.", "랄프는 좀처럼 웃는 법이 없었지만, 이번만큼은 웃음에 최대한 가깝게 흉내를 냈어."], ["Squeers had enjoyed the professional joke to his heart’s content, inquired what had brought him to town.", "그러고는 스퀴어스 씨가 그 직업적인 농담을 실컷 즐길 때까지 기다렸다가, 무슨 일로 도시에 왔냐고 물었지."]], [["‘Some bothering law business,’ replied Squeers, scratching his head, ‘connected with an action, for what they call neglect of a boy.", "\"골치 아픈 법률 일 때문이오.\" 스퀴어스가 머리를 긁적이며 대답했어."], ["I don’t know what they would have.", "\"그 사람들 말로 학생을 방치했다나 뭐라나, 그것 때문에 거는 소송하고 얽힌 일이지."], ["He had as good grazing, that boy had, as there is about us.’", "대체 뭘 어쩌라는 건지 모르겠소. 그 녀석은 우리 동네 어디 못지않게 좋은 풀밭을 누렸거든.\""]], [["Ralph looked as if he did not quite understand the observation.", "랄프는 그 말을 잘 못 알아들은 듯한 표정을 지었어."]], [["‘Grazing,’ said Squeers, raising his voice, under the impression that as Ralph failed to comprehend him, he must be deaf. ‘When a boy gets weak and ill and don’t relish his meals, we give him a change of diet--turn him out, for an hour or so every day, into a neighbour’s turnip field, or sometimes, if it’s a delicate case, a turnip field and a piece of carrots alternately, and let him eat as many as he likes. There an’t better land in the country than this perwerse lad grazed on, and yet he goes and catches cold and indigestion and what not, and then his friends brings a lawsuit against ME! Now, you’d hardly suppose,’ added Squeers, moving in his chair with the impatience of an ill-used man, ‘that people’s ingratitude would carry them quite as far as that; would you?’", "\"풀밭 말이오.\" 스퀴어스가 목소리를 높이며 말했어. 랄프가 못 알아듣는 걸 보고, 귀가 먼 모양이라고 여겼거든. \"애가 약해지고 아프고 밥맛이 없어지면, 우린 식단을 바꿔 준다오. 하루에 한 시간쯤 이웃 순무밭에 내보내거나, 아니면 가끔, 좀 까다로운 경우엔 순무밭하고 당근밭을 번갈아 내보내서, 먹고 싶은 만큼 먹게 하지. 이 못된 녀석이 풀을 뜯은 데보다 더 좋은 땅은 이 나라에 없을 거요. 그런데도 가서 감기에 소화불량에 별걸 다 걸리더니, 그 녀석 친구들이 나한테 소송을 건단 말이오! 자, 설마 사람들 배은망덕이 그 지경까지 갈 줄은 미처 몰랐을 거요.\" 스퀴어스가 부당한 일을 당한 사람처럼 조바심을 내며 의자에서 몸을 들썩이며 덧붙였어. \"안 그렇소?\""]], [["‘A hard case, indeed,’ observed Ralph.", "\"참 딱한 일이군요.\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘You don’t say more than the truth when you say that,’ replied Squeers.", "\"그렇게 말하는 게 딱 맞는 말이오.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."], ["‘I don’t suppose there’s a man going, as possesses the fondness for youth that I do.", "\"나만큼 아이들을 끔찍이 아끼는 사람이 또 있을 것 같진 않소."], ["There’s youth to the amount of eight hundred pound a year at Dotheboys Hall at this present time.", "지금 두더보이즈 홀엔 한 해에 팔백 파운드어치 아이들이 있다오."], ["I’d take sixteen hundred pound worth if I could get ‘em, and be as fond of every individual twenty pound among ‘em as nothing should equal it!’", "천육백 파운드어치라도 데려올 수 있다면 데려와서, 그 한 명 한 명 이십 파운드짜리 하나하나를 무엇과도 견줄 수 없을 만큼 아껴 줄 텐데!\""]], [["‘Are you stopping at your old quarters?’ asked Ralph.", "\"전에 묵던 데 그대로요?\" 랄프가 물었어."]], [["‘Yes, we are at the Saracen,’ replied Squeers, ‘and as it don’t want very long to the end of the half-year, we shall continney to stop there till I’ve collected the money, and some new boys too, I hope.", "\"그렇소, 사라센 여관에 있다오.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."], ["I’ve brought little Wackford up, on purpose to show to parents and guardians.", "\"이제 학기 끝날 날도 얼마 안 남았으니, 돈을 다 거둘 때까지, 또 새 학생들도 좀 받을 때까지 거기 계속 묵을 거요."], ["I shall put him in the advertisement, this time.", "꼬마 왝퍼드를 일부러 데려온 것도 부모하고 보호자들한테 보여 주려고 그런 거지. 이번엔 광고에도 넣을 거요."], ["Look at that boy--himself a pupil.", "이 아이 좀 보쇼, 제 자신이 학생이오."], ["Why he’s a miracle of high feeding, that boy is!’", "글쎄, 이 아이야말로 잘 먹인 기적이라니까!\""]], [["‘I should like to have a word with you,’ said Ralph, who had both spoken and listened mechanically for some time, and seemed to have been thinking.", "\"긴히 한마디 나누고 싶소만.\" 랄프가 말했어. 그는 한동안 말도 듣기도 기계적으로 해 왔고, 무슨 생각에 잠겨 있는 듯했지."]], [["‘As many words as you like, sir,’ rejoined Squeers.", "\"얼마든지 말씀하시오, 선생.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어."], ["‘Wackford, you go and play in the back office, and don’t move about too much or you’ll get thin, and that won’t do.", "\"왝퍼드, 너는 뒤 사무실에 가서 놀아라. 너무 많이 움직이진 말고, 안 그럼 살 빠진다, 그럼 안 되니까."], ["You haven’t got such a thing as twopence, Mr Nickleby, have you?’ said Squeers, rattling a bunch of keys in his coat pocket, and muttering something about its being all silver.", "혹시 이 펜스 같은 거 가지고 계시오, 니클비 씨?\" 스퀴어스가 외투 호주머니 속 열쇠 꾸러미를 달그락거리며, 죄다 은화뿐이라고 우물거리며 말했어."]], [["‘I--think I have,’ said Ralph, very slowly, and producing, after much rummaging in an old drawer, a penny, a halfpenny, and two farthings.", "\"있을, 거요, 아마.\" 랄프가 아주 천천히 말했어. 그러고는 낡은 서랍을 한참 뒤진 끝에, 일 페니짜리 하나에 반 페니짜리 하나, 사 분의 일 페니짜리 둘을 꺼냈어."]], [["‘Thankee,’ said Squeers, bestowing it upon his son.", "\"고맙소.\" 스퀴어스가 그걸 아들한테 주며 말했어."], ["‘Here!", "\"자! 가서 타르트 하나 사 먹어라."], ["You go and buy a tart--Mr.", "니클비 씨 일꾼이 어딘지 알려 줄 거야. 그리고 명심해라, 진한 걸로 사야 한다."], ["Nickleby’s man will show you where--and mind you buy a rich one.", "페이스트리는,\" 스퀴어스가 왝퍼드 도련님 뒤로 문을 닫으며 덧붙였어."], ["Pastry,’ added Squeers, closing the door on Master Wackford, ‘makes his flesh shine a good deal, and parents thinks that a healthy sign.’", "\"이 녀석 살에 윤기를 꽤 내 주거든. 그럼 부모들은 그걸 건강한 표시로 여기지.\""]], [["With this explanation, and a peculiarly knowing look to eke it out, Mr.", "이렇게 설명을 늘어놓고는, 그 말을 거들려고 유난히 의미심장한 표정을 지으며, 스퀴어스 씨는 의자를 옮겨서 랄프 니클비와 그리 멀지 않게 마주 보도록 했어."], ["Squeers moved his chair so as to bring himself opposite to Ralph Nickleby at no great distance off; and having planted it to his entire satisfaction, sat down.", "그러고는 마음에 쏙 들게 자리를 잡고 앉았지."]], [["‘Attend to me,’ said Ralph, bending forward a little.", "\"내 말 잘 들으시오.\" 랄프가 몸을 조금 앞으로 숙이며 말했어."]], [["Squeers nodded.", "스퀴어스가 고개를 끄덕였어."]], [["‘I am not to suppose,’ said Ralph, ‘that you are dolt enough to forgive or forget, very readily, the violence that was committed upon you, or the exposure which accompanied it?’", "\"설마 당신이,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"당신한테 가해진 그 폭력이나, 그에 따른 망신을 그리 쉽게 용서하거나 잊을 멍청이라고는 생각하지 않소만?\""]], [["‘Devil a bit,’ replied Squeers, tartly.", "\"천만에.\" 스퀴어스가 쌀쌀맞게 대답했어."]], [["‘Or to lose an opportunity of repaying it with interest, if you could get one?’ said Ralph.", "\"아니면 기회가 생기면 그걸 이자까지 쳐서 되갚아 줄 기회를 놓칠 사람도 아니고?\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Show me one, and try,’ rejoined Squeers.", "\"하나 보여 주고 시험해 보쇼.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Some such object it was, that induced you to call on me?’ said Ralph, raising his eyes to the schoolmaster’s face.", "\"바로 그런 목적이 있어서 나를 찾아온 거 아니오?\" 랄프가 교장 얼굴로 눈을 들며 말했어."]], [["‘N-n-no, I don’t know that,’ replied Squeers.", "\"아, 아, 아니, 그건 잘 모르겠소.\""], ["‘I thought that if it was in your power to make me, besides the trifle of money you sent, any compensation--’", "스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"내 생각은, 당신이 보내 준 그 푼돈 말고도, 혹시 나한테 무슨 보상이라도 해 줄 능력이 있다면—\""]], [["‘Ah!’ cried Ralph, interrupting him.", "\"아!\" 랄프가 그의 말을 끊으며 외쳤어."], ["‘You needn’t go on.’", "\"더 말 안 해도 되오.\""]], [["After a long pause, during which Ralph appeared absorbed in contemplation, he again broke silence by asking:", "오랜 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 랄프는 생각에 잠긴 듯하다가, 이렇게 물으며 다시 침묵을 깼어."]], [["‘Who is this boy that he took with him?’", "\"그놈이 데려간 그 애는 누구요?\""]], [["Squeers stated his name.", "스퀴어스가 그 이름을 말했어."]], [["‘Was he young or old, healthy or sickly, tractable or rebellious?", "\"어렸소 나이가 들었소, 건강했소 병약했소, 고분고분했소 반항적이었소?"], ["Speak out, man,’ retorted Ralph.", "똑똑히 말해 보쇼, 이 사람아.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어."]], [["‘Why, he wasn’t young,’ answered Squeers; ‘that is, not young for a boy, you know.’", "\"글쎄, 어리진 않았소.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"그러니까, 애치고는 어리진 않았단 말이오, 알겠소?\""]], [["‘That is, he was not a boy at all, I suppose?’ interrupted Ralph.", "\"그러니까, 애라고 할 수도 없었던 거로군?\" 랄프가 끼어들었어."]], [["‘Well,’ returned Squeers, briskly, as if he felt relieved by the suggestion, ‘he might have been nigh twenty. He wouldn’t seem so old, though, to them as didn’t know him, for he was a little wanting here,’ touching his forehead; ‘nobody at home, you know, if you knocked ever so often.’", "\"음.\" 스퀴어스가 그 말에 안도한 듯 활기차게 대꾸했어. \"거의 스물쯤 됐을 거요. 그래도 모르는 사람들 눈엔 그렇게 늙어 보이진 않았소. 여기가 좀 모자랐거든.\" 그가 제 이마를 짚으며 말했어. \"아무리 두드려도 집에 아무도 없는 거지, 알겠소?\""]], [["‘And you DID knock pretty often, I dare say?’ muttered Ralph.", "\"그래서 당신이 꽤 자주 두드려 댔겠구먼, 내 짐작에?\" 랄프가 중얼거렸어."]], [["‘Pretty well,’ returned Squeers with a grin.", "\"제법 그랬지.\" 스퀴어스가 씩 웃으며 대꾸했어."]], [["‘When you wrote to acknowledge the receipt of this trifle of money as you call it,’ said Ralph, ‘you told me his friends had deserted him long ago, and that you had not the faintest clue or trace to tell you who he was.", "\"당신이 이 푼돈을, 당신 말마따나, 받았다고 확인하는 편지를 썼을 때,\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["Is that the truth?’", "\"그 애 친구들이 오래전에 버렸고, 그 애가 누군지 알아낼 단서나 흔적이라곤 털끝만큼도 없다고 했었지. 그게 사실이오?\""]], [["‘It is, worse luck!’ replied Squeers, becoming more and more easy and familiar in his manner, as Ralph pursued his inquiries with the less reserve. ‘It’s fourteen years ago, by the entry in my book, since a strange man brought him to my place, one autumn night, and left him there; paying five pound five, for his first quarter in advance. He might have been five or six year old at that time--not more.’", "\"그렇소, 재수 없게도!\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. 랄프가 거리낌 없이 캐물을수록, 그의 태도도 점점 편하고 스스럼없어졌지. \"내 장부 기록으로 보면 십사 년 전 일이오. 어떤 낯선 남자가 어느 가을밤에 그 애를 우리 집에 데려와서 두고 갔지. 첫 분기 수업료로 오 파운드 오 실링을 선불로 내고 말이오. 그때 그 애는 대여섯 살쯤 됐을 거요. 그 이상은 아니고.\""]], [["‘What more do you know about him?’ demanded Ralph.", "\"그 애에 대해 더 아는 건 뭐요?\" 랄프가 다그쳤어."]], [["‘Devilish little, I’m sorry to say,’ replied Squeers.", "\"빌어먹게 별로 없소, 안타깝게도.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."], ["‘The money was paid for some six or eight year, and then it stopped.", "\"수업료가 한 육칠 년쯤 들어오다가, 그러고 끊겼지."], ["He had given an address in London, had this chap; but when it came to the point, of course nobody knowed anything about him.", "이 사람이 런던 주소를 하나 주긴 했소만, 막상 따져 보니 당연히 아무도 그 사람을 아는 이가 없더군."], ["So I kept the lad out of--out of--’", "그래서 내가 그 녀석을, 그러니까, 그러니까—\""]], [["‘Charity?’ suggested Ralph drily.", "\"자선으로?\" 랄프가 무뚝뚝하게 거들었어."]], [["‘Charity, to be sure,’ returned Squeers, rubbing his knees, ‘and when he begins to be useful in a certain sort of way, this young scoundrel of a Nickleby comes and carries him off. But the most vexatious and aggeravating part of the whole affair is,’ said Squeers, dropping his voice, and drawing his chair still closer to Ralph, ‘that some questions have been asked about him at last--not of me, but, in a roundabout kind of way, of people in our village. So, that just when I might have had all arrears paid up, perhaps, and perhaps--who knows? such things have happened in our business before--a present besides for putting him out to a farmer, or sending him to sea, so that he might never turn up to disgrace his parents, supposing him to be a natural boy, as many of our boys are--damme, if that villain of a Nickleby don’t collar him in open day, and commit as good as highway robbery upon my pocket.’", "\"자선으로, 그렇지.\" 스퀴어스가 제 무릎을 문지르며 대꾸했어. \"그러다 그놈이 어떤 식으로든 좀 쓸모가 생길 만하니까, 그 니클비라는 젊은 악당 놈이 와서 데려가 버린 거요. 근데 이 일에서 제일 약 오르고 분통 터지는 건 말이오,\" 스퀴어스가 목소리를 낮추고 의자를 랄프 쪽으로 더 바짝 끌어당기며 말했어. \"마침내 그 애에 대해 이것저것 캐묻는 일이 생겼다는 거요. 나한테 직접 물은 건 아니고, 빙 둘러서 우리 마을 사람들한테 말이오. 그러니, 어쩌면 밀린 돈을 다 받아 낼 수 있었을 바로 그때, 어쩌면, 누가 알겠소? 우리 업계에선 전에도 이런 일이 있었거든. 그 애를 농부한테 보내거나 배에 태워 보내는 대가로 사례금까지 받을 수 있었을 텐데 말이오. 그 애가 우리 애들 상당수가 그렇듯 사생아라면, 다신 나타나서 제 부모 망신시키지 않게 말이오. 빌어먹을, 그 니클비라는 악당 놈이 대낮에 그 애 멱살을 잡아채서, 내 주머니에 노상강도질이나 다름없는 짓을 저지른 거요.\""]], [["‘We will both cry quits with him before long,’ said Ralph, laying his hand on the arm of the Yorkshire schoolmaster.", "\"우리 둘 다 머지않아 그놈하고 셈을 다 치르게 될 거요.\" 랄프가 요크셔 교장의 팔에 손을 얹으며 말했어."]], [["‘Quits!’ echoed Squeers.", "\"셈을 치른다!\" 스퀴어스가 따라 외쳤어."], ["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["and I should like to leave a small balance in his favour, to be settled when he can.", "나야 그놈 앞으로 작은 빚을 좀 남겨 두고 싶소만. 그놈이 갚을 수 있을 때 받게 말이오."], ["I only wish Mrs. Squeers could catch hold of him.", "그저 우리 스퀴어스 부인이 그놈을 붙잡았으면 좋겠소."], ["Bless her heart!", "아이고, 그 사람!"], ["She’d murder him, Mr.", "그놈을 죽여 버릴 거요, 니클비 씨."], ["Nickleby--she would, as soon as eat her dinner.’", "그러고말고, 제 저녁밥 먹듯 거뜬히 말이오.\""]], [["‘We will talk of this again,’ said Ralph.", "\"이 얘긴 다음에 다시 합시다.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘I must have time to think of it.", "\"생각할 시간이 좀 필요하니까."], ["To wound him through his own affections and fancies--.", "그놈을 그놈 자신의 정과 환상을 통해 찌르자면—."], ["If I could strike him through this boy--’", "만약 내가 이 애를 통해 그놈을 칠 수만 있다면—\""]], [["‘Strike him how you like, sir,’ interrupted Squeers, ‘only hit him hard enough, that’s all--and with that, I’ll say good-morning. Here!--just chuck that little boy’s hat off that corner peg, and lift him off the stool will you?’", "\"맘대로 치쇼, 선생.\" 스퀴어스가 끼어들었어. \"그저 충분히 세게만 치쇼, 그게 다요. 그럼 이만 작별 인사 하겠소. 여기!—저기 구석 못에 걸린 저 꼬마 모자 좀 휙 던져 주고, 걸상에서 좀 내려놔 주겠나?\""]], [["Bawling these requests to Newman Noggs, Mr.", "뉴먼 노그스한테 이런 부탁을 큰 소리로 외치며, 스퀴어스 씨는 작은 뒤 사무실로 갔어."], ["Squeers betook himself to the little back-office, and fitted on his child’s hat with parental anxiety, while Newman, with his pen behind his ear, sat, stiff and immovable, on his stool, regarding the father and son by turns with a broad stare.", "그러고는 아비 된 마음으로 정성스레 제 아이한테 모자를 씌워 줬지. 그러는 동안 뉴먼은 펜을 귀에 꽂은 채, 걸상에 뻣뻣하게 꼼짝 않고 앉아서, 그 아비와 아들을 번갈아 빤히 쳐다보고 있었어."]], [["‘He’s a fine boy, an’t he?’ said Squeers, throwing his head a little on one side, and falling back to the desk, the better to estimate the proportions of little Wackford.", "\"잘생긴 녀석이지, 안 그렇소?\" 스퀴어스가 고개를 살짝 한쪽으로 젖히고, 꼬마 왝퍼드의 균형을 더 잘 가늠하려고 책상 쪽으로 물러서며 말했어."]], [["‘Very,’ said Newman.", "\"아주요.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."]], [["‘Pretty well swelled out, an’t he?’ pursued Squeers.", "\"제법 살이 통통하게 올랐지, 안 그렇소?\" 스퀴어스가 이어 말했어."], ["‘He has the fatness of twenty boys, he has.’", "\"이 녀석은 애 스무 명 살이 다 붙었다오.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ replied Newman, suddenly thrusting his face into that of Squeers, ‘he has;--the fatness of twenty!--more!", "\"아!\" 뉴먼이 갑자기 제 얼굴을 스퀴어스 얼굴에 들이밀며 대꾸했어."], ["He’s got it all.", "\"그렇구먼. 스무 명 살이!"], ["God help that others.", "더 되겠어! 다 이 녀석이 가져갔구먼."], ["Ha!", "다른 애들은 하느님이 도우셔야지."], ["ha!", "하!"], ["Oh Lord!’", "하! 아이고 맙소사!\""]], [["Having uttered these fragmentary observations, Newman dropped upon his desk and began to write with most marvellous rapidity.", "이렇게 두서없는 말을 내뱉고는, 뉴먼이 책상에 엎드려 놀랄 만큼 빠른 속도로 글을 쓰기 시작했어."]], [["‘Why, what does the man mean?’ cried Squeers, colouring.", "\"아니, 이 사람 무슨 소릴 하는 거요?\" 스퀴어스가 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤어."], ["‘Is he drunk?’", "\"취했나?\""]], [["Newman made no reply.", "뉴먼은 아무 대답도 안 했어."]], [["‘Is he mad?’ said Squeers.", "\"미쳤나?\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."]], [["But, still Newman betrayed no consciousness of any presence save his own; so, Mr.", "하지만 뉴먼은 여전히 제 자신 말고 누가 있다는 걸 전혀 알아채는 기색이 없었어."], ["Squeers comforted himself by saying that he was both drunk AND mad; and, with this parting observation, he led his hopeful son away.", "그래서 스퀴어스 씨는 이 사람이 취한 데다 미치기까지 했다고 혼잣말하며 스스로를 달랬지. 그러고는 이 작별의 한마디를 남기고, 그 장래가 촉망되는 아들을 데리고 나갔어."]], [["In exact proportion as Ralph Nickleby became conscious of a struggling and lingering regard for Kate, had his detestation of Nicholas augmented.", "케이트를 향한 마음이 안간힘을 쓰며 잔잔히 남아 있다는 걸 랄프 니클비가 의식하게 된 만큼, 꼭 그만큼 니콜라스를 향한 미움도 커졌어."], ["It might be, that to atone for the weakness of inclining to any one person, he held it necessary to hate some other more intensely than before; but such had been the course of his feelings.", "한 사람한테 마음이 기우는 그 약함을 만회하려고, 다른 누군가를 전보다 더 지독하게 미워해야 한다고 여긴 건지도 몰라. 어쨌든 그의 감정은 그렇게 흘러왔지."], ["And now, to be defied and spurned, to be held up to her in the worst and most repulsive colours, to know that she was taught to hate and despise him: to feel that there was infection in his touch, and taint in his companionship--to know all this, and to know that the mover of it all was that same boyish poor relation who had twitted him in their very first interview, and openly bearded and braved him since, wrought his quiet and stealthy malignity to such a pitch, that there was scarcely anything he would not have hazarded to gratify it, if he could have seen his way to some immediate retaliation.", "그런데 이제, 도전받고 멸시당하고, 케이트 앞에서 가장 추악하고 역겨운 빛깔로 그려지고, 그녀가 자기를 미워하고 경멸하도록 배웠다는 걸 알게 되니, 자기 손길엔 병균이 있고 자기와 함께 있는 것만으로도 오염된다고 케이트가 느낀다는 걸 알게 되니, 이 모든 걸 알게 되고, 게다가 이 모든 일을 꾸민 장본인이 바로 그 애송이 가난뱅이 친척이라는 걸, 처음 만난 자리에서부터 자기를 비웃고, 그 뒤로도 드러내 놓고 자기한테 맞서고 대든 바로 그놈이라는 걸 알게 되니, 그의 조용하고 은밀한 악의는 어떤 지경까지 치달았어. 당장 보복할 길만 보였다면, 그걸 채우려고 무슨 위험이든 무릅쓰지 않았을 게 거의 없을 정도였지."]], [["But, fortunately for Nicholas, Ralph Nickleby did not; and although he cast about all that day, and kept a corner of his brain working on the one anxious subject through all the round of schemes and business that came with it, night found him at last, still harping on the same theme, and still pursuing the same unprofitable reflections.", "하지만 니콜라스한테는 다행스럽게도, 랄프 니클비는 그 길을 못 봤어. 그날 하루 종일 궁리하고, 닥쳐오는 온갖 계략과 일거리 와중에도 머리 한구석을 그 한 가지 고민거리에 쉬지 않고 굴렸는데도, 밤이 되어서야 결국 그는 여전히 같은 주제를 곱씹으며, 여전히 그 부질없는 생각만 좇고 있었어."]], [["‘When my brother was such as he,’ said Ralph, ‘the first comparisons were drawn between us--always in my disfavour. HE was open, liberal, gallant, gay; I a crafty hunks of cold and stagnant blood, with no passion but love of saving, and no spirit beyond a thirst for gain. I recollected it well when I first saw this whipster; but I remember it better now.’", "\"내 동생이 저놈만 할 때,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"우리 둘을 처음으로 견주기 시작했지. 늘 나한테 불리하게 말이야. 그 애는 트인 데다 너그럽고, 씩씩하고 쾌활했어. 나는 차갑고 고인 피가 흐르는 교활한 구두쇠였고, 아끼는 것 말곤 정열이라곤 없고, 돈 욕심 말곤 패기도 없었지. 처음 이 애송이를 봤을 때 그 일이 똑똑히 떠올랐는데, 지금은 더더욱 또렷이 떠오르는군.\""]], [["He had been occupied in tearing Nicholas’s letter into atoms; and as he spoke, he scattered it in a tiny shower about him.", "그는 니콜라스의 편지를 갈기갈기 찢고 있었어. 그러고는 말하면서, 그걸 자기 둘레에 자잘한 종잇조각 비처럼 흩뿌렸지."]], [["‘Recollections like these,’ pursued Ralph, with a bitter smile, ‘flock upon me--when I resign myself to them--in crowds, and from countless quarters.", "\"이런 기억들이,\" 랄프가 쓴웃음을 지으며 말을 이었어."], ["As a portion of the world affect to despise the power of money, I must try and show them what it is.’", "\"내가 거기에 나를 내맡길 때면, 떼 지어, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 데서 몰려들어. 세상 사람들 일부가 돈의 힘을 깔보는 척하니, 내가 그게 어떤 건지 보여 줘야겠어.\""]], [["And being, by this time, in a pleasant frame of mind for slumber, Ralph Nickleby went to bed.", "그리고 이때쯤엔 잠들기 좋은 흐뭇한 기분이 되어서, 랄프 니클비는 잠자리에 들었어."]], [["# CHAPTER 35", "# CHAPTER 35"]], [["Smike becomes known to Mrs. Nickleby and Kate.", "어머니와 누이를 그 마음씨 고운 세밀화가의 집에 자리 잡게 하고, 멀버리 호크 경이 목숨을 잃을 위험은 없다는 걸 확인하고 나서, 니콜라스는 가엾은 스마이크에게 생각을 돌렸어."], ["Nicholas also meets with new Acquaintances.", "스마이크는 뉴먼 노그스와 아침을 먹고 나서도 그 착한 사람의 하숙집에 풀이 죽은 채 머물러 있었어."], ["Brighter Days seem to dawn upon the Family\nHaving established his mother and sister in the apartments of the kind-hearted miniature painter, and ascertained that Sir Mulberry Hawk was in no danger of losing his life, Nicholas turned his thoughts to poor Smike, who, after breakfasting with Newman Noggs, had remained, in a disconsolate state, at that worthy creature’s lodgings, waiting, with much anxiety, for further intelligence of his protector.", "자기를 지켜 주는 사람의 소식을 더 듣고 싶어서 안절부절못하며 기다리고 있었지."]], [["‘As he will be one of our own little household, wherever we live, or whatever fortune is in reserve for us,’ thought Nicholas, ‘I must present the poor fellow in due form. They will be kind to him for his own sake, and if not (on that account solely) to the full extent I could wish, they will stretch a point, I am sure, for mine.’", "'우리가 어디서 살든, 앞으로 어떤 운명이 기다리든, 저 친구는 우리 작은 식구 가운데 하나가 될 거야.' 니콜라스는 생각했어. '그러니 저 가엾은 친구를 제대로 소개해 줘야지. 식구들은 그 자체로 저 친구한테 잘해 줄 거야. 그게 안 되면, 그 이유만으로 내가 바라는 만큼 다 못 해 준다 해도, 나를 봐서라도 한 발짝 양보해 줄 거야. 틀림없어.'"]], [["Nicholas said ‘they’, but his misgivings were confined to one person.", "니콜라스는 '식구들'이라고 말했지만, 사실 마음에 걸리는 사람은 한 명뿐이었어. 케이트는 확신했지."], ["He was sure of Kate, but he knew his mother’s peculiarities, and was not quite so certain that Smike would find favour in the eyes of Mrs Nickleby.", "하지만 어머니의 별난 성격을 알고 있었거든. 그래서 스마이크가 니클비 부인의 눈에 들지 어떨지는 그리 자신할 수가 없었어."]], [["‘However,’ thought Nicholas as he departed on his benevolent errand; ‘she cannot fail to become attached to him, when she knows what a devoted creature he is, and as she must quickly make the discovery, his probation will be a short one.’", "'그래도,' 자비로운 심부름을 떠나면서 니콜라스는 생각했어. '저 친구가 얼마나 헌신적인지 알면 어머니도 정이 안 들 리가 없지. 그리고 그걸 금방 알아채실 테니까, 시험 기간은 짧을 거야.'"]], [["‘I was afraid,’ said Smike, overjoyed to see his friend again, ‘that you had fallen into some fresh trouble; the time seemed so long, at last, that I almost feared you were lost.’", "\"무슨 새로운 곤경에 빠지신 줄 알았어요.\" 친구를 다시 보고 너무 기뻐서 스마이크가 말했어. \"시간이 어찌나 길게 느껴지던지, 나중엔 형이 영영 못 돌아오는 게 아닌가 겁이 났어요.\""]], [["‘Lost!’ replied Nicholas gaily.", "\"못 돌아온다고!\" 니콜라스가 명랑하게 대꾸했어."], ["‘You will not be rid of me so easily, I promise you.", "\"날 그렇게 쉽게 떼어 낼 순 없을걸, 약속하지."], ["I shall rise to the surface many thousand times yet, and the harder the thrust that pushes me down, the more quickly I shall rebound, Smike.", "난 앞으로도 수천 번은 더 물 위로 떠오를 거야. 그리고 날 밀어 내리는 손길이 거셀수록, 난 그만큼 더 빨리 튀어 오를 거라고, 스마이크."], ["But come; my errand here is to take you home.’", "자, 이리 와. 내가 여기 온 건 널 집으로 데려가려는 거야.\""]], [["‘Home!’ faltered Smike, drawing timidly back.", "\"집이요!\" 스마이크는 머뭇거리며 슬며시 뒤로 물러섰어."]], [["‘Ay,’ rejoined Nicholas, taking his arm.", "\"그래.\" 니콜라스가 그의 팔을 잡으며 대꾸했어."], ["‘Why not?’", "\"안 될 게 뭐 있어?\""]], [["‘I had such hopes once,’ said Smike; ‘day and night, day and night, for many years. I longed for home till I was weary, and pined away with grief, but now--’", "\"한때는 그런 꿈도 꿨어요.\" 스마이크가 말했어. \"낮이고 밤이고, 낮이고 밤이고, 여러 해 동안요. 지칠 때까지 집을 그리워했고, 슬픔에 사무쳐 야위어 갔죠. 하지만 이제는……\""]], [["‘And what now?’ asked Nicholas, looking kindly in his face.", "\"이제는 어떻다는 거야?\" 니콜라스가 그의 얼굴을 다정하게 들여다보며 물었어."], ["‘What now, old friend?’", "\"이제 어떻다고, 친구?\""]], [["‘I could not part from you to go to any home on earth,’ replied Smike, pressing his hand; ‘except one, except one. I shall never be an old man; and if your hand placed me in the grave, and I could think, before I died, that you would come and look upon it sometimes with one of your kind smiles, and in the summer weather, when everything was alive--not dead like me--I could go to that home almost without a tear.’", "\"이제는 형하고 떨어져서는 이 세상 어떤 집에도 갈 수 없어요.\" 스마이크가 그의 손을 꼭 쥐며 대답했어. \"딱 한 곳만 빼고요, 딱 한 곳만요. 난 결코 노인이 되진 못할 거예요. 그리고 만약에 형 손으로 날 무덤에 묻어 준다면, 그리고 죽기 전에, 형이 가끔 그 무덤을 찾아와 그 다정한 미소 가운데 하나로 내려다봐 줄 거라고, 여름날, 모든 게 살아 숨 쉴 때 — 나처럼 죽은 게 아니라요 — 그렇게 생각할 수 있다면, 나는 거의 눈물 한 방울 없이 그 집으로 갈 수 있을 거예요.\""]], [["‘Why do you talk thus, poor boy, if your life is a happy one with me?’ said Nicholas.", "\"왜 그런 말을 하는 거야, 가엾은 녀석아, 나하고 사는 게 행복하다면서?\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Because I should change; not those about me.", "\"내가 변할 테니까요. 내 곁에 있는 사람들이 아니라요."], ["And if they forgot me, I should never know it,’ replied Smike.", "그리고 그 사람들이 날 잊는다 해도, 난 결코 알지 못할 테니까요.\" 스마이크가 대답했어."], ["‘In the churchyard we are all alike, but here there are none like me.", "\"교회 묘지에서는 우리 모두 똑같아요. 하지만 여기엔 나 같은 사람이 없어요."], ["I am a poor creature, but I know that.’", "난 보잘것없는 녀석이에요. 하지만 그건 나도 알아요.\""]], [["‘You are a foolish, silly creature,’ said Nicholas cheerfully.", "\"넌 어리석고 바보 같은 녀석이야.\" 니콜라스가 쾌활하게 말했어."], ["‘If that is what you mean, I grant you that.", "\"네 말 뜻이 그거라면, 그건 인정해 주지."], ["Why, here’s a dismal face for ladies’ company!--my pretty sister too, whom you have so often asked me about.", "아니, 숙녀들과 함께할 자리에 이런 우울한 얼굴이라니! 게다가 네가 그렇게 자주 나한테 물어보던 내 예쁜 누이도 있는데 말이야."], ["Is this your Yorkshire gallantry?", "이게 네 요크셔식 신사도냐?"], ["For shame!", "부끄러운 줄 알아!"], ["for shame!’", "부끄러운 줄!\""]], [["Smike brightened up and smiled.", "스마이크는 얼굴이 환해지더니 미소를 지었어."]], [["‘When I talk of home,’ pursued Nicholas, ‘I talk of mine--which is yours of course. If it were defined by any particular four walls and a roof, God knows I should be sufficiently puzzled to say whereabouts it lay; but that is not what I mean. When I speak of home, I speak of the place where--in default of a better--those I love are gathered together; and if that place were a gypsy’s tent, or a barn, I should call it by the same good name notwithstanding. And now, for what is my present home, which, however alarming your expectations may be, will neither terrify you by its extent nor its magnificence!’", "\"내가 집 이야기를 할 때는,\" 니콜라스가 말을 이었어. \"내 집을 말하는 거야. 그러니까 당연히 네 집이기도 하지. 그게 특정한 벽 네 개와 지붕 하나로 정해진 거라면, 솔직히 그게 어디쯤 있는지 말하기가 꽤 난감할 거야. 하지만 내가 말하는 건 그런 게 아니야. 내가 집이라고 할 때는, 더 나은 데가 없으니, 내가 사랑하는 사람들이 한데 모인 곳을 말하는 거야. 그리고 그곳이 집시의 천막이든 헛간이든, 난 변함없이 그 좋은 이름으로 부를 거야. 자, 그럼 지금 내 집이 어떤 곳이냐 하면 — 네 기대가 아무리 거창하다 해도, 그 넓이로 널 놀라게 하지도, 그 웅장함으로 널 겁먹게 하지도 않을 거야!\""]], [["So saying, Nicholas took his companion by the arm, and saying a great deal more to the same purpose, and pointing out various things to amuse and interest him as they went along, led the way to Miss La Creevy’s house.", "이렇게 말하면서 니콜라스는 친구의 팔을 잡았어. 그리고 같은 뜻으로 훨씬 더 많은 이야기를 들려주며, 가는 길에 그의 흥미를 끌고 즐겁게 해 줄 이런저런 것들을 가리켜 보이면서, 미스 라 크리비의 집으로 앞장서 갔어."]], [["‘And this, Kate,’ said Nicholas, entering the room where his sister sat alone, ‘is the faithful friend and affectionate fellow-traveller whom I prepared you to receive.’", "\"케이트, 이 사람이,\" 누이가 혼자 앉아 있는 방으로 들어서며 니콜라스가 말했어. \"내가 너한테 맞아 달라고 미리 말해 둔, 그 진실한 친구이자 다정한 길동무야.\""]], [["Poor Smike was bashful, and awkward, and frightened enough, at first, but Kate advanced towards him so kindly, and said, in such a sweet voice, how anxious she had been to see him after all her brother had told her, and how much she had to thank him for having comforted Nicholas so greatly in their very trying reverses, that he began to be very doubtful whether he should shed tears or not, and became still more flurried. However, he managed to say, in a broken voice, that Nicholas was his only friend, and that he would lay down his life to help him; and Kate, although she was so kind and considerate, seemed to be so wholly unconscious of his distress and embarrassment, that he recovered almost immediately and felt quite at home.", "가엾은 스마이크는 처음엔 수줍고 어색하고 잔뜩 겁을 먹었어. 하지만 케이트가 어찌나 다정하게 다가와, 어찌나 고운 목소리로, 오빠한테 그 이야기를 다 듣고 나서 얼마나 만나고 싶었는지, 그토록 힘든 역경 속에서 오빠를 그렇게 크게 위로해 준 게 얼마나 고마운지를 말해 주는지, 그는 눈물을 흘려야 할지 말지 영 갈피를 못 잡고 더욱 허둥대고 말았어. 그래도 그는 떨리는 목소리로, 니콜라스가 자기의 유일한 친구이고, 그를 돕기 위해서라면 목숨이라도 내놓겠다고 간신히 말했어. 그리고 케이트는 그토록 친절하고 사려 깊으면서도 그의 괴로움과 당혹감을 전혀 눈치채지 못하는 듯 보였어. 그래서 그는 거의 곧바로 마음을 가다듬고 아주 편안해졌지."]], [["Then, Miss La Creevy came in; and to her Smike had to be presented also.", "그때 미스 라 크리비가 들어왔어. 그래서 스마이크는 그녀에게도 소개되어야 했지."], ["And Miss La Creevy was very kind too, and wonderfully talkative: not to Smike, for that would have made him uneasy at first, but to Nicholas and his sister.", "미스 라 크리비도 무척 친절했고, 놀랄 만큼 말이 많았어. 스마이크한테가 아니라 — 그러면 처음엔 그가 불편해질 테니까 — 니콜라스와 그의 누이한테 말이지."], ["Then, after a time, she would speak to Smike himself now and then, asking him whether he was a judge of likenesses, and whether he thought that picture in the corner was like herself, and whether he didn’t think it would have looked better if she had made herself ten years younger, and whether he didn’t think, as a matter of general observation, that young ladies looked better not only in pictures, but out of them too, than old ones; with many more small jokes and facetious remarks, which were delivered with such good-humour and merriment, that Smike thought, within himself, she was the nicest lady he had ever seen; even nicer than Mrs.", "그러다가 얼마쯤 지나서는 이따금 스마이크한테도 직접 말을 걸었어. 그림 보는 안목이 있느냐고, 저 구석에 있는 그림이 자기랑 닮은 것 같으냐고, 자기를 열 살쯤 젊게 그렸으면 더 나았을 것 같지 않으냐고, 또 일반적으로 봐서 젊은 아가씨들이 그림 속에서뿐 아니라 그림 밖에서도 나이 든 사람들보다 더 예뻐 보이지 않느냐고 물었어."], ["Grudden, of Mr.", "그 밖에도 자잘한 농담과 익살스러운 말을 많이 늘어놓았는데, 어찌나 유쾌하고 명랑하게 풀어내는지, 스마이크는 속으로 그녀가 여태껏 본 가장 멋진 숙녀라고 생각했어."], ["Vincent Crummles’s theatre; and she was a nice lady too, and talked, perhaps more, but certainly louder, than Miss La Creevy.", "빈센트 크럼블스 극단의 그러든 부인보다도 더 멋지다고 말이야. 그 부인도 멋진 숙녀였고, 어쩌면 말은 더 많이 했지만, 확실히 미스 라 크리비보다 목소리가 더 컸거든."]], [["At length the door opened again, and a lady in mourning came in; and Nicholas kissing the lady in mourning affectionately, and calling her his mother, led her towards the chair from which Smike had risen when she entered the room.", "마침내 문이 다시 열리더니, 상복을 입은 부인이 들어왔어. 그리고 니콜라스는 그 상복 입은 부인에게 다정하게 입을 맞추고 어머니라고 부르며, 부인이 방에 들어올 때 스마이크가 일어선 그 의자 쪽으로 그녀를 데려갔어."]], [["‘You are always kind-hearted, and anxious to help the oppressed, my dear mother,’ said Nicholas, ‘so you will be favourably disposed towards him, I know.’", "\"어머닌 늘 마음이 따뜻하시고, 짓눌린 사람들을 돕고 싶어 하시잖아요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그러니 이 친구한테도 잘 대해 주실 거예요, 저는 알아요.\""]], [["‘I am sure, my dear Nicholas,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby, looking very hard at her new friend, and bending to him with something more of majesty than the occasion seemed to require: ‘I am sure any friend of yours has, as indeed he naturally ought to have, and must have, of course, you know, a great claim upon me, and of course, it is a very great pleasure to me to be introduced to anybody you take an interest in. There can be no doubt about that; none at all; not the least in the world,’ said Mrs Nickleby. ‘At the same time I must say, Nicholas, my dear, as I used to say to your poor dear papa, when he WOULD bring gentlemen home to dinner, and there was nothing in the house, that if he had come the day before yesterday--no, I don’t mean the day before yesterday now; I should have said, perhaps, the year before last--we should have been better able to entertain him.’", "\"그럼, 그렇고말고, 우리 니콜라스.\" 니클비 부인은 새 친구를 아주 빤히 쳐다보며 대답했어. 그러고는 그 자리에 어울리는 것보다 좀 더 위엄을 담아 그에게 고개를 숙였지. \"네 친구라면 누구든, 당연히 그래야 마땅하고, 또 그래야만 하는 거지만, 나한테 큰 권리를 가지는 게 분명하지. 그리고 당연히, 네가 관심을 두는 사람을 소개받는 건 나한테는 아주 큰 기쁨이고. 그건 의심할 여지가 없어. 전혀 없지. 손톱만큼도 없어.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"그래도 한 가지 말해 둬야겠다, 니콜라스, 얘야. 네 가엾은 아버지한테 늘 하던 말인데, 그이가 집에 손님을 데려와 저녁을 먹이겠다는데 집에 아무것도 없을 때면 말이야, 그이가 그저께 왔더라면 — 아니, 지금 그저께를 말하는 게 아니라, 아마 재작년이라고 해야겠지 — 손님을 더 잘 대접할 수 있었을 거라고 했지.\""]], [["With which remarks, Mrs. Nickleby turned to her daughter, and inquired, in an audible whisper, whether the gentleman was going to stop all night.", "이렇게 한마디 하고서 니클비 부인은 딸 쪽으로 몸을 돌리더니, 들릴 만한 속삭임으로 저 신사가 밤새 묵을 거냐고 물었어."]], [["‘Because, if he is, Kate, my dear,’ said Mrs.", "\"왜냐하면, 만약에 그렇다면 말이야, 케이트 얘야,\""], ["Nickleby, ‘I don’t see that it’s possible for him to sleep anywhere, and that’s the truth.’", "니클비 부인이 말했어. \"저이가 어디서 잘 수 있을지 도무지 모르겠거든, 정말이야.\""]], [["Kate stepped gracefully forward, and without any show of annoyance or irritation, breathed a few words into her mother’s ear.", "케이트가 우아하게 앞으로 나서더니, 짜증이나 화난 기색은 조금도 없이, 어머니 귀에 몇 마디를 속삭였어."]], [["‘La, Kate, my dear,’ said Mrs.", "\"어머나, 케이트, 얘야,\""], ["Nickleby, shrinking back, ‘how you do tickle one!", "니클비 부인이 흠칫 물러서며 말했어. \"너 사람을 왜 이렇게 간지럽히니!"], ["Of course, I understand THAT, my love, without your telling me; and I said the same to Nicholas, and I AM very much pleased.", "당연히 나도 그쯤은 안다, 얘야, 네가 말 안 해도. 나도 니콜라스한테 똑같이 말했고, 정말 기쁘기도 하단다."], ["You didn’t tell me, Nicholas, my dear,’ added Mrs.", "니콜라스, 얘야, 넌 나한테 말 안 해 줬구나,\""], ["Nickleby, turning round with an air of less reserve than she had before assumed, ‘what your friend’s name is.’", "니클비 부인은 아까보다 좀 덜 점잔 빼는 태도로 돌아서며 덧붙였어. \"네 친구 이름이 뭔지 말이야.\""]], [["‘His name, mother,’ replied Nicholas, ‘is Smike.’", "\"얘 이름은요, 어머니,\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"스마이크예요.\""]], [["The effect of this communication was by no means anticipated; but the name was no sooner pronounced, than Mrs.", "이 말이 가져온 효과는 전혀 예상 밖이었어."], ["Nickleby dropped upon a chair, and burst into a fit of crying.", "그 이름이 입에서 나오기가 무섭게 니클비 부인이 의자에 털썩 주저앉더니, 와락 울음을 터뜨렸거든."]], [["‘What is the matter?’ exclaimed Nicholas, running to support her.", "\"무슨 일이세요?\" 니콜라스가 부인을 부축하러 달려가며 외쳤어."]], [["‘It’s so like Pyke,’ cried Mrs.", "\"파이크하고 어쩜 그렇게 똑같니.\" 니클비 부인이 울먹였어."], ["Nickleby; ‘so exactly like Pyke.", "\"파이크하고 어쩌면 그렇게 빼다 박았는지."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["don’t speak to me--I shall be better presently.’", "나한테 말 걸지 마라 — 곧 괜찮아질 테니까.\""]], [["And after exhibiting every symptom of slow suffocation in all its stages, and drinking about a tea-spoonful of water from a full tumbler, and spilling the remainder, Mrs.", "그러고는 천천히 숨이 막혀 가는 온갖 단계의 증세를 다 보여 주고, 가득 찬 컵에서 찻숟가락 하나쯤 되는 물을 마시고 나머지는 흘린 다음, 니클비 부인은 괜찮아졌어."], ["Nickleby WAS better, and remarked, with a feeble smile, that she was very foolish, she knew.", "그러고는 힘없는 미소를 띠며, 자기가 참 어리석다는 걸 안다고 말했어."]], [["‘It’s a weakness in our family,’ said Mrs.", "\"우리 집안 내력이란다.\""], ["Nickleby, ‘so, of course, I can’t be blamed for it.", "니클비 부인이 말했어. \"그러니 당연히 내 탓은 아니지."], ["Your grandmama, Kate, was exactly the same--precisely.", "케이트야, 네 할머니도 꼭 이러셨어 — 정말로 똑같이."], ["The least excitement, the slightest surprise--she fainted away directly.", "조금만 흥분해도, 아주 살짝만 놀라도 — 곧바로 기절하셨지."], ["I have heard her say, often and often, that when she was a young lady, and before she was married, she was turning a corner into Oxford Street one day, when she ran against her own hairdresser, who, it seems, was escaping from a bear;--the mere suddenness of the encounter made her faint away directly.", "할머니가 자주, 아주 자주 말씀하셨는데, 처녀였을 때, 결혼하기 전에, 어느 날 옥스퍼드 거리로 모퉁이를 돌다가 자기 미용사하고 딱 부딪쳤다는 거야. 그런데 그 미용사가 곰한테서 도망치던 참이었다지 뭐냐 — 그렇게 갑작스럽게 마주친 것만으로도 곧장 기절을 하셨대."], ["Wait, though,’ added Mrs.", "가만, 그런데,\" 니클비 부인이 잠시 멈추고 곰곰이 생각하며 덧붙였어."], ["Nickleby, pausing to consider.", "\"내 말이 맞는지 좀 보자."], ["‘Let me be sure I’m right.", "곰한테서 도망친 게 그 미용사였나, 아니면 미용실에서 도망친 게 곰이었나?"], ["Was it her hairdresser who had escaped from a bear, or was it a bear who had escaped from her hairdresser’s?", "정말이지 지금은 기억이 안 나는구나. 그래도 그 미용사가 아주 잘생긴 남자였다는 건 알지."], ["I declare I can’t remember just now, but the hairdresser was a very handsome man, I know, and quite a gentleman in his manners; so that it has nothing to do with the point of the story.’", "매너도 아주 신사다웠고. 뭐, 그건 이야기의 핵심하고는 상관없는 일이지만.\""]], [["Mrs.", "니클비 부인은 자기도 모르게 회상에 잠기는 기분에 빠져들더니, 그 순간부터 기분이 한결 나아졌어."], ["Nickleby having fallen imperceptibly into one of her retrospective moods, improved in temper from that moment, and glided, by an easy change of the conversation occasionally, into various other anecdotes, no less remarkable for their strict application to the subject in hand.", "그러고는 가끔씩 슬쩍슬쩍 화제를 바꿔 가며, 지금 다루는 주제하고 딱 들어맞는 점에서 조금도 뒤지지 않는 이런저런 다른 일화들 속으로 미끄러져 들어갔지."]], [["‘Mr. Smike is from Yorkshire, Nicholas, my dear?’ said Mrs.", "\"스마이크 씨는 요크셔 출신이라고, 니콜라스, 얘야?\""], ["Nickleby, after dinner, and when she had been silent for some time.", "저녁을 먹고 한동안 말이 없다가 니클비 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘Certainly, mother,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"그럼요, 어머니.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘I see you have not forgotten his melancholy history.’", "\"얘의 가슴 아픈 사연을 안 잊으셨군요.\""]], [["‘O dear no,’ cried Mrs. Nickleby.", "\"오, 그럼 안 잊었지.\""], ["‘Ah! melancholy, indeed.", "니클비 부인이 외쳤어."], ["You don’t happen, Mr.", "\"아! 정말 가슴 아픈 일이지."], ["Smike, ever to have dined with the Grimbles of Grimble Hall, somewhere in the North Riding, do you?’ said the good lady, addressing herself to him.", "스마이크 씨, 혹시 노스 라이딩 어디쯤에 있는 그림블 홀의 그림블 가에서 식사를 한 적이 있나요?\" 착한 부인이 그에게 말을 걸었어."], ["‘A very proud man, Sir Thomas Grimble, with six grown-up and most lovely daughters, and the finest park in the county.’", "\"토머스 그림블 경이라고, 아주 자존심이 센 양반인데, 다 큰 딸이 여섯이나 되고 하나같이 어찌나 사랑스러운지, 그 고을에서 제일 좋은 정원을 가졌지요.\""]], [["‘My dear mother,’ reasoned Nicholas, ‘do you suppose that the unfortunate outcast of a Yorkshire school was likely to receive many cards of invitation from the nobility and gentry in the neighbourhood?’", "\"어머니,\" 니콜라스가 타이르듯 말했어. \"요크셔 학교에서 불행하게 버려졌던 아이가 그 동네 귀족이나 신사들한테서 초대장을 그렇게 많이 받았을 것 같으세요?\""]], [["‘Really, my dear, I don’t know why it should be so very extraordinary,’ said Mrs.", "\"글쎄다, 얘야, 그게 왜 그렇게 별난 일인지 모르겠구나.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby.", "\"나는 학교 다닐 때, 적어도 한 학기에 두 번씩은 톤턴 베일의 호킨스 가에 갔단다."], ["‘I know that when I was at school, I always went at least twice every half-year to the Hawkinses at Taunton Vale, and they are much richer than the Grimbles, and connected with them in marriage; so you see it’s not so very unlikely, after all.’", "그 사람들은 그림블 가보다 훨씬 부자였고, 게다가 그림블 가하고 혼인으로 맺어진 사이였거든. 그러니 봐라, 그렇게 있을 법하지 않은 일도 아니잖니.\""]], [["Having put down Nicholas in this triumphant manner, Mrs. Nickleby was suddenly seized with a forgetfulness of Smike’s real name, and an irresistible tendency to call him Mr.", "이렇게 니콜라스를 보기 좋게 눌러 놓고서, 니클비 부인은 갑자기 스마이크의 진짜 이름을 까먹더니, 그를 슬래먼스 씨라고 부르고 싶은 걸 도무지 참을 수 없어 했어."], ["Slammons; which circumstance she attributed to the remarkable similarity of the two names in point of sound both beginning with an S, and moreover being spelt with an M.", "그 까닭을 부인은 두 이름이 소리에서 무척 비슷한 데 있다고 둘러댔지. 둘 다 에스로 시작하고, 게다가 엠 자가 들어간다는 거였어."], ["But whatever doubt there might be on this point, there was none as to his being a most excellent listener; which circumstance had considerable influence in placing them on the very best terms, and inducing Mrs Nickleby to express the highest opinion of his general deportment and disposition.", "하지만 그 점이야 어떻든, 그가 더없이 훌륭한 청자라는 데는 의심할 여지가 없었어. 그래서 그 점이 둘 사이를 더없이 좋은 관계로 만드는 데 적잖은 영향을 미쳤고, 니클비 부인이 그의 전반적인 행실과 됨됨이에 최고의 평을 내리게 만들었지."]], [["Thus, the little circle remained, on the most amicable and agreeable footing, until the Monday morning, when Nicholas withdrew himself from it for a short time, seriously to reflect upon the state of his affairs, and to determine, if he could, upon some course of life, which would enable him to support those who were so entirely dependent upon his exertions.", "이렇게 작은 식구들은 더없이 화목하고 즐거운 사이로 지냈어. 그러다 월요일 아침이 되자 니콜라스는 잠시 그 자리에서 물러났지. 자기 형편을 진지하게 돌아보고, 할 수만 있다면 어떤 삶의 길을 정하기 위해서였어. 오로지 자기 노력에만 기대 사는 사람들을 부양할 수 있는 길 말이야."]], [["Mr.", "크럼블스 씨가 한두 번 떠오르긴 했어."], ["Crummles occurred to him more than once; but although Kate was acquainted with the whole history of his connection with that gentleman, his mother was not; and he foresaw a thousand fretful objections, on her part, to his seeking a livelihood upon the stage.", "하지만 케이트는 그 사람과 얽힌 사연을 죄다 알고 있어도 어머니는 몰랐거든. 그리고 자기가 무대에서 밥벌이를 한다고 하면 어머니가 천 가지나 되는 짜증 섞인 반대를 할 게 뻔히 보였어."], ["There were graver reasons, too, against his returning to that mode of life.", "그 생활로 돌아가는 데는 더 무거운 이유들도 있었지."], ["Independently of those arising out of its spare and precarious earnings, and his own internal conviction that he could never hope to aspire to any great distinction, even as a provincial actor, how could he carry his sister from town to town, and place to place, and debar her from any other associates than those with whom he would be compelled, almost without distinction, to mingle?", "벌이가 빠듯하고 불안정하다는 것, 그리고 지방 배우로서조차 큰 명성에 오르기를 바랄 수 없다는 자기 속마음에서 비롯된 이유들은 그렇다 치더라도, 어떻게 누이를 이 도시에서 저 도시로, 이곳저곳으로 끌고 다니며, 자기가 거의 가리지 않고 어울릴 수밖에 없는 그런 사람들 말고는 다른 누구와도 못 사귀게 한단 말인가? \"안 되겠어.\""], ["‘It won’t do,’ said Nicholas, shaking his head; ‘I must try something else.’", "니콜라스가 고개를 저으며 말했어. \"다른 걸 찾아봐야지.\""]], [["It was much easier to make this resolution than to carry it into effect.", "이런 결심을 하기는 그걸 실행에 옮기기보다 훨씬 쉬웠어."], ["With no greater experience of the world than he had acquired for himself in his short trials; with a sufficient share of headlong rashness and precipitation (qualities not altogether unnatural at his time of life); with a very slender stock of money, and a still more scanty stock of friends; what could he do?", "짧은 시도들 속에서 스스로 얻은 것 말고는 세상 경험이 없는 데다, 무모하고 경솔한 면도 적잖이 가진(그 나이 때 아주 부자연스러운 자질은 아니지만) 처지에, 돈도 쥐꼬리만 하고 친구는 더더욱 손에 꼽을 만큼밖에 없으니, 무엇을 할 수 있겠어? \"에라!\""], ["‘Egad!’ said Nicholas, ‘I’ll try that Register Office again.’", "니콜라스가 말했어. \"그 직업소개소나 다시 한번 가 봐야지.\""]], [["He smiled at himself as he walked away with a quick step; for, an instant before, he had been internally blaming his own precipitation. He did not laugh himself out of the intention, however, for on he went: picturing to himself, as he approached the place, all kinds of splendid possibilities, and impossibilities too, for that matter, and thinking himself, perhaps with good reason, very fortunate to be endowed with so buoyant and sanguine a temperament.", "빠른 걸음으로 걸어가면서 그는 혼자 피식 웃었어. 바로 조금 전에 자기 경솔함을 속으로 탓했으니까 말이야. 그래도 웃으며 그 생각을 떨쳐 버리진 않았어. 계속 걸어갔거든. 그곳에 다가가면서, 온갖 근사한 가능성을, 그리고 따지고 보면 불가능한 것들까지 머릿속에 그려 보았어. 그리고 자기가 이렇게 들뜨고 낙천적인 기질을 타고난 게 — 어쩌면 그럴 만한 이유가 있어서겠지만 — 무척 운이 좋다고 생각했지."]], [["The office looked just the same as when he had left it last, and, indeed, with one or two exceptions, there seemed to be the very same placards in the window that he had seen before.", "소개소는 지난번에 떠날 때하고 똑같아 보였어. 사실 한두 개만 빼면, 진열창에 붙은 광고지도 전에 봤던 바로 그것들 같았지."], ["There were the same unimpeachable masters and mistresses in want of virtuous servants, and the same virtuous servants in want of unimpeachable masters and mistresses, and the same magnificent estates for the investment of capital, and the same enormous quantities of capital to be invested in estates, and, in short, the same opportunities of all sorts for people who wanted to make their fortunes.", "흠잡을 데 없는 주인 나리와 마님들이 정직한 하인을 구하고 있었고, 그 정직한 하인들은 흠잡을 데 없는 주인 나리와 마님들을 구하고 있었어. 자본을 투자할 으리으리한 영지들도 그대로였고, 영지에 투자할 어마어마한 자본도 그대로였지."], ["And a most extraordinary proof it was of the national prosperity, that people had not been found to avail themselves of such advantages long ago.", "한마디로, 한밑천 잡고 싶어 하는 사람들을 위한 온갖 기회가 그대로 있었던 거야. 그리고 사람들이 진작에 이런 좋은 기회들을 붙잡으려 들지 않았다는 게, 이 나라가 얼마나 번영하고 있는지를 보여 주는 더없이 놀라운 증거였지."]], [["As Nicholas stopped to look in at the window, an old gentleman happened to stop too; and Nicholas, carrying his eye along the window-panes from left to right in search of some capital-text placard which should be applicable to his own case, caught sight of this old gentleman’s figure, and instinctively withdrew his eyes from the window, to observe the same more closely.", "니콜라스가 걸음을 멈추고 진열창을 들여다보는데, 마침 한 노신사도 멈춰 섰어. 니콜라스는 자기 처지에 딱 맞는 큼지막한 글씨의 광고지를 찾느라 진열창 유리를 왼쪽에서 오른쪽으로 죽 훑어 가다가, 이 노신사의 모습을 보게 됐어. 그러고는 저도 모르게 진열창에서 눈을 거두어 그를 좀 더 가까이 살펴봤지."]], [["He was a sturdy old fellow in a broad-skirted blue coat, made pretty large, to fit easily, and with no particular waist; his bulky legs clothed in drab breeches and high gaiters, and his head protected by a low-crowned broad-brimmed white hat, such as a wealthy grazier might wear. He wore his coat buttoned; and his dimpled double chin rested in the folds of a white neckerchief--not one of your stiff-starched apoplectic cravats, but a good, easy, old-fashioned white neckcloth that a man might go to bed in and be none the worse for. But what principally attracted the attention of Nicholas was the old gentleman’s eye,--never was such a clear, twinkling, honest, merry, happy eye, as that. And there he stood, looking a little upward, with one hand thrust into the breast of his coat, and the other playing with his old-fashioned gold watch-chain: his head thrown a little on one side, and his hat a little more on one side than his head, (but that was evidently accident; not his ordinary way of wearing it,) with such a pleasant smile playing about his mouth, and such a comical expression of mingled slyness, simplicity, kind-heartedness, and good-humour, lighting up his jolly old face, that Nicholas would have been content to have stood there and looked at him until evening, and to have forgotten, meanwhile, that there was such a thing as a soured mind or a crabbed countenance to be met with in the whole wide world.", "그는 다부진 노인이었어. 폭이 넓고 자락이 큰 파란 코트를 입었는데, 넉넉하게 만들어져 허리가 따로 잡히지 않은 옷이었지. 굵은 다리에는 누런 반바지와 긴 각반을 둘렀고, 머리에는 부유한 목축업자가 쓸 법한, 챙이 넓고 모자통이 낮은 흰 모자를 쓰고 있었어. 코트는 단추를 채워 입었고, 보조개 패인 이중 턱은 흰 목도리 주름 속에 묻혀 있었지. 빳빳하게 풀 먹인, 졸도라도 할 것 같은 그런 넥타이가 아니라, 그대로 베고 자도 아무렇지 않을 만한, 편안하고 예스러운 흰 목도리였어. 그런데 니콜라스의 눈길을 무엇보다 끈 건 그 노신사의 눈이었어. 그렇게 맑고, 반짝이고, 정직하고, 유쾌하고, 행복해 보이는 눈은 처음이었지. 그는 거기 서서, 한 손은 코트 가슴팍에 찔러 넣고, 다른 손으로는 예스러운 금시계 줄을 만지작거리며, 약간 위쪽을 바라보고 있었어. 고개를 살짝 한쪽으로 기울이고, 모자는 머리보다 좀 더 한쪽으로 기울어져 있었지만(그건 분명 어쩌다 그런 거지, 평소 쓰는 모양은 아니었어), 입가에는 아주 흐뭇한 미소가 어려 있었어. 그리고 교활함과 천진함, 따뜻한 마음과 유쾌함이 한데 어우러진 익살스러운 표정이 그 명랑한 늙은 얼굴을 환히 밝히고 있어서, 니콜라스는 그 자리에 서서 저녁때까지 그를 바라봐도 좋겠다 싶었어. 그러면서 이 넓은 세상에 마음 비뚤어진 사람이나 심술궂은 얼굴 같은 게 있다는 것조차 까맣게 잊어버릴 만큼 말이야."]], [["But, even a very remote approach to this gratification was not to be made, for although he seemed quite unconscious of having been the subject of observation, he looked casually at Nicholas; and the latter, fearful of giving offence, resumed his scrutiny of the window instantly.", "하지만 그런 흐뭇함은 아주 잠깐도 누릴 수가 없었어. 노신사는 자기가 누군가의 구경거리가 됐다는 걸 전혀 모르는 듯하더니, 무심코 니콜라스 쪽을 쳐다봤거든. 그러자 니콜라스는 실례가 될까 봐 얼른 다시 진열창을 살피기 시작했어."]], [["Still, the old gentleman stood there, glancing from placard to placard, and Nicholas could not forbear raising his eyes to his face again.", "그래도 노신사는 거기 서서 광고지를 하나하나 훑어보고 있었어. 니콜라스는 다시 그 얼굴로 눈길을 들지 않을 수가 없었지."], ["Grafted upon the quaintness and oddity of his appearance, was something so indescribably engaging, and bespeaking so much worth, and there were so many little lights hovering about the corners of his mouth and eyes, that it was not a mere amusement, but a positive pleasure and delight to look at him.", "예스럽고 별난 그 차림새 위에는 뭐라 말할 수 없이 사람을 끄는 무언가가 새겨져 있었고, 어찌나 됨됨이가 훌륭해 보이는지, 게다가 입가와 눈가에는 자잘한 빛들이 어찌나 많이 떠도는지, 그를 보는 건 그저 재미있는 정도가 아니라 분명한 즐거움이자 기쁨이었어."]], [["This being the case, it is no wonder that the old man caught Nicholas in the fact, more than once.", "이러니, 그 노인이 니콜라스를 그 짓을 하던 현장에서 한두 번 들킨 것도 당연한 일이지. 그럴 때마다 니콜라스는 얼굴을 붉히고 당황한 기색을 보였어."], ["At such times, Nicholas coloured and looked embarrassed: for the truth is, that he had begun to wonder whether the stranger could, by any possibility, be looking for a clerk or secretary; and thinking this, he felt as if the old gentleman must know it.", "실은, 혹시 이 낯선 이가 어쩌면 사무원이나 비서를 구하고 있는 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 들기 시작했거든. 그리고 그런 생각을 하니, 노신사도 분명 그걸 알 것만 같았던 거야."]], [["Long as all this takes to tell, it was not more than a couple of minutes in passing.", "이걸 다 이야기하는 데는 오래 걸리지만, 실제로 지나간 시간은 이삼 분도 안 됐어."], ["As the stranger was moving away, Nicholas caught his eye again, and, in the awkwardness of the moment, stammered out an apology.", "낯선 이가 자리를 뜨려 할 때, 니콜라스는 다시 그와 눈이 마주쳤어. 그리고 어색한 그 순간에 더듬더듬 사과의 말을 내뱉었지."]], [["‘No offence.", "\"괜찮네."], ["Oh no offence!’ said the old man.", "아니, 괜찮아!\" 노인이 말했어."]], [["This was said in such a hearty tone, and the voice was so exactly what it should have been from such a speaker, and there was such a cordiality in the manner, that Nicholas was emboldened to speak again.", "이 말이 어찌나 정겨운 어조로 나오는지, 그 목소리가 그런 사람한테서 나올 법한 바로 그 목소리였고, 그 태도에 어찌나 다정함이 묻어나는지, 니콜라스는 용기를 내어 다시 말을 걸었어."]], [["‘A great many opportunities here, sir,’ he said, half smiling as he motioned towards the window.", "\"여기엔 기회가 참 많네요, 어르신.\" 그가 진열창 쪽으로 손짓하며 반쯤 웃으며 말했어."]], [["‘A great many people willing and anxious to be employed have seriously thought so very often, I dare say,’ replied the old man.", "\"일자리를 구하고 싶어 안달이 난 사람들 가운데 그렇게 진지하게 생각한 이가 아주 많았겠지, 틀림없이.\" 노인이 대답했어."], ["‘Poor fellows, poor fellows!’", "\"가엾은 사람들, 가엾은 사람들!\""]], [["He moved away as he said this; but seeing that Nicholas was about to speak, good-naturedly slackened his pace, as if he were unwilling to cut him short.", "이렇게 말하며 그는 자리를 떴어. 하지만 니콜라스가 막 말을 하려는 걸 보고는, 그의 말허리를 자르기 싫다는 듯 너그럽게 걸음을 늦췄지."], ["After a little of that hesitation which may be sometimes observed between two people in the street who have exchanged a nod, and are both uncertain whether they shall turn back and speak, or not, Nicholas found himself at the old man’s side.", "거리에서 목례를 주고받은 두 사람이 돌아서서 말을 걸까 말까 둘 다 망설일 때 흔히 보이는, 그런 머뭇거림이 잠시 흐른 뒤에, 니콜라스는 어느새 노인 곁에 가 있었어."]], [["‘You were about to speak, young gentleman; what were you going to say?’", "\"뭔가 말하려던 참이었지, 젊은이. 무슨 말을 하려던 건가?\""]], [["‘Merely that I almost hoped--I mean to say, thought--you had some object in consulting those advertisements,’ said Nicholas.", "\"그저, 저는 어쩌면 바랐어요 — 아니, 그러니까, 생각했어요 — 어르신이 저 광고들을 보시는 데 무슨 목적이 있으신가 하고요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Ay, ay?", "\"허, 그래?"], ["what object now--what object?’ returned the old man, looking slyly at Nicholas.", "무슨 목적? 무슨 목적이라는 건가?\""], ["‘Did you think I wanted a situation now--eh?", "노인이 니콜라스를 곁눈질하며 대꾸했어. \"이 사람이 지금 일자리를 구하는 줄 알았나, 응?"], ["Did you think I did?’", "그렇게 생각한 게야?\""]], [["Nicholas shook his head.", "니콜라스가 고개를 저었어."]], [["‘Ha!", "\"하!"], ["ha!’ laughed the old gentleman, rubbing his hands and wrists as if he were washing them.", "하!\" 노신사는 마치 손을 씻기라도 하는 것처럼 두 손과 손목을 비비며 웃었어."], ["‘A very natural thought, at all events, after seeing me gazing at those bills.", "\"내가 저 광고들을 들여다보고 있는 걸 봤으니, 어쨌든 아주 자연스러운 생각이지."], ["I thought the same of you, at first; upon my word I did.’", "나도 처음엔 자네를 그렇게 생각했다네. 정말이지 그랬어.\""]], [["‘If you had thought so at last, too, sir, you would not have been far from the truth,’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"끝까지 그렇게 생각하셨더라도, 어르신, 진실에서 그리 멀진 않았을 거예요.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Eh?’ cried the old man, surveying him from head to foot.", "\"응?\" 노인이 그를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 훑어보며 외쳤어."], ["‘What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["Dear me!", "이런, 세상에!"], ["No, no.", "아니, 아니."], ["Well-behaved young gentleman reduced to such a necessity!", "행실 바른 젊은이가 그런 처지에 내몰리다니! 아니지, 아니야."], ["No no, no no.’", "아니고말고.\""]], [["Nicholas bowed, and bidding him good-morning, turned upon his heel.", "니콜라스는 고개 숙여 인사하고, 안녕히 가시라는 말과 함께 발길을 돌렸어."]], [["‘Stay,’ said the old man, beckoning him into a bye street, where they could converse with less interruption.", "\"잠깐.\" 노인이 그를 옆 골목으로 불러들이며 말했어."], ["‘What d’ye mean, eh?’", "거기라면 방해를 덜 받고 이야기할 수 있었거든. \"그게 무슨 뜻인가, 응?\""]], [["‘Merely that your kind face and manner--both so unlike any I have ever seen--tempted me into an avowal, which, to any other stranger in this wilderness of London, I should not have dreamt of making,’ returned Nicholas.", "\"그저, 어르신의 인자한 얼굴과 태도가 — 둘 다 제가 여태껏 본 누구하고도 너무 달라서요 — 저도 모르게 털어놓게 만든 거예요. 이 런던이라는 황무지에서 다른 낯선 사람한테는 꿈에도 하지 않았을 말을요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Wilderness!", "\"황무지!"], ["Yes, it is, it is.", "그래, 그렇지, 그래."], ["Good!", "옳아!"], ["It IS a wilderness,’ said the old man with much animation.", "여긴 황무지야.\" 노인이 무척 활기차게 말했어."], ["‘It was a wilderness to me once.", "\"나한테도 한때는 황무지였지."], ["I came here barefoot.", "난 맨발로 여기 왔다네."], ["I have never forgotten it.", "한 번도 잊은 적이 없어."], ["Thank God!’ and he raised his hat from his head, and looked very grave.", "고맙게도 말이야!\" 그러고는 머리에서 모자를 벗어 들고, 아주 엄숙한 표정을 지었어."]], [["‘What’s the matter?", "\"무슨 일인가?"], ["What is it?", "어찌 된 일이야?"], ["How did it all come about?’ said the old man, laying his hand on the shoulder of Nicholas, and walking him up the street.", "다 어쩌다 그렇게 됐나?\" 노인이 니콜라스의 어깨에 손을 얹고, 그를 데리고 거리를 걸어 올라가며 말했어."], ["‘You’re--Eh?’ laying his finger on the sleeve of his black coat.", "\"자네 — 응?\" 그의 검은 코트 소맷자락에 손가락을 갖다 대며."], ["‘Who’s it for, eh?’", "\"누구 때문인가, 응?\""]], [["‘My father,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"제 아버지요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Ah!’ said the old gentleman quickly.", "\"아!\" 노신사가 얼른 말했어."], ["‘Bad thing for a young man to lose his father.", "\"젊은이가 아버지를 잃는 건 안 좋은 일이지."], ["Widowed mother, perhaps?’", "어머니는 혼자 되셨고, 그렇지?\""]], [["Nicholas sighed.", "니콜라스가 한숨을 쉬었어."]], [["‘Brothers and sisters too?", "\"형제자매도 있고?"], ["Eh?’", "응?\""]], [["‘One sister,’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"누이가 하나 있어요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Poor thing, poor thing!", "\"가엾어라, 가엾어!"], ["You are a scholar too, I dare say?’ said the old man, looking wistfully into the face of the young one.", "자네는 학식도 있겠지, 틀림없이?\" 노인이 젊은이의 얼굴을 그윽이 들여다보며 말했어."]], [["‘I have been tolerably well educated,’ said Nicholas.", "\"그럭저럭 잘 배운 편이에요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Fine thing,’ said the old gentleman, ‘education a great thing: a very great thing!", "\"좋은 일이지.\" 노신사가 말했어."], ["I never had any.", "\"배움이란 대단한 거야."], ["I admire it the more in others.", "아주 대단한 거지! 난 배운 게 없어서, 남이 가진 걸 더 우러러본다네."], ["A very fine thing.", "아주 좋은 거지."], ["Yes, yes.", "그래, 그래."], ["Tell me more of your history.", "자네 이야기를 더 들려주게."], ["Let me hear it all.", "전부 들어 보세."], ["No impertinent curiosity--no, no, no.’", "주제넘은 호기심은 아니야 — 아니, 아니고말고.\""]], [["There was something so earnest and guileless in the way in which all this was said, and such a complete disregard of all conventional restraints and coldnesses, that Nicholas could not resist it.", "이 모든 말을 하는 그 태도에는 어찌나 진실되고 꾸밈없는 데가 있는지, 또 온갖 형식적인 격식과 쌀쌀함을 어찌나 깡그리 무시하는지, 니콜라스는 도무지 거기에 저항할 수가 없었어."], ["Among men who have any sound and sterling qualities, there is nothing so contagious as pure openness of heart.", "건전하고 참된 자질을 지닌 사람들 사이에서는, 순수하게 열린 마음만큼 전염성 강한 것도 없거든."], ["Nicholas took the infection instantly, and ran over the main points of his little history without reserve: merely suppressing names, and touching as lightly as possible upon his uncle’s treatment of Kate.", "니콜라스는 곧바로 그 마음에 전염됐고, 거리낌 없이 자기 짧은 이력의 요점들을 죽 풀어놓았어. 다만 이름들만 빼고, 삼촌이 케이트한테 한 짓은 되도록 가볍게 짚고 넘어갔지."], ["The old man listened with great attention, and when he had concluded, drew his arm eagerly through his own.", "노인은 아주 골똘히 귀를 기울였어. 그리고 이야기가 끝나자, 그의 팔을 자기 팔에 다정하게 끼웠어."]], [["‘Don’t say another word.", "\"한마디도 더 하지 말게."], ["Not another word’ said he.", "한마디도.\" 그가 말했어."], ["‘Come along with me.", "\"나하고 같이 가세."], ["We mustn’t lose a minute.’", "한순간도 허비해선 안 돼.\""]], [["So saying, the old gentleman dragged him back into Oxford Street, and hailing an omnibus on its way to the city, pushed Nicholas in before him, and followed himself.", "이렇게 말하면서 노신사는 그를 다시 옥스퍼드 거리로 끌고 갔어. 그러고는 시내로 가는 합승마차를 불러 세우더니, 니콜라스를 먼저 밀어 넣고 자기도 뒤따라 탔지."]], [["As he appeared in a most extraordinary condition of restless excitement, and whenever Nicholas offered to speak, immediately interposed with: ‘Don’t say another word, my dear sir, on any account--not another word,’ the young man thought it better to attempt no further interruption.", "그가 도무지 가만있지 못하는 들뜬 상태인 데다, 니콜라스가 말을 하려 들 때마다 \"한마디도 더 하지 말게, 이보게, 무슨 일이 있어도 — 한마디도\" 하고 곧장 가로막는 통에, 젊은이는 더 이상 끼어들지 않는 게 낫겠다고 생각했어."], ["Into the city they journeyed accordingly, without interchanging any conversation; and the farther they went, the more Nicholas wondered what the end of the adventure could possibly be.", "그래서 두 사람은 아무 대화도 나누지 않고 시내로 들어갔지. 그리고 갈수록 니콜라스는 이 모험이 대체 어떻게 끝날지 점점 더 궁금해졌어."]], [["The old gentleman got out, with great alacrity, when they reached the Bank, and once more taking Nicholas by the arm, hurried him along Threadneedle Street, and through some lanes and passages on the right, until they, at length, emerged in a quiet shady little square.", "은행에 다다르자 노신사는 아주 잽싸게 내렸어. 그러고는 다시 한번 니콜라스의 팔을 잡고, 그를 데리고 스레드니들 거리를 따라 서둘러 갔지."], ["Into the oldest and cleanest-looking house of business in the square, he led the way.", "오른쪽으로 몇 갈래 골목과 통로를 지나, 마침내 조용하고 그늘진 작은 광장으로 나왔어. 그는 그 광장에서 가장 오래되고 가장 깨끗해 보이는 상점으로 앞장서 들어갔지."], ["The only inscription on the door-post was ‘Cheeryble, Brothers;’ but from a hasty glance at the directions of some packages which were lying about, Nicholas supposed that the brothers Cheeryble were German merchants.", "문기둥에 적힌 글귀라곤 '치어리블 형제 상회'뿐이었어. 하지만 여기저기 놓인 짐짝들의 행선지를 얼핏 보고, 니콜라스는 치어리블 형제가 독일 상인이리라 짐작했지."]], [["Passing through a warehouse which presented every indication of a thriving business, Mr.", "번창하는 사업의 온갖 흔적이 묻어나는 창고를 지나, 치어리블 씨는(니콜라스는 지나친 창고지기와 짐꾼들이 보인 존경의 태도로 보아 그가 치어리블 씨라고 짐작했어) 그를 칸막이로 막아 놓은 작은 회계실로 데려갔어. 커다란 유리 상자 같은 곳이었지."], ["Cheeryble (for such Nicholas supposed him to be, from the respect which had been shown him by the warehousemen and porters whom they passed) led him into a little partitioned-off counting-house like a large glass case, in which counting-house there sat--as free from dust and blemish as if he had been fixed into the glass case before the top was put on, and had never come out since--a fat, elderly, large-faced clerk, with silver spectacles and a powdered head.", "그 회계실 안에는 — 마치 유리 상자 뚜껑을 덮기 전에 그 안에 박혀서는 그 뒤로 한 번도 나온 적이 없는 양, 먼지 한 점 티 하나 없이 — 얼굴이 큼지막한 뚱뚱한 노년의 사무원이 앉아 있었어. 은테 안경을 끼고 머리에 분을 바른 사람이었지."]], [["‘Is my brother in his room, Tim?’ said Mr.", "\"내 동생은 자기 방에 있나, 팀?\""], ["Cheeryble, with no less kindness of manner than he had shown to Nicholas.", "치어리블 씨가 니콜라스한테 보인 것 못지않은 다정한 태도로 말했어."]], [["‘Yes, he is, sir,’ replied the fat clerk, turning his spectacle-glasses towards his principal, and his eyes towards Nicholas, ‘but Mr.", "\"네, 계십니다, 어르신.\" 뚱뚱한 사무원이 안경알은 주인 쪽으로, 눈은 니콜라스 쪽으로 돌리며 대답했어."], ["Trimmers is with him.’", "\"하지만 트리머스 씨가 같이 계세요.\""]], [["‘Ay!", "\"그래!"], ["And what has he come about, Tim?’ said Mr.", "그 사람은 무슨 일로 왔나, 팀?\""], ["Cheeryble.", "치어리블 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘He is getting up a subscription for the widow and family of a man who was killed in the East India Docks this morning, sir,’ rejoined Tim.", "\"오늘 아침 동인도 부두에서 사고로 죽은 사람의 미망인과 식구들을 위해 모금을 하고 있답니다, 어르신.\" 팀이 대답했어."], ["‘Smashed, sir, by a cask of sugar.’", "\"설탕 통에 깔려 으스러졌다는군요, 어르신.\""]], [["‘He is a good creature,’ said Mr.", "\"착한 사람이야.\""], ["Cheeryble, with great earnestness.", "치어리블 씨가 무척 진지하게 말했어."], ["‘He is a kind soul.", "\"마음씨 고운 사람이지."], ["I am very much obliged to Trimmers.", "트리머스한테 참 고마워."], ["Trimmers is one of the best friends we have.", "트리머스는 우리한테 둘도 없는 친구 가운데 하나야."], ["He makes a thousand cases known to us that we should never discover of ourselves.", "우리 힘으로는 결코 알아내지 못했을 딱한 사정을 천 가지나 알려 주거든."], ["I am VERY much obliged to Trimmers.’ Saying which, Mr.", "트리머스한테 정말 고마워.\""], ["Cheeryble rubbed his hands with infinite delight, and Mr.", "이렇게 말하며 치어리블 씨는 더없이 흐뭇한 표정으로 두 손을 비볐어."], ["Trimmers happening to pass the door that instant, on his way out, shot out after him and caught him by the hand.", "그리고 마침 그때 트리머스 씨가 나가는 길에 문 앞을 지나가자, 그가 쏜살같이 뒤쫓아 나가 그의 손을 붙잡았어."]], [["‘I owe you a thousand thanks, Trimmers, ten thousand thanks.", "\"천 번 만 번 고맙네, 트리머스. 정말 고마워."], ["I take it very friendly of you, very friendly indeed,’ said Mr.", "자네가 참 살갑게 해 줘서, 정말 살갑게 말이야.\""], ["Cheeryble, dragging him into a corner to get out of hearing.", "치어리블 씨가 그를 한구석으로 끌고 가, 남들이 못 듣게 하며 말했어."], ["‘How many children are there, and what has my brother Ned given, Trimmers?’", "\"아이가 몇이나 되나, 그리고 내 동생 네드는 얼마를 냈나, 트리머스?\""]], [["‘There are six children,’ replied the gentleman, ‘and your brother has given us twenty pounds.’", "\"아이는 여섯이고요,\" 그 신사가 대답했어. \"동생분은 우리한테 이십 파운드를 주셨습니다.\""]], [["‘My brother Ned is a good fellow, and you’re a good fellow too, Trimmers,’ said the old man, shaking him by both hands with trembling eagerness.", "\"내 동생 네드는 착한 녀석이야. 그리고 자네도 착한 사람이고, 트리머스.\""], ["‘Put me down for another twenty--or--stop a minute, stop a minute.", "노인이 그의 두 손을 떨릴 만큼 열심히 흔들며 말했어. \"나도 이십 파운드 적어 주게 — 아니 — 잠깐만, 잠깐만."], ["We mustn’t look ostentatious; put me down ten pound, and Tim Linkinwater ten pound.", "너무 티 나게 굴면 안 되지. 나는 십 파운드만 적어 주고, 팀 링킨워터도 십 파운드 적어 주게."], ["A cheque for twenty pound for Mr. Trimmers, Tim.", "팀, 트리머스 씨한테 이십 파운드짜리 수표를 끊어 주게."], ["God bless you, Trimmers--and come and dine with us some day this week; you’ll always find a knife and fork, and we shall be delighted.", "신의 가호가 있기를, 트리머스 — 그리고 이번 주 어느 날 우리하고 저녁 먹으러 오게. 나이프하고 포크는 언제든 마련돼 있을 테니, 우리도 기쁠 걸세."], ["Now, my dear sir--cheque from Mr. Linkinwater, Tim.", "자, 이보게 — 팀, 링킨워터 씨 몫의 수표도."], ["Smashed by a cask of sugar, and six poor children--oh dear, dear, dear!’", "설탕 통에 깔려 으스러지다니, 게다가 가엾은 아이가 여섯이라니 — 오, 저런, 저런, 저런!\""]], [["Talking on in this strain, as fast as he could, to prevent any friendly remonstrances from the collector of the subscription on the large amount of his donation, Mr.", "이런 식으로, 자기가 낸 큰 액수의 기부금을 두고 모금 담당자가 살갑게 만류라도 할까 봐, 치어리블 씨는 되도록 빠르게 말을 늘어놓았어."], ["Cheeryble led Nicholas, equally astonished and affected by what he had seen and heard in this short space, to the half-opened door of another room.", "그러고는 이 짧은 사이에 보고 들은 것에 똑같이 놀라고 감동한 니콜라스를 데리고, 반쯤 열린 또 다른 방문 앞으로 갔지."]], [["‘Brother Ned,’ said Mr.", "\"네드 동생,\""], ["Cheeryble, tapping with his knuckles, and stooping to listen, ‘are you busy, my dear brother, or can you spare time for a word or two with me?’", "치어리블 씨가 주먹으로 똑똑 두드리고 몸을 숙여 귀를 기울이며 말했어. \"바쁘니, 동생아, 아니면 나하고 한두 마디 나눌 시간이 좀 있을까?\""]], [["‘Brother Charles, my dear fellow,’ replied a voice from the inside, so like in its tones to that which had just spoken, that Nicholas started, and almost thought it was the same, ‘don’t ask me such a question, but come in directly.’", "\"찰스 형, 형도 참,\" 안에서 목소리가 대답했는데, 그 어조가 방금 말한 사람하고 어찌나 똑같은지, 니콜라스는 흠칫 놀라며 같은 사람인 줄 알 뻔했어. \"그런 걸 묻지 말고, 어서 들어와요.\""]], [["They went in, without further parley.", "두 사람은 더 묻고 따질 것 없이 안으로 들어갔어."], ["What was the amazement of Nicholas when his conductor advanced, and exchanged a warm greeting with another old gentleman, the very type and model of himself--the same face, the same figure, the same coat, waistcoat, and neckcloth, the same breeches and gaiters--nay, there was the very same white hat hanging against the wall!", "니콜라스를 데려온 사람이 다가가 또 다른 노신사와 따뜻하게 인사를 나누는데, 그가 자기하고 꼭 빼다 박은 본보기 같은 사람이었으니 니콜라스가 얼마나 놀랐겠어. 같은 얼굴, 같은 체격, 같은 코트와 조끼와 목도리, 같은 반바지와 각반 — 아니, 벽에 걸린 흰 모자까지 똑같은 거야!"]], [["As they shook each other by the hand: the face of each lighted up by beaming looks of affection, which would have been most delightful to behold in infants, and which, in men so old, was inexpressibly touching: Nicholas could observe that the last old gentleman was something stouter than his brother; this, and a slight additional shade of clumsiness in his gait and stature, formed the only perceptible difference between them. Nobody could have doubted their being twin brothers.", "두 사람이 서로 손을 맞잡고 흔드는데, 각자의 얼굴이 다정함이 넘치는 환한 표정으로 빛났어. 그런 표정은 갓난아이들한테서 보면 더없이 흐뭇하겠지만, 이렇게 나이 든 사람들한테서 보니 말할 수 없이 뭉클했지. 니콜라스가 보니, 나중에 본 노신사가 형보다 좀 더 통통했어. 이 점하고, 걸음걸이와 체구에 살짝 덧붙은 굼뜬 기운 정도가, 둘 사이에서 알아볼 수 있는 유일한 차이였어. 두 사람이 쌍둥이 형제라는 걸 누구도 의심할 수 없었지."]], [["‘Brother Ned,’ said Nicholas’s friend, closing the room-door, ‘here is a young friend of mine whom we must assist. We must make proper inquiries into his statements, in justice to him as well as to ourselves, and if they are confirmed--as I feel assured they will be--we must assist him, we must assist him, brother Ned.’", "\"네드 동생,\" 니콜라스의 친구가 방문을 닫으며 말했어. \"여기 내가 도와줘야 할 젊은 친구가 하나 있다. 이 친구를 위해서나 우리를 위해서나 공정하게, 이 친구가 한 말을 제대로 알아봐야 해. 그리고 그게 사실로 밝혀지면 — 나는 분명히 그러리라 믿는다만 — 우리가 이 친구를 도와야지, 도와야 한다, 네드 동생.\""]], [["‘It is enough, my dear brother, that you say we should,’ returned the other.", "\"형이 도와야 한다고 하면, 그걸로 충분해요, 형.\" 동생이 대답했어."], ["‘When you say that, no further inquiries are needed.", "\"형이 그렇게 말하면, 더 알아볼 것도 없죠."], ["He SHALL be assisted.", "이 친구는 도움을 받게 될 거예요."], ["What are his necessities, and what does he require?", "뭐가 필요하대요, 뭘 원하는데요?"], ["Where is Tim Linkinwater?", "팀 링킨워터는 어디 있죠?"], ["Let us have him here.’", "그를 이리 부릅시다.\""]], [["Both the brothers, it may be here remarked, had a very emphatic and earnest delivery; both had lost nearly the same teeth, which imparted the same peculiarity to their speech; and both spoke as if, besides possessing the utmost serenity of mind that the kindliest and most unsuspecting nature could bestow, they had, in collecting the plums from Fortune’s choicest pudding, retained a few for present use, and kept them in their mouths.", "여기서 한마디 덧붙이자면, 두 형제는 둘 다 말하는 투가 아주 힘 있고 진지했어. 둘 다 거의 같은 자리의 이가 빠져서, 말에 똑같은 특이함이 묻어났지. 그리고 둘 다 마치, 더없이 인자하고 의심을 모르는 천성이 줄 수 있는 가장 평온한 마음을 지닌 데다가, 운명의 가장 좋은 푸딩에서 건포도를 추려 내면서 지금 쓸 몇 알을 남겨 입에 물고 있기라도 한 것처럼 말했어."]], [["‘Where is Tim Linkinwater?’ said brother Ned.", "\"팀 링킨워터는 어디 있죠?\" 네드 동생이 말했어."]], [["‘Stop, stop, stop!’ said brother Charles, taking the other aside.", "\"잠깐, 잠깐, 잠깐!\" 찰스 형이 동생을 한쪽으로 데려가며 말했어."], ["‘I’ve a plan, my dear brother, I’ve a plan.", "\"나한테 계획이 있다, 동생아, 계획이 있어. 팀도 이제 늙어 가고 있어."], ["Tim is getting old, and Tim has been a faithful servant, brother Ned; and I don’t think pensioning Tim’s mother and sister, and buying a little tomb for the family when his poor brother died, was a sufficient recompense for his faithful services.’", "그리고 팀은 충실한 일꾼이었지, 네드 동생. 팀의 어머니와 누이한테 연금을 주고, 그 가엾은 형이 죽었을 때 가족 묘를 조그맣게 하나 사 준 것만으로는, 그 충실한 봉사에 충분한 보답이 못 됐다는 생각이 든다.\""]], [["‘No, no, no,’ replied the other.", "\"아냐, 아냐, 아냐.\" 동생이 대답했어."], ["‘Certainly not.", "\"물론 모자라죠."], ["Not half enough, not half.’", "반의반도 안 돼요, 반의반도.\""]], [["‘If we could lighten Tim’s duties,’ said the old gentleman, ‘and prevail upon him to go into the country, now and then, and sleep in the fresh air, besides, two or three times a week (which he could, if he began business an hour later in the morning), old Tim Linkinwater would grow young again in time; and he’s three good years our senior now.", "\"우리가 팀의 일을 좀 덜어 줄 수 있다면,\" 노신사가 말했어."], ["Old Tim Linkinwater young again!", "\"그리고 가끔 시골에 내려가도록, 게다가 일주일에 두세 번은 신선한 공기를 마시며 자도록 구슬릴 수 있다면(아침에 일을 한 시간 늦게 시작하면 그럴 수 있잖아), 늙은 팀 링킨워터도 머잖아 다시 젊어질 거야. 게다가 지금 우리보다 꼬박 세 살이나 위잖니."], ["Eh, brother Ned, eh?", "늙은 팀 링킨워터가 다시 젊어진다!"], ["Why, I recollect old Tim Linkinwater quite a little boy, don’t you?", "응, 네드 동생, 응? 아니, 난 팀 링킨워터가 아주 꼬맹이였을 때가 기억나는데, 너는?"], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하!"], ["Poor Tim, poor Tim!’", "가엾은 팀, 가엾은 팀!\""]], [["And the fine old fellows laughed pleasantly together: each with a tear of regard for old Tim Linkinwater standing in his eye.", "그러고는 이 멋진 노인들은 함께 정답게 웃었어. 둘 다 눈에 늙은 팀 링킨워터를 아끼는 눈물 한 방울씩을 그렁그렁 머금고 말이야."]], [["‘But hear this first--hear this first, brother Ned,’ said the old man, hastily, placing two chairs, one on each side of Nicholas: ‘I’ll tell it you myself, brother Ned, because the young gentleman is modest, and is a scholar, Ned, and I shouldn’t feel it right that he should tell us his story over and over again as if he was a beggar, or as if we doubted him. No, no no.’", "\"그래도 이것부터 들어 봐 — 이것부터, 네드 동생.\" 노인이 니콜라스 양옆에 의자를 하나씩 놓으며 서둘러 말했어. \"이건 내가 직접 너한테 얘기하마, 네드 동생. 이 젊은이는 겸손한 데다, 학식도 있어, 네드. 그러니 마치 거지라도 되는 양, 아니면 우리가 자기를 못 믿기라도 하는 양, 이 친구가 자기 사연을 몇 번이고 되풀이하게 하는 건 옳지 않다 싶구나. 아니지, 아니야, 아냐.\""]], [["‘No, no, no,’ returned the other, nodding his head gravely.", "\"아냐, 아냐, 아냐.\" 동생이 진지하게 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했어."], ["‘Very right, my dear brother, very right.’", "\"지당해요, 형, 지당하죠.\""]], [["‘He will tell me I’m wrong, if I make a mistake,’ said Nicholas’s friend.", "\"내가 잘못 말하면, 이 친구가 틀렸다고 일러 줄 거야.\" 니콜라스의 친구가 말했어."], ["‘But whether I do or not, you’ll be very much affected, brother Ned, remembering the time when we were two friendless lads, and earned our first shilling in this great city.’", "\"하지만 내가 그러든 안 그러든, 너는 무척 뭉클해질 거다, 네드 동생. 우리가 친구 하나 없는 두 소년이었던 시절, 이 큰 도시에서 처음으로 일 실링을 벌던 그때가 떠올라서 말이야.\""]], [["The twins pressed each other’s hands in silence; and in his own homely manner, brother Charles related the particulars he had heard from Nicholas.", "쌍둥이는 말없이 서로의 손을 꼭 쥐었어. 그리고 찰스 형은 자기 특유의 수수한 말투로, 니콜라스한테서 들은 사연을 들려줬지."], ["The conversation which ensued was a long one, and when it was over, a secret conference of almost equal duration took place between brother Ned and Tim Linkinwater in another room.", "그 뒤로 이어진 대화는 길었어. 그리고 그게 끝나자, 네드 동생과 팀 링킨워터 사이에 또 다른 방에서 거의 그만큼 긴 비밀 회의가 벌어졌어."], ["It is no disparagement to Nicholas to say, that before he had been closeted with the two brothers ten minutes, he could only wave his hand at every fresh expression of kindness and sympathy, and sob like a little child.", "두 형제와 마주 앉은 지 채 십 분도 안 되어, 니콜라스가 새로 베풀어지는 친절과 동정의 말이 나올 때마다 그저 손을 내저으며 어린아이처럼 흐느꼈다고 해서, 그를 깎아내릴 일은 아니지."]], [["At length brother Ned and Tim Linkinwater came back together, when Tim instantly walked up to Nicholas and whispered in his ear in a very brief sentence (for Tim was ordinarily a man of few words), that he had taken down the address in the Strand, and would call upon him that evening, at eight.", "마침내 네드 동생과 팀 링킨워터가 함께 돌아왔어. 그러자 팀이 곧장 니콜라스한테 걸어가, 아주 짤막한 한 문장을(팀은 평소 말이 적은 사람이었거든) 그의 귀에 속삭였어."], ["Having done which, Tim wiped his spectacles and put them on, preparatory to hearing what more the brothers Cheeryble had got to say.", "스트랜드의 주소를 적어 두었으니, 그날 저녁 여덟 시에 찾아가겠다는 거였지. 그러고 나서 팀은 치어리블 형제가 또 무슨 할 말이 있는지 들을 채비로, 안경을 닦아 다시 썼어."]], [["‘Tim,’ said brother Charles, ‘you understand that we have an intention of taking this young gentleman into the counting-house?’", "\"팀,\" 찰스 형이 말했어. \"우리가 이 젊은이를 회계실에 들일 작정이라는 거, 알겠지?\""]], [["Brother Ned remarked that Tim was aware of that intention, and quite approved of it; and Tim having nodded, and said he did, drew himself up and looked particularly fat, and very important.", "네드 동생은 팀이 그 작정을 이미 알고 있고, 아주 찬성한다고 한마디 했어. 그리고 팀은 고개를 끄덕이며 그렇다고 하고는, 몸을 쭉 펴고서 유난히 뚱뚱하고 아주 중요한 사람처럼 굴었어."], ["After which, there was a profound silence.", "그러고 나선 깊은 침묵이 흘렀지."]], [["‘I’m not coming an hour later in the morning, you know,’ said Tim, breaking out all at once, and looking very resolute.", "\"난 아침에 한 시간 늦게 안 나올 거예요, 아시잖아요.\" 팀이 갑자기 다 쏟아 내듯, 아주 단호한 얼굴로 말했어."], ["‘I’m not going to sleep in the fresh air; no, nor I’m not going into the country either.", "\"신선한 공기 마시며 잠도 안 잘 거고요. 아니, 시골에도 안 갈 거예요."], ["A pretty thing at this time of day, certainly.", "이 나이에 참 어울리는 일이네요, 정말."], ["Pho!’", "흥!\""]], [["‘Damn your obstinacy, Tim Linkinwater,’ said brother Charles, looking at him without the faintest spark of anger, and with a countenance radiant with attachment to the old clerk.", "\"네 고집은 빌어먹을 노릇이다, 팀 링킨워터.\" 찰스 형이 노여움이라곤 손톱만큼도 없이, 그 늙은 사무원을 향한 애정으로 환히 빛나는 얼굴로 그를 바라보며 말했어."], ["‘Damn your obstinacy, Tim Linkinwater, what do you mean, sir?’", "\"네 고집은 빌어먹을 노릇이야, 팀 링킨워터. 대체 무슨 소리냐, 응?\""]], [["‘It’s forty-four year,’ said Tim, making a calculation in the air with his pen, and drawing an imaginary line before he cast it up, ‘forty-four year, next May, since I first kept the books of Cheeryble, Brothers.", "\"올해로 사십사 년이에요.\" 팀이 펜으로 허공에 셈을 하면서, 합산하기 전에 가상의 선을 죽 그으며 말했어."], ["I’ve opened the safe every morning all that time (Sundays excepted) as the clock struck nine, and gone over the house every night at half-past ten (except on Foreign Post nights, and then twenty minutes before twelve) to see the doors fastened, and the fires out.", "\"오는 오월이면 사십사 년이죠."], ["I’ve never slept out of the back-attic one single night.", "제가 처음으로 치어리블 형제 상회의 장부를 맡은 지가요."], ["There’s the same mignonette box in the middle of the window, and the same four flower-pots, two on each side, that I brought with me when I first came.", "그동안 내내(일요일만 빼고) 매일 아침 시계가 아홉 시를 칠 때 금고를 열었고요, 매일 밤 열 시 반에(해외 우편 날만 빼고요, 그날은 열두 시 이십 분 전에) 온 집을 돌며 문이 잠겼는지, 불은 꺼졌는지 살폈죠. 뒤쪽 다락방을 벗어나 잔 적이 단 하루도 없어요."], ["There an’t--I’ve said it again and again, and I’ll maintain it--there an’t such a square as this in the world.", "창문 한가운데엔 똑같은 목서초 화분이 있고, 양쪽에 두 개씩, 똑같은 화분 네 개가 있는데, 다 제가 처음 왔을 때 가져온 거예요. 세상에 — 몇 번이고 말했고, 앞으로도 우길 거예요 — 세상에 이런 광장은 없어요."], ["I KNOW there an’t,’ said Tim, with sudden energy, and looking sternly about him.", "없다는 걸 제가 안다고요.\" 팀이 갑자기 힘이 솟은 듯, 주위를 매섭게 둘러보며 말했어."], ["‘Not one.", "\"단 하나도요."], ["For business or pleasure, in summer-time or winter--I don’t care which--there’s nothing like it.", "일을 하든 즐기든, 여름이든 겨울이든 — 어느 쪽이든 상관없어요 — 이만한 데가 없어요. 영국에 저 아치 밑 펌프 같은 샘은 없어요."], ["There’s not such a spring in England as the pump under the archway.", "영국에 제 창문에서 내다보는 것 같은 경치도 없고요. 면도하기 전에 매일 아침 봐 왔으니, 제가 그 정도는 좀 알죠."], ["There’s not such a view in England as the view out of my window; I’ve seen it every morning before I shaved, and I ought to know something about it.", "저는 그 방에서 잤어요.\" 팀이 목소리를 좀 낮추며 덧붙였어."], ["I have slept in that room,’ added Tim, sinking his voice a little, ‘for four-and-forty year; and if it wasn’t inconvenient, and didn’t interfere with business, I should request leave to die there.’", "\"사십사 년 동안요. 그리고 폐가 안 되고 일에 지장만 안 된다면, 거기서 죽게 해 달라고 청하고 싶을 정도예요.\""]], [["‘Damn you, Tim Linkinwater, how dare you talk about dying?’ roared the twins by one impulse, and blowing their old noses violently.", "\"네 이놈, 팀 링킨워터, 감히 죽는다는 소리를 입에 담아?\" 쌍둥이가 동시에 버럭 소리치며, 늙은 코를 요란하게 풀었어."]], [["‘That’s what I’ve got to say, Mr. Edwin and Mr.", "\"제가 드릴 말씀은 그거예요, 에드윈 어른, 찰스 어른.\""], ["Charles,’ said Tim, squaring his shoulders again.", "팀이 다시 어깨를 펴며 말했어."], ["‘This isn’t the first time you’ve talked about superannuating me; but, if you please, we’ll make it the last, and drop the subject for evermore.’", "\"두 분이 저를 퇴직시키겠다는 얘기를 꺼낸 게 이번이 처음은 아니죠. 하지만 괜찮으시다면, 이번을 마지막으로 하고, 그 얘기는 영영 그만 접도록 하죠.\""]], [["With these words, Tim Linkinwater stalked out, and shut himself up in his glass case, with the air of a man who had had his say, and was thoroughly resolved not to be put down.", "이 말과 함께 팀 링킨워터는 성큼성큼 나가 버렸어. 그러고는 할 말을 다 했고 결코 꺾이지 않겠다고 단단히 마음먹은 사람의 기세로, 자기 유리 상자 안에 틀어박혔지."]], [["The brothers interchanged looks, and coughed some half-dozen times without speaking.", "두 형제는 서로 눈짓을 주고받더니, 말없이 대여섯 번쯤 헛기침을 했어."]], [["‘He must be done something with, brother Ned,’ said the other, warmly; ‘we must disregard his old scruples; they can’t be tolerated, or borne. He must be made a partner, brother Ned; and if he won’t submit to it peaceably, we must have recourse to violence.’", "\"저 친구한테 무슨 수를 써야겠다, 네드 동생.\" 형이 열을 내며 말했어. \"저 케케묵은 망설임은 무시해 버려야지. 그냥 두고 봐 줄 수도, 참아 줄 수도 없어. 저 친구를 동업자로 만들어야겠다, 네드 동생. 그리고 순순히 따르지 않겠다면, 우리가 힘이라도 써야지.\""]], [["‘Quite right,’ replied brother Ned, nodding his head as a man thoroughly determined; ‘quite right, my dear brother.", "\"옳은 말이에요.\" 네드 동생이 단단히 마음먹은 사람처럼 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했어."], ["If he won’t listen to reason, we must do it against his will, and show him that we are determined to exert our authority.", "\"지당해요, 형. 저 친구가 사리를 안 듣겠다면, 우린 그 뜻을 거슬러서라도 해내야죠."], ["We must quarrel with him, brother Charles.’", "그리고 우리가 권한을 행사하기로 마음먹었다는 걸 보여 줘야죠. 우리가 저 친구하고 한바탕 싸워야겠어요, 찰스 형.\""]], [["‘We must.", "\"그래야지. 우린 분명 팀 링킨워터하고 한판 붙어야 해.\""], ["We certainly must have a quarrel with Tim Linkinwater,’ said the other.", "형이 말했어. \"하지만 그동안에, 동생아, 우리가 우리 젊은 친구를 붙들어 두고 있구나."], ["‘But in the meantime, my dear brother, we are keeping our young friend; and the poor lady and her daughter will be anxious for his return.", "그리고 그 가엾은 부인과 따님은 이 친구가 돌아오기를 애타게 기다리고 있을 거다. 그러니 일단은 작별 인사를 하자꾸나."], ["So let us say goodbye for the present, and--there, there--take care of that box, my dear sir--and--no, no, not a word now; but be careful of the crossings and--’", "그리고 — 자, 자 — 그 상자 조심하게, 이보게 — 그리고 — 아니, 아니, 이젠 한마디도 말고. 그저 길 건널 때 조심하고 또 —\""]], [["And with any disjointed and unconnected words which would prevent Nicholas from pouring forth his thanks, the brothers hurried him out: shaking hands with him all the way, and affecting very unsuccessfully--they were poor hands at deception!--to be wholly unconscious of the feelings that completely mastered him.", "이렇게 니콜라스가 고맙다는 말을 쏟아 내지 못하도록 두서없고 토막 난 말들을 늘어놓으며, 두 형제는 그를 서둘러 내보냈어. 가는 내내 그의 손을 흔들며, 그를 완전히 사로잡은 그 감정을 통 모르는 척하려 했지만 — 정말이지 거짓말엔 영 서툰 사람들이었어! — 영 신통치 않게 말이지."]], [["Nicholas’s heart was too full to allow of his turning into the street until he had recovered some composure.", "니콜라스는 가슴이 너무 벅차서, 어느 정도 마음을 가라앉히기 전엔 거리로 나설 수가 없었어."], ["When he at last glided out of the dark doorway corner in which he had been compelled to halt, he caught a glimpse of the twins stealthily peeping in at one corner of the glass case, evidently undecided whether they should follow up their late attack without delay, or for the present postpone laying further siege to the inflexible Tim Linkinwater.", "마침내 그가 어쩔 수 없이 걸음을 멈췄던 그 어두운 문간 구석에서 슬며시 빠져나오는데, 쌍둥이가 유리 상자 한쪽 모퉁이로 살그머니 안을 엿보는 게 얼핏 눈에 들어왔어. 좀 전의 그 공격을 지체 없이 밀어붙일지, 아니면 일단은 그 꿈쩍 않는 팀 링킨워터를 향한 추가 공세를 미뤄 둘지, 영 마음을 정하지 못한 게 분명했지."]], [["To recount all the delight and wonder which the circumstances just detailed awakened at Miss La Creevy’s, and all the things that were done, said, thought, expected, hoped, and prophesied in consequence, is beside the present course and purpose of these adventures.", "방금 자세히 늘어놓은 이 일들이 미스 라 크리비의 집에서 불러일으킨 온갖 기쁨과 놀라움을, 그리고 그 일로 행해지고, 입에 오르고, 떠올려지고, 기대되고, 바라지고, 점쳐진 온갖 것을 일일이 늘어놓는 건, 지금 이 모험의 흐름과 목적에서 벗어난 일이야."], ["It is sufficient to state, in brief, that Mr. Timothy Linkinwater arrived, punctual to his appointment; that, oddity as he was, and jealous, as he was bound to be, of the proper exercise of his employers’ most comprehensive liberality, he reported strongly and warmly in favour of Nicholas; and that, next day, he was appointed to the vacant stool in the counting-house of Cheeryble, Brothers, with a present salary of one hundred and twenty pounds a year.", "간단히 말해, 티머시 링킨워터 씨가 약속 시간에 딱 맞춰 도착했다는 것, 그가 별난 사람인 데다 주인들의 더없이 폭넓은 너그러움이 제대로 발휘되는지를 당연히 깐깐하게 따지는 사람이었음에도, 니콜라스에 대해 힘차고 따뜻하게 호의적인 보고를 했다는 것, 그리고 이튿날 니콜라스가 치어리블 형제 상회 회계실의 빈 의자에 임명되어, 당장 연봉 백이십 파운드를 받게 됐다는 것만 밝혀 두면 충분하지."]], [["‘And I think, my dear brother,’ said Nicholas’s first friend, ‘that if we were to let them that little cottage at Bow which is empty, at something under the usual rent, now?", "\"그리고 내 생각엔 말이다, 동생아,\" 니콜라스의 첫 친구가 말했어."], ["Eh, brother Ned?’", "\"지금 비어 있는 보 근처 그 작은 오두막을, 보통 집세보다 좀 싸게 저들한테 빌려주면 어떨까, 응? 어떠냐, 네드 동생?\""]], [["‘For nothing at all,’ said brother Ned.", "\"공짜로 주죠.\" 네드 동생이 말했어."], ["‘We are rich, and should be ashamed to touch the rent under such circumstances as these.", "\"우린 부자인데, 이런 형편에 처한 사람들한테서 집세를 받는다는 건 부끄러운 일이죠."], ["Where is Tim Linkinwater?--for nothing at all, my dear brother, for nothing at all.’", "팀 링킨워터는 어디 있죠? — 그냥 공짜로요, 형, 그냥 공짜로 줍시다.\""]], [["‘Perhaps it would be better to say something, brother Ned,’ suggested the other, mildly; ‘it would help to preserve habits of frugality, you know, and remove any painful sense of overwhelming obligations.", "\"그래도 얼마쯤은 받는 게 나을지도 모른다, 네드 동생.\" 형이 부드럽게 제안했어."], ["We might say fifteen pound, or twenty pound, and if it was punctually paid, make it up to them in some other way.", "\"그래야 절약하는 습관을 지키는 데 도움이 되고, 또 감당 못 할 신세를 졌다는 괴로운 마음도 덜어 주거든. 십오 파운드나 이십 파운드쯤 받기로 하고, 그걸 또박또박 내면, 다른 방법으로 그만큼 채워 주면 돼."], ["And I might secretly advance a small loan towards a little furniture, and you might secretly advance another small loan, brother Ned; and if we find them doing well--as we shall; there’s no fear, no fear--we can change the loans into gifts.", "그리고 내가 가구를 좀 들이게 슬쩍 작은 돈을 빌려주고, 너도 따로 슬쩍 작은 돈을 빌려주고, 네드 동생. 그러다 저들이 잘 지내는 걸 보면 — 분명 그럴 거야, 걱정 없어, 걱정 없지 — 그 빚을 선물로 바꿔 주면 돼."], ["Carefully, brother Ned, and by degrees, and without pressing upon them too much; what do you say now, brother?’", "조심조심, 네드 동생, 차근차근, 너무 부담을 주지 않으면서 말이야. 자, 이제 어떠냐, 동생아?\""]], [["Brother Ned gave his hand upon it, and not only said it should be done, but had it done too; and, in one short week, Nicholas took possession of the stool, and Mrs.", "네드 동생은 그 자리에서 손을 내밀어 그러자고 했어. 그러자고 말만 한 게 아니라 실제로 그렇게 해 버렸지."], ["Nickleby and Kate took possession of the house, and all was hope, bustle, and light-heartedness.", "그래서 짧은 한 주 만에, 니콜라스는 그 의자를 차지했고, 니클비 부인과 케이트는 그 집을 차지했어. 그리고 모든 게 희망과 분주함, 그리고 가벼운 마음으로 가득했지."]], [["There surely never was such a week of discoveries and surprises as the first week of that cottage.", "정말이지 그 오두막에서의 첫 주만큼 발견과 놀라움이 가득한 한 주는 없었을 거야."], ["Every night when Nicholas came home, something new had been found out.", "니콜라스가 집에 올 때마다 밤마다 새로운 게 발견됐어."], ["One day it was a grapevine, and another day it was a boiler, and another day it was the key of the front-parlour closet at the bottom of the water-butt, and so on through a hundred items.", "어느 날은 포도 덩굴이었고, 또 어느 날은 보일러였고, 또 어느 날은 물통 밑바닥에서 나온 앞쪽 응접실 벽장 열쇠였고, 그런 식으로 백 가지나 되는 것들이 줄줄이 나왔지. 그러고는 이 방은 모슬린 커튼으로 꾸며졌고, 저 방은 창문 블라인드로 제법 우아해졌고, 누구도 가능하리라 생각 못 했을 단장들이 이루어졌어."], ["Then, this room was embellished with a muslin curtain, and that room was rendered quite elegant by a window-blind, and such improvements were made, as no one would have supposed possible.", "그리고 미스 라 크리비도 있었지. 하루 이틀 머물며 거들려고 합승마차를 타고 나온 사람인데, 양철 압정이 든 아주 작은 갈색 종이 꾸러미하고 아주 큼지막한 망치를 줄곧 잃어버리고, 소맷부리를 걷어붙인 채 이리저리 뛰어다니고, 사다리에서 굴러떨어져 어디를 호되게 다치기도 했어 — 그리고 니클비 부인도 있었지."], ["Then there was Miss La Creevy, who had come out in the omnibus to stop a day or two and help, and who was perpetually losing a very small brown-paper parcel of tin tacks and a very large hammer, and running about with her sleeves tucked up at the wrists, and falling off pairs of steps and hurting herself very much--and Mrs.", "쉴 새 없이 떠들면서 가끔 뭔가를 하긴 했지만, 자주는 아니었어 — 그리고 케이트도 있었지. 어디서나 소리 없이 부지런히 움직이며, 무엇이든 다 좋아했어 — 그리고 스마이크도 있었지."], ["Nickleby, who talked incessantly, and did something now and then, but not often--and Kate, who busied herself noiselessly everywhere, and was pleased with everything--and Smike, who made the garden a perfect wonder to look upon--and Nicholas, who helped and encouraged them every one--all the peace and cheerfulness of home restored, with such new zest imparted to every frugal pleasure, and such delight to every hour of meeting, as misfortune and separation alone could give!", "정원을 보기만 해도 그저 감탄스러운 곳으로 만들어 놓았어 — 그리고 니콜라스도 있었지. 모두를 거들고 북돋워 주었어 — 집의 온갖 평화와 즐거움이 되살아났고, 검소한 기쁨 하나하나에 그토록 새로운 맛이 더해지고, 함께하는 매 순간이 그토록 기뻤으니, 그건 오직 불행과 이별만이 줄 수 있는 것이었지!"]], [["In short, the poor Nicklebys were social and happy; while the rich Nickleby was alone and miserable.", "한마디로, 가난한 니클비네 식구는 정답고 행복했어. 반면에 부유한 니클비는 외롭고 비참했지."]], [["# CHAPTER 36", "# CHAPTER 36"]], [["Private and confidential; relating to Family Matters.", "저녁 일곱 시쯤이었어."], ["Showing how Mr Kenwigs underwent violent Agitation, and how Mrs.", "골든 스퀘어 근처의 좁은 골목길에 어둠이 내려앉기 시작할 무렵이었지."], ["Kenwigs was as well as could be expected\nIt might have been seven o’clock in the evening, and it was growing dark in the narrow streets near Golden Square, when Mr.", "케이크위그스 씨는 가장 싼 흰 새끼염소 가죽 장갑 한 켤레를 사 오라고 사람을 보냈어. 십사 펜스짜리 장갑이었지."], ["Kenwigs sent out for a pair of the cheapest white kid gloves--those at fourteen-pence--and selecting the strongest, which happened to be the right-hand one, walked downstairs with an air of pomp and much excitement, and proceeded to muffle the knob of the street-door knocker therein.", "그러고는 그중 제일 튼튼한 걸 골랐는데, 마침 오른손 장갑이었어. 그는 거드름을 피우면서, 한껏 들떠서 아래층으로 내려갔어."], ["Having executed this task with great nicety, Mr.", "그러고는 현관문 노커의 손잡이를 그 장갑으로 둘둘 감았지."], ["Kenwigs pulled the door to, after him, and just stepped across the road to try the effect from the opposite side of the street.", "이 일을 아주 정성껏 해낸 다음, 케이크위그스 씨는 문을 끌어당겨 닫고 길 건너편으로 잠깐 가서 효과가 어떤지 살펴봤어."], ["Satisfied that nothing could possibly look better in its way, Mr.", "도무지 이보다 더 멋질 수는 없다는 데 흡족해진 케이크위그스 씨는 다시 길을 건너왔어."], ["Kenwigs then stepped back again, and calling through the keyhole to Morleena to open the door, vanished into the house, and was seen no longer.", "그러고는 열쇠 구멍에 대고 몰리나에게 문을 열라고 외친 다음, 집 안으로 사라져 더는 보이지 않았어."]], [["Now, considered as an abstract circumstance, there was no more obvious cause or reason why Mr.", "자, 그냥 따로 떼어 놓고 생각해 보면, 케이크위그스 씨가 굳이 이 노커를 감싸느라 수고를 들일 이유가 별로 없었어."], ["Kenwigs should take the trouble of muffling this particular knocker, than there would have been for his muffling the knocker of any nobleman or gentleman resident ten miles off; because, for the greater convenience of the numerous lodgers, the street-door always stood wide open, and the knocker was never used at all.", "십 마일이나 떨어진 곳에 사는 어느 귀족이나 신사의 노커를 감싸는 것만큼이나 뜬금없는 일이었지. 왜냐하면 수많은 하숙인들이 편하게 드나들도록 현관문은 늘 활짝 열려 있었고, 노커는 한 번도 쓰인 적이 없었거든."], ["The first floor, the second floor, and the third floor, had each a bell of its own.", "이 층도, 삼 층도, 사 층도 저마다 따로 초인종이 있었어. 다락방으로 말하자면, 거길 찾아오는 사람은 아무도 없었지."], ["As to the attics, no one ever called on them; if anybody wanted the parlours, they were close at hand, and all he had to do was to walk straight into them; while the kitchen had a separate entrance down the area steps.", "그리고 누가 일 층 응접실에 볼일이 있으면, 그건 바로 코앞이라서 그냥 곧장 걸어 들어가면 그만이었어. 부엌은 또 지하로 내려가는 계단 쪽에 따로 입구가 있었고."], ["As a question of mere necessity and usefulness, therefore, this muffling of the knocker was thoroughly incomprehensible.", "그러니 그저 필요하냐 쓸모가 있느냐만 따지면, 이 노커를 감싸는 일은 도무지 이해할 수 없는 짓이었지."]], [["But knockers may be muffled for other purposes than those of mere utilitarianism, as, in the present instance, was clearly shown.", "하지만 노커란 그저 실용적인 목적 말고도 다른 까닭으로 감쌀 수 있는 거야. 이번 경우가 바로 그걸 똑똑히 보여 줬지."], ["There are certain polite forms and ceremonies which must be observed in civilised life, or mankind relapse into their original barbarism.", "문명사회에는 반드시 지켜야 할 점잖은 격식과 의례가 있거든. 안 그러면 사람들은 원래의 야만 상태로 되돌아가 버리고 마니까."], ["No genteel lady was ever yet confined--indeed, no genteel confinement can possibly take place--without the accompanying symbol of a muffled knocker.", "점잖은 부인이 해산을 하는데 — 아니, 점잖은 해산이라면 당연히 — 노커를 감싸는 상징이 따라붙지 않는 법은 결코 없었어."], ["Mrs. Kenwigs was a lady of some pretensions to gentility; Mrs Kenwigs was confined.", "케이크위그스 부인은 점잖음을 어느 정도 자처하는 부인이었고, 마침 해산을 하는 참이었지."], ["And, therefore, Mr. Kenwigs tied up the silent knocker on the premises in a white kid glove.", "그래서 케이크위그스 씨는 이 집의 잠잠한 노커를 흰 새끼염소 가죽 장갑으로 묶어 둔 거야."]], [["‘I’m not quite certain neither,’ said Mr.", "\"근데 말이지,\""], ["Kenwigs, arranging his shirt-collar, and walking slowly upstairs, ‘whether, as it’s a boy, I won’t have it in the papers.’", "케이크위그스 씨는 셔츠 깃을 매만지며 천천히 계단을 올라가면서 중얼거렸어. \"아들이니까, 이걸 신문에 내야 하나 말아야 하나, 아직 잘 모르겠단 말이야.\""]], [["Pondering upon the advisability of this step, and the sensation it was likely to create in the neighbourhood, Mr.", "이렇게 하는 게 과연 좋을지, 또 동네에 어떤 화젯거리가 될지 곰곰이 따져 보면서, 케이크위그스 씨는 거실로 들어갔어."], ["Kenwigs betook himself to the sitting-room, where various extremely diminutive articles of clothing were airing on a horse before the fire, and Mr.", "거기엔 아주 자그마한 옷가지들이 난로 앞 빨래걸이에 널려서 바람을 쐬고 있었고, 의사인 럼비 씨가 아기를 어르고 있었지."], ["Lumbey, the doctor, was dandling the baby--that is, the old baby--not the new one.", "그러니까 새 아기가 아니라 그 위의 아기를 말이야."]], [["‘It’s a fine boy, Mr. Kenwigs,’ said Mr.", "\"훌륭한 사내아이로군요, 케이크위그스 씨.\""], ["Lumbey, the doctor.", "의사 럼비 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘You consider him a fine boy, do you, sir?’ returned Mr.", "\"이 아이가 훌륭한 사내아이라고 보시는 거죠, 선생님?\""], ["Kenwigs.", "케이크위그스 씨가 되받았어."]], [["‘It’s the finest boy I ever saw in all my life,’ said the doctor.", "\"내 평생 본 중에 가장 훌륭한 사내아이입니다.\" 의사가 말했어."], ["‘I never saw such a baby.’", "\"이런 아기는 본 적이 없어요.\""]], [["It is a pleasant thing to reflect upon, and furnishes a complete answer to those who contend for the gradual degeneration of the human species, that every baby born into the world is a finer one than the last.", "생각해 보면 참 흐뭇한 일이야. 세상에 태어나는 아기는 하나같이 그 앞의 아기보다 더 훌륭하다니 말이지. 인류가 점점 퇴화한다고 우기는 사람들한테 이건 완벽한 대답이 되거든."]], [["‘I ne--ver saw such a baby,’ said Mr.", "\"이런 아기는 정말이지 본 적이 없어요.\""], ["Lumbey, the doctor.", "의사 럼비 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Morleena was a fine baby,’ remarked Mr.", "\"몰리나도 훌륭한 아기였죠.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 한마디 했어."], ["Kenwigs; as if this were rather an attack, by implication, upon the family.", "마치 그 말이 은근히 자기 집안을 깎아내리는 거라도 되는 양 말이야."]], [["‘They were all fine babies,’ said Mr.", "\"다들 훌륭한 아기였죠.\""], ["Lumbey. And Mr.", "럼비 씨가 말했어."], ["Lumbey went on nursing the baby with a thoughtful look.", "그러고는 럼비 씨는 생각에 잠긴 표정으로 계속 아기를 어르고 있었어."], ["Whether he was considering under what head he could best charge the nursing in the bill, was best known to himself.", "어쩌면 청구서에 이 어르는 값을 어느 항목으로 매기는 게 제일 나을까 따져 보던 건지도 모르지. 그건 자기만 알 일이야."]], [["During this short conversation, Miss Morleena, as the eldest of the family, and natural representative of her mother during her indisposition, had been hustling and slapping the three younger Miss Kenwigses, without intermission; which considerate and affectionate conduct brought tears into the eyes of Mr.", "이 짤막한 대화가 오가는 동안, 몰리나 양은 집안의 맏이로서, 또 어머니가 자리에 누운 동안 어머니를 대신하는 사람으로서, 세 동생을 쉴 새 없이 떠밀고 찰싹찰싹 때리고 있었어. 이런 사려 깊고 다정한 행동에 케이크위그스 씨는 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁해졌지."], ["Kenwigs, and caused him to declare that, in understanding and behaviour, that child was a woman.", "그러고는 저 아이는 철든 것이나 처신하는 것이나 어엿한 어른이라고 했어."]], [["‘She will be a treasure to the man she marries, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"저 아이는 시집가는 남자한테 보배가 될 겁니다, 선생님.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 반쯤 혼잣말처럼 말했어."], ["Kenwigs, half aside; ‘I think she’ll marry above her station, Mr. Lumbey.’", "\"내 생각엔 제 신분보다 높은 데로 시집갈 것 같아요, 럼비 씨.\""]], [["‘I shouldn’t wonder at all,’ replied the doctor.", "\"전혀 이상할 게 없죠.\" 의사가 대답했어."]], [["‘You never see her dance, sir, did you?’ asked Mr.", "\"저 아이가 춤추는 거 한 번도 못 보셨죠, 선생님?\""], ["Kenwigs.", "케이크위그스 씨가 물었어."]], [["The doctor shook his head.", "의사는 고개를 저었어."]], [["‘Ay!’ said Mr.", "\"저런!\""], ["Kenwigs, as though he pitied him from his heart, ‘then you don’t know what she’s capable of.’", "케이크위그스 씨가 진심으로 그를 딱하게 여기는 듯이 말했어. \"그럼 저 아이가 어떤 재주가 있는지 모르시겠군요.\""]], [["All this time there had been a great whisking in and out of the other room; the door had been opened and shut very softly about twenty times a minute (for it was necessary to keep Mrs.", "이러는 내내 옆방에서는 사람들이 부산하게 들락날락했어. 문이 일 분에 스무 번쯤은 아주 조용히 열렸다 닫혔지."], ["Kenwigs quiet); and the baby had been exhibited to a score or two of deputations from a select body of female friends, who had assembled in the passage, and about the street-door, to discuss the event in all its bearings.", "케이크위그스 부인을 조용히 쉬게 해야 했으니까. 그리고 아기는 복도며 현관문 언저리에 모여든 여자 친구들의 선발 무리에서 보낸 대표단 스물 몇 팀에게 차례로 선을 보였어."], ["Indeed, the excitement extended itself over the whole street, and groups of ladies might be seen standing at the doors, (some in the interesting condition in which Mrs.", "그 여자들은 이 사건을 온갖 방향에서 따져 보려고 모여 있었지. 사실 이 흥분은 온 거리로 퍼져 나갔어."], ["Kenwigs had last appeared in public,) relating their experiences of similar occurrences.", "문가에 서 있는 부인네들 무리도 보였는데, 그중 몇몇은 케이크위그스 부인이 사람들 앞에 마지막으로 나타났을 때와 똑같은 흥미로운 상태였지. 그들은 저마다 비슷한 일을 겪은 경험담을 늘어놓고 있었어."], ["Some few acquired great credit from having prophesied, the day before yesterday, exactly when it would come to pass; others, again, related, how that they guessed what it was, directly they saw Mr.", "몇몇은 그저께 정확히 언제 그 일이 일어날지 미리 점쳤다며 큰 칭송을 받았지. 또 어떤 이들은, 케이크위그스 씨가 새파랗게 질려서 있는 힘껏 거리를 내달리는 걸 보자마자 무슨 일인지 단박에 알아챘다고 떠벌렸어."], ["Kenwigs turn pale and run up the street as hard as ever he could go.", "이 사람은 이 말을 하고, 저 사람은 저 말을 했지."], ["Some said one thing, and some another; but all talked together, and all agreed upon two points: first, that it was very meritorious and highly praiseworthy in Mrs.", "하지만 다들 한꺼번에 떠들어 댔고, 두 가지 점에서는 모두 의견이 일치했어. 첫째, 케이크위그스 부인이 한 일은 아주 갸륵하고 더없이 칭찬받을 만하다는 것."], ["Kenwigs to do as she had done: and secondly, that there never was such a skilful and scientific doctor as that Dr Lumbey.", "둘째, 저 럼비 선생만큼 솜씨 좋고 과학적인 의사는 일찍이 없었다는 것이었지."]], [["In the midst of this general hubbub, Dr Lumbey sat in the first-floor front, as before related, nursing the deposed baby, and talking to Mr Kenwigs.", "이 떠들썩한 소란 한가운데서, 럼비 선생은 앞서 말한 대로 이 층 앞방에 앉아, 자리에서 밀려난 아기를 어르며 케이크위그스 씨와 이야기를 나누고 있었어. 그는 퉁퉁하고 무뚝뚝해 보이는 신사였는데, 셔츠 깃이랄 것도 거의 없었고, 어제 아침부터 자란 수염이 까칠하게 돋아 있었지."], ["He was a stout bluff-looking gentleman, with no shirt-collar to speak of, and a beard that had been growing since yesterday morning; for Dr Lumbey was popular, and the neighbourhood was prolific; and there had been no less than three other knockers muffled, one after the other within the last forty-eight hours.", "럼비 선생은 인기가 많았고 이 동네는 아기가 많이 태어나는 곳이었거든. 그래서 지난 마흔여덟 시간 사이에 노커를 감싼 집이 이 집 말고도 잇따라 세 군데나 더 있었던 거야."]], [["‘Well, Mr.", "\"자, 케이크위그스 씨.\""], ["Kenwigs,’ said Dr Lumbey, ‘this makes six.", "럼비 선생이 말했어. \"이걸로 여섯이로군요."], ["You’ll have a fine family in time, sir.’", "머잖아 식구가 아주 번창하시겠어요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘I think six is almost enough, sir,’ returned Mr.", "\"여섯이면 거의 충분한 것 같은데요, 선생님.\""], ["Kenwigs.", "케이크위그스 씨가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Pooh!", "\"쯧쯧!"], ["pooh!’ said the doctor.", "무슨 말씀을!\" 의사가 말했어."], ["‘Nonsense!", "\"터무니없는 소리!"], ["not half enough.’", "절반도 안 됩니다.\""]], [["With this, the doctor laughed; but he didn’t laugh half as much as a married friend of Mrs.", "이렇게 말하며 의사는 웃었어. 그런데 케이크위그스 부인의 결혼한 친구 하나가 웃는 것의 절반도 안 되게 웃었지."], ["Kenwigs’s, who had just come in from the sick chamber to report progress, and take a small sip of brandy-and-water: and who seemed to consider it one of the best jokes ever launched upon society.", "그 부인은 마침 산실에서 나와 경과를 알리고 브랜디 탄 물을 한 모금 홀짝이러 온 참이었는데, 이걸 세상에 나온 농담 중 가장 멋진 축에 든다고 여기는 모양이었어."]], [["‘They’re not altogether dependent upon good fortune, neither,’ said Mr.", "\"이 아이들이 그저 운에만 매달려 사는 건 아니지요.\""], ["Kenwigs, taking his second daughter on his knee; ‘they have expectations.’", "케이크위그스 씨가 둘째 딸을 무릎에 앉히며 말했어. \"이 아이들한테는 기대할 데가 있거든요.\""]], [["‘Oh, indeed!’ said Mr.", "\"오, 그래요!\""], ["Lumbey, the doctor.", "의사 럼비 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘And very good ones too, I believe, haven’t they?’ asked the married lady.", "\"게다가 아주 든든한 데죠? 안 그래요?\" 결혼한 부인이 물었어."]], [["‘Why, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "\"글쎄요, 부인.\""], ["Kenwigs, ‘it’s not exactly for me to say what they may be, or what they may not be.", "케이크위그스 씨가 말했어. \"그게 얼마나 될지 안 될지 콕 집어 말하는 건 제 처지에 할 일이 아니죠."], ["It’s not for me to boast of any family with which I have the honour to be connected; at the same time, Mrs Kenwigs’s is--I should say,’ said Mr.", "영광스럽게도 제가 연을 맺은 집안 자랑을 늘어놓는 것도 제가 할 일이 아니고요. 다만 케이크위그스 집안은 — 제가 드리고 싶은 말씀은,\""], ["Kenwigs, abruptly, and raising his voice as he spoke, ‘that my children might come into a matter of a hundred pound apiece, perhaps.", "케이크위그스 씨가 갑자기 목소리를 높이며 말했어. \"제 아이들이 어쩌면 한 명당 백 파운드쯤 손에 넣게 될지도 모른다는 겁니다."], ["Perhaps more, but certainly that.’", "어쩌면 그보다 더 될 수도 있고요, 하지만 그만큼은 확실하죠.\""]], [["‘And a very pretty little fortune,’ said the married lady.", "\"아주 아담하고 어여쁜 재산이네요.\" 결혼한 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘There are some relations of Mrs. Kenwigs’s,’ said Mr.", "\"케이크위그스 집안에는 친척이 몇 분 있는데요.\""], ["Kenwigs, taking a pinch of snuff from the doctor’s box, and then sneezing very hard, for he wasn’t used to it, ‘that might leave their hundred pound apiece to ten people, and yet not go begging when they had done it.’", "케이크위그스 씨가 의사의 코담배 통에서 코담배를 한 줌 집어 들고는, 익숙지가 않아서 아주 세게 재채기를 하며 말했어. \"그분들은 열 사람한테 백 파운드씩 남겨 주고도, 그러고 나서 동냥하러 다닐 일은 없는 분들이지요.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["I know who you mean,’ observed the married lady, nodding her head.", "누구 말씀인지 알겠어요.\" 결혼한 부인이 고개를 끄덕이며 말했어."]], [["‘I made mention of no names, and I wish to make mention of no names,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, with a portentous look.", "\"저는 아무 이름도 입에 올리지 않았고, 또 어떤 이름도 입에 올리고 싶지 않습니다.\""], ["‘Many of my friends have met a relation of Mrs.", "케이크위그스 씨가 의미심장한 표정으로 말했어."], ["Kenwigs’s in this very room, as would do honour to any company; that’s all.’", "\"제 친구들 중에 바로 이 방에서 케이크위그스 집안의 어떤 친척을 만나 본 분이 많은데, 그분은 어떤 자리에 가더라도 그 자리를 빛낼 분이지요. 제 말은 그게 다입니다.\""]], [["‘I’ve met him,’ said the married lady, with a glance towards Dr Lumbey.", "\"저도 그분을 만나 봤어요.\" 결혼한 부인이 럼비 선생 쪽을 흘긋 보며 말했어."]], [["‘It’s naterally very gratifying to my feelings as a father, to see such a man as that, a kissing and taking notice of my children,’ pursued Mr Kenwigs.", "\"그런 분이 제 아이들한테 입을 맞추고 귀여워해 주는 걸 보면, 아비 된 제 마음으로선 당연히 더없이 흐뭇하지요.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 말을 이었어."], ["‘It’s naterally very gratifying to my feelings as a man, to know that man.", "\"그런 분을 안다는 것만으로도 사내로서 제 마음은 당연히 더없이 흐뭇하고요."], ["It will be naterally very gratifying to my feelings as a husband, to make that man acquainted with this ewent.’", "그리고 그분께 이번 일을 알려 드리는 건 남편 된 제 마음으로선 당연히 더없이 흐뭇한 일이 될 겁니다.\""]], [["Having delivered his sentiments in this form of words, Mr. Kenwigs arranged his second daughter’s flaxen tail, and bade her be a good girl and mind what her sister, Morleena, said.", "이런 식으로 자기 심정을 다 늘어놓은 다음, 케이크위그스 씨는 둘째 딸의 아마빛 땋은 머리를 매만지고는, 착한 아이가 되어서 언니 몰리나가 하는 말을 잘 들으라고 일렀어."]], [["‘That girl grows more like her mother every day,’ said Mr.", "\"저 아이는 날이 갈수록 제 엄마를 더 닮아 가네요.\""], ["Lumbey, suddenly stricken with an enthusiastic admiration of Morleena.", "럼비 씨가 갑자기 몰리나에게 열렬한 감탄이 솟구친 듯 말했어."]], [["‘There!’ rejoined the married lady. ‘What I always say; what I always did say! She’s the very picter of her.’ Having thus directed the general attention to the young lady in question, the married lady embraced the opportunity of taking another sip of the brandy-and-water--and a pretty long sip too.", "\"거 봐요!\" 결혼한 부인이 맞장구를 쳤어. \"제가 늘 하는 말이에요. 늘 해 왔던 말이고요! 저 아이는 엄마를 빼다 박았다니까요.\" 이렇게 모두의 시선을 그 아가씨에게 쏠리게 한 다음, 결혼한 부인은 이 틈을 타 브랜디 탄 물을 한 모금 더 마셨어. 그것도 꽤나 긴 한 모금을 말이야."]], [["‘Yes!", "\"맞아요!"], ["there is a likeness,’ said Mr.", "닮은 데가 있죠.\""], ["Kenwigs, after some reflection.", "케이크위그스 씨가 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 말했어."], ["‘But such a woman as Mrs. Kenwigs was, afore she was married!", "\"하지만 케이크위그스 부인이 시집오기 전에 어땠는지!"], ["Good gracious, such a woman!’", "세상에, 그런 여자가 또 없었다니까요!\""]], [["Mr. Lumbey shook his head with great solemnity, as though to imply that he supposed she must have been rather a dazzler.", "럼비 씨는 그녀가 어지간히 눈부신 미인이었나 보다 하는 듯이, 아주 엄숙하게 고개를 가로저었어."]], [["‘Talk of fairies!’ cried Mr.", "\"요정 얘기를 한다지만!\" 케이크위그스 씨가 외쳤어."], ["Kenwigs ‘I never see anybody so light to be alive, never.", "\"그렇게 사뿐사뿐 가벼운 사람은 살아생전 본 적이 없어요, 한 번도."], ["Such manners too; so playful, and yet so sewerely proper!", "게다가 그 몸가짐이라니, 그렇게 발랄하면서도 또 그렇게 빈틈없이 점잖았다니까요!"], ["As for her figure!", "그 자태로 말하자면!"], ["It isn’t generally known,’ said Mr.", "널리 알려진 일은 아니지만,\""], ["Kenwigs, dropping his voice; ‘but her figure was such, at that time, that the sign of the Britannia, over in the Holloway Road, was painted from it!’", "케이크위그스 씨가 목소리를 낮추며 말했어. \"그 시절 그 사람 자태가 얼마나 멋졌던지, 홀로웨이 로드 건너편 브리타니아 간판 그림을 그 사람을 본떠서 그렸을 정도라니까요!\""]], [["‘But only see what it is now,’ urged the married lady.", "\"하지만 지금은 어떤지 좀 보세요.\" 결혼한 부인이 다그쳤어."], ["‘Does SHE look like the mother of six?’", "\"저 사람이 여섯 아이의 엄마처럼 보이나요?\""]], [["‘Quite ridiculous,’ cried the doctor.", "\"말도 안 되죠.\" 의사가 외쳤어."]], [["‘She looks a deal more like her own daughter,’ said the married lady.", "\"오히려 제 딸이라고 해도 믿겠어요.\" 결혼한 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘So she does,’ assented Mr.", "\"그렇고말고요.\" 럼비 씨가 동의했어."], ["Lumbey. ‘A great deal more.’", "\"훨씬 더 그렇죠.\""]], [["Mr. Kenwigs was about to make some further observations, most probably in confirmation of this opinion, when another married lady, who had looked in to keep up Mrs.", "케이크위그스 씨가 이 의견에 힘을 보태려는 듯 뭔가 더 말을 보태려는 참이었어."], ["Kenwigs’s spirits, and help to clear off anything in the eating and drinking way that might be going about, put in her head to announce that she had just been down to answer the bell, and that there was a gentleman at the door who wanted to see Mr.", "그때 또 다른 결혼한 부인이 — 케이크위그스 부인의 기운을 북돋우고, 또 돌아다니는 먹을 것 마실 것을 좀 치우는 걸 거들려고 들렀던 사람이지 — 머리를 들이밀고는, 자기가 방금 아래로 내려가 초인종에 응대했는데, 문 앞에 케이크위그스 씨를 \"꼭 좀\""], ["Kenwigs ‘most particular.’", "만나고 싶어 하는 신사 한 분이 와 있다고 알렸어."]], [["Shadowy visions of his distinguished relation flitted through the brain of Mr.", "이 전갈을 듣자, 케이크위그스 씨의 머릿속에는 그 훌륭한 친척의 어렴풋한 모습이 어른거렸어."], ["Kenwigs, as this message was delivered; and under their influence, he dispatched Morleena to show the gentleman up straightway.", "그 영향을 받아서, 케이크위그스 씨는 당장 그 신사를 올려 보내라고 몰리나를 보냈지."]], [["‘Why, I do declare,’ said Mr.", "\"아니, 이거 참.\""], ["Kenwigs, standing opposite the door so as to get the earliest glimpse of the visitor, as he came upstairs, ‘it’s Mr.", "케이크위그스 씨는 손님이 계단을 올라올 때 제일 먼저 얼굴을 볼 수 있도록 문 맞은편에 서서 말했어. \"존슨 씨 아니십니까!"], ["Johnson! How do you find yourself, sir?’", "어떻게 지내셨어요, 선생님?\""]], [["Nicholas shook hands, kissed his old pupils all round, intrusted a large parcel of toys to the guardianship of Morleena, bowed to the doctor and the married ladies, and inquired after Mrs.", "니콜라스는 악수를 하고, 옛 제자들에게 차례로 입을 맞추고는, 커다란 장난감 꾸러미 하나를 몰리나에게 맡겼어. 그리고 의사와 결혼한 부인들에게 인사를 하고는, 케이크위그스 부인의 안부를 진심 어린 어조로 물었어."], ["Kenwigs in a tone of interest, which went to the very heart and soul of the nurse, who had come in to warm some mysterious compound, in a little saucepan over the fire.", "그 어조가 어찌나 살뜰했던지, 마침 작은 냄비에 무슨 알 수 없는 약을 데우러 난로 앞에 와 있던 산파의 마음 깊은 곳까지 울렸지."]], [["‘I ought to make a hundred apologies to you for calling at such a season,’ said Nicholas, ‘but I was not aware of it until I had rung the bell, and my time is so fully occupied now, that I feared it might be some days before I could possibly come again.’", "\"이런 때에 찾아와서 백번이라도 사과드려야 마땅하지요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그런데 초인종을 누르고 나서야 사정을 알았답니다. 게다가 요즘은 제가 시간이 너무 빠듯해서, 다시 들르려면 며칠은 걸릴까 봐 걱정이 됐거든요.\""]], [["‘No time like the present, sir,’ said Mr. Kenwigs.", "\"지금만 한 때가 또 어디 있겠습니까, 선생님.\""], ["‘The sitiwation of Mrs Kenwigs, sir, is no obstacle to a little conversation between you and me, I hope?’", "케이크위그스 씨가 말했어. \"케이크위그스 부인의 처지가, 선생님과 저 사이에 잠깐 이야기 나누는 데에 걸림돌이 되지는 않겠지요?\""]], [["‘You are very good,’ said Nicholas.", "\"참 너그러우십니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["At this juncture, proclamation was made by another married lady, that the baby had begun to eat like anything; whereupon the two married ladies, already mentioned, rushed tumultuously into the bedroom to behold him in the act.", "바로 그때, 또 다른 결혼한 부인이 아기가 무섭게 먹기 시작했다고 외쳤어. 그러자 앞서 말한 두 결혼한 부인이 그 광경을 보겠다고 우르르 침실로 몰려갔지."]], [["‘The fact is,’ resumed Nicholas, ‘that before I left the country, where I have been for some time past, I undertook to deliver a message to you.’", "\"사실은요.\" 니콜라스가 말을 이었어. \"제가 한동안 시골에 가 있었는데, 거길 떠나기 전에 선생님께 전갈 하나를 전해 드리기로 했거든요.\""]], [["‘Ay, ay?’ said Mr.", "\"아, 그래요?\""], ["Kenwigs.", "케이크위그스 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘And I have been,’ added Nicholas, ‘already in town for some days, without having had an opportunity of doing so.’", "\"그런데 제가요,\" 니콜라스가 덧붙였어. \"벌써 며칠 전부터 시내에 와 있으면서도 그럴 기회가 통 없었네요.\""]], [["‘It’s no matter, sir,’ said Mr. Kenwigs.", "\"괜찮습니다, 선생님.\""], ["‘I dare say it’s none the worse for keeping cold.", "케이크위그스 씨가 말했어. \"좀 묵힌다고 나빠질 것도 없겠죠, 뭐."], ["Message from the country!’ said Mr.", "시골에서 온 전갈이라!\""], ["Kenwigs, ruminating; ‘that’s curious.", "케이크위그스 씨가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했어."], ["I don’t know anybody in the country.’", "\"그것참 묘하네요. 저는 시골에 아는 사람이 하나도 없는데요.\""]], [["‘Miss Petowker,’ suggested Nicholas.", "\"피토커 양 말입니다.\" 니콜라스가 일러 줬어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["from her, is it?’ said Mr.", "그 사람한테서 온 거로군요?\" 케이크위그스 씨가 말했어."], ["Kenwigs. ‘Oh dear, yes.", "\"아이고, 그렇죠."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["Mrs. Kenwigs will be glad to hear from her.", "케이크위그스 부인이 그 사람 소식을 들으면 반가워할 거예요."], ["Henrietta Petowker, eh?", "헨리에타 피토커 말이죠, 그렇죠?"], ["How odd things come about, now!", "세상일이 참 묘하게도 풀리네요!"], ["That you should have met her in the country!", "선생님이 시골에서 그 사람을 만나다니!"], ["Well!’", "거참!\""]], [["Hearing this mention of their old friend’s name, the four Miss Kenwigses gathered round Nicholas, open eyed and mouthed, to hear more.", "옛 친구의 이름이 나오는 걸 듣자, 케이크위그스 집안의 네 자매가 눈과 입을 떡 벌리고 니콜라스 둘레로 모여들어 더 들어 보려 했어."], ["Mr. Kenwigs looked a little curious too, but quite comfortable and unsuspecting.", "케이크위그스 씨도 조금 궁금한 표정이었지만, 아주 편안하고 아무 의심도 없는 얼굴이었지."]], [["‘The message relates to family matters,’ said Nicholas, hesitating.", "\"이 전갈은 집안일에 관한 겁니다.\" 니콜라스가 머뭇거리며 말했어."]], [["‘Oh, never mind,’ said Kenwigs, glancing at Mr.", "\"오, 괜찮아요.\" 케이크위그스 씨가 럼비 씨를 흘긋 보며 말했어."], ["Lumbey, who, having rashly taken charge of little Lillyvick, found nobody disposed to relieve him of his precious burden.", "럼비 씨는 경솔하게 어린 릴리빅을 떠맡았다가, 그 소중한 짐을 대신 받아 줄 사람을 아무도 찾지 못한 참이었지."], ["‘All friends here.’", "\"여긴 다 친구들뿐이니까요.\""]], [["Nicholas hemmed once or twice, and seemed to have some difficulty in proceeding.", "니콜라스는 한두 번 헛기침을 했어. 그러고는 말을 잇기가 좀 어려운 모양이었지."]], [["‘At Portsmouth, Henrietta Petowker is,’ observed Mr.", "\"헨리에타 피토커는 포츠머스에 있지요.\""], ["Kenwigs.", "케이크위그스 씨가 한마디 했어."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Nicholas, ‘Mr.", "\"네.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["Lillyvick is there.’", "\"릴리빅 씨도 거기 계세요.\""]], [["Mr.", "케이크위그스 씨의 얼굴이 새파래졌어."], ["Kenwigs turned pale, but he recovered, and said, THAT was an odd coincidence also.", "하지만 그는 곧 정신을 추스르고는, 그것도 묘한 우연이라고 말했어."]], [["‘The message is from him,’ said Nicholas.", "\"전갈은 그분에게서 온 겁니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["Mr. Kenwigs appeared to revive.", "케이크위그스 씨는 다시 살아난 듯했어."], ["He knew that his niece was in a delicate state, and had, no doubt, sent word that they were to forward full particulars.", "그는 자기 조카딸이 산달이 가까운 걸 알고 있었으니, 그분이 분명 자세한 사정을 알려 달라고 기별을 보낸 거라고 여겼지."], ["Yes.", "그래."], ["That was very kind of him; so like him too!", "정말 친절한 분이야. 그분답기도 하고!"]], [["‘He desired me to give his kindest love,’ said Nicholas.", "\"그분이 저더러 가장 다정한 안부를 전해 달라고 하셨어요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Very much obliged to him, I’m sure.", "\"정말 고마운 분이지요."], ["Your great-uncle, Lillyvick, my dears!’ interposed Mr.", "너희들의 작은할아버지인 릴리빅 어른이시란다, 얘들아!\""], ["Kenwigs, condescendingly explaining it to the children.", "케이크위그스 씨가 자못 너그럽게 끼어들어 아이들에게 설명해 줬어."]], [["‘His kindest love,’ resumed Nicholas; ‘and to say that he had no time to write, but that he was married to Miss Petowker.’", "\"가장 다정한 안부를 전한다고요.\" 니콜라스가 말을 이었어. \"그리고 편지 쓸 짬이 없었지만, 자기가 피토커 양과 결혼했다는 말을 전해 달라고 하셨습니다.\""]], [["Mr.", "케이크위그스 씨는 돌처럼 굳어진 눈으로 자리에서 벌떡 일어났어."], ["Kenwigs started from his seat with a petrified stare, caught his second daughter by her flaxen tail, and covered his face with his pocket-handkerchief.", "그러고는 둘째 딸의 아마빛 땋은 머리를 움켜쥐고는, 손수건으로 얼굴을 가렸지."], ["Morleena fell, all stiff and rigid, into the baby’s chair, as she had seen her mother fall when she fainted away, and the two remaining little Kenwigses shrieked in affright.", "몰리나는 어머니가 기절할 때 쓰러지던 모습을 본 그대로, 온몸을 뻣뻣하게 굳히고 아기 의자에 풀썩 쓰러졌어. 그리고 남은 두 어린 케이크위그스는 놀라서 비명을 질렀지."]], [["‘My children, my defrauded, swindled infants!’ cried Mr.", "\"내 자식들, 사기당하고 등쳐 먹힌 내 어린것들!\""], ["Kenwigs, pulling so hard, in his vehemence, at the flaxen tail of his second daughter, that he lifted her up on tiptoe, and kept her, for some seconds, in that attitude.", "케이크위그스 씨가 흥분에 못 이겨 둘째 딸의 아마빛 땋은 머리를 어찌나 세게 잡아당겼던지, 아이가 발끝으로 들려 올라가 몇 초 동안 그대로 매달려 있었어."], ["‘Villain, ass, traitor!’", "\"악당, 멍청이, 배신자 같으니!\""]], [["‘Drat the man!’ cried the nurse, looking angrily around.", "\"이 양반이 정말!\" 산파가 화가 나서 둘러보며 외쳤어."], ["‘What does he mean by making that noise here?’", "\"여기서 이런 소동을 피우면 어쩌자는 거예요?\""]], [["‘Silence, woman!’ said Mr.", "\"조용히 하시오, 여편네!\""], ["Kenwigs, fiercely.", "케이크위그스 씨가 사납게 말했어."]], [["‘I won’t be silent,’ returned the nurse.", "\"조용히 안 할 거예요.\" 산파가 받아쳤어."], ["‘Be silent yourself, you wretch.", "\"당신이나 조용히 해요, 이 못된 양반아."], ["Have you no regard for your baby?’", "당신 아기는 안중에도 없어요?\""]], [["‘No!’ returned Mr.", "\"없소!\""], ["Kenwigs.", "케이크위그스 씨가 대답했어."]], [["‘More shame for you,’ retorted the nurse.", "\"그러니 더 부끄러운 줄 아세요.\" 산파가 쏘아붙였어."], ["‘Ugh!", "\"흥!"], ["you unnatural monster.’", "이 인정머리 없는 괴물 같으니.\""]], [["‘Let him die,’ cried Mr.", "\"죽으라 그래.\""], ["Kenwigs, in the torrent of his wrath.", "케이크위그스 씨가 노여움이 쏟아져 나오는 와중에 외쳤어."], ["‘Let him die!", "\"죽으라 그래!"], ["He has no expectations, no property to come into.", "그 녀석은 이제 기대할 것도 없고, 물려받을 재산도 없어."], ["We want no babies here,’ said Mr.", "우리한텐 아기 따위 필요 없어.\""], ["Kenwigs recklessly.", "케이크위그스 씨가 막무가내로 말했어."], ["‘Take ‘em away, take ‘em away to the Fondling!’", "\"데려가, 다 데려가, 고아원으로 데려가 버려!\""]], [["With these awful remarks, Mr. Kenwigs sat himself down in a chair, and defied the nurse, who made the best of her way into the adjoining room, and returned with a stream of matrons: declaring that Mr.", "이 끔찍한 말을 내뱉고는, 케이크위그스 씨는 의자에 털썩 앉아 산파한테 어디 한번 해 보라는 듯이 버텼어."], ["Kenwigs had spoken blasphemy against his family, and must be raving mad.", "산파는 부랴부랴 옆방으로 들어가더니, 부인네들을 줄줄이 몰고 돌아왔지. 케이크위그스 씨가 자기 식구한테 못된 막말을 퍼부었으니, 분명 미쳐서 헛소리를 하는 게 틀림없다고 떠들면서 말이야."]], [["Appearances were certainly not in Mr.", "케이크위그스 씨에게 유리한 정황은 분명 아니었어."], ["Kenwigs’s favour, for the exertion of speaking with so much vehemence, and yet in such a tone as should prevent his lamentations reaching the ears of Mrs.", "그렇게 격하게 말을 쏟아 내면서도, 그 한탄이 케이크위그스 부인의 귀에 들어가지 않도록 목소리를 누르느라 애쓰다 보니, 그의 얼굴이 시커멓게 변해 버렸거든."], ["Kenwigs, had made him very black in the face; besides which, the excitement of the occasion, and an unwonted indulgence in various strong cordials to celebrate it, had swollen and dilated his features to a most unusual extent. But, Nicholas and the doctor--who had been passive at first, doubting very much whether Mr.", "게다가 이 일로 한껏 들떠서, 그걸 자축하느라 평소 안 마시던 독한 술을 이것저것 들이켠 탓에, 얼굴이 영 보기 드물게 퉁퉁 부어오르고 벌게져 있었지."], ["Kenwigs could be in earnest--interfering to explain the immediate cause of his condition, the indignation of the matrons was changed to pity, and they implored him, with much feeling, to go quietly to bed.", "하지만 니콜라스와 의사는 — 처음엔 케이크위그스 씨가 진심인지 어떤지 영 의심스러워 가만히 있었지만 — 그가 이렇게 된 직접적인 까닭을 설명하려고 끼어들었어. 그러자 부인네들의 분노는 동정으로 바뀌었고, 그들은 그에게 자뜩 마음을 담아, 조용히 가서 자라고 사정했지."]], [["‘The attention,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 베푼 정성이라니,\""], ["Kenwigs, looking around with a plaintive air, ‘the attention that I’ve shown to that man!", "케이크위그스 씨가 처량한 표정으로 둘러보며 말했어. \"그 인간한테 베푼 정성이라니!"], ["The hyseters he has eat, and the pints of ale he has drank, in this house--!’", "이 집에서 그자가 먹어 치운 굴이며, 들이켠 에일이 몇 파인트인데—!\""]], [["‘It’s very trying, and very hard to bear, we know,’ said one of the married ladies; ‘but think of your dear darling wife.’", "\"정말 견디기 힘들고 참기 어려운 일이라는 거, 우리도 알아요.\" 결혼한 부인 중 하나가 말했어. \"하지만 사랑하는 부인을 생각하세요.\""]], [["‘Oh yes, and what she’s been a undergoing of, only this day,’ cried a great many voices.", "\"오 그럼요, 그것도 바로 오늘 그 사람이 겪은 고생을 생각해 보세요.\" 여러 사람이 한꺼번에 외쳤어."], ["‘There’s a good man, do.’", "\"착하시니까, 좀 그러세요.\""]], [["‘The presents that have been made to him,’ said Mr.", "\"그 인간한테 안겨 준 선물이라니,\""], ["Kenwigs, reverting to his calamity, ‘the pipes, the snuff-boxes--a pair of india-rubber goloshes, that cost six-and-six--’", "케이크위그스 씨가 다시 자기 비탄으로 돌아가 말했어. \"그 파이프며, 코담배 통이며, 육 실링 육 펜스나 주고 산 고무 덧신 한 켤레까지—\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아! 정말이지 생각만 해도 못 견딜 일이에요.\""], ["it won’t bear thinking of, indeed,’ cried the matrons generally; ‘but it’ll all come home to him, never fear.’", "부인네들이 다 같이 외쳤어. \"하지만 그 인간한테도 죄다 되돌아갈 거예요, 걱정 마세요.\""]], [["Mr.", "케이크위그스 씨는 부인네들을 어두운 눈빛으로 바라봤어."], ["Kenwigs looked darkly upon the ladies, as if he would prefer its all coming home to HIM, as there was nothing to be got by it; but he said nothing, and resting his head upon his hand, subsided into a kind of doze.", "그래 봤자 자기한테 떨어질 게 하나도 없으니, 차라리 그게 다 자기한테 되돌아오는 편이 낫겠다는 듯이 말이야. 하지만 그는 아무 말도 않고, 손에 머리를 기댄 채 일종의 선잠 속으로 가라앉아 버렸어."]], [["Then, the matrons again expatiated on the expediency of taking the good gentleman to bed; observing that he would be better tomorrow, and that they knew what was the wear and tear of some men’s minds when their wives were taken as Mrs.", "그러자 부인네들은 다시, 이 착한 신사를 침대로 데려가는 게 좋겠다고 늘어놓았어. 내일이면 나아질 거라고, 또 케이크위그스 부인처럼 아내가 그날 그런 일을 겪을 때 어떤 남자들의 속이 얼마나 닳고 시달리는지 자기들도 안다고 말이야."], ["Kenwigs had been that day, and that it did him great credit, and there was nothing to be ashamed of in it; far from it; they liked to see it, they did, for it showed a good heart.", "그리고 이건 그가 아주 갸륵한 거지, 부끄러워할 일이 결코 아니라고, 천만에, 오히려 보기 좋다고, 정말 보기 좋다고, 그건 마음이 착하다는 증거라고들 했지. 그리고 한 부인은 지금 이 경우에 딱 맞는 사례라며 이렇게 말했어."], ["And one lady observed, as a case bearing upon the present, that her husband was often quite light-headed from anxiety on similar occasions, and that once, when her little Johnny was born, it was nearly a week before he came to himself again, during the whole of which time he did nothing but cry ‘Is it a boy, is it a boy?’ in a manner which went to the hearts of all his hearers.", "자기 남편도 비슷한 일이 있을 때면 곧잘 걱정에 정신을 못 차린다고, 그래서 한번은 자기 어린 조니가 태어났을 때 거의 일주일이 지나서야 제정신으로 돌아왔는데, 그동안 내내 \"아들이오, 아들이오?\" 하고만 외쳐 댔다고, 듣는 사람들 마음을 다 울릴 만큼 그랬다고 말이야."]], [["At length, Morleena (who quite forgot she had fainted, when she found she was not noticed) announced that a chamber was ready for her afflicted parent; and Mr.", "마침내 몰리나가 — 아무도 자기를 알아봐 주지 않자 기절했던 일은 까맣게 잊어버린 채로 — 시름에 잠긴 아버지를 위해 방이 준비되었다고 알렸어."], ["Kenwigs, having partially smothered his four daughters in the closeness of his embrace, accepted the doctor’s arm on one side, and the support of Nicholas on the other, and was conducted upstairs to a bedroom which been secured for the occasion.", "케이크위그스 씨는 네 딸을 품에 너무 꼭 끌어안아 거의 숨이 막힐 지경으로 만든 다음, 한쪽으로는 의사의 팔에 기대고 다른 한쪽으로는 니콜라스의 부축을 받으며, 이번 일을 위해 마련해 둔 침실로 이 층으로 인도되어 갔지."]], [["Having seen him sound asleep, and heard him snore most satisfactorily, and having further presided over the distribution of the toys, to the perfect contentment of all the little Kenwigses, Nicholas took his leave.", "니콜라스는 그가 푹 잠든 걸 확인하고, 더없이 만족스럽게 코를 고는 소리까지 듣고는, 거기다 장난감 나눠 주는 일까지 도맡아 어린 케이크위그스들이 모두 흡족해하는 걸 본 뒤에야 작별을 고했어."], ["The matrons dropped off one by one, with the exception of six or eight particular friends, who had determined to stop all night; the lights in the houses gradually disappeared; the last bulletin was issued that Mrs.", "부인네들은 하나둘씩 떠나갔지만, 밤새 묵기로 작정한 각별한 친구 예닐곱 명만은 남았지. 집집마다 불빛이 차츰 사라졌고, 케이크위그스 부인이 더 바랄 수 없을 만큼 잘 있다는 마지막 소식이 전해졌어."], ["Kenwigs was as well as could be expected; and the whole family were left to their repose.", "그러고는 온 식구가 편히 쉬게 되었지."]], [["# CHAPTER 37", "# CHAPTER 37"]], [["Nicholas finds further Favour in the Eyes of the brothers Cheeryble and Mr. Timothy Linkinwater.", "치어리블 형제의 회계 사무소가 자리 잡은 그 광장은, 팀 링킨워터가 침이 마르도록 늘어놓는 찬사를 듣고 낯선 사람이 잔뜩 부풀려 그려 볼 법한 기대를 다 채워 주지는 못했어."], ["The brothers give a Banquet on a great Annual Occasion. Nicholas, on returning Home from it, receives a mysterious and important Disclosure from the Lips of Mrs.", "그래도 런던 같은 분주한 도시 한복판에 있는, 제법 그럴듯하고 아늑한 구석이긴 했지."], ["Nickleby\nThe square in which the counting-house of the brothers Cheeryble was situated, although it might not wholly realise the very sanguine expectations which a stranger would be disposed to form on hearing the fervent encomiums bestowed upon it by Tim Linkinwater, was, nevertheless, a sufficiently desirable nook in the heart of a busy town like London, and one which occupied a high place in the affectionate remembrances of several grave persons domiciled in the neighbourhood, whose recollections, however, dated from a much more recent period, and whose attachment to the spot was far less absorbing, than were the recollections and attachment of the enthusiastic Tim.", "그리고 그 근처에 사는 점잖은 사람들 몇몇은 그곳을 애틋하게 기억하고 있었어. 다만 그 사람들의 기억은 훨씬 더 최근에 생긴 것이었고, 그곳을 향한 애착도 열성적인 팀의 기억과 애착에 비하면 한참 덜한 것이었지만 말이야."]], [["And let not those whose eyes have been accustomed to the aristocratic gravity of Grosvenor Square and Hanover Square, the dowager barrenness and frigidity of Fitzroy Square, or the gravel walks and garden seats of the Squares of Russell and Euston, suppose that the affections of Tim Linkinwater, or the inferior lovers of this particular locality, had been awakened and kept alive by any refreshing associations with leaves, however dingy, or grass, however bare and thin.", "그로스베너 광장이나 하노버 광장의 귀족적인 무게감, 피츠로이 광장의 과부 같은 황량함과 쌀쌀맞음, 러셀 광장과 유스턴 광장의 자갈길과 정원 벤치에 익숙해진 사람들이라면, 팀 링킨워터나 이 동네를 그보다 덜 사랑하는 사람들의 애정이 잎사귀나 잔디 같은 것과의 정겨운 인연으로 깨어나 이어졌으리라고 생각하지는 말아야 해. 아무리 칙칙한 잎사귀라도, 아무리 헐벗고 듬성듬성한 잔디라도 말이야."], ["The city square has no enclosure, save the lamp-post in the middle: and no grass, but the weeds which spring up round its base.", "도시의 광장에는 한가운데에 가로등 기둥 말고는 따로 둘러친 울타리도 없고, 그 기둥 발치에 돋아난 잡초 말고는 잔디라고 할 것도 없거든. 거기는 조용하고 사람 발길이 드문, 외진 곳이야."], ["It is a quiet, little-frequented, retired spot, favourable to melancholy and contemplation, and appointments of long-waiting; and up and down its every side the Appointed saunters idly by the hour together wakening the echoes with the monotonous sound of his footsteps on the smooth worn stones, and counting, first the windows, and then the very bricks of the tall silent houses that hem him round about.", "우울과 사색에, 그리고 오래 기다리는 약속에 딱 어울리는 곳이지. 그래서 약속한 사람은 광장의 사면을 위아래로 한 시간씩 멍하니 어슬렁거려."], ["In winter-time, the snow will linger there, long after it has melted from the busy streets and highways.", "반들반들 닳은 돌 위로 단조롭게 울리는 제 발소리로 메아리를 깨우면서, 처음엔 창문을, 그다음엔 자기를 빙 둘러싼 키 큰 침묵의 집들 벽돌까지 하나하나 세어 가면서 말이야."], ["The summer’s sun holds it in some respect, and while he darts his cheerful rays sparingly into the square, keeps his fiery heat and glare for noisier and less-imposing precincts.", "겨울이면 분주한 거리와 큰길에서는 진작 녹아 버린 눈이 거기서는 오래오래 남아 있어. 여름 해도 그곳만큼은 좀 봐주는지, 광장 안으로는 밝은 햇살을 아껴서 비추고, 제 불타는 열기와 눈부심은 더 시끄럽고 덜 점잖은 동네에 쏟아붓거든."], ["It is so quiet, that you can almost hear the ticking of your own watch when you stop to cool in its refreshing atmosphere.", "어찌나 조용한지, 그 상쾌한 공기 속에서 더위를 식히려 걸음을 멈추면 제 회중시계 똑딱이는 소리까지 거의 들릴 정도야. 멀리서 웅웅거리는 소리가 들리긴 해."], ["There is a distant hum--of coaches, not of insects--but no other sound disturbs the stillness of the square.", "벌레 소리가 아니라 마차 소리지. 하지만 그 말고는 어떤 소리도 광장의 고요를 깨뜨리지 않아."], ["The ticket porter leans idly against the post at the corner: comfortably warm, but not hot, although the day is broiling.", "짐꾼은 모퉁이 기둥에 한가로이 기대 있어. 푹푹 찌는 날인데도 그는 덥지 않고 그저 알맞게 따뜻하기만 하지."], ["His white apron flaps languidly in the air, his head gradually droops upon his breast, he takes very long winks with both eyes at once; even he is unable to withstand the soporific influence of the place, and is gradually falling asleep.", "그의 하얀 앞치마는 공중에서 나른하게 펄럭이고, 고개는 차츰 가슴께로 떨어지고, 두 눈을 한꺼번에 아주 길게 깜빡거려. 그조차도 이곳의 졸음을 부르는 기운을 견디지 못하고 차츰 잠에 빠져드는 거야."], ["But now, he starts into full wakefulness, recoils a step or two, and gazes out before him with eager wildness in his eye.", "그런데 갑자기 그가 화들짝 깨어나 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러서더니, 눈에 사납고 열띤 빛을 띠고 앞을 뚫어져라 바라봐. 일감이 생긴 건가, 아니면 구슬치기하는 아이가 나타난 건가?"], ["Is it a job, or a boy at marbles?", "유령이라도 본 건가, 풍금 소리라도 들은 건가?"], ["Does he see a ghost, or hear an organ?", "아니야. 그보다 더 보기 드문 광경이지."], ["No; sight more unwonted still--there is a butterfly in the square--a real, live butterfly!", "광장에 나비가 한 마리 있는 거야. 진짜로 살아 있는 나비가!"], ["astray from flowers and sweets, and fluttering among the iron heads of the dusty area railings.", "꽃과 단것들에서 길을 잃고, 먼지 낀 지하층 난간의 쇠창살 머리 사이를 팔랑팔랑 날아다니는 거지."]], [["But if there were not many matters immediately without the doors of Cheeryble Brothers, to engage the attention or distract the thoughts of the young clerk, there were not a few within, to interest and amuse him. There was scarcely an object in the place, animate or inanimate, which did not partake in some degree of the scrupulous method and punctuality of Mr. Timothy Linkinwater. Punctual as the counting-house dial, which he maintained to be the best time-keeper in London next after the clock of some old, hidden, unknown church hard by, (for Tim held the fabled goodness of that at the Horse Guards to be a pleasant fiction, invented by jealous West-enders,) the old clerk performed the minutest actions of the day, and arranged the minutest articles in the little room, in a precise and regular order, which could not have been exceeded if it had actually been a real glass case, fitted with the choicest curiosities. Paper, pens, ink, ruler, sealing-wax, wafers, pounce-box, string-box, fire-box, Tim’s hat, Tim’s scrupulously-folded gloves, Tim’s other coat--looking precisely like a back view of himself as it hung against the wall--all had their accustomed inches of space. Except the clock, there was not such an accurate and unimpeachable instrument in existence as the little thermometer which hung behind the door. There was not a bird of such methodical and business-like habits in all the world, as the blind blackbird, who dreamed and dozed away his days in a large snug cage, and had lost his voice, from old age, years before Tim first bought him. There was not such an eventful story in the whole range of anecdote, as Tim could tell concerning the acquisition of that very bird; how, compassionating his starved and suffering condition, he had purchased him, with the view of humanely terminating his wretched life; how he determined to wait three days and see whether the bird revived; how, before half the time was out, the bird did revive; and how he went on reviving and picking up his appetite and good looks until he gradually became what--‘what you see him now, sir,’--Tim would say, glancing proudly at the cage. And with that, Tim would utter a melodious chirrup, and cry ‘Dick;’ and Dick, who, for any sign of life he had previously given, might have been a wooden or stuffed representation of a blackbird indifferently executed, would come to the side of the cage in three small jumps, and, thrusting his bill between the bars, turn his sightless head towards his old master--and at that moment it would be very difficult to determine which of the two was the happier, the bird or Tim Linkinwater.", "하지만 치어리블 형제의 사무소 문밖에는 젊은 사무원의 눈길을 끌거나 생각을 흩어 놓을 만한 일이 그리 많지 않았어도, 문 안에는 그의 흥미를 돋우고 즐겁게 해 줄 것이 적지 않았어. 그곳에는 살아 있는 것이든 죽어 있는 것이든, 티머시 링킨워터 씨의 꼼꼼한 질서와 정확함을 어느 정도 닮지 않은 물건이 거의 없었거든. 그 늙은 사무원은 사무소 해시계만큼이나 정확했어. 그는 그 해시계가 런던에서 가장 정확한 시계라고 우겼지. 단, 근처에 숨어 있는, 아무도 모르는 어느 오래된 교회 시계 다음으로 말이야. (팀은 호스 가즈 시계가 좋다는 소문을, 시샘 많은 웨스트엔드 사람들이 지어낸 유쾌한 헛소리라고 여겼거든.) 그 늙은 사무원은 하루의 가장 자질구레한 행동 하나하나를, 또 그 작은 방의 가장 자질구레한 물건 하나하나를, 정확하고 규칙적인 순서대로 처리하고 정돈했어. 그 방이 정말로 진귀한 골동품을 넣어 둔 유리 진열장이었다 해도 그보다 더 잘 정돈할 수는 없었을 거야. 종이, 펜, 잉크, 자, 봉랍, 봉함지, 가루통, 끈 통, 부싯깃 통, 팀의 모자, 팀의 빈틈없이 개어 놓은 장갑, 팀의 또 다른 외투까지 — 벽에 걸려 있는 모양이 꼭 그의 뒷모습을 그대로 보는 것 같았지 — 모두가 정해진 자리를 한 치도 어김없이 차지하고 있었어. 시계를 빼고는, 문 뒤에 걸린 그 작은 온도계만큼 정확하고 흠잡을 데 없는 기구는 세상에 또 없었지. 그리고 온 세상을 다 뒤져도, 그 눈먼 검은지빠귀만큼 규칙적이고 사무적인 습관을 가진 새는 없었어. 그 새는 크고 아늑한 새장 안에서 꿈꾸듯 졸며 하루하루를 보냈는데, 팀이 처음 사 오기 여러 해 전에 이미 늙어서 목소리를 잃은 상태였어. 그리고 온갖 일화를 다 뒤져 봐도, 팀이 바로 그 새를 얻게 된 사연만큼 파란만장한 이야기는 없었지. 굶주려 고통받는 그 새의 처지가 가여워서, 그 비참한 목숨을 인정 많게 끝내 줄 셈으로 새를 사 왔다는 거야. 그러고는 사흘을 기다려 새가 살아나는지 보기로 마음먹었는데, 절반도 지나기 전에 새가 살아났다는 거지. 그리고 새는 점점 살아나서 입맛을 되찾고 보기 좋아지더니, 마침내 — \"지금 보시는 저 모습이 된 겁니다, 손님.\" 팀은 새장을 자랑스럽게 흘끔거리며 이렇게 말하곤 했어. 그러면서 팀은 곱고 맑은 소리로 짹짹 흉내를 내고는 \"딕!\" 하고 불렀지. 그러면 그때까지는 살아 있다는 기색이라곤 도무지 없어서, 그저 솜씨 없이 깎은 나무 지빠귀나 박제로밖에 안 보이던 딕이, 작은 깡충질 세 번으로 새장 옆으로 다가와서는, 부리를 창살 사이로 쑥 내밀고 앞 못 보는 머리를 옛 주인 쪽으로 돌리는 거야. 그 순간에는 둘 중 누가 더 행복한지, 그 새인지 팀 링킨워터인지 가리기가 참 어려웠어."]], [["Nor was this all.", "이게 다가 아니었어."], ["Everything gave back, besides, some reflection of the kindly spirit of the brothers.", "게다가 모든 것이 형제의 다정한 마음씨를 어느 정도 비추고 있었지."], ["The warehousemen and porters were such sturdy, jolly fellows, that it was a treat to see them.", "창고지기와 짐꾼들은 어찌나 튼튼하고 유쾌한 친구들인지, 보고 있으면 그저 흐뭇했어."], ["Among the shipping announcements and steam-packet lists which decorated the counting-house wall, were designs for almshouses, statements of charities, and plans for new hospitals.", "사무소 벽을 장식한 선박 공고문과 증기선 운항표 사이에는, 양로원 설계도며 자선 사업 명세서며 새 병원 도면이 끼어 있었어."], ["A blunderbuss and two swords hung above the chimney-piece, for the terror of evil-doers, but the blunderbuss was rusty and shattered, and the swords were broken and edgeless.", "벽난로 위에는 나팔총 한 자루와 칼 두 자루가 걸려 있었지. 나쁜 짓 하는 자들을 겁주려는 것이었지만, 나팔총은 녹슬고 부서졌고, 칼은 부러지고 날도 없었어."], ["Elsewhere, their open display in such a condition would have realised a smile; but, there, it seemed as though even violent and offensive weapons partook of the reigning influence, and became emblems of mercy and forbearance.", "다른 데였다면 그런 꼴을 떡하니 걸어 놓은 게 웃음거리가 됐겠지만, 거기서는 그 사납고 위험한 무기들마저 그곳을 다스리는 기운에 물들어, 자비와 너그러움의 상징이 된 것만 같았어."]], [["Such thoughts as these occurred to Nicholas very strongly, on the morning when he first took possession of the vacant stool, and looked about him, more freely and at ease, than he had before enjoyed an opportunity of doing.", "니콜라스는 빈 의자를 처음 차지하고 앉던 날 아침에 이런 생각들이 아주 강하게 떠올랐어. 전에는 누려 보지 못한 여유로움으로 마음 편히 주위를 둘러보면서 말이야."], ["Perhaps they encouraged and stimulated him to exertion, for, during the next two weeks, all his spare hours, late at night and early in the morning, were incessantly devoted to acquiring the mysteries of book-keeping and some other forms of mercantile account.", "어쩌면 그런 생각들이 그를 북돋우고 일하게 만들었는지도 몰라. 그 뒤로 두 주 동안, 늦은 밤이든 이른 아침이든 그의 빈 시간은 모조리 부기와 그 밖의 몇 가지 상업 회계 방식의 비밀을 익히는 데 쉴 새 없이 바쳐졌거든."], ["To these, he applied himself with such steadiness and perseverance that, although he brought no greater amount of previous knowledge to the subject than certain dim recollections of two or three very long sums entered into a ciphering-book at school, and relieved for parental inspection by the effigy of a fat swan tastefully flourished by the writing-master’s own hand, he found himself, at the end of a fortnight, in a condition to report his proficiency to Mr. Linkinwater, and to claim his promise that he, Nicholas Nickleby, should now be allowed to assist him in his graver labours.", "그는 어찌나 꾸준하고 끈질기게 매달렸던지, 사실 그가 가진 사전 지식이라곤 학교 다닐 때 계산 공책에 적던 아주 긴 셈 두세 개에 대한 흐릿한 기억뿐이었는데도 — 그 공책은 글씨 선생이 손수 멋들어지게 그려 넣은 살찐 백조 그림으로 장식되어 부모님 검사를 무사히 통과하곤 했지 — 두 주 만에 링킨워터 씨에게 자신의 실력을 보고하고, 이제 니콜라스 니클비가 그의 더 중요한 일을 거들게 해 주겠다던 약속을 지켜 달라고 청할 만한 수준에 이르렀어."]], [["It was a sight to behold Tim Linkinwater slowly bring out a massive ledger and day-book, and, after turning them over and over, and affectionately dusting their backs and sides, open the leaves here and there, and cast his eyes, half mournfully, half proudly, upon the fair and unblotted entries.", "팀 링킨워터가 묵직한 원장과 일계장을 천천히 꺼내 오는 모습은 한번 볼만했어. 그것들을 이리저리 뒤집어 보고, 등과 옆면의 먼지를 애틋하게 털어 내고는, 여기저기 책장을 펼쳐 보며, 깨끗하고 얼룩 하나 없는 기입 내용을 반은 서글프게 반은 자랑스럽게 바라보는 거야."]], [["‘Four-and-forty year, next May!’ said Tim.", "\"오는 오월이면 사십사 년째지!\" 팀이 말했어."], ["‘Many new ledgers since then.", "\"그 뒤로 새 원장이 참 많기도 했어."], ["Four-and-forty year!’", "사십사 년이라니!\""]], [["Tim closed the book again.", "팀은 다시 책을 덮었어."]], [["‘Come, come,’ said Nicholas, ‘I am all impatience to begin.’", "\"자, 자.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"어서 시작하고 싶어서 좀이 쑤시는걸요.\""]], [["Tim Linkinwater shook his head with an air of mild reproof.", "팀 링킨워터는 부드럽게 나무라는 기색으로 고개를 저었어."], ["Mr. Nickleby was not sufficiently impressed with the deep and awful nature of his undertaking.", "니클비 씨는 이 일이 얼마나 깊고 엄숙한 일인지를 충분히 깨닫지 못하고 있었거든."], ["Suppose there should be any mistake--any scratching out!", "혹시라도 잘못 적기라도 하면 — 그래서 지워 내야 하기라도 하면 어쩔 셈인가!"]], [["Young men are adventurous.", "젊은이는 무모한 법이지."], ["It is extraordinary what they will rush upon, sometimes.", "가끔 그들이 무슨 일에 겁 없이 덤벼드는지 보면 참 놀라워."], ["Without even taking the precaution of sitting himself down upon his stool, but standing leisurely at the desk, and with a smile upon his face--actually a smile--there was no mistake about it; Mr Linkinwater often mentioned it afterwards--Nicholas dipped his pen into the inkstand before him, and plunged into the books of Cheeryble Brothers!", "니콜라스는 의자에 앉는 조심성조차 부리지 않고, 책상 앞에 느긋하게 선 채로, 얼굴에 미소까지 띠고서 — 정말로 미소였어, 그건 틀림없었지, 링킨워터 씨가 나중에 자주 그 얘기를 했거든 — 앞에 놓인 잉크병에 펜을 푹 담그더니, 치어리블 형제의 장부 속으로 곧장 뛰어들었어!"]], [["Tim Linkinwater turned pale, and tilting up his stool on the two legs nearest Nicholas, looked over his shoulder in breathless anxiety.", "팀 링킨워터는 얼굴이 하얗게 질려서, 니콜라스 쪽에 가까운 의자 두 다리를 들어 기우뚱 기대고는, 숨도 못 쉴 만큼 마음을 졸이며 그의 어깨너머를 들여다봤어. 그때 찰스 형과 네드 형이 함께 사무소로 들어왔어."], ["Brother Charles and brother Ned entered the counting-house together; but Tim Linkinwater, without looking round, impatiently waved his hand as a caution that profound silence must be observed, and followed the nib of the inexperienced pen with strained and eager eyes.", "하지만 팀 링킨워터는 돌아보지도 않고 손을 다급하게 내저어, 쥐 죽은 듯 조용히 해야 한다고 신호를 보냈어. 그러고는 그 서툰 펜촉의 움직임을 한껏 긴장한 간절한 눈으로 좇았지."]], [["The brothers looked on with smiling faces, but Tim Linkinwater smiled not, nor moved for some minutes.", "형제는 웃는 얼굴로 지켜봤지만, 팀 링킨워터는 몇 분 동안 웃지도, 움직이지도 않았어. 마침내 그는 길고 느리게 숨을 한 번 내쉬고는, 여전히 기우뚱 기댄 의자 위에서 자세를 그대로 유지한 채, 찰스 형을 흘끔 보더니, 펜 깃털로 니콜라스를 슬쩍 가리키고는, 진지하고 단호하게 고개를 끄덕였어."], ["At length, he drew a long slow breath, and still maintaining his position on the tilted stool, glanced at brother Charles, secretly pointed with the feather of his pen towards Nicholas, and nodded his head in a grave and resolute manner, plainly signifying ‘He’ll do.’", "\"이 친구, 되겠어.\" 하고 말하는 게 분명했지."]], [["Brother Charles nodded again, and exchanged a laughing look with brother Ned; but, just then, Nicholas stopped to refer to some other page, and Tim Linkinwater, unable to contain his satisfaction any longer, descended from his stool, and caught him rapturously by the hand.", "찰스 형도 마주 고개를 끄덕이고는 네드 형과 웃음 띤 눈빛을 주고받았어. 그런데 바로 그때 니콜라스가 다른 쪽을 참고하려고 손을 멈췄지. 그러자 팀 링킨워터는 더 이상 흐뭇함을 참지 못하고 의자에서 내려와, 황홀해하며 그의 손을 덥석 잡았어."]], [["‘He has done it!’ said Tim, looking round at his employers and shaking his head triumphantly.", "\"해냈어요!\" 팀이 고용주들을 돌아보며 의기양양하게 고개를 저으며 말했어."], ["‘His capital B’s and D’s are exactly like mine; he dots all his small i’s and crosses every t as he writes it.", "\"이 친구가 쓴 대문자 B하고 D가 제 것하고 똑같아요. 작은 i마다 점을 다 찍고, t를 쓸 때마다 가로획도 다 그어요."], ["There an’t such a young man as this in all London,’ said Tim, clapping Nicholas on the back; ‘not one.", "온 런던을 다 뒤져도 이런 젊은이는 없어요.\" 팀은 니콜라스의 등을 탁탁 두드리며 말했어."], ["Don’t tell me!", "\"단 한 명도요."], ["The city can’t produce his equal.", "어림없는 소리! 이 도시는 이만한 짝을 못 만들어 내요."], ["I challenge the city to do it!’", "어디 한번 만들어 보라고 시내 전체에 도전하겠어요!\""]], [["With this casting down of his gauntlet, Tim Linkinwater struck the desk such a blow with his clenched fist, that the old blackbird tumbled off his perch with the start it gave him, and actually uttered a feeble croak, in the extremity of his astonishment.", "이렇게 도전장을 던지며, 팀 링킨워터는 꽉 쥔 주먹으로 책상을 어찌나 세게 내리쳤던지, 그 바람에 깜짝 놀란 늙은 검은지빠귀가 횃대에서 떨어지면서, 너무 놀란 나머지 정말로 가냘프게 깍 하고 울음소리까지 냈어."]], [["‘Well said, Tim--well said, Tim Linkinwater!’ cried brother Charles, scarcely less pleased than Tim himself, and clapping his hands gently as he spoke.", "\"잘 말했네, 팀. 잘 말했어, 팀 링킨워터!\""], ["‘I knew our young friend would take great pains, and I was quite certain he would succeed, in no time.", "찰스 형이 외쳤어. 팀 못지않게 기뻐하며, 말하는 동안 부드럽게 손뼉을 쳤지."], ["Didn’t I say so, brother Ned?’", "\"나는 우리 젊은 친구가 무던히 애쓸 줄 알았고, 금세 해낼 거라고 확신했지. 안 그런가, 네드?\""]], [["‘You did, my dear brother; certainly, my dear brother, you said so, and you were quite right,’ replied Ned.", "\"그렇게 말했지, 형님. 정말이지, 형님이 그렇게 말했고, 그 말이 딱 맞았어.\""], ["‘Quite right.", "네드가 대답했어."], ["Tim Linkinwater is excited, but he is justly excited, properly excited.", "\"딱 맞았지. 팀 링킨워터가 흥분하긴 했지만, 흥분할 만하니까, 마땅히 흥분할 만하니까 흥분한 거야."], ["Tim is a fine fellow.", "팀은 참 좋은 친구야."], ["Tim Linkinwater, sir--you’re a fine fellow.’", "팀 링킨워터 씨, 자네는 참 좋은 사람이야.\""]], [["‘Here’s a pleasant thing to think of!’ said Tim, wholly regardless of this address to himself, and raising his spectacles from the ledger to the brothers.", "\"이거 생각만 해도 흐뭇한 일이지요!\" 팀은 자기한테 하는 이 말은 아랑곳하지 않고, 원장에서 형제에게로 안경을 들어 올리며 말했어."], ["‘Here’s a pleasant thing.", "\"참 흐뭇한 일이에요. 제가 떠나고 나면 이 장부들이 어떻게 될까, 제가 그걸 자주 생각하지 않았을 것 같으세요?"], ["Do you suppose I haven’t often thought of what would become of these books when I was gone?", "제가 가고 나면 여기 일이 들쭉날쭉하고 엉망이 될지 모른다고 자주 걱정하지 않았을 것 같으세요? 그런데 이제는,\""], ["Do you suppose I haven’t often thought that things might go on irregular and untidy here, after I was taken away?", "팀이 니콜라스 쪽으로 집게손가락을 뻗으며 말했어. \"이제는, 이 친구한테 조금만 더 가르쳐 놓으면, 마음이 놓여요."], ["But now,’ said Tim, extending his forefinger towards Nicholas, ‘now, when I’ve shown him a little more, I’m satisfied.", "제가 죽고 나서도 일은, 제가 살아 있을 때만큼 잘 굴러갈 거예요. 똑같이요."], ["The business will go on, when I’m dead, as well as it did when I was alive--just the same--and I shall have the satisfaction of knowing that there never were such books--never were such books!", "그러면 저는, 치어리블 형제의 장부 같은 장부는 일찍이 없었다는 걸 알고 흐뭇하게 갈 수 있어요. 그런 장부는 없었어요!"], ["No, nor never will be such books--as the books of Cheeryble Brothers.’", "아니, 앞으로도 그런 장부는 없을 거예요. 치어리블 형제의 장부 같은 건요.\""]], [["Having thus expressed his sentiments, Mr.", "이렇게 속마음을 털어놓고 나서, 링킨워터 씨는 짧게 웃음을 한 번 터뜨렸어."], ["Linkinwater gave vent to a short laugh, indicative of defiance to the cities of London and Westminster, and, turning again to his desk, quietly carried seventy-six from the last column he had added up, and went on with his work.", "런던 시와 웨스트민스터 시 전체에 도전하는 듯한 웃음이었지. 그러고는 다시 책상으로 돌아앉아, 방금 더하던 마지막 세로 칸에서 칠십육을 조용히 받아 올리고는, 하던 일을 계속했어."]], [["‘Tim Linkinwater, sir,’ said brother Charles; ‘give me your hand, sir.", "\"팀 링킨워터 씨.\" 찰스 형이 말했어."], ["This is your birthday.", "\"손 좀 이리 주게."], ["How dare you talk about anything else till you have been wished many happy returns of the day, Tim Linkinwater?", "오늘이 자네 생일이잖나. 자네한테 생일 축하 인사도 하기 전에, 어떻게 감히 딴 얘기를 꺼낼 수 있겠나, 팀 링킨워터?"], ["God bless you, Tim!", "하느님이 자네를 축복하시길, 팀!"], ["God bless you!’", "하느님의 축복이 있기를!\""]], [["‘My dear brother,’ said the other, seizing Tim’s disengaged fist, ‘Tim Linkinwater looks ten years younger than he did on his last birthday.’", "\"형님.\" 다른 노인이 팀의 빈 주먹을 마저 잡으며 말했어. \"팀 링킨워터가 지난 생일 때보다 열 살은 더 젊어 보이는데.\""]], [["‘Brother Ned, my dear boy,’ returned the other old fellow, ‘I believe that Tim Linkinwater was born a hundred and fifty years old, and is gradually coming down to five-and-twenty; for he’s younger every birthday than he was the year before.’", "\"네드, 이 친구야.\" 또 다른 노인이 대꾸했어. \"내 생각에 팀 링킨워터는 백쉰 살로 태어나서 차츰 스물다섯 살로 내려오고 있는 것 같아. 생일이 돌아올 때마다 작년보다 더 젊어지니 말이야.\""]], [["‘So he is, brother Charles, so he is,’ replied brother Ned.", "\"그렇고말고, 형님. 그렇고말고.\""], ["‘There’s not a doubt about it.’", "네드 형이 대답했어. \"거기엔 의심의 여지가 없지.\""]], [["‘Remember, Tim,’ said brother Charles, ‘that we dine at half-past five today instead of two o’clock; we always depart from our usual custom on this anniversary, as you very well know, Tim Linkinwater.", "\"잊지 말게, 팀.\" 찰스 형이 말했어."], ["Mr. Nickleby, my dear sir, you will make one.", "\"오늘은 두 시가 아니라 다섯 시 반에 저녁을 먹는다는 걸 말이야."], ["Tim Linkinwater, give me your snuff-box as a remembrance to brother Charles and myself of an attached and faithful rascal, and take that, in exchange, as a feeble mark of our respect and esteem, and don’t open it until you go to bed, and never say another word upon the subject, or I’ll kill the blackbird.", "이 기념일에는 늘 평소 습관을 깨잖나, 자네도 잘 알다시피, 팀 링킨워터. 니클비 씨, 자네도 함께하게."], ["A dog! He should have had a golden cage half-a-dozen years ago, if it would have made him or his master a bit the happier.", "팀 링킨워터, 정 많고 충직한 이 녀석을 찰스 형하고 내가 기억할 수 있게, 자네 코담배갑을 주게."], ["Now, brother Ned, my dear fellow, I’m ready.", "그리고 그 대신 이걸 받게나. 우리의 존경과 애정을 담은 보잘것없는 표시일세."], ["At half-past five, remember, Mr.", "잠자리에 들기 전에는 열어 보지 말고, 이 얘긴 더 이상 입에 올리지 말게. 안 그러면 저 검은지빠귀를 죽여 버릴 테니까."], ["Nickleby!", "아이고, 이 양반아!"], ["Tim Linkinwater, sir, take care of Mr.", "저 새한테나 그 주인한테 조금이라도 더 기쁨이 됐을 거라면, 육 년 전에 진작 금새장을 사 줬어야 했어. 자, 네드, 이 친구야, 나는 준비됐네."], ["Nickleby at half-past five.", "다섯 시 반이야, 잊지 말게, 니클비 씨! 팀 링킨워터 씨, 다섯 시 반에 니클비 씨 잘 챙기게."], ["Now, brother Ned.’", "자, 가세, 네드.\""]], [["Chattering away thus, according to custom, to prevent the possibility of any thanks or acknowledgment being expressed on the other side, the twins trotted off, arm-in-arm; having endowed Tim Linkinwater with a costly gold snuff-box, enclosing a bank note worth more than its value ten times told.", "이렇게 늘 하던 대로 쉴 새 없이 떠들어 댄 건, 상대편에서 고맙다거나 하는 인사를 도무지 꺼낼 틈을 주지 않으려는 것이었지. 쌍둥이 형제는 값비싼 금 코담배갑을 팀 링킨워터에게 안겨 주고는 — 그 안에는 갑값의 열 배도 넘는 은행권 한 장이 들어 있었어 — 팔짱을 끼고 종종걸음으로 떠났어."]], [["At a quarter past five o’clock, punctual to the minute, arrived, according to annual usage, Tim Linkinwater’s sister; and a great to-do there was, between Tim Linkinwater’s sister and the old housekeeper, respecting Tim Linkinwater’s sister’s cap, which had been dispatched, per boy, from the house of the family where Tim Linkinwater’s sister boarded, and had not yet come to hand: notwithstanding that it had been packed up in a bandbox, and the bandbox in a handkerchief, and the handkerchief tied on to the boy’s arm; and notwithstanding, too, that the place of its consignment had been duly set forth, at full length, on the back of an old letter, and the boy enjoined, under pain of divers horrible penalties, the full extent of which the eye of man could not foresee, to deliver the same with all possible speed, and not to loiter by the way. Tim Linkinwater’s sister lamented; the housekeeper condoled; and both kept thrusting their heads out of the second-floor window to see if the boy was ‘coming’--which would have been highly satisfactory, and, upon the whole, tantamount to his being come, as the distance to the corner was not quite five yards--when, all of a sudden, and when he was least expected, the messenger, carrying the bandbox with elaborate caution, appeared in an exactly opposite direction, puffing and panting for breath, and flushed with recent exercise; as well he might be; for he had taken the air, in the first instance, behind a hackney coach that went to Camberwell, and had followed two Punches afterwards and had seen the Stilts home to their own door. The cap was all safe, however--that was one comfort--and it was no use scolding him--that was another; so the boy went upon his way rejoicing, and Tim Linkinwater’s sister presented herself to the company below-stairs, just five minutes after the half-hour had struck by Tim Linkinwater’s own infallible clock.", "다섯 시 십오 분, 한 치도 어김없이 정각에, 해마다 그랬듯이 팀 링킨워터의 누이가 도착했어. 그리고 팀 링킨워터의 누이와 늙은 가정부 사이에는 한바탕 소동이 벌어졌지. 팀 링킨워터의 누이가 묵고 있는 집에서 심부름 아이 편에 보낸 모자가 아직 도착하지 않은 탓이었어. 모자는 모자 상자에 담겨 있었고, 그 상자는 손수건에 싸여 있었고, 그 손수건은 아이의 팔에 묶여 있었는데도 말이야. 게다가 모자를 보낼 곳을 낡은 편지 뒷면에 빠짐없이 길게 적어 두었고, 아이한테는 사람 눈으로는 미처 다 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 온갖 무시무시한 벌을 주겠다고 으름장을 놓으며, 되도록 빨리 갖다주고 도중에 한눈팔지 말라고 단단히 일러 두었는데도 그랬어. 팀 링킨워터의 누이는 탄식했고, 가정부는 위로했어. 그리고 둘은 아이가 \"오고 있는지\" 보려고 연신 이층 창밖으로 고개를 내밀었지. 모퉁이까지의 거리가 채 다섯 발짝도 안 됐으니, 아이가 오는 게 보이기만 하면 그건 아주 흡족한 일이었고, 따지고 보면 도착한 거나 마찬가지였거든. 그런데 바로 그때, 전혀 기대하지 않은 순간에, 심부름꾼이 모자 상자를 조심조심 받쳐 들고 정반대 방향에서 나타났어. 헉헉 숨을 몰아쉬며, 방금 한바탕 뛰어다닌 탓에 얼굴이 벌게져서 말이야. 그럴 만도 했지. 그 아이는 우선 캠버웰로 가는 삯마차 뒤꽁무니에 매달려 바람을 쐤고, 그다음엔 펀치 인형극 두 패를 졸졸 따라다녔고, 죽마 탄 광대들이 제 집 문 앞까지 가는 걸 배웅하고 왔거든. 그래도 모자는 멀쩡했으니 — 그게 하나의 위안이었지 — 그리고 야단쳐 봐야 소용없으니 — 그게 또 하나의 위안이었어. 그래서 아이는 신이 나서 제 갈 길을 갔고, 팀 링킨워터의 누이는 팀 링킨워터의 그 한 치도 틀림없는 시계로 정각 삼십 분이 친 지 딱 오 분 만에 아래층 손님들 앞에 모습을 드러냈어."]], [["The company consisted of the brothers Cheeryble, Tim Linkinwater, a ruddy-faced white-headed friend of Tim’s (who was a superannuated bank clerk), and Nicholas, who was presented to Tim Linkinwater’s sister with much gravity and solemnity. The party being now completed, brother Ned rang for dinner, and, dinner being shortly afterwards announced, led Tim Linkinwater’s sister into the next room, where it was set forth with great preparation. Then, brother Ned took the head of the table, and brother Charles the foot; and Tim Linkinwater’s sister sat on the left hand of brother Ned, and Tim Linkinwater himself on his right: and an ancient butler of apoplectic appearance, and with very short legs, took up his position at the back of brother Ned’s armchair, and, waving his right arm preparatory to taking off the covers with a flourish, stood bolt upright and motionless.", "손님은 치어리블 형제, 팀 링킨워터, 팀의 혈색 좋고 머리가 하얀 친구 한 명(은퇴한 은행 사무원이었지), 그리고 니콜라스로 이루어져 있었어. 니콜라스는 아주 진지하고 엄숙하게 팀 링킨워터의 누이에게 소개되었지. 이제 일행이 다 모이자, 네드 형이 종을 울려 저녁을 청했고, 곧 저녁 준비가 다 됐다는 알림이 오자, 팀 링킨워터의 누이를 옆방으로 이끌었어. 거기에는 푸짐하게 준비한 식사가 차려져 있었지. 그러고는 네드 형이 식탁 상석에, 찰스 형이 말석에 앉았어. 팀 링킨워터의 누이는 네드 형의 왼편에, 팀 링킨워터 본인은 그의 오른편에 앉았지. 그리고 중풍 걸린 듯한 인상에 다리가 아주 짧은 늙은 집사가 네드 형의 안락의자 뒤에 자리를 잡고는, 멋들어진 동작으로 음식 덮개를 벗길 채비로 오른팔을 한 번 휘젓고서, 꼿꼿이 선 채로 꼼짝도 하지 않았어."]], [["‘For these and all other blessings, brother Charles,’ said Ned.", "\"이것들과 그 밖의 모든 은혜에 대하여, 찰스 형.\" 네드가 말했어."]], [["‘Lord, make us truly thankful, brother Ned,’ said Charles.", "\"주여, 우리를 진실로 감사하게 하소서, 네드.\" 찰스가 말했어."]], [["Whereupon the apoplectic butler whisked off the top of the soup tureen, and shot, all at once, into a state of violent activity.", "그러자 중풍 걸린 듯한 집사가 수프 그릇 뚜껑을 휙 벗기더니, 별안간 격렬하게 부산을 떨기 시작했어."]], [["There was abundance of conversation, and little fear of its ever flagging, for the good-humour of the glorious old twins drew everybody out, and Tim Linkinwater’s sister went off into a long and circumstantial account of Tim Linkinwater’s infancy, immediately after the very first glass of champagne--taking care to premise that she was very much Tim’s junior, and had only become acquainted with the facts from their being preserved and handed down in the family. This history concluded, brother Ned related how that, exactly thirty-five years ago, Tim Linkinwater was suspected to have received a love-letter, and how that vague information had been brought to the counting-house of his having been seen walking down Cheapside with an uncommonly handsome spinster; at which there was a roar of laughter, and Tim Linkinwater being charged with blushing, and called upon to explain, denied that the accusation was true; and further, that there would have been any harm in it if it had been; which last position occasioned the superannuated bank clerk to laugh tremendously, and to declare that it was the very best thing he had ever heard in his life, and that Tim Linkinwater might say a great many things before he said anything which would beat THAT.", "대화는 넘쳐 났고, 끊길 걱정 따위는 거의 없었어. 그 멋진 노쌍둥이의 좋은 기분이 모두의 입을 열게 했거든. 그리고 팀 링킨워터의 누이는 샴페인 첫 잔을 마시자마자 팀 링킨워터의 어린 시절 이야기를 길고 자세하게 늘어놓기 시작했어. 자기는 팀보다 훨씬 어려서, 이 일들은 그저 집안에 전해 내려오는 이야기로만 알게 됐다는 걸 미리 밝히는 것도 잊지 않았지. 이 이야기가 끝나자, 네드 형이 다른 이야기를 꺼냈어. 정확히 삼십오 년 전에, 팀 링킨워터가 연애편지를 받은 것 같다는 의심을 산 적이 있었다는 거야. 그리고 그가 보기 드물게 어여쁜 노처녀와 칩사이드 거리를 걷는 걸 누가 봤다는 어렴풋한 소식이 사무소까지 들려왔다는 거지. 그 말에 한바탕 웃음이 터졌고, 팀 링킨워터는 얼굴을 붉혔다는 추궁을 받으며 해명하라는 요구를 받았어. 그러자 그는 그 비난이 사실이 아니라고 부인했고, 한술 더 떠 설령 사실이었다 한들 그게 무슨 흠 될 일이냐고 했지. 이 마지막 말에 은퇴한 은행 사무원이 어찌나 크게 웃어 댔는지, 평생 들어 본 것 중에 가장 멋진 말이라면서, 팀 링킨워터가 앞으로 무슨 말을 백 번을 한들 그 말을 이길 말은 못 할 거라고 했어."]], [["There was one little ceremony peculiar to the day, both the matter and manner of which made a very strong impression upon Nicholas.", "그날에만 있는 자그마한 의식이 하나 있었는데, 그 내용도 방식도 니콜라스에게 아주 깊은 인상을 남겼어."], ["The cloth having been removed and the decanters sent round for the first time, a profound silence succeeded, and in the cheerful faces of the brothers there appeared an expression, not of absolute melancholy, but of quiet thoughtfulness very unusual at a festive table.", "식탁보가 걷히고 술병이 처음으로 한 바퀴 돌고 나자, 깊은 침묵이 뒤따랐어. 그리고 형제의 환한 얼굴에는, 완전한 우울은 아니지만, 잔칫상에서는 좀처럼 보기 힘든 조용한 사색의 빛이 떠올랐지."], ["As Nicholas, struck by this sudden alteration, was wondering what it could portend, the brothers rose together, and the one at the top of the table leaning forward towards the other, and speaking in a low voice as if he were addressing him individually, said:", "니콜라스가 이 갑작스러운 변화에 마음이 쓰여 이게 무슨 뜻인가 궁금해하고 있는데, 형제가 함께 일어섰어. 그리고 식탁 상석에 앉은 쪽이 다른 쪽을 향해 몸을 기울이고는, 마치 그 한 사람에게만 말하듯 낮은 목소리로 이렇게 말했어."]], [["‘Brother Charles, my dear fellow, there is another association connected with this day which must never be forgotten, and never can be forgotten, by you and me.", "\"찰스 형, 이 친구야, 오늘과 얽혀 있는 또 다른 기억이 하나 있지. 그건 형이나 나나 결코 잊어서는 안 되고, 또 결코 잊을 수도 없는 거야."], ["This day, which brought into the world a most faithful and excellent and exemplary fellow, took from it the kindest and very best of parents, the very best of parents to us both.", "오늘은 더없이 충직하고 훌륭하고 본받을 만한 한 친구를 이 세상에 데려온 날이지만, 동시에 더없이 자애롭고 가장 좋은 부모님을, 우리 둘에게 더없이 좋은 부모님을 이 세상에서 데려간 날이기도 해."], ["I wish that she could have seen us in our prosperity, and shared it, and had the happiness of knowing how dearly we loved her in it, as we did when we were two poor boys; but that was not to be.", "어머니가 우리가 잘된 모습을 보시고, 그걸 함께 누리시고, 우리가 가난한 두 소년이었을 때 그랬던 것처럼 그때도 우리가 어머니를 얼마나 사랑했는지 아셨더라면 얼마나 좋았을까. 하지만 그럴 수는 없었지."], ["My dear brother--The Memory of our Mother.’", "형, 우리 어머니를 추억하며.\""]], [["‘Good Lord!’ thought Nicholas, ‘and there are scores of people of their own station, knowing all this, and twenty thousand times more, who wouldn’t ask these men to dinner because they eat with their knives and never went to school!’", "'세상에,' 니콜라스는 생각했어. '이 모든 걸, 아니 이보다 만 배는 더한 것까지 다 알면서도, 이분들과 같은 신분의 사람 수십 명이 이분들을 저녁에 초대조차 않는단 말이지. 그저 칼로 음식을 떠먹고 학교를 안 다녔다는 이유로!'"]], [["But there was no time to moralise, for the joviality again became very brisk, and the decanter of port being nearly out, brother Ned pulled the bell, which was instantly answered by the apoplectic butler.", "하지만 도덕을 곱씹을 새는 없었어. 다시 흥겨운 분위기가 아주 활기차게 살아났거든. 그리고 포트와인 병이 거의 비자, 네드 형이 종을 당겼고, 그 즉시 중풍 걸린 듯한 집사가 대답하며 나타났어."]], [["‘David,’ said brother Ned.", "\"데이비드.\" 네드 형이 말했어."]], [["‘Sir,’ replied the butler.", "\"네, 어르신.\" 집사가 대답했어."]], [["‘A magnum of the double-diamond, David, to drink the health of Mr Linkinwater.’", "\"더블 다이아몬드 큰 병으로 하나 가져오게, 데이비드. 링킨워터 씨의 건강을 위해 마실 테니.\""]], [["Instantly, by a feat of dexterity, which was the admiration of all the company, and had been, annually, for some years past, the apoplectic butler, bringing his left hand from behind the small of his back, produced the bottle with the corkscrew already inserted; uncorked it at a jerk; and placed the magnum and the cork before his master with the dignity of conscious cleverness.", "그러자 그 자리에 있는 모두가 감탄하는, 게다가 지난 몇 해 동안 해마다 감탄해 온 솜씨로, 중풍 걸린 듯한 집사가 등 뒤 허리께에서 왼손을 척 내밀었어. 그 손에는 이미 코르크 따개가 꽂힌 병이 들려 있었지. 그는 한 번에 코르크를 쑥 뽑고는, 자기 솜씨를 흐뭇하게 의식하는 위엄을 풍기며 큰 병과 코르크를 주인 앞에 내려놓았어."]], [["‘Ha!’ said brother Ned, first examining the cork and afterwards filling his glass, while the old butler looked complacently and amiably on, as if it were all his own property, but the company were quite welcome to make free with it, ‘this looks well, David.’", "\"하!\" 네드 형이 먼저 코르크를 살펴보고는 잔을 채우며 말했어. 그동안 늙은 집사는 흐뭇하고 상냥하게 지켜보고 있었지. 마치 그게 다 제 것인데 손님들이 마음껏 들어도 좋다는 듯한 표정으로 말이야. \"이거 좋아 보이는데, 데이비드.\""]], [["‘It ought to, sir,’ replied David.", "\"좋을 수밖에요, 어르신.\" 데이비드가 대답했어."], ["‘You’d be troubled to find such a glass of wine as is our double-diamond, and that Mr.", "\"우리 더블 다이아몬드만 한 술은 어디 가서도 찾기 힘드실 겁니다. 그건 링킨워터 씨도 잘 아시고요."], ["Linkinwater knows very well. That was laid down when Mr.", "이 술은 링킨워터 씨가 처음 오셨을 때 담가 둔 거예요."], ["Linkinwater first come: that wine was, gentlemen.’", "바로 그 술입죠, 여러분.\""]], [["‘Nay, David, nay,’ interposed brother Charles.", "\"아니야, 데이비드, 아니야.\" 찰스 형이 끼어들었어."]], [["‘I wrote the entry in the cellar-book myself, sir, if you please,’ said David, in the tone of a man, quite confident in the strength of his facts.", "\"제가 직접 지하 저장고 장부에 적어 넣었습니다, 어르신, 죄송하지만요.\" 데이비드가, 제 사실에 한 치의 흔들림도 없다는 사람의 어조로 말했어."], ["‘Mr. Linkinwater had only been here twenty year, sir, when that pipe of double-diamond was laid down.’", "\"이 더블 다이아몬드 통을 담갔을 때, 링킨워터 씨는 여기 오신 지 딱 이십 년밖에 안 됐을 때였습니다, 어르신.\""]], [["‘David is quite right, quite right, brother Charles,’ said Ned: ‘are the people here, David?’", "\"데이비드 말이 딱 맞아, 딱 맞아, 찰스 형.\" 네드가 말했어. \"사람들 왔나, 데이비드?\""]], [["‘Outside the door, sir,’ replied the butler.", "\"문밖에 와 있습니다, 어르신.\" 집사가 대답했어."]], [["‘Show ‘em in, David, show ‘em in.’", "\"들여보내게, 데이비드, 들여보내.\""]], [["At this bidding, the older butler placed before his master a small tray of clean glasses, and opening the door admitted the jolly porters and warehousemen whom Nicholas had seen below.", "이 분부에, 늙은 집사는 깨끗한 잔이 담긴 작은 쟁반을 주인 앞에 놓고는, 문을 열어 니콜라스가 아래층에서 봤던 그 유쾌한 짐꾼과 창고지기들을 들였어."], ["They were four in all, and as they came in, bowing, and grinning, and blushing, the housekeeper, and cook, and housemaid, brought up the rear.", "모두 넷이었는데, 그들이 절을 하고 싱글거리고 얼굴을 붉히며 들어오자, 그 뒤를 이어 가정부와 요리사와 하녀가 따라 들어왔지."]], [["‘Seven,’ said brother Ned, filling a corresponding number of glasses with the double-diamond, ‘and David, eight.", "\"일곱.\" 네드 형이 그만큼의 잔에 더블 다이아몬드를 채우며 말했어."], ["There!", "\"그리고 데이비드까지 여덟."], ["Now, you’re all of you to drink the health of your best friend Mr.", "자! 이제 모두들, 너희들의 가장 좋은 친구인 티머시 링킨워터 씨의 건강을 위해 마시거라."], ["Timothy Linkinwater, and wish him health and long life and many happy returns of this day, both for his own sake and that of your old masters, who consider him an inestimable treasure.", "그리고 그의 건강과 만수무강과, 오늘 같은 날이 여러 번 돌아오기를 빌어 주거라. 그를 위해서도, 또 그를 더없이 값진 보배로 여기는 너희 옛 주인들을 위해서도 말이야."], ["Tim Linkinwater, sir, your health.", "팀 링킨워터 씨, 자네의 건강을 위하여."], ["Devil take you, Tim Linkinwater, sir, God bless you.’", "빌어먹을 친구 같으니, 팀 링킨워터 씨, 하느님의 축복이 있기를.\""]], [["With this singular contradiction of terms, brother Ned gave Tim Linkinwater a slap on the back, which made him look, for the moment, almost as apoplectic as the butler: and tossed off the contents of his glass in a twinkling.", "이렇게 앞뒤가 안 맞는 묘한 말을 하며, 네드 형은 팀 링킨워터의 등을 한 대 철썩 때렸어. 그 바람에 팀은 그 순간 집사 못지않게 중풍이라도 걸린 듯한 얼굴이 됐지. 그러고는 네드 형은 눈 깜짝할 사이에 잔을 쭉 비웠어."]], [["The toast was scarcely drunk with all honour to Tim Linkinwater, when the sturdiest and jolliest subordinate elbowed himself a little in advance of his fellows, and exhibiting a very hot and flushed countenance, pulled a single lock of grey hair in the middle of his forehead as a respectful salute to the company, and delivered himself as follows--rubbing the palms of his hands very hard on a blue cotton handkerchief as he did so:", "팀 링킨워터를 위한 축배가 한껏 격식을 갖춰 다 들이켜지기도 전에, 그 가운데 가장 튼튼하고 유쾌한 아랫사람이 동료들보다 조금 앞으로 팔꿈치로 비집고 나섰어. 그러고는 아주 화끈거리고 벌게진 얼굴을 내보이며, 이마 한가운데 난 잿빛 머리카락 한 줌을 잡아당겨 좌중에 공손히 인사를 하고는, 이렇게 입을 열었어. 그러면서 파란 무명 손수건에 두 손바닥을 아주 세게 비벼 대면서 말이야."]], [["‘We’re allowed to take a liberty once a year, gen’lemen, and if you please we’ll take it now; there being no time like the present, and no two birds in the hand worth one in the bush, as is well known--leastways in a contrairy sense, which the meaning is the same. (A pause--the butler unconvinced.) What we mean to say is, that there never was (looking at the butler)--such--(looking at the cook) noble--excellent--(looking everywhere and seeing nobody) free, generous-spirited masters as them as has treated us so handsome this day. And here’s thanking of ‘em for all their goodness as is so constancy a diffusing of itself over everywhere, and wishing they may live long and die happy!’", "\"저희가 일 년에 한 번 버릇없이 굴어도 되는 날입죠, 여러분. 괜찮으시다면 지금 그래 보겠습니다. 지금만 한 때가 또 없고, 손안의 두 마리 새가 덤불 속 한 마리만 못하다는 건 다들 잘 아실 테니까요. 적어도 거꾸로 따지면 그렇다는 거고, 뜻은 매한가지입죠. (잠시 멈춤 — 집사는 납득하지 못함.) 저희가 드리려는 말씀은, 그러니까 일찍이 (집사를 보며) — 이렇게나 — (요리사를 보며) 고결하고 — 훌륭하고 — (여기저기 둘러보지만 아무도 안 봄) 너그럽고 마음 넓으신 주인 어르신들은 없었다는 겁니다. 오늘 저희를 이토록 후하게 대해 주신 분들 말입죠. 그리고 어디에나 한결같이 베풀어 주시는 그 모든 은혜에 감사드리며, 어르신들께서 오래오래 사시고 복되게 가시기를 비는 바입니다!\""]], [["When the foregoing speech was over--and it might have been much more elegant and much less to the purpose--the whole body of subordinates under command of the apoplectic butler gave three soft cheers; which, to that gentleman’s great indignation, were not very regular, inasmuch as the women persisted in giving an immense number of little shrill hurrahs among themselves, in utter disregard of the time. This done, they withdrew; shortly afterwards, Tim Linkinwater’s sister withdrew; in reasonable time after that, the sitting was broken up for tea and coffee, and a round game of cards.", "이 연설이 끝나자 — 훨씬 더 우아하면서도 훨씬 덜 장황할 수도 있었겠지만 — 중풍 걸린 듯한 집사를 우두머리로 한 아랫사람들 전체가 조용히 만세 삼창을 했어. 그런데 그게 집사의 큰 분통을 살 만큼 영 가지런하지 못했어. 여자들이 박자는 아랑곳없이, 자기네들끼리 가늘고 높은 만세 소리를 끝도 없이 질러 댔거든. 이게 끝나자 그들은 물러갔어. 얼마 안 있어 팀 링킨워터의 누이도 물러갔고, 그러고 적당히 시간이 지나자 자리는 차와 커피, 그리고 다 함께 둘러앉아 하는 카드놀이를 위해 파했지."]], [["At half-past ten--late hours for the square--there appeared a little tray of sandwiches and a bowl of bishop, which bishop coming on the top of the double-diamond, and other excitements, had such an effect upon Tim Linkinwater, that he drew Nicholas aside, and gave him to understand, confidentially, that it was quite true about the uncommonly handsome spinster, and that she was to the full as good-looking as she had been described--more so, indeed--but that she was in too much of a hurry to change her condition, and consequently, while Tim was courting her and thinking of changing his, got married to somebody else.", "열 시 반에 — 이 광장으로서는 늦은 시각이지 — 샌드위치가 담긴 작은 쟁반 하나와 비숍 한 사발이 나왔어. 그 비숍이 더블 다이아몬드 위에 거기다 다른 흥까지 더해 들어가니, 팀 링킨워터한테 어떤 효과를 냈냐면, 그가 니콜라스를 한쪽으로 끌고 가더니, 은밀히 털어놨어."], ["‘After all, I dare say it was my fault,’ said Tim.", "보기 드물게 어여뻤다는 그 노처녀 이야기가 다 사실이라는 거야. 그리고 그 여자가 소문대로 더없이 아름다웠다고, 아니, 사실은 그 이상이었다고 했어."], ["‘I’ll show you a print I have got upstairs, one of these days.", "하지만 그 여자가 너무 서둘러 처지를 바꾸려는 통에, 팀이 구애를 하며 자기 처지를 바꿔 볼까 고민하는 사이에, 다른 사람하고 결혼해 버렸다는 거지. \"따지고 보면 내 잘못이었겠지.\""], ["It cost me five-and-twenty shillings.", "팀이 말했어. \"조만간 위층에 있는 판화 한 장을 보여 주겠네."], ["I bought it soon after we were cool to each other.", "이십오 실링이나 주고 산 거야. 우리가 서먹해진 직후에 샀지."], ["Don’t mention it, but it’s the most extraordinary accidental likeness you ever saw--her very portrait, sir!’", "남한텐 말하지 말게나, 그런데 그게 자네가 평생 본 것 중에 가장 기막히게 우연히 닮은 그림이거든. 바로 그 여자 초상이야, 손님!\""]], [["By this time it was past eleven o’clock; and Tim Linkinwater’s sister declaring that she ought to have been at home a full hour ago, a coach was procured, into which she was handed with great ceremony by brother Ned, while brother Charles imparted the fullest directions to the coachman, and besides paying the man a shilling over and above his fare, in order that he might take the utmost care of the lady, all but choked him with a glass of spirits of uncommon strength, and then nearly knocked all the breath out of his body in his energetic endeavours to knock it in again.", "이 무렵엔 열한 시가 넘어 있었어. 그리고 팀 링킨워터의 누이가 한 시간도 더 전에 집에 갔어야 했다고 말하자, 마차를 한 대 불러 왔지. 네드 형이 아주 정중하게 누이를 마차에 태워 주는 동안, 찰스 형은 마부에게 가장 자세하게 길을 일러 줬어. 그리고 그 부인을 더없이 정성껏 모시라며 삯에 더해 일 실링을 얹어 주는 데다, 보기 드물게 독한 술을 한 잔 먹여 마부의 숨통을 막을 뻔했지. 그러고는 그 숨을 다시 불어넣어 주겠다고 기를 쓰며 마부의 몸에서 남은 숨까지 죄다 두들겨 빼낼 뻔했어."]], [["At length the coach rumbled off, and Tim Linkinwater’s sister being now fairly on her way home, Nicholas and Tim Linkinwater’s friend took their leaves together, and left old Tim and the worthy brothers to their repose.", "마침내 마차가 덜컹거리며 떠났고, 팀 링킨워터의 누이가 이제 무사히 집으로 가는 길에 오르자, 니콜라스와 팀 링킨워터의 친구는 함께 작별 인사를 하고는, 늙은 팀과 그 갸륵한 형제를 남겨 두고 떠났어. 그들이 편히 쉬도록 말이야."]], [["As Nicholas had some distance to walk, it was considerably past midnight by the time he reached home, where he found his mother and Smike sitting up to receive him.", "니콜라스는 걸어야 할 거리가 좀 되었던 터라, 집에 닿았을 때는 자정이 한참 지나 있었어. 집에서는 어머니와 스마이크가 자지 않고 그를 기다리고 있었지."], ["It was long after their usual hour of retiring, and they had expected him, at the very latest, two hours ago; but the time had not hung heavily on their hands, for Mrs.", "평소 잠자리에 들 시각을 한참 넘긴 데다, 두 사람은 아무리 늦어도 두 시간 전에는 그가 올 줄로 알았거든. 그래도 시간이 지루하지는 않았어."], ["Nickleby had entertained Smike with a genealogical account of her family by the mother’s side, comprising biographical sketches of the principal members, and Smike had sat wondering what it was all about, and whether it was learnt from a book, or said out of Mrs.", "니클비 부인이 외가 쪽 집안 내력을 족보 풀어 가며 스마이크한테 들려줬거든. 주요 인물들의 약전까지 곁들여서 말이야."], ["Nickleby’s own head; so that they got on together very pleasantly.", "그리고 스마이크는 그게 다 무슨 얘긴지, 책에서 배운 건지 아니면 니클비 부인이 머릿속에서 지어낸 건지 궁금해하며 앉아 있었지. 그렇게 둘은 아주 즐겁게 시간을 보냈어."]], [["Nicholas could not go to bed without expatiating on the excellences and munificence of the brothers Cheeryble, and relating the great success which had attended his efforts that day. But before he had said a dozen words, Mrs.", "니콜라스는 치어리블 형제의 훌륭함과 후한 인심을 한참 늘어놓고, 그날 자기 노력이 거둔 큰 성공을 들려주지 않고서는 도무지 잠자리에 들 수가 없었어."], ["Nickleby, with many sly winks and nods, observed, that she was sure Mr. Smike must be quite tired out, and that she positively must insist on his not sitting up a minute longer.", "그런데 그가 채 열 마디도 하기 전에, 니클비 부인이 짐짓 눈을 찡긋찡긋, 고개를 끄덕끄덕하더니, 스마이크 씨가 분명 몹시 지쳤을 거라며, 단 한순간도 더 앉아 있게 둘 수 없다고 한사코 우겼어."]], [["‘A most biddable creature he is, to be sure,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, when Smike had wished them good-night and left the room. ‘I know you’ll excuse me, Nicholas, my dear, but I don’t like to do this before a third person; indeed, before a young man it would not be quite proper, though really, after all, I don’t know what harm there is in it, except that to be sure it’s not a very becoming thing, though some people say it is very much so, and really I don’t know why it should not be, if it’s well got up, and the borders are small-plaited; of course, a good deal depends upon that.’", "\"참, 어찌나 말 잘 듣는 아이인지.\" 스마이크가 잘 자라는 인사를 하고 방을 나가자 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"네가 이해해 줄 줄 안다, 니콜라스. 그런데 나는 제삼자 앞에서 이걸 하는 게 영 싫구나. 사실 젊은 남자 앞이라면 그리 점잖은 일은 못 되지. 하긴 따지고 보면 그게 뭐 그리 흠 될 일인가 싶기도 하다만. 그저 그리 보기 좋은 건 아니니까. 그래도 어떤 사람들은 아주 보기 좋다고 하던데, 사실 잘 만들기만 하고 가장자리 주름만 곱게 잡으면 안 될 게 또 뭐냐 싶기도 하고. 물론 그건 만들기 나름이겠지만 말이다.\""]], [["With which preface, Mrs.", "이런 서두를 늘어놓고서, 니클비 부인은 아주 큰 기도서 책장 사이에 작게 접어 끼워 두었던 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자를 꺼내, 끈을 매기 시작했어."], ["Nickleby took her nightcap from between the leaves of a very large prayer-book where it had been folded up small, and proceeded to tie it on: talking away in her usual discursive manner, all the time.", "그 내내 늘 하던 대로 두서없이 떠들어 대면서 말이야."]], [["‘People may say what they like,’ observed Mrs. Nickleby, ‘but there’s a great deal of comfort in a nightcap, as I’m sure you would confess, Nicholas my dear, if you would only have strings to yours, and wear it like a Christian, instead of sticking it upon the very top of your head like a blue-coat boy. You needn’t think it an unmanly or quizzical thing to be particular about your nightcap, for I have often heard your poor dear papa, and the Reverend Mr. What’s-his-name, who used to read prayers in that old church with the curious little steeple that the weathercock was blown off the night week before you were born,--I have often heard them say, that the young men at college are uncommonly particular about their nightcaps, and that the Oxford nightcaps are quite celebrated for their strength and goodness; so much so, indeed, that the young men never dream of going to bed without ‘em, and I believe it’s admitted on all hands that THEY know what’s good, and don’t coddle themselves.’", "\"남들이야 뭐라든,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"잠잘 때 쓰는 모자에는 큰 편안함이 있단다. 너도 분명 인정할 거다, 니콜라스. 네 것에도 끈만 달아서, 청색 자선학교 아이마냥 정수리에 얹기만 하지 말고, 사람답게 제대로 쓰기만 한다면 말이야. 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자에 까다롭게 구는 게 남자답지 못하다거나 우습다고 여길 건 없어. 네 가엾은 아버지하고, 또 저, 이름이 뭐더라 하는 그 목사님이 — 네가 태어나기 일주일 전날 밤에 풍향계가 바람에 날아가 버린, 그 묘하고 자그마한 첨탑이 달린 오래된 교회에서 기도문을 읽어 주시던 분 말이다 — 그분들이 이런 말씀 하시는 걸 자주 들었거든. 대학에 다니는 젊은이들은 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자에 유난히 까다롭다고. 그리고 옥스퍼드 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자는 튼튼하고 좋기로 아주 유명하다고. 어찌나 그런지, 젊은이들이 그걸 안 쓰고는 잠자리에 들 엄두도 못 낸다더구나. 그 사람들이 좋은 게 뭔지 알고, 제 몸 함부로 다루지 않는다는 건 누구나 인정하는 바라고 나는 믿는다.\""]], [["Nicholas laughed, and entering no further into the subject of this lengthened harangue, reverted to the pleasant tone of the little birthday party.", "니콜라스는 웃었어. 그리고 이 길어진 일장 연설의 주제로는 더 이상 발을 들이지 않고, 자그마한 생일 잔치의 즐거운 이야기로 화제를 되돌렸어."], ["And as Mrs. Nickleby instantly became very curious respecting it, and made a great number of inquiries touching what they had had for dinner, and how it was put on table, and whether it was overdone or underdone, and who was there, and what ‘the Mr.", "그러자 니클비 부인이 그 일에 부쩍 호기심을 보이며, 저녁으로 뭘 먹었는지, 상은 어떻게 차렸는지, 너무 익었는지 덜 익었는지, 누가 왔는지, '그 체리블 씨네 분들'은 뭐라고 했는지, 니콜라스는 뭐라고 했는지, 그가 그 말을 하니까 체리블 씨네 분들은 또 뭐라고 했는지, 수없이 캐물었어."], ["Cherrybles’ said, and what Nicholas said, and what the Mr. Cherrybles said when he said that; Nicholas described the festivities at full length, and also the occurrences of the morning.", "그래서 니콜라스는 잔치며 그날 아침의 일들까지 죄다 자세히 들려주었지."]], [["‘Late as it is,’ said Nicholas, ‘I am almost selfish enough to wish that Kate had been up to hear all this. I was all impatience, as I came along, to tell her.’", "\"늦긴 했지만,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"케이트가 일어나 있어서 이 얘기를 다 들었더라면 싶어요. 저, 욕심이 좀 나는걸요. 오는 길 내내 동생한테 얘기해 주고 싶어서 좀이 쑤셨거든요.\""]], [["‘Why, Kate,’ said Mrs.", "\"아, 케이트 말이냐.\""], ["Nickleby, putting her feet upon the fender, and drawing her chair close to it, as if settling herself for a long talk.", "니클비 부인이 두 발을 난로 가리개에 올리고, 의자를 바짝 끌어당기며 말했어. 긴 이야기를 할 채비를 하는 것처럼 말이야."], ["‘Kate has been in bed--oh!", "\"케이트는 잠자리에 든 지 — 아! 두어 시간은 됐단다."], ["a couple of hours--and I’m very glad, Nicholas my dear, that I prevailed upon her not to sit up, for I wished very much to have an opportunity of saying a few words to you.", "그리고 나는 그 애를 깨어 있지 않게 한 게 참 다행이구나, 니콜라스. 너한테 몇 마디 할 기회가 정말이지 무척 갖고 싶었거든."], ["I am naturally anxious about it, and of course it’s a very delightful and consoling thing to have a grown-up son that one can put confidence in, and advise with; indeed I don’t know any use there would be in having sons at all, unless people could put confidence in them.’", "이건 당연히 내가 걱정스러운 일이고, 또 믿고 의논할 수 있는 다 큰 아들이 있다는 건 더없이 기쁘고 위로가 되는 일이지. 사실 사람이 아들을 믿지 못한다면, 도대체 아들이 무슨 소용인가 싶다.\""]], [["Nicholas stopped in the middle of a sleepy yawn, as his mother began to speak: and looked at her with fixed attention.", "니콜라스는 졸린 하품을 하다 말고 멈췄어. 어머니가 말을 꺼내자, 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라봤지."]], [["‘There was a lady in our neighbourhood,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, ‘speaking of sons puts me in mind of it--a lady in our neighbourhood when we lived near Dawlish, I think her name was Rogers; indeed I am sure it was if it wasn’t Murphy, which is the only doubt I have--’", "\"우리 동네에 한 부인이 살았는데,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"아들 얘기가 나오니까 생각나는구나. 우리가 돌리시 근처에 살 때 동네에 살던 부인인데, 이름이 로저스였던 것 같아. 아니, 머피가 아니라면 분명 로저스였어. 내가 헷갈리는 건 딱 그 하나뿐이니까.\""]], [["‘Is it about her, mother, that you wished to speak to me?’ said Nicholas quietly.", "\"어머니가 저한테 하시려던 얘기가 그 부인에 관한 거예요?\" 니콜라스가 조용히 물었어."]], [["‘About HER!’ cried Mrs.", "\"그 부인이라니!\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 외쳤어."], ["‘Good gracious, Nicholas, my dear, how CAN you be so ridiculous!", "\"아이고, 니콜라스, 어쩜 그리 엉뚱하니! 그런데 그게 꼭 네 가엾은 아버지 버릇이었어."], ["But that was always the way with your poor dear papa,--just his way--always wandering, never able to fix his thoughts on any one subject for two minutes together.", "딱 그 버릇이지. 늘 딴 데로 새고, 두 시간은커녕 단 한 가지 일에도 생각을 붙들어 매지 못했어."], ["I think I see him now!’ said Mrs.", "지금도 그이가 눈에 선하구나!\" 니클비 부인이 눈가를 훔치며 말했어."], ["Nickleby, wiping her eyes, ‘looking at me while I was talking to him about his affairs, just as if his ideas were in a state of perfect conglomeration!", "\"내가 그이 일에 대해 얘기하고 있으면, 그이는 나를 빤히 쳐다봤지. 머릿속 생각이 죄다 뒤죽박죽 엉켜 있는 사람처럼 말이야!"], ["Anybody who had come in upon us suddenly, would have supposed I was confusing and distracting him instead of making things plainer; upon my word they would.’", "누가 우리한테 불쑥 들어왔다면, 내가 일을 더 분명하게 해 주기는커녕 그이를 헷갈리게 하고 정신을 흩어 놓는 줄로 알았을 거다. 정말이지 그랬을 거야.\""]], [["‘I am very sorry, mother, that I should inherit this unfortunate slowness of apprehension,’ said Nicholas, kindly; ‘but I’ll do my best to understand you, if you’ll only go straight on: indeed I will.’", "\"제가 이 안타까운 굼뜬 머리를 물려받았다니 정말 죄송해요, 어머니.\" 니콜라스가 다정하게 말했어. \"하지만 어머니가 곧장 본론만 말씀해 주신다면, 알아들으려고 최선을 다할게요. 정말로요.\""]], [["‘Your poor pa!’ said Mrs.", "\"네 가엾은 아버지!\""], ["Nickleby, pondering.", "니클비 부인이 곰곰이 생각하며 말했어."], ["‘He never knew, till it was too late, what I would have had him do!’", "\"그이는 너무 늦어 버릴 때까지도, 내가 그이한테 뭘 시키고 싶어 했는지 도통 몰랐단다!\""]], [["This was undoubtedly the case, inasmuch as the deceased Mr.", "이건 의심할 바 없이 사실이었어."], ["Nickleby had not arrived at the knowledge when he died. Neither had Mrs.", "고인이 된 니클비 씨가 죽을 때까지도 그걸 알아내지 못했으니까 말이야."], ["Nickleby herself; which is, in some sort, an explanation of the circumstance.", "니클비 부인 자신도 몰랐고. 어찌 보면 그게 이 일에 대한 설명이 되겠지."]], [["‘However,’ said Mrs.", "\"하지만,\" 니클비 부인이 눈물을 닦으며 말했어."], ["Nickleby, drying her tears, ‘this has nothing to do--certainly nothing whatever to do--with the gentleman in the next house.’", "\"이건 옆집 그 신사하고는 아무 상관도 없어. 정말이지 손톱만큼도 상관없는 일이지.\""]], [["‘I should suppose that the gentleman in the next house has as little to do with us,’ returned Nicholas.", "\"제 생각엔 옆집 그 신사야말로 우리하고 상관이 거의 없을 것 같은데요.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘There can be no doubt,’ said Mrs.", "\"그가 신사라는 건,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby, ‘that he IS a gentleman, and has the manners of a gentleman, and the appearance of a gentleman, although he does wear smalls and grey worsted stockings.", "\"의심할 여지가 없어. 신사다운 몸가짐에, 신사다운 풍채를 지녔지."], ["That may be eccentricity, or he may be proud of his legs.", "무릎까지 오는 짧은 바지에 잿빛 털양말을 신긴 하다만. 그건 별난 취향일 수도 있고, 아니면 제 다리에 자부심이 있는 건지도 모르지."], ["I don’t see why he shouldn’t be.", "그러면 안 될 게 또 뭐냐."], ["The Prince Regent was proud of his legs, and so was Daniel Lambert, who was also a fat man; HE was proud of his legs.", "섭정 왕자도 제 다리에 자부심이 있었고, 대니얼 램버트도 그랬어. 그이는 뚱뚱보였는데도 제 다리를 자랑스러워했지."], ["So was Miss Biffin: she was--no,’ added Mrs.", "비핀 양도 그랬어. 그 여자는 — 아니,\""], ["Nickleby, correcting, herself, ‘I think she had only toes, but the principle is the same.’", "니클비 부인이 스스로 고쳐 말했어. \"그 여자는 발가락밖에 없었던 것 같다만, 그래도 이치는 매한가지지.\""]], [["Nicholas looked on, quite amazed at the introduction of this new theme.", "니콜라스는 이 새로운 화제가 튀어나온 것에 어안이 벙벙해서 지켜봤어."], ["Which seemed just what Mrs. Nickleby had expected him to be.", "그런데 그건 니클비 부인이 그가 그러기를 바라던 바로 그 반응인 듯했지."]], [["‘You may well be surprised, Nicholas, my dear,’ she said, ‘I am sure I was.", "\"놀랄 만도 하다, 니콜라스.\" 그녀가 말했어."], ["It came upon me like a flash of fire, and almost froze my blood.", "\"나도 정말 놀랐으니까. 불꽃이 번쩍하듯 들이닥쳐서, 피가 다 얼어붙는 줄 알았어."], ["The bottom of his garden joins the bottom of ours, and of course I had several times seen him sitting among the scarlet-beans in his little arbour, or working at his little hot-beds.", "그 사람네 정원 끝이 우리 정원 끝하고 맞닿아 있잖니. 그래서 당연히 나는 그 사람이 자기 자그마한 정자에서 붉은강낭콩 사이에 앉아 있거나, 자그마한 온상에서 일하는 걸 여러 번 봤지."], ["I used to think he stared rather, but I didn’t take any particular notice of that, as we were newcomers, and he might be curious to see what we were like.", "좀 빤히 쳐다본다 싶긴 했다만, 우리가 갓 이사 온 사람들이니 우리가 어떤지 궁금한가 보다 하고 별 신경을 안 썼어."], ["But when he began to throw his cucumbers over our wall--’", "그런데 그 사람이 자기 오이를 우리 담 너머로 던지기 시작하면서—\""]], [["‘To throw his cucumbers over our wall!’ repeated Nicholas, in great astonishment.", "\"오이를 우리 담 너머로 던지다니요!\" 니콜라스가 몹시 놀라 되뇌었어."]], [["‘Yes, Nicholas, my dear,’ replied Mrs.", "\"그래, 니콜라스.\""], ["Nickleby in a very serious tone; ‘his cucumbers over our wall.", "니클비 부인이 아주 진지한 어조로 대답했어. \"자기 오이를 우리 담 너머로 던졌어."], ["And vegetable marrows likewise.’", "호박도 마찬가지고.\""]], [["‘Confound his impudence!’ said Nicholas, firing immediately.", "\"그 뻔뻔한 작자 같으니!\" 니콜라스가 대번에 발끈하며 말했어."], ["‘What does he mean by that?’", "\"도대체 무슨 셈으로 그러는 거예요?\""]], [["‘I don’t think he means it impertinently at all,’ replied Mrs.", "\"내 생각엔 그게 무례하게 굴려는 건 전혀 아닌 것 같다.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 대답했어."]], [["‘What!’ said Nicholas, ‘cucumbers and vegetable marrows flying at the heads of the family as they walk in their own garden, and not meant impertinently!", "\"뭐라고요!\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["Why, mother--’", "\"식구들이 제집 정원을 걷는데 그 머리 위로 오이며 호박이 날아드는데, 무례하게 굴려는 게 아니라고요! 아니, 어머니—\""]], [["Nicholas stopped short; for there was an indescribable expression of placid triumph, mingled with a modest confusion, lingering between the borders of Mrs.", "니콜라스는 말을 뚝 끊었어. 니클비 부인의 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자 가장자리 사이로, 뭐라 말로 다 할 수 없는, 차분한 승리감이 수줍은 당혹감과 뒤섞인 표정이 어른거리고 있었거든."], ["Nickleby’s nightcap, which arrested his attention suddenly.", "그게 갑자기 그의 눈길을 붙들었지."]], [["‘He must be a very weak, and foolish, and inconsiderate man,’ said Mrs. Nickleby; ‘blamable indeed--at least I suppose other people would consider him so; of course I can’t be expected to express any opinion on that point, especially after always defending your poor dear papa when other people blamed him for making proposals to me; and to be sure there can be no doubt that he has taken a very singular way of showing it. Still at the same time, his attentions are--that is, as far as it goes, and to a certain extent of course--a flattering sort of thing; and although I should never dream of marrying again with a dear girl like Kate still unsettled in life--’", "\"그 사람은 분명 아주 약하고, 어리석고, 분별없는 사람이야.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"정말이지 나무랄 만한 사람이지. 적어도 남들은 그렇게 볼 게다. 물론 나야 그 점에 대해 뭐라 의견을 낼 처지는 못 되지. 특히나 남들이 네 가엾은 아버지가 나한테 청혼한 걸 나무랄 때도 나는 늘 그이 편을 들었으니까 말이야. 그리고 그 사람이 그걸 아주 별난 방식으로 드러낸 건 두말할 나위가 없지. 그래도 그와 동시에, 그 사람의 마음 씀은 — 그러니까, 어느 정도까지는, 또 일정 선까지는 물론 — 우쭐해지게 하는 데가 있긴 하단다. 그리고 케이트같이 사랑스러운 아이가 아직 자리를 못 잡고 있는 마당에 내가 다시 결혼할 생각 따위는 꿈에도 안 하지만—\""]], [["‘Surely, mother, such an idea never entered your brain for an instant?’ said Nicholas.", "\"설마 어머니, 그런 생각이 단 한순간이라도 머릿속에 들어온 적은 없으시겠죠?\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Bless my heart, Nicholas my dear,’ returned his mother in a peevish tone, ‘isn’t that precisely what I am saying, if you would only let me speak?", "\"아이고 맙소사, 니콜라스.\" 그의 어머니가 짜증스러운 어조로 대꾸했어."], ["Of course, I never gave it a second thought, and I am surprised and astonished that you should suppose me capable of such a thing.", "\"내가 말을 좀 끝내게 둔다면, 그게 바로 내가 하려는 말 그대로 아니냐? 당연히 두 번 다시 생각도 안 했고, 네가 나를 그런 짓을 할 사람으로 여긴다니 놀랍고 기가 막힌다."], ["All I say is, what step is the best to take, so as to reject these advances civilly and delicately, and without hurting his feelings too much, and driving him to despair, or anything of that kind?", "내가 하려는 말은 그저, 이 구애를 정중하고 곱게, 그러면서도 그 사람 마음을 너무 다치게 하거나 절망으로 몰아넣거나 하는 일 없이 물리치려면, 어떤 수가 가장 좋겠냐는 거야. 아이고 세상에!\""], ["My goodness me!’ exclaimed Mrs.", "니클비 부인이 반쯤 헤벌쭉 웃으며 외쳤어."], ["Nickleby, with a half-simper, ‘suppose he was to go doing anything rash to himself.", "\"그 사람이 제 몸에 무슨 무모한 짓이라도 하면 어쩌겠니."], ["Could I ever be happy again, Nicholas?’", "그럼 내가 다시 행복할 수 있겠어, 니콜라스?\""]], [["Despite his vexation and concern, Nicholas could scarcely help smiling, as he rejoined, ‘Now, do you think, mother, that such a result would be likely to ensue from the most cruel repulse?’", "니콜라스는 속이 상하고 걱정스러우면서도, 웃음을 참기가 좀처럼 어려웠어. 그래서 이렇게 받아쳤지. \"그래, 어머니, 아무리 모질게 퇴짜를 놓는다 한들 그런 결과가 정말 따라오겠어요?\""]], [["‘Upon my word, my dear, I don’t know,’ returned Mrs.", "\"글쎄다, 얘야, 나도 모르겠다.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어."], ["Nickleby; ‘really, I don’t know.", "\"정말이지 모르겠어. 그저께 신문에, 프랑스 신문에서 옮겨 온 기사가 하나 있었던 게 분명해."], ["I am sure there was a case in the day before yesterday’s paper, extracted from one of the French newspapers, about a journeyman shoemaker who was jealous of a young girl in an adjoining village, because she wouldn’t shut herself up in an air-tight three-pair-of-stairs, and charcoal herself to death with him; and who went and hid himself in a wood with a sharp-pointed knife, and rushed out, as she was passing by with a few friends, and killed himself first, and then all the friends, and then her--no, killed all the friends first, and then herself, and then HIMself--which it is quite frightful to think of.", "어느 구두장이 직공 얘긴데, 이웃 마을 처녀 하나한테 질투가 나서 그랬다는 거야. 그 처녀가 그 사람하고 함께 공기 안 통하는 사 층 방에 틀어박혀서 숯을 피워 같이 죽기를 마다했다는 이유로 말이야."], ["Somehow or other,’ added Mrs.", "그래서 그 사람이 끝이 뾰족한 칼을 들고 숲속에 숨어 있다가, 그 처녀가 친구 몇이랑 지나갈 때 뛰쳐나와서, 자기가 먼저 죽고, 그다음 친구들을 다 죽이고, 그다음 그 처녀를 죽였다는 거야. 아니, 친구들을 먼저 다 죽이고, 그다음 자기가 죽고, 그다음 그 처녀를 — 생각만 해도 아주 끔찍하다."], ["Nickleby, after a momentary pause, ‘they always ARE journeyman shoemakers who do these things in France, according to the papers.", "그런데 어찌 된 영문인지,\" 니클비 부인이 잠시 말을 멈췄다가 덧붙였어."], ["I don’t know how it is--something in the leather, I suppose.’", "\"신문을 보면 프랑스에서 이런 짓을 하는 건 늘 구두장이 직공들이더구나. 왜 그런지는 모르겠다만 — 가죽에 뭔가 있나 보지.\""]], [["‘But this man, who is not a shoemaker--what has he done, mother, what has he said?’ inquired Nicholas, fretted almost beyond endurance, but looking nearly as resigned and patient as Mrs.", "\"그런데 그 사람은 구두장이도 아니잖아요 — 그 사람이 뭘 했어요, 어머니, 뭐라고 했어요?\""], ["Nickleby herself.", "니콜라스가 물었어."], ["‘You know, there is no language of vegetables, which converts a cucumber into a formal declaration of attachment.’", "더는 참기 어려울 만큼 안달이 났지만, 겉으로는 니클비 부인 못지않게 체념한 듯 참을성 있게 보이면서 말이야. \"아시다시피, 오이를 정식 사랑 고백으로 바꿔 주는 채소의 언어 같은 건 없잖아요.\""]], [["‘My dear,’ replied Mrs.", "\"얘야,\""], ["Nickleby, tossing her head and looking at the ashes in the grate, ‘he has done and said all sorts of things.’", "니클비 부인이 고개를 까딱 젖히고 난로 속 재를 바라보며 대답했어. \"그 사람은 별의별 짓을 다 하고, 별의별 말을 다 했어.\""]], [["‘Is there no mistake on your part?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"어머니가 잘못 보신 건 아니고요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘Mistake!’ cried Mrs. Nickleby.", "\"잘못 보다니!\""], ["‘Lord, Nicholas my dear, do you suppose I don’t know when a man’s in earnest?’", "니클비 부인이 외쳤어. \"아이고, 니콜라스, 남자가 진심인지 아닌지 내가 모를 성싶으냐?\""]], [["‘Well, well!’ muttered Nicholas.", "\"알았어요, 알았어요!\" 니콜라스가 중얼거렸어."]], [["‘Every time I go to the window,’ said Mrs.", "\"내가 창가에 갈 때마다,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby, ‘he kisses one hand, and lays the other upon his heart--of course it’s very foolish of him to do so, and I dare say you’ll say it’s very wrong, but he does it very respectfully--very respectfully indeed--and very tenderly, extremely tenderly.", "\"그 사람은 한 손에 입을 맞추고 다른 손은 가슴에 얹는단다. 물론 그러는 게 아주 어리석은 짓이고, 너는 분명 아주 잘못된 거라고 하겠지만, 그래도 그 사람이 그러는 게 아주 공손하단다."], ["So far, he deserves the greatest credit; there can be no doubt about that.", "정말이지 아주 공손해. 그리고 아주 다정하지, 더없이 다정해."], ["Then, there are the presents which come pouring over the wall every day, and very fine they certainly are, very fine; we had one of the cucumbers at dinner yesterday, and think of pickling the rest for next winter.", "거기까지만 보면 칭찬받아 마땅하고, 그건 두말할 나위가 없어. 그리고 날마다 담 너머로 쏟아져 오는 선물들도 있는데, 정말이지 아주 훌륭해, 아주 훌륭하단다."], ["And last evening,’ added Mrs.", "어제 저녁상에 그 오이 하나를 먹었고, 나머지는 다음 겨울에 쓰게 절여 둘까 한다. 그리고 어젯밤에는,\""], ["Nickleby, with increased confusion, ‘he called gently over the wall, as I was walking in the garden, and proposed marriage, and an elopement.", "니클비 부인이 한층 더 당황해하며 덧붙였어. \"내가 정원을 거닐고 있는데 그 사람이 담 너머로 가만히 부르더니, 결혼하자고, 같이 도망가자고 청하더구나."], ["His voice is as clear as a bell or a musical glass--very like a musical glass indeed--but of course I didn’t listen to it.", "그 사람 목소리가 종소리나 유리 악기 소리처럼 맑아. 정말이지 유리 악기 소리하고 꼭 닮았어."], ["Then, the question is, Nicholas my dear, what am I to do?’", "그래도 물론 나는 귀담아듣지 않았지. 그래서 문제는, 니콜라스, 내가 어떡해야 하느냐는 거다.\""]], [["‘Does Kate know of this?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"케이트도 이걸 알아요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘I have not said a word about it yet,’ answered his mother.", "\"아직 한마디도 안 했다.\" 그의 어머니가 대답했어."]], [["‘Then, for Heaven’s sake,’ rejoined Nicholas, rising, ‘do not, for it would make her very unhappy.", "\"그렇다면, 제발,\" 니콜라스가 일어서며 받았어."], ["And with regard to what you should do, my dear mother, do what your good sense and feeling, and respect for my father’s memory, would prompt.", "\"말하지 마세요. 그 애가 무척 마음 아파할 테니까요."], ["There are a thousand ways in which you can show your dislike of these preposterous and doting attentions.", "그리고 어머니가 어떡하셔야 하느냐 하면, 어머니, 어머니의 그 분별과 마음씀씀이가, 또 아버지를 기리는 마음이 시키는 대로 하세요."], ["If you act as decidedly as you ought and they are still continued, and to your annoyance, I can speedily put a stop to them.", "이 터무니없고 노망난 마음 씀을 싫어한다는 걸 드러낼 방법이야 수천 가지나 있어요. 어머니가 마땅히 그래야 하는 만큼 단호하게 처신하셨는데도 그게 계속되고, 그래서 어머니를 성가시게 한다면, 제가 당장 끝장내 드릴게요."], ["But I should not interfere in a matter so ridiculous, and attach importance to it, until you have vindicated yourself.", "하지만 이렇게 우스꽝스러운 일에 제가 끼어들어서 거기다 무게를 실어 주는 짓은, 어머니가 먼저 스스로 떳떳하게 처신하시기 전에는 하지 않을래요."], ["Most women can do that, but especially one of your age and condition, in circumstances like these, which are unworthy of a serious thought.", "여자들 대부분이 그쯤은 할 수 있고, 더구나 어머니 연세에 어머니 처지라면 더더욱 그렇죠. 게다가 진지하게 생각할 가치도 없는 이런 일에서는 말이에요."], ["I would not shame you by seeming to take them to heart, or treat them earnestly for an instant.", "제가 이걸 마음에 담아 두는 척하거나 단 한순간이라도 진지하게 받아들여서 어머니를 부끄럽게 하지는 않을 거예요."], ["Absurd old idiot!’", "어처구니없는 늙은 멍청이 같으니!\""]], [["So saying, Nicholas kissed his mother, and bade her good-night, and they retired to their respective chambers.", "이렇게 말하고서, 니콜라스는 어머니에게 입을 맞추고 잘 자라는 인사를 했어. 그리고 두 사람은 각자의 방으로 물러갔지."]], [["To do Mrs. Nickleby justice, her attachment to her children would have prevented her seriously contemplating a second marriage, even if she could have so far conquered her recollections of her late husband as to have any strong inclinations that way.", "니클비 부인을 위해 공정하게 말하자면, 그녀가 자식들을 향해 품은 정만으로도 재혼을 진지하게 고려하는 일은 없었을 거야."], ["But, although there was no evil and little real selfishness in Mrs.", "설령 죽은 남편에 대한 기억을 이겨 내고 그쪽으로 강한 마음이 동했다 하더라도 말이지."], ["Nickleby’s heart, she had a weak head and a vain one; and there was something so flattering in being sought (and vainly sought) in marriage at this time of day, that she could not dismiss the passion of the unknown gentleman quite so summarily or lightly as Nicholas appeared to deem becoming.", "그래도 니클비 부인의 마음에는 악의가 없고 진짜 이기심도 거의 없었지만, 그녀는 머리가 모자라고 또 허영심이 있었어. 그리고 이 나이에 와서 청혼을 받는다는 것에는 — 그것도 헛된 청혼이긴 했지만 — 어찌나 우쭐해지는 데가 있었던지, 니콜라스가 마땅하다고 여기는 듯한 그런 식으로, 그 정체 모를 신사의 연정을 그렇게 단번에 또 가볍게 떨쳐 버릴 수가 없었어."]], [["‘As to its being preposterous, and doting, and ridiculous,’ thought Mrs Nickleby, communing with herself in her own room, ‘I don’t see that, at all.", "'그게 터무니없고, 노망났고, 우스꽝스럽다는 것에 대해선,' 니클비 부인이 제 방에서 혼자 곰곰이 따져 보며 생각했어."], ["It’s hopeless on his part, certainly; but why he should be an absurd old idiot, I confess I don’t see.", "'나는 도무지 그렇게 안 보이는걸. 그 사람 쪽에서야 가망 없는 일인 건 분명하지."], ["He is not to be supposed to know it’s hopeless.", "하지만 왜 그 사람이 어처구니없는 늙은 멍청이여야 하는지, 솔직히 나는 모르겠어. 그 사람이 가망 없는 일이란 걸 안다고 볼 수도 없잖아."], ["Poor fellow!", "가엾은 사람!"], ["He is to be pitied, I think!’", "내 생각엔 측은히 여겨야 할 사람이야!'"]], [["Having made these reflections, Mrs. Nickleby looked in her little dressing-glass, and walking backward a few steps from it, tried to remember who it was who used to say that when Nicholas was one-and-twenty he would have more the appearance of her brother than her son.", "이렇게 곰곰이 생각하고 나서, 니클비 부인은 자그마한 화장 거울을 들여다보고는, 거기서 몇 걸음 뒤로 물러서서, 니콜라스가 스물한 살이 되면 제 아들이라기보다 오라비처럼 보일 거라고 누가 그러더라, 그게 누구였는지 떠올려 보려 애썼어."], ["Not being able to call the authority to mind, she extinguished her candle, and drew up the window-blind to admit the light of morning, which had, by this time, begun to dawn.", "그 말을 한 사람이 누구인지 도무지 생각나지 않자, 그녀는 촛불을 끄고 창문 가리개를 걷어, 이 무렵 이미 동트기 시작한 아침 햇살을 들였어."]], [["‘It’s a bad light to distinguish objects in,’ murmured Mrs.", "\"물건을 알아보기엔 영 어두운 빛이네.\""], ["Nickleby, peering into the garden, ‘and my eyes are not very good--I was short-sighted from a child--but, upon my word, I think there’s another large vegetable marrow sticking, at this moment, on the broken glass bottles at the top of the wall!’", "니클비 부인이 정원을 들여다보며 중얼거렸어. \"내 눈이 그리 좋지도 않고 — 어려서부터 근시였으니까 — 그래도 정말이지, 지금 이 순간 담 꼭대기 깨진 유리병 위에 큼지막한 호박이 또 하나 떡하니 박혀 있는 것 같단 말이야!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 38", "# CHAPTER 38"]], [["Comprises certain Particulars arising out of a Visit of Condolence, which may prove important hereafter.", "조의를 표하러 간 방문에서 비롯된 몇 가지 사정이 담겨 있는데, 이건 나중에 중요하게 밝혀질지도 몰라."], ["Smike unexpectedly encounters a very old Friend, who invites him to his House, and will take no Denial", "스마이크는 뜻밖에 아주 오래된 친구와 마주치는데, 그 친구는 자기 집으로 가자며 어떤 거절도 듣지 않으려 들어."]], [["Quite unconscious of the demonstrations of their amorous neighbour, or their effects upon the susceptible bosom of her mama, Kate Nickleby had, by this time, begun to enjoy a settled feeling of tranquillity and happiness, to which, even in occasional and transitory glimpses, she had long been a stranger.", "연정에 빠진 이웃의 그 갖가지 행동도, 그것이 케이트 어머니의 여린 가슴에 미친 영향도 케이트 니클비는 전혀 모르고 있었어. 그러는 동안 케이트는 차분하고 행복한 기분을 느끼기 시작했어."], ["Living under the same roof with the beloved brother from whom she had been so suddenly and hardly separated: with a mind at ease, and free from any persecutions which could call a blush into her cheek, or a pang into her heart, she seemed to have passed into a new state of being.", "오래도록 가끔, 그것도 잠깐씩만 맛볼 수 있었던 그런 마음이었지. 그토록 갑작스럽고 모질게 떨어져 있어야 했던 사랑하는 오빠와 한 지붕 아래 살게 되었고, 마음이 편안했고, 뺨을 붉히게 하거나 가슴을 찌르는 어떤 괴롭힘에서도 벗어나 있으니, 마치 새로운 삶으로 옮겨 온 것 같았어."], ["Her former cheerfulness was restored, her step regained its elasticity and lightness, the colour which had forsaken her cheek visited it once again, and Kate Nickleby looked more beautiful than ever.", "옛날의 명랑함이 되살아났고, 발걸음은 다시 탄력 있고 가벼워졌어. 뺨을 떠났던 핏기가 다시 찾아왔고, 케이트 니클비는 그 어느 때보다 아름다워 보였어."]], [["Such was the result to which Miss La Creevy’s ruminations and observations led her, when the cottage had been, as she emphatically said, ‘thoroughly got to rights, from the chimney-pots to the street-door scraper,’ and the busy little woman had at length a moment’s time to think about its inmates.", "라 크리비 양이 곰곰이 생각하고 살펴본 끝에 이른 결론이 바로 그거였어. 그녀가 힘주어 말했듯이 오두막이 \"굴뚝 꼭대기부터 현관문 흙털개까지 구석구석 말끔하게 정리되고\" 나서, 이 부지런한 자그마한 여인이 마침내 잠깐 짬을 내어 집 식구들 생각을 할 수 있게 되었을 때 말이야."]], [["‘Which I declare I haven’t had since I first came down here,’ said Miss La Creevy; ‘for I have thought of nothing but hammers, nails, screwdrivers, and gimlets, morning, noon, and night.’", "\"정말이지 여기 처음 내려온 뒤로 그럴 틈이 한 번도 없었거든요.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어. \"아침이고 낮이고 밤이고 망치며 못이며 드라이버며 송곳 생각밖에 안 했으니까요.\""]], [["‘You never bestowed one thought upon yourself, I believe,’ returned Kate, smiling.", "\"정작 자기 생각은 한 번도 안 하셨겠네요.\" 케이트가 미소 지으며 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Upon my word, my dear, when there are so many pleasanter things to think of, I should be a goose if I did,’ said Miss La Creevy.", "\"세상에, 얘야, 생각할 즐거운 일이 그렇게 많은데 내 생각이나 하고 있으면 그게 바보지.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어."], ["‘By-the-bye, I HAVE thought of somebody too.", "\"그런데 말이지, 나도 누구 생각을 좀 하긴 했단다. 너 그거 아니?"], ["Do you know, that I observe a great change in one of this family--a very extraordinary change?’", "이 집 식구 중 한 사람한테서 큰 변화를 봤어. 아주 예사롭지 않은 변화 말이야.\""]], [["‘In whom?’ asked Kate, anxiously.", "\"누구한테요?\" 케이트가 걱정스레 물었어."], ["‘Not in--’", "\"설마—\""]], [["‘Not in your brother, my dear,’ returned Miss La Creevy, anticipating the close of the sentence, ‘for he is always the same affectionate good-natured clever creature, with a spice of the--I won’t say who--in him when there’s any occasion, that he was when I first knew you. No. Smike, as he WILL be called, poor fellow! for he won’t hear of a MR before his name, is greatly altered, even in this short time.’", "\"네 오빠는 아니란다, 얘야.\" 라 크리비 양이 말끝을 미리 짐작하고 대꾸했어. \"오빠야 처음 너희를 알았을 때나 지금이나 늘 한결같이 다정하고 마음씨 좋고 영리한 사람이지. 필요할 땐 그 누구라고는 말 안 하겠지만, 그 사람 닮은 매운맛도 좀 섞여 있고 말이야. 아니. 스마이크 말이야, 가엾게도 다들 그렇게만 부르지! 자기 이름 앞에 '미스터' 붙이는 걸 도무지 들으려 하질 않으니까. 그 애가 이 짧은 시간에도 아주 많이 달라졌어.\""]], [["‘How?’ asked Kate.", "\"어떻게요?\" 케이트가 물었어."], ["‘Not in health?’", "\"몸이 안 좋은 건 아니죠?\""]], [["‘N--n--o; perhaps not in health exactly,’ said Miss La Creevy, pausing to consider, ‘although he is a worn and feeble creature, and has that in his face which it would wring my heart to see in yours. No; not in health.’", "\"아, 아니. 꼭 몸이 안 좋은 건 아닐지도 몰라.\" 라 크리비 양이 잠깐 멈춰 생각하며 말했어. \"물론 야위고 기운 없는 애이긴 하고, 그 얼굴에는 만약 네 얼굴에 그런 게 있으면 내 가슴이 미어질 그런 게 어려 있긴 하지만. 아니, 몸이 안 좋은 건 아니야.\""]], [["‘How then?’", "\"그럼 뭔데요?\""]], [["‘I scarcely know,’ said the miniature painter.", "\"나도 잘 모르겠어.\" 초상화가가 말했어."], ["‘But I have watched him, and he has brought the tears into my eyes many times.", "\"그렇지만 그 애를 지켜봤는데, 몇 번이나 내 눈에 눈물이 핑 돌게 하더구나."], ["It is not a very difficult matter to do that, certainly, for I am easily melted; still I think these came with good cause and reason.", "하긴 나야 워낙 잘 우니까 그게 그리 어려운 일은 아니지만, 그래도 이번엔 그럴 만한 까닭이 있어서 그랬던 것 같아."], ["I am sure that since he has been here, he has grown, from some strong cause, more conscious of his weak intellect.", "그 애가 여기 온 뒤로 무슨 강한 이유 때문인지, 자기 머리가 모자라다는 걸 점점 더 의식하게 된 게 분명해."], ["He feels it more.", "그걸 더 절실히 느끼는 거야."], ["It gives him greater pain to know that he wanders sometimes, and cannot understand very simple things.", "가끔 정신이 멍하고 아주 간단한 것도 이해 못 한다는 걸 알게 되니까 더 괴로운 거지. 네가 없을 때 그 애를 지켜봤단다, 얘야."], ["I have watched him when you have not been by, my dear, sit brooding by himself, with such a look of pain as I could scarcely bear to see, and then get up and leave the room: so sorrowfully, and in such dejection, that I cannot tell you how it has hurt me.", "혼자 앉아서 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있는데, 그 괴로운 표정이 어찌나 보기 힘들던지. 그러다 일어나서 방을 나가는데, 어찌나 서글프고 풀이 죽었던지, 내 마음이 얼마나 아팠는지 말로 다 못 한다."], ["Not three weeks ago, he was a light-hearted busy creature, overjoyed to be in a bustle, and as happy as the day was long.", "삼 주도 안 됐는데, 그땐 마음 가벼운, 부지런한 애였잖아. 북적거리는 게 좋아서 신나하고, 온종일 행복해하던 애였는데."], ["Now, he is another being--the same willing, harmless, faithful, loving creature--but the same in nothing else.’", "지금은 딴사람이 됐어. 똑같이 잘 도우려 하고, 남 해칠 줄 모르고, 충직하고 사랑스럽긴 한데, 그것 말고는 다 변해 버렸어.\""]], [["‘Surely this will all pass off,’ said Kate.", "\"분명 다 지나갈 거예요.\" 케이트가 말했어."], ["‘Poor fellow!’", "\"가엾은 애!\""]], [["‘I hope,’ returned her little friend, with a gravity very unusual in her, ‘it may. I hope, for the sake of that poor lad, it may. However,’ said Miss La Creevy, relapsing into the cheerful, chattering tone, which was habitual to her, ‘I have said my say, and a very long say it is, and a very wrong say too, I shouldn’t wonder at all. I shall cheer him up tonight, at all events, for if he is to be my squire all the way to the Strand, I shall talk on, and on, and on, and never leave off, till I have roused him into a laugh at something. So the sooner he goes, the better for him, and the sooner I go, the better for me, I am sure, or else I shall have my maid gallivanting with somebody who may rob the house--though what there is to take away, besides tables and chairs, I don’t know, except the miniatures: and he is a clever thief who can dispose of them to any great advantage, for I can’t, I know, and that’s the honest truth.’", "\"그러길 바라.\" 그녀의 자그마한 친구가, 평소답지 않게 무거운 어조로 대꾸했어. \"그러길 바란다. 그 가엾은 애를 위해서라도 그러길 바라. 어쨌든,\" 라 크리비 양이 평소 버릇대로 명랑하게 재잘대는 말투로 돌아오며 말했어. \"내 할 말은 다 했네. 어찌나 긴 말이었는지, 게다가 틀린 말도 많았을 거야, 하나도 놀랍지 않아. 어쨌든 오늘 밤엔 그 애 기운을 좀 북돋워 줄 거야. 스트랜드까지 내내 나를 바래다주는 호위 기사가 되어 줄 거라면, 뭔가에 웃음이 터질 때까지 쉴 새 없이 떠들고 또 떠들고 멈추지 않을 거니까. 그러니 그 애는 빨리 갈수록 좋고, 나도 빨리 갈수록 좋아. 안 그러면 우리 집 하녀가 누군가하고 싸돌아다니다가 집을 털릴지도 모르거든. 하긴 탁자랑 의자 말고 가져갈 게 뭐가 있을지 모르겠지만. 초상화들 빼고 말이야. 그런데 그걸 어디다 비싸게 팔아넘길 수 있는 사람이라면 정말 대단한 도둑이지. 나도 못 하는걸. 그게 솔직한 사실이야.\""]], [["So saying, little Miss La Creevy hid her face in a very flat bonnet, and herself in a very big shawl; and fixing herself tightly into the latter, by means of a large pin, declared that the omnibus might come as soon as it pleased, for she was quite ready.", "이렇게 말하면서 자그마한 라 크리비 양은 아주 납작한 보닛 속에 얼굴을 파묻고, 아주 큰 숄로 몸을 감쌌어. 그러고는 커다란 핀으로 그 숄을 단단히 여미더니, 자기는 채비가 다 됐으니 합승마차가 오고 싶을 때 와도 좋다고 선언했어."]], [["But there was still Mrs.", "하지만 아직 니클비 부인하고 작별 인사를 해야 했지."], ["Nickleby to take leave of; and long before that good lady had concluded some reminiscences bearing upon, and appropriate to, the occasion, the omnibus arrived.", "그리고 이 마음씨 좋은 부인이 이 자리에 어울리는 옛 추억담을 미처 마치기도 전에 합승마차가 도착했어. 이 바람에 라 크리비 양은 몹시 부산해졌고, 그 통에 현관문 뒤에서 하녀에게 몰래 십팔 펜스를 쥐여 주려다가 그만 손지갑에서 십 펜스어치 반 페니 동전들을 쏟아 버렸어."], ["This put Miss La Creevy in a great bustle, in consequence whereof, as she secretly rewarded the servant girl with eighteen-pence behind the street-door, she pulled out of her reticule ten-pennyworth of halfpence, which rolled into all possible corners of the passage, and occupied some considerable time in the picking up.", "동전들이 복도 구석구석으로 다 굴러가 버려서, 그걸 줍느라 꽤 오랜 시간이 걸렸지. 이 의식이 끝나고 나니 당연히 케이트와 니클비 부인에게 두 번째로 입을 맞추고, 작은 바구니와 갈색 종이 꾸러미를 챙기는 일이 뒤따랐어."], ["This ceremony had, of course, to be succeeded by a second kissing of Kate and Mrs.", "그러는 동안에도 라 크리비 양 말마따나 \"그 합승마차가 어찌나 끔찍하게 욕을 해 대는지, 듣기에 정말 무서울 지경\"이었지. 마침내 마차가 떠나려는 시늉을 하자, 라 크리비 양이 후다닥 뛰쳐나갔다가 후다닥 올라타며 승객 모두에게 청산유수로 사과를 했어."], ["Nickleby, and a gathering together of the little basket and the brown-paper parcel, during which proceedings, ‘the omnibus,’ as Miss La Creevy protested, ‘swore so dreadfully, that it was quite awful to hear it.’ At length and at last, it made a feint of going away, and then Miss La Creevy darted out, and darted in, apologising with great volubility to all the passengers, and declaring that she wouldn’t purposely have kept them waiting on any account whatever.", "일부러 기다리게 한 건 절대로, 무슨 일이 있어도 아니라고 우기면서 말이야. 그녀가 앉을 만한 자리를 두리번거리는 사이, 차장이 스마이크를 밀어 넣고는 다 됐다고 외쳤어."], ["While she was looking about for a convenient seat, the conductor pushed Smike in, and cried that it was all right--though it wasn’t--and away went the huge vehicle, with the noise of half-a-dozen brewers’ drays at least.", "사실은 안 됐는데도 말이야. 그러고는 그 거대한 마차가 맥주 양조장 짐수레 적어도 대여섯 대는 굴러가는 듯한 요란한 소리를 내며 떠나갔어."]], [["Leaving it to pursue its journey at the pleasure of the conductor aforementioned, who lounged gracefully on his little shelf behind, smoking an odoriferous cigar; and leaving it to stop, or go on, or gallop, or crawl, as that gentleman deemed expedient and advisable; this narrative may embrace the opportunity of ascertaining the condition of Sir Mulberry Hawk, and to what extent he had, by this time, recovered from the injuries consequent on being flung violently from his cabriolet, under the circumstances already detailed.", "그 마차는 앞서 말한 차장이 가고 싶은 대로 가게 내버려 두자. 그 차장은 뒤쪽 자기 작은 발판에 우아하게 기대어 향내 나는 시가를 피우고 있었지. 그가 적당하다 싶고 마땅하다 싶을 때 마차를 세우든 가든 내달리든 기어가든 마음대로 하게 내버려 두고, 이 이야기는 이참에 멀버리 호크 경의 상태가 어떤지, 앞서 자세히 이야기한 그 상황에서 이륜마차에서 거칠게 내동댕이쳐져 입은 부상을 그가 이제 얼마나 회복했는지 살펴보기로 하자."]], [["With a shattered limb, a body severely bruised, a face disfigured by half-healed scars, and pallid from the exhaustion of recent pain and fever, Sir Mulberry Hawk lay stretched upon his back, on the couch to which he was doomed to be a prisoner for some weeks yet to come.", "다리가 부러지고, 몸은 심하게 멍들고, 얼굴은 아물다 만 흉터로 일그러지고, 최근의 고통과 열병으로 기진맥진해 핼쑥해진 채, 멀버리 호크 경은 등을 대고 길게 누워 있었어."], ["Mr. Pyke and Mr.", "앞으로도 몇 주는 더 갇혀 지내야 할 운명인 그 긴 의자에 말이야."], ["Pluck sat drinking hard in the next room, now and then varying the monotonous murmurs of their conversation with a half-smothered laugh, while the young lord--the only member of the party who was not thoroughly irredeemable, and who really had a kind heart--sat beside his Mentor, with a cigar in his mouth, and read to him, by the light of a lamp, such scraps of intelligence from a paper of the day, as were most likely to yield him interest or amusement.", "파이크 씨와 플럭 씨는 옆방에 앉아 진탕 술을 마시며, 단조롭게 웅얼대던 대화에 이따금 반쯤 죽인 웃음소리를 섞어 넣고 있었어. 한편 젊은 귀족은, 이 패거리 가운데 유일하게 완전히 구제 불능은 아니고 정말로 마음씨가 따뜻한 사람이었는데, 입에 시가를 문 채 스승 곁에 앉아 그날 신문에서 그가 흥미나 재미를 느낄 만한 기삿거리를 골라 등불 빛에 비춰 읽어 주고 있었어."]], [["‘Curse those hounds!’ said the invalid, turning his head impatiently towards the adjoining room; ‘will nothing stop their infernal throats?’", "\"저 개자식들 같으니!\" 환자가 옆방 쪽으로 짜증스럽게 고개를 돌리며 말했어. \"저 빌어먹을 목구멍을 막을 방법은 없는 거냐?\""]], [["Messrs Pyke and Pluck heard the exclamation, and stopped immediately: winking to each other as they did so, and filling their glasses to the brim, as some recompense for the deprivation of speech.", "파이크 씨와 플럭 씨는 그 외침을 듣고 곧바로 입을 다물었어. 그러면서 서로 눈짓을 주고받았지. 그리고 말을 빼앗긴 데 대한 보상이라도 되는 양 잔을 가장자리까지 가득 채웠어."]], [["‘Damn!’ muttered the sick man between his teeth, and writhing impatiently in his bed.", "\"제기랄!\" 병든 사내가 이를 악물고 중얼거리며 침대에서 짜증스럽게 몸부림쳤어."], ["‘Isn’t this mattress hard enough, and the room dull enough, and pain bad enough, but THEY must torture me?", "\"이 매트리스는 충분히 딱딱하지 않고, 이 방은 충분히 따분하지 않고, 통증은 충분히 지독하지 않다는 거냐. 저것들이 기어이 날 괴롭혀야겠어?"], ["What’s the time?’", "지금 몇 시야?\""]], [["‘Half-past eight,’ replied his friend.", "\"여덟 시 반일세.\" 친구가 대답했어."]], [["‘Here, draw the table nearer, and let us have the cards again,’ said Sir Mulberry.", "\"이리, 탁자를 더 가까이 끌어다 놓고, 다시 카드를 하지.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어."], ["‘More piquet.", "\"피켓 한 판 더."], ["Come.’", "자.\""]], [["It was curious to see how eagerly the sick man, debarred from any change of position save the mere turning of his head from side to side, watched every motion of his friend in the progress of the game; and with what eagerness and interest he played, and yet how warily and coolly.", "병든 사내가 고개를 좌우로 돌리는 것 말고는 자세를 바꿀 수도 없는 처지인데, 게임이 진행되는 내내 친구의 동작 하나하나를 얼마나 열심히 지켜보던지, 또 얼마나 열의와 흥미를 가지고 게임을 하면서도 얼마나 신중하고 침착하던지, 보고 있으면 묘했어. 그의 수완과 솜씨는 상대보다 스무 배는 더 나았어."], ["His address and skill were more than twenty times a match for his adversary, who could make little head against them, even when fortune favoured him with good cards, which was not often the case.", "상대는 운이 좋아 좋은 패가 들어와도 — 그런 일은 자주 없었지만 — 도무지 당해 낼 수가 없었지. 멀버리 경은 매 판을 이겼어."], ["Sir Mulberry won every game; and when his companion threw down the cards, and refused to play any longer, thrust forth his wasted arm and caught up the stakes with a boastful oath, and the same hoarse laugh, though considerably lowered in tone, that had resounded in Ralph Nickleby’s dining-room, months before.", "그러다 상대가 카드를 던지고 더는 안 하겠다고 하자, 그는 야윈 팔을 쭉 뻗어 판돈을 그러쥐며 으스대듯 욕설을 내뱉었어. 그리고 몇 달 전 랄프 니클비의 식당에서 울려 퍼졌던 것과 똑같은 그 쉰 웃음소리를, 비록 톤은 꽤 낮아졌지만 똑같이 내뱉었지."]], [["While he was thus occupied, his man appeared, to announce that Mr. Ralph Nickleby was below, and wished to know how he was, tonight.", "이러고 있는데 그의 하인이 나타나, 랄프 니클비 씨가 아래층에 와서 오늘 밤 그가 어떤지 알고 싶어 한다고 알렸어."]], [["‘Better,’ said Sir Mulberry, impatiently.", "\"나아졌다고 해.\" 멀버리 경이 짜증스럽게 말했어."]], [["‘Mr. Nickleby wishes to know, sir--’", "\"니클비 씨가 알고 싶어 하시는데요, 나리—\""]], [["‘I tell you, better,’ replied Sir Mulberry, striking his hand upon the table.", "\"나아졌다니까.\" 멀버리 경이 탁자를 손바닥으로 내리치며 대꾸했어."]], [["The man hesitated for a moment or two, and then said that Mr. Nickleby had requested permission to see Sir Mulberry Hawk, if it was not inconvenient.", "하인은 잠깐 한두 차례 머뭇거리더니, 니클비 씨가 불편하지 않으시다면 멀버리 호크 경을 뵙게 해 달라고 청한다고 말했어."]], [["‘It IS inconvenient.", "\"불편해. 못 봐."], ["I can’t see him.", "아무도 못 만나.\""], ["I can’t see anybody,’ said his master, more violently than before.", "주인이 아까보다 더 거칠게 말했어."], ["‘You know that, you blockhead.’", "\"알면서 그러는 거냐, 이 멍청아.\""]], [["‘I am very sorry, sir,’ returned the man.", "\"정말 죄송합니다, 나리.\" 하인이 대꾸했어."], ["‘But Mr. Nickleby pressed so much, sir--’", "\"그런데 니클비 씨가 하도 졸라 대셔서요, 나리—\""]], [["The fact was, that Ralph Nickleby had bribed the man, who, being anxious to earn his money with a view to future favours, held the door in his hand, and ventured to linger still.", "사실 랄프 니클비가 하인에게 돈을 찔러 준 거였어. 하인은 그 돈을 벌어 두면 앞으로 또 잘 봐줄까 싶어, 문손잡이를 잡은 채 감히 더 어물거리고 있었던 거지."]], [["‘Did he say whether he had any business to speak about?’ inquired Sir Mulberry, after a little impatient consideration.", "\"무슨 볼일이 있어서 이야기하고 싶다던가?\" 멀버리 경이 잠깐 짜증스럽게 생각하더니 물었어."]], [["‘No, sir.", "\"아니요, 나리."], ["He said he wished to see you, sir.", "그냥 나리를 뵙고 싶다고만 하셨습니다."], ["Particularly, Mr. Nickleby said, sir.’", "니클비 씨가 특별히 그렇게 말씀하셨습니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘Tell him to come up.", "\"올라오라고 해. 이봐.\""], ["Here,’ cried Sir Mulberry, calling the man back, as he passed his hand over his disfigured face, ‘move that lamp, and put it on the stand behind me.", "멀버리 경이 일그러진 얼굴을 손으로 쓸며, 나가려는 하인을 다시 불렀어. \"저 등불을 옮겨서 내 뒤 받침대에 놓아."], ["Wheel that table away, and place a chair there--further off.", "저 탁자는 치우고, 의자 하나를 거기다 둬. 더 멀찍이."], ["Leave it so.’", "그렇게 둬.\""]], [["The man obeyed these directions as if he quite comprehended the motive with which they were dictated, and left the room.", "하인은 그 지시에 담긴 의도를 다 알아챘다는 듯 그대로 따르고는 방을 나갔어."], ["Lord Frederick Verisopht, remarking that he would look in presently, strolled into the adjoining apartment, and closed the folding door behind him.", "프레더릭 베리소프트 경은 곧 다시 들여다보겠다고 말하며 옆방으로 어슬렁어슬렁 들어가 접이문을 닫았어."]], [["Then was heard a subdued footstep on the stairs; and Ralph Nickleby, hat in hand, crept softly into the room, with his body bent forward as if in profound respect, and his eyes fixed upon the face of his worthy client.", "그러자 계단에서 죽인 발소리가 들려왔어. 그리고 랄프 니클비가 모자를 손에 들고, 깊이 존경한다는 듯 몸을 앞으로 숙인 채, 그 갸륵한 고객의 얼굴에 눈길을 고정하고 살그머니 방으로 들어왔어."]], [["‘Well, Nickleby,’ said Sir Mulberry, motioning him to the chair by the couch side, and waving his hand in assumed carelessness, ‘I have had a bad accident, you see.’", "\"어, 니클비.\" 멀버리 경이 긴 의자 곁의 의자를 가리키며, 짐짓 무심한 척 손을 내저으며 말했어. \"보다시피 큰 사고를 당했지.\""]], [["‘I see,’ rejoined Ralph, with the same steady gaze.", "\"그러게요.\" 랄프가 변함없이 가만히 응시하며 대꾸했어."], ["‘Bad, indeed!", "\"정말 심하군요!"], ["I should not have known you, Sir Mulberry.", "못 알아볼 뻔했습니다, 멀버리 경. 저런, 저런!"], ["Dear, dear! This IS bad.’", "이거 정말 심합니다.\""]], [["Ralph’s manner was one of profound humility and respect; and the low tone of voice was that, which the gentlest consideration for a sick man would have taught a visitor to assume.", "랄프의 태도는 더없이 겸손하고 정중했어. 낮은 목소리는 병자를 가장 자상하게 배려하는 문병객이 짓는 그런 어조였지."], ["But the expression of his face, Sir Mulberry’s being averted, was in extraordinary contrast; and as he stood, in his usual attitude, calmly looking on the prostrate form before him, all that part of his features which was not cast into shadow by his protruding and contracted brows, bore the impress of a sarcastic smile.", "그런데 멀버리 경이 얼굴을 돌리고 있는 사이, 랄프의 얼굴 표정은 그것과 놀랍도록 딴판이었어. 그가 평소 자세 그대로 서서 눈앞에 누운 사람을 차분히 내려다보는데, 튀어나온 채 잔뜩 찌푸린 눈썹 그늘에 가리지 않은 얼굴 부분에는 죄다 비웃는 미소가 어려 있었어."]], [["‘Sit down,’ said Sir Mulberry, turning towards him, as though by a violent effort.", "\"앉지.\" 멀버리 경이 안간힘을 쓰듯 그를 향해 돌아보며 말했어."], ["‘Am I a sight, that you stand gazing there?’", "\"내가 무슨 구경거리라도 되나, 거기 서서 빤히 쳐다보게?\""]], [["As he turned his face, Ralph recoiled a step or two, and making as though he were irresistibly impelled to express astonishment, but was determined not to do so, sat down with well-acted confusion.", "그가 얼굴을 돌리자 랄프는 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러났어. 그러고는 도저히 놀라움을 감출 수 없지만 굳이 내색하지 않으려는 듯 시늉하며, 능청맞게 당황한 척하고 자리에 앉았어."]], [["‘I have inquired at the door, Sir Mulberry, every day,’ said Ralph, ‘twice a day, indeed, at first--and tonight, presuming upon old acquaintance, and past transactions by which we have mutually benefited in some degree, I could not resist soliciting admission to your chamber. Have you--have you suffered much?’ said Ralph, bending forward, and allowing the same harsh smile to gather upon his face, as the other closed his eyes.", "\"매일 문간에서 안부를 여쭈었습니다, 멀버리 경.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"처음엔 하루에 두 번씩이나요. 그리고 오늘 밤엔 오랜 친분과, 우리가 어느 정도 서로 득을 본 지난 거래들을 믿고서, 침실로 들여보내 달라 청하지 않을 수가 없었습니다. 많이, 많이 아프셨습니까?\" 랄프가 몸을 앞으로 숙이며 물었어. 상대가 눈을 감자 그 얼굴에 또다시 모진 미소가 번지도록 내버려 두면서 말이야."]], [["‘More than enough to please me, and less than enough to please some broken-down hacks that you and I know of, and who lay their ruin between us, I dare say,’ returned Sir Mulberry, tossing his arm restlessly upon the coverlet.", "\"내 마음에 들 만큼보다는 더, 그리고 자네하고 내가 아는 어떤 망해 빠진 한물간 자들 마음에 들 만큼보다는 덜 아팠지. 그자들은 제 신세 망친 걸 자네하고 내 탓으로 돌리고 있을 거야, 아마.\" 멀버리 경이 이불 위에서 팔을 안절부절못하며 휘저으며 대꾸했어."]], [["Ralph shrugged his shoulders in deprecation of the intense irritation with which this had been said; for there was an aggravating, cold distinctness in his speech and manner which so grated on the sick man that he could scarcely endure it.", "랄프는 어깨를 으쓱하며, 이 말에 담긴 격한 짜증을 못마땅해하는 시늉을 했어. 랄프의 말투와 태도에는 사람 부아를 돋우는 차갑고 또렷한 데가 있어서, 병든 사내가 도무지 견디기 힘들 만큼 신경을 긁었거든."]], [["‘And what is it in these “past transactions,”", "\"그 '지난 거래들'"], ["that brought you here tonight?’ asked Sir Mulberry.", "중에 대체 뭐 때문에 오늘 밤 여기 온 거지?\" 멀버리 경이 물었어."]], [["‘Nothing,’ replied Ralph.", "\"아무것도 아닙니다.\" 랄프가 대답했어."], ["‘There are some bills of my lord’s which need renewal; but let them be till you are well.", "\"각하의 어음 몇 장이 갱신이 필요하긴 하지만, 그건 나으실 때까지 미뤄 두지요. 저, 저는 왔습니다.\""], ["I--I--came,’ said Ralph, speaking more slowly, and with harsher emphasis, ‘I came to say how grieved I am that any relative of mine, although disowned by me, should have inflicted such punishment on you as--’", "랄프가 더 천천히, 더 모질게 힘주어 말했어. \"제 친척이, 비록 제가 의절한 자이긴 합니다만, 경께 이런 벌을 가했다는 게 얼마나 가슴 아픈지 말씀드리러 왔습니다—\""]], [["‘Punishment!’ interposed Sir Mulberry.", "\"벌이라고!\" 멀버리 경이 끼어들었어."]], [["‘I know it has been a severe one,’ said Ralph, wilfully mistaking the meaning of the interruption, ‘and that has made me the more anxious to tell you that I disown this vagabond--that I acknowledge him as no kin of mine--and that I leave him to take his deserts from you, and every man besides.", "\"가혹한 일이었다는 거 압니다.\" 랄프가 그 말허리를 일부러 곡해하며 말했어."], ["You may wring his neck if you please.", "\"그래서 더더욱 말씀드리고 싶었습니다. 저는 그 떠돌이를 의절했고, 그놈을 제 핏줄로 인정하지 않으며, 경께서든 그 누구에게서든 마땅한 응징을 받도록 내버려 둔다고요."], ["I shall not interfere.’", "원하시면 그놈 목을 비틀어 버리셔도 됩니다. 저는 끼어들지 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["‘This story that they tell me here, has got abroad then, has it?’ asked Sir Mulberry, clenching his hands and teeth.", "\"그럼 여기서 다들 나한테 떠들어 대는 그 이야기가 밖에 다 퍼졌단 말이지, 그래?\" 멀버리 경이 주먹과 이를 악물며 물었어."]], [["‘Noised in all directions,’ replied Ralph. ‘Every club and gaming-room has rung with it. There has been a good song made about it, as I am told,’ said Ralph, looking eagerly at his questioner. ‘I have not heard it myself, not being in the way of such things, but I have been told it’s even printed--for private circulation--but that’s all over town, of course.’", "\"사방에 퍼졌습니다.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"클럽이며 도박장이며 죄다 그 이야기로 시끌벅적합니다. 듣자 하니 그걸로 그럴듯한 노래까지 만들어졌다더군요.\" 랄프가 질문한 사람을 뚫어지게 보며 말했어. \"제가 직접 들어 보진 못했습니다. 그런 데를 다닐 처지가 아니라서요. 그래도 듣기로는 인쇄까지 됐다더군요. 사적으로만 돌려 본다고는 하던데, 그래 봐야 온 장안에 다 퍼진 거나 마찬가지죠.\""]], [["‘It’s a lie!’ said Sir Mulberry; ‘I tell you it’s all a lie.", "\"거짓말이야!\" 멀버리 경이 말했어."], ["The mare took fright.’", "\"전부 거짓말이라니까. 말이 놀란 거라고.\""]], [["‘They SAY he frightened her,’ observed Ralph, in the same unmoved and quiet manner.", "\"그놈이 말을 놀라게 했다더군요.\" 랄프가 여전히 흔들림 없이 차분하게 말했어."], ["‘Some say he frightened you, but THAT’S a lie, I know.", "\"어떤 사람들은 그놈이 경을 겁먹게 했다고도 하던데, 그건 거짓말이지요, 저도 압니다."], ["I have said that boldly--oh, a score of times!", "제가 대놓고 그렇게 말했습니다. 오, 스무 번도 넘게요!"], ["I am a peaceable man, but I can’t hear folks tell that of you.", "저야 화평한 사람이지만, 사람들이 경에 대해 그렇게 떠드는 건 못 듣겠더군요."], ["No, no.’", "아니, 아니고말고요.\""]], [["When Sir Mulberry found coherent words to utter, Ralph bent forward with his hand to his ear, and a face as calm as if its every line of sternness had been cast in iron.", "멀버리 경이 가까스로 말이 될 만한 소리를 짜내자, 랄프는 손을 귀에 갖다 대고 몸을 앞으로 숙였어. 얼굴은 그 모진 선 하나하나가 무쇠로 빚어진 듯 태연했지."]], [["‘When I am off this cursed bed,’ said the invalid, actually striking at his broken leg in the ecstasy of his passion, ‘I’ll have such revenge as never man had yet.", "\"이 빌어먹을 침대에서 일어나기만 하면,\" 환자가 격정에 사로잡혀 정말로 자기 부러진 다리를 후려치며 말했어."], ["By God, I will.", "\"여태 그 누구도 한 적 없는 복수를 해 줄 테다."], ["Accident favouring him, he has marked me for a week or two, but I’ll put a mark on him that he shall carry to his grave.", "맹세코 그러고말고. 운이 그놈 편을 들어 줘서 나한테 한두 주짜리 자국을 남겼다만, 나는 그놈한테 무덤까지 가져갈 자국을 남겨 줄 거야."], ["I’ll slit his nose and ears, flog him, maim him for life.", "코하고 귀를 째 버리고, 채찍질하고, 평생 불구로 만들어 줄 테다."], ["I’ll do more than that; I’ll drag that pattern of chastity, that pink of prudery, the delicate sister, through--’", "그뿐인 줄 알아. 그 정숙함의 본보기, 그 새침함의 극치인 그 어여쁜 누이동생을 끌어다가—\""]], [["It might have been that even Ralph’s cold blood tingled in his cheeks at that moment.", "그 순간 랄프의 차가운 피마저 뺨에서 화끈 달아올랐는지도 몰라."], ["It might have been that Sir Mulberry remembered, that, knave and usurer as he was, he must, in some early time of infancy, have twined his arm about her father’s neck.", "멀버리 경이 문득, 자기가 악당에 고리대금업자이긴 해도 갓난아기였던 그 옛날 언젠가는 그 애 아버지의 목을 끌어안았을 게 분명하다는 사실을 떠올렸는지도 모르지."], ["He stopped, and menacing with his hand, confirmed the unuttered threat with a tremendous oath.", "그는 말을 멈추더니, 손으로 위협하는 시늉을 하며 미처 내뱉지 못한 위협을 무시무시한 욕설로 마무리했어."]], [["‘It is a galling thing,’ said Ralph, after a short term of silence, during which he had eyed the sufferer keenly, ‘to think that the man about town, the rake, the ROUE, the rook of twenty seasons should be brought to this pass by a mere boy!’", "\"부아가 치미는 일이지요.\" 랄프가 잠깐 입을 다물고 있다가 말했어. 그동안 그는 고통받는 사내를 날카롭게 살피고 있었지. \"장안의 한량, 난봉꾼, 탕아, 스무 시즌이나 닳고 닳은 노름꾼이 한낱 애송이 하나한테 이 지경이 되다니, 생각하면 말입니다!\""]], [["Sir Mulberry darted a wrathful look at him, but Ralph’s eyes were bent upon the ground, and his face wore no other expression than one of thoughtfulness.", "멀버리 경이 그에게 분노에 찬 눈길을 쏘았어. 하지만 랄프의 눈은 바닥을 향해 있었고, 그 얼굴에는 그저 생각에 잠긴 표정 말고는 아무것도 없었어."]], [["‘A raw, slight stripling,’ continued Ralph, ‘against a man whose very weight might crush him; to say nothing of his skill in--I am right, I think,’ said Ralph, raising his eyes, ‘you WERE a patron of the ring once, were you not?’", "\"풋내기에 호리호리한 애송이가,\" 랄프가 말을 이었어. \"제 몸무게만으로도 그놈을 깔아뭉갤 수 있는 사내를 상대로 말입니다. 게다가 경의 그 솜씨야 말할 것도 없고요. 제 말이 맞지요, 아마.\" 랄프가 눈을 들며 말했어. \"예전에 권투계 후원자셨지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["The sick man made an impatient gesture, which Ralph chose to consider as one of acquiescence.", "병든 사내가 짜증스러운 몸짓을 했는데, 랄프는 그걸 굳이 수긍의 표시로 받아들이기로 했어."]], [["‘Ha!’ he said, ‘I thought so.", "\"하!\" 그가 말했어."], ["That was before I knew you, but I was pretty sure I couldn’t be mistaken.", "\"그럴 줄 알았습니다. 그건 제가 경을 알기 전 일이지만, 제가 틀릴 리 없다고 꽤나 확신했습니다."], ["He is light and active, I suppose.", "그놈은 가볍고 날쌔겠지요, 아마."], ["But those were slight advantages compared with yours.", "하지만 그런 건 경의 장점에 비하면 보잘것없는 이점이지요."], ["Luck, luck!", "운이지요, 운!"], ["These hang-dog outcasts have it.’", "그 비럭질하는 천덕꾸러기들이 그걸 타고난단 말입니다.\""]], [["‘He’ll need the most he has, when I am well again,’ said Sir Mulberry Hawk, ‘let him fly where he will.’", "\"내가 다시 멀쩡해지면 그놈은 가진 운을 다 끌어 써야 할 거야.\" 멀버리 호크 경이 말했어. \"어디로 달아나든 말이지.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ returned Ralph quickly, ‘he doesn’t dream of that. He is here, good sir, waiting your pleasure, here in London, walking the streets at noonday; carrying it off jauntily; looking for you, I swear,’ said Ralph, his face darkening, and his own hatred getting the upper hand of him, for the first time, as this gay picture of Nicholas presented itself; ‘if we were only citizens of a country where it could be safely done, I’d give good money to have him stabbed to the heart and rolled into the kennel for the dogs to tear.’", "\"오!\" 랄프가 잽싸게 대꾸했어. \"그놈은 그럴 생각이 꿈에도 없습니다. 그놈은 여기, 경께서 손볼 날을 기다리며 여기 런던에 있습니다. 한낮에 버젓이 거리를 활보하면서요. 으스대며 뽐내고 다닙니다. 맹세컨대 경을 찾고 있어요.\" 랄프가 말했어. 이 의기양양한 니콜라스의 모습이 떠오르자 그의 얼굴이 어두워졌고, 난생처음 제 증오심이 그를 휘어잡았지. \"안전하게 그럴 수 있는 나라 사람이기만 하다면, 저는 기꺼이 돈을 들여 그놈 심장에 칼을 꽂고, 개들이 물어뜯게 시궁창에 처박아 버리겠습니다.\""]], [["As Ralph, somewhat to the surprise of his old client, vented this little piece of sound family feeling, and took up his hat preparatory to departing, Lord Frederick Verisopht looked in.", "랄프가 오랜 고객을 다소 놀라게 하면서 이 작은 혈육의 정을 한바탕 쏟아 내고는 떠날 채비로 모자를 집어 들었을 때, 프레더릭 베리소프트 경이 들여다봤어."]], [["‘Why what in the deyvle’s name, Hawk, have you and Nickleby been talking about?’ said the young man.", "\"아니 대체 무슨 얘길 하고 있었던 거야, 호크, 자네하고 니클비 말이야?\" 젊은이가 말했어."], ["‘I neyver heard such an insufferable riot.", "\"이렇게 못 들어 줄 시끄러운 소란은 처음 들어 보네."], ["Croak, croak, croak.", "깍깍, 깍깍, 깍깍."], ["Bow, wow, wow.", "멍멍, 멍멍, 멍멍."], ["What has it all been about?’", "다 무슨 얘기였어?\""]], [["‘Sir Mulberry has been angry, my Lord,’ said Ralph, looking towards the couch.", "\"멀버리 경께서 화가 나셔서요, 각하.\" 랄프가 긴 의자 쪽을 보며 말했어."]], [["‘Not about money, I hope?", "\"돈 얘긴 아니겠지?"], ["Nothing has gone wrong in business, has it, Nickleby?’", "사업에 뭐 잘못된 거 없지, 니클비?\""]], [["‘No, my Lord, no,’ returned Ralph.", "\"없습니다, 각하, 없어요.\" 랄프가 대꾸했어."], ["‘On that point we always agree.", "\"그 점에선 저희가 늘 의견이 맞으니까요."], ["Sir Mulberry has been calling to mind the cause of--’", "멀버리 경께서는 그 원인을 떠올리고 계셨던 겁니다—\""]], [["There was neither necessity nor opportunity for Ralph to proceed; for Sir Mulberry took up the theme, and vented his threats and oaths against Nicholas, almost as ferociously as before.", "랄프가 말을 이을 필요도, 그럴 틈도 없었어. 멀버리 경이 그 화제를 받아 들고는, 거의 아까만큼이나 사납게 니콜라스를 향해 위협과 욕설을 퍼부었거든."]], [["Ralph, who was no common observer, was surprised to see that as this tirade proceeded, the manner of Lord Frederick Verisopht, who at the commencement had been twirling his whiskers with a most dandified and listless air, underwent a complete alteration.", "여간한 관찰자가 아닌 랄프는, 이 장광설이 이어지는 동안 프레더릭 베리소프트 경의 태도가 완전히 달라지는 걸 보고 놀랐어. 처음에는 더없이 멋쟁이답고 나른한 태도로 구레나룻을 배배 꼬고 있던 그가 말이야."], ["He was still more surprised when, Sir Mulberry ceasing to speak, the young lord angrily, and almost unaffectedly, requested never to have the subject renewed in his presence.", "그리고 멀버리 경이 말을 멈추자, 젊은 귀족이 성을 내며, 거의 꾸밈없는 태도로, 다시는 자기 앞에서 그 화제를 꺼내지 말라고 요구하는 데는 더욱 놀랐지."]], [["‘Mind that, Hawk!’ he added, with unusual energy.", "\"명심해, 호크!\" 그가 유난스러운 기세로 덧붙였어."], ["‘I never will be a party to, or permit, if I can help it, a cowardly attack upon this young fellow.’", "\"나는 이 젊은 친구한테 비겁하게 덤벼드는 짓에 절대 끼지도 않을 거고, 내가 막을 수 있는 한 허락하지도 않을 거야.\""]], [["‘Cowardly!’ interrupted his friend.", "\"비겁하다고!\" 친구가 끼어들었어."]], [["‘Ye-es,’ said the other, turning full upon him.", "\"그, 래.\" 상대가 그를 정면으로 돌아보며 말했어."], ["‘If you had told him who you were; if you had given him your card, and found out, afterwards, that his station or character prevented your fighting him, it would have been bad enough then; upon my soul it would have been bad enough then.", "\"자네가 그자한테 자네가 누군지 밝혔더라면, 명함을 건넸더라면, 그러고 나서 그자의 신분이나 처지 탓에 결투를 할 수 없다는 걸 알게 됐더라면, 그때도 충분히 못된 짓이었겠지. 맹세코 그때도 충분히 못된 짓이었을 거야."], ["As it is, you did wrong.", "그런데 실제로 자네가 한 짓은 잘못이었어."], ["I did wrong too, not to interfere, and I am sorry for it.", "나도 끼어들지 않은 게 잘못이었고, 그게 후회돼."], ["What happened to you afterwards, was as much the consequence of accident as design, and more your fault than his; and it shall not, with my knowledge, be cruelly visited upon him, it shall not indeed.’", "그 뒤에 자네한테 일어난 일은 계획했다기보다 사고에 가까운 거였고, 그자보다 자네 탓이 더 컸어. 그러니 내가 아는 한, 그걸 그자한테 잔인하게 되갚게 두진 않겠어, 절대 그러지 않을 거야.\""]], [["With this emphatic repetition of his concluding words, the young lord turned upon his heel; but before he had reached the adjoining room he turned back again, and said, with even greater vehemence than he had displayed before,", "이렇게 자기가 맺음말을 힘주어 되풀이하고는, 젊은 귀족이 홱 돌아섰어. 그런데 옆방에 채 닿기도 전에 다시 돌아서더니, 아까보다 한층 더 격하게 말했어."]], [["‘I do believe, now; upon my honour I do believe, that the sister is as virtuous and modest a young lady as she is a handsome one; and of the brother, I say this, that he acted as her brother should, and in a manly and spirited manner. And I only wish, with all my heart and soul, that any one of us came out of this matter half as well as he does.’", "\"나는 이제 정말로 믿어. 명예를 걸고 정말로 믿는다고. 그 누이동생이 아름다운 만큼이나 정숙하고 얌전한 아가씨라는 걸 말이야. 그리고 그 오빠에 대해서는 이렇게 말하겠어. 그자는 누이의 오빠로서 마땅히 해야 할 대로 행동했고, 사내답고 기개 있게 처신했다고. 나는 진심으로, 온 마음과 영혼을 다해 바라네. 우리 가운데 누구든 이 일에서 그자의 반만큼이라도 떳떳하게 빠져나왔으면 좋겠다고 말이야.\""]], [["So saying, Lord Frederick Verisopht walked out of the room, leaving Ralph Nickleby and Sir Mulberry in most unpleasant astonishment.", "이렇게 말하고 프레더릭 베리소프트 경은 방을 걸어 나갔어. 랄프 니클비와 멀버리 경을 더없이 떨떠름한 놀라움 속에 남겨 둔 채로 말이야."]], [["‘Is this your pupil?’ asked Ralph, softly, ‘or has he come fresh from some country parson?’", "\"저게 자네 제자인가?\" 랄프가 나직이 물었어. \"아니면 어느 시골 목사한테서 막 배워 온 건가?\""]], [["‘Green fools take these fits sometimes,’ replied Sir Mulberry Hawk, biting his lip, and pointing to the door.", "\"풋내기 바보들이 가끔 저런 발작을 하지.\" 멀버리 호크 경이 입술을 깨물며 문 쪽을 가리키며 대꾸했어."], ["‘Leave him to me.’", "\"저놈은 나한테 맡겨 둬.\""]], [["Ralph exchanged a familiar look with his old acquaintance; for they had suddenly grown confidential again in this alarming surprise; and took his way home, thoughtfully and slowly.", "랄프는 옛 지인과 허물없는 눈빛을 주고받았어. 이 섬뜩한 놀라움 속에서 둘은 별안간 다시 흉허물 없는 사이로 돌아왔거든. 그러고는 생각에 잠겨 천천히 집으로 향했어."]], [["While these things were being said and done, and long before they were concluded, the omnibus had disgorged Miss La Creevy and her escort, and they had arrived at her own door.", "이런 말들이 오가고 일들이 벌어지는 동안, 그리고 그게 다 끝나기 한참 전에, 합승마차는 라 크리비 양과 그 호위 기사를 토해 냈고, 둘은 그녀의 집 문 앞에 도착해 있었어. 그런데 이 자그마한 초상화가의 마음씨가 좋아서, 스마이크가 뭔가 속을 든든하게 할 것을 한 모금 마시고 모둠 비스킷이라도 좀 먹어 기운을 차리기 전에는 도무지 그를 다시 걸어 돌아가게 둘 수가 없었어."], ["Now, the good-nature of the little miniature painter would by no means allow of Smike’s walking back again, until he had been previously refreshed with just a sip of something comfortable and a mixed biscuit or so; and Smike, entertaining no objection either to the sip of something comfortable, or the mixed biscuit, but, considering on the contrary that they would be a very pleasant preparation for a walk to Bow, it fell out that he delayed much longer than he originally intended, and that it was some half-hour after dusk when he set forth on his journey home.", "스마이크도 속을 든든하게 할 한 모금이든 모둠 비스킷이든 마다할 생각이 없었고, 오히려 그것들이 보까지 걸어갈 채비로 아주 흐뭇하겠다 싶었지. 그러다 보니 그는 애초에 작정했던 것보다 훨씬 오래 머물게 되었고, 집으로 길을 나섰을 때는 땅거미가 진 지 반 시간쯤 지난 뒤였어."]], [["There was no likelihood of his losing his way, for it lay quite straight before him, and he had walked into town with Nicholas, and back alone, almost every day.", "길을 잃을 염려는 없었어. 길이 그의 앞에 곧게 뻗어 있었고, 그는 거의 매일 니콜라스와 함께 시내로 걸어 들어왔다가 혼자 걸어 돌아갔으니까."], ["So, Miss La Creevy and he shook hands with mutual confidence, and, being charged with more kind remembrances to Mrs. and Miss Nickleby, Smike started off.", "그래서 라 크리비 양과 그는 서로 믿음 가득한 마음으로 악수를 했고, 니클비 부인과 니클비 양에게 다정한 안부를 더 잔뜩 전해 달라는 부탁까지 받고, 스마이크는 출발했어."]], [["At the foot of Ludgate Hill, he turned a little out of the road to satisfy his curiosity by having a look at Newgate.", "러드게이트 힐 기슭에서 그는 길을 살짝 벗어나, 뉴게이트를 한번 구경하고픈 호기심을 채우려 했어. 길 건너편에서 그 음침한 벽을 한참 동안 잔뜩 조심스럽고 두려운 마음으로 올려다보다가, 그는 다시 원래 가던 길로 돌아와 도심을 씩씩하게 걸어갔어."], ["After staring up at the sombre walls, from the opposite side of the way, with great care and dread for some minutes, he turned back again into the old track, and walked briskly through the city; stopping now and then to gaze in at the window of some particularly attractive shop, then running for a little way, then stopping again, and so on, as any other country lad might do.", "이따금 멈춰 서서 유난히 눈길을 끄는 가게 진열창을 들여다보고, 또 잠깐 달려가다가, 또 멈춰 서고, 그러기를 되풀이하면서 말이야. 여느 시골 소년이라면 누구나 그러듯이."]], [["He had been gazing for a long time through a jeweller’s window, wishing he could take some of the beautiful trinkets home as a present, and imagining what delight they would afford if he could, when the clocks struck three-quarters past eight; roused by the sound, he hurried on at a very quick pace, and was crossing the corner of a by-street when he felt himself violently brought to, with a jerk so sudden that he was obliged to cling to a lamp-post to save himself from falling. At the same moment, a small boy clung tight round his leg, and a shrill cry of ‘Here he is, father! Hooray!’ vibrated in his ears.", "그는 한참 동안 어느 보석상 진열창을 들여다보고 있었어. 그 아름다운 장신구를 몇 개 집으로 선물로 가져갈 수 있으면 얼마나 좋을까 바라면서, 그럴 수 있다면 다들 얼마나 기뻐할까 그려 보면서 말이야. 그때 시계들이 여덟 시 사십오 분을 쳤어. 그 소리에 정신이 들어 그는 아주 빠른 걸음으로 서둘러 갔어. 그리고 어느 골목 모퉁이를 가로지르는데, 갑자기 어찌나 세게 홱 잡아채이는지 넘어지지 않으려고 가로등 기둥을 붙잡아야 했지. 바로 그 순간, 어린 사내아이 하나가 그의 다리를 꽉 끌어안았고, \"여기 있어요, 아버지! 만세!\" 하는 새된 외침이 그의 귀에 쟁쟁 울렸어."]], [["Smike knew that voice too well.", "스마이크는 그 목소리를 너무도 잘 알았어."], ["He cast his despairing eyes downward towards the form from which it had proceeded, and, shuddering from head to foot, looked round.", "그는 절망에 찬 눈으로 그 소리가 나온 형체를 내려다봤고, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 부르르 떨며 주위를 둘러봤어."], ["Mr. Squeers had hooked him in the coat collar with the handle of his umbrella, and was hanging on at the other end with all his might and main.", "스퀴어스 씨가 우산 손잡이로 그의 코트 깃을 걸어, 반대쪽 끝에 온 힘을 다해 매달려 있었어."], ["The cry of triumph proceeded from Master Wackford, who, regardless of all his kicks and struggles, clung to him with the tenacity of a bull-dog!", "그 의기양양한 외침은 와크퍼드 도련님한테서 나온 거였는데, 이 녀석은 스마이크가 아무리 발버둥치고 차도 아랑곳 않고 불도그처럼 끈질기게 그에게 들러붙어 있었지!"]], [["One glance showed him this; and in that one glance the terrified creature became utterly powerless and unable to utter a sound.", "한눈에 이 모든 게 그의 눈에 들어왔어. 그리고 그 한 번의 눈길에 겁에 질린 가엾은 그는 완전히 무력해져, 소리 한마디 낼 수 없게 되어 버렸어."]], [["‘Here’s a go!’ cried Mr.", "\"이게 웬 횡재냐!\""], ["Squeers, gradually coming hand-over-hand down the umbrella, and only unhooking it when he had got tight hold of the victim’s collar.", "스퀴어스 씨가 우산을 손으로 한 칸 한 칸 옮겨 잡으며 내려오다가, 먹잇감의 깃을 단단히 움켜쥐고 나서야 우산을 풀며 외쳤어."], ["‘Here’s a delicious go!", "\"이게 웬 기막힌 횡재야!"], ["Wackford, my boy, call up one of them coaches.’", "와크퍼드, 얘야, 저기 마차 한 대 불러라.\""]], [["‘A coach, father!’ cried little Wackford.", "\"마차요, 아버지!\" 어린 와크퍼드가 외쳤어."]], [["‘Yes, a coach, sir,’ replied Squeers, feasting his eyes upon the countenance of Smike.", "\"그래, 마차다, 이놈아.\" 스퀴어스가 스마이크의 얼굴을 두 눈에 실컷 담으며 대꾸했어."], ["‘Damn the expense.", "\"돈 따위 알 게 뭐냐."], ["Let’s have him in a coach.’", "이놈을 마차에 태우자.\""]], [["‘What’s he been a doing of?’ asked a labourer with a hod of bricks, against whom and a fellow-labourer Mr.", "\"이놈이 뭘 했길래요?\" 벽돌을 짊어진 한 인부가 물었어."], ["Squeers had backed, on the first jerk of the umbrella.", "스퀴어스 씨가 우산을 처음 홱 잡아챘을 때, 이 인부와 동료 인부 쪽으로 떠밀려 부딪혔거든."]], [["‘Everything!’ replied Mr.", "\"안 한 게 없지!\""], ["Squeers, looking fixedly at his old pupil in a sort of rapturous trance.", "스퀴어스 씨가 옛 제자를 황홀경에 빠진 듯 뚫어지게 보며 대꾸했어."], ["‘Everything--running away, sir--joining in bloodthirsty attacks upon his master--there’s nothing that’s bad that he hasn’t done.", "\"안 한 게 없어. 도망치고, 이놈아, 제 주인한테 피에 굶주린 공격을 함께 가하고, 나쁜 짓이란 나쁜 짓은 안 해 본 게 없지."], ["Oh, what a delicious go is this here, good Lord!’", "오, 이게 웬 기막힌 횡재냐, 맙소사!\""]], [["The man looked from Squeers to Smike; but such mental faculties as the poor fellow possessed, had utterly deserted him. The coach came up; Master Wackford entered; Squeers pushed in his prize, and following close at his heels, pulled up the glasses. The coachman mounted his box and drove slowly off, leaving the two bricklayers, and an old apple-woman, and a town-made little boy returning from an evening school, who had been the only witnesses of the scene, to meditate upon it at their leisure.", "인부는 스퀴어스에게서 스마이크에게로 눈길을 옮겼어. 하지만 그 가엾은 친구가 지녔던 그나마의 정신머리는 깡그리 달아나 버린 뒤였지. 마차가 다가왔어. 와크퍼드 도련님이 올라탔어. 스퀴어스가 제 노획물을 밀어 넣고, 그 뒤를 바짝 따라 올라타더니 유리창을 끌어 올렸어. 마부가 마부석에 올라 천천히 마차를 몰고 떠나며, 이 장면을 유일하게 목격했던 벽돌공 둘과 늙은 사과 장수, 그리고 야간 학교에서 돌아오던 도회지 꼬마 하나를 남겨 두었어. 한가로이 이 일을 곱씹어 보라고 말이야."]], [["Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨는 불운한 스마이크의 맞은편 자리에 앉아, 두 손을 무릎에 단단히 짚고 한 오 분쯤 그를 바라봤어."], ["Squeers sat himself down on the opposite seat to the unfortunate Smike, and, planting his hands firmly on his knees, looked at him for some five minutes, when, seeming to recover from his trance, he uttered a loud laugh, and slapped his old pupil’s face several times--taking the right and left sides alternately.", "그러다 황홀경에서 깨어난 듯 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨리더니, 옛 제자의 뺨을 여러 차례 후려쳤어. 오른쪽 왼쪽 번갈아 가면서 말이야."]], [["‘It isn’t a dream!’ said Squeers.", "\"꿈이 아니야!\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘That’s real flesh and blood!", "\"이건 진짜 살과 피라고!"], ["I know the feel of it!’ and being quite assured of his good fortune by these experiments, Mr.", "만져 보니 알겠어!\""], ["Squeers administered a few boxes on the ear, lest the entertainments should seem to partake of sameness, and laughed louder and longer at every one.", "그리고 이 실험으로 자기 행운을 확실히 확인하고 나자, 스퀴어스 씨는 즐거움이 너무 똑같아 보일까 봐 귀싸대기도 몇 대 더 올려붙였어. 그러고는 한 대 칠 때마다 더 크게, 더 길게 웃었지."]], [["‘Your mother will be fit to jump out of her skin, my boy, when she hears of this,’ said Squeers to his son.", "\"네 어머니가 이 소식을 들으면 좋아서 펄쩍펄쩍 뛸 거다, 얘야.\" 스퀴어스가 아들에게 말했어."]], [["‘Oh, won’t she though, father?’ replied Master Wackford.", "\"안 그러겠어요, 아버지?\" 와크퍼드 도련님이 대꾸했어."]], [["‘To think,’ said Squeers, ‘that you and me should be turning out of a street, and come upon him at the very nick; and that I should have him tight, at only one cast of the umbrella, as if I had hooked him with a grappling-iron! Ha, ha!’", "\"생각해 봐라.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"너하고 내가 길모퉁이를 돌아 나오다가 딱 그 순간에 이놈하고 마주치다니. 그것도 우산을 단 한 번 휙 던졌을 뿐인데, 마치 갈고리로 콱 낚아챈 것처럼 이놈을 단단히 붙잡다니. 하, 하!\""]], [["‘Didn’t I catch hold of his leg, neither, father?’ said little Wackford.", "\"제가 다리도 꽉 붙들었잖아요, 아버지?\" 어린 와크퍼드가 말했어."]], [["‘You did; like a good ‘un, my boy,’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 아주 잘했다, 얘야.\""], ["Squeers, patting his son’s head, ‘and you shall have the best button-over jacket and waistcoat that the next new boy brings down, as a reward of merit.", "스퀴어스 씨가 아들 머리를 쓰다듬으며 말했어. \"그러니 다음에 새로 들어오는 애가 가져오는 것 중에 제일 좋은, 단추 다는 윗도리하고 조끼를 상으로 주마."], ["Mind that.", "명심해라."], ["You always keep on in the same path, and do them things that you see your father do, and when you die you’ll go right slap to Heaven and no questions asked.’", "너는 늘 이 길을 그대로 걷고, 네 아버지가 하는 걸 보고 그대로 하거라. 그러면 죽어서 곧장 천국으로 직행할 거다, 아무도 토 안 달고 말이야.\""]], [["Improving the occasion in these words, Mr.", "이런 말로 이 기회를 잘 써먹으면서, 스퀴어스 씨는 아들 머리를 다시 한번 쓰다듬고는 스마이크 머리도 쓰다듬었어."], ["Squeers patted his son’s head again, and then patted Smike’s--but harder; and inquired in a bantering tone how he found himself by this time.", "다만 더 세게 말이야. 그러고는 놀리는 말투로 이제 좀 어떠냐고 물었어."]], [["‘I must go home,’ replied Smike, looking wildly round.", "\"집에 가야 해요.\" 스마이크가 미친 듯이 주위를 둘러보며 대답했어."]], [["‘To be sure you must.", "\"그야 가야지."], ["You’re about right there,’ replied Mr.", "거 옳은 말 했다.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 대꾸했어."], ["Squeers. ‘You’ll go home very soon, you will.", "\"곧 집에 가게 될 거다, 그럼."], ["You’ll find yourself at the peaceful village of Dotheboys, in Yorkshire, in something under a week’s time, my young friend; and the next time you get away from there, I give you leave to keep away.", "일주일이 채 안 돼서 요크셔의 그 평화로운 마을 두더보이즈에 가 있게 될 거다, 젊은 친구. 그리고 다음번에 거기서 또 달아나거든, 그땐 영영 안 돌아와도 좋다고 내가 허락하마."], ["Where’s the clothes you run off in, you ungrateful robber?’ said Mr.", "네가 도망칠 때 입고 간 옷은 어디 있냐, 이 배은망덕한 도둑놈아?\""], ["Squeers, in a severe voice.", "스퀴어스 씨가 매서운 목소리로 말했어."]], [["Smike glanced at the neat attire which the care of Nicholas had provided for him; and wrung his hands.", "스마이크는 니콜라스가 보살펴 마련해 준 말끔한 옷차림을 흘끗 내려다보며 두 손을 비틀었어."]], [["‘Do you know that I could hang you up, outside of the Old Bailey, for making away with them articles of property?’ said Squeers.", "\"그 재물을 빼돌린 죄로 내가 너를 올드 베일리 밖에 매달아 버릴 수도 있다는 거 알아?\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘Do you know that it’s a hanging matter--and I an’t quite certain whether it an’t an anatomy one besides--to walk off with up’ards of the valley of five pound from a dwelling-house?", "\"남의 집에서 오 파운드어치가 넘는 걸 들고 튀는 게 교수형감이라는 거 알아? 게다가 해부용으로 넘겨질지도 모른다는 거, 나도 확실치는 않다만."], ["Eh?", "응?"], ["Do you know that?", "그거 알아?"], ["What do you suppose was the worth of them clothes you had?", "네가 입었던 그 옷이 얼마짜리였을 거 같아?"], ["Do you know that that Wellington boot you wore, cost eight-and-twenty shillings when it was a pair, and the shoe seven-and-six?", "네가 신었던 그 웰링턴 부츠가 한 켤레에 이십팔 실링이었고, 구두는 칠 실링 육 펜스였다는 거 알아?"], ["But you came to the right shop for mercy when you came to me, and thank your stars that it IS me as has got to serve you with the article.’", "하지만 나한테 온 건 자비를 구하러 제대로 찾아온 거다. 그러니 너한테 그 자비를 베풀어 줄 사람이 바로 나라는 걸 네 별한테 감사하거라.\""]], [["Anybody not in Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨의 속을 모르는 사람이라면, 그가 누구에게든 내줄 그 물건을 잔뜩 쌓아 두기는커녕 아예 다 떨어진 줄로 알았을 거야."], ["Squeers’s confidence would have supposed that he was quite out of the article in question, instead of having a large stock on hand ready for all comers; nor would the opinion of sceptical persons have undergone much alteration when he followed up the remark by poking Smike in the chest with the ferrule of his umbrella, and dealing a smart shower of blows, with the ribs of the same instrument, upon his head and shoulders.", "그리고 그가 이 말끝에 우산 끝 쇠붙이로 스마이크의 가슴을 쿡쿡 찌르고, 같은 우산의 살로 그의 머리와 어깨에 매서운 소나기 같은 매질을 퍼붓는 걸 본대도, 의심 많은 사람들의 생각은 별로 달라지지 않았을 거야."]], [["‘I never threshed a boy in a hackney coach before,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 삯마차 안에서 애를 패 보긴 처음이군.\""], ["Squeers, when he stopped to rest.", "스퀴어스 씨가 쉬려고 손을 멈추며 말했어."], ["‘There’s inconveniency in it, but the novelty gives it a sort of relish, too!’", "\"불편하긴 한데, 그래도 새롭다 보니 또 색다른 맛이 있단 말이지!\""]], [["Poor Smike! He warded off the blows, as well as he could, and now shrunk into a corner of the coach, with his head resting on his hands, and his elbows on his knees; he was stunned and stupefied, and had no more idea that any act of his, would enable him to escape from the all-powerful Squeers, now that he had no friend to speak to or to advise with, than he had had in all the weary years of his Yorkshire life which preceded the arrival of Nicholas.", "가엾은 스마이크! 그는 매를 할 수 있는 한 막아 내다가, 이제 마차 한구석에 웅크려 머리를 두 손에 묻고 팔꿈치를 무릎에 괴고 있었어. 멍하고 얼이 빠진 채로 말이야. 이제 그에게는 말을 건네거나 의논할 친구가 하나도 없으니, 자기가 무슨 짓을 한들 그 전능한 스퀴어스한테서 벗어날 수 있으리라는 생각은 조금도 들지 않았어. 니콜라스가 나타나기 전, 요크셔에서 보낸 그 지긋지긋한 세월 내내 그랬던 것처럼 말이야."]], [["The journey seemed endless; street after street was entered and left behind; and still they went jolting on.", "여정은 끝이 없는 것 같았어. 거리 하나를 들어섰다가 또 하나를 지나치기를 거듭하며, 마차는 여전히 덜컹거리며 나아갔어."], ["At last Mr. Squeers began to thrust his head out of the widow every half-minute, and to bawl a variety of directions to the coachman; and after passing, with some difficulty, through several mean streets which the appearance of the houses and the bad state of the road denoted to have been recently built, Mr.", "마침내 스퀴어스 씨가 삼십 초마다 한 번씩 창밖으로 머리를 내밀며 마부에게 온갖 지시를 고래고래 외쳐 대기 시작했어."], ["Squeers suddenly tugged at the check string with all his might, and cried, ‘Stop!’", "그리고 집들의 생김새와 형편없는 도로 상태로 보아 최근에 지어진 듯한 초라한 거리 몇 군데를 어렵사리 지난 뒤, 스퀴어스 씨가 갑자기 신호 줄을 있는 힘껏 잡아당기며 \"서요!\" 하고 외쳤어."]], [["‘What are you pulling a man’s arm off for?’ said the coachman looking angrily down.", "\"왜 남의 팔을 뽑아 버릴 듯이 잡아당기는 거요?\" 마부가 화난 눈으로 내려다보며 말했어."]], [["‘That’s the house,’ replied Squeers.", "\"저 집이오.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어."], ["‘The second of them four little houses, one story high, with the green shutters.", "\"저 단층짜리 작은 집 네 채 중에 두 번째, 초록색 덧창 달린 집 말이오."], ["There’s brass plate on the door, with the name of Snawley.’", "문에 놋쇠 명패가 붙어 있고, 스놀리라는 이름이 적혀 있을 거요.\""]], [["‘Couldn’t you say that without wrenching a man’s limbs off his body?’ inquired the coachman.", "\"남의 팔다리를 몸에서 비틀어 뽑지 않고서는 그 말을 못 하겠소?\" 마부가 따져 물었어."]], [["‘No!’ bawled Mr. Squeers.", "\"못 해!\""], ["‘Say another word, and I’ll summons you for having a broken winder.", "스퀴어스 씨가 고래고래 외쳤어. \"한마디만 더 해 봐, 유리창 깨뜨린 죄로 고발해 버릴 테니."], ["Stop!’", "서요!\""]], [["Obedient to this direction, the coach stopped at Mr. Snawley’s door.", "이 지시에 따라 마차는 스놀리 씨의 집 문 앞에 섰어."], ["Mr. Snawley may be remembered as the sleek and sanctified gentleman who confided two sons (in law) to the parental care of Mr.", "스놀리 씨로 말할 것 같으면, 이 이야기 제4장에서 이야기했듯이, 의붓아들 둘을 스퀴어스 씨의 어버이 같은 보살핌에 맡긴 그 매끈하고 거룩한 척하는 신사로 기억할 거야."], ["Squeers, as narrated in the fourth chapter of this history. Mr.", "스놀리 씨의 집은 서머스 타운에 잇닿은 새 주택지의 맨 변두리에 있었어."], ["Snawley’s house was on the extreme borders of some new settlements adjoining Somers Town, and Mr. Squeers had taken lodgings therein for a short time, as his stay was longer than usual, and the Saracen, having experience of Master Wackford’s appetite, had declined to receive him on any other terms than as a full-grown customer.", "스퀴어스 씨는 이번에 평소보다 오래 머물게 되어 그 집에 잠깐 셋방을 얻었는데, 늘 묵던 새러슨 여관이 와크퍼드 도련님의 식성을 익히 겪은 터라, 그를 다 큰 손님으로 치지 않고는 받아 주려 하지 않았거든."]], [["‘Here we are!’ said Squeers, hurrying Smike into the little parlour, where Mr.", "\"자, 다 왔다!\" 스퀴어스가 스마이크를 작은 응접실로 떠밀며 말했어."], ["Snawley and his wife were taking a lobster supper.", "거기서는 스놀리 씨 부부가 바닷가재로 저녁을 먹고 있었지."], ["‘Here’s the vagrant--the felon--the rebel--the monster of unthankfulness.’", "\"여기 그 부랑자가, 그 흉악범이, 그 반역자가, 그 배은망덕의 괴물이 왔습니다.\""]], [["‘What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["The boy that run away!’ cried Snawley, resting his knife and fork upright on the table, and opening his eyes to their full width.", "도망쳤던 그 애 말입니까!\" 스놀리가 나이프와 포크를 식탁에 곧추세운 채, 눈을 한껏 휘둥그레 뜨고 외쳤어."]], [["‘The very boy’, said Squeers, putting his fist close to Smike’s nose, and drawing it away again, and repeating the process several times, with a vicious aspect.", "\"바로 그 녀석이지요.\" 스퀴어스가 스마이크의 코앞에 주먹을 들이댔다가 도로 빼기를, 험상궂은 낯빛으로 여러 차례 되풀이하며 말했어."], ["‘If there wasn’t a lady present, I’d fetch him such a--: never mind, I’ll owe it him.’", "\"여기 부인만 안 계셨어도 한 대 이렇게 — : 됐고, 외상으로 달아 두지.\""]], [["And here Mr. Squeers related how, and in what manner, and when and where, he had picked up the runaway.", "그러고는 스퀴어스 씨가 이 도망자를 어떻게, 어떤 식으로, 언제, 어디서 붙잡았는지 이야기했어."]], [["‘It’s clear that there has been a Providence in it, sir,’ said Mr Snawley, casting down his eyes with an air of humility, and elevating his fork, with a bit of lobster on the top of it, towards the ceiling.", "\"이건 분명 거기에 하느님의 섭리가 있었던 겁니다, 선생.\" 스놀리 씨가 겸손한 척 눈을 내리깔고, 바닷가재 한 점을 꽂은 포크를 천장 쪽으로 치켜들며 말했어."]], [["‘Providence is against him, no doubt,’ replied Mr.", "\"섭리가 저놈한테 등을 돌린 건 틀림없지요.\""], ["Squeers, scratching his nose.", "스퀴어스 씨가 코를 긁적이며 대꾸했어."], ["‘Of course; that was to be expected.", "\"당연하지요. 그야 예상한 일이고요."], ["Anybody might have known that.’", "누구든 알 만한 일이었지요.\""]], [["‘Hard-heartedness and evil-doing will never prosper, sir,’ said Mr Snawley.", "\"모진 마음과 못된 짓은 절대 잘되는 법이 없지요, 선생.\" 스놀리 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Never was such a thing known,’ rejoined Squeers, taking a little roll of notes from his pocket-book, to see that they were all safe.", "\"그런 일은 들어 본 적도 없습니다.\" 스퀴어스가 지갑에서 지폐 한 뭉치를 꺼내 다 무사한지 살펴보며 대꾸했어."]], [["‘I have been, Mr. Snawley,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 말이오, 스놀리 씨.\""], ["Squeers, when he had satisfied himself upon this point, ‘I have been that chap’s benefactor, feeder, teacher, and clother.", "스퀴어스 씨가 이 점을 흡족히 확인하고 나서 말했어. \"내가 저놈의 은인이자, 밥을 먹여 준 사람이자, 가르쳐 준 사람이자, 옷을 입혀 준 사람입니다."], ["I have been that chap’s classical, commercial, mathematical, philosophical, and trigonomical friend.", "내가 저놈의 고전, 상업, 수학, 철학, 삼각법 친구였단 말입니다."], ["My son--my only son, Wackford--has been his brother; Mrs.", "내 아들, 내 외아들 와크퍼드가 저놈의 형제였고요."], ["Squeers has been his mother, grandmother, aunt,--ah!", "스퀴어스 부인은 저놈의 어머니이자, 할머니이자, 이모였고, 아!"], ["and I may say uncle too, all in one.", "삼촌 노릇까지 도맡았다 해도 좋아요."], ["She never cottoned to anybody, except them two engaging and delightful boys of yours, as she cottoned to this chap.", "그 사람은 댁의 그 사랑스럽고 마음에 쏙 드는 두 아드님 말고는, 그 누구한테도 이 녀석한테만큼 정을 준 적이 없습니다."], ["What’s my return?", "그런데 내가 받은 보답이 뭡니까?"], ["What’s come of my milk of human kindness?", "내 인정의 젖이 어떻게 됐냐고요."], ["It turns into curds and whey when I look at him.’", "저놈을 보고 있으면 그게 응고된 우유 덩어리하고 유청으로 변해 버린단 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Well it may, sir,’ said Mrs.", "\"그럴 만도 하지요, 선생.\" 스놀리 부인이 말했어."], ["Snawley.", "\"오!"], ["‘Oh! Well it may, sir.’", "그럴 만도 하고말고요, 선생.\""]], [["‘Where has he been all this time?’ inquired Snawley.", "\"이놈이 이때까지 어디 있었답니까?\" 스놀리가 물었어."], ["‘Has he been living with--?’", "\"혹시 같이 살았던 게—?\""]], [["‘Ah, sir!’ interposed Squeers, confronting him again.", "\"아, 그래요, 선생!\" 스퀴어스가 다시 그와 마주서며 끼어들었어."], ["‘Have you been a living with that there devilish Nickleby, sir?’", "\"너 그 빌어먹을 마귀 같은 니클비하고 같이 살았던 거지, 응?\""]], [["But no threats or cuffs could elicit from Smike one word of reply to this question; for he had internally resolved that he would rather perish in the wretched prison to which he was again about to be consigned, than utter one syllable which could involve his first and true friend.", "하지만 어떤 위협이나 매질로도 이 물음에 스마이크의 입에서 단 한마디 대답도 끌어낼 수 없었어. 다시 끌려가게 될 그 비참한 감옥에서 죽어 버릴지언정, 자기 첫 번째이자 진정한 친구를 끌어들일 단 한 음절도 내뱉지 않겠다고 속으로 다짐했거든."], ["He had already called to mind the strict injunctions of secrecy as to his past life, which Nicholas had laid upon him when they travelled from Yorkshire; and a confused and perplexed idea that his benefactor might have committed some terrible crime in bringing him away, which would render him liable to heavy punishment if detected, had contributed, in some degree, to reduce him to his present state of apathy and terror.", "그는 요크셔에서 함께 떠나올 때 니콜라스가 자기 과거에 대해 단단히 일러두었던 함구령을 벌써 떠올린 터였어. 게다가 자기 은인이 자기를 데리고 나오면서 무슨 끔찍한 죄를 저질렀을지도 모르고, 그게 발각되면 무거운 벌을 받게 될지도 모른다는 어수선하고 갈피 못 잡는 생각이, 그를 지금의 무기력하고 겁에 질린 상태로 몰아넣는 데 어느 정도 한몫했지."]], [["Such were the thoughts--if to visions so imperfect and undefined as those which wandered through his enfeebled brain, the term can be applied--which were present to the mind of Smike, and rendered him deaf alike to intimidation and persuasion. Finding every effort useless, Mr Squeers conducted him to a little back room up-stairs, where he was to pass the night; and, taking the precaution of removing his shoes, and coat and waistcoat, and also of locking the door on the outside, lest he should muster up sufficient energy to make an attempt at escape, that worthy gentleman left him to his meditations.", "이런 게 스마이크의 마음속에 들어찬 생각들이었어. 쇠약해진 그의 머릿속을 떠도는, 그토록 어설프고 흐릿한 영상들에 그런 말을 갖다 붙일 수 있다면 말이지. 그게 그를 위협에도 설득에도 똑같이 귀먹게 만든 거야. 무슨 수를 써도 소용없다는 걸 알게 되자, 스퀴어스 씨는 그를 위층의 작은 뒷방으로 데려갔어. 거기서 밤을 지내게 한 거지. 그리고 그가 행여 달아날 엄두를 낼 만큼 기운을 그러모을까 봐, 신발과 코트와 조끼를 벗기고, 바깥에서 문을 잠그는 조심까지 한 뒤에, 이 갸륵한 신사는 그를 혼자 생각에 잠기게 내버려 두고 떠났어."]], [["What those meditations were, and how the poor creature’s heart sunk within him when he thought--when did he, for a moment, cease to think?--of his late home, and the dear friends and familiar faces with which it was associated, cannot be told.", "그 생각들이 어떤 것이었는지, 그리고 자기가 떠나온 보금자리와, 거기 얽힌 정든 친구들과 낯익은 얼굴들을 떠올릴 때 — 아니, 그가 한순간이라도 그 생각을 멈춘 적이 있기나 했을까? — 그 가엾은 것의 가슴이 얼마나 철렁 내려앉았는지, 그건 다 말할 수가 없어."], ["To prepare the mind for such a heavy sleep, its growth must be stopped by rigour and cruelty in childhood; there must be years of misery and suffering, lightened by no ray of hope; the chords of the heart, which beat a quick response to the voice of gentleness and affection, must have rusted and broken in their secret places, and bear the lingering echo of no old word of love or kindness.", "마음이 그토록 무거운 잠에 들 채비를 하려면, 어린 시절에 모진 가혹함과 잔인함으로 그 자람이 멎어 버려야 해. 한 줄기 희망의 빛도 없이 비참함과 고통으로 점철된 세월이 있어야 하지."], ["Gloomy, indeed, must have been the short day, and dull the long, long twilight, preceding such a night of intellect as his.", "다정함과 사랑의 목소리에 재빨리 화답해 뛰던 마음의 현들이, 그 은밀한 자리에서 녹슬고 끊어진 채, 사랑이나 친절의 옛말 한마디 그 메아리조차 남아 있지 않아야 해. 그런 지성의 밤에 앞선 짧은 낮은 참으로 음울했을 테고, 그 길고 긴 황혼은 참으로 흐릿했을 거야."]], [["There were voices which would have roused him, even then; but their welcome tones could not penetrate there; and he crept to bed the same listless, hopeless, blighted creature, that Nicholas had first found him at the Yorkshire school.", "그래도 그를 일깨워 줄 목소리들이 있긴 했어, 그 순간에도 말이야. 하지만 그 반가운 목소리들은 거기까지 닿을 수가 없었어. 그래서 그는, 니콜라스가 요크셔 학교에서 처음 발견했던 그때와 똑같이, 무기력하고 희망 없고 시들어 버린 모습 그대로 기어 들어가 잠자리에 누웠어."]], [["# CHAPTER 39", "# CHAPTER 39"]], [["In which another old Friend encounters Smike, very opportunely and to some Purpose\nThe night, fraught with so much bitterness to one poor soul, had given place to a bright and cloudless summer morning, when a north-country mail-coach traversed, with cheerful noise, the yet silent streets of Islington, and, giving brisk note of its approach with the lively winding of the guard’s horn, clattered onward to its halting-place hard by the Post Office.", "한 가엾은 영혼에게 그토록 쓰라림으로 가득 찼던 밤이 물러가고, 밝고 구름 한 점 없는 여름 아침이 찾아왔어. 그 무렵 북부 지방에서 온 우편 마차 한 대가 아직 고요한 이즐링턴 거리를 경쾌한 소리를 내며 가로질렀지. 차장이 활기차게 나팔을 불어 마차가 다가온다는 걸 알리며, 우체국 바로 옆에 있는 정거장을 향해 덜커덕덜커덕 달려갔어."]], [["The only outside passenger was a burly, honest-looking countryman on the box, who, with his eyes fixed upon the dome of St Paul’s Cathedral, appeared so wrapt in admiring wonder, as to be quite insensible to all the bustle of getting out the bags and parcels, until one of the coach windows being let sharply down, he looked round, and encountered a pretty female face which was just then thrust out.", "바깥 좌석에 탄 손님은 마부석에 앉은 건장하고 정직해 보이는 시골 사내 한 명뿐이었어. 그는 세인트 폴 대성당의 둥근 지붕에 눈을 박은 채 감탄에 푹 빠져 있었지. 어찌나 넋을 잃었던지 가방이며 소포를 꺼내는 그 부산한 소동을 도통 알아차리지 못할 정도였어. 그러다 마차 창문 하나가 탁 내려가자, 그제야 그는 고개를 돌렸어. 그리고 마침 그때 쑥 내밀어진 예쁜 여자 얼굴과 마주쳤지."]], [["‘See there, lass!’ bawled the countryman, pointing towards the object of his admiration.", "\"저기 좀 봐, 색시!\" 시골 사내가 자기가 감탄하던 것을 가리키며 소리쳤어."], ["‘There be Paul’s Church.", "\"저게 폴 성당이여."], ["‘Ecod, he be a soizable ‘un, he be.’", "워따, 엄청시리 큰 놈이네, 큰 놈이여.\""]], [["‘Goodness, John!", "\"세상에, 존!"], ["I shouldn’t have thought it could have been half the size.", "저 절반 크기도 안 될 줄 알았는데."], ["What a monster!’", "무슨 괴물 같네요!\""]], [["‘Monsther!--Ye’re aboot right theer, I reckon, Mrs.", "\"괴물이라!--그 말이 딱 맞구먼, 브로디 부인.\""], ["Browdie,’ said the countryman good-humouredly, as he came slowly down in his huge top-coat; ‘and wa’at dost thee tak yon place to be noo--thot’un owor the wa’?", "시골 사내가 커다란 외투를 걸친 채 느릿느릿 내려오며 사람 좋게 말했어. \"그라믄 저짝 건 뭐로 보이냐?--저기 길 건너편 거 말여."], ["Ye’d never coom near it ‘gin you thried for twolve moonths.", "일 년을 들여다봐도 못 맞힐 거구먼."], ["It’s na’ but a Poast Office!", "저게 다른 게 아니라 우체국이여!"], ["Ho!", "호!"], ["ho!", "호!"], ["They need to charge for dooble-latthers. A Poast Office!", "두 배짜리 편지에 요금 매기는 게 당연하지."], ["Wa’at dost thee think o’ thot?", "우체국이라니! 어찌 생각혀?"], ["‘Ecod, if thot’s on’y a Poast Office, I’d loike to see where the Lord Mayor o’ Lunnun lives.’", "워따, 저게 고작 우체국이믄 런던 시장은 대체 어떤 데서 사는지 한번 보고 잡네.\""]], [["So saying, John Browdie--for he it was--opened the coach-door, and tapping Mrs.", "이렇게 말하면서 존 브로디는 — 바로 그 사내였지 — 마차 문을 열었어."], ["Browdie, late Miss Price, on the cheek as he looked in, burst into a boisterous fit of laughter.", "그리고 안을 들여다보며 브로디 부인의, 그러니까 옛날 프라이스 양이었던 그녀의 뺨을 톡톡 두드리더니, 왁자하게 웃음을 터뜨렸어."]], [["‘Weel!’ said John.", "\"이런!\" 존이 말했어."], ["‘Dang my bootuns if she bean’t asleep agean!’", "\"단추를 다 떼 버려도 좋아, 또 자고 있구먼!\""]], [["‘She’s been asleep all night, and was, all yesterday, except for a minute or two now and then,’ replied John Browdie’s choice, ‘and I was very sorry when she woke, for she has been SO cross!’", "\"밤새 잤어요. 어제도 종일 자더라고요. 가끔 일이 분 깨는 것 말고는요.\" 존 브로디의 어여쁜 신부가 대답했어. \"깨었을 땐 어찌나 짜증을 부리던지, 차라리 안 깨었으면 싶었다니까요!\""]], [["The subject of these remarks was a slumbering figure, so muffled in shawl and cloak, that it would have been matter of impossibility to guess at its sex but for a brown beaver bonnet and green veil which ornamented the head, and which, having been crushed and flattened, for two hundred and fifty miles, in that particular angle of the vehicle from which the lady’s snores now proceeded, presented an appearance sufficiently ludicrous to have moved less risible muscles than those of John Browdie’s ruddy face.", "이런 말의 주인공은 숄과 망토로 칭칭 싸매여 잠든 형체였어. 그 모습으로는 도무지 남자인지 여자인지 알아낼 도리가 없었지. 다만 머리를 장식한 갈색 비버 가죽 보닛과 초록색 베일이 있어서 겨우 짐작할 수 있었어. 그런데 그 보닛은 이백오십 마일을 오는 동안 — 바로 지금 이 숙녀의 코 고는 소리가 새어 나오는 마차의 그 한구석에서 — 짓눌리고 납작해진 탓에, 존 브로디의 불그레한 얼굴보다 훨씬 덜 잘 웃는 근육이라도 들썩이게 할 만큼 우스꽝스러운 꼴이 되어 있었어."]], [["‘Hollo!’ cried John, twitching one end of the dragged veil.", "\"여보쇼!\" 존이 늘어진 베일 한쪽 끝을 잡아당기며 외쳤어."], ["‘Coom, wakken oop, will ‘ee?’", "\"자, 일어나 봐, 응?\""]], [["After several burrowings into the old corner, and many exclamations of impatience and fatigue, the figure struggled into a sitting posture; and there, under a mass of crumpled beaver, and surrounded by a semicircle of blue curl-papers, were the delicate features of Miss Fanny Squeers.", "옛 구석 자리로 몇 번이나 더 파고들며 짜증과 피로에 찬 소리를 여러 번 내뱉은 끝에, 그 형체는 끙끙대며 일어나 앉았어. 그러자 구겨진 비버 가죽 더미 아래로, 파란 종이로 만 머리카락이 반원을 그리며 둘러싼 가운데, 패니 스퀴어스 양의 고운 이목구비가 드러났지."]], [["‘Oh, ‘Tilda!’ cried Miss Squeers, ‘how you have been kicking of me through this blessed night!’", "\"어머, 틸다!\" 스퀴어스 양이 외쳤어. \"이 멀쩡한 밤새 나를 어찌나 발로 차 댔는지!\""]], [["‘Well, I do like that,’ replied her friend, laughing, ‘when you have had nearly the whole coach to yourself.’", "\"어머, 그것참 좋은 소리네.\" 친구가 웃으며 대꾸했어. \"마차를 거의 혼자 다 차지하고선 말이야.\""]], [["‘Don’t deny it, ‘Tilda,’ said Miss Squeers, impressively, ‘because you have, and it’s no use to go attempting to say you haven’t. You mightn’t have known it in your sleep, ‘Tilda, but I haven’t closed my eyes for a single wink, and so I THINK I am to be believed.’", "\"발뺌하지 마, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 자못 진지하게 말했어. \"분명히 그랬으니까. 안 그랬다고 우겨 봐야 소용없어. 잠결이라 몰랐겠지만, 틸다, 난 한숨도 못 붙였어. 그러니까 내 말을 믿어야 한다고 생각해.\""]], [["With which reply, Miss Squeers adjusted the bonnet and veil, which nothing but supernatural interference and an utter suspension of nature’s laws could have reduced to any shape or form; and evidently flattering herself that it looked uncommonly neat, brushed off the sandwich-crumbs and bits of biscuit which had accumulated in her lap, and availing herself of John Browdie’s proffered arm, descended from the coach.", "이렇게 대꾸하면서 스퀴어스 양은 보닛과 베일을 매만졌어. 초자연적인 힘이 끼어들어 자연의 법칙을 송두리째 멈추기 전에는 도무지 어떤 모양으로도 잡힐 수 없는 물건이었지. 그러고는 분명 자기 매무새가 유난히 단정해 보인다고 흐뭇해하면서, 무릎에 쌓인 샌드위치 부스러기와 비스킷 조각을 털어 냈어. 그리고 존 브로디가 내미는 팔에 의지해 마차에서 내려왔지."]], [["‘Noo,’ said John, when a hackney coach had been called, and the ladies and the luggage hurried in, ‘gang to the Sarah’s Head, mun.’", "\"자,\" 합승 마차가 불려 오고 숙녀들과 짐이 서둘러 실리자 존이 말했어. \"사라네 머리로 가 주쇼.\""]], [["‘To the VERE?’ cried the coachman.", "\"어디로요?\" 마부가 외쳤어."]], [["‘Lawk, Mr.", "\"어머, 브로디 씨!\""], ["Browdie!’ interrupted Miss Squeers.", "스퀴어스 양이 끼어들었어."], ["‘The idea!", "\"별소릴 다!"], ["Saracen’s Head.’", "새러슨즈 헤드예요.\""]], [["‘Sure-ly,’ said John, ‘I know’d it was something aboot Sarah’s Son’s Head.", "\"그렇지,\" 존이 말했어."], ["Dost thou know thot?’", "\"사라네 아들 머리 어쩌고 하는 거였구먼. 그게 어디 있는지 아쇼?\""]], [["‘Oh, ah!", "\"아, 예!"], ["I know that,’ replied the coachman gruffly, as he banged the door.", "그건 알지요.\" 마부가 문을 쾅 닫으며 퉁명스레 대답했어."]], [["‘’Tilda, dear, really,’ remonstrated Miss Squeers, ‘we shall be taken for I don’t know what.’", "\"틸다, 정말이지.\" 스퀴어스 양이 항의했어. \"이러다 우리가 뭐로 보일지 모르겠어.\""]], [["‘Let them tak’ us as they foind us,’ said John Browdie; ‘we dean’t come to Lunnun to do nought but ‘joy oursel, do we?’", "\"보고 싶은 대로 보라지.\" 존 브로디가 말했어. \"우리가 런던에 즐기러 온 거 말고 뭐가 있어, 응?\""]], [["‘I hope not, Mr.", "\"그러길 바라요, 브로디 씨.\""], ["Browdie,’ replied Miss Squeers, looking singularly dismal.", "스퀴어스 양이 유난히 시무룩한 얼굴로 대답했어."]], [["‘Well, then,’ said John, ‘it’s no matther.", "\"그럼 됐어.\" 존이 말했어."], ["I’ve only been a married man fower days, ‘account of poor old feyther deein, and puttin’ it off.", "\"아무 상관 없잖여. 나야 결혼한 지 나흘밖에 안 됐어."], ["Here be a weddin’ party--broide and broide’s-maid, and the groom--if a mun dean’t ‘joy himsel noo, when ought he, hey?", "불쌍한 우리 아부지가 돌아가시는 바람에 미뤄졌거든. 여기 신혼 일행이 다 모였구먼--신부에 신부 들러리에 신랑까지."], ["Drat it all, thot’s what I want to know.’", "이럴 때도 안 즐기믄 대체 언제 즐기겠어, 응? 젠장, 난 그게 알고 잡다고.\""]], [["So, in order that he might begin to enjoy himself at once, and lose no time, Mr.", "그래서 한시도 허비하지 않고 당장 즐기기 시작하려고, 브로디 씨는 아내에게 진한 입맞춤을 했어. 그리고 스퀴어스 양에게서도 한 번 빼앗는 데 성공했지."], ["Browdie gave his wife a hearty kiss, and succeeded in wresting another from Miss Squeers, after a maidenly resistance of scratching and struggling on the part of that young lady, which was not quite over when they reached the Saracen’s Head.", "그 아가씨가 처녀답게 할퀴고 버둥대며 저항하긴 했지만 말이야. 그 실랑이는 일행이 새러슨즈 헤드에 도착할 때까지도 다 끝나지 않았어."]], [["Here, the party straightway retired to rest; the refreshment of sleep being necessary after so long a journey; and here they met again about noon, to a substantial breakfast, spread by direction of Mr. John Browdie, in a small private room upstairs commanding an uninterrupted view of the stables.", "여기서 일행은 곧장 쉬러 들어갔어. 그렇게 긴 여행 끝이라 잠으로 기운을 차리는 게 꼭 필요했거든. 그리고 정오 무렵에 다시 모여 든든한 아침을 먹었지. 존 브로디 씨가 시킨 대로 위층의 작은 별실에 차려진 식사였어. 그 방에서는 마구간이 한눈에 훤히 내려다보였지."]], [["To have seen Miss Squeers now, divested of the brown beaver, the green veil, and the blue curl-papers, and arrayed in all the virgin splendour of a white frock and spencer, with a white muslin bonnet, and an imitative damask rose in full bloom on the inside thereof--her luxuriant crop of hair arranged in curls so tight that it was impossible they could come out by any accident, and her bonnet-cap trimmed with little damask roses, which might be supposed to be so many promising scions of the big rose--to have seen all this, and to have seen the broad damask belt, matching both the family rose and the little roses, which encircled her slender waist, and by a happy ingenuity took off from the shortness of the spencer behind,--to have beheld all this, and to have taken further into account the coral bracelets (rather short of beads, and with a very visible black string) which clasped her wrists, and the coral necklace which rested on her neck, supporting, outside her frock, a lonely cornelian heart, typical of her own disengaged affections--to have contemplated all these mute but expressive appeals to the purest feelings of our nature, might have thawed the frost of age, and added new and inextinguishable fuel to the fire of youth.", "이제 스퀴어스 양을 보면 말이지, 갈색 비버 가죽 보닛도, 초록 베일도, 파란 종이 말이도 다 벗어 던지고, 처녀다운 화려함으로 한껏 차려입은 모습이었어. 하얀 원피스에 짧은 윗옷을 걸치고, 하얀 모슬린 보닛을 썼는데, 그 안쪽엔 활짝 핀 다마스크 장미 모조품 한 송이가 달려 있었지. 풍성한 머리채는 어찌나 단단하게 말아 올렸는지 어떤 사고로도 풀릴 리 없어 보였어. 보닛 가장자리에는 작은 다마스크 장미들을 둘렀는데, 그건 큰 장미에서 돋아난 장래성 있는 햇가지들로 여겨도 좋을 법했지. 이 모든 걸 다 보고서, 게다가 그 집안의 큰 장미와 작은 장미들에 다 맞춘 넓은 다마스크 허리띠까지 봤다고 치자. 그 띠는 그녀의 가느다란 허리를 둘렀는데, 절묘한 솜씨로 뒤쪽 윗옷이 짧은 걸 가려 주었지. 이 모든 걸 다 바라보고, 거기다 손목을 감싼 산호 팔찌까지 셈에 넣어 보자. 구슬이 좀 모자라고 까만 실이 훤히 비치긴 했지만 말이야. 그리고 목에 걸린 산호 목걸이도 있었는데, 거기엔 원피스 바깥으로 외로운 홍옥수 하트 하나가 매달려 있었어. 그건 임자 없는 그녀의 애정을 상징하는 것이었지. 우리 본성의 가장 순수한 감정에 호소하는 이 말 없되 웅변적인 것들을 죄다 곰곰이 들여다본다면, 늙음의 서리도 녹아내리고, 젊음의 불길에 새롭고 끌 수 없는 땔감이 더해졌을 거야."]], [["The waiter was touched.", "웨이터도 마음이 흔들렸어."], ["Waiter as he was, he had human passions and feelings, and he looked very hard at Miss Squeers as he handed the muffins.", "웨이터라곤 해도 그에게도 사람의 정념과 감정이 있었거든. 그래서 머핀을 건네면서 스퀴어스 양을 뚫어지게 쳐다봤지."]], [["‘Is my pa in, do you know?’ asked Miss Squeers with dignity.", "\"우리 아빠 와 계신지 아세요?\" 스퀴어스 양이 위엄 있게 물었어."]], [["‘Beg your pardon, miss?’", "\"뭐라고 하셨죠, 아가씨?\""]], [["‘My pa,’ repeated Miss Squeers; ‘is he in?’", "\"우리 아빠 말이에요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 되풀이했어. \"와 계세요?\""]], [["‘In where, miss?’", "\"어디에요, 아가씨?\""]], [["‘In here--in the house!’ replied Miss Squeers.", "\"여기요--이 집에요!\" 스퀴어스 양이 대답했어."], ["‘My pa--Mr. Wackford Squeers--he’s stopping here.", "\"우리 아빠--워크퍼드 스퀴어스 씨요--여기 묵고 계시잖아요."], ["Is he at home?’", "안에 계세요?\""]], [["‘I didn’t know there was any gen’l’man of that name in the house, miss’ replied the waiter.", "\"그런 성함의 신사분이 이 집에 계신 줄은 몰랐는데요, 아가씨.\" 웨이터가 대답했어."], ["‘There may be, in the coffee-room.’", "\"커피실에 계실지도 모르겠네요.\""]], [["MAY BE.", "계실지도 모른다니."], ["Very pretty this, indeed!", "거참, 듣기 좋은 소리네!"], ["Here was Miss Squeers, who had been depending, all the way to London, upon showing her friends how much at home she would be, and how much respectful notice her name and connections would excite, told that her father MIGHT be there!", "스퀴어스 양은 런던에 오는 내내 친구들에게 자기가 여기서 얼마나 제집처럼 편할지, 그리고 자기 이름과 인맥이 얼마나 깍듯한 대접을 받게 할지 보여 줄 생각만 하고 있었거든. 그런데 아버지가 거기 계실지도 모른다는 소리나 듣다니!"], ["‘As if he was a feller!’ observed Miss Squeers, with emphatic indignation.", "\"무슨 떠돌이라도 되는 것처럼!\" 스퀴어스 양이 분에 차서 힘주어 한마디 했어."]], [["‘Ye’d betther inquire, mun,’ said John Browdie.", "\"한번 알아보라구, 이 사람아.\" 존 브로디가 말했어."], ["‘An’ hond up another pigeon-pie, will ‘ee?", "\"그라고 비둘기 파이 하나 더 갖다주겠어? 젠장할 놈.\""], ["Dang the chap,’ muttered John, looking into the empty dish as the waiter retired; ‘does he ca’ this a pie--three yoong pigeons and a troifling matther o’ steak, and a crust so loight that you doant know when it’s in your mooth and when it’s gane?", "웨이터가 물러나자 존이 빈 접시를 들여다보며 투덜거렸어. \"이걸 파이라고 부르나--새끼 비둘기 세 마리에 소고기 몇 점, 그리고 어찌나 가벼운 껍질인지 입에 들었는지 사라졌는지도 모르겠구먼."], ["I wonder hoo many pies goes to a breakfast!’", "아침 한 끼에 파이가 대체 몇 개나 드는지 모르겠네!\""]], [["After a short interval, which John Browdie employed upon the ham and a cold round of beef, the waiter returned with another pie, and the information that Mr.", "존 브로디가 햄과 차가운 소고기 한 덩이를 먹어 치우는 동안 잠깐 틈이 있었어. 그러고 나서 웨이터가 파이를 하나 더 들고 돌아왔지."], ["Squeers was not stopping in the house, but that he came there every day and that directly he arrived, he should be shown upstairs.", "그러면서 스퀴어스 씨는 이 집에 묵는 건 아니지만 날마다 여기 들른다고, 도착하면 바로 위층으로 안내하겠다고 일러 주었어. 그러고는 물러갔지."], ["With this, he retired; and he had not retired two minutes, when he returned with Mr. Squeers and his hopeful son.", "그런데 물러난 지 이 분도 안 되어서, 스퀴어스 씨와 그의 미더운 아들을 데리고 다시 돌아왔어."]], [["‘Why, who’d have thought of this?’ said Mr.", "\"아니, 이게 웬일이냐?\""], ["Squeers, when he had saluted the party and received some private family intelligence from his daughter.", "스퀴어스 씨가 일행에게 인사를 하고 딸에게서 집안의 은밀한 소식을 좀 전해 들은 뒤 말했어."]], [["‘Who, indeed, pa!’ replied that young lady, spitefully.", "\"웬일이긴요, 아빠!\" 그 아가씨가 앵돌아진 목소리로 대꾸했어."], ["‘But you see ‘Tilda IS married at last.’", "\"보세요, 틸다가 드디어 결혼했잖아요.\""]], [["‘And I stond threat for a soight o’ Lunnun, schoolmeasther,’ said John, vigorously attacking the pie.", "\"내가 런던 구경 한턱내는 길이오, 선생.\" 존이 파이를 힘차게 공략하며 말했어."]], [["‘One of them things that young men do when they get married,’ returned Squeers; ‘and as runs through with their money like nothing at all!", "\"젊은 사내들이 결혼하면 하는 그런 짓 중 하나지.\" 스퀴어스가 말을 받았어."], ["How much better wouldn’t it be now, to save it up for the eddication of any little boys, for instance!", "\"그리고 돈이 눈 깜짝할 새 다 빠져나가는 짓이고! 가령 그 돈을 모아 뒀다가 어린 사내애들 교육에 쓰면 얼마나 더 나으냐고!"], ["They come on you,’ said Mr.", "그놈들은 말이지,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 훈계조로 말했어."], ["Squeers in a moralising way, ‘before you’re aware of it; mine did upon me.’", "\"당신이 미처 알아채기도 전에 들이닥친다구. 내 새끼들도 나한테 그랬으니까.\""]], [["‘Will ‘ee pick a bit?’ said John.", "\"좀 드시겠어?\" 존이 말했어."]], [["‘I won’t myself,’ returned Squeers; ‘but if you’ll just let little Wackford tuck into something fat, I’ll be obliged to you. Give it him in his fingers, else the waiter charges it on, and there’s lot of profit on this sort of vittles without that. If you hear the waiter coming, sir, shove it in your pocket and look out of the window, d’ye hear?’", "\"난 됐네.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"그래도 어린 워크퍼드한테 기름진 거 좀 먹게 해 주면 고맙겠구먼. 손가락으로 집어서 주게. 안 그러면 웨이터가 계산서에 올려 버리니까. 이런 음식은 그러지 않아도 이문이 많이 남거든. 웨이터 오는 소리 들리면, 얘야, 얼른 주머니에 쑤셔 넣고 창밖을 내다봐, 알겠지?\""]], [["‘I’m awake, father,’ replied the dutiful Wackford.", "\"깨어 있어요, 아버지.\" 효성 지극한 워크퍼드가 대답했어."]], [["‘Well,’ said Squeers, turning to his daughter, ‘it’s your turn to be married next.", "\"자,\" 스퀴어스가 딸에게 돌아서며 말했어."], ["You must make haste.’", "\"이젠 네가 결혼할 차례다. 서둘러야지.\""]], [["‘Oh, I’m in no hurry,’ said Miss Squeers, very sharply.", "\"어머, 난 안 급해요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 아주 쌀쌀맞게 말했어."]], [["‘No, Fanny?’ cried her old friend with some archness.", "\"아니라고, 패니?\" 옛 친구가 짓궂은 기색으로 외쳤어."]], [["‘No, ‘Tilda,’ replied Miss Squeers, shaking her head vehemently.", "\"그래, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 거세게 고개를 저으며 대답했어."], ["‘I can wait.’", "\"난 기다릴 수 있어.\""]], [["‘So can the young men, it seems, Fanny,’ observed Mrs.", "\"젊은 사내들도 기다릴 수 있나 보네, 패니.\""], ["Browdie.", "브로디 부인이 한마디 했어."]], [["‘They an’t draw’d into it by ME, ‘Tilda,’ retorted Miss Squeers.", "\"내가 끌어들인 게 아니잖아, 틸다.\" 스퀴어스 양이 쏘아붙였어."]], [["‘No,’ returned her friend; ‘that’s exceedingly true.’", "\"그래.\" 친구가 받았어. \"그건 더없이 맞는 말이지.\""]], [["The sarcastic tone of this reply might have provoked a rather acrimonious retort from Miss Squeers, who, besides being of a constitutionally vicious temper--aggravated, just now, by travel and recent jolting--was somewhat irritated by old recollections and the failure of her own designs upon Mr.", "이 대답의 비꼬는 말투에 스퀴어스 양은 꽤 매서운 반박을 했을지도 몰라. 그녀는 타고나길 심술궂은 성미인 데다--지금은 여행과 방금까지의 덜컹거림으로 더 사나워진 참이었지--옛 기억과 브로디 씨를 향한 자기 계획이 실패한 일로 적잖이 약이 올라 있었거든."], ["Browdie; and the acrimonious retort might have led to a great many other retorts, which might have led to Heaven knows what, if the subject of conversation had not been, at that precise moment, accidentally changed by Mr.", "그리고 그 매서운 반박은 또 다른 수많은 반박으로 이어졌을 테고, 그게 또 무슨 일로 번졌을지는 하늘만 알았겠지."], ["Squeers himself", "만약 바로 그 순간 스퀴어스 씨가 우연히 화제를 바꿔 놓지 않았더라면 말이야."]], [["‘What do you think?’ said that gentleman; ‘who do you suppose we have laid hands on, Wackford and me?’", "\"이게 무슨 일인지 알겠나?\" 그 신사가 말했어. \"워크퍼드하고 내가 누구를 붙잡았는지 짐작이나 하겠어?\""]], [["‘Pa!", "\"아빠! 설마 그--?\""], ["not Mr--?’ Miss Squeers was unable to finish the sentence, but Mrs Browdie did it for her, and added, ‘Nickleby?’", "스퀴어스 양은 말을 끝맺지 못했지만, 브로디 부인이 대신 끝맺으며 덧붙였어. \"니클비요?\""]], [["‘No,’ said Squeers.", "\"아니.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘But next door to him though.’", "\"그래도 그 바로 옆사람이긴 하지.\""]], [["‘You can’t mean Smike?’ cried Miss Squeers, clapping her hands.", "\"설마 스마이크는 아니겠죠?\" 스퀴어스 양이 손뼉을 치며 외쳤어."]], [["‘Yes, I can though,’ rejoined her father.", "\"아니, 바로 그놈이다.\" 아버지가 받았어."], ["‘I’ve got him, hard and fast.’", "\"꽉 붙들어 놨지, 단단히.\""]], [["‘Wa’at!’ exclaimed John Browdie, pushing away his plate.", "\"뭐라고!\" 존 브로디가 접시를 밀어 내며 외쳤어."], ["‘Got that poor--dom’d scoondrel?", "\"그 불쌍한--빌어먹을 놈을 잡았다고?"], ["Where?’", "어디서?\""]], [["‘Why, in the top back room, at my lodging,’ replied Squeers, ‘with him on one side, and the key on the other.’", "\"왜, 내 하숙집 꼭대기 뒷방에 있지.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"한쪽엔 그놈, 한쪽엔 열쇠가 있고 말이야.\""]], [["‘At thy loodgin’!", "\"당신 하숙집에!"], ["Thee’st gotten him at thy loodgin’?", "당신 하숙집에 그놈을 잡아 뒀다고?"], ["Ho!", "호!"], ["ho!", "호!"], ["The schoolmeasther agin all England.", "선생이 온 잉글랜드를 다 이겼구먼."], ["Give us thee hond, mun; I’m darned but I must shak thee by the hond for thot.--Gotten him at thy loodgin’?’", "손 좀 줘 봐, 이 사람아. 빌어먹을, 이건 손이라도 한번 잡아 줘야겠어.--당신 하숙집에 잡아 뒀다고?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Squeers, staggering in his chair under the congratulatory blow on the chest which the stout Yorkshireman dealt him; ‘thankee.", "\"그래.\""], ["Don’t do it again.", "스퀴어스가 대답했어."], ["You mean it kindly, I know, but it hurts rather.", "건장한 요크셔 사내가 축하랍시고 가슴을 친 통에 의자째 휘청거리면서 말이야. \"고맙구먼."], ["Yes, there he is.", "또 그러진 말게. 호의로 그러는 줄은 알지만, 좀 아프단 말이야."], ["That’s not so bad, is it?’", "그래, 그놈이 거기 있어. 나쁘지 않지, 응?\""]], [["‘Ba’ad!’ repeated John Browdie.", "\"나쁘지 않냐고!\" 존 브로디가 되받았어."], ["‘It’s eneaf to scare a mun to hear tell on.’", "\"듣기만 해도 사람 간이 다 떨어지겠구먼.\""]], [["‘I thought it would surprise you a bit,’ said Squeers, rubbing his hands.", "\"좀 놀랄 줄 알았지.\" 스퀴어스가 두 손을 비비며 말했어."], ["‘It was pretty neatly done, and pretty quick too.’", "\"꽤 깔끔하게 해치웠어. 게다가 아주 잽싸게 말이야.\""]], [["‘Hoo wor it?’ inquired John, sitting down close to him.", "\"우찌 된 일이여?\" 존이 그 옆에 바싹 다가앉으며 물었어."], ["‘Tell us all aboot it, mun; coom, quick!’", "\"다 얘기해 봐, 이 사람아. 어서, 빨리!\""]], [["Although he could not keep pace with John Browdie’s impatience, Mr Squeers related the lucky chance by which Smike had fallen into his hands, as quickly as he could, and, except when he was interrupted by the admiring remarks of his auditors, paused not in the recital until he had brought it to an end.", "존 브로디의 조급함을 다 따라가진 못했지만, 스퀴어스 씨는 스마이크가 어떤 운 좋은 우연으로 자기 손에 떨어졌는지 되도록 빨리 들려주었어. 그리고 듣는 이들의 감탄 어린 말이 끼어들 때 말고는, 이야기를 끝맺을 때까지 한 번도 멈추지 않았지."]], [["‘For fear he should give me the slip, by any chance,’ observed Squeers, when he had finished, looking very cunning, ‘I’ve taken three outsides for tomorrow morning--for Wackford and him and me--and have arranged to leave the accounts and the new boys to the agent, don’t you see? So it’s very lucky you come today, or you’d have missed us; and as it is, unless you could come and tea with me tonight, we shan’t see anything more of you before we go away.’", "\"혹시라도 어찌어찌 그놈이 내 손을 빠져나갈까 봐,\" 스퀴어스가 이야기를 마치고 아주 교활한 표정을 지으며 말했어. \"내일 아침 바깥 좌석을 셋 끊어 뒀어--워크퍼드하고 그놈하고 나, 이렇게 말이야. 그리고 회계 정리하고 새 학생 받는 일은 대리인한테 맡기기로 해 놨지, 알겠어? 그러니 자네가 오늘 와서 정말 다행이야. 안 그랬으면 우릴 못 봤을 거 아닌가. 사정이 이러니, 오늘 밤에 와서 나하고 차라도 한잔하지 않으면, 우리가 떠나기 전엔 더 못 보게 될 거야.\""]], [["‘Dean’t say anoother wurd,’ returned the Yorkshireman, shaking him by the hand.", "\"두말 말어.\" 요크셔 사내가 그의 손을 흔들며 대꾸했어."], ["‘We’d coom, if it was twonty mile.’", "\"이십 마일 거리라도 가겠구먼.\""]], [["‘No, would you though?’ returned Mr.", "\"아니, 정말 그러겠어?\" 스퀴어스 씨가 받았어."], ["Squeers, who had not expected quite such a ready acceptance of his invitation, or he would have considered twice before he gave it.", "이렇게 선뜻 초대를 받아들일 줄은 미처 몰랐던 모양이야. 알았더라면 청하기 전에 두 번은 생각했겠지."]], [["John Browdie’s only reply was another squeeze of the hand, and an assurance that they would not begin to see London till tomorrow, so that they might be at Mr.", "존 브로디는 그저 그의 손을 한 번 더 꽉 쥐는 걸로 대답을 대신했어. 그리고 런던 구경은 내일부터 시작할 테니 여섯 시까지는 틀림없이 스놀리 씨 댁에 가 있겠노라고 다짐했지."], ["Snawley’s at six o’clock without fail; and after some further conversation, Mr. Squeers and his son departed.", "그러고서 좀 더 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 스퀴어스 씨와 아들은 떠났어."]], [["During the remainder of the day, Mr.", "그날 나머지 시간 동안 브로디 씨는 아주 묘하고 들뜬 상태였어."], ["Browdie was in a very odd and excitable state; bursting occasionally into an explosion of laughter, and then taking up his hat and running into the coach-yard to have it out by himself.", "가끔 웃음을 펑 터뜨리고는, 모자를 집어 들고 마차 마당으로 달려 나가 혼자 실컷 웃어 댔지. 어찌나 안절부절못하던지, 끊임없이 들락날락하고, 손가락을 딱딱 튕기고, 투박한 시골 춤사위 한 토막씩을 추는가 하면, 한마디로 어찌나 유별나게 굴던지, 스퀴어스 양은 그가 미쳐 가는 거라고 단정했어."], ["He was very restless too, constantly walking in and out, and snapping his fingers, and dancing scraps of uncouth country dances, and, in short, conducting himself in such a very extraordinary manner, that Miss Squeers opined he was going mad, and, begging her dear ‘Tilda not to distress herself, communicated her suspicions in so many words. Mrs.", "그러고는 사랑하는 틸다더러 너무 속상해하지 말라면서, 자기 의심을 죄다 입 밖으로 털어놨지."], ["Browdie, however, without discovering any great alarm, observed that she had seen him so once before, and that although he was almost sure to be ill after it, it would not be anything very serious, and therefore he was better left alone.", "그렇지만 브로디 부인은 별로 놀라는 기색도 없이, 전에도 한 번 그가 이러는 걸 봤다고 했어. 그러고서 거의 틀림없이 앓아눕긴 하겠지만 그리 큰일은 아니라고, 그러니 그냥 혼자 내버려 두는 게 낫다고 했지."]], [["The result proved her to be perfectly correct for, while they were all sitting in Mr.", "결과를 보니 그녀의 말이 완벽하게 맞았어."], ["Snawley’s parlour that night, and just as it was beginning to get dusk, John Browdie was taken so ill, and seized with such an alarming dizziness in the head, that the whole company were thrown into the utmost consternation.", "그날 밤 다들 스놀리 씨네 응접실에 앉아 있을 때, 막 어둑어둑해지기 시작할 무렵, 존 브로디가 어찌나 심하게 앓는지, 머리가 무섭도록 어지럽다고 하는 통에, 온 일행이 더없는 경악에 빠졌거든. 그래도 그 자리에서 정신줄을 놓지 않은 사람은 그의 착한 아내뿐이었어."], ["His good lady, indeed, was the only person present, who retained presence of mind enough to observe that if he were allowed to lie down on Mr.", "그녀는 스퀴어스 씨의 침대에 한 시간쯤 눕혀 두고 완전히 혼자 내버려 두면, 앓아누운 만큼이나 빨리 틀림없이 도로 회복될 거라고 했지."], ["Squeers’s bed for an hour or so, and left entirely to himself, he would be sure to recover again almost as quickly as he had been taken ill.", "의사를 부르기 전에 이렇게 그럴듯한 제안의 효험을 한번 시험해 보자는데, 누구도 마다할 수 없었어. 그래서 존은 큰 어려움 끝에 부축을 받아 위층으로 올라갔지."], ["Nobody could refuse to try the effect of so reasonable a proposal, before sending for a surgeon.", "어찌나 엄청난 덩치였던지, 세 계단을 끌어 올릴 때마다 어김없이 두 계단씩 도로 미끄러져 내렸거든."], ["Accordingly, John was supported upstairs, with great difficulty; being a monstrous weight, and regularly tumbling down two steps every time they hoisted him up three; and, being laid on the bed, was left in charge of his wife, who, after a short interval, reappeared in the parlour, with the gratifying intelligence that he had fallen fast asleep.", "그리고 침대에 눕혀진 뒤 아내에게 맡겨졌어. 아내는 잠시 후 응접실에 다시 나타나, 그가 깊이 잠들었다는 반가운 소식을 전했지."]], [["Now, the fact was, that at that particular moment, John Browdie was sitting on the bed with the reddest face ever seen, cramming the corner of the pillow into his mouth, to prevent his roaring out loud with laughter. He had no sooner succeeded in suppressing this emotion, than he slipped off his shoes, and creeping to the adjoining room where the prisoner was confined, turned the key, which was on the outside, and darting in, covered Smike’s mouth with his huge hand before he could utter a sound.", "그런데 사실인즉, 바로 그 순간 존 브로디는 침대에 앉아 있었어. 여태껏 본 적 없을 만큼 새빨개진 얼굴로, 베개 모서리를 입에 쑤셔 넣고 있었지. 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨리지 않으려고 말이야. 그 감정을 가까스로 억누르자마자 그는 신발을 벗어 던졌어. 그리고 포로가 갇혀 있는 옆방으로 살금살금 기어가, 바깥쪽에 꽂힌 열쇠를 돌렸지. 그러고는 안으로 쏜살같이 뛰어들어, 스마이크가 소리 한번 내기도 전에 그 커다란 손으로 입을 틀어막았어."]], [["‘Ods-bobs, dost thee not know me, mun?’ whispered the Yorkshireman to the bewildered lad.", "\"이런, 날 모르겠어, 이 사람아?\" 요크셔 사내가 어리둥절한 청년에게 속삭였어."], ["‘Browdie.", "\"브로디여."], ["Chap as met thee efther schoolmeasther was banged?’", "선생이 흠씬 두들겨 맞은 뒤에 자네를 만났던 그 사람 말이여.\""]], [["‘Yes, yes,’ cried Smike.", "\"네, 네.\" 스마이크가 외쳤어."], ["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["help me.’", "절 좀 도와주세요.\""]], [["‘Help thee!’ replied John, stopping his mouth again, the instant he had said this much.", "\"도와 달라고!\" 존이 이 말이 떨어지기 무섭게 다시 그의 입을 막으며 대꾸했어."], ["‘Thee didn’t need help, if thee warn’t as silly yoongster as ever draw’d breath.", "\"자네가 세상천지 둘도 없는 멍청이만 아니었어도 도움 따위 필요 없었어."], ["Wa’at did ‘ee come here for, then?’", "그라믄 대체 여긴 뭐 하러 왔어?\""]], [["‘He brought me; oh!", "\"저 사람이 데려왔어요. 아!"], ["he brought me,’ cried Smike.", "저 사람이 절 데려왔다고요.\" 스마이크가 외쳤어."]], [["‘Brout thee!’ replied John.", "\"데려왔다고!\" 존이 받았어."], ["‘Why didn’t ‘ee punch his head, or lay theeself doon and kick, and squeal out for the pollis?", "\"그라믄 왜 그놈 머리통을 한 대 쥐어박거나, 땅바닥에 드러누워 발버둥 치면서 순경 불러 달라고 소리치지 그랬어?"], ["I’d ha’ licked a doozen such as him when I was yoong as thee.", "나 같으믄 자네 나이 때 그런 놈 열둘은 두들겨 팼겠구먼. 하긴 자네는 가엾게도 다 부서져 버린 사람이지.\""], ["But thee be’est a poor broken-doon chap,’ said John, sadly, ‘and God forgi’ me for bragging ower yan o’ his weakest creeturs!’", "존이 서글프게 말했어. \"이 양반의 가장 약한 피조물 중 하나를 두고 큰소리쳤으니, 하느님 용서하소서!\""]], [["Smike opened his mouth to speak, but John Browdie stopped him.", "스마이크가 뭔가 말하려고 입을 열었지만, 존 브로디가 그를 막았어."]], [["‘Stan’ still,’ said the Yorkshireman, ‘and doant’ee speak a morsel o’ talk till I tell’ee.’", "\"가만있어.\" 요크셔 사내가 말했어. \"그라고 내가 말하랄 때까진 한마디도 입 떼지 말어.\""]], [["With this caution, John Browdie shook his head significantly, and drawing a screwdriver from his pocket, took off the box of the lock in a very deliberate and workmanlike manner, and laid it, together with the implement, on the floor.", "이렇게 주의를 준 뒤, 존 브로디는 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕였어. 그리고 주머니에서 드라이버를 꺼내, 아주 신중하고 능숙한 솜씨로 자물쇠 통을 떼어 냈지. 그러고는 그 연장과 함께 바닥에 내려놓았어."]], [["‘See thot?’ said John ‘Thot be thy doin’.", "\"이거 보이지?\" 존이 말했어."], ["Noo, coot awa’!’", "\"이건 자네가 한 짓이여. 자, 튀어!\""]], [["Smike looked vacantly at him, as if unable to comprehend his meaning.", "스마이크는 그 말뜻을 못 알아듣는 듯, 그를 멍하니 쳐다봤어."]], [["‘I say, coot awa’,’ repeated John, hastily.", "\"튀라니까, 튀어.\" 존이 다급하게 되풀이했어."], ["‘Dost thee know where thee livest?", "\"자네 사는 데 어딘지 알지?"], ["Thee dost?", "알아?"], ["Weel.", "좋아."], ["Are yon thy clothes, or schoolmeasther’s?’", "저게 자네 옷이여, 아니믄 선생 옷이여?\""]], [["‘Mine,’ replied Smike, as the Yorkshireman hurried him to the adjoining room, and pointed out a pair of shoes and a coat which were lying on a chair.", "\"제 거예요.\" 스마이크가 대답했어. 요크셔 사내가 그를 옆방으로 급히 끌고 가, 의자 위에 놓인 신발 한 켤레와 외투를 가리켰지."]], [["‘On wi’ ‘em,’ said John, forcing the wrong arm into the wrong sleeve, and winding the tails of the coat round the fugitive’s neck. ‘Noo, foller me, and when thee get’st ootside door, turn to the right, and they wean’t see thee pass.’", "\"걸쳐.\" 존이 한쪽 팔을 엉뚱한 소매에 억지로 쑤셔 넣고, 외투 자락을 도망자의 목에 둘둘 감으며 말했어. \"자, 날 따라와. 그라고 바깥문에 나서면 오른쪽으로 돌아. 그라믄 자네가 지나가는 걸 못 볼 거여.\""]], [["‘But--but--he’ll hear me shut the door,’ replied Smike, trembling from head to foot.", "\"하지만--하지만--제가 문 닫는 소리를 들을 거예요.\" 스마이크가 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨며 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Then dean’t shut it at all,’ retorted John Browdie.", "\"그라믄 아예 닫지 말어.\" 존 브로디가 받았어."], ["‘Dang it, thee bean’t afeard o’ schoolmeasther’s takkin cold, I hope?’", "\"젠장, 설마 선생이 감기 들까 봐 겁나는 건 아니지?\""]], [["‘N-no,’ said Smike, his teeth chattering in his head.", "\"아--아뇨.\" 스마이크가 이를 딱딱 부딪치며 말했어."], ["‘But he brought me back before, and will again.", "\"하지만 저 사람이 전에도 절 도로 끌고 왔어요. 또 그럴 거예요."], ["He will, he will indeed.’", "그럴 거예요, 정말 그럴 거라고요.\""]], [["‘He wull, he wull!’ replied John impatiently.", "\"그러긴 그래!\" 존이 답답하다는 듯 받았어."], ["‘He wean’t, he wean’t.", "\"안 그래, 안 그런다니까."], ["Look’ee!", "들어 봐!"], ["I wont to do this neighbourly loike, and let them think thee’s gotten awa’ o’ theeself, but if he cooms oot o’ thot parlour awhiles theer’t clearing off, he mun’ have mercy on his oun boans, for I wean’t.", "난 이걸 이웃끼리 좋게 처리하고 싶어. 자네가 혼자 알아서 도망친 걸로 믿게 만들고 싶다 이거여."], ["If he foinds it oot, soon efther, I’ll put ‘un on a wrong scent, I warrant ‘ee.", "그래도 만약 자네가 빠져나가는 사이에 저놈이 저 응접실에서 나오기라도 하믄, 제 뼈다귀나 잘 간수해야 할 거여. 난 봐줄 생각 없으니까."], ["But if thee keep’st a good hart, thee’lt be at whoam afore they know thee’st gotten off.", "저놈이 곧 알아채면, 내가 엉뚱한 데로 냄새를 풍겨 줄 테니 걱정 말어. 그러니 자네가 마음만 단단히 먹으믄, 도망친 걸 저놈들이 알기도 전에 집에 가 있을 거여."], ["Coom!’", "가자!\""]], [["Smike, who comprehended just enough of this to know it was intended as encouragement, prepared to follow with tottering steps, when John whispered in his ear.", "스마이크는 이 말을 딱 격려라는 것만 알아들을 만큼은 알아듣고, 비틀거리는 걸음으로 따라나설 채비를 했어. 그때 존이 그의 귀에 대고 속삭였지."]], [["‘Thee’lt just tell yoong Measther that I’m sploiced to ‘Tilly Price, and to be heerd on at the Saracen by latther, and that I bean’t jealous of ‘un--dang it, I’m loike to boost when I think o’ that neight! ‘Cod, I think I see ‘un now, a powderin’ awa’ at the thin bread an’ butther!’", "\"젊은 선생한테 이렇게 전해 줘. 내가 틸리 프라이스하고 짝이 됐고, 새러슨에 편지 보내면 소식 닿을 거라고. 그라고 난 그 양반 질투하지 않는다고 말이여--젠장, 그날 밤 생각만 하믄 배꼽이 빠질 것 같구먼! 워따, 지금도 눈에 선해. 그 얇은 버터 빵을 정신없이 먹어 치우던 꼴이!\""]], [["It was rather a ticklish recollection for John just then, for he was within an ace of breaking out into a loud guffaw.", "존에게는 그 순간 좀 위험한 기억이었어. 하마터면 큰 소리로 껄껄 웃음을 터뜨릴 뻔했거든."], ["Restraining himself, however, just in time, by a great effort, he glided downstairs, hauling Smike behind him; and placing himself close to the parlour door, to confront the first person that might come out, signed to him to make off.", "그래도 가까스로 제때에 안간힘을 써서 참고는, 스마이크를 뒤에 끌고 살그머니 아래층으로 내려갔어. 그리고 누구든 먼저 나오는 사람과 맞닥뜨리려고 응접실 문 바로 옆에 자리를 잡고서, 그에게 어서 내빼라고 손짓했지."]], [["Having got so far, Smike needed no second bidding.", "여기까지 오자 스마이크는 두 번 시킬 것도 없었어."], ["Opening the house-door gently, and casting a look of mingled gratitude and terror at his deliverer, he took the direction which had been indicated to him, and sped away like the wind.", "현관문을 살며시 열고, 고마움과 두려움이 뒤섞인 눈길을 자기 구원자에게 던지더니, 일러 준 방향으로 바람처럼 내달렸지."]], [["The Yorkshireman remained on his post for a few minutes, but, finding that there was no pause in the conversation inside, crept back again unheard, and stood, listening over the stair-rail, for a full hour.", "요크셔 사내는 몇 분 더 제자리를 지켰어. 그러다 안에서 이야기가 끊이지 않는 걸 알고는, 들키지 않게 도로 살금살금 기어 올라갔지."], ["Everything remaining perfectly quiet, he got into Mr.", "그러고는 계단 난간에 기대 꼬박 한 시간을 귀 기울이고 서 있었어."], ["Squeers’s bed, once more, and drawing the clothes over his head, laughed till he was nearly smothered.", "모든 게 더없이 조용하자, 그는 다시 스퀴어스 씨의 침대로 기어들었어. 그러고는 이불을 머리까지 끌어 올린 채, 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이 되도록 웃어 댔지."]], [["If there could only have been somebody by, to see how the bedclothes shook, and to see the Yorkshireman’s great red face and round head appear above the sheets, every now and then, like some jovial monster coming to the surface to breathe, and once more dive down convulsed with the laughter which came bursting forth afresh--that somebody would have been scarcely less amused than John Browdie himself.", "그 곁에 누군가 있어서 이불이 어떻게 들썩이는지, 또 요크셔 사내의 커다랗고 빨간 얼굴과 둥근 머리가 시트 위로 이따금 불쑥 솟아오르는 걸 봤더라면 말이야. 마치 어떤 유쾌한 괴물이 숨을 쉬러 수면 위로 떠올랐다가, 새로 터져 나오는 웃음에 다시 발작하듯 잠겨 드는 것 같았거든--그 누군가가 있었다면, 존 브로디 자신 못지않게 즐거워했을 거야."]], [["# CHAPTER 40", "# CHAPTER 40"]], [["In which Nicholas falls in Love.", "니콜라스가 사랑에 빠지는 이야기."], ["He employs a Mediator, whose Proceedings are crowned with unexpected Success, excepting in one solitary Particular", "그는 중재자를 한 사람 내세우는데, 그의 활약은 단 한 가지만 빼고 뜻밖의 성공을 거둔다."]], [["Once more out of the clutches of his old persecutor, it needed no fresh stimulation to call forth the utmost energy and exertion that Smike was capable of summoning to his aid. Without pausing for a moment to reflect upon the course he was taking, or the probability of its leading him homewards or the reverse, he fled away with surprising swiftness and constancy of purpose, borne upon such wings as only Fear can wear, and impelled by imaginary shouts in the well remembered voice of Squeers, who, with a host of pursuers, seemed to the poor fellow’s disordered senses to press hard upon his track; now left at a greater distance in the rear, and now gaining faster and faster upon him, as the alternations of hope and terror agitated him by turns. Long after he had become assured that these sounds were but the creation of his excited brain, he still held on, at a pace which even weakness and exhaustion could scarcely retard. It was not until the darkness and quiet of a country road, recalled him to a sense of external objects, and the starry sky, above, warned him of the rapid flight of time, that, covered with dust and panting for breath, he stopped to listen and look about him.", "오랜 박해자의 손아귀에서 다시 한번 벗어나자, 스마이크가 끌어모을 수 있는 온 힘과 안간힘을 다 짜내는 데에 새삼스런 자극 따위는 필요 없었다. 자기가 가고 있는 길이 어디인지, 그게 집 쪽으로 향하는 길인지 반대 방향인지 따져 볼 겨를도 없이, 그는 놀라울 만큼 빠르고 한결같은 마음으로 달아났다. 오직 두려움만이 달 수 있는 날개를 단 채, 또 똑똑히 기억나는 스퀴어스의 목소리로 들려오는 환청 같은 고함 소리에 떠밀려서. 가엾은 그 사내의 어지러운 감각 속에서는, 스퀴어스가 한 무리의 추격자들과 함께 바짝 뒤를 쫓아오는 것만 같았다. 어떤 때는 그것이 한참 뒤로 멀어졌다가, 또 어떤 때는 점점 빠르게 가까워졌다. 희망과 공포가 번갈아 그를 뒤흔드는 대로 말이다. 그 소리들이 그저 흥분한 머릿속이 만들어 낸 것뿐이라는 걸 확실히 깨달은 뒤로도, 그는 한참을 더 내달렸다. 쇠약함과 탈진도 거의 늦추지 못할 만큼 빠른 걸음으로. 시골길의 어둠과 고요가 그를 바깥세상의 사물로 다시 끌어다 놓고, 머리 위의 별이 총총한 하늘이 시간이 얼마나 빠르게 흘렀는지 일러 주고 나서야, 그는 비로소 멈춰 섰다. 먼지를 뒤집어쓰고 숨을 헐떡이며, 귀를 기울이고 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["All was still and silent.", "사방이 잠잠하고 고요했다. 멀리서 빛이 환하게 번지며 하늘에 따스한 노을빛을 드리운 곳, 거기가 거대한 도시가 자리한 자리였다."], ["A glare of light in the distance, casting a warm glow upon the sky, marked where the huge city lay.", "울타리와 도랑으로 갈라진 외딴 들판들이, 그가 달아나면서 부딪치고 기어서 넘은 곳도 많았는데, 그 들판들이 그가 온 길 양옆으로, 또 길 건너편으로도 죽 펼쳐져 있었다. 어느새 밤이 깊었다."], ["Solitary fields, divided by hedges and ditches, through many of which he had crashed and scrambled in his flight, skirted the road, both by the way he had come and upon the opposite side.", "그가 지나온 그런 샛길로는 추격자들도 그를 따라잡기 어려웠다. 그러니 그가 자기 거처로 돌아갈 수 있다면, 분명 바로 이런 시각에, 어둠을 틈타서여야 했다."], ["It was late now.", "이 사실은 차츰차츰, 스마이크의 머릿속에도 꽤 또렷하게 떠올랐다. 처음에 그는 어렴풋하고 어린아이 같은 생각을 품었다."], ["They could scarcely trace him by such paths as he had taken, and if he could hope to regain his own dwelling, it must surely be at such a time as that, and under cover of the darkness.", "시골로 한 십이 마일쯤 더 들어갔다가, 런던을 멀찍이 피해 크게 빙 돌아서 집으로 돌아가자는 거였다. 혼자 거리를 가로지르는 게 어찌나 무서웠던지, 또다시 그 무서운 적과 마주칠까 봐 두려웠던 것이다."], ["This, by degrees, became pretty plain, even to the mind of Smike.", "하지만 이런 생각들이 떠올려 준 결론에 따르기로 하고, 그는 발길을 돌렸다. 그리고 두렵고 미심쩍은 마음이 적잖았지만, 큰길로 나서서 다시 런던을 향해 걸었다."], ["He had, at first, entertained some vague and childish idea of travelling into the country for ten or a dozen miles, and then returning homewards by a wide circuit, which should keep him clear of London--so great was his apprehension of traversing the streets alone, lest he should again encounter his dreaded enemy--but, yielding to the conviction which these thoughts inspired, he turned back, and taking the open road, though not without many fears and misgivings, made for London again, with scarcely less speed of foot than that with which he had left the temporary abode of Mr. Squeers.", "스퀴어스의 그 임시 거처를 떠날 때 못지않게 빠른 걸음으로."]], [["By the time he re-entered it, at the western extremity, the greater part of the shops were closed.", "그가 도시 서쪽 끝으로 다시 들어섰을 무렵엔 가게들이 거의 다 문을 닫은 뒤였다."], ["Of the throngs of people who had been tempted abroad after the heat of the day, but few remained in the streets, and they were lounging home.", "낮의 더위가 가시자 거리로 나왔던 그 많은 사람들 중에도 거리에 남은 이는 몇 안 되었고, 그나마도 어슬렁어슬렁 집으로 가는 길이었다."], ["But of these he asked his way from time to time, and by dint of repeated inquiries, he at length reached the dwelling of Newman Noggs.", "하지만 그는 그 사람들에게 이따금 길을 물었고, 묻고 또 물은 끝에 마침내 뉴먼 노그스가 사는 곳에 다다랐다."]], [["All that evening, Newman had been hunting and searching in byways and corners for the very person who now knocked at his door, while Nicholas had been pursuing the same inquiry in other directions. He was sitting, with a melancholy air, at his poor supper, when Smike’s timorous and uncertain knock reached his ears. Alive to every sound, in his anxious and expectant state, Newman hurried downstairs, and, uttering a cry of joyful surprise, dragged the welcome visitor into the passage and up the stairs, and said not a word until he had him safe in his own garret and the door was shut behind them, when he mixed a great mug-full of gin-and-water, and holding it to Smike’s mouth, as one might hold a bowl of medicine to the lips of a refractory child, commanded him to drain it to the last drop.", "그날 저녁 내내, 뉴먼은 지금 자기 집 문을 두드리는 바로 그 사람을 찾아 골목과 구석구석을 뒤지고 다녔다. 그러는 동안 니콜라스도 다른 방향으로 똑같이 찾아 헤맸다. 뉴먼이 우울한 얼굴로 초라한 저녁을 들고 있는데, 스마이크의 머뭇거리고 자신 없는 노크 소리가 그의 귀에 들려왔다. 불안하고 무언가를 기다리는 마음에 작은 소리에도 곤두서 있던 뉴먼은 서둘러 아래층으로 내려갔다. 그러고는 반갑고 놀란 외마디 소리를 지르며 이 고마운 손님을 복도로, 다시 위층으로 끌어올렸다. 한마디도 하지 않다가, 자기 다락방에 무사히 들여놓고 등 뒤로 문을 닫고 나서야, 진과 물을 큰 잔 가득 섞었다. 그리고 떼쓰는 아이 입에 약사발을 갖다 대듯 스마이크의 입에 갖다 대고는, 한 방울도 남기지 말고 다 마시라고 명령했다."]], [["Newman looked uncommonly blank when he found that Smike did little more than put his lips to the precious mixture; he was in the act of raising the mug to his own mouth with a deep sigh of compassion for his poor friend’s weakness, when Smike, beginning to relate the adventures which had befallen him, arrested him half-way, and he stood listening, with the mug in his hand.", "그런데 스마이크가 그 귀한 혼합주에 입술만 살짝 대고 마는 걸 보고는, 뉴먼은 유난히 맥이 빠진 표정이 되었다. 그는 가엾은 친구의 약한 꼴에 깊은 동정의 한숨을 쉬며 그 잔을 자기 입으로 가져가던 참이었다. 그때 스마이크가 자기가 겪은 모험을 이야기하기 시작하는 바람에 그는 손을 멈췄고, 잔을 손에 든 채 가만히 서서 귀를 기울였다."]], [["It was odd enough to see the change that came over Newman as Smike proceeded. At first he stood, rubbing his lips with the back of his hand, as a preparatory ceremony towards composing himself for a draught; then, at the mention of Squeers, he took the mug under his arm, and opening his eyes very wide, looked on, in the utmost astonishment. When Smike came to the assault upon himself in the hackney coach, he hastily deposited the mug upon the table, and limped up and down the room in a state of the greatest excitement, stopping himself with a jerk, every now and then, as if to listen more attentively. When John Browdie came to be spoken of, he dropped, by slow and gradual degrees, into a chair, and rubbing his hands upon his knees--quicker and quicker as the story reached its climax--burst, at last, into a laugh composed of one loud sonorous ‘Ha! ha!’ having given vent to which, his countenance immediately fell again as he inquired, with the utmost anxiety, whether it was probable that John Browdie and Squeers had come to blows.", "스마이크가 이야기를 이어 가는 동안 뉴먼에게 일어난 변화를 보는 건 꽤나 우스운 노릇이었다. 처음에 그는 손등으로 입술을 문지르며 서 있었다. 한 모금 들이켤 마음의 준비를 하는 의식 같은 거였다. 그러다 스퀴어스 이야기가 나오자, 잔을 겨드랑이에 끼고 눈을 휘둥그렇게 뜨더니, 잔뜩 놀란 얼굴로 지켜보았다. 스마이크가 삯마차 안에서 자기를 덮쳤다는 대목에 이르자, 그는 황급히 잔을 탁자에 내려놓고는 방 안을 절뚝거리며 왔다 갔다 했다. 잔뜩 흥분한 채로, 이따금 더 귀를 기울이려는 듯 홱 멈춰 서기도 하면서. 존 브라우디 이야기가 나오자, 그는 천천히, 차츰차츰 의자에 주저앉았다. 그러고는 무릎에 두 손을 비벼 댔는데 — 이야기가 절정에 다다를수록 점점 더 빠르게 비벼 대다가 — 마침내 \"하! 하!\" 하고 한 번 크고 우렁차게 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그 웃음을 토해 내자마자 그의 얼굴은 곧바로 다시 어두워졌고, 더없이 불안한 얼굴로 물었다. 존 브라우디하고 스퀴어스가 주먹다짐을 벌였을 것 같으냐고."]], [["‘No!", "\"아니요!"], ["I think not,’ replied Smike.", "그렇진 않을 거예요.\" 스마이크가 대답했다."], ["‘I don’t think he could have missed me till I had got quite away.’", "\"제가 완전히 벗어날 때까지 그 사람은 제가 없어진 걸 몰랐을 거예요.\""]], [["Newman scratched his head with a shout of great disappointment, and once more lifting up the mug, applied himself to the contents; smiling meanwhile, over the rim, with a grim and ghastly smile at Smike.", "뉴먼은 크게 실망한 외마디 소리와 함께 머리를 긁적였다. 그러고는 다시 한번 잔을 들어 그 속에 든 걸 입에 댔다. 그러면서 잔 가장자리 너머로 스마이크를 향해 음산하고 섬뜩한 미소를 지었다."]], [["‘You shall stay here,’ said Newman; ‘you’re tired--fagged.", "\"여기 있어.\" 뉴먼이 말했다."], ["I’ll tell them you’re come back.", "\"너 지쳤잖아 — 녹초가 됐어. 네가 돌아왔다고 내가 알려 줄게."], ["They have been half mad about you.", "다들 너 때문에 반쯤 미칠 지경이었거든."], ["Mr. Nicholas--’", "니콜라스 도련님은—\""]], [["‘God bless him!’ cried Smike.", "\"그분께 신의 축복이 있기를!\" 스마이크가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Amen!’ returned Newman.", "\"아멘!\" 뉴먼이 받아 말했다."], ["‘He hasn’t had a minute’s rest or peace; no more has the old lady, nor Miss Nickleby.’", "\"그분은 잠시도 쉬거나 마음 편할 틈이 없었어. 노부인도, 케이트 아가씨도 마찬가지였고.\""]], [["‘No, no.", "\"네, 네."], ["Has SHE thought about me?’ said Smike.", "그 아가씨가 제 생각을 했다고요?\" 스마이크가 말했다."], ["‘Has she though?", "\"정말 그랬어요?"], ["oh, has she, has she?", "아, 정말요, 정말요?"], ["Don’t tell me so if she has not.’", "아니면 그렇다고 말하지 마세요.\""]], [["‘She has,’ cried Newman.", "\"했어.\" 뉴먼이 외쳤다."], ["‘She is as noble-hearted as she is beautiful.’", "\"그 아가씬 아름다운 만큼 마음씨도 고결하거든.\""]], [["‘Yes, yes!’ cried Smike.", "\"네, 네!\" 스마이크가 외쳤다."], ["‘Well said!’", "\"맞는 말이에요!\""]], [["‘So mild and gentle,’ said Newman.", "\"어찌나 온화하고 상냥한지.\" 뉴먼이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, yes!’ cried Smike, with increasing eagerness.", "\"네, 네!\" 스마이크가 점점 더 간절하게 외쳤다."]], [["‘And yet with such a true and gallant spirit,’ pursued Newman.", "\"게다가 그렇게 진실하고 늠름한 기개까지 갖췄지.\" 뉴먼이 말을 이었다."]], [["He was going on, in his enthusiasm, when, chancing to look at his companion, he saw that he had covered his face with his hands, and that tears were stealing out between his fingers.", "그가 신이 나서 계속 떠들려는데, 마침 옆을 보니 스마이크가 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 있었다. 그리고 손가락 사이로 눈물이 흘러나오고 있었다."]], [["A moment before, the boy’s eyes were sparkling with unwonted fire, and every feature had been lighted up with an excitement which made him appear, for the moment, quite a different being.", "조금 전만 해도 그 소년의 눈은 여느 때 없던 불꽃으로 반짝였고, 얼굴 구석구석이 흥분으로 환하게 빛나, 그 순간만큼은 아주 딴사람처럼 보였는데 말이다."]], [["‘Well, well,’ muttered Newman, as if he were a little puzzled.", "\"이런, 이런.\" 뉴먼은 조금 어리둥절한 듯 중얼거렸다."], ["‘It has touched ME, more than once, to think such a nature should have been exposed to such trials; this poor fellow--yes, yes,--he feels that too--it softens him--makes him think of his former misery.", "\"이런 천성을 타고난 애가 그런 시련을 겪었다니, 생각하면 나도 한두 번 마음이 아렸지. 이 가엾은 녀석도 — 그래, 그래 — 똑같이 느끼는 거야 — 그래서 마음이 약해진 거지 — 옛날 그 비참했던 시절이 떠오르는 거야."], ["Hah!", "허!"], ["That’s it?", "그런 거지?"], ["Yes, that’s--hum!’", "그래, 그게—흠!\""]], [["It was by no means clear, from the tone of these broken reflections, that Newman Noggs considered them as explaining, at all satisfactorily, the emotion which had suggested them.", "이렇게 띄엄띄엄 내뱉은 혼잣말의 어조로 보아, 뉴먼 노그스가 정작 그 말들이 그런 감정을 불러일으킨 까닭을 만족스럽게 풀어 준다고 여겼는지는 도무지 분명하지 않았다."], ["He sat, in a musing attitude, for some time, regarding Smike occasionally with an anxious and doubtful glance, which sufficiently showed that he was not very remotely connected with his thoughts.", "그는 한동안 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 자세로 앉아, 이따금 불안하고 미심쩍은 눈길로 스마이크를 바라보았다. 그 눈길은 그가 그 생각거리와 그리 동떨어진 처지가 아니라는 걸 충분히 보여 주었다."]], [["At length he repeated his proposition that Smike should remain where he was for that night, and that he (Noggs) should straightway repair to the cottage to relieve the suspense of the family. But, as Smike would not hear of this--pleading his anxiety to see his friends again--they eventually sallied forth together; and the night being, by this time, far advanced, and Smike being, besides, so footsore that he could hardly crawl along, it was within an hour of sunrise when they reached their destination.", "마침내 그는 아까 한 제안을 되풀이했다. 스마이크는 그날 밤 여기 그대로 있고, 자기가 — 그러니까 노그스가 — 곧장 오두막으로 가서 식구들의 애타는 마음을 덜어 주겠다는 거였다. 하지만 스마이크가 친구들을 다시 보고 싶어 견딜 수 없다며 그 말을 듣지 않았다. 그래서 둘은 결국 함께 길을 나섰다. 그런데 이때쯤엔 밤이 한참 깊었고, 게다가 스마이크는 발이 어찌나 부르텄던지 기다시피 겨우 걸을 지경이라, 그들이 목적지에 다다른 건 해 뜨기 한 시간 전쯤이었다."]], [["At the first sound of their voices outside the house, Nicholas, who had passed a sleepless night, devising schemes for the recovery of his lost charge, started from his bed, and joyfully admitted them. There was so much noisy conversation, and congratulation, and indignation, that the remainder of the family were soon awakened, and Smike received a warm and cordial welcome, not only from Kate, but from Mrs. Nickleby also, who assured him of her future favour and regard, and was so obliging as to relate, for his entertainment and that of the assembled circle, a most remarkable account extracted from some work the name of which she had never known, of a miraculous escape from some prison, but what one she couldn’t remember, effected by an officer whose name she had forgotten, confined for some crime which she didn’t clearly recollect.", "집 밖에서 두 사람의 목소리가 처음 들려오자, 잃어버린 보호 대상을 되찾을 궁리를 짜내느라 한숨도 못 잔 니콜라스가 침대에서 벌떡 일어나, 기쁘게 둘을 맞아들였다. 시끌벅적한 이야기와 축하와 분개가 어찌나 오갔던지, 나머지 식구들도 곧 잠에서 깼다. 스마이크는 따뜻하고 다정한 환영을 받았다. 케이트뿐 아니라 니클비 부인한테서도 말이다. 부인은 앞으로도 그를 아끼고 잘 봐주겠다고 약속했다. 그러고는 친절하게도, 그와 둘러앉은 식구들의 흥을 돋워 주려고 아주 놀라운 이야기 하나를 들려주었다. 어느 책에서 읽은 거라는데 그 책 제목은 한 번도 안 적이 없었고, 어떤 감옥에서 기적처럼 탈출한 이야기라는데 무슨 감옥인지는 기억이 안 났고, 그 탈출을 해낸 건 어떤 장교라는데 이름은 잊어버렸으며, 그 장교가 무슨 죄로 갇혔던 건지도 또렷이 떠오르지 않는, 그런 이야기였다."]], [["At first Nicholas was disposed to give his uncle credit for some portion of this bold attempt (which had so nearly proved successful) to carry off Smike; but on more mature consideration, he was inclined to think that the full merit of it rested with Mr.", "처음에 니콜라스는 스마이크를 빼돌리려던 이 대담한 시도가(하마터면 성공할 뻔했다) 어느 정도는 삼촌의 짓이려니 했다. 하지만 좀 더 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에, 그 공로 전부가 스퀴어스 씨한테 돌아간다고 보는 쪽으로 마음이 기울었다."], ["Squeers.", "일이 정말 어떻게 된 건지 존 브라우디를 통해 알아낼 수 있으면 알아내야겠다고 마음먹고, 그는 날마다 하는 일에 나섰다."], ["Determined to ascertain, if he could, through John Browdie, how the case really stood, he betook himself to his daily occupation: meditating, as he went, on a great variety of schemes for the punishment of the Yorkshire schoolmaster, all of which had their foundation in the strictest principles of retributive justice, and had but the one drawback of being wholly impracticable.", "가는 길에 그는 그 요크셔 교장을 벌줄 갖가지 방법을 궁리했다. 하나같이 응보적 정의의 가장 엄정한 원칙에 바탕을 둔 것들이었지만, 죄다 도무지 실행할 수 없다는 단 하나의 흠이 있었다."]], [["‘A fine morning, Mr.", "\"날씨 좋은 아침이네요, 링킨워터 씨!\""], ["Linkinwater!’ said Nicholas, entering the office.", "니콜라스가 사무실에 들어서며 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!’ replied Tim, ‘talk of the country, indeed!", "\"아!\" 팀이 대꾸했다."], ["What do you think of this, now, for a day--a London day--eh?’", "\"시골 타령은 무슨! 자, 이런 날을 어떻게 생각하나 — 런던의 하루 말일세 — 어때?\""]], [["‘It’s a little clearer out of town,’ said Nicholas.", "\"시내를 좀 벗어나면 공기가 조금 더 맑긴 하죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했다."]], [["‘Clearer!’ echoed Tim Linkinwater.", "\"맑다고!\" 팀 링킨워터가 따라 외쳤다."], ["‘You should see it from my bedroom window.’", "\"내 침실 창문에서 한번 봐야 하는데.\""]], [["‘You should see it from MINE,’ replied Nicholas, with a smile.", "\"제 방 창문에서도 봐야 하고요.\" 니콜라스가 웃으며 대꾸했다."]], [["‘Pooh!", "\"쳇! 쳇!\""], ["pooh!’ said Tim Linkinwater, ‘don’t tell me.", "팀 링킨워터가 말했다. \"내게 그런 소리 말게."], ["Country!’ (Bow was quite a rustic place to Tim.) ‘Nonsense!", "시골이라니!\" (팀에게는 보우 같은 데도 영락없는 시골이었다.) \"말도 안 돼!"], ["What can you get in the country but new-laid eggs and flowers?", "시골에서 뭘 얻을 수 있겠나, 갓 낳은 달걀하고 꽃밖에 더 있어? 갓 낳은 달걀이야 아침 먹기 전에 아무 날이나 레든홀 시장에서 살 수 있지."], ["I can buy new-laid eggs in Leadenhall Market, any morning before breakfast; and as to flowers, it’s worth a run upstairs to smell my mignonette, or to see the double wallflower in the back-attic window, at No. 6, in the court.’", "꽃으로 말하자면, 위층에 한달음에 올라가서 내 미뇨네트 냄새를 맡거나, 골목 6번지 뒤쪽 다락 창문에 핀 겹꽃 꽃무를 볼 만한 가치가 있다네.\""]], [["‘There is a double wallflower at No.", "\"골목 6번지에 겹꽃 꽃무가 있단 말이죠?\""], ["6, in the court, is there?’ said Nicholas.", "니콜라스가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, is there!’ replied Tim, ‘and planted in a cracked jug, without a spout.", "\"그래, 있고말고!\" 팀이 대꾸했다."], ["There were hyacinths there, this last spring, blossoming, in--but you’ll laugh at that, of course.’", "\"주둥이도 떨어져 나간 금 간 항아리에 심어져 있지. 지난봄엔 거기 히아신스도 피었는데, 어디 핀 거냐면 — 아니, 자넨 어차피 웃겠지.\""]], [["‘At what?’", "\"뭘 보고요?\""]], [["‘At their blossoming in old blacking-bottles,’ said Tim.", "\"낡은 구두약 병에서 핀 걸 보고 말일세.\" 팀이 말했다."]], [["‘Not I, indeed,’ returned Nicholas.", "\"전 안 웃어요, 정말로요.\" 니콜라스가 받아 말했다."]], [["Tim looked wistfully at him, for a moment, as if he were encouraged by the tone of this reply to be more communicative on the subject; and sticking behind his ear, a pen that he had been making, and shutting up his knife with a smart click, said,", "팀은 잠시 그를 그윽이 바라보았다. 마치 이 대답의 어조에 힘을 얻어 그 일에 대해 더 털어놓아도 되겠다 싶은 듯이. 그러고는 다듬던 펜을 귀 뒤에 꽂고, 칼을 딸깍 경쾌하게 접더니 말했다."]], [["‘They belong to a sickly bedridden hump-backed boy, and seem to be the only pleasure, Mr.", "\"그 꽃들은 병약하고 자리보전한 곱사등이 소년의 것이라네. 그리고 그게 그 애의 그 서글픈 삶에서 하나뿐인 낙인 것 같아, 니클비 군."], ["Nickleby, of his sad existence.", "그 애를 처음 눈여겨본 게 몇 해 전인지,\""], ["How many years is it,’ said Tim, pondering, ‘since I first noticed him, quite a little child, dragging himself about on a pair of tiny crutches?", "팀이 곰곰이 헤아리며 말했다. \"조그만 목발 한 쌍에 몸을 끌면서 다니던, 아주 어린아이였을 때 말이야."], ["Well!", "글쎄!"], ["Well!", "글쎄! 그리 오래되진 않았어."], ["Not many; but though they would appear nothing, if I thought of other things, they seem a long, long time, when I think of him.", "하지만 다른 일들을 생각하면 아무것도 아닐 것 같은 그 세월이, 그 애를 생각하면 길고 긴 세월처럼 느껴진다네. 참 서글픈 일이지,\""], ["It is a sad thing,’ said Tim, breaking off, ‘to see a little deformed child sitting apart from other children, who are active and merry, watching the games he is denied the power to share in.", "팀이 말을 끊었다가 이었다. \"조그맣게 몸이 비뚤어진 아이가 다른 아이들하고 떨어져 앉아서, 활기차고 즐거운 그 애들이 노는 걸 지켜보는 모습은 말이야."], ["He made my heart ache very often.’", "자긴 끼어들 힘도 없는 그 놀이를. 그 애 때문에 내 가슴이 참 자주 아렸지.\""]], [["‘It is a good heart,’ said Nicholas, ‘that disentangles itself from the close avocations of every day, to heed such things.", "\"그렇게 매일같이 바쁜 일에서 마음을 떼어 그런 걸 헤아릴 줄 아는 건,\" 니콜라스가 말했다."], ["You were saying--’", "\"선한 마음이지요. 아까 하시던 말씀이—\""]], [["‘That the flowers belonged to this poor boy,’ said Tim; ‘that’s all.", "\"그 꽃들이 그 가엾은 소년 거라는 얘기 말이지.\" 팀이 말했다."], ["When it is fine weather, and he can crawl out of bed, he draws a chair close to the window, and sits there, looking at them and arranging them, all day long.", "\"그게 다야. 날씨가 좋아서 침대에서 기어 나올 수 있으면, 그 애는 의자를 창문 가까이 끌어다 거기 앉아서, 하루 종일 꽃을 바라보고 매만진다네."], ["He used to nod, at first, and then we came to speak.", "처음엔 그저 고개만 끄덕이곤 했는데, 그러다 우리는 말을 나누는 사이가 됐지."], ["Formerly, when I called to him of a morning, and asked him how he was, he would smile, and say, “Better!”", "예전엔 아침에 내가 부르면서 좀 어떠냐고 물으면, 그 애는 빙긋 웃으며 '더 나아졌어요!' 하고 답하곤 했어."], ["but now he shakes his head, and only bends more closely over his old plants.", "그런데 이젠 고개를 가로젓고는, 그저 자기 낡은 화분들 위로 더 가까이 몸을 숙일 뿐이라네."], ["It must be dull to watch the dark housetops and the flying clouds, for so many months; but he is very patient.’", "그렇게 여러 달을 어두운 지붕들하고 흘러가는 구름이나 바라보고 있자면 따분할 텐데도, 그 애는 참 잘 견딘다네.\""]], [["‘Is there nobody in the house to cheer or help him?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"집안에 그 애를 위로해 주거나 도와줄 사람이 아무도 없나요?\" 니콜라스가 물었다."]], [["‘His father lives there, I believe,’ replied Tim, ‘and other people too; but no one seems to care much for the poor sickly cripple. I have asked him, very often, if I can do nothing for him; his answer is always the same. “Nothing.” His voice is growing weak of late, but I can SEE that he makes the old reply. He can’t leave his bed now, so they have moved it close beside the window, and there he lies, all day: now looking at the sky, and now at his flowers, which he still makes shift to trim and water, with his own thin hands. At night, when he sees my candle, he draws back his curtain, and leaves it so, till I am in bed. It seems such company to him to know that I am there, that I often sit at my window for an hour or more, that he may see I am still awake; and sometimes I get up in the night to look at the dull melancholy light in his little room, and wonder whether he is awake or sleeping.", "\"아버지가 거기 사는 것 같긴 해.\" 팀이 대답했다. \"다른 사람들도 있고. 하지만 그 가엾고 병든 절름발이를 그리 마음에 두는 사람은 아무도 없는 듯해. 내가 아주 자주 물어봤다네, 너를 위해 내가 해 줄 게 없겠냐고. 그 애 대답은 늘 똑같아. '없어요.' 요즘 들어 목소리가 점점 약해지고 있지만, 그래도 늘 그 똑같은 대답을 하는 게 눈에 보이지. 이젠 침대를 떠나지도 못해서, 식구들이 침대를 창문 바로 옆으로 옮겨 줬어. 그래서 그 애는 하루 종일 거기 누워, 어떤 땐 하늘을 보고 어떤 땐 자기 꽃을 보지. 그 꽃을 아직도 제 가느다란 손으로 어떻게든 다듬고 물을 준다네. 밤이 되어 내 촛불이 보이면, 그 애는 커튼을 젖혀 두고 내가 잠자리에 들 때까지 그렇게 둬. 내가 거기 있다는 걸 아는 게 그 애한테 그렇게 큰 벗이 되는가 봐. 그래서 나도 한 시간이고 그 이상이고 자주 창가에 앉아 있곤 한다네. 내가 아직 깨어 있는 걸 그 애가 볼 수 있게 말이야. 그리고 가끔은 밤중에 일어나서, 그 애 작은 방의 흐릿하고 우울한 불빛을 바라보며, 그 애가 깨어 있을까 잠들었을까 헤아려 본다네."]], [["‘The night will not be long coming,’ said Tim, ‘when he will sleep, and never wake again on earth.", "밤은 머지않아 올 거야.\" 팀이 말했다."], ["We have never so much as shaken hands in all our lives; and yet I shall miss him like an old friend.", "\"그 애가 잠들어 다시는 이 땅에서 깨어나지 않을 밤 말이야. 우리는 평생 한 번도 손 한 번 맞잡은 적 없지만, 그래도 난 그 애를 오랜 친구처럼 그리워하게 될 걸세."], ["Are there any country flowers that could interest me like these, do you think?", "시골 꽃 중에 이 꽃들만큼 내 마음을 끄는 게 있겠나, 자네 생각엔?"], ["Or do you suppose that the withering of a hundred kinds of the choicest flowers that blow, called by the hardest Latin names that were ever invented, would give me one fraction of the pain that I shall feel when these old jugs and bottles are swept away as lumber?", "아니면 말일세, 여태 만들어진 가장 어려운 라틴어 이름이 붙은 가장 귀한 꽃을 백 가지 종류 시들게 한들, 이 낡은 항아리와 병들이 잡동사니라고 쓸려 나갈 때 내가 느낄 아픔의 한 조각만큼이라도 줄 수 있겠나? 시골이라니!\""], ["Country!’ cried Tim, with a contemptuous emphasis; ‘don’t you know that I couldn’t have such a court under my bedroom window, anywhere, but in London?’", "팀이 경멸하듯 힘주어 외쳤다. \"내 침실 창문 아래에 이런 골목을 둘 수 있는 데가 런던 말고 어디 또 있는 줄 아나?\""]], [["With which inquiry, Tim turned his back, and pretending to be absorbed in his accounts, took an opportunity of hastily wiping his eyes when he supposed Nicholas was looking another way.", "이렇게 물으면서 팀은 등을 돌리고는, 장부에 푹 빠진 척하다가, 니콜라스가 딴 데를 보는 줄로 알 때 슬쩍 눈가를 훔칠 틈을 냈다."]], [["Whether it was that Tim’s accounts were more than usually intricate that morning, or whether it was that his habitual serenity had been a little disturbed by these recollections, it so happened that when Nicholas returned from executing some commission, and inquired whether Mr.", "그날 아침따라 팀의 장부가 여느 때보다 유난히 복잡했던 탓인지, 아니면 그 추억들 때문에 평소의 차분함이 조금 흐트러진 탓인지, 어쨌든 이런 일이 벌어졌다. 니콜라스가 어떤 심부름을 다녀와서 찰스 치어리블 씨가 방에 혼자 있느냐고 묻자, 팀이 냉큼, 조금도 망설이지 않고 그렇다고 대답한 것이다."], ["Charles Cheeryble was alone in his room, Tim promptly, and without the smallest hesitation, replied in the affirmative, although somebody had passed into the room not ten minutes before, and Tim took especial and particular pride in preventing any intrusion on either of the brothers when they were engaged with any visitor whatever.", "십 분도 채 안 되어 누가 그 방으로 들어갔는데도 말이다. 게다가 팀은 형제 중 누구든 손님과 무슨 일이든 하고 있을 때 아무도 끼어들지 못하게 막는 걸 유난히, 특별히 자랑으로 여기는 사람이었는데도."]], [["‘I’ll take this letter to him at once,’ said Nicholas, ‘if that’s the case.’ And with that, he walked to the room and knocked at the door.", "\"그렇다면 이 편지를 지금 바로 갖다 드릴게요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다. 그러고는 그 방으로 걸어가 문을 두드렸다."]], [["No answer.", "대답이 없었다."]], [["Another knock, and still no answer.", "다시 한번 두드렸지만, 여전히 대답이 없었다."]], [["‘He can’t be here,’ thought Nicholas.", "'안 계신가 봐.' 니콜라스는 생각했다."], ["‘I’ll lay it on his table.’", "'책상에 올려놓아야겠다.'"]], [["So, Nicholas opened the door and walked in; and very quickly he turned to walk out again, when he saw, to his great astonishment and discomfiture, a young lady upon her knees at Mr. Cheeryble’s feet, and Mr Cheeryble beseeching her to rise, and entreating a third person, who had the appearance of the young lady’s female attendant, to add her persuasions to his to induce her to do so.", "그래서 니콜라스는 문을 열고 안으로 들어갔다. 그런데 곧바로 다시 나가려고 몸을 돌렸다. 어떤 젊은 아가씨가 치어리블 씨 발치에 무릎을 꿇고 있고, 치어리블 씨는 그녀에게 일어나라고 간청하고 있는 걸 보았기 때문이다. 더없이 놀랍고 난처한 광경이었다. 그리고 아가씨의 시중을 드는 하녀로 보이는 세 번째 사람한테, 자기를 도와 함께 설득해서 아가씨를 일으켜 달라고 사정하고 있었다."]], [["Nicholas stammered out an awkward apology, and was precipitately retiring, when the young lady, turning her head a little, presented to his view the features of the lovely girl whom he had seen at the register-office on his first visit long before. Glancing from her to the attendant, he recognised the same clumsy servant who had accompanied her then; and between his admiration of the young lady’s beauty, and the confusion and surprise of this unexpected recognition, he stood stock-still, in such a bewildered state of surprise and embarrassment that, for the moment, he was quite bereft of the power either to speak or move.", "니콜라스는 어색하게 사과의 말을 더듬더듬 내뱉고는 황급히 물러나려 했다. 그때 아가씨가 고개를 살짝 돌리는 바람에, 그녀의 얼굴이 그의 눈에 들어왔다. 오래전 처음 찾아갔던 그날 소개소에서 보았던 바로 그 사랑스러운 아가씨였다. 그녀에게서 하녀에게로 눈길을 옮기니, 그때 그녀를 따라왔던 바로 그 굼뜬 하녀였다. 아가씨의 아름다움에 감탄하는 마음과, 이 뜻밖의 알아봄에서 온 당혹과 놀라움 사이에서, 그는 못 박힌 듯 꼼짝 못 하고 서 있었다. 어찌나 놀라고 어쩔 줄 몰랐던지, 그 순간만큼은 말을 할 힘도 움직일 힘도 완전히 잃은 채였다."]], [["‘My dear ma’am--my dear young lady,’ cried brother Charles in violent agitation, ‘pray don’t--not another word, I beseech and entreat you!", "\"이런 부인 — 아니 아가씨.\" 찰스 형제가 몹시 안절부절못하며 외쳤다."], ["I implore you--I beg of you--to rise.", "\"제발 — 한마디도 더 마시오, 부디 빌고 또 비오! 청하고 또 청하니 — 부디 일어나시오."], ["We--we--are not alone.’", "우리는 — 우리는 — 둘만 있는 게 아니라오.\""]], [["As he spoke, he raised the young lady, who staggered to a chair and swooned away.", "말을 하면서 그는 아가씨를 일으켰는데, 그녀는 비틀거리며 의자로 가더니 그만 정신을 잃고 말았다."]], [["‘She has fainted, sir,’ said Nicholas, darting eagerly forward.", "\"기절하셨어요, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 다급히 앞으로 뛰쳐나오며 말했다."]], [["‘Poor dear, poor dear!’ cried brother Charles ‘Where is my brother Ned?", "\"가엾어라, 가엾어라!\" 찰스 형제가 외쳤다."], ["Ned, my dear brother, come here pray.’", "\"내 동생 네드는 어디 있나? 네드, 동생, 어서 이리 좀 와 주게.\""]], [["‘Brother Charles, my dear fellow,’ replied his brother, hurrying into the room, ‘what is the--ah!", "\"찰스 형님, 무슨 일이세요.\" 동생이 방으로 급히 들어오며 대답했다."], ["what--’", "\"무슨 — 아! 무슨—\""]], [["‘Hush!", "\"쉿! 쉿!"], ["hush!--not a word for your life, brother Ned,’ returned the other.", "— 무슨 일이 있어도 한마디도 하지 말게, 네드.\" 형이 받아 말했다."], ["‘Ring for the housekeeper, my dear brother--call Tim Linkinwater!", "\"가정부를 부르는 종을 울려 주게, 동생 — 팀 링킨워터를 부르고!"], ["Here, Tim Linkinwater, sir--Mr. Nickleby, my dear sir, leave the room, I beg and beseech of you.’", "여기, 팀 링킨워터 씨 — 니클비 군, 미안하네만 방에서 좀 나가 주게, 부탁하고 또 비네.\""]], [["‘I think she is better now,’ said Nicholas, who had been watching the patient so eagerly, that he had not heard the request.", "\"이제 좀 나아지신 것 같아요.\" 니콜라스는 환자를 어찌나 애타게 지켜보고 있었던지, 그 부탁을 듣지도 못한 채 말했다."]], [["‘Poor bird!’ cried brother Charles, gently taking her hand in his, and laying her head upon his arm.", "\"가엾은 새 같으니!\" 찰스 형제가 외치며 그녀의 손을 자기 손에 살며시 쥐고, 그녀의 머리를 자기 팔에 받쳐 주었다."], ["‘Brother Ned, my dear fellow, you will be surprised, I know, to witness this, in business hours; but--’ here he was again reminded of the presence of Nicholas, and shaking him by the hand, earnestly requested him to leave the room, and to send Tim Linkinwater without an instant’s delay.", "\"네드, 동생, 업무 시간에 이런 일이 벌어진 걸 보고 자네가 놀랄 거 잘 아네만 — \" 여기서 그는 다시 니콜라스가 있다는 걸 떠올리고는, 그의 손을 흔들며 방에서 나가 달라고, 그리고 한순간도 지체 말고 팀 링킨워터를 보내 달라고 간곡히 청했다."]], [["Nicholas immediately withdrew and, on his way to the counting-house, met both the old housekeeper and Tim Linkinwater, jostling each other in the passage, and hurrying to the scene of action with extraordinary speed.", "니콜라스는 곧바로 물러났다. 그리고 사무실로 가는 길에 늙은 가정부와 팀 링킨워터를 둘 다 마주쳤는데, 둘은 복도에서 서로 밀치며 엄청난 속도로 사건 현장으로 달려가고 있었다."], ["Without waiting to hear his message, Tim Linkinwater darted into the room, and presently afterwards Nicholas heard the door shut and locked on the inside.", "팀 링킨워터는 그의 전갈을 들을 새도 없이 방으로 휙 뛰어들었고, 곧이어 니콜라스는 문이 닫히고 안에서 잠기는 소리를 들었다."]], [["He had abundance of time to ruminate on this discovery, for Tim Linkinwater was absent during the greater part of an hour, during the whole of which time Nicholas thought of nothing but the young lady, and her exceeding beauty, and what could possibly have brought her there, and why they made such a mystery of it.", "이 발견을 곱씹어 볼 시간은 차고 넘쳤다. 팀 링킨워터가 거의 한 시간이나 자리를 비웠으니 말이다."], ["The more he thought of all this, the more it perplexed him, and the more anxious he became to know who and what she was.", "그 시간 내내 니콜라스는 오로지 그 아가씨 생각, 그녀의 빼어난 아름다움, 대체 무엇 때문에 그녀가 거기 온 것인지, 또 왜 그 일을 그렇게 쉬쉬하는지만 생각했다. 이 모든 걸 생각하면 할수록 더 어리둥절해졌고, 그녀가 누구이며 어떤 사람인지 알고 싶어 더욱 애가 탔다."], ["‘I should have known her among ten thousand,’ thought Nicholas.", "'만 명 가운데 있어도 그녀를 알아봤을 거야.' 니콜라스는 생각했다."], ["And with that he walked up and down the room, and recalling her face and figure (of which he had a peculiarly vivid remembrance), discarded all other subjects of reflection and dwelt upon that alone.", "그러고는 방 안을 왔다 갔다 하며, 그녀의 얼굴과 자태를(이건 유난히 또렷하게 기억하고 있었다) 떠올렸다. 다른 생각거리는 죄다 내던지고 오직 그 생각에만 매달렸다."]], [["At length Tim Linkinwater came back--provokingly cool, and with papers in his hand, and a pen in his mouth, as if nothing had happened.", "마침내 팀 링킨워터가 돌아왔는데 — 얄미울 만큼 태연했다. 손에는 서류를 들고 입에는 펜을 문 채, 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이."]], [["‘Is she quite recovered?’ said Nicholas, impetuously.", "\"그분 완전히 회복되셨어요?\" 니콜라스가 다급하게 물었다."]], [["‘Who?’ returned Tim Linkinwater.", "\"누가?\" 팀 링킨워터가 되물었다."]], [["‘Who!’ repeated Nicholas.", "\"누구라뇨!\" 니콜라스가 되받았다."], ["‘The young lady.’", "\"그 아가씨요.\""]], [["‘What do you make, Mr.", "\"니클비 군, 자네가 계산해 보게.\""], ["Nickleby,’ said Tim, taking his pen out of his mouth, ‘what do you make of four hundred and twenty-seven times three thousand two hundred and thirty-eight?’", "팀이 입에서 펜을 빼며 말했다. \"사백이십칠 곱하기 삼천이백삼십팔이 얼만지 계산해 보겠나?\""]], [["‘Nay,’ returned Nicholas, ‘what do you make of my question first?", "\"아니,\" 니콜라스가 받아 말했다."], ["I asked you--’", "\"제 질문에 먼저 답해 주세요. 제가 여쭌 건—\""]], [["‘About the young lady,’ said Tim Linkinwater, putting on his spectacles.", "\"그 아가씨 얘기지.\" 팀 링킨워터가 안경을 쓰며 말했다."], ["‘To be sure.", "\"그렇고말고."], ["Yes.", "그래."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["she’s very well.’", "그분은 아주 잘 계시네.\""]], [["‘Very well, is she?’ returned Nicholas.", "\"아주 잘 계신다고요?\" 니콜라스가 되받았다."]], [["‘Very well,’ replied Mr.", "\"아주 잘.\""], ["Linkinwater, gravely.", "링킨워터 씨가 진지하게 대답했다."]], [["‘Will she be able to go home today?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"오늘 집에 돌아가실 수 있을까요?\" 니콜라스가 물었다."]], [["‘She’s gone,’ said Tim.", "\"가셨네.\" 팀이 말했다."]], [["‘Gone!’\n‘Yes.’", "\"가셨다고요!\""]], [["‘I hope she has not far to go?’ said Nicholas, looking earnestly at the other.", "\"그래.\" \"먼 데까지 가시는 건 아니면 좋겠는데요?\" 니콜라스가 상대를 간절히 바라보며 말했다."]], [["‘Ay,’ replied the immovable Tim, ‘I hope she hasn’t.’", "\"그래.\" 꿈쩍도 않는 팀이 대답했다. \"나도 멀지 않으면 좋겠네.\""]], [["Nicholas hazarded one or two further remarks, but it was evident that Tim Linkinwater had his own reasons for evading the subject, and that he was determined to afford no further information respecting the fair unknown, who had awakened so much curiosity in the breast of his young friend. Nothing daunted by this repulse, Nicholas returned to the charge next day, emboldened by the circumstance of Mr. Linkinwater being in a very talkative and communicative mood; but, directly he resumed the theme, Tim relapsed into a state of most provoking taciturnity, and from answering in monosyllables, came to returning no answers at all, save such as were to be inferred from several grave nods and shrugs, which only served to whet that appetite for intelligence in Nicholas, which had already attained a most unreasonable height.", "니콜라스는 한두 마디 더 슬쩍 던져 보았다. 하지만 팀 링킨워터에게는 그 화제를 피하는 나름의 까닭이 있다는 게, 그리고 그가 자기 젊은 친구의 가슴에 그토록 큰 호기심을 불러일으킨 그 아리따운 낯선 여인에 대해서는 더는 한마디도 알려 주지 않기로 단단히 작정했다는 게 빤히 보였다. 이 퇴짜에도 전혀 굴하지 않고, 니콜라스는 이튿날 다시 공세를 폈다. 링킨워터 씨가 아주 수다스럽고 말이 많은 기분이라는 점에 용기를 얻어서였다. 하지만 그가 그 화제를 다시 꺼내자마자, 팀은 더없이 얄미운 침묵 속으로 도로 빠져들었다. 한 마디 단어로만 답하다가, 나중엔 아예 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그저 몇 번 진지하게 고개를 끄덕이고 어깨를 으쓱하는 데서 짐작할 만한 것밖에는. 그건 이미 터무니없을 만큼 부풀어 오른 니콜라스의 그 소식에 대한 갈망을 더 돋울 뿐이었다."]], [["Foiled in these attempts, he was fain to content himself with watching for the young lady’s next visit, but here again he was disappointed.", "이런 시도에서 번번이 좌절하자, 그는 아가씨가 다음에 찾아오기를 기다리는 걸로 만족할 수밖에 없었다. 하지만 여기서도 그는 실망했다."], ["Day after day passed, and she did not return.", "하루하루가 지나도 그녀는 다시 오지 않았다."], ["He looked eagerly at the superscription of all the notes and letters, but there was not one among them which he could fancy to be in her handwriting.", "그는 모든 쪽지와 편지의 겉봉을 애타게 들여다봤지만, 그중에 그녀의 필체이지 싶은 건 하나도 없었다."], ["On two or three occasions he was employed on business which took him to a distance, and had formerly been transacted by Tim Linkinwater.", "두세 번쯤 그는 멀리까지 다녀와야 하는 일을 맡았는데, 전에는 팀 링킨워터가 처리하던 일이었다."], ["Nicholas could not help suspecting that, for some reason or other, he was sent out of the way on purpose, and that the young lady was there in his absence.", "니콜라스는 무슨 까닭에선지 자기가 일부러 자리를 비우도록 보내진 거고, 그가 없는 동안 아가씨가 거기 왔던 게 아닐까 하는 의심을 떨칠 수 없었다."], ["Nothing transpired, however, to confirm this suspicion, and Tim could not be entrapped into any confession or admission tending to support it in the smallest degree.", "하지만 이 의심을 뒷받침할 만한 일은 아무것도 드러나지 않았고, 팀에게서는 그걸 조금이라도 뒷받침할 만한 어떤 자백이나 시인도 끌어낼 수 없었다."]], [["Mystery and disappointment are not absolutely indispensable to the growth of love, but they are, very often, its powerful auxiliaries.", "비밀과 실망이 사랑이 자라는 데 꼭 없어서는 안 될 것은 아니지만, 아주 흔히 사랑의 강력한 조력자가 되어 준다."], ["‘Out of sight, out of mind,’ is well enough as a proverb applicable to cases of friendship, though absence is not always necessary to hollowness of heart, even between friends, and truth and honesty, like precious stones, are perhaps most easily imitated at a distance, when the counterfeits often pass for real.", "'눈에서 멀어지면 마음에서도 멀어진다'는 말은 우정의 경우에 들어맞는 속담으로는 그럭저럭 괜찮다. 친구 사이라도 마음이 비는 데 꼭 떨어져 있어야 하는 건 아니고, 진실과 정직은 보석과 마찬가지로 멀리 떨어진 데서 가장 흉내 내기 쉬워서, 그 가짜가 종종 진짜로 통하긴 하지만 말이다."], ["Love, however, is very materially assisted by a warm and active imagination: which has a long memory, and will thrive, for a considerable time, on very slight and sparing food.", "그러나 사랑은 따뜻하고 활발한 상상력의 도움을 단단히 받는다. 그 상상력은 기억이 길고, 아주 보잘것없는 양식으로도 꽤 오랫동안 무성하게 자란다."], ["Thus it is, that it often attains its most luxuriant growth in separation and under circumstances of the utmost difficulty; and thus it was, that Nicholas, thinking of nothing but the unknown young lady, from day to day and from hour to hour, began, at last, to think that he was very desperately in love with her, and that never was such an ill-used and persecuted lover as he.", "그래서 사랑은 흔히 떨어져 있을 때, 그리고 더없이 어려운 처지에서 가장 무성하게 자라난다. 그리하여 니콜라스도, 날이면 날마다 시간마다 그 낯선 아가씨 생각밖에 안 하다가, 마침내 자기가 그녀에게 형편없이 깊이 빠져 있으며, 자기만큼 학대받고 박해받은 연인은 세상에 없다고까지 여기게 되었다."]], [["Still, though he loved and languished after the most orthodox models, and was only deterred from making a confidante of Kate by the slight considerations of having never, in all his life, spoken to the object of his passion, and having never set eyes upon her, except on two occasions, on both of which she had come and gone like a flash of lightning--or, as Nicholas himself said, in the numerous conversations he held with himself, like a vision of youth and beauty much too bright to last--his ardour and devotion remained without its reward. The young lady appeared no more; so there was a great deal of love wasted (enough indeed to have set up half-a-dozen young gentlemen, as times go, with the utmost decency), and nobody was a bit the wiser for it; not even Nicholas himself, who, on the contrary, became more dull, sentimental, and lackadaisical, every day.", "그래도 그가 가장 정통적인 본보기대로 사랑하며 애태우면서, 케이트에게 그 사연을 털어놓는 것만큼은 그저 사소한 사정 때문에 망설이고 있었다 — 즉 평생 그 연모의 대상에게 말 한마디 건넨 적이 없고, 그녀를 본 것도 단 두 번뿐인데, 그 두 번 다 그녀가 번개처럼 — 아니 니콜라스 자신이 혼잣말로 수없이 되뇐 대로 하자면, 너무 눈부셔서 오래갈 수 없는 젊음과 아름다움의 환영처럼 — 왔다가 사라져 버렸다는 사정 말이다 — 어쨌든 그의 열정과 헌신은 보답을 받지 못한 채였다. 아가씨는 더 이상 나타나지 않았다. 그래서 엄청난 양의 사랑이 허비되었고(요즘 세상에 젊은 신사 대여섯 명쯤은 더없이 점잖게 차려 줄 만큼 충분한 양이었다), 그 덕에 한 톨이라도 더 똑똑해진 사람은 아무도 없었다. 니콜라스 자신조차 그랬다. 오히려 그는 날이 갈수록 더 멍하고 감상적이고 맥 빠진 사람이 되어 갔다."]], [["While matters were in this state, the failure of a correspondent of the brothers Cheeryble, in Germany, imposed upon Tim Linkinwater and Nicholas the necessity of going through some very long and complicated accounts, extending over a considerable space of time.", "일이 이런 지경일 때, 독일에 있는 치어리블 형제의 한 거래처가 파산하는 바람에, 팀 링킨워터와 니콜라스는 아주 길고 복잡한, 꽤 긴 기간에 걸친 장부를 죄다 훑어봐야 하는 처지가 됐다. 그걸 더 빨리 끝내려고, 팀 링킨워터는 한 일주일쯤 밤 열 시까지 사무실에 남아 일하자고 제안했다."], ["To get through them with the greater dispatch, Tim Linkinwater proposed that they should remain at the counting-house, for a week or so, until ten o’clock at night; to this, as nothing damped the zeal of Nicholas in the service of his kind patrons--not even romance, which has seldom business habits--he cheerfully assented.", "친절한 후원자들을 위해 일하는 니콜라스의 열의는 그 무엇으로도 식힐 수 없었으므로 — 좀처럼 부지런한 습성과는 거리가 먼 연애 감정조차도 — 그는 흔쾌히 그러자고 했다. 이렇게 늦게까지 일하게 된 바로 그 첫날 밤, 정각 아홉 시에 누군가 찾아왔다."], ["On the very first night of these later hours, at nine exactly, there came: not the young lady herself, but her servant, who, being closeted with brother Charles for some time, went away, and returned next night at the same hour, and on the next, and on the next again.", "아가씨 본인이 아니라 그녀의 하녀였다. 하녀는 한동안 찰스 형제와 단둘이 방에 들어가 있다가 돌아갔고, 이튿날 밤에도 같은 시각에 다시 왔고, 그다음 날도, 또 그다음 날도 왔다."]], [["These repeated visits inflamed the curiosity of Nicholas to the very highest pitch.", "이렇게 거듭되는 방문에 니콜라스의 호기심은 그야말로 극에 달했다. 견딜 수 없을 만큼 감질나고 들떴지만, 제 할 일을 게을리하지 않고는 그 비밀을 캐낼 도리가 없었다."], ["Tantalised and excited, beyond all bearing, and unable to fathom the mystery without neglecting his duty, he confided the whole secret to Newman Noggs, imploring him to be on the watch next night; to follow the girl home; to set on foot such inquiries relative to the name, condition, and history of her mistress, as he could, without exciting suspicion; and to report the result to him with the least possible delay.", "그래서 그는 그 비밀을 죄다 뉴먼 노그스에게 털어놓으며 사정했다. 다음 날 밤 망을 봐 달라고, 그 하녀를 따라 집까지 가 달라고, 의심을 사지 않는 선에서 그 여주인의 이름과 처지와 내력에 관해 알아볼 수 있는 데까지 알아봐 달라고, 그리고 그 결과를 되도록 빨리 자기에게 알려 달라고."]], [["Beyond all measure proud of this commission, Newman Noggs took up his post, in the square, on the following evening, a full hour before the needful time, and planting himself behind the pump and pulling his hat over his eyes, began his watch with an elaborate appearance of mystery, admirably calculated to excite the suspicion of all beholders. Indeed, divers servant girls who came to draw water, and sundry little boys who stopped to drink at the ladle, were almost scared out of their senses, by the apparition of Newman Noggs looking stealthily round the pump, with nothing of him visible but his face, and that wearing the expression of a meditative Ogre.", "이 임무를 더없이 자랑스러워하며, 뉴먼 노그스는 이튿날 저녁 약속된 시각보다 한 시간이나 일찍 광장에 자리를 잡았다. 펌프 뒤에 몸을 숨기고 모자를 눈까지 푹 눌러쓴 채, 거창하게 수상쩍어 보이는 자세로 망을 보기 시작했다. 누가 봐도 의심을 살 만한 폼이었다. 실제로 물을 길으러 온 여러 하녀들이며, 국자로 물을 마시려고 멈춰 선 여러 꼬마들이, 펌프 둘레를 슬그머니 살피는 뉴먼 노그스의 모습에 거의 혼비백산할 지경이었다. 얼굴 말고는 아무것도 안 보이는데, 그 얼굴이 또 생각에 잠긴 도깨비 같은 표정을 짓고 있었으니 말이다."]], [["Punctual to her time, the messenger came again, and, after an interview of rather longer duration than usual, departed.", "시간을 어김없이 지켜, 그 심부름꾼이 다시 왔다. 그리고 여느 때보다 좀 더 긴 면담을 마친 뒤 떠났다."], ["Newman had made two appointments with Nicholas: one for the next evening, conditional on his success: and one the next night following, which was to be kept under all circumstances.", "뉴먼은 니콜라스와 두 번의 약속을 잡아 두었다. 하나는 일이 잘 풀린다는 조건으로 다음 날 저녁, 또 하나는 무슨 일이 있어도 지키기로 한 그다음 날 밤이었다."], ["The first night he was not at the place of meeting (a certain tavern about half-way between the city and Golden Square), but on the second night he was there before Nicholas, and received him with open arms.", "첫째 날 밤엔 그가 약속 장소(시내와 골든 스퀘어 중간쯤에 있는 어느 선술집)에 나타나지 않았다. 하지만 둘째 날 밤엔 니콜라스보다 먼저 와 있다가, 두 팔을 벌려 그를 맞았다."]], [["‘It’s all right,’ whispered Newman.", "\"다 잘됐어.\" 뉴먼이 속삭였다."], ["‘Sit down.", "\"앉아."], ["Sit down, there’s a dear young man, and let me tell you all about it.’", "앉으라고, 착한 젊은이. 내가 다 얘기해 줄 테니까.\""]], [["Nicholas needed no second invitation, and eagerly inquired what was the news.", "니콜라스는 두 번 권할 것도 없이, 무슨 소식이냐고 다급히 물었다."]], [["‘There’s a great deal of news,’ said Newman, in a flutter of exultation.", "\"소식이 한가득이야.\" 뉴먼이 의기양양하게 들떠 말했다."], ["‘It’s all right.", "\"다 잘됐어."], ["Don’t be anxious.", "조바심 내지 마."], ["I don’t know where to begin. Never mind that.", "어디서부터 얘기해야 할지 모르겠군."], ["Keep up your spirits.", "그건 됐고. 기운 내."], ["It’s all right.’", "다 잘됐다니까.\""]], [["‘Well?’ said Nicholas eagerly.", "\"그래서요?\" 니콜라스가 다급히 말했다."], ["‘Yes?’", "\"네?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Newman.", "\"그래.\" 뉴먼이 대답했다."], ["‘That’s it.’", "\"바로 그거지.\""]], [["‘What’s it?’ said Nicholas.", "\"그게 뭔데요?\" 니콜라스가 말했다."], ["‘The name--the name, my dear fellow!’", "\"이름이요 — 이름 말이에요, 형!\""]], [["‘The name’s Bobster,’ replied Newman.", "\"이름은 봅스터야.\" 뉴먼이 대답했다."]], [["‘Bobster!’ repeated Nicholas, indignantly.", "\"봅스터요!\" 니콜라스가 발끈하며 되뇌었다."]], [["‘That’s the name,’ said Newman.", "\"그게 이름이야.\" 뉴먼이 말했다."], ["‘I remember it by lobster.’", "\"랍스터 가지고 외워 뒀지.\""]], [["‘Bobster!’ repeated Nicholas, more emphatically than before.", "\"봅스터라고요!\" 니콜라스가 아까보다 더 힘주어 되뇌었다."], ["‘That must be the servant’s name.’", "\"그건 하녀 이름이겠죠.\""]], [["‘No, it an’t,’ said Newman, shaking his head with great positiveness.", "\"아니, 그렇지 않아.\" 뉴먼이 아주 단호하게 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["‘Miss Cecilia Bobster.’", "\"세실리아 봅스터 아가씨.\""]], [["‘Cecilia, eh?’ returned Nicholas, muttering the two names together over and over again in every variety of tone, to try the effect.", "\"세실리아라, 응?\" 니콜라스가 받아 말하며, 그 두 이름을 갖가지 어조로 한데 묶어 거듭 중얼거리며 어떤 느낌인지 시험해 봤다."], ["‘Well, Cecilia is a pretty name.’", "\"음, 세실리아는 예쁜 이름이네요.\""]], [["‘Very.", "\"아주 예쁘지."], ["And a pretty creature too,’ said Newman.", "그리고 그 아가씨 본인도 예쁘고.\" 뉴먼이 말했다."]], [["‘Who?’ said Nicholas.", "\"누가요?\" 니콜라스가 말했다."]], [["‘Miss Bobster.’", "\"봅스터 아가씨.\""]], [["‘Why, where have you seen her?’ demanded Nicholas.", "\"아니, 형이 그 아가씨를 어디서 봤어요?\" 니콜라스가 따져 물었다."]], [["‘Never mind, my dear boy,’ retorted Noggs, clapping him on the shoulder.", "\"그건 신경 쓰지 마, 젊은이.\" 노그스가 그의 어깨를 툭 치며 받아 말했다."], ["‘I HAVE seen her.", "\"내가 그 아가씨를 봤어."], ["You shall see her.", "너도 보게 될 거야."], ["I’ve managed it all.’", "내가 다 손을 써 놨거든.\""]], [["‘My dear Newman,’ cried Nicholas, grasping his hand, ‘are you serious?’", "\"뉴먼 형,\" 니콜라스가 그의 손을 꽉 잡으며 외쳤다. \"진심이에요?\""]], [["‘I am,’ replied Newman.", "\"진심이지.\" 뉴먼이 대답했다."], ["‘I mean it all.", "\"다 진심이야."], ["Every word.", "한마디 한마디 다."], ["You shall see her tomorrow night.", "너 내일 밤에 그 아가씨를 보게 될 거야."], ["She consents to hear you speak for yourself.", "그분이 네가 직접 마음을 고백하는 걸 들어주기로 했어."], ["I persuaded her.", "내가 설득했지."], ["She is all affability, goodness, sweetness, and beauty.’", "더없이 상냥하고, 착하고, 다정하고, 아름다운 분이야.\""]], [["‘I know she is; I know she must be, Newman!’ said Nicholas, wringing his hand.", "\"그럴 줄 알았어요. 분명 그러실 거예요, 형!\" 니콜라스가 그의 손을 쥐어짜며 말했다."]], [["‘You are right,’ returned Newman.", "\"네 말이 맞아.\" 뉴먼이 받아 말했다."]], [["‘Where does she live?’ cried Nicholas.", "\"그 아가씨 어디 살아요?\" 니콜라스가 외쳤다."], ["‘What have you learnt of her history?", "\"그분 내력은 뭘 알아냈어요?"], ["Has she a father--mother--any brothers--sisters?", "아버지가 있어요 — 어머니는요 — 형제는 — 자매는요?"], ["What did she say?", "그 아가씬 뭐라고 했어요?"], ["How came you to see her?", "어쩌다 그분을 보게 된 거예요?"], ["Was she not very much surprised?", "무척 놀라지 않았어요?"], ["Did you say how passionately I have longed to speak to her?", "내가 그분과 얼마나 간절히 말하고 싶어 했는지 얘기해 줬어요?"], ["Did you tell her where I had seen her?", "내가 그분을 어디서 봤는지 말해 줬어요?"], ["Did you tell her how, and when, and where, and how long, and how often, I have thought of that sweet face which came upon me in my bitterest distress like a glimpse of some better world--did you, Newman--did you?’", "어떻게, 언제, 어디서, 얼마나 오래, 얼마나 자주 내가 그 고운 얼굴을 떠올렸는지 말해 줬어요? 더없이 쓰라리던 시절에 더 나은 세상을 잠깐 엿본 것처럼 내게 다가온 그 얼굴을 — 말해 줬어요, 형 — 그랬어요?\""]], [["Poor Noggs literally gasped for breath as this flood of questions rushed upon him, and moved spasmodically in his chair at every fresh inquiry, staring at Nicholas meanwhile with a most ludicrous expression of perplexity.", "가엾은 노그스는 이 질문의 홍수가 한꺼번에 들이닥치자 말 그대로 숨을 헐떡였다. 새 질문이 나올 때마다 의자에서 발작하듯 들썩이며, 그러는 내내 더없이 우스꽝스럽게 어리둥절한 표정으로 니콜라스를 빤히 쳐다봤다."]], [["‘No,’ said Newman, ‘I didn’t tell her that.’", "\"아니,\" 뉴먼이 말했다. \"그건 말 안 했어.\""]], [["‘Didn’t tell her which?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"뭘 말 안 했다는 거예요?\" 니콜라스가 물었다."]], [["‘About the glimpse of the better world,’ said Newman.", "\"더 나은 세상을 엿봤다는 거.\" 뉴먼이 말했다."], ["‘I didn’t tell her who you were, either, or where you’d seen her.", "\"네가 누군지도 말 안 했고, 네가 그 아가씨를 어디서 봤는지도 말 안 했어."], ["I said you loved her to distraction.’", "그냥 네가 그분을 정신 못 차릴 만큼 사랑한다고 했지.\""]], [["‘That’s true, Newman,’ replied Nicholas, with his characteristic vehemence.", "\"그건 사실이에요, 형.\" 니콜라스가 특유의 격정으로 대답했다."], ["‘Heaven knows I do!’", "\"하늘이 알아요, 정말 그래요!\""]], [["‘I said too, that you had admired her for a long time in secret,’ said Newman.", "\"네가 오랫동안 남몰래 그분을 흠모해 왔다고도 했고.\" 뉴먼이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, yes.", "\"네, 네."], ["What did she say to that?’ asked Nicholas.", "거기에 그분이 뭐라고 했어요?\" 니콜라스가 물었다."]], [["‘Blushed,’ said Newman.", "\"얼굴을 붉히던데.\" 뉴먼이 말했다."]], [["‘To be sure. Of course she would,’ said Nicholas approvingly. Newman then went on to say, that the young lady was an only child, that her mother was dead, that she resided with her father, and that she had been induced to allow her lover a secret interview, at the intercession of her servant, who had great influence with her. He further related how it required much moving and great eloquence to bring the young lady to this pass; how it was expressly understood that she merely afforded Nicholas an opportunity of declaring his passion; and how she by no means pledged herself to be favourably impressed with his attentions. The mystery of her visits to the brothers Cheeryble remained wholly unexplained, for Newman had not alluded to them, either in his preliminary conversations with the servant or his subsequent interview with the mistress, merely remarking that he had been instructed to watch the girl home and plead his young friend’s cause, and not saying how far he had followed her, or from what point. But Newman hinted that from what had fallen from the confidante, he had been led to suspect that the young lady led a very miserable and unhappy life, under the strict control of her only parent, who was of a violent and brutal temper; a circumstance which he thought might in some degree account, both for her having sought the protection and friendship of the brothers, and her suffering herself to be prevailed upon to grant the promised interview. The last he held to be a very logical deduction from the premises, inasmuch as it was but natural to suppose that a young lady, whose present condition was so unenviable, would be more than commonly desirous to change it.", "\"그렇겠죠. 당연히 그러셨을 거예요.\" 니콜라스가 흐뭇하게 말했다. 그러자 뉴먼은 이어서 말했다. 그 아가씨는 외동딸이고, 어머니는 돌아가셨으며, 아버지와 함께 살고 있는데, 자기한테 큰 영향을 미치는 하녀가 사정사정한 덕에 연인과 남몰래 만나는 데 응하게 됐다고. 그는 또 말했다. 아가씨를 이 지경까지 데려오는 데 어찌나 어르고 달래고 입에 침이 마르게 떠들어야 했는지. 그리고 그건 어디까지나 니콜라스가 자기 마음을 고백할 기회를 그저 마련해 주는 것일 뿐, 그분이 결코 그의 구애에 좋은 인상을 받기로 약속한 건 아니라는 점이 분명히 못 박혔다는 것도. 그녀가 치어리블 형제를 찾아간 까닭이라는 그 수수께끼는 전혀 풀리지 않은 채 남았다. 뉴먼이 그 일은 입에 올리지 않았기 때문이다. 하녀와 먼저 나눈 이야기에서도, 그 뒤 여주인과의 면담에서도. 그는 그저 자기가 그 처녀를 따라 집까지 가서 젊은 친구의 사정을 변호하라는 지시를 받았다고만 말했지, 그녀를 어디서부터 어디까지 뒤따랐는지는 말하지 않았다. 하지만 뉴먼은 넌지시 비쳤다. 그 측근에게서 흘러나온 말로 미루어, 그 아가씨가 아주 비참하고 불행한 삶을 살고 있는 듯하다는 것이다. 난폭하고 잔인한 성미를 지닌 하나뿐인 부모의 엄한 통제 아래서 말이다. 그는 이 사정이, 그녀가 형제의 보호와 우정을 구한 까닭과, 약속된 면담에 응하도록 설득당한 까닭 둘 다를 어느 정도 설명해 주지 않을까 생각했다. 그는 이 마지막 점을 전제에서 끌어낸 아주 논리적인 추론이라고 여겼다. 지금 처지가 그토록 부러울 게 없는 아가씨라면, 그걸 바꾸고 싶은 마음이 남보다 더 간절할 거라고 보는 게 자연스럽기 때문이었다."]], [["It appeared, on further questioning--for it was only by a very long and arduous process that all this could be got out of Newman Noggs--that Newman, in explanation of his shabby appearance, had represented himself as being, for certain wise and indispensable purposes connected with that intrigue, in disguise; and, being questioned how he had come to exceed his commission so far as to procure an interview, he responded, that the lady appearing willing to grant it, he considered himself bound, both in duty and gallantry, to avail himself of such a golden means of enabling Nicholas to prosecute his addresses. After these and all possible questions had been asked and answered twenty times over, they parted, undertaking to meet on the following night at half-past ten, for the purpose of fulfilling the appointment; which was for eleven o’clock.", "더 캐물어 보니 — 이 모든 걸 뉴먼 노그스한테서 끄집어내는 데는 아주 길고 힘겨운 과정이 필요했다 — 이런 게 드러났다. 뉴먼은 자기 초라한 행색을 둘러대느라, 이 은밀한 계략과 관련된 어떤 현명하고 꼭 필요한 목적 때문에 변장을 하고 있는 거라고 둘러댔다는 것이다. 그리고 어쩌다가 면담까지 주선하는, 그렇게 지나치게 임무를 넘어선 일을 했느냐고 묻자, 그는 이렇게 대답했다. 아가씨가 면담을 허락할 마음이 있어 보이기에, 도리로 보나 사내다운 의협심으로 보나, 니콜라스가 구애를 밀고 나갈 수 있게 해 줄 그 황금 같은 수단을 붙잡아야 마땅하다고 여겼다는 것이다. 이런 질문과 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 질문을 스무 번씩 묻고 답한 뒤에, 둘은 헤어졌다. 약속을 지키려고 이튿날 밤 열 시 반에 만나기로 하고서. 약속 시각은 열한 시였다."]], [["‘Things come about very strangely!’ thought Nicholas, as he walked home.", "'일이란 게 참 묘하게 풀리는군!' 니콜라스는 집으로 걸어가며 생각했다."], ["‘I never contemplated anything of this kind; never dreamt of the possibility of it.", "'이런 건 꿈에도 그려 본 적이 없는데. 이런 일이 가능하리라곤 상상도 못 했어."], ["To know something of the life of one in whom I felt such interest; to see her in the street, to pass the house in which she lived, to meet her sometimes in her walks, to hope that a day might come when I might be in a condition to tell her of my love, this was the utmost extent of my thoughts.", "그렇게 마음이 끌리던 사람의 삶을 조금이라도 알게 되는 것, 그분을 거리에서 보는 것, 그분이 사는 집 앞을 지나가는 것, 어쩌다 산책길에서 그분과 마주치는 것, 언젠가는 내 사랑을 그분께 말할 수 있는 처지가 될 날이 오기를 바라는 것 — 내 생각은 기껏해야 거기까지였는데."], ["Now, however--but I should be a fool, indeed, to repine at my own good fortune!’", "그런데 이제는 — 아니, 내가 내 행운을 두고 불평한다면 그거야말로 바보 같은 짓이지!'"]], [["Still, Nicholas was dissatisfied; and there was more in the dissatisfaction than mere revulsion of feeling. He was angry with the young lady for being so easily won, ‘because,’ reasoned Nicholas, ‘it is not as if she knew it was I, but it might have been anybody,’--which was certainly not pleasant. The next moment, he was angry with himself for entertaining such thoughts, arguing that nothing but goodness could dwell in such a temple, and that the behaviour of the brothers sufficiently showed the estimation in which they held her. ‘The fact is, she’s a mystery altogether,’ said Nicholas. This was not more satisfactory than his previous course of reflection, and only drove him out upon a new sea of speculation and conjecture, where he tossed and tumbled, in great discomfort of mind, until the clock struck ten, and the hour of meeting drew nigh.", "그래도 니콜라스는 마음이 흡족하지 않았다. 그리고 그 불만 속엔 단순히 감정이 뒤바뀐 것 이상의 무언가가 있었다. 그는 그 아가씨가 그렇게 쉽게 넘어온 게 화가 났다. '왜냐하면,' 니콜라스는 따져 봤다. '그분이 그게 나인 줄 알고 그런 게 아니라, 아무나였어도 마찬가지였을 거 아냐.' 분명 유쾌한 노릇은 아니었다. 그러나 다음 순간, 그는 그런 생각을 품은 자신에게 화가 났다. 그런 신전 같은 사람 안엔 선함밖에 깃들 수 없으며, 형제가 그녀를 대하는 태도만 봐도 그분을 얼마나 높이 여기는지 충분히 알 수 있다고 따지면서. '사실 그분은 통째로 수수께끼야.' 니콜라스는 말했다. 이것도 앞서 한 생각보다 더 흡족하진 않았고, 그저 그를 새로운 억측과 추측의 바다로 떠밀어 냈을 뿐이다. 그는 거기서 마음이 몹시 불편한 채로 일렁이고 뒤척였다. 시계가 열 시를 칠 때까지, 그리고 약속 시각이 다가올 때까지."]], [["Nicholas had dressed himself with great care, and even Newman Noggs had trimmed himself up a little; his coat presenting the phenomenon of two consecutive buttons, and the supplementary pins being inserted at tolerably regular intervals. He wore his hat, too, in the newest taste, with a pocket-handkerchief in the crown, and a twisted end of it straggling out behind after the fashion of a pigtail, though he could scarcely lay claim to the ingenuity of inventing this latter decoration, inasmuch as he was utterly unconscious of it: being in a nervous and excited condition which rendered him quite insensible to everything but the great object of the expedition.", "니콜라스는 아주 공들여 차려입었고, 뉴먼 노그스조차 자기 매무새를 조금 다듬었다. 그의 외투에는 단추 두 개가 잇달아 붙어 있는 진풍경이 펼쳐졌고, 모자라는 자리를 메운 핀들은 꽤 일정한 간격으로 꽂혀 있었다. 모자도 최신 멋에 맞춰 썼는데, 모자 꼭대기에 손수건을 끼우고 그 끄트머리를 꼬아 댕기머리처럼 뒤로 삐죽 내놓은 모양새였다. 하지만 이 마지막 장식을 고안해 낸 재간만큼은 그의 공으로 돌리기 어려웠다. 정작 본인은 그게 거기 있는 줄도 전혀 몰랐으니까. 그는 신경이 곤두서고 들뜬 상태라, 이번 원정의 그 큰 목표 말고는 모든 것에 도통 무감각했던 것이다."]], [["They traversed the streets in profound silence; and after walking at a round pace for some distance, arrived in one, of a gloomy appearance and very little frequented, near the Edgeware Road.", "두 사람은 깊은 침묵 속에 거리를 가로질렀다. 그리고 한참을 빠른 걸음으로 걸은 끝에, 음침한 분위기에 인적도 거의 없는 어느 거리에 다다랐다. 에지웨어 로드 근처였다."]], [["‘Number twelve,’ said Newman.", "\"12번지야.\" 뉴먼이 말했다."]], [["‘Oh!’ replied Nicholas, looking about him.", "\"오!\" 니콜라스가 주위를 둘러보며 대답했다."]], [["‘Good street?’ said Newman.", "\"괜찮은 거리지?\" 뉴먼이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ returned Nicholas.", "\"네.\" 니콜라스가 받아 말했다."], ["‘Rather dull.’", "\"좀 칙칙하긴 하네요.\""]], [["Newman made no answer to this remark, but, halting abruptly, planted Nicholas with his back to some area railings, and gave him to understand that he was to wait there, without moving hand or foot, until it was satisfactorily ascertained that the coast was clear. This done, Noggs limped away with great alacrity; looking over his shoulder every instant, to make quite certain that Nicholas was obeying his directions; and, ascending the steps of a house some half-dozen doors off, was lost to view.", "뉴먼은 이 말엔 대꾸하지 않았다. 그저 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 니콜라스를 어느 지하실 난간에 등을 댄 자세로 세워 놓고는, 주변에 아무도 없다는 게 확실히 확인될 때까지 손도 발도 까딱 말고 거기 기다리라고 일러 줬다. 그러고 나서 노그스는 아주 잰걸음으로 절뚝거리며 떠났다. 시시각각 어깨너머로 돌아보며 니콜라스가 자기 지시를 따르고 있는지 단단히 확인하면서. 그리고 여섯 집쯤 떨어진 어느 집 계단을 올라가더니 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["After a short delay, he reappeared, and limping back again, halted midway, and beckoned Nicholas to follow him.", "잠시 뒤, 그가 다시 나타나 절뚝거리며 돌아오다가, 중간쯤에서 멈춰 서서 니콜라스에게 따라오라고 손짓했다."]], [["‘Well?’ said Nicholas, advancing towards him on tiptoe.", "\"어때요?\" 니콜라스가 발끝으로 살금살금 그에게 다가가며 말했다."]], [["‘All right,’ replied Newman, in high glee.", "\"다 됐어.\" 뉴먼이 신이 나서 대답했다."], ["‘All ready; nobody at home.", "\"준비 끝. 집에 아무도 없어."], ["Couldn’t be better.", "이보다 좋을 순 없지."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!’", "하!\""]], [["With this fortifying assurance, he stole past a street-door, on which Nicholas caught a glimpse of a brass plate, with ‘BOBSTER,’ in very large letters; and, stopping at the area-gate, which was open, signed to his young friend to descend.", "이 든든한 장담과 함께, 그는 어느 현관문 앞을 살그머니 지나쳤다. 니콜라스는 그 문에 '봅스터'라고 아주 큼지막한 글자로 새겨진 놋쇠 명패가 붙어 있는 걸 언뜻 봤다. 그리고 그는 열려 있는 지하실 출입문 앞에서 멈춰, 젊은 친구에게 내려가라고 손짓했다."]], [["‘What the devil!’ cried Nicholas, drawing back.", "\"이게 무슨 짓이에요!\" 니콜라스가 뒷걸음치며 외쳤다."], ["‘Are we to sneak into the kitchen, as if we came after the forks?’", "\"포크라도 훔치러 온 것처럼 부엌으로 슬그머니 기어 들어가야 한단 말이에요?\""]], [["‘Hush!’ replied Newman.", "\"쉿!\" 뉴먼이 대답했다."], ["‘Old Bobster--ferocious Turk.", "\"봅스터 영감 — 사나운 터키 사람이야."], ["He’d kill ‘em all--box the young lady’s ears--he does--often.’", "그자라면 죄다 죽일 사람이야 — 아가씨 따귀를 갈긴다고 — 진짜로 — 자주.\""]], [["‘What!’ cried Nicholas, in high wrath, ‘do you mean to tell me that any man would dare to box the ears of such a--’", "\"뭐라고요!\" 니콜라스가 잔뜩 화가 나서 외쳤다. \"어떤 작자가 감히 그런 분의 따귀를 갈긴단 말이에요, 그런—\""]], [["He had no time to sing the praises of his mistress, just then, for Newman gave him a gentle push which had nearly precipitated him to the bottom of the area steps.", "그러나 그때 그는 자기가 연모하는 여인을 칭송하는 노래를 부를 새가 없었다. 뉴먼이 그를 살짝 떠미는 바람에 하마터면 지하실 계단 바닥까지 곤두박질칠 뻔했으니까."], ["Thinking it best to take the hint in good part, Nicholas descended, without further remonstrance, but with a countenance bespeaking anything rather than the hope and rapture of a passionate lover.", "그 눈치를 순순히 받아들이는 게 상책이다 싶어, 니콜라스는 더는 군말 없이 내려갔다. 하지만 그 얼굴은 열정에 찬 연인의 기대나 황홀과는 거리가 먼, 다른 무언가를 드러내고 있었다."], ["Newman followed--he would have followed head first, but for the timely assistance of Nicholas--and, taking his hand, led him through a stone passage, profoundly dark, into a back-kitchen or cellar, of the blackest and most pitchy obscurity, where they stopped.", "뉴먼이 뒤따랐는데 — 니콜라스가 때맞춰 받쳐 주지 않았더라면 머리부터 곤두박질칠 뻔했다 — 그러고는 니콜라스의 손을 잡고, 칠흑같이 캄캄한 돌 통로를 지나, 더없이 새까맣고 칠흑같이 어두운 뒤편 부엌인지 지하 창고인지로 그를 이끌고 가서 멈춰 섰다."]], [["‘Well!’ said Nicholas, in a discontented whisper, ‘this is not all, I suppose, is it?’", "\"자!\" 니콜라스가 불만스러운 목소리로 속삭였다. \"이게 다는 아니겠죠, 설마?\""]], [["‘No, no,’ rejoined Noggs; ‘they’ll be here directly.", "\"아니, 아니지.\" 노그스가 받아 말했다."], ["It’s all right.’", "\"곧 그분들이 여기 올 거야. 다 잘됐어.\""]], [["‘I am glad to hear it,’ said Nicholas.", "\"그렇다니 다행이네요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다."], ["‘I shouldn’t have thought it, I confess.’", "\"솔직히 그럴 줄은 몰랐거든요.\""]], [["They exchanged no further words, and there Nicholas stood, listening to the loud breathing of Newman Noggs, and imagining that his nose seemed to glow like a red-hot coal, even in the midst of the darkness which enshrouded them.", "둘은 더 이상 말을 나누지 않았다. 니콜라스는 거기 선 채로 뉴먼 노그스의 거친 숨소리에 귀를 기울였다."], ["Suddenly the sound of cautious footsteps attracted his ear, and directly afterwards a female voice inquired if the gentleman was there.", "그리고 그를 에워싼 어둠 속에서도 그의 코가 벌겋게 단 숯덩이처럼 이글거리는 것만 같다고 생각했다. 문득 조심스러운 발소리가 그의 귀를 끌었고, 곧이어 여자 목소리가 신사분이 거기 계시냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Nicholas, turning towards the corner from which the voice proceeded.", "\"네.\" 니콜라스가 그 목소리가 들려온 구석을 향해 돌아서며 대답했다."], ["‘Who is that?’", "\"누구시죠?\""]], [["‘Only me, sir,’ replied the voice.", "\"저예요, 선생님.\" 그 목소리가 대답했다."], ["‘Now if you please, ma’am.’", "\"자, 이제 들어오시죠, 아가씨.\""]], [["A gleam of light shone into the place, and presently the servant girl appeared, bearing a light, and followed by her young mistress, who seemed to be overwhelmed by modesty and confusion.", "그곳에 빛 한 줄기가 비쳐 들었고, 곧 하녀가 불을 들고 나타났다. 그 뒤로는 젊은 여주인이 따라왔는데, 부끄러움과 당혹감에 어쩔 줄 모르는 것 같았다."]], [["At sight of the young lady, Nicholas started and changed colour; his heart beat violently, and he stood rooted to the spot. At that instant, and almost simultaneously with her arrival and that of the candle, there was heard a loud and furious knocking at the street-door, which caused Newman Noggs to jump up, with great agility, from a beer-barrel on which he had been seated astride, and to exclaim abruptly, and with a face of ashy paleness, ‘Bobster, by the Lord!’", "아가씨를 본 순간, 니콜라스는 흠칫하며 낯빛이 변했다. 가슴이 사납게 뛰었고, 그는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서 있었다. 바로 그 순간, 그녀가 도착하고 촛불이 들어온 것과 거의 동시에, 현관문을 쾅쾅 사납게 두드리는 큰 소리가 들려왔다. 그 소리에 뉴먼 노그스가, 걸터앉아 있던 맥주통에서 아주 날쌔게 펄쩍 뛰어오르더니, 잿빛으로 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 다급히 외쳤다. \"봅스터다, 맙소사!\""]], [["The young lady shrieked, the attendant wrung her hands, Nicholas gazed from one to the other in apparent stupefaction, and Newman hurried to and fro, thrusting his hands into all his pockets successively, and drawing out the linings of every one in the excess of his irresolution.", "아가씨는 비명을 질렀고, 하녀는 두 손을 비틀어 짰고, 니콜라스는 멍하니 넋이 나간 듯 이쪽저쪽을 번갈아 바라봤다. 뉴먼은 이리저리 허둥대며 주머니란 주머니를 차례로 죄다 손으로 쑤셔 보고는, 갈피를 못 잡는 통에 주머니마다 안감을 끄집어냈다."], ["It was but a moment, but the confusion crowded into that one moment no imagination can exaggerate.", "그저 한순간이었지만, 그 한순간에 몰아닥친 혼란은 어떤 상상으로도 부풀려 말할 수 없을 정도였다."]], [["‘Leave the house, for Heaven’s sake!", "\"제발, 이 집에서 나가세요!"], ["We have done wrong, we deserve it all,’ cried the young lady.", "우리가 잘못했어요, 이 꼴을 당해도 싸요.\" 아가씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘Leave the house, or I am ruined and undone for ever.’", "\"이 집에서 나가세요, 안 그러면 전 영영 끝장이에요.\""]], [["‘Will you hear me say but one word?’ cried Nicholas.", "\"제 말 딱 한마디만 들어주시겠어요?\" 니콜라스가 외쳤다."], ["‘Only one.", "\"딱 한마디예요."], ["I will not detain you.", "붙잡지 않을게요."], ["Will you hear me say one word, in explanation of this mischance?’", "이 불상사에 대해 해명할 딱 한마디만 들어주시겠어요?\""]], [["But Nicholas might as well have spoken to the wind, for the young lady, with distracted looks, hurried up the stairs.", "하지만 니콜라스가 그 말을 바람에 대고 한 거나 다름없었다. 아가씨는 정신없는 표정으로 계단을 뛰어 올라가 버렸으니까."], ["He would have followed her, but Newman, twisting his hand in his coat collar, dragged him towards the passage by which they had entered.", "그는 그녀를 뒤따르려 했지만, 뉴먼이 그의 외투 깃을 손으로 비틀어 쥐고는 그들이 들어왔던 통로 쪽으로 그를 끌어당겼다."]], [["‘Let me go, Newman, in the Devil’s name!’ cried Nicholas.", "\"놓으라고요, 뉴먼 형, 제발 좀!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤다."], ["‘I must speak to her. I will!", "\"그분께 말해야 해요."], ["I will not leave this house without.’", "그러고 말 거예요! 그러기 전엔 이 집을 떠나지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["‘Reputation--character--violence--consider,’ said Newman, clinging round him with both arms, and hurrying him away.", "\"평판 — 체면 — 폭력 — 생각 좀 해.\" 뉴먼이 두 팔로 그를 끌어안고 매달리며 서둘러 끌고 갔다."], ["‘Let them open the door.", "\"저 사람들이 문을 열게 두자고."], ["We’ll go, as we came, directly it’s shut.", "문이 닫히는 대로 우리가 들어온 그대로 나가는 거야."], ["Come.", "가자."], ["This way.", "이쪽."], ["Here.’", "여기.\""]], [["Overpowered by the remonstrances of Newman, and the tears and prayers of the girl, and the tremendous knocking above, which had never ceased, Nicholas allowed himself to be hurried off; and, precisely as Mr.", "뉴먼의 만류와, 처녀의 눈물과 애원과, 한순간도 멎지 않는 위층의 그 어마어마한 노크 소리에 짓눌려, 니콜라스는 끌려 나가도록 자신을 내맡겼다."], ["Bobster made his entrance by the street-door, he and Noggs made their exit by the area-gate.", "그리하여 봅스터 씨가 현관문으로 들어오는 바로 그 순간, 그와 노그스는 지하실 출입문으로 빠져나갔다."]], [["They hurried away, through several streets, without stopping or speaking.", "둘은 멈추지도 말하지도 않고 여러 거리를 지나 급히 달아났다."], ["At last, they halted and confronted each other with blank and rueful faces.", "마침내 둘은 걸음을 멈추고, 멍하고 처량한 얼굴로 서로를 마주 봤다."]], [["‘Never mind,’ said Newman, gasping for breath.", "\"괜찮아.\" 뉴먼이 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["‘Don’t be cast down.", "\"기죽지 마."], ["It’s all right.", "다 잘됐어."], ["More fortunate next time.", "다음번엔 운이 더 좋겠지."], ["It couldn’t be helped.", "어쩔 수 없는 일이었어."], ["I did MY part.’", "난 내 몫은 다 했어.\""]], [["‘Excellently,’ replied Nicholas, taking his hand.", "\"훌륭했어요.\" 니콜라스가 그의 손을 잡으며 대답했다."], ["‘Excellently, and like the true and zealous friend you are.", "\"훌륭했어요. 형이 그렇게 진실하고 열성적인 친구다운 모습 그대로요."], ["Only--mind, I am not disappointed, Newman, and feel just as much indebted to you--only IT WAS THE WRONG LADY.’", "다만 — 잘 들어요, 난 실망한 게 아니에요, 형. 그리고 형한테 고마운 마음도 똑같아요 — 다만 그분은 엉뚱한 아가씨였어요.\""]], [["‘Eh?’ cried Newman Noggs.", "\"응?\" 뉴먼 노그스가 외쳤다."], ["‘Taken in by the servant?’", "\"하녀한테 속은 거냐?\""]], [["‘Newman, Newman,’ said Nicholas, laying his hand upon his shoulder: ‘it was the wrong servant too.’", "\"뉴먼 형, 뉴먼 형.\" 니콜라스가 그의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"그 하녀도 엉뚱한 하녀였어요.\""]], [["Newman’s under-jaw dropped, and he gazed at Nicholas, with his sound eye fixed fast and motionless in his head.", "뉴먼의 아래턱이 툭 떨어졌고, 그는 멀쩡한 한쪽 눈을 머릿속에 박힌 듯 고정한 채 꼼짝 않고 니콜라스를 빤히 바라봤다."]], [["‘Don’t take it to heart,’ said Nicholas; ‘it’s of no consequence; you see I don’t care about it; you followed the wrong person, that’s all.’", "\"너무 마음 쓰지 말아요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다. \"별일 아니에요. 보다시피 난 신경 안 써요. 형은 엉뚱한 사람을 뒤따라간 거예요, 그게 다예요.\""]], [["That WAS all.", "정말 그게 다였다."], ["Whether Newman Noggs had looked round the pump, in a slanting direction, so long, that his sight became impaired; or whether, finding that there was time to spare, he had recruited himself with a few drops of something stronger than the pump could yield--by whatsoever means it had come to pass, this was his mistake.", "뉴먼 노그스가 펌프 둘레를 비스듬한 각도로 너무 오래 살피는 바람에 눈이 침침해진 건지, 아니면 시간이 남는 걸 알고 펌프가 내줄 수 있는 것보다 좀 더 독한 무언가로 몇 방울 기운을 차린 건지 — 어떤 까닭으로 그리됐든 간에, 이게 그의 실수였다."], ["And Nicholas went home to brood upon it, and to meditate upon the charms of the unknown young lady, now as far beyond his reach as ever.", "그리고 니콜라스는 집으로 돌아가 그 일을 곱씹으며, 이제는 여전히 손에 닿지 않는 그 낯선 아가씨의 매력을 곰곰이 떠올렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER 41", "# CHAPTER 41"]], [["Containing some Romantic Passages between Mrs.", "니콜라스와 그 중대한 대화를 나눈 그날 이후로, 니클비 부인은 자기 몸치장에 부쩍 정성을 들이기 시작했어."], ["Nickleby and the Gentleman in the Small-clothes next Door\nEver since her last momentous conversation with her son, Mrs.", "그동안 늘 입고 다니던 점잖고 아낙네다운 옷차림에 이런저런 장식과 꾸밈을 차츰차츰 덧붙여 갔지."], ["Nickleby had begun to display unusual care in the adornment of her person, gradually superadding to those staid and matronly habiliments, which had, up to that time, formed her ordinary attire, a variety of embellishments and decorations, slight perhaps in themselves, but, taken together, and considered with reference to the subject of her disclosure, of no mean importance.", "하나하나 따지면 별것 아닐지 몰라도, 다 합쳐 놓고, 게다가 부인이 털어놓았던 그 사연과 견주어 보면 결코 가볍게 볼 일이 아니었어. 검은 드레스마저도 멋들어지게 차려입은 덕에 어딘가 죽은 듯하면서도 살아 있는 듯한 분위기를 풍겼지."], ["Even her black dress assumed something of a deadly-lively air from the jaunty style in which it was worn; and, eked out as its lingering attractions were; by a prudent disposal, here and there, of certain juvenile ornaments of little or no value, which had, for that reason alone, escaped the general wreck and been permitted to slumber peacefully in odd corners of old drawers and boxes where daylight seldom shone, her mourning garments assumed quite a new character.", "그리고 아직 남아 있던 매력에 한 가지를 더 보탰는데, 값나가지도 않는 자잘한 젊은 시절 장신구들을 여기저기 야무지게 달아 둔 거야. 바로 그 값어치 없다는 이유 하나로 살림이 거덜 날 때도 무사히 살아남아, 햇빛이 잘 들지 않는 낡은 서랍이며 상자 구석에서 곱게 잠자고 있던 것들이었지."], ["From being the outward tokens of respect and sorrow for the dead, they became converted into signals of very slaughterous and killing designs upon the living.", "그 덕에 부인의 상복은 완전히 다른 성격을 띠게 됐어. 죽은 이를 향한 존경과 슬픔의 표시이던 것이, 산 사람을 향한 아주 살벌하고 치명적인 작전의 신호로 바뀐 거야."]], [["Mrs. Nickleby might have been stimulated to this proceeding by a lofty sense of duty, and impulses of unquestionable excellence.", "니클비 부인이 이런 일을 벌이게 된 건 어쩌면 드높은 의무감과, 더할 나위 없이 갸륵한 충동 때문이었을지도 몰라."], ["She might, by this time, have become impressed with the sinfulness of long indulgence in unavailing woe, or the necessity of setting a proper example of neatness and decorum to her blooming daughter.", "어쩌면 이 무렵에 이르러, 아무 쓸모도 없는 슬픔에 오래 빠져 있는 게 죄스럽다는 생각이 들었을 수도 있지. 아니면 한창 피어나는 딸에게 단정하고 예의 바른 본보기를 보여 줘야 한다는 생각이 들었거나."], ["Considerations of duty and responsibility apart, the change might have taken its rise in feelings of the purest and most disinterested charity.", "의무니 책임이니 하는 건 다 제쳐 두더라도, 이 변화는 더없이 순수하고 사심 없는 자비심에서 비롯되었을 수도 있어. 니콜라스가 옆집 신사를 험담했잖아."], ["The gentleman next door had been vilified by Nicholas; rudely stigmatised as a dotard and an idiot; and for these attacks upon his understanding, Mrs.", "늙어 빠진 데다 머리까지 돈 멍청이라고 거칠게 낙인을 찍었지. 그런데 그 사람의 정신을 두고 그렇게 깎아내린 데에는 니클비 부인한테도 어느 정도 책임이 있었어."], ["Nickleby was, in some sort, accountable.", "부인은 어쩌면 이런 생각이 들었을 거야."], ["She might have felt that it was the act of a good Christian to show by all means in her power, that the abused gentleman was neither the one nor the other.", "그 모욕당한 신사가 늙은이도 멍청이도 아니라는 걸 자기 힘껏 보여 주는 게 착한 기독교인의 도리라고 말이야. 그렇게 갸륵하고 칭찬받을 만한 목적을 위해서, 부인 자신이 직접 증명해 보이는 것보다 더 나은 방법이 어디 있겠어?"], ["And what better means could she adopt, towards so virtuous and laudable an end, than proving to all men, in her own person, that his passion was the most rational and reasonable in the world, and just the very result, of all others, which discreet and thinking persons might have foreseen, from her incautiously displaying her matured charms, without reserve, under the very eye, as it were, of an ardent and too-susceptible man?", "그 사람의 정념이야말로 세상에서 가장 합리적이고 이치에 맞는 것이며, 분별 있고 사려 깊은 사람이라면 누구나 능히 내다봤을 당연한 결과라는 걸 말이야. 부인이 무르익은 매력을 거리낌 없이, 그것도 열정 넘치고 마음 약한 남자의 바로 코앞에서 무심코 펼쳐 보였으니, 그렇게 될 수밖에 없었다는 걸 증명해 보이는 것보다 더 나은 방법이 어디 있겠어?"]], [["‘Ah!’ said Mrs.", "\"아!\" 니클비 부인이 자못 심각하게 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했어."], ["Nickleby, gravely shaking her head; ‘if Nicholas knew what his poor dear papa suffered before we were engaged, when I used to hate him, he would have a little more feeling.", "\"니콜라스가 알기만 했어도. 가엾은 제 아버지가 우리가 약혼하기 전에 얼마나 마음고생을 했는지, 내가 그이를 미워하던 시절에 말이야."], ["Shall I ever forget the morning I looked scornfully at him when he offered to carry my parasol?", "그걸 알았다면 그 애도 좀 더 헤아릴 줄 알 텐데. 그이가 내 양산을 들어 주겠다고 했을 때, 내가 그이를 비웃듯 쳐다보던 그 아침을 내가 어떻게 잊겠어?"], ["Or that night, when I frowned at him?", "아니면 내가 그이한테 눈살을 찌푸리던 그날 밤은 또 어떻고?"], ["It was a mercy he didn’t emigrate.", "그이가 다른 나라로 떠나 버리지 않은 게 천만다행이지."], ["It very nearly drove him to it.’", "정말 거의 그럴 뻔했거든.\""]], [["Whether the deceased might not have been better off if he had emigrated in his bachelor days, was a question which his relict did not stop to consider; for Kate entered the room, with her workbox, in this stage of her reflections; and a much slighter interruption, or no interruption at all, would have diverted Mrs.", "세상을 떠난 그 양반이 차라리 총각 시절에 멀리 떠나 버렸으면 더 나았을지도 모른다는 문제는, 미망인이 굳이 따져 보려고 멈추지 않았어. 마침 그 생각이 이쯤 이르렀을 때 케이트가 반짇고리를 들고 방으로 들어왔거든."], ["Nickleby’s thoughts into a new channel at any time.", "게다가 이보다 훨씬 사소한 방해가 있었어도, 아니 아무 방해가 없었더라도, 니클비 부인의 생각은 어느 때든 금세 다른 곳으로 흘러가 버렸을 테지."]], [["‘Kate, my dear,’ said Mrs.", "\"케이트, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby; ‘I don’t know how it is, but a fine warm summer day like this, with the birds singing in every direction, always puts me in mind of roast pig, with sage and onion sauce, and made gravy.’", "\"어째서 그런지는 모르겠다만, 이렇게 따뜻하고 화창한 여름날, 사방에서 새들이 지저귀는 날이면 늘 통돼지구이가 떠오르는구나. 세이지와 양파 소스에 그레이비까지 곁들인 그 요리 말이야.\""]], [["‘That’s a curious association of ideas, is it not, mama?’", "\"그건 좀 이상한 연상 아니에요, 엄마?\""]], [["‘Upon my word, my dear, I don’t know,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby. ‘Roast pig; let me see. On the day five weeks after you were christened, we had a roast--no, that couldn’t have been a pig, either, because I recollect there were a pair of them to carve, and your poor papa and I could never have thought of sitting down to two pigs--they must have been partridges. Roast pig! I hardly think we ever could have had one, now I come to remember, for your papa could never bear the sight of them in the shops, and used to say that they always put him in mind of very little babies, only the pigs had much fairer complexions; and he had a horror of little babies, too, because he couldn’t very well afford any increase to his family, and had a natural dislike to the subject. It’s very odd now, what can have put that in my head! I recollect dining once at Mrs. Bevan’s, in that broad street round the corner by the coachmaker’s, where the tipsy man fell through the cellar-flap of an empty house nearly a week before the quarter-day, and wasn’t found till the new tenant went in--and we had roast pig there. It must be that, I think, that reminds me of it, especially as there was a little bird in the room that would keep on singing all the time of dinner--at least, not a little bird, for it was a parrot, and he didn’t sing exactly, for he talked and swore dreadfully: but I think it must be that. Indeed I am sure it must. Shouldn’t you say so, my dear?’", "\"글쎄다, 얘야, 나도 모르겠구나.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어. \"통돼지구이라. 어디 보자. 네 세례식이 끝나고 다섯 주 뒤 되던 날, 우리가 통구이를 먹었는데. 아니, 그건 돼지는 아니었을 거야. 왜냐면 썰어야 할 게 두 마리였던 게 기억나거든. 가엾은 네 아버지랑 나랑 돼지를 두 마리나 놓고 앉을 생각은 도무지 못 했을 테니까. 그건 자고새였을 거야. 통돼지구이라! 가만 생각해 보니 우리가 통돼지구이를 먹은 적이 있기나 한지 모르겠구나. 네 아버지는 가게에 그게 걸려 있는 꼴을 도무지 못 봤거든. 그걸 보면 늘 아주 조그만 갓난아기가 떠오른다고 했어. 다만 돼지가 살결이 훨씬 더 곱다는 게 다르다고 했지. 그이는 갓난아기도 끔찍이 싫어했어. 식구가 늘어나는 걸 감당할 형편이 못 됐으니까. 그래서 그 얘기 자체를 본능적으로 꺼렸지. 참 이상하기도 하지, 대체 무엇 때문에 그게 머릿속에 떠올랐을까! 언젠가 베반 부인 댁에서 저녁을 먹은 적이 있는데, 마차 만드는 집 모퉁이를 돌면 나오는 그 널찍한 거리에 있었지. 거기서 술 취한 남자가 빈 집 지하실 뚜껑을 밟고 빠졌는데, 분기 결산일을 거의 일주일 앞두고 그랬는데도 새 세입자가 들어올 때까지 아무도 못 찾았다지 뭐냐. 아무튼 거기서 통돼지구이를 먹었어. 그게 떠올라서 그런가 보다, 그런 생각이 드는구나. 특히 그 방에 새 한 마리가 있어서 저녁 내내 줄곧 노래를 불러 댔거든. 아니, 새라기보다는 앵무새였지. 노래를 부른 것도 아니고 말을 했어. 그것도 무섭게 욕을 해 댔지. 아무튼 그것 때문일 거야. 정말 틀림없이 그래. 너도 그렇게 생각하지 않니, 얘야?\""]], [["‘I should say there was not a doubt about it, mama,’ returned Kate, with a cheerful smile.", "\"의심할 여지가 없네요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 명랑하게 미소 지으며 대답했어."]], [["‘No; but DO you think so, Kate?’ said Mrs.", "\"아니, 정말로 그렇게 생각하니, 케이트?\""], ["Nickleby, with as much gravity as if it were a question of the most imminent and thrilling interest.", "니클비 부인이 마치 더없이 급박하고 짜릿한 문제라도 되는 양 심각하게 물었어. \"안 그러면 안 그렇다고 당장 말하렴, 알겠지."], ["‘If you don’t, say so at once, you know; because it’s just as well to be correct, particularly on a point of this kind, which is very curious and worth settling while one thinks about it.’", "정확한 게 좋으니까. 특히 이런 종류의 문제는 아주 흥미로워서 생각난 김에 매듭지어 둘 만하거든.\""]], [["Kate laughingly replied that she was quite convinced; and as her mama still appeared undetermined whether it was not absolutely essential that the subject should be renewed, proposed that they should take their work into the summer-house, and enjoy the beauty of the afternoon.", "케이트는 웃으며, 자기도 완전히 확신한다고 대답했어. 그래도 엄마는 이 문제를 다시 꺼내는 게 꼭 필요한 일이 아닌가 하고 여전히 마음을 정하지 못한 눈치였지."], ["Mrs.", "그래서 케이트는 일감을 챙겨 정자로 가서 화창한 오후의 아름다움을 즐기자고 했어."], ["Nickleby readily assented, and to the summer-house they repaired, without further discussion.", "니클비 부인은 선뜻 그러자고 했고, 두 사람은 더 이상 따지지 않고 정자로 향했지."]], [["‘Well, I will say,’ observed Mrs.", "\"그래, 내 이건 꼭 말해야겠다.\""], ["Nickleby, as she took her seat, ‘that there never was such a good creature as Smike.", "니클비 부인이 자리에 앉으며 말했어. \"스마이크 같은 착한 아이는 정말 없을 거야."], ["Upon my word, the pains he has taken in putting this little arbour to rights, and training the sweetest flowers about it, are beyond anything I could have--I wish he wouldn’t put ALL the gravel on your side, Kate, my dear, though, and leave nothing but mould for me.’", "정말이지, 그 애가 이 작은 정자를 손질하고, 둘레에 더없이 어여쁜 꽃들을 길러 놓느라 들인 정성은 내가 도저히 뭐라 할 수가— 그런데 자갈을 죄다 네 쪽에만 깔아 놓고, 케이트, 얘야, 내 쪽에는 흙만 남겨 둔 건 좀 안 그랬으면 좋겠구나.\""]], [["‘Dear mama,’ returned Kate, hastily, ‘take this seat--do--to oblige me, mama.’", "\"엄마.\" 케이트가 얼른 말했어. \"이 자리로 앉으세요. 어서요. 저 좀 봐서요, 엄마.\""]], [["‘No, indeed, my dear.", "\"아니, 됐다, 얘야."], ["I shall keep my own side,’ said Mrs.", "나는 내 자리를 지킬란다.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby.", "\"어머나!"], ["‘Well! I declare!’", "세상에!\""]], [["Kate looked up inquiringly.", "케이트가 묻는 눈빛으로 고개를 들었어."]], [["‘If he hasn’t been,’ said Mrs.", "\"글쎄 그 애가,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby, ‘and got, from somewhere or other, a couple of roots of those flowers that I said I was so fond of, the other night, and asked you if you were not--no, that YOU said YOU were so fond of, the other night, and asked me if I wasn’t--it’s the same thing.", "\"어디서 구해 왔는지 그 꽃 뿌리를 두어 포기 가져다 놨지 뭐냐. 내가 그저께 밤에 그렇게 좋아한다고 했던 그 꽃 말이야."], ["Now, upon my word, I take that as very kind and attentive indeed!", "그러고는 너한테도 좋아하지 않느냐고 물었던— 아니, 네가 그저께 밤에 그렇게 좋아한다고 했던 거구나. 그러고는 나한테 안 좋아하느냐고 물었던— 뭐 마찬가지지."], ["I don’t see,’ added Mrs.", "자, 정말이지, 나는 그게 참 친절하고 세심한 일이라고 생각한다! 그런데,\""], ["Nickleby, looking narrowly about her, ‘any of them on my side, but I suppose they grow best near the gravel.", "니클비 부인이 주위를 꼼꼼히 둘러보며 덧붙였어. \"내 쪽에는 하나도 안 보이는구나."], ["You may depend upon it they do, Kate, and that’s the reason they are all near you, and he has put the gravel there, because it’s the sunny side.", "하긴 그게 자갈 근처에서 제일 잘 자라나 보지. 틀림없이 그럴 거야, 케이트."], ["Upon my word, that’s very clever now!", "그래서 다 네 쪽에 있는 거고, 그 애가 거기다 자갈을 깐 것도 거기가 볕이 잘 드는 쪽이라서 그런 거지."], ["I shouldn’t have had half as much thought myself!’", "정말이지, 그거 참 영리하구나! 나라면 그 절반도 생각 못 했을 거야!\""]], [["‘Mama,’ said Kate, bending over her work so that her face was almost hidden, ‘before you were married--’", "\"엄마.\" 케이트가 일감 위로 고개를 숙여 얼굴이 거의 보이지 않게 한 채 말했어. \"엄마가 결혼하기 전에는—\""]], [["‘Dear me, Kate,’ interrupted Mrs.", "\"아이고, 케이트야.\" 니클비 부인이 가로막았어."], ["Nickleby, ‘what in the name of goodness graciousness makes you fly off to the time before I was married, when I’m talking to you about his thoughtfulness and attention to me?", "\"도대체 무슨 영문으로 내가 결혼하기 전 얘기로 훌쩍 날아가는 거냐. 내가 지금 그 애가 나한테 얼마나 사려 깊고 세심한지를 얘기하고 있는데 말이야."], ["You don’t seem to take the smallest interest in the garden.’", "너는 이 정원에는 손톱만큼도 관심이 없는 모양이구나.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"어머! 엄마.\""], ["mama,’ said Kate, raising her face again, ‘you know I do.’", "케이트가 다시 얼굴을 들며 말했어. \"관심 있는 거 아시잖아요.\""]], [["‘Well then, my dear, why don’t you praise the neatness and prettiness with which it’s kept?’ said Mrs.", "\"그럼, 얘야, 어째서 이 정원이 얼마나 단정하고 예쁘게 가꿔져 있는지 칭찬을 안 하는 거니?\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby. ‘How very odd you are, Kate!’", "\"너 참 이상하기도 하다, 케이트!\""]], [["‘I do praise it, mama,’ answered Kate, gently.", "\"칭찬하잖아요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 부드럽게 대답했어."], ["‘Poor fellow!’", "\"가엾은 아이!\""]], [["‘I scarcely ever hear you, my dear,’ retorted Mrs. Nickleby; ‘that’s all I’ve got to say.’ By this time the good lady had been a long while upon one topic, so she fell at once into her daughter’s little trap, if trap it were, and inquired what she had been going to say.", "\"내 귀에는 좀처럼 안 들리던데, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 받아쳤어. \"내가 할 말은 그게 다야.\" 이쯤 되자 이 착한 부인은 한 가지 화제에 한참 매달려 있었던 터라, 딸이 놓은 작은 덫에—그게 덫이라면 말이지만—단번에 걸려들고 말았어. 그러고는 무슨 말을 하려던 거냐고 물었지."]], [["‘About what, mama?’ said Kate, who had apparently quite forgotten her diversion.", "\"뭐에 대해서요, 엄마?\" 케이트가 물었어. 자기가 화제를 돌렸던 걸 까맣게 잊은 모양이었지."]], [["‘Lor, Kate, my dear,’ returned her mother, ‘why, you’re asleep or stupid!", "\"아이고, 케이트야.\" 엄마가 대꾸했어."], ["About the time before I was married.’", "\"왜, 너 졸고 있거나 멍하구나! 내가 결혼하기 전 얘기 말이야.\""]], [["‘Oh yes!’ said Kate, ‘I remember. I was going to ask, mama, before you were married, had you many suitors?’", "\"아 참!\" 케이트가 말했어. \"기억나요. 여쭤보려던 거예요, 엄마. 엄마가 결혼하기 전에 구혼자가 많았어요?\""]], [["‘Suitors, my dear!’ cried Mrs.", "\"구혼자라니, 얘야!\""], ["Nickleby, with a smile of wonderful complacency.", "니클비 부인이 더없이 흐뭇한 미소를 지으며 외쳤어."], ["‘First and last, Kate, I must have had a dozen at least.’", "\"처음부터 끝까지 따져 보면, 케이트, 적어도 열두 명은 됐을 거야.\""]], [["‘Mama!’ returned Kate, in a tone of remonstrance.", "\"엄마!\" 케이트가 나무라는 투로 받았어."]], [["‘I had indeed, my dear,’ said Mrs. Nickleby; ‘not including your poor papa, or a young gentleman who used to go, at that time, to the same dancing school, and who WOULD send gold watches and bracelets to our house in gilt-edged paper, (which were always returned,) and who afterwards unfortunately went out to Botany Bay in a cadet ship--a convict ship I mean--and escaped into a bush and killed sheep, (I don’t know how they got there,) and was going to be hung, only he accidentally choked himself, and the government pardoned him. Then there was young Lukin,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, beginning with her left thumb and checking off the names on her fingers--‘Mogley--Tipslark--Cabbery--Smifser--’", "\"정말이란다, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어. \"가엾은 네 아버지는 빼고 말이야. 그리고 그 시절 같은 무용 학원에 다니던 어떤 젊은 신사도 빼고. 그 사람은 금시계며 팔찌를 금테 두른 종이에 싸서 우리 집으로 보내 오곤 했지. (늘 도로 돌려보냈지만 말이야.) 그러다 나중에 안됐게도 사관 후보생 배를 타고 보타니 만으로 가게 됐는데— 아니, 죄수 호송선이었지. 그러다 덤불숲으로 달아나서 양들을 죽였어. (그 양들이 어떻게 거기 있었는지는 모르겠다만.) 그래서 교수형을 당할 뻔했는데, 그만 어쩌다 제 손으로 목이 막혀 버려서, 정부가 사면을 해 줬단다. 그리고 젊은 루킨이 있었지.\" 니클비 부인이 왼손 엄지부터 시작해 손가락을 하나씩 꼽으며 말했어. \"모글리— 팁슬락— 캐버리— 스미프서—\""]], [["Having now reached her little finger, Mrs.", "이제 새끼손가락까지 다 꼽은 니클비 부인이 헤아림을 다른 손으로 넘기려던 참이었어."], ["Nickleby was carrying the account over to the other hand, when a loud ‘Hem!’ which appeared to come from the very foundation of the garden-wall, gave both herself and her daughter a violent start.", "그때 정원 담장 밑바닥에서 올라오는 듯한 커다란 \"에헴!\" 소리가 부인과 딸을 둘 다 화들짝 놀라게 했지."]], [["‘Mama!", "\"엄마!"], ["what was that?’ said Kate, in a low tone of voice.", "저게 뭐죠?\" 케이트가 낮은 목소리로 말했어."]], [["‘Upon my word, my dear,’ returned Mrs.", "\"정말이지, 얘야.\""], ["Nickleby, considerably startled, ‘unless it was the gentleman belonging to the next house, I don’t know what it could possibly--’", "니클비 부인이 적잖이 놀라며 대꾸했어. \"옆집에 사는 그 신사가 아니라면, 대체 무엇 때문에 저런—\""]], [["‘A--hem!’ cried the same voice; and that, not in the tone of an ordinary clearing of the throat, but in a kind of bellow, which woke up all the echoes in the neighbourhood, and was prolonged to an extent which must have made the unseen bellower quite black in the face.", "\"에—헴!\" 똑같은 목소리가 외쳤어. 이번엔 흔히 목을 가다듬는 그런 소리가 아니라, 일종의 우렁찬 고함 같은 거였어. 동네의 메아리를 죄다 깨워 놓을 만큼 컸고, 어찌나 길게 끌었던지 보이지 않는 그 고함꾼은 얼굴이 새파래졌을 게 분명했지."]], [["‘I understand it now, my dear,’ said Mrs.", "\"이제 알겠다, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 케이트의 손에 손을 얹으며 말했어."], ["Nickleby, laying her hand on Kate’s; ‘don’t be alarmed, my love, it’s not directed to you, and is not intended to frighten anybody.", "\"놀라지 마라, 얘야. 너한테 하는 게 아니고, 누굴 겁주려는 것도 아니란다."], ["Let us give everybody their due, Kate; I am bound to say that.’", "케이트, 누구든 제 몫은 인정해 줘야지. 나는 그렇게 말할 수밖에 없구나.\""]], [["So saying, Mrs.", "이렇게 말하면서 니클비 부인은 고개를 끄덕이고는 딸의 손등을 여러 번 토닥였어."], ["Nickleby nodded her head, and patted the back of her daughter’s hand, a great many times, and looked as if she could tell something vastly important if she chose, but had self-denial, thank Heaven; and wouldn’t do it.", "그러고는 마음만 먹으면 굉장히 중요한 얘기를 들려줄 수도 있지만, 다행히도 자제할 줄 알아서 그러지 않겠다는 듯한 표정을 지었지."]], [["‘What do you mean, mama?’ demanded Kate, in evident surprise.", "\"무슨 말씀이세요, 엄마?\" 케이트가 눈에 띄게 놀라며 물었어."]], [["‘Don’t be flurried, my dear,’ replied Mrs. Nickleby, looking towards the garden-wall, ‘for you see I’m not, and if it would be excusable in anybody to be flurried, it certainly would--under all the circumstances--be excusable in me, but I am not, Kate--not at all.’", "\"허둥대지 마라, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 정원 담장 쪽을 바라보며 대답했어. \"보다시피 나는 안 그러잖니. 누군가 허둥대도 봐줄 만한 사람이 있다면, 이 모든 사정을 고려할 때 그건 분명 나일 거야. 그래도 나는 안 그런단다, 케이트. 조금도 말이야.\""]], [["‘It seems designed to attract our attention, mama,’ said Kate.", "\"우리 주의를 끌려는 것 같아요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 말했어."]], [["‘It is designed to attract our attention, my dear; at least,’ rejoined Mrs.", "\"우리 주의를 끌려는 거지, 얘야. 적어도,\""], ["Nickleby, drawing herself up, and patting her daughter’s hand more blandly than before, ‘to attract the attention of one of us.", "니클비 부인이 몸을 곧추세우며, 아까보다 더 부드럽게 딸의 손을 토닥이며 받았어. \"우리 둘 중 하나의 주의를 끌려는 거야."], ["Hem!", "에헴!"], ["you needn’t be at all uneasy, my dear.’", "너는 조금도 불편해할 것 없단다, 얘야.\""]], [["Kate looked very much perplexed, and was apparently about to ask for further explanation, when a shouting and scuffling noise, as of an elderly gentleman whooping, and kicking up his legs on loose gravel, with great violence, was heard to proceed from the same direction as the former sounds; and before they had subsided, a large cucumber was seen to shoot up in the air with the velocity of a sky-rocket, whence it descended, tumbling over and over, until it fell at Mrs. Nickleby’s feet.", "케이트가 몹시 어리둥절한 표정을 지으며 더 설명해 달라고 물으려던 참이었어. 그때 앞서 들렸던 소리와 같은 쪽에서 고함치고 발버둥치는 소리가 들려왔지. 나이 지긋한 신사가 헐거운 자갈 위에서 우렁차게 소리치며 다리를 마구 차 대는 듯한 소리였어. 그리고 그 소리가 잦아들기도 전에, 커다란 오이 하나가 폭죽처럼 빠르게 공중으로 솟구쳤어. 그러더니 빙글빙글 굴러떨어져 니클비 부인의 발치에 툭 떨어졌지."]], [["This remarkable appearance was succeeded by another of a precisely similar description; then a fine vegetable marrow, of unusually large dimensions, was seen to whirl aloft, and come toppling down; then, several cucumbers shot up together; and, finally, the air was darkened by a shower of onions, turnip-radishes, and other small vegetables, which fell rolling and scattering, and bumping about, in all directions.", "이 진기한 광경에 이어 똑같은 일이 또 한 번 일어났어. 그러더니 유난히 큼지막한 호박 하나가 공중으로 빙글빙글 솟았다가 비틀비틀 떨어졌지. 그다음엔 오이 여러 개가 한꺼번에 솟구쳤어. 그리고 마침내 양파와 무, 그 밖의 자잘한 채소들이 소나기처럼 쏟아져 하늘을 어둑하게 했지. 그것들은 사방으로 데굴데굴 구르고 흩어지고 부딪치며 떨어졌어."]], [["As Kate rose from her seat, in some alarm, and caught her mother’s hand to run with her into the house, she felt herself rather retarded than assisted in her intention; and following the direction of Mrs. Nickleby’s eyes, was quite terrified by the apparition of an old black velvet cap, which, by slow degrees, as if its wearer were ascending a ladder or pair of steps, rose above the wall dividing their garden from that of the next cottage, (which, like their own, was a detached building,) and was gradually followed by a very large head, and an old face, in which were a pair of most extraordinary grey eyes: very wild, very wide open, and rolling in their sockets, with a dull, languishing, leering look, most ugly to behold.", "케이트가 좀 놀라서 자리에서 일어나 엄마 손을 잡고 집 안으로 달려가려는데, 도와주기는커녕 오히려 발목을 잡히는 듯한 느낌이 들었어. 그래서 니클비 부인의 시선을 따라가 보니, 낡은 검정 벨벳 모자 하나가 나타나 케이트는 그만 질겁하고 말았지. 그 모자는 마치 쓴 사람이 사다리나 계단을 한 칸씩 올라오는 것처럼 천천히, 자기네 정원과 옆집 오두막 정원을 가르는 담장 위로 솟아올랐어. (옆집 역시 자기네 집처럼 따로 떨어진 단독 건물이었지.) 그리고 그 뒤로 아주 커다란 머리통과 늙은 얼굴이 차츰 따라 올라왔어. 그 얼굴엔 더없이 기묘한 잿빛 두 눈이 박혀 있었지. 아주 사납고, 한껏 부릅뜬 채, 눈구멍 속에서 흐릿하고 게슴츠레하니 곁눈질하듯 굴러다니는데, 보기에 더없이 흉측했어."]], [["‘Mama!’ cried Kate, really terrified for the moment, ‘why do you stop, why do you lose an instant?", "\"엄마!\" 케이트가 그 순간 정말로 겁에 질려 외쳤어."], ["Mama, pray come in!’", "\"왜 멈춰 서세요, 왜 한순간도 머뭇거리세요? 엄마, 제발 들어가요!\""]], [["‘Kate, my dear,’ returned her mother, still holding back, ‘how can you be so foolish?", "\"케이트, 얘야.\" 엄마가 여전히 버티며 대꾸했어."], ["I’m ashamed of you.", "\"어쩜 그렇게 어리석을 수가 있니?"], ["How do you suppose you are ever to get through life, if you’re such a coward as this?", "내가 다 창피하구나. 그렇게 겁쟁이여서야 어떻게 세상을 헤쳐 나갈 작정이니?"], ["What do you want, sir?’ said Mrs.", "무슨 일이세요, 선생님?\""], ["Nickleby, addressing the intruder with a sort of simpering displeasure.", "니클비 부인이 야릇하게 새침을 떨며 못마땅한 투로 침입자에게 말을 건넸어."], ["‘How dare you look into this garden?’", "\"어떻게 감히 이 정원을 들여다보시는 거예요?\""]], [["‘Queen of my soul,’ replied the stranger, folding his hands together, ‘this goblet sip!’", "\"내 영혼의 여왕이여.\" 낯선 사람이 두 손을 맞잡으며 대답했어. \"이 술잔을 한 모금 드시오!\""]], [["‘Nonsense, sir,’ said Mrs.", "\"무슨 말도 안 되는 소리예요, 선생님.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["‘Kate, my love, pray be quiet.’", "\"케이트, 얘야, 제발 좀 가만있으렴.\""]], [["‘Won’t you sip the goblet?’ urged the stranger, with his head imploringly on one side, and his right hand on his breast.", "\"이 술잔을 한 모금 안 드시겠소?\" 낯선 사람이 머리를 한쪽으로 애원하듯 기울이고 오른손을 가슴에 얹은 채 졸랐어."], ["‘Oh, do sip the goblet!’", "\"오, 부디 이 술잔을 한 모금 드시오!\""]], [["‘I shall not consent to do anything of the kind, sir,’ said Mrs Nickleby.", "\"나는 그런 짓은 절대 하지 않을 거예요, 선생님.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["‘Pray, begone.’", "\"부디 물러가세요.\""]], [["‘Why is it,’ said the old gentleman, coming up a step higher, and leaning his elbows on the wall, with as much complacency as if he were looking out of window, ‘why is it that beauty is always obdurate, even when admiration is as honourable and respectful as mine?’ Here he smiled, kissed his hand, and made several low bows. ‘Is it owing to the bees, who, when the honey season is over, and they are supposed to have been killed with brimstone, in reality fly to Barbary and lull the captive Moors to sleep with their drowsy songs? Or is it,’ he added, dropping his voice almost to a whisper, ‘in consequence of the statue at Charing Cross having been lately seen, on the Stock Exchange at midnight, walking arm-in-arm with the Pump from Aldgate, in a riding-habit?’", "\"어째서일까요.\" 노신사가 한 칸 더 올라와 마치 창밖을 내다보듯 더없이 흐뭇한 표정으로 담장에 팔꿈치를 괴며 말했어. \"어째서 아름다움이란 늘 이리도 매몰찰까요. 나처럼 흠모하는 마음이 이토록 떳떳하고 정중한데도 말이지요.\" 여기서 그는 미소를 짓고 손에 입을 맞추더니 여러 번 깊이 절을 했어. \"그건 꿀벌들 때문일까요? 꿀 철이 끝나 유황으로 죽임을 당했다고들 여겨질 때, 실은 바르바리로 날아가 사로잡힌 무어인들을 그 나른한 노래로 잠재운다는 그 꿀벌들 말이오. 아니면,\" 그가 목소리를 거의 속삭임으로 낮추며 덧붙였어. \"채링 크로스의 동상이 요전에 한밤중 증권 거래소에서 목격된 탓일까요? 승마복을 차려입고 올드게이트의 펌프와 팔짱을 끼고 거니는 모습으로 말이오.\""]], [["‘Mama,’ murmured Kate, ‘do you hear him?’", "\"엄마.\" 케이트가 중얼거렸어. \"저 사람 말 들리세요?\""]], [["‘Hush, my dear!’ replied Mrs.", "\"쉿, 얘야!\" 니클비 부인이 같은 투로 대답했어."], ["Nickleby, in the same tone of voice, ‘he is very polite, and I think that was a quotation from the poets.", "\"저분은 아주 정중하시잖니. 게다가 방금 그건 시인의 글을 인용한 것 같구나."], ["Pray, don’t worry me so--you’ll pinch my arm black and blue.", "제발 나를 그렇게 괴롭히지 마라— 내 팔을 시퍼렇게 꼬집어 놓겠다."], ["Go away, sir!’", "물러가세요, 선생님!\""]], [["‘Quite away?’ said the gentleman, with a languishing look.", "\"아주 물러가라고요?\" 신사가 게슴츠레한 눈빛으로 말했어."], ["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["quite away?’", "아주 물러가라고요?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ returned Mrs.", "\"네.\""], ["Nickleby, ‘certainly.", "니클비 부인이 대답했어."], ["You have no business here.", "\"그럼요. 여기는 당신이 있을 곳이 아니에요."], ["This is private property, sir; you ought to know that.’", "여긴 사유지예요, 선생님. 그쯤은 아셔야죠.\""]], [["‘I do know,’ said the old gentleman, laying his finger on his nose, with an air of familiarity, most reprehensible, ‘that this is a sacred and enchanted spot, where the most divine charms’--here he kissed his hand and bowed again--‘waft mellifluousness over the neighbours’ gardens, and force the fruit and vegetables into premature existence. That fact I am acquainted with. But will you permit me, fairest creature, to ask you one question, in the absence of the planet Venus, who has gone on business to the Horse Guards, and would otherwise--jealous of your superior charms--interpose between us?’", "\"내가 알기로는,\" 노신사가 더없이 못마땅하게도 허물없는 태도로 콧등에 손가락을 얹으며 말했어. \"여기가 신성하고 마법에 걸린 곳이라는 것이오. 더없이 거룩한 매력이\"— 여기서 그는 또 손에 입을 맞추고 절을 했어. \"감미로움을 이웃집 정원까지 실어 보내, 과일과 채소를 철도 되기 전에 억지로 자라나게 하는 곳 말이오. 그 사실만큼은 내가 익히 알고 있소. 하지만 더없이 아리따운 분이여, 한 가지 물어봐도 되겠소? 마침 금성이 자리를 비운 틈에 말이오. 그분이 호스 가즈로 볼일을 보러 가셨거든. 안 그랬으면 당신의 더 빼어난 매력을 시샘해서 우리 사이를 가로막았을 텐데.\""]], [["‘Kate,’ observed Mrs.", "\"케이트.\""], ["Nickleby, turning to her daughter, ‘it’s very awkward, positively.", "니클비 부인이 딸을 돌아보며 말했어. \"이거 참 난처하구나, 정말로."], ["I really don’t know what to say to this gentleman.", "이 신사분께 뭐라고 해야 할지 모르겠어."], ["One ought to be civil, you know.’", "너도 알다시피 예의는 지켜야 하잖니.\""]], [["‘Dear mama,’ rejoined Kate, ‘don’t say a word to him, but let us run away as fast as we can, and shut ourselves up till Nicholas comes home.’", "\"엄마.\" 케이트가 받았어. \"저 사람한테 한마디도 하지 마시고, 우리 어서 달아나요. 그래서 니콜라스가 돌아올 때까지 문을 꼭 닫고 있어요.\""]], [["Mrs.", "니클비 부인은 이 굴욕적인 제안에 거만하다 못해 경멸하는 듯한 표정을 지었어."], ["Nickleby looked very grand, not to say contemptuous, at this humiliating proposal; and, turning to the old gentleman, who had watched them during these whispers with absorbing eagerness, said:", "그러고는 이 속닥거림 내내 두 사람을 넋을 잃고 열심히 지켜보던 노신사를 돌아보며 말했지."]], [["‘If you will conduct yourself, sir, like the gentleman I should imagine you to be, from your language and--and--appearance, (quite the counterpart of your grandpapa, Kate, my dear, in his best days,) and will put your question to me in plain words, I will answer it.’", "\"선생님, 만약 당신이 내가 짐작하기에 마땅한 신사답게— 말씨로 보나, 그리고 그— 그— 풍채로 보나 그래 보이는데요. (한창때의 네 할아버지를 빼다박았구나, 케이트, 얘야.) 그렇게 처신하시고, 질문도 또렷한 말로 해 주신다면, 제가 대답해 드리지요.\""]], [["If Mrs.", "만약 니클비 부인의 그 훌륭한 아버지가 한창때 지금 담장 너머로 넘겨다보는 이 이웃과 닮았었다면, 적어도 한창 시절에 아주 괴상하게 생긴 노신사였던 게 틀림없었어."], ["Nickleby’s excellent papa had borne, in his best days, a resemblance to the neighbour now looking over the wall, he must have been, to say the least, a very queer-looking old gentleman in his prime.", "어쩌면 케이트도 그렇게 생각했는지, 그 살아 있는 초상화를 어느 정도 유심히 살펴보았지."], ["Perhaps Kate thought so, for she ventured to glance at his living portrait with some attention, as he took off his black velvet cap, and, exhibiting a perfectly bald head, made a long series of bows, each accompanied with a fresh kiss of the hand.", "신사가 검정 벨벳 모자를 벗고 완전히 벗어진 머리를 드러내며 줄줄이 절을 해 대는 동안 말이야. 절을 할 때마다 새로 손에 입을 맞추는 것도 잊지 않았어."], ["After exhausting himself, to all appearance, with this fatiguing performance, he covered his head once more, pulled the cap very carefully over the tips of his ears, and resuming his former attitude, said,", "보아하니 이 고된 동작에 완전히 진이 빠진 듯하자, 그는 다시 머리에 모자를 쓰고는 귓등까지 아주 조심스럽게 끌어내렸어. 그러고는 아까의 자세로 돌아가 말했지."]], [["‘The question is--’", "\"질문은—\""]], [["Here he broke off to look round in every direction, and satisfy himself beyond all doubt that there were no listeners near. Assured that there were not, he tapped his nose several times, accompanying the action with a cunning look, as though congratulating himself on his caution; and stretching out his neck, said in a loud whisper,", "여기서 그는 말을 멈추고 사방을 둘러보았어. 근처에 엿듣는 사람이 없다는 걸 한 점 의심 없이 확인하려는 거였지. 아무도 없다고 안심하자, 그는 콧등을 여러 번 톡톡 두드렸어. 그러면서 교활한 눈빛을 곁들였는데, 마치 신중을 기한 자신을 대견해하는 듯했지. 그러더니 목을 쭉 빼고 큰 소리로 속삭였어."]], [["‘Are you a princess?’", "\"당신은 공주요?\""]], [["‘You are mocking me, sir,’ replied Mrs.", "\"나를 놀리시는군요, 선생님.\""], ["Nickleby, making a feint of retreating towards the house.", "니클비 부인이 집 쪽으로 물러나는 시늉을 하며 대답했어."]], [["‘No, but are you?’ said the old gentleman.", "\"아니, 정말로, 당신 공주요?\" 노신사가 말했어."]], [["‘You know I am not, sir,’ replied Mrs.", "\"아닌 거 아시잖아요, 선생님.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 대답했어."]], [["‘Then are you any relation to the Archbishop of Canterbury?’ inquired the old gentleman with great anxiety, ‘or to the Pope of Rome?", "\"그럼 캔터베리 대주교와 친척이오?\" 노신사가 몹시 애가 타서 물었어."], ["Or the Speaker of the House of Commons?", "\"아니면 로마 교황과? 아니면 하원 의장과?"], ["Forgive me, if I am wrong, but I was told you were niece to the Commissioners of Paving, and daughter-in-law to the Lord Mayor and Court of Common Council, which would account for your relationship to all three.’", "내가 틀렸다면 용서하시오만, 듣자 하니 당신이 포장 위원회의 조카요, 시장 어른과 시 참사회의 며느리라던데. 그렇다면 그 세 분 모두와 친척인 셈이 되니까 말이오.\""]], [["‘Whoever has spread such reports, sir,’ returned Mrs.", "\"누가 그런 소문을 퍼뜨렸든, 선생님.\" 니클비 부인이 다소 발끈해서 받았어."], ["Nickleby, with some warmth, ‘has taken great liberties with my name, and one which I am sure my son Nicholas, if he was aware of it, would not allow for an instant.", "\"내 이름을 가지고 아주 함부로 군 거예요. 우리 아들 니콜라스가 그걸 안다면 한순간도 가만두지 않을 거라고 장담해요."], ["The idea!’ said Mrs.", "별 소리를 다!\""], ["Nickleby, drawing herself up, ‘niece to the Commissioners of Paving!’", "니클비 부인이 몸을 곧추세우며 말했어. \"포장 위원회의 조카라니!\""]], [["‘Pray, mama, come away!’ whispered Kate.", "\"제발, 엄마, 가요!\" 케이트가 속삭였어."]], [["‘“Pray mama!”", "\"'제발 엄마!'"], ["Nonsense, Kate,’ said Mrs.", "말도 안 되는 소리, 케이트.\""], ["Nickleby, angrily, ‘but that’s just the way.", "니클비 부인이 성을 내며 말했어. \"꼭 그런 식이지."], ["If they had said I was niece to a piping bullfinch, what would you care?", "누가 나더러 피리 소리 내는 멋쟁이새의 조카라고 했으면 네가 신경이나 썼겠니?"], ["But I have no sympathy,’ whimpered Mrs.", "하지만 나를 알아주는 사람이 없구나.\" 니클비 부인이 훌쩍였어."], ["Nickleby. ‘I don’t expect it, that’s one thing.’", "\"기대도 안 한다만, 그게 한 가지 위안이지.\""]], [["‘Tears!’ cried the old gentleman, with such an energetic jump, that he fell down two or three steps and grated his chin against the wall.", "\"눈물이군!\" 노신사가 어찌나 힘차게 펄쩍 뛰었던지 두세 칸을 미끄러져 내려가 턱을 담장에 긁히며 외쳤어."], ["‘Catch the crystal globules--catch ‘em--bottle ‘em up--cork ‘em tight--put sealing wax on the top--seal ‘em with a cupid--label ‘em “Best quality”--and stow ‘em away in the fourteen binn, with a bar of iron on the top to keep the thunder off!’", "\"저 수정 방울을 받아라— 받으라니까— 병에 담아— 마개를 꽉 막고— 위에 봉랍을 발라— 큐피드 무늬로 봉인을 찍고— '최상품'이라고 딱지를 붙여— 그러고는 열네 번 칸에 넣어 둬. 위에 쇠막대를 얹어서 천둥이 못 들어오게 하고 말이야!\""]], [["Issuing these commands, as if there were a dozen attendants all actively engaged in their execution, he turned his velvet cap inside out, put it on with great dignity so as to obscure his right eye and three-fourths of his nose, and sticking his arms a-kimbo, looked very fiercely at a sparrow hard by, till the bird flew away, when he put his cap in his pocket with an air of great satisfaction, and addressed himself with respectful demeanour to Mrs.", "이런 명령을 내리는 모습이, 마치 그 일을 부지런히 실행에 옮길 하인이 열두 명쯤 있기라도 한 것 같았어. 그러더니 그는 벨벳 모자를 뒤집어 까서 더없이 위엄 있게 눌러썼는데, 오른쪽 눈과 코의 사 분의 삼이 가려질 만큼 푹 눌러썼지."], ["Nickleby.", "그러고는 두 팔을 양옆구리에 척 짚고서 가까이 있는 참새 한 마리를 어찌나 사납게 노려보던지, 새가 날아가 버리고 말았어. 그제야 그는 더없이 흡족한 표정으로 모자를 주머니에 집어넣고는, 정중한 태도로 니클비 부인에게 말을 건넸어."]], [["‘Beautiful madam,’ such were his words, ‘if I have made any mistake with regard to your family or connections, I humbly beseech you to pardon me. If I supposed you to be related to Foreign Powers or Native Boards, it is because you have a manner, a carriage, a dignity, which you will excuse my saying that none but yourself (with the single exception perhaps of the tragic muse, when playing extemporaneously on the barrel organ before the East India Company) can parallel. I am not a youth, ma’am, as you see; and although beings like you can never grow old, I venture to presume that we are fitted for each other.’", "\"아름다운 부인이여.\" 그의 말은 이랬어. \"내가 당신의 가문이나 친척에 대해 무슨 착오를 일으켰다면, 부디 너그러이 용서해 주시오. 내가 당신을 외국의 권력자들이나 본국의 위원회와 친척이라고 짐작했다면, 그건 당신에게 어떤 몸가짐과 자태, 그리고 기품이 있기 때문이오. 외람되지만, 당신 말고는 그 누구도— 굳이 따지자면 비극의 여신이 동인도 회사 앞에서 손풍금을 즉흥으로 연주할 때라면 모를까— 그 누구도 견줄 수 없는 기품 말이오. 보다시피 나는 풋내기가 아니오, 부인. 비록 당신 같은 분은 결코 늙는 법이 없겠지만, 외람되이 짐작건대 우리는 천생연분인 것 같소.\""]], [["‘Really, Kate, my love!’ said Mrs.", "\"정말이지, 케이트, 얘야!\""], ["Nickleby faintly, and looking another way.", "니클비 부인이 힘없이 말하며 딴 데로 눈길을 돌렸어."]], [["‘I have estates, ma’am,’ said the old gentleman, flourishing his right hand negligently, as if he made very light of such matters, and speaking very fast; ‘jewels, lighthouses, fish-ponds, a whalery of my own in the North Sea, and several oyster-beds of great profit in the Pacific Ocean.", "\"나는 영지를 가지고 있소, 부인.\" 노신사가 그런 건 아주 대수롭지 않다는 듯 오른손을 한가로이 휘저으며 아주 빠르게 말했어."], ["If you will have the kindness to step down to the Royal Exchange and to take the cocked-hat off the stoutest beadle’s head, you will find my card in the lining of the crown, wrapped up in a piece of blue paper.", "\"보석에, 등대에, 양어장에, 북해에 내 소유의 포경장이 있고, 태평양에는 이문이 두둑한 굴 양식장이 여럿 있소. 수고스럽지만 왕립 거래소로 내려가서 가장 뚱뚱한 안내인의 머리에서 삼각모를 벗겨 보시오."], ["My walking-stick is also to be seen on application to the chaplain of the House of Commons, who is strictly forbidden to take any money for showing it.", "그럼 모자 꼭대기 안감 속에 파란 종이에 싸인 내 명함이 들어 있을 거요. 내 지팡이도 하원 전속 목사에게 청하면 볼 수 있소."], ["I have enemies about me, ma’am,’ he looked towards his house and spoke very low, ‘who attack me on all occasions, and wish to secure my property.", "그 목사는 그걸 보여 주고 돈을 받는 게 엄격히 금지돼 있지요. 내 둘레에는 적들이 있소, 부인.\""], ["If you bless me with your hand and heart, you can apply to the Lord Chancellor or call out the military if necessary--sending my toothpick to the commander-in-chief will be sufficient--and so clear the house of them before the ceremony is performed.", "그가 자기 집 쪽을 바라보며 아주 낮은 소리로 말했어. \"틈만 나면 나를 공격하고, 내 재산을 가로채려는 자들이오."], ["After that, love, bliss and rapture; rapture, love and bliss.", "만약 당신이 손과 마음을 내게 주신다면, 당신은 대법관에게 청을 넣을 수도 있고, 필요하면 군대를 부를 수도 있소— 내 이쑤시개를 총사령관에게 보내면 충분할 거요— 그래서 예식을 치르기 전에 그자들을 집에서 싹 몰아낼 수 있지요. 그러고 나면, 사랑과 더없는 기쁨과 황홀함이오."], ["Be mine, be mine!’", "황홀함과 사랑과 더없는 기쁨이지. 내 사람이 되어 주오, 내 사람이 되어 주오!\""]], [["Repeating these last words with great rapture and enthusiasm, the old gentleman put on his black velvet cap again, and looking up into the sky in a hasty manner, said something that was not quite intelligible concerning a balloon he expected, and which was rather after its time.", "이 마지막 말을 더없는 황홀함과 열정으로 거듭 외치고는, 노신사는 검정 벨벳 모자를 다시 쓰고 황급히 하늘을 올려다보았어. 그러더니 자기가 기다리는 기구가 예정보다 좀 늦는다는, 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 말을 중얼거렸지."]], [["‘Be mine, be mine!’ repeated the old gentleman.", "\"내 사람이 되어 주오, 내 사람이 되어 주오!\" 노신사가 거듭 외쳤어."]], [["‘Kate, my dear,’ said Mrs.", "\"케이트, 얘야.\""], ["Nickleby, ‘I have hardly the power to speak; but it is necessary for the happiness of all parties that this matter should be set at rest for ever.’", "니클비 부인이 말했어. \"말을 할 기운도 거의 없다만, 모두의 행복을 위해서 이 문제는 영영 매듭을 지어 둘 필요가 있구나.\""]], [["‘Surely there is no necessity for you to say one word, mama?’ reasoned Kate.", "\"엄마가 한마디라도 하셔야 할 까닭이 정말 없잖아요?\" 케이트가 타일렀어."]], [["‘You will allow me, my dear, if you please, to judge for myself,’ said Mrs.", "\"미안하다만, 얘야, 그건 내가 알아서 판단하게 해 다오.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘Be mine, be mine!’ cried the old gentleman.", "\"내 사람이 되어 주오, 내 사람이 되어 주오!\" 노신사가 외쳤어."]], [["‘It can scarcely be expected, sir,’ said Mrs.", "\"낯선 분께,\""], ["Nickleby, fixing her eyes modestly on the ground, ‘that I should tell a stranger whether I feel flattered and obliged by such proposals, or not.", "니클비 부인이 다소곳이 눈을 땅에 떨군 채 말했어. \"이런 청혼에 내가 우쭐하고 고마운지 아닌지를 말해 드리리라고 기대하시기는 어렵겠지요, 선생님."], ["They certainly are made under very singular circumstances; still at the same time, as far as it goes, and to a certain extent of course’ (Mrs.", "분명 아주 별난 상황에서 하신 청이긴 해요. 그래도 그것대로, 또 어느 정도까지는 물론\""], ["Nickleby’s customary qualification), ‘they must be gratifying and agreeable to one’s feelings.’", "(니클비 부인이 늘 붙이는 단서였지.) \"사람의 마음에 흐뭇하고 기껍지 않을 수 없는 일이긴 하지요.\""]], [["‘Be mine, be mine,’ cried the old gentleman.", "\"내 사람이 되어 주오, 내 사람이 되어 주오.\""], ["‘Gog and Magog, Gog and Magog.", "노신사가 외쳤어. \"고그와 마고그, 고그와 마고그."], ["Be mine, be mine!’", "내 사람이 되어 주오, 내 사람이 되어 주오!\""]], [["‘It will be sufficient for me to say, sir,’ resumed Mrs.", "\"이 말만 하면 될 것 같군요, 선생님.\""], ["Nickleby, with perfect seriousness--‘and I’m sure you’ll see the propriety of taking an answer and going away--that I have made up my mind to remain a widow, and to devote myself to my children.", "니클비 부인이 더없이 진지하게 말을 이었어. \"그리고 대답을 듣고 물러가는 게 마땅하다는 걸 당신도 분명 아실 거예요."], ["You may not suppose I am the mother of two children--indeed many people have doubted it, and said that nothing on earth could ever make ‘em believe it possible--but it is the case, and they are both grown up.", "나는 과부로 남아서 아이들에게 헌신하기로 마음을 굳혔답니다. 당신은 내가 두 아이의 어미라고는 짐작 못 하실 거예요— 실제로 많은 이들이 그걸 의심했고, 세상 무엇으로도 그게 가능하다고는 도저히 못 믿겠다고들 했지요— 하지만 사실이고, 둘 다 다 컸어요."], ["We shall be very glad to have you for a neighbour--very glad; delighted, I’m sure--but in any other character it’s quite impossible, quite.", "우리는 당신을 이웃으로 두게 되면 무척 기쁠 거예요— 정말 기쁘지요. 더없이 반가운 일이고요— 하지만 그 외의 다른 사이로는 도저히 안 될 일이에요, 정말로요."], ["As to my being young enough to marry again, that perhaps may be so, or it may not be; but I couldn’t think of it for an instant, not on any account whatever.", "내가 다시 결혼할 만큼 젊으냐 하면, 어쩌면 그럴 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 단 한순간도 그런 건 생각할 수 없어요."], ["I said I never would, and I never will.", "무슨 일이 있어도 말이에요. 나는 결코 안 그러겠다고 했고, 결코 안 그럴 거예요."], ["It’s a very painful thing to have to reject proposals, and I would much rather that none were made; at the same time this is the answer that I determined long ago to make, and this is the answer I shall always give.’", "청혼을 거절해야 하는 건 참으로 가슴 아픈 일이라서, 아예 아무도 청혼하지 않았으면 좋겠어요. 그래도 이건 내가 오래전에 마음먹은 대답이고, 앞으로도 늘 할 대답이에요.\""]], [["These observations were partly addressed to the old gentleman, partly to Kate, and partly delivered in soliloquy.", "이 말은 한편으로는 노신사에게, 한편으로는 케이트에게 한 것이었고, 또 한편으로는 혼잣말로 흘러나온 거였어."], ["Towards their conclusion, the suitor evinced a very irreverent degree of inattention, and Mrs.", "말이 끝나갈 무렵, 구혼자는 더없이 불경스러울 만큼 딴청을 피웠어."], ["Nickleby had scarcely finished speaking, when, to the great terror both of that lady and her daughter, he suddenly flung off his coat, and springing on the top of the wall, threw himself into an attitude which displayed his small-clothes and grey worsteds to the fullest advantage, and concluded by standing on one leg, and repeating his favourite bellow with increased vehemence.", "그리고 니클비 부인이 말을 채 끝내기도 전에, 그 부인과 딸을 둘 다 소스라치게 하더니, 그는 별안간 외투를 휙 벗어 던지고 담장 꼭대기로 펄쩍 뛰어올랐어. 그러고는 반바지와 잿빛 모직 양말을 한껏 자랑하는 자세를 취하더니, 마침내 한 발로 서서 그 좋아하는 고함을 더욱 우렁차게 거듭 질렀지."]], [["While he was still dwelling on the last note, and embellishing it with a prolonged flourish, a dirty hand was observed to glide stealthily and swiftly along the top of the wall, as if in pursuit of a fly, and then to clasp with the utmost dexterity one of the old gentleman’s ankles.", "그가 여전히 그 마지막 음을 길게 끌며 거기에 한껏 멋을 부려 장식하고 있는데, 꾀죄죄한 손 하나가 담장 꼭대기를 따라 살그머니 재빠르게 미끄러져 가는 게 보였어. 마치 파리를 쫓는 것 같았지."], ["This done, the companion hand appeared, and clasped the other ankle.", "그러더니 더없이 날렵한 솜씨로 노신사의 발목 한쪽을 덥석 움켜잡았어. 그러고 나자 짝꿍 손이 나타나 다른 발목을 움켜잡았지."]], [["Thus encumbered the old gentleman lifted his legs awkwardly once or twice, as if they were very clumsy and imperfect pieces of machinery, and then looking down on his own side of the wall, burst into a loud laugh.", "이렇게 발목을 붙잡힌 노신사는 두 다리를 어색하게 한두 번 들썩였어. 마치 아주 서투르고 엉성한 기계 부품이라도 되는 것처럼 말이야. 그러더니 담장 자기 쪽을 내려다보고는 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸어."]], [["‘It’s you, is it?’ said the old gentleman.", "\"너로구나, 그렇지?\" 노신사가 말했어."]], [["‘Yes, it’s me,’ replied a gruff voice.", "\"네, 접니다.\" 걸걸한 목소리가 대답했어."]], [["‘How’s the Emperor of Tartary?’ said the old gentleman.", "\"타타르 황제는 어떠시냐?\" 노신사가 말했어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["he’s much the same as usual,’ was the reply.", "늘 그러시지요.\" 대답이 돌아왔어."], ["‘No better and no worse.’", "\"더 좋지도 더 나쁘지도 않습니다.\""]], [["‘The young Prince of China,’ said the old gentleman, with much interest.", "\"중국의 어린 왕자는,\" 노신사가 자못 관심을 보이며 말했어."], ["‘Is he reconciled to his father-in-law, the great potato salesman?’", "\"그 장인인 위대한 감자 장수와 화해했느냐?\""]], [["‘No,’ answered the gruff voice; ‘and he says he never will be, that’s more.’", "\"아니요.\" 걸걸한 목소리가 대답했어. \"게다가 결코 화해 안 할 거라고 하던데요.\""]], [["‘If that’s the case,’ observed the old gentleman, ‘perhaps I’d better come down.’", "\"그렇다면,\" 노신사가 말했어. \"아무래도 내려가는 게 낫겠구나.\""]], [["‘Well,’ said the man on the other side, ‘I think you had, perhaps.’", "\"글쎄요.\" 담장 저쪽 사내가 말했어. \"그러시는 게 나을 것 같습니다, 아마도.\""]], [["One of the hands being then cautiously unclasped, the old gentleman dropped into a sitting posture, and was looking round to smile and bow to Mrs.", "그러자 한쪽 손이 조심스럽게 풀렸고, 노신사는 주저앉는 자세로 내려앉았어."], ["Nickleby, when he disappeared with some precipitation, as if his legs had been pulled from below.", "그러고는 니클비 부인에게 미소 짓고 절하려고 둘러보던 참에, 마치 누가 밑에서 다리를 잡아당기기라도 한 듯 후다닥 사라져 버렸지."]], [["Very much relieved by his disappearance, Kate was turning to speak to her mama, when the dirty hands again became visible, and were immediately followed by the figure of a coarse squat man, who ascended by the steps which had been recently occupied by their singular neighbour.", "그가 사라지자 한결 마음이 놓인 케이트가 엄마에게 말을 건네려고 돌아서는데, 그 꾀죄죄한 손이 다시 나타났어. 그리고 곧이어 거칠고 땅딸막한 사내의 모습이 따라 올라왔지. 조금 전 그 별난 이웃이 딛고 섰던 바로 그 계단을 밟고서 말이야."]], [["‘Beg your pardon, ladies,’ said this new comer, grinning and touching his hat.", "\"실례합니다, 아씨들.\" 새로 나타난 이가 씩 웃으며 모자에 손을 대고 말했어."], ["‘Has he been making love to either of you?’", "\"저분이 두 분 중 누구한테 사랑 고백이라도 하던가요?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ said Kate.", "\"네.\" 케이트가 말했어."]], [["‘Ah!’ rejoined the man, taking his handkerchief out of his hat and wiping his face, ‘he always will, you know.", "\"아!\" 사내가 모자에서 손수건을 꺼내 얼굴을 닦으며 받았어."], ["Nothing will prevent his making love.’", "\"원래 늘 그러신답니다, 아시겠지만요. 사랑 고백은 무슨 수로도 못 말려요.\""]], [["‘I need not ask you if he is out of his mind, poor creature,’ said Kate.", "\"가엾기도 하지, 저분이 정신이 온전치 않은 거냐고는 굳이 안 물어봐도 되겠네요.\" 케이트가 말했어."]], [["‘Why no,’ replied the man, looking into his hat, throwing his handkerchief in at one dab, and putting it on again.", "\"아무렴요.\" 사내가 모자 안을 들여다보고는 손수건을 한 번에 휙 던져 넣고 다시 쓰며 대답했어."], ["‘That’s pretty plain, that is.’", "\"그건 뻔히 보이지요, 그렇고말고요.\""]], [["‘Has he been long so?’ asked Kate.", "\"오래되셨나요?\" 케이트가 물었어."]], [["‘A long while.’", "\"오래됐지요.\""]], [["‘And is there no hope for him?’ said Kate, compassionately", "\"그럼 회복될 가망은 없는 건가요?\" 케이트가 가엾다는 듯 물었어."]], [["‘Not a bit, and don’t deserve to be,’ replied the keeper.", "\"눈곱만큼도 없고, 그럴 자격도 없는 분이에요.\" 간수가 대답했어."], ["‘He’s a deal pleasanter without his senses than with ‘em.", "\"정신이 있을 때보다 없을 때가 훨씬 나으시거든요."], ["He was the cruellest, wickedest, out-and-outerest old flint that ever drawed breath.’", "숨 쉬는 사람 중에 그렇게 잔인하고, 그렇게 사악하고, 그렇게 지독한 늙은 구두쇠는 다시 없을 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ said Kate.", "\"정말이요?\" 케이트가 말했어."]], [["‘By George!’ replied the keeper, shaking his head so emphatically that he was obliged to frown to keep his hat on.", "\"맙소사!\" 간수가 어찌나 힘주어 고개를 흔들었던지, 모자가 벗겨지지 않게 인상을 찌푸려야 할 정도였어."], ["‘I never come across such a vagabond, and my mate says the same.", "\"저런 망나니는 난생처음 봤어요. 제 동료도 똑같이 말하지요."], ["Broke his poor wife’s heart, turned his daughters out of doors, drove his sons into the streets; it was a blessing he went mad at last, through evil tempers, and covetousness, and selfishness, and guzzling, and drinking, or he’d have drove many others so.", "가엾은 아내는 속을 썩여 죽게 만들고, 딸들은 집에서 내쫓고, 아들들은 길거리로 내몰았지요. 그나마 끝에 가서 미쳐 버린 게 다행이었어요."], ["Hope for HIM, an old rip!", "못된 성질에, 탐욕에, 이기심에, 게걸스레 처먹고 퍼마신 끝에 그리됐으니까요. 안 그랬으면 다른 사람들까지 여럿 미치게 했을 겁니다."], ["There isn’t too much hope going, but I’ll bet a crown that what there is, is saved for more deserving chaps than him, anyhow.’", "저분한테 가망이라니요, 저런 늙은 망나니한테! 세상에 가망이란 게 그리 흔하지도 않은데, 그나마 있는 가망은 저런 자보다 더 자격 있는 친구들 몫으로 남겨 둘 거라고, 내 한 닢 걸고 장담합니다.\""]], [["With which confession of his faith, the keeper shook his head again, as much as to say that nothing short of this would do, if things were to go on at all; and touching his hat sulkily--not that he was in an ill humour, but that his subject ruffled him--descended the ladder, and took it away.", "이런 신앙 고백을 늘어놓고서 간수는 또 한 번 고개를 흔들었어. 마치 일이 제대로 굴러가려면 이쯤은 돼야 한다는 듯이 말이야. 그러고는 시무룩하게 모자에 손을 댔지— 기분이 나빠서가 아니라, 그 화제가 마음을 거슬리게 한 거였어— 그러더니 사다리를 내려가 그걸 치워 버렸어."]], [["During this conversation, Mrs. Nickleby had regarded the man with a severe and steadfast look.", "이 대화가 오가는 동안 니클비 부인은 그 사내를 매섭고도 한결같은 눈빛으로 바라보았어."], ["She now heaved a profound sigh, and pursing up her lips, shook her head in a slow and doubtful manner.", "이제 부인은 깊은 한숨을 내쉬고는 입술을 꼭 다물고서, 천천히 미심쩍게 고개를 절레절레 흔들었지."]], [["‘Poor creature!’ said Kate.", "\"가엾은 분!\" 케이트가 말했어."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["poor indeed!’ rejoined Mrs.", "정말 가엾지!\" 니클비 부인이 받았어."], ["Nickleby. ‘It’s shameful that such things should be allowed.", "\"저런 일이 허용된다는 게 부끄러운 노릇이야."], ["Shameful!’", "부끄러운 일이고말고!\""]], [["‘How can they be helped, mama?’ said Kate, mournfully.", "\"그걸 어떻게 어쩌겠어요, 엄마?\" 케이트가 서글피 말했어."], ["‘The infirmities of nature--’", "\"타고난 병약함이란—\""]], [["‘Nature!’ said Mrs.", "\"타고난 병약함이라니!\""], ["Nickleby. ‘What!", "니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Do YOU suppose this poor gentleman is out of his mind?’", "\"뭐라고! 너는 저 가엾은 신사가 정신이 나갔다고 생각하는 거니?\""]], [["‘Can anybody who sees him entertain any other opinion, mama?’", "\"저분을 본 사람이라면 누군들 달리 생각할 수 있겠어요, 엄마?\""]], [["‘Why then, I just tell you this, Kate,’ returned Mrs.", "\"그럼 내 너한테 딱 하나만 말해 주마, 케이트.\" 니클비 부인이 받았어."], ["Nickleby, ‘that, he is nothing of the kind, and I am surprised you can be so imposed upon.", "\"저분은 전혀 그런 게 아니야. 나는 네가 그렇게 속아 넘어갈 수 있다는 게 놀랍구나."], ["It’s some plot of these people to possess themselves of his property--didn’t he say so himself?", "저건 저 사람들이 저분 재산을 가로채려는 무슨 음모야— 저분이 직접 그렇게 말했잖니? 저분이 좀 별나고 들떠 있을지는 모르지."], ["He may be a little odd and flighty, perhaps, many of us are that; but downright mad!", "우리 중에도 그런 사람이 많잖니. 하지만 완전히 미쳤다니!"], ["and express himself as he does, respectfully, and in quite poetical language, and making offers with so much thought, and care, and prudence--not as if he ran into the streets, and went down upon his knees to the first chit of a girl he met, as a madman would!", "게다가 저렇게 정중하게, 그것도 아주 시적인 말씨로 자기 마음을 표현하고, 청혼도 저토록 신중하고 정성스럽고 사려 깊게 하시는데 말이야— 미친 사람이라면 길거리로 뛰쳐나가 처음 마주친 풋내기 계집애한테 무릎을 꿇었을 텐데, 저분은 그러지 않았잖니! 아니야, 아니야, 케이트."], ["No, no, Kate, there’s a great deal too much method in HIS madness; depend upon that, my dear.’", "저분의 광기에는 너무나 조리가 서 있어. 그건 믿어도 좋다, 얘야.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 42", "# CHAPTER 42"]], [["Illustrative of the convivial Sentiment, that the best of Friends must sometimes part\nThe pavement of Snow Hill had been baking and frying all day in the heat, and the twain Saracens’ heads guarding the entrance to the hostelry of whose name and sign they are the duplicate presentments, looked--or seemed, in the eyes of jaded and footsore passers-by, to look--more vicious than usual, after blistering and scorching in the sun, when, in one of the inn’s smallest sitting-rooms, through whose open window there rose, in a palpable steam, wholesome exhalations from reeking coach-horses, the usual furniture of a tea-table was displayed in neat and inviting order, flanked by large joints of roast and boiled, a tongue, a pigeon pie, a cold fowl, a tankard of ale, and other little matters of the like kind, which, in degenerate towns and cities, are generally understood to belong more particularly to solid lunches, stage-coach dinners, or unusually substantial breakfasts.", "스노 힐의 보도는 온종일 더위에 굽고 지져졌어. 그 여관 입구를 지키는 사라센 머리 둘은—여관 이름이자 간판이기도 한 그 머리를 똑같이 본떠 둔 거였지—햇볕에 부풀어 오르고 그슬린 탓에 평소보다 더 사납게 보였어. 적어도 지치고 발이 부르튼 행인들 눈에는 그렇게 보였지. 바로 그때, 여관에서 가장 작은 응접실 하나에서는—열린 창문으로 김을 내뿜는 마차 말들의 구수한 냄새가 손에 잡힐 듯 모락모락 피어올랐어—찻상에 차려진 흔한 살림살이가 깔끔하고 먹음직스럽게 놓여 있었어. 그 둘레로는 큼직한 구운 고기와 삶은 고기 덩이, 우설 한 점, 비둘기 파이, 차가운 닭 한 마리, 에일 한 잔, 그 밖에 자잘한 것들이 늘어서 있었지. 이런 음식들은 타락한 마을이나 도시에서는 보통 든든한 점심이나 역마차 저녁상, 아니면 유난히 푸짐한 아침상에나 어울린다고 여겨지는 것들이었어."]], [["Mr. John Browdie, with his hands in his pockets, hovered restlessly about these delicacies, stopping occasionally to whisk the flies out of the sugar-basin with his wife’s pocket-handkerchief, or to dip a teaspoon in the milk-pot and carry it to his mouth, or to cut off a little knob of crust, and a little corner of meat, and swallow them at two gulps like a couple of pills.", "존 브라우디 씨는 주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣은 채 이 진수성찬 둘레를 안절부절못하고 맴돌았어."], ["After every one of these flirtations with the eatables, he pulled out his watch, and declared with an earnestness quite pathetic that he couldn’t undertake to hold out two minutes longer.", "이따금 멈춰 서서 아내 손수건으로 설탕 그릇에 앉은 파리를 휙휙 쫓기도 하고, 찻숟가락을 우유 단지에 담갔다 입으로 가져가기도 하고, 빵 껍질을 조금 잘라 내고 고기 한 귀퉁이를 베어 알약 두 알 삼키듯 두 번에 꿀꺽 넘기기도 했지. 이렇게 음식과 한 번씩 추파를 주고받을 때마다, 그는 시계를 꺼내 들고는 도무지 이 분도 더는 못 참겠다며 자못 처량할 만큼 진지하게 선언했어."]], [["‘Tilly!’ said John to his lady, who was reclining half awake and half asleep upon a sofa.", "\"틸리!\" 존이 자기 부인을 불렀어. 부인은 소파에 비스듬히 기댄 채 반쯤 깨고 반쯤 졸고 있었지."]], [["‘Well, John!’", "\"왜요, 존!\""]], [["‘Well, John!’ retorted her husband, impatiently.", "\"왜요, 존이라니!\" 남편이 답답한 듯 받아쳤어."], ["‘Dost thou feel hoongry, lass?’", "\"여보, 시장하지 않아?\""]], [["‘Not very,’ said Mrs.", "\"별로요.\""], ["Browdie.", "브라우디 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘Not vary!’ repeated John, raising his eyes to the ceiling.", "\"별로라니!\" 존이 천장으로 눈을 치켜뜨며 되뇌었어."], ["‘Hear her say not vary, and us dining at three, and loonching off pasthry thot aggravates a mon ‘stead of pacifying him!", "\"세 시에 밥 먹고, 사람 달래기는커녕 약만 올리는 페이스트리로 점심을 때운 마당에 별로라니!"], ["Not vary!’", "별로라고!\""]], [["‘Here’s a gen’l’man for you, sir,’ said the waiter, looking in.", "\"손님 오셨습니다, 선생님.\" 웨이터가 안을 들여다보며 말했어."]], [["‘A wa’at for me?’ cried John, as though he thought it must be a letter, or a parcel.", "\"내, 뭐가 왔다고?\" 존이 마치 편지나 소포 얘긴 줄 안 것처럼 소리쳤어."]], [["‘A gen’l’man, sir.’", "\"손님이요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Stars and garthers, chap!’ said John, ‘wa’at dost thou coom and say thot for?", "\"이 친구야, 별 희한한 소릴 다 하네!\" 존이 말했어."], ["In wi’ ‘un.’", "\"와서 그게 무슨 소리야? 어서 모셔 와.\""]], [["‘Are you at home, sir?’", "\"안에 계십니까, 선생님?\""]], [["‘At whoam!’ cried John, ‘I wish I wur; I’d ha tea’d two hour ago.", "\"안에 있냐고!\" 존이 소리쳤어."], ["Why, I told t’oother chap to look sharp ootside door, and tell ‘un d’rectly he coom, thot we war faint wi’ hoonger.", "\"있고말고, 두 시간 전에 차라도 마셨으면 좋았을 걸! 아니, 내가 딴 친구한테 문밖에서 똑똑히 지키고 섰다가 손님 오시면 곧장 알리라 했잖아, 우리가 배고파 죽을 지경이라고 말이야."], ["In wi’ ‘un.", "어서 모셔 와."], ["Aha!", "아하!"], ["Thee hond, Misther Nickleby.", "손 좀 줘 보쇼, 니클비 씨."], ["This is nigh to be the proodest day o’ my life, sir.", "오늘은 내 평생에 둘도 없이 자랑스러운 날이오."], ["Hoo be all wi’ ye?", "다들 잘 지내셨소?"], ["Ding!", "어이쿠!"], ["But, I’m glod o’ this!’", "정말이지 기쁘구려!\""]], [["Quite forgetting even his hunger in the heartiness of his salutation, John Browdie shook Nicholas by the hand again and again, slapping his palm with great violence between each shake, to add warmth to the reception.", "존 브라우디는 인사가 어찌나 진심에서 우러났던지 배고픈 것도 까맣게 잊고, 니콜라스의 손을 잡고 몇 번이고 흔들어 댔어. 흔들 때마다 그 손바닥을 어찌나 세게 철썩철썩 치던지, 환영하는 마음에 온기를 더하려는 셈이었지."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["there she be,’ said John, observing the look which Nicholas directed towards his wife.", "저기 있구려.\" 존이, 니콜라스가 아내 쪽으로 보내는 눈길을 알아채고 말했어."], ["‘There she be--we shan’t quarrel about her noo--eh?", "\"저기 있다오—이제 저 사람 두고 우리가 다툴 일은 없겠지요—안 그렇소?"], ["Ecod, when I think o’ thot--but thou want’st soom’at to eat.", "야, 그때 일을 생각하면—아니, 그보다 뭐 좀 먹어야지."], ["Fall to, mun, fall to, and for wa’at we’re aboot to receive--’", "자, 들어요, 어서 들어. 그리고 우리가 받으려는 이 음식에 대하여—\""]], [["No doubt the grace was properly finished, but nothing more was heard, for John had already begun to play such a knife and fork, that his speech was, for the time, gone.", "식전 기도는 분명 제대로 끝까지 마쳤겠지만, 그 뒤로는 한마디도 들리지 않았어. 존이 벌써 칼과 포크를 어찌나 부지런히 놀려 대던지, 당분간은 말이 쑥 들어가 버렸거든."]], [["‘I shall take the usual licence, Mr.", "\"저는 늘 하던 대로 마음껏 굴겠습니다, 브라우디 씨.\""], ["Browdie,’ said Nicholas, as he placed a chair for the bride.", "니콜라스가 신부를 위해 의자를 놓아 주며 말했어."]], [["‘Tak’ whatever thou like’st,’ said John, ‘and when a’s gane, ca’ for more.’", "\"좋을 대로 하쇼.\" 존이 말했어. \"다 떨어지면 더 시키고.\""]], [["Without stopping to explain, Nicholas kissed the blushing Mrs. Browdie, and handed her to her seat.", "니콜라스는 굳이 설명할 것도 없이 얼굴이 발그레해진 브라우디 부인에게 입을 맞추고는 자리로 안내했어."]], [["‘I say,’ said John, rather astounded for the moment, ‘mak’ theeself quite at whoam, will ‘ee?’", "\"이런,\" 존이 잠깐 어리둥절해서 말했어. \"아주 제 집처럼 편하게 굴구려, 응?\""]], [["‘You may depend upon that,’ replied Nicholas; ‘on one condition.’", "\"그건 믿어도 좋습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"단, 한 가지 조건이 있어요.\""]], [["‘And wa’at may thot be?’ asked John.", "\"그게 뭔데요?\" 존이 물었어."]], [["‘That you make me a godfather the very first time you have occasion for one.’", "\"대부가 필요할 일이 처음 생기면, 꼭 저를 대부로 삼아 주셔야 합니다.\""]], [["‘Eh!", "\"어!"], ["d’ye hear thot?’ cried John, laying down his knife and fork.", "저 소리 들었소?\" 존이 칼과 포크를 내려놓으며 외쳤어."], ["‘A godfeyther!", "\"대부라고!"], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["Tilly--hear till ‘un--a godfeyther!", "틸리—저 양반 말 좀 들어 봐—대부라네!"], ["Divn’t say a word more, ye’ll never beat thot.", "더는 한마디도 마쇼, 그건 도저히 못 당하겠소."], ["Occasion for ‘un--a godfeyther!", "대부가 필요할 일이라니—대부라네!"], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["ha!’", "하!\""]], [["Never was man so tickled with a respectable old joke, as John Browdie was with this.", "점잖은 옛날 농담에 존 브라우디만큼 자지러지게 웃은 사람은 없을 거야. 그는 킥킥대다 와하하 터뜨리다, 큼직한 소고기 덩이를 웃으면서 들이마셔 숨통이 반쯤 막혔다가, 다시 웃어 젖히고, 그러면서도 기어이 먹기를 계속하고, 얼굴이 벌게지고 이마는 새카매지고, 기침을 하고, 눈물을 흘리고, 좀 나아졌다가, 속으로 또 웃음이 도지고, 더 심해지고, 목이 메어, 등을 두들겨 맞고, 발을 동동 구르고, 아내를 깜짝 놀라게 하고, 마침내 기진맥진한 끝에 눈에서 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 진정했어."], ["He chuckled, roared, half suffocated himself by laughing large pieces of beef into his windpipe, roared again, persisted in eating at the same time, got red in the face and black in the forehead, coughed, cried, got better, went off again laughing inwardly, got worse, choked, had his back thumped, stamped about, frightened his wife, and at last recovered in a state of the last exhaustion and with the water streaming from his eyes, but still faintly ejaculating, ‘A godfeyther--a godfeyther, Tilly!’ in a tone bespeaking an exquisite relish of the sally, which no suffering could diminish.", "그러면서도 여전히 가냘프게 \"대부라네—대부라니, 틸리!\" 하고 내뱉었는데, 그 어떤 고통으로도 줄어들지 않을 만큼 그 농담을 절묘하게 음미하는 말투였지."]], [["‘You remember the night of our first tea-drinking?’ said Nicholas.", "\"우리가 처음 차를 마시던 그날 밤 기억하시죠?\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Shall I e’er forget it, mun?’ replied John Browdie.", "\"내가 그걸 잊을 리가 있겠소?\" 존 브라우디가 대답했어."]], [["‘He was a desperate fellow that night though, was he not, Mrs.", "\"그날 밤 이분은 정말 무시무시한 사람이었지요, 안 그래요, 브라우디 부인?\""], ["Browdie?’ said Nicholas.", "니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘Quite a monster!’", "\"완전히 괴물이었어요!\""]], [["‘If you had only heard him as we were going home, Mr. Nickleby, you’d have said so indeed,’ returned the bride.", "\"집에 돌아가는 길에 이 사람이 하는 말을 들으셨으면, 정말 그렇게 말씀하셨을 거예요, 니클비 씨.\""], ["‘I never was so frightened in all my life.’", "신부가 대답했어. \"평생 그렇게 무서웠던 적이 없다니까요.\""]], [["‘Coom, coom,’ said John, with a broad grin; ‘thou know’st betther than thot, Tilly.’", "\"아니, 그건 말이지,\" 존이 입이 함박만 하게 벌어지도록 씩 웃으며 말했어. \"그게 아니란 걸 너도 잘 알잖아, 틸리.\""]], [["‘So I was,’ replied Mrs.", "\"무서웠다니까요.\" 브라우디 부인이 대꾸했어."], ["Browdie. ‘I almost made up my mind never to speak to you again.’", "\"다시는 당신하고 말도 안 섞겠다고 마음을 거의 굳혔다고요.\""]], [["‘A’most!’ said John, with a broader grin than the last.", "\"거의라!\" 존이 아까보다 더 입을 벌리고 웃으며 말했어."], ["‘A’most made up her mind!", "\"마음을 거의 굳혔다고!"], ["And she wur coaxin’, and coaxin’, and wheedlin’, and wheedlin’ a’ the blessed wa’.", "그래 놓고 오는 내내 어르고 또 어르고, 살살 달래고 또 달래고 그랬지."], ["“Wa’at didst thou let yon chap mak’ oop tiv’ee for?”", "'아니 너는 어쩌자고 저 친구가 너한테 수작 걸게 놔뒀냐?'"], ["says I. “I deedn’t, John,”", "하고 내가 물었더니, '안 그랬어요, 존'"], ["says she, a squeedgin my arm.", "하고 내 팔을 꼭 쥐더라고."], ["“You deedn’t?”", "'안 그랬다고?'"], ["says I. “Noa,”", "하니까, '네'"], ["says she, a squeedgin of me agean.’", "하면서 또 한 번 꼭 쥐었지.\""]], [["‘Lor, John!’ interposed his pretty wife, colouring very much.", "\"어머나, 존!\" 예쁜 아내가 얼굴이 새빨개져서 끼어들었어."], ["‘How can you talk such nonsense?", "\"어쩜 그런 헛소릴 다 해요?"], ["As if I should have dreamt of such a thing!’", "내가 그런 걸 꿈엔들 생각했겠어요!\""]], [["‘I dinnot know whether thou’d ever dreamt of it, though I think that’s loike eneaf, mind,’ retorted John; ‘but thou didst it.", "\"네가 그런 꿈을 꿨는지 어쨌는지야 내가 알 수 없지, 하긴 그럴 법도 하다 싶다만.\" 존이 받아쳤어."], ["“Ye’re a feeckle, changeable weathercock, lass,”", "\"어쨌든 네가 그랬잖아. '너는 변덕스럽고 잘도 휙휙 도는 풍향계 같은 계집애야'"], ["says I. “Not feeckle, John,”", "하니까, '변덕 아니에요, 존'"], ["says she.", "하더군."], ["“Yes,”", "'맞아'"], ["says I, “feeckle, dom’d feeckle.", "하고 내가 말했지."], ["Dinnot tell me thou bean’t, efther yon chap at schoolmeasther’s,”", "'변덕이야, 지독한 변덕이지. 학교 선생네 그 친구 두고 변덕 아니라곤 못할걸'"], ["says I. “Him!”", "하니까, '그 사람이요!'"], ["says she, quite screeching. “Ah!", "하고 막 째지게 소릴 지르더라고."], ["him!”", "'아!"], ["says I. “Why, John,”", "그 사람!'"], ["says she--and she coom a deal closer and squeedged a deal harder than she’d deane afore--“dost thou think it’s nat’ral noo, that having such a proper mun as thou to keep company wi’, I’d ever tak’ opp wi’ such a leetle scanty whipper-snapper as yon?”", "했더니, '아니, 존' 하면서—전보다 한참 더 바짝 다가와 더 세게 꼭 쥐고는—'당신 같은 번듯한 사람을 곁에 두고서 내가 저런 비실비실하고 빼빼 마른 애송이한테 넘어가는 게 말이 된다고 생각해요?'"], ["she says. Ha!", "하더라니까."], ["ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["She said whipper-snapper!", "하! 애송이라잖아!"], ["“Ecod!”", "'아이고!'"], ["I says, “efther thot, neame the day, and let’s have it ower!”", "하고 내가 말했지. '그렇다면 날을 잡아서 후딱 해치워 버리자!'"], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["ha!’", "하!\""]], [["Nicholas laughed very heartily at this story, both on account of its telling against himself, and his being desirous to spare the blushes of Mrs.", "니콜라스는 이 이야기에 진심으로 크게 웃었어. 그게 제 흉을 보는 얘기이기도 했고, 또 브라우디 부인의 얼굴 붉어짐을 덜어 주고 싶기도 했거든."], ["Browdie, whose protestations were drowned in peals of laughter from her husband.", "부인의 항변은 남편의 폭소에 묻혀 버렸지."], ["His good-nature soon put her at her ease; and although she still denied the charge, she laughed so heartily at it, that Nicholas had the satisfaction of feeling assured that in all essential respects it was strictly true.", "존의 좋은 성품 덕에 부인은 곧 마음이 편해졌어. 그리고 여전히 그 얘기를 아니라고 부인하면서도 어찌나 신나게 웃던지, 니콜라스는 그 얘기가 본질적으로는 한 치도 틀림없는 사실이라고 확신하고 흐뭇해졌지."]], [["‘This is the second time,’ said Nicholas, ‘that we have ever taken a meal together, and only third I have ever seen you; and yet it really seems to me as if I were among old friends.’", "\"우리가 함께 식사하는 건 이번이 두 번째군요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그리고 제가 두 분을 본 건 겨우 세 번째인데도, 정말이지 오랜 친구들 틈에 있는 것만 같습니다.\""]], [["‘Weel!’ observed the Yorkshireman, ‘so I say.’", "\"그렇소!\" 요크셔 사나이가 말했어. \"내 말이 그 말이오.\""]], [["‘And I am sure I do,’ added his young wife.", "\"저도 정말 그래요.\" 젊은 아내가 덧붙였어."]], [["‘I have the best reason to be impressed with the feeling, mind,’ said Nicholas; ‘for if it had not been for your kindness of heart, my good friend, when I had no right or reason to expect it, I know not what might have become of me or what plight I should have been in by this time.’", "\"저야 누구보다 그렇게 느낄 까닭이 충분하지요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"제가 그런 걸 바라거나 기대할 처지가 전혀 아니었는데도, 좋은 친구분의 그 따뜻한 마음씨가 없었더라면, 제가 어찌 됐을지, 지금쯤 어떤 신세가 됐을지 모를 일이니까요.\""]], [["‘Talk aboot soom’at else,’ replied John, gruffly, ‘and dinnot bother.’", "\"딴 얘길 하쇼.\" 존이 퉁명스럽게 대꾸했어. \"그런 걸로 사람 귀찮게 하지 말고.\""]], [["‘It must be a new song to the same tune then,’ said Nicholas, smiling. ‘I told you in my letter that I deeply felt and admired your sympathy with that poor lad, whom you released at the risk of involving yourself in trouble and difficulty; but I can never tell you how grateful he and I, and others whom you don’t know, are to you for taking pity on him.’", "\"그럼 같은 곡조에 가사만 새로 붙여 부르지요.\" 니콜라스가 미소 지으며 말했어. \"편지에도 적었습니다만, 그 가엾은 소년을 향한 당신의 따뜻한 마음에 저는 깊이 감동했고 감탄했습니다. 당신 자신이 곤경과 어려움에 휘말릴 위험을 무릅쓰고 그 애를 풀어 주셨으니까요. 하지만 그 애와 제가, 또 당신이 알지 못하는 다른 사람들까지, 그 애를 가엾이 여겨 주신 당신께 얼마나 고마워하는지는 도저히 다 말로 못 합니다.\""]], [["‘Ecod!’ rejoined John Browdie, drawing up his chair; ‘and I can never tell YOU hoo gratful soom folks that we do know would be loikewise, if THEY know’d I had takken pity on him.’", "\"아이고!\" 존 브라우디가 의자를 바짝 당기며 받았어. \"그리고 나도 당신한테 도저히 다 말 못 하겠소. 우리가 아는 어떤 사람들도, 내가 그 애를 가엾이 여겨 줬다는 걸 알면 똑같이 고마워할 거란 걸 말이오.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"아!\" 브라우디 부인이 탄성을 질렀어."], ["Browdie, ‘what a state I was in that night!’", "\"그날 밤 내가 어떤 꼴이었는데요!\""]], [["‘Were they at all disposed to give you credit for assisting in the escape?’ inquired Nicholas of John Browdie.", "\"그자들은 당신이 그 애 도망을 거든 걸 두고 의심이라도 했나요?\" 니콜라스가 존 브라우디에게 물었어."]], [["‘Not a bit,’ replied the Yorkshireman, extending his mouth from ear to ear.", "\"전혀.\" 요크셔 사나이가 입을 귀밑까지 쭉 벌리며 대답했어."], ["‘There I lay, snoog in schoolmeasther’s bed long efther it was dark, and nobody coom nigh the pleace.", "\"날이 어두워진 뒤로도 한참, 나는 선생네 침대에 떡하니 누워 있었는데, 아무도 가까이 오질 않더라고. '음!'"], ["“Weel!”", "하고 나는 생각했지. '저 녀석 출발 한번 그럴듯하게 했군."], ["thinks I, “he’s got a pretty good start, and if he bean’t whoam by noo, he never will be; so you may coom as quick as you loike, and foind us reddy”--that is, you know, schoolmeasther might coom.’", "지금쯤 집에 못 들어갔으면 영영 못 들어갈 테지. 그러니 올 테면 빨리들 와 보라지, 다 준비됐으니까'—그러니까, 알겠소만, 선생이 올지도 모른다는 얘기였지.\""]], [["‘I understand,’ said Nicholas.", "\"알겠습니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Presently,’ resumed John, ‘he DID coom.", "\"이윽고,\" 존이 말을 이었어."], ["I heerd door shut doonstairs, and him a warking, oop in the daark.", "\"선생이 정말 왔지. 아래층에서 문 닫히는 소리가 들리고, 그 양반이 어둠 속을 더듬더듬 올라오더군."], ["“Slow and steddy,”", "'천천히, 차분하게.'"], ["I says to myself, “tak’ your time, sir--no hurry.”", "나는 속으로 말했지. '느긋하게 하쇼, 선생—서두를 거 없소.'"], ["He cooms to the door, turns the key--turns the key when there warn’t nothing to hoold the lock--and ca’s oot “Hallo, there!”--“Yes,”", "그가 문 앞에 와서 열쇠를 돌리더군—자물쇠를 채울 것도 없는데 열쇠를 돌리더니—'어이, 거기!' 하고 소리쳤어."], ["thinks I, “you may do thot agean, and not wakken anybody, sir.”", "'그래' 하고 나는 생각했지."], ["“Hallo, there,” he says, and then he stops.", "'몇 번을 더 그래 봐야 아무도 안 깰 거요, 선생.'"], ["“Thou’d betther not aggravate me,”", "'어이, 거기' 하고 그가 또 부르고는 뚝 멈추더군."], ["says schoolmeasther, efther a little time.", "'날 약 올리지 않는 게 좋을 거다' 하고 잠시 뒤에 선생이 말했지."], ["“I’ll brak’ every boan in your boddy, Smike,”", "'뼈마디를 죄다 부러뜨려 놓겠다, 스마이크'"], ["he says, efther another little time.", "하고 또 한참 뒤에 말하더라고."], ["Then all of a soodden, he sings oot for a loight, and when it cooms--ecod, such a hoorly-boorly!", "그러더니 갑자기 불을 가져오라고 빽 소리를 질렀어. 불이 오니까—야, 그 난리 법석이라니!"], ["“Wa’at’s the matter?”", "'무슨 일이오?' 하고 내가 물었지."], ["says I. “He’s gane,”", "'그 녀석이 도망쳤어'"], ["says he,--stark mad wi’ vengeance.", "하더군—복수심에 완전히 미쳐 날뛰면서."], ["“Have you heerd nought?”", "'아무 소리도 못 들었나?'"], ["“Ees,”", "'들었소'"], ["says I, “I heerd street-door shut, no time at a’ ago.", "하고 내가 말했지. '바로 조금 전에 길 쪽 문이 닫히는 소리가 났소."], ["I heerd a person run doon there”", "누가 저쪽으로 뛰어가는 소리도 들었고요'(딴 쪽을 가리키면서 말이야—응?)."], ["(pointing t’other wa’--eh?) “Help!”", "'사람 살려!' 하고 그가 외쳤어."], ["he cries.", "'내가 도와주겠소'"], ["“I’ll help you,”", "하고 내가 말했지."], ["says I; and off we set--the wrong wa’!", "그래서 우리는 출발했어—엉뚱한 길로!"], ["Ho!", "호!"], ["ho!", "호!"], ["ho!’", "호!\""]], [["‘Did you go far?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"멀리까지 갔습니까?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘Far!’ replied John; ‘I run him clean off his legs in quarther of an hoor.", "\"멀리!\" 존이 대답했어."], ["To see old schoolmeasther wi’out his hat, skimming along oop to his knees in mud and wather, tumbling over fences, and rowling into ditches, and bawling oot like mad, wi’ his one eye looking sharp out for the lad, and his coat-tails flying out behind, and him spattered wi’ mud all ower, face and all!", "\"십오 분 만에 그자 다리가 풀려 자빠지게 만들어 놨지. 모자도 안 쓴 선생이, 무릎까지 푹푹 빠지는 진흙탕과 물구덩이를 헤치며 휙휙 달리고, 울타리를 넘다 고꾸라지고, 도랑에 처박히고, 미친 사람처럼 고래고래 소리치고, 외눈으로는 그 녀석을 매섭게 찾으려 두리번거리고, 외투 자락은 뒤로 펄럭이고, 얼굴이고 어디고 온통 진흙투성이가 된 꼴이라니!"], ["I tho’t I should ha’ dropped doon, and killed myself wi’ laughing.’", "나는 그만 푹 쓰러져 웃다가 죽는 줄 알았소.\""]], [["John laughed so heartily at the mere recollection, that he communicated the contagion to both his hearers, and all three burst into peals of laughter, which were renewed again and again, until they could laugh no longer.", "존이 그저 떠올리기만 해도 어찌나 진심으로 웃어 대던지, 듣던 두 사람한테까지 그 웃음이 옮아서, 셋이 다 폭소를 터뜨렸어. 그 웃음은 몇 번이고 되살아나, 마침내 더는 웃을 수 없을 때까지 이어졌지."]], [["‘He’s a bad ‘un,’ said John, wiping his eyes; ‘a very bad ‘un, is schoolmeasther.’", "\"그자는 못된 인간이오.\" 존이 눈물을 닦으며 말했어. \"아주 못된 인간이지, 그 선생은.\""]], [["‘I can’t bear the sight of him, John,’ said his wife.", "\"나는 그 사람 꼴도 보기 싫어요, 존.\" 아내가 말했어."]], [["‘Coom,’ retorted John, ‘thot’s tidy in you, thot is.", "\"이런,\" 존이 받아쳤어."], ["If it wa’nt along o’ you, we shouldn’t know nought aboot ‘un.", "\"그런 소리 잘도 하네, 그래. 너 아니었으면 우리가 그자를 알 일도 없었잖아."], ["Thou know’d ‘un first, Tilly, didn’t thou?’", "네가 먼저 알았던 거 아니야, 틸리?\""]], [["‘I couldn’t help knowing Fanny Squeers, John,’ returned his wife; ‘she was an old playmate of mine, you know.’", "\"패니 스퀴어스를 안 알 수가 있어야지요, 존.\" 아내가 대답했어. \"걔는 내 옛날 소꿉친구였잖아요.\""]], [["‘Weel,’ replied John, ‘dean’t I say so, lass?", "\"음,\" 존이 대답했어."], ["It’s best to be neighbourly, and keep up old acquaintance loike; and what I say is, dean’t quarrel if ‘ee can help it.", "\"내 말이 그 말 아니냐? 이웃끼리 사이좋게 지내고 옛 정분을 이어 가는 게 좋은 거지."], ["Dinnot think so, Mr.", "그리고 내가 늘 하는 말이, 웬만하면 다투지 말자는 거야."], ["Nickleby?’", "안 그렇소, 니클비 씨?\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ returned Nicholas; ‘and you acted upon that principle when I meet you on horseback on the road, after our memorable evening.’", "\"그럼요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"그리고 그 잊지 못할 저녁 뒤에 길에서 말 타고 가시던 당신을 만났을 때, 당신은 바로 그 원칙대로 행동하셨지요.\""]], [["‘Sure-ly,’ said John.", "\"암, 그렇소.\" 존이 말했어."], ["‘Wa’at I say, I stick by.’", "\"내가 한번 한 말은 끝까지 지키지요.\""]], [["‘And that’s a fine thing to do, and manly too,’ said Nicholas, ‘though it’s not exactly what we understand by “coming Yorkshire over us”", "\"그건 훌륭한 일이고, 사내답기도 합니다.\""], ["in London.", "니콜라스가 말했어."], ["Miss Squeers is stopping with you, you said in your note.’", "\"비록 런던에서 '요크셔 식으로 사람을 속여먹는다'고 할 때 떠올리는 그런 건 아니지만요. 쪽지에 보니 스퀴어스 양이 댁에 와 있다고 하셨던데요.\""]], [["‘Yes,’ replied John, ‘Tilly’s bridesmaid; and a queer bridesmaid she be, too. She wean’t be a bride in a hurry, I reckon.’", "\"그렇소.\" 존이 대답했어. \"틸리의 들러리지요. 그것도 별난 들러리고. 그 처녀, 신부가 되긴 한참 멀었을걸요, 내 짐작엔.\""]], [["‘For shame, John,’ said Mrs.", "\"부끄러운 줄 아세요, 존.\""], ["Browdie; with an acute perception of the joke though, being a bride herself.", "브라우디 부인이 말했어. 자기도 신부면서 그 농담의 뜻만은 예리하게 알아채고서 말이야."]], [["‘The groom will be a blessed mun,’ said John, his eyes twinkling at the idea.", "\"그 신랑은 복받은 사내일 거요.\" 존이 그 생각에 눈을 반짝이며 말했어."], ["‘He’ll be in luck, he will.’", "\"운수 대통한 거지, 그자는.\""]], [["‘You see, Mr.", "\"있잖아요, 니클비 씨.\" 아내가 말했어."], ["Nickleby,’ said his wife, ‘that it was in consequence of her being here, that John wrote to you and fixed tonight, because we thought that it wouldn’t be pleasant for you to meet, after what has passed.’", "\"그 애가 여기 와 있는 까닭에, 존이 당신께 편지를 써서 오늘 밤으로 약속을 잡은 거예요. 지난 일이 있고 나서 두 분이 마주치는 게 유쾌하진 않을 거라고 생각했거든요.\""]], [["‘Unquestionably.", "\"두말할 것 없지요."], ["You were quite right in that,’ said Nicholas, interrupting.", "잘 생각하셨습니다.\" 니콜라스가 끼어들며 말했어."]], [["‘Especially,’ observed Mrs.", "\"특히,\" 브라우디 부인이 자못 짓궂은 표정으로 말했어."], ["Browdie, looking very sly, ‘after what we know about past and gone love matters.’", "\"지난날 다 끝나 버린 연애 사건에 관해 우리가 아는 걸 생각하면 더더욱 그렇죠.\""]], [["‘We know, indeed!’ said Nicholas, shaking his head.", "\"우리가 알다마다요!\" 니콜라스가 고개를 저으며 말했어."], ["‘You behaved rather wickedly there, I suspect.’", "\"그때 당신이 좀 짓궂게 굴었던 것 같던데요.\""]], [["‘O’ course she did,’ said John Browdie, passing his huge forefinger through one of his wife’s pretty ringlets, and looking very proud of her.", "\"그야 당연하지요.\" 존 브라우디가 큼직한 집게손가락으로 아내의 예쁜 곱슬머리 한 가닥을 쓸어 넘기며, 아내가 무척 자랑스럽다는 듯이 말했어."], ["‘She wur always as skittish and full o’ tricks as a--’", "\"이 사람은 늘 어찌나 까불고 장난기가 넘치던지, 꼭—\""]], [["‘Well, as a what?’ said his wife.", "\"그래, 꼭 뭐 같은데요?\" 아내가 물었어."]], [["‘As a woman,’ returned John.", "\"꼭 여자 같았지.\" 존이 대답했어."], ["‘Ding!", "\"야!"], ["But I dinnot know ought else that cooms near it.’", "그거 말고는 달리 비길 만한 게 없어서 말이야.\""]], [["‘You were speaking about Miss Squeers,’ said Nicholas, with the view of stopping some slight connubialities which had begun to pass between Mr and Mrs.", "\"스퀴어스 양 얘기를 하고 계셨지요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["Browdie, and which rendered the position of a third party in some degree embarrassing, as occasioning him to feel rather in the way than otherwise.", "브라우디 부부 사이에 막 오가기 시작한 자잘한 부부간 정담을 멈추려는 뜻이었지. 그 정담 탓에 곁에 끼인 제삼자 처지가 좀 난처해졌고, 그 자리에 있는 게 거추장스럽게 느껴졌거든."]], [["‘Oh yes,’ rejoined Mrs.", "\"아 참, 그래요.\""], ["Browdie. ‘John ha’ done.", "브라우디 부인이 대답했어."], ["John fixed tonight, because she had settled that she would go and drink tea with her father.", "\"존이 다 했어요. 존이 오늘 밤으로 잡은 건, 그 애가 아버지한테 가서 차를 마시겠다고 정해 놨기 때문이에요."], ["And to make quite sure of there being nothing amiss, and of your being quite alone with us, he settled to go out there and fetch her home.’", "그리고 아무 탈도 없도록, 또 당신이 우리하고만 오붓하게 있도록 확실히 하려고, 존이 직접 거기 가서 그 애를 데려오기로 한 거예요.\""]], [["‘That was a very good arrangement,’ said Nicholas, ‘though I am sorry to be the occasion of so much trouble.’", "\"아주 잘 짜셨네요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그래도 저 때문에 이렇게 폐를 끼치게 돼서 죄송합니다.\""]], [["‘Not the least in the world,’ returned Mrs.", "\"천만에요, 조금도요.\" 브라우디 부인이 대답했어."], ["Browdie; ‘for we have looked forward to see you--John and I have--with the greatest possible pleasure.", "\"저희는 당신을 뵐 날을—존이랑 저랑—더없이 큰 기쁨으로 손꼽아 기다렸는걸요."], ["Do you know, Mr. Nickleby,’ said Mrs.", "그런데 있잖아요, 니클비 씨,\""], ["Browdie, with her archest smile, ‘that I really think Fanny Squeers was very fond of you?’", "브라우디 부인이 더없이 짓궂은 미소를 지으며 말했어. \"저는 정말이지 패니 스퀴어스가 당신을 무척 좋아했다고 생각해요.\""]], [["‘I am very much obliged to her,’ said Nicholas; ‘but upon my word, I never aspired to making any impression upon her virgin heart.’", "\"고마운 일이군요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"하지만 맹세코, 저는 그 처녀의 순결한 마음에 무슨 인상이라도 남기겠다고 바란 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["‘How you talk!’ tittered Mrs. Browdie.", "\"별말씀을 다 하시네!\""], ["‘No, but do you know that really--seriously now and without any joking--I was given to understand by Fanny herself, that you had made an offer to her, and that you two were going to be engaged quite solemn and regular.’", "브라우디 부인이 키득거렸어. \"아니, 그런데 정말로 있잖아요—이제 농담 다 빼고 진지하게요—제가 패니한테 직접 들은 바로는, 당신이 그 애한테 청혼을 했고, 두 사람이 아주 엄숙하고 정식으로 약혼을 하기로 했다지 뭐예요.\""]], [["‘Was you, ma’am--was you?’ cried a shrill female voice, ‘was you given to understand that I--I--was going to be engaged to an assassinating thief that shed the gore of my pa?", "\"당신이—당신이 그렇게 들었다고요?\" 날카로운 여자 목소리가 외쳤어."], ["Do you--do you think, ma’am--that I was very fond of such dirt beneath my feet, as I couldn’t condescend to touch with kitchen tongs, without blacking and crocking myself by the contract?", "\"내가—이 내가—우리 아빠 피를 흘리게 한 살인마 도둑놈하고 약혼하려 했다는 말을 당신이 들었다고요? 당신은—당신은 정말로 생각하나요, 부인—내가 발밑의 흙처럼 천한 그자를, 약속이라도 했다간 손에 시커멓게 검댕만 묻을 그자를, 부엌 집게로도 차마 건드리기 싫은 그자를 무척 좋아했다고요?"], ["Do you, ma’am--do you?", "그래요, 부인—정말 그래요?"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["base and degrading ‘Tilda!’", "비열하고 천박한 틸다!\""]], [["With these reproaches Miss Squeers flung the door wide open, and disclosed to the eyes of the astonished Browdies and Nicholas, not only her own symmetrical form, arrayed in the chaste white garments before described (a little dirtier), but the form of her brother and father, the pair of Wackfords.", "이렇게 비난을 퍼부으며 스퀴어스 양이 문을 활짝 열어젖혔어. 그러고는 깜짝 놀란 브라우디 부부와 니콜라스의 눈앞에, 앞서 말한 그 순결한 흰옷(조금 더 더러워진)을 차려입은 자신의 균형 잡힌 자태뿐 아니라, 오라비와 아버지, 즉 워크퍼드 부자(父子)의 모습까지 드러내 보였지."]], [["‘This is the hend, is it?’ continued Miss Squeers, who, being excited, aspirated her h’s strongly; ‘this is the hend, is it, of all my forbearance and friendship for that double-faced thing--that viper, that--that--mermaid?’ (Miss Squeers hesitated a long time for this last epithet, and brought it out triumphantly at last, as if it quite clinched the business.) ‘This is the hend, is it, of all my bearing with her deceitfulness, her lowness, her falseness, her laying herself out to catch the admiration of vulgar minds, in a way which made me blush for my--for my--’", "\"이게 결말인가요, 응?\" 스퀴어스 양이 말을 이었어. 어찌나 흥분했던지 'ㅎ' 발음에 잔뜩 힘이 들어갔지. \"이게 결말인가요, 응? 그 두 얼굴 가진 것한테—그 독사한테—그 무슨—무슨—인어한테 내가 베푼 온갖 참을성과 우정의 결말이? (스퀴어스 양은 이 마지막 욕설을 한참 고르고 고르더니, 마침내 이게 모든 걸 못 박는다는 듯 의기양양하게 내뱉었어.) 이게 결말인가요, 응? 그 애의 속임수, 그 천박함, 그 거짓됨을, 또 천박한 마음들의 찬사나 낚으려고 갖은 교태를 부리는 그 꼴을, 내가—내 무엇을 위해 부끄러워 얼굴이 붉어지도록 참아 준 결말이?\""]], [["‘Gender,’ suggested Mr.", "\"성별이지.\""], ["Squeers, regarding the spectators with a malevolent eye--literally A malevolent eye.", "스퀴어스 씨가 구경꾼들을 악의에 찬 눈으로—말 그대로 한쪽 눈으로—노려보며 거들었어."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Miss Squeers; ‘but I thank my stars that my ma is of the same--’", "\"그래요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 말했어. \"하지만 우리 엄마도 같은—이라는 게 천만다행이죠—\""]], [["‘Hear, hear!’ remarked Mr.", "\"옳소, 옳소!\" 스퀴어스 씨가 한마디 했어."], ["Squeers; ‘and I wish she was here to have a scratch at this company.’", "\"그 사람이 여기 와서 이 작자들을 한바탕 할퀴어 줬으면 좋겠구나.\""]], [["‘This is the hend, is it,’ said Miss Squeers, tossing her head, and looking contemptuously at the floor, ‘of my taking notice of that rubbishing creature, and demeaning myself to patronise her?’", "\"이게 결말인가요, 응?\" 스퀴어스 양이 고개를 홱 젖히고 바닥을 경멸하듯 내려다보며 말했어. \"내가 저 쓰레기 같은 것한테 관심을 베풀고, 내 몸을 낮춰 가며 돌봐 준 결말이?\""]], [["‘Oh, come,’ rejoined Mrs.", "\"아이참, 그만해.\""], ["Browdie, disregarding all the endeavours of her spouse to restrain her, and forcing herself into a front row, ‘don’t talk such nonsense as that.’", "브라우디 부인이 자기를 말리려는 남편의 온갖 시도를 무시하고 맨 앞줄로 밀고 나서며 받아쳤어. \"그런 말도 안 되는 소리 좀 그만해.\""]], [["‘Have I not patronised you, ma’am?’ demanded Miss Squeers.", "\"내가 너를 돌봐 주지 않았던가, 응?\" 스퀴어스 양이 따졌어."]], [["‘No,’ returned Mrs.", "\"아니.\""], ["Browdie.", "브라우디 부인이 대답했어."]], [["‘I will not look for blushes in such a quarter,’ said Miss Squeers, haughtily, ‘for that countenance is a stranger to everything but hignominiousness and red-faced boldness.’", "\"저런 데서 부끄러워 붉어지는 낯빛 따위는 바라지도 않겠어.\" 스퀴어스 양이 거만하게 말했어. \"저 얼굴은 수치심이랑 뻔뻔한 철면피 말고는 아무것도 모르니까.\""]], [["‘I say,’ interposed John Browdie, nettled by these accumulated attacks on his wife, ‘dra’ it mild, dra’ it mild.’", "\"이봐요.\" 아내한테 쏟아지는 이 거듭된 공격에 약이 오른 존 브라우디가 끼어들었어. \"좀 점잖게 하쇼, 점잖게.\""]], [["‘You, Mr.", "\"당신은요, 브라우디 씨.\""], ["Browdie,’ said Miss Squeers, taking him up very quickly, ‘I pity.", "스퀴어스 양이 그의 말을 잽싸게 받았어. \"딱하게 여길 뿐이에요."], ["I have no feeling for you, sir, but one of unliquidated pity.’", "당신한테는 끝없는 연민 말고는 아무 감정도 없어요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ said John.", "\"오!\" 존이 말했어."]], [["‘No,’ said Miss Squeers, looking sideways at her parent, ‘although I AM a queer bridesmaid, and SHAN’T be a bride in a hurry, and although my husband WILL be in luck, I entertain no sentiments towards you, sir, but sentiments of pity.’", "\"네.\" 스퀴어스 양이 제 아비를 곁눈질하며 말했어. \"비록 나는 별난 들러리고, 신부가 되긴 한참 멀었고, 또 내 남편 될 사람은 운수 대통할 거라지만요, 선생님께 품은 감정은 연민의 감정뿐이에요.\""]], [["Here Miss Squeers looked sideways at her father again, who looked sideways at her, as much as to say, ‘There you had him.’", "이러면서 스퀴어스 양은 다시 아버지를 곁눈질했어. 아버지도 그녀를 곁눈질했지. 마치 '그래, 한 방 제대로 먹였다'는 투로 말이야."]], [["‘I know what you’ve got to go through,’ said Miss Squeers, shaking her curls violently.", "\"당신이 앞으로 어떤 일을 겪어야 할지 나는 알아요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 곱슬머리를 사납게 흔들며 말했어."], ["‘I know what life is before you, and if you was my bitterest and deadliest enemy, I could wish you nothing worse.’", "\"당신 앞에 어떤 삶이 펼쳐질지 안다고요. 당신이 내 철천지원수라 해도, 그보다 더 나쁜 일을 빌어 줄 순 없을 거예요.\""]], [["‘Couldn’t you wish to be married to him yourself, if that was the case?’ inquired Mrs.", "\"그렇담 자기가 직접 저 사람하고 결혼하고 싶진 않아?\""], ["Browdie, with great suavity of manner.", "브라우디 부인이 더없이 상냥한 말투로 물었어."]], [["‘Oh, ma’am, how witty you are,’ retorted Miss Squeers with a low curtsy, ‘almost as witty, ma’am, as you are clever.", "\"어머, 부인, 어쩜 그리 재치가 넘치실까.\" 스퀴어스 양이 낮게 절을 하며 받아쳤어."], ["How very clever it was in you, ma’am, to choose a time when I had gone to tea with my pa, and was sure not to come back without being fetched!", "\"재치가 영악함에 거의 맞먹으시네요, 부인. 내가 아빠랑 차 마시러 가서 데리러 오기 전엔 절대 안 돌아올 게 뻔한 그 시간을 골라 잡다니, 어쩜 그리도 영악하실까요, 부인!"], ["What a pity you never thought that other people might be as clever as yourself and spoil your plans!’", "남들도 자기만큼 영악해서 그 계획을 망쳐 놓을 수 있다는 걸 한 번도 생각 못 하셨다니, 참 안됐네요!\""]], [["‘You won’t vex me, child, with such airs as these,’ said the late Miss Price, assuming the matron.", "\"얘야, 그런 잘난 척으론 나를 화나게 못 한단다.\" 전날의 프라이스 양이 마님 행세를 하며 말했어."]], [["‘Don’t MISSIS me, ma’am, if you please,’ returned Miss Squeers, sharply.", "\"제발 나한테 '얘' 소리 마세요, 부인.\""], ["‘I’ll not bear it.", "스퀴어스 양이 날카롭게 받았어."], ["Is THIS the hend--’", "\"그건 못 참아요. 이게 결말—\""]], [["‘Dang it a’,’ cried John Browdie, impatiently.", "\"에잇, 빌어먹을.\" 존 브라우디가 답답한 듯 외쳤어."], ["‘Say thee say out, Fanny, and mak’ sure it’s the end, and dinnot ask nobody whether it is or not.’", "\"할 말 있으면 다 해 버려, 패니. 그게 결말인지 똑똑히 못 박고, 결말이냐 아니냐 남한테 묻지나 말고.\""]], [["‘Thanking you for your advice which was not required, Mr.", "\"청하지도 않은 충고 고맙기도 하네요, 브라우디 씨.\""], ["Browdie,’ returned Miss Squeers, with laborious politeness, ‘have the goodness not to presume to meddle with my Christian name.", "스퀴어스 양이 애써 정중하게 받아쳤어. \"내 세례명을 함부로 입에 올리는 주제넘은 짓일랑 삼가 주시지요."], ["Even my pity shall never make me forget what’s due to myself, Mr.", "아무리 연민을 베푼다 해도, 나 스스로에 대한 도리를 잊을 만큼은 아니니까요, 브라우디 씨."], ["Browdie.", "틸다,\""], ["‘Tilda,’ said Miss Squeers, with such a sudden accession of violence that John started in his boots, ‘I throw you off for ever, miss.", "스퀴어스 양이 어찌나 갑자기 사납게 목소리를 높이던지 존이 신발 안에서 발을 흠칫했어. \"너를 영영 내쳐 버리겠어, 이 아가씨야."], ["I abandon you.", "너를 버리겠어."], ["I renounce you.", "너와 의절하겠어. 나는,\""], ["I wouldn’t,’ cried Miss Squeers in a solemn voice, ‘have a child named ‘Tilda, not to save it from its grave.’", "스퀴어스 양이 엄숙한 목소리로 외쳤어. \"무덤에서 구해 내는 한이 있어도 자식한테 틸다라는 이름은 절대 안 붙일 거야.\""]], [["‘As for the matther o’ that,’ observed John, ‘it’ll be time eneaf to think aboot neaming of it when it cooms.’", "\"그 일이라면,\" 존이 한마디 했어. \"그놈이 태어나거든 그때 이름을 생각해도 늦지 않을 거요.\""]], [["‘John!’ interposed his wife, ‘don’t tease her.’", "\"존!\" 아내가 끼어들었어. \"그만 약 올려요.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Tease, indeed!’ cried Miss Squeers, bridling up.", "약을 올린다고요!\" 스퀴어스 양이 발끈 성을 내며 외쳤어."], ["‘Tease, indeed!", "\"약을 올린다고!"], ["He, he!", "흐, 흐!"], ["Tease, too!", "약 올린다네!"], ["No, don’t tease her.", "아니, 약 올리지 마세요."], ["Consider her feelings, pray!’", "부디 저 사람 마음 좀 헤아려 주시지!\""]], [["‘If it’s fated that listeners are never to hear any good of themselves,’ said Mrs.", "\"엿듣는 사람은 제 흉밖에 못 듣는 게 정해진 이치라면,\" 브라우디 부인이 말했어."], ["Browdie, ‘I can’t help it, and I am very sorry for it.", "\"그야 나도 어쩔 수 없고, 그건 정말 미안해."], ["But I will say, Fanny, that times out of number I have spoken so kindly of you behind your back, that even you could have found no fault with what I said.’", "하지만 이건 말해 두겠어, 패니. 나는 네 등 뒤에서 수도 없이 네 얘기를 좋게 했고, 그 말들은 너조차 흠잡을 데 하나 없었을 거야.\""]], [["‘Oh, I dare say not, ma’am!’ cried Miss Squeers, with another curtsy.", "\"오, 그러셨겠지요, 부인!\" 스퀴어스 양이 또 한 번 절을 하며 외쳤어."], ["‘Best thanks to you for your goodness, and begging and praying you not to be hard upon me another time!’", "\"그 갸륵한 마음씨에 깊이 감사드리고, 다음번엔 부디 저를 모질게 대하지 말아 주십사 빌고 또 빕니다!\""]], [["‘I don’t know,’ resumed Mrs.", "\"내가 지금 이 순간까지,\""], ["Browdie, ‘that I have said anything very bad of you, even now.", "브라우디 부인이 말을 이었어. \"네 얘기를 그렇게 나쁘게 한 건 없는 것 같은데."], ["At all events, what I did say was quite true; but if I have, I am very sorry for it, and I beg your pardon.", "어쨌거나 내가 한 말은 다 사실이었어. 그래도 혹시 그랬다면, 정말 미안하고 용서를 빌게."], ["You have said much worse of me, scores of times, Fanny; but I have never borne any malice to you, and I hope you’ll not bear any to me.’", "너는 수십 번도 더 나를 훨씬 더 나쁘게 말했지만, 패니, 나는 너한테 한 번도 앙심을 품은 적이 없어. 그러니 너도 나한테 앙심 품지 않았으면 좋겠어.\""]], [["Miss Squeers made no more direct reply than surveying her former friend from top to toe, and elevating her nose in the air with ineffable disdain.", "스퀴어스 양은 이렇다 할 대꾸 대신, 옛 친구를 머리부터 발끝까지 훑어보고는 형언할 수 없는 경멸로 코를 치켜드는 게 다였어. 하지만 '요 계집', '여우 같은 것', '하찮은 것'"], ["But some indistinct allusions to a ‘puss,’ and a ‘minx,’ and a ‘contemptible creature,’ escaped her; and this, together with a severe biting of the lips, great difficulty in swallowing, and very frequent comings and goings of breath, seemed to imply that feelings were swelling in Miss Squeers’s bosom too great for utterance.", "따위의 알아듣기 힘든 말들이 입에서 새어 나왔지. 게다가 입술을 사납게 깨물고, 침을 삼키기를 무척 힘겨워하고, 숨이 자꾸만 들락날락하는 걸 보니, 스퀴어스 양의 가슴속에서도 입 밖에 못 낼 만큼 거센 감정이 부풀어 오르는 모양이었어."]], [["While the foregoing conversation was proceeding, Master Wackford, finding himself unnoticed, and feeling his preponderating inclinations strong upon him, had by little and little sidled up to the table and attacked the food with such slight skirmishing as drawing his fingers round and round the inside of the plates, and afterwards sucking them with infinite relish; picking the bread, and dragging the pieces over the surface of the butter; pocketing lumps of sugar, pretending all the time to be absorbed in thought; and so forth.", "위와 같은 대화가 오가는 동안, 워크퍼드 도련님은 아무도 자기를 안 보는 틈을 타, 압도적인 식욕이 강하게 도지는 걸 느끼고서, 조금씩 조금씩 식탁 쪽으로 슬그머니 다가갔어. 그러고는 접시 안쪽을 손가락으로 빙빙 돌려 훑고서 그 손가락을 더없이 맛나게 쪽쪽 빠는가 하면, 빵을 뜯어 그 조각을 버터 위에 쓱쓱 끌고 다니고, 줄곧 깊은 생각에 잠긴 척하면서 설탕 덩이를 주머니에 슬쩍 챙기는 식으로, 가벼운 잽을 날리며 음식을 공략하기 시작했지."], ["Finding that no interference was attempted with these small liberties, he gradually mounted to greater, and, after helping himself to a moderately good cold collation, was, by this time, deep in the pie.", "이 자잘한 방종에 아무도 간섭하지 않는 걸 알아채자, 그는 차차 더 큰 짓으로 올라갔어. 그리고 적당히 괜찮은 찬 음식을 한 접시 떠먹고 나서는, 이 무렵엔 파이 속에 깊숙이 빠져 있었지."]], [["Nothing of this had been unobserved by Mr.", "이 모든 게 스퀴어스 씨의 눈을 비껴가진 않았어."], ["Squeers, who, so long as the attention of the company was fixed upon other objects, hugged himself to think that his son and heir should be fattening at the enemy’s expense.", "사람들의 관심이 다른 데 쏠려 있는 동안에는, 제 아들이자 후계자가 적의 비용으로 살을 찌우고 있다는 생각에 스퀴어스 씨는 속으로 흐뭇해했지."], ["But there being now an appearance of a temporary calm, in which the proceedings of little Wackford could scarcely fail to be observed, he feigned to be aware of the circumstance for the first time, and inflicted upon the face of that young gentleman a slap that made the very tea-cups ring.", "그런데 이제 잠깐 조용해진 틈이 생겨, 어린 워크퍼드의 행각이 도저히 눈에 안 띌 수 없게 되자, 그는 마치 이제야 그 일을 처음 알아챈 척하며, 그 젊은 도련님의 뺨을 철썩 갈겼어. 그 바람에 찻잔들이 다 쟁그랑 울릴 정도였지."]], [["‘Eating!’ cried Mr.", "\"처먹어!\""], ["Squeers, ‘of what his father’s enemies has left!", "스퀴어스 씨가 외쳤어. \"그것도 제 아비 원수들이 남긴 걸!"], ["It’s fit to go and poison you, you unnat’ral boy.’", "너를 독살하고도 남을 음식이다, 이 후레자식 같은 놈.\""]], [["‘It wean’t hurt him,’ said John, apparently very much relieved by the prospect of having a man in the quarrel; ‘let’ un eat.", "\"애한테 탈 안 나오.\" 싸움에 사내 하나가 끼어든다는 생각에 적잖이 안도한 기색으로 존이 말했어."], ["I wish the whole school was here.", "\"먹게 놔두쇼. 차라리 그 학교 애들 전부가 여기 왔으면 좋겠소."], ["I’d give’em soom’at to stay their unfort’nate stomachs wi’, if I spent the last penny I had!’", "내 마지막 한 푼까지 털어서라도, 그 불쌍한 배들 좀 채워 줄 걸 먹였으면 좋겠다고!\""]], [["Squeers scowled at him with the worst and most malicious expression of which his face was capable--it was a face of remarkable capability, too, in that way--and shook his fist stealthily.", "스퀴어스는 그 얼굴이 지을 수 있는 가장 흉악하고 악의에 찬 표정으로—그쪽으로는 또 어찌나 표정이 다채로운 얼굴인지—그를 노려보며 몰래 주먹을 흔들었어."]], [["‘Coom, coom, schoolmeasther,’ said John, ‘dinnot make a fool o’ thyself; for if I was to sheake mine--only once--thou’d fa’ doon wi’ the wind o’ it.’", "\"이봐요, 이봐, 선생.\" 존이 말했어. \"바보짓 좀 마쇼. 내가 이걸 한 번만—딱 한 번만—흔들었다간, 그 바람에 당신이 푹 자빠질 테니까.\""]], [["‘It was you, was it,’ returned Squeers, ‘that helped off my runaway boy?", "\"네가, 응?\" 스퀴어스가 받아쳤어."], ["It was you, was it?’", "\"내 도망친 녀석을 도와 빼돌린 게 네놈이야? 네놈이었어?\""]], [["‘Me!’ returned John, in a loud tone.", "\"나요!\" 존이 큰 소리로 받았어."], ["‘Yes, it wa’ me, coom; wa’at o’ that?", "\"그래, 나였소, 어쩔 거요?"], ["It wa’ me.", "나였다니까."], ["Noo then!’", "자, 그래서?\""]], [["‘You hear him say he did it, my child!’ said Squeers, appealing to his daughter.", "\"이 애야, 저자가 제 입으로 했다고 하는 거 들었지!\" 스퀴어스가 딸한테 호소하며 말했어."], ["‘You hear him say he did it!’", "\"저자가 제 입으로 했다고 하는 거 들었어!\""]], [["‘Did it!’ cried John. ‘I’ll tell ‘ee more; hear this, too. If thou’d got another roonaway boy, I’d do it agean. If thou’d got twonty roonaway boys, I’d do it twonty times ower, and twonty more to thot; and I tell thee more,’ said John, ‘noo my blood is oop, that thou’rt an old ra’ascal; and that it’s weel for thou, thou be’est an old ‘un, or I’d ha’ poonded thee to flour when thou told an honest mun hoo thou’d licked that poor chap in t’ coorch.’", "\"했고말고!\" 존이 외쳤어. \"한마디 더 해 주지, 이것도 들어 두쇼. 당신한테 도망친 녀석이 또 있으면, 나는 또 그렇게 해 줄 거요. 도망친 녀석이 스무 명 있으면, 스무 번을 또 해 주고, 거기다 스무 번을 더 해 주겠소. 그리고 한마디 더 하지.\" 존이 말했어. \"이젠 내 피가 끓어오르니 말인데, 당신은 늙은 악당이오. 그리고 당신이 늙은이라서 다행인 줄 아쇼, 안 그랬으면 가루가 되도록 빻아 놨을 거요. 당신이 점잖은 사람한테, 교회에서 그 불쌍한 녀석을 두들겨 팼다고 떠벌릴 때 말이오.\""]], [["‘An honest man!’ cried Squeers, with a sneer.", "\"점잖은 사람이라고!\" 스퀴어스가 비웃으며 외쳤어."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아! 점잖은 사람이오.\""], ["an honest man,’ replied John; ‘honest in ought but ever putting legs under seame table wi’ such as thou.’", "존이 대꾸했어. \"당신 같은 작자랑 한 식탁에 다리를 들이미는 것만 빼면, 매사에 점잖은 사람이지.\""]], [["‘Scandal!’ said Squeers, exultingly.", "\"중상모략이다!\" 스퀴어스가 의기양양하게 말했어."], ["‘Two witnesses to it; Wackford knows the nature of an oath, he does; we shall have you there, sir.", "\"여기 증인이 둘이야. 워크퍼드는 맹세가 뭔지 알지, 알고말고."], ["Rascal, eh?’ Mr.", "당신을 그걸로 걸어 주겠어, 선생."], ["Squeers took out his pocketbook and made a note of it.", "악당이라고, 응?\" 스퀴어스 씨가 수첩을 꺼내 그걸 적어 넣었어."], ["‘Very good.", "\"아주 좋아."], ["I should say that was worth full twenty pound at the next assizes, without the honesty, sir.’", "다음 순회재판에서 이건, 점잖다는 말은 빼고도, 족히 이십 파운드짜리는 되겠는걸, 선생.\""]], [["‘’Soizes,’ cried John, ‘thou’d betther not talk to me o’ ‘Soizes. Yorkshire schools have been shown up at ‘Soizes afore noo, mun, and it’s a ticklish soobjact to revive, I can tell ye.’", "\"순회재판이라.\" 존이 외쳤어. \"나한테 순회재판 운운하지 않는 게 좋을 거요. 요크셔 학교들이 순회재판에서 까발려진 게 한두 번이 아니거든, 이 양반아. 그건 다시 들춰 봐야 좋을 게 없는 께름칙한 얘기지, 내 장담하는데.\""]], [["Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨는 위협하듯 고개를 흔들었어."], ["Squeers shook his head in a threatening manner, looking very white with passion; and taking his daughter’s arm, and dragging little Wackford by the hand, retreated towards the door.", "격분해서 얼굴이 새하얗게 질린 채 말이야. 그러고는 딸의 팔을 붙잡고 어린 워크퍼드의 손을 끌면서 문 쪽으로 물러났어."]], [["‘As for you,’ said Squeers, turning round and addressing Nicholas, who, as he had caused him to smart pretty soundly on a former occasion, purposely abstained from taking any part in the discussion, ‘see if I ain’t down upon you before long.", "\"그리고 너로 말하면,\" 스퀴어스가 몸을 돌려 니콜라스를 향해 말했어."], ["You’ll go a kidnapping of boys, will you?", "니콜라스는 전에 한 번 이자를 호되게 따끔하게 해 준 적이 있던 터라, 일부러 이 언쟁에 끼어들지 않고 있었지. \"내가 머잖아 너를 가만 안 두나 봐라."], ["Take care their fathers don’t turn up--mark that--take care their fathers don’t turn up, and send ‘em back to me to do as I like with, in spite of you.’", "아이들을 납치하고 다니겠다, 그거지? 그 애비들이 나타나지 않게 조심하는 게 좋을 거다—명심해—그 애비들이 나타나서, 네놈이 아무리 막아도 그 애들을 도로 내게 보내 내 마음대로 하게 만들지 않도록 조심하란 말이다.\""]], [["‘I am not afraid of that,’ replied Nicholas, shrugging his shoulders contemptuously, and turning away.", "\"그건 두렵지 않습니다.\" 니콜라스가 경멸하듯 어깨를 으쓱하고는 돌아서며 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Ain’t you!’ retorted Squeers, with a diabolical look.", "\"안 두렵다고!\" 스퀴어스가 악마 같은 표정으로 받아쳤어."], ["‘Now then, come along.’", "\"자, 그럼, 가자.\""]], [["‘I leave such society, with my pa, for Hever,’ said Miss Squeers, looking contemptuously and loftily round.", "\"나는 아빠랑 함께, 이런 무리를 영영 떠나겠어요.\" 스퀴어스 양이 경멸하듯 거만하게 둘러보며 말했어."], ["‘I am defiled by breathing the air with such creatures.", "\"이런 것들이랑 같은 공기를 마시는 것만으로도 더러워진다고요."], ["Poor Mr. Browdie!", "가엾은 브라우디 씨!"], ["He!", "흐!"], ["he!", "흐!"], ["he!", "흐!"], ["I do pity him, that I do; he’s so deluded.", "정말 딱해요, 정말로요. 저렇게 속고 있다니."], ["He!", "흐!"], ["he!", "흐!"], ["he!--Artful and designing ‘Tilda!’", "흐!—교활하고 음흉한 틸다!\""]], [["With this sudden relapse into the sternest and most majestic wrath, Miss Squeers swept from the room; and having sustained her dignity until the last possible moment, was heard to sob and scream and struggle in the passage.", "이렇게 갑작스레 다시 더없이 매섭고 위풍당당한 분노로 빠져들면서, 스퀴어스 양은 방을 휙 쓸고 나갔어. 그리고 마지막 순간까지 위엄을 지키더니, 복도에 나가서는 흐느끼고 비명을 지르며 발버둥 치는 소리가 들려왔지."]], [["John Browdie remained standing behind the table, looking from his wife to Nicholas, and back again, with his mouth wide open, until his hand accidentally fell upon the tankard of ale, when he took it up, and having obscured his features therewith for some time, drew a long breath, handed it over to Nicholas, and rang the bell.", "존 브라우디는 식탁 뒤에 선 채, 입을 떡 벌리고 아내와 니콜라스를 번갈아 보고 또 봤어. 그러다 손이 우연히 에일 잔에 닿자, 그걸 집어 들고는 한동안 거기다 얼굴을 파묻었다가, 긴 숨을 한 번 내쉬고는 잔을 니콜라스에게 건네고 벨을 울렸지."]], [["‘Here, waither,’ said John, briskly.", "\"이봐요, 웨이터.\" 존이 활기차게 말했어."], ["‘Look alive here.", "\"여기 좀 봐요."], ["Tak’ these things awa’, and let’s have soomat broiled for sooper--vary comfortable and plenty o’ it--at ten o’clock.", "이것들 다 치우고, 열 시에 야식으로 뭐 좀 구워 와요—아주 든든하게, 푸짐하게 말이오."], ["Bring soom brandy and soom wather, and a pair o’ slippers--the largest pair in the house--and be quick aboot it.", "브랜디랑 물도 좀 가져오고, 슬리퍼 한 켤레도—집에서 제일 큰 걸로—얼른 가져와요. 어이쿠!\""], ["Dash ma wig!’ said John, rubbing his hands, ‘there’s no ganging oot to neeght, noo, to fetch anybody whoam, and ecod, we’ll begin to spend the evening in airnest.’", "존이 두 손을 비비며 말했어. \"이제 오늘 밤엔 누구 데리러 나갈 일도 없으니, 야, 이제 본격적으로 저녁 한번 제대로 즐겨 봅시다.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 43", "# CHAPTER 43"]], [["Officiates as a kind of Gentleman Usher, in bringing various People together\nThe storm had long given place to a calm the most profound, and the evening was pretty far advanced--indeed supper was over, and the process of digestion proceeding as favourably as, under the influence of complete tranquillity, cheerful conversation, and a moderate allowance of brandy-and-water, most wise men conversant with the anatomy and functions of the human frame will consider that it ought to have proceeded, when the three friends, or as one might say, both in a civil and religious sense, and with proper deference and regard to the holy state of matrimony, the two friends, (Mr.", "폭풍은 진작에 더없이 깊은 고요에 자리를 내주었고, 저녁도 꽤 깊어 있었다. 사실 저녁 식사는 이미 끝났고, 인체의 구조와 기능에 밝은 똑똑한 사람들이라면 누구나 그래야 마땅하다고 여길 만큼 — 그러니까 완전한 평온과 즐거운 대화, 그리고 적당량의 브랜디 물을 곁들인 가운데 — 소화도 순조롭게 진행되고 있던 참이었다."], ["and Mrs.", "그때 세 친구, 아니 시민적으로나 종교적으로나, 또 신성한 결혼의 상태에 마땅한 존중과 경의를 담아 말하자면 두 친구가 — 브라우디 부부는 하나로 쳐야 하니까 — 아래층에서 들려오는 사납고 성난 고함 소리에 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["Browdie counting as no more than one,) were startled by the noise of loud and angry threatenings below stairs, which presently attained so high a pitch, and were conveyed besides in language so towering, sanguinary, and ferocious, that it could hardly have been surpassed, if there had actually been a Saracen’s head then present in the establishment, supported on the shoulders and surmounting the trunk of a real, live, furious, and most unappeasable Saracen.", "그 소리는 곧 어찌나 높이 치솟던지, 게다가 어찌나 거창하고 살벌하고 험악한 말로 쏟아지던지, 정말로 그 여관에 사라센 사람의 머리가, 그것도 진짜로 살아 있고 분노에 차서 도무지 달랠 길 없는 사라센 사람의 몸통과 어깨 위에 얹힌 머리가 있었다 해도 그보다 더하지는 못했을 정도였다."]], [["This turmoil, instead of quickly subsiding after the first outburst, (as turmoils not unfrequently do, whether in taverns, legislative assemblies, or elsewhere,) into a mere grumbling and growling squabble, increased every moment; and although the whole din appeared to be raised by but one pair of lungs, yet that one pair was of so powerful a quality, and repeated such words as ‘scoundrel,’ ‘rascal,’ ‘insolent puppy,’ and a variety of expletives no less flattering to the party addressed, with such great relish and strength of tone, that a dozen voices raised in concert under any ordinary circumstances would have made far less uproar and created much smaller consternation.", "이 소동은, 술집이든 입법 회의장이든 다른 어디서든 흔히 그러듯 처음 한바탕 터지고 나서 금세 그저 투덜대고 으르렁대는 말다툼으로 잦아드는 게 아니라, 시시각각 더 커져만 갔다. 그 온갖 소음이 폐 하나에서 나오는 것 같았는데도, 그 폐 하나가 어찌나 힘이 좋던지 \"악당\", \"불한당\", \"건방진 강아지\" 같은 말과, 상대를 추켜세우기로는 그에 못지않은 갖가지 욕설을 어찌나 신나고 우렁차게 되풀이하던지, 보통 때 같으면 열두 사람이 한꺼번에 목청을 높여도 그보다 소란은 훨씬 덜하고 일으키는 놀라움도 훨씬 작았을 정도였다."]], [["‘Why, what’s the matter?’ said Nicholas, moving hastily towards the door.", "\"아니, 무슨 일이지?\" 니콜라스가 문 쪽으로 황급히 다가서며 말했다."]], [["John Browdie was striding in the same direction when Mrs.", "존 브라우디도 같은 방향으로 성큼성큼 걸어가는데, 브라우디 부인이 얼굴이 새파래져서는 의자에 기댄 채 가냘픈 목소리로 그에게 일렀다."], ["Browdie turned pale, and, leaning back in her chair, requested him with a faint voice to take notice, that if he ran into any danger it was her intention to fall into hysterics immediately, and that the consequences might be more serious than he thought for.", "만약 그가 위험한 데로 뛰어들면 자기는 당장 히스테리를 일으킬 작정이고, 그 결과가 그가 생각하는 것보다 훨씬 심각할 수도 있으니 명심하라는 거였다. 존은 이 소식에 좀 난처한 기색이었는데, 그러면서도 얼굴에는 슬그머니 웃음이 비쳤다."], ["John looked rather disconcerted by this intelligence, though there was a lurking grin on his face at the same time; but, being quite unable to keep out of the fray, he compromised the matter by tucking his wife’s arm under his own, and, thus accompanied, following Nicholas downstairs with all speed.", "하지만 도무지 싸움판에서 빠질 수가 없었던 그는 절충안을 찾았다. 아내의 팔을 자기 팔에 끼고는, 그렇게 동반한 채 니콜라스를 따라 있는 힘껏 빠르게 아래층으로 내려간 것이다."]], [["The passage outside the coffee-room door was the scene of disturbance, and here were congregated the coffee-room customers and waiters, together with two or three coachmen and helpers from the yard. These had hastily assembled round a young man who from his appearance might have been a year or two older than Nicholas, and who, besides having given utterance to the defiances just now described, seemed to have proceeded to even greater lengths in his indignation, inasmuch as his feet had no other covering than a pair of stockings, while a couple of slippers lay at no great distance from the head of a prostrate figure in an opposite corner, who bore the appearance of having been shot into his present retreat by means of a kick, and complimented by having the slippers flung about his ears afterwards.", "소란이 벌어진 곳은 커피룸 문밖 복도였다. 거기엔 커피룸 손님들과 종업원들이, 거기다 마당에서 온 마부와 일꾼 두세 명까지 한데 모여 있었다. 이들은 한 젊은이를 황급히 둘러싸고 있었는데, 그 젊은이는 보아하니 니콜라스보다 한두 살쯤 위인 듯했다. 그는 방금 말한 그런 호언장담을 늘어놓았을 뿐 아니라, 분이 더 치밀어 한층 더 나아간 모양이었다. 그의 발에는 양말 한 켤레 말고는 아무것도 신겨 있지 않았고, 슬리퍼 한 켤레는 맞은편 구석에 엎어진 사람의 머리에서 그리 멀지 않은 데 놓여 있었다. 그 엎어진 사람은 발길질에 차여 지금 그 자리로 처박힌 모양이었고, 그러고 나서 슬리퍼를 양쪽 귀에 얻어맞는 호사까지 누린 듯했다."]], [["The coffee-room customers, and the waiters, and the coachmen, and the helpers--not to mention a barmaid who was looking on from behind an open sash window--seemed at that moment, if a spectator might judge from their winks, nods, and muttered exclamations, strongly disposed to take part against the young gentleman in the stockings.", "커피룸 손님들도, 종업원들도, 마부들도, 일꾼들도 — 열린 미닫이창 너머로 구경하던 술집 아가씨는 말할 것도 없고 — 그 순간 그들의 눈짓과 끄덕임과 중얼거리는 소리로 미루어 보면, 양말 차림의 젊은 신사한테 단단히 등을 돌릴 기색이었다."], ["Observing this, and that the young gentleman was nearly of his own age and had in nothing the appearance of an habitual brawler, Nicholas, impelled by such feelings as will influence young men sometimes, felt a very strong disposition to side with the weaker party, and so thrust himself at once into the centre of the group, and in a more emphatic tone, perhaps, than circumstances might seem to warrant, demanded what all that noise was about.", "이걸 알아챈 니콜라스는, 그 젊은 신사가 자기와 거의 같은 나이인 데다 어디로 보나 싸움을 일삼는 사람 같지 않다는 것도 보고서, 가끔 젊은이들을 움직이게 하는 그런 감정에 떠밀려 약한 쪽 편을 들고 싶은 마음이 아주 강하게 솟구쳤다. 그래서 단번에 무리 한가운데로 비집고 들어가, 어쩌면 상황이 보장하는 것보다 좀 더 단호한 어조로, 대체 이 소란이 다 무슨 일이냐고 따져 물었다."]], [["‘Hallo!’ said one of the men from the yard, ‘this is somebody in disguise, this is.’", "\"어이!\" 마당에서 온 사람 하나가 말했다. \"이건 변장한 누구신가 보네, 이거.\""]], [["‘Room for the eldest son of the Emperor of Roosher, gen’l’men!’ cried another fellow.", "\"러시아 황제의 큰아드님 나가신다, 자리 좀 비키시오, 신사 여러분!\" 또 다른 녀석이 외쳤다."]], [["Disregarding these sallies, which were uncommonly well received, as sallies at the expense of the best-dressed persons in a crowd usually are, Nicholas glanced carelessly round, and addressing the young gentleman, who had by this time picked up his slippers and thrust his feet into them, repeated his inquiries with a courteous air.", "군중 속에서 제일 옷을 잘 차려입은 사람을 비웃는 농담이 으레 그렇듯 이 농지거리들도 유난히 잘 먹혀들었지만, 니콜라스는 그 말들을 무시하고 무심하게 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러고는 그새 슬리퍼를 주워 발을 꿴 그 젊은 신사를 향해, 공손한 태도로 아까 했던 질문을 되풀이했다."]], [["‘A mere nothing!’ he replied.", "\"별것 아닙니다!\" 젊은이가 대답했다."]], [["At this a murmur was raised by the lookers-on, and some of the boldest cried, ‘Oh, indeed!--Wasn’t it though?--Nothing, eh?--He called that nothing, did he? Lucky for him if he found it nothing.’ These and many other expressions of ironical disapprobation having been exhausted, two or three of the out-of-door fellows began to hustle Nicholas and the young gentleman who had made the noise: stumbling against them by accident, and treading on their toes, and so forth. But this being a round game, and one not necessarily limited to three or four players, was open to John Browdie too, who, bursting into the little crowd--to the great terror of his wife--and falling about in all directions, now to the right, now to the left, now forwards, now backwards, and accidentally driving his elbow through the hat of the tallest helper, who had been particularly active, speedily caused the odds to wear a very different appearance; while more than one stout fellow limped away to a respectful distance, anathematising with tears in his eyes the heavy tread and ponderous feet of the burly Yorkshireman.", "이 말에 구경꾼들 사이에서 웅성거림이 일었다. 그중 대담한 몇몇이 외쳤다. \"허, 그래?— 정말 별것 아니라고?— 아무것도 아니라, 응?— 저게 별것 아니라는 거야, 정말?— 정말 별것 아니면 다행인 줄 알아.\" 이런 말과 함께 비아냥대는 못마땅한 소리들을 잔뜩 쏟아낸 다음, 마당에서 온 녀석 두세 명이 니콜라스와 소동을 일으킨 그 젊은 신사를 떠밀기 시작했다. 실수인 척 부딪치고, 발등을 밟고, 그런 식으로 말이다. 그런데 이건 둥근 놀이판 같은 거라서, 꼭 서너 명만 끼라는 법은 없었고, 존 브라우디한테도 열려 있었다. 그가 — 아내가 기겁하는 가운데 — 작은 무리 속으로 와락 뛰어들어 사방으로 몸을 부딪쳐 댔다. 오른쪽으로, 왼쪽으로, 앞으로, 뒤로, 그러다 유난히 설치던 키 큰 일꾼의 모자를 팔꿈치로 얼결에 푹 뚫어 버리니, 순식간에 형세가 아주 딴판으로 바뀌었다. 그러는 동안 우람한 녀석 여럿이 절뚝거리며 점잖게 거리를 두고 물러나, 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채 이 덩치 큰 요크셔 사람의 묵직한 걸음과 육중한 발을 저주했다."]], [["‘Let me see him do it again,’ said he who had been kicked into the corner, rising as he spoke, apparently more from the fear of John Browdie’s inadvertently treading upon him, than from any desire to place himself on equal terms with his late adversary.", "\"어디 또 한 번 해 보라지.\" 구석으로 차여 처박혔던 사내가 일어서며 말했다."], ["‘Let me see him do it again.", "보아하니 방금 전의 적수와 대등하게 맞붙겠다는 마음보다는, 존 브라우디가 얼떨결에 자기를 밟을까 봐 무서워서 일어선 듯했다. \"어디 또 한 번 해 보라고."], ["That’s all.’", "그게 다야.\""]], [["‘Let me hear you make those remarks again,’ said the young man, ‘and I’ll knock that head of yours in among the wine-glasses behind you there.’", "\"그 소리 또 한 번 해 봐.\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"그러면 네 그 대가리를 저 뒤에 있는 와인 잔들 속에다 처박아 줄 테니까.\""]], [["Here a waiter who had been rubbing his hands in excessive enjoyment of the scene, so long as only the breaking of heads was in question, adjured the spectators with great earnestness to fetch the police, declaring that otherwise murder would be surely done, and that he was responsible for all the glass and china on the premises.", "이때 그저 머리 깨지는 일만 벌어지는 동안엔 신이 나서 손을 비비며 구경하던 종업원 하나가, 어서 경찰을 불러오라고 구경꾼들에게 아주 간곡히 애원했다. 안 그러면 틀림없이 살인이 날 거라며, 이 집 안의 유리며 도자기는 죄다 자기 책임이라는 거였다."]], [["‘No one need trouble himself to stir,’ said the young gentleman, ‘I am going to remain in the house all night, and shall be found here in the morning if there is any assault to answer for.’", "\"아무도 수고스럽게 움직일 것 없습니다.\" 젊은 신사가 말했다. \"저는 오늘 밤 이 집에 묵을 거고, 무슨 폭행 책임이라도 물을 게 있으면 아침에 여기서 저를 찾으면 됩니다.\""]], [["‘What did you strike him for?’ asked one of the bystanders.", "\"왜 저 사람을 친 거요?\" 곁에 선 사람 하나가 물었다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"그래!"], ["what did you strike him for?’ demanded the others.", "왜 저 사람을 쳤소?\" 다른 사람들도 따져 물었다."]], [["The unpopular gentleman looked coolly round, and addressing himself to Nicholas, said:", "인심을 잃은 그 신사는 차분하게 둘러보고는, 니콜라스를 향해 말했다."]], [["‘You inquired just now what was the matter here.", "\"방금 여기서 무슨 일이냐고 물으셨죠."], ["The matter is simply this.", "일은 간단합니다."], ["Yonder person, who was drinking with a friend in the coffee-room when I took my seat there for half an hour before going to bed, (for I have just come off a journey, and preferred stopping here tonight, to going home at this hour, where I was not expected until tomorrow,) chose to express himself in very disrespectful, and insolently familiar terms, of a young lady, whom I recognised from his description and other circumstances, and whom I have the honour to know.", "저기 저 사람이 말입니다, 제가 자기 전에 삼십 분쯤 커피룸에 앉아 있었는데 — 막 여행에서 돌아온 참이라, 내일까지는 아무도 저를 기다리지 않는 집으로 이 시간에 가느니 오늘 밤은 여기 묵는 게 낫겠다 싶었거든요 — 어떤 친구랑 술을 마시면서, 한 젊은 아가씨에 대해 아주 무례하고 건방지게 허물없는 말투로 떠들기 시작했습니다. 그 묘사며 다른 정황으로 보아 저는 그 아가씨가 누군지 알아봤고, 또 그 아가씨를 아는 영광을 누리고 있는 사람입니다."], ["As he spoke loud enough to be overheard by the other guests who were present, I informed him most civilly that he was mistaken in his conjectures, which were of an offensive nature, and requested him to forbear.", "그가 같이 있던 다른 손님들한테까지 들릴 만큼 큰 소리로 떠들기에, 저는 아주 정중하게 그 추측이 — 듣기에 거북한 추측이었지요 — 틀렸다고 일러 주고 그만두라고 청했습니다. 그가 잠깐은 그러더니, 방을 나가면서 또 아까보다 더 거북한 투로 그 얘기를 다시 꺼내지 뭡니까."], ["He did so for a little time, but as he chose to renew his conversation when leaving the room, in a more offensive strain than before, I could not refrain from making after him, and facilitating his departure by a kick, which reduced him to the posture in which you saw him just now.", "그래서 저는 그 뒤를 쫓아가 발길질로 떠나는 걸 도와주지 않을 수 없었고, 그래서 방금 보신 그 꼴이 된 겁니다. 제 일은 제가 제일 잘 안다고 봅니다.\""], ["I am the best judge of my own affairs, I take it,’ said the young man, who had certainly not quite recovered from his recent heat; ‘if anybody here thinks proper to make this quarrel his own, I have not the smallest earthly objection, I do assure him.’", "아직 방금 전의 흥분이 완전히 가시지는 않은 젊은이가 말했다. \"여기 누구든 이 싸움을 자기 일로 삼고 싶은 분이 있다면, 저는 눈곱만큼도 반대하지 않습니다, 확실히 말씀드리지요.\""]], [["Of all possible courses of proceeding under the circumstances detailed, there was certainly not one which, in his then state of mind, could have appeared more laudable to Nicholas than this.", "이렇게 자세히 들은 정황에서 취할 수 있는 온갖 행동 가운데, 그때의 니콜라스 마음 상태로는 이보다 더 칭찬할 만한 것은 분명 하나도 없었다."], ["There were not many subjects of dispute which at that moment could have come home to his own breast more powerfully, for having the unknown uppermost in his thoughts, it naturally occurred to him that he would have done just the same if any audacious gossiper durst have presumed in his hearing to speak lightly of her.", "그 순간 그의 가슴에 이보다 더 절절히 와닿을 다툼거리도 별로 없었다. 그 이름 모를 아가씨가 마음속에 늘 으뜸으로 자리하고 있었으니, 만약 무엄한 수다쟁이가 감히 자기 듣는 데서 그녀를 가볍게 입에 올렸다면 자기도 똑같이 했으리라는 생각이 절로 떠오른 것이다."], ["Influenced by these considerations, he espoused the young gentleman’s quarrel with great warmth, protesting that he had done quite right, and that he respected him for it; which John Browdie (albeit not quite clear as to the merits) immediately protested too, with not inferior vehemence.", "이런 마음에 이끌려 니콜라스는 그 젊은 신사의 싸움을 아주 뜨겁게 편들며, 잘했다고, 그래서 그를 존경한다고 힘주어 말했다. 그러자 존 브라우디도 — 비록 시비의 옳고 그름은 잘 가늠하지 못했지만 — 그에 못지않은 기세로 곧장 맞장구를 쳤다."]], [["‘Let him take care, that’s all,’ said the defeated party, who was being rubbed down by a waiter, after his recent fall on the dusty boards.", "\"조심하는 게 좋을 거야, 그게 다야.\" 패한 쪽이 말했다."], ["‘He don’t knock me about for nothing, I can tell him that.", "그는 방금 먼지투성이 바닥에 나동그라진 뒤 종업원한테 옷을 털리는 중이었다."], ["A pretty state of things, if a man isn’t to admire a handsome girl without being beat to pieces for it!’", "\"공연히 나를 두들겨 패고 그냥 넘어갈 줄 알아, 두고 보라지. 사내가 예쁜 처녀 하나 칭찬했다고 박살이 나야 한다면, 세상 참 꼴좋겠군!\""]], [["This reflection appeared to have great weight with the young lady in the bar, who (adjusting her cap as she spoke, and glancing at a mirror) declared that it would be a very pretty state of things indeed; and that if people were to be punished for actions so innocent and natural as that, there would be more people to be knocked down than there would be people to knock them down, and that she wondered what the gentleman meant by it, that she did.", "이 말은 술집 카운터 안의 젊은 아가씨한테 크게 와닿은 모양이었다. 그녀는 (말하면서 모자를 매만지고 거울을 흘끔거리며) 정말이지 세상 꼴 한번 좋겠다고 했다. 그렇게 순진하고 자연스러운 행동으로 벌을 받아야 한다면, 때려눕히는 사람보다 얻어맞을 사람이 더 많아질 거라고, 대체 저 신사 양반은 무슨 속셈으로 그러는지 모르겠다고, 정말 모르겠다고 했다."]], [["‘My dear girl,’ said the young gentleman in a low voice, advancing towards the sash window.", "\"이봐요, 아가씨.\" 젊은 신사가 미닫이창 쪽으로 다가서며 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Nonsense, sir!’ replied the young lady sharply, smiling though as she turned aside, and biting her lip, (whereat Mrs.", "\"별말씀을요, 손님!\" 젊은 아가씨가 쌀쌀맞게 받았다."], ["Browdie, who was still standing on the stairs, glanced at her with disdain, and called to her husband to come away).", "그러면서도 고개를 옆으로 돌리며 미소 짓고 입술을 깨물었다. (이걸 보고, 아직 계단에 서 있던 브라우디 부인이 경멸하듯 그녀를 흘겨보고는 남편더러 이리 오라고 불렀다.)"]], [["‘No, but listen to me,’ said the young man.", "\"아니, 그래도 제 말 좀 들어 봐요.\" 젊은이가 말했다."], ["‘If admiration of a pretty face were criminal, I should be the most hopeless person alive, for I cannot resist one.", "\"예쁜 얼굴에 반하는 게 죄라면, 저야말로 세상에서 제일 가망 없는 사람일 겁니다. 도무지 그걸 못 참거든요."], ["It has the most extraordinary effect upon me, checks and controls me in the most furious and obstinate mood.", "그게 저한테는 아주 기막힌 효력을 발휘해서, 제가 아무리 사납고 고집스러운 기분일 때도 저를 다잡고 다스려 줍니다."], ["You see what an effect yours has had upon me already.’", "아가씨 얼굴이 벌써 저한테 어떤 효력을 냈는지 보세요.\""]], [["‘Oh, that’s very pretty,’ replied the young lady, tossing her head, ‘but--’", "\"어머, 말씀 참 예쁘게 하시네요.\" 젊은 아가씨가 고개를 살짝 젖히며 받았다. \"하지만—\""]], [["‘Yes, I know it’s very pretty,’ said the young man, looking with an air of admiration in the barmaid’s face; ‘I said so, you know, just this moment. But beauty should be spoken of respectfully--respectfully, and in proper terms, and with a becoming sense of its worth and excellence, whereas this fellow has no more notion--’", "\"그럼요, 예쁜 줄 압니다.\" 젊은이가 감탄하는 표정으로 술집 아가씨의 얼굴을 바라보며 말했다. \"방금 그렇게 말했잖아요, 알다시피. 하지만 아름다움이란 존중하는 마음으로 말해야지요 — 존중하는 마음으로, 알맞은 말로, 그 가치와 빼어남에 걸맞은 마음가짐으로 말입니다. 그런데 저 작자는 그런 생각이라곤 손톱만큼도—\""]], [["The young lady interrupted the conversation at this point, by thrusting her head out of the bar-window, and inquiring of the waiter in a shrill voice whether that young man who had been knocked down was going to stand in the passage all night, or whether the entrance was to be left clear for other people.", "이 대목에서 젊은 아가씨가 술집 창밖으로 머리를 쑥 내밀어 대화를 끊고는, 종업원에게 날카로운 목소리로 물었다. 얻어맞고 나동그라졌던 저 젊은이가 밤새 복도에 서 있을 작정이냐고, 아니면 다른 사람들 드나들게 입구를 좀 비워 둘 거냐고."], ["The waiters taking the hint, and communicating it to the hostlers, were not slow to change their tone too, and the result was, that the unfortunate victim was bundled out in a twinkling.", "종업원들이 그 눈치를 채고 마부들한테도 일러 주자, 그들도 재빨리 태도를 바꾸었다. 그 결과 불운한 희생자는 눈 깜짝할 새에 밖으로 떠밀려 나갔다."]], [["‘I am sure I have seen that fellow before,’ said Nicholas.", "\"분명 저 친구를 전에 본 적이 있는데.\" 니콜라스가 말했다."]], [["‘Indeed!’ replied his new acquaintance.", "\"그래요?\" 새로 사귄 사람이 받았다."]], [["‘I am certain of it,’ said Nicholas, pausing to reflect.", "\"틀림없어요.\" 니콜라스가 곰곰이 생각하며 말을 멈췄다."], ["‘Where can I have--stop!--yes, to be sure--he belongs to a register-office up at the west end of the town.", "\"어디서 봤더라— 가만— 그래, 맞아— 시내 서쪽 끝에 있는 직업 소개소 사람이에요."], ["I knew I recollected the face.’", "얼굴이 기억난다 했지.\""]], [["It was, indeed, Tom, the ugly clerk.", "그건 정말이지 못생긴 사무원, 톰이었다."]], [["‘That’s odd enough!’ said Nicholas, ruminating upon the strange manner in which the register-office seemed to start up and stare him in the face every now and then, and when he least expected it.", "\"거 참 묘하군!\" 니콜라스가 중얼거렸다. 그 직업 소개소가 가끔, 그것도 전혀 생각지도 못한 순간에 불쑥불쑥 튀어나와 자기 얼굴을 빤히 들여다보는 그 묘한 방식을 곱씹으면서 말이다."]], [["‘I am much obliged to you for your kind advocacy of my cause when it most needed an advocate,’ said the young man, laughing, and drawing a card from his pocket.", "\"제 일이 변호인을 가장 필요로 할 때 그렇게 친절히 편들어 주셔서 정말 고맙습니다.\" 젊은이가 웃으며 호주머니에서 명함 한 장을 꺼냈다."], ["‘Perhaps you’ll do me the favour to let me know where I can thank you.’", "\"어디로 가면 감사 인사를 드릴 수 있는지 알려 주시면 고맙겠습니다.\""]], [["Nicholas took the card, and glancing at it involuntarily as he returned the compliment, evinced very great surprise.", "니콜라스는 명함을 받아 들고, 답례 인사를 하며 무심코 거기에 눈길을 던졌다가 깜짝 놀랐다."]], [["‘Mr. Frank Cheeryble!’ said Nicholas.", "\"프랭크 치어리블 씨!\""], ["‘Surely not the nephew of Cheeryble Brothers, who is expected tomorrow!’", "니콜라스가 말했다. \"설마 내일 오기로 한 치어리블 형제 상회의 조카분은 아니시겠죠!\""]], [["‘I don’t usually call myself the nephew of the firm,’ returned Mr.", "\"보통 저를 그 상회의 조카라고는 부르지 않습니다만,\""], ["Frank, good-humouredly; ‘but of the two excellent individuals who compose it, I am proud to say I AM the nephew.", "프랭크 씨가 사람 좋게 받았다. \"그 상회를 이루고 있는 두 훌륭한 분의 조카라는 건, 그래요, 자랑스럽게 말씀드릴 수 있습니다."], ["And you, I see, are Mr. Nickleby, of whom I have heard so much!", "그리고 보아하니 당신은 제가 그토록 많이 들어 온 니클비 씨로군요!"], ["This is a most unexpected meeting, but not the less welcome, I assure you.’", "정말 뜻밖의 만남입니다만, 그래서 더더욱 반갑습니다, 정말이에요.\""]], [["Nicholas responded to these compliments with others of the same kind, and they shook hands warmly.", "니콜라스도 같은 식의 인사로 화답했고, 두 사람은 따뜻하게 악수를 나누었다."], ["Then he introduced John Browdie, who had remained in a state of great admiration ever since the young lady in the bar had been so skilfully won over to the right side.", "그러고 나서 니콜라스가 존 브라우디를 소개했다. 존은 술집 카운터의 젊은 아가씨가 그렇게 솜씨 좋게 옳은 편으로 넘어온 이후로 줄곧 감탄에 빠져 있던 참이었다."], ["Then Mrs. John Browdie was introduced, and finally they all went upstairs together and spent the next half-hour with great satisfaction and mutual entertainment; Mrs.", "그다음에 존 브라우디 부인을 소개했고, 마침내 다 함께 위층으로 올라가 다음 삼십 분을 더없이 흡족하게, 서로 즐겁게 어울리며 보냈다."], ["John Browdie beginning the conversation by declaring that of all the made-up things she ever saw, that young woman below-stairs was the vainest and the plainest.", "존 브라우디 부인은 자기가 여태 본 온갖 꾸민 것들 가운데 아래층의 그 젊은 여자가 제일 허영스럽고 제일 못났더라는 말로 이야기를 열었다."]], [["This Mr.", "이 프랭크 치어리블 씨는, 방금 벌어진 일로 미루어 보면 다혈질의 젊은이였지만 (이게 자연에서 무슨 절대적인 기적이나 진기한 현상은 아니다), 발랄하고 사람 좋고 유쾌한 친구였다."], ["Frank Cheeryble, although, to judge from what had recently taken place, a hot-headed young man (which is not an absolute miracle and phenomenon in nature), was a sprightly, good-humoured, pleasant fellow, with much both in his countenance and disposition that reminded Nicholas very strongly of the kind-hearted brothers.", "얼굴 생김새며 성품에 그 마음씨 고운 형제를 니콜라스에게 아주 강하게 떠올리게 하는 데가 많았다. 그의 태도는 그들 못지않게 꾸밈없었고, 거동에는 그 진심 어린 다정함이 가득했는데, 이는 마음속에 너그러운 구석이 조금이라도 있는 사람이라면 누구나 유난히 호감을 갖게 되는 그런 것이었다."], ["His manner was as unaffected as theirs, and his demeanour full of that heartiness which, to most people who have anything generous in their composition, is peculiarly prepossessing.", "거기다 그는 잘생기고 영리했으며, 활기가 넘쳐 났고, 더없이 명랑했으며, 단 오 분 만에 존 브라우디의 온갖 별난 구석에 마치 어릴 적부터 알고 지낸 사이인 양 손쉽게 맞춰 주었다."], ["Add to this, that he was good-looking and intelligent, had a plentiful share of vivacity, was extremely cheerful, and accommodated himself in five minutes’ time to all John Browdie’s oddities with as much ease as if he had known him from a boy; and it will be a source of no great wonder that, when they parted for the night, he had produced a most favourable impression, not only upon the worthy Yorkshireman and his wife, but upon Nicholas also, who, revolving all these things in his mind as he made the best of his way home, arrived at the conclusion that he had laid the foundation of a most agreeable and desirable acquaintance.", "그러니 밤이 되어 헤어질 무렵, 그가 저 훌륭한 요크셔 사람과 그 아내뿐 아니라 니콜라스에게까지 더없이 좋은 인상을 남긴 것도 그리 놀랄 일은 아니었다. 니콜라스는 집으로 부지런히 걸어가며 이 모든 일을 마음속으로 곱씹은 끝에, 자기가 더없이 유쾌하고 바람직한 교제의 첫걸음을 떼었다는 결론에 이르렀다."]], [["‘But it’s a most extraordinary thing about that register-office fellow!’ thought Nicholas.", "\"그런데 그 직업 소개소 작자 일은 참 기막힌 노릇이야!\" 니콜라스는 생각했다."], ["‘Is it likely that this nephew can know anything about that beautiful girl?", "\"이 조카가 그 아름다운 아가씨에 대해 뭔가 알고 있을 가능성이 있을까?"], ["When Tim Linkinwater gave me to understand the other day that he was coming to take a share in the business here, he said he had been superintending it in Germany for four years, and that during the last six months he had been engaged in establishing an agency in the north of England.", "며칠 전 팀 링컨워터가 이 사람이 여기 사업에 한몫 끼러 온다고 일러 줄 때, 사 년 동안 독일에서 사업을 관리했고, 지난 여섯 달 동안은 잉글랜드 북부에 대리점을 세우는 일을 맡고 있었다고 했지. 그러면 사 년 반— 사 년 반이야."], ["That’s four years and a half--four years and a half.", "그 아가씨가 열일곱 살을 넘지는 않았을 거야— 많아야 열여덟이라고 치자."], ["She can’t be more than seventeen--say eighteen at the outside.", "그러면 그가 떠날 땐 아가씨가 아직 어린애였겠지."], ["She was quite a child when he went away, then.", "내가 보기엔 그는 아가씨에 대해 아무것도 모르고 본 적도 없을 테니, 나한테 알려 줄 게 없을 거야."], ["I should say he knew nothing about her and had never seen her, so HE can give me no information.", "어쨌든,\" 니콜라스는 자기 마음속 진짜 핵심에 이르러 생각했다."], ["At all events,’ thought Nicholas, coming to the real point in his mind, ‘there can be no danger of any prior occupation of her affections in that quarter; that’s quite clear.’", "\"그쪽에서 그녀의 마음을 먼저 차지했을 위험은 없는 거지. 그건 아주 분명해.\""]], [["Is selfishness a necessary ingredient in the composition of that passion called love, or does it deserve all the fine things which poets, in the exercise of their undoubted vocation, have said of it?", "이기심이란 사랑이라 불리는 그 열정의 성분에 꼭 들어가야 하는 것일까, 아니면 사랑은 시인들이 의심할 바 없는 그들의 소명을 다하며 갖다 붙인 그 온갖 아름다운 말을 다 누릴 자격이 있는 것일까? 분명, 더없이 고결한 정황에서 신사들이 훌륭한 연적에게 숙녀를 양보하고 숙녀들이 신사를 양보한 진짜 사례들이 있긴 하다."], ["There are, no doubt, authenticated instances of gentlemen having given up ladies and ladies having given up gentlemen to meritorious rivals, under circumstances of great high-mindedness; but is it quite established that the majority of such ladies and gentlemen have not made a virtue of necessity, and nobly resigned what was beyond their reach; as a private soldier might register a vow never to accept the order of the Garter, or a poor curate of great piety and learning, but of no family--save a very large family of children--might renounce a bishopric?", "하지만 그런 숙녀와 신사 대다수가 어쩔 수 없는 일을 미덕으로 둔갑시켜, 어차피 자기 손에 닿지 않는 것을 고결하게 단념한 게 아니라고 정말 확실히 단정할 수 있을까? 사병이 결코 가터 훈장을 받지 않겠노라 맹세하는 것처럼, 또는 신앙심 깊고 학식 높지만 집안이라곤 — 그저 자식들만 잔뜩 거느린 큰 식구 말고는 — 변변찮은 가난한 부목사가 주교 자리를 마다하는 것처럼 말이다."]], [["Here was Nicholas Nickleby, who would have scorned the thought of counting how the chances stood of his rising in favour or fortune with the brothers Cheeryble, now that their nephew had returned, already deep in calculations whether that same nephew was likely to rival him in the affections of the fair unknown--discussing the matter with himself too, as gravely as if, with that one exception, it were all settled; and recurring to the subject again and again, and feeling quite indignant and ill-used at the notion of anybody else making love to one with whom he had never exchanged a word in all his life. To be sure, he exaggerated rather than depreciated the merits of his new acquaintance; but still he took it as a kind of personal offence that he should have any merits at all--in the eyes of this particular young lady, that is; for elsewhere he was quite welcome to have as many as he pleased. There was undoubted selfishness in all this, and yet Nicholas was of a most free and generous nature, with as few mean or sordid thoughts, perhaps, as ever fell to the lot of any man; and there is no reason to suppose that, being in love, he felt and thought differently from other people in the like sublime condition.", "여기 니콜라스 니클비를 보라. 그는 조카가 돌아온 지금 치어리블 형제의 총애나 재산에서 자기 처지가 나아질 가능성을 따져 본다는 생각 따위는 경멸했을 사람이건만, 어느새 그 조카가 이름 모를 그 미인의 마음을 두고 자기와 겨룰 만한지 깊이 계산하고 있었다. 그것도 그 한 가지만 빼면 다 정해진 일인 양 진지하게 혼자 그 문제를 따져 보고, 그 주제로 거듭거듭 되돌아가면서, 평생 단 한마디도 나눠 본 적 없는 사람한테 다른 누군가가 구애한다는 생각에 잔뜩 분개하며 부당한 일을 당한 기분까지 느꼈다. 사실 그는 새로 사귄 사람의 장점을 깎아내리기는커녕 오히려 부풀렸다. 그러면서도 그 사람한테 장점이라는 게 조금이라도 있다는 것을 — 그러니까 이 특정한 젊은 아가씨의 눈에 그렇다는 것을 — 일종의 개인적인 모욕으로 받아들였다. 다른 데서야 그가 원하는 만큼 장점을 얼마든지 가져도 좋았지만 말이다. 이 모든 데에 의심할 바 없는 이기심이 있었다. 그러나 니콜라스는 더없이 자유롭고 너그러운 천성을 지닌 사람이었고, 아마 어느 누구 못지않게 천하거나 더러운 생각이 적은 사람이었다. 그러니 사랑에 빠진 그가 같은 숭고한 처지에 놓인 다른 사람들과 다르게 느끼고 생각했으리라고 볼 까닭은 없다."]], [["He did not stop to set on foot an inquiry into his train of thought or state of feeling, however; but went thinking on all the way home, and continued to dream on in the same strain all night.", "하지만 그는 멈춰 서서 자기 생각의 갈피나 감정의 상태를 따져 보지는 않았다. 그저 집으로 가는 내내 그 생각에 잠겨 있었고, 밤새 같은 가락으로 꿈꾸듯 이어 갔다."], ["For, having satisfied himself that Frank Cheeryble could have no knowledge of, or acquaintance with, the mysterious young lady, it began to occur to him that even he himself might never see her again; upon which hypothesis he built up a very ingenious succession of tormenting ideas which answered his purpose even better than the vision of Mr.", "프랭크 치어리블이 그 수수께끼 같은 아가씨에 대해 알 리도, 아는 사이일 리도 없다고 스스로 납득하고 나니, 이번엔 자기마저 그녀를 영영 다시 못 볼지도 모른다는 생각이 들기 시작했다."], ["Frank Cheeryble, and tantalised and worried him, waking and sleeping.", "그 가정 위에 그는 아주 교묘하게 줄을 잇는 괴로운 상념들을 쌓아 올렸는데, 그것들은 프랭크 치어리블 씨라는 환영보다 오히려 그 목적에 더 잘 들어맞아, 깨어 있을 때나 잠들었을 때나 그를 애태우고 들볶았다."]], [["Notwithstanding all that has been said and sung to the contrary, there is no well-established case of morning having either deferred or hastened its approach by the term of an hour or so for the mere gratification of a splenetic feeling against some unoffending lover: the sun having, in the discharge of his public duty, as the books of precedent report, invariably risen according to the almanacs, and without suffering himself to be swayed by any private considerations.", "그와 반대로 아무리 많은 말과 노래가 있었다 한들, 아침이 그저 어떤 죄 없는 연인에게 심술을 부려 흡족함을 누리겠다고 한 시간쯤 그 다가옴을 늦추거나 앞당긴 적이 있다는, 제대로 입증된 사례는 하나도 없다. 옛 기록서들이 전하는 바에 따르면, 해는 자기 공적인 임무를 다하느라 어김없이 책력대로 떠올랐고, 결코 어떤 사사로운 고려에 휘둘리는 법이 없었다."], ["So, morning came as usual, and with it business-hours, and with them Mr Frank Cheeryble, and with him a long train of smiles and welcomes from the worthy brothers, and a more grave and clerk-like, but scarcely less hearty reception from Mr. Timothy Linkinwater.", "그리하여 아침은 여느 때처럼 찾아왔고, 그와 함께 업무 시간이, 그와 함께 프랭크 치어리블 씨가, 그와 함께 그 훌륭한 형제의 길게 이어지는 미소와 환대가, 그리고 티모시 링컨워터 씨의 좀 더 진중하고 사무원다운, 그러면서도 그에 못지않게 진심 어린 맞이가 따라왔다."]], [["‘That Mr.", "\"프랭크 도련님하고 니클비 씨가 어젯밤에 만났다는 게 말이오,\""], ["Frank and Mr.", "팀 링컨워터가 천천히 걸상에서 내려와, 뭔가 아주 특별한 할 말이 있을 때면 으레 그러듯 책상에 등을 딱 붙이고 서서 회계실을 둘러보며 말했다."], ["Nickleby should have met last night,’ said Tim Linkinwater, getting slowly off his stool, and looking round the counting-house with his back planted against the desk, as was his custom when he had anything very particular to say: ‘that those two young men should have met last night in that manner is, I say, a coincidence, a remarkable coincidence.", "\"그 두 젊은이가 어젯밤 그런 식으로 만났다니, 이건 말이오, 우연이오, 놀라운 우연이란 말이지. 아니, 내 단언하건대,\""], ["Why, I don’t believe now,’ added Tim, taking off his spectacles, and smiling as with gentle pride, ‘that there’s such a place in all the world for coincidences as London is!’", "팀이 안경을 벗고 잔잔한 자부심에 차서 미소 지으며 덧붙였다. \"온 세상에 런던만큼 우연이 잘 일어나는 곳은 없을 거요!\""]], [["‘I don’t know about that,’ said Mr.", "\"글쎄, 그건 모르겠는데요.\" 프랭크 씨가 말했다."], ["Frank; ‘but--’", "\"하지만—\""]], [["‘Don’t know about it, Mr.", "\"모르겠다니요, 프랜시스 도련님!\""], ["Francis!’ interrupted Tim, with an obstinate air.", "팀이 고집스러운 기색으로 말을 끊었다."], ["‘Well, but let us know.", "\"그래, 어디 좀 압시다."], ["If there is any better place for such things, where is it?", "그런 일에 런던보다 나은 데가 있으면, 그게 어디요?"], ["Is it in Europe?", "유럽에 있소?"], ["No, that it isn’t.", "아니, 없지."], ["Is it in Asia?", "아시아에 있소?"], ["Why, of course it’s not.", "그야 당연히 없고말고."], ["Is it in Africa?", "아프리카에 있소?"], ["Not a bit of it.", "어림도 없지."], ["Is it in America?", "아메리카에 있소?"], ["YOU know better than that, at all events.", "그건 도련님이 더 잘 아실 테고요. 자, 그럼,\""], ["Well, then,’ said Tim, folding his arms resolutely, ‘where is it?’", "팀이 단호하게 팔짱을 끼며 말했다. \"대체 어디 있단 말이오?\""]], [["‘I was not about to dispute the point, Tim,’ said young Cheeryble, laughing.", "\"그 점을 따지려던 게 아니었어요, 팀.\" 젊은 치어리블이 웃으며 말했다."], ["‘I am not such a heretic as that.", "\"저도 그 정도로 이단은 아니랍니다."], ["All I was going to say was, that I hold myself under an obligation to the coincidence, that’s all.’", "제가 하려던 말은 그저, 제가 그 우연에 신세를 졌다고 여긴다는 것뿐이에요.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["if you don’t dispute it,’ said Tim, quite satisfied, ‘that’s another thing.", "따지려는 게 아니라면,\" 팀이 아주 흡족해하며 말했다."], ["I’ll tell you what though.", "\"그건 또 다른 얘기지요. 그래도 한마디 합시다."], ["I wish you had.", "도련님이 따졌더라면 좋았을 텐데."], ["I wish you or anybody would.", "도련님이든 누구든 따져 줬으면 좋겠단 말이오. 내가 그 사람을 아주 코를 납작하게 만들어 줄 텐데,\""], ["I would so put that man down,’ said Tim, tapping the forefinger of his left hand emphatically with his spectacles, ‘so put that man down by argument--’", "팀이 안경으로 왼손 집게손가락을 힘주어 톡톡 두드리며 말했다. \"논리로 그 사람을 아주 코를 납작하게—\""]], [["It was quite impossible to find language to express the degree of mental prostration to which such an adventurous wight would be reduced in the keen encounter with Tim Linkinwater, so Tim gave up the rest of his declaration in pure lack of words, and mounted his stool again.", "그렇게 무모한 작자가 팀 링컨워터와의 날 선 맞대결에서 얼마나 정신적으로 무너져 내릴지를 표현할 말이라곤 도무지 찾을 수가 없었다. 그래서 팀은 그저 말이 모자란 탓에 선언의 나머지를 포기하고는 다시 걸상에 올라앉았다."]], [["‘We may consider ourselves, brother Ned,’ said Charles, after he had patted Tim Linkinwater approvingly on the back, ‘very fortunate in having two such young men about us as our nephew Frank and Mr.", "\"에드 동생, 우리는,\""], ["Nickleby.", "찰스가 팀 링컨워터의 등을 칭찬하듯 토닥인 뒤에 말했다."], ["It should be a source of great satisfaction and pleasure to us.’", "\"조카 프랭크하고 니클비 씨 같은 두 젊은이를 곁에 두었으니 정말 운이 좋다고 여겨도 되겠구나. 이건 우리한테 크나큰 만족과 기쁨의 샘이 되어야 마땅하지.\""]], [["‘Certainly, Charles, certainly,’ returned the other.", "\"그럼요, 찰스 형님, 그럼요.\" 다른 한 사람이 받았다."]], [["‘Of Tim,’ added brother Ned, ‘I say nothing whatever, because Tim is a mere child--an infant--a nobody that we never think of or take into account at all. Tim, you villain, what do you say to that, sir?’", "\"팀에 대해선,\" 에드 동생이 덧붙였다. \"내 아무 말도 하지 않겠소. 팀은 그저 어린애니까— 갓난애지— 우리가 도무지 생각도 안 하고 셈에도 안 넣는 아무것도 아닌 사람이거든. 팀, 이 악당아, 거기 대해 뭐라고 할 텐가, 응?\""]], [["‘I am jealous of both of ‘em,’ said Tim, ‘and mean to look out for another situation; so provide yourselves, gentlemen, if you please.’", "\"전 두 사람 다 질투가 나서요,\" 팀이 말했다. \"다른 일자리를 알아볼 작정입니다. 그러니 두 분께서도 알아서 사람을 구해 두시지요, 괜찮으시다면요.\""]], [["Tim thought this such an exquisite, unparalleled, and most extraordinary joke, that he laid his pen upon the inkstand, and rather tumbling off his stool than getting down with his usual deliberation, laughed till he was quite faint, shaking his head all the time so that little particles of powder flew palpably about the office.", "팀은 이게 어찌나 절묘하고 둘도 없고 더없이 기막힌 농담이라고 여겼던지, 펜을 잉크병 위에 내려놓고는 여느 때처럼 신중하게 내려오는 게 아니라 걸상에서 거의 굴러떨어지다시피 하며, 기진맥진할 때까지 웃어 댔다. 그러면서 줄곧 고개를 흔들어 대니 머릿가루 부스러기가 사무실 안에 눈에 띄게 풀풀 날렸다."], ["Nor were the brothers at all behind-hand, for they laughed almost as heartily at the ludicrous idea of any voluntary separation between themselves and old Tim. Nicholas and Mr.", "형제도 결코 뒤지지 않았으니, 자기들과 늙은 팀이 자진해서 헤어진다는 그 우스꽝스러운 발상에 거의 그만큼이나 진심으로 웃어 댔다."], ["Frank laughed quite boisterously, perhaps to conceal some other emotion awakened by this little incident, (and so, indeed, did the three old fellows after the first burst,) so perhaps there was as much keen enjoyment and relish in that laugh, altogether, as the politest assembly ever derived from the most poignant witticism uttered at any one person’s expense.", "니콜라스와 프랭크 씨도 아주 떠들썩하게 웃었는데, 아마 이 작은 소동이 불러일으킨 다른 어떤 감정을 감추려는 것이었으리라 (사실 세 노인도 처음 한바탕 웃고 난 뒤에는 그랬다). 그러니 어쩌면 그 웃음 속에는, 어느 한 사람을 겨냥해 던진 더없이 신랄한 재담에서 아무리 점잖은 모임이 끌어냈을 즐거움 못지않은 날카로운 즐거움과 흥취가 통틀어 깃들어 있었는지도 모른다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"니클비 씨,\""], ["Nickleby,’ said brother Charles, calling him aside, and taking him kindly by the hand, ‘I--I--am anxious, my dear sir, to see that you are properly and comfortably settled in the cottage.", "찰스 형이 그를 한쪽으로 부르더니 다정하게 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"내가— 내가 말이오, 니클비 씨, 자네가 그 오두막에 제대로 편안히 자리 잡았는지 보고 싶어 안달이라네."], ["We cannot allow those who serve us well to labour under any privation or discomfort that it is in our power to remove.", "우리를 위해 그렇게 잘 일해 주는 사람이, 우리 힘으로 없애 줄 수 있는 어떤 궁핍이나 불편을 겪게 둘 수야 없지."], ["I wish, too, to see your mother and sister: to know them, Mr.", "그리고 자네 어머님과 누이도 뵙고 싶고, 그분들과 알고 지내고 싶다네, 니클비 씨."], ["Nickleby, and have an opportunity of relieving their minds by assuring them that any trifling service we have been able to do them is a great deal more than repaid by the zeal and ardour you display.--Not a word, my dear sir, I beg.", "또 우리가 그분들께 해 드린 자그마한 도움이라는 게 자네가 보여 주는 열의와 열성으로 차고 넘치게 되갚아졌다고 안심시켜, 그분들 마음을 덜어 드릴 기회도 갖고 싶고 말일세. — 한마디도 마시게, 니클비 씨, 부탁이네."], ["Tomorrow is Sunday.", "내일이 일요일이지. 내 염치 불고하고 차 마실 시간에 나가 보겠네."], ["I shall make bold to come out at teatime, and take the chance of finding you at home; if you are not, you know, or the ladies should feel a delicacy in being intruded on, and would rather not be known to me just now, why I can come again another time, any other time would do for me.", "그래서 자네가 집에 있나 한번 보지. 만약 자네가 없거나, 아니면 부인들께서 불쑥 찾아오는 게 거북해서 지금은 나한테 알려지고 싶지 않으시다면, 그땐 또 다른 날에 오면 되니까."], ["Let it remain upon that understanding.", "나야 아무 때라도 괜찮다네. 그렇게 알고 있기로 하지."], ["Brother Ned, my dear fellow, let me have a word with you this way.’", "에드 동생, 이보게, 이쪽으로 와서 잠깐 얘기 좀 하세.\""]], [["The twins went out of the office arm-in-arm, and Nicholas, who saw in this act of kindness, and many others of which he had been the subject that morning, only so many delicate renewals on the arrival of their nephew of the kind assurance which the brothers had given him in his absence, could scarcely feel sufficient admiration and gratitude for such extraordinary consideration.", "쌍둥이 형제는 팔짱을 끼고 사무실을 나섰다. 니콜라스는 이 친절한 처사와, 그날 아침 자기가 받은 다른 여러 친절에서, 그저 조카가 돌아온 마당에 형제가 자기 없는 자리에서 해 주었던 그 다정한 약속을 곱게 새로이 되새겨 주는 것일 뿐이라고 여겼다. 그러니 이렇게 유별난 배려에 아무리 감탄하고 고마워해도 모자랄 지경이었다."]], [["The intelligence that they were to have a visitor--and such a visitor--next day, awakened in the breast of Mrs.", "다음 날 손님이, 그것도 그런 손님이 온다는 소식은 니클비 부인의 가슴속에 환희와 아쉬움이 뒤섞인 감정을 불러일으켰다. 한편으로는 자기가 곧 좋은 사교계로, 또 거의 잊고 지내던 아침 방문과 저녁 차 모임의 즐거움으로 되돌아갈 징조라며 반겼지만, 다른 한편으로는 쓰라린 마음으로 떠올리지 않을 수 없었던 것이다."], ["Nickleby mingled feelings of exultation and regret; for whereas on the one hand she hailed it as an omen of her speedy restoration to good society and the almost-forgotten pleasures of morning calls and evening tea-drinkings, she could not, on the other, but reflect with bitterness of spirit on the absence of a silver teapot with an ivory knob on the lid, and a milk-jug to match, which had been the pride of her heart in days of yore, and had been kept from year’s end to year’s end wrapped up in wash-leather on a certain top shelf which now presented itself in lively colours to her sorrowing imagination.", "뚜껑에 상아 손잡이가 달린 은제 찻주전자와 그에 어울리는 우유 단지가 이제 없다는 사실을. 그것들은 옛 시절 그녀의 자랑거리였고, 한 해가 다 가도록 무두질한 가죽에 싸여 어떤 맨 위 선반에 모셔져 있었는데, 그 선반이 이제 슬픔에 잠긴 그녀의 상상 속에 또렷한 빛깔로 떠올랐다."]], [["‘I wonder who’s got that spice-box,’ said Mrs.", "\"그 양념통은 누구 손에 들어갔을까.\""], ["Nickleby, shaking her head.", "니클비 부인이 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["‘It used to stand in the left-hand corner, next but two to the pickled onions.", "\"왼쪽 구석에, 절인 양파에서 두 칸 옆에 놓여 있었지."], ["You remember that spice-box, Kate?’", "그 양념통 기억나니, 케이트?\""]], [["‘Perfectly well, mama.’", "\"아주 잘 기억나요, 엄마.\""]], [["‘I shouldn’t think you did, Kate,’ returned Mrs.", "\"넌 기억 안 날 것 같은데, 케이트.\""], ["Nickleby, in a severe manner, ‘talking about it in that cold and unfeeling way!", "니클비 부인이 매서운 투로 받았다. \"그렇게 차갑고 무덤덤하게 말하는 걸 보니!"], ["If there is any one thing that vexes me in these losses more than the losses themselves, I do protest and declare,’ said Mrs.", "이런 잃어버린 것들 때문에 정작 그 물건을 잃은 것보다 더 속이 상하는 게 한 가지 있다면, 내 똑똑히 따지고 단언하건대,\""], ["Nickleby, rubbing her nose with an impassioned air, ‘that it is to have people about me who take things with such provoking calmness.’", "니클비 부인이 격앙된 기색으로 코를 문지르며 말했다. \"그건 바로 곁에 있는 사람들이 매사를 이렇게 부아가 치밀 만큼 태평하게 받아들이는 거란다.\""]], [["‘My dear mama,’ said Kate, stealing her arm round her mother’s neck, ‘why do you say what I know you cannot seriously mean or think, or why be angry with me for being happy and content? You and Nicholas are left to me, we are together once again, and what regard can I have for a few trifling things of which we never feel the want? When I have seen all the misery and desolation that death can bring, and known the lonesome feeling of being solitary and alone in crowds, and all the agony of separation in grief and poverty when we most needed comfort and support from each other, can you wonder that I look upon this as a place of such delicious quiet and rest, that with you beside me I have nothing to wish for or regret? There was a time, and not long since, when all the comforts of our old home did come back upon me, I own, very often--oftener than you would think perhaps--but I affected to care nothing for them, in the hope that you would so be brought to regret them the less. I was not insensible, indeed. I might have felt happier if I had been. Dear mama,’ said Kate, in great agitation, ‘I know no difference between this home and that in which we were all so happy for so many years, except that the kindest and gentlest heart that ever ached on earth has passed in peace to heaven.’", "\"엄마,\" 케이트가 슬며시 팔을 어머니 목에 두르며 말했다. \"엄마가 진심으로 그렇게 생각하지도, 그러려는 것도 아닌 줄 빤히 아는데 왜 그런 말씀을 하세요? 또 제가 행복하고 만족해한다고 왜 화를 내세요? 저한텐 엄마와 니콜라스 오빠가 남아 있고, 우리가 다시 함께 있게 되었잖아요. 그러니 평소에 아쉬울 일도 없는 자질구레한 물건 몇 개에 제가 무슨 미련을 두겠어요? 죽음이 가져올 수 있는 온갖 비참함과 황폐함을 다 겪어 보고, 사람들 틈에서도 외톨이로 홀로인 그 쓸쓸함을 알아 보고, 또 서로의 위로와 의지가 가장 절실하던 때에 슬픔과 가난 속에서 헤어져야 했던 그 모든 고통을 겪고 나서, 제가 이곳을 이토록 달콤한 고요와 안식의 자리로 여기는 게, 엄마가 곁에 있으니 더 바랄 것도 후회할 것도 없다고 여기는 게 그렇게 이상한가요? 한때, 그것도 그리 오래지 않은 때엔, 옛집의 그 온갖 안락함이 정말 자꾸자꾸 제게 되살아났어요, 솔직히요— 엄마가 생각하시는 것보다 더 자주요. 하지만 저는 아무렇지 않은 척했어요. 그러면 엄마가 그것들을 덜 아쉬워하시게 될까 싶어서요. 정말이지 저도 무덤덤하진 않았어요. 차라리 무덤덤했다면 더 행복했을지도 모르죠. 사랑하는 엄마,\" 케이트가 몹시 격해져서 말했다. \"저는 이 집과, 우리 모두가 그토록 오랜 세월 행복했던 그 집 사이에 아무런 차이도 모르겠어요. 다만 이 세상에서 아파 본 그 어떤 마음보다도 가장 다정하고 가장 따스했던 그 마음이 평안히 천국으로 가셨다는 것만 빼고요.\""]], [["‘Kate my dear, Kate,’ cried Mrs.", "\"케이트, 우리 케이트.\""], ["Nickleby, folding her in her arms.", "니클비 부인이 딸을 품에 끌어안으며 외쳤다."]], [["‘I have so often thought,’ sobbed Kate, ‘of all his kind words--of the last time he looked into my little room, as he passed upstairs to bed, and said “God bless you, darling.” There was a paleness in his face, mama--the broken heart--I know it was--I little thought so--then--’", "\"전 너무 자주 생각했어요,\" 케이트가 흐느꼈다. \"아빠의 그 다정한 말들을 전부요— 아빠가 침실로 올라가시면서 마지막으로 제 작은 방을 들여다보고 '잘 자거라, 우리 딸' 하고 말씀하시던 그 마지막 순간을요. 아빠 얼굴이 창백했어요, 엄마— 그 무너진 가슴이— 그런 거였어요— 그때는— 그런 줄 몰랐지만요—\""]], [["A gush of tears came to her relief, and Kate laid her head upon her mother’s breast, and wept like a little child.", "울음이 왈칵 터져 나와 케이트의 마음을 풀어 주었고, 케이트는 어머니의 가슴에 머리를 묻고 어린아이처럼 흐느껴 울었다."]], [["It is an exquisite and beautiful thing in our nature, that when the heart is touched and softened by some tranquil happiness or affectionate feeling, the memory of the dead comes over it most powerfully and irresistibly.", "우리 본성에는 더없이 절묘하고 아름다운 점이 있으니, 어떤 잔잔한 행복이나 애틋한 정에 마음이 닿아 누그러질 때면, 세상을 떠난 이들에 대한 기억이 더없이 강하게, 거역할 수 없이 밀려든다는 것이다."], ["It would almost seem as though our better thoughts and sympathies were charms, in virtue of which the soul is enabled to hold some vague and mysterious intercourse with the spirits of those whom we dearly loved in life.", "마치 우리의 더 선한 생각과 마음씀이 일종의 주문이라서, 그 힘으로 영혼이 우리가 살아생전 애틋이 사랑했던 이들의 혼령과 어떤 어렴풋하고 신비로운 교감을 나눌 수 있게 되는 것만 같다."], ["Alas! how often and how long may those patient angels hover above us, watching for the spell which is so seldom uttered, and so soon forgotten!", "아아, 그 참을성 있는 천사들은 얼마나 자주, 또 얼마나 오래도록 우리 머리 위를 맴돌며, 그토록 좀처럼 읊어지지 않고 그토록 금세 잊히고 마는 그 주문을 기다리고 있을 것인가!"]], [["Poor Mrs. Nickleby, accustomed to give ready utterance to whatever came uppermost in her mind, had never conceived the possibility of her daughter’s dwelling upon these thoughts in secret, the more especially as no hard trial or querulous reproach had ever drawn them from her.", "가엾은 니클비 부인은 마음속에 떠오르는 것이라면 무엇이든 곧장 입 밖에 내는 데 익숙한 사람이라, 딸이 이런 생각들을 가슴속에 남몰래 품고 있을 수 있으리라곤 도무지 헤아려 본 적이 없었다."], ["But now, when the happiness of all that Nicholas had just told them, and of their new and peaceful life, brought these recollections so strongly upon Kate that she could not suppress them, Mrs.", "더구나 무슨 모진 시련이나 푸념 섞인 원망으로 그런 속내가 케이트의 입에서 새어 나온 적도 없었기에 더욱 그랬다."], ["Nickleby began to have a glimmering that she had been rather thoughtless now and then, and was conscious of something like self-reproach as she embraced her daughter, and yielded to the emotions which such a conversation naturally awakened.", "그런데 이제 니콜라스가 방금 들려준 모든 일의 행복과, 그들의 새롭고 평화로운 삶이, 그 기억들을 케이트에게 어찌나 강하게 불러일으켰던지 도저히 억누를 수가 없게 되자, 니클비 부인은 자기가 이따금 좀 생각이 모자랐구나 하는 깨달음이 어렴풋이 들기 시작했다. 그리고 딸을 끌어안고, 그런 대화가 자연히 일깨우는 감정에 자신을 내맡기며, 자책 비슷한 무언가를 느꼈다."]], [["There was a mighty bustle that night, and a vast quantity of preparation for the expected visitor, and a very large nosegay was brought from a gardener’s hard by, and cut up into a number of very small ones, with which Mrs.", "그날 밤엔 굉장한 부산을 떨었고, 오기로 한 손님을 맞을 채비가 엄청났다. 근처 화원에서 아주 커다란 꽃다발을 가져와, 그걸 자잘한 꽃다발 여러 개로 잘라 나누었다."], ["Nickleby would have garnished the little sitting-room, in a style that certainly could not have failed to attract anybody’s attention, if Kate had not offered to spare her the trouble, and arranged them in the prettiest and neatest manner possible.", "니클비 부인은 그것으로 작은 거실을 꾸미려 했는데, 그 솜씨라면 분명 누구의 눈길이든 끌고도 남았을 게 틀림없다. 만약 케이트가 그 수고를 덜어 드리겠다고 나서서, 더없이 예쁘고 정갈하게 손수 꽂아 두지 않았다면 말이다."], ["If the cottage ever looked pretty, it must have been on such a bright and sunshiny day as the next day was. But Smike’s pride in the garden, or Mrs.", "오두막이 예뻐 보인 적이 있다면, 그건 다음 날처럼 그렇게 환하고 햇살 가득한 날이었으리라."], ["Nickleby’s in the condition of the furniture, or Kate’s in everything, was nothing to the pride with which Nicholas looked at Kate herself; and surely the costliest mansion in all England might have found in her beautiful face and graceful form its most exquisite and peerless ornament.", "하지만 정원을 향한 스마이크의 자랑이나, 가구 상태를 향한 니클비 부인의 자랑이나, 매사를 향한 케이트의 자랑도, 케이트 자신을 바라보는 니콜라스의 자랑에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다. 그리고 분명, 잉글랜드에서 가장 값비싼 저택이라 한들, 그녀의 아름다운 얼굴과 우아한 자태에서 더없이 빼어나고 견줄 데 없는 장식을 찾았을 것이다."]], [["About six o’clock in the afternoon Mrs.", "오후 여섯 시쯤, 니클비 부인은 오래 기다리던 문 두드리는 소리에 잔뜩 마음이 들떴다."], ["Nickleby was thrown into a great flutter of spirits by the long-expected knock at the door, nor was this flutter at all composed by the audible tread of two pair of boots in the passage, which Mrs.", "게다가 복도에서 부츠 두 켤레의 또렷한 발소리가 들려오니 그 들뜸은 도무지 가라앉질 않았다. 니클비 부인은 숨죽인 채, 저게 분명 '치어리블 형제 두 분'일 거라고 점쳤다."], ["Nickleby augured, in a breathless state, must be ‘the two Mr. Cheerybles;’ as it certainly was, though not the two Mrs.", "정말 그렇긴 했지만, 니클비 부인이 기다리던 그 두 사람은 아니었다."], ["Nickleby expected, because it was Mr. Charles Cheeryble, and his nephew, Mr.", "찰스 치어리블 씨와 그의 조카 프랭크 씨였으니까."], ["Frank, who made a thousand apologies for his intrusion, which Mrs. Nickleby (having tea-spoons enough and to spare for all) most graciously received.", "프랭크 씨는 불쑥 찾아온 데 대해 수없이 사과했고, 니클비 부인은 (다 같이 쓰고도 남을 만큼 찻숟가락이 넉넉했으므로) 더없이 우아하게 그를 맞았다."], ["Nor did the appearance of this unexpected visitor occasion the least embarrassment, (save in Kate, and that only to the extent of a blush or two at first,) for the old gentleman was so kind and cordial, and the young gentleman imitated him in this respect so well, that the usual stiffness and formality of a first meeting showed no signs of appearing, and Kate really more than once detected herself in the very act of wondering when it was going to begin.", "이 뜻밖의 손님이 나타났다고 해서 (케이트만 빼고, 그것도 처음에 얼굴이 한두 번 붉어지는 정도였지만) 조금도 어색해지지 않았다. 늙은 신사가 어찌나 친절하고 다정한지, 또 젊은 신사가 그 점을 어찌나 잘 흉내 내는지, 첫 만남에 으레 따르는 그 뻣뻣함과 격식이 나타날 기미조차 보이지 않아서, 케이트는 정말이지 한 번 이상이나, 대체 그게 언제 시작되려나 하고 궁금해하고 있는 자기 자신을 알아채곤 했다."]], [["At the tea-table there was plenty of conversation on a great variety of subjects, nor were there wanting jocose matters of discussion, such as they were; for young Mr. Cheeryble’s recent stay in Germany happening to be alluded to, old Mr.", "차 마시는 자리에선 아주 갖가지 주제로 이야기가 넘쳐 났고, 그런대로 우스갯거리 화젯거리도 빠지지 않았다."], ["Cheeryble informed the company that the aforesaid young Mr. Cheeryble was suspected to have fallen deeply in love with the daughter of a certain German burgomaster.", "젊은 치어리블 씨가 최근 독일에 머물렀던 일이 화제에 오르자, 늙은 치어리블 씨가 일동에게 일러 주기를, 앞서 말한 젊은 치어리블 씨가 어느 독일 시장님의 딸과 깊은 사랑에 빠진 게 아닌가 하는 의심을 산다는 것이었다."], ["This accusation young Mr. Cheeryble most indignantly repelled, upon which Mrs.", "이 혐의를 젊은 치어리블 씨가 더없이 분개하며 물리치자, 니클비 부인이 슬쩍 한마디 했다."], ["Nickleby slyly remarked, that she suspected, from the very warmth of the denial, there must be something in it. Young Mr.", "부인하는 그 열기로 미루어 보아 뭔가 있는 게 분명하다는 의심이 든다는 거였다."], ["Cheeryble then earnestly entreated old Mr. Cheeryble to confess that it was all a jest, which old Mr.", "그러자 젊은 치어리블 씨가 늙은 치어리블 씨에게, 다 농담이었다고 고백해 달라고 간곡히 졸랐다."], ["Cheeryble at last did, young Mr. Cheeryble being so much in earnest about it, that--as Mrs.", "늙은 치어리블 씨가 마침내 그렇게 했는데, 젊은 치어리블 씨가 어찌나 진지하게 매달렸던지, 니클비 부인이 나중에 그 장면을 떠올리며 수천 번도 더 말했듯이, 그는 '얼굴이 아주 빨개졌다'."], ["Nickleby said many thousand times afterwards in recalling the scene--he ‘quite coloured,’ which she rightly considered a memorable circumstance, and one worthy of remark, young men not being as a class remarkable for modesty or self-denial, especially when there is a lady in the case, when, if they colour at all, it is rather their practice to colour the story, and not themselves.", "부인은 이를 두고두고 새겨 둘 만하고 언급할 가치가 있는 사건으로 여겼는데, 젊은이들이란 본래 부류로 보아 겸손함이나 자제력으로 이름난 사람들이 아니거니와, 특히 거기 숙녀가 끼어 있을 때는 더더욱 그러하여, 얼굴을 붉히는 일이 있다 해도 대개는 자기가 붉어지기보다 이야기를 부풀려 그럴싸하게 꾸미기 일쑤이기 때문이다."]], [["After tea there was a walk in the garden, and the evening being very fine they strolled out at the garden-gate into some lanes and bye-roads, and sauntered up and down until it grew quite dark.", "차를 마신 뒤엔 정원을 거닐었고, 저녁이 무척 좋았던지라 정원 문을 나서서 몇몇 좁은 길과 샛길로 빠져나가, 아주 어두워질 때까지 이리저리 한가로이 거닐었다. 일행 모두에게 시간이 무척 빨리 흐르는 듯했다."], ["The time seemed to pass very quickly with all the party.", "케이트가 오빠의 팔에 기대어 앞장서서, 오빠와 프랭크 치어리블 씨와 이야기를 나누며 걸었다."], ["Kate went first, leaning upon her brother’s arm, and talking with him and Mr. Frank Cheeryble; and Mrs.", "그리고 니클비 부인과 나이 든 신사가 조금 뒤떨어져 따라갔다."], ["Nickleby and the elder gentleman followed at a short distance, the kindness of the good merchant, his interest in the welfare of Nicholas, and his admiration of Kate, so operating upon the good lady’s feelings, that the usual current of her speech was confined within very narrow and circumscribed limits.", "그 마음씨 좋은 상인의 친절함과, 니콜라스의 앞날에 대한 그의 관심과, 케이트를 향한 그의 감탄이 이 착한 부인의 마음에 어찌나 작용했던지, 평소 거침없이 흐르던 그녀의 말이 아주 좁고 옹색한 테두리 안에 갇혀 버렸다. 스마이크는 (평생 한 번이라도 관심의 대상이 된 적이 있다면, 바로 그날이 그런 날이었다) 그들과 함께하며, 어떤 때는 이쪽 무리에, 어떤 때는 저쪽 무리에 끼었다."], ["Smike (who, if he had ever been an object of interest in his life, had been one that day) accompanied them, joining sometimes one group and sometimes the other, as brother Charles, laying his hand upon his shoulder, bade him walk with him, or Nicholas, looking smilingly round, beckoned him to come and talk with the old friend who understood him best, and who could win a smile into his careworn face when none else could.", "찰스 형이 그의 어깨에 손을 얹고 같이 걷자고 하기도 했고, 니콜라스가 미소 띤 얼굴로 돌아보며, 이리 와서 그를 가장 잘 이해하는 옛 친구와 이야기하자고 손짓하기도 했기 때문이다. 그 옛 친구는, 다른 누구도 못 할 때 그 시름에 찌든 얼굴에 미소를 끌어낼 줄 아는 사람이었다."]], [["Pride is one of the seven deadly sins; but it cannot be the pride of a mother in her children, for that is a compound of two cardinal virtues--faith and hope.", "자만은 일곱 가지 큰 죄 가운데 하나다. 하지만 그것이 자식을 향한 어머니의 자랑일 수는 없으니, 그것은 두 가지 으뜸가는 미덕, 곧 믿음과 희망이 어우러진 것이기 때문이다."], ["This was the pride which swelled Mrs.", "그날 밤 니클비 부인의 가슴을 벅차오르게 한 것이 바로 이 자랑이었다."], ["Nickleby’s heart that night, and this it was which left upon her face, glistening in the light when they returned home, traces of the most grateful tears she had ever shed.", "그리고 집으로 돌아왔을 때 그녀의 얼굴에, 불빛에 반짝이며 남아 있던 것도 바로 이것이었으니, 그것은 그녀가 여태껏 흘려 본 가장 고마운 눈물의 흔적이었다."]], [["There was a quiet mirth about the little supper, which harmonised exactly with this tone of feeling, and at length the two gentlemen took their leave.", "조촐한 저녁 식사 자리엔 잔잔한 흥겨움이 감돌았는데, 이 감정의 결과 정확히 어우러졌다. 그러다 마침내 두 신사가 작별을 고했다."], ["There was one circumstance in the leave-taking which occasioned a vast deal of smiling and pleasantry, and that was, that Mr Frank Cheeryble offered his hand to Kate twice over, quite forgetting that he had bade her adieu already.", "그 작별 인사에는 한바탕 웃음과 농담을 자아낸 일이 한 가지 있었으니, 바로 프랭크 치어리블 씨가 케이트한테 손을 두 번이나 내밀었다는 것이다. 이미 그녀에게 작별 인사를 했다는 걸 까맣게 잊은 채 말이다."], ["This was held by the elder Mr Cheeryble to be a convincing proof that he was thinking of his German flame, and the jest occasioned immense laughter.", "늙은 치어리블 씨는 이를 두고, 그가 독일의 옛 연인을 생각하고 있다는 확실한 증거라고 했고, 그 농담에 엄청난 웃음이 터졌다."], ["So easy is it to move light hearts.", "가벼운 마음이란 이토록 쉽게 움직이는 법이다."]], [["In short, it was a day of serene and tranquil happiness; and as we all have some bright day--many of us, let us hope, among a crowd of others--to which we revert with particular delight, so this one was often looked back to afterwards, as holding a conspicuous place in the calendar of those who shared it.", "요컨대, 그날은 맑고 고요한 행복의 하루였다. 그리고 우리 모두에게 — 바라건대 우리 가운데 많은 이에게는, 다른 여러 날들 틈에 섞여 — 유난한 기쁨으로 되돌아보는 어떤 환한 날이 있듯이, 이날도 그날을 함께한 이들의 달력 속에서 두드러진 자리를 차지한 날로, 그 뒤로 두고두고 되새겨졌다."]], [["Was there one exception, and that one he who needed to have been most happy?", "단 하나 예외가 있었던가, 그것도 가장 행복해야 마땅했던 바로 그 사람이?"]], [["Who was that who, in the silence of his own chamber, sunk upon his knees to pray as his first friend had taught him, and folding his hands and stretching them wildly in the air, fell upon his face in a passion of bitter grief?", "자기 방의 고요 속에서 무릎을 꿇고, 첫 친구가 가르쳐 준 대로 기도를 올리려다가, 두 손을 모았다가 그것을 허공에 미친 듯이 내뻗고는, 쓰라린 슬픔의 격정에 휩싸여 얼굴을 땅에 묻고 엎드린 그 사람은 누구였던가?"]], [["# CHAPTER 44", "# CHAPTER 44"]], [["Mr. Ralph Nickleby cuts an old Acquaintance.", "세상에는 이런 사람들이 있어."], ["It would also appear from the Contents hereof, that a Joke, even between Husband and Wife, may be sometimes carried too far\nThere are some men who, living with the one object of enriching themselves, no matter by what means, and being perfectly conscious of the baseness and rascality of the means which they will use every day towards this end, affect nevertheless--even to themselves--a high tone of moral rectitude, and shake their heads and sigh over the depravity of the world.", "무슨 수를 써서든 자기 배만 불리겠다는 한 가지 목적으로 살아가면서, 그 목적을 위해 날마다 써먹는 수법이 얼마나 비열하고 못된 짓인지 자기들도 똑똑히 알고 있지. 그런데도 겉으로는 — 심지어 자기 자신한테까지 — 도덕적으로 아주 올바른 척하면서, 세상이 타락했다고 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 한숨을 쉰단 말이야."], ["Some of the craftiest scoundrels that ever walked this earth, or rather--for walking implies, at least, an erect position and the bearing of a man--that ever crawled and crept through life by its dirtiest and narrowest ways, will gravely jot down in diaries the events of every day, and keep a regular debtor and creditor account with Heaven, which shall always show a floating balance in their own favour.", "이 땅 위를 걸어 다닌, 아니 걷는다는 건 적어도 똑바로 서서 사람답게 처신한다는 뜻이니까, 차라리 가장 더럽고 좁은 길로 평생을 기어 다닌 가장 교활한 악당들 가운데 몇몇은, 날마다 일기장에 그날그날의 일을 진지하게 적어 넣곤 하지. 그리고 하늘과 정식으로 빚지고 빚 받는 장부를 매기는데, 그 장부에는 늘 자기 쪽에 흑자가 떠 있어."], ["Whether this is a gratuitous (the only gratuitous) part of the falsehood and trickery of such men’s lives, or whether they really hope to cheat Heaven itself, and lay up treasure in the next world by the same process which has enabled them to lay up treasure in this--not to question how it is, so it is.", "이게 그런 사람들의 거짓되고 속임수투성이인 삶에서 그나마 거저 하는 짓인지 — 거저 하는 유일한 짓 말이야 — 아니면 정말로 하늘마저 속여서, 이승에서 재물을 쌓게 해 준 바로 그 방법으로 저승에서도 보물을 쌓으려는 건지, 그건 따지지 않기로 하자. 어쨌든 그렇다는 거야."], ["And, doubtless, such book-keeping (like certain autobiographies which have enlightened the world) cannot fail to prove serviceable, in the one respect of sparing the recording Angel some time and labour.", "그리고 그런 장부 매기기는 (세상을 일깨워 준 어떤 자서전들처럼) 한 가지 점에서는 분명 쓸모가 있을 거야. 기록하는 천사의 수고와 시간을 좀 덜어 줄 테니까."]], [["Ralph Nickleby was not a man of this stamp. Stern, unyielding, dogged, and impenetrable, Ralph cared for nothing in life, or beyond it, save the gratification of two passions, avarice, the first and predominant appetite of his nature, and hatred, the second. Affecting to consider himself but a type of all humanity, he was at little pains to conceal his true character from the world in general, and in his own heart he exulted over and cherished every bad design as it had birth. The only scriptural admonition that Ralph Nickleby heeded, in the letter, was ‘know thyself.’ He knew himself well, and choosing to imagine that all mankind were cast in the same mould, hated them; for, though no man hates himself, the coldest among us having too much self-love for that, yet most men unconsciously judge the world from themselves, and it will be very generally found that those who sneer habitually at human nature, and affect to despise it, are among its worst and least pleasant samples.", "랄프 니클비는 그런 부류의 사람이 아니었어. 엄격하고, 고집스럽고, 끈질기고, 속을 도무지 알 수 없는 랄프는, 이승에서든 저승에서든 두 가지 욕망을 채우는 것 말고는 그 무엇에도 관심이 없었지. 하나는 탐욕이었어. 그의 천성에서 가장 먼저, 가장 크게 자리 잡은 욕구였지. 또 하나는 미움이었어. 그는 자기야말로 온 인류의 본보기일 뿐이라고 여기는 척하면서, 자기 본모습을 세상 사람들한테 굳이 감추려 들지도 않았어. 그리고 마음속으로는 못된 꿍꿍이가 떠오를 때마다 그걸 즐기고 아꼈지. 랄프 니클비가 글자 그대로 마음에 새긴 성경 말씀은 딱 하나, \"너 자신을 알라\"였어. 그는 자기 자신을 잘 알았어. 그러고는 온 인류가 다 자기와 같은 틀에서 찍혀 나왔다고 멋대로 생각하며 사람들을 미워했지. 사람은 누구도 자기 자신을 미워하진 않거든. 우리 가운데 가장 차가운 사람조차 그러기엔 자기애가 너무 크니까. 그런데도 대부분의 사람들은 저도 모르게 자기를 잣대 삼아 세상을 판단해. 그래서 흔히 보면, 입버릇처럼 인간 본성을 비웃고 깔보는 척하는 자들이야말로 그 본성의 가장 못나고 가장 정 안 가는 표본이란 말이지."]], [["But the present business of these adventures is with Ralph himself, who stood regarding Newman Noggs with a heavy frown, while that worthy took off his fingerless gloves, and spreading them carefully on the palm of his left hand, and flattening them with his right to take the creases out, proceeded to roll them up with an absent air as if he were utterly regardless of all things else, in the deep interest of the ceremonial.", "그런데 지금 이 이야기에서 다룰 사람은 랄프 본인이야. 랄프는 잔뜩 찌푸린 얼굴로 뉴먼 노그스를 바라보고 서 있었어. 그러는 동안 그 충실한 사내는 손가락 부분이 없는 장갑을 벗더니, 왼손바닥에 조심스레 펼쳐 놓고는 오른손으로 주름을 펴며 꾹꾹 눌렀어. 그러고는 멍하니 그걸 돌돌 마는 거야. 마치 그 의식이 너무나 흥미로워서 다른 건 죄다 안중에 없다는 듯이 말이지."]], [["‘Gone out of town!’ said Ralph, slowly.", "\"마을을 떠났다고!\" 랄프가 천천히 말했어."], ["‘A mistake of yours.", "\"네가 잘못 안 거겠지."], ["Go back again.’", "다시 가 봐.\""]], [["‘No mistake,’ returned Newman.", "\"잘못 안 거 아니에요.\" 뉴먼이 대꾸했어."], ["‘Not even going; gone.’", "\"떠날 참인 것도 아니고, 아예 떠나 버렸다고요.\""]], [["‘Has he turned girl or baby?’ muttered Ralph, with a fretful gesture.", "\"이놈이 계집애가 됐나, 아니면 갓난쟁이가 됐나?\" 랄프가 짜증 섞인 몸짓으로 중얼거렸어."]], [["‘I don’t know,’ said Newman, ‘but he’s gone.’", "\"그건 모르죠.\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"어쨌든 떠났어요.\""]], [["The repetition of the word ‘gone’ seemed to afford Newman Noggs inexpressible delight, in proportion as it annoyed Ralph Nickleby. He uttered the word with a full round emphasis, dwelling upon it as long as he decently could, and when he could hold out no longer without attracting observation, stood gasping it to himself as if even that were a satisfaction.", "뉴먼 노그스는 \"떠났다\"는 말을 되풀이하는 게 이루 말할 수 없이 즐거운 모양이었어. 그 말이 랄프 니클비를 약 올리는 만큼 말이지. 뉴먼은 그 말에 묵직하게 힘을 잔뜩 실어서, 점잖게 봐줄 만한 한도까지 길게 끌었어. 그러고는 더 끌었다간 남이 눈치챌까 싶을 때쯤, 혼자 그 말을 숨죽여 되뇌며 서 있었지. 그것만으로도 만족스럽다는 듯이."]], [["‘And WHERE has he gone?’ said Ralph.", "\"그래서 어디로 갔는데?\" 랄프가 물었어."]], [["‘France,’ replied Newman.", "\"프랑스요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어."], ["‘Danger of another attack of erysipelas--a worse attack--in the head.", "\"단독이 또 도질 위험이 있대요. 이번엔 더 심하게, 머리에요."], ["So the doctors ordered him off.", "그래서 의사들이 떠나라고 했죠."], ["And he’s gone.’", "그래서 떠났어요.\""]], [["‘And Lord Frederick--?’ began Ralph.", "\"그럼 프레더릭 경은—?\" 랄프가 입을 뗐어."]], [["‘He’s gone too,’ replied Newman.", "\"그분도 떠났어요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어."]], [["‘And he carries his drubbing with him, does he?’ said Ralph, turning away; ‘pockets his bruises, and sneaks off without the retaliation of a word, or seeking the smallest reparation!’", "\"그래서 얻어터진 걸 고스란히 안고 가 버렸단 말이지?\" 랄프가 돌아서며 말했어. \"멍든 몸뚱이를 챙겨서, 말 한마디로 앙갚음하지도, 손톱만큼 보상을 받아 내려고도 하지 않고 슬그머니 꽁무니를 빼?\""]], [["‘He’s too ill,’ said Newman.", "\"너무 아프니까요.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."]], [["‘Too ill!’ repeated Ralph.", "\"너무 아파!\" 랄프가 되받았어."], ["‘Why I would have it if I were dying; in that case I should only be the more determined to have it, and that without delay--I mean if I were he.", "\"나라면 죽어 가는 한이 있어도 받아 냈을 거다. 오히려 그럴 때일수록 더 악착같이, 그것도 당장 받아 냈겠지 — 내가 그 사람이라면 말이야."], ["But he’s too ill!", "그런데 너무 아프시다고!"], ["Poor Sir Mulberry!", "가엾은 멀버리 경!"], ["Too ill!’", "너무 아프시대!\""]], [["Uttering these words with supreme contempt and great irritation of manner, Ralph signed hastily to Newman to leave the room; and throwing himself into his chair, beat his foot impatiently upon the ground.", "이 말을 더없는 경멸과 잔뜩 약 오른 태도로 내뱉고 나서, 랄프는 뉴먼한테 방에서 나가라고 황급히 손짓했어. 그러고는 의자에 털썩 몸을 던지더니, 초조하게 바닥을 발로 탁탁 굴렀지."]], [["‘There is some spell about that boy,’ said Ralph, grinding his teeth.", "\"그 녀석한테는 무슨 마법 같은 게 있어.\" 랄프가 이를 갈며 말했어."], ["‘Circumstances conspire to help him.", "\"온갖 상황이 짜고서 그 녀석을 돕는단 말이야."], ["Talk of fortune’s favours!", "행운의 여신이 베푸는 은혜?"], ["What is even money to such Devil’s luck as this?’", "이런 악마 같은 운수에 비하면 돈 따위가 다 뭐야?\""]], [["He thrust his hands impatiently into his pockets, but notwithstanding his previous reflection there was some consolation there, for his face relaxed a little; and although there was still a deep frown upon the contracted brow, it was one of calculation, and not of disappointment.", "랄프는 짜증스럽게 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣었어. 그런데 방금 그런 생각을 했는데도, 그 안에 무언가 위안거리가 있었던 모양이야. 얼굴이 조금 풀렸거든. 잔뜩 찌푸린 이마에는 여전히 깊은 주름이 잡혀 있었지만, 그건 실망이 아니라 셈을 굴리는 주름이었어."]], [["‘This Hawk will come back, however,’ muttered Ralph; ‘and if I know the man (and I should by this time) his wrath will have lost nothing of its violence in the meanwhile.", "\"하지만 이 호크 녀석은 돌아올 거야.\" 랄프가 중얼거렸어."], ["Obliged to live in retirement--the monotony of a sick-room to a man of his habits--no life--no drink--no play--nothing that he likes and lives by.", "\"내가 그 사람을 안다면 — 이제쯤이면 알 만도 하지 — 그동안 그 분노는 조금도 사그라들지 않았을 테고. 어쩔 수 없이 틀어박혀 지내야 하는 데다, 그런 생활에 익숙한 사람한테 병실의 따분함이라니."], ["He is not likely to forget his obligations to the cause of all this.", "활개 치며 살지도 못하고, 술도 못 마시고, 노름도 못 하고, 좋아하고 그걸로 사는 게 죄다 막혀 버렸으니. 이 모든 일의 원인이 누군지를 잊을 위인이 아니지."], ["Few men would; but he of all others?", "잊을 사람이야 드물겠지만, 하필 그 사람이?"], ["No, no!’", "아니, 아니고말고!\""]], [["He smiled and shook his head, and resting his chin upon his hand, fell a musing, and smiled again.", "랄프는 빙긋 웃고 고개를 저었어. 그러고는 턱을 손에 괴고 생각에 잠겼다가, 다시 웃었지."], ["After a time he rose and rang the bell.", "한참 뒤에 자리에서 일어나 종을 울렸어."]], [["‘That Mr.", "\"그 스퀴어스라는 자, 여기 왔었나?\""], ["Squeers; has he been here?’ said Ralph.", "랄프가 물었어."]], [["‘He was here last night.", "\"어젯밤에 왔었죠."], ["I left him here when I went home,’ returned Newman.", "제가 집에 갈 때 여기 두고 갔는데요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어."]], [["‘I know that, fool, do I not?’ said Ralph, irascibly.", "\"그건 나도 알아, 이 멍청아. 모를까 봐?\""], ["‘Has he been here since?", "랄프가 발끈해서 말했어."], ["Was he here this morning?’", "\"그 뒤에 또 왔냐고. 오늘 아침에 왔어?\""]], [["‘No,’ bawled Newman, in a very loud key.", "\"아뇨.\" 뉴먼이 아주 큰 소리로 외쳤어."]], [["‘If he comes while I am out--he is pretty sure to be here by nine tonight--let him wait.", "\"내가 나가 있는 동안에 오거든 — 오늘 밤 아홉 시면 거의 틀림없이 올 텐데 — 기다리게 해."], ["And if there’s another man with him, as there will be--perhaps,’ said Ralph, checking himself, ‘let him wait too.’", "그리고 같이 온 사람이 또 하나 있을 텐데, 아마—\" 랄프가 말을 끊었다가, \"그 사람도 기다리게 하고.\""]], [["‘Let ‘em both wait?’ said Newman.", "\"둘 다 기다리게 하라고요?\" 뉴먼이 말했어."]], [["‘Ay,’ replied Ralph, turning upon him with an angry look.", "\"그래.\" 랄프가 성난 얼굴로 그를 홱 돌아보며 대답했어."], ["‘Help me on with this spencer, and don’t repeat after me, like a croaking parrot.’", "\"이 짧은 외투나 입혀 줘. 그리고 까악거리는 앵무새처럼 내 말 따라 하지 마.\""]], [["‘I wish I was a parrot,’ Newman, sulkily.", "\"제가 앵무새였으면 좋겠네요.\" 뉴먼이 뾰로통하게 말했어."]], [["‘I wish you were,’ rejoined Ralph, drawing his spencer on; ‘I’d have wrung your neck long ago.’", "\"나도 네가 앵무새였으면 좋겠다.\" 랄프가 외투를 걸치며 받아쳤어. \"그랬으면 진작에 네 모가지를 비틀어 버렸을 텐데.\""]], [["Newman returned no answer to this compliment, but looked over Ralph’s shoulder for an instant, (he was adjusting the collar of the spencer behind, just then,) as if he were strongly disposed to tweak him by the nose.", "뉴먼은 이 칭찬에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았어. 다만 잠깐 랄프의 어깨너머를 들여다봤지."], ["Meeting Ralph’s eye, however, he suddenly recalled his wandering fingers, and rubbed his own red nose with a vehemence quite astonishing.", "(그때 랄프는 외투 뒷깃을 매만지던 참이었어.) 마치 랄프의 코를 확 비틀어 주고 싶어서 안달이 난 듯이 말이야. 그런데 랄프와 눈이 마주치자, 뉴먼은 어슬렁대던 손가락을 얼른 거둬들이고는, 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 거칠게 자기 빨간 코를 문질렀어."]], [["Bestowing no further notice upon his eccentric follower than a threatening look, and an admonition to be careful and make no mistake, Ralph took his hat and gloves, and walked out.", "이 별난 부하한테 위협하는 눈초리 한 번과, 조심해서 실수하지 말라는 당부 말고는 더 신경 쓰지 않은 채, 랄프는 모자와 장갑을 챙겨 들고 밖으로 나갔어."]], [["He appeared to have a very extraordinary and miscellaneous connection, and very odd calls he made, some at great rich houses, and some at small poor ones, but all upon one subject: money. His face was a talisman to the porters and servants of his more dashing clients, and procured him ready admission, though he trudged on foot, and others, who were denied, rattled to the door in carriages. Here he was all softness and cringing civility; his step so light, that it scarcely produced a sound upon the thick carpets; his voice so soft that it was not audible beyond the person to whom it was addressed. But in the poorer habitations Ralph was another man; his boots creaked upon the passage floor as he walked boldly in; his voice was harsh and loud as he demanded the money that was overdue; his threats were coarse and angry. With another class of customers, Ralph was again another man. These were attorneys of more than doubtful reputation, who helped him to new business, or raised fresh profits upon old. With them Ralph was familiar and jocose, humorous upon the topics of the day, and especially pleasant upon bankruptcies and pecuniary difficulties that made good for trade. In short, it would have been difficult to have recognised the same man under these various aspects, but for the bulky leather case full of bills and notes which he drew from his pocket at every house, and the constant repetition of the same complaint, (varied only in tone and style of delivery,) that the world thought him rich, and that perhaps he might be if he had his own; but there was no getting money in when it was once out, either principal or interest, and it was a hard matter to live; even to live from day to day.", "랄프는 아주 어마어마하고도 잡다한 인맥을 가진 듯했어. 그리고 그가 들르는 곳도 참 이상했지. 어떤 데는 으리으리하게 큰 부잣집이었고, 어떤 데는 자그마한 가난한 집이었어. 그래도 용건은 죄다 하나, 돈이었지. 좀 으스대는 고객들의 문지기나 하인들한테는 랄프의 얼굴이 부적이나 마찬가지였어. 그래서 그는 걸어서 터덜터덜 가는데도 척척 들여보내 줬지. 마차를 덜컹대며 문 앞까지 대고 온 다른 사람들은 거절당하는데도 말이야. 이런 데서 랄프는 더없이 부드럽고 굽실거리며 공손했어. 발걸음이 어찌나 가벼운지 두툼한 양탄자 위에서 거의 소리도 안 났고, 목소리가 어찌나 나직한지 상대방 말고는 들리지도 않을 정도였어. 그런데 가난한 집에서는 랄프가 딴사람이 됐지. 복도 마룻바닥에서 그의 구두가 삐걱대며 거침없이 들어섰고, 밀린 돈을 내놓으라고 다그칠 땐 목소리가 거칠고 컸어. 으름장도 험하고 성이 잔뜩 나 있었지. 또 다른 부류의 손님들한테 랄프는 또 딴사람이 됐어. 이들은 평판이 영 미심쩍은 변호사들이었지. 새 일거리를 물어다 주거나, 묵은 일에서 새 이익을 짜내 주는 자들이었어. 이런 자들한테 랄프는 스스럼없고 농담도 잘했어. 요즘 돌아가는 일에 익살을 떨었고, 특히 장사에 도움이 되는 파산이나 돈에 쪼들리는 사정 얘기가 나오면 신이 났지. 한마디로, 이렇게 갖가지로 변하는 모습만 봐서는 같은 사람이라고 알아보기 어려웠을 거야. 다만 두 가지는 한결같았지. 집집마다 주머니에서 어음과 차용증이 가득 든 두툼한 가죽 지갑을 꺼낸다는 것. 그리고 똑같은 푸념을 끊임없이 되풀이한다는 것이었어. (말투와 늘어놓는 방식만 그때그때 달랐지.) 세상은 자기를 부자로 알지만, 받을 걸 다 받았다면 모를까 그렇지도 않다는 거야. 일단 돈이 나가고 나면 원금이든 이자든 도무지 거둬들일 길이 없고, 살아가기가, 하루하루 입에 풀칠하기조차 여간 힘든 게 아니라는 거였지."]], [["It was evening before a long round of such visits (interrupted only by a scanty dinner at an eating-house) terminated at Pimlico, and Ralph walked along St James’s Park, on his way home.", "이렇게 길게 이어진 방문이 (식당에서 변변찮은 저녁을 먹느라 한 번 끊긴 것 말고는) 핌리코에서 끝났을 때는 이미 저녁이었어. 랄프는 집으로 가는 길에 세인트제임스 공원을 따라 걸었지."]], [["There were some deep schemes in his head, as the puckered brow and firmly-set mouth would have abundantly testified, even if they had been unaccompanied by a complete indifference to, or unconsciousness of, the objects about him. So complete was his abstraction, however, that Ralph, usually as quick-sighted as any man, did not observe that he was followed by a shambling figure, which at one time stole behind him with noiseless footsteps, at another crept a few paces before him, and at another glided along by his side; at all times regarding him with an eye so keen, and a look so eager and attentive, that it was more like the expression of an intrusive face in some powerful picture or strongly marked dream, than the scrutiny even of a most interested and anxious observer.", "랄프의 머릿속에는 깊은 꿍꿍이가 몇 가지 들어 있었어. 주름진 이마와 굳게 다문 입만 봐도 그건 넉넉히 알 만했지. 둘레의 것들에는 도통 무관심하다 못해 아예 의식도 못 하는 그 모습이 아니더라도 말이야. 어찌나 깊이 골똘했던지, 평소엔 누구 못지않게 눈썰미가 빠른 랄프가, 뒤를 밟는 비척대는 그림자를 알아채지 못했어. 그 그림자는 어떤 때는 발소리도 없이 랄프 뒤로 슬그머니 따라붙었고, 어떤 때는 그보다 몇 발짝 앞으로 살금살금 나아갔고, 또 어떤 때는 옆에 나란히 미끄러지듯 따라왔지. 그러면서 줄곧 어찌나 날카로운 눈으로, 어찌나 간절하고 빤히 살피는 표정으로 랄프를 쳐다봤는지, 그건 아무리 관심 많고 애타는 관찰자의 살핌이라기보다는, 무슨 강렬한 그림이나 또렷이 각인된 꿈속에 불쑥 끼어든 얼굴의 표정에 더 가까웠어."]], [["The sky had been lowering and dark for some time, and the commencement of a violent storm of rain drove Ralph for shelter to a tree.", "한동안 하늘이 잔뜩 내려앉아 어두컴컴하더니, 사나운 빗줄기가 쏟아지기 시작했어. 그 바람에 랄프는 나무 밑으로 비를 피했지."], ["He was leaning against it with folded arms, still buried in thought, when, happening to raise his eyes, he suddenly met those of a man who, creeping round the trunk, peered into his face with a searching look.", "그가 팔짱을 끼고 나무에 기대선 채 여전히 생각에 잠겨 있는데, 문득 눈을 들었다가, 나무줄기를 돌아 그의 얼굴을 샅샅이 들여다보는 한 사내의 눈과 딱 마주쳤어. 그 순간 이 고리대금업자의 표정에 무언가가 있었던 모양이야."], ["There was something in the usurer’s expression at the moment, which the man appeared to remember well, for it decided him; and stepping close up to Ralph, he pronounced his name.", "사내는 그걸 똑똑히 기억하는 눈치였고, 그 덕에 마음을 굳혔거든. 사내는 랄프에게 바짝 다가서더니 그의 이름을 불렀어."]], [["Astonished for the moment, Ralph fell back a couple of paces and surveyed him from head to foot. A spare, dark, withered man, of about his own age, with a stooping body, and a very sinister face rendered more ill-favoured by hollow and hungry cheeks, deeply sunburnt, and thick black eyebrows, blacker in contrast with the perfect whiteness of his hair; roughly clothed in shabby garments, of a strange and uncouth make; and having about him an indefinable manner of depression and degradation--this, for a moment, was all he saw. But he looked again, and the face and person seemed gradually to grow less strange; to change as he looked, to subside and soften into lineaments that were familiar, until at last they resolved themselves, as if by some strange optical illusion, into those of one whom he had known for many years, and forgotten and lost sight of for nearly as many more.", "랄프는 순간 놀라서 두어 발짝 물러나며 그를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 훑어봤어. 마르고, 거무튀튀하고, 시들어 버린 사내였는데, 나이는 랄프와 엇비슷하고, 몸은 구부정했어. 게다가 아주 음흉한 얼굴이었지. 햇볕에 새카맣게 그을린 데다 홀쭉하고 굶주린 양 볼 때문에 더 험상궂어 보였어. 짙고 검은 눈썹은, 새하얗게 센 머리카락과 대비되어 더 새까매 보였지. 옷차림은 거칠고 후줄근했고, 모양새가 이상하고 어설펐어. 그리고 온몸에 뭐라 꼬집어 말하기 힘든 의기소침과 영락한 기색이 배어 있었지. 한순간엔 이게 그가 본 전부였어. 그런데 다시 들여다보니, 그 얼굴과 모습이 차츰 덜 낯설어지는 거야. 보는 사이에 자꾸 바뀌더니, 누그러지고 부드러워지면서 낯익은 윤곽으로 잡혔어. 그러다 마침내, 무슨 이상한 착시처럼, 여러 해 알고 지냈다가 그만큼이나 오래 잊고 잃어버렸던 어떤 사람의 얼굴로 또렷이 떠오른 거지."]], [["The man saw that the recognition was mutual, and beckoning to Ralph to take his former place under the tree, and not to stand in the falling rain, of which, in his first surprise, he had been quite regardless, addressed him in a hoarse, faint tone.", "사내는 서로 알아봤다는 걸 눈치챘어. 그러고는 랄프에게 다시 나무 밑 아까 그 자리로 들어와, 쏟아지는 빗속에 서 있지 말라고 손짓했어. 정작 자기는 처음 놀란 통에 그 비를 까맣게 잊고 있었지만 말이야. 그러고는 쉰, 가냘픈 목소리로 랄프에게 말을 건넸어."]], [["‘You would hardly have known me from my voice, I suppose, Mr.", "\"제 목소리만 들어선 절 알아보기 힘드셨을 거예요, 니클비 씨.\""], ["Nickleby?’ he said.", "사내가 말했어."]], [["‘No,’ returned Ralph, bending a severe look upon him.", "\"그렇지.\" 랄프가 매서운 눈길을 그에게 던지며 대답했어."], ["‘Though there is something in that, that I remember now.’", "\"그래도 거기에 이제 보니 기억나는 게 좀 있군.\""]], [["‘There is little in me that you can call to mind as having been there eight years ago, I dare say?’ observed the other.", "\"제 모습에서 팔 년 전에도 있던 게 떠오를 만한 건 거의 없겠지요?\" 상대가 말했어."]], [["‘Quite enough,’ said Ralph, carelessly, and averting his face.", "\"충분해.\" 랄프가 무심하게 얼굴을 돌리며 말했어."], ["‘More than enough.’", "\"넘치도록 충분하지.\""]], [["‘If I had remained in doubt about YOU, Mr.", "\"제가 당신을 못 알아봤다 해도요, 니클비 씨.\""], ["Nickleby,’ said the other, ‘this reception, and YOUR manner, would have decided me very soon.’", "사내가 말했어. \"이 푸대접하며 당신 태도를 보니 금방 알아봤을 거예요.\""]], [["‘Did you expect any other?’ asked Ralph, sharply.", "\"다른 대접을 바라기라도 했나?\" 랄프가 쏘아붙였어."]], [["‘No!’ said the man.", "\"아뇨!\" 사내가 말했어."]], [["‘You were right,’ retorted Ralph; ‘and as you feel no surprise, need express none.’", "\"잘 알고 있군.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어. \"그러니 놀랍지도 않을 테고, 새삼 놀란 척할 것도 없겠지.\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"니클비 씨.\" 사내가 잠깐 뜸을 들인 뒤 퉁명스럽게 말했어."], ["Nickleby,’ said the man, bluntly, after a brief pause, during which he had seemed to struggle with an inclination to answer him by some reproach, ‘will you hear a few words that I have to say?’", "그 잠깐 동안 그는 무슨 책망으로 받아치고 싶은 마음과 씨름하는 눈치였지. \"제가 드릴 말씀 몇 마디만 들어 주시겠어요?\""]], [["‘I am obliged to wait here till the rain holds a little,’ said Ralph, looking abroad.", "\"비가 좀 멎을 때까지 여기 기다려야 하는 처지니까.\" 랄프가 먼 데를 바라보며 말했어."], ["‘If you talk, sir, I shall not put my fingers in my ears, though your talking may have as much effect as if I did.’", "\"말을 하겠다면, 손가락으로 귀를 틀어막진 않겠네. 막은 거나 매한가지일 테지만.\""]], [["‘I was once in your confidence--’ thus his companion began.", "\"제가 한때 당신의 신임을 받던—\" 사내가 이렇게 말을 꺼냈어."], ["Ralph looked round, and smiled involuntarily.", "랄프는 돌아보며 저도 모르게 피식 웃었지."]], [["‘Well,’ said the other, ‘as much in your confidence as you ever chose to let anybody be.’", "\"뭐, 그렇죠.\" 사내가 말했어. \"당신이 누구한테든 허락하는 만큼은, 저도 신임을 받았었죠.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ rejoined Ralph, folding his arms; ‘that’s another thing, quite another thing.’", "\"하!\" 랄프가 팔짱을 끼며 받아쳤어. \"그건 또 다른 얘기지, 아주 다른 얘기야.\""]], [["‘Don’t let us play upon words, Mr.", "\"말꼬리 잡고 늘어지진 맙시다, 니클비 씨."], ["Nickleby, in the name of humanity.’", "제발 인정이라도 좀 있게.\""]], [["‘Of what?’ said Ralph.", "\"뭐가 있게?\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Of humanity,’ replied the other, sternly. ‘I am hungry and in want. If the change that you must see in me after so long an absence--must see, for I, upon whom it has come by slow and hard degrees, see it and know it well--will not move you to pity, let the knowledge that bread; not the daily bread of the Lord’s Prayer, which, as it is offered up in cities like this, is understood to include half the luxuries of the world for the rich, and just as much coarse food as will support life for the poor--not that, but bread, a crust of dry hard bread, is beyond my reach today--let that have some weight with you, if nothing else has.’", "\"인정 말이에요.\" 사내가 엄숙하게 대답했어. \"전 굶주리고 궁핍합니다. 이렇게 오래 떨어져 있던 끝에 제게서 보실 수밖에 없는 이 변화가 — 보실 수밖에 없죠, 더디고 모진 단계를 거쳐 그 변화를 겪은 저 자신은 그걸 보고 잘 아니까요 — 그게 당신을 가엾게 여기게 못 한다면, 그럼 이거라도. 빵 말입니다. 주기도문에 나오는 그 일용할 양식 말고요. 이런 도시에서 그 기도를 올릴 땐, 부자한테는 세상 사치의 절반쯤을 다 끼워 넣은 거고, 가난뱅이한테는 목숨이나 부지할 만큼의 거친 음식이나 가리키는 거잖습니까. 그게 아니라, 그냥 빵, 마른 딱딱한 빵 한 조각조차 오늘 제 손에 안 들어옵니다. 다른 건 다 안 통해도, 이거라도 좀 마음에 와닿았으면 합니다.\""]], [["‘If this is the usual form in which you beg, sir,’ said Ralph, ‘you have studied your part well; but if you will take advice from one who knows something of the world and its ways, I should recommend a lower tone; a little lower tone, or you stand a fair chance of being starved in good earnest.’", "\"이게 자네가 늘 동냥하는 식이라면, 이보게.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"배역 공부 한번 잘했군그래. 그래도 세상 물정 좀 아는 사람의 충고를 듣겠다면, 좀 더 수그러진 어조를 권하지. 조금만 더 낮춰. 안 그러면 정말로 쫄쫄 굶어 죽기 딱 좋으니까.\""]], [["As he said this, Ralph clenched his left wrist tightly with his right hand, and inclining his head a little on one side and dropping his chin upon his breast, looked at him whom he addressed with a frowning, sullen face.", "이렇게 말하면서 랄프는 오른손으로 왼쪽 손목을 꽉 움켜쥐고, 고개를 살짝 한쪽으로 기울이고 턱을 가슴에 떨군 채, 찌푸리고 뚱한 얼굴로 상대를 바라봤어."], ["The very picture of a man whom nothing could move or soften.", "그 무엇으로도 흔들거나 누그러뜨릴 수 없는 사내의 본보기 그대로였지."]], [["‘Yesterday was my first day in London,’ said the old man, glancing at his travel-stained dress and worn shoes.", "\"어제가 런던에 온 첫날이었습니다.\" 노인이 여행에 찌든 옷과 닳아빠진 신발을 흘끗 내려다보며 말했어."]], [["‘It would have been better for you, I think, if it had been your last also,’ replied Ralph.", "\"내 생각엔 그게 자네 마지막 날이었으면 더 좋았을 텐데.\" 랄프가 대답했어."]], [["‘I have been seeking you these two days, where I thought you were most likely to be found,’ resumed the other more humbly, ‘and I met you here at last, when I had almost given up the hope of encountering you, Mr Nickleby.’", "\"이틀 동안 당신을 찾아다녔습니다. 당신이 있을 법한 곳들을 짚어 가면서요.\" 사내가 더 비굴하게 말을 이었어. \"그러다 마침내 여기서 당신을 만났네요. 당신을 마주칠 희망을 거의 접었을 때 말입니다, 니클비 씨.\""]], [["He seemed to wait for some reply, but Ralph giving him none, he continued:", "사내는 무슨 대답을 기다리는 눈치였어. 그런데 랄프가 아무 말도 안 하자, 그가 계속 말했어."]], [["‘I am a most miserable and wretched outcast, nearly sixty years old, and as destitute and helpless as a child of six.’", "\"전 더없이 비참하고 가련한 떠돌이입니다. 나이는 예순에 가깝지만, 여섯 살짜리 아이만큼이나 빈털터리에 무력하지요.\""]], [["‘I am sixty years old, too,’ replied Ralph, ‘and am neither destitute nor helpless.", "\"나도 예순일세.\" 랄프가 대답했어."], ["Work.", "\"그래도 빈털터리도 무력하지도 않아."], ["Don’t make fine play-acting speeches about bread, but earn it.’", "일을 해. 빵 타령 하면서 그럴듯한 연극 대사 늘어놓지 말고, 벌어.\""]], [["‘How?’ cried the other.", "\"어떻게요?\" 사내가 부르짖었어."], ["‘Where?", "\"어디서요?"], ["Show me the means.", "방법을 좀 일러 주십쇼."], ["Will you give them to me--will you?’", "그걸 제게 주시겠어요? 주실 거예요?\""]], [["‘I did once,’ replied Ralph, composedly; ‘you scarcely need ask me whether I will again.’", "\"한 번 준 적 있지.\" 랄프가 차분하게 대답했어. \"또 줄 거냐고는 굳이 안 물어봐도 알 텐데.\""]], [["‘It’s twenty years ago, or more,’ said the man, in a suppressed voice, ‘since you and I fell out.", "\"이십 년 전, 아니 그보다 더 됐죠.\" 사내가 억눌린 목소리로 말했어."], ["You remember that?", "\"당신하고 제가 틀어진 게요. 기억하시죠?"], ["I claimed a share in the profits of some business I brought to you, and, as I persisted, you arrested me for an old advance of ten pounds, odd shillings, including interest at fifty per cent, or so.’", "제가 당신한테 물어다 준 어떤 일의 이익을 나눠 달라고 했어요. 그런데 제가 우기니까, 당신은 오래전에 빌려준 십 파운드 얼마에 — 오십 퍼센트쯤 되는 이자까지 쳐서 — 절 잡아 가뒀죠.\""]], [["‘I remember something of it,’ replied Ralph, carelessly.", "\"그 비슷한 일이 기억나는군.\" 랄프가 무심하게 대답했어."], ["‘What then?’", "\"그래서 뭐?\""]], [["‘That didn’t part us,’ said the man.", "\"그걸로 우리가 갈라선 건 아니죠.\" 사내가 말했어."], ["‘I made submission, being on the wrong side of the bolts and bars; and as you were not the made man then that you are now, you were glad enough to take back a clerk who wasn’t over nice, and who knew something of the trade you drove.’", "\"전 빗장과 쇠창살 안에 갇힌 신세였으니 고분고분 굴복했고요. 당신도 그땐 지금처럼 자수성가한 인물이 아니어서, 그리 까다롭지 않고 당신이 하던 장사를 좀 아는 서기를 다시 들이는 게 반가웠을 테죠.\""]], [["‘You begged and prayed, and I consented,’ returned Ralph.", "\"자네가 빌고 또 빌어서, 내가 받아 줬지.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어."], ["‘That was kind of me.", "\"내가 친절했던 거야."], ["Perhaps I did want you.", "아마 자네가 필요하긴 했나 보지. 잊어버렸어."], ["I forget.", "그랬을 거야."], ["I should think I did, or you would have begged in vain.", "안 그랬으면 자넨 빌어도 헛수고였을 테니까. 쓸모가 있었지."], ["You were useful; not too honest, not too delicate, not too nice of hand or heart; but useful.’", "너무 정직하지도 않고, 너무 까다롭지도 않고, 손도 마음도 너무 깔끔하지 않은. 그래도 쓸모는 있었어.\""]], [["‘Useful, indeed!’ said the man.", "\"쓸모라, 그렇겠죠!\" 사내가 말했어."], ["‘Come.", "\"이거 봐요."], ["You had pinched and ground me down for some years before that, but I had served you faithfully up to that time, in spite of all your dog’s usage.", "당신은 그 전에도 몇 해를 절 쥐어짜고 등쳐 먹었어요. 그래도 전 그때까지 충실하게 당신을 섬겼다고요."], ["Had I?’", "그 개 같은 대접을 다 받으면서도 말이죠. 안 그랬습니까?\""]], [["Ralph made no reply.", "랄프는 아무 대꾸도 안 했어."]], [["‘Had I?’ said the man again.", "\"안 그랬냐고요?\" 사내가 다시 말했어."]], [["‘You had had your wages,’ rejoined Ralph, ‘and had done your work. We stood on equal ground so far, and could both cry quits.’", "\"품삯을 받았잖나.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어. \"그리고 자네 일을 했고. 거기까진 우리가 서로 비긴 거지. 둘 다 빚진 거 없다고 할 수 있어.\""]], [["‘Then, but not afterwards,’ said the other.", "\"그땐 그랬죠. 하지만 그 뒤론 아니에요.\" 사내가 말했어."]], [["‘Not afterwards, certainly, nor even then, for (as you have just said) you owed me money, and do still,’ replied Ralph.", "\"그 뒤론 당연히 아니지. 아니, 그때조차 아니야. (방금 자네가 말했듯이) 자넨 나한테 빚이 있었고, 지금도 그러니까.\" 랄프가 대답했어."]], [["‘That’s not all,’ said the man, eagerly.", "\"그게 다가 아니에요.\" 사내가 다급하게 말했어."], ["‘That’s not all.", "\"그게 다가 아니라고요."], ["Mark that.", "잘 들어요."], ["I didn’t forget that old sore, trust me.", "저는 그 묵은 상처를 잊지 않았어요, 정말로요. 한편으론 그게 기억나서, 한편으론 언젠가 그걸로 돈을 벌어 보려는 속셈에서, 전 당신 곁에 있던 처지를 이용해서 당신의 약점을 하나 손에 넣었죠."], ["Partly in remembrance of that, and partly in the hope of making money someday by the scheme, I took advantage of my position about you, and possessed myself of a hold upon you, which you would give half of all you have to know, and never can know but through me.", "당신이 가진 걸 절반이라도 내놓고 알고 싶어 할, 그런데 저를 통하지 않고는 절대 알 수 없는 약점을요. 그러고 나서 전 당신을 떠났어요."], ["I left you--long after that time, remember--and, for some poor trickery that came within the law, but was nothing to what you money-makers daily practise just outside its bounds, was sent away a convict for seven years.", "그게 한참 뒤였다는 거, 기억하세요. 그러다 법에 걸리는 자잘한 속임수를 좀 쳤다가 — 당신네 돈벌이꾼들이 법 테두리 바로 바깥에서 날마다 해 먹는 짓에 비하면 아무것도 아니었지만 — 칠 년 징역살이를 하러 끌려갔죠."], ["I have returned what you see me.", "그러고는 지금 당신이 보는 이 꼴로 돌아왔습니다. 자, 니클비 씨.\""], ["Now, Mr Nickleby,’ said the man, with a strange mixture of humility and sense of power, ‘what help and assistance will you give me; what bribe, to speak out plainly?", "사내가 비굴함과 무슨 힘을 쥐었다는 의식을 묘하게 뒤섞어 말했어. \"제게 무슨 도움을, 무슨 보탬을 주시겠어요?"], ["My expectations are not monstrous, but I must live, and to live I must eat and drink.", "까놓고 말해서, 입막음으로 얼마를 주실 거냐고요? 제 바람이 그리 터무니없진 않습니다."], ["Money is on your side, and hunger and thirst on mine.", "그래도 살아야 하고, 살려면 먹고 마셔야죠. 돈은 당신 쪽에 있고, 굶주림과 목마름은 제 쪽에 있어요."], ["You may drive an easy bargain.’", "흥정하기엔 당신이 유리하지요.\""]], [["‘Is that all?’ said Ralph, still eyeing his companion with the same steady look, and moving nothing but his lips.", "\"그게 단가?\" 랄프가 여전히 같은 흔들림 없는 눈길로 상대를 쏘아보며, 입술만 움직여 말했어."]], [["‘It depends on you, Mr.", "\"그게 단지 아닌지는 당신한테 달렸죠, 니클비 씨.\""], ["Nickleby, whether that’s all or not,’ was the rejoinder.", "사내가 받아쳤어."]], [["‘Why then, harkye, Mr--, I don’t know by what name I am to call you,’ said Ralph.", "\"그렇다면 잘 들어, 그—, 자네를 무슨 이름으로 불러야 할지 모르겠군.\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘By my old one, if you like.’", "\"옛 이름으로 부르시죠, 원하신다면.\""]], [["‘Why then, harkye, Mr.", "\"그렇다면 잘 들어, 브루커 씨.\""], ["Brooker,’ said Ralph, in his harshest accents, ‘and don’t expect to draw another speech from me.", "랄프가 더없이 험한 말투로 말했어. \"그리고 나한테서 또 한바탕 말이 나올 거라곤 기대하지 마."], ["Harkye, sir.", "잘 들어, 이 사람아."], ["I know you of old for a ready scoundrel, but you never had a stout heart; and hard work, with (maybe) chains upon those legs of yours, and shorter food than when I “pinched”", "난 자네가 옛날부터 약삭빠른 악당인 줄은 알았지만, 자네한텐 한 번도 배짱이란 게 없었지. 게다가 모진 노동에다, (어쩌면) 그 다리에 쇠사슬까지 차고, 내가 자넬 '쥐어짜고'"], ["and “ground”", "'등쳐 먹던'"], ["you, has blunted your wits, or you would not come with such a tale as this to me.", "때보다 더 적게 먹었으니, 머리가 무뎌졌나 보군. 안 그러면 나한테 이런 얘길 들고 올 리가 없지."], ["You a hold upon me!", "자네가 내 약점을 쥐었다고!"], ["Keep it, or publish it to the world, if you like.’", "쥐고 있든가, 아니면 세상에 까발리든가, 마음대로 해.\""]], [["‘I can’t do that,’ interposed Brooker.", "\"그건 못 하죠.\" 브루커가 끼어들었어."], ["‘That wouldn’t serve me.’", "\"그래선 제게 득될 게 없으니까요.\""]], [["‘Wouldn’t it?’ said Ralph.", "\"안 돼?\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘It will serve you as much as bringing it to me, I promise you.", "\"나한테 들고 온 거랑 딱 그만큼 득이 될 거다, 장담하지."], ["To be plain with you, I am a careful man, and know my affairs thoroughly.", "솔직히 말하면, 난 빈틈없는 사람이고 내 일은 속속들이 알아."], ["I know the world, and the world knows me.", "난 세상을 알고, 세상도 날 알지."], ["Whatever you gleaned, or heard, or saw, when you served me, the world knows and magnifies already.", "자네가 나를 섬길 적에 주워듣거나 보거나 알아낸 게 뭐든, 세상은 이미 다 알고 부풀려 떠들고 있어. 자네가 세상을 놀라게 할 만한 건 하나도 못 일러 줘."], ["You could tell it nothing that would surprise it, unless, indeed, it redounded to my credit or honour, and then it would scout you for a liar.", "그게 정말 내 신용이나 명예에 보탬이 되는 거라면 모를까, 그땐 오히려 자넬 거짓말쟁이라고 쫓아낼걸. 그런데도 내 장사는 한가하지 않고, 손님들도 까다롭게 굴지 않아."], ["And yet I don’t find business slack, or clients scrupulous.", "오히려 그 반대지. 난 날마다 이놈저놈한테 욕먹고 위협받아.\""], ["Quite the contrary.", "랄프가 말했어."], ["I am reviled or threatened every day by one man or another,’ said Ralph; ‘but things roll on just the same, and I don’t grow poorer either.’", "\"그래도 일은 똑같이 굴러가고, 내가 더 가난해지지도 않지.\""]], [["‘I neither revile nor threaten,’ rejoined the man.", "\"전 욕하지도 위협하지도 않아요.\" 사내가 받아쳤어."], ["‘I can tell you of what you have lost by my act, what I only can restore, and what, if I die without restoring, dies with me, and never can be regained.’", "\"전 당신한테 일러 줄 수 있어요. 제 짓 때문에 당신이 뭘 잃었는지, 오직 저만 되돌려 놓을 수 있는 게 뭔지, 그리고 제가 그걸 안 되돌려 놓고 죽으면 저랑 같이 죽어 영영 되찾을 수 없는 게 뭔지를요.\""]], [["‘I tell my money pretty accurately, and generally keep it in my own custody,’ said Ralph.", "\"난 내 돈을 꽤 정확히 세고, 대개는 내 손으로 간수하지.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘I look sharply after most men that I deal with, and most of all I looked sharply after you.", "\"거래하는 사람들 대부분을 날카롭게 지켜보는데, 그중에서도 자넬 가장 날카롭게 지켜봤어."], ["You are welcome to all you have kept from me.’", "자네가 나한테서 빼돌린 게 있다면, 죄다 자네나 가지라고.\""]], [["‘Are those of your own name dear to you?’ said the man emphatically.", "\"당신과 같은 이름을 쓰는 이들이 소중하긴 하오?\" 사내가 힘주어 말했어."], ["‘If they are--’", "\"만약 그렇다면—\""]], [["‘They are not,’ returned Ralph, exasperated at this perseverance, and the thought of Nicholas, which the last question awakened. ‘They are not. If you had come as a common beggar, I might have thrown a sixpence to you in remembrance of the clever knave you used to be; but since you try to palm these stale tricks upon one you might have known better, I’ll not part with a halfpenny--nor would I to save you from rotting. And remember this, ‘scape-gallows,’ said Ralph, menacing him with his hand, ‘that if we meet again, and you so much as notice me by one begging gesture, you shall see the inside of a jail once more, and tighten this hold upon me in intervals of the hard labour that vagabonds are put to. There’s my answer to your trash. Take it.’", "\"안 소중해.\" 랄프가 대답했어. 이렇게 끈질기게 구는 데다, 마지막 물음이 일깨운 니콜라스 생각에 화가 치밀어서 말이야. \"안 소중하다고. 자네가 그냥 흔한 거지로 왔다면, 옛날의 그 약삭빠른 악당이 떠올라 육 펜스쯤 던져 줬을지도 모르지. 그런데 진작에 더 잘 알았어야 할 사람한테 이런 케케묵은 수작을 떠넘기려 드니, 반 펜스도 안 내놓겠어. 자네가 썩어 문드러지는 걸 막을 수 있대도 안 줘. 그리고 이걸 명심해, 이 교수대를 모면한 놈아.\" 랄프가 그에게 손을 들어 위협하며 말했어. \"우리가 다시 만났을 때, 자네가 동냥하는 몸짓 하나라도 내게 보이기만 해 봐. 그땐 또 한 번 감옥 속을 구경하게 될 테고, 떠돌이한테 시키는 그 모진 노역 틈틈이 내 약점을 더 옥죄게 될 거야. 이게 자네 헛소리에 대한 내 대답이야. 가져가.\""]], [["With a disdainful scowl at the object of his anger, who met his eye but uttered not a word, Ralph walked away at his usual pace, without manifesting the slightest curiosity to see what became of his late companion, or indeed once looking behind him. The man remained on the same spot with his eyes fixed upon his retreating figure until it was lost to view, and then drawing his arm about his chest, as if the damp and lack of food struck coldly to him, lingered with slouching steps by the wayside, and begged of those who passed along.", "랄프는 화풀이 상대를 경멸 어린 눈으로 노려봤어. 사내는 그 눈길을 받으면서도 한마디도 안 했지. 랄프는 늘 그렇듯 평소 걸음으로 걸어갔어. 방금 같이 있던 자가 어찌 되는지 조금도 궁금해하지 않았고, 정말 한 번도 뒤를 돌아보지 않았지. 사내는 그 자리에 그대로 서서, 멀어져 가는 랄프의 모습이 보이지 않을 때까지 눈을 떼지 않았어. 그러고는 축축한 기운과 굶주림이 차갑게 파고드는 듯 팔로 가슴팍을 감싸 안고, 구부정한 걸음으로 길가에서 서성이며 지나가는 사람들한테 동냥했어."]], [["Ralph, in no-wise moved by what had lately passed, further than as he had already expressed himself, walked deliberately on, and turning out of the Park and leaving Golden Square on his right, took his way through some streets at the west end of the town until he arrived in that particular one in which stood the residence of Madame Mantalini. The name of that lady no longer appeared on the flaming door-plate, that of Miss Knag being substituted in its stead; but the bonnets and dresses were still dimly visible in the first-floor windows by the decaying light of a summer’s evening, and excepting this ostensible alteration in the proprietorship, the establishment wore its old appearance.", "랄프는 방금 일어난 일에 조금도 마음이 흔들리지 않았어. 이미 입 밖으로 다 내뱉은 것 말고는 말이야. 그는 또박또박 걸어 나가 공원을 벗어났어. 그리고 골든 스퀘어를 오른쪽으로 두고서, 도시 서쪽 끝의 거리 몇 개를 지나, 망탈리니 부인의 집이 있는 바로 그 거리에 다다랐지. 그 부인의 이름은 이제 번쩍이는 문패에 더 이상 보이지 않았어. 대신 내그 양의 이름이 들어가 있었지. 그래도 모자와 드레스 들은 여전히 이 층 창문 너머로 어렴풋이 보였어. 여름 저녁 스러져 가는 햇빛 속에서 말이야. 그리고 이렇게 겉으로 주인이 바뀐 것 말고는, 그 가게는 옛 모습 그대로였지."]], [["‘Humph!’ muttered Ralph, drawing his hand across his mouth with a connoisseur-like air, and surveying the house from top to bottom; ‘these people look pretty well. They can’t last long; but if I know of their going in good time, I am safe, and a fair profit too. I must keep them closely in view; that’s all.’", "\"흠!\" 랄프가 감정가인 양 손으로 입을 쓱 훔치며 집을 위에서 아래까지 훑어보고는 중얼거렸어. \"이 사람들 꽤 잘되는 것 같군. 오래는 못 가겠지만. 그래도 망하는 때를 미리 알아 두면 난 안전하고, 짭짤한 이익도 챙기겠지. 이 사람들을 바짝 지켜봐야겠어. 그게 다야.\""]], [["So, nodding his head very complacently, Ralph was leaving the spot, when his quick ear caught the sound of a confused noise and hubbub of voices, mingled with a great running up and down stairs, in the very house which had been the subject of his scrutiny; and while he was hesitating whether to knock at the door or listen at the keyhole a little longer, a female servant of Madame Mantalini’s (whom he had often seen) opened it abruptly and bounced out, with her blue cap-ribbons streaming in the air.", "그래서 아주 흡족하게 고개를 끄덕이며 랄프가 그 자리를 막 떠나려는데, 그의 빠른 귀에 무슨 어수선한 소리가 들렸어. 와글거리는 목소리에 계단을 우당탕 오르내리는 소리까지 뒤섞여서 말이야. 그것도 방금 그가 꼼꼼히 살펴보던 바로 그 집에서 나는 거였지. 그가 문을 두드릴까, 아니면 열쇠 구멍에 좀 더 귀를 대고 있을까 망설이는 사이에, 망탈리니 부인네 하녀 하나가 (랄프가 자주 본 아이였어) 문을 벌컥 열고 튀어나왔어. 파란 모자 끈을 바람에 휘날리면서 말이야."]], [["‘Hallo here.", "\"이봐, 거기."], ["Stop!’ cried Ralph.", "멈춰!\" 랄프가 외쳤어."], ["‘What’s the matter?", "\"무슨 일이야?"], ["Here am I.", "나 여기 있다."], ["Didn’t you hear me knock?’", "내가 두드리는 소리 못 들었어?\""]], [["‘Oh! Mr.", "\"어머!"], ["Nickleby, sir,’ said the girl.", "니클비 씨, 나리.\" 하녀가 말했어."], ["‘Go up, for the love of Gracious.", "\"제발 좀 올라가 보세요."], ["Master’s been and done it again.’", "주인 나리가 또 일을 저질렀어요.\""]], [["‘Done what?’ said Ralph, tartly; ‘what d’ye mean?’", "\"뭘 저질렀다는 거야?\" 랄프가 퉁명스럽게 말했어. \"무슨 소리야?\""]], [["‘I knew he would if he was drove to it,’ cried the girl.", "\"이렇게 몰아세우면 저럴 줄 알았어요.\" 하녀가 부르짖었어."], ["‘I said so all along.’", "\"내내 그렇게 말했다고요.\""]], [["‘Come here, you silly wench,’ said Ralph, catching her by the wrist; ‘and don’t carry family matters to the neighbours, destroying the credit of the establishment. Come here; do you hear me, girl?’", "\"이리 와, 이 멍청한 계집애야.\" 랄프가 그녀의 손목을 붙잡으며 말했어. \"그리고 집안일을 이웃에 떠벌려서 가게 신용을 망치지 마. 이리 오라니까. 내 말 들리냐, 얘?\""]], [["Without any further expostulation, he led or rather pulled the frightened handmaid into the house, and shut the door; then bidding her walk upstairs before him, followed without more ceremony.", "랄프는 더 따질 것도 없이, 겁먹은 하녀를 집 안으로 이끌었어. 이끌었다기보다 끌어당겼다는 게 맞겠지. 그러고는 문을 닫았어. 그러고 나서 하녀더러 앞장서서 위층으로 올라가라 이르고는, 별 격식 없이 그 뒤를 따라갔어."]], [["Guided by the noise of a great many voices all talking together, and passing the girl in his impatience, before they had ascended many steps, Ralph quickly reached the private sitting-room, when he was rather amazed by the confused and inexplicable scene in which he suddenly found himself.", "여럿이 한꺼번에 떠들어 대는 소리를 길잡이 삼아, 랄프는 조바심에 몇 계단 못 올라가서 하녀를 앞질렀어. 그러고는 금세 안쪽 거실에 다다랐지. 그런데 거기서 느닷없이 맞닥뜨린 어수선하고 알 수 없는 광경에 적잖이 놀라고 말았어."]], [["There were all the young-lady workers, some with bonnets and some without, in various attitudes expressive of alarm and consternation; some gathered round Madame Mantalini, who was in tears upon one chair; and others round Miss Knag, who was in opposition tears upon another; and others round Mr. Mantalini, who was perhaps the most striking figure in the whole group, for Mr. Mantalini’s legs were extended at full length upon the floor, and his head and shoulders were supported by a very tall footman, who didn’t seem to know what to do with them, and Mr Mantalini’s eyes were closed, and his face was pale and his hair was comparatively straight, and his whiskers and moustache were limp, and his teeth were clenched, and he had a little bottle in his right hand, and a little tea-spoon in his left; and his hands, arms, legs, and shoulders, were all stiff and powerless. And yet Madame Mantalini was not weeping upon the body, but was scolding violently upon her chair; and all this amidst a clamour of tongues perfectly deafening, and which really appeared to have driven the unfortunate footman to the utmost verge of distraction.", "거기엔 가게의 젊은 여공들이 죄다 모여 있었어. 모자를 쓴 애도 있고 안 쓴 애도 있었는데, 다들 놀라고 어쩔 줄 모르는 갖가지 자세였지. 몇몇은 한 의자에 앉아 눈물짓는 망탈리니 부인 둘레에 모여 있었고. 또 몇몇은 맞은편 다른 의자에 앉아 역시 눈물짓는 내그 양 둘레에 모여 있었어. 또 몇몇은 망탈리니 씨 둘레에 모여 있었는데, 이 무리 가운데 어쩌면 가장 눈에 띄는 인물이 바로 그였지. 망탈리니 씨의 두 다리는 바닥에 쭉 뻗어 있었고, 머리와 어깨는 키가 아주 큰 하인이 떠받치고 있었는데, 그 하인은 그걸 어찌해야 할지 모르는 눈치였어. 망탈리니 씨의 두 눈은 감겨 있었고, 얼굴은 핼쑥했고, 머리카락은 평소보다 곧게 펴져 있었어. 구레나룻과 콧수염은 축 늘어졌고, 이는 꽉 악물려 있었지. 오른손엔 작은 병을, 왼손엔 작은 찻숟가락을 쥐고 있었어. 그리고 손이며 팔이며 다리며 어깨가 죄다 뻣뻣하게 굳어 힘이 하나도 없었어. 그런데도 망탈리니 부인은 그 몸뚱이를 붙들고 울기는커녕, 의자에 앉아 거세게 잔소리를 퍼붓고 있었지. 게다가 이 모든 일이 귀가 멍멍할 만큼 떠들썩한 입씨름 한복판에서 벌어지고 있었고, 그 소동은 정말이지 가엾은 하인을 거의 미치기 직전까지 몰아붙인 듯했어."]], [["‘What is the matter here?’ said Ralph, pressing forward.", "\"여기 무슨 일이야?\" 랄프가 앞으로 헤집고 나서며 말했어."]], [["At this inquiry, the clamour was increased twenty-fold, and an astounding string of such shrill contradictions as ‘He’s poisoned himself’--‘He hasn’t’--‘Send for a doctor’--‘Don’t’--‘He’s dying’--‘He isn’t, he’s only pretending’--with various other cries, poured forth with bewildering volubility, until Madame Mantalini was seen to address herself to Ralph, when female curiosity to know what she would say, prevailed, and, as if by general consent, a dead silence, unbroken by a single whisper, instantaneously succeeded.", "이 물음에 소동은 스무 배로 커졌어. 그리고 귀청 떨어질 듯 날카롭게 엇갈리는 말들이 쏟아졌지. \"독약을 먹었어요\"—\"아니에요\"—\"의사를 불러요\"—\"부르지 마요\"—\"죽어 가요\"—\"아니에요, 죽는 척하는 거예요\"—온갖 다른 외침까지 어찌나 정신없이 빠르게 쏟아지는지. 그러다 망탈리니 부인이 랄프한테 말을 거는 게 보이자, 그녀가 무슨 말을 할지 궁금해하는 여자들의 호기심이 이겨 버렸어. 그러더니 약속이라도 한 듯, 속삭임 하나 없는 죽은 듯한 정적이 순식간에 뒤따랐지."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"니클비 씨.\""], ["Nickleby,’ said Madame Mantalini; ‘by what chance you came here, I don’t know.’", "망탈리니 부인이 말했어. \"무슨 일로 여기 오셨는지는 모르겠네요.\""]], [["Here a gurgling voice was heard to ejaculate, as part of the wanderings of a sick man, the words ‘Demnition sweetness!’ but nobody heeded them except the footman, who, being startled to hear such awful tones proceeding, as it were, from between his very fingers, dropped his master’s head upon the floor with a pretty loud crash, and then, without an effort to lift it up, gazed upon the bystanders, as if he had done something rather clever than otherwise.", "이때 부글거리는 목소리가, 병든 사내의 헛소리처럼 \"지긋지긋한 달콤함!\" 하고 내뱉는 게 들렸어. 그런데 아무도 그 말에 신경 쓰지 않았지. 딱 그 하인만 빼고 말이야. 하인은 자기 손가락 사이에서 그런 끔찍한 소리가 나오는 걸 듣고 깜짝 놀라, 주인 머리를 꽤 큰 소리가 나게 바닥에 떨어뜨렸어. 그러고는 그걸 다시 들어 올릴 생각도 안 하고, 둘러선 사람들을 멀뚱히 바라봤지. 마치 영 못난 짓이 아니라 제법 잘난 짓이라도 한 것처럼 말이야."]], [["‘I will, however,’ continued Madame Mantalini, drying her eyes, and speaking with great indignation, ‘say before you, and before everybody here, for the first time, and once for all, that I never will supply that man’s extravagances and viciousness again. I have been a dupe and a fool to him long enough. In future, he shall support himself if he can, and then he may spend what money he pleases, upon whom and how he pleases; but it shall not be mine, and therefore you had better pause before you trust him further.’", "\"하지만 전요.\" 망탈리니 부인이 눈물을 훔치며 잔뜩 화가 난 목소리로 말을 이었어. \"당신 앞에서, 여기 모인 모든 사람 앞에서, 처음으로, 그리고 영영 단번에 말하겠어요. 저 인간의 낭비와 못된 짓을 다시는 절대 대 주지 않겠다고요. 저 인간한테 속아 넘어간 바보 노릇은 이제 할 만큼 했어요. 앞으로는 할 수 있으면 제 손으로 벌어먹으라죠. 그럼 그 돈으로 누구한테 어떻게 쓰든 마음대로 하든가요. 하지만 그게 제 돈은 아닐 거예요. 그러니 당신도 저 인간을 더 믿기 전에 잘 생각해 보는 게 좋을 거예요.\""]], [["Thereupon Madame Mantalini, quite unmoved by some most pathetic lamentations on the part of her husband, that the apothecary had not mixed the prussic acid strong enough, and that he must take another bottle or two to finish the work he had in hand, entered into a catalogue of that amiable gentleman’s gallantries, deceptions, extravagances, and infidelities (especially the last), winding up with a protest against being supposed to entertain the smallest remnant of regard for him; and adducing, in proof of the altered state of her affections, the circumstance of his having poisoned himself in private no less than six times within the last fortnight, and her not having once interfered by word or deed to save his life.", "이쯤에서 망탈리니 부인은, 약제사가 청산을 충분히 진하게 안 타 줘서 손대던 일을 마저 끝내려면 한두 병 더 먹어야겠다는 남편의 더없이 애처로운 푸념에도 조금도 흔들리지 않았어. 그러고는 이 사랑스러운 신사의 바람기며, 속임수며, 낭비며, 부정(특히 마지막 거)을 줄줄이 늘어놓기 시작했지. 그러고는 자기가 저 인간한테 손톱만큼이라도 애정을 품고 있다고 여겨선 안 된다고 못박으며 마무리했어. 그리고 자기 마음이 변했다는 증거로, 저 인간이 지난 보름 사이에 혼자서 무려 여섯 번이나 독약을 먹었는데도, 자기가 말 한마디 행동 하나로 목숨을 구하려 든 적이 한 번도 없었다는 사실을 들었지."]], [["‘And I insist on being separated and left to myself,’ said Madame Mantalini, sobbing. ‘If he dares to refuse me a separation, I’ll have one in law--I can--and I hope this will be a warning to all girls who have seen this disgraceful exhibition.’", "\"그리고 저는 갈라서서 혼자 지낼 것을 요구해요.\" 망탈리니 부인이 흐느끼며 말했어. \"저 인간이 감히 별거를 거부한다면, 법대로 갈라설 거예요. 그럴 수 있다고요. 그리고 이 꼴사나운 구경거리를 본 모든 아가씨들한테 이게 경고가 되길 바라요.\""]], [["Miss Knag, who was unquestionably the oldest girl in company, said with great solemnity, that it would be a warning to HER, and so did the young ladies generally, with the exception of one or two who appeared to entertain some doubts whether such whispers could do wrong.", "이 자리에서 누가 봐도 가장 나이 많은 아가씨인 내그 양이 아주 엄숙하게, 자기한테 경고가 되겠다고 말했어. 그러자 다른 젊은 아가씨들도 대체로 그렇다고 했지. 다만 한둘은, 그런 속삭임이 그렇게까지 나쁜 일일까 의심하는 눈치였어."]], [["‘Why do you say all this before so many listeners?’ said Ralph, in a low voice.", "\"왜 이런 얘길 이렇게 많은 사람 앞에서 하는 거요?\" 랄프가 낮은 목소리로 말했어."], ["‘You know you are not in earnest.’", "\"진심이 아니란 거 당신도 알잖소.\""]], [["‘I AM in earnest,’ replied Madame Mantalini, aloud, and retreating towards Miss Knag.", "\"전 진심이에요.\" 망탈리니 부인이 큰 소리로 대답하며 내그 양 쪽으로 물러섰어."]], [["‘Well, but consider,’ reasoned Ralph, who had a great interest in the matter.", "\"그래도, 잘 생각해 보시오.\" 이 일에 큰 이해관계가 걸린 랄프가 타일렀어."], ["‘It would be well to reflect.", "\"곰곰이 따져 보는 게 좋아요."], ["A married woman has no property.’", "결혼한 여자한텐 재산이 없는 법이니까.\""]], [["‘Not a solitary single individual dem, my soul,’ and Mr.", "\"단 하나도 없지, 한 푼도 빌어먹게 없지, 내 영혼아.\""], ["Mantalini, raising himself upon his elbow.", "망탈리니 씨가 팔꿈치로 몸을 일으키며 말했어."]], [["‘I am quite aware of that,’ retorted Madame Mantalini, tossing her head; ‘and I have none.", "\"저도 그건 잘 알아요.\" 망탈리니 부인이 고개를 홱 젖히며 받아쳤어."], ["The business, the stock, this house, and everything in it, all belong to Miss Knag.’", "\"그래서 전 가진 게 없죠. 사업도, 재고도, 이 집도, 그 안에 있는 모든 게 다 내그 양 거예요.\""]], [["‘That’s quite true, Madame Mantalini,’ said Miss Knag, with whom her late employer had secretly come to an amicable understanding on this point. ‘Very true, indeed, Madame Mantalini--hem--very true. And I never was more glad in all my life, that I had strength of mind to resist matrimonial offers, no matter how advantageous, than I am when I think of my present position as compared with your most unfortunate and most undeserved one, Madame Mantalini.’", "\"맞는 말씀이에요, 망탈리니 부인.\" 내그 양이 말했어. 전 주인과 이 점에 대해 몰래 좋게 합의를 봐 둔 터였지. \"정말 맞는 말씀이고말고요, 망탈리니 부인—흠—정말 맞는 말씀이죠. 그리고 전 평생 이렇게 기뻤던 적이 없어요. 아무리 유리한 청혼이라도 다 뿌리칠 만큼 마음이 굳건했다는 게요. 지금 제 처지를, 그토록 불행하고 그토록 억울한 부인 처지와 견줘 보면 더욱 그렇답니다, 망탈리니 부인.\""]], [["‘Demmit!’ cried Mr.", "\"빌어먹을!\""], ["Mantalini, turning his head towards his wife.", "망탈리니 씨가 아내 쪽으로 고개를 돌리며 외쳤어."], ["‘Will it not slap and pinch the envious dowager, that dares to reflect upon its own delicious?’", "\"이 녀석은 자기 달콤한 임을 감히 헐뜯는 저 샘 많은 늙은 과부를 찰싹 때리고 꼬집어 주지 않을 텐가?\""]], [["But the day of Mr.", "그런데 망탈리니 씨의 달콤한 말이 먹히던 시절은 이미 끝나 버렸어."], ["Mantalini’s blandishments had departed.", "\"내그 양은요, 여보.\" 그의 아내가 말했어."], ["‘Miss Knag, sir,’ said his wife, ‘is my particular friend;’ and although Mr Mantalini leered till his eyes seemed in danger of never coming back to their right places again, Madame Mantalini showed no signs of softening.", "\"제 둘도 없는 친구예요.\" 망탈리니 씨가 눈알이 영영 제자리로 돌아오지 못할 만큼 곁눈질을 해 댔지만, 망탈리니 부인은 누그러질 기미가 전혀 없었지."]], [["To do the excellent Miss Knag justice, she had been mainly instrumental in bringing about this altered state of things, for, finding by daily experience, that there was no chance of the business thriving, or even continuing to exist, while Mr. Mantalini had any hand in the expenditure, and having now a considerable interest in its well-doing, she had sedulously applied herself to the investigation of some little matters connected with that gentleman’s private character, which she had so well elucidated, and artfully imparted to Madame Mantalini, as to open her eyes more effectually than the closest and most philosophical reasoning could have done in a series of years. To which end, the accidental discovery by Miss Knag of some tender correspondence, in which Madame Mantalini was described as ‘old’ and ‘ordinary,’ had most providentially contributed.", "훌륭한 내그 양을 두고 공평하게 말하자면, 이렇게 사정이 달라진 데에는 그녀의 공이 컸어. 매일 겪어 보니 망탈리니 씨가 씀씀이에 조금이라도 손을 대는 한 사업이 번창할 가망은커녕 명맥을 잇기도 글렀다는 걸 알게 됐거든. 게다가 이제 사업이 잘되는 데 적잖은 이해가 걸린 그녀는, 그 신사의 사생활에 얽힌 자잘한 일들을 부지런히 캐고 다녔지. 그러고는 그걸 아주 잘 밝혀내서 교묘하게 망탈리니 부인한테 일러 줬어. 그러니 몇 해에 걸친 그 어떤 빈틈없고 철학적인 설득보다도 더 톡톡히 부인의 눈을 뜨게 한 거지. 그 일에는 내그 양이 우연히 발견한 어떤 애틋한 편지가 더없이 절묘하게 한몫했어. 그 편지에서 망탈리니 부인은 \"늙은이\"에 \"평범한 여자\"라고 적혀 있었거든."]], [["However, notwithstanding her firmness, Madame Mantalini wept very piteously; and as she leant upon Miss Knag, and signed towards the door, that young lady and all the other young ladies with sympathising faces, proceeded to bear her out.", "그래도 그렇게 마음을 굳게 먹었으면서도, 망탈리니 부인은 아주 가엾게 울었어. 그러고는 내그 양에게 기댄 채 문 쪽으로 손짓했지. 그러자 그 젊은 아가씨와 다른 모든 젊은 아가씨들이 가엾어하는 얼굴로 부인을 부축해 데리고 나갔어."]], [["‘Nickleby,’ said Mr.", "\"니클비.\" 망탈리니 씨가 눈물을 흘리며 말했어."], ["Mantalini in tears, ‘you have been made a witness to this demnition cruelty, on the part of the demdest enslaver and captivator that never was, oh dem!", "\"당신은 이 빌어먹게 잔인한 짓을 똑똑히 목격했소. 세상에 둘도 없는 빌어먹게 매혹적인 마녀의 짓 말이오, 아 빌어먹을!"], ["I forgive that woman.’", "난 저 여자를 용서하오.\""]], [["‘Forgive!’ repeated Madame Mantalini, angrily.", "\"용서한다고요!\" 망탈리니 부인이 성나서 되받았어."]], [["‘I do forgive her, Nickleby,’ said Mr.", "\"난 저 여자를 용서하오, 니클비.\""], ["Mantalini.", "망탈리니 씨가 말했어."], ["‘You will blame me, the world will blame me, the women will blame me; everybody will laugh, and scoff, and smile, and grin most demnebly.", "\"당신은 날 탓하겠지, 세상이 날 탓하겠지, 여자들이 날 탓하겠지. 다들 비웃고, 비아냥대고, 미소 짓고, 빌어먹게 히죽댈 거요."], ["They will say, “She had a blessing.", "사람들이 이러겠지. '저 여잔 복을 받았는데 그걸 몰랐어."], ["She did not know it.", "그 남잔 너무 여렸어, 너무 착했어."], ["He was too weak; he was too good; he was a dem’d fine fellow, but he loved too strong; he could not bear her to be cross, and call him wicked names.", "빌어먹게 멋진 사내였는데 사랑이 너무 깊었지. 아내가 토라져서 못된 욕을 퍼붓는 걸 견딜 수가 없었어."], ["It was a dem’d case, there never was a demder.”", "빌어먹을 일이었지, 그보다 더 빌어먹을 일은 없었어.'"], ["But I forgive her.’", "그래도 난 저 여자를 용서하오.\""]], [["With this affecting speech Mr. Mantalini fell down again very flat, and lay to all appearance without sense or motion, until all the females had left the room, when he came cautiously into a sitting posture, and confronted Ralph with a very blank face, and the little bottle still in one hand and the tea-spoon in the other.", "이 가슴 저미는 연설을 마치고 망탈리니 씨는 다시 납작하게 쓰러졌어. 그러고는 정신도 움직임도 없는 듯 누워 있었지. 모든 여자들이 방을 나갈 때까지 말이야. 그러더니 슬그머니 일어나 앉는 자세를 취하고는, 더없이 멍한 얼굴로 랄프와 마주했어. 작은 병은 여전히 한 손에, 찻숟가락은 다른 손에 쥔 채로."]], [["‘You may put away those fooleries now, and live by your wits again,’ said Ralph, coolly putting on his hat.", "\"이제 그 어릿광대짓은 집어치우고, 다시 머리 굴려서 먹고살 궁리나 하시오.\" 랄프가 태연히 모자를 쓰며 말했어."]], [["‘Demmit, Nickleby, you’re not serious?’", "\"빌어먹을, 니클비, 진심은 아니지?\""]], [["‘I seldom joke,’ said Ralph.", "\"난 좀처럼 농담을 안 하오.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘Good-night.’", "\"잘 자시오.\""]], [["‘No, but Nickleby--’ said Mantalini.", "\"아니, 근데 니클비—\" 망탈리니가 말했어."]], [["‘I am wrong, perhaps,’ rejoined Ralph.", "\"내가 틀렸을 수도 있겠지.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어."], ["‘I hope so.", "\"그러길 바라오."], ["You should know best.", "당신이 제일 잘 알 테지."], ["Good-night.’", "잘 자시오.\""]], [["Affecting not to hear his entreaties that he would stay and advise with him, Ralph left the crest-fallen Mr. Mantalini to his meditations, and left the house quietly.", "랄프는 좀 더 있으면서 의논 좀 해 달라는 망탈리니의 간청을 못 들은 척, 풀이 죽은 그를 명상에 빠지게 내버려 두고 조용히 집을 나섰어."]], [["‘Oho!’ he said, ‘sets the wind that way so soon?", "\"오호!\" 그가 말했어."], ["Half knave and half fool, and detected in both characters?", "\"벌써 바람이 그쪽으로 부나? 반은 악당, 반은 바보, 그러고도 양쪽 다 들통이 났군?"], ["I think your day is over, sir.’", "당신 시대는 끝난 것 같소, 이 양반아.\""]], [["As he said this, he made some memorandum in his pocket-book in which Mr Mantalini’s name figured conspicuously, and finding by his watch that it was between nine and ten o’clock, made all speed home.", "이렇게 말하면서 랄프는 수첩에 뭔가를 적어 넣었어. 거기엔 망탈리니 씨의 이름이 큼지막하게 들어가 있었지. 그러고는 시계를 보니 아홉 시에서 열 시 사이였어. 그래서 부리나케 집으로 돌아갔지."]], [["‘Are they here?’ was the first question he asked of Newman.", "\"그 사람들 와 있나?\" 랄프가 뉴먼에게 던진 첫 물음이었어."]], [["Newman nodded.", "뉴먼이 고개를 끄덕였어."], ["‘Been here half an hour.’", "\"삼십 분 전부터 와 있어요.\""]], [["‘Two of them?", "\"둘이?"], ["One a fat sleek man?’", "하나는 살집 있고 매끈한 사내?\""]], [["‘Ay,’ said Newman.", "\"네.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."], ["‘In your room now.’", "\"지금 사장님 방에 있어요.\""]], [["‘Good,’ rejoined Ralph.", "\"좋아.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어."], ["‘Get me a coach.’", "\"마차 한 대 불러 줘.\""]], [["‘A coach!", "\"마차요!"], ["What, you--going to--eh?’ stammered Newman.", "뭐, 사장님이—어딜—네?\" 뉴먼이 말을 더듬었어."]], [["Ralph angrily repeated his orders, and Noggs, who might well have been excused for wondering at such an unusual and extraordinary circumstance (for he had never seen Ralph in a coach in his life) departed on his errand, and presently returned with the conveyance.", "랄프가 성난 목소리로 명령을 되풀이했어. 그러자 노그스는, 이런 색다르고 예사롭지 않은 일에 어리둥절해할 만도 했지만 (랄프가 마차 타는 걸 평생 한 번도 본 적이 없었거든), 시키는 대로 나갔다가 이내 마차를 끌고 돌아왔어."]], [["Into it went Mr.", "마차에 스퀴어스 씨와 랄프, 그리고 뉴먼 노그스가 한 번도 본 적 없는 세 번째 사내가 올라탔어."], ["Squeers, and Ralph, and the third man, whom Newman Noggs had never seen.", "뉴먼은 문간 계단에 서서 그들을 배웅했지."], ["Newman stood upon the door-step to see them off, not troubling himself to wonder where or upon what business they were going, until he chanced by mere accident to hear Ralph name the address whither the coachman was to drive.", "그들이 어디로, 무슨 일로 가는지 굳이 궁금해하지도 않으면서 말이야. 그러다 그저 우연히, 마부더러 어디로 몰라고 랄프가 일러 주는 주소를 듣고 말았어."]], [["Quick as lightning and in a state of the most extreme wonder, Newman darted into his little office for his hat, and limped after the coach as if with the intention of getting up behind; but in this design he was balked, for it had too much the start of him and was soon hopelessly ahead, leaving him gaping in the empty street.", "번개처럼 빠르게, 그리고 더없이 놀란 채로, 뉴먼은 모자를 가지러 자기 작은 사무실로 쏜살같이 달려 들어갔어. 그러고는 마차 뒤에 매달릴 작정인 듯 절뚝거리며 쫓아갔지. 그런데 이 계획은 틀어지고 말았어. 마차가 너무 앞서 출발한 데다 금세 가망 없이 멀어져서, 뉴먼은 텅 빈 거리에서 입을 떡 벌린 채 남겨졌거든."]], [["‘I don’t know though,’ said Noggs, stopping for breath, ‘any good that I could have done by going too.", "\"하긴 모르겠다.\" 노그스가 숨을 돌리려 멈춰 서서 말했어."], ["He would have seen me if I had.", "\"나까지 갔어도 무슨 도움이 됐을지. 갔으면 사장님 눈에 띄었겠지."], ["Drive THERE!", "저기로 가다니!"], ["What can come of this?", "이게 무슨 일이람?"], ["If I had only known it yesterday I could have told--drive there!", "어제만 알았어도 일러 줄 수 있었을 텐데—저기로 가다니!"], ["There’s mischief in it.", "이건 무슨 못된 짓이야."], ["There must be.’", "틀림없어.\""]], [["His reflections were interrupted by a grey-haired man of a very remarkable, though far from prepossessing appearance, who, coming stealthily towards him, solicited relief.", "그의 생각은, 아주 눈에 띄지만 결코 호감 가지 않는 행색의 백발 사내 때문에 끊겼어. 그 사내는 슬그머니 그에게 다가와 적선을 청했지."]], [["Newman, still cogitating deeply, turned away; but the man followed him, and pressed him with such a tale of misery that Newman (who might have been considered a hopeless person to beg from, and who had little enough to give) looked into his hat for some halfpence which he usually kept screwed up, when he had any, in a corner of his pocket-handkerchief.", "뉴먼은 여전히 깊이 골똘한 채 돌아섰어. 그런데 사내가 그를 따라오며 어찌나 비참한 사연을 들이대는지, 뉴먼은 (동냥하기엔 가망 없는 사람으로 보였을 테고, 줄 것도 거의 없었지만) 모자 안을 들여다봤어. 평소 돈이 좀 생기면 손수건 한쪽 구석에 비틀어 묶어 두던 반 펜스 동전 몇 닢을 찾으려고 말이야."]], [["While he was busily untwisting the knot with his teeth, the man said something which attracted his attention; whatever that something was, it led to something else, and in the end he and Newman walked away side by side--the strange man talking earnestly, and Newman listening.", "뉴먼이 이로 그 매듭을 부지런히 푸는 동안, 사내가 무슨 말을 했는데 그게 그의 주의를 끌었어. 그게 무슨 말이었든, 또 다른 말로 이어졌고, 결국 그와 뉴먼은 나란히 걸어가고 있었지—그 낯선 사내는 간절하게 얘기하고, 뉴먼은 귀 기울여 들으면서 말이야."]], [["Containing Matter of a surprising Kind\n‘As we gang awa’ fra’ Lunnun tomorrow neeght, and as I dinnot know that I was e’er so happy in a’ my days, Misther Nickleby, Ding!", "\"우리는 내일 밤이면 런던을 떠날 거고, 내 평생 이렇게 행복했던 적이 또 있었나 싶은데, 니클비 씨, 에라!"], ["but I WILL tak’ anoother glass to our next merry meeting!’", "우리 다음에 또 즐겁게 만날 날을 위해 한 잔 더 들어야겠소!\""]], [["So said John Browdie, rubbing his hands with great joyousness, and looking round him with a ruddy shining face, quite in keeping with the declaration.", "존 브라우디는 이렇게 말하며, 무척 즐거운 듯 두 손을 비비고는, 그 말과 딱 어울리는 불그레하고 환한 얼굴로 둘레를 휘 둘러봤어."]], [["The time at which John found himself in this enviable condition was the same evening to which the last chapter bore reference; the place was the cottage; and the assembled company were Nicholas, Mrs.", "존이 이렇게 부러울 만한 기분에 젖어 있던 때는, 앞 장에서 이야기한 바로 그날 저녁이었어."], ["Nickleby, Mrs Browdie, Kate Nickleby, and Smike.", "장소는 그 작은 집이었고, 모인 사람들은 니콜라스, 니클비 부인, 브라우디 부인, 케이트 니클비, 그리고 스마이크였지."]], [["A very merry party they had been. Mrs.", "다들 아주 즐거운 모임을 보내고 있었어."], ["Nickleby, knowing of her son’s obligations to the honest Yorkshireman, had, after some demur, yielded her consent to Mr. and Mrs.", "니클비 부인은 정직한 요크셔 사람한테 아들이 신세를 졌다는 걸 알고 있었거든."], ["Browdie being invited out to tea; in the way of which arrangement, there were at first sundry difficulties and obstacles, arising out of her not having had an opportunity of ‘calling’ upon Mrs.", "그래서 한참 망설인 끝에 브라우디 부부를 차 마시러 초대하자는 데 동의를 해 줬지. 그런데 이 일을 두고 처음엔 이런저런 어려움과 걸림돌이 있었어."], ["Browdie first; for although Mrs.", "부인이 브라우디 부인한테 먼저 '방문'할 기회가 없었다는 게 문제였지."], ["Nickleby very often observed with much complacency (as most punctilious people do), that she had not an atom of pride or formality about her, still she was a great stickler for dignity and ceremonies; and as it was manifest that, until a call had been made, she could not be (politely speaking, and according to the laws of society) even cognisant of the fact of Mrs.", "니클비 부인은 까다로운 사람들이 흔히 그러듯, 자기는 거만하거나 격식 차리는 구석이 눈곱만큼도 없다고 흐뭇하게 자주 말하곤 했어. 그래도 품위와 예법에는 무척 까다로운 사람이었거든."], ["Browdie’s existence, she felt her situation to be one of peculiar delicacy and difficulty.", "그러니 방문을 하기 전에는 — 점잖게 말해서, 또 사교계의 법도대로라면 — 브라우디 부인이라는 사람이 존재한다는 사실조차 알 수 없는 셈이라, 부인은 자기 처지가 유난히 미묘하고 곤란하다고 느꼈어."]], [["‘The call MUST originate with me, my dear,’ said Mrs.", "\"방문은 내가 먼저 시작해야 한단다, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby, ‘that’s indispensable.", "\"그건 꼭 그래야 하는 거야."], ["The fact is, my dear, that it’s necessary there should be a sort of condescension on my part, and that I should show this young person that I am willing to take notice of her.", "사실은 말이다, 얘야, 내 쪽에서 일종의 격을 낮춰 주는 게 필요해. 내가 그 젊은 여자를 기꺼이 봐주겠다는 걸 보여 줘야 한다는 거지."], ["There’s a very respectable-looking young man,’ added Mrs.", "아주 점잖아 보이는 젊은이가 하나 있는데,\" 니클비 부인이 잠깐 생각하더니 덧붙였어."], ["Nickleby, after a short consideration, ‘who is conductor to one of the omnibuses that go by here, and who wears a glazed hat--your sister and I have noticed him very often--he has a wart upon his nose, Kate, you know, exactly like a gentleman’s servant.’", "\"여기를 지나다니는 합승마차 차장이야. 번들거리는 모자를 쓰고 다니지 — 너랑 나랑 그 사람을 아주 자주 봤잖니 — 코에 사마귀가 하나 있단다, 케이트, 너도 알지, 꼭 신사 집안 하인처럼 말이야.\""]], [["‘Have all gentlemen’s servants warts upon their noses, mother?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"신사 집안 하인들은 다 코에 사마귀가 있나요, 어머니?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘Nicholas, my dear, how very absurd you are,’ returned his mother; ‘of course I mean that his glazed hat looks like a gentleman’s servant, and not the wart upon his nose; though even that is not so ridiculous as it may seem to you, for we had a footboy once, who had not only a wart, but a wen also, and a very large wen too, and he demanded to have his wages raised in consequence, because he found it came very expensive. Let me see, what was I--oh yes, I know. The best way that I can think of would be to send a card, and my compliments, (I’ve no doubt he’d take ‘em for a pot of porter,) by this young man, to the Saracen with Two Necks. If the waiter took him for a gentleman’s servant, so much the better. Then all Mrs. Browdie would have to do would be to send her card back by the carrier (he could easily come with a double knock), and there’s an end of it.’", "\"니콜라스, 얘야, 너는 어쩜 그렇게 엉뚱하니.\" 어머니가 대꾸했어. \"당연히 내 말은 그 사람 번들거리는 모자가 신사 집안 하인 같다는 거지, 코에 난 사마귀가 그렇다는 게 아니야. 하지만 그것도 네가 생각하는 것만큼 우스운 일은 아니란다. 옛날에 우리 집에 사환 아이가 하나 있었는데, 사마귀뿐 아니라 혹까지 있었거든. 그것도 아주 큰 혹이었지. 그래서 그 아이는 그 때문에 돈이 많이 든다면서 품삯을 올려 달라고 했단다. 가만, 내가 무슨 얘기를 — 아 그래, 알겠다. 내가 생각하기에 제일 좋은 방법은, 그 젊은이 편에 카드 한 장이랑 내 인사를 써서('새러슨 위드 투 넥스'로 보내는 거지. 그 사람은 흑맥주 한 잔으로 알아들을 게 분명하지만 말이야) 보내는 거야. 종업원이 그 사람을 신사 집안 하인으로 봐 준다면 더 좋고. 그러면 브라우디 부인은 짐꾼 편에 카드를 도로 보내기만 하면 돼(그 사람이 문을 쾅쾅 두 번 두드리며 오면 되니까). 그러면 끝이지.\""]], [["‘My dear mother,’ said Nicholas, ‘I don’t suppose such unsophisticated people as these ever had a card of their own, or ever will have.’", "\"어머니,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"이렇게 꾸밈없는 사람들은 자기 카드 같은 걸 가져 본 적도 없고, 앞으로 가질 일도 없을 거예요.\""]], [["‘Oh that, indeed, Nicholas, my dear,’ returned Mrs.", "\"오, 그렇다면야, 니콜라스, 얘야,\" 니클비 부인이 대꾸했어."], ["Nickleby, ‘that’s another thing.", "\"그건 또 다른 얘기지."], ["If you put it upon that ground, why, of course, I have no more to say, than that I have no doubt they are very good sort of persons, and that I have no kind of objection to their coming here to tea if they like, and shall make a point of being very civil to them if they do.’", "네가 그렇게 따진다면야, 나도 더 할 말이 없구나. 그 사람들이 아주 착한 사람들인 건 틀림없고, 차 마시러 오고 싶다면 오는 거 나는 아무 반대도 없고, 정말 온다면 아주 친절하게 대해 줄 거라는 것 말고는 말이다.\""]], [["The point being thus effectually set at rest, and Mrs. Nickleby duly placed in the patronising and mildly-condescending position which became her rank and matrimonial years, Mr.", "이렇게 해서 이 문제가 깔끔하게 정리됐고, 니클비 부인은 자기 지위와 결혼한 햇수에 걸맞게 너그럽고 살짝 격을 낮춰 주는 자리에 자리 잡았지."], ["and Mrs. Browdie were invited and came; and as they were very deferential to Mrs.", "그래서 브라우디 부부는 초대를 받아 왔어."], ["Nickleby, and seemed to have a becoming appreciation of her greatness, and were very much pleased with everything, the good lady had more than once given Kate to understand, in a whisper, that she thought they were the very best-meaning people she had ever seen, and perfectly well behaved.", "부부는 니클비 부인한테 무척 깍듯했고, 부인의 대단함을 제대로 알아주는 듯했고, 모든 게 다 마음에 드는 모양이었어. 그래서 이 착한 부인은 케이트한테 한 번 이상 귓속말로, 자기가 본 사람 중에 제일 착한 사람들 같고 행실도 더없이 점잖다고 일러 줬지."]], [["And thus it came to pass, that John Browdie declared, in the parlour after supper, to wit, and twenty minutes before eleven o’clock p.m., that he had never been so happy in all his days.", "이렇게 해서 존 브라우디는 저녁을 먹은 뒤 거실에서, 그러니까 밤 열한 시 이십 분 전쯤에, 평생 이렇게 행복했던 적이 없다고 떠들게 된 거야."]], [["Nor was Mrs.", "브라우디 부인도 이 점에서는 남편에게 별로 뒤지지 않았어."], ["Browdie much behind her husband in this respect, for that young matron, whose rustic beauty contrasted very prettily with the more delicate loveliness of Kate, and without suffering by the contrast either, for each served as it were to set off and decorate the other, could not sufficiently admire the gentle and winning manners of the young lady, or the engaging affability of the elder one.", "이 젊은 새댁의 시골스러운 아름다움은 케이트의 더 섬세한 사랑스러움과 아주 곱게 대비됐거든. 그렇다고 어느 쪽이 못나 보이는 것도 아니었어."], ["Then Kate had the art of turning the conversation to subjects upon which the country girl, bashful at first in strange company, could feel herself at home; and if Mrs.", "둘은 서로를 돋보이게 해 주고 꾸며 주는 셈이었으니까. 새댁은 젊은 아가씨의 부드럽고 사랑스러운 몸가짐과, 나이 든 부인의 상냥한 붙임성에 감탄해 마지않았어."], ["Nickleby was not quite so felicitous at times in the selection of topics of discourse, or if she did seem, as Mrs.", "게다가 케이트는 화제를 잘 돌리는 재주가 있어서, 처음엔 낯선 자리에서 수줍어하던 시골 처녀가 편안함을 느낄 만한 이야기로 이끌어 갔지."], ["Browdie expressed it, ‘rather high in her notions,’ still nothing could be kinder, and that she took considerable interest in the young couple was manifest from the very long lectures on housewifery with which she was so obliging as to entertain Mrs.", "니클비 부인은 가끔 화제를 고르는 데 그만큼 능숙하지는 못했고, 브라우디 부인 말마따나 '생각이 좀 높은' 듯도 했지만, 그래도 그보다 더 다정할 수는 없었어."], ["Browdie’s private ear, which were illustrated by various references to the domestic economy of the cottage, in which (those duties falling exclusively upon Kate) the good lady had about as much share, either in theory or practice, as any one of the statues of the Twelve Apostles which embellish the exterior of St Paul’s Cathedral.", "그리고 부인이 이 젊은 부부에게 적잖이 관심을 두고 있다는 건, 브라우디 부인 귀에다 친절하게도 살림살이에 관한 기나긴 강의를 늘어놓는 데서 분명히 드러났지. 그 강의는 이 작은 집의 살림 형편을 이런저런 예로 들어 가며 곁들였는데, 정작 그 살림일은 (전부 케이트 몫이었거든) 이론으로든 실제로든 이 착한 부인이 끼어든 정도가, 세인트 폴 대성당 바깥을 장식하는 열두 사도 조각상 가운데 하나만큼이나 되었어."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"브라우디 씨는,\""], ["Browdie,’ said Kate, addressing his young wife, ‘is the best-humoured, the kindest and heartiest creature I ever saw.", "케이트가 그의 젊은 아내에게 말을 건넸어. \"제가 본 사람 중에 제일 마음씨 좋고, 제일 친절하고, 제일 다정한 분이에요."], ["If I were oppressed with I don’t know how many cares, it would make me happy only to look at him.’", "제가 무슨 걱정거리에 얼마나 짓눌려 있든, 그분을 보기만 해도 행복해질 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘He does seem indeed, upon my word, a most excellent creature, Kate,’ said Mrs.", "\"정말이지, 보아하니 더없이 훌륭한 분 같구나, 케이트.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby; ‘most excellent.", "\"더없이 훌륭해."], ["And I am sure that at all times it will give me pleasure--really pleasure now--to have you, Mrs.", "그리고 브라우디 부인, 이렇게 수수하고 소박하게 우리 집에 와서 나를 봐 주는 건 언제든 정말 기쁜 일이에요 — 정말로 기쁘답니다."], ["Browdie, to see me in this plain and homely manner.", "우리는 거창하게 차리지 않아요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["We make no display,’ said Mrs Nickleby, with an air which seemed to insinuate that they could make a vast deal if they were so disposed; ‘no fuss, no preparation; I wouldn’t allow it.", "마음만 먹으면 엄청나게 거창하게 차릴 수도 있다는 걸 넌지시 비치는 듯한 투였지. \"야단법석도 없고, 준비도 없고요."], ["I said, “Kate, my dear, you will only make Mrs.", "나는 그런 걸 용납하지 않거든요. 내가 그랬어요."], ["Browdie feel uncomfortable, and how very foolish and inconsiderate that would be!”’", "'케이트, 얘야, 그러면 브라우디 부인이 불편해하기만 할 거다. 그건 얼마나 어리석고 경솔한 짓이겠니!'\""]], [["‘I am very much obliged to you, I am sure, ma’am,’ returned Mrs.", "\"정말 감사합니다, 부인.\" 브라우디 부인이 고마운 마음으로 대답했어."], ["Browdie, gratefully.", "\"거의 열한 시예요, 존."], ["‘It’s nearly eleven o’clock, John. I am afraid we are keeping you up very late, ma’am.’", "부인을 너무 늦게까지 못 주무시게 하는 건 아닌지 모르겠네요, 부인.\""]], [["‘Late!’ cried Mrs.", "\"늦다니요!\""], ["Nickleby, with a sharp thin laugh, and one little cough at the end, like a note of admiration expressed.", "니클비 부인이 날카롭고 가는 웃음을 터뜨리고는, 끝에 짧게 기침을 한 번 했어. 꼭 감탄 부호를 찍는 것처럼 말이야."], ["‘This is quite early for us.", "\"우리한테 이건 아주 이른 시간이에요."], ["We used to keep such hours!", "우리가 어떤 시간까지 깨어 있었는데요!"], ["Twelve, one, two, three o’clock was nothing to us.", "열두 시, 한 시, 두 시, 세 시도 우리한텐 아무것도 아니었답니다."], ["Balls, dinners, card-parties!", "무도회, 만찬, 카드놀이 모임!"], ["Never were such rakes as the people about where we used to live.", "우리가 살던 동네 사람들처럼 그렇게 흥청거리는 사람들은 없었어요. 지금 생각하면 정말이지, 우리가 그걸 어떻게 다 견뎌 냈는지 참 놀라울 따름이에요."], ["I often think now, I am sure, that how we ever could go through with it is quite astonishing, and that is just the evil of having a large connection and being a great deal sought after, which I would recommend all young married people steadily to resist; though of course, and it’s perfectly clear, and a very happy thing too, I think, that very few young married people can be exposed to such temptations.", "그게 바로 인맥이 넓고 사람들이 많이 찾아 주는 데서 오는 폐단이지요. 나는 젊은 부부들한테 꼭 그걸 꿋꿋이 견디라고 권하고 싶어요."], ["There was one family in particular, that used to live about a mile from us--not straight down the road, but turning sharp off to the left by the turnpike where the Plymouth mail ran over the donkey--that were quite extraordinary people for giving the most extravagant parties, with artificial flowers and champagne, and variegated lamps, and, in short, every delicacy of eating and drinking that the most singular epicure could possibly require.", "물론, 또 아주 분명한 얘기지만, 그리고 참 다행스러운 일이라고 생각하는데, 그런 유혹에 노출될 수 있는 젊은 부부는 거의 없긴 하지요. 특히 한 집안이 있었는데, 우리 집에서 한 일 마일쯤 떨어진 데 살았어요 — 길을 곧장 내려가는 게 아니라, 플리머스 우편마차가 당나귀를 치고 지나간 그 통행료 받는 길에서 왼쪽으로 홱 꺾어 들어가야 했지요 — 그 사람들은 정말 유난스럽게 호화로운 잔치를 열곤 했어요."], ["I don’t think that there ever were such people as those Peltiroguses.", "조화에 샴페인에, 알록달록한 등불에, 한마디로 제아무리 까다로운 미식가라도 바랄 만한 온갖 진수성찬을 다 차렸지요. 그 펠티로거스네 같은 사람들은 또 없을 거예요."], ["You remember the Peltiroguses, Kate?’", "펠티로거스네 기억나지, 케이트?\""]], [["Kate saw that for the ease and comfort of the visitors it was high time to stay this flood of recollection, so answered that she entertained of the Peltiroguses a most vivid and distinct remembrance; and then said that Mr.", "케이트는 손님들이 편안하고 마음 놓이게 하려면 이제 이 추억의 물결을 막을 때가 됐다는 걸 알아챘어. 그래서 펠티로거스네에 대해 아주 생생하고 또렷이 기억한다고 대답했지."], ["Browdie had half promised, early in the evening, that he would sing a Yorkshire song, and that she was most impatient that he should redeem his promise, because she was sure it would afford her mama more amusement and pleasure than it was possible to express.", "그러고는 브라우디 씨가 저녁 일찍 요크셔 노래를 한 곡 불러 주기로 반쯤 약속했었다며, 그 약속을 꼭 지켜 줬으면 좋겠다고 했어. 그러면 어머니가 말로 다 못 할 만큼 즐겁고 흐뭇해하실 게 분명하다면서 말이야."]], [["Mrs. Nickleby confirming her daughter with the best possible grace--for there was patronage in that too, and a kind of implication that she had a discerning taste in such matters, and was something of a critic--John Browdie proceeded to consider the words of some north-country ditty, and to take his wife’s recollection respecting the same.", "니클비 부인은 더없이 우아하게 딸 말에 맞장구를 쳤어 — 거기에도 너그러이 봐주는 기색이, 또 자기가 그런 방면에 안목이 있고 제법 비평가쯤 된다는 듯한 투가 묻어 있었지 — 그러자 존 브라우디는 어느 북부 지방 가락의 가사를 떠올리려 애쓰며, 그 노래에 관해 아내의 기억을 빌렸어."], ["This done, he made divers ungainly movements in his chair, and singling out one particular fly on the ceiling from the other flies there asleep, fixed his eyes upon him, and began to roar a meek sentiment (supposed to be uttered by a gentle swain fast pining away with love and despair) in a voice of thunder.", "그러고는 의자에 앉은 채로 어색하게 이리저리 몸을 움직이더니, 천장에서 잠들어 있는 다른 파리들 가운데 파리 한 마리를 콕 골라 거기에 눈을 박고는, 부드러운 정감(사랑과 절망에 야위어 가는 점잖은 시골 총각이 읊는 것으로 되어 있는)을 천둥 같은 목소리로 우렁차게 뽑아 대기 시작했어."]], [["At the end of the first verse, as though some person without had waited until then to make himself audible, was heard a loud and violent knocking at the street-door; so loud and so violent, indeed, that the ladies started as by one accord, and John Browdie stopped.", "첫 절이 끝날 무렵, 마치 바깥에 있던 누군가가 그때까지 기다렸다가 제 소리를 들리게 하려는 듯, 길 쪽 현관문을 쾅쾅 사납게 두드리는 소리가 들렸어. 어찌나 크고 사납던지, 부인들은 마치 약속이라도 한 듯 한꺼번에 흠칫 놀랐고, 존 브라우디도 노래를 멈췄지."]], [["‘It must be some mistake,’ said Nicholas, carelessly.", "\"뭔가 잘못 온 거겠지요.\" 니콜라스가 대수롭잖게 말했어."], ["‘We know nobody who would come here at this hour.’", "\"이 시간에 여기 올 사람은 없으니까요.\""]], [["Mrs. Nickleby surmised, however, that perhaps the counting-house was burnt down, or perhaps ‘the Mr.", "그래도 니클비 부인은 이런저런 짐작을 했어."], ["Cheerybles’ had sent to take Nicholas into partnership (which certainly appeared highly probable at that time of night), or perhaps Mr. Linkinwater had run away with the property, or perhaps Miss La Creevy was taken in, or perhaps--", "어쩌면 회계 사무소가 불타 버렸거나, 어쩌면 '치어리블 형제'가 니콜라스를 동업자로 들이려고 사람을 보냈거나(이 밤중에 그럴 가능성이 정말 높아 보였지), 어쩌면 링킨워터 씨가 재산을 가지고 달아났거나, 어쩌면 라 크리비 양이 속아 넘어갔거나, 어쩌면 —"]], [["But a hasty exclamation from Kate stopped her abruptly in her conjectures, and Ralph Nickleby walked into the room.", "하지만 케이트가 황급히 내지른 소리에 부인의 짐작은 뚝 끊겼어. 그리고 랄프 니클비가 방 안으로 걸어 들어왔지."]], [["‘Stay,’ said Ralph, as Nicholas rose, and Kate, making her way towards him, threw herself upon his arm.", "\"가만.\" 니콜라스가 일어서고 케이트가 그에게 다가가 팔에 매달리자, 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘Before that boy says a word, hear me.’", "\"저 녀석이 한마디라도 하기 전에, 내 말을 들어라.\""]], [["Nicholas bit his lip and shook his head in a threatening manner, but appeared for the moment unable to articulate a syllable.", "니콜라스는 입술을 깨물고 위협하듯 고개를 저었지만, 그 순간만큼은 한마디도 내뱉을 수가 없는 듯했어. 케이트는 그의 팔에 더 바싹 매달렸고, 스마이크는 그들 뒤로 물러섰지."], ["Kate clung closer to his arm, Smike retreated behind them, and John Browdie, who had heard of Ralph, and appeared to have no great difficulty in recognising him, stepped between the old man and his young friend, as if with the intention of preventing either of them from advancing a step further.", "그리고 랄프에 대해 들은 적이 있어서 그를 알아보는 데 별 어려움이 없어 보이던 존 브라우디가, 노인과 자기 젊은 친구 사이로 끼어들었어. 마치 둘 중 누구도 한 발짝 더 나아가지 못하게 막으려는 듯이 말이야."]], [["‘Hear me, I say,’ said Ralph, ‘and not him.’", "\"내 말을 들으라니까,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"저 녀석 말 말고.\""]], [["‘Say what thou’st gotten to say then, sir,’ retorted John; ‘and tak’ care thou dinnot put up angry bluid which thou’dst betther try to quiet.’", "\"그럼 할 말이 있거든 하시오, 영감.\" 존이 받아쳤어. \"그라고 가라앉히는 게 나을 화딱지를 괜히 돋우지 않게 조심하슈.\""]], [["‘I should know YOU,’ said Ralph, ‘by your tongue; and HIM’ (pointing to Smike) ‘by his looks.’", "\"네놈이 누군지는,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그 말투를 보고 알겠다. 그리고 저놈은\" (스마이크를 가리키며) \"그 몰골을 보고 알겠고.\""]], [["‘Don’t speak to him,’ said Nicholas, recovering his voice.", "\"저 사람한테 말 걸지 마요.\" 니콜라스가 목소리를 되찾으며 말했어."], ["‘I will not have it.", "\"그건 못 참아요."], ["I will not hear him.", "저 사람 말을 듣지 않을 겁니다."], ["I do not know that man.", "나는 저 사람을 몰라요."], ["I cannot breathe the air that he corrupts.", "저 사람이 더럽히는 공기를 마실 수가 없어요."], ["His presence is an insult to my sister.", "저 사람이 여기 있는 건 내 누이에 대한 모욕이에요."], ["It is shame to see him.", "저 사람을 보는 것조차 수치스럽고요."], ["I will not bear it.’", "못 견디겠어요.\""]], [["‘Stand!’ cried John, laying his heavy hand upon his chest.", "\"가만있게!\" 존이 그의 가슴에 묵직한 손을 얹으며 소리쳤어."]], [["‘Then let him instantly retire,’ said Nicholas, struggling.", "\"그럼 당장 저 사람을 물러가게 해요.\" 니콜라스가 버둥대며 말했어."], ["‘I am not going to lay hands upon him, but he shall withdraw.", "\"내가 손을 대진 않을 거예요. 하지만 저 사람은 나가야 해요."], ["I will not have him here.", "여기 두지 않겠어요."], ["John, John Browdie, is this my house, am I a child?", "존, 존 브라우디, 여기가 내 집이 아닙니까, 내가 어린애예요? 저 사람이 저렇게 서서,\""], ["If he stands there,’ cried Nicholas, burning with fury, ‘looking so calmly upon those who know his black and dastardly heart, he’ll drive me mad.’", "니콜라스가 분노로 활활 타오르며 외쳤어. \"저 사람의 시커멓고 비열한 속을 다 아는 사람들을 저렇게 태연히 내려다보고 있으면, 나는 미쳐 버릴 거예요.\""]], [["To all these exclamations John Browdie answered not a word, but he retained his hold upon Nicholas; and when he was silent again, spoke.", "이 모든 외침에 존 브라우디는 한마디도 대꾸하지 않았어. 그저 니콜라스를 붙든 손을 놓지 않았지. 그리고 니콜라스가 다시 잠잠해지자 입을 열었어."]], [["‘There’s more to say and hear than thou think’st for,’ said John.", "\"자네 생각보다 할 말도, 들을 말도 더 많아.\""], ["‘I tell’ee I ha’ gotten scent o’ thot already.", "존이 말했어. \"나도 벌써 낌새를 챘다니께."], ["Wa’at be that shadow ootside door there?", "저기 문밖에 어른거리는 게 뭐여?"], ["Noo, schoolmeasther, show thyself, mun; dinnot be sheame-feaced.", "자, 선상님, 모습을 드러내 보슈, 양반. 부끄러워 말고."], ["Noo, auld gen’l’man, let’s have schoolmeasther, coom.’", "자, 영감, 선상님 좀 나오게 합시다, 어서.\""]], [["Hearing this adjuration, Mr.", "이 호통을 듣자, 들어오기에 알맞은 때가 되어 효과 있게 나타날 수 있을 때까지 복도에서 머뭇거리던 스퀴어스 씨는, 별수 없이 좀 위신 없고 슬그머니 기어드는 꼴로 모습을 드러냈어."], ["Squeers, who had been lingering in the passage until such time as it should be expedient for him to enter and he could appear with effect, was fain to present himself in a somewhat undignified and sneaking way; at which John Browdie laughed with such keen and heartfelt delight, that even Kate, in all the pain, anxiety, and surprise of the scene, and though the tears were in her eyes, felt a disposition to join him.", "그 꼴에 존 브라우디가 어찌나 날카롭고 진심 어린 즐거움으로 깔깔 웃어 대던지, 케이트조차 그 자리의 온갖 고통과 불안과 놀라움 속에서, 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채로도 따라 웃고 싶은 마음이 들 정도였지."]], [["‘Have you done enjoying yourself, sir?’ said Ralph, at length.", "\"실컷 즐기셨소, 양반?\" 마침내 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Pratty nigh for the prasant time, sir,’ replied John.", "\"지끔으로선 거진 다 즐겼소이다.\" 존이 대꾸했어."]], [["‘I can wait,’ said Ralph.", "\"기다리지.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘Take your own time, pray.’", "\"천천히 하게나.\""]], [["Ralph waited until there was a perfect silence, and then turning to Mrs Nickleby, but directing an eager glance at Kate, as if more anxious to watch his effect upon her, said:", "랄프는 완전히 조용해질 때까지 기다렸어. 그러고는 니클비 부인 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸지만, 케이트한테 열렬한 눈길을 던졌어. 마치 케이트한테 미치는 효과를 더 살피고 싶다는 듯이 말이야. 그러고는 입을 열었어."]], [["‘Now, ma’am, listen to me.", "\"자, 부인, 내 말을 들으시오. 댁의 그 아들 녀석이 나한테 보낸 그 거창한 일장 연설에 부인이 한몫 끼었다고는 생각하지 않소."], ["I don’t imagine that you were a party to a very fine tirade of words sent me by that boy of yours, because I don’t believe that under his control, you have the slightest will of your own, or that your advice, your opinion, your wants, your wishes, anything which in nature and reason (or of what use is your great experience?) ought to weigh with him, has the slightest influence or weight whatever, or is taken for a moment into account.’", "저 녀석한테 휘둘리는 처지에서 부인한테 손톱만큼이라도 자기 의지가 있다고는 믿지 않으니까. 부인의 충고든, 의견이든, 바람이든, 소원이든, 자연과 이치로 따져 마땅히 (안 그러면 그 풍부한 경험이 다 무슨 소용이오?) 저 녀석이 새겨들어야 할 것들이, 손톱만큼이라도 영향이나 무게가 있다고도, 한순간이라도 고려된다고도 믿지 않소.\""]], [["Mrs.", "니클비 부인은 고개를 절레절레 젓고 한숨을 쉬었어."], ["Nickleby shook her head and sighed, as if there were a good deal in that, certainly.", "거기에 분명 일리가 있다는 듯이 말이야."]], [["‘For this reason,’ resumed Ralph, ‘I address myself to you, ma’am. For this reason, partly, and partly because I do not wish to be disgraced by the acts of a vicious stripling whom I was obliged to disown, and who, afterwards, in his boyish majesty, feigns to--ha! ha!--to disown ME, I present myself here tonight. I have another motive in coming: a motive of humanity. I come here,’ said Ralph, looking round with a biting and triumphant smile, and gloating and dwelling upon the words as if he were loath to lose the pleasure of saying them, ‘to restore a parent his child. Ay, sir,’ he continued, bending eagerly forward, and addressing Nicholas, as he marked the change of his countenance, ‘to restore a parent his child; his son, sir; trepanned, waylaid, and guarded at every turn by you, with the base design of robbing him some day of any little wretched pittance of which he might become possessed.’", "\"바로 그래서,\" 랄프가 말을 이었어. \"부인한테 말을 하는 거요. 한편으론 그래서고, 또 한편으론, 내가 어쩔 수 없이 의절해야 했던 비뚤어진 애송이, 그러고는 나중에 제 어린 위세를 부리며 — 하! 하! — 도리어 나를 의절한 척하는 그 녀석의 행실 때문에 망신당하고 싶지 않아서, 오늘 밤 여기 온 거요. 또 다른 동기도 있소. 인정에서 우러난 동기지. 나는 여기 왔소,\" 랄프가 신랄하고 의기양양한 미소를 띠고 둘레를 둘러보며 말했어. 마치 이 말을 하는 즐거움을 놓치기 싫다는 듯, 그 말 한마디 한마디를 음미하고 곱씹으면서 말이야. \"한 아비에게 그 자식을 돌려주려고 말이오. 그렇소, 양반.\" 그는 니콜라스의 낯빛이 변하는 걸 보고는, 열심히 앞으로 몸을 숙이며 니콜라스를 향해 말을 이었어. \"한 아비에게 그 자식을 돌려주려고 왔소. 그 아들을 말이야, 양반. 자네가 함정에 빠뜨리고, 가로채고, 굽이굽이마다 지키고 섰던 그 아들 말이야. 언젠가 저 애가 얼마 안 되는 비참한 돈푼이라도 갖게 되면 그걸 빼앗으려는 비열한 속셈으로 말이지.\""]], [["‘In that, you know you lie,’ said Nicholas, proudly.", "\"그건, 당신도 거짓말인 줄 알면서 하는 소리예요.\" 니콜라스가 당당하게 말했어."]], [["‘In this, I know I speak the truth.", "\"이건, 내가 진실을 말하는 줄 알면서 하는 소리지."], ["I have his father here,’ retorted Ralph.", "저 애 아비를 여기 데려왔다.\" 랄프가 받아쳤어."]], [["‘Here!’ sneered Squeers, stepping forward.", "\"여기!\" 스퀴어스가 앞으로 나서며 비웃었어."], ["‘Do you hear that?", "\"들었나?"], ["Here!", "여기!"], ["Didn’t I tell you to be careful that his father didn’t turn up and send him back to me?", "내가 그랬지, 저 애 아비가 나타나서 저 애를 나한테 돌려보낼지 모르니 조심하라고?"], ["Why, his father’s my friend; he’s to come back to me directly, he is.", "거 봐, 저 애 아비는 내 친구야. 저 애는 곧장 나한테 돌아올 거라고."], ["Now, what do you say--eh!--now--come--what do you say to that--an’t you sorry you took so much trouble for nothing?", "자, 이제 무슨 말을 할 텐가 — 엉! — 자 — 어디 — 이거에 뭐라고 할 거냔 말이야 — 괜히 헛수고한 게 분하지 않나?"], ["an’t you?", "안 분해?"], ["an’t you?’", "안 분하냐고?\""]], [["‘You bear upon your body certain marks I gave you,’ said Nicholas, looking quietly away, ‘and may talk in acknowledgment of them as much as you please.", "\"당신 몸뚱이엔 내가 새겨 준 자국이 몇 개 있을 텐데,\" 니콜라스가 조용히 눈길을 돌리며 말했어."], ["You’ll talk a long time before you rub them out, Mr Squeers.’", "\"그걸 빌미로 떠들고 싶은 만큼 떠들어요. 그걸 지우려면 한참 걸릴 테니까, 스퀴어스 씨.\""]], [["The estimable gentleman last named cast a hasty look at the table, as if he were prompted by this retort to throw a jug or bottle at the head of Nicholas, but he was interrupted in this design (if such design he had) by Ralph, who, touching him on the elbow, bade him tell the father that he might now appear and claim his son.", "방금 말한 그 훌륭하신 양반은 식탁을 흘끔 쳐다봤어. 이 대꾸에 발끈해서 물주전자나 병이라도 니콜라스 머리에 던질 작정인 양 말이야. 하지만 그런 작정이 있었다 한들 랄프가 가로막았어. 랄프는 스퀴어스의 팔꿈치를 툭 건드리며, 이제 아비를 나오게 해서 제 아들을 데려가라고 하라고 일렀거든."]], [["This being purely a labour of love, Mr. Squeers readily complied, and leaving the room for the purpose, almost immediately returned, supporting a sleek personage with an oily face, who, bursting from him, and giving to view the form and face of Mr. Snawley, made straight up to Smike, and tucking that poor fellow’s head under his arm in a most uncouth and awkward embrace, elevated his broad-brimmed hat at arm’s length in the air as a token of devout thanksgiving, exclaiming, meanwhile, ‘How little did I think of this here joyful meeting, when I saw him last! Oh, how little did I think it!’", "이건 순전히 즐거워서 하는 일이라, 스퀴어스 씨는 선뜻 따랐어. 그러려고 방을 나간 그는 거의 곧바로 돌아왔는데, 기름기 흐르는 얼굴에 매끈한 사내 하나를 부축하고 왔지. 그 사내는 스퀴어스한테서 떨어져 나오며 스놀리 씨의 모습과 얼굴을 드러냈어. 그러고는 곧장 스마이크한테 다가가, 그 가엾은 녀석의 머리를 제 팔 밑에 끼고 더없이 거칠고 어설프게 끌어안았지. 그러고는 챙 넓은 모자를 팔을 쭉 뻗어 공중에 높이 치켜들었어. 경건한 감사의 표시로 말이야. 그러면서 이렇게 외쳤지. \"이 녀석을 마지막으로 봤을 때, 이렇게 기쁜 만남을 어찌 짐작이나 했겠소! 오, 어찌 짐작이나 했겠소!\""]], [["‘Be composed, sir,’ said Ralph, with a gruff expression of sympathy, ‘you have got him now.’", "\"진정하시오.\" 랄프가 무뚝뚝하게 동정하는 투로 말했어. \"이제 자식을 찾았잖소.\""]], [["‘Got him!", "\"찾았다!"], ["Oh, haven’t I got him!", "오, 내가 이 애를 찾다니!"], ["Have I got him, though?’ cried Mr Snawley, scarcely able to believe it.", "정말 내가 이 애를 찾은 거요?\" 스놀리 씨가 도무지 믿기지 않는 듯 외쳤어."], ["‘Yes, here he is, flesh and blood, flesh and blood.’", "\"그래, 여기 있구나, 피와 살이, 피와 살이.\""]], [["‘Vary little flesh,’ said John Browdie.", "\"살은 쥐꼬리만큼밖에 없구먼.\" 존 브라우디가 말했어."]], [["Mr.", "스놀리 씨는 아비의 정에 너무 사로잡혀 이 말을 알아채지도 못했어."], ["Snawley was too much occupied by his parental feelings to notice this remark; and, to assure himself more completely of the restoration of his child, tucked his head under his arm again, and kept it there.", "그러고는 자식을 되찾았다는 걸 더 확실히 하려는 듯, 그 머리를 다시 제 팔 밑에 끼고는 거기서 빼지 않았지."]], [["‘What was it,’ said Snawley, ‘that made me take such a strong interest in him, when that worthy instructor of youth brought him to my house?", "\"대체 뭐였을까,\" 스놀리가 말했어."], ["What was it that made me burn all over with a wish to chastise him severely for cutting away from his best friends, his pastors and masters?’", "\"그 훌륭한 청소년 교육자께서 이 애를 우리 집에 데려왔을 때, 이 애한테 그토록 강한 관심이 일었던 게 말이오? 대체 뭐였길래, 제일 좋은 친구들, 자기 목사며 스승한테서 도망친 죄로 이 애를 호되게 벌하고 싶어 온몸이 다 달아올랐던 거요?\""]], [["‘It was parental instinct, sir,’ observed Squeers.", "\"그게 바로 부정(父情)의 본능이지요, 선생.\" 스퀴어스가 한마디 했어."]], [["‘That’s what it was, sir,’ rejoined Snawley; ‘the elevated feeling, the feeling of the ancient Romans and Grecians, and of the beasts of the field and birds of the air, with the exception of rabbits and tom-cats, which sometimes devour their offspring.", "\"바로 그거였소.\" 스놀리가 맞장구쳤어."], ["My heart yearned towards him.", "\"그 고결한 감정, 옛 로마인과 그리스인의 감정, 또 들판의 짐승과 공중의 새들이 지닌 감정이지요. 가끔 제 새끼를 잡아먹는 토끼하고 수고양이만 빼고 말이오."], ["I could have--I don’t know what I couldn’t have done to him in the anger of a father.’", "내 가슴이 이 애를 향해 사무쳤다오. 아비의 노여움에 휩싸여 이 애한테 무슨 짓을 — 무슨 짓인들 못 했을지 나도 모르겠소.\""]], [["‘It only shows what Natur is, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"그게 다 본성이라는 게 뭔지 보여 주는 거지요, 선생.\""], ["Squeers.", "스퀴어스 씨가 말했어."], ["‘She’s rum ‘un, is Natur.’", "\"본성이란 참 별난 놈이거든요.\""]], [["‘She is a holy thing, sir,’ remarked Snawley.", "\"본성은 거룩한 거지요, 선생.\" 스놀리가 말했어."]], [["‘I believe you,’ added Mr.", "\"옳은 말씀입니다.\""], ["Squeers, with a moral sigh.", "스퀴어스 씨가 짐짓 점잖게 한숨을 쉬며 덧붙였어."], ["‘I should like to know how we should ever get on without her.", "\"본성 없이 우리가 대체 어떻게 살아갈지 모르겠어요."], ["Natur,’ said Mr.", "본성이란,\""], ["Squeers, solemnly, ‘is more easier conceived than described.", "스퀴어스 씨가 엄숙하게 말했어. \"말로 그리기보다 마음으로 그리기가 더 쉬운 거지요."], ["Oh what a blessed thing, sir, to be in a state of natur!’", "오, 본성 그대로의 상태에 있다는 건 얼마나 복된 일입니까, 선생!\""]], [["Pending this philosophical discourse, the bystanders had been quite stupefied with amazement, while Nicholas had looked keenly from Snawley to Squeers, and from Squeers to Ralph, divided between his feelings of disgust, doubt, and surprise.", "이 철학적인 이야기가 오가는 동안, 옆에 있던 사람들은 놀라움에 그저 얼이 빠져 있었어. 한편 니콜라스는 스놀리에게서 스퀴어스로, 스퀴어스에게서 랄프로 날카롭게 눈길을 옮기며, 혐오와 의심과 놀라움 사이에서 갈피를 못 잡고 있었지."], ["At this juncture, Smike escaping from his father fled to Nicholas, and implored him, in most moving terms, never to give him up, but to let him live and die beside him.", "바로 그때, 스마이크가 아비한테서 빠져나와 니콜라스한테로 달아났어. 그러고는 더없이 애절한 말로, 제발 자기를 내주지 말고 당신 곁에서 살다 죽게 해 달라고 애원했지."]], [["‘If you are this boy’s father,’ said Nicholas, ‘look at the wreck he is, and tell me that you purpose to send him back to that loathsome den from which I brought him.’", "\"당신이 이 애 아비라면,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"이 애가 얼마나 망가졌는지 보세요. 그러고도 내가 데려온 그 역겨운 소굴로 이 애를 돌려보낼 작정이라고 말할 수 있어요?\""]], [["‘Scandal again!’ cried Squeers.", "\"또 험담이군!\" 스퀴어스가 외쳤어."], ["‘Recollect, you an’t worth powder and shot, but I’ll be even with you one way or another.’", "\"잘 새겨 둬, 자넨 화약이고 총알이고 들일 가치도 없는 놈이지만, 이러나저러나 내 꼭 앙갚음하고 말 테니까.\""]], [["‘Stop,’ interposed Ralph, as Snawley was about to speak.", "\"그만.\" 스놀리가 막 말을 꺼내려는데 랄프가 가로막았어."], ["‘Let us cut this matter short, and not bandy words here with hare-brained profligates.", "\"이 일을 짧게 끝냅시다. 머리 빈 난봉꾼들이랑 여기서 입씨름하지 말고."], ["This is your son, as you can prove. And you, Mr.", "이 애는 댁의 아들이오, 댁이 증명할 수 있듯이."], ["Squeers, you know this boy to be the same that was with you for so many years under the name of Smike.", "그리고 스퀴어스 씨, 당신은 이 애가 그토록 여러 해 스마이크라는 이름으로 당신한테 있던 바로 그 아이라는 걸 알지."], ["Do you?’", "안 그렇소?\""]], [["‘Do I!’ returned Squeers.", "\"내가 아냐고요!\" 스퀴어스가 받았어."], ["‘Don’t I?’", "\"모를 리가요?\""]], [["‘Good,’ said Ralph; ‘a very few words will be sufficient here.", "\"좋소.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["You had a son by your first wife, Mr.", "\"여기선 몇 마디면 충분하겠군."], ["Snawley?’", "댁은 첫 아내한테서 아들을 하나 얻었소, 스놀리 씨?\""]], [["‘I had,’ replied that person, ‘and there he stands.’", "\"그랬지요.\" 그자가 대답했어. \"그리고 저기 그 애가 서 있고요.\""]], [["‘We’ll show that presently,’ said Ralph.", "\"그건 곧 밝혀집시다.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘You and your wife were separated, and she had the boy to live with her, when he was a year old.", "\"댁과 댁의 아내는 헤어졌고, 아이가 한 살 때 아내가 데려가 키웠지."], ["You received a communication from her, when you had lived apart a year or two, that the boy was dead; and you believed it?’", "따로 산 지 한두 해쯤 됐을 때, 아내한테서 그 애가 죽었다는 기별을 받았고, 댁은 그걸 믿었소?\""]], [["‘Of course I did!’ returned Snawley.", "\"당연히 믿었지요!\" 스놀리가 대답했어."], ["‘Oh the joy of--’", "\"오, 이 기쁨이란 —\""]], [["‘Be rational, sir, pray,’ said Ralph.", "\"제발 정신 좀 차리시오.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘This is business, and transports interfere with it.", "\"이건 일이오. 감격에 겨워하면 일에 방해가 된단 말이오."], ["This wife died a year and a half ago, or thereabouts--not more--in some obscure place, where she was housekeeper in a family.", "그 아내는 한 일 년 반쯤 전에, 아니 그쯤 — 그보다 더는 아니고 — 어느 외진 곳에서 죽었소. 한 집안의 가정부로 있던 데서 말이오."], ["Is that the case?’", "그렇소?\""]], [["‘That’s the case,’ replied Snawley.", "\"그렇습니다.\" 스놀리가 대답했어."]], [["‘Having written on her death-bed a letter or confession to you, about this very boy, which, as it was not directed otherwise than in your name, only reached you, and that by a circuitous course, a few days since?’", "\"죽기 직전에 댁한테 바로 이 애에 관한 편지인지 고백인지를 써 놓았고, 그게 댁 이름 말고는 달리 받는 데가 적혀 있지 않아서 댁한테만, 그것도 빙 돌아 며칠 전에야 닿았소?\""]], [["‘Just so,’ said Snawley.", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 스놀리가 말했어."], ["‘Correct in every particular, sir.’", "\"하나하나 다 정확합니다, 선생.\""]], [["‘And this confession,’ resumed Ralph, ‘is to the effect that his death was an invention of hers to wound you--was a part of a system of annoyance, in short, which you seem to have adopted towards each other--that the boy lived, but was of weak and imperfect intellect--that she sent him by a trusty hand to a cheap school in Yorkshire--that she had paid for his education for some years, and then, being poor, and going a long way off, gradually deserted him, for which she prayed forgiveness?’", "\"그리고 그 고백은,\" 랄프가 말을 이었어. \"이런 내용이지. 아이가 죽었다는 건 댁한테 상처를 주려고 아내가 꾸며 낸 거짓말이었다 — 한마디로, 두 사람이 서로한테 써먹은 듯한 그 괴롭힘의 한 수단이었던 거지 — 아이는 살아 있었지만 머리가 약하고 모자랐다 — 그래서 믿을 만한 사람 편에 요크셔의 어느 싼 학교로 보냈다 — 몇 해 동안 학비를 댔지만, 그러다 형편이 어려워지고 멀리 떠나게 되면서 차차 그 애를 버렸다, 그 일을 용서해 달라고 빌었다 — 그렇소?\""]], [["Snawley nodded his head, and wiped his eyes; the first slightly, the last violently.", "스놀리는 고개를 끄덕이고 눈을 닦았어. 끄덕이는 건 가볍게, 닦는 건 격렬하게."]], [["‘The school was Mr.", "\"그 학교가 바로 스퀴어스 씨의 학교였고,\""], ["Squeers’s,’ continued Ralph; ‘the boy was left there in the name of Smike; every description was fully given, dates tally exactly with Mr.", "랄프가 말을 이었어. \"아이는 스마이크라는 이름으로 거기 맡겨졌소."], ["Squeers’s books, Mr.", "생김새가 죄다 빠짐없이 적혀 있고, 날짜도 스퀴어스 씨의 장부와 정확히 맞아떨어지지."], ["Squeers is lodging with you at this time; you have two other boys at his school: you communicated the whole discovery to him, he brought you to me as the person who had recommended to him the kidnapper of his child; and I brought you here.", "스퀴어스 씨는 지금 댁 집에 묵고 있고, 댁은 그 학교에 아들 둘을 더 보내 두었고. 댁은 이 모든 사실을 그한테 다 알렸고, 그는 제 자식을 납치한 자를 자기한테 소개해 준 사람이라며 댁을 나한테 데려왔고, 나는 댁을 여기로 데려왔소."], ["Is that so?’", "그렇소?\""]], [["‘You talk like a good book, sir, that’s got nothing in its inside but what’s the truth,’ replied Snawley.", "\"선생은 꼭 좋은 책처럼 말씀하시는구려. 속에 진실 말고는 아무것도 안 든 그런 책 말이오.\" 스놀리가 대답했어."]], [["‘This is your pocket-book,’ said Ralph, producing one from his coat; ‘the certificates of your first marriage and of the boy’s birth, and your wife’s two letters, and every other paper that can support these statements directly or by implication, are here, are they?’", "\"이건 댁의 수첩이고,\" 랄프가 외투에서 수첩 하나를 꺼내며 말했어. \"댁의 첫 혼인 증명서와 아이의 출생 증명서, 그리고 댁 아내의 편지 두 통, 그 밖에 이 진술들을 직접으로든 넌지시로든 뒷받침할 온갖 서류가 다 여기 들어 있소, 그렇소?\""]], [["‘Every one of ‘em, sir.’", "\"하나도 빠짐없이요, 선생.\""]], [["‘And you don’t object to their being looked at here, so that these people may be convinced of your power to substantiate your claim at once in law and reason, and you may resume your control over your own son without more delay.", "\"그리고 댁은 이 서류들을 여기서 살펴보는 데 반대하지 않지. 이 사람들이, 댁이 법으로나 이치로나 당장 그 권리를 입증할 힘이 있다는 걸 똑똑히 알게, 또 댁이 더 미룰 것 없이 제 아들에 대한 권리를 되찾게 말이오."], ["Do I understand you?’", "내 말 알아듣겠소?\""]], [["‘I couldn’t have understood myself better, sir.’", "\"제가 직접 말해도 이보다 더 잘 알아듣게는 못 했을 겁니다, 선생.\""]], [["‘There, then,’ said Ralph, tossing the pocket-book upon the table. ‘Let them see them if they like; and as those are the original papers, I should recommend you to stand near while they are being examined, or you may chance to lose some.’", "\"그럼 자, 여기 있소.\" 랄프가 수첩을 식탁 위에 툭 던지며 말했어. \"보고 싶으면 보라고 하시오. 그게 원본 서류니까, 살펴보는 동안 댁이 가까이 서 있는 게 좋겠소. 안 그러면 몇 장 잃어버릴지도 모르니.\""]], [["With these words Ralph sat down unbidden, and compressing his lips, which were for the moment slightly parted by a smile, folded his arms, and looked for the first time at his nephew.", "이 말과 함께 랄프는 권하지도 않았는데 앉았어. 그러고는 잠깐 미소로 살짝 벌어졌던 입술을 꼭 다물고, 팔짱을 끼고는, 처음으로 조카를 쳐다봤어."]], [["Nicholas, stung by the concluding taunt, darted an indignant glance at him; but commanding himself as well as he could, entered upon a close examination of the documents, at which John Browdie assisted.", "니콜라스는 그 마지막 비꼼에 발끈해서 그에게 분노 어린 눈길을 쏘아붙였어. 하지만 최대한 자신을 다잡고는, 그 서류들을 꼼꼼히 살펴보기 시작했지."], ["There was nothing about them which could be called in question.", "존 브라우디가 거들었고. 그 서류엔 트집 잡을 만한 데가 하나도 없었어."], ["The certificates were regularly signed as extracts from the parish books, the first letter had a genuine appearance of having been written and preserved for some years, the handwriting of the second tallied with it exactly, (making proper allowance for its having been written by a person in extremity,) and there were several other corroboratory scraps of entries and memoranda which it was equally difficult to question.", "증명서들은 교구 장부에서 베껴 낸 것으로 제대로 서명이 되어 있었고, 첫 편지는 정말로 몇 해 전에 쓰여 보관되어 온 듯한 진짜배기 분위기가 났고, 둘째 편지의 글씨체도 그것과 정확히 들어맞았어(죽어 가는 사람이 쓴 거라는 점을 감안하면 말이야). 그 밖에도 뒷받침이 될 만한 자잘한 기록과 메모 쪼가리가 여럿 있었는데, 이것들도 똑같이 트집 잡기 어려웠지."]], [["‘Dear Nicholas,’ whispered Kate, who had been looking anxiously over his shoulder, ‘can this be really the case?", "\"오빠,\" 니콜라스 어깨너머로 불안하게 들여다보던 케이트가 속삭였어."], ["Is this statement true?’", "\"이게 정말 사실일 수 있어? 이 진술이 진짜야?\""]], [["‘I fear it is,’ answered Nicholas.", "\"그런 것 같아.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘What say you, John?’", "\"자넨 어떻게 보나, 존?\""]], [["John scratched his head and shook it, but said nothing at all.", "존은 머리를 긁적이며 절레절레 흔들기만 했을 뿐, 아무 말도 하지 않았어."]], [["‘You will observe, ma’am,’ said Ralph, addressing himself to Mrs Nickleby, ‘that this boy being a minor and not of strong mind, we might have come here tonight, armed with the powers of the law, and backed by a troop of its myrmidons. I should have done so, ma’am, unquestionably, but for my regard for the feelings of yourself, and your daughter.’", "\"부인, 잘 보아 두시오.\" 랄프가 니클비 부인을 향해 말했어. \"이 애가 미성년인 데다 머리도 온전치 못하니, 우리는 오늘 밤 법의 권한으로 무장하고, 법의 졸개들을 한 떼 거느리고 여기 올 수도 있었소. 분명히 그렇게 했을 거요, 부인. 부인과 따님의 마음을 헤아리지 않았더라면 말이오.\""]], [["‘You have shown your regard for HER feelings well,’ said Nicholas, drawing his sister towards him.", "\"누이의 마음을 참 잘도 헤아려 주셨군요.\" 니콜라스가 누이를 제 쪽으로 끌어당기며 말했어."]], [["‘Thank you,’ replied Ralph.", "\"고맙군.\" 랄프가 대답했어."], ["‘Your praise, sir, is commendation, indeed.’", "\"자네 칭찬은, 양반, 정말이지 찬사로구먼.\""]], [["‘Well,’ said Squeers, ‘what’s to be done?", "\"그래,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["Them hackney-coach horses will catch cold if we don’t think of moving; there’s one of ‘em a sneezing now, so that he blows the street door right open.", "\"이제 어쩐다? 우리가 움직일 생각을 안 하면 저 삯마차 말들이 감기 들겠는데."], ["What’s the order of the day?", "지금 한 놈이 재채기를 하는 통에 현관문이 활짝 열리잖아."], ["Is Master Snawley to come along with us?’", "오늘 일정은 뭐요? 스놀리 도련님은 우리랑 같이 가는 거요?\""]], [["‘No, no, no,’ replied Smike, drawing back, and clinging to Nicholas.", "\"싫어, 싫어, 싫어요.\" 스마이크가 뒷걸음치며 니콜라스에게 매달려 외쳤어."], ["‘No.", "\"싫어요."], ["Pray, no.", "제발, 싫어요."], ["I will not go from you with him.", "저 사람이랑은 당신 곁을 떠나지 않을 거예요."], ["No, no.’", "싫어요, 싫어.\""]], [["‘This is a cruel thing,’ said Snawley, looking to his friends for support.", "\"이건 잔인한 짓이오.\" 스놀리가 자기 편들을 도와 달라는 듯 둘러보며 말했어."], ["‘Do parents bring children into the world for this?’", "\"부모가 자식을 이러려고 세상에 낳소?\""]], [["‘Do parents bring children into the world for THOT?’ said John Browdie bluntly, pointing, as he spoke, to Squeers.", "\"부모가 자식을 저러려고 세상에 낳냔 말이여?\" 존 브라우디가 말하며 무뚝뚝하게 스퀴어스를 가리켰어."]], [["‘Never you mind,’ retorted that gentleman, tapping his nose derisively.", "\"네놈은 상관 마.\" 그 양반이 비웃듯 제 코를 톡톡 두드리며 받아쳤어."]], [["‘Never I mind!’ said John, ‘no, nor never nobody mind, say’st thou, schoolmeasther.", "\"내가 상관 마!\" 존이 말했어."], ["It’s nobody’s minding that keeps sike men as thou afloat.", "\"그려, 아무도 상관 안 한다는 거 아녀, 선상님. 아무도 상관 안 헝께 너 같은 놈이 떠다니는 거여."], ["Noo then, where be’est thou coomin’ to?", "자 그럼, 어디로 오는 거여?"], ["Dang it, dinnot coom treadin’ ower me, mun.’", "제기랄, 날 짓밟고 오지 말랑께, 양반아.\""]], [["Suiting the action to the word, John Browdie just jerked his elbow into the chest of Mr.", "말이 떨어지기 무섭게, 존 브라우디는 스마이크에게 다가오던 스퀴어스 씨의 가슴팍에 팔꿈치를 홱 박았어."], ["Squeers who was advancing upon Smike; with so much dexterity that the schoolmaster reeled and staggered back upon Ralph Nickleby, and being unable to recover his balance, knocked that gentleman off his chair, and stumbled heavily upon him.", "어찌나 잽쌌던지 그 학교 선생은 비틀비틀 뒷걸음치다 랄프 니클비한테 부딪쳤고, 몸을 가누지 못해 그 양반을 의자에서 넘어뜨리고는 그 위로 묵직하게 자빠졌지."]], [["This accidental circumstance was the signal for some very decisive proceedings.", "이 우연한 사태가 아주 단호한 행동들의 신호탄이 됐어."], ["In the midst of a great noise, occasioned by the prayers and entreaties of Smike, the cries and exclamations of the women, and the vehemence of the men, demonstrations were made of carrying off the lost son by violence.", "스마이크의 기도와 애원, 여자들의 비명과 외침, 사내들의 격앙이 뒤엉켜 큰 소란이 이는 가운데, 잃었던 아들을 힘으로 끌어내려는 시도가 벌어졌지."], ["Squeers had actually begun to haul him out, when Nicholas (who, until then, had been evidently undecided how to act) took him by the collar, and shaking him so that such teeth as he had, chattered in his head, politely escorted him to the room-door, and thrusting him into the passage, shut it upon him.", "스퀴어스가 정말로 스마이크를 끌어내기 시작했는데, 바로 그때 니콜라스가(그때까진 어떻게 해야 할지 분명히 마음을 정하지 못하고 있었어) 그의 멱살을 잡았어. 그러고는 몇 개 안 남은 이가 머릿속에서 딱딱 부딪칠 만큼 그를 흔들어 댄 뒤, 정중하게 방문까지 데려다주고는, 복도로 떠밀어 내고 문을 닫아 버렸지."]], [["‘Now,’ said Nicholas to the other two, ‘have the goodness to follow your friend.’", "\"자,\" 니콜라스가 나머지 두 사람에게 말했어. \"친절을 베풀어서 친구분을 따라가시지요.\""]], [["‘I want my son,’ said Snawley.", "\"내 아들을 내놓으시오.\" 스놀리가 말했어."]], [["‘Your son,’ replied Nicholas, ‘chooses for himself.", "\"댁의 아들은,\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["He chooses to remain here, and he shall.’", "\"스스로 고를 거요. 여기 남는 쪽을 골랐으니, 그렇게 될 거고요.\""]], [["‘You won’t give him up?’ said Snawley.", "\"애를 안 내놓겠다는 거요?\" 스놀리가 말했어."]], [["‘I would not give him up against his will, to be the victim of such brutality as that to which you would consign him,’ replied Nicholas, ‘if he were a dog or a rat.’", "\"이 애가 개나 쥐라 해도, 본인 뜻을 거슬러서, 댁이 떠밀어 넣으려는 그런 잔인한 짓의 제물로 내주지는 않을 거요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Knock that Nickleby down with a candlestick,’ cried Mr.", "\"누가 촛대로 저 니클비 놈을 후려쳐 줘,\""], ["Squeers, through the keyhole, ‘and bring out my hat, somebody, will you, unless he wants to steal it.’", "스퀴어스 씨가 열쇠 구멍에 대고 외쳤어. \"그리고 누가 내 모자 좀 내와 줘, 저놈이 훔칠 생각이 아니라면 말이야.\""]], [["‘I am very sorry, indeed,’ said Mrs.", "\"정말이지, 너무 미안하구나.\""], ["Nickleby, who, with Mrs.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Browdie, had stood crying and biting her fingers in a corner, while Kate (very pale, but perfectly quiet) had kept as near her brother as she could.", "부인은 브라우디 부인과 함께 한구석에서 울며 손가락을 깨물고 서 있었고, 케이트는(낯빛이 아주 창백했지만 더없이 차분하게) 되도록 오빠 곁에 바싹 붙어 있었지."], ["‘I am very sorry, indeed, for all this.", "\"이 모든 일이 정말이지 너무 미안하구나."], ["I really don’t know what would be best to do, and that’s the truth.", "뭘 어떻게 하는 게 제일 좋을지 정말 모르겠다, 솔직히."], ["Nicholas ought to be the best judge, and I hope he is.", "니콜라스가 제일 잘 판단할 테고, 그러길 바란다."], ["Of course, it’s a hard thing to have to keep other people’s children, though young Mr.", "물론 남의 자식을 떠맡아 키운다는 건 힘든 일이지."], ["Snawley is certainly as useful and willing as it’s possible for anybody to be; but, if it could be settled in any friendly manner--if old Mr.", "스놀리 도련님이 누구 못지않게 쓸모 있고 싹싹하긴 하다만 말이다."], ["Snawley, for instance, would settle to pay something certain for his board and lodging, and some fair arrangement was come to, so that we undertook to have fish twice a week, and a pudding twice, or a dumpling, or something of that sort--I do think that it might be very satisfactory and pleasant for all parties.’", "하지만 무슨 다정한 방법으로 해결될 수만 있다면 — 이를테면 스놀리 영감이 저 애 먹고 자는 값으로 얼마쯤 딱 정해서 내기로 하고, 적당한 합의가 이뤄져서, 우리가 일주일에 생선을 두 번, 푸딩을 두 번, 아니면 경단이나 뭐 그런 걸 내기로 한다면 — 모두한테 아주 흡족하고 좋은 일이 될 것 같구나.\""]], [["This compromise, which was proposed with abundance of tears and sighs, not exactly meeting the point at issue, nobody took any notice of it; and poor Mrs.", "이 타협안은 눈물과 한숨을 한껏 곁들여 내놓은 것이었지만, 정작 핵심에서는 어긋나 있었어. 그래서 아무도 거기에 신경 쓰지 않았지."], ["Nickleby accordingly proceeded to enlighten Mrs. Browdie upon the advantages of such a scheme, and the unhappy results flowing, on all occasions, from her not being attended to when she proffered her advice.", "그러자 가엾은 니클비 부인은 브라우디 부인을 붙들고, 그런 방안이 얼마나 좋은지, 또 자기가 충고를 내놓았을 때 사람들이 귀담아듣지 않아서 늘 얼마나 불행한 결과가 빚어지는지를 일러 주기 시작했어."]], [["‘You, sir,’ said Snawley, addressing the terrified Smike, ‘are an unnatural, ungrateful, unlovable boy.", "\"이놈아,\" 스놀리가 겁에 질린 스마이크를 향해 말했어."], ["You won’t let me love you when I want to.", "\"너는 천륜도 모르고, 은혜도 모르고, 사랑스럽지도 못한 놈이다. 내가 사랑해 주고 싶어도 사랑하게 두질 않는구나."], ["Won’t you come home, won’t you?’", "집에 안 갈 테냐, 안 갈 거야?\""]], [["‘No, no, no,’ cried Smike, shrinking back.", "\"싫어요, 싫어요, 싫어요.\" 스마이크가 움츠러들며 외쳤어."]], [["‘He never loved nobody,’ bawled Squeers, through the keyhole.", "\"저놈은 누구도 사랑한 적이 없어.\" 스퀴어스가 열쇠 구멍에 대고 악을 썼어."], ["‘He never loved me; he never loved Wackford, who is next door but one to a cherubim.", "\"나도 사랑한 적이 없지. 천사 바로 옆자리쯤 되는 워크퍼드도 사랑한 적이 없고."], ["How can you expect that he’ll love his father?", "그러니 제 아비를 사랑하길 어찌 바라겠나?"], ["He’ll never love his father, he won’t.", "저놈은 절대 제 아비를 사랑 안 해, 절대."], ["He don’t know what it is to have a father.", "아비가 있다는 게 뭔지도 몰라."], ["He don’t understand it.", "도통 이해를 못 해."], ["It an’t in him.’", "그런 게 저놈한텐 없거든.\""]], [["Mr. Snawley looked steadfastly at his son for a full minute, and then covering his eyes with his hand, and once more raising his hat in the air, appeared deeply occupied in deploring his black ingratitude.", "스놀리 씨는 일 분 내내 제 아들을 뚫어지게 바라봤어."], ["Then drawing his arm across his eyes, he picked up Mr.", "그러고는 손으로 두 눈을 가리고, 다시 한번 모자를 공중에 치켜들고는, 아들의 시커먼 배은망덕을 깊이 한탄하는 데 골몰한 척했지."], ["Squeers’s hat, and taking it under one arm, and his own under the other, walked slowly and sadly out.", "그런 다음 팔로 두 눈을 슥 훔치고는, 스퀴어스 씨의 모자를 집어 들었어. 그러고는 그 모자를 한쪽 팔에, 제 모자를 다른 쪽 팔에 끼고, 느릿느릿 서글프게 걸어 나갔지."]], [["‘Your romance, sir,’ said Ralph, lingering for a moment, ‘is destroyed, I take it.", "\"자네의 그 로맨스는, 양반,\" 랄프가 잠깐 머뭇거리며 말했어."], ["No unknown; no persecuted descendant of a man of high degree; but the weak, imbecile son of a poor, petty tradesman.", "\"이걸로 다 깨진 셈이군. 정체 모를 사람도 아니고, 지체 높은 가문 후손이 박해받는 것도 아니고, 그저 가난하고 보잘것없는 장사꾼의 약하고 모자란 아들일 뿐이지."], ["We shall see how your sympathy melts before plain matter of fact.’", "자네의 그 동정심이 명백한 사실 앞에서 어떻게 녹아내리는지 두고 보겠네.\""]], [["‘You shall,’ said Nicholas, motioning towards the door.", "\"두고 보시지요.\" 니콜라스가 문 쪽으로 손짓하며 말했어."]], [["‘And trust me, sir,’ added Ralph, ‘that I never supposed you would give him up tonight.", "\"그리고 내 말 믿게, 양반,\" 랄프가 덧붙였어."], ["Pride, obstinacy, reputation for fine feeling, were all against it.", "\"나는 자네가 오늘 밤 저 애를 내줄 거라곤 한 번도 생각 안 했네. 자존심, 고집, 고운 마음씨라는 평판, 그게 다 그걸 막았을 테니까."], ["These must be brought down, sir, lowered, crushed, as they shall be soon.", "그것들은 무너져야 하네, 양반. 낮아지고, 짓밟혀야 해."], ["The protracted and wearing anxiety and expense of the law in its most oppressive form, its torture from hour to hour, its weary days and sleepless nights, with these I’ll prove you, and break your haughty spirit, strong as you deem it now.", "머잖아 그렇게 될 거고. 가장 가혹한 형태의 법, 그 질질 끌고 진을 빼는 불안과 비용, 시시각각의 고문, 지치는 낮과 잠 못 드는 밤 — 그것들로 자네를 시험하고, 지금은 아무리 강하다고 자부하는 그 오만한 기개를 꺾어 놓겠네."], ["And when you make this house a hell, and visit these trials upon yonder wretched object (as you will; I know you), and those who think you now a young-fledged hero, we’ll go into old accounts between us two, and see who stands the debtor, and comes out best at last, even before the world.’", "그리고 자네가 이 집을 지옥으로 만들고, 저기 저 가엾은 것한테 이 시련을 떠안길 때(자넨 그럴 테지, 내가 자넬 아니까), 또 지금 자네를 갓 날개 돋친 영웅쯤으로 여기는 사람들한테 그럴 때, 우리 둘 사이의 묵은 셈을 따져 보자고. 누가 빚진 쪽인지, 누가 끝에 가서, 온 세상 앞에서까지 잘되는지 어디 보세.\""]], [["Ralph Nickleby withdrew.", "랄프 니클비는 물러갔어. 하지만 이 마지막 한마디를 얼마쯤 들은 스퀴어스 씨는, 이때쯤 거의 전에 없던 무력한 악의로 잔뜩 약이 올라 있던 터라, 거실 문으로 도로 돌아오지 않을 수가 없었지."], ["But Mr.", "그러고는 정말로 갖가지 찡그린 낯과 흉측한 표정을 지어 가며 열댓 번이나 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰어 댔어."], ["Squeers, who had heard a portion of this closing address, and was by this time wound up to a pitch of impotent malignity almost unprecedented, could not refrain from returning to the parlour door, and actually cutting some dozen capers with various wry faces and hideous grimaces, expressive of his triumphant confidence in the downfall and defeat of Nicholas.", "니콜라스의 몰락과 패배를 의기양양하게 확신한다는 표시로 말이야."]], [["Having concluded this war-dance, in which his short trousers and large boots had borne a very conspicuous figure, Mr.", "이 전쟁 춤을 다 추고 나서 — 그 춤에서 짧은 바지와 큼지막한 장화가 아주 눈에 띄는 모양새를 했지 — 스퀴어스 씨는 친구들을 뒤따라갔어."], ["Squeers followed his friends, and the family were left to meditate upon recent occurrences.", "그리고 식구들만 남아 방금 벌어진 일들을 곱씹게 됐지."]], [["# CHAPTER 46", "# CHAPTER 46"]], [["Throws some Light upon Nicholas’s Love; but whether for Good or Evil the Reader must determine\nAfter an anxious consideration of the painful and embarrassing position in which he was placed, Nicholas decided that he ought to lose no time in frankly stating it to the kind brothers.", "니콜라스가 처한 괴롭고 난처한 처지를 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에, 그는 마음씨 좋은 형제에게 이 일을 솔직하게 털어놓는 데 한시도 지체하면 안 되겠다고 마음먹었어. 그래서 이튿날 일이 끝날 무렵 찰스 치어리블 씨와 단둘이 있게 된 첫 기회를 놓치지 않고, 스마이크의 짤막한 사연을 들려줬지."], ["Availing himself of the first opportunity of being alone with Mr.", "그러고는 조심스럽지만 단호하게 자기 바람을 밝혔어."], ["Charles Cheeryble at the close of next day, he accordingly related Smike’s little history, and modestly but firmly expressed his hope that the good old gentleman would, under such circumstances as he described, hold him justified in adopting the extreme course of interfering between parent and child, and upholding the latter in his disobedience; even though his horror and dread of his father might seem, and would doubtless be represented as, a thing so repulsive and unnatural, as to render those who countenanced him in it, fit objects of general detestation and abhorrence.", "자기가 말한 그런 사정이라면, 부모와 자식 사이에 끼어드는 극단적인 길을 택해서 아버지에게 불복하는 자식 편을 들어 준 일을 이 착한 노신사가 정당하다고 봐주기를 바란다고. 비록 그 아이가 아버지를 무서워하고 끔찍해하는 마음이 너무나 거슬리고 부자연스러운 일처럼 보이고, 또 틀림없이 그렇게 떠벌려질 테고, 그래서 그 아이를 거든 사람들이 온 세상의 미움과 손가락질을 받기 딱 좋은 처지가 된다 해도 말이야."]], [["‘So deeply rooted does this horror of the man appear to be,’ said Nicholas, ‘that I can hardly believe he really is his son. Nature does not seem to have implanted in his breast one lingering feeling of affection for him, and surely she can never err.’", "\"그 사람을 무서워하는 마음이 어찌나 뿌리 깊은지,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"정말 그 사람 아들이 맞는지 저는 도무지 믿기지가 않습니다. 그 애 가슴속에는 아버지를 향한 애틋한 정이 단 한 조각도 자연이 심어 놓지 않은 것 같아요. 그런데 자연은 결코 잘못을 저지르지 않잖습니까.\""]], [["‘My dear sir,’ replied brother Charles, ‘you fall into the very common mistake of charging upon Nature, matters with which she has not the smallest connection, and for which she is in no way responsible. Men talk of Nature as an abstract thing, and lose sight of what is natural while they do so. Here is a poor lad who has never felt a parent’s care, who has scarcely known anything all his life but suffering and sorrow, presented to a man who he is told is his father, and whose first act is to signify his intention of putting an end to his short term of happiness, of consigning him to his old fate, and taking him from the only friend he has ever had--which is yourself. If Nature, in such a case, put into that lad’s breast but one secret prompting which urged him towards his father and away from you, she would be a liar and an idiot.’", "\"이보게,\" 찰스 형이 대답했어. \"자네는 아주 흔한 실수를 저지르고 있네. 자연과는 눈곱만큼도 상관없고 자연이 책임질 일도 아닌 것을 자연 탓으로 돌리고 있단 말이야. 사람들은 자연을 무슨 추상적인 것인 양 입에 올리면서, 정작 그러는 동안 무엇이 자연스러운지를 놓쳐 버리지. 여기 한 가엾은 아이가 있네. 부모의 보살핌이라곤 한 번도 받아 본 적 없고, 평생 고생과 슬픔 말고는 거의 아무것도 몰랐던 아이지. 그런 아이 앞에 아버지라는 사람이 나타나서, 첫마디로 그 짧은 행복을 끝장내겠다고, 옛날의 그 비참한 운명으로 다시 처넣겠다고, 그리고 평생 단 하나뿐인 친구 — 그게 바로 자네지 — 그 친구한테서 떼어 놓겠다고 으름장을 놓는단 말일세. 이런 마당에 자연이 그 아이 가슴속에 아버지에게로 끌리고 자네한테서 멀어지라고 부추기는 은밀한 속삭임을 단 하나라도 심어 놓는다면, 그건 자연이 거짓말쟁이에 바보라는 얘기지.\""]], [["Nicholas was delighted to find that the old gentleman spoke so warmly, and in the hope that he might say something more to the same purpose, made no reply.", "니콜라스는 노신사가 이렇게 따뜻하게 말해 주는 게 너무 기뻤어. 그래서 같은 뜻으로 무슨 말을 더 해 주지 않을까 싶어 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았지."]], [["‘The same mistake presents itself to me, in one shape or other, at every turn,’ said brother Charles. ‘Parents who never showed their love, complain of want of natural affection in their children; children who never showed their duty, complain of want of natural feeling in their parents; law-makers who find both so miserable that their affections have never had enough of life’s sun to develop them, are loud in their moralisings over parents and children too, and cry that the very ties of nature are disregarded. Natural affections and instincts, my dear sir, are the most beautiful of the Almighty’s works, but like other beautiful works of His, they must be reared and fostered, or it is as natural that they should be wholly obscured, and that new feelings should usurp their place, as it is that the sweetest productions of the earth, left untended, should be choked with weeds and briers. I wish we could be brought to consider this, and remembering natural obligations a little more at the right time, talk about them a little less at the wrong one.’", "\"바로 그 똑같은 실수가 이런저런 모습으로 어디서나 내 눈에 띄거든,\" 찰스 형이 말했어. \"사랑을 보여 준 적 없는 부모가 자식들한테 타고난 정이 없다고 불평하지. 도리를 다한 적 없는 자식들이 부모한테 타고난 정이 없다고 불평하고. 또 그 둘이 다 너무 가엾은 처지라 그 정이라는 게 햇볕을 충분히 못 받아 자라지도 못했는데, 법을 만드는 사람들은 그 부모와 자식을 두고 떠들썩하게 훈계를 늘어놓으며 천륜이 무시당했다고 부르짖지. 이보게, 타고난 정과 본능이란 전능하신 분의 작품 가운데 가장 아름다운 것일세. 하지만 그분의 다른 아름다운 작품들과 마찬가지로, 잘 길러 주고 보살펴 줘야 하는 거야. 안 그러면 그게 아예 가려져 버리고 새로운 감정이 그 자리를 차지하는 게 오히려 자연스러운 일이지. 땅에서 나는 가장 달콤한 것들도 돌보지 않고 내버려 두면 잡초와 가시덤불에 뒤덮이고 마는 것처럼 말이야. 우리가 이 점을 좀 생각하게 됐으면 좋겠어. 그래서 마땅한 때에 천륜의 도리를 조금 더 기억하고, 엉뚱한 때에 그걸 조금 덜 떠들었으면 싶네.\""]], [["After this, brother Charles, who had talked himself into a great heat, stopped to cool a little, and then continued:", "이렇게 말하다 보니 잔뜩 열이 오른 찰스 형은 잠시 멈춰 좀 식혔어. 그러고는 다시 말을 이었지."]], [["‘I dare say you are surprised, my dear sir, that I have listened to your recital with so little astonishment.", "\"이보게, 자네 얘기를 듣고도 내가 별로 놀라지 않으니 자네가 의아할 거야."], ["That is easily explained.", "그건 쉽게 풀리는 일일세."], ["Your uncle has been here this morning.’", "자네 삼촌이 오늘 아침에 여기 왔었거든.\""]], [["Nicholas coloured, and drew back a step or two.", "니콜라스는 얼굴이 붉어지며 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러섰어."]], [["‘Yes,’ said the old gentleman, tapping his desk emphatically, ‘here, in this room.", "\"그래,\" 노신사가 책상을 콕콕 두드리며 말했어."], ["He would listen neither to reason, feeling, nor justice.", "\"여기, 이 방에 말이야. 그 사람은 이치도, 정도, 정의도 도무지 들으려 하지 않더군."], ["But brother Ned was hard upon him; brother Ned, sir, might have melted a paving-stone.’", "하지만 네드 형이 호되게 몰아붙였지. 이보게, 네드 형은 포장 돌멩이도 녹일 만한 사람이야.\""]], [["‘He came to--’ said Nicholas.", "\"그분이 온 게—\" 니콜라스가 말을 꺼냈어."]], [["‘To complain of you,’ returned brother Charles, ‘to poison our ears with calumnies and falsehoods; but he came on a fruitless errand, and went away with some wholesome truths in his ear besides.", "\"자네를 헐뜯으러 왔지,\" 찰스 형이 대답했어."], ["Brother Ned, my dear Mr.", "\"비방과 거짓말로 우리 귀를 더럽히려고 말이야."], ["Nickleby--brother Ned, sir, is a perfect lion.", "하지만 헛걸음이었고, 오히려 따끔한 진실 몇 마디를 귀에 담아 가지고 갔지. 이보게 니클비 군, 네드 형은 — 우리 네드 형은 완벽한 사자 같은 사람이야."], ["So is Tim Linkinwater; Tim is quite a lion.", "팀 링컨워터도 마찬가지지. 팀은 아주 사자 같은 친구야."], ["We had Tim in to face him at first, and Tim was at him, sir, before you could say “Jack Robinson.”’", "처음엔 팀을 들여보내 그 사람을 상대하게 했는데, '잭 로빈슨' 하고 말할 새도 없이 팀이 그 사람한테 달려들었다네.\""]], [["‘How can I ever thank you for all the deep obligations you impose upon me every day?’ said Nicholas.", "\"매일같이 제게 이렇게 깊은 은혜를 베푸시니, 제가 무엇으로 다 감사를 드려야 할지요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘By keeping silence upon the subject, my dear sir,’ returned brother Charles.", "\"이 일에 대해 입을 다물어 주면 되네,\" 찰스 형이 대답했어."], ["‘You shall be righted.", "\"자네는 바로잡힐 걸세."], ["At least you shall not be wronged.", "적어도 부당한 일을 당하진 않을 거야."], ["Nobody belonging to you shall be wronged.", "자네와 관계된 누구도 부당한 일을 당하지 않을 걸세."], ["They shall not hurt a hair of your head, or the boy’s head, or your mother’s head, or your sister’s head.", "그들은 자네 머리카락 하나, 그 아이 머리카락 하나, 자네 어머니나 누이의 머리카락 하나도 건드리지 못해."], ["I have said it, brother Ned has said it, Tim Linkinwater has said it.", "내가 그렇게 말했고, 네드 형도 그렇게 말했고, 팀 링컨워터도 그렇게 말했어."], ["We have all said it, and we’ll all do it.", "우리 모두가 그렇게 말했으니, 다 같이 그렇게 할 걸세."], ["I have seen the father--if he is the father--and I suppose he must be.", "그 아버지라는 사람을 만나 봤어 — 정말 아버지가 맞다면 말이지. 뭐 맞긴 맞겠지."], ["He is a barbarian and a hypocrite, Mr.", "그 사람은 야만인에다 위선자야, 니클비 군."], ["Nickleby.", "내가 그랬지."], ["I told him, “You are a barbarian, sir.”", "'당신은 야만인이오.'"], ["I did.", "그렇게 말했네."], ["I said, “You’re a barbarian, sir.”", "'당신은 야만인이오' 하고 말이야."], ["And I’m glad of it, I am VERY glad I told him he was a barbarian, very glad indeed!’", "그리고 그렇게 말해 줘서 아주 기뻐. 그 사람더러 야만인이라고 말해 준 게 정말이지 아주 기쁘다네!\""]], [["By this time brother Charles was in such a very warm state of indignation, that Nicholas thought he might venture to put in a word, but the moment he essayed to do so, Mr.", "이쯤 되자 찰스 형은 어찌나 화가 잔뜩 달아올랐던지, 니콜라스는 이제 한마디 끼어들어도 되겠다 싶었어."], ["Cheeryble laid his hand softly upon his arm, and pointed to a chair.", "그런데 막상 그러려는 순간, 치어리블 씨가 부드럽게 그의 팔에 손을 얹더니 의자를 가리켰어."]], [["‘The subject is at an end for the present,’ said the old gentleman, wiping his face.", "\"이 얘기는 당분간 여기서 끝내세,\" 노신사가 얼굴을 닦으며 말했어."], ["‘Don’t revive it by a single word.", "\"한마디도 다시 꺼내지 말게."], ["I am going to speak upon another subject, a confidential subject, Mr.", "다른 얘기를 하려고 하네, 은밀한 얘기야, 니클비 군."], ["Nickleby. We must be cool again, we must be cool.’", "우리 다시 차분해져야 해, 차분해져야 한다고.\""]], [["After two or three turns across the room he resumed his seat, and drawing his chair nearer to that on which Nicholas was seated, said:", "방을 두세 번 가로질러 거닐던 그는 다시 자리에 앉았어. 그러고는 의자를 니콜라스가 앉은 의자 쪽으로 바짝 당기며 말했지."]], [["‘I am about to employ you, my dear sir, on a confidential and delicate mission.’", "\"이보게, 자네한테 은밀하고 미묘한 임무를 하나 맡기려 하네.\""]], [["‘You might employ many a more able messenger, sir,’ said Nicholas, ‘but a more trustworthy or zealous one, I may be bold to say, you could not find.’", "\"저보다 훨씬 유능한 심부름꾼이야 얼마든지 쓰실 수 있겠지요,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"하지만 저보다 더 믿음직하고 열성적인 사람은 못 찾으실 거라고, 감히 말씀드릴 수 있습니다.\""]], [["‘Of that I am well assured,’ returned brother Charles, ‘well assured.", "\"그건 나도 잘 알고 있네,\" 찰스 형이 대답했어."], ["You will give me credit for thinking so, when I tell you that the object of this mission is a young lady.’", "\"아주 잘 알지. 이 임무의 대상이 한 젊은 아가씨라고 말하면, 내가 그렇게 생각한다는 걸 자네도 인정해 줄 걸세.\""]], [["‘A young lady, sir!’ cried Nicholas, quite trembling for the moment with his eagerness to hear more.", "\"젊은 아가씨라고요?\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어. 더 듣고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 간절했던지, 그 순간 온몸이 떨릴 지경이었지."]], [["‘A very beautiful young lady,’ said Mr.", "\"아주 아름다운 아가씨라네,\""], ["Cheeryble, gravely.", "치어리블 씨가 진지하게 말했어."]], [["‘Pray go on, sir,’ returned Nicholas.", "\"어서 말씀해 주세요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘I am thinking how to do so,’ said brother Charles; sadly, as it seemed to his young friend, and with an expression allied to pain.", "\"어떻게 말을 꺼내야 하나 생각 중일세,\" 찰스 형이 말했어."], ["‘You accidentally saw a young lady in this room one morning, my dear sir, in a fainting fit.", "그 젊은 친구가 보기에는 어딘가 슬프고, 고통에 가까운 표정이었지. \"이보게, 어느 날 아침 자네가 이 방에서 한 젊은 아가씨가 정신을 잃고 쓰러진 걸 우연히 봤지."], ["Do you remember?", "기억하나?"], ["Perhaps you have forgotten.’", "어쩌면 잊었을지도 모르겠군.\""]], [["‘Oh no,’ replied Nicholas, hurriedly.", "\"아뇨, 천만에요,\" 니콜라스가 다급히 대답했어."], ["‘I--I--remember it very well indeed.’", "\"저는—저는—아주 또렷이 기억하고 있습니다.\""]], [["‘SHE is the lady I speak of,’ said brother Charles.", "\"그 아가씨가 바로 내가 말하는 사람이야,\" 찰스 형이 말했어."], ["Like the famous parrot, Nicholas thought a great deal, but was unable to utter a word.", "그 유명한 앵무새처럼, 니콜라스는 머릿속으로는 온갖 생각을 다 했지만 한마디도 입 밖에 낼 수가 없었지."]], [["‘She is the daughter,’ said Mr.", "\"그 아가씨는 한 부인의 딸이라네,\""], ["Cheeryble, ‘of a lady who, when she was a beautiful girl herself, and I was very many years younger, I--it seems a strange word for me to utter now--I loved very dearly.", "치어리블 씨가 말했어. \"그 부인이 아름다운 처녀였고 내가 지금보다 한참 젊었을 때, 나는—이제 와서 이런 말을 입에 올리려니 참 이상하지만—그분을 무척 사랑했었네."], ["You will smile, perhaps, to hear a grey-headed man talk about such things.", "백발이 다 된 남자가 이런 얘길 하니 자네는 웃음이 날지도 모르지."], ["You will not offend me, for when I was as young as you, I dare say I should have done the same.’", "하지만 그래도 나는 화나지 않아. 내가 자네만큼 젊었을 때라면 나도 똑같이 그랬을 테니까.\""]], [["‘I have no such inclination, indeed,’ said Nicholas.", "\"전 정말 그럴 마음 없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘My dear brother Ned,’ continued Mr.", "\"내 사랑하는 네드 형은,\" 치어리블 씨가 말을 이었어."], ["Cheeryble, ‘was to have married her sister, but she died.", "\"그 부인의 언니와 결혼할 사이였는데, 그 언니가 죽고 말았지."], ["She is dead too now, and has been for many years.", "이제 그 부인도 죽었어, 죽은 지 여러 해 됐네."], ["She married her choice; and I wish I could add that her after-life was as happy as God knows I ever prayed it might be!’", "그분은 제가 고른 사람과 결혼했지. 그 뒤의 삶이, 하느님은 아시지만 내가 늘 빌었던 만큼 행복했다고 덧붙일 수 있으면 얼마나 좋겠나!\""]], [["A short silence intervened, which Nicholas made no effort to break.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀지만, 니콜라스는 그걸 깨려 들지 않았어."]], [["‘If trial and calamity had fallen as lightly on his head, as in the deepest truth of my own heart I ever hoped (for her sake) it would, his life would have been one of peace and happiness,’ said the old gentleman calmly. ‘It will be enough to say that this was not the case; that she was not happy; that they fell into complicated distresses and difficulties; that she came, twelve months before her death, to appeal to my old friendship; sadly changed, sadly altered, broken-spirited from suffering and ill-usage, and almost broken-hearted. He readily availed himself of the money which, to give her but one hour’s peace of mind, I would have poured out as freely as water--nay, he often sent her back for more--and yet even while he squandered it, he made the very success of these, her applications to me, the groundwork of cruel taunts and jeers, protesting that he knew she thought with bitter remorse of the choice she had made, that she had married him from motives of interest and vanity (he was a gay young man with great friends about him when she chose him for her husband), and venting in short upon her, by every unjust and unkind means, the bitterness of that ruin and disappointment which had been brought about by his profligacy alone. In those times this young lady was a mere child. I never saw her again until that morning when you saw her also, but my nephew, Frank--’", "\"내 마음 가장 깊은 곳에서 (그분을 위해) 늘 바랐던 대로, 시련과 재앙이 그 사람 머리 위에 가볍게만 내렸더라면, 그 부인의 삶은 평화롭고 행복했을 걸세,\" 노신사가 차분하게 말했어. \"하지만 그렇지 못했다는 것만 말해 두면 충분하겠지. 그분은 행복하지 않았어. 둘은 얽히고설킨 고난과 어려움에 빠졌지. 그리고 그분은 죽기 열두 달 전에, 오랜 우정에 기대 보려고 나를 찾아왔네. 슬프게 변하고, 슬프게 달라져서, 고생과 학대에 기가 꺾이고, 마음이 거의 무너진 채로 말이야. 그 사람은 냉큼 돈을 받아 챙겼지. 그분 마음에 단 한 시간의 평화라도 주기 위해서라면 나는 물 쓰듯 아낌없이 내줬을 그 돈을 말이야 — 아니, 그 사람은 더 받아 오라고 그분을 자꾸 돌려보냈어 — 그래 놓고 그 돈을 흥청망청 쓰는 와중에도, 그분이 나한테 매달려 받아 낸 그 도움을 빌미로 모질게 비아냥대고 빈정거렸지. 그분이 자기 선택을 두고 쓰라린 후회에 잠겨 있는 걸 안다느니, 잇속과 허영 때문에 자기랑 결혼한 거라느니 하고 우겨 댔어 (그분이 남편으로 그를 골랐을 무렵 그는 주위에 대단한 친구들을 거느린 명랑한 청년이었거든). 한마디로 그 사람은 순전히 제 방탕 탓에 빚어진 그 파멸과 좌절의 쓰라림을 온갖 부당하고 모진 수단으로 그분에게 죄다 퍼부어 댔던 거야. 그 시절 이 젊은 아가씨는 그저 어린아이였네. 그 뒤로 나는 그 아가씨를 다시는 못 봤어, 자네도 본 그날 아침까지는 말이야. 그런데 내 조카 프랭크가—\""]], [["Nicholas started, and indistinctly apologising for the interruption, begged his patron to proceed.", "니콜라스는 흠칫 놀랐어. 그러고는 끼어든 것을 우물우물 사과하며, 어른께 어서 말씀을 이어 가시라고 청했지."]], [["‘--My nephew, Frank, I say,’ resumed Mr. Cheeryble, ‘encountered her by accident, and lost sight of her almost in a minute afterwards, within two days after he returned to England. Her father lay in some secret place to avoid his creditors, reduced, between sickness and poverty, to the verge of death, and she, a child,--we might almost think, if we did not know the wisdom of all Heaven’s decrees--who should have blessed a better man, was steadily braving privation, degradation, and everything most terrible to such a young and delicate creature’s heart, for the purpose of supporting him. She was attended, sir,’ said brother Charles, ‘in these reverses, by one faithful creature, who had been, in old times, a poor kitchen wench in the family, who was then their solitary servant, but who might have been, for the truth and fidelity of her heart--who might have been--ah! the wife of Tim Linkinwater himself, sir!’", "\"—내 조카 프랭크 말일세,\" 치어리블 씨가 다시 말을 이었어. \"그 애가 영국에 돌아온 지 이틀도 안 돼서 우연히 그 아가씨와 마주쳤는데, 거의 일 분도 안 돼서 그만 놓쳐 버렸다네. 그 아가씨 아버지는 빚쟁이들을 피해 어딘가 은밀한 곳에 숨어 지냈지. 병과 가난 사이에서 죽음의 문턱까지 내몰린 채로 말이야. 그리고 그 아가씨는, 어린아이였는데 — 하늘의 모든 뜻이 얼마나 지혜로운지를 우리가 모른다면, 더 나은 사람한테 갔어야 할 아이라고 여길 뻔했지 — 그 아버지를 부양하려고 궁핍과 천대를, 그토록 어리고 여린 마음에 가장 끔찍할 온갖 것을 꿋꿋이 견뎌 냈던 거야. 이보게,\" 찰스 형이 말했어. \"그 아가씨는 이 역경 속에서 충직한 사람 하나의 시중을 받았네. 옛날에 그 집안에서 부엌데기로 일하던 가난한 처녀였는데, 그때는 단 하나 남은 하인이었지. 하지만 그 마음의 참됨과 충실함으로 치자면 — 그 처녀는 — 아! 팀 링컨워터의 아내가 되고도 남을 사람이었어, 그렇고말고!\""]], [["Pursuing this encomium upon the poor follower with such energy and relish as no words can describe, brother Charles leant back in his chair, and delivered the remainder of his relation with greater composure.", "말로는 다 담을 수 없는 열정과 흐뭇함으로 그 가난한 하녀에 대한 찬사를 늘어놓던 찰스 형은, 의자에 등을 기대고는 남은 이야기를 한결 차분하게 들려주었어."]], [["It was in substance this: That proudly resisting all offers of permanent aid and support from her late mother’s friends, because they were made conditional upon her quitting the wretched man, her father, who had no friends left, and shrinking with instinctive delicacy from appealing in their behalf to that true and noble heart which he hated, and had, through its greatest and purest goodness, deeply wronged by misconstruction and ill report, this young girl had struggled alone and unassisted to maintain him by the labour of her hands. That through the utmost depths of poverty and affliction she had toiled, never turning aside for an instant from her task, never wearied by the petulant gloom of a sick man sustained by no consoling recollections of the past or hopes of the future; never repining for the comforts she had rejected, or bewailing the hard lot she had voluntarily incurred. That every little accomplishment she had acquired in happier days had been put into requisition for this purpose, and directed to this one end. That for two long years, toiling by day and often too by night, working at the needle, the pencil, and the pen, and submitting, as a daily governess, to such caprices and indignities as women (with daughters too) too often love to inflict upon their own sex when they serve in such capacities, as though in jealousy of the superior intelligence which they are necessitated to employ,--indignities, in ninety-nine cases out of every hundred, heaped upon persons immeasurably and incalculably their betters, but outweighing in comparison any that the most heartless blackleg would put upon his groom--that for two long years, by dint of labouring in all these capacities and wearying in none, she had not succeeded in the sole aim and object of her life, but that, overwhelmed by accumulated difficulties and disappointments, she had been compelled to seek out her mother’s old friend, and, with a bursting heart, to confide in him at last.", "요지인즉 이런 거였어. 그 아가씨는 돌아가신 어머니 친구들이 내미는 영구적인 도움과 부양 제안을 다 자존심을 지키며 마다했지. 그 제안들이 다 친구라곤 하나도 안 남은 비참한 아버지를 버리는 걸 조건으로 달았기 때문이야. 게다가 그 아가씨는, 아버지가 미워하던 그 참되고 고결한 마음 — 가장 크고 순수한 선함 때문에 도리어 오해와 험담에 휘말려 깊은 누명을 쓴 그 마음 — 에다 대고 아버지 대신 도움을 구하는 일을, 타고난 섬세함으로 본능처럼 꺼렸어. 그래서 이 어린 처녀는 홀로, 아무 도움 없이, 제 두 손으로 일해서 아버지를 먹여 살리려 발버둥을 쳤던 거야. 가난과 고난의 가장 깊은 바닥을 헤치며 고생하면서도, 그 일에서 한순간도 곁눈질하지 않았고, 지난날의 위안 어린 추억이나 앞날의 희망 한 자락 없이 짜증과 우울에 빠진 병자에게 시달리면서도 지치지 않았으며, 자기가 마다한 안락을 두고 투덜대거나 제 발로 떠안은 모진 팔자를 한탄하지도 않았지. 더 행복하던 시절에 익힌 자그마한 재주는 하나하나 다 이 목적에 동원되었고, 오직 이 한 가지 목표로 향했어. 꼬박 두 해 동안, 낮에는 물론 밤에도 자주 고생하며 바늘과 연필과 펜을 들고 일했지. 또 입주하지 않는 가정교사로 일하면서, 여자들이 — 게다가 딸까지 둔 여자들이 — 같은 여자가 그런 처지로 봉사할 때 곧잘 가하기 좋아하는 온갖 변덕과 모욕을 참아 냈어. 마치 어쩔 수 없이 부려야 하는 그 뛰어난 지성을 시샘하기라도 하듯이 말이야 — 그런 모욕은 백 번 가운데 아흔아홉 번은 자기들보다 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 잘난 사람들 머리 위에 퍼부어지는 것이고, 아무리 매정한 사기 도박꾼이라도 제 마부한테 차마 그러지 못할 만큼 지독한 것이지. 꼬박 두 해 동안 이 온갖 일을 죄다 해내며 어느 하나에도 지치지 않았건만, 그 아가씨는 제 삶의 단 하나뿐인 목표를 이루지 못했어. 그러다 쌓이고 쌓인 어려움과 좌절에 짓눌린 끝에, 어머니의 옛 친구를 찾아내, 터질 듯한 가슴을 안고 마침내 그에게 속을 털어놓을 수밖에 없었던 거지."]], [["‘If I had been poor,’ said brother Charles, with sparkling eyes; ‘if I had been poor, Mr.", "\"내가 가난했더라면,\" 찰스 형이 눈을 반짝이며 말했어."], ["Nickleby, my dear sir, which thank God I am not, I would have denied myself (of course anybody would under such circumstances) the commonest necessaries of life, to help her.", "\"이보게 니클비 군, 내가 가난했더라면 — 다행히 그렇지 않다만 — 그 아가씨를 돕기 위해 가장 흔한 생필품마저도 안 쓰고 견뎠을 걸세 (물론 그런 처지면 누구나 그러겠지). 그런데 사정이 사정이라, 이게 어려운 일이란 말이야."], ["As it is, the task is a difficult one.", "그 아버지가 죽었다면 더없이 쉬울 텐데."], ["If her father were dead, nothing could be easier, for then she should share and cheer the happiest home that brother Ned and I could have, as if she were our child or sister.", "그러면 그 아가씨가 네드 형과 내가 마련해 줄 가장 행복한 보금자리를 함께 누리며 그 집을 환하게 밝힐 텐데, 마치 우리 딸이나 누이라도 되는 것처럼 말이야. 하지만 그 아버지가 아직 살아 있지."], ["But he is still alive.", "아무도 그 사람은 도울 수가 없어."], ["Nobody can help him; that has been tried a thousand times; he was not abandoned by all without good cause, I know.’", "수천 번도 더 시도해 봤지만 말이야. 다들 까닭 없이 그 사람을 저버린 게 아니라는 걸 나도 알아.\""]], [["‘Cannot she be persuaded to--’ Nicholas hesitated when he had got thus far.", "\"그 아가씨를 설득해서—\" 니콜라스는 여기까지 말하다 멈칫했어."]], [["‘To leave him?’ said brother Charles.", "\"아버지를 떠나게 한단 말인가?\" 찰스 형이 말했어."], ["‘Who could entreat a child to desert her parent?", "\"자식더러 부모를 버리라고 누가 권할 수 있겠나?"], ["Such entreaties, limited to her seeing him occasionally, have been urged upon her--not by me--but always with the same result.’", "가끔씩만 아버지를 보면 어떻겠느냐는, 그 정도로 한정한 권유는 그 아가씨한테 해 봤지 — 내가 한 건 아니고 — 하지만 늘 결과는 똑같았어.\""]], [["‘Is he kind to her?’ said Nicholas.", "\"그 사람은 아가씨한테 다정합니까?\""], ["‘Does he requite her affection?’", "니콜라스가 말했어. \"딸의 정을 보답해 줍니까?\""]], [["‘True kindness, considerate self-denying kindness, is not in his nature,’ returned Mr.", "\"진정한 다정함, 사려 깊고 자기를 희생하는 다정함이란 그 사람 천성에 없어,\" 치어리블 씨가 대답했어."], ["Cheeryble. ‘Such kindness as he knows, he regards her with, I believe.", "\"그래도 자기가 아는 만큼의 다정함으로는 딸을 대하는 것 같긴 해."], ["The mother was a gentle, loving, confiding creature, and although he wounded her from their marriage till her death as cruelly and wantonly as ever man did, she never ceased to love him.", "그 어머니는 온화하고 정 많고 사람을 잘 믿는 사람이었어. 그 사람이 결혼할 때부터 죽을 때까지 어떤 남자 못지않게 모질고 함부로 그분에게 상처를 줬건만, 그분은 그 사람 사랑하기를 끝내 멈추지 않았지."], ["She commended him on her death-bed to her child’s care.", "그분은 임종의 자리에서 딸에게 그 사람을 보살펴 달라고 당부했어."], ["Her child has never forgotten it, and never will.’", "그 딸은 그 말을 한 번도 잊은 적 없고, 앞으로도 잊지 않을 거야.\""]], [["‘Have you no influence over him?’ asked Nicholas.", "\"그 사람한테 영향력을 좀 미칠 순 없습니까?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘I, my dear sir!", "\"내가 말인가, 이보게!"], ["The last man in the world.", "세상에서 가장 안 될 사람이지."], ["Such are his jealousy and hatred of me, that if he knew his daughter had opened her heart to me, he would render her life miserable with his reproaches; although--this is the inconsistency and selfishness of his character--although if he knew that every penny she had came from me, he would not relinquish one personal desire that the most reckless expenditure of her scanty stock could gratify.’", "그 사람은 나를 어찌나 시샘하고 미워하는지, 만약 제 딸이 나한테 속을 털어놨다는 걸 알면 갖은 비난으로 그 아가씨 삶을 비참하게 만들 걸세. 하기야 — 이게 그 사람 성격의 모순이자 이기심인데 — 그 아가씨가 가진 돈이 죄다 나한테서 나온 줄 알면서도, 그 얼마 안 되는 밑천을 제멋대로 펑펑 써서 채울 수 있는 자기 욕심이라면 단 하나도 마다하지 않을 위인이야.\""]], [["‘An unnatural scoundrel!’ said Nicholas, indignantly.", "\"천륜도 모르는 악당이군요!\" 니콜라스가 분개하며 말했어."]], [["‘We will use no harsh terms,’ said brother Charles, in a gentle voice; ‘but accommodate ourselves to the circumstances in which this young lady is placed. Such assistance as I have prevailed upon her to accept, I have been obliged, at her own earnest request, to dole out in the smallest portions, lest he, finding how easily money was procured, should squander it even more lightly than he is accustomed to do. She has come to and fro, to and fro, secretly and by night, to take even this; and I cannot bear that things should go on in this way, Mr Nickleby, I really cannot bear it.’", "\"모진 말은 쓰지 마세,\" 찰스 형이 부드러운 목소리로 말했어. \"그저 이 아가씨가 놓인 처지에 우리를 맞추자고. 내가 겨우 설득해서 그 아가씨가 받게 한 도움도, 그 아가씨가 간곡히 청하는 바람에 아주 잔돈푼으로 조금씩 나눠 줄 수밖에 없었어. 그 아버지가 돈을 너무 쉽게 구할 수 있다는 걸 알면 평소보다 더 헤프게 흥청망청 써 댈까 봐서지. 그 아가씨는 이것마저도 받으려고 남몰래, 밤을 틈타 오가고 또 오갔어. 그런데 이보게 니클비 군, 나는 일이 이런 식으로 흘러가는 걸 도무지 못 견디겠어, 정말 못 견디겠단 말일세.\""]], [["Then it came out by little and little, how that the twins had been revolving in their good old heads manifold plans and schemes for helping this young lady in the most delicate and considerate way, and so that her father should not suspect the source whence the aid was derived; and how they had at last come to the conclusion, that the best course would be to make a feint of purchasing her little drawings and ornamental work at a high price, and keeping up a constant demand for the same. For the furtherance of which end and object it was necessary that somebody should represent the dealer in such commodities, and after great deliberation they had pitched upon Nicholas to support this character.", "그러고 나서 조금씩 조금씩 드러나는 거였어. 이 쌍둥이 형제가 그 착하고 늙은 머리를 맞대고, 이 젊은 아가씨를 가장 섬세하고 사려 깊은 방식으로 도울 갖가지 계획과 궁리를 짜내 왔다는 게 말이야. 그것도 아버지가 그 도움의 출처를 의심하지 못하게끔 말이지. 그래서 마침내 이런 결론에 이르렀던 거야. 가장 좋은 방법은, 그 아가씨가 그린 자그마한 그림이며 장식 세공품을 비싼 값에 사들이는 척하면서 그걸 끊임없이 주문해 주는 거라고. 그런데 이 목적과 의도를 이루려면 누군가가 그런 물건을 거래하는 상인 행세를 해야 했어. 그래서 한참 의논한 끝에, 이 역할을 맡을 사람으로 니콜라스를 꼽았던 거지."]], [["‘He knows me,’ said brother Charles, ‘and he knows my brother Ned. Neither of us would do. Frank is a very good fellow--a very fine fellow--but we are afraid that he might be a little flighty and thoughtless in such a delicate matter, and that he might, perhaps--that he might, in short, be too susceptible (for she is a beautiful creature, sir; just what her poor mother was), and falling in love with her before he knew well his own mind, carry pain and sorrow into that innocent breast, which we would be the humble instruments of gradually making happy. He took an extraordinary interest in her fortunes when he first happened to encounter her; and we gather from the inquiries we have made of him, that it was she in whose behalf he made that turmoil which led to your first acquaintance.’", "\"그 사람은 나를 알아,\" 찰스 형이 말했어. \"그리고 네드 형도 알지. 그러니 우리 둘 다 안 되네. 프랭크는 아주 좋은 녀석이야 — 아주 훌륭한 녀석이지 — 하지만 이런 미묘한 일에는 좀 들뜨고 경솔하지 않을까 걱정이야. 어쩌면 그 애가 — 한마디로, 너무 마음이 쉽게 흔들려서 (그 아가씨는 아름다운 사람이거든. 그 가엾은 어머니가 꼭 그랬듯이 말이야) 제 마음을 미처 추스르기도 전에 그 아가씨한테 반해 버려서는, 우리가 보잘것없는 손길로나마 차차 행복하게 해 주려는 그 순결한 가슴에 아픔과 슬픔을 가져다줄까 봐 그래. 그 애는 처음 우연히 그 아가씨와 마주쳤을 때 그분 처지에 유난히 마음을 쓰더군. 그 애한테 이것저것 물어본 걸로 미루어 보면, 그 애가 소동을 벌여서 자네랑 처음 알게 된 그 일도 바로 그 아가씨 때문이었던 것 같아.\""]], [["Nicholas stammered out that he had before suspected the possibility of such a thing; and in explanation of its having occurred to him, described when and where he had seen the young lady himself.", "니콜라스는 자기도 전부터 그런 일이 있었을지 모른다고 짐작했었다고 더듬더듬 말했어. 그리고 그런 생각이 어쩌다 들었는지 설명하느라, 자기가 그 아가씨를 직접 본 게 언제 어디서였는지 이야기했지."]], [["‘Well; then you see,’ continued brother Charles, ‘that HE wouldn’t do. Tim Linkinwater is out of the question; for Tim, sir, is such a tremendous fellow, that he could never contain himself, but would go to loggerheads with the father before he had been in the place five minutes. You don’t know what Tim is, sir, when he is aroused by anything that appeals to his feelings very strongly; then he is terrific, sir, is Tim Linkinwater, absolutely terrific. Now, in you we can repose the strictest confidence; in you we have seen--or at least I have seen, and that’s the same thing, for there’s no difference between me and my brother Ned, except that he is the finest creature that ever lived, and that there is not, and never will be, anybody like him in all the world--in you we have seen domestic virtues and affections, and delicacy of feeling, which exactly qualify you for such an office. And you are the man, sir.’", "\"자, 그래서 보다시피,\" 찰스 형이 말을 이었어. \"그 애는 안 되는 거야. 팀 링컨워터는 아예 말도 안 되고. 이보게, 팀은 워낙 불같은 친구라 도무지 참지를 못해서, 그 자리에 들어선 지 오 분도 안 돼서 아버지랑 한판 붙고 말 걸세. 자네는 모를 거야, 무언가가 팀의 감정을 세게 건드리면 그가 어떤 사람이 되는지를 말이야. 그땐 무시무시하다네, 팀 링컨워터는 정말 무시무시해. 그런데 자네라면 우리가 가장 굳게 믿을 수 있거든. 자네한테서는 — 적어도 내가 봤지, 뭐 그게 그거지만. 나랑 네드 형 사이엔 아무 차이도 없으니까. 다만 네드 형이 세상에 둘도 없이 훌륭한 사람이고, 온 세상에 그런 사람은 없고 앞으로도 없을 거란 것만 빼면 말이야 — 자네한테서는 가정적인 미덕과 정, 그리고 섬세한 감정을 봤어. 그게 바로 이런 일에 딱 맞는 자질이지. 그러니 자네가 적임자야.\""]], [["‘The young lady, sir,’ said Nicholas, who felt so embarrassed that he had no small difficulty in saying anything at all--‘Does--is--is she a party to this innocent deceit?’", "\"그 아가씨는 말입니다,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. 너무 멋쩍어서 아무 말이라도 꺼내기가 여간 힘들지 않았지. \"그분은—그분도—이 죄 없는 속임수에 한편입니까?\""]], [["‘Yes, yes,’ returned Mr.", "\"그래, 그래,\" 치어리블 씨가 대답했어."], ["Cheeryble; ‘at least she knows you come from us; she does NOT know, however, but that we shall dispose of these little productions that you’ll purchase from time to time; and, perhaps, if you did it very well (that is, VERY well indeed), perhaps she might be brought to believe that we--that we made a profit of them.", "\"적어도 자네가 우리 쪽에서 왔다는 건 알지. 하지만 자네가 이따금씩 사들이는 그 자그마한 작품들을 우리가 팔아 줄 거라는 건 몰라."], ["Eh?", "그리고 어쩌면, 자네가 그걸 아주 잘 해낸다면 (그러니까, 아주아주 잘 해낸다면 말일세) 어쩌면 그 아가씨가 우리가—우리가 그걸로 이문을 남긴다고 믿게 될지도 몰라."], ["Eh?’", "응? 응?\""]], [["In this guileless and most kind simplicity, brother Charles was so happy, and in this possibility of the young lady being led to think that she was under no obligation to him, he evidently felt so sanguine and had so much delight, that Nicholas would not breathe a doubt upon the subject.", "이렇게 천진하고 더없이 다정한 순박함 속에서 찰스 형은 너무도 행복해했어. 또 그 아가씨가 자기한테 신세 진 게 없다고 여기게 될지도 모른다는 그 가능성에, 그는 분명 한껏 들떠서 그렇게나 기뻐했지. 그래서 니콜라스는 그 일에 의심 한 자락도 내비치지 않았어."]], [["All this time, however, there hovered upon the tip of his tongue a confession that the very same objections which Mr.", "그런데 그러는 내내, 그의 혀끝에는 고백 하나가 맴돌고 있었어."], ["Cheeryble had stated to the employment of his nephew in this commission applied with at least equal force and validity to himself, and a hundred times had he been upon the point of avowing the real state of his feelings, and entreating to be released from it.", "치어리블 씨가 조카를 이 일에 쓰면 안 된다며 든 바로 그 이유들이, 적어도 똑같은 무게와 타당성으로 자기 자신한테도 들어맞는다는 고백 말이야. 그래서 그는 백 번도 더 자기 진짜 마음을 털어놓고 이 일에서 빼 달라고 청할 뻔했지."], ["But as often, treading upon the heels of this impulse, came another which urged him to refrain, and to keep his secret to his own breast.", "하지만 그 충동의 뒤꿈치를 밟듯 번번이 또 다른 충동이 따라붙어, 그만두라고, 비밀을 가슴속에만 묻어 두라고 부추겼어. '내가 왜,'"], ["‘Why should I,’ thought Nicholas, ‘why should I throw difficulties in the way of this benevolent and high-minded design?", "니콜라스는 생각했어. '내가 왜 이 인자하고 고결한 계획에 훼방을 놓아야 하지?"], ["What if I do love and reverence this good and lovely creature.", "내가 이 착하고 사랑스러운 사람을 사랑하고 우러러본다 한들 어떻단 말인가."], ["Should I not appear a most arrogant and shallow coxcomb if I gravely represented that there was any danger of her falling in love with me?", "그 아가씨가 나한테 반할 위험이 있다는 둥 정색하고 떠벌리면, 나야말로 더없이 거만하고 속 빈 멋쟁이로 보이지 않겠나?"], ["Besides, have I no confidence in myself?", "게다가, 나 자신을 못 믿는단 말인가?"], ["Am I not now bound in honour to repress these thoughts?", "명예를 걸고 이런 생각을 억눌러야 하지 않나?"], ["Has not this excellent man a right to my best and heartiest services, and should any considerations of self deter me from rendering them?’", "이 훌륭한 분이 내 최선의, 가장 진심 어린 봉사를 받을 자격이 있는데, 나 자신을 위한 어떤 잿속이 그걸 막아서야 되겠나?'"]], [["Asking himself such questions as these, Nicholas mentally answered with great emphasis ‘No!’ and persuading himself that he was a most conscientious and glorious martyr, nobly resolved to do what, if he had examined his own heart a little more carefully, he would have found he could not resist.", "이런 물음들을 스스로에게 던지며, 니콜라스는 마음속으로 아주 힘주어 \"안 돼!\" 하고 답했어."], ["Such is the sleight of hand by which we juggle with ourselves, and change our very weaknesses into stanch and most magnanimous virtues!", "그러고는 자기가 더없이 양심적이고 영광스러운 순교자라고 자신을 구슬리면서, 사실 제 마음을 조금만 더 꼼꼼히 들여다봤다면 도저히 거역할 수 없는 일임을 알았을 그 일을 기꺼이 떠맡기로 마음을 굳혔지. 우리가 스스로를 속여 넘기며, 제 약점을 굳건하고 더없이 너그러운 미덕으로 둔갑시키는 솜씨란 바로 이런 거야!"]], [["Mr.", "물론 치어리블 씨는 그 젊은 친구의 머릿속에 이런 생각들이 떠오르고 있는 줄은 까맣게 몰랐어. 그래서 첫 방문에 필요한 소개장과 지시 사항을 일러 주었지."], ["Cheeryble, being of course wholly unsuspicious that such reflections were presenting themselves to his young friend, proceeded to give him the needful credentials and directions for his first visit, which was to be made next morning; and all preliminaries being arranged, and the strictest secrecy enjoined, Nicholas walked home for the night very thoughtfully indeed.", "그 방문은 이튿날 아침에 하기로 되어 있었어. 그렇게 사전 준비를 다 마치고 가장 철저한 비밀을 단단히 당부받고서, 니콜라스는 그날 밤 집으로 걸어가는 내내 정말이지 깊은 생각에 잠겼어."]], [["The place to which Mr. Cheeryble had directed him was a row of mean and not over-cleanly houses, situated within ‘the Rules’ of the King’s Bench Prison, and not many hundred paces distant from the obelisk in St George’s Fields. The Rules are a certain liberty adjoining the prison, and comprising some dozen streets in which debtors who can raise money to pay large fees, from which their creditors do NOT derive any benefit, are permitted to reside by the wise provisions of the same enlightened laws which leave the debtor who can raise no money to starve in jail, without the food, clothing, lodging, or warmth, which are provided for felons convicted of the most atrocious crimes that can disgrace humanity. There are many pleasant fictions of the law in constant operation, but there is not one so pleasant or practically humorous as that which supposes every man to be of equal value in its impartial eye, and the benefits of all laws to be equally attainable by all men, without the smallest reference to the furniture of their pockets.", "치어리블 씨가 그에게 일러 준 곳은, 킹스 벤치 감옥의 '룰스' 안에 있는 초라하고 그리 깨끗하지도 않은 집들이 늘어선 한 줄거리였어. 세인트 조지 들판에 선 오벨리스크에서 몇백 걸음 떨어지지 않은 곳이었지. 룰스란 감옥에 딸린 일종의 자유 구역으로, 열 몇 개쯤 되는 거리를 아우르는데, 큰 수수료를 낼 돈을 마련할 수 있는 채무자들이 거기에 살도록 허락된 곳이야. 그 수수료가 채권자들한테는 한 푼도 돌아가지 않는데도 말이지. 이게 다 저 똑똑한 법들의 어진 조항 덕분이라네. 그 똑같은 법이, 돈을 한 푼도 마련하지 못하는 채무자한테는, 인간을 더럽히는 가장 끔찍한 죄를 지은 흉악범에게도 베푸는 먹을 것, 입을 것, 잠자리, 온기조차 주지 않은 채 감옥에서 굶어 죽게 내버려 두지. 법에는 늘 작동하는 그럴듯한 허구가 많지만, 그 가운데 이만큼 그럴듯하고 우습기까지 한 것도 없어. 그 공평무사한 눈에는 사람이 죄다 똑같은 값어치를 지녔고, 주머니 사정 따위는 눈곱만큼도 따지지 않고 모든 법의 혜택을 누구나 똑같이 누릴 수 있다고 여기는, 그런 허구 말이야."]], [["To the row of houses indicated to him by Mr. Charles Cheeryble, Nicholas directed his steps, without much troubling his head with such matters as these; and at this row of houses--after traversing a very dirty and dusty suburb, of which minor theatricals, shell-fish, ginger-beer, spring vans, greengrocery, and brokers’ shops, appeared to compose the main and most prominent features--he at length arrived with a palpitating heart.", "니콜라스는 그런 일들로 머리를 그리 골치 썩이지 않고, 찰스 치어리블 씨가 일러 준 그 집들이 늘어선 줄거리로 발걸음을 옮겼어."], ["There were small gardens in front which, being wholly neglected in all other respects, served as little pens for the dust to collect in, until the wind came round the corner and blew it down the road.", "그러고는 무척 더럽고 먼지투성이인 변두리 — 싸구려 연극이며 조개며 진저비어며 짐수레며 채소 가게며 중고품 가게가 주된 풍경을 이루는 듯한 동네 — 를 가로지른 끝에, 마침내 두근거리는 가슴으로 그 집들 앞에 다다랐지. 집집마다 앞에는 자그마한 정원이 있었는데, 다른 건 죄다 손 놓고 내버려 둔 탓에 그저 먼지가 쌓이는 작은 우리 노릇이나 했어."], ["Opening the rickety gate which, dangling on its broken hinges before one of these, half admitted and half repulsed the visitor, Nicholas knocked at the street door with a faltering hand.", "그러다 모퉁이를 돌아온 바람이 그 먼지를 길로 불어 날리곤 했지. 그 가운데 한 집 앞에 부서진 경첩에 위태롭게 매달린 채, 찾아온 손님을 반쯤은 들이고 반쯤은 밀어내는 흔들거리는 문을 밀어 열고, 니콜라스는 떨리는 손으로 현관문을 두드렸어."]], [["It was in truth a shabby house outside, with very dim parlour windows and very small show of blinds, and very dirty muslin curtains dangling across the lower panes on very loose and limp strings. Neither, when the door was opened, did the inside appear to belie the outward promise, as there was faded carpeting on the stairs and faded oil-cloth in the passage; in addition to which discomforts a gentleman Ruler was smoking hard in the front parlour (though it was not yet noon), while the lady of the house was busily engaged in turpentining the disjointed fragments of a tent-bedstead at the door of the back parlour, as if in preparation for the reception of some new lodger who had been fortunate enough to engage it.", "겉보기에 그건 정말이지 허름한 집이었어. 응접실 창은 어둑하고, 블라인드는 거의 없다시피 했지. 더럽고 얇은 무명 커튼이 헐겁고 축 늘어진 줄에 걸린 채 아래쪽 유리창을 가로질러 대롱대롱 매달려 있었어. 문이 열렸을 때도 안쪽이 겉모습의 약속을 저버리지 않더군. 계단에는 빛바랜 카펫이, 복도에는 빛바랜 유포가 깔려 있었거든. 게다가 그 불편함에 더해, 한 신사 '룰러'가 앞쪽 응접실에서 (아직 정오도 안 됐는데) 담배를 뻑뻑 피워 대고 있었어. 한편 집주인 여자는 뒤쪽 응접실 문간에서, 흐트러진 텐트 침대 조각들에 테레빈유를 칠하느라 분주했지. 운 좋게 그 방을 빌리게 된 새 하숙인을 맞을 채비라도 하는 듯이 말이야."]], [["Nicholas had ample time to make these observations while the little boy, who went on errands for the lodgers, clattered down the kitchen stairs and was heard to scream, as in some remote cellar, for Miss Bray’s servant, who, presently appearing and requesting him to follow her, caused him to evince greater symptoms of nervousness and disorder than so natural a consequence of his having inquired for that young lady would seem calculated to occasion.", "니콜라스는 이런 것들을 두루 살펴볼 시간이 넉넉했어. 하숙인들 심부름을 다니는 어린 사내아이가 부엌 계단을 덜커덕거리며 내려가더니, 어느 깊은 지하실에서인 양 브레이 양의 하녀를 부르며 소리치는 게 들렸거든. 이내 하녀가 나타나서 따라오라고 했는데, 니콜라스는 그저 그 아가씨를 찾았을 뿐인데도 그 자연스러운 결과치고는 어울리지 않을 만큼 잔뜩 긴장하고 어쩔 줄 몰라 하는 기색을 내비쳤어."]], [["Upstairs he went, however, and into a front room he was shown, and there, seated at a little table by the window, on which were drawing materials with which she was occupied, sat the beautiful girl who had so engrossed his thoughts, and who, surrounded by all the new and strong interest which Nicholas attached to her story, seemed now, in his eyes, a thousand times more beautiful than he had ever yet supposed her.", "아무튼 그는 위층으로 올라갔고, 앞쪽 방으로 안내받았어. 그리고 거기, 창가의 자그마한 탁자 앞에 그 아름다운 처녀가 앉아 있었지. 탁자 위에는 그림 도구가 놓여 있었고, 그 처녀는 거기에 열중하고 있었어. 그토록 니콜라스의 마음을 사로잡았던 그 처녀가, 이제 그가 그 사연에 품게 된 새롭고 강렬한 온갖 관심에 둘러싸여, 그의 눈에는 여태껏 짐작했던 것보다 천 배는 더 아름다워 보였어."]], [["But how the graces and elegancies which she had dispersed about the poorly-furnished room went to the heart of Nicholas!", "그런데 그 처녀가 초라하게 꾸며진 방 여기저기에 흩어 놓은 그 우아함과 고상함이라니, 니콜라스의 가슴을 어찌나 울렸는지!"], ["Flowers, plants, birds, the harp, the old piano whose notes had sounded so much sweeter in bygone times; how many struggles had it cost her to keep these two last links of that broken chain which bound her yet to home!", "꽃이며, 화초며, 새며, 하프며, 지난날엔 그 가락이 훨씬 더 감미롭게 울려 퍼졌을 낡은 피아노 — 집과 그 처녀를 아직 이어 주는 그 끊어진 사슬의 마지막 두 고리를 지켜 내느라 그 처녀가 얼마나 안간힘을 썼을까!"], ["With every slender ornament, the occupation of her leisure hours, replete with that graceful charm which lingers in every little tasteful work of woman’s hands, how much patient endurance and how many gentle affections were entwined!", "여가 시간에 만든 그 가냘픈 장식 하나하나에는, 여인의 손이 빚은 자그마한 정성스러운 작품마다 깃드는 그 우아한 매력이 가득했고, 거기엔 또 얼마나 많은 묵묵한 인내와 다정한 정이 엮여 있었는지! 그는 마치 그 작은 방에 하늘의 미소가 어린 것만 같았어."], ["He felt as though the smile of Heaven were on the little chamber; as though the beautiful devotion of so young and weak a creature had shed a ray of its own on the inanimate things around, and made them beautiful as itself; as though the halo with which old painters surround the bright angels of a sinless world played about a being akin in spirit to them, and its light were visibly before him.", "그토록 어리고 여린 사람의 아름다운 헌신이, 둘레의 생명 없는 것들에까지 제 빛을 한 줄기 드리워 그것들을 저 자신처럼 아름답게 만든 것 같았지. 마치 옛 화가들이 죄 없는 세상의 빛나는 천사들을 둘러 그린 그 후광이, 영혼이 그 천사들과 닮은 한 존재의 둘레에 어른거리고, 그 빛이 그의 눈앞에 또렷이 보이는 것만 같았어."]], [["And yet Nicholas was in the Rules of the King’s Bench Prison!", "그런데도 니콜라스는 킹스 벤치 감옥의 룰스 안에 있었던 거야!"], ["If he had been in Italy indeed, and the time had been sunset, and the scene a stately terrace!", "차라리 그곳이 이탈리아였고, 때가 해 질 녘이었고, 무대가 웅장한 테라스였더라면!"], ["But, there is one broad sky over all the world, and whether it be blue or cloudy, the same heaven beyond it; so, perhaps, he had no need of compunction for thinking as he did.", "하지만 온 세상 위에 펼쳐진 하늘은 하나뿐이고, 그게 파랗든 흐리든 그 너머의 천국은 똑같으니, 어쩌면 그는 그렇게 생각했다고 양심에 거리낄 필요가 없었는지도 몰라."]], [["It is not to be supposed that he took in everything at one glance, for he had as yet been unconscious of the presence of a sick man propped up with pillows in an easy-chair, who, moving restlessly and impatiently in his seat, attracted his attention.", "그가 모든 걸 한눈에 다 알아챘다고 여겨선 안 돼. 그때까지 그는 안락의자에 베개를 기대고 앉은 병든 남자가 있다는 걸 알아채지 못했거든. 그 남자가 자리에서 안절부절못하며 짜증스레 몸을 뒤척이는 통에, 그제야 그의 눈길을 끌었지."]], [["He was scarce fifty, perhaps, but so emaciated as to appear much older.", "그는 기껏해야 쉰이 채 안 됐을 텐데, 어찌나 수척한지 훨씬 더 나이 들어 보였어."], ["His features presented the remains of a handsome countenance, but one in which the embers of strong and impetuous passions were easier to be traced than any expression which would have rendered a far plainer face much more prepossessing.", "그 이목구비에는 한때 잘생겼던 얼굴의 자취가 남아 있었지만, 거기엔 어떤 표정 — 훨씬 더 못난 얼굴조차 한결 호감 가게 만들어 줬을 그런 표정 — 보다, 강하고 격렬한 열정의 타다 남은 잿불이 오히려 더 또렷이 읽혔지. 그 행색은 무척 초췌했고, 팔다리와 몸은 글자 그대로 뼈만 남도록 야위었어."], ["His looks were very haggard, and his limbs and body literally worn to the bone, but there was something of the old fire in the large sunken eye notwithstanding, and it seemed to kindle afresh as he struck a thick stick, with which he seemed to have supported himself in his seat, impatiently on the floor twice or thrice, and called his daughter by her name.", "하지만 그래도 움푹 꺼진 그 커다란 눈에는 옛날의 그 불길 같은 것이 어려 있었지. 그리고 그가, 자리에 앉은 몸을 지탱하는 데 쓰던 듯한 굵은 지팡이로 바닥을 짜증스레 두세 번 쾅쾅 내리치며 딸의 이름을 부를 때, 그 불길이 다시 타오르는 것 같았어."]], [["‘Madeline, who is this?", "\"마들렌, 이 사람은 누구냐?"], ["What does anybody want here?", "누가 여기서 뭘 원한다는 거야?"], ["Who told a stranger we could be seen?", "누가 낯선 사람한테 우리를 만나도 된다고 했어?"], ["What is it?’", "무슨 일이냐?\""]], [["‘I believe--’ the young lady began, as she inclined her head with an air of some confusion, in reply to the salutation of Nicholas.", "\"제 생각엔—\" 젊은 아가씨가 입을 떼며, 니콜라스의 인사에 대답하느라 조금 당황한 기색으로 고개를 숙였어."]], [["‘You always believe,’ returned her father, petulantly.", "\"넌 늘 생각만 하지,\" 아버지가 짜증스레 받아쳤어."], ["‘What is it?’", "\"무슨 일이냐고?\""]], [["By this time Nicholas had recovered sufficient presence of mind to speak for himself, so he said (as it had been agreed he should say) that he had called about a pair of hand-screens, and some painted velvet for an ottoman, both of which were required to be of the most elegant design possible, neither time nor expense being of the smallest consideration. He had also to pay for the two drawings, with many thanks, and, advancing to the little table, he laid upon it a bank note, folded in an envelope and sealed.", "이쯤 되자 니콜라스도 직접 입을 열 만큼 정신을 추슬렀어. 그래서 (미리 그렇게 말하기로 약속한 대로) 손가리개 한 쌍과 오토만에 씌울 채색 벨벳을 좀 사러 왔다고 말했지. 둘 다 더없이 우아한 도안이어야 하고, 시간이나 비용은 조금도 따지지 않는다고 말이야. 또 그는 그림 두 점에 대한 값도 치러야 한다며 거듭 고마워했어. 그러고는 자그마한 탁자로 다가가, 봉투에 접어 넣고 봉인한 지폐 한 장을 그 위에 내려놓았지."]], [["‘See that the money is right, Madeline,’ said the father.", "\"돈이 맞는지 봐라, 마들렌,\" 아버지가 말했어."], ["‘Open the paper, my dear.’", "\"그 봉투를 열어 봐, 얘야.\""]], [["‘It’s quite right, papa, I’m sure.’", "\"틀림없이 맞아요, 아빠.\""]], [["‘Here!’ said Mr.", "\"이리 줘 봐!\""], ["Bray, putting out his hand, and opening and shutting his bony fingers with irritable impatience.", "브레이 씨가 손을 내밀며, 짜증스러운 조바심에 앙상한 손가락을 폈다 오므렸다 하며 말했어."], ["‘Let me see.", "\"어디 보자."], ["What are you talking about, Madeline?", "마들렌, 무슨 소릴 하는 거냐?"], ["You’re sure?", "맞다고?"], ["How can you be sure of any such thing?", "그런 걸 네가 어떻게 확신하느냐?"], ["Five pounds--well, is THAT right?’", "오 파운드 — 자, 이게 맞느냐?\""]], [["‘Quite,’ said Madeline, bending over him.", "\"맞아요,\" 마들렌이 아버지 위로 몸을 굽히며 말했어."], ["She was so busily employed in arranging the pillows that Nicholas could not see her face, but as she stooped he thought he saw a tear fall.", "그녀가 베개를 매만지느라 어찌나 분주했던지 니콜라스는 그 얼굴을 볼 수 없었어. 하지만 그녀가 몸을 숙일 때, 그는 눈물 한 방울이 떨어지는 걸 본 것 같았지."]], [["‘Ring the bell, ring the bell,’ said the sick man, with the same nervous eagerness, and motioning towards it with such a quivering hand that the bank note rustled in the air.", "\"종을 울려라, 종을 울려,\" 병든 남자가 똑같이 안달하는 기색으로 말했어."], ["‘Tell her to get it changed, to get me a newspaper, to buy me some grapes, another bottle of the wine that I had last week--and--and--I forget half I want just now, but she can go out again.", "그쪽을 손짓하는데, 그 떨리는 손에 지폐가 바스락거리며 허공에서 소리를 냈지. \"그 애더러 이걸 잔돈으로 바꿔 오라고 해라."], ["Let her get those first, those first.", "신문도 한 부 사 오고, 포도도 좀 사 오고, 지난주에 마신 그 포도주도 한 병 더 사 오라고 — 그리고 — 그리고 — 지금 당장 필요한 게 절반은 생각이 안 나는구나, 하지만 그 애가 또 나가면 되지."], ["Now, Madeline, my love, quick, quick!", "그것들부터 사 오라고 해, 그것들부터. 자, 마들렌, 얘야, 어서, 어서!"], ["Good God, how slow you are!’", "맙소사, 어쩜 이리 굼뜨냐!\""]], [["‘He remembers nothing that SHE wants!’ thought Nicholas.", "'그 아가씨한테 필요한 건 하나도 기억 못 하는군!' 니콜라스는 생각했어."], ["Perhaps something of what he thought was expressed in his countenance, for the sick man, turning towards him with great asperity, demanded to know if he waited for a receipt.", "어쩌면 그가 생각한 게 얼마쯤 얼굴에 드러났는지도 몰라. 병든 남자가 잔뜩 가시 돋친 얼굴로 그를 돌아보더니, 영수증을 기다리는 거냐고 따져 물었거든."]], [["‘It is no matter at all,’ said Nicholas.", "\"전혀 상관없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘No matter!", "\"상관없다고!"], ["what do you mean, sir?’ was the tart rejoinder.", "그게 무슨 소리요?\" 톡 쏘는 대꾸가 돌아왔어."], ["‘No matter!", "\"상관없다고!"], ["Do you think you bring your paltry money here as a favour or a gift; or as a matter of business, and in return for value received?", "당신이 이 푼돈을 호의나 선물로 가져온 줄 아쇼? 아니면 거래로, 받은 값어치에 대한 대가로 가져온 줄 아쇼?"], ["D--n you, sir, because you can’t appreciate the time and taste which are bestowed upon the goods you deal in, do you think you give your money away?", "빌어먹을, 당신이 다루는 물건에 들어간 시간과 안목을 알아보지 못한다고 해서, 그래 당신 돈을 그냥 거저 주는 거라 생각하쇼?"], ["Do you know that you are talking to a gentleman, sir, who at one time could have bought up fifty such men as you and all you have?", "당신이 지금 누구하고 얘기하고 있는지 알기나 하쇼? 한때는 당신 같은 사람 쉰 명에다 당신네 가진 거 전부를 다 사들이고도 남았을 신사란 말이오."], ["What do you mean?’", "무슨 뜻으로 하는 소리요?\""]], [["‘I merely mean that as I shall have many dealings with this lady, if she will kindly allow me, I will not trouble her with such forms,’ said Nicholas.", "\"저는 그저, 아가씨께서 너그러이 허락만 하신다면 앞으로 이 아가씨와 거래할 일이 많을 테니, 이런 격식으로 번거롭게 해 드리지 않으려는 것뿐입니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Then I mean, if you please, that we’ll have as many forms as we can, returned the father.", "\"그렇다면 나는, 미안하지만, 격식을 차릴 수 있는 만큼 죄다 차리겠소,\" 아버지가 받아쳤어."], ["‘My daughter, sir, requires no kindness from you or anybody else.", "\"내 딸은 당신이든 누구든한테서 호의 따위 받을 필요 없소."], ["Have the goodness to confine your dealings strictly to trade and business, and not to travel beyond it.", "거래와 장사에만 딱 매여서, 그 선을 넘지 않도록 해 주시오."], ["Every petty tradesman is to begin to pity her now, is he?", "이제 어중이떠중이 장사꾼마다 죄 이 애를 동정하기 시작하시겠다, 이거요?"], ["Upon my soul!", "원, 별꼴이군!"], ["Very pretty.", "아주 좋아."], ["Madeline, my dear, give him a receipt; and mind you always do so.’", "마들렌, 얘야, 이 사람한테 영수증을 써 줘라. 그리고 앞으로도 늘 그렇게 해.\""]], [["While she was feigning to write it, and Nicholas was ruminating upon the extraordinary but by no means uncommon character thus presented to his observation, the invalid, who appeared at times to suffer great bodily pain, sank back in his chair and moaned out a feeble complaint that the girl had been gone an hour, and that everybody conspired to goad him.", "그녀가 영수증을 쓰는 척하고, 니콜라스가 이렇게 눈앞에 펼쳐진 별난, 하지만 결코 드물지 않은 인물됨을 곱씹는 동안, 때때로 몸이 몹시 아픈 듯하던 그 병자는 의자에 푹 기댄 채 힘없이 투덜거렸어. 그 애가 나간 지 한 시간이 됐다느니, 다들 짜고서 자기를 들들 볶는다느니 하고 말이야."]], [["‘When,’ said Nicholas, as he took the piece of paper, ‘when shall I call again?’", "\"언제,\" 니콜라스가 그 종잇장을 받아 들며 말했어. \"언제 다시 들를까요?\""]], [["This was addressed to the daughter, but the father answered immediately.", "이 말은 딸한테 한 거였지만, 아버지가 곧장 대답했어."]], [["‘When you’re requested to call, sir, and not before.", "\"당신더러 들르라고 청할 때, 그 전엔 안 되오."], ["Don’t worry and persecute.", "귀찮게 들볶지 마시오."], ["Madeline, my dear, when is this person to call again?’", "마들렌, 얘야, 이 사람이 언제 다시 오면 되겠느냐?\""]], [["‘Oh, not for a long time, not for three or four weeks; it is not necessary, indeed; I can do without,’ said the young lady, with great eagerness.", "\"아, 한참 동안은 오지 마세요, 서너 주는요. 정말 그럴 필요 없어요. 없어도 돼요.\" 젊은 아가씨가 무척 간곡하게 말했어."]], [["‘Why, how are we to do without?’ urged her father, not speaking above his breath.", "\"아니, 없이 어떻게 지내려고?\" 아버지가 숨소리보다 크지 않은 목소리로 다그쳤어."], ["‘Three or four weeks, Madeline!", "\"서너 주라니, 마들렌!"], ["Three or four weeks!’", "서너 주라니!\""]], [["‘Then sooner, sooner, if you please,’ said the young lady, turning to Nicholas.", "\"그럼 더 빨리, 더 빨리 오세요, 부탁이에요.\" 젊은 아가씨가 니콜라스를 돌아보며 말했어."]], [["‘Three or four weeks!’ muttered the father.", "\"서너 주라니!\" 아버지가 중얼거렸어."], ["‘Madeline, what on earth--do nothing for three or four weeks!’", "\"마들렌, 도대체 어쩌자고 — 서너 주씩이나 아무것도 안 하겠다니!\""]], [["‘It is a long time, ma’am,’ said Nicholas.", "\"긴 시간이네요, 아가씨.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘YOU think so, do you?’ retorted the father, angrily.", "\"당신은 그렇게 생각하쇼?\" 아버지가 성을 내며 받아쳤어."], ["‘If I chose to beg, sir, and stoop to ask assistance from people I despise, three or four months would not be a long time; three or four years would not be a long time.", "\"내가 구걸이라도 하겠다고 마음먹고, 내가 경멸하는 인간들한테 굽실거리며 도움을 청하기로 한다면야, 서너 달도 긴 시간이 아닐 거요. 서너 해도 긴 시간이 아니지."], ["Understand, sir, that is if I chose to be dependent; but as I don’t, you may call in a week.’", "알아 두쇼, 그게 다 내가 남한테 기대 살기로 한다면 그렇다는 거요. 하지만 난 그럴 생각이 없으니, 일주일 뒤에 와도 좋소.\""]], [["Nicholas bowed low to the young lady and retired, pondering upon Mr Bray’s ideas of independence, and devoutly hoping that there might be few such independent spirits as he mingling with the baser clay of humanity.", "니콜라스는 젊은 아가씨에게 깊숙이 고개 숙여 인사하고 물러났어. 브레이 씨가 말하는 독립이라는 게 어떤 건지 곱씹으면서, 그리고 인간이라는 천한 흙덩이에 그 사람처럼 독립적인 영혼들이 섞여 있는 일은 부디 드물기를 간절히 빌면서 말이야."]], [["He heard a light footstep above him as he descended the stairs, and looking round saw that the young lady was standing there, and glancing timidly towards him, seemed to hesitate whether she should call him back or no.", "계단을 내려오는데 위쪽에서 가벼운 발소리가 들렸어. 돌아보니 그 젊은 아가씨가 거기 서서 그를 머뭇머뭇 바라보고 있었지."], ["The best way of settling the question was to turn back at once, which Nicholas did.", "그를 다시 부를까 말까 망설이는 것 같았어. 이 물음을 푸는 가장 좋은 방법은 곧장 되돌아가는 거였고, 니콜라스는 그렇게 했지."]], [["‘I don’t know whether I do right in asking you, sir,’ said Madeline, hurriedly, ‘but pray, pray, do not mention to my poor mother’s dear friends what has passed here today.", "\"이런 부탁을 드리는 게 옳은지 모르겠어요,\" 마들렌이 다급히 말했어."], ["He has suffered much, and is worse this morning.", "\"하지만 제발, 제발 오늘 여기서 있었던 일을 제 가엾은 어머니의 그 다정한 친구분들께 말씀하지 말아 주세요. 아버지는 고생을 많이 하셨고, 오늘 아침엔 상태가 더 안 좋으세요."], ["I beg you, sir, as a boon, a favour to myself.’", "부탁드려요, 저를 위한 은혜로, 호의로 여기시고요.\""]], [["‘You have but to hint a wish,’ returned Nicholas fervently, ‘and I would hazard my life to gratify it.’", "\"바라시는 걸 그저 한마디 비치기만 하셔도,\" 니콜라스가 뜨겁게 대답했어. \"저는 그걸 들어 드리려고 제 목숨이라도 걸겠습니다.\""]], [["‘You speak hastily, sir.’", "\"성급히 말씀하시네요.\""]], [["‘Truly and sincerely,’ rejoined Nicholas, his lips trembling as he formed the words, ‘if ever man spoke truly yet.", "\"진실로, 진심으로 드리는 말씀입니다,\" 니콜라스가 그 말을 빚어내는 동안 입술을 떨며 대꾸했어."], ["I am not skilled in disguising my feelings, and if I were, I could not hide my heart from you.", "\"여태껏 그 누가 진실을 말한 적이 있다면, 바로 이만큼 진실하게요. 저는 제 감정을 감추는 데 서툴고, 설사 그럴 줄 안다 해도 아가씨께만은 제 마음을 숨길 수가 없습니다."], ["Dear madam, as I know your history, and feel as men and angels must who hear and see such things, I do entreat you to believe that I would die to serve you.’", "아가씨, 저는 아가씨의 사연을 알고, 그런 일을 듣고 보는 사람과 천사라면 누구나 그러하듯 느끼는 사람으로서, 부디 믿어 주시기를 간청합니다. 저는 아가씨를 위해서라면 기꺼이 죽겠습니다.\""]], [["The young lady turned away her head, and was plainly weeping.", "젊은 아가씨는 고개를 돌렸고, 누가 봐도 울고 있었어."]], [["‘Forgive me,’ said Nicholas, with respectful earnestness, ‘if I seem to say too much, or to presume upon the confidence which has been intrusted to me.", "\"용서하세요,\" 니콜라스가 정중하면서도 간절하게 말했어."], ["But I could not leave you as if my interest and sympathy expired with the commission of the day.", "\"제가 말이 지나쳤거나, 제게 맡겨진 그 믿음을 함부로 넘어선 것처럼 보였다면요. 하지만 제 관심과 동정이 오늘 맡은 그 일과 함께 끝나 버리기라도 하듯 아가씨 곁을 떠날 수는 없었습니다."], ["I am your faithful servant, humbly devoted to you from this hour, devoted in strict truth and honour to him who sent me here, and in pure integrity of heart, and distant respect for you.", "저는 이 순간부터 아가씨께 겸손히 헌신하는 충실한 종입니다. 저를 이리로 보내신 분께는 한 치의 거짓 없는 진실과 명예로, 또 아가씨께는 순수하고 올곧은 마음과 정중한 거리를 지켜 헌신하는 사람입니다."], ["If I meant more or less than this, I should be unworthy his regard, and false to the very nature that prompts the honest words I utter.’", "만약 제 뜻이 이보다 더하거나 덜하다면, 저는 그분의 신임을 받을 자격이 없는 사람이고, 제가 지금 내뱉는 이 정직한 말을 부추기는 그 천성마저 저버리는 사람일 겁니다.\""]], [["She waved her hand, entreating him to be gone, but answered not a word.", "그녀는 어서 가 달라고 청하며 손을 내저었지만, 한마디도 대답하지 않았어."], ["Nicholas could say no more, and silently withdrew.", "니콜라스는 더 이상 아무 말도 할 수 없어 말없이 물러났지."], ["And thus ended his first interview with Madeline Bray.", "그렇게 마들렌 브레이와의 첫 만남이 끝났어."]], [["# CHAPTER 47", "# CHAPTER 47"]], [["Mr. Ralph Nickleby has some confidential Intercourse with another old Friend.", "이웃 교회의 종소리에 귀를 기울이던 뉴먼 노그스가 중얼거렸다."], ["They concert between them a Project, which promises well for both\n‘There go the three-quarters past!’ muttered Newman Noggs, listening to the chimes of some neighbouring church ‘and my dinner time’s two.", "\"벌써 사십오 분 종이 울리네! 내 점심시간은 두 시인데."], ["He does it on purpose.", "저 양반은 일부러 그러는 거야."], ["He makes a point of it.", "작정하고 그러는 거라고."], ["It’s just like him.’", "딱 저 양반답지.\""]], [["It was in his own little den of an office and on the top of his official stool that Newman thus soliloquised; and the soliloquy referred, as Newman’s grumbling soliloquies usually did, to Ralph Nickleby.", "뉴먼이 이렇게 혼잣말을 한 건 자기만의 좁은 사무실, 그 높은 사무용 의자 위에서였다. 그리고 그 혼잣말은, 뉴먼이 투덜대는 혼잣말이 늘 그렇듯, 랄프 니클비를 두고 하는 소리였다."]], [["‘I don’t believe he ever had an appetite,’ said Newman, ‘except for pounds, shillings, and pence, and with them he’s as greedy as a wolf.", "\"저 양반은 입맛이란 게 있었던 적이 없는 것 같아.\" 뉴먼이 말했다."], ["I should like to have him compelled to swallow one of every English coin.", "\"파운드, 실링, 펜스 빼고는 말이지. 그런데 그걸 두고는 늑대처럼 게걸스럽거든."], ["The penny would be an awkward morsel--but the crown--ha!", "영국 동전을 종류별로 하나씩 삼키게 만들어 봤으면 좋겠어. 일 펜스짜리는 삼키기 거북한 한 입이겠지만, 크라운짜리는, 하하!"], ["ha!’", "하하!\""]], [["His good-humour being in some degree restored by the vision of Ralph Nickleby swallowing, perforce, a five-shilling piece, Newman slowly brought forth from his desk one of those portable bottles, currently known as pocket-pistols, and shaking the same close to his ear so as to produce a rippling sound very cool and pleasant to listen to, suffered his features to relax, and took a gurgling drink, which relaxed them still more. Replacing the cork, he smacked his lips twice or thrice with an air of great relish, and, the taste of the liquor having by this time evaporated, recurred to his grievance again.", "랄프 니클비가 어쩔 수 없이 오 실링짜리 동전을 삼키는 모습을 상상하니 기분이 어느 정도 풀렸다. 뉴먼은 책상에서 흔히 '포켓 권총'이라 불리는, 들고 다니는 작은 병 하나를 천천히 꺼냈다. 그러고는 귀에 바짝 대고 흔들어, 듣기에 시원하고 기분 좋은 찰랑찰랑 소리를 냈다. 그러자 굳었던 얼굴이 풀어졌고, 꿀꺽꿀꺽 한 모금 마시니 표정이 한층 더 풀어졌다. 코르크 마개를 도로 막고는, 아주 흡족하다는 듯 입맛을 두세 번 쩝쩝 다셨다. 그러다 이제 술맛이 사라지자, 다시 자기 신세 한탄으로 돌아갔다."]], [["‘Five minutes to three,’ growled Newman; ‘it can’t want more by this time; and I had my breakfast at eight o’clock, and SUCH a breakfast!", "\"세 시 오 분 전이군.\" 뉴먼이 으르렁거렸다."], ["and my right dinner-time two!", "\"이쯤 됐으면 더 걸릴 것도 없겠지."], ["And I might have a nice little bit of hot roast meat spoiling at home all this time--how does HE know I haven’t?", "게다가 나는 여덟 시에 아침을 먹었어. 그것도 그런 아침을 말이야!"], ["“Don’t go till I come back,”", "그리고 내 진짜 점심시간은 두 시라고!"], ["“Don’t go till I come back,” day after day.", "지금 이 시간에 집에서는 뜨끈한 구운 고기 한 점이 식어 가며 망가지고 있을지도 모르는데, 저 양반이 그걸 어찌 알겠어?"], ["What do you always go out at my dinner-time for then--eh?", "'내가 돌아올 때까지 가지 마라', '내가 돌아올 때까지 가지 마라', 날이면 날마다 그 소리지."], ["Don’t you know it’s nothing but aggravation--eh?’", "그러면 왜 늘 내 점심시간에 나가는 건데, 응? 그게 사람 약 올리는 짓 말고 뭐냐고, 응?\""]], [["These words, though uttered in a very loud key, were addressed to nothing but empty air.", "이 말은 아주 큰 소리로 내뱉은 것이었지만, 듣는 거라곤 빈 허공뿐이었다."], ["The recital of his wrongs, however, seemed to have the effect of making Newman Noggs desperate; for he flattened his old hat upon his head, and drawing on the everlasting gloves, declared with great vehemence, that come what might, he would go to dinner that very minute.", "하지만 자기 억울함을 늘어놓고 나니 뉴먼 노그스는 마음이 독해진 모양이었다. 그는 낡은 모자를 머리에 눌러쓰고, 늘 끼는 장갑을 당겨 끼더니, 무슨 일이 있어도 바로 이 순간 점심을 먹으러 가겠다고 아주 단호하게 선언했다."]], [["Carrying this resolution into instant effect, he had advanced as far as the passage, when the sound of the latch-key in the street door caused him to make a precipitate retreat into his own office again.", "이 결심을 곧장 실행에 옮겨, 그가 복도까지 나왔을 때였다. 길 쪽 현관문에서 열쇠가 돌아가는 소리가 나는 바람에, 그는 부랴부랴 다시 자기 사무실로 후퇴했다."]], [["‘Here he is,’ growled Newman, ‘and somebody with him.", "\"왔군.\" 뉴먼이 으르렁거렸다."], ["Now it’ll be “Stop till this gentleman’s gone.”", "\"누구랑 같이 왔어. 이제 '이 신사분 가실 때까지 있어라'"], ["But I won’t.", "하겠지. 하지만 나는 안 그럴 거야."], ["That’s flat.’", "그건 확실해.\""]], [["So saying, Newman slipped into a tall empty closet which opened with two half doors, and shut himself up; intending to slip out directly Ralph was safe inside his own room.", "이렇게 말하면서 뉴먼은 반쪽짜리 문 두 짝이 달린, 키 큰 빈 벽장 속으로 슬쩍 들어가 안에 틀어박혔다. 랄프가 자기 방으로 무사히 들어가자마자 빠져나올 작정이었다."]], [["‘Noggs!’ cried Ralph, ‘where is that fellow, Noggs?’", "\"노그스!\" 랄프가 외쳤다. \"그 녀석 어디 갔어, 노그스?\""]], [["But not a word said Newman.", "하지만 뉴먼은 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["‘The dog has gone to his dinner, though I told him not,’ muttered Ralph, looking into the office, and pulling out his watch.", "\"가지 말라고 했는데 점심 먹으러 갔군, 이놈.\" 랄프가 사무실 안을 들여다보며 회중시계를 꺼내고는 중얼거렸다."], ["‘Humph!’ You had better come in here, Gride.", "\"흥! 그라이드, 자네는 이리 들어오는 게 낫겠어."], ["My man’s out, and the sun is hot upon my room.", "내 직원이 나갔고, 내 방은 햇볕이 뜨거워."], ["This is cool and in the shade, if you don’t mind roughing it.’", "여기가 시원하고 그늘이 졌네, 좀 누추한 건 괜찮다면 말이지.\""]], [["‘Not at all, Mr. Nickleby, oh not at all!", "\"전혀 괜찮습니다, 니클비 씨, 아 전혀요!"], ["All places are alike to me, sir.", "저는 어디든 다 똑같습니다, 선생님."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["very nice indeed.", "아주 좋은데요."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["very nice!’", "아주 좋아요!\""]], [["The parson who made this reply was a little old man, of about seventy or seventy-five years of age, of a very lean figure, much bent and slightly twisted. He wore a grey coat with a very narrow collar, an old-fashioned waistcoat of ribbed black silk, and such scanty trousers as displayed his shrunken spindle-shanks in their full ugliness. The only articles of display or ornament in his dress were a steel watch-chain to which were attached some large gold seals; and a black ribbon into which, in compliance with an old fashion scarcely ever observed in these days, his grey hair was gathered behind. His nose and chin were sharp and prominent, his jaws had fallen inwards from loss of teeth, his face was shrivelled and yellow, save where the cheeks were streaked with the colour of a dry winter apple; and where his beard had been, there lingered yet a few grey tufts which seemed, like the ragged eyebrows, to denote the badness of the soil from which they sprung. The whole air and attitude of the form was one of stealthy cat-like obsequiousness; the whole expression of the face was concentrated in a wrinkled leer, compounded of cunning, lecherousness, slyness, and avarice.", "이렇게 대답한 노인은 일흔이나 일흔다섯쯤 된 작은 체구의 사람이었다. 몸이 무척 야위었고, 등이 많이 굽었으며 약간 비틀려 있었다. 그는 깃이 아주 좁은 회색 코트를 입고, 검은 비단으로 골이 진 구식 조끼를 걸쳤다. 그리고 어찌나 빈약한 바지인지, 쪼그라든 막대기 같은 정강이가 그 흉한 모습을 고스란히 드러냈다. 옷에서 자랑거리나 장식이라고는 딱 두 가지뿐이었다. 큼직한 금 도장이 몇 개 달린 강철 시곗줄, 그리고 요즘은 거의 보기 힘든 옛날식을 따라 회색 머리카락을 뒤로 묶은 검은 리본이었다. 그의 코와 턱은 날카롭고 뾰족했으며, 이가 빠져 턱이 안쪽으로 움푹 들어가 있었다. 얼굴은 쪼글쪼글하고 누랬는데, 마른 겨울 사과 빛이 줄무늬처럼 번진 두 뺨만 빼고는 그랬다. 그리고 수염이 났던 자리에는 아직 회색 수염 몇 가닥이 듬성듬성 남아 있었는데, 그건 너덜너덜한 눈썹과 마찬가지로 그것들이 돋아난 토양이 얼마나 메마른지를 말해 주는 것 같았다. 그 몸 전체에서 풍기는 분위기와 태도는 고양이처럼 살금살금 다가드는 비굴함이었다. 그리고 얼굴 전체의 표정은 주름진 곁눈질 하나에 다 모여 있었는데, 거기에는 교활함과 음탕함, 능청스러움과 탐욕이 한데 뒤섞여 있었다."]], [["Such was old Arthur Gride, in whose face there was not a wrinkle, in whose dress there was not one spare fold or plait, but expressed the most covetous and griping penury, and sufficiently indicated his belonging to that class of which Ralph Nickleby was a member. Such was old Arthur Gride, as he sat in a low chair looking up into the face of Ralph Nickleby, who, lounging upon the tall office stool, with his arms upon his knees, looked down into his; a match for him on whatever errand he had come.", "이런 사람이 바로 늙은 아서 그라이드였다. 그의 얼굴에는 주름 하나, 옷에는 남는 주름이나 접힌 자락 하나 없었지만, 그 모든 것이 더없이 욕심 많고 인색한 궁핍함을 드러냈다. 그리고 그가 랄프 니클비가 속한 부류의 사람임을 충분히 일러 주었다. 이런 사람이 바로 늙은 아서 그라이드였다. 그는 낮은 의자에 앉아 랄프 니클비의 얼굴을 올려다보았고, 랄프는 높은 사무용 의자에 느긋이 걸터앉아 두 팔을 무릎에 올린 채 그를 내려다보았다. 그가 무슨 용건으로 왔든 만만치 않은 상대였다."]], [["‘And how have you been?’ said Gride, feigning great interest in Ralph’s state of health.", "\"그래, 그동안 어떻게 지내셨소?\" 그라이드가 랄프의 건강 상태에 무척 관심 있는 척하며 말했다."], ["‘I haven’t seen you for--oh!", "\"못 본 지가, 오!"], ["not for--’", "정말로 못 본 지가.\""]], [["‘Not for a long time,’ said Ralph, with a peculiar smile, importing that he very well knew it was not on a mere visit of compliment that his friend had come. ‘It was a narrow chance that you saw me now, for I had only just come up to the door as you turned the corner.’", "\"오랜만이지.\" 랄프가 묘한 미소를 지으며 말했다. 친구가 단지 안부나 물으러 온 게 아니라는 걸 잘 알고 있다는 뜻이었다. \"이렇게 만난 것도 아슬아슬한 거였어. 자네가 모퉁이를 돌 때 마침 내가 막 문 앞에 도착한 참이었거든.\""]], [["‘I am very lucky,’ observed Gride.", "\"제가 운이 아주 좋군요.\" 그라이드가 말했다."]], [["‘So men say,’ replied Ralph, drily.", "\"다들 그렇게 말하지.\" 랄프가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다."]], [["The older money-lender wagged his chin and smiled, but he originated no new remark, and they sat for some little time without speaking.", "나이 든 고리대금업자는 턱을 까딱이며 미소를 지었다. 그러나 새로 무슨 말을 꺼내지는 않았고, 두 사람은 한동안 말없이 앉아 있었다."], ["Each was looking out to take the other at a disadvantage.", "둘 다 상대의 허점을 잡으려고 살피는 중이었다."]], [["‘Come, Gride,’ said Ralph, at length; ‘what’s in the wind today?’", "\"이봐, 그라이드.\" 마침내 랄프가 말했다. \"오늘은 무슨 바람이 분 건가?\""]], [["‘Aha!", "\"아하!"], ["you’re a bold man, Mr.", "당신은 대담한 분이오, 니클비 씨.\""], ["Nickleby,’ cried the other, apparently very much relieved by Ralph’s leading the way to business.", "상대가 외쳤다. 랄프가 먼저 본론으로 이끌어 주자 무척 안심한 기색이었다."], ["‘Oh dear, dear, what a bold man you are!’", "\"이런, 이런, 정말 대담한 분이라니까!\""]], [["‘Why, you have a sleek and slinking way with you that makes me seem so by contrast,’ returned Ralph.", "\"왜, 자네가 워낙 매끄럽고 슬그머니 구는 데가 있으니, 그에 비해 내가 그렇게 보이는 거겠지.\" 랄프가 받아쳤다."], ["‘I don’t know but that yours may answer better, but I want the patience for it.’", "\"자네 방식이 더 나을지도 모르지만, 나는 그럴 만한 인내심이 없어서 말이야.\""]], [["‘You were born a genius, Mr.", "\"당신은 천재로 태어난 분입니다, 니클비 씨.\""], ["Nickleby,’ said old Arthur.", "늙은 아서가 말했다."], ["‘Deep, deep, deep.", "\"깊고, 깊고, 깊어요."], ["Ah!’", "아!\""]], [["‘Deep enough,’ retorted Ralph, ‘to know that I shall need all the depth I have, when men like you begin to compliment. You know I have stood by when you fawned and flattered other people, and I remember pretty well what THAT always led to.’", "\"충분히 깊지.\" 랄프가 되받았다. \"자네 같은 사람이 칭찬을 시작하면, 내가 가진 깊이를 다 끌어와야 한다는 걸 알 만큼은 말이야. 자네가 다른 사람들한테 아첨하고 알랑거릴 때 내가 옆에서 지켜봤잖아. 그게 늘 어떤 결말로 이어졌는지도 꽤 잘 기억하고 있어.\""]], [["‘Ha, ha, ha!’ rejoined Arthur, rubbing his hands.", "\"하, 하, 하!\" 아서가 두 손을 비비며 응수했다."], ["‘So you do, so you do, no doubt.", "\"그렇지요, 그렇고말고요, 틀림없이."], ["Not a man knows it better.", "그걸 당신보다 잘 아는 사람도 없지요."], ["Well, it’s a pleasant thing now to think that you remember old times.", "자, 이제 당신이 옛 시절을 기억해 준다고 생각하니 참 기분 좋은 일입니다."], ["Oh dear!’", "이런!\""]], [["‘Now then,’ said Ralph, composedly; ‘what’s in the wind, I ask again?", "\"자, 그럼.\" 랄프가 침착하게 말했다."], ["What is it?’", "\"무슨 바람이 분 거냐고 다시 묻겠네. 뭔가?\""]], [["‘See that now!’ cried the other.", "\"저것 좀 보게!\" 상대가 외쳤다."], ["‘He can’t even keep from business while we’re chatting over bygones.", "\"지난 얘기를 나누는 중에도 일 얘기를 못 참는다니까."], ["Oh dear, dear, what a man it is!’", "이런, 이런, 정말 대단한 양반이야!\""]], [["‘WHICH of the bygones do you want to revive?’ said Ralph.", "\"옛날 일 가운데 뭘 들춰내고 싶은 건가?\" 랄프가 말했다."], ["‘One of them, I know, or you wouldn’t talk about them.’", "\"그 가운데 하나겠지, 아니면 그 얘기를 꺼낼 리가 없으니까.\""]], [["‘He suspects even me!’ cried old Arthur, holding up his hands.", "\"이 양반이 나까지 의심하네!\" 늙은 아서가 두 손을 치켜들며 외쳤다."], ["‘Even me!", "\"나까지도!"], ["Oh dear, even me.", "이런, 나까지도."], ["What a man it is!", "정말 대단한 양반이야!"], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하!"], ["What a man it is!", "정말 대단한 양반이라니까!"], ["Mr Nickleby against all the world.", "온 세상을 상대하는 니클비 씨."], ["There’s nobody like him.", "저런 사람은 또 없지."], ["A giant among pigmies, a giant, a giant!’", "난쟁이들 틈의 거인이야, 거인, 거인이라고!\""]], [["Ralph looked at the old dog with a quiet smile as he chuckled on in this strain, and Newman Noggs in the closet felt his heart sink within him as the prospect of dinner grew fainter and fainter.", "랄프는 그 늙은 능구렁이가 이런 식으로 낄낄거리는 모습을 잔잔한 미소를 지으며 바라보았다. 그리고 벽장 속의 뉴먼 노그스는 점심을 먹을 가망이 점점 희미해져 가자 가슴이 철렁 내려앉는 기분이었다."]], [["‘I must humour him though,’ cried old Arthur; ‘he must have his way--a wilful man, as the Scotch say--well, well, they’re a wise people, the Scotch.", "\"그래도 비위를 맞춰 줘야지.\" 늙은 아서가 외쳤다."], ["He will talk about business, and won’t give away his time for nothing.", "\"이 양반은 제 뜻대로 해야 직성이 풀리니까. 스코틀랜드 사람들 말마따나 고집 센 사람이거든."], ["He’s very right.", "뭐, 그래, 스코틀랜드 사람들은 현명한 민족이지. 이 양반은 일 얘기를 하려 들고, 제 시간을 거저 내주는 법이 없어."], ["Time is money, time is money.’", "아주 옳은 일이지. 시간이 돈이니까, 시간이 돈이니까.\""]], [["‘He was one of us who made that saying, I should think,’ said Ralph.", "\"그 말은 우리 같은 사람이 만든 것 같은데.\" 랄프가 말했다."], ["‘Time is money, and very good money too, to those who reckon interest by it.", "\"시간은 돈이지, 그것도 그걸로 이자를 따지는 사람한테는 아주 좋은 돈이고."], ["Time IS money!", "시간이 돈이라!"], ["Yes, and time costs money; it’s rather an expensive article to some people we could name, or I forget my trade.’", "그래, 그리고 시간은 돈이 들기도 하지. 누군지 우리가 댈 수 있는 어떤 사람들한테는 꽤 비싼 물건이야, 안 그러면 내가 내 장사를 잊은 거고.\""]], [["In rejoinder to this sally, old Arthur again raised his hands, again chuckled, and again ejaculated ‘What a man it is!’ which done, he dragged the low chair a little nearer to Ralph’s high stool, and looking upwards into his immovable face, said,", "이 비꼬는 말에 늙은 아서는 다시 두 손을 치켜들고, 다시 낄낄거리고, 다시 \"정말 대단한 양반이야!\" 하고 내뱉었다. 그러고는 낮은 의자를 랄프의 높은 의자 쪽으로 조금 더 끌어당기더니, 꿈쩍도 않는 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다."]], [["‘What would you say to me, if I was to tell you that I was--that I was--going to be married?’", "\"내가 만약, 내가 만약 결혼을 할 거라고 한다면, 당신은 뭐라고 하시겠소?\""]], [["‘I should tell you,’ replied Ralph, looking coldly down upon him, ‘that for some purpose of your own you told a lie, and that it wasn’t the first time and wouldn’t be the last; that I wasn’t surprised and wasn’t to be taken in.’", "\"내가 자네한테 이렇게 말하겠지.\" 랄프가 차갑게 그를 내려다보며 대답했다. \"자네 무슨 속셈이 있어서 거짓말을 하는 거라고. 그것도 이번이 처음이 아니고 마지막도 아닐 거라고. 나는 놀라지도 않았고 속아 넘어갈 사람도 아니라고 말이야.\""]], [["‘Then I tell you seriously that I am,’ said old Arthur.", "\"그러면 진지하게 말하리다, 나 정말 결혼하오.\" 늙은 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘And I tell you seriously,’ rejoined Ralph, ‘what I told you this minute.", "\"그러면 나도 진지하게 말하겠네.\""], ["Stay.", "랄프가 받아쳤다."], ["Let me look at you.", "\"방금 한 그 말 그대로야. 가만."], ["There’s a liquorish devilry in your face.", "어디 좀 보세. 자네 얼굴에 음탕한 악마가 들었군."], ["What is this?’", "이게 무슨 일이야?\""]], [["‘I wouldn’t deceive YOU, you know,’ whined Arthur Gride; ‘I couldn’t do it, I should be mad to try.", "\"당신을 속이려는 게 아니오, 알잖소.\" 아서 그라이드가 칭얼거렸다."], ["I, I, to deceive Mr.", "\"그럴 수도 없거니와, 그러려고 들면 미친 짓이지."], ["Nickleby!", "내가, 내가 니클비 씨를 속이다니!"], ["The pigmy to impose upon the giant.", "난쟁이가 거인을 속이려 들다니. 다시 묻겠소."], ["I ask again--he, he, he!--what should you say to me if I was to tell you that I was going to be married?’", "헤, 헤, 헤! 내가 만약 결혼을 할 거라고 한다면, 당신은 뭐라고 하시겠소?\""]], [["‘To some old hag?’ said Ralph.", "\"어디 늙은 노파한테?\" 랄프가 말했다."]], [["‘No, No,’ cried Arthur, interrupting him, and rubbing his hands in an ecstasy.", "\"아니오, 아니오.\" 아서가 그의 말을 가로막으며, 황홀해서 두 손을 비비며 외쳤다."], ["‘Wrong, wrong again. Mr.", "\"틀렸소, 또 틀렸소."], ["Nickleby for once at fault; out, quite out!", "니클비 씨가 한 번은 헛짚었군. 빗나갔소, 완전히 빗나갔어!"], ["To a young and beautiful girl; fresh, lovely, bewitching, and not nineteen.", "젊고 아름다운 처녀한테요. 싱싱하고, 사랑스럽고, 호리는 듯하고, 아직 열아홉도 안 됐소."], ["Dark eyes, long eyelashes, ripe and ruddy lips that to look at is to long to kiss, beautiful clustering hair that one’s fingers itch to play with, such a waist as might make a man clasp the air involuntarily, thinking of twining his arm about it, little feet that tread so lightly they hardly seem to walk upon the ground--to marry all this, sir, this--hey, hey!’", "검은 눈동자, 긴 속눈썹, 보기만 해도 입 맞추고 싶어지는 무르익은 붉은 입술, 손가락으로 만지작거리고 싶어 안달 나는 탐스럽게 굽이치는 머릿결, 그 둘레로 저절로 팔을 감고 싶어져 허공을 끌어안게 만드는 그런 허리, 어찌나 사뿐히 걷는지 땅을 밟는 것 같지도 않은 작은 발. 이 모든 걸 다 데리고 결혼하는 거요, 선생, 이걸, 헤, 헤!\""]], [["‘This is something more than common drivelling,’ said Ralph, after listening with a curled lip to the old sinner’s raptures.", "\"이건 흔한 헛소리보다 좀 더한데.\" 랄프가 그 늙은 죄인의 황홀경을 입술을 비죽이며 듣고는 말했다."], ["‘The girl’s name?’", "\"그 처녀 이름이?\""]], [["‘Oh deep, deep!", "\"오 깊다, 깊어!"], ["See now how deep that is!’ exclaimed old Arthur.", "자, 보게, 저게 얼마나 깊은지!\" 늙은 아서가 외쳤다."], ["‘He knows I want his help, he knows he can give it me, he knows it must all turn to his advantage, he sees the thing already.", "\"이 양반은 내가 자기 도움이 필요하다는 걸 알아. 자기가 그걸 줄 수 있다는 것도 알고, 그게 다 자기한테 이득이 되리란 것도 알지."], ["Her name--is there nobody within hearing?’", "벌써 일이 어찌 돌아갈지 다 보이는 거야. 그 처녀 이름은, 근처에 듣는 사람 없소?\""]], [["‘Why, who the devil should there be?’ retorted Ralph, testily.", "\"아니, 도대체 여기 누가 있다고 그래?\" 랄프가 짜증스럽게 받아쳤다."]], [["‘I didn’t know but that perhaps somebody might be passing up or down the stairs,’ said Arthur Gride, after looking out at the door and carefully reclosing it; ‘or but that your man might have come back and might have been listening outside.", "\"혹시 누가 계단을 오르내리며 지나갈지도 모르잖소.\" 아서 그라이드가 문밖을 내다보고 조심스레 다시 닫고 나서 말했다."], ["Clerks and servants have a trick of listening, and I should have been very uncomfortable if Mr.", "\"아니면 당신 직원이 돌아와서 밖에서 엿듣고 있을지도 모르고. 서기나 하인들은 엿듣는 버릇이 있거든."], ["Noggs--’", "노그스 씨가 만약.\""]], [["‘Curse Mr.", "\"노그스 씨 따위 빌어먹을.\""], ["Noggs,’ said Ralph, sharply, ‘and go on with what you have to say.’", "랄프가 날카롭게 말했다. \"할 얘기나 계속해.\""]], [["‘Curse Mr.", "\"노그스 씨 따위 빌어먹을, 좋고말고요.\""], ["Noggs, by all means,’ rejoined old Arthur; ‘I am sure I have not the least objection to that.", "늙은 아서가 받았다. \"그거야 나도 조금도 반대할 게 없지요."], ["Her name is--’", "그 처녀 이름은.\""]], [["‘Well,’ said Ralph, rendered very irritable by old Arthur’s pausing again ‘what is it?’", "\"어서.\" 늙은 아서가 또 뜸을 들이는 바람에 잔뜩 짜증이 난 랄프가 말했다. \"뭐야?\""]], [["‘Madeline Bray.’", "\"매들린 브레이요.\""]], [["Whatever reasons there might have been--and Arthur Gride appeared to have anticipated some--for the mention of this name producing an effect upon Ralph, or whatever effect it really did produce upon him, he permitted none to manifest itself, but calmly repeated the name several times, as if reflecting when and where he had heard it before.", "이 이름이 랄프에게 어떤 영향을 미칠 만한 이유가 무엇이었든, 아서 그라이드는 그런 게 좀 있으리라고 짐작한 듯했다. 그리고 그 이름이 실제로 그에게 어떤 영향을 미쳤든, 랄프는 그것을 조금도 드러내지 않았다. 다만 차분하게 그 이름을 몇 번 되뇌었다. 마치 그것을 전에 언제 어디서 들었는지 떠올려 보는 것처럼."]], [["‘Bray,’ said Ralph.", "\"브레이.\" 랄프가 말했다."], ["‘Bray--there was young Bray of--no, he never had a daughter.’", "\"브레이, 어디의 젊은 브레이라는 사람이 있었지, 아니, 그 사람한테는 딸이 없었어.\""]], [["‘You remember Bray?’ rejoined Arthur Gride.", "\"브레이 기억하지요?\" 아서 그라이드가 받았다."]], [["‘No,’ said Ralph, looking vacantly at him.", "\"아니.\" 랄프가 멍하니 그를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["‘Not Walter Bray!", "\"월터 브레이 말이오!"], ["The dashing man, who used his handsome wife so ill?’", "그 멋쟁이 사내, 잘난 마누라를 그렇게 못되게 다룬 자!\""]], [["‘If you seek to recall any particular dashing man to my recollection by such a trait as that,’ said Ralph, shrugging his shoulders, ‘I shall confound him with nine-tenths of the dashing men I have ever known.’", "\"그런 점을 들어서 어떤 특정한 멋쟁이 사내를 내 기억에 떠올리게 하려는 거라면,\" 랄프가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"내가 알았던 멋쟁이 사내들 열에 아홉과 헷갈릴 거야.\""]], [["‘Tut, tut.", "\"쯧, 쯧."], ["That Bray who is now in the Rules of the Bench,’ said old Arthur.", "지금 채무자 구역에 있는 그 브레이 말이오.\" 늙은 아서가 말했다."], ["‘You can’t have forgotten Bray.", "\"브레이를 잊었을 리가 없는데."], ["Both of us did business with him.", "우리 둘 다 그자랑 거래했잖소."], ["Why, he owes you money!’", "아니, 그자가 당신한테 돈을 빚졌잖소!\""]], [["‘Oh HIM!’ rejoined Ralph.", "\"오 그 사람!\" 랄프가 받았다."], ["‘Ay, ay.", "\"그래, 그래."], ["Now you speak.", "이제 말이 되는군."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["It’s HIS daughter, is it?’", "그 사람 딸이라, 그런가?\""]], [["Naturally as this was said, it was not said so naturally but that a kindred spirit like old Arthur Gride might have discerned a design upon the part of Ralph to lead him on to much more explicit statements and explanations than he would have volunteered, or that Ralph could in all likelihood have obtained by any other means.", "이 말은 아주 자연스럽게 나온 것 같았지만, 늙은 아서 그라이드 같은 동류의 인간이라면 알아챌 만큼은 자연스럽지 못했다. 랄프가 그를 부추겨, 그가 자진해서 털어놓았을 법한 것보다, 또는 랄프가 다른 어떤 수로 얻어 냈을 법한 것보다 훨씬 더 분명한 진술과 설명을 끌어내려는 속셈이었던 것이다."], ["Old Arthur, however, was so intent upon his own designs, that he suffered himself to be overreached, and had no suspicion but that his good friend was in earnest.", "그러나 늙은 아서는 자기 속셈에 너무 골몰해 있어서, 자기도 모르게 넘어가고 말았다. 좋은 친구가 진심으로 묻는 거라고만 믿어 의심치 않았다."]], [["‘I knew you couldn’t forget him, when you came to think for a moment,’ he said.", "\"잠깐만 생각해 보면 그자를 잊었을 리 없을 줄 알았소.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["‘You were right,’ answered Ralph.", "\"자네 말이 맞아.\" 랄프가 대답했다."], ["‘But old Arthur Gride and matrimony is a most anomalous conjunction of words; old Arthur Gride and dark eyes and eyelashes, and lips that to look at is to long to kiss, and clustering hair that he wants to play with, and waists that he wants to span, and little feet that don’t tread upon anything--old Arthur Gride and such things as these is more monstrous still; but old Arthur Gride marrying the daughter of a ruined “dashing man”", "\"하지만 늙은 아서 그라이드와 결혼이라니, 정말 이상하게 어울리지 않는 말의 조합이군. 늙은 아서 그라이드와 검은 눈동자며 속눈썹이며, 보기만 해도 입 맞추고 싶어지는 입술이며, 만지작거리고 싶은 굽이치는 머릿결이며, 팔로 감고 싶은 허리며, 아무것도 밟지 않는 작은 발이라니, 늙은 아서 그라이드와 그런 것들이라니 더더욱 괴이쩍어."], ["in the Rules of the Bench, is the most monstrous and incredible of all.", "그렇지만 늙은 아서 그라이드가 채무자 구역에 갇힌 망한 '멋쟁이 사내'의 딸과 결혼한다니, 그게 무엇보다도 괴이쩍고 믿기지 않는 일이야. 솔직히 말하자고, 아서 그라이드 친구."], ["Plainly, friend Arthur Gride, if you want any help from me in this business (which of course you do, or you would not be here), speak out, and to the purpose.", "자네가 이 일에 내 도움이 필요하다면, 당연히 그렇겠지, 안 그러면 여기 왔을 리 없으니까, 터놓고, 핵심을 말하게. 그리고 무엇보다도 이게 나한테 이득이 된다는 소리는 꺼내지도 마."], ["And, above all, don’t talk to me of its turning to my advantage, for I know it must turn to yours also, and to a good round tune too, or you would have no finger in such a pie as this.’", "자네한테도 이득이 된다는 걸 내가 아니까, 그것도 두둑이 말이야. 안 그러면 자네가 이런 일에 손가락 하나 까딱할 리 없거든.\""]], [["There was enough acerbity and sarcasm not only in the matter of Ralph’s speech, but in the tone of voice in which he uttered it, and the looks with which he eked it out, to have fired even the ancient usurer’s cold blood and flushed even his withered cheek.", "랄프의 말 내용에도, 그가 내뱉은 목소리 가락에도, 거기 곁들인 눈빛에도, 그 늙은 고리대금업자의 차가운 피마저 끓게 하고 메마른 뺨마저 붉히게 할 만큼 신랄함과 비아냥이 가득했다. 그러나 아서는 분노를 조금도 드러내지 않았다."], ["But he gave vent to no demonstration of anger, contenting himself with exclaiming as before, ‘What a man it is!’ and rolling his head from side to side, as if in unrestrained enjoyment of his freedom and drollery.", "그저 아까처럼 \"정말 대단한 양반이야!\" 하고 외치는 것으로 만족하며, 마음껏 자유와 익살을 즐기듯 머리를 좌우로 흔들었다."], ["Clearly observing, however, from the expression in Ralph’s features, that he had best come to the point as speedily as might be, he composed himself for more serious business, and entered upon the pith and marrow of his negotiation.", "하지만 랄프의 표정을 보고 되도록 빨리 본론으로 들어가는 게 상책임을 분명히 알아챈 그는, 좀 더 진지한 일을 위해 마음을 가다듬고는 협상의 알맹이로 들어갔다."]], [["First, he dwelt upon the fact that Madeline Bray was devoted to the support and maintenance, and was a slave to every wish, of her only parent, who had no other friend on earth; to which Ralph rejoined that he had heard something of the kind before, and that if she had known a little more of the world, she wouldn’t have been such a fool.", "먼저 그는 매들린 브레이가 이 세상에 다른 친구라고는 하나도 없는 단 하나뿐인 아비를 부양하고 보살피는 데 헌신하고 있으며, 그 아비의 모든 소원에 종처럼 매여 있다는 사실을 늘어놓았다. 그러자 랄프는 그런 비슷한 얘기는 전에도 들은 적이 있다고, 그 처녀가 세상 물정을 조금만 더 알았더라면 그런 바보짓은 안 했을 거라고 받아쳤다."]], [["Secondly, he enlarged upon the character of her father, arguing, that even taking it for granted that he loved her in return with the utmost affection of which he was capable, yet he loved himself a great deal better; which Ralph said it was quite unnecessary to say anything more about, as that was very natural, and probable enough.", "둘째로 그는 그 처녀 아비의 성격에 대해 늘어놓았다. 설령 그자가 제 딴에 할 수 있는 한 가장 깊은 애정으로 딸을 사랑한다 치더라도, 자기 자신은 그보다 훨씬 더 사랑한다고 따지고 들었다. 그러자 랄프는 그건 더 말할 필요도 없다고, 아주 당연하고 충분히 있을 법한 일이라고 했다."]], [["And, thirdly, old Arthur premised that the girl was a delicate and beautiful creature, and that he had really a hankering to have her for his wife.", "셋째로 늙은 아서는 그 처녀가 가냘프고 아름다운 사람이며, 자기가 정말로 그녀를 아내로 삼고 싶어 안달이 났다고 운을 뗐다."], ["To this Ralph deigned no other rejoinder than a harsh smile, and a glance at the shrivelled old creature before him, which were, however, sufficiently expressive.", "이에 랄프는 그저 모진 미소 한 번과, 자기 앞에 있는 쪼그라든 늙은이를 향한 눈길 한 번을 던질 뿐, 그 이상은 응수할 가치도 없다는 태도였다. 하지만 그것만으로도 뜻은 충분히 전해졌다."]], [["‘Now,’ said Gride, ‘for the little plan I have in my mind to bring this about; because, I haven’t offered myself even to the father yet, I should have told you.", "\"자.\" 그라이드가 말했다."], ["But that you have gathered already?", "\"이 일을 성사시키려고 내가 마음속에 품은 자그마한 계획에 대해 말하리다. 사실 나는 아직 그 아비한테도 청혼을 안 했소, 그걸 말했어야 했는데."], ["Ah!", "하지만 그건 벌써 눈치챘겠지?"], ["oh dear, oh dear, what an edged tool you are!’", "아! 이런, 이런, 당신은 정말 날 선 연장 같은 사람이야!\""]], [["‘Don’t play with me then,’ said Ralph impatiently.", "\"그럼 나를 가지고 놀지 말게.\" 랄프가 참지 못하고 말했다."], ["‘You know the proverb.’", "\"속담 알잖아.\""]], [["‘A reply always on the tip of his tongue!’ cried old Arthur, raising his hands and eyes in admiration.", "\"늘 대답이 혀끝에 준비돼 있다니까!\" 늙은 아서가 감탄하여 두 손과 두 눈을 치켜들며 외쳤다."], ["‘He is always prepared!", "\"이 양반은 늘 만반의 준비가 돼 있어!"], ["Oh dear, what a blessing to have such a ready wit, and so much ready money to back it!’ Then, suddenly changing his tone, he went on: ‘I have been backwards and forwards to Bray’s lodgings several times within the last six months.", "이런, 그렇게 잘 도는 머리에다, 그걸 뒷받침할 그렇게 넉넉한 돈까지 갖췄으니 얼마나 복인가!\" 그러더니 갑자기 어조를 바꿔 말을 이었다."], ["It is just half a year since I first saw this delicate morsel, and, oh dear, what a delicate morsel it is!", "\"지난 반년 동안 브레이의 거처를 여러 번 들락거렸소. 이 가냘픈 한 입을 처음 본 지 딱 반년이 됐지, 오 이런, 어찌나 가냘픈 한 입인지!"], ["But that is neither here nor there.", "하지만 그건 지금 중요한 게 아니고."], ["I am his detaining creditor for seventeen hundred pounds!’", "나는 천칠백 파운드 빚으로 그자를 잡아 두고 있는 채권자요!\""]], [["‘You talk as if you were the only detaining creditor,’ said Ralph, pulling out his pocket-book.", "\"마치 자네만 그자를 잡아 둔 채권자인 것처럼 말하는군.\" 랄프가 수첩을 꺼내며 말했다."], ["‘I am another for nine hundred and seventy-five pounds four and threepence.’", "\"나도 구백칠십오 파운드 사 실링 삼 펜스로 또 한 사람이야.\""]], [["‘The only other, Mr.", "\"오로지 당신 둘뿐이지요, 니클비 씨.\""], ["Nickleby,’ said old Arthur, eagerly.", "늙은 아서가 열을 올리며 말했다."], ["‘The only other.", "\"오로지 둘뿐이오."], ["Nobody else went to the expense of lodging a detainer, trusting to our holding him fast enough, I warrant you.", "다른 누구도 구금 신청을 하는 비용을 들이지 않았소, 우리가 그자를 단단히 붙잡아 두리라 믿었던 거지, 내 장담하리다."], ["We both fell into the same snare; oh dear, what a pitfall it was; it almost ruined me!", "우리 둘 다 똑같은 덫에 걸린 거요. 오 이런, 어찌나 깊은 함정이던지, 그 때문에 나는 거의 망할 뻔했소!"], ["And lent him our money upon bills, with only one name besides his own, which to be sure everybody supposed to be a good one, and was as negotiable as money, but which turned out you know how.", "어음으로 그자한테 돈을 빌려줬는데, 그자 이름 말고 보증인 이름은 딱 하나뿐이었소. 다들 틀림없이 믿을 만한 자라고 여겼고, 돈만큼이나 잘 유통됐지만, 결국 어찌 됐는지는 당신도 알잖소."], ["Just as we should have come upon him, he died insolvent.", "우리가 막 그자를 덮치려는 참에, 그자는 빈털터리로 죽어 버렸소."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["it went very nigh to ruin me, that loss did!’", "그 손해로 정말 망할 뻔했다니까!\""]], [["‘Go on with your scheme,’ said Ralph.", "\"자네 계획이나 마저 말하게.\" 랄프가 말했다."], ["‘It’s of no use raising the cry of our trade just now; there’s nobody to hear us!’", "\"지금 와서 우리 장사의 푸념을 늘어놓아 봐야 소용없어. 들어 줄 사람도 없잖나!\""]], [["‘It’s always as well to talk that way,’ returned old Arthur, with a chuckle, ‘whether there’s anybody to hear us or not.", "\"그렇게 말하는 게 언제든 좋은 법이지요.\" 늙은 아서가 낄낄거리며 받았다."], ["Practice makes perfect, you know.", "\"들을 사람이 있든 없든 말이오. 익숙해야 완벽해지는 거니까, 알잖소."], ["Now, if I offer myself to Bray as his son-in-law, upon one simple condition that the moment I am fast married he shall be quietly released, and have an allowance to live just t’other side the water like a gentleman (he can’t live long, for I have asked his doctor, and he declares that his complaint is one of the Heart and it is impossible), and if all the advantages of this condition are properly stated and dwelt upon to him, do you think he could resist me?", "자, 내가 브레이한테 사위로 나서서, 단 한 가지 간단한 조건을 거는 거요. 내가 무사히 결혼하는 순간 그자를 조용히 풀어 주고, 강 건너편에서 신사처럼 살 수 있게 생활비를 대 준다는 조건이지."], ["And if he could not resist ME, do you think his daughter could resist HIM?", "그자는 오래 못 살아, 내가 의사한테 물어봤는데 그자 병이 심장병이라 가망이 없다고 딱 잘라 말하더군. 그리고 이 조건의 온갖 이점을 그자한테 제대로 짚어 가며 늘어놓으면, 그자가 나를 거절할 수 있겠소?"], ["Shouldn’t I have her Mrs. Arthur Gride--pretty Mrs.", "그리고 그자가 나를 거절하지 못한다면, 그 딸이 그자를 거역할 수 있겠소?"], ["Arthur Gride--a tit-bit--a dainty chick--shouldn’t I have her Mrs. Arthur Gride in a week, a month, a day--any time I chose to name?’", "그러면 그 처녀를 아서 그라이드 부인으로, 예쁜 아서 그라이드 부인으로, 별미 같은, 앙증맞은 새끼 새 같은, 그 처녀를 아서 그라이드 부인으로 만들 수 있지 않겠소, 일주일이든 한 달이든 하루든, 내가 정하는 어느 때든 말이오?\""]], [["‘Go on,’ said Ralph, nodding his head deliberately, and speaking in a tone whose studied coldness presented a strange contrast to the rapturous squeak to which his friend had gradually mounted.", "\"계속하게.\" 랄프가 천천히 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["‘Go on.", "일부러 꾸민 듯한 그 차가운 어조는, 친구가 차츰차츰 끌어올린 황홀한 새된 소리와 묘한 대조를 이루었다."], ["You didn’t come here to ask me that.’", "\"계속해. 자네가 나한테 그걸 물으러 온 건 아니잖아.\""]], [["‘Oh dear, how you talk!’ cried old Arthur, edging himself closer still to Ralph.", "\"오 이런, 말씀 한번 잘하시네!\" 늙은 아서가 랄프 쪽으로 몸을 더 바짝 붙이며 외쳤다."], ["‘Of course I didn’t, I don’t pretend I did!", "\"물론 아니지요, 그런 척도 안 하리다!"], ["I came to ask what you would take from me, if I prospered with the father, for this debt of yours.", "내가 온 건, 만약 그 아비한테서 일이 잘 풀린다면, 당신 그 빚을 두고 내게서 얼마를 받겠는지 물으려는 거요. 일 파운드당 오 실링?"], ["Five shillings in the pound, six and-eightpence, ten shillings?", "육 실링 팔 펜스? 십 실링?"], ["I WOULD go as far as ten for such a friend as you, we have always been on such good terms, but you won’t be so hard upon me as that, I know.", "당신처럼 좋은 친구라면 십 실링까지도 내겠소, 우리가 늘 그렇게 좋은 사이였으니까. 하지만 당신은 그렇게까지 모질게 굴진 않을 거야, 나도 알아요."], ["Now, will you?’", "자, 그러시겠지요?\""]], [["‘There’s something more to be told,’ said Ralph, as stony and immovable as ever.", "\"아직 할 말이 더 남았어.\" 랄프가 여전히 돌처럼 꿈쩍도 않고 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, yes, there is, but you won’t give me time,’ returned Arthur Gride.", "\"그래요, 그래요, 그렇소만, 시간을 안 주시잖소.\" 아서 그라이드가 받았다."], ["‘I want a backer in this matter; one who can talk, and urge, and press a point, which you can do as no man can.", "\"이 일에 나는 뒤를 봐줄 사람이 필요하오. 말을 잘하고, 밀어붙이고, 요점을 다그칠 수 있는 사람 말이오, 당신만큼 그걸 잘하는 사람이 없거든."], ["I can’t do that, for I am a poor, timid, nervous creature.", "나는 그걸 못 해, 나는 가엾고, 겁 많고, 신경 약한 인간이니까."], ["Now, if you get a good composition for this debt, which you long ago gave up for lost, you’ll stand my friend, and help me.", "자, 당신이 오래전에 떼인 셈 친 이 빚을 두고 두둑한 합의금을 받게 된다면, 당신은 내 친구로서 나를 도와주겠지요."], ["Won’t you?’", "안 그러겠소?\""]], [["‘There’s something more,’ said Ralph.", "\"할 말이 더 있어.\" 랄프가 말했다."]], [["‘No, no, indeed,’ cried Arthur Gride.", "\"아니오, 아니, 없소.\" 아서 그라이드가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Yes, yes, indeed.", "\"그래, 있어."], ["I tell you yes,’ said Ralph.", "있다니까.\" 랄프가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh!’ returned old Arthur feigning to be suddenly enlightened.", "\"오!\" 늙은 아서가 갑자기 깨달은 척하며 받았다."], ["‘You mean something more, as concerns myself and my intention.", "\"내 일과 내 속셈에 관해 할 말이 더 있다는 거구려."], ["Ay, surely, surely.", "아, 그렇지, 그렇고말고."], ["Shall I mention that?’", "그걸 말해야 하오?\""]], [["‘I think you had better,’ rejoined Ralph, drily.", "\"그러는 게 낫겠지.\" 랄프가 무뚝뚝하게 받았다."]], [["‘I didn’t like to trouble you with that, because I supposed your interest would cease with your own concern in the affair,’ said Arthur Gride.", "\"그 얘기로 당신을 번거롭게 하고 싶지 않았소, 당신 관심이야 당신 몫이 걸린 일에서 끝날 거라고 여겼으니까.\" 아서 그라이드가 말했다."], ["‘That’s kind of you to ask.", "\"물어봐 주다니 친절하기도 하지."], ["Oh dear, how very kind of you!", "오 이런, 어찌나 친절한지!"], ["Why, supposing I had a knowledge of some property--some little property--very little--to which this pretty chick was entitled; which nobody does or can know of at this time, but which her husband could sweep into his pouch, if he knew as much as I do, would that account for--’", "그러니까, 만약 내가 어떤 재산을, 자그마한 재산을, 아주 자그마한 재산을 알고 있다고 칩시다. 이 예쁜 새끼 새한테 권리가 있는 재산인데, 지금은 아무도 모르고 알 수도 없지만, 그 남편이 나만큼 알면 제 주머니로 싹 쓸어 담을 수 있는 그런 재산 말이오, 그러면 설명이 되지 않겠소?\""]], [["‘For the whole proceeding,’ rejoined Ralph, abruptly.", "\"이 일 전체가 다 설명이 되지.\" 랄프가 불쑥 받았다."], ["‘Now, let me turn this matter over, and consider what I ought to have if I should help you to success.’", "\"자, 이 문제를 한번 곰곰이 따져 보고, 내가 자네 성공을 도와준다면 얼마를 받아야 할지 생각해 봐야겠어.\""]], [["‘But don’t be hard,’ cried old Arthur, raising his hands with an imploring gesture, and speaking, in a tremulous voice.", "\"하지만 모질게 굴지 마시오.\" 늙은 아서가 애원하는 몸짓으로 두 손을 치켜들고 떨리는 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["‘Don’t be too hard upon me.", "\"나한테 너무 모질게 굴지 마."], ["It’s a very small property, it is indeed.", "그건 아주 작은 재산이오, 정말로요."], ["Say the ten shillings, and we’ll close the bargain.", "십 실링으로 합시다, 그러고 흥정을 끝냅시다."], ["It’s more than I ought to give, but you’re so kind--shall we say the ten?", "그건 내가 줘야 할 것보다 많은 거지만, 당신이 워낙 친절하니까, 십 실링으로 할까요?"], ["Do now, do.’", "그럽시다, 그래요.\""]], [["Ralph took no notice of these supplications, but sat for three or four minutes in a brown study, looking thoughtfully at the person from whom they proceeded.", "랄프는 이 애원에 아무런 반응도 보이지 않고, 삼사 분 동안 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채, 그 애원을 늘어놓는 사람을 곰곰이 바라보며 앉아 있었다."], ["After sufficient cogitation he broke silence, and it certainly could not be objected that he used any needless circumlocution, or failed to speak directly to the purpose.", "충분히 머리를 굴린 뒤 그가 침묵을 깼는데, 쓸데없이 빙빙 돌려 말한다거나 본론을 곧장 짚지 못한다고 트집 잡힐 데는 분명 없었다."]], [["‘If you married this girl without me,’ said Ralph, ‘you must pay my debt in full, because you couldn’t set her father free otherwise.", "\"자네가 나 없이 이 처녀와 결혼한다면,\" 랄프가 말했다."], ["It’s plain, then, that I must have the whole amount, clear of all deduction or incumbrance, or I should lose from being honoured with your confidence, instead of gaining by it.", "\"자네는 내 빚을 전액 갚아야 해."], ["That’s the first article of the treaty.", "안 그러면 그 아비를 풀어 줄 수 없을 테니까."], ["For the second, I shall stipulate that for my trouble in negotiation and persuasion, and helping you to this fortune, I have five hundred pounds.", "그러니 분명한 건, 나는 전액을 받아야 한다는 거야, 한 푼도 깎거나 떼이는 일 없이 말이지. 안 그러면 나는 자네 비밀을 들어 준 덕에 손해를 보게 되는 셈이야, 이득을 보기는커녕."], ["That’s very little, because you have the ripe lips, and the clustering hair, and what not, all to yourself.", "그게 첫 번째 조항이야. 두 번째로, 이 협상하고 설득하고, 자네가 이 재산을 손에 넣게 도운 수고에 대해, 나는 오백 파운드를 받겠어."], ["For the third and last article, I require that you execute a bond to me, this day, binding yourself in the payment of these two sums, before noon of the day of your marriage with Madeline Bray.", "그건 아주 적은 거지, 자네는 무르익은 입술이며 굽이치는 머릿결이며 그런 것들을 죄다 독차지하게 되니까 말이야. 세 번째이자 마지막 조항으로, 자네가 오늘 당장 나한테 증서를 써 줘야 해."], ["You have told me I can urge and press a point.", "매들린 브레이와 결혼하는 날 정오 전에 이 두 금액을 지불하겠다는 증서 말이야."], ["I press this one, and will take nothing less than these terms.", "자네가 나더러 요점을 다그치고 밀어붙일 수 있다고 했지. 나는 이걸 다그치겠어, 그리고 이 조건 말고는 한 푼도 깎아 주지 않겠어."], ["Accept them if you like.", "좋으면 받아들이고."], ["If not, marry her without me if you can.", "싫으면 자네 능력껏 나 없이 결혼하든가."], ["I shall still get my debt.’", "어쨌든 내 빚은 받아 낼 테니까.\""]], [["To all entreaties, protestations, and offers of compromise between his own proposals and those which Arthur Gride had first suggested, Ralph was deaf as an adder.", "온갖 간청에도, 항변에도, 자기가 처음 내놓은 제안과 랄프의 제안 사이에서 절충을 보자는 제의에도, 랄프는 독사처럼 귀를 막았다."], ["He would enter into no further discussion of the subject, and while old Arthur dilated upon the enormity of his demands and proposed modifications of them, approaching by degrees nearer and nearer to the terms he resisted, sat perfectly mute, looking with an air of quiet abstraction over the entries and papers in his pocket-book.", "그는 이 문제로 더 이상 논의하려 들지 않았다. 그리고 늙은 아서가 그 요구가 얼마나 터무니없는지 늘어놓으며 수정안을 내놓고, 차츰차츰 자기가 버티던 조건에 가까이 다가오는 동안에도, 랄프는 완전히 입을 다문 채 조용히 딴생각에 잠긴 표정으로 수첩에 적힌 항목과 서류만 들여다보고 있었다."], ["Finding that it was impossible to make any impression upon his staunch friend, Arthur Gride, who had prepared himself for some such result before he came, consented with a heavy heart to the proposed treaty, and upon the spot filled up the bond required (Ralph kept such instruments handy), after exacting the condition that Mr.", "자기의 흔들림 없는 친구에게는 도무지 통하지 않는다는 걸 알자, 오기 전에 이미 이런 결과를 어느 정도 각오하고 있던 아서 그라이드는 무거운 마음으로 그 협상에 동의했다. 그러고는 그 자리에서 요구받은 증서를 채워 넣었다."], ["Nickleby should accompany him to Bray’s lodgings that very hour, and open the negotiation at once, should circumstances appear auspicious and favourable to their designs.", "랄프는 그런 증서를 늘 가까이 두고 있었던 것이다. 다만 한 가지 조건을 내걸었는데, 니클비 씨가 바로 지금 자기와 함께 브레이의 거처로 가서, 정황이 좋고 그들 계획에 유리해 보이면 곧장 협상을 시작해 달라는 것이었다."]], [["In pursuance of this last understanding the worthy gentlemen went out together shortly afterwards, and Newman Noggs emerged, bottle in hand, from the cupboard, out of the upper door of which, at the imminent risk of detection, he had more than once thrust his red nose when such parts of the subject were under discussion as interested him most.", "이 마지막 합의에 따라, 두 훌륭한 신사는 곧 함께 밖으로 나갔다. 그러자 뉴먼 노그스가 병을 손에 든 채 벽장에서 나왔다. 그는 자기가 가장 관심 있는 대목이 논의될 때면, 들킬 위험을 무릅쓰고 벽장 위쪽 문틈으로 붉은 코를 한 번 이상 쑥 내밀었던 터였다."]], [["‘I have no appetite now,’ said Newman, putting the flask in his pocket.", "\"이젠 입맛이 없네.\" 뉴먼이 병을 주머니에 넣으며 말했다."], ["‘I’ve had MY dinner.’", "\"내 점심은 다 먹었으니까.\""]], [["Having delivered this observation in a very grievous and doleful tone, Newman reached the door in one long limp, and came back again in another.", "이 말을 아주 비통하고 구슬픈 어조로 내뱉고는, 뉴먼은 절름거리며 한걸음에 문까지 갔다가, 다시 한걸음에 돌아왔다."]], [["‘I don’t know who she may be, or what she may be,’ he said: ‘but I pity her with all my heart and soul; and I can’t help her, nor can I any of the people against whom a hundred tricks, but none so vile as this, are plotted every day!", "\"그 처녀가 누군지, 어떤 사람인지는 모르겠어.\" 그가 말했다."], ["Well, that adds to my pain, but not to theirs.", "\"하지만 온 마음과 온 영혼으로 그 처녀가 가엾어. 그런데도 나는 그 처녀를 도울 수가 없어."], ["The thing is no worse because I know it, and it tortures me as well as them.", "날마다 수백 가지 음모에 걸려드는 사람들 가운데 누구도 도울 수가 없지, 이렇게 비열한 음모는 또 없겠지만! 그래, 그게 내 고통만 더할 뿐, 그 사람들 고통을 더는 건 아니야."], ["Gride and Nickleby!", "내가 안다고 해서 일이 더 나빠지는 것도 아니고, 그저 그 사람들 못지않게 나까지 괴로울 뿐이지."], ["Good pair for a curricle.", "그라이드와 니클비라! 쌍두마차에 딱 맞는 한 쌍이군."], ["Oh roguery!", "오 악당 놈들!"], ["roguery!", "악당 놈들!"], ["roguery!’", "악당 놈들!\""]], [["With these reflections, and a very hard knock on the crown of his unfortunate hat at each repetition of the last word, Newman Noggs, whose brain was a little muddled by so much of the contents of the pocket-pistol as had found their way there during his recent concealment, went forth to seek such consolation as might be derivable from the beef and greens of some cheap eating-house.", "이런 생각들과 함께, 마지막 말을 되풀이할 때마다 가엾은 모자 꼭대기를 호되게 한 대씩 내리치면서, 뉴먼 노그스는 길을 나섰다. 방금 숨어 있는 동안 포켓 권총의 내용물이 적잖이 그의 머릿속까지 흘러든 탓에 머리가 조금 어질어질했다. 그는 어느 싸구려 식당의 소고기와 채소에서나마 얼마간 위안을 얻어 보려고 발걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["Meanwhile the two plotters had betaken themselves to the same house whither Nicholas had repaired for the first time but a few mornings before, and having obtained access to Mr.", "한편 두 음모꾼은, 불과 며칠 전 아침 니콜라스가 처음 찾아갔던 바로 그 집으로 향했다. 그리고 브레이 씨를 만나는 데 성공했고, 마침 그 딸은 외출하고 없었다."], ["Bray, and found his daughter from home, had by a train of the most masterly approaches that Ralph’s utmost skill could frame, at length laid open the real object of their visit.", "랄프의 온갖 재주를 짜낸 더없이 노련한 접근의 과정을 거쳐, 마침내 그들은 찾아온 진짜 목적을 털어놓았다."]], [["‘There he sits, Mr.", "\"저기 앉아 있는 사람입니다, 브레이 씨.\""], ["Bray,’ said Ralph, as the invalid, not yet recovered from his surprise, reclined in his chair, looking alternately at him and Arthur Gride.", "랄프가 말했다. 아직 놀라움이 가시지 않은 병자는 의자에 기댄 채, 랄프와 아서 그라이드를 번갈아 바라보고 있었다."], ["‘What if he has had the ill-fortune to be one cause of your detention in this place?", "\"저 사람이 불운하게도 당신이 이곳에 갇힌 한 가지 원인이었다 한들 어떻습니까? 나도 또 다른 원인이었지요."], ["I have been another; men must live; you are too much a man of the world not to see that in its true light.", "사람은 먹고살아야 하니까요. 당신은 그걸 제대로 볼 줄 모를 만큼 세상 물정에 어두운 분이 아니잖습니까."], ["We offer the best reparation in our power.", "우리는 우리가 할 수 있는 최선의 보상을 내놓는 겁니다."], ["Reparation!", "보상이라!"], ["Here is an offer of marriage, that many a titled father would leap at, for his child.", "여기, 작위 가진 아비라도 제 자식을 위해서라면 덥석 물 만한 청혼이 있습니다."], ["Mr Arthur Gride, with the fortune of a prince.", "아서 그라이드 씨, 왕자만 한 재산을 가진 분이지요."], ["Think what a haul it is!’", "얼마나 큰 횡재인지 생각해 보십시오!\""]], [["‘My daughter, sir,’ returned Bray, haughtily, ‘as I have brought her up, would be a rich recompense for the largest fortune that a man could bestow in exchange for her hand.’", "\"내 딸은요, 선생.\" 브레이가 거만하게 받았다. \"내가 키운 그대로, 어떤 남자가 그 애 손을 얻는 대가로 내놓을 수 있는 가장 큰 재산에도 충분히 값하고도 남는 보답이오.\""]], [["‘Precisely what I told you,’ said the artful Ralph, turning to his friend, old Arthur.", "\"바로 그게 내가 당신한테 한 말입니다.\" 교활한 랄프가 친구인 늙은 아서 쪽을 돌아보며 말했다."], ["‘Precisely what made me consider the thing so fair and easy.", "\"바로 그래서 이 일이 그토록 공평하고 수월하다고 본 거지요."], ["There is no obligation on either side.", "어느 쪽에도 빚지는 게 없습니다."], ["You have money, and Miss Madeline has beauty and worth.", "당신은 돈이 있고, 매들린 양은 아름다움과 인품이 있지요."], ["She has youth, you have money.", "그쪽은 젊음이 있고, 당신은 돈이 있습니다."], ["She has not money, you have not youth.", "그쪽은 돈이 없고, 당신은 젊음이 없지요."], ["Tit for tat, quits, a match of Heaven’s own making!’", "주고받기, 비기는 셈, 하늘이 손수 맺어 준 짝입니다!\""]], [["‘Matches are made in Heaven, they say,’ added Arthur Gride, leering hideously at the father-in-law he wanted.", "\"짝은 하늘에서 맺어진다고들 하지요.\" 아서 그라이드가 자기가 탐내는 장인을 향해 흉측하게 곁눈질하며 덧붙였다."], ["‘If we are married, it will be destiny, according to that.’", "\"우리가 결혼한다면, 그 말대로 운명이겠지요.\""]], [["‘Then think, Mr.", "\"그러니 생각해 보십시오, 브레이 씨.\""], ["Bray,’ said Ralph, hastily substituting for this argument considerations more nearly allied to earth, ‘think what a stake is involved in the acceptance or rejection of these proposals of my friend.’", "랄프가 이 논리를 얼른 좀 더 땅에 발 붙인 고려로 바꾸며 말했다. \"내 친구의 이 제안을 받아들이느냐 물리치느냐에 무엇이 걸려 있는지 생각해 보세요.\""]], [["‘How can I accept or reject,’ interrupted Mr.", "\"내가 어떻게 받아들이거나 물리친단 말이오.\""], ["Bray, with an irritable consciousness that it really rested with him to decide.", "정말로 결정권이 자기한테 있다는 걸 짜증스럽게 의식하며 브레이 씨가 끼어들었다."], ["‘It is for my daughter to accept or reject; it is for my daughter.", "\"받아들이거나 물리치는 건 내 딸의 몫이오. 내 딸의 몫이라고."], ["You know that.’", "그건 당신도 알잖소.\""]], [["‘True,’ said Ralph, emphatically; ‘but you have still the power to advise; to state the reasons for and against; to hint a wish.’", "\"맞습니다.\" 랄프가 힘주어 말했다. \"하지만 당신한테는 여전히 조언할 힘이 있지요. 찬성과 반대의 이유를 늘어놓고, 바라는 바를 슬쩍 비칠 힘이요.\""]], [["‘To hint a wish, sir!’ returned the debtor, proud and mean by turns, and selfish at all times.", "\"바라는 바를 슬쩍 비치라고요, 선생!\" 채무자가 받아쳤다."], ["‘I am her father, am I not?", "그는 자존심이 셌다가 비굴해졌다가 했지만, 언제나 이기적이었다."], ["Why should I hint, and beat about the bush?", "\"내가 그 애 아비요, 안 그렇소?"], ["Do you suppose, like her mother’s friends and my enemies--a curse upon them all!--that there is anything in what she has done for me but duty, sir, but duty?", "내가 왜 슬쩍 비치고 빙빙 돌려 말해야 하오? 당신은 그 애 어미의 친척들, 내 적들 말이오, 그것들 다 빌어먹을!"], ["Or do you think that my having been unfortunate is a sufficient reason why our relative positions should be changed, and that she should command and I should obey?", "그것들처럼 그 애가 나한테 해 준 일이 의무가 아닌 다른 무엇이라고 생각하오, 선생, 그저 의무지! 아니면 내가 불운을 겪었으니 우리 부녀의 처지가 뒤바뀌어, 그 애가 명령하고 내가 따라야 마땅하다고 생각하오?"], ["Hint a wish, too!", "바라는 바를 비치라니!"], ["Perhaps you think, because you see me in this place and scarcely able to leave this chair without assistance, that I am some broken-spirited dependent creature, without the courage or power to do what I may think best for my own child.", "당신은 아마, 내가 이런 데 있고 부축 없이는 이 의자에서 일어나기도 힘든 걸 보고는, 내가 무슨 기죽은 식객 신세라서, 제 자식을 위해 내가 최선이라 여기는 일을 할 용기도 힘도 없는 줄 아는가 보군. 바라는 바를 비칠 힘이라니!"], ["Still the power to hint a wish! I hope so!’", "그 정도야 있고말고!\""]], [["‘Pardon me,’ returned Ralph, who thoroughly knew his man, and had taken his ground accordingly; ‘you do not hear me out. I was about to say that your hinting a wish, even hinting a wish, would surely be equivalent to commanding.’", "\"용서하시오.\" 랄프가 받았다. 그는 자기가 상대하는 사람을 속속들이 알았고, 그에 맞춰 입지를 정해 둔 터였다. \"내 말을 끝까지 안 들으시는군요. 내가 하려던 말은, 당신이 바라는 바를 비치는 것, 그저 비치기만 해도, 그건 분명 명령하는 것이나 다름없으리란 거였습니다.\""]], [["‘Why, of course it would,’ retorted Mr.", "\"아니, 그야 당연하지.\""], ["Bray, in an exasperated tone.", "브레이 씨가 격앙된 어조로 받아쳤다."], ["‘If you don’t happen to have heard of the time, sir, I tell you that there was a time, when I carried every point in triumph against her mother’s whole family, although they had power and wealth on their side, by my will alone.’", "\"혹시 당신이 그 일을 못 들었다면 말해 주리다, 선생, 한때는 말이오, 그 애 어미네 온 집안이 권력과 재산을 등에 업었어도, 내가 내 의지 하나만으로 그것들을 상대로 사사건건 보란 듯이 이긴 시절이 있었소.\""]], [["‘Still,’ rejoined Ralph, as mildly as his nature would allow him, ‘you have not heard me out.", "\"그래도,\" 랄프가 자기 천성이 허락하는 한 부드럽게 받았다."], ["You are a man yet qualified to shine in society, with many years of life before you; that is, if you lived in freer air, and under brighter skies, and chose your own companions.", "\"내 말을 끝까지 안 들으셨소. 당신은 아직 사교계에서 빛날 자격이 있는 분이오, 앞으로 살날도 많고요."], ["Gaiety is your element, you have shone in it before.", "그러니까, 더 자유로운 공기 속에서, 더 밝은 하늘 아래에서, 직접 동무를 골라 가며 산다면 말입니다."], ["Fashion and freedom for you.", "흥겨움이 당신 본바탕이지요, 전에도 거기서 빛났잖습니까."], ["France, and an annuity that would support you there in luxury, would give you a new lease of life, would transfer you to a new existence.", "당신한테는 멋과 자유가 어울려요. 프랑스, 그리고 거기서 호사스럽게 지낼 수 있는 연금이라면, 당신한테 새 삶을 빌려줄 거고, 당신을 새로운 인생으로 옮겨 줄 겁니다."], ["The town rang with your expensive pleasures once, and you could blaze up on a new scene again, profiting by experience, and living a little at others’ cost, instead of letting others live at yours.", "한때 당신의 호화로운 즐길 거리로 온 도시가 떠들썩했지요, 그러니 새 무대에서 다시 한번 화려하게 빛날 수도 있어요. 경험을 밑천 삼아, 남들이 당신 돈으로 살게 두는 대신, 남들 돈으로 좀 살아 가면서 말입니다."], ["What is there on the reverse side of the picture?", "그 반대편 그림에는 뭐가 있습니까?"], ["What is there?", "뭐가 있냐고요?"], ["I don’t know which is the nearest churchyard, but a gravestone there, wherever it is, and a date, perhaps two years hence, perhaps twenty.", "어느 교회 묘지가 제일 가까운지는 모르겠으나, 거기 어딘가에 묘비 하나, 그리고 날짜 하나, 어쩌면 이 년 뒤, 어쩌면 이십 년 뒤겠지요."], ["That’s all.’", "그게 전부입니다.\""]], [["Mr. Bray rested his elbow on the arm of his chair, and shaded his face with his hand.", "브레이 씨는 의자 팔걸이에 팔꿈치를 괴고, 한 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다."]], [["‘I speak plainly,’ said Ralph, sitting down beside him, ‘because I feel strongly.", "\"내가 이렇게 터놓고 말하는 건,\" 랄프가 그의 곁에 앉으며 말했다."], ["It’s my interest that you should marry your daughter to my friend Gride, because then he sees me paid--in part, that is.", "\"마음이 절실하기 때문입니다. 당신이 딸을 내 친구 그라이드와 결혼시키는 게 내 이익이오, 그래야 저 사람이 내 빚을 갚아 줄 테니까요, 일부라도 말이죠."], ["I don’t disguise it.", "숨기지 않겠습니다."], ["I acknowledge it openly.", "드러내 놓고 인정해요."], ["But what interest have you in recommending her to such a step?", "하지만 당신은 딸한테 그런 일을 권하는 데 무슨 이익이 있습니까?"], ["Keep that in view.", "그걸 잘 새겨 두세요."], ["She might object, remonstrate, shed tears, talk of his being too old, and plead that her life would be rendered miserable.", "따님은 싫다고 할 수도 있고, 항변할 수도 있고, 눈물을 흘릴 수도 있고, 저 사람이 너무 늙었다고 할 수도, 제 인생이 비참해질 거라고 호소할 수도 있겠지요."], ["But what is it now?’", "하지만 지금은 어떻습니까?\""]], [["Several slight gestures on the part of the invalid showed that these arguments were no more lost upon him, than the smallest iota of his demeanour was upon Ralph.", "병자가 보인 몇 가지 자그마한 몸짓은, 이 논리가 그에게 헛되지 않았음을 드러냈다. 그의 그 자그마한 거동 하나하나가 랄프에게 헛되지 않았듯이 말이다."]], [["‘What is it now, I say,’ pursued the wily usurer, ‘or what has it a chance of being?", "\"지금은 어떠냐 이겁니다.\" 교활한 고리대금업자가 말을 이었다."], ["If you died, indeed, the people you hate would make her happy.", "\"아니면 앞으로 어떻게 될 가망이 있느냐고요? 정말이지 당신이 죽으면, 당신이 미워하는 그 사람들이 따님을 행복하게 해 주겠지요."], ["But can you bear the thought of that?’", "그런데 당신은 그 생각을 견딜 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["‘No!’ returned Bray, urged by a vindictive impulse he could not repress.", "\"못 하지!\" 브레이가 억누를 수 없는 앙심에 떠밀려 받았다."]], [["‘I should imagine not, indeed!’ said Ralph, quietly.", "\"못 하시겠지요, 정말로!\" 랄프가 조용히 말했다."], ["‘If she profits by anybody’s death,’ this was said in a lower tone, ‘let it be by her husband’s.", "\"따님이 누군가의 죽음으로 덕을 본다면,\" 이 말은 좀 더 낮은 어조로 했다."], ["Don’t let her have to look back to yours, as the event from which to date a happier life.", "\"그건 남편의 죽음이게 하시오. 따님이 당신의 죽음을 돌아보며, 그때부터 더 행복한 삶이 시작됐다고 헤아리게 만들지 마세요."], ["Where is the objection?", "반대할 게 어디 있습니까?"], ["Let me hear it stated.", "어디 한번 들어 봅시다."], ["What is it?", "뭡니까?"], ["That her suitor is an old man?", "구혼자가 늙은이라서?"], ["Why, how often do men of family and fortune, who haven’t your excuse, but have all the means and superfluities of life within their reach, how often do they marry their daughters to old men, or (worse still) to young men without heads or hearts, to tickle some idle vanity, strengthen some family interest, or secure some seat in Parliament!", "아니, 가문 좋고 재산 있는 사람들, 당신 같은 핑계도 없이 삶의 온갖 수단과 사치를 다 누리는 사람들이, 얼마나 자주 제 딸을 늙은이한테, 아니 (그보다 더한 건) 머리도 마음도 없는 젊은 놈한테 시집보내는데요. 하찮은 허영을 채우려고, 어느 집안과의 이해관계를 다지려고, 의회 의석 하나를 챙기려고 말입니다!"], ["Judge for her, sir, judge for her.", "따님을 위해 판단하세요, 선생, 따님을 위해 판단하시라고요."], ["You must know best, and she will live to thank you.’", "당신이 가장 잘 알 테고, 따님은 살아서 당신한테 고마워할 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["hush!’ cried Mr.", "쉿!\""], ["Bray, suddenly starting up, and covering Ralph’s mouth with his trembling hand.", "브레이 씨가 갑자기 벌떡 일어나 떨리는 손으로 랄프의 입을 막으며 외쳤다."], ["‘I hear her at the door!’", "\"그 애가 문간에 와 있는 소리가 들려요!\""]], [["There was a gleam of conscience in the shame and terror of this hasty action, which, in one short moment, tore the thin covering of sophistry from the cruel design, and laid it bare in all its meanness and heartless deformity. The father fell into his chair pale and trembling; Arthur Gride plucked and fumbled at his hat, and durst not raise his eyes from the floor; even Ralph crouched for the moment like a beaten hound, cowed by the presence of one young innocent girl!", "이 황급한 행동의 부끄러움과 두려움 속에는 양심의 번득임이 있었다. 그것은 한순간에, 그 잔인한 계략을 덮고 있던 얇은 궤변의 껍질을 벗겨 내, 그 비열함과 무정한 추악함을 온전히 드러내고 말았다. 아비는 창백하게 떨며 의자에 주저앉았고, 아서 그라이드는 제 모자를 만지작거리며 잡아당길 뿐, 감히 바닥에서 눈을 들지 못했다. 랄프조차 그 순간만큼은 두들겨 맞은 사냥개처럼 움츠러들었다. 순결한 어린 처녀 하나 앞에서 기가 죽은 것이다!"]], [["The effect was almost as brief as sudden.", "그 효과는 갑작스러웠던 만큼이나 거의 순식간에 끝났다."], ["Ralph was the first to recover himself, and observing Madeline’s looks of alarm, entreated the poor girl to be composed, assuring her that there was no cause for fear.", "랄프가 가장 먼저 정신을 차렸다. 매들린의 놀란 표정을 보고는, 그 가엾은 처녀에게 마음을 가라앉히라고, 두려워할 까닭은 전혀 없다고 안심시켜 주었다."]], [["‘A sudden spasm,’ said Ralph, glancing at Mr.", "\"갑작스러운 경련입니다.\" 랄프가 브레이 씨를 흘끗 보며 말했다."], ["Bray. ‘He is quite well now.’", "\"이젠 아주 멀쩡하세요.\""]], [["It might have moved a very hard and worldly heart to see the young and beautiful creature, whose certain misery they had been contriving but a minute before, throw her arms about her father’s neck, and pour forth words of tender sympathy and love, the sweetest a father’s ear can know, or child’s lips form.", "불과 일 분 전까지 그들이 확실한 불행을 꾸미고 있던 그 젊고 아름다운 사람이, 아비의 목을 끌어안고 다정한 위로와 사랑의 말을 쏟아 내는 모습은, 아비의 귀가 들을 수 있는, 자식의 입이 빚어낼 수 있는 가장 달콤한 말이었으니, 아무리 모질고 속물적인 마음이라도 움직일 만했다."], ["But Ralph looked coldly on; and Arthur Gride, whose bleared eyes gloated only over the outward beauties, and were blind to the spirit which reigned within, evinced--a fantastic kind of warmth certainly, but not exactly that kind of warmth of feeling which the contemplation of virtue usually inspires.", "그러나 랄프는 차갑게 지켜보기만 했다. 그리고 흐릿한 눈으로 겉모습의 아름다움만 탐할 뿐 그 안에 깃든 정신은 보지 못하는 아서 그라이드는, 분명 어떤 야릇한 종류의 열기를 드러내긴 했지만, 그건 미덕을 바라볼 때 흔히 솟아나는 그런 종류의 따뜻한 감정은 결코 아니었다."]], [["‘Madeline,’ said her father, gently disengaging himself, ‘it was nothing.’", "\"매들린.\" 아비가 딸을 부드럽게 떼어 놓으며 말했다. \"아무것도 아니란다.\""]], [["‘But you had that spasm yesterday, and it is terrible to see you in such pain.", "\"하지만 어제도 그 경련을 일으키셨잖아요, 그렇게 아파하시는 걸 보면 저는 너무 무서워요."], ["Can I do nothing for you?’", "제가 해 드릴 일이 정말 없어요?\""]], [["‘Nothing just now.", "\"지금은 없단다."], ["Here are two gentlemen, Madeline, one of whom you have seen before.", "여기 두 신사분이 와 계신다, 매들린, 한 분은 전에 본 적이 있지."], ["She used to say,’ added Mr.", "저 애가 늘 그랬소,\""], ["Bray, addressing Arthur Gride, ‘that the sight of you always made me worse.", "브레이 씨가 아서 그라이드를 향해 말을 덧붙였다. \"당신만 보면 내가 더 안 좋아진다고 말이오."], ["That was natural, knowing what she did, and only what she did, of our connection and its results.", "우리 사이와 그 결과에 대해 그 애가 아는 것만, 딱 그것만 알았으니 그럴 만도 했지."], ["Well, well.", "뭐, 그래."], ["Perhaps she may change her mind on that point; girls have leave to change their minds, you know.", "어쩌면 그 점에 대해 마음을 바꿀지도 모르오. 여자아이들은 마음을 바꿔도 되는 거니까, 알잖소."], ["You are very tired, my dear.’", "너 많이 지쳤구나, 얘야.\""]], [["‘I am not, indeed.’", "\"안 지쳤어요, 정말요.\""]], [["‘Indeed you are.", "\"지쳤어, 정말로."], ["You do too much.’", "넌 너무 무리를 해.\""]], [["‘I wish I could do more.’", "\"더 못 해 드리는 게 마음에 걸려요.\""]], [["‘I know you do, but you overtask your strength.", "\"네 마음은 안다, 하지만 넌 네 힘에 부치게 애를 쓰잖니."], ["This wretched life, my love, of daily labour and fatigue, is more than you can bear, I am sure it is.", "날마다 고된 일에 시달리는 이 비참한 삶이, 얘야, 너한테는 견디기 버거운 거다, 분명 그래."], ["Poor Madeline!’", "가엾은 매들린!\""]], [["With these and many more kind words, Mr. Bray drew his daughter to him and kissed her cheek affectionately.", "이런 다정한 말을 비롯해 더 많은 말을 건네며, 브레이 씨는 딸을 끌어당겨 그 뺨에 애정 어린 입맞춤을 했다."], ["Ralph, watching him sharply and closely in the meantime, made his way towards the door, and signed to Gride to follow him.", "그동안 날카롭게 유심히 그를 지켜보던 랄프는 문 쪽으로 걸음을 옮기며, 그라이드에게 따라오라고 신호했다."]], [["‘You will communicate with us again?’ said Ralph.", "\"다시 우리한테 연락 주시는 거지요?\" 랄프가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, yes,’ returned Mr.", "\"그래요, 그래요.\""], ["Bray, hastily thrusting his daughter aside.", "브레이 씨가 황급히 딸을 옆으로 밀어내며 받았다."], ["‘In a week.", "\"일주일 안에."], ["Give me a week.’", "일주일만 주시오.\""]], [["‘One week,’ said Ralph, turning to his companion, ‘from today.", "\"일주일이오.\" 랄프가 동행을 돌아보며 말했다."], ["Good-morning.", "\"오늘부터요."], ["Miss Madeline, I kiss your hand.’", "그럼 이만. 매들린 양, 손에 입맞춤을 드립니다.\""]], [["‘We will shake hands, Gride,’ said Mr.", "\"악수를 합시다, 그라이드.\""], ["Bray, extending his, as old Arthur bowed.", "브레이 씨가 손을 내밀며 말했고, 늙은 아서는 고개를 숙였다."], ["‘You mean well, no doubt.", "\"당신도 뜻은 좋겠지요, 틀림없이."], ["I am bound to say so now.", "이제 그렇게 말하지 않을 수 없군."], ["If I owed you money, that was not your fault.", "내가 당신한테 돈을 빚졌다 한들, 그게 당신 잘못은 아니었으니."], ["Madeline, my love, your hand here.’", "매들린, 얘야, 여기 손을 다오.\""]], [["‘Oh dear!", "\"오 이런!"], ["If the young lady would condescent!", "아가씨가 허락만 해 주신다면!"], ["Only the tips of her fingers,’ said Arthur, hesitating and half retreating.", "손가락 끝만이라도요.\" 아서가 머뭇거리며 반쯤 물러서며 말했다."]], [["Madeline shrunk involuntarily from the goblin figure, but she placed the tips of her fingers in his hand and instantly withdrew them.", "매들린은 그 도깨비 같은 모습 앞에서 저도 모르게 움츠러들었다. 그래도 손가락 끝을 그의 손에 갖다 댔다가 곧바로 거두었다."], ["After an ineffectual clutch, intended to detain and carry them to his lips, old Arthur gave his own fingers a mumbling kiss, and with many amorous distortions of visage went in pursuit of his friend, who was by this time in the street.", "그것을 붙잡아 제 입술로 가져가려고 헛되이 한번 움켜쥐어 본 늙은 아서는, 제 손가락에 우물거리는 입맞춤을 하고는, 얼굴을 갖가지 음탕한 표정으로 일그러뜨리며 친구를 뒤쫓아 갔다. 그 무렵 친구는 이미 거리에 나와 있었다."]], [["‘What does he say, what does he say?", "\"뭐랍니까, 뭐래요?"], ["What does the giant say to the pigmy?’ inquired Arthur Gride, hobbling up to Ralph.", "거인은 난쟁이한테 뭐라고 합니까?\" 아서 그라이드가 절뚝거리며 랄프에게 다가가 물었다."]], [["‘What does the pigmy say to the giant?’ rejoined Ralph, elevating his eyebrows and looking down upon his questioner.", "\"난쟁이는 거인한테 뭐라고 하는데?\" 랄프가 눈썹을 치켜올리고 물어 오는 자를 내려다보며 받았다."]], [["‘He doesn’t know what to say,’ replied Arthur Gride.", "\"이 양반은 뭐라고 해야 할지 모르겠소.\" 아서 그라이드가 대답했다."], ["‘He hopes and fears.", "\"기대도 되고 겁도 나고."], ["But is she not a dainty morsel?’", "한데 그 처녀, 별미 같지 않소?\""]], [["‘I have no great taste for beauty,’ growled Ralph.", "\"나는 미인에 별 취미가 없어.\" 랄프가 으르렁거렸다."]], [["‘But I have,’ rejoined Arthur, rubbing his hands.", "\"하지만 나는 있지요.\" 아서가 두 손을 비비며 받았다."], ["‘Oh dear!", "\"오 이런!"], ["How handsome her eyes looked when she was stooping over him!", "그 처녀가 아비한테 몸을 숙일 때 그 눈이 어찌나 고와 보이던지!"], ["Such long lashes, such delicate fringe!", "그렇게 긴 속눈썹, 그렇게 섬세한 가장자리라니!"], ["She--she--looked at me so soft.’", "그 처녀가, 그 처녀가 나를 그렇게 부드럽게 보더군.\""]], [["‘Not over-lovingly, I think,’ said Ralph.", "\"그리 사랑스러운 눈길은 아니었던 것 같은데.\" 랄프가 말했다."], ["‘Did she?’", "\"안 그래?\""]], [["‘No, you think not?’ replied old Arthur.", "\"아니, 안 그랬던 것 같소?\" 늙은 아서가 받았다."], ["‘But don’t you think it can be brought about?", "\"하지만 이 일이 잘 풀릴 거라고 보지 않소?"], ["Don’t you think it can?’", "잘 풀릴 것 같지 않냐고요?\""]], [["Ralph looked at him with a contemptuous frown, and replied with a sneer, and between his teeth:", "랄프는 경멸 어린 찡그린 얼굴로 그를 보며, 비웃음을 머금고 이를 악문 채 대답했다."]], [["‘Did you mark his telling her she was tired and did too much, and overtasked her strength?’", "\"그자가 딸한테 지쳤다고, 너무 무리한다고, 힘에 부치게 애쓴다고 하던 거 눈여겨봤나?\""]], [["‘Ay, ay.", "\"그래요, 그래."], ["What of it?’", "그게 어쨌다고요?\""]], [["‘When do you think he ever told her that before?", "\"그자가 전에 그 애한테 언제 그런 말을 한 적이 있었을 것 같아?"], ["The life is more than she can bear.", "이 삶은 그 애가 견디기 버겁다고 말이야."], ["Yes, yes.", "그래, 그래."], ["He’ll change it for her.’", "그자가 딸을 위해 그 삶을 바꿔 줄 거야.\""]], [["‘D’ye think it’s done?’ inquired old Arthur, peering into his companion’s face with half-closed eyes.", "\"이게 다 된 거라고 보오?\" 늙은 아서가 눈을 반쯤 감고 동행의 얼굴을 들여다보며 물었다."]], [["‘I am sure it’s done,’ said Ralph.", "\"틀림없이 다 됐어.\" 랄프가 말했다."], ["‘He is trying to deceive himself, even before our eyes, already.", "\"그자는 자기 자신을 속이려 들고 있어, 그것도 우리 눈앞에서, 벌써부터."], ["He is making believe that he thinks of her good and not his own.", "자기가 제 이익이 아니라 딸의 행복을 생각하는 척하고 있지."], ["He is acting a virtuous part, and so considerate and affectionate, sir, that the daughter scarcely knew him.", "미덕 있는 역할을 연기하는 거야, 어찌나 사려 깊고 다정한지, 선생, 그 딸이 제 아비를 못 알아볼 지경이었어."], ["I saw a tear of surprise in her eye.", "그 애 눈에 놀란 눈물이 한 방울 비치는 걸 봤지."], ["There’ll be a few more tears of surprise there before long, though of a different kind.", "머지않아 거기 놀란 눈물이 몇 방울 더 비칠 거야, 그건 다른 종류의 눈물이겠지만."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["we may wait with confidence for this day week.’", "우리는 자신만만하게 오늘부터 일주일을 기다리면 돼.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 48", "# CHAPTER 48"]], [["Being for the Benefit of Mr.", "니콜라스는 무겁고 슬픈 마음으로, 괴로운 생각들에 짓눌린 채 다시 동쪽으로 발길을 돌렸어."], ["Vincent Crummles, and positively his last Appearance on this Stage\nIt was with a very sad and heavy heart, oppressed by many painful ideas, that Nicholas retraced his steps eastward and betook himself to the counting-house of Cheeryble Brothers.", "그러고는 치어리블 형제의 사무실로 향했지."], ["Whatever the idle hopes he had suffered himself to entertain, whatever the pleasant visions which had sprung up in his mind and grouped themselves round the fair image of Madeline Bray, they were now dispelled, and not a vestige of their gaiety and brightness remained.", "그동안 그가 헛되이 품어 보았던 희망이 어떤 것이었든, 마음속에 솟아올라 매들린 브레이의 고운 모습 둘레에 모여들던 즐거운 환상이 어떤 것이었든, 그것들은 이제 다 흩어져 버렸어. 그 환하고 기쁘던 기운은 흔적조차 남지 않았지."]], [["It would be a poor compliment to Nicholas’s better nature, and one which he was very far from deserving, to insinuate that the solution, and such a solution, of the mystery which had seemed to surround Madeline Bray, when he was ignorant even of her name, had damped his ardour or cooled the fervour of his admiration. If he had regarded her before, with such a passion as young men attracted by mere beauty and elegance may entertain, he was now conscious of much deeper and stronger feelings. But, reverence for the truth and purity of her heart, respect for the helplessness and loneliness of her situation, sympathy with the trials of one so young and fair and admiration of her great and noble spirit, all seemed to raise her far above his reach, and, while they imparted new depth and dignity to his love, to whisper that it was hopeless.", "니콜라스는 매들린 브레이의 이름조차 모르던 시절, 그녀를 둘러싼 것만 같던 그 비밀이 마침내 풀렸어. 그것도 그런 식으로 풀렸지. 그렇다고 해서 그의 열정이 식거나 흠모의 마음이 차게 가라앉았다고 말하는 건, 니콜라스의 더 나은 본성을 깎아내리는 일이야. 그건 그가 받아 마땅한 평가도 결코 아니지. 전에 그가 그녀를 향해 품었던 마음이 그저 아름다움과 우아함에 끌린 젊은이들의 흔한 사랑 같은 것이었다면, 이제는 훨씬 더 깊고 강한 감정을 느끼고 있었거든. 하지만 그녀의 진실하고 순결한 마음에 대한 존경, 의지할 데 없고 외로운 처지에 대한 배려, 그토록 어리고 고운 사람이 겪는 시련에 대한 연민, 그리고 그녀의 크고 고결한 정신에 대한 감탄, 이 모든 것이 그녀를 그가 닿을 수 없는 저 높은 곳으로 끌어올리는 것만 같았어. 그리고 그것들이 그의 사랑에 새로운 깊이와 품위를 더해 주는 한편, 이 사랑은 가망이 없다고 속삭이는 것만 같았지."]], [["‘I will keep my word, as I have pledged it to her,’ said Nicholas, manfully.", "\"나는 그녀에게 한 약속을 꼭 지킬 거야.\" 니콜라스가 사내답게 말했어."], ["‘This is no common trust that I have to discharge, and I will perform the double duty that is imposed upon me most scrupulously and strictly.", "\"내가 맡은 건 결코 가벼운 임무가 아니야. 나한테 지워진 이 두 가지 의무를 더없이 꼼꼼하고 철저하게 해낼 거야."], ["My secret feelings deserve no consideration in such a case as this, and they shall have none.’", "이런 일에서 내 은밀한 감정 따위는 고려할 가치가 없어. 그러니 조금도 고려하지 않겠어.\""]], [["Still, there were the secret feelings in existence just the same, and in secret Nicholas rather encouraged them than otherwise; reasoning (if he reasoned at all) that there they could do no harm to anybody but himself, and that if he kept them to himself from a sense of duty, he had an additional right to entertain himself with them as a reward for his heroism.", "그래도 그 은밀한 감정은 여전히 그대로 거기에 있었어. 그리고 니콜라스는 남몰래 그 감정을 누르기는커녕 오히려 부추기는 편이었지. 이렇게 따져 본 거야 — 따졌다고 한다면 말이지만. 그 감정을 마음속에만 두면 자기 말고는 누구한테도 해를 끼치지 않을 테고, 의무감 때문에 그걸 혼자만 간직한다면, 그 영웅적인 행동의 상으로 가끔 그 감정에 잠겨도 될 권리쯤은 더 생기는 거라고 말이야."]], [["All these thoughts, coupled with what he had seen that morning and the anticipation of his next visit, rendered him a very dull and abstracted companion; so much so, indeed, that Tim Linkinwater suspected he must have made the mistake of a figure somewhere, which was preying upon his mind, and seriously conjured him, if such were the case, to make a clean breast and scratch it out, rather than have his whole life embittered by the tortures of remorse.", "이런 온갖 생각에, 그날 아침 본 광경과 다음 방문에 대한 기대까지 겹치니, 그는 더없이 멍하고 넋 나간 동무가 되어 버렸어. 어찌나 그랬던지 팀 링킨워터는 그가 어딘가에서 숫자 계산을 틀려 그게 마음에 걸리는 거라고 의심했지. 그래서 정말 그런 거라면 다 털어놓고 그 부분을 지워 버리라고 진지하게 권했어. 양심의 가책 때문에 평생을 괴로움 속에 보내느니 그게 낫다면서 말이야."]], [["But in reply to these considerate representations, and many others both from Tim and Mr.", "그런데 팀의, 그리고 프랭크 씨의 이런 사려 깊은 말과 다른 여러 권유에도 니콜라스는 그저 한마디만 했어. 살면서 이보다 더 즐거운 적이 없다고 말이야."], ["Frank, Nicholas could only be brought to state that he was never merrier in his life; and so went on all day, and so went towards home at night, still turning over and over again the same subjects, thinking over and over again the same things, and arriving over and over again at the same conclusions.", "그렇게 하루를 보냈고, 밤이 되자 그렇게 집으로 향했지. 여전히 똑같은 문제를 몇 번이고 되씹으면서, 똑같은 생각을 몇 번이고 거듭하면서, 그리고 몇 번이고 똑같은 결론에 다다르면서 말이야."]], [["In this pensive, wayward, and uncertain state, people are apt to lounge and loiter without knowing why, to read placards on the walls with great attention and without the smallest idea of one word of their contents, and to stare most earnestly through shop-windows at things which they don’t see.", "이렇게 시름에 잠겨 갈피를 못 잡고 마음이 들떠 있을 때, 사람들은 까닭도 모른 채 어슬렁대고 빈둥거리기 마련이지. 벽에 붙은 광고를 잔뜩 들여다보면서도 그 내용은 한 단어도 머리에 안 들어오고, 가게 진열창을 뚫어지게 바라보면서도 거기 있는 물건은 하나도 안 보이는 거야."], ["It was thus that Nicholas found himself poring with the utmost interest over a large play-bill hanging outside a Minor Theatre which he had to pass on his way home, and reading a list of the actors and actresses who had promised to do honour to some approaching benefit, with as much gravity as if it had been a catalogue of the names of those ladies and gentlemen who stood highest upon the Book of Fate, and he had been looking anxiously for his own.", "니콜라스도 그랬어. 그가 집으로 가는 길에 지나야 했던 어느 작은 극장 바깥에 큼지막한 연극 광고가 걸려 있었는데, 그는 거기 푹 빠져 들여다보고 있었지."], ["He glanced at the top of the bill, with a smile at his own dulness, as he prepared to resume his walk, and there saw announced, in large letters with a large space between each of them, ‘Positively the last appearance of Mr.", "곧 있을 어느 자선 공연에 출연하기로 한 남녀 배우들의 명단을 읽고 있었는데, 그 표정이 어찌나 진지했던지, 마치 운명의 책에서 가장 높은 자리에 오른 신사 숙녀들의 명단을 보며 거기서 제 이름을 애타게 찾고 있는 것만 같았어. 그러다 다시 걸음을 옮기려고 광고 맨 위쪽을 흘끗 보면서 자기가 그렇게 멍해 있는 게 우스워 빙긋 웃었지."], ["Vincent Crummles of Provincial Celebrity!!!’", "그런데 거기에 큼지막한 글자로, 글자 하나하나 사이에 큼지막한 간격을 두고 이렇게 적혀 있는 게 보였어. \"지방의 명배우 빈센트 크럼블스 씨, 정녕 마지막 무대!!!\""]], [["‘Nonsense!’ said Nicholas, turning back again.", "\"말도 안 돼!\" 니콜라스가 다시 돌아서며 말했어."], ["‘It can’t be.’", "\"그럴 리가 없는데.\""]], [["But there it was.", "그런데 정말 그렇게 적혀 있었어."], ["In one line by itself was an announcement of the first night of a new melodrama; in another line by itself was an announcement of the last six nights of an old one; a third line was devoted to the re-engagement of the unrivalled African Knife-swallower, who had kindly suffered himself to be prevailed upon to forego his country engagements for one week longer; a fourth line announced that Mr.", "한 줄에는 새 멜로드라마의 첫날 공연 소식이 따로 적혀 있었고, 또 한 줄에는 오래된 작품의 마지막 엿새 공연 소식이 따로 적혀 있었지. 세 번째 줄은 천하무적 아프리카 칼 삼키기 명인의 재출연에 바쳐졌는데, 이 사람은 친절하게도 설득에 못 이겨 지방 공연을 일주일 더 미뤄 주기로 했다는 거야."], ["Snittle Timberry, having recovered from his late severe indisposition, would have the honour of appearing that evening; a fifth line said that there were ‘Cheers, Tears, and Laughter!’ every night; a sixth, that that was positively the last appearance of Mr.", "네 번째 줄에는 스니틀 팀베리 씨가 최근의 심한 병환에서 회복하여 그날 저녁 영광스럽게 무대에 선다고 적혀 있었어."], ["Vincent Crummles of Provincial Celebrity.", "다섯 번째 줄에는 밤마다 \"환호와 눈물과 웃음!\"이 넘친다고 했고, 여섯 번째 줄에는 지방의 명배우 빈센트 크럼블스 씨의 정녕 마지막 무대라고 적혀 있었지."]], [["‘Surely it must be the same man,’ thought Nicholas.", "\"분명 그 사람이 틀림없어.\" 니콜라스는 생각했어."], ["‘There can’t be two Vincent Crummleses.’", "\"빈센트 크럼블스가 둘일 리는 없잖아.\""]], [["The better to settle this question he referred to the bill again, and finding that there was a Baron in the first piece, and that Roberto (his son) was enacted by one Master Crummles, and Spaletro (his nephew) by one Master Percy Crummles--THEIR last appearances--and that, incidental to the piece, was a characteristic dance by the characters, and a castanet pas seul by the Infant Phenomenon--HER last appearance--he no longer entertained any doubt; and presenting himself at the stage-door, and sending in a scrap of paper with ‘Mr. Johnson’ written thereon in pencil, was presently conducted by a Robber, with a very large belt and buckle round his waist, and very large leather gauntlets on his hands, into the presence of his former manager.", "이 문제를 확실히 매듭짓고 싶어서 그는 다시 광고를 살펴보았어. 그랬더니 첫 작품에 남작이 나오는데, 그 아들 로베르토 역을 어린 크럼블스 도련님이 맡았고, 조카 스팔레트로 역을 어린 퍼시 크럼블스 도련님이 맡았다는 거야. 그것도 그들의 마지막 무대라면서 말이지. 게다가 그 작품 중간에 등장인물들의 특색 있는 춤이 들어가고, 신동 아가씨의 캐스터네츠 독무가 있다는데, 이것도 그녀의 마지막 무대라는 거야. 이제 그는 더 이상 의심하지 않았어. 그래서 무대 출입문 앞에 가서 연필로 \"존슨 씨\"라고 적은 쪽지를 들여보냈지. 그러자 곧 허리에 아주 커다란 허리띠와 버클을 두르고 손에 아주 커다란 가죽 장갑을 낀 도둑 하나가 그를 옛 단장 앞으로 안내했어."]], [["Mr. Crummles was unfeignedly glad to see him, and starting up from before a small dressing-glass, with one very bushy eyebrow stuck on crooked over his left eye, and the fellow eyebrow and the calf of one of his legs in his hand, embraced him cordially; at the same time observing, that it would do Mrs.", "크럼블스 씨는 그를 보고 진심으로 반가워했어."], ["Crummles’s heart good to bid him goodbye before they went.", "작은 화장 거울 앞에 앉아 있다가 벌떡 일어났는데, 왼쪽 눈 위에는 아주 덥수룩한 눈썹 하나가 비뚜름하게 붙어 있었고, 한 손에는 짝이 되는 눈썹과 한쪽 다리의 종아리를 들고 있었지. 그러고는 그를 다정하게 끌어안으며, 떠나기 전에 작별 인사를 나누면 크럼블스 부인이 무척 기뻐할 거라고 했어."]], [["‘You were always a favourite of hers, Johnson,’ said Crummles, ‘always were from the first.", "\"자네는 늘 그 사람이 아끼던 사람이었지, 존슨.\" 크럼블스가 말했어."], ["I was quite easy in my mind about you from that first day you dined with us.", "\"처음부터 그랬어. 자네가 우리랑 처음 저녁을 먹던 그날부터 나는 자네에 대해선 마음이 아주 놓였다네."], ["One that Mrs. Crummles took a fancy to, was sure to turn out right.", "크럼블스 부인이 마음에 들어 한 사람은 틀림없이 잘 풀리게 마련이거든."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["Johnson, what a woman that is!’", "존슨, 그 사람은 정말 대단한 여자야!\""]], [["‘I am sincerely obliged to her for her kindness in this and all other respects,’ said Nicholas.", "\"이 일이며 다른 모든 일에서 보여 주신 친절에 진심으로 감사드립니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘But where are you going, that you talk about bidding goodbye?’", "\"그런데 작별 인사 얘기를 하시는 걸 보니, 대체 어디로 가시는 겁니까?\""]], [["‘Haven’t you seen it in the papers?’ said Crummles, with some dignity.", "\"신문에서 못 봤나?\" 크럼블스가 제법 위엄 있게 말했어."]], [["‘No,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"못 봤습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘I wonder at that,’ said the manager.", "\"그것참 이상하군.\" 단장이 말했어."], ["‘It was among the varieties.", "\"잡보란에 실렸는데."], ["I had the paragraph here somewhere--but I don’t know--oh, yes, here it is.’", "여기 어딘가에 그 기사를 두었는데 — 글쎄, 어디 있더라 — 아, 그래, 여기 있군.\""]], [["So saying, Mr.", "이렇게 말하면서 크럼블스 씨는 잃어버린 줄 알았다는 시늉을 한참 하더니, 평소 입는 바지 주머니에서 한 치쯤 되는 신문 조각을 꺼냈어."], ["Crummles, after pretending that he thought he must have lost it, produced a square inch of newspaper from the pocket of the pantaloons he wore in private life (which, together with the plain clothes of several other gentlemen, lay scattered about on a kind of dresser in the room), and gave it to Nicholas to read:", "그 바지는 다른 신사 몇 사람의 평상복과 함께 방 안의 화장대 같은 데 여기저기 널려 있었지. 크럼블스 씨는 그 조각을 니콜라스에게 읽어 보라고 건넸어."]], [["‘The talented Vincent Crummles, long favourably known to fame as a country manager and actor of no ordinary pretensions, is about to cross the Atlantic on a histrionic expedition.", "\"재능 있는 빈센트 크럼블스 씨는 오래도록 지방 극단장이자 예사롭지 않은 기량의 배우로 이름을 떨쳐 온 분으로, 이제 연극 원정을 떠나 대서양을 건널 참이다."], ["Crummles is to be accompanied, we hear, by his lady and gifted family.", "듣자 하니 크럼블스 씨는 부인과 재능 넘치는 가족을 데리고 간다고 한다."], ["We know no man superior to Crummles in his particular line of character, or one who, whether as a public or private individual, could carry with him the best wishes of a larger circle of friends.", "우리는 그만의 그 독특한 배역에서 크럼블스 씨보다 뛰어난 이를 알지 못하며, 공인으로서나 사인으로서나 이토록 폭넓은 친구들의 진심 어린 축복을 받으며 떠날 만한 사람도 알지 못한다."], ["Crummles is certain to succeed.’", "크럼블스 씨는 반드시 성공할 것이다.\""]], [["‘Here’s another bit,’ said Mr.", "\"여기 또 한 토막 있네.\""], ["Crummles, handing over a still smaller scrap.", "크럼블스 씨가 더 작은 조각을 건네며 말했어."], ["‘This is from the notices to correspondents, this one.’", "\"이건 독자 문의 답란에서 나온 거야, 이건.\""]], [["Nicholas read it aloud. ‘“Philo-Dramaticus.", "니콜라스는 그것을 소리 내어 읽었어."], ["Crummles, the country manager and actor, cannot be more than forty-three, or forty-four years of age.", "\"필로 드라마티쿠스 님께. 지방 극단장이자 배우인 크럼블스 씨는 마흔셋이나 마흔넷을 넘었을 리 없습니다."], ["Crummles is NOT a Prussian, having been born at Chelsea.”", "또한 크럼블스 씨는 프로이센 사람이 아니며, 첼시 태생입니다.\" \"흠!\""], ["Humph!’ said Nicholas, ‘that’s an odd paragraph.’", "니콜라스가 말했어. \"참 묘한 기사로군요.\""]], [["‘Very,’ returned Crummles, scratching the side of his nose, and looking at Nicholas with an assumption of great unconcern.", "\"아주 묘하지.\" 크럼블스가 콧등을 긁으며 대답했어."], ["‘I can’t think who puts these things in.", "그러고는 짐짓 아무렇지도 않은 척하며 니콜라스를 바라보았지. \"이런 걸 대체 누가 신문에 싣는지 모르겠단 말이야."], ["I didn’t.’", "나는 안 그랬는데.\""]], [["Still keeping his eye on Nicholas, Mr.", "크럼블스 씨는 여전히 니콜라스에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 더없이 진지한 표정으로 고개를 두세 번 절레절레 저었어."], ["Crummles shook his head twice or thrice with profound gravity, and remarking, that he could not for the life of him imagine how the newspapers found out the things they did, folded up the extracts and put them in his pocket again.", "그러고는 신문이 대체 어떻게 그런 걸 알아내는지 도무지 모르겠다고 한마디 하더니, 그 기사 조각들을 다시 접어 주머니에 넣었지."]], [["‘I am astonished to hear this news,’ said Nicholas.", "\"이 소식을 들으니 정말 놀랍군요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘Going to America!", "\"미국으로 가시다니요!"], ["You had no such thing in contemplation when I was with you.’", "제가 함께 있을 땐 그런 생각을 전혀 안 하셨잖아요.\""]], [["‘No,’ replied Crummles, ‘I hadn’t then. The fact is that Mrs Crummles--most extraordinary woman, Johnson.’ Here he broke off and whispered something in his ear.", "\"그래.\" 크럼블스가 대답했어. \"그땐 그런 생각이 없었지. 실은 말이야, 크럼블스 부인이 — 정말 대단한 여자라니까, 존슨.\" 그러고는 말을 끊더니 니콜라스의 귀에 대고 뭐라고 속삭였어."]], [["‘Oh!’ said Nicholas, smiling.", "\"오!\" 니콜라스가 빙긋 웃으며 말했어."], ["‘The prospect of an addition to your family?’", "\"가족이 늘어날 참이라고요?\""]], [["‘The seventh addition, Johnson,’ returned Mr.", "\"일곱 번째 식구라네, 존슨.\""], ["Crummles, solemnly.", "크럼블스 씨가 엄숙하게 대답했어."], ["‘I thought such a child as the Phenomenon must have been a closer; but it seems we are to have another.", "\"신동 같은 애가 나왔으니 그게 마지막인 줄 알았는데, 또 하나 보게 생겼지 뭔가."], ["She is a very remarkable woman.’", "정말 비범한 여자야.\""]], [["‘I congratulate you,’ said Nicholas, ‘and I hope this may prove a phenomenon too.’", "\"축하드립니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"이 아이도 또 하나의 신동으로 자라기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["‘Why, it’s pretty sure to be something uncommon, I suppose,’ rejoined Mr. Crummles.", "\"글쎄, 뭔가 예사롭지 않은 게 나오긴 할 거야, 아마.\""], ["‘The talent of the other three is principally in combat and serious pantomime.", "크럼블스 씨가 대꾸했어. \"다른 셋의 재능은 주로 격투와 진지한 무언극에 있거든."], ["I should like this one to have a turn for juvenile tragedy; I understand they want something of that sort in America very much.", "이 애는 소년 비극 쪽에 소질이 있으면 좋겠는데. 미국에서는 그런 걸 무척 원한다고 하더라고."], ["However, we must take it as it comes.", "그래도 뭐, 나오는 대로 받아들여야지."], ["Perhaps it may have a genius for the tight-rope.", "어쩌면 줄타기에 천재일지도 몰라. 한마디로 어떤 천재든 될 수 있지, 제 어미를 닮는다면 말이야, 존슨."], ["It may have any sort of genius, in short, if it takes after its mother, Johnson, for she is an universal genius; but, whatever its genius is, that genius shall be developed.’", "그 사람은 만능 천재거든. 어쨌든 천재성이 뭐든 간에, 그 천재성은 꼭 길러 줄 거야.\""]], [["Expressing himself after these terms, Mr.", "이렇게 말하고 나서 크럼블스 씨는 나머지 눈썹 한 짝과 두 다리의 종아리를 붙이고, 그다음 두 다리를 신었어."], ["Crummles put on his other eyebrow, and the calves of his legs, and then put on his legs, which were of a yellowish flesh-colour, and rather soiled about the knees, from frequent going down upon those joints, in curses, prayers, last struggles, and other strong passages.", "그 다리는 누르스름한 살색이었는데, 무릎께가 꽤 더러워져 있었지. 저주하고 기도하고 마지막 발버둥을 치고, 그 밖에 격정적인 대목마다 그 관절로 자주 무릎을 꿇은 탓이었어."]], [["While the ex-manager completed his toilet, he informed Nicholas that as he should have a fair start in America from the proceeds of a tolerably good engagement which he had been fortunate enough to obtain, and as he and Mrs.", "이 전직 단장은 화장을 마무리하면서 니콜라스에게 이런저런 이야기를 들려주었어. 운 좋게 꽤 괜찮은 공연 계약을 따낸 덕에 그 수익으로 미국에서 좋은 출발을 할 수 있을 거고, 또 자기와 크럼블스 부인이 영원히 무대에 설 수는 없는 노릇이라 — 명성의 숨결 속에서나, 비유적인 의미로나 모를까, 둘 다 불사신은 아니니까 — 그래서 그곳에 아주 눌러앉기로 마음먹었다는 거야."], ["Crummles could scarcely hope to act for ever (not being immortal, except in the breath of Fame and in a figurative sense) he had made up his mind to settle there permanently, in the hope of acquiring some land of his own which would support them in their old age, and which they could afterwards bequeath to their children.", "자기 땅을 좀 마련해서 노년에 그것에 기대 살다가, 나중에 자식들에게 물려줄 수 있기를 바라면서 말이지. 니콜라스가 그 결심을 크게 칭찬하자, 크럼블스 씨는 두 사람이 함께 아는 친구들에 관해 재미있을 만한 소식을 마저 들려주었어."], ["Nicholas, having highly commended the resolution, Mr. Crummles went on to impart such further intelligence relative to their mutual friends as he thought might prove interesting; informing Nicholas, among other things, that Miss Snevellicci was happily married to an affluent young wax-chandler who had supplied the theatre with candles, and that Mr.", "그중에서도 스네벨리치 양이 극장에 양초를 대 주던 부유한 젊은 양초 장수와 행복하게 결혼했다는 것, 그리고 릴리비크 씨가 제 영혼이 제 것이라고 감히 말도 못 할 처지가 됐다는 것을 알려 줬지."], ["Lillyvick didn’t dare to say his soul was his own, such was the tyrannical sway of Mrs Lillyvick, who reigned paramount and supreme.", "릴리비크 부인이 최고 권좌에 올라 무소불위로 군림하며 어찌나 폭군처럼 휘두르는지 말이야."]], [["Nicholas responded to this confidence on the part of Mr. Crummles, by confiding to him his own name, situation, and prospects, and informing him, in as few general words as he could, of the circumstances which had led to their first acquaintance.", "니콜라스도 크럼블스 씨가 이렇게 속내를 털어놓아 준 데 답하여, 자기 본명과 처지와 앞날에 대해 그에게 털어놓았어."], ["After congratulating him with great heartiness on the improved state of his fortunes, Mr.", "그리고 두 사람이 처음 알게 된 사연도 되도록 짤막하게 두루뭉술한 말로 알려 주었지."], ["Crummles gave him to understand that next morning he and his were to start for Liverpool, where the vessel lay which was to carry them from the shores of England, and that if Nicholas wished to take a last adieu of Mrs.", "크럼블스 씨는 그의 형편이 나아진 것을 진심으로 따뜻하게 축하해 준 뒤, 다음 날 아침 자기와 식구들이 리버풀로 떠난다고 알려 주었어. 영국 해안을 떠나 그들을 실어 갈 배가 거기 정박해 있다면서 말이야."], ["Crummles, he must repair with him that night to a farewell supper, given in honour of the family at a neighbouring tavern; at which Mr.", "그리고 니콜라스가 크럼블스 부인에게 마지막 작별 인사를 하고 싶다면, 그날 밤 자기와 함께 가야 한다고 했어."], ["Snittle Timberry would preside, while the honours of the vice-chair would be sustained by the African Swallower.", "근처 술집에서 가족을 위한 송별 만찬이 열리는데, 거기서 스니틀 팀베리 씨가 좌장을 맡고, 부좌장의 영예는 아프리카 칼 삼키기 명인이 떠맡을 거라면서 말이지."]], [["The room being by this time very warm and somewhat crowded, in consequence of the influx of four gentlemen, who had just killed each other in the piece under representation, Nicholas accepted the invitation, and promised to return at the conclusion of the performances; preferring the cool air and twilight out of doors to the mingled perfume of gas, orange-peel, and gunpowder, which pervaded the hot and glaring theatre.", "이때쯤 방은 무척 더워지고 사람도 좀 북적였어. 공연 중인 작품에서 방금 서로를 죽인 신사 넷이 들이닥친 탓이었지. 그래서 니콜라스는 초대를 받아들이고, 공연이 끝나면 돌아오겠다고 약속했어. 가스와 오렌지 껍질과 화약 냄새가 뒤섞여 가득한 그 덥고 번쩍이는 극장보다는, 바깥의 시원한 공기와 어스름이 더 좋았거든."]], [["He availed himself of this interval to buy a silver snuff-box--the best his funds would afford--as a token of remembrance for Mr.", "그는 이 짬을 타서 은담뱃갑 하나를 샀어. 주머니 사정이 허락하는 한 가장 좋은 걸로 골랐지."], ["Crummles, and having purchased besides a pair of ear-rings for Mrs.", "크럼블스 씨에게 줄 기념 선물이었어."], ["Crummles, a necklace for the Phenomenon, and a flaming shirt-pin for each of the young gentlemen, he refreshed himself with a walk, and returning a little after the appointed time, found the lights out, the theatre empty, the curtain raised for the night, and Mr.", "그 밖에 크럼블스 부인에게 줄 귀걸이 한 쌍, 신동 아가씨에게 줄 목걸이, 그리고 어린 신사들 하나하나에게 줄 화려한 셔츠 핀까지 사고는, 산책으로 기분을 좀 풀었지. 그리고 약속한 시간보다 조금 늦게 돌아와 보니, 불은 다 꺼졌고 극장은 텅 비었으며, 막은 그날 밤을 위해 내려져 있었어."], ["Crummles walking up and down the stage expecting his arrival.", "그리고 크럼블스 씨가 그가 오기를 기다리며 무대를 이리저리 거닐고 있었지."]], [["‘Timberry won’t be long,’ said Mr.", "\"팀베리는 오래 안 걸릴 거야.\" 크럼블스 씨가 말했어."], ["Crummles. ‘He played the audience out tonight.", "\"오늘 밤 손님들을 다 내보낼 때까지 연기했거든."], ["He does a faithful black in the last piece, and it takes him a little longer to wash himself.’", "마지막 작품에서 충직한 흑인 역을 하는데, 그래서 몸 씻는 데 시간이 좀 더 걸려.\""]], [["‘A very unpleasant line of character, I should think?’ said Nicholas.", "\"꽤 불쾌한 배역이겠는데요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘No, I don’t know,’ replied Mr.", "\"아니, 글쎄, 그렇지도 않아.\" 크럼블스 씨가 대답했어."], ["Crummles; ‘it comes off easily enough, and there’s only the face and neck.", "\"지우기는 꽤 쉽거든. 얼굴이랑 목만 칠하니까."], ["We had a first-tragedy man in our company once, who, when he played Othello, used to black himself all over.", "예전에 우리 극단에 일급 비극 배우가 하나 있었는데, 그 친구는 오셀로를 연기할 때 온몸을 새까맣게 칠하곤 했지. 하지만 그건 배역에 푹 빠져 진짜처럼 들어가는 거야."], ["But that’s feeling a part and going into it as if you meant it; it isn’t usual; more’s the pity.’", "흔한 일은 아니지. 안타까운 노릇이지만 말이야.\""]], [["Mr. Snittle Timberry now appeared, arm-in-arm with the African Swallower, and, being introduced to Nicholas, raised his hat half a foot, and said he was proud to know him.", "이제 스니틀 팀베리 씨가 아프리카 칼 삼키기 명인과 팔짱을 끼고 나타났어."], ["The Swallower said the same, and looked and spoke remarkably like an Irishman.", "그리고 니콜라스에게 소개를 받자, 모자를 반 자나 들어 올리며 만나서 영광이라고 했지. 칼 삼키기 명인도 똑같이 말했는데, 생김새며 말투며 놀랄 만큼 아일랜드 사람 같았어."]], [["‘I see by the bills that you have been ill, sir,’ said Nicholas to Mr Timberry.", "\"광고를 보니 편찮으셨다고 하던데요, 선생님.\" 니콜라스가 팀베리 씨에게 말했어."], ["‘I hope you are none the worse for your exertions tonight?’", "\"오늘 밤 무리하신 탓에 더 나빠지지는 않으셨기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["Mr.", "팀베리 씨는 대답 대신 침울한 표정으로 고개를 절레절레 흔들더니, 의미심장하게 가슴을 여러 번 톡톡 두드렸어."], ["Timberry, in reply, shook his head with a gloomy air, tapped his chest several times with great significancy, and drawing his cloak more closely about him, said, ‘But no matter, no matter.", "그러고는 망토를 몸에 더 단단히 여미며 말했지. \"하지만 괜찮습니다, 괜찮아요."], ["Come!’", "자, 갑시다!\""]], [["It is observable that when people upon the stage are in any strait involving the very last extremity of weakness and exhaustion, they invariably perform feats of strength requiring great ingenuity and muscular power.", "눈여겨볼 만한 점은, 무대 위 사람들이 기력이 다 빠져 더는 어쩔 수 없는 막다른 지경에 몰리면, 그때마다 어김없이 대단한 재주와 근력을 요하는 힘쓰는 묘기를 펼친다는 거야. 그러니까 피를 흘리며 죽어 가는 왕자나 산적 두목이, 너무 기진해서 더없이 부드러운 음악이 흐를 때나 겨우 움직이고 — 그것도 손과 무릎으로 기어서야 움직이는데 — 도움을 청하러 오두막 문으로 다가가는 장면을 보면 말이지."], ["Thus, a wounded prince or bandit chief, who is bleeding to death and too faint to move, except to the softest music (and then only upon his hands and knees), shall be seen to approach a cottage door for aid in such a series of writhings and twistings, and with such curlings up of the legs, and such rollings over and over, and such gettings up and tumblings down again, as could never be achieved save by a very strong man skilled in posture-making. And so natural did this sort of performance come to Mr.", "어찌나 몸을 비틀고 꼬고, 다리를 오므렸다 펴고, 이리저리 뒹굴고, 일어났다가 또 고꾸라지는지, 그건 자세 잡기에 능한 아주 힘센 사람이 아니고서는 도저히 해낼 수 없는 거란 말이야."], ["Snittle Timberry, that on their way out of the theatre and towards the tavern where the supper was to be holden, he testified the severity of his recent indisposition and its wasting effects upon the nervous system, by a series of gymnastic performances which were the admiration of all witnesses.", "스니틀 팀베리 씨에게는 이런 연기가 어찌나 자연스럽게 몸에 뱄던지, 극장을 나와 만찬이 열릴 술집으로 가는 길에 그는 최근의 병환이 얼마나 심했는지, 또 그게 신경계에 얼마나 큰 손상을 입혔는지를 일련의 체조 같은 동작으로 증명해 보였어. 그 모습은 지켜본 모두의 감탄을 자아냈지."]], [["‘Why this is indeed a joy I had not looked for!’ said Mrs.", "\"세상에, 이건 정말 뜻밖의 기쁨이군요!\""], ["Crummles, when Nicholas was presented.", "니콜라스가 소개되자 크럼블스 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘Nor I,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"저도 그렇습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘It is by a mere chance that I have this opportunity of seeing you, although I would have made a great exertion to have availed myself of it.’", "\"이렇게 뵐 기회를 얻은 건 정말 우연이에요. 알았더라면 무슨 수를 써서라도 찾아왔을 텐데요.\""]], [["‘Here is one whom you know,’ said Mrs.", "\"자네가 아는 아이가 여기 있네.\" 크럼블스 부인이 신동 아가씨를 앞으로 떠밀며 말했어."], ["Crummles, thrusting forward the Phenomenon in a blue gauze frock, extensively flounced, and trousers of the same; ‘and here another--and another,’ presenting the Master Crummleses.", "아이는 자락을 잔뜩 댄 푸른 망사 원피스를 입고, 같은 천으로 된 바지를 입고 있었지. \"그리고 여기 또 하나, 또 하나.\""], ["‘And how is your friend, the faithful Digby?’", "부인은 어린 크럼블스 도련님들을 차례로 내보이며 말했어. \"그런데 자네 친구, 그 충직한 딕비는 어떻게 지내나?\""]], [["‘Digby!’ said Nicholas, forgetting at the instant that this had been Smike’s theatrical name.", "\"딕비요!\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘Oh yes.", "그 순간 이게 스마이크의 연극 무대 이름이었다는 걸 깜빡한 거야. \"아, 그래요."], ["He’s quite--what am I saying?--he is very far from well.’", "그 친구는 아주 — 내가 무슨 소릴 하는 거지? — 그 친구는 영 좋지가 않습니다.\""]], [["‘How!’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"세상에!\""], ["Crummles, with a tragic recoil.", "크럼블스 부인이 비극적으로 움찔하며 외쳤어."]], [["‘I fear,’ said Nicholas, shaking his head, and making an attempt to smile, ‘that your better-half would be more struck with him now than ever.’", "\"제 생각엔 말입니다.\" 니콜라스가 고개를 저으며 억지로 웃어 보이려 애썼어. \"지금 그 친구를 보시면 부군께서 예전보다 더 깊은 인상을 받으실 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘What mean you?’ rejoined Mrs.", "\"무슨 뜻인가?\""], ["Crummles, in her most popular manner.", "크럼블스 부인이 그 특유의 인기 있는 말투로 되물었어."], ["‘Whence comes this altered tone?’", "\"어디서 이런 달라진 어조가 나오는 건가?\""]], [["‘I mean that a dastardly enemy of mine has struck at me through him, and that while he thinks to torture me, he inflicts on him such agonies of terror and suspense as--You will excuse me, I am sure,’ said Nicholas, checking himself. ‘I should never speak of this, and never do, except to those who know the facts, but for a moment I forgot myself.’", "\"제 비열한 원수 하나가 그 친구를 통해 저를 친 거예요. 그자는 저를 괴롭힌다고 여기면서, 그 친구한테 차마 말 못 할 공포와 불안의 고통을 안기고 있죠 — 죄송합니다, 정말로요.\" 니콜라스가 스스로 말을 멈췄어. \"이런 얘기는 절대 입에 올리면 안 되는데. 사정을 아는 사람들 말고는 한 적도 없고요. 잠깐 제 정신이 아니었네요.\""]], [["With this hasty apology Nicholas stooped down to salute the Phenomenon, and changed the subject; inwardly cursing his precipitation, and very much wondering what Mrs.", "이렇게 황급히 사과하고 나서 니콜라스는 몸을 굽혀 신동 아가씨에게 인사하며 화제를 바꿨어."], ["Crummles must think of so sudden an explosion.", "속으로는 자신의 경솔함을 욕하면서, 크럼블스 부인이 이런 갑작스러운 폭발을 어떻게 생각할지 무척 궁금해하면서 말이지."]], [["That lady seemed to think very little about it, for the supper being by this time on table, she gave her hand to Nicholas and repaired with a stately step to the left hand of Mr.", "그 부인은 그 일을 별로 마음에 두지 않는 눈치였어. 이때쯤 만찬상이 다 차려졌거든."], ["Snittle Timberry. Nicholas had the honour to support her, and Mr.", "부인은 니콜라스에게 손을 내밀고는, 위엄 있는 걸음걸이로 스니틀 팀베리 씨의 왼편으로 가서 앉았지."], ["Crummles was placed upon the chairman’s right; the Phenomenon and the Master Crummleses sustained the vice.", "니콜라스는 영광스럽게도 그녀를 부축했고, 크럼블스 씨는 좌장의 오른편에 자리를 잡았어. 신동 아가씨와 어린 크럼블스 도련님들은 부좌석을 맡았지."]], [["The company amounted in number to some twenty-five or thirty, being composed of such members of the theatrical profession, then engaged or disengaged in London, as were numbered among the most intimate friends of Mr.", "손님은 모두 스물다섯에서 서른 명쯤 됐어. 당시 런던에서 일하거나 쉬고 있던 연극계 사람들 가운데, 크럼블스 부부와 가장 가까운 친구로 꼽히는 이들로 이루어져 있었지."], ["and Mrs. Crummles.", "남자 손님과 여자 손님의 수는 거의 비슷하게 맞춰져 있었어."], ["The ladies and gentlemen were pretty equally balanced; the expenses of the entertainment being defrayed by the latter, each of whom had the privilege of inviting one of the former as his guest.", "잔치 비용은 남자 손님들이 댔는데, 저마다 여자 손님 하나를 자기 손님으로 초대할 권리가 있었거든."]], [["It was upon the whole a very distinguished party, for independently of the lesser theatrical lights who clustered on this occasion round Mr.", "전체적으로 보면 꽤 쟁쟁한 모임이었어. 이날 스니틀 팀베리 씨 주위에 모여든 자잘한 연극계 인사들은 차치하고라도, 한 문인이 와 있었거든."], ["Snittle Timberry, there was a literary gentleman present who had dramatised in his time two hundred and forty-seven novels as fast as they had come out--some of them faster than they had come out--and who WAS a literary gentleman in consequence.", "이 사람은 한창때 소설이 나오는 족족 무려 이백마흔일곱 편을 각색했는데 — 그중 몇 편은 소설이 나오기도 전에 각색했지 — 그 덕에 문인이 된 사람이었어."]], [["This gentleman sat on the left hand of Nicholas, to whom he was introduced by his friend the African Swallower, from the bottom of the table, with a high eulogium upon his fame and reputation.", "이 신사는 니콜라스의 왼편에 앉았는데, 그를 니콜라스에게 소개한 건 식탁 끝에 앉은 그의 친구 아프리카 칼 삼키기 명인이었어. 명인은 이 문인의 명성과 평판을 한껏 추켜세우며 소개했지."]], [["‘I am happy to know a gentleman of such great distinction,’ said Nicholas, politely.", "\"이렇게 명망 높은 분을 알게 되어 기쁩니다.\" 니콜라스가 정중하게 말했어."]], [["‘Sir,’ replied the wit, ‘you’re very welcome, I’m sure.", "\"선생,\" 그 재담꾼이 대답했어."], ["The honour is reciprocal, sir, as I usually say when I dramatise a book.", "\"이쪽이야말로 환영입니다. 영광은 서로의 것이지요, 제가 책을 각색할 때 늘 하는 말처럼 말입니다."], ["Did you ever hear a definition of fame, sir?’", "선생, 명성이 뭔지 정의를 들어 본 적 있으십니까?\""]], [["‘I have heard several,’ replied Nicholas, with a smile.", "\"여러 가지를 들어 봤지요.\" 니콜라스가 빙긋 웃으며 대답했어."], ["‘What is yours?’", "\"선생의 정의는 뭡니까?\""]], [["‘When I dramatise a book, sir,’ said the literary gentleman, ‘THAT’S fame.", "\"제가 책을 각색하면 말입니다, 선생.\" 그 문인이 말했어."], ["For its author.’", "\"그게 바로 명성이지요. 그 책 지은이에게는요.\""]], [["‘Oh, indeed!’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"오, 그렇군요!\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘That’s fame, sir,’ said the literary gentleman.", "\"그게 명성입니다, 선생.\" 그 문인이 말했어."]], [["‘So Richard Turpin, Tom King, and Jerry Abershaw have handed down to fame the names of those on whom they committed their most impudent robberies?’ said Nicholas.", "\"그럼 리처드 터핀이며 톰 킹이며 제리 애버쇼는, 자기들이 가장 뻔뻔하게 강도질한 상대들의 이름을 후대에 길이 명성으로 남겨 준 셈이로군요?\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘I don’t know anything about that, sir,’ answered the literary gentleman.", "\"그건 저도 잘 모르는 일입니다, 선생.\" 그 문인이 대답했어."]], [["‘Shakespeare dramatised stories which had previously appeared in print, it is true,’ observed Nicholas.", "\"셰익스피어가 전에 이미 활자로 나온 이야기들을 각색한 건 사실이지요.\" 니콜라스가 한마디 했어."]], [["‘Meaning Bill, sir?’ said the literary gentleman.", "\"빌 말씀이시군요, 선생?\" 그 문인이 말했어."], ["‘So he did.", "\"그랬지요."], ["Bill was an adapter, certainly, so he was--and very well he adapted too--considering.’", "빌도 분명 각색을 한 사람이긴 합니다. 게다가 각색을 아주 잘했죠 — 그 시절치고는요.\""]], [["‘I was about to say,’ rejoined Nicholas, ‘that Shakespeare derived some of his plots from old tales and legends in general circulation; but it seems to me, that some of the gentlemen of your craft, at the present day, have shot very far beyond him--’", "\"제가 하려던 말은 이겁니다.\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어. \"셰익스피어는 두루 떠돌던 옛 설화와 전설에서 줄거리를 더러 따왔지요. 그런데 제가 보기엔, 요즘 선생 같은 직업을 가진 분들 가운데 몇몇은 그를 훨씬 앞질러 갔더군요 —\""]], [["‘You’re quite right, sir,’ interrupted the literary gentleman, leaning back in his chair and exercising his toothpick.", "\"아주 맞는 말씀입니다, 선생.\" 그 문인이 의자에 등을 기대고 이쑤시개를 놀리며 끼어들었어."], ["‘Human intellect, sir, has progressed since his time, is progressing, will progress.’", "\"인간의 지성은 말입니다, 선생, 그의 시대 이후로 발전해 왔고, 지금도 발전하고 있고, 앞으로도 발전할 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Shot beyond him, I mean,’ resumed Nicholas, ‘in quite another respect, for, whereas he brought within the magic circle of his genius, traditions peculiarly adapted for his purpose, and turned familiar things into constellations which should enlighten the world for ages, you drag within the magic circle of your dulness, subjects not at all adapted to the purposes of the stage, and debase as he exalted. For instance, you take the uncompleted books of living authors, fresh from their hands, wet from the press, cut, hack, and carve them to the powers and capacities of your actors, and the capability of your theatres, finish unfinished works, hastily and crudely vamp up ideas not yet worked out by their original projector, but which have doubtless cost him many thoughtful days and sleepless nights; by a comparison of incidents and dialogue, down to the very last word he may have written a fortnight before, do your utmost to anticipate his plot--all this without his permission, and against his will; and then, to crown the whole proceeding, publish in some mean pamphlet, an unmeaning farrago of garbled extracts from his work, to which your name as author, with the honourable distinction annexed, of having perpetrated a hundred other outrages of the same description. Now, show me the distinction between such pilfering as this, and picking a man’s pocket in the street: unless, indeed, it be, that the legislature has a regard for pocket-handkerchiefs, and leaves men’s brains, except when they are knocked out by violence, to take care of themselves.’", "\"제 말은, 그를 앞질러 갔다는 게,\" 니콜라스가 다시 말을 이었어. \"전혀 다른 의미에서 그렇다는 겁니다. 그는 자기 천재성의 마법 같은 울타리 안으로 자기 목적에 꼭 맞는 전설들을 끌어들여, 흔하디흔한 것들을 별자리로 바꿔 놓았지요. 오랜 세월 세상을 밝혀 줄 별자리로 말입니다. 그런데 선생들은 자기네 둔함의 울타리 안으로, 무대에는 전혀 어울리지도 않는 소재를 끌고 들어와서, 그가 끌어올린 것을 도리어 깎아내리지요. 예를 들면, 선생들은 살아 있는 작가의 아직 끝나지도 않은 책을, 그 사람 손에서 나온 지 얼마 안 돼 인쇄 잉크도 채 안 마른 걸 가져다가, 선생네 배우들의 능력과 역량, 그리고 극장 사정에 맞춰 잘라 내고 토막 내고 도려내지요. 끝나지도 않은 작품을 끝내 버리고, 원작자가 아직 풀어내지도 않은 착상을 허둥지둥 어설프게 갖다 붙입니다. 그 착상이란 게 분명 그 사람이 며칠을 골똘히 고민하고 밤잠을 설치며 짜낸 것일 텐데 말입니다. 게다가 그가 보름 전에 마지막으로 써 둔 글귀까지 사건이며 대사를 죄다 견주어 가면서, 그의 줄거리를 앞질러 발표하려고 갖은 애를 다 쓰지요 — 이 모든 걸 그 사람 허락도 없이, 그 사람 뜻을 거슬러 가면서 말입니다. 그러고는 이 모든 짓의 마무리로, 그 작품에서 토막 낸 글들을 알맹이 없이 뒤죽박죽 섞은 걸 어느 시시한 소책자로 펴내면서, 거기에 선생 이름을 작가랍시고 박아 넣는 거지요. 똑같은 짓을 백 번도 더 저질렀다는 그 명예로운 칭호까지 덧붙여서요. 자, 이런 식의 좀도둑질과 길거리에서 남의 호주머니를 터는 게 뭐가 다른지 말씀해 보십시오. 그저 입법자들이 손수건은 끔찍이 아끼면서, 사람의 머릿속만큼은 — 폭력으로 두들겨 맞아 박살 나는 경우 말고는 — 알아서 제 앞가림하라고 내버려 두는 거라면 또 모르지만요.\""]], [["‘Men must live, sir,’ said the literary gentleman, shrugging his shoulders.", "\"사람은 먹고살아야 하지 않습니까, 선생.\" 그 문인이 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했어."]], [["‘That would be an equally fair plea in both cases,’ replied Nicholas; ‘but if you put it upon that ground, I have nothing more to say, than, that if I were a writer of books, and you a thirsty dramatist, I would rather pay your tavern score for six months, large as it might be, than have a niche in the Temple of Fame with you for the humblest corner of my pedestal, through six hundred generations.’", "\"그건 두 경우 다 똑같이 그럴듯한 핑계겠지요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"하지만 굳이 그걸 근거로 삼으시겠다면, 저는 이 말밖에는 더 할 게 없습니다. 만약 제가 책을 쓰는 사람이고 선생이 목마른 극작가라면, 저는 선생의 술집 외상값을 여섯 달치나 — 그게 아무리 많더라도 — 대신 갚아 드리는 편을 택하겠습니다. 명예의 전당에서 육백 대를 두고 선생과 한자리를 차지하느니 말이지요. 그것도 제 좌대의 가장 초라한 구석 자리라 해도 말입니다.\""]], [["The conversation threatened to take a somewhat angry tone when it had arrived thus far, but Mrs.", "이쯤 이르자 대화가 다소 험악한 쪽으로 흘러갈 낌새였어. 그런데 마침 크럼블스 부인이 끼어들어 거친 충돌로 번지는 걸 막아 주었지."], ["Crummles opportunely interposed to prevent its leading to any violent outbreak, by making some inquiries of the literary gentleman relative to the plots of the six new pieces which he had written by contract to introduce the African Knife-swallower in his various unrivalled performances.", "부인은 그 문인에게 새 작품 여섯 편의 줄거리에 대해 이것저것 물었거든. 그건 아프리카 칼 삼키기 명인을 그 갖가지 천하무적 묘기 속에 등장시키려고 그가 계약을 맺고 쓴 작품들이었어."], ["This speedily engaged him in an animated conversation with that lady, in the interest of which, all recollection of his recent discussion with Nicholas very quickly evaporated.", "그러자 그는 곧장 부인과 활기찬 대화에 빠져들었고, 거기에 정신이 팔린 나머지 방금 니콜라스와 벌인 논쟁 따위는 순식간에 까맣게 잊어버렸지."]], [["The board being now clear of the more substantial articles of food, and punch, wine, and spirits being placed upon it and handed about, the guests, who had been previously conversing in little groups of three or four, gradually fell off into a dead silence, while the majority of those present glanced from time to time at Mr. Snittle Timberry, and the bolder spirits did not even hesitate to strike the table with their knuckles, and plainly intimate their expectations, by uttering such encouragements as ‘Now, Tim,’ ‘Wake up, Mr. Chairman,’ ‘All charged, sir, and waiting for a toast,’ and so forth.", "이제 식탁에서 든든한 음식들이 다 치워지고, 그 위에 펀치와 포도주와 독한 술이 놓여 두루 돌려졌어. 그러자 그전까지 서넛씩 작은 무리로 이야기를 나누던 손님들이 차츰 입을 다물어 쥐 죽은 듯 조용해졌지. 그리고 자리에 있던 사람들 대부분이 이따금 스니틀 팀베리 씨를 흘끔거렸고, 좀 더 대담한 이들은 손가락 마디로 식탁을 두드리는 것조차 서슴지 않으며 대놓고 기대를 내비쳤어. \"자, 팀,\" \"정신 차리세요, 좌장님,\" \"다들 잔 채웠습니다, 선생, 건배사만 기다리고 있어요\" 따위의 부추기는 말을 던지면서 말이지."]], [["To these remonstrances Mr.", "이런 채근에도 팀베리 씨는 가슴을 두드리고 숨을 헐떡이는 것 말고는 어떤 대꾸도 하지 않았어."], ["Timberry deigned no other rejoinder than striking his chest and gasping for breath, and giving many other indications of being still the victim of indisposition--for a man must not make himself too cheap either on the stage or off--while Mr.", "그러고는 자기가 아직도 병환에 시달리고 있다는 걸 갖가지로 내비쳤지 — 무대에서든 무대 밖에서든 사람이 너무 값싸게 굴어서는 안 되는 법이니까. 한편 크럼블스 씨는 앞으로 나올 건배가 바로 자기를 위한 것임을 빤히 알고 있었어."], ["Crummles, who knew full well that he would be the subject of the forthcoming toast, sat gracefully in his chair with his arm thrown carelessly over the back, and now and then lifted his glass to his mouth and drank a little punch, with the same air with which he was accustomed to take long draughts of nothing, out of the pasteboard goblets in banquet scenes.", "그래서 의자에 우아하게 앉아 한 팔을 등받이에 무심한 듯 걸치고 있다가, 이따금 잔을 입가로 들어 펀치를 조금씩 마셨지. 그 모습이 꼭 연회 장면에서 마분지 술잔으로 아무것도 안 든 걸 길게 들이켜던 그 태도 그대로였어."]], [["At length Mr. Snittle Timberry rose in the most approved attitude, with one hand in the breast of his waistcoat and the other on the nearest snuff-box, and having been received with great enthusiasm, proposed, with abundance of quotations, his friend Mr.", "마침내 스니틀 팀베리 씨가 더없이 그럴듯한 자세로 일어섰어."], ["Vincent Crummles: ending a pretty long speech by extending his right hand on one side and his left on the other, and severally calling upon Mr. and Mrs.", "한 손은 조끼 가슴께에 찔러 넣고, 다른 손은 가장 가까이 있는 담뱃갑 위에 얹은 채로 말이지."], ["Crummles to grasp the same. This done, Mr.", "그러고는 큰 환호를 받으며, 인용구를 잔뜩 곁들여 자기 친구 빈센트 크럼블스 씨를 위해 건배를 제안했어."], ["Vincent Crummles returned thanks, and that done, the African Swallower proposed Mrs.", "꽤 긴 연설을 마무리하면서 그는 오른팔을 한쪽으로, 왼팔을 다른 쪽으로 쭉 뻗어, 크럼블스 씨와 크럼블스 부인에게 각각 그 손을 잡으라고 청했지."], ["Vincent Crummles, in affecting terms. Then were heard loud moans and sobs from Mrs.", "이렇게 하고 나자 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 답사를 했고, 그게 끝나자 아프리카 칼 삼키기 명인이 감동적인 말로 빈센트 크럼블스 부인을 위해 건배를 제안했어."], ["Crummles and the ladies, despite of which that heroic woman insisted upon returning thanks herself, which she did, in a manner and in a speech which has never been surpassed and seldom equalled.", "그러자 크럼블스 부인과 여자 손님들에게서 크나큰 신음과 흐느낌이 터져 나왔지. 그런데도 그 영웅적인 여인은 한사코 직접 답사를 하겠다고 고집을 부렸고, 정말로 그렇게 했어."], ["It then became the duty of Mr. Snittle Timberry to give the young Crummleses, which he did; after which Mr.", "그 태도와 연설은 그 누구도 능가한 적 없고, 견줄 만한 이조차 거의 없을 만한 것이었지."], ["Vincent Crummles, as their father, addressed the company in a supplementary speech, enlarging on their virtues, amiabilities, and excellences, and wishing that they were the sons and daughter of every lady and gentleman present.", "그다음엔 스니틀 팀베리 씨가 어린 크럼블스 남매를 위해 건배를 제안할 차례였고, 그는 그렇게 했어. 그 뒤로 빈센트 크럼블스 씨가 아버지로서 보충 연설로 일동에게 말을 건넸지."], ["These solemnities having been succeeded by a decent interval, enlivened by musical and other entertainments, Mr.", "아이들의 덕성과 사랑스러움과 빼어남을 한껏 늘어놓으면서, 이 아이들이 자리에 있는 신사 숙녀 모두의 아들딸이었으면 좋겠다고 바랐어."], ["Crummles proposed that ornament of the profession, the African Swallower, his very dear friend, if he would allow him to call him so; which liberty (there being no particular reason why he should not allow it) the African Swallower graciously permitted.", "이런 엄숙한 순서들이 끝난 뒤엔 음악을 비롯한 여러 여흥으로 흥겨워진 적당한 간격이 이어졌고, 그러고 나서 크럼블스 씨가 그 직업의 자랑인 아프리카 칼 삼키기 명인을 위해 건배를 제안했어. 자기의 더없이 소중한 벗이라고, 그렇게 불러도 된다면 말이지."], ["The literary gentleman was then about to be drunk, but it being discovered that he had been drunk for some time in another acceptation of the term, and was then asleep on the stairs, the intention was abandoned, and the honour transferred to the ladies.", "그렇게 부르도록 허락하는 데 딱히 거리낄 이유가 없었으니, 아프리카 칼 삼키기 명인은 흔쾌히 그러라고 했지. 그다음엔 그 문인을 위해 건배하려 했는데, 알고 보니 그는 또 다른 의미로 진작부터 취해 있었고, 그때 이미 계단에서 잠들어 있는 게 발견됐어."], ["Finally, after a very long sitting, Mr Snittle Timberry vacated the chair, and the company with many adieux and embraces dispersed.", "그래서 그 뜻은 접고, 그 영예는 여자 손님들에게 돌렸지. 마침내 아주 오래 자리가 이어진 끝에 스니틀 팀베리 씨가 좌장 자리에서 일어났고, 일동은 숱한 작별 인사와 포옹을 나누며 흩어졌어."]], [["Nicholas waited to the last to give his little presents. When he had said goodbye all round and came to Mr.", "니콜라스는 작은 선물들을 건네려고 맨 마지막까지 남았어."], ["Crummles, he could not but mark the difference between their present separation and their parting at Portsmouth.", "모두에게 작별을 고하고 크럼블스 씨 차례가 되자, 그는 이번 이별과 포츠머스에서의 작별이 사뭇 다르다는 걸 느끼지 않을 수 없었지. 그의 그 연극배우다운 태도는 한 톨도 남아 있지 않았어."], ["Not a jot of his theatrical manner remained; he put out his hand with an air which, if he could have summoned it at will, would have made him the best actor of his day in homely parts, and when Nicholas shook it with the warmth he honestly felt, appeared thoroughly melted.", "그가 내민 손짓에는 어떤 기색이 어려 있었는데, 만약 그걸 마음대로 불러낼 수만 있었다면 그는 그 시대 소박한 배역에서 으뜸가는 배우가 됐을 거야. 그리고 니콜라스가 진심으로 느끼는 따뜻함을 담아 그 손을 맞잡자, 그는 완전히 마음이 녹아내린 듯했지."]], [["‘We were a very happy little company, Johnson,’ said poor Crummles.", "\"우리는 참 행복한 작은 극단이었지, 존슨.\" 가엾은 크럼블스가 말했어."], ["‘You and I never had a word.", "\"자네하고 나는 한 번도 다툰 적이 없었어."], ["I shall be very glad tomorrow morning to think that I saw you again, but now I almost wish you hadn’t come.’", "내일 아침이면 자네를 다시 봤다는 걸 떠올리며 무척 기뻐하겠지. 그래도 지금은 차라리 자네가 안 왔더라면 싶을 만큼 마음이 그렇구먼.\""]], [["Nicholas was about to return a cheerful reply, when he was greatly disconcerted by the sudden apparition of Mrs.", "니콜라스가 막 밝은 대답을 하려는데, 그러던 그를 그루든 부인이 불쑥 나타나 몹시 당황하게 만들었어."], ["Grudden, who it seemed had declined to attend the supper in order that she might rise earlier in the morning, and who now burst out of an adjoining bedroom, habited in very extraordinary white robes; and throwing her arms about his neck, hugged him with great affection.", "부인은 아침에 더 일찍 일어나려고 만찬에는 안 오겠다고 한 모양이었어. 그런데 이제 옆방 침실에서 아주 별난 흰 가운을 걸친 채 불쑥 뛰쳐나와, 그의 목에 두 팔을 두르고 무척이나 다정하게 끌어안았지."]], [["‘What!", "\"아니! 부인도 함께 가시는 겁니까?\""], ["Are you going too?’ said Nicholas, submitting with as good a grace as if she had been the finest young creature in the world.", "니콜라스가 말했어. 그러면서도 마치 그녀가 세상에서 가장 어여쁜 처녀라도 되는 양 점잖게 받아 주었지."]], [["‘Going?’ returned Mrs.", "\"가느냐고?\" 그루든 부인이 대꾸했어."], ["Grudden. ‘Lord ha’ mercy, what do you think they’d do without me?’", "\"어이구, 맙소사, 내가 없으면 저 사람들이 어쩌겠나?\""]], [["Nicholas submitted to another hug with even a better grace than before, if that were possible, and waving his hat as cheerfully as he could, took farewell of the Vincent Crummleses.", "니콜라스는 또 한 번 포옹을 받아 주었어. 그것도 아까보다 더, 그럴 수 있다면 말이지만, 한결 점잖은 태도로 말이야. 그러고는 되도록 쾌활하게 모자를 흔들어, 빈센트 크럼블스 일가에게 작별을 고했어."]], [["# CHAPTER 49", "# CHAPTER 49"]], [["Chronicles the further Proceedings of the Nickleby Family, and the Sequel of the Adventure of the Gentleman in the Small-clothes\nWhile Nicholas, absorbed in the one engrossing subject of interest which had recently opened upon him, occupied his leisure hours with thoughts of Madeline Bray, and in execution of the commissions which the anxiety of brother Charles in her behalf imposed upon him, saw her again and again, and each time with greater danger to his peace of mind and a more weakening effect upon the lofty resolutions he had formed, Mrs.", "니콜라스는 요즘 새롭게 눈앞에 펼쳐진 그 한 가지 일에만 온통 마음을 빼앗겨, 한가한 시간이면 매들린 브레이 생각에 잠기거나, 그녀를 위해 애쓰는 찰스 형제의 부탁을 들어주느라 그녀를 보고 또 봤어. 그리고 볼 때마다 마음의 평화가 점점 더 위태로워졌고, 굳게 세운 다짐도 점점 약해져 갔지."], ["Nickleby and Kate continued to live in peace and quiet, agitated by no other cares than those which were connected with certain harassing proceedings taken by Mr.", "그동안 니클비 부인과 케이트는 평화롭고 조용하게 지냈어. 스놀리 씨가 아들을 되찾겠다며 끈질기게 벌이는 소송과, 스마이크 본인을 걱정하는 마음 말고는 다른 근심거리가 없었지."], ["Snawley for the recovery of his son, and their anxiety for Smike himself, whose health, long upon the wane, began to be so much affected by apprehension and uncertainty as sometimes to occasion both them and Nicholas considerable uneasiness, and even alarm.", "스마이크는 오래전부터 건강이 나빠지고 있었는데, 불안과 막막함에 시달리면서 상태가 점점 더 나빠졌어. 그래서 가끔은 그들과 니콜라스 모두 상당히 불안해했고, 심지어 깜짝 놀라기도 했지."]], [["It was no complaint or murmur on the part of the poor fellow himself that thus disturbed them.", "그들을 이렇게 마음 졸이게 한 건 가엾은 그 청년의 불평이나 투정 때문이 아니었어."], ["Ever eager to be employed in such slight services as he could render, and always anxious to repay his benefactors with cheerful and happy looks, less friendly eyes might have seen in him no cause for any misgiving.", "스마이크는 자기가 할 수 있는 자잘한 일이라도 늘 기꺼이 맡으려 했고, 은인들에게 밝고 행복한 얼굴로 보답하려고 언제나 애썼거든. 그러니 그를 별로 아끼지 않는 사람의 눈에는 걱정할 만한 구석이 하나도 없어 보였을 거야."], ["But there were times, and often too, when the sunken eye was too bright, the hollow cheek too flushed, the breath too thick and heavy in its course, the frame too feeble and exhausted, to escape their regard and notice.", "하지만 어떤 때, 그것도 자주, 푹 꺼진 눈이 너무 반짝였고, 홀쭉한 뺨이 너무 발갛게 달아올랐고, 숨이 너무 거칠고 무겁게 오갔고, 몸이 너무 약하고 지쳐 보였어. 그 가족의 눈을 피할 수가 없을 만큼 말이야."]], [["There is a dread disease which so prepares its victim, as it were, for death; which so refines it of its grosser aspect, and throws around familiar looks unearthly indications of the coming change; a dread disease, in which the struggle between soul and body is so gradual, quiet, and solemn, and the result so sure, that day by day, and grain by grain, the mortal part wastes and withers away, so that the spirit grows light and sanguine with its lightening load, and, feeling immortality at hand, deems it but a new term of mortal life; a disease in which death and life are so strangely blended, that death takes the glow and hue of life, and life the gaunt and grisly form of death; a disease which medicine never cured, wealth never warded off, or poverty could boast exemption from; which sometimes moves in giant strides, and sometimes at a tardy sluggish pace, but, slow or quick, is ever sure and certain.", "세상에는 무서운 병이 하나 있어. 마치 희생자를 죽음에 맞게 다듬어 놓는 듯한 병이지. 그 추한 겉모습을 곱게 가다듬고, 낯익은 얼굴에 다가올 변화의 이 세상 것 같지 않은 기운을 둘러놓는 병이야. 영혼과 몸의 싸움이 어찌나 서서히, 조용히, 엄숙하게 이뤄지는지, 그리고 그 결과가 어찌나 확실한지, 하루하루, 한 알 한 알 죽어 가는 부분이 닳고 시들어 가. 그래서 짐이 가벼워질수록 영혼은 가뿐하고 밝아지지. 그리고 영원한 삶이 가까이 왔음을 느끼면서도 그것을 그저 새로운 인생의 한 단계쯤으로 여겨. 죽음과 삶이 어찌나 이상하게 뒤섞이는지, 죽음은 삶의 빛과 색을 띠고, 삶은 죽음의 앙상하고 끔찍한 형상을 띠는 병이야. 약으로도 결코 고친 적이 없고, 부자라고 막을 수도 없으며, 가난한 이라고 피할 수도 없는 병. 어떤 때는 성큼성큼 거인의 걸음으로 다가오고, 어떤 때는 굼뜨고 느릿느릿 다가오지만, 느리든 빠르든 언제나 어김없고 확실한 병이지."]], [["It was with some faint reference in his own mind to this disorder, though he would by no means admit it, even to himself, that Nicholas had already carried his faithful companion to a physician of great repute.", "비록 자기 자신에게조차 결코 인정하려 하지 않았지만, 니콜라스는 마음 한구석에 이 병을 어렴풋이 떠올리며 이미 충실한 동무를 명망 높은 의사에게 데려갔었어."], ["There was no cause for immediate alarm, he said.", "의사는 당장 놀랄 일은 없다고 했어."], ["There were no present symptoms which could be deemed conclusive.", "단정 지을 만한 증상이 지금은 없다는 거였지."], ["The constitution had been greatly tried and injured in childhood, but still it MIGHT not be--and that was all.", "어릴 때 몸이 크게 시달리고 상하긴 했지만, 그래도 그 병이 아닐 수도 있다고 했어. 그게 전부였지."]], [["But he seemed to grow no worse, and, as it was not difficult to find a reason for these symptoms of illness in the shock and agitation he had recently undergone, Nicholas comforted himself with the hope that his poor friend would soon recover. This hope his mother and sister shared with him; and as the object of their joint solicitude seemed to have no uneasiness or despondency for himself, but each day answered with a quiet smile that he felt better than he had upon the day before, their fears abated, and the general happiness was by degrees restored.", "하지만 스마이크는 더 나빠지는 것 같지도 않았어. 그리고 그가 최근에 겪은 충격과 동요에서 이런 병색의 이유를 찾는 게 어렵지 않았으니, 니콜라스는 가엾은 친구가 곧 나으리라는 희망으로 스스로를 다독였지. 어머니와 누이도 그 희망을 함께 품었어. 그리고 모두가 한마음으로 걱정하는 그 당사자가 정작 본인은 불안해하거나 풀 죽은 기색이 없었거든. 오히려 날마다 조용히 미소 지으며 어제보다 한결 나은 것 같다고 대답했어. 그러니 그들의 두려움은 가라앉았고, 집안의 행복도 차츰 되살아났지."]], [["Many and many a time in after years did Nicholas look back to this period of his life, and tread again the humble quiet homely scenes that rose up as of old before him. Many and many a time, in the twilight of a summer evening, or beside the flickering winter’s fire--but not so often or so sadly then--would his thoughts wander back to these old days, and dwell with a pleasant sorrow upon every slight remembrance which they brought crowding home. The little room in which they had so often sat long after it was dark, figuring such happy futures; Kate’s cheerful voice and merry laugh; how, if she were from home, they used to sit and watch for her return scarcely breaking silence but to say how dull it seemed without her; the glee with which poor Smike would start from the darkened corner where he used to sit, and hurry to admit her, and the tears they often saw upon his face, half wondering to see them too, and he so pleased and happy; every little incident, and even slight words and looks of those old days little heeded then, but well remembered when busy cares and trials were quite forgotten, came fresh and thick before him many and many a time, and, rustling above the dusty growth of years, came back green boughs of yesterday.", "훗날 여러 해가 지나는 동안 니콜라스는 자기 인생의 이 시절을 몇 번이고 되돌아보았어. 옛날처럼 눈앞에 떠오르는 그 소박하고 조용하고 정겨운 장면들을 다시금 거닐었지. 몇 번이고, 여름 저녁 어스름 속에서, 혹은 가물거리는 겨울 난롯가에서 — 그때는 이만큼 자주도, 이만큼 슬프게도 떠오르진 않았지만 — 그의 생각은 그 옛 시절로 거슬러 올라가, 그 시절이 잔뜩 몰고 오는 자잘한 기억 하나하나에 즐거운 슬픔으로 머물곤 했어. 날이 어두워진 뒤에도 한참을 함께 앉아 그런 행복한 미래를 그려 보던 그 작은 방. 케이트의 명랑한 목소리와 즐거운 웃음소리. 케이트가 집을 비우면 식구들이 둘러앉아 그녀가 돌아오기를 기다리며, 케이트가 없으니 얼마나 적적한지 그 말이나 할 때 말고는 좀처럼 침묵을 깨지 않던 일. 가엾은 스마이크가 늘 앉아 있던 어두운 구석에서 신이 나서 벌떡 일어나 문을 열어 주러 달려가던 모습. 그리고 그의 얼굴에서 자주 보이던 눈물. 그 자신도 눈물이 흐르는 걸 보고 반쯤 의아해하면서도, 그렇게 기뻐하고 행복해하던 모습. 그 시절의 자잘한 사건 하나하나, 그때는 거의 마음에 두지 않았지만 분주한 근심과 시련을 까맣게 잊은 뒤에야 또렷이 떠오르는 사소한 말과 표정까지, 몇 번이고 생생하고도 무성하게 그의 앞에 되살아났어. 그리고 세월의 먼지 쌓인 더께 위로 살랑이며, 어제의 푸른 가지들이 되돌아왔지."]], [["But there were other persons associated with these recollections, and many changes came about before they had being.", "하지만 이 추억들에 얽힌 다른 사람들도 있었고, 그 추억이 생기기까지 많은 변화가 있었어."], ["A necessary reflection for the purposes of these adventures, which at once subside into their accustomed train, and shunning all flighty anticipations or wayward wanderings, pursue their steady and decorous course.", "이 이야기를 풀어 나가기 위해 짚어 둬야 할 대목이지. 이 이야기는 곧 익숙한 흐름으로 가라앉아, 들뜬 예감이나 변덕스러운 곁길을 모두 피하고, 차분하고 점잖은 걸음을 이어 갈 거야."]], [["If the brothers Cheeryble, as they found Nicholas worthy of trust and confidence, bestowed upon him every day some new and substantial mark of kindness, they were not less mindful of those who depended on him.", "치어리블 형제는 니콜라스가 믿고 신뢰할 만한 사람임을 알게 되면서 날마다 그에게 새롭고 든든한 친절을 베풀었어."], ["Various little presents to Mrs.", "그러면서 그에게 딸린 사람들도 못지않게 챙겼지."], ["Nickleby, always of the very things they most required, tended in no slight degree to the improvement and embellishment of the cottage.", "니클비 부인에게 자잘한 선물을 이것저것 보냈는데, 늘 그 집에 가장 필요한 물건들이었어. 그래서 그 오두막은 적잖이 살림이 나아지고 보기에도 좋아졌지."], ["Kate’s little store of trinkets became quite dazzling; and for company!", "케이트의 자그마한 장신구함은 눈이 부실 만큼 가득 찼어. 그리고 말동무로 말할 것 같으면!"], ["If brother Charles and brother Ned failed to look in for at least a few minutes every Sunday, or one evening in the week, there was Mr.", "찰스 형제와 네드 형제가 일요일마다, 아니면 한 주에 한 번 저녁에 적어도 잠깐씩 들르지 않는 날이 없었어. 게다가 팀 링킨워터 씨가 있었지."], ["Tim Linkinwater (who had never made half-a-dozen other acquaintances in all his life, and who took such delight in his new friends as no words can express) constantly coming and going in his evening walks, and stopping to rest; while Mr.", "그는 평생 다른 사람과 여섯 명도 채 사귀어 본 적이 없는 사람인데, 새 친구들을 어찌나 좋아했는지 말로 다 할 수 없을 정도였어. 그래서 저녁 산책길에 끊임없이 오가며 쉬어 가곤 했지."], ["Frank Cheeryble happened, by some strange conjunction of circumstances, to be passing the door on some business or other at least three nights in the week.", "그런가 하면 프랭크 치어리블 씨도 무슨 묘한 우연들이 겹쳤는지, 무슨 볼일이 있다며 한 주에 적어도 사흘 밤은 그 집 문 앞을 지나가곤 했어."]], [["‘He is the most attentive young man I ever saw, Kate,’ said Mrs.", "\"내 평생 그렇게 자상한 청년은 본 적이 없구나, 케이트.\""], ["Nickleby to her daughter one evening, when this last-named gentleman had been the subject of the worthy lady’s eulogium for some time, and Kate had sat perfectly silent.", "어느 날 저녁 니클비 부인이 딸에게 말했어. 한참 동안 그 부인이 마지막에 말한 그 신사를 침이 마르도록 칭찬하는데도, 케이트는 입을 꾹 다물고 앉아만 있었거든."]], [["‘Attentive, mama!’ rejoined Kate.", "\"자상하다고요, 엄마?\" 케이트가 되물었어."]], [["‘Bless my heart, Kate!’ cried Mrs.", "\"어머나, 케이트!\" 니클비 부인이 늘 그렇듯 불쑥 소리쳤어."], ["Nickleby, with her wonted suddenness, ‘what a colour you have got; why, you’re quite flushed!’", "\"얼굴이 왜 그렇게 빨개졌니. 아니, 얼굴이 온통 달아올랐잖니!\""]], [["‘Oh, mama!", "\"어머, 엄마!"], ["what strange things you fancy!’", "별 이상한 생각을 다 하시네요!\""]], [["‘It wasn’t fancy, Kate, my dear, I’m certain of that,’ returned her mother.", "\"이상한 생각이 아니란다, 얘야, 내 분명히 봤다.\" 어머니가 대꾸했어."], ["‘However, it’s gone now at any rate, so it don’t much matter whether it was or not.", "\"어쨌든 이젠 가셨으니까, 그랬는지 안 그랬는지는 그다지 중요하지 않구나."], ["What was it we were talking about?", "우리가 무슨 얘길 하고 있었지?"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["Mr. Frank.", "프랭크 씨 말이야."], ["I never saw such attention in MY life, never.’", "내 평생 그렇게 자상한 사람은 본 적이 없어, 한 번도.\""]], [["‘Surely you are not serious,’ returned Kate, colouring again; and this time beyond all dispute.", "\"설마 진심으로 하시는 말씀은 아니죠.\" 케이트가 다시 얼굴을 붉히며 대꾸했어. 이번에는 누가 봐도 분명했지."]], [["‘Not serious!’ returned Mrs.", "\"진심이 아니라니!\""], ["Nickleby; ‘why shouldn’t I be serious?", "니클비 부인이 받아쳤어. \"왜 진심이 아니겠니?"], ["I’m sure I never was more serious.", "이보다 더 진심인 적이 없었다."], ["I will say that his politeness and attention to me is one of the most becoming, gratifying, pleasant things I have seen for a very long time.", "그분이 나한테 보이는 그 예의와 자상함이야말로 아주 오랜만에 보는, 가장 흐뭇하고 보기 좋고 기분 좋은 일이라고 말해 두마."], ["You don’t often meet with such behaviour in young men, and it strikes one more when one does meet with it.’", "요즘 젊은이들한테선 그런 행동을 좀처럼 보기 힘들지. 그러니까 막상 보게 되면 더 마음에 와닿는 거란다.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아! 엄마한테 자상하단 말씀이군요.\""], ["attention to YOU, mama,’ rejoined Kate quickly--‘oh yes.’", "케이트가 얼른 받았어. \"아, 네.\""]], [["‘Dear me, Kate,’ retorted Mrs.", "\"세상에, 케이트.\""], ["Nickleby, ‘what an extraordinary girl you are!", "니클비 부인이 되받았어. \"넌 참 별난 애로구나!"], ["Was it likely I should be talking of his attention to anybody else?", "내가 그분이 다른 사람한테 자상하단 얘길 하고 있었겠니?"], ["I declare I’m quite sorry to think he should be in love with a German lady, that I am.’", "그분이 독일 아가씨를 사랑한다고 생각하니 정말이지 마음이 안됐구나, 그래.\""]], [["‘He said very positively that it was no such thing, mama,’ returned Kate.", "\"그분은 그런 거 아니라고 아주 분명히 말씀하셨잖아요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 대꾸했어."], ["‘Don’t you remember his saying so that very first night he came here?", "\"이 집에 처음 오신 날 밤에 그렇게 말씀하신 거 기억 안 나세요? 게다가,\""], ["Besides,’ she added, in a more gentle tone, ‘why should WE be sorry if it is the case?", "케이트가 한결 부드러운 말투로 덧붙였어. \"설령 그렇다 한들 우리가 왜 안타까워해야 해요?"], ["What is it to us, mama?’", "그게 우리랑 무슨 상관인데요, 엄마?\""]], [["‘Nothing to US, Kate, perhaps,’ said Mrs.", "\"우리랑은 상관없을지 몰라도, 케이트.\" 니클비 부인이 힘주어 말했어."], ["Nickleby, emphatically; ‘but something to ME, I confess.", "\"나한테는 상관이 있단다, 솔직히 말하면. 나는 영국 사람이라면 철저히 영국 사람이었으면 좋겠어."], ["I like English people to be thorough English people, and not half English and half I don’t know what.", "반쯤은 영국 사람이고 반쯤은 뭔지도 모를 그런 거 말고. 다음에 오면 대놓고 말해야겠다."], ["I shall tell him point-blank next time he comes, that I wish he would marry one of his own country-women; and see what he says to that.’", "자기 나라 여자랑 결혼했으면 좋겠다고 말이야. 그러면 뭐라고 하는지 좀 보자꾸나.\""]], [["‘Pray don’t think of such a thing, mama,’ returned Kate, hastily; ‘not for the world.", "\"제발 그런 생각 마세요, 엄마.\" 케이트가 다급히 말했어."], ["Consider.", "\"절대로요."], ["How very--’", "생각해 보세요. 그건 너무나—\""]], [["‘Well, my dear, how very what?’ said Mrs.", "\"그래, 얘야, 너무나 뭐?\""], ["Nickleby, opening her eyes in great astonishment.", "니클비 부인이 깜짝 놀라 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 말했어."]], [["Before Kate had returned any reply, a queer little double knock announced that Miss La Creevy had called to see them; and when Miss La Creevy presented herself, Mrs.", "케이트가 미처 대답하기도 전에, 묘하게 두 번 두드리는 노크 소리가 라 크리비 양이 그들을 보러 왔음을 알렸어."], ["Nickleby, though strongly disposed to be argumentative on the previous question, forgot all about it in a gush of supposes about the coach she had come by; supposing that the man who drove must have been either the man in the shirt-sleeves or the man with the black eye; that whoever he was, he hadn’t found that parasol she left inside last week; that no doubt they had stopped a long while at the Halfway House, coming down; or that perhaps being full, they had come straight on; and, lastly, that they, surely, must have passed Nicholas on the road.", "그리고 라 크리비 양이 모습을 드러내자, 니클비 부인은 앞서 그 문제로 한바탕 따져 보고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았지만, 라 크리비 양이 타고 온 마차에 관한 온갖 추측을 쏟아 내느라 그 일을 까맣게 잊고 말았어. 마차를 몬 사람이 셔츠 바람의 그 남자였을 거라느니, 아니면 눈에 멍든 그 남자였을 거라느니, 누구였든 간에 지난주에 안에 두고 내린 그 양산은 못 찾았을 거라느니, 내려오는 길에 분명 하프웨이 하우스에서 한참 쉬었을 거라느니, 아니 어쩌면 손님이 꽉 차서 곧장 내처 왔을지도 모른다느니, 그리고 마지막으로 오는 길에 틀림없이 니콜라스를 지나쳤을 거라느니 하면서 말이야."]], [["‘I saw nothing of him,’ answered Miss La Creevy; ‘but I saw that dear old soul Mr.", "\"그 사람은 못 봤어요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대답했어."], ["Linkinwater.’", "\"그런데 그 정겨운 노인장, 링킨워터 씨는 봤지 뭐예요.\""]], [["‘Taking his evening walk, and coming on to rest here, before he turns back to the city, I’ll be bound!’ said Mrs.", "\"저녁 산책을 하다가, 시내로 돌아가기 전에 여기 들러서 쉬려고 오는 거겠죠, 틀림없어요!\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘I should think he was,’ returned Miss La Creevy; ‘especially as young Mr.", "\"그런 것 같던데요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대답했어."], ["Cheeryble was with him.’", "\"특히 젊은 치어리블 씨도 함께 있었으니까요.\""]], [["‘Surely that is no reason why Mr.", "\"그렇다고 링킨워터 씨가 여기 올 이유가 되진 않잖아요.\""], ["Linkinwater should be coming here,’ said Kate.", "케이트가 말했어."]], [["‘Why I think it is, my dear,’ said Miss La Creevy.", "\"아니, 난 그게 이유가 된다고 봐, 얘야.\" 라 크리비 양이 말했어."], ["‘For a young man, Mr Frank is not a very great walker; and I observe that he generally falls tired, and requires a good long rest, when he has come as far as this.", "\"프랭크 씨는 젊은이치고 그리 잘 걷는 사람이 아니거든. 그런데 여기까지 오면 으레 지쳐서 한참을 푹 쉬어야 하더라고."], ["But where is my friend?’ said the little woman, looking about, after having glanced slyly at Kate.", "그런데 내 친구는 어디 있죠?\" 그 자그마한 여인이 케이트를 슬쩍 곁눈질하더니 두리번거리며 말했어."], ["‘He has not been run away with again, has he?’", "\"또 어디 끌려간 건 아니겠죠?\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["where is Mr. Smike?’ said Mrs.", "스마이크 씨는 어디 있지?\""], ["Nickleby; ‘he was here this instant.’", "니클비 부인이 말했어. \"방금 전까지 여기 있었는데.\""]], [["Upon further inquiry, it turned out, to the good lady’s unbounded astonishment, that Smike had, that moment, gone upstairs to bed.", "좀 더 알아보니, 이 마음씨 좋은 부인이 깜짝 놀랄 만하게도, 스마이크는 방금 위층으로 자러 올라갔다는 거였어."]], [["‘Well now,’ said Mrs.", "\"어머나,\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby, ‘he is the strangest creature!", "\"정말 별난 사람이라니까! 지난 화요일에도—화요일이었나?"], ["Last Tuesday--was it Tuesday?", "그래, 분명 화요일이었어."], ["Yes, to be sure it was; you recollect, Kate, my dear, the very last time young Mr.", "기억나지, 케이트, 얘야, 젊은 치어리블 씨가 바로 지난번에 여기 왔던 날 말이야—지난 화요일 밤에도 똑같이 이상하게 사라졌잖니. 바로 문에서 노크 소리가 나던 그 순간에."], ["Cheeryble was here--last Tuesday night he went off in just the same strange way, at the very moment the knock came to the door.", "사람을 싫어해서 그런 건 아닐 거야. 니콜라스를 좋아하는 사람이면 누구든 늘 좋아하니까."], ["It cannot be that he don’t like company, because he is always fond of people who are fond of Nicholas, and I am sure young Mr Cheeryble is.", "그리고 젊은 치어리블 씨가 그런 사람인 건 분명하고. 그런데 정말 이상한 건, 그 사람이 잠을 자러 가는 게 아니라는 거야."], ["And the strangest thing is, that he does not go to bed; therefore it cannot be because he is tired.", "그러니까 피곤해서 그런 것도 아니지. 잠자리에 안 드는 걸 내가 아는데, 내 방이 바로 옆방이거든."], ["I know he doesn’t go to bed, because my room is the next one, and when I went upstairs last Tuesday, hours after him, I found that he had not even taken his shoes off; and he had no candle, so he must have sat moping in the dark all the time.", "지난 화요일에 그 사람보다 몇 시간이나 늦게 위층에 올라갔는데, 보니까 신발도 안 벗고 있더라니까. 게다가 촛불도 없었어."], ["Now, upon my word,’ said Mrs.", "그러니까 내내 어둠 속에 우두커니 앉아 있었던 거지. 정말이지,\""], ["Nickleby, ‘when I come to think of it, that’s very extraordinary!’", "니클비 부인이 말했어. \"곰곰이 생각해 보니, 그거 참 희한한 일이구나!\""]], [["As the hearers did not echo this sentiment, but remained profoundly silent, either as not knowing what to say, or as being unwilling to interrupt, Mrs. Nickleby pursued the thread of her discourse after her own fashion.", "듣는 사람들이 이 말에 맞장구를 치기는커녕, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰라서인지, 아니면 말을 끊고 싶지 않아서인지 깊은 침묵에 잠겨 있자, 니클비 부인은 제 방식대로 이야기 실타래를 이어 갔어."]], [["‘I hope,’ said that lady, ‘that this unaccountable conduct may not be the beginning of his taking to his bed and living there all his life, like the Thirsty Woman of Tutbury, or the Cock-lane Ghost, or some of those extraordinary creatures.", "\"이렇게 영문 모를 행동이,\" 그 부인이 말했어."], ["One of them had some connection with our family.", "\"혹시 침대에 드러누워 평생을 거기서 보내는 일의 시작은 아니었으면 좋겠구나."], ["I forget, without looking back to some old letters I have upstairs, whether it was my great-grandfather who went to school with the Cock-lane Ghost, or the Thirsty Woman of Tutbury who went to school with my grandmother.", "터트베리의 목마른 여인이나 코크 골목의 유령처럼 말이야. 그런 별난 사람들 가운데 하나처럼."], ["Miss La Creevy, you know, of course.", "그중 한 사람은 우리 집안과 무슨 인연이 있었지."], ["Which was it that didn’t mind what the clergyman said?", "위층에 둔 오래된 편지들을 다시 들춰 보지 않고는 잘 모르겠는데, 우리 증조할아버지가 코크 골목의 유령과 같은 학교를 다녔던 건지, 아니면 터트베리의 목마른 여인이 우리 할머니와 같은 학교를 다녔던 건지 헷갈리는구나. 라 크리비 양, 당연히 아시겠죠."], ["The Cock-lane Ghost or the Thirsty Woman of Tutbury?’", "목사님이 하는 말을 귀담아듣지 않았던 게 누구였죠? 코크 골목의 유령이었나요, 터트베리의 목마른 여인이었나요?\""]], [["‘The Cock-lane Ghost, I believe.’", "\"코크 골목의 유령이었던 것 같은데요.\""]], [["‘Then I have no doubt,’ said Mrs.", "\"그럼 틀림없어요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby, ‘that it was with him my great-grandfather went to school; for I know the master of his school was a dissenter, and that would, in a great measure, account for the Cock-lane Ghost’s behaving in such an improper manner to the clergyman when he grew up.", "\"우리 증조할아버지가 같은 학교를 다닌 게 바로 그 유령이었던 거예요. 그분이 다닌 학교 선생이 비국교도였다는 걸 내가 아는데, 그러니까 코크 골목의 유령이 어른이 되어서 목사님한테 그렇게 버릇없이 군 게 다 그 탓이었던 거죠."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["Train up a Ghost--child, I mean--’", "유령을 가르치라—아니, 아이 말이에요—\""]], [["Any further reflections on this fruitful theme were abruptly cut short by the arrival of Tim Linkinwater and Mr.", "이 풍성한 주제에 대한 더 이상의 생각은 팀 링킨워터와 프랭크 치어리블 씨가 들이닥치는 바람에 뚝 끊기고 말았어."], ["Frank Cheeryble; in the hurry of receiving whom, Mrs. Nickleby speedily lost sight of everything else.", "그들을 맞이하느라 부산을 떠는 통에 니클비 부인은 다른 모든 걸 후딱 잊어버렸지."]], [["‘I am so sorry Nicholas is not at home,’ said Mrs.", "\"니콜라스가 집에 없어서 정말 안타깝네요.\" 니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["Nickleby. ‘Kate, my dear, you must be both Nicholas and yourself.’", "\"케이트, 얘야, 네가 니콜라스 노릇도 하고 네 노릇도 해야겠다.\""]], [["‘Miss Nickleby need be but herself,’ said Frank.", "\"니클비 양은 그저 자기 자신이기만 하면 됩니다.\" 프랭크가 말했어."], ["‘I--if I may venture to say so--oppose all change in her.’", "\"저는—이렇게 말씀드려도 괜찮다면—그분이 조금이라도 바뀌는 건 반대입니다.\""]], [["‘Then at all events she shall press you to stay,’ returned Mrs.", "\"그렇다면 어쨌든 케이트가 더 계시라고 붙잡아야겠네요.\""], ["Nickleby. ‘Mr.", "니클비 부인이 대꾸했어."], ["Linkinwater says ten minutes, but I cannot let you go so soon; Nicholas would be very much vexed, I am sure.", "\"링킨워터 씨는 십 분이라고 하시지만, 그렇게 빨리 보내 드릴 순 없죠. 니콜라스가 무척 서운해할 거예요, 분명히."], ["Kate, my dear!’", "케이트, 얘야!\""]], [["In obedience to a great number of nods, and winks, and frowns of extra significance, Kate added her entreaties that the visitors would remain; but it was observable that she addressed them exclusively to Tim Linkinwater; and there was, besides, a certain embarrassment in her manner, which, although it was as far from impairing its graceful character as the tinge it communicated to her cheek was from diminishing her beauty, was obvious at a glance even to Mrs.", "유난히 의미심장한 고갯짓과 눈짓과 찌푸린 표정을 한껏 받은 케이트는 손님들에게 더 머물러 달라고 거들었어. 그런데 가만 보니 케이트는 그 청을 오로지 팀 링킨워터에게만 건네는 거였어."], ["Nickleby.", "게다가 그 태도에는 어딘가 어색한 구석이 있었지. 비록 그 어색함이 케이트의 우아함을 조금도 해치지 않았고, 뺨에 번진 발그레함이 미모를 조금도 깎아내리지 않았지만, 니클비 부인조차 한눈에 알아챌 정도였어."], ["Not being of a very speculative character, however, save under circumstances when her speculations could be put into words and uttered aloud, that discreet matron attributed the emotion to the circumstance of her daughter’s not happening to have her best frock on: ‘though I never saw her look better, certainly,’ she reflected at the same time.", "하지만 그 신중한 부인은 자기 추측을 말로 옮겨 큰 소리로 떠벌릴 수 있는 경우가 아니고서는 그리 추리에 밝은 사람이 아니었거든. 그래서 그 묘한 기색을 두고, 딸이 마침 제일 좋은 옷을 안 입고 있어서 그러려니 하고 넘겨짚었어."], ["Having settled the question in this way, and being most complacently satisfied that in this, and in all other instances, her conjecture could not fail to be the right one, Mrs.", "\"그래도 저렇게 예뻐 보인 적이 없긴 한데, 그건 분명하지.\" 부인은 그러면서도 동시에 이렇게 생각했지."], ["Nickleby dismissed it from her thoughts, and inwardly congratulated herself on being so shrewd and knowing.", "이런 식으로 문제를 정리하고 나서, 이 경우든 다른 모든 경우든 자기 짐작이 틀림없이 옳다고 흐뭇하게 믿어 의심치 않으며, 니클비 부인은 그 생각을 머리에서 떨쳐 냈어. 그리고 자기가 이렇게 영민하고 눈치 빠른 사람이라는 데 속으로 흐뭇해했지."]], [["Nicholas did not come home nor did Smike reappear; but neither circumstance, to say the truth, had any great effect upon the little party, who were all in the best humour possible.", "니콜라스는 집에 돌아오지 않았고, 스마이크도 다시 나타나지 않았어. 그렇지만 솔직히 말하면, 그 어느 쪽도 이 작은 모임에 별다른 영향을 끼치진 못했어."], ["Indeed, there sprung up quite a flirtation between Miss La Creevy and Tim Linkinwater, who said a thousand jocose and facetious things, and became, by degrees, quite gallant, not to say tender.", "다들 더없이 기분이 좋았거든. 정말이지 라 크리비 양과 팀 링킨워터 사이에는 제법 시시덕거리는 분위기가 피어올랐어."], ["Little Miss La Creevy, on her part, was in high spirits, and rallied Tim on having remained a bachelor all his life with so much success, that Tim was actually induced to declare, that if he could get anybody to have him, he didn’t know but what he might change his condition even yet.", "팀이 우스갯소리와 익살을 수도 없이 늘어놓더니, 차츰 자상하다 못해 다정하다고 할 만큼 멋을 부렸지."], ["Miss La Creevy earnestly recommended a lady she knew, who would exactly suit Mr.", "자그마한 라 크리비 양도 한껏 들떠서, 팀이 평생 그렇게 잘도 총각으로 지내 왔다고 놀려 댔어."], ["Linkinwater, and had a very comfortable property of her own; but this latter qualification had very little effect upon Tim, who manfully protested that fortune would be no object with him, but that true worth and cheerfulness of disposition were what a man should look for in a wife, and that if he had these, he could find money enough for the moderate wants of both.", "어찌나 그러는지 팀이 급기야, 누가 자기를 받아 주기만 한다면 지금이라도 처지를 바꿔 볼 수 있을지 모른다고 선언하기에 이르렀지. 라 크리비 양이 자기가 아는 부인을 진지하게 추천했는데, 링킨워터 씨에게 꼭 맞고 게다가 제 앞으로 꽤 넉넉한 재산도 있다는 거였어."], ["This avowal was considered so honourable to Tim, that neither Mrs.", "하지만 이 마지막 조건은 팀에게 별 효과가 없었어."], ["Nickleby nor Miss La Creevy could sufficiently extol it; and stimulated by their praises, Tim launched out into several other declarations also manifesting the disinterestedness of his heart, and a great devotion to the fair sex: which were received with no less approbation.", "팀은 사내답게 펄쩍 뛰며, 자기한테 재산 따위는 중요하지 않다고, 사내가 아내에게서 찾아야 할 건 참된 됨됨이와 밝은 성품이라고, 그것만 갖추면 두 사람의 알맞은 살림에 쓸 돈이야 자기가 넉넉히 마련할 수 있다고 했지. 이 고백은 어찌나 팀의 명예를 드높이는 것이었는지, 니클비 부인도 라 크리비 양도 칭찬을 아끼지 않았어."], ["This was done and said with a comical mixture of jest and earnest, and, leading to a great amount of laughter, made them very merry indeed.", "그리고 그 칭찬에 신이 난 팀은 자기 마음의 욕심 없음과 여성에 대한 깊은 헌신을 드러내는 다른 선언들을 줄줄이 쏟아 냈고, 그것 역시 못지않게 박수를 받았지. 이 모든 말과 행동에는 우스개와 진심이 익살스럽게 뒤섞여 있어서, 한바탕 웃음을 자아냈고, 정말이지 다들 즐거워졌어."]], [["Kate was commonly the life and soul of the conversation at home; but she was more silent than usual upon this occasion (perhaps because Tim and Miss La Creevy engrossed so much of it), and, keeping aloof from the talkers, sat at the window watching the shadows as the evening closed in, and enjoying the quiet beauty of the night, which seemed to have scarcely less attractions to Frank, who first lingered near, and then sat down beside, her. No doubt, there are a great many things to be said appropriate to a summer evening, and no doubt they are best said in a low voice, as being most suitable to the peace and serenity of the hour; long pauses, too, at times, and then an earnest word or so, and then another interval of silence which, somehow, does not seem like silence either, and perhaps now and then a hasty turning away of the head, or drooping of the eyes towards the ground, all these minor circumstances, with a disinclination to have candles introduced and a tendency to confuse hours with minutes, are doubtless mere influences of the time, as many lovely lips can clearly testify. Neither is there the slightest reason why Mrs. Nickleby should have expressed surprise when, candles being at length brought in, Kate’s bright eyes were unable to bear the light which obliged her to avert her face, and even to leave the room for some short time; because, when one has sat in the dark so long, candles ARE dazzling, and nothing can be more strictly natural than that such results should be produced, as all well-informed young people know. For that matter, old people know it too, or did know it once, but they forget these things sometimes, and more’s the pity.", "평소 집안에서 대화의 활력이자 중심은 으레 케이트였어. 그런데 이날만큼은 평소보다 말수가 적었지(아마 팀과 라 크리비 양이 대화를 워낙 독차지해서였을 거야). 케이트는 이야기 무리에서 떨어져 창가에 앉아, 저녁이 깃들면서 짙어 가는 그림자들을 바라보며 밤의 고요한 아름다움을 즐기고 있었어. 그 아름다움은 프랭크에게도 그에 못지않게 끌리는 것이었나 봐. 그도 처음엔 곁을 서성이다가 이내 그녀 옆에 앉았거든. 여름 저녁에 어울리는 할 말이 무척 많다는 건 두말할 나위가 없지. 그리고 그 말들은 낮은 목소리로 하는 게 가장 좋다는 것도, 그게 그 시간의 평화와 고요에 가장 잘 어울리니까. 또 때때로 긴 침묵이 이어지고, 그러다 간절한 한두 마디가 오가고, 그러다 또 한 번 침묵이 찾아오는데 이게 어쩐지 침묵 같지가 않고, 어쩌면 이따금 황급히 고개를 돌린다거나 눈길을 바닥으로 떨군다거나 하는 거지. 이런 자잘한 일들이며, 촛불 들여오기를 꺼리는 마음이며, 시간을 분과 헷갈리는 버릇이며, 이 모든 게 다 그 시간이 부리는 조화일 뿐이라는 건, 사랑스러운 입술들이 숱하게 똑똑히 증언해 줄 거야. 그러니 마침내 촛불을 들여왔을 때 케이트의 반짝이는 눈이 그 빛을 견디지 못해 얼굴을 돌려야 했고, 심지어 잠시 방을 나가야 했던 걸 두고 니클비 부인이 놀랄 까닭은 조금도 없었어. 어둠 속에 그렇게 오래 앉아 있으면 촛불이 눈부신 법이거든. 그러니 그런 결과가 생기는 건 더없이 자연스러운 일이라는 걸, 세상 물정 아는 젊은이라면 누구나 알지. 그 점은 노인들도 알아, 아니 한때는 알았지. 그런데 가끔 이런 걸 잊어버리니, 그게 더 안타까운 노릇이야."]], [["The good lady’s surprise, however, did not end here.", "하지만 이 마음씨 좋은 부인의 놀라움은 여기서 끝나지 않았어."], ["It was greatly increased when it was discovered that Kate had not the least appetite for supper: a discovery so alarming that there is no knowing in what unaccountable efforts of oratory Mrs.", "케이트가 저녁 식사에 입맛이 조금도 없다는 게 드러나자 놀라움은 한층 커졌지. 어찌나 큰일인지, 만약 바로 그때 모두의 시선이 아주 이상하고 흔치 않은 소리에 쏠리지 않았더라면, 니클비 부인의 걱정이 무슨 알 수 없는 웅변으로 터져 나왔을지 모를 일이었어."], ["Nickleby’s apprehensions might have been vented, if the general attention had not been attracted, at the moment, by a very strange and uncommon noise, proceeding, as the pale and trembling servant girl affirmed, and as everybody’s sense of hearing seemed to affirm also, ‘right down’ the chimney of the adjoining room.", "그 소리는 얼굴이 새파랗게 질려 떨고 있는 하녀 아이의 말에 따르면, 그리고 모두의 귀에도 그렇게 들리는 듯했는데, 옆방 굴뚝에서 \"곧장 아래로\" 내려오는 소리였어."]], [["It being quite plain to the comprehension of all present that, however extraordinary and improbable it might appear, the noise did nevertheless proceed from the chimney in question; and the noise (which was a strange compound of various shuffling, sliding, rumbling, and struggling sounds, all muffled by the chimney) still continuing, Frank Cheeryble caught up a candle, and Tim Linkinwater the tongs, and they would have very quickly ascertained the cause of this disturbance if Mrs. Nickleby had not been taken very faint, and declined being left behind, on any account. This produced a short remonstrance, which terminated in their all proceeding to the troubled chamber in a body, excepting only Miss La Creevy, who, as the servant girl volunteered a confession of having been subject to fits in her infancy, remained with her to give the alarm and apply restoratives, in case of extremity.", "그게 아무리 기이하고 있을 법하지 않게 들려도, 어쨌든 그 소리가 정말로 그 굴뚝에서 나고 있다는 건 그 자리에 있는 모두가 똑똑히 알 수 있었어. 그리고 그 소리는(질질 끌고, 미끄러지고, 우르릉거리고, 버둥거리는 온갖 소리가 굴뚝에 막혀 한데 뒤섞인 묘한 소리였지) 여전히 계속됐어. 그래서 프랭크 치어리블이 촛불을 집어 들고, 팀 링킨워터는 부집게를 집어 들었지. 그들은 이 소동의 원인을 금세 밝혀냈을 거야. 니클비 부인이 그만 정신이 아득해져서는, 절대로 혼자 남지 않겠다고 버티지만 않았더라면 말이야. 그 바람에 잠깐 실랑이가 벌어졌는데, 결국 모두 한 무리가 되어 그 소란스러운 방으로 향하는 걸로 끝이 났어. 다만 라 크리비 양만 빠졌지. 하녀 아이가 어릴 적에 발작을 일으킨 적이 있다고 자진해서 털어놓는 바람에, 라 크리비 양이 그 애와 함께 남아 만일의 사태에 대비해 소리쳐 알리고 정신이 들게 해 주기로 했거든."]], [["Advancing to the door of the mysterious apartment, they were not a little surprised to hear a human voice, chanting with a highly elaborated expression of melancholy, and in tones of suffocation which a human voice might have produced from under five or six feather-beds of the best quality, the once popular air of ‘Has she then failed in her truth, the beautiful maid I adore?’ Nor, on bursting into the room without demanding a parley, was their astonishment lessened by the discovery that these romantic sounds certainly proceeded from the throat of some man up the chimney, of whom nothing was visible but a pair of legs, which were dangling above the grate; apparently feeling, with extreme anxiety, for the top bar whereon to effect a landing.", "그 수상한 방의 문 앞으로 다가가던 그들은 사람 목소리가 들려와 적잖이 놀랐어. 한껏 멋을 부린 구슬픈 가락으로, 게다가 마치 최고급 깃털 이불 대여섯 장 밑에 깔린 사람이 낼 법한 숨 막히는 목소리로, 한때 유행하던 \"내가 사모하는 그 아리따운 처녀, 그녀의 진심이 그예 식어 버렸나?\"라는 노래를 부르고 있었던 거야. 게다가 양해를 구할 새도 없이 방으로 들이닥쳐서도 그 놀라움은 가시지 않았어. 그 낭만적인 소리가 분명 굴뚝 위 어떤 남자의 목구멍에서 나오고 있었거든. 그런데 보이는 건 두 다리뿐이었어. 난로 위에 대롱대롱 매달린 채, 디딜 만한 맨 위 가로대를 어디 없나 하고 몹시 안달하며 더듬고 있는 것 같았지."]], [["A sight so unusual and unbusiness-like as this, completely paralysed Tim Linkinwater, who, after one or two gentle pinches at the stranger’s ankles, which were productive of no effect, stood clapping the tongs together, as if he were sharpening them for another assault, and did nothing else.", "이렇게 흔치 않고 사리에 안 맞는 광경에 팀 링킨워터는 그만 얼어붙고 말았어. 그는 낯선 이의 발목을 한두 번 살살 꼬집어 봤지만 아무 소용이 없자, 마치 다음번 공격을 위해 부집게를 벼리기라도 하는 양 부집게를 딱딱 맞부딪치며 서 있을 뿐, 다른 건 아무것도 하지 못했어."]], [["‘This must be some drunken fellow,’ said Frank.", "\"이건 분명 술 취한 사람일 거예요.\" 프랭크가 말했어."], ["‘No thief would announce his presence thus.’", "\"도둑이라면 이렇게 제 존재를 알리진 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["As he said this, with great indignation, he raised the candle to obtain a better view of the legs, and was darting forward to pull them down with very little ceremony, when Mrs.", "그는 잔뜩 분개해서 이렇게 말하며 두 다리를 좀 더 잘 보려고 촛불을 들어 올렸어. 그러고는 사정 봐줄 것 없이 그 다리를 끌어내리려고 앞으로 달려들던 참이었지."], ["Nickleby, clasping her hands, uttered a sharp sound, something between a scream and an exclamation, and demanded to know whether the mysterious limbs were not clad in small-clothes and grey worsted stockings, or whether her eyes had deceived her.", "그때 니클비 부인이 두 손을 맞잡더니, 비명인지 탄성인지 모를 날카로운 소리를 내며, 저 수상한 다리가 무릎바지에 회색 털양말을 신고 있는 게 아니냐고, 아니면 자기 눈이 잘못 본 거냐고 물었어."]], [["‘Yes,’ cried Frank, looking a little closer.", "\"맞아요.\" 프랭크가 좀 더 가까이 들여다보며 외쳤어."], ["‘Small-clothes certainly, and--and--rough grey stockings, too.", "\"무릎바지가 분명하고요, 그리고—그리고—거친 회색 양말도 신었네요."], ["Do you know him, ma’am?’", "아는 사람입니까, 부인?\""]], [["‘Kate, my dear,’ said Mrs.", "\"케이트, 얘야.\""], ["Nickleby, deliberately sitting herself down in a chair with that sort of desperate resignation which seemed to imply that now matters had come to a crisis, and all disguise was useless, ‘you will have the goodness, my love, to explain precisely how this matter stands.", "니클비 부인이 의자에 천천히 주저앉으며 말했어. 마치 이제 일이 막판에 이르렀으니 더 감추는 건 부질없다는 듯한, 자포자기 같은 체념이 서린 태도였지."], ["I have given him no encouragement--none whatever--not the least in the world.", "\"얘야, 일이 정확히 어떻게 된 건지 좀 설명해 주련. 난 그분한테 부추긴 적이 없다—전혀, 눈곱만큼도 없어."], ["You know that, my dear, perfectly well.", "너도 잘 알잖니, 얘야. 그분이 고백할 때 아주 정중했어, 더없이 정중했지."], ["He was very respectful, exceedingly respectful, when he declared, as you were a witness to; still at the same time, if I am to be persecuted in this way, if vegetable what’s-his-names and all kinds of garden-stuff are to strew my path out of doors, and gentlemen are to come choking up our chimneys at home, I really don’t know--upon my word I do NOT know--what is to become of me.", "너도 봤잖니. 그래도 이런 식으로 시달려야 한다면, 채소 뭐시기들이며 온갖 텃밭 작물들이 바깥에서 내 길에 흩뿌려지고, 신사분이 집 안 굴뚝으로 기어들어 와 숨이 막힐 지경이 된다면, 난 정말 모르겠다—정말이지 모르겠어—내가 어찌 될지 말이다."], ["It’s a very hard case--harder than anything I was ever exposed to, before I married your poor dear papa, though I suffered a good deal of annoyance then--but that, of course, I expected, and made up my mind for.", "이건 정말 딱한 일이야—내가 네 가엾은 아빠랑 결혼하기 전에 겪었던 그 어떤 일보다 더 딱한 일이지. 그때도 꽤나 성가신 일을 겪긴 했다만—하기야 그건 으레 그러려니 하고 각오한 일이었으니까."], ["When I was not nearly so old as you, my dear, there was a young gentleman who sat next us at church, who used, almost every Sunday, to cut my name in large letters in the front of his pew while the sermon was going on.", "내가 지금 너보다 한참 어렸을 때, 교회에서 우리 옆자리에 앉던 젊은 신사가 있었단다. 그 사람은 거의 일요일마다 설교가 진행되는 동안 자기 신도석 앞쪽에다 내 이름을 큼지막한 글씨로 새기곤 했어."], ["It was gratifying, of course, naturally so, but still it was an annoyance, because the pew was in a very conspicuous place, and he was several times publicly taken out by the beadle for doing it.", "물론 흐뭇한 일이었지, 자연스레 그렇기도 했고. 그래도 성가신 일이긴 했어."], ["But that was nothing to this.", "그 신도석이 아주 눈에 띄는 자리였거든."], ["This is a great deal worse, and a great deal more embarrassing.", "그 사람은 그러다가 여러 번 사람들 앞에서 교회지기한테 끌려 나갔단다. 하지만 그건 이 일에 비하면 아무것도 아니야."], ["I would rather, Kate, my dear,’ said Mrs.", "이건 훨씬 더 고약하고, 훨씬 더 난처한 일이지. 차라리, 케이트, 얘야,\""], ["Nickleby, with great solemnity, and an effusion of tears: ‘I would rather, I declare, have been a pig-faced lady, than be exposed to such a life as this!’", "니클비 부인이 자못 엄숙하게, 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 말했어. \"차라리 돼지 얼굴을 한 여자로 사는 게 낫지, 이런 꼴을 당하느니 정말이지 그게 낫겠다!\""]], [["Frank Cheeryble and Tim Linkinwater looked, in irrepressible astonishment, first at each other and then at Kate, who felt that some explanation was necessary, but who, between her terror at the apparition of the legs, her fear lest their owner should be smothered, and her anxiety to give the least ridiculous solution of the mystery that it was capable of bearing, was quite unable to utter a single word.", "프랭크 치어리블과 팀 링킨워터는 도무지 놀라움을 참지 못해, 먼저 서로를 보았다가 케이트를 보았어. 케이트는 무슨 설명이 필요하다는 걸 알았지만, 그 다리의 출현에 대한 공포와, 그 임자가 숨이 막혀 죽지나 않을까 하는 걱정과, 이 수수께끼를 가능한 한 가장 우스꽝스럽지 않게 풀어 주고 싶은 마음 사이에서, 단 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 못했지."]], [["‘He gives me great pain,’ continued Mrs.", "\"그분은 나한테 큰 고통을 준단다.\" 니클비 부인이 눈물을 닦으며 말을 이었어."], ["Nickleby, drying her eyes, ‘great pain; but don’t hurt a hair of his head, I beg.", "\"큰 고통을 줘. 하지만 머리카락 한 올도 다치게 하지 말아 다오, 제발."], ["On no account hurt a hair of his head.’", "무슨 일이 있어도 머리카락 한 올 다치게 하면 안 돼.\""]], [["It would not, under existing circumstances, have been quite so easy to hurt a hair of the gentleman’s head as Mrs.", "지금 형편으로 보면, 그 신사의 머리카락 한 올을 다치게 하기란 니클비 부인이 생각하는 것만큼 그리 쉬운 일이 아니었어."], ["Nickleby seemed to imagine, inasmuch as that part of his person was some feet up the chimney, which was by no means a wide one.", "그 머리 부분이 좁다란 굴뚝 안쪽 몇 자나 위에 박혀 있었으니까. 그런데 그동안 그 신사가 아리따운 처녀의 진심이 파산했다는 노래를 줄곧 멈추지 않더니, 이제는 아주 힘없이 꺽꺽거릴 뿐 아니라, 숨쉬기가 어려워졌는지 다리를 사납게 버둥거리기 시작했어."], ["But, as all this time he had never left off singing about the bankruptcy of the beautiful maid in respect of truth, and now began not only to croak very feebly, but to kick with great violence as if respiration became a task of difficulty, Frank Cheeryble, without further hesitation, pulled at the shorts and worsteds with such heartiness as to bring him floundering into the room with greater precipitation than he had quite calculated upon.", "그러자 프랭크 치어리블이 더는 머뭇거리지 않고 그 무릎바지와 털양말을 힘껏 잡아당겼지. 어찌나 세게 당겼는지, 그 신사는 프랭크가 예상한 것보다 훨씬 더 곤두박질치며 방 안으로 굴러떨어졌어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["yes, yes,’ said Kate, directly the whole figure of this singular visitor appeared in this abrupt manner.", "네, 네.\" 이 별난 손님의 온몸이 이렇게 느닷없이 나타나자마자 케이트가 말했어."], ["‘I know who it is.", "\"누군지 알겠어요."], ["Pray don’t be rough with him.", "제발 그분한테 거칠게 하지 마세요. 다쳤나요?"], ["Is he hurt? I hope not.", "안 다쳤으면 좋겠는데."], ["Oh, pray see if he is hurt.’", "아, 제발 다쳤는지 좀 봐 주세요.\""]], [["‘He is not, I assure you,’ replied Frank, handling the object of his surprise, after this appeal, with sudden tenderness and respect.", "\"안 다쳤습니다, 정말이에요.\" 프랭크가 대답했어."], ["‘He is not hurt in the least.’", "이 청을 듣고는, 자기를 놀라게 한 그 대상을 갑자기 다정하고 정중하게 다루면서 말이야. \"조금도 다치지 않았어요.\""]], [["‘Don’t let him come any nearer,’ said Kate, retiring as far as she could.", "\"더 가까이 못 오게 해 주세요.\" 케이트가 갈 수 있는 데까지 물러나며 말했어."]], [["‘Oh, no, he shall not,’ rejoined Frank.", "\"아, 안 됩니다, 그러지 않을 거예요.\" 프랭크가 대꾸했어."], ["‘You see I have him secure here.", "\"보세요, 제가 여기 단단히 붙잡고 있잖아요."], ["But may I ask you what this means, and whether you expected this old gentleman?’", "그런데 이게 무슨 일인지 물어봐도 될까요? 그리고 이 노신사가 올 줄 알고 계셨나요?\""]], [["‘Oh, no,’ said Kate, ‘of course not; but he--mama does not think so, I believe--but he is a mad gentleman who has escaped from the next house, and must have found an opportunity of secreting himself here.’", "\"아, 아니요.\" 케이트가 말했어. \"당연히 몰랐죠. 하지만 그분은—엄마는 그렇게 생각 안 하시는 것 같지만—옆집에서 빠져나온 미친 신사분이에요. 여기 몰래 숨어들 기회를 잡으셨던 게 분명해요.\""]], [["‘Kate,’ interposed Mrs.", "\"케이트.\""], ["Nickleby with severe dignity, ‘I am surprised at you.’", "니클비 부인이 근엄한 위엄을 갖추고 끼어들었어. \"네가 그런다니 놀랍구나.\""]], [["‘Dear mama,’ Kate gently remonstrated.", "\"엄마.\" 케이트가 부드럽게 항변했어."]], [["‘I am surprised at you,’ repeated Mrs.", "\"네가 그런다니 놀랍다.\" 니클비 부인이 거듭 말했어."], ["Nickleby; ‘upon my word, Kate, I am quite astonished that you should join the persecutors of this unfortunate gentleman, when you know very well that they have the basest designs upon his property, and that that is the whole secret of it.", "\"정말이지, 케이트, 이 가엾은 신사분을 못살게 구는 사람들 편에 네가 끼어들다니 어이가 없구나. 그자들이 이분 재산을 노리는 비열한 속셈을 품고 있다는 걸, 그게 이 일의 모든 내막이라는 걸 너도 잘 알면서 말이야."], ["It would be much kinder of you, Kate, to ask Mr.", "케이트, 차라리 링킨워터 씨나 치어리블 씨한테 이분 편을 들어 달라고, 이분이 억울한 일 안 당하게 해 달라고 부탁하는 게 훨씬 마음씨 고운 일이겠다. 네 감정에 휘둘려선 안 돼."], ["Linkinwater or Mr.", "그건 옳지 않아, 전혀 옳지 않지."], ["Cheeryble to interfere in his behalf, and see him righted.", "그럼 내 심정은 어떻겠니, 생각해 봤니? 분개해야 할 사람이 있다면 누구겠어?"], ["You ought not to allow your feelings to influence you; it’s not right, very far from it.", "당연히 나지, 그게 마땅하고. 그래도 난 무슨 일이 있어도 그런 부당한 짓은 하지 않을 거야."], ["What should my feelings be, do you suppose?", "그래,\" 니클비 부인이 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우고는 수줍은 듯하면서도 위엄 있게 딴 데를 보며 말을 이었어."], ["If anybody ought to be indignant, who is it?", "\"이 신사분도 내 말을 이해하실 거야."], ["I, of course, and very properly so.", "내가 며칠 전에 드린 대답을 그대로 되풀이하겠다고."], ["Still, at the same time, I wouldn’t commit such an injustice for the world.", "앞으로도 늘 그렇게 되풀이하겠다고. 비록 이분이 나 때문에 이렇게 끔찍한 처지에 스스로 빠지는 걸 보면 진심이라는 건 믿지만 말이야."], ["No,’ continued Mrs Nickleby, drawing herself up, and looking another way with a kind of bashful stateliness; ‘this gentleman will understand me when I tell him that I repeat the answer I gave him the other day; that I always will repeat it, though I do believe him to be sincere when I find him placing himself in such dreadful situations on my account; and that I request him to have the goodness to go away directly, or it will be impossible to keep his behaviour a secret from my son Nicholas.", "그리고 이분께 곧장 점잖게 물러가 주십사 부탁드린다고. 안 그러면 이분 행동을 내 아들 니콜라스한테 비밀로 할 수가 없을 테니까."], ["I am obliged to him, very much obliged to him, but I cannot listen to his addresses for a moment.", "고맙긴 해, 정말 고마운 분이지. 하지만 잠시라도 이분의 구애에 귀 기울일 수는 없어."], ["It’s quite impossible.’", "그건 도무지 안 될 일이야.\""]], [["While this address was in course of delivery, the old gentleman, with his nose and cheeks embellished with large patches of soot, sat upon the ground with his arms folded, eyeing the spectators in profound silence, and with a very majestic demeanour.", "이 말이 이어지는 동안, 그 노신사는 코와 뺨에 큼지막한 검댕 자국을 잔뜩 묻힌 채 팔짱을 끼고 바닥에 앉아, 깊은 침묵 속에서 자못 위엄 있는 태도로 구경꾼들을 바라보고 있었어."], ["He did not appear to take the smallest notice of what Mrs.", "그는 니클비 부인의 말에 조금도 관심을 두는 기색이 없었지."], ["Nickleby said, but when she ceased to speak he honoured her with a long stare, and inquired if she had quite finished.", "그런데 부인이 말을 그치자, 그녀를 한참 빤히 쳐다보는 영광을 베풀더니, 이제 다 끝난 거냐고 물었어."]], [["‘I have nothing more to say,’ replied that lady modestly.", "\"더 할 말은 없어요.\" 그 부인이 다소곳이 대답했어."], ["‘I really cannot say anything more.’", "\"정말이지 더는 할 말이 없네요.\""]], [["‘Very good,’ said the old gentleman, raising his voice, ‘then bring in the bottled lightning, a clean tumbler, and a corkscrew.’", "\"아주 좋소.\" 노신사가 목소리를 높이며 말했어. \"그럼 병에 든 번개와 깨끗한 술잔, 그리고 병따개를 가져오시오.\""]], [["Nobody executing this order, the old gentleman, after a short pause, raised his voice again and demanded a thunder sandwich.", "이 명령을 아무도 따르지 않자, 노신사는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 다시 목소리를 높여 천둥 샌드위치를 가져오라고 했어."], ["This article not being forthcoming either, he requested to be served with a fricassee of boot-tops and goldfish sauce, and then laughing heartily, gratified his hearers with a very long, very loud, and most melodious bellow.", "이것 역시 나오지 않자, 그는 장화 윗부분 프리카세에 금붕어 소스를 곁들여 달라고 청하더니, 호탕하게 웃으며 아주 길고, 아주 크고, 더없이 가락진 고함을 내질러 듣는 이들을 즐겁게 해 주었지."]], [["But still Mrs.", "그래도 니클비 부인은 주위 사람들의 의미심장한 눈길에 답하듯 고개를 가로저었어."], ["Nickleby, in reply to the significant looks of all about her, shook her head as though to assure them that she saw nothing whatever in all this, unless, indeed, it were a slight degree of eccentricity.", "마치 이 모든 일에서 자기는 아무것도 보지 못했다고, 굳이 따지자면 그저 약간 별난 구석 정도라고 안심시키려는 듯이 말이야."], ["She might have remained impressed with these opinions down to the latest moment of her life, but for a slight train of circumstances, which, trivial as they were, altered the whole complexion of the case.", "부인은 생의 마지막 순간까지도 이 생각을 굳게 품고 있었을지 몰라. 자질구레하나마 사건의 양상을 통째로 뒤바꿔 놓은 일련의 일들만 없었더라면 말이지."]], [["It happened that Miss La Creevy, finding her patient in no very threatening condition, and being strongly impelled by curiosity to see what was going forward, bustled into the room while the old gentleman was in the very act of bellowing.", "마침 라 크리비 양은 자기가 돌보던 환자가 그리 위험한 상태가 아니라는 걸 알게 됐어. 그리고 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 보고 싶은 호기심에 못 이겨, 노신사가 한창 고함을 내지르고 있을 때 방 안으로 부산스레 들어왔지."], ["It happened, too, that the instant the old gentleman saw her, he stopped short, skipped suddenly on his feet, and fell to kissing his hand violently: a change of demeanour which almost terrified the little portrait painter out of her senses, and caused her to retreat behind Tim Linkinwater with the utmost expedition.", "그런데 마침 그 노신사가 라 크리비 양을 본 순간, 그는 우뚝 멈추더니, 갑자기 폴짝 일어서서는 손에 미친 듯이 입을 맞추기 시작했어. 이렇게 태도가 돌변하는 통에 그 자그마한 초상화가는 거의 정신이 나갈 만큼 겁에 질려, 부랴부랴 팀 링킨워터 뒤로 도망쳐 숨었지."]], [["‘Aha!’ cried the old gentleman, folding his hands, and squeezing them with great force against each other.", "\"아하!\" 노신사가 두 손을 맞잡고 힘껏 맞비비며 외쳤어."], ["‘I see her now; I see her now!", "\"이제야 그녀가 보이는구나."], ["My love, my life, my bride, my peerless beauty.", "이제야 보여! 내 사랑, 내 생명, 내 신부, 견줄 데 없는 미인이여."], ["She is come at last--at last--and all is gas and gaiters!’", "마침내 그녀가 왔도다—마침내—이제 모든 게 가스등이요 각반이로다!\""]], [["Mrs.", "니클비 부인은 잠시 다소 당황한 기색이었어."], ["Nickleby looked rather disconcerted for a moment, but immediately recovering, nodded to Miss La Creevy and the other spectators several times, and frowned, and smiled gravely, giving them to understand that she saw where the mistake was, and would set it all to rights in a minute or two.", "하지만 곧 정신을 차리고는, 라 크리비 양과 다른 구경꾼들을 향해 몇 번이고 고개를 끄덕이고, 눈살을 찌푸렸다가, 진지하게 미소 지었지. 자기는 어디서 오해가 생겼는지 다 알고 있으며, 잠깐이면 다 바로잡겠다는 걸 알려 주려는 거였어."]], [["‘She is come!’ said the old gentleman, laying his hand upon his heart.", "\"그녀가 왔도다!\" 노신사가 가슴에 손을 얹고 말했어."], ["‘Cormoran and Blunderbore!", "\"코모런과 블런더보어여!"], ["She is come!", "그녀가 왔도다!"], ["All the wealth I have is hers if she will take me for her slave.", "그녀가 나를 노예로 삼아 준다면 내가 가진 모든 재산이 다 그녀의 것이로다."], ["Where are grace, beauty, and blandishments, like those?", "이만한 기품과 미모와 매혹이 또 어디 있단 말인가?"], ["In the Empress of Madagascar?", "마다가스카르의 여제에게? 아니."], ["No. In the Queen of Diamonds?", "다이아몬드의 여왕에게?"], ["No. In Mrs.", "아니."], ["Rowland, who every morning bathes in Kalydor for nothing?", "매일 아침 칼리도르 목욕을 공짜로 하는 롤런드 부인에게? 아니지."], ["No. Melt all these down into one, with the three Graces, the nine Muses, and fourteen biscuit-bakers’ daughters from Oxford Street, and make a woman half as lovely.", "이들을 죄다 하나로 녹이고, 거기에 세 우미의 여신과 아홉 뮤즈, 옥스퍼드 거리의 비스킷 가게 딸 열넷을 더해 봐, 그래서 그 절반만큼이라도 사랑스러운 여인을 만들어 보라고."], ["Pho!", "흥!"], ["I defy you.’", "어디 한번 해 보시지.\""]], [["After uttering this rhapsody, the old gentleman snapped his fingers twenty or thirty times, and then subsided into an ecstatic contemplation of Miss La Creevy’s charms.", "이 황홀한 찬가를 늘어놓고 나서, 노신사는 손가락을 스무 번 서른 번쯤 딱딱 튕기더니, 라 크리비 양의 매력을 황홀하게 바라보는 명상에 잠겨 버렸어."], ["This affording Mrs. Nickleby a favourable opportunity of explanation, she went about it straight.", "이 덕분에 니클비 부인은 설명할 좋은 기회를 잡았고, 곧장 그 일에 나섰지."]], [["‘I am sure,’ said the worthy lady, with a prefatory cough, ‘that it’s a great relief, under such trying circumstances as these, to have anybody else mistaken for me--a very great relief; and it’s a circumstance that never occurred before, although I have several times been mistaken for my daughter Kate. I have no doubt the people were very foolish, and perhaps ought to have known better, but still they did take me for her, and of course that was no fault of mine, and it would be very hard indeed if I was to be made responsible for it. However, in this instance, of course, I must feel that I should do exceedingly wrong if I suffered anybody--especially anybody that I am under great obligations to--to be made uncomfortable on my account. And therefore I think it my duty to tell that gentleman that he is mistaken, that I am the lady who he was told by some impertinent person was niece to the Council of Paving-stones, and that I do beg and entreat of him to go quietly away, if it’s only for,’ here Mrs. Nickleby simpered and hesitated, ‘for MY sake.’", "\"제 생각엔,\" 그 마음씨 좋은 부인이 헛기침을 한 번 하고 운을 뗐어. \"이런 딱한 상황에서 다른 누군가가 저로 오해받는다는 건 정말 마음이 놓이는 일이에요—아주 큰 위안이죠. 이런 일은 전에 한 번도 없었거든요. 제가 제 딸 케이트로 오해받은 적은 여러 번 있었지만요. 그 사람들이 어지간히 어리석었던 게 분명하고, 좀 더 분별이 있었어야 마땅하지만, 그래도 어쨌든 저를 케이트로 본 거잖아요. 물론 그건 제 잘못이 아니고, 제가 그 책임을 져야 한다면 정말이지 너무 억울한 노릇이죠. 어쨌든 이번 경우엔, 당연히, 제가 누군가를—특히 제가 큰 신세를 진 분을—저 때문에 불편하게 만든다면 그건 제가 아주 잘못하는 일이라고 느끼지 않을 수 없어요. 그러니 저 신사분께 오해하셨다고 말씀드리는 게 제 도리라고 생각해요. 어떤 무례한 사람이 저를 두고 포장석 의회의 조카딸이라고 일러바쳤다던데, 제가 바로 그 부인이라고요. 그리고 제발 부탁이고 간청이니 조용히 가 주십사고요. 그게 다른 누구도 아닌,\" 여기서 니클비 부인은 새치름하게 웃으며 머뭇거렸어. \"저를 위해서라면 말이죠.\""]], [["It might have been expected that the old gentleman would have been penetrated to the heart by the delicacy and condescension of this appeal, and that he would at least have returned a courteous and suitable reply.", "이렇게 섬세하고 겸손하게 호소했으니, 노신사가 마음 깊이 감동받아 적어도 정중하고 알맞은 답이라도 돌려주었으리라 기대할 만했지."], ["What, then, was the shock which Mrs.", "그런데 니클비 부인이 받은 충격이 어떠했겠어."], ["Nickleby received, when, accosting HER in the most unmistakable manner, he replied in a loud and sonourous voice: ‘Avaunt!", "노신사가 누가 봐도 부인을 똑바로 겨냥하며, 크고 쩌렁쩌렁한 목소리로 이렇게 대꾸했으니 말이야. \"썩 물러가라!"], ["Cat!’", "고양이야!\""]], [["‘Sir!’ cried Mrs.", "\"이보세요!\""], ["Nickleby, in a faint tone.", "니클비 부인이 기어드는 소리로 외쳤어."]], [["‘Cat!’ repeated the old gentleman.", "\"고양이!\""], ["‘Puss, Kit, Tit, Grimalkin, Tabby, Brindle!", "노신사가 거듭 말했어."], ["Whoosh!’ with which last sound, uttered in a hissing manner between his teeth, the old gentleman swung his arms violently round and round, and at the same time alternately advanced on Mrs.", "\"야옹이, 나비, 깡총이, 잿빛 마녀, 얼룩이, 줄무늬!\" 그러고는 이 사이로 쉭쉭 소리를 내뿜으며, 두 팔을 사납게 빙빙 돌리는 동시에, 니클비 부인에게 다가갔다 물러섰다 했어."], ["Nickleby, and retreated from her, in that species of savage dance with which boys on market-days may be seen to frighten pigs, sheep, and other animals, when they give out obstinate indications of turning down a wrong street.", "마치 장날에 사내아이들이 돼지나 양 같은 짐승이 엉뚱한 골목으로 고집스레 꺾어 들려 할 때 겁을 주느라 추는, 그런 야만스러운 춤을 추듯이 말이지."]], [["Mrs. Nickleby wasted no words, but uttered an exclamation of horror and surprise, and immediately fainted away.", "니클비 부인은 한마디 말도 더 하지 않고, 공포와 놀라움에 찬 비명을 지르더니, 곧장 까무러쳐 버렸어."]], [["‘I’ll attend to mama,’ said Kate, hastily; ‘I am not at all frightened. But pray take him away: pray take him away!’", "\"엄마는 제가 볼게요.\" 케이트가 다급히 말했어. \"전 하나도 안 무서워요. 그러니 제발 저분을 데려가 주세요. 제발 데려가 주세요!\""]], [["Frank was not at all confident of his power of complying with this request, until he bethought himself of the stratagem of sending Miss La Creevy on a few paces in advance, and urging the old gentleman to follow her.", "프랭크는 이 부탁을 들어줄 수 있을지 영 자신이 없었어. 그러다 라 크리비 양을 몇 걸음 앞서 보내고 노신사더러 그녀를 따라가게 하는 꾀를 떠올렸지."], ["It succeeded to a miracle; and he went away in a rapture of admiration, strongly guarded by Tim Linkinwater on one side, and Frank himself on the other.", "그 수가 기막히게 들어맞았어. 노신사는 한쪽엔 팀 링킨워터, 다른 한쪽엔 프랭크 본인이 단단히 호위하는 가운데, 황홀한 흠모에 젖어 따라 나갔거든."]], [["‘Kate,’ murmured Mrs.", "\"케이트.\""], ["Nickleby, reviving when the coast was clear, ‘is he gone?’", "위험이 사라지자 정신이 든 니클비 부인이 중얼거렸어. \"그 사람 갔니?\""]], [["She was assured that he was.", "갔다고 누군가 일러 주었지."]], [["‘I shall never forgive myself, Kate,’ said Mrs.", "\"난 평생 나 자신을 용서 못 할 거다, 케이트.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["‘Never!", "\"절대로!"], ["That gentleman has lost his senses, and I am the unhappy cause.’", "저 신사분이 정신을 잃은 거야. 그리고 그 가엾은 원인이 바로 나고.\""]], [["‘YOU the cause!’ said Kate, greatly astonished.", "\"엄마가 원인이라고요!\" 케이트가 깜짝 놀라 말했어."]], [["‘I, my love,’ replied Mrs.", "\"그래, 나야, 얘야.\""], ["Nickleby, with a desperate calmness.", "니클비 부인이 자포자기한 듯 차분하게 대답했어."], ["‘You saw what he was the other day; you see what he is now.", "\"며칠 전에 그 사람이 어떤지 너도 봤지. 그리고 지금 어떤지도 보잖니."], ["I told your brother, weeks and weeks ago, Kate, that I hoped a disappointment might not be too much for him.", "내가 몇 주, 몇 주 전에 네 오빠한테 말했단다, 케이트. 행여나 그 사람이 실연을 견디지 못할까 걱정된다고 말이야."], ["You see what a wreck he is.", "저렇게 폐인이 된 꼴을 좀 보렴."], ["Making allowance for his being a little flighty, you know how rationally, and sensibly, and honourably he talked, when we saw him in the garden.", "좀 들떠 있긴 했어도, 정원에서 봤을 때 그 사람이 얼마나 조리 있고 분별 있고 점잖게 얘기했는지 너도 알잖니."], ["You have heard the dreadful nonsense he has been guilty of this night, and the manner in which he has gone on with that poor unfortunate little old maid.", "그런데 오늘 밤 그 사람이 저지른 그 끔찍한 헛소리며, 저 가엾고 불행한 자그마한 노처녀한테 들러붙던 꼴을 너도 들었지."], ["Can anybody doubt how all this has been brought about?’", "이게 다 어쩌다 이렇게 됐는지 누가 의심할 수 있겠니?\""]], [["‘I should scarcely think they could,’ said Kate mildly.", "\"의심할 사람은 거의 없을 것 같네요.\" 케이트가 부드럽게 말했어."]], [["‘I should scarcely think so, either,’ rejoined her mother.", "\"나도 그럴 것 같구나.\" 어머니가 대꾸했어."], ["‘Well!", "\"그래!"], ["if I am the unfortunate cause of this, I have the satisfaction of knowing that I am not to blame.", "내가 이 일의 불행한 원인이라면, 그래도 내가 탓할 일은 아니라는 걸 아니 그나마 마음이 놓인다. 내가 니콜라스한테 말했어."], ["I told Nicholas, I said to him, “Nicholas, my dear, we should be very careful how we proceed.”", "이렇게 말했지. '니콜라스, 얘야, 우리 아주 조심해서 일을 처리해야 한다.'"], ["He would scarcely hear me.", "그런데 그 애가 도통 내 말을 들으려 하질 않더구나."], ["If the matter had only been properly taken up at first, as I wished it to be!", "처음부터 내가 바라던 대로 제대로 다뤘더라면 얼마나 좋았겠니!"], ["But you are both of you so like your poor papa.", "그런데 너희 둘 다 어쩜 그렇게 너희 가엾은 아빠를 빼다 박았는지."], ["However, I have MY consolation, and that should be enough for me!’", "어쨌든 나한텐 나름의 위안이 있고, 그거면 됐다!\""]], [["Washing her hands, thus, of all responsibility under this head, past, present, or to come, Mrs.", "이렇게 이 일에 대한 모든 책임을, 지난 일이든 지금 일이든 앞으로 올 일이든 다 손 털어 버리고 나서, 니클비 부인은 자기 자식들도 제 어미만큼만 스스로를 탓할 일이 있기를 바란다고 다정하게 덧붙였어."], ["Nickleby kindly added that she hoped her children might never have greater cause to reproach themselves than she had, and prepared herself to receive the escort, who soon returned with the intelligence that the old gentleman was safely housed, and that they found his custodians, who had been making merry with some friends, wholly ignorant of his absence.", "그러고는 호위대를 맞을 채비를 했지. 그들은 곧 돌아와, 노신사를 무사히 집에 들여놓았다는 소식과, 친구들과 함께 흥에 겨워 놀고 있던 그의 보호인들이 그가 없어진 줄도 까맣게 모르고 있더라는 소식을 전했어."]], [["Quiet being again restored, a delicious half-hour--so Frank called it, in the course of subsequent conversation with Tim Linkinwater as they were walking home--was spent in conversation, and Tim’s watch at length apprising him that it was high time to depart, the ladies were left alone, though not without many offers on the part of Frank to remain until Nicholas arrived, no matter what hour of the night it might be, if, after the late neighbourly irruption, they entertained the least fear of being left to themselves. As their freedom from all further apprehension, however, left no pretext for his insisting on mounting guard, he was obliged to abandon the citadel, and to retire with the trusty Tim.", "다시 고요가 찾아오자, 황홀한 반 시간이—훗날 집으로 걸어가는 길에 팀 링킨워터와 이야기를 나누며 프랭크가 그렇게 불렀지—이런저런 대화 속에 흘러갔어. 마침내 팀의 회중시계가 떠날 때가 한참 됐다고 일러 주자, 부인들만 남게 됐지. 그렇다고 프랭크가 그냥 간 건 아니야. 그는 밤이 아무리 깊더라도 니콜라스가 올 때까지 남아 있겠다고 몇 번이고 청했거든. 좀 전에 이웃이 그렇게 쳐들어왔으니, 혹시라도 둘만 남는 게 조금이라도 무섭다면 말이야. 하지만 두 사람이 더는 아무것도 두려워하지 않는 통에, 그가 보초를 서겠다고 우길 핑계가 없어졌어. 그래서 그는 어쩔 수 없이 그 성채를 떠나, 믿음직한 팀과 함께 물러갈 수밖에 없었지."]], [["Nearly three hours of silence passed away.", "거의 세 시간 동안 침묵이 흘렀어."], ["Kate blushed to find, when Nicholas returned, how long she had been sitting alone, occupied with her own thoughts.", "니콜라스가 돌아왔을 때, 케이트는 자기가 그토록 오래 혼자 앉아 제 생각에 잠겨 있었다는 걸 알고 얼굴을 붉혔지."]], [["‘I really thought it had not been half an hour,’ she said.", "\"정말이지 반 시간도 안 지난 줄 알았어.\" 케이트가 말했어."]], [["‘They must have been pleasant thoughts, Kate,’ rejoined Nicholas gaily, ‘to make time pass away like that.", "\"즐거운 생각이었나 보구나, 케이트.\" 니콜라스가 명랑하게 받았어."], ["What were they now?’", "\"시간이 그렇게 훌쩍 지나가게 하다니. 그래, 무슨 생각이었는데?\""]], [["Kate was confused; she toyed with some trifle on the table, looked up and smiled, looked down and dropped a tear.", "케이트는 당황했어. 탁자 위의 자잘한 물건을 만지작거리다가, 고개를 들어 미소 짓고, 다시 고개를 떨구며 눈물 한 방울을 흘렸지."]], [["‘Why, Kate,’ said Nicholas, drawing his sister towards him and kissing her, ‘let me see your face.", "\"이런, 케이트.\" 니콜라스가 누이를 끌어당겨 입을 맞추며 말했어."], ["No? Ah!", "\"얼굴 좀 보자."], ["that was but a glimpse; that’s scarcely fair.", "안 돼? 아!"], ["A longer look than that, Kate.", "그건 그저 흘끗이잖아. 그건 좀 너무하지."], ["Come--and I’ll read your thoughts for you.’", "그보다 더 오래 보자꾸나, 케이트. 자—그럼 내가 네 생각을 읽어 줄게.\""]], [["There was something in this proposition, albeit it was said without the slightest consciousness or application, which so alarmed his sister, that Nicholas laughingly changed the subject to domestic matters, and thus gathered, by degrees, as they left the room and went upstairs together, how lonely Smike had been all night--and by very slow degrees, too; for on this subject also, Kate seemed to speak with some reluctance.", "이 말에는, 비록 조금의 의식도 없이 무심코 던진 말이었지만, 어딘가 누이를 깜짝 놀라게 하는 구석이 있었어. 그래서 니콜라스는 웃으며 화제를 집안일로 돌렸지. 그리고 둘이 방을 나와 함께 위층으로 올라가는 동안, 스마이크가 밤새 얼마나 외로워했는지를 조금씩 알게 됐어. 그것도 아주 더디게야 알게 됐지. 이 일에 대해서도 케이트가 어딘가 말하기를 꺼리는 눈치였거든."]], [["‘Poor fellow,’ said Nicholas, tapping gently at his door, ‘what can be the cause of all this?’", "\"가엾은 친구.\" 니콜라스가 그의 방문을 가만히 두드리며 말했어. \"대체 무엇 때문에 이러는 걸까?\""]], [["Kate was hanging on her brother’s arm.", "케이트는 오빠의 팔에 매달려 있었어."], ["The door being quickly opened, she had not time to disengage herself, before Smike, very pale and haggard, and completely dressed, confronted them.", "문이 곧 열리는 바람에, 케이트는 미처 팔을 풀 새도 없었어. 얼굴이 몹시 창백하고 초췌한 스마이크가 옷을 다 차려입은 채 그들 앞에 마주 섰거든."]], [["‘And have you not been to bed?’ said Nicholas.", "\"아직 잠자리에 안 들었어?\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘N--n--no,’ was the reply.", "\"아—아—안 잤어요.\" 그가 대답했지."]], [["Nicholas gently detained his sister, who made an effort to retire; and asked, ‘Why not?’", "니콜라스는 물러나려는 누이를 가만히 붙잡고는 물었어. \"왜 안 잤는데?\""]], [["‘I could not sleep,’ said Smike, grasping the hand which his friend extended to him.", "\"잠이 안 와서요.\" 스마이크가 친구가 내미는 손을 꼭 잡으며 말했어."]], [["‘You are not well?’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"몸이 안 좋은 거야?\" 니콜라스가 다시 물었어."]], [["‘I am better, indeed.", "\"많이 나아졌어요, 정말로요."], ["A great deal better,’ said Smike quickly.", "아주 많이요.\" 스마이크가 얼른 말했어."]], [["‘Then why do you give way to these fits of melancholy?’ inquired Nicholas, in his kindest manner; ‘or why not tell us the cause?", "\"그런데 왜 이렇게 자꾸 우울한 기분에 빠지는 거지?\" 니콜라스가 더없이 다정하게 물었어."], ["You grow a different creature, Smike.’", "\"아니면 왜 우리한테 그 까닭을 말하지 않는 거야? 넌 점점 딴사람이 되어 가, 스마이크.\""]], [["‘I do; I know I do,’ he replied.", "\"그래요, 저도 알아요.\" 그가 대답했어."], ["‘I will tell you the reason one day, but not now.", "\"언젠가 그 이유를 말할게요. 지금은 아니고요."], ["I hate myself for this; you are all so good and kind.", "이런 제가 정말 밉네요. 다들 이렇게 좋고 친절하게 대해 주시는데."], ["But I cannot help it.", "그런데 어쩔 수가 없어요."], ["My heart is very full; you do not know how full it is.’", "가슴이 너무 벅차요. 얼마나 벅찬지 모르실 거예요.\""]], [["He wrung Nicholas’s hand before he released it; and glancing, for a moment, at the brother and sister as they stood together, as if there were something in their strong affection which touched him very deeply, withdrew into his chamber, and was soon the only watcher under that quiet roof.", "그는 니콜라스의 손을 놓기 전에 한 번 꼭 쥐었어. 그러고는 함께 서 있는 오누이를 잠시 바라보았지. 마치 그 둘의 깊은 정에 무언가 가슴을 뭉클하게 하는 게 있다는 듯이. 그러더니 자기 방으로 들어갔어. 그리고 이내, 그 고요한 지붕 아래서 홀로 깨어 있는 사람이 되었지."]], [["Involves a serious Catastrophe\nThe little race-course at Hampton was in the full tide and height of its gaiety; the day as dazzling as day could be; the sun high in the cloudless sky, and shining in its fullest splendour. Every gaudy colour that fluttered in the air from carriage seat and garish tent top, shone out in its gaudiest hues. Old dingy flags grew new again, faded gilding was re-burnished, stained rotten canvas looked a snowy white, the very beggars’ rags were freshened up, and sentiment quite forgot its charity in its fervent admiration of poverty so picturesque.", "햄프턴의 작은 경마장은 흥겨움이 한껏 무르익어 절정에 이르고 있었어. 날씨는 더없이 눈부셨지. 구름 한 점 없는 하늘 높이 해가 떠올라 가장 화려한 빛을 뿜어내고 있었어. 마차 좌석에서, 또 야단스러운 천막 꼭대기에서 바람에 펄럭이는 온갖 요란한 색깔들이 저마다 가장 화사한 빛을 내뿜었지. 낡고 칙칙하던 깃발이 새것처럼 되살아났고, 바래 가던 금박은 다시 반짝였어. 얼룩지고 다 삭은 천막 천도 새하얗게 보였지. 거지들의 누더기마저 산뜻해 보였어. 사람들은 이토록 그림 같은 가난에 푹 빠져 감탄하느라 자선이라는 마음씨조차 까맣게 잊고 말았지."]], [["It was one of those scenes of life and animation, caught in its very brightest and freshest moments, which can scarcely fail to please; for if the eye be tired of show and glare, or the ear be weary with a ceaseless round of noise, the one may repose, turn almost where it will, on eager, happy, and expectant faces, and the other deaden all consciousness of more annoying sounds in those of mirth and exhilaration. Even the sunburnt faces of gypsy children, half naked though they be, suggest a drop of comfort. It is a pleasant thing to see that the sun has been there; to know that the air and light are on them every day; to feel that they ARE children, and lead children’s lives; that if their pillows be damp, it is with the dews of Heaven, and not with tears; that the limbs of their girls are free, and that they are not crippled by distortions, imposing an unnatural and horrible penance upon their sex; that their lives are spent, from day to day, at least among the waving trees, and not in the midst of dreadful engines which make young children old before they know what childhood is, and give them the exhaustion and infirmity of age, without, like age, the privilege to die. God send that old nursery tales were true, and that gypsies stole such children by the score!", "가장 밝고 싱그러운 순간에 포착된, 생명과 활기가 넘치는 그런 풍경 가운데 하나였어. 보는 이의 마음에 들지 않을 수 없는 풍경이었지. 눈이 그 화려함과 번쩍임에 지친다 해도, 또 귀가 끝없이 이어지는 소음에 시달린다 해도, 눈은 거의 어디로 돌리든 들뜨고 행복하고 기대에 찬 얼굴들에서 쉴 수 있었거든. 그리고 귀는 그보다 더 거슬리는 소리에 대한 의식을, 즐겁고 흥겨운 소리 속에 묻어 버릴 수 있었어. 햇볕에 그을린 집시 아이들의 얼굴마저, 비록 반쯤 헐벗었다 해도, 한 줌의 위안을 안겨 주었지. 그 아이들에게 햇볕이 닿았다는 걸 보는 건 기분 좋은 일이야. 날마다 그 아이들에게 바람과 햇빛이 쏟아진다는 걸 아는 것도, 그 아이들이 정말 아이답게 아이의 삶을 살고 있다는 걸 느끼는 것도 말이지. 혹시 그 아이들의 베개가 젖어 있다면, 그건 눈물이 아니라 하늘에서 내린 이슬 때문이라는 것. 여자아이들의 팔다리가 자유롭고, 끔찍하게 뒤틀려 불구가 되거나, 제 성에 부자연스럽고 무시무시한 형벌을 짊어지는 일이 없다는 것. 그 아이들의 삶이 적어도 날마다 무시무시한 기계 한복판이 아니라 흔들리는 나무들 사이에서 흘러간다는 것. 그런 기계들은 어린아이가 어린 시절이 뭔지 알기도 전에 늙은이로 만들어 버리고, 늙음이 주는 죽음의 특권조차 없이 노년의 탈진과 쇠약만 안겨 주는데 말이야. 부디 옛날 옛적 동화가 사실이어서, 집시들이 그런 아이들을 수십 명씩 훔쳐 갔으면 좋겠어!"]], [["The great race of the day had just been run; and the close lines of people, on either side of the course, suddenly breaking up and pouring into it, imparted a new liveliness to the scene, which was again all busy movement. Some hurried eagerly to catch a glimpse of the winning horse; others darted to and fro, searching, no less eagerly, for the carriages they had left in quest of better stations. Here, a little knot gathered round a pea and thimble table to watch the plucking of some unhappy greenhorn; and there, another proprietor with his confederates in various disguises--one man in spectacles; another, with an eyeglass and a stylish hat; a third, dressed as a farmer well to do in the world, with his top-coat over his arm and his flash notes in a large leathern pocket-book; and all with heavy-handled whips to represent most innocent country fellows who had trotted there on horseback--sought, by loud and noisy talk and pretended play, to entrap some unwary customer, while the gentlemen confederates (of more villainous aspect still, in clean linen and good clothes), betrayed their close interest in the concern by the anxious furtive glance they cast on all new comers. These would be hanging on the outskirts of a wide circle of people assembled round some itinerant juggler, opposed, in his turn, by a noisy band of music, or the classic game of ‘Ring the Bull,’ while ventriloquists holding dialogues with wooden dolls, and fortune-telling women smothering the cries of real babies, divided with them, and many more, the general attention of the company. Drinking-tents were full, glasses began to clink in carriages, hampers to be unpacked, tempting provisions to be set forth, knives and forks to rattle, champagne corks to fly, eyes to brighten that were not dull before, and pickpockets to count their gains during the last heat. The attention so recently strained on one object of interest, was now divided among a hundred; and look where you would, there was a motley assemblage of feasting, laughing, talking, begging, gambling, and mummery.", "그날의 큰 경주가 막 끝난 참이었어. 경주로 양옆에 빽빽이 늘어서 있던 사람들이 갑자기 흩어져 경주로 안으로 쏟아져 들어왔지. 그러자 풍경에 새로운 활기가 돌면서, 다시 온통 분주하게 움직였어. 어떤 이들은 우승한 말을 한번 보겠다고 다급하게 서둘러 갔어. 다른 이들은 더 좋은 자리를 찾아 떠나며 두고 온 마차를 찾느라 못지않게 다급하게 이리저리 내달렸지. 여기서는 한 무리가 콩과 골무 노름판 둘레에 모여, 어수룩한 풋내기 하나가 탈탈 털리는 걸 구경했어. 저기서는 또 다른 노름판 주인이 갖가지로 변장한 한패와 함께 있었지. 한 사람은 안경을 썼고, 또 한 사람은 외알 안경에 멋진 모자를 썼어. 세 번째 사람은 살림살이 넉넉한 농부 차림으로, 외투를 팔에 걸치고 큼지막한 가죽 지갑에 가짜 지폐를 잔뜩 넣고 있었어. 게다가 다들 손잡이가 묵직한 채찍을 들고서, 말을 타고 이곳까지 느긋이 달려온 더없이 순박한 시골 사람인 척했지. 그렇게 시끄럽게 떠들고 노름하는 척하며 방심한 손님 하나를 낚으려 했어. 그러는 동안 한패인 신사들은 — 깨끗한 셔츠에 좋은 옷차림으로 인상은 더 음흉했지 — 새로 오는 사람마다 불안하게 곁눈질하며 이 일에 깊은 관심이 있음을 드러냈어. 이들은 떠돌이 곡예사 둘레에 빙 둘러선 사람들 바깥쪽에 어슬렁거렸지. 그 곡예사는 또 시끄러운 악대나 '황소 고리 던지기'라는 옛날 놀이에 밀려나기도 했어. 한편 복화술사들은 나무 인형과 대화를 나누었고, 점치는 여자들은 진짜 아기들의 울음소리를 틀어막았지. 이들 또한 다른 많은 것들과 함께 사람들의 관심을 골고루 나눠 가졌어. 술 천막은 꽉 찼고, 마차 안에서는 잔이 부딪히기 시작했지. 광주리가 풀리고, 군침 도는 음식이 차려졌어. 나이프와 포크가 달그락거렸고, 샴페인 코르크가 펑펑 날아올랐지. 흐릿하지 않던 눈들이 더 환하게 빛났고, 소매치기들은 지난 경주 동안 챙긴 수확을 세고 있었어. 조금 전까지 한 가지 흥밋거리에 쏠려 있던 관심은 이제 백 가지로 흩어졌지. 어디를 보든, 잔치를 벌이고 웃고 떠들고 구걸하고 노름하고 익살을 부리는 잡다한 무리가 있었어."]], [["Of the gambling-booths there was a plentiful show, flourishing in all the splendour of carpeted ground, striped hangings, crimson cloth, pinnacled roofs, geranium pots, and livery servants.", "노름 천막은 잔뜩 늘어서 있었어. 바닥에 깔린 양탄자며, 줄무늬 휘장이며, 진홍색 천이며, 뾰족한 지붕이며, 제라늄 화분이며, 제복 입은 하인들이며, 온갖 화려함을 뽐내며 성업 중이었지."], ["There were the Stranger’s club-house, the Athenaeum club-house, the Hampton club-house, the St James’s club-house, and half a mile of club-houses to play IN; and there were ROUGE-ET-NOIR, French hazard, and other games to play AT.", "'이방인 클럽 회관', '아테나이움 클럽 회관', '햄프턴 클럽 회관', '세인트 제임스 클럽 회관'이 있었고, 그 안에서 노름할 수 있는 클럽 회관이 반 마일이나 늘어서 있었어. 또 '루주 에 누아', '프렌치 해저드', 그 밖에 즐길 노름들도 있었지."], ["It is into one of these booths that our story takes its way.", "우리 이야기가 향하는 곳이 바로 이 천막들 가운데 하나야."]], [["Fitted up with three tables for the purposes of play, and crowded with players and lookers on, it was, although the largest place of the kind upon the course, intensely hot, notwithstanding that a portion of the canvas roof was rolled back to admit more air, and there were two doors for a free passage in and out. Excepting one or two men who, each with a long roll of half-crowns, chequered with a few stray sovereigns, in his left hand, staked their money at every roll of the ball with a business-like sedateness which showed that they were used to it, and had been playing all day, and most probably all the day before, there was no very distinctive character about the players, who were chiefly young men, apparently attracted by curiosity, or staking small sums as part of the amusement of the day, with no very great interest in winning or losing. There were two persons present, however, who, as peculiarly good specimens of a class, deserve a passing notice.", "노름을 위해 탁자 세 개가 갖춰져 있었고, 노름꾼과 구경꾼으로 미어터졌어. 경마장에서 그런 곳 가운데 가장 큰 곳이었는데도, 안은 푹푹 쪘지. 바람이 더 들어오라고 천막 지붕 천을 한쪽 걷어 놓았고, 드나들기 편하게 문도 두 개나 있었는데도 말이야. 한두 사람은 왼손에 길게 만 반 크라운 동전 뭉치를 쥐고 — 그 사이사이에 소버린 금화가 몇 개 끼어 있었지 — 공이 구를 때마다 차분하게, 마치 일하듯 능숙하게 돈을 걸었어. 이건 그들이 노름에 익숙하고 하루 종일, 아마 그 전날도 하루 종일 노름을 했다는 걸 보여 주었지. 그런 한두 사람을 빼면, 노름꾼들에게 별달리 두드러진 점은 없었어. 대개 젊은이들이었는데, 호기심에 끌렸거나, 그날의 놀이 삼아 작은 돈을 걸고 따든 잃든 그리 크게 마음 쓰지 않는 듯했지. 하지만 그 자리에는 두 사람이 있었어. 어떤 부류의 유난히 좋은 본보기라서, 잠깐 짚고 넘어갈 만하지."]], [["Of these, one was a man of six or eight and fifty, who sat on a chair near one of the entrances of the booth, with his hands folded on the top of his stick, and his chin appearing above them.", "이 가운데 한 사람은 쉰여섯에서 쉰여덟쯤 된 남자였어. 천막 입구 가운데 하나 가까이 의자에 앉아, 두 손을 지팡이 끝에 모으고 그 위로 턱을 괴고 있었지."], ["He was a tall, fat, long-bodied man, buttoned up to the throat in a light green coat, which made his body look still longer than it was.", "키가 크고 뚱뚱하며 몸통이 긴 남자였어. 연두색 외투를 목까지 단추로 채워서, 가뜩이나 긴 몸이 더 길어 보였지."], ["He wore, besides, drab breeches and gaiters, a white neckerchief, and a broad-brimmed white hat.", "그 밖에 누르스름한 반바지에 각반을 둘렀고, 흰 목도리를 매고, 챙이 넓은 흰 모자를 썼어."], ["Amid all the buzzing noise of the games, and the perpetual passing in and out of the people, he seemed perfectly calm and abstracted, without the smallest particle of excitement in his composition.", "노름판의 온갖 웅성거림과 끊임없이 드나드는 사람들 한가운데서, 그는 더없이 차분하고 멍하니 있는 듯했지. 그 어떤 흥분의 기색도 손톱만큼 없었어."], ["He exhibited no indication of weariness, nor, to a casual observer, of interest either.", "지친 기색도, 또 무심히 보는 이의 눈에는 무언가에 관심 있는 기색도 드러내지 않았지."], ["There he sat, quite still and collected.", "그는 거기 앉아 아주 가만히, 차분히 있었어."], ["Sometimes, but very rarely, he nodded to some passing face, or beckoned to a waiter to obey a call from one of the tables.", "가끔, 하지만 아주 드물게, 지나가는 얼굴에 고개를 까딱하거나, 탁자 쪽에서 부르는 소리에 응하라고 종업원에게 손짓했지."], ["The next instant he subsided into his old state.", "그러고는 다음 순간 다시 예전 상태로 가라앉았어."], ["He might have been some profoundly deaf old gentleman, who had come in to take a rest, or he might have been patiently waiting for a friend, without the least consciousness of anybody’s presence, or fixed in a trance, or under the influence of opium.", "그는 잠깐 쉬러 들어온 귀가 몹시 먼 노신사 같기도 했고, 누구의 존재도 전혀 의식하지 못한 채 친구를 끈기 있게 기다리는 사람 같기도 했지. 아니면 무아지경에 빠졌거나, 아편에 취한 사람 같기도 했어."], ["People turned round and looked at him; he made no gesture, caught nobody’s eye, let them pass away, and others come on and be succeeded by others, and took no notice.", "사람들이 돌아서서 그를 쳐다봤지만, 그는 아무런 몸짓도 하지 않았고, 누구와도 눈을 마주치지 않았어. 그들이 지나가게 두었고, 다른 이들이 와서 또 다른 이들로 이어지게 두면서, 아무것도 알아채지 못하는 척했지."], ["When he did move, it seemed wonderful how he could have seen anything to occasion it.", "그가 막상 움직일 때면, 대체 무얼 보고 그러는 건지 신기할 정도였어."], ["And so, in truth, it was.", "사실이 그랬지."], ["But there was not a face that passed in or out, which this man failed to see; not a gesture at any one of the three tables that was lost upon him; not a word, spoken by the bankers, but reached his ear; not a winner or loser he could not have marked.", "하지만 드나드는 얼굴 가운데 이 남자가 못 보고 지나치는 얼굴은 하나도 없었어. 세 탁자 어느 곳에서든 그의 눈을 벗어나는 몸짓 하나 없었고, 물주들이 내뱉는 말 한마디도 그의 귀에 안 들어오는 게 없었지."], ["And he was the proprietor of the place.", "그가 짚어 내지 못하는 승자나 패자도 없었어. 그리고 그가 바로 이곳의 주인이었지."]], [["The other presided over the ROUGE-ET-NOIR table. He was probably some ten years younger, and was a plump, paunchy, sturdy-looking fellow, with his under-lip a little pursed, from a habit of counting money inwardly as he paid it, but with no decidedly bad expression in his face, which was rather an honest and jolly one than otherwise. He wore no coat, the weather being hot, and stood behind the table with a huge mound of crowns and half-crowns before him, and a cash-box for notes. This game was constantly playing. Perhaps twenty people would be staking at the same time. This man had to roll the ball, to watch the stakes as they were laid down, to gather them off the colour which lost, to pay those who won, to do it all with the utmost dispatch, to roll the ball again, and to keep this game perpetually alive. He did it all with a rapidity absolutely marvellous; never hesitating, never making a mistake, never stopping, and never ceasing to repeat such unconnected phrases as the following, which, partly from habit, and partly to have something appropriate and business-like to say, he constantly poured out with the same monotonous emphasis, and in nearly the same order, all day long:", "다른 한 사람은 '루주 에 누아' 탁자를 맡고 있었어. 아마 열 살쯤 젊은 듯했고, 통통하고 배가 나오고 다부진 사내였지. 아랫입술이 조금 오므라들어 있었는데, 돈을 치를 때마다 속으로 세는 버릇 때문이었어. 하지만 얼굴에 딱히 나쁜 표정은 없었고, 오히려 정직하고 쾌활한 편이었지. 날씨가 더워서 외투는 걸치지 않았어. 그는 탁자 뒤에 서 있었는데, 앞에는 크라운과 반 크라운 동전이 산더미처럼 쌓여 있었고, 지폐를 넣는 돈궤도 있었지. 이 노름은 쉼 없이 돌아갔어. 한 번에 스무 명쯤이 돈을 걸기도 했지. 이 남자는 공을 굴려야 했고, 돈이 놓이는 걸 지켜봐야 했어. 진 색깔에서 돈을 거둬들이고, 이긴 사람에게 돈을 치러야 했지. 그 모든 걸 더없이 재빠르게 해치우고, 다시 공을 굴려, 이 노름을 끝없이 살아 있게 해야 했어. 그는 그 모든 걸 그야말로 놀라운 속도로 해냈지. 머뭇거리는 법도, 실수하는 법도, 멈추는 법도 없었어. 그리고 다음과 같은 두서없는 말들을 쉴 새 없이 늘어놓았지. 반은 버릇이고 반은 뭔가 그럴듯하고 일하는 티가 나는 말을 하려고, 하루 종일 똑같이 단조로운 강조를 실어, 거의 똑같은 순서로 줄줄 쏟아 냈어."]], [["‘Rooge-a-nore from Paris! Gentlemen, make your game and back your own opinions--any time while the ball rolls--rooge-a-nore from Paris, gentlemen, it’s a French game, gentlemen, I brought it over myself, I did indeed!--Rooge-a-nore from Paris--black wins--black--stop a minute, sir, and I’ll pay you, directly--two there, half a pound there, three there--and one there--gentlemen, the ball’s a rolling--any time, sir, while the ball rolls!--The beauty of this game is, that you can double your stakes or put down your money, gentlemen, any time while the ball rolls--black again--black wins--I never saw such a thing--I never did, in all my life, upon my word I never did; if any gentleman had been backing the black in the last five minutes he must have won five-and-forty pound in four rolls of the ball, he must indeed. Gentlemen, we’ve port, sherry, cigars, and most excellent champagne. Here, wai-ter, bring a bottle of champagne, and let’s have a dozen or fifteen cigars here--and let’s be comfortable, gentlemen--and bring some clean glasses--any time while the ball rolls!--I lost one hundred and thirty-seven pound yesterday, gentlemen, at one roll of the ball, I did indeed!--how do you do, sir?’ (recognising some knowing gentleman without any halt or change of voice, and giving a wink so slight that it seems an accident), ‘will you take a glass of sherry, sir?--here, wai-ter! bring a clean glass, and hand the sherry to this gentleman--and hand it round, will you, waiter?--this is the rooge-a-nore from Paris, gentlemen--any time while the ball rolls!--gentlemen, make your game, and back your own opinions--it’s the rooge-a-nore from Paris--quite a new game, I brought it over myself, I did indeed--gentlemen, the ball’s a-rolling!’", "\"파리에서 온 루주 에 누아입니다! 신사 여러분, 돈을 거시고 소신껏 걸어 보세요. 공이 구르는 동안에는 언제든 됩니다. 파리에서 온 루주 에 누아, 신사 여러분, 프랑스 노름입니다, 신사 여러분, 제가 직접 들여왔어요, 정말이라니까요! 파리에서 온 루주 에 누아, 검정 승! 검정! 잠깐만요, 손님, 곧 치러 드리겠습니다. 거기 둘, 거기 반 파운드, 거기 셋, 그리고 거기 하나. 신사 여러분, 공이 구르고 있습니다, 공이 구르는 동안에는 언제든 됩니다, 손님! 이 노름의 묘미는 말이죠, 신사 여러분, 공이 구르는 동안에는 언제든 판돈을 두 배로 걸거나 돈을 내려놓을 수 있다는 겁니다. 또 검정! 검정 승! 이런 건 처음 봅니다. 평생 한 번도 본 적이 없어요, 정말이지 단 한 번도요. 지난 오 분 동안 누구든 검정에 걸었다면 공이 네 번 구르는 사이에 마흔다섯 파운드는 땄을 겁니다, 틀림없이요. 신사 여러분, 포트와인, 셰리주, 시가, 그리고 더없이 훌륭한 샴페인도 있습니다. 여기, 종업원, 샴페인 한 병 가져오고, 여기 시가도 열두 개나 열다섯 개쯤 가져와요. 편안하게들 즐기시죠, 신사 여러분. 깨끗한 잔도 좀 가져오고요. 공이 구르는 동안에는 언제든 됩니다! 어제 저는 공이 한 번 구르는 데 백서른일곱 파운드를 잃었습니다, 신사 여러분, 정말이라니까요! 안녕하십니까, 손님?\"(말을 멈추거나 목소리를 바꾸지 않고 아는 신사 하나를 알아보며, 우연인가 싶을 만큼 살짝 윙크를 보냈지.) \"셰리주 한 잔 드시겠습니까, 손님? 여기, 종업원! 깨끗한 잔 하나 가져와서 이 신사분께 셰리주를 따라 드리고, 빙 돌려 드려요, 종업원? 이게 파리에서 온 루주 에 누아입니다, 신사 여러분. 공이 구르는 동안에는 언제든 됩니다! 신사 여러분, 돈을 거시고 소신껏 걸어 보세요. 파리에서 온 루주 에 누아입니다, 아주 새로운 노름이죠, 제가 직접 들여왔어요, 정말이라니까요. 신사 여러분, 공이 구르고 있습니다!\""]], [["This officer was busily plying his vocation when half-a-dozen persons sauntered through the booth, to whom, but without stopping either in his speech or work, he bowed respectfully; at the same time directing, by a look, the attention of a man beside him to the tallest figure in the group, in recognition of whom the proprietor pulled off his hat. This was Sir Mulberry Hawk, with whom were his friend and pupil, and a small train of gentlemanly-dressed men, of characters more doubtful than obscure.", "이 일꾼이 한창 제 일에 매달려 있는데, 예닐곱 사람이 천막을 어슬렁거리며 지나갔어. 그는 말도 일도 멈추지 않으면서, 그들에게 정중히 고개를 숙였지. 동시에 곁에 있던 사람에게 눈짓으로 그 무리 가운데 가장 키 큰 사람을 가리켰어. 그를 알아본 주인이 모자를 벗었지. 바로 멀버리 호크 경이었어. 그와 함께 그의 친구이자 제자, 그리고 신사 차림을 한 사내들 몇이 따라왔지. 정체가 모호하다기보다는 수상쩍은 자들이었어."]], [["The proprietor, in a low voice, bade Sir Mulberry good-day.", "주인이 낮은 목소리로 멀버리 경에게 안녕하시냐고 인사했어."], ["Sir Mulberry, in the same tone, bade the proprietor go to the devil, and turned to speak with his friends.", "멀버리 경은 똑같이 낮은 목소리로 주인더러 꺼지라고 하고는, 돌아서서 친구들과 이야기를 나누었지."]], [["There was evidently an irritable consciousness about him that he was an object of curiosity, on this first occasion of showing himself in public after the accident that had befallen him; and it was easy to perceive that he appeared on the race-course, that day, more in the hope of meeting with a great many people who knew him, and so getting over as much as possible of the annoyance at once, than with any purpose of enjoying the sport. There yet remained a slight scar upon his face, and whenever he was recognised, as he was almost every minute by people sauntering in and out, he made a restless effort to conceal it with his glove; showing how keenly he felt the disgrace he had undergone.", "그가 호기심의 대상이 되었다는 걸 짜증스러워하며 의식하고 있는 게 분명했어. 자기에게 닥친 사고 이후 처음으로 사람들 앞에 모습을 드러내는 자리였으니까. 그리고 쉽게 알 수 있었지. 그날 그가 경마장에 나온 건 경기를 즐기려는 마음보다는, 자기를 아는 사람을 잔뜩 만나서 그 짜증스러운 일을 한꺼번에 최대한 넘겨 버리려는 속셈이었어. 얼굴에는 아직 옅은 흉터가 남아 있었지. 그리고 거의 매분마다 어슬렁거리며 드나드는 사람들에게 알아보일 때마다, 그는 장갑으로 그 흉터를 가리려고 안절부절못했어. 자기가 당한 망신을 얼마나 뼈저리게 느끼는지 드러내는 거였지."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아! 호크.\""], ["Hawk,’ said one very sprucely-dressed personage in a Newmarket coat, a choice neckerchief, and all other accessories of the most unexceptionable kind.", "무척 말쑥하게 차려입은 한 인물이 말했어. 뉴마켓 외투를 입고, 고급 목도리를 매고, 그 밖에 흠잡을 데 없는 온갖 장신구를 두르고 있었지."], ["‘How d’ye do, old fellow?’", "\"잘 지냈나, 친구?\""]], [["This was a rival trainer of young noblemen and gentlemen, and the person of all others whom Sir Mulberry most hated and dreaded to meet.", "이자는 젊은 귀족과 신사들을 길들이는 일에서 경쟁자였어. 멀버리 경이 무엇보다 가장 싫어하고 마주치기를 두려워하는 자였지."], ["They shook hands with excessive cordiality.", "둘은 더없이 살갑게 악수를 나눴어."]], [["‘And how are you now, old fellow, hey?’", "\"그래, 이제 좀 어떤가, 친구, 응?\""]], [["‘Quite well, quite well,’ said Sir Mulberry.", "\"아주 좋아, 아주 좋아.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어."]], [["‘That’s right,’ said the other.", "\"다행이군.\" 상대가 말했지."], ["‘How d’ye do, Verisopht?", "\"잘 지내나, 버리소프트?"], ["He’s a little pulled down, our friend here.", "우리 친구가 좀 풀이 죽었군."], ["Rather out of condition still, hey?’", "아직 컨디션이 영 아닌가, 응?\""]], [["It should be observed that the gentleman had very white teeth, and that when there was no excuse for laughing, he generally finished with the same monosyllable, which he uttered so as to display them.", "이 신사는 이가 무척 하얗다는 걸 짚어 둬야겠어. 그리고 웃을 핑계가 없을 때면, 대개 그 똑같은 외마디로 말을 맺었는데, 그걸 이가 드러나게 발음하곤 했지."]], [["‘He’s in very good condition; there’s nothing the matter with him,’ said the young man carelessly.", "\"이 친구 컨디션 아주 좋아. 아무 문제 없어.\" 젊은이가 무심하게 말했어."]], [["‘Upon my soul I’m glad to hear it,’ rejoined the other.", "\"이거 정말 다행이군.\" 상대가 받아쳤지."], ["‘Have you just returned from Brussels?’", "\"브뤼셀에서 막 돌아왔나?\""]], [["‘We only reached town late last night,’ said Lord Frederick.", "\"어젯밤 늦게야 런던에 닿았어.\" 프레더릭 경이 말했어."], ["Sir Mulberry turned away to speak to one of his own party, and feigned not to hear.", "멀버리 경은 자기 일행 가운데 한 사람에게 말을 걸려고 돌아서며, 못 들은 척했지."]], [["‘Now, upon my life,’ said the friend, affecting to speak in a whisper, ‘it’s an uncommonly bold and game thing in Hawk to show himself so soon.", "\"이거 참, 정말이지,\" 친구가 속삭이는 척하며 말했어."], ["I say it advisedly; there’s a vast deal of courage in it.", "\"이렇게 빨리 모습을 드러내다니 호크도 어지간히 대담하고 배짱 좋군. 내 신중히 하는 말일세."], ["You see he has just rusticated long enough to excite curiosity, and not long enough for men to have forgotten that deuced unpleasant--by-the-bye--you know the rights of the affair, of course?", "거기엔 엄청난 용기가 필요하거든. 보다시피 그는 딱 호기심을 자극할 만큼만 시골에 처박혀 있었지, 사람들이 그 빌어먹게 불쾌한 일을 잊어버릴 만큼은 아니었어."], ["Why did you never give those confounded papers the lie?", "그건 그렇고, 자네야 물론 그 일의 진상을 알겠지? 어째서 그 망할 신문들에 거짓말이라고 따지지 않았나?"], ["I seldom read the papers, but I looked in the papers for that, and may I be--’", "나야 신문을 거의 안 보지만, 그 일만큼은 신문에서 찾아봤지. 그런데 내가 정말이지—\""]], [["‘Look in the papers,’ interrupted Sir Mulberry, turning suddenly round, ‘tomorrow--no, next day, will you?’", "\"신문을 보게.\" 멀버리 경이 갑자기 홱 돌아서며 끼어들었어. \"내일—아니, 모레, 보겠나?\""]], [["‘Upon my life, my dear fellow, I seldom or never read the papers,’ said the other, shrugging his shoulders, ‘but I will, at your recommendation.", "\"이거 참, 친구, 나야 신문을 보는 일이 거의, 아니 아예 없지만,\" 상대가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했어."], ["What shall I look for?’", "\"자네가 권하니 보겠네. 뭘 찾아보면 되나?\""]], [["‘Good day,’ said Sir Mulberry, turning abruptly on his heel, and drawing his pupil with him.", "\"그럼 잘 가게.\" 멀버리 경이 홱 발길을 돌리며 제자를 끌어당겼어."], ["Falling, again, into the loitering, careless pace at which they had entered, they lounged out, arm in arm.", "그러고는 들어올 때처럼 다시 어슬렁어슬렁 무심한 걸음으로, 둘은 팔짱을 끼고 느릿느릿 빠져나갔지."]], [["‘I won’t give him a case of murder to read,’ muttered Sir Mulberry with an oath; ‘but it shall be something very near it if whipcord cuts and bludgeons bruise.’", "\"그자한테 살인 사건을 읽으라고 주진 않겠어.\" 멀버리 경이 욕설을 섞어 중얼거렸지. \"하지만 채찍이 살을 가르고 몽둥이가 멍을 들게 한다면, 그거에 아주 가까운 걸 읽게 되겠지.\""]], [["His companion said nothing, but there was something in his manner which galled Sir Mulberry to add, with nearly as much ferocity as if his friend had been Nicholas himself:", "그의 동행은 아무 말도 하지 않았어. 하지만 그 태도에 뭔가가 있어서, 멀버리 경은 자극을 받아 마치 그 친구가 니콜라스 본인이라도 되는 양 거의 똑같이 사납게 덧붙였지."]], [["‘I sent Jenkins to old Nickleby before eight o’clock this morning.", "\"오늘 아침 여덟 시도 되기 전에 젱킨스를 늙은 니클비한테 보냈어."], ["He’s a staunch one; he was back with me before the messenger.", "충직한 놈이야. 심부름꾼보다 먼저 나한테 돌아왔지."], ["I had it all from him in the first five minutes.", "처음 오 분 만에 다 알아냈어."], ["I know where this hound is to be met with; time and place both.", "이 개자식을 어디서 만날 수 있는지 안다고. 시간도 장소도 다."], ["But there’s no need to talk; tomorrow will soon be here.’", "하지만 떠들 것 없어. 내일은 곧 올 테니까.\""]], [["‘And wha-at’s to be done tomorrow?’ inquired Lord Frederick.", "\"그래서 내일 무, 무얼 할 셈인가?\" 프레더릭 경이 물었어."]], [["Sir Mulberry Hawk honoured him with an angry glance, but condescended to return no verbal answer to this inquiry.", "멀버리 호크 경은 성난 눈길을 한 번 던지는 걸로 답을 대신했지. 하지만 이 물음에 말로는 한마디도 답하지 않으려 했어."], ["Both walked sullenly on, as though their thoughts were busily occupied, until they were quite clear of the crowd, and almost alone, when Sir Mulberry wheeled round to return.", "둘은 마치 생각에 골똘히 잠긴 듯 시무룩하게 걸었지. 그러다 사람들 무리에서 완전히 벗어나 거의 단둘이 되자, 멀버리 경이 돌아가려고 몸을 휙 돌렸어."]], [["‘Stop,’ said his companion, ‘I want to speak to you in earnest.", "\"멈춰.\" 그의 동행이 말했어."], ["Don’t turn back.", "\"자네한테 진지하게 할 말이 있어."], ["Let us walk here, a few minutes.’", "돌아가지 말게. 여기서 몇 분만 걷지.\""]], [["‘What have you to say to me, that you could not say yonder as well as here?’ returned his Mentor, disengaging his arm.", "\"여기서가 아니라 저기서도 똑같이 할 수 있는 말을, 대체 나한테 뭐라고 하려는 건가?\" 그의 스승이 팔을 빼며 받아쳤어."]], [["‘Hawk,’ rejoined the other, ‘tell me; I must know.’", "\"호크.\" 상대가 받아쳤지. \"말해 주게. 난 꼭 알아야겠어.\""]], [["‘MUST know,’ interrupted the other disdainfully.", "\"꼭 알아야겠다고.\" 상대가 깔보듯 끼어들었어."], ["‘Whew!", "\"휘유!"], ["Go on.", "말해 보게."], ["If you must know, of course there’s no escape for me.", "자네가 꼭 알아야겠다면, 물론 나야 빠져나갈 길이 없겠지."], ["Must know!’", "꼭 알아야겠다라!\""]], [["‘Must ask then,’ returned Lord Frederick, ‘and must press you for a plain and straightforward answer.", "\"그럼 꼭 물어야겠어.\" 프레더릭 경이 받아쳤지."], ["Is what you have just said only a mere whim of the moment, occasioned by your being out of humour and irritated, or is it your serious intention, and one that you have actually contemplated?’", "\"그리고 솔직하고 분명한 답을 꼭 들어야겠어. 방금 자네가 한 말은, 그저 기분이 상하고 짜증이 나서 순간적으로 든 변덕인가, 아니면 자네의 진지한 뜻이고 실제로 마음먹은 일인가?\""]], [["‘Why, don’t you remember what passed on the subject one night, when I was laid up with a broken limb?’ said Sir Mulberry, with a sneer.", "\"아니, 그 일에 관해 어느 날 밤에 오간 이야기를 기억 못 하나? 내가 다리가 부러져 누워 있을 때 말이야.\" 멀버리 경이 비웃으며 말했어."]], [["‘Perfectly well.’", "\"아주 똑똑히 기억하지.\""]], [["‘Then take that for an answer, in the devil’s name,’ replied Sir Mulberry, ‘and ask me for no other.’", "\"그럼 그걸 답으로 알아, 빌어먹을,\" 멀버리 경이 대꾸했지. \"그리고 다른 답은 묻지 마.\""]], [["Such was the ascendancy he had acquired over his dupe, and such the latter’s general habit of submission, that, for the moment, the young man seemed half afraid to pursue the subject.", "그가 제 봉에게 휘두르던 위세가 이만했고, 또 그 젊은이가 평소 굽히는 게 몸에 배어 있어서, 순간 젊은이는 그 일을 더 캐묻기를 반쯤 겁내는 듯했어."], ["He soon overcame this feeling, however, if it had restrained him at all, and retorted angrily:", "하지만 그 마음이 잠시나마 그를 붙들었다 해도, 곧 그걸 떨쳐 내고 성을 내며 받아쳤지."]], [["‘If I remember what passed at the time you speak of, I expressed a strong opinion on this subject, and said that, with my knowledge or consent, you never should do what you threaten now.’", "\"자네가 말하는 그때 오간 이야기를 기억한다면, 난 이 일에 관해 강한 의견을 밝혔어. 내가 알거나 동의하는 한, 자네가 지금 으름장 놓는 그런 짓은 절대 못 한다고 말했지.\""]], [["‘Will you prevent me?’ asked Sir Mulberry, with a laugh.", "\"자네가 날 막겠다고?\" 멀버리 경이 웃으며 물었어."]], [["‘Ye-es, if I can,’ returned the other, promptly.", "\"그, 그래, 할 수 있다면.\" 상대가 곧장 대꾸했지."]], [["‘A very proper saving clause, that last,’ said Sir Mulberry; ‘and one you stand in need of.", "\"마지막 그 단서가 아주 적절하군.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어."], ["Oh!", "\"또 자네한테 꼭 필요한 단서고."], ["look to your own business, and leave me to look to mine.’", "오! 자네 일이나 신경 쓰고, 내 일은 나한테 맡겨 두게.\""]], [["‘This IS mine,’ retorted Lord Frederick.", "\"이건 내 일이야.\" 프레더릭 경이 받아쳤지."], ["‘I make it mine; I will make it mine.", "\"내가 내 일로 삼겠어. 내 일로 삼고 말 거야."], ["It’s mine already.", "이미 내 일이지."], ["I am more compromised than I should be, as it is.’", "지금만 해도 난 마땅한 것보다 더 깊이 얽혀 있어.\""]], [["‘Do as you please, and what you please, for yourself,’ said Sir Mulberry, affecting an easy good-humour.", "\"자네 좋을 대로, 자네 하고 싶은 대로, 자네 자신을 위해 하게.\" 멀버리 경이 짐짓 느긋하고 너그러운 척하며 말했어."], ["‘Surely that must content you!", "\"그럼 분명 만족스럽겠지!"], ["Do nothing for me; that’s all.", "날 위해서는 아무것도 하지 마."], ["I advise no man to interfere in proceedings that I choose to take.", "그게 다야. 내가 하기로 마음먹은 일에 끼어들라고 누구한테도 권하지 않아."], ["I am sure you know me better than to do so.", "자네는 날 그것보단 잘 알 텐데."], ["The fact is, I see, you mean to offer me advice.", "사실 보아하니 자네는 나한테 충고를 하려는 모양이군."], ["It is well meant, I have no doubt, but I reject it.", "좋은 뜻인 건 의심하지 않아. 하지만 난 거절하네."], ["Now, if you please, we will return to the carriage.", "자, 괜찮다면 마차로 돌아가지."], ["I find no entertainment here, but quite the reverse.", "여긴 즐길 게 없어, 오히려 그 반대야."], ["If we prolong this conversation, we might quarrel, which would be no proof of wisdom in either you or me.’", "이 이야기를 더 끌면 다툴지도 몰라. 그건 자네한테든 나한테든 현명한 짓이 못 되지.\""]], [["With this rejoinder, and waiting for no further discussion, Sir Mulberry Hawk yawned, and very leisurely turned back.", "이렇게 받아치고는, 더 이상 따질 틈도 주지 않고 멀버리 호크 경은 하품을 하더니 아주 느긋하게 발길을 돌렸어."]], [["There was not a little tact and knowledge of the young lord’s disposition in this mode of treating him.", "이렇게 그를 다루는 방식에는 적잖은 요령과 젊은 귀족의 기질에 대한 이해가 담겨 있었어. 멀버리 경은 똑똑히 알았지."], ["Sir Mulberry clearly saw that if his dominion were to last, it must be established now.", "자기 지배력이 이어지려면 지금 다져 놓아야 한다는 걸. 그는 자기가 거칠게 나가는 순간 젊은이도 거칠게 나오리라는 걸 알았어."], ["He knew that the moment he became violent, the young man would become violent too.", "지배력을 약하게 만드는 무슨 일이 생길 때마다, 그는 여러 번 이런 냉정하고 짤막한 태도를 취해서 영향력을 다시 굳히곤 했지."], ["He had, many times, been enabled to strengthen his influence, when any circumstance had occurred to weaken it, by adopting this cool and laconic style; and he trusted to it now, with very little doubt of its entire success.", "그래서 이번에도 거기에 기댔어. 그게 완전히 먹히리라는 걸 거의 의심하지 않으면서 말이야."]], [["But while he did this, and wore the most careless and indifferent deportment that his practised arts enabled him to assume, he inwardly resolved, not only to visit all the mortification of being compelled to suppress his feelings, with additional severity upon Nicholas, but also to make the young lord pay dearly for it, one day, in some shape or other.", "하지만 그는 이렇게 하면서, 또 갈고닦은 재주로 지을 수 있는 가장 무심하고 심드렁한 태도를 꾸미면서, 속으로 결심했지. 감정을 억눌러야 했던 그 모든 굴욕을 니콜라스에게 한층 더 호되게 갚아 주겠다고."], ["So long as he had been a passive instrument in his hands, Sir Mulberry had regarded him with no other feeling than contempt; but, now that he presumed to avow opinions in opposition to his, and even to turn upon him with a lofty tone and an air of superiority, he began to hate him.", "그뿐 아니라 언젠가 어떤 식으로든 젊은 귀족도 그 대가를 톡톡히 치르게 만들겠다고 말이야. 젊은이가 자기 손안에서 고분고분한 도구일 때는, 멀버리 경은 그를 그저 경멸의 눈으로만 봤어."], ["Conscious that, in the vilest and most worthless sense of the term, he was dependent upon the weak young lord, Sir Mulberry could the less brook humiliation at his hands; and when he began to dislike him he measured his dislike--as men often do--by the extent of the injuries he had inflicted upon its object.", "하지만 이제 감히 자기와 반대되는 의견을 내세우고, 게다가 거만한 말투와 잘난 척하는 태도로 대드니, 그는 젊은이가 미워지기 시작했지. 더없이 천하고 하잘것없는 의미에서 자기가 그 약한 젊은 귀족에게 빌붙어 산다는 걸 알기에, 멀버리 경은 그의 손에 굴욕을 당하는 걸 더더욱 참을 수 없었어."], ["When it is remembered that Sir Mulberry Hawk had plundered, duped, deceived, and fooled his pupil in every possible way, it will not be wondered at, that, beginning to hate him, he began to hate him cordially.", "그리고 그가 젊은이를 싫어하기 시작하자, 그 미움의 크기를 — 사람들이 흔히 그러듯 — 자기가 그 상대에게 입힌 해악의 정도로 가늠했지. 멀버리 호크 경이 제자를 온갖 수단으로 등쳐 먹고, 속이고, 기만하고, 골려 먹었다는 걸 떠올려 보면, 그가 젊은이를 미워하기 시작하면서 진심으로 미워하게 된 것도 놀랄 일이 아니야."]], [["On the other hand, the young lord having thought--which he very seldom did about anything--and seriously too, upon the affair with Nicholas, and the circumstances which led to it, had arrived at a manly and honest conclusion.", "한편 젊은 귀족은 — 무슨 일에 대해서든 거의 하지 않던 짓이지만 — 니콜라스와의 일과 그 일로 이어진 정황을 곰곰이, 그것도 진지하게 생각해 본 끝에, 사내답고 정직한 결론에 다다랐어. 문제의 그 사건에서 멀버리 경이 보인 거칠고 모욕적인 행동이 그의 마음에 깊은 인상을 남겼거든."], ["Sir Mulberry’s coarse and insulting behaviour on the occasion in question had produced a deep impression on his mind; a strong suspicion of his having led him on to pursue Miss Nickleby for purposes of his own, had been lurking there for some time; he was really ashamed of his share in the transaction, and deeply mortified by the misgiving that he had been gulled.", "멀버리 경이 제 속셈을 채우려고 자기를 부추겨 니클비 양을 쫓게 했다는 강한 의심이, 얼마 전부터 마음속에 도사리고 있었지. 그는 자기가 그 일에 끼었던 게 정말 부끄러웠고, 자기가 속아 넘어갔다는 께름칙함에 깊이 괴로웠어."], ["He had had sufficient leisure to reflect upon these things, during their late retirement; and, at times, when his careless and indolent nature would permit, had availed himself of the opportunity.", "요즈음 시골에 물러나 있는 동안, 그는 이런 일들을 곱씹어 볼 시간이 넉넉했지. 그리고 그 무심하고 게으른 천성이 허락하는 때면, 이따금 그 기회를 활용했어."], ["Slight circumstances, too, had occurred to increase his suspicion.", "자잘한 일들도 일어나서 그의 의심을 키웠지."], ["It wanted but a very slight circumstance to kindle his wrath against Sir Mulberry.", "멀버리 경에 대한 그의 분노에 불을 붙이는 데는 아주 자잘한 일 하나면 충분했어."], ["This his disdainful and insolent tone in their recent conversation (the only one they had held upon the subject since the period to which Sir Mulberry referred), effected.", "그리고 방금 나눈 대화에서 멀버리 경이 보인 그 깔보고 거만한 말투가 — 멀버리 경이 언급한 그때 이후로 이 일에 관해 둘이 나눈 유일한 대화였지 — 바로 그 불을 댕긴 거였어."]], [["Thus they rejoined their friends: each with causes of dislike against the other rankling in his breast: and the young man haunted, besides, with thoughts of the vindictive retaliation which was threatened against Nicholas, and the determination to prevent it by some strong step, if possible.", "이렇게 둘은 친구들에게 돌아왔어. 저마다 상대를 향한 미움의 까닭을 가슴속에 사무치게 품은 채로 말이야."], ["But this was not all.", "게다가 젊은이는 니콜라스를 향해 으름장 놓아진 그 앙심에 찬 보복 생각에, 또 가능하다면 무슨 강한 수를 써서라도 그걸 막겠다는 결심에 시달렸지. 하지만 그게 다가 아니었어."], ["Sir Mulberry, conceiving that he had silenced him effectually, could not suppress his triumph, or forbear from following up what he conceived to be his advantage.", "멀버리 경은 자기가 젊은이의 입을 확실히 막았다고 여기고는, 그 우쭐함을 누르지 못했어. 자기가 우위를 잡았다 싶은 걸 끝까지 밀어붙이지 않고는 못 배겼지."], ["Mr. Pyke was there, and Mr.", "거기엔 파이크 씨도 있었고, 플럭 씨도 있었어."], ["Pluck was there, and Colonel Chowser, and other gentlemen of the same caste, and it was a great point for Sir Mulberry to show them that he had not lost his influence.", "차우저 대령도, 같은 부류의 다른 신사들도 있었지. 멀버리 경에게는 자기가 영향력을 잃지 않았다는 걸 그들에게 보여 주는 게 큰일이었어."], ["At first, the young lord contented himself with a silent determination to take measures for withdrawing himself from the connection immediately.", "처음에 젊은 귀족은 당장 그 관계에서 발을 빼기로 조용히 결심하는 걸로 만족했지. 하지만 차츰 화가 치밀었어."], ["By degrees, he grew more angry, and was exasperated by jests and familiarities which, a few hours before, would have been a source of amusement to him.", "몇 시간 전이었다면 재밋거리였을 농담과 허물없는 짓에 약이 올랐지. 그래도 그건 그에게 도움이 안 됐어."], ["This did not serve him; for, at such bantering or retort as suited the company, he was no match for Sir Mulberry.", "일행한테 어울리는 그런 빈정거림이나 받아치기에서는 멀버리 경의 상대가 못 되었거든. 그래도 거칠게 틀어지는 일은 일어나지 않았어."], ["Still, no violent rupture took place.", "그들은 런던으로 돌아갔지."], ["They returned to town; Messrs Pyke and Pluck and other gentlemen frequently protesting, on the way thither, that Sir Mulberry had never been in such tiptop spirits in all his life.", "파이크 씨와 플럭 씨, 그 밖의 신사들은 돌아가는 길에 자주 떠들어 댔어. 멀버리 경이 평생 이렇게 기분이 째진 적이 없었다고 말이야."]], [["They dined together, sumptuously.", "그들은 함께 푸짐하게 저녁을 먹었어."], ["The wine flowed freely, as indeed it had done all day.", "술이 거침없이 흘렀지, 사실 하루 종일 그랬듯이 말이야."], ["Sir Mulberry drank to recompense himself for his recent abstinence; the young lord, to drown his indignation; and the remainder of the party, because the wine was of the best and they had nothing to pay.", "멀버리 경은 최근 술을 끊은 걸 벌충하려고 마셨고, 젊은 귀족은 분을 가라앉히려고 마셨어. 나머지 일행은 술이 최상품인 데다 자기들이 낼 돈이 없었으니까 마셨지."], ["It was nearly midnight when they rushed out, wild, burning with wine, their blood boiling, and their brains on fire, to the gaming-table.", "그들이 술에 미친 듯 취하고, 피가 끓고, 머릿속이 불타오른 채 노름판으로 우르르 몰려 나간 건 거의 자정 무렵이었어."]], [["Here, they encountered another party, mad like themselves.", "거기서 그들은 자기들처럼 미쳐 있는 또 다른 무리와 마주쳤어. 노름의 흥분과 후끈한 방, 번쩍이는 불빛은 그 흥분의 열기를 가라앉힐 만한 게 못 됐지."], ["The excitement of play, hot rooms, and glaring lights was not calculated to allay the fever of the time.", "그 어질어질한 소음과 혼란의 소용돌이 속에서, 사내들은 제정신이 아니었어. 이 순간의 사납게 취한 흥분 속에서 누가 돈이며 파산이며 내일을 떠올렸겠어?"], ["In that giddy whirl of noise and confusion, the men were delirious.", "술을 더 가져오라고 외쳤고, 잔이 연거푸 비워졌지. 바싹 마르고 타들어 가는 입은 갈증으로 갈라졌어."], ["Who thought of money, ruin, or the morrow, in the savage intoxication of the moment?", "술은 활활 타는 불에 기름을 붓듯 쏟아졌지. 그런데도 난동은 계속됐어."], ["More wine was called for, glass after glass was drained, their parched and scalding mouths were cracked with thirst.", "흥청거림은 절정에 이르렀지. 손이 잔을 입까지 가져가지도 못하고 바닥에 내동댕이쳤어."], ["Down poured the wine like oil on blazing fire.", "욕설을 내뱉을 말조차 제대로 빚어내지 못하는 입에서 욕설이 터져 나왔지."], ["And still the riot went on.", "술 취해 돈을 잃은 자들은 저주하고 고래고래 소리쳤어."], ["The debauchery gained its height; glasses were dashed upon the floor by hands that could not carry them to lips; oaths were shouted out by lips which could scarcely form the words to vent them in; drunken losers cursed and roared; some mounted on the tables, waving bottles above their heads and bidding defiance to the rest; some danced, some sang, some tore the cards and raved.", "어떤 자들은 탁자 위에 올라가 술병을 머리 위로 흔들며 나머지에게 덤벼 보라고 했지. 어떤 자들은 춤추고, 어떤 자들은 노래하고, 어떤 자들은 카드를 찢으며 미친 듯이 날뛰었어."], ["Tumult and frenzy reigned supreme; when a noise arose that drowned all others, and two men, seizing each other by the throat, struggled into the middle of the room.", "소란과 광기가 천지를 뒤덮었지. 그때 다른 모든 소리를 삼켜 버리는 소음이 일었고, 두 남자가 서로의 멱살을 움켜쥔 채 몸싸움을 하며 방 한가운데로 휘청휘청 나왔어."]], [["A dozen voices, until now unheard, called aloud to part them.", "그때까지 들리지 않던 열댓 명의 목소리가 둘을 떼어 놓으라고 큰 소리로 외쳤어."], ["Those who had kept themselves cool, to win, and who earned their living in such scenes, threw themselves upon the combatants, and, forcing them asunder, dragged them some space apart.", "따려고 정신을 차리고 있던 자들, 그런 판에서 밥벌이를 하는 자들이 싸우는 둘에게 달려들었지. 그러고는 둘을 억지로 갈라놓고 얼마쯤 떼어 놓았어."]], [["‘Let me go!’ cried Sir Mulberry, in a thick hoarse voice; ‘he struck me!", "\"놓으시오!\" 멀버리 경이 걸걸하고 쉰 목소리로 외쳤어."], ["Do you hear?", "\"이자가 날 쳤소!"], ["I say, he struck me.", "들었소? 이자가 날 쳤다니까."], ["Have I a friend here?", "여기 내 편 없소?"], ["Who is this?", "이게 누구요?"], ["Westwood.", "웨스트우드."], ["Do you hear me say he struck me?’", "이자가 날 쳤다고 내가 하는 말 들었소?\""]], [["‘I hear, I hear,’ replied one of those who held him.", "\"들었어, 들었어.\" 그를 붙든 자들 가운데 하나가 대꾸했지."], ["‘Come away for tonight!’", "\"오늘 밤은 그만 가세!\""]], [["‘I will not, by G--,’ he replied.", "\"못 가, 절대로,\" 그가 대꾸했어."], ["‘A dozen men about us saw the blow.’", "\"여기 열댓 명이 그 매질을 봤어.\""]], [["‘Tomorrow will be ample time,’ said the friend.", "\"내일이면 시간은 넉넉해.\" 친구가 말했지."]], [["‘It will not be ample time!’ cried Sir Mulberry.", "\"시간은 넉넉하지 않아!\" 멀버리 경이 외쳤어."], ["‘Tonight, at once, here!’ His passion was so great, that he could not articulate, but stood clenching his fist, tearing his hair, and stamping upon the ground.", "\"오늘 밤, 당장, 여기서!\" 그의 격분이 어찌나 컸던지 말도 또박또박 못 하고, 주먹을 부르쥐고 머리카락을 쥐어뜯고 발을 쾅쾅 구르며 서 있었지."]], [["‘What is this, my lord?’ said one of those who surrounded him.", "\"이게 무슨 일입니까, 각하?\" 그를 둘러싼 자들 가운데 하나가 말했어."], ["‘Have blows passed?’", "\"매질이 오갔습니까?\""]], [["‘ONE blow has,’ was the panting reply.", "\"한 대 오갔지.\" 헐떡이며 답이 나왔어."], ["‘I struck him.", "\"내가 그를 쳤소."], ["I proclaim it to all here!", "여기 모두에게 알리는 바요!"], ["I struck him, and he knows why.", "내가 그를 쳤고, 그도 왜인지 알아."], ["I say, with him, let this quarrel be adjusted now.", "그와 함께, 이 다툼을 지금 매듭짓겠소. 애덤스 대위,\""], ["Captain Adams,’ said the young lord, looking hurriedly about him, and addressing one of those who had interposed, ‘let me speak with you, I beg.’", "젊은 귀족이 황급히 둘레를 둘러보며, 끼어들었던 자들 가운데 하나에게 말했어. \"이야기 좀 합시다, 부탁이오.\""]], [["The person addressed stepped forward, and taking the young man’s arm, they retired together, followed shortly afterwards by Sir Mulberry and his friend.", "말을 들은 사람이 앞으로 나서서 젊은이의 팔을 잡고, 둘은 함께 물러났어. 곧이어 멀버리 경과 그의 친구도 뒤따랐지."]], [["It was a profligate haunt of the worst repute, and not a place in which such an affair was likely to awaken any sympathy for either party, or to call forth any further remonstrance or interposition. Elsewhere, its further progress would have been instantly prevented, and time allowed for sober and cool reflection; but not there. Disturbed in their orgies, the party broke up; some reeled away with looks of tipsy gravity; others withdrew noisily discussing what had just occurred; the gentlemen of honour who lived upon their winnings remarked to each other, as they went out, that Hawk was a good shot; and those who had been most noisy, fell fast asleep upon the sofas, and thought no more about it.", "거긴 더없이 평판 나쁜 방탕한 소굴이었어. 그런 일이 어느 쪽이든 동정을 불러일으키거나, 더 이상의 만류나 끼어듦을 부를 만한 곳이 못 됐지. 다른 데였다면 그 일이 더 번지는 걸 당장 막고, 차분하고 냉정하게 돌아볼 틈을 주었을 거야. 하지만 거긴 아니었어. 흥청거림이 깨진 무리는 흩어졌지. 어떤 자들은 취해서 짐짓 점잔 빼는 얼굴로 비틀비틀 가 버렸고, 어떤 자들은 방금 벌어진 일을 시끄럽게 떠들며 물러났어. 남이 잃은 돈으로 먹고사는 명예로운 신사들은 나가면서 서로에게 호크가 명사수라고 했지. 그리고 가장 시끄럽게 굴던 자들은 소파에 곯아떨어져, 그 일은 더 생각지도 않았어."]], [["Meanwhile, the two seconds, as they may be called now, after a long conference, each with his principal, met together in another room. Both utterly heartless, both men upon town, both thoroughly initiated in its worst vices, both deeply in debt, both fallen from some higher estate, both addicted to every depravity for which society can find some genteel name and plead its most depraving conventionalities as an excuse, they were naturally gentlemen of most unblemished honour themselves, and of great nicety concerning the honour of other people.", "그러는 동안 이제 입회인이라 부를 만한 두 사람은, 저마다 제 당사자와 길게 이야기를 나눈 뒤 다른 방에서 만났어. 둘 다 더없이 매정했고, 둘 다 도시 한량이었지. 둘 다 도시의 더없이 못된 악덕에 푹 절어 있었고, 둘 다 빚에 깊이 빠져 있었어. 둘 다 한때 더 높은 자리에서 굴러떨어졌고, 둘 다 세상이 그럴듯한 이름을 붙여 주고 가장 타락한 관습을 핑계로 둘러대 주는 온갖 타락에 빠져 있었지. 그러니 당연히 그들 자신은 더없이 흠 없는 명예의 신사들이었고, 남의 명예에 대해서는 무척이나 까다로웠어."]], [["These two gentlemen were unusually cheerful just now; for the affair was pretty certain to make some noise, and could scarcely fail to enhance their reputations.", "이 두 신사는 지금 유달리 들떠 있었어. 이 일은 꽤나 떠들썩해질 게 뻔했고, 그들의 명성을 드높여 줄 게 틀림없었거든."]], [["‘This is an awkward affair, Adams,’ said Mr.", "\"이거 거북한 일이군요, 애덤스.\""], ["Westwood, drawing himself up.", "웨스트우드 씨가 몸을 곧추세우며 말했어."]], [["‘Very,’ returned the captain; ‘a blow has been struck, and there is but one course, OF course.’", "\"아주요.\" 대위가 대꾸했지. \"매질이 오갔으니, 길은 당연히 하나뿐이지요.\""]], [["‘No apology, I suppose?’ said Mr.", "\"사과는 없겠지요?\""], ["Westwood.", "웨스트우드 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Not a syllable, sir, from my man, if we talk till doomsday,’ returned the captain.", "\"우리가 최후의 심판 날까지 떠든들, 제 쪽 사람한테서는 한마디도 안 나올 겁니다.\" 대위가 대꾸했지."], ["‘The original cause of dispute, I understand, was some girl or other, to whom your principal applied certain terms, which Lord Frederick, defending the girl, repelled.", "\"제가 알기로 다툼의 본래 발단은 어떤 여자였답니다. 댁의 당사자가 그 여자한테 어떤 말을 했고, 프레더릭 경이 그 여자를 두둔하며 받아쳤지요."], ["But this led to a long recrimination upon a great many sore subjects, charges, and counter-charges.", "한데 이게 온갖 아픈 주제와 비난, 맞비난으로 길게 번졌습니다."], ["Sir Mulberry was sarcastic; Lord Frederick was excited, and struck him in the heat of provocation, and under circumstances of great aggravation.", "멀버리 경은 빈정댔고, 프레더릭 경은 흥분했어요. 그리고 자극이 몹시 격해진 마당에, 화가 머리끝까지 치밀어 그를 친 겁니다."], ["That blow, unless there is a full retraction on the part of Sir Mulberry, Lord Frederick is ready to justify.’", "멀버리 경 쪽에서 완전히 철회하지 않는 한, 프레더릭 경은 그 매질을 정당하다 내세울 작정입니다.\""]], [["‘There is no more to be said,’ returned the other, ‘but to settle the hour and the place of meeting.", "\"그렇다면 더 할 말은 없군요.\" 상대가 대꾸했지."], ["It’s a responsibility; but there is a strong feeling to have it over.", "\"만날 시간과 장소만 정하면 됩니다. 책임이 따르는 일이지요."], ["Do you object to say at sunrise?’", "하지만 어서 끝내자는 마음이 강합니다. 해 뜰 무렵이라면 어떻습니까?\""]], [["‘Sharp work,’ replied the captain, referring to his watch; ‘however, as this seems to have been a long time breeding, and negotiation is only a waste of words, no.’", "\"빠듯하군요.\" 대위가 시계를 보며 대꾸했어. \"하지만 이 일이 오래 곪아 온 듯하고, 협상이란 말의 낭비일 뿐이니, 좋습니다.\""]], [["‘Something may possibly be said, out of doors, after what passed in the other room, which renders it desirable that we should be off without delay, and quite clear of town,’ said Mr.", "\"저쪽 방에서 벌어진 일이 있으니, 바깥에서 무슨 말이 나올지도 모릅니다. 그러니 지체 없이, 런던에서 완전히 벗어나는 게 좋겠습니다.\""], ["Westwood.", "웨스트우드 씨가 말했어."], ["‘What do you say to one of the meadows opposite Twickenham, by the river-side?’", "\"트위크넘 맞은편 강가 풀밭 하나는 어떻습니까?\""]], [["The captain saw no objection.", "대위는 반대할 게 없다고 봤어."]], [["‘Shall we join company in the avenue of trees which leads from Petersham to Ham House, and settle the exact spot when we arrive there?’ said Mr Westwood.", "\"피터셤에서 햄 하우스로 이어지는 가로수길에서 합류하고, 거기 도착해서 정확한 장소를 정할까요?\" 웨스트우드 씨가 말했지."]], [["To this the captain also assented.", "대위도 여기에 동의했어."], ["After a few other preliminaries, equally brief, and having settled the road each party should take to avoid suspicion, they separated.", "몇 가지 다른 절차를 마찬가지로 짤막하게 거치고, 의심을 피하려고 양쪽이 갈 길을 정한 뒤, 둘은 헤어졌어."]], [["‘We shall just have comfortable time, my lord,’ said the captain, when he had communicated the arrangements, ‘to call at my rooms for a case of pistols, and then jog coolly down. If you will allow me to dismiss your servant, we’ll take my cab; for yours, perhaps, might be recognised.’", "\"꼭 알맞게 여유가 있을 겁니다, 각하.\" 대위가 약속한 사항을 전한 뒤 말했지. \"제 방에 들러 권총 한 상자를 챙기고, 그런 다음 느긋하게 내려가시죠. 하인을 보내도 괜찮으시다면, 제 마차를 타죠. 각하 마차는 알아보일지도 모르니까요.\""]], [["What a contrast, when they reached the street, to the scene they had just left!", "거리에 나섰을 때, 그들이 방금 떠나온 그 광경과 어찌나 딴판이던지!"], ["It was already daybreak.", "벌써 동이 트고 있었어."], ["For the flaring yellow light within, was substituted the clear, bright, glorious morning; for a hot, close atmosphere, tainted with the smell of expiring lamps, and reeking with the steams of riot and dissipation, the free, fresh, wholesome air.", "안의 그 이글거리던 누런 불빛 대신, 맑고 환하고 눈부신 아침이 들어섰지. 후끈하고 답답한 공기, 꺼져 가는 등불 냄새가 배고 난동과 방탕의 김이 풍기던 그 공기 대신, 거리낌 없고 싱그럽고 건강한 공기가 들어섰어."], ["But to the fevered head on which that cool air blew, it seemed to come laden with remorse for time misspent and countless opportunities neglected.", "하지만 그 차가운 공기가 불어든 열에 들뜬 머리에는, 그 공기가 헛되이 보낸 시간과 흘려보낸 숱한 기회에 대한 회한을 잔뜩 싣고 오는 듯했지."], ["With throbbing veins and burning skin, eyes wild and heavy, thoughts hurried and disordered, he felt as though the light were a reproach, and shrunk involuntarily from the day as if he were some foul and hideous thing.", "핏줄이 욱신거리고 살갗이 타오르며, 눈은 거칠고 무겁고, 생각은 다급하고 어지러운 채로, 그는 그 빛이 마치 자기를 꾸짖는 것만 같았어. 그래서 자기가 무슨 더럽고 흉측한 것이라도 되는 양, 저도 모르게 그 햇빛을 피해 움츠러들었지."]], [["‘Shivering?’ said the captain.", "\"떠시는군요?\" 대위가 말했어."], ["‘You are cold.’", "\"추우신가 봅니다.\""]], [["‘Rather.’", "\"좀 그러네.\""]], [["‘It does strike cool, coming out of those hot rooms.", "\"저 후끈한 방에서 나오니 쌀쌀하게 느껴지지요."], ["Wrap that cloak about you.", "그 망토를 두르세요. 자, 그렇게."], ["So, so; now we’re off.’", "자, 이제 갑시다.\""]], [["They rattled through the quiet streets, made their call at the captain’s lodgings, cleared the town, and emerged upon the open road, without hindrance or molestation.", "그들은 조용한 거리를 덜컹덜컹 지나, 대위의 숙소에 들렀고, 런던을 벗어나, 막힘이나 시비 없이 탁 트인 길로 나섰어."]], [["Fields, trees, gardens, hedges, everything looked very beautiful; the young man scarcely seemed to have noticed them before, though he had passed the same objects a thousand times. There was a peace and serenity upon them all, strangely at variance with the bewilderment and confusion of his own half-sobered thoughts, and yet impressive and welcome. He had no fear upon his mind; but, as he looked about him, he had less anger; and though all old delusions, relative to his worthless late companion, were now cleared away, he rather wished he had never known him than thought of its having come to this.", "들판, 나무, 정원, 산울타리, 모든 게 무척 아름다워 보였어. 젊은이는 전에는 이것들을 거의 눈여겨본 적 없는 듯했지. 천 번도 더 똑같은 풍경을 지나쳤는데도 말이야. 그 모든 것 위에 평화와 고요가 깃들어 있었어. 어설프게 깬 그의 머릿속 어지러움과 혼란과는 묘하게 어긋났지만, 그래도 가슴에 와닿고 반가웠지. 그의 마음에 두려움은 없었어. 하지만 둘레를 둘러보니 노여움이 줄어들었지. 그리고 보잘것없던 옛 동행에 관한 온갖 헛된 믿음이 이제 다 걷혔지만, 그는 일이 이 지경에 이른 걸 떠올리기보다, 차라리 그자를 아예 몰랐더라면 싶었어."]], [["The past night, the day before, and many other days and nights beside, all mingled themselves up in one unintelligible and senseless whirl; he could not separate the transactions of one time from those of another.", "지난밤, 그 전날, 그 밖의 숱한 낮과 밤이 죄다 하나의 알 수 없고 갈피 없는 소용돌이 속에 뒤섞였어. 어느 때 일을 다른 때 일과 갈라낼 수가 없었지."], ["Now, the noise of the wheels resolved itself into some wild tune in which he could recognise scraps of airs he knew; now, there was nothing in his ears but a stunning and bewildering sound, like rushing water.", "어느 순간엔 바퀴 소리가 그가 아는 가락의 토막들이 들리는 어떤 거친 곡조로 풀려나기도 했어. 또 어느 순간엔 귓속에 콸콸 흐르는 물 같은, 멍하고 어리둥절한 소리밖에 없었지."], ["But his companion rallied him on being so silent, and they talked and laughed boisterously.", "하지만 동행이 너무 말이 없다고 핀잔을 주자, 둘은 시끌벅적 이야기하고 웃었어."], ["When they stopped, he was a little surprised to find himself in the act of smoking; but, on reflection, he remembered when and where he had taken the cigar.", "마차가 멈췄을 때, 그는 자기가 시가를 피우고 있는 걸 알고 좀 놀랐지. 하지만 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 언제 어디서 그 시가를 받았는지 떠올랐어."]], [["They stopped at the avenue gate and alighted, leaving the carriage to the care of the servant, who was a smart fellow, and nearly as well accustomed to such proceedings as his master. Sir Mulberry and his friend were already there. All four walked in profound silence up the aisle of stately elm trees, which, meeting far above their heads, formed a long green perspective of Gothic arches, terminating, like some old ruin, in the open sky.", "그들은 가로수길 어귀에 마차를 세우고 내렸어. 마차는 하인에게 맡겼지. 약삭빠른 녀석으로, 그런 일에 제 주인 못지않게 이골이 나 있었어. 멀버리 경과 그의 친구는 벌써 와 있었지. 넷은 깊은 침묵 속에 늘어선 위풍당당한 느릅나무들 사이의 길을 걸어 올라갔어. 머리 위 한참 높은 곳에서 맞닿은 나무들이, 고딕식 아치가 길게 이어지는 풍경을 이루었지. 마치 어떤 옛 폐허처럼, 그 끝은 탁 트인 하늘로 열려 있었어."]], [["After a pause, and a brief conference between the seconds, they, at length, turned to the right, and taking a track across a little meadow, passed Ham House and came into some fields beyond.", "잠시 멈춰 입회인들끼리 짤막하게 의논한 끝에, 그들은 마침내 오른쪽으로 돌았어. 작은 풀밭을 가로지르는 길을 따라, 햄 하우스를 지나 그 너머 들판으로 들어섰지."], ["In one of these, they stopped.", "그 가운데 한 들판에서 그들은 멈췄어."], ["The ground was measured, some usual forms gone through, the two principals were placed front to front at the distance agreed upon, and Sir Mulberry turned his face towards his young adversary for the first time.", "거리를 재고, 흔히 거치는 절차 몇 가지를 마쳤지. 두 당사자가 합의한 거리만큼 떨어져 마주 보고 섰고, 멀버리 경은 처음으로 젊은 적수 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌렸어."], ["He was very pale, his eyes were bloodshot, his dress disordered, and his hair dishevelled.", "그는 무척 창백했지. 눈에는 핏발이 섰고, 옷매무새는 흐트러졌으며, 머리는 헝클어져 있었어."], ["For the face, it expressed nothing but violent and evil passions.", "얼굴은 사납고 흉악한 격정밖에 드러내지 않았지. 그는 손으로 눈을 가리고 적수를 한동안 뚫어지게 노려봤어."], ["He shaded his eyes with his hand; grazed at his opponent, steadfastly, for a few moments; and, then taking the weapon which was tendered to him, bent his eyes upon that, and looked up no more until the word was given, when he instantly fired.", "그러고는 건네받은 무기를 들고 거기에 눈을 떨군 채, 신호가 떨어질 때까지 다시 고개를 들지 않았지. 그러다 신호가 떨어지자 곧바로 쏘았어."]], [["The two shots were fired, as nearly as possible, at the same instant.", "두 발의 총성은 거의 같은 순간에 울렸어."], ["In that instant, the young lord turned his head sharply round, fixed upon his adversary a ghastly stare, and without a groan or stagger, fell down dead.", "그 순간, 젊은 귀족은 고개를 홱 옆으로 돌리더니, 끔찍한 눈길로 적수를 쏘아본 채, 신음도 비틀거림도 없이 쓰러져 죽었어."]], [["‘He’s gone!’ cried Westwood, who, with the other second, had run up to the body, and fallen on one knee beside it.", "\"갔어!\" 웨스트우드가 외쳤어. 다른 입회인과 함께 시신으로 달려가, 그 곁에 한쪽 무릎을 꿇으며 말이야."]], [["‘His blood on his own head,’ said Sir Mulberry.", "\"제 피는 제 머리 위에 있을 뿐이지.\" 멀버리 경이 말했어."], ["‘He brought this upon himself, and forced it upon me.’", "\"이건 제가 자초한 거야. 나한테 억지로 떠민 거라고.\""]], [["‘Captain Adams,’ cried Westwood, hastily, ‘I call you to witness that this was fairly done.", "\"애덤스 대위,\" 웨스트우드가 황급히 외쳤어."], ["Hawk, we have not a moment to lose.", "\"이게 정정당당하게 된 일임을 증언해 주시오. 호크, 한순간도 지체할 수 없네."], ["We must leave this place immediately, push for Brighton, and cross to France with all speed.", "당장 여길 떠나야 해. 브라이턴으로 내달려서, 있는 힘껏 빨리 프랑스로 건너가야 하네."], ["This has been a bad business, and may be worse, if we delay a moment.", "이건 고약한 일이야, 잠시라도 꾸물대면 더 고약해질지 몰라. 애덤스, 댁의 안전이나 챙기고, 여기 있지 마시오."], ["Adams, consult your own safety, and don’t remain here; the living before the dead; goodbye!’", "죽은 자보다 산 자가 먼저요. 잘 가시오!\""]], [["With these words, he seized Sir Mulberry by the arm, and hurried him away.", "이 말과 함께 그는 멀버리 경의 팔을 붙들고 서둘러 끌고 갔어."], ["Captain Adams--only pausing to convince himself, beyond all question, of the fatal result--sped off in the same direction, to concert measures with his servant for removing the body, and securing his own safety likewise.", "애덤스 대위는 — 결과가 죽음이라는 걸 한 점 의심 없이 확인하려고 잠깐 멈췄을 뿐 — 같은 방향으로 내달렸지. 자기 하인과 함께 시신을 옮길 방도를 짜고, 제 안전도 챙기려고 말이야."]], [["So died Lord Frederick Verisopht, by the hand which he had loaded with gifts, and clasped a thousand times; by the act of him, but for whom, and others like him, he might have lived a happy man, and died with children’s faces round his bed.", "이렇게 프레더릭 버리소프트 경은 죽었어. 자기가 선물을 잔뜩 안겨 주고 천 번도 더 맞잡았던 그 손에. 그가 아니었다면, 또 그자 같은 자들이 아니었다면, 그는 행복한 사람으로 살다가 침대 둘레에 자식들 얼굴을 둘러두고 죽었을지도 모르는데, 바로 그자의 손에 말이야."]], [["The sun came proudly up in all his majesty, the noble river ran its winding course, the leaves quivered and rustled in the air, the birds poured their cheerful songs from every tree, the short-lived butterfly fluttered its little wings; all the light and life of day came on; and, amidst it all, and pressing down the grass whose every blade bore twenty tiny lives, lay the dead man, with his stark and rigid face turned upwards to the sky.", "해가 온 위엄을 떨치며 당당하게 떠올랐어. 고귀한 강은 굽이진 물길을 따라 흘렀지. 나뭇잎은 바람에 파르르 떨며 살랑거렸어. 새들은 나무마다 즐거운 노래를 쏟아 냈지. 덧없는 나비는 작은 날개를 팔랑였어. 낮의 온갖 빛과 생명이 깨어났지. 그리고 그 모든 것 한가운데, 잎새마다 스무 개의 자그마한 목숨을 품은 풀들을 짓누른 채, 죽은 사람이 누워 있었어. 뻣뻣하게 굳은 얼굴을 하늘로 향한 채 말이야."]], [["# CHAPTER 51", "# CHAPTER 51"]], [["The Project of Mr. Ralph Nickleby and his Friend approaching a successful Issue, becomes unexpectedly known to another Party, not admitted into their Confidence\nIn an old house, dismal dark and dusty, which seemed to have withered, like himself, and to have grown yellow and shrivelled in hoarding him from the light of day, as he had in hoarding his money, lived Arthur Gride. Meagre old chairs and tables, of spare and bony make, and hard and cold as misers’ hearts, were ranged, in grim array, against the gloomy walls; attenuated presses, grown lank and lantern-jawed in guarding the treasures they enclosed, and tottering, as though from constant fear and dread of thieves, shrunk up in dark corners, whence they cast no shadows on the ground, and seemed to hide and cower from observation. A tall grim clock upon the stairs, with long lean hands and famished face, ticked in cautious whispers; and when it struck the time, in thin and piping sounds, like an old man’s voice, rattled, as if it were pinched with hunger.", "음침하고 어둡고 먼지가 켜켜이 쌓인 낡은 집이 있었어. 그 집은 마치 주인을 꼭 닮은 듯, 그를 햇빛으로부터 꽁꽁 숨겨 두느라 — 그가 돈을 꽁꽁 숨겨 두었던 것처럼 — 누렇게 시들고 쪼그라든 것 같았지. 바로 거기에 아서 그라이드가 살고 있었어. 빈약하고 앙상한 모양에다 구두쇠 마음처럼 딱딱하고 차가운 낡은 의자와 탁자가, 음울한 벽을 따라 으스스하게 줄지어 늘어서 있었어. 속에 든 보물을 지키느라 비쩍 마르고 홀쭉해진 장롱들은, 도둑이 들까 늘 무섭고 두려운 듯 비틀거리며 어두운 구석에 웅크리고 있었지. 거기서는 바닥에 그림자조차 드리우지 않아서, 마치 남의 눈을 피해 숨고 움츠리는 것 같았어. 계단 위에는 키 크고 으스스한 시계가 있었어. 길고 가느다란 바늘에 굶주린 얼굴을 하고서, 조심스러운 속삭임처럼 똑딱거렸지. 그러다 시각을 알릴 때면, 늙은이 목소리 같은 가늘고 째지는 소리로, 배고픔에 꼬집힌 듯 덜덜거렸어."]], [["No fireside couch was there, to invite repose and comfort.", "거기엔 편안히 쉬라고 손짓하는 난롯가 긴 의자 따위는 없었어. 팔걸이의자는 있었지만, 마음이 불편해 보였지."], ["Elbow-chairs there were, but they looked uneasy in their minds, cocked their arms suspiciously and timidly, and kept upon their guard.", "의심스럽다는 듯 겁먹은 듯 팔걸이를 곧추세우고는, 경계를 늦추지 않았어. 어떤 것들은 기이하게 으스스하고 앙상했어."], ["Others, were fantastically grim and gaunt, as having drawn themselves up to their utmost height, and put on their fiercest looks to stare all comers out of countenance.", "키를 한껏 잔뜩 세우고 가장 사나운 표정을 지어, 들어오는 사람을 죄다 노려보며 기를 죽이려는 것 같았지. 또 어떤 것들은 옆엣것에 부딪히거나 벽에 기대어 몸을 지탱하고 있었어 — 좀 유난스럽게 말이야."], ["Others, again, knocked up against their neighbours, or leant for support against the wall--somewhat ostentatiously, as if to call all men to witness that they were not worth the taking.", "마치 자기들은 가져갈 가치가 없다고 온 세상 사람한테 증언이라도 시키려는 듯이. 어둡고 네모지고 육중한 침대들은 뒤숭숭한 꿈이나 꾸라고 만들어 놓은 것 같았어."], ["The dark square lumbering bedsteads seemed built for restless dreams; the musty hangings seemed to creep in scanty folds together, whispering among themselves, when rustled by the wind, their trembling knowledge of the tempting wares that lurked within the dark and tight-locked closets.", "곰팡내 나는 휘장은 바람에 흔들릴 때면 듬성듬성 주름을 모으며 자기들끼리 소곤거리는 것 같았지. 어둡고 단단히 잠긴 벽장 속에 숨어 있는, 그 탐나는 물건들에 대한 떨리는 비밀을 말이야."]], [["From out the most spare and hungry room in all this spare and hungry house there came, one morning, the tremulous tones of old Gride’s voice, as it feebly chirruped forth the fag end of some forgotten song, of which the burden ran:", "이 빈약하고 굶주린 집 안에서도 가장 빈약하고 굶주린 방에서, 어느 날 아침 아서 그라이드 영감의 떨리는 목소리가 흘러나왔어. 잊힌 옛 노래의 끝자락을 힘없이 흥얼거리는 소리였는데, 후렴은 이랬지."]], [["Ta--ran--tan--too, Throw the old shoe, And may the wedding be lucky!", "타라란탄투, 낡은 신발을 던져라, 이 결혼에 행운이 깃들기를!"]], [["which he repeated, in the same shrill quavering notes, again and again, until a violent fit of coughing obliged him to desist, and to pursue in silence, the occupation upon which he was engaged.", "그는 똑같이 새되고 떨리는 가락으로 이 대목을 몇 번이고 되풀이했어. 그러다 심한 기침이 터지는 바람에 그만둘 수밖에 없었고, 하던 일을 잠자코 계속했지."]], [["This occupation was, to take down from the shelves of a worm-eaten wardrobe a quantity of frouzy garments, one by one; to subject each to a careful and minute inspection by holding it up against the light, and after folding it with great exactness, to lay it on one or other of two little heaps beside him. He never took two articles of clothing out together, but always brought them forth, singly, and never failed to shut the wardrobe door, and turn the key, between each visit to its shelves.", "그 일이란, 좀먹은 옷장 선반에서 케케묵은 옷가지를 한 벌씩 꺼내는 거였어. 그러고는 옷을 햇빛에 비춰 보며 하나하나 꼼꼼하게 살핀 다음, 아주 정확하게 개어서 옆에 놓인 두 무더기 가운데 어느 한쪽에 올려놓았지. 그는 한 번에 옷 두 벌을 꺼내는 법이 없었어. 늘 한 벌씩만 꺼냈지. 그리고 선반에 손을 댈 때마다 어김없이 옷장 문을 닫고 열쇠를 돌려 잠갔어."]], [["‘The snuff-coloured suit,’ said Arthur Gride, surveying a threadbare coat.", "\"코담배 빛깔 양복이라.\" 아서 그라이드가 닳아빠진 외투를 살펴보며 중얼거렸어."], ["‘Did I look well in snuff-colour?", "\"내가 코담배 빛이 잘 어울렸던가?"], ["Let me think.’", "어디 보자.\""]], [["The result of his cogitations appeared to be unfavourable, for he folded the garment once more, laid it aside, and mounted on a chair to get down another, chirping while he did so:", "곰곰이 따져 본 결과는 영 신통치 않았던 모양이야. 그는 옷을 다시 개어 한쪽에 치워 두고, 또 한 벌을 꺼내려고 의자에 올라섰어. 그러면서 흥얼거렸지."]], [["Young, loving, and fair, Oh what happiness there!", "젊고, 사랑스럽고, 어여뻐라, 오 그 얼마나 큰 행복인가!"], ["The wedding is sure to be lucky!", "이 결혼엔 틀림없이 행운이 깃들리!"]], [["‘They always put in “young,”’ said old Arthur, ‘but songs are only written for the sake of rhyme, and this is a silly one that the poor country-people sang, when I was a little boy.", "\"노래엔 늘 '젊고'를 넣지.\" 아서 영감이 말했어."], ["Though stop--young is quite right too--it means the bride--yes.", "\"하지만 노래야 운을 맞추려고 짓는 거잖아. 이건 내가 어릴 때 가난한 시골 사람들이 부르던 어리석은 노래고."], ["He, he, he! It means the bride.", "그래도 잠깐 — '젊다'는 것도 딱 맞는 말이지."], ["Oh dear, that’s good.", "그건 신부를 뜻하니까 — 그래. 헤, 헤, 헤!"], ["That’s very good.", "신부를 뜻하는 거야. 오 이런, 그것참 좋군."], ["And true besides, quite true!’", "아주 좋아. 게다가 사실이기도 하고, 아주 사실이지!\""]], [["In the satisfaction of this discovery, he went over the verse again, with increased expression, and a shake or two here and there.", "이 발견에 흡족해진 그는, 곳곳에 떨림을 한두 번 곁들이고 표현을 한껏 살려 다시 한 번 그 구절을 불렀어."], ["He then resumed his employment.", "그러고는 하던 일을 다시 시작했지."]], [["‘The bottle-green,’ said old Arthur; ‘the bottle-green was a famous suit to wear, and I bought it very cheap at a pawnbroker’s, and there was--he, he, he!--a tarnished shilling in the waistcoat pocket.", "\"병 초록색이라.\" 아서 영감이 말했어."], ["To think that the pawnbroker shouldn’t have known there was a shilling in it!", "\"병 초록색은 입고 다니기에 끝내주는 양복이었지. 게다가 전당포에서 아주 싸게 샀고, 거기엔 — 헤, 헤, 헤!"], ["I knew it! I felt it when I was examining the quality.", "— 조끼 주머니에 빛바랜 일 실링짜리가 들어 있었단 말이야."], ["Oh, what a dull dog of a pawnbroker!", "전당포 주인이 그 안에 일 실링이 든 줄도 몰랐다니! 난 알았거든!"], ["It was a lucky suit too, this bottle-green.", "옷감 품질을 살피면서 그게 만져졌단 말이지. 오, 그 멍청한 전당포 주인 같으니!"], ["The very day I put it on first, old Lord Mallowford was burnt to death in his bed, and all the post-obits fell in.", "이 병 초록색은 운수 좋은 양복이기도 했어. 이걸 처음 입은 바로 그날, 늙은 맬로포드 경이 침대에서 불에 타 죽는 바람에, 사후 변제 어음이 죄다 굴러들었거든."], ["I’ll be married in the bottle-green.", "난 병 초록색을 입고 결혼하겠어."], ["Peg.", "페그."], ["Peg Sliderskew--I’ll wear the bottle-green!’", "페그 슬라이더스큐 — 난 병 초록색을 입을 테야!\""]], [["This call, loudly repeated twice or thrice at the room-door, brought into the apartment a short, thin, weasen, blear-eyed old woman, palsy-stricken and hideously ugly, who, wiping her shrivelled face upon her dirty apron, inquired, in that subdued tone in which deaf people commonly speak:", "방문 앞에서 이렇게 두세 번 큰 소리로 부르자, 한 노파가 방 안으로 들어왔어. 키가 작고 비쩍 마르고 쪼글쪼글하고 눈이 짓무른, 중풍에 걸려 끔찍하게 못생긴 노파였지. 그 여자는 시든 얼굴을 더러운 앞치마에 닦으면서, 귀먹은 사람들이 흔히 쓰는 그 나직한 말투로 물었어."]], [["‘Was that you a calling, or only the clock a striking?", "\"부르신 거예요, 아니면 시계가 친 거예요?"], ["My hearing gets so bad, I never know which is which; but when I hear a noise, I know it must be one of you, because nothing else never stirs in the house.’", "귀가 하도 어두워서, 어느 게 어느 건지 통 모르겠네요. 그래도 무슨 소리가 들리면, 이 집엔 그것 말고는 아무것도 움직이는 게 없으니까 둘 중 하나구나 하는 거죠.\""]], [["‘Me, Peg, me,’ said Arthur Gride, tapping himself on the breast to render the reply more intelligible.", "\"날세, 페그, 나야.\" 아서 그라이드가 알아듣게 하려고 자기 가슴을 톡톡 치며 말했어."]], [["‘You, eh?’ returned Peg.", "\"영감님이요, 그래요?\" 페그가 되받았어."], ["‘And what do YOU want?’", "\"그래, 영감님은 뭘 원하시는데요?\""]], [["‘I’ll be married in the bottle-green,’ cried Arthur Gride.", "\"난 병 초록색을 입고 결혼하겠어.\" 아서 그라이드가 외쳤어."]], [["‘It’s a deal too good to be married in, master,’ rejoined Peg, after a short inspection of the suit.", "\"이건 결혼식에 입기엔 너무 멀쩡한데요, 나리.\" 양복을 잠깐 살펴본 페그가 대꾸했어."], ["‘Haven’t you got anything worse than this?’", "\"이것보다 더 허름한 건 없어요?\""]], [["‘Nothing that’ll do,’ replied old Arthur.", "\"쓸 만한 게 없어.\" 아서 영감이 대답했어."]], [["‘Why not do?’ retorted Peg.", "\"왜 쓸 만한 게 없어요?\""], ["‘Why don’t you wear your every-day clothes, like a man--eh?’", "페그가 쏘아붙였어. \"사내답게 그냥 평소 입는 옷을 입지 그래요 — 응?\""]], [["‘They an’t becoming enough, Peg,’ returned her master.", "\"그건 어울림이 모자라, 페그.\" 주인이 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Not what enough?’ said Peg.", "\"뭐가 모자라요?\" 페그가 물었어."]], [["‘Becoming.’", "\"어울림.\""]], [["‘Becoming what?’ said Peg, sharply.", "\"뭐에 어울려요?\" 페그가 날카롭게 되물었어."], ["‘Not becoming too old to wear?’", "\"입기엔 너무 늙어서 안 어울린단 말이에요?\""]], [["Arthur Gride muttered an imprecation on his housekeeper’s deafness, as he roared in her ear:", "아서 그라이드는 가정부의 귀먹음에 대고 욕설을 중얼거리고는, 그 여자 귀에다 대고 버럭 소리쳤어."]], [["‘Not smart enough!", "\"맵시가 모자란다고!"], ["I want to look as well as I can.’", "난 되도록 잘 보이고 싶단 말이야.\""]], [["‘Look?’ cried Peg.", "\"잘 보여요?\" 페그가 외쳤어."], ["‘If she’s as handsome as you say she is, she won’t look much at you, master, take your oath of that; and as to how you look yourself--pepper-and-salt, bottle-green, sky-blue, or tartan-plaid will make no difference in you.’", "\"그 여자가 영감님 말대로 그렇게 곱다면, 영감님은 거들떠보지도 않을걸요, 나리, 그건 맹세해도 좋아요. 그리고 영감님 모양새로 말하자면 — 후추소금 빛이든, 병 초록색이든, 하늘색이든, 격자무늬든 영감님한텐 그게 그거예요.\""]], [["With which consolatory assurance, Peg Sliderskew gathered up the chosen suit, and folding her skinny arms upon the bundle, stood, mouthing, and grinning, and blinking her watery eyes, like an uncouth figure in some monstrous piece of carving.", "이렇게 위로랍시고 한마디 던지고는, 페그 슬라이더스큐가 골라 둔 양복을 챙겨 들었어. 그러고는 깡마른 두 팔을 보따리 위에 포갠 채 서서, 입을 우물거리고 히죽거리며 눈물 고인 눈을 깜빡거렸지. 마치 어떤 괴이한 조각 작품에 새겨진 투박한 형상처럼 말이야."]], [["‘You’re in a funny humour, an’t you, Peg?’ said Arthur, with not the best possible grace.", "\"자네 기분이 별나구먼, 안 그런가, 페그?\" 아서가 영 마뜩잖은 태도로 말했어."]], [["‘Why, isn’t it enough to make me?’ rejoined the old woman.", "\"왜요, 그럴 만하지 않아요?\" 노파가 되받았어."], ["‘I shall, soon enough, be put out, though, if anybody tries to domineer it over me: and so I give you notice, master.", "\"하지만 누가 날 누르고 부리려 들면, 난 곧장 토라질 거라고요. 그러니 미리 말해 둘게요, 나리."], ["Nobody shall be put over Peg Sliderskew’s head, after so many years; you know that, and so I needn’t tell you!", "이렇게 오랜 세월이 지났는데, 누구도 이 페그 슬라이더스큐 위에 올라설 순 없어요. 그건 영감님도 아실 테니 굳이 말 안 해도 되겠죠!"], ["That won’t do for me--no, no, nor for you.", "그런 건 나한테 안 통해요 — 안 돼요, 안 되고말고요, 영감님한테도 안 좋고요."], ["Try that once, and come to ruin--ruin--ruin!’", "한 번만 그래 봐요, 그럼 망하는 거예요 — 망해요 — 망한다고요!\""]], [["‘Oh dear, dear, I shall never try it,’ said Arthur Gride, appalled by the mention of the word, ‘not for the world. It would be very easy to ruin me; we must be very careful; more saving than ever, with another mouth to feed. Only we--we mustn’t let her lose her good looks, Peg, because I like to see ‘em.’", "\"오 이런, 이런, 난 절대 그러지 않을 거야.\" 아서 그라이드가 그 말에 질겁하며 말했어. \"무슨 일이 있어도 안 그래. 날 망하게 하는 건 아주 쉬운 일이지. 우린 아주 조심해야 해. 입 하나가 더 늘었으니, 전보다 더 아껴야 하고. 다만 우리는 — 우리는 그 여자가 고운 얼굴을 잃게 둬선 안 돼, 페그. 난 그걸 보는 게 좋거든.\""]], [["‘Take care you don’t find good looks come expensive,’ returned Peg, shaking her forefinger.", "\"고운 얼굴이 돈깨나 든다는 걸 알게 되지 않도록 조심해요.\" 페그가 집게손가락을 흔들며 대꾸했어."]], [["‘But she can earn money herself, Peg,’ said Arthur Gride, eagerly watching what effect his communication produced upon the old woman’s countenance: ‘she can draw, paint, work all manner of pretty things for ornamenting stools and chairs: slippers, Peg, watch-guards, hair-chains, and a thousand little dainty trifles that I couldn’t give you half the names of. Then she can play the piano, (and, what’s more, she’s got one), and sing like a little bird. She’ll be very cheap to dress and keep, Peg; don’t you think she will?’", "\"하지만 그 여잔 스스로 돈을 벌 수도 있어, 페그.\" 아서 그라이드가 자기 말이 노파 얼굴에 어떤 효과를 내는지 열심히 살피며 말했어. \"그림도 그리고, 색칠도 하고, 걸상이며 의자 장식할 온갖 예쁜 것들을 만들 수 있단 말이야. 슬리퍼며, 페그, 시곗줄이며, 머리 사슬이며, 내가 이름의 절반도 못 댈 자잘하고 앙증맞은 물건을 천 가지나 말이지. 게다가 피아노도 칠 줄 알고 (게다가 더 좋은 건, 자기 피아노도 가졌어), 작은 새처럼 노래도 한단 말이야. 입히고 거두는 데 아주 돈이 적게 들 거야, 페그. 자네도 그렇게 생각하지?\""]], [["‘If you don’t let her make a fool of you, she may,’ returned Peg.", "\"그 여자가 영감님을 바보로 만들게 두지만 않는다면, 그럴 수도 있겠죠.\" 페그가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘A fool of ME!’ exclaimed Arthur. ‘Trust your old master not to be fooled by pretty faces, Peg; no, no, no--nor by ugly ones neither, Mrs Sliderskew,’ he softly added by way of soliloquy.", "\"날 바보로 만든다고!\" 아서가 소리쳤어. \"이 늙은 주인이 고운 얼굴에 안 넘어갈 거라고 믿어 두라고, 페그. 아니, 아니, 아니야 — 못생긴 얼굴에도 안 넘어가지, 슬라이더스큐 부인.\" 그는 혼잣말처럼 나직이 덧붙였어."]], [["‘You’re a saying something you don’t want me to hear,’ said Peg; ‘I know you are.’", "\"내가 못 듣게 뭔가 말하고 있잖아요.\" 페그가 말했어. \"그러고 있는 거 다 안다고요.\""]], [["‘Oh dear!", "\"오 이런!"], ["the devil’s in this woman,’ muttered Arthur; adding with an ugly leer, ‘I said I trusted everything to you, Peg.", "이 여편네한테 마귀가 붙었나.\" 아서가 중얼거리고는, 능글맞게 곁눈질하며 덧붙였어."], ["That was all.’", "\"모든 걸 자네한테 맡긴다고 했어, 페그. 그게 다야.\""]], [["‘You do that, master, and all your cares are over,’ said Peg approvingly.", "\"그렇게 하세요, 나리, 그럼 영감님 걱정은 다 끝나는 거예요.\" 페그가 흡족하게 말했어."]], [["‘WHEN I do that, Peg Sliderskew,’ thought Arthur Gride, ‘they will be.’", "'내가 그렇게 할 때면, 페그 슬라이더스큐,' 아서 그라이드는 속으로 생각했어. '자네 걱정도 다 끝나는 거지.'"]], [["Although he thought this very distinctly, he durst not move his lips lest the old woman should detect him.", "이걸 아주 또렷이 생각하면서도, 그는 노파가 눈치챌까 봐 감히 입술을 움직이지 못했어."], ["He even seemed half afraid that she might have read his thoughts; for he leered coaxingly upon her, as he said aloud:", "심지어 그 여자가 제 속마음을 읽었을까 봐 반쯤 겁이 난 것 같기도 했지. 그래서 그는 노파를 어르듯 곁눈질하며 큰 소리로 말했어."]], [["‘Take up all loose stitches in the bottle-green with the best black silk.", "\"병 초록색에 풀린 솔기는 죄다 제일 좋은 검정 명주실로 꿰매."], ["Have a skein of the best, and some new buttons for the coat, and--this is a good idea, Peg, and one you’ll like, I know--as I have never given her anything yet, and girls like such attentions, you shall polish up a sparking necklace that I have got upstairs, and I’ll give it her upon the wedding morning--clasp it round her charming little neck myself--and take it away again next day.", "제일 좋은 실 한 타래를 마련하고, 외투엔 새 단추도 달고 — 이건 좋은 생각이야, 페그, 자네도 좋아할 거야, 내 알지 — 내가 아직 그 여자한테 아무것도 안 줬으니, 그리고 처녀들이 그런 마음 씀씀이를 좋아하잖아, 위층에 반짝거리는 목걸이가 하나 있는데 그걸 자네가 닦아 둬. 그럼 결혼식 날 아침에 그 여자한테 줄 거야 — 내 손으로 그 매력적인 가느다란 목에 둘러 주고 — 그러고는 이튿날 도로 가져오는 거지."], ["He, he, he!", "헤, 헤, 헤!"], ["I’ll lock it up for her, Peg, and lose it.", "그걸 그 여자 대신 넣어 잠가 두고, 잃어버리는 거야, 페그."], ["Who’ll be made the fool of there, I wonder, to begin with--eh, Peg?’", "자, 누가 먼저 바보가 되는지 궁금하군 — 응, 페그?\""]], [["Mrs. Sliderskew appeared to approve highly of this ingenious scheme, and expressed her satisfaction by various rackings and twitchings of her head and body, which by no means enhanced her charms.", "슬라이더스큐 부인은 이 기발한 꾀가 아주 마음에 드는 모양이었어."], ["These she prolonged until she had hobbled to the door, when she exchanged them for a sour malignant look, and twisting her under-jaw from side to side, muttered hearty curses upon the future Mrs.", "그러고는 머리와 몸을 이리저리 들썩이고 씰룩거리며 흡족함을 드러냈는데, 그게 그 여자 매력을 조금도 더해 주진 못했지. 그 여자는 문가까지 절뚝거리며 갈 때까지 이걸 계속하다가, 이번엔 시큰둥하고 심술궂은 표정으로 바꿨어."], ["Gride, as she crept slowly down the stairs, and paused for breath at nearly every one.", "그러고는 아래턱을 좌우로 비틀며, 천천히 계단을 기어 내려가다가 거의 한 계단마다 숨을 고르면서, 장차 그라이드 부인이 될 여자에게 진심 어린 저주를 중얼거렸지."]], [["‘She’s half a witch, I think,’ said Arthur Gride, when he found himself again alone.", "\"저 여잔 반쯤 마녀야, 내 보기엔.\" 아서 그라이드가 다시 혼자가 되자 말했어."], ["‘But she’s very frugal, and she’s very deaf.", "\"하지만 아주 알뜰하고, 아주 귀가 먹었지."], ["Her living costs me next to nothing; and it’s no use her listening at keyholes; for she can’t hear.", "저 여자 거두는 데 돈은 거의 안 들어. 게다가 열쇠 구멍에 귀를 대고 엿들어 봐야 소용없어, 못 들으니까."], ["She’s a charming woman--for the purpose; a most discreet old housekeeper, and worth her weight in--copper.’", "참 좋은 여자지 — 이런 목적엔 말이야. 더없이 입이 무거운 늙은 가정부고, 제 무게만큼이나 값어치가 나가지 — 구리만큼이나 말이야.\""]], [["Having extolled the merits of his domestic in these high terms, old Arthur went back to the burden of his song.", "이렇게 거창한 말로 가정부의 미덕을 한껏 추켜세운 다음, 아서 영감은 다시 노래의 후렴으로 돌아갔어."], ["The suit destined to grace his approaching nuptials being now selected, he replaced the others with no less care than he had displayed in drawing them from the musty nooks where they had silently reposed for many years.", "다가오는 혼례를 빛내 줄 양복도 골라 놨겠다, 그는 다른 옷들을 도로 제자리에 넣었지. 여러 해 동안 잠자코 잠들어 있던 곰팡내 나는 구석에서 옷을 꺼낼 때 못지않은 정성을 들여서 말이야."]], [["Startled by a ring at the door, he hastily concluded this operation, and locked the press; but there was no need for any particular hurry, as the discreet Peg seldom knew the bell was rung unless she happened to cast her dim eyes upwards, and to see it shaking against the kitchen ceiling. After a short delay, however, Peg tottered in, followed by Newman Noggs.", "문에서 울린 초인종 소리에 깜짝 놀란 그는, 서둘러 이 작업을 끝내고 장롱을 잠갔어. 하지만 그렇게 서두를 필요는 없었지. 입 무거운 페그는 어쩌다 흐릿한 눈을 위로 들어 부엌 천장에 걸린 종이 흔들리는 걸 보지 않는 한, 종이 울린 줄도 좀처럼 몰랐거든. 그래도 잠깐 지나자, 페그가 비척비척 들어왔어. 그 뒤로 뉴먼 노그스가 따라 들어왔지."]], [["‘Ah! Mr.", "\"아!"], ["Noggs!’ cried Arthur Gride, rubbing his hands.", "노그스 씨!\" 아서 그라이드가 두 손을 비비며 외쳤어."], ["‘My good friend, Mr. Noggs, what news do you bring for me?’", "\"내 좋은 친구, 노그스 씨, 무슨 소식을 가져오셨소?\""]], [["Newman, with a steadfast and immovable aspect, and his fixed eye very fixed indeed, replied, suiting the action to the word, ‘A letter.", "뉴먼은 흔들림 없는 무표정한 얼굴로, 가뜩이나 한곳에 박힌 눈을 더더욱 한곳에 박은 채, 말에 동작을 맞춰 대답했어. \"편지요."], ["From Mr. Nickleby.", "니클비 씨한테서."], ["Bearer waits.’", "심부름꾼이 기다리고 있소.\""]], [["‘Won’t you take a--a--’", "\"한—한—\""]], [["Newman looked up, and smacked his lips.", "뉴먼이 고개를 들고 입맛을 다셨어."]], [["‘--A chair?’ said Arthur Gride.", "\"—의자에 좀 앉으시겠소?\" 아서 그라이드가 말했어."]], [["‘No,’ replied Newman.", "\"아니요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어."], ["‘Thankee.’", "\"고맙소만.\""]], [["Arthur opened the letter with trembling hands, and devoured its contents with the utmost greediness; chuckling rapturously over it, and reading it several times, before he could take it from before his eyes.", "아서는 떨리는 손으로 편지를 뜯어, 그 내용을 더없이 게걸스럽게 삼키듯 읽었어. 황홀하게 낄낄거리며 몇 번이고 거듭 읽다가, 겨우 눈앞에서 편지를 떼어 놨지."], ["So many times did he peruse and re-peruse it, that Newman considered it expedient to remind him of his presence.", "어찌나 읽고 또 읽었던지, 뉴먼은 자기가 와 있다는 걸 상기시켜 주는 게 낫겠다 싶었어."]], [["‘Answer,’ said Newman.", "\"답장이오.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."], ["‘Bearer waits.’", "\"심부름꾼이 기다리고 있소.\""]], [["‘True,’ replied old Arthur.", "\"맞소.\" 아서 영감이 대답했어."], ["‘Yes--yes; I almost forgot, I do declare.’", "\"그래—그래. 깜빡 잊을 뻔했군, 정말이지.\""]], [["‘I thought you were forgetting,’ said Newman.", "\"잊으시는 줄 알았소.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."]], [["‘Quite right to remind me, Mr. Noggs.", "\"일깨워 줘서 고맙소, 노그스 씨."], ["Oh, very right indeed,’ said Arthur.", "오, 정말 잘했소.\" 아서가 말했어."], ["‘Yes.", "\"그래."], ["I’ll write a line.", "몇 줄 적으리다."], ["I’m--I’m--rather flurried, Mr. Noggs.", "내가—내가—좀 들떠서 말이오, 노그스 씨."], ["The news is--’", "소식이—\""]], [["‘Bad?’ interrupted Newman.", "\"나쁜 거요?\" 뉴먼이 말을 잘랐어."]], [["‘No, Mr. Noggs, thank you; good, good.", "\"아니오, 노그스 씨, 고맙소만."], ["The very best of news.", "좋은 거요, 좋아. 더없이 좋은 소식이지."], ["Sit down.", "앉으시오."], ["I’ll get the pen and ink, and write a line in answer.", "펜과 잉크를 가져와서 몇 줄 답장을 쓰리다."], ["I’ll not detain you long.", "오래 붙들진 않으리다."], ["I know you’re a treasure to your master, Mr. Noggs.", "당신이 주인한테 보물 같은 사람이라는 건 나도 알아요, 노그스 씨."], ["He speaks of you in such terms, sometimes, that, oh dear!", "그분이 가끔 어찌나 당신을 좋게 말하는지, 오 이런!"], ["you’d be astonished.", "당신이 들으면 깜짝 놀랄 거요."], ["I may say that I do too, and always did.", "나도 그렇게 말한다고 해도 되겠지, 늘 그래 왔고."], ["I always say the same of you.’", "난 늘 당신을 똑같이 말하거든.\""]], [["‘That’s “Curse Mr. Noggs with all my heart!”", "'당신이 그런다면, 그건 \"노그스 씨한테 진심으로 저주나 내려라!\"겠지.'"], ["then, if you do,’ thought Newman, as Gride hurried out.", "그라이드가 서둘러 나가자 뉴먼이 속으로 생각했어."]], [["The letter had fallen on the ground. Looking carefully about him for an instant, Newman, impelled by curiosity to know the result of the design he had overheard from his office closet, caught it up and rapidly read as follows:", "편지가 바닥에 떨어져 있었어. 뉴먼은 잠깐 주위를 조심스레 살펴봤지. 자기 사무실 골방에서 엿들었던 그 음모가 어떻게 됐는지 알고 싶은 호기심에 못 이겨, 그는 편지를 집어 들고 재빨리 읽어 내려갔어. 내용은 이랬어. \"그라이드."]], [["‘I saw Bray again this morning, and proposed the day after tomorrow (as you suggested) for the marriage.", "오늘 아침에 브레이를 다시 만나, 자네가 일러 준 대로 모레로 결혼 날짜를 잡자고 했네."], ["There is no objection on his part, and all days are alike to his daughter.", "그쪽은 반대가 없고, 그 딸한테는 어느 날이든 매한가지라네."], ["We will go together, and you must be with me by seven in the morning.", "우리는 함께 갈 거고, 자네는 아침 일곱 시까지 내게 와 있어야 하네."], ["I need not tell you to be punctual.", "시간을 지키라는 말은 굳이 안 해도 되겠지."]], [["‘Make no further visits to the girl in the meantime.", "그동안 그 처녀한테는 더 이상 찾아가지 말게."], ["You have been there, of late, much oftener than you should.", "요즘 들어 자네는 가야 할 것보다 훨씬 자주 갔어."], ["She does not languish for you, and it might have been dangerous.", "그 여잔 자넬 그리워하는 게 아니니, 자칫 위험할 수도 있었네."], ["Restrain your youthful ardour for eight-and-forty hours, and leave her to the father.", "마흔여덟 시간만 그 젊은 혈기를 누르고, 처녀는 아비한테 맡겨 두게."], ["You only undo what he does, and does well.", "자넨 그 아비가 하는 일을, 그것도 잘하고 있는 일을 망쳐 놓을 뿐이야."]], [["‘Yours,", "자네의 벗,"]], [["A footstep was heard without. Newman dropped the letter on the same spot again, pressed it with his foot to prevent its fluttering away, regained his seat in a single stride, and looked as vacant and unconscious as ever mortal looked. Arthur Gride, after peering nervously about him, spied it on the ground, picked it up, and sitting down to write, glanced at Newman Noggs, who was staring at the wall with an intensity so remarkable, that Arthur was quite alarmed.", "랄프 니클비.\" 바깥에서 발소리가 들렸어. 뉴먼은 편지를 다시 그 자리에 떨어뜨리고, 날아가지 않게 발로 지그시 눌렀지. 그러고는 단번에 성큼 제자리로 돌아가, 세상 누구보다 멍하고 아무것도 모르는 얼굴을 했어. 아서 그라이드는 신경질적으로 주위를 두리번거리다가, 바닥에 떨어진 편지를 발견하고 집어 들었어. 그러고는 답장을 쓰려고 앉다가, 뉴먼 노그스를 흘끗 봤지. 뉴먼은 벽을 어찌나 뚫어지게 노려보고 있던지, 아서는 깜짝 놀라고 말았어."]], [["‘Do you see anything particular, Mr.", "\"뭐 특별한 거라도 보이오, 노그스 씨?\""], ["Noggs?’ said Arthur, trying to follow the direction of Newman’s eyes--which was an impossibility, and a thing no man had ever done.", "아서가 뉴먼의 시선을 따라가 보려 애쓰며 말했어 — 그건 불가능한 일이었고, 여태 누구도 해낸 적 없는 일이었지."]], [["‘Only a cobweb,’ replied Newman.", "\"거미줄이오.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["is that all?’", "그게 다요?\""]], [["‘No,’ said Newman.", "\"아니오.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."], ["‘There’s a fly in it.’", "\"거기에 파리가 한 마리 걸려 있소.\""]], [["‘There are a good many cobwebs here,’ observed Arthur Gride.", "\"여긴 거미줄이 꽤 많소.\" 아서 그라이드가 말했어."]], [["‘So there are in our place,’ returned Newman; ‘and flies too.’", "\"우리 집도 그렇소.\" 뉴먼이 대꾸했어. \"파리도 많고.\""]], [["Newman appeared to derive great entertainment from this repartee, and to the great discomposure of Arthur Gride’s nerves, produced a series of sharp cracks from his finger-joints, resembling the noise of a distant discharge of small artillery.", "뉴먼은 이 응수에서 큰 재미를 보는 모양이었어. 그러고는 아서 그라이드의 신경을 잔뜩 거슬리게 하면서, 손가락 마디에서 날카로운 소리를 연거푸 냈는데, 멀리서 소총 일제 사격을 하는 소리 같았지."], ["Arthur succeeded in finishing his reply to Ralph’s note, nevertheless, and at length handed it over to the eccentric messenger for delivery.", "그래도 아서는 랄프의 쪽지에 답장을 마저 다 써냈고, 마침내 그걸 그 별난 심부름꾼에게 건네며 전해 달라고 했어."]], [["‘That’s it, Mr.", "\"이거요, 노그스 씨.\""], ["Noggs,’ said Gride.", "그라이드가 말했어."]], [["Newman gave a nod, put it in his hat, and was shuffling away, when Gride, whose doting delight knew no bounds, beckoned him back again, and said, in a shrill whisper, and with a grin which puckered up his whole face, and almost obscured his eyes:", "뉴먼은 고개를 끄덕이고, 그걸 모자 속에 넣고는, 발을 끌며 나가려 했어. 그런데 노망 든 기쁨에 어쩔 줄 모르던 그라이드가 그를 도로 불러서는, 얼굴 전체가 쪼글쪼글 일그러져 눈이 거의 안 보일 정도로 히죽거리며, 새된 목소리로 속삭였어."]], [["‘Will you--will you take a little drop of something--just a taste?’", "\"뭘—뭘 좀 한 방울 들지 않겠소—그저 맛만이라도?\""]], [["In good fellowship (if Arthur Gride had been capable of it) Newman would not have drunk with him one bubble of the richest wine that was ever made; but to see what he would be at, and to punish him as much as he could, he accepted the offer immediately.", "진짜 우정에서라면 (아서 그라이드한테 그런 게 있었다면 말이지) 뉴먼은 세상에서 가장 좋은 포도주라도 거품 한 방울 함께 마시지 않았을 거야. 하지만 그가 무슨 꿍꿍이인지 보려고, 또 할 수 있는 만큼 그를 골탕 먹이려고, 뉴먼은 그 제안을 냉큼 받아들였어."]], [["Arthur Gride, therefore, again applied himself to the press, and from a shelf laden with tall Flemish drinking-glasses, and quaint bottles: some with necks like so many storks, and others with square Dutch-built bodies and short fat apoplectic throats: took down one dusty bottle of promising appearance, and two glasses of curiously small size.", "그래서 아서 그라이드는 다시 장롱으로 다가가, 키 큰 플랑드르식 술잔과 기묘한 병이 잔뜩 놓인 선반에서 — 어떤 병은 황새처럼 목이 길었고, 어떤 병은 네모반듯한 네덜란드식 몸통에 짧고 통통한 중풍 걸린 듯한 목을 하고 있었지 — 그럴듯해 보이는 먼지 낀 병 하나와, 신기할 만큼 자그마한 잔 둘을 꺼냈어."]], [["‘You never tasted this,’ said Arthur.", "\"이건 한 번도 맛본 적 없을 거요.\" 아서가 말했어."], ["‘It’s EAU-D’OR--golden water.", "\"이건 오 도르요—황금물이지."], ["I like it on account of its name.", "난 그 이름 때문에 이걸 좋아하오."], ["It’s a delicious name.", "멋진 이름이잖소."], ["Water of gold, golden water!", "황금의 물, 황금물!"], ["O dear me, it seems quite a sin to drink it!’", "오 이런, 이걸 마신다는 게 죄짓는 것 같구려!\""]], [["As his courage appeared to be fast failing him, and he trifled with the stopper in a manner which threatened the dismissal of the bottle to its old place, Newman took up one of the little glasses, and clinked it, twice or thrice, against the bottle, as a gentle reminder that he had not been helped yet.", "용기가 빠르게 사그라드는 모양이었어. 그가 병을 도로 제자리에 갖다 놓을 것처럼 마개를 만지작거리자, 뉴먼은 그 작은 잔 하나를 집어 들고는, 아직 한 잔도 못 받았다는 점잖은 채근으로 병에다 두세 번 쨍 부딪쳤지."], ["With a deep sigh, Arthur Gride slowly filled it--though not to the brim--and then filled his own.", "아서 그라이드는 깊은 한숨을 쉬며 천천히 잔을 채웠어—그래도 가장자리까지는 아니었지—그러고는 자기 잔도 채웠어."]], [["‘Stop, stop; don’t drink it yet,’ he said, laying his hand on Newman’s; ‘it was given to me, twenty years ago, and when I take a little taste, which is ve--ry seldom, I like to think of it beforehand, and tease myself.", "\"멈춰요, 멈춰. 아직 마시지 마시오.\""], ["We’ll drink a toast.", "그가 뉴먼의 손에 제 손을 얹으며 말했어. \"이건 이십 년 전에 받은 거요."], ["Shall we drink a toast, Mr.", "그리고 난 가끔, 아—주 드물게 한 모금 맛볼 때면, 미리 그걸 떠올리며 나 자신을 약 올리는 걸 좋아한다오. 우리 건배합시다."], ["Noggs?’", "건배합시다, 노그스 씨, 어떻소?\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said Newman, eyeing his little glass impatiently.", "\"아!\" 뉴먼이 제 작은 잔을 안달하며 쳐다봤어."], ["‘Look sharp.", "\"빨리 합시다."], ["Bearer waits.’", "심부름꾼이 기다린다고.\""]], [["‘Why, then, I’ll tell you what,’ tittered Arthur, ‘we’ll drink--he, he, he!--we’ll drink a lady.’", "\"그럼, 내가 뭘 할지 말해 주리다.\" 아서가 킥킥거렸어. \"우리 건배합시다—헤, 헤, 헤!—우리 한 숙녀를 위해 건배합시다.\""]], [["‘THE ladies?’ said Newman.", "\"숙녀분들 말이오?\" 뉴먼이 말했어."]], [["‘No, no, Mr.", "\"아니, 아니오, 노그스 씨.\""], ["Noggs,’ replied Gride, arresting his hand, ‘A lady.", "그라이드가 그의 손을 멈추며 대답했어. \"한 숙녀요."], ["You wonder to hear me say A lady.", "내가 한 숙녀라고 하니 이상하지요."], ["I know you do, I know you do.", "알아요, 알고말고."], ["Here’s little Madeline.", "자, 어린 매들린이오."], ["That’s the toast. Mr.", "그게 건배사요, 노그스 씨."], ["Noggs. Little Madeline!’", "어린 매들린!\""]], [["‘Madeline!’ said Newman; inwardly adding, ‘and God help her!’", "\"매들린!\" 뉴먼이 말하고는, 속으로 덧붙였어. '하느님 그녀를 도우소서!'"]], [["The rapidity and unconcern with which Newman dismissed his portion of the golden water, had a great effect upon the old man, who sat upright in his chair, and gazed at him, open-mouthed, as if the sight had taken away his breath.", "뉴먼이 제 몫의 황금물을 어찌나 잽싸고 태평하게 비워 버렸던지, 영감은 큰 충격을 받았어. 그는 의자에 꼿꼿이 앉아, 그 광경에 숨이 멎기라도 한 듯 입을 떡 벌리고 그를 멍하니 바라봤지."], ["Quite unmoved, however, Newman left him to sip his own at leisure, or to pour it back again into the bottle, if he chose, and departed; after greatly outraging the dignity of Peg Sliderskew by brushing past her, in the passage, without a word of apology or recognition.", "하지만 뉴먼은 눈 하나 깜짝 안 하고, 영감이야 천천히 제 잔을 홀짝이든 병에 도로 따라 넣든 내버려 둔 채 떠나 버렸어. 그러면서 복도에서 페그 슬라이더스큐를 스치고 지나가며, 사과 한마디 인사 한마디 없이 그 여자의 위엄을 단단히 짓밟아 놓고 말았지."]], [["Mr.", "그라이드 씨와 가정부는 둘만 남자마자, 곧장 방법과 수단을 따지는 위원회로 둔갑해, 어린 신부를 맞을 준비를 어떻게 할지 의논했어."], ["Gride and his housekeeper, immediately on being left alone, resolved themselves into a committee of ways and means, and discussed the arrangements which should be made for the reception of the young bride.", "그런데 이들은 다른 몇몇 위원회들처럼, 논의가 지독히도 따분하고 장황했어. 그러니 이 이야기는 뉴먼 노그스의 발자취를 좇기로 하지."], ["As they were, like some other committees, extremely dull and prolix in debate, this history may pursue the footsteps of Newman Noggs; thereby combining advantage with necessity; for it would have been necessary to do so under any circumstances, and necessity has no law, as all the world knows.", "그러면 필요한 일에 이로움까지 더해지는 셈이거든. 어차피 어떤 상황에서든 그렇게 해야 했을 테고, 세상이 다 알다시피 필요 앞엔 법도 없으니까."]], [["‘You’ve been a long time,’ said Ralph, when Newman returned.", "\"오래도 걸렸군.\" 뉴먼이 돌아오자 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘HE was a long time,’ replied Newman.", "\"그쪽이 오래 걸렸지요.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어."]], [["‘Bah!’ cried Ralph impatiently.", "\"흥!\" 랄프가 안달하며 외쳤어."], ["‘Give me his note, if he gave you one: his message, if he didn’t.", "\"쪽지를 주거든 그 쪽지를 내놓고, 안 줬으면 전갈을 내놔."], ["And don’t go away.", "그리고 가지 말게."], ["I want a word with you, sir.’", "자네한테 할 말이 있으니, 이 사람아.\""]], [["Newman handed in the note, and looked very virtuous and innocent while his employer broke the seal, and glanced his eye over it.", "뉴먼은 쪽지를 건네고, 주인이 봉인을 뜯어 쭉 훑어보는 동안 아주 점잖고 결백한 얼굴을 했어."]], [["‘He’ll be sure to come,’ muttered Ralph, as he tore it to pieces; ‘why of course, I know he’ll be sure to come.", "\"틀림없이 올 거야.\" 랄프가 쪽지를 갈가리 찢으며 중얼거렸어."], ["What need to say that?", "\"왜, 그야 당연히 그 자가 틀림없이 올 줄 알지. 그걸 뭐하러 적어?"], ["Noggs!", "노그스!"], ["Pray, sir, what man was that, with whom I saw you in the street last night?’", "자, 이 사람아, 어젯밤 길에서 자네랑 같이 있던 그 사내는 누구였나?\""]], [["‘I don’t know,’ replied Newman.", "\"모릅니다.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어."]], [["‘You had better refresh your memory, sir,’ said Ralph, with a threatening look.", "\"기억을 좀 되살리는 게 좋을 거야, 이 사람아.\" 랄프가 위협하는 눈빛으로 말했어."]], [["‘I tell you,’ returned Newman boldly, ‘that I don’t know.", "\"말씀드리지만,\" 뉴먼이 대담하게 대꾸했어."], ["He came here twice, and asked for you.", "\"전 모릅니다. 그 자는 여기 두 번 와서 사장님을 찾았죠."], ["You were out.", "사장님은 안 계셨고요."], ["He came again.", "그 자가 또 왔습니다."], ["You packed him off, yourself.", "사장님이 직접 쫓아 보냈고요."], ["He gave the name of Brooker.’", "브루커라고 이름을 댔죠.\""]], [["‘I know he did,’ said Ralph; ‘what then?’", "\"그건 나도 알아.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"그래서?\""]], [["‘What then?", "\"그래서요?"], ["Why, then he lurked about and dogged me in the street.", "그야, 그 뒤로 그 자가 근처에서 어슬렁거리며 길에서 절 따라다닙니다."], ["He follows me, night after night, and urges me to bring him face to face with you; as he says he has been once, and not long ago either.", "밤마다 절 쫓아다니며, 사장님이랑 얼굴을 맞대게 해 달라고 졸라 대죠. 전에 한 번 그런 적이 있다나요, 그것도 얼마 안 됐다고."], ["He wants to see you face to face, he says, and you’ll soon hear him out, he warrants.’", "사장님을 얼굴 맞대고 보고 싶다는 거예요. 그러면 사장님이 곧 자기 말을 끝까지 들으실 거라고 장담하더군요.\""]], [["‘And what say you to that?’ inquired Ralph, looking keenly at his drudge.", "\"그래서 자넨 뭐라고 했나?\" 랄프가 제 머슴을 날카롭게 쳐다보며 물었어."]], [["‘That it’s no business of mine, and I won’t.", "\"그건 제 알 바 아니니 안 한다고요."], ["I told him he might catch you in the street, if that was all he wanted, but no!", "그게 다라면 길에서 사장님을 붙들 수도 있을 거라고 했죠. 근데 안 된대요!"], ["that wouldn’t do.", "그건 안 된다는 거예요."], ["You wouldn’t hear a word there, he said.", "거기선 사장님이 한마디도 안 들으실 거라더군요."], ["He must have you alone in a room with the door locked, where he could speak without fear, and you’d soon change your tone, and hear him patiently.’", "문을 잠근 방에서 사장님과 단둘이 있어야 한다고요. 거기서라면 겁 없이 말할 수 있고, 그러면 사장님도 곧 태도가 달라져서 진득하게 자기 말을 들으실 거라나요.\""]], [["‘An audacious dog!’ Ralph muttered.", "\"건방진 개 같은 놈!\" 랄프가 중얼거렸어."]], [["‘That’s all I know,’ said Newman.", "\"제가 아는 건 그게 다입니다.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."], ["‘I say again, I don’t know what man he is.", "\"다시 말씀드리지만, 전 그 자가 누군지 몰라요."], ["I don’t believe he knows himself.", "그 자도 자기가 누군지 모를 거라고 봅니다."], ["You have seen him; perhaps YOU do.’", "사장님은 그 자를 보셨으니, 어쩌면 사장님은 아실지도 모르죠.\""]], [["‘I think I do,’ replied Ralph.", "\"아는 것 같군.\" 랄프가 대답했어."]], [["‘Well,’ retored Newman, sulkily, ‘don’t expect me to know him too; that’s all.", "\"흥.\" 뉴먼이 뚱하게 받아쳤어."], ["You’ll ask me, next, why I never told you this before.", "\"저까지 그 자를 알기를 바라진 마세요. 그게 다예요."], ["What would you say, if I was to tell you all that people say of you?", "다음엔 왜 이걸 진작 말 안 했냐고 물으시겠죠. 사람들이 사장님을 두고 떠드는 말을 제가 죄다 일러바치면 뭐라고 하실 건데요?"], ["What do you call me when I sometimes do?", "제가 가끔 그러면 절 뭐라고 부르세요?"], ["“Brute, ass!”", "'짐승, 멍청이!'"], ["and snap at me like a dragon.’", "하면서 용처럼 절 물어뜯잖아요.\""]], [["This was true enough; though the question which Newman anticipated, was, in fact, upon Ralph’s lips at the moment.", "이건 꽤 사실이었어. 사실 뉴먼이 미리 짚은 그 질문이, 바로 그 순간 랄프의 입가에 막 떠올라 있었거든."]], [["‘He is an idle ruffian,’ said Ralph; ‘a vagabond from beyond the sea where he travelled for his crimes; a felon let loose to run his neck into the halter; a swindler, who has the audacity to try his schemes on me who know him well.", "\"그 자는 빈둥거리는 불한당이야.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["The next time he tampers with you, hand him over to the police, for attempting to extort money by lies and threats,--d’ye hear?--and leave the rest to me.", "\"제 죄 때문에 바다 건너로 떠돌던 부랑자고. 제 목을 교수형 밧줄에 들이밀려고 풀려난 중죄인이지."], ["He shall cool his heels in jail a little time, and I’ll be bound he looks for other folks to fleece, when he comes out.", "그리고 제 속을 빤히 아는 나한테 감히 수작을 부려 보려는 사기꾼이고. 그 자가 다음에 또 자넬 꼬드기거든, 거짓말과 협박으로 돈을 뜯어내려 했다고 경찰에 넘겨 버려—알겠나?—나머지는 나한테 맡기고."], ["You mind what I say, do you?’", "잠시 감옥에서 발이나 식히게 해 주면, 나오고 나선 등쳐 먹을 다른 사람을 찾아 나설 게 틀림없어. 내 말 새겨듣는 건가, 응?\""]], [["‘I hear,’ said Newman.", "\"듣고 있습니다.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."]], [["‘Do it then,’ returned Ralph, ‘and I’ll reward you.", "\"그럼 그렇게 해.\" 랄프가 대꾸했어."], ["Now, you may go.’", "\"그럼 내 자네한테 상을 주지. 자, 이제 가도 좋아.\""]], [["Newman readily availed himself of the permission, and, shutting himself up in his little office, remained there, in very serious cogitation, all day.", "뉴먼은 냉큼 그 허락을 받아들였어. 그러고는 자기 작은 사무실에 틀어박혀, 온종일 거기 앉아 아주 심각하게 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었지."], ["When he was released at night, he proceeded, with all the expedition he could use, to the city, and took up his old position behind the pump, to watch for Nicholas.", "밤에 풀려나자, 그는 쓸 수 있는 모든 속도를 다해 도심으로 가서, 펌프 뒤 옛 자리를 잡고 니콜라스를 기다렸어."], ["For Newman Noggs was proud in his way, and could not bear to appear as his friend, before the brothers Cheeryble, in the shabby and degraded state to which he was reduced.", "뉴먼 노그스는 나름대로 자존심이 있어서, 자기가 떨어진 이 초라하고 비참한 꼴로 치어리블 형제 앞에서 니콜라스의 친구로 나서는 걸 차마 견딜 수 없었거든."]], [["He had not occupied this position many minutes, when he was rejoiced to see Nicholas approaching, and darted out from his ambuscade to meet him.", "이 자리를 잡은 지 몇 분 안 돼서, 그는 니콜라스가 다가오는 걸 보고 반가워하며, 매복 장소에서 뛰쳐나가 그를 맞이했어."], ["Nicholas, on his part, was no less pleased to encounter his friend, whom he had not seen for some time; so, their greeting was a warm one.", "니콜라스도 한동안 못 본 친구를 만나게 되어 그에 못지않게 기뻤지. 그래서 둘의 인사는 따뜻했어."]], [["‘I was thinking of you, at that moment,’ said Nicholas.", "\"마침 그때 당신 생각을 하고 있었어요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘That’s right,’ rejoined Newman, ‘and I of you.", "\"그것참 잘됐군.\" 뉴먼이 대꾸했어."], ["I couldn’t help coming up, tonight.", "\"나도 자네 생각을 했어. 오늘 밤엔 안 올 수가 없었네."], ["I say, I think I am going to find out something.’", "저기, 내가 뭔가 알아낼 것 같단 말이야.\""]], [["‘And what may that be?’ returned Nicholas, smiling at this odd communication.", "\"그게 뭔데요?\" 니콜라스가 이 묘한 말에 웃으며 되물었어."]], [["‘I don’t know what it may be, I don’t know what it may not be,’ said Newman; ‘it’s some secret in which your uncle is concerned, but what, I’ve not yet been able to discover, although I have my strong suspicions. I’ll not hint ‘em now, in case you should be disappointed.’", "\"그게 뭘 수도 있는지, 뭐가 아닐 수도 있는지 나도 모르겠어.\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"자네 삼촌이 얽힌 무슨 비밀인데, 그게 뭔진 아직 못 알아냈어. 짚이는 데는 강하게 있지만 말이야. 지금은 안 떠벌릴 거야, 자네가 실망할까 봐.\""]], [["‘I disappointed!’ cried Nicholas; ‘am I interested?’", "\"내가 실망한다고요!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어. \"나랑 관계가 있는 거예요?\""]], [["‘I think you are,’ replied Newman.", "\"있는 것 같아.\" 뉴먼이 대답했어."], ["‘I have a crotchet in my head that it must be so.", "\"그래야만 한다는 생각이 머릿속에 박혀 있거든."], ["I have found out a man, who plainly knows more than he cares to tell at once.", "분명 한꺼번에 다 털어놓고 싶어 하지 않는데, 그래도 더 많이 아는 사내를 하나 찾아냈어."], ["And he has already dropped such hints to me as puzzle me--I say, as puzzle me,’ said Newman, scratching his red nose into a state of violent inflammation, and staring at Nicholas with all his might and main meanwhile.", "게다가 그 자가 벌써 나한테 어찌나 아리송한 말을 흘렸는지—그러니까, 날 어리둥절하게 만드는 말이지.\" 뉴먼은 빨간 코를 벅벅 긁어 시뻘겋게 부어오르게 하면서, 그러는 내내 있는 힘껏 니콜라스를 빤히 쳐다보며 말했어."]], [["Admiring what could have wound his friend up to such a pitch of mystery, Nicholas endeavoured, by a series of questions, to elucidate the cause; but in vain.", "대체 무엇이 친구를 이토록 수수께끼에 휩싸이게 만들었는지 궁금했던 니콜라스는, 잇따라 질문을 던지며 그 까닭을 밝혀 보려 했어. 하지만 헛수고였지."], ["Newman could not be drawn into any more explicit statement than a repetition of the perplexities he had already thrown out, and a confused oration, showing, How it was necessary to use the utmost caution; how the lynx-eyed Ralph had already seen him in company with his unknown correspondent; and how he had baffled the said Ralph by extreme guardedness of manner and ingenuity of speech; having prepared himself for such a contingency from the first.", "뉴먼한테서는, 이미 던져 놓은 의문들을 되풀이하는 것과, 두서없는 일장 연설 말고는 더 분명한 말을 끌어낼 수 없었어. 그 연설인즉, 어떻게 최대한 조심해야 하는지, 어떻게 스라소니 눈을 한 랄프가 이미 그 정체 모를 상대와 함께 있는 자기를 봤는지, 또 어떻게 자기가 처음부터 그런 일에 대비해 두었던 덕에, 극도로 신중한 태도와 교묘한 말솜씨로 그 랄프를 따돌렸는지에 관한 거였어."]], [["Remembering his companion’s propensity,--of which his nose, indeed, perpetually warned all beholders like a beacon,--Nicholas had drawn him into a sequestered tavern.", "뉴먼의 코가, 사실 봉화처럼 보는 사람 모두에게 끊임없이 경고를 보내는 그 버릇을 떠올린 니콜라스는, 그를 외진 선술집으로 데려갔어. 거기서 둘은 자기들 사귐의 시작과 그 흘러온 과정을 되짚어 봤지."], ["Here, they fell to reviewing the origin and progress of their acquaintance, as men sometimes do, and tracing out the little events by which it was most strongly marked, came at last to Miss Cecilia Bobster.", "사람들이 가끔 그러듯이 말이야. 그러다 그 사귐에서 가장 또렷한 자취를 남긴 자잘한 일들을 더듬어 가던 끝에, 마침내 세실리아 봅스터 양 이야기까지 다다랐어."]], [["‘And that reminds me,’ said Newman, ‘that you never told me the young lady’s real name.’", "\"그러고 보니 생각났는데,\" 뉴먼이 말했어. \"자넨 그 아가씨 진짜 이름을 한 번도 말 안 했어.\""]], [["‘Madeline!’ said Nicholas.", "\"매들린이에요!\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Madeline!’ cried Newman.", "\"매들린!\" 뉴먼이 외쳤어."], ["‘What Madeline?", "\"무슨 매들린?"], ["Her other name.", "성을 말해."], ["Say her other name.’", "성을 말해 보라고.\""]], [["‘Bray,’ said Nicholas, in great astonishment.", "\"브레이요.\" 니콜라스가 몹시 놀라며 말했어."]], [["‘It’s the same!’ cried Newman.", "\"바로 그 여자야!\" 뉴먼이 외쳤어."], ["‘Sad story!", "\"딱한 사연이지!"], ["Can you stand idly by, and let that unnatural marriage take place without one attempt to save her?’", "자넨 이 천륜에 어긋난 결혼이, 그 여자를 구하려는 시도 한 번 없이 벌어지는 걸 멀거니 보고만 있을 텐가?\""]], [["‘What do you mean?’ exclaimed Nicholas, starting up; ‘marriage!", "\"무슨 소리예요?\" 니콜라스가 벌떡 일어서며 소리쳤어."], ["are you mad?’", "\"결혼이라뇨! 미친 거예요?\""]], [["‘Are you? Is she?", "\"자네가 미친 거 아닌가?"], ["Are you blind, deaf, senseless, dead?’ said Newman.", "그 여자가? 자넨 눈멀고, 귀먹고, 정신 나가고, 죽었나?\""], ["‘Do you know that within one day, by means of your uncle Ralph, she will be married to a man as bad as he, and worse, if worse there is?", "뉴먼이 말했어. \"하루 안에, 자네 삼촌 랄프의 손을 거쳐, 그 여자가 그자만큼이나 못된 놈한테, 아니 더 못된 놈이 있다면 그보다 더한 놈한테 시집간다는 걸 모르나?"], ["Do you know that, within one day, she will be sacrificed, as sure as you stand there alive, to a hoary wretch--a devil born and bred, and grey in devils’ ways?’", "하루 안에, 자네가 거기 살아 서 있는 게 확실한 것만큼이나 확실하게, 그 여자가 백발의 비열한 놈한테—태어날 때부터 마귀로 나서 마귀의 길에서 늙어 빠진 놈한테—제물로 바쳐진다는 걸 모르냐고?\""]], [["‘Be careful what you say,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"말조심해요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘For Heaven’s sake be careful!", "\"제발 부탁이니 조심하라고요!"], ["I am left here alone, and those who could stretch out a hand to rescue her are far away.", "난 여기 혼자 남겨졌고, 그 여자를 구하러 손 내밀어 줄 사람들은 다 멀리 있어요."], ["What is it that you mean?’", "대체 무슨 소리예요?\""]], [["‘I never heard her name,’ said Newman, choking with his energy.", "\"난 그 여자 이름을 들은 적이 없었어.\" 뉴먼이 격해진 나머지 목이 메어 말했어."], ["‘Why didn’t you tell me?", "\"왜 나한테 말 안 했나?"], ["How was I to know?", "내가 어떻게 알았겠어?"], ["We might, at least, have had some time to think!’", "그랬으면 적어도 생각해 볼 시간이라도 좀 있었을 텐데!\""]], [["‘What is it that you mean?’ cried Nicholas.", "\"대체 무슨 소리냐고요?\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어."]], [["It was not an easy task to arrive at this information; but, after a great quantity of extraordinary pantomime, which in no way assisted it, Nicholas, who was almost as wild as Newman Noggs himself, forced the latter down upon his seat and held him down until he began his tale.", "이 사정을 알아내기란 쉬운 일이 아니었어. 하지만 아무짝에도 도움 안 되는 야단스러운 손짓 발짓을 한바탕 거친 끝에, 뉴먼 노그스 못지않게 거의 제정신이 아니던 니콜라스가, 그를 의자에 억지로 눌러 앉히고 이야기를 시작할 때까지 꽉 붙들어 놨지."]], [["Rage, astonishment, indignation, and a storm of passions, rushed through the listener’s heart, as the plot was laid bare.", "음모가 낱낱이 드러나자, 듣는 이의 가슴속으로 분노와 경악과 격분과 격정의 폭풍이 휘몰아쳤어."], ["He no sooner understood it all, than with a face of ashy paleness, and trembling in every limb, he darted from the house.", "그는 모든 걸 다 알아듣자마자, 잿빛으로 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 온 사지를 떨면서, 집 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔어."]], [["‘Stop him!’ cried Newman, bolting out in pursuit.", "\"저 사람 잡아요!\" 뉴먼이 뒤쫓아 뛰쳐나가며 외쳤어."], ["‘He’ll be doing something desperate; he’ll murder somebody.", "\"저 사람 무슨 끔찍한 짓을 저지를 거야. 누굴 죽일 거라고."], ["Hallo!", "이봐요!"], ["there, stop him.", "거기, 저 사람 좀 잡아요."], ["Stop thief!", "도둑이야!"], ["stop thief!’", "도둑이야!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 52", "# CHAPTER 52"]], [["Nicholas despairs of rescuing Madeline Bray, but plucks up his Spirits again, and determines to attempt it. Domestic Intelligence of the Kenwigses and Lillyvicks", "옛날 일을 다시 떠올려야겠다."]], [["Finding that Newman was determined to arrest his progress at any hazard, and apprehensive that some well-intentioned passenger, attracted by the cry of ‘Stop thief,’ might lay violent hands upon his person, and place him in a disagreeable predicament from which he might have some difficulty in extricating himself, Nicholas soon slackened his pace, and suffered Newman Noggs to come up with him: which he did, in so breathless a condition, that it seemed impossible he could have held out for a minute longer.", "뉴먼이 무슨 수를 써서라도 자기를 멈춰 세우려 한다는 걸 알아차린 니콜라스는, 혹시 \"도둑이야!\" 하는 소리에 마음씨 좋은 행인이 자기를 덥석 붙들어서 빠져나오기 힘든 곤란한 처지에 몰아넣지나 않을까 걱정이 되었다. 그래서 곧 걸음을 늦추고, 뉴먼 노그스가 따라붙도록 내버려 두었다. 따라붙은 뉴먼은 숨이 어찌나 가빴던지, 일 분만 더 갔으면 도저히 못 버텼을 것 같았다."]], [["‘I will go straight to Bray’s,’ said Nicholas.", "\"브레이의 집으로 곧장 가겠어요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다."], ["‘I will see this man.", "\"그 사람을 만나야겠어요."], ["If there is a feeling of humanity lingering in his breast, a spark of consideration for his own child, motherless and friendless as she is, I will awaken it.’", "그 가슴에 인정이 조금이라도 남아 있다면, 엄마도 친구도 없는 제 자식한테 마음 한 자락이라도 쓸 줄 안다면, 제가 그 마음을 일깨워 보겠어요.\""]], [["‘You will not,’ replied Newman.", "\"안 돼.\" 뉴먼이 대꾸했다."], ["‘You will not, indeed.’", "\"정말 안 돼.\""]], [["‘Then,’ said Nicholas, pressing onward, ‘I will act upon my first impulse, and go straight to Ralph Nickleby.’", "\"그럼,\" 니콜라스가 앞으로 밀고 나가며 말했다. \"처음 먹었던 마음대로 하겠어요. 곧장 랄프 니클비한테 가겠어요.\""]], [["‘By the time you reach his house he will be in bed,’ said Newman.", "\"네가 그 집에 닿을 때쯤이면 벌써 잠자리에 들어 있을 거다.\" 뉴먼이 말했다."]], [["‘I’ll drag him from it,’ cried Nicholas.", "\"잠자리에서 끌어내겠어요.\" 니콜라스가 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Tut, tut,’ said Noggs.", "\"쯧, 쯧.\" 노그스가 말했다."], ["‘Be yourself.’", "\"정신 좀 차려라.\""]], [["‘You are the best of friends to me, Newman,’ rejoined Nicholas after a pause, and taking his hand as he spoke. ‘I have made head against many trials; but the misery of another, and such misery, is involved in this one, that I declare to you I am rendered desperate, and know not how to act.’", "\"당신은 제게 둘도 없는 친구예요, 뉴먼.\" 니콜라스가 잠시 말을 멈췄다가, 그의 손을 잡으며 다시 말했다. \"저는 많은 시련을 이겨 냈어요. 그렇지만 이번 일에는 다른 사람의 불행이, 그것도 이런 끔찍한 불행이 얽혀 있어요. 솔직히 말하면 저는 절망에 빠졌고, 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["In truth, it did seem a hopeless case.", "사실 정말 가망 없는 일처럼 보였다."], ["It was impossible to make any use of such intelligence as Newman Noggs had gleaned, when he lay concealed in the closet.", "뉴먼 노그스가 벽장 안에 숨어 있을 때 주워들은 그런 정보로는 아무것도 할 수가 없었다."], ["The mere circumstance of the compact between Ralph Nickleby and Gride would not invalidate the marriage, or render Bray averse to it, who, if he did not actually know of the existence of some such understanding, doubtless suspected it.", "랄프 니클비와 그라이드가 짜고 든 거래가 있다는 사실만으로는 그 결혼을 무효로 만들 수도 없고, 브레이가 결혼을 마다하게 만들 수도 없었다. 브레이는 그런 밀약이 있다는 걸 정확히 알지는 못해도, 어렴풋이 눈치는 채고 있을 게 분명했다."], ["What had been hinted with reference to some fraud on Madeline, had been put, with sufficient obscurity by Arthur Gride, but coming from Newman Noggs, and obscured still further by the smoke of his pocket-pistol, it became wholly unintelligible, and involved in utter darkness.", "매들린을 속이려는 어떤 사기가 있다는 식의 암시가 있긴 했지만, 아서 그라이드가 워낙 흐릿하게 흘렸던 데다, 그걸 뉴먼 노그스가 전하면서 권총 연기 때문에 더 뿌예진 통에, 완전히 알아들을 수 없는 캄캄한 이야기가 되어 버렸다."]], [["‘There seems no ray of hope,’ said Nicholas.", "\"한 줄기 희망도 안 보여요.\" 니콜라스가 말했다."]], [["‘The greater necessity for coolness, for reason, for consideration, for thought,’ said Newman, pausing at every alternate word, to look anxiously in his friend’s face.", "\"그러니 더더욱 침착해야 한다. 이성을 잃지 말고, 잘 따져 보고, 곰곰이 생각해야지.\""], ["‘Where are the brothers?’", "뉴먼이 한 단어 건너 한 번씩 말을 멈추며, 걱정스럽게 친구의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. \"그 형제분들은 어디 계시냐?\""]], [["‘Both absent on urgent business, as they will be for a week to come.’", "\"두 분 다 급한 일로 떠나셨어요. 앞으로 일주일은 안 돌아오실 거예요.\""]], [["‘Is there no way of communicating with them?", "\"연락할 길이 없냐?"], ["No way of getting one of them here by tomorrow night?’", "내일 밤까지 한 분이라도 여기로 모셔 올 방법이 없어?\""]], [["‘Impossible!’ said Nicholas, ‘the sea is between us and them.", "\"불가능해요!\" 니콜라스가 말했다."], ["With the fairest winds that ever blew, to go and return would take three days and nights.’", "\"우리하고 그분들 사이에 바다가 가로놓여 있어요. 아무리 바람이 잘 불어 준다 해도, 갔다 오는 데만 사흘 낮밤이 걸려요.\""]], [["‘Their nephew,’ said Newman, ‘their old clerk.’", "\"그분들 조카,\" 뉴먼이 말했다. \"아니면 그 늙은 서기.\""]], [["‘What could either do, that I cannot?’ rejoined Nicholas.", "\"그 사람들이 한들, 제가 못 할 무슨 일을 할 수 있겠어요?\""], ["‘With reference to them, especially, I am enjoined to the strictest silence on this subject.", "니콜라스가 대꾸했다. \"특히 그분들에 관해서는, 이 일을 철저히 입 다물라는 분부를 받았어요."], ["What right have I to betray the confidence reposed in me, when nothing but a miracle can prevent this sacrifice?’", "기적이 아니고서는 이 희생을 막을 길이 없는데, 저한테 맡겨진 비밀을 무슨 권리로 떠벌리겠어요?\""]], [["‘Think,’ urged Newman.", "\"생각해 봐라.\" 뉴먼이 다그쳤다."], ["‘Is there no way?’", "\"정말 길이 없어?\""]], [["‘There is none,’ said Nicholas, in utter dejection.", "\"없어요.\" 니콜라스가 완전히 풀이 죽어 말했다."], ["‘Not one.", "\"하나도요."], ["The father urges, the daughter consents.", "아버지는 몰아붙이고, 딸은 받아들였어요."], ["These demons have her in their toils; legal right, might, power, money, and every influence are on their side.", "그 악마 같은 자들이 그 여자를 손아귀에 넣었어요. 법적 권리며, 힘이며, 권력이며, 돈이며, 온갖 영향력이 다 그들 편이에요."], ["How can I hope to save her?’", "제가 무슨 수로 그 여자를 구하기를 바라겠어요?\""]], [["‘Hope to the last!’ said Newman, clapping him on the back.", "\"끝까지 희망을 가져!\" 뉴먼이 그의 등을 탁 치며 말했다."], ["‘Always hope; that’s a dear boy.", "\"언제나 희망을 가져, 착하지."], ["Never leave off hoping; it don’t answer.", "절대 희망을 놓지 마. 그래 봐야 소용없으니까."], ["Do you mind me, Nick?", "내 말 알겠냐, 닉?"], ["It don’t answer.", "소용없는 짓이야."], ["Don’t leave a stone unturned.", "손 안 댄 데가 없게 다 뒤집어 봐."], ["It’s always something, to know you’ve done the most you could.", "할 수 있는 만큼 다 했다는 걸 아는 것만으로도 늘 뭔가 남는 거야."], ["But, don’t leave off hoping, or it’s of no use doing anything.", "그렇지만 희망을 놓아 버리면, 무슨 일을 해도 다 헛수고다."], ["Hope, hope, to the last!’", "희망을 가져, 끝까지 희망을 가져!\""]], [["Nicholas needed encouragement.", "니콜라스에게는 격려가 필요했다."], ["The suddenness with which intelligence of the two usurers’ plans had come upon him, the little time which remained for exertion, the probability, almost amounting to certainty itself, that a few hours would place Madeline Bray for ever beyond his reach, consign her to unspeakable misery, and perhaps to an untimely death; all this quite stunned and overwhelmed him.", "두 고리대금업자의 계략이 너무도 갑자기 닥쳐온 것이며, 손쓸 시간이 얼마 남지 않은 것이며, 몇 시간만 지나면 매들린 브레이가 영영 손닿을 수 없는 곳으로 멀어져 말할 수 없는 불행에, 어쩌면 때 이른 죽음에까지 내던져지리라는 거의 확신에 가까운 짐작이며, 이 모든 것이 그를 얼떨떨하게 짓눌렀다."], ["Every hope connected with her that he had suffered himself to form, or had entertained unconsciously, seemed to fall at his feet, withered and dead.", "자기도 모르게 품어 왔거나, 스스로 품게 내버려 두었던, 그 여자와 얽힌 모든 희망이 발치에 시들어 죽은 채 떨어지는 것 같았다."], ["Every charm with which his memory or imagination had surrounded her, presented itself before him, only to heighten his anguish and add new bitterness to his despair.", "그의 기억이나 상상이 그 여자를 둘러쌌던 온갖 매력이 하나하나 눈앞에 떠올라, 그저 그의 괴로움만 더하고 절망에 새로운 쓰라림을 보탤 뿐이었다."], ["Every feeling of sympathy for her forlorn condition, and of admiration for her heroism and fortitude, aggravated the indignation which shook him in every limb, and swelled his heart almost to bursting.", "그 여자의 외로운 처지에 대한 가엾은 마음과, 그 여자의 꿋꿋함과 의연함에 대한 감탄이, 온몸을 뒤흔드는 분노를 부추겨, 가슴이 터질 듯 부풀어 올랐다."]], [["But, if Nicholas’s own heart embarrassed him, Newman’s came to his relief.", "그런데 니콜라스 자신의 마음이 그를 어쩔 줄 모르게 만든 그때, 뉴먼의 마음이 그를 구해 주었다."], ["There was so much earnestness in his remonstrance, and such sincerity and fervour in his manner, odd and ludicrous as it always was, that it imparted to Nicholas new firmness, and enabled him to say, after he had walked on for some little way in silence:", "늘 그렇듯 엉뚱하고 우스꽝스럽긴 했지만, 그의 충고에는 어찌나 진심이 담겨 있었고, 그 태도에는 어찌나 정성과 열의가 가득했던지, 그것이 니콜라스에게 새로운 굳건함을 불어넣어 주었다. 그래서 니콜라스는 잠시 말없이 걷다가 입을 열 수 있었다."]], [["‘You read me a good lesson, Newman, and I will profit by it. One step, at least, I may take--am bound to take indeed--and to that I will apply myself tomorrow.’", "\"당신이 제게 좋은 가르침을 주셨어요, 뉴먼. 그 가르침을 새기겠어요. 적어도 한 걸음은 내디딜 수 있어요. 아니, 꼭 내디뎌야만 하죠. 그 일에 내일부터 매달리겠어요.\""]], [["‘What is that?’ asked Noggs wistfully.", "\"그게 뭔데?\" 노그스가 간절히 물었다."], ["‘Not to threaten Ralph?", "\"랄프를 협박하려는 건 아니지?"], ["Not to see the father?’", "아버지를 만나려는 것도 아니고?\""]], [["‘To see the daughter, Newman,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"그 딸을 만나려는 거예요, 뉴먼.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다."], ["‘To do what, after all, is the utmost that the brothers could do, if they were here, as Heaven send they were!", "\"결국, 그 형제분들이 여기 계셨다면 하셨을 그 일을, 하늘이 그분들을 보내 주셨더라면 좋았을 텐데요, 제가 하려는 거예요!"], ["To reason with her upon this hideous union, to point out to her all the horrors to which she is hastening; rashly, it may be, and without due reflection.", "그 끔찍한 결혼에 대해 그 여자와 이야기를 나누고, 그 여자가 지금 어쩌면 경솔하게, 깊이 생각해 보지도 않고 서둘러 뛰어드는 그 온갖 무서운 일들을 일러 주려는 거예요."], ["To entreat her, at least, to pause.", "적어도 잠시만이라도 멈춰 보라고 사정해 보려고요."], ["She can have had no counsellor for her good.", "그 여자한테는 자기를 위해 조언해 줄 사람이 하나도 없었을 거예요."], ["Perhaps even I may move her so far yet, though it is the eleventh hour, and she upon the very brink of ruin.’", "지금이 마지막 순간이고 그 여자가 파멸의 벼랑 끝에 서 있긴 하지만, 어쩌면 저라도 그 여자의 마음을 그 정도는 움직일 수 있을지 몰라요.\""]], [["‘Bravely spoken!’ said Newman.", "\"훌륭한 말이다!\" 뉴먼이 말했다."], ["‘Well done, well done!", "\"잘했다, 잘했어!"], ["Yes.", "그래."], ["Very good.’", "아주 좋아.\""]], [["‘And I do declare,’ cried Nicholas, with honest enthusiasm, ‘that in this effort I am influenced by no selfish or personal considerations, but by pity for her, and detestation and abhorrence of this scheme; and that I would do the same, were there twenty rivals in the field, and I the last and least favoured of them all.’", "\"그리고 분명히 말하건대,\" 니콜라스가 진심에서 우러난 열의로 외쳤다. \"이번 일에서 저는 이기적이거나 사사로운 마음 때문에 움직이는 게 아니에요. 그 여자가 가엾고, 이 계략이 너무 끔찍하고 역겨워서 그러는 거예요. 그리고 만약 경쟁자가 스무 명이나 있고, 제가 그중 맨 꼴찌에 가장 인기 없는 사람이라 해도, 저는 똑같이 했을 거예요.\""]], [["‘You would, I believe,’ said Newman.", "\"그랬을 거라고 믿는다.\" 뉴먼이 말했다."], ["‘But where are you hurrying now?’", "\"그런데 지금 어디로 그렇게 서두르는 거냐?\""]], [["‘Homewards,’ answered Nicholas.", "\"집으로요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했다."], ["‘Do you come with me, or I shall say good-night?’", "\"같이 가시겠어요? 아니면 여기서 작별 인사를 드릴까요?\""]], [["‘I’ll come a little way, if you will but walk: not run,’ said Noggs.", "\"네가 뛰지 말고 걸어 주기만 한다면, 조금 같이 가마.\" 노그스가 말했다."]], [["‘I cannot walk tonight, Newman,’ returned Nicholas, hurriedly.", "\"오늘 밤엔 걸을 수가 없어요, 뉴먼.\" 니콜라스가 다급하게 대답했다."], ["‘I must move rapidly, or I could not draw my breath.", "\"빨리 움직여야지, 안 그러면 숨도 못 쉬겠어요."], ["I’ll tell you what I’ve said and done tomorrow.’", "제가 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 일을 했는지는 내일 알려 드릴게요.\""]], [["Without waiting for a reply, he darted off at a rapid pace, and, plunging into the crowds which thronged the street, was quickly lost to view.", "대답도 기다리지 않고 그는 빠른 걸음으로 내달려, 거리를 가득 메운 인파 속으로 뛰어들더니 이내 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["‘He’s a violent youth at times,’ said Newman, looking after him; ‘and yet I like him for it.", "\"저 녀석은 가끔 너무 격해진단 말이야.\" 뉴먼이 그 뒷모습을 바라보며 말했다."], ["There’s cause enough now, or the deuce is in it.", "\"그래도 난 그 점이 좋아. 지금이야 그럴 만도 하지, 안 그러면 별일이고."], ["Hope!", "희망이라!"], ["I SAID hope, I think!", "내가 희망을 가지라고 했지!"], ["Ralph Nickleby and Gride with their heads together!", "랄프 니클비와 그라이드가 머리를 맞대고 있는데!"], ["And hope for the opposite party!", "상대편한테 희망을 가지라니!"], ["Ho!", "호!"], ["ho!’", "호!\""]], [["It was with a very melancholy laugh that Newman Noggs concluded this soliloquy; and it was with a very melancholy shake of the head, and a very rueful countenance, that he turned about, and went plodding on his way.", "뉴먼 노그스는 이 혼잣말을 아주 서글픈 웃음으로 맺었다. 그러고는 아주 서글프게 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, 몹시 침울한 얼굴로 돌아서서 터덜터덜 제 갈 길을 갔다."]], [["This, under ordinary circumstances, would have been to some small tavern or dram-shop; that being his way, in more senses than one.", "여느 때 같았으면 그는 작은 선술집이나 술집으로 발길을 옮겼을 것이다. 그게 여러모로 그의 버릇이었으니까."], ["But, Newman was too much interested, and too anxious, to betake himself even to this resource, and so, with many desponding and dismal reflections, went straight home.", "그렇지만 뉴먼은 이 일에 너무 마음이 쓰이고 너무 걱정이 되어, 그런 데에 기댈 생각조차 못 한 채, 우울하고 침울한 생각에 잠겨 곧장 집으로 갔다."]], [["It had come to pass, that afternoon, that Miss Morleena Kenwigs had received an invitation to repair next day, per steamer from Westminster Bridge, unto the Eel-pie Island at Twickenham: there to make merry upon a cold collation, bottled beer, shrub, and shrimps, and to dance in the open air to the music of a locomotive band, conveyed thither for the purpose: the steamer being specially engaged by a dancing-master of extensive connection for the accommodation of his numerous pupils, and the pupils displaying their appreciation of the dancing-master’s services, by purchasing themselves, and inducing their friends to do the like, divers light-blue tickets, entitling them to join the expedition. Of these light-blue tickets, one had been presented by an ambitious neighbour to Miss Morleena Kenwigs, with an invitation to join her daughters; and Mrs. Kenwigs, rightly deeming that the honour of the family was involved in Miss Morleena’s making the most splendid appearance possible on so short a notice, and testifying to the dancing-master that there were other dancing-masters besides him, and to all fathers and mothers present that other people’s children could learn to be genteel besides theirs, had fainted away twice under the magnitude of her preparations, but, upheld by a determination to sustain the family name or perish in the attempt, was still hard at work when Newman Noggs came home.", "그날 오후, 모를리나 켄위그스 양에게 초대장이 한 장 와 있었다. 다음 날 웨스트민스터 다리에서 증기선을 타고 트위크넘에 있는 일파이 섬으로 놀러 가자는 것이었다. 거기서 찬 음식과 병맥주, 셔브, 새우를 곁들여 신나게 놀고, 그 자리를 위해 일부러 데려온 악단의 연주에 맞춰 야외에서 춤을 추기로 되어 있었다. 그 증기선은 인맥이 넓은 어느 무용 선생이 자기 수많은 제자들을 위해 따로 빌린 것이었다. 제자들은 옅은 파란색 표를 여러 장 사들이고 친구들한테도 사도록 권하는 것으로, 무용 선생의 노고에 대한 고마움을 나타냈다. 그 표가 있어야 이 나들이에 낄 수 있었던 것이다. 야심만만한 어느 이웃이 이 옅은 파란색 표 가운데 한 장을 모를리나 켄위그스 양에게 건네며, 자기 딸들과 함께 가자고 청했다. 켄위그스 부인은 이렇게 촉박한 통고에도 모를리나 양이 가장 화려한 차림으로 나서야 가문의 명예가 선다고 마땅히 여겼다. 또 그렇게 해서 그 무용 선생 말고도 다른 무용 선생이 있다는 걸 그에게 보여 주고, 그 자리에 모인 모든 아버지와 어머니들에게 자기네 자식 말고도 남의 자식 역시 점잖아질 수 있다는 걸 증명해 보이고 싶었다. 그래서 준비할 일이 어찌나 어마어마한지 두 번이나 까무러쳤지만, 가문의 이름을 지키지 못하면 차라리 죽고 말겠다는 각오로 버티며, 뉴먼 노그스가 집에 돌아왔을 때에도 여전히 한창 일에 매달려 있었다."]], [["Now, between the italian-ironing of frills, the flouncing of trousers, the trimming of frocks, the faintings and the comings-to again, incidental to the occasion, Mrs.", "그런데 이 일에 따라붙는 주름 장식 다리미질이며, 바지 자락 손질이며, 원피스 단장이며, 까무러쳤다 다시 깨어났다 하는 통에, 켄위그스 부인은 완전히 정신이 팔려 있었다. 그래서 불과 삼십 분 전에서야, 모를리나 양의 아마빛 머리 갈래가 말하자면 제멋대로 자라 부스스해졌다는 걸 알아차렸다."], ["Kenwigs had been so entirely occupied, that she had not observed, until within half an hour before, that the flaxen tails of Miss Morleena’s hair were, in a manner, run to seed; and that, unless she were put under the hands of a skilful hairdresser, she never could achieve that signal triumph over the daughters of all other people, anything less than which would be tantamount to defeat.", "솜씨 좋은 미용사의 손을 빌리지 않고서는, 모를리나가 다른 집 딸들을 보란 듯이 압도하는 그 눈부신 승리를 결코 거둘 수 없을 터였다. 그만 못한 것은 곧 패배나 마찬가지였다."], ["This discovery drove Mrs.", "이 발견에 켄위그스 부인은 절망에 빠졌다."], ["Kenwigs to despair; for the hairdresser lived three streets and eight dangerous crossings off; Morleena could not be trusted to go there alone, even if such a proceeding were strictly proper: of which Mrs.", "미용실이 세 골목 너머, 위험한 건널목을 여덟 개나 지나야 하는 곳에 있었기 때문이다. 설령 그게 격식에 어긋나지 않는 일이라 쳐도 — 켄위그스 부인은 그 점도 미심쩍게 여겼다 — 모를리나 혼자 거기까지 가게 둘 수는 없었다."], ["Kenwigs had her doubts; Mr. Kenwigs had not returned from business; and there was nobody to take her.", "켄위그스 씨는 아직 일에서 돌아오지 않았고, 모를리나를 데려갈 사람이 아무도 없었다."], ["So, Mrs. Kenwigs first slapped Miss Kenwigs for being the cause of her vexation, and then shed tears.", "그래서 켄위그스 부인은 우선 자기를 속상하게 만든 죄로 켄위그스 양을 찰싹 때렸다가, 이내 눈물을 흘렸다."]], [["‘You ungrateful child!’ said Mrs.", "\"이 은혜도 모르는 것아!\""], ["Kenwigs, ‘after I have gone through what I have, this night, for your good.’", "켄위그스 부인이 말했다. \"내가 오늘 밤 너 잘되라고 그 고생을 다 했는데.\""]], [["‘I can’t help it, ma,’ replied Morleena, also in tears; ‘my hair WILL grow.’", "\"제 잘못이 아니잖아요, 엄마.\" 모를리나도 울면서 대꾸했다. \"머리가 자라는 걸 어떡해요.\""]], [["‘Don’t talk to me, you naughty thing!’ said Mrs.", "\"입 다물어, 이 못된 것아!\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했다."], ["Kenwigs, ‘don’t!", "\"그만해!"], ["Even if I was to trust you by yourself and you were to escape being run over, I know you’d run in to Laura Chopkins,’ who was the daughter of the ambitious neighbour, ‘and tell her what you’re going to wear tomorrow, I know you would.", "너를 혼자 보내서 차에 안 치이고 무사히 간다 쳐도, 넌 분명 로라 촙킨스네로 쪼르르 달려가서,\" 로라는 그 야심만만한 이웃의 딸이었다."], ["You’ve no proper pride in yourself, and are not to be trusted out of sight for an instant.’", "\"내일 무슨 옷을 입을 건지 죄다 일러바칠 거다, 분명히 그럴 거야. 너는 제 앞가림이라곤 모르고, 잠깐도 눈 밖에 두면 못 믿을 애야.\""]], [["Deploring the evil-mindedness of her eldest daughter in these terms, Mrs Kenwigs distilled fresh drops of vexation from her eyes, and declared that she did believe there never was anybody so tried as she was.", "이렇게 큰딸의 못된 심보를 한탄하면서, 켄위그스 부인은 눈에서 새로운 속상함의 눈물방울을 짜냈다. 그러고는 세상에 자기만큼 시달리며 사는 사람은 없을 거라고 단언했다."], ["Thereupon, Morleena Kenwigs wept afresh, and they bemoaned themselves together.", "그러자 모를리나 켄위그스도 다시 울음을 터뜨려, 둘이 함께 신세 한탄을 했다."]], [["Matters were at this point, as Newman Noggs was heard to limp past the door on his way upstairs; when Mrs.", "일이 이 지경에 이르렀을 때, 뉴먼 노그스가 위층으로 올라가느라 절뚝거리며 문 앞을 지나가는 소리가 들렸다."], ["Kenwigs, gaining new hope from the sound of his footsteps, hastily removed from her countenance as many traces of her late emotion as were effaceable on so short a notice: and presenting herself before him, and representing their dilemma, entreated that he would escort Morleena to the hairdresser’s shop.", "켄위그스 부인은 그 발소리에 새로운 희망을 얻어, 이렇게 짧은 사이에 지울 수 있는 만큼 방금 흘린 감정의 흔적을 얼른 얼굴에서 닦아 냈다. 그러고는 그의 앞에 나서서 자기네 딱한 사정을 늘어놓으며, 모를리나를 미용실까지 바래다 달라고 사정했다."]], [["‘I wouldn’t ask you, Mr. Noggs,’ said Mrs.", "\"노그스 씨가 얼마나 착하고 마음씨 따뜻한 분인지 몰랐다면, 이런 부탁 안 드렸을 거예요.\""], ["Kenwigs, ‘if I didn’t know what a good, kind-hearted creature you are; no, not for worlds.", "켄위그스 부인이 말했다. \"정말이지, 무슨 일이 있어도요."], ["I am a weak constitution, Mr.", "저는 몸이 약하답니다, 노그스 씨."], ["Noggs, but my spirit would no more let me ask a favour where I thought there was a chance of its being refused, than it would let me submit to see my children trampled down and trod upon, by envy and lowness!’", "그렇지만 제 자존심은, 거절당할 것 같은 데에 가서 부탁하는 것을 도저히 못 견뎌요. 제 아이들이 시샘과 천박함에 짓밟히고 깔리는 꼴을 보고만 있지 못하는 것처럼요!\""]], [["Newman was too good-natured not to have consented, even without this avowal of confidence on the part of Mrs. Kenwigs.", "뉴먼은 켄위그스 부인이 이렇게 믿음을 드러내지 않았더라도 두말없이 응했을 만큼 마음씨가 고운 사람이었다."], ["Accordingly, a very few minutes had elapsed, when he and Miss Morleena were on their way to the hairdresser’s.", "그리하여 몇 분도 채 지나지 않아, 그와 모를리나 양은 미용실로 가는 길에 올라 있었다."]], [["It was not exactly a hairdresser’s; that is to say, people of a coarse and vulgar turn of mind might have called it a barber’s; for they not only cut and curled ladies elegantly, and children carefully, but shaved gentlemen easily. Still, it was a highly genteel establishment--quite first-rate in fact--and there were displayed in the window, besides other elegancies, waxen busts of a light lady and a dark gentleman which were the admiration of the whole neighbourhood. Indeed, some ladies had gone so far as to assert, that the dark gentleman was actually a portrait of the spirted young proprietor; and the great similarity between their head-dresses--both wore very glossy hair, with a narrow walk straight down the middle, and a profusion of flat circular curls on both sides--encouraged the idea. The better informed among the sex, however, made light of this assertion, for however willing they were (and they were very willing) to do full justice to the handsome face and figure of the proprietor, they held the countenance of the dark gentleman in the window to be an exquisite and abstract idea of masculine beauty, realised sometimes, perhaps, among angels and military men, but very rarely embodied to gladden the eyes of mortals.", "엄밀히 말하면 그곳은 미용실이라기보다는, 거칠고 천박한 사람들이라면 이발소라고 불렀을 만한 곳이었다. 거기서는 숙녀들의 머리를 우아하게 자르고 곱슬곱슬 말아 주고, 아이들 머리를 정성껏 손질해 주었을 뿐 아니라, 신사들의 수염도 손쉽게 깎아 주었기 때문이다. 그래도 그곳은 무척 점잖은 가게였다. 사실 일류라 할 만했다. 진열창에는 다른 우아한 물건들과 함께, 살결 흰 여인과 거무스름한 신사의 밀랍 흉상이 놓여 있었는데, 온 동네의 감탄을 자아내는 것이었다. 정말이지 어떤 부인들은, 그 거무스름한 신사가 다름 아닌 그 패기 넘치는 젊은 주인의 초상이라고까지 우겨 댔다. 둘의 머리 모양이 어찌나 닮았는지 — 둘 다 반들반들한 머리카락을 한가운데로 곧게 가르마를 타고, 양옆으로는 납작한 동그란 곱슬머리를 잔뜩 늘어뜨리고 있었다 — 그런 생각을 부추겼다. 하지만 좀 더 사정에 밝은 여인들은 이 주장을 대수롭잖게 여겼다. 그들은 주인의 잘생긴 얼굴과 풍채를 흔쾌히 인정하긴 했지만 — 사실 아주 기꺼이 인정했다 — 진열창 속 거무스름한 신사의 얼굴만큼은 남성미의 더없이 빼어나고 추상적인 표상이라고 보았기 때문이다. 그런 얼굴은 어쩌면 천사들이나 군인들 가운데서는 이따금 실현될지 몰라도, 인간의 눈을 즐겁게 해 주려고 실물로 나타나는 일은 아주 드문 것이었다."]], [["It was to this establishment that Newman Noggs led Miss Kenwigs in safety.", "뉴먼 노그스는 켄위그스 양을 이 가게까지 무사히 데려다주었다."], ["The proprietor, knowing that Miss Kenwigs had three sisters, each with two flaxen tails, and all good for sixpence apiece, once a month at least, promptly deserted an old gentleman whom he had just lathered for shaving, and handing him over to the journeyman, (who was not very popular among the ladies, by reason of his obesity and middle age,) waited on the young lady himself.", "주인은 켄위그스 양에게 누이가 셋이나 있고, 저마다 아마빛 머리 갈래가 둘씩 있어 적어도 한 달에 한 번은 머리당 육 펜스씩 벌게 해 준다는 걸 알고 있었다. 그래서 막 비누 거품을 발라 면도를 시작하려던 노신사를 얼른 내팽개치고, 그 손님을 직공에게 넘겨 버린 채 — 그 직공은 뚱뚱하고 나이가 지긋한 탓에 숙녀들 사이에서 별로 인기가 없었다 — 몸소 그 아가씨를 맡았다."]], [["Just as this change had been effected, there presented himself for shaving, a big, burly, good-humoured coal-heaver with a pipe in his mouth, who, drawing his hand across his chin, requested to know when a shaver would be disengaged.", "이렇게 손님이 바뀌자마자, 입에 파이프를 문 덩치 크고 우락부락하면서도 사람 좋아 보이는 석탄 일꾼 하나가 면도를 하러 나타났다. 그는 턱을 손으로 쓱 문지르며, 면도해 줄 사람이 언제 손이 비는지 알고 싶다고 했다."]], [["The journeyman, to whom this question was put, looked doubtfully at the young proprietor, and the young proprietor looked scornfully at the coal-heaver: observing at the same time:", "이 질문을 받은 직공은 미심쩍은 눈으로 젊은 주인을 쳐다보았고, 젊은 주인은 그 석탄 일꾼을 경멸하듯 바라보며 이렇게 말했다."]], [["‘You won’t get shaved here, my man.’", "\"여기선 면도 못 해 드립니다, 손님.\""]], [["‘Why not?’ said the coal-heaver.", "\"왜요?\" 석탄 일꾼이 말했다."]], [["‘We don’t shave gentlemen in your line,’ remarked the young proprietor.", "\"우린 당신 같은 일 하는 분들 면도는 안 합니다.\" 젊은 주인이 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Why, I see you a shaving of a baker, when I was a looking through the winder, last week,’ said the coal-heaver.", "\"아니, 지난주에 창문으로 들여다봤더니 빵집 사람을 면도해 주던데요.\" 석탄 일꾼이 말했다."]], [["‘It’s necessary to draw the line somewheres, my fine feller,’ replied the principal.", "\"어딘가에 선은 그어야 하지 않겠습니까, 이 양반아.\" 주인이 대꾸했다."], ["‘We draw the line there.", "\"우린 거기에 선을 긋습니다."], ["We can’t go beyond bakers.", "빵집 사람보다 아래로는 안 내려가요."], ["If we was to get any lower than bakers, our customers would desert us, and we might shut up shop.", "빵집 사람보다 더 낮은 데까지 손대기 시작하면, 손님들이 다 떨어져 나가서 가게 문을 닫아야 할 겁니다."], ["You must try some other establishment, sir.", "다른 가게를 찾아보셔야겠어요, 손님."], ["We couldn’t do it here.’", "여기선 안 됩니다.\""]], [["The applicant stared; grinned at Newman Noggs, who appeared highly entertained; looked slightly round the shop, as if in depreciation of the pomatum pots and other articles of stock; took his pipe out of his mouth and gave a very loud whistle; and then put it in again, and walked out.", "손님은 멀거니 쳐다보더니, 무척 재미있어하는 표정인 뉴먼 노그스를 보고 씩 웃었다. 그러고는 머릿기름 단지며 다른 물건들을 깔보는 듯 가게 안을 슬쩍 둘러보고는, 입에서 파이프를 빼 아주 큰 소리로 휘파람을 한 번 불더니, 다시 파이프를 물고 걸어 나갔다."]], [["The old gentleman who had just been lathered, and who was sitting in a melancholy manner with his face turned towards the wall, appeared quite unconscious of this incident, and to be insensible to everything around him in the depth of a reverie--a very mournful one, to judge from the sighs he occasionally vented--in which he was absorbed.", "방금 비누 거품을 바른 채, 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌리고 침울하게 앉아 있던 노신사는 이 소동을 전혀 알아차리지 못한 듯, 깊은 상념에 빠져 주위의 모든 것에 무감각한 모양이었다. 이따금 내쉬는 한숨으로 미루어, 무척이나 서글픈 상념에 잠겨 있는 것 같았다."], ["Affected by this example, the proprietor began to clip Miss Kenwigs, the journeyman to scrape the old gentleman, and Newman Noggs to read last Sunday’s paper, all three in silence: when Miss Kenwigs uttered a shrill little scream, and Newman, raising his eyes, saw that it had been elicited by the circumstance of the old gentleman turning his head, and disclosing the features of Mr.", "그 본보기에 영향을 받았는지, 주인은 켄위그스 양의 머리를 자르고, 직공은 노신사의 수염을 밀고, 뉴먼 노그스는 지난 일요일 신문을 읽으며, 셋 다 말없이 있었다. 그때 켄위그스 양이 가느다란 비명을 짧게 질렀다."], ["Lillyvick the collector.", "뉴먼이 눈을 들어 보니, 노신사가 고개를 돌리는 바람에 수금원 릴리비크 씨의 얼굴이 드러났던 것이다."]], [["The features of Mr.", "분명 릴리비크 씨의 얼굴이긴 했지만, 묘하게 달라져 있었다."], ["Lillyvick they were, but strangely altered.", "언제나 깨끗하고 말끔하게 면도를 하고 사람들 앞에 나서기로 작정한 노신사가 있다면, 그게 바로 릴리비크 씨였다."], ["If ever an old gentleman had made a point of appearing in public, shaved close and clean, that old gentleman was Mr. Lillyvick.", "수금원답게 처신하며, 온 세상이 자기 장부에 올라 있고 그게 전부 두 분기치나 밀려 있기라도 한 듯, 모든 사람 앞에서 엄숙하고 위풍당당한 위엄을 떨치는 수금원이 있다면, 그게 바로 릴리비크 씨였다."], ["If ever a collector had borne himself like a collector, and assumed, before all men, a solemn and portentous dignity as if he had the world on his books and it was all two quarters in arrear, that collector was Mr. Lillyvick.", "그런데 지금 그는 적어도 일주일은 묵은 수염 자국이 턱에 거치적거리고, 가슴 위에는 당당하게 빳빳이 선 게 아니라 더럽고 구겨진 셔츠 주름 장식이 말하자면 잔뜩 움츠러든 채, 너무도 풀이 죽고 처지고 낙담한, 그래서 굴욕과 슬픔과 부끄러움이 묻어나는 태도로 앉아 있었다."], ["And now, there he sat, with the remains of a beard at least a week old encumbering his chin; a soiled and crumpled shirt-frill crouching, as it were, upon his breast, instead of standing boldly out; a demeanour so abashed and drooping, so despondent, and expressive of such humiliation, grief, and shame; that if the souls of forty unsubstantial housekeepers, all of whom had had their water cut off for non-payment of the rate, could have been concentrated in one body, that one body could hardly have expressed such mortification and defeat as were now expressed in the person of Mr. Lillyvick the collector.", "요금을 안 내 수돗물이 끊긴 보잘것없는 셋방살이꾼 마흔 명의 넋을 한 몸뚱이에 죄다 모아 놓는다 해도, 지금 수금원 릴리비크 씨의 모습에 어린 그런 굴욕과 패배감만큼은 도저히 드러내지 못했을 것이다."]], [["Newman Noggs uttered his name, and Mr.", "뉴먼 노그스가 그의 이름을 부르자, 릴리비크 씨는 끙 신음을 했다."], ["Lillyvick groaned: then coughed to hide it.", "그러고는 그걸 감추려고 기침을 했다."], ["But the groan was a full-sized groan, and the cough was but a wheeze.", "하지만 신음은 제대로 된 신음이었고, 기침은 쌕쌕거리는 소리에 지나지 않았다."]], [["‘Is anything the matter?’ said Newman Noggs.", "\"무슨 일이라도 있으세요?\" 뉴먼 노그스가 말했다."]], [["‘Matter, sir!’ cried Mr. Lillyvick.", "\"무슨 일이냐고요!\""], ["‘The plug of life is dry, sir, and but the mud is left.’", "릴리비크 씨가 외쳤다. \"삶의 마개가 다 말라 버리고, 진흙만 남았소이다.\""]], [["This speech--the style of which Newman attributed to Mr. Lillyvick’s recent association with theatrical characters--not being quite explanatory, Newman looked as if he were about to ask another question, when Mr.", "이 말은 — 뉴먼이 보기에 릴리비크 씨가 요즘 연극쟁이들과 어울린 탓에 생긴 말투였다 — 도무지 무슨 뜻인지 알 수가 없었다."], ["Lillyvick prevented him by shaking his hand mournfully, and then waving his own.", "뉴먼이 또 한 가지 물어보려는 듯한 표정을 짓자, 릴리비크 씨가 슬프게 그의 손을 흔들어 잡았다가 제 손을 휘저으며 그를 막았다."]], [["‘Let me be shaved!’ said Mr.", "\"면도나 합시다!\""], ["Lillyvick.", "릴리비크 씨가 말했다."], ["‘It shall be done before Morleena; it IS Morleena, isn’t it?’", "\"모를리나가 끝나기 전에 마쳐야지. 모를리나 맞지요?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ said Newman.", "\"네.\" 뉴먼이 말했다."]], [["‘Kenwigses have got a boy, haven’t they?’ inquired the collector.", "\"켄위그스네는 사내아이를 봤지요, 안 그래요?\" 수금원이 물었다."]], [["Again Newman said ‘Yes.’", "뉴먼이 또 \"네.\" 하고 말했다."]], [["‘Is it a nice boy?’ demanded the collector.", "\"잘생긴 아이요?\" 수금원이 물었다."]], [["‘It ain’t a very nasty one,’ returned Newman, rather embarrassed by the question.", "\"썩 못난 애는 아니에요.\" 뉴먼이 그 질문에 다소 거북해하며 대답했다."]], [["‘Susan Kenwigs used to say,’ observed the collector, ‘that if ever she had another boy, she hoped it might be like me.", "\"수전 켄위그스가 늘 그랬소.\" 수금원이 말했다."], ["Is this one like me, Mr Noggs?’", "\"또 사내아이를 낳으면 나를 닮았으면 좋겠다고요. 이 아이가 나를 닮았소, 노그스 씨?\""]], [["This was a puzzling inquiry; but Newman evaded it, by replying to Mr Lillyvick, that he thought the baby might possibly come like him in time.", "이건 난처한 질문이었다. 하지만 뉴먼은, 그 아기가 어쩌면 시간이 지나면 그를 닮게 될지도 모르겠다고 릴리비크 씨에게 대답하며 슬쩍 피해 갔다."]], [["‘I should be glad to have somebody like me, somehow,’ said Mr.", "\"어떻게든 나를 닮은 사람이 하나 있으면 좋겠소.\" 릴리비크 씨가 말했다."], ["Lillyvick, ‘before I die.’", "\"내가 죽기 전에 말이오.\""]], [["‘You don’t mean to do that, yet awhile?’ said Newman.", "\"아직 그러실 생각은 없으시잖아요?\" 뉴먼이 말했다."]], [["Unto which Mr.", "이에 릴리비크 씨는 엄숙한 목소리로 \"면도나 합시다!\""], ["Lillyvick replied in a solemn voice, ‘Let me be shaved!’ and again consigning himself to the hands of the journeyman, said no more.", "하고 대답하고는, 다시 직공의 손에 몸을 맡긴 채 더는 말이 없었다."]], [["This was remarkable behaviour.", "이건 별난 행동이었다."], ["So remarkable did it seem to Miss Morleena, that that young lady, at the imminent hazard of having her ear sliced off, had not been able to forbear looking round, some score of times, during the foregoing colloquy. Of her, however, Mr.", "모를리나 양이 보기에 어찌나 별나던지, 그 아가씨는 자칫 귀가 잘려 나갈지도 모르는 위험을 무릅쓰고, 방금 그 대화가 오가는 동안 스무 번도 넘게 고개를 돌려 돌아보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Lillyvick took no notice: rather striving (so, at least, it seemed to Newman Noggs) to evade her observation, and to shrink into himself whenever he attracted her regards.", "그렇지만 릴리비크 씨는 그 애를 아랑곳하지 않았다. 오히려 (적어도 뉴먼 노그스가 보기에는) 그 애의 눈길을 피하려 애쓰며, 그 애가 자기를 쳐다볼 때마다 자기 속으로 움츠러들려는 듯했다."], ["Newman wondered very much what could have occasioned this altered behaviour on the part of the collector; but, philosophically reflecting that he would most likely know, sooner or later, and that he could perfectly afford to wait, he was very little disturbed by the singularity of the old gentleman’s deportment.", "뉴먼은 무엇이 수금원의 이 달라진 행동을 불러왔는지 몹시 궁금했다. 하지만 머잖아 알게 될 테고, 얼마든지 기다릴 수 있다고 차분히 생각하며, 그 노신사의 별난 처신에 별로 마음을 쓰지 않았다."]], [["The cutting and curling being at last concluded, the old gentleman, who had been some time waiting, rose to go, and, walking out with Newman and his charge, took Newman’s arm, and proceeded for some time without making any observation. Newman, who in power of taciturnity was excelled by few people, made no attempt to break silence; and so they went on, until they had very nearly reached Miss Morleena’s home, when Mr Lillyvick said:", "마침내 머리 손질과 곱슬 말기가 끝나자, 한참을 기다리고 있던 노신사가 가려고 일어섰다. 그러고는 뉴먼, 그리고 뉴먼이 맡은 아이와 함께 걸어 나오며 뉴먼의 팔을 잡았다. 한동안 그는 아무 말도 없이 걸었다. 말수 적기로는 어디 가서 빠지지 않는 뉴먼도 굳이 침묵을 깨려 하지 않았다. 그렇게 둘은 걸어가다가 모를리나 양의 집에 거의 다 이르렀을 때, 릴리비크 씨가 입을 열었다."]], [["‘Were the Kenwigses very much overpowered, Mr. Noggs, by that news?’", "\"켄위그스네는 그 소식에 무척 충격을 받았소, 노그스 씨?\""]], [["‘What news?’ returned Newman.", "\"무슨 소식 말씀이세요?\" 뉴먼이 되물었다."]], [["‘That about--my--being--’", "\"그게, 내가, 그러니까……\""]], [["‘Married?’ suggested Newman.", "\"결혼하셨다는 거요?\" 뉴먼이 말을 거들었다."]], [["‘Ah!’ replied Mr.", "\"아!\""], ["Lillyvick, with another groan; this time not even disguised by a wheeze.", "릴리비크 씨가 또 한 번 신음하며 대답했다. 이번에는 쌕쌕 기침으로 감추지도 않았다."]], [["‘It made ma cry when she knew it,’ interposed Miss Morleena, ‘but we kept it from her for a long time; and pa was very low in his spirits, but he is better now; and I was very ill, but I am better too.’", "\"엄마는 그 소식을 듣고 울었어요.\" 모를리나 양이 끼어들었다. \"그래도 우리가 엄마한테 한참 동안 숨겼어요. 아빠는 풀이 많이 죽었지만 지금은 나아지셨고요. 저도 많이 아팠는데, 저도 이제 나았어요.\""]], [["‘Would you give your great-uncle Lillyvick a kiss if he was to ask you, Morleena?’ said the collector, with some hesitation.", "\"내가 청하면 이 작은할아버지 릴리비크한테 입맞춰 주겠니, 모를리나?\" 수금원이 조금 머뭇거리며 말했다."]], [["‘Yes; uncle Lillyvick, I would,’ returned Miss Morleena, with the energy of both her parents combined; ‘but not aunt Lillyvick. She’s not an aunt of mine, and I’ll never call her one.’", "\"네, 릴리비크 할아버지, 해 드릴게요.\" 모를리나 양이 부모 둘의 기운을 합쳐 놓은 듯한 기세로 대답했다. \"그렇지만 릴리비크 할머니한테는 안 해요. 그 사람은 제 할머니가 아니에요. 절대 할머니라고 안 부를 거예요.\""]], [["Immediately upon the utterance of these words, Mr. Lillyvick caught Miss Morleena up in his arms, and kissed her; and, being by this time at the door of the house where Mr.", "이 말이 떨어지기가 무섭게, 릴리비크 씨는 모를리나 양을 두 팔로 번쩍 안아 올려 입을 맞추었다."], ["Kenwigs lodged (which, as has been before mentioned, usually stood wide open), he walked straight up into Mr Kenwigs’s sitting-room, and put Miss Morleena down in the midst. Mr.", "그러고는 이미 켄위그스 씨가 세 들어 사는 집 문 앞에 와 있었던지라 (앞서 말했듯이 그 문은 늘 활짝 열려 있었다), 곧장 켄위그스 씨의 거실로 걸어 올라가 모를리나 양을 한가운데에 내려놓았다."], ["and Mrs. Kenwigs were at supper.", "켄위그스 부부는 저녁을 먹고 있었다."], ["At sight of their perjured relative, Mrs Kenwigs turned faint and pale, and Mr. Kenwigs rose majestically.", "배신한 친척의 모습을 보자, 켄위그스 부인은 정신이 아득해지며 핏기가 가셨고, 켄위그스 씨는 위풍당당하게 일어섰다."]], [["‘Kenwigs,’ said the collector, ‘shake hands.’", "\"켄위그스,\" 수금원이 말했다. \"악수합시다.\""]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"이보세요.\""], ["Kenwigs, ‘the time has been, when I was proud to shake hands with such a man as that man as now surweys me.", "켄위그스 씨가 말했다. \"한때는 지금 나를 빤히 쳐다보는 저 사람 같은 분과 악수하는 게 자랑스럽던 시절이 있었지요."], ["The time has been, sir,’ said Mr.", "한때는요.\" 켄위그스 씨가 말했다."], ["Kenwigs, ‘when a wisit from that man has excited in me and my family’s boozums sensations both nateral and awakening.", "\"저 사람이 찾아오면 나와 우리 식구 가슴에 자연스럽고도 가슴 벅찬 감정이 일던 시절이 있었습니다."], ["But, now, I look upon that man with emotions totally surpassing everythink, and I ask myself where is his Honour, where is his straight-for’ardness, and where is his human natur?’", "그런데 지금은, 저 사람을 무엇으로도 다 못 담을 감정으로 바라보면서 자문해 봅니다. 저 사람의 명예는 어디로 갔으며, 그 정직함은 어디로 갔으며, 그 인간된 도리는 어디로 갔느냐고요.\""]], [["‘Susan Kenwigs,’ said Mr.", "\"수전 켄위그스.\""], ["Lillyvick, turning humbly to his niece, ‘don’t you say anything to me?’", "릴리비크 씨가 조카에게 겸손하게 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"너는 나한테 아무 말도 안 하니?\""]], [["‘She is not equal to it, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"이 사람은 그럴 기운도 없습니다, 이보세요.\""], ["Kenwigs, striking the table emphatically.", "켄위그스 씨가 탁자를 힘주어 내리치며 말했다."], ["‘What with the nursing of a healthy babby, and the reflections upon your cruel conduct, four pints of malt liquor a day is hardly able to sustain her.’", "\"튼튼한 갓난아기 젖 먹이랴, 당신의 그 매정한 처사를 곱씹으랴, 하루에 보리술 넉 파인트를 마셔도 몸을 가누기 힘들 지경이라고요.\""]], [["‘I am glad,’ said the poor collector meekly, ‘that the baby is a healthy one.", "\"갓난아기가 튼튼하다니 기쁘구나.\" 가엾은 수금원이 풀죽은 목소리로 말했다."], ["I am very glad of that.’", "\"그것 참 다행이야.\""]], [["This was touching the Kenwigses on their tenderest point. Mrs.", "이건 켄위그스 부부의 가장 여린 곳을 건드린 셈이었다."], ["Kenwigs instantly burst into tears, and Mr. Kenwigs evinced great emotion.", "켄위그스 부인은 당장 울음을 터뜨렸고, 켄위그스 씨도 크게 마음이 동했다."]], [["‘My pleasantest feeling, all the time that child was expected,’ said Mr Kenwigs, mournfully, ‘was a thinking, “If it’s a boy, as I hope it may be; for I have heard its uncle Lillyvick say again and again he would prefer our having a boy next, if it’s a boy, what will his uncle Lillyvick say? What will he like him to be called? Will he be Peter, or Alexander, or Pompey, or Diorgeenes, or what will he be?” And now when I look at him; a precious, unconscious, helpless infant, with no use in his little arms but to tear his little cap, and no use in his little legs but to kick his little self--when I see him a lying on his mother’s lap, cooing and cooing, and, in his innocent state, almost a choking hisself with his little fist--when I see him such a infant as he is, and think that that uncle Lillyvick, as was once a-going to be so fond of him, has withdrawed himself away, such a feeling of wengeance comes over me as no language can depicter, and I feel as if even that holy babe was a telling me to hate him.’", "\"그 아이를 기다리던 내내 내가 가장 기뻤던 건요.\" 켄위그스 씨가 서글프게 말했다. \"이런 생각을 한 거였지요. '사내아이라면, 부디 사내아이이길 바라지만, 그 애 외삼촌 릴리비크가 다음번엔 사내를 봤으면 좋겠다고 입버릇처럼 말씀하셨으니까, 사내아이라면 그 애 외삼촌 릴리비크가 뭐라고 하실까? 어떤 이름으로 불러 주길 바라실까? 피터일까, 알렉산더일까, 폼페이일까, 디오게네스일까, 아니면 뭐가 될까?' 그런데 지금 그 애를 들여다보면요. 아무것도 모르고 어쩔 줄 모르는 귀여운 갓난아기, 작은 팔로 할 줄 아는 거라곤 제 작은 모자를 잡아당기는 것뿐이고, 작은 다리로 할 줄 아는 거라곤 제 몸뚱이를 버둥거리는 것뿐인 아기를요. 그 애가 어미 무릎에 누워 옹알옹알 옹알이를 하며, 천진하게도 제 작은 주먹으로 제 목을 막다시피 하는 걸 보면요. 그 애가 그렇게 어린 갓난아기인 걸 보고, 한때 그 애를 그토록 예뻐하려던 외삼촌 릴리비크가 발길을 끊어 버렸다고 생각하면, 무슨 말로도 그려 낼 수 없는 복수심이 솟구쳐서, 저 거룩한 갓난아기조차 나더러 그를 미워하라고 일러 주는 것만 같단 말입니다.\""]], [["This affecting picture moved Mrs. Kenwigs deeply.", "이 가슴 뭉클한 그림에 켄위그스 부인은 깊이 동했다."], ["After several imperfect words, which vainly attempted to struggle to the surface, but were drowned and washed away by the strong tide of her tears, she spake.", "솟구쳐 올라오려고 안간힘을 쓰다가 거센 눈물의 물살에 휩쓸려 떠내려가 버린 몇 마디 어설픈 말을 늘어놓은 끝에, 그녀가 입을 열었다."]], [["‘Uncle,’ said Mrs.", "\"외삼촌.\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했다."], ["Kenwigs, ‘to think that you should have turned your back upon me and my dear children, and upon Kenwigs which is the author of their being--you who was once so kind and affectionate, and who, if anybody had told us such a thing of, we should have withered with scorn like lightning--you that little Lillyvick, our first and earliest boy, was named after at the very altar!", "\"외삼촌이 저와 제 사랑하는 아이들한테, 그리고 그 아이들을 있게 한 켄위그스한테 등을 돌리시다니요. 한때는 그토록 다정하고 정 깊으셨던 외삼촌이, 누가 외삼촌에 대해 그런 말을 했더라면 우리가 번개처럼 비웃으며 콧방귀를 뀌었을 그런 외삼촌이!"], ["Oh gracious!’", "우리 첫째이자 맏아들 어린 릴리비크의 이름을 바로 그 제단 앞에서 외삼촌을 따 지었는데! 아이고, 세상에!\""]], [["‘Was it money that we cared for?’ said Mr.", "\"우리가 돈을 바랐답니까?\" 켄위그스 씨가 말했다."], ["Kenwigs. ‘Was it property that we ever thought of?’", "\"우리가 재산을 한 번이라도 마음에 둔 적 있냐고요?\""]], [["‘No,’ cried Mrs.", "\"아니요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 외쳤다."], ["Kenwigs, ‘I scorn it.’", "\"나는 그딴 거 우습게 봐요.\""]], [["‘So do I,’ said Mr.", "\"나도 그래요.\""], ["Kenwigs, ‘and always did.’", "켄위그스 씨가 말했다. \"늘 그랬고요.\""]], [["‘My feelings have been lancerated,’ said Mrs.", "\"내 마음은 갈가리 찢겼어요.\" 켄위그스 부인이 말했다."], ["Kenwigs, ‘my heart has been torn asunder with anguish, I have been thrown back in my confinement, my unoffending infant has been rendered uncomfortable and fractious, Morleena has pined herself away to nothing; all this I forget and forgive, and with you, uncle, I never can quarrel.", "\"괴로움에 내 가슴이 찢어졌고, 산후조리도 도로 망쳤고, 아무 죄 없는 갓난아기까지 보채고 칭얼대게 되었고, 모를리나는 비쩍 말라 버렸어요. 이 모든 걸 저는 잊고 용서하겠어요."], ["But never ask me to receive HER, never do it, uncle.", "그리고 외삼촌하고는 절대 다투지 않을 거예요. 그렇지만 그 여자를 받아들이라는 말씀만은 하지 마세요, 절대로요, 외삼촌."], ["For I will not, I will not, I won’t, I won’t, I won’t!’", "저는 안 할 거예요, 안 해요, 안 할 거예요, 안 할 거예요, 안 할 거라고요!\""]], [["‘Susan, my dear,’ said Mr.", "\"수전, 여보.\" 켄위그스 씨가 말했다."], ["Kenwigs, ‘consider your child.’", "\"당신 아이를 생각해요.\""]], [["‘Yes,’ shrieked Mrs.", "\"그래요.\""], ["Kenwigs, ‘I will consider my child!", "켄위그스 부인이 비명을 질렀다."], ["I will consider my child!", "\"내 아이를 생각하겠어요!"], ["My own child, that no uncles can deprive me of; my own hated, despised, deserted, cut-off little child.’ And, here, the emotions of Mrs.", "내 아이를 생각할 거예요! 어떤 외삼촌도 나한테서 빼앗지 못할 내 아이, 미움받고 멸시받고 버림받고 내쳐진 내 어린 아이를요.\""], ["Kenwigs became so violent, that Mr. Kenwigs was fain to administer hartshorn internally, and vinegar externally, and to destroy a staylace, four petticoat strings, and several small buttons.", "여기서 켄위그스 부인의 감정이 어찌나 격해졌던지, 켄위그스 씨는 별수 없이 안으로는 사슴뿔 정기를 먹이고 밖으로는 식초를 발라 주었으며, 코르셋 끈 하나와 속치마 끈 넷, 자잘한 단추 여러 개를 못 쓰게 만들었다."]], [["Newman had been a silent spectator of this scene; for Mr.", "뉴먼은 이 광경을 말없이 지켜보고 있었다."], ["Lillyvick had signed to him not to withdraw, and Mr. Kenwigs had further solicited his presence by a nod of invitation.", "릴리비크 씨가 그에게 나가지 말라고 신호했고, 켄위그스 씨도 고개를 끄덕여 함께 있어 달라고 청했기 때문이다."], ["When Mrs. Kenwigs had been, in some degree, restored, and Newman, as a person possessed of some influence with her, had remonstrated and begged her to compose herself, Mr Lillyvick said in a faltering voice:", "켄위그스 부인이 어느 정도 정신을 차리고, 그녀에게 다소 영향력이 있는 사람으로서 뉴먼이 타이르며 진정하시라고 사정하자, 릴리비크 씨가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘I never shall ask anybody here to receive my--I needn’t mention the word; you know what I mean.", "\"나는 여기 누구한테도 내 — 굳이 그 말을 입에 올릴 것 없지. 무슨 말인지 알 테니까 — 받아들이라고 하지 않겠다."], ["Kenwigs and Susan, yesterday was a week she eloped with a half-pay captain!’", "켄위그스, 그리고 수전, 어제로 꼭 일주일 전에 그 여자가 반쯤 봉급 받는 퇴역 대위와 달아났단다!\""]], [["Mr. and Mrs. Kenwigs started together.", "켄위그스 부부가 동시에 움찔했다."]], [["‘Eloped with a half-pay captain,’ repeated Mr.", "\"퇴역 대위와 달아났어.\" 릴리비크 씨가 되풀이했다."], ["Lillyvick, ‘basely and falsely eloped with a half-pay captain.", "\"비열하고 거짓되게 퇴역 대위와 달아나 버렸지."], ["With a bottle-nosed captain that any man might have considered himself safe from.", "누구라도 안심하고 봤을 만한 술병코 대위와 말이야. 내가 헨리에타 페토커를 처음 본 게 바로 이 방이었지.\""], ["It was in this room,’ said Mr. Lillyvick, looking sternly round, ‘that I first see Henrietta Petowker.", "릴리비크 씨가 엄한 눈으로 둘러보며 말했다."], ["It is in this room that I turn her off, for ever.’", "\"그리고 그 여자를 영영 내치는 것도 바로 이 방이야.\""]], [["This declaration completely changed the whole posture of affairs. Mrs. Kenwigs threw herself upon the old gentleman’s neck, bitterly reproaching herself for her late harshness, and exclaiming, if she had suffered, what must his sufferings have been! Mr. Kenwigs grasped his hand, and vowed eternal friendship and remorse. Mrs. Kenwigs was horror-stricken to think that she should ever have nourished in her bosom such a snake, adder, viper, serpent, and base crocodile as Henrietta Petowker. Mr. Kenwigs argued that she must have been bad indeed not to have improved by so long a contemplation of Mrs. Kenwigs’s virtue. Mrs. Kenwigs remembered that Mr. Kenwigs had often said that he was not quite satisfied of the propriety of Miss Petowker’s conduct, and wondered how it was that she could have been blinded by such a wretch. Mr. Kenwigs remembered that he had had his suspicions, but did not wonder why Mrs. Kenwigs had not had hers, as she was all chastity, purity, and truth, and Henrietta all baseness, falsehood, and deceit. And Mr. and Mrs. Kenwigs both said, with strong feelings and tears of sympathy, that everything happened for the best; and conjured the good collector not to give way to unavailing grief, but to seek consolation in the society of those affectionate relations whose arms and hearts were ever open to him.", "이 선언이 사태를 완전히 뒤바꿔 놓았다. 켄위그스 부인은 노신사의 목을 끌어안고, 조금 전의 매정함을 가슴 아프게 자책하며, 자기가 괴로웠다면 외삼촌의 괴로움은 오죽했겠느냐고 외쳤다! 켄위그스 씨는 그의 손을 꽉 잡고 영원한 우정과 뉘우침을 맹세했다. 켄위그스 부인은 자기가 헨리에타 페토커 같은 뱀이며 살무사며 독사며 구렁이며 비열한 악어를 가슴에 품고 키웠다는 생각에 소름이 끼쳤다. 켄위그스 씨는, 그 여자가 켄위그스 부인의 미덕을 그렇게 오래 곁에서 보고도 나아지지 않았다면 정말이지 못된 사람이었던 게 틀림없다고 따졌다. 켄위그스 부인은, 켄위그스 씨가 페토커 양의 행실이 영 미덥지 않다고 자주 말했던 것을 떠올리며, 자기가 어쩌다 그런 못된 것한테 눈이 멀었었는지 의아해했다. 켄위그스 씨는 자기도 의심을 품고 있었노라고 떠올렸지만, 켄위그스 부인이 의심하지 않았던 건 조금도 이상할 게 없다고 했다. 그녀는 더없이 정숙하고 순결하고 진실한 사람인 반면, 헨리에타는 더없이 비열하고 거짓되고 음흉한 사람이었으니 말이다. 그러고는 켄위그스 부부가 둘 다 북받치는 마음과 동정의 눈물로, 모든 일에는 다 그럴 만한 까닭이 있는 법이라고 하며, 부디 부질없는 슬픔에 빠지지 말고 언제나 두 팔과 가슴을 활짝 열고 있는 정 깊은 친척들과 어울리며 위안을 찾으시라고 착한 수금원에게 간청했다."]], [["‘Out of affection and regard for you, Susan and Kenwigs,’ said Mr Lillyvick, ‘and not out of revenge and spite against her, for she is below it, I shall, tomorrow morning, settle upon your children, and make payable to the survivors of them when they come of age of marry, that money that I once meant to leave ‘em in my will. The deed shall be executed tomorrow, and Mr. Noggs shall be one of the witnesses. He hears me promise this, and he shall see it done.’", "\"수전과 켄위그스, 자네들을 아끼고 위하는 마음에서,\" 릴리비크 씨가 말했다. \"그 여자에 대한 복수나 앙심에서가 아니라네. 그 여자는 그럴 가치도 없으니까. 나는 내일 아침에 자네 아이들 앞으로 재산을 떼어 주겠네. 그리고 그 아이들이 성년이 되거나 결혼할 때 살아남은 아이들한테 지급되도록 하겠어. 한때 유언장에 남겨 주려던 그 돈을 말일세. 증서는 내일 작성할 거고, 노그스 씨가 증인 가운데 한 사람이 되어 줄 거야. 노그스 씨도 내가 이렇게 약속하는 걸 들었으니, 그 일이 이루어지는 것도 지켜봐 줄 거라네.\""]], [["Overpowered by this noble and generous offer, Mr. Kenwigs, Mrs.", "이 고귀하고 너그러운 제안에 압도되어, 켄위그스 씨와 켄위그스 부인, 그리고 모를리나 켄위그스 양이 모두 함께 흐느끼기 시작했다."], ["Kenwigs, and Miss Morleena Kenwigs, all began to sob together; and the noise of their sobbing, communicating itself to the next room, where the children lay a-bed, and causing them to cry too, Mr.", "그 흐느끼는 소리가 옆방까지 전해져, 거기 잠자리에 든 아이들마저 따라 울게 만들었다."], ["Kenwigs rushed wildly in, and bringing them out in his arms, by two and two, tumbled them down in their nightcaps and gowns at the feet of Mr. Lillyvick, and called upon them to thank and bless him.", "그러자 켄위그스 씨가 미친 듯이 뛰어 들어가, 아이들을 둘씩 둘씩 안아 데리고 나와, 잠자리 모자와 잠옷 바람으로 릴리비크 씨 발치에 우르르 내려놓고는, 외삼촌에게 고마워하고 복을 빌라고 일렀다."]], [["‘And now,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 이제.\""], ["Lillyvick, when a heart-rending scene had ensued and the children were cleared away again, ‘give me some supper.", "가슴 미어지는 장면이 한바탕 지나가고 아이들이 다시 치워지자, 릴리비크 씨가 말했다. \"저녁이나 좀 주게."], ["This took place twenty mile from town.", "이 일은 시내에서 이십 마일 떨어진 데서 벌어졌네. 나는 오늘 아침에 올라와서, 자네들을 보러 올 마음을 못 먹고 하루 종일 어슬렁거렸지."], ["I came up this morning, and have being lingering about all day, without being able to make up my mind to come and see you.", "나는 그 여자가 하자는 대로 다 해 줬고, 그 여자는 제멋대로였고, 하고 싶은 대로 다 했는데, 이제 와서 이런 짓을 한 거야. 찻숟가락 열두 개에 금화로 이십사 파운드가 있었는데, 그게 없어진 걸 제일 먼저 알았지."], ["I humoured her in everything, she had her own way, she did just as she pleased, and now she has done this.", "정말 시련이야. 이제 순회를 돌 때 다시는 문을 두 번 두드릴 기운도 못 낼 것 같아."], ["There was twelve teaspoons and twenty-four pound in sovereigns--I missed them first--it’s a trial--I feel I shall never be able to knock a double knock again, when I go my rounds--don’t say anything more about it, please--the spoons were worth--never mind--never mind!’", "제발 그 얘긴 더 하지 말아 주게. 그 숟가락들이 값이 나가는 거였는데, 아니, 됐네, 됐어!\""]], [["With such muttered outpourings as these, the old gentleman shed a few tears; but, they got him into the elbow-chair, and prevailed upon him, without much pressing, to make a hearty supper, and by the time he had finished his first pipe, and disposed of half-a-dozen glasses out of a crown bowl of punch, ordered by Mr.", "이렇게 중얼중얼 속을 털어놓으며 노신사는 몇 방울 눈물을 흘렸다. 하지만 식구들이 그를 안락의자에 앉히고, 그리 조르지 않아도 저녁을 든든히 들도록 했다."], ["Kenwigs, in celebration of his return to the bosom of his family, he seemed, though still very humble, quite resigned to his fate, and rather relieved than otherwise by the flight of his wife.", "그리고 그가 첫 파이프를 다 피우고, 켄위그스 씨가 그의 귀가를 축하하며 가족의 품으로 돌아온 것을 기려 시킨 큰 펀치 사발에서 여섯 잔쯤 비웠을 무렵에는, 여전히 무척 풀이 죽긴 했어도 제 운명에 꽤 체념한 듯 보였고, 아내가 달아난 것이 오히려 다행스럽다는 기색마저 들었다."]], [["‘When I see that man,’ said Mr. Kenwigs, with one hand round Mrs Kenwigs’s waist: his other hand supporting his pipe (which made him wink and cough very much, for he was no smoker): and his eyes on Morleena, who sat upon her uncle’s knee, ‘when I see that man as mingling, once again, in the spear which he adorns, and see his affections deweloping themselves in legitimate sitiwations, I feel that his nature is as elewated and expanded, as his standing afore society as a public character is unimpeached, and the woices of my infant children purvided for in life, seem to whisper to me softly, “This is an ewent at which Evins itself looks down!”’", "\"저 사람을 보면 말이야.\" 켄위그스 씨가 한 손으로 켄위그스 부인의 허리를 감고, 다른 손으로는 파이프를 받쳐 들고 — 그는 담배를 안 피우는 사람이라 그 탓에 자꾸 눈을 깜빡이고 기침을 해 댔다 — 외삼촌 무릎에 앉은 모를리나에게 눈길을 두며 말했다. \"저 사람이 다시 한번 자기가 빛내던 그 자리에 어울려 들고, 그 정이 떳떳한 자리에서 펼쳐지는 걸 보면, 나는 저 사람의 됨됨이가 그렇게 고매하고 너른 만큼, 공인으로서 사회에 선 그 위치도 흠 하나 없다는 걸 느낍니다. 그리고 앞날을 보장받은 내 어린 자식들의 목소리가 나한테 가만히 속삭이는 것만 같아요. '이건 하늘 자체도 굽어보는 경사로구나!'\""]], [["# CHAPTER 53", "# CHAPTER 53"]], [["Containing the further Progress of the Plot contrived by Mr. Ralph Nickleby and Mr. Arthur Gride", "랄프 니클비 씨와 아서 그라이드 씨가 꾸민 음모가 어떻게 더 진행되는지를 담은 이야기"]], [["With that settled resolution, and steadiness of purpose to which extreme circumstances so often give birth, acting upon far less excitable and more sluggish temperaments than that which was the lot of Madeline Bray’s admirer, Nicholas started, at dawn of day, from the restless couch which no sleep had visited on the previous night, and prepared to make that last appeal, by whose slight and fragile thread her only remaining hope of escape depended.", "극단적인 상황은 흔히 어떤 굳은 결심과 변치 않는 의지를 사람에게 불어넣곤 해. 매들린 브레이를 사모하는 청년보다 훨씬 덜 흥분하고 느긋한 기질을 가진 사람한테도 그런 힘이 작용하지. 니콜라스도 그랬어. 전날 밤 한숨도 자지 못한 채 뒤척이던 잠자리에서 동이 틀 무렵 벌떡 일어났어. 그러고는 마지막 호소를 하러 갈 채비를 했지. 매들린이 빠져나갈 단 하나 남은 희망이, 그 가늘고 약한 실 한 가닥에 매달려 있었거든."]], [["Although, to restless and ardent minds, morning may be the fitting season for exertion and activity, it is not always at that time that hope is strongest or the spirit most sanguine and buoyant.", "쉴 새 없이 움직이고 열정이 가득한 사람한테는 아침이 힘을 쏟고 활동하기에 알맞은 때일지도 몰라. 하지만 희망이 가장 크고 마음이 가장 들뜨고 가벼운 때가 늘 아침은 아니야."], ["In trying and doubtful positions, youth, custom, a steady contemplation of the difficulties which surround us, and a familiarity with them, imperceptibly diminish our apprehensions and beget comparative indifference, if not a vague and reckless confidence in some relief, the means or nature of which we care not to foresee.", "힘들고 막막한 처지에 놓이면, 젊음과 습관, 우리를 둘러싼 어려움을 차분히 들여다보는 일, 그리고 그 어려움에 익숙해지는 일이 우리도 모르는 사이에 두려움을 조금씩 누그러뜨리지. 그러고는 어떤 막연하고 무모한 안도감까지는 아니어도, 그에 가까운 무심함을 만들어 내."], ["But when we come, fresh, upon such things in the morning, with that dark and silent gap between us and yesterday; with every link in the brittle chain of hope, to rivet afresh; our hot enthusiasm subdued, and cool calm reason substituted in its stead; doubt and misgiving revive.", "그 구원이 어떤 방법으로 어떤 모습으로 올지는 굳이 내다보려 하지도 않으면서 말이야. 그런데 아침에 새삼스레 그런 일들과 다시 마주하면, 우리와 어제 사이에 깜깜하고 고요한 틈이 벌어져 있는 거야."], ["As the traveller sees farthest by day, and becomes aware of rugged mountains and trackless plains which the friendly darkness had shrouded from his sight and mind together, so, the wayfarer in the toilsome path of human life sees, with each returning sun, some new obstacle to surmount, some new height to be attained.", "희망이라는 약한 사슬의 고리 하나하나를 다시 단단히 박아야 하고, 뜨거운 열의는 가라앉아 그 자리에 차갑고 침착한 이성이 들어서지. 그러면 의심과 불안이 되살아나."], ["Distances stretch out before him which, last night, were scarcely taken into account, and the light which gilds all nature with its cheerful beams, seems but to shine upon the weary obstacles that yet lie strewn between him and the grave.", "나그네가 낮에 가장 멀리 보고, 다정한 어둠이 눈과 마음에서 함께 가려 주었던 험한 산과 길 없는 들판을 비로소 알아차리는 것처럼, 고단한 인생길을 걷는 사람도 해가 다시 떠오를 때마다 넘어야 할 새 장애물, 올라야 할 새 봉우리를 보게 되는 거야. 어젯밤에는 거의 셈에 넣지도 않았던 먼 거리가 그 앞에 쭉 펼쳐지고, 온 자연을 환한 빛으로 물들이는 햇살마저 그저 그와 무덤 사이에 흩어져 있는 그 지긋지긋한 장애물 위로만 비치는 것 같아."]], [["So thought Nicholas, when, with the impatience natural to a situation like his, he softly left the house, and, feeling as though to remain in bed were to lose most precious time, and to be up and stirring were in some way to promote the end he had in view, wandered into London; perfectly well knowing that for hours to come he could not obtain speech with Madeline, and could do nothing but wish the intervening time away.", "니콜라스도 그렇게 생각했어. 그런 처지에 으레 따르는 조바심에 못 이겨 그는 살그머니 집을 나섰어. 침대에 누워 있는 건 더없이 귀한 시간을 허비하는 일 같고, 일어나서 움직이는 것이 어쩐지 그가 마음먹은 목적을 앞당기는 일 같았지. 그래서 런던 거리를 정처 없이 걸었어. 앞으로 몇 시간 동안은 매들린과 말을 나눌 수 없고, 그저 그 사이 시간이 얼른 지나가기만 바라는 것 말고는 할 일이 없다는 걸 뻔히 알면서도 말이야."]], [["And, even now, as he paced the streets, and listlessly looked round on the gradually increasing bustle and preparation for the day, everything appeared to yield him some new occasion for despondency. Last night, the sacrifice of a young, affectionate, and beautiful creature, to such a wretch, and in such a cause, had seemed a thing too monstrous to succeed; and the warmer he grew, the more confident he felt that some interposition must save her from his clutches. But now, when he thought how regularly things went on, from day to day, in the same unvarying round; how youth and beauty died, and ugly griping age lived tottering on; how crafty avarice grew rich, and manly honest hearts were poor and sad; how few they were who tenanted the stately houses, and how many of those who lay in noisome pens, or rose each day and laid them down each night, and lived and died, father and son, mother and child, race upon race, and generation upon generation, without a home to shelter them or the energies of one single man directed to their aid; how, in seeking, not a luxurious and splendid life, but the bare means of a most wretched and inadequate subsistence, there were women and children in that one town, divided into classes, numbered and estimated as regularly as the noble families and folks of great degree, and reared from infancy to drive most criminal and dreadful trades; how ignorance was punished and never taught; how jail-doors gaped, and gallows loomed, for thousands urged towards them by circumstances darkly curtaining their very cradles’ heads, and but for which they might have earned their honest bread and lived in peace; how many died in soul, and had no chance of life; how many who could scarcely go astray, be they vicious as they would, turned haughtily from the crushed and stricken wretch who could scarce do otherwise, and who would have been a greater wonder had he or she done well, than even they had they done ill; how much injustice, misery, and wrong, there was, and yet how the world rolled on, from year to year, alike careless and indifferent, and no man seeking to remedy or redress it; when he thought of all this, and selected from the mass the one slight case on which his thoughts were bent, he felt, indeed, that there was little ground for hope, and little reason why it should not form an atom in the huge aggregate of distress and sorrow, and add one small and unimportant unit to swell the great amount.", "그리고 지금도 그는 거리를 거닐며 점점 북적이고 하루를 준비하는 풍경을 멍하니 둘러보았어. 그런데 무엇 하나가 죄다 그에게 새로운 절망거리를 안겨 주는 것 같았어. 어젯밤만 해도, 젊고 다정하고 아름다운 사람을 그런 비열한 자에게, 그것도 그런 까닭으로 제물로 바친다는 게 너무나 끔찍해서 도무지 이루어질 수 없는 일처럼 보였어. 그리고 마음이 뜨거워질수록 어떤 일이 가로막고 들어서서 그녀를 그 손아귀에서 구해 낼 거라는 확신이 점점 굳어졌지. 그런데 지금은 이런 생각이 들었어. 세상일이 날마다 얼마나 한결같이 똑같은 굴레 속에서 돌아가는지. 젊음과 아름다움은 죽어 가는데 추하고 인색한 늙은이는 비틀거리며 살아남고, 약삭빠른 탐욕은 부자가 되는데 사내답고 정직한 마음은 가난하고 슬프지. 으리으리한 집에 사는 사람은 얼마나 적고, 또 얼마나 많은 사람들이 악취 나는 우리 같은 곳에 누워 지내는지. 날마다 아침에 일어나 밤에 다시 눕고, 살다 죽기를 아비와 아들, 어미와 자식, 대를 잇고 또 잇건만, 몸을 누일 집 한 칸 없고 단 한 사람도 그들을 도우려 힘쓰지 않지. 호화롭고 멋진 삶이 아니라 그저 가장 비참하고 모자란 입에 풀칠할 수단을 구하는 일조차, 그 한 도시 안에서 여자와 아이들이 계급으로 나뉘어, 귀족 가문이나 지체 높은 사람들만큼이나 꼬박꼬박 셈해지고 헤아려지지. 그러고는 갓난아기 때부터 가장 죄스럽고 무서운 일에 내몰리도록 길러진다는 거야. 무지는 벌을 받을 뿐 가르쳐지지는 않고, 수천 명을 향해 감옥 문이 입을 벌리고 교수대가 어른거리지. 그 사람들은 요람 머리맡부터 어둡게 드리운 사정에 떠밀려 그리로 가지만, 그 사정만 아니었다면 정직하게 밥벌이를 하며 평화롭게 살 수도 있었어. 얼마나 많은 사람이 영혼이 죽은 채로 살 기회조차 얻지 못하는지. 또 마음만 먹으면 얼마든지 악해질 수 있는데도 좀처럼 빗나가지 않을 처지에 있는 사람들이, 도무지 달리 어쩔 수 없어 짓밟히고 망가진 가엾은 자들에게서 거만하게 등을 돌리지. 그런데 그 가엾은 자들이 착하게 산다면, 도리어 그게 더 놀라운 일이야. 처지 좋은 사람들이 나쁘게 사는 것보다 말이야. 세상에 부당함과 비참함과 그름이 얼마나 많은지. 그런데도 세상은 해가 가고 또 가도 한결같이 무심하고 냉담하게 굴러가고, 그것을 바로잡거나 고치려는 사람은 하나도 없지. 이 모든 걸 떠올리다가, 그 무더기 속에서 자기 마음이 매달린 사소한 한 가지 일을 끄집어내자, 니콜라스는 정말로 희망을 걸 만한 구석이 거의 없다고 느꼈어. 그것이 거대한 고통과 슬픔의 더미 속 한 톨이 되어, 그 엄청난 양을 불리는 작고 보잘것없는 하나가 되지 못할 까닭이 없다고 말이야."]], [["But youth is not prone to contemplate the darkest side of a picture it can shift at will.", "하지만 젊음은 마음대로 바꿀 수 있는 그림의 가장 어두운 면만 들여다보고 있지는 않아."], ["By dint of reflecting on what he had to do, and reviving the train of thought which night had interrupted, Nicholas gradually summoned up his utmost energy, and when the morning was sufficiently advanced for his purpose, had no thought but that of using it to the best advantage.", "해야 할 일을 곱씹고, 밤이 끊어 놓았던 생각의 줄기를 다시 되살리면서, 니콜라스는 차츰차츰 있는 힘을 다 끌어모았어. 그리고 아침이 그의 목적에 알맞을 만큼 무르익자, 그는 그 시간을 가장 잘 쓰는 것 말고는 다른 생각이 없었어."], ["A hasty breakfast taken, and such affairs of business as required prompt attention disposed of, he directed his steps to the residence of Madeline Bray: whither he lost no time in arriving.", "서둘러 아침을 먹고, 바로 처리해야 할 일들을 마치고 나서, 그는 매들린 브레이가 사는 집으로 발걸음을 옮겼어. 한시도 지체하지 않고 그곳에 닿았지."]], [["It had occurred to him that, very possibly, the young lady might be denied, although to him she never had been; and he was still pondering upon the surest method of obtaining access to her in that case, when, coming to the door of the house, he found it had been left ajar--probably by the last person who had gone out. The occasion was not one upon which to observe the nicest ceremony; therefore, availing himself of this advantage, Nicholas walked gently upstairs and knocked at the door of the room into which he had been accustomed to be shown. Receiving permission to enter, from some person on the other side, he opened the door and walked in.", "그는 문득 이런 생각이 들었어. 그 아가씨가 면회를 거절당할지도 모른다고 말이야. 자기한테는 한 번도 그런 적이 없었지만. 그래서 그런 경우에 그녀를 만날 가장 확실한 방법이 무엇일까 아직 궁리하고 있었어. 그런데 집 문 앞에 다다라 보니 문이 살짝 열려 있는 거야. 아마 마지막에 나간 사람이 그렇게 두고 간 모양이었지. 그건 지나치게 격식을 차릴 만한 처지가 아니었어. 그래서 니콜라스는 이 기회를 이용해 조용히 위층으로 올라가, 늘 안내받던 그 방의 문을 두드렸어. 안에서 누군가 들어오라고 하자, 그는 문을 열고 들어갔지."]], [["Bray and his daughter were sitting there alone. It was nearly three weeks since he had seen her last, but there was a change in the lovely girl before him which told Nicholas, in startling terms, how much mental suffering had been compressed into that short time. There are no words which can express, nothing with which can be compared, the perfect pallor, the clear transparent whiteness, of the beautiful face which turned towards him when he entered. Her hair was a rich deep brown, but shading that face, and straying upon a neck that rivalled it in whiteness, it seemed by the strong contrast raven black. Something of wildness and restlessness there was in the dark eye, but there was the same patient look, the same expression of gentle mournfulness which he well remembered, and no trace of a single tear. Most beautiful--more beautiful, perhaps, than ever--there was something in her face which quite unmanned him, and appeared far more touching than the wildest agony of grief. It was not merely calm and composed, but fixed and rigid, as though the violent effort which had summoned that composure beneath her father’s eye, while it mastered all other thoughts, had prevented even the momentary expression they had communicated to the features from subsiding, and had fastened it there, as an evidence of its triumph.", "브레이와 그의 딸이 거기 둘이서만 앉아 있었어. 그가 그녀를 마지막으로 본 지 거의 삼 주가 다 되어 갔지. 그런데 그의 앞에 있는 이 사랑스러운 아가씨에게는 어떤 변화가 있었어. 그 짧은 시간 동안 얼마나 큰 마음의 고통이 짓눌려 담겨 있었는지를, 니콜라스에게 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 똑똑히 말해 주는 변화였지. 그가 들어섰을 때 그를 향해 돌린 그 아름다운 얼굴의 완벽한 창백함, 그 맑고 투명한 흰빛은 무슨 말로도 표현할 수 없고, 무엇에도 견줄 수 없었어. 그녀의 머리카락은 짙고 깊은 갈색이었어. 하지만 그 얼굴에 그늘을 드리우고, 그에 못지않게 흰 목덜미 위로 흘러내리니, 그 강한 대비 탓에 까마귀처럼 새까매 보였지. 어두운 눈에는 어딘지 사납고 안절부절못하는 기운이 있었어. 하지만 그가 잘 기억하는 그 참을성 있는 눈빛, 그 부드러운 슬픔의 빛도 똑같이 어려 있었고, 눈물 자국은 한 방울도 없었어. 더없이 아름다웠어. 어쩌면 그 어느 때보다 더 아름다웠지. 그런데 그녀의 얼굴에는 그를 완전히 무너뜨리는 무언가가 있었어. 그건 가장 사나운 슬픔의 몸부림보다 훨씬 더 가슴을 저미는 것 같았지. 그건 그저 차분하고 침착한 게 아니라, 굳어 버린 듯 뻣뻣했어. 아버지의 눈앞에서 그 침착함을 불러내느라 쏟은 격렬한 안간힘이 다른 모든 생각을 억눌렀을 뿐 아니라, 그 안간힘이 얼굴에 잠깐 새겨 넣은 표정마저 가라앉지 못하게 막아, 마치 그 승리의 증거인 양 거기에 못 박아 둔 것 같았어."]], [["The father sat opposite to her; not looking directly in her face, but glancing at her, as he talked with a gay air which ill disguised the anxiety of his thoughts.", "아버지는 그녀의 맞은편에 앉아 있었어. 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 보지는 않고, 명랑한 척하며 이야기하는 사이사이 그녀를 흘끔거렸지."], ["The drawing materials were not on their accustomed table, nor were any of the other tokens of her usual occupations to be seen.", "하지만 그 명랑한 태도는 속마음의 불안을 제대로 감추지 못했어. 그림 도구는 늘 놓이던 탁자 위에 없었고, 그녀가 평소 하던 일을 보여 주는 다른 흔적도 하나 보이지 않았어."], ["The little vases which Nicholas had always seen filled with fresh flowers were empty, or supplied only with a few withered stalks and leaves.", "니콜라스가 늘 싱싱한 꽃으로 가득 차 있는 걸 보던 작은 꽃병들은 텅 비어 있거나, 시든 줄기와 잎사귀 몇 개만 꽂혀 있을 뿐이었어."], ["The bird was silent.", "새는 잠잠했어."], ["The cloth that covered his cage at night was not removed.", "밤에 새장을 덮어 두는 천도 걷히지 않은 채였지."], ["His mistress had forgotten him.", "그 주인이 새를 잊어버린 거야."]], [["There are times when, the mind being painfully alive to receive impressions, a great deal may be noted at a glance.", "마음이 괴롭도록 예민해져 무엇이든 받아들이려 할 때면, 한눈에 많은 것을 알아챌 수 있는 법이지. 지금이 바로 그런 때였어."], ["This was one, for Nicholas had but glanced round him when he was recognised by Mr. Bray, who said impatiently:", "니콜라스가 방 안을 한 번 휙 둘러보기만 했는데, 벌써 브레이 씨가 그를 알아보고는 짜증스럽게 말했거든."]], [["‘Now, sir, what do you want?", "\"자, 무슨 일이오? 여기 온 용건을 얼른 말하시오."], ["Name your errand here, quickly, if you please, for my daughter and I are busily engaged with other and more important matters than those you come about.", "부탁이니. 내 딸과 나는 당신이 온 일보다 훨씬 더 중요한 다른 일로 바쁘니까."], ["Come, sir, address yourself to your business at once.’", "자, 당장 본론으로 들어가시오.\""]], [["Nicholas could very well discern that the irritability and impatience of this speech were assumed, and that Bray, in his heart, was rejoiced at any interruption which promised to engage the attention of his daughter.", "니콜라스는 이 말투에 담긴 짜증과 조바심이 꾸며 낸 것이고, 브레이가 속으로는 딸의 주의를 끌어 줄 만한 일이라면 무엇이든 끼어드는 걸 반긴다는 걸 잘 알아챘어."], ["He bent his eyes involuntarily upon the father as he spoke, and marked his uneasiness; for he coloured and turned his head away.", "그는 말하는 그 아버지에게 저도 모르게 눈길을 던지고는 그의 불안을 눈여겨봤지. 브레이가 얼굴을 붉히며 고개를 돌려 버렸거든."]], [["The device, however, so far as it was a device for causing Madeline to interfere, was successful.", "하지만 그 수작이, 매들린을 끼어들게 하려는 수작인 한에서는 먹혀들었어."], ["She rose, and advancing towards Nicholas paused half-way, and stretched out her hand as expecting a letter.", "그녀가 자리에서 일어나 니콜라스 쪽으로 다가오다 중간에 멈춰 서서, 편지라도 기다리는 듯 손을 내밀었어."]], [["‘Madeline,’ said her father impatiently, ‘my love, what are you doing?’", "\"매들린,\" 아버지가 짜증스럽게 말했어. \"얘야, 뭐 하는 거냐?\""]], [["‘Miss Bray expects an inclosure perhaps,’ said Nicholas, speaking very distinctly, and with an emphasis she could scarcely misunderstand.", "\"브레이 양은 아마 동봉된 게 있나 하고 기다리시는 모양입니다.\" 니콜라스가 아주 또렷하게, 그녀가 좀처럼 못 알아들을 수 없을 만큼 힘주어 말했어."], ["‘My employer is absent from England, or I should have brought a letter with me.", "\"제 고용주가 지금 영국을 떠나 있어서요. 안 그랬으면 편지를 가져왔을 텐데요."], ["I hope she will give me time--a little time.", "시간을 좀 주셨으면 합니다. 아주 조금만요."], ["I ask a very little time.’", "정말 잠깐이면 됩니다.\""]], [["‘If that is all you come about, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"그 일로 온 거라면,\" 브레이 씨가 말했어."], ["Bray, ‘you may make yourself easy on that head.", "\"그 점은 마음 놓아도 되오."], ["Madeline, my dear, I didn’t know this person was in your debt?’", "매들린, 얘야, 이 사람한테 네가 빚진 게 있는 줄은 몰랐구나.\""]], [["‘A--a trifle, I believe,’ returned Madeline, faintly.", "\"네, 그저 좀 사소한 거예요.\" 매들린이 힘없이 대답했어."]], [["‘I suppose you think now,’ said Bray, wheeling his chair round and confronting Nicholas, ‘that, but for such pitiful sums as you bring here, because my daughter has chosen to employ her time as she has, we should starve?’", "\"이제 너는 이렇게 생각하겠지.\" 브레이가 의자를 빙 돌려 니콜라스와 마주 보며 말했어. \"내 딸이 제 시간을 그렇게 쓰기로 한 덕에 네가 여기 가져오는 그 보잘것없는 푼돈이 없으면, 우리가 굶어 죽기라도 할 거라고 말이야?\""]], [["‘I have not thought about it,’ returned Nicholas.", "\"그런 생각은 한 적 없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘You have not thought about it!’ sneered the invalid.", "\"생각한 적이 없다고!\" 병자가 비웃었어."], ["‘You know you HAVE thought about it, and have thought that, and think so every time you come here.", "\"분명 생각했으면서. 그렇게 생각했고, 여기 올 때마다 그렇게 생각하지."], ["Do you suppose, young man, that I don’t know what little purse-proud tradesmen are, when, through some fortunate circumstances, they get the upper hand for a brief day--or think they get the upper hand--of a gentleman?’", "젊은이, 내가 그런 졸렬한 장사치들 속을 모를 줄 알아? 어쩌다 운 좋게 신사보다 잠깐 윗자리에 서게 되면 — 아니면 윗자리에 섰다고 착각하면 — 으스대는 그런 부류 말이야.\""]], [["‘My business,’ said Nicholas respectfully, ‘is with a lady.’", "\"제 용무는,\" 니콜라스가 공손하게 말했어. \"한 숙녀분께 드리는 것입니다.\""]], [["‘With a gentleman’s daughter, sir,’ returned the sick man, ‘and the pettifogging spirit is the same.", "\"신사의 딸한테겠지.\" 병든 자가 받아쳤어."], ["But perhaps you bring ORDERS, eh?", "\"그래도 그 째째한 근성은 매한가지야."], ["Have you any fresh ORDERS for my daughter, sir?’", "그런데 어쩌면 자네는 주문을 가져온 건가, 응? 내 딸한테 새 주문이라도 가져온 거냐고?\""]], [["Nicholas understood the tone of triumph in which this interrogatory was put; but remembering the necessity of supporting his assumed character, produced a scrap of paper purporting to contain a list of some subjects for drawings which his employer desired to have executed; and with which he had prepared himself in case of any such contingency.", "니콜라스는 이 물음이 던져진 의기양양한 말투를 알아챘어. 하지만 자기가 꾸며 낸 신분을 지켜야 한다는 걸 떠올리고는, 종이쪽지 하나를 꺼냈어. 자기 고용주가 그려 달라고 한 그림 소재 몇 가지의 목록인 척하는 종이였지. 혹시나 이런 일이 생길까 봐 미리 준비해 둔 거였어."]], [["‘Oh!’ said Mr. Bray.", "\"오!\""], ["‘These are the orders, are they?’", "브레이 씨가 말했어. \"이게 그 주문이라는 건가?\""]], [["‘Since you insist upon the term, sir, yes,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"굳이 그렇게 부르시겠다면, 네, 그렇습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘Then you may tell your master,’ said Bray, tossing the paper back again, with an exulting smile, ‘that my daughter, Miss Madeline Bray, condescends to employ herself no longer in such labours as these; that she is not at his beck and call, as he supposes her to be; that we don’t live upon his money, as he flatters himself we do; that he may give whatever he owes us, to the first beggar that passes his shop, or add it to his own profits next time he calculates them; and that he may go to the devil for me. That’s my acknowledgment of his orders, sir!’", "\"그럼 자네 주인한테 전하게.\" 브레이가 의기양양한 미소를 지으며 종이를 도로 던지며 말했어. \"내 딸, 매들린 브레이 양은 이제 이런 잡일 따위에 황송하게 제 몸을 쓰지 않겠다고. 그 사람 생각처럼 내 딸이 부르면 달려가는 사람이 아니라고. 우리가 그 사람 돈으로 사는 줄 제 멋대로 우쭐대지만 천만의 말씀이라고. 우리한테 갚을 게 있거든 가게 앞을 지나는 첫 거지한테나 주든지, 아니면 다음번 이익을 셈할 때 제 몫에다 보태라고 말이야. 그리고 나로서는 지옥에나 가라지. 그게 그자의 주문에 대한 내 대답일세!\""]], [["‘And this is the independence of a man who sells his daughter as he has sold that weeping girl!’ thought Nicholas.", "'딸을, 저 울고 있는 아가씨를 팔아넘기는 사내가 떠드는 독립이라는 게 고작 이거로구나!' 니콜라스는 생각했어."]], [["The father was too much absorbed with his own exultation to mark the look of scorn which, for an instant, Nicholas could not have suppressed had he been upon the rack. ‘There,’ he continued, after a short silence, ‘you have your message and can retire--unless you have any further--ha!--any further orders.’", "아버지는 자기 의기양양함에 너무 푹 빠진 나머지, 니콜라스가 한순간 짓고 만 경멸의 표정을 알아채지 못했어. 고문대에 묶였다 해도 억누를 수 없었을 그런 표정을 말이야. \"자,\" 그가 잠깐의 침묵 끝에 말을 이었어. \"전할 말을 들었으니 이제 물러가도 좋네. 더 — 하! — 더 전할 주문이 없다면 말이지.\""]], [["‘I have none,’ said Nicholas; ‘nor, in the consideration of the station you once held, have I used that or any other word which, however harmless in itself, could be supposed to imply authority on my part or dependence on yours. I have no orders, but I have fears--fears that I will express, chafe as you may--fears that you may be consigning that young lady to something worse than supporting you by the labour of her hands, had she worked herself dead. These are my fears, and these fears I found upon your own demeanour. Your conscience will tell you, sir, whether I construe it well or not.’", "\"없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"그리고 한때 당신이 가졌던 지위를 생각해서, 그 말이든 다른 어떤 말이든, 그 자체로는 아무리 거슬릴 게 없다 해도, 제 쪽의 권위나 당신 쪽의 종속을 내비치는 듯한 말은 쓰지 않았습니다. 저한테는 주문이 없습니다. 하지만 두려움이 있습니다. 당신이 아무리 화를 내셔도 말하고야 말 두려움이요. 저 아가씨가 손이 닳도록 일해서 죽도록 당신을 부양하는 것보다 더 못한 무언가에 떠밀리고 있는 게 아닐까 하는 두려움입니다. 이것이 제 두려움이고, 저는 바로 당신 자신의 태도에서 이 두려움을 느낍니다. 제가 그것을 옳게 읽었는지 아닌지는, 당신의 양심이 말해 줄 겁니다.\""]], [["‘For Heaven’s sake!’ cried Madeline, interposing in alarm between them.", "\"제발요!\" 매들린이 깜짝 놀라 둘 사이에 끼어들며 외쳤어."], ["‘Remember, sir, he is ill.’", "\"기억하세요, 이분은 편찮으세요.\""]], [["‘Ill!’ cried the invalid, gasping and catching for breath.", "\"편찮다고!\" 병자가 숨을 헐떡이며 가쁘게 외쳤어."], ["‘Ill! Ill!", "\"편찮다니!"], ["I am bearded and bullied by a shop-boy, and she beseeches him to pity me and remember I am ill!’", "편찮다니! 가게 점원 따위한테 수염을 잡히고 윽박질림을 당하는데, 저 애는 그자한테 나를 불쌍히 여기고 내가 아프단 걸 기억해 달라고 빌고 있구나!\""]], [["He fell into a paroxysm of his disorder, so violent that for a few moments Nicholas was alarmed for his life; but finding that he began to recover, he withdrew, after signifying by a gesture to the young lady that he had something important to communicate, and would wait for her outside the room. He could hear that the sick man came gradually, but slowly, to himself, and that without any reference to what had just occurred, as though he had no distinct recollection of it as yet, he requested to be left alone.", "그는 발작을 일으켰어. 어찌나 심한지 한순간 니콜라스가 그의 목숨이 위태로운가 싶어 겁이 날 정도였지. 하지만 그가 차츰 회복되기 시작하는 걸 보고, 니콜라스는 물러났어. 아가씨에게 손짓으로 중요하게 전할 말이 있다는 것, 방 밖에서 기다리겠다는 것을 알리고 나서 말이야. 병든 자가 차츰, 하지만 더디게 정신을 차리는 소리가 들렸어. 그리고 방금 있었던 일은 한마디도 입에 올리지 않은 채, 마치 아직 그 일을 또렷이 기억하지 못하는 듯, 혼자 있게 해 달라고 청하는 소리도 들렸지."]], [["‘Oh!’ thought Nicholas, ‘that this slender chance might not be lost, and that I might prevail, if it were but for one week’s time and reconsideration!’", "'아!' 니콜라스는 생각했어. '이 가느다란 기회가 사라지지 않기를, 단 한 주의 시간과 다시 생각할 틈만이라도 얻어 낼 수 있다면!'"]], [["‘You are charged with some commission to me, sir,’ said Madeline, presenting herself in great agitation.", "\"제게 무슨 전할 말씀이 있으시다고요.\" 매들린이 몹시 떨면서 모습을 드러내며 말했어."], ["‘Do not press it now, I beg and pray you.", "\"지금은 그 말씀을 강요하지 말아 주세요, 빌고 또 비옵니다."], ["The day after tomorrow; come here then.’", "모레 — 그때 여기로 와 주세요.\""]], [["‘It will be too late--too late for what I have to say,’ rejoined Nicholas, ‘and you will not be here. Oh, madam, if you have but one thought of him who sent me here, but one last lingering care for your own peace of mind and heart, I do for God’s sake urge you to give me a hearing.’", "\"그땐 너무 늦습니다. 제가 드릴 말씀을 하기엔 너무 늦어요.\" 니콜라스가 받아 말했어. \"그리고 그땐 당신이 여기 안 계실 거고요. 오, 아가씨. 저를 여기 보낸 그분을 단 한 번이라도 생각해 보신다면, 당신 자신의 마음의 평화를 위한 마지막 미련이 단 한 가닥이라도 남아 있다면, 제발, 부디 제 말을 한 번 들어주십시오.\""]], [["She attempted to pass him, but Nicholas gently detained her.", "그녀가 그를 지나치려 했지만, 니콜라스는 부드럽게 그녀를 붙들었어."]], [["‘A hearing,’ said Nicholas.", "\"들어주세요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘I ask you but to hear me: not me alone, but him for whom I speak, who is far away and does not know your danger.", "\"그저 제 말을 들어주시기만을 청합니다. 저뿐만 아니라, 제가 대신해 말하는 그분을 위해서요."], ["In the name of Heaven hear me!’", "그분은 멀리 떨어져 계셔서 당신의 위험을 모르십니다. 하느님의 이름으로 제 말을 들어주세요!\""]], [["The poor attendant, with her eyes swollen and red with weeping, stood by; and to her Nicholas appealed in such passionate terms that she opened a side-door, and, supporting her mistress into an adjoining room, beckoned Nicholas to follow them.", "울다 지쳐 눈이 붓고 빨개진 가엾은 시중꾼이 곁에 서 있었어. 니콜라스가 어찌나 간절한 말로 그녀에게 호소하던지, 그녀는 옆문을 열고 주인 아가씨를 부축해 옆방으로 데려가더니, 니콜라스에게 따라 들어오라고 손짓했지."]], [["‘Leave me, sir, pray,’ said the young lady.", "\"저를 두고 가 주세요, 부탁이에요.\" 아가씨가 말했어."]], [["‘I cannot, will not leave you thus,’ returned Nicholas.", "\"이대로는 당신을 두고 갈 수 없습니다. 가지 않겠습니다.\""], ["‘I have a duty to discharge; and, either here, or in the room from which we have just now come, at whatever risk or hazard to Mr.", "니콜라스가 대답했어. \"저는 다해야 할 도리가 있습니다."], ["Bray, I must beseech you to contemplate again the fearful course to which you have been impelled.’", "그러니 여기서든, 방금 우리가 나온 그 방에서든, 브레이 씨에게 어떤 위험이나 화가 닥치든, 저는 당신께 다시 한번 간곡히 빌어야겠습니다. 당신이 떠밀려 온 그 무서운 길을 한 번만 더 헤아려 봐 달라고요.\""]], [["‘What course is this you speak of, and impelled by whom, sir?’ demanded the young lady, with an effort to speak proudly.", "\"당신이 말하는 그 길이 무엇이며, 누가 저를 떠밀었다는 거죠?\" 아가씨가 도도하게 말하려 애쓰며 물었어."]], [["‘I speak of this marriage,’ returned Nicholas, ‘of this marriage, fixed for tomorrow, by one who never faltered in a bad purpose, or lent his aid to any good design; of this marriage, the history of which is known to me, better, far better, than it is to you. I know what web is wound about you. I know what men they are from whom these schemes have come. You are betrayed and sold for money; for gold, whose every coin is rusted with tears, if not red with the blood of ruined men, who have fallen desperately by their own mad hands.’", "\"저는 이 결혼을 말하는 겁니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"내일로 정해진 이 결혼을요. 나쁜 목적이라면 한 번도 머뭇거린 적 없고, 좋은 일에는 손 한 번 보태 준 적 없는 자가 꾸민 결혼 말입니다. 그 내막을 저는 당신보다 훨씬, 훨씬 더 잘 알고 있는 이 결혼을요. 어떤 그물이 당신을 휘감고 있는지 저는 압니다. 이 음모를 꾸민 자들이 어떤 인간들인지도 알아요. 당신은 돈에 배신당하고 팔려 가는 겁니다. 동전 한 닢 한 닢이 눈물로 녹슬어 있는, 아니 제 미친 손으로 절망에 빠져 스러진 망한 이들의 피로 시뻘건 그 황금에 말입니다.\""]], [["‘You say you have a duty to discharge,’ said Madeline, ‘and so have I.", "\"당신은 다해야 할 도리가 있다고 했지요.\" 매들린이 말했어."], ["And with the help of Heaven I will perform it.’", "\"저에게도 있습니다. 그리고 하늘의 도움으로 저는 그것을 다할 거예요.\""]], [["‘Say rather with the help of devils,’ replied Nicholas, ‘with the help of men, one of them your destined husband, who are--’", "\"차라리 악마의 도움이라고 하시지요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어. \"인간들의 도움이라고요. 그중 하나가 당신의 정해진 남편이고, 그자들은—\""]], [["‘I must not hear this,’ cried the young lady, striving to repress a shudder, occasioned, as it seemed, even by this slight allusion to Arthur Gride.", "\"이건 듣지 않겠어요.\" 아가씨가 외쳤어."], ["‘This evil, if evil it be, has been of my own seeking.", "아서 그라이드를 슬쩍 언급한 것만으로도 솟아오르는 듯한 몸서리를 억누르려 애쓰면서 말이야. \"이 불행이, 그게 불행이라면, 제가 스스로 택한 거예요."], ["I am impelled to this course by no one, but follow it of my own free will.", "저는 누구한테도 이 길로 떠밀린 게 아니라, 제 자유의지로 따라가는 거예요. 보세요, 저는 강요받지도 떠밀리지도 않았어요."], ["You see I am not constrained or forced.", "이걸 전해 주세요.\" 매들린이 말했어."], ["Report this,’ said Madeline, ‘to my dear friend and benefactor, and, taking with you my prayers and thanks for him and for yourself, leave me for ever!’", "\"제 소중한 벗이자 은인께요. 그리고 그분과 당신을 위한 제 기도와 감사를 가지고, 영영 저를 떠나 주세요!\""]], [["‘Not until I have besought you, with all the earnestness and fervour by which I am animated,’ cried Nicholas, ‘to postpone this marriage for one short week.", "\"저를 채우고 있는 이 온 마음의 진심과 열정을 다해 당신께 빌기 전에는 그럴 수 없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어."], ["Not until I have besought you to think more deeply than you can have done, influenced as you are, upon the step you are about to take.", "\"이 결혼을 단 한 주, 짧은 한 주만 미뤄 달라고 빌기 전에는요. 지금처럼 휘둘리는 채로가 아니라, 당신이 내디디려는 이 걸음을 지금껏 할 수 있었던 것보다 더 깊이 생각해 달라고 빌기 전에는요."], ["Although you cannot be fully conscious of the villainy of this man to whom you are about to give your hand, some of his deeds you know.", "당신이 손을 내주려는 이 사내의 악랄함을 다 알지는 못하시겠지만, 그자가 한 짓 몇 가지는 아시잖아요. 그자가 말하는 걸 들으셨고, 그 얼굴을 보셨잖아요."], ["You have heard him speak, and have looked upon his face.", "늦기 전에 헤아려 보세요, 헤아려 보시라고요. 마음으로는 도무지 함께할 수 없는 정절을 제단 앞에서 그자에게 맹세하는 그 우롱을요."], ["Reflect, reflect, before it is too late, on the mockery of plighting to him at the altar, faith in which your heart can have no share--of uttering solemn words, against which nature and reason must rebel--of the degradation of yourself in your own esteem, which must ensue, and must be aggravated every day, as his detested character opens upon you more and more.", "본성과 이성이 거스를 수밖에 없는 엄숙한 말을 입에 담는 일을요. 그자의 그 역겨운 인간됨이 날마다 점점 더 드러날수록, 당신 스스로 보기에 자신이 천해지는 그 일이 따라올 수밖에 없고, 또 날마다 더 심해질 수밖에 없다는 걸요."], ["Shrink from the loathsome companionship of this wretch as you would from corruption and disease.", "이 비열한 자와 함께하는 끔찍한 동거를, 썩음과 병을 피하듯 피하세요."], ["Suffer toil and labour if you will, but shun him, shun him, and be happy.", "정 그래야 한다면 수고와 고생을 견디세요. 하지만 그자만은 피하세요, 피하시고 행복하세요."], ["For, believe me, I speak the truth; the most abject poverty, the most wretched condition of human life, with a pure and upright mind, would be happiness to that which you must undergo as the wife of such a man as this!’", "제 말을 믿으세요, 저는 진실을 말하고 있습니다. 가장 비참한 가난, 가장 처참한 처지의 삶이라도, 깨끗하고 올곧은 마음만 있다면, 이런 사내의 아내로 겪어야 할 그것에 비하면 행복일 거예요!\""]], [["Long before Nicholas ceased to speak, the young lady buried her face in her hands, and gave her tears free way.", "니콜라스가 말을 마치기 한참 전부터, 아가씨는 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 눈물을 마음껏 쏟아 냈어."], ["In a voice at first inarticulate with emotion, but gradually recovering strength as she proceeded, she answered him:", "처음에는 북받치는 감정에 말이 제대로 나오지 않았지만, 말을 이어 가면서 차츰 목소리에 힘을 되찾은 그녀가 그에게 대답했어."]], [["‘I will not disguise from you, sir--though perhaps I ought--that I have undergone great pain of mind, and have been nearly broken-hearted since I saw you last.", "\"당신께 숨기지 않을게요. 어쩌면 숨겨야 할지도 모르지만요."], ["I do NOT love this gentleman.", "지난번 당신을 본 뒤로 저는 마음이 몹시 아팠고, 가슴이 거의 무너질 뻔했어요. 저는 이분을 사랑하지 않아요."], ["The difference between our ages, tastes, and habits, forbids it.", "우리 사이의 나이며 취향이며 습관의 차이가 그걸 가로막죠. 이건 그분도 알아요."], ["This he knows, and knowing, still offers me his hand.", "그러면서도 제게 손을 내밀어요. 그 손을 받아들이는 것, 오직 그 한 걸음만이, 이곳에서 죽어 가는 제 아버지를 풀려나게 할 수 있어요."], ["By accepting it, and by that step alone, I can release my father who is dying in this place; prolong his life, perhaps, for many years; restore him to comfort--I may almost call it affluence; and relieve a generous man from the burden of assisting one, by whom, I grieve to say, his noble heart is little understood.", "어쩌면 아버지의 목숨을 여러 해 늘려 드릴 수도 있고요. 아버지를 다시 편안하게 — 거의 풍족하다고 해도 좋을 만큼 — 해 드릴 수 있어요."], ["Do not think so poorly of me as to believe that I feign a love I do not feel.", "그리고 한 너그러운 분을, 슬프게도 그 고귀한 마음을 거의 알아주지 못하는 사람을 돕는 짐에서 풀어 드릴 수 있어요. 저를 그렇게 못나게 여기지 말아 주세요."], ["Do not report so ill of me, for THAT I could not bear.", "느끼지도 않는 사랑을 꾸며 낸다고는 믿지 말아 주세요. 저에 대해 그렇게 나쁘게 전하지 말아 주세요."], ["If I cannot, in reason or in nature, love the man who pays this price for my poor hand, I can discharge the duties of a wife: I can be all he seeks in me, and will.", "그건 제가 견딜 수 없으니까요. 이 가엾은 손을 위해 이런 대가를 치르는 사람을 이성으로든 본성으로든 사랑할 수 없다 해도, 저는 아내의 도리는 다할 수 있어요."], ["He is content to take me as I am.", "그가 제게 바라는 모든 것이 될 수 있고, 또 되겠어요."], ["I have passed my word, and should rejoice, not weep, that it is so.", "그는 저를 있는 그대로 받아들이는 데 만족해요. 저는 약속을 했고, 그러니 기뻐해야지 울어선 안 되겠죠."], ["I do.", "정말 그래요."], ["The interest you take in one so friendless and forlorn as I, the delicacy with which you have discharged your trust, the faith you have kept with me, have my warmest thanks: and, while I make this last feeble acknowledgment, move me to tears, as you see.", "저처럼 벗도 없고 의지할 데 없는 사람에게 당신이 보여 준 관심, 당신이 맡은 일을 다해 낸 그 섬세함, 저와 지켜 준 그 신의에 진심으로 감사드려요. 그리고 이렇게 마지막으로 변변찮은 감사를 드리면서, 보시다시피 저는 눈물이 나네요."], ["But I do not repent, nor am I unhappy.", "하지만 저는 후회하지 않아요. 불행하지도 않고요."], ["I am happy in the prospect of all I can achieve so easily.", "이렇게 쉽게 이뤄 낼 수 있는 모든 것을 생각하면 저는 행복해요."], ["I shall be more so when I look back upon it, and all is done, I know.’", "다 끝나고 나서 돌이켜 볼 때면 더 행복할 거예요. 알아요.\""]], [["‘Your tears fall faster as you talk of happiness,’ said Nicholas, ‘and you shun the contemplation of that dark future which must be laden with so much misery to you.", "\"행복을 말하면서도 당신의 눈물은 더 빨리 흐르는군요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["Defer this marriage for a week.", "\"그리고 당신은 그 어두운 앞날을 들여다보길 피하고 있어요. 당신에게 그토록 많은 비참함을 안겨 줄 게 분명한 그 앞날을요."], ["For but one week!’", "이 결혼을 한 주만 미루세요. 단 한 주만요!\""]], [["‘He was talking, when you came upon us just now, with such smiles as I remember to have seen of old, and have not seen for many and many a day, of the freedom that was to come tomorrow,’ said Madeline, with momentary firmness, ‘of the welcome change, the fresh air: all the new scenes and objects that would bring fresh life to his exhausted frame. His eye grew bright, and his face lightened at the thought. I will not defer it for an hour.’", "\"방금 당신이 우리한테 들어왔을 때, 아버지는 이야기를 하고 계셨어요. 제가 옛날에 보던, 그러고는 정말 오래오래 보지 못했던 그런 미소를 띠고서요. 내일이면 찾아올 자유에 대해서요.\" 매들린이 한순간 굳센 목소리로 말했어. \"반가운 변화, 신선한 공기, 지친 몸에 새 생기를 불어넣어 줄 온갖 새로운 풍경과 것들에 대해서요. 그 생각에 아버지 눈이 반짝였고 얼굴이 환해졌어요. 저는 단 한 시간도 미루지 않겠어요.\""]], [["‘These are but tricks and wiles to urge you on,’ cried Nicholas.", "\"그건 당신을 떠밀려는 속임수와 꼼수일 뿐이에요.\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어."]], [["‘I’ll hear no more,’ said Madeline, hurriedly; ‘I have heard too much--more than I should--already.", "\"더는 듣지 않겠어요.\" 매들린이 황급히 말했어."], ["What I have said to you, sir, I have said as to that dear friend to whom I trust in you honourably to repeat it.", "\"이미 너무 많이 들었는걸요. 들어야 할 것보다 더 많이요."], ["Some time hence, when I am more composed and reconciled to my new mode of life, if I should live so long, I will write to him.", "제가 당신께 드린 말씀은, 당신이 정직하게 전해 주리라 믿는 그 소중한 벗께 드린 거예요. 얼마쯤 지나서, 제가 좀 더 마음이 가라앉고 새 삶에 익숙해지면, 그때까지 제가 산다면, 그분께 편지를 쓸게요."], ["Meantime, all holy angels shower blessings on his head, and prosper and preserve him.’", "그동안 온 거룩한 천사들이 그분의 머리 위에 축복을 쏟아붓고, 그분을 번성하게 하고 지켜 주기를 빌어요.\""]], [["She was hurrying past Nicholas, when he threw himself before her, and implored her to think, but once again, upon the fate to which she was precipitately hastening.", "그녀가 니콜라스를 지나쳐 급히 가려는데, 그가 그녀 앞에 몸을 던지며 그녀가 무모하게 서둘러 달려가는 그 운명을 단 한 번만 더 생각해 달라고 애원했어."]], [["‘There is no retreat,’ said Nicholas, in an agony of supplication; ‘no withdrawing!", "\"물러설 길이 없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 애원에 몸부림치며 말했어."], ["All regret will be unavailing, and deep and bitter it must be.", "\"발을 뺄 수도 없어요! 모든 후회가 다 부질없을 거고, 그 후회는 깊고 쓰라릴 게 분명합니다."], ["What can I say, that will induce you to pause at this last moment?", "이 마지막 순간에 당신을 멈추게 하려면 제가 무슨 말을 해야 할까요?"], ["What can I do to save you?’", "당신을 구하려면 제가 무엇을 할 수 있을까요?\""]], [["‘Nothing,’ she incoherently replied.", "\"아무것도요.\" 그녀가 두서없이 대답했어."], ["‘This is the hardest trial I have had.", "\"이건 제가 겪은 가장 힘든 시련이에요."], ["Have mercy on me, sir, I beseech, and do not pierce my heart with such appeals as these.", "제발 저를 가엾이 여겨 주세요, 빕니다. 이런 호소로 제 가슴을 찌르지 말아 주세요."], ["I--I hear him calling.", "저— 저 아버지가 부르시는 소리가 들려요."], ["I--I--must not, will not, remain here for another instant.’", "저— 저는 단 한순간도 더 여기 있어선 안 되고, 있지 않겠어요.\""]], [["‘If this were a plot,’ said Nicholas, with the same violent rapidity with which she spoke, ‘a plot, not yet laid bare by me, but which, with time, I might unravel; if you were (not knowing it) entitled to fortune of your own, which, being recovered, would do all that this marriage can accomplish, would you not retract?’", "\"만약 이것이 음모라면,\" 니콜라스가 그녀가 말하던 것과 똑같이 격하고 빠른 어조로 말했어. \"제가 아직 밝혀내진 못했지만 시간이 있으면 풀어낼 수도 있는 음모라면요. 만약 당신이 (모르고 계시지만) 당신 몫의 재산을 가질 권리가 있고, 그걸 되찾으면 이 결혼이 이뤄 낼 모든 걸 다 할 수 있다면, 당신은 물러서지 않으시겠어요?\""]], [["‘No, no, no!", "\"아니에요, 아니에요, 아니에요!"], ["It is impossible; it is a child’s tale.", "그건 불가능해요. 그건 어린애 이야기일 뿐이에요."], ["Time would bring his death.", "시간이 흐르면 아버지가 돌아가실 거예요."], ["He is calling again!’", "또 부르시잖아요!\""]], [["‘It may be the last time we shall ever meet on earth,’ said Nicholas, ‘it may be better for me that we should never meet more.’", "\"이게 우리가 이 세상에서 만나는 마지막일지도 모릅니다.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"어쩌면 우리가 두 번 다시 만나지 않는 편이 제게는 나을지도 모르지요.\""]], [["‘For both, for both,’ replied Madeline, not heeding what she said.", "\"우리 둘 다에게요, 둘 다에게요.\" 매들린이 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모른 채 대답했어."], ["‘The time will come when to recall the memory of this one interview might drive me mad.", "\"이 한 번의 만남을 떠올리는 것만으로 제가 미쳐 버릴 것 같은 때가 올 거예요."], ["Be sure to tell them, that you left me calm and happy.", "꼭 전해 주세요. 저를 차분하고 행복한 채로 두고 왔다고요."], ["And God be with you, sir, and my grateful heart and blessing!’", "그리고 하느님이 당신과 함께하시길, 제 감사한 마음과 축복도요!\""]], [["She was gone.", "그녀는 가 버렸어."], ["Nicholas, staggering from the house, thought of the hurried scene which had just closed upon him, as if it were the phantom of some wild, unquiet dream.", "니콜라스는 비틀거리며 집을 나서면서, 방금 자기 앞에서 막을 내린 그 다급한 장면을 떠올렸어. 마치 어떤 거칠고 어수선한 꿈의 허깨비 같았지."], ["The day wore on; at night, having been enabled in some measure to collect his thoughts, he issued forth again.", "하루가 지나갔어. 밤이 되어, 어느 정도 생각을 추스를 수 있게 되자, 그는 다시 밖으로 나섰어."]], [["That night, being the last of Arthur Gride’s bachelorship, found him in tiptop spirits and great glee.", "그날 밤은 아서 그라이드의 총각 신세 마지막 밤이었는데, 그는 더없이 들떠서 무척 신이 나 있었어. 병 같은 초록빛 양복은 솔질이 되어 내일을 위해 준비되어 있었지."], ["The bottle-green suit had been brushed, ready for the morrow.", "페그 슬라이더스큐는 지난 살림살이의 계산을 내놓았어. 일 실링 육 펜스가 어김없이 다 셈해졌지(그녀한테는 한 번에 그보다 큰돈을 맡긴 적이 없었고, 계산은 보통 하루에 두 번 넘게 맞춰 보는 일이 없었거든)."], ["Peg Sliderskew had rendered the accounts of her past housekeeping; the eighteen-pence had been rigidly accounted for (she was never trusted with a larger sum at once, and the accounts were not usually balanced more than twice a day); every preparation had been made for the coming festival; and Arthur might have sat down and contemplated his approaching happiness, but that he preferred sitting down and contemplating the entries in a dirty old vellum-book with rusty clasps.", "다가올 잔치를 위한 채비는 다 되어 있었어. 그래서 아서는 앉아서 다가오는 행복을 그려 보고 있어도 좋았을 텐데, 그보다는 녹슨 걸쇠가 달린 더럽고 오래된 양피지 장부의 적힌 항목들을 들여다보며 앉아 있는 걸 더 좋아했어."]], [["‘Well-a-day!’ he chuckled, as sinking on his knees before a strong chest screwed down to the floor, he thrust in his arm nearly up to the shoulder, and slowly drew forth this greasy volume.", "\"아이고 좋다!\" 그가 키득거렸어."], ["‘Well-a-day now, this is all my library, but it’s one of the most entertaining books that were ever written!", "바닥에 나사로 단단히 박아 둔 튼튼한 궤짝 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 거의 어깨까지 팔을 쑤셔 넣어 이 기름때 낀 책을 천천히 끄집어내면서 말이야."], ["It’s a delightful book, and all true and real--that’s the best of it--true as the Bank of England, and real as its gold and silver.", "\"아이고, 이게 내 서재 전부지만, 여태껏 쓰인 책 중에 가장 재미있는 것 중 하나지! 신나는 책이고, 죄다 진짜고 사실이야 — 그게 제일 좋은 점이지 — 잉글랜드 은행만큼 참되고, 거기 금은만큼 진짜라고."], ["Written by Arthur Gride.", "아서 그라이드가 썼지."], ["He, he, he!", "히, 히, 히!"], ["None of your storybook writers will ever make as good a book as this, I warrant me.", "너희 이야기책 나부랭이를 쓰는 작자들은 절대 이만큼 좋은 책은 못 만들어, 내가 장담하지."], ["It’s composed for private circulation, for my own particular reading, and nobody else’s.", "이건 사사로이 돌려 보려고, 오직 나 혼자만 읽으려고 지은 거고, 다른 누구도 아니야."], ["He, he, he!’", "히, 히, 히!\""]], [["Muttering this soliloquy, Arthur carried his precious volume to the table, and, adjusting it upon a dusty desk, put on his spectacles, and began to pore among the leaves.", "이렇게 혼잣말을 중얼거리며 아서는 그 귀한 책을 탁자로 가져갔어. 그러고는 먼지 낀 책상에 책을 맞춰 놓고, 안경을 쓰고, 책장을 뚫어져라 들여다보기 시작했지."]], [["‘It’s a large sum to Mr.", "\"니클비 씨한테 갈 게 큰돈이군.\""], ["Nickleby,’ he said, in a dolorous voice.", "그가 구슬픈 목소리로 말했어."], ["‘Debt to be paid in full, nine hundred and seventy-five, four, three.", "\"온전히 갚을 빚이 구백칠십오 파운드, 사 실링, 삼 펜스."], ["Additional sum as per bond, five hundred pound.", "증서에 따라 더 줄 돈이 오백 파운드."], ["One thousand, four hundred and seventy-five pounds, four shillings, and threepence, tomorrow at twelve o’clock.", "합쳐서 천사백칠십오 파운드, 사 실링, 삼 펜스를, 내일 열두 시에 말이지."], ["On the other side, though, there’s the PER CONTRA, by means of this pretty chick.", "그래도 반대쪽엔, 이 예쁜 병아리 덕에 차감될 게 있지. 그런데 또 이런 의문이 들어."], ["But, again, there’s the question whether I mightn’t have brought all this about, myself.", "이 모든 걸 나 혼자서 해낼 수도 있지 않았을까 하는 거지. '겁 많은 가슴은 미인을 얻은 적이 없다.'"], ["“Faint heart never won fair lady.”", "내 가슴은 왜 그리 겁이 많았을까?"], ["Why was my heart so faint? Why didn’t I boldly open it to Bray myself, and save one thousand four hundred and seventy-five, four, three?’", "어째서 내가 직접 브레이한테 대담하게 속을 털어놓고, 천사백칠십오 파운드, 사 실링, 삼 펜스를 아끼지 못했을까?\""]], [["These reflections depressed the old usurer so much, as to wring a feeble groan or two from his breast, and cause him to declare, with uplifted hands, that he would die in a workhouse. Remembering on further cogitation, however, that under any circumstances he must have paid, or handsomely compounded for, Ralph’s debt, and being by no means confident that he would have succeeded had he undertaken his enterprise alone, he regained his equanimity, and chattered and mowed over more satisfactory items, until the entrance of Peg Sliderskew interrupted him.", "이런 생각들이 늙은 고리대금업자를 어찌나 풀이 죽게 했던지, 가슴에서 가냘픈 신음이 한두 번 새어 나왔고, 그는 두 손을 치켜들며 자기가 구빈원에서 죽고 말 거라고 내뱉었어. 하지만 더 곰곰이 따져 보니, 어떤 경우든 랄프의 빚은 자기가 갚거나 그럴듯하게 셈을 맞춰 줘야 했을 거라는 걸 떠올렸어. 게다가 이 일을 혼자 떠맡았다면 과연 성공했을지 도무지 자신이 없었지. 그래서 그는 다시 마음의 평정을 되찾고, 더 흐뭇한 항목들을 두고 재잘대며 입을 우물거렸어. 페그 슬라이더스큐가 들어오는 바람에 멈추기 전까지 말이야."]], [["‘Aha, Peg!’ said Arthur, ‘what is it?", "\"오, 페그!\" 아서가 말했어."], ["What is it now, Peg?’", "\"뭔가? 무슨 일이야, 페그?\""]], [["‘It’s the fowl,’ replied Peg, holding up a plate containing a little, a very little one.", "\"닭이에요.\" 페그가 작은, 아주 작은 닭 한 마리가 담긴 접시를 들어 보이며 대답했어."], ["Quite a phenomenon of a fowl.", "그야말로 닭의 별종이었지."], ["So very small and skinny.", "어찌나 작고 비쩍 말랐던지."]], [["‘A beautiful bird!’ said Arthur, after inquiring the price, and finding it proportionate to the size.", "\"아름다운 새로구나!\" 아서가 값을 물어보고는 그 크기에 걸맞다는 걸 알고 나서 말했어."], ["‘With a rasher of ham, and an egg made into sauce, and potatoes, and greens, and an apple pudding, Peg, and a little bit of cheese, we shall have a dinner for an emperor.", "\"베이컨 한 점에다, 달걀을 깨 넣은 소스에다, 감자에, 채소에, 사과 푸딩에, 페그, 그리고 치즈 조금이면, 우리는 황제 같은 만찬을 먹는 거지."], ["There’ll only be she and me--and you, Peg, when we’ve done.’", "그 애랑 나밖에 없을 거야 — 그리고 우리가 다 먹고 나면 너도, 페그.\""]], [["‘Don’t you complain of the expense afterwards,’ said Mrs.", "\"나중에 가서 돈 들었다고 투덜대지나 마세요.\""], ["Sliderskew, sulkily.", "슬라이더스큐 부인이 뚱하게 말했어."]], [["‘I am afraid we must live expensively for the first week,’ returned Arthur, with a groan, ‘and then we must make up for it. I won’t eat more than I can help, and I know you love your old master too much to eat more than YOU can help, don’t you, Peg?’", "\"처음 한 주는 아무래도 돈을 좀 써야겠지.\" 아서가 끙 앓는 소리를 내며 대답했어. \"그러고 나선 그걸 메워야 해. 나는 어쩔 수 없는 것보다 더 먹진 않을 거야. 그리고 너도 네 늙은 주인을 너무 사랑해서, 어쩔 수 없는 것보다 더 먹진 않을 거지, 안 그러냐, 페그?\""]], [["‘Don’t I what?’ said Peg.", "\"내가 뭘 안 한다고요?\" 페그가 말했어."]], [["‘Love your old master too much--’", "\"네 늙은 주인을 너무 사랑해서—\""]], [["‘No, not a bit too much,’ said Peg.", "\"아뇨, 손톱만큼도 너무는 아니에요.\" 페그가 말했어."]], [["‘Oh, dear, I wish the devil had this woman!’ cried Arthur: ‘love him too much to eat more than you can help at his expense.’", "\"오, 이런, 악마가 이 여편네를 데려가 버렸으면!\" 아서가 외쳤어. \"주인을 너무 사랑해서 그 주인 돈으로 어쩔 수 없는 것보다 더 먹진 않을 거란 말이야.\""]], [["‘At his what?’ said Peg.", "\"그 주인 뭐로요?\" 페그가 말했어."]], [["‘Oh dear!", "\"오, 이런!"], ["she can never hear the most important word, and hears all the others!’ whined Gride.", "가장 중요한 말은 절대 못 듣고, 다른 건 죄다 듣는다니까!\" 그라이드가 칭얼대듯 말했어."], ["‘At his expense--you catamaran!’", "\"주인 돈으로 말이야 — 이 못된 할망구야!\""]], [["The last-mentioned tribute to the charms of Mrs.", "슬라이더스큐 부인의 매력에 바치는 이 마지막 찬사는 속삭임으로 내뱉어졌으니, 그 부인은 거친 으르렁 소리로 그 일반적인 주장에 동의했어."], ["Sliderskew being uttered in a whisper, that lady assented to the general proposition by a harsh growl, which was accompanied by a ring at the street-door.", "그러자 때맞춰 현관문에서 종소리가 울렸지."]], [["‘There’s the bell,’ said Arthur.", "\"종이 울리네.\" 아서가 말했어."]], [["‘Ay, ay; I know that,’ rejoined Peg.", "\"네, 네. 저도 알아요.\" 페그가 받아쳤어."]], [["‘Then why don’t you go?’ bawled Arthur.", "\"그럼 왜 안 나가?\" 아서가 버럭 소리쳤어."]], [["‘Go where?’ retorted Peg.", "\"어디를 나가요?\" 페그가 되받았어."], ["‘I ain’t doing any harm here, am I?’", "\"저 여기서 나쁜 짓 하나도 안 하잖아요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["Arthur Gride in reply repeated the word ‘bell’ as loud as he could roar; and, his meaning being rendered further intelligible to Mrs.", "이에 아서 그라이드는 있는 힘껏 으르렁대며 '종' 소리라는 말을 되풀이했어."], ["Sliderskew’s dull sense of hearing by pantomime expressive of ringing at a street-door, Peg hobbled out, after sharply demanding why he hadn’t said there was a ring before, instead of talking about all manner of things that had nothing to do with it, and keeping her half-pint of beer waiting on the steps.", "그리고 현관문에서 종을 당기는 시늉의 손짓으로 그 뜻을 슬라이더스큐 부인의 둔한 귀에 더 알아듣게 전하자, 페그는 절뚝이며 나갔어. 그러면서 종이 울렸다고 진작 말하지 않고 그것과 아무 상관도 없는 온갖 잡소리만 늘어놓아, 자기가 마실 맥주 반 파인트를 계단에 기다리게 만들었다고 쏘아붙이고 나서 말이야."]], [["‘There’s a change come over you, Mrs.", "\"자네한테 뭔가 변한 게 있어, 페그 부인.\""], ["Peg,’ said Arthur, following her out with his eyes.", "아서가 그녀가 나가는 걸 눈으로 좇으며 말했어."], ["‘What it means I don’t quite know; but, if it lasts, we shan’t agree together long I see.", "\"그게 뭔지는 잘 모르겠지만, 이게 계속되면 우리가 오래 사이좋게 지내진 못하겠는데. 자네 머리가 돌아가는 모양이야, 내 보기엔."], ["You are turning crazy, I think. If you are, you must take yourself off, Mrs.", "정말 그렇다면 자네는 알아서 나가야 해, 페그 부인 — 아니면 끌려 나가든지."], ["Peg--or be taken off.", "나야 어느 쪽이든 마찬가지지.\""], ["All’s one to me.’ Turning over the leaves of his book as he muttered this, he soon lighted upon something which attracted his attention, and forgot Peg Sliderskew and everything else in the engrossing interest of its pages.", "이렇게 중얼거리며 책장을 넘기던 그는, 곧 자기 눈길을 사로잡는 무언가에 닿았어. 그러고는 그 책장의 빠져드는 흥미에 페그 슬라이더스큐고 다른 무엇이고 죄다 잊어버렸지."]], [["The room had no other light than that which it derived from a dim and dirt-clogged lamp, whose lazy wick, being still further obscured by a dark shade, cast its feeble rays over a very little space, and left all beyond in heavy shadow.", "방에는 흐릿하고 때 낀 등잔에서 나오는 빛 말고는 다른 빛이 없었어. 게으른 심지가 어두운 갓에 한층 더 가려진 채, 아주 좁은 데에만 그 희미한 빛을 던지고, 그 너머는 죄다 짙은 그림자 속에 남겨 두었지."], ["This lamp the money-lender had drawn so close to him, that there was only room between it and himself for the book over which he bent; and as he sat, with his elbows on the desk, and his sharp cheek-bones resting on his hands, it only served to bring out his ugly features in strong relief, together with the little table at which he sat, and to shroud all the rest of the chamber in a deep sullen gloom.", "이 등잔을 고리대금업자가 어찌나 바짝 끌어다 놓았던지, 그것과 자기 사이에는 그가 굽어보는 책 하나 놓일 자리밖에 없었어. 그가 책상에 팔꿈치를 괴고, 날카로운 광대뼈를 두 손에 받친 채 앉아 있으니, 등잔불은 그저 그 추한 얼굴을, 그리고 그가 앉은 작은 탁자를 도드라지게 비춰 줄 뿐, 방의 나머지는 죄다 깊고 음침한 어둠 속에 묻어 버렸어."], ["Raising his eyes, and looking vacantly into this gloom as he made some mental calculation, Arthur Gride suddenly met the fixed gaze of a man.", "무슨 셈을 머릿속으로 하느라 눈을 들어 이 어둠 속을 멍하니 바라보던 아서 그라이드는, 문득 한 사내의 못 박힌 듯한 응시와 마주쳤어."]], [["‘Thieves!", "\"도둑이야!"], ["thieves!’ shrieked the usurer, starting up and folding his book to his breast.", "도둑이야!\" 고리대금업자가 벌떡 일어나 책을 가슴에 끌어안으며 비명을 질렀어."], ["‘Robbers!", "\"강도야!"], ["Murder!’", "사람 살려!\""]], [["‘What is the matter?’ said the form, advancing.", "\"무슨 일입니까?\" 그 형체가 다가서며 말했어."]], [["‘Keep off!’ cried the trembling wretch.", "\"가까이 오지 마!\" 벌벌 떠는 가련한 자가 외쳤어."], ["‘Is it a man or a--a--’", "\"사람이냐, 아니면 — 아니면—\""]], [["‘For what do you take me, if not for a man?’ was the inquiry.", "\"사람이 아니면 저를 뭐로 보십니까?\" 하는 물음이 돌아왔어."]], [["‘Yes, yes,’ cried Arthur Gride, shading his eyes with his hand, ‘it is a man, and not a spirit.", "\"그래, 그래.\" 아서 그라이드가 손으로 눈을 가리며 외쳤어."], ["It is a man.", "\"사람이지, 귀신이 아니야. 사람이야."], ["Robbers!", "강도야!"], ["robbers!’", "강도야!\""]], [["‘For what are these cries raised?", "\"무엇 때문에 이런 비명을 지르십니까?"], ["Unless indeed you know me, and have some purpose in your brain?’ said the stranger, coming close up to him.", "혹시 저를 알아보고, 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있는 게 아니라면요?\" 낯선 이가 그에게 바짝 다가서며 말했어."], ["‘I am no thief.’", "\"저는 도둑이 아닙니다.\""]], [["‘What then, and how come you here?’ cried Gride, somewhat reassured, but still retreating from his visitor: ‘what is your name, and what do you want?’", "\"그럼 뭐냐, 어떻게 여기 들어왔어?\" 그라이드가 다소 안심하면서도 여전히 손님에게서 물러나며 외쳤어. \"이름이 뭐고, 무슨 볼일이야?\""]], [["‘My name you need not know,’ was the reply.", "\"제 이름은 알 것 없습니다.\" 하는 대답이었어."], ["‘I came here, because I was shown the way by your servant.", "\"당신네 하인이 길을 알려 줘서 여기 들어왔어요."], ["I have addressed you twice or thrice, but you were too profoundly engaged with your book to hear me, and I have been silently waiting until you should be less abstracted.", "두세 번 말을 걸었는데, 당신이 책에 너무 깊이 빠져 있어 듣지 못하더군요. 그래서 당신이 좀 덜 정신을 놓을 때까지 잠자코 기다린 겁니다."], ["What I want I will tell you, when you can summon up courage enough to hear and understand me.’", "제가 뭘 원하는지는, 당신이 제 말을 듣고 알아들을 만큼 용기를 그러모았을 때 말씀드리지요.\""]], [["Arthur Gride, venturing to regard his visitor more attentively, and perceiving that he was a young man of good mien and bearing, returned to his seat, and muttering that there were bad characters about, and that this, with former attempts upon his house, had made him nervous, requested his visitor to sit down.", "아서 그라이드는 큰맘 먹고 손님을 좀 더 주의 깊게 살펴봤어. 그러고는 그가 풍채와 거동이 점잖은 젊은이라는 걸 알아채고는, 자리로 돌아갔어."], ["This, however, he declined.", "그러고는 요즘 못된 자들이 들끓는데, 이번 일에다 전에도 집을 노린 일들이 있어 자기가 신경이 곤두서 있었다고 중얼거리며, 손님에게 앉으라고 권했어. 하지만 그는 사양했지."]], [["‘Good God!", "\"맙소사!"], ["I don’t stand up to have you at an advantage,’ said Nicholas (for Nicholas it was), as he observed a gesture of alarm on the part of Gride.", "당신보다 유리한 자리를 차지하려고 서 있는 게 아닙니다.\" 니콜라스가 (바로 니콜라스였거든) 말했어."], ["‘Listen to me.", "그라이드가 겁먹은 몸짓을 하는 걸 보고서였지."], ["You are to be married tomorrow morning.’", "\"제 말을 들으세요. 당신은 내일 아침 결혼합니다.\""]], [["‘N--n--no,’ rejoined Gride.", "\"아, 아, 아니야.\""], ["‘Who said I was?", "그라이드가 받아쳤어."], ["How do you know that?’", "\"누가 그래? 그걸 어떻게 알아?\""]], [["‘No matter how,’ replied Nicholas, ‘I know it.", "\"어떻게 아는지는 상관없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["The young lady who is to give you her hand hates and despises you.", "\"저는 압니다. 당신에게 손을 내줄 그 아가씨는 당신을 미워하고 경멸합니다."], ["Her blood runs cold at the mention of your name; the vulture and the lamb, the rat and the dove, could not be worse matched than you and she.", "당신 이름만 들어도 피가 싸늘해진다고요. 독수리와 양, 쥐와 비둘기도 당신과 그녀보다 더 어울리지 않는 짝은 못 될 겁니다."], ["You see I know her.’", "보십시오, 저는 그녀를 압니다.\""]], [["Gride looked at him as if he were petrified with astonishment, but did not speak; perhaps lacking the power.", "그라이드는 깜짝 놀라 돌처럼 굳어 버린 듯 그를 바라봤지만, 말은 하지 않았어. 어쩌면 말할 기운이 없었는지도 몰라."]], [["‘You and another man, Ralph Nickleby by name, have hatched this plot between you,’ pursued Nicholas.", "\"당신과 또 다른 사내, 랄프 니클비라는 자가 둘이서 이 음모를 꾸며 냈죠.\" 니콜라스가 말을 이었어."], ["‘You pay him for his share in bringing about this sale of Madeline Bray.", "\"매들린 브레이를 이렇게 팔아넘기는 일에 그자가 한몫한 대가로 당신이 그자에게 돈을 치르는 거죠."], ["You do.", "그렇잖습니까."], ["A lie is trembling on your lips, I see.’", "거짓말이 당신 입술에서 떨리는 게 보입니다.\""]], [["He paused; but, Arthur making no reply, resumed again.", "그가 말을 멈췄어. 하지만 아서가 아무 대꾸도 하지 않자, 다시 말을 이었지."]], [["‘You pay yourself by defrauding her.", "\"당신은 그녀를 속여 가로채는 걸로 당신 몫을 챙기는 겁니다."], ["How or by what means--for I scorn to sully her cause by falsehood or deceit--I do not know; at present I do not know, but I am not alone or single-handed in this business.", "어떻게, 무슨 수로 — 거짓이나 속임수로 그녀의 일을 더럽히긴 싫으니 — 저는 모릅니다. 지금은 모르지만, 이 일에서 저는 혼자도 아니고 외톨이도 아닙니다."], ["If the energy of man can compass the discovery of your fraud and treachery before your death; if wealth, revenge, and just hatred, can hunt and track you through your windings; you will yet be called to a dear account for this.", "인간의 힘으로 당신의 사기와 배신을 당신이 죽기 전에 밝혀낼 수 있다면, 재물과 복수와 정당한 증오로 당신이 빠져나가는 굽이굽이마다 쫓고 추적할 수 있다면, 당신은 이 일로 톡톡히 대가를 치르게 될 겁니다. 우리는 이미 냄새를 맡았어요."], ["We are on the scent already; judge you, who know what we do not, when we shall have you down!’", "우리가 모르는 걸 아는 당신이, 우리가 당신을 무너뜨릴 때가 언제일지 한번 점쳐 보시지요!\""]], [["He paused again, and still Arthur Gride glared upon him in silence.", "그가 다시 말을 멈췄지만, 아서 그라이드는 여전히 말없이 그를 노려보고만 있었어."]], [["‘If you were a man to whom I could appeal with any hope of touching his compassion or humanity,’ said Nicholas, ‘I would urge upon you to remember the helplessness, the innocence, the youth, of this lady; her worth and beauty, her filial excellence, and last, and more than all, as concerning you more nearly, the appeal she has made to your mercy and your manly feeling.", "\"제가 호소해서 그 동정심이나 인간다움을 건드릴 가망이 조금이라도 있는 사람이라면,\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["But, I take the only ground that can be taken with men like you, and ask what money will buy you off.", "\"저는 이 아가씨의 무력함과 순결함과 젊음을 기억해 달라고 당신께 간곡히 권했을 겁니다. 그녀의 인품과 아름다움, 효심의 갸륵함, 그리고 마지막으로, 무엇보다 당신과 더 가까이 얽힌 일로서, 그녀가 당신의 자비와 사내다운 마음에 호소했다는 걸요."], ["Remember the danger to which you are exposed.", "하지만 저는 당신 같은 사람들에게 취할 수 있는 유일한 방법을 택해, 묻겠습니다."], ["You see I know enough to know much more with very little help.", "돈을 얼마나 주면 당신이 손을 떼겠습니까. 당신이 처한 위험을 기억하세요."], ["Bate some expected gain for the risk you save, and say what is your price.’", "보다시피 저는 아주 약간의 도움만 있으면 훨씬 더 많은 걸 알아낼 만큼 이미 알고 있어요. 당신이 모면하는 위험을 따져 기대 이익에서 얼마쯤 깎고, 당신의 값을 말해 보세요.\""]], [["Old Arthur Gride moved his lips, but they only formed an ugly smile and were motionless again.", "늙은 아서 그라이드는 입술을 움직였지만, 그건 그저 흉한 미소를 지어 보일 뿐 다시 가만히 멈췄어."]], [["‘You think,’ said Nicholas, ‘that the price would not be paid.", "\"당신은 생각하겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["Miss Bray has wealthy friends who would coin their very hearts to save her in such a strait as this.", "\"그 값이 치러지지 않을 거라고요. 브레이 양에게는 부유한 벗들이 있어서, 이런 궁지에서 그녀를 구하기 위해서라면 제 심장이라도 돈으로 바꿀 사람들이지요."], ["Name your price, defer these nuptials for but a few days, and see whether those I speak of, shrink from the payment.", "값을 말하세요. 이 혼례를 단 며칠만 미루고, 제가 말한 그 사람들이 그 돈을 치르길 꺼리는지 어떤지 한번 보시라고요."], ["Do you hear me?’", "제 말 듣고 있습니까?\""]], [["When Nicholas began, Arthur Gride’s impression was, that Ralph Nickleby had betrayed him; but, as he proceeded, he felt convinced that however he had come by the knowledge he possessed, the part he acted was a genuine one, and that with Ralph he had no concern.", "니콜라스가 말을 꺼냈을 때 아서 그라이드는 처음엔 랄프 니클비가 자기를 배신했구나 싶었어. 하지만 그가 말을 이어 갈수록, 그가 가진 지식을 어떻게 손에 넣었든 그가 하는 역할은 진짜이고, 랄프와는 아무 상관이 없다는 확신이 들었지."], ["All he seemed to know, for certain, was, that he, Gride, paid Ralph’s debt; but that, to anybody who knew the circumstances of Bray’s detention--even to Bray himself, on Ralph’s own statement--must be perfectly notorious.", "그가 확실히 아는 건 그저, 자기 그라이드가 랄프의 빚을 갚는다는 것뿐인 듯했어. 하지만 그건 브레이가 잡혀 있게 된 사정을 아는 사람이라면 — 랄프 자신의 말대로라면 브레이 본인에게까지도 — 누구에게나 빤히 알려진 일이었지."], ["As to the fraud on Madeline herself, his visitor knew so little about its nature or extent, that it might be a lucky guess, or a hap-hazard accusation.", "매들린 본인을 속이는 사기에 대해서는, 이 손님이 그 본질이나 규모에 대해 아는 게 너무 적어서, 그건 운 좋은 짐작이거나 막무가내로 던진 비난일 수도 있었어."], ["Whether or no, he had clearly no key to the mystery, and could not hurt him who kept it close within his own breast.", "어쨌든 그는 이 비밀의 열쇠를 분명 쥐고 있지 않았고, 그 비밀을 제 가슴속에 꽁꽁 간직한 그자를 해칠 수는 없었지."], ["The allusion to friends, and the offer of money, Gride held to be mere empty vapouring, for purposes of delay.", "벗들에 대한 언급이며 돈 제안이며, 그라이드는 그저 시간을 끌려는 속 빈 허풍으로 여겼어. '그리고 돈을 손에 넣을 수 있다 쳐도,'"], ["‘And even if money were to be had,’ thought Arthur Gride, as he glanced at Nicholas, and trembled with passion at his boldness and audacity, ‘I’d have that dainty chick for my wife, and cheat YOU of her, young smooth-face!’", "아서 그라이드는 니콜라스를 흘끔 보며, 그 대담함과 뻔뻔함에 분노로 떨면서 생각했어. '저 곱상한 병아리를 내 아내로 삼고, 너한테서 가로채 주마, 이 풋내기 매끈둥이야!'"]], [["Long habit of weighing and noting well what clients said, and nicely balancing chances in his mind and calculating odds to their faces, without the least appearance of being so engaged, had rendered Gride quick in forming conclusions, and arriving, from puzzling, intricate, and often contradictory premises, at very cunning deductions.", "오랜 세월 고객들이 하는 말을 꼼꼼히 재고 헤아리며, 머릿속으로 가능성을 정밀하게 저울질하고 면전에서 승산을 따지되 조금도 그러는 티를 내지 않는 버릇 덕에, 그라이드는 결론을 빨리 내리고, 헷갈리고 뒤얽히고 때로는 앞뒤가 맞지 않는 전제들에서 아주 교활한 추론을 끌어내는 데 능했어."], ["Hence it was that, as Nicholas went on, he followed him closely with his own constructions, and, when he ceased to speak, was as well prepared as if he had deliberated for a fortnight.", "그래서 니콜라스가 말을 이어 가는 동안, 그는 자기 나름의 해석으로 바짝 뒤따라갔지. 그리고 니콜라스가 말을 멈췄을 때, 그는 마치 보름 동안 곰곰이 따진 것처럼 만반의 준비가 되어 있었어."]], [["‘I hear you,’ he cried, starting from his seat, casting back the fastenings of the window-shutters, and throwing up the sash.", "\"듣고말고.\" 그가 자리에서 벌떡 일어나, 덧창의 걸쇠를 도로 젖히고 미닫이창을 확 올리며 외쳤어."], ["‘Help here!", "\"여기 사람 살려! 사람 살려!"], ["Help! Help!’", "사람 살려!\""]], [["‘What are you doing?’ said Nicholas, seizing him by the arm.", "\"뭐 하는 겁니까?\" 니콜라스가 그의 팔을 붙잡으며 말했어."]], [["‘I’ll cry robbers, thieves, murder, alarm the neighbourhood, struggle with you, let loose some blood, and swear you came to rob me, if you don’t quit my house,’ replied Gride, drawing in his head with a frightful grin, ‘I will!’", "\"강도야, 도둑이야, 사람 죽인다 소리쳐서 동네를 깨우고, 너랑 몸싸움을 벌여 피를 좀 흘리고, 네가 날 털러 왔다고 맹세할 테다, 네가 내 집에서 나가지 않으면.\" 그라이드가 무시무시하게 이를 드러내며 고개를 안으로 들이밀며 대답했어. \"정말이야!\""]], [["‘Wretch!’ cried Nicholas.", "\"이 가련한 인간!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어."]], [["‘YOU’LL bring your threats here, will you?’ said Gride, whom jealousy of Nicholas and a sense of his own triumph had converted into a perfect fiend.", "\"감히 네 협박을 여기 들고 와, 그래?\" 그라이드가 말했어."], ["‘You, the disappointed lover?", "니콜라스에 대한 질투와 제 승리에 대한 감격이 그를 완전한 악귀로 바꿔 놓았지. \"너, 퇴짜 맞은 연인 양반?"], ["Oh dear!", "이런 세상에!"], ["He!", "히!"], ["he!", "히!"], ["he!", "히!"], ["But you shan’t have her, nor she you.", "하지만 넌 그 애를 못 가져, 그 애도 널 못 가져."], ["She’s my wife, my doting little wife.", "그 앤 내 아내야, 나를 끔찍이 위하는 내 작은 마누라라고."], ["Do you think she’ll miss you?", "그 애가 널 그리워할 것 같아?"], ["Do you think she’ll weep?", "그 애가 울 것 같으냐고?"], ["I shall like to see her weep, I shan’t mind it.", "그 애가 우는 걸 한번 보고 싶구나, 난 개의치 않아."], ["She looks prettier in tears.’", "눈물을 흘리면 더 예뻐 보이거든.\""]], [["‘Villain!’ said Nicholas, choking with his rage.", "\"이 악당!\" 니콜라스가 분노에 목이 메어 말했어."]], [["‘One minute more,’ cried Arthur Gride, ‘and I’ll rouse the street with such screams, as, if they were raised by anybody else, should wake me even in the arms of pretty Madeline.’", "\"일 분만 더.\" 아서 그라이드가 외쳤어. \"그럼 온 거리를 비명으로 깨워 주마. 다른 누가 그런 비명을 질렀다면, 예쁜 매들린의 품에 안겨 있던 나라도 잠을 깰 만큼.\""]], [["‘You hound!’ said Nicholas.", "\"이 개 같은 놈!\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘If you were but a younger man--’", "\"네놈이 좀 더 젊은 자였다면—\""]], [["‘Oh yes!’ sneered Arthur Gride, ‘If I was but a younger man it wouldn’t be so bad; but for me, so old and ugly! To be jilted by little Madeline for me!’", "\"아, 그래!\" 아서 그라이드가 비웃었어. \"내가 좀 더 젊은 자였다면 이렇게까지는 아니겠지. 하지만 나처럼 이렇게 늙고 추한 자한테! 작은 매들린이 나 같은 자 때문에 너를 차 버리다니!\""]], [["‘Hear me,’ said Nicholas, ‘and be thankful I have enough command over myself not to fling you into the street, which no aid could prevent my doing if I once grappled with you.", "\"내 말 들어.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["I have been no lover of this lady’s.", "\"그리고 내가 너를 거리로 내던지지 않을 만큼 나 자신을 다스리고 있는 걸 고맙게 여겨. 일단 너랑 맞붙으면 그 누구도 못 말릴 짓을 말이야."], ["No contract or engagement, no word of love, has ever passed between us.", "나는 이 아가씨의 연인이었던 적이 없어. 우리 사이엔 어떤 언약도 약혼도 없었고, 사랑한다는 말 한마디 오간 적도 없어."], ["She does not even know my name.’", "그녀는 내 이름조차 몰라.\""]], [["‘I’ll ask it for all that.", "\"그래도 내가 물어볼 테다. 입을 맞춰 가며 그 애한테 졸라 댈 거야.\""], ["I’ll beg it of her with kisses,’ said Arthur Gride.", "아서 그라이드가 말했어. \"그래, 그럼 그 애가 말해 주고, 입맞춤도 되돌려 주겠지."], ["‘Yes, and she’ll tell me, and pay them back, and we’ll laugh together, and hug ourselves, and be very merry, when we think of the poor youth that wanted to have her, but couldn’t because she was bespoke by me!’", "그럼 우린 함께 웃고, 서로 부둥켜안고, 아주 즐거워하는 거야. 그 애를 갖고 싶어 했지만 이미 나한테 약속된 몸이라 못 가진 그 가엾은 풋내기를 떠올리면서 말이지!\""]], [["This taunt brought such an expression into the face of Nicholas, that Arthur Gride plainly apprehended it to be the forerunner of his putting his threat of throwing him into the street in immediate execution; for he thrust his head out of the window, and holding tight on with both hands, raised a pretty brisk alarm.", "이 비웃음에 니콜라스의 얼굴에 어찌나 무서운 표정이 어렸던지, 아서 그라이드는 그게 곧 자기를 거리로 내던지겠다던 그 협박을 당장 실행에 옮길 전조라고 똑똑히 알아챘어. 그래서 그는 창밖으로 머리를 내밀고 두 손으로 단단히 매달려서는, 꽤나 요란하게 비명을 질러 댔지."], ["Not thinking it necessary to abide the issue of the noise, Nicholas gave vent to an indignant defiance, and stalked from the room and from the house.", "니콜라스는 그 소란의 결말을 기다릴 필요가 없다고 여기고, 분에 차서 한바탕 호통을 내뱉고는 방을 나와 집을 떠났어."], ["Arthur Gride watched him across the street, and then, drawing in his head, fastened the window as before, and sat down to take breath.", "아서 그라이드는 그가 거리를 가로질러 가는 걸 지켜봤어. 그러고는 머리를 안으로 들이밀고, 전처럼 창문을 단단히 잠그고, 숨을 돌리려 앉았지."]], [["‘If she ever turns pettish or ill-humoured, I’ll taunt her with that spark,’ he said, when he had recovered.", "\"저 애가 언제고 토라지거나 심술을 부리면, 저 풋내기 얘기로 약을 올려 줘야지.\" 그가 마음을 가라앉히고 나서 말했어."], ["‘She’ll little think I know about him; and, if I manage it well, I can break her spirit by this means and have her under my thumb.", "\"내가 저놈을 안다는 건 꿈에도 모를 거야. 그리고 잘만 다루면, 이걸로 그 애 기를 꺾어 내 엄지손가락 밑에 둘 수 있겠지."], ["I’m glad nobody came.", "아무도 안 와서 다행이야."], ["I didn’t call too loud.", "너무 크게 부르진 않았으니."], ["The audacity to enter my house, and open upon me!", "감히 내 집에 들어와 나한테 들이대다니!"], ["But I shall have a very good triumph tomorrow, and he’ll be gnawing his fingers off: perhaps drown himself or cut his throat!", "하지만 내일은 아주 멋진 승리를 거둘 테고, 저놈은 제 손가락을 물어뜯고 있겠지. 어쩌면 물에 빠져 죽거나 제 목을 그을지도 몰라!"], ["I shouldn’t wonder!", "그래도 난 놀라지 않을 거야!"], ["That would make it quite complete, that would: quite.’", "그러면 아주 완벽해지지, 그래, 그야말로 완벽하게 말이야.\""]], [["When he had become restored to his usual condition by these and other comments on his approaching triumph, Arthur Gride put away his book, and, having locked the chest with great caution, descended into the kitchen to warn Peg Sliderskew to bed, and scold her for having afforded such ready admission to a stranger.", "다가오는 승리에 대한 이런저런 평을 늘어놓으며 평소의 상태로 돌아온 아서 그라이드는 책을 치웠어. 그리고 아주 조심스럽게 궤짝을 잠그고 나서, 부엌으로 내려가 페그 슬라이더스큐한테 잠자리에 들라고 이르고, 낯선 자를 그렇게 선뜻 들여보냈다고 야단을 쳤지."]], [["The unconscious Peg, however, not being able to comprehend the offence of which she had been guilty, he summoned her to hold the light, while he made a tour of the fastenings, and secured the street-door with his own hands.", "하지만 영문을 모르는 페그가 자기가 저지른 잘못을 도무지 알아듣지 못하자, 그는 그녀에게 불을 들고 있으라고 했어. 그동안 자기가 문단속을 한 바퀴 돌고, 현관문을 제 손으로 직접 잠갔지."]], [["‘Top bolt,’ muttered Arthur, fastening as he spoke, ‘bottom bolt, chain, bar, double lock, and key out to put under my pillow!", "\"위쪽 빗장,\" 아서가 말하면서 잠그며 중얼거렸어."], ["So, if any more rejected admirers come, they may come through the keyhole.", "\"아래쪽 빗장, 사슬, 가로대, 이중 자물쇠, 그리고 열쇠는 빼서 내 베개 밑에 둔다! 자, 퇴짜 맞은 구애자가 또 온다면, 열쇠 구멍으로나 들어오라지."], ["And now I’ll go to sleep till half-past five, when I must get up to be married, Peg!’", "이제 다섯 시 반까지 자야겠다. 그때 일어나 결혼하러 가야 하니까, 페그!\""]], [["With that, he jocularly tapped Mrs.", "그러면서 그는 장난스럽게 슬라이더스큐 부인의 턱을 톡톡 두드렸어."], ["Sliderskew under the chin, and appeared, for the moment, inclined to celebrate the close of his bachelor days by imprinting a kiss on her shrivelled lips.", "그러고는 잠깐, 그녀의 쪼글쪼글한 입술에 입맞춤을 찍어 총각 시절의 끝을 기념하고 싶은 마음이 든 듯했지."], ["Thinking better of it, however, he gave her chin another tap, in lieu of that warmer familiarity, and stole away to bed.", "하지만 다시 생각을 고쳐먹고는, 그 좀 더 따뜻한 친밀함 대신 그녀의 턱을 한 번 더 톡 두드리고, 슬그머니 잠자리로 갔어."]], [["The Crisis of the Project and its Result\nThere are not many men who lie abed too late, or oversleep themselves, on their wedding morning.", "결혼식 날 아침에 너무 늦게까지 침대에 누워 있거나 늦잠을 자는 사람은 그리 많지 않아."], ["A legend there is of somebody remarkable for absence of mind, who opened his eyes upon the day which was to give him a young wife, and forgetting all about the matter, rated his servants for providing him with such fine clothes as had been prepared for the festival.", "옛날이야기 중에 정신이 딴 데 가 있기로 유명한 어떤 사람 얘기가 있어. 젊은 아내를 맞을 바로 그날 아침에 눈을 떴는데, 그 일을 까맣게 잊어버리고는 잔칫날을 위해 차려 놓은 그 좋은 옷을 두고 하인들을 나무랐다는 거야."], ["There is also a legend of a young gentleman, who, not having before his eyes the fear of the canons of the church for such cases made and provided, conceived a passion for his grandmother.", "또 어떤 젊은 신사 이야기도 있지. 그런 경우를 위해 교회법이 정해 놓은 규율 따위는 안중에도 없이 자기 할머니한테 반해 버렸다지 뭐야."], ["Both cases are of a singular and special kind and it is very doubtful whether either can be considered as a precedent likely to be extensively followed by succeeding generations.", "두 경우 다 아주 별나고 특이한 일이라, 다음 세대 사람들이 널리 따를 만한 선례가 될 수 있을지는 자못 의심스러워."]], [["Arthur Gride had enrobed himself in his marriage garments of bottle-green, a full hour before Mrs.", "아서 그라이드는 한 시간도 더 전에 병녹색 결혼 예복을 차려입었어. 슬라이더스큐 부인이 깊은 잠을 떨치고 그의 방문을 두드리기 꼬박 한 시간도 더 전에 말이야."], ["Sliderskew, shaking off her more heavy slumbers, knocked at his chamber door; and he had hobbled downstairs in full array and smacked his lips over a scanty taste of his favourite cordial, ere that delicate piece of antiquity enlightened the kitchen with her presence.", "그러고는 잔뜩 빼입은 채로 절뚝절뚝 아래층으로 내려가, 좋아하는 강장제를 찔끔 맛보며 입맛을 다셨지. 그 골동품 같은 노인네가 부엌에 모습을 드러내 빛을 비추기도 전에 말이야."]], [["‘Faugh!’ said Peg, grubbing, in the discharge of her domestic functions, among a scanty heap of ashes in the rusty grate.", "\"퉤!\""], ["‘Wedding indeed!", "페그가 말했어."], ["A precious wedding!", "집안일을 한답시고 녹슨 벽난로 안에 얼마 안 되는 잿더미를 뒤적이면서 말이야. \"결혼이라니!"], ["He wants somebody better than his old Peg to take care of him, does he?", "거 참 대단한 결혼일세! 자기를 돌봐 줄 사람으로 늙은 페그보다 더 나은 누군가가 필요하시다 이거지?"], ["And what has he said to me, many and many a time, to keep me content with short food, small wages, and little fire?", "그런데 나한테 몇 번이고 뭐라고 했더라? 적은 밥에, 쥐꼬리만 한 품삯에, 변변찮은 불이나 때면서 만족하라고 할 때 말이야."], ["“My will, Peg!", "'내 뜻이야, 페그!"], ["my will!”", "내 뜻이라고!'"], ["says he: “I’m a bachelor--no friends--no relations, Peg.”", "그러셨지. '난 홀아비야 ― 친구도 없고 ― 친척도 없어, 페그.'"], ["Lies!", "거짓말!"], ["And now he’s to bring home a new mistress, a baby-faced chit of a girl!", "그래 놓고 이제 와서 새 안주인을 데려온다 이거지, 그것도 애 같은 얼굴을 한 계집애를!"], ["If he wanted a wife, the fool, why couldn’t he have one suitable to his age, and that knew his ways?", "아내가 필요했으면, 이 바보 영감탱이가, 어째서 제 나이에 어울리고 제 버릇도 잘 아는 여자를 못 골랐담?"], ["She won’t come in MY way, he says.", "그 여자는 내 일에 끼어들지 않을 거라고?"], ["No, that she won’t, but you little think why, Arthur boy!’", "그래, 안 끼어들겠지, 그런데 그 까닭은 까맣게 모르시겠지, 아서 영감!\""]], [["While Mrs. Sliderskew, influenced possibly by some lingering feelings of disappointment and personal slight, occasioned by her old master’s preference for another, was giving loose to these grumblings below stairs, Arthur Gride was cogitating in the parlour upon what had taken place last night.", "슬라이더스큐 부인이 아래층에서 이렇게 투덜대는 동안 ― 아마 옛 주인이 다른 여자를 더 좋아한 탓에 생긴, 가시지 않은 실망과 무시당했다는 서운함 때문이었겠지 ― 아서 그라이드는 응접실에서 간밤에 있었던 일을 곰곰이 따져 보고 있었어."]], [["‘I can’t think how he can have picked up what he knows,’ said Arthur, ‘unless I have committed myself--let something drop at Bray’s, for instance--which has been overheard.", "\"그자가 어떻게 알아낸 건지 도무지 모르겠단 말이야.\" 아서가 중얼거렸어."], ["Perhaps I may. I shouldn’t be surprised if that was it.", "\"내가 뭔가 실수를 한 게 아니라면 ― 이를테면 브레이네 집에서 뭔가 흘렸는데 그걸 누가 엿들었다든가."], ["Mr.", "그럴지도 모르지."], ["Nickleby was often angry at my talking to him before we got outside the door.", "그게 사실이라 해도 놀랍지 않아. 니클비 씨는 문밖으로 나가기 전에 내가 그 사람한테 떠드는 걸 자주 못마땅해했으니까."], ["I mustn’t tell him that part of the business, or he’ll put me out of sorts, and make me nervous for the day.’", "그 부분은 말하지 말아야겠어. 안 그러면 기분 상하게 해서 하루 종일 나를 안절부절못하게 만들 거야.\""]], [["Ralph was universally looked up to, and recognised among his fellows as a superior genius, but upon Arthur Gride his stern unyielding character and consummate art had made so deep an impression, that he was actually afraid of him. Cringing and cowardly to the core by nature, Arthur Gride humbled himself in the dust before Ralph Nickleby, and, even when they had not this stake in common, would have licked his shoes and crawled upon the ground before him rather than venture to return him word for word, or retort upon him in any other spirit than one of the most slavish and abject sycophancy.", "랄프는 모두에게 우러러보였어. 동료들 사이에서도 뛰어난 천재로 인정받았지. 그런데 아서 그라이드한테는 랄프의 엄하고 굽힐 줄 모르는 성격과 능수능란한 솜씨가 어찌나 깊은 인상을 남겼던지, 그는 정말로 랄프를 무서워했어. 천성이 뼛속까지 비굴하고 겁이 많은 아서 그라이드는 랄프 니클비 앞에서 흙바닥에 엎드리듯 자신을 한껏 낮췄어. 이런 공동의 이해관계가 없을 때조차도, 그는 랄프한테 말대꾸를 하거나 노예처럼 빌붙는 비굴한 아첨 말고 다른 마음으로 대들기는커녕, 차라리 그의 신발을 핥고 발치에 기어 다니려 들었지."]], [["To Ralph Nickleby’s, Arthur Gride now betook himself according to appointment; and to Ralph Nickleby he related how, last night, some young blustering blade, whom he had never seen, forced his way into his house, and tried to frighten him from the proposed nuptials. Told, in short, what Nicholas had said and done, with the slight reservation upon which he had determined.", "아서 그라이드는 이제 약속대로 랄프 니클비를 찾아갔어. 그리고 랄프 니클비한테 이야기했지. 간밤에 한 번도 본 적 없는 어떤 젊고 으스대는 놈이 자기 집에 쳐들어와서, 예정된 결혼을 못 하게 겁을 주려 했다고 말이야. 한마디로, 니콜라스가 한 말과 행동을 다 들려준 거야. 단, 자기가 마음먹은 대로 살짝 빼놓은 부분만 빼고."]], [["‘Well, and what then?’ said Ralph.", "\"그래, 그래서 어떻게 됐는데?\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["nothing more,’ rejoined Gride.", "그게 다예요.\" 그라이드가 대답했어."]], [["‘He tried to frighten you,’ said Ralph, ‘and you WERE frightened I suppose; is that it?’", "\"그자가 자네를 겁주려 했고,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"내 짐작엔 자네가 겁먹었겠지. 그런 거 아닌가?\""]], [["‘I frightened HIM by crying thieves and murder,’ replied Gride.", "\"내가 도둑이야 살인이야 소리쳐서 그자를 겁줬는걸요.\" 그라이드가 대꾸했어."], ["‘Once I was in earnest, I tell you that, for I had more than half a mind to swear he uttered threats, and demanded my life or my money.’", "\"한순간은 진심이었어요, 정말이지. 그자가 협박을 했다고, 내 목숨이나 돈을 내놓으라 했다고 맹세할 마음이 반 넘게 들었거든요.\""]], [["‘Oho!’ said Ralph, eyeing him askew.", "\"오호!\" 랄프가 그를 곁눈질하며 말했어."], ["‘Jealous too!’", "\"질투까지 하는군!\""]], [["‘Dear now, see that!’ cried Arthur, rubbing his hands and affecting to laugh.", "\"이런, 저것 좀 보게나!\" 아서가 두 손을 비비며 짐짓 웃는 척 외쳤어."]], [["‘Why do you make those grimaces, man?’ said Ralph; ‘you ARE jealous--and with good cause I think.’", "\"이 사람아, 왜 그렇게 얼굴을 찡그리나?\" 랄프가 말했어. \"자네 질투하는 거 맞잖아 ― 게다가 그럴 만한 까닭도 있고 말이야.\""]], [["‘No, no, no; not with good cause, hey?", "\"아니, 아니, 아니지. 그럴 만한 까닭은 없어요, 응?"], ["You don’t think with good cause, do you?’ cried Arthur, faltering.", "그럴 만한 까닭이 있다고 생각하는 건 아니지요, 그렇지요?\" 아서가 우물거리며 외쳤어."], ["‘Do you though, hey?’", "\"그래도 그렇게 생각해요, 응?\""]], [["‘Why, how stands the fact?’ returned Ralph. ‘Here is an old man about to be forced in marriage upon a girl; and to this old man there comes a handsome young fellow--you said he was handsome, didn’t you?’", "\"아니, 사실이 어떤데 그래?\" 랄프가 되받았어. \"여기 한 늙은이가 있어. 어린 처녀와 억지로 결혼하려는 참이지. 그런데 이 늙은이한테 잘생긴 젊은 놈이 찾아온 거야 ― 자네가 그놈더러 잘생겼다고 했지, 안 그래?\""]], [["‘No!’ snarled Arthur Gride.", "\"아니!\" 아서 그라이드가 으르렁댔어."]], [["‘Oh!’ rejoined Ralph, ‘I thought you did.", "\"어!\" 랄프가 대꾸했어."], ["Well!", "\"그랬다고 한 줄 알았는데. 뭐!"], ["Handsome or not handsome, to this old man there comes a young fellow who casts all manner of fierce defiances in his teeth--gums I should rather say--and tells him in plain terms that his mistress hates him.", "잘생겼든 안 생겼든, 이 늙은이한테 젊은 놈이 찾아와서 온갖 험악한 도전을 그의 이빨에다 ― 아니, 잇몸에다 대고라고 해야겠군 ― 퍼부었지. 그러고는 대놓고 말했다지."], ["What does he do that for?", "당신 신붓감이 당신을 미워한다고."], ["Philanthropy’s sake?’", "그놈이 왜 그랬겠어? 자선을 베푸느라?\""]], [["‘Not for love of the lady,’ replied Gride, ‘for he said that no word of love--his very words--had ever passed between ‘em.’", "\"그 아가씨를 사랑해서는 아니에요.\" 그라이드가 대답했어. \"그놈이 그랬거든요. 둘 사이엔 사랑한다는 말이 ― 바로 그놈이 한 말이에요 ― 오간 적이 한 번도 없다고요.\""]], [["‘He said!’ repeated Ralph, contemptuously. ‘But I like him for one thing, and that is, his giving you this fair warning to keep your--what is it?--Tit-tit or dainty chick--which?--under lock and key. Be careful, Gride, be careful. It’s a triumph, too, to tear her away from a gallant young rival: a great triumph for an old man! It only remains to keep her safe when you have her--that’s all.’", "\"그놈이 그랬다!\" 랄프가 비웃듯 되뇌었어. \"하지만 한 가지 점에서는 그놈이 마음에 들어. 자네한테 친절히 미리 경고해 줬으니까. 자네의 ― 그게 뭐더라? ― 어여쁜 새 색시인지 앙증맞은 병아리인지 ― 어느 쪽이지? ― 를 단단히 자물쇠로 채워 두라고 말이야. 조심해, 그라이드, 조심하라고. 게다가 이건 승리야. 씩씩한 젊은 연적한테서 그 여자를 빼앗아 오는 거니까. 늙은이한테는 대단한 승리지! 이제 손에 넣고 나서 안전하게 지키는 일만 남았어 ― 그게 다야.\""]], [["‘What a man it is!’ cried Arthur Gride, affecting, in the extremity of his torture, to be highly amused. And then he added, anxiously, ‘Yes; to keep her safe, that’s all. And that isn’t much, is it?’", "\"참 대단한 사람이라니까!\" 아서 그라이드가 외쳤어. 괴로움이 극에 달했는데도 짐짓 무척 즐거운 척하면서 말이야. 그러고는 걱정스레 덧붙였지. \"그래요. 안전하게 지키는 거, 그게 다죠. 그건 별일 아니지요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["‘Much!’ said Ralph, with a sneer.", "\"별일 아니라고!\" 랄프가 비웃으며 말했어."], ["‘Why, everybody knows what easy things to understand and to control, women are.", "\"아니, 여자가 얼마나 다루기 쉽고 마음대로 휘두르기 쉬운지는 누구나 다 알잖아."], ["But come, it’s very nearly time for you to be made happy.", "그건 그렇고, 이제 자네가 행복해질 시간이 거의 다 됐군."], ["You’ll pay the bond now, I suppose, to save us trouble afterwards.’", "나중에 번거롭지 않게, 지금 차용증 돈을 갚아 두는 게 좋겠지?\""]], [["‘Oh what a man you are!’ croaked Arthur.", "\"아유, 정말 대단한 양반이라니까요!\" 아서가 갈라진 목소리로 말했어."]], [["‘Why not?’ said Ralph.", "\"안 될 거 있나?\""], ["‘Nobody will pay you interest for the money, I suppose, between this and twelve o’clock; will they?’", "랄프가 말했어. \"지금부터 열두 시 사이에 그 돈 이자를 자네한테 쳐줄 사람은 아무도 없을 텐데, 안 그래?\""]], [["‘But nobody would pay you interest for it either, you know,’ returned Arthur, leering at Ralph with all the cunning and slyness he could throw into his face.", "\"하지만 당신한테 이자를 쳐줄 사람도 없잖아요, 안 그래요.\" 아서가 얼굴에 짜낼 수 있는 온갖 교활하고 능청스러운 빛을 띠며 랄프를 곁눈질하며 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Besides which,’ said Ralph, suffering his lip to curl into a smile, ‘you haven’t the money about you, and you weren’t prepared for this, or you’d have brought it with you; and there’s nobody you’d so much like to accommodate as me.", "\"게다가,\" 랄프가 입술 끝을 슬쩍 비틀어 웃으며 말했어."], ["I see.", "\"자네는 지금 그 돈을 갖고 있지도 않고, 이럴 줄 미리 대비하지도 않았지. 안 그랬으면 가지고 왔을 테니까."], ["We trust each other in about an equal degree.", "그리고 자네가 나만큼 기꺼이 편의를 봐주고 싶은 사람도 없을 거고. 다 알겠어."], ["Are you ready?’", "우리는 서로 비슷한 만큼 믿는 거지. 준비됐나?\""]], [["Gride, who had done nothing but grin, and nod, and chatter, during this last speech of Ralph’s, answered in the affirmative; and, producing from his hat a couple of large white favours, pinned one on his breast, and with considerable difficulty induced his friend to do the like.", "랄프의 이 마지막 말이 이어지는 동안 그라이드는 그저 히죽거리고 고개를 끄덕이고 종알대기만 했는데, 이윽고 그렇다고 대답했어. 그러고는 모자에서 커다란 흰 장식 리본 두 개를 꺼내, 하나를 제 가슴에 꽂고, 적잖이 애를 써서 친구한테도 똑같이 달게 했지."], ["Thus accoutred, they got into a hired coach which Ralph had in waiting, and drove to the residence of the fair and most wretched bride.", "이렇게 차림을 갖춘 두 사람은 랄프가 대기시켜 둔 삯마차에 올라타고, 아름답고 더없이 가엾은 신부의 집으로 달려갔어."]], [["Gride, whose spirits and courage had gradually failed him more and more as they approached nearer and nearer to the house, was utterly dismayed and cowed by the mournful silence which pervaded it.", "그라이드는 집에 가까워지면 가까워질수록 점점 더 기운과 용기가 빠져나갔는데, 집 안에 감도는 구슬픈 정적에 완전히 겁을 먹고 풀이 죽어 버렸어."], ["The face of the poor servant girl, the only person they saw, was disfigured with tears and want of sleep.", "그들이 마주친 단 한 사람, 가엾은 하녀의 얼굴은 눈물과 잠 못 잔 흔적으로 일그러져 있었지. 그들을 맞이하거나 반겨 주는 사람은 아무도 없었어."], ["There was nobody to receive or welcome them; and they stole upstairs into the usual sitting-room, more like two burglars than the bridegroom and his friend.", "두 사람은 살금살금 위층 여느 거실로 올라갔지. 신랑과 그 친구라기보다는 도둑 두 명 같은 꼴로 말이야."]], [["‘One would think,’ said Ralph, speaking, in spite of himself, in a low and subdued voice, ‘that there was a funeral going on here, and not a wedding.’", "\"이건 마치,\" 랄프가 저도 모르게 낮고 가라앉은 목소리로 말했어. \"결혼식이 아니라 장례식이 치러지는 것 같군.\""]], [["‘He, he!’ tittered his friend, ‘you are so--so very funny!’", "\"히, 히!\" 친구가 킥킥거렸어. \"당신은 정말 ― 정말 우스운 양반이라니까요!\""]], [["‘I need be,’ remarked Ralph, drily, ‘for this is rather dull and chilling.", "\"우스워야지.\" 랄프가 무뚝뚝하게 말했어."], ["Look a little brisker, man, and not so hangdog like!’", "\"여기가 좀 음침하고 으스스하니까 말이야. 좀 더 기운차게 굴게, 이 사람아, 그렇게 풀 죽은 꼴 하지 말고!\""]], [["‘Yes, yes, I will,’ said Gride.", "\"네, 네, 그러죠.\" 그라이드가 말했어."], ["‘But--but--you don’t think she’s coming just yet, do you?’", "\"그런데 ― 그런데 ― 그 여자가 벌써 오는 건 아니겠지요, 그렇지요?\""]], [["‘Why, I suppose she’ll not come till she is obliged,’ returned Ralph, looking at his watch, ‘and she has a good half-hour to spare yet.", "\"글쎄, 어쩔 수 없을 때까지는 안 나오겠지.\" 랄프가 시계를 보며 대꾸했어."], ["Curb your impatience.’", "\"아직 반 시간은 더 여유가 있어. 그 조바심 좀 다스리게.\""]], [["‘I--I--am not impatient,’ stammered Arthur.", "\"나 ― 나 ― 조바심 내는 거 아니에요.\" 아서가 더듬거렸어."], ["‘I wouldn’t be hard with her for the world.", "\"그 여자한테 모질게 굴 생각은 결코 없어요."], ["Oh dear, dear, not on any account.", "아유, 원, 천만에."], ["Let her take her time--her own time.", "그 여자가 천천히 하게 둬요 ― 제 시간껏 말이에요."], ["Her time shall be ours by all means.’", "그 여자 시간은 곧 우리 시간이기도 하니까, 얼마든지요.\""]], [["While Ralph bent upon his trembling friend a keen look, which showed that he perfectly understood the reason of this great consideration and regard, a footstep was heard upon the stairs, and Bray himself came into the room on tiptoe, and holding up his hand with a cautious gesture, as if there were some sick person near, who must not be disturbed.", "랄프가 떨고 있는 친구를 날카로운 눈길로 쏘아봤어. 이 지극한 배려와 친절의 까닭을 완전히 꿰뚫고 있다는 듯한 눈빛이었지. 바로 그때 계단에서 발소리가 들렸어. 그리고 브레이가 직접 발끝으로 살금살금 방에 들어왔지. 마치 근처에 깨우면 안 되는 환자라도 있는 양, 조심하라는 손짓으로 손을 들어 올리면서 말이야."]], [["‘Hush!’ he said, in a low voice.", "\"쉿!\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 말했어."], ["‘She was very ill last night.", "\"그 애가 어젯밤 몹시 아팠어요."], ["I thought she would have broken her heart.", "가슴이 찢어지는 줄 알았다니까요."], ["She is dressed, and crying bitterly in her own room; but she’s better, and quite quiet.", "지금은 옷을 다 차려입고 제 방에서 서럽게 울고 있어요. 하지만 한결 나아져서 아주 조용해요."], ["That’s everything!’", "그거면 됐죠!\""]], [["‘She is ready, is she?’ said Ralph.", "\"준비가 됐다는 거지요?\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Quite ready,’ returned the father.", "\"다 됐어요.\" 아버지가 대답했어."]], [["‘And not likely to delay us by any young-lady weaknesses--fainting, or so forth?’ said Ralph.", "\"기절이라든가 뭐 그런, 처녀 애들 특유의 나약한 짓으로 우리를 붙들어 둘 일은 없겠지요?\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘She may be safely trusted now,’ returned Bray.", "\"이젠 안심해도 돼요.\" 브레이가 대답했어."], ["‘I have been talking to her this morning.", "\"오늘 아침에 그 애와 이야기를 나눴거든요."], ["Here!", "자!"], ["Come a little this way.’", "이리 좀 와 봐요.\""]], [["He drew Ralph Nickleby to the further end of the room, and pointed towards Gride, who sat huddled together in a corner, fumbling nervously with the buttons of his coat, and exhibiting a face, of which every skulking and base expression was sharpened and aggravated to the utmost by his anxiety and trepidation.", "그는 랄프 니클비를 방 저쪽 끝으로 데려가서는, 한쪽 구석에 잔뜩 웅크리고 앉아 외투 단추를 초조하게 만지작거리는 그라이드를 가리켰어. 그라이드의 얼굴은 그 음흉하고 비열한 표정 하나하나가, 불안과 두려움 탓에 더할 나위 없이 날카롭고 심하게 도드라져 있었지."]], [["‘Look at that man,’ whispered Bray, emphatically.", "\"저 사람을 봐요.\" 브레이가 힘주어 속삭였어."], ["‘This seems a cruel thing, after all.’", "\"이건 아무래도 잔인한 짓이오.\""]], [["‘What seems a cruel thing?’ inquired Ralph, with as much stolidity of face, as if he really were in utter ignorance of the other’s meaning.", "\"뭐가 잔인한 짓이라는 거요?\" 랄프가 물었어. 정말로 상대방 말뜻을 도무지 모르겠다는 듯, 무덤덤한 얼굴로 말이야."]], [["‘This marriage,’ answered Bray.", "\"이 결혼 말이오.\" 브레이가 대답했어."], ["‘Don’t ask me what.", "\"뭐가 잔인하냐고 묻지 말아요."], ["You know as well as I do.’", "나만큼 당신도 잘 알잖소.\""]], [["Ralph shrugged his shoulders, in silent deprecation of Bray’s impatience, and elevated his eyebrows, and pursed his lips, as men do when they are prepared with a sufficient answer to some remark, but wait for a more favourable opportunity of advancing it, or think it scarcely worth while to answer their adversary at all.", "랄프는 어깨를 으쓱했어. 브레이의 조바심을 말없이 나무라는 거였지. 그러고는 눈썹을 치켜올리고 입술을 오므렸어. 어떤 말에 충분한 답을 마련해 두고도 그걸 꺼낼 더 좋은 기회를 기다리거나, 상대한테 굳이 대꾸할 가치도 없다 싶을 때 사람들이 하는 그런 몸짓이었어."]], [["‘Look at him.", "\"저 사람을 봐요."], ["Does it not seem cruel?’ said Bray.", "잔인해 보이지 않소?\" 브레이가 말했어."]], [["‘No!’ replied Ralph, boldly.", "\"아니오!\" 랄프가 당당하게 대답했어."]], [["‘I say it does,’ retorted Bray, with a show of much irritation.", "\"내 말은 잔인하다는 거요.\" 브레이가 잔뜩 짜증 난 기색으로 받아쳤어."], ["‘It is a cruel thing, by all that’s bad and treacherous!’", "\"이건 잔인한 짓이오, 온갖 못되고 배신스러운 것에 걸고 말이지!\""]], [["When men are about to commit, or to sanction the commission of some injustice, it is not uncommon for them to express pity for the object either of that or some parallel proceeding, and to feel themselves, at the time, quite virtuous and moral, and immensely superior to those who express no pity at all. This is a kind of upholding of faith above works, and is very comfortable. To do Ralph Nickleby justice, he seldom practised this sort of dissimulation; but he understood those who did, and therefore suffered Bray to say, again and again, with great vehemence, that they were jointly doing a very cruel thing, before he again offered to interpose a word.", "사람들이 어떤 부당한 짓을 저지르려 하거나 그런 짓을 거들려 할 때, 바로 그 대상이나 그와 비슷한 처지에 놓인 이를 두고 동정을 늘어놓는 일은 드물지 않아. 그러면서 그 순간 자기가 아주 고결하고 도덕적인 사람이라고, 동정심이라곤 전혀 드러내지 않는 자들보다 한참 낫다고 여기는 거지. 이건 행실보다 믿음을 떠받드는 격인데, 마음은 무척 편안하지. 랄프 니클비를 공정하게 말하자면, 그는 이런 식의 위선을 좀처럼 부리지 않았어. 하지만 그러는 사람들 속내는 다 꿰고 있었지. 그래서 브레이가 둘이 함께 아주 잔인한 짓을 하고 있다고 몇 번이고 열을 올려 떠들도록 내버려 뒀어. 다시 한마디 끼어들기 전까지 말이야."]], [["‘You see what a dry, shrivelled, withered old chip it is,’ returned Ralph, when the other was at length silent.", "\"보다시피 저건 바짝 마르고 쪼그라들고 시들어 빠진 늙은 부스러기일 뿐이오.\""], ["‘If he were younger, it might be cruel, but as it is--harkee, Mr.", "마침내 상대가 입을 다물자 랄프가 대꾸했어. \"저 사람이 젊다면야 잔인할 수도 있겠지."], ["Bray, he’ll die soon, and leave her a rich young widow!", "하지만 지금 형편이 이러니 ― 들어 봐요, 브레이 씨, 저 사람은 곧 죽을 거고, 그러면 그 애한테 젊고 부유한 과부 자리를 남겨 주는 거요!"], ["Miss Madeline consults your tastes this time; let her consult her own next.’", "매들린 양은 이번엔 당신 뜻을 따르는 거고, 다음번엔 제 뜻대로 하라고 하면 되지.\""]], [["‘True, true,’ said Bray, biting his nails, and plainly very ill at ease.", "\"맞아, 맞는 말이오.\" 브레이가 손톱을 깨물며 말했어."], ["‘I couldn’t do anything better for her than advise her to accept these proposals, could I?", "영 마음이 편치 않은 게 빤히 보였지. \"그 애를 위해서 이 청혼을 받아들이라고 권하는 것보다 더 나은 일은 없지 않겠소?"], ["Now, I ask you, Nickleby, as a man of the world; could I?’", "자, 당신한테 묻겠소, 니클비, 세상 물정 아는 사람으로서 말이오. 더 나은 게 있겠소?\""]], [["‘Surely not,’ answered Ralph. ‘I tell you what, sir; there are a hundred fathers, within a circuit of five miles from this place; well off; good, rich, substantial men; who would gladly give their daughters, and their own ears with them, to that very man yonder, ape and mummy as he looks.’", "\"당연히 없지요.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"내 말 들어 봐요, 선생. 여기서 오 마일 반경 안에 아버지가 백 명은 있을 거요. 형편이 넉넉하고, 좋은 집안에, 부유하고 든든한 사람들이지. 그 사람들이라면 기꺼이 제 딸을, 거기다 제 두 귀까지 얹어서, 저기 저 사람한테 줄 거요. 원숭이에 미라 같은 몰골이긴 해도 말이오.\""]], [["‘So there are!’ exclaimed Bray, eagerly catching at anything which seemed a justification of himself.", "\"정말 그렇소!\" 브레이가 자신을 정당화해 줄 만한 것이라면 뭐든 덥석 붙들며 외쳤어."], ["‘And so I told her, both last night and today.’", "\"나도 그 애한테 그렇게 말했소, 어젯밤에도 오늘도 말이오.\""]], [["‘You told her truth,’ said Ralph, ‘and did well to do so; though I must say, at the same time, that if I had a daughter, and my freedom, pleasure, nay, my very health and life, depended on her taking a husband whom I pointed out, I should hope it would not be necessary to advance any other arguments to induce her to consent to my wishes.’", "\"바른말을 한 거요.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"잘하셨소. 하지만 동시에 한마디 해야겠는데, 만약 나한테 딸이 있고, 내 자유와 즐거움, 아니 내 건강과 목숨까지도 그 애가 내가 점찍어 준 남편을 받아들이느냐에 달려 있다면, 내 바람에 따르라고 그 애를 설득하느라 다른 말을 더 늘어놓을 필요는 없었으면 좋겠소.\""]], [["Bray looked at Ralph as if to see whether he spoke in earnest, and having nodded twice or thrice in unqualified assent to what had fallen from him, said:", "브레이는 랄프가 진심으로 하는 말인지 살피려는 듯 그를 쳐다봤어. 그러고는 그 말에 두말없이 동의한다는 듯 두세 번 고개를 끄덕이더니 이렇게 말했어."]], [["‘I must go upstairs for a few minutes, to finish dressing.", "\"옷매무새를 마저 다듬으러 잠깐 위층에 올라갔다 와야겠소. 내려올 때 매들린을 데리고 오리다."], ["When I come down, I’ll bring Madeline with me.", "그런데 말이오, 어젯밤에 아주 이상한 꿈을 꿨는데, 지금 이 순간까지 까맣게 잊고 있었지 뭐요. 꿈에서 바로 오늘 아침이었고, 당신과 내가 방금처럼 이야기를 나누고 있었소."], ["Do you know, I had a very strange dream last night, which I have not remembered till this instant.", "그러다 내가 위층으로 올라갔지. 지금 올라가려는 바로 그 일 때문에 말이오."], ["I dreamt that it was this morning, and you and I had been talking as we have been this minute; that I went upstairs, for the very purpose for which I am going now; and that as I stretched out my hand to take Madeline’s, and lead her down, the floor sunk with me, and after falling from such an indescribable and tremendous height as the imagination scarcely conceives, except in dreams, I alighted in a grave.’", "그런데 매들린의 손을 잡아 데리고 내려오려고 손을 뻗는 순간, 바닥이 나와 함께 푹 꺼지는 거요. 그리고 꿈에서나 그려 볼 수 있을, 상상조차 하기 힘든 어마어마한 높이에서 떨어진 끝에, 무덤 속에 내려앉았지 뭐요.\""]], [["‘And you awoke, and found you were lying on your back, or with your head hanging over the bedside, or suffering some pain from indigestion?’ said Ralph.", "\"그러다 잠에서 깨어 보니 똑바로 누워 있었거나, 머리가 침대 밖으로 늘어져 있었거나, 체해서 어디가 좀 아팠던 거겠지요?\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘Pshaw, Mr. Bray!", "\"쳇, 브레이 씨!"], ["Do as I do (you will have the opportunity, now that a constant round of pleasure and enjoyment opens upon you), and, occupying yourself a little more by day, have no time to think of what you dream by night.’", "나처럼 해 봐요. (이제 끊임없는 즐거움과 기쁨이 당신 앞에 펼쳐질 테니 기회가 있을 거요.) 낮에 좀 더 바쁘게 지내면서, 밤에 꾼 꿈 따위를 생각할 틈을 두지 마시오.\""]], [["Ralph followed him, with a steady look, to the door; and, turning to the bridegroom, when they were again alone, said,", "랄프는 그가 문까지 가는 모습을 한결같은 눈길로 좇았어. 그러고는 다시 둘만 남게 되자 신랑 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했어."]], [["‘Mark my words, Gride, you won’t have to pay HIS annuity very long.", "\"내 말 잘 새겨 두게, 그라이드. 자네는 저 사람한테 연금을 오래 낼 일은 없을 거야."], ["You have the devil’s luck in bargains, always.", "자네는 흥정에서는 늘 악마 같은 운을 타고났으니 말이야."], ["If he is not booked to make the long voyage before many months are past and gone, I wear an orange for a head!’", "저 사람이 몇 달 안에 저세상으로 떠나는 긴 여행 명단에 오르지 않는다면, 내 머리에 오렌지를 얹고 다니겠네!\""]], [["To this prophecy, so agreeable to his ears, Arthur returned no answer than a cackle of great delight.", "귀에 어찌나 듣기 좋은 예언이었던지, 아서는 무척 기뻐 깔깔대는 것 말고는 아무 대답도 하지 않았어. 랄프가 의자에 털썩 몸을 던지자, 두 사람은 깊은 침묵 속에 앉아 기다렸지."], ["Ralph, throwing himself into a chair, they both sat waiting in profound silence.", "랄프는 입가에 비웃음을 띤 채, 그날 달라진 브레이의 태도를 생각하고 있었어."], ["Ralph was thinking, with a sneer upon his lips, on the altered manner of Bray that day, and how soon their fellowship in a bad design had lowered his pride and established a familiarity between them, when his attentive ear caught the rustling of a female dress upon the stairs, and the footstep of a man.", "나쁜 음모에 함께 발을 담그자 그자의 자존심이 어찌 그리 금세 꺾이고 둘 사이에 허물없는 사이가 생겨났는지를 말이야. 그런데 그때 귀 기울이고 있던 그의 귀에 계단을 스치는 여자 옷자락 소리와 한 남자의 발소리가 들려왔어."]], [["‘Wake up,’ he said, stamping his foot impatiently upon the ground, ‘and be something like life, man, will you?", "\"정신 차리게.\" 랄프가 조바심에 발을 쿵 구르며 말했어."], ["They are here.", "\"산 사람처럼 좀 굴어 봐, 이 사람아, 응?"], ["Urge those dry old bones of yours this way.", "그들이 왔어. 그 메마른 늙은 뼈다귀들을 이리로 좀 끌고 오라고."], ["Quick, man, quick!’", "빨리, 이 사람아, 빨리!\""]], [["Gride shambled forward, and stood, leering and bowing, close by Ralph’s side, when the door opened and there entered in haste--not Bray and his daughter, but Nicholas and his sister Kate.", "그라이드가 비척비척 앞으로 나와, 히죽거리고 굽실대며 랄프 곁에 바짝 붙어 섰어. 그때 문이 열리고 누군가 황급히 들어왔지 ― 그런데 브레이와 그 딸이 아니라, 니콜라스와 그의 누이 케이트였어."]], [["If some tremendous apparition from the world of shadows had suddenly presented itself before him, Ralph Nickleby could not have been more thunder-stricken than he was by this surprise.", "그림자의 세계에서 어떤 무시무시한 유령이 갑자기 눈앞에 나타났다 해도, 랄프 니클비가 이 뜻밖의 일에 받은 충격만큼 벼락을 맞은 듯하지는 않았을 거야. 두 손이 힘없이 옆구리로 툭 떨어졌고, 그는 비틀비틀 뒷걸음질 쳤지."], ["His hands fell powerless by his side, he reeled back; and with open mouth, and a face of ashy paleness, stood gazing at them in speechless rage: his eyes so prominent, and his face so convulsed and changed by the passions which raged within him, that it would have been difficult to recognise in him the same stern, composed, hard-featured man he had been not a minute ago.", "그러고는 입을 떡 벌리고 잿빛으로 새하얗게 질린 얼굴로, 말도 못 한 채 분노에 차서 그들을 노려보고 섰어. 두 눈이 어찌나 튀어나오고, 속에서 들끓는 격정에 얼굴이 어찌나 일그러지고 변했던지, 일 분 전만 해도 그였던 그 엄하고 침착하고 굳은 인상의 사내라고는 알아보기 어려울 정도였어."]], [["‘The man that came to me last night,’ whispered Gride, plucking at his elbow.", "\"어젯밤에 나한테 찾아온 그놈이에요.\" 그라이드가 그의 팔꿈치를 잡아당기며 속삭였어."], ["‘The man that came to me last night!’", "\"어젯밤에 나한테 찾아온 그놈!\""]], [["‘I see,’ muttered Ralph, ‘I know!", "\"알아.\" 랄프가 중얼거렸어."], ["I might have guessed as much before.", "\"안다고! 진작에 짐작했어야 했는데."], ["Across my every path, at every turn, go where I will, do what I may, he comes!’", "내가 가는 길마다, 모퉁이를 돌 때마다, 어딜 가든 무슨 짓을 하든, 저놈이 나타난단 말이야!\""]], [["The absence of all colour from the face; the dilated nostril; the quivering of the lips which, though set firmly against each other, would not be still; showed what emotions were struggling for the mastery with Nicholas.", "얼굴에서 핏기가 싹 가신 것, 벌름거리는 콧구멍, 굳게 맞물렸으면서도 가만있지 못하고 떨리는 입술 ― 이런 것들이 니콜라스 안에서 어떤 감정들이 서로 주도권을 다투고 있는지 드러내 줬어. 하지만 그는 그것들을 꾹 눌렀어."], ["But he kept them down, and gently pressing Kate’s arm to reassure her, stood erect and undaunted, front to front with his unworthy relative.", "그리고 케이트를 안심시키려 그 팔을 살며시 잡아 주고는, 똑바로 서서 한 점 흔들림 없이, 못난 친척과 정면으로 마주 섰지."]], [["As the brother and sister stood side by side, with a gallant bearing which became them well, a close likeness between them was apparent, which many, had they only seen them apart, might have failed to remark.", "오누이가 나란히, 그들에게 잘 어울리는 늠름한 자태로 서 있으니, 둘 사이의 빼닮은 모습이 또렷이 드러났어. 따로따로 봤더라면 많은 사람들이 알아채지 못했을 그런 닮음이었지."], ["The air, carriage, and very look and expression of the brother were all reflected in the sister, but softened and refined to the nicest limit of feminine delicacy and attraction.", "오빠의 분위기와 몸가짐, 그리고 그 눈빛과 표정까지도 누이에게 고스란히 비쳤어. 다만 여성스러운 섬세함과 매력의 가장 고운 경계까지 부드럽고 우아하게 다듬어져 있었지."], ["More striking still was some indefinable resemblance, in the face of Ralph, to both.", "더 놀라운 건, 랄프의 얼굴에도 두 사람을 닮은 무언가가 어렴풋이 서려 있다는 거였어."], ["While they had never looked more handsome, nor he more ugly; while they had never held themselves more proudly, nor he shrunk half so low; there never had been a time when this resemblance was so perceptible, or when all the worst characteristics of a face rendered coarse and harsh by evil thoughts were half so manifest as now.", "오누이가 그 어느 때보다 더 잘생겨 보이고 랄프는 그 어느 때보다 더 추해 보이는 순간, 오누이가 그 어느 때보다 더 당당하게 몸을 세우고 랄프는 그 절반만큼도 못 되게 움츠러든 순간, 바로 그때만큼 이 닮음이 또렷하게 드러난 적은 없었어. 또 사악한 생각들로 거칠고 험악해진 얼굴의 온갖 추한 특징이, 그때 절반만큼이라도 뚜렷하게 나타난 적도 없었지."]], [["‘Away!’ was the first word he could utter as he literally gnashed his teeth.", "\"꺼져!\" 그가 정말로 이를 갈며 처음으로 내뱉을 수 있었던 말이었어."], ["‘Away!", "\"꺼지라고!"], ["What brings you here?", "여긴 무슨 일로 왔어?"], ["Liar, scoundrel, dastard, thief!’", "거짓말쟁이, 악당, 비겁자, 도둑놈!\""]], [["‘I come here,’ said Nicholas in a low deep voice, ‘to save your victim if I can. Liar and scoundrel you are, in every action of your life; theft is your trade; and double dastard you must be, or you were not here today. Hard words will not move me, nor would hard blows. Here I stand, and will, till I have done my errand.’", "\"내가 여기 온 건,\" 니콜라스가 낮고 깊은 목소리로 말했어. \"할 수만 있다면 당신 희생자를 구하기 위해서야. 당신이야말로 평생 하는 짓마다 거짓말쟁이에 악당이지. 도둑질은 당신 생업이고. 게다가 오늘 여기 와 있는 걸 보면 갑절로 비겁한 자임에 틀림없어. 모진 말로도 날 움직이지 못해. 모진 매질로도 마찬가지지. 나는 여기 서서, 내 할 일을 끝낼 때까지 떠나지 않을 거야.\""]], [["‘Girl!’ said Ralph, ‘retire!", "\"아가씨!\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["We can use force to him, but I would not hurt you if I could help it.", "\"물러가시지! 저놈한테는 힘을 쓸 수도 있지만, 너한테는 되도록 손대고 싶지 않으니까."], ["Retire, you weak and silly wench, and leave this dog to be dealt with as he deserves.’", "물러가라, 이 가냘프고 어리석은 계집애야. 이 개 같은 놈은 마땅한 대로 처리하게 내버려 두고 말이야.\""]], [["‘I will not retire,’ cried Kate, with flashing eyes and the red blood mantling in her cheeks.", "\"물러가지 않겠어요.\" 케이트가 눈을 번뜩이며, 두 볼에 붉은 피가 확 오르며 외쳤어."], ["‘You will do him no hurt that he will not repay.", "\"당신이 오빠한테 무슨 해를 끼친들 오빠가 갚지 못할 건 없어요. 나한테는 힘을 쓸 수도 있겠죠."], ["You may use force with me; I think you will, for I AM a girl, and that would well become you.", "그러실 것 같네요. 나는 여자니까, 그게 당신한테 딱 어울리는 짓이겠죠."], ["But if I have a girl’s weakness, I have a woman’s heart, and it is not you who in a cause like this can turn that from its purpose.’", "하지만 나한테 여자의 연약함이 있다면, 여자의 마음도 있어요. 이런 일에서 그 마음을 본래 뜻에서 돌려놓을 수 있는 사람은 당신이 아니에요.\""]], [["‘And what may your purpose be, most lofty lady?’ said Ralph.", "\"그래, 그 뜻이라는 게 뭔지 들어나 보지, 이 도도하기 짝이 없는 아가씨야?\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘To offer to the unhappy subject of your treachery, at this last moment,’ replied Nicholas, ‘a refuge and a home.", "\"당신의 배신에 희생된 그 불행한 사람한테, 이 마지막 순간에,\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["If the near prospect of such a husband as you have provided will not prevail upon her, I hope she may be moved by the prayers and entreaties of one of her own sex.", "\"피난처와 보금자리를 내어 주려는 거야. 당신이 마련한 그런 남편이 코앞에 닥쳤다는 사실로도 그분 마음을 돌릴 수 없다면, 같은 여자의 간청과 호소에는 마음이 움직이기를 바라는 거지."], ["At all events they shall be tried.", "어찌 됐든 한번 해 볼 거야."], ["I myself, avowing to her father from whom I come and by whom I am commissioned, will render it an act of greater baseness, meanness, and cruelty in him if he still dares to force this marriage on.", "나도 직접, 누구한테서 와서 누구의 부탁을 받고 왔는지 그분 아버지한테 밝히겠어. 그래도 이 결혼을 기어이 밀어붙인다면, 그건 그 사람에게 더 큰 비열함이고 치사함이고 잔인함이 될 테니까."], ["Here I wait to see him and his daughter.", "나는 그분과 그분 따님을 만나려고 여기서 기다리는 거야."], ["For this I came and brought my sister even into your presence.", "이러려고 내가 왔고, 누이까지 당신 앞에 데려온 거라고."], ["Our purpose is not to see or speak with you; therefore to you we stoop to say no more.’", "우리 목적은 당신을 보거나 당신과 이야기하려는 게 아니야. 그러니 당신한테는 더 말하기도 아까워.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ said Ralph.", "\"그래?\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘You persist in remaining here, ma’am, do you?’", "\"기어이 여기 남아 있겠다, 이거지, 아가씨?\""]], [["His niece’s bosom heaved with the indignant excitement into which he had lashed her, but she gave him no reply.", "조카딸의 가슴은 그가 부추겨 놓은 분한 격정으로 들썩였지만, 그녀는 그에게 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았어."]], [["‘Now, Gride, see here,’ said Ralph. ‘This fellow--I grieve to say my brother’s son: a reprobate and profligate, stained with every mean and selfish crime--this fellow, coming here today to disturb a solemn ceremony, and knowing that the consequence of his presenting himself in another man’s house at such a time, and persisting in remaining there, must be his being kicked into the streets and dragged through them like the vagabond he is--this fellow, mark you, brings with him his sister as a protection, thinking we would not expose a silly girl to the degradation and indignity which is no novelty to him; and, even after I have warned her of what must ensue, he still keeps her by him, as you see, and clings to her apron-strings like a cowardly boy to his mother’s. Is not this a pretty fellow to talk as big as you have heard him now?’", "\"자, 그라이드, 여기 좀 보게.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"이놈은 ― 내 형의 아들이라고 말하기도 슬프지만 ― 온갖 비열하고 이기적인 죄로 더럽혀진, 타락하고 방탕한 놈이야. 이놈이 오늘 여기 와서 엄숙한 예식을 방해하는 거지. 이런 때 남의 집에 제 발로 들어와 기어이 버티고 있으면, 거리로 걷어차여 나가 제 꼴에 걸맞은 부랑자처럼 질질 끌려다닐 게 뻔한데도 말이야. 자, 잘 보게, 이놈이 제 누이를 방패 삼아 데려왔어. 우리가 어리석은 계집애를 ― 이놈한테야 새삼스럽지도 않은 ― 그 굴욕과 모욕에 내놓지 못할 거라고 여긴 거지. 게다가 내가 이 애한테 무슨 일이 닥칠지 경고까지 했는데도, 보다시피 이놈은 여태 이 애를 곁에 붙들어 두고, 겁쟁이 사내애가 제 엄마 치맛자락에 매달리듯 이 애의 앞치마 끈에 매달려 있단 말이야. 방금 자네가 들은 것처럼 큰소리치는 놈치고는 참 가관 아닌가?\""]], [["‘And as I heard him last night,’ said Arthur Gride; ‘as I heard him last night when he sneaked into my house, and--he! he!", "\"어젯밤에 내가 들은 것처럼 말이지요.\""], ["he!--very soon sneaked out again, when I nearly frightened him to death.", "아서 그라이드가 말했어."], ["And HE wanting to marry Miss Madeline too!", "\"어젯밤에 이놈이 살금살금 내 집에 기어들었다가 ― 히!"], ["Oh dear!", "히! 히!"], ["Is there anything else he’d like?", "― 내가 죽도록 겁을 줬더니 금세 다시 슬그머니 빠져나갔을 때 말이에요. 그러면서 이놈도 매들린 양과 결혼하고 싶어 한다 이거지요!"], ["Anything else we can do for him, besides giving her up?", "아이고 참! 또 뭐 바라는 거 없답니까?"], ["Would he like his debts paid and his house furnished, and a few bank notes for shaving paper if he shaves at all?", "그 여자를 포기하는 거 말고, 우리가 또 뭘 해 주면 될까요? 빚을 갚아 주고, 집에 가구도 들여 주고, 면도라도 한다면 면도지로 쓸 지폐 몇 장 줄까요?"], ["He!", "히!"], ["he!", "히!"], ["he!’", "히!\""]], [["‘You will remain, girl, will you?’ said Ralph, turning upon Kate again, ‘to be hauled downstairs like a drunken drab, as I swear you shall if you stop here?", "\"기어이 남겠다, 아가씨, 그렇지?\" 랄프가 다시 케이트를 돌아보며 말했어."], ["No answer! Thank your brother for what follows.", "\"여기 버티고 있으면, 술 취한 갈보처럼 계단 아래로 질질 끌려 내려가게 될 거야, 내 맹세코 그렇게 하고 말 테니."], ["Gride, call down Bray--and not his daughter.", "대답이 없네! 그렇게 되면 네 오빠한테나 고마워해라."], ["Let them keep her above.’", "그라이드, 브레이를 불러 내려오게 해 ― 딸은 말고. 그 애는 위층에 붙들어 두라고 하게.\""]], [["‘If you value your head,’ said Nicholas, taking up a position before the door, and speaking in the same low voice in which he had spoken before, and with no more outward passion than he had before displayed; ‘stay where you are!’", "\"네 머리가 아깝거든,\" 니콜라스가 문 앞에 자리를 잡고 서서, 아까처럼 낮은 목소리로, 아까 드러낸 것 이상으로 겉으로 격정을 드러내지 않고 말했어. \"있는 자리에 그대로 있어!\""]], [["‘Mind me, and not him, and call down Bray,’ said Ralph.", "\"저놈 말고 내 말을 들어, 브레이를 불러 내려오게 하라고.\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Mind yourself rather than either of us, and stay where you are!’ said Nicholas.", "\"우리 둘 중 누구 말도 듣지 말고 네 한 몸이나 챙겨. 그러니 있는 자리에 그대로 있어!\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Will you call down Bray?’ cried Ralph.", "\"브레이를 불러 내려오게 할 텐가?\" 랄프가 외쳤어."]], [["‘Remember that you come near me at your peril,’ said Nicholas.", "\"내게 다가오면 무슨 일을 당할지 명심하라고.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["Gride hesitated.", "그라이드가 망설였어."], ["Ralph being, by this time, as furious as a baffled tiger, made for the door, and, attempting to pass Kate, clasped her arm roughly with his hand.", "이때쯤 랄프는 함정에 빠진 호랑이처럼 사나워져서, 문 쪽으로 돌진했지. 그러고는 케이트를 지나치려다 그 팔을 손으로 거칠게 움켜쥐었어."], ["Nicholas, with his eyes darting fire, seized him by the collar.", "니콜라스가 두 눈에 불을 뿜으며 그의 멱살을 잡았지."], ["At that moment, a heavy body fell with great violence on the floor above, and, in an instant afterwards, was heard a most appalling and terrific scream.", "바로 그 순간, 위층에서 묵직한 것이 엄청난 소리를 내며 바닥에 쿵 쓰러졌어. 그리고 곧이어, 더없이 소름 끼치고 끔찍한 비명이 들려왔지."]], [["They all stood still, and gazed upon each other. Scream succeeded scream; a heavy pattering of feet succeeded; and many shrill voices clamouring together were heard to cry, ‘He is dead!’", "그들은 모두 멈춰 서서 서로를 바라봤어. 비명에 비명이 이어졌지. 뒤이어 우당탕탕 요란한 발소리가 들렸어. 그리고 여러 날카로운 목소리가 한꺼번에 아우성치며 외치는 게 들렸어. \"돌아가셨어!\""]], [["‘Stand off!’ cried Nicholas, letting loose all the passion he had restrained till now; ‘if this is what I scarcely dare to hope it is, you are caught, villains, in your own toils.’", "\"비켜!\" 니콜라스가 그때껏 억눌렀던 격정을 죄다 터뜨리며 외쳤어. \"내가 감히 바라기도 두려웠던 그 일이라면, 이 악당들, 너희는 너희가 놓은 덫에 걸린 거다.\""]], [["He burst from the room, and, darting upstairs to the quarter from whence the noise proceeded, forced his way through a crowd of persons who quite filled a small bed-chamber, and found Bray lying on the floor quite dead; his daughter clinging to the body.", "그는 방을 박차고 나가, 소리가 난 쪽을 향해 위층으로 내달렸어. 그리고 자그마한 침실을 꽉 메운 사람들을 헤치고 들어갔지. 거기엔 브레이가 바닥에 쓰러져 완전히 숨이 끊어진 채 누워 있었어. 그의 딸은 그 주검에 매달려 있었지."]], [["‘How did this happen?’ he cried, looking wildly about him.", "\"어쩌다 이렇게 됐습니까?\" 그가 사방을 미친 듯이 둘러보며 외쳤어."]], [["Several voices answered together, that he had been observed, through the half-opened door, reclining in a strange and uneasy position upon a chair; that he had been spoken to several times, and not answering, was supposed to be asleep, until some person going in and shaking him by the arm, he fell heavily to the ground and was discovered to be dead.", "여러 목소리가 한꺼번에 대답했어. 반쯤 열린 문틈으로 보니 그가 이상하고 불편한 자세로 의자에 기대 있더라고. 몇 번이나 말을 걸어도 대답이 없어서 잠든 줄로만 알았는데, 누군가 들어가 팔을 흔들었더니 바닥으로 쿵 쓰러졌고, 그제야 숨이 끊어진 걸 알았다는 거야."]], [["‘Who is the owner of this house?’ said Nicholas, hastily.", "\"이 집 주인이 누굽니까?\" 니콜라스가 다급히 말했어."]], [["An elderly woman was pointed out to him; and to her he said, as he knelt down and gently unwound Madeline’s arms from the lifeless mass round which they were entwined: ‘I represent this lady’s nearest friends, as her servant here knows, and must remove her from this dreadful scene.", "사람들이 한 나이 든 여자를 가리켰어. 니콜라스는 무릎을 꿇고, 매들린의 두 팔을 그것이 휘감고 있던 싸늘한 주검에서 살며시 풀어내면서 그 여자한테 말했어."], ["This is my sister to whose charge you confide her.", "\"여기 이 아가씨의 하녀도 알다시피, 저는 이 아가씨의 가장 가까운 친구들을 대신해서 온 사람입니다. 이 끔찍한 자리에서 아가씨를 데려가야겠어요."], ["My name and address are upon that card, and you shall receive from me all necessary directions for the arrangements that must be made.", "여기 이 사람은 제 누이로, 아가씨를 맡길 사람입니다. 제 이름과 주소는 저 명함에 적혀 있어요."], ["Stand aside, every one of you, and give me room and air for God’s sake!’", "처리해야 할 일들에 필요한 지시는 제가 다 알려 드리겠습니다. 다들 비켜 주세요, 제발 제가 움직일 자리와 숨 쉴 틈을 좀 주세요!\""]], [["The people fell back, scarce wondering more at what had just occurred, than at the excitement and impetuosity of him who spoke. Nicholas, taking the insensible girl in his arms, bore her from the chamber and downstairs into the room he had just quitted, followed by his sister and the faithful servant, whom he charged to procure a coach directly, while he and Kate bent over their beautiful charge and endeavoured, but in vain, to restore her to animation. The girl performed her office with such expedition, that in a very few minutes the coach was ready.", "사람들이 뒤로 물러섰어. 방금 일어난 일 못지않게, 말하는 그 사람의 격앙되고 거센 기세에 놀란 거였지. 니콜라스는 정신을 잃은 아가씨를 두 팔로 안아 들고, 방에서 나와 방금 떠나온 아래층 방으로 데려갔어. 누이와 충직한 하녀가 그 뒤를 따랐지. 그는 하녀한테 당장 마차를 구해 오라고 일렀어. 그러는 동안 그와 케이트는 그 아름다운 아가씨 위로 몸을 숙이고 정신을 차리게 하려 애썼지만, 헛수고였지. 하녀가 어찌나 잽싸게 일을 해냈던지, 몇 분도 안 돼 마차가 준비됐어."]], [["Ralph Nickleby and Gride, stunned and paralysed by the awful event which had so suddenly overthrown their schemes (it would not otherwise, perhaps, have made much impression on them), and carried away by the extraordinary energy and precipitation of Nicholas, which bore down all before him, looked on at these proceedings like men in a dream or trance.", "랄프 니클비와 그라이드는 자신들의 계획을 그토록 갑작스레 뒤엎어 버린 그 끔찍한 사건에 충격받아 마비된 듯했어. (그렇지 않았다면 아마 그 일이 그들에게 그리 큰 충격을 주지는 못했겠지.) 그리고 앞을 가로막는 모든 걸 휩쓸어 버리는 니콜라스의 엄청난 기세와 다급함에 휩쓸려, 두 사람은 꿈을 꾸거나 넋이 나간 사람처럼 이 광경을 멍하니 바라보고 있었어."], ["It was not until every preparation was made for Madeline’s immediate removal that Ralph broke silence by declaring she should not be taken away.", "매들린을 당장 데려갈 채비가 다 끝나고 나서야, 랄프가 침묵을 깨고 그 애를 데려가서는 안 된다고 선언했지."]], [["‘Who says so?’ cried Nicholas, rising from his knee and confronting them, but still retaining Madeline’s lifeless hand in his.", "\"누가 그래?\" 니콜라스가 무릎을 펴고 일어나 그들과 맞서며 외쳤어. 그러면서도 매들린의 싸늘한 손은 여전히 제 손에 쥐고 있었지."]], [["‘I!’ answered Ralph, hoarsely.", "\"내가!\" 랄프가 쉰 목소리로 대답했어."]], [["‘Hush, hush!’ cried the terrified Gride, catching him by the arm again.", "\"쉿, 쉿!\" 겁에 질린 그라이드가 다시 그의 팔을 붙잡으며 외쳤어."], ["‘Hear what he says.’", "\"저놈이 하는 말을 들어 봐요.\""]], [["‘Ay!’ said Nicholas, extending his disengaged hand in the air, ‘hear what he says.", "\"그래!\" 니콜라스가 잡히지 않은 한 손을 허공에 뻗으며 말했어."], ["That both your debts are paid in the one great debt of nature.", "\"저 사람이 하는 말을 들어 봐. 당신들 두 사람의 빚이 다 자연이라는 그 큰 빚 하나로 갚아졌다고."], ["That the bond, due today at twelve, is now waste paper.", "오늘 열두 시가 만기인 그 차용증은 이제 휴지 조각이 됐다고."], ["That your contemplated fraud shall be discovered yet.", "당신들이 꾸민 사기는 끝내 들통날 거라고."], ["That your schemes are known to man, and overthrown by Heaven.", "당신들 계략은 세상에 다 알려졌고, 하늘이 그걸 뒤엎어 버렸다고."], ["Wretches, that he defies you both to do your worst.’", "이 비열한 자들아, 저 사람은 너희 둘한테 할 테면 어디 마음껏 해 보라고 도전하는 거야.\""]], [["‘This man,’ said Ralph, in a voice scarcely intelligible, ‘this man claims his wife, and he shall have her.’", "\"이 사람이,\" 랄프가 거의 알아듣기 힘든 목소리로 말했어. \"이 사람이 자기 아내를 데려가겠다는데, 데려가게 둬야지.\""]], [["‘That man claims what is not his, and he should not have her if he were fifty men, with fifty more to back him,’ said Nicholas.", "\"저 사람은 제 것도 아닌 걸 데려가겠다는 거고, 설령 쉰 사람이고 거기다 뒤를 받쳐 줄 쉰 사람이 더 있다 해도 데려가지 못해.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Who shall prevent him?’", "\"누가 못 데려가게 한다는 거냐?\""]], [["‘I will.’", "\"내가.\""]], [["‘By what right I should like to know,’ said Ralph.", "\"무슨 권리로 그러는지 한번 들어 보자.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘By what right I ask?’", "\"무슨 권리로 그러느냐 말이다.\""]], [["‘By this right.", "\"이런 권리로."], ["That, knowing what I do, you dare not tempt me further,’ said Nicholas, ‘and by this better right; that those I serve, and with whom you would have done me base wrong and injury, are her nearest and her dearest friends.", "내가 아는 사실이 있는 한, 당신은 감히 나를 더는 떠보지 못할 거야.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["In their name I bear her hence.", "\"그리고 이 더 떳떳한 권리로. 내가 섬기는 분들, 당신이 나를 비열하게 속이고 해치려 들었던 그분들이야말로 이 아가씨의 가장 가깝고 가장 소중한 친구들이거든."], ["Give way!’", "그분들의 이름으로 내가 아가씨를 여기서 데려가는 거야. 비켜!\""]], [["‘One word!’ cried Ralph, foaming at the mouth.", "\"한마디만!\" 랄프가 입에 거품을 물고 외쳤어."]], [["‘Not one,’ replied Nicholas, ‘I will not hear of one--save this.", "\"한마디도 안 돼.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["Look to yourself, and heed this warning that I give you!", "\"한마디도 듣지 않겠어 ― 이 말만 빼고. 당신 앞가림이나 잘하고, 내가 하는 이 경고를 새겨들어!"], ["Your day is past, and night is comin’ on.’", "당신 세상은 다 갔고, 밤이 다가오고 있으니까.\""]], [["‘My curse, my bitter, deadly curse, upon you, boy!’", "\"내 저주를, 모질고 지독한 저주를, 네놈한테 퍼붓는다, 이놈!\""]], [["‘Whence will curses come at your command? Or what avails a curse or blessing from a man like you? I tell you, that misfortune and discovery are thickening about your head; that the structures you have raised, through all your ill-spent life, are crumbling into dust; that your path is beset with spies; that this very day, ten thousand pounds of your hoarded wealth have gone in one great crash!’", "\"당신이 명령한다고 저주가 어디서 떨어지기라도 한답디까? 아니, 당신 같은 사람한테서 나온 저주든 축복이든 무슨 소용이 있겠어? 똑똑히 말해 두지. 불운과 파멸이 당신 머리 위로 점점 짙게 몰려들고 있어. 당신이 그 못된 평생에 걸쳐 쌓아 올린 그 모든 것들이 먼지로 부서지고 있다고. 당신이 가는 길에는 첩자들이 깔려 있고. 바로 오늘, 당신이 쌓아 둔 재산 중 만 파운드가 단번에 와르르 날아갔단 말이야!\""]], [["‘’Tis false!’ cried Ralph, shrinking back.", "\"거짓말이다!\" 랄프가 움찔 뒷걸음질 치며 외쳤어."]], [["‘’Tis true, and you shall find it so.", "\"사실이야, 곧 그렇다는 걸 알게 될 거야. 더는 입 아프게 떠들 말도 없어."], ["I have no more words to waste.", "문에서 비켜. 케이트, 네가 먼저 가."], ["Stand from the door. Kate, do you go first.", "이 아가씨한테든, 저 여자한테든, 나한테든 손 하나 대지 마."], ["Lay not a hand on her, or on that woman, or on me, or so much a brush their garments as they pass you by!--You let them pass, and he blocks the door again!’", "지나갈 때 옷자락 하나 스치지도 말라고! ― 비켜서 지나가게 두나 했더니, 저자가 또 문을 막아서는군!\""]], [["Arthur Gride happened to be in the doorway, but whether intentionally or from confusion was not quite apparent.", "아서 그라이드가 마침 문간에 서 있었어. 일부러 그런 건지 정신이 없어서 그런 건지는 분명치 않았지."], ["Nicholas swung him away, with such violence as to cause him to spin round the room until he was caught by a sharp angle of the wall, and there knocked down; and then taking his beautiful burden in his arms rushed out.", "니콜라스가 그를 어찌나 거세게 밀쳐 냈던지, 그는 방 안을 빙그르르 돌다가 벽 모서리에 부딪혀 그대로 쿵 쓰러졌어. 그러고 나서 니콜라스는 그 아름다운 짐을 두 팔에 안고 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔지."], ["No one cared to stop him, if any were so disposed.", "막으려는 사람이 있었다 해도, 아무도 그를 막으려 들지 않았어."], ["Making his way through a mob of people, whom a report of the circumstances had attracted round the house, and carrying Madeline, in his excitement, as easily as if she were an infant, he reached the coach in which Kate and the girl were already waiting, and, confiding his charge to them, jumped up beside the coachman and bade him drive away.", "그 일의 소문을 듣고 집 주위에 몰려든 사람들 틈을 헤치고, 흥분한 나머지 매들린을 갓난아기라도 되는 양 가뿐히 안은 채, 그는 케이트와 하녀가 이미 기다리고 있는 마차에 다다랐어. 그러고는 자기가 안고 온 아가씨를 두 사람한테 맡기고, 마부 옆자리로 훌쩍 뛰어올라 어서 출발하라고 일렀어."]], [["# CHAPTER 55", "# CHAPTER 55"]], [["Of Family Matters, Cares, Hopes, Disappointments, and Sorrows\nAlthough Mrs.", "니클비 부인은 아들과 딸한테서 매들린 브레이의 사연 가운데 그들이 아는 건 빠짐없이 다 들어 두었어."], ["Nickleby had been made acquainted by her son and daughter with every circumstance of Madeline Bray’s history which was known to them; although the responsible situation in which Nicholas stood had been carefully explained to her, and she had been prepared, even for the possible contingency of having to receive the young lady in her own house, improbable as such a result had appeared only a few minutes before it came about, still, Mrs.", "니콜라스가 떠맡게 된 막중한 처지도 차근차근 설명을 들었지. 그 아가씨를 자기 집에 들여야 할지도 모른다는, 일이 벌어지기 몇 분 전만 해도 도무지 그럴 성싶지 않던 일까지도 마음의 준비를 해 둔 참이었어."], ["Nickleby, from the moment when this confidence was first reposed in her, late on the previous evening, had remained in an unsatisfactory and profoundly mystified state, from which no explanations or arguments could relieve her, and which every fresh soliloquy and reflection only aggravated more and more.", "그런데도 니클비 부인은 전날 밤 늦게 처음 이 비밀을 털어놓고 들은 순간부터, 영 개운치 않고 깊은 안갯속에 빠진 듯한 상태에서 헤어나지를 못했어. 아무리 설명을 하고 따져 줘도 풀어 줄 수가 없었고, 혼잣말과 곱씹는 생각이 새로 떠오를 때마다 점점 더 심해지기만 했지."]], [["‘Bless my heart, Kate!’ so the good lady argued; ‘if the Mr.", "\"아이고, 케이트야!\" 이 착한 부인은 이렇게 따져 들었어."], ["Cheerybles don’t want this young lady to be married, why don’t they file a bill against the Lord Chancellor, make her a Chancery ward, and shut her up in the Fleet prison for safety?--I have read of such things in the newspapers a hundred times.", "\"치어리블 형제분이 이 아가씨가 결혼하는 걸 원치 않는다면, 어째서 대법관한테 소송을 걸어서 아가씨를 법정 후견 대상으로 만들고, 안전하게 플리트 감옥에 가둬 두지 않는 거니? 그런 얘기라면 신문에서 수백 번도 더 읽었다."], ["Or, if they are so very fond of her as Nicholas says they are, why don’t they marry her themselves--one of them I mean?", "아니면, 니콜라스 말처럼 그분들이 아가씨를 그렇게나 끔찍이 아낀다면, 어째서 직접 결혼하지 않는 거야? 둘 중 한 분이 말이다."], ["And even supposing they don’t want her to be married, and don’t want to marry her themselves, why in the name of wonder should Nicholas go about the world, forbidding people’s banns?’", "그리고 설령 아가씨가 결혼하는 것도 원치 않고, 직접 결혼할 마음도 없다 쳐도, 도대체 어쩌자고 니콜라스가 온 세상을 돌아다니면서 남의 혼인을 막고 다닌단 말이냐?\""]], [["‘I don’t think you quite understand,’ said Kate, gently.", "\"엄마가 잘 이해를 못 하시는 것 같아요.\" 케이트가 부드럽게 말했어."]], [["‘Well I am sure, Kate, my dear, you’re very polite!’ replied Mrs Nickleby.", "\"어머나 케이트야, 너 정말 말 한번 곱게 한다!\" 니클비 부인이 받아쳤어."], ["‘I have been married myself I hope, and I have seen other people married.", "\"나도 결혼이란 걸 해 봤고, 다른 사람들 결혼하는 것도 봐 왔다."], ["Not understand, indeed!’", "이해를 못 한다니, 원!\""]], [["‘I know you have had great experience, dear mama,’ said Kate; ‘I mean that perhaps you don’t quite understand all the circumstances in this instance.", "\"엄마가 경험이 많으신 건 저도 알아요.\" 케이트가 말했어."], ["We have stated them awkwardly, I dare say.’", "\"제 말은 이번 일의 사정을 다 이해하지 못하실 수도 있다는 거예요. 저희가 어설프게 말씀드린 탓일 거예요.\""]], [["‘That I dare say you have,’ retorted her mother, briskly. ‘That’s very likely. I am not to be held accountable for that; though, at the same time, as the circumstances speak for themselves, I shall take the liberty, my love, of saying that I do understand them, and perfectly well too; whatever you and Nicholas may choose to think to the contrary. Why is such a great fuss made because this Miss Magdalen is going to marry somebody who is older than herself? Your poor papa was older than I was, four years and a half older. Jane Dibabs--the Dibabses lived in the beautiful little thatched white house one story high, covered all over with ivy and creeping plants, with an exquisite little porch with twining honysuckles and all sorts of things: where the earwigs used to fall into one’s tea on a summer evening, and always fell upon their backs and kicked dreadfully, and where the frogs used to get into the rushlight shades when one stopped all night, and sit up and look through the little holes like Christians--Jane Dibabs, SHE married a man who was a great deal older than herself, and WOULD marry him, notwithstanding all that could be said to the contrary, and she was so fond of him that nothing was ever equal to it. There was no fuss made about Jane Dibabs, and her husband was a most honourable and excellent man, and everybody spoke well of him. Then why should there by any fuss about this Magdalen?’", "\"그래, 너희가 그랬겠지.\" 어머니가 톡 쏘아붙였어. \"충분히 그럴 만하지. 그게 내 탓은 아니잖니. 그래도 동시에, 사정이 다 뻔히 보이니까, 얘야, 내가 한마디 해야겠다. 나는 그 사정을 이해한다, 그것도 아주 똑똑히 말이다. 너랑 니콜라스가 아니라고 생각하든 말든 상관없어. 이 매그덜린 아가씨가 자기보다 나이 많은 사람하고 결혼한다고 해서 어째서 이렇게 야단법석을 떠는 거니? 너희 가엾은 아버지도 나보다 나이가 많았다, 네 살 반이나 위였어. 제인 디뱁스 말이다. 디뱁스 집안은 그 예쁘장한 단층 초가집에 살았지. 온통 담쟁이며 덩굴풀로 뒤덮이고, 인동덩굴이 휘감긴 아담한 현관이 딸려 있던 집 말이다. 여름 저녁이면 집게벌레가 찻잔에 툭툭 떨어져서는, 늘 등을 대고 자빠진 채 다리를 버둥거리며 발버둥을 쳤고, 밤새 묵을라치면 개구리가 골풀 등잔갓 속으로 기어들어 가서 떡하니 앉아서는 작은 구멍으로 사람처럼 내다보던 그 집 말이야. 그 제인 디뱁스가, 자기보다 한참 나이 많은 남자랑 결혼했어. 아무리 말려도 기어이 그 사람하고 결혼하겠다고 했지. 어찌나 그 남자를 끔찍이 아끼던지 견줄 데가 없을 정도였다. 제인 디뱁스 때는 아무도 야단법석을 안 떨었어. 게다가 그 남편은 더없이 점잖고 훌륭한 사람이라 누구나 좋게 말했지. 그런데 어째서 이 매그덜린 일로는 이렇게 호들갑을 떠는 거냐?\""]], [["‘Her husband is much older; he is not her own choice; his character is the very reverse of that which you have just described.", "\"그 아가씨 남편은 훨씬 나이가 많은 데다, 아가씨가 직접 고른 사람도 아니에요. 그 사람 성품도 방금 엄마가 말씀하신 거랑은 정반대고요."], ["Don’t you see a broad destinction between the two cases?’ said Kate.", "두 경우가 확연히 다른 게 안 보이세요?\" 케이트가 말했어."]], [["To this, Mrs. Nickleby only replied that she durst say she was very stupid, indeed she had no doubt she was, for her own children almost as much as told her so, every day of her life; to be sure she was a little older than they, and perhaps some foolish people might think she ought reasonably to know best. However, no doubt she was wrong; of course she was; she always was, she couldn’t be right, she couldn’t be expected to be; so she had better not expose herself any more; and to all Kate’s conciliations and concessions for an hour ensuing, the good lady gave no other replies than Oh, certainly, why did they ask HER?, HER opinion was of no consequence, it didn’t matter what SHE said, with many other rejoinders of the same class.", "이 말에 니클비 부인은 그저 이렇게 대꾸할 뿐이었어. 자기가 어지간히 멍청한 모양이다, 정말 의심할 여지가 없다, 제 자식들마저 날마다 거의 대놓고 그렇게 말하지 않느냐고 말이야. 하긴 자기가 저희보다 나이가 좀 많고, 어쩌면 어리석은 사람들은 그러니 당연히 자기가 제일 잘 알아야 한다고 여길지도 모르지만. 그래도 자기가 틀렸겠지, 당연히 틀렸을 거다, 늘 틀리니까, 옳을 리가 없고, 옳기를 바랄 수도 없다고. 그러니 더는 망신을 사지 않는 게 낫겠다는 거였어. 그러고는 그 뒤로 한 시간 동안 케이트가 아무리 달래고 양보해도, 착한 부인은 다른 대꾸 없이 그저 이렇게만 했어. 아이고, 그럼 그렇지, 어째서 나한테 묻는 거니, 내 의견이야 아무 소용도 없는데, 내가 무슨 말을 하든 상관없잖니, 하고 똑같은 식의 대꾸만 잔뜩 늘어놓는 거야."]], [["In this frame of mind (expressed, when she had become too resigned for speech, by nods of the head, upliftings of the eyes, and little beginnings of groans, converted, as they attracted attention, into short coughs), Mrs.", "이런 마음 상태로 (말하기도 너무 단념한 지경이 되자, 고개를 끄덕이고 눈을 치뜨고 끙 하는 신음을 막 시작하다가, 남이 알아채면 얼른 짧은 헛기침으로 바꾸는 식으로 표현하면서) 니클비 부인은 니콜라스와 케이트가 걱정하던 그 아가씨를 데리고 돌아올 때까지 그러고 있었어. 그러다 이제는 자기 존재가 중요하다는 걸 확인한 데다, 그토록 젊고 아름다운 사람이 겪은 시련에 마음이 쓰이게 되니, 부인은 더없는 열성과 정성을 쏟았을 뿐 아니라, 아들이 택한 그 처사를 자기가 추천했노라며 큰 공을 자기 몫으로 돌렸어."], ["Nickleby remained until Nicholas and Kate returned with the object of their solicitude; when, having by this time asserted her own importance, and becoming besides interested in the trials of one so young and beautiful, she not only displayed the utmost zeal and solicitude, but took great credit to herself for recommending the course of procedure which her son had adopted: frequently declaring, with an expressive look, that it was very fortunate things were AS they were: and hinting, that but for great encouragement and wisdom on her own part, they never could have been brought to that pass.", "그러고는 의미심장한 표정으로 자주 이렇게 말하곤 했지. 일이 지금처럼 된 게 정말 다행이다, 자기가 크게 북돋고 지혜를 보태지 않았더라면 결코 여기까지 오지 못했을 거라고 넌지시 비치면서 말이야."]], [["Not to strain the question whether Mrs. Nickleby had or had not any great hand in bringing matters about, it is unquestionable that she had strong ground for exultation.", "니클비 부인이 일을 이렇게 끌어오는 데 정말 큰 몫을 했는지 안 했는지는 굳이 따지지 않더라도, 부인이 우쭐할 만한 확실한 근거가 있었던 건 틀림없어."], ["The brothers, on their return, bestowed such commendations on Nicholas for the part he had taken, and evinced so much joy at the altered state of events and the recovery of their young friend from trials so great and dangers so threatening, that, as she more than once informed her daughter, she now considered the fortunes of the family ‘as good as’ made. Mr.", "형제분은 돌아와서 니콜라스가 맡았던 역할을 어찌나 칭찬했던지, 게다가 상황이 달라지고 그 젊은 친구가 그토록 큰 시련과 위협스러운 위험에서 벗어난 데 어찌나 기뻐했던지, 부인은 딸에게 한두 번이 아니게 일러 주었듯이 이제 집안의 앞날이 \"거의 다 된 거나 마찬가지\"라고 여기게 되었지."], ["Charles Cheeryble, indeed, Mrs. Nickleby positively asserted, had, in the first transports of his surprise and delight, ‘as good as’ said so.", "사실 찰스 치어리블 씨가 놀라움과 기쁨이 처음 북받쳐 오를 때 \"거의 다 된 거나 마찬가지\"라고 말씀하셨다고, 니클비 부인은 딱 잘라 주장했어."], ["Without precisely explaining what this qualification meant, she subsided, whenever she mentioned the subject, into such a mysterious and important state, and had such visions of wealth and dignity in perspective, that (vague and clouded though they were) she was, at such times, almost as happy as if she had really been permanently provided for, on a scale of great splendour.", "이 단서가 정확히 무슨 뜻인지는 똑똑히 설명하지 못하면서도, 부인은 그 얘기만 나오면 그렇게 알 듯 말 듯 거드름 피우는 상태로 가라앉았어. 그리고 앞날에 펼쳐질 부와 위엄의 환상이 어찌나 어른거리던지 (희미하고 안개에 싸인 것이긴 했지만), 그럴 때면 정말로 으리으리한 규모로 평생 먹고살 길이 마련되기라도 한 것처럼 그만큼이나 행복해했지."]], [["The sudden and terrible shock she had received, combined with the great affliction and anxiety of mind which she had, for a long time, endured, proved too much for Madeline’s strength.", "부인이 받은 그 갑작스럽고도 끔찍한 충격에, 오랫동안 견뎌 온 크나큰 고통과 마음의 불안까지 겹치니, 매들린의 기력으로는 도저히 감당이 안 됐어."], ["Recovering from the state of stupefaction into which the sudden death of her father happily plunged her, she only exchanged that condition for one of dangerous and active illness.", "아버지의 갑작스러운 죽음으로 다행히 빠져들었던 멍한 상태에서 깨어나기는 했지만, 그저 그 상태가 위태롭고 격렬한 병으로 바뀌었을 뿐이었지."], ["When the delicate physical powers which have been sustained by an unnatural strain upon the mental energies and a resolute determination not to yield, at last give way, their degree of prostration is usually proportionate to the strength of the effort which has previously upheld them.", "정신의 기운을 무리하게 쥐어짜고 결코 무너지지 않겠다는 굳은 결심으로 버텨 온 연약한 몸의 힘이 마침내 꺾일 때, 그 쇠약해지는 정도는 대개 그동안 그 몸을 떠받쳐 온 그 안간힘의 크기에 비례하는 법이야."], ["Thus it was that the illness which fell on Madeline was of no slight or temporary nature, but one which, for a time, threatened her reason, and--scarcely worse--her life itself.", "그래서 매들린에게 닥친 병은 가볍거나 잠깐 스쳐 가는 게 아니라, 한동안 그녀의 정신을 위협하고, 또 그에 못지않게 끔찍하게도 목숨 그 자체까지 위협하는 병이었어."]], [["Who, slowly recovering from a disorder so severe and dangerous, could be insensible to the unremitting attentions of such a nurse as gentle, tender, earnest Kate?", "그토록 모질고 위태로운 병에서 더디게 회복해 가는 사람이라면, 누군들 케이트처럼 다정하고 따뜻하고 진심 어린 간병인의 끊임없는 보살핌에 마음이 움직이지 않을 수 있겠어?"], ["On whom could the sweet soft voice, the light step, the delicate hand, the quiet, cheerful, noiseless discharge of those thousand little offices of kindness and relief which we feel so deeply when we are ill, and forget so lightly when we are well--on whom could they make so deep an impression as on a young heart stored with every pure and true affection that women cherish; almost a stranger to the endearments and devotion of its own sex, save as it learnt them from itself; and rendered, by calamity and suffering, keenly susceptible of the sympathy so long unknown and so long sought in vain?", "그 곱고 부드러운 목소리, 가벼운 발걸음, 섬세한 손길, 우리가 아플 때면 그토록 깊이 느끼다가 다 나으면 그토록 쉽게 잊어버리는 그 수천 가지 자잘한 친절과 위안을, 조용하고 밝고 소리 없이 베풀어 주는 그 손길이 마음 깊이 새겨지지 않을 사람이 어디 있겠어? 더구나 여자들이 소중히 여기는 온갖 순수하고 참된 애정으로 가득 찬, 그리고 같은 여자한테서 받는 다정함과 헌신이라곤 거의 모르고 자란 — 스스로 익힌 것 말고는 — 게다가 재앙과 고통을 겪으며, 그토록 오래 알지 못하고 그토록 오래 헛되이 찾아 헤매던 그 동정에 절절히 사무치게 된 젊은 마음이라면 더더욱 그렇지."], ["What wonder that days became as years in knitting them together!", "날들이 세월처럼 쌓이며 두 사람을 하나로 엮어 간들 무슨 이상할 게 있겠어!"], ["What wonder, if with every hour of returning health, there came some stronger and sweeter recognition of the praises which Kate, when they recalled old scenes--they seemed old now, and to have been acted years ago--would lavish on her brother!", "건강이 돌아오는 시간마다, 케이트가 옛 장면을 떠올리며 — 이제는 옛일처럼, 몇 해 전에 있었던 일처럼 느껴졌지 — 제 오빠한테 아낌없이 늘어놓던 그 칭찬을, 매들린이 점점 더 강하고 달콤하게 받아들이게 된들 무슨 이상할 게 있겠어!"], ["Where would have been the wonder, even, if those praises had found a quick response in the breast of Madeline, and if, with the image of Nicholas so constantly recurring in the features of his sister that she could scarcely separate the two, she had sometimes found it equally difficult to assign to each the feelings they had first inspired, and had imperceptibly mingled with her gratitude to Nicholas, some of that warmer feeling which she had assigned to Kate?", "그 칭찬이 매들린의 가슴에 곧바로 메아리쳤다 한들, 또 케이트의 얼굴에 늘 니콜라스의 모습이 어른거려서 두 사람을 좀처럼 떼어 놓고 생각할 수 없게 된 나머지, 처음에 둘이 각각 불러일으켰던 그 감정을 누구한테 줘야 할지 똑같이 헷갈려 하다가, 케이트한테 품었던 그 더 따스한 감정의 얼마쯤을 니콜라스를 향한 고마움에 자기도 모르게 섞어 버렸다 한들, 그게 무슨 이상할 게 있었겠어?"]], [["‘My dear,’ Mrs.", "\"얘야,\""], ["Nickleby would say, coming into the room with an elaborate caution, calculated to discompose the nerves of an invalid rather more than the entry of a horse-soldier at full gallop; ‘how do you find yourself tonight?", "니클비 부인은 방으로 들어오며 이렇게 말하곤 했어. 환자의 신경을 거스르지 않으려고 어찌나 공들여 조심하는지, 차라리 전속력으로 달려드는 기병이 들이닥치는 편이 더 나을 지경이었지."], ["I hope you are better.’", "\"오늘 밤은 좀 어떠니? 한결 나았으면 좋겠구나.\""]], [["‘Almost well, mama,’ Kate would reply, laying down her work, and taking Madeline’s hand in hers.", "\"거의 다 나았어요, 엄마.\" 케이트는 하던 일감을 내려놓고 매들린의 손을 제 손에 쥐며 대답하곤 했어."]], [["‘Kate!’ Mrs.", "\"케이트!\" 니클비 부인이 나무라듯 말하곤 했지."], ["Nickleby would say, reprovingly, ‘don’t talk so loud’ (the worthy lady herself talking in a whisper that would have made the blood of the stoutest man run cold in his veins).", "\"그렇게 큰 소리로 말하지 마라.\" (정작 이 착한 부인은, 아무리 담대한 남자라도 핏줄의 피가 얼어붙을 만큼 으스스한 속삭임으로 말하면서 말이야.)"]], [["Kate would take this reproof very quietly, and Mrs.", "케이트는 이 꾸지람을 아주 잠자코 받아들이곤 했어."], ["Nickleby, making every board creak and every thread rustle as she moved stealthily about, would add:", "그러면 니클비 부인은 살금살금 움직인답시고 마룻장이란 마룻장은 다 삐걱거리게 하고 실오라기 하나까지 바스락거리게 하면서 이렇게 덧붙이곤 했지."]], [["‘My son Nicholas has just come home, and I have come, according to custom, my dear, to know, from your own lips, exactly how you are; for he won’t take my account, and never will.’", "\"내 아들 니콜라스가 방금 집에 왔단다. 그래서 늘 하던 대로, 얘야, 네 입으로 직접 네가 정확히 어떤지 들으러 왔어. 그 애가 내 말은 통 안 믿고, 앞으로도 절대 안 믿을 테니까.\""]], [["‘He is later than usual to-night,’ perhaps Madeline would reply.", "\"오늘 밤은 평소보다 늦으셨네요.\" 매들린이 그렇게 대꾸하기도 했어."], ["‘Nearly half an hour.’", "\"거의 반 시간이나요.\""]], [["‘Well, I never saw such people in all my life as you are, for time, up here!’ Mrs.", "\"세상에, 시간 따지는 데는 여기 너희 같은 사람들을 내 평생 본 적이 없다!\""], ["Nickleby would exclaim in great astonishment; ‘I declare I never did!", "니클비 부인은 잔뜩 놀라서 외치곤 했지. \"정말이지 처음이야!"], ["I had not the least idea that Nicholas was after his time, not the smallest. Mr.", "니콜라스가 늦었다는 건 나는 손톱만큼도 몰랐다, 눈곱만큼도."], ["Nickleby used to say--your poor papa, I am speaking of, Kate my dear--used to say, that appetite was the best clock in the world, but you have no appetite, my dear Miss Bray, I wish you had, and upon my word I really think you ought to take something that would give you one.", "니클비 씨가 늘 말했지, 네 가엾은 아버지 말이다, 케이트야, 식욕이야말로 세상에서 제일 좋은 시계라고 말이다. 그런데 너는 식욕이 없구나, 브레이 양, 좀 있었으면 좋으련만, 정말이지 식욕이 생길 만한 걸 좀 들어야 할 것 같다."], ["I am sure I don’t know, but I have heard that two or three dozen native lobsters give an appetite, though that comes to the same thing after all, for I suppose you must have an appetite before you can take ‘em.", "나도 잘은 모르지만, 토종 바닷가재를 두세 다스쯤 먹으면 식욕이 돈다고 들었다. 하긴 그것도 결국 같은 얘기지, 가재를 먹으려면 먼저 식욕이 있어야 할 테니까."], ["If I said lobsters, I meant oysters, but of course it’s all the same, though really how you came to know about Nicholas--’", "가재라고 했지만 굴 말이었어, 뭐 다 똑같지만. 그건 그렇고, 정말 너는 어떻게 니콜라스 일을 알았—\""]], [["‘We happened to be just talking about him, mama; that was it.’", "\"마침 그 애 얘기를 하던 참이었어요, 엄마. 그래서요.\""]], [["‘You never seem to me to be talking about anything else, Kate, and upon my word I am quite surprised at your being so very thoughtless.", "\"내 보기엔 너는 늘 다른 얘기는 안 하는 것 같더라, 케이트야. 정말이지 네가 그렇게 생각이 없는 데는 놀랄 따름이다."], ["You can find subjects enough to talk about sometimes, and when you know how important it is to keep up Miss Bray’s spirits, and interest her, and all that, it really is quite extraordinary to me what can induce you to keep on prose, prose, prose, din, din, din, everlastingly, upon the same theme.", "가끔은 얘깃거리를 얼마든지 찾아내면서, 브레이 양의 기운을 북돋고 흥미를 끌어 주는 게 얼마나 중요한지 뻔히 알면서, 어쩌면 그렇게 노상 똑같은 얘기만 주절주절, 주절주절, 시끌시끌, 시끌시끌 끝없이 늘어놓는지 정말 알다가도 모를 일이다. 너는 아주 친절한 간병인이고, 아주 솜씨도 좋고, 마음씨도 곱다는 건 안다."], ["You are a very kind nurse, Kate, and a very good one, and I know you mean very well; but I will say this--that if it wasn’t for me, I really don’t know what would become of Miss Bray’s spirits, and so I tell the doctor every day.", "그래도 이 말은 해야겠다. 내가 없었으면 브레이 양의 기운이 어떻게 됐을지 정말 모르겠다고 말이야."], ["He says he wonders how I sustain my own, and I am sure I very often wonder myself how I can contrive to keep up as I do.", "그래서 의사 선생한테도 날마다 그렇게 말한단다. 선생은 내가 어떻게 내 기운을 그렇게 잘 챙기는지 신기해하던데, 나도 정말 어떻게 이렇게 버텨 내는지 스스로도 자주 신기할 지경이다."], ["Of course it’s an exertion, but still, when I know how much depends upon me in this house, I am obliged to make it.", "물론 힘든 일이지, 그래도 이 집에서 나한테 얼마나 많은 게 달려 있는지 아니까, 안 할 수가 없는 거야."], ["There’s nothing praiseworthy in that, but it’s necessary, and I do it.’", "칭찬받을 일은 아니지만, 꼭 필요한 일이니까 하는 거지.\""]], [["With that, Mrs. Nickleby would draw up a chair, and for some three-quarters of an hour run through a great variety of distracting topics in the most distracting manner possible; tearing herself away, at length, on the plea that she must now go and amuse Nicholas while he took his supper.", "그러고 나서 니클비 부인은 의자를 끌어다 앉아서는, 한 사십오 분 동안 정신 사나운 온갖 화제를 더없이 정신 사나운 방식으로 줄줄이 늘어놓곤 했어."], ["After a preliminary raising of his spirits with the information that she considered the patient decidedly worse, she would further cheer him up by relating how dull, listless, and low-spirited Miss Bray was, because Kate foolishly talked about nothing else but him and family matters.", "그러다 마침내, 이제 니콜라스가 저녁을 먹는 동안 즐겁게 해 줘야 한다는 핑계로 자리를 떴지. 우선 환자가 분명히 더 나빠졌다는 소식으로 아들 기운을 한 번 북돋워 준 다음, 케이트가 어리석게도 오빠 얘기랑 집안일 말고는 통 다른 얘기를 안 하는 통에 브레이 양이 얼마나 따분하고 맥없고 풀 죽어 있는지 늘어놓으며 한층 더 기운을 차리게 해 주곤 했어."], ["When she had made Nicholas thoroughly comfortable with these and other inspiriting remarks, she would discourse at length on the arduous duties she had performed that day; and, sometimes, be moved to tears in wondering how, if anything were to happen to herself, the family would ever get on without her.", "이런저런 신나는 말로 니콜라스를 완전히 편안하게 만들어 준 다음, 그날 자기가 해낸 고된 일들을 길게 늘어놓곤 했지. 그러다 간혹, 혹시라도 자기한테 무슨 일이 생기면 이 집안이 자기 없이 어떻게 꾸려 갈까 싶어 눈물을 흘리기까지 했어."]], [["At other times, when Nicholas came home at night, he would be accompanied by Mr.", "또 어떤 때는 니콜라스가 밤에 집에 올 때 프랭크 치어리블 씨를 데려오기도 했어."], ["Frank Cheeryble, who was commissioned by the brothers to inquire how Madeline was that evening.", "그날 저녁 매들린이 어떤지 알아보라고 형제분이 보낸 거였지."], ["On such occasions (and they were of very frequent occurrence), Mrs.", "그럴 때면 (그런 일은 아주 자주 있었어) 니클비 부인은 정신을 바짝 차리는 게 특히 중요하다고 여겼어."], ["Nickleby deemed it of particular importance that she should have her wits about her; for, from certain signs and tokens which had attracted her attention, she shrewdly suspected that Mr.", "부인의 눈길을 끈 어떤 낌새와 조짐들로 미루어, 프랭크 씨가 삼촌들만큼이나 매들린한테 마음을 쓰는 양하지만 사실은 그 안부를 묻는 것 못지않게 케이트를 보러 오는 거라고 눈치 빠르게 짐작했거든."], ["Frank, interested as his uncles were in Madeline, came quite as much to see Kate as to inquire after her; the more especially as the brothers were in constant communication with the medical man, came backwards and forwards very frequently themselves, and received a full report from Nicholas every morning.", "더구나 형제분은 의사와 늘 연락을 주고받았고, 본인들도 아주 자주 들락거렸으며, 매일 아침 니콜라스한테서 자세한 보고를 받았으니 말이야. 니클비 부인한테는 이게 으쓱한 시절이었어."], ["These were proud times for Mrs Nickleby; never was anybody half so discreet and sage as she, or half so mysterious withal; and never were there such cunning generalship, and such unfathomable designs, as she brought to bear upon Mr.", "부인만큼 신중하고 사려 깊은 사람도, 또 부인만큼 알 듯 말 듯 속을 안 내비치는 사람도 없었지. 그리고 자기 의심이 맞는지 알아내려고, 또 맞다면 프랭크 씨를 애간장 태워서 자기한테 속내를 털어놓고 자비를 빌게 만들려고 그한테 펼친 만큼 교묘한 작전과 헤아릴 수 없는 책략도 없었어."], ["Frank, with the view of ascertaining whether her suspicions were well founded: and if so, of tantalising him into taking her into his confidence and throwing himself upon her merciful consideration.", "이 거창한 계획을 밀어붙이려고 니클비 부인이 동원한 대포는, 무거운 것이며 가벼운 것이며 그 규모가 어마어마했어. 마음먹은 목적을 이루려고 쓴 수단도 가지각색이고 서로 정반대였지."], ["Extensive was the artillery, heavy and light, which Mrs.", "어떤 때는 온통 살갑고 편안하게 굴다가, 또 어떤 때는 온통 뻣뻣하고 쌀쌀맞게 굴었어."], ["Nickleby brought into play for the furtherance of these great schemes; various and opposite the means which she employed to bring about the end she had in view. At one time, she was all cordiality and ease; at another, all stiffness and frigidity.", "어느 때는 그 가엾은 희생자한테 마음을 송두리째 열어 보이는 듯하다가도, 다음번에 만날 때는 더없이 서먹하고 짐짓 꾸민 듯한 태도로 맞이하곤 했지."], ["Now, she would seem to open her whole heart to her unhappy victim; the next time they met, she would receive him with the most distant and studious reserve, as if a new light had broken in upon her, and, guessing his intentions, she had resolved to check them in the bud; as if she felt it her bounden duty to act with Spartan firmness, and at once and for ever to discourage hopes which never could be realised.", "마치 무슨 새로운 깨달음이 번뜩 떠올라서 그의 속셈을 알아채고는, 그 싹을 미리 잘라 버리기로 작정한 듯이 말이야. 마치 스파르타인 같은 단호함으로 행동해서, 결코 이뤄질 수 없는 희망을 단번에 영원히 꺾어 버리는 게 자기의 마땅한 도리라고 느끼는 것처럼."], ["At other times, when Nicholas was not there to overhear, and Kate was upstairs busily tending her sick friend, the worthy lady would throw out dark hints of an intention to send her daughter to France for three or four years, or to Scotland for the improvement of her health impaired by her late fatigues, or to America on a visit, or anywhere that threatened a long and tedious separation.", "또 어떤 때는, 니콜라스가 엿들을 데 없고 케이트가 위층에서 아픈 친구를 부지런히 돌보고 있을 적이면, 이 착한 부인은 딸을 프랑스로 서너 해 보내겠다거나, 요즘 피로로 상한 건강을 다스리러 스코틀랜드로 보내겠다거나, 미국으로 방문을 보내겠다거나, 아무튼 길고 지루한 이별이 닥칠 만한 데로 보내겠다는 둥 알 수 없는 암시를 흘리곤 했어. 아니, 심지어 어렴풋이 이런 암시까지 했어."], ["Nay, she even went so far as to hint, obscurely, at an attachment entertained for her daughter by the son of an old neighbour of theirs, one Horatio Peltirogus (a young gentleman who might have been, at that time, four years old, or thereabouts), and to represent it, indeed, as almost a settled thing between the families--only waiting for her daughter’s final decision, to come off with the sanction of the church, and to the unspeakable happiness and content of all parties.", "옛 이웃의 아들 하나가 — 호레이쇼 펠티로거스라는 (그때 한 네 살쯤 됐을 법한 어린 도령이었지) — 제 딸한테 마음을 두고 있다고 말이야. 게다가 그건 두 집안 사이에 거의 다 정해진 일이나 마찬가지고, 딸의 최종 결정만 기다리면 교회의 축복 속에 성사되어 모든 식구가 이루 말할 수 없이 행복하고 흡족해질 거라는 식으로 떠벌리기까지 했어."]], [["It was in the full pride and glory of having sprung this last mine one night with extraordinary success, that Mrs.", "어느 날 밤, 이 마지막 지뢰를 어찌나 멋들어지게 터뜨렸던지 그 우쭐함과 의기양양함이 한껏 부풀어 오른 김에, 니클비 부인은 잠자리에 들기 전 아들과 단둘이 남게 된 틈을 타서 줄곧 머릿속을 차지하던 그 화제로 아들의 속을 떠보았어."], ["Nickleby took the opportunity of being left alone with her son before retiring to rest, to sound him on the subject which so occupied her thoughts: not doubting that they could have but one opinion respecting it.", "이 일에 대해서야 둘의 의견이 다를 리 없다고 철석같이 믿으면서 말이야."], ["To this end, she approached the question with divers laudatory and appropriate remarks touching the general amiability of Mr. Frank Cheeryble.", "그래서 부인은 프랭크 치어리블 씨가 두루 얼마나 상냥한지 칭찬하는 적절한 말부터 이런저런 꺼내며 그 문제에 다가갔어."]], [["‘You are quite right, mother,’ said Nicholas, ‘quite right.", "\"엄마 말씀이 옳아요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["He is a fine fellow.’", "\"정말 옳고말고요. 그 사람 멋진 친구죠.\""]], [["‘Good-looking, too,’ said Mrs.", "\"잘생기기도 했고.\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."]], [["‘Decidedly good-looking,’ answered Nicholas.", "\"확실히 잘생겼죠.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."]], [["‘What may you call his nose, now, my dear?’ pursued Mrs.", "\"얘야, 그 사람 코는 뭐라고 해야 하니?\""], ["Nickleby, wishing to interest Nicholas in the subject to the utmost.", "니클비 부인은 어떻게든 니콜라스가 이 화제에 흠뻑 빠져들게 하려고 계속 물었어."]], [["‘Call it?’ repeated Nicholas.", "\"뭐라고 하다니요?\" 니콜라스가 되물었어."]], [["‘Ah!’ returned his mother, ‘what style of nose?", "\"아!\" 어머니가 말을 받았어."], ["What order of architecture, if one may say so.", "\"어떤 모양의 코냐 말이다. 말하자면 어떤 양식의 건축이냐고."], ["I am not very learned in noses.", "내가 코에 대해선 그리 밝지 못해서."], ["Do you call it a Roman or a Grecian?’", "로마식이라고 해야 하니, 그리스식이라고 해야 하니?\""]], [["‘Upon my word, mother,’ said Nicholas, laughing, ‘as well as I remember, I should call it a kind of Composite, or mixed nose.", "\"맙소사, 엄마.\" 니콜라스가 웃으며 말했어."], ["But I have no very strong recollection on the subject.", "\"내 기억으로는, 일종의 혼합식이라고나, 뒤섞인 코라고나 해야 할 것 같은데요."], ["If it will afford you any gratification, I’ll observe it more closely, and let you know.’", "그런데 그 점에 대해선 그리 또렷한 기억이 없네요. 엄마가 그렇게 궁금하시다면, 좀 더 눈여겨봤다가 알려 드릴게요.\""]], [["‘I wish you would, my dear,’ said Mrs.", "\"그래 주면 좋겠구나, 얘야.\""], ["Nickleby, with an earnest look.", "니클비 부인이 진지한 표정으로 말했어."]], [["‘Very well,’ returned Nicholas.", "\"좋아요.\" 니콜라스가 대답했어."], ["‘I will.’", "\"그럴게요.\""]], [["Nicholas returned to the perusal of the book he had been reading, when the dialogue had gone thus far.", "대화가 여기까지 이르자, 니콜라스는 읽던 책으로 다시 눈을 돌렸어."], ["Mrs. Nickleby, after stopping a little for consideration, resumed.", "니클비 부인은 잠깐 멈춰 생각하더니 다시 말을 이었지."]], [["‘He is very much attached to you, Nicholas, my dear.’", "\"그 사람은 너를 무척 따른단다, 니콜라스.\""]], [["Nicholas laughingly said, as he closed his book, that he was glad to hear it, and observed that his mother seemed deep in their new friend’s confidence already.", "니콜라스는 책을 덮으며 웃는 얼굴로, 그 말을 들으니 기쁘다고 했어. 그러고는 엄마가 벌써 그 새 친구의 속을 깊이 꿰뚫고 계신 모양이라고 한마디 했지."]], [["‘Hem!’ said Mrs.", "\"흠!\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 말했어."], ["‘I don’t know about that, my dear, but I think it is very necessary that somebody should be in his confidence; highly necessary.’", "\"그건 모르겠다만, 얘야, 누군가는 그 사람 속을 알아 둘 필요가 꼭 있다고 생각한다. 아주 꼭 필요한 일이지.\""]], [["Elated by a look of curiosity from her son, and the consciousness of possessing a great secret, all to herself, Mrs. Nickleby went on with great animation:", "아들이 보내는 호기심 어린 눈빛에, 또 자기 혼자만 큰 비밀을 쥐고 있다는 뿌듯함에 우쭐해진 니클비 부인은 잔뜩 신이 나서 말을 이었어."]], [["‘I am sure, my dear Nicholas, how you can have failed to notice it, is, to me, quite extraordinary; though I don’t know why I should say that, either, because, of course, as far as it goes, and to a certain extent, there is a great deal in this sort of thing, especially in this early stage, which, however clear it may be to females, can scarcely be expected to be so evident to men.", "\"정말이지, 니콜라스, 네가 어떻게 그걸 못 알아챘는지 나는 도무지 이해가 안 간다. 하긴 내가 왜 그런 말을 하는지도 모르겠다만, 물론 어느 정도까지는, 또 어느 선까지는, 이런 일에는, 특히 이런 초기 단계에는, 여자들 눈에는 아무리 뻔히 보여도 남자들 눈에는 그렇게까지 환히 보이기를 바라기 어려운 구석이 많은 법이거든."], ["I don’t say that I have any particular penetration in such matters.", "내가 이런 일에 무슨 특별한 통찰력이 있다는 말은 아니야. 있을 수도 있지."], ["I may have; those about me should know best about that, and perhaps do know.", "그건 내 곁에 있는 사람들이 제일 잘 알 테고, 아마 알고들 있겠지."], ["Upon that point I shall express no opinion, it wouldn’t become me to do so, it’s quite out of the question, quite.’", "그 점에 대해선 내 의견을 말하지 않으련다. 내가 그러는 건 어울리지 않으니까, 어림도 없는 일이지, 어림도 없어.\""]], [["Nicholas snuffed the candles, put his hands in his pockets, and, leaning back in his chair, assumed a look of patient suffering and melancholy resignation.", "니콜라스는 촛불 심지를 다듬고, 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고는, 의자에 등을 기대고 꾹 참고 견디는 듯한, 우울하게 단념한 듯한 표정을 지었어."]], [["‘I think it my duty, Nicholas, my dear,’ resumed his mother, ‘to tell you what I know: not only because you have a right to know it too, and to know everything that happens in this family, but because you have it in your power to promote and assist the thing very much; and there is no doubt that the sooner one can come to a clear understanding on such subjects, it is always better, every way. There are a great many things you might do; such as taking a walk in the garden sometimes, or sitting upstairs in your own room for a little while, or making believe to fall asleep occasionally, or pretending that you recollected some business, and going out for an hour or so, and taking Mr. Smike with you. These seem very slight things, and I dare say you will be amused at my making them of so much importance; at the same time, my dear, I can assure you (and you’ll find this out, Nicholas, for yourself one of these days, if you ever fall in love with anybody; as I trust and hope you will, provided she is respectable and well conducted, and of course you’d never dream of falling in love with anybody who was not), I say, I can assure you that a great deal more depends upon these little things than you would suppose possible. If your poor papa was alive, he would tell you how much depended on the parties being left alone. Of course, you are not to go out of the room as if you meant it and did it on purpose, but as if it was quite an accident, and to come back again in the same way. If you cough in the passage before you open the door, or whistle carelessly, or hum a tune, or something of that sort, to let them know you’re coming, it’s always better; because, of course, though it’s not only natural but perfectly correct and proper under the circumstances, still it is very confusing if you interrupt young people when they are--when they are sitting on the sofa, and--and all that sort of thing: which is very nonsensical, perhaps, but still they will do it.’", "\"내가 아는 걸 너한테 말해 주는 게 도리라고 생각한다, 니콜라스.\" 어머니가 다시 말을 이었어. \"너도 그걸 알 권리가 있고, 이 집안에서 일어나는 일은 죄다 알 권리가 있어서만이 아니라, 네가 이 일을 크게 거들고 도울 수 있는 처지여서 그래. 그리고 이런 일은 빨리 분명하게 매듭짓는 게 어느 모로 보나 언제나 더 낫다는 데는 의심의 여지가 없거든. 네가 할 수 있는 일이 아주 많단다. 이를테면 가끔 정원을 거닌다거나, 잠시 위층 네 방에 앉아 있는다거나, 이따금 잠든 척한다거나, 무슨 볼일이 생각난 척하고 한 시간쯤 나갔다 오는 거지, 스마이크 씨를 데리고 말이야. 이게 아주 사소한 일처럼 보일 거다. 내가 이런 걸 그렇게 대단하게 여기니 너는 우습겠지. 그래도 얘야, 내 장담하건대 (그리고 너도 언젠가는 직접 알게 될 거다, 니콜라스, 네가 누군가를 사랑하게 되면 말이야. 나는 네가 꼭 그러기를 바라고 또 그러리라 믿는다. 물론 점잖고 행실 바른 여자라야지, 그렇지 않은 여자를 사랑하는 일이야 너로선 꿈에도 없을 테고) 이 사소한 일들에 네가 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 게 달려 있단다. 네 가엾은 아버지가 살아 있었다면, 두 사람을 단둘이 있게 두는 게 얼마나 중요한지 일러 줬을 거다. 물론 일부러 그러는 양 작정하고 방을 나가서는 안 되고, 아주 우연인 척해야 하고, 돌아올 때도 똑같이 해야 해. 문을 열기 전에 복도에서 헛기침을 한다거나, 무심한 척 휘파람을 분다거나, 콧노래를 흥얼거린다거나, 뭐 그런 식으로 네가 온다는 걸 알려 주면 늘 더 낫지. 왜냐하면, 물론 그건 자연스러울 뿐 아니라 그런 상황에선 더없이 옳고 마땅한 일이긴 해도, 젊은 사람들이 한창—한창 소파에 앉아서—그런 온갖 일을 하고 있을 때 끼어들면 아주 난처해지거든. 어쩌면 참 쓸데없는 짓이겠지만, 그래도 젊은 애들은 그러는 법이니까.\""]], [["The profound astonishment with which her son regarded her during this long address, gradually increasing as it approached its climax in no way discomposed Mrs.", "이 긴 연설 내내 아들이 어머니를 바라보던 그 깊은 놀라움은, 연설이 절정으로 치달을수록 점점 커져만 갔어."], ["Nickleby, but rather exalted her opinion of her own cleverness; therefore, merely stopping to remark, with much complacency, that she had fully expected him to be surprised, she entered on a vast quantity of circumstantial evidence of a particularly incoherent and perplexing kind; the upshot of which was, to establish, beyond the possibility of doubt, that Mr.", "그런데도 니클비 부인은 조금도 당황하기는커녕, 오히려 제 영리함에 대한 자부심만 한껏 부풀어 올랐지. 그래서 부인은 흡족한 얼굴로, 네가 놀랄 줄 진작 알았다고 한마디 던져 두고는, 유난히 두서없고 종잡을 수 없는 정황 증거를 어마어마하게 늘어놓기 시작했어."], ["Frank Cheeryble had fallen desperately in love with Kate.", "그 결론인즉, 프랭크 치어리블 씨가 케이트한테 홀딱 빠져 버렸다는 사실을 의심의 여지 없이 못 박는 거였지."]], [["‘With whom?’ cried Nicholas.", "\"누구한테요?\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어."]], [["Mrs. Nickleby repeated, with Kate.", "니클비 부인은 케이트한테라고 다시 말했어."]], [["‘What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["OUR Kate!", "우리 케이트요!"], ["My sister!’", "제 누이동생이요!\""]], [["‘Lord, Nicholas!’ returned Mrs.", "\"세상에, 니콜라스!\" 니클비 부인이 받아쳤어."], ["Nickleby, ‘whose Kate should it be, if not ours; or what should I care about it, or take any interest in it for, if it was anybody but your sister?’", "\"우리 케이트가 아니면 누구네 케이트겠니. 게다가 그게 네 누이동생이 아니라 딴 사람이었다면 내가 왜 신경을 쓰고 관심을 두겠어?\""]], [["‘Dear mother,’ said Nicholas, ‘surely it can’t be!’", "\"엄마.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"설마 그럴 리가요!\""]], [["‘Very good, my dear,’ replied Mrs.", "\"좋아, 얘야.\""], ["Nickleby, with great confidence.", "니클비 부인이 자신만만하게 대답했어."], ["‘Wait and see.’", "\"두고 보면 알 거다.\""]], [["Nicholas had never, until that moment, bestowed a thought upon the remote possibility of such an occurrence as that which was now communicated to him; for, besides that he had been much from home of late and closely occupied with other matters, his own jealous fears had prompted the suspicion that some secret interest in Madeline, akin to that which he felt himself, occasioned those visits of Frank Cheeryble which had recently become so frequent.", "니콜라스는 그 순간까지 지금 들은 그런 일이 일어날 가능성이라곤 털끝만큼도 생각해 본 적이 없었어. 요즘 집을 비우는 일이 잦았고 다른 일들에 푹 매여 있었던 데다, 제 안의 질투 어린 두려움이 이런 의심을 부추겼거든."], ["Even now, although he knew that the observation of an anxious mother was much more likely to be correct in such a case than his own, and although she reminded him of many little circumstances which, taken together, were certainly susceptible of the construction she triumphantly put upon them, he was not quite convinced but that they arose from mere good-natured thoughtless gallantry, which would have dictated the same conduct towards any other girl who was young and pleasing.", "프랭크 치어리블이 요즘 그렇게 자주 찾아오는 건, 매들린한테 자기와 비슷한 어떤 은밀한 마음을 품고 있어서가 아닐까 하고 말이야. 지금도, 이런 일에는 애가 타는 어머니의 관찰이 제 눈보다 훨씬 더 정확할 공산이 크다는 걸 알았고, 또 어머니가 일러 준 자잘한 정황들이 죄다 모아 놓고 보면 어머니가 의기양양하게 갖다 붙인 그 해석에 분명 들어맞기는 했지만, 그래도 그게 그저 사람 좋은 데서 나온 무심한 친절일 뿐, 젊고 사랑스러운 다른 어떤 처녀한테라도 똑같이 했을 행동이 아닐까 하는 생각을 떨치지 못했어."], ["At all events, he hoped so, and therefore tried to believe it.", "어쨌든 그러기를 바랐고, 그래서 그렇게 믿으려 애썼지."]], [["‘I am very much disturbed by what you tell me,’ said Nicholas, after a little reflection, ‘though I yet hope you may be mistaken.’", "\"엄마가 해 주신 얘기를 들으니 마음이 무척 어지럽네요.\" 니콜라스가 잠시 생각하더니 말했어. \"그래도 엄마가 잘못 보신 거였으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["‘I don’t understand why you should hope so,’ said Mrs.", "\"네가 왜 그러기를 바라는지는 나도 모르겠다만, 솔직히.\""], ["Nickleby, ‘I confess; but you may depend upon it I am not.’", "니클비 부인이 말했어. \"내가 잘못 본 게 아니라는 건 너도 믿어도 된다.\""]], [["‘What of Kate?’ inquired Nicholas.", "\"케이트는요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘Why that, my dear,’ returned Mrs. Nickleby, ‘is just the point upon which I am not yet satisfied. During this sickness, she has been constantly at Madeline’s bedside--never were two people so fond of each other as they have grown--and to tell you the truth, Nicholas, I have rather kept her away now and then, because I think it’s a good plan, and urges a young man on. He doesn’t get too sure, you know.’", "\"그게 말이다, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 대답했어. \"그게 바로 내가 아직 마음을 못 놓는 대목이야. 이번에 병치레하는 동안 케이트가 줄곧 매들린 곁을 지켰거든. 그 둘이 정이 든 만큼 서로 좋아하는 사이도 없을 거다. 그래서 솔직히 말하면, 니콜라스, 내가 가끔 일부러 케이트를 떼어 놓곤 했어. 그게 좋은 수라고 보거든. 젊은 남자를 다그치는 거지. 그래야 그 애가 너무 마음을 놓지 않으니까, 알겠니.\""]], [["She said this with such a mingling of high delight and self-congratulation, that it was inexpressibly painful to Nicholas to dash her hopes; but he felt that there was only one honourable course before him, and that he was bound to take it.", "부인이 이 말을 어찌나 한껏 들뜬 데다 제 잘한 일을 흐뭇해하며 하던지, 니콜라스로서는 그 희망을 꺾어 버리기가 이루 말할 수 없이 괴로웠어. 하지만 그는 자기 앞에 떳떳한 길은 하나뿐이고, 마땅히 그 길을 가야 한다고 느꼈지."]], [["‘Dear mother,’ he said kindly, ‘don’t you see that if there were really any serious inclination on the part of Mr.", "\"엄마.\" 그가 다정하게 말했어."], ["Frank towards Kate, and we suffered ourselves for a moment to encourage it, we should be acting a most dishonourable and ungrateful part?", "\"만약에 정말로 프랭크 씨가 케이트한테 진지한 마음을 품고 있는데, 우리가 잠깐이라도 그걸 부추기게 둔다면, 우리는 더없이 떳떳치 못하고 배은망덕한 짓을 하는 게 된다는 걸 모르시겠어요?"], ["I ask you if you don’t see it, but I need not say that I know you don’t, or you would have been more strictly on your guard.", "모르시겠냐고 여쭙긴 하지만, 엄마가 모르신다는 건 굳이 말씀 안 드려도 알아요. 아셨다면 더 단단히 조심하셨을 테니까요."], ["Let me explain my meaning to you.", "제 뜻을 설명해 드릴게요."], ["Remember how poor we are.’", "우리가 얼마나 가난한지 떠올려 보세요.\""]], [["Mrs. Nickleby shook her head, and said, through her tears, that poverty was not a crime.", "니클비 부인은 고개를 저으며, 가난이 죄는 아니지 않느냐고 눈물 속에 말했어."]], [["‘No,’ said Nicholas, ‘and for that reason poverty should engender an honest pride, that it may not lead and tempt us to unworthy actions, and that we may preserve the self-respect which a hewer of wood and drawer of water may maintain, and does better in maintaining than a monarch in preserving his. Think what we owe to these two brothers: remember what they have done, and what they do every day for us with a generosity and delicacy for which the devotion of our whole lives would be a most imperfect and inadequate return. What kind of return would that be which would be comprised in our permitting their nephew, their only relative, whom they regard as a son, and for whom it would be mere childishness to suppose they have not formed plans suitably adapted to the education he has had, and the fortune he will inherit--in our permitting him to marry a portionless girl: so closely connected with us, that the irresistible inference must be, that he was entrapped by a plot; that it was a deliberate scheme, and a speculation amongst us three? Bring the matter clearly before yourself, mother. Now, how would you feel, if they were married, and the brothers, coming here on one of those kind errands which bring them here so often, you had to break out to them the truth? Would you be at ease, and feel that you had played an open part?’", "\"그렇죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"바로 그렇기 때문에 가난은 정직한 자존심을 길러야 해요. 그래야 그게 우리를 떳떳치 못한 짓으로 이끌고 꾀어내지 못하고, 또 우리가 자존심을 지킬 수 있으니까요. 나무를 패고 물을 긷는 사람도 지킬 수 있는 자존심을, 그것도 임금이 제 자존심을 지키는 것보다 더 훌륭히 지킬 수 있는 그 자존심을요. 우리가 그 두 형제분께 얼마나 신세를 졌는지 생각해 보세요. 그분들이 해 주신 일, 그리고 날마다 우리를 위해 베푸시는 일을 떠올려 보세요. 우리가 평생을 바쳐 헌신해도 더없이 모자라고 어림없는 보답밖에 안 될 만큼 너그럽고 섬세하게 말이에요. 그분들이 친아들처럼 여기는 단 하나뿐인 친척, 그 조카가 — 그분들이 그가 받은 교육과 물려받을 재산에 걸맞은 계획을 세워 두지 않았으리라 여기는 건 어린애 같은 생각이죠 — 그런 조카가, 지참금 한 푼 없는 데다 우리와 이토록 가까운 처녀와 결혼하도록 우리가 내버려 둔다면, 그게 어떤 보답이 되겠어요? 그러면 누가 봐도 그가 어떤 음모에 걸려든 거라고, 이건 우리 셋이 짠 치밀한 계략이자 한탕 노린 짓이라고 짐작할 수밖에 없잖아요. 이 일을 똑똑히 그려 보세요, 엄마. 자, 만약 그 둘이 결혼하고, 그분들이 늘 그러시듯 친절한 볼일로 여기 들르셨을 때, 엄마가 그분들께 그 사실을 털어놓아야 한다면 기분이 어떠시겠어요? 마음이 편하시겠어요, 떳떳하게 행동했다는 생각이 드시겠어요?\""]], [["Poor Mrs. Nickleby, crying more and more, murmured that of course Mr Frank would ask the consent of his uncles first.", "가엾은 니클비 부인은 점점 더 울먹이면서, 물론 프랭크 씨가 먼저 삼촌들의 허락을 구할 거라고 중얼거렸어."]], [["‘Why, to be sure, that would place HIM in a better situation with them,’ said Nicholas, ‘but we should still be open to the same suspicions; the distance between us would still be as great; the advantages to be gained would still be as manifest as now. We may be reckoning without our host in all this,’ he added more cheerfully, ‘and I trust, and almost believe we are. If it be otherwise, I have that confidence in Kate that I know she will feel as I do--and in you, dear mother, to be assured that after a little consideration you will do the same.’", "\"그야 그러면 그 사람 처지는 그분들 앞에서 나아지긴 하겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"하지만 우리는 여전히 똑같은 의심을 사게 돼요. 우리 사이의 격차는 여전히 그대로일 테고, 그 사람이 얻을 이득도 지금처럼 빤히 드러나 보일 테니까요. 어쩌면 우리가 이 모든 걸 혼자 김칫국부터 마시는 건지도 몰라요.\" 그가 한결 밝은 목소리로 덧붙였어. \"저는 그러기를 바라고, 거의 그렇다고 믿어요. 만약 그렇지 않다 해도, 저는 케이트를 믿으니까 그 애가 저와 똑같이 느끼리라는 걸 알아요. 그리고 엄마도 믿어요, 조금만 생각해 보시면 엄마도 똑같이 해 주시리라고요.\""]], [["After many more representations and entreaties, Nicholas obtained a promise from Mrs.", "니콜라스가 여러 번 거듭 설명하고 사정한 끝에, 니클비 부인한테서 약속을 받아 냈어."], ["Nickleby that she would try all she could to think as he did; and that if Mr. Frank persevered in his attentions she would endeavour to discourage them, or, at the least, would render him no countenance or assistance.", "자기도 아들처럼 생각하려고 최선을 다하겠다는 것, 그리고 만약 프랭크 씨가 계속 마음을 표하면 그걸 단념시키도록 애써 보겠다는 것, 아니면 적어도 그한테 아무런 호의나 도움도 보이지 않겠다는 약속이었지."], ["He determined to forbear mentioning the subject to Kate until he was quite convinced that there existed a real necessity for his doing so; and resolved to assure himself, as well as he could by close personal observation, of the exact position of affairs.", "니콜라스는 정말로 케이트한테 그 얘기를 꺼낼 필요가 있다는 확신이 설 때까지는 그 일을 입에 담지 않기로 마음먹었어. 그리고 가까이서 직접 지켜보아 일이 정확히 어떤 형편인지 되도록 잘 가늠해 두기로 작정했지."], ["This was a very wise resolution, but he was prevented from putting it in practice by a new source of anxiety and uneasiness.", "아주 현명한 결심이었지만, 그는 이걸 실행에 옮기지 못했어. 새로운 근심과 불안거리가 생겼거든."]], [["Smike became alarmingly ill; so reduced and exhausted that he could scarcely move from room to room without assistance; and so worn and emaciated, that it was painful to look upon him. Nicholas was warned, by the same medical authority to whom he had at first appealed, that the last chance and hope of his life depended on his being instantly removed from London. That part of Devonshire in which Nicholas had been himself bred was named as the most favourable spot; but this advice was cautiously coupled with the information, that whoever accompanied him thither must be prepared for the worst; for every token of rapid consumption had appeared, and he might never return alive.", "스마이크가 위태로울 만큼 앓아누웠어. 어찌나 쇠약하고 기진했던지, 부축 없이는 이 방에서 저 방으로 옮겨 가기도 힘들 지경이었지. 게다가 어찌나 야위고 수척했던지 바라보기가 안쓰러울 정도였어. 니콜라스는 처음에 의논했던 그 의사한테서 경고를 들었어. 그가 살아날 마지막 기회이자 희망은 당장 런던을 떠나는 데 달려 있다는 거였지. 니콜라스가 자란 데번셔의 그 지방이 가장 좋은 곳으로 꼽혔어. 하지만 이 충고에는 신중하게 이런 말이 따라붙었지. 그를 데려가는 사람이 누구든 최악의 경우를 각오해야 한다고 말이야. 빠르게 진행되는 폐병의 온갖 징후가 다 나타났으니, 어쩌면 그는 살아서 돌아오지 못할지도 모른다는 거였어."]], [["The kind brothers, who were acquainted with the poor creature’s sad history, dispatched old Tim to be present at this consultation.", "그 가엾은 청년의 슬픈 사연을 알고 있던 친절한 형제분은, 이 진찰 자리에 입회하라고 늙은 팀을 보냈어."], ["That same morning, Nicholas was summoned by brother Charles into his private room, and thus addressed:", "바로 그날 아침, 니콜라스는 형 찰스의 부름을 받고 그의 개인 방으로 갔는데, 형은 이렇게 말했어."]], [["‘My dear sir, no time must be lost.", "\"이보게, 한시도 지체해선 안 되네."], ["This lad shall not die, if such human means as we can use can save his life; neither shall he die alone, and in a strange place.", "우리가 쓸 수 있는 사람의 힘으로 이 아이의 목숨을 구할 수 있다면, 이 아이를 죽게 둬선 안 돼. 그리고 혼자, 낯선 데서 죽게 둬서도 안 되지."], ["Remove him tomorrow morning, see that he has every comfort that his situation requires, and don’t leave him; don’t leave him, my dear sir, until you know that there is no longer any immediate danger.", "내일 아침에 그 애를 데리고 가게. 그 애 처지에 필요한 온갖 편의를 다 갖춰 주고, 곁을 떠나지 말게."], ["It would be hard, indeed, to part you now. No, no, no!", "떠나지 말게, 이보게, 당장의 위험이 더는 없다는 걸 알게 될 때까지는 말이야."], ["Tim shall wait upon you tonight, sir; Tim shall wait upon you tonight with a parting word or two.", "지금 자네 둘을 떼어 놓는 건 정말이지 모진 일일 테니까. 안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼!"], ["Brother Ned, my dear fellow, Mr.", "오늘 밤 팀이 자네를 찾아갈 걸세. 오늘 밤 팀이 작별 인사 삼아 한두 마디 전하러 갈 거야."], ["Nickleby waits to shake hands and say goodbye; Mr.", "네드, 이 사람아, 니클비 씨가 악수를 나누고 작별 인사를 하려고 기다리네. 니클비 씨가 오래 떠나 있진 않을 거야."], ["Nickleby won’t be long gone; this poor chap will soon get better, very soon get better; and then he’ll find out some nice homely country-people to leave him with, and will go backwards and forwards sometimes--backwards and forwards you know, Ned.", "이 가엾은 친구도 곧 나을 거야, 아주 곧 나을 거고. 그러면 그 애를 맡길 만한 마음씨 좋고 소박한 시골 사람들을 찾아내서, 이따금 오갈 거야."], ["And there’s no cause to be downhearted, for he’ll very soon get better, very soon.", "오가고 또 오가고 하는 거지, 그렇잖나 네드. 그러니 풀이 죽을 까닭이 없어."], ["Won’t he, won’t he, Ned?’", "그 애는 곧 나을 테니까, 아주 곧. 그렇잖나, 그렇잖나, 네드?\""]], [["What Tim Linkinwater said, or what he brought with him that night, needs not to be told.", "팀 링킨워터가 그날 밤 무슨 말을 했는지, 또 무엇을 가지고 왔는지는 굳이 말할 필요가 없겠지."], ["Next morning Nicholas and his feeble companion began their journey.", "이튿날 아침 니콜라스와 그 가냘픈 동행은 길을 나섰어."]], [["And who but one--and that one he who, but for those who crowded round him then, had never met a look of kindness, or known a word of pity--could tell what agony of mind, what blighted thoughts, what unavailing sorrow, were involved in that sad parting?", "그리고 그 슬픈 작별에 어떤 마음의 고통이, 어떤 시들어 버린 생각이, 어떤 부질없는 슬픔이 깃들어 있었는지를, 누가 헤아릴 수 있었겠어? 그때 자기를 둘러싼 그 사람들이 아니었더라면 다정한 눈길 한 번 받아 본 적 없고 따뜻한 동정의 말 한마디 들어 본 적 없었을 바로 그 사람, 오직 그 사람 말고는 아무도 헤아릴 수 없었을 거야."]], [["‘See,’ cried Nicholas eagerly, as he looked from the coach window, ‘they are at the corner of the lane still!", "\"보세요!\" 니콜라스가 마차 창밖을 내다보며 다급히 외쳤어."], ["And now there’s Kate, poor Kate, whom you said you couldn’t bear to say goodbye to, waving her handkerchief.", "\"아직도 골목 모퉁이에 다들 서 계세요! 그리고 저기 케이트가, 가엾은 케이트가, 형이 차마 작별 인사를 못 하겠다던 그 애가 손수건을 흔들고 있어요."], ["Don’t go without one gesture of farewell to Kate!’", "케이트한테 작별 인사 한 번 없이 떠나면 안 되잖아요!\""]], [["‘I cannot make it!’ cried his trembling companion, falling back in his seat and covering his eyes.", "\"난 못 하겠어!\" 떨고 있던 동행이 외치며 자리에 털썩 기대고는 두 눈을 가렸어."], ["‘Do you see her now?", "\"지금 그 애가 보여?"], ["Is she there still?’", "아직도 거기 있어?\""]], [["‘Yes, yes!’ said Nicholas earnestly.", "\"응, 응!\" 니콜라스가 간곡히 말했어."], ["‘There!", "\"저기!"], ["She waves her hand again!", "또 손을 흔드네!"], ["I have answered it for you--and now they are out of sight.", "내가 자네 대신 답해 줬어. 그리고 이제 안 보이게 됐어."], ["Do not give way so bitterly, dear friend, don’t.", "그렇게 서럽게 무너지지 마, 친구, 그러지 마."], ["You will meet them all again.’", "자네는 그 사람들을 모두 다시 만날 거야.\""]], [["He whom he thus encouraged, raised his withered hands and clasped them fervently together.", "이렇게 격려를 받은 그는 시든 두 손을 들어 올려 간절히 맞잡았어."]], [["‘In heaven.", "\"하늘에서요."], ["I humbly pray to God in heaven.’", "저는 하늘에 계신 하느님께 겸손히 기도드려요.\""]], [["It sounded like the prayer of a broken heart.", "그 말은 마치 부서진 가슴이 올리는 기도처럼 들렸어."]], [["# CHAPTER 56", "# CHAPTER 56"]], [["Ralph Nickleby, baffled by his Nephew in his late Design, hatches a Scheme of Retaliation which Accident suggests to him, and takes into his Counsels a tried Auxiliary", "조카에게 마지막 계획을 가로막힌 랄프 니클비가 우연히 떠오른 복수의 음모를 꾸미고, 믿을 만한 옛 조력자를 끌어들이다"]], [["The course which these adventures shape out for themselves, and imperatively call upon the historian to observe, now demands that they should revert to the point they attained previously to the commencement of the last chapter, when Ralph Nickleby and Arthur Gride were left together in the house where death had so suddenly reared his dark and heavy banner.", "이 모험들이 스스로 그려 가는 줄거리, 그리고 이야기를 들려주는 사람이 반드시 따라가야 할 그 줄거리가 이제 우리에게 요구하는 건, 지난 장이 시작되기 직전에 다다랐던 그 지점으로 되돌아가는 일이야. 그러니까 랄프 니클비와 아서 그라이드가, 죽음이 그토록 갑작스럽게 어둡고 무거운 깃발을 세운 그 집에 단둘이 남겨졌던 그때 말이지."]], [["With clenched hands, and teeth ground together so firm and tight that no locking of the jaws could have fixed and riveted them more securely, Ralph stood, for some minutes, in the attitude in which he had last addressed his nephew: breathing heavily, but as rigid and motionless in other respects as if he had been a brazen statue.", "두 주먹을 꽉 쥐고, 어떤 자물쇠로 턱을 채워도 그보다 더 단단히 맞물릴 수 없을 만큼 이를 악문 채로, 랄프는 조카에게 마지막으로 말을 던졌던 그 자세 그대로 한동안 서 있었어. 거칠게 숨을 몰아쉬면서도, 다른 데는 마치 청동 조각상이라도 된 양 빳빳하게 굳어 꼼짝도 안 했지."], ["After a time, he began, by slow degrees, as a man rousing himself from heavy slumber, to relax.", "한참이 지나자 그는 깊은 잠에서 깨어나는 사람처럼 아주 천천히, 조금씩 몸을 풀기 시작했어."], ["For a moment he shook his clasped fist towards the door by which Nicholas had disappeared; and then thrusting it into his breast, as if to repress by force even this show of passion, turned round and confronted the less hardy usurer, who had not yet risen from the ground.", "잠깐 그는 니콜라스가 사라진 문 쪽을 향해 쥐고 있던 주먹을 흔들었어. 그러더니 그 마지막 격정의 표시마저 억지로 눌러 버리려는 듯 주먹을 품속에 찔러 넣고는, 몸을 돌려 아직 바닥에서 일어나지도 못한, 자기보다 배짱이 약한 고리대금업자와 마주했어."]], [["The cowering wretch, who still shook in every limb, and whose few grey hairs trembled and quivered on his head with abject dismay, tottered to his feet as he met Ralph’s eye, and, shielding his face with both hands, protested, while he crept towards the door, that it was no fault of his.", "그 잔뜩 움츠린 가엾은 자는 여전히 온몸을 사지마다 떨고 있었고, 머리에 몇 가닥 안 남은 흰머리까지 비참한 공포에 부르르 떨리고 있었어. 그러다 랄프의 눈과 마주치자 비틀비틀 일어섰지. 그러고는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 문 쪽으로 슬금슬금 기어가면서, 자기 잘못이 아니라고 하소연했어."]], [["‘Who said it was, man?’ returned Ralph, in a suppressed voice.", "\"누가 자네 잘못이라고 했나?\" 랄프가 억누른 목소리로 되받았어."], ["‘Who said it was?’", "\"누가 그랬어?\""]], [["‘You looked as if you thought I was to blame,’ said Gride, timidly.", "\"꼭 제 탓이라고 여기시는 표정이라서요.\" 그라이드가 주뼛주뼛 말했어."]], [["‘Pshaw!’ Ralph muttered, forcing a laugh.", "\"쳇!\" 랄프가 억지로 웃음을 지으며 중얼거렸어."], ["‘I blame him for not living an hour longer.", "\"내가 탓하는 건 그자가 한 시간만 더 살지 못한 거야."], ["One hour longer would have been long enough.", "한 시간만 더 살았어도 충분했을 텐데."], ["I blame no one else.’", "다른 사람은 아무도 탓하지 않아.\""]], [["‘N--n--no one else?’ said Gride.", "\"아, 아무도요?\" 그라이드가 물었어."]], [["‘Not for this mischance,’ replied Ralph. ‘I have an old score to clear with that young fellow who has carried off your mistress; but that has nothing to do with his blustering just now, for we should soon have been quit of him, but for this cursed accident.’", "\"이번 불운에 대해서는 말이지.\" 랄프가 대답했어. \"자네 신부를 채 간 그 젊은 놈하고는 갚아야 할 묵은 빚이 있긴 해. 하지만 그건 방금 그놈이 떠들어 댄 거랑은 상관없는 일이야. 이 빌어먹을 사고만 없었다면 우리는 금세 그놈을 떼어 냈을 테니까.\""]], [["There was something so unnatural in the calmness with which Ralph Nickleby spoke, when coupled with his face, the expression of the features, to which every nerve and muscle, as it twitched and throbbed with a spasm whose workings no effort could conceal, gave, every instant, some new and frightful aspect--there was something so unnatural and ghastly in the contrast between his harsh, slow, steady voice (only altered by a certain halting of the breath which made him pause between almost every word like a drunken man bent upon speaking plainly), and these evidences of the most intense and violent passion, and the struggle he made to keep them under; that if the dead body which lay above had stood, instead of him, before the cowering Gride, it could scarcely have presented a spectacle which would have terrified him more.", "랄프 니클비가 말하는 그 침착함에는 어딘가 부자연스러운 데가 있었어. 게다가 그 얼굴, 그 표정과 어우러지니 더욱 그랬지. 아무리 애를 써도 감출 수 없는 경련으로 신경이며 근육이 죄다 씰룩이고 욱신거리며 순간순간 새롭고 끔찍한 모습을 빚어내고 있었거든. 거칠고 느리고 한결같은 그 목소리는 — 거의 단어마다 사이를 두고 멈추는, 어떻게든 또박또박 말하려고 안간힘을 쓰는 술 취한 사람처럼, 숨이 자꾸 멎는 것 말고는 흐트러짐이 없었어 — 그 목소리와, 더없이 격렬하고 사나운 격정의 흔적들, 그리고 그걸 억누르려고 그가 벌이는 사투 사이의 그 대조에는 어딘가 부자연스럽고 소름 끼치는 데가 있었어. 그래서 만약 위층에 누워 있는 그 시체가 그의 자리에 일어서서 잔뜩 움츠린 그라이드 앞에 섰더라도, 이보다 더 그를 떨게 할 광경을 보여 주지는 못했을 거야."]], [["‘The coach,’ said Ralph after a time, during which he had struggled like some strong man against a fit.", "\"마차 말이야.\" 강한 사람이 발작과 싸우듯 안간힘을 쓴 한참 뒤에 랄프가 입을 열었어."], ["‘We came in a coach.", "\"우리 마차 타고 왔잖아."], ["Is it waiting?’", "그거 기다리고 있나?\""]], [["Gride gladly availed himself of the pretext for going to the window to see.", "그라이드는 잘됐다 싶어 핑계 삼아 창가로 가서 살폈어."], ["Ralph, keeping his face steadily the other way, tore at his shirt with the hand which he had thrust into his breast, and muttered in a hoarse whisper:", "랄프는 얼굴을 줄곧 반대쪽으로 돌린 채, 품에 찔러 넣었던 손으로 셔츠를 잡아 뜯으며 쉰 목소리로 속삭이듯 중얼거렸어."]], [["‘Ten thousand pounds!", "\"만 파운드라니!"], ["He said ten thousand!", "만 파운드라고 그랬어!"], ["The precise sum paid in but yesterday for the two mortgages, and which would have gone out again, at heavy interest, tomorrow.", "바로 어제 그 두 건의 저당 잡힌 돈으로 치른 그 액수가 딱 그만큼이었고, 내일이면 다시 비싼 이자를 붙여 나갈 돈이었는데."], ["If that house has failed, and he the first to bring the news!--Is the coach there?’", "그 상점이 무너졌고, 게다가 그놈이 그 소식을 제일 먼저 들고 왔단 말이지! 마차는 와 있나?\""]], [["‘Yes, yes,’ said Gride, startled by the fierce tone of the inquiry.", "\"네, 네.\" 그라이드가 그 사납게 캐묻는 말투에 놀라 대답했어."], ["‘It’s here.", "\"여기 있어요."], ["Dear, dear, what a fiery man you are!’", "아이고, 아이고, 어쩜 이렇게 불같으신지!\""]], [["‘Come here,’ said Ralph, beckoning to him.", "\"이리 와.\" 랄프가 그를 손짓해 부르며 말했어."], ["‘We mustn’t make a show of being disturbed.", "\"동요하는 티를 내선 안 돼."], ["We’ll go down arm in arm.’", "팔짱을 끼고 내려가자고.\""]], [["‘But you pinch me black and blue,’ urged Gride.", "\"근데 멍이 들도록 꼬집으시잖아요.\" 그라이드가 항의했어."]], [["Ralph let him go impatiently, and descending the stairs with his usual firm and heavy tread, got into the coach.", "랄프는 짜증스럽게 그를 놓아주고는, 늘 그렇듯 단단하고 무거운 걸음으로 계단을 내려가 마차에 올랐어. 아서 그라이드가 뒤따랐지."], ["Arthur Gride followed.", "마부가 어디로 가느냐고 묻자 그라이드는 랄프를 미심쩍게 쳐다봤어."], ["After looking doubtfully at Ralph when the man asked where he was to drive, and finding that he remained silent, and expressed no wish upon the subject, Arthur mentioned his own house, and thither they proceeded.", "그런데 랄프가 잠자코 아무 뜻도 내비치지 않자, 아서는 자기 집을 댔어. 그래서 둘은 그쪽으로 향했지."]], [["On their way, Ralph sat in the furthest corner with folded arms, and uttered not a word.", "가는 동안 랄프는 가장 구석진 자리에 팔짱을 끼고 앉아 한 마디도 안 했어."], ["With his chin sunk upon his breast, and his downcast eyes quite hidden by the contraction of his knotted brows, he might have been asleep for any sign of consciousness he gave until the coach stopped, when he raised his head, and glancing through the window, inquired what place that was.", "턱을 가슴에 묻고, 매듭처럼 뭉친 눈썹이 잔뜩 찌푸려져 내리뜬 눈을 완전히 가리고 있어서, 마차가 멈출 때까지는 깨어 있다는 기색이라곤 도무지 없어서 잠든 사람 같았어. 마차가 서자 그는 고개를 들고 창밖을 흘긋 보더니, 여기가 어디냐고 물었어."]], [["‘My house,’ answered the disconsolate Gride, affected perhaps by its loneliness.", "\"제 집이지요.\" 풀이 죽은 그라이드가 대답했어."], ["‘Oh dear!", "어쩌면 그 집의 외로운 모습에 마음이 흔들렸는지도 몰라."], ["my house.’", "\"아이고! 제 집이에요.\""]], [["‘True,’ said Ralph ‘I have not observed the way we came.", "\"그렇군.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["I should like a glass of water.", "\"오는 길을 살펴보질 못했어."], ["You have that in the house, I suppose?’", "물 한 잔 마셨으면 좋겠는데. 집에 그건 있겠지?\""]], [["‘You shall have a glass of--of anything you like,’ answered Gride, with a groan.", "\"물이든, 뭐든 원하시는 건 다 드리지요.\" 그라이드가 신음하듯 대답했어."], ["‘It’s no use knocking, coachman.", "\"두드려 봐야 소용없어요, 마부 양반."], ["Ring the bell!’", "종을 울려요!\""]], [["The man rang, and rang, and rang again; then, knocked until the street re-echoed with the sounds; then, listened at the keyhole of the door.", "마부는 종을 울리고, 또 울리고, 다시 울렸어. 그러고는 거리가 쩌렁쩌렁 울리도록 문을 두드렸지."], ["Nobody came.", "그다음에는 문의 열쇠 구멍에 귀를 댔어."], ["The house was silent as the grave.", "아무도 안 나왔어. 집은 무덤처럼 고요했지."]], [["‘How’s this?’ said Ralph impatiently.", "\"어찌 된 거야?\" 랄프가 짜증스레 말했어."]], [["‘Peg is so very deaf,’ answered Gride with a look of anxiety and alarm.", "\"페그가 귀가 아주 어둡거든요.\" 그라이드가 불안하고 겁먹은 얼굴로 대답했어."], ["‘Oh dear!", "\"아이고!"], ["Ring again, coachman.", "다시 울려요, 마부 양반."], ["She SEES the bell.’", "종은 눈으로 보는 사람이니까요.\""]], [["Again the man rang and knocked, and knocked and rang again.", "마부는 다시 종을 울리고 두드리고, 또 두드리고 울렸어."], ["Some of the neighbours threw up their windows, and called across the street to each other that old Gride’s housekeeper must have dropped down dead.", "이웃 몇몇이 창문을 열어젖히고는 길 건너로 서로 소리쳤지. 늙은 그라이드네 가정부가 쓰러져 죽은 게 틀림없다고 말이야."], ["Others collected round the coach, and gave vent to various surmises; some held that she had fallen asleep; some, that she had burnt herself to death; some, that she had got drunk; and one very fat man that she had seen something to eat which had frightened her so much (not being used to it) that she had fallen into a fit.", "또 어떤 이들은 마차 둘레로 모여들어 이런저런 추측을 늘어놓았어. 누구는 잠들었을 거라고 하고, 누구는 불에 타 죽었다 하고, 누구는 술에 취했다고 했지."], ["This last suggestion particularly delighted the bystanders, who cheered it rather uproariously, and were, with some difficulty, deterred from dropping down the area and breaking open the kitchen door to ascertain the fact.", "그리고 아주 뚱뚱한 한 남자는, 먹을 게 눈에 띄었는데 (워낙 못 먹어 본지라) 너무 놀라서 기절했을 거라고 했어. 이 마지막 말이 구경꾼들을 어찌나 즐겁게 했던지, 다들 떠들썩하게 환호했고, 진짜인지 확인하겠다고 지하 입구로 내려가 부엌문을 부수려는 걸 가까스로 말렸을 정도였어."], ["Nor was this all.", "그게 다가 아니었어."], ["Rumours having gone abroad that Arthur was to be married that morning, very particular inquiries were made after the bride, who was held by the majority to be disguised in the person of Mr.", "아서가 그날 아침 결혼한다는 소문이 퍼진 터라, 신부가 어딨냐고 자꾸 캐묻는 거였어. 대다수는 신부가 랄프 니클비 씨로 변장하고 있는 거라고 우겼어."], ["Ralph Nickleby, which gave rise to much jocose indignation at the public appearance of a bride in boots and pantaloons, and called forth a great many hoots and groans.", "그래서 부츠에 판탈롱 차림으로 신부가 공개적으로 나타났다며 농지거리 섞인 분개가 터져 나왔고, 야유와 신음 소리도 잔뜩 쏟아졌지."], ["At length, the two money-lenders obtained shelter in a house next door, and, being accommodated with a ladder, clambered over the wall of the back-yard--which was not a high one--and descended in safety on the other side.", "마침내 두 고리대금업자는 옆집에 몸을 피해, 사다리를 빌려서 뒷마당 담을 — 그리 높진 않았어 — 기어 넘어, 반대편으로 무사히 내려갔어."]], [["‘I am almost afraid to go in, I declare,’ said Arthur, turning to Ralph when they were alone.", "\"솔직히 들어가기가 무서울 지경이오.\" 둘만 남게 되자 아서가 랄프를 돌아보며 말했어."], ["‘Suppose she should be murdered.", "\"그 여자가 살해당했으면 어쩌지."], ["Lying with her brains knocked out by a poker, eh?’", "부지깽이로 머리를 얻어맞아 골이 쏟아진 채 누워 있으면 말이야, 응?\""]], [["‘Suppose she were,’ said Ralph.", "\"그러면 또 어때.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘I tell you, I wish such things were more common than they are, and more easily done.", "\"내 말하지만, 나는 그런 일이 지금보다 더 흔하고 더 쉽게 일어났으면 좋겠어. 자네는 눈이 휘둥그레져서 벌벌 떨 테지."], ["You may stare and shiver. I do!’", "나는 그러길 바란다고!\""]], [["He applied himself to a pump in the yard; and, having taken a deep draught of water and flung a quantity on his head and face, regained his accustomed manner and led the way into the house: Gride following close at his heels.", "그는 마당의 펌프로 다가갔어. 그러고는 물을 깊이 들이켜고 머리와 얼굴에 한 움큼 끼얹더니, 평소의 태도를 되찾고 앞장서서 집 안으로 들어갔어. 그라이드는 바짝 뒤꽁무니를 따라붙었지."]], [["It was the same dark place as ever: every room dismal and silent as it was wont to be, and every ghostly article of furniture in its customary place. The iron heart of the grim old clock, undisturbed by all the noise without, still beat heavily within its dusty case; the tottering presses slunk from the sight, as usual, in their melancholy corners; the echoes of footsteps returned the same dreary sound; the long-legged spider paused in his nimble run, and, scared by the sight of men in that his dull domain, hung motionless on the wall, counterfeiting death until they should have passed him by.", "여전히 똑같이 어두컴컴한 곳이었어. 방마다 늘 그렇듯 음침하고 고요했고, 으스스한 가구들도 죄다 제자리에 놓여 있었지. 그 음산한 낡은 시계의 무쇠 심장은 바깥의 온갖 소동에도 끄떡없이, 여전히 먼지 낀 통 속에서 무겁게 박동하고 있었어. 비틀거리는 찬장들은 늘 그렇듯 우중충한 구석으로 시야에서 슬그머니 숨어들었고, 발소리의 메아리는 똑같이 음울한 소리로 되돌아왔어. 다리가 긴 거미는 날쌘 걸음을 멈췄어. 그러고는 제 칙칙한 영토에 사람들이 들어온 걸 보고 겁을 먹고는, 그들이 지나갈 때까지 죽은 척하며 벽에 꼼짝 않고 매달려 있었지."]], [["From cellar to garret went the two usurers, opening every creaking door and looking into every deserted room.", "지하실부터 다락방까지 두 고리대금업자는 삐걱대는 문을 죄다 열어 가며, 텅 빈 방마다 들여다봤어."], ["But no Peg was there.", "하지만 페그는 어디에도 없었지."], ["At last, they sat them down in the apartment which Arthur Gride usually inhabited, to rest after their search.", "결국 둘은 아서 그라이드가 평소 지내던 방에 주저앉아, 한바탕 뒤진 뒤 숨을 돌렸어."]], [["‘The hag is out, on some preparation for your wedding festivities, I suppose,’ said Ralph, preparing to depart.", "\"그 노파가 자네 결혼 잔치 준비라도 하러 나갔나 보군.\" 랄프가 떠날 채비를 하며 말했어."], ["‘See here!", "\"이걸 봐!"], ["I destroy the bond; we shall never need it now.’", "내가 이 증서를 없애 버리겠네. 이제 이건 우리한테 필요 없을 테니까.\""]], [["Gride, who had been peering narrowly about the room, fell, at that moment, upon his knees before a large chest, and uttered a terrible yell.", "방 안을 샅샅이 살피던 그라이드가 바로 그 순간 큰 궤짝 앞에 무릎을 꺾고 주저앉으며, 끔찍한 비명을 질렀어."]], [["‘How now?’ said Ralph, looking sternly round.", "\"또 왜 그래?\" 랄프가 매섭게 돌아보며 말했어."]], [["‘Robbed!", "\"털렸어!"], ["robbed!’ screamed Arthur Gride.", "도둑맞았어!\" 아서 그라이드가 비명을 질렀어."]], [["‘Robbed!", "\"털려?"], ["of money?’", "돈을?\""]], [["‘No, no, no.", "\"아니, 아니, 아니야."], ["Worse!", "더 나빠!"], ["far worse!’", "훨씬 더 나쁘다고!\""]], [["‘Of what then?’ demanded Ralph.", "\"그럼 뭘?\" 랄프가 다그쳤어."]], [["‘Worse than money, worse than money!’ cried the old man, casting the papers out of the chest, like some beast tearing up the earth.", "\"돈보다 더한 거, 돈보다 더한 거야!\" 노인이 짐승이 땅을 파헤치듯 궤짝에서 서류를 마구 끄집어 던지며 울부짖었어."], ["‘She had better have stolen money--all my money--I haven’t much!", "\"차라리 돈을 훔쳐 갔으면 — 내 돈 전부를 — 많지도 않은데!"], ["She had better have made me a beggar than have done this!’", "차라리 날 거지로 만들었으면, 이런 짓을 하느니 그게 나았다고!\""]], [["‘Done what?’ said Ralph.", "\"무슨 짓?\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘Done what, you devil’s dotard?’", "\"무슨 짓을 했다는 거야, 이 노망난 악마 같은 늙은이야?\""]], [["Still Gride made no answer, but tore and scratched among the papers, and yelled and screeched like a fiend in torment.", "그래도 그라이드는 대답 없이 서류 사이를 마구 찢고 헤집으며, 고통받는 악귀처럼 악을 쓰고 비명을 질러 댔어."]], [["‘There is something missing, you say,’ said Ralph, shaking him furiously by the collar.", "\"뭔가 없어졌다는 거지.\" 랄프가 그의 멱살을 사납게 흔들며 말했어."], ["‘What is it?’", "\"그게 뭐야?\""]], [["‘Papers, deeds.", "\"서류, 증서야. 난 망했어."], ["I am a ruined man. Lost, lost!", "잃어버렸어, 잃어버렸다고!"], ["I am robbed, I am ruined!", "도둑맞았어, 난 끝장났어!"], ["She saw me reading it--reading it of late--I did very often--She watched me, saw me put it in the box that fitted into this, the box is gone, she has stolen it.", "그 여자가 내가 그걸 읽는 걸 봤어 — 요새 그걸 읽었거든 — 자주 그랬지 — 그 여자가 날 지켜보고 있었어, 내가 그걸 이 안에 꼭 맞게 들어가는 상자에 넣는 걸 봤다고, 그 상자가 사라졌어, 그 여자가 훔쳐 갔어."], ["Damnation seize her, she has robbed me!’", "빌어먹을 년, 날 털어 갔어!\""]], [["‘Of WHAT?’ cried Ralph, on whom a sudden light appeared to break, for his eyes flashed and his frame trembled with agitation as he clutched Gride by his bony arm. ‘Of what?’", "\"뭘?\" 랄프가 소리쳤어. 그에게 갑자기 어떤 빛이 번뜩이는 듯, 두 눈이 번쩍이고 온몸이 흥분으로 떨렸어. 그러면서 그라이드의 앙상한 팔을 와락 움켜쥐었지. \"뭘 훔쳐 갔다고?\""]], [["‘She don’t know what it is; she can’t read!’ shrieked Gride, not heeding the inquiry.", "\"그게 뭔지 그 여잔 몰라. 글을 못 읽으니까!\""], ["‘There’s only one way in which money can be made of it, and that is by taking it to her.", "그라이드가 그 물음은 아랑곳없이 비명을 질렀어. \"그걸로 돈을 만들 길은 딱 하나뿐이야."], ["Somebody will read it for her, and tell her what to do.", "그 여자한테 가져가는 거지. 누군가 그 여자한테 읽어 주고, 뭘 해야 할지 일러 주겠지."], ["She and her accomplice will get money for it and be let off besides; they’ll make a merit of it--say they found it--knew it--and be evidence against me.", "그 여자랑 한패가 그걸로 돈을 받아 챙기고, 게다가 풀려나기까지 할 거야. 자기들 공인 양 떠벌리겠지 — 자기들이 찾았다고, 알고 있었다고 — 그러고는 나한테 불리한 증언을 할 거야."], ["The only person it will fall upon is me, me, me!’", "화를 다 뒤집어쓸 사람은 나뿐이라고, 나, 나 말이야!\""]], [["‘Patience!’ said Ralph, clutching him still tighter and eyeing him with a sidelong look, so fixed and eager as sufficiently to denote that he had some hidden purpose in what he was about to say.", "\"진정해!\" 랄프가 그를 더 꽉 움켜쥐고는, 곁눈으로 흘끔 그를 쏘아봤어."], ["‘Hear reason.", "그 눈길이 어찌나 고정되고 간절한지, 이제 하려는 말에 무슨 숨은 속셈이 있다는 게 충분히 드러났지."], ["She can’t have been gone long.", "\"내 말 좀 들어 봐. 그 여자가 멀리 갔을 리는 없어."], ["I’ll call the police.", "내가 경찰을 부르겠네."], ["Do you but give information of what she has stolen, and they’ll lay hands upon her, trust me.", "자네는 그 여자가 뭘 훔쳤는지만 신고하면 돼. 그러면 경찰이 그 여자를 붙잡을 거야, 나만 믿어."], ["Here!", "여기요!"], ["Help!’", "사람 좀!\""]], [["‘No, no, no!’ screamed the old man, putting his hand on Ralph’s mouth.", "\"안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼!\" 노인이 랄프의 입에 손을 갖다 대며 비명을 질렀어."], ["‘I can’t, I daren’t.’", "\"못 해, 못 한다고.\""]], [["‘Help!", "\"사람 좀!"], ["help!’ cried Ralph.", "거기 누구 없소!\" 랄프가 외쳤어."]], [["‘No, no, no!’ shrieked the other, stamping on the ground with the energy of a madman.", "\"안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼!\" 다른 쪽이 미친 사람의 기세로 바닥을 쾅쾅 구르며 비명을 질렀어."], ["‘I tell you no.", "\"안 된다니까."], ["I daren’t, I daren’t!’", "못 해, 못 한다고!\""]], [["‘Daren’t make this robbery public?’ said Ralph.", "\"이 도둑맞은 걸 공개적으로 못 알리겠다고?\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘No!’ rejoined Gride, wringing his hands.", "\"못 해!\" 그라이드가 두 손을 비틀며 되받았어."], ["‘Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["Hush!", "쉿!"], ["Not a word of this; not a word must be said.", "이 일은 한 마디도 안 돼. 단 한 마디도 입에 올리면 안 돼."], ["I am undone.", "난 끝장이야."], ["Whichever way I turn, I am undone.", "어느 쪽으로 돌아서든 난 끝장이라고."], ["I am betrayed.", "난 배신당했어."], ["I shall be given up.", "난 넘겨질 거야."], ["I shall die in Newgate!’", "난 뉴게이트에서 죽게 될 거라고!\""]], [["With frantic exclamations such as these, and with many others in which fear, grief, and rage, were strangely blended, the panic-stricken wretch gradually subdued his first loud outcry, until it had softened down into a low despairing moan, chequered now and then by a howl, as, going over such papers as were left in the chest, he discovered some new loss. With very little excuse for departing so abruptly, Ralph left him, and, greatly disappointing the loiterers outside the house by telling them there was nothing the matter, got into the coach, and was driven to his own home.", "이런 미친 듯한 외침들, 그리고 두려움과 슬픔과 분노가 기묘하게 뒤섞인 다른 숱한 외침들을 쏟아 내며, 공포에 질린 그 가엾은 자는 처음의 그 큰 부르짖음을 차츰 가라앉혔어. 그러다 마침내 낮고 절망적인 신음으로 잦아들었지. 궤짝에 남은 서류들을 뒤지다 또 새로 없어진 걸 발견할 때마다, 그 신음 사이사이로 이따금 울부짖음이 끼어들었어. 이렇게 갑자기 떠날 핑계랄 게 거의 없었지만, 랄프는 그를 두고 나왔어. 그러고는 집 밖의 어슬렁대던 사람들에게 아무 일도 없다고 말해 크게 실망시킨 다음, 마차에 올라 자기 집으로 향했지."]], [["A letter lay on his table.", "탁자 위에 편지 한 통이 놓여 있었어. 그는 한동안 그걸 그대로 놔뒀어."], ["He let it lie there for some time, as if he had not the courage to open it, but at length did so and turned deadly pale.", "마치 뜯어 볼 용기가 안 나는 듯이 말이야. 그러다 마침내 뜯어 보더니, 새파랗게 질렸어."]], [["‘The worst has happened,’ he said; ‘the house has failed.", "\"최악의 일이 터졌군.\" 그가 말했어."], ["I see.", "\"그 상점이 무너졌어."], ["The rumour was abroad in the city last night, and reached the ears of those merchants.", "알겠어. 그 소문이 어젯밤 시내에 퍼져서, 그 상인들 귀에까지 들어간 거야."], ["Well, well!’", "그래, 그래!\""]], [["He strode violently up and down the room and stopped again.", "그는 방 안을 사납게 오락가락하다가 다시 멈춰 섰어."]], [["‘Ten thousand pounds!", "\"만 파운드라니!"], ["And only lying there for a day--for one day!", "그것도 거기 단 하루 놓여 있었을 뿐인데 — 단 하루!"], ["How many anxious years, how many pinching days and sleepless nights, before I scraped together that ten thousand pounds!--Ten thousand pounds!", "그 만 파운드를 긁어모으기까지 얼마나 많은 애태운 세월을, 얼마나 많은 쪼들린 낮과 잠 못 이룬 밤을 보냈는데! 만 파운드라니!"], ["How many proud painted dames would have fawned and smiled, and how many spendthrift blockheads done me lip-service to my face and cursed me in their hearts, while I turned that ten thousand pounds into twenty!", "내가 그 만 파운드를 두 배로 불리는 동안, 잘난 척 분칠한 귀부인들이 얼마나 알랑거리고 미소 지었을 테며, 돈 헤프게 쓰는 멍청이들이 내 면전에서는 입에 발린 소리를 하고 속으로는 나를 욕했을까!"], ["While I ground, and pinched, and used these needy borrowers for my pleasure and profit, what smooth-tongued speeches, and courteous looks, and civil letters, they would have given me!", "내가 이 궁한 돈 빌리는 자들을 짜내고 쥐어짜며 내 즐거움과 잇속을 위해 부려 먹는 동안, 그들이 나한테 얼마나 매끄러운 말과 공손한 표정과 정중한 편지를 바쳤을까!"], ["The cant of the lying world is, that men like me compass our riches by dissimulation and treachery: by fawning, cringing, and stooping.", "거짓투성이 세상의 헛소리는, 나 같은 사람이 위선과 배신으로, 알랑거리고 굽실대고 비굴하게 굴어 재산을 모은다는 거야."], ["Why, how many lies, what mean and abject evasions, what humbled behaviour from upstarts who, but for my money, would spurn me aside as they do their betters every day, would that ten thousand pounds have brought me in!", "흥, 그 만 파운드가 나한테 얼마나 많은 거짓말을, 얼마나 비열하고 천한 핑계를, 얼마나 굽실대는 짓거리를 가져다줬겠어. 내 돈이 아니었으면 매일 저보다 나은 사람들을 발로 차듯 나를 밀쳐 냈을 그 벼락출세한 것들한테서 말이야!"], ["Grant that I had doubled it--made cent.", "내가 그걸 두 배로 불려 — 백 퍼센트를 남겨 — 금화 한 닢이 또 한 닢을 불러왔다 치자."], ["per cent.--for every sovereign told another--there would not be one piece of money in all the heap which wouldn’t represent ten thousand mean and paltry lies, told, not by the money-lender, oh no!", "그 무더기 속 동전 한 닢 한 닢이 죄다 만 개의 비열하고 하찮은 거짓말을 나타내지 않는 게 없을 거야. 그것도 돈 빌려준 자가 한 거짓말이 아니라, 오 천만에!"], ["but by the money-borrowers, your liberal, thoughtless, generous, dashing folks, who wouldn’t be so mean as save a sixpence for the world!’", "돈 빌린 자들, 그 후하고 생각 없고 인심 좋고 화통한 작자들, 죽어도 육 펜스 한 푼 아껴 둘 만큼 쩨쩨하진 않은 그자들이 한 거짓말이지!\""]], [["Striving, as it would seem, to lose part of the bitterness of his regrets in the bitterness of these other thoughts, Ralph continued to pace the room.", "이 다른 쓰디쓴 생각들로 자기 후회의 쓰라림을 얼마쯤 잊으려는 듯, 랄프는 계속 방 안을 서성거렸어."], ["There was less and less of resolution in his manner as his mind gradually reverted to his loss; at length, dropping into his elbow-chair and grasping its sides so firmly that they creaked again, he said:", "그런데 마음이 차츰 잃어버린 돈으로 되돌아가면서, 그 태도에서 결연함이 점점 옅어졌어. 마침내 그는 안락의자에 털썩 주저앉아, 다시 삐걱댈 만큼 양옆을 단단히 움켜쥐고는 말했어."]], [["‘The time has been when nothing could have moved me like the loss of this great sum.", "\"한때는 이 큰돈을 잃은 것만큼 나를 흔들 수 있는 게 없었지. 아무것도 없었어."], ["Nothing. For births, deaths, marriages, and all the events which are of interest to most men, have (unless they are connected with gain or loss of money) no interest for me.", "출생이며 죽음이며 결혼이며, 대다수 사람들에게 흥미로운 그 모든 일들이 (돈을 따거나 잃는 거랑 얽히지 않은 한) 나한테는 아무 흥미도 없었으니까."], ["But now, I swear, I mix up with the loss, his triumph in telling it.", "하지만 이제는, 맹세컨대, 이 손해에다 그놈이 그걸 일러 주며 우쭐대던 게 뒤섞인단 말이야."], ["If he had brought it about,--I almost feel as if he had,--I couldn’t hate him more.", "그놈이 이 일을 꾸민 거라면 — 거의 그놈이 그런 것만 같아 — 더 미워할 수도 없을 만큼 밉겠지."], ["Let me but retaliate upon him, by degrees, however slow--let me but begin to get the better of him, let me but turn the scale--and I can bear it.’", "그놈한테 복수만 할 수 있다면, 아무리 더디더라도 조금씩이라도 말이야 — 그놈을 한 발씩 이기기 시작만 할 수 있다면, 저울추를 돌려놓기만 할 수 있다면 — 그러면 나는 이걸 견딜 수 있어.\""]], [["His meditations were long and deep.", "그의 궁리는 길고도 깊었어."], ["They terminated in his dispatching a letter by Newman, addressed to Mr.", "그 끝에 그는 뉴먼을 시켜 편지 한 통을 보냈지."], ["Squeers at the Saracen’s Head, with instructions to inquire whether he had arrived in town, and, if so, to wait an answer.", "사라센스 헤드에 묵는 스퀴어스 씨 앞으로 보내는 거였어."], ["Newman brought back the information that Mr.", "그가 시내에 도착했는지 알아보고, 도착했으면 답을 기다리라고 일러 두었지."], ["Squeers had come by mail that morning, and had received the letter in bed; but that he sent his duty, and word that he would get up and wait upon Mr Nickleby directly.", "뉴먼이 가져온 소식은, 스퀴어스 씨가 그날 아침 우편 마차로 와서 침대에서 그 편지를 받았다는 거였어. 그리고 안부를 전하며, 곧 일어나 니클비 씨를 찾아뵙겠다고 했대."]], [["The interval between the delivery of this message, and the arrival of Mr Squeers, was very short; but, before he came, Ralph had suppressed every sign of emotion, and once more regained the hard, immovable, inflexible manner which was habitual to him, and to which, perhaps, was ascribable no small part of the influence which, over many men of no very strong prejudices on the score of morality, he could exert, almost at will.", "이 전갈을 받은 때와 스퀴어스 씨가 도착한 때 사이의 틈은 아주 짧았어. 하지만 그가 오기 전에 랄프는 감정의 기색을 죄다 눌러 버리고, 다시 한번 그 단단하고 꿈쩍 않고 굽힐 줄 모르는, 몸에 밴 태도를 되찾았어. 어쩌면 그가 도덕에 관해 그리 강한 편견이 없는 숱한 사람들에게 거의 마음대로 부릴 수 있던 그 영향력의 적잖은 부분이, 바로 그런 태도 덕분이었는지도 몰라."]], [["‘Well, Mr.", "\"그래, 스퀴어스 씨.\" 그가 늘 짓던 그 미소로 이 훌륭한 양반을 맞으며 말했어."], ["Squeers,’ he said, welcoming that worthy with his accustomed smile, of which a sharp look and a thoughtful frown were part and parcel: ‘how do YOU do?’", "그 미소에는 날카로운 눈초리와 생각에 잠긴 듯한 찌푸림이 한 짝으로 따라붙어 있었지. \"어떻게 지내시나?\""]], [["‘Why, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"아, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어."], ["Squeers, ‘I’m pretty well.", "\"저야 그럭저럭 잘 지냅니다."], ["So’s the family, and so’s the boys, except for a sort of rash as is a running through the school, and rather puts ‘em off their feed.", "식구들도, 애들도 다 그렇고요. 다만 학교에 뭔 발진 같은 게 돌아서 애들이 통 입맛을 잃긴 했지만요."], ["But it’s a ill wind as blows no good to nobody; that’s what I always say when them lads has a wisitation.", "그래도 아무한테도 득 안 되는 나쁜 바람은 없는 법이지요. 그게 애들한테 시련이 닥칠 때마다 제가 늘 하는 말입니다."], ["A wisitation, sir, is the lot of mortality.", "시련이란 게, 선생님, 죽을 운명을 타고난 자의 몫이지요."], ["Mortality itself, sir, is a wisitation.", "죽음 그 자체가, 선생님, 하나의 시련이고요."], ["The world is chock full of wisitations; and if a boy repines at a wisitation and makes you uncomfortable with his noise, he must have his head punched.", "세상은 시련으로 꽉꽉 들어차 있어요. 그러니 애가 시련에 투덜대며 시끄럽게 굴어 사람을 불편하게 하면, 머리통을 한 대 쥐어박아야 하는 겁니다."], ["That’s going according to the Scripter, that is.’", "그게 바로 성경 말씀대로 하는 거지요, 그게.\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"스퀴어스 씨.\""], ["Squeers,’ said Ralph, drily.", "랄프가 무뚝뚝하게 말했어."]], [["‘Sir.’", "\"네.\""]], [["‘We’ll avoid these precious morsels of morality if you please, and talk of business.’", "\"이 귀하신 도덕 부스러기는 그만두고, 일 얘기나 합시다.\""]], [["‘With all my heart, sir,’ rejoined Squeers, ‘and first let me say--’", "\"기꺼이요, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 되받았어. \"그럼 먼저 제가 한마디 드리자면—\""]], [["‘First let ME say, if you please.--Noggs!’", "\"먼저 내가 한마디 하지. — 노그스!\""]], [["Newman presented himself when the summons had been twice or thrice repeated, and asked if his master called.", "뉴먼은 두세 번 거듭 부르고 나서야 나타나, 주인이 부르셨냐고 물었어."]], [["‘I did.", "\"불렀지."], ["Go to your dinner.", "밥 먹으러 가게."], ["And go at once.", "당장 가."], ["Do you hear?’", "알아들었나?\""]], [["‘It an’t time,’ said Newman, doggedly.", "\"아직 시간이 안 됐습니다.\" 뉴먼이 고집스럽게 말했어."], ["‘My time is yours, and I say it is,’ returned Ralph.", "\"내 시간이 곧 자네 시간이고, 지금이 그 시간이라면 그런 거야.\" 랄프가 되받았어."]], [["‘You alter it every day,’ said Newman.", "\"날마다 시간을 바꾸시잖아요.\" 뉴먼이 말했어."], ["‘It isn’t fair.’", "\"그건 공평치 않아요.\""]], [["‘You don’t keep many cooks, and can easily apologise to them for the trouble,’ retorted Ralph.", "\"자네가 무슨 요리사를 여럿 거느린 것도 아니니, 그자들한테 폐 끼친 거야 쉽게 사과하면 되겠지.\" 랄프가 쏘아붙였어."], ["‘Begone, sir!’", "\"썩 나가게!\""]], [["Ralph not only issued this order in his most peremptory manner, but, under pretence of fetching some papers from the little office, saw it obeyed, and, when Newman had left the house, chained the door, to prevent the possibility of his returning secretly, by means of his latch-key.", "랄프는 이 명령을 더없이 단호하게 내렸을 뿐 아니라, 작은 사무실에서 서류를 가지러 가는 척하면서 그게 지켜지는지 지켜봤어. 그리고 뉴먼이 집을 나서자, 그가 빗장열쇠로 몰래 되돌아올 가능성을 막으려고 문에 사슬을 채웠지."]], [["‘I have reason to suspect that fellow,’ said Ralph, when he returned to his own office.", "\"저놈을 의심할 만한 이유가 있단 말이야.\" 자기 사무실로 돌아오며 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘Therefore, until I have thought of the shortest and least troublesome way of ruining him, I hold it best to keep him at a distance.’", "\"그러니 저놈을 가장 빠르고 가장 손 덜 가게 망쳐 버릴 방법을 궁리해 낼 때까지는, 멀찍이 떼어 놓는 게 상책이지.\""]], [["‘It wouldn’t take much to ruin him, I should think,’ said Squeers, with a grin.", "\"그자를 망치는 데야 그리 많은 게 들지도 않을 것 같습니다만.\" 스퀴어스가 히죽 웃으며 말했어."]], [["‘Perhaps not,’ answered Ralph.", "\"아마 그렇겠지.\" 랄프가 대답했어."], ["‘Nor to ruin a great many people whom I know.", "\"내가 아는 숱한 사람들을 망치는 데도 별로 안 들 테고."], ["You were going to say--?’", "그래, 무슨 말을 하려던 거였소?\""]], [["Ralph’s summary and matter-of-course way of holding up this example, and throwing out the hint that followed it, had evidently an effect (as doubtless it was designed to have) upon Mr.", "랄프가 이런 예를 막힘없이 당연하다는 듯 들어 보이고, 그 뒤에 슬쩍 흘린 그 암시는, 스퀴어스 씨에게 분명 (틀림없이 그러려고 의도한 대로) 어떤 효과를 냈어."], ["Squeers, who said, after a little hesitation and in a much more subdued tone:", "그는 잠깐 망설이더니 한결 죽은 목소리로 말했어."]], [["‘Why, what I was a-going to say, sir, is, that this here business regarding of that ungrateful and hard-hearted chap, Snawley senior, puts me out of my way, and occasions a inconveniency quite unparalleled, besides, as I may say, making, for whole weeks together, Mrs.", "\"아, 제가 하려던 말은요, 선생님. 그 배은망덕하고 매정한 작자, 스놀리 영감을 둘러싼 이 일이 저를 영 곤란하게 한다는 겁니다."], ["Squeers a perfect widder.", "비할 데 없는 불편을 끼친다는 거지요."], ["It’s a pleasure to me to act with you, of course.’", "게다가, 말하자면, 몇 주씩이나 스퀴어스 부인을 영락없는 과부 신세로 만들기도 하고요. 물론 선생님과 함께 일하는 건 저한테 기쁨이지만요.\""]], [["‘Of course,’ said Ralph, drily.", "\"물론이지.\" 랄프가 무뚝뚝하게 말했어."]], [["‘Yes, I say of course,’ resumed Mr.", "\"네, 물론이라고 했지요.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 무릎을 문지르며 말을 이었어."], ["Squeers, rubbing his knees, ‘but at the same time, when one comes, as I do now, better than two hundred and fifty mile to take a afferdavid, it does put a man out a good deal, letting alone the risk.’", "\"하지만 동시에, 지금 저처럼 이백오십 마일도 넘게 와서 진술서에 선서를 하려니, 그게 사람을 꽤나 곤란하게 한단 말입니다. 위험은 차치하고서라도요.\""]], [["‘And where may the risk be, Mr.", "\"그래, 그 위험이라는 게 대체 어디 있는데요, 스퀴어스 씨?\""], ["Squeers?’ said Ralph.", "랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘I said, letting alone the risk,’ replied Squeers, evasively.", "\"위험은 차치하고서라고 했습니다.\" 스퀴어스가 얼버무리며 대답했어."]], [["‘And I said, where was the risk?’", "\"그래서 내가 묻잖소, 그 위험이 어디 있냐고?\""]], [["‘I wasn’t complaining, you know, Mr.", "\"불평하는 건 아니에요, 아시잖아요, 니클비 씨.\""], ["Nickleby,’ pleaded Squeers.", "스퀴어스가 사정조로 말했어."], ["‘Upon my word I never see such a--’", "\"정말이지, 저는 이런 분은 처음—\""]], [["‘I ask you where is the risk?’ repeated Ralph, emphatically.", "\"위험이 어디 있냐고 묻고 있잖소.\" 랄프가 힘주어 거듭 말했어."]], [["‘Where the risk?’ returned Squeers, rubbing his knees still harder.", "\"위험이 어디냐고요?\" 스퀴어스가 무릎을 더 세게 문지르며 되받았어."], ["‘Why, it an’t necessary to mention.", "\"아니, 그거야 입에 올릴 필요가 없는 거지요."], ["Certain subjects is best awoided.", "어떤 얘긴 안 꺼내는 게 상책이고요."], ["Oh, you know what risk I mean.’", "아, 제가 무슨 위험을 말하는지 아시잖아요.\""]], [["‘How often have I told you,’ said Ralph, ‘and how often am I to tell you, that you run no risk?", "\"내가 몇 번이나 말했고,\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["What have you sworn, or what are you asked to swear, but that at such and such a time a boy was left with you in the name of Smike; that he was at your school for a given number of years, was lost under such and such circumstances, is now found, and has been identified by you in such and such keeping?", "\"또 몇 번이나 말해 줘야 하나, 당신한테는 아무 위험도 없다고? 당신이 선서한 게, 아니 선서하라고 요청받은 게 뭐요?"], ["This is all true; is it not?’", "그저, 언제 어느 때 스마이크라는 이름의 아이가 당신한테 맡겨졌고, 그 아이가 몇 해 동안 당신 학교에 있었고, 이러저러한 사정으로 사라졌다가, 이제 다시 찾았고, 당신이 어디 어디 맡겨진 그 아이를 알아봤다는 것뿐이잖소? 이건 다 사실이지, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Squeers, ‘that’s all true.’", "\"네.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"그건 다 사실이지요.\""]], [["‘Well, then,’ said Ralph, ‘what risk do you run?", "\"그럼 됐잖소.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["Who swears to a lie but Snawley; a man whom I have paid much less than I have you?’", "\"무슨 위험을 무릅쓴다는 거요? 거짓 선서를 하는 건 스놀리뿐이지, 내가 당신보다 훨씬 적게 돈을 준 그자 말이오.\""]], [["‘He certainly did it cheap, did Snawley,’ observed Squeers.", "\"스놀리는 확실히 싸게 했지요, 그 사람은.\" 스퀴어스가 한마디 했어."]], [["‘He did it cheap!’ retorted Ralph, testily; ‘yes, and he did it well, and carries it off with a hypocritical face and a sanctified air, but you!", "\"싸게 했다고!\" 랄프가 짜증스레 받아쳤어."], ["Risk!", "\"그래, 게다가 잘도 해냈지."], ["What do you mean by risk?", "위선적인 얼굴에 거룩한 척하는 태도로 그걸 척척 해내고 있잖소. 근데 당신은!"], ["The certificates are all genuine, Snawley HAD another son, he HAS been married twice, his first wife IS dead, none but her ghost could tell that she didn’t write that letter, none but Snawley himself can tell that this is not his son, and that his son is food for worms!", "위험이라니! 위험이라는 게 대체 무슨 소리요?"], ["The only perjury is Snawley’s, and I fancy he is pretty well used to it.", "증명서는 죄다 진짜고, 스놀리한테 다른 아들이 있었고, 그는 두 번 결혼했고, 첫 부인은 죽었고, 그 여자가 그 편지를 안 썼다는 건 그 여자 귀신 말고는 아무도 못 밝히고, 이 아이가 제 아들이 아니라는 것도, 제 아들은 구더기 밥이 됐다는 것도, 스놀리 본인 말고는 아무도 못 밝힌단 말이오! 위증이라곤 스놀리가 하는 것뿐이고, 그자야 그런 거 꽤나 이골이 났을 거요."], ["Where’s your risk?’", "당신 위험이 어디 있다는 거요?\""]], [["‘Why, you know,’ said Squeers, fidgeting in his chair, ‘if you come to that, I might say where’s yours?’", "\"아니, 그게 말이죠.\" 스퀴어스가 의자에서 안절부절못하며 말했어. \"그렇게 따지자면, 저도 선생님 위험은 어디 있냐고 물을 수 있겠는데요.\""]], [["‘You might say where’s mine!’ returned Ralph; ‘you may say where’s mine.", "\"내 위험이 어디 있냐고 물을 수 있다!\" 랄프가 되받았어."], ["I don’t appear in the business, neither do you.", "\"그래, 내 위험이 어디 있냐고 물어도 좋소."], ["All Snawley’s interest is to stick well to the story he has told; and all his risk is, to depart from it in the least.", "나는 이 일에 나서지도 않고, 당신도 마찬가지요. 스놀리한테 득이 되는 건 자기가 한 얘기를 끝까지 단단히 붙잡는 것뿐이고, 그자가 무릅쓰는 위험이라곤 그 얘기에서 조금이라도 벗어나는 것뿐이지."], ["Talk of YOUR risk in the conspiracy!’", "그 음모에서 당신 위험을 운운하다니!\""]], [["‘I say,’ remonstrated Squeers, looking uneasily round: ‘don’t call it that!", "\"이봐요.\" 스퀴어스가 불안하게 둘레를 살피며 항의했어."], ["Just as a favour, don’t.’", "\"그걸 그렇게 부르지 마세요! 그저 부탁이니까, 그러지 마시라고요.\""]], [["‘Call it what you like,’ said Ralph, irritably, ‘but attend to me.", "\"부르고 싶은 대로 부르시오.\" 랄프가 짜증스레 말했어."], ["This tale was originally fabricated as a means of annoyance against one who hurt your trade and half cudgelled you to death, and to enable you to obtain repossession of a half-dead drudge, whom you wished to regain, because, while you wreaked your vengeance on him for his share in the business, you knew that the knowledge that he was again in your power would be the best punishment you could inflict upon your enemy.", "\"하지만 내 말 잘 들어요. 이 이야기는 본디, 당신 장사를 망치고 당신을 거의 때려죽일 뻔한 한 놈을 골탕 먹이는 수단으로, 그리고 당신이 되찾고 싶어 하던 반쯤 죽은 일꾼 하나를 도로 손에 넣게 해 주려고 꾸며진 거였소."], ["Is that so, Mr.", "당신이 그놈한테 그 일에 끼어든 죗값으로 앙갚음하는 동안, 그놈이 다시 당신 손아귀에 들어왔다는 걸 안다는 게, 당신 원수한테 줄 수 있는 가장 큰 벌이라는 걸 당신이 알았으니까."], ["Squeers?’", "그렇지 않소, 스퀴어스 씨?\""]], [["‘Why, sir,’ returned Squeers, almost overpowered by the determination which Ralph displayed to make everything tell against him, and by his stern unyielding manner, ‘in a measure it was.’", "\"아, 선생님.\" 스퀴어스가 되받았어. 랄프가 모든 걸 자기한테 불리하게 돌려놓겠다고 마음먹은 그 결연함과, 그 매섭고 굽힐 줄 모르는 태도에 거의 압도된 채로 말이야. \"어느 정도는 그랬지요.\""]], [["‘What does that mean?’ said Ralph.", "\"그게 무슨 뜻이오?\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Why, in a measure means,’ returned Squeers, ‘as it may be, that it wasn’t all on my account, because you had some old grudge to satisfy, too.’", "\"아니, 어느 정도라는 건요.\" 스퀴어스가 되받았어. \"어쩌면, 그게 다 저 때문만은 아니었다는 거지요. 선생님도 풀어야 할 묵은 원한이 좀 있었으니까요.\""]], [["‘If I had not had,’ said Ralph, in no way abashed by the reminder, ‘do you think I should have helped you?’", "\"내가 그게 없었다면,\" 랄프가 그 상기시키는 말에 조금도 무안해하지 않고 말했어. \"내가 당신을 도왔을 것 같소?\""]], [["‘Why no, I don’t suppose you would,’ Squeers replied.", "\"아니, 그러진 않으셨겠지요.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."], ["‘I only wanted that point to be all square and straight between us.’", "\"전 그저 그 점이 우리 사이에 깔끔하고 분명하게 정리됐으면 했던 겁니다.\""]], [["‘How can it ever be otherwise?’ retorted Ralph.", "\"그게 어떻게 달리 될 수가 있겠소?\" 랄프가 되받았어."], ["‘Except that the account is against me, for I spend money to gratify my hatred, and you pocket it, and gratify yours at the same time.", "\"셈이 나한테 불리하다는 것 말고는 말이오. 나는 내 증오를 채우려고 돈을 쓰는데, 당신은 그걸 챙기면서 동시에 당신 증오까지 채우니까."], ["You are, at least, as avaricious as you are revengeful.", "당신은 적어도 복수심만큼이나 탐욕스럽소. 나도 그렇고."], ["So am I. Which is best off?", "누가 더 이득이오?"], ["You, who win money and revenge, at the same time and by the same process, and who are, at all events, sure of money, if not of revenge; or I, who am only sure of spending money in any case, and can but win bare revenge at last?’", "같은 시간에 같은 과정으로 돈과 복수를 다 얻고, 복수는 못 해도 적어도 돈은 확실히 손에 쥐는 당신이오? 아니면 어떤 경우든 돈을 쓰는 것만 확실하고, 기껏해야 마지막에 알맹이뿐인 복수나 거머쥘 수 있는 나요?\""]], [["As Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨는 이 물음에 그저 어깨를 으쓱하고 미소를 짓는 것밖에 대답할 게 없었어."], ["Squeers could only answer this proposition by shrugs and smiles, Ralph bade him be silent, and thankful that he was so well off; and then, fixing his eyes steadily upon him, proceeded to say:", "그러자 랄프는 그더러 잠자코 있으라고, 그만큼 형편이 좋은 걸 고맙게 여기라고 했지. 그러고는 그에게 눈을 단단히 고정한 채 말을 이었어."]], [["First, that Nicholas had thwarted him in a plan he had formed for the disposal in marriage of a certain young lady, and had, in the confusion attendant on her father’s sudden death, secured that lady himself, and borne her off in triumph.", "첫째, 니콜라스가 어떤 젊은 아가씨를 시집보내려고 자기가 짜 둔 계획을 가로막고는, 그 아가씨 아버지가 갑자기 죽으며 벌어진 혼란을 틈타 그 아가씨를 제 손에 넣어 의기양양하게 데려가 버렸다는 것."]], [["Secondly, that by some will or settlement--certainly by some instrument in writing, which must contain the young lady’s name, and could be, therefore, easily selected from others, if access to the place where it was deposited were once secured--she was entitled to property which, if the existence of this deed ever became known to her, would make her husband (and Ralph represented that Nicholas was certain to marry her) a rich and prosperous man, and most formidable enemy.", "둘째, 어떤 유언장이나 양도 증서 — 분명 글로 쓰인 어떤 증서로, 거기엔 그 아가씨 이름이 들어 있을 테니, 일단 그게 보관된 곳에 들어가기만 한다면 다른 것들 사이에서 쉽게 골라낼 수 있는 그런 증서 — 에 따라, 그 아가씨가 어떤 재산을 받을 권리가 있다는 것. 그리고 이 증서의 존재를 그 아가씨가 알게 된다면, 그 남편을 (랄프는 니콜라스가 틀림없이 그 아가씨와 결혼할 거라고 단언했지) 부유하고 잘나가는 사람으로, 더없이 무서운 적으로 만들어 줄 거라는 것."]], [["Thirdly, that this deed had been, with others, stolen from one who had himself obtained or concealed it fraudulently, and who feared to take any steps for its recovery; and that he (Ralph) knew the thief.", "셋째, 이 증서가 다른 것들과 함께, 그걸 손에 넣었거나 부정하게 감춰 둔 어떤 자에게서 도둑맞았는데, 그자는 그걸 되찾으려 무슨 손을 쓰는 걸 두려워한다는 것. 그리고 자기(랄프)가 그 도둑이 누군지 안다는 것."]], [["To all this Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨는 이 모든 걸 한 음절 한 음절 집어삼킬 듯 탐욕스러운 귀로 들었어."], ["Squeers listened, with greedy ears that devoured every syllable, and with his one eye and his mouth wide open: marvelling for what special reason he was honoured with so much of Ralph’s confidence, and to what it all tended.", "하나뿐인 눈과 입을 떡 벌린 채로 말이야. 무슨 특별한 까닭으로 자기가 랄프의 이런 깊은 신임을 입게 됐는지, 또 이 모든 게 다 무엇을 향한 건지 어리둥절해하면서 말이지."]], [["‘Now,’ said Ralph, leaning forward, and placing his hand on Squeers’s arm, ‘hear the design which I have conceived, and which I must--I say, must, if I can ripen it--have carried into execution. No advantage can be reaped from this deed, whatever it is, save by the girl herself, or her husband; and the possession of this deed by one or other of them is indispensable to any advantage being gained. THAT I have discovered beyond the possibility of doubt. I want that deed brought here, that I may give the man who brings it fifty pounds in gold, and burn it to ashes before his face.’", "\"자.\" 랄프가 몸을 앞으로 기울이며 스퀴어스의 팔에 손을 얹고 말했어. \"내가 품은 계획을 들어 보시오. 내가 이걸 무르익게만 할 수 있다면, 반드시 — 내 말하지만, 반드시 — 실행에 옮겨야 할 계획이오. 이 증서로, 그게 뭐든 간에, 이득을 거둘 수 있는 사람은 그 아가씨 본인이나 그 남편뿐이오. 그리고 둘 중 누구라도 이득을 보려면, 이 증서를 손에 쥐는 게 반드시 필요하지. 그 점은 내가 한 치 의심의 여지 없이 알아냈소. 나는 그 증서를 여기로 가져왔으면 하오. 그래서 그걸 가져온 자한테 금화 오십 파운드를 주고, 그자 면전에서 그걸 재가 되도록 태워 버릴 작정이오.\""]], [["Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨는 랄프의 손이 벽난로 쪽을 가리키는 동작을, 마치 지금 당장 그 종이를 태워 없애기라도 하는 양 눈으로 좇았어."], ["Squeers, after following with his eye the action of Ralph’s hand towards the fire-place as if he were at that moment consuming the paper, drew a long breath, and said:", "그러더니 길게 숨을 내쉬며 말했지."]], [["‘Yes; but who’s to bring it?’", "\"네, 근데 그걸 누가 가져옵니까?\""]], [["‘Nobody, perhaps, for much is to be done before it can be got at,’ said Ralph.", "\"어쩌면 아무도 못 할지도 모르지. 거기 손대기까지 할 일이 많으니까.\""], ["‘But if anybody--you!’", "랄프가 말했어. \"하지만 만약 누군가가 한다면 — 바로 당신이오!\""]], [["Mr. Squeers’s first tokens of consternation, and his flat relinquishment of the task, would have staggered most men, if they had not immediately occasioned an utter abandonment of the proposition.", "스퀴어스 씨가 처음에 보인 그 경악한 기색이며, 그 일을 딱 잘라 못 하겠다고 손사래 친 것은, 대부분의 사람이라면 휘청하게 만들었을 거야."], ["On Ralph they produced not the slightest effect.", "그래서 곧장 그 제안을 아예 거두어들이게 했을 테지. 그런데 랄프한테는 조금도 영향을 못 미쳤어."], ["Resuming, when the schoolmaster had quite talked himself out of breath, as coolly as if he had never been interrupted, Ralph proceeded to expatiate on such features of the case as he deemed it most advisable to lay the greatest stress on.", "교장이 숨이 차도록 실컷 떠들고 나자, 랄프는 끊긴 적도 없었던 양 태연하게 말을 다시 이었어. 그러고는 가장 힘주어 강조하는 게 좋겠다 싶은 점들을 길게 늘어놓기 시작했지."]], [["These were, the age, decrepitude, and weakness of Mrs. Sliderskew; the great improbability of her having any accomplice or even acquaintance: taking into account her secluded habits, and her long residence in such a house as Gride’s; the strong reason there was to suppose that the robbery was not the result of a concerted plan: otherwise she would have watched an opportunity of carrying off a sum of money; the difficulty she would be placed in when she began to think on what she had done, and found herself encumbered with documents of whose nature she was utterly ignorant; and the comparative ease with which somebody, with a full knowledge of her position, obtaining access to her, and working on her fears, if necessary, might worm himself into her confidence and obtain, under one pretence or another, free possession of the deed. To these were added such considerations as the constant residence of Mr. Squeers at a long distance from London, which rendered his association with Mrs Sliderskew a mere masquerading frolic, in which nobody was likely to recognise him, either at the time or afterwards; the impossibility of Ralph’s undertaking the task himself, he being already known to her by sight; and various comments on the uncommon tact and experience of Mr Squeers: which would make his overreaching one old woman a mere matter of child’s play and amusement. In addition to these influences and persuasions, Ralph drew, with his utmost skill and power, a vivid picture of the defeat which Nicholas would sustain, should they succeed, in linking himself to a beggar, where he expected to wed an heiress--glanced at the immeasurable importance it must be to a man situated as Squeers, to preserve such a friend as himself--dwelt on a long train of benefits, conferred since their first acquaintance, when he had reported favourably of his treatment of a sickly boy who had died under his hands (and whose death was very convenient to Ralph and his clients, but this he did NOT say), and finally hinted that the fifty pounds might be increased to seventy-five, or, in the event of very great success, even to a hundred.", "그건 이런 거였어. 슬라이더스큐 부인이 나이가 많고 늙어 빠지고 약하다는 것. 한패는커녕 아는 사람조차 있을 가능성이 거의 없다는 것 — 그녀의 두문불출하는 습성과, 그라이드네 같은 집에서 오래 살아온 점을 미루어 보면 말이지. 이 도둑질이 미리 짜 둔 계획의 결과가 아니라고 볼 만한 강력한 근거가 있다는 것 — 안 그랬다면 그녀는 한몫 챙길 기회를 노렸을 테니까. 자기가 한 짓을 곰곰이 생각해 보다가, 무슨 내용인지 도무지 모르는 서류들을 잔뜩 짊어진 신세라는 걸 깨닫게 됐을 때 그녀가 처할 곤란한 처지. 그리고 그녀의 형편을 훤히 아는 누군가가 그녀에게 접근해, 필요하다면 그녀의 두려움을 부추겨 가며, 이런저런 핑계로 그녀의 신임을 파고들어 그 증서를 손에 넣는 게 비교적 쉬우리라는 것. 여기에 이런 점들도 덧붙였어. 스퀴어스 씨가 늘 런던에서 한참 떨어진 데 산다는 것 — 그러니 그가 슬라이더스큐 부인과 어울린다 해도 그냥 가장무도회 놀음 같은 거라, 그때든 나중이든 그를 알아볼 사람이 없으리라는 것. 랄프 본인은 그녀가 이미 얼굴을 아는 터라 이 일을 직접 맡을 수 없다는 것. 그리고 스퀴어스 씨의 보기 드문 수완과 경험에 관한 갖가지 칭찬 — 그 정도면 노파 하나 구워삶는 거야 애들 장난이자 심심풀이일 거라는 것. 이런 회유와 설득에 더해, 랄프는 온 솜씨와 힘을 다해, 만약 일이 잘되면 니콜라스가 겪을 낭패를 생생하게 그려 보였어. 상속녀와 결혼하리라 기대했던 자리에서 거지와 짝지어지는 꼴 말이야. 또 스퀴어스 같은 처지의 사람에게 자기 같은 친구를 곁에 두는 게 얼마나 헤아릴 수 없이 중요한지 슬쩍 내비쳤어. 처음 알고 지낸 이래로 베풀어 준 숱한 은혜를 길게 늘어놓기도 했지. 스퀴어스의 손에서 죽은 병약한 아이 하나를 두고, 그가 그 아이를 잘 보살폈다고 좋게 보고해 준 일도 (그 아이의 죽음은 랄프와 그의 고객들한테 아주 요긴했지만, 이 말은 안 했어) 들먹였어. 그리고 끝으로, 그 오십 파운드를 칠십오 파운드로 올려 줄 수도 있고, 일이 아주 크게 잘되면 백 파운드까지도 줄 수 있다고 넌지시 비쳤지."]], [["These arguments at length concluded, Mr.", "이 설득이 마침내 끝나자, 스퀴어스 씨는 다리를 꼬았다가 풀고, 머리를 긁고, 눈을 비비고, 손바닥을 들여다보고, 손톱을 물어뜯었어."], ["Squeers crossed his legs, uncrossed them, scratched his head, rubbed his eye, examined the palms of his hands, and bit his nails, and after exhibiting many other signs of restlessness and indecision, asked ‘whether one hundred pound was the highest that Mr.", "그렇게 안절부절못하고 망설이는 온갖 기색을 다 보이고 나서, \"백 파운드가 니클비 씨가 줄 수 있는 최고 액수냐\"고 물었지. 그렇다는 대답을 듣자 그는 다시 안절부절못했어."], ["Nickleby could go.’ Being answered in the affirmative, he became restless again, and, after some thought, and an unsuccessful inquiry ‘whether he couldn’t go another fifty,’ said he supposed he must try and do the most he could for a friend: which was always his maxim, and therefore he undertook the job.", "그러더니 한참 생각한 끝에, \"오십 파운드를 더 못 올려 주시겠냐\"고 헛물을 켜 보고는, 친구를 위해서라면 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해 봐야겠다고, 그게 늘 자기 좌우명이라며 그 일을 맡겠다고 했어."]], [["‘But how are you to get at the woman?’ he said; ‘that’s what it is as puzzles me.’", "\"근데 그 여자한테는 어떻게 다가갑니까?\" 그가 말했어. \"그게 바로 절 헷갈리게 하는 거란 말이죠.\""]], [["‘I may not get at her at all,’ replied Ralph, ‘but I’ll try.", "\"아예 못 다가갈 수도 있지.\" 랄프가 대답했어."], ["I have hunted people in this city, before now, who have been better hid than she; and I know quarters in which a guinea or two, carefully spent, will often solve darker riddles than this.", "\"하지만 해 보겠소. 나는 전에도 이 도시에서, 그 여자보다 더 잘 숨은 사람들을 추적해 본 적이 있소."], ["Ay, and keep them close too, if need be!", "그리고 기니 한두 닢을 잘만 쓰면 이보다 더 깜깜한 수수께끼도 곧잘 풀리는 동네들을 알지."], ["I hear my man ringing at the door.", "그래, 필요하면 입까지 단단히 다물게 해 두는 데도 말이오!"], ["We may as well part.", "내 하인이 문에서 종을 울리는군."], ["You had better not come to and fro, but wait till you hear from me.’", "이제 그만 헤어지는 게 낫겠소. 당신은 오락가락하지 말고, 내 연락이 갈 때까지 기다리는 게 좋겠소.\""]], [["‘Good!’ returned Squeers.", "\"좋습니다!\" 스퀴어스가 되받았어."], ["‘I say!", "\"참!"], ["If you shouldn’t find her out, you’ll pay expenses at the Saracen, and something for loss of time?’", "혹시 그 여자를 못 찾아내시면, 사라센에서 묵은 비용이랑, 시간 허비한 거 얼마쯤은 쳐 주시는 거죠?\""]], [["‘Well,’ said Ralph, testily; ‘yes!", "\"알았소.\" 랄프가 짜증스레 말했어."], ["You have nothing more to say?’", "\"그래! 더 할 말 없소?\""]], [["Squeers shaking his head, Ralph accompanied him to the streetdoor, and audibly wondering, for the edification of Newman, why it was fastened as if it were night, let him in and Squeers out, and returned to his own room.", "스퀴어스가 고개를 젓자, 랄프는 그를 데리고 현관문까지 갔어. 그러고는 뉴먼더러 들으라는 듯, 왜 한밤중도 아닌데 문이 잠겨 있는지 모르겠다고 큰 소리로 의아해하면서, 뉴먼은 들이고 스퀴어스는 내보낸 뒤 제 방으로 돌아갔어."]], [["‘Now!’ he muttered, ‘come what come may, for the present I am firm and unshaken. Let me but retrieve this one small portion of my loss and disgrace; let me but defeat him in this one hope, dear to his heart as I know it must be; let me but do this; and it shall be the first link in such a chain which I will wind about him, as never man forged yet.’", "\"자!\" 그가 중얼거렸어. \"이제 무슨 일이 닥치든, 당장은 나는 단단하고 흔들림이 없어. 내 손해와 치욕의 이 작은 한 조각만이라도 되찾을 수 있다면, 그놈한테 틀림없이 가슴에 소중할 게 분명한 이 한 가지 희망에서만이라도 그놈을 꺾을 수 있다면, 이것만 해낼 수 있다면, 이건 내가 그놈을 칭칭 옭아맬 사슬의 첫 고리가 될 거야. 아직 그 누구도 벼려 낸 적 없는 그런 사슬 말이지.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 57", "# CHAPTER 57"]], [["How Ralph Nickleby’s Auxiliary went about his Work, and how he prospered with it\nIt was a dark, wet, gloomy night in autumn, when in an upper room of a mean house situated in an obscure street, or rather court, near Lambeth, there sat, all alone, a one-eyed man grotesquely habited, either for lack of better garments or for purposes of disguise, in a loose greatcoat, with arms half as long again as his own, and a capacity of breadth and length which would have admitted of his winding himself in it, head and all, with the utmost ease, and without any risk of straining the old and greasy material of which it was composed.", "깊어 가는 가을밤이었어. 어둡고 비가 내리고 음울한 밤이었지. 램버스 근처의 외진 거리, 아니 골목이라고 해야 할 곳에 자리 잡은 허름한 집의 위층 방에, 외눈박이 사내 하나가 혼자 앉아 있었어. 차림새가 우스꽝스러웠지. 더 나은 옷이 없어서였는지, 아니면 변장을 하려고 그랬는지, 헐렁한 외투 하나를 걸치고 있었거든. 소매는 제 팔보다 절반은 더 길었고, 품과 길이도 어찌나 넉넉한지 머리까지 통째로 그 안에 둘둘 말고도 남을 정도였어. 그것도 아주 손쉽게, 오래되고 기름때 낀 그 천을 조금도 잡아당기지 않고도 말이야."]], [["So attired, and in a place so far removed from his usual haunts and occupations, and so very poor and wretched in its character, perhaps Mrs Squeers herself would have had some difficulty in recognising her lord: quickened though her natural sagacity doubtless would have been by the affectionate yearnings and impulses of a tender wife.", "이런 차림을 하고, 평소 드나들던 곳이나 하던 일과는 한참 동떨어진 데에, 그것도 이렇게 초라하고 비참한 곳에 있으니, 아마 스퀴어스 부인조차 자기 남편을 알아보기가 쉽지 않았을 거야. 다정한 아내의 애틋한 그리움과 본능이 타고난 눈썰미를 한껏 곤두세웠을 텐데도 말이지."], ["But Mrs.", "하지만 그는 분명 스퀴어스 부인의 남편이었어."], ["Squeers’s lord it was; and in a tolerably disconsolate mood Mrs.", "그리고 스퀴어스 부인의 남편은 꽤나 울적한 기분에 잠겨 있는 듯했지."], ["Squeers’s lord appeared to be, as, helping himself from a black bottle which stood on the table beside him, he cast round the chamber a look, in which very slight regard for the objects within view was plainly mingled with some regretful and impatient recollection of distant scenes and persons.", "옆 탁자에 놓인 검은 병에서 술을 따라 마시며, 방 안을 한 바퀴 둘러보는 눈길에는 눈앞의 것들에 대한 무관심이 또렷이 묻어났거든. 거기에 멀리 떨어진 풍경과 사람들을 떠올리는 아쉬움과 조바심도 뒤섞여 있었어."]], [["There were, certainly, no particular attractions, either in the room over which the glance of Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨의 눈길이 그렇게 못마땅하게 헤매던 방에도, 그가 창가로 다가가 볼 마음만 먹었다면 눈에 들어왔을 그 좁은 거리에도, 딱히 끌릴 만한 구석이라곤 하나도 없었어. 그가 앉아 있던 다락방은 휑하고 초라했지."], ["Squeers so discontentedly wandered, or in the narrow street into which it might have penetrated, if he had thought fit to approach the window.", "침대 틀이며 그 안에 놓인 몇 안 되는 살림살이는 죄다 가장 흔해 빠진 것들이었어. 다 부서져 가는 데다, 보기에도 영 정이 안 갔지."], ["The attic chamber in which he sat was bare and mean; the bedstead, and such few other articles of necessary furniture as it contained, were of the commonest description, in a most crazy state, and of a most uninviting appearance.", "거리는 진창에 더럽고 인적도 끊겨 있었어. 빠져나가는 길이 하나뿐이라 어느 때든 거기 사는 사람 말고는 지나는 이가 거의 없었지."], ["The street was muddy, dirty, and deserted.", "게다가 그날 밤은 누구나 집 안에 들어앉아 있고 싶어 하는 그런 밤이었어."], ["Having but one outlet, it was traversed by few but the inhabitants at any time; and the night being one of those on which most people are glad to be within doors, it now presented no other signs of life than the dull glimmering of poor candles from the dirty windows, and few sounds but the pattering of the rain, and occasionally the heavy closing of some creaking door.", "그래서 지금은 살아 있는 기척이라곤, 더러운 창문 너머로 새어 나오는 보잘것없는 촛불의 희미한 빛뿐이었지. 소리라곤 후두두 떨어지는 빗소리와, 가끔 삐걱대는 문이 쿵 하고 무겁게 닫히는 소리 정도였어."]], [["Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨는 계속 울적하게 주위를 둘러보았어."], ["Squeers continued to look disconsolately about him, and to listen to these noises in profound silence, broken only by the rustling of his large coat, as he now and then moved his arm to raise his glass to his lips.", "그러고는 이 소리들에 깊은 침묵 속에서 귀를 기울였지. 그 침묵을 깨는 거라곤, 잔을 입으로 가져가려고 이따금 팔을 움직일 때 그 커다란 외투가 바스락거리는 소리뿐이었어."], ["Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨는 한동안 그러고 있었어."], ["Squeers continued to do this for some time, until the increasing gloom warned him to snuff the candle.", "그러다 점점 어둠이 짙어지자 촛불 심지를 잘라야겠다는 생각이 들었지."], ["Seeming to be slightly roused by this exertion, he raised his eye to the ceiling, and fixing it upon some uncouth and fantastic figures, traced upon it by the wet and damp which had penetrated through the roof, broke into the following soliloquy:", "이 일로 좀 정신이 든 듯, 그는 눈을 천장으로 들어 올렸어. 그러고는 지붕으로 스며든 비와 습기 때문에 천장에 그려진, 기괴하고 묘한 무늬에 눈길을 고정한 채 이렇게 혼잣말을 늘어놓기 시작했어."]], [["‘Well, this is a pretty go, is this here!", "\"흠, 이거 참 꼴좋게 됐군, 이게 다 뭐람! 정말이지 꼴좋아!"], ["An uncommon pretty go!", "내가 여기서 도대체 몇 주를 보낸 거야."], ["Here have I been, a matter of how many weeks--hard upon six--a follering up this here blessed old dowager petty larcenerer,’--Mr.", "거진 여섯 주는 됐지. 이 빌어먹을 늙은 좀도둑 마나님 뒤꽁무니나 쫓아다니면서 말이야.\""], ["Squeers delivered himself of this epithet with great difficulty and effort,--‘and Dotheboys Hall a-running itself regularly to seed the while!", "스퀴어스 씨는 이 호칭을 입에 올리느라 무척 애를 먹고 힘들어했어. \"그러는 동안 두더보이즈 홀은 알아서 척척 엉망이 돼 가고 있겠지!"], ["That’s the worst of ever being in with a owdacious chap like that old Nickleby.", "그 늙은 니클비 같은 뻔뻔스러운 작자랑 엮이면 이게 제일 골치 아픈 점이라니까."], ["You never know when he’s done with you, and if you’re in for a penny, you’re in for a pound.’", "그자가 언제 너랑 손을 끊을지 도무지 알 수가 없거든. 그리고 한 푼이라도 발을 담갔다 하면, 결국 일 파운드까지 끌려 들어가는 거야.\""]], [["This remark, perhaps, reminded Mr. Squeers that he was in for a hundred pound at any rate.", "이 말을 하고 나니 스퀴어스 씨는 어쨌든 자기가 백 파운드에 발을 담그고 있다는 게 떠올랐는지도 몰라."], ["His countenance relaxed, and he raised his glass to his mouth with an air of greater enjoyment of its contents than he had before evinced.", "표정이 풀어졌어. 그러고는 아까보다 훨씬 흐뭇하게 술맛을 음미하는 기색으로 잔을 입으로 가져갔지."]], [["‘I never see,’ soliloquised Mr.", "\"내 평생,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 혼잣말을 이어 갔어."], ["Squeers in continuation, ‘I never see nor come across such a file as that old Nickleby.", "\"저 늙은 니클비 같은 능구렁이는 본 적도, 마주친 적도 없어. 단 한 번도!"], ["Never! He’s out of everybody’s depth, he is.", "그자는 누구도 속을 헤아릴 수가 없다니까."], ["He’s what you may call a rasper, is Nickleby.", "그자는, 뭐랄까, 진짜 독한 놈이야, 니클비 말이야."], ["To see how sly and cunning he grubbed on, day after day, a-worming and plodding and tracing and turning and twining of hisself about, till he found out where this precious Mrs.", "어찌나 교활하고 음흉하게 파고드는지, 날이면 날마다 벌레처럼 기어들고 끈질기게 캐고 더듬고 돌고 휘감으면서, 마침내 이 귀하신 페그 마나님이 어디 숨었는지 알아내고는, 내가 일할 자리를 깔끔하게 닦아 놓잖아."], ["Peg was hid, and cleared the ground for me to work upon.", "살금살금 기고 미끄러지듯 다니는 게, 꼭 추하고 늙은 데다 눈만 번뜩이고 피가 썩어 가는 독사 같다니까! 아!"], ["Creeping and crawling and gliding, like a ugly, old, bright-eyed, stagnation-blooded adder! Ah!", "우리 쪽 일에도 잘 어울리는 인물이었을 텐데, 그자한테는 너무 좁았을 거야."], ["He’d have made a good ‘un in our line, but it would have been too limited for him; his genius would have busted all bonds, and coming over every obstacle, broke down all before it, till it erected itself into a monneyment of--Well, I’ll think of the rest, and say it when conwenient.’", "그자의 재주는 어떤 굴레도 다 터뜨려 버리고, 어떤 장애물도 다 넘고, 앞을 가로막는 건 죄다 부숴 버려서, 마침내 스스로 우뚝 솟아올라 하나의 기념비가 되었을걸. 그 무슨 기념비냐면, 음, 나머지는 나중에 생각해 두었다가 적당한 때 말해야지.\""]], [["Making a halt in his reflections at this place, Mr.", "여기서 잠시 생각을 멈춘 스퀴어스 씨는 다시 잔을 입으로 가져갔어. 그러고는 주머니에서 꼬깃꼬깃한 편지 한 통을 꺼내, 그 내용을 찬찬히 훑어보기 시작했지."], ["Squeers again put his glass to his lips, and drawing a dirty letter from his pocket, proceeded to con over its contents with the air of a man who had read it very often, and now refreshed his memory rather in the absence of better amusement than for any specific information.", "여러 번 읽은 게 분명한 사람의 태도로 말이야. 무슨 새로운 정보를 얻으려는 게 아니라, 딱히 할 만한 다른 재미가 없어서 기억이나 되살리려는 듯한 모습이었어."]], [["‘The pigs is well,’ said Mr.", "\"돼지들은 잘 있고,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 읽었어."], ["Squeers, ‘the cows is well, and the boys is bobbish.", "\"소들도 잘 있고, 아이들도 멀쩡하다. 어린 스프라우터 녀석이 눈을 찡긋거렸다고?"], ["Young Sprouter has been a-winking, has he?", "돌아가면 내가 그 눈을 찡긋하게 해 주지. '코비는 밥을 먹으면서 한사코 훌쩍거렸는데, 쇠고기가 너무 독해서 그렇다고 했다.'"], ["I’ll wink him when I get back.", "좋아, 코비, 어디 쇠고기 없이도 네가 좀 훌쩍거리게 못 만드나 두고 보자. '피처가 또 열병에 걸렸고,'"], ["“Cobbey would persist in sniffing while he was a-eating his dinner, and said that the beef was so strong it made him.”--Very good, Cobbey, we’ll see if we can’t make you sniff a little without beef.", "당연히 그랬겠지. '친구들이 데리러 와서, 집에 도착한 다음 날 죽었다.'"], ["“Pitcher was took with another fever,”--of course he was--“and being fetched by his friends, died the day after he got home,”--of course he did, and out of aggravation; it’s part of a deep-laid system.", "당연히 그랬겠지, 약 올리려고 그런 거야. 이건 다 깊이 짜인 수작의 일부라니까."], ["There an’t another chap in the school but that boy as would have died exactly at the end of the quarter: taking it out of me to the very last, and then carrying his spite to the utmost extremity.", "학교에 그 녀석 말고 분기 끝나는 딱 그날에 죽을 놈은 또 없거든. 끝까지 나한테서 본전을 뽑아내고는, 제 심술을 끝장까지 부린 거지."], ["“The juniorest Palmer said he wished he was in Heaven.”", "'막내 팔머는 자기가 천국에 있었으면 좋겠다고 했다.'"], ["I really don’t know, I do NOT know what’s to be done with that young fellow; he’s always a-wishing something horrid.", "나는 정말이지, 정말 그 녀석을 어찌해야 좋을지 모르겠어. 늘 끔찍한 소원만 빈다니까."], ["He said once, he wished he was a donkey, because then he wouldn’t have a father as didn’t love him!", "한번은 자기가 당나귀였으면 좋겠다고 했어. 그럼 자기를 사랑하지 않는 아버지가 없을 테니까!"], ["Pretty wicious that for a child of six!’", "여섯 살짜리 애가 참 고약하기도 하지!\""]], [["Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨는 이렇게 어린 녀석이 이토록 모진 마음을 품고 있다는 생각에 어찌나 심란해졌던지, 화가 나서 편지를 도로 집어넣었어."], ["Squeers was so much moved by the contemplation of this hardened nature in one so young, that he angrily put up the letter, and sought, in a new train of ideas, a subject of consolation.", "그러고는 새로운 생각 줄기를 따라 위안거리를 찾으려 했지."]], [["‘It’s a long time to have been a-lingering in London,’ he said; ‘and this is a precious hole to come and live in, even if it has been only for a week or so.", "\"런던에서 이렇게 오래 죽치고 있은 것도 참 길었어.\" 그가 말했어."], ["Still, one hundred pound is five boys, and five boys takes a whole year to pay one hundred pounds, and there’s their keep to be substracted, besides.", "\"게다가 여긴 와서 살기에 정말 끔찍한 소굴이라니까. 고작 한 주쯤이라 해도 말이야."], ["There’s nothing lost, neither, by one’s being here; because the boys’ money comes in just the same as if I was at home, and Mrs.", "그래도 백 파운드면 애 다섯이야. 애 다섯이 백 파운드를 채우려면 꼬박 일 년이 걸리지."], ["Squeers she keeps them in order.", "거기다 그 녀석들 먹이고 재우는 비용까지 빼야 하고. 게다가 내가 여기 있어도 손해 볼 건 없어."], ["There’ll be some lost time to make up, of course.", "애들 돈은 내가 집에 있으나 없으나 똑같이 들어오고, 스퀴어스 부인이 알아서 애들을 잡고 있을 테니까. 물론 못 챙긴 시간은 메워야겠지."], ["There’ll be an arrear of flogging as’ll have to be gone through: still, a couple of days makes that all right, and one don’t mind a little extra work for one hundred pound.", "밀린 매질도 처리해야 할 거고. 그래도 며칠이면 다 바로잡을 수 있어."], ["It’s pretty nigh the time to wait upon the old woman.", "백 파운드를 위해서라면 좀 더 일하는 거야 별것 아니지. 이제 슬슬 그 노파를 보러 갈 때가 됐군."], ["From what she said last night, I suspect that if I’m to succeed at all, I shall succeed tonight; so I’ll have half a glass more, to wish myself success, and put myself in spirits.", "어젯밤 그 여자가 한 말로 보아, 내가 성공할 거라면 오늘 밤에 할 것 같아. 그러니 반 잔만 더 마시면서 성공을 빌고, 기운을 좀 차려야지."], ["Mrs. Squeers, my dear, your health!’", "스퀴어스 부인, 여보, 당신의 건강을 위하여!\""]], [["Leering with his one eye as if the lady to whom he drank had been actually present, Mr.", "마치 잔을 권하는 부인이 정말 거기 있기라도 한 듯, 스퀴어스 씨는 외눈으로 곁눈질을 했어."], ["Squeers--in his enthusiasm, no doubt--poured out a full glass, and emptied it; and as the liquor was raw spirits, and he had applied himself to the same bottle more than once already, it is not surprising that he found himself, by this time, in an extremely cheerful state, and quite enough excited for his purpose.", "그러고는 분명 그 흥에 겨워, 잔을 가득 채우더니 쭉 비워 버렸지. 그 술이 독한 독주인 데다 이미 같은 병에 한 번 이상 손을 댔으니, 이쯤 되어 그가 무척 들뜬 상태가 된 것도, 제 목적을 이룰 만큼 충분히 흥분한 것도 놀랄 일은 아니었어."]], [["What this purpose was soon appeared; for, after a few turns about the room to steady himself, he took the bottle under his arm and the glass in his hand, and blowing out the candle as if he purposed being gone some time, stole out upon the staircase, and creeping softly to a door opposite his own, tapped gently at it.", "그 목적이 무엇인지는 곧 드러났어. 그는 몸을 가누려고 방 안을 몇 바퀴 돈 다음, 병을 겨드랑이에 끼고 잔을 손에 들었지. 그러고는 한참 자리를 비울 작정인 듯 촛불을 불어 끄고, 계단으로 살그머니 나갔어. 자기 방 맞은편 문으로 가만가만 다가가서는, 가볍게 문을 두드렸지."]], [["‘But what’s the use of tapping?’ he said, ‘She’ll never hear. I suppose she isn’t doing anything very particular; and if she is, it don’t much matter, that I see.’", "\"근데 두드려 봐야 무슨 소용이람?\" 그가 말했어. \"어차피 못 들을 텐데. 뭐 별로 대단한 일을 하고 있진 않겠지. 설령 그렇대도, 내가 보기엔 별 상관 없고.\""]], [["With this brief preface, Mr. Squeers applied his hand to the latch of the door, and thrusting his head into a garret far more deplorable than that he had just left, and seeing that there was nobody there but an old woman, who was bending over a wretched fire (for although the weather was still warm, the evening was chilly), walked in, and tapped her on the shoulder.", "이렇게 간단히 운을 떼고 나서, 스퀴어스 씨는 문고리에 손을 댔어. 그러고는 방금 나온 곳보다 훨씬 더 처참한 다락방 안으로 머리를 들이밀었지. 거기엔 노파 하나밖에 없었어. 그 여자는 초라한 불가에 몸을 숙이고 있었지. 날씨는 아직 따뜻했지만 저녁은 쌀쌀했거든. 그는 안으로 들어가 노파의 어깨를 톡톡 두드렸어."]], [["‘Well, my Slider,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 내 슬라이더 양반.\""], ["Squeers, jocularly.", "스퀴어스 씨가 농담조로 말했어."]], [["‘Is that you?’ inquired Peg.", "\"당신이오?\" 페그가 물었어."]], [["‘Ah! it’s me, and me’s the first person singular, nominative case, agreeing with the verb “it’s”, and governed by Squeers understood, as a acorn, a hour; but when the h is sounded, the a only is to be used, as a and, a art, a ighway,’ replied Mr. Squeers, quoting at random from the grammar. ‘At least, if it isn’t, you don’t know any better, and if it is, I’ve done it accidentally.’", "\"아! 나요. 그리고 '나'는 일인칭 단수 주격이지. 동사 '~이오'와 호응하고, 생략된 스퀴어스의 지배를 받는단 말이야. 도토리 앞엔 '하나의', 한 시간 앞엔 '하나의'. 다만 h 소리가 날 때는 a만 쓰는 거지. '그리고' 앞엔 a, '기술' 앞엔 a, '큰길' 앞엔 a처럼.\" 스퀴어스 씨가 문법책에서 아무렇게나 끌어다 대답했어. \"뭐, 설령 틀렸어도 당신은 더 잘 알지도 못할 테고, 맞았다면 그건 어쩌다 그렇게 된 거고.\""]], [["Delivering this reply in his accustomed tone of voice, in which of course it was inaudible to Peg, Mr.", "이 대답을 늘 하던 목소리 크기로 했으니, 페그한테 들릴 리가 없었지. 스퀴어스 씨는 걸상 하나를 불가로 끌어다, 노파를 마주 보고 앉았어."], ["Squeers drew a stool to the fire, and placing himself over against her, and the bottle and glass on the floor between them, roared out again, very loud,", "병과 잔은 둘 사이 바닥에 내려놓았지. 그러고는 다시 아주 큰 소리로 외쳤어."]], [["‘Well, my Slider!’", "\"자, 내 슬라이더 양반!\""]], [["‘I hear you,’ said Peg, receiving him very graciously.", "\"들려요.\" 페그가 그를 아주 정중하게 맞으며 말했어."]], [["‘I’ve come according to promise,’ roared Squeers.", "\"약속대로 왔수다.\" 스퀴어스가 외쳤어."]], [["‘So they used to say in that part of the country I come from,’ observed Peg, complacently, ‘but I think oil’s better.’", "\"내가 살던 시골에선 그렇게들 말하곤 했지.\" 페그가 흐뭇하게 한마디 했어. \"그래도 난 기름이 더 낫다고 봐요.\""]], [["‘Better than what?’ roared Squeers, adding some rather strong language in an undertone.", "\"뭐보다 낫다고?\" 스퀴어스가 외쳤어. 그러고는 나직이 꽤 험한 말을 한마디 덧붙였지."]], [["‘No,’ said Peg, ‘of course not.’", "\"아니,\" 페그가 말했어. \"당연히 아니지.\""]], [["‘I never saw such a monster as you are!’ muttered Squeers, looking as amiable as he possibly could the while; for Peg’s eye was upon him, and she was chuckling fearfully, as though in delight at having made a choice repartee, ‘Do you see this? This is a bottle.’", "\"내 평생 당신 같은 괴물은 처음 봐!\" 스퀴어스가 중얼거렸어. 그러면서도 한껏 상냥한 표정을 지으려 애썼지. 페그의 눈이 그를 향하고 있었고, 그 여자가 무슨 그럴듯한 재담이라도 던진 게 신나는지 무섭게 킬킬거리고 있었거든. \"이게 보여? 이건 병이야.\""]], [["‘I see it,’ answered Peg.", "\"보여요.\" 페그가 대답했어."]], [["‘Well, and do you see THIS?’ bawled Squeers.", "\"좋아, 그럼 이건 보여?\" 스퀴어스가 고래고래 소리쳤어."], ["‘This is a glass.’ Peg saw that too.", "\"이건 잔이야.\" 페그는 그것도 보였어."]], [["‘See here, then,’ said Squeers, accompanying his remarks with appropriate action, ‘I fill the glass from the bottle, and I say “Your health, Slider,” and empty it; then I rinse it genteelly with a little drop, which I’m forced to throw into the fire--hallo! we shall have the chimbley alight next--fill it again, and hand it over to you.’", "\"그럼 잘 봐.\" 스퀴어스가 말에 맞춰 적절히 행동까지 보태며 말했어. \"내가 병에서 잔에 따라. 그러고는 '당신의 건강을 위하여, 슬라이더' 하고 쭉 비우지. 그다음 점잖게 한 방울로 헹궈서, 어쩔 수 없이 불에 휙 던지는 거야. 어이쿠! 이러다 다음엔 굴뚝에 불이 붙겠네. 다시 채워서, 당신한테 건네주는 거야.\""]], [["‘YOUR health,’ said Peg.", "\"당신의 건강을 위하여.\" 페그가 말했어."]], [["‘She understands that, anyways,’ muttered Squeers, watching Mrs Sliderskew as she dispatched her portion, and choked and gasped in a most awful manner after so doing.", "\"이건 그래도 알아듣는군.\" 스퀴어스가 중얼거렸어."], ["‘Now then, let’s have a talk.", "슬라이더스큐 부인이 제 몫을 단숨에 들이켜고는, 그러고 나서 끔찍하게 캑캑거리고 숨을 헐떡이는 걸 지켜보면서 말이야. \"자 그럼, 얘기 좀 해 봅시다."], ["How’s the rheumatics?’", "그 류머티즘은 어때?\""]], [["Mrs. Sliderskew, with much blinking and chuckling, and with looks expressive of her strong admiration of Mr.", "슬라이더스큐 부인은 연신 눈을 깜빡이고 킬킬거리면서, 스퀴어스 씨와 그의 사람됨, 태도, 말솜씨에 푹 빠진 듯한 표정을 지었어."], ["Squeers, his person, manners, and conversation, replied that the rheumatics were better.", "그러고는 류머티즘이 좀 나아졌다고 대답했지."]], [["‘What’s the reason,’ said Mr.", "\"그 이유가 뭘까,\""], ["Squeers, deriving fresh facetiousness from the bottle; ‘what’s the reason of rheumatics?", "스퀴어스 씨가 병에서 새로운 익살을 끌어내며 말했어. \"류머티즘이 생기는 이유가 뭐냐고?"], ["What do they mean?", "그게 무슨 뜻이지?"], ["What do people have’em for--eh?’", "사람들은 왜 그런 걸 앓는 거야, 응?\""]], [["Mrs.", "슬라이더스큐 부인은 모른다고 했어."], ["Sliderskew didn’t know, but suggested that it was possibly because they couldn’t help it.", "그래도 어쩔 도리가 없어서 그런 게 아닐까 하고 짐작은 했지."]], [["‘Measles, rheumatics, hooping-cough, fevers, agers, and lumbagers,’ said Mr.", "\"홍역, 류머티즘, 백일해, 열병, 학질, 그리고 요통,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어."], ["Squeers, ‘is all philosophy together; that’s what it is.", "\"이게 다 한통속으로 철학이라니까. 바로 그거야."], ["The heavenly bodies is philosophy, and the earthly bodies is philosophy.", "천체도 철학이고, 지상의 몸뚱이도 철학이지. 천체에 나사가 하나 헐거워지면, 그게 철학이야."], ["If there’s a screw loose in a heavenly body, that’s philosophy; and if there’s screw loose in a earthly body, that’s philosophy too; or it may be that sometimes there’s a little metaphysics in it, but that’s not often.", "그리고 지상의 몸뚱이에 나사가 하나 헐거워지면, 그것도 철학이고. 아니면 가끔 거기 형이상학이 조금 섞여 있을 수도 있지만, 그건 자주 있는 일은 아니지."], ["Philosophy’s the chap for me.", "철학이야말로 나한테 딱 맞는 녀석이야. 부모가 고전이니 상업이니 수학 쪽으로 뭘 물어보잖아?"], ["If a parent asks a question in the classical, commercial, or mathematical line, says I, gravely, “Why, sir, in the first place, are you a philosopher?”--“No, Mr.", "그럼 내가 엄숙하게 이러는 거지. '저, 선생님, 우선 선생께서는 철학자이십니까?'"], ["Squeers,”", "그럼 그 사람이 이러지."], ["he says, “I an’t.”", "'아닙니다, 스퀴어스 씨."], ["“Then, sir,”", "저는 철학자가 아닙니다.'"], ["says I, “I am sorry for you, for I shan’t be able to explain it.”", "그럼 내가 '그렇다면, 선생님, 안타깝군요. 제가 그걸 설명해 드릴 수가 없겠습니다'"], ["Naturally, the parent goes away and wishes he was a philosopher, and, equally naturally, thinks I’m one.’", "하는 거야. 그러면 자연히 그 부모는 돌아가면서 자기도 철학자였으면 하고 바라게 되고, 또 그만큼 자연히 나를 철학자로 여기게 되지.\""]], [["Saying this, and a great deal more, with tipsy profundity and a serio-comic air, and keeping his eye all the time on Mrs.", "이 말을, 그리고 그보다 훨씬 많은 말을, 술기운 어린 심오함과 진지한 듯 우스꽝스러운 태도로 늘어놓으며, 스퀴어스 씨는 내내 슬라이더스큐 부인한테서 눈을 떼지 않았어."], ["Sliderskew, who was unable to hear one word, Mr.", "한마디도 못 알아듣는 그 여자한테 말이지."], ["Squeers concluded by helping himself and passing the bottle: to which Peg did becoming reverence.", "그러고는 자기 잔을 채우고 병을 건네며 말을 맺었어. 페그는 거기에 합당한 경의를 표했지."]], [["‘That’s the time of day!’ said Mr.", "\"바로 이 맛이야!\" 스퀴어스 씨가 말했어."], ["Squeers. ‘You look twenty pound ten better than you did.’", "\"당신, 아까보다 이십 파운드 십 실링어치는 더 좋아 보여.\""]], [["Again Mrs.", "슬라이더스큐 부인은 또 킬킬거렸어."], ["Sliderskew chuckled, but modesty forbade her assenting verbally to the compliment.", "하지만 차마 그 칭찬에 말로 맞장구를 칠 수는 없는 듯, 겸손을 떨었지."]], [["‘Twenty pound ten better,’ repeated Mr.", "\"이십 파운드 십 실링어치는 더 좋아 보인다고.\""], ["Squeers, ‘than you did that day when I first introduced myself.", "스퀴어스 씨가 거듭 말했어. \"내가 처음 인사하러 왔던 그날보다 말이야."], ["Don’t you know?’", "모르겠어?\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said Peg, shaking her head, ‘but you frightened me that day.’", "\"아!\" 페그가 고개를 절레절레 저으며 말했어. \"그날 당신이 날 어찌나 놀라게 했는지.\""]], [["‘Did I?’ said Squeers; ‘well, it was rather a startling thing for a stranger to come and recommend himself by saying that he knew all about you, and what your name was, and why you were living so quiet here, and what you had boned, and who you boned it from, wasn’t it?’", "\"내가?\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"뭐, 좀 깜짝 놀랄 만하긴 했지. 낯선 사람이 와서는 다짜고짜 당신에 대해 다 안다고 하면서, 당신 이름이 뭔지, 왜 여기서 이렇게 조용히 지내는지, 뭘 슬쩍했는지, 또 누구한테서 슬쩍했는지까지 줄줄이 읊으며 인사를 했으니, 안 그래?\""]], [["Peg nodded her head in strong assent.", "페그는 그렇다는 듯 고개를 힘껏 끄덕였어."]], [["‘But I know everything that happens in that way, you see,’ continued Squeers. ‘Nothing takes place, of that kind, that I an’t up to entirely. I’m a sort of a lawyer, Slider, of first-rate standing, and understanding too; I’m the intimate friend and confidential adwiser of pretty nigh every man, woman, and child that gets themselves into difficulties by being too nimble with their fingers, I’m--’", "\"하지만 난 그런 식으로 벌어지는 일은 죄다 안단 말이지.\" 스퀴어스가 말을 이었어. \"그런 부류의 일은 내가 훤히 꿰지 못하는 게 하나도 없어. 난 말하자면 변호사 같은 사람이야, 슬라이더. 그것도 일류로, 그것도 머리까지 잘 돌아가는. 손가락이 너무 날래서 곤경에 빠진 거의 모든 남자, 여자, 아이의 둘도 없는 친구이자 은밀한 조언자가 바로 나라고. 나는 말이야……\""]], [["Mr. Squeers’s catalogue of his own merits and accomplishments, which was partly the result of a concerted plan between himself and Ralph Nickleby, and flowed, in part, from the black bottle, was here interrupted by Mrs.", "스퀴어스 씨가 늘어놓던 제 잘난 점과 재능의 목록은, 일부는 자신과 랄프 니클비 사이에 미리 짜 둔 계획에서 나온 것이고, 일부는 그 검은 병에서 흘러나온 것이었지."], ["Sliderskew.", "그런데 여기서 슬라이더스큐 부인이 끼어드는 바람에 끊기고 말았어."]], [["‘Ha, ha, ha!’ she cried, folding her arms and wagging her head; ‘and so he wasn’t married after all, wasn’t he.", "\"하, 하, 하!\" 그 여자가 팔짱을 끼고 고개를 까딱거리며 소리쳤어."], ["Not married after all?’", "\"그래서 그 작자가 결국 결혼을 못 했다 이거지, 응? 결국 장가를 못 갔다고?\""]], [["‘No,’ replied Squeers, ‘that he wasn’t!’", "\"그래.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"못 갔지!\""]], [["‘And a young lover come and carried off the bride, eh?’ said Peg.", "\"그리고 젊은 정인이 와서 신부를 채 갔다, 응?\" 페그가 말했어."]], [["‘From under his very nose,’ replied Squeers; ‘and I’m told the young chap cut up rough besides, and broke the winders, and forced him to swaller his wedding favour which nearly choked him.’", "\"그자 코앞에서 말이야.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"게다가 그 젊은 녀석이 험하게 굴기까지 했다더군. 창문을 다 깨부수고, 결혼식 꽃 장식을 억지로 삼키게 해서 그자가 하마터면 목이 막힐 뻔했다지.\""]], [["‘Tell me all about it again,’ cried Peg, with a malicious relish of her old master’s defeat, which made her natural hideousness something quite fearful; ‘let’s hear it all again, beginning at the beginning now, as if you’d never told me. Let’s have it every word--now--now--beginning at the very first, you know, when he went to the house that morning!’", "\"그 얘길 처음부터 다시 다 해 봐요.\" 페그가 소리쳤어. 옛 주인이 당한 낭패를 고소해하는 그 심술궂은 표정에, 타고난 흉한 얼굴이 정말이지 무시무시해 보였지. \"다시 한번 다 들어 봅시다. 이번엔 처음부터, 나한테 한 번도 얘기 안 한 것처럼 시작해 봐요. 한 마디 한 마디 다 들려줘요. 자, 자, 맨 처음부터 말이야. 그자가 그날 아침 그 집에 갔을 때부터!\""]], [["Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨는 슬라이더스큐 부인한테 술을 아낌없이 따라 주었어."], ["Squeers, plying Mrs.", "그리고 그렇게 큰 소리로 떠드는 수고를 견디려고 자기도 자주 술병에 손을 댔지. 그러고는 그 청을 들어주어, 아서 그라이드가 당한 망신을 묘사해 주었어."], ["Sliderskew freely with the liquor, and sustaining himself under the exertion of speaking so loud by frequent applications to it himself, complied with this request by describing the discomfiture of Arthur Gride, with such improvements on the truth as happened to occur to him, and the ingenious invention and application of which had been very instrumental in recommending him to her notice in the beginning of their acquaintance.", "그때그때 떠오르는 대로 진실에 적당히 살을 붙여 가면서 말이야. 사실 이렇게 그럴듯한 이야기를 지어내고 갖다 붙이는 솜씨가, 두 사람이 처음 알게 됐을 무렵 그가 노파의 환심을 사는 데 큰 몫을 했거든."], ["Mrs.", "슬라이더스큐 부인은 기쁨에 겨워 어쩔 줄을 몰랐어."], ["Sliderskew was in an ecstasy of delight, rolling her head about, drawing up her skinny shoulders, and wrinkling her cadaverous face into so many and such complicated forms of ugliness, as awakened the unbounded astonishment and disgust even of Mr Squeers.", "고개를 이리저리 굴리고, 앙상한 어깨를 추켜올리고, 시체 같은 얼굴을 온갖 복잡하고 흉한 모양으로 일그러뜨렸지. 그 꼴에 스퀴어스 씨조차 끝없는 놀라움과 역겨움이 일 정도였어."]], [["‘He’s a treacherous old goat,’ said Peg, ‘and cozened me with cunning tricks and lying promises, but never mind.", "\"그 작자는 음흉한 늙은 염소야.\" 페그가 말했어."], ["I’m even with him.", "\"교활한 수작이랑 거짓 약속으로 날 속여먹었지. 하지만 상관없어."], ["I’m even with him.’", "난 그자랑 비긴 셈이니까. 비긴 셈이지.\""]], [["‘More than even, Slider,’ returned Squeers; ‘you’d have been even with him if he’d got married; but with the disappointment besides, you’re a long way ahead. Out of sight, Slider, quite out of sight. And that reminds me,’ he added, handing her the glass, ‘if you want me to give you my opinion of them deeds, and tell you what you’d better keep and what you’d better burn, why, now’s your time, Slider.’", "\"비긴 것보다 더하지, 슬라이더.\" 스퀴어스가 받았어. \"그자가 결혼을 했다면 비긴 셈이었겠지만, 거기다 낭패까지 봤으니 당신이 한참 앞선 거야. 비교도 안 돼, 슬라이더, 아주 비교도 안 되게 앞섰지. 아, 그러고 보니 생각났는데,\" 그가 노파에게 잔을 건네며 덧붙였어. \"그 증서들에 대해 내 의견을 듣고 싶으면, 어떤 걸 챙기고 어떤 걸 태우는 게 좋을지 알고 싶으면, 지금이 딱 좋은 때야, 슬라이더.\""]], [["‘There an’t no hurry for that,’ said Peg, with several knowing looks and winks.", "\"그건 서두를 거 없어요.\" 페그가 뭔가 안다는 듯 여러 번 눈짓을 하고 윙크를 하며 말했어."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["very well!’ observed Squeers, ‘it don’t matter to me; you asked me, you know.", "좋을 대로!\" 스퀴어스가 한마디 했어."], ["I shouldn’t charge you nothing, being a friend.", "\"나야 상관없지. 당신이 부탁한 거잖아."], ["You’re the best judge of course.", "친구니까 한 푼도 안 받을 생각이었고."], ["But you’re a bold woman, Slider.’", "물론 당신이 제일 잘 판단하겠지. 그래도 당신, 참 대담한 여자야, 슬라이더.\""]], [["‘How do you mean, bold?’ said Peg.", "\"대담하다니, 그게 무슨 소리요?\" 페그가 말했어."]], [["‘Why, I only mean that if it was me, I wouldn’t keep papers as might hang me, littering about when they might be turned into money--them as wasn’t useful made away with, and them as was, laid by somewheres, safe; that’s all,’ returned Squeers; ‘but everybody’s the best judge of their own affairs. All I say is, Slider, I wouldn’t do it.’", "\"뭐, 내 말은 그냥, 나 같으면 자칫 내 목을 매달 수도 있는 서류를 여기저기 늘어놓진 않겠다는 거야. 돈으로 바꿀 수 있을 때 말이지. 쓸모없는 건 없애 버리고, 쓸모 있는 건 어디 안전한 데 잘 챙겨 두고. 그게 다야.\" 스퀴어스가 받았어. \"하지만 자기 일은 누구나 자기가 제일 잘 판단하는 법이지. 내가 하고 싶은 말은 그저, 슬라이더, 나라면 그렇게 안 둔다는 거야.\""]], [["‘Come,’ said Peg, ‘then you shall see ‘em.’", "\"이리 와요.\" 페그가 말했어. \"그럼 보여 줄게.\""]], [["‘I don’t want to see ‘em,’ replied Squeers, affecting to be out of humour; ‘don’t talk as if it was a treat.", "\"보고 싶지 않은데.\" 스퀴어스가 시큰둥한 척하며 대답했어."], ["Show ‘em to somebody else, and take their advice.’", "\"무슨 대단한 구경거리라도 되는 양 말하지 말라고. 다른 사람한테 보여 주고 그 사람 조언이나 들으시지.\""]], [["Mr. Squeers would, very likely, have carried on the farce of being offended a little longer, if Mrs.", "스퀴어스 씨는 아마 좀 더 오래 삐친 척 연극을 이어 갔을 거야."], ["Sliderskew, in her anxiety to restore herself to her former high position in his good graces, had not become so extremely affectionate that he stood at some risk of being smothered by her caresses.", "만약 슬라이더스큐 부인이 그의 호감 속에서 예전의 그 높은 자리를 되찾으려는 마음에 어찌나 애살스럽게 굴었던지, 그 애무에 숨이 막힐 위험에 처하지 않았더라면 말이지."], ["Repressing, with as good a grace as possible, these little familiarities--for which, there is reason to believe, the black bottle was at least as much to blame as any constitutional infirmity on the part of Mrs.", "그는 되도록 점잖게 이 자질구레한 친밀함을 막아 냈어. 사실 그 친밀함은 슬라이더스큐 부인의 타고난 약점 못지않게, 적어도 그 검은 병 탓도 컸다고 볼 만했지."], ["Sliderskew--he protested that he had only been joking: and, in proof of his unimpaired good-humour, that he was ready to examine the deeds at once, if, by so doing, he could afford any satisfaction or relief of mind to his fair friend.", "그는 그저 농담이었을 뿐이라고 둘러댔어. 그리고 자기 기분이 조금도 상하지 않았다는 증거로, 그렇게 해서 이 어여쁜 친구의 마음에 조금이나마 흡족함이나 위안을 줄 수 있다면 당장이라도 증서들을 살펴보겠다고 했지."]], [["‘And now you’re up, my Slider,’ bawled Squeers, as she rose to fetch them, ‘bolt the door.’", "\"그래, 일어선 김에, 내 슬라이더 양반,\" 노파가 증서를 가지러 일어서자 스퀴어스가 고래고래 소리쳤어. \"문에 빗장을 걸어.\""]], [["Peg trotted to the door, and after fumbling at the bolt, crept to the other end of the room, and from beneath the coals which filled the bottom of the cupboard, drew forth a small deal box.", "페그는 종종걸음으로 문으로 가서, 빗장을 더듬더듬 만지작거렸어. 그러고는 방 반대편 끝으로 살금살금 가더니, 찬장 바닥을 가득 메운 석탄 밑에서 작은 전나무 상자 하나를 꺼냈어."], ["Having placed this on the floor at Squeers’s feet, she brought, from under the pillow of her bed, a small key, with which she signed to that gentleman to open it.", "그걸 스퀴어스의 발치 바닥에 내려놓고는, 침대 베개 밑에서 작은 열쇠 하나를 가져왔지."], ["Mr.", "그러고는 그 신사에게 그것으로 상자를 열어 보라고 손짓했어."], ["Squeers, who had eagerly followed her every motion, lost no time in obeying this hint: and, throwing back the lid, gazed with rapture on the documents which lay within.", "노파의 동작을 하나하나 열심히 좇고 있던 스퀴어스 씨는, 이 눈짓에 지체 없이 따랐지. 뚜껑을 젖히고는, 그 안에 들어 있는 서류들을 황홀하게 바라봤어."]], [["‘Now you see,’ said Peg, kneeling down on the floor beside him, and staying his impatient hand; ‘what’s of no use we’ll burn; what we can get any money by, we’ll keep; and if there’s any we could get him into trouble by, and fret and waste away his heart to shreds, those we’ll take particular care of; for that’s what I want to do, and what I hoped to do when I left him.’", "\"자, 봐요.\" 페그가 그의 옆 바닥에 무릎을 꿇고 앉으며, 조바심치는 그의 손을 막고는 말했어. \"쓸모없는 건 우리가 태워 버리고, 돈이 될 만한 건 챙겨 두지. 그리고 혹시 그자를 곤경에 빠뜨릴 만한 게 있어서, 그 마음을 갈가리 찢어 놓고 시들시들 말라죽게 할 수 있는 거라면, 그런 건 각별히 잘 간수하는 거야. 그게 바로 내가 하고 싶은 거고, 그자를 떠날 때 내가 바라던 거니까.\""]], [["‘I thought,’ said Squeers, ‘that you didn’t bear him any particular good-will.", "\"내 생각엔,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["But, I say, why didn’t you take some money besides?’", "\"당신이 그자한테 별로 좋은 감정은 없는 줄 알았지. 근데 말이야, 왜 돈도 좀 같이 안 챙긴 거야?\""]], [["‘Some what?’ asked Peg.", "\"좀 뭐를?\" 페그가 물었어."]], [["‘Some money,’ roared Squeers.", "\"돈 좀.\" 스퀴어스가 외쳤어."], ["‘I do believe the woman hears me, and wants to make me break a wessel, so that she may have the pleasure of nursing me.", "\"이 여자가 내 말을 알아듣고는, 내 핏줄이라도 터뜨리게 만들려는 게 분명해. 그래야 날 간호하는 재미를 누릴 테니까."], ["Some money, Slider, money!’", "돈 좀, 슬라이더, 돈 말이야!\""]], [["‘Why, what a man you are to ask!’ cried Peg, with some contempt.", "\"아니, 그런 걸 다 묻다니 무슨 사람이 그래!\" 페그가 좀 깔보듯 소리쳤어."], ["‘If I had taken money from Arthur Gride, he’d have scoured the whole earth to find me--aye, and he’d have smelt it out, and raked it up, somehow, if I had buried it at the bottom of the deepest well in England.", "\"내가 아서 그라이드한테서 돈을 챙겼다면, 그자는 날 찾아내려고 온 세상을 다 뒤졌을 거야. 그래, 어떻게든 냄새를 맡고 캐냈을 거라고."], ["No, no!", "내가 그 돈을 잉글랜드에서 제일 깊은 우물 밑바닥에 묻었더라도 말이지. 아니, 아니야!"], ["I knew better than that.", "난 그보다는 똑똑했어. 난 그자의 비밀이 숨겨져 있을 만한 걸 챙겼지."], ["I took what I thought his secrets were hid in: and them he couldn’t afford to make public, let’em be worth ever so much money.", "그자가 도저히 세상에 드러낼 수 없는 것들 말이야. 그게 아무리 큰돈이 되더라도 말이지."], ["He’s an old dog; a sly, old, cunning, thankless dog!", "그자는 늙은 개야. 교활하고 음흉하고 은혜도 모르는 늙은 개라고!"], ["He first starved, and then tricked me; and if I could I’d kill him.’", "처음엔 날 굶기더니, 그다음엔 날 속여먹었어. 할 수만 있다면 난 그자를 죽여 버릴 거야.\""]], [["‘All right, and very laudable,’ said Squeers.", "\"좋아, 아주 갸륵하군.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘But, first and foremost, Slider, burn the box.", "\"근데 슬라이더, 무엇보다 먼저, 그 상자를 태워."], ["You should never keep things as may lead to discovery.", "들통날 수 있는 물건은 절대 두면 안 돼."], ["Always mind that.", "늘 그걸 명심하라고."], ["So while you pull it to pieces (which you can easily do, for it’s very old and rickety) and burn it in little bits, I’ll look over the papers and tell you what they are.’", "그러니 당신이 그걸 조각조각 부수는 동안 — 아주 낡고 삐걱대니까 쉽게 부술 수 있을 거야 — 그리고 그 잔조각들을 태우는 동안, 내가 서류들을 훑어보고 그게 뭔지 말해 줄게.\""]], [["Peg, expressing her acquiescence in this arrangement, Mr.", "페그가 이 방식에 따르겠다고 하자, 스퀴어스 씨는 상자를 거꾸로 뒤집어 안에 든 것들을 바닥에 와르르 쏟았어. 그러고는 빈 상자를 그 여자한테 건넸지."], ["Squeers turned the box bottom upwards, and tumbling the contents upon the floor, handed it to her; the destruction of the box being an extemporary device for engaging her attention, in case it should prove desirable to distract it from his own proceedings.", "상자를 부수게 한 건 노파의 주의를 붙들어 두려고 즉석에서 꾸며 낸 수작이었어. 혹시 자기가 하는 일에서 그 여자의 시선을 돌릴 필요가 생길 경우를 대비해서 말이지."]], [["‘There!’ said Squeers; ‘you poke the pieces between the bars, and make up a good fire, and I’ll read the while.", "\"자!\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["Let me see, let me see.’ And taking the candle down beside him, Mr.", "\"당신은 그 조각들을 살창 사이에 쑤셔 넣고 불을 잘 지펴 봐. 그동안 난 읽을 테니까."], ["Squeers, with great eagerness and a cunning grin overspreading his face, entered upon his task of examination.", "어디 보자, 어디 보자.\" 그러고는 촛불을 옆으로 끌어다 놓고, 스퀴어스 씨는 잔뜩 들떠서 얼굴 가득 음흉한 웃음을 띤 채 살펴보는 일에 착수했어."]], [["If the old woman had not been very deaf, she must have heard, when she last went to the door, the breathing of two persons close behind it: and if those two persons had been unacquainted with her infirmity, they must probably have chosen that moment either for presenting themselves or taking to flight.", "만약 그 노파가 그렇게 귀가 먹지 않았더라면, 마지막으로 문에 갔을 때 바로 그 뒤에 바짝 붙어 있던 두 사람의 숨소리를 분명 들었을 거야. 그리고 그 두 사람이 노파의 그 약점을 몰랐더라면, 아마 그 순간 모습을 드러내든지 아니면 달아나든지 둘 중 하나를 택했겠지."], ["But, knowing with whom they had to deal, they remained quite still, and now, not only appeared unobserved at the door--which was not bolted, for the bolt had no hasp--but warily, and with noiseless footsteps, advanced into the room.", "하지만 자기들이 상대하는 게 누군지 잘 알았기에, 그들은 가만히 있었어. 그러고는 이제 문 앞에 들키지 않고 나타났을 뿐 아니라 — 그 문은 빗장이 걸려 있지 않았거든, 빗장에 고리가 없었으니까 — 조심스럽게, 발소리도 없이 방 안으로 들어왔지."]], [["As they stole farther and farther in by slight and scarcely perceptible degrees, and with such caution that they scarcely seemed to breathe, the old hag and Squeers little dreaming of any such invasion, and utterly unconscious of there being any soul near but themselves, were busily occupied with their tasks. The old woman, with her wrinkled face close to the bars of the stove, puffing at the dull embers which had not yet caught the wood; Squeers stooping down to the candle, which brought out the full ugliness of his face, as the light of the fire did that of his companion; both intently engaged, and wearing faces of exultation which contrasted strongly with the anxious looks of those behind, who took advantage of the slightest sound to cover their advance, and, almost before they had moved an inch, and all was silent, stopped again. This, with the large bare room, damp walls, and flickering doubtful light, combined to form a scene which the most careless and indifferent spectator (could any have been present) could scarcely have failed to derive some interest from, and would not readily have forgotten.", "그들이 살그머니, 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 조금씩 더 깊이 들어오는 동안, 그것도 숨도 쉬지 않는 듯 조심하면서 들어오는 동안, 늙은 마귀할멈과 스퀴어스는 그런 침입은 꿈에도 모른 채, 자기들 말고 가까이 누가 있다는 건 전혀 눈치채지 못하고 제 일에만 바삐 매달려 있었어. 노파는 주름진 얼굴을 난로 살창에 바짝 갖다 대고, 아직 장작에 옮겨붙지 않은 흐릿한 잉걸불을 후후 불고 있었지. 스퀴어스는 촛불 위로 몸을 숙이고 있었는데, 그 불빛이 그의 얼굴을 한껏 흉하게 드러냈어. 불빛이 옆에 있던 노파의 얼굴을 그렇게 비춘 것처럼 말이야. 둘 다 일에 정신이 팔려, 의기양양한 얼굴을 하고 있었지. 그 뒤에 있는 두 사람의 불안한 표정과는 너무도 대조되는 얼굴이었어. 그 둘은 아주 작은 소리라도 들리면 그 틈을 타 발걸음을 옮겼고, 한 치를 채 움직이기도 전에 다시 사방이 조용해지면 또 멈춰 섰지. 이 모든 게 휑하니 텅 빈 방, 축축한 벽, 가물거리는 미덥잖은 불빛과 어우러져 하나의 광경을 이루었어. 아무리 무심하고 무덤덤한 구경꾼이라도 — 거기 누가 있었더라면 말이지 — 거기서 어떤 흥미를 느끼지 않고는 못 배겼을, 그리고 쉽사리 잊지 못했을 그런 광경이었지."]], [["Of the stealthy comers, Frank Cheeryble was one, and Newman Noggs the other.", "그 살금살금 들어온 사람들 가운데 하나는 프랭크 치어리블이었고, 다른 하나는 뉴먼 노그스였어. 뉴먼은 녹슨 주둥이를 잡고 낡은 풀무 한 짝을 집어 들었지."], ["Newman had caught up, by the rusty nozzle, an old pair of bellows, which were just undergoing a flourish in the air preparatory to a descent upon the head of Mr.", "그러고는 그걸 허공에 한 번 휘둘러 스퀴어스 씨의 머리통 위로 내리치려던 참이었어. 그 순간 프랭크가 진지한 몸짓으로 그의 팔을 붙들었어."], ["Squeers, when Frank, with an earnest gesture, stayed his arm, and, taking another step in advance, came so close behind the schoolmaster that, by leaning slightly forward, he could plainly distinguish the writing which he held up to his eye.", "그러고는 한 걸음 더 앞으로 나아가, 그 교장 바로 뒤에 바짝 다가섰지. 살짝 몸을 앞으로 기울이면, 그가 눈앞에 들어 올린 글씨를 또렷이 알아볼 수 있을 정도로 가까이 말이야."]], [["Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨는 그리 학식이 깊은 편이 아니어서, 이 첫 번째 노획물 앞에서 꽤나 쩔쩔매는 듯했어. 그건 정자체로 쓰여 있었는데, 익숙한 눈이 아니고서는 알아보기가 영 쉽지 않았거든."], ["Squeers, not being remarkably erudite, appeared to be considerably puzzled by this first prize, which was in an engrossing hand, and not very legible except to a practised eye.", "그는 왼쪽에서 오른쪽으로 읽어 보았어. 그다음엔 오른쪽에서 왼쪽으로도 읽어 봤지."], ["Having tried it by reading from left to right, and from right to left, and finding it equally clear both ways, he turned it upside down with no better success.", "그런데 어느 쪽으로 읽어도 똑같이 알쏭달쏭하니까, 이번엔 거꾸로 뒤집어 봤어. 그래도 별 소득은 없었지."]], [["‘Ha, ha, ha!’ chuckled Peg, who, on her knees before the fire, was feeding it with fragments of the box, and grinning in most devilish exultation.", "\"하, 하, 하!\" 페그가 킬킬거렸어."], ["‘What’s that writing about, eh?’", "불 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 앉아, 상자 조각들을 불에 넣어 주면서, 더없이 악마 같은 환희에 차서 이를 드러내고 웃었지. \"그게 무슨 글이래, 응?\""]], [["‘Nothing particular,’ replied Squeers, tossing it towards her.", "\"별거 아니야.\" 스퀴어스가 그것을 그 여자 쪽으로 휙 던지며 대답했어."], ["‘It’s only an old lease, as well as I can make out.", "\"내가 보기엔 그냥 오래된 임대 계약서야."], ["Throw it in the fire.’", "불에 던져.\""]], [["Mrs.", "슬라이더스큐 부인은 시키는 대로 했어."], ["Sliderskew complied, and inquired what the next one was.", "그러고는 다음 건 뭐냐고 물었지."]], [["‘This,’ said Squeers, ‘is a bundle of overdue acceptances and renewed bills of six or eight young gentlemen, but they’re all MPs, so it’s of no use to anybody. Throw it in the fire!’ Peg did as she was bidden, and waited for the next.", "\"이건,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"기한 지난 어음하고 갱신된 어음 한 묶음인데, 젊은 신사 예닐곱 명 거야. 근데 죄다 국회의원이라 누구한테도 쓸모가 없어. 불에 던져!\" 페그는 시키는 대로 하고는, 다음 걸 기다렸어."]], [["‘This,’ said Squeers, ‘seems to be some deed of sale of the right of presentation to the rectory of Purechurch, in the valley of Cashup.", "\"이건,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["Take care of that, Slider, literally for God’s sake.", "\"캐셥 골짜기에 있는 퓨어처치 교구 목사직 임명권 매매 증서인 것 같군. 그건 잘 챙겨 둬, 슬라이더, 정말이지 신을 봐서라도 말이야."], ["It’ll fetch its price at the Auction Mart.’", "경매장에 내놓으면 제값을 받을 거야.\""]], [["‘What’s the next?’ inquired Peg.", "\"다음 건 뭐예요?\" 페그가 물었어."]], [["‘Why, this,’ said Squeers, ‘seems, from the two letters that’s with it, to be a bond from a curate down in the country, to pay half a year’s wages of forty pound for borrowing twenty. Take care of that, for if he don’t pay it, his bishop will very soon be down upon him. We know what the camel and the needle’s eye means; no man as can’t live upon his income, whatever it is, must expect to go to heaven at any price. It’s very odd; I don’t see anything like it yet.’", "\"음, 이건,\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"함께 든 편지 두 통으로 보아, 시골에 있는 어느 부목사가 이십 파운드를 빌리고 반년 치 봉급인 사십 파운드를 갚겠다고 쓴 채무 증서 같군. 그건 챙겨 둬. 그자가 안 갚으면, 그의 주교가 곧장 그를 들이닥쳐 닦달할 테니까. 우리야 낙타랑 바늘귀가 무슨 뜻인지 알지. 제 수입이 얼마든 그걸로 못 사는 사람은 어떤 값을 치러도 천국에 갈 생각은 하지 말아야지. 거 참 이상하네. 아직 그 비슷한 것도 안 보여.\""]], [["‘What’s the matter?’ said Peg.", "\"무슨 일이에요?\" 페그가 말했어."]], [["‘Nothing,’ replied Squeers, ‘only I’m looking for--’", "\"아무것도 아니야.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어. \"그냥 내가 찾는 게……\""]], [["Newman raised the bellows again.", "뉴먼이 다시 풀무를 들어 올렸어."], ["Once more, Frank, by a rapid motion of his arm, unaccompanied by any noise, checked him in his purpose.", "또 한 번, 프랭크가 소리 하나 없이 팔을 잽싸게 휘둘러 그를 멈춰 세웠지."]], [["‘Here you are,’ said Squeers, ‘bonds--take care of them. Warrant of attorney--take care of that. Two cognovits--take care of them. Lease and release--burn that. Ah! “Madeline Bray--come of age or marry--the said Madeline”--here, burn THAT!’", "\"여기 있군.\" 스퀴어스가 말했어. \"채무 증서들이군. 챙겨 둬. 위임장. 그것도 챙겨 두고. 자백 증서 두 장. 챙겨 둬. 임대 및 해제 증서. 그건 태워. 아! '매덜린 브레이 — 성년이 되거나 결혼하면 — 앞서 말한 매덜린' — 자, 이건 태워!\""]], [["Eagerly throwing towards the old woman a parchment that he caught up for the purpose, Squeers, as she turned her head, thrust into the breast of his large coat, the deed in which these words had caught his eye, and burst into a shout of triumph.", "스퀴어스는 그러려고 양피지 한 장을 얼른 집어 노파한테 던졌어. 그리고 그 여자가 고개를 돌리는 사이, 방금 그 말이 눈에 들어왔던 그 증서를 자기 커다란 외투 품속에 쑤셔 넣었지. 그러고는 의기양양하게 함성을 내질렀어."]], [["‘I’ve got it!’ said Squeers.", "\"찾았다!\" 스퀴어스가 말했어."], ["‘I’ve got it!", "\"찾았어!"], ["Hurrah!", "만세!"], ["The plan was a good one, though the chance was desperate, and the day’s our own at last!’", "가망 없는 도박이긴 했지만 계획은 좋았지. 그리고 마침내 이긴 거야!\""]], [["Peg demanded what he laughed at, but no answer was returned.", "페그가 뭐가 그리 우습냐고 물었지만, 아무 대답도 돌아오지 않았어."], ["Newman’s arm could no longer be restrained; the bellows, descending heavily and with unerring aim on the very centre of Mr.", "뉴먼의 팔은 더 이상 붙들어 둘 수가 없었지. 풀무가 묵직하게, 한 치도 빗나가지 않고 스퀴어스 씨의 머리통 한복판으로 내리꽂혔어."], ["Squeers’s head, felled him to the floor, and stretched him on it flat and senseless.", "그러고는 그를 바닥에 쓰러뜨려, 납작하게 뻗은 채 정신을 잃게 만들었지."]], [["In which one Scene of this History is closed\nDividing the distance into two days’ journey, in order that his charge might sustain the less exhaustion and fatigue from travelling so far, Nicholas, at the end of the second day from their leaving home, found himself within a very few miles of the spot where the happiest years of his life had been passed, and which, while it filled his mind with pleasant and peaceful thoughts, brought back many painful and vivid recollections of the circumstances in which he and his had wandered forth from their old home, cast upon the rough world and the mercy of strangers.", "옛 집에서 그토록 멀리 떠나오는 길에 자기가 돌보는 친구가 덜 지치고 덜 힘들도록, 니콜라스는 그 거리를 이틀 여정으로 나누었어. 그래서 집을 떠난 지 이틀째 저물녘에, 그는 제 삶에서 가장 행복했던 시절을 보낸 그곳에서 불과 몇 마일 떨어진 곳에 다다랐지. 그곳은 마음을 즐겁고 평온한 생각으로 가득 채워 주면서도, 한편으로는 아프고 또렷한 기억들을 도로 불러왔어. 그와 그의 식구가 옛 집을 떠나 거친 세상으로, 낯선 사람들의 인정에 기대어 떠돌게 된 그 시절의 기억 말이야."]], [["It needed no such reflections as those which the memory of old days, and wanderings among scenes where our childhood has been passed, usually awaken in the most insensible minds, to soften the heart of Nicholas, and render him more than usually mindful of his drooping friend.", "옛날에 대한 추억과, 어린 시절을 보낸 곳을 거니는 일이 아무리 무딘 사람의 마음에도 흔히 불러일으키는 그런 감상 같은 건, 니콜라스의 마음을 부드럽게 만들고 시들어 가는 친구를 평소보다 더 마음 쓰게 하는 데 굳이 필요하지도 않았어."], ["By night and day, at all times and seasons: always watchful, attentive, and solicitous, and never varying in the discharge of his self-imposed duty to one so friendless and helpless as he whose sands of life were now fast running out and dwindling rapidly away: he was ever at his side.", "밤이고 낮이고, 언제 어느 때든, 늘 지켜보고 살피고 마음을 졸이면서, 친구도 없고 의지할 곳도 없는, 이제 삶의 모래가 빠르게 흘러내려 점점 줄어드는 그런 이를 위해 스스로 짊어진 의무를 한 번도 게을리하지 않고, 그는 늘 친구 곁에 있었어. 그는 한순간도 친구를 떠나지 않았지."], ["He never left him. To encourage and animate him, administer to his wants, support and cheer him to the utmost of his power, was now his constant and unceasing occupation.", "친구를 북돋고 기운 차리게 하고, 필요한 것을 챙겨 주고, 할 수 있는 한 최대로 부축하고 달래 주는 일이, 이제 그가 끊임없이 매달리는 일이 되었어."]], [["They procured a humble lodging in a small farmhouse, surrounded by meadows where Nicholas had often revelled when a child with a troop of merry schoolfellows; and here they took up their rest.", "그들은 작은 농가에 소박한 거처를 구했어. 둘레에는 풀밭이 펼쳐져 있었는데, 니콜라스가 어릴 적 명랑한 학교 친구들과 어울려 자주 뛰어놀던 곳이었지. 그들은 거기서 머물기로 했어."]], [["At first, Smike was strong enough to walk about, for short distances at a time, with no other support or aid than that which Nicholas could afford him. At this time, nothing appeared to interest him so much as visiting those places which had been most familiar to his friend in bygone days. Yielding to this fancy, and pleased to find that its indulgence beguiled the sick boy of many tedious hours, and never failed to afford him matter for thought and conversation afterwards, Nicholas made such spots the scenes of their daily rambles: driving him from place to place in a little pony-chair, and supporting him on his arm while they walked slowly among these old haunts, or lingered in the sunlight to take long parting looks of those which were most quiet and beautiful.", "처음에 스마이크는 짧은 거리쯤은 걸어 다닐 만큼 기운이 있었어. 니콜라스가 부축해 주는 것 말고는 다른 도움이 필요하지 않았지. 그 무렵 그가 무엇보다 좋아한 건, 옛날 니콜라스에게 가장 친숙했던 곳들을 찾아가 보는 일이었어. 니콜라스는 이 바람을 들어주었어. 그렇게 하면 병든 소년이 지루한 시간을 잊고, 게다가 뒤에 두고두고 생각하고 이야기할 거리가 늘 생긴다는 걸 알고는 기뻤거든. 그래서 니콜라스는 그런 곳들을 날마다 거니는 산책길로 삼았어. 작은 조랑말 수레에 친구를 태우고 이곳저곳 데려다주기도 하고, 함께 그 옛 추억의 자리들 사이를 천천히 거닐 때면 팔로 부축해 주기도 했지. 또 가장 고요하고 아름다운 곳에서는 햇살 속에 한참을 머물며 작별의 눈길을 길게 보내곤 했어."]], [["It was on such occasions as these, that Nicholas, yielding almost unconsciously to the interest of old associations, would point out some tree that he had climbed, a hundred times, to peep at the young birds in their nest; and the branch from which he used to shout to little Kate, who stood below terrified at the height he had gained, and yet urging him higher still by the intensity of her admiration. There was the old house too, which they would pass every day, looking up at the tiny window through which the sun used to stream in and wake him on the summer mornings--they were all summer mornings then--and climbing up the garden-wall and looking over, Nicholas could see the very rose-bush which had come, a present to Kate, from some little lover, and she had planted with her own hands. There were the hedgerows where the brother and sister had so often gathered wild flowers together, and the green fields and shady paths where they had so often strayed. There was not a lane, or brook, or copse, or cottage near, with which some childish event was not entwined, and back it came upon the mind--as events of childhood do--nothing in itself: perhaps a word, a laugh, a look, some slight distress, a passing thought or fear: and yet more strongly and distinctly marked, and better remembered, than the hardest trials or severest sorrows of a year ago.", "이런 때면 니콜라스는 거의 저도 모르게 옛 추억의 정에 빠져들어, 어떤 나무를 가리키곤 했어. 둥지 속 새끼 새들을 엿보려고 백 번도 더 기어올랐던 나무 말이야. 그리고 그 가지에서 어린 케이트한테 소리치곤 했다고도 했지. 케이트는 밑에 서서 오빠가 올라간 높이에 겁을 잔뜩 먹으면서도, 한편으로는 너무나 감탄한 나머지 더 높이 올라가라고 부추겼다는 거야. 옛 집도 있었어. 그들은 날마다 그 앞을 지났는데, 여름 아침이면 햇살이 흘러 들어와 니콜라스를 깨우던 그 자그마한 창을 올려다보곤 했지. 그때는 다 여름 아침뿐이었어. 정원 담을 기어올라 안을 들여다보면, 니콜라스의 눈에 바로 그 장미 덤불이 보였어. 어떤 어린 짝꿍이 케이트한테 선물로 보낸 걸, 케이트가 제 손으로 심은 것이었지. 남매가 함께 들꽃을 따곤 하던 산울타리도 있었고, 자주 거닐던 푸른 들판과 그늘진 오솔길도 있었어. 가까운 곳에는 골목 하나, 시냇물 하나, 작은 숲 하나, 오두막 하나도 어린 시절의 어떤 일과 얽히지 않은 게 없었어. 그러면 그 기억이 마음속에 되살아났지. 어린 시절의 일들이 다 그렇듯, 그 자체로는 별것도 아니었어. 말 한마디, 웃음 한 번, 눈길 한 번, 사소한 속상함, 스쳐 간 생각이나 두려움 같은 것이었지. 그런데도 일 년 전의 가장 모진 시련이나 가장 깊은 슬픔보다 훨씬 더 강하고 또렷하게 새겨져, 더 잘 기억에 남아 있었어."]], [["One of these expeditions led them through the churchyard where was his father’s grave. ‘Even here,’ said Nicholas softly, ‘we used to loiter before we knew what death was, and when we little thought whose ashes would rest beneath; and, wondering at the silence, sit down to rest and speak below our breath. Once, Kate was lost, and after an hour of fruitless search, they found her, fast asleep, under that tree which shades my father’s grave. He was very fond of her, and said when he took her up in his arms, still sleeping, that whenever he died he would wish to be buried where his dear little child had laid her head. You see his wish was not forgotten.’", "이런 나들이 가운데 한 번은 그들이 니콜라스 아버지의 무덤이 있는 교회 묘지를 지나게 되었어. \"여기서도,\" 니콜라스가 나직이 말했어. \"우리는 죽음이 뭔지도 모를 때부터 여길 어슬렁거렸지. 누구의 유해가 이 아래 묻힐지는 꿈에도 모르고 말이야. 그러다 이 고요함이 신기해서 쉬려고 앉아서는 숨죽여 소곤거리곤 했어. 한번은 케이트가 없어졌는데, 한 시간을 헛되이 찾아 헤맨 끝에 발견했지. 아버지 무덤에 그늘을 드리운 저 나무 아래에서 새근새근 잠들어 있었어. 아버지는 케이트를 무척 아끼셨어. 잠든 케이트를 팔에 안아 올리시며 말씀하셨지. 당신이 죽거든 사랑하는 어린 딸이 머리를 뉘었던 그 자리에 묻히고 싶다고. 봐, 그 소원은 잊히지 않았어.\""]], [["Nothing more passed at the time, but that night, as Nicholas sat beside his bed, Smike started from what had seemed to be a slumber, and laying his hand in his, prayed, as the tears coursed down his face, that he would make him one solemn promise.", "그때는 더 이상 별다른 말이 오가지 않았어. 그런데 그날 밤, 니콜라스가 침대 곁에 앉아 있는데, 잠든 듯하던 스마이크가 화들짝 깨어났어. 그러고는 니콜라스의 손에 제 손을 얹고, 얼굴 위로 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며, 한 가지만 굳게 약속해 달라고 빌었어."]], [["‘What is that?’ said Nicholas, kindly.", "\"그게 뭔데?\" 니콜라스가 다정하게 말했어."], ["‘If I can redeem it, or hope to do so, you know I will.’", "\"내가 들어줄 수 있는 거라면, 아니 들어줄 가망이라도 있는 거라면, 들어줄 거란 거 너도 알잖아.\""]], [["‘I am sure you will,’ was the reply.", "\"틀림없이 들어줄 거예요.\" 그가 대답했어."], ["‘Promise me that when I die, I shall be buried near--as near as they can make my grave--to the tree we saw today.’", "\"내가 죽으면 그 나무 가까이에 묻어 주겠다고 약속해 줘요. 오늘 우리가 본 그 나무 말이에요, 무덤을 낼 수 있는 한 최대한 가까이요.\""]], [["Nicholas gave the promise; he had few words to give it in, but they were solemn and earnest.", "니콜라스는 약속했어. 그 약속에 보탤 말은 많지 않았지만, 그 말들은 엄숙하고 진심이 담겨 있었지."], ["His poor friend kept his hand in his, and turned as if to sleep.", "가엾은 친구는 니콜라스의 손을 제 손에 쥔 채로 잠을 청하려는 듯 돌아누웠어."], ["But there were stifled sobs; and the hand was pressed more than once, or twice, or thrice, before he sank to rest, and slowly loosed his hold.", "그런데 흐느낌을 삼키는 소리가 났어. 그리고 그 손은 그가 잠들어 천천히 쥔 손을 풀기까지, 한 번도, 두 번도, 세 번도 아니게 거듭 꼭 쥐어졌지."]], [["In a fortnight’s time, he became too ill to move about.", "두 주가 지나자, 그는 너무 아파서 돌아다닐 수도 없게 되었어. 한두 번 니콜라스가 베개로 받쳐 그를 수레에 태워 데리고 나가 보았지만, 수레의 흔들림이 그에게는 고통스러웠고, 그 바람에 기절하는 일이 생겼지."], ["Once or twice, Nicholas drove him out, propped up with pillows; but the motion of the chaise was painful to him, and brought on fits of fainting, which, in his weakened state, were dangerous.", "약해질 대로 약해진 그에게는 위험한 일이었어. 집에는 낡은 긴 의자가 하나 있었는데, 낮 동안 그가 가장 좋아하는 쉼터였어."], ["There was an old couch in the house, which was his favourite resting-place by day; and when the sun shone, and the weather was warm, Nicholas had this wheeled into a little orchard which was close at hand, and his charge being well wrapped up and carried out to it, they used to sit there sometimes for hours together.", "그래서 해가 나고 날이 따뜻하면, 니콜라스는 그 의자를 바로 옆에 있는 작은 과수원으로 밀고 갔지. 그리고 친구를 잘 싸매서 안고 나가서는, 거기 앉아 둘이서 몇 시간씩 함께 보내곤 했어."]], [["It was on one of these occasions that a circumstance took place, which Nicholas, at the time, thoroughly believed to be the mere delusion of an imagination affected by disease; but which he had, afterwards, too good reason to know was of real and actual occurrence.", "이렇게 보내던 어느 날, 한 가지 일이 벌어졌어. 그때 니콜라스는 그게 병으로 흐트러진 머릿속이 빚어낸 헛것에 지나지 않는다고 굳게 믿었지. 그런데 나중에 그게 실제로 진짜 있었던 일임을 알게 될, 너무도 충분한 이유가 생겼어."]], [["He had brought Smike out in his arms--poor fellow!", "니콜라스는 스마이크를 품에 안아 데리고 나왔어. 가엾기도 하지!"], ["a child might have carried him then--to see the sunset, and, having arranged his couch, had taken his seat beside it.", "이제는 아이라도 그를 안고 다닐 수 있을 정도였거든. 해 지는 걸 보여 주려고 데리고 나와서, 긴 의자를 잘 놓아 준 다음 그 곁에 자리를 잡았지."], ["He had been watching the whole of the night before, and being greatly fatigued both in mind and body, gradually fell asleep.", "그는 전날 밤을 꼬박 새운 데다 몸도 마음도 몹시 지쳐 있어서, 차츰 잠이 들고 말았어."]], [["He could not have closed his eyes five minutes, when he was awakened by a scream, and starting up in that kind of terror which affects a person suddenly roused, saw, to his great astonishment, that his charge had struggled into a sitting posture, and with eyes almost starting from their sockets, cold dew standing on his forehead, and in a fit of trembling which quite convulsed his frame, was calling to him for help.", "눈을 감은 지 채 오 분도 안 됐을 텐데, 비명 소리에 깨어났어. 갑자기 깨어난 사람이 으레 느끼는 그런 두려움에 휩싸여 벌떡 일어난 그는, 깜짝 놀라고 말았지. 그가 돌보던 친구가 안간힘을 써서 일어나 앉아 있었거든. 눈은 거의 눈구멍에서 튀어나올 듯했고, 이마에는 차가운 식은땀이 맺혀 있었으며, 온몸을 뒤흔드는 떨림에 사로잡혀 그에게 살려 달라고 외치고 있었어."]], [["‘Good Heaven, what is this?’ said Nicholas, bending over him.", "\"맙소사, 이게 무슨 일이야?\" 니콜라스가 그에게로 몸을 숙이며 말했어."], ["‘Be calm; you have been dreaming.’", "\"진정해. 꿈을 꾼 거야.\""]], [["‘No, no, no!’ cried Smike, clinging to him.", "\"아니, 아니, 아니에요!\" 스마이크가 니콜라스에게 매달리며 외쳤어."], ["‘Hold me tight.", "\"꼭 붙잡아 줘요."], ["Don’t let me go.", "날 놓지 마요."], ["There, there.", "저기, 저기요."], ["Behind the tree!’", "나무 뒤예요!\""]], [["Nicholas followed his eyes, which were directed to some distance behind the chair from which he himself had just risen.", "니콜라스는 그의 눈길을 따라갔어. 그 눈길은 조금 전 니콜라스가 막 일어난 그 의자 뒤편 멀찍한 곳을 향해 있었지."], ["But, there was nothing there.", "그런데 거기엔 아무것도 없었어."]], [["‘This is nothing but your fancy,’ he said, as he strove to compose him; ‘nothing else, indeed.’", "\"이건 그냥 네 착각이야.\" 니콜라스가 그를 달래려 애쓰며 말했어. \"정말이지, 다른 건 없어.\""]], [["‘I know better.", "\"난 분명히 봤어요."], ["I saw as plain as I see now,’ was the answer.", "지금 형을 보는 것만큼이나 똑똑히 봤다고요.\" 그가 대답했어."], ["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["say you’ll keep me with you.", "날 형 곁에 두겠다고 말해 줘요."], ["Swear you won’t leave me for an instant!’", "한순간도 날 떠나지 않겠다고 맹세해 줘요!\""]], [["‘Do I ever leave you?’ returned Nicholas.", "\"내가 너를 떠난 적 있어?\" 니콜라스가 되받았어."], ["‘Lie down again--there!", "\"다시 누워. 자!"], ["You see I’m here.", "봐, 내가 여기 있잖아."], ["Now, tell me; what was it?’", "자, 말해 봐. 그게 뭐였는데?\""]], [["‘Do you remember,’ said Smike, in a low voice, and glancing fearfully round, ‘do you remember my telling you of the man who first took me to the school?’", "\"기억나요?\" 스마이크가 낮은 목소리로, 겁에 질려 주위를 두리번거리며 말했어. \"나를 학교로 처음 데려간 그 남자 이야기, 내가 형한테 했던 거 기억나요?\""]], [["‘Yes, surely.’", "\"그럼, 기억하지.\""]], [["‘I raised my eyes, just now, towards that tree--that one with the thick trunk--and there, with his eyes fixed on me, he stood!’", "\"방금 저 나무 쪽으로 눈을 들었어요. 저기 줄기가 굵은 저 나무요. 그런데 거기, 그 남자가 나한테 눈을 박고 서 있었다고요!\""]], [["‘Only reflect for one moment,’ said Nicholas; ‘granting, for an instant, that it’s likely he is alive and wandering about a lonely place like this, so far removed from the public road, do you think that at this distance of time you could possibly know that man again?’", "\"잠깐만 생각해 봐.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"설령 그자가 살아 있어서, 큰길에서 이렇게 멀찍이 떨어진 이런 외딴곳을 어슬렁거리고 있다고 치자. 이렇게 오랜 세월이 지났는데, 그 남자를 다시 알아볼 수 있을 것 같아?\""]], [["‘Anywhere--in any dress,’ returned Smike; ‘but, just now, he stood leaning upon his stick and looking at me, exactly as I told you I remembered him. He was dusty with walking, and poorly dressed--I think his clothes were ragged--but directly I saw him, the wet night, his face when he left me, the parlour I was left in, and the people that were there, all seemed to come back together. When he knew I saw him, he looked frightened; for he started, and shrunk away. I have thought of him by day, and dreamt of him by night. He looked in my sleep, when I was quite a little child, and has looked in my sleep ever since, as he did just now.’", "\"어디서든, 어떤 옷차림이든 알아봐요.\" 스마이크가 대답했어. \"그런데 방금 그자가 지팡이에 기대서 나를 보고 서 있었어요. 내가 기억한다고 형한테 말한 그 모습 그대로요. 걷느라 먼지투성이였고, 허름한 차림이었어요. 옷이 다 해진 것 같았어요. 그런데 그 사람을 보자마자, 그 비 오던 밤이며, 나를 떠날 때 그 사람 얼굴이며, 내가 혼자 남겨졌던 응접실이며, 거기 있던 사람들이며, 죄다 한꺼번에 되살아나는 것 같았어요. 내가 자기를 본 걸 알아채자 겁먹은 얼굴이 됐어요. 흠칫하더니 슬그머니 물러났거든요. 난 낮에는 그 사람을 생각하고, 밤에는 그 사람 꿈을 꿔요. 아주 어린아이였을 때도 그 사람은 내 잠 속을 들여다봤어요. 그리고 그 뒤로 줄곧, 방금처럼 내 잠 속을 들여다봐 왔어요.\""]], [["Nicholas endeavoured, by every persuasion and argument he could think of, to convince the terrified creature that his imagination had deceived him, and that this close resemblance between the creation of his dreams and the man he supposed he had seen was but a proof of it; but all in vain. When he could persuade him to remain, for a few moments, in the care of the people to whom the house belonged, he instituted a strict inquiry whether any stranger had been seen, and searched himself behind the tree, and through the orchard, and upon the land immediately adjoining, and in every place near, where it was possible for a man to lie concealed; but all in vain. Satisfied that he was right in his original conjecture, he applied himself to calming the fears of Smike, which, after some time, he partially succeeded in doing, though not in removing the impression upon his mind; for he still declared, again and again, in the most solemn and fervid manner, that he had positively seen what he had described, and that nothing could ever remove his conviction of its reality.", "니콜라스는 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 말과 이치를 다 동원해서, 이 겁에 질린 친구에게 그건 상상이 자기를 속인 것이며, 꿈이 만들어 낸 모습과 봤다고 믿는 그 남자가 이렇게 닮았다는 게 오히려 그 증거라는 걸 납득시키려 애썼지. 하지만 다 헛수고였어. 한동안 그를 달래서 그 집 주인들에게 잠시 맡겨 둘 수 있게 되자, 니콜라스는 낯선 사람이 보인 적 없는지 꼼꼼히 캐물었어. 그리고 자기도 직접 나무 뒤며, 과수원 곳곳이며, 바로 옆에 붙은 땅이며, 사람이 숨을 만한 가까운 곳은 죄다 뒤졌지. 하지만 다 헛수고였어. 처음 짐작이 맞았다고 확신한 니콜라스는, 스마이크의 두려움을 가라앉히는 데 매달렸어. 한참 만에 어느 정도는 성공했지만, 그의 마음에 박힌 인상까지 지우지는 못했지. 그는 거듭, 또 거듭, 더없이 엄숙하고 간절하게, 자기가 말한 그것을 분명히 봤으며, 그게 진짜였다는 확신은 무엇으로도 절대 지울 수 없다고 단언했거든."]], [["And now, Nicholas began to see that hope was gone, and that, upon the partner of his poverty, and the sharer of his better fortune, the world was closing fast.", "이제 니콜라스는 알기 시작했어. 희망은 사라졌고, 가난을 함께 나누고 더 나은 운수도 함께 누렸던 이 동반자에게 세상이 빠르게 닫혀 가고 있다는 걸 말이야."], ["There was little pain, little uneasiness, but there was no rallying, no effort, no struggle for life.", "고통도 거의 없고, 괴로움도 거의 없었지만, 다시 기운을 차리는 일도, 안간힘도, 살려는 발버둥도 없었어."], ["He was worn and wasted to the last degree; his voice had sunk so low, that he could scarce be heard to speak.", "그는 더할 나위 없이 야위고 쇠약해졌어. 목소리는 어찌나 낮게 잠겼는지, 말을 해도 거의 알아들을 수가 없었지."], ["Nature was thoroughly exhausted, and he had lain him down to die.", "자연의 힘은 완전히 바닥나 버렸고, 그는 죽으려고 자리에 누운 거였어."]], [["On a fine, mild autumn day, when all was tranquil and at peace: when the soft sweet air crept in at the open window of the quiet room, and not a sound was heard but the gentle rustling of the leaves: Nicholas sat in his old place by the bedside, and knew that the time was nearly come.", "화창하고 온화한 어느 가을날, 모든 게 고요하고 평화롭던 그날, 부드럽고 향긋한 바람이 조용한 방의 열린 창으로 살그머니 스며들고, 나뭇잎이 가볍게 바스락거리는 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 들리지 않던 그날, 니콜라스는 침대 곁 늘 앉던 자리에 앉아 있었어. 그리고 그때가 거의 다가왔음을 알았지."], ["So very still it was, that, every now and then, he bent down his ear to listen for the breathing of him who lay asleep, as if to assure himself that life was still there, and that he had not fallen into that deep slumber from which on earth there is no waking.", "어찌나 고요했던지, 그는 이따금 귀를 가까이 숙여 잠든 친구의 숨소리에 귀를 기울이곤 했어. 아직 거기 생명이 있는지, 이 세상에서는 다시 깨어날 수 없는 그 깊은 잠에 빠져 버린 건 아닌지 확인이라도 하려는 듯이 말이야."]], [["While he was thus employed, the closed eyes opened, and on the pale face there came a placid smile.", "니콜라스가 그러고 있는데, 감겨 있던 두 눈이 떠지더니, 창백한 얼굴 위로 잔잔한 미소가 번졌어."]], [["‘That’s well!’ said Nicholas.", "\"다행이다!\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘The sleep has done you good.’", "\"잠을 자고 나니 한결 나아졌구나.\""]], [["‘I have had such pleasant dreams,’ was the answer.", "\"정말 좋은 꿈을 꿨어요.\" 그가 대답했어."], ["‘Such pleasant, happy dreams!’", "\"참 즐겁고 행복한 꿈을요!\""]], [["‘Of what?’ said Nicholas.", "\"무슨 꿈?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["The dying boy turned towards him, and, putting his arm about his neck, made answer, ‘I shall soon be there!’", "죽어 가는 소년은 그에게로 돌아누워, 그의 목에 팔을 두르고 대답했어. \"난 곧 거기 가게 될 거예요!\""]], [["After a short silence, he spoke again.", "잠깐 말이 없다가, 그가 다시 입을 열었어."]], [["‘I am not afraid to die,’ he said.", "\"난 죽는 게 두렵지 않아요.\" 그가 말했어."], ["‘I am quite contented.", "\"아주 만족스러워요."], ["I almost think that if I could rise from this bed quite well I would not wish to do so, now.", "이 침대에서 멀쩡하게 일어날 수 있다 해도, 이제는 그러고 싶지 않을 것 같다는 생각마저 들어요. 형이 우리가 다시 만나게 될 거라고 자주 말해 줬잖아요."], ["You have so often told me we shall meet again--so very often lately, and now I feel the truth of that so strongly--that I can even bear to part from you.’", "요즘 들어 정말 자주요. 그리고 지금은 그게 진실이라는 게 너무 강하게 느껴져서, 형과 헤어지는 것까지도 견딜 수 있을 것 같아요.\""]], [["The trembling voice and tearful eye, and the closer grasp of the arm which accompanied these latter words, showed how they filled the speaker’s heart; nor were there wanting indications of how deeply they had touched the heart of him to whom they were addressed.", "떨리는 목소리와 눈물 어린 눈, 그리고 이 마지막 말과 함께 팔을 더 꼭 끌어안는 모습은, 그 말들이 말하는 이의 가슴을 얼마나 가득 채우고 있는지를 보여 주었어. 그리고 그 말을 듣는 이의 가슴을 얼마나 깊이 울렸는지도 드러나 있었지."]], [["‘You say well,’ returned Nicholas at length, ‘and comfort me very much, dear fellow.", "\"네 말이 맞아.\" 마침내 니콜라스가 입을 열었어."], ["Let me hear you say you are happy, if you can.’", "\"그리고 내게 큰 위로가 된다, 친구야. 할 수 있다면, 네가 행복하다는 말을 들려주렴.\""]], [["‘I must tell you something, first.", "\"먼저 말해 둘 게 있어요."], ["I should not have a secret from you.", "형한테 비밀을 두면 안 되니까요."], ["You would not blame me, at a time like this, I know.’", "이런 때 형은 나를 탓하지 않을 거예요. 알아요.\""]], [["‘I blame you!’ exclaimed Nicholas.", "\"내가 너를 탓한다고!\" 니콜라스가 소리쳤어."]], [["‘I am sure you would not.", "\"형은 절대 안 그럴 거예요."], ["You asked me why I was so changed, and--and sat so much alone.", "형은 내가 왜 그렇게 변했는지, 그리고, 그리고 왜 그렇게 자주 혼자 앉아 있었는지 물었죠."], ["Shall I tell you why?’", "그 까닭을 말해 줄까요?\""]], [["‘Not if it pains you,’ said Nicholas.", "\"네가 괴롭다면 안 해도 돼.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘I only asked that I might make you happier, if I could.’", "\"난 그저 할 수 있다면 너를 더 행복하게 해 주려고 물어본 것뿐이야.\""]], [["‘I know. I felt that, at the time.’ He drew his friend closer to him. ‘You will forgive me; I could not help it, but though I would have died to make her happy, it broke my heart to see--I know he loves her dearly--Oh! who could find that out so soon as I?’", "\"알아요. 그때 그렇게 느꼈어요.\" 그는 친구를 더 가까이 끌어당겼어. \"형은 날 용서해 줄 거예요. 어쩔 수가 없었어요. 난 그분을 행복하게 할 수 있다면 죽어도 좋았을 텐데, 그걸 보는 게 가슴이 찢어졌어요. 그분이 그녀를 끔찍이 아낀다는 걸 난 알아요. 오! 누가 나만큼 그걸 빨리 알아챘겠어요?\""]], [["The words which followed were feebly and faintly uttered, and broken by long pauses; but, from them, Nicholas learnt, for the first time, that the dying boy, with all the ardour of a nature concentrated on one absorbing, hopeless, secret passion, loved his sister Kate.", "이어진 말들은 가냘프고 희미하게 새어 나왔고, 긴 침묵으로 자꾸 끊겼어. 하지만 그 말에서 니콜라스는 처음으로 알게 되었지. 죽어 가는 이 소년이, 단 하나에 사로잡힌 가망 없고 은밀한 사랑에 온 본성을 쏟아붓는 그 열정으로, 누이 케이트를 사랑했다는 걸 말이야."]], [["He had procured a lock of her hair, which hung at his breast, folded in one or two slight ribbons she had worn. He prayed that, when he was dead, Nicholas would take it off, so that no eyes but his might see it, and that when he was laid in his coffin and about to be placed in the earth, he would hang it round his neck again, that it might rest with him in the grave.", "그는 케이트의 머리카락 한 타래를 구해 두었더랬어. 그녀가 매던 가느다란 리본 한두 개에 싸서 가슴에 걸고 있었지. 그는 자기가 죽거든 니콜라스가 그걸 떼어 내어, 자기 눈 말고는 아무 눈에도 보이지 않게 해 달라고 빌었어. 그리고 자기가 관에 누여 땅에 묻히려 할 때, 다시 자기 목에 그걸 걸어 달라고 했지. 무덤 속에서도 자기와 함께 있도록 말이야."]], [["Upon his knees Nicholas gave him this pledge, and promised again that he should rest in the spot he had pointed out.", "니콜라스는 무릎을 꿇고 그에게 이 약속을 했어. 그리고 그가 가리켰던 그 자리에 묻어 주겠다고 다시 한번 약속했지."], ["They embraced, and kissed each other on the cheek.", "그들은 서로 끌어안고, 뺨에 입을 맞추었어."]], [["‘Now,’ he murmured, ‘I am happy.’", "\"이제,\" 그가 가만히 중얼거렸어. \"난 행복해요.\""]], [["He fell into a light slumber, and waking smiled as before; then, spoke of beautiful gardens, which he said stretched out before him, and were filled with figures of men, women, and many children, all with light upon their faces; then, whispered that it was Eden--and so died.", "그는 가벼운 잠에 빠졌다가, 깨어나서는 아까처럼 미소 지었어. 그러고는 아름다운 정원 이야기를 했지. 그 정원이 자기 앞에 펼쳐져 있다고, 거기에는 남자며 여자며 수많은 아이들이 가득한데, 모두의 얼굴에 빛이 어려 있다고 했어. 그러더니 거기가 에덴이라고 속삭였어. 그렇게 그는 숨을 거두었어."]], [["# CHAPTER 59", "# CHAPTER 59"]], [["The Plots begin to fail, and Doubts and Dangers to disturb the Plotter\nRalph sat alone, in the solitary room where he was accustomed to take his meals, and to sit of nights when no profitable occupation called him abroad. Before him was an untasted breakfast, and near to where his fingers beat restlessly upon the table, lay his watch. It was long past the time at which, for many years, he had put it in his pocket and gone with measured steps downstairs to the business of the day, but he took as little heed of its monotonous warning, as of the meat and drink before him, and remained with his head resting on one hand, and his eyes fixed moodily on the ground.", "랄프는 혼자 앉아 있었어. 늘 끼니를 때우고, 돈벌이가 되는 일로 밖에 나갈 일이 없는 밤이면 앉아 있곤 하던 그 외딴 방에서 말이야. 그 앞에는 손도 안 댄 아침상이 놓여 있었어. 그리고 그의 손가락이 탁자 위를 안절부절 두드리는 곳 가까이에 회중시계가 놓여 있었지. 여러 해 동안 그 시계를 주머니에 넣고 또박또박한 걸음으로 아래층으로 내려가 그날의 일을 시작하던 시각이 한참 지났어. 그런데 그는 그 단조로운 경고에도, 눈앞의 음식과 마실 것에도 거의 신경을 쓰지 않았어. 한 손으로 머리를 받친 채, 우울한 눈을 바닥에 떨군 그대로 가만히 있었지."]], [["This departure from his regular and constant habit, in one so regular and unvarying in all that appertained to the daily pursuit of riches, would almost of itself have told that the usurer was not well.", "날마다 돈을 좇는 일에 그토록 한결같고 변함없던 사람이, 늘 지키던 규칙적인 습관에서 이렇게 벗어났다는 건, 그것만으로도 이 고리대금업자가 멀쩡하지 않다는 걸 거의 말해 주는 셈이었어. 그가 마음이든 몸이든 어딘가 탈이 나 있다는 것, 그것도 이런 사람을 이만큼 흔들어 놓을 정도면 결코 가벼운 탈이 아니라는 것은, 그의 핼쑥한 얼굴과 지친 기색, 그리고 푹 꺼지고 생기 없는 눈을 보면 충분히 알 수 있었어."], ["That he laboured under some mental or bodily indisposition, and that it was one of no slight kind so to affect a man like him, was sufficiently shown by his haggard face, jaded air, and hollow languid eyes: which he raised at last with a start and a hasty glance around him, as one who suddenly awakes from sleep, and cannot immediately recognise the place in which he finds himself.", "그러다 그는 마침내 흠칫 놀라며 눈을 들어 황급히 주위를 둘러봤어. 갑자기 잠에서 깨어 자기가 있는 곳을 얼른 알아보지 못하는 사람처럼 말이야."]], [["‘What is this,’ he said, ‘that hangs over me, and I cannot shake off?", "\"대체 뭐지,\" 그가 말했어."], ["I have never pampered myself, and should not be ill.", "\"내 위에 드리워져서 도무지 떨쳐 낼 수가 없는 이게 뭐냔 말이야? 나는 한 번도 나 자신을 호강시킨 적이 없으니, 병이 날 까닭이 없는데."], ["I have never moped, and pined, and yielded to fancies; but what CAN a man do without rest?’", "나는 한 번도 시무룩하게 풀이 죽거나, 헛된 망상에 휘둘린 적이 없는데. 그렇지만 쉬지를 못하는데 사람이 무슨 수가 있겠어?\""]], [["He pressed his hand upon his forehead.", "그는 손으로 이마를 짓눌렀어."]], [["‘Night after night comes and goes, and I have no rest.", "\"밤이 오고 또 가는데, 나는 도무지 쉬질 못해. 잠이 든다 한들, 그 잠이 무슨 쉼이 되겠어?"], ["If I sleep, what rest is that which is disturbed by constant dreams of the same detested faces crowding round me--of the same detested people, in every variety of action, mingling with all I say and do, and always to my defeat?", "늘 같은 그 지긋지긋한 얼굴들이 떼를 지어 내 둘레로 몰려드는 꿈에 시달리는데. 같은 그 지긋지긋한 사람들이 온갖 모습으로, 내가 말하고 행하는 모든 일에 끼어들고, 늘 나를 무너뜨리는 그런 꿈에 말이야."], ["Waking, what rest have I, constantly haunted by this heavy shadow of--I know not what--which is its worst character?", "깨어 있은들 무슨 쉼이 있겠어? 이 무거운 그림자가, 정체가 뭔지도 모를 이게 끊임없이 나를 따라붙는데."], ["I must have rest.", "정체를 모른다는 게 가장 끔찍한 점이지."], ["One night’s unbroken rest, and I should be a man again.’", "나는 쉬어야 해. 하룻밤만이라도 끊기지 않고 푹 쉬면, 다시 사람다워질 텐데.\""]], [["Pushing the table from him while he spoke, as though he loathed the sight of food, he encountered the watch: the hands of which were almost upon noon.", "말을 하면서 그는 음식 보는 것조차 진저리가 난다는 듯 탁자를 밀쳤어. 그러다 시계에 손이 닿았지. 바늘은 거의 정오를 가리키고 있었어."]], [["‘This is strange!’ he said; ‘noon, and Noggs not here!", "\"이거 이상한데!\" 그가 말했어."], ["What drunken brawl keeps him away?", "\"정오인데, 노그스가 안 와!"], ["I would give something now--something in money even after that dreadful loss--if he had stabbed a man in a tavern scuffle, or broken into a house, or picked a pocket, or done anything that would send him abroad with an iron ring upon his leg, and rid me of him.", "무슨 술주정뱅이 싸움에 휘말려서 못 오는 거지? 이번엔 뭐라도 내놓겠어."], ["Better still, if I could throw temptation in his way, and lure him on to rob me.", "그 끔찍한 손해를 보고 난 뒤지만, 돈이라도 내놓겠어. 그 녀석이 술집 싸움에서 누구를 칼로 찌르거나, 남의 집에 침입하거나, 소매치기를 하거나, 다리에 쇠고랑을 차고 끌려갈 짓을 해서 내게서 사라져 주기만 한다면 말이야."], ["He should be welcome to what he took, so I brought the law upon him; for he is a traitor, I swear!", "더 좋기로는, 내가 미끼를 슬쩍 던져서 그 녀석이 나를 털도록 꾀어낼 수 있다면 좋겠어. 가져가는 거야 얼마든지 가져가라지, 내가 그 녀석한테 법의 심판을 안길 수만 있다면."], ["How, or when, or where, I don’t know, though I suspect.’", "그 녀석은 배신자거든, 정말이야! 어떻게, 언제, 어디서 그랬는지는 모르겠지만, 짐작은 가.\""]], [["After waiting for another half-hour, he dispatched the woman who kept his house to Newman’s lodging, to inquire if he were ill, and why he had not come or sent.", "그는 다시 반 시간을 기다리고 나서, 집을 봐 주는 여자를 노그스의 하숙집으로 보냈어. 그가 아픈 건지, 왜 오지도 않고 기별도 없는 건지 알아보라고 말이야."], ["She brought back answer that he had not been home all night, and that no one could tell her anything about him.", "여자는 답을 가지고 돌아왔어. 그가 밤새 집에 들어오지 않았고, 그에 대해 아무것도 아는 사람이 없다는 거였어."]], [["‘But there is a gentleman, sir,’ she said, ‘below, who was standing at the door when I came in, and he says--’", "\"그런데 어떤 신사분이 계세요, 나리.\" 여자가 말했어. \"아래층에요. 제가 들어올 때 문가에 서 계셨는데, 그분이 말씀하시기를······.\""]], [["‘What says he?’ demanded Ralph, turning angrily upon her.", "\"뭐라고 하던가?\" 랄프가 그녀를 향해 화를 내며 따졌어."], ["‘I told you I would see nobody.’", "\"아무도 안 만난다고 했잖아.\""]], [["‘He says,’ replied the woman, abashed by his harshness, ‘that he comes on very particular business which admits of no excuse; and I thought perhaps it might be about--’", "\"그분 말씀이,\" 여자가 그의 거친 말투에 주눅이 들어 대답했어. \"아주 특별한 일로 오셨다고, 핑계를 댈 수 없는 일이라고요. 그래서 제 생각엔 혹시 그게······.\""]], [["‘About what, in the devil’s name?’ said Ralph.", "\"그게 뭐, 대체 무슨 일이라는 거야?\""], ["‘You spy and speculate on people’s business with me, do you?’", "랄프가 말했어. \"넌 내 일을 캐고 넘겨짚고 다니는구나, 그런 거야?\""]], [["‘Dear, no, sir!", "\"아유, 아니에요, 나리!"], ["I saw you were anxious, and thought it might be about Mr Noggs; that’s all.’", "나리께서 마음 졸이시는 게 보여서, 혹시 노그스 씨 일인가 했어요. 그게 다예요.\""]], [["‘Saw I was anxious!’ muttered Ralph; ‘they all watch me, now.", "\"내가 마음 졸이는 게 보였다고!\" 랄프가 중얼거렸어."], ["Where is this person?", "\"이제 다들 나를 감시하는군."], ["You did not say I was not down yet, I hope?’", "그 사람은 어디 있나? 내가 아직 안 내려왔다고 한 건 아니겠지?\""]], [["The woman replied that he was in the little office, and that she had said her master was engaged, but she would take the message.", "여자는 그가 작은 사무실에 있다고 대답했어. 주인이 바쁘다고 말해 두긴 했지만, 전갈은 전하겠다고 했다는 거야."]], [["‘Well,’ said Ralph, ‘I’ll see him.", "\"좋아,\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["Go you to your kitchen, and keep there.", "\"내가 만나지. 넌 부엌으로 가서 거기 있어라."], ["Do you mind me?’", "내 말 알아들었나?\""]], [["Glad to be released, the woman quickly disappeared.", "풀려난 게 반가운 여자는 얼른 사라졌어."], ["Collecting himself, and assuming as much of his accustomed manner as his utmost resolution could summon, Ralph descended the stairs.", "랄프는 마음을 추슬렀어. 그리고 온 힘을 다해 평소의 태도를 최대한 끌어올린 채 계단을 내려갔어."], ["After pausing for a few moments, with his hand upon the lock, he entered Newman’s room, and confronted Mr. Charles Cheeryble.", "자물쇠에 손을 얹고 잠깐 멈춰 섰다가, 그는 노그스의 방으로 들어가 찰스 치어리블 씨와 마주 섰어."]], [["Of all men alive, this was one of the last he would have wished to meet at any time; but, now that he recognised in him only the patron and protector of Nicholas, he would rather have seen a spectre. One beneficial effect, however, the encounter had upon him. It instantly roused all his dormant energies; rekindled in his breast the passions that, for many years, had found an improving home there; called up all his wrath, hatred, and malice; restored the sneer to his lip, and the scowl to his brow; and made him again, in all outward appearance, the same Ralph Nickleby whom so many had bitter cause to remember.", "살아 있는 모든 사람 중에서, 이 사람이야말로 그가 어느 때든 가장 만나고 싶지 않은 축에 드는 사람이었어. 그런데 지금은 그가 니콜라스의 후원자이자 보호자로밖에 보이지 않으니, 차라리 유령을 보는 편이 나았을 거야. 그렇지만 이 만남이 그에게 한 가지 좋은 효과를 주긴 했어. 그 즉시 잠들어 있던 모든 기운을 깨워 일으킨 거야. 여러 해 동안 그의 가슴속에 무럭무럭 자라며 자리 잡고 있던 격정에 다시 불을 붙였지. 그의 분노와 증오와 악의를 모조리 불러냈어. 그의 입술에는 비웃음을, 이마에는 험상궂은 주름을 되살려 놨지. 그리고 겉모습만큼은 다시, 그토록 많은 사람들이 쓰라리게 기억하는 그 랄프 니클비로 만들어 놨어."]], [["‘Humph!’ said Ralph, pausing at the door.", "\"흠!\" 랄프가 문가에 멈춰 서서 말했어."], ["‘This is an unexpected favour, sir.’", "\"이거 뜻밖의 영광이군요, 선생.\""]], [["‘And an unwelcome one,’ said brother Charles; ‘an unwelcome one, I know.’", "\"반갑지 않은 영광이지요.\" 찰스 형제가 말했어. \"반갑지 않다는 거, 나도 압니다.\""]], [["‘Men say you are truth itself, sir,’ replied Ralph.", "\"사람들이 그러더군요, 선생은 진실 그 자체라고.\""], ["‘You speak truth now, at all events, and I’ll not contradict you.", "랄프가 대꾸했어. \"어쨌든 지금 진실을 말씀하고 계시니, 나도 부정하지 않겠습니다."], ["The favour is, at least, as unwelcome as it is unexpected.", "적어도 이 영광이 뜻밖인 만큼이나 반갑지 않은 건 사실이니까요."], ["I can scarcely say more.’", "그 이상은 뭐라 할 말이 없군요.\""]], [["‘Plainly, sir--’ began brother Charles.", "\"분명히 말씀드리자면, 선생······.\" 찰스 형제가 입을 열었어."]], [["‘Plainly, sir,’ interrupted Ralph, ‘I wish this conference to be a short one, and to end where it begins.", "\"분명히 말씀드리자면, 선생,\" 랄프가 가로막았어."], ["I guess the subject upon which you are about to speak, and I’ll not hear you.", "\"나는 이 만남이 짧게 끝나기를, 시작한 자리에서 그대로 끝나기를 바랍니다. 무슨 얘길 하시려는지 짐작이 갑니다만, 나는 듣지 않겠습니다."], ["You like plainness, I believe; there it is.", "선생은 솔직한 걸 좋아하신다죠? 자, 솔직히 말씀드렸습니다."], ["Here is the door as you see.", "보시다시피 저기 문이 있습니다."], ["Our way lies in very different directions.", "우리가 가는 길은 아주 다른 방향이지요."], ["Take yours, I beg of you, and leave me to pursue mine in quiet.’", "부디 선생 갈 길이나 가시고, 나는 조용히 내 길을 가게 내버려 두십시오.\""]], [["‘In quiet!’ repeated brother Charles mildly, and looking at him with more of pity than reproach.", "\"조용히!\" 찰스 형제가 부드럽게 되뇌었어."], ["‘To pursue HIS way in quiet!’", "나무람보다는 안쓰러움이 더 어린 눈으로 그를 바라보면서 말이야. \"조용히 자기 길을 가겠다니!\""]], [["‘You will scarcely remain in my house, I presume, sir, against my will,’ said Ralph; ‘or you can scarcely hope to make an impression upon a man who closes his ears to all that you can say, and is firmly and resolutely determined not to hear you.’", "\"내 뜻을 거슬러 가면서까지 내 집에 머무르시진 않겠지요, 설마.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"아니면, 선생이 무슨 말을 하든 귀를 닫아 버린 사람, 절대로 듣지 않겠다고 단단히 작정한 사람한테 무슨 영향을 줄 수 있다고 기대하시기도 어려울 테고요.\""]], [["‘Mr. Nickleby, sir,’ returned brother Charles: no less mildly than before, but firmly too: ‘I come here against my will, sorely and grievously against my will. I have never been in this house before; and, to speak my mind, sir, I don’t feel at home or easy in it, and have no wish ever to be here again. You do not guess the subject on which I come to speak to you; you do not indeed. I am sure of that, or your manner would be a very different one.’", "\"니클비 씨, 선생.\" 찰스 형제가 대답했어. 아까처럼 부드럽게, 그러나 단호하기도 한 말투였지. \"나는 내 뜻을 거슬러 여기 왔습니다. 정말이지 괴롭고 마음 아프게도, 내 뜻을 거슬러서요. 이 집에 와 본 건 처음입니다. 그리고 솔직히 말씀드리자면, 이 집은 편하지도 마음 놓이지도 않아서, 다시는 오고 싶지 않군요. 선생은 내가 무슨 얘길 하러 왔는지 짐작 못 하십니다. 정말로 못 하세요. 분명합니다. 만약 짐작했다면 선생 태도가 아주 달랐을 테니까요.\""]], [["Ralph glanced keenly at him, but the clear eye and open countenance of the honest old merchant underwent no change of expression, and met his look without reserve.", "랄프는 날카롭게 그를 흘끔거렸어. 그렇지만 이 정직한 늙은 상인의 맑은 눈과 환한 얼굴은 표정 하나 바뀌지 않았어. 거리낌 없이 그의 눈길을 맞받았지."]], [["‘Shall I go on?’ said Mr.", "\"계속해도 될까요?\""], ["Cheeryble.", "치어리블 씨가 말했어."]], [["‘Oh, by all means, if you please,’ returned Ralph drily. ‘Here are walls to speak to, sir, a desk, and two stools: most attentive auditors, and certain not to interrupt you. Go on, I beg; make my house yours, and perhaps by the time I return from my walk, you will have finished what you have to say, and will yield me up possession again.’", "\"아, 그러시죠, 정 그러시다면.\" 랄프가 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸했어. \"여기 말씀하실 벽이 있고, 선생, 책상 하나에 의자가 둘 있지요. 더없이 귀 기울여 줄 청중이고, 선생 말을 끊는 일도 절대 없을 겁니다. 어서 계속하시죠. 내 집을 선생 집처럼 쓰세요. 그러면 아마 내가 산책에서 돌아올 때쯤이면, 하실 말씀을 다 끝내시고 내게 집을 돌려주시겠지요.\""]], [["So saying, he buttoned his coat, and turning into the passage, took down his hat.", "이렇게 말하고서 그는 외투 단추를 채웠어. 그러고는 복도로 돌아서 모자를 내려 들었지."], ["The old gentleman followed, and was about to speak, when Ralph waved him off impatiently, and said:", "노신사가 뒤따라오며 막 입을 열려는데, 랄프가 짜증스럽게 손을 내저으며 말했어."]], [["‘Not a word.", "\"한마디도 마세요."], ["I tell you, sir, not a word.", "분명히 말씀드립니다, 선생, 한마디도요."], ["Virtuous as you are, you are not an angel yet, to appear in men’s houses whether they will or no, and pour your speech into unwilling ears.", "아무리 덕망이 높으셔도, 선생은 아직 천사가 아니잖습니까. 사람들이 원하든 말든 남의 집에 나타나서, 듣기 싫다는 귀에 말을 쏟아붓는 그런 천사 말이오."], ["Preach to the walls I tell you; not to me!’", "벽에다 설교하시라니까요. 내가 아니라!\""]], [["‘I am no angel, Heaven knows,’ returned brother Charles, shaking his head, ‘but an erring and imperfect man; nevertheless, there is one quality which all men have, in common with the angels, blessed opportunities of exercising, if they will; mercy. It is an errand of mercy that brings me here. Pray let me discharge it.’", "\"내가 천사가 아닌 거야, 하늘도 알지요.\" 찰스 형제가 고개를 저으며 대답했어. \"잘못도 저지르고 모자란 사람이지요. 그렇지만 모든 사람이 천사와 똑같이 가지고 있는 자질이 하나 있어요. 마음만 먹으면 누구나 베풀 수 있는 복된 기회 말입니다. 바로 자비지요. 나를 여기 데려온 건 자비라는 심부름입니다. 부디 그걸 다하게 해 주세요.\""]], [["‘I show no mercy,’ retorted Ralph with a triumphant smile, ‘and I ask none. Seek no mercy from me, sir, in behalf of the fellow who has imposed upon your childish credulity, but let him expect the worst that I can do.’", "\"나는 자비 따위 베풀지 않습니다.\" 랄프가 의기양양한 미소를 지으며 받아쳤어. \"그리고 구하지도 않고요. 선생의 어린애 같은 잘 속아 넘어가는 마음을 이용해 먹은 그 녀석을 위해서라면, 내게서 자비를 구하지 마세요. 내가 할 수 있는 최악의 일을 각오하라고 하십시오.\""]], [["‘HE ask mercy at your hands!’ exclaimed the old merchant warmly; ‘ask it at his, sir; ask it at his. If you will not hear me now, when you may, hear me when you must, or anticipate what I would say, and take measures to prevent our ever meeting again. Your nephew is a noble lad, sir, an honest, noble lad. What you are, Mr. Nickleby, I will not say; but what you have done, I know. Now, sir, when you go about the business in which you have been recently engaged, and find it difficult of pursuing, come to me and my brother Ned, and Tim Linkinwater, sir, and we’ll explain it for you--and come soon, or it may be too late, and you may have it explained with a little more roughness, and a little less delicacy--and never forget, sir, that I came here this morning, in mercy to you, and am still ready to talk to you in the same spirit.’", "\"그 애가 선생 손에서 자비를 구한다고요!\" 늙은 상인이 발끈해서 외쳤어. \"선생이야말로 그 애한테 자비를 구하셔야지요, 선생. 그 애한테요. 들을 수 있는 지금 내 말을 안 들으시겠다면, 들어야만 할 때 들으세요. 아니면 내가 할 말을 미리 헤아려서, 우리가 다시는 마주칠 일 없도록 손을 쓰시든가요. 선생 조카는 고귀한 청년입니다, 선생. 정직하고 고귀한 청년이에요. 선생이 어떤 사람인지는, 니클비 씨, 말하지 않겠습니다. 그렇지만 선생이 무슨 짓을 했는지는, 나도 압니다. 자, 선생, 요즘 손대신 그 일을 밀고 나가다가 막혀서 더는 못 하시겠거든, 나와 내 동생 네드, 그리고 팀 링킨워터한테 오세요, 선생. 우리가 풀어 설명해 드리지요. 어서 오세요, 안 그러면 너무 늦을지 모릅니다. 그땐 좀 더 거칠게, 좀 덜 점잖게 설명을 듣게 되실지도 모르니까요. 그리고 절대 잊지 마세요, 선생. 내가 오늘 아침 여기 온 건 선생을 향한 자비 때문이고, 나는 아직도 그 같은 마음으로 선생과 이야기할 준비가 되어 있다는 걸요.\""]], [["With these words, uttered with great emphasis and emotion, brother Charles put on his broad-brimmed hat, and, passing Ralph Nickleby without any other remark, trotted nimbly into the street. Ralph looked after him, but neither moved nor spoke for some time: when he broke what almost seemed the silence of stupefaction, by a scornful laugh.", "이 말을 아주 힘주어, 또 격앙된 감정으로 내뱉고서, 찰스 형제는 챙 넓은 모자를 썼어. 그러고는 다른 말 없이 랄프 니클비를 지나쳐, 잰걸음으로 거리로 나가 버렸어. 랄프는 그의 뒷모습을 바라봤어. 그렇지만 한동안 움직이지도 입을 열지도 않았지. 그러다 그는, 거의 멍하니 넋이 나간 듯한 그 침묵을 비웃는 웃음 한 번으로 깨뜨렸어."]], [["‘This,’ he said, ‘from its wildness, should be another of those dreams that have so broken my rest of late.", "\"이건,\" 그가 말했어."], ["In mercy to me!", "\"하도 터무니없으니, 요즘 내 잠을 그렇게 망쳐 놓은 그 꿈들 중 하나가 분명해. 나를 향한 자비라니!"], ["Pho!", "흥!"], ["The old simpleton has gone mad.’", "저 늙은 멍청이가 미쳐 버렸군.\""]], [["Although he expressed himself in this derisive and contemptuous manner, it was plain that, the more Ralph pondered, the more ill at ease he became, and the more he laboured under some vague anxiety and alarm, which increased as the time passed on and no tidings of Newman Noggs arrived. After waiting until late in the afternoon, tortured by various apprehensions and misgivings, and the recollection of the warning which his nephew had given him when they last met: the further confirmation of which now presented itself in one shape of probability, now in another, and haunted him perpetually: he left home, and, scarcely knowing why, save that he was in a suspicious and agitated mood, betook himself to Snawley’s house. His wife presented herself; and, of her, Ralph inquired whether her husband was at home.", "이렇게 비웃고 깔보는 투로 내뱉긴 했지만, 분명한 건, 랄프는 곰곰이 생각할수록 점점 더 불안해졌다는 거야. 그리고 무언가 막연한 근심과 두려움에 점점 더 시달렸어. 시간이 흘러도 뉴먼 노그스의 소식이 오지 않자, 그 근심은 점점 더 커졌지. 그는 오후 늦게까지 기다렸어. 온갖 불안과 의구심에, 또 마지막으로 만났을 때 조카가 했던 그 경고가 떠올라 괴로워하면서 말이야. 그 경고가 사실이라는 또 다른 증거가 이런 모습으로, 또 저런 모습으로 머릿속에 떠올라 끊임없이 그를 따라붙었거든. 그는 집을 나섰어. 그리고 자기도 왜인지 잘 모른 채, 그저 의심과 동요에 사로잡힌 기분에 이끌려 스놀리의 집으로 향했지. 그의 아내가 나왔어. 랄프는 그녀에게 남편이 집에 있느냐고 물었어."]], [["‘No,’ she said sharply, ‘he is not indeed, and I don’t think he will be at home for a very long time; that’s more.’", "\"없어요.\" 그녀가 쏘아붙였어. \"정말로 없어요. 그리고 한참 동안은 집에 안 들어올 것 같네요. 그게 답이에요.\""]], [["‘Do you know who I am?’ asked Ralph.", "\"내가 누군지 알겠소?\" 랄프가 물었어."]], [["‘Oh yes, I know you very well; too well, perhaps, and perhaps he does too, and sorry am I that I should have to say it.’", "\"아, 그럼요, 잘 알지요. 어쩌면 너무 잘 알아서 탈이지요. 그리고 우리 남편도 어쩌면 그럴 거고요. 이런 말을 해야 한다니 참 안타깝네요.\""]], [["‘Tell him that I saw him through the window-blind above, as I crossed the road just now, and that I would speak to him on business,’ said Ralph.", "\"방금 길을 건너오면서 위층 창문 가리개 너머로 그를 봤으니, 일이 있어서 그와 얘기를 하고 싶다고 전하시오.\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘Do you hear?’", "\"알아들었소?\""]], [["‘I hear,’ rejoined Mrs.", "\"알아들었어요.\""], ["Snawley, taking no further notice of the request.", "스놀리 부인이 그 부탁에는 더 신경도 쓰지 않고 대꾸했어."]], [["‘I knew this woman was a hypocrite, in the way of psalms and Scripture phrases,’ said Ralph, passing quietly by, ‘but I never knew she drank before.’", "\"이 여자가 찬송가랑 성경 구절이나 들먹이는 위선자인 건 알고 있었지만,\" 랄프가 슬쩍 옆을 지나치며 말했어. \"술까지 마시는 줄은 미처 몰랐는걸.\""]], [["‘Stop!", "\"멈춰요!"], ["You don’t come in here,’ said Mr.", "여긴 못 들어와요.\""], ["Snawley’s better-half, interposing her person, which was a robust one, in the doorway.", "스놀리 씨의 아내가 우람한 몸을 문간에 들이밀며 가로막았어."], ["‘You have said more than enough to him on business, before now.", "\"당신은 이미 우리 남편한테 일 얘기로 할 만큼은 다 했어요."], ["I always told him what dealing with you and working out your schemes would come to.", "당신이랑 손잡고 당신 꿍꿍이를 받들다간 어떻게 되겠냐고, 내가 늘 그이한테 말해 왔다고요."], ["It was either you or the schoolmaster--one of you, or the two between you--that got the forged letter done; remember that!", "그 위조 편지를 꾸민 게 당신 아니면 그 교장이에요. 둘 중 하나, 아니면 둘이 짜고 한 짓이지!"], ["That wasn’t his doing, so don’t lay it at his door.’", "그건 기억해 둬요! 그건 우리 남편이 한 짓이 아니니까, 그이한테 뒤집어씌우지 마요.\""]], [["‘Hold your tongue, you Jezebel,’ said Ralph, looking fearfully round.", "\"입 다물어, 이 못된 것아.\" 랄프가 겁먹은 듯 주위를 둘러보며 말했어."]], [["‘Ah, I know when to hold my tongue, and when to speak, Mr.", "\"흥, 나는 언제 입을 다물고 언제 말을 해야 하는지 잘 알아요, 니클비 씨.\""], ["Nickleby,’ retorted the dame.", "부인이 받아쳤어."], ["‘Take care that other people know when to hold theirs.’", "\"다른 사람들도 자기 입 다물 때를 잘 아는지나 조심하시지.\""]], [["‘You jade,’ said Ralph, ‘if your husband has been idiot enough to trust you with his secrets, keep them; keep them, she-devil that you are!’", "\"이 화냥년아,\" 랄프가 말했어. \"네 남편이 멍청하게도 너한테 비밀을 털어놨다면, 잘 간직이나 해. 간직하라고, 이 마귀할멈 같은 것아!\""]], [["‘Not so much his secrets as other people’s secrets, perhaps,’ retorted the woman; ‘not so much his secrets as yours.", "\"어쩌면 우리 남편 비밀이라기보다 남의 비밀일지도 모르죠.\" 여자가 받아쳤어."], ["None of your black looks at me!", "\"우리 남편 비밀이라기보다 당신 비밀 말이에요."], ["You’ll want ‘em all, perhaps, for another time.", "그 험상궂은 눈초리, 나한테 보내지 마요! 어쩌면 그거, 나중에 다 필요할 테니까."], ["You had better keep ‘em.’", "아껴 두는 게 좋을걸요.\""]], [["‘Will you,’ said Ralph, suppressing his passion as well as he could, and clutching her tightly by the wrist; ‘will you go to your husband and tell him that I know he is at home, and that I must see him?", "\"그래,\" 랄프가 끓어오르는 화를 최대한 억누르며, 그녀의 손목을 꽉 움켜쥐고 말했어."], ["And will you tell me what it is that you and he mean by this new style of behaviour?’", "\"네 남편한테 가서, 내가 그가 집에 있는 걸 알고 있으니 꼭 만나야겠다고 전하겠어? 그리고 너랑 그가 이 새로운 행동거지로 대체 뭘 어쩌겠다는 건지 나한테 말해 주겠어?\""]], [["‘No,’ replied the woman, violently disengaging herself, ‘I’ll do neither.’", "\"싫어요.\" 여자가 거칠게 손을 뿌리치며 대답했어. \"둘 다 안 해요.\""]], [["‘You set me at defiance, do you?’ said Ralph.", "\"나한테 대들겠다는 거냐, 그래?\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Yes,’ was the answer.", "\"네.\" 답이 돌아왔어."], ["I do.’", "\"그러겠다고요.\""]], [["For an instant Ralph had his hand raised, as though he were about to strike her; but, checking himself, and nodding his head and muttering as though to assure her he would not forget this, walked away.", "한순간 랄프는 손을 치켜들었어. 마치 그녀를 때리려는 것처럼 말이야. 그렇지만 스스로 멈추더니, 이 일을 잊지 않겠다고 그녀에게 다짐이라도 하듯 고개를 끄덕이며 뭐라고 중얼거리고는, 자리를 떴어."]], [["Thence, he went straight to the inn which Mr.", "거기서 그는 곧장 스퀴어스 씨가 드나들던 여관으로 갔어. 그가 마지막으로 거기 들른 게 언제인지 물어봤지."], ["Squeers frequented, and inquired when he had been there last; in the vague hope that, successful or unsuccessful, he might, by this time, have returned from his mission and be able to assure him that all was safe.", "일이 잘됐든 안 됐든, 이쯤이면 그가 임무를 마치고 돌아와 다 무사하다고 안심시켜 줄 수 있지 않을까 하는 막연한 기대였어. 그런데 스퀴어스 씨는 열흘째 거기 나타나지 않았어."], ["But Mr. Squeers had not been there for ten days, and all that the people could tell about him was, that he had left his luggage and his bill.", "사람들이 그에 대해 해 줄 수 있는 말이라곤, 그가 짐과 셈할 돈만 남겨 두고 갔다는 게 다였지."]], [["Disturbed by a thousand fears and surmises, and bent upon ascertaining whether Squeers had any suspicion of Snawley, or was, in any way, a party to this altered behaviour, Ralph determined to hazard the extreme step of inquiring for him at the Lambeth lodging, and having an interview with him even there. Bent upon this purpose, and in that mood in which delay is insupportable, he repaired at once to the place; and being, by description, perfectly acquainted with the situation of his room, crept upstairs and knocked gently at the door.", "천 가지 두려움과 억측에 마음이 어지러워진 랄프는, 스퀴어스가 스놀리를 의심하고 있는지, 아니면 어떤 식으로든 이 달라진 행동에 한패로 끼어 있는지 알아내야겠다고 작정했어. 그래서 그는 극단적인 모험을 무릅쓰기로 했지. 램버스의 하숙집으로 그를 찾아가, 거기서라도 만나 보기로 한 거야. 이 목적에 골몰한 채, 한순간의 지체도 견디기 힘든 그런 기분으로, 그는 곧장 그곳으로 향했어. 그리고 들은 설명만으로도 그의 방 위치를 훤히 꿰고 있었기에, 살금살금 위층으로 올라가 문을 가만히 두드렸어."]], [["Not one, nor two, nor three, nor yet a dozen knocks, served to convince Ralph, against his wish, that there was nobody inside.", "한 번, 두 번, 세 번을 두드려도, 아니 열두 번을 두드려도, 안에 아무도 없다는 사실을 랄프는 도무지 받아들이려 하지 않았어."], ["He reasoned that he might be asleep; and, listening, almost persuaded himself that he could hear him breathe.", "그는 그가 자고 있을지도 모른다고 생각했어. 그리고 귀를 기울이더니, 그가 숨 쉬는 소리가 들리는 것 같다고 거의 자기 자신을 믿게 만들 정도였지."], ["Even when he was satisfied that he could not be there, he sat patiently on a broken stair and waited; arguing, that he had gone out upon some slight errand, and must soon return.", "그가 거기 없는 게 확실해진 뒤에도, 그는 끈기 있게 부서진 계단에 앉아 기다렸어. 그가 무슨 자질구레한 심부름이라도 나간 거라서 곧 돌아올 게 분명하다고 우기면서 말이야."]], [["Many feet came up the creaking stairs; and the step of some seemed to his listening ear so like that of the man for whom he waited, that Ralph often stood up to be ready to address him when he reached the top; but, one by one, each person turned off into some room short of the place where he was stationed: and at every such disappointment he felt quite chilled and lonely.", "삐걱대는 계단으로 많은 발소리가 올라왔어. 그리고 그중 어떤 발소리는 그가 기다리는 사람의 것과 너무 비슷하게 들려서, 랄프는 그가 계단 끝에 다다르면 곧장 말을 걸 수 있게 자주 벌떡 일어서곤 했어. 그렇지만 사람들은 하나하나, 그가 진을 치고 있는 곳에 못 미친 어느 방으로 꺾어 들어갔어. 그렇게 실망할 때마다 그는 온몸이 으슬으슬 추워지고 외로워졌지."]], [["At length he felt it was hopeless to remain, and going downstairs again, inquired of one of the lodgers if he knew anything of Mr. Squeers’s movements--mentioning that worthy by an assumed name which had been agreed upon between them. By this lodger he was referred to another, and by him to someone else, from whom he learnt, that, late on the previous night, he had gone out hastily with two men, who had shortly afterwards returned for the old woman who lived on the same floor; and that, although the circumstance had attracted the attention of the informant, he had not spoken to them at the time, nor made any inquiry afterwards.", "마침내 그는 더 머물러 봤자 가망이 없다는 걸 느꼈어. 그래서 다시 아래층으로 내려가, 하숙인 하나에게 스퀴어스 씨의 행방을 아는 게 없느냐고 물었어. 그 사람을, 둘이서 미리 정해 둔 가명으로 부르면서 말이야. 그 하숙인은 다른 사람을 가리켜 줬고, 그 사람은 또 다른 사람을 가리켜 줬어. 거기서 그는 이런 얘기를 들었지. 전날 밤늦게 그가 남자 둘과 황급히 나갔고, 그들이 얼마 뒤 같은 층에 사는 노파를 데리러 다시 왔다는 거야. 그리고 그 일이 일러 준 사람의 눈길을 끌긴 했지만, 그때 그들에게 말을 걸지도 않았고 나중에 캐묻지도 않았다는 거였어."]], [["This possessed him with the idea that, perhaps, Peg Sliderskew had been apprehended for the robbery, and that Mr.", "이 말에 그는 한 가지 생각이 들었어. 어쩌면 페그 슬라이더스큐가 그 도둑질 때문에 붙잡혔고, 그 자리에 같이 있던 스퀴어스 씨도 공범으로 의심받아 같이 붙잡힌 게 아닐까 하는 거였지."], ["Squeers, being with her at the time, had been apprehended also, on suspicion of being a confederate.", "만약 그렇다면, 그 사실을 그라이드는 알고 있을 게 틀림없었어. 그래서 그는 그라이드의 집 쪽으로 발걸음을 옮겼어."], ["If this were so, the fact must be known to Gride; and to Gride’s house he directed his steps; now thoroughly alarmed, and fearful that there were indeed plots afoot, tending to his discomfiture and ruin.", "이제는 완전히 겁에 질려서, 정말로 음모가 진행되고 있는 게 아닌지, 자기를 곤경에 빠뜨리고 파멸시키려는 음모가 꾸며지고 있는 게 아닌지 두려워하면서 말이야."]], [["Arrived at the usurer’s house, he found the windows close shut, the dingy blinds drawn down; all was silent, melancholy, and deserted.", "고리대금업자의 집에 다다른 그는, 창문이 꼭 닫혀 있고 거무칙칙한 가리개가 내려져 있는 걸 봤어. 모든 게 고요하고, 음울하고, 인기척이 없었지."], ["But this was its usual aspect.", "그렇지만 그건 늘 보던 그 집의 모습이었어. 그는 문을 두드렸어."], ["He knocked--gently at first--then loud and vigorously.", "처음엔 살살, 그다음엔 크고 세차게 말이야. 아무도 나오지 않았어."], ["Nobody came.", "그는 연필로 카드에 몇 마디 적어 문 밑으로 밀어 넣고는 막 돌아서려 했어."], ["He wrote a few words in pencil on a card, and having thrust it under the door was going away, when a noise above, as though a window-sash were stealthily raised, caught his ear, and looking up he could just discern the face of Gride himself, cautiously peering over the house parapet from the window of the garret.", "그런데 위쪽에서 무슨 소리가, 마치 창틀을 몰래 들어 올리는 것 같은 소리가 그의 귀를 붙잡았어. 올려다보니, 다락방 창문에서 집 난간 너머로 조심스레 내다보는 그라이드 본인의 얼굴이 어렴풋이 보였어."], ["Seeing who was below, he drew it in again; not so quickly, however, but that Ralph let him know he was observed, and called to him to come down.", "아래에 누가 있는지 알아본 그는 얼굴을 도로 집어넣었지. 그런데 그렇게 빠르지는 못해서, 랄프는 그가 본 걸 알아챘다는 걸 그라이드에게 알리고 내려오라고 소리쳤어."]], [["The call being repeated, Gride looked out again, so cautiously that no part of the old man’s body was visible.", "부름이 거듭되자, 그라이드가 다시 내다봤어. 어찌나 조심스러운지 노인의 몸은 한 군데도 보이지 않을 정도였지."], ["The sharp features and white hair appearing alone, above the parapet, looked like a severed head garnishing the wall.", "날카로운 이목구비와 흰머리만 난간 위로 불쑥 솟아 있는 모습이, 마치 잘린 머리 하나가 벽을 장식하고 있는 것 같았어."]], [["‘Hush!’ he cried.", "\"쉿!\" 그가 외쳤어."], ["‘Go away, go away!’", "\"가요, 가!\""]], [["‘Come down,’ said Ralph, beckoning him.", "\"내려와요.\" 랄프가 그에게 손짓하며 말했어."]], [["‘Go a--way!’ squeaked Gride, shaking his head in a sort of ecstasy of impatience. ‘Don’t speak to me, don’t knock, don’t call attention to the house, but go away.’", "\"가라니―까!\" 그라이드가 일종의 미칠 듯한 조바심에 휩싸여 고개를 내저으며 끽끽거렸어. \"나한테 말 걸지 마요. 두드리지도 말고. 이 집에 시선 끌지 말고, 그냥 가요.\""]], [["‘I’ll knock, I swear, till I have your neighbours up in arms,’ said Ralph, ‘if you don’t tell me what you mean by lurking there, you whining cur.’", "\"맹세코 두드리겠어. 동네 사람들이 다 들고일어날 때까지 두드릴 테야.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"거기서 왜 숨어 있는지 말 안 하면 말이야, 이 징징대는 비겁한 놈아.\""]], [["‘I can’t hear what you say--don’t talk to me--it isn’t safe--go away--go away!’ returned Gride.", "\"무슨 말인지 안 들려요. 나한테 말 걸지 마요. 안전하지 않아요. 가요, 가!\" 그라이드가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Come down, I say.", "\"내려오라니까."], ["Will you come down?’ said Ralph fiercely.", "내려올 거야 말 거야?\" 랄프가 사납게 말했어."]], [["‘No--o--o--oo,’ snarled Gride. He drew in his head; and Ralph, left standing in the street, could hear the sash closed, as gently and carefully as it had been opened.", "\"안―돼―애―애.\" 그라이드가 으르렁댔어. 그는 머리를 도로 집어넣었어. 거리에 홀로 남겨진 랄프의 귀에, 창틀이 닫히는 소리가 들렸어. 열렸을 때처럼 가만가만, 조심스럽게 말이야."]], [["‘How is this,’ said he, ‘that they all fall from me, and shun me like the plague, these men who have licked the dust from my feet?", "\"이게 어찌 된 일이지,\" 그가 말했어."], ["IS my day past, and is this indeed the coming on of night?", "\"다들 나한테서 떨어져 나가, 나를 역병처럼 피하다니. 내 발밑의 먼지까지 핥아 대던 이놈들이 말이야."], ["I’ll know what it means!", "내 시절이 끝난 건가, 그리고 이게 정말 밤이 닥쳐오는 건가?"], ["I will, at any cost.", "무슨 영문인지 알아내고 말겠어!"], ["I am firmer and more myself, just now, than I have been these many days.’", "무슨 수를 써서라도 말이야. 나는 요즘 며칠보다 지금 더 단단하고, 더 나다워졌어.\""]], [["Turning from the door, which, in the first transport of his rage, he had meditated battering upon until Gride’s very fears should impel him to open it, he turned his face towards the city, and working his way steadily through the crowd which was pouring from it (it was by this time between five and six o’clock in the afternoon) went straight to the house of business of the brothers Cheeryble, and putting his head into the glass case, found Tim Linkinwater alone.", "그는 문에서 돌아섰어. 처음 화가 치밀어 오를 때는, 그라이드가 겁에 질려 제 발로 열게 될 때까지 그 문을 두들겨 부술까 하는 생각도 했었지. 그는 시내 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌렸어. 그리고 그곳에서 쏟아져 나오는 인파를 (이때쯤이면 오후 다섯 시에서 여섯 시 사이였지) 꾸준히 헤치고 나아가, 치어리블 형제의 사업장으로 곧장 갔어. 유리 칸막이 안으로 머리를 들이밀어 보니, 팀 링킨워터가 혼자 있었어."]], [["‘My name’s Nickleby,’ said Ralph.", "\"내 이름은 니클비요.\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘I know it,’ replied Tim, surveying him through his spectacles.", "\"압니다.\" 팀이 안경 너머로 그를 훑어보며 대답했어."]], [["‘Which of your firm was it who called on me this morning?’ demanded Ralph.", "\"오늘 아침 나를 찾아온 게 당신네 회사 누구였소?\" 랄프가 따져 물었어."]], [["‘Mr. Charles.’", "\"찰스 씨요.\""]], [["‘Then, tell Mr. Charles I want to see him.’", "\"그럼 찰스 씨한테 내가 만나고 싶다고 전하시오.\""]], [["‘You shall see,’ said Tim, getting off his stool with great agility, ‘you shall see, not only Mr.", "\"만나시게 될 겁니다.\" 팀이 아주 날렵하게 의자에서 내려오며 말했어."], ["Charles, but Mr. Ned likewise.’", "\"찰스 씨뿐 아니라 네드 씨까지 만나시게 될 거예요.\""]], [["Tim stopped, looked steadily and severely at Ralph, nodded his head once, in a curt manner which seemed to say there was a little more behind, and vanished.", "팀은 멈춰 서더니, 랄프를 똑바로, 그리고 매섭게 바라봤어. 그리고 더 할 말이 좀 남아 있다는 듯한 무뚝뚝한 태도로 고개를 한 번 끄덕이고는 사라졌어."], ["After a short interval, he returned, and, ushering Ralph into the presence of the two brothers, remained in the room himself.", "잠시 뒤 그가 돌아왔어. 그리고 랄프를 두 형제 앞으로 안내한 다음, 자기도 그 방에 그대로 남아 있었지."]], [["‘I want to speak to you, who spoke to me this morning,’ said Ralph, pointing out with his finger the man whom he addressed.", "\"오늘 아침 나한테 말한 사람, 당신과 얘기하고 싶소.\" 랄프가 자기가 말 거는 사람을 손가락으로 가리키며 말했어."]], [["‘I have no secrets from my brother Ned, or from Tim Linkinwater,’ observed brother Charles quietly.", "\"나는 내 동생 네드한테도, 팀 링킨워터한테도 숨기는 게 없습니다.\" 찰스 형제가 조용히 말했어."]], [["‘I have,’ said Ralph.", "\"나는 있소.\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"니클비 씨, 선생.\""], ["Nickleby, sir,’ said brother Ned, ‘the matter upon which my brother Charles called upon you this morning is one which is already perfectly well known to us three, and to others besides, and must unhappily soon become known to a great many more.", "네드 형제가 말했어. \"오늘 아침 내 형 찰스가 선생을 찾아간 그 일은, 이미 우리 세 사람한테, 그리고 그 밖의 다른 사람들한테도 아주 잘 알려져 있습니다."], ["He waited upon you, sir, this morning, alone, as a matter of delicacy and consideration.", "그리고 안타깝게도 머지않아 훨씬 더 많은 사람들이 알게 될 일이고요. 형이 오늘 아침 혼자 선생을 찾아간 건, 신중과 배려 차원에서였습니다."], ["We feel, now, that further delicacy and consideration would be misplaced; and, if we confer together, it must be as we are or not at all.’", "그런데 지금 우리는 더 이상의 신중과 배려는 어울리지 않는다고 느낍니다. 그러니 우리가 함께 이야기를 나눈다면, 지금 이대로 함께 하든지, 아니면 아예 하지 않든지 둘 중 하나입니다.\""]], [["‘Well, gentlemen,’ said Ralph with a curl of the lip, ‘talking in riddles would seem to be the peculiar forte of you two, and I suppose your clerk, like a prudent man, has studied the art also with a view to your good graces.", "\"좋습니다, 신사 양반들.\" 랄프가 입술을 비죽이며 말했어."], ["Talk in company, gentlemen, in God’s name.", "\"수수께끼처럼 말하는 게 당신들 두 분 특유의 장기인가 보군요. 게다가 저 서기 양반도, 영리한 사람답게, 당신들 비위를 맞추려고 그 기술을 익혀 둔 모양이고."], ["I’ll humour you.’", "여럿이 모여서 얘기하시구려, 신사 양반들, 어디 한번. 내가 맞춰 드리지요.\""]], [["‘Humour!’ cried Tim Linkinwater, suddenly growing very red in the face.", "\"맞춰 줘!\" 팀 링킨워터가 별안간 얼굴이 새빨개지며 소리쳤어."], ["‘He’ll humour us!", "\"이 사람이 우리를 맞춰 준대!"], ["He’ll humour Cheeryble Brothers!", "치어리블 형제를 맞춰 준다고!"], ["Do you hear that?", "저 말 들으셨어요?"], ["Do you hear him?", "저 사람 말 들으셨냐고요?"], ["DO you hear him say he’ll humour Cheeryble Brothers?’", "치어리블 형제를 맞춰 주겠다는 저 말, 들으셨습니까?\""]], [["‘Tim,’ said Charles and Ned together, ‘pray, Tim, pray now, don’t.’", "\"팀,\" 찰스와 네드가 동시에 말했어. \"제발, 팀, 제발 좀, 그만하게.\""]], [["Tim, taking the hint, stifled his indignation as well as he could, and suffered it to escape through his spectacles, with the additional safety-valve of a short hysterical laugh now and then, which seemed to relieve him mightily.", "팀은 그 뜻을 알아채고, 분을 최대한 억눌렀어. 그리고 그 분을 안경 너머로 새어 나가게 하면서, 이따금 짧고 발작적인 웃음이라는 추가 안전판까지 동원했지. 그게 그의 속을 어지간히 풀어 주는 듯했어."]], [["‘As nobody bids me to a seat,’ said Ralph, looking round, ‘I’ll take one, for I am fatigued with walking. And now, if you please, gentlemen, I wish to know--I demand to know; I have the right--what you have to say to me, which justifies such a tone as you have assumed, and that underhand interference in my affairs which, I have reason to suppose, you have been practising. I tell you plainly, gentlemen, that little as I care for the opinion of the world (as the slang goes), I don’t choose to submit quietly to slander and malice. Whether you suffer yourselves to be imposed upon too easily, or wilfully make yourselves parties to it, the result to me is the same. In either case, you can’t expect from a plain man like myself much consideration or forbearance.’", "\"아무도 나한테 앉으라는 말이 없으니,\" 랄프가 둘러보며 말했어. \"내가 알아서 하나 차지하겠소. 걷느라 지쳤거든. 자, 이제, 괜찮으시다면, 신사 양반들, 나는 알고 싶소. 아니, 알기를 요구합니다. 그럴 권리가 있으니까요. 당신들이 그런 말투를 쓰는 게, 그리고 내 일에 그렇게 음흉하게 간섭하는 게, 무슨 근거가 있는지 말이오. 당신들이 그래 왔다고 믿을 만한 까닭이 내겐 있소. 분명히 말해 두지요, 신사 양반들. 내가 세상의 평판 따위는 (흔히들 하는 말로) 별로 신경 쓰지 않는 사람이지만, 중상과 악의에 가만히 굴복할 생각은 없소. 당신들이 너무 쉽게 속아 넘어가는 거든, 아니면 일부러 거기에 한패로 끼어든 거든, 내게 돌아오는 결과는 똑같소. 어느 쪽이든, 나 같은 단순한 사람한테서 많은 배려나 너그러움을 기대하진 마시오.\""]], [["So coolly and deliberately was this said, that nine men out of ten, ignorant of the circumstances, would have supposed Ralph to be really an injured man. There he sat, with folded arms; paler than usual, certainly, and sufficiently ill-favoured, but quite collected--far more so than the brothers or the exasperated Tim--and ready to face out the worst.", "이 말을 어찌나 차분하고 신중하게 하던지, 사정을 모르는 사람이라면 열에 아홉은 랄프가 정말로 억울한 피해자라고 여겼을 거야. 그는 팔짱을 끼고 거기 앉아 있었어. 평소보다 창백한 건 분명했고, 인상도 충분히 험악했지만, 아주 침착했어. 두 형제나 잔뜩 약이 오른 팀보다 훨씬 더 침착했지. 그리고 최악의 상황이라도 맞받아칠 태세였어."]], [["‘Very well, sir,’ said brother Charles.", "\"좋습니다, 선생.\" 찰스 형제가 말했어."], ["‘Very well.", "\"아주 좋아요."], ["Brother Ned, will you ring the bell?’", "네드 동생, 종을 울려 주겠나?\""]], [["‘Charles, my dear fellow!", "\"찰스, 형님!"], ["stop one instant,’ returned the other.", "잠깐만요.\" 다른 쪽이 대답했어."], ["‘It will be better for Mr. Nickleby and for our object that he should remain silent, if he can, till we have said what we have to say.", "\"니클비 씨를 위해서나 우리 목적을 위해서나, 우리가 할 말을 다 마칠 때까지는, 가능하다면 저분이 잠자코 있는 편이 나을 겁니다."], ["I wish him to understand that.’", "그걸 저분이 알아주셨으면 합니다.\""]], [["‘Quite right, quite right,’ said brother Charles.", "\"옳은 말이야, 옳은 말이고말고.\" 찰스 형제가 말했어."]], [["Ralph smiled, but made no reply.", "랄프는 미소를 지었지만, 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았어. 종이 울렸어."], ["The bell was rung; the room-door opened; a man came in, with a halting walk; and, looking round, Ralph’s eyes met those of Newman Noggs.", "방문이 열렸어. 한 남자가 절뚝거리는 걸음으로 들어왔어."], ["From that moment, his heart began to fail him.", "둘러보던 랄프의 눈이 뉴먼 노그스의 눈과 마주쳤지. 그 순간부터, 그의 가슴이 무너지기 시작했어."]], [["‘This is a good beginning,’ he said bitterly.", "\"이거 시작이 참 좋군.\" 그가 씁쓸하게 말했어."], ["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["this is a good beginning.", "시작이 아주 좋아."], ["You are candid, honest, open-hearted, fair-dealing men!", "당신들은 솔직하고, 정직하고, 마음이 열려 있고, 공정하게 거래하는 사람들이지!"], ["I always knew the real worth of such characters as yours!", "당신들 같은 인품의 진짜 값어치를 내가 늘 알고 있었지!"], ["To tamper with a fellow like this, who would sell his soul (if he had one) for drink, and whose every word is a lie.", "이런 녀석을 꼬드기다니. 술이라면 (영혼이 있다면) 제 영혼이라도 팔아 치울 놈이고, 입만 열면 거짓말인 놈을 말이야."], ["What men are safe if this is done?", "이런 짓이 통한다면 안전할 사람이 어디 있겠소?"], ["Oh, it’s a good beginning!’", "오, 시작 한번 좋다!\""]], [["‘I WILL speak,’ cried Newman, standing on tiptoe to look over Tim’s head, who had interposed to prevent him.", "\"내가 말하겠어요.\" 뉴먼이 외쳤어."], ["‘Hallo, you sir--old Nickleby!--what do you mean when you talk of “a fellow like this”?", "그를 막으려고 끼어든 팀의 머리 너머로 보려고 발끝으로 서서 말이야. \"이봐요, 당신―늙은 니클비!―당신이 '이런 녀석'이라고 할 때 그게 무슨 뜻이오?"], ["Who made me “a fellow like this”?", "누가 나를 '이런 녀석'으로 만들었소?"], ["If I would sell my soul for drink, why wasn’t I a thief, swindler, housebreaker, area sneak, robber of pence out of the trays of blind men’s dogs, rather than your drudge and packhorse?", "내가 술이라면 영혼이라도 팔 놈이라면, 왜 도둑이나 사기꾼이나 빈집털이나 뒷마당 좀도둑이나, 눈먼 사람의 개 밥그릇에서 푼돈이나 훔치는 놈이 안 되고, 당신 종놈에 짐말이 됐겠소?"], ["If my every word was a lie, why wasn’t I a pet and favourite of yours?", "내 입만 열면 거짓말이라면, 왜 당신이 아끼는 총애꾼이 안 됐겠소? 거짓말이라고!"], ["Lie! When did I ever cringe and fawn to you.", "내가 언제 당신한테 굽실거리고 아첨한 적 있소?"], ["Tell me that!", "그 말이나 해 보시오!"], ["I served you faithfully.", "나는 당신을 충실하게 섬겼소."], ["I did more work, because I was poor, and took more hard words from you because I despised you and them, than any man you could have got from the parish workhouse.", "가난했으니까 더 많은 일을 했고, 당신과 당신 같은 작자들을 경멸했으니까 더 모진 말을 참아 냈소. 당신이 구빈원에서 데려올 수 있는 그 어떤 사람보다도 더 말이오."], ["I did.", "정말이오. 내가 당신을 섬긴 건 자존심 때문이었소."], ["I served you because I was proud; because I was a lonely man with you, and there were no other drudges to see my degradation; and because nobody knew, better than you, that I was a ruined man: that I hadn’t always been what I am: and that I might have been better off, if I hadn’t been a fool and fallen into the hands of you and others who were knaves.", "당신 곁에서 외톨이였으니까, 내 비참한 꼴을 볼 다른 종놈이 없었으니까. 그리고 내가 망한 사람이라는 걸, 늘 지금 같은 처지였던 게 아니라는 걸, 내가 바보처럼 굴어서 당신이며 다른 악당들 손아귀에 떨어지지만 않았으면 더 잘 살았을지도 모른다는 걸, 당신만큼 잘 아는 사람도 없었으니까."], ["Do you deny that?’", "그걸 부정하겠소?\""]], [["‘Gently,’ reasoned Tim; ‘you said you wouldn’t.’", "\"진정하게.\" 팀이 타일렀어. \"안 하겠다고 했잖나.\""]], [["‘I said I wouldn’t!’ cried Newman, thrusting him aside, and moving his hand as Tim moved, so as to keep him at arm’s length; ‘don’t tell me! Here, you Nickleby! Don’t pretend not to mind me; it won’t do; I know better. You were talking of tampering, just now. Who tampered with Yorkshire schoolmasters, and, while they sent the drudge out, that he shouldn’t overhear, forgot that such great caution might render him suspicious, and that he might watch his master out at nights, and might set other eyes to watch the schoolmaster? Who tampered with a selfish father, urging him to sell his daughter to old Arthur Gride, and tampered with Gride too, and did so in the little office, WITH A CLOSET IN THE ROOM?’", "\"안 하겠다고 했지!\" 뉴먼이 그를 밀치며 외쳤어. 팀이 움직이는 대로 손을 움직여 그를 팔 길이만큼 떼어 놓으면서 말이야. \"나한테 말하지 마! 이봐, 니클비! 내 말 신경 안 쓰는 척 마시오. 그래 봐야 소용없어. 내가 더 잘 아니까. 방금 꼬드기는 얘길 했지? 누가 요크셔 교장을 꼬드겼소? 그것도 그 종놈이 한마디도 못 엿듣게 밖으로 내보내 놓고서, 그렇게 지나치게 조심하면 도리어 의심을 살 수 있다는 걸, 그래서 종놈이 밤에 주인이 나가는 걸 지켜볼 수도 있고, 다른 눈을 시켜 교장을 지켜보게 할 수도 있다는 걸 까맣게 잊고서 말이오. 누가 이기적인 아비를 꼬드겨, 제 딸을 늙은 아서 그라이드한테 팔라고 부추겼소? 그리고 그라이드까지 꼬드겼고, 그것도 그 작은 사무실에서 그랬지. 방 안에 벽장이 딸린 그 사무실에서 말이오!\""]], [["Ralph had put a great command upon himself; but he could not have suppressed a slight start, if he had been certain to be beheaded for it next moment.", "랄프는 자신을 무던히도 다잡고 있었어. 그렇지만 만약 그 때문에 다음 순간 목이 잘리는 게 확실했다 해도, 살짝 움찔하는 것만은 억누르지 못했을 거야."]], [["‘Aha!’ cried Newman, ‘you mind me now, do you? What first set this fag to be jealous of his master’s actions, and to feel that, if he hadn’t crossed him when he might, he would have been as bad as he, or worse? That master’s cruel treatment of his own flesh and blood, and vile designs upon a young girl who interested even his broken-down, drunken, miserable hack, and made him linger in his service, in the hope of doing her some good (as, thank God, he had done others once or twice before), when he would, otherwise, have relieved his feelings by pummelling his master soundly, and then going to the Devil. He would--mark that; and mark this--that I’m here now, because these gentlemen thought it best. When I sought them out (as I did; there was no tampering with me), I told them I wanted help to find you out, to trace you down, to go through with what I had begun, to help the right; and that when I had done it, I’d burst into your room and tell you all, face to face, man to man, and like a man. Now I’ve said my say, and let anybody else say theirs, and fire away!’", "\"아하!\" 뉴먼이 외쳤어. \"이제 내 말에 신경이 쓰이는 모양이지? 무엇 때문에 이 머슴이 주인 하는 짓에 의심을 품게 됐고, 막을 수 있을 때 막지 않았더라면 자기도 그자만큼, 아니 그보다 더 나쁜 놈이 됐을 거라고 느끼게 됐는 줄 아시오? 그건 그 주인이 제 핏줄한테 한 잔인한 짓 때문이었소. 그리고 한 어린 처녀를 노린 더러운 속셈 때문이었지. 그 처녀는 망가지고 술에 절고 비참한 그 부려 먹히는 종놈마저도 마음 쓰이게 했고, 그래서 그가 그 처녀한테 무슨 도움이라도 줄 수 있을까 하는 마음에 (감사하게도, 전에도 한두 번 다른 사람들한테 그래 본 적이 있었으니까) 그 일을 그만두지 못하고 머물게 했단 말이오. 안 그랬으면 그는 주인을 흠씬 두들겨 패서 속을 풀고, 그러고는 악마한테나 가 버렸을 거요. 정말 그랬을 거요. 그건 똑똑히 새겨 두시오. 그리고 이것도 새겨 두시오. 내가 지금 여기 있는 건, 이 신사분들이 그게 최선이라고 여겼기 때문이오. 내가 이분들을 찾아갔을 때 (정말 내가 찾아간 거요. 나를 꼬드긴 사람은 없었소), 나는 이분들한테 말했소. 당신을 찾아내고, 당신을 추적하고, 내가 시작한 일을 끝까지 해내고, 옳은 편을 돕는 데 도움이 필요하다고. 그리고 그 일을 다 해내면, 당신 방에 쳐들어가 모든 걸 다 말해 주겠다고. 얼굴을 마주하고, 사내 대 사내로, 사내답게 말이오. 자, 이제 내가 할 말은 다 했소. 다른 사람이 할 말 있으면 하라고 하시오. 어서 쏟아 내 보시오!\""]], [["With this concluding sentiment, Newman Noggs, who had been perpetually sitting down and getting up again all through his speech, which he had delivered in a series of jerks; and who was, from the violent exercise and the excitement combined, in a state of most intense and fiery heat; became, without passing through any intermediate stage, stiff, upright, and motionless, and so remained, staring at Ralph Nickleby with all his might and main.", "이 마무리 한마디와 함께, 뉴먼 노그스는, 이 연설을 띄엄띄엄 토막토막 내뱉는 내내 줄곧 앉았다 일어서기를 되풀이했고, 그 격렬한 운동과 흥분이 한데 겹쳐 더없이 후끈 달아오른 상태였는데, 그 중간 단계라곤 하나도 거치지 않고 곧장 뻣뻣하게, 꼿꼿하게, 미동도 없이 굳어 버렸어. 그러고는 그대로 랄프 니클비를 있는 힘껏 노려보고 있었지."]], [["Ralph looked at him for an instant, and for an instant only; then, waved his hand, and beating the ground with his foot, said in a choking voice:", "랄프는 그를 한순간, 딱 한순간만 바라봤어. 그러고는 손을 내젓고 발로 바닥을 구르며, 목멘 소리로 말했어."]], [["‘Go on, gentlemen, go on!", "\"계속해 보시오, 신사 양반들, 계속해!"], ["I’m patient, you see.", "보다시피 나는 참을성이 있으니까."], ["There’s law to be had, there’s law.", "법이라는 게 있소, 법이 있단 말이오."], ["I shall call you to an account for this.", "내가 이 일을 두고 당신들한테 책임을 묻겠소."], ["Take care what you say; I shall make you prove it.’", "말조심하시오. 내가 다 증명하게 만들 테니까.\""]], [["‘The proof is ready,’ returned brother Charles, ‘quite ready to our hands.", "\"증거는 준비돼 있습니다.\" 찰스 형제가 대답했어."], ["The man Snawley, last night, made a confession.’", "\"이미 우리 손에 다 갖춰져 있어요. 스놀리란 사람이 어젯밤 자백했습니다.\""]], [["‘Who may “the man Snawley”", "\"'스놀리란 사람'이 누구일 수도 있고,\" 랄프가 받아쳤어."], ["be,’ returned Ralph, ‘and what may his “confession” have to do with my affairs?’", "\"그 사람의 '자백'이라는 게 내 일과 무슨 상관이 있을 수도 있겠소?\""]], [["To this inquiry, put with a dogged inflexibility of manner, the old gentleman returned no answer, but went on to say, that to show him how much they were in earnest, it would be necessary to tell him, not only what accusations were made against him, but what proof of them they had, and how that proof had been acquired.", "고집스럽게 꿈쩍도 않는 태도로 던진 이 물음에, 노신사는 아무 대답도 하지 않았어. 다만, 자기들이 얼마나 진심인지 그에게 보여 주려면, 그에게 어떤 혐의가 씌워졌는지뿐 아니라, 그 증거가 무엇이고 그 증거를 어떻게 손에 넣었는지까지 말해 줄 필요가 있겠다고 했어."], ["This laying open of the whole question brought up brother Ned, Tim Linkinwater, and Newman Noggs, all three at once; who, after a vast deal of talking together, and a scene of great confusion, laid before Ralph, in distinct terms, the following statement.", "이렇게 문제를 통째로 펼쳐 놓자, 네드 형제와 팀 링킨워터, 그리고 뉴먼 노그스가 셋이서 한꺼번에 나섰어. 그들은 한참을 함께 떠들고 큰 혼란이 한바탕 벌어진 끝에, 랄프에게 다음과 같은 이야기를 또렷한 말로 펼쳐 놓았어."]], [["That, Newman, having been solemnly assured by one not then producible that Smike was not the son of Snawley, and this person having offered to make oath to that effect, if necessary, they had by this communication been first led to doubt the claim set up, which they would otherwise have seen no reason to dispute, supported as it was by evidence which they had no power of disproving. That, once suspecting the existence of a conspiracy, they had no difficulty in tracing back its origin to the malice of Ralph, and the vindictiveness and avarice of Squeers. That, suspicion and proof being two very different things, they had been advised by a lawyer, eminent for his sagacity and acuteness in such practice, to resist the proceedings taken on the other side for the recovery of the youth as slowly and artfully as possible, and meanwhile to beset Snawley (with whom it was clear the main falsehood must rest); to lead him, if possible, into contradictory and conflicting statements; to harass him by all available means; and so to practise on his fears, and regard for his own safety, as to induce him to divulge the whole scheme, and to give up his employer and whomsoever else he could implicate. That, all this had been skilfully done; but that Snawley, who was well practised in the arts of low cunning and intrigue, had successfully baffled all their attempts, until an unexpected circumstance had brought him, last night, upon his knees.", "이러했어. 뉴먼은, 그때는 데려올 수 없는 어떤 사람으로부터, 스마이크가 스놀리의 아들이 아니라는 사실을 엄숙히 확언받았어. 그리고 그 사람이 필요하다면 그 점을 선서로 증언하겠다고까지 했지. 그들은 이 이야기를 듣고 비로소, 상대편이 내세운 그 주장을 의심하게 됐어. 그게 아니었더라면 그 주장을 의심할 까닭이 없었을 거야. 그건 그들이 반박할 힘이 없는 증거로 떠받쳐져 있었으니까. 일단 음모가 있다고 의심하기 시작하자, 그들은 어렵지 않게 그 뿌리를 거슬러 올라가, 랄프의 악의와 스퀴어스의 앙심과 탐욕에 다다랐어. 그렇지만 의심과 증거는 전혀 다른 거잖아. 그래서 그들은, 이런 일에서 명석하고 예리하기로 이름난 한 변호사의 조언을 받았어. 그 청년을 되찾으려고 상대편이 벌이는 소송 절차에 대해서는 최대한 느리고 교묘하게 맞서라는 것, 그러는 한편 스놀리를 (분명히 가장 큰 거짓말이 그에게 달려 있을 테니까) 집요하게 물고 늘어지라는 것이었지. 가능하다면 그를 앞뒤가 안 맞고 서로 어긋나는 진술로 몰아넣고, 동원할 수 있는 모든 수단으로 그를 괴롭히고, 그렇게 그의 두려움과 제 한 몸 지키려는 마음을 파고들어, 이 음모 전체를 다 털어놓게 만들고, 그를 고용한 자와 그가 끌어들일 수 있는 다른 누구든 다 넘기게 만들라는 거였어. 이 모든 게 솜씨 좋게 진행됐어. 그렇지만 스놀리는 비열한 술수와 모략의 기술에 능숙한 자라서, 그들의 모든 시도를 보기 좋게 따돌렸지. 그러다 뜻밖의 사건이 어젯밤 그를 무릎 꿇리게 만들 때까지 말이야."]], [["It thus arose.", "일은 이렇게 됐어. 뉴먼 노그스가, 스퀴어스가 다시 시내에 와 있고, 그와 랄프 사이에 어찌나 은밀한 만남이 이뤄졌던지 그가 한마디도 엿듣지 못하게 집 밖으로 내보내질 정도였다고 알려 왔어."], ["When Newman Noggs reported that Squeers was again in town, and that an interview of such secrecy had taken place between him and Ralph that he had been sent out of the house, plainly lest he should overhear a word, a watch was set upon the schoolmaster, in the hope that something might be discovered which would throw some light upon the suspected plot.", "그래서 그 교장에게 감시가 붙었지. 의심스러운 그 음모에 무슨 실마리라도 던져 줄 뭔가가 밝혀지지 않을까 하는 기대에서 말이야."], ["It being found, however, that he held no further communication with Ralph, nor any with Snawley, and lived quite alone, they were completely at fault; the watch was withdrawn, and they would have observed his motions no longer, if it had not happened that, one night, Newman stumbled unobserved on him and Ralph in the street together.", "그런데 그가 랄프와 더 이상 연락을 주고받지도 않고, 스놀리와도 아무 연락이 없으며, 아주 혼자 지낸다는 게 밝혀지자, 그들은 완전히 막막해졌어. 감시는 거둬졌지."], ["Following them, he discovered, to his surprise, that they repaired to various low lodging-houses, and taverns kept by broken gamblers, to more than one of whom Ralph was known, and that they were in pursuit--so he found by inquiries when they had left--of an old woman, whose description exactly tallied with that of deaf Mrs Sliderskew.", "그리고 어느 날 밤 뉴먼이 거리에서 그와 랄프가 함께 있는 걸 들키지 않고 우연히 마주치는 일이 없었더라면, 그들은 더 이상 그의 움직임을 살피지 않았을 거야. 뉴먼은 그들을 뒤따라갔어."], ["Affairs now appearing to assume a more serious complexion, the watch was renewed with increased vigilance; an officer was procured, who took up his abode in the same tavern with Squeers: and by him and Frank Cheeryble the footsteps of the unconscious schoolmaster were dogged, until he was safely housed in the lodging at Lambeth.", "그리고 놀랍게도, 그들이 여러 허름한 하숙집과, 망한 노름꾼들이 운영하는 술집들을 들락거린다는 걸 알아냈지. 그중 한 곳 넘게 랄프를 아는 사람이 있었어."], ["Mr.", "그리고 그들이 한 노파를 찾고 있다는 것도 알아냈는데, 그건 그들이 떠난 뒤 캐물어서 알아낸 거였어. 그 노파의 인상착의는 귀먹은 슬라이더스큐 부인의 것과 정확히 들어맞았지."], ["Squeers having shifted his lodging, the officer shifted his, and lying concealed in the same street, and, indeed, in the opposite house, soon found that Mr.", "이제 사태가 한층 더 심각한 빛깔을 띠기 시작하는 듯 보이자, 감시는 한층 더 빈틈없이 다시 이뤄졌어. 한 수사관을 구해, 스퀴어스와 같은 술집에 묵게 했지."], ["Squeers and Mrs.", "그리고 그 수사관과 프랭크 치어리블이, 아무것도 모르는 교장의 발자취를 뒤쫓았어. 마침내 그가 램버스의 하숙집에 무사히 자리 잡을 때까지 말이야."], ["Sliderskew were in constant communication.", "스퀴어스 씨가 하숙을 옮기자 수사관도 자기 하숙을 옮겼어. 그리고 같은 거리에, 사실 바로 맞은편 집에 숨어 있다가, 곧 스퀴어스 씨와 슬라이더스큐 부인이 끊임없이 연락을 주고받고 있다는 걸 알아냈지."]], [["In this state of things, Arthur Gride was appealed to.", "이런 상황에서, 아서 그라이드에게 도움을 청했어. 그 도둑질은, 일부는 이웃들의 캐묻기 좋아하는 성미 탓에, 일부는 그 자신의 비탄과 분노 탓에, 진작에 알려진 일이었어."], ["The robbery, partly owing to the inquisitiveness of the neighbours, and partly to his own grief and rage, had, long ago, become known; but he positively refused to give his sanction or yield any assistance to the old woman’s capture, and was seized with such a panic at the idea of being called upon to give evidence against her, that he shut himself up close in his house, and refused to hold communication with anybody.", "그렇지만 그는 그 노파를 붙잡는 데 동의하거나 조금이라도 거들기를 딱 잘라 거절했어. 그리고 그녀에게 불리한 증언을 하라는 요청을 받을지도 모른다는 생각에 어찌나 겁에 질렸던지, 집에 꼭 틀어박혀 누구와도 연락을 끊어 버렸지."], ["Upon this, the pursuers took counsel together, and, coming so near the truth as to arrive at the conclusion that Gride and Ralph, with Squeers for their instrument, were negotiating for the recovery of some of the stolen papers which would not bear the light, and might possibly explain the hints relative to Madeline which Newman had overheard, resolved that Mrs Sliderskew should be taken into custody before she had parted with them: and Squeers too, if anything suspicious could be attached to him.", "이에 추적하던 이들이 함께 머리를 맞댔어. 그리고 진실에 아주 가까이 다가가, 이런 결론에 이르렀지."], ["Accordingly, a search-warrant being procured, and all prepared, Mr Squeers’s window was watched, until his light was put out, and the time arrived when, as had been previously ascertained, he usually visited Mrs.", "그라이드와 랄프가, 스퀴어스를 도구 삼아, 도난당한 서류 중 빛을 볼 수 없는 어떤 것들을 되찾으려 흥정하고 있다는 것이었어. 그 서류들은 어쩌면 뉴먼이 엿들은 매들린에 관한 그 암시들을 설명해 줄지도 모를 것들이었지."], ["Sliderskew. This done, Frank Cheeryble and Newman stole upstairs to listen to their discourse, and to give the signal to the officer at the most favourable time.", "그래서 그들은, 슬라이더스큐 부인이 그 서류들을 넘기기 전에 그녀를 붙잡아 들이기로 결정했어."], ["At what an opportune moment they arrived, how they listened, and what they heard, is already known to the reader.", "그리고 스퀴어스도, 그에게 의심스러운 무엇이라도 걸 수 있다면 함께 붙잡기로 했지. 그리하여 수색 영장을 구하고 만반의 준비를 갖춘 다음, 스퀴어스 씨의 창문을 지켜봤어."], ["Mr Squeers, still half stunned, was hurried off with a stolen deed in his possession, and Mrs.", "그의 불이 꺼지고, 미리 알아낸 대로 그가 보통 슬라이더스큐 부인을 찾아가는 시각이 올 때까지 말이야. 이렇게 한 뒤, 프랭크 치어리블과 뉴먼이 살금살금 위층으로 올라가 그들의 대화를 엿들었어."], ["Sliderskew was apprehended likewise.", "그리고 가장 알맞은 때에 수사관에게 신호를 보내기로 했지. 그들이 얼마나 절묘한 순간에 다다랐는지, 어떻게 엿들었는지, 무엇을 들었는지는 이미 독자가 다 아는 일이야."], ["The information being promptly carried to Snawley that Squeers was in custody--he was not told for what--that worthy, first extorting a promise that he should be kept harmless, declared the whole tale concerning Smike to be a fiction and forgery, and implicated Ralph Nickleby to the fullest extent.", "아직 반쯤 얼이 빠져 있는 스퀴어스 씨는 훔친 증서를 지닌 채 끌려갔고, 슬라이더스큐 부인도 마찬가지로 붙잡혔어. 스퀴어스가 붙잡혔다는 소식이 스놀리에게 곧장 전해졌지."], ["As to Mr.", "무슨 일로 붙잡혔는지는 알려 주지 않았어."], ["Squeers, he had, that morning, undergone a private examination before a magistrate; and, being unable to account satisfactorily for his possession of the deed or his companionship with Mrs.", "그러자 그 작자는, 자기는 처벌받지 않게 해 주겠다는 약속부터 받아 낸 다음, 스마이크에 관한 그 이야기 전부가 꾸며 낸 거짓이자 위조라고 털어놓았어. 그리고 랄프 니클비를 빠짐없이 끌어들였지."], ["Sliderskew, had been, with her, remanded for a week.", "스퀴어스 씨로 말하자면, 그날 아침 치안판사 앞에서 비공개 심문을 받았어. 그런데 그 증서를 어떻게 손에 넣었는지, 슬라이더스큐 부인과는 어떻게 한패가 됐는지를 흡족하게 설명하지 못해서, 그녀와 함께 일주일간 구류 처분을 받았어."]], [["All these discoveries were now related to Ralph, circumstantially, and in detail.", "이 모든 사실이 이제 랄프에게 자초지종이 낱낱이 다 이야기됐어."], ["Whatever impression they secretly produced, he suffered no sign of emotion to escape him, but sat perfectly still, not raising his frowning eyes from the ground, and covering his mouth with his hand.", "그것들이 속으로 그에게 어떤 인상을 남겼든, 그는 어떤 감정의 기색도 새어 나가게 하지 않았어. 찌푸린 눈을 바닥에서 들지 않고, 한 손으로 입을 가린 채, 그저 가만히 앉아 있었지."], ["When the narrative was concluded; he raised his head hastily, as if about to speak, but on brother Charles resuming, fell into his old attitude again.", "이야기가 끝나자, 그는 무슨 말을 하려는 듯 황급히 고개를 들었어. 그렇지만 찰스 형제가 다시 입을 열자, 그는 다시 아까의 그 자세로 가라앉았어."]], [["‘I told you this morning,’ said the old gentleman, laying his hand upon his brother’s shoulder, ‘that I came to you in mercy. How far you may be implicated in this last transaction, or how far the person who is now in custody may criminate you, you best know. But, justice must take its course against the parties implicated in the plot against this poor, unoffending, injured lad. It is not in my power, or in the power of my brother Ned, to save you from the consequences. The utmost we can do is, to warn you in time, and to give you an opportunity of escaping them. We would not have an old man like you disgraced and punished by your near relation; nor would we have him forget, like you, all ties of blood and nature. We entreat you--brother Ned, you join me, I know, in this entreaty, and so, Tim Linkinwater, do you, although you pretend to be an obstinate dog, sir, and sit there frowning as if you didn’t--we entreat you to retire from London, to take shelter in some place where you will be safe from the consequences of these wicked designs, and where you may have time, sir, to atone for them, and to become a better man.’", "\"오늘 아침 선생한테 말씀드렸지요.\" 노신사가 동생의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했어. \"내가 자비를 품고 선생을 찾아왔다고요. 이 마지막 거래에 선생이 얼마나 깊이 연루돼 있는지, 또 지금 구류 중인 그 사람이 선생을 얼마나 죄에 끌어들일지는, 선생이 가장 잘 아실 겁니다. 그렇지만 이 가엾고, 아무 잘못 없고, 억울한 청년을 노린 음모에 가담한 자들에게는, 정의가 제 길을 가야 합니다. 그 결과에서 선생을 구해 드리는 건 내 힘으로도, 내 동생 네드의 힘으로도 안 됩니다. 우리가 할 수 있는 최선은, 선생에게 제때 경고를 해서 그 결과를 피할 기회를 드리는 거지요. 우리는 선생 같은 노인이 가까운 친척의 손에 망신을 당하고 벌을 받게 하고 싶지 않습니다. 또 그 친척이 선생처럼 핏줄과 천륜의 정을 죄다 잊어버리는 것도 바라지 않고요. 우리는 선생께 간청합니다. 네드 동생, 자네도 이 간청에 함께하지, 나도 아네. 그리고 팀 링킨워터, 자네도 그렇지. 자네야 고집불통인 척하면서 거기 앉아 그러지 않은 양 인상을 쓰고 있지만 말이야. 우리는 선생께 간청합니다. 런던을 떠나, 이 사악한 음모의 결과로부터 안전할 어딘가에 몸을 숨기시라고요. 그리고 거기서, 선생, 그 죄를 속죄하고 더 나은 사람이 될 시간을 가지시라고요.\""]], [["‘And do you think,’ returned Ralph, rising, ‘and do you think, you will so easily crush ME? Do you think that a hundred well-arranged plans, or a hundred suborned witnesses, or a hundred false curs at my heels, or a hundred canting speeches full of oily words, will move me? I thank you for disclosing your schemes, which I am now prepared for. You have not the man to deal with that you think; try me! and remember that I spit upon your fair words and false dealings, and dare you--provoke you--taunt you--to do to me the very worst you can!’", "\"그래, 당신들 생각엔,\" 랄프가 일어서며 대답했어. \"당신들 생각엔, 그렇게 쉽게 나를 짓밟을 수 있을 것 같소? 잘 짜인 계략 백 가지든, 매수한 증인 백 명이든, 내 뒤꿈치를 물고 늘어지는 거짓된 똥개 백 마리든, 번지르르한 말로 가득 찬 위선적인 연설 백 마디든, 그게 나를 흔들 수 있을 것 같소? 당신들 꿍꿍이를 까발려 줘서 고맙군. 이제 내가 대비를 했으니. 당신들이 상대하는 사람은 당신들이 생각하는 그런 위인이 아니오. 어디 해 봐! 그리고 명심하시오. 나는 당신들의 번드르르한 말과 거짓된 수작에 침을 뱉고, 감히 당신들한테 요구하겠소. 아니, 부추기겠소. 약 올려서라도 시키겠소. 나한테 할 수 있는 짓 가운데 가장 지독한 짓을 해 보라고 말이오!\""]], [["Thus they parted, for that time; but the worst had not come yet.", "이렇게 그들은 그때는 헤어졌어. 그렇지만 최악은 아직 오지 않았어."]], [["The Dangers thicken, and the Worst is told\nInstead of going home, Ralph threw himself into the first street cabriolet he could find, and, directing the driver towards the police-office of the district in which Mr.", "집으로 가는 대신, 랄프는 눈에 띄는 첫 번째 거리 마차에 몸을 던지듯 올라탔어. 그러고는 스퀴어스 씨가 봉변을 당한 그 동네 경찰서 쪽으로 마부에게 가자고 했지."], ["Squeers’s misfortunes had occurred, alighted at a short distance from it, and, discharging the man, went the rest of his way thither on foot.", "경찰서에서 조금 떨어진 곳에서 내려 마부를 돌려보내고, 나머지 길은 걸어서 갔어. 그가 그토록 걱정하던 사람의 행방을 물어보니, 마침 때를 잘 맞춰 온 셈이었어."], ["Inquiring for the object of his solicitude, he learnt that he had timed his visit well; for Mr Squeers was, in fact, at that moment waiting for a hackney coach he had ordered, and in which he purposed proceeding to his week’s retirement, like a gentleman.", "스퀴어스 씨는 바로 그 순간 미리 불러 둔 삯마차를 기다리고 있었거든. 그걸 타고 일주일짜리 유치 생활을 하러, 신사답게 떠날 작정이었지."]], [["Demanding speech with the prisoner, he was ushered into a kind of waiting-room in which, by reason of his scholastic profession and superior respectability, Mr. Squeers had been permitted to pass the day.", "죄수와 이야기 좀 하게 해 달라고 하자, 그는 대기실 같은 방으로 안내받았어."], ["Here, by the light of a guttering and blackened candle, he could barely discern the schoolmaster, fast asleep on a bench in a remote corner.", "스퀴어스 씨는 교사라는 직업과 남다른 점잖음 덕분에 거기서 하루를 보내도 좋다는 허락을 받은 모양이었어."], ["An empty glass stood on a table before him, which, with his somnolent condition and a very strong smell of brandy and water, forewarned the visitor that Mr.", "촛농을 흘리며 시커멓게 타들어 가는 촛불 하나에 의지해, 랄프는 저 멀리 구석 벤치에서 깊이 잠든 교장의 모습을 겨우 알아볼 수 있었어. 그의 앞 탁자에는 빈 잔이 놓여 있었지."], ["Squeers had been seeking, in creature comforts, a temporary forgetfulness of his unpleasant situation.", "그 빈 잔에다 졸음에 푹 빠진 모양새, 그리고 브랜디와 물 냄새가 코를 찌르는 걸 보니, 스퀴어스 씨가 술기운에 기대 제 처지의 답답함을 잠시나마 잊어 보려 했다는 걸 손님은 미리 짐작할 수 있었지."]], [["It was not a very easy matter to rouse him: so lethargic and heavy were his slumbers. Regaining his faculties by slow and faint glimmerings, he at length sat upright; and, displaying a very yellow face, a very red nose, and a very bristly beard: the joint effect of which was considerably heightened by a dirty white handkerchief, spotted with blood, drawn over the crown of his head and tied under his chin: stared ruefully at Ralph in silence, until his feelings found a vent in this pithy sentence:", "그를 깨우는 일은 그리 쉽지가 않았어. 잠이 어찌나 깊고 무거운지 말이야. 흐릿하고 느릿하게 정신을 차리더니, 마침내 몸을 똑바로 일으켜 앉았어. 그러자 누런 얼굴에 새빨간 코, 뻣뻣한 수염이 드러났지. 게다가 피 얼룩이 진 더러운 흰 손수건을 머리 정수리에 둘러 턱 밑에서 묶어 놓은 탓에, 그 꼴이 한층 더 가관이었어. 그는 한참 말없이 랄프를 처량하게 쳐다보다가, 끓어오르는 심정을 이런 짤막한 한마디로 터뜨렸어."]], [["‘I say, young fellow, you’ve been and done it now; you have!’", "\"이봐, 젊은 양반, 이번엔 당신이 단단히 일을 저질렀어. 그래, 당신이 그랬다고!\""]], [["‘What’s the matter with your head?’ asked Ralph.", "\"머리가 왜 그래?\" 랄프가 물었어."]], [["‘Why, your man, your informing kidnapping man, has been and broke it,’ rejoined Squeers sulkily; ‘that’s what’s the matter with it.", "\"왜기는, 당신 부하가, 그 밀고하고 사람 납치하는 그 부하 놈이 깨 놨지.\" 스퀴어스가 뾰로통하게 대꾸했어."], ["You’ve come at last, have you?’", "\"그래서 이 꼴이 된 거야. 이제야 왔구먼, 그렇지?\""]], [["‘Why have you not sent to me?’ said Ralph.", "\"왜 나한테 사람을 보내지 않았소?\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["‘How could I come till I knew what had befallen you?’", "\"당신한테 무슨 일이 닥쳤는지 모르는데 내가 어떻게 올 수가 있겠소?\""]], [["‘My family!’ hiccuped Mr.", "\"우리 가족!\""], ["Squeers, raising his eye to the ceiling: ‘my daughter, as is at that age when all the sensibilities is a-coming out strong in blow--my son as is the young Norval of private life, and the pride and ornament of a doting willage--here’s a shock for my family!", "스퀴어스 씨가 딸꾹질을 하며 천장으로 눈을 치켜떴어. \"딱 한창 감수성이 활짝 꽃피어 오를 나이인 내 딸, 사사로운 삶에서야 어린 노발이요, 자식을 끔찍이 아끼는 마을의 자랑이자 꽃인 내 아들 ― 우리 가족한테 이 무슨 날벼락이냐고!"], ["The coat-of-arms of the Squeerses is tore, and their sun is gone down into the ocean wave!’", "스퀴어스 집안의 문장이 찢기고, 그 집안의 해가 저 바다 물결 속으로 가라앉아 버렸단 말이다!\""]], [["‘You have been drinking,’ said Ralph, ‘and have not yet slept yourself sober.’", "\"당신 술 마셨군.\" 랄프가 말했어. \"아직 술이 안 깬 거고.\""]], [["‘I haven’t been drinking YOUR health, my codger,’ replied Mr.", "\"당신 건강을 빌면서 마신 건 아니니까, 영감.\""], ["Squeers; ‘so you have nothing to do with that.’", "스퀴어스 씨가 대꾸했어. \"당신이 상관할 바 아니지.\""]], [["Ralph suppressed the indignation which the schoolmaster’s altered and insolent manner awakened, and asked again why he had not sent to him.", "랄프는 교장의 달라진 데다 건방지기까지 한 태도에 치미는 분노를 꾹 눌렀어. 그러고는 왜 자기한테 사람을 보내지 않았느냐고 다시 물었어."]], [["‘What should I get by sending to you?’ returned Squeers.", "\"당신한테 사람 보내서 내가 득 볼 게 뭐가 있는데?\" 스퀴어스가 받아쳤어."], ["‘To be known to be in with you wouldn’t do me a deal of good, and they won’t take bail till they know something more of the case, so here am I hard and fast: and there are you, loose and comfortable.’", "\"당신이랑 한패인 게 알려져 봐야 나한테 좋을 거 하나 없고, 이 사건 사정을 좀 더 알아내기 전까진 저들이 보석도 안 받아 줘. 그래서 나는 여기 꽁꽁 묶여 있고, 당신은 거기서 자유롭게 편하게 있는 거잖아.\""]], [["‘And so must you be in a few days,’ retorted Ralph, with affected good-humour.", "\"당신도 며칠이면 그렇게 될 거요.\" 랄프가 짐짓 기분 좋은 척하며 받아쳤어."], ["‘They can’t hurt you, man.’", "\"저들이 당신을 어쩌지 못해.\""]], [["‘Why, I suppose they can’t do much to me, if I explain how it was that I got into the good company of that there ca-daverous old Slider,’ replied Squeers viciously, ‘who I wish was dead and buried, and resurrected and dissected, and hung upon wires in a anatomical museum, before ever I’d had anything to do with her.", "\"하긴, 어쩌다가 내가 그 송장 같은 늙은이 슬라이더랑 한패가 됐는지 설명만 하면, 나한테 큰일 못 하겠지.\" 스퀴어스가 독기를 품고 대꾸했어."], ["This is what him with the powdered head says this morning, in so many words: “Prisoner!", "\"그 할망구는 내가 엮이기 전에 차라리 죽어서 묻히고, 다시 파헤쳐져 해부당하고, 해부 박물관에 철사로 매달려 있었으면 좋겠다고. 오늘 아침에 그 머리에 분 칠한 양반이 한 말이 딱 이거였어."], ["As you have been found in company with this woman; as you were detected in possession of this document; as you were engaged with her in fraudulently destroying others, and can give no satisfactory account of yourself; I shall remand you for a week, in order that inquiries may be made, and evidence got.", "'죄인! 그대가 이 여자와 함께 있다가 붙잡혔고, 이 문서를 소지한 채 적발되었으며, 그 여자와 짜고 다른 문서들을 부정하게 없애려 했고, 그러면서 자기에 대한 변명도 시원찮으니, 조사를 하고 증거를 모으도록 일주일간 그대를 유치하겠노라."], ["And meanwhile I can’t take any bail for your appearance.”", "그동안에는 그대의 출두를 보장하는 어떤 보석도 받지 않겠다.'"], ["Well then, what I say now is, that I CAN give a satisfactory account of myself; I can hand in the card of my establishment and say, “I am the Wackford Squeers as is therein named, sir.", "자, 그럼 내가 지금 하는 말은, 내가 나에 대해 시원하게 변명할 수 있다는 거야. 내 학원 명함을 내밀면서 이렇게 말하면 돼."], ["I am the man as is guaranteed, by unimpeachable references, to be a out-and-outer in morals and uprightness of principle.", "'저는 여기 적힌 그 와크포드 스퀴어스올시다, 나리. 저로 말씀드리면, 흠잡을 데 없는 추천장으로 도덕성과 곧은 신념이 둘째가라면 서러운 사람이라고 보증받은 몸입니다."], ["Whatever is wrong in this business is no fault of mine.", "이 일에서 뭐가 잘못됐든 그건 제 잘못이 아닙니다."], ["I had no evil design in it, sir.", "저는 나쁜 마음이라곤 없었어요, 나리."], ["I was not aware that anything was wrong.", "무엇이 잘못됐는지조차 몰랐다고요."], ["I was merely employed by a friend, my friend Mr. Ralph Nickleby, of Golden Square.", "저는 그저 친구한테, 골든 스퀘어에 사는 제 친구 랄프 니클비 씨한테 고용됐을 뿐입니다."], ["Send for him, sir, and ask him what he has to say; he’s the man; not me!”’", "그 사람을 불러서 할 말 있으면 해 보라고 하십시오, 나리. 그 사람이 장본인이지 제가 아닙니다!'\""]], [["‘What document was it that you had?’ asked Ralph, evading, for the moment, the point just raised.", "\"당신이 가지고 있던 문서가 뭐였소?\" 랄프가 방금 나온 이야기는 잠시 비껴가며 물었어."]], [["‘What document?", "\"문서가 뭐냐고?"], ["Why, THE document,’ replied Squeers.", "아니, 그 문서지.\" 스퀴어스가 대답했어."], ["‘The Madeline What’s-her-name one.", "\"그 매들린 뭐시기 거 말이야."], ["It was a will; that’s what it was.’", "유언장이었어, 그게 바로 유언장이었다고.\""]], [["‘Of what nature, whose will, when dated, how benefiting her, to what extent?’ asked Ralph hurriedly.", "\"어떤 성격의, 누구의 유언장이고, 작성된 날짜는 언제고, 그 여자한테 어떻게, 얼마나 이로운 거였소?\" 랄프가 다급하게 물었어."]], [["‘A will in her favour; that’s all I know,’ rejoined Squeers, ‘and that’s more than you’d have known, if you’d had them bellows on your head.", "\"그 여자한테 유리한 유언장이라는 거, 그게 내가 아는 전부야.\" 스퀴어스가 대꾸했어."], ["It’s all owing to your precious caution that they got hold of it.", "\"그리고 그 풀무 같은 게 당신 머리에 떨어졌어도 그 정도밖에는 못 알았을걸. 저들이 그걸 손에 넣은 건 다 당신이 그 잘난 신중함을 부린 탓이야."], ["If you had let me burn it, and taken my word that it was gone, it would have been a heap of ashes behind the fire, instead of being whole and sound, inside of my great-coat.’", "그냥 나한테 태우게 두고 없어졌다는 내 말만 믿었으면, 그건 지금쯤 벽난로 뒤에 잿더미가 됐을 거 아니냐고. 멀쩡하고 온전한 채로 내 외투 속에 들어 있는 대신에 말이야.\""]], [["‘Beaten at every point!’ muttered Ralph.", "\"가는 데마다 한 방씩 얻어맞는구나!\" 랄프가 중얼거렸어."]], [["‘Ah!’ sighed Squeers, who, between the brandy and water and his broken head, wandered strangely, ‘at the delightful village of Dotheboys near Greta Bridge in Yorkshire, youth are boarded, clothed, booked, washed, furnished with pocket-money, provided with all necessaries, instructed in all languages living and dead, mathematics, orthography, geometry, astronomy, trigonometry--this is a altered state of trigonomics, this is!", "\"아!\" 스퀴어스가 한숨을 내쉬었어."], ["A double 1--all, everything--a cobbler’s weapon.", "브랜디와 물에다 깨진 머리까지 겹쳐서 정신이 묘하게 오락가락하고 있었거든. \"요크셔의 그레타 다리 근처, 그 즐거운 마을 두더보이즈에서는 말이야, 청소년들을 재워 주고, 입혀 주고, 책 사 주고, 씻겨 주고, 용돈 주고, 필요한 건 다 대 주고, 살아 있는 말 죽은 말 다 가르치고, 수학, 철자법, 기하학, 천문학, 삼각법까지 가르쳐 ― 이거야말로 삼각법의 변천이지, 이게 바로!"], ["U-p-up, adjective, not down.", "엘이 둘인 올(all) ― 전부, 모조리 ― 구두장이 연장."], ["S-q-u-double e-r-s-Squeers, noun substantive, a educator of youth.", "위(up), 형용사, 다운(down) 아니고. 에스, 큐, 더블 이, 알, 에스 ― 스퀴어스, 명사, 청소년을 가르치는 사람."], ["Total, all up with Squeers!’", "합계, 스퀴어스는 이제 다 끝장!\""]], [["His running on, in this way, had afforded Ralph an opportunity of recovering his presence of mind, which at once suggested to him the necessity of removing, as far as possible, the schoolmaster’s misgivings, and leading him to believe that his safety and best policy lay in the preservation of a rigid silence.", "이렇게 그가 횡설수설하는 동안, 랄프는 정신을 가다듬을 틈을 얻었어. 그리고 곧 깨달았지. 교장의 불안을 최대한 가라앉히고, 입을 꾹 다물고 침묵을 지키는 게 가장 안전하고 현명한 길이라고 믿게 만들어야 한다는 걸 말이야."]], [["‘I tell you, once again,’ he said, ‘they can’t hurt you.", "\"한 번 더 말하지만,\" 그가 말했어."], ["You shall have an action for false imprisonment, and make a profit of this, yet.", "\"저들은 당신을 어쩌지 못해. 당신은 불법 구금으로 소송을 걸어서, 오히려 이걸로 한몫 챙길 수도 있어."], ["We will devise a story for you that should carry you through twenty times such a trivial scrape as this; and if they want security in a thousand pounds for your reappearance in case you should be called upon, you shall have it.", "이런 시시한 곤경쯤은 스무 번도 더 빠져나가게 해 줄 이야기를 우리가 지어내 줄게. 그리고 혹시 당신이 불려 나오게 될 경우에 대비해 천 파운드짜리 출두 보증을 원한다면, 그것도 마련해 주지."], ["All you have to do is, to keep back the truth.", "당신이 할 일은 그저 진실을 감추는 것뿐이야. 오늘 밤엔 술기운이 좀 있어서 평소만큼 이걸 똑똑히 못 볼지도 몰라."], ["You’re a little fuddled tonight, and may not be able to see this as clearly as you would at another time; but this is what you must do, and you’ll need all your senses about you; for a slip might be awkward.’", "그래도 당신이 해야 할 일은 이거고, 정신을 바짝 차려야 해. 까딱 실수하면 곤란해지니까.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ said Squeers, who had looked cunningly at him, with his head stuck on one side, like an old raven.", "\"오!\" 스퀴어스가 고개를 한쪽으로 비스듬히 기울인 채, 늙은 까마귀처럼 교활하게 그를 쳐다보며 말했어."], ["‘That’s what I’m to do, is it?", "\"그게 내가 할 일이다, 이거지?"], ["Now then, just you hear a word or two from me.", "그럼 이번엔 내 얘기 한두 마디 좀 들어 봐."], ["I an’t a-going to have any stories made for me, and I an’t a-going to stick to any.", "난 누가 나 대신 이야기 지어 주는 거 안 받을 거고, 그런 거에 매달릴 생각도 없어."], ["If I find matters going again me, I shall expect you to take your share, and I’ll take care you do.", "일이 나한테 불리하게 돌아간다 싶으면, 당신도 제 몫을 떠안길 바라겠어. 내가 꼭 그렇게 만들 거고."], ["You never said anything about danger.", "당신은 위험에 대해선 한마디도 안 했어."], ["I never bargained for being brought into such a plight as this, and I don’t mean to take it as quiet as you think.", "난 이런 곤경에 끌려들 거라곤 흥정한 적이 없고, 당신 생각만큼 얌전히 받아들일 마음도 없다고. 내가 당신한테 끌려서 이 일 저 일로 따라온 건, 우리가 어떤 식으로든 한데 얽혀 있었기 때문이야."], ["I let you lead me on, from one thing to another, because we had been mixed up together in a certain sort of a way, and if you had liked to be ill-natured you might perhaps have hurt the business, and if you liked to be good-natured you might throw a good deal in my way.", "당신이 심술을 부릴 마음만 먹으면 아마 내 사업을 망칠 수도 있었을 거고, 좋게 나올 마음을 먹으면 나한테 적잖이 일거리를 던져 줄 수도 있었으니까. 자, 지금 다 잘 풀린다면야 그거 아주 좋고, 나도 불만 없어."], ["Well; if all goes right now, that’s quite correct, and I don’t mind it; but if anything goes wrong, then times are altered, and I shall just say and do whatever I think may serve me most, and take advice from nobody.", "그런데 뭐가 하나라도 틀어지면, 그땐 사정이 달라지는 거야. 그땐 나한테 제일 득이 될 것 같은 말이면 뭐든 하고, 뭐든 하고, 누구 충고도 안 들을 거야."], ["My moral influence with them lads,’ added Mr.", "그 녀석들에 대한 내 도덕적 권위가,\" 스퀴어스 씨가 한층 더 무게를 잡으며 덧붙였어."], ["Squeers, with deeper gravity, ‘is a tottering to its basis.", "\"그 밑바닥부터 흔들리고 있다고. 먹을 게 없어 굶주리는 스퀴어스 부인, 내 딸, 내 아들 와크포드의 모습이 끊임없이 눈앞에 어른거려."], ["The images of Mrs.", "그 앞에서는 다른 생각이란 생각은 죄다 녹아 사라져 버려."], ["Squeers, my daughter, and my son Wackford, all short of vittles, is perpetually before me; every other consideration melts away and vanishes, in front of these; the only number in all arithmetic that I know of, as a husband and a father, is number one, under this here most fatal go!’", "남편이자 아버지로서 내가 아는 산수에서 단 하나뿐인 숫자는, 이 지긋지긋한 마당에 일 번, 바로 나란 말이야!\""]], [["How long Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨가 얼마나 더 일장 연설을 늘어놓았을지, 아니면 그 연설이 얼마나 험악한 말다툼으로 번졌을지는 아무도 몰라."], ["Squeers might have declaimed, or how stormy a discussion his declamation might have led to, nobody knows.", "마침 이 대목에서 마차가 도착하고 그를 데려갈 호송원이 나타나는 바람에 말이 끊겼거든."], ["Being interrupted, at this point, by the arrival of the coach and an attendant who was to bear him company, he perched his hat with great dignity on the top of the handkerchief that bound his head; and, thrusting one hand in his pocket, and taking the attendant’s arm with the other, suffered himself to be led forth.", "그는 머리를 동여맨 손수건 위에다 모자를 위엄 있게 척 얹었어. 그러고는 한 손은 주머니에 찌르고 다른 손으로는 호송원의 팔을 붙든 채, 순순히 끌려 나갔지."]], [["‘As I supposed from his not sending!’ thought Ralph.", "'사람을 안 보낸 걸 보고 짐작했던 대로군!' 랄프는 생각했어."], ["‘This fellow, I plainly see through all his tipsy fooling, has made up his mind to turn upon me.", "'이놈은, 술 취해 떠드는 그 헛소리 사이로 빤히 보이는데, 나한테 등을 돌리기로 작정했어."], ["I am so beset and hemmed in, that they are not only all struck with fear, but, like the beasts in the fable, have their fling at me now, though time was, and no longer ago than yesterday too, when they were all civility and compliance.", "내가 이렇게 사방에서 포위당하고 옴짝달싹 못 하게 몰리니까, 다들 겁에 질렸을 뿐 아니라, 우화 속 짐승들처럼 이제 나한테 한 방씩 먹이려 드는구나. 어제만 해도, 그래 바로 어제까지만 해도 다들 굽실거리며 고분고분했으면서 말이야."], ["But they shall not move me.", "하지만 저들이 날 흔들지는 못해."], ["I’ll not give way.", "난 물러서지 않아."], ["I will not budge one inch!’", "한 치도 꿈쩍 안 할 거다!'"]], [["He went home, and was glad to find his housekeeper complaining of illness, that he might have an excuse for being alone and sending her away to where she lived: which was hard by.", "그는 집으로 갔어. 가정부가 몸이 아프다고 투덜대는 걸 보고는 차라리 다행이다 싶었지."], ["Then, he sat down by the light of a single candle, and began to think, for the first time, on all that had taken place that day.", "혼자 있을 핑계가 생기는 데다, 가까이 있는 그녀의 집으로 보내 버릴 구실이 됐으니까. 그러고는 촛불 하나 켜 놓고 그 앞에 앉아, 그날 일어난 모든 일을 그제야 처음으로 곰곰이 되짚어 보기 시작했어."]], [["He had neither eaten nor drunk since last night, and, in addition to the anxiety of mind he had undergone, had been travelling about, from place to place almost incessantly, for many hours. He felt sick and exhausted, but could taste nothing save a glass of water, and continued to sit with his head upon his hand; not resting nor thinking, but laboriously trying to do both, and feeling that every sense but one of weariness and desolation, was for the time benumbed.", "그는 어젯밤 이후로 아무것도 먹지도 마시지도 않았어. 게다가 마음 졸인 것도 모자라 여러 시간 동안 거의 쉴 새 없이 이곳저곳을 돌아다녔지. 속이 메스껍고 기진맥진했지만, 물 한 잔 말고는 아무것도 입에 댈 수가 없었어. 그는 한 손에 머리를 괸 채 계속 앉아 있었어. 쉬는 것도 아니고 생각하는 것도 아니었지. 그저 둘 다 해 보려고 안간힘을 쓸 뿐이었어. 그리고 지금만큼은 피로와 황량함, 그 한 가지 감각 말고는 모든 감각이 다 마비된 것 같았어."]], [["It was nearly ten o’clock when he heard a knocking at the door, and still sat quiet as before, as if he could not even bring his thoughts to bear upon that.", "거의 열 시가 다 됐을 무렵, 문 두드리는 소리가 들렸어. 그래도 그는 거기에 생각을 모을 수조차 없는 사람처럼 아까처럼 가만히 앉아 있었지."], ["It had been often repeated, and he had, several times, heard a voice outside, saying there was a light in the window (meaning, as he knew, his own candle), before he could rouse himself and go downstairs.", "두드리는 소리는 몇 번이고 되풀이됐어. 그리고 바깥에서 누군가가 창문에 불빛이 보인다고(그가 알기로는 자기가 켜 둔 촛불을 두고 하는 말이었지) 말하는 걸 여러 번 듣고 나서야, 그는 겨우 몸을 일으켜 아래층으로 내려갔어."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"니클비 씨, 당신한테 끔찍한 소식이 있습니다."], ["Nickleby, there is terrible news for you, and I am sent to beg you will come with me directly,’ said a voice he seemed to recognise.", "저는 당신더러 당장 저와 함께 가 주십사 부탁하러 왔어요.\" 어디서 들어 본 듯한 목소리였어."], ["He held his hand above his eyes, and, looking out, saw Tim Linkinwater on the steps.", "그가 눈 위에 손을 얹고 내다보니, 계단에 팀 링킨워터가 서 있었어."]], [["‘Come where?’ demanded Ralph.", "\"어딜 가자는 거요?\" 랄프가 따져 물었어."]], [["‘To our house, where you came this morning.", "\"우리 집으로요. 오늘 아침에 오셨던 곳 말입니다."], ["I have a coach here.’", "마차를 대 놨어요.\""]], [["‘Why should I go there?’ said Ralph.", "\"내가 왜 거길 가야 하지?\" 랄프가 말했어."]], [["‘Don’t ask me why, but pray come with me.’", "\"왜냐고 묻지 말고, 제발 저와 같이 가 주세요.\""]], [["‘Another edition of today!’ returned Ralph, making as though he would shut the door.", "\"오늘 일을 또 한 번 되풀이하자는 거군!\" 랄프가 문을 닫으려는 시늉을 하며 받아쳤어."]], [["‘No, no!’ cried Tim, catching him by the arm and speaking most earnestly; ‘it is only that you may hear something that has occurred: something very dreadful, Mr.", "\"아니, 아니에요!\" 팀이 그의 팔을 붙잡고 더없이 간절하게 외쳤어."], ["Nickleby, which concerns you nearly.", "\"그냥, 그동안 일어난 일을 좀 들으셔야 한다는 것뿐입니다. 아주 무서운 일이에요, 니클비 씨."], ["Do you think I would tell you so or come to you like this, if it were not the case?’", "당신과 깊이 관련된 일이고요. 사실이 아니라면 제가 당신한테 이런 말을 하거나 이렇게 찾아왔겠습니까?\""]], [["Ralph looked at him more closely.", "랄프는 그를 더 찬찬히 들여다봤어."], ["Seeing that he was indeed greatly excited, he faltered, and could not tell what to say or think.", "그가 정말로 몹시 흥분해 있는 걸 보고는, 그도 흠칫 망설여졌어. 무슨 말을 해야 할지, 무슨 생각을 해야 할지 알 수가 없었지."]], [["‘You had better hear this now, than at any other time,’ said Tim; ‘it may have some influence with you.", "\"이건 다른 어느 때보다 지금 들으시는 게 낫습니다.\" 팀이 말했어."], ["For Heaven’s sake come!’", "\"당신한테 어떤 영향을 끼칠지도 모르고요. 제발 좀 와 주세요!\""]], [["Perhaps, at, another time, Ralph’s obstinacy and dislike would have been proof against any appeal from such a quarter, however emphatically urged; but now, after a moment’s hesitation, he went into the hall for his hat, and returning, got into the coach without speaking a word.", "다른 때였다면, 랄프의 고집과 반감이 그런 쪽 사람의 호소 따위는, 아무리 간절하게 졸라 댄들 끄떡없이 막아 냈을지도 몰라. 하지만 이번에는, 잠시 머뭇거리고 나서 그는 모자를 가지러 현관으로 들어갔어. 그러고는 돌아와 한마디 말도 없이 마차에 올라탔어."]], [["Tim well remembered afterwards, and often said, that as Ralph Nickleby went into the house for this purpose, he saw him, by the light of the candle which he had set down upon a chair, reel and stagger like a drunken man.", "팀은 나중에 또렷이 기억했고, 두고두고 이야기했어. 랄프 니클비가 그러려고 집 안으로 들어갈 때, 의자에 내려놓은 촛불 빛에 비친 그가 술 취한 사람처럼 비틀비틀 휘청거리는 걸 봤다고 말이야."], ["He well remembered, too, that when he had placed his foot upon the coach-steps, he turned round and looked upon him with a face so ashy pale and so very wild and vacant that it made him shudder, and for the moment almost afraid to follow.", "그리고 또 똑똑히 기억했지. 랄프가 마차 발판에 발을 올려놓고는 몸을 돌려 자기를 쳐다봤는데, 그 얼굴이 어찌나 잿빛으로 창백하고 어찌나 사납고 텅 비어 있던지, 자기도 모르게 몸서리가 쳐졌고, 순간 따라가기조차 무서웠다고 말이야."], ["People were fond of saying that he had some dark presentiment upon him then, but his emotion might, perhaps, with greater show of reason, be referred to what he had undergone that day.", "사람들은 그때 그에게 어떤 어두운 예감이 들었던 거라고 즐겨 이야기하곤 했어. 하지만 그 감정은, 따지고 보면 그날 그가 겪은 일들 탓이라고 보는 편이 더 그럴듯할지도 몰라."]], [["A profound silence was observed during the ride.", "마차를 타고 가는 동안 깊은 침묵이 흘렀어."], ["Arrived at their place of destination, Ralph followed his conductor into the house, and into a room where the two brothers were.", "목적지에 다다르자, 랄프는 안내인을 따라 집 안으로, 그리고 두 형제가 있는 방으로 들어갔어."], ["He was so astounded, not to say awed, by something of a mute compassion for himself which was visible in their manner and in that of the old clerk, that he could scarcely speak.", "그들의 태도며 늙은 서기의 태도에 자기를 향한 어떤 말 없는 연민 같은 게 어려 있는 걸 보고, 그는 어찌나 어안이 벙벙했던지, 아니 두렵기까지 했던지, 입이 거의 떨어지지 않았어."]], [["Having taken a seat, however, he contrived to say, though in broken words, ‘What--what have you to say to me--more than has been said already?’", "그래도 자리에 앉고 나서, 그는 끊어지는 말투로나마 가까스로 입을 뗐어. \"무슨 ― 나한테 무슨 할 말이 있다는 거요 ― 아까 이미 한 말 말고 더?\""]], [["The room was old and large, very imperfectly lighted, and terminated in a bay window, about which hung some heavy drapery.", "방은 낡고 컸어. 불빛은 영 시원찮았고, 한쪽 끝은 내닫이 창으로 막혀 있었지."], ["Casting his eyes in this direction as he spoke, he thought he made out the dusky figure of a man.", "그 창가에는 묵직한 휘장이 드리워져 있었어. 말을 하며 그쪽으로 눈길을 던지던 그는, 거기서 한 남자의 거뭇한 형체를 알아본 것 같았어."], ["He was confirmed in this impression by seeing that the object moved, as if uneasy under his scrutiny.", "그 형체가 자기 시선을 받아 불편한 듯 움직이는 걸 보고, 그 느낌은 더욱 확실해졌지."]], [["‘Who’s that yonder?’ he said.", "\"저기 저게 누구요?\" 그가 말했어."]], [["‘One who has conveyed to us, within these two hours, the intelligence which caused our sending to you,’ replied brother Charles. ‘Let him be, sir, let him be for the present.’", "\"지난 두 시간 사이에 우리한테 어떤 소식을 전해 준 사람이오. 당신을 부르게 만든 그 소식 말이오.\" 형 찰스가 대답했어. \"그냥 두시오, 선생. 지금은 그냥 두시오.\""]], [["‘More riddles!’ said Ralph, faintly.", "\"또 수수께끼군!\" 랄프가 힘없이 말했어."], ["‘Well, sir?’", "\"그래서요, 선생?\""]], [["In turning his face towards the brothers he was obliged to avert it from the window; but, before either of them could speak, he had looked round again. It was evident that he was rendered restless and uncomfortable by the presence of the unseen person; for he repeated this action several times, and at length, as if in a nervous state which rendered him positively unable to turn away from the place, sat so as to have it opposite him, muttering as an excuse that he could not bear the light.", "두 형제 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌리려면 그 창에서 눈을 떼야만 했어. 하지만 둘 중 누가 입을 열기도 전에, 그는 다시 그쪽을 돌아보고 말았지. 보이지 않는 그 사람이 있다는 게 그를 안절부절못하고 불편하게 만든 게 분명했어. 그는 이 동작을 몇 번이고 되풀이했거든. 그러다 마침내, 도무지 그쪽에서 눈을 떼지 못하게 만드는 신경증에라도 걸린 사람처럼, 아예 그 형체가 자기 정면에 오도록 자리를 잡고 앉았어. 그러고는 불빛이 눈부셔 못 견디겠다는 핑계를 중얼거렸지."]], [["The brothers conferred apart for a short time: their manner showing that they were agitated.", "두 형제는 잠깐 따로 떨어져 의논을 했어. 그 모습으로 보아 둘 다 마음이 흔들리고 있는 게 분명했지."], ["Ralph glanced at them twice or thrice, and ultimately said, with a great effort to recover his self-possession, ‘Now, what is this?", "랄프는 그들을 두세 번 흘끔거리다가, 마침내 안간힘을 써서 침착함을 되찾으며 말했어. \"자, 이게 다 무슨 일이오?"], ["If I am brought from home at this time of night, let it be for something.", "이 야밤에 나를 집에서 끌어냈으면, 그만한 까닭이 있어야지. 나한테 무슨 할 말이 있는 거요?\""], ["What have you got to tell me?’ After a short pause, he added, ‘Is my niece dead?’", "잠깐 뜸을 들이더니 그가 덧붙였어. \"내 조카딸이 죽었소?\""]], [["He had struck upon a key which rendered the task of commencement an easier one.", "그는 이야기를 꺼내기 한결 쉽게 만들어 주는 실마리를 건드린 셈이었어."], ["Brother Charles turned, and said that it was a death of which they had to tell him, but that his niece was well.", "형 찰스가 몸을 돌리며, 자기들이 그에게 전해야 할 게 죽음에 관한 소식인 건 맞지만 그의 조카딸은 무사하다고 말했어."]], [["‘You don’t mean to tell me,’ said Ralph, as his eyes brightened, ‘that her brother’s dead?", "\"설마 그 애 오빠가 죽었다는 말은 아니겠지요.\" 랄프가 눈을 반짝이며 말했어."], ["No, that’s too good.", "\"아니, 그건 너무 잘된 일이라."], ["I’d not believe it, if you told me so.", "그렇게 말해도 난 안 믿어요."], ["It would be too welcome news to be true.’", "사실이라기엔 너무 반가운 소식이거든.\""]], [["‘Shame on you, you hardened and unnatural man,’ cried the other brother, warmly.", "\"부끄러운 줄 아시오, 이 모질고 인정머리 없는 사람아.\" 다른 형제가 발끈해서 외쳤어."], ["‘Prepare yourself for intelligence which, if you have any human feeling in your breast, will make even you shrink and tremble.", "\"마음 단단히 먹으시오. 당신 가슴에 사람다운 감정이 한 조각이라도 있다면, 당신마저 움츠러들고 떨게 만들 소식이니."], ["What if we tell you that a poor unfortunate boy: a child in everything but never having known one of those tender endearments, or one of those lightsome hours which make our childhood a time to be remembered like a happy dream through all our after life: a warm-hearted, harmless, affectionate creature, who never offended you, or did you wrong, but on whom you have vented the malice and hatred you have conceived for your nephew, and whom you have made an instrument for wreaking your bad passions upon him: what if we tell you that, sinking under your persecution, sir, and the misery and ill-usage of a life short in years but long in suffering, this poor creature has gone to tell his sad tale where, for your part in it, you must surely answer?’", "한 가엾고 불운한 소년이 ― 한 번도 그런 다정한 손길이나, 우리네 어린 시절을 평생토록 행복한 꿈처럼 추억하게 해 주는 그런 환한 시간 한 번 누려 보지 못했다는 것만 빼면 여느 아이와 다를 바 없던 아이가 ― 마음 따뜻하고, 해라곤 끼칠 줄 모르고, 정 많은 그 아이가, 당신한테 거슬리는 짓 한 번 한 적 없고 당신을 해코지한 적도 없는데, 당신이 조카한테 품은 악의와 증오를 죄다 그 아이한테 쏟아붓고, 그 아이를 조카에게 당신의 못된 화풀이를 해 대는 도구로 삼았는데 ― 그 아이가, 선생, 당신의 박해와, 햇수로는 짧으나 고통으로는 길었던 삶의 비참함과 학대에 짓눌려, 제 슬픈 사연을 하소연하러 떠나 버렸다고 말한다면 어쩌겠소? 그 사연 속 당신의 몫에 대해, 당신은 반드시 책임져야 할 그곳으로 말이오.\""]], [["‘If you tell me,’ said Ralph; ‘if you tell me that he is dead, I forgive you all else.", "\"그 애가 죽었다고 말해 준다면,\" 랄프가 말했어."], ["If you tell me that he is dead, I am in your debt and bound to you for life.", "\"그 애가 죽었다고 말해 준다면, 나머지는 다 용서하리다. 그 애가 죽었다고 말해 준다면, 나는 당신들한테 빚진 거고 평생 신세를 진 거요."], ["He is!", "죽었군!"], ["I see it in your faces.", "당신들 얼굴에 다 쓰여 있어."], ["Who triumphs now?", "이제 누가 이긴 거지?"], ["Is this your dreadful news; this your terrible intelligence?", "이게 당신들이 말하던 그 무서운 소식이오? 이게 그 끔찍한 소식이라고?"], ["You see how it moves me.", "거 봐요, 이게 날 얼마나 움직이는지."], ["You did well to send.", "사람 보내길 잘했소."], ["I would have travelled a hundred miles afoot, through mud, mire, and darkness, to hear this news just at this time.’", "바로 이런 때에 이 소식을 들을 수만 있다면, 나는 진흙탕이며 수렁이며 어둠을 헤치고 백 마일이라도 걸어왔을 거요.\""]], [["Even then, moved as he was by this savage joy, Ralph could see in the faces of the two brothers, mingling with their look of disgust and horror, something of that indefinable compassion for himself which he had noticed before.", "그렇게 야만스러운 기쁨에 들떠 있으면서도, 랄프는 두 형제의 얼굴에서 볼 수 있었어. 혐오와 공포의 표정에 뒤섞여, 아까 봤던 그 자기를 향한 무어라 말할 수 없는 연민 같은 게 어려 있는 걸 말이야."]], [["‘And HE brought you the intelligence, did he?’ said Ralph, pointing with his finger towards the recess already mentioned; ‘and sat there, no doubt, to see me prostrated and overwhelmed by it! Ha, ha, ha! But I tell him that I’ll be a sharp thorn in his side for many a long day to come; and I tell you two, again, that you don’t know him yet; and that you’ll rue the day you took compassion on the vagabond.’", "\"그래서 저 작자가 당신들한테 그 소식을 가져왔다, 이거지?\" 랄프가 아까 말한 그 움푹한 자리를 손가락으로 가리키며 말했어. \"그러고는 거기 앉아서, 그 소식에 내가 무너지고 짓눌리는 꼴을 구경할 속셈이었겠지! 하, 하, 하! 하지만 저자한테 말해 두지. 나는 앞으로 두고두고 오랜 세월 저자 옆구리에 박힌 날카로운 가시가 돼 줄 거라고. 그리고 당신들 둘한테도 다시 말하는데, 당신들은 아직 저자를 몰라. 저 부랑자한테 동정을 베푼 그날을 두고두고 후회하게 될 거야.\""]], [["‘You take me for your nephew,’ said a hollow voice; ‘it would be better for you, and for me too, if I were he indeed.’", "\"당신은 나를 당신 조카로 착각하는군.\" 텅 빈 목소리가 말했어. \"내가 정말 그였다면, 당신한테도, 나한테도 더 나았을 텐데.\""]], [["The figure that he had seen so dimly, rose, and came slowly down.", "그가 그토록 어렴풋이 봤던 그 형체가 자리에서 일어나, 천천히 다가왔어."], ["He started back, for he found that he confronted--not Nicholas, as he had supposed, but Brooker.", "그는 흠칫 뒤로 물러섰어. 그가 마주한 건 짐작했던 니콜라스가 아니라 브루커였거든."]], [["Ralph had no reason, that he knew, to fear this man; he had never feared him before; but the pallor which had been observed in his face when he issued forth that night, came upon him again. He was seen to tremble, and his voice changed as he said, keeping his eyes upon him,", "랄프는 자기가 아는 한, 이 남자를 두려워할 이유가 없었어. 전에는 한 번도 두려워한 적이 없었지. 그런데 그날 밤 집을 나설 때 그의 얼굴에 어려 있던 그 창백함이 다시 그를 덮쳤어. 그가 떠는 게 보였어. 그리고 그자에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 달라진 목소리로 말했어."]], [["‘What does this fellow here?", "\"이 작자가 여기서 뭘 하는 거요?"], ["Do you know he is a convict, a felon, a common thief?’", "이자가 전과자에, 중죄인에, 흔해 빠진 도둑놈이라는 걸 알기나 하시오?\""]], [["‘Hear what he has to tell you. Oh, Mr.", "\"이자가 당신한테 할 말을 들어 보시오."], ["Nickleby, hear what he has to tell you, be he what he may!’ cried the brothers, with such emphatic earnestness, that Ralph turned to them in wonder.", "오, 니클비 씨, 이자가 누구든 간에, 그가 할 말을 좀 들어 보시오!\" 두 형제가 어찌나 힘주어 간절하게 외쳤던지, 랄프는 의아해하며 그들 쪽으로 돌아섰어."], ["They pointed to Brooker.", "그들이 브루커를 가리켰어."], ["Ralph again gazed at him: as it seemed mechanically.", "랄프는 다시 그를 응시했어. 거의 기계처럼 말이야."]], [["‘That boy,’ said the man, ‘that these gentlemen have been talking of--’", "\"저 소년은,\" 그 남자가 말했어. \"여기 두 분 신사께서 이야기하시던 저 소년은 ―\""]], [["‘That boy,’ repeated Ralph, looking vacantly at him.", "\"저 소년은,\" 랄프가 멍하니 그를 바라보며 따라 말했어."]], [["‘Whom I saw, stretched dead and cold upon his bed, and who is now in his grave--’", "\"제가 침대에 차갑게 식은 채 뻗어 죽어 있는 걸 보았고, 지금은 무덤 속에 있는 그 아이는 ―\""]], [["‘Who is now in his grave,’ echoed Ralph, like one who talks in his sleep.", "\"지금은 무덤 속에 있는,\" 랄프가 잠꼬대하는 사람처럼 되뇌었어."]], [["The man raised his eyes, and clasped his hands solemnly together:", "그 남자가 눈을 치켜뜨고, 두 손을 엄숙하게 맞잡았어."]], [["‘--Was your only son, so help me God in heaven!’", "\"― 당신의 하나뿐인 아들이었소, 하늘에 계신 하느님께 맹세코!\""]], [["In the midst of a dead silence, Ralph sat down, pressing his two hands upon his temples.", "죽음 같은 침묵 한가운데서, 랄프는 두 손으로 관자놀이를 짓누르며 주저앉았어."], ["He removed them, after a minute, and never was there seen, part of a living man undisfigured by any wound, such a ghastly face as he then disclosed.", "잠시 후 그가 손을 떼었는데, 어디 한 군데 상처로 일그러진 데도 없는 산 사람의 얼굴치고, 그때 그가 드러낸 것만큼 송장 같은 얼굴은 일찍이 본 적이 없었어."], ["He looked at Brooker, who was by this time standing at a short distance from him; but did not say one word, or make the slightest sound or gesture.", "그는 어느새 자기한테서 조금 떨어져 서 있던 브루커를 바라봤어. 하지만 한마디 말도, 조그만 소리도, 손짓 하나도 하지 않았어."]], [["‘Gentlemen,’ said the man, ‘I offer no excuses for myself.", "\"여러분,\" 그 남자가 말했어."], ["I am long past that.", "\"저는 제 변명 따위는 하지 않겠습니다."], ["If, in telling you how this has happened, I tell you that I was harshly used, and perhaps driven out of my real nature, I do it only as a necessary part of my story, and not to shield myself.", "그러기엔 너무 멀리 와 버렸으니까요. 이 일이 어쩌다 이렇게 됐는지 말씀드리면서, 제가 모질게 부려졌고 어쩌면 제 본래 성품에서 벗어나도록 내몰렸다고 한다면, 그건 그저 제 이야기에 빠질 수 없는 한 부분이라서일 뿐, 저를 감싸려는 게 아닙니다."], ["I am a guilty man.’", "저는 죄지은 사람이에요.\""]], [["He stopped, as if to recollect, and looking away from Ralph, and addressing himself to the brothers, proceeded in a subdued and humble tone:", "그가 무언가를 떠올리려는 듯 말을 멈추었어. 그리고 랄프에게서 눈길을 돌려 두 형제를 향해, 가라앉고 겸허한 어조로 이야기를 이어 갔어."]], [["‘Among those who once had dealings with this man, gentlemen--that’s from twenty to five-and-twenty years ago--there was one: a rough fox-hunting, hard-drinking gentleman, who had run through his own fortune, and wanted to squander away that of his sister: they were both orphans, and she lived with him and managed his house.", "\"한때 이 사람과 거래가 있던 이들 중에, 여러분 ― 한 이십 년, 아니 이십오 년쯤 전 일입니다 ― 한 사람이 있었습니다. 여우 사냥을 즐기고 술을 진탕 마셔 대는 거친 신사였는데, 제 재산은 이미 다 날려 먹고, 누이동생 재산까지 탕진해 버리려고 했죠."], ["I don’t know whether it was, originally, to back his influence and try to over-persuade the young woman or not, but he,’ pointing, to Ralph, ‘used to go down to the house in Leicestershire pretty often, and stop there many days at a time.", "둘 다 고아였고, 누이는 그 오빠랑 살면서 집안 살림을 맡고 있었어요. 본래 그 오빠의 영향력을 등에 업고 그 처녀를 설득해 넘기려던 거였는지 아닌지는 모르겠습니다만, 이 사람이,\""], ["They had had a great many dealings together, and he may have gone on some of those, or to patch up his client’s affairs, which were in a ruinous state; of course he went for profit.", "그가 랄프를 가리켰어. \"레스터셔에 있는 그 집에 꽤 자주 내려가서, 한 번에 며칠씩 묵곤 했어요."], ["The gentlewoman was not a girl, but she was, I have heard say, handsome, and entitled to a pretty large property.", "둘은 같이 한 거래가 아주 많았으니, 그중 어떤 일로 갔을 수도 있고, 아니면 폐허나 다름없던 그 의뢰인의 일을 수습하러 갔을 수도 있죠."], ["In course of time, he married her.", "물론 가는 거야 잇속을 챙기려는 거였고요."], ["The same love of gain which led him to contract this marriage, led to its being kept strictly private; for a clause in her father’s will declared that if she married without her brother’s consent, the property, in which she had only some life interest while she remained single, should pass away altogether to another branch of the family.", "그 부인은 처녀라기엔 나이가 좀 있었지만, 제가 듣기로는 미인이었고, 꽤 큰 재산을 물려받을 자격이 있었답니다. 시간이 흐르면서, 이 사람이 그 여자와 결혼을 했어요."], ["The brother would give no consent that the sister didn’t buy, and pay for handsomely; Mr.", "그를 이 결혼으로 이끈 그 잇속에 대한 욕심이, 결혼을 철저히 비밀에 부치게 만든 원인이기도 했죠."], ["Nickleby would consent to no such sacrifice; and so they went on, keeping their marriage secret, and waiting for him to break his neck or die of a fever.", "그 여자 아버지의 유언장에 이런 조항이 있었거든요. 만약 딸이 오빠의 동의 없이 결혼하면, 그 재산은 ― 딸이 결혼 안 하고 있는 동안에만 평생 수익권 정도를 갖던 그 재산은 ― 통째로 집안의 다른 갈래로 넘어간다는 거였어요."], ["He did neither, and meanwhile the result of this private marriage was a son.", "오빠는 누이가 두둑이 값을 치르고 사지 않는 한 절대 동의를 안 해 줬고, 니클비 씨는 그런 희생은 죽어도 못 하겠다고 했죠."], ["The child was put out to nurse, a long way off; his mother never saw him but once or twice, and then by stealth; and his father--so eagerly did he thirst after the money which seemed to come almost within his grasp now, for his brother-in-law was very ill, and breaking more and more every day--never went near him, to avoid raising any suspicion.", "그래서 둘은 결혼을 비밀로 한 채, 그 오빠가 목이 부러져 죽거나 열병으로 죽기만을 기다리며 지냈어요. 그런데 오빠는 둘 다 안 죽었고, 그러는 사이에 이 비밀 결혼의 결실로 아들이 하나 태어났습니다."], ["The brother lingered on; Mr.", "그 아이는 멀리 떨어진 곳으로 보내져 유모 손에 맡겨졌어요."], ["Nickleby’s wife constantly urged him to avow their marriage; he peremptorily refused.", "어머니는 그 아이를 한두 번밖에 못 봤고, 그것도 몰래였죠. 그리고 아버지는 ― 이제 거의 손아귀에 들어오는 듯한 그 돈을 어찌나 갈급하게 탐냈던지, 처남이 아주 위중해서 날이 갈수록 점점 더 쇠약해지고 있었거든요 ― 행여 의심이라도 살까 봐 그 아이 근처엔 얼씬도 안 했어요."], ["She remained alone in a dull country house: seeing little or no company but riotous, drunken sportsmen.", "오빠는 질질 명을 이어 갔고, 니클비 씨의 아내는 자기들 결혼을 떳떳이 밝히자고 줄곧 졸랐지만, 그는 단호히 거절했어요. 그 여자는 적막한 시골집에 홀로 남겨져, 흥청대며 술 마시는 사냥꾼들 말고는 사람 구경을 거의 못 하고 지냈어요."], ["He lived in London and clung to his business.", "그는 런던에 살면서 제 사업에만 매달렸죠."], ["Angry quarrels and recriminations took place, and when they had been married nearly seven years, and were within a few weeks of the time when the brother’s death would have adjusted all, she eloped with a younger man, and left him.’", "성난 말다툼과 서로 헐뜯는 일이 벌어졌고, 결혼한 지 칠 년이 다 되어 ― 오빠가 죽어서 모든 게 정리될 날을 몇 주 앞두고 ― 그 여자는 더 젊은 남자와 눈이 맞아 달아나, 그를 버리고 떠났습니다.\""]], [["Here he paused, but Ralph did not stir, and the brothers signed to him to proceed.", "여기서 그가 말을 멈췄어. 하지만 랄프는 꼼짝도 안 했지. 그러자 두 형제가 그에게 계속하라고 손짓했어."]], [["‘It was then that I became acquainted with these circumstances from his own lips.", "\"이런 사정들을 제가 그 사람 입으로 직접 알게 된 게 바로 그때였습니다. 그땐 이미 비밀도 아니었어요."], ["They were no secrets then; for the brother, and others, knew them; but they were communicated to me, not on this account, but because I was wanted.", "오빠며 다른 사람들도 다 알고 있었으니까요. 하지만 저한테 그 얘기를 한 건 그래서가 아니라, 제가 필요했기 때문이었죠."], ["He followed the fugitives.", "그는 달아난 두 사람을 뒤쫓았어요."], ["Some said to make money of his wife’s shame, but, I believe, to take some violent revenge, for that was as much his character as the other; perhaps more.", "누구는 아내의 수치를 빌미로 돈을 뜯어내려는 거라 했지만, 제 생각엔 무슨 끔찍한 복수를 하려던 거였어요. 그게 다른 쪽 못지않게, 아니 어쩌면 그 이상으로 그 사람 본성이었으니까요."], ["He didn’t find them, and she died not long after.", "그는 두 사람을 못 찾았고, 그 여자는 얼마 안 가 죽었습니다."], ["I don’t know whether he began to think he might like the child, or whether he wished to make sure that it should never fall into its mother’s hands; but, before he went, he intrusted me with the charge of bringing it home.", "그가 그 아이를 좋아하게 될지도 모른다고 생각하기 시작한 건지, 아니면 그 아이가 절대 제 어미 손에 들어가지 못하게 하고 싶었던 건지는 모르겠어요. 다만 떠나기 전에, 그는 그 아이를 집으로 데려오는 일을 저한테 맡겼습니다."], ["And I did so.’", "그리고 저는 그렇게 했고요.\""]], [["He went on, from this point, in a still more humble tone, and spoke in a very low voice; pointing to Ralph as he resumed.", "그는 이 대목에서부터 한층 더 겸허한 어조로, 아주 낮은 목소리로 말을 이어 갔어. 다시 이야기를 시작하면서 랄프를 가리켰지."]], [["‘He had used me ill--cruelly--I reminded him in what, not long ago when I met him in the street--and I hated him.", "\"그는 저를 모질게 ― 잔인하게 ― 부려 먹었어요. 얼마 전 길에서 그를 만났을 때 어떻게 그랬는지 일깨워 줬죠."], ["I brought the child home to his own house, and lodged him in the front garret.", "그래서 저는 그를 미워했습니다. 저는 그 아이를 그 사람 집으로 데려와, 앞쪽 다락방에 들였어요."], ["Neglect had made him very sickly, and I was obliged to call in a doctor, who said he must be removed for change of air, or he would die.", "돌봄을 못 받아 아이는 몹시 병약했고, 저는 어쩔 수 없이 의사를 불렀습니다. 의사는 공기 좋은 데로 옮기지 않으면 아이가 죽을 거라고 했어요."], ["I think that first put it in my head. I did it then.", "그 말이 제 머릿속에 처음으로 그 생각을 심어 준 것 같아요."], ["He was gone six weeks, and when he came back, I told him--with every circumstance well planned and proved; nobody could have suspected me--that the child was dead and buried.", "저는 그때 그 짓을 저질렀습니다. 그가 여섯 주 동안 집을 비웠다가 돌아왔을 때, 저는 그한테 말했어요 ― 정황을 빈틈없이 꾸미고 짜 맞춰서, 아무도 저를 의심할 수 없게 ― 그 아이가 죽어서 묻혔다고요."], ["He might have been disappointed in some intention he had formed, or he might have had some natural affection, but he WAS grieved at THAT, and I was confirmed in my design of opening up the secret one day, and making it a means of getting money from him.", "그가 무슨 속셈을 품었다가 어그러진 건지, 아니면 천륜의 정 같은 게 있었던 건지, 어쨌든 그는 그 일에 슬퍼했어요. 그래서 저는 언젠가 이 비밀을 까발려서 그한테서 돈을 뜯어내는 수단으로 삼겠다는 결심을 굳혔죠."], ["I had heard, like most other men, of Yorkshire schools.", "저도 다른 사람들 대부분처럼 요크셔 학교 이야기를 들어 본 적이 있었어요."], ["I took the child to one kept by a man named Squeers, and left it there.", "저는 그 아이를 스퀴어스라는 사람이 운영하는 학교에 데려가서 거기 맡겼습니다. 아이한테 스마이크라는 이름을 지어 줬고요."], ["I gave him the name of Smike.", "해마다, 육 년 동안 그 아이 몫으로 일 년에 이십 파운드씩 냈어요."], ["Year by year, I paid twenty pounds a-year for him for six years; never breathing the secret all the time; for I had left his father’s service after more hard usage, and quarrelled with him again.", "그러는 내내 비밀은 한마디도 입 밖에 안 냈죠. 또 한 번 모진 대접을 받고 나서 그의 일자리를 그만두었고, 그하고 또 다투었거든요."], ["I was sent away from this country.", "저는 이 나라에서 쫓겨났어요. 거의 팔 년을 떠나 있었습니다."], ["I have been away nearly eight years.", "다시 고국에 돌아오자마자, 저는 요크셔로 내려가서 저녁 무렵 그 마을에 몰래 숨어들어 학교 아이들에 대해 알아봤어요."], ["Directly I came home again, I travelled down into Yorkshire, and, skulking in the village of an evening-time, made inquiries about the boys at the school, and found that this one, whom I had placed there, had run away with a young man bearing the name of his own father.", "그러다 알게 됐죠. 제가 거기 맡겼던 그 아이가, 제 친아버지와 같은 이름을 가진 젊은이와 함께 달아나 버렸다는 걸요."], ["I sought his father out in London, and hinting at what I could tell him, tried for a little money to support life; but he repulsed me with threats.", "저는 런던에서 그 아버지를 찾아내서, 제가 그한테 해 줄 수 있는 이야기가 있다는 걸 넌지시 비치며 입에 풀칠할 약간의 돈을 얻어 보려 했어요. 하지만 그는 협박을 하며 저를 내쫓았죠."], ["I then found out his clerk, and, going on from little to little, and showing him that there were good reasons for communicating with me, learnt what was going on; and it was I who told him that the boy was no son of the man who claimed to be his father.", "그래서 저는 그의 서기를 찾아냈고, 조금씩 조금씩 다가가며, 저와 이야기를 나눌 만한 그럴듯한 까닭이 있다는 걸 그에게 보여 주면서, 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지를 알아냈습니다. 그 아이가 제 아버지라고 주장하는 그 사람의 아들이 아니라고 그에게 말해 준 게 바로 저였어요."], ["All this time I had never seen the boy.", "이 모든 동안 저는 그 아이를 한 번도 본 적이 없었습니다."], ["At length, I heard from this same source that he was very ill, and where he was.", "마침내 저는 바로 그 서기한테서, 그 아이가 몹시 아프다는 것과 어디 있는지를 들었어요. 저는 그곳으로 내려갔습니다."], ["I travelled down there, that I might recall myself, if possible, to his recollection and confirm my story.", "할 수만 있다면 그 아이 기억 속에 저를 다시 떠올리게 해서 제 이야기를 뒷받침하려고요. 저는 느닷없이 그 아이 앞에 나타났어요."], ["I came upon him unexpectedly; but before I could speak he knew me--he had good cause to remember me, poor lad!--and I would have sworn to him if I had met him in the Indies.", "그런데 제가 미처 말을 꺼내기도 전에 아이가 저를 알아봤죠 ― 그 가엾은 것이 저를 기억할 만한 사연이 충분했으니까요! ― 그리고 저도, 인도에서 그 아이를 만났대도 그 아이라고 맹세할 수 있었을 겁니다."], ["I knew the piteous face I had seen in the little child.", "그 어린아이한테서 봤던 그 애처로운 얼굴을 알아본 거죠."], ["After a few days’ indecision, I applied to the young gentleman in whose care he was, and I found that he was dead.", "며칠을 망설이다가, 저는 그 아이를 돌보던 젊은 신사를 찾아갔어요. 그리고 그 아이가 죽었다는 걸 알게 됐습니다."], ["He knows how quickly he recognised me again, how often he had described me and my leaving him at the school, and how he told him of a garret he recollected: which is the one I have spoken of, and in his father’s house to this day.", "그분이 알아요. 그 아이가 얼마나 금방 저를 다시 알아봤는지, 저를, 그리고 제가 자기를 그 학교에 두고 떠난 일을 얼마나 자주 이야기했는지, 그리고 자기가 기억하는 다락방 이야기를 어떻게 그분한테 했는지 ― 그게 바로 제가 말씀드린 그 다락방이고, 지금 이날까지도 그 아이 아버지 집에 그대로 있는 곳입니다."], ["This is my story.", "이게 제 이야기예요. 저는 저 교장과 얼굴을 맞대게 해 달라고 요구합니다."], ["I demand to be brought face to face with the schoolmaster, and put to any possible proof of any part of it, and I will show that it’s too true, and that I have this guilt upon my soul.’", "이 이야기의 어느 부분이든 가능한 모든 증거를 들이대 보세요. 이게 너무나 사실이라는 것과, 제 영혼에 이 죄가 얹혀 있다는 걸 제가 보여 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Unhappy man!’ said the brothers.", "\"가엾은 사람 같으니!\" 형제들이 말했어."], ["‘What reparation can you make for this?’", "\"이 일을 어떻게 보상할 셈이오?\""]], [["‘None, gentlemen, none!", "\"못 합니다, 여러분, 못 해요!"], ["I have none to make, and nothing to hope now.", "저는 보상할 길도 없고, 이제 바랄 것도 없습니다."], ["I am old in years, and older still in misery and care.", "저는 나이도 늙었고, 비참함과 시름으로는 더 늙어 버렸어요."], ["This confession can bring nothing upon me but new suffering and punishment; but I make it, and will abide by it whatever comes.", "이 고백이 제게 가져다줄 거라곤 새로운 고통과 벌뿐입니다. 하지만 저는 고백하고, 무슨 일이 닥치든 거기에 따르겠습니다."], ["I have been made the instrument of working out this dreadful retribution upon the head of a man who, in the hot pursuit of his bad ends, has persecuted and hunted down his own child to death.", "저는 한 사람의 머리 위로 이 끔찍한 응보가 떨어지게 만드는 도구로 쓰였어요. 못된 목적을 그악스럽게 좇다가 제 친자식을 박해하고 몰아세워 죽음에 이르게 한 사람의 머리 위로 말이죠."], ["It must descend upon me too.", "그건 저한테도 떨어질 게 분명합니다."], ["I know it must fall.", "떨어질 거라는 걸 압니다."], ["My reparation comes too late; and, neither in this world nor in the next, can I have hope again!’", "제 보상은 너무 늦게 왔어요. 그리고 이 세상에서도, 저세상에서도, 저는 다시는 희망을 가질 수 없습니다!\""]], [["He had hardly spoken, when the lamp, which stood upon the table close to where Ralph was seated, and which was the only one in the room, was thrown to the ground, and left them in darkness. There was some trifling confusion in obtaining another light; the interval was a mere nothing; but when the light appeared, Ralph Nickleby was gone.", "그가 말을 채 끝내기도 전에, 랄프가 앉아 있던 자리 바로 옆 탁자 위에 놓인, 그 방의 단 하나뿐인 등불이 바닥으로 내동댕이쳐졌어. 그래서 그들은 어둠 속에 남겨졌지. 다른 불을 켜느라 약간의 소란이 있었어. 그 짬은 정말 아무것도 아닐 만큼 짧았지. 그런데 불이 다시 켜졌을 때, 랄프 니클비는 사라지고 없었어."]], [["The good brothers and Tim Linkinwater occupied some time in discussing the probability of his return; and, when it became apparent that he would not come back, they hesitated whether or no to send after him.", "마음씨 좋은 두 형제와 팀 링킨워터는 그가 돌아올지 어떨지를 두고 한동안 이야기를 나눴어. 그러다 그가 돌아오지 않으리라는 게 분명해지자, 그를 뒤쫓아 사람을 보낼지 말지 망설였지."], ["At length, remembering how strangely and silently he had sat in one immovable position during the interview, and thinking he might possibly be ill, they determined, although it was now very late, to send to his house on some pretence.", "마침내, 그가 그 면담 내내 한 자세로 꼼짝도 않고 묘하게 입을 다문 채 앉아 있던 걸 떠올리고는, 어쩌면 그가 병이 났을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었어. 그래서 이제 시간이 아주 늦긴 했지만, 무슨 핑계를 대서라도 그의 집에 사람을 보내기로 마음먹었어."], ["Finding an excuse in the presence of Brooker, whom they knew not how to dispose of without consulting his wishes, they concluded to act upon this resolution before going to bed.", "브루커를 어찌 처리해야 할지, 그의 뜻을 물어보지 않고는 알 수가 없어서 그걸 핑계로 삼아, 잠자리에 들기 전에 이 결정을 실행에 옮기기로 했어."]], [["# CHAPTER 61", "# CHAPTER 61"]], [["Wherein Nicholas and his Sister forfeit the good Opinion of all worldly and prudent People\nOn the next morning after Brooker’s disclosure had been made, Nicholas returned home.", "브루커가 비밀을 털어놓은 다음 날 아침, 니콜라스는 집으로 돌아왔어. 그와 집에 남아 있던 사람들의 만남은 양쪽 모두에게 깊은 감정이 북받쳐 오르는 것이었지."], ["The meeting between him and those whom he had left there was not without strong emotion on both sides; for they had been informed by his letters of what had occurred: and, besides that his griefs were theirs, they mourned with him the death of one whose forlorn and helpless state had first established a claim upon their compassion, and whose truth of heart and grateful earnest nature had, every day, endeared him to them more and more.", "그들은 니콜라스의 편지를 통해 무슨 일이 있었는지 이미 알고 있었거든. 게다가 니콜라스의 슬픔이 곧 그들의 슬픔이기도 했고, 처음엔 외롭고 의지할 데 없는 처지 때문에 그들의 동정을 샀지만 날이 갈수록 진실한 마음과 고마워할 줄 아는 성실한 천성으로 점점 더 사랑받던 한 사람의 죽음을, 그들도 니콜라스와 함께 슬퍼했지."]], [["‘I am sure,’ said Mrs. Nickleby, wiping her eyes, and sobbing bitterly, ‘I have lost the best, the most zealous, and most attentive creature that has ever been a companion to me in my life--putting you, my dear Nicholas, and Kate, and your poor papa, and that well-behaved nurse who ran away with the linen and the twelve small forks, out of the question, of course. Of all the tractable, equal-tempered, attached, and faithful beings that ever lived, I believe he was the most so. To look round upon the garden, now, that he took so much pride in, or to go into his room and see it filled with so many of those little contrivances for our comfort that he was so fond of making, and made so well, and so little thought he would leave unfinished--I can’t bear it, I cannot really. Ah! This is a great trial to me, a great trial. It will be comfort to you, my dear Nicholas, to the end of your life, to recollect how kind and good you always were to him--so it will be to me, to think what excellent terms we were always upon, and how fond he always was of me, poor fellow! It was very natural you should have been attached to him, my dear--very--and of course you were, and are very much cut up by this. I am sure it’s only necessary to look at you and see how changed you are, to see that; but nobody knows what my feelings are--nobody can--it’s quite impossible!’", "\"정말이지,\" 니클비 부인이 눈물을 훔치며 서럽게 흐느꼈어. \"나는 평생 내 곁에 있어 준 동무들 가운데 가장 착하고, 가장 열성적이고, 가장 세심한 아이를 잃었어. 물론 우리 니콜라스, 케이트, 그리고 너희 불쌍한 아빠랑, 빨래랑 작은 포크 열두 개를 가지고 달아난 그 얌전했던 보모는 빼고 말이야. 세상에 살았던 그 모든 순한 아이들, 한결같은 성품에 정 많고 충실한 아이들 가운데서도 그 애가 단연 으뜸이었다고 믿어. 그 애가 그렇게 자랑스러워하던 정원을 지금 둘러보거나, 그 애 방에 들어가서 우리 편하라고 만든 그 자잘한 물건들이 가득한 걸 보면 말이야. 그 애가 그렇게 만들기를 좋아했고, 그렇게 잘 만들었고, 미처 다 못 끝낸 채 떠날 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐을 그 물건들 말이야. 나는 도무지 견딜 수가 없어, 정말로 못 견디겠어. 아! 이건 나한테 큰 시련이야, 정말 큰 시련이지. 우리 니콜라스, 네가 그 애한테 늘 얼마나 다정하고 착하게 대해 줬는지 떠올리면 죽는 날까지 위로가 될 거야. 나도 그 애랑 늘 얼마나 좋은 사이였는지, 그 애가 나를 얼마나 따랐는지 생각하면 그게 위로가 되거든, 가엾은 것! 네가 그 애한테 정이 들었던 건 아주 당연한 일이야, 얘야. 아주 그렇지. 물론 너는 정이 들었고, 이번 일로 무척 마음이 무너졌겠지. 너를 보고 네가 얼마나 변했는지 보면 그건 단번에 알 수 있어. 그래도 내 심정이 어떤지는 아무도 몰라. 아무도 알 수가 없지. 도무지 불가능해!\""]], [["While Mrs.", "니클비 부인이 더없이 진심으로, 늘 자기를 맨 앞에 세우는 그 특유의 방식대로 슬픔을 쏟아 내는 동안, 그런 감정에 잠긴 사람은 부인만이 아니었어."], ["Nickleby, with the utmost sincerity, gave vent to her sorrows after her own peculiar fashion of considering herself foremost, she was not the only one who indulged such feelings.", "케이트는 남을 먼저 헤아릴 때면 으레 자기 자신을 잊는 데 익숙했지만, 이번만큼은 슬픔을 누를 수가 없었지. 매들린도 케이트 못지않게 마음이 흔들렸어."], ["Kate, although well accustomed to forget herself when others were to be considered, could not repress her grief; Madeline was scarcely less moved than she; and poor, hearty, honest little Miss La Creevy, who had come upon one of her visits while Nicholas was away, and had done nothing, since the sad news arrived, but console and cheer them all, no sooner beheld him coming in at the door, than she sat herself down upon the stairs, and bursting into a flood of tears, refused for a long time to be comforted.", "그리고 마음씨 따뜻하고 정직한 가엾은 라크리비 아주머니는 니콜라스가 없는 사이에 들렀다가, 슬픈 소식이 전해진 뒤로는 모두를 위로하고 다독이는 일밖에 한 게 없었는데, 니콜라스가 문으로 들어오는 걸 보자마자 계단에 털썩 주저앉아 눈물을 펑펑 쏟으며 한참을 달래도 그치지 않았어."]], [["‘It hurts me so,’ cried the poor body, ‘to see him come back alone.", "\"너무 마음이 아파서요,\" 가엾은 아주머니가 울먹였어."], ["I can’t help thinking what he must have suffered himself.", "\"혼자 돌아오는 걸 보니까 말이에요. 저 사람이 얼마나 힘들었을까 하는 생각을 떨칠 수가 없어요."], ["I wouldn’t mind so much if he gave way a little more; but he bears it so manfully.’", "차라리 좀 더 무너지면 이렇게까지 마음이 안 아플 텐데. 저렇게 꿋꿋하게 견디니까 더 그래요.\""]], [["‘Why, so I should,’ said Nicholas, ‘should I not?’", "\"아니, 그래야지요,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"안 그렇겠어요?\""]], [["‘Yes, yes,’ replied the little woman, ‘and bless you for a good creature! but this does seem at first to a simple soul like me--I know it’s wrong to say so, and I shall be sorry for it presently--this does seem such a poor reward for all you have done.’", "\"맞아요, 맞아,\" 자그마한 아주머니가 대답했어. \"그 착한 마음씨에 복 받을 거예요! 하지만 나처럼 단순한 사람한테는 처음엔 이게 꼭 이런 것 같단 말이에요. 이런 말 하면 안 되는 거 알아요, 곧 후회할 거고요. 그래도 이건 당신이 한 그 모든 일에 비하면 너무 보잘것없는 보답 같단 말이에요.\""]], [["‘Nay,’ said Nicholas gently, ‘what better reward could I have, than the knowledge that his last days were peaceful and happy, and the recollection that I was his constant companion, and was not prevented, as I might have been by a hundred circumstances, from being beside him?’", "\"아니에요,\" 니콜라스가 부드럽게 말했어. \"그 애의 마지막 나날이 평화롭고 행복했다는 걸 아는 것, 그리고 내가 늘 그 애 곁을 지켰고, 백 가지 사정 때문에 못 그럴 수도 있었는데 그 애 곁에 있을 수 있었다는 걸 기억하는 것, 그보다 더 좋은 보답이 어디 있겠어요?\""]], [["‘To be sure,’ sobbed Miss La Creevy; ‘it’s very true, and I’m an ungrateful, impious, wicked little fool, I know.’", "\"그렇고말고요,\" 라크리비 아주머니가 흐느꼈어. \"정말 맞는 말이에요. 나야말로 은혜도 모르고 불경하고 못된 바보 늙은이지요, 알아요.\""]], [["With that, the good soul fell to crying afresh, and, endeavouring to recover herself, tried to laugh.", "그러더니 마음씨 착한 아주머니는 다시 울음을 터뜨렸고, 정신을 차려 보려고 웃으려 애를 썼어."], ["The laugh and the cry, meeting each other thus abruptly, had a struggle for the mastery; the result was, that it was a drawn battle, and Miss La Creevy went into hysterics.", "웃음과 울음이 그렇게 갑작스레 맞부딪쳐서 서로 주도권을 다투었지. 그 결과는 무승부였고, 라크리비 아주머니는 그만 히스테리를 일으키고 말았어."]], [["Waiting until they were all tolerably quiet and composed again, Nicholas, who stood in need of some rest after his long journey, retired to his own room, and throwing himself, dressed as he was, upon the bed, fell into a sound sleep.", "다들 어지간히 잠잠해지고 마음을 추스를 때까지 기다린 뒤, 긴 여행 끝에 쉴 필요가 있었던 니콜라스는 자기 방으로 물러갔어. 그러고는 옷을 입은 채 그대로 침대에 몸을 던지고는 깊은 잠에 빠졌지."], ["When he awoke, he found Kate sitting by his bedside, who, seeing that he had opened his eyes, stooped down to kiss him.", "잠에서 깨어 보니 케이트가 침대맡에 앉아 있었어. 케이트는 니콜라스가 눈을 뜬 걸 보고 몸을 숙여 입을 맞췄지."]], [["‘I came to tell you how glad I am to see you home again.’", "\"오빠가 다시 집에 와서 얼마나 기쁜지 말해 주려고 왔어.\""]], [["‘But I can’t tell you how glad I am to see you, Kate.’", "\"그래도 내가 너를 보고 얼마나 기쁜지는 말로 못 한단다, 케이트.\""]], [["‘We have been wearying so for your return,’ said Kate, ‘mama and I, and--and Madeline.’", "\"우린 오빠가 돌아오기를 어찌나 애타게 기다렸는지 몰라,\" 케이트가 말했어. \"엄마랑 나랑, 그리고 매들린도.\""]], [["‘You said in your last letter that she was quite well,’ said Nicholas, rather hastily, and colouring as he spoke.", "\"네 지난번 편지에선 그분이 아주 잘 지낸다고 했지,\" 니콜라스가 다소 서둘러 말했고, 말하면서 얼굴이 붉어졌어."], ["‘Has nothing been said, since I have been away, about any future arrangements that the brothers have in contemplation for her?’", "\"내가 없는 동안 그분 앞날에 대해 형제분들이 무슨 계획을 세우고 있다는 얘기는 없었니?\""]], [["‘Oh, not a word,’ replied Kate.", "\"아, 한마디도 없었어,\" 케이트가 대답했어."], ["‘I can’t think of parting from her without sorrow; and surely, Nicholas, YOU don’t wish it!’", "\"난 그 언니랑 헤어진다고 생각하면 슬퍼서 견딜 수가 없어. 오빠도 분명히 그러길 바라지 않잖아, 니콜라스!\""]], [["Nicholas coloured again, and, sitting down beside his sister on a little couch near the window, said:", "니콜라스는 다시 얼굴이 붉어졌어. 그러고는 창가의 작은 소파에 누이 옆으로 앉으며 말했어."]], [["‘No, Kate, no, I do not.", "\"그래, 케이트, 그래, 나도 바라지 않아."], ["I might strive to disguise my real feelings from anybody but you; but I will tell you that--briefly and plainly, Kate--that I love her.’", "다른 사람한테라면 내 진짜 마음을 숨기려고 애썼겠지만, 너한테는 말할게. 짧고 분명하게, 케이트, 나는 그 사람을 사랑해.\""]], [["Kate’s eyes brightened, and she was going to make some reply, when Nicholas laid his hand upon her arm, and went on:", "케이트의 눈이 반짝였어. 뭔가 대답을 하려는데, 니콜라스가 케이트의 팔에 손을 얹고 말을 이었어."]], [["‘Nobody must know this but you.", "\"이건 너 말고는 아무도 몰라야 해."], ["She, last of all.’\n‘Dear Nicholas!’", "그 사람은 누구보다도 마지막까지 몰라야 하고.\""]], [["‘Last of all; never, though never is a long day. Sometimes, I try to think that the time may come when I may honestly tell her this; but it is so far off; in such distant perspective, so many years must elapse before it comes, and when it does come (if ever) I shall be so unlike what I am now, and shall have so outlived my days of youth and romance--though not, I am sure, of love for her--that even I feel how visionary all such hopes must be, and try to crush them rudely myself, and have the pain over, rather than suffer time to wither them, and keep the disappointment in store. No, Kate! Since I have been absent, I have had, in that poor fellow who is gone, perpetually before my eyes, another instance of the munificent liberality of these noble brothers. As far as in me lies, I will deserve it, and if I have wavered in my bounden duty to them before, I am now determined to discharge it rigidly, and to put further delays and temptations beyond my reach.’", "\"오빠!\" \"누구보다도 마지막까지. 절대로, 절대라는 게 까마득히 먼 날이긴 하지만 말이야. 가끔은 내가 떳떳하게 이 마음을 그 사람한테 말할 수 있는 날이 올지도 모른다고 생각해 보려고 해. 그런데 그건 너무 멀어. 너무나 까마득해서, 그날이 오기까지 수많은 세월이 흘러야 하고, 그날이 정말 온다 해도 (온다면 말이지) 나는 지금의 나와는 아주 딴사람이 되어 있을 거야. 젊음과 낭만의 시절을 훌쩍 넘겨 버린 채로. 그 사람을 향한 사랑만큼은 끝나지 않았겠지만 말이야. 그러니 나조차도 그런 희망이 다 얼마나 헛된 꿈인지 느껴져. 차라리 내 손으로 거칠게 짓밟아 버리고 아픔을 한 번에 끝내는 게 낫지, 세월이 그 희망을 천천히 시들게 하면서 실망을 두고두고 남겨 두게 두느니 말이야. 안 돼, 케이트! 내가 떠나 있는 동안, 세상을 떠난 그 가엾은 아이가 늘 내 눈앞에 있었어. 그 고결한 형제분들의 넘치는 너그러움을 보여 주는 또 하나의 본보기로 말이야. 내가 할 수 있는 한, 나는 그 너그러움에 걸맞은 사람이 될 거야. 전에는 그분들에 대한 마땅한 도리에 흔들렸을지 몰라도, 이제는 그 도리를 엄격히 지키고, 더 이상의 망설임과 유혹은 내 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 치워 버리기로 마음먹었어.\""]], [["‘Before you say another word, dear Nicholas,’ said Kate, turning pale, ‘you must hear what I have to tell you.", "\"오빠가 한마디 더 하기 전에,\" 케이트가 새파랗게 질려서 말했어."], ["I came on purpose, but I had not the courage.", "\"내가 할 얘기를 들어야 해. 일부러 그 얘기를 하러 왔는데, 용기가 안 났어."], ["What you say now, gives me new heart.’ She faltered, and burst into tears.", "그런데 오빠가 지금 한 말이 나한테 새 용기를 줬어.\" 케이트는 머뭇거리다가 울음을 터뜨렸어."]], [["There was that in her manner which prepared Nicholas for what was coming.", "케이트의 그런 태도에는 다음에 무슨 말이 나올지 니콜라스가 짐작하게 하는 무언가가 있었어."], ["Kate tried to speak, but her tears prevented her.", "케이트가 말을 꺼내려 했지만 눈물 때문에 막혔지."]], [["‘Come, you foolish girl,’ said Nicholas; ‘why, Kate, Kate, be a woman!", "\"자, 이 바보 같은 녀석,\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["I think I know what you would tell me.", "\"케이트, 케이트, 의젓하게 굴어야지! 네가 무슨 말을 하려는지 알 것 같아."], ["It concerns Mr. Frank, does it not?’", "프랭크 씨 얘기지, 그렇지?\""]], [["Kate sunk her head upon his shoulder, and sobbed out ‘Yes.’", "케이트는 오빠 어깨에 고개를 묻고 \"응\" 하고 흐느꼈어."]], [["‘And he has offered you his hand, perhaps, since I have been away,’ said Nicholas; ‘is that it?", "\"그리고 아마 내가 없는 동안 그 사람이 너한테 청혼했겠지,\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["Yes.", "\"그렇지? 그래."], ["Well, well; it is not so difficult, you see, to tell me, after all.", "그래, 그래. 봐, 막상 나한테 말하는 게 그렇게 어렵지도 않잖아."], ["He offered you his hand?’", "그 사람이 청혼했지?\""]], [["‘Which I refused,’ said Kate.", "\"내가 거절했어,\" 케이트가 말했어."]], [["‘Yes; and why?’", "\"그래, 그런데 왜?\""]], [["‘I told him,’ she said, in a trembling voice, ‘all that I have since found you told mama; and while I could not conceal from him, and cannot from you, that--that it was a pang and a great trial, I did so firmly, and begged him not to see me any more.’", "\"그 사람한테 말했어,\" 케이트가 떨리는 목소리로 말했어. \"오빠가 엄마한테 했던 그 얘기를 다 했어, 나중에야 알게 된 거지만. 그 사람한테도 숨길 수 없었고 오빠한테도 못 숨기겠는데, 그게, 그게 아프고 큰 시련이긴 했지만, 그래도 단호하게 그렇게 했어. 그리고 다시는 나를 보러 오지 말라고 부탁했어.\""]], [["‘That’s my own brave Kate!’ said Nicholas, pressing her to his breast.", "\"그게 바로 내 용감한 케이트지!\" 니콜라스가 케이트를 가슴에 꼭 끌어안으며 말했어."], ["‘I knew you would.’", "\"네가 그럴 줄 알았어.\""]], [["‘He tried to alter my resolution,’ said Kate, ‘and declared that, be my decision what it might, he would not only inform his uncles of the step he had taken, but would communicate it to you also, directly you returned. I am afraid,’ she added, her momentary composure forsaking her, ‘I am afraid I may not have said, strongly enough, how deeply I felt such disinterested love, and how earnestly I prayed for his future happiness. If you do talk together, I should--I should like him to know that.’", "\"그 사람은 내 결심을 돌려 보려고 했어,\" 케이트가 말했어. \"그리고 내 결정이 어떻든 간에, 자기가 한 일을 삼촌들한테 알릴 뿐 아니라 오빠한테도 돌아오는 대로 곧장 알리겠다고 했어. 내가 아무래도,\" 케이트는 잠깐 되찾았던 침착함을 다시 잃으며 덧붙였어. \"그렇게 사심 없는 사랑을 내가 얼마나 깊이 느꼈는지, 그 사람의 앞날의 행복을 얼마나 간절히 빌었는지, 충분히 강하게 말하지 못한 것 같아. 만약 오빠가 그 사람이랑 얘기하게 되면, 나는, 나는 그 사람이 그걸 알았으면 좋겠어.\""]], [["‘And did you suppose, Kate, when you had made this sacrifice to what you knew was right and honourable, that I should shrink from mine?’ said Nicholas tenderly.", "\"케이트, 너는 옳고 떳떳하다고 믿는 일을 위해 이렇게 희생을 했으면서, 내가 내 희생은 망설일 거라고 생각했니?\" 니콜라스가 다정하게 말했어."]], [["‘Oh no!", "\"아, 아니!"], ["not if your position had been the same, but--’", "오빠 처지가 나랑 같았다면 안 그랬겠지만, 그래도—\""]], [["‘But it is the same,’ interrupted Nicholas.", "\"그런데 똑같아,\" 니콜라스가 말을 잘랐어."], ["‘Madeline is not the near relation of our benefactors, but she is closely bound to them by ties as dear; and I was first intrusted with her history, specially because they reposed unbounded confidence in me, and believed that I was as true as steel.", "\"매들린이 우리 은인분들의 가까운 친척은 아니지만, 그 못지않게 소중한 인연으로 그분들과 단단히 얽혀 있어. 그리고 내가 그분의 사연을 맨 처음 맡게 된 건, 그분들이 나를 한없이 믿고, 내가 강철처럼 진실하다고 믿어 주셨기 때문이야."], ["How base would it be of me to take advantage of the circumstances which placed her here, or of the slight service I was happily able to render her, and to seek to engage her affections when the result must be, if I succeeded, that the brothers would be disappointed in their darling wish of establishing her as their own child, and that I must seem to hope to build my fortunes on their compassion for the young creature whom I had so meanly and unworthily entrapped: turning her very gratitude and warmth of heart to my own purpose and account, and trading in her misfortunes!", "그분이 우리 집에 머물게 된 사정이나, 다행히 내가 그분께 해 드릴 수 있었던 그 자그마한 도움을 이용해서 그분의 마음을 얻으려 든다면, 그게 얼마나 비열한 짓이겠어. 만약 내가 성공한다면, 그 결과는 형제분들이 그분을 친자식으로 삼으려던 그 간절한 소망에서 실망하게 되는 거고, 또 내가 그토록 비열하고 떳떳하지 못하게 옭아맨 그 젊은 처자를 향한 그분들의 동정심에 내 출세를 기대려는 꼴이 되는 거잖아."], ["I, too, whose duty, and pride, and pleasure, Kate, it is to have other claims upon me which I will never forget; and who have the means of a comfortable and happy life already, and have no right to look beyond it!", "그분의 그 고마운 마음과 따뜻한 정마저 내 잇속과 셈속에 써먹고, 그분의 불행을 가지고 장사를 하는 거지! 게다가 나는, 케이트, 결코 잊지 않을 다른 책임들을 지는 게 내 의무이자 자랑이고 기쁨인 사람이야."], ["I have determined to remove this weight from my mind.", "그리고 이미 편안하고 행복하게 살아갈 방편이 있으니, 그 너머를 넘볼 권리도 없는 사람이고! 나는 이 짐을 내 마음에서 치우기로 마음먹었어."], ["I doubt whether I have not done wrong, even now; and today I will, without reserve or equivocation, disclose my real reasons to Mr Cherryble, and implore him to take immediate measures for removing this young lady to the shelter of some other roof.’", "지금까지도 내가 잘못한 게 아닐까 싶어. 그래서 오늘, 나는 아무것도 숨기거나 얼버무리지 않고 체리블 씨께 내 진짜 이유를 털어놓고, 이 아가씨를 당장 다른 집 보호 아래로 옮겨 달라고 간청할 거야.\""]], [["‘Today?", "\"오늘?"], ["so very soon?’", "그렇게 빨리?\""]], [["‘I have thought of this for weeks, and why should I postpone it?", "\"몇 주 동안 이 생각을 했는데, 왜 미루겠어?"], ["If the scene through which I have just passed has taught me to reflect, and has awakened me to a more anxious and careful sense of duty, why should I wait until the impression has cooled?", "방금 내가 겪은 그 일이 나를 돌아보게 하고, 더 절실하고 신중하게 의무를 느끼도록 일깨워 줬다면, 그 인상이 식을 때까지 왜 기다리겠어?"], ["You would not dissuade me, Kate; now would you?’", "너는 나를 말리진 않을 거지, 케이트, 그렇지?\""]], [["‘You may grow rich, you know,’ said Kate.", "\"오빠도 알겠지만, 부자가 될 수도 있잖아,\" 케이트가 말했어."]], [["‘I may grow rich!’ repeated Nicholas, with a mournful smile, ‘ay, and I may grow old!", "\"내가 부자가 될 수도 있다고!\" 니콜라스가 서글픈 미소를 지으며 되뇌었어."], ["But rich or poor, or old or young, we shall ever be the same to each other, and in that our comfort lies.", "\"그래, 늙을 수도 있지! 그래도 부자든 가난뱅이든, 늙든 젊든, 우리 둘은 서로에게 언제나 똑같을 거고, 우리의 위안은 바로 거기에 있어."], ["What if we have but one home?", "우리한테 집이 하나뿐이라 한들 어때?"], ["It can never be a solitary one to you and me.", "너와 나에게 그 집이 외로운 곳이 될 리는 없잖아."], ["What if we were to remain so true to these first impressions as to form no others?", "우리가 이 첫 마음에 끝까지 충실해서 다른 인연을 안 맺는다 한들 어때?"], ["It is but one more link to the strong chain that binds us together.", "그건 우리를 묶어 주는 그 단단한 사슬에 고리 하나가 더해지는 것뿐이야."], ["It seems but yesterday that we were playfellows, Kate, and it will seem but tomorrow when we are staid old people, looking back to these cares as we look back, now, to those of our childish days: and recollecting with a melancholy pleasure that the time was, when they could move us.", "우리가 소꿉동무였던 게 어제 일 같은데, 케이트, 우리가 점잖은 노인이 되어 이 근심들을 돌아보게 되는 날도 내일 일처럼 금세 올 거야. 지금 우리가 어린 시절의 근심을 돌아보듯이 말이야."], ["Perhaps then, when we are quaint old folks and talk of the times when our step was lighter and our hair not grey, we may be even thankful for the trials that so endeared us to each other, and turned our lives into that current, down which we shall have glided so peacefully and calmly.", "그리고 한때는 이런 일들이 우리를 흔들 수 있었지, 하고 서글픈 즐거움으로 떠올리게 되겠지. 어쩌면 그때, 우리가 별난 노인이 되어 발걸음이 더 가볍고 머리가 세지 않았던 시절을 얘기할 때면, 우리를 이토록 서로에게 정들게 하고, 우리 삶을 그 평온하고 잔잔히 미끄러져 내려온 물줄기로 돌려놓은 그 시련들에 오히려 고마워하게 될지도 몰라."], ["And having caught some inkling of our story, the young people about us--as young as you and I are now, Kate--may come to us for sympathy, and pour distresses which hope and inexperience could scarcely feel enough for, into the compassionate ears of the old bachelor brother and his maiden sister.’", "그리고 우리 사연을 어렴풋이 전해 들은 우리 주변의 젊은이들이, 지금의 너와 나만큼 젊은 그 아이들이, 위로를 받으러 우리한테 찾아와서, 희망과 미숙함으로는 미처 절실히 느끼기도 어려운 괴로움을 늙은 노총각 오빠와 노처녀 누이의 너그러운 귀에 쏟아 놓겠지.\""]], [["Kate smiled through her tears as Nicholas drew this picture; but they were not tears of sorrow, although they continued to fall when he had ceased to speak.", "니콜라스가 이런 그림을 그려 보이자 케이트는 눈물을 글썽이면서도 미소를 지었어. 하지만 그건 슬픔의 눈물은 아니었고, 니콜라스가 말을 멈춘 뒤에도 계속 흘러내렸지."]], [["‘Am I not right, Kate?’ he said, after a short silence.", "\"내 말이 맞지, 케이트?\" 잠깐 침묵이 흐른 뒤에 니콜라스가 말했어."]], [["‘Quite, quite, dear brother; and I cannot tell you how happy I am that I have acted as you would have had me.’", "\"꼭 맞아, 정말 그래, 오빠. 그리고 내가 오빠가 바랐을 대로 행동했다는 게 얼마나 기쁜지 몰라.\""]], [["‘You don’t regret?’", "\"후회는 안 해?\""]], [["‘N--n--no,’ said Kate timidly, tracing some pattern upon the ground with her little foot. ‘I don’t regret having done what was honourable and right, of course; but I do regret that this should have ever happened--at least sometimes I regret it, and sometimes I--I don’t know what I say; I am but a weak girl, Nicholas, and it has agitated me very much.’", "\"아, 아, 아니야,\" 케이트가 작은 발로 바닥에 무슨 무늬를 그리며 머뭇머뭇 말했어. \"물론 떳떳하고 옳은 일을 한 건 후회 안 해. 그래도 이런 일이 일어났다는 건 후회돼. 적어도 가끔은 후회가 돼, 그러다 또 가끔은, 나도 무슨 말을 하는지 모르겠어. 난 그저 연약한 계집애일 뿐이잖아, 니콜라스. 그래서 이 일로 무척 마음이 흔들렸어.\""]], [["It is no vaunt to affirm that if Nicholas had had ten thousand pounds at the minute, he would, in his generous affection for the owner of the blushing cheek and downcast eye, have bestowed its utmost farthing, in perfect forgetfulness of himself, to secure her happiness.", "이 순간 니콜라스에게 만 파운드가 있었다면, 그가 발그레한 뺨에 눈을 내리뜬 그 아이를 향한 너그러운 사랑으로, 자기 자신은 까맣게 잊은 채 케이트의 행복을 지키기 위해 그 마지막 한 푼까지 다 내주었으리라고 단언해도 결코 허풍이 아니야."], ["But all he could do was to comfort and console her by kind words; and words they were of such love and kindness, and cheerful encouragement, that poor Kate threw her arms about his neck, and declared she would weep no more.", "하지만 그가 할 수 있는 거라곤 다정한 말로 케이트를 다독이고 위로하는 것뿐이었지. 그 말들이 어찌나 사랑과 다정함으로, 또 밝은 격려로 가득했던지, 가엾은 케이트는 오빠 목을 끌어안고는 다시는 울지 않겠다고 했어."]], [["‘What man,’ thought Nicholas proudly, while on his way, soon afterwards, to the brothers’ house, ‘would not be sufficiently rewarded for any sacrifice of fortune by the possession of such a heart as Kate’s, which, but that hearts weigh light, and gold and silver heavy, is beyond all praise?", "\"어떤 남자가,\" 니콜라스는 얼마 뒤 형제분들의 집으로 가는 길에 자랑스러운 마음으로 생각했어."], ["Frank has money, and wants no more.", "\"케이트 같은 마음을 갖는 것만으로 어떤 재산의 희생이든 충분히 보상받지 못하겠는가."], ["Where would it buy him such a treasure as Kate?", "마음이란 가볍고 금은이란 무겁다는 것만 빼면, 그 마음은 어떤 칭송으로도 모자란데 말이야. 프랭크한테는 돈이 있고, 더 바랄 것도 없지."], ["And yet, in unequal marriages, the rich party is always supposed to make a great sacrifice, and the other to get a good bargain!", "그 돈으로 케이트 같은 보물을 어디서 살 수 있겠어? 그런데도 어울리지 않는 결혼에서는 늘 부자 쪽이 큰 희생을 하고, 다른 쪽이 횡재를 하는 걸로들 여기지!"], ["But I am thinking like a lover, or like an ass: which I suppose is pretty nearly the same.’", "하지만 이건 사랑에 빠진 사람처럼, 아니면 멍청이처럼 하는 생각이군. 뭐, 그 둘은 거의 비슷한 거겠지만.\""]], [["Checking thoughts so little adapted to the business on which he was bound, by such self-reproofs as this and many others no less sturdy, he proceeded on his way and presented himself before Tim Linkinwater.", "지금 가는 길의 용건에는 도무지 어울리지 않는 그런 생각들을, 이런 자책과 그 못지않게 단단한 다른 자책들로 다잡으면서, 니콜라스는 가던 길을 계속 가서 팀 링킨워터 앞에 모습을 드러냈어."]], [["‘Ah! Mr.", "\"아!"], ["Nickleby!’ cried Tim, ‘God bless you!", "니클비 씨!\" 팀이 외쳤어."], ["how d’ye do?", "\"복 받으세요!"], ["Well?", "어떻게 지내셨어요?"], ["Say you’re quite well and never better.", "잘 지내셨죠? 아주 잘 지냈고 더없이 좋았다고 말해 봐요."], ["Do now.’", "어서요.\""]], [["‘Quite,’ said Nicholas, shaking him by both hands.", "\"아주 잘 지냈어요,\" 니콜라스가 두 손으로 팀의 손을 맞잡으며 말했어."]], [["‘Ah!’ said Tim, ‘you look tired though, now I come to look at you.", "\"아!\" 팀이 말했어."], ["Hark! there he is, d’ye hear him?", "\"그런데 막상 보니 피곤해 보이는데요."], ["That was Dick, the blackbird.", "들어 봐요! 저기 있어요, 들리세요?"], ["He hasn’t been himself since you’ve been gone.", "저게 딕이에요, 검은새 말이에요. 당신이 떠난 뒤로는 영 제정신이 아니었답니다."], ["He’d never get on without you, now; he takes as naturally to you as he does to me.’", "이제 당신 없이는 못 지내요. 나한테 그러는 것만큼이나 당신한테도 자연스럽게 따른다니까요.\""]], [["‘Dick is a far less sagacious fellow than I supposed him, if he thinks I am half so well worthy of his notice as you,’ replied Nicholas.", "\"내가 당신만큼이나 자기 눈길을 받을 만한 가치가 있다고 여긴다면, 딕은 내가 생각했던 것보다 훨씬 영리하지 못한 녀석이군요,\" 니콜라스가 대꾸했어."]], [["‘Why, I’ll tell you what, sir,’ said Tim, standing in his favourite attitude and pointing to the cage with the feather of his pen, ‘it’s a very extraordinary thing about that bird, that the only people he ever takes the smallest notice of, are Mr.", "\"아니, 내가 말씀드릴게요,\" 팀이 즐겨 취하는 자세로 서서 펜 깃으로 새장을 가리키며 말했어."], ["Charles, and Mr. Ned, and you, and me.’", "\"저 새한테 아주 신기한 점이 있는데요, 저 녀석이 조금이라도 눈길을 주는 사람은 오직 찰스 씨하고, 네드 씨하고, 당신하고, 나뿐이라는 거예요.\""]], [["Here, Tim stopped and glanced anxiously at Nicholas; then unexpectedly catching his eye repeated, ‘And you and me, sir, and you and me.’ And then he glanced at Nicholas again, and, squeezing his hand, said, ‘I am a bad one at putting off anything I am interested in. I didn’t mean to ask you, but I should like to hear a few particulars about that poor boy. Did he mention Cheeryble Brothers at all?’", "여기서 팀은 말을 멈추고 걱정스레 니콜라스를 흘긋 보았어. 그러다 뜻밖에 니콜라스와 눈이 마주치자 다시 말했지. \"당신하고 나뿐이에요, 당신하고 나뿐이라고요.\" 그러고는 다시 니콜라스를 흘긋 보더니, 그의 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했어. \"나는 마음에 걸리는 걸 미루는 데는 영 젬병이라서요. 물어보지 않으려고 했는데, 그 가엾은 아이에 대해 좀 자세히 듣고 싶어요. 그 애가 체리블 형제 얘기를 조금이라도 하던가요?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ said Nicholas, ‘many and many a time.’", "\"네,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"여러 번, 정말 여러 번 했지요.\""]], [["‘That was right of him,’ returned Tim, wiping his eyes; ‘that was very right of him.’", "\"그 애답네요,\" 팀이 눈물을 훔치며 대꾸했어. \"정말 그 애다워요.\""]], [["‘And he mentioned your name a score of times,’ said Nicholas, ‘and often bade me carry back his love to Mr.", "\"그리고 당신 이름도 스무 번은 입에 올렸어요,\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["Linkinwater.’", "\"링킨워터 씨께 자기 사랑을 전해 달라고 자주 부탁했지요.\""]], [["‘No, no, did he though?’ rejoined Tim, sobbing outright.", "\"아니, 아니, 정말 그랬어요?\" 팀이 대놓고 흐느끼며 되물었어."], ["‘Poor fellow!", "\"가엾은 녀석!"], ["I wish we could have had him buried in town.", "그 애를 시내에 묻을 수 있었으면 좋았을 텐데요."], ["There isn’t such a burying-ground in all London as that little one on the other side of the square--there are counting-houses all round it, and if you go in there, on a fine day, you can see the books and safes through the open windows.", "광장 건너편의 그 자그마한 묘지만 한 곳이 런던을 통틀어 없거든요. 둘레에 회계 사무소들이 빙 둘러 있어서, 날씨 좋은 날 거기 들어가면 열린 창문 너머로 장부랑 금고가 다 보인답니다."], ["And he sent his love to me, did he?", "그 애가 나한테 사랑을 전했다고요?"], ["I didn’t expect he would have thought of me.", "그 애가 나를 떠올릴 줄은 몰랐는데요."], ["Poor fellow, poor fellow!", "가엾은 녀석, 가엾은 녀석!"], ["His love too!’", "게다가 사랑을 전하다니!\""]], [["Tim was so completely overcome by this little mark of recollection, that he was quite unequal to any more conversation at the moment.", "팀은 이 자그마한 기억의 표시에 어찌나 완전히 무너졌던지, 그 순간 더 이상 대화를 이어 갈 수가 없었어."], ["Nicholas therefore slipped quietly out, and went to brother Charles’s room.", "그래서 니콜라스는 조용히 빠져나와 찰스 형의 방으로 갔지."]], [["If he had previously sustained his firmness and fortitude, it had been by an effort which had cost him no little pain; but the warm welcome, the hearty manner, the homely unaffected commiseration, of the good old man, went to his heart, and no inward struggle could prevent his showing it.", "전까지 그가 꿋꿋함과 의연함을 지켜 왔다면, 그건 적잖은 아픔을 치르며 안간힘을 쓴 덕분이었어. 하지만 그 마음씨 좋은 노신사의 따뜻한 환대와 정겨운 태도, 꾸밈없는 소박한 위로에 그의 가슴이 뭉클해졌고, 아무리 속으로 안간힘을 써도 그걸 드러내지 않을 수가 없었지."]], [["‘Come, come, my dear sir,’ said the benevolent merchant; ‘we must not be cast down; no, no.", "\"자, 자, 이보게,\" 인정 많은 상인이 말했어."], ["We must learn to bear misfortune, and we must remember that there are many sources of consolation even in death.", "\"우리가 의기소침해지면 안 되네, 안 되지. 우리는 불행을 견디는 법을 배워야 하고, 죽음 속에도 위안의 샘이 많다는 걸 기억해야 해."], ["Every day that this poor lad had lived, he must have been less and less qualified for the world, and more and more unhappy in is own deficiencies.", "이 가엾은 아이는 하루하루 살아갈수록 세상에 점점 덜 어울리게 되고, 자기 부족함 때문에 점점 더 불행해졌을 거야."], ["It is better as it is, my dear sir.", "이렇게 된 게 차라리 나아, 이보게."], ["Yes, yes, yes, it’s better as it is.’", "그래, 그래, 그래, 이렇게 된 게 나아.\""]], [["‘I have thought of all that, sir,’ replied Nicholas, clearing his throat.", "\"그 점도 다 생각해 봤습니다, 어르신,\" 니콜라스가 목을 가다듬으며 대답했어."], ["‘I feel it, I assure you.’", "\"정말이지 저도 그렇게 느낍니다.\""]], [["‘Yes, that’s well,’ replied Mr.", "\"그래, 그게 잘된 거야,\" 체리블 씨가 대답했어."], ["Cheeryble, who, in the midst of all his comforting, was quite as much taken aback as honest old Tim; ‘that’s well.", "그는 그렇게 위로하는 와중에도 정직한 늙은 팀 못지않게 마음이 흔들리고 있었지. \"그게 잘된 거야."], ["Where is my brother Ned?", "내 동생 네드는 어디 있나?"], ["Tim Linkinwater, sir, where is my brother Ned?’", "팀 링킨워터, 내 동생 네드는 어디 있나?\""]], [["‘Gone out with Mr.", "\"트리머스 씨랑 같이 나가셨어요."], ["Trimmers, about getting that unfortunate man into the hospital, and sending a nurse to his children,’ said Tim.", "그 딱한 사람을 병원에 들여보내고, 그 사람 아이들한테 간호사를 보내는 일 때문에요,\" 팀이 말했어."]], [["‘My brother Ned is a fine fellow, a great fellow!’ exclaimed brother Charles as he shut the door and returned to Nicholas.", "\"내 동생 네드는 훌륭한 사람이야, 대단한 사람이지!\" 찰스 형은 문을 닫고 니콜라스에게 돌아오며 외쳤어."], ["‘He will be overjoyed to see you, my dear sir.", "\"동생이 자네를 보면 무척 기뻐할 거야, 이보게."], ["We have been speaking of you every day.’", "우리는 날마다 자네 얘기를 했다네.\""]], [["‘To tell you the truth, sir, I am glad to find you alone,’ said Nicholas, with some natural hesitation; ‘for I am anxious to say something to you.", "\"사실 말씀드리면, 어르신께서 혼자 계셔서 다행입니다,\" 니콜라스가 자연스레 다소 머뭇거리며 말했어."], ["Can you spare me a very few minutes?’", "\"긴히 드릴 말씀이 있어서요. 잠깐만 시간을 내주실 수 있을까요?\""]], [["‘Surely, surely,’ returned brother Charles, looking at him with an anxious countenance.", "\"물론이지, 물론이고말고,\" 찰스 형이 걱정스러운 낯빛으로 니콜라스를 바라보며 대답했어."], ["‘Say on, my dear sir, say on.’", "\"말해 보게, 이보게, 말해 봐.\""]], [["‘I scarcely know how, or where, to begin,’ said Nicholas.", "\"어디서, 어떻게 시작해야 할지 잘 모르겠습니다,\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘If ever one mortal had reason to be penetrated with love and reverence for another: with such attachment as would make the hardest service in his behalf a pleasure and delight: with such grateful recollections as must rouse the utmost zeal and fidelity of his nature: those are the feelings which I should entertain for you, and do, from my heart and soul, believe me!’", "\"만약 어떤 사람이 다른 사람에게 사랑과 존경으로 가득 찰 까닭이 있다면, 그를 위한 일이라면 아무리 힘든 봉사도 기쁨과 즐거움이 될 만한 그런 정으로, 또 자기 천성의 온 열성과 충성을 불러일으킬 만한 그런 고마운 기억으로 가득 찰 까닭이 있다면, 그게 바로 제가 어르신께 품은 마음입니다. 제 마음과 영혼을 다해 그렇습니다, 믿어 주십시오!\""]], [["‘I do believe you,’ replied the old gentleman, ‘and I am happy in the belief.", "\"믿네,\" 노신사가 대답했어."], ["I have never doubted it; I never shall.", "\"그렇게 믿으니 나도 행복하군. 한 번도 의심한 적 없고, 앞으로도 의심하지 않을 걸세."], ["I am sure I never shall.’", "정말 절대 그러지 않을 거야.\""]], [["‘Your telling me that so kindly,’ said Nicholas, ‘emboldens me to proceed.", "\"그렇게 다정히 말씀해 주시니,\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["When you first took me into your confidence, and dispatched me on those missions to Miss Bray, I should have told you that I had seen her long before; that her beauty had made an impression upon me which I could not efface; and that I had fruitlessly endeavoured to trace her, and become acquainted with her history.", "\"용기를 내서 계속 말씀드리겠습니다. 처음 저를 믿고 브레이 양에게 심부름을 보내셨을 때, 저는 그분을 훨씬 전부터 보아 왔다고 말씀드렸어야 했습니다."], ["I did not tell you so, because I vainly thought I could conquer my weaker feelings, and render every consideration subservient to my duty to you.’", "그분의 아름다움이 제게 지울 수 없는 인상을 남겼고, 그분을 찾아내 그 사연을 알아보려고 애썼지만 헛수고였다고 말입니다. 그런데도 말씀드리지 않았던 건, 제 약한 감정을 이겨 내고 어르신에 대한 의무를 위해 다른 모든 마음을 다스릴 수 있으리라고 부질없이 믿었기 때문입니다.\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"니클비 군,\""], ["Nickleby,’ said brother Charles, ‘you did not violate the confidence I placed in you, or take an unworthy advantage of it.", "찰스 형이 말했어. \"자네는 내가 자네한테 둔 믿음을 저버리지 않았고, 그걸 떳떳지 못하게 이용하지도 않았어."], ["I am sure you did not.’", "분명 그러지 않았다고 믿네.\""]], [["‘I did not,’ said Nicholas, firmly.", "\"그러지 않았습니다,\""], ["‘Although I found that the necessity for self-command and restraint became every day more imperious, and the difficulty greater, I never, for one instant, spoke or looked but as I would have done had you been by.", "니콜라스가 단호하게 말했어. \"스스로를 다잡고 억눌러야 할 필요가 날마다 더 절박해지고 그만큼 더 어려워졌지만, 저는 단 한순간도 어르신께서 곁에 계셨더라면 그랬을 것과 다르게 말하거나 바라본 적이 없습니다."], ["I never, for one moment, deserted my trust, nor have I to this instant.", "단 한순간도 제게 맡겨진 일을 저버린 적이 없고, 지금 이 순간까지도 그렇습니다."], ["But I find that constant association and companionship with this sweet girl is fatal to my peace of mind, and may prove destructive to the resolutions I made in the beginning, and up to this time have faithfully kept.", "그런데 이 사랑스러운 아가씨와 끊임없이 가까이 지내고 함께 있는 것이 제 마음의 평화에 치명적이고, 처음에 세워서 지금껏 충실히 지켜 온 제 결심마저 무너뜨릴지도 모르겠다는 걸 알았습니다."], ["In short, sir, I cannot trust myself, and I implore and beseech you to remove this young lady from under the charge of my mother and sister without delay.", "한마디로, 어르신, 저는 제 자신을 믿을 수가 없습니다. 그러니 이 아가씨를 제 어머니와 누이의 보살핌에서 지체 없이 옮겨 주시기를 간청하고 또 애원합니다."], ["I know that to anyone but myself--to you, who consider the immeasurable distance between me and this young lady, who is now your ward, and the object of your peculiar care--my loving her, even in thought, must appear the height of rashness and presumption.", "저 말고 다른 사람에게는, 그러니까 저와 이 아가씨 사이의 헤아릴 수 없는 거리를 아시는 어르신께는, 이제 어르신의 피후견인이자 각별한 보살핌을 받는 이 아가씨를 제가 마음으로나마 사랑한다는 게 더없는 경솔이자 주제넘은 짓으로 보일 줄 압니다."], ["I know it is so.", "그렇다는 걸 압니다."], ["But who can see her as I have seen, who can know what her life has been, and not love her?", "하지만 제가 본 대로 그분을 보고, 그분의 삶이 어떠했는지 아는 사람이라면 누가 그분을 사랑하지 않을 수 있겠습니까?"], ["I have no excuse but that; and as I cannot fly from this temptation, and cannot repress this passion, with its object constantly before me, what can I do but pray and beseech you to remove it, and to leave me to forget her?’", "제겐 그것 말고는 변명이 없습니다. 그리고 이 유혹에서 달아날 수도 없고, 그 대상이 끊임없이 눈앞에 있는데 이 마음을 억누를 수도 없으니, 그분을 옮겨 달라고, 그래서 제가 그분을 잊을 수 있게 해 달라고 비는 것 말고 제가 무엇을 할 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"니클비 군,\""], ["Nickleby,’ said the old man, after a short silence, ‘you can do no more.", "노신사가 잠깐 침묵한 뒤에 말했어. \"자네는 더 할 수 있는 게 없네."], ["I was wrong to expose a young man like you to this trial.", "자네 같은 젊은이를 이런 시련에 내몬 게 내 잘못이었어."], ["I might have foreseen what would happen.", "무슨 일이 벌어질지 미리 내다봤어야 했는데."], ["Thank you, sir, thank you.", "고맙네, 이보게, 고마워."], ["Madeline shall be removed.’", "매들린은 옮기도록 하겠네.\""]], [["‘If you would grant me one favour, dear sir, and suffer her to remember me with esteem, by never revealing to her this confession--’", "\"한 가지 부탁을 들어주신다면, 어르신, 그분께는 이 고백을 절대 알리지 마셔서, 그분이 저를 좋게 기억할 수 있게 해 주십시오—\""]], [["‘I will take care,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇게 하지,\" 체리블 씨가 말했어."], ["Cheeryble. ‘And now, is this all you have to tell me?’", "\"그런데, 나한테 할 얘기가 이게 전부인가?\""]], [["‘No!’ returned Nicholas, meeting his eye, ‘it is not.’", "\"아닙니다!\" 니콜라스가 그의 눈을 마주 보며 대답했어. \"전부가 아닙니다.\""]], [["‘I know the rest,’ said Mr.", "\"나머지는 내가 아네,\""], ["Cheeryble, apparently very much relieved by this prompt reply.", "체리블 씨가 이 선뜻한 대답에 적잖이 안도한 듯 말했어."], ["‘When did it come to your knowledge?’", "\"언제 알게 됐나?\""]], [["‘When I reached home this morning.’", "\"오늘 아침 집에 도착했을 때입니다.\""]], [["‘You felt it your duty immediately to come to me, and tell me what your sister no doubt acquainted you with?’", "\"곧장 나한테 와서, 자네 누이가 분명 일러 줬을 그 얘기를 하는 게 도리라고 느꼈군?\""]], [["‘I did,’ said Nicholas, ‘though I could have wished to have spoken to Mr Frank first.’", "\"그렇습니다,\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"프랭크 씨와 먼저 얘기를 나눴으면 했지만요.\""]], [["‘Frank was with me last night,’ replied the old gentleman.", "\"프랭크는 어젯밤에 나랑 같이 있었네,\" 노신사가 대답했어."], ["‘You have done well, Mr.", "\"자네는 잘했어, 니클비 군."], ["Nickleby--very well, sir--and I thank you again.’", "아주 잘했네, 이보게. 다시 한번 고맙네.\""]], [["Upon this head, Nicholas requested permission to add a few words.", "이 대목에서 니콜라스는 몇 마디 덧붙일 수 있게 해 달라고 청했어."], ["He ventured to hope that nothing he had said would lead to the estrangement of Kate and Madeline, who had formed an attachment for each other, any interruption of which would, he knew, be attended with great pain to them, and, most of all, with remorse and pain to him, as its unhappy cause.", "그는 자기가 한 말이 케이트와 매들린의 사이를 갈라놓는 일로 이어지지 않기를 감히 바랐지. 두 사람은 서로 정이 들었는데, 그 사이가 끊긴다면 둘에게 큰 아픔이 될 거고, 무엇보다도 그 불행한 원인이 된 자기에게 회한과 아픔이 될 거라는 걸 그는 알았거든."], ["When these things were all forgotten, he hoped that Frank and he might still be warm friends, and that no word or thought of his humble home, or of her who was well contented to remain there and share his quiet fortunes, would ever again disturb the harmony between them.", "이 모든 일이 다 잊히고 나면, 프랭크와 자기가 여전히 따뜻한 벗으로 남기를, 그리고 자기의 보잘것없는 집이나, 그 집에 기꺼이 남아 자기의 조용한 살림을 함께 나누려는 그 사람에 대한 어떤 말이나 생각도 두 사람 사이의 화목을 다시는 흐트러뜨리지 않기를 바랐어."], ["He recounted, as nearly as he could, what had passed between himself and Kate that morning: speaking of her with such warmth of pride and affection, and dwelling so cheerfully upon the confidence they had of overcoming any selfish regrets and living contented and happy in each other’s love, that few could have heard him unmoved.", "그는 그날 아침 자기와 케이트 사이에 오간 일을 할 수 있는 한 그대로 들려주었어. 케이트를 이야기하는 그의 목소리에는 그토록 따뜻한 자랑과 애정이 묻어났고, 어떤 이기적인 미련도 이겨 내고 서로의 사랑 안에서 만족하고 행복하게 살아갈 수 있다는 확신을 그토록 밝게 펼쳐 보였기에, 그 얘기를 듣고 마음이 움직이지 않을 사람은 거의 없었을 거야."], ["More moved himself than he had been yet, he expressed in a few hurried words--as expressive, perhaps, as the most eloquent phrases--his devotion to the brothers, and his hope that he might live and die in their service.", "그 자신도 그 어느 때보다 마음이 움직여서, 형제분들에 대한 헌신과, 그분들을 섬기며 살다가 죽기를 바라는 마음을, 서둘러 몇 마디로 — 어쩌면 가장 유창한 말 못지않게 절절하게 — 털어놓았지."]], [["To all this, brother Charles listened in profound silence, and with his chair so turned from Nicholas that his face could not be seen.", "이 모든 이야기를 찰스 형은 깊은 침묵 속에서 들었어. 의자를 니콜라스 쪽에서 돌려 앉아서 그 얼굴이 보이지 않았지."], ["He had not spoken either, in his accustomed manner, but with a certain stiffness and embarrassment very foreign to it.", "그 역시 입을 열 때는 평소의 그 태도가 아니라, 그에게는 영 낯선 어떤 뻣뻣함과 어색함이 묻어났어."], ["Nicholas feared he had offended him.", "니콜라스는 자기가 그분의 심기를 거스른 게 아닐까 두려웠어."], ["He said, ‘No, no, he had done quite right,’ but that was all.", "노신사는 \"아니, 아니야, 자네는 아주 잘했네\" 하고 말했지만, 그게 다였지."]], [["‘Frank is a heedless, foolish fellow,’ he said, after Nicholas had paused for some time; ‘a very heedless, foolish fellow.", "\"프랭크는 무심하고 어리석은 녀석이야,\" 니콜라스가 한동안 말을 멈춘 뒤에 노신사가 말했어."], ["I will take care that this is brought to a close without delay.", "\"아주 무심하고 어리석은 녀석이지. 이 일이 지체 없이 마무리되도록 내가 챙기겠네."], ["Let us say no more upon the subject; it’s a very painful one to me.", "이 얘기는 더 하지 마세, 나한테는 아주 괴로운 얘기니까."], ["Come to me in half an hour; I have strange things to tell you, my dear sir, and your uncle has appointed this afternoon for your waiting upon him with me.’", "삼십 분 뒤에 나한테 오게. 자네한테 들려줄 이상한 일들이 있고, 자네 삼촌이 오늘 오후에 나랑 같이 자기를 찾아오라고 정해 두었으니 말일세.\""]], [["‘Waiting upon him!", "\"삼촌을 찾아간다고요!"], ["With you, sir!’ cried Nicholas.", "어르신과 함께요!\" 니콜라스가 외쳤어."]], [["‘Ay, with me,’ replied the old gentleman.", "\"그래, 나와 함께,\" 노신사가 대답했어."], ["‘Return to me in half an hour, and I’ll tell you more.’", "\"삼십 분 뒤에 나한테 다시 오게. 그러면 더 얘기해 줌세.\""]], [["Nicholas waited upon him at the time mentioned, and then learnt all that had taken place on the previous day, and all that was known of the appointment Ralph had made with the brothers; which was for that night; and for the better understanding of which it will be requisite to return and follow his own footsteps from the house of the twin brothers. Therefore, we leave Nicholas somewhat reassured by the restored kindness of their manner towards him, and yet sensible that it was different from what it had been (though he scarcely knew in what respect): so he was full of uneasiness, uncertainty, and disquiet.", "니콜라스는 말한 시간에 그를 다시 찾아갔고, 그제야 전날 있었던 일과, 랄프가 형제들과 잡은 약속에 대해 알려진 모든 것을 듣게 되었어. 그 약속은 그날 밤으로 잡혀 있었지. 그런데 이걸 제대로 이해하려면, 다시 거슬러 올라가 쌍둥이 형제의 집에서 나선 랄프 자신의 발자취를 따라가 볼 필요가 있어. 그러니 우리는 니콜라스를 여기 남겨 두기로 하지. 자기를 대하는 그분들의 태도에 다시 다정함이 돌아와 다소 마음이 놓이면서도, 그게 예전과는 어딘가 달라졌다는 걸 (어떤 점에서인지는 잘 몰랐지만) 느끼고 있는 니콜라스를 말이야. 그래서 그는 불안과 막막함과 심란함으로 가득 차 있었어."]], [["Ralph makes one last Appointment--and keeps it", "친애하는 독자 여러분... wait, no."]], [["Creeping from the house, and slinking off like a thief; groping with his hands, when first he got into the street, as if he were a blind man; and looking often over his shoulder while he hurried away, as though he were followed in imagination or reality by someone anxious to question or detain him; Ralph Nickleby left the city behind him, and took the road to his own home.", "집에서 살금살금 빠져나와 도둑처럼 슬그머니 자리를 떴어. 거리로 처음 나섰을 때는 마치 앞 못 보는 사람처럼 두 손으로 더듬거렸지. 그리고 서둘러 걸으면서 자꾸 어깨 너머를 돌아봤어. 상상 속에서든 실제로든 누군가가 따라오면서 자기를 붙잡고 캐물으려는 것만 같았거든. 그렇게 랄프 니클비는 도심을 뒤로하고 제 집으로 가는 길에 올랐어."]], [["The night was dark, and a cold wind blew, driving the clouds, furiously and fast, before it. There was one black, gloomy mass that seemed to follow him: not hurrying in the wild chase with the others, but lingering sullenly behind, and gliding darkly and stealthily on. He often looked back at this, and, more than once, stopped to let it pass over; but, somehow, when he went forward again, it was still behind him, coming mournfully and slowly up, like a shadowy funeral train.", "밤은 어두웠고, 찬 바람이 불었어. 바람은 구름을 앞으로 사납고 빠르게 몰아댔지. 그런데 그를 따라오는 것만 같은 검고 음울한 구름 덩어리 하나가 있었어. 그것만은 다른 구름들과 함께 거센 추격에 끼어들지 않고, 뒤에서 시무룩하게 꾸물거리며 어둡고 은밀하게 미끄러져 오는 거야. 그는 그걸 자주 돌아봤어. 한두 번은 멈춰 서서 그게 지나가게 두기도 했지. 그런데 어찌 된 일인지 다시 앞으로 나아가면 그것은 여전히 뒤에 있었어. 그림자 같은 장례 행렬처럼 구슬프게 천천히 다가오면서 말이야."]], [["He had to pass a poor, mean burial-ground--a dismal place, raised a few feet above the level of the street, and parted from it by a low parapet-wall and an iron railing; a rank, unwholesome, rotten spot, where the very grass and weeds seemed, in their frouzy growth, to tell that they had sprung from paupers’ bodies, and had struck their roots in the graves of men, sodden, while alive, in steaming courts and drunken hungry dens. And here, in truth, they lay, parted from the living by a little earth and a board or two--lay thick and close--corrupting in body as they had in mind--a dense and squalid crowd. Here they lay, cheek by jowl with life: no deeper down than the feet of the throng that passed there every day, and piled high as their throats. Here they lay, a grisly family, all these dear departed brothers and sisters of the ruddy clergyman who did his task so speedily when they were hidden in the ground!", "그는 초라하고 보잘것없는 묘지 하나를 지나야 했어. 거리보다 몇 피트 높이 솟아 있고, 낮은 난간 벽과 쇠 울타리로 거리와 갈라진 음산한 곳이었지. 무성하고 병들고 썩어 가는 자리였어. 풀과 잡초마저도 그 추레한 모습으로 이렇게 말하는 것 같았어. 자기들이 빈민들의 몸에서 돋아났고, 산 채로 김 오르는 골목과 술과 굶주림의 소굴에 절어 있던 사람들의 무덤에 뿌리를 박았다고 말이야. 그리고 정말로 그들은 거기 누워 있었어. 흙 한 줌과 널빤지 한두 장으로 산 자들과 갈라진 채, 빽빽하고 다닥다닥 누워 있었지. 마음이 썩었던 것처럼 몸도 썩어 가면서, 빽빽하고 추레한 무리로 말이야. 그들은 거기 누워 있었어. 삶과 뺨을 맞대고서. 날마다 그 위를 지나는 사람들의 발보다 깊지도 않은 곳에, 그들의 목까지 높이 쌓인 채로. 거기 그들은 누워 있었어. 소름 끼치는 한 가족으로. 그들이 땅에 묻혔을 때 그토록 후딱 일을 해치우던 혈색 좋은 목사의, 사랑하는 죽은 형제자매들이 죄다 거기 있었던 거야!"]], [["As he passed here, Ralph called to mind that he had been one of a jury, long before, on the body of a man who had cut his throat; and that he was buried in this place. He could not tell how he came to recollect it now, when he had so often passed and never thought about him, or how it was that he felt an interest in the circumstance; but he did both; and stopping, and clasping the iron railings with his hands, looked eagerly in, wondering which might be his grave.", "이곳을 지나면서 랄프는 한 가지 일을 떠올렸어. 오래전에 자기 목을 그어 죽은 사람의 시신을 두고 배심원으로 참여한 적이 있었던 거야. 그리고 그 사람이 바로 이 자리에 묻혔다는 것도 말이야. 어째서 지금 와서 그게 떠오르는지 그는 알 수 없었어. 그동안 그렇게 자주 지나다니면서도 한 번도 그 사람을 생각한 적이 없었으니까. 또 어째서 이 일에 관심이 가는지도 알 수 없었지. 그래도 그는 떠올렸고, 관심도 갔어. 그리고 멈춰 서서 두 손으로 쇠 울타리를 움켜쥐고는 안을 골똘히 들여다봤어. 어느 게 그 사람 무덤일까 궁금해하면서 말이야."]], [["While he was thus engaged, there came towards him, with noise of shouts and singing, some fellows full of drink, followed by others, who were remonstrating with them and urging them to go home in quiet.", "그가 이러고 있을 때, 고함과 노랫소리를 내며 술에 잔뜩 취한 사내 몇이 그를 향해 다가왔어. 그 뒤로는 그들에게 따지면서 조용히 집에 가라고 다그치는 다른 사람들이 따라왔지."], ["They were in high good-humour; and one of them, a little, weazen, hump-backed man, began to dance.", "술꾼들은 기분이 한껏 좋았어. 그 가운데 작고 쪼글쪼글하고 등이 굽은 사내 하나가 춤을 추기 시작했어."], ["He was a grotesque, fantastic figure, and the few bystanders laughed.", "우스꽝스럽고 기괴한 모습이라, 몇 안 되는 구경꾼들이 웃었지."], ["Ralph himself was moved to mirth, and echoed the laugh of one who stood near and who looked round in his face.", "랄프도 웃음이 나서, 곁에 서서 자기 얼굴을 돌아보는 사람의 웃음에 맞장구를 쳤어. 그들이 지나가 버리고 그가 다시 혼자 남았을 때, 그는 새로운 종류의 흥미를 느끼며 아까 생각을 다시 이어 갔어."], ["When they had passed on, and he was left alone again, he resumed his speculation with a new kind of interest; for he recollected that the last person who had seen the suicide alive, had left him very merry, and he remembered how strange he and the other jurors had thought that at the time.", "그 자살한 사람을 살아 있을 때 마지막으로 본 사람이, 그가 아주 즐거워하는 모습으로 헤어졌다고 한 게 떠올랐거든. 그리고 그때 자기와 다른 배심원들이 그 점을 얼마나 이상하게 여겼는지도 기억났어."]], [["He could not fix upon the spot among such a heap of graves, but he conjured up a strong and vivid idea of the man himself, and how he looked, and what had led him to do it; all of which he recalled with ease. By dint of dwelling upon this theme, he carried the impression with him when he went away; as he remembered, when a child, to have had frequently before him the figure of some goblin he had once seen chalked upon a door. But as he drew nearer and nearer home he forgot it again, and began to think how very dull and solitary the house would be inside.", "그렇게 많은 무덤 더미 속에서 그 자리를 콕 집어낼 수는 없었어. 하지만 그 사람 자체에 대해서는 강렬하고 또렷한 모습을 머릿속에 떠올렸지. 그가 어떻게 생겼는지, 무엇 때문에 그런 짓을 했는지 말이야. 그 모든 걸 그는 쉽게 떠올렸어. 이 생각에 골똘히 매달린 탓에, 그는 떠나면서도 그 인상을 함께 데리고 갔어. 어릴 적에 누가 문에다 분필로 그려 놓은 도깨비 형상을 한번 본 뒤로 자주 눈앞에 어른거리던 것처럼 말이야. 그런데 집에 점점 가까워질수록 그는 그걸 또 잊어버렸어. 그리고 집 안이 얼마나 따분하고 쓸쓸할까 생각하기 시작했지."]], [["This feeling became so strong at last, that when he reached his own door, he could hardly make up his mind to turn the key and open it.", "이 느낌이 마침내 어찌나 강해졌던지, 자기 집 문 앞에 이르렀을 때 그는 열쇠를 돌려 문을 열 마음을 좀처럼 먹지 못했어."], ["When he had done that, and gone into the passage, he felt as though to shut it again would be to shut out the world.", "그래도 그걸 하고 복도로 들어섰을 땐, 문을 다시 닫으면 세상을 닫아 버리는 것 같은 기분이 들었어."], ["But he let it go, and it closed with a loud noise.", "그래도 그는 문을 놓아 버렸고, 문은 요란한 소리를 내며 닫혔지."], ["There was no light.", "불빛 하나 없었어."], ["How very dreary, cold, and still it was!", "어찌나 음산하고 차갑고 고요하던지!"]], [["Shivering from head to foot, he made his way upstairs into the room where he had been last disturbed.", "머리끝부터 발끝까지 부들부들 떨면서, 그는 위층으로 올라가 아까 마지막으로 방해를 받았던 방으로 갔어."], ["He had made a kind of compact with himself that he would not think of what had happened until he got home.", "그는 자기 자신과 일종의 약속을 해 둔 터였어. 집에 도착할 때까지는 일어난 일을 생각하지 않겠다고 말이야."], ["He was at home now, and suffered himself to consider it.", "이제 그는 집에 왔으니, 스스로에게 그 일을 곱씹도록 허락했지."]], [["His own child, his own child!", "자기 자식이었어, 자기 친자식! 그는 그 이야기를 한순간도 의심하지 않았어."], ["He never doubted the tale; he felt it was true; knew it as well, now, as if he had been privy to it all along.", "사실이라고 느꼈거든. 줄곧 그 일을 다 알고 있었던 것처럼, 이제 또렷이 알았던 거야."], ["His own child!", "자기 친자식이!"], ["And dead too.", "게다가 죽기까지 했어."], ["Dying beside Nicholas, loving him, and looking upon him as something like an angel.", "니콜라스 곁에서, 그를 사랑하고 마치 천사 같은 존재로 우러르면서 죽어 갔던 거야."], ["That was the worst!", "그게 제일 견디기 힘든 일이었어!"]], [["They had all turned from him and deserted him in his very first need. Even money could not buy them now; everything must come out, and everybody must know all. Here was the young lord dead, his companion abroad and beyond his reach, ten thousand pounds gone at one blow, his plot with Gride overset at the very moment of triumph, his after-schemes discovered, himself in danger, the object of his persecution and Nicholas’s love, his own wretched boy; everything crumbled and fallen upon him, and he beaten down beneath the ruins and grovelling in the dust.", "그들은 모두 그를 외면하고 저버렸어. 그가 정작 도움이 가장 필요한 첫 순간에 말이야. 이제는 돈으로도 그들을 살 수 없었어. 모든 게 드러날 테고, 모두가 다 알게 될 테지. 젊은 귀족은 죽었고, 그 동행은 멀리 떠나 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있고, 만 파운드는 단번에 날아갔어. 그라이드와 짜 놓은 음모는 승리의 바로 그 순간에 뒤엎어졌고, 그 뒤에 꾸민 계략들도 들통났어. 자기 자신은 위험에 빠졌고, 자기가 괴롭히던 대상이자 니콜라스가 사랑하던 그 아이는, 다름 아닌 자기 가엾은 아들이었던 거야. 모든 게 무너져 내려 그를 덮쳤고, 그는 그 잔해에 짓눌려 흙먼지 속에서 엎드려 기었지."]], [["If he had known his child to be alive; if no deceit had been ever practised, and he had grown up beneath his eye; he might have been a careless, indifferent, rough, harsh father--like enough--he felt that; but the thought would come that he might have been otherwise, and that his son might have been a comfort to him, and they two happy together. He began to think now, that his supposed death and his wife’s flight had had some share in making him the morose, hard man he was. He seemed to remember a time when he was not quite so rough and obdurate; and almost thought that he had first hated Nicholas because he was young and gallant, and perhaps like the stripling who had brought dishonour and loss of fortune on his head.", "만약 자기 아이가 살아 있다는 걸 알았더라면, 아무런 속임수도 없었고 그 아이가 제 눈앞에서 자랐더라면, 그는 무심하고 데면데면하고 거칠고 모진 아버지였을지도 몰라. 그럴 법하지. 그도 그걸 느꼈어. 그래도 자기가 달랐을 수도 있다는 생각이 자꾸 들었어. 아들이 자기에게 위안이 되었을 수도 있고, 둘이 함께 행복했을 수도 있다고 말이야. 그는 이제 이런 생각이 들기 시작했어. 아들이 죽었다고 믿은 일과 아내가 달아난 일이, 자기를 이렇게 음울하고 모진 사람으로 만드는 데 한몫했을지도 모른다고. 자기가 지금처럼 거칠고 완고하지만은 않던 때가 있었던 것 같았어. 그리고 처음에 니콜라스를 미워하게 된 건, 그 애가 젊고 늠름한 데다, 어쩌면 제 머리 위에 불명예와 재산 손실을 안겨 준 그 풋내기와 닮아서였던 것 같다는 생각마저 들었지."]], [["But one tender thought, or one of natural regret, in his whirlwind of passion and remorse, was as a drop of calm water in a stormy maddened sea. His hatred of Nicholas had been fed upon his own defeat, nourished on his interference with his schemes, fattened upon his old defiance and success. There were reasons for its increase; it had grown and strengthened gradually. Now it attained a height which was sheer wild lunacy. That his, of all others, should have been the hands to rescue his miserable child; that he should have been his protector and faithful friend; that he should have shown him that love and tenderness which, from the wretched moment of his birth, he had never known; that he should have taught him to hate his own parent and execrate his very name; that he should now know and feel all this, and triumph in the recollection; was gall and madness to the usurer’s heart. The dead boy’s love for Nicholas, and the attachment of Nicholas to him, was insupportable agony. The picture of his deathbed, with Nicholas at his side, tending and supporting him, and he breathing out his thanks, and expiring in his arms, when he would have had them mortal enemies and hating each other to the last, drove him frantic. He gnashed his teeth and smote the air, and looking wildly round, with eyes which gleamed through the darkness, cried aloud:", "하지만 격정과 회한이 휘몰아치는 가운데 떠오른 다정한 생각 하나, 또는 자연스러운 후회 하나는, 폭풍에 미쳐 날뛰는 바다에 떨어진 잔잔한 물방울 한 방울 같았어. 니콜라스를 향한 그의 증오는 자기 자신의 패배를 먹고 자랐어. 자기 계획에 끼어든 일을 양분 삼았고, 옛날의 그 반항과 성공으로 살을 찌웠지. 그 증오가 커진 데는 이유들이 있었어. 그것은 차츰차츰 자라고 굳세졌지. 이제 그것은 완전히 미친 광기의 높이에까지 다다랐어. 다른 누구도 아닌 그의 손이 자기 가엾은 아이를 구한 손이었다니. 그가 그 아이의 보호자이자 충실한 친구였다니. 그가 그 아이에게, 비참하게 태어난 순간부터 한 번도 받아 본 적 없는 그 사랑과 다정함을 베풀어 주었다니. 그가 그 아이에게 제 친아버지를 미워하고 그 이름마저 저주하라고 가르쳤다니. 그리고 이제 이 모든 걸 알고 느끼며 그 기억 속에서 의기양양해하다니. 이건 고리대금업자의 가슴에 쓸개즙이자 광기였어. 죽은 아이가 니콜라스를 사랑한 것, 그리고 니콜라스가 그 아이에게 정을 쏟은 것은 견딜 수 없는 고통이었어. 그 아이의 임종 장면이 떠올랐어. 니콜라스가 곁에서 돌보고 부축하고, 그 아이는 고맙다는 말을 내쉬며 그의 품에서 숨을 거두는 그 광경 말이야. 그는 그 둘이 서로 철천지원수가 되어 끝까지 미워하기를 바랐는데. 그 생각이 그를 미쳐 날뛰게 했어. 그는 이를 갈고 허공을 내리쳤어. 그리고 어둠 속에서 번뜩이는 눈으로 사납게 주위를 둘러보며 큰 소리로 외쳤지."]], [["‘I am trampled down and ruined.", "\"나는 짓밟혀 망했어."], ["The wretch told me true.", "그 비열한 놈 말이 옳았어."], ["The night has come!", "밤이 왔어!"], ["Is there no way to rob them of further triumph, and spurn their mercy and compassion?", "저들에게서 더 이상의 승리를 빼앗고, 저들의 자비와 동정을 걷어찰 길은 없는 걸까?"], ["Is there no devil to help me?’", "나를 도와줄 악마는 없는 거야?\""]], [["Swiftly, there glided again into his brain the figure he had raised that night.", "그날 밤 그가 떠올렸던 그 형상이 다시 휙 그의 머릿속으로 미끄러져 들어왔어."], ["It seemed to lie before him.", "그것이 눈앞에 누워 있는 것 같았어."], ["The head was covered now.", "이번엔 머리가 덮여 있었어."], ["So it was when he first saw it.", "그가 그걸 처음 봤을 때도 그랬지."], ["The rigid, upturned, marble feet too, he remembered well.", "뻣뻣하게 위를 향한, 대리석 같은 발도 그는 잘 기억했어."], ["Then came before him the pale and trembling relatives who had told their tale upon the inquest--the shrieks of women--the silent dread of men--the consternation and disquiet--the victory achieved by that heap of clay, which, with one motion of its hand, had let out the life and made this stir among them--", "이어서 그의 눈앞에 검시 자리에서 그 사연을 들려주던, 창백하게 떨던 친척들이 떠올랐어. 여자들의 비명, 남자들의 말없는 두려움, 그 경악과 동요, 손 한 번 까딱하는 것으로 제 목숨을 끊고 그들 사이에 이런 소동을 일으킨 그 진흙 더미가 거둔 승리가 떠올랐던 거야."]], [["He spoke no more; but, after a pause, softly groped his way out of the room, and up the echoing stairs--up to the top--to the front garret--where he closed the door behind him, and remained.", "그는 더 이상 말하지 않았어. 그러다 잠시 뒤, 가만히 더듬어 방을 빠져나왔어. 그리고 메아리치는 계단을 따라 올라갔지. 맨 꼭대기까지, 앞쪽 다락방까지. 거기서 그는 등 뒤로 문을 닫고, 머물렀어."]], [["It was a mere lumber-room now, but it yet contained an old dismantled bedstead; the one on which his son had slept; for no other had ever been there. He avoided it hastily, and sat down as far from it as he could.", "이제 그곳은 그저 잡동사니를 쌓아 두는 방일 뿐이었어. 그래도 거기엔 분해된 낡은 침대 틀 하나가 아직 남아 있었지. 그의 아들이 자던 그 침대였어. 그 방엔 다른 침대가 놓인 적이 한 번도 없었거든. 그는 황급히 그것을 피해, 거기서 될 수 있는 한 멀찍이 떨어진 곳에 앉았어."]], [["The weakened glare of the lights in the street below, shining through the window which had no blind or curtain to intercept it, was enough to show the character of the room, though not sufficient fully to reveal the various articles of lumber, old corded trunks and broken furniture, which were scattered about. It had a shelving roof; high in one part, and at another descending almost to the floor. It was towards the highest part that Ralph directed his eyes; and upon it he kept them fixed steadily for some minutes, when he rose, and dragging thither an old chest upon which he had been seated, mounted on it, and felt along the wall above his head with both hands. At length, they touched a large iron hook, firmly driven into one of the beams.", "아래 거리의 불빛이 희미하게 비쳐 들었어. 그 빛을 막아 줄 블라인드도 커튼도 없는 창문을 통해 들어온 빛이었지. 그것만으로도 방의 분위기는 짐작할 만했어. 다만 여기저기 흩어진 갖가지 잡동사니, 줄로 묶은 낡은 트렁크들, 부서진 가구들을 죄다 환히 드러내 보일 만큼은 못 되었지. 천장은 비스듬한 경사 지붕이었어. 한쪽은 높고, 다른 쪽은 거의 바닥까지 내려와 있었지. 랄프는 그 가장 높은 쪽으로 눈길을 돌렸어. 그리고 몇 분 동안 거기에 눈을 가만히 박고 있다가 일어섰어. 그러고는 앉아 있던 낡은 궤짝을 그쪽으로 끌어다 그 위에 올라가서는, 두 손으로 머리 위쪽 벽을 더듬었어. 마침내 두 손에 커다란 쇠갈고리 하나가 닿았어. 들보 하나에 단단히 박혀 있는 것이었지."]], [["At that moment, he was interrupted by a loud knocking at the door below.", "바로 그 순간, 아래층 문을 쾅쾅 두드리는 소리가 그를 멈춰 세웠어."], ["After a little hesitation he opened the window, and demanded who it was.", "잠시 머뭇거리다가 그는 창문을 열고 누구냐고 물었지."]], [["‘I want Mr.", "\"니클비 씨를 뵈러 왔습니다.\""], ["Nickleby,’ replied a voice.", "한 목소리가 대답했어."]], [["‘What with him?’", "\"그 사람한테 무슨 볼일인데?\""]], [["‘That’s not Mr.", "\"그건 니클비 씨 목소리가 아닌 것 같은데요?\""], ["Nickleby’s voice, surely?’ was the rejoinder.", "상대가 되받았어."]], [["It was not like it; but it was Ralph who spoke, and so he said.", "목소리가 닮지 않은 건 맞았어. 하지만 말한 사람은 랄프였으니, 그는 자기가 맞다고 했지."]], [["The voice made answer that the twin brothers wished to know whether the man whom he had seen that night was to be detained; and that although it was now midnight they had sent, in their anxiety to do right.", "그 목소리가 대답하기를, 그 쌍둥이 형제분들이 오늘 밤 그가 만난 그 사람을 붙잡아 둬야 하는지 알고 싶어 한다고 했어. 그리고 지금이 한밤중이긴 하지만, 일을 제대로 처리하려는 마음에 사람을 보냈다고 했지."]], [["‘Yes,’ cried Ralph, ‘detain him till tomorrow; then let them bring him here--him and my nephew--and come themselves, and be sure that I will be ready to receive them.’", "\"그래,\" 랄프가 외쳤어. \"그자를 내일까지 붙들어 둬. 그런 다음 그를 이리로 데려오라고 해. 그자랑 내 조카랑 말이야. 그리고 자기들도 직접 오라고 해. 내가 틀림없이 그들을 맞을 채비를 하고 있을 테니까.\""]], [["‘At what hour?’ asked the voice.", "\"몇 시에요?\" 목소리가 물었어."]], [["‘At any hour,’ replied Ralph fiercely.", "\"아무 때나.\" 랄프가 사납게 대답했어."], ["‘In the afternoon, tell them.", "\"오후에 오라고 전해."], ["At any hour, at any minute.", "아무 때, 아무 분이든."], ["All times will be alike to me.’", "나한테는 어느 때나 다 똑같으니까.\""]], [["He listened to the man’s retreating footsteps until the sound had passed, and then, gazing up into the sky, saw, or thought he saw, the same black cloud that had seemed to follow him home, and which now appeared to hover directly above the house.", "그는 그 사람이 멀어져 가는 발소리에 귀를 기울였어. 그 소리가 다 사라질 때까지 말이야. 그러고는 하늘을 올려다보다가, 자기를 집까지 따라오는 것만 같던 그 검은 구름을 봤어. 아니, 봤다고 생각했지. 그것은 이제 집 바로 위에 떠 있는 것처럼 보였어."]], [["‘I know its meaning now,’ he muttered, ‘and the restless nights, the dreams, and why I have quailed of late.", "\"이제 저게 무슨 뜻인지 알겠어.\" 그가 중얼거렸어."], ["All pointed to this.", "\"잠 못 이루던 밤들이며, 그 꿈들이며, 요즘 들어 내가 왜 그렇게 움츠러들었는지도 말이야. 다 이걸 가리키고 있었던 거야."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["if men by selling their own souls could ride rampant for a term, for how short a term would I barter mine tonight!’", "제 영혼을 팔아 한동안 마음껏 날뛸 수만 있다면, 오늘 밤 나는 얼마나 짧은 동안이라도 좋으니 내 영혼을 내놓을 텐데!\""]], [["The sound of a deep bell came along the wind.", "깊은 종소리 하나가 바람을 타고 들려왔어."], ["One.", "한 시였지."]], [["‘Lie on!’ cried the usurer, ‘with your iron tongue!", "\"실컷 울려라!\" 고리대금업자가 외쳤어."], ["Ring merrily for births that make expectants writhe, and marriages that are made in hell, and toll ruefully for the dead whose shoes are worn already!", "\"그 쇠 혓바닥으로 말이야! 상속만 기다리는 자들 속을 뒤틀리게 하는 탄생을 위해서나, 지옥에서 맺어진 혼인을 위해서나 흥겹게 울려라."], ["Call men to prayers who are godly because not found out, and ring chimes for the coming in of every year that brings this cursed world nearer to its end.", "그리고 신발이 벌써 다 닳아 버린 죽은 자들을 위해서나 구슬프게 조종을 울려라! 들키지 않아서 경건한 척하는 자들을 기도하러 불러 모으고, 이 저주받은 세상을 종말에 더 가까이 데려가는 해가 올 때마다 종을 울려 대라."], ["No bell or book for me!", "나한테는 종도 성경책도 필요 없어!"], ["Throw me on a dunghill, and let me rot there, to infect the air!’", "나를 두엄 더미에나 내던져, 거기서 썩어 공기나 더럽히게 두라고!\""]], [["With a wild look around, in which frenzy, hatred, and despair were horribly mingled, he shook his clenched hand at the sky above him, which was still dark and threatening, and closed the window.", "광기와 증오와 절망이 무섭게 뒤섞인 사나운 눈길로 그는 주위를 둘러봤어. 그리고 여전히 어둡고 위협적인 머리 위 하늘을 향해 움켜쥔 주먹을 흔들고는, 창문을 닫았지."]], [["The rain and hail pattered against the glass; the chimneys quaked and rocked; the crazy casement rattled with the wind, as though an impatient hand inside were striving to burst it open. But no hand was there, and it opened no more.", "비와 우박이 유리창을 두드렸어. 굴뚝들이 흔들리고 휘청거렸지. 다 낡은 여닫이창은 바람에 덜컹거렸어. 마치 안에서 누군가 성난 손으로 그걸 박차고 열려는 것처럼 말이야. 하지만 거기엔 아무 손도 없었고, 그 창문은 다시는 열리지 않았어."]], [["‘How’s this?’ cried one.", "\"이게 어찌 된 일이지?\" 한 사람이 외쳤어."], ["‘The gentleman say they can’t make anybody hear, and have been trying these two hours.’", "\"그 신사분들이 그러는데, 아무리 불러도 사람이 나오질 않는다는 거예요. 두 시간째 두드려 봤다고요.\""]], [["‘And yet he came home last night,’ said another; ‘for he spoke to somebody out of that window upstairs.’", "\"그런데도 어젯밤에는 집에 들어왔어.\" 다른 사람이 말했어. \"저 위층 창문으로 누군가한테 말을 했으니까.\""]], [["They were a little knot of men, and, the window being mentioned, went out into the road to look up at it. This occasioned their observing that the house was still close shut, as the housekeeper had said she had left it on the previous night, and led to a great many suggestions: which terminated in two or three of the boldest getting round to the back, and so entering by a window, while the others remained outside, in impatient expectation.", "그들은 몇 사람 모인 작은 무리였어. 창문 얘기가 나오자, 그들은 길로 나가 그 창문을 올려다봤지. 그러다 보니 집이 여전히 꼭 닫혀 있다는 걸 알아챘어. 가정부가 전날 밤에 그렇게 잠가 두고 나왔다고 한 그대로였지. 그 바람에 온갖 추측이 쏟아졌어. 결국엔 그 가운데 가장 대담한 두세 사람이 뒤로 돌아가, 창문으로 안에 들어가기로 했지. 나머지는 밖에 남아 애가 타서 결과를 기다리고 있었어."]], [["They looked into all the rooms below: opening the shutters as they went, to admit the fading light: and still finding nobody, and everything quiet and in its place, doubted whether they should go farther. One man, however, remarking that they had not yet been into the garret, and that it was there he had been last seen, they agreed to look there too, and went up softly; for the mystery and silence made them timid.", "그들은 아래층 방을 죄다 들여다봤어. 들어가면서 덧문을 열어, 스러져 가는 빛을 들였지. 그래도 아무도 못 찾았고, 모든 게 조용하고 제자리에 놓여 있었어. 그들은 더 들어가 봐야 하나 망설였지. 그런데 한 사람이, 아직 다락방엔 안 가 봤다고, 그리고 그가 마지막으로 목격된 곳이 바로 거기라고 말했어. 그래서 그들은 거기도 들여다보기로 하고, 가만가만 올라갔어. 그 알 수 없는 분위기와 정적이 그들을 겁먹게 했거든."]], [["After they had stood for an instant, on the landing, eyeing each other, he who had proposed their carrying the search so far, turned the handle of the door, and, pushing it open, looked through the chink, and fell back directly.", "층계참에 잠시 서서 서로 눈치를 살핀 뒤, 수색을 거기까지 하자고 제안한 사람이 문손잡이를 돌렸어. 그리고 문을 밀어 열고는 그 틈으로 들여다보더니, 곧장 뒤로 물러섰지."]], [["‘It’s very odd,’ he whispered, ‘he’s hiding behind the door!", "\"거 참 이상하네.\" 그가 속삭였어."], ["Look!’", "\"이 사람이 문 뒤에 숨어 있어! 봐!\""]], [["They pressed forward to see; but one among them thrusting the others aside with a loud exclamation, drew a clasp-knife from his pocket, and dashing into the room, cut down the body.", "그들은 보려고 앞으로 몰려들었어. 그런데 그 가운데 한 사람이 큰 소리를 지르며 다른 사람들을 옆으로 밀쳐 내고는, 주머니에서 접이칼을 꺼내 방 안으로 뛰어들어 그 몸뚱이를 끊어 내렸어."]], [["He had torn a rope from one of the old trunks, and hung himself on an iron hook immediately below the trap-door in the ceiling--in the very place to which the eyes of his son, a lonely, desolate, little creature, had so often been directed in childish terror, fourteen years before.", "그는 낡은 트렁크 하나에서 밧줄을 뜯어내, 천장의 들창 바로 아래 박힌 쇠갈고리에 목을 매단 거였어. 바로 그 자리, 십사 년 전 외롭고 쓸쓸한 어린것이던 그의 아들이, 어린아이다운 공포에 질려 그토록 자주 눈길을 던지던 그 자리였지."]], [["# CHAPTER 63", "# CHAPTER 63"]], [["The Brothers Cheeryble make various Declarations for themselves and others.", "치어리블 형제는 자신들을 위해서, 또 남들을 위해서 여러 가지를 선언한다."], ["Tim Linkinwater makes a Declaration for himself", "팀 링킨워터는 자기 자신을 위해 선언을 한다."]], [["Some weeks had passed, and the first shock of these events had subsided.", "몇 주가 흘렀고, 그 일들로 받은 첫 충격도 가라앉았다. 매덜린은 떠났고, 프랭크는 자리를 비웠다."], ["Madeline had been removed; Frank had been absent; and Nicholas and Kate had begun to try in good earnest to stifle their own regrets, and to live for each other and for their mother--who, poor lady, could in nowise be reconciled to this dull and altered state of affairs--when there came one evening, per favour of Mr.", "니콜라스와 케이트는 자기들 슬픔을 진심으로 억누르려 애쓰기 시작했다. 서로를 위해, 또 어머니를 위해 살아 보려고 말이다."], ["Linkinwater, an invitation from the brothers to dinner on the next day but one: comprehending, not only Mrs.", "그 가엾은 부인은 이 칙칙하고 달라진 살림살이에 도무지 적응하지 못했다. 그러던 어느 날 저녁, 링킨워터 씨가 친절하게도 형제의 초대장을 들고 왔다."], ["Nickleby, Kate, and Nicholas, but little Miss La Creevy, who was most particularly mentioned.", "다음다음 날 저녁 식사에 와 달라는 것이었다. 니클비 부인과 케이트, 니콜라스뿐 아니라 라 크리비 양까지 초대했는데, 특히 라 크리비 양은 따로 콕 집어 언급해 두었다."]], [["‘Now, my dears,’ said Mrs.", "\"자, 얘들아.\" 니클비 부인이 말했다."], ["Nickleby, when they had rendered becoming honour to the bidding, and Tim had taken his departure, ‘what does THIS mean?’", "초대에 합당한 예를 갖추고 팀이 떠난 뒤였다. \"이게 무슨 뜻일까?\""]], [["‘What do YOU mean, mother?’ asked Nicholas, smiling.", "\"무슨 뜻이라뇨, 어머니?\" 니콜라스가 웃으며 물었다."]], [["‘I say, my dear,’ rejoined that lady, with a face of unfathomable mystery, ‘what does this invitation to dinner mean?", "\"내 말은 말이다, 얘야.\" 부인이 도무지 속을 알 수 없다는 표정으로 대꾸했다."], ["What is its intention and object?’", "\"이 저녁 식사 초대가 무슨 뜻이냐는 거야. 그 의도랑 목적이 뭐겠니?\""]], [["‘I conclude it means, that on such a day we are to eat and drink in their house, and that its intent and object is to confer pleasure upon us,’ said Nicholas.", "\"제 생각엔 그날 그 집에서 우리가 먹고 마신다는 뜻이고, 그 의도와 목적은 우리를 즐겁게 해 주려는 거겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 말했다."]], [["‘And that’s all you conclude it is, my dear?’", "\"그게 다라고 생각하니, 얘야?\""]], [["‘I have not yet arrived at anything deeper, mother.’", "\"아직 그보다 더 깊은 데까진 못 미쳤는데요, 어머니.\""]], [["‘Then I’ll just tell you one thing,’ said Mrs.", "\"그럼 내가 딱 한 가지만 말해 주마.\""], ["Nickleby, you’ll find yourself a little surprised; that’s all.", "니클비 부인이 말했다. \"넌 좀 놀라게 될 거다, 그게 전부야."], ["You may depend upon it that this means something besides dinner.’", "이게 저녁 식사 말고 다른 뭔가를 뜻하는 거라고, 내 장담하마.\""]], [["‘Tea and supper, perhaps,’ suggested Nicholas.", "\"차하고 야식 정도겠죠.\" 니콜라스가 넌지시 던졌다."]], [["‘I wouldn’t be absurd, my dear, if I were you,’ replied Mrs.", "\"내가 너라면 그런 우스운 소리는 안 하겠다, 얘야.\""], ["Nickleby, in a lofty manner, ‘because it’s not by any means becoming, and doesn’t suit you at all.", "니클비 부인이 거만하게 대꾸했다. \"그건 도무지 어울리지도 않고, 너한테 전혀 안 맞아."], ["What I mean to say is, that the Mr. Cheerybles don’t ask us to dinner with all this ceremony for nothing.", "내 말은 말이다, 치어리블 형제분이 이렇게 격식까지 차려서 우리를 저녁 식사에 부르는 게 그냥 아무것도 아닌 일은 아니라는 거야."], ["Never mind; wait and see.", "됐다, 두고 보면 알아."], ["You won’t believe anything I say, of course.", "너야 물론 내 말은 한마디도 안 믿겠지."], ["It’s much better to wait; a great deal better; it’s satisfactory to all parties, and there can be no disputing.", "기다리는 게 훨씬 나아, 훨씬 더. 그래야 모두가 만족하고, 다툴 일도 없거든."], ["All I say is, remember what I say now, and when I say I said so, don’t say I didn’t.’", "내가 하고 싶은 말은, 지금 내가 한 말을 기억해 두라는 거야. 그리고 내가 그랬다고 하거든, 내가 안 그랬다고 하진 말아라.\""]], [["With this stipulation, Mrs.", "이렇게 못을 박아 둔 니클비 부인은, 밤이고 낮이고 한 가지 환상에 시달리고 있었다."], ["Nickleby, who was troubled, night and day, with a vision of a hot messenger tearing up to the door to announce that Nicholas had been taken into partnership, quitted that branch of the subject, and entered upon a new one.", "숨이 턱에 찬 전령이 문 앞으로 달려와 니콜라스가 동업자로 들어가게 됐다고 알리는 환상이었다. 부인은 그 화제의 가지를 거기서 접고, 새로운 화제로 넘어갔다."]], [["‘It’s a very extraordinary thing,’ she said, ‘a most extraordinary thing, that they should have invited Miss La Creevy. It quite astonishes me, upon my word it does. Of course it’s very pleasant that she should be invited, very pleasant, and I have no doubt that she’ll conduct herself extremely well; she always does. It’s very gratifying to think that we should have been the means of introducing her into such society, and I’m quite glad of it--quite rejoiced--for she certainly is an exceedingly well-behaved and good-natured little person. I could wish that some friend would mention to her how very badly she has her cap trimmed, and what very preposterous bows those are, but of course that’s impossible, and if she likes to make a fright of herself, no doubt she has a perfect right to do so. We never see ourselves--never do, and never did--and I suppose we never shall.’", "\"참 별일이야.\" 부인이 말했다. \"정말이지 별일도 다 있지, 라 크리비 양을 초대하다니. 정말 놀랍다, 진짜로 놀라워. 물론 그 사람이 초대받은 건 아주 반가운 일이지, 아주 반가워. 그 사람이 처신을 아주 잘하리란 것도 의심할 여지가 없고. 늘 그러니까. 우리가 그 사람을 그런 사교 자리에 끌어들이는 다리가 됐다고 생각하면 참 흐뭇하다. 정말 기뻐, 정말 기쁘다니까. 그 사람이 정말 더없이 예의 바르고 사람 좋은 자그마한 양반인 건 분명하니까. 누군가 친구가 그 사람한테 한마디 해 줬으면 싶긴 해. 모자 장식을 어쩌면 그렇게 형편없이 달았는지, 또 그 리본 매듭은 또 얼마나 터무니없는지 말이야. 하지만 그야 물론 안 될 일이지. 그 사람이 제 꼴을 망가뜨리고 싶다면, 그야 분명 그럴 권리가 있으니까. 우리는 우리 자신을 못 본단다. 한 번도 못 봐, 못 봤고. 앞으로도 못 보겠지.\""]], [["This moral reflection reminding her of the necessity of being peculiarly smart on the occasion, so as to counterbalance Miss La Creevy, and be herself an effectual set-off and atonement, led Mrs. Nickleby into a consultation with her daughter relative to certain ribbons, gloves, and trimmings: which, being a complicated question, and one of paramount importance, soon routed the previous one, and put it to flight.", "이런 교훈적인 생각을 하다 보니, 부인은 이번 자리에서 유난히 맵시 있게 차려입어야겠다는 데까지 생각이 미쳤다. 그래야 라 크리비 양과 균형을 맞추고, 자기가 그것을 효과적으로 상쇄하고 메워 줄 수 있을 테니까. 이 생각은 부인을 딸과의 의논으로 이끌었다. 어떤 리본과 장갑, 장식을 할지에 관한 의논이었다. 이건 복잡한 문제인 데다가 더없이 중대한 일이었으므로, 곧 앞의 화제를 몰아내고 멀리 쫓아 버렸다."]], [["The great day arriving, the good lady put herself under Kate’s hands an hour or so after breakfast, and, dressing by easy stages, completed her toilette in sufficient time to allow of her daughter’s making hers, which was very simple, and not very long, though so satisfactory that she had never appeared more charming or looked more lovely. Miss La Creevy, too, arrived with two bandboxes (whereof the bottoms fell out as they were handed from the coach) and something in a newspaper, which a gentleman had sat upon, coming down, and which was obliged to be ironed again, before it was fit for service. At last, everybody was dressed, including Nicholas, who had come home to fetch them, and they went away in a coach sent by the brothers for the purpose: Mrs. Nickleby wondering very much what they would have for dinner, and cross-examining Nicholas as to the extent of his discoveries in the morning; whether he had smelt anything cooking at all like turtle, and if not, what he had smelt; and diversifying the conversation with reminiscences of dinners to which she had gone some twenty years ago, concerning which she particularised not only the dishes but the guests, in whom her hearers did not feel a very absorbing interest, as not one of them had ever chanced to hear their names before.", "대망의 날이 다가오자, 이 착한 부인은 아침을 먹고 한 시간쯤 뒤부터 케이트의 손에 자기 몸을 맡겼다. 그리고 천천히 단계를 밟아 가며 옷을 차려입어, 딸이 자기 채비를 할 만큼 시간을 충분히 남기고서 단장을 마쳤다. 케이트의 단장은 아주 간소해서 그리 오래 걸리지도 않았지만, 그 결과는 어찌나 만족스러웠던지 케이트가 그날처럼 매력적이고 사랑스러워 보인 적은 없었다. 라 크리비 양도 도착했는데, 모자 상자 두 개를 들고 왔다. 마차에서 건네받다가 그만 두 상자 다 밑바닥이 빠져 버렸다. 게다가 신문지에 싼 무언가도 들고 왔는데, 내려오는 길에 어떤 신사가 깔고 앉는 바람에 쓸 만하게 만들려고 다시 다림질을 해야 했다. 마침내 다들 옷을 갖춰 입었다. 그들을 데리러 집에 온 니콜라스까지 말이다. 그리고 형제가 일부러 보내 준 마차를 타고 떠났다. 니클비 부인은 저녁으로 뭐가 나올지 무척 궁금해하며, 니콜라스가 아침에 알아낸 게 어디까지인지 캐물었다. 자라 비슷한 냄새라도 좀 맡지 않았는지, 안 맡았다면 무슨 냄새를 맡았는지 말이다. 그러면서 한 이십 년 전에 갔던 만찬들 이야기로 대화에 변화를 주었는데, 음식만이 아니라 손님들까지 일일이 짚어 가며 이야기했다. 하지만 듣는 이들은 그다지 푹 빠져들 만큼 흥미를 느끼지 못했다. 그 이름들을 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없었으니까."]], [["The old butler received them with profound respect and many smiles, and ushered them into the drawing-room, where they were received by the brothers with so much cordiality and kindness that Mrs.", "늙은 집사가 깊은 존경과 숱한 미소로 그들을 맞이하더니, 응접실로 안내했다. 거기서 형제가 어찌나 다정하고 친절하게 그들을 맞아 주었던지, 니클비 부인은 그만 가슴이 두근거려서, 라 크리비 양에게 생색을 낼 정신조차 거의 없을 지경이었다."], ["Nickleby was quite in a flutter, and had scarcely presence of mind enough, even to patronise Miss La Creevy.", "케이트는 그 환대에 한층 더 마음이 흔들렸다."], ["Kate was still more affected by the reception: for, knowing that the brothers were acquainted with all that had passed between her and Frank, she felt her position a most delicate and trying one, and was trembling on the arm of Nicholas, when Mr.", "형제가 자기와 프랭크 사이에 있었던 일을 죄다 알고 있다는 걸 아는 터라, 자기 처지가 더없이 미묘하고 견디기 힘들게 느껴졌기 때문이다."], ["Charles took her in his, and led her to another part of the room.", "케이트는 니콜라스의 팔에 매달려 떨고 있었는데, 그때 찰스 씨가 그녀를 자기 팔로 부축해서 방의 다른 쪽으로 데려갔다."]], [["‘Have you seen Madeline, my dear,’ he said, ‘since she left your house?’", "\"매덜린을 본 적 있니, 얘야.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 애가 너희 집을 떠난 뒤로 말이다.\""]], [["‘No, sir!’ replied Kate.", "\"아니요, 어르신!\" 케이트가 대답했다."], ["‘Not once.’", "\"한 번도요.\""]], [["‘And not heard from her, eh?", "\"소식도 못 들었고, 응?"], ["Not heard from her?’", "소식도 못 들었어?\""]], [["‘I have only had one letter,’ rejoined Kate, gently.", "\"편지를 딱 한 통 받았어요.\" 케이트가 부드럽게 대꾸했다."], ["‘I thought she would not have forgotten me quite so soon.’", "\"그렇게 빨리 저를 잊을 거라곤 생각 못 했는데요.\""]], [["‘Ah,’ said the old man, patting her on the head, and speaking as affectionately as if she had been his favourite child.", "\"아.\" 노인이 그녀의 머리를 토닥이며 말했다."], ["‘Poor dear!", "마치 그녀가 자기가 제일 아끼는 자식이라도 되는 양 다정한 목소리였다."], ["what do you think of this, brother Ned?", "\"가엾은 것! 이걸 어떻게 생각하나, 네드 형제?"], ["Madeline has only written to her once, only once, Ned, and she didn’t think she would have forgotten her quite so soon, Ned.’", "매덜린이 케이트한테 딱 한 번밖에 안 썼대, 딱 한 번, 네드. 그리고 케이트는 그 애가 그렇게 빨리 자길 잊을 거라곤 생각 못 했다는군, 네드.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오! 슬프다, 슬퍼."], ["sad, sad; very sad!’ said Ned.", "정말 슬프구나!\" 네드가 말했다."]], [["The brothers interchanged a glance, and looking at Kate for a little time without speaking, shook hands, and nodded as if they were congratulating each other on something very delightful.", "형제는 서로 눈짓을 주고받았다. 그리고 잠시 말없이 케이트를 바라보더니, 손을 맞잡고 고개를 끄덕였다. 마치 무언가 아주 기쁜 일을 두고 서로 축하라도 하는 듯했다."]], [["‘Well, well,’ said brother Charles, ‘go into that room, my dear--that door yonder--and see if there’s not a letter for you from her. I think there’s one upon the table. You needn’t hurry back, my love, if there is, for we don’t dine just yet, and there’s plenty of time. Plenty of time.’", "\"자, 자.\" 찰스 형제가 말했다. \"저 방으로 들어가 보렴, 얘야. 저쪽 문 말이다. 거기 그 애한테서 온 편지가 없는지 보아라. 탁자 위에 한 통 있을 거다. 만약 있거든 서둘러 돌아올 것 없다, 얘야. 우리가 아직 식사를 시작하는 것도 아니고, 시간은 넉넉하니까. 아주 넉넉해.\""]], [["Kate retired as she was directed.", "케이트는 시키는 대로 물러갔다."], ["Brother Charles, having followed her graceful figure with his eyes, turned to Mrs. Nickleby, and said:", "찰스 형제는 그 우아한 자태를 눈으로 좇다가, 니클비 부인 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했다."]], [["‘We took the liberty of naming one hour before the real dinner-time, ma’am, because we had a little business to speak about, which would occupy the interval.", "\"실은 진짜 저녁 식사 시간보다 한 시간 일찍 모이자고 무례를 좀 부렸습니다, 부인. 잠깐 의논드릴 일이 좀 있어서요."], ["Ned, my dear fellow, will you mention what we agreed upon?", "그 짬을 메울 만한 일이지요. 네드, 이보게, 우리가 합의한 걸 자네가 말해 주겠나?"], ["Mr. Nickleby, sir, have the goodness to follow me.’", "니클비 씨, 괜찮으시면 나를 따라오시지요.\""]], [["Without any further explanation, Mrs.", "더 이상 설명도 없이, 니클비 부인과 라 크리비 양, 그리고 네드 형제만 함께 남겨졌다. 니콜라스는 찰스 형제를 따라 그의 개인 방으로 들어갔다."], ["Nickleby, Miss La Creevy, and brother Ned, were left alone together, and Nicholas followed brother Charles into his private room; where, to his great astonishment, he encountered Frank, whom he supposed to be abroad.", "거기서 그는 깜짝 놀라고 말았다. 외국에 있는 줄로만 알았던 프랭크와 마주쳤기 때문이다."]], [["‘Young men,’ said Mr.", "\"젊은이들.\""], ["Cheeryble, ‘shake hands!’", "치어리블 씨가 말했다. \"악수하게!\""]], [["‘I need no bidding to do that,’ said Nicholas, extending his.", "\"그러라는 분부가 필요 없습니다.\" 니콜라스가 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["‘Nor I,’ rejoined Frank, as he clasped it heartily.", "\"저도 그렇습니다.\" 프랭크가 그 손을 진심으로 꼭 잡으며 대꾸했다."]], [["The old gentleman thought that two handsomer or finer young fellows could scarcely stand side by side than those on whom he looked with so much pleasure.", "이렇게 흐뭇하게 바라보는 두 사람보다 더 잘생기고 멋진 젊은이 둘이 나란히 설 수는 없으리라고, 노신사는 생각했다."], ["Suffering his eyes to rest upon them, for a short time in silence, he said, while he seated himself at his desk:", "그는 잠시 말없이 두 사람에게 눈길을 두었다가, 책상 앞에 자리를 잡으며 말했다."]], [["‘I wish to see you friends--close and firm friends--and if I thought you otherwise, I should hesitate in what I am about to say.", "\"나는 자네들이 친구가 되기를 바라네. 가깝고 단단한 친구 말이야."], ["Frank, look here! Mr.", "만약 그렇지 않을 거라 여겼다면, 지금 하려는 말을 망설였을 걸세."], ["Nickleby, will you come on the other side?’", "프랭크, 여기 좀 보게! 니클비 씨, 자네는 반대쪽으로 와 주겠나?\""]], [["The young men stepped up on either hand of brother Charles, who produced a paper from his desk, and unfolded it.", "두 젊은이는 찰스 형제의 양옆으로 다가섰다. 찰스 형제가 책상에서 서류 한 장을 꺼내 펼쳤다."]], [["‘This,’ he said, ‘is a copy of the will of Madeline’s maternal grandfather, bequeathing her the sum of twelve thousand pounds, payable either upon her coming of age or marrying. It would appear that this gentleman, angry with her (his only relation) because she would not put herself under his protection, and detach herself from the society of her father, in compliance with his repeated overtures, made a will leaving this property (which was all he possessed) to a charitable institution. He would seem to have repented this determination, however, for three weeks afterwards, and in the same month, he executed this. By some fraud, it was abstracted immediately after his decease, and the other--the only will found--was proved and administered. Friendly negotiations, which have only just now terminated, have been proceeding since this instrument came into our hands, and, as there is no doubt of its authenticity, and the witnesses have been discovered (after some trouble), the money has been refunded. Madeline has therefore obtained her right, and is, or will be, when either of the contingencies which I have mentioned has arisen, mistress of this fortune. You understand me?’", "\"이것은.\" 그가 말했다. \"매덜린의 외할아버지 유언장 사본이라네. 그 애한테 만 이천 파운드를 물려준다는 내용이지. 성년이 되거나 결혼할 때 지급한다는 조건으로 말이야. 사연인즉슨 이렇다네. 이 노신사는 매덜린한테 화가 나 있었어. 매덜린은 그의 유일한 혈육이었는데, 거듭된 권유에도 그의 보호 아래로 들어와 제 아버지 곁을 떠나려 하지 않았거든. 그래서 그는 이 재산을, 그게 그가 가진 전부였는데, 어느 자선 단체에 남긴다는 유언장을 만들었어. 그런데 그 결정을 후회했던 모양이야. 삼 주 뒤, 같은 달에 이 유언장을 작성했으니까. 그런데 그가 세상을 떠나자마자 누군가 이걸 부정한 수단으로 빼돌렸어. 그래서 다른 것, 그러니까 유일하게 발견된 유언장이 검인을 받고 집행됐지. 이 문서가 우리 손에 들어온 뒤로 우호적인 교섭이 진행됐는데, 그게 이제 막 마무리됐다네. 이게 진본이라는 데 의심의 여지가 없고, 증인들도 (꽤 애를 먹은 끝에) 찾아냈으니, 돈은 되돌려졌네. 그래서 매덜린은 자기 권리를 되찾은 거야. 내가 말한 두 가지 조건 가운데 하나가 일어나면, 그 애는 이 재산의 주인이 되네. 아니, 이미 주인이지. 내 말 알아듣겠나?\""]], [["Frank replied in the affirmative.", "프랭크는 그렇다고 대답했다."], ["Nicholas, who could not trust himself to speak lest his voice should be heard to falter, bowed his head.", "니콜라스는 목소리가 떨리는 게 들킬까 봐 차마 입을 열지 못하고, 고개를 숙였다."]], [["‘Now, Frank,’ said the old gentleman, ‘you were the immediate means of recovering this deed. The fortune is but a small one; but we love Madeline; and such as it is, we would rather see you allied to her with that, than to any other girl we know who has three times the money. Will you become a suitor to her for her hand?’", "\"자, 프랭크.\" 노신사가 말했다. \"이 증서를 되찾는 데 직접적인 계기가 된 건 자네였네. 재산이 그리 큰 건 아니야. 하지만 우리는 매덜린을 사랑하네. 그러니 그게 어떻든, 우리는 그 세 배의 돈을 가진 어떤 처녀와 자네가 맺어지는 것보다, 그 애와 맺어지는 걸 더 보고 싶다네. 자네가 그 애한테 청혼하겠나?\""]], [["‘No, sir.", "\"아닙니다, 어르신."], ["I interested myself in the recovery of that instrument, believing that her hand was already pledged to one who has a thousand times the claims upon her gratitude, and, if I mistake not, upon her heart, that I or any other man can ever urge.", "제가 그 증서를 되찾는 일에 마음을 쓴 건, 매덜린의 손이 이미 어떤 사람에게 약속됐다고 믿었기 때문입니다. 저나 그 어떤 남자가 내세울 수 있는 것보다 천 배는 더, 그녀의 감사를 받을 자격이 있는 사람, 그리고 제가 잘못 본 게 아니라면 그녀의 마음까지 차지한 사람에게 말입니다."], ["In this it seems I judged hastily.’", "이 점에선 제가 성급하게 판단한 모양이군요.\""]], [["‘As you always do, sir,’ cried brother Charles, utterly forgetting his assumed dignity, ‘as you always do.", "\"자네는 늘 그렇지, 이 사람아!\" 찰스 형제가 짐짓 꾸민 위엄도 까맣게 잊고 외쳤다."], ["How dare you think, Frank, that we would have you marry for money, when youth, beauty, and every amiable virtue and excellence were to be had for love?", "\"늘 그래. 어떻게 감히 그런 생각을 하나, 프랭크."], ["How dared you, Frank, go and make love to Mr.", "젊음과 아름다움과 온갖 사랑스러운 미덕과 훌륭함을 사랑으로 얻을 수 있는데, 우리가 자네더러 돈 보고 결혼하라고 시킬 줄 알았나?"], ["Nickleby’s sister without telling us first what you meant to do, and letting us speak for you?’", "어떻게 감히, 프랭크, 자네가 어쩌려는지 우리한테 먼저 말도 안 하고, 우리가 자네 대신 나설 기회도 안 주고서, 니클비 씨의 여동생한테 마음을 줬느냔 말이야?\""]], [["‘I hardly dared to hope--’", "\"저는 감히 바랄 엄두도 못 냈—\""]], [["‘You hardly dared to hope!", "\"감히 바랄 엄두도 못 냈다고!"], ["Then, so much the greater reason for having our assistance! Mr.", "그렇다면 더더욱 우리 도움을 받았어야지!"], ["Nickleby, sir, Frank, although he judged hastily, judged, for once, correctly.", "니클비 씨, 프랭크가 성급하게 판단하긴 했어도, 이번 한 번은 제대로 봤다네. 매덜린의 마음은 이미 임자가 있어."], ["Madeline’s heart IS occupied.", "자네 손을 이리 주게."], ["Give me your hand, sir; it is occupied by you, and worthily and naturally.", "그 임자는 바로 자네야. 떳떳하고도 당연한 일이지."], ["This fortune is destined to be yours, but you have a greater fortune in her, sir, than you would have in money were it forty times told.", "이 재산은 자네 것이 될 운명이지만, 자네는 돈보다 그 애한테서 더 큰 재산을 얻는 걸세. 그 돈을 마흔 배로 불려도 못 미칠 재산을 말이야."], ["She chooses you, Mr. Nickleby.", "그 애가 자네를 택했네, 니클비 씨."], ["She chooses as we, her dearest friends, would have her choose.", "우리, 그 애의 가장 가까운 벗들이 바라던 그대로 택한 거지."], ["Frank chooses as we would have HIM choose.", "프랭크도 우리가 바라던 그대로 택했고."], ["He should have your sister’s little hand, sir, if she had refused it a score of times; ay, he should, and he shall!", "자네 여동생이 스무 번을 거절했어도 프랭크는 그 작은 손을 차지해야 마땅하네. 암, 그래야 하고, 그렇게 될 거야!"], ["You acted nobly, not knowing our sentiments, but now you know them, sir, you must do as you are bid.", "자네는 우리 마음을 모르고서도 고귀하게 처신했네. 하지만 이제 우리 마음을 알았으니, 시키는 대로 해야 하네."], ["What!", "아니!"], ["You are the children of a worthy gentleman!", "자네들은 훌륭한 신사의 자식들이야! 한때는 말일세, 나의 사랑하는 동생 네드와 내가 가난하고 순박한 두 소년이던 시절이 있었네."], ["The time was, sir, when my dear brother Ned and I were two poor simple-hearted boys, wandering, almost barefoot, to seek our fortunes: are we changed in anything but years and worldly circumstances since that time?", "거의 맨발로 떠돌며 살길을 찾던 시절이지. 그때 이후로 우리가 나이와 형편 말고 달라진 게 있던가?"], ["No, God forbid!", "아니, 천만에!"], ["Oh, Ned, Ned, Ned, what a happy day this is for you and me!", "오, 네드, 네드, 네드, 오늘이 자네와 나한테 얼마나 행복한 날인가!"], ["If our poor mother had only lived to see us now, Ned, how proud it would have made her dear heart at last!’", "우리 가엾은 어머니가 지금의 우릴 볼 때까지 살아 계셨더라면, 네드, 그 다정한 마음이 끝내 얼마나 뿌듯했겠나!\""]], [["Thus apostrophised, brother Ned, who had entered with Mrs. Nickleby, and who had been before unobserved by the young men, darted forward, and fairly hugged brother Charles in his arms.", "이렇게 부름을 받은 네드 형제는, 니클비 부인과 함께 들어와 있던 터라 젊은이들 눈에는 미처 띄지 않았는데, 앞으로 뛰쳐나와 찰스 형제를 두 팔로 와락 끌어안았다."]], [["‘Bring in my little Kate,’ said the latter, after a short silence.", "\"내 어린 케이트를 데려오게.\" 찰스 형제가 잠시 입을 다물었다가 말했다."], ["‘Bring her in, Ned.", "\"데려오게, 네드."], ["Let me see Kate, let me kiss her.", "케이트를 보게 해 줘. 입을 맞추게 해 다오."], ["I have a right to do so now; I was very near it when she first came; I have often been very near it.", "이제 나도 그럴 권리가 있으니까. 처음 그 애가 왔을 때도 입맞춤할 뻔했어."], ["Ah! Did you find the letter, my bird?", "여러 번 그럴 뻔했지."], ["Did you find Madeline herself, waiting for you and expecting you?", "아! 편지는 찾았느냐, 내 새 같은 것아?"], ["Did you find that she had not quite forgotten her friend and nurse and sweet companion?", "매덜린이 직접 너를 기다리고 있던 걸 봤느냐? 그 애가 제 벗이자 간병인이자 다정한 동무를 아주 잊지는 않았다는 걸 알았느냐?"], ["Why, this is almost the best of all!’", "이런, 이게 무엇보다 제일 기쁜 일이로구나!\""]], [["‘Come, come,’ said Ned, ‘Frank will be jealous, and we shall have some cutting of throats before dinner.’", "\"자, 자.\" 네드가 말했다. \"프랭크가 질투하겠어. 저녁 식사 전에 칼부림이라도 나겠는걸.\""]], [["‘Then let him take her away, Ned, let him take her away.", "\"그럼 프랭크더러 데려가라고 하게, 네드, 데려가라고 해."], ["Madeline’s in the next room.", "매덜린은 옆방에 있네."], ["Let all the lovers get out of the way, and talk among themselves, if they’ve anything to say.", "사랑하는 것들은 죄다 비키게 하고, 할 말이 있거든 저희끼리 이야기하게 두자고."], ["Turn ‘em out, Ned, every one!’", "다 내보내게, 네드, 하나도 빠짐없이!\""]], [["Brother Charles began the clearance by leading the blushing girl to the door, and dismissing her with a kiss. Frank was not very slow to follow, and Nicholas had disappeared first of all. So there only remained Mrs Nickleby and Miss La Creevy, who were both sobbing heartily; the two brothers; and Tim Linkinwater, who now came in to shake hands with everybody: his round face all radiant and beaming with smiles.", "찰스 형제가 정리를 시작했다. 얼굴이 발그레해진 케이트를 문 앞으로 데려가, 입을 맞추고 내보낸 것이다. 프랭크도 그리 꾸물대지 않고 뒤를 따랐다. 니콜라스는 제일 먼저 사라진 뒤였다. 그래서 남은 건 니클비 부인과 라 크리비 양뿐이었는데, 둘 다 진심으로 흐느끼고 있었다. 그리고 두 형제, 또 이제 막 들어와 모두와 악수를 나누는 팀 링킨워터가 있었다. 그의 둥근 얼굴은 온통 환하게 빛나며 미소가 가득했다."]], [["‘Well, Tim Linkinwater, sir,’ said brother Charles, who was always spokesman, ‘now the young folks are happy, sir.’", "\"자, 팀 링킨워터.\" 늘 대변인 노릇을 하는 찰스 형제가 말했다. \"이제 젊은이들이 행복해졌네.\""]], [["‘You didn’t keep ‘em in suspense as long as you said you would, though,’ returned Tim, archly.", "\"그래도 말씀하신 만큼 그렇게 오래 애태우진 않으셨네요.\""], ["‘Why, Mr. Nickleby and Mr.", "팀이 짓궂게 대꾸했다."], ["Frank were to have been in your room for I don’t know how long; and I don’t know what you weren’t to have told them before you came out with the truth.’", "\"아니, 니콜라스 씨랑 프랭크 씨를 얼마나 오래 방에 붙들어 둘 거라고 하셨어요. 게다가 진실을 털어놓기 전에 두 사람한테 별의별 얘기를 다 늘어놓을 거라고 하지 않으셨습니까.\""]], [["‘Now, did you ever know such a villain as this, Ned?’ said the old gentleman; ‘did you ever know such a villain as Tim Linkinwater? He accusing me of being impatient, and he the very man who has been wearying us morning, noon, and night, and torturing us for leave to go and tell ‘em what was in store, before our plans were half complete, or we had arranged a single thing. A treacherous dog!’", "\"이런 악당을 또 본 적 있나, 네드?\" 노신사가 말했다. \"팀 링킨워터 같은 악당을 또 본 적 있어? 나더러 성미가 급하다고 몰아세우다니. 정작 아침, 점심, 저녁으로 우릴 들들 볶은 게 바로 이 사람인데. 우리 계획이 절반도 갖춰지기 전에, 무얼 하나 제대로 정하지도 못했는데, 가서 무슨 일이 기다리고 있는지 알려 주게 해 달라고 우릴 들볶지 않았나. 배신을 일삼는 개야!\""]], [["‘So he is, brother Charles,’ returned Ned; ‘Tim is a treacherous dog.", "\"맞고말고, 찰스 형제.\" 네드가 대꾸했다."], ["Tim is not to be trusted.", "\"팀은 배신을 일삼는 개야. 도무지 믿을 수가 없어."], ["Tim is a wild young fellow.", "팀은 철없는 젊은 녀석이거든. 진중함도 차분함도 없어."], ["He wants gravity and steadiness; he must sow his wild oats, and then perhaps he’ll become in time a respectable member of society.’", "한바탕 객기를 다 부려 봐야지. 그러고 나면 언젠가는 점잖은 사회 구성원이 될지도 모르지.\""]], [["This being one of the standing jokes between the old fellows and Tim, they all three laughed very heartily, and might have laughed much longer, but that the brothers, seeing that Mrs.", "이건 노형제와 팀 사이의 단골 농담 가운데 하나였으므로, 셋 다 아주 신나게 웃었다."], ["Nickleby was labouring to express her feelings, and was really overwhelmed by the happiness of the time, took her between them, and led her from the room under pretence of having to consult her on some most important arrangements.", "훨씬 더 오래 웃을 수도 있었겠지만, 형제는 니클비 부인이 자기 감정을 표현하려고 안간힘을 쓰고 있고, 이 행복한 시간에 진심으로 벅차오른 걸 보았다. 그래서 부인을 두 사람 사이에 모시고, 더없이 중대한 어떤 준비에 대해 의논해야 한다는 핑계로 방에서 데리고 나갔다."]], [["Now, Tim and Miss La Creevy had met very often, and had always been very chatty and pleasant together--had always been great friends--and consequently it was the most natural thing in the world that Tim, finding that she still sobbed, should endeavour to console her. As Miss La Creevy sat on a large old-fashioned window-seat, where there was ample room for two, it was also natural that Tim should sit down beside her; and as to Tim’s being unusually spruce and particular in his attire that day, why it was a high festival and a great occasion, and that was the most natural thing of all.", "그런데 팀과 라 크리비 양은 그동안 아주 자주 만난 사이였다. 둘은 늘 수다스럽고 즐겁게 어울렸고, 늘 막역한 친구였다. 그러니 팀이 그녀가 여태 흐느끼는 걸 보고 달래 주려 애쓴 건 세상에서 가장 자연스러운 일이었다. 라 크리비 양이 둘이 앉아도 넉넉한 커다란 구식 창가 자리에 앉아 있었으니, 팀이 그 옆에 앉은 것도 자연스러운 일이었다. 그리고 그날 팀이 평소와 달리 옷차림에 유난히 신경 쓰고 멋을 부린 건 어떻고. 그야 큰 잔치이자 대단한 행사였으니, 그게 무엇보다도 가장 자연스러운 일이었다."]], [["Tim sat down beside Miss La Creevy, and, crossing one leg over the other so that his foot--he had very comely feet and happened to be wearing the neatest shoes and black silk stockings possible--should come easily within the range of her eye, said in a soothing way:", "팀은 라 크리비 양 옆에 앉아, 한쪽 다리를 다른 쪽 다리 위에 꼬았다. 그래야 자기 발이—그는 발 모양이 아주 잘생긴 데다, 마침 그날따라 더없이 말끔한 구두에 검은 비단 양말을 신고 있었다—그녀의 눈에 잘 들어올 테니까. 그러고는 달래는 투로 말했다."]], [["‘Don’t cry!’", "\"울지 마세요!\""]], [["‘I must,’ rejoined Miss La Creevy.", "\"우는 수밖에요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대꾸했다."]], [["‘No, don’t,’ said Tim.", "\"아니, 울지 마요.\" 팀이 말했다."], ["‘Please don’t; pray don’t.’", "\"제발 울지 말아요. 부디요.\""]], [["‘I am so happy!’ sobbed the little woman.", "\"제가 너무 행복해서요!\" 자그마한 여인이 흐느꼈다."]], [["‘Then laugh,’ said Tim.", "\"그럼 웃어요.\" 팀이 말했다."], ["‘Do laugh.’", "\"웃으셔야죠.\""]], [["What in the world Tim was doing with his arm, it is impossible to conjecture, but he knocked his elbow against that part of the window which was quite on the other side of Miss La Creevy; and it is clear that it could have no business there.", "팀이 도대체 자기 팔로 무얼 하고 있었는지는 짐작조차 할 수 없지만, 그가 팔꿈치로 창문을 쳤다. 그것도 라 크리비 양의 한참 반대편 쪽에 있는 부분을 말이다. 그쪽에 팔꿈치가 있을 일이 전혀 없었던 게 분명한데도."]], [["‘Do laugh,’ said Tim, ‘or I’ll cry.’", "\"웃으세요.\" 팀이 말했다. \"안 그러면 제가 울 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Why should you cry?’ asked Miss La Creevy, smiling.", "\"왜 우시려고요?\" 라 크리비 양이 미소 지으며 물었다."]], [["‘Because I’m happy too,’ said Tim.", "\"저도 행복하니까요.\" 팀이 말했다."], ["‘We are both happy, and I should like to do as you do.’", "\"우리 둘 다 행복하니, 저도 당신처럼 하고 싶거든요.\""]], [["Surely, there never was a man who fidgeted as Tim must have done then; for he knocked the window again--almost in the same place--and Miss La Creevy said she was sure he’d break it.", "분명 그때의 팀처럼 안절부절못한 남자는 또 없었으리라. 그가 또다시 창문을 쳤으니까. 거의 같은 자리를. 라 크리비 양은 그러다 창문 깨뜨리겠다고 했다."]], [["‘I knew,’ said Tim, ‘that you would be pleased with this scene.’", "\"저는 알았어요.\" 팀이 말했다. \"당신이 이 장면을 보고 기뻐하실 줄 말이죠.\""]], [["‘It was very thoughtful and kind to remember me,’ returned Miss La Creevy.", "\"저를 기억해 주시다니 참 사려 깊고 친절하셨어요.\" 라 크리비 양이 대꾸했다."], ["‘Nothing could have delighted me half so much.’", "\"이만큼 절 기쁘게 한 일은 또 없을 거예요.\""]], [["Why on earth should Miss La Creevy and Tim Linkinwater have said all this in a whisper?", "도대체 라 크리비 양과 팀 링킨워터는 무엇 때문에 이 모든 말을 속삭이며 했을까?"], ["It was no secret.", "비밀도 아니었는데."], ["And why should Tim Linkinwater have looked so hard at Miss La Creevy, and why should Miss La Creevy have looked so hard at the ground?", "그리고 팀 링킨워터는 왜 그렇게 라 크리비 양을 뚫어지게 바라봤으며, 라 크리비 양은 또 왜 그렇게 땅바닥을 뚫어지게 내려다봤을까?"]], [["‘It’s a pleasant thing,’ said Tim, ‘to people like us, who have passed all our lives in the world alone, to see young folks that we are fond of, brought together with so many years of happiness before them.’", "\"기쁜 일이지요.\" 팀이 말했다. \"우리처럼 평생을 세상에 혼자 지내 온 사람들한테는요. 우리가 아끼는 젊은이들이 맺어져서, 앞에 그 많은 행복한 세월을 두고 있는 걸 보는 게 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ cried the little woman with all her heart, ‘that it is!’", "\"아!\" 자그마한 여인이 진심을 담아 외쳤다. \"정말 그래요!\""]], [["‘Although,’ pursued Tim ‘although it makes one feel quite solitary and cast away.", "\"그래도 말이죠.\" 팀이 말을 이었다."], ["Now don’t it?’", "\"그래도 이러고 나면 영 외롭고 버림받은 기분이 들지 않나요? 안 그래요?\""]], [["Miss La Creevy said she didn’t know.", "라 크리비 양은 모르겠다고 했다."], ["And why should she say she didn’t know?", "한데 왜 모르겠다고 했을까?"], ["Because she must have known whether it did or not.", "그런지 안 그런지는 분명히 알고 있었을 텐데."]], [["‘It’s almost enough to make us get married after all, isn’t it?’ said Tim.", "\"이러다 우리도 결국 결혼하게 되는 거 아닐까요, 안 그래요?\" 팀이 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, nonsense!’ replied Miss La Creevy, laughing.", "\"오, 말도 안 돼요!\" 라 크리비 양이 웃으며 대꾸했다."], ["‘We are too old.’", "\"우린 너무 늙었는걸요.\""]], [["‘Not a bit,’ said Tim; ‘we are too old to be single.", "\"천만에요.\" 팀이 말했다."], ["Why shouldn’t we both be married, instead of sitting through the long winter evenings by our solitary firesides?", "\"혼자 살기엔 너무 늙었죠. 긴 겨울 저녁 내내 각자 외로운 난롯가에 앉아 있느니, 우리 둘 다 결혼하면 안 될 게 뭡니까?"], ["Why shouldn’t we make one fireside of it, and marry each other?’", "그걸 하나의 난롯가로 만들어서, 서로 결혼하면 안 될 게 뭐냐고요?\""]], [["‘Oh, Mr. Linkinwater, you’re joking!’", "\"오, 링킨워터 씨, 농담이시군요!\""]], [["‘No, no, I’m not.", "\"아니, 아니에요."], ["I’m not indeed,’ said Tim.", "정말 농담 아닙니다.\" 팀이 말했다."], ["‘I will, if you will.", "\"당신이 그러겠다면 저도 그러겠어요."], ["Do, my dear!’", "그래 줘요, 그대!\""]], [["‘It would make people laugh so.’", "\"사람들이 한참 웃을 거예요.\""]], [["‘Let ‘em laugh,’ cried Tim stoutly; ‘we have good tempers I know, and we’ll laugh too.", "\"웃으라죠.\" 팀이 씩씩하게 외쳤다."], ["Why, what hearty laughs we have had since we’ve known each other!’", "\"우린 성미가 좋으니까, 우리도 같이 웃으면 됩니다. 아니, 우리가 서로 알고 지낸 뒤로 얼마나 실컷 웃었습니까!\""]], [["‘So we have,’ cried Miss La Creevy--giving way a little, as Tim thought.", "\"그건 그래요.\" 라 크리비 양이 외쳤다. 팀이 보기엔 마음이 조금 누그러진 것 같았다."]], [["‘It has been the happiest time in all my life; at least, away from the counting-house and Cheeryble Brothers,’ said Tim.", "\"제 평생 가장 행복한 시간이었어요. 적어도 회계 사무실이랑 치어리블 형제분을 떠나서는요.\""], ["‘Do, my dear!", "팀이 말했다."], ["Now say you will.’", "\"그래 줘요, 그대! 어서 그러겠다고 해 줘요.\""]], [["‘No, no, we mustn’t think of it,’ returned Miss La Creevy.", "\"아니, 아니에요. 그런 생각은 하면 안 돼요.\""], ["‘What would the brothers say?’", "라 크리비 양이 대꾸했다. \"형제분이 뭐라고 하시겠어요?\""]], [["‘Why, God bless your soul!’ cried Tim, innocently, ‘you don’t suppose I should think of such a thing without their knowing it!", "\"아니, 이런 맙소사!\" 팀이 천연덕스럽게 외쳤다."], ["Why they left us here on purpose.’", "\"내가 그분들 모르게 그런 일을 생각이나 할 것 같아요? 아니, 그분들이 일부러 우릴 여기 남겨 두셨는데요.\""]], [["‘I can never look ‘em in the face again!’ exclaimed Miss La Creevy, faintly.", "\"전 다시는 그분들 얼굴을 못 볼 거예요!\" 라 크리비 양이 기어드는 소리로 외쳤다."]], [["‘Come,’ said Tim, ‘let’s be a comfortable couple.", "\"자, 봐요.\" 팀이 말했다."], ["We shall live in the old house here, where I have been for four-and-forty year; we shall go to the old church, where I’ve been, every Sunday morning, all through that time; we shall have all my old friends about us--Dick, the archway, the pump, the flower-pots, and Mr.", "\"우리 단란한 부부가 됩시다. 내가 사십사 년을 지낸 이 오래된 집에서 함께 살고, 그 세월 내내 일요일 아침마다 다닌 그 오래된 교회에 함께 가는 거예요."], ["Frank’s children, and Mr.", "내 오랜 벗들도 죄다 우리 곁에 있을 거고요."], ["Nickleby’s children, that we shall seem like grandfather and grandmother to.", "딕도, 아치형 통로도, 펌프도, 화분들도, 그리고 프랭크 씨의 아이들이랑 니콜라스 씨의 아이들도요."], ["Let’s be a comfortable couple, and take care of each other!", "우리는 그 애들한테 할아버지 할머니 같은 존재가 될 겁니다."], ["And if we should get deaf, or lame, or blind, or bed-ridden, how glad we shall be that we have somebody we are fond of, always to talk to and sit with!", "우리 단란한 부부가 되어서, 서로 돌봐 줍시다! 그리고 혹시 우리가 귀가 멀거나, 다리를 절거나, 눈이 멀거나, 자리에 눕게 되더라도, 늘 곁에서 이야기하고 함께 앉아 있어 줄, 아끼는 사람이 있다는 게 얼마나 기쁘겠어요!"], ["Let’s be a comfortable couple.", "우리 단란한 부부가 됩시다."], ["Now, do, my dear!’", "자, 그래 줘요, 그대!\""]], [["Five minutes after this honest and straightforward speech, little Miss La Creevy and Tim were talking as pleasantly as if they had been married for a score of years, and had never once quarrelled all the time; and five minutes after that, when Miss La Creevy had bustled out to see if her eyes were red and put her hair to rights, Tim moved with a stately step towards the drawing-room, exclaiming as he went, ‘There an’t such another woman in all London!", "이 솔직하고 거침없는 말이 끝나고 오 분 뒤, 자그마한 라 크리비 양과 팀은 마치 이십 년을 부부로 살면서 그동안 단 한 번도 다툰 적 없는 사이처럼 즐겁게 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 그리고 다시 오 분 뒤, 라 크리비 양이 눈이 빨갛지 않은지 살피고 머리를 매만지러 부산하게 나가자, 팀은 위풍당당한 걸음걸이로 응접실 쪽으로 향하며 가는 길에 이렇게 외쳤다."], ["I KNOW there an’t!’", "\"온 런던을 통틀어도 저런 여자는 또 없어! 없고말고, 내 알지!\""]], [["By this time, the apoplectic butler was nearly in fits, in consequence of the unheard-of postponement of dinner.", "이쯤 되니 졸도할 지경의 집사는 듣도 보도 못한 저녁 식사 연기 탓에 거의 발작이라도 일으킬 판이었다."], ["Nicholas, who had been engaged in a manner in which every reader may imagine for himself or herself, was hurrying downstairs in obedience to his angry summons, when he encountered a new surprise.", "니콜라스는, 독자들이 저마다 알아서 짐작할 만한 식으로 바쁜 시간을 보내다가, 그 성난 부름에 따라 서둘러 계단을 내려가던 참이었다. 그때 또 한 번 뜻밖의 일과 마주쳤다."]], [["On his way down, he overtook, in one of the passages, a stranger genteelly dressed in black, who was also moving towards the dining-room.", "내려가던 길에, 그는 복도 한 군데에서 검은 옷을 단정하게 차려입은 낯선 사람을 따라잡았다. 그 사람도 식당 쪽으로 가고 있었다."], ["As he was rather lame, and walked slowly, Nicholas lingered behind, and was following him step by step, wondering who he was, when he suddenly turned round and caught him by both hands.", "다리를 좀 저는지 천천히 걷는 통에, 니콜라스는 뒤에 처져 한 걸음 한 걸음 그를 따라가며 도대체 누구일까 궁금해하고 있었다. 그런데 그 사람이 갑자기 홱 돌아서서 니콜라스의 두 손을 덥석 잡았다."]], [["‘Newman Noggs!’ cried Nicholas joyfully", "\"뉴먼 노그스!\" 니콜라스가 기쁨에 차서 외쳤다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Newman, your own Newman, your own old faithful Newman!", "뉴먼, 자네의 뉴먼, 변함없이 충실한 자네의 옛 뉴먼이라네! 사랑하는 녀석, 사랑하는 닉, 기뻐하게."], ["My dear boy, my dear Nick, I give you joy--health, happiness, every blessing!", "건강도, 행복도, 온갖 축복도 다 누리게! 못 견디겠어."], ["I can’t bear it--it’s too much, my dear boy--it makes a child of me!’", "너무 벅차, 사랑하는 녀석아. 내가 다 어린애가 되는구나!\""]], [["‘Where have you been?’ said Nicholas.", "\"어디 계셨어요?\" 니콜라스가 말했다."], ["‘What have you been doing?", "\"뭘 하고 계셨고요?"], ["How often have I inquired for you, and been told that I should hear before long!’", "제가 얼마나 자주 아저씨를 찾았는데요. 그때마다 머지않아 소식을 듣게 될 거란 말만 들었다고요!\""]], [["‘I know, I know!’ returned Newman.", "\"알아, 알아!\" 뉴먼이 대꾸했다."], ["‘They wanted all the happiness to come together.", "\"다들 행복이 한꺼번에 오길 바랐거든."], ["I’ve been helping ‘em.", "나도 그분들을 거들고 있었어."], ["I--I--look at me, Nick, look at me!’", "나—나—날 좀 보게, 닉, 나를 좀 보라고!\""]], [["‘You would never let ME do that,’ said Nicholas in a tone of gentle reproach.", "\"저한텐 한 번도 그렇게 차려입게 해 주신 적 없잖아요.\" 니콜라스가 부드럽게 나무라는 투로 말했다."]], [["‘I didn’t mind what I was, then.", "\"그땐 내 꼴이 어떻든 상관없었어."], ["I shouldn’t have had the heart to put on gentleman’s clothes.", "신사 옷을 걸칠 엄두가 안 났거든."], ["They would have reminded me of old times and made me miserable.", "그런 옷은 옛날을 떠오르게 해서 날 비참하게 만들었을 테니까."], ["I am another man now, Nick.", "난 이제 딴사람이야, 닉."], ["My dear boy, I can’t speak.", "사랑하는 녀석아, 말이 안 나오는구나."], ["Don’t say anything to me.", "나한테 아무 말도 하지 마."], ["Don’t think the worse of me for these tears.", "이 눈물 때문에 날 나쁘게 보지도 말고."], ["You don’t know what I feel today; you can’t, and never will!’", "자넨 내가 오늘 어떤 기분인지 모를 거야. 알 수도 없고, 앞으로도 영영 모르겠지!\""]], [["They walked in to dinner arm-in-arm, and sat down side by side.", "두 사람은 팔짱을 끼고 저녁 식사 자리로 걸어 들어가, 나란히 앉았다."]], [["Never was such a dinner as that, since the world began. There was the superannuated bank clerk, Tim Linkinwater’s friend; and there was the chubby old lady, Tim Linkinwater’s sister; and there was so much attention from Tim Linkinwater’s sister to Miss La Creevy, and there were so many jokes from the superannuated bank clerk, and Tim Linkinwater himself was in such tiptop spirits, and little Miss La Creevy was in such a comical state, that of themselves they would have composed the pleasantest party conceivable. Then, there was Mrs Nickleby, so grand and complacent; Madeline and Kate, so blushing and beautiful; Nicholas and Frank, so devoted and proud; and all four so silently and tremblingly happy; there was Newman so subdued yet so overjoyed, and there were the twin brothers so delighted and interchanging such looks, that the old servant stood transfixed behind his master’s chair, and felt his eyes grow dim as they wandered round the table.", "세상이 시작된 이래 그런 만찬은 또 없었다. 팀 링킨워터의 친구인 퇴직한 은행 사무원이 있었고, 팀 링킨워터의 누이인 통통한 노부인도 있었다. 팀 링킨워터의 누이가 라 크리비 양에게 어찌나 살뜰히 마음을 쓰는지, 또 퇴직한 은행 사무원이 어찌나 농담을 많이 던지는지, 게다가 팀 링킨워터 본인은 또 어찌나 기분이 한껏 좋았는지, 자그마한 라 크리비 양은 어찌나 우스꽝스러운 모양새였는지, 이들만으로도 상상할 수 있는 가장 즐거운 모임이 이뤄졌을 정도였다. 게다가 니클비 부인은 또 얼마나 위엄 있고 흐뭇해했는지. 매덜린과 케이트는 또 얼마나 발그레하니 아름다웠는지. 니콜라스와 프랭크는 또 얼마나 헌신적이고 자랑스러워했는지. 그리고 그 넷은 또 얼마나 조용히, 또 떨리는 마음으로 행복해했는지. 뉴먼은 또 얼마나 차분하면서도 더없이 기뻐했는지. 그리고 쌍둥이 형제는 또 얼마나 기뻐하며 그런 눈빛을 주고받았는지, 늙은 하인은 주인의 의자 뒤에 못 박힌 듯 서서, 식탁을 빙 둘러보는 동안 두 눈이 흐릿해지는 걸 느낄 정도였다."]], [["When the first novelty of the meeting had worn off, and they began truly to feel how happy they were, the conversation became more general, and the harmony and pleasure if possible increased.", "만남의 첫 신기함이 가시고, 다들 자기들이 얼마나 행복한지 진정으로 느끼기 시작하자, 대화는 한층 두루두루 오갔고, 화목함과 즐거움도 가능하다면 더욱 커졌다."], ["The brothers were in a perfect ecstasy; and their insisting on saluting the ladies all round, before they would permit them to retire, gave occasion to the superannuated bank clerk to say so many good things, that he quite outshone himself, and was looked upon as a prodigy of humour.", "형제는 더없이 황홀해했다. 그들이 부인들을 물러가게 하기 전에 한 사람씩 다 입맞춤하겠다고 고집을 부리는 바람에, 퇴직한 은행 사무원은 재치 있는 말을 어찌나 많이 쏟아냈는지, 자기 자신을 완전히 능가해서 익살의 천재로 여겨질 지경이었다."]], [["‘Kate, my dear,’ said Mrs.", "\"케이트, 얘야.\" 니클비 부인이 위층으로 올라가자마자 딸을 한쪽으로 데려가며 말했다."], ["Nickleby, taking her daughter aside, as soon as they got upstairs, ‘you don’t really mean to tell me that this is actually true about Miss La Creevy and Mr. Linkinwater?’", "\"라 크리비 양이랑 링킨워터 씨 일이 정말로 사실이라고, 진짜로 그렇게 말하려는 건 아니겠지?\""]], [["‘Indeed it is, mama.’", "\"정말 사실이에요, 어머니.\""]], [["‘Why, I never heard such a thing in my life!’ exclaimed Mrs.", "\"아니, 내 평생 그런 얘긴 들어 본 적이 없다!\""], ["Nickleby.", "니클비 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"링킨워터 씨는 더없이 훌륭한 분이에요.\""], ["Linkinwater is a most excellent creature,’ reasoned Kate, ‘and, for his age, quite young still.’", "케이트가 조곤조곤 말했다. \"게다가 나이에 비하면 아직 아주 젊으신걸요.\""]], [["‘For HIS age, my dear!’ returned Mrs.", "\"그분 나이에 비하면, 얘야!\" 니클비 부인이 대꾸했다."], ["Nickleby, ‘yes; nobody says anything against him, except that I think he is the weakest and most foolish man I ever knew.", "\"그래, 그분을 두고 흉보는 사람은 없지. 내가 본 사람 중에 가장 줏대 없고 가장 어리석은 사람이란 것만 빼면 말이다."], ["It’s HER age I speak of.", "내가 말하는 건 그 여자 나이야."], ["That he should have gone and offered himself to a woman who must be--ah, half as old again as I am--and that she should have dared to accept him!", "그분이 가서 청혼을 했다는 게, 그것도 나보다—아, 나이가 절반은 더 많을 게 분명한 여자한테 말이야. 게다가 그 여자가 감히 그걸 받아들였다는 게!"], ["It don’t signify, Kate; I’m disgusted with her!’", "됐다, 케이트. 난 그 여자한테 정나미가 떨어졌어!\""]], [["Shaking her head very emphatically indeed, Mrs.", "니클비 부인은 고개를 정말이지 단호하게 가로젓고는 휑하니 나가 버렸다."], ["Nickleby swept away; and all the evening, in the midst of the merriment and enjoyment that ensued, and in which with that exception she freely participated, conducted herself towards Miss La Creevy in a stately and distant manner, designed to mark her sense of the impropriety of her conduct, and to signify her extreme and cutting disapprobation of the misdemeanour she had so flagrantly committed.", "그리고 그날 저녁 내내, 뒤이은 흥겨움과 즐거움 속에서, 그 한 가지만 빼고는 부인도 거리낌 없이 어울리면서도, 라 크리비 양에게만은 위엄 있고 쌀쌀맞게 굴었다. 그 여자의 처신이 얼마나 부적절한지를 똑똑히 알린다는 뜻이었고, 그 여자가 그토록 뻔뻔하게 저지른 비행에 대한 자신의 극심하고 매서운 못마땅함을 드러내려는 것이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 64", "# CHAPTER 64"]], [["An old Acquaintance is recognised under melancholy Circumstances, and Dotheboys Hall breaks up for ever\nNicholas was one of those whose joy is incomplete unless it is shared by the friends of adverse and less fortunate days.", "니콜라스는 어려운 시절, 덜 운 좋던 날들을 함께한 친구들과 나누지 않으면 기쁨이 온전치 못한 사람이었어. 사랑과 희망의 온갖 매력에 둘러싸여 있으면서도, 그의 따뜻한 마음은 수수한 존 브로디를 향해 가닿았지."], ["Surrounded by every fascination of love and hope, his warm heart yearned towards plain John Browdie.", "그는 처음 만났던 일을 미소로 떠올렸고, 두 번째 만남은 눈물로 떠올렸어."], ["He remembered their first meeting with a smile, and their second with a tear; saw poor Smike once again with the bundle on his shoulder trudging patiently by his side; and heard the honest Yorkshireman’s rough words of encouragement as he left them on their road to London.", "가엾은 스마이크가 어깨에 보따리를 메고 참을성 있게 곁에서 터덜터덜 걷던 모습이 다시 보였지. 그리고 런던으로 향하는 길에서 그들과 헤어지며 건넨, 그 정직한 요크셔 사내의 거친 격려의 말이 다시 들리는 것 같았어."]], [["Madeline and he sat down, very many times, jointly to produce a letter which should acquaint John at full length with his altered fortunes, and assure him of his friendship and gratitude.", "매들린과 그는 여러 번 함께 앉아 편지를 쓰려고 했어. 존에게 바뀐 자기 형편을 낱낱이 알리고, 우정과 고마움을 전하는 편지였지."], ["It so happened, however, that the letter could never be written.", "그런데 어쩐 일인지 그 편지는 끝내 써지지가 않았어. 세상에서 가장 좋은 마음으로 매달려 보았지만, 매번 다른 이야기로 빠져 버리곤 했거든."], ["Although they applied themselves to it with the best intentions in the world, it chanced that they always fell to talking about something else, and when Nicholas tried it by himself, he found it impossible to write one-half of what he wished to say, or to pen anything, indeed, which on reperusal did not appear cold and unsatisfactory compared with what he had in his mind.", "그리고 니콜라스가 혼자 써 보려고 해도, 하고 싶은 말의 절반도 적을 수가 없었어. 사실 무엇을 써 놓아도 다시 읽어 보면, 마음속에 품은 것에 비해 차갑고 못마땅하게만 보였지."], ["At last, after going on thus from day to day, and reproaching himself more and more, he resolved (the more readily as Madeline strongly urged him) to make a hasty trip into Yorkshire, and present himself before Mr.", "마침내 날마다 이렇게 끌면서 자꾸만 자신을 탓하던 끝에, 그는 마음을 정했어. 매들린이 강하게 권하기도 해서 더 선뜻 그러기로 했지."], ["and Mrs. Browdie without a word of notice.", "요크셔로 후딱 다녀오기로, 그리고 아무 기별도 없이 브로디 부부 앞에 불쑥 나타나기로 한 거야."]], [["Thus it was that between seven and eight o’clock one evening, he and Kate found themselves in the Saracen’s Head booking-office, securing a place to Greta Bridge by the next morning’s coach.", "그래서 어느 날 저녁 일곱 시에서 여덟 시 사이, 그와 케이트는 사라센스 헤드 매표소에 가 있었어. 다음 날 아침 마차로 그레타 다리까지 가는 자리를 잡으려고 말이야."], ["They had to go westward, to procure some little necessaries for his journey, and, as it was a fine night, they agreed to walk there, and ride home.", "두 사람은 여행에 필요한 자질구레한 것들을 사러 서쪽으로 가야 했어. 그리고 날이 좋아서, 갈 때는 걷고 올 때는 마차를 타기로 했지."]], [["The place they had just been in called up so many recollections, and Kate had so many anecdotes of Madeline, and Nicholas so many anecdotes of Frank, and each was so interested in what the other said, and both were so happy and confiding, and had so much to talk about, that it was not until they had plunged for a full half-hour into that labyrinth of streets which lies between Seven Dials and Soho, without emerging into any large thoroughfare, that Nicholas began to think it just possible they might have lost their way.", "방금 다녀온 곳이 워낙 많은 추억을 불러일으켰어. 케이트는 매들린 얘기를 잔뜩 늘어놓았고, 니콜라스는 프랭크 얘기를 잔뜩 늘어놓았지. 서로 상대방의 말에 푹 빠져 있었고, 둘 다 너무 행복하고 마음이 통했고, 할 얘기가 너무 많았어. 그러다 보니 두 사람은 세븐 다이얼스와 소호 사이에 얽힌 그 골목 미로 속을 꼬박 삼십 분이나 헤매 들어가고서도 큰길 하나 나오질 않았어. 그제야 니콜라스는 길을 잃었을지도 모른다는 생각이 들기 시작했지."]], [["The possibility was soon converted into a certainty; for, on looking about, and walking first to one end of the street and then to the other, he could find no landmark he could recognise, and was fain to turn back again in quest of some place at which he could seek a direction.", "그 가능성은 곧 확실한 사실이 되어 버렸어. 주위를 둘러보고, 길 이쪽 끝까지 갔다가 저쪽 끝까지 가 봐도, 알아볼 만한 표지 하나 찾을 수가 없었거든. 그래서 그는 길을 물어볼 만한 데를 찾아 다시 발길을 돌릴 수밖에 없었지."]], [["It was a by-street, and there was nobody about, or in the few wretched shops they passed. Making towards a faint gleam of light which streamed across the pavement from a cellar, Nicholas was about to descend two or three steps so as to render himself visible to those below and make his inquiry, when he was arrested by a loud noise of scolding in a woman’s voice.", "거기는 뒷골목이었어. 주위에도 사람이 없었고, 지나치는 몇 안 되는 초라한 가게에도 사람이 없었지. 지하실에서 인도 위로 희미한 빛 한 줄기가 새어 나오고 있었어. 니콜라스는 그쪽으로 다가갔지. 아래에 있는 사람들 눈에 자기 모습이 보이도록 두세 계단을 내려가 길을 물으려는 참이었어. 그런데 여자 목소리로 큰 소리로 야단치는 소리가 들려 그는 멈칫했지."]], [["‘Oh come away!’ said Kate, ‘they are quarrelling.", "\"아, 그냥 가요!\" 케이트가 말했어."], ["You’ll be hurt.’", "\"싸우고 있잖아요. 오빠가 다칠지도 몰라요.\""]], [["‘Wait one instant, Kate.", "\"잠깐만, 케이트."], ["Let us hear if there’s anything the matter,’ returned her brother.", "무슨 일인지 한번 들어 보자.\" 오빠가 대꾸했어."], ["‘Hush!’", "\"쉿!\""]], [["‘You nasty, idle, vicious, good-for-nothing brute,’ cried the woman, stamping on the ground, ‘why don’t you turn the mangle?’", "\"이 더럽고 게으르고 못돼 처먹은 쓸모없는 짐승아.\" 여자가 발을 쿵쿵 구르며 소리쳤어. \"왜 빨래 짜는 기계를 안 돌리는 거야?\""]], [["‘So I am, my life and soul!’ replied the man’s voice.", "\"돌리고 있잖아, 내 목숨, 내 영혼아!\" 남자 목소리가 대답했어."], ["‘I am always turning.", "\"나는 늘 돌리고 있어."], ["I am perpetually turning, like a demd old horse in a demnition mill.", "빌어먹을 방앗간에서 빌어먹을 늙은 말처럼 끝도 없이 돌리고 있다고."], ["My life is one demd horrid grind!’", "내 인생은 그저 빌어먹게 끔찍한 노동이야!\""]], [["‘Then why don’t you go and list for a soldier?’ retorted the woman; ‘you’re welcome to.’", "\"그럼 군대나 가서 입대해 버리지 그래?\" 여자가 쏘아붙였어. \"얼마든지 가도 돼.\""]], [["‘For a soldier!’ cried the man.", "\"군대라니!\" 남자가 소리쳤어."], ["‘For a soldier!", "\"군대라고!"], ["Would his joy and gladness see him in a coarse red coat with a little tail?", "그대의 기쁨이자 즐거움인 이 사람이, 꽁지 달린 거친 빨간 외투를 입은 꼴을 보고 싶어?"], ["Would she hear of his being slapped and beat by drummers demnebly?", "북 치는 놈들한테 빌어먹게 얻어맞고 두들겨 맞는 소리를 듣고 싶어?"], ["Would she have him fire off real guns, and have his hair cut, and his whiskers shaved, and his eyes turned right and left, and his trousers pipeclayed?’", "진짜 총을 쏘게 하고, 머리를 깎이고, 콧수염을 밀리고, 두 눈을 좌우로 굴리게 하고, 바지에 흰 가루칠이나 하게 하고 싶다는 거야?\""]], [["‘Dear Nicholas,’ whispered Kate, ‘you don’t know who that is.", "\"니콜라스 오빠.\" 케이트가 속삭였어."], ["It’s Mr Mantalini I am confident.’", "\"저 사람이 누군지 모르죠. 만탈리니 씨가 분명해요.\""]], [["‘Do make sure!", "\"확인해 봐!"], ["Peep at him while I ask the way,’ said Nicholas.", "내가 길을 묻는 동안 슬쩍 들여다보렴.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["‘Come down a step or two.", "\"한두 계단만 내려와."], ["Come!’", "자!\""]], [["Drawing her after him, Nicholas crept down the steps and looked into a small boarded cellar. There, amidst clothes-baskets and clothes, stripped up to his shirt-sleeves, but wearing still an old patched pair of pantaloons of superlative make, a once brilliant waistcoat, and moustache and whiskers as of yore, but lacking their lustrous dye--there, endeavouring to mollify the wrath of a buxom female--not the lawful Madame Mantalini, but the proprietress of the concern--and grinding meanwhile as if for very life at the mangle, whose creaking noise, mingled with her shrill tones, appeared almost to deafen him--there was the graceful, elegant, fascinating, and once dashing Mantalini.", "니콜라스는 케이트를 끌어당기며 계단을 살금살금 내려가, 판자로 둘러친 작은 지하실 안을 들여다봤어. 거기, 빨래 바구니와 옷가지 틈에서, 셔츠 소매까지 걷어붙이고 있는 사내가 있었지. 그래도 여전히 더없이 잘 만들어진 낡고 기운 바지를 입고 있었고, 한때 화려했던 조끼를 걸치고 있었어. 콧수염과 구레나룻도 예전 그대로였지만, 윤기 흐르던 염색만은 빠져 있었지. 거기, 풍만한 여자의 화를 달래려고 애쓰는 사내가 있었어. 그 여자는 정식 부인인 만탈리니 부인이 아니라, 그 가게의 주인이었지. 그러면서 그는 죽을힘을 다하듯 빨래 짜는 기계를 돌리고 있었어. 기계의 삐걱대는 소리가 여자의 새된 목소리와 뒤섞여, 그의 귀를 거의 멀게 할 지경이었지. 거기, 우아하고 멋스럽고 매혹적이며 한때 그토록 멋들어졌던 만탈리니가 있었어."]], [["‘Oh you false traitor!’ cried the lady, threatening personal violence on Mr.", "\"이 거짓말쟁이 배신자!\""], ["Mantalini’s face.", "여자가 만탈리니 씨의 얼굴에 손찌검을 하려고 들면서 소리쳤어."]], [["‘False! Oh dem!", "\"거짓말쟁이라니!"], ["Now my soul, my gentle, captivating, bewitching, and most demnebly enslaving chick-a-biddy, be calm,’ said Mr.", "아, 제기랄! 자, 내 영혼아, 내 다정하고 사람 홀리고 마음 빼앗고 빌어먹게도 나를 사로잡는 귀여운 병아리야, 진정해.\""], ["Mantalini, humbly.", "만탈리니 씨가 굽실거리며 말했어."]], [["‘I won’t!’ screamed the woman.", "\"싫어!\" 여자가 비명을 질렀어."], ["‘I’ll tear your eyes out!’", "\"네 눈깔을 후벼 파 버릴 거야!\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["What a demd savage lamb!’ cried Mr.", "이 무슨 빌어먹게 사나운 양 같으니!\""], ["Mantalini.", "만탈리니 씨가 소리쳤어."]], [["‘You’re never to be trusted,’ screamed the woman; ‘you were out all day yesterday, and gallivanting somewhere I know. You know you were! Isn’t it enough that I paid two pound fourteen for you, and took you out of prison and let you live here like a gentleman, but must you go on like this: breaking my heart besides?’", "\"당신은 도무지 믿을 수가 없어.\" 여자가 비명을 질렀어. \"어제는 하루 종일 나가 있었지. 어디서 시시덕거리고 다닌 거 다 알아. 당신도 알잖아! 내가 당신 빼내려고 이 파운드 십사 실링이나 내고, 감옥에서 꺼내서, 여기서 신사처럼 살게 해 줬는데, 그걸로 모자라서 이렇게 굴어야겠어? 게다가 내 가슴까지 찢어 놓으면서?\""]], [["‘I will never break its heart, I will be a good boy, and never do so any more; I will never be naughty again; I beg its little pardon,’ said Mr.", "\"절대로 그 마음을 찢어 놓지 않을게. 착한 아이가 될게."], ["Mantalini, dropping the handle of the mangle, and folding his palms together; ‘it is all up with its handsome friend!", "다시는 안 그럴게. 다시는 못된 짓 안 할게."], ["He has gone to the demnition bow-wows.", "그 작은 마음에 용서를 빌게.\" 만탈리니 씨가 빨래 기계 손잡이를 놓고 두 손바닥을 모으며 말했어."], ["It will have pity?", "\"이 잘생긴 친구는 이제 끝장이 났어! 빌어먹을 개죽음이나 당하게 됐다고."], ["It will not scratch and claw, but pet and comfort?", "불쌍히 여겨 주겠지? 할퀴고 쥐어뜯지 말고, 쓰다듬고 달래 주겠지?"], ["Oh, demmit!’", "아, 제기랄!\""]], [["Very little affected, to judge from her action, by this tender appeal, the lady was on the point of returning some angry reply, when Nicholas, raising his voice, asked his way to Piccadilly.", "여자는 이 애틋한 호소에 거의 마음이 움직이지 않은 듯했어. 그 몸짓으로 봐서는 말이야. 막 화난 대답을 쏘아붙이려는 참이었지. 그때 니콜라스가 목소리를 높여 피카딜리로 가는 길을 물었어."]], [["Mr.", "만탈리니 씨가 몸을 돌렸어."], ["Mantalini turned round, caught sight of Kate, and, without another word, leapt at one bound into a bed which stood behind the door, and drew the counterpane over his face: kicking meanwhile convulsively.", "케이트를 보더니, 더는 한마디도 없이 문 뒤에 놓인 침대로 단번에 풀쩍 뛰어들었지. 그러고는 이불을 끌어다 얼굴을 덮고, 그러는 내내 발버둥을 쳐 댔어."]], [["‘Demmit,’ he cried, in a suffocating voice, ‘it’s little Nickleby!", "\"제기랄.\" 그가 숨 막히는 목소리로 외쳤어."], ["Shut the door, put out the candle, turn me up in the bedstead!", "\"꼬마 니클비잖아! 문 닫아, 촛불 꺼, 나를 침대 속에 처박아 줘!"], ["Oh, dem, dem, dem!’", "아, 제기, 제기, 제기랄!\""]], [["The woman looked, first at Nicholas, and then at Mr. Mantalini, as if uncertain on whom to visit this extraordinary behaviour; but Mr Mantalini happening by ill-luck to thrust his nose from under the bedclothes, in his anxiety to ascertain whether the visitors were gone, she suddenly, and with a dexterity which could only have been acquired by long practice, flung a pretty heavy clothes-basket at him, with so good an aim that he kicked more violently than before, though without venturing to make any effort to disengage his head, which was quite extinguished. Thinking this a favourable opportunity for departing before any of the torrent of her wrath discharged itself upon him, Nicholas hurried Kate off, and left the unfortunate subject of this unexpected recognition to explain his conduct as he best could.", "여자는 먼저 니콜라스를 보고, 그다음 만탈리니 씨를 봤어. 이 해괴한 짓을 누구한테 따져야 할지 갈피를 못 잡는 모양이었지. 그런데 만탈리니 씨가 운 나쁘게도 손님들이 갔는지 확인하고 싶은 마음에 이불 밑에서 코를 쑥 내밀었어. 그러자 여자는 느닷없이, 오랜 연습으로만 익혔을 법한 솜씨로, 제법 묵직한 빨래 바구니를 그에게 집어 던졌지. 어찌나 정확히 맞혔던지 그는 아까보다 더 사납게 발버둥을 쳤어. 하지만 완전히 파묻혀 버린 머리를 빼낼 엄두는 내지 못했지. 니콜라스는 여자의 분노가 쏟아져 내리기 전에 떠날 좋은 기회라고 생각했어. 그래서 케이트를 서둘러 데리고 나왔지. 이 뜻밖의 재회로 곤경에 빠진 가엾은 사내가 알아서 제 처신을 둘러대도록 내버려 두고 말이야."]], [["The next morning he began his journey. It was now cold, winter weather: forcibly recalling to his mind under what circumstances he had first travelled that road, and how many vicissitudes and changes he had since undergone. He was alone inside the greater part of the way, and sometimes, when he had fallen into a doze, and, rousing himself, looked out of the window, and recognised some place which he well remembered as having passed, either on his journey down, or in the long walk back with poor Smike, he could hardly believe but that all which had since happened had been a dream, and that they were still plodding wearily on towards London, with the world before them.", "다음 날 아침 그는 길을 떠났어. 이제 날은 춥고 겨울 날씨였지. 그 길을 처음 갔을 때 어떤 처지였는지, 그 뒤로 얼마나 많은 우여곡절과 변화를 겪었는지가 새삼 또렷이 떠올랐어. 그는 길의 대부분을 마차 안에 혼자 있었지. 가끔 깜빡 졸다가 정신을 차리고 창밖을 내다보면, 잘 기억하는 곳이 눈에 들어왔어. 내려갈 때 지났거나, 가엾은 스마이크와 함께 오래 걸어 돌아올 때 지났던 곳이었지. 그럴 때면 그 뒤로 일어난 모든 일이 한낱 꿈만 같았어. 자기들은 여전히 세상을 앞에 두고 런던을 향해 지쳐 터벅터벅 걷고 있는 것만 같았지."]], [["To render these recollections the more vivid, it came on to snow as night set in; and, passing through Stamford and Grantham, and by the little alehouse where he had heard the story of the bold Baron of Grogzwig, everything looked as if he had seen it but yesterday, and not even a flake of the white crust on the roofs had melted away. Encouraging the train of ideas which flocked upon him, he could almost persuade himself that he sat again outside the coach, with Squeers and the boys; that he heard their voices in the air; and that he felt again, but with a mingled sensation of pain and pleasure now, that old sinking of the heart, and longing after home. While he was yet yielding himself up to these fancies he fell asleep, and, dreaming of Madeline, forgot them.", "이런 추억을 더 생생하게 만들려는 듯, 밤이 깃들자 눈이 내리기 시작했어. 스탬퍼드와 그랜섬을 지나고, 그로그즈위그의 용감한 남작 이야기를 들었던 작은 선술집을 지나칠 때, 모든 것이 어제 본 듯이 그대로였지. 지붕에 쌓인 흰 눈 껍질은 한 조각도 녹지 않은 것 같았어. 떼지어 몰려드는 생각들을 그대로 두자, 그는 거의 이렇게 믿을 뻔했어. 자기가 다시 마차 바깥에 스퀴어스며 아이들과 함께 앉아 있다고, 허공에 그들의 목소리가 들린다고 말이야. 그리고 그 옛날의 가라앉던 마음과 집을 향한 그리움이 다시 느껴졌어. 다만 지금은 아픔과 즐거움이 뒤섞인 느낌이었지. 이런 공상에 빠져들던 중에 그는 잠이 들었고, 매들린의 꿈을 꾸며 그것들을 잊어버렸어."]], [["He slept at the inn at Greta Bridge on the night of his arrival, and, rising at a very early hour next morning, walked to the market town, and inquired for John Browdie’s house.", "그는 도착한 날 밤을 그레타 다리의 여관에서 묵었어. 다음 날 아침 아주 이른 시각에 일어나 장이 서는 마을까지 걸어가, 존 브로디의 집을 물었지."], ["John lived in the outskirts, now he was a family man; and as everbody knew him, Nicholas had no difficulty in finding a boy who undertook to guide him to his residence.", "존은 이제 가정을 꾸린 몸이라 마을 외곽에 살고 있었어. 다들 그를 알았기 때문에, 니콜라스는 그 집까지 안내해 주겠다는 소년 하나를 어렵잖게 찾았지."]], [["Dismissing his guide at the gate, and in his impatience not even stopping to admire the thriving look of cottage or garden either, Nicholas made his way to the kitchen door, and knocked lustily with his stick.", "대문 앞에서 안내해 준 소년을 보내고, 니콜라스는 조급한 마음에 아담한 집이나 정원의 풍성한 모습을 감상할 새도 없이 부엌문 쪽으로 다가갔어. 그러고는 지팡이로 힘껏 문을 두드렸지."]], [["‘Halloa!’ cried a voice inside.", "\"이봐!\" 안에서 목소리가 외쳤어."], ["‘Wa’et be the matther noo?", "\"대체 무슨 일이여?"], ["Be the toon a-fire?", "마을에 불이라도 난 겨?"], ["Ding, but thou mak’st noise eneaf!’", "어이쿠, 무슨 소리를 그리 시끄럽게 내고 그려!\""]], [["With these words, John Browdie opened the door himself, and opening his eyes too to their utmost width, cried, as he clapped his hands together, and burst into a hearty roar:", "이렇게 외치며 존 브로디가 직접 문을 열었어. 그러고는 두 눈도 한껏 크게 뜨더니, 두 손을 탁 마주치며 호탕하게 웃음을 터뜨리며 소리쳤지."]], [["‘Ecod, it be the godfeyther, it be the godfeyther!", "\"이런, 대부님이여, 대부님이여!"], ["Tilly, here be Misther Nickleby.", "틸리, 여기 니클비 씨가 오셨어."], ["Gi’ us thee hond, mun.", "손 좀 줘 봐, 이 사람아."], ["Coom awa’, coom awa’.", "어여 와, 어여 와."], ["In wi ‘un, doon beside the fire; tak’ a soop o’ thot.", "들어와, 불가에 앉아. 이거 한 모금 마셔."], ["Dinnot say a word till thou’st droonk it a’!", "다 마실 때까정 한마디도 하지 말어!"], ["Oop wi’ it, mun. Ding!", "쭉 들이켜, 이 사람아."], ["but I’m reeght glod to see thee.’", "어이구! 자네를 보니께 참말로 반갑네그려.\""]], [["Adapting his action to his text, John dragged Nicholas into the kitchen, forced him down upon a huge settle beside a blazing fire, poured out from an enormous bottle about a quarter of a pint of spirits, thrust it into his hand, opened his mouth and threw back his head as a sign to him to drink it instantly, and stood with a broad grin of welcome overspreading his great red face like a jolly giant.", "존은 말에 맞게 곧장 행동에 옮겼어. 니콜라스를 부엌으로 끌고 들어가, 활활 타오르는 불가의 커다란 긴 의자에 억지로 앉혔지. 그러고는 엄청나게 큰 병에서 술을 사분의 일 파인트쯤 따라 그의 손에 쥐여 줬어. 당장 마시라는 신호로 입을 떡 벌리고 고개를 뒤로 젖혀 보였지. 그러고는 그 큼지막한 붉은 얼굴에 환영의 미소를 활짝 띤 채, 유쾌한 거인처럼 서 있었어."]], [["‘I might ha’ knowa’d,’ said John, ‘that nobody but thou would ha’ coom wi’ sike a knock as you.", "\"나는 알아봤어야 혔는디.\" 존이 말했어."], ["Thot was the wa’ thou knocked at schoolmeasther’s door, eh?", "\"그렇게 두드리고 들어올 사람은 자네밖에 없다는 걸 말여. 그게 자네가 교장 선생 문 두드리던 식 아녀, 응?"], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하하하!"], ["But I say; wa’at be a’ this aboot schoolmeasther?’", "그나저나 말여, 교장 선생 얘기는 다 어찌 된 겨?\""]], [["‘You know it then?’ said Nicholas.", "\"그럼 그 일을 아시나요?\" 니콜라스가 물었어."]], [["‘They were talking aboot it, doon toon, last neeght,’ replied John, ‘but neane on ‘em seemed quite to un’erstan’ it, loike.’", "\"어젯밤에 마을에서 다들 그 얘기를 허고 있더라고.\" 존이 대답했어. \"그런디 아무도 영 제대로 알아듣질 못하는 것 같더라고, 어째.\""]], [["‘After various shiftings and delays,’ said Nicholas, ‘he has been sentenced to be transported for seven years, for being in the unlawful possession of a stolen will; and, after that, he has to suffer the consequence of a conspiracy.’", "\"여러 번 미뤄지고 끌었지만요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어. \"훔친 유언장을 불법으로 가지고 있던 죄로 칠 년 유배형을 선고받았어요. 그리고 그 뒤엔 음모를 꾸민 죗값까지 치러야 하고요.\""]], [["‘Whew!’ cried John, ‘a conspiracy!", "\"휘유!\" 존이 외쳤어."], ["Soom’at in the pooder-plot wa’?", "\"음모라고! 화약 음모 같은 거여?"], ["Eh?", "응?"], ["Soom’at in the Guy Faux line?’", "가이 포크스 부류의 그런 거?\""]], [["‘No, no, no, a conspiracy connected with his school; I’ll explain it presently.’", "\"아뇨, 아뇨, 아뇨, 그 학교와 얽힌 음모예요. 이따 설명해 드릴게요.\""]], [["‘Thot’s reeght!’ said John, ‘explain it arter breakfast, not noo, for thou be’est hoongry, and so am I; and Tilly she mun’ be at the bottom o’ a’ explanations, for she says thot’s the mutual confidence. Ha, ha, ha! Ecod, it’s a room start, is the mutual confidence!’", "\"그게 좋겄어!\" 존이 말했어. \"아침 먹고 설명혀, 지금 말고. 자네도 배고프고 나도 배고프니께. 그리고 틸리가 모든 설명에 끼어 있어야 혀. 그게 부부간의 믿음이라고 그러거든. 하하하! 어이구, 그 부부간의 믿음이란 게 참 별난 거여!\""]], [["The entrance of Mrs. Browdie, with a smart cap on, and very many apologies for their having been detected in the act of breakfasting in the kitchen, stopped John in his discussion of this grave subject, and hastened the breakfast: which, being composed of vast mounds of toast, new-laid eggs, boiled ham, Yorkshire pie, and other cold substantials (of which heavy relays were constantly appearing from another kitchen under the direction of a very plump servant), was admirably adapted to the cold bleak morning, and received the utmost justice from all parties. At last, it came to a close; and the fire which had been lighted in the best parlour having by this time burnt up, they adjourned thither, to hear what Nicholas had to tell.", "브로디 부인이 깜찍한 모자를 쓰고 들어왔어. 부엌에서 아침을 먹다 들킨 걸 두고 거듭거듭 변명을 늘어놓으면서 말이야. 그 바람에 존은 이 진지한 화제에 대한 이야기를 멈추고 아침상을 서둘렀지. 아침상은 산더미 같은 토스트, 갓 낳은 달걀, 삶은 햄, 요크셔 파이, 그 밖에 차가운 든든한 음식들로 차려졌어. 아주 통통한 하녀의 손에서 그런 음식들이 다른 부엌으로부터 묵직하게 끊임없이 추가로 날라져 왔지. 춥고 황량한 아침에 더없이 어울리는 상이었고, 모두에게 톡톡히 대접받았어. 마침내 식사가 끝났지. 그동안 제일 좋은 응접실에 피워 둔 불이 활활 타올라서, 그들은 그쪽으로 자리를 옮겼어. 니콜라스가 들려줄 이야기를 들으려고 말이야."]], [["Nicholas told them all, and never was there a story which awakened so many emotions in the breasts of two eager listeners. At one time, honest John groaned in sympathy, and at another roared with joy; at one time he vowed to go up to London on purpose to get a sight of the brothers Cheeryble; and, at another, swore that Tim Linkinwater should receive such a ham by coach, and carriage free, as mortal knife had never carved. When Nicholas began to describe Madeline, he sat with his mouth wide open, nudging Mrs. Browdie from time to time, and exclaiming under his breath that she must be ‘raa’ther a tidy sart,’ and when he heard at last that his young friend had come down purposely to communicate his good fortune, and to convey to him all those assurances of friendship which he could not state with sufficient warmth in writing--that the only object of his journey was to share his happiness with them, and to tell them that when he was married they must come up to see him, and that Madeline insisted on it as well as he--John could hold out no longer, but after looking indignantly at his wife, and demanding to know what she was whimpering for, drew his coat sleeve over his eyes and blubbered outright.", "니콜라스는 모든 것을 다 이야기했어. 두 명의 열성적인 청중의 가슴에 그토록 많은 감정을 불러일으킨 이야기는 일찍이 없었지. 어떤 대목에서는 정직한 존이 공감하며 끙끙 앓는 소리를 냈고, 또 어떤 대목에서는 기뻐서 큰 소리로 웃었어. 어떤 때는 일부러 런던에 올라가 치어리블 형제를 한번 보겠노라 다짐했고, 또 어떤 때는 팀 링킨워터에게 마차로 운임까지 다 내고 햄을 보내 주겠다고 맹세했지. 인간의 칼로는 한 번도 썰어 본 적 없을 만큼 큰 햄을 말이야. 니콜라스가 매들린 이야기를 시작하자, 존은 입을 떡 벌린 채 앉아서 이따금 브로디 부인을 쿡쿡 찌르며 나직이 외쳤어. 그 아가씨가 \"꽤 괜찮은 처자\"임에 틀림없다고 말이야. 그리고 마침내 그 젊은 친구가 자기 행운을 알리고, 글로는 충분히 따뜻하게 전할 수 없던 그 모든 우정의 다짐을 전하려고 일부러 내려왔다는 말을 들었을 때, 이번 길의 유일한 목적이 자기 행복을 그들과 나누고, 결혼하면 꼭 올라와서 봐 달라고, 매들린도 자기만큼이나 그러길 바란다고 전하려는 것임을 들었을 때, 존은 더 이상 참지 못했어. 아내를 못마땅한 듯 노려보고는 대체 뭘 훌쩍거리느냐고 따져 묻더니, 외투 소매를 두 눈에 갖다 대고 엉엉 울어 버렸지."]], [["‘Tell’ee wa’at though,’ said John seriously, when a great deal had been said on both sides, ‘to return to schoolmeasther. If this news aboot ‘un has reached school today, the old ‘ooman wean’t have a whole boan in her boddy, nor Fanny neither.’", "\"근디 말여.\" 양쪽에서 많은 이야기가 오간 뒤, 존이 진지하게 말했어. \"교장 선생 얘기로 돌아가서. 만약에 그놈 소식이 오늘 학교에 닿았으면, 그 늙은 마누라는 성한 뼈 하나 안 남을 거여. 패니도 마찬가지고.\""]], [["‘Oh, John!’ cried Mrs.", "\"아, 존!\""], ["Browdie.", "브로디 부인이 외쳤어."]], [["‘Ah! and Oh, John agean,’ replied the Yorkshireman.", "\"아, 아, 존이라고 또!\""], ["‘I dinnot know what they lads mightn’t do.", "요크셔 사내가 대꾸했어."], ["When it first got aboot that schoolmeasther was in trouble, some feythers and moothers sent and took their young chaps awa’.", "\"나는 그 녀석들이 무슨 짓을 할지 모른다 그 말여. 교장 선생이 곤경에 빠졌단 소문이 처음 돌았을 때, 어떤 부모들은 사람을 보내 제 자식들을 데려갔어."], ["If them as is left, should know waat’s coom tiv’un, there’ll be sike a revolution and rebel!--Ding!", "남은 녀석들이 그놈한테 무슨 일이 닥쳤는지 알면, 그런 난리와 반란이 일어날 거여! 어이구!"], ["But I think they’ll a’ gang daft, and spill bluid like wather!’", "내 생각엔 죄다 미쳐 가지고, 물처럼 피를 쏟아 낼 것 같다니께!\""]], [["In fact, John Browdie’s apprehensions were so strong that he determined to ride over to the school without delay, and invited Nicholas to accompany him, which, however, he declined, pleading that his presence might perhaps aggravate the bitterness of their adversity.", "사실 존 브로디는 어찌나 걱정이 컸던지, 지체 없이 학교로 말을 달려가기로 마음먹었어. 그러고는 니콜라스에게 같이 가자고 했지. 그런데 니콜라스는 거절했어. 자기가 나타나면 어쩌면 그들의 불행에 쓰라림만 더할지도 모른다는 핑계를 댔지."]], [["‘Thot’s true!’ said John; ‘I should ne’er ha’ thought o’ thot.’", "\"그건 맞는 말이여!\" 존이 말했어. \"나는 그 생각을 미처 못 혔네.\""]], [["‘I must return tomorrow,’ said Nicholas, ‘but I mean to dine with you today, and if Mrs.", "\"저는 내일 돌아가야 해요.\" 니콜라스가 말했어."], ["Browdie can give me a bed--’", "\"하지만 오늘은 두 분과 함께 저녁을 먹으려고요. 그리고 브로디 부인께서 잠자리를 내주실 수 있다면—\""]], [["‘Bed!’ cried John, ‘I wish thou couldst sleep in fower beds at once. Ecod, thou shouldst have ‘em a’. Bide till I coom back; on’y bide till I coom back, and ecod we’ll make a day of it.’", "\"잠자리!\" 존이 외쳤어. \"자네가 침대 네 개에서 한꺼번에 잘 수 있으면 좋겄구먼. 어이구, 그럼 다 내주지. 내가 돌아올 때까정 있어. 돌아올 때까정만 있어. 그럼 어이구, 우리 하루 진하게 보내 보자고.\""]], [["Giving his wife a hearty kiss, and Nicholas a no less hearty shake of the hand, John mounted his horse and rode off: leaving Mrs.", "존은 아내에게 진하게 입을 맞추고, 니콜라스에게는 그에 못지않게 진하게 손을 흔들어 잡았어. 그러고는 말에 올라타 달려갔지."], ["Browdie to apply herself to hospitable preparations, and his young friend to stroll about the neighbourhood, and revisit spots which were rendered familiar to him by many a miserable association.", "브로디 부인은 손님맞이 준비에 매달리도록 남겨 두고, 그 젊은 친구는 동네를 거닐며 수많은 비참한 기억으로 익숙해진 곳들을 다시 둘러보도록 두고 말이야."]], [["John cantered away, and arriving at Dotheboys Hall, tied his horse to a gate and made his way to the schoolroom door, which he found locked on the inside. A tremendous noise and riot arose from within, and, applying his eye to a convenient crevice in the wall, he did not remain long in ignorance of its meaning.", "존은 가볍게 말을 달려 갔어. 두더보이즈 홀에 다다르자 말을 대문에 매어 두고 교실 문 쪽으로 갔지. 그런데 문은 안쪽에서 잠겨 있었어. 안에서 엄청난 소음과 소란이 일고 있었지. 벽에 난 적당한 틈에 눈을 갖다 대니, 그게 무슨 영문인지 알아채는 데는 오래 걸리지 않았어."]], [["The news of Mr.", "스퀴어스 씨가 몰락했다는 소식이 두더보이즈에 닿은 거야."], ["Squeers’s downfall had reached Dotheboys; that was quite clear.", "그건 아주 분명했어."], ["To all appearance, it had very recently become known to the young gentlemen; for the rebellion had just broken out.", "보아하니 그 소식은 도련님들에게 아주 막 알려진 모양이었지. 반란이 방금 터졌으니 말이야."]], [["It was one of the brimstone-and-treacle mornings, and Mrs.", "그날은 유황과 당밀을 먹이는 아침 가운데 하루였어."], ["Squeers had entered school according to custom with the large bowl and spoon, followed by Miss Squeers and the amiable Wackford: who, during his father’s absence, had taken upon him such minor branches of the executive as kicking the pupils with his nailed boots, pulling the hair of some of the smaller boys, pinching the others in aggravating places, and rendering himself, in various similar ways, a great comfort and happiness to his mother.", "스퀴어스 부인이 늘 하던 대로 큰 그릇과 숟가락을 들고 교실에 들어왔지. 그 뒤로 스퀴어스 양과 사랑스러운 워크퍼드가 따라왔어."], ["Their entrance, whether by premeditation or a simultaneous impulse, was the signal of revolt.", "워크퍼드는 아버지가 없는 동안 자질구레한 집행 업무를 떠맡고 있었지. 못이 박힌 장화로 학생들을 걷어차고, 작은 아이들 머리채를 잡아당기고, 다른 아이들은 약 오르는 데를 꼬집고, 그 밖에 여러 비슷한 방식으로 제 어머니에게 큰 위안과 행복이 되는 일들 말이야."], ["While one detachment rushed to the door and locked it, and another mounted on the desks and forms, the stoutest (and consequently the newest) boy seized the cane, and confronting Mrs.", "이들이 들어선 것이, 미리 짠 것인지 동시에 솟구친 충동인지는 몰라도, 반란의 신호가 되었어. 한 무리가 문으로 달려가 문을 잠그고, 다른 무리가 책상과 걸상 위로 올라가는 동안, 가장 덩치 큰—따라서 가장 새로 온—아이가 회초리를 움켜쥐었어."], ["Squeers with a stern countenance, snatched off her cap and beaver bonnet, put them on his own head, armed himself with the wooden spoon, and bade her, on pain of death, go down upon her knees and take a dose directly.", "그러고는 험상궂은 얼굴로 스퀴어스 부인 앞에 맞서서, 그녀의 모자와 비버 가죽 보닛을 낚아채 제 머리에 썼지. 나무 숟가락으로 무장하고는, 죽기 싫으면 당장 무릎을 꿇고 약을 한 입 받아 먹으라고 명령했어."], ["Before that estimable lady could recover herself, or offer the slightest retaliation, she was forced into a kneeling posture by a crowd of shouting tormentors, and compelled to swallow a spoonful of the odious mixture, rendered more than usually savoury by the immersion in the bowl of Master Wackford’s head, whose ducking was intrusted to another rebel.", "그 갸륵한 부인이 정신을 차리거나 조금이라도 맞받아칠 새도 없이, 소리 지르는 괴롭힘꾼들 무리가 그녀를 무릎 꿇는 자세로 떠밀었지. 그러고는 그 역겨운 혼합물을 한 숟가락 삼키게 했어."], ["The success of this first achievement prompted the malicious crowd, whose faces were clustered together in every variety of lank and half-starved ugliness, to further acts of outrage.", "게다가 그 약은 워크퍼드 도련님의 머리가 그릇에 처박힌 덕에 평소보다 더 구수해진 것이었지. 워크퍼드를 그릇에 처박는 일은 또 다른 반란자에게 맡겨졌어."], ["The leader was insisting upon Mrs.", "이 첫 성공에 고무되어, 갖가지 핼쑥하고 굶주린 추한 얼굴들이 한데 모인 그 악의에 찬 무리는 더 험한 짓들로 나아갔어."], ["Squeers repeating her dose, Master Squeers was undergoing another dip in the treacle, and a violent assault had been commenced on Miss Squeers, when John Browdie, bursting open the door with a vigorous kick, rushed to the rescue.", "우두머리는 스퀴어스 부인에게 약을 한 번 더 먹으라고 우기고 있었고, 스퀴어스 도련님은 또 한 번 당밀에 처박히고 있었지. 그리고 스퀴어스 양에게도 거센 공격이 시작된 참이었어."], ["The shouts, screams, groans, hoots, and clapping of hands, suddenly ceased, and a dead silence ensued.", "그때 존 브로디가 힘찬 발길질로 문을 박차고 구하러 달려들었지. 외침과 비명과 신음과 야유와 손뼉 소리가 갑자기 뚝 그치고, 죽은 듯한 정적이 흘렀어."]], [["‘Ye be noice chaps,’ said John, looking steadily round.", "\"너희 잘난 녀석들이로구나.\" 존이 차분히 둘러보며 말했어."], ["‘What’s to do here, thou yoong dogs?’", "\"여기서 뭣 하는 짓이여, 이 어린 개구쟁이들아?\""]], [["‘Squeers is in prison, and we are going to run away!’ cried a score of shrill voices.", "\"스퀴어스가 감옥에 갇혔어요, 우리는 도망칠 거예요!\" 스무 개쯤 되는 새된 목소리가 외쳤어."], ["‘We won’t stop, we won’t stop!’", "\"우리는 안 멈춰요, 안 멈춰요!\""]], [["‘Weel then, dinnot stop,’ replied John; ‘who waants thee to stop? Roon awa’ loike men, but dinnot hurt the women.’", "\"그래, 그럼 멈추지 말어.\" 존이 대꾸했어. \"누가 너희한테 멈추랬어? 사내답게 도망쳐. 하지만 여자들은 다치게 하지 말어.\""]], [["‘Hurrah!’ cried the shrill voices, more shrilly still.", "\"만세!\" 새된 목소리들이 더더욱 새되게 외쳤어."]], [["‘Hurrah?’ repeated John.", "\"만세?\" 존이 따라 말했어."], ["‘Weel, hurrah loike men too.", "\"그래, 만세도 사내답게 혀."], ["Noo then, look out.", "자 그럼, 잘 봐."], ["Hip--hip,--hip--hurrah!’", "힙, 힙, 힙, 만세!\""]], [["‘Hurrah!’ cried the voices.", "\"만세!\" 목소리들이 외쳤어."]], [["‘Hurrah!", "\"만세!"], ["Agean;’ said John.", "다시!\" 존이 말했어."], ["‘Looder still.’", "\"더 크게.\""]], [["The boys obeyed.", "아이들이 따랐어."]], [["‘Anoother!’ said John.", "\"한 번 더!\""], ["‘Dinnot be afeared on it.", "존이 말했어. \"겁먹지 말고."], ["Let’s have a good ‘un!’", "제대로 한번 혀 보자고!\""]], [["‘Hurrah!’", "\"만세!\""]], [["‘Noo then,’ said John, ‘let’s have yan more to end wi’, and then coot off as quick as you loike. Tak’a good breath noo--Squeers be in jail--the school’s brokken oop--it’s a’ ower--past and gane--think o’ thot, and let it be a hearty ‘un! Hurrah!’", "\"자 그럼.\" 존이 말했어. \"마지막으로 한 번만 더 하고, 그담엔 너희 좋을 대로 후딱 튀어. 자, 숨을 크게 들이쉬어. 스퀴어스는 감옥에 갇혔다. 학교는 끝장났다. 죄다 끝이여. 다 지나가고 사라졌다. 그걸 생각하면서, 진하게 한번 혀 보자고! 만세!\""]], [["Such a cheer arose as the walls of Dotheboys Hall had never echoed before, and were destined never to respond to again.", "두더보이즈 홀의 벽이 일찍이 한 번도 울려 본 적 없는, 그리고 다시는 응답할 일 없을 그런 환호가 터져 나왔어."], ["When the sound had died away, the school was empty; and of the busy noisy crowd which had peopled it but five minutes before, not one remained.", "그 소리가 잦아들었을 때, 교실은 텅 비어 있었지. 오 분 전만 해도 그곳을 가득 메우던 분주하고 시끄러운 무리 가운데, 하나도 남지 않았어."]], [["‘Very well, Mr.", "\"잘하셨네요, 브로디 씨!\""], ["Browdie!’ said Miss Squeers, hot and flushed from the recent encounter, but vixenish to the last; ‘you’ve been and excited our boys to run away.", "스퀴어스 양이 말했어. 방금 전 소동으로 얼굴이 달아오르고 벌게져 있었지만, 끝까지 표독스러웠지."], ["Now see if we don’t pay you out for that, sir!", "\"당신이 와서 우리 애들을 부추겨 도망치게 만들었어요."], ["If my pa IS unfortunate and trod down by henemies, we’re not going to be basely crowed and conquered over by you and ‘Tilda.’", "그 대가를 톡톡히 치르게 해 드릴 테니 두고 보세요, 선생! 우리 아빠가 불운하게 원수들한테 짓밟혔다고 해서, 당신이랑 틸다한테 비열하게 비웃음당하고 짓눌릴 우리가 아니라고요.\""]], [["‘Noa!’ replied John bluntly, ‘thou bean’t. Tak’ thy oath o’ thot. Think betther o’ us, Fanny. I tell ‘ee both, that I’m glod the auld man has been caught out at last--dom’d glod--but ye’ll sooffer eneaf wi’out any crowin’ fra’ me, and I be not the mun to crow, nor be Tilly the lass, so I tell ‘ee flat. More than thot, I tell ‘ee noo, that if thou need’st friends to help thee awa’ from this place--dinnot turn up thy nose, Fanny, thou may’st--thou’lt foind Tilly and I wi’ a thout o’ old times aboot us, ready to lend thee a hond. And when I say thot, dinnot think I be asheamed of waa’t I’ve deane, for I say again, Hurrah! and dom the schoolmeasther. There!’", "\"아니지!\" 존이 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸했어. \"너흰 그런 꼴 안 당해. 내 맹세허지. 우리를 좀 좋게 봐, 패니. 둘 다한테 말허는디, 나는 그 늙은이가 마침내 덜미 잡힌 게 기뻐. 더럽게 기뻐. 하지만 너흰 내 비웃음 없이도 충분히 고생할 거여. 나는 비웃을 사람이 아녀. 틸리도 그럴 처자가 아니고. 그러니께 똑똑히 말해 두는 거여. 그뿐 아녀, 지금 말허는디, 만약에 이곳을 떠날 때 도와줄 친구가 필요허면—코 쳐들지 말어, 패니, 그래도 돼—옛정을 떠올리며 손 내밀 준비가 된 틸리와 내가 있다는 걸 알게 될 거여. 그리고 이 말 헌다고 내가 한 짓을 부끄러워한다고는 생각 말어. 다시 한번 말허니께. 만세! 그리고 교장 선생, 망해 버려라. 자!\""]], [["His parting words concluded, John Browdie strode heavily out, remounted his nag, put him once more into a smart canter, and, carolling lustily forth some fragments of an old song, to which the horse’s hoofs rang a merry accompaniment, sped back to his pretty wife and to Nicholas.", "작별의 말을 마치고, 존 브로디는 묵직하게 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔어. 다시 말에 올라타, 또 한 번 가볍게 박차를 가했지. 그러고는 옛 노래 몇 소절을 우렁차게 흥얼거리며 달려갔어. 말발굽이 그 노래에 흥겨운 장단을 맞춰 울렸지. 그렇게 그는 예쁜 아내와 니콜라스에게로 서둘러 돌아갔어."]], [["For some days afterwards, the neighbouring country was overrun with boys, who, the report went, had been secretly furnished by Mr.", "그 뒤로 며칠 동안, 이웃 고장은 소년들로 넘쳐 났어."], ["and Mrs Browdie, not only with a hearty meal of bread and meat, but with sundry shillings and sixpences to help them on their way.", "소문에 따르면 브로디 부부가 몰래 그들에게 빵과 고기로 든든한 한 끼를 챙겨 줬을 뿐 아니라, 가는 길에 보태라고 실링과 식스펜스도 이런저런 만큼 쥐여 줬다는 거였지. 이 소문에 존은 늘 단호히 아니라고 했어."], ["To this rumour John always returned a stout denial, which he accompanied, however, with a lurking grin, that rendered the suspicious doubtful, and fully confirmed all previous believers.", "그런데 그러면서도 슬며시 씩 웃었지. 그 웃음 때문에 의심하던 이들은 헷갈렸고, 진작부터 믿던 이들은 확신을 굳혔어."]], [["There were a few timid young children, who, miserable as they had been, and many as were the tears they had shed in the wretched school, still knew no other home, and had formed for it a sort of attachment, which made them weep when the bolder spirits fled, and cling to it as a refuge.", "겁 많은 어린아이들도 몇몇 있었어. 비참하게 지냈고, 그 끔찍한 학교에서 숱한 눈물을 흘렸으면서도, 다른 집을 알지 못해 거기에 일종의 정 같은 것을 붙인 아이들이었지."], ["Of these, some were found crying under hedges and in such places, frightened at the solitude.", "그래서 더 대담한 녀석들이 달아날 때 울었고, 피난처인 양 거기에 매달렸어. 이 아이들 가운데 몇몇은 산울타리 밑이나 그런 곳에서 외로움에 겁먹은 채 울고 있는 것을 발견했지."], ["One had a dead bird in a little cage; he had wandered nearly twenty miles, and when his poor favourite died, lost courage, and lay down beside him.", "한 아이는 작은 새장에 죽은 새를 넣어 가지고 있었어. 그 아이는 거의 이십 마일이나 헤매다, 가엾은 자기 새가 죽자 용기를 잃고 그 옆에 드러누워 있었지."], ["Another was discovered in a yard hard by the school, sleeping with a dog, who bit at those who came to remove him, and licked the sleeping child’s pale face.", "또 다른 아이는 학교 근처 마당에서 발견됐어. 개와 함께 자고 있었는데, 그 개는 아이를 떼어 내려고 다가오는 사람들을 물려고 들었고, 잠든 아이의 핼쑥한 얼굴을 핥았어."]], [["They were taken back, and some other stragglers were recovered, but by degrees they were claimed, or lost again; and, in course of time, Dotheboys Hall and its last breaking-up began to be forgotten by the neighbours, or to be only spoken of as among the things that had been.", "아이들은 다시 데려와졌고, 다른 낙오자 몇몇도 거두어졌어. 하지만 차츰차츰 아이들은 누군가 데려가거나 다시 사라졌지. 그리고 세월이 흐르면서, 두더보이즈 홀과 그 마지막 해산은 이웃들에게 잊히기 시작했어. 아니면 그저 지나간 일들 가운데 하나로만 입에 오르내리게 되었지."]], [["Conclusion\nWhen her term of mourning had expired, Madeline gave her hand and fortune to Nicholas; and, on the same day and at the same time, Kate became Mrs.", "마침내 상복을 벗을 때가 되자, 매들린은 니콜라스에게 자기 손과 재산을 함께 주었어."], ["Frank Cheeryble.", "그리고 같은 날 같은 시각에, 케이트는 프랭크 치어리블 부인이 되었지."], ["It was expected that Tim Linkinwater and Miss La Creevy would have made a third couple on the occasion, but they declined, and two or three weeks afterwards went out together one morning before breakfast, and, coming back with merry faces, were found to have been quietly married that day.", "이날 팀 링킨워터와 라 크리비 양도 세 번째 짝이 될 거라고들 짐작했지만, 두 사람은 마다했어. 그러더니 두세 주쯤 지난 어느 날 아침, 식사도 하기 전에 함께 나갔다가 환한 얼굴로 돌아왔는데, 알고 보니 그날 둘이 조용히 결혼식을 올린 거였어."]], [["The money which Nicholas acquired in right of his wife he invested in the firm of Cheeryble Brothers, in which Frank had become a partner.", "니콜라스는 아내 덕분에 얻은 돈을 치어리블 형제 상회에 투자했어. 프랭크가 그곳의 동업자가 되어 있었거든."], ["Before many years elapsed, the business began to be carried on in the names of ‘Cheeryble and Nickleby,’ so that Mrs.", "몇 해 지나지 않아 사업은 '치어리블 앤드 니클비'라는 이름으로 굴러가기 시작했어."], ["Nickleby’s prophetic anticipations were realised at last.", "그러니 니클비 부인이 앞날을 내다보며 했던 기대가 마침내 이루어진 셈이지."]], [["The twin brothers retired.", "쌍둥이 형제는 일선에서 물러났어."], ["Who needs to be told that THEY were happy?", "그들이 행복했다는 걸 누가 새삼 말해 줄 필요가 있을까?"], ["They were surrounded by happiness of their own creation, and lived but to increase it.", "두 사람은 스스로 만들어 낸 행복에 둘러싸여 있었고, 그 행복을 더 키우는 것 말고는 다른 삶이 없었거든."]], [["Tim Linkinwater condescended, after much entreaty and brow-beating, to accept a share in the house; but he could never be prevailed upon to suffer the publication of his name as a partner, and always persisted in the punctual and regular discharge of his clerkly duties.", "팀 링킨워터는 거듭된 간청과 다그침 끝에 마지못해 상회의 지분을 받아들였어. 하지만 자기 이름을 동업자로 내거는 것만큼은 끝내 받아들이지 않았지. 그러고는 늘 사무원의 일을 한 치 어김없이 꼬박꼬박 해냈어."]], [["He and his wife lived in the old house, and occupied the very bedchamber in which he had slept for four-and-forty years. As his wife grew older, she became even a more cheerful and light-hearted little creature; and it was a common saying among their friends, that it was impossible to say which looked the happier, Tim as he sat calmly smiling in his elbow-chair on one side of the fire, or his brisk little wife chatting and laughing, and constantly bustling in and out of hers, on the other.", "그와 아내는 옛집에서 살았어. 팀이 사십사 년 동안 잠들었던 바로 그 침실을 그대로 썼지. 아내는 나이를 먹을수록 더 명랑하고 마음 가벼운 작은 사람이 되어 갔어. 친구들 사이에서는 이런 말이 흔히 오갔어. 난롯가 한쪽에서 안락의자에 앉아 잔잔히 웃고 있는 팀과, 맞은편에서 재잘대고 웃으며 끊임없이 의자를 들락날락하는 자그마하고 발랄한 그의 아내 중 누가 더 행복해 보이는지 도무지 가릴 수가 없다고 말이야."]], [["Dick, the blackbird, was removed from the counting-house and promoted to a warm corner in the common sitting-room.", "검은지빠귀 딕은 회계실에서 옮겨져 거실의 따뜻한 구석 자리로 승진했어. 새장 아래에는 작은 초상화 두 점이 걸려 있었지."], ["Beneath his cage hung two miniatures, of Mrs.", "링킨워터 부인이 직접 그린 그림이었어."], ["Linkinwater’s execution; one representing herself, and the other Tim; and both smiling very hard at all beholders.", "하나는 자기 모습이고 다른 하나는 팀이었는데, 둘 다 보는 사람마다 향해 아주 열심히 웃고 있었어."], ["Tim’s head being powdered like a twelfth cake, and his spectacles copied with great nicety, strangers detected a close resemblance to him at the first glance, and this leading them to suspect that the other must be his wife, and emboldening them to say so without scruple, Mrs.", "팀의 머리는 십이일절 케이크처럼 분을 뿌린 듯했고 안경도 아주 정성껏 베껴 그려서, 낯선 사람들도 첫눈에 팀과 꼭 닮았다는 걸 알아봤어. 그러니 다른 한 점은 그의 아내일 거라고 짐작했고, 거리낌 없이 그렇게 말할 만큼 자신만만해졌지."], ["Linkinwater grew very proud of these achievements in time, and considered them among the most successful likenesses she had ever painted.", "링킨워터 부인은 시간이 흐를수록 이 솜씨를 무척 자랑스러워했고, 자기가 그린 것 가운데 가장 잘 닮게 그린 그림으로 꼽았어."], ["Tim had the profoundest faith in them, likewise; for on this, as on all other subjects, they held but one opinion; and if ever there were a ‘comfortable couple’ in the world, it was Mr.", "팀도 그 그림을 깊이 믿었지. 이 일에서나 다른 모든 일에서나 두 사람은 늘 같은 생각이었거든."], ["and Mrs. Linkinwater.", "세상에 '사이좋은 부부'가 있다면, 그건 바로 링킨워터 부부였어."]], [["Ralph, having died intestate, and having no relations but those with whom he had lived in such enmity, they would have become in legal course his heirs. But they could not bear the thought of growing rich on money so acquired, and felt as though they could never hope to prosper with it. They made no claim to his wealth; and the riches for which he had toiled all his days, and burdened his soul with so many evil deeds, were swept at last into the coffers of the state, and no man was the better or the happier for them.", "랄프는 유언장도 남기지 않고 죽었어. 게다가 그토록 원수처럼 지내던 사람들 말고는 친척이 없었으니, 법대로 따지자면 그 사람들이 그의 상속자가 될 판이었지. 하지만 그들은 그렇게 손에 들어온 돈으로 부자가 된다는 생각을 견딜 수가 없었어. 그 돈으로는 결코 잘될 리 없다고 느꼈거든. 그래서 그들은 랄프의 재산에 아무런 권리도 주장하지 않았어. 랄프가 평생을 바쳐 모으고 그 많은 못된 짓으로 제 영혼을 짓눌러 가며 쌓은 재물은, 결국 국고 속으로 깡그리 쓸려 들어갔어. 그 돈으로 더 나아지거나 더 행복해진 사람은 아무도 없었지."]], [["Arthur Gride was tried for the unlawful possession of the will, which he had either procured to be stolen, or had dishonestly acquired and retained by other means as bad. By dint of an ingenious counsel, and a legal flaw, he escaped; but only to undergo a worse punishment; for, some years afterwards, his house was broken open in the night by robbers, tempted by the rumours of his great wealth, and he was found murdered in his bed.", "아서 그라이드는 유언장을 불법으로 가지고 있었던 죄로 재판을 받았어. 그 유언장은 사람을 시켜 훔쳐 낸 것이거나, 아니면 그에 못지않게 나쁜 다른 수법으로 부정하게 손에 넣어 감춰 둔 것이었지. 교활한 변호사와 법의 허점 덕분에 그는 빠져나갔어. 하지만 그건 더 끔찍한 벌을 받기 위해서였을 뿐이야. 몇 해 뒤, 그가 엄청난 부자라는 소문에 혹한 강도들이 한밤중에 그의 집을 부수고 들어왔고, 그는 침대에서 살해된 채 발견되었거든."]], [["Mrs. Sliderskew went beyond the seas at nearly the same time as Mr Squeers, and in the course of nature never returned.", "슬라이더스큐 부인은 스퀴어스와 거의 같은 무렵에 바다 건너로 떠났고, 순리대로 다시는 돌아오지 못했어."], ["Brooker died penitent.", "브루커는 뉘우치며 죽었지."], ["Sir Mulberry Hawk lived abroad for some years, courted and caressed, and in high repute as a fine dashing fellow.", "멀버리 호크 경은 몇 해 동안 외국에서 살았어. 떠받들리고 귀여움받으며, 멋지고 잘나가는 사내로 명성이 자자했지."], ["Ultimately, returning to this country, he was thrown into jail for debt, and there perished miserably, as such high spirits generally do.", "그러다 끝내 이 나라로 돌아왔다가 빚 때문에 감옥에 갇혔고, 거기서 비참하게 죽고 말았어. 그런 한껏 우쭐대던 자들이 대개 그렇게 되듯이 말이야."]], [["The first act of Nicholas, when he became a rich and prosperous merchant, was to buy his father’s old house.", "니콜라스는 부유하고 번창하는 상인이 되자 가장 먼저 아버지의 옛집을 사들였어. 세월이 흐르고 그의 곁에 사랑스러운 아이들이 하나둘 모여들면서, 그 집은 고쳐지고 넓어졌지."], ["As time crept on, and there came gradually about him a group of lovely children, it was altered and enlarged; but none of the old rooms were ever pulled down, no old tree was ever rooted up, nothing with which there was any association of bygone times was ever removed or changed.", "하지만 옛 방은 하나도 헐리지 않았어. 오래된 나무도 한 그루 뽑히지 않았고, 지난 시절의 추억이 깃든 것은 무엇 하나 치워지거나 바뀌지 않았어."]], [["Within a stone’s throw was another retreat, enlivened by children’s pleasant voices too; and here was Kate, with many new cares and occupations, and many new faces courting her sweet smile (and one so like her own, that to her mother she seemed a child again), the same true gentle creature, the same fond sister, the same in the love of all about her, as in her girlish days.", "돌을 던지면 닿을 만한 가까운 곳에 또 하나의 보금자리가 있었는데, 그곳에도 아이들의 즐거운 목소리가 넘쳤어. 여기엔 케이트가 살았지. 새로운 걱정거리와 할 일이 많았고, 그녀의 다정한 미소를 받고 싶어 하는 새 얼굴들도 많았어. 그중 하나는 어찌나 그녀를 닮았는지, 어머니 눈에는 케이트가 다시 어린아이가 된 것처럼 보였지. 케이트는 처녀 시절과 똑같았어. 한결같이 참되고 다정한 사람이었고, 한결같은 사랑스러운 누이였으며, 주위 모든 이의 사랑을 받는 것도 그대로였어."]], [["Mrs. Nickleby lived, sometimes with her daughter, and sometimes with her son, accompanying one or other of them to London at those periods when the cares of business obliged both families to reside there, and always preserving a great appearance of dignity, and relating her experiences (especially on points connected with the management and bringing-up of children) with much solemnity and importance.", "니클비 부인은 어떤 때는 딸과, 어떤 때는 아들과 함께 살았어."], ["It was a very long time before she could be induced to receive Mrs.", "사업상의 일로 두 집안이 다 런던에 머물러야 할 때면 둘 중 하나를 따라 런던으로 갔지. 그리고 늘 위엄 있는 태도를 한껏 지키며, 자기 경험담을 — 특히 아이를 다루고 키우는 일에 관해서라면 — 아주 엄숙하고 거창하게 늘어놓았어."], ["Linkinwater into favour, and it is even doubtful whether she ever thoroughly forgave her.", "부인이 링킨워터 부인을 호의로 받아들이기까지는 아주 오랜 시간이 걸렸어. 게다가 부인이 그녀를 정말로 끝까지 용서했는지조차 알 수 없는 일이었지."]], [["There was one grey-haired, quiet, harmless gentleman, who, winter and summer, lived in a little cottage hard by Nicholas’s house, and, when he was not there, assumed the superintendence of affairs.", "흰머리에 조용하고 사람 좋은 신사가 한 명 있었어. 그는 겨울이고 여름이고 니콜라스의 집 바로 옆 작은 오두막에서 살았지."], ["His chief pleasure and delight was in the children, with whom he was a child himself, and master of the revels.", "그리고 니콜라스가 집을 비울 때면 집안일을 도맡아 챙겼어. 그가 가장 기뻐하고 즐거워하는 건 아이들과 함께 있는 일이었어."], ["The little people could do nothing without dear Newman Noggs.", "아이들과 어울릴 때면 자기도 아이가 되어 놀이의 대장 노릇을 했지. 꼬마들은 사랑하는 뉴먼 노그스 없이는 아무것도 할 수가 없었어."]], [["The grass was green above the dead boy’s grave, and trodden by feet so small and light, that not a daisy drooped its head beneath their pressure. Through all the spring and summertime, garlands of fresh flowers, wreathed by infant hands, rested on the stone; and, when the children came to change them lest they should wither and be pleasant to him no longer, their eyes filled with tears, and they spoke low and softly of their poor dead cousin.", "세상을 떠난 그 소년의 무덤 위로는 풀이 푸르게 자랐어. 그 위를 밟는 발이 어찌나 작고 가벼운지, 그 무게에 고개를 숙이는 데이지 한 송이조차 없었지. 봄과 여름 내내, 어린 손들이 엮은 싱싱한 꽃다발이 그 비석 위에 놓였어. 그리고 아이들이 꽃이 시들어 그에게 더는 기쁨이 되지 못할까 봐 새것으로 갈아 주러 올 때면, 눈에는 눈물이 그렁그렁 고였어. 그러고는 가엾게 죽은 사촌 이야기를 나직나직 속삭였지."]]];
